// Generated by scripts/build-english.cjs from the German data and i18n/en/. Do not edit; run npm run build:english.
window.VOCAB_RUNTIME_DETAILS = [
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は学生です。","romaji":"Watashi wa gakusei desu.","german":"Ich bin Student.","furigana":"わたし|がくせい"},{"japanese":"私はドイツから来ました。","romaji":"Watashi wa Doitsu kara kimashita.","german":"Ich komme aus Deutschland.","furigana":"わたし|き"}],"notes":"Neutrale bis höfliche Selbstbezeichnung. In sachlichen und beruflichen Gesprächen für alle Geschlechter geeignet. Wenn aus dem Zusammenhang klar ist, wer spricht, lässt man 私 häufig weg. 私の bezeichnet Besitz oder Zugehörigkeit; die formellere Lesung わたくし ist eine eigene Lesung.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの人は誰ですか。","romaji":"Ano hito wa dare desu ka.","german":"Who is that person?","furigana":"ひと|だれ"},{"japanese":"この店には人がたくさんいます。","romaji":"Kono mise ni wa hito ga takusan imasu.","german":"There are a lot of people in this shop.","furigana":"みせ|ひと"}],"notes":"Refers to a human being without specifying gender; あの人 points to a particular person. To count people, use 一人、二人、三人 with their own special readings, rather than simply putting a number before ひと. 人が多い describes a large crowd.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの男の人は先生です。","romaji":"Ano otoko no hito wa sensei desu.","german":"That man is a teacher.","furigana":"おとこ|ひと|せんせい"},{"japanese":"男の子が走っています。","romaji":"Otoko no ko ga hashitte imasu.","german":"A boy is running.","furigana":"おとこ|こ|はし"}],"notes":"男 on its own can sound rather blunt when referring to someone you do not know. 男の人 is often the more polite form in everyday speech, and 男性 the neutral, formal one. 男の子 means a boy, so adding の changes the reference.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 40"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの女の人は医者です。","romaji":"Ano onna no hito wa isha desu.","german":"That woman is a doctor.","furigana":"おんな|ひと|いしゃ"},{"japanese":"女の子が歌っています。","romaji":"Onna no ko ga utatte imasu.","german":"A girl is singing.","furigana":"おんな|こ|うた"}],"notes":"女 on its own can sound abrupt when referring to a stranger. 女の人 is often more polite in everyday speech, and 女性 is common in neutral texts. 女の子 means a girl; 女 is not a polite form of address.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 17"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子供が公園で遊んでいます。","romaji":"Kodomo ga kōen de asonde imasu.","german":"The child is playing in the park.","furigana":"こども|こうえん|あそ"},{"japanese":"私には子供が三人います。","romaji":"Watashi ni wa kodomo ga sannin imasu.","german":"I have three children.","furigana":"わたし|こども|さんにん"}],"notes":"Can refer to one child or several; the number is clear from context. 子供がいる means, depending on context, that children are present or that someone has children. 子ども is a common alternative spelling. Adult offspring can also be called 子供.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"友達と映画を見ました。","romaji":"Tomodachi to eiga o mimashita.","german":"I watched a movie with a friend.","furigana":"ともだち|えいが|み"},{"japanese":"友達に手紙を書きました。","romaji":"Tomodachi ni tegami o kakimashita.","german":"I wrote a letter to a friend.","furigana":"ともだち|てがみ|か"}],"notes":"Common in everyday speech; depending on context it can mean one friend or several. 友達と marks doing something together, while 友達に marks, for example, the recipient of a message. 友人 is more neutral or formal. The word does not by itself denote a romantic relationship.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先生はとても優しいです。","romaji":"Sensei wa totemo yasashii desu.","german":"The teacher is very kind.","furigana":"せんせい|やさ"},{"japanese":"先生に質問します。","romaji":"Sensei ni shitsumon shimasu.","german":"I ask the teacher a question.","furigana":"せんせい|しつもん"},{"japanese":"先生、のどが痛いです。","romaji":"Sensei, nodo ga itai desu.","german":"Doctor, my throat hurts.","furigana":"せんせい|いた"}],"notes":"Used as an occupational term and as a form of address for teachers, but also for doctors, for example. A name can come directly before it: 田中先生. When stating your own occupation, you normally say 教師です or 教員です rather than calling yourself by the honorific 先生.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は大学の学生です。","romaji":"Watashi wa daigaku no gakusei desu.","german":"I am a university student.","furigana":"わたし|だいがく|がくせい"},{"japanese":"学生がたくさんいます。","romaji":"Gakusei ga takusan imasu.","german":"There are a lot of students.","furigana":"がくせい"},{"japanese":"学生はこの切符を安く買えますか。","romaji":"Gakusei wa kono kippu o yasuku kaemasu ka.","german":"Can students buy this ticket at a discount?","furigana":"がくせい|きっぷ|やす|か"}],"notes":"Mainly used for people studying at universities and colleges; depending on context it can be broader. For school pupils, 生徒 is more accurate, and for elementary school children, 児童. Common in 大学生, 学生生活 and 学生証. 学生の＋noun assigns something to the student sphere.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"家族は四人です。","romaji":"Kazoku wa yonin desu.","german":"There are four people in my family.","furigana":"かぞく|よにん"},{"japanese":"家族で旅行に行きます。","romaji":"Kazoku de ryokō ni ikimasu.","german":"I go on a trip with my family.","furigana":"かぞく|りょこう|い"}],"notes":"家族は四人です counts the members of a family. 家族で＋verb means the family does something together; で marks the acting group here. For another person's family, ご家族 is a polite form.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お父さんは会社員です。","romaji":"Otōsan wa kaishain desu.","german":"My father is an office worker.","furigana":"とう|かいしゃいん"},{"japanese":"お父さんは毎朝新聞を読みます。","romaji":"Otōsan wa maiasa shinbun o yomimasu.","german":"My father reads the newspaper every morning.","furigana":"とう|まいあさしんぶん|よ"},{"japanese":"お父さん、手伝ってくれる？","romaji":"Otōsan, tetsudatte kureru?","german":"Dad, will you help me?","furigana":"とう|てつだ"}],"notes":"お父さん is a respectful term for another person's father and a common way to address your own father. In familiar family conversation it can also mean \"my father\", as in the first examples. When speaking to outsiders, you normally use 父（ちち）for your own father.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 7"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お母さんは料理が上手です。","romaji":"Okāsan wa ryōri ga jōzu desu.","german":"My mother is good at cooking.","furigana":"かあ|りょうり|じょうず"},{"japanese":"お母さんに電話をかけます。","romaji":"Okāsan ni denwa o kakemasu.","german":"I call my mother.","furigana":"かあ|でんわ"},{"japanese":"お母さん、行ってきます。","romaji":"Okāsan, itte kimasu.","german":"Mom, I'm off. See you later!","furigana":"かあ|い"}],"notes":"お母さん is used to address your own mother or respectfully for another person's mother. In familiar conversation it can also refer to your own mother. In a formal introduction to outsiders, you normally say 母（はは）.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 7"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お兄さんは大学生です。","romaji":"Oniisan wa daigakusei desu.","german":"My older brother is a university student.","furigana":"にい|だいがくせい"},{"japanese":"お兄さんは背が高いです。","romaji":"Oniisan wa se ga takai desu.","german":"My older brother is tall.","furigana":"にい|せ|たか"},{"japanese":"お兄さん、この問題を教えて。","romaji":"Oniisan, kono mondai o oshiete.","german":"Big brother, help me with this problem.","furigana":"にい|もんだい|おし"}],"notes":"お兄さん respectfully refers to another person's older brother and serves as a form of address for your own older brother. The first examples should be understood as familiar family conversation. To outsiders, you normally call your own older brother 兄（あに）. It does not mean \"younger brother\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 11"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お姉さんは東京に住んでいます。","romaji":"Onēsan wa Tōkyō ni sunde imasu.","german":"My older sister lives in Tokyo.","furigana":"ねえ|とうきょう|す"},{"japanese":"お姉さんは先生になりました。","romaji":"Onēsan wa sensei ni narimashita.","german":"My older sister became a teacher.","furigana":"ねえ|せんせい"},{"japanese":"お姉さん、これを見て。","romaji":"Onēsan, kore o mite.","german":"Big sister, look at this.","furigana":"ねえ|み"}],"notes":"お姉さん is a respectful term for an older sister and a possible form of address for your own older sister. In the first examples, someone is talking about their own sister in a familiar setting. To outsiders, 姉（あね）is normally used. For a younger sister, say 妹.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 11"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"弟は十歳です。","romaji":"Otōto wa jussai desu.","german":"My younger brother is ten years old.","furigana":"おとうと|じゅっさい"},{"japanese":"弟と一緒にサッカーをします。","romaji":"Otōto to issho ni sakkā o shimasu.","german":"I play soccer with my younger brother.","furigana":"おとうと|いっしょ"}],"notes":"弟 means a younger brother, whether he is a child or an adult. For your own brother you normally say 弟; for another person's younger brother, 弟さん is polite. The age is relative to the person in question, not to the speaker in every situation.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 11"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"妹は高校生です。","romaji":"Imōto wa kōkōsei desu.","german":"My younger sister is a high school student.","furigana":"いもうと|こうこうせい"},{"japanese":"妹は歌が上手です。","romaji":"Imōto wa uta ga jōzu desu.","german":"My younger sister sings well.","furigana":"いもうと|うた|じょうず"},{"japanese":"明日は妹と映画を見に行きます。","romaji":"Ashita wa imōto to eiga o mi ni ikimasu.","german":"Tomorrow I'm going to a movie with my younger sister.","furigana":"あした|いもうと|えいが|み|い"}],"notes":"妹 means \"younger sister\" and does not fix an absolute age. 妹 is used for your own sister, and 妹さん is polite for another person's younger sister. The sister can therefore also be an adult. 妹と＋verb describes a joint action.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 11"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"朝ごはんを食べます。","romaji":"Asagohan o tabemasu.","german":"I eat breakfast.","furigana":"あさ|た"},{"japanese":"寿司を食べたいです。","romaji":"Sushi o tabetai desu.","german":"I want to eat sushi.","furigana":"すし|た"}],"notes":"食べる is a transitive ichidan verb: 食べます・食べて・食べない. The food usually takes を. 食べたい expresses a wish to eat; depending on context, 食べる can mean \"to eat\" or \"to have a meal\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"水を飲みます。","romaji":"Mizu o nomimasu.","german":"Ich trinke Wasser.","furigana":"みず|の"},{"japanese":"お茶を飲みませんか。","romaji":"Ocha o nomimasen ka.","german":"Möchten Sie Tee trinken?","furigana":"ちゃ|の"},{"japanese":"食後に薬を飲みます。","romaji":"Shokugo ni kusuri o nomimasu.","german":"Ich nehme nach dem Essen Medikamente ein.","furigana":"しょくご|くすり|の"}],"notes":"飲む steht mit を für das Getränk; auch 薬を飲む heißt „Medikamente einnehmen“. Das transitive Godan-Verb bildet 飲みます・飲んで・飲まない. 飲みませんか ist oft eine Einladung und verlangt keine verneinende deutsche Übersetzung.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"学校に行きます。","romaji":"Gakkō ni ikimasu.","german":"I go to school.","furigana":"がっこう|い"},{"japanese":"明日東京に行きます。","romaji":"Ashita Tōkyō ni ikimasu.","german":"I'm going to Tokyo tomorrow.","furigana":"あしたとうきょう|い"},{"japanese":"仕事はうまくいっています。","romaji":"Shigoto wa umaku itte imasu.","german":"Work is going well.","furigana":"しごと"}],"notes":"行く describes movement away from the respective reference point; the destination takes に or へ. Depending on the means of transport, it can mean \"to go\" or \"to travel\". This godan verb has the special forms 行って・行った, but regular 行きます・行かない. In うまくいく it means \"to go well\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"友達が来ます。","romaji":"Tomodachi ga kimasu.","german":"A friend is coming.","furigana":"ともだち|き"},{"japanese":"日本から来ました。","romaji":"Nihon kara kimashita.","german":"I came from Japan.","furigana":"にほん|き"},{"japanese":"春が来ました。","romaji":"Haru ga kimashita.","german":"Spring has come.","furigana":"はる|き"}],"notes":"来る describes movement toward the speaker or another reference point. This irregular verb changes its reading: 来ます（きます）・来て（きて）・来ない（こない）. 日本から来ました names the place of origin; with a season, 来る can also express its arrival.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"家に帰ります。","romaji":"Ie ni kaerimasu.","german":"I go home.","furigana":"いえ|かえ"},{"japanese":"五時に帰ります。","romaji":"Goji ni kaerimasu.","german":"I'll be home at five o'clock.","furigana":"ごじ|かえ"},{"japanese":"お客さんはもう帰りました。","romaji":"Okyakusan wa mō kaerimashita.","german":"The guest has already left.","furigana":"きゃく|かえ"}],"notes":"帰る means returning home or to a familiar place of origin. For guests, it can mean \"to leave, to say goodbye\". Despite its -える ending, it is a godan verb: 帰ります・帰って・帰らない. 帰る and 変える（to change）are different words.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"テレビを見ます。","romaji":"Terebi o mimasu.","german":"I watch TV.","furigana":"み"},{"japanese":"桜を見に行きましょう。","romaji":"Sakura o mi ni ikimashō.","german":"Let's go see the cherry blossoms.","furigana":"さくら|み|い"}],"notes":"見る is a transitive ichidan verb: 見ます・見て・見ない. What is looked at takes を. 見に行く combines the verb stem 見 with に and 行く: you go somewhere in order to look at something. For deliberate watching, 観る is also used; it is not necessarily interchangeable with 見る in every sense.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"音楽を聞きます。","romaji":"Ongaku o kikimasu.","german":"I listen to music.","furigana":"おんがく|き"},{"japanese":"先生に聞いてください。","romaji":"Sensei ni kiite kudasai.","german":"Please ask the teacher.","furigana":"せんせい|き"}],"notes":"聞く can mean \"to hear, to listen\" or \"to ask\". 音楽を聞く names what is heard with を; 先生に聞く names the person asked with に. This transitive godan verb forms 聞きます・聞いて・聞かない. Context decides between the two meanings.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"本を読みます。","romaji":"Hon o yomimasu.","german":"I read a book.","furigana":"ほん|よ"},{"japanese":"毎日新聞を読みます。","romaji":"Mainichi shinbun o yomimasu.","german":"I read the newspaper every day.","furigana":"まいにちしんぶん|よ"},{"japanese":"この漢字は何と読みますか。","romaji":"Kono kanji wa nan to yomimasu ka.","german":"How do you read this kanji?","furigana":"かんじ|なん|よ"}],"notes":"読む is a transitive godan verb: 読みます・読んで・読まない. Besides reading texts, it can indicate how a character is pronounced: 何と読みますか asks for the reading. The material being read usually takes を.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"手紙を書きます。","romaji":"Tegami o kakimasu.","german":"I write a letter.","furigana":"てがみ|か"},{"japanese":"名前を書いてください。","romaji":"Namae o kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write your name.","furigana":"なまえ|か"}],"notes":"書く refers to writing a text or individual characters. What is written takes を: 手紙を書く, 名前を書く. The て-form is 書いて; the polite form is 書きます.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本語を話します。","romaji":"Nihongo o hanashimasu.","german":"I speak Japanese.","furigana":"にほんご|はな"},{"japanese":"友達と電話で話しました。","romaji":"Tomodachi to denwa de hanashimashita.","german":"I talked with a friend on the phone.","furigana":"ともだち|でんわ|はな"}],"notes":"With a language as the object, 話す means \"to speak a language\": 日本語を話す. The person you talk to is attached with と, the means of communication with で: 友達と電話で話す. Do not confuse it with the similar 離す (\"to separate\").","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"パンを買います。","romaji":"Pan o kaimasu.","german":"I buy bread.","furigana":"か"},{"japanese":"そのかばんをいくらで買いましたか。","romaji":"Sono kaban o ikura de kaimashita ka.","german":"How much did you pay for that bag?","furigana":"か"}],"notes":"買う means to buy something with money. The item takes を; the purchase price can be given with で: いくらで買う (\"to buy for how much\"). For this godan verb, う becomes って in the て-form: 買って.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"勉強をします。","romaji":"Benkyō o shimasu.","german":"I study.","furigana":"べんきょう"},{"japanese":"テニスをしませんか。","romaji":"Tenisu o shimasen ka.","german":"Won't you play tennis with me?"}],"notes":"する is irregular: します, して, した. It forms verbs with action nouns, such as 勉強する; 勉強をする is also possible. For sports, you say, for example, テニスをする. The form ～しませんか invites someone to do something together. 為る is a rare kanji spelling of this verb; する in hiragana is the norm.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"机の上に本があります。","romaji":"Tsukue no ue ni hon ga arimasu.","german":"There is a book on the desk.","furigana":"つくえ|うえ|ほん"},{"japanese":"今日は時間がありません。","romaji":"Kyō wa jikan ga arimasen.","german":"I don't have time today.","furigana":"きょう|じかん"}],"notes":"ある expresses the existence of inanimate things and the availability of, for example, time. A place takes に, and the thing that exists takes が: 机の上に本がある. For people and animals, いる is normally used. The plain negative is ない, the polite one ありません.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"兄がいます。","romaji":"Ani ga imasu.","german":"I have an older brother.","furigana":"あに"},{"japanese":"部屋に猫がいます。","romaji":"Heya ni neko ga imasu.","german":"There is a cat in the room.","furigana":"へや|ねこ"}],"notes":"いる refers to the existence or presence of people and animals; for things, ある is used. Do not confuse it with similarly pronounced verbs like 要る (\"to need\"). In statements of location, the place takes に and the living being takes が: 部屋に猫がいる.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本語が分かります。","romaji":"Nihongo ga wakarimasu.","german":"I understand Japanese.","furigana":"にほんご|わ"},{"japanese":"この問題が分かりません。","romaji":"Kono mondai ga wakarimasen.","german":"I don't understand this problem.","furigana":"もんだい|わ"}],"notes":"With 分かる, the thing understood normally takes が: 日本語が分かる. Despite its -る ending, the verb is a godan verb: 分かります, 分かって. 分かりました can also mean \"Understood!\" as a reply.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ここで待ってください。","romaji":"Koko de matte kudasai.","german":"Please wait here.","furigana":"ま"},{"japanese":"バスを待っています。","romaji":"Basu o matte imasu.","german":"I'm waiting for the bus.","furigana":"ま"}],"notes":"What or whom you wait for takes を: バスを待つ. A waiting place is marked with で. The て-form is 待って; ～待ってください is a request, and ～待っています describes ongoing waiting.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"立ってください。","romaji":"Tatte kudasai.","german":"Please stand up.","furigana":"た"},{"japanese":"みんなの前に立ちます。","romaji":"Minna no mae ni tachimasu.","german":"I stand in front of everyone.","furigana":"まえ|た"}],"notes":"立つ means \"to stand up\" or \"to stand\". For an existing position, 立っています is often used; the spot takes に. The て-form is 立って. For \"to set up someone or something\", the transitive 立てる is used.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 6"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"椅子に座ってください。","romaji":"Isu ni suwatte kudasai.","german":"Please sit on the chair.","furigana":"いす|すわ"},{"japanese":"父は窓のそばに座っています。","romaji":"Chichi wa mado no soba ni suwatte imasu.","german":"My father is sitting by the window.","furigana":"ちち|まど|すわ"}],"notes":"座る means \"to sit down\"; 座っています describes sitting as a state. The seat or place to sit takes に: 椅子に座る. Despite -る, it is a godan verb, hence 座って and 座ります.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 6"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"十一時に寝ます。","romaji":"Jūichiji ni nemasu.","german":"I go to bed at eleven o'clock.","furigana":"じゅういちじ|ね"},{"japanese":"赤ちゃんはもう寝ています。","romaji":"Akachan wa mō nete imasu.","german":"The baby is already asleep.","furigana":"あか|ね"}],"notes":"寝る can mean \"to sleep\" or \"to go to bed\". A clock time with に often indicates bedtime: 十一時に寝る. 寝ています means \"is sleeping\" in the right context. The ichidan forms are 寝ます and 寝て.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"毎朝六時に起きます。","romaji":"Maiasa rokuji ni okimasu.","german":"I get up at six every morning.","furigana":"まいあさろくじ|お"},{"japanese":"赤ちゃんはまだ起きています。","romaji":"Akachan wa mada okite imasu.","german":"The baby is still awake.","furigana":"あか|お"}],"notes":"起きる refers to waking up or getting up. 起きています can mean \"to be awake\", while 立っています describes physically standing. The clock time takes に. This ichidan verb forms 起きます and 起きて.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"銀行で働いています。","romaji":"Ginkō de hataraite imasu.","german":"I work at a bank.","furigana":"ぎんこう|はたら"},{"japanese":"一日に八時間働きます。","romaji":"Ichinichi ni hachijikan hatarakimasu.","german":"I work eight hours a day.","furigana":"いちにち|はちじかんはたら"}],"notes":"働く here means to do a job. The workplace is attached with で: 銀行で働く. Regular professional activity is often described with 働いています. A work duration can be given with ～時間.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 11"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"疲れたので、少し休みます。","romaji":"Tsukareta node, sukoshi yasumimasu.","german":"I'm tired, so I'll rest for a bit.","furigana":"つか|すこ|やす"},{"japanese":"月曜日は学校を休みます。","romaji":"Getsuyōbi wa gakkō o yasumimasu.","german":"I'll be absent from school on Monday.","furigana":"げつようび|がっこう|やす"}],"notes":"休む can express a rest break or staying away from work or class. What is missed takes を: 学校を休む. Therefore 学校を休みます means \"I don't go to school\", not \"I rest at school\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 6"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"英語を教えています。","romaji":"Eigo o oshiete imasu.","german":"I teach English.","furigana":"えいご|おし"},{"japanese":"駅への道を教えてください。","romaji":"Eki e no michi o oshiete kudasai.","german":"Please tell me the way to the station.","furigana":"えき|みち|おし"}],"notes":"教える means not only \"to teach\" but also \"to tell or show someone something\". The person addressed takes に, and the content conveyed takes を: 人に英語を教える. 道を教えてください is a request for directions.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 6"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ピアノを習っています。","romaji":"Piano o naratte imasu.","german":"I'm taking piano lessons.","furigana":"なら"},{"japanese":"先生に漢字の書き方を習いました。","romaji":"Sensei ni kanji no kakikata o naraimashita.","german":"I learned how to write kanji from a teacher.","furigana":"せんせい|かんじ|か|かた|なら"}],"notes":"習う emphasizes learning through lessons or instruction, for example in music or a language. The subject learned takes を, and the teacher takes に or から. For general learning or studying on your own, 勉強する is often used.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 11"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"パソコンを使います。","romaji":"Pasokon o tsukaimasu.","german":"I use a computer.","furigana":"つか"},{"japanese":"旅行でたくさんお金を使いました。","romaji":"Ryokō de takusan okane o tsukaimashita.","german":"I spent a lot of money on the trip.","furigana":"りょこう|かね|つか"}],"notes":"使う refers to using a tool or means. With money, お金を使う means \"to spend money\", and with time, 時間を使う means \"to spend time\". What is used or consumed takes を; the て-form is 使って.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 6"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ケーキを作ります。","romaji":"Kēki o tsukurimasu.","german":"I make a cake.","furigana":"つく"},{"japanese":"紙で小さな箱を作りました。","romaji":"Kami de chīsana hako o tsukurimashita.","german":"I made a small box out of paper.","furigana":"かみ|ちい|はこ|つく"},{"japanese":"この会社は船を造っています。","romaji":"Kono kaisha wa fune o tsukutte imasu.","german":"This company builds ships.","furigana":"かいしゃ|ふね|つく"}],"notes":"作る emphasizes that something comes into being or is prepared, such as an object or food. The result takes を, the material takes で: 紙で箱を作る. The general する, by contrast, denotes an activity without necessarily producing something new. 造る is often used for building larger things or for industrial production, as in 船を造る (\"to build ships\"). The reading stays つくる.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 8"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"部屋に入ります。","romaji":"Heya ni hairimasu.","german":"I enter the room.","furigana":"へや|はい"},{"japanese":"私は学校のテニスクラブに入りました。","romaji":"Watashi wa gakkō no tenisu kurabu ni hairimashita.","german":"I joined the school tennis club.","furigana":"わたし|がっこう|はい"}],"notes":"入る means \"to go in\" and also \"to join a group\". The place entered or the group takes に: 部屋に入る, クラブに入る. Although the word ends in -いる, it is a godan verb: 入ります, 入って. For \"to put something in\", 入れる is used.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 6"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"家を出ます。","romaji":"Ie o demasu.","german":"I leave the house.","furigana":"いえ|で"},{"japanese":"今日は日本語の授業に出ます。","romaji":"Kyō wa nihongo no jugyō ni demasu.","german":"I'm attending Japanese class today.","furigana":"きょう|にほんご|じゅぎょう|で"}],"notes":"When leaving a place, 出る is used with を: 家を出る. It can also mean \"to attend/be present\"; then an event or class takes に: 授業に出る. This ichidan verb forms 出ます and 出て.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 9"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"買い物に出かけます。","romaji":"Kaimono ni dekakemasu.","german":"I'm going out shopping.","furigana":"か|もの|で"},{"japanese":"友達と出かけます。","romaji":"Tomodachi to dekakemasu.","german":"I'm going out with friends.","furigana":"ともだち|で"}],"notes":"出かける means to set off from home or one's previous location to do something. The purpose can take に: 買い物に出かける; a companion takes と. 出掛ける is another spelling of the same word.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"公園を歩きます。","romaji":"Kōen o arukimasu.","german":"I walk in the park.","furigana":"こうえん|ある"},{"japanese":"駅まで歩きます。","romaji":"Eki made arukimasu.","german":"I walk to the station.","furigana":"えき|ある"}],"notes":"歩く explicitly means \"to go on foot\", as opposed to running, 走る. A route traversed can take を: 公園を歩く. The destination or end of the route can be given with まで: 駅まで歩く.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 20"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"毎朝走ります。","romaji":"Maiasa hashirimasu.","german":"I run every morning.","furigana":"まいあさはし"},{"japanese":"時間がないので、駅まで走りました。","romaji":"Jikan ga nai node, eki made hashirimashita.","german":"I had no time, so I ran to the station.","furigana":"じかん|えき|はし"}],"notes":"走る here means \"to run/jog\", while 歩く refers to walking on foot. Despite -る, 走る is a godan verb: 走ります, 走って. With まで the endpoint of the run can be named, for example 駅まで走る.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 22"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"プールで泳ぎます。","romaji":"Pūru de oyogimasu.","german":"I swim in the pool.","furigana":"およ"},{"japanese":"魚が川を泳いでいます。","romaji":"Sakana ga kawa o oyoide imasu.","german":"Fish are swimming in the river.","furigana":"さかな|かわ|およ"}],"notes":"泳ぐ describes swimming by people and animals. The place of a swimming activity takes で, and a space swum through can take を. For this godan verb ending in -ぐ, the て-form is 泳いで, not 泳いて.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"友達と遊びます。","romaji":"Tomodachi to asobimasu.","german":"I hang out with friends.","furigana":"ともだち|あそ"},{"japanese":"子供たちが公園で遊んでいます。","romaji":"Kodomotachi ga kōen de asonde imasu.","german":"The children are playing in the park.","furigana":"こども|こうえん|あそ"}],"notes":"遊ぶ can mean children's play or spending free time together. 友達と遊ぶ can also mean \"to spend time with friends\", depending on the situation, without naming a particular game. For a specific sport or instrument, use more fitting verbs, such as テニスをする and ピアノを弾く.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 6"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"カラオケで歌います。","romaji":"Karaoke de utaimasu.","german":"I sing at karaoke.","furigana":"うた"},{"japanese":"この歌を歌ってください。","romaji":"Kono uta o utatte kudasai.","german":"Please sing this song.","furigana":"うた|うた"}],"notes":"歌う means \"to sing\". The song takes を, and the place takes, for example, で: 歌を歌う, カラオケで歌う. This godan verb forms the te-form 歌って and the negative 歌わない.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 7"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"写真を撮ります。","romaji":"Shashin o torimasu.","german":"I take a photo.","furigana":"しゃしん|と"},{"japanese":"富士山を撮りました。","romaji":"Fujisan o torimashita.","german":"I took a photo of Mt. Fuji.","furigana":"ふじさん|と"},{"japanese":"ここでは写真を撮らないでください。","romaji":"Koko de wa shashin o toranaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't take photos here.","furigana":"しゃしん|と"}],"notes":"写真を撮る is the usual combination for \"to take a photo\"; the person or thing photographed can also take を. 撮る here refers to taking pictures and should be distinguished from 取る (\"to take\") and 捕る (\"to catch\"). The te-form is 撮って.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"窓を開けてください。","romaji":"Mado o akete kudasai.","german":"Please open the window.","furigana":"まど|あ"},{"japanese":"ドアを開けてもいいですか。","romaji":"Doa o akete mo ii desu ka.","german":"May I open the door?","furigana":"あ"},{"japanese":"朝九時に店を開けます。","romaji":"Asa kuji ni mise o akemasu.","german":"I open the shop at nine in the morning.","furigana":"あさくじ|みせ|あ"}],"notes":"開ける is transitive: someone opens something, such as 窓を開ける. The matching intransitive verb is 開く（あく）: 窓が開く \"the window opens\". 開ける can also refer to opening a business; as an ichidan verb it forms 開けて and 開けない.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 6"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ドアを閉めてください。","romaji":"Doa o shimete kudasai.","german":"Please close the door.","furigana":"し"},{"japanese":"窓を閉めてください。","romaji":"Mado o shimete kudasai.","german":"Please close the window.","furigana":"まど|し"},{"japanese":"夜八時に店を閉めます。","romaji":"Yoru hachiji ni mise o shimemasu.","german":"I close the shop at eight in the evening.","furigana":"よるはちじ|みせ|し"}],"notes":"閉める means \"to close something\" and takes its object with を: ドアを閉める. 閉まる, by contrast, describes something closing or being shut: ドアが閉まる. This ichidan verb forms 閉めて; the identically pronounced 締める means, among other things, \"to tighten\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 6"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"電気をつけてください。","romaji":"Denki o tsukete kudasai.","german":"Please turn on the light.","furigana":"でんき"},{"japanese":"テレビをつけてもいいですか。","romaji":"Terebi o tsukete mo ii desu ka.","german":"May I turn on the TV?"},{"japanese":"暗いので、電気をつけます。","romaji":"Kurai node, denki o tsukemasu.","german":"It's dark, so I'll turn on the light.","furigana":"くら|でんき"}],"notes":"Here つける means \"to turn on a device or light\" and is also written 点ける or 付ける. Depending on context, つける can also mean \"to attach\" or \"to wear\"; the identically pronounced 浸ける means \"to soak\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 6"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"電気を消してください。","romaji":"Denki o keshite kudasai.","german":"Please turn off the light.","furigana":"でんき|け"},{"japanese":"テレビを消してください。","romaji":"Terebi o keshite kudasai.","german":"Please turn off the TV.","furigana":"け"},{"japanese":"間違えた字を消しました。","romaji":"Machigaeta ji o keshimashita.","german":"I erased the wrongly written character.","furigana":"まちが|じ|け"}],"notes":"消す is used both for switching off lights or devices and for erasing writing: 電気を消す, 字を消す. It is transitive; 消える, by contrast, describes going out or disappearing. This godan verb forms 消して and 消さない.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 6"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"傘を持っていますか。","romaji":"Kasa o motte imasu ka.","german":"Do you have an umbrella?","furigana":"かさ|も"},{"japanese":"重い荷物を持ちます。","romaji":"Omoi nimotsu o mochimasu.","german":"I carry heavy luggage.","furigana":"おも|にもつ|も"}],"notes":"持つ can mean \"to hold in the hand\", \"to carry\" or \"to own\"; the thing takes を. 持っています often describes an ongoing state, such as having an umbrella. This godan verb forms 持って; \"to take along\" is often 持っていく.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 6"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"東京に住んでいます。","romaji":"Tōkyō ni sunde imasu.","german":"I live in Tokyo.","furigana":"とうきょう|す"},{"japanese":"大阪に住みたいです。","romaji":"Ōsaka ni sumitai desu.","german":"I want to live in Osaka.","furigana":"おおさか|す"},{"japanese":"この家には誰も住んでいません。","romaji":"Kono ie ni wa dare mo sunde imasen.","german":"Nobody lives in this house.","furigana":"いえ|だれ|す"}],"notes":"With 住む, the place of residence takes に: 東京に住んでいます. For a current residence, 住んでいます is usual; 住みたい expresses the wish to live somewhere. 住む is intransitive and, as a godan verb, forms 住んで.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 7"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この人を知っていますか。","romaji":"Kono hito o shitte imasu ka.","german":"Do you know this person?","furigana":"ひと|し"},{"japanese":"あの店を知っていますか。","romaji":"Ano mise o shitte imasu ka.","german":"Do you know that shop?","furigana":"みせ|し"},{"japanese":"昨日、初めてそのニュースを知りました。","romaji":"Kinō, hajimete sono nyūsu o shirimashita.","german":"Yesterday I learned about that news for the first time.","furigana":"きのう|はじ|し"}],"notes":"\"I know it\" or \"I know ...\" is usually 知っています. The usual negative is 知りません. 知る can also denote the moment of finding out, as in ニュースを知る \"to learn of a piece of news\"; it is a transitive godan verb with the te-form 知って.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 7"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いいと思います。","romaji":"Ii to omoimasu.","german":"I think it's good.","furigana":"おも"},{"japanese":"明日は雨だと思います。","romaji":"Ashita wa ame da to omoimasu.","german":"I think it will rain tomorrow.","furigana":"あした|あめ|おも"}],"notes":"Before 思う comes a statement in plain form with と: 雨だと思います \"I think it will rain\". With nouns and na-adjectives, だ is included; with i-adjectives there is no だ. 思います here marks a personal assessment, not an established fact.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 8"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"友達に会います。","romaji":"Tomodachi ni aimasu.","german":"I meet a friend.","furigana":"ともだち|あ"},{"japanese":"昨日先生に会いました。","romaji":"Kinō sensei ni aimashita.","german":"Yesterday I met my teacher.","furigana":"きのうせんせい|あ"},{"japanese":"明日、駅で会いましょう。","romaji":"Ashita, eki de aimashō.","german":"Let's meet at the station tomorrow.","furigana":"あした|えき|あ"}],"notes":"The person you meet often takes に: 友達に会う. と can emphasize an arranged or joint meeting. 会う is intransitive; its te-form is 会って. For meeting someone for the first time, 初めて会う is a natural combination.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"電車に乗ります。","romaji":"Densha ni norimasu.","german":"I get on the train.","furigana":"でんしゃ|の"},{"japanese":"バスに乗りましょう。","romaji":"Basu ni norimashō.","german":"Let's take the bus.","furigana":"の"},{"japanese":"毎日、自転車に乗って学校へ行きます。","romaji":"Mainichi, jitensha ni notte gakkō e ikimasu.","german":"Every day I ride my bicycle to school.","furigana":"まいにち|じてんしゃ|の|がっこう|い"}],"notes":"With 乗る, the means of transport takes に: 電車に乗る, バスに乗る. The verb can refer both to boarding and to the ride itself. It is an intransitive godan verb and forms 乗って; the opposite, for getting off, is 降りる.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"次の駅で降ります。","romaji":"Tsugi no eki de orimasu.","german":"I get off at the next station.","furigana":"つぎ|えき|お"},{"japanese":"ここで降りましょう。","romaji":"Koko de orimashō.","german":"Let's get off here.","furigana":"お"},{"japanese":"バスを降りてから、歩きます。","romaji":"Basu o orite kara, arukimasu.","german":"After getting off the bus, I walk.","furigana":"お|ある"}],"notes":"When getting off, the vehicle often takes を and the place of getting off takes で: バスを降りる, 駅で降りる. 降りる is nonetheless intransitive and belongs to the ichidan group; the te-form is 降りて. The reading おりる must not be confused with 降る（ふる）\"to fall, to rain\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 6"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"右に曲がってください。","romaji":"Migi ni magatte kudasai.","german":"Please turn right.","furigana":"みぎ|ま"},{"japanese":"次の角を左に曲がります。","romaji":"Tsugi no kado o hidari ni magarimasu.","german":"I turn left at the next corner.","furigana":"つぎ|かど|ひだり|ま"},{"japanese":"この道は川の近くで曲がっています。","romaji":"Kono michi wa kawa no chikaku de magatte imasu.","german":"This road bends near the river.","furigana":"みち|かわ|ちか|ま"}],"notes":"When turning, に indicates the direction: 右に曲がる, 左に曲がる. The corner passed through can take を, even though 曲がる is intransitive. The verb also means \"to bend, to be curved\"; for actively bending an object, 曲げる is used. The te-form is 曲がって. The shortened dictionary spelling 曲る has the same reading まがる; in your own writing, 曲がる is the usual spelling.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 20"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"橋を渡ります。","romaji":"Hashi o watarimasu.","german":"I cross the bridge.","furigana":"はし|わた"},{"japanese":"道を渡ってください。","romaji":"Michi o watatte kudasai.","german":"Please cross the street.","furigana":"みち|わた"},{"japanese":"船で川を渡りました。","romaji":"Fune de kawa o watarimashita.","german":"I crossed the river by boat.","furigana":"ふね|かわ|わた"}],"notes":"With 渡る, the road, river or bridge crossed takes を: 橋を渡る, 川を渡る. This を marks the space traversed; 渡る remains intransitive. For the means of transport, で is usual, such as 船で. This godan verb forms 渡って.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この家は大きいです。","romaji":"Kono ie wa ōkii desu.","german":"This house is big.","furigana":"いえ|おお"},{"japanese":"この犬は大きいです。","romaji":"Kono inu wa ōkii desu.","german":"This dog is big.","furigana":"いぬ|おお"},{"japanese":"このかばんは大きいので、たくさん入ります。","romaji":"Kono kaban wa ōkii node, takusan hairimasu.","german":"This bag is big, so a lot fits in it.","furigana":"おお|はい"}],"notes":"大きい is an i-adjective: 大きい家, 家が大きい. Here it describes physical size; for a person's height, 背が高い is usual. 大きな also stands before nouns, but is not used as a sentence predicate with です. The negative is 大きくない.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 8"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"小さい猫がいます。","romaji":"Chīsai neko ga imasu.","german":"There is a small cat.","furigana":"ちい|ねこ"},{"japanese":"小さい子供が泣いています。","romaji":"Chīsai kodomo ga naite imasu.","german":"A small child is crying.","furigana":"ちい|こども|な"}],"notes":"小さい is an i-adjective and describes small size: 小さい猫. For children it can also mean \"still young, little\". 小さな is an alternative form directly before a noun; as a sentence predicate, use 小さいです. The negative is 小さくない.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 8"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい車を買いました。","romaji":"Atarashii kuruma o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a new car.","furigana":"あたら|くるま|か"},{"japanese":"新しい靴を買いたいです。","romaji":"Atarashii kutsu o kaitai desu.","german":"I want to buy new shoes.","furigana":"あたら|くつ|か"},{"japanese":"新しい先生は、今日から来ます。","romaji":"Atarashii sensei wa, kyō kara kimasu.","german":"The new teacher starts today.","furigana":"あたら|せんせい|きょう|き"}],"notes":"新しい can mean \"newly made or bought\" as well as \"newly added\": 新しい車, 新しい先生. It is an i-adjective and stands directly before the noun. The past form is 新しかった; the opposite for things is 古い.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 8"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この建物は古いです。","romaji":"Kono tatemono wa furui desu.","german":"This building is old.","furigana":"たてもの|ふる"},{"japanese":"古い写真を見ました。","romaji":"Furui shashin o mimashita.","german":"I looked at old photos.","furigana":"ふる|しゃしん|み"}],"notes":"古い describes old things or something from long ago: 古い建物, 古い写真. For a person's old age, you normally use 年を取っている rather than 古い. This i-adjective forms 古くない and 古かった; the opposite for new things is 新しい.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 8"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この山は高いです。","romaji":"Kono yama wa takai desu.","german":"This mountain is high.","furigana":"やま|たか"},{"japanese":"この服は高いです。","romaji":"Kono fuku wa takai desu.","german":"These clothes are expensive.","furigana":"ふく|たか"}],"notes":"高い means both height and a high price. 山が高い means \"the mountain is high\", 服が高い \"the clothes are expensive\"; 背が高い means \"tall in stature\". Depending on the meaning, the opposites are 低い \"low\" and 安い \"cheap\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 8"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このレストランは安いです。","romaji":"Kono resutoran wa yasui desu.","german":"This restaurant is cheap.","furigana":"やす"},{"japanese":"安い店を知っていますか。","romaji":"Yasui mise o shitte imasu ka.","german":"Do you know a cheap shop?","furigana":"やす|みせ|し"},{"japanese":"このかばんは千円で、安かったです。","romaji":"Kono kaban wa sen'en de, yasukatta desu.","german":"This bag cost a thousand yen and was cheap.","furigana":"せんえん|やす"}],"notes":"安い here means \"inexpensive, good value\" and refers to the price, not automatically to poor quality. 安い店 is a shop with low prices. This i-adjective forms 安かった and 安くない. For \"easy to do\", on the other hand, the verb suffix ～やすい is used.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 8"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この道は長いです。","romaji":"Kono michi wa nagai desu.","german":"This road is long.","furigana":"みち|なが"},{"japanese":"この映画は長いです。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa nagai desu.","german":"This movie is long.","furigana":"えいが|なが"}],"notes":"長い describes spatial length and duration in time: 長い道, 長い映画. It is an i-adjective and forms 長くない and 長かった. For the height of a mountain or person, 高い is used; the opposite of 長い is 短い. 永い is another spelling for length of time. For spatial length, such as a long road, 長い is used.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 16"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"髪が短いです。","romaji":"Kami ga mijikai desu.","german":"My hair is short.","furigana":"かみ|みじか"},{"japanese":"短い鉛筆を使っています。","romaji":"Mijikai enpitsu o tsukatte imasu.","german":"I'm using a short pencil.","furigana":"みじか|えんぴつ|つか"},{"japanese":"昼休みは短いです。","romaji":"Hiruyasumi wa mijikai desu.","german":"The lunch break is short.","furigana":"ひるやす|みじか"}],"notes":"短い means \"short\" in terms of length or duration: 短い髪, 短い休み. It does not refer to small size in general; 小さい is used for that. This i-adjective forms 短くない and 短かった; its opposite is 長い.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 16"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"天気が良いです。","romaji":"Tenki ga yoi desu.","german":"The weather is good.","furigana":"てんき|よ"},{"japanese":"この本は良いです。","romaji":"Kono hon wa yoi desu.","german":"This book is good.","furigana":"ほん|よ"}],"notes":"良い（よい）is the slightly more literary form of いい. When conjugating, the stem よ is also used for いい: よくない \"not good\", よかった \"was good\". Here the word evaluates weather or a book positively; it is an i-adjective.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"天気が悪いです。","romaji":"Tenki ga warui desu.","german":"The weather is bad.","furigana":"てんき|わる"},{"japanese":"成績が悪いです。","romaji":"Seiseki ga warui desu.","german":"The grades are bad.","furigana":"せいせき|わる"}],"notes":"悪い is the general opposite of 良い: 天気が悪い \"bad weather\", 成績が悪い \"poor performance\". With things and results, it usually denotes an unfavorable state or low quality. This i-adjective forms 悪くない and 悪かった.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 8"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日は暑いです。","romaji":"Kyō wa atsui desu.","german":"It's hot today.","furigana":"きょう|あつ"},{"japanese":"暑いので、窓を開けます。","romaji":"Atsui node, mado o akemasu.","german":"It's hot, so I'll open the window.","furigana":"あつ|まど|あ"}],"notes":"暑い describes heat in the surroundings, especially in weather. For a hot object or hot drink, 熱い is written instead; both words are read あつい. The negative is 暑くない, the past 暑かった.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"冬は寒いです。","romaji":"Fuyu wa samui desu.","german":"It's cold in winter.","furigana":"ふゆ|さむ"},{"japanese":"寒いので、コートを着ます。","romaji":"Samui node, kōto o kimasu.","german":"It's cold, so I'll wear a coat.","furigana":"さむ|き"}],"notes":"寒い describes cold weather, a cold environment or the feeling of being cold. 冷たい, by contrast, is used for something cold to the touch, such as water. As an い-adjective, 寒い forms the negative 寒くない and the past 寒かった.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 8"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日は暖かいです。","romaji":"Kyō wa atatakai desu.","german":"It's warm today.","furigana":"きょう|あたた"},{"japanese":"暖かい服を持ってきてください。","romaji":"Atatakai fuku o motte kite kudasai.","german":"Please bring warm clothes.","furigana":"あたた|ふく|も"}],"notes":"暖かい is used especially for pleasantly warm air and for clothing that protects against the cold. 温かい often refers to the tangible warmth of food or drinks; both are read あたたかい. Before a noun the い is kept: 暖かい服.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 10"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"秋は涼しいです。","romaji":"Aki wa suzushii desu.","german":"It's cool in autumn.","furigana":"あき|すず"},{"japanese":"涼しい部屋で勉強したいです。","romaji":"Suzushii heya de benkyō shitai desu.","german":"I want to study in a cool room.","furigana":"すず|へや|べんきょう"}],"notes":"涼しい usually means pleasant, refreshing coolness in the air or weather. It is therefore more positive than 寒い (\"cold/chilly\"). The form 涼しく can be used adverbially; before a noun, say 涼しい部屋.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 12"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このケーキはおいしいです。","romaji":"Kono kēki wa oishii desu.","german":"This cake is delicious."},{"japanese":"おいしかったので、もう一つ食べました。","romaji":"Oishikatta node, mō hitotsu tabemashita.","german":"It was so delicious that I ate another one.","furigana":"ひと|た"}],"notes":"おいしい evaluates the taste of food and drinks positively. The spelling 美味しい is also common. For the past, い is replaced by かった: おいしかった. Figurative uses for attractive offers are not meant here.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 8"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この料理はまずいです。","romaji":"Kono ryōri wa mazui desu.","german":"This dish tastes bad.","furigana":"りょうり"},{"japanese":"このコーヒーはまずくて、飲めません。","romaji":"Kono kōhī wa mazukute, nomemasen.","german":"This coffee tastes so bad that I can't drink it.","furigana":"の"}],"notes":"With food, まずい means \"bad-tasting\" and comes across as a clearly direct judgment. In polite conversation, あまりおいしくない (\"not particularly tasty\") is often more reserved. The connecting form is まずくて. This card deals with taste, not the meaning \"problematic\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 43"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"旅行は楽しいです。","romaji":"Ryokō wa tanoshii desu.","german":"Traveling is fun.","furigana":"りょこう|たの"},{"japanese":"昨日のパーティーはとても楽しかったです。","romaji":"Kinō no pātī wa totemo tanoshikatta desu.","german":"The party yesterday was a lot of fun.","furigana":"きのう|たの"}],"notes":"楽しい describes something that gives pleasure, such as an activity or time spent together. It is an い-adjective: 楽しかった (\"was fun\") and 楽しく (\"in an enjoyable way\"). Do not equate it with 楽な (\"effortless/comfortable\").","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 8"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この映画は面白いです。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa omoshiroi desu.","german":"This movie is interesting.","furigana":"えいが|おもしろ"},{"japanese":"彼の話は面白くて、みんな笑いました。","romaji":"Kare no hanashi wa omoshirokute, minna waraimashita.","german":"His story was funny, and everyone laughed.","furigana":"かれ|はなし|おもしろ|わら"}],"notes":"面白い can mean \"interesting\" or \"funny\". With a movie or book, context decides whether interest or humor is emphasized. The connecting form is 面白くて; the い-adjective can also stand directly before a noun: 面白い話.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本語は難しいです。","romaji":"Nihongo wa muzukashii desu.","german":"Japanese is difficult.","furigana":"にほんご|むずか"},{"japanese":"この問題は難しくて、答えが分かりません。","romaji":"Kono mondai wa muzukashikute, kotae ga wakarimasen.","german":"This problem is so difficult that I don't know the answer.","furigana":"もんだい|むずか|こた|わ"}],"notes":"難しい describes something that is hard to understand or to cope with. For great weight, 重い is used instead. The form 難しくて connects the difficulty with a further statement; the negative is 難しくない.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 8"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この問題は易しいです。","romaji":"Kono mondai wa yasashii desu.","german":"This problem is easy.","furigana":"もんだい|やさ"},{"japanese":"この本は易しい言葉で書かれています。","romaji":"Kono hon wa yasashii kotoba de kakarete imasu.","german":"This book is written in simple words.","furigana":"ほん|やさ|ことば|か"}],"notes":"易しい means \"easy/simple\" and is often written やさしい. Do not confuse it with 優しい (\"kind, gentle\"). This い-adjective can evaluate a task or stand before a noun: 易しい言葉 (\"simple words\").","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 8"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は優しい人です。","romaji":"Kanojo wa yasashii hito desu.","german":"She is a kind person.","furigana":"かのじょ|やさ|ひと"},{"japanese":"先生は優しく説明してくれました。","romaji":"Sensei wa yasashiku setsumei shite kuremashita.","german":"The teacher explained it to me kindly.","furigana":"せんせい|やさ|せつめい"}],"notes":"優しい describes kindness, gentleness or caring behavior. For \"easy\", the identically pronounced 易しい is written instead. The form 優しく describes how someone acts: 優しく説明する (\"to explain kindly\").","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 28"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日はとても忙しいです。","romaji":"Kyō wa totemo isogashii desu.","german":"I'm very busy today.","furigana":"きょう|いそが"},{"japanese":"忙しくて、昼ご飯を食べる時間がありません。","romaji":"Isogashikute, hirugohan o taberu jikan ga arimasen.","german":"I'm so busy that I have no time to eat lunch.","furigana":"いそが|ひる|はん|た|じかん"}],"notes":"忙しい means that many tasks or appointments take up one's time. It describes being busy, not merely moving around. The connecting form is 忙しくて; for the negative, say 忙しくない. A time period can be emphasized with は.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 8"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"駅は近いです。","romaji":"Eki wa chikai desu.","german":"The station is close.","furigana":"えき|ちか"},{"japanese":"夏休みが近いです。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi ga chikai desu.","german":"The summer vacation is near.","furigana":"なつやす|ちか"}],"notes":"近い can express spatial or temporal closeness. 駅が近い means \"the station is close\", 夏休みが近い \"the summer vacation is coming up\". As a place noun, 近く is used, for example 駅の近く (\"near the station\").","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 12"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"空港は遠いです。","romaji":"Kūkō wa tōi desu.","german":"The airport is far.","furigana":"くうこう|とお"},{"japanese":"学校が遠いので、バスで行きます。","romaji":"Gakkō ga tōi node, basu de ikimasu.","german":"The school is far away, so I go by bus.","furigana":"がっこう|とお|い"}],"notes":"遠い here denotes a large spatial distance. A reference point can be named with から: 家から遠い (\"far from home\"). For a large or spacious room, 広い is used instead. The reading とおい contains a long o and is romanized tōi.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 12"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この部屋は広いです。","romaji":"Kono heya wa hiroi desu.","german":"This room is spacious.","furigana":"へや|ひろ"},{"japanese":"広い公園で子供たちが遊んでいます。","romaji":"Hiroi kōen de kodomotachi ga asonde imasu.","german":"Children are playing in a big park.","furigana":"ひろ|こうえん|こども|あそ"}],"notes":"広い refers to a large area or lots of available space: a spacious room or an expansive park. It does not mean \"far away\"; 遠い is used for that. Before a noun the い is kept: 広い公園.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 13"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この道は狭いです。","romaji":"Kono michi wa semai desu.","german":"This road is narrow.","furigana":"みち|せま"},{"japanese":"この部屋は狭いです。","romaji":"Kono heya wa semai desu.","german":"This room is small.","furigana":"へや|せま"}],"notes":"狭い describes little width or little available space. With a road it means \"narrow\", with a room \"cramped/small\". It is the counterpart of 広い. The negative is 狭くない, the past 狭かった.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 13"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この部屋は明るいです。","romaji":"Kono heya wa akarui desu.","german":"This room is bright.","furigana":"へや|あか"},{"japanese":"朝の空は明るいです。","romaji":"Asa no sora wa akarui desu.","german":"The morning sky is bright.","furigana":"あさ|そら|あか"},{"japanese":"姉は明るい性格で、友達が多いです。","romaji":"Ane wa akarui seikaku de, tomodachi ga ōi desu.","german":"My older sister has a cheerful personality and has many friends.","furigana":"あね|あか|せいかく|ともだち|おお"}],"notes":"As an i-adjective, 明るい stands directly before a noun: 明るい部屋 (a bright room). It also describes a cheerful person or good prospects for the future. The negative is 明るくない. With a field of expertise, ～に明るい means \"to be well versed in something\". 明い is recognized as a search variant; for your own writing, use 明るい.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 16"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"夜は暗いです。","romaji":"Yoru wa kurai desu.","german":"It's dark at night.","furigana":"よる|くら"},{"japanese":"部屋が暗いので、電気をつけます。","romaji":"Heya ga kurai node, denki o tsukemasu.","german":"The room is dark, so I'll turn on the light.","furigana":"へや|くら|でんき"}],"notes":"暗い here describes low brightness in a room or outdoors. The negative 暗くない means \"not dark\"; the opposite is 明るい. A color can also be dark, but this card focuses on lighting conditions.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 16"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この荷物は重いです。","romaji":"Kono nimotsu wa omoi desu.","german":"This luggage is heavy.","furigana":"にもつ|おも"},{"japanese":"この箱は重くて、一人では持てません。","romaji":"Kono hako wa omokute, hitori de wa motemasen.","german":"This box is so heavy that I can't carry it alone.","furigana":"はこ|おも|ひとり|も"}],"notes":"重い here means \"heavy\" in terms of weight. For a difficult task, 難しい is used, not automatically 重い. The form 重くて can link the weight with a consequence; 軽い is the opposite.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 12"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このかばんは軽いです。","romaji":"Kono kaban wa karui desu.","german":"This bag is light.","furigana":"かる"},{"japanese":"旅行には軽いかばんを持っていきます。","romaji":"Ryokō ni wa karui kaban o motte ikimasu.","german":"I take a light bag on trips.","furigana":"りょこう|かる|も"}],"notes":"軽い here means \"light\" in the sense of low weight. For a low level of difficulty, 易しい is used. The opposite of 軽い is 重い. The adjective stands before a noun without な: 軽いかばん.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 12"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人が多いです。","romaji":"Hito ga ōi desu.","german":"There are many people.","furigana":"ひと|おお"},{"japanese":"宿題が多くて、まだ終わりません。","romaji":"Shukudai ga ōkute, mada owarimasen.","german":"I have so much homework that I'm not done yet.","furigana":"しゅくだい|おお|お"}],"notes":"多い can describe a large number or a large quantity. The usual sentence form is 人が多い (\"there are many people\"). Before a noun, 多くの or たくさんの is usually used for \"many ...\", not simply 多い. おおい is written as ōi with a long o.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 12"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お金が少ないです。","romaji":"Okane ga sukunai desu.","german":"There is little money.","furigana":"かね|すく"},{"japanese":"今日は生徒が少ないので、教室が静かです。","romaji":"Kyō wa seito ga sukunai node, kyōshitsu ga shizuka desu.","german":"There are few students today, so the classroom is quiet.","furigana":"きょう|せいと|すく|きょうしつ|しず"}],"notes":"少ない evaluates a small number or amount, as in 人が少ない and お金が少ない. As an adjective it describes the quantity of something; 少し, by contrast, is a quantity adverb, for example 少し食べる (\"to eat a little\"). The connecting form is 少なくて.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 17"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"朝早く起きます。","romaji":"Asa hayaku okimasu.","german":"I get up early in the morning.","furigana":"あさはや|お"},{"japanese":"彼は返事が早いです。","romaji":"Kare wa henji ga hayai desu.","german":"He replies quickly.","furigana":"かれ|へんじ|はや"}],"notes":"早い refers to an early point in time or to quick completion or reaction. 速い is used mainly for high speed of movement or progress. Before a verb, 早い becomes 早く: 早く起きる (\"to get up early\").","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"もう遅いです。","romaji":"Mō osoi desu.","german":"It's already late.","furigana":"おそ"},{"japanese":"彼は歩くのが遅いです。","romaji":"Kare wa aruku no ga osoi desu.","german":"He walks slowly.","furigana":"かれ|ある|おそ"}],"notes":"遅い can describe a late point in time or a slow pace. もう遅いです means \"it's already late\" in the right context. 歩くのが遅い, by contrast, means \"to walk slowly\". For arriving late, 遅れる is often used.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 12"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は強い人です。","romaji":"Kare wa tsuyoi hito desu.","german":"He is a strong person.","furigana":"かれ|つよ|ひと"},{"japanese":"今日は風が強いです。","romaji":"Kyō wa kaze ga tsuyoi desu.","german":"The wind is strong today.","furigana":"きょう|かぜ|つよ"}],"notes":"強い can express physical strength or high intensity, as with a person or a strong wind. The opposite is 弱い. The form 強く is used adverbially; before a noun, say 強い人 without な.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 19"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"体が弱いので、よく休みます。","romaji":"Karada ga yowai node, yoku yasumimasu.","german":"I'm physically weak, so I often rest.","furigana":"からだ|よわ|やす"},{"japanese":"このチームは弱いです。","romaji":"Kono chīmu wa yowai desu.","german":"This team is weak.","furigana":"よわ"}],"notes":"弱い denotes little strength, little resilience or little competitive strength. 体が弱い describes a weak physical constitution, チームが弱い the low playing strength of a team. The opposite is 強い; the connecting form is 弱くて.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 19"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"白い花がきれいです。","romaji":"Shiroi hana ga kirei desu.","german":"The white flowers are pretty.","furigana":"しろ|はな"},{"japanese":"雪は白いです。","romaji":"Yuki wa shiroi desu.","german":"Snow is white.","furigana":"ゆき|しろ"},{"japanese":"学校へ行くとき、白いシャツを着ます。","romaji":"Gakkō e iku toki, shiroi shatsu o kimasu.","german":"When I go to school, I wear a white shirt.","furigana":"がっこう|い|しろ|き"}],"notes":"白い is the i-adjective \"white\" and stands directly before a noun: 白い花. The color name as a noun is 白（しろ）. For a piece of clothing, you can therefore say 白いシャツ or 白のシャツ; the negative of the adjective is 白くない.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 8"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"黒い猫がいます。","romaji":"Kuroi neko ga imasu.","german":"There is a black cat.","furigana":"くろ|ねこ"},{"japanese":"黒い服を着ています。","romaji":"Kuroi fuku o kite imasu.","german":"I'm wearing black clothes.","furigana":"くろ|ふく|き"}],"notes":"黒い is an i-adjective: 黒い猫 \"black cat\". The color name as a noun is 黒（くろ）, as in 黒の服. Here the color is being described; in other contexts, 黒い can also be used figuratively. The negative is 黒くない.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 8"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"赤い車が好きです。","romaji":"Akai kuruma ga suki desu.","german":"I like red cars.","furigana":"あか|くるま|す"},{"japanese":"赤いりんごをください。","romaji":"Akai ringo o kudasai.","german":"A red apple, please.","furigana":"あか"},{"japanese":"信号が赤いので、止まってください。","romaji":"Shingō ga akai node, tomatte kudasai.","german":"The light is red, so please stop.","furigana":"しんごう|あか|と"}],"notes":"赤い means \"red\" and is an i-adjective: 赤いりんご. 赤（あか）, by contrast, is the noun for the color. For traffic lights, red is also described with 赤; \"to turn red\" is 赤くなる. The negative is 赤くない.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 8"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"空は青いです。","romaji":"Sora wa aoi desu.","german":"The sky is blue.","furigana":"そら|あお"},{"japanese":"青い海がきれいです。","romaji":"Aoi umi ga kirei desu.","german":"The blue sea is beautiful.","furigana":"あお|うみ"},{"japanese":"この青いシャツをください。","romaji":"Kono aoi shatsu o kudasai.","german":"This blue shirt, please.","furigana":"あお"}],"notes":"青い is an i-adjective and usually means \"blue\", as in 青い空. In set contexts, 青 can also denote green, especially for traffic lights or unripe fruit. For the color green in general, 緑（みどり）is usual. The negative of 青い is 青くない.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 8"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このケーキは甘いです。","romaji":"Kono kēki wa amai desu.","german":"Dieser Kuchen ist süß.","furigana":"あま"},{"japanese":"みかんは甘いです。","romaji":"Mikan wa amai desu.","german":"Mandarinen sind süß.","furigana":"あま"},{"japanese":"甘いものはあまり食べません。","romaji":"Amai mono wa amari tabemasen.","german":"Ich esse nicht besonders viel Süßes.","furigana":"あま|た"}],"notes":"甘い beschreibt hier einen süßen Geschmack: 甘いケーキ. 甘いもの heißt „etwas Süßes, Süßigkeiten“. Es ist ein i-Adjektiv und bildet 甘くない. Für „niedlich“ verwendet man かわいい; das deutsche „süß“ wird in diesem Sinn nicht mit 甘い übersetzt.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 12"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このカレーは辛いです。","romaji":"Kono karē wa karai desu.","german":"This curry is spicy.","furigana":"から"},{"japanese":"このキムチは辛いです。","romaji":"Kono kimuchi wa karai desu.","german":"This kimchi is spicy.","furigana":"から"},{"japanese":"辛いものは好きではありません。","romaji":"Karai mono wa suki de wa arimasen.","german":"I don't like spicy food.","furigana":"から|す"}],"notes":"辛い（からい）here refers to spicy food. 辛いもの means spicy dishes. The same spelling also has the reading つらい, \"painful, hard to bear\"; that reading is not part of the taste meaning practiced here. The negative of からい is 辛くない（からくない）.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 12"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"月は丸いです。","romaji":"Tsuki wa marui desu.","german":"The moon is round.","furigana":"つき|まる"},{"japanese":"丸いボールがあります。","romaji":"Marui bōru ga arimasu.","german":"There is a round ball.","furigana":"まる"},{"japanese":"丸いテーブルでご飯を食べます。","romaji":"Marui tēburu de gohan o tabemasu.","german":"We eat at a round table.","furigana":"まる|はん|た"}],"notes":"丸い describes a round, circular or spherical shape. 丸い is especially common for spherical things; the alternative spelling 円い tends to emphasize a circular shape. As an i-adjective, 丸い stands directly before the noun and forms 丸くない. 円い is also listed as a spelling; the note on circular shape also applies when searching via this form.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 30"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お元気ですか。","romaji":"Ogenki desu ka.","german":"How are you?","furigana":"げんき"},{"japanese":"子供たちは元気に遊んでいます。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa genki ni asonde imasu.","german":"The children are playing energetically.","furigana":"こども|げんき|あそ"}],"notes":"元気 is a na-adjective: 元気な子供 \"lively child\", 元気に遊ぶ \"to play energetically\". お元気ですか politely asks how someone is doing. The word can refer to health and energy; it is not conjugated like an i-adjective. The negative is 元気ではありません.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 32"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本料理が好きです。","romaji":"Nihon ryōri ga suki desu.","german":"Ich mag japanisches Essen.","furigana":"にほんりょうり|す"},{"japanese":"音楽が好きです。","romaji":"Ongaku ga suki desu.","german":"Ich mag Musik.","furigana":"おんがく|す"},{"japanese":"好きな本を選んでください。","romaji":"Suki na hon o erande kudasai.","german":"Bitte wählen Sie ein Buch aus, das Sie mögen.","furigana":"す|ほん|えら"}],"notes":"好き ist im Japanischen ein na-Adjektiv, obwohl die deutsche Übersetzung „mögen“ ein Verb ist. Das Gemochte steht gewöhnlich mit が: 音楽が好きです. Vor Nomen steht な: 好きな本 „ein Buch, das man mag“. Die Verneinung lautet 好きではありません.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"虫が嫌いです。","romaji":"Mushi ga kirai desu.","german":"I dislike insects.","furigana":"むし|きら"},{"japanese":"運動が嫌いです。","romaji":"Undō ga kirai desu.","german":"I dislike exercise.","furigana":"うんどう|きら"},{"japanese":"野菜は嫌いですが、毎日食べます。","romaji":"Yasai wa kirai desu ga, mainichi tabemasu.","german":"I don't like vegetables, but I eat them every day.","furigana":"やさい|きら|まいにちた"}],"notes":"Despite its い ending, 嫌い is a na-adjective: 嫌いな食べ物. The disliked thing usually takes が. 嫌いです can express a clear dislike; 好きではありません \"I don't like it\" often sounds more reserved. 嫌いではありません means \"I don't mind it\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は料理が上手です。","romaji":"Kanojo wa ryōri ga jōzu desu.","german":"She is good at cooking.","furigana":"かのじょ|りょうり|じょうず"},{"japanese":"彼はテニスが上手です。","romaji":"Kare wa tenisu ga jōzu desu.","german":"He is good at tennis.","furigana":"かれ|じょうず"},{"japanese":"母は上手に魚を焼きます。","romaji":"Haha wa jōzu ni sakana o yakimasu.","german":"My mother grills fish skillfully.","furigana":"はは|じょうず|さかな|や"}],"notes":"上手（じょうず）is a na-adjective for skill or ability. The activity often takes が: 料理が上手です. 上手に describes how an action is performed. The word is often suited to praising others; for your own strong point, 得意（とくい）is often more natural.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 8"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は歌が下手です。","romaji":"Watashi wa uta ga heta desu.","german":"I'm bad at singing.","furigana":"わたし|うた|へた"},{"japanese":"私は絵が下手です。","romaji":"Watashi wa e ga heta desu.","german":"I'm bad at drawing.","furigana":"わたし|え|へた"},{"japanese":"歌が下手なので、毎日練習します。","romaji":"Uta ga heta na node, mainichi renshū shimasu.","german":"I'm not good at singing, so I practice every day.","furigana":"うた|へた|まいにちれんしゅう"}],"notes":"下手（へた）is a na-adjective and means \"not skilled at something\". The activity often takes が: 歌が下手です. 下手な is the form before a noun, 下手に the adverbial form. The term can be hurtful when used to judge others; the opposite is 上手.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 8"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この人は有名です。","romaji":"Kono hito wa yūmei desu.","german":"This person is famous.","furigana":"ひと|ゆうめい"},{"japanese":"この寺は有名です。","romaji":"Kono tera wa yūmei desu.","german":"This temple is famous.","furigana":"てら|ゆうめい"},{"japanese":"有名な店なので、いつも人が多いです。","romaji":"Yūmei na mise na node, itsumo hito ga ōi desu.","german":"It's a famous shop, so there are always a lot of people.","furigana":"ゆうめい|みせ|ひと|おお"}],"notes":"有名 is a na-adjective: 有名な人 \"famous person\". For \"to be known for something\", ～で有名 is usual, such as 桜で有名 \"famous for cherry blossoms\". 有名 says something about renown; whether the person or thing is good only becomes clear from context.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 8"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この町は静かです。","romaji":"Kono machi wa shizuka desu.","german":"This town is quiet.","furigana":"まち|しず"},{"japanese":"図書館は静かです。","romaji":"Toshokan wa shizuka desu.","german":"The library is quiet.","furigana":"としょかん|しず"},{"japanese":"図書館では静かに話してください。","romaji":"Toshokan de wa shizuka ni hanashite kudasai.","german":"Please speak quietly in the library.","furigana":"としょかん|しず|はな"}],"notes":"静か is a na-adjective for quiet or little noise: 静かな町. 静かに describes a quiet action, such as 静かに話す \"to speak quietly\". For a negative assessment of a deserted environment, 静か alone is not always enough; the word can mean pleasantly quiet.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この通りは賑やかです。","romaji":"Kono tōri wa nigiyaka desu.","german":"This street is bustling.","furigana":"とお|にぎ"},{"japanese":"お祭りは賑やかです。","romaji":"Omatsuri wa nigiyaka desu.","german":"The festival is lively.","furigana":"まつ|にぎ"}],"notes":"賑やか describes a bustling environment or a lively, often cheerful atmosphere: 賑やかな通り, 賑やかな祭り. It is a na-adjective; the adverbial form is 賑やかに. The word is often positive or neutral, whereas うるさい criticizes disturbing noise.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この花はきれいです。","romaji":"Kono hana wa kirei desu.","german":"This flower is pretty.","furigana":"はな"},{"japanese":"この部屋はきれいです。","romaji":"Kono heya wa kirei desu.","german":"This room is clean.","furigana":"へや"}],"notes":"きれい means \"beautiful\" or \"clean, tidy\", depending on context. Despite the い ending, it is a na-adjective: きれいな花, きれいな部屋. The negative is きれいではありません, not きれくない. The kanji spellings 綺麗 and 奇麗 are also in use.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この辞書は便利です。","romaji":"Kono jisho wa benri desu.","german":"This dictionary is handy.","furigana":"じしょ|べんり"},{"japanese":"駅の近くに住むと便利です。","romaji":"Eki no chikaku ni sumu to benri desu.","german":"It's convenient to live near the station.","furigana":"えき|ちか|す|べんり"}],"notes":"便利 is a na-adjective for practical usefulness or a favorable location: 便利な辞書, 便利な場所. It does not necessarily mean physically comfortable; a comfortable seat might be 座り心地がいい, for example. 便利ではありません is the polite negative; 不便（ふべん）means \"inconvenient\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 7"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"昨日は大変寒かったです。","romaji":"Kinō wa taihen samukatta desu.","german":"It was very cold yesterday.","furigana":"きのう|たいへんさむ"},{"japanese":"引っ越しは大変です。","romaji":"Hikkoshi wa taihen desu.","german":"Moving is hard work.","furigana":"ひ|こ|たいへん"}],"notes":"As an adverb, 大変 intensifies a following adjective or verb in the sense of \"very\"; as a な-adjective in 大変だ／です it describes a difficult or strenuous situation.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大丈夫ですか。","romaji":"Daijōbu desu ka.","german":"Are you all right?","furigana":"だいじょうぶ"},{"japanese":"熱がないから大丈夫です。","romaji":"Netsu ga nai kara daijōbu desu.","german":"I have no fever, so I'm fine.","furigana":"ねつ|だいじょうぶ"},{"japanese":"明日は休みなので、遅くても大丈夫です。","romaji":"Ashita wa yasumi na node, osokute mo daijōbu desu.","german":"Tomorrow is a day off, so it's fine even if it gets late.","furigana":"あした|やす|おそ|だいじょうぶ"}],"notes":"大丈夫 here means \"all right, no problem\". 大丈夫ですか asks how someone is or whether something is possible. In conversation, 大丈夫です can also decline an offer, so pay attention to situation and tone. It is a na-adjective, not an i-adjective.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"水をください。","romaji":"Mizu o kudasai.","german":"Water, please.","furigana":"みず"},{"japanese":"冷たい水をください。","romaji":"Tsumetai mizu o kudasai.","german":"Cold water, please.","furigana":"つめ|みず"},{"japanese":"花に水をあげます。","romaji":"Hana ni mizu o agemasu.","german":"I water the flowers.","furigana":"はな|みず"}],"notes":"水 means water, in everyday speech especially cold or unheated water. Hot water is normally お湯（おゆ）. 水を飲む means \"to drink water\", and 水をあげる means, for example, \"to water a plant\". For a polite order, お水をください is also common.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 6"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お茶を飲みますか。","romaji":"Ocha o nomimasu ka.","german":"Would you like some tea?","furigana":"ちゃ|の"},{"japanese":"食事の後でお茶を飲みます。","romaji":"Shokuji no ato de ocha o nomimasu.","german":"I drink tea after meals.","furigana":"しょくじ|あと|ちゃ|の"}],"notes":"お茶 is the usual polite word for tea; the お often stays even in your own everyday speech. Without further detail, Japanese green tea is often meant, or other tea depending on the situation. For black tea, 紅茶（こうちゃ）is usual. A cup of tea can be counted with お茶を一杯.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 6"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"牛乳を買います。","romaji":"Gyūnyū o kaimasu.","german":"I buy milk.","furigana":"ぎゅうにゅう|か"},{"japanese":"毎朝牛乳を飲みます。","romaji":"Maiasa gyūnyū o nomimasu.","german":"I drink milk every morning.","furigana":"まいあさぎゅうにゅう|の"}],"notes":"牛乳 means cow's milk. It is a noun and takes を as a drink or purchase: 牛乳を飲む, 牛乳を買う. Plant-based drinks are normally named more precisely, such as 豆乳（とうにゅう）\"soy milk\". For quantity, 一杯 or a packaging word such as 一本 is possible.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 18"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"肉を食べます。","romaji":"Niku o tabemasu.","german":"I eat meat.","furigana":"にく|た"},{"japanese":"スーパーで肉を買います。","romaji":"Sūpā de niku o kaimasu.","german":"I buy meat at the supermarket.","furigana":"にく|か"}],"notes":"In a food context, 肉 means \"meat\". The type of animal can be given with a compound: 牛肉（ぎゅうにく）, 豚肉（ぶたにく）, 鶏肉（とりにく）. \"To eat meat\" is 肉を食べる; as a substance, meat is often ordered by weight, such as 百グラム.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 6"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"魚が好きです。","romaji":"Sakana ga suki desu.","german":"I like fish.","furigana":"さかな|す"},{"japanese":"魚を焼きます。","romaji":"Sakana o yakimasu.","german":"I grill fish.","furigana":"さかな|や"},{"japanese":"川に魚がいます。","romaji":"Kawa ni sakana ga imasu.","german":"There are fish in the river.","furigana":"かわ|さかな"}],"notes":"魚（さかな）refers both to live fish and to fish as food. For a live fish, use いる, not ある, for \"there is\". The reading うお also occurs, especially in compounds; here the common everyday reading さかな is practiced.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 6"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"野菜を食べましょう。","romaji":"Yasai o tabemashō.","german":"Let's eat vegetables.","furigana":"やさい|た"},{"japanese":"子供は野菜が嫌いです。","romaji":"Kodomo wa yasai ga kirai desu.","german":"The child doesn't like vegetables.","furigana":"こども|やさい|きら"}],"notes":"野菜 is the collective term for vegetables and has no plural ending in Japanese. 野菜を食べる means \"to eat vegetables\"; individual kinds or quantities are named more precisely when needed. 果物（くだもの）is the everyday counterpart for fruit.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 6"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"果物が好きです。","romaji":"Kudamono ga suki desu.","german":"I like fruit.","furigana":"くだもの|す"},{"japanese":"果物を毎日食べます。","romaji":"Kudamono o mainichi tabemasu.","german":"I eat fruit every day.","furigana":"くだもの|まいにちた"},{"japanese":"りんごは果物ですが、にんじんは野菜です。","romaji":"Ringo wa kudamono desu ga, ninjin wa yasai desu.","german":"Apples are fruit, but carrots are vegetables.","furigana":"くだもの|やさい"}],"notes":"果物 is the collective term for fruit and refers to edible fruits such as apples or oranges. It takes no plural ending. For individual pieces, suitable counters are used depending on the fruit, often 個. 野菜（やさい）means vegetables.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 6"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"卵を二つください。","romaji":"Tamago o futatsu kudasai.","german":"Two eggs, please.","furigana":"たまご|ふた"},{"japanese":"卵でオムレツを作ります。","romaji":"Tamago de omuretsu o tsukurimasu.","german":"I make an omelet with eggs.","furigana":"たまご|つく"}],"notes":"卵 means an egg, in everyday speech often a chicken egg. In cooking, the spelling 玉子 is also seen. Eggs can be counted with 個; the general counting form 二つ is also possible. In 医者の卵 \"doctor in the making\", on the other hand, it has a figurative meaning.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 6"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"朝パンを食べます。","romaji":"Asa pan o tabemasu.","german":"I eat bread in the morning.","furigana":"あさ|た"},{"japanese":"焼きたてのパンが好きです。","romaji":"Yakitate no pan ga suki desu.","german":"I like freshly baked bread.","furigana":"や|す"}],"notes":"パン means bread and in everyday Japanese also covers various rolls and sweet baked goods such as 菓子パン. It is a noun, not a verb. 焼きたてのパン means bread that has just been freshly baked.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 6"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ご飯を食べましょう。","romaji":"Gohan o tabemashō.","german":"Let's eat.","furigana":"はん|た"},{"japanese":"ご飯とみそ汁が好きです。","romaji":"Gohan to misoshiru ga suki desu.","german":"I like rice and miso soup.","furigana":"はん|しる|す"}],"notes":"ご飯 means cooked rice and, depending on context, a meal as a whole. ご飯を食べる can therefore simply mean \"to eat\". Uncooked rice grains are called 米. In ご飯とみそ汁, on the other hand, the rice is meant as an individual component.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本料理が好きです。","romaji":"Nihon ryōri ga suki desu.","german":"I like Japanese food.","furigana":"にほんりょうり|す"},{"japanese":"母の料理がおいしいです。","romaji":"Haha no ryōri ga oishii desu.","german":"My mother's cooking is delicious.","furigana":"はは|りょうり"},{"japanese":"料理をするのが好きです。","romaji":"Ryōri o suru no ga suki desu.","german":"I like cooking.","furigana":"りょうり|す"}],"notes":"料理 can refer to the activity of cooking, a prepared dish or a cuisine as a style. 日本料理 means \"Japanese cuisine\", 母の料理 the food prepared by one's mother. For the activity, 料理する or 料理をする is used.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 9"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"朝ご飯を食べましたか。","romaji":"Asagohan o tabemashita ka.","german":"Did you have breakfast?","furigana":"あさ|はん|た"},{"japanese":"朝ご飯にパンを食べます。","romaji":"Asagohan ni pan o tabemasu.","german":"For breakfast I eat bread.","furigana":"あさ|はん|た"},{"japanese":"父が家族の朝ご飯を用意しています。","romaji":"Chichi ga kazoku no asagohan o yōi shite imasu.","german":"My father is preparing breakfast for the family.","furigana":"ちち|かぞく|あさ|はん|ようい"}],"notes":"朝ご飯 refers to the whole breakfast meal, even if you eat bread instead of rice. 朝ご飯を食べる means \"to have breakfast\"; 朝ご飯にパンを食べる names what you eat for breakfast. 朝食 is the more formal term, for example on a hotel schedule. 朝御飯 is an attested, less common spelling with the same reading.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"昼ご飯は何ですか。","romaji":"Hirugohan wa nan desu ka.","german":"What's for lunch?","furigana":"ひる|はん|なん"},{"japanese":"昼ご飯を一緒に食べましょう。","romaji":"Hirugohan o issho ni tabemashō.","german":"Let's have lunch together.","furigana":"ひる|はん|いっしょ|た"}],"notes":"昼ご飯 refers to the midday meal. 昼御飯 and 昼ごはん are also found as spellings. With 何ですか you ask what food there is; 一緒に食べましょう invites someone to have lunch together.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"晩ご飯を作ります。","romaji":"Bangohan o tsukurimasu.","german":"I make dinner.","furigana":"ばん|はん|つく"},{"japanese":"晩ご飯は七時に食べます。","romaji":"Bangohan wa shichiji ni tabemasu.","german":"I eat dinner at seven o'clock.","furigana":"ばん|はん|しちじ|た"}],"notes":"晩ご飯 means the evening meal. 晩御飯 and 晩ごはん are other common spellings; 夕食 is another term for dinner. A mealtime is attached with に, such as 七時に食べる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"学校に行きます。","romaji":"Gakkō ni ikimasu.","german":"I go to school.","furigana":"がっこう|い"},{"japanese":"学校は九時に始まります。","romaji":"Gakkō wa kuji ni hajimarimasu.","german":"School starts at nine.","furigana":"がっこう|くじ|はじ"}],"notes":"学校 refers to a school as an institution and, in everyday speech, also to school life. 学校に行く names the school as a destination; 学校で勉強する names the school as a place of learning. The small っ in がっこう is written as a double consonant kk, the long o as ō.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大学で日本語を勉強します。","romaji":"Daigaku de nihongo o benkyō shimasu.","german":"I study Japanese at university.","furigana":"だいがく|にほんご|べんきょう"},{"japanese":"大学は家から遠いです。","romaji":"Daigaku wa ie kara tōi desu.","german":"The university is far from home.","furigana":"だいがく|いえ|とお"}],"notes":"大学 means a university or college. The place of an activity takes で: 大学で勉強する. In 大学は家から遠い, 家から names the reference point of the distance. This card does not cover historical administrative schools or the classic book title 大学.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"病院に行きます。","romaji":"Byōin ni ikimasu.","german":"I go to the hospital.","furigana":"びょういん|い"},{"japanese":"病院で薬をもらいました。","romaji":"Byōin de kusuri o moraimashita.","german":"I got medicine at the hospital.","furigana":"びょういん|くすり"}],"notes":"病院 means a hospital or clinic. 病院に行く can also mean a visit to the doctor in everyday speech, without anyone being admitted. For the destination use に, for an action there で. Do not confuse it with 美容院（びよういん, hair salon）.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"銀行は駅の近くです。","romaji":"Ginkō wa eki no chikaku desu.","german":"The bank is near the station.","furigana":"ぎんこう|えき|ちか"},{"japanese":"銀行でお金をおろします。","romaji":"Ginkō de okane o oroshimasu.","german":"I withdraw money at the bank.","furigana":"ぎんこう|かね"}],"notes":"銀行 means a financial institution, not a bench. The location can be described with ～の近く. For \"to withdraw money\", the combination お金をおろす is used; the bank as the place of action takes で.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"郵便局はどこですか。","romaji":"Yūbinkyoku wa doko desu ka.","german":"Where is the post office?","furigana":"ゆうびんきょく"},{"japanese":"郵便局で手紙を出します。","romaji":"Yūbinkyoku de tegami o dashimasu.","german":"I mail a letter at the post office.","furigana":"ゆうびんきょく|てがみ|だ"}],"notes":"郵便局 means a post office or postal branch, that is, the place for postal services. 郵便 on its own is mail as items sent or as a service. With 郵便局で手紙を出す you describe mailing a letter at the post office.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"図書館で本を借ります。","romaji":"Toshokan de hon o karimasu.","german":"I borrow books from the library.","furigana":"としょかん|ほん|か"},{"japanese":"図書館で勉強します。","romaji":"Toshokan de benkyō shimasu.","german":"I study at the library.","furigana":"としょかん|べんきょう"}],"notes":"図書館 is a library with books to read or borrow, not a bookstore（本屋）. For activities there, the place takes で. 本を借りる means to borrow a book; 本を返す means to return it.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"会社に行きます。","romaji":"Kaisha ni ikimasu.","german":"I go to the office.","furigana":"かいしゃ|い"},{"japanese":"会社は大きいです。","romaji":"Kaisha wa ōkii desu.","german":"The company is big.","furigana":"かいしゃ|おお"}],"notes":"会社 means a company and in everyday speech can also refer to one's own workplace. That is why 会社に行く often means \"to go to work\". An activity at the workplace is linked with で, such as 会社で働く. 大きい会社 can mean a large organization, not just a large building.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの店は安いです。","romaji":"Ano mise wa yasui desu.","german":"That shop is cheap.","furigana":"みせ|やす"},{"japanese":"この店のケーキはおいしいです。","romaji":"Kono mise no kēki wa oishii desu.","german":"The cake at this shop is delicious.","furigana":"みせ"}],"notes":"店（みせ）means a shop and can also refer to a food and drink business. この店のケーキ are the cakes that this shop sells. In compounds, 店 is often read テン, as in 喫茶店; this card uses みせ.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 6"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"レストランで食べます。","romaji":"Resutoran de tabemasu.","german":"I eat at a restaurant.","furigana":"た"},{"japanese":"レストランを予約しました。","romaji":"Resutoran o yoyaku shimashita.","german":"I made a reservation at a restaurant.","furigana":"よやく"}],"notes":"レストラン means a restaurant; the word is used especially often for restaurants with Western-style cuisine, without being strictly limited to them. The place of eating takes で. 予約する describes a reservation, for example for a restaurant visit.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 6"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ホテルに泊まります。","romaji":"Hoteru ni tomarimasu.","german":"I stay at a hotel.","furigana":"と"},{"japanese":"ホテルの部屋は広いです。","romaji":"Hoteru no heya wa hiroi desu.","german":"The hotel room is spacious.","furigana":"へや|ひろ"}],"notes":"ホテル means a hotel. For staying overnight in accommodation, ～に泊まる is used: ホテルに泊まる. ホテルの部屋 is a hotel room; の links the accommodation and the room belonging to it.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 12"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"空港まで一時間です。","romaji":"Kūkō made ichijikan desu.","german":"It takes an hour to the airport.","furigana":"くうこう|いちじかん"},{"japanese":"空港はとても大きいです。","romaji":"Kūkō wa totemo ōkii desu.","german":"The airport is very big.","furigana":"くうこう|おお"}],"notes":"空港 means an airport, not an airplane（飛行機）. ～まで names a destination point; 一時間 then describes the time needed to get there. The long vowels u and o in くうこう are written with macrons in Hepburn: kūkō.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 12"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"駅はどこですか。","romaji":"Eki wa doko desu ka.","german":"Where is the station?","furigana":"えき"},{"japanese":"駅まで歩いて十分です。","romaji":"Eki made aruite juppun desu.","german":"It's a ten-minute walk to the station.","furigana":"えき|ある|じゅっぷん"}],"notes":"駅 means a train station or rail stop, including city trains and subways. A bus stop is normally called バス停. 駅まで歩いて十分 describes the walking time to the station; 十分 is read じゅっぷん here.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"電車で行きます。","romaji":"Densha de ikimasu.","german":"I go by train.","furigana":"でんしゃ|い"},{"japanese":"電車が来ました。","romaji":"Densha ga kimashita.","german":"The train has come.","furigana":"でんしゃ|き"}],"notes":"電車 means a train, literally an electrically powered train in particular. As a means of transport it takes で: 電車で行く. When boarding, に is used: 電車に乗る. 電車が来る describes the arrival of a train.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"バスに乗ります。","romaji":"Basu ni norimasu.","german":"I take the bus.","furigana":"の"},{"japanese":"バスは何時に来ますか。","romaji":"Basu wa nanji ni kimasu ka.","german":"What time does the bus come?","furigana":"なんじ|き"}],"notes":"バス here means the bus as a means of transport. For boarding or riding along, say バスに乗る, and for the way of travelling, バスで行く. Identically written loanwords for bath, bass or fish belong to other meanings.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"タクシーで帰ります。","romaji":"Takushī de kaerimasu.","german":"I go back by taxi.","furigana":"かえ"},{"japanese":"タクシーを呼んでください。","romaji":"Takushī o yonde kudasai.","german":"Please call a taxi.","furigana":"よ"}],"notes":"タクシー means a taxi. With で you name it as a means of transport, with を as the vehicle being called: タクシーを呼ぶ. The long i at the end of the word comes from ー and is romanized takushī.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"飛行機で旅行します。","romaji":"Hikōki de ryokō shimasu.","german":"I travel by plane.","furigana":"ひこうき|りょこう"},{"japanese":"飛行機のチケットを買いました。","romaji":"Hikōki no chiketto o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a plane ticket.","furigana":"ひこうき|か"}],"notes":"飛行機 means an airplane. As a means of travel it takes で. 飛行機のチケット is a flight ticket; a shorter term for it is 航空券. Do not confuse it with 空港 (\"airport\"). ひこうき is written hikōki.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自転車で学校に行きます。","romaji":"Jitensha de gakkō ni ikimasu.","german":"I go to school by bicycle.","furigana":"じてんしゃ|がっこう|い"},{"japanese":"自転車のタイヤに空気を入れます。","romaji":"Jitensha no taiya ni kūki o iremasu.","german":"I pump air into the bicycle tire.","furigana":"じてんしゃ|くうき|い"}],"notes":"自転車 is a bicycle. For the means of transport, use で: 自転車で学校に行く. For getting on or riding a bicycle, 乗る takes に. The reading is じてんしゃ, not じでんしゃ.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"車を運転します。","romaji":"Kuruma o unten shimasu.","german":"I drive a car.","furigana":"くるま|うんてん"},{"japanese":"赤い車が好きです。","romaji":"Akai kuruma ga suki desu.","german":"I like red cars.","furigana":"あか|くるま|す"}],"notes":"車（くるま）usually means a car in everyday speech, but can refer to a vehicle more generally. 車を運転する means to drive or steer a car. For \"to go by car\" as a means of travel, say 車で行く. The meaning \"wheel\" is not intended here.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この道をまっすぐ行ってください。","romaji":"Kono michi o massugu itte kudasai.","german":"Please go straight along this road.","furigana":"みち|い"},{"japanese":"この道は暗いです。","romaji":"Kono michi wa kurai desu.","german":"This road is dark.","furigana":"みち|くら"}],"notes":"道（みち）means a street, a path or a route. When moving, the stretch traversed can take を: この道を行く. In directions, まっすぐ means \"straight ahead\". Figurative meanings such as the path of life are not taught here.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 23"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"橋を渡ります。","romaji":"Hashi o watarimasu.","german":"I cross the bridge.","furigana":"はし|わた"},{"japanese":"新しい橋ができました。","romaji":"Atarashii hashi ga dekimashita.","german":"A new bridge has been built.","furigana":"あたら|はし"}],"notes":"橋（はし）is a noun for a bridge. \"To cross a bridge\" is 橋を渡る; for a position, use something like 橋の上 \"on the bridge\". The identically pronounced 箸 means \"chopsticks\"; the script, context and pitch accent help to tell them apart.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 23"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"家に帰ります。","romaji":"Ie ni kaerimasu.","german":"I go home.","furigana":"いえ|かえ"},{"japanese":"新しい家に引っ越しました。","romaji":"Atarashii ie ni hikkoshimashita.","german":"I moved into a new house.","furigana":"あたら|いえ|ひ|こ"}],"notes":"家（いえ）means a house or home. 家に帰る means \"to return home\"; with a place of residence, に is used. For your own home, うち is also very common. The reading いえ is practiced here; 家 also has other readings in compounds.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"部屋はきれいです。","romaji":"Heya wa kirei desu.","german":"The room is clean.","furigana":"へや"},{"japanese":"部屋を掃除します。","romaji":"Heya o sōji shimasu.","german":"I clean the room.","furigana":"へや|そうじ"},{"japanese":"このホテルの部屋を予約しました。","romaji":"Kono hoteru no heya o yoyaku shimashita.","german":"I reserved a room at this hotel.","furigana":"へや|よやく"}],"notes":"部屋 means \"room\". The place of presence usually takes に, an action in the room takes で: 部屋にいる, 部屋で勉強する. \"To clean the room\" is 部屋を掃除する. The word can also refer to a hotel room.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"台所で料理します。","romaji":"Daidokoro de ryōri shimasu.","german":"I cook in the kitchen.","furigana":"だいどころ|りょうり"},{"japanese":"台所で食事をします。","romaji":"Daidokoro de shokuji o shimasu.","german":"I eat in the kitchen.","furigana":"だいどころ|しょくじ"}],"notes":"台所 means the kitchen as a room or work area in the household. For activities, で is used: 台所で料理する. キッチン is a common alternative. In 台所にいる, on the other hand, に is used because only the location is stated.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 27"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お風呂に入ります。","romaji":"Ofuro ni hairimasu.","german":"I take a bath.","furigana":"ふろ|はい"},{"japanese":"お風呂はとても気持ちいいです。","romaji":"Ofuro wa totemo kimochi ii desu.","german":"The bath feels very nice.","furigana":"ふろ|きも"},{"japanese":"お風呂のお湯が熱いです。","romaji":"Ofuro no oyu ga atsui desu.","german":"The water in the bathtub is hot.","furigana":"ふろ|ゆ|あつ"}],"notes":"お風呂 is the usual polite word for a bath or bathtub; the お is often used even for one's own bath. お風呂に入る means \"to take a bath\". A shower, by contrast, is taken with シャワーを浴びる. The hot bathwater is usually called お湯.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 6"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"トイレはどこですか。","romaji":"Toire wa doko desu ka.","german":"Where is the restroom?"},{"japanese":"トイレを使ってもいいですか。","romaji":"Toire o tsukatte mo ii desu ka.","german":"May I use the restroom?","furigana":"つか"}],"notes":"トイレ means the toilet or the restroom. トイレはどこですか asks the way there; トイレに行く means \"to go to the restroom\". お手洗い（おてあらい）is a more polite term often seen on signs or in restaurants.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"窓を開けてください。","romaji":"Mado o akete kudasai.","german":"Please open the window.","furigana":"まど|あ"},{"japanese":"窓から山が見えます。","romaji":"Mado kara yama ga miemasu.","german":"You can see the mountain from the window.","furigana":"まど|やま|み"}],"notes":"窓 is the noun for a window. \"To open or close a window\" is 窓を開ける・閉める. 窓から describes the view from there, as in 窓から山が見える. For a window seat, 窓側（まどがわ）is common.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 10"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ドアを閉めてください。","romaji":"Doa o shimete kudasai.","german":"Please close the door.","furigana":"し"},{"japanese":"ドアの前に靴があります。","romaji":"Doa no mae ni kutsu ga arimasu.","german":"There are shoes in front of the door.","furigana":"まえ|くつ"}],"notes":"ドア means a door, often one on hinges. ドアを開ける・閉める means \"to open or close the door\". ドアの前 is the area in front of the door. For the entrance area of a house, 玄関（げんかん）is often used.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 10"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"机の上に本があります。","romaji":"Tsukue no ue ni hon ga arimasu.","german":"There is a book on the desk.","furigana":"つくえ|うえ|ほん"},{"japanese":"新しい机を買いました。","romaji":"Atarashii tsukue o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a new desk.","furigana":"あたら|つくえ|か"}],"notes":"机 is a table for working, studying or writing, usually a desk. 机の上 means \"on the desk\" and 机の下 \"under the desk\". A dining or living room table is often called テーブル; the usage matters for word choice.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"椅子に座ってください。","romaji":"Isu ni suwatte kudasai.","german":"Please sit down.","furigana":"いす|すわ"},{"japanese":"この椅子は座り心地がいいです。","romaji":"Kono isu wa suwari gokochi ga ii desu.","german":"This chair is comfortable to sit in.","furigana":"いす|すわ|"},{"japanese":"会議の前に椅子を十脚並べました。","romaji":"Kaigi no mae ni isu o jukkyaku narabemashita.","german":"I set up ten chairs before the meeting.","furigana":"かいぎ|まえ|いす|じゅっきゃくなら"}],"notes":"椅子に座る means to sit on a chair; the seat is marked with に. 椅子を運ぶ or 椅子を並べる, on the other hand, describe moving or arranging chairs. When counting, 一脚（いっきゃく）is often used. Figuratively, 椅子 can also mean a post or position, as in 社長の椅子.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"電話をかけます。","romaji":"Denwa o kakemasu.","german":"I make a phone call.","furigana":"でんわ"},{"japanese":"電話で話しています。","romaji":"Denwa de hanashite imasu.","german":"I am talking on the phone.","furigana":"でんわ|はな"},{"japanese":"電話が鳴っています。","romaji":"Denwa ga natte imasu.","german":"The phone is ringing.","furigana":"でんわ|な"}],"notes":"電話 can mean the telephone or a phone call. 電話をかける means \"to call\" and 電話で話す \"to talk on the phone\". 電話する is also common. In 電話が鳴る \"the phone rings\", the device itself is in the foreground.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"時計を見てください。","romaji":"Tokei o mite kudasai.","german":"Please look at the clock.","furigana":"とけい|み"},{"japanese":"時計が止まりました。","romaji":"Tokei ga tomarimashita.","german":"The clock has stopped.","furigana":"とけい|と"}],"notes":"時計 means a clock or watch; the context shows whether a wristwatch, wall clock or other timepiece is meant. 腕時計（うでどけい）is explicitly a wristwatch. 時計を見る means \"to look at the clock\"; for the time of day, 時間 or an expression with 時 is used.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"傘を忘れました。","romaji":"Kasa o wasuremashita.","german":"I forgot my umbrella.","furigana":"かさ|わす"},{"japanese":"雨なので傘を持ちます。","romaji":"Ame na node kasa o mochimasu.","german":"Since it is raining, I will take an umbrella.","furigana":"あめ|かさ|も"}],"notes":"傘 means a rain umbrella or parasol; in everyday speech it usually means the rain umbrella. \"To put up or use an umbrella\" is 傘を差す（さす）. 傘を持つ can mean holding it or carrying it along. Umbrellas are counted with 本, as in 傘を一本.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鍵を持っていますか。","romaji":"Kagi o motte imasu ka.","german":"Do you have the key?","furigana":"かぎ|も"},{"japanese":"鍵をかけてください。","romaji":"Kagi o kakete kudasai.","german":"Please lock up.","furigana":"かぎ"}],"notes":"鍵 is a key; the word can also mean the lock. 鍵をかける means \"to lock\" as a fixed expression, and 鍵を開ける \"to unlock\". The door is often the topic or marked with に. Keys are usually counted with 本.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"財布を忘れました。","romaji":"Saifu o wasuremashita.","german":"I forgot my wallet.","furigana":"さいふ|わす"},{"japanese":"財布にお金が入っています。","romaji":"Saifu ni okane ga haitte imasu.","german":"There is money in the wallet.","furigana":"さいふ|かね|はい"}],"notes":"財布 is a wallet or purse for cash and cards. 財布にお金を入れる means \"to put money into the wallet\"; 財布を忘れる \"to forget one's wallet\". For a small coin purse, 小銭入れ（こぜにいれ）is also used.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"かばんの中に何がありますか。","romaji":"Kaban no naka ni nani ga arimasu ka.","german":"What is in the bag?","furigana":"なか|なに"},{"japanese":"かばんに本を入れます。","romaji":"Kaban ni hon o iremasu.","german":"I put the book in the bag.","furigana":"ほん|い"}],"notes":"かばん means a bag, such as a school bag, travel bag or briefcase. The kanji spelling is 鞄. かばんに本を入れる means \"to put a book in the bag\" and かばんの中 \"the inside of the bag\". A simple sack or plastic bag is more likely called 袋（ふくろ）.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この本は面白いです。","romaji":"Kono hon wa omoshiroi desu.","german":"This book is interesting.","furigana":"ほん|おもしろ"},{"japanese":"図書館で本を借りました。","romaji":"Toshokan de hon o karimashita.","german":"I borrowed a book from the library.","furigana":"としょかん|ほん|か"}],"notes":"As an independent noun, 本 means \"book\". Books are counted with 冊: 本を一冊 \"one book\". The character 本 also appears as a counter for long, thin objects; in that case it is not the noun \"book\". 図書館で本を借りる means \"to borrow a book from the library\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新聞を読みます。","romaji":"Shinbun o yomimasu.","german":"I read the newspaper.","furigana":"しんぶん|よ"},{"japanese":"朝新聞を読みます。","romaji":"Asa shinbun o yomimasu.","german":"I read the newspaper in the morning.","furigana":"あさしんぶん|よ"},{"japanese":"駅で新聞を買いました。","romaji":"Eki de shinbun o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a newspaper at the station.","furigana":"えき|しんぶん|か"}],"notes":"新聞 is a newspaper, not just any news. 新聞を読む means \"to read the newspaper\" and 新聞を買う \"to buy a newspaper\". A magazine is called 雑誌. A single copy of a newspaper can be counted with 部, as in 新聞を一部.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"雑誌を買いました。","romaji":"Zasshi o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a magazine.","furigana":"ざっし|か"},{"japanese":"この雑誌は面白いです。","romaji":"Kono zasshi wa omoshiroi desu.","german":"This magazine is interesting.","furigana":"ざっし|おもしろ"}],"notes":"雑誌 means a magazine, that is, a periodically published publication. 新聞, by contrast, is a newspaper. 雑誌を読む・買う are common combinations. Individual issues can be counted with 冊, as in 雑誌を一冊.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"辞書を使ってください。","romaji":"Jisho o tsukatte kudasai.","german":"Bitte benutzen Sie das Wörterbuch.","furigana":"じしょ|つか"},{"japanese":"辞書で調べてください。","romaji":"Jisho de shirabete kudasai.","german":"Bitte schlagen Sie es im Wörterbuch nach.","furigana":"じしょ|しら"},{"japanese":"これは日本語とドイツ語の辞書です。","romaji":"Kore wa Nihongo to Doitsugo no jisho desu.","german":"Das ist ein Wörterbuch für Japanisch und Deutsch.","furigana":"にほんご|ご|じしょ"}],"notes":"辞書 ist ein Wörterbuch. 辞書で調べる heißt „im Wörterbuch nachschlagen“; で markiert dabei das Hilfsmittel. 日本語の辞書 kann ein Wörterbuch für Japanisch sein. Auch Wörterbuch-Apps oder elektronische Wörterbücher werden je nach Zusammenhang 辞書 genannt.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ノートに書いてください。","romaji":"Nōto ni kaite kudasai.","german":"Bitte schreiben Sie es ins Heft.","furigana":"か"},{"japanese":"ノートを忘れました。","romaji":"Nōto o wasuremashita.","german":"Ich habe mein Heft vergessen.","furigana":"わす"}],"notes":"ノート bezeichnet hier ein Heft oder Notizbuch. ノートに書く heißt „ins Heft schreiben“; に markiert die Fläche oder den Zielort des Eintrags. ノートを取る ist die feste Verbindung für „Notizen machen“. Das deutsche „Note“ im Sinne einer Schulnote entspricht dieser Bedeutung nicht.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鉛筆で書いてください。","romaji":"Enpitsu de kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write in pencil.","furigana":"えんぴつ|か"},{"japanese":"この鉛筆は短いです。","romaji":"Kono enpitsu wa mijikai desu.","german":"This pencil is short.","furigana":"えんぴつ|みじか"}],"notes":"鉛筆 means a pencil, usually a wooden one. 鉛筆で書く means \"to write in pencil\"; で marks the writing tool. For a mechanical pencil, シャープペンシル or the short form シャーペン is common. Pencils are counted with 本.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ペンを貸してください。","romaji":"Pen o kashite kudasai.","german":"Please lend me a pen.","furigana":"か"},{"japanese":"赤いペンをください。","romaji":"Akai pen o kudasai.","german":"A red pen, please.","furigana":"あか"},{"japanese":"ペンで名前を書きます。","romaji":"Pen de namae o kakimasu.","german":"I write my name with a pen.","furigana":"なまえ|か"}],"notes":"ペン is a writing instrument with ink, or a nib pen; a pencil is called 鉛筆. ボールペン explicitly means a ballpoint pen. ペンで書く means \"to write with a pen\"; individual pens are counted with 本.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"写真を撮りましょう。","romaji":"Shashin o torimashō.","german":"Let's take a photo.","furigana":"しゃしん|と"},{"japanese":"旅行の写真を見せてください。","romaji":"Ryokō no shashin o misete kudasai.","german":"Please show me your trip photos.","furigana":"りょこう|しゃしん|み"}],"notes":"写真 is a photo or photograph. \"To take a photo\" is 写真を撮る, not 写真を作る. 写真を見せる means \"to show photos\". Photos, as flat objects, are counted with 枚, as in 写真を一枚.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 6"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"映画を見に行きます。","romaji":"Eiga o mi ni ikimasu.","german":"I am going to see a movie.","furigana":"えいが|み|い"},{"japanese":"日本の映画が好きです。","romaji":"Nihon no eiga ga suki desu.","german":"I like Japanese movies.","furigana":"にほん|えいが|す"}],"notes":"映画 means a film as a work, or a film screening. 映画を見る means \"to watch a movie\"; 映画を見に行く \"to go out to see a movie\". The cinema as a building is 映画館（えいがかん）. \"Japanese films\" can be expressed as 日本の映画.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"音楽を聞きます。","romaji":"Ongaku o kikimasu.","german":"I listen to music.","furigana":"おんがく|き"},{"japanese":"クラシック音楽が好きです。","romaji":"Kurashikku ongaku ga suki desu.","german":"I like classical music.","furigana":"おんがく|す"},{"japanese":"隣の部屋から音楽が聞こえます。","romaji":"Tonari no heya kara ongaku ga kikoemasu.","german":"Music can be heard from the next room.","furigana":"となり|へや|おんがく|き"}],"notes":"音楽 is the general term for music. 音楽を聞く means \"to listen to music\"; for attentive or enjoyable listening, 聴く is also written. A genre can come before it, as in クラシック音楽. A single piece of music is called 曲（きょく）.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 9"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"旅行が好きです。","romaji":"Ryokō ga suki desu.","german":"I like traveling.","furigana":"りょこう|す"},{"japanese":"来月旅行に行きます。","romaji":"Raigetsu ryokō ni ikimasu.","german":"I am going on a trip next month.","furigana":"らいげつりょこう|い"}],"notes":"旅行 means a trip and forms the verb \"to travel\" with する. 旅行に行く is also a common combination. A single everyday trip to school is normally not a 旅行; the word emphasizes a journey or excursion.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 7"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"公園を散歩します。","romaji":"Kōen o sanpo shimasu.","german":"I take a walk in the park.","furigana":"こうえん|さんぽ"},{"japanese":"毎朝犬と散歩します。","romaji":"Maiasa inu to sanpo shimasu.","german":"I go for a walk with the dog every morning.","furigana":"まいあさいぬ|さんぽ"}],"notes":"散歩 is a walk, often without a particular destination and for relaxation. 散歩する means \"to take a walk\". The space walked through can be marked with を, and a companion with と: 公園を散歩する, 犬と散歩する.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日の天気はどうですか。","romaji":"Kyō no tenki wa dō desu ka.","german":"How is the weather today?","furigana":"きょう|てんき"},{"japanese":"明日の天気は晴れです。","romaji":"Ashita no tenki wa hare desu.","german":"Tomorrow's weather will be sunny.","furigana":"あした|てんき|は"}],"notes":"天気 means the weather in general. With どうですか you ask about its condition, and with 晴れ, 雨 or 雪 you state the weather situation. \"Nice weather\" is いい天気. 今日の天気 connects the day with the noun by の.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 12"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"明日は雨です。","romaji":"Ashita wa ame desu.","german":"It will rain tomorrow.","furigana":"あした|あめ"},{"japanese":"雨の日は家にいます。","romaji":"Ame no hi wa ie ni imasu.","german":"I stay home on rainy days.","furigana":"あめ|ひ|いえ"}],"notes":"雨 is a noun: 雨です means \"it is raining/there will be rain\" in a weather report. For ongoing precipitation, 雨が降っています is used. 雨の日 means a rainy day; の connects the weather with the day.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 12"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"雪が降っています。","romaji":"Yuki ga futte imasu.","german":"It is snowing.","furigana":"ゆき|ふ"},{"japanese":"今年は雪が多いです。","romaji":"Kotoshi wa yuki ga ōi desu.","german":"There is a lot of snow this year.","furigana":"ことし|ゆき|おお"}],"notes":"雪 means snow and snowfall. 雪が降る means \"it snows\"; 雪が降っています describes snow that is falling right now. For an amount of snow, 雪が多い is one example. Personal names with the same reading are not meant here.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 12"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日は風が強いです。","romaji":"Kyō wa kaze ga tsuyoi desu.","german":"The wind is strong today.","furigana":"きょう|かぜ|つよ"},{"japanese":"秋は風が涼しいです。","romaji":"Aki wa kaze ga suzushii desu.","german":"In autumn the wind is cool.","furigana":"あき|かぜ|すず"}],"notes":"風（かぜ）means wind here. 強い describes its strength and 涼しい its pleasantly cool effect. A cold is normally written 風邪, also read かぜ. The reading ふう in other uses is not the reading of this card.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 12"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"空がきれいです。","romaji":"Sora ga kirei desu.","german":"The sky is beautiful.","furigana":"そら"},{"japanese":"空に星が見えます。","romaji":"Sora ni hoshi ga miemasu.","german":"You can see stars in the sky.","furigana":"そら|ほし|み"}],"notes":"空（そら）means the sky or the air space. 空に星が見える describes the position of visible stars with に. The same kanji is also read から or くう in other words; those readings are not used here.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 27"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"山に登ります。","romaji":"Yama ni noborimasu.","german":"I climb the mountain.","furigana":"やま|のぼ"},{"japanese":"富士山は日本で一番高い山です。","romaji":"Fujisan wa Nihon de ichiban takai yama desu.","german":"Mt. Fuji is the highest mountain in Japan.","furigana":"ふじさん|にほん|いちばんたか|やま"}],"notes":"山（やま）means a mountain or hill. 山に登る means to climb a mountain. In many mountain names, 山 is read さん, as in 富士山（ふじさん）; so 富士山 is not *ふじやま in the standard name used here.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 8"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"川で泳ぎます。","romaji":"Kawa de oyogimasu.","german":"I swim in the river.","furigana":"かわ|およ"},{"japanese":"川の水はきれいです。","romaji":"Kawa no mizu wa kirei desu.","german":"The river water is clean.","furigana":"かわ|みず"}],"notes":"川（かわ）means a river or stream. For an activity in the river, the place is marked with で. 川の水 means river water. In river names the reading can change to がわ through sound linking, as in 多摩川（たまがわ）. 河 is also a recorded spelling of かわ for a river; the first example spelling remains 川.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 13"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"海で泳ぎたいです。","romaji":"Umi de oyogitai desu.","german":"I want to swim in the sea.","furigana":"うみ|およ"},{"japanese":"夏に海に行きます。","romaji":"Natsu ni umi ni ikimasu.","german":"I go to the sea in summer.","furigana":"なつ|うみ|い"}],"notes":"海 means the sea or ocean. The combination 海に行く can mean a trip to the coast, not necessarily into the water. For an activity in the sea, で is used: 海で泳ぐ. The destination with 行く is marked with に.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 12"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"花がきれいです。","romaji":"Hana ga kirei desu.","german":"The flowers are beautiful.","furigana":"はな"},{"japanese":"庭に花を植えました。","romaji":"Niwa ni hana o uemashita.","german":"I planted flowers in the garden.","furigana":"にわ|はな|う"}],"notes":"花（はな）means a flower or blossom. It can mean several flowers, although the noun needs no separate plural form. A planting place is given with に: 庭に花を植える. 鼻 (\"nose\") has the same reading but is a different word.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 7"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大きい木があります。","romaji":"Ōkii ki ga arimasu.","german":"There is a big tree.","furigana":"おお|き"},{"japanese":"木の下で休みます。","romaji":"Ki no shita de yasumimasu.","german":"I rest under the tree.","furigana":"き|した|やす"}],"notes":"木（き）means a tree here. 木 can also mean wood, but the examples concern a living tree. 木の下 means under the tree; the place of an activity is then marked with で.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 10"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"犬が好きです。","romaji":"Inu ga suki desu.","german":"I like dogs.","furigana":"いぬ|す"},{"japanese":"犬と散歩します。","romaji":"Inu to sanpo shimasu.","german":"I go for a walk with my dog.","furigana":"いぬ|さんぽ"}],"notes":"犬 means dog. To express the presence of a dog, いる is used, since it is a living being. 犬と散歩する means to go for a walk with a dog; と names the companion. Dogs are usually counted with 匹, and large animals, depending on context, with 頭.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 10"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"猫がいます。","romaji":"Neko ga imasu.","german":"There is a cat.","furigana":"ねこ"},{"japanese":"この猫は魚が好きです。","romaji":"Kono neko wa sakana ga suki desu.","german":"This cat likes fish.","furigana":"ねこ|さかな|す"}],"notes":"猫 means a cat. As a living being, its existence is expressed with いる. 猫は魚が好きです has two roles: 猫は is the topic and 魚が is what is liked. Cats are usually counted with 匹.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 10"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鳥が飛んでいます。","romaji":"Tori ga tonde imasu.","german":"A bird is flying.","furigana":"とり|と"},{"japanese":"朝、鳥が鳴いています。","romaji":"Asa, tori ga naite imasu.","german":"Birds are singing in the morning.","furigana":"あさ|とり|な"}],"notes":"鳥 means bird. For flying, one says 鳥が飛ぶ; bird calls are normally described with 鳴く. Poultry meat is often written 鶏肉; this card deals with living birds. For animals of this kind, 羽 is a common counter.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 27"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日は何曜日ですか。","romaji":"Kyō wa nan'yōbi desu ka.","german":"What day of the week is it today?","furigana":"きょう|なんようび"},{"japanese":"今日はいい天気です。","romaji":"Kyō wa ii tenki desu.","german":"The weather is nice today.","furigana":"きょう|てんき"}],"notes":"今日（きょう）means today. As a simple time expression it normally needs no に; は highlights today as the topic. Before a noun it is connected with の: 今日の天気. The long reading きょう is written kyō.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"明日は日曜日です。","romaji":"Ashita wa nichiyōbi desu.","german":"Morgen ist Sonntag.","furigana":"あした|にちようび"},{"japanese":"明日は何をしますか。","romaji":"Ashita wa nani o shimasu ka.","german":"Was machen Sie morgen?","furigana":"あした|なに"}],"notes":"明日（あした）ist der Tag nach heute. Das deutsche „morgen“ ist hier nicht die Tageszeit Morgen, die 朝 heißt. Als Zeitangabe braucht 明日 normalerweise kein に; 明日は setzt die Planung des nächsten Tages als Thema.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"昨日映画を見ました。","romaji":"Kinō eiga o mimashita.","german":"I watched a movie yesterday.","furigana":"きのうえいが|み"},{"japanese":"昨日は忙しかったです。","romaji":"Kinō wa isogashikatta desu.","german":"I was busy yesterday.","furigana":"きのう|いそが"}],"notes":"昨日（きのう）means the day before today. Statements about completed actions are often in the past tense, as in 見ました. Adjectives also form a past tense: 忙しかった. The long o is written kinō.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"朝早く起きます。","romaji":"Asa hayaku okimasu.","german":"I get up early in the morning.","furigana":"あさはや|お"},{"japanese":"朝ご飯の前に顔を洗います。","romaji":"Asagohan no mae ni kao o araimasu.","german":"I wash my face before breakfast.","furigana":"あさ|はん|まえ|かお|あら"}],"notes":"朝 means the morning as a time of day, not the next day（明日）. It can stand directly as a time expression: 朝早く起きる. 朝ご飯 is breakfast; 朝ご飯の前に means before breakfast. The card uses the reading あさ.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"昼ご飯を食べます。","romaji":"Hirugohan o tabemasu.","german":"I eat lunch.","furigana":"ひる|はん|た"},{"japanese":"昼は暑いです。","romaji":"Hiru wa atsui desu.","german":"It is hot during the day.","furigana":"ひる|あつ"}],"notes":"昼 can mean the midday period or, more generally, the bright part of the day. 昼ご飯 means lunch. In contrast to 夜, 昼 is often \"during the day\", not just exactly twelve o'clock. The reading is ひる.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"夜テレビを見ます。","romaji":"Yoru terebi o mimasu.","german":"I watch TV in the evening.","furigana":"よる|み"},{"japanese":"夜は静かです。","romaji":"Yoru wa shizuka desu.","german":"It is quiet at night.","furigana":"よる|しず"}],"notes":"夜（よる）can mean the evening after dark or the night. As a time expression it can stand directly before the activity: 夜テレビを見る. 夜は highlights the night time as the topic. For \"every night/every evening\", 毎晩 is used.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 6"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今何時ですか。","romaji":"Ima nanji desu ka.","german":"What time is it now?","furigana":"いまなんじ"},{"japanese":"今テレビを見ています。","romaji":"Ima terebi o mite imasu.","german":"I am watching TV now.","furigana":"いま|み"}],"notes":"今 means now, the present moment. It is narrower than 今日 (\"today\"). 今何時ですか asks for the current time; 今～ています emphasizes an action in progress. Normally no に follows 今 as a time expression.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"毎日日本語を勉強します。","romaji":"Mainichi nihongo o benkyō shimasu.","german":"I study Japanese every day.","furigana":"まいにちにほんご|べんきょう"},{"japanese":"毎日散歩します。","romaji":"Mainichi sanpo shimasu.","german":"I take a walk every day.","furigana":"まいにちさんぽ"}],"notes":"毎日 means every day and describes regular repetition. It usually stands before the activity without に. A period within the day can be added, as in 毎日一時間 (\"one hour every day\"). 今日, by contrast, means only today.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"毎朝コーヒーを飲みます。","romaji":"Maiasa kōhī o nomimasu.","german":"I drink coffee every morning.","furigana":"まいあさ|の"},{"japanese":"毎朝六時に起きます。","romaji":"Maiasa rokuji ni okimasu.","german":"I get up at six every morning.","furigana":"まいあさろくじ|お"}],"notes":"毎朝 means every morning. It denotes repetition on each morning and is normally used without に. A specific time can be added with に: 毎朝六時に. The first あ in あさ is kept: まいあさ.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"毎晩本を読みます。","romaji":"Maiban hon o yomimasu.","german":"I read a book every evening.","furigana":"まいばんほん|よ"},{"japanese":"毎晩お風呂に入ります。","romaji":"Maiban ofuro ni hairimasu.","german":"I take a bath every evening.","furigana":"まいばん|ふろ|はい"}],"notes":"毎晩 means every evening or every night. It is a regular time expression that does not need に. 毎日, by contrast, covers the whole daily rhythm. 晩 is read ばん in this word; both examples describe recurring evening habits.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"毎週テニスをします。","romaji":"Maishū tenisu o shimasu.","german":"I play tennis every week.","furigana":"まいしゅう"},{"japanese":"毎週日曜日に買い物をします。","romaji":"Maishū nichiyōbi ni kaimono o shimasu.","german":"I go shopping every Sunday.","furigana":"まいしゅうにちようび|か|もの"}],"notes":"毎週 means every week and usually stands before an activity without に. You can add the day of the week: 毎週日曜日に (\"every week on Sunday\"). 毎日, by contrast, describes daily repetition; 週 is the week.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 8"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いつもここで食べます。","romaji":"Itsumo koko de tabemasu.","german":"I always eat here.","furigana":"た"},{"japanese":"いつもこの道を歩きます。","romaji":"Itsumo kono michi o arukimasu.","german":"I always walk along this street.","furigana":"みち|ある"}],"notes":"いつも means always or regularly as usual. It stands before the habit described, for example いつもここで食べる. 時々, by contrast, is only occasionally. The card deals with the frequency usage, not a place name read the same way. The kanji spelling 何時も is read いつも; the kana form is usual.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 6"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"時々映画を見ます。","romaji":"Tokidoki eiga o mimasu.","german":"I sometimes watch movies.","furigana":"ときどきえいが|み"},{"japanese":"時々レストランで食べます。","romaji":"Tokidoki resutoran de tabemasu.","german":"I sometimes eat at a restaurant.","furigana":"ときどき|た"}],"notes":"時々 means sometimes or from time to time, that is, not a daily or exceptionless habit. The character 々 repeats 時 in the spelling; the reading is ときどき. As a time adverb it normally stands without に.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 6"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"よく日本料理を食べます。","romaji":"Yoku nihon ryōri o tabemasu.","german":"I often eat Japanese food.","furigana":"にほんりょうり|た"},{"japanese":"日本語がよく分かります。","romaji":"Nihongo ga yoku wakarimasu.","german":"I understand Japanese well.","furigana":"にほんご|わ"}],"notes":"よく means \"often\" or \"well/thoroughly\", depending on the context. よく食べる can describe a frequent activity; よく分かる a good understanding. It is an adverb, not the predicate adjective いい. 欲 (\"desire\") and 翼 (\"wing\") are other words with the same reading.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 9"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"すぐ行きます。","romaji":"Sugu ikimasu.","german":"I will go right away.","furigana":"い"},{"japanese":"駅はすぐそこです。","romaji":"Eki wa sugu soko desu.","german":"The station is just over there.","furigana":"えき"}],"notes":"すぐ can mean \"immediately\" in time or \"close by\" in space. すぐ行く is to go at once; すぐそこ is right there, without much distance. The word here is an adverb, not the old verb すぐ (\"to pass by\").","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 14"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"まだ雨が降っています。","romaji":"Mada ame ga futte imasu.","german":"It is still raining.","furigana":"あめ|ふ"},{"japanese":"宿題がまだ終わっていません。","romaji":"Shukudai ga mada owatte imasen.","german":"My homework is not finished yet.","furigana":"しゅくだい|お"}],"notes":"まだ with an affirmative statement means \"still\": まだ雨が降っています. With a negated statement it means \"not yet\": まだ終わっていません. It signals that an expected change or completion has not yet occurred.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 7"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"もう食べました。","romaji":"Mō tabemashita.","german":"I have already eaten.","furigana":"た"},{"japanese":"もう帰りましょう。","romaji":"Mō kaerimashō.","german":"Let's head back now.","furigana":"かえ"},{"japanese":"もう食べません。","romaji":"Mō tabemasen.","german":"I won't eat any more.","furigana":"た"}],"notes":"もう denotes a point in time or a state that has already been reached: もう食べました. With a negation it means \"no longer\": もう食べません. It thus contrasts with まだ (\"still/not yet\"). もう帰りましょう means that it is time to go back now.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 7"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この花はとてもきれいです。","romaji":"Kono hana wa totemo kirei desu.","german":"This flower is very beautiful.","furigana":"はな"},{"japanese":"とても疲れたので、早く寝ます。","romaji":"Totemo tsukareta node, hayaku nemasu.","german":"I am very tired, so I will go to bed early.","furigana":"つか|はや|ね"}],"notes":"とても intensifies a gradable quality and means \"very\". It can be used with い- and な-adjectives and suitable expressions of state. It does not state a quantity of objects; たくさん is suitable for that. The stronger everyday form とっても is also common. 迚も is a rare kanji spelling of とても; the kana form is usual.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 8"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本語が少し分かります。","romaji":"Nihongo ga sukoshi wakarimasu.","german":"I understand a little Japanese.","furigana":"にほんご|すこ|わ"},{"japanese":"少し休みましょう。","romaji":"Sukoshi yasumimashō.","german":"Let's rest for a little while.","furigana":"すこ|やす"}],"notes":"少し can mean a small amount, a low degree or a short time. 少し分かる means to understand somewhat; 少し休む to take a short break. To describe a small amount of something as a quality, 少ない is used instead, as in お金が少ない.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 9"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"本をたくさん読みます。","romaji":"Hon o takusan yomimasu.","german":"I read a lot of books.","furigana":"ほん|よ"},{"japanese":"たくさん買い物をしました。","romaji":"Takusan kaimono o shimashita.","german":"I did a lot of shopping.","furigana":"か|もの"}],"notes":"たくさん means a large amount or number. It can directly accompany the action, as in 本をたくさん読む. Before a noun, の is often used: たくさんの本. The spelling 沢山 is possible; here the word is shown in kana.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 9"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"全部食べました。","romaji":"Zenbu tabemashita.","german":"I ate all of it.","furigana":"ぜんぶた"},{"japanese":"全部で千円です。","romaji":"Zenbu de sen en desu.","german":"It is 1,000 yen in total.","furigana":"ぜんぶ|せんえん"}],"notes":"全部 means all or the whole. It can sum up a whole quantity as the object of an action. 全部で indicates a total sum, such as a price. It should be distinguished from たくさん: \"a lot\" need not be \"all\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 24"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一緒に行きましょう。","romaji":"Issho ni ikimashō.","german":"Let's go together.","furigana":"いっしょ|い"},{"japanese":"友達と一緒に住んでいます。","romaji":"Tomodachi to issho ni sunde imasu.","german":"I live together with a friend.","furigana":"ともだち|いっしょ|す"}],"notes":"一緒に means together or jointly. Another person involved is added with と: 友達と一緒に. The に is part of the adverbial phrase taught here; it is not attached again in addition.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"りんごを一つください。","romaji":"Ringo o hitotsu kudasai.","german":"One apple, please.","furigana":"ひと"},{"japanese":"一つ選んでください。","romaji":"Hitotsu erande kudasai.","german":"Please choose one.","furigana":"ひと|えら"}],"notes":"一つ is the general counting form for one thing, often used when no special counter applies. It can follow the object: りんごを一つ. Before a noun it is connected with の: 一つのりんご. The reading is ひとつ, not いちつ.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 9"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"卵を二つ買います。","romaji":"Tamago o futatsu kaimasu.","german":"I buy two eggs.","furigana":"たまご|ふた|か"},{"japanese":"二つの道があります。","romaji":"Futatsu no michi ga arimasu.","german":"There are two roads.","furigana":"ふた|みち"}],"notes":"二つ counts two things with the general ～つ series. After an object the counter can stand without a particle: 卵を二つ買う. Before a noun, の is needed: 二つの道. The reading is ふたつ, not につ.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 9"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"箱が三つあります。","romaji":"Hako ga mittsu arimasu.","german":"There are three boxes.","furigana":"はこ|みっ"},{"japanese":"みかんを三つ食べました。","romaji":"Mikan o mittsu tabemashita.","german":"I ate three mandarins.","furigana":"みっ|た"}],"notes":"三つ counts three things in the general ～つ series. The small っ in みっつ is written as a double consonant in romaji: mittsu. The number can follow the counted thing with が or を: 箱が三つある, みかんを三つ食べる.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 9"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お金がありません。","romaji":"Okane ga arimasen.","german":"I have no money.","furigana":"かね"},{"japanese":"お金を使いすぎました。","romaji":"Okane o tsukaisugimashita.","german":"I spent too much money.","furigana":"かね|つか"}],"notes":"お金 is the usual word for money. The お belongs to the common polite word form; it is also used for one's own money. お金がある describes available money, お金を使う spending it. 円, by contrast, names the currency yen.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 7"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"切符を買います。","romaji":"Kippu o kaimasu.","german":"I buy a ticket.","furigana":"きっぷ|か"},{"japanese":"切符をなくしました。","romaji":"Kippu o nakushimashita.","german":"I lost my ticket.","furigana":"きっぷ"}],"notes":"切符 means a ticket, especially for transport. Paper tickets are counted with 枚: 切符を二枚. The small っ leads to a double p in romanization: kippu. Do not confuse it with 切手 (\"postage stamp\").","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 7"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"切手をください。","romaji":"Kitte o kudasai.","german":"A stamp, please.","furigana":"きって"},{"japanese":"この切手はきれいですね。","romaji":"Kono kitte wa kirei desu ne.","german":"This stamp is pretty, isn't it?","furigana":"きって"}],"notes":"切手 means a postage stamp for mailing. The common combination 切手を貼る means to stick on a stamp. As flat pieces of paper, they are counted with 枚. The reading きって has a small っ; 切符（きっぷ）, by contrast, is a ticket.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 11"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"手紙を書きます。","romaji":"Tegami o kakimasu.","german":"I write a letter.","furigana":"てがみ|か"},{"japanese":"友達から手紙が来ました。","romaji":"Tomodachi kara tegami ga kimashita.","german":"A letter has come from my friend.","furigana":"ともだち|てがみ|き"}],"notes":"手紙 is a letter or written message, normally a personal one. 手紙を書く means to write a letter. When receiving, から names the sender: 友達から手紙が来る. 手紙 does not stand for a postage stamp or ticket.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 6"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お名前は何ですか。","romaji":"Onamae wa nan desu ka.","german":"What is your name?","furigana":"なまえ|なん"},{"japanese":"この花の名前を知っていますか。","romaji":"Kono hana no namae o shitte imasu ka.","german":"Do you know the name of this flower?","furigana":"はな|なまえ|し"}],"notes":"名前 means a name in general and, depending on the context, also a first name. お名前 is the polite form when asking another person. With の you name whose or what's name is meant: この花の名前.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 14"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本語の言葉を覚えます。","romaji":"Nihongo no kotoba o oboemasu.","german":"Ich lerne japanische Wörter.","furigana":"にほんご|ことば|おぼ"},{"japanese":"この言葉の読み方を教えてください。","romaji":"Kono kotoba no yomikata o oshiete kudasai.","german":"Bitte sagen Sie mir, wie man dieses Wort liest.","furigana":"ことば|よ|かた|おし"},{"japanese":"日本とドイツでは、言葉が違います。","romaji":"Nihon to Doitsu de wa, kotoba ga chigaimasu.","german":"In Japan und Deutschland spricht man unterschiedliche Sprachen.","furigana":"にほん|ことば|ちが"}],"notes":"言葉 kann ein einzelnes Wort, einen Ausdruck oder eine Sprache bezeichnen. この言葉の読み方 fragt nach der Lesung eines Wortes. Wenn man sagt, dass Länder verschiedene 言葉 haben, meint man ihre Sprachen. の verbindet Wort und seine Lesung.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 13"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この言葉の意味は何ですか。","romaji":"Kono kotoba no imi wa nan desu ka.","german":"What does this word mean?","furigana":"ことば|いみ|なん"},{"japanese":"この漢字の意味がわかりません。","romaji":"Kono kanji no imi ga wakarimasen.","german":"I don't understand the meaning of this kanji.","furigana":"かんじ|いみ"}],"notes":"意味 is the meaning or sense of a word, character or statement. この言葉の意味は何ですか asks for an explanation. When understanding, 意味 takes が with 分かる. It is not the same as 読み方, the pronunciation or reading.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 24"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"質問がありますか。","romaji":"Shitsumon ga arimasu ka.","german":"Do you have any questions?","furigana":"しつもん"},{"japanese":"先生に質問してもいいですか。","romaji":"Sensei ni shitsumon shite mo ii desu ka.","german":"May I ask the teacher a question?","furigana":"せんせい|しつもん"}],"notes":"質問 is a question asked of someone. 質問する means to ask a question. The person asked is attached with に: 先生に質問する. 質問がありますか asks whether someone has a question; it is not a school exercise item like 問題.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"答えを書いてください。","romaji":"Kotae o kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write the answer.","furigana":"こた|か"},{"japanese":"この問題の答えがわかりません。","romaji":"Kono mondai no kotae ga wakarimasen.","german":"I don't know the answer to this problem.","furigana":"もんだい|こた"}],"notes":"答え is an answer and, for problems, also the solution. The combination 答えを書く means to write down the answer. This noun has no verb conjugation; the related verb is 答える. 問題の答え means the solution to a problem.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 14"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"問題はありません。","romaji":"Mondai wa arimasen.","german":"There is no problem.","furigana":"もんだい"},{"japanese":"この問題は難しいです。","romaji":"Kono mondai wa muzukashii desu.","german":"This question is difficult.","furigana":"もんだい|むずか"}],"notes":"問題 can mean a practical problem or a question or exercise on an exam. 問題はありません means \"there is no problem\". この問題は難しい can mean a difficult school problem. A question put to someone is usually called 質問.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 14"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"明日テストがあります。","romaji":"Ashita tesuto ga arimasu.","german":"There is a test tomorrow.","furigana":"あした"},{"japanese":"テストは来週の月曜日です。","romaji":"Tesuto wa raishū no getsuyōbi desu.","german":"The test is next Monday.","furigana":"らいしゅう|げつようび"},{"japanese":"テストで百点を取りました。","romaji":"Tesuto de hyakuten o torimashita.","german":"I got a hundred points on the test.","furigana":"ひゃくてん|と"}],"notes":"テスト means a test or an examination of knowledge and skills here. テストがある announces an exam date; テストを受ける means \"to take an exam\". The result can be given with 点, such as 百点 \"a hundred points\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"宿題をしましたか。","romaji":"Shukudai o shimashita ka.","german":"Did you do your homework?","furigana":"しゅくだい"},{"japanese":"宿題を忘れてしまいました。","romaji":"Shukudai o wasurete shimaimashita.","german":"I forgot my homework.","furigana":"しゅくだい|わす"}],"notes":"宿題 means homework or an assigned task; Japanese needs no plural ending for it. 宿題をする means \"to do homework\". 宿題を忘れる can mean that one did not do it or did not bring the material; the context decides.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 6"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本語の勉強は楽しいです。","romaji":"Nihongo no benkyō wa tanoshii desu.","german":"Studying Japanese is fun.","furigana":"にほんご|べんきょう|たの"},{"japanese":"毎日二時間勉強します。","romaji":"Mainichi nijikan benkyō shimasu.","german":"I study for two hours every day.","furigana":"まいにちにじかんべんきょう"}],"notes":"勉強 is the noun for studying or learning; with する it becomes \"to study\". The subject studied can stand with の, as in 日本語の勉強, or with を before 勉強する. 二時間勉強する gives the duration; for a fixed point in time, に is used instead.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 8"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仕事が終わりました。","romaji":"Shigoto ga owarimashita.","german":"The work is finished.","furigana":"しごと|お"},{"japanese":"仕事が終わってから帰ります。","romaji":"Shigoto ga owatte kara kaerimasu.","german":"I will go home after work is finished.","furigana":"しごと|お|かえ"},{"japanese":"今、新しい仕事を探しています。","romaji":"Ima, atarashii shigoto o sagashite imasu.","german":"I am looking for a new job now.","furigana":"いま|あたら|しごと|さが"}],"notes":"仕事 can mean a concrete job, a task, or one's occupation. 仕事をする means \"to work\" and 仕事が終わる \"the work ends\". 新しい仕事を探す usually means looking for a new position; for a single task, the context is decisive.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"毎日練習します。","romaji":"Mainichi renshū shimasu.","german":"I practice every day.","furigana":"まいにちれんしゅう"},{"japanese":"ピアノの練習をしています。","romaji":"Piano no renshū o shite imasu.","german":"I am practicing the piano.","furigana":"れんしゅう"},{"japanese":"練習の後で水を飲みます。","romaji":"Renshū no ato de mizu o nomimasu.","german":"I drink water after practice.","furigana":"れんしゅう|あと|みず|の"}],"notes":"練習 is practice or training to improve a skill. 練習する means \"to practice\"; the practice subject often stands with を or の, as in ピアノを練習する, ピアノの練習. It denotes targeted repetition and application; general studying is often 勉強.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 19"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"右に曲がってください。","romaji":"Migi ni magatte kudasai.","german":"Please turn right.","furigana":"みぎ|ま"},{"japanese":"次の角を右に曲がってください。","romaji":"Tsugi no kado o migi ni magatte kudasai.","german":"Please turn right at the next corner.","furigana":"つぎ|かど|みぎ|ま"},{"japanese":"右のポケットに鍵があります。","romaji":"Migi no poketto ni kagi ga arimasu.","german":"There is a key in the right pocket.","furigana":"みぎ|かぎ"}],"notes":"右 is a location and direction noun: 右に曲がる \"to turn right\". Before another noun, の is used, as in 右のポケット \"the right pocket\". \"On the right side of ...\" is ～の右に. The direction depends on the point of view; the opposite is 左.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 10"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"左に行ってください。","romaji":"Hidari ni itte kudasai.","german":"Please go left.","furigana":"ひだり|い"},{"japanese":"左の道をまっすぐ行ってください。","romaji":"Hidari no michi o massugu itte kudasai.","german":"Please go straight along the left road.","furigana":"ひだり|みち|い"},{"japanese":"郵便局は銀行の左にあります。","romaji":"Yūbinkyoku wa ginkō no hidari ni arimasu.","german":"The post office is to the left of the bank.","furigana":"ゆうびんきょく|ぎんこう|ひだり"}],"notes":"左 means the left side or direction. 左に行く means \"to go left\" and 左の道 \"the left road\". For position relative to something, ～の左に is used. The opposite is 右; the word is a noun and often needs の for further specification.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 10"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"机の上にあります。","romaji":"Tsukue no ue ni arimasu.","german":"It is on the desk.","furigana":"つくえ|うえ"},{"japanese":"テーブルの上に本があります。","romaji":"Tēburu no ue ni hon ga arimasu.","german":"There is a book on the table.","furigana":"うえ|ほん"},{"japanese":"上を見てください。","romaji":"Ue o mite kudasai.","german":"Please look up.","furigana":"うえ|み"}],"notes":"上（うえ）is a location noun here meaning \"above, over, on\". \"On the desk\" is 机の上; with に you describe the place of a thing, with で the place of an action. 上を見る means \"to look up\". Other readings of 上, such as じょう in compounds, are to be learned separately.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 10"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"机の下にあります。","romaji":"Tsukue no shita ni arimasu.","german":"It is under the desk.","furigana":"つくえ|した"},{"japanese":"机の下に猫がいます。","romaji":"Tsukue no shita ni neko ga imasu.","german":"There is a cat under the desk.","furigana":"つくえ|した|ねこ"},{"japanese":"下の階で待っています。","romaji":"Shita no kai de matte imasu.","german":"I am waiting on the floor below.","furigana":"した|かい|ま"}],"notes":"下（した）here means the area under something or a lower position. 机の下に means \"under the desk\"; for an action taking place there, で is used. 下の階 is the floor below. The word is a noun, not an i-adjective; other readings such as げ belong to other combinations.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 10"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"かばんの中にあります。","romaji":"Kaban no naka ni arimasu.","german":"It is in the bag.","furigana":"なか"},{"japanese":"箱の中に何がありますか。","romaji":"Hako no naka ni nani ga arimasu ka.","german":"What is in the box?","furigana":"はこ|なか|なに"},{"japanese":"雨なので、家の中で遊びます。","romaji":"Ame na node, ie no naka de asobimasu.","german":"Since it is raining, I will play inside the house.","furigana":"あめ|いえ|なか|あそ"}],"notes":"中（なか）here means the inside of a container or room: かばんの中, 家の中. For the place where one is, に is used, and for an action carried out there, で. The readings ちゅう or じゅう occur in other combinations, such as 勉強中 or 一日中; they are not the reading of the location noun practiced here.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 10"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"外は寒いです。","romaji":"Soto wa samui desu.","german":"It is cold outside.","furigana":"そと|さむ"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"靴が汚れているので、家の外で脱いでください。","romaji":"Kutsu ga yogorete iru node, ie no soto de nuide kudasai.","german":"The shoes are dirty, so please take them off outside the house.","furigana":"くつ|よご|いえ|そと|ぬ"}],"notes":"A location noun: 外に出る denotes movement outside, 外で遊ぶ an action outdoors. 建物の外 denotes the area outside a building. The reading ほか belongs to another use and is not treated here as an equivalent reading.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 10"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"駅の前にいます。","romaji":"Eki no mae ni imasu.","german":"I am in front of the station.","furigana":"えき|まえ"},{"japanese":"駅の前で待っています。","romaji":"Eki no mae de matte imasu.","german":"I am waiting in front of the station.","furigana":"えき|まえ|ま"},{"japanese":"寝る前に本を読みます。","romaji":"Neru mae ni hon o yomimasu.","german":"I read a book before going to bed.","furigana":"ね|まえ|ほん|よ"}],"notes":"前（まえ）can mean \"in front of, ahead\" spatially and \"before\" in time. 駅の前 denotes the area in front of the station. For the time before an activity, the plain verb form comes before 前に: 寝る前に \"before going to bed\". With a noun, の connects, as in 食事の前に.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 10"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"後ろを見てください。","romaji":"Ushiro o mite kudasai.","german":"Please look behind you.","furigana":"うし|み"},{"japanese":"学校の後ろに公園があります。","romaji":"Gakkō no ushiro ni kōen ga arimasu.","german":"There is a park behind the school.","furigana":"がっこう|うし|こうえん"}],"notes":"後ろ（うしろ）is the spatial back or the area behind something. ～の後ろに means \"behind ...\" and 後ろを見る \"to look behind\". For \"afterwards\" or \"after an activity\", 後（あと）is usually used, as in 食事の後で; that is a different reading and use. The spelling 後 without ろ is also recorded for the same reading; it deviates from the usual okurigana spelling 後ろ. Here the meaning remains spatial \"back, behind\"; a future point in time is not meant.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"隣に誰がいますか。","romaji":"Tonari ni dare ga imasu ka.","german":"Who is next door?","furigana":"となり|だれ"},{"japanese":"隣の人に聞いてください。","romaji":"Tonari no hito ni kiite kudasai.","german":"Please ask the person next to you.","furigana":"となり|ひと|き"}],"notes":"隣（となり）denotes the person, thing or apartment immediately adjacent. 隣の人 means \"the person next to me/next door\" and ～の隣に \"right next to ...\". 隣 alone can stand for the house next door. 近く, by contrast, denotes closeness in general and does not require immediate adjacency.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 10"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"駅の近くに住んでいます。","romaji":"Eki no chikaku ni sunde imasu.","german":"I live near the station.","furigana":"えき|ちか|す"},{"japanese":"この近くにコンビニがありますか。","romaji":"Kono chikaku ni konbini ga arimasu ka.","german":"Is there a convenience store nearby?","furigana":"ちか"}],"notes":"近く is a location noun here for the vicinity or surroundings. 駅の近くに means \"near the station\" and この近く \"near here\". Unlike 隣, the place need not be directly adjacent. 近い is the related i-adjective for \"near\"; the form 近く practiced here is used as a noun.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 10"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"家と駅の間にあります。","romaji":"Ie to eki no aida ni arimasu.","german":"It is between the house and the station.","furigana":"いえ|えき|あいだ"},{"japanese":"東京と大阪の間を新幹線で移動します。","romaji":"Tōkyō to Ōsaka no aida o shinkansen de idō shimasu.","german":"I travel between Tokyo and Osaka by Shinkansen.","furigana":"とうきょう|おおさか|あいだ|しんかんせん|いどう"},{"japanese":"授業の間は静かにしてください。","romaji":"Jugyō no aida wa shizuka ni shite kudasai.","german":"Please be quiet during class.","furigana":"じゅぎょう|あいだ|しず"}],"notes":"間（あいだ）denotes a spatial gap or a span of time. Between two things is ～と～の間; \"during class\" is 授業の間. 間は usually covers the whole duration, while 間に highlights an event within that time. The reading ま denotes other uses, such as a pause or gap.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 10"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"春は暖かいです。","romaji":"Haru wa atatakai desu.","german":"It is warm in spring.","furigana":"はる|あたた"},{"japanese":"春になると桜が咲きます。","romaji":"Haru ni naru to sakura ga sakimasu.","german":"When spring comes, the cherry trees bloom.","furigana":"はる|さくら|さ"}],"notes":"春 is spring as a season. In general statements 春は can be used; 春になる means \"it becomes spring\". For an action in this season, 春に is also possible. The related 春休み（はるやすみ）means spring vacation.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 12"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"夏は暑いです。","romaji":"Natsu wa atsui desu.","german":"It is hot in summer.","furigana":"なつ|あつ"},{"japanese":"夏は暑いですから、海に行きます。","romaji":"Natsu wa atsui desu kara, umi ni ikimasu.","german":"It is hot in summer, so I go to the sea.","furigana":"なつ|あつ|うみ|い"}],"notes":"夏 is summer. 夏は暑いです describes the typical weather; 夏に海へ行く an activity in the season. 夏休み（なつやすみ）is the summer vacation. 暑い is written for hot weather; 熱い, by contrast, is used for hot water or hot objects, for example.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 12"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"秋は涼しいです。","romaji":"Aki wa suzushii desu.","german":"It is cool in autumn.","furigana":"あき|すず"},{"japanese":"秋は紅葉がきれいです。","romaji":"Aki wa kōyō ga kirei desu.","german":"The autumn leaves are beautiful in autumn.","furigana":"あき|こうよう"}],"notes":"秋 is autumn. 秋は涼しいです describes the season in general; 秋に旅行する names an activity in autumn. 紅葉（こうよう）here denotes the autumn coloring of leaves, not just foliage without a color change.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 12"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"冬は寒いです。","romaji":"Fuyu wa samui desu.","german":"It is cold in winter.","furigana":"ふゆ|さむ"},{"japanese":"冬はスキーに行きたいです。","romaji":"Fuyu wa sukī ni ikitai desu.","german":"I want to go skiing in winter.","furigana":"ふゆ|い"}],"notes":"冬 is winter. 冬は寒いです describes the typical weather; 冬にスキーに行く names an outing in this season. 冬休み（ふゆやすみ）is the winter vacation. 寒い denotes the cold of the surroundings, while 冷たい denotes, for example, the coldness of a touched object.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 12"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"月がきれいです。","romaji":"Tsuki ga kirei desu.","german":"The moon is beautiful.","furigana":"つき"},{"japanese":"月がきれいですね。","romaji":"Tsuki ga kirei desu ne.","german":"The moon is beautiful, isn't it?","furigana":"つき"},{"japanese":"次の月は、三月です。","romaji":"Tsugi no tsuki wa, sangatsu desu.","german":"The next month is March.","furigana":"つぎ|つき|さんがつ"}],"notes":"月（つき）means the moon and, as an independent noun, can also mean a month. Calendar months, by contrast, are named with ～月（がつ）, such as 三月（さんがつ）\"March\". For a counted duration, ～か月（かげつ）is usual. These forms have different readings from the つき practiced here.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 21"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この年は雨が多かったです。","romaji":"Kono toshi wa ame ga ōkatta desu.","german":"It rained a lot this year.","furigana":"とし|あめ|おお"},{"japanese":"年が変わりました。","romaji":"Toshi ga kawarimashita.","german":"A new year has begun.","furigana":"とし|か"}],"notes":"年（とし）is the independent noun for a year and can also denote age, as in 年を取る \"to grow older\". Calendar years and counted years mostly use ねん, such as 二〇二六年（にせんにじゅうろくねん）. That reading does not belong to the independent form とし practiced here.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"来週テストがあります。","romaji":"Raishū tesuto ga arimasu.","german":"There is a test next week.","furigana":"らいしゅう"},{"japanese":"一週間に三回運動します。","romaji":"Isshūkan ni sankai undō shimasu.","german":"I exercise three times a week.","furigana":"いっしゅうかん|さんかいうんどう"}],"notes":"週 is the week. Compounds such as 来週（らいしゅう）refer to a specific week; 一週間（いっしゅうかん）is a duration of one week. 一週間に三回 means \"three times a week\". The doubled consonant in いっしゅうかん is part of the pronunciation of this counting form.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"午前九時に始まります。","romaji":"Gozen kuji ni hajimarimasu.","german":"It starts at nine in the morning.","furigana":"ごぜんくじ|はじ"},{"japanese":"午前中に買い物に行きます。","romaji":"Gozenchū ni kaimono ni ikimasu.","german":"I go shopping in the morning.","furigana":"ごぜんちゅう|か|もの|い"}],"notes":"午前 denotes the morning and, in clock times, the period from midnight until before noon, that is, a.m. 午前九時 is 9 a.m.; 午前二時 would be 2 a.m. 午前中 means the morning as a period. The opposite term for clock times is 午後.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"午後三時に会いましょう。","romaji":"Gogo sanji ni aimashō.","german":"Let's meet at three in the afternoon.","furigana":"ごごさんじ|あ"},{"japanese":"午後から授業があります。","romaji":"Gogo kara jugyō ga arimasu.","german":"I have class from the afternoon.","furigana":"ごご|じゅぎょう"}],"notes":"午後 denotes the afternoon and, in clock times, the period from noon until before midnight, that is, p.m. 午後三時 is 3 p.m. (15:00); 午後八時 is 8 p.m. (20:00) and falls in the evening. 午後から means \"from the afternoon\". The opposite term for clock times is 午前.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"誕生日はいつですか。","romaji":"Tanjōbi wa itsu desu ka.","german":"When is your birthday?","furigana":"たんじょうび"},{"japanese":"誕生日にケーキを食べます。","romaji":"Tanjōbi ni kēki o tabemasu.","german":"I eat cake on my birthday.","furigana":"たんじょうび|た"}],"notes":"誕生日 denotes the yearly recurring birthday. For an event on this day, に is used: 誕生日にケーキを食べる. With いつですか you ask for the date. 日 is read び in this word, not ひ.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"明日は休みです。","romaji":"Ashita wa yasumi desu.","german":"I have tomorrow off.","furigana":"あした|やす"},{"japanese":"授業と授業の間に十分の休みがあります。","romaji":"Jugyō to jugyō no aida ni juppun no yasumi ga arimasu.","german":"There is a ten-minute break between classes.","furigana":"じゅぎょう|じゅぎょう|あいだ|じゅっぷん|やす"}],"notes":"休み is a noun for a break, free time or vacation. 休みです means \"I have the day off\" or \"it is closed\", depending on the context. A short break is also 休み, for example during class. The related verb is 休む, not *休みる.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"何色が好きですか。","romaji":"Naniiro ga suki desu ka.","german":"What color do you like?","furigana":"なにいろ|す"},{"japanese":"車の色は青です。","romaji":"Kuruma no iro wa ao desu.","german":"The color of the car is blue.","furigana":"くるま|いろ|あお"}],"notes":"色 means a color. 何色 asks \"what color\" and is read なにいろ in this sentence. With の you name the object: 車の色. Blue as a color name is the noun 青; 青い is the related い-adjective.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 9"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい服を買いました。","romaji":"Atarashii fuku o kaimashita.","german":"I bought new clothes.","furigana":"あたら|ふく|か"},{"japanese":"服を洗います。","romaji":"Fuku o araimasu.","german":"I wash clothes.","furigana":"ふく|あら"}],"notes":"服 means clothes and in daily life often refers to Western-style garments. 服を着る means to get dressed or wear clothes, 服を洗う to wash them. It can mean several items without the Japanese noun getting a special plural form.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 15"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"靴を脱いでください。","romaji":"Kutsu o nuide kudasai.","german":"Please take off your shoes.","furigana":"くつ|ぬ"},{"japanese":"この靴はちょっと小さいです。","romaji":"Kono kutsu wa chotto chīsai desu.","german":"These shoes are a little small.","furigana":"くつ|ちい"}],"notes":"靴 means shoes or footwear. To put them on, 履く is used, and to take them off, 脱ぐ. Shoes as a pair are counted with 足（そく）, but the word itself needs no separate plural form. この靴は小さい can mean that the shoes do not fit.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"帽子をかぶります。","romaji":"Bōshi o kaburimasu.","german":"I put on a hat.","furigana":"ぼうし"},{"japanese":"今日は帽子をかぶっています。","romaji":"Kyō wa bōshi o kabutte imasu.","german":"I am wearing a hat today.","furigana":"きょう|ぼうし"}],"notes":"帽子 covers hats and caps. For headwear, かぶる is used, not the general 着る for clothing. 帽子をかぶります denotes putting it on; 帽子をかぶっています the subsequent wearing as a state.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 22"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"メガネをかけています。","romaji":"Megane o kakete imasu.","german":"I am wearing glasses."},{"japanese":"メガネをかけている人は田中さんです。","romaji":"Megane o kakete iru hito wa Tanaka-san desu.","german":"The person wearing glasses is Mr. Tanaka.","furigana":"ひと|たなか"}],"notes":"メガネ means glasses; the spelling 眼鏡 is also common. The matching verb is かける: メガネをかける. ～かけています describes wearing. A person can be identified by メガネをかけている人 (\"the person who is wearing glasses\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"体に気をつけてください。","romaji":"Karada ni ki o tsukete kudasai.","german":"Take care of your health.","furigana":"からだ|き"},{"japanese":"運動は体にいいです。","romaji":"Undō wa karada ni ii desu.","german":"Exercise is good for the body.","furigana":"うんどう|からだ"}],"notes":"体（からだ）is the body and, in a health context, can mean one's physical condition. 体に気をつける means to take care of one's health, 体にいい \"good for the body\". The card uses からだ; other kanji readings in compounds are not equated with it. 身体 can also be read からだ; this special whole-word reading is treated as gikun. There is also the reading しんたい; this card continues to use からだ.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 16"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"頭が痛いです。","romaji":"Atama ga itai desu.","german":"I have a headache.","furigana":"あたま|いた"},{"japanese":"頭を下げました。","romaji":"Atama o sagemashita.","german":"I bowed my head.","furigana":"あたま|さ"}],"notes":"頭（あたま）means the head here. 頭が痛い is the usual way to say you have a headache. 頭を下げる means to lower one's head and, in the right context, can describe a bow. This card deals neither with a leader nor with a down payment.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 16"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"顔を洗います。","romaji":"Kao o araimasu.","german":"I wash my face.","furigana":"かお|あら"},{"japanese":"彼女の顔を覚えています。","romaji":"Kanojo no kao o oboete imasu.","german":"I remember her face.","furigana":"かのじょ|かお|おぼ"}],"notes":"顔 means face. 顔を洗う is to wash one's face. Faces can also be recognized or remembered, as in 顔を覚えている. Meanings such as reputation or influence are figurative uses and are not taught here.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 16"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"目が大きいです。","romaji":"Me ga ōkii desu.","german":"My eyes are big.","furigana":"め|おお"},{"japanese":"目を閉じてください。","romaji":"Me o tojite kudasai.","german":"Please close your eyes.","furigana":"め|と"}],"notes":"目（め）means one eye or both eyes; the number is understood from the context. 目を閉じる means to close one's eyes. The kanji also serves as an ordinal suffix, as in 二つ目, but this card deals with the body part.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 16"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"耳が痛いです。","romaji":"Mimi ga itai desu.","german":"My ear hurts.","furigana":"みみ|いた"},{"japanese":"耳をふさいでください。","romaji":"Mimi o fusaide kudasai.","german":"Please cover your ears.","furigana":"みみ"}],"notes":"耳 means ear. For earache, 耳が痛い is used; when covering, the ear is the object with を. 耳 can mean both ears without a plural marker. Meanings such as the crust of bread belong to other uses.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 16"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"口を開けてください。","romaji":"Kuchi o akete kudasai.","german":"Please open your mouth.","furigana":"くち|あ"},{"japanese":"口を閉じて食べてください。","romaji":"Kuchi o tojite tabete kudasai.","german":"Please eat with your mouth closed.","furigana":"くち|と|た"}],"notes":"口（くち）means the mouth here. Opening is 口を開ける, closing 口を閉じる. It appears with を as the body part being moved. Openings of objects or the activity of talking are other uses that the card does not treat as the basic meaning.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 16"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"手を洗ってください。","romaji":"Te o aratte kudasai.","german":"Please wash your hands.","furigana":"て|あら"},{"japanese":"右手を上げてください。","romaji":"Migite o agete kudasai.","german":"Please raise your right hand.","furigana":"みぎて|あ"}],"notes":"手（て）means a hand; the form can also mean several hands. 右手 and 左手 mean the right and left hand. 手を洗う is to wash one's hands, 手を上げる to raise a hand. Other meanings such as method are not meant here.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 7"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"足が痛いです。","romaji":"Ashi ga itai desu.","german":"My foot hurts.","furigana":"あし|いた"},{"japanese":"彼は足が長いです。","romaji":"Kare wa ashi ga nagai desu.","german":"He has long legs.","furigana":"かれ|あし|なが"}],"notes":"足（あし）can mean a foot or the whole leg; the context decides. 足が痛い can therefore describe foot or leg pain, depending on the body area meant. For the leg, 脚 is also common. 足が長い normally refers to long legs.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 16"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お腹がすきました。","romaji":"Onaka ga sukimashita.","german":"I'm hungry.","furigana":"なか"},{"japanese":"お腹が痛いです。","romaji":"Onaka ga itai desu.","german":"I have a stomachache.","furigana":"なか|いた"}],"notes":"お腹（おなか）means stomach or belly. The usual polite form with お is also used for one's own belly. お腹がすく means to get hungry, お腹が痛い to have a stomachache. The reading is the whole-word form おなか, not an arbitrary combination of other 腹 readings.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"背が高いです。","romaji":"Se ga takai desu.","german":"He/She is tall.","furigana":"せ|たか"},{"japanese":"壁に背を向けて座ります。","romaji":"Kabe ni se o mukete suwarimasu.","german":"I sit with my back to the wall.","furigana":"かべ|せ|む|すわ"}],"notes":"背（せ）can mean back or body height. 背が高い means physically tall; 背が低い short. In 背を向ける, by contrast, the back is meant. For the body's back, the longer word 背中（せなか）is also used.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 16"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"声が大きいです。","romaji":"Koe ga ōkii desu.","german":"The voice is loud.","furigana":"こえ|おお"},{"japanese":"あの歌手はいい声ですね。","romaji":"Ano kashu wa ii koe desu ne.","german":"That singer has a nice voice.","furigana":"かしゅ|こえ"}],"notes":"声 means voice. 声が大きい means a loud voice, not its physical size. いい声 is a pleasant or beautiful voice. The examples concern people; general noises are often called 音.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 27"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"薬を飲んでください。","romaji":"Kusuri o nonde kudasai.","german":"Please take your medicine.","furigana":"くすり|の"},{"japanese":"薬を買いに薬局へ行きました。","romaji":"Kusuri o kai ni yakkyoku e ikimashita.","german":"I went to the pharmacy to buy medicine.","furigana":"くすり|か|やっきょく|い"}],"notes":"薬 is medicine or a medication. For tablets and other oral medication one says 薬を飲む; an ointment is applied with 塗る. 薬局 is the pharmacy. The card deals with medicines, not illegal drugs or other chemicals.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 17"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"病気になりました。","romaji":"Byōki ni narimashita.","german":"I got sick.","furigana":"びょうき"},{"japanese":"病気の時は休んでください。","romaji":"Byōki no toki wa yasunde kudasai.","german":"When you are sick, please rest.","furigana":"びょうき|とき|やす"}],"notes":"病気 means illness and, with です, can also express \"to be sick\". 病気になる means to become ill. Before the noun 時, の is used: 病気の時 (\"when one is sick\"). The word is not an い-adjective; forms like *病気い are wrong.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 17"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どの国から来ましたか。","romaji":"Dono kuni kara kimashita ka.","german":"Which country do you come from?","furigana":"くに|き"},{"japanese":"どの国に行きたいですか。","romaji":"Dono kuni ni ikitai desu ka.","german":"Which country do you want to go to?","furigana":"くに|い"}],"notes":"国（くに）means a country or state here. どの国 asks \"which country\". With から you name the country of origin, with に a travel destination. The word does not automatically mean a city or a state within any given country.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 7"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この町は静かです。","romaji":"Kono machi wa shizuka desu.","german":"This town is quiet.","furigana":"まち|しず"},{"japanese":"この町には駅がありません。","romaji":"Kono machi ni wa eki ga arimasen.","german":"There is no station in this town.","furigana":"まち|えき"}],"notes":"町（まち）can mean a town, a smaller place or a neighborhood. The exact administrative category does not follow from the word alone. The location of a place is given with に; 町には can introduce a statement about existing facilities. 街 is another recorded spelling of まち; this card practices the place and neighborhood sense.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 8"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"公園で遊びます。","romaji":"Kōen de asobimasu.","german":"I play in the park.","furigana":"こうえん|あそ"},{"japanese":"公園は駅の隣にあります。","romaji":"Kōen wa eki no tonari ni arimasu.","german":"The park is next to the station.","furigana":"こうえん|えき|となり"}],"notes":"公園 is a public park. An activity in the park takes で: 公園で遊ぶ. For its location, に with ある is used, as in 駅の隣にある. The place itself is not a living being and therefore takes ある instead of いる.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 10"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"プールで泳ぎます。","romaji":"Pūru de oyogimasu.","german":"I swim in the pool.","furigana":"およ"},{"japanese":"夏はプールに行きます。","romaji":"Natsu wa pūru ni ikimasu.","german":"In summer I go to the pool.","furigana":"なつ|い"}],"notes":"プール means a swimming pool or the swimming pool as a facility here. For swimming, で is used; for a visit as a destination, に. Identically written meanings for pool billiards or pooled resources are not meant here; the long u is written pūru.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 13"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"教室に入ってください。","romaji":"Kyōshitsu ni haitte kudasai.","german":"Please enter the classroom.","furigana":"きょうしつ|はい"},{"japanese":"教室に学生が二十人います。","romaji":"Kyōshitsu ni gakusei ga nijūnin imasu.","german":"There are twenty students in the classroom.","furigana":"きょうしつ|がくせい|にじゅうにん"}],"notes":"教室 means a classroom or teaching room here. For entering and for the location of people present, に is used. Classes as a course or university departments are further meanings but not the rooms of these examples. きょうしつ has a long o.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"黒板を見てください。","romaji":"Kokuban o mite kudasai.","german":"Please look at the blackboard.","furigana":"こくばん|み"},{"japanese":"先生が黒板に字を書きます。","romaji":"Sensei ga kokuban ni ji o kakimasu.","german":"The teacher writes characters on the blackboard.","furigana":"せんせい|こくばん|じ|か"}],"notes":"黒板 is the board people usually write on with chalk. 黒板に字を書く means \"to write characters on the blackboard\": に marks the surface on which the writing appears. For a white writing board, ホワイトボード is usual; 黒板 does not refer to just any board.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地図を見ます。","romaji":"Chizu o mimasu.","german":"I look at the map.","furigana":"ちず|み"},{"japanese":"地図を見て道を探しましょう。","romaji":"Chizu o mite michi o sagashimashō.","german":"Let's look at the map and find the way.","furigana":"ちず|み|みち|さが"},{"japanese":"駅から家までの地図をかきます。","romaji":"Eki kara ie made no chizu o kakimasu.","german":"I draw a map from the station to my house.","furigana":"えき|いえ|ちず"}],"notes":"地図 is a map or plan for spatial orientation; a city map can also be called that. 地図を見る means \"to look at the map\", 地図で調べる \"to check using the map\". A drawn route map is also called 地図, not just a map of a whole country.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 14"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"テレビを見ます。","romaji":"Terebi o mimasu.","german":"I watch TV.","furigana":"み"},{"japanese":"テレビのニュースを見ました。","romaji":"Terebi no nyūsu o mimashita.","german":"I watched the news on TV.","furigana":"み"},{"japanese":"新しいテレビを買いました。","romaji":"Atarashii terebi o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a new TV.","furigana":"あたら|か"}],"notes":"テレビ can mean the television set or television as a medium. テレビを買う means \"to buy a TV\", テレビを見る \"to watch TV\". テレビのニュース is TV news. テレビをつける and テレビを消す are about switching the set on and off.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"パソコンを使います。","romaji":"Pasokon o tsukaimasu.","german":"I use the computer.","furigana":"つか"},{"japanese":"パソコンでメールを送ります。","romaji":"Pasokon de mēru o okurimasu.","german":"I send e-mail from the computer.","furigana":"おく"},{"japanese":"仕事のためにパソコンを買いました。","romaji":"Shigoto no tame ni pasokon o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a computer for work.","furigana":"しごと|か"}],"notes":"パソコン is the common short form of パーソナルコンピューター and means a personal computer, such as a desktop PC or laptop. パソコンでメールを送る uses で for the means. A laptop is also called ノートパソコン; パソコン does not automatically mean a smartphone.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 7"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"カメラを持っていますか。","romaji":"Kamera o motte imasu ka.","german":"Do you have a camera?","furigana":"も"},{"japanese":"旅行にカメラを持って行きます。","romaji":"Ryokō ni kamera o motte ikimasu.","german":"I take a camera on the trip.","furigana":"りょこう|も|い"}],"notes":"カメラ is a camera for taking pictures. カメラを持っている can express owning or carrying one; カメラを持って行く means \"to take a camera along\". The picture itself is called 写真. \"To take a photo with a camera\" is カメラで写真を撮る, with で for the device used.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"エアコンをつけてください。","romaji":"Eakon o tsukete kudasai.","german":"Please turn on the air conditioner."},{"japanese":"エアコンをつけてもいいですか。","romaji":"Eakon o tsukete mo ii desu ka.","german":"May I turn on the air conditioner?"},{"japanese":"この部屋にはエアコンがありません。","romaji":"Kono heya ni wa eakon ga arimasen.","german":"There is no air conditioner in this room.","furigana":"へや"}],"notes":"エアコン is the short form for an air conditioner. エアコンをつける means \"to turn the air conditioner on\", エアコンを消す \"to turn it off\". Many units can also heat; the word alone does not specify cooling. 冷房 denotes cooling mode, 暖房 heating mode.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 14"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"電気をつけてください。","romaji":"Denki o tsukete kudasai.","german":"Please turn on the light.","furigana":"でんき"},{"japanese":"電気を消してください。","romaji":"Denki o keshite kudasai.","german":"Please turn off the light.","furigana":"でんき|け"},{"japanese":"電気がないと、パソコンは使えません。","romaji":"Denki ga nai to, pasokon wa tsukaemasen.","german":"Without electricity, the computer can't be used.","furigana":"でんき|つか"}],"notes":"電気 means electricity or, in everyday speech, electric light. With 電気をつける and 電気を消す, the light is usually meant. 電気で動く means \"to run on electricity\"; で marks the energy source. Whether \"electricity\" or \"light\" fits is decided by the action in the sentence.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お土産を買いました。","romaji":"Omiyage o kaimashita.","german":"I bought souvenirs.","furigana":"みやげ|か"},{"japanese":"お土産を買って帰ります。","romaji":"Omiyage o katte kaerimasu.","german":"I will buy souvenirs and go home.","furigana":"みやげ|か|かえ"},{"japanese":"友達の家で、お土産を渡しました。","romaji":"Tomodachi no ie de, omiyage o watashimashita.","german":"At my friend's house, I handed over a gift.","furigana":"ともだち|いえ|みやげ|わた"}],"notes":"お土産 is often a gift one buys for others on a trip, for example something typical of the place visited. It can also be a gift a guest brings when visiting. The お is a polite prefix; the full reading is おみやげ. お土産を渡す means \"to hand over a souvenir/gift\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 7"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"荷物が多いです。","romaji":"Nimotsu ga ōi desu.","german":"There is a lot of luggage.","furigana":"にもつ|おお"},{"japanese":"荷物が多くて大変です。","romaji":"Nimotsu ga ōkute taihen desu.","german":"I have a lot of luggage and it is tiring.","furigana":"にもつ|おお|たいへん"},{"japanese":"荷物はここに置いてください。","romaji":"Nimotsu wa koko ni oite kudasai.","german":"Please put your luggage here.","furigana":"にもつ|お"}],"notes":"荷物 means luggage or transported goods and, depending on the context, can also mean a package. The word has no plural ending: 荷物が多い means \"to have a lot of luggage\". 荷物を預ける means \"to check in luggage\", 荷物を持つ \"to carry luggage\". How many individual pieces are meant follows from the sentence.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 7"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"誰ですか。","romaji":"Dare desu ka.","german":"Wer ist das?","furigana":"だれ"},{"japanese":"あの人は誰ですか。","romaji":"Ano hito wa dare desu ka.","german":"Wer ist diese Person?","furigana":"ひと|だれ"},{"japanese":"誰がこの料理を作りましたか。","romaji":"Dare ga kono ryōri o tsukurimashita ka.","german":"Wer hat dieses Gericht gekocht?","furigana":"だれ|りょうり|つく"}],"notes":"誰 fragt nach einer Person: „wer?“. 誰が来ますか fragt, wer kommt; 誰に聞きますか, wen man fragt. Die Partikel richtet sich nach der Rolle der gesuchten Person. In einem höflicheren Gespräch ist どなた üblich. Eine höfliche Frage mit 誰 endet gewöhnlich mit か, ohne dass die Wortstellung wie im Deutschen umgestellt wird.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"何を食べますか。","romaji":"Nani o tabemasu ka.","german":"What do you eat?","furigana":"なに|た"},{"japanese":"何を食べたいですか。","romaji":"Nani o tabetai desu ka.","german":"What do you want to eat?","furigana":"なに|た"},{"japanese":"箱の中に何が入っていますか。","romaji":"Hako no naka ni nani ga haitte imasu ka.","german":"What is in the box?","furigana":"はこ|なか|なに|はい"}],"notes":"何 is read なに here and asks about a thing or action: 何を食べる \"eat what\", 何がある \"what is there\". Before です, the reading なん is usual, as in 何ですか. The reading should therefore be learned together with its combination; the examples here practice なに specifically.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 6"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"トイレはどこですか。","romaji":"Toire wa doko desu ka.","german":"Where is the restroom?"},{"japanese":"どこに行きますか。","romaji":"Doko ni ikimasu ka.","german":"Where are you going?","furigana":"い"}],"notes":"どこ asks about a place. どこですか means \"where is it?\", どこに行く \"where to go\", どこで買う \"where to buy\". に marks the destination of a movement or a location, で the place of an action. The particle therefore provides the more precise meaning of the place question. The spelling 何処 is also read どこ. In normal text, the kana form どこ is usual.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いつ日本に行きますか。","romaji":"Itsu Nihon ni ikimasu ka.","german":"When are you going to Japan?","furigana":"にほん|い"},{"japanese":"いつ日本に来ましたか。","romaji":"Itsu Nihon ni kimashita ka.","german":"When did you come to Japan?","furigana":"にほん|き"},{"japanese":"いつまで待ちますか。","romaji":"Itsu made machimasu ka.","german":"Until when will you wait?","furigana":"ま"}],"notes":"いつ asks about a point in time or a date. Unlike a fixed clock time, the simple question form normally needs no に: いつ行きますか. いつから asks for the start, いつまで for the endpoint of a time span. The answer may be a clock time, a day or a longer period. The rare kanji spelling 何時 can be read いつ. For this question, the kana form いつ is usual; the same kanji can also be read なんじ \"what time\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"なぜ日本語を勉強しますか。","romaji":"Naze Nihongo o benkyō shimasu ka.","german":"Why do you study Japanese?","furigana":"にほんご|べんきょう"},{"japanese":"なぜ日本語を勉強していますか。","romaji":"Naze Nihongo o benkyō shite imasu ka.","german":"Why are you studying Japanese?","furigana":"にほんご|べんきょう"},{"japanese":"なぜ、電車が止まっていますか。","romaji":"Naze, densha ga tomatte imasu ka.","german":"Why is the train stopped?","furigana":"でんしゃ|と"}],"notes":"なぜ asks for a reason or cause. With a person, the question can aim at a motive, with an event at its explanation. なぜ often sounds matter-of-fact or probing; in everyday conversation, どうして and the more casual なんで are also common. A direct why-question can sound demanding depending on tone.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 42"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本語はどうですか。","romaji":"Nihongo wa dō desu ka.","german":"How is Japanese?","furigana":"にほんご"},{"japanese":"日本の生活はどうですか。","romaji":"Nihon no seikatsu wa dō desu ka.","german":"How is life in Japan?","furigana":"にほん|せいかつ"},{"japanese":"このパソコンはどう使いますか。","romaji":"Kono pasokon wa dō tsukaimasu ka.","german":"How do you use this computer?","furigana":"つか"}],"notes":"どう asks about a manner or an assessment. 日本の生活はどうですか asks for an impression of life in Japan; どう使いますか asks about operation. Before a noun, どんな is normally used for \"what kind of ...\", not どう. In more polite questions, いかが can be used. 如何 is a rare kanji spelling of どう. The kana form どう is usual; the kanji can also be read いかが.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 8"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"はい、分かりました。","romaji":"Hai, wakarimashita.","german":"Yes, I understand.","furigana":"わ"},{"japanese":"はい、お願いします。","romaji":"Hai, onegai shimasu.","german":"Yes, please.","furigana":"ねが"}],"notes":"はい is the polite answer \"yes\" and can also signal \"understood\" or \"I'm listening\". はい、分かりました confirms understanding; はい、お願いします accepts an offer. A single はい while listening does not yet mean agreement with a proposal. To decline, the answer itself is explained, for example with いいえ.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いいえ、違います。","romaji":"Iie, chigaimasu.","german":"No, that's not right.","furigana":"ちが"},{"japanese":"「いいえ、まだです。」","romaji":"\"Iie, mada desu.\"","german":"No, not yet."},{"japanese":"「ありがとうございます。」「いいえ、どういたしまして。」","romaji":"\"Arigatō gozaimasu.\" \"Iie, dō itashimashite.\"","german":"\"Thank you.\" \"Not at all, you're welcome.\""}],"notes":"いいえ is a negative answer. いいえ、違います corrects an assumption; いいえ、まだです means \"no, not yet\". As a modest reply to thanks, いいえ can also mean \"don't mention it\". This use does not deny the thanks itself but plays down one's own help. The kanji spelling 否 is documented as a rare form for this reading in older dictionary entries. For ordinary answers, the kana spelling いいえ is used. The same kanji is also read いな or いや in other expressions; those readings belong to other words.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"おはようございます、先生。","romaji":"Ohayō gozaimasu, sensei.","german":"Good morning, teacher. (Greeting to a teacher.)","furigana":"せんせい"},{"japanese":"先生に「おはようございます」と言いました。","romaji":"Sensei ni \"ohayō gozaimasu\" to iimashita.","german":"I said \"Good morning\" to the teacher.","furigana":"せんせい|い"},{"japanese":"電話で「おはようございます。田中です」と言いました。","romaji":"Denwa de \"ohayō gozaimasu. Tanaka desu\" to iimashita.","german":"On the phone I said: \"Good morning. This is Tanaka.\"","furigana":"でんわ|たなか|い"}],"notes":"おはようございます is the polite morning greeting, for example to teachers or at work. Among familiar people, おはよう is often enough. On the phone, one's own name can follow the greeting. Some workplaces also use the greeting at the start of a shift at other times of day; normally it is a morning greeting. お早うございます is also read おはようございます; the phrase is usually written in kana.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"こんにちは、お元気ですか。","romaji":"Konnichiwa, ogenki desu ka.","german":"Hello, how are you?","furigana":"げんき"},{"japanese":"こんにちは、田中さん。","romaji":"Konnichiwa, Tanaka-san.","german":"Hello, Mr./Ms. Tanaka.","furigana":"たなか"},{"japanese":"昼、店に入って「こんにちは」と言いました。","romaji":"Hiru, mise ni haitte \"konnichiwa\" to iimashita.","german":"At noon, I entered the shop and said \"Hello\".","furigana":"ひる|みせ|はい|い"}],"notes":"こんにちは is a daytime greeting, especially from late morning to the afternoon. The final は is pronounced わ: konnichiwa. You can then ask how someone is or start a conversation. The form is also common as a greeting when entering a small shop; other greetings are used in the morning and evening.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 0"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"こんばんは、田中さん。","romaji":"Konbanwa, Tanaka-san.","german":"Good evening, Mr./Ms. Tanaka.","furigana":"たなか"},{"japanese":"「こんばんは、いい天気ですね。」","romaji":"\"Konbanwa, ii tenki desu ne.\"","german":"\"Good evening, nice weather, isn't it?\"","furigana":"てんき"},{"japanese":"夜、ビデオで話すとき、最初に「こんばんは」と言います。","romaji":"Yoru, bideo de hanasu toki, saisho ni \"konbanwa\" to iimasu.","german":"When I talk by video in the evening, I first say \"Good evening\".","furigana":"よる|はな|さいしょ|い"}],"notes":"こんばんは is the greeting when meeting in the evening. The final は is pronounced わ: konbanwa. The form opens contact, for example in person or in an evening video call. It is not a greeting for going to bed; for that, おやすみなさい is said.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 0"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"さようなら、また明日。","romaji":"Sayōnara, mata ashita.","german":"Goodbye, see you tomorrow.","furigana":"あした"},{"japanese":"友達に「さようなら」と手を振りました。","romaji":"Tomodachi ni \"sayōnara\" to te o furimashita.","german":"I waved goodbye to my friend.","furigana":"ともだち|て|ふ"},{"japanese":"日本を出る前に、友達に「さようなら」と言いました。","romaji":"Nihon o deru mae ni, tomodachi ni \"sayōnara\" to iimashita.","german":"Before leaving Japan, I said \"Goodbye\" to my friends.","furigana":"にほん|で|まえ|ともだち|い"}],"notes":"さようなら is a farewell greeting. It is used at school, for example, and can seem more serious for longer separations. Among friends, またね or じゃあ、また are often used for a quick reunion. However, さようなら does not necessarily mean a final farewell; さようなら、また明日 is possible. 左様なら is a rare kanji spelling; さようなら is usual.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 0"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ありがとうございます。助かりました。","romaji":"Arigatō gozaimasu. Tasukarimashita.","german":"Thank you very much. That helped me.","furigana":"たす"},{"japanese":"プレゼントをもらって「ありがとうございます」と言いました。","romaji":"Purezento o moratte \"arigatō gozaimasu\" to iimashita.","german":"I received a present and said \"Thank you\".","furigana":"い"}],"notes":"ありがとうございます is the polite expression of thanks, for example for help, information or a gift. Among familiar people, ありがとう can be enough. For help that has already been given, ありがとうございました is also said. The fixed phrase is pronounced arigatō gozaimasu; the long ō belongs to the pronunciation. 有難うございます is a dictionary spelling of the thanks formula; the kana form ありがとうございます is usual.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 0"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"すみません、道を教えてください。","romaji":"Sumimasen, michi o oshiete kudasai.","german":"Excuse me, please tell me the way.","furigana":"みち|おし"},{"japanese":"遅れてすみません。","romaji":"Okurete sumimasen.","german":"Sorry for being late.","furigana":"おく"}],"notes":"すみません is used both to apologize and to politely get attention. すみません、道を教えてください opens a request for directions; 遅れてすみません apologizes for one's own lateness. In such an apology, the cause can stand in the te-form directly before すみません. 済みません is a dictionary spelling of the phrase; normally すみません is written in kana.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 15"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コーヒーをお願いします。","romaji":"Kōhī o onegai shimasu.","german":"A coffee, please.","furigana":"ねが"},{"japanese":"「お願いします」と店員に言いました。","romaji":"\"Onegai shimasu\" to ten'in ni iimashita.","german":"I said \"please\" to the shop assistant.","furigana":"ねが|てんいん|い"},{"japanese":"この仕事をお願いします。","romaji":"Kono shigoto o onegai shimasu.","german":"Please take care of this work.","furigana":"しごと|ねが"}],"notes":"お願いします politely asks for something or asks someone to take on a task. The thing requested can come before it with を: コーヒーをお願いします \"a coffee, please\", この仕事をお願いします \"please take care of this work\". The phrase can stand alone when it is clear what is meant. It makes a request of others and is not a general reply to thanks.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いただきます！","romaji":"Itadakimasu!","german":"Itadakimasu! (Phrase said before eating.)"},{"japanese":"ごはんの前に「いただきます」と言います。","romaji":"Gohan no mae ni \"itadakimasu\" to iimasu.","german":"Before the meal, one says \"Itadakimasu\".","furigana":"まえ|い"},{"japanese":"一人で食べるときも、「いただきます」と言います。","romaji":"Hitori de taberu toki mo, \"itadakimasu\" to iimasu.","german":"Even when I eat alone, I say \"Itadakimasu\".","furigana":"ひとり|た|い"}],"notes":"いただきます is said by the person who is about to eat, as an expression of thanks before the meal. It is therefore not a wish of \"Enjoy your meal\" to someone else. The phrase can also be used when eating alone. After the meal, ごちそうさまでした is usually said; the two phrases play different roles in time. 戴きます is listed in dictionaries only as a search spelling of this meal phrase. For your own writing, use いただきます.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 0"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ごちそうさまでした。おいしかったです。","romaji":"Gochisōsama deshita. Oishikatta desu.","german":"Thank you for the meal. It was delicious."},{"japanese":"レストランを出るとき、店員さんに「ごちそうさまでした」と言いました。","romaji":"Resutoran o deru toki, ten'in-san ni \"gochisōsama deshita\" to iimashita.","german":"When I left the restaurant, I said to the staff: \"Thank you for the meal\".","furigana":"で|てんいん|い"}],"notes":"ごちそうさまでした is the fixed expression of thanks after a meal. It can be addressed to the person who cooked or invited, but it is also said without direct address. Before eating, いただきます is used. The form with でした looks back on the finished meal. ご馳走様でした is another spelling with the reading ごちそうさまでした. The phrase is usually written in kana.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 0"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"初めまして。田中です。","romaji":"Hajimemashite. Tanaka desu.","german":"Nice to meet you. I'm Tanaka.","furigana":"はじ|たなか"},{"japanese":"メールの初めに「初めまして」と書きました。","romaji":"Mēru no hajime ni \"hajimemashite\" to kakimashita.","german":"At the beginning of the e-mail I wrote: \"Nice to meet you\".","furigana":"はじ|はじ|か"}],"notes":"初めまして is said when meeting for the first time, often before stating one's own name. The phrase can also open a first message to a previously unknown person. It does not fit again when meeting a second time. The common kana form はじめまして has the same reading. Although the ending looks like a て-form, it is learned as a fixed greeting. 始めまして is also recorded as a spelling of the greeting; はじめまして is commonly written.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 0"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どうぞよろしくお願いします。","romaji":"Dōzo yoroshiku onegai shimasu.","german":"Ich freue mich auf die Zusammenarbeit.","furigana":"ねが"},{"japanese":"新しい同僚に「どうぞよろしく」と言いました。","romaji":"Atarashii dōryō ni \"dōzo yoroshiku\" to iimashita.","german":"Ich sagte zum neuen Kollegen: „Freut mich.“","furigana":"あたら|どうりょう|い"}],"notes":"どうぞよろしく wird beim Kennenlernen oder zu Beginn einer gemeinsamen Tätigkeit gesagt. Je nach Situation passt „Freut mich“ oder „auf gute Zusammenarbeit“; die Formel lässt sich nicht Wort für Wort als einzelnes deutsches Verb übersetzen. Mit お願いします wird sie höflicher und vollständiger. Die Wörterbuchvariante どうぞ宜しく hat dieselbe Lesung; die Formel wird üblicherweise in Kana geschrieben.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ただいま！","romaji":"Tadaima!","german":"I'm back!"},{"japanese":"会社に戻って、同僚に「ただいま」と言いました。","romaji":"Kaisha ni modotte, dōryō ni \"tadaima\" to iimashita.","german":"When I returned to the office, I said to a colleague: \"I'm back.\"","furigana":"かいしゃ|もど|どうりょう|い"}],"notes":"ただいま accompanies a return, usually coming home: \"I'm back!\" Others often reply with おかえり or おかえりなさい. Among familiar colleagues it can also be said when returning to the office; a formal report would be something like ただいま戻りました. The spelling ただ今 has the same reading here. Temporal meanings such as \"right now\" belong to a different use from the greeting of this card.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 0"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"おかえりなさい、お疲れさま。","romaji":"Okaerinasai, otsukaresama.","german":"Welcome back. Thank you for your hard work.","furigana":"つか"},{"japanese":"家族が帰ったら「おかえりなさい」と言います。","romaji":"Kazoku ga kaettara \"okaerinasai\" to iimasu.","german":"When the family comes home, one says \"Welcome back\".","furigana":"かぞく|かえ|い"}],"notes":"おかえりなさい greets a person who is coming back home. The reply to ただいま is often this phrase or the shorter おかえり. なさい is learned here as part of the greeting: the phrase does not tell the arriving person to leave again or to come home first.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 0"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"授業が始まります。","romaji":"Jugyō ga hajimarimasu.","german":"Class begins.","furigana":"じゅぎょう|はじ"},{"japanese":"映画は八時に始まります。","romaji":"Eiga wa hachiji ni hajimarimasu.","german":"The movie starts at eight o'clock.","furigana":"えいが|はちじ|はじ"}],"notes":"始まる is intransitive: an event begins, as in 授業が始まる. To actively begin something, use the transitive 始める with を: 授業を始める. Despite the ending る, 始まる is a Godan verb; the て-form is 始まって.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 9"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"映画が終わりました。","romaji":"Eiga ga owarimashita.","german":"The movie is over.","furigana":"えいが|お"},{"japanese":"授業は三時に終わります。","romaji":"Jugyō wa sanji ni owarimasu.","german":"Class ends at three o'clock.","furigana":"じゅぎょう|さんじ|お"}],"notes":"終わる here describes an event or activity ending. The ending event takes が or, as the topic, は. 終わりました reports that the end has occurred, while 終わります can state a scheduled end time. The て-form of this Godan verb is 終わって.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 9"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"漢字を覚えます。","romaji":"Kanji o oboemasu.","german":"I learn kanji.","furigana":"かんじ|おぼ"},{"japanese":"友達の名前を覚えました。","romaji":"Tomodachi no namae o oboemashita.","german":"I remembered my friend's name.","furigana":"ともだち|なまえ|おぼ"}],"notes":"覚える here means to take something into memory or to commit it to memory. The content learned takes を. 覚えました says that one has learned it; 覚えています means, in the right context, \"I remember\" or \"I have it in memory\". It is an Ichidan verb: 覚えて, 覚えない.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 9"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"名前を忘れました。","romaji":"Namae o wasuremashita.","german":"I forgot the name.","furigana":"なまえ|わす"},{"japanese":"かさを電車に忘れました。","romaji":"Kasa o densha ni wasuremashita.","german":"I left my umbrella on the train.","furigana":"でんしゃ|わす"}],"notes":"忘れる can mean to forget a memory or to accidentally leave an object behind. Both take を as the object. For an object left behind, に names the place: 電車に忘れる. This is not automatically the same as losing, for which, for example, なくす is used. The て-form is 忘れて.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 6"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"医者になりたいです。","romaji":"Isha ni naritai desu.","german":"I want to become a doctor.","furigana":"いしゃ"},{"japanese":"寒くなりました。","romaji":"Samuku narimashita.","german":"It has become cold.","furigana":"さむ"},{"japanese":"この本は日本語の勉強のためになります。","romaji":"Kono hon wa Nihongo no benkyō no tame ni narimasu.","german":"This book is useful for studying Japanese.","furigana":"ほん|にほんご|べんきょう"}],"notes":"なる here means to become or to take on a state. After nouns and な-adjectives, に is used: 医者になる, 静かになる. An い-adjective takes く: 寒くなる. なる is intransitive and a Godan verb; なって is the て-form. The identically read 鳴る, by contrast, means to sound. The phrase ためになる means to be useful or to benefit someone, as in 日本語の勉強のためになる. The rare kanji spelling 為る is also recorded for this verb; normally it is written なる. 為る can be read する for another verb, while this card specifically practices なる.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 10"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"本を貸してください。","romaji":"Hon o kashite kudasai.","german":"Please lend me a book.","furigana":"ほん|か"},{"japanese":"友達に本を貸しました。","romaji":"Tomodachi ni hon o kashimashita.","german":"I lent a book to my friend.","furigana":"ともだち|ほん|か"}],"notes":"貸す means to lend something to someone, from the lender's point of view. The object takes を, the recipient に: 友達に本を貸す. 借りる describes the same lending situation from the borrower's point of view. 貸してください therefore asks to be given something to use. The て-form is 貸して.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 13"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"図書館で本を借ります。","romaji":"Toshokan de hon o karimasu.","german":"I borrow a book at the library.","furigana":"としょかん|ほん|か"},{"japanese":"友達からかさを借りました。","romaji":"Tomodachi kara kasa o karimashita.","german":"I borrowed an umbrella from a friend.","furigana":"ともだち|か"}],"notes":"借りる means to borrow something, from the borrower's point of view. を marks the object, から or に the person one gets it from. 図書館で, by contrast, names the place of borrowing. Despite りる, it is an Ichidan verb: 借りて, 借りない. The counterpart for the lender is 貸す.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 6"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"手を洗ってください。","romaji":"Te o aratte kudasai.","german":"Please wash your hands.","furigana":"て|あら"},{"japanese":"食べた後で、お皿を洗います。","romaji":"Tabeta ato de, osara o araimasu.","german":"After eating, I wash the dishes.","furigana":"た|あと|さら|あら"}],"notes":"洗う means to wash or clean something with water. The thing being washed takes を: 手を洗う or お皿を洗う. It applies both to personal hygiene and to objects. As a godan verb ending in う, it forms 洗って and 洗わない; *洗うて is not a standard form.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 8"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コートを着ます。","romaji":"Kōto o kimasu.","german":"I put on a coat.","furigana":"き"},{"japanese":"白いシャツを着ています。","romaji":"Shiroi shatsu o kite imasu.","german":"I am wearing a white shirt.","furigana":"しろ|き"}],"notes":"着る is used for clothing worn from the shoulders down, such as a shirt, coat or dress. 着ます can refer to the act of putting on; 着ています describes wearing as a state. For shoes and trousers one uses 履く, for hats かぶる. 着る is an ichidan verb with the te-form 着て; 切る, which is read the same, is a different verb.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 7"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"靴を脱いでください。","romaji":"Kutsu o nuide kudasai.","german":"Please take off your shoes.","furigana":"くつ|ぬ"},{"japanese":"暑いので、コートを脱ぎました。","romaji":"Atsui node, kōto o nugimashita.","german":"Because it was hot, I took off my coat.","furigana":"あつ|ぬ"}],"notes":"脱ぐ means to take off clothing or shoes. Unlike putting on, where different verbs are used depending on the item, the same verb works for both coats and shoes here. The item taken off takes を. This godan verb ending in ぐ forms 脱いで and 脱いだ, that is nuide and nuida, not *nugite.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 17"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"電話をかけます。","romaji":"Denwa o kakemasu.","german":"I make a phone call.","furigana":"でんわ"},{"japanese":"メガネをかけています。","romaji":"Megane o kakete imasu.","german":"I am wearing glasses."}],"notes":"かける has many meanings; on this card the fixed collocation decides. 電話をかける means to make a phone call, メガネをかける to put on glasses. メガネをかけています means to be wearing glasses. For a phone call, に can name the person called: 友達に電話をかける. Both uses are transitive and ichidan: かけて.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 19"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"雨が降っています。","romaji":"Ame ga futte imasu.","german":"Es regnet.","furigana":"あめ|ふ"},{"japanese":"昨日、雪が降りました。","romaji":"Kinō, yuki ga furimashita.","german":"Gestern hat es geschneit.","furigana":"きのう|ゆき|ふ"}],"notes":"降る wird für fallenden Niederschlag benutzt, zum Beispiel 雨が降る und 雪が降る. 雨が降っています beschreibt laufenden Regen. Die deutsche Übersetzung verwendet meist das Wetterverb regnen oder schneien. 降る ist Godan und bildet 降って; das gleich gelesene 振る bedeutet dagegen schütteln oder schwenken.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"風が吹いています。","romaji":"Kaze ga fuite imasu.","german":"The wind is blowing.","furigana":"かぜ|ふ"},{"japanese":"笛を吹きました。","romaji":"Fue o fukimashita.","german":"I blew a whistle.","furigana":"ふえ|ふ"}],"notes":"With 風が, 吹く describes the wind blowing. With a wind instrument and を, it means to play it or blow into it: 笛を吹く. Depending on the chosen meaning, the verb is intransitive or transitive. The te-form of this godan verb ending in く is 吹いて, not *吹って.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 22"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鳥が飛んでいます。","romaji":"Tori ga tonde imasu.","german":"A bird is flying.","furigana":"とり|と"},{"japanese":"飛行機で東京に飛びます。","romaji":"Hikōki de Tōkyō ni tobimasu.","german":"I will fly to Tokyo by plane.","furigana":"ひこうき|とうきょう|と"}],"notes":"Here 飛ぶ means to fly. The flying creature takes が, a travel destination takes に. 飛行機で names the means of transport. As a godan verb ending in ぶ, it forms 飛んで and 飛んだ. The other possible meaning, to jump, is not taught here as the same movement.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"山に登ります。","romaji":"Yama ni noborimasu.","german":"I climb the mountain.","furigana":"やま|のぼ"},{"japanese":"階段を登りました。","romaji":"Kaidan o noborimashita.","german":"I climbed the stairs.","furigana":"かいだん|のぼ"}],"notes":"登る means to climb up or ascend, especially a mountain. 山に names the goal of the climb; 階段を the route traversed. This を does not make the verb transitive. 登る is godan and forms 登って. 上る is also read のぼる, but 登る particularly emphasizes climbing.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 11"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"バスが止まりました。","romaji":"Basu ga tomarimashita.","german":"The bus stopped.","furigana":"と"},{"japanese":"時計が止まりました。","romaji":"Tokei ga tomarimashita.","german":"The clock stopped.","furigana":"とけい|と"},{"japanese":"小鳥が庭の木の枝に留まっています。","romaji":"Kotori ga niwa no ki no eda ni tomatte imasu.","german":"A small bird is perched on a branch of a tree in the garden.","furigana":"ことり|にわ|き|えだ|と"}],"notes":"止まる（とまる）describes a movement or operation coming to a standstill; the vehicle or device affected takes が. For a bird that settles on a branch and sits there, 留まる can also be written. The perch takes に. This intransitive godan verb forms とまります and とまって. 留まる with the longer reading とどまる means to remain; 泊まる（とまる）means to stay overnight.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ホテルに泊まります。","romaji":"Hoteru ni tomarimasu.","german":"I will stay at a hotel.","furigana":"と"},{"japanese":"友達の家に泊まりました。","romaji":"Tomodachi no ie ni tomarimashita.","german":"I stayed at a friend's house.","furigana":"ともだち|いえ|と"}],"notes":"泊まる means to stay overnight or to stay temporarily in lodgings. The place of stay takes に: ホテルに泊まる, 友達の家に泊まる. 一泊（いっぱく）counts one overnight stay. 泊まる is godan and forms 泊まって; 止まる, which sounds the same, means to stop.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 10"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お金を払います。","romaji":"Okane o haraimasu.","german":"I pay.","furigana":"かね|はら"},{"japanese":"カードで払いました。","romaji":"Kādo de haraimashita.","german":"I paid by card.","furigana":"はら"}],"notes":"Here 払う means to pay. The amount or bill takes を; the method of payment takes で, as in カードで払う. お金を払う does not yet specify whether one pays in cash or by card. This godan verb ending in う forms 払って and 払わない; other meanings such as brushing off dust do not belong to this card.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 10"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"手紙を送ります。","romaji":"Tegami o okurimasu.","german":"I send a letter.","furigana":"てがみ|おく"},{"japanese":"友達を駅まで送ります。","romaji":"Tomodachi o eki made okurimasu.","german":"I accompany my friend to the station.","furigana":"ともだち|えき|おく"}],"notes":"送る means to send when something is dispatched: 手紙を送る. With a person as the object it can also mean to see off or accompany, as in 友達を駅まで送る. を marks the object sent or the person accompanied, and まで marks the end point of the accompanying. For the recipient of a letter, に is used. This godan verb forms 送って.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 14"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"荷物が届きました。","romaji":"Nimotsu ga todokimashita.","german":"The package has arrived.","furigana":"にもつ|とど"},{"japanese":"明日手紙が届きます。","romaji":"Ashita tegami ga todokimasu.","german":"A letter will arrive tomorrow.","furigana":"あしたてがみ|とど"}],"notes":"Intransitive verb: the item that arrives takes ga. Todokimashita describes an arrival that has already happened. The destination can be added with に: 家に届く. By contrast, 届ける is transitive and means actively delivering something, with を. 届く is a Godan verb: 届いて.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二階に上がります。","romaji":"Nikai ni agarimasu.","german":"I go up to the second floor (the first floor above ground level, counting the Japanese way).","furigana":"にかい|あ"},{"japanese":"温度が上がりました。","romaji":"Ondo ga agarimashita.","german":"The temperature has risen.","furigana":"おんど|あ"}],"notes":"上がる describes someone going up or a value rising: 二階に上がる, 温度が上がる. In these meanings it is intransitive; the thing that rises takes が, and a spatial destination takes に. \"To raise something\" is 上げる instead. Despite the ending る, 上がる is a godan verb: 上がります, 上がって. The dictionary spelling 上る for あがる is marked as irregular okurigana. Normally write this reading as 上がる; 上る with the reading のぼる can denote a different verb.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"温度が下がりました。","romaji":"Ondo ga sagarimashita.","german":"The temperature has dropped.","furigana":"おんど|さ"},{"japanese":"値段が下がりました。","romaji":"Nedan ga sagarimashita.","german":"The price has gone down.","furigana":"ねだん|さ"},{"japanese":"少し後ろに下がってください。","romaji":"Sukoshi ushiro ni sagatte kudasai.","german":"Please step back a little.","furigana":"すこ|うし|さ"}],"notes":"下がる describes a value falling: 温度が下がる, 値段が下がる. It can also mean \"to step back\", as in 少し後ろに下がる. The verb is intransitive: the price drops without a direct object being named. \"To lower something\" is 下げる. The godan forms are 下がります and 下がって. The dictionary spelling 下る for さがる has irregular okurigana; normally write this reading as 下がる. 下る can denote a different verb with other readings.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"机の上に置いてください。","romaji":"Tsukue no ue ni oite kudasai.","german":"Please put it on the desk.","furigana":"つくえ|うえ|お"},{"japanese":"テーブルの上に置いてください。","romaji":"Tēburu no ue ni oite kudasai.","german":"Please put it on the table.","furigana":"うえ|お"},{"japanese":"荷物を家に置いて、出かけました。","romaji":"Nimotsu o ie ni oite, dekakemashita.","german":"I left my luggage at home and went out.","furigana":"にもつ|いえ|お|で"}],"notes":"置く means \"to put down, to set\" or \"to leave behind\". The object takes を, the place where it is put takes に: 本を机に置く. The word does not specify whether something lies or stands; the object determines that. The te-form is 置いて. The auxiliary-verb construction ～ておく, for preparation, is a different use.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 21"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ペンを取ります。","romaji":"Pen o torimasu.","german":"I take a pen.","furigana":"と"},{"japanese":"棚から本を取ってください。","romaji":"Tana kara hon o totte kudasai.","german":"Please take the book from the shelf.","furigana":"たな|ほん|と"},{"japanese":"服についたごみを取ります。","romaji":"Fuku ni tsuita gomi o torimasu.","german":"I remove the lint from my clothes.","furigana":"ふく|と"}],"notes":"取る is a transitive verb meaning \"to take, to pick up\"; the object takes を. A source can be marked with から: 棚から本を取る. 服についたごみを取る, on the other hand, means \"to remove dirt that is stuck on\". The te-form is 取って. For taking photographs one usually writes 写真を撮る, not 写真を取る.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 11"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"好きなのを選んでください。","romaji":"Suki na no o erande kudasai.","german":"Please choose the one you like.","furigana":"す|えら"},{"japanese":"好きな色を選んでください。","romaji":"Suki na iro o erande kudasai.","german":"Please choose the color you like.","furigana":"す|いろ|えら"},{"japanese":"安いホテルを選んで、予約しました。","romaji":"Yasui hoteru o erande, yoyaku shimashita.","german":"I chose a cheap hotel and made a reservation.","furigana":"やす|えら|よやく"}],"notes":"選ぶ means \"to choose from several possibilities\". The thing chosen takes を; the set of options can take から, as in 三つから一つ選ぶ. A decision can be based on preferences or on a criterion such as price. This godan verb forms 選びます and 選んで, not 選ぶて.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 17"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ここに並んでください。","romaji":"Koko ni narande kudasai.","german":"Please line up here.","furigana":"なら"},{"japanese":"みんな並んで待っています。","romaji":"Minna narande matte imasu.","german":"Everyone is standing in line, waiting.","furigana":"なら|ま"},{"japanese":"この道には、小さい店が並んでいます。","romaji":"Kono michi ni wa, chīsai mise ga narande imasu.","german":"Small shops line this street.","furigana":"みち|ちい|みせ|なら"}],"notes":"並ぶ describes people or things standing in a row. For people it can mean \"to queue up\"; 店が並んでいる describes a row of shops. The verb is intransitive: 店が並ぶ. When someone actively lines objects up, 並べる with を is used. The te-form of 並ぶ is 並んで.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"質問に答えてください。","romaji":"Shitsumon ni kotaete kudasai.","german":"Bitte beantworten Sie die Frage.","furigana":"しつもん|こた"},{"japanese":"先生の質問に答えました。","romaji":"Sensei no shitsumon ni kotaemashita.","german":"Ich habe die Frage des Lehrers beantwortet.","furigana":"せんせい|しつもん|こた"},{"japanese":"名前を呼ばれて、「はい」と答えました。","romaji":"Namae o yobarete, \"hai\" to kotaemashita.","german":"Als mein Name aufgerufen wurde, antwortete ich mit „Hier“.","furigana":"なまえ|よ|こた"}],"notes":"答える heißt „antworten“. Die Frage oder Person, auf die man antwortet, steht mit に: 質問に答える. Der Wortlaut einer Antwort kann mit と stehen: 「はい」と答える. Das Verb ist trotz der deutschen Übersetzung „eine Frage beantworten“ intransitiv. Es ist ein Ichidan-Verb: 答えます, 答えて.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"困りました。","romaji":"Komarimashita.","german":"I'm in trouble.","furigana":"こま"},{"japanese":"道に迷って困りました。","romaji":"Michi ni mayotte komarimashita.","german":"I got lost and was in trouble.","furigana":"みち|まよ|こま"},{"japanese":"ここに車を止めると、困ります。","romaji":"Koko ni kuruma o tomeru to, komarimasu.","german":"It is a problem for me if you park a car here.","furigana":"くるま|と|こま"}],"notes":"困る describes being at a loss, having difficulties, or being affected by something. 道に迷って困る gives the cause with the te-form. ここに車を止めると困ります can be a polite way to point out that parking causes a problem. 困りました can also express a problem that has just arisen and still persists.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 16"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"それは違います。","romaji":"Sore wa chigaimasu.","german":"That is wrong.","furigana":"ちが"},{"japanese":"この答えは違います。","romaji":"Kono kotae wa chigaimasu.","german":"This answer is wrong.","furigana":"こた|ちが"},{"japanese":"このかばんは、私のかばんと色が違います。","romaji":"Kono kaban wa, watashi no kaban to iro ga chigaimasu.","german":"This bag is a different color from my bag.","furigana":"わたし|いろ|ちが"}],"notes":"違う is a verb meaning \"to be different, to differ\" and \"to be incorrect\". この答えは違います judges an answer as wrong. For a comparison と is often used: 私のかばんと違う \"different from my bag\". The polite form is 違います, the negative 違いません or 違わない; the word is not a na-adjective.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 23"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"パスポートが必要です。","romaji":"Pasupōto ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"A passport is necessary.","furigana":"ひつよう"},{"japanese":"時間が必要です。","romaji":"Jikan ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"Time is needed.","furigana":"じかん|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"必要な物をかばんに入れます。","romaji":"Hitsuyō na mono o kaban ni iremasu.","german":"I put the things I need into my bag.","furigana":"ひつよう|もの|い"}],"notes":"必要 is a na-adjective: 必要な物 means \"needed things\". In ～が必要です it is said that something is necessary, such as a passport or time. The negative is 必要ではありません or 必要じゃありません. 必要 denotes a requirement; it does not express that someone merely wants something.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"色々な国に行きました。","romaji":"Iroiro na kuni ni ikimashita.","german":"I have been to various countries.","furigana":"いろいろ|くに|い"},{"japanese":"色々なお店があります。","romaji":"Iroiro na omise ga arimasu.","german":"There are all kinds of shops.","furigana":"いろいろ|みせ"},{"japanese":"この問題について、色々考えました。","romaji":"Kono mondai ni tsuite, iroiro kangaemashita.","german":"I thought about this problem in many ways.","furigana":"もんだい|いろいろかんが"}],"notes":"色々 expresses variety, not just a large number of identical things. Before a noun, な usually follows: 色々な国 \"various countries\". As an adverb, 色々考える or 色々と考える can mean \"to think about something in many ways\". The mark 々 indicates repetition of 色; the kana spelling いろいろ is also common.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 20"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"同じ学校です。","romaji":"Onaji gakkō desu.","german":"It is the same school.","furigana":"おな|がっこう"},{"japanese":"私の鞄と同じですね。","romaji":"Watashi no kaban to onaji desu ne.","german":"It's the same as my bag, isn't it?","furigana":"わたし|かばん|おな"}],"notes":"同じ means \"the same\". Before a noun it normally appears without な: 同じ学校 \"the same school\". As a predicate it is 同じです; the thing compared can take と, as in 私のかばんと同じ. Whether the very same item or an identical model is meant depends on the context.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 21"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ちょっと待ってください。","romaji":"Chotto matte kudasai.","german":"Please wait a moment.","furigana":"ま"},{"japanese":"今日はちょっと寒いです。","romaji":"Kyō wa chotto samui desu.","german":"It is a little cold today.","furigana":"きょう|さむ"},{"japanese":"「明日、来ますか。」「明日はちょっと……。」","romaji":"\"Ashita, kimasu ka.\" \"Ashita wa chotto...\"","german":"\"Will you come tomorrow?\" \"Tomorrow is a bit difficult ...\"","furigana":"あした|き|あした"}],"notes":"Before an adjective, ちょっと means \"a little\"; in a request it can mean \"briefly, for a moment\". 明日はちょっと…… can be a reserved refusal: the difficulty is left unsaid. The context decides whether a small amount, a short duration, or a politely hinted problem is meant. The rare kanji spelling 一寸 can be read ちょっと. As a unit of length, however, it is read いっすん; normally write the adverb as ちょっと.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 6"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"まっすぐ行ってください。","romaji":"Massugu itte kudasai.","german":"Please go straight ahead.","furigana":"い"},{"japanese":"まっすぐ行って、右に曲がってください。","romaji":"Massugu itte, migi ni magatte kudasai.","german":"Go straight and turn right.","furigana":"い|みぎ|ま"},{"japanese":"背中をまっすぐにして座ります。","romaji":"Senaka o massugu ni shite suwarimasu.","german":"I sit with my back straight.","furigana":"せなか|すわ"}],"notes":"まっすぐ means \"straight, straight ahead, upright\" in a spatial sense. まっすぐ行く is a direction; まっすぐにする means \"to make straight\". The word can be used before a verb or with に. The small っ is pronounced as a double s: massugu. Physical straightness should be distinguished from a moral judgment.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 14"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ゆっくり話してください。","romaji":"Yukkuri hanashite kudasai.","german":"Please speak slowly.","furigana":"はな"},{"japanese":"ゆっくり歩きます。","romaji":"Yukkuri arukimasu.","german":"I walk slowly.","furigana":"ある"},{"japanese":"今日は家でゆっくりと休みます。","romaji":"Kyō wa ie de yukkuri to yasumimasu.","german":"Today I will rest at home in peace.","furigana":"きょう|いえ|やす"}],"notes":"ゆっくり describes a slow, unhurried pace or resting at ease. It comes before the verb: ゆっくり話す, ゆっくり休む. In these combinations the particle と can be added: ゆっくりと休む. ゆっくりと is thus the same adverbial use with と; the meaning does not become a new word.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 14"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"だんだん暖かくなります。","romaji":"Dandan atatakaku narimasu.","german":"It gets gradually warmer.","furigana":"あたた"},{"japanese":"日本語がだんだん分かるようになりました。","romaji":"Nihongo ga dandan wakaru yō ni narimashita.","german":"I have gradually come to understand Japanese.","furigana":"にほんご|わ"}],"notes":"だんだん describes a step-by-step change, for example in the weather or in acquiring a skill. With なる, a state comes about gradually: 暖かくなる, 分かるようになる. The change can also be a decrease, as in だんだん寒くなる. と can often follow; だんだん alone is also complete.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 19"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"もっと食べてください。","romaji":"Motto tabete kudasai.","german":"Please eat more.","furigana":"た"},{"japanese":"もっと大きい声で話してください。","romaji":"Motto ōkii koe de hanashite kudasai.","german":"Please speak louder.","furigana":"おお|こえ|はな"}],"notes":"もっと means \"more\" or \"even more strongly\" than before. In もっと食べる it is about the amount of food; もっと大きい声で話す asks for a higher volume. The point of comparison can be left unspoken when it is clear from the conversation. The word denotes an increase, not a mere repetition of the same action.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 11"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"初めて日本に来ました。","romaji":"Hajimete Nihon ni kimashita.","german":"I came to Japan for the first time.","furigana":"はじ|にほん|き"},{"japanese":"初めて富士山を見ました。","romaji":"Hajimete Fujisan o mimashita.","german":"I saw Mount Fuji for the first time.","furigana":"はじ|ふじさん|み"},{"japanese":"初めての旅行は楽しかったです。","romaji":"Hajimete no ryokō wa tanoshikatta desu.","german":"My first trip was fun.","furigana":"はじ|りょこう|たの"}],"notes":"初めて means that one experiences or does something for the first time. Before a noun, の follows: 初めての旅行 \"the first trip\". Here the word describes not a mere order among several activities but a first experience. The similar-sounding 始めて is a form of 始める \"to begin\" and is written differently.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 12"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これが一番好きです。","romaji":"Kore ga ichiban suki desu.","german":"I like this the most.","furigana":"いちばんす"},{"japanese":"このレストランが一番おいしいです。","romaji":"Kono resutoran ga ichiban oishii desu.","german":"This restaurant is the most delicious.","furigana":"いちばん"},{"japanese":"テストで一番になりました。","romaji":"Tesuto de ichiban ni narimashita.","german":"I came first in the test.","furigana":"いちばん"}],"notes":"一番 stands before a quality for \"most, -est\": 一番好き \"like best\", 一番おいしい \"most delicious\". It can also denote first place or number one. 一番になる means \"to take first place\". What is being compared must be clear from the sentence or the conversation.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 10"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"多分雨が降ります。","romaji":"Tabun ame ga furimasu.","german":"It will probably rain.","furigana":"たぶんあめ|ふ"},{"japanese":"多分明日は雨でしょう。","romaji":"Tabun ashita wa ame deshō.","german":"It will probably rain tomorrow.","furigana":"たぶんあした|あめ"},{"japanese":"多分、田中さんは家にいます。","romaji":"Tabun, Tanaka-san wa ie ni imasu.","german":"Mr./Ms. Tanaka is probably at home.","furigana":"たぶん|たなか|いえ"}],"notes":"多分 marks a conjecture, not a certain fact. It can introduce a prediction or an assessment of a present situation. でしょう often appears at the end of the sentence, but it is not required. As \"probably, perhaps\", 多分 describes the certainty of a statement; here it is not a quantity word meaning \"much\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 12"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"本当においしいです。","romaji":"Hontō ni oishii desu.","german":"It is really delicious.","furigana":"ほんとう"},{"japanese":"この映画は本当に面白いです。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa hontō ni omoshiroi desu.","german":"This movie is really interesting.","furigana":"えいが|ほんとう|おもしろ"},{"japanese":"本当に明日来ますか。","romaji":"Hontō ni ashita kimasu ka.","german":"Are you really coming tomorrow?","furigana":"ほんとう|あしたき"}],"notes":"本当に means \"really, truly\". It can intensify an evaluation, as in 本当においしい, or check whether a statement actually holds: 本当に明日来ますか. The combination is pronounced hontō ni with a long ō. A follow-up question with it can signal surprise or doubt; tone and context decide.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 18"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大体分かりました。","romaji":"Daitai wakarimashita.","german":"I understood it on the whole.","furigana":"だいたいわ"},{"japanese":"大体千円です。","romaji":"Daitai sen en desu.","german":"It's about a thousand yen.","furigana":"だいたいせんえん"}],"notes":"大体 can express an approximate quantity or a general overview; before a number it means \"about\". 大体分かりました means \"I understood it on the whole\", not necessarily completely and in every detail. 大体千円 is an approximate price. The informal spelling だいたい is also common.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 9"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"結構です。","romaji":"Kekkō desu.","german":"That's fine, thank you.","furigana":"けっこう"},{"japanese":"結構です、ありがとうございます。","romaji":"Kekkō desu, arigatō gozaimasu.","german":"That's fine, thank you very much.","furigana":"けっこう"},{"japanese":"この量で結構です。","romaji":"Kono ryō de kekkō desu.","german":"This amount is enough.","furigana":"りょう|けっこう"},{"japanese":"この本は結構おもしろいです。","romaji":"Kono hon wa kekkō omoshiroi desu.","german":"This book is quite interesting.","furigana":"ほん|けっこう"},{"japanese":"「お茶はいかがですか。」「いいえ、結構です。」","romaji":"\"Ocha wa ikaga desu ka.\" \"Iie, kekkō desu.\"","german":"\"Would you like some tea?\" \"No, thank you.\"","furigana":"ちゃ|けっこう"}],"notes":"Before an adjective, 結構 means \"quite\", as in 結構おもしろい. この量で結構です confirms that an amount is sufficient. As a reply to an offer, however, いいえ、結構です can mean \"no, thank you\". A bare 結構です therefore depends on the context; a clear refusal additionally says いいえ or explains that one needs nothing more.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"もちろん行きます。","romaji":"Mochiron ikimasu.","german":"Of course I'll go.","furigana":"い"},{"japanese":"もちろん行きますよ。","romaji":"Mochiron ikimasu yo.","german":"Of course I'll go!","furigana":"い"},{"japanese":"「この部屋で食べてもいいですか。」「もちろんです。」","romaji":"\"Kono heya de tabete mo ii desu ka.\" \"Mochiron desu.\"","german":"\"May I eat in this room?\" \"Of course.\"","furigana":"へや|た"}],"notes":"もちろん marks something as self-evident or gives a firm yes. もちろん行きます is a confident commitment; as an answer to a question about permission, もちろんです can be used. Unlike 多分, the word does not express a mere conjecture. It can answer on its own when the intended statement is clear from the question.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 21"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"砂糖を入れますか。","romaji":"Satō o iremasu ka.","german":"Would you like sugar?","furigana":"さとう|い"},{"japanese":"コーヒーに砂糖を入れますか。","romaji":"Kōhī ni satō o iremasu ka.","german":"Do you take sugar in your coffee?","furigana":"さとう|い"},{"japanese":"このケーキには、砂糖を百グラム使います。","romaji":"Kono kēki ni wa, satō o hyakuguramu tsukaimasu.","german":"I use a hundred grams of sugar for this cake.","furigana":"さとう|ひゃく|つか"}],"notes":"砂糖 is sugar as an ingredient or sweetener. \"To put sugar in coffee\" is コーヒーに砂糖を入れる: を marks the sugar, に the drink. The amount can be given with グラム. The word itself denotes the substance; the quality \"sweet\" is 甘い.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 14"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"塩を取ってください。","romaji":"Shio o totte kudasai.","german":"Please pass the salt.","furigana":"しお|と"},{"japanese":"もう少し塩を入れてください。","romaji":"Mō sukoshi shio o irete kudasai.","german":"Please add a little more salt.","furigana":"すこ|しお|い"}],"notes":"塩 is salt as a substance or seasoning. 塩を取ってください is a request at the table to pass the salt; 塩を入れる means \"to add salt\". もう少し塩を入れる means \"to add a little more salt\". For \"salty\" one uses an adjective such as しょっぱい or 塩辛い; 塩 itself is a noun.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 14"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"醤油をかけます。","romaji":"Shōyu o kakemasu.","german":"I put soy sauce on it.","furigana":"しょうゆ"},{"japanese":"この醤油は少し甘いです。","romaji":"Kono shōyu wa sukoshi amai desu.","german":"This soy sauce is a bit sweet.","furigana":"しょうゆ|すこ|あま"}],"notes":"醤油 is soy sauce. 醤油をかける means to pour soy sauce over something; かける here means neither to make a phone call nor to put on glasses. The food it is poured on can take に. The full reading is しょうゆ with a long o, that is shōyu. 醬油 is a rare kanji spelling of 醤油; the reading remains しょうゆ.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 34"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"味噌を少し入れて、味噌汁を作ります。","romaji":"Miso o sukoshi irete, misoshiru o tsukurimasu.","german":"I add a little miso and make miso soup.","furigana":"みそ|すこ|い|みそしる|つく"},{"japanese":"味噌は冷蔵庫にあります。","romaji":"Miso wa reizōko ni arimasu.","german":"The miso is in the refrigerator.","furigana":"みそ|れいぞうこ"}],"notes":"味噌 is miso, the fermented seasoning paste, often made from soybeans. It is an ingredient; 味噌汁, by contrast, is the miso soup made from it. 味噌を入れる describes adding the paste. The card deals with the food, not the colloquial meaning \"the key point\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お弁当を持っていきます。","romaji":"Obentō o motte ikimasu.","german":"I take a bento with me.","furigana":"べんとう|も"},{"japanese":"お弁当を食べます。","romaji":"Obentō o tabemasu.","german":"I eat my bento.","furigana":"べんとう|た"}],"notes":"お弁当 is a meal packed in a box to take along. The usual お is part of the polite full form taught here and can also be used for one's own bento. 持っていく means to take something to another place; 食べる refers to the contents, not the box.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 9"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お菓子を買いました。","romaji":"Okashi o kaimashita.","german":"I bought some sweets.","furigana":"かし|か"},{"japanese":"子供たちにお菓子をあげました。","romaji":"Kodomotachi ni okashi o agemashita.","german":"I gave the children some sweets.","furigana":"こども|かし"}],"notes":"お菓子 means sweets and sweet baked goods, for example candy or cake. It need not refer to one particular kind or number of pieces. The polite お is often used in everyday speech even for one's own sweets. When giving, the gift takes を and the recipient takes に.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 11"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"りんごを一つください。","romaji":"Ringo o hitotsu kudasai.","german":"One apple, please.","furigana":"ひと"},{"japanese":"朝ご飯に、りんごを食べます。","romaji":"Asagohan ni, ringo o tabemasu.","german":"I eat an apple for breakfast.","furigana":"あさ|はん|た"}],"notes":"Here りんご means the apple as a fruit. The word is usually written in kana; 林檎 is the corresponding kanji spelling. For whole apples the general counter つ can be used: 一つ, 三つ. An apple at breakfast is expressed with 朝ご飯に as part of the meal.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 11"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"みかんが好きです。","romaji":"Mikan ga suki desu.","german":"I like mandarin oranges.","furigana":"す"},{"japanese":"みかんの皮をむいて食べます。","romaji":"Mikan no kawa o muite tabemasu.","german":"I peel a mandarin orange and eat it.","furigana":"かわ|た"}],"notes":"みかん is a mandarin orange, in everyday Japanese especially the satsuma mandarin. The word is often written in hiragana; the kanji are 蜜柑. 皮をむく means to remove the peel. 未完 and 未刊, which are read the same, mean unfinished and unpublished respectively.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 11"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コーヒーを飲みます。","romaji":"Kōhī o nomimasu.","german":"I drink coffee.","furigana":"の"},{"japanese":"この店のコーヒーはおいしいです。","romaji":"Kono mise no kōhī wa oishii desu.","german":"The coffee at this cafe is delicious.","furigana":"みせ"}],"notes":"コーヒー means coffee. The two ー marks here indicate a long o and a long i: kōhī. コーヒーを飲む describes drinking; 店のコーヒー can refer to the coffee served at a particular cafe. The noun needs no special plural form for several servings. 珈琲 is a kanji spelling of コーヒー marked as ateji. The katakana form コーヒー is the usual one; the reading remains kōhī.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ジュースを飲みますか。","romaji":"Jūsu o nomimasu ka.","german":"Would you like some juice?","furigana":"の"},{"japanese":"オレンジジュースをください。","romaji":"Orenji jūsu o kudasai.","german":"An orange juice, please."}],"notes":"Here ジュース means juice. A flavor can come directly before it, as in オレンジジュース. In everyday speech the word can also refer to sweet fruit drinks; it does not by itself guarantee any particular fruit content. The small ュ forms one syllable together with ジ, and ー lengthens u: jūsu.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 6"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お酒を飲みますか。","romaji":"Osake o nomimasu ka.","german":"Do you drink alcohol?","furigana":"さけ|の"},{"japanese":"お酒は飲みません。","romaji":"Osake wa nomimasen.","german":"I don't drink alcohol.","furigana":"さけ|の"}],"notes":"お酒 refers to alcoholic drinks in general and, depending on the context, can mean sake specifically. The お is an ordinary polite prefix and does not oblige you to speak only of another person's drink. お酒は飲みません is a natural way to say that you do not drink alcohol.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ビールを一杯ください。","romaji":"Bīru o ippai kudasai.","german":"One beer, please.","furigana":"いっぱい"},{"japanese":"このビールは冷たいです。","romaji":"Kono bīru wa tsumetai desu.","german":"This beer is cold.","furigana":"つめ"}],"notes":"ビール means beer. 一杯（いっぱい）counts a filled drinking vessel or one serving here and is read ippai. For an order, をください is used; for an evaluation of temperature, は冷たいです. The long i in ビール is written bīru.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 6"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お皿を洗います。","romaji":"Osara o araimasu.","german":"I wash the plates.","furigana":"さら|あら"},{"japanese":"お皿を二枚ください。","romaji":"Osara o nimai kudasai.","german":"Two plates, please.","furigana":"さら|にまい"}],"notes":"お皿 is a plate or dish for food. The polite prefix お is common in everyday speech. Flat plates are counted with 枚: 二枚（にまい）. お皿を洗う means to wash the dishes; the card names the tableware and not automatically the food on it.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コップに水を入れます。","romaji":"Koppu ni mizu o iremasu.","german":"I pour water into the glass.","furigana":"みず|い"},{"japanese":"このコップは割れています。","romaji":"Kono koppu wa warete imasu.","german":"This glass is broken.","furigana":"わ"}],"notes":"コップ is a drinking glass or a simple drinking cup. In コップに水を入れる, に is the container and を the contents poured in. コップは割れています, on the other hand, describes a glass that is already broken as a state. The small ッ doubles the p: koppu.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 29"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"箸を使えますか。","romaji":"Hashi o tsukaemasu ka.","german":"Can you use chopsticks?","furigana":"はし|つか"},{"japanese":"箸の使い方を教えてください。","romaji":"Hashi no tsukaikata o oshiete kudasai.","german":"Please show me how to use chopsticks.","furigana":"はし|つか|かた|おし"}],"notes":"箸（はし）are chopsticks for eating. 箸を使う means to use chopsticks; 箸の使い方 is the way of using them. A pair is counted with 一膳（いちぜん）. 橋, which is read the same, means bridge and has a different meaning; the kanji spelling prevents confusion here.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"スプーンをください。","romaji":"Supūn o kudasai.","german":"A spoon, please."},{"japanese":"スプーンでスープを食べます。","romaji":"Supūn de sūpu o tabemasu.","german":"I eat soup with a spoon.","furigana":"た"}],"notes":"スプーン means spoon. You ask for a spoon with スプーンをください. As an eating tool it takes で: スプーンで食べる. The long u is written supūn; the fishing lure that can also be called a spoon does not belong to the cutlery on this card.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 7"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"フォークを使います。","romaji":"Fōku o tsukaimasu.","german":"I use a fork.","furigana":"つか"},{"japanese":"フォークは引き出しにあります。","romaji":"Fōku wa hikidashi ni arimasu.","german":"The fork is in the drawer.","furigana":"ひ|だ"}],"notes":"Here フォーク means the fork as cutlery. フォークを使う describes using it, フォークで食べる eating with it. The long o is written fōku. The same katakana word for folk music or a baseball pitch is not meant on this card.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 7"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ナイフで切ります。","romaji":"Naifu de kirimasu.","german":"I cut it with a knife.","furigana":"き"},{"japanese":"ナイフを洗ってください。","romaji":"Naifu o aratte kudasai.","german":"Please wash the knife.","furigana":"あら"}],"notes":"ナイフ is a knife, for example for eating or cutting. As a cutting tool it takes で, while the thing cut takes を: ナイフで肉を切る. ナイフを洗う, on the other hand, treats the knife itself as the object. The reading naifu has no long ai.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 7"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"冷蔵庫は台所にあります。","romaji":"Reizōko wa daidokoro ni arimasu.","german":"The refrigerator is in the kitchen.","furigana":"れいぞうこ|だいどころ"},{"japanese":"牛乳は冷蔵庫に入れてください。","romaji":"Gyūnyū wa reizōko ni irete kudasai.","german":"Please put the milk in the refrigerator.","furigana":"ぎゅうにゅう|れいぞうこ|い"}],"notes":"冷蔵庫 is a refrigerator. For its location, に is used with ある; for something put into it, に is likewise used with 入れる. The food itself can be the topic with は or the object with を. ぞう has a long o, so reizōko; れい remains rei in Modified Hepburn.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 10"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"洗濯機がこわれました。","romaji":"Sentakuki ga kowaremashita.","german":"The washing machine has broken down.","furigana":"せんたくき"},{"japanese":"洗濯機で服を洗います。","romaji":"Sentakuki de fuku o araimasu.","german":"I wash the clothes in the washing machine.","furigana":"せんたくき|ふく|あら"}],"notes":"洗濯機 means a washing machine for clothes. With で it is named as the appliance used: 洗濯機で服を洗う. As an appliance that breaks down, it takes が with こわれる. The card reading is せんたくき; the accent of the whole word is not composed from 洗濯 and 機.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 33"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"部屋の掃除をします。","romaji":"Heya no sōji o shimasu.","german":"I clean the room.","furigana":"へや|そうじ"},{"japanese":"掃除の後で、窓を開けます。","romaji":"Sōji no ato de, mado o akemasu.","german":"After cleaning, I open the window.","furigana":"そうじ|あと|まど|あ"}],"notes":"掃除 is cleaning, for example of a room. With する it becomes the activity: 掃除をする or 掃除する. 部屋の掃除 names the cleaning of the room; 掃除の後 is the time after cleaning. The card remains a noun, even though it can be used as a する expression.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"洗濯をします。","romaji":"Sentaku o shimasu.","german":"I do the laundry.","furigana":"せんたく"},{"japanese":"洗濯が終わったら、服を干します。","romaji":"Sentaku ga owattara, fuku o hoshimasu.","german":"When the laundry is done, I hang the clothes up to dry.","furigana":"せんたく|お|ふく|ほ"}],"notes":"洗濯 is the washing of clothes, or laundry as a chore. 洗濯をする means to do laundry; 洗濯が終わる reports the end of this process. Hanging the clothes up afterwards is 服を干す. 選択, which is read the same, means selection and is a different word.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"買い物に行きます。","romaji":"Kaimono ni ikimasu.","german":"I'm going shopping.","furigana":"か|もの|い"},{"japanese":"買い物の前に、必要なものを書きます。","romaji":"Kaimono no mae ni, hitsuyō na mono o kakimasu.","german":"Before shopping, I write down what I need.","furigana":"か|もの|まえ|ひつよう|か"}],"notes":"買い物 is shopping and can also mean the goods bought. 買い物に行く names shopping as the purpose of going; 買い物をする is the activity itself. A shopping list can be made before the purchase. The word is not a freely conjugating verb ending in もの.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"食事をしましょう。","romaji":"Shokuji o shimashō.","german":"Let's have a meal.","furigana":"しょくじ"},{"japanese":"食事の時間です。","romaji":"Shokuji no jikan desu.","german":"It is mealtime.","furigana":"しょくじ|じかん"}],"notes":"食事 denotes a meal or the act of having a meal. 食事をする means to eat, and 食事の時間 means mealtime. The invitation 食事をしましょう is polite and consists of する with the ending ましょう. Diet as a lasting regimen is another meaning and is not meant here.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"スポーツが好きです。","romaji":"Supōtsu ga suki desu.","german":"I like sports.","furigana":"す"},{"japanese":"週末は公園でスポーツをします。","romaji":"Shūmatsu wa kōen de supōtsu o shimasu.","german":"On weekends I play sports in the park.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|こうえん"}],"notes":"スポーツ means sports in general. With が好き one expresses interest or preference, without specifying whether one plays or watches. スポーツをする means to play sports; で names the place. The long o is written supōtsu and is not pronounced as an additional syllable.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 9"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"サッカーをします。","romaji":"Sakkā o shimasu.","german":"I play soccer."},{"japanese":"サッカーの試合を見ました。","romaji":"Sakkā no shiai o mimashita.","german":"I watched a soccer match.","furigana":"しあい|み"}],"notes":"サッカー means soccer. サッカーをする means to play soccer oneself; サッカーの試合を見る means to watch a match. The small ッ doubles k and ー lengthens a: sakkā. Names or textile terms with the same spelling do not belong to this sport.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 6"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"テニスが上手です。","romaji":"Tenisu ga jōzu desu.","german":"He/She is good at tennis.","furigana":"じょうず"},{"japanese":"テニスをしたことがありますか。","romaji":"Tenisu o shita koto ga arimasu ka.","german":"Have you ever played tennis?"}],"notes":"テニス means tennis. テニスが上手です evaluates skill at playing; テニスをする means to play. ～したことがありますか, on the other hand, asks whether someone has ever had the experience, without claiming good performance. 上手 is read じょうず here, that is jōzu.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 6"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"野球を見ます。","romaji":"Yakyū o mimasu.","german":"I watch baseball.","furigana":"やきゅう|み"},{"japanese":"日本では野球が人気です。","romaji":"Nihon de wa yakyū ga ninki desu.","german":"Baseball is popular in Japan.","furigana":"にほん|やきゅう|にんき"}],"notes":"野球 is baseball as a sport. 野球をする means \"to play baseball\", 野球を見る \"to watch baseball\". A single game can be called 野球の試合. The long ū belongs to the reading やきゅう; the spelling consists of 野 and 球.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 9"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"試合を見に行きます。","romaji":"Shiai o mi ni ikimasu.","german":"I'm going to watch a game.","furigana":"しあい|み|い"},{"japanese":"今日の試合は面白かったです。","romaji":"Kyō no shiai wa omoshirokatta desu.","german":"Today's game was interesting.","furigana":"きょう|しあい|おもしろ"},{"japanese":"明日の試合に出ます。","romaji":"Ashita no shiai ni demasu.","german":"I will take part in tomorrow's match.","furigana":"あした|しあい|で"}],"notes":"試合 is a sports game or a contest between opponents. 試合を見る means \"to watch a game\", 試合に出る \"to take part in a contest\". A game for entertainment can be called ゲーム; 試合 emphasizes competition. The noun can also be combined with する, but is learned here as a noun.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 21"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ゲームをしましょう。","romaji":"Gēmu o shimashō.","german":"Let's play a game."},{"japanese":"子供とゲームをしました。","romaji":"Kodomo to gēmu o shimashita.","german":"I played a game with the child.","furigana":"こども"},{"japanese":"このゲームのルールは簡単です。","romaji":"Kono gēmu no rūru wa kantan desu.","german":"The rules of this game are simple.","furigana":"かんたん"}],"notes":"ゲーム is a game and is often used with する: ゲームをする. Depending on the context it can mean a board game, a video game or another rule-based game. ゲームのルール are the rules of the game. The long ē in gēmu corresponds to the length mark ー.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 28"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ピアノを弾きます。","romaji":"Piano o hikimasu.","german":"I play the piano.","furigana":"ひ"},{"japanese":"ピアノを弾くことができますか。","romaji":"Piano o hiku koto ga dekimasu ka.","german":"Can you play the piano?","furigana":"ひ"},{"japanese":"ピアノが大きいので、部屋に入りません。","romaji":"Piano ga ōkii node, heya ni hairimasen.","german":"The piano is big, so it won't fit in the room.","furigana":"おお|へや|はい"}],"notes":"ピアノ means the piano as an instrument here. It is played with 弾く: ピアノを弾く. The construction ～ことができる asks about an ability. With ピアノが大きい, on the other hand, the size of the instrument can be described. The musical direction \"softly\" is a different use of the identically written word.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 18"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ギターを弾いています。","romaji":"Gitā o hiite imasu.","german":"I am playing the guitar.","furigana":"ひ"},{"japanese":"ギターを弾くのが好きです。","romaji":"Gitā o hiku no ga suki desu.","german":"I like playing the guitar.","furigana":"ひ|す"},{"japanese":"友達にギターを借りました。","romaji":"Tomodachi ni gitā o karimashita.","german":"I borrowed a guitar from a friend.","furigana":"ともだち|か"}],"notes":"ギター is a guitar. For playing it, 弾く is used: ギターを弾く, with the te-form 弾いて. ギターを借りる means \"to borrow a guitar\"; the person you borrow it from takes に. The long ā at the end is written with ー.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 9"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"絵を描きます。","romaji":"E o kakimasu.","german":"I draw a picture.","furigana":"え|か"},{"japanese":"この絵は誰が描きましたか。","romaji":"Kono e wa dare ga kakimashita ka.","german":"Who painted this picture?","furigana":"え|だれ|か"},{"japanese":"壁に絵をかけました。","romaji":"Kabe ni e o kakemashita.","german":"I hung a picture on the wall.","furigana":"かべ|え"}],"notes":"絵 is a picture, drawing or painting. 絵を描く means \"to draw or paint a picture\"; the verb is read かく in both cases. A photograph is normally 写真. A physical 絵 can be looked at, hung up or drawn; the word does not distinguish between painting and drawing techniques.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 9"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"踊りが上手です。","romaji":"Odori ga jōzu desu.","german":"She dances well.","furigana":"おど|じょうず"},{"japanese":"日本の踊りを見ました。","romaji":"Nihon no odori o mimashita.","german":"I saw a Japanese dance.","furigana":"にほん|おど|み"}],"notes":"踊り is the noun \"dance\"; the related verb is 踊る \"to dance\". 踊りが上手 describes a person's skill at dancing. 日本の踊り can mean a Japanese dance form or a corresponding performance. For one's own action, one says 踊る, not 踊りです.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"歯を磨きます。","romaji":"Ha o migakimasu.","german":"I brush my teeth.","furigana":"は|みが"},{"japanese":"毎日歯を磨きます。","romaji":"Mainichi ha o migakimasu.","german":"I brush my teeth every day.","furigana":"まいにちは|みが"},{"japanese":"歯が痛いので、歯医者に行きます。","romaji":"Ha ga itai node, haisha ni ikimasu.","german":"My tooth hurts, so I am going to the dentist.","furigana":"は|いた|はいしゃ|い"}],"notes":"歯 means one tooth or several teeth; the number is clear from the context. 歯を磨く means \"to brush one's teeth\". For a toothache, 歯が痛い is used, with が marking the aching part of the body. 歯医者 is a dentist or a dental practice. The meaning practiced here is a person's teeth.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 16"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"髪を切りました。","romaji":"Kami o kirimashita.","german":"I got my hair cut.","furigana":"かみ|き"},{"japanese":"髪を切りたいです。","romaji":"Kami o kiritai desu.","german":"I want to get my hair cut.","furigana":"かみ|き"},{"japanese":"妹は髪が長いです。","romaji":"Imōto wa kami ga nagai desu.","german":"My younger sister has long hair.","furigana":"いもうと|かみ|なが"}],"notes":"髪 refers to the hair on the head. 髪を切る means \"to cut one's hair\" and in everyday speech can also describe a visit to the hairdresser, where one has it cut. 髪が長い describes long hair. For body hair or animal hair, 毛 is often used; the 髪 learned here is hair on the head.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 16"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"指を切りました。","romaji":"Yubi o kirimashita.","german":"I cut my finger.","furigana":"ゆび|き"},{"japanese":"指を切ってしまいました。","romaji":"Yubi o kitte shimaimashita.","german":"I accidentally cut my finger.","furigana":"ゆび|き"},{"japanese":"指で数を数えます。","romaji":"Yubi de kazu o kazoemasu.","german":"I count with my fingers.","furigana":"ゆび|かず|かぞ"}],"notes":"指 is a finger or toe. For a clear distinction one says 手の指 \"finger\" and 足の指 \"toe\". 指を切る in everyday use often means \"to cut one's finger\", not necessarily to cut it off. With 指で the finger can be named as a tool, for example when counting.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"弟の鼻は小さいです。","romaji":"Otōto no hana wa chīsai desu.","german":"My younger brother has a small nose.","furigana":"おとうと|はな|ちい"},{"japanese":"鼻で息をします。","romaji":"Hana de iki o shimasu.","german":"I breathe through my nose.","furigana":"はな|いき"}],"notes":"鼻 is the nose. 鼻が小さい describes its size; 鼻で息をする means \"to breathe through the nose\". The reading はな is also used for 花 \"flower\"; the spelling distinguishes the two words. The examples explicitly describe the body part and its function.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 16"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"首が痛いです。","romaji":"Kubi ga itai desu.","german":"My neck hurts.","furigana":"くび|いた"},{"japanese":"寒いので、首にマフラーを巻きました。","romaji":"Samui node, kubi ni mafurā o makimashita.","german":"Because it is cold, I wrapped a scarf around my neck.","furigana":"さむ|くび|ま"}],"notes":"首 denotes the neck area as the connection between head and body. 首が痛い can mean \"my neck hurts\" or \"my throat area hurts\", depending on the spot. For pain in the throat, のど is often used instead. 首にマフラーを巻く means \"to wrap a scarf around one's neck\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"肩が痛いです。","romaji":"Kata ga itai desu.","german":"My shoulder hurts.","furigana":"かた|いた"},{"japanese":"肩がこりました。","romaji":"Kata ga korimashita.","german":"My shoulders are stiff.","furigana":"かた"},{"japanese":"かばんを肩にかけます。","romaji":"Kaban o kata ni kakemasu.","german":"I hang my bag over my shoulder.","furigana":"かた"}],"notes":"肩 is a shoulder or the shoulder area. 肩が痛い describes pain, 肩がこる describes stiffness. Both are expressed in Japanese with が on the body part. A bag is carried over the shoulder, for example, with かばんを肩にかける; に then marks the place where it hangs.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"腕が痛いです。","romaji":"Ude ga itai desu.","german":"My arm hurts.","furigana":"うで|いた"},{"japanese":"腕時計をしていますか。","romaji":"Udedokei o shite imasu ka.","german":"Are you wearing a wristwatch?","furigana":"うでどけい"},{"japanese":"腕を上げてください。","romaji":"Ude o agete kudasai.","german":"Please raise your arm.","furigana":"うで|あ"}],"notes":"腕 is the arm. 腕が痛い describes arm pain; 腕を上げる means \"to raise one's arm\". In 腕時計 \"wristwatch\", 腕 is a component of a compound word of its own. Its pronunciation and accent must be learned separately; the accent given here belongs to the full word 腕.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"膝が痛いです。","romaji":"Hiza ga itai desu.","german":"My knee hurts.","furigana":"ひざ|いた"},{"japanese":"膝が痛くて走れません。","romaji":"Hiza ga itakute hashiremasen.","german":"My knee hurts and I can't run.","furigana":"ひざ|いた|はし"},{"japanese":"猫が膝の上にいます。","romaji":"Neko ga hiza no ue ni imasu.","german":"There is a cat on my lap.","furigana":"ねこ|ひざ|うえ"}],"notes":"膝 is the knee; when sitting it can also mean the lap. 膝が痛い means \"my knee hurts\". 猫が膝の上にいる describes a cat on the lap, not necessarily on a single knee joint. The reading is ひざ; how many knees are meant becomes clear from the context.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"風邪をひきました。","romaji":"Kaze o hikimashita.","german":"I caught a cold.","furigana":"かぜ"},{"japanese":"風邪が治ったので、学校に行きます。","romaji":"Kaze ga naotta node, gakkō ni ikimasu.","german":"My cold has gone, so I am going to school.","furigana":"かぜ|なお|がっこう|い"}],"notes":"風邪 is a cold. The fixed phrase 風邪をひく means \"to catch a cold\"; 風邪が治る means \"the cold goes away\". The spelling distinguishes 風邪 from 風 \"wind\", although both are read かぜ. The examples use 風邪 in the everyday sense of \"cold\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"熱があります。","romaji":"Netsu ga arimasu.","german":"I have a fever.","furigana":"ねつ"},{"japanese":"熱がありますから、病院に行きます。","romaji":"Netsu ga arimasu kara, byōin ni ikimasu.","german":"I have a fever, so I am going to the hospital.","furigana":"ねつ|びょういん|い"},{"japanese":"ストーブの熱で部屋が暖かくなります。","romaji":"Sutōbu no netsu de heya ga atatakaku narimasu.","german":"The heat of the stove makes the room warm.","furigana":"ねつ|へや|あたた"}],"notes":"熱 is fever, or warmth and heat. In a conversation about the body, 熱がある usually means \"to have a fever\". ストーブの熱, on the other hand, means the warmth of a stove. The word is a noun; \"hot\" as a property of an object is 熱い, which has a different form.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 17"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"頭が痛いです。","romaji":"Atama ga itai desu.","german":"My head hurts.","furigana":"あたま|いた"},{"japanese":"お腹が痛いです。","romaji":"Onaka ga itai desu.","german":"My stomach hurts.","furigana":"なか|いた"},{"japanese":"痛いときは、手を上げてください。","romaji":"Itai toki wa, te o agete kudasai.","german":"If it hurts, please raise your hand.","furigana":"いた|て|あ"}],"notes":"痛い is an i-adjective for pain. The aching part of the body takes が: 頭が痛い, お腹が痛い. Without a named body part, 痛い can also mean \"that hurts\". The negative is 痛くない, the past tense 痛かった. \"To be in pain\" is therefore not expressed with 痛いをする.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 12"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"とても眠いです。","romaji":"Totemo nemui desu.","german":"I am very sleepy.","furigana":"ねむ"},{"japanese":"昨日よく眠れなかったので、眠いです。","romaji":"Kinō yoku nemurenakatta node, nemui desu.","german":"I couldn't sleep well yesterday, so I am sleepy.","furigana":"きのう|ねむ|ねむ"},{"japanese":"眠いので、今日は早く寝ます。","romaji":"Nemui node, kyō wa hayaku nemasu.","german":"Because I am sleepy, I will go to bed early today.","furigana":"ねむ|きょう|はや|ね"}],"notes":"眠い means \"sleepy\", that is, wanting to sleep. It is not the same as 疲れた \"exhausted, tired\", although both can apply at once. This i-adjective forms 眠くない and 眠かった. 眠くなる means \"to become sleepy\"; 眠いので gives sleepiness as the reason for a further action.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 19"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"プレゼントをもらって嬉しいです。","romaji":"Purezento o moratte ureshii desu.","german":"I am happy to have received a present.","furigana":"うれ"},{"japanese":"また会えて嬉しいです。","romaji":"Mata aete ureshii desu.","german":"I am glad to see you again.","furigana":"あ|うれ"}],"notes":"嬉しい describes personal joy about something, such as a present or a reunion. A cause can be given with the te-form: もらって嬉しい, 会えて嬉しい. It is an i-adjective with the forms 嬉しかった and 嬉しくない. 楽しい, on the other hand, often describes an activity or experience being fun.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 30"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"映画を見て悲しかったです。","romaji":"Eiga o mite kanashikatta desu.","german":"I was sad after watching the movie.","furigana":"えいが|み|かな"},{"japanese":"友達が引っ越して悲しいです。","romaji":"Tomodachi ga hikkoshite kanashii desu.","german":"My friend moved away and I am sad.","furigana":"ともだち|ひ|こ|かな"},{"japanese":"これは悲しい話です。","romaji":"Kore wa kanashii hanashi desu.","german":"This is a sad story.","furigana":"かな|はなし"}],"notes":"悲しい can describe one's own feeling of being sad or the sad content of a story. 映画を見て悲しかった means here that the person was sad after the movie; 悲しい話 judges the story itself. This i-adjective forms 悲しかった in the past tense and 悲しくない in the negative.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 33"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一人で寂しいです。","romaji":"Hitori de sabishii desu.","german":"I feel lonely being alone.","furigana":"ひとり|さび"},{"japanese":"友達が国へ帰って、寂しくなりました。","romaji":"Tomodachi ga kuni e kaette, sabishiku narimashita.","german":"My friend went back to his/her home country, and I have become lonely.","furigana":"ともだち|くに|かえ|さび"},{"japanese":"この道は人が少なくて、寂しいです。","romaji":"Kono michi wa hito ga sukunakute, sabishii desu.","german":"There are few people on this road; it feels deserted.","furigana":"みち|ひと|すく|さび"}],"notes":"寂しい describes loneliness or a lack of closeness to other people. For a place it can also mean \"deserted, sparsely populated\". 寂しくなる means \"to become lonely\"; the i-ending changes to く before なる. Sadness and loneliness can occur together, but here 寂しい emphasizes the absence of company or of life in a place.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 13"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの映画は怖いです。","romaji":"Ano eiga wa kowai desu.","german":"That movie is scary.","furigana":"えいが|こわ"},{"japanese":"あの映画は怖かったです。","romaji":"Ano eiga wa kowakatta desu.","german":"That movie was scary.","furigana":"えいが|こわ"},{"japanese":"犬が怖くて、近くに行けません。","romaji":"Inu ga kowakute, chikaku ni ikemasen.","german":"I am afraid of dogs and can't go near them.","furigana":"いぬ|こわ|ちか|い"}],"notes":"怖い describes something frightening or one's own fear of it. あの映画は怖い judges a movie as scary. 犬が怖い, on the other hand, can mean \"I am afraid of dogs\"; the frightening trigger takes が. This i-adjective forms 怖かった and 怖くない. It does not mean \"bad\" in general.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"間違えて恥ずかしいです。","romaji":"Machigaete hazukashii desu.","german":"I am embarrassed that I made a mistake.","furigana":"まちが|は"},{"japanese":"みんなの前で話すのは恥ずかしいです。","romaji":"Minna no mae de hanasu no wa hazukashii desu.","german":"I am embarrassed to speak in front of everyone.","furigana":"まえ|はな|は"}],"notes":"恥ずかしい describes embarrassment, shame or self-consciousness. After a mistake it can mean \"I am embarrassed\"; in front of many people it can express shyness about speaking. It is an i-adjective: 恥ずかしかった, 恥ずかしくない. A te-form like 間違えて can name the cause of the feeling.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 39"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"隣の部屋がうるさいです。","romaji":"Tonari no heya ga urusai desu.","german":"The room next door is noisy.","furigana":"となり|へや"},{"japanese":"うるさいなあ。ちょっと静かにして。","romaji":"Urusai nā. Chotto shizuka ni shite.","german":"How annoying! Be quiet for a bit.","furigana":"しず"}],"notes":"うるさい means \"noisy, disturbing\" and can also describe something annoying. In 隣の部屋がうるさい, it is about disturbing noise. Saying うるさい！ directly to a person can sound harsh and is meant to silence them. The examples distinguish a description from an irritated demand. A polite request would be something like 静かにしてください. 五月蝿い is a special spelling of うるさい marked as ateji. The usual kana form is easier to use; the reading is not built up from the individual kanji.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 32"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"手が汚いです。","romaji":"Te ga kitanai desu.","german":"My hands are dirty.","furigana":"て|きたな"},{"japanese":"部屋が汚いから掃除します。","romaji":"Heya ga kitanai kara sōji shimasu.","german":"The room is dirty, so I will clean it.","furigana":"へや|きたな|そうじ"}],"notes":"汚い is an い-adjective meaning dirty. It stands directly before a noun: 汚い手. As a polite statement it is 汚いです, and in the negative 汚くないです. With から, dirtiness can give the reason for cleaning afterwards. This card does not use it as an insulting judgment of a person.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 39"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"危ない！車が来ますよ。","romaji":"Abunai! Kuruma ga kimasu yo.","german":"Watch out! A car is coming.","furigana":"あぶ|くるま|き"},{"japanese":"この道は危ないですから、気をつけてください。","romaji":"Kono michi wa abunai desu kara, ki o tsukete kudasai.","german":"This road is dangerous, so please be careful.","furigana":"みち|あぶ|き"}],"notes":"危ない means dangerous or risky. Exclaimed on its own, 危ない！ is also the warning \"Watch out!\"; this fixed warning function supplements the normal adjectival statement. This い-adjective forms 危なくない for \"not dangerous\". It refers to a concrete danger and is not a general translation of \"difficult\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 17"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しいパソコンが欲しいです。","romaji":"Atarashii pasokon ga hoshii desu.","german":"I want a new computer.","furigana":"あたら|ほ"},{"japanese":"誕生日に何が欲しいですか。","romaji":"Tanjōbi ni nani ga hoshii desu ka.","german":"What do you want for your birthday?","furigana":"たんじょうび|なに|ほ"}],"notes":"欲しい is an い-adjective for the wish to have something. The desired object takes が, not を as with an ordinary purchase verb. In a statement one typically speaks of one's own wish; a question can ask about the other person's wish. For a desired action, the verb form ～たい is used instead.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 13"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"正しい答えはどれですか。","romaji":"Tadashii kotae wa dore desu ka.","german":"Which is the correct answer?","furigana":"ただ|こた"},{"japanese":"名前を正しく書いてください。","romaji":"Namae o tadashiku kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write your name correctly.","furigana":"なまえ|ただ|か"}],"notes":"正しい means correct or right. Directly before a noun it stays 正しい, as in 正しい答え. When the correct performance of an action is meant, it becomes 正しく: 正しく書く. The negative is 正しくない. The moral sense \"just\" is another meaning that is not taught here.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 35"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"珍しい花ですね。","romaji":"Mezurashii hana desu ne.","german":"That's a rare flower, isn't it?","furigana":"めずら|はな"},{"japanese":"今日は雪が降っています。ここでは珍しいです。","romaji":"Kyō wa yuki ga futte imasu. Koko de wa mezurashii desu.","german":"It is snowing today. That is unusual here.","furigana":"きょう|ゆき|ふ|めずら"}],"notes":"珍しい means rare or unusual in the given context. It can describe a thing or evaluate an event that does not often occur in that place. It does not automatically mean \"newly bought\". As an い-adjective it stands directly before 花 and forms 珍しくない for \"not unusual\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 35"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"厚い本を読んでいます。","romaji":"Atsui hon o yonde imasu.","german":"I am reading a thick book.","furigana":"あつ|ほん|よ"},{"japanese":"寒いので、厚いコートを着ます。","romaji":"Samui node, atsui kōto o kimasu.","german":"Because it is cold, I will wear a thick coat.","furigana":"さむ|あつ|き"}],"notes":"厚い describes the thickness of an object, for example a book or a coat. It is the counterpart of 薄い. For the width of a pen or a trunk, 太い is used instead. 暑い and 熱い, which are read the same, concern heat; they are not spelling variants of this thickness word.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 42"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"薄い紙を使います。","romaji":"Usui kami o tsukaimasu.","german":"Ich benutze dünnes Papier.","furigana":"うす|かみ|つか"},{"japanese":"薄い青のシャツを買いました。","romaji":"Usui ao no shatsu o kaimashita.","german":"Ich habe ein hellblaues Hemd gekauft.","furigana":"うす|あお|か"}],"notes":"薄い kann die geringe Dicke eines Materials oder eine helle, blasse Farbe beschreiben. 薄い紙 ist dünnes Papier; 薄い青 ist helles Blau. Der Bezug entscheidet, ob die deutsche Übersetzung dünn oder hell lautet. Das い-Adjektiv bildet 薄く und 薄くない.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 42"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"太いペンを使います。","romaji":"Futoi pen o tsukaimasu.","german":"I use a thick pen.","furigana":"ふと|つか"},{"japanese":"この歌手は声が太いです。","romaji":"Kono kashu wa koe ga futoi desu.","german":"This singer has a deep, full voice.","furigana":"かしゅ|こえ|ふと"}],"notes":"太い describes, for example, a thick pen or trunk, that is, a large cross-section. For a voice it can also mean deep and full: 声が太い. This second use is not a statement about the singer's physical thickness. As an い-adjective it forms 太く; for the thickness of a book or material, 厚い is usually used.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 34"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"細い道を歩きます。","romaji":"Hosoi michi o arukimasu.","german":"I walk along a narrow road.","furigana":"ほそ|みち|ある"},{"japanese":"細い糸でボタンをつけます。","romaji":"Hosoi ito de botan o tsukemasu.","german":"I sew the button on with a thin thread.","furigana":"ほそ|いと"}],"notes":"細い means thin or narrow, as in a thread or a narrow path. It describes a small width or a small cross-section. A thin sheet of paper, on the other hand, is usually 薄い紙. As an い-adjective it stands directly before 糸 or 道 and forms 細く.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 34"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"若い人が多いです。","romaji":"Wakai hito ga ōi desu.","german":"There are many young people.","furigana":"わか|ひと|おお"},{"japanese":"先生は若くて元気です。","romaji":"Sensei wa wakakute genki desu.","german":"The teacher is young and energetic.","furigana":"せんせい|わか|げんき"}],"notes":"Here 若い refers to young age. It stands directly before 人 and is joined to another quality with ～くて: 若くて元気. The negative is 若くない. The word does not automatically mean immature; that judgment is a different, context-dependent use.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 16"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"授業は何時からですか。","romaji":"Jugyō wa nanji kara desu ka.","german":"What time does class start?","furigana":"じゅぎょう|なんじ"},{"japanese":"午後の授業は何ですか。","romaji":"Gogo no jugyō wa nan desu ka.","german":"What class do you have in the afternoon?","furigana":"ごご|じゅぎょう|なん"}],"notes":"授業 means a class or a lesson. 何時から asks for its start time; 午後の授業 asks about the class in the afternoon. It denotes the event, whereas 教室 is the room. じゅぎょう has a long o at the end and is written jugyō.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 11"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"プレゼントをあげます。","romaji":"Purezento o agemasu.","german":"I give a present."},{"japanese":"誕生日のプレゼントを買いました。","romaji":"Tanjōbi no purezento o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a birthday present.","furigana":"たんじょうび|か"}],"notes":"プレゼント is a present. When giving, it takes を with あげる; when receiving, for example, を with もらう. 誕生日のプレゼント means a present for a birthday. With する the word can also be used as \"to give a present\"; the card itself remains a noun.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 7"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"約束を守ります。","romaji":"Yakusoku o mamorimasu.","german":"I keep my promise.","furigana":"やくそく|まも"},{"japanese":"友達と約束があります。","romaji":"Tomodachi to yakusoku ga arimasu.","german":"I have an appointment with a friend.","furigana":"ともだち|やくそく"}],"notes":"約束 can be a promise or an appointment. 約束を守る means to keep a promise; 約束がある means to have a fixed appointment. と names the other person involved. The word alone does not say whether the appointment is romantic, friendly or business-related.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 9"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"心が温かいです。","romaji":"Kokoro ga atatakai desu.","german":"She has a warm heart.","furigana":"こころ|あたた"},{"japanese":"大切なことを心に決めました。","romaji":"Taisetsu na koto o kokoro ni kimemashita.","german":"I made up my mind about something important.","furigana":"たいせつ|こころ|き"}],"notes":"心（こころ）denotes inner feeling, the mind, or the heart in a figurative sense. 心が温かい describes warm-heartedness; 心に決める means to firmly resolve something inwardly. For the physical heart, 心臓 is common. The card uses こころ, not other 心 readings automatically taken from compounds.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 48"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"昨日の夜、変な夢を見ました。","romaji":"Kinō no yoru, hen na yume o mimashita.","german":"Letzte Nacht hatte ich einen seltsamen Traum.","furigana":"きのう|よる|へん|ゆめ|み"},{"japanese":"将来の夢は何ですか。","romaji":"Shōrai no yume wa nan desu ka.","german":"Was ist Ihr Traum für die Zukunft?","furigana":"しょうらい|ゆめ|なん"}],"notes":"夢 kann ein Traum im Schlaf oder ein Wunsch für die Zukunft sein. 夢を見る heißt im Schlaf träumen; 将来の夢 nennt eine persönliche Zukunftsvorstellung. Die deutsche Übersetzung Traum ist in beiden Fällen möglich, doch der Kontext unterscheidet Erlebnis und Ziel. Es wird hier nicht als bloße Illusion abgewertet.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 21"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いい思い出です。","romaji":"Ii omoide desu.","german":"It is a good memory.","furigana":"おも|で"},{"japanese":"旅行の思い出を友達に話します。","romaji":"Ryokō no omoide o tomodachi ni hanashimasu.","german":"I tell a friend about my memories of the trip.","furigana":"りょこう|おも|で|ともだち|はな"}],"notes":"思い出 is a memory of a past experience. A single pleasant memory can be いい思い出; one can tell about travel memories with 思い出を話す. The noun denotes the content of the memory. Actively recalling something, by contrast, is expressed with the verb 思い出す.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 23"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"世界中を旅行したいです。","romaji":"Sekaijū o ryokō shitai desu.","german":"I want to travel all over the world.","furigana":"せかいじゅう|りょこう"},{"japanese":"世界にはいろいろな国があります。","romaji":"Sekai ni wa iroiro na kuni ga arimasu.","german":"There are various countries in the world.","furigana":"せかい|くに"}],"notes":"Here 世界 means the world in the geographical sense. 世界中（せかいじゅう）means the whole world or everywhere in the world; 中 is read じゅう there. In 世界には, に names the space in which countries exist. A field of expertise as a particular \"world\" is another use that these examples do not need.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 12"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"文字を書いてください。","romaji":"Moji o kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write the characters.","furigana":"もじ|か"},{"japanese":"この文字が読めません。","romaji":"Kono moji ga yomemasen.","german":"I can't read this character.","furigana":"もじ|よ"}],"notes":"文字 is a written character, such as a letter or a kanji. It does not by itself mean a whole word. 文字を書く means to write characters, 文字が読めない means to be unable to read them. The reading of this card is もじ; other readings in technical terms are not equated with it.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"漢字を覚えます。","romaji":"Kanji o oboemasu.","german":"I memorize kanji.","furigana":"かんじ|おぼ"},{"japanese":"この漢字は難しいです。","romaji":"Kono kanji wa muzukashii desu.","german":"This kanji is difficult.","furigana":"かんじ|むずか"}],"notes":"漢字 are the characters of Chinese origin used in Japanese. The word can refer to one character or several, with no special plural ending. 漢字を覚える means to memorize them; この漢字は難しい judges a specific character as difficult. A character and its complete word are not automatically the same thing.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 9"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ひらがなを書けますか。","romaji":"Hiragana o kakemasu ka.","german":"Can you write hiragana?","furigana":"か"},{"japanese":"名前をひらがなで書いてください。","romaji":"Namae o hiragana de kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write your name in hiragana.","furigana":"なまえ|か"}],"notes":"ひらがな is the Japanese kana script, used among other things for grammatical endings and particles. で is used for writing in this script: ひらがなで書く. ひらがなを書けますか asks about the ability to write the characters. The word itself is often written in hiragana.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 9"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"カタカナは外来語に使います。","romaji":"Katakana wa gairaigo ni tsukaimasu.","german":"Katakana is used for loanwords.","furigana":"がいらいご|つか"},{"japanese":"カタカナも勉強しています。","romaji":"Katakana mo benkyō shite imasu.","german":"I am studying katakana too.","furigana":"べんきょう"}],"notes":"カタカナ is the Japanese kana script, used especially for loanwords. It is also used for other purposes such as emphasis; it therefore does not mean exclusively foreign names. カタカナを勉強する means to study the script. The a vowels in カ and ナ are short.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"紙をください。","romaji":"Kami o kudasai.","german":"Please give me some paper.","furigana":"かみ"},{"japanese":"紙に名前を書いてください。","romaji":"Kami ni namae o kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write your name on the paper.","furigana":"かみ|なまえ|か"}],"notes":"紙（かみ）means paper. A request for paper uses をください; the surface written on takes に: 紙に名前を書く. Sheets of paper are counted with 枚. 髪 for hair and 神 for a deity are different words that are read the same, not meanings of this paper card.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 7"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"封筒に入れてください。","romaji":"Fūtō ni irete kudasai.","german":"Please put it in the envelope.","furigana":"ふうとう|い"},{"japanese":"封筒に切手を貼ります。","romaji":"Fūtō ni kitte o harimasu.","german":"I put a stamp on the envelope.","furigana":"ふうとう|きって|は"}],"notes":"封筒 is an envelope. 封筒に入れる means to put something inside; 封筒に切手を貼る means to stick a stamp on its surface. に can therefore mean a target container or a place to stick; the verb decides. Both vowels in ふうとう are long: fūtō.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 11"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"電話番号を教えてください。","romaji":"Denwa bangō o oshiete kudasai.","german":"Please tell me your telephone number.","furigana":"でんわばんごう|おし"},{"japanese":"あなたの部屋の番号は何番ですか。","romaji":"Anata no heya no bangō wa nanban desu ka.","german":"What is your room number?","furigana":"へや|ばんごう|なんばん"}],"notes":"番号 is a number or sequence of digits used for identification. 電話番号 means telephone number; 部屋の番号 the room number. 何番（なんばん）asks which number. It is about an identifier, not automatically the quantity of things present. The long o at the end is written bangō.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"住所を書いてください。","romaji":"Jūsho o kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write your address.","furigana":"じゅうしょ|か"},{"japanese":"新しい住所を友達に教えました。","romaji":"Atarashii jūsho o tomodachi ni oshiemashita.","german":"I told my friend my new address.","furigana":"あたら|じゅうしょ|ともだち|おし"}],"notes":"住所 is the postal or physical address of a home or business. 住所を書く means to write it down, 住所を教える to tell it to someone. The person who learns it takes に. An e-mail address is normally called メールアドレス and is not designated by 住所 alone.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 14"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地下鉄に乗ります。","romaji":"Chikatetsu ni norimasu.","german":"I take the subway.","furigana":"ちかてつ|の"},{"japanese":"地下鉄で会社に行きます。","romaji":"Chikatetsu de kaisha ni ikimasu.","german":"I go to work by subway.","furigana":"ちかてつ|かいしゃ|い"},{"japanese":"地下鉄の中で財布をなくしました。","romaji":"Chikatetsu no naka de saifu o nakushimashita.","german":"I lost my wallet on the subway.","furigana":"ちかてつ|なか|さいふ"}],"notes":"地下鉄 is the subway. 地下鉄に乗る describes boarding or using a subway; 地下鉄で行く names it as the means of transport. に belongs to 乗る here, and で marks the means of travel. The word refers to the railway and its network, not merely to the underground space 地下.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"信号のところで道を渡ってください。","romaji":"Shingō no tokoro de michi o watatte kudasai.","german":"Please cross the street at the traffic light.","furigana":"しんごう|みち|わた"},{"japanese":"信号が赤ですから、止まってください。","romaji":"Shingō ga aka desu kara, tomatte kudasai.","german":"The light is red, so please stop.","furigana":"しんごう|あか|と"}],"notes":"Here 信号 means a traffic light. 信号が赤 is a red light; 信号のところで道を渡る means \"to cross the street at the traffic light\". The light serves as a landmark. The more precise 信号機 is also possible; the examples practice the everyday short form 信号.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 23"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"交差点を右に曲がります。","romaji":"Kōsaten o migi ni magarimasu.","german":"I turn right at the intersection.","furigana":"こうさてん|みぎ|ま"},{"japanese":"次の交差点を左に曲がってください。","romaji":"Tsugi no kōsaten o hidari ni magatte kudasai.","german":"Please turn left at the next intersection.","furigana":"つぎ|こうさてん|ひだり|ま"},{"japanese":"交差点の近くに銀行があります。","romaji":"Kōsaten no chikaku ni ginkō ga arimasu.","german":"There is a bank near the intersection.","furigana":"こうさてん|ちか|ぎんこう"}],"notes":"交差点 is a road intersection. In directions, 交差点を右に曲がる can be used: を marks the point where the route changes direction. 交差点の近く, on the other hand, means the area around the intersection. The long ō in kōsaten belongs to the reading こうさてん.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 23"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"角を曲がってください。","romaji":"Kado o magatte kudasai.","german":"Please turn at the corner.","furigana":"かど|ま"},{"japanese":"あの角を曲がってください。","romaji":"Ano kado o magatte kudasai.","german":"Please turn at that corner.","furigana":"かど|ま"},{"japanese":"机の角に足をぶつけました。","romaji":"Tsukue no kado ni ashi o butsukemashita.","german":"I bumped my foot on the corner of the desk.","furigana":"つくえ|かど|あし"}],"notes":"Here 角 is read かど and means a corner or edge. In directions, 角を曲がる means \"to turn at the corner\". 机の角 is the corner of a desk. Other readings of 角, such as つの \"horn\" or かく in geometric words, do not belong to the reading かど practiced here.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 23"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大きい建物が見えます。","romaji":"Ōkii tatemono ga miemasu.","german":"I can see a large building.","furigana":"おお|たてもの|み"},{"japanese":"あの建物は何ですか。","romaji":"Ano tatemono wa nan desu ka.","german":"What is that building?","furigana":"たてもの|なん"}],"notes":"建物 is a building as a structure, such as a house, a school or an office building. 建物が見える describes that a building is visible. あの建物は何ですか asks what a particular building is or what it is used for. The word is more general than a specific building type such as 学校 or 病院.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 23"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"三階に行きます。","romaji":"Sangai ni ikimasu.","german":"I'm going to the 3rd floor.","furigana":"さんがい|い"},{"japanese":"このビルは何階建てですか。","romaji":"Kono biru wa nangai-date desu ka.","german":"How many stories does this building have?","furigana":"なんがいだ"}],"notes":"階 denotes a floor of a building and is used after numbers to count floors. In Japan 一階 is usually the ground floor; 三階 is therefore the floor two levels above it (the 3rd floor in the American sense). 三階 is read さんがい or さんかい, 何階 なんがい or なんかい. The question about a building's height is 何階建てですか. The accent of the single word 階 does not automatically apply to number combinations.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 20"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"エレベーターで行きます。","romaji":"Erebētā de ikimasu.","german":"I take the elevator.","furigana":"い"},{"japanese":"エレベーターで五階に行きます。","romaji":"Erebētā de gokai ni ikimasu.","german":"I take the elevator to the 5th floor.","furigana":"ごかい|い"},{"japanese":"エレベーターが動かないので、階段で行きます。","romaji":"Erebētā ga ugokanai node, kaidan de ikimasu.","german":"The elevator is not working, so I will take the stairs.","furigana":"うご|かいだん|い"}],"notes":"エレベーター is an elevator. エレベーターで行く names it as the means of reaching another floor; the destination takes に. エレベーターが動かない means \"the elevator is not working\". The two length marks in エレベーター are rendered as ē and ā.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"玄関で靴を脱ぎます。","romaji":"Genkan de kutsu o nugimasu.","german":"You take off your shoes at the entrance.","furigana":"げんかん|くつ|ぬ"},{"japanese":"玄関で靴を脱いでください。","romaji":"Genkan de kutsu o nuide kudasai.","german":"Please take off your shoes in the entryway.","furigana":"げんかん|くつ|ぬ"},{"japanese":"玄関で友達を待っています。","romaji":"Genkan de tomodachi o matte imasu.","german":"I am waiting for a friend in the entryway.","furigana":"げんかん|ともだち|ま"}],"notes":"玄関 is the entrance area of a house or apartment. In many Japanese homes, shoes are taken off there. で is used for an action performed there: 玄関で靴を脱ぐ. For something located there, に is usually used, as in 玄関に靴がある. The word can mean more than just the front door.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 30"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ベッドで寝ます。","romaji":"Beddo de nemasu.","german":"I sleep in bed.","furigana":"ね"},{"japanese":"新しいベッドを買いました。","romaji":"Atarashii beddo o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a new bed.","furigana":"あたら|か"}],"notes":"ベッド is a bed as a piece of furniture. ベッドで寝る means \"to sleep in bed\"; で marks the place of the sleeping. ベッドを買う means buying the bed itself. The small ッ produces the double d in beddo and is not pronounced as a separate syllable tsu.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 10"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"布団を敷きます。","romaji":"Futon o shikimasu.","german":"I lay out the futon.","furigana":"ふとん|し"},{"japanese":"日本では布団で寝る人もいます。","romaji":"Nihon de wa futon de neru hito mo imasu.","german":"In Japan, some people sleep on a futon.","furigana":"にほん|ふとん|ね|ひと"}],"notes":"布団 is Japanese bedding, which can include a mattress pad and a quilt. 布団を敷く means \"to lay out the futon, that is, the sleeping mat\". 布団で寝る describes sleeping with or on it. In Japan, a futon is not necessarily a Western-style folding sofa bed; the examples refer to bedding.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 22"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"棚に本を置きます。","romaji":"Tana ni hon o okimasu.","german":"I put the book on the shelf.","furigana":"たな|ほん|お"},{"japanese":"棚に本を並べました。","romaji":"Tana ni hon o narabemashita.","german":"I lined up the books on the shelf.","furigana":"たな|ほん|なら"},{"japanese":"新しい棚を作りました。","romaji":"Atarashii tana o tsukurimashita.","german":"I made a new shelf.","furigana":"あたら|たな|つく"}],"notes":"棚 is a shelf or a shelf board. 棚に本を置く means \"to put books on the shelf\"; 棚に本を並べる emphasizes lining them up neatly. For things on an upper surface, 棚の上 can be used. The word denotes the shelf, not automatically a closed cupboard.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 10"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"引き出しの中を見てください。","romaji":"Hikidashi no naka o mite kudasai.","german":"Please look inside the drawer.","furigana":"ひ|だ|なか|み"},{"japanese":"引き出しの中にペンがあります。","romaji":"Hikidashi no naka ni pen ga arimasu.","german":"There is a pen in the drawer.","furigana":"ひ|だ|なか"},{"japanese":"引き出しが開きません。","romaji":"Hikidashi ga akimasen.","german":"The drawer won't open.","furigana":"ひ|だ|あ"}],"notes":"引き出し is a drawer. 引き出しの中 means its inside; 引き出しが開かない means that it cannot be opened. The word is a complete noun. The identically written stem form of 引き出す belongs to a different grammatical use and does not provide the accent of the drawer.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 30"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ボタンを押してください。","romaji":"Botan o oshite kudasai.","german":"Please press the button.","furigana":"お"},{"japanese":"このボタンを押してください。","romaji":"Kono botan o oshite kudasai.","german":"Please press this button.","furigana":"お"},{"japanese":"重い箱を押して、部屋に入れました。","romaji":"Omoi hako o oshite, heya ni iremashita.","german":"I pushed the heavy box into the room.","furigana":"おも|はこ|お|へや|い"}],"notes":"押す means \"to press\" or \"to push\". ボタンを押す means \"to press a button\"; 箱を押す \"to push a box\". The thing moved or pressed takes を. This godan verb forms 押します and 押して. On a door, 押す can be the instruction \"push\"; the opposite is 引く \"pull\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 18"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ドアを引いてください。","romaji":"Doa o hiite kudasai.","german":"Please pull the door open.","furigana":"ひ"},{"japanese":"定規で線を引いてください。","romaji":"Jōgi de sen o hiite kudasai.","german":"Please draw a line with the ruler.","furigana":"じょうぎ|せん|ひ"}],"notes":"Here 引く means \"to pull\", and in 線を引く \"to draw a line\". With a door, you pull it toward you; with a ruler, you draw a line. The object takes を, a tool such as 定規 takes で. The godan forms are 引きます and 引いて, with a long i in hiite.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"紙を切ります。","romaji":"Kami o kirimasu.","german":"I cut paper.","furigana":"かみ|き"},{"japanese":"野菜を小さく切ります。","romaji":"Yasai o chīsaku kirimasu.","german":"I cut the vegetables small.","furigana":"やさい|ちい|き"},{"japanese":"電話を切りました。","romaji":"Denwa o kirimashita.","german":"I hung up the phone.","furigana":"でんわ|き"}],"notes":"切る means \"to cut\", for example paper or vegetables. 電話を切る also means \"to end the call, to hang up\". The object takes を. It is a godan verb: 切ります, 切って. 着る \"to put on\", which is read the same, is by contrast an ichidan verb and has different forms.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 8"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"歯を磨きます。","romaji":"Ha o migakimasu.","german":"I brush my teeth.","furigana":"は|みが"},{"japanese":"歯を磨いてから寝ます。","romaji":"Ha o migaite kara nemasu.","german":"I brush my teeth and then go to bed.","furigana":"は|みが|ね"},{"japanese":"出かける前に、靴を磨きます。","romaji":"Dekakeru mae ni, kutsu o migakimasu.","german":"Before going out, I polish my shoes.","furigana":"で|まえ|くつ|みが"}],"notes":"磨く refers to cleaning or polishing by rubbing or brushing. 歯を磨く means \"to brush one's teeth\", 靴を磨く \"to polish shoes until they shine\". The object takes を. This godan verb forms 磨きます and 磨いて; with ～てから, the second action follows only after the brushing.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 13"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"触れないでください。","romaji":"Furenaide kudasai.","german":"Please do not touch.","furigana":"ふ"},{"japanese":"この絵に触れないでください。","romaji":"Kono e ni furenaide kudasai.","german":"Please do not touch this painting.","furigana":"え|ふ"},{"japanese":"子供が初めて雪に触れました。","romaji":"Kodomo ga hajimete yuki ni furemashita.","german":"The child touched snow for the first time.","furigana":"こども|はじ|ゆき|ふ"}],"notes":"触れる means \"to touch\" here. In the usual construction the thing touched takes に: 絵に触れる, 雪に触れる. The negative is 触れない; 触れないでください asks someone not to touch something. It is an Ichidan verb with 触れます and 触れて, not a Godan verb despite the ending る.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ボールを投げます。","romaji":"Bōru o nagemasu.","german":"I throw the ball.","furigana":"な"},{"japanese":"ボールを投げてください。","romaji":"Bōru o nagete kudasai.","german":"Please throw the ball.","furigana":"な"},{"japanese":"川にごみを投げないでください。","romaji":"Kawa ni gomi o nagenaide kudasai.","german":"Please do not throw trash into the river.","furigana":"かわ|な"}],"notes":"投げる means \"to throw\" or \"to hurl\". The thing thrown takes を; a destination can follow with に, as in 川にごみを投げる. It is an ichidan verb: 投げます, 投げて. A request is 投げてください, a prohibition 投げないでください. The examples practice literal throwing.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"虫を捕まえました。","romaji":"Mushi o tsukamaemashita.","german":"I caught an insect.","furigana":"むし|つか"},{"japanese":"魚を捕まえました。","romaji":"Sakana o tsukamaemashita.","german":"I caught a fish.","furigana":"さかな|つか"},{"japanese":"駅の前でタクシーを捕まえました。","romaji":"Eki no mae de takushī o tsukamaemashita.","german":"I caught a taxi in front of the station.","furigana":"えき|まえ|つか"}],"notes":"捕まえる means \"to catch\" and can also mean \"to stop, to nab\". 虫を捕まえる means catching an insect; タクシーを捕まえる means stopping or getting a taxi. The object takes を. It is an ichidan verb: 捕まえます, 捕まえて. 捕まる, by contrast, describes someone or something being caught.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"荷物を届けます。","romaji":"Nimotsu o todokemasu.","german":"I deliver the package.","furigana":"にもつ|とど"},{"japanese":"荷物を届けに行きます。","romaji":"Nimotsu o todoke ni ikimasu.","german":"I am going to deliver the package.","furigana":"にもつ|とど|い"},{"japanese":"道で見つけた財布を交番に届けました。","romaji":"Michi de mitsuketa saifu o kōban ni todokemashita.","german":"I took the wallet I found on the street to the police box.","furigana":"みち|み|さいふ|こうばん|とど"}],"notes":"届ける means \"to deliver, to hand in\" and describes bringing something to a recipient or place. The thing delivered takes を, the recipient or destination takes に. 届けに行く uses the verb stem to name the purpose of going. 届く, by contrast, means \"to arrive\": 荷物が届く.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"友達にプレゼントをあげます。","romaji":"Tomodachi ni purezento o agemasu.","german":"I give my friend a present.","furigana":"ともだち"},{"japanese":"友達にプレゼントをあげました。","romaji":"Tomodachi ni purezento o agemashita.","german":"I gave my friend a present.","furigana":"ともだち"},{"japanese":"弟に日本語を教えてあげました。","romaji":"Otōto ni Nihongo o oshiete agemashita.","german":"I taught my younger brother Japanese.","furigana":"おとうと|にほんご|おし"}],"notes":"あげる describes giving to another person: 友達にプレゼントをあげる. The recipient takes に, the gift takes を. After a te-form, ～てあげる means doing something for someone, such as 教えてあげる. The focus is on the giving or helping side; the identically read meanings \"to raise\" and \"to deep-fry\" are not meant here.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 14"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"友達にプレゼントをもらいました。","romaji":"Tomodachi ni purezento o moraimashita.","german":"I received a present from my friend.","furigana":"ともだち"},{"japanese":"先生にほめてもらいました。","romaji":"Sensei ni homete moraimashita.","german":"I was praised by the teacher.","furigana":"せんせい"}],"notes":"もらう describes receiving: 友達にプレゼントをもらう. The giver can take に or から, the thing takes を. After a te-form, ～てもらう means that one receives an action as a help or favor: ほめてもらう. This godan verb forms もらいます and もらって; it is not an ichidan verb.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 9"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"友達が本をくれました。","romaji":"Tomodachi ga hon o kuremashita.","german":"My friend gave me a book.","furigana":"ともだち|ほん"},{"japanese":"母がお弁当を作ってくれました。","romaji":"Haha ga obentō o tsukutte kuremashita.","german":"My mother made a bento for me.","furigana":"はは|べんとう|つく"}],"notes":"くれる means that someone gives something to me or to a person close to me. The giver takes が, the gift takes を. After a te-form, ～てくれる can express an action done for me: 作ってくれる. The viewpoint lies with the receiving side. The verb is ichidan but has the special imperative くれ. 呉れる is a rare kanji spelling. For giving to me and the ～てくれる construction, the kana form くれる is usual.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 14"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"タクシーを呼びます。","romaji":"Takushī o yobimasu.","german":"I call a taxi.","furigana":"よ"},{"japanese":"タクシーを呼んでください。","romaji":"Takushī o yonde kudasai.","german":"Please call a taxi.","furigana":"よ"},{"japanese":"先生が私の名前を呼びました。","romaji":"Sensei ga watashi no namae o yobimashita.","german":"The teacher called my name.","furigana":"せんせい|わたし|なまえ|よ"}],"notes":"呼ぶ can mean \"to call over, to summon\". タクシーを呼ぶ means \"to call or order a taxi\"; 名前を呼ぶ \"to call out a name\". The thing called takes を. The godan forms are 呼びます and 呼んで, with んで in the te-form. You can call a taxi without 呼ぶ itself meaning exclusively \"to telephone\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 19"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"みんな笑っています。","romaji":"Minna waratte imasu.","german":"Everyone is laughing.","furigana":"わら"},{"japanese":"赤ちゃんが笑っています。","romaji":"Akachan ga waratte imasu.","german":"The baby is laughing.","furigana":"あか|わら"},{"japanese":"写真を撮るとき、笑ってください。","romaji":"Shashin o toru toki, waratte kudasai.","german":"Please smile when the photo is taken.","furigana":"しゃしん|と|わら"}],"notes":"笑う can mean \"to laugh\" or \"to smile\". In みんな笑っている laughing is meant; for a photo, 笑ってください can ask someone to smile. The person usually takes が. This godan verb forms 笑います and 笑って. The examples concern joy or a friendly face, not laughing at a person.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 16"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"赤ちゃんが泣いています。","romaji":"Akachan ga naite imasu.","german":"The baby is crying.","furigana":"あか|な"},{"japanese":"映画を見て泣きました。","romaji":"Eiga o mite nakimashita.","german":"I cried while watching the movie.","furigana":"えいが|み|な"}],"notes":"泣く means to cry or to shed tears. The person crying takes が. 泣いています describes crying that is going on right now; 映画を見て泣く links it with watching a movie. The godan verb ending in く forms 泣いて and 泣いた, not *泣って.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 13"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先生が怒りました。","romaji":"Sensei ga okorimashita.","german":"The teacher got angry.","furigana":"せんせい|おこ"},{"japanese":"怒らないで、話を聞いてください。","romaji":"Okoranaide, hanashi o kiite kudasai.","german":"Please listen to me without getting angry.","furigana":"おこ|はなし|き"}],"notes":"怒る here means to get angry. 怒っています can describe the state of being angry; 怒らないで asks someone not to get angry. It is an intransitive godan verb with 怒って as its て-form. The other use, to scold someone, is not automatically included in every statement about anger.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 19"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子供が喜んでいます。","romaji":"Kodomo ga yorokonde imasu.","german":"The child is happy.","furigana":"こども|よろこ"},{"japanese":"友達はプレゼントを喜んでくれました。","romaji":"Tomodachi wa purezento o yorokonde kuremashita.","german":"My friend was pleased with the present, which made me happy.","furigana":"ともだち|よろこ"}],"notes":"喜ぶ means to be glad or pleased. With が one describes the person who is pleased; with を one can name what pleases them: プレゼントを喜ぶ. 喜んでくれる makes the other person's joy appear as something pleasing to the speaker. The godan verb ending in ぶ forms 喜んで.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大きな音に驚きました。","romaji":"Ōkina oto ni odorokimashita.","german":"I was startled by the loud noise.","furigana":"おお|おと|おどろ"},{"japanese":"突然のプレゼントに驚きました。","romaji":"Totsuzen no purezento ni odorokimashita.","german":"I was surprised by the unexpected present.","furigana":"とつぜん|おどろ"}],"notes":"驚く can mean to be surprised or to be startled by something. The triggers often take に: 音に驚く, プレゼントに驚く. The context decides whether the reaction is pleasant or frightened. This intransitive godan verb forms 驚いて; actively surprising another person is 驚かす.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"レストランでコーヒーを頼みました。","romaji":"Resutoran de kōhī o tanomimashita.","german":"I ordered a coffee at the restaurant.","furigana":"たの"},{"japanese":"友達に手伝いを頼みました。","romaji":"Tomodachi ni tetsudai o tanomimashita.","german":"I asked my friend for help.","furigana":"ともだち|てつだ|たの"}],"notes":"頼む means to ask someone for something and can also mean to order. に names the person, を the thing requested: 友達に手伝いを頼む. In a restaurant, コーヒーを頼む orders a coffee. This godan verb ending in む forms 頼んで. For \"to ask a favor\", お願いする is also common.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 18"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"手伝ってください。","romaji":"Tetsudatte kudasai.","german":"Please help me.","furigana":"てつだ"},{"japanese":"弟の宿題を手伝いました。","romaji":"Otōto no shukudai o tetsudaimashita.","german":"I helped my younger brother with his homework.","furigana":"おとうと|しゅくだい|てつだ"}],"notes":"手伝う means to help out with a task. The activity being assisted can take を: 宿題を手伝う. The person to whom the task belongs can be specified with の. This transitive godan verb ending in う forms 手伝って and 手伝わない. A request for help is 手伝ってください.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 6"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"旅行のお金が集まりました。","romaji":"Ryokō no okane ga atsumarimashita.","german":"The money for the trip has been collected.","furigana":"りょこう|かね|あつ"},{"japanese":"駅の前にみんなが集まりました。","romaji":"Eki no mae ni minna ga atsumarimashita.","german":"Everyone gathered in front of the station.","furigana":"えき|まえ|あつ"}],"notes":"集まる describes people coming together or things accumulating. The thing gathered takes が, and the meeting place can take に. Someone who actively collects something uses 集める with を instead. 集まる is godan with 集まって; its る ending does not make it ichidan. The usual spelling is 集まる. The dictionary variant 集る has irregular okurigana; use 集まる in your own writing.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"駅で友達と別れました。","romaji":"Eki de tomodachi to wakaremashita.","german":"I said goodbye to my friend at the station.","furigana":"えき|ともだち|わか"},{"japanese":"恋人と別れました。","romaji":"Koibito to wakaremashita.","german":"I broke up with my partner.","furigana":"こいびと|わか"}],"notes":"別れる means to part from one another. With friends it can simply mean going separate ways after meeting; with 恋人 it can mean ending a relationship. と names the other person. 別れる is intransitive and ichidan: 別れて, 別れない. The context decides between a temporary farewell and a permanent separation.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 12"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本語ができますか。","romaji":"Nihongo ga dekimasu ka.","german":"Can you speak Japanese?","furigana":"にほんご"},{"japanese":"ここで写真を撮ることができます。","romaji":"Koko de shashin o toru koto ga dekimasu.","german":"You can take photos here.","furigana":"しゃしん|と"}],"notes":"できる means to be able to or to be possible. With a noun and が, it names an ability or a field one has mastered, as in 日本語ができる. An action expressed by a verb uses the dictionary form plus ことができる: 写真を撮ることができる. This ichidan verb forms できない; additional meanings such as \"to be finished\" are not automatically meant in every ability statement.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 14"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お金が要ります。","romaji":"Okane ga irimasu.","german":"Ich brauche Geld.","furigana":"かね|い"},{"japanese":"袋は要りません。","romaji":"Fukuro wa irimasen.","german":"Ich brauche keine Tüte.","furigana":"ふくろ|い"}],"notes":"要る heißt benötigt werden oder nötig sein. Die benötigte Sache steht im Japanischen mit が, auch wenn die deutsche Übersetzung „ich brauche“ transitiv klingt. Es ist Godan: 要ります, 要らない. Das gleich gelesene 居る für anwesende Lebewesen ist dagegen Ichidan mit います und いない.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二に三を足すと、五になります。","romaji":"Ni ni san o tasu to, go ni narimasu.","german":"Two plus three makes five.","furigana":"に|さん|た|ご"},{"japanese":"コーヒーに砂糖を足しますか。","romaji":"Kōhī ni satō o tashimasu ka.","german":"Do you add sugar to your coffee?","furigana":"さとう|た"}],"notes":"足す means to add numbers or to add one thing to another. In 二に三を足す, 三 is the added value and 二 is the starting value. In コーヒーに砂糖を足す, the coffee is the target and the sugar is the addition. This transitive godan verb ending in す forms 足して.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"電話が鳴っています。","romaji":"Denwa ga natte imasu.","german":"The phone is ringing.","furigana":"でんわ|な"},{"japanese":"朝六時に目覚まし時計が鳴りました。","romaji":"Asa rokuji ni mezamashidokei ga narimashita.","german":"The alarm clock rang at six in the morning.","furigana":"あさろくじ|めざ|どけい|な"}],"notes":"鳴る means that something produces a sound, such as a phone or an alarm clock. The thing that sounds takes が. 鳴っています describes ringing that is audible right now. This godan verb forms 鳴って; actively causing a sound is 鳴らす. The identically read なる meaning \"to become\" is a different lexeme.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"星が光っています。","romaji":"Hoshi ga hikatte imasu.","german":"The stars are shining.","furigana":"ほし|ひか"},{"japanese":"この指輪は光っています。","romaji":"Kono yubiwa wa hikatte imasu.","german":"This ring is sparkling.","furigana":"ゆびわ|ひか"}],"notes":"光る means to shine or to glitter. With 星 \"shine\" usually fits, with a piece of jewelry \"glitter\". The shining or glittering object takes が, or は as the topic. 光っています describes the visible state. The godan verb ending in る forms 光って, not *光いて.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"東京で生まれました。","romaji":"Tōkyō de umaremashita.","german":"I was born in Tokyo.","furigana":"とうきょう|う"},{"japanese":"赤ちゃんが生まれたので、家族が喜んでいます。","romaji":"Akachan ga umareta node, kazoku ga yorokonde imasu.","german":"A baby was born, so the family is delighted.","furigana":"あか|う|かぞく|よろこ"}],"notes":"生まれる means to be born, but in Japanese it is already a verb in its own right, an intransitive ichidan verb. It is not formed by adding a passive ending. で names the place of birth: 東京で生まれる. 生まれました reports a birth; 生まれたので can give it as the reason for the joy that follows.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"祖父は去年死にました。","romaji":"Sofu wa kyonen shinimashita.","german":"My grandfather died last year.","furigana":"そふ|きょねんし"},{"japanese":"この魚はもう死んでいます。","romaji":"Kono sakana wa mō shinde imasu.","german":"This fish is already dead.","furigana":"さかな|し"}],"notes":"死ぬ means to die. 死にました reports death as an event; 死んでいます means to be already dead and describes the state. The form ends in ぬ and belongs to godan: 死んで, 死なない. The word is direct; for people who have passed away, the more reserved 亡くなる is also common.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 6"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"最近太りました。","romaji":"Saikin futorimashita.","german":"I have put on weight lately.","furigana":"さいきんふと"},{"japanese":"太ったので、このズボンがきついです。","romaji":"Futotta node, kono zubon ga kitsui desu.","german":"I have gained weight, so these trousers are tight.","furigana":"ふと"}],"notes":"太る means to gain body weight or to become fatter. 太りました states the change; 太っている can describe the resulting state. It is an intransitive godan verb with 太って. A statement about weight does not automatically contain a health-related or moral judgment.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 7"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"少し痩せました。","romaji":"Sukoshi yasemashita.","german":"I have lost a little weight.","furigana":"すこ|や"},{"japanese":"兄は痩せています。","romaji":"Ani wa yasete imasu.","german":"My older brother is slim.","furigana":"あに|や"}],"notes":"痩せる means to lose weight or to become thinner. 痩せました reports a change; 痩せています can describe a slim state and does not automatically mean currently losing weight. It is ichidan: 痩せて, 痩せない. A statement about body shape alone does not specify a cause of the change.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"桜が咲いています。","romaji":"Sakura ga saite imasu.","german":"The cherry trees are blooming.","furigana":"さくら|さ"},{"japanese":"春になると、公園にたくさんの花が咲きます。","romaji":"Haru ni naru to, kōen ni takusan no hana ga sakimasu.","german":"When spring comes, many flowers bloom in the park.","furigana":"はる|こうえん|はな|さ"}],"notes":"咲く means to bloom or to open as a blossom. The flowers that bloom take が. 咲いています describes that they are in bloom right now; 春になると can introduce a recurring blooming season. This godan verb ending in く forms 咲いて, without an additional small っ.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 18"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"食品売り場はどこですか。","romaji":"Shokuhin uriba wa doko desu ka.","german":"Where is the food department?","furigana":"しょくひんう|ば"},{"japanese":"売り場で店員さんに聞きました。","romaji":"Uriba de ten'in-san ni kikimashita.","german":"I asked a clerk at the sales floor.","furigana":"う|ば|てんいん|き"}],"notes":"売り場 is the area where goods are sold, such as a sales department or a sales stand. The type of goods can precede it: 食品売り場. For an action in this area, で is used. The word denotes the place of sale, not a favorable time to sell here.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"おつりをどうぞ。","romaji":"Otsuri o dōzo.","german":"Here is your change."},{"japanese":"おつりを忘れないでください。","romaji":"Otsuri o wasurenaide kudasai.","german":"Please do not forget your change.","furigana":"わす"}],"notes":"おつり is the change after a purchase, that is, the amount returned. The spelling お釣り is also common. おつりをどうぞ accompanies the handing over; おつりを忘れないでください reminds someone to take it along. When paying, one can ask with おつりをください for the change that is still missing.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"レジで払います。","romaji":"Reji de haraimasu.","german":"I pay at the register.","furigana":"はら"},{"japanese":"レジの前に三人並んでいます。","romaji":"Reji no mae ni sannin narande imasu.","german":"Three people are lined up in front of the register.","furigana":"まえ|さんにんなら"}],"notes":"レジ is the cash register, both the machine and the checkout area in a store. It is a short form of レジスター. For paying, レジで is used; レジの前に names the area in front of it. The word alone does not say whether one pays in cash or by card.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"袋をもらえますか。","romaji":"Fukuro o moraemasu ka.","german":"May I have a bag?","furigana":"ふくろ"},{"japanese":"袋に入れてください。","romaji":"Fukuro ni irete kudasai.","german":"Please put it in a bag.","furigana":"ふくろ|い"}],"notes":"袋（ふくろ）is a bag, pouch or sack. When requesting one it takes を; as a container for packed goods it takes に. 袋をもらえますか politely asks whether one can get one. The word itself does not specify whether it is made of paper, cloth or plastic.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 29"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"家にお客さんが来ました。","romaji":"Ie ni okyakusan ga kimashita.","german":"A guest came to our house.","furigana":"いえ|きゃく|き"},{"japanese":"この店はいつもお客さんが多いです。","romaji":"Kono mise wa itsumo okyakusan ga ōi desu.","german":"This store always has many customers.","furigana":"みせ|きゃく|おお"}],"notes":"お客さん can be a visitor or guest and, in a shop, a customer. お and さん form a respectful way to refer to the other person here. The context decides between a private visit and business customers. The full reading is おきゃくさん, in which きゃ forms a single mora.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 17"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"隣人に挨拶します。","romaji":"Rinjin ni aisatsu shimasu.","german":"I greet my neighbor.","furigana":"りんじん|あいさつ"},{"japanese":"隣人から野菜をもらいました。","romaji":"Rinjin kara yasai o moraimashita.","german":"I received vegetables from my neighbor.","furigana":"りんじん|やさい"}],"notes":"隣人（りんじん）is a neighbor or a person in the neighborhood. The word sounds more formal than the common everyday 近所の人. When greeting, the neighbor takes に; when receiving something from them, から. The term alone says nothing about gender or kinship.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"車を止めてください。","romaji":"Kuruma o tomete kudasai.","german":"Please stop the car.","furigana":"くるま|と"},{"japanese":"出かける前にエアコンを止めてください。","romaji":"Dekakeru mae ni eakon o tomete kudasai.","german":"Please turn off the air conditioner before you go out.","furigana":"で|まえ|と"},{"japanese":"名札を安全ピンで服に留めました。","romaji":"Nafuda o anzenpin de fuku ni tomemashita.","german":"I fastened the name tag to my clothes with a safety pin.","furigana":"なふだ|あんぜん|ふく|と"},{"japanese":"警察はその男を署に留めて、事情を聞きました。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa sono otoko o sho ni tomete, jijō o kikimashita.","german":"The police held the man at the station and questioned him about the circumstances.","furigana":"けいさつ|おとこ|しょ|と|じじょう|き"}],"notes":"止める（とめる）is transitive: one stops something or switches it off, and the object takes を. For fastening, for example with a pin, 留める is used in particular. 留める can also mean to hold someone or keep them in custody. This ichidan verb forms とめます and とめて. 止まる, by contrast, describes stopping without an active object; the readings やめる and とどめる belong to other entries.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"手紙を渡します。","romaji":"Tegami o watashimasu.","german":"I hand over the letter.","furigana":"てがみ|わた"},{"japanese":"書類を渡してください。","romaji":"Shorui o watashite kudasai.","german":"Please hand over the documents.","furigana":"しょるい|わた"},{"japanese":"この鍵を受付の人に渡してから、帰ってください。","romaji":"Kono kagi o uketsuke no hito ni watashite kara, kaette kudasai.","german":"Please hand this key to the person at the reception desk before you leave.","furigana":"かぎ|うけつけ|ひと|わた|かえ"}],"notes":"Here 渡す means \"to hand over, to pass\". The thing takes を, the receiving person takes に. A handover can be personal, for example a letter or leaving a key at the reception desk. This godan verb forms 渡します and 渡して. 渡る, on the other hand, means to cross something oneself; it is not the form for \"to hand something to someone\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"触らないでください。","romaji":"Sawaranaide kudasai.","german":"Please do not touch.","furigana":"さわ"},{"japanese":"壁に触らないでください。","romaji":"Kabe ni sawaranaide kudasai.","german":"Please do not touch the wall.","furigana":"かべ|さわ"},{"japanese":"この布を触ってみてください。とても柔らかいです。","romaji":"Kono nuno o sawatte mite kudasai. Totemo yawarakai desu.","german":"Please feel this cloth. It is very soft.","furigana":"ぬの|さわ|やわ"}],"notes":"触る means \"to touch\". One can say 絵に触る; when touching deliberately, the thing touched can also take を, as in 布を触る. 触らないでください prohibits touching, and 触ってみてください invites someone to try. Despite the ending る, it is a godan verb: 触ります, 触って.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 21"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"財布を落としました。","romaji":"Saifu o otoshimashita.","german":"I dropped my wallet.","furigana":"さいふ|お"},{"japanese":"財布を落としてしまいました。","romaji":"Saifu o otoshite shimaimashita.","german":"I accidentally dropped my wallet.","furigana":"さいふ|お"},{"japanese":"石けんで手の汚れを落とします。","romaji":"Sekken de te no yogore o otoshimasu.","german":"I wash the dirt off my hands with soap.","furigana":"せっ|て|よご|お"}],"notes":"落とす means \"to drop\" and, for lost objects, also \"to lose\". The object affected takes を: 財布を落とす. 汚れを落とす, by contrast, means \"to remove dirt\". 落としてしまう emphasizes an undesired outcome. This transitive verb differs from 落ちる: an apple falls with が, while someone drops it with を. The dictionary variant 落す is also read おとす; use 落とす in your own writing.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 18"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"りんごが落ちました。","romaji":"Ringo ga ochimashita.","german":"The apple fell.","furigana":"お"},{"japanese":"木からりんごが落ちました。","romaji":"Ki kara ringo ga ochimashita.","german":"An apple fell from the tree.","furigana":"き|お"},{"japanese":"試験に落ちたので、もう一度勉強します。","romaji":"Shiken ni ochita node, mō ichido benkyō shimasu.","german":"I failed the exam, so I will study again.","furigana":"しけん|お|いちどべんきょう"}],"notes":"落ちる describes something falling; the thing that falls takes が, and the starting point can take から. In 試験に落ちる, \"to fail an exam\", the exam takes に. This ichidan verb forms 落ちます and 落ちて. 落とす, by contrast, describes someone dropping an object. The dictionary spelling 落る is marked as irregular okurigana; use 落ちる in your own writing.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 18"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"本を返します。","romaji":"Hon o kaeshimasu.","german":"I return the book.","furigana":"ほん|かえ"},{"japanese":"図書館に本を返さなければなりません。","romaji":"Toshokan ni hon o kaesanakereba narimasen.","german":"I have to return the book to the library.","furigana":"としょかん|ほん|かえ"},{"japanese":"お金を借りたので、来週返します。","romaji":"Okane o karita node, raishū kaeshimasu.","german":"I borrowed money, so I will pay it back next week.","furigana":"かね|か|らいしゅうかえ"}],"notes":"返す means \"to give back\" or \"to pay back\". The thing returned takes を, the person or institution takes に. With borrowed money, お金を返す is a repayment. It is a godan verb with 返します and 返して. 帰る, on the other hand, means to go back or go home oneself.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 6"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"試験を受けます。","romaji":"Shiken o ukemasu.","german":"I am taking the exam.","furigana":"しけん|う"},{"japanese":"テストを受けました。","romaji":"Tesuto o ukemashita.","german":"I took the test.","furigana":"う"},{"japanese":"先生から説明を受けました。","romaji":"Sensei kara setsumei o ukemashita.","german":"I received an explanation from the teacher.","furigana":"せんせい|せつめい|う"}],"notes":"受ける has several meanings. 試験を受ける means \"to take an exam\", not \"to pass it\". 説明を受ける means \"to receive an explanation\". The exam or the service received takes を; the person explaining can take から. This ichidan verb forms 受けます and 受けて.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 23"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"荷物を預けます。","romaji":"Nimotsu o azukemasu.","german":"I check my luggage.","furigana":"にもつ|あず"},{"japanese":"荷物を預けてもいいですか。","romaji":"Nimotsu o azukete mo ii desu ka.","german":"May I leave my luggage with you?","furigana":"にもつ|あず"},{"japanese":"毎月、銀行にお金を預けています。","romaji":"Maitsuki, ginkō ni okane o azukete imasu.","german":"I deposit money at the bank every month.","furigana":"まいつき|ぎんこう|かね|あず"}],"notes":"預ける means to hand something over for safekeeping or care. The item handed over takes を, the responsible person or institution takes に. With luggage it is temporary storage; with a bank it can mean depositing money. The Ichidan verb forms 預けます and 預けて. 預かる describes the point of view of the person who takes something into their care.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"結果を知らせます。","romaji":"Kekka o shirasemasu.","german":"I will let you know the result.","furigana":"けっか|し"},{"japanese":"結果を知らせてください。","romaji":"Kekka o shirasete kudasai.","german":"Please let me know the result.","furigana":"けっか|し"},{"japanese":"遅れるときは、電話で知らせてください。","romaji":"Okureru toki wa, denwa de shirasete kudasai.","german":"If you will be late, please let us know by phone.","furigana":"おく|でんわ|し"}],"notes":"知らせる means \"to inform, to notify\" and makes information known to someone. The information takes を, the person notified takes に. 知らせてください asks for a message; a condition like 遅れるときは explains when one should get in touch. This ichidan verb forms 知らせます and 知らせて.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"よろしく伝えてください。","romaji":"Yoroshiku tsutaete kudasai.","german":"Please give them my regards.","furigana":"つた"},{"japanese":"先生に伝えてください。","romaji":"Sensei ni tsutaete kudasai.","german":"Please tell the teacher.","furigana":"せんせい|つた"},{"japanese":"絵で自分の気持ちを伝えました。","romaji":"E de jibun no kimochi o tsutaemashita.","german":"I conveyed my feelings through a picture.","furigana":"え|じぶん|きも|つた"}],"notes":"伝える means \"to convey, to communicate\". The person receiving takes に, the content takes を. よろしく伝えてください is the fixed request \"Please give my regards to them\". Not only words but also a picture can convey feelings. This ichidan verb forms 伝えます and 伝えて.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 20"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"山が見えます。","romaji":"Yama ga miemasu.","german":"I can see the mountain.","furigana":"やま|み"},{"japanese":"ここから山が見えます。","romaji":"Koko kara yama ga miemasu.","german":"You can see the mountains from here.","furigana":"やま|み"},{"japanese":"この部屋は広く見えます。","romaji":"Kono heya wa hiroku miemasu.","german":"This room looks spacious.","furigana":"へや|ひろ|み"}],"notes":"見える describes that something is visible without one having to look at it deliberately. The thing visible takes が; ここから names the vantage point. With 広く見える it means \"to look big or spacious\", without fixing the actual size. This ichidan verb is intransitive: 見ます \"I look\" and 見えます \"it can be seen\" are different verbs.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 15"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"音楽が聞こえます。","romaji":"Ongaku ga kikoemasu.","german":"I can hear music.","furigana":"おんがく|き"},{"japanese":"すみません、よく聞こえません。もう一度お願いします。","romaji":"Sumimasen, yoku kikoemasen. Mō ichido onegai shimasu.","german":"Excuse me, I can't hear you well. One more time, please.","furigana":"き|いちど|ねが"}],"notes":"聞こえる means that a sound is audible or reaches the ears. The thing audible takes が: 音楽が聞こえる. よく聞こえません explains that one cannot hear well, for example on the phone; the request to repeat fits with this. It is an intransitive ichidan verb with 聞こえます and 聞こえて. 聞く, by contrast, also denotes deliberate listening or asking.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 20"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鍵を見つけました。","romaji":"Kagi o mitsukemashita.","german":"I found the key.","furigana":"かぎ|み"},{"japanese":"やっと鍵を見つけました。","romaji":"Yatto kagi o mitsukemashita.","german":"I finally found the key.","furigana":"かぎ|み"},{"japanese":"散歩の途中で、小さなパン屋を見つけました。","romaji":"Sanpo no tochū de, chīsana pan'ya o mitsukemashita.","german":"On my walk I discovered a small bakery.","furigana":"さんぽ|とちゅう|ちい|や|み"}],"notes":"見つける means \"to find, to discover\". The thing found takes を. It can be the result of a search, such as for a key, or an unexpected discovery along the way. やっと emphasizes that it finally succeeded. This transitive ichidan verb forms 見つけます and 見つけて; 見つかる, by contrast, describes something being found.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 21"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仕事を探しています。","romaji":"Shigoto o sagashite imasu.","german":"I am looking for a job.","furigana":"しごと|さが"},{"japanese":"鍵を探しています。","romaji":"Kagi o sagashite imasu.","german":"I am looking for my key.","furigana":"かぎ|さが"},{"japanese":"かばんの中を探しましたが、鍵はありませんでした。","romaji":"Kaban no naka o sagashimashita ga, kagi wa arimasen deshita.","german":"I searched inside the bag, but the key was not there.","furigana":"なか|さが|かぎ"}],"notes":"探す describes the search, not its success. 仕事を探す means \"to look for work\", 鍵を探す \"to look for a key\". In かばんの中を探す, を marks the area searched. This godan verb forms 探します and 探して. 見つける says that something was actually found.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 15"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"道に迷いました。","romaji":"Michi ni mayoimashita.","german":"I got lost.","furigana":"みち|まよ"},{"japanese":"道に迷ってしまいました。","romaji":"Michi ni mayotte shimaimashita.","german":"I ended up getting lost.","furigana":"みち|まよ"},{"japanese":"どちらの料理を食べるか迷っています。","romaji":"Dochira no ryōri o taberu ka mayotte imasu.","german":"I can't decide which of the two dishes to eat.","furigana":"りょうり|た|まよ"}],"notes":"迷う means \"to get lost\" or \"to be unable to decide\". 道に迷う is \"to lose one's way\". In どちらを食べるか迷う one is unsure which of two options to choose. This intransitive Godan verb forms 迷います and 迷って. A decision with か can come before it as the content of the uncertainty.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"財布を無くしました。","romaji":"Saifu o nakushimashita.","german":"I lost my wallet.","furigana":"さいふ|な"},{"japanese":"大切な物を無くしました。","romaji":"Taisetsu na mono o nakushimashita.","german":"I lost something important.","furigana":"たいせつ|もの|な"},{"japanese":"ミスを無くすために、もう一度確認します。","romaji":"Misu o nakusu tame ni, mō ichido kakunin shimasu.","german":"To eliminate mistakes, I will check once more.","furigana":"な|いちどかくにん"}],"notes":"無くす means \"to lose\" when something can no longer be found. It can also mean \"to eliminate\": ミスを無くす means to avoid mistakes or eliminate them entirely. The affected object takes を. This godan verb forms 無くします and 無くして. The kana spelling なくす is also common; 亡くす, for a death, is a different spelling.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"心配しないでください。","romaji":"Shinpai shinaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't worry.","furigana":"しんぱい"},{"japanese":"母のことが心配です。","romaji":"Haha no koto ga shinpai desu.","german":"I am worried about my mother.","furigana":"はは|しんぱい"},{"japanese":"両親は私の試験のことを心配しています。","romaji":"Ryōshin wa watashi no shiken no koto o shinpai shite imasu.","german":"My parents are worried about my exam.","furigana":"りょうしん|わたし|しけん|しんぱい"}],"notes":"心配する means \"to worry\". The cause stands before the verb, for example as 私の試験のことを; のことを highlights what one is worried about. 心配しないでください is a reassuring request not to worry. There is also the state description 心配です, as in 母のことが心配です. Without する, 心配 is also used as a noun, or with な before a noun. The forms of する change, for example 心配します, 心配した and 心配して.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 12"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日は疲れました。","romaji":"Kyō wa tsukaremashita.","german":"I'm exhausted today.","furigana":"きょう|つか"},{"japanese":"たくさん歩いて疲れました。","romaji":"Takusan aruite tsukaremashita.","german":"I'm exhausted from walking a lot.","furigana":"ある|つか"},{"japanese":"父は疲れた顔で帰ってきました。","romaji":"Chichi wa tsukareta kao de kaette kimashita.","german":"My father came home with a tired look on his face.","furigana":"ちち|つか|かお|かえ"}],"notes":"疲れた means \"exhausted, tired from exertion\", not simply \"sleepy\" like 眠い. It is the ta-form of 疲れる and can express exhaustion felt at present. Before a noun it stays unchanged: 疲れた顔. The polite form in the examples is 疲れました; neither な nor another い is attached to 疲れた.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"電車は速いです。","romaji":"Densha wa hayai desu.","german":"The train is fast.","furigana":"でんしゃ|はや"},{"japanese":"この電車は速いです。","romaji":"Kono densha wa hayai desu.","german":"This train is fast.","furigana":"でんしゃ|はや"},{"japanese":"駅まで速く走ったので、電車に間に合いました。","romaji":"Eki made hayaku hashitta node, densha ni maniaimashita.","german":"I ran to the station fast, so I made it in time for the train.","furigana":"えき|はや|はし|でんしゃ|ま|あ"}],"notes":"速い denotes high speed. A train can be 速い; 速く走る describes running fast and uses the ku-form before a verb. For \"early\" in the temporal sense, 早い is usually written, although the reading is the same. As an i-adjective, 速い forms the negative 速くない and the past 速かった.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"四角い箱です。","romaji":"Shikakui hako desu.","german":"It is a square box.","furigana":"しかく|はこ"},{"japanese":"この箱は四角いです。","romaji":"Kono hako wa shikakui desu.","german":"This box is square.","furigana":"はこ|しかく"},{"japanese":"四角い紙を半分に折って、カードを作ります。","romaji":"Shikakui kami o hanbun ni otte, kādo o tsukurimasu.","german":"I fold a square sheet of paper in half and make a card.","furigana":"しかく|かみ|はんぶん|お|つく"}],"notes":"四角い describes a four-cornered shape and can mean \"square\" or \"rectangular\". It does not say that all sides must be equally long. Before a noun it is used directly, as in 四角い紙, without な. The ku-form is 四角く. The word is more precise than a general \"angular\", which could include other shapes.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このパンは固いです。","romaji":"Kono pan wa katai desu.","german":"This bread is hard.","furigana":"かた"},{"japanese":"このパンは固いですね。","romaji":"Kono pan wa katai desu ne.","german":"This bread is hard, isn't it?","furigana":"かた"},{"japanese":"このびんのふたは固くて、開けられません。","romaji":"Kono bin no futa wa katakute, akeraremasen.","german":"The lid of this jar is so tight that I can't open it.","furigana":"かた|あ"}],"notes":"固い means hard or firm and can also mean stuck fast. 固いパン is hard bread; a stiff bottle cap can also be 固い. 硬い often emphasizes the hardness of a material, such as stone, while 堅い is used for a solid, reliable nature. The spellings overlap but have different typical combinations. 固い is correct for bread. This i-adjective forms 固く and 固くない.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"柔らかいクッションです。","romaji":"Yawarakai kusshon desu.","german":"It is a soft cushion.","furigana":"やわ"},{"japanese":"この布は柔らかいです。","romaji":"Kono nuno wa yawarakai desu.","german":"This cloth is soft.","furigana":"ぬの|やわ"},{"japanese":"朝の柔らかい光が窓から入ってきます。","romaji":"Asa no yawarakai hikari ga mado kara haitte kimasu.","german":"The soft morning light comes in through the window.","furigana":"あさ|やわ|ひかり|まど|はい"}],"notes":"柔らかい means \"soft\" or, for impressions, also \"mild, gentle\". A cushion or cloth gives way when touched; 柔らかい光 describes gentle light. Before a noun this i-adjective stands without な. The forms are 柔らかく and 柔らかくない. The reading is やわらかい; the shorter form やわらか has a different grammatical use.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この花は綺麗です。","romaji":"Kono hana wa kirei desu.","german":"This flower is beautiful.","furigana":"はな|きれい"},{"japanese":"この公園は綺麗ですね。","romaji":"Kono kōen wa kirei desu ne.","german":"This park is beautiful, isn't it?","furigana":"こうえん|きれい"},{"japanese":"掃除をして、部屋が綺麗になりました。","romaji":"Sōji o shite, heya ga kirei ni narimashita.","german":"I cleaned, and the room became tidy.","furigana":"そうじ|へや|きれい"}],"notes":"綺麗 can mean \"beautiful\" or \"clean, tidy\". With flowers, beauty is meant; after cleaning, a room can be 綺麗. Despite the reading きれい, it is not an i-adjective: before a noun it is 綺麗な, and for change 綺麗になる. The kana spelling きれい is also common. The kanji spelling 奇麗 is also read きれい and can mean \"beautiful\" or \"clean\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"立派な建物です。","romaji":"Rippa na tatemono desu.","german":"It is a splendid building.","furigana":"りっぱ|たてもの"},{"japanese":"立派な建物ですね。","romaji":"Rippa na tatemono desu ne.","german":"It's an impressive building, isn't it?","furigana":"りっぱ|たてもの"},{"japanese":"弟は毎日家の仕事を手伝っています。立派ですね。","romaji":"Otōto wa mainichi ie no shigoto o tetsudatte imasu. Rippa desu ne.","german":"My younger brother helps with the housework every day. That's admirable, isn't it?","furigana":"おとうと|まいにちいえ|しごと|てつだ|りっぱ"}],"notes":"立派 means \"impressive, splendid\" and can also acknowledge a praiseworthy achievement or attitude. A 立派な建物 looks impressive; a helpful person can be called 立派 for their behavior. It is a na-adjective with 立派な before a noun. The small っ in the reading appears as a double p in rippa.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"丈夫な靴です。","romaji":"Jōbu na kutsu desu.","german":"They are sturdy shoes.","furigana":"じょうぶ|くつ"},{"japanese":"このかばんは丈夫です。","romaji":"Kono kaban wa jōbu desu.","german":"This bag is sturdy.","furigana":"じょうぶ"},{"japanese":"祖母は体が丈夫で、毎朝散歩しています。","romaji":"Sobo wa karada ga jōbu de, maiasa sanpo shite imasu.","german":"My grandmother is physically robust and goes for a walk every morning.","furigana":"そぼ|からだ|じょうぶ|まいあささんぽ"}],"notes":"丈夫 means \"sturdy, durable\" for objects and \"healthy, resilient\" for people. 丈夫な靴 withstands wear; 体が丈夫 describes a strong constitution. This na-adjective takes な before a noun and です as a statement. The reading じょうぶ contains a long ō: jōbu.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"家族は大切です。","romaji":"Kazoku wa taisetsu desu.","german":"Family is important.","furigana":"かぞく|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"これは祖母からもらった大切な時計です。","romaji":"Kore wa sobo kara moratta taisetsu na tokei desu.","german":"This is a precious watch that I got from my grandmother.","furigana":"そぼ|たいせつ|とけい"}],"notes":"大切 means \"important\" and, for personal belongings, \"precious, dear\". 家族は大切です expresses the importance of family. A 大切な時計 can have sentimental value without being expensive. Before a noun, な is used; 大切にする means to treat something with care or to cherish it. The context shows whether significance or personal attachment is in the foreground.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 21"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大事にしてください。","romaji":"Daiji ni shite kudasai.","german":"Please take good care of it.","furigana":"だいじ"},{"japanese":"健康が一番大事です。","romaji":"Kenkō ga ichiban daiji desu.","german":"Health is the most important thing.","furigana":"けんこう|いちばんだいじ"}],"notes":"大事 denotes something important or precious. As a な-adjective it takes な before a noun: 大事なもの. 大事にする means to treat something carefully or to value it; 大事にしてください therefore asks for careful handling. 大事です, by contrast, evaluates how important something is.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この問題は簡単です。","romaji":"Kono mondai wa kantan desu.","german":"This problem is easy.","furigana":"もんだい|かんたん"},{"japanese":"この料理は簡単に作れます。","romaji":"Kono ryōri wa kantan ni tsukuremasu.","german":"This dish can be made easily.","furigana":"りょうり|かんたん|つく"}],"notes":"簡単 describes something that is easy to solve or can be done without much effort. It is a な-adjective: 簡単な問題. With に it describes the manner of an action, as in 簡単に作れる: easy to make. Here \"simple\" means low difficulty, not automatically low quality.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 10"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この地図は複雑です。","romaji":"Kono chizu wa fukuzatsu desu.","german":"This map is complicated.","furigana":"ちず|ふくざつ"},{"japanese":"複雑なことを簡単に説明してください。","romaji":"Fukuzatsu na koto o kantan ni setsumei shite kudasai.","german":"Please explain complicated things in a simple way.","furigana":"ふくざつ|かんたん|せつめい"}],"notes":"複雑 means complicated or complex, for example when many parts or relationships interlock. Before a noun it takes な: 複雑なこと. 複雑です describes the thing itself; a difficult explanation can nevertheless be put in an understandable way. The opposite for a simple structure is 簡単.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"電車がなくて不便です。","romaji":"Densha ga nakute fuben desu.","german":"It is inconvenient because there is no train.","furigana":"でんしゃ|ふべん"},{"japanese":"電話が使えないと、連絡するのに不便です。","romaji":"Denwa ga tsukaenai to, renraku suru no ni fuben desu.","german":"If you can't use the phone, it is inconvenient to get in touch.","furigana":"でんわ|つか|れんらく|ふべん"}],"notes":"不便 means impractical or inconvenient because something makes everyday life harder. It is the counterpart of 便利 and a な-adjective: 不便な場所. 電車がなくて names a reason, while ～のに不便です states for which activity something is a hindrance. For an uncomfortable chair, 座りにくい fits better depending on the context.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"親切な人ですね。","romaji":"Shinsetsu na hito desu ne.","german":"He/She is a kind person, isn't he/she?","furigana":"しんせつ|ひと"},{"japanese":"店員さんが親切に道を教えてくれました。","romaji":"Ten'in-san ga shinsetsu ni michi o oshiete kuremashita.","german":"The clerk kindly showed me the way.","furigana":"てんいん|しんせつ|みち|おし"}],"notes":"親切 describes helpful, considerate kindness. 親切な人 is a kind person; 親切に explains how someone helps or explains something. It is about how one treats others, not merely a friendly face. 親切にしてくれました emphasizes that the kindness benefited oneself.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 7"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"真面目な学生です。","romaji":"Majime na gakusei desu.","german":"He/She is a serious student.","furigana":"まじめ|がくせい"},{"japanese":"真面目に答えてください。","romaji":"Majime ni kotaete kudasai.","german":"Please answer seriously.","furigana":"まじめ|こた"}],"notes":"真面目 means serious or conscientious. 真面目な学生 describes a person's attitude; 真面目に答える asks for a serious answer. The word does not necessarily mean sad or humorless. な stands before a noun, に before an action; the predicate form is 真面目です.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"やっぱり行きます。","romaji":"Yappari ikimasu.","german":"I will go after all.","furigana":"い"},{"japanese":"やっぱり日本料理がおいしいですね。","romaji":"Yappari Nihon ryōri ga oishii desu ne.","german":"Japanese food really is delicious, isn't it?","furigana":"にほんりょうり"}],"notes":"やっぱり is the spoken form of やはり. It can confirm an expectation: \"as I thought\" or \"after all\". In a decision such as やっぱり行きます it often means \"I'll go after all\", after one had hesitated or thought differently. It therefore does not simply express an uncertain guess like たぶん. 矢っ張り is a rare kanji spelling marked as ateji; やっぱり is usual.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 17"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"たぶん大丈夫です。","romaji":"Tabun daijōbu desu.","german":"It's probably all right.","furigana":"だいじょうぶ"},{"japanese":"たぶん明日は雨でしょう。","romaji":"Tabun ashita wa ame deshō.","german":"It will probably rain tomorrow.","furigana":"あした|あめ"}],"notes":"たぶん marks a conjecture: probably or perhaps. The speaker considers something plausible but does not guarantee it. That is why it also goes with でしょう, which expresses a conjecture. Compared with きっと, たぶん usually sounds less convinced; it does not fix a specific percentage.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 21"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"きっと上手になります。","romaji":"Kitto jōzu ni narimasu.","german":"You will surely get good at it.","furigana":"じょうず"},{"japanese":"鍵はきっと家にあります。","romaji":"Kagi wa kitto ie ni arimasu.","german":"The key is surely at home.","furigana":"かぎ|いえ"}],"notes":"きっと shows strong conviction or confident expectation: \"surely\". It is usually more decisive than たぶん, but remains the speaker's assessment. When encouraging, it expresses trust; for a presumed location, a firm assumption. No factual guarantee follows from it. 屹度 is a rare kanji spelling marked as ateji. In normal text, きっと is used.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 21"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ちょうど三時です。","romaji":"Chōdo sanji desu.","german":"It is exactly three o'clock.","furigana":"さんじ"},{"japanese":"このシャツはサイズがちょうどいいです。","romaji":"Kono shatsu wa saizu ga chōdo ii desu.","german":"This shirt is just the right size."}],"notes":"ちょうど means exactly, just or fitting. With a clock time it concerns a precise point in time; in ちょうどいい, a fitting amount or size. It is an adverb and takes neither な nor its own verb ending. Homophones meaning furniture or consistency do not belong to this card. 恰度 is a rare spelling of ちょうど marked as ateji. The kana form ちょうど is the usual one.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 46"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"だいたい分かりました。","romaji":"Daitai wakarimashita.","german":"I understood it roughly.","furigana":"わ"},{"japanese":"だいたい三十分かかります。","romaji":"Daitai sanjuppun kakarimasu.","german":"It takes about thirty minutes.","furigana":"さんじゅっぷん"}],"notes":"だいたい expresses an approximate quantity or the greater part of something. だいたい三十分 is about half an hour, not an exact duration. With 分かりました, it means that one has understood the essentials; individual details may be missing. The usual kanji spelling is 大体.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 9"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ぜんぜん分かりません。","romaji":"Zenzen wakarimasen.","german":"I don't understand at all.","furigana":"わ"},{"japanese":"ぜんぜん疲れていません。","romaji":"Zenzen tsukarete imasen.","german":"I am not tired at all.","furigana":"つか"}],"notes":"ぜんぜん strengthens negative sentences to \"not at all\": ぜんぜん分かりません. The negation is contained in the verb or adjective, not in the adverb alone. Colloquially one also hears positive phrases such as 全然いいよ: \"That's perfectly fine\". For basic polite usage, the negative examples are especially useful.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"すごく楽しいです。","romaji":"Sugoku tanoshii desu.","german":"It's incredibly fun.","furigana":"たの"},{"japanese":"弟はすごく速く走れます。","romaji":"Otōto wa sugoku hayaku hashiremasu.","german":"My younger brother can run very fast.","furigana":"おとうと|はや|はし"}],"notes":"すごく intensifies an adjective or a description: very, incredibly. It is the ku-form of すごい, but is often used like a degree adverb in its own right. すごく楽しい evaluates an experience; すごく速く走る intensifies the speed of an action. In conversation it often sounds more lively than とても.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 11"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"久しぶりですね。","romaji":"Hisashiburi desu ne.","german":"Long time no see!","furigana":"ひさ"},{"japanese":"久しぶりに自転車に乗りました。","romaji":"Hisashiburi ni jitensha ni norimashita.","german":"I rode a bicycle for the first time in a long time.","furigana":"ひさ|じてんしゃ|の"}],"notes":"久しぶり is said when meeting again after a longer time. With ですね the greeting is polite, and with お久しぶりです it is somewhat more respectful. In 久しぶりに～する, on the other hand, it means doing something again after a long pause. It does not fix a specific number of days or years. 久し振り is another spelling with the same reading ひさしぶり.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 11"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"誕生日おめでとう！","romaji":"Tanjōbi omedetō!","german":"Happy birthday!","furigana":"たんじょうび"},{"japanese":"結婚した友達に「おめでとう」とメールを送りました。","romaji":"Kekkon shita tomodachi ni \"omedetō\" to mēru o okurimashita.","german":"I sent a friend who got married an email saying \"Congratulations\".","furigana":"けっこん|ともだち|おく"}],"notes":"おめでとう congratulates someone on a happy occasion, such as a birthday, a wedding or a passed exam. Among friends the short form fits; おめでとうございます is more polite. The occasion can come before it: 誕生日おめでとう. The phrase also suits a written message of congratulations. お目出度う is listed only as a dictionary search spelling. Normally write the congratulation as おめでとう.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お大事にしてください。","romaji":"Odaiji ni shite kudasai.","german":"Gute Besserung!","furigana":"だいじ"},{"japanese":"病気の友達への手紙に「お大事に」と書きました。","romaji":"Byōki no tomodachi e no tegami ni \"odaiji ni\" to kakimashita.","german":"In einen Brief an meinen kranken Freund schrieb ich: „Gute Besserung“.","furigana":"びょうき|ともだち|てがみ|だいじ|か"}],"notes":"お大事に wünscht einer kranken oder verletzten Person, auf sich achtzugeben und bald gesund zu werden. Auf Deutsch passt meist „Gute Besserung“. お大事にしてください ist eine längere höfliche Form. Man kann die Worte auch in einem Brief an einen kranken Freund verwenden; es ist keine allgemeine Begrüßung gesunder Besucher.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 12"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いらっしゃいませ！何名様ですか。","romaji":"Irasshaimase! Nanmei-sama desu ka.","german":"Welcome! How many people are in your party?","furigana":"なんめいさま"},{"japanese":"お店に入ると「いらっしゃいませ」と言われます。","romaji":"Omise ni hairu to \"irasshaimase\" to iwaremasu.","german":"When you enter a shop, you are greeted with \"Welcome\".","furigana":"みせ|はい|い"}],"notes":"いらっしゃいませ greets customers or guests in a shop or restaurant. It is said by the staff; customers do not normally have to answer the greeting with the same phrase. In a restaurant, a question about the number of people may follow. To welcome a friend to your home, いらっしゃい is more fitting.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お邪魔します。わあ、広い部屋ですね。","romaji":"Ojama shimasu. Wā, hiroi heya desu ne.","german":"Excuse me for intruding. Wow, what a spacious room.","furigana":"じゃま|ひろ|へや"},{"japanese":"友達の家に入る前に「お邪魔します」と言います。","romaji":"Tomodachi no ie ni hairu mae ni \"ojama shimasu\" to iimasu.","german":"Before entering a friend's house, you say \"Excuse me for intruding\".","furigana":"ともだち|いえ|はい|まえ|じゃま|い"}],"notes":"お邪魔します is said by a visitor when entering someone else's home. It politely acknowledges that one is making use of the other person's private space. \"Excuse me for intruding\" is a visiting greeting here; there does not have to be any actual disturbance. When leaving after the visit you hear お邪魔しました.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お帰りなさい。旅行はどうでしたか。","romaji":"Okaerinasai. Ryokō wa dō deshita ka.","german":"Welcome back. How was the trip?","furigana":"かえ|りょこう"},{"japanese":"「ただいま」と聞こえたので、「お帰りなさい」と答えました。","romaji":"\"Tadaima\" to kikoeta node, \"okaerinasai\" to kotaemashita.","german":"When I heard \"I'm home\", I answered, \"Welcome back\".","furigana":"き|かえ|こた"}],"notes":"お帰りなさい greets someone who is coming back home. The returning person often says ただいま, and the person who stayed at home answers with お帰りなさい. The greeting also fits when someone returns home after a trip. The なさい at the end is part of the fixed phrase and is not a command to come back here.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 0"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"頑張ってください。","romaji":"Ganbatte kudasai.","german":"Do your best!","furigana":"がんば"},{"japanese":"テストのために頑張ります。","romaji":"Tesuto no tame ni ganbarimasu.","german":"I will do my best for the test.","furigana":"がんば"}],"notes":"頑張る means to make an effort, to persevere or to do one's best. 頑張ってください encourages another person; 頑張ります announces one's own effort. This godan verb forms the て-form 頑張って. It promises effort, but not yet a successful outcome.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 13"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"電車に間に合いました。","romaji":"Densha ni maniaimashita.","german":"I made it to the train in time.","furigana":"でんしゃ|ま|あ"},{"japanese":"急がないと、授業に間に合いませんよ。","romaji":"Isoganai to, jugyō ni maniaimasen yo.","german":"If you don't hurry, you won't make it to class on time.","furigana":"いそ|じゅぎょう|ま|あ"}],"notes":"間に合う means to be on time or to still reach something in time. The train, class or appointment takes に: 電車に間に合う. The verb is intransitive and godan with 間に合って. 間に合いません negates being in time; here it does not mean that something is sufficient in quantity.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 22"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"明日行くつもりです。","romaji":"Ashita iku tsumori desu.","german":"I intend to go tomorrow.","furigana":"あしたい"},{"japanese":"今日はテレビを見ないつもりです。","romaji":"Kyō wa terebi o minai tsumori desu.","german":"I intend not to watch TV today.","furigana":"きょう|み"}],"notes":"つもり expresses a personal intention. The dictionary form of the planned action stands before it: 行くつもりです. A negative dictionary form like 見ないつもりです means that one intends not to do something. It is a noun, not a verb of its own, and describes the plan rather than an action that has already happened. The dictionary spelling 積もり is read つもり; for this construction of intention the kana form is usual.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"明日の予定は何ですか。","romaji":"Ashita no yotei wa nan desu ka.","german":"What are your plans for tomorrow?","furigana":"あした|よてい|なん"},{"japanese":"会議は三時に始まる予定です。","romaji":"Kaigi wa sanji ni hajimaru yotei desu.","german":"The meeting is scheduled to start at three o'clock.","furigana":"かいぎ|さんじ|はじ|よてい"}],"notes":"予定 means plans or a scheduled timetable. 明日の予定 asks about the plans for tomorrow. In ～予定です, an action whose course is planned can also stand before 予定: 始まる予定です. In contrast to the personal intention of つもり, 予定 often emphasizes arranged or planned organization.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 30"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"相談してもいいですか。","romaji":"Sōdan shite mo ii desu ka.","german":"May I ask for your advice?","furigana":"そうだん"},{"japanese":"旅行の予定について、家族と相談しました。","romaji":"Ryokō no yotei ni tsuite, kazoku to sōdan shimashita.","german":"I discussed the travel plans with my family.","furigana":"りょこう|よてい|かぞく|そうだん"}],"notes":"相談 is a consultation or discussion; with する it means to consult or ask for advice. 先生に相談する names the person one seeks advice from; 家族と相談する emphasizes discussing together. The topic can take ～について. 相談してもいいですか politely asks whether one may discuss a matter.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"連絡してください。","romaji":"Renraku shite kudasai.","german":"Please contact me.","furigana":"れんらく"},{"japanese":"学校から連絡がありました。","romaji":"Gakkō kara renraku ga arimashita.","german":"There was a message from the school.","furigana":"がっこう|れんらく"}],"notes":"連絡 means making contact or a message. 連絡する means to get in touch or to notify someone; the person contacted often takes に. 連絡がある means that a message arrives, and ～から names its source. The word does not specify whether one phones, writes or sends a message.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"友達を紹介します。","romaji":"Tomodachi o shōkai shimasu.","german":"I will introduce my friend.","furigana":"ともだち|しょうかい"},{"japanese":"自己紹介をお願いします。","romaji":"Jiko shōkai o onegai shimasu.","german":"Please introduce yourself.","furigana":"じこしょうかい|ねが"}],"notes":"紹介 is an introduction. With する it becomes \"to introduce\": the person introduced takes を, the person they are introduced to takes に. 自己紹介 is introducing oneself. One can also introduce a place or a product. The accent of 紹介 alone does not automatically apply to the whole word 自己紹介.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"返事をしてください。","romaji":"Henji o shite kudasai.","german":"Please reply.","furigana":"へんじ"},{"japanese":"メールの返事がまだ来ません。","romaji":"Mēru no henji ga mada kimasen.","german":"The reply to the email still hasn't come.","furigana":"へんじ|き"}],"notes":"返事 is a reply or response to a message, question or call. 返事をする means \"to reply\", 返事が来る \"a reply comes\". In メールの返事, の marks the message being replied to. The word can also be a short spoken reaction; it is not limited to written replies.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 29"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"理解できましたか。","romaji":"Rikai dekimashita ka.","german":"Did you understand?","furigana":"りかい"},{"japanese":"先生の説明が理解できました。","romaji":"Sensei no setsumei ga rikai dekimashita.","german":"I understood the teacher's explanation.","furigana":"せんせい|せつめい|りかい"},{"japanese":"なぜ遅れたのか、理由を理解しました。","romaji":"Naze okureta no ka, riyū o rikai shimashita.","german":"I understood the reason why I was late.","furigana":"おく|りゆう|りかい"}],"notes":"理解 means \"understanding\"; 理解する means to understand some content. 理解できる says that one can understand it or has understood it. With the potential form the content understood can take が: 説明が理解できる. A connection or a reason can also be understood; 理解する does not automatically mean agreeing with an opinion.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"説明してください。","romaji":"Setsumei shite kudasai.","german":"Please explain.","furigana":"せつめい"},{"japanese":"もう一度説明してください。","romaji":"Mō ichido setsumei shite kudasai.","german":"Please explain it once more.","furigana":"いちどせつめい"},{"japanese":"機械のそばに、使い方の説明が書いてあります。","romaji":"Kikai no soba ni, tsukaikata no setsumei ga kaite arimasu.","german":"Next to the machine, there is a written explanation of how to use it.","furigana":"きかい|つか|かた|せつめい|か"}],"notes":"説明 is an explanation or description. 説明する means \"to explain\"; the thing explained takes を, the listener takes に. A 説明 can also exist in writing, for example next to a device. もう一度説明してください asks for a repetition without saying that the first explanation was wrong.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いい経験です。","romaji":"Ii keiken desu.","german":"It is a good experience.","furigana":"けいけん"},{"japanese":"いい経験になりました。","romaji":"Ii keiken ni narimashita.","german":"It turned out to be a good experience.","furigana":"けいけん"},{"japanese":"料理の仕事の経験はありませんが、家で毎日作っています。","romaji":"Ryōri no shigoto no keiken wa arimasen ga, ie de mainichi tsukutte imasu.","german":"I have no work experience in cooking, but I cook at home every day.","furigana":"りょうり|しごと|けいけん|いえ|まいにちつく"}],"notes":"経験 is an experience one has had oneself, or accumulated practical experience. いい経験になった evaluates an experience in retrospect. 経験がある means to already have experience with something; 経験する means \"to experience oneself\". The word does not yet say whether the experience was positive or negative.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 28"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"失敗しました。","romaji":"Shippai shimashita.","german":"I failed.","furigana":"しっぱい"},{"japanese":"テストに失敗しました。","romaji":"Tesuto ni shippai shimashita.","german":"I did badly on the test.","furigana":"しっぱい"},{"japanese":"この失敗から、次はもっと早く準備しようと思いました。","romaji":"Kono shippai kara, tsugi wa motto hayaku junbi shiyō to omoimashita.","german":"From this failure, I decided to prepare earlier next time.","furigana":"しっぱい|つぎ|はや|じゅんび|おも"}],"notes":"失敗 is a failure or mistake. 失敗する means that something fails or one makes a mistake. For a test, テストに失敗した first says only that it went badly; a formal failure is more precisely 試験に落ちた. The expression 失敗から学ぶ means to learn from a mistake.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"成功しました。","romaji":"Seikō shimashita.","german":"It was a success.","furigana":"せいこう"},{"japanese":"プロジェクトが成功しました。","romaji":"Purojekuto ga seikō shimashita.","german":"The project was a success.","furigana":"せいこう"},{"japanese":"成功の理由をみんなで話しました。","romaji":"Seikō no riyū o minna de hanashimashita.","german":"We talked together about the reason for the success.","furigana":"せいこう|りゆう|はな"}],"notes":"成功 means \"success\"; 成功する means \"to succeed, to be successful\". A successful project can take が, a successful action takes に: 実験に成功する. 成功の理由 asks about the reason for the success. The word describes a successful result and is not the same as the mere attempt to do something.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"用意ができました。","romaji":"Yōi ga dekimashita.","german":"Everything is ready.","furigana":"ようい"},{"japanese":"パーティーの用意をしています。","romaji":"Pātī no yōi o shite imasu.","german":"I am preparing for the party.","furigana":"ようい"},{"japanese":"お客さんのために、お茶を用意しました。","romaji":"Okyakusan no tame ni, ocha o yōi shimashita.","german":"I prepared tea for the guests.","furigana":"きゃく|ちゃ|ようい"}],"notes":"用意 is preparation or the providing of something. 用意ができた means that everything is prepared. 用意する is also used for concrete things one provides, such as drinks or documents. The thing provided takes を; パーティーの用意 means the preparations for the party, not automatically only the food.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"用事があります。","romaji":"Yōji ga arimasu.","german":"I have something to take care of.","furigana":"ようじ"},{"japanese":"今日は用事があります。","romaji":"Kyō wa yōji ga arimasu.","german":"I have something to take care of today.","furigana":"きょう|ようじ"},{"japanese":"銀行の用事が終わったら、駅で会いましょう。","romaji":"Ginkō no yōji ga owattara, eki de aimashō.","german":"Let's meet at the station once I'm done with my errand at the bank.","furigana":"ぎんこう|ようじ|お|えき|あ"}],"notes":"用事 is something one has to take care of, or another obligation. 用事がある can explain why one has no time, without giving private details. 用事で出かける gives an errand as the reason for going out. The word is a noun; for \"to run an errand\", 用事する is not normally said.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 9"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"案内してください。","romaji":"Annai shite kudasai.","german":"Please guide me.","furigana":"あんない"},{"japanese":"駅員さんに案内してもらいました。","romaji":"Ekiin-san ni annai shite moraimashita.","german":"A station attendant guided me.","furigana":"えきいん|あんない"},{"japanese":"入口の案内を読んで、会場に入りました。","romaji":"Iriguchi no annai o yonde, kaijō ni hairimashita.","german":"I read the information at the entrance and then entered the venue.","furigana":"いりぐち|あんない|よ|かいじょう|はい"}],"notes":"案内 is guiding, directions or information. 案内する can mean to lead someone to a place or to show something. A written 案内, by contrast, informs about a place or procedure. With 駅員さんに案内してもらう one receives help from the station staff; it is not about guiding this person oneself.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"景色がきれいです。","romaji":"Keshiki ga kirei desu.","german":"The view is beautiful.","furigana":"けしき"},{"japanese":"山の景色がきれいです。","romaji":"Yama no keshiki ga kirei desu.","german":"The mountain scenery is beautiful.","furigana":"やま|けしき"},{"japanese":"景色のいい部屋を予約しました。","romaji":"Keshiki no ii heya o yoyaku shimashita.","german":"I reserved a room with a nice view.","furigana":"けしき|へや|よやく"}],"notes":"景色 is the visible landscape or view, also in a city. 景色がきれい describes what one sees. 景色のいい部屋 is a room with a nice view, literally a room whose view is good. の can mark the subject of いい here instead of が. The word denotes the scene being looked at, not the weather or a travel plan.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 27"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自然が豊かです。","romaji":"Shizen ga yutaka desu.","german":"There is abundant nature.","furigana":"しぜん|ゆた"},{"japanese":"自然の中を散歩するのが好きです。","romaji":"Shizen no naka o sanpo suru no ga suki desu.","german":"I like walking in nature.","furigana":"しぜん|なか|さんぽ|す"},{"japanese":"この写真の笑顔は自然です。","romaji":"Kono shashin no egao wa shizen desu.","german":"The smile in this photo looks natural.","furigana":"しゃしん|えがお|しぜん"}],"notes":"自然 as a noun is nature, as in 自然の中 \"in nature\". As a na-adjective it means \"natural\": 自然な笑顔 looks unforced and not artificial. 自然です evaluates this impression. The two uses differ grammatically; the meaning \"by itself\" has other constructions and is not practiced here.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 31"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"空気がきれいです。","romaji":"Kūki ga kirei desu.","german":"The air is clean.","furigana":"くうき"},{"japanese":"山の空気はおいしいです。","romaji":"Yama no kūki wa oishii desu.","german":"The air in the mountains is delicious.","furigana":"やま|くうき"},{"japanese":"先生が怒ったので、教室の空気が重くなりました。","romaji":"Sensei ga okotta node, kyōshitsu no kūki ga omoku narimashita.","german":"The teacher got angry, so the atmosphere in the classroom became heavy.","furigana":"せんせい|おこ|きょうしつ|くうき|おも"}],"notes":"空気 refers to the air we breathe. 空気がおいしい is a common positive description of fresh air, not literal eating. In conversation, 空気 can also mean the mood. 教室の空気が重い describes an oppressive atmosphere. The context shows whether air as a substance or the social atmosphere is meant.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 8"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地球は丸いです。","romaji":"Chikyū wa marui desu.","german":"The Earth is round.","furigana":"ちきゅう|まる"},{"japanese":"地球のために、ゴミを減らしましょう。","romaji":"Chikyū no tame ni, gomi o herashimashō.","german":"Let's reduce garbage for the sake of the Earth.","furigana":"ちきゅう|へ"}],"notes":"地球 is the Earth as a planet. 地球は丸い describes its shape; 地球のために gives protecting our planet as the purpose. For earth as soil or material, other words are used, such as 土. The long ū belongs to the reading ちきゅう; the small ゅ forms a single syllable kyu with き.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 21"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"太陽が出ています。","romaji":"Taiyō ga dete imasu.","german":"The sun is out.","furigana":"たいよう|で"},{"japanese":"太陽は東から昇ります。","romaji":"Taiyō wa higashi kara noborimasu.","german":"The sun rises in the east.","furigana":"たいよう|ひがし|のぼ"}],"notes":"太陽 is the sun as a celestial body. 太陽が出ている means that the sun can be seen or is shining. 太陽が昇る describes its rising; 東から names the direction it rises from. The word is a noun, and the long final syllable is rendered with ō.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 32"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"星がたくさん見えます。","romaji":"Hoshi ga takusan miemasu.","german":"I can see many stars.","furigana":"ほし|み"},{"japanese":"今夜は星がきれいですね。","romaji":"Konya wa hoshi ga kirei desu ne.","german":"The stars are beautiful tonight, aren't they?","furigana":"こんや|ほし"},{"japanese":"カードに星の形を描きました。","romaji":"Kādo ni hoshi no katachi o kakimashita.","german":"I drew a star shape on a card.","furigana":"ほし|かたち|か"}],"notes":"星 is a star in the sky and can also be a star shape used as a symbol. 星が見える says that stars are visible; 星の形を描く means to draw a star shape. In everyday use the points of light visible at night are meant, without distinguishing every astronomical type exactly. The spelling for the family name Hoshi does not provide the accent of this noun.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 32"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本は島国です。","romaji":"Nihon wa shimaguni desu.","german":"Japan is an island country.","furigana":"にほん|しまぐに"},{"japanese":"日本には島がたくさんあります。","romaji":"Nihon ni wa shima ga takusan arimasu.","german":"Japan has many islands.","furigana":"にほん|しま"},{"japanese":"この島に住む友達に、船で会いに行きます。","romaji":"Kono shima ni sumu tomodachi ni, fune de ai ni ikimasu.","german":"I will go by boat to meet a friend who lives on this island.","furigana":"しま|す|ともだち|ふね|あ|い"}],"notes":"島 is an island. One can name an island as a destination with 船で島に行く; 島に住む describes living there. 島国 is a compound word of its own, \"island country\". Its accent cannot be read off from 島 alone. Names written with 島 also have their own readings and accents.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 35"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"海岸を歩きました。","romaji":"Kaigan o arukimashita.","german":"I walked along the coast.","furigana":"かいがん|ある"},{"japanese":"海岸を散歩しました。","romaji":"Kaigan o sanpo shimashita.","german":"I took a walk along the coast.","furigana":"かいがん|さんぽ"},{"japanese":"海岸の近くに、小さなホテルがあります。","romaji":"Kaigan no chikaku ni, chīsana hoteru ga arimasu.","german":"There is a small hotel near the coast.","furigana":"かいがん|ちか|ちい"}],"notes":"海岸 is the coast or the area by the sea and can also denote a beach. Not every 海岸 consists of sand. 海岸を歩く or 散歩する describes the route of a walk; 海岸の近くに, by contrast, names a location on the coast. The word is more general than a specific bathing bay.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 38"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"森の中は涼しいです。","romaji":"Mori no naka wa suzushii desu.","german":"It is cool in the forest.","furigana":"もり|なか|すず"},{"japanese":"森の中を歩きました。","romaji":"Mori no naka o arukimashita.","german":"I walked through the forest.","furigana":"もり|なか|ある"}],"notes":"森 is a forest. 森の中は涼しい describes a property of the space in the forest; 森の中を歩く the route through it. For an action at a place, で is often suitable, for the route traversed を. 森 can also occur in names; here the ordinary word for a forest is practiced.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"湖で泳ぎました。","romaji":"Mizuumi de oyogimashita.","german":"I swam in the lake.","furigana":"みずうみ|およ"},{"japanese":"この湖はとても大きいです。","romaji":"Kono mizuumi wa totemo ōkii desu.","german":"This lake is very big.","furigana":"みずうみ|おお"}],"notes":"湖 is a lake, that is, an inland body of water. 湖で泳ぐ names the lake as the place of swimming; この湖は大きい describes its size. The word is not the sea and is read みずうみ, romanized mizuumi, not みずみ. For a location by the lake, 湖のそば can be used.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 11"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"石を投げないでください。","romaji":"Ishi o nagenaide kudasai.","german":"Please do not throw stones.","furigana":"いし|な"},{"japanese":"子供が石を投げています。","romaji":"Kodomo ga ishi o nagete imasu.","german":"The child is throwing stones.","furigana":"こども|いし|な"},{"japanese":"風で紙が飛ばないように、紙の上に石を置きました。","romaji":"Kaze de kami ga tobanai yō ni, kami no ue ni ishi o okimashita.","german":"I put a stone on the paper so that it would not be blown away by the wind.","furigana":"かぜ|かみ|と|かみ|うえ|いし|お"}],"notes":"石 is a stone or pebble. 石を投げる means throwing a stone; 石を置く, by contrast, can use it as a weight. In both cases the object takes を. A request like 投げないでください is a prohibition. The meaning practiced here is the ordinary stone, not automatically a gemstone or a game piece.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 32"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"砂で遊びます。","romaji":"Suna de asobimasu.","german":"I play with sand.","furigana":"すな|あそ"},{"japanese":"海岸の砂は白いです。","romaji":"Kaigan no suna wa shiroi desu.","german":"The sand on the beach is white.","furigana":"かいがん|すな|しろ"},{"japanese":"靴の中に砂が入ったので、出しました。","romaji":"Kutsu no naka ni suna ga haitta node, dashimashita.","german":"Sand got into my shoes, so I took it out.","furigana":"くつ|なか|すな|はい|だ"}],"notes":"砂 is sand. 砂で遊ぶ describes playing with sand as a material; 海岸の砂 means the sand on a coast. When there is sand in a shoe, one can say 靴の中に砂が入った. The word can denote an indefinite amount; it does not automatically need a number or a plural.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"虫が怖いです。","romaji":"Mushi ga kowai desu.","german":"I am afraid of insects.","furigana":"むし|こわ"},{"japanese":"うちの子供は虫が好きです。","romaji":"Uchi no kodomo wa mushi ga suki desu.","german":"Our child likes insects.","furigana":"こども|むし|す"},{"japanese":"庭で、虫が葉を食べています。","romaji":"Niwa de, mushi ga ha o tabete imasu.","german":"In the garden, insects are eating the leaves.","furigana":"にわ|むし|は|た"}],"notes":"虫 here means an insect or small crawling creature in everyday language. It is not a special word only for a beetle. 虫が怖い means that one is afraid of insects; 虫が好き describes interest or preference. In a garden, 虫 can also appear as animals that eat leaves.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"道に葉が落ちています。","romaji":"Michi ni ha ga ochite imasu.","german":"Leaves have fallen on the road.","furigana":"みち|は|お"},{"japanese":"秋になると、この木の葉は赤くなります。","romaji":"Aki ni naru to, kono ki no ha wa akaku narimasu.","german":"In autumn, the leaves of this tree turn red.","furigana":"あき|き|は|あか"},{"japanese":"葉の裏に、小さな虫がいました。","romaji":"Ha no ura ni, chīsana mushi ga imashita.","german":"There was a small insect on the underside of the leaf.","furigana":"は|うら|ちい|むし"}],"notes":"葉 is a leaf of a plant. 木の葉 are the leaves of a tree; 葉の裏 is the underside of a leaf. 葉が落ちている can describe the result of falling: the leaves are already lying on the ground. Not all plants change the color of their leaves in the same way; the autumn sentence therefore refers to a specific tree.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 35"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"枝を折らないでください。","romaji":"Eda o oranaide kudasai.","german":"Please do not break the branches.","furigana":"えだ|お"},{"japanese":"木の枝が折れました。","romaji":"Ki no eda ga oremashita.","german":"The branch of the tree broke.","furigana":"き|えだ|お"},{"japanese":"鳥が枝に止まっています。","romaji":"Tori ga eda ni tomatte imasu.","german":"A bird is perched on the branch.","furigana":"とり|えだ|と"}],"notes":"枝 is a branch or twig. 枝を折る means that someone breaks it off; 枝が折れる describes the branch breaking by itself. A branch can also be a perch for a bird: 枝に止まる. The word is more general than just a thick branch and also fits a thin twig.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 29"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"草の上に座りました。","romaji":"Kusa no ue ni suwarimashita.","german":"I sat down on the grass.","furigana":"くさ|うえ|すわ"},{"japanese":"週末に庭の草を取りました。","romaji":"Shūmatsu ni niwa no kusa o torimashita.","german":"On the weekend I weeded the garden.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|にわ|くさ|と"}],"notes":"草 means grass or weeds, depending on where the plants grow. 草の上に座る describes sitting on grass. 庭の草を取る means removing unwanted plants in the garden, that is, weeding. The internet use of 草 for laughter does not belong to this plant usage.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"氷が溶けました。","romaji":"Kōri ga tokemashita.","german":"The ice has melted.","furigana":"こおり|と"},{"japanese":"飲み物に氷を入れてください。","romaji":"Nomimono ni kōri o irete kudasai.","german":"Please put ice in the drink.","furigana":"の|もの|こおり|い"}],"notes":"氷 is frozen water. In a drink it usually means ice cubes or pieces of ice, not ice cream. 氷が溶ける describes melting; 氷を入れる describes putting it in. The reading こおり has a long o sound: kōri.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"煙が見えます。","romaji":"Kemuri ga miemasu.","german":"I can see smoke.","furigana":"けむり|み"},{"japanese":"煙が目に入って、涙が出ました。","romaji":"Kemuri ga me ni haitte, namida ga demashita.","german":"Smoke got into my eyes and tears came out.","furigana":"けむり|め|はい|なみだ|で"}],"notes":"煙 means smoke, for example from a fire or from cooking. 煙が見える describes its visibility. 煙が目に入る means that smoke gets into the eyes; 目に is the destination here. Unlike 湯気, 煙 does not simply denote water vapor.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 41"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"変な音がします。","romaji":"Hen na oto ga shimasu.","german":"There is a strange noise.","furigana":"へん|おと"},{"japanese":"雨の音で目が覚めました。","romaji":"Ame no oto de me ga samemashita.","german":"I woke up to the sound of the rain.","furigana":"あめ|おと|め|さ"}],"notes":"音 is a sound or noise. 音がする says that a sound is produced or perceptible; 音が聞こえる emphasizes hearing. 雨の音 names the source of the sound with の. A voice is normally 声, even though both are audible.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 23"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いい匂いがします。","romaji":"Ii nioi ga shimasu.","german":"It smells good.","furigana":"にお"},{"japanese":"この服にはたばこの匂いがついています。","romaji":"Kono fuku ni wa tabako no nioi ga tsuite imasu.","german":"The smell of tobacco clings to these clothes.","furigana":"ふく|にお"}],"notes":"匂い means smell or scent. いい匂いがする is \"to smell good\"; 匂いがつく describes a smell that sticks to something. For unpleasant smells the spelling 臭い is also common, but 匂い is not limited to pleasant scents. The reading におい is nioi. The kana form におい can also stand for this smell.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"味はどうですか。","romaji":"Aji wa dō desu ka.","german":"How does it taste?","furigana":"あじ"},{"japanese":"この料理は味がいいですね。","romaji":"Kono ryōri wa aji ga ii desu ne.","german":"This dish tastes good, doesn't it?","furigana":"りょうり|あじ"}],"notes":"味 denotes the taste of food or drinks. 味はどうですか asks for an assessment. 味がいい praises the taste; 味が濃い, by contrast, describes a strong taste and is not automatically praise. The figurative meaning of 味 for appeal or charm is not needed here.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 28"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"面白い形です。","romaji":"Omoshiroi katachi desu.","german":"It has an interesting shape.","furigana":"おもしろ|かたち"},{"japanese":"紙を丸い形に切りました。","romaji":"Kami o marui katachi ni kirimashita.","german":"I cut the paper into a round shape.","furigana":"かみ|まる|かたち|き"}],"notes":"形 means the visible shape or form of an object. 面白い形 evaluates an unusual shape. In 丸い形に切る, に names the shape that results from cutting. The word says nothing yet about material or size.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 27"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大きさはどのくらいですか。","romaji":"Ōkisa wa dono kurai desu ka.","german":"How big is it?","furigana":"おお"},{"japanese":"このかばんの大きさはちょうどいいです。","romaji":"Kono kaban no ōkisa wa chōdo ii desu.","german":"The size of this bag is just right.","furigana":"おお"}],"notes":"大きさ is the size of an object. The noun is formed from 大きい with さ and often follows の: かばんの大きさ. 大きさはどのくらいですか asks about the extent; ちょうどいい evaluates the size as fitting. For weight, 重さ is used.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 40"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"重さを量ります。","romaji":"Omosa o hakarimasu.","german":"I measure the weight.","furigana":"おも|はか"},{"japanese":"この荷物の重さはどのくらいですか。","romaji":"Kono nimotsu no omosa wa dono kurai desu ka.","german":"How much does this luggage weigh?","furigana":"にもつ|おも"}],"notes":"重さ means weight and is a noun formed from 重い with さ. 重さを量る means to determine the weight, usually with a scale. ～の重さ asks for the weight of the object named. 大きさ, by contrast, describes its size, not its mass.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 40"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"長さはどのくらいですか。","romaji":"Nagasa wa dono kurai desu ka.","german":"How long is it?","furigana":"なが"},{"japanese":"この部屋の長さは六メートルです。","romaji":"Kono heya no nagasa wa roku mētoru desu.","german":"The length of this room is six meters.","furigana":"へや|なが|ろく"}],"notes":"長さ is length, formed from 長い with さ. ～の長さ names the length of a particular object. An answer like 六メートル describes a linear dimension; it is not the area of the room. In a question, どのくらい leaves the unit of measurement open at first.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 40"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"部屋の広さは十畳です。","romaji":"Heya no hirosa wa jū-jō desu.","german":"The room is ten tatami mats in size.","furigana":"へや|ひろ|じゅうじょう"},{"japanese":"公園の広さにびっくりしました。","romaji":"Kōen no hirosa ni bikkuri shimashita.","german":"I was surprised by the size of the park.","furigana":"こうえん|ひろ"}],"notes":"広さ describes the area or spatial extent. For a room the measurement can be given in 畳, a unit based on tatami mats. 公園の広さに, on the other hand, names the extent that impresses someone. 長さ measures only a length; 広さ can mean all the available space.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"車の速さはどのくらいですか。","romaji":"Kuruma no hayasa wa dono kurai desu ka.","german":"How fast is the car?","furigana":"くるま|はや"},{"japanese":"新幹線の速さはすごいですね。","romaji":"Shinkansen no hayasa wa sugoi desu ne.","german":"The speed of the Shinkansen is amazing, isn't it?","furigana":"しんかんせん|はや"}],"notes":"速さ is speed or quickness and a noun from 速い. 車の速さ asks about the speed of the car. For the early time of day or an early point in time, the identically read spelling 早さ is often used. This card uses 速さ for the speed of movement.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"明日試験があります。","romaji":"Ashita shiken ga arimasu.","german":"I have an exam tomorrow.","furigana":"あしたしけん"},{"japanese":"試験に合格しました。","romaji":"Shiken ni gōkaku shimashita.","german":"I passed the exam.","furigana":"しけん|ごうかく"}],"notes":"試験 is an exam or a test. 試験がある announces that an exam is taking place. 試験に合格する means to pass the exam; 試験を受ける only means to take part and says nothing about the result. The part of speech of the card remains a noun.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"消しゴムを貸してください。","romaji":"Keshigomu o kashite kudasai.","german":"Please lend me an eraser.","furigana":"け|か"},{"japanese":"間違えたところを消しゴムで消しました。","romaji":"Machigaeta tokoro o keshigomu de keshimashita.","german":"I erased the wrong part with an eraser.","furigana":"まちが|け|け"}],"notes":"消しゴム is an eraser. 消しゴムを貸してください asks to borrow one. When erasing, で marks the tool: 消しゴムで消す. 消し is fixed in this compound noun; the verb form of the action comes from 消す.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 7"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"定規で線を引きます。","romaji":"Jōgi de sen o hikimasu.","german":"I draw a line with the ruler.","furigana":"じょうぎ|せん|ひ"},{"japanese":"この定規は三十センチです。","romaji":"Kono jōgi wa sanjū-senchi desu.","german":"This ruler is thirty centimeters long.","furigana":"じょうぎ|さんじゅう"}],"notes":"定規 is a ruler for measuring or drawing straight lines. 定規で線を引く uses it as a tool; で corresponds to \"with\" here. A value in センチ can state the length of the ruler. The reading じょうぎ has a long o: jōgi.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"歌を歌いましょう。","romaji":"Uta o utaimashō.","german":"Let's sing a song.","furigana":"うた|うた"},{"japanese":"あの歌を知っていますか。","romaji":"Ano uta o shitte imasu ka.","german":"Do you know that song?","furigana":"うた|し"}],"notes":"歌 means song and can also refer to singing as an activity. 歌を歌う is the normal combination \"to sing a song\". あの歌 names a particular song; 知っていますか asks whether it is known. A classical Japanese poem is not meant here.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 9"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"趣味は何ですか。","romaji":"Shumi wa nan desu ka.","german":"What is your hobby?","furigana":"しゅみ|なん"},{"japanese":"趣味は読書です。","romaji":"Shumi wa dokusho desu.","german":"My hobby is reading.","furigana":"しゅみ|どくしょ"}],"notes":"趣味 means hobby or leisure pastime. 趣味は何ですか asks about such an activity, 趣味は読書です names it. There can be several hobbies; the word alone does not require the singular. The meaning \"taste\" or \"preference\" in other phrases is not needed here.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 18"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"運動が好きです。","romaji":"Undō ga suki desu.","german":"I like exercise.","furigana":"うんどう|す"},{"japanese":"毎朝運動しています。","romaji":"Maiasa undō shite imasu.","german":"I exercise every morning.","furigana":"まいあさうんどう"}],"notes":"運動 here means physical movement, training or sports. 運動が好きです expresses a preference; 運動する means actually being physically active. 毎朝運動しています describes a habit. Political movements or physical motion are other uses of this word.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"美術館に行きました。","romaji":"Bijutsukan ni ikimashita.","german":"I went to the art museum.","furigana":"びじゅつかん|い"},{"japanese":"美術館は月曜日が休みです。","romaji":"Bijutsukan wa getsuyōbi ga yasumi desu.","german":"The art museum is closed on Mondays.","furigana":"びじゅつかん|げつようび|やす"}],"notes":"美術館 is an art museum or an art gallery. With に one names the destination of a visit, with で the place of an activity in the museum. Opening and closing days can be given with ～は休みです. The じゅ in びじゅつかん forms a single mora.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"動物園でパンダを見ました。","romaji":"Dōbutsuen de panda o mimashita.","german":"I saw a panda at the zoo.","furigana":"どうぶつえん|み"},{"japanese":"動物園のチケットはいくらですか。","romaji":"Dōbutsuen no chiketto wa ikura desu ka.","german":"How much is a ticket to the zoo?","furigana":"どうぶつえん"}],"notes":"動物園 is a zoo. 動物園で names the place where one sees animals, 動物園に the destination of an outing. When planning a visit, one can ask about the ticket price. The reading どうぶつえん is dōbutsuen; つ and え remain separate syllables.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 31"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"交番はどこですか。","romaji":"Kōban wa doko desu ka.","german":"Where is the police box?","furigana":"こうばん"},{"japanese":"道がわからなかったら、交番で聞いてください。","romaji":"Michi ga wakaranakattara, kōban de kiite kudasai.","german":"If you don't know the way, please ask at the police box.","furigana":"みち|こうばん|き"}],"notes":"交番 is a small police station in a neighborhood. You can ask there for directions, for example. 交番はどこですか looks for its location; 交番で聞く names it as the place to get information. The reading is kōban, with a long o.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 29"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"薬局で薬を買います。","romaji":"Yakkyoku de kusuri o kaimasu.","german":"I buy medicine at the pharmacy.","furigana":"やっきょく|くすり|か"},{"japanese":"薬局は郵便局の隣です。","romaji":"Yakkyoku wa yūbinkyoku no tonari desu.","german":"The pharmacy is next to the post office.","furigana":"やっきょく|ゆうびんきょく|となり"}],"notes":"薬局 is a pharmacy. 薬局で薬を買う describes a purchase; 薬局は～の隣です gives its location next to another building. 薬 means medicine, 薬局 is the place. In やっきょく the small っ doubles the k sound; きょ is a single mora.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"神社にお参りします。","romaji":"Jinja ni omairi shimasu.","german":"I pray at the Shinto shrine.","furigana":"じんじゃ|まい"},{"japanese":"この神社には大きな鳥居があります。","romaji":"Kono jinja ni wa ōkina torii ga arimasu.","german":"This Shinto shrine has a large torii gate.","furigana":"じんじゃ|おお|とりい"}],"notes":"神社 is a Shinto shrine. The word does not mean a Buddhist temple; that is 寺. 神社にお参りする means a religious visit or praying at the shrine. 鳥居 is the characteristic gate that can be seen at a shrine.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 16"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お寺を見学しました。","romaji":"Otera o kengaku shimashita.","german":"I toured the temple.","furigana":"てら|けんがく"},{"japanese":"この寺は駅から歩いて十分です。","romaji":"Kono tera wa eki kara aruite juppun desu.","german":"This temple is a ten-minute walk from the station.","furigana":"てら|えき|ある|じゅっぷん"}],"notes":"寺 is a Buddhist temple. With the polite お one often hears お寺. 見学する means a visit for observation; ～から歩いて十分 gives a distance as walking time. The Shinto shrine, by contrast, is 神社. The reading てら is tera, and with お accordingly otera.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"近所にスーパーがあります。","romaji":"Kinjo ni sūpā ga arimasu.","german":"There is a supermarket in the neighborhood.","furigana":"きんじょ"},{"japanese":"近所の人に挨拶をします。","romaji":"Kinjo no hito ni aisatsu o shimasu.","german":"I greet the people in the neighborhood.","furigana":"きんじょ|ひと|あいさつ"}],"notes":"近所 is the neighborhood or nearby surroundings. 近所に names a place in this area; 近所の人 means the people living there. The word is not itself \"a neighbor\" but denotes the area. With の it can stand before another noun.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 35"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"階段を上がってください。","romaji":"Kaidan o agatte kudasai.","german":"Please go up the stairs.","furigana":"かいだん|あ"},{"japanese":"階段はエレベーターの隣です。","romaji":"Kaidan wa erebētā no tonari desu.","german":"The stairs are next to the elevator.","furigana":"かいだん|となり"}],"notes":"階段 is a staircase. In 階段を上がる, を marks the route traveled and not an object being worked on. With ～の隣です one can explain the location of the stairs. For the floors of a building, however, 階 is used, as in 三階.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"屋根の上に猫がいます。","romaji":"Yane no ue ni neko ga imasu.","german":"There is a cat on the roof.","furigana":"やね|うえ|ねこ"},{"japanese":"台風で家の屋根が壊れました。","romaji":"Taifū de ie no yane ga kowaremashita.","german":"The roof of the house was damaged by the typhoon.","furigana":"たいふう|いえ|やね|こわ"}],"notes":"屋根 is the roof of a building, not the ceiling of a room. 屋根の上に names a place on the roof. 屋根が壊れる describes damage without naming a person who caused it as an object. 家の indicates the building it belongs to.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"壁に絵を掛けます。","romaji":"Kabe ni e o kakemasu.","german":"I hang a picture on the wall.","furigana":"かべ|え|か"},{"japanese":"この壁は白いです。","romaji":"Kono kabe wa shiroi desu.","german":"This wall is white.","furigana":"かべ|しろ"}],"notes":"壁 is a wall. 壁に絵を掛ける means to hang a picture on the wall; に marks the place of attachment. 壁は白いです, by contrast, describes the color. The figurative meaning \"obstacle\" is not used in these examples.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 29"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"床がきれいです。","romaji":"Yuka ga kirei desu.","german":"The floor is clean.","furigana":"ゆか"},{"japanese":"床に座ってください。","romaji":"Yuka ni suwatte kudasai.","german":"Please sit on the floor.","furigana":"ゆか|すわ"}],"notes":"床 means floor. 床がきれいです can describe it as clean or well kept; 床に座る names the floor as a seat. The reading ゆか refers here to the floor of a room. The identically written reading とこ is used for other words and phrases.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 46"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"天井が高いです。","romaji":"Tenjō ga takai desu.","german":"The ceiling is high.","furigana":"てんじょう|たか"},{"japanese":"天井を掃除しました。","romaji":"Tenjō o sōji shimashita.","german":"I cleaned the ceiling of the room.","furigana":"てんじょう|そうじ"}],"notes":"天井 is the ceiling of a room. 天井が高い describes its large distance from the floor. 天井を掃除する names it as the surface being cleaned. For a blanket on a bed a different word is used, such as 毛布; the roof of a house is 屋根.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"布団は押入れにあります。","romaji":"Futon wa oshiire ni arimasu.","german":"The futon is in the closet.","furigana":"ふとん|おしい"},{"japanese":"押入れの中を掃除しました。","romaji":"Oshiire no naka o sōji shimashita.","german":"I cleaned the inside of the closet.","furigana":"おしい|なか|そうじ"}],"notes":"押入れ is a built-in closet, often used for storing futons. The spelling 押し入れ is also common. 押入れにある describes an object lying there; 押入れの中を掃除する the cleaning of the interior. The long i sound in おしいれ arises from two consecutive い.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"畳の部屋が好きです。","romaji":"Tatami no heya ga suki desu.","german":"I like tatami rooms.","furigana":"たたみ|へや|す"},{"japanese":"畳の部屋に入る前に、靴を脱いでください。","romaji":"Tatami no heya ni hairu mae ni, kutsu o nuide kudasai.","german":"Please take off your shoes before entering the tatami room.","furigana":"たたみ|へや|はい|まえ|くつ|ぬ"}],"notes":"畳 is a tatami mat, a traditional Japanese floor covering. 畳の部屋 means a room with this covering. Before entering, one may be asked to take off one's shoes. The card reading is たたみ; as a unit of area after a number, one often hears じょう.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"枕が柔らかいです。","romaji":"Makura ga yawarakai desu.","german":"The pillow is soft.","furigana":"まくら|やわ"},{"japanese":"新しい枕を買いました。","romaji":"Atarashii makura o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a new pillow.","furigana":"あたら|まくら|か"}],"notes":"枕 is a pillow or headrest for sleeping. 枕が柔らかい judges its texture; 新しい枕を買う describes a purchase. For a general seat or sofa cushion you often hear クッション. The narrator's introduction, which can also be called 枕, is not meant here.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鏡を見ます。","romaji":"Kagami o mimasu.","german":"I look in the mirror.","furigana":"かがみ|み"},{"japanese":"壁に大きな鏡があります。","romaji":"Kabe ni ōkina kagami ga arimasu.","german":"There is a large mirror on the wall.","furigana":"かべ|おお|かがみ"}],"notes":"鏡 is a mirror. 鏡を見る means to look in the mirror; the reflection does not need to be named as an object of its own. 壁に鏡がある describes the mirror as a piece of furnishing. The figurative meaning \"model\" belongs to a different dictionary entry.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 30"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"石鹸で手を洗います。","romaji":"Sekken de te o araimasu.","german":"I wash my hands with soap.","furigana":"せっけん|て|あら"},{"japanese":"この石鹸はいい匂いがします。","romaji":"Kono sekken wa ii nioi ga shimasu.","german":"This soap smells nice.","furigana":"せっけん|にお"}],"notes":"石鹸 means soap. When washing hands, 石鹸で marks the means used and 手を the body part washed. いい匂いがする can describe the scent of a soap. The small っ in せっけん doubles k: sekken. 石鹼 is listed in the dictionary only as a search form; 石鹸 is suitable for writing.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 39"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"歯ブラシを買います。","romaji":"Haburashi o kaimasu.","german":"I buy a toothbrush.","furigana":"は|か"},{"japanese":"歯ブラシは洗面所にあります。","romaji":"Haburashi wa senmenjo ni arimasu.","german":"The toothbrush is in the bathroom.","furigana":"は|せんめんじょ"}],"notes":"歯ブラシ is a toothbrush. The word combines 歯, tooth, with the katakana word ブラシ. 歯ブラシを買う describes a purchase; 歯ブラシは～にあります gives its storage place. The reading is はぶらし, with no long vowel.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"タオルを貸してください。","romaji":"Taoru o kashite kudasai.","german":"Please lend me a towel.","furigana":"か"},{"japanese":"タオルで手を拭いてください。","romaji":"Taoru de te o fuite kudasai.","german":"Please wipe your hands with the towel.","furigana":"て|ふ"}],"notes":"タオル is a towel. タオルを貸してください asks for a borrowed towel. When drying off, タオルで names the tool and 手を the hands being dried. 拭く has the て-form 拭いて, read ふいて.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 39"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ゴミを出してください。","romaji":"Gomi o dashite kudasai.","german":"Please take out the trash.","furigana":"だ"},{"japanese":"道にゴミを捨てないでください。","romaji":"Michi ni gomi o sutenaide kudasai.","german":"Please do not throw trash on the street.","furigana":"みち|す"}],"notes":"ゴミ means trash or garbage. ゴミを出す means to take it out for collection or disposal. ゴミを捨てる means to throw away; in the negative request, 道に names the unwanted place of disposal. The spellings ごみ and ゴミ are both common. For the reading ごみ, the kanji spelling 塵 is also recorded; in everyday life the usual kana forms ごみ and ゴミ are suitable. 塵芥 is also attested as a difficult kanji spelling with the reading ごみ. The same kanji can also be read じんかい or ちりあくた; this card practices only ごみ. In ordinary notices and conversations, ごみ and ゴミ remain a good choice.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ゴミ箱に捨ててください。","romaji":"Gomibako ni sutete kudasai.","german":"Please throw it in the trash can.","furigana":"ばこ|す"},{"japanese":"ゴミ箱はあそこにあります。","romaji":"Gomibako wa asoko ni arimasu.","german":"The trash can is over there.","furigana":"ばこ"}],"notes":"ゴミ箱 is a trash can or waste container. ゴミ箱に捨てる names it as the destination for discarded trash. With ゴミ箱はどこですか you can ask for its location. In ごみばこ, 箱 is read ばこ here, unlike the standalone はこ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"漬物が好きです。","romaji":"Tsukemono ga suki desu.","german":"I like pickled vegetables.","furigana":"つけもの|す"},{"japanese":"日本の漬物を食べたことがありますか。","romaji":"Nihon no tsukemono o tabeta koto ga arimasu ka.","german":"Have you ever eaten Japanese pickled vegetables?","furigana":"にほん|つけもの|た"}],"notes":"漬物 is pickled vegetables as a food. 漬物が好きです expresses a preference; 食べたことがありますか asks about past experience. The spelling 漬け物 is also common. The word does not say which kind of vegetable is pickled.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この丼は大きいです。","romaji":"Kono donburi wa ōkii desu.","german":"This bowl is big.","furigana":"どんぶり|おお"},{"japanese":"昼ご飯に丼を食べました。","romaji":"Hirugohan ni donburi o tabemashita.","german":"I had a donburi rice dish for lunch.","furigana":"ひる|はん|どんぶり|た"}],"notes":"丼（どんぶり）can mean a deep bowl or a rice dish served in such a bowl. With 食べる, the dish is meant, not the tableware. In compound dish names you often hear どん, as in 牛丼: gyūdon. The full reading on this card remains donburi.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 34"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お弁当を作りました。","romaji":"Obentō o tsukurimashita.","german":"I made a bento.","furigana":"べんとう|つく"},{"japanese":"弁当を持って公園に行きました。","romaji":"Bentō o motte kōen ni ikimashita.","german":"I took a bento and went to the park.","furigana":"べんとう|も|こうえん|い"}],"notes":"弁当 is a prepared meal in a box, for example to take along. It means the food together with how it is put together; the empty box is called 弁当箱. With the polite お the form is お弁当. 弁当を作る is to prepare a bento, 弁当を持って行く is to take one along.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"おにぎりを二つ買いました。","romaji":"Onigiri o futatsu kaimashita.","german":"I bought two rice balls.","furigana":"ふた|か"},{"japanese":"母が作ったおにぎりを学校で食べました。","romaji":"Haha ga tsukutta onigiri o gakkō de tabemashita.","german":"At school I ate the rice balls my mother made.","furigana":"はは|つく|がっこう|た"}],"notes":"おにぎり are shaped portions of rice, often triangular and sometimes filled. \"Rice ball\" names the food, not necessarily a round ball. 二つ counts two pieces here. A description such as 母が作った names the person who prepared them.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"うどんを食べます。","romaji":"Udon o tabemasu.","german":"I eat udon noodles.","furigana":"た"},{"japanese":"冬はうどんが食べたいです。","romaji":"Fuyu wa udon ga tabetai desu.","german":"In winter I want to eat udon.","furigana":"ふゆ|た"}],"notes":"うどん are thick Japanese wheat noodles. うどんを食べる refers to actual eating; うどんが食べたい expresses the wish for it. たい attaches to the verb stem and means \"want to\", not an action that has already begun. The kana spelling is usual for this food.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"蕎麦が好きです。","romaji":"Soba ga suki desu.","german":"I like soba noodles.","furigana":"そば|す"},{"japanese":"冷たいそばを食べました。","romaji":"Tsumetai soba o tabemashita.","german":"I ate cold soba noodles.","furigana":"つめ|た"}],"notes":"そば here means soba noodles made from buckwheat. The kanji spelling 蕎麦 makes the food meaning clear. The identically pronounced そば meaning \"nearby\" is a different word. 冷たいそば means noodles served cold, not a cold place nearby.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"天ぷらを注文しました。","romaji":"Tenpura o chūmon shimashita.","german":"Ich habe Tempura bestellt.","furigana":"てん|ちゅうもん"},{"japanese":"天ぷらが大好きです。","romaji":"Tenpura ga daisuki desu.","german":"Ich liebe Tempura.","furigana":"てん|だいす"}],"notes":"天ぷら ist Tempura: Fisch oder Gemüse in einem leichten Teigmantel frittiert. 天ぷらを注文する heißt es bestellen; 天ぷらが大好きです äußert eine starke Vorliebe. Die Lesung てんぷら wird in Modified Hepburn tenpura geschrieben. Das deutsche Speisewort lautet weiterhin Tempura.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 12"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"刺身が新鮮です。","romaji":"Sashimi ga shinsen desu.","german":"The sashimi is fresh.","furigana":"さしみ|しんせん"},{"japanese":"三人で刺身を分けました。","romaji":"Sannin de sashimi o wakemashita.","german":"The three of us shared the sashimi.","furigana":"さんにん|さしみ|わ"}],"notes":"刺身 is sashimi, sliced raw fish or other raw seafood. 新鮮です judges the freshness of the food. 刺身を分ける means dividing a portion among several people. The card does not say which kind of fish is used.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 12"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お寿司を食べに行きましょう。","romaji":"Osushi o tabe ni ikimashō.","german":"Let's go eat sushi.","furigana":"すし|た|い"},{"japanese":"家で寿司を作りました。","romaji":"Ie de sushi o tsukurimashita.","german":"I made sushi at home.","furigana":"いえ|すし|つく"}],"notes":"寿司 is sushi, a dish of seasoned vinegared rice with various toppings. It does not always contain raw fish. 寿司を食べに行く means going out to eat sushi; 寿司を作る describes preparing it. With the polite お you also hear お寿司.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"焼き鳥を食べました。","romaji":"Yakitori o tabemashita.","german":"I ate yakitori.","furigana":"や|とり|た"},{"japanese":"焼き鳥を食べに行きましょう。","romaji":"Yakitori o tabe ni ikimashō.","german":"Let's go eat yakitori.","furigana":"や|とり|た|い"}],"notes":"焼き鳥 is yakitori, grilled meat on skewers, usually chicken. 焼き鳥を食べました reports a meal. 食べに行きましょう, on the other hand, invites someone to go out together for this food. The word names no particular shop and no particular seasoning.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お湯を沸かします。","romaji":"Oyu o wakashimasu.","german":"I boil water.","furigana":"ゆ|わ"},{"japanese":"カップにお湯を入れてください。","romaji":"Kappu ni oyu o irete kudasai.","german":"Please pour hot water into the cup.","furigana":"ゆ|い"}],"notes":"お湯 is hot water. お湯を沸かす is the fixed combination for heating or boiling water. カップにお湯を入れる describes pouring it in afterward; に names the vessel. The polite お belongs to the usual word, and the full reading is おゆ.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 17"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"氷水をください。","romaji":"Kōrimizu o kudasai.","german":"Ice water, please.","furigana":"こおりみず"},{"japanese":"氷水で飲み物を冷やしました。","romaji":"Kōrimizu de nomimono o hiyashimashita.","german":"I cooled the drink with ice water.","furigana":"こおりみず|の|もの|ひ"}],"notes":"氷水 is water with ice or very cold ice water. 氷水をください orders it as a drink. With 氷水で冷やす it can also be used to cool other things. The reading of this card is こおりみず, kōrimizu; other dictionary readings are not confused with it.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"乾杯しましょう。","romaji":"Kanpai shimashō.","german":"Let's raise a toast!","furigana":"かんぱい"},{"japanese":"パーティーで「乾杯」と言いました。","romaji":"Pātī de \"kanpai\" to iimashita.","german":"At the party I said \"Cheers!\"","furigana":"かんぱい|い"}],"notes":"乾杯 is the call \"Cheers!\" and can also refer to the act of clinking glasses. 乾杯しましょう invites people to toast together. 乾杯と言う quotes the call. The combination with する uses the noun; it is not an independent godan verb.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 19"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"頂きます。","romaji":"Itadakimasu.","german":"Danke für das Essen. (Vor dem Essen)","furigana":"いただ"},{"japanese":"食べる前に「頂きます」と言うのを忘れました。","romaji":"Taberu mae ni \"itadakimasu\" to iu no o wasuremashita.","german":"Ich habe vergessen, vor dem Essen „Itadakimasu“ zu sagen.","furigana":"た|まえ|いただ|い|わす"}],"notes":"頂きます sagt die essende Person vor einer Mahlzeit, meist in der Kana-Schreibung いただきます. Es drückt Dank für das empfangene Essen aus. Die deutsche Wendung „Guten Appetit“ erfüllt nicht genau dieselbe Rolle, weil sie anderen einen Wunsch ausspricht. Nach dem Essen verwendet man dagegen ごちそうさま.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 0"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ご馳走様でした。","romaji":"Gochisōsama deshita.","german":"Thank you for the meal. (after eating)","furigana":"ちそうさま"},{"japanese":"母へのメモに「ご馳走様」と書きました。","romaji":"Haha e no memo ni \"gochisōsama\" to kakimashita.","german":"In a note to my mother I wrote \"thank you for the meal\".","furigana":"はは|ちそうさま|か"}],"notes":"ご馳走様 thanks someone for the food after a meal. The kana spelling ごちそうさま is also common. With でした it becomes the polite longer form ご馳走様でした. Besides thanking someone directly, the short formula can also be written in a message to the person who cooked. ご馳走さま is another spelling of the short formula of thanks.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 0"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"セーターを着ます。","romaji":"Sētā o kimasu.","german":"I put on a sweater.","furigana":"き"},{"japanese":"このセーターは洗濯機で洗えますか。","romaji":"Kono sētā wa sentakuki de araemasu ka.","german":"Can this sweater be washed in the washing machine?","furigana":"せんたくき|あら"}],"notes":"セーター is a sweater. セーターを着る means to put it on or wear it, depending on the verb form. With 洗濯機で洗えますか you ask whether it is machine washable; で names the appliance. The long katakana vowels are shown with macrons in sētā.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 22"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コートを着てください。","romaji":"Kōto o kite kudasai.","german":"Please put on your coat.","furigana":"き"},{"japanese":"コートをハンガーに掛けました。","romaji":"Kōto o hangā ni kakemashita.","german":"I hung my coat on a hanger.","furigana":"か"}],"notes":"コート here is a coat. The identically sounding word for a sports court is a different word. コートを着る means to put on a coat; ハンガーに掛ける means to hang it on a hanger. The reading kōto contains a long o.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 22"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"手袋をしています。","romaji":"Tebukuro o shite imasu.","german":"I am wearing gloves.","furigana":"てぶくろ"},{"japanese":"冬は手袋が必要です。","romaji":"Fuyu wa tebukuro ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"You need gloves in winter.","furigana":"ふゆ|てぶくろ|ひつよう"}],"notes":"手袋 are gloves or mittens. 手袋をする is the usual combination for putting them on or wearing them. 手袋をしています describes the state of wearing them. 手袋が必要です says they are needed, without naming a particular number of pairs.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 10"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"マフラーを巻きます。","romaji":"Mafurā o makimasu.","german":"I wrap a scarf around my neck.","furigana":"ま"},{"japanese":"このマフラーは母からもらいました。","romaji":"Kono mafurā wa haha kara moraimashita.","german":"I got this scarf from my mother.","furigana":"はは"}],"notes":"マフラー here means a scarf. マフラーを巻く describes wrapping it around the neck; 母からもらう names the source of a gift. The similar-sounding word for an exhaust muffler is not meant in these contexts. In mafurā, the last a is long.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 14"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ネクタイを締めます。","romaji":"Nekutai o shimemasu.","german":"I tie my necktie.","furigana":"し"},{"japanese":"ネクタイをしなければなりません。","romaji":"Nekutai o shinakereba narimasen.","german":"I have to wear a necktie."}],"notes":"ネクタイ is a necktie. ネクタイを締める means to put it on or tie it; ネクタイをする means to wear it. しなければなりません expresses an obligation. It does not automatically say whether one is already wearing a tie.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ハンカチを持っていますか。","romaji":"Hankachi o motte imasu ka.","german":"Do you have a handkerchief?","furigana":"も"},{"japanese":"ハンカチで手をふきました。","romaji":"Hankachi de te o fukimashita.","german":"I dried my hands with a handkerchief.","furigana":"て"}],"notes":"ハンカチ is a cloth handkerchief. 持っていますか asks whether someone has one with them. With ハンカチで手をふく you can dry your hands. It is not the usual word for a paper tissue; for that you hear ティッシュ.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 41"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"指輪をもらいました。","romaji":"Yubiwa o moraimashita.","german":"I received a ring.","furigana":"ゆびわ"},{"japanese":"きれいな指輪ですね。","romaji":"Kirei na yubiwa desu ne.","german":"What a pretty ring!","furigana":"ゆびわ"}],"notes":"指輪 is a finger ring as jewelry. 指輪をもらう means to receive a ring; きれいな指輪 praises its appearance. The word alone does not say whether it is a wedding ring. Before 指輪, the na-adjective きれい takes な.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 38"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"眼鏡をかけています。","romaji":"Megane o kakete imasu.","german":"I wear glasses.","furigana":"めがね"},{"japanese":"眼鏡をかけないと見えません。","romaji":"Megane o kakenai to miemasen.","german":"I can't see without my glasses.","furigana":"めがね|み"}],"notes":"眼鏡 is a pair of glasses. 眼鏡をかける means to put them on; 眼鏡をかけています means to be wearing them. 眼鏡をかけないと見えません expresses \"I can't see without glasses\" as a condition. The figurative meaning of good judgment is not intended here.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 22"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鞄の中に何がありますか。","romaji":"Kaban no naka ni nani ga arimasu ka.","german":"What is in the bag?","furigana":"かばん|なか|なに"},{"japanese":"新しい鞄を買いたいです。","romaji":"Atarashii kaban o kaitai desu.","german":"I want to buy a new bag.","furigana":"あたら|かばん|か"}],"notes":"鞄 is a bag, for example for carrying personal items. The kana spelling かばん is also common. 鞄の中に asks about its contents; 鞄を買いたい expresses a wish to get one. The word does not refer to a trouser pocket.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"皿を洗います。","romaji":"Sara o araimasu.","german":"I wash the dishes.","furigana":"さら|あら"},{"japanese":"皿の上にケーキがあります。","romaji":"Sara no ue ni kēki ga arimasu.","german":"There is cake on the plate.","furigana":"さら|うえ"}],"notes":"皿 is a plate or a flat serving dish. 皿を洗う describes washing up, 皿の上に the place of a food on it. As a counter, 皿 can also count a portion; here the examples are ordinary objects. The card reading is さら.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 14"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"茶碗にご飯を入れます。","romaji":"Chawan ni gohan o iremasu.","german":"I put rice in the bowl.","furigana":"ちゃわん|はん|い"},{"japanese":"この茶碗は祖母からもらいました。","romaji":"Kono chawan wa sobo kara moraimashita.","german":"I got this bowl from my grandmother.","furigana":"ちゃわん|そぼ"}],"notes":"茶碗 can be a rice bowl or a teacup. In 茶碗にご飯を入れる a rice bowl is meant; に names the container. 茶碗をもらう describes receiving such a piece of tableware. The ちゃ in ちゃわん forms a single mora. 茶わん and the kana form ちゃわん also refer to the same item.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"急須でお茶を入れます。","romaji":"Kyūsu de ocha o iremasu.","german":"I brew tea in the teapot.","furigana":"きゅうす|ちゃ|い"},{"japanese":"急須のふたをなくしました。","romaji":"Kyūsu no futa o nakushimashita.","german":"I lost the lid of the teapot.","furigana":"きゅうす"}],"notes":"急須 is a small teapot for brewing and pouring tea. 急須でお茶を入れる names it as the device used for preparation. 急須のふた is its lid; の connects the part with the object. The reading きゅうす is kyūsu, with a long vowel in きゅう.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"扇風機を回します。","romaji":"Senpūki o mawashimasu.","german":"I turn on the fan.","furigana":"せんぷうき|まわ"},{"japanese":"扇風機の風が気持ちいいです。","romaji":"Senpūki no kaze ga kimochi ii desu.","german":"The breeze from the fan feels nice.","furigana":"せんぷうき|かぜ|きも"}],"notes":"扇風機 is an electric fan. 扇風機を回す means to run it; the verb describes the rotating motion. 扇風機の風 names the air flow of the device. The reading せんぷうき has a long u: senpūki.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"最初に自己紹介をします。","romaji":"Saisho ni jikoshōkai o shimasu.","german":"First, I will introduce myself.","furigana":"さいしょ|じこしょうかい"},{"japanese":"最初は日本語が分かりませんでしたが、今は少し分かります。","romaji":"Saisho wa Nihongo ga wakarimasen deshita ga, ima wa sukoshi wakarimasu.","german":"At first I didn't understand Japanese, but now I understand a little.","furigana":"さいしょ|にほんご|わ|いま|すこ|わ"}],"notes":"最初 means beginning or first. 最初に names the first step of a sequence. 最初は, by contrast, describes the state at the beginning, often in comparison with later. With 最初の, the word can also modify a noun, for example 最初の日.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"最後まで頑張ります。","romaji":"Saigo made ganbarimasu.","german":"I will do my best until the end.","furigana":"さいご|がんば"},{"japanese":"最後に会ったのはいつですか。","romaji":"Saigo ni atta no wa itsu desu ka.","german":"When was the last time we met?","furigana":"さいご|あ"}],"notes":"最後 is the end or the last time. 最後まで means until the end and marks the limit of an activity. 最後に can mean \"lastly\", for example at the last meeting. For the last piece or the last person, 最後の stands before the noun.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"最近忙しいです。","romaji":"Saikin isogashii desu.","german":"I have been busy lately.","furigana":"さいきんいそが"},{"japanese":"最近、新しい自転車を買いました。","romaji":"Saikin, atarashii jitensha o kaimashita.","german":"I recently bought a new bicycle.","furigana":"さいきん|あたら|じてんしゃ|か"}],"notes":"最近 means recently or lately. It can refer to a state that is still continuing or to a single recent event. It does not fix an exact number of days. 最近の before a noun means, for example, \"the latest\" or \"the more recent\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 21"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"将来何になりたいですか。","romaji":"Shōrai nani ni naritai desu ka.","german":"What do you want to be in the future?","furigana":"しょうらいなに"},{"japanese":"将来、日本に住みたいと思っています。","romaji":"Shōrai, Nihon ni sumitai to omotte imasu.","german":"In the future I want to live in Japan.","furigana":"しょうらい|にほん|す|おも"}],"notes":"将来 is the future, often with a view to later life plans. 将来何になりたいですか asks about a future occupation or role. 将来、日本に住みたい states a wish for later in life. It does not fix a concrete date such as tomorrow or next year.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 11"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"去年日本に行きました。","romaji":"Kyonen Nihon ni ikimashita.","german":"I went to Japan last year.","furigana":"きょねんにほん|い"},{"japanese":"去年の夏はとても暑かったです。","romaji":"Kyonen no natsu wa totemo atsukatta desu.","german":"Last summer was very hot.","furigana":"きょねん|なつ|あつ"}],"notes":"去年 is the year before the current year. It can stand as a time expression without a particle: 去年行きました. With の it refers to a part of that year, such as 去年の夏. The reading きょねん contains きょ as a single mora and no long vowel.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"来年また来ます。","romaji":"Rainen mata kimasu.","german":"I'll come again next year.","furigana":"らいねん|き"},{"japanese":"来年は大学に入ります。","romaji":"Rainen wa daigaku ni hairimasu.","german":"Next year I will start university.","furigana":"らいねん|だいがく|はい"}],"notes":"来年 is the year after the current year. As a time expression it often needs no particle, but it can become the topic with は. また来ます announces another visit; 大学に入ります the start at a university. No particular month is meant.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今度一緒に行きましょう。","romaji":"Kondo issho ni ikimashō.","german":"Let's go together next time.","furigana":"こんどいっしょ|い"},{"japanese":"今度は上手にできました。","romaji":"Kondo wa jōzu ni dekimashita.","german":"This time I did it well.","furigana":"こんど|じょうず"}],"notes":"今度 can mean the next time or this time. In an invitation it often looks ahead to an upcoming occasion. In 今度は上手にできました, by contrast, it is about this successful attempt as opposed to earlier ones. The verb tense and the context help you choose.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 9"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"丁度三時です。","romaji":"Chōdo sanji desu.","german":"It is exactly three o'clock.","furigana":"ちょうどさんじ"},{"japanese":"丁度いい大きさですね。","romaji":"Chōdo ii ōkisa desu ne.","german":"It's just the right size, isn't it?","furigana":"ちょうど|おお"}],"notes":"丁度 is the kanji spelling of the common ちょうど. With a clock time it means \"exactly\"; with いい it judges a size or amount as fitting. It modifies the following word as an adverb and takes no な. The reading remains chōdo with a long o.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 46"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"急に雨が降りました。","romaji":"Kyū ni ame ga furimashita.","german":"It suddenly rained.","furigana":"きゅう|あめ|ふ"},{"japanese":"急に雨が降り出しました。","romaji":"Kyū ni ame ga furidashimashita.","german":"It suddenly started to rain.","furigana":"きゅう|あめ|ふ|だ"},{"japanese":"電話が急に切れたので、かけ直しました。","romaji":"Denwa ga kyū ni kireta node, kakenaoshimashita.","german":"The phone call was suddenly cut off, so I called back.","furigana":"でんわ|きゅう|き|なお"}],"notes":"急に here describes a sudden, unexpected event. It stands before the process described, for example 急に雨が降る. It does not simply say that someone acts quickly on purpose; 急いで may fit for that. The whole combination きゅうに is practiced as an adverb. For a noun, 急な is used instead, for example 急な予定変更.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 40"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"偶に映画を見ます。","romaji":"Tama ni eiga o mimasu.","german":"I occasionally watch a movie.","furigana":"たま|えいが|み"},{"japanese":"偶に映画を見に行きます。","romaji":"Tama ni eiga o mi ni ikimasu.","german":"Once in a while I go to see a movie.","furigana":"たま|えいが|み|い"},{"japanese":"偶に仕事が早く終わるので、その日は家族と夕飯を食べます。","romaji":"Tama ni shigoto ga hayaku owaru node, sono hi wa kazoku to yūhan o tabemasu.","german":"Occasionally I finish work early, so on those days I eat dinner with my family.","furigana":"たま|しごと|はや|お|ひ|かぞく|ゆうはん|た"}],"notes":"偶に means \"once in a while, occasionally\" and describes a low frequency. It fits recurring activities or exceptions to the daily routine. The kana spelling たまに is very common in ordinary text. It means neither \"by chance\" in the sense of a random event nor \"every time\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"すっかり忘れました。","romaji":"Sukkari wasuremashita.","german":"I completely forgot.","furigana":"わす"},{"japanese":"すっかり忘れていました。","romaji":"Sukkari wasurete imashita.","german":"I had completely forgotten.","furigana":"わす"},{"japanese":"雪がすっかり溶けて、道を歩けるようになりました。","romaji":"Yuki ga sukkari tokete, michi o arukeru yō ni narimashita.","german":"The snow has completely melted, so I can walk on the road now.","furigana":"ゆき|と|みち|ある"}],"notes":"すっかり means \"completely, entirely\". It emphasizes, for example, total forgetting or a completed change. すっかり忘れていた describes the state of having completely forgotten something. すっかり溶けた says that none of the snow in question is left. It does not merely mean \"very\" in every adjective sentence.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この服はぴったりです。","romaji":"Kono fuku wa pittari desu.","german":"This outfit fits perfectly.","furigana":"ふく"},{"japanese":"この靴はぴったりです。","romaji":"Kono kutsu wa pittari desu.","german":"These shoes fit perfectly.","furigana":"くつ"},{"japanese":"五時ぴったりに着きました。","romaji":"Goji pittari ni tsukimashita.","german":"I arrived at exactly five o'clock.","furigana":"ごじ|つ"}],"notes":"ぴったり means \"fitting exactly\" and also \"exact\". Clothes or shoes can be ぴったり when the size is right. 五時ぴったりに着く means \"to arrive at exactly five o'clock\". The small っ is rendered as a double t in pittari. A fitting garment does not automatically have to be uncomfortably tight.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 43"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"のんびり過ごしました。","romaji":"Nonbiri sugoshimashita.","german":"I spent the time leisurely.","furigana":"す"},{"japanese":"休みの日はのんびり過ごします。","romaji":"Yasumi no hi wa nonbiri sugoshimasu.","german":"On days off I spend my time leisurely.","furigana":"やす|ひ|す"},{"japanese":"今日は仕事がないので、家でのんびりします。","romaji":"Kyō wa shigoto ga nai node, ie de nonbiri shimasu.","german":"I don't have work today, so I will take it easy at home.","furigana":"きょう|しごと|いえ"}],"notes":"のんびり describes a relaxed time without haste. Before a verb it names the manner of the activity, for example のんびり過ごす. With する, のんびりする means \"to relax, to take it easy\". It can describe a pleasant break and does not automatically mean laziness. The reading is written nonbiri, with n before b.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 22"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"はっきり言ってください。","romaji":"Hakkiri itte kudasai.","german":"Please say it clearly.","furigana":"い"},{"japanese":"今日は山がはっきり見えます。","romaji":"Kyō wa yama ga hakkiri miemasu.","german":"You can see the mountains clearly today.","furigana":"きょう|やま|み"},{"japanese":"まだ予定がはっきりしません。","romaji":"Mada yotei ga hakkiri shimasen.","german":"The plans still aren't settled.","furigana":"よてい"}],"notes":"はっきり means \"clear, distinct\". It can concern pronunciation or visibility. はっきりする also means that something is settled or becomes clear; はっきりしない means that it is still unclear. The request はっきり言ってください can ask for clear pronunciation or an unambiguous statement, depending on the conversation.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 27"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ちゃんと食べてください。","romaji":"Chanto tabete kudasai.","german":"Please eat properly.","furigana":"た"},{"japanese":"ちゃんと宿題をしなさい。","romaji":"Chanto shukudai o shinasai.","german":"Do your homework properly.","furigana":"しゅくだい"},{"japanese":"この時計はちゃんと動いています。","romaji":"Kono tokei wa chanto ugoite imasu.","german":"This clock is working properly.","furigana":"とけい|うご"}],"notes":"ちゃんと means \"properly, reliably, sufficiently\", depending on the context. With eating it asks for a proper or sufficient meal, with homework for careful work. ちゃんと動く says that something works as it should. It is colloquial; しなさい sounds like a clear instruction, for example from a parent to a child.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 45"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"なるべく早く来てください。","romaji":"Narubeku hayaku kite kudasai.","german":"Please come as early as possible.","furigana":"はや|き"},{"japanese":"健康のために、なるべく階段を使うようにしています。","romaji":"Kenkō no tame ni, narubeku kaidan o tsukau yō ni shite imasu.","german":"For my health, I take the stairs whenever possible.","furigana":"けんこう|かいだん|つか"}],"notes":"なるべく means \"as much as possible, as far as it goes\". It expresses a goal and allows for practical limits. なるべく早く asks for the earliest time possible; なるべく階段を使う means taking the stairs when it is practicable. It does not demand the same action without exception under all circumstances. 成るべく is also recorded; the kana form なるべく is usual.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"部屋を片付けてください。","romaji":"Heya o katazukete kudasai.","german":"Please tidy up the room.","furigana":"へや|かたづ"},{"japanese":"今日の仕事を片付けてから、帰ります。","romaji":"Kyō no shigoto o katazukete kara, kaerimasu.","german":"I will finish today's work first and then go home.","furigana":"きょう|しごと|かたづ|かえ"}],"notes":"片付ける means \"to tidy up, to put away\" and, with work, also \"to get done\". A room takes を as the area being tidied; 今日の仕事を片付ける means finishing off the tasks. This ichidan verb forms 片付けます and 片付けて. The reading contains づ, which in Hepburn is written zu: katazukeru.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ゴミを捨てます。","romaji":"Gomi o sutemasu.","german":"I throw away the trash.","furigana":"す"},{"japanese":"古い新聞を捨ててください。","romaji":"Furui shinbun o sutete kudasai.","german":"Please throw away the old newspapers.","furigana":"ふる|しんぶん|す"},{"japanese":"ここにゴミを捨てないでください。持って帰ってください。","romaji":"Koko ni gomi o sutenaide kudasai. Motte kaette kudasai.","german":"Please do not throw trash here. Take it home with you.","furigana":"す|も|かえ"}],"notes":"捨てる means \"to throw away, to dispose of\". The thing thrown away takes を. A place can follow with に: ここにゴミを捨てる. 捨てないでください prohibits throwing things away at this spot. This ichidan verb forms 捨てます and 捨てて; it does not simply describe dropping something by accident.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 15"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"財布を拾いました。","romaji":"Saifu o hiroimashita.","german":"I picked up a wallet.","furigana":"さいふ|ひろ"},{"japanese":"道でお金を拾いました。","romaji":"Michi de okane o hiroimashita.","german":"I picked up some money on the street.","furigana":"みち|かね|ひろ"},{"japanese":"公園のゴミを拾って、袋に入れました。","romaji":"Kōen no gomi o hirotte, fukuro ni iremashita.","german":"I picked up the trash in the park and put it in a bag.","furigana":"こうえん|ひろ|ふくろ|い"}],"notes":"拾う means \"to pick up, to gather\". It can concern an object that has been found or collecting trash while cleaning. The thing takes を, the place where it is found or the work is done takes で. This godan verb forms 拾います and 拾って. Picking up a wallet does not say that it belongs to oneself.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 22"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本の生活に慣れました。","romaji":"Nihon no seikatsu ni naremashita.","german":"I have gotten used to life in Japan.","furigana":"にほん|せいかつ|な"},{"japanese":"日本の生活に慣れましたか。","romaji":"Nihon no seikatsu ni naremashita ka.","german":"Have you gotten used to life in Japan?","furigana":"にほん|せいかつ|な"},{"japanese":"毎日練習して、車の運転に慣れました。","romaji":"Mainichi renshū shite, kuruma no unten ni naremashita.","german":"I practiced every day and got used to driving.","furigana":"まいにちれんしゅう|くるま|うんてん|な"},{"japanese":"初めは逃げていた猫が、少しずつ人に馴れてきました。","romaji":"Hajime wa nigete ita neko ga, sukoshi zutsu hito ni narete kimashita.","german":"The cat that ran away at first has gradually become tame around people.","furigana":"はじ|に|ねこ|すこ|ひと|な"},{"japanese":"この鳥はまだ人に馴れていないので、近づくと逃げます。","romaji":"Kono tori wa mada hito ni narete inai node, chikazuku to nigemasu.","german":"This bird is not yet used to people, so it flees when you get close.","furigana":"とり|ひと|な|ちか|に"}],"notes":"慣れる means to get used to something or to become familiar with it. What one gets used to stands before に. Practice can lead to familiarity with an activity. For an animal that becomes tame or trusting, the spelling 馴れる is especially encountered; here 人に names the people the animal becomes more familiar with. This intransitive ichidan verb describes the change in the animal itself and forms なれます and なれて.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 17"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お母さんに似ています。","romaji":"Okāsan ni nite imasu.","german":"You look like your mother.","furigana":"かあ|に"},{"japanese":"この二人はよく似ていますね。","romaji":"Kono futari wa yoku nite imasu ne.","german":"These two people look very much alike, don't they?","furigana":"ふたり|に"},{"japanese":"この二つの言葉は発音が似ています。","romaji":"Kono futatsu no kotoba wa hatsuon ga nite imasu.","german":"The pronunciation of these two words is similar.","furigana":"ふた|ことば|はつおん|に"}],"notes":"似る means \"to resemble\". The point of comparison takes に: お母さんに似ている. For an existing resemblance, 似ている is usually appropriate. The similarity can concern appearance, characteristics or pronunciation. This ichidan verb forms 似ます and 似て; a similarity does not mean that two things are identical.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"天気が変わりました。","romaji":"Tenki ga kawarimashita.","german":"The weather has changed.","furigana":"てんき|か"},{"japanese":"駅の名前が変わったので、古い地図では分かりません。","romaji":"Eki no namae ga kawatta node, furui chizu de wa wakarimasen.","german":"The name of the station has changed, so I can't tell on the old map.","furigana":"えき|なまえ|か|ふる|ちず|わ"}],"notes":"変わる describes something changing. The thing that changes takes が, without the sentence having to name an agent who causes it. The counterpart 変える means to change something oneself, with を. 変わる is godan: 変わります, 変わって. When a name changes, an old map may therefore no longer fit.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"何にするか決めましたか。","romaji":"Nani ni suru ka kimemashita ka.","german":"Have you decided?","furigana":"なに|き"},{"japanese":"旅行の日を決めましょう。","romaji":"Ryokō no hi o kimemashō.","german":"Let's decide on the day of the trip.","furigana":"りょこう|ひ|き"}],"notes":"決める means \"to decide, to fix\". A chosen date or content takes を; a question such as 何にするか can state what is being decided. 決めましょう invites a joint decision. This ichidan verb forms 決めます and 決めて. 決まる, by contrast, describes that something is settled.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 10"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"切手を集めています。","romaji":"Kitte o atsumete imasu.","german":"I collect stamps.","furigana":"きって|あつ"},{"japanese":"切手を集めるのが趣味です。","romaji":"Kitte o atsumeru no ga shumi desu.","german":"Collecting stamps is my hobby.","furigana":"きって|あつ|しゅみ"},{"japanese":"旅行の前に、みんなの意見を集めました。","romaji":"Ryokō no mae ni, minna no iken o atsumemashita.","german":"Before the trip, I gathered everyone's opinions.","furigana":"りょこう|まえ|いけん|あつ"}],"notes":"集める means \"to collect, to gather\". It fits a collection of objects and also information or opinions. The thing collected takes を. 集めています can describe a regular collecting activity. This ichidan verb forms 集めます and 集めて; 集まる describes coming together without the same active collector.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 16"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"窓を壊しました。","romaji":"Mado o kowashimashita.","german":"I broke the window.","furigana":"まど|こわ"},{"japanese":"おもちゃを壊さないでください。","romaji":"Omocha o kowasanaide kudasai.","german":"Please do not break the toy.","furigana":"こわ"},{"japanese":"働きすぎて、体を壊しました。","romaji":"Hatarakisugite, karada o kowashimashita.","german":"I worked too much and ruined my health.","furigana":"はたら|からだ|こわ"}],"notes":"壊す means \"to break, to destroy\". The thing affected takes を. It can also mean to damage one's health: 体を壊す means to become unwell. This godan verb forms 壊します and 壊して. 壊れる describes something breaking; 壊す names the damaging action or cause on the acting side.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 18"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"パソコンが壊れました。","romaji":"Pasokon ga kowaremashita.","german":"The computer has broken.","furigana":"こわ"},{"japanese":"このいすは古いので、すぐ壊れそうです。","romaji":"Kono isu wa furui node, sugu kowaresō desu.","german":"This chair is old, so it looks like it will break soon.","furigana":"ふる|こわ"}],"notes":"壊れる means \"to break\" or \"to stop working\". The thing affected takes が. A computer can lose its function, an old chair can fall apart. 壊れそうです describes an expected danger, not yet an actual break. This intransitive ichidan verb forms 壊れます and 壊れて.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 18"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自転車を直します。","romaji":"Jitensha o naoshimasu.","german":"I am repairing the bicycle.","furigana":"じてんしゃ|なお"},{"japanese":"時計を直してもらいました。","romaji":"Tokei o naoshite moraimashita.","german":"I had the watch repaired.","furigana":"とけい|なお"},{"japanese":"作文の間違いを直しました。","romaji":"Sakubun no machigai o naoshimashita.","german":"I corrected the mistake in the essay.","furigana":"さくぶん|まちが|なお"}],"notes":"直す means \"to repair, to correct\". The thing repaired or the mistake corrected takes を. 直してもらう says that someone else took over the repair for one. This godan verb forms 直します and 直して. The whole expression 書き直す, as a compound verb, has its own usage and accent.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 16"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自転車が直ったので、また使えます。","romaji":"Jitensha ga naotta node, mata tsukaemasu.","german":"The bicycle is fixed, so I can use it again.","furigana":"じてんしゃ|なお|つか"},{"japanese":"この文の間違いは、もう直っています。","romaji":"Kono bun no machigai wa, mō naotte imasu.","german":"The mistake in this sentence has already been corrected.","furigana":"ぶん|まちが|なお"}],"notes":"直る describes something being in order again: a repaired bicycle or a corrected mistake. The thing affected takes が. It is an intransitive godan verb: 直ります, 直って. 直す, by contrast, means repairing or correcting something oneself. For recovering from an illness, the spelling 治る is usual.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"洗濯物が乾きました。","romaji":"Sentakumono ga kawakimashita.","german":"The laundry has dried.","furigana":"せんたくもの|かわ"},{"japanese":"雨がやんで、道がすぐ乾きました。","romaji":"Ame ga yande, michi ga sugu kawakimashita.","german":"The rain stopped and the road dried quickly.","furigana":"あめ|みち|かわ"}],"notes":"乾く means \"to become dry\". The thing drying takes が: 洗濯物が乾く. It names the process or its result, without anyone drying as an agent. For deliberate drying, 乾かす is used. 乾く is godan and forms 乾きます and 乾いて; thirst is expressed with the differently written 渇く.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"雨で濡れました。","romaji":"Ame de nuremashita.","german":"I got wet in the rain.","furigana":"あめ|ぬ"},{"japanese":"雨で服が濡れました。","romaji":"Ame de fuku ga nuremashita.","german":"My clothes got wet in the rain.","furigana":"あめ|ふく|ぬ"},{"japanese":"コップの水がこぼれて、机が濡れました。","romaji":"Koppu no mizu ga koborete, tsukue ga nuremashita.","german":"Water spilled from the glass and the desk got wet.","furigana":"みず|つくえ|ぬ"}],"notes":"濡れる means \"to get wet\". The thing that got wet takes が, a cause like rain takes で. Spilled water can also cause it. This ichidan verb forms 濡れます and 濡れて. 濡れている describes the wet state, not necessarily the moment of getting wet. The kana spelling ぬれる denotes the same verb.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地震で揺れました。","romaji":"Jishin de yuremashita.","german":"It shook in the earthquake.","furigana":"じしん|ゆ"},{"japanese":"地震で建物が揺れました。","romaji":"Jishin de tatemono ga yuremashita.","german":"The building shook in the earthquake.","furigana":"じしん|たてもの|ゆ"},{"japanese":"風でカーテンがゆっくり揺れています。","romaji":"Kaze de kāten ga yukkuri yurete imasu.","german":"The curtain is swaying slowly in the wind.","furigana":"かぜ|ゆ"}],"notes":"揺れる means \"to move, to sway, to shake\". The thing moving takes が; 地震で names an earthquake as the cause, 風で the wind. The movement can be violent or go slowly back and forth. This intransitive ichidan verb forms 揺れます and 揺れて. It does not say that someone deliberately shakes the object.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"花が枯れました。","romaji":"Hana ga karemashita.","german":"The flower has withered.","furigana":"はな|か"},{"japanese":"花が枯れてしまいました。","romaji":"Hana ga karete shimaimashita.","german":"The flower has unfortunately withered.","furigana":"はな|か"},{"japanese":"夏の暑さで草が枯れ、庭が茶色になりました。","romaji":"Natsu no atsusa de kusa ga kare, niwa ga chairo ni narimashita.","german":"The grass withered from the summer heat, and the garden turned brown.","furigana":"なつ|あつ|くさ|か|にわ|ちゃいろ"}],"notes":"枯れる describes plants withering or dying. The plant takes が. 枯れてしまった can express regret that it has withered. It is an intransitive Ichidan verb: 枯れます, 枯れて. The word is not the general \"to dry\" used for laundry or a road.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"野菜を育てています。","romaji":"Yasai o sodatete imasu.","german":"I am growing vegetables.","furigana":"やさい|そだ"},{"japanese":"子供を育てるのは大変です。","romaji":"Kodomo o sodateru no wa taihen desu.","german":"Raising children is hard work.","furigana":"こども|そだ|たいへん"}],"notes":"育てる means \"to raise\" or to promote growth. It fits children and also plants, such as vegetables in the garden. The thing cared for or raised takes を. This transitive ichidan verb forms 育てます and 育てて. 育つ, by contrast, describes something growing; 育てる does not automatically mean genetic breeding.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 22"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"花を植えました。","romaji":"Hana o uemashita.","german":"I planted flowers.","furigana":"はな|う"},{"japanese":"庭に花を植えました。","romaji":"Niwa ni hana o uemashita.","german":"I planted flowers in the garden.","furigana":"にわ|はな|う"},{"japanese":"庭に木を植えて、夏に休める場所を作りたいです。","romaji":"Niwa ni ki o uete, natsu ni yasumeru basho o tsukuritai desu.","german":"I want to plant a tree in the garden and create a place where I can rest in summer.","furigana":"にわ|き|う|なつ|やす|ばしょ|つく"}],"notes":"植える means \"to plant\": one sets a plant or seed in a place. The plant takes を, the place takes に. It is a transitive ichidan verb: 植えます, 植えて. 植える denotes the planting itself; 育てる describes the subsequent care and raising.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"雪が溶けました。","romaji":"Yuki ga tokemashita.","german":"The snow has melted.","furigana":"ゆき|と"},{"japanese":"アイスクリームが溶けてしまいました。","romaji":"Aisukurīmu ga tokete shimaimashita.","german":"The ice cream has unfortunately melted.","furigana":"と"},{"japanese":"砂糖が水に溶けました。","romaji":"Satō ga mizu ni tokemashita.","german":"The sugar dissolved in the water.","furigana":"さとう|みず|と"}],"notes":"溶ける describes something melting or dissolving in a liquid. The substance affected takes が; 水に names the water in which it dissolves. It is intransitive: 溶けます, 溶けて. 雪が溶ける concerns warmth, 砂糖が水に溶ける concerns dissolving. 溶けてしまう can express regret about melted ice cream.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"水が凍りました。","romaji":"Mizu ga kōrimashita.","german":"The water has frozen.","furigana":"みず|こお"},{"japanese":"冬になると、この湖が凍ります。","romaji":"Fuyu ni naru to, kono mizuumi ga kōrimasu.","german":"This lake freezes over in winter.","furigana":"ふゆ|みずうみ|こお"}],"notes":"凍る means \"to freeze, to freeze over\". Water or a lake takes が as the thing that freezes. It is an intransitive Godan verb even though it ends in る: 凍ります, 凍って. The reading こおる has a long o: kōru. 冬になると describes, in a suitable context, a regularly occurring result, not a claim about every lake.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お湯が沸きました。","romaji":"Oyu ga wakimashita.","german":"The water has boiled.","furigana":"ゆ|わ"},{"japanese":"お湯が沸いたら、火を止めてください。","romaji":"Oyu ga waitara, hi o tomete kudasai.","german":"When the water boils, please turn off the heat.","furigana":"ゆ|わ|ひ|と"}],"notes":"沸く describes water starting to boil or boiling. The water takes が: お湯が沸く. It is intransitive; 沸かす, by contrast, means bringing water to a boil. This godan verb forms 沸きます and 沸いて. 沸いたら names here the point from which the heat should be turned off.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"肉を焼きます。","romaji":"Niku o yakimasu.","german":"I grill the meat.","furigana":"にく|や"},{"japanese":"肉を焼きましょう。","romaji":"Niku o yakimashō.","german":"Let's grill the meat.","furigana":"にく|や"},{"japanese":"オーブンでケーキを焼いて、友達の誕生日に持って行きました。","romaji":"Ōbun de kēki o yaite, tomodachi no tanjōbi ni motte ikimashita.","german":"I baked a cake in the oven and took it to a friend's birthday.","furigana":"や|ともだち|たんじょうび|も|い"}],"notes":"焼く denotes frying, grilling, baking or roasting with heat. The food prepared takes を; オーブンで names the appliance. It does not mean boiling something in water. This transitive godan verb forms 焼きます and 焼いて. Whether to translate it as \"fry\" or \"bake\" depends on the food and the preparation.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 21"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"野菜を煮ます。","romaji":"Yasai o nimasu.","german":"I simmer the vegetables.","furigana":"やさい|に"},{"japanese":"野菜を煮ています。","romaji":"Yasai o nite imasu.","german":"I am simmering the vegetables.","furigana":"やさい|に"},{"japanese":"肉を弱い火で長く煮ると、柔らかくなります。","romaji":"Niku o yowai hi de nagaku niru to, yawarakaku narimasu.","german":"If you simmer meat for a long time over low heat, it becomes tender.","furigana":"にく|よわ|ひ|なが|に|やわ"}],"notes":"煮る means to cook, simmer or braise food in liquid. The food takes を. The Ichidan verb forms 煮ます and 煮て. 野菜を煮ている can describe cooking that is going on right now. 弱い火で is about low heat; 煮る does not always require vigorously boiling water. It is a different word from 似る \"to resemble\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"よく混ぜてください。","romaji":"Yoku mazete kudasai.","german":"Please mix it well.","furigana":"ま"},{"japanese":"サラダを混ぜてください。","romaji":"Sarada o mazete kudasai.","german":"Please toss the salad.","furigana":"ま"},{"japanese":"赤い絵の具と白い絵の具を混ぜると、ピンクになります。","romaji":"Akai enogu to shiroi enogu o mazeru to, pinku ni narimasu.","german":"If you mix red and white paint, you get pink.","furigana":"あか|え|ぐ|しろ|え|ぐ|ま"}],"notes":"混ぜる means \"to mix, to blend, to stir\". The things mixed take を; と can connect two ingredients. For a liquid \"stir\" fits, for salad or paint \"mix\" often fits. The Ichidan verb forms 混ぜます and 混ぜて. よく混ぜてください asks for thorough mixing without naming a particular ingredient itself.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"プレゼントを包みます。","romaji":"Purezento o tsutsumimasu.","german":"I wrap the present.","furigana":"つつ"},{"japanese":"プレゼントを包みました。","romaji":"Purezento o tsutsumimashita.","german":"I wrapped the present.","furigana":"つつ"},{"japanese":"割れないように、コップを新聞紙で包みました。","romaji":"Warenai yō ni, koppu o shinbunshi de tsutsumimashita.","german":"I wrapped the glass in newspaper so that it would not break.","furigana":"わ|しんぶんし|つつ"}],"notes":"包む means \"to wrap, to pack\". The thing wrapped takes を; the material can follow with で. A present is wrapped for handing over, a glass to protect it from breaking. This godan verb forms 包みます and 包んで. 包む does not automatically mean using a solid box; paper or cloth is enough in a suitable context.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 21"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一から十まで数えます。","romaji":"Ichi kara jū made kazoemasu.","german":"I count from one to ten.","furigana":"いち|じゅう|かぞ"},{"japanese":"お金を数えてください。","romaji":"Okane o kazoete kudasai.","german":"Please count the money.","furigana":"かね|かぞ"}],"notes":"数える means \"to count\". One can recite a sequence of numbers or count objects or money. The thing counted takes を; から and まで give the start and end of a sequence. It is an Ichidan verb: 数えます, 数えて. The verb stays the same even though different counters are needed when stating the result.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二つを比べます。","romaji":"Futatsu o kurabemasu.","german":"I compare the two.","furigana":"ふた|くら"},{"japanese":"二つの値段を比べました。","romaji":"Futatsu no nedan o kurabemashita.","german":"I compared the two prices.","furigana":"ふた|ねだん|くら"},{"japanese":"去年と比べると、今年は暖かいです。","romaji":"Kyonen to kuraberu to, kotoshi wa atatakai desu.","german":"Compared with last year, this year is warm.","furigana":"きょねん|くら|ことし|あたた"}],"notes":"比べる means \"to compare\". AとBを比べる names both objects of comparison; Aと比べると sets A as the point of comparison for the following statement. One can compare prices or compare today's state with earlier. This transitive ichidan verb forms 比べます and 比べて. Comparing alone does not yet say that one has decided.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お辞儀をします。","romaji":"Ojigi o shimasu.","german":"I bow.","furigana":"じぎ"},{"japanese":"授業の初めに立って、先生にお辞儀をします。","romaji":"Jugyō no hajime ni tatte, sensei ni ojigi o shimasu.","german":"At the start of class you stand up and bow to the teacher.","furigana":"じゅぎょう|はじ|た|せんせい|じぎ"},{"japanese":"店員さんは「ありがとうございました」と言って、お辞儀をしました。","romaji":"Ten'in-san wa \"arigatō gozaimashita\" to itte, ojigi o shimashita.","german":"The shop assistant said \"Thank you very much\" and bowed.","furigana":"てんいん|い|じぎ"}],"notes":"お辞儀 means \"bow\". お辞儀をする or お辞儀する means \"to bow\"; に names the person one bows to. It occurs in greetings and when thanking. In the school routine 起立、礼、着席 one first stands up, bows and then sits down. The spellings お辞儀 and 御辞儀 refer to the same word here.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"挨拶をしましょう。","romaji":"Aisatsu o shimashō.","german":"Let's greet each other.","furigana":"あいさつ"},{"japanese":"朝、隣の人に挨拶しました。","romaji":"Asa, tonari no hito ni aisatsu shimashita.","german":"In the morning I greeted my neighbor.","furigana":"あさ|となり|ひと|あいさつ"},{"japanese":"式の初めに、校長先生が短い挨拶をしました。","romaji":"Shiki no hajime ni, kōchō-sensei ga mijikai aisatsu o shimashita.","german":"At the start of the ceremony, the principal gave a short address.","furigana":"しき|はじ|こうちょうせんせい|みじか|あいさつ"}],"notes":"挨拶 means \"greeting\" and can also refer to a short speech of welcome at an event. With する it becomes \"to greet\" or \"to give a short address\". The person greeted takes に. 朝の挨拶 concerns the everyday greeting; 校長先生の挨拶 a speech by the school principal. The kana reading is あいさつ, without a small っ.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 33"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"気持ちがいいです。","romaji":"Kimochi ga ii desu.","german":"It feels good.","furigana":"きも"},{"japanese":"相手の気持ちを大切にしよう。","romaji":"Aite no kimochi o taisetsu ni shiyō.","german":"Let's respect other people's feelings.","furigana":"あいて|きも|たいせつ"}],"notes":"気持ち denotes a feeling, a sensation or a mood. 気持ちがいい means \"it feels pleasant\", for example with pleasant air or movement. 相手の気持ち, by contrast, concerns the inner experience of the other person. 気持ちを大切にする means to take their feelings seriously. The noun here is not the similarly written adjective 気持ちいい.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 33"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"心配しないでください。","romaji":"Shinpai shinaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't worry.","furigana":"しんぱい"},{"japanese":"試験のことが心配です。","romaji":"Shiken no koto ga shinpai desu.","german":"I am worried about the exam.","furigana":"しけん|しんぱい"}],"notes":"心配 denotes worry or concern. 心配する means \"to worry\"; 心配しないでください tries to reassure someone. In 試験のことが心配です, the exam stands with が as the object of the worry. With the verb, 試験のことを心配する is also possible. 心配な顔 is a worried face; the form before a noun needs な here.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 22"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"安心しました。","romaji":"Anshin shimashita.","german":"I am relieved.","furigana":"あんしん"},{"japanese":"テストが終わって安心しました。","romaji":"Tesuto ga owatte anshin shimashita.","german":"I was relieved that the test was over.","furigana":"お|あんしん"},{"japanese":"この店なら、安心して買い物ができます。","romaji":"Kono mise nara, anshin shite kaimono ga dekimasu.","german":"At this store I can shop with peace of mind.","furigana":"みせ|あんしん|か|もの"}],"notes":"安心 denotes reassurance or a feeling of security. 安心する means \"to be reassured, to be relieved\". After a stressful task, 安心した can express relief. 安心して買い物ができる, by contrast, describes being able to shop with confidence. 安心して names the inner attitude; it is not an objective guarantee that every risk is excluded.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"我慢してください。","romaji":"Gaman shite kudasai.","german":"Please be patient.","furigana":"がまん"},{"japanese":"もう少し我慢してください。","romaji":"Mō sukoshi gaman shite kudasai.","german":"Please be patient a little longer.","furigana":"すこ|がまん"},{"japanese":"欲しい物がありましたが、お金を使わずに我慢しました。","romaji":"Hoshii mono ga arimashita ga, okane o tsukawazu ni gaman shimashita.","german":"There was something I wanted, but I held back and did not spend the money.","furigana":"ほ|もの|かね|つか|がまん"}],"notes":"我慢 means \"to endure\" or \"to hold oneself back\", with する as the verb. A request like 我慢してください asks someone to put up with an unpleasant situation a little longer. It can also mean doing without something one wants. 我慢 is therefore not just waiting for the next person; the context shows which burden or which desire is meant.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"無理しないでください。","romaji":"Muri shinaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't push yourself too hard.","furigana":"むり"},{"japanese":"それは無理です。","romaji":"Sore wa muri desu.","german":"That is impossible.","furigana":"むり"}],"notes":"無理です can mean \"that's impossible, that won't work\", for example when time or means are lacking. 無理する, by contrast, means taking on too much or overexerting oneself. 無理しないでください is a request to limit one's own strain. Before a noun, な is often used: 無理な計画, an unworkable plan.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"特別な日です。","romaji":"Tokubetsu na hi desu.","german":"It is a special day.","furigana":"とくべつ|ひ"},{"japanese":"今日は特別な日です。","romaji":"Kyō wa tokubetsu na hi desu.","german":"Today is a special day.","furigana":"きょう|とくべつ|ひ"},{"japanese":"今日は特別に、授業を早く終わります。","romaji":"Kyō wa tokubetsu ni, jugyō o hayaku owarimasu.","german":"Today, as a special exception, we will finish class early.","furigana":"きょう|とくべつ|じゅぎょう|はや|お"}],"notes":"特別 means \"special\". Before a noun, 特別な is usual: 特別な日, a special day. 特別に can say that something is done differently as an exception. 特別に授業を早く終わる describes a deviation from the normal routine. \"Special\" here does not automatically mean \"very\" before any adjective.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"普通の生活がしたいです。","romaji":"Futsū no seikatsu ga shitai desu.","german":"I want to live a normal life.","furigana":"ふつう|せいかつ"},{"japanese":"普通の生活が好きです。","romaji":"Futsū no seikatsu ga suki desu.","german":"I like a normal life.","furigana":"ふつう|せいかつ|す"},{"japanese":"この駅には普通しか止まりません。","romaji":"Kono eki ni wa futsū shika tomarimasen.","german":"Only local trains stop at this station.","furigana":"えき|ふつう|と"}],"notes":"普通 describes what is normal or ordinary. Before a noun, の is very common: 普通の生活, a normal life. In rail contexts, 普通 also means the local train that stops at all the designated stations. 普通しか止まらない means in this context that only such trains stop there. The long final syllable is written futsū in Hepburn.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"反対側に行ってください。","romaji":"Hantai gawa ni itte kudasai.","german":"Please go to the other side.","furigana":"はんたいがわ|い"},{"japanese":"私は反対です。","romaji":"Watashi wa hantai desu.","german":"I am against it.","furigana":"わたし|はんたい"}],"notes":"反対 can mean \"opposite\" spatially or \"against\" in a discussion. 反対側 is the opposite or other side. 私は反対です expresses opposition to a proposal. With する, Aに反対する means \"to speak out against A\". The context separates a statement of place from an opinion; an opposite side is not an objecting person.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 47"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自由に使ってください。","romaji":"Jiyū ni tsukatte kudasai.","german":"Please feel free to use it.","furigana":"じゆう|つか"},{"japanese":"週末は自由な時間があります。","romaji":"Shūmatsu wa jiyū na jikan ga arimasu.","german":"I have free time on the weekend.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|じゆう|じかん"}],"notes":"自由 means \"free, freedom\". 自由に使う allows use at one's own discretion; 自由な時間 is time one can dispose of oneself. Before a noun, な is used here, for the manner of an action に. 自由 does not automatically mean \"free of charge\"; there is 無料 for that. The reading じゆう ends with a long u: jiyū.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 22"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"バス停はどこですか。","romaji":"Basutei wa doko desu ka.","german":"Where is the bus stop?","furigana":"てい"},{"japanese":"バス停で待っています。","romaji":"Basutei de matte imasu.","german":"I am waiting at the bus stop.","furigana":"てい|ま"}],"notes":"バス停 is a bus stop. どこですか asks about its location; で marks an activity there, such as waiting. The mixed spelling バス停 combines the katakana word バス with 停, read てい. バス停 refers to the stopping point, not the bus itself and not necessarily a large bus terminal.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"切符売り場はあちらです。","romaji":"Kippu uriba wa achira desu.","german":"The ticket office is over there.","furigana":"きっぷう|ば"},{"japanese":"切符売り場の前に長い列ができています。","romaji":"Kippu uriba no mae ni nagai retsu ga dekite imasu.","german":"A long line has formed in front of the ticket office.","furigana":"きっぷう|ば|まえ|なが|れつ"}],"notes":"切符売り場 is the place where you buy tickets, whether travel or admission tickets. In these examples the ticket office is meant. 売り場 is the place of sale; 切符 says what is sold. の前に describes the area in front, for example for a queue. The word does not refer to the ticket gate you pass through to reach the platform.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"改札口を通ります。","romaji":"Kaisatsuguchi o tōrimasu.","german":"I go through the ticket gate.","furigana":"かいさつぐち|とお"},{"japanese":"改札口を通ってホームに行きます。","romaji":"Kaisatsuguchi o tōtte hōmu ni ikimasu.","german":"I go through the ticket gate to the platform.","furigana":"かいさつぐち|とお|い"},{"japanese":"改札口の外で、友達を待っています。","romaji":"Kaisatsuguchi no soto de, tomodachi o matte imasu.","german":"I am waiting for a friend outside the ticket gate.","furigana":"かいさつぐち|そと|ともだち|ま"}],"notes":"改札口 is the entrance with the ticket check, often with barriers between the entrance area and the platforms. 改札口を通る means to pass this checkpoint; を marks the place passed through here. 改札口の外 is the area outside. It is a handy meeting point, but not a counter for buying tickets.","levelNote":"Compound: 改札 (N2) + 口 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"三番ホームに行ってください。","romaji":"Sanban hōmu ni itte kudasai.","german":"Please go to platform 3.","furigana":"さんばん|い"},{"japanese":"電車は三番ホームに来ます。","romaji":"Densha wa sanban hōmu ni kimasu.","german":"The train arrives at platform three.","furigana":"でんしゃ|さんばん|き"}],"notes":"ホーム means \"platform\" here. 三番ホーム is platform three, which one can go to or where a train can arrive. に names the destination in both examples. The pronunciation has a long o: hōmu. This word comes from \"platform\" and in a railway context is not the similar-sounding English \"home\" meaning a residence.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どの乗り物で行きますか。","romaji":"Dono norimono de ikimasu ka.","german":"Which vehicle will you go by?","furigana":"の|もの|い"},{"japanese":"どの乗り物が好きですか。","romaji":"Dono norimono ga suki desu ka.","german":"Which vehicle do you like?","furigana":"の|もの|す"}],"notes":"乗り物 is a vehicle or means of transport that one travels in. どの乗り物 asks which one among several is meant. With で it names the transport used, with が in 好きです the preferred one. The word says nothing yet about schedule or propulsion; bus, train and bicycle are possible examples.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 36"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"歩道橋を渡ってください。","romaji":"Hodōkyō o watatte kudasai.","german":"Please cross the pedestrian bridge.","furigana":"ほどうきょう|わた"},{"japanese":"この歩道橋から富士山がよく見えます。","romaji":"Kono hodōkyō kara Fujisan ga yoku miemasu.","german":"You can see Mt. Fuji well from this pedestrian bridge.","furigana":"ほどうきょう|ふじさん|み"}],"notes":"歩道橋 is a pedestrian bridge, often over a road. 歩道橋を渡る means to cross it; を marks the path that is crossed. 歩道橋から names it as the starting point of the view. The word means a bridge for pedestrians and not just the sidewalk at the edge of the road. The long vowels are written hodōkyō.","levelNote":"Compound: 歩道 (N3) + 橋 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"駐輪場に自転車を止めます。","romaji":"Chūrinjō ni jitensha o tomemasu.","german":"I park my bicycle at the bicycle parking area.","furigana":"ちゅうりんじょう|じてんしゃ|と"},{"japanese":"駐輪場に自転車を止めてください。","romaji":"Chūrinjō ni jitensha o tomete kudasai.","german":"Please park your bicycle at the bicycle parking area.","furigana":"ちゅうりんじょう|じてんしゃ|と"},{"japanese":"駅の駐輪場はいっぱいだったので、別の場所を探しました。","romaji":"Eki no chūrinjō wa ippai datta node, betsu no basho o sagashimashita.","german":"The bicycle parking area at the station was full, so I looked for another place.","furigana":"えき|ちゅうりんじょう|べつ|ばしょ|さが"}],"notes":"駐輪場 is an area for parking bicycles. With に you name the parking place, with で an activity there. The word differs from 駐車場, the parking lot for cars and, in the right context, other vehicles. By itself it gives no information on whether the bicycle parking is free. The reading is chūrinjō.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"踏切で待ちます。","romaji":"Fumikiri de machimasu.","german":"I wait at the railroad crossing.","furigana":"ふみきり|ま"},{"japanese":"踏切を渡ると、右に郵便局があります。","romaji":"Fumikiri o wataru to, migi ni yūbinkyoku ga arimasu.","german":"If you cross the railroad crossing, the post office is on the right.","furigana":"ふみきり|わた|みぎ|ゆうびんきょく"}],"notes":"踏切 is a railroad crossing where a road or path crosses the tracks at the same level. で names the place of an activity such as waiting, を names a path that is crossed. You do not normally board the train there; for that you go to the station and platform. In these examples only the railroad crossing is meant.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"商店街で買い物します。","romaji":"Shōtengai de kaimono shimasu.","german":"I shop at the shopping street.","furigana":"しょうてんがい|か|もの"},{"japanese":"商店街で買い物をしました。","romaji":"Shōtengai de kaimono o shimashita.","german":"I shopped at the shopping street.","furigana":"しょうてんがい|か|もの"},{"japanese":"商店街の店は、日曜日も開いています。","romaji":"Shōtengai no mise wa, nichiyōbi mo aite imasu.","german":"The shops in the shopping street are open on Sundays too.","furigana":"しょうてんがい|みせ|にちようび|あ"}],"notes":"商店街 is a shopping street or a district with many shops. で names it as the place of shopping. の describes something within this shopping area, such as 商店街の店. It does not have to be a single shopping mall; it often includes several small, independent shops. The long first syllable is written shōtengai.","levelNote":"Compound: 商店 (N2) + 街 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"背中が痛いです。","romaji":"Senaka ga itai desu.","german":"My back hurts.","furigana":"せなか|いた"},{"japanese":"背中がかゆいのに、手が届きません。","romaji":"Senaka ga kayui noni, te ga todokimasen.","german":"My back itches, but I can't reach it with my hand.","furigana":"せなか|て|とど"}],"notes":"背中 is the back as a part of the body. With が one names it, for example, as the place of pain or itching. 背中がかゆいのに connects the itch with the unexpected difficulty of reaching the spot. The word here denotes neither the rear part of a building nor in general everything that lies \"behind\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"腰が痛いです。","romaji":"Koshi ga itai desu.","german":"My lower back hurts.","furigana":"こし|いた"},{"japanese":"腰が痛いので休みます。","romaji":"Koshi ga itai node yasumimasu.","german":"My lower back hurts, so I'm going to rest.","furigana":"こし|いた|やす"},{"japanese":"腰に手を当てて、写真を撮りました。","romaji":"Koshi ni te o atete, shashin o torimashita.","german":"I put my hand on my hip for the photo.","furigana":"こし|て|あ|しゃしん|と"}],"notes":"腰 refers to the lower back or the area around the waist and hips. 腰が痛い is commonly understood in everyday speech as \"I have back pain\"; it does not have to mean the hip joint specifically. 腰に names a position on this part of the body. The body-part sense practiced here is not コシ, the firmness of noodles or paper.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"足首を捻りました。","romaji":"Ashikubi o hinerimashita.","german":"I sprained my ankle.","furigana":"あしくび|ひね"},{"japanese":"足首をひねりました。","romaji":"Ashikubi o hinerimashita.","german":"I sprained my ankle.","furigana":"あしくび"},{"japanese":"靴下が短くて、足首が見えています。","romaji":"Kutsushita ga mijikakute, ashikubi ga miete imasu.","german":"My socks are short, so my ankles are showing.","furigana":"くつした|みじか|あしくび|み"}],"notes":"足首 is the ankle joint, or the area around the ankle connecting foot and lower leg. 足首をひねる means to twist or roll it. The form with を names the affected spot; 足首が見える says it is visible. It does not mean the whole foot, even though 足 is in the word.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"爪を切ります。","romaji":"Tsume o kirimasu.","german":"Ich schneide mir die Nägel.","furigana":"つめ|き"},{"japanese":"爪を切らなければなりません。","romaji":"Tsume o kiranakereba narimasen.","german":"Ich muss mir die Nägel schneiden.","furigana":"つめ|き"},{"japanese":"猫の爪は鋭いです。","romaji":"Neko no tsume wa surudoi desu.","german":"Die Krallen der Katze sind scharf.","furigana":"ねこ|つめ|するど"}],"notes":"爪 bezeichnet hier Finger- und Zehennägel; bei Tieren kann es auch eine Kralle sein. 爪を切る heißt, die Nägel zu schneiden. Für einen Nagel, den man in ein Brett schlägt, verwendet man 釘. Das japanische Wort 爪 kann in einem Satz einen oder mehrere Nägel meinen; die deutsche Übersetzung richtet sich nach der Situation.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"皮膚が乾燥しています。","romaji":"Hifu ga kansō shite imasu.","german":"The skin is dry.","furigana":"ひふ|かんそう"},{"japanese":"冬になると、皮膚がかゆくなります。","romaji":"Fuyu ni naru to, hifu ga kayuku narimasu.","german":"In winter my skin starts to itch.","furigana":"ふゆ|ひふ"}],"notes":"皮膚 is skin as body tissue. It is more technical than the common everyday word 肌, which often stresses the visible condition of the skin. 皮膚が乾燥している describes dry skin; かゆくなる describes a change toward itching. The word alone does not name a cause or a particular skin disease.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"血が出ています。","romaji":"Chi ga dete imasu.","german":"I am bleeding.","furigana":"ち|で"},{"japanese":"指を切って血が出ました。","romaji":"Yubi o kitte chi ga demashita.","german":"I cut my finger and it bled.","furigana":"ゆび|き|ち|で"},{"japanese":"シャツに血が付いたので、すぐ洗いました。","romaji":"Shatsu ni chi ga tsuita node, sugu araimashita.","german":"Blood got on my shirt, so I washed it right away.","furigana":"ち|つ|あら"}],"notes":"血 means \"blood\". 血が出る is the usual combination for \"to bleed\"; the blood takes が as what comes out. 血が付く describes blood sticking to something, such as a shirt. The reading of the single word is ち; in compound words the same kanji can be read differently.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"涙が出ました。","romaji":"Namida ga demashita.","german":"Mir kamen die Tränen.","furigana":"なみだ|で"},{"japanese":"映画を見て涙が出ました。","romaji":"Eiga o mite namida ga demashita.","german":"Beim Film kamen mir die Tränen.","furigana":"えいが|み|なみだ|で"},{"japanese":"玉ねぎを切ると、涙が出ます。","romaji":"Tamanegi o kiru to, namida ga demasu.","german":"Wenn ich Zwiebeln schneide, kommen mir die Tränen.","furigana":"たま|き|なみだ|で"}],"notes":"涙 sind Tränen. 涙が出る heißt „Tränen kommen“, sei es durch Gefühle oder eine körperliche Reaktion. 映画を見て nennt den Anlass, ohne allein festzulegen, ob der Film traurig oder rührend war. 玉ねぎ kann als körperlicher Auslöser stehen. Die Form des Nomens ändert sich nicht, wenn auf Deutsch mehrere Tränen gemeint sind.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 44"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"汗をかきました。","romaji":"Ase o kakimashita.","german":"I sweated.","furigana":"あせ"},{"japanese":"運動して汗をかきました。","romaji":"Undō shite ase o kakimashita.","german":"I exercised and worked up a sweat.","furigana":"うんどう|あせ"},{"japanese":"タオルで顔の汗を拭きました。","romaji":"Taoru de kao no ase o fukimashita.","german":"I wiped the sweat off my face with a towel.","furigana":"かお|あせ|ふ"}],"notes":"汗 is sweat. The usual combination 汗をかく means \"to sweat\"; かく in this combination does not mean \"to write\". 汗を拭く means to wipe off the sweat. 運動して can name the preceding activity. The examples concern sweat on the body, not condensation on a cold glass.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 39"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"息が苦しいです。","romaji":"Iki ga kurushii desu.","german":"It's hard for me to breathe.","furigana":"いき|くる"},{"japanese":"深く息をしてください。","romaji":"Fukaku iki o shite kudasai.","german":"Please breathe deeply.","furigana":"ふか|いき"}],"notes":"息 is breath or breathing. 息をする means \"to breathe\"; 息が苦しい describes difficulty breathing. 深く息をする asks for deep breathing and does not by itself mean only breathing in. For \"to breathe in\" 息を吸う is more exact, for \"to breathe out\" 息を吐く. In these examples physical breathing is meant.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"咳が止まりません。","romaji":"Seki ga tomarimasen.","german":"My cough won't stop.","furigana":"せき|と"},{"japanese":"咳が出るときは、マスクをしてください。","romaji":"Seki ga deru toki wa, masuku o shite kudasai.","german":"When you have a cough, please wear a mask.","furigana":"せき|で"}],"notes":"咳 means \"cough\" as a noun. 咳が出る describes having to cough; 咳が止まらない, that the cough does not stop. 咳をする is also a common combination. ときは names, in the second sentence, the situation in which the request to wear a mask applies. The word itself does not specify any cause of illness.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 12"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"怪我をしました。","romaji":"Kega o shimashita.","german":"I got hurt.","furigana":"けが"},{"japanese":"サッカーで怪我をしました。","romaji":"Sakkā de kega o shimashita.","german":"I got injured playing soccer.","furigana":"けが"},{"japanese":"怪我が治ったので、また学校に歩いて行けます。","romaji":"Kega ga naotta node, mata gakkō ni aruite ikemasu.","german":"My injury has healed, so I can walk to school again.","furigana":"けが|なお|がっこう|ある|い"}],"notes":"怪我 is an injury. 怪我をする and 怪我する mean \"to get injured\". で can name the occasion, such as サッカーで. It is not the general word for illness; an injury can come from a fall or an accident. The kanji are read as the word けが, not assembled individually from their usual readings.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"親に感謝します。","romaji":"Oya ni kansha shimasu.","german":"Ich bin meinen Eltern dankbar.","furigana":"おや|かんしゃ"},{"japanese":"親に電話をかけます。","romaji":"Oya ni denwa o kakemasu.","german":"Ich rufe meine Eltern an.","furigana":"おや|でんわ"}],"notes":"親 kann ein Elternteil oder beide Eltern bezeichnen; der Satzkontext bestimmt die deutsche Zahl. 親に感謝する nennt die Eltern als Personen, denen man dankbar ist. 親に電話をかける nennt sie als Empfänger des Anrufs. Das Wort ist im gewöhnlichen Gespräch sachlich; für die gezielte Nennung von Vater und Mutter gibt es 父 und 母. In Kana lautet dieses Nomen おや; es ist vom gleich klingenden Ausruf おや („Oh!“) zu unterscheiden.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 48"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"祖父は元気です。","romaji":"Sofu wa genki desu.","german":"My grandfather is well.","furigana":"そふ|げんき"},{"japanese":"祖父は八十歳です。","romaji":"Sofu wa hachijussai desu.","german":"My grandfather is eighty years old.","furigana":"そふ|はちじゅっさい"}],"notes":"祖父 means \"grandfather\" and is used especially when talking to others about one's own grandfather. When addressing him directly, おじいさん or おじいちゃん is usual. 祖父は元気です describes his well-being; 八十歳 names his age. When speaking about one's own family, this plain family term usually takes no さん.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 41"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"祖母の家に行きます。","romaji":"Sobo no ie ni ikimasu.","german":"Ich gehe zum Haus meiner Großmutter.","furigana":"そぼ|いえ|い"},{"japanese":"祖母の料理がおいしいです。","romaji":"Sobo no ryōri ga oishii desu.","german":"Das Essen meiner Großmutter ist lecker.","furigana":"そぼ|りょうり"}],"notes":"祖母 heißt „Großmutter“ und passt zum sachlichen Erzählen über die eigene Familie. Beim direkten Ansprechen sind おばあさん oder おばあちゃん häufig. 祖母の家 benennt ihr Haus; 祖母の料理 die von ihr zubereiteten Gerichte. の zeigt hier also verschiedene Beziehungen, die auf Deutsch unterschiedlich ausgedrückt werden können.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 41"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"孫が三人います。","romaji":"Mago ga sannin imasu.","german":"Ich habe drei Enkelkinder.","furigana":"まご|さんにん"},{"japanese":"祖母は孫の写真を見るのが好きです。","romaji":"Sobo wa mago no shashin o miru no ga suki desu.","german":"Meine Großmutter schaut sich gern Fotos ihrer Enkel an.","furigana":"そぼ|まご|しゃしん|み|す"}],"notes":"孫 heißt „Enkelkind“; das einzelne Wort legt das Geschlecht nicht fest. 三人 zählt hier drei Enkelkinder, weil 人 für Personen steht. 孫の写真 kann im Kontext Fotos der Enkelkinder meinen, nicht nur Fotos, die ihnen gehören. Ob auf Deutsch Enkel, Enkelin oder Enkelkinder passt, ergibt sich aus weiteren Angaben.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 41"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"従兄弟と遊びました。","romaji":"Itoko to asobimashita.","german":"I played with my cousin.","furigana":"いとこ|あそ"},{"japanese":"従兄弟は大阪に住んでいて、夏休みに会います。","romaji":"Itoko wa Ōsaka ni sunde ite, natsuyasumi ni aimasu.","german":"My cousin lives in Osaka; I see him during the summer vacation.","furigana":"いとこ|おおさか|す|なつやす|あ"}],"notes":"従兄弟 means a male cousin in this spelling. The reading いとこ is often written in kana in everyday use and, depending on the context, can also be used without regard to gender. For a female cousin there is the spelling 従姉妹. With と you name the person you play with; に in 夏休みに gives the time of the meeting.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"甥が来ました。","romaji":"Oi ga kimashita.","german":"My nephew came.","furigana":"おい|き"},{"japanese":"甥は五歳です。","romaji":"Oi wa gosai desu.","german":"My nephew is five years old.","furigana":"おい|ごさい"}],"notes":"甥 is a nephew, that is, the son of one's own brother or sister. It is a male kinship term; 姪 means niece. 甥が来た reports his arrival, 甥は五歳です his age. The reading おい contains two separate vowels and is written oi.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"姪は五歳です。","romaji":"Mei wa gosai desu.","german":"My niece is five years old.","furigana":"めい|ごさい"},{"japanese":"姪にプレゼントをあげました。","romaji":"Mei ni purezento o agemashita.","german":"I gave my niece a present.","furigana":"めい"}],"notes":"姪 is a niece, that is, the daughter of one's own brother or sister. 甥 is the male counterpart, \"nephew\". 姪にプレゼントをあげる names the recipient with に and the present with を. The reading めい is written mei; here it is not a single long e in the Hepburn spelling.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"まだ独身です。","romaji":"Mada dokushin desu.","german":"I am still single.","furigana":"どくしん"},{"japanese":"田中さんはまだ独身です。","romaji":"Tanaka-san wa mada dokushin desu.","german":"Mr. Tanaka is still single.","furigana":"たなか|どくしん"},{"japanese":"独身の人も、この会に参加できます。","romaji":"Dokushin no hito mo, kono kai ni sanka dekimasu.","german":"Unmarried people can also take part in this meeting.","furigana":"どくしん|ひと|かい|さんか"}],"notes":"独身 means \"unmarried, single\". By itself it does not say whether someone lives alone or is in a relationship at all. Before a noun, use の: 独身の人. まだ独身です stresses \"still\"; without まだ the statement is more neutral. The word describes current marital status and does not prove that someone was never married.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 15"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仲の良い夫婦です。","romaji":"Naka no yoi fūfu desu.","german":"They are a happily married couple.","furigana":"なか|よ|ふうふ"},{"japanese":"あの夫婦はとても仲がいいです。","romaji":"Ano fūfu wa totemo naka ga ii desu.","german":"That couple gets along very well.","furigana":"ふうふ|なか"},{"japanese":"あの夫婦は二人で小さな店を開きました。","romaji":"Ano fūfu wa futari de chiisana mise o hirakimashita.","german":"That couple opened a small shop together.","furigana":"ふうふ|ふたり|ちい|みせ|ひら"}],"notes":"夫婦 means a married couple and refers to both married partners together. 仲の良い夫婦 describes their good relationship; 夫婦で can say that they do something together as a couple. It is not a general word for two friends. The reading ふうふ has a long u: fūfu.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 14"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"双子の兄弟です。","romaji":"Futago no kyōdai desu.","german":"They are twin brothers.","furigana":"ふたご|きょうだい"},{"japanese":"双子の誕生日を祝いました。","romaji":"Futago no tanjōbi o iwaimashita.","german":"I celebrated the twins' birthday.","furigana":"ふたご|たんじょうび|いわ"}],"notes":"双子 refers to twins as a pair or to a single twin child. With の you describe the relationship: 双子の兄弟 are twin brothers. Even for a joint birthday party, 双子 remains an ordinary noun; it needs no separate plural ending.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 38"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"赤ちゃんが生まれました。","romaji":"Akachan ga umaremashita.","german":"A baby was born.","furigana":"あか|う"},{"japanese":"赤ちゃんが泣いています。","romaji":"Akachan ga naite imasu.","german":"The baby is crying.","furigana":"あか|な"}],"notes":"赤ちゃん is the usual word for a baby. The ちゃん is part of the whole word here; the meaning is not literally \"little red\". 赤ちゃんが生まれる reports a birth, and 赤ちゃんが泣いている describes a baby that is crying right now. The kana form あかちゃん has the same meaning and reading.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 38"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大人になりたいです。","romaji":"Otona ni naritai desu.","german":"I want to become an adult.","furigana":"おとな"},{"japanese":"大人は千円です。","romaji":"Otona wa sen'en desu.","german":"Adults are 1,000 yen.","furigana":"おとな|せんえん"}],"notes":"The reading of 大人 is おとな. With 大人になる you talk about growing up. On price lists, 大人 is the category \"adults\": in 大人は千円です the admission price is meant, even though 料金 is not stated explicitly.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 39"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"若者に人気があります。","romaji":"Wakamono ni ninki ga arimasu.","german":"It is popular with young people.","furigana":"わかもの|にんき"},{"japanese":"若者が公園でスポーツをしています。","romaji":"Wakamono ga kōen de supōtsu o shite imasu.","german":"Young people are playing sports in the park.","furigana":"わかもの|こうえん"}],"notes":"若者 can refer to one young person or to young people in general. In 若者に人気がある, に names the group among whom something is popular. With 若者が, on the other hand, you describe the young people themselves as the ones acting; the number is clear from the context.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"老人がゆっくり歩いています。","romaji":"Rōjin ga yukkuri aruite imasu.","german":"An elderly person is walking slowly.","furigana":"ろうじん|ある"},{"japanese":"老人ホームで働いています。","romaji":"Rōjin hōmu de hataraite imasu.","german":"I work at a nursing home.","furigana":"ろうじん|はたら"}],"notes":"老人 means an old or elderly person. In 老人ホーム it comes before the loanword ホーム and forms the term for a nursing home. Note the long vowels in rōjin and hōmu; in a sentence about a person, 老人 can also stand alone.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"男性が三人います。","romaji":"Dansei ga sannin imasu.","german":"There are three men.","furigana":"だんせい|さんにん"},{"japanese":"あの男性は田中さんです。","romaji":"Ano dansei wa Tanaka-san desu.","german":"That man is Mr. Tanaka.","furigana":"だんせい|たなか"}],"notes":"男性 is a neutral, formal term for a man or a male person. To count people, 人 follows the number: 男性が三人. With あの男性 you refer to a man at some distance; あの corresponds to \"that\" here.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 47"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"女性の先生です。","romaji":"Josei no sensei desu.","german":"She is a female teacher.","furigana":"じょせい|せんせい"},{"japanese":"あの女性はとても親切です。","romaji":"Ano josei wa totemo shinsetsu desu.","german":"That woman is very kind.","furigana":"じょせい|しんせつ"}],"notes":"女性 refers to a woman or a female person. 女性の先生 makes it explicit that the teacher is a woman; 先生 alone does not specify gender. In あの女性は… a particular woman at some distance becomes the topic of the sentence.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 47"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"客にお茶を出しました。","romaji":"Kyaku ni ocha o dashimashita.","german":"I served tea to a guest.","furigana":"きゃく|ちゃ|だ"},{"japanese":"あの店には客が大勢います。","romaji":"Ano mise ni wa kyaku ga ōzei imasu.","german":"There are a lot of customers in that shop.","furigana":"みせ|きゃく|おおぜい"}],"notes":"客 means \"guest\" or \"customer\" depending on the situation. When hosting, a guest is meant; in a shop, customers. The polite form is お客さん. In a report the plain 客 can also appear, for example in 客にお茶を出す: to serve tea to a guest.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"店員に聞いてください。","romaji":"Ten'in ni kiite kudasai.","german":"Please ask a shop assistant.","furigana":"てんいん|き"},{"japanese":"店員さんが商品を並べています。","romaji":"Ten'in-san ga shōhin o narabete imasu.","german":"A shop assistant is arranging the goods.","furigana":"てんいん|しょうひん|なら"}],"notes":"店員 refers to a person who works in a shop. The word does not specify gender. 店員に聞く means to turn to the shop staff; 店員さん is the polite form. In Hepburn romanization, the apostrophe in ten'in separates ん from the following い.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"父はタクシーの運転手です。","romaji":"Chichi wa takushī no untenshu desu.","german":"My father is a taxi driver.","furigana":"ちち|うんてんしゅ"},{"japanese":"バスの運転手さんに聞きました。","romaji":"Basu no untenshu-san ni kikimashita.","german":"I asked the bus driver.","furigana":"うんてんしゅ|き"}],"notes":"運転手 is a driver, especially as a job title. The vehicle can precede it with の: タクシーの運転手 or バスの運転手. When speaking politely about the person, 運転手さん is possible; さん does not change the meaning of the occupation.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 40"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"看護師になりたいです。","romaji":"Kangoshi ni naritai desu.","german":"I want to become a nurse.","furigana":"かんごし"},{"japanese":"看護師さんが優しかったです。","romaji":"Kangoshi-san ga yasashikatta desu.","german":"The nurse was kind.","furigana":"かんごし|やさ"}],"notes":"看護師 refers to a nurse, regardless of gender. 看護師になる describes entering this profession; になりたい expresses a career wish. 看護師さん is a polite way to refer to the person, for example in a hospital.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"駅の前に警察官が立っています。","romaji":"Eki no mae ni keisatsukan ga tatte imasu.","german":"A police officer is standing in front of the station.","furigana":"えき|まえ|けいさつかん|た"},{"japanese":"警察官に道を聞きました。","romaji":"Keisatsukan ni michi o kikimashita.","german":"I asked a police officer for directions.","furigana":"けいさつかん|みち|き"}],"notes":"警察官 is the job title for a police officer. When you ask the person something, に is used: 警察官に道を聞く means \"ask the police for directions\". 警察 means the police, 警察官 the individual person in this occupation.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"消防士が助けてくれました。","romaji":"Shōbōshi ga tasukete kuremashita.","german":"A firefighter helped me.","furigana":"しょうぼうし|たす"},{"japanese":"消防士になりたい子供が多いです。","romaji":"Shōbōshi ni naritai kodomo ga ōi desu.","german":"Many children want to become firefighters.","furigana":"しょうぼうし|こども|おお"}],"notes":"消防士 refers to a person in the fire department. The ending 士 is part of the occupational title. 消防士になりたい is a career wish; 消防士が助けてくれた describes help that benefited oneself. In shōbōshi both o are long.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"農家の人が野菜を売っています。","romaji":"Nōka no hito ga yasai o utte imasu.","german":"A farmer is selling vegetables.","furigana":"のうか|ひと|やさい|う"},{"japanese":"祖父は農家です。","romaji":"Sofu wa nōka desu.","german":"My grandfather is a farmer.","furigana":"そふ|のうか"}],"notes":"農家 can mean a farmer or a farming family. 農家の人 explicitly makes the person from an agricultural household the topic. In 祖父は農家です the grandfather's occupation is meant; 家 is read か in this word.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大工さんが家を建てます。","romaji":"Daiku-san ga ie o tatemasu.","german":"The carpenter builds a house.","furigana":"だいく|いえ|た"},{"japanese":"大工さんにドアの修理を頼みました。","romaji":"Daiku-san ni doa no shūri o tanomimashita.","german":"I asked the carpenter to repair the door.","furigana":"だいく|しゅうり|たの"}],"notes":"大工 is a craftsman who mainly does woodwork on houses. 大工さん is the polite way to refer to the person. For a repair job the person takes に and the requested work takes を: 大工さんに修理を頼む.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仲間と一緒に頑張ります。","romaji":"Nakama to issho ni ganbarimasu.","german":"I will do my best together with my comrades.","furigana":"なかま|いっしょ|がんば"},{"japanese":"サッカーの仲間と旅行に行きました。","romaji":"Sakkā no nakama to ryokō ni ikimashita.","german":"I went on a trip with my soccer buddies.","furigana":"なかま|りょこう|い"}],"notes":"仲間 are people with whom you do something together or share an interest. サッカーの仲間, for example, are the people from your own soccer group. 仲間と一緒に emphasizes acting together; the word itself can refer to one person or several.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 37"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先輩に教えてもらいました。","romaji":"Senpai ni oshiete moraimashita.","german":"I had my senpai teach me.","furigana":"せんぱい|おし"},{"japanese":"先輩、お先に失礼します。","romaji":"Senpai, osaki ni shitsurei shimasu.","german":"Senpai, I'll be leaving ahead of you.","furigana":"せんぱい|さき|しつれい"}],"notes":"先輩 is a person who has been at the same school or workplace for longer. What matters is this relationship, not just age. You can learn from a 先輩 or address someone that way; お先に失礼します is the polite phrase for leaving before others.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 22"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"後輩からメールが来ました。","romaji":"Kōhai kara mēru ga kimashita.","german":"I got an email from my kōhai.","furigana":"こうはい|き"},{"japanese":"新しく入った後輩に仕事を教えました。","romaji":"Atarashiku haitta kōhai ni shigoto o oshiemashita.","german":"I taught the work to a newly joined kōhai.","furigana":"あたら|はい|こうはい|しごと|おし"}],"notes":"後輩 is the counterpart of 先輩: a person who joined the same school or workplace later. They do not have to be younger. 後輩から names them as the sender of a message; 後輩に names them as the recipient of an explanation.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 22"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"上司に報告します。","romaji":"Jōshi ni hōkoku shimasu.","german":"I report to my boss.","furigana":"じょうし|ほうこく"},{"japanese":"上司と会議に出ました。","romaji":"Jōshi to kaigi ni demashita.","german":"I attended a meeting with my boss.","furigana":"じょうし|かいぎ|で"}],"notes":"上司 is a superior in the workplace. Reporting to a 上司 is 上司に報告する; に names the recipient. In 上司と会議に出る, on the other hand, と indicates the person accompanying you to the meeting. In jōshi and hōkoku the first o is long in each.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"部下を育てます。","romaji":"Buka o sodatemasu.","german":"I train my subordinates.","furigana":"ぶか|そだ"},{"japanese":"部下に仕事を頼みました。","romaji":"Buka ni shigoto o tanomimashita.","german":"I asked my subordinate to take on a task.","furigana":"ぶか|しごと|たの"}],"notes":"部下 refers to a person who is under one's authority at work. 部下を育てる means professional development, not raising children. 部下に仕事を頼む means to ask this person to take on a task; に names the person being addressed.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 22"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"同僚と昼ご飯を食べます。","romaji":"Dōryō to hirugohan o tabemasu.","german":"I eat lunch with my colleagues.","furigana":"どうりょう|ひる|はん|た"},{"japanese":"同僚に仕事を手伝ってもらいました。","romaji":"Dōryō ni shigoto o tetsudatte moraimashita.","german":"A colleague helped me with my work.","furigana":"どうりょう|しごと|てつだ"}],"notes":"同僚 are colleagues from the same work environment. With 同僚と you name the companion in a shared activity. 同僚に手伝ってもらう emphasizes that you receive help from colleagues. Both o in dōryō are long.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 21"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"社長にお会いしました。","romaji":"Shachō ni oai shimashita.","german":"I met the company president.","furigana":"しゃちょう|あ"},{"japanese":"社長はとても忙しいです。","romaji":"Shachō wa totemo isogashii desu.","german":"The company president is very busy.","furigana":"しゃちょう|いそが"}],"notes":"社長 refers to the head of a company. By itself it does not say who owns the company. In 社長にお会いしました, 会う is expressed politely as お会いする; the person met takes に. 社長は makes the company president the topic of a description.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 18"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"会社員の友達からメールが来ました。","romaji":"Kaishain no tomodachi kara mēru ga kimashita.","german":"I got an email from a friend who is a company employee.","furigana":"かいしゃいん|ともだち|き"},{"japanese":"父は会社員です。","romaji":"Chichi wa kaishain desu.","german":"My father is a company employee.","furigana":"ちち|かいしゃいん"}],"notes":"会社員 is a person employed at a company. The word names the type of employment, not a concrete task such as sales or accounting. With の you can add this information to a person: 会社員の友達 is a friend who is employed at a company.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"公務員になりたいです。","romaji":"Kōmuin ni naritai desu.","german":"I want to become a public servant.","furigana":"こうむいん"},{"japanese":"兄は公務員です。","romaji":"Ani wa kōmuin desu.","german":"My older brother is a public servant.","furigana":"あに|こうむいん"}],"notes":"公務員 refers to people employed in the public service, for example at a government agency. 公務員になる means to take up this kind of employment. 公務員になりたい expresses a career wish; 公務員です, by contrast, describes a person's current employment.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"給料日は25日です。","romaji":"Kyūryōbi wa nijūgo-nichi desu.","german":"Payday is on the 25th.","furigana":"きゅうりょうび|にち"},{"japanese":"来月から給料が上がります。","romaji":"Raigetsu kara kyūryō ga agarimasu.","german":"My salary goes up from next month.","furigana":"らいげつ|きゅうりょう|あ"}],"notes":"給料 is the salary or wages from a job. 給料日 is payday. For a raise you say 給料が上がる: the salary goes up. 来月から names the start of the change; 給料 itself says nothing about the amount or the payment date.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 28"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"休憩しましょう。","romaji":"Kyūkei shimashō.","german":"Let's take a break.","furigana":"きゅうけい"},{"japanese":"休憩は二時までです。","romaji":"Kyūkei wa niji made desu.","german":"The break lasts until two o'clock.","furigana":"きゅうけい|にじ"}],"notes":"休憩 is a break during work or an activity. With する it becomes \"to take a break\": 休憩しましょう is an invitation to do so together. As a noun, 休憩 can also refer to the period of time; 休憩は二時までです gives the end of the break.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日は残業です。","romaji":"Kyō wa zangyō desu.","german":"I'm working overtime today.","furigana":"きょう|ざんぎょう"},{"japanese":"残業の後で、友達と夕ご飯を食べました。","romaji":"Zangyō no ato de, tomodachi to yūgohan o tabemashita.","german":"After overtime, I had dinner with a friend.","furigana":"ざんぎょう|あと|ともだち|ゆう|はん|た"}],"notes":"残業 refers to work beyond regular working hours. 今日は残業です is a short statement that you are working overtime today. With 残業の後で you link overtime, as a noun, to a following activity.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 17"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"通勤に一時間かかります。","romaji":"Tsūkin ni ichijikan kakarimasu.","german":"The commute takes an hour.","furigana":"つうきん|いちじかん"},{"japanese":"電車で通勤しています。","romaji":"Densha de tsūkin shite imasu.","german":"I commute to work by train.","furigana":"でんしゃ|つうきん"}],"notes":"通勤 is the regular trip to work, not the trip to school. 通勤する means \"to commute to work\". In 通勤に一時間かかる, に names the activity that takes time; 電車で, on the other hand, names the means of transport.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"三時から会議があります。","romaji":"Sanji kara kaigi ga arimasu.","german":"There is a meeting from three o'clock.","furigana":"さんじ|かいぎ"},{"japanese":"会議で意見を言いました。","romaji":"Kaigi de iken o iimashita.","german":"I gave my opinion at the meeting.","furigana":"かいぎ|いけん|い"}],"notes":"会議 is a meeting or conference. 会議がある announces that one is taking place; から names the start. In 会議で意見を言う, で marks the setting in which one voices an opinion. The spelling 会議 belongs here to the noun.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"名刺を交換しましょう。","romaji":"Meishi o kōkan shimashō.","german":"Let's exchange business cards.","furigana":"めいし|こうかん"},{"japanese":"大事な会議なのに、名刺を忘れてしまいました。","romaji":"Daiji na kaigi na noni, meishi o wasurete shimaimashita.","german":"Even though it's an important meeting, I forgot my business cards.","furigana":"だいじ|かいぎ|めいし|わす"}],"notes":"名刺 is a business card with a name and contact details. 名刺を交換する means to exchange business cards. 名刺を忘れてしまう describes forgetting them with a tone of regret; なのに contrasts it with the important occasion.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"書類にサインしてください。","romaji":"Shorui ni sain shite kudasai.","german":"Please sign the document.","furigana":"しょるい"},{"japanese":"書類をかばんに入れました。","romaji":"Shorui o kaban ni iremashita.","german":"I put the papers in my bag.","furigana":"しょるい|い"}],"notes":"書類 are documents or papers; the word can also refer to a single document. 書類にサインする means to sign the document. In 書類をかばんに入れる, on the other hand, 書類 is the object that you put in the bag.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 29"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"レストランの予約をしました。","romaji":"Resutoran no yoyaku o shimashita.","german":"I made a reservation at the restaurant.","furigana":"よやく"},{"japanese":"予約は必要ですか。","romaji":"Yoyaku wa hitsuyō desu ka.","german":"Is a reservation necessary?","furigana":"よやく|ひつよう"}],"notes":"予約 means a reservation, booking or appointment made in advance. As a noun it appears in 予約をする; with する alone it becomes 予約する. 予約は必要ですか asks whether a reservation is required. The context says what is to be booked.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 10"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"届を出してください。","romaji":"Todoke o dashite kudasai.","german":"Please submit the notification.","furigana":"とどけ|だ"},{"japanese":"届の書き方が分かりません。","romaji":"Todoke no kakikata ga wakarimasen.","german":"I don't know how to fill out the notification.","furigana":"とどけ|か|かた|わ"}],"notes":"届 is a written notification or report submitted to an office. The reading is とどけ; the spelling 届け is also encountered. 届を出す means \"to submit a notification\", and 届の書き方 refers to how one writes the notification.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"申込書に記入してください。","romaji":"Mōshikomisho ni kinyū shite kudasai.","german":"Please fill out the application form.","furigana":"もうしこみしょ|きにゅう"},{"japanese":"申込書は受付にあります。","romaji":"Mōshikomisho wa uketsuke ni arimasu.","german":"The application forms are at the reception desk.","furigana":"もうしこみしょ|うけつけ"}],"notes":"申込書 is an application or registration form. In 申込書に記入する, に marks the form into which details are entered. You can also ask for the form itself with 申込書はどこにありますか. The ending 書 is read しょ here.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お釣りをください。","romaji":"Otsuri o kudasai.","german":"Please give me my change.","furigana":"つ"},{"japanese":"お釣りは三百円です。","romaji":"Otsuri wa sanbyaku-en desu.","german":"The change is 300 yen.","furigana":"つ|さんびゃくえん"}],"notes":"お釣り is the change you get back after paying. It does not simply mean coins in a wallet. The お is the polite prefix and belongs to the form on this card. お釣りは三百円です states the amount to be returned.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 14"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"値段はいくらですか。","romaji":"Nedan wa ikura desu ka.","german":"How much is it?","furigana":"ねだん"},{"japanese":"この値段は高いですね。","romaji":"Kono nedan wa takai desu ne.","german":"This price is high, isn't it?","furigana":"ねだん|たか"}],"notes":"値段 refers to the price of something. 値段はいくらですか asks for the amount. With 値段が高い or 値段が安い you judge the price as high or low; 高い with 値段 does not mean spatial height. The kana spelling ねだん also means this price.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 28"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"割引はありますか。","romaji":"Waribiki wa arimasu ka.","german":"Is there a discount?","furigana":"わりびき"},{"japanese":"割引で安く買えました。","romaji":"Waribiki de yasuku kaemashita.","german":"Thanks to the discount, I was able to buy it cheaply.","furigana":"わりびき|やす|か"}],"notes":"割引 is a discount or price reduction. 割引はありますか asks whether there is a reduction. In 割引で安く買えました, で names the discount as the reason for being able to buy cheaply; 買えました is the potential form of 買う. 割引き is another spelling of the noun \"discount\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 33"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"プレゼントの包装をお願いします。","romaji":"Purezento no hōsō o onegai shimasu.","german":"Please wrap the present.","furigana":"ほうそう|ねが"},{"japanese":"包装がきれいですね。","romaji":"Hōsō ga kirei desu ne.","german":"The wrapping is pretty, isn't it?","furigana":"ほうそう"}],"notes":"包装 refers to wrapping something or to the packaging of a thing. 包装する means \"to wrap\"; in a shop you can say 包装してください. When you say 包装がきれいです, you are commenting on the packaging. Both o in hōsō are long.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"品物を選びます。","romaji":"Shinamono o erabimasu.","german":"I choose the goods.","furigana":"しなもの|えら"},{"japanese":"この品物はいくらですか。","romaji":"Kono shinamono wa ikura desu ka.","german":"How much is this article?","furigana":"しなもの"}],"notes":"品物 is an object or article, often as goods when shopping. 品物を選ぶ describes choosing; この品物は… makes a particular article the topic of a question. The word can refer to a single item as well as to goods in general.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 28"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日は曇りです。","romaji":"Kyō wa kumori desu.","german":"It is cloudy today.","furigana":"きょう|くも"},{"japanese":"曇りの日は、公園を散歩します。","romaji":"Kumori no hi wa, kōen o sanpo shimasu.","german":"On cloudy days I take a walk in the park.","furigana":"くも|ひ|こうえん|さんぽ"}],"notes":"曇り is the noun for cloudy weather. That is why a weather statement uses です: 今日は曇りです. Before another noun it is joined with の, as in 曇りの日. The verb form 曇る, by contrast, means \"to become cloudy\". 曇 is also listed for this reading; the fuller spelling 曇り shows the noun clearly.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 12"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"明日は晴れです。","romaji":"Ashita wa hare desu.","german":"It will be sunny tomorrow.","furigana":"あした|は"},{"japanese":"晴れの日に洗濯をします。","romaji":"Hare no hi ni sentaku o shimasu.","german":"On sunny days I do laundry.","furigana":"は|ひ|せんたく"}],"notes":"晴れ is the noun for clear, fine weather. 明日は晴れです is a weather statement for tomorrow. With 晴れの日に you name a day with this weather as the time of an activity. In this meaning, 晴れ is joined with の, not with な.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 12"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"台風が来ます。","romaji":"Taifū ga kimasu.","german":"A typhoon is coming.","furigana":"たいふう|き"},{"japanese":"台風で学校が休みになりました。","romaji":"Taifū de gakkō ga yasumi ni narimashita.","german":"School was canceled because of the typhoon.","furigana":"たいふう|がっこう|やす"}],"notes":"台風 is a typhoon. 台風が来る speaks of its approach. With 台風で you name it as the cause of a consequence, such as a school closure. In taifū the u is long; the word refers to the cyclone, not simply to any strong wind.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 39"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"梅雨の季節です。","romaji":"Tsuyu no kisetsu desu.","german":"It is the rainy season.","furigana":"つゆ|きせつ"},{"japanese":"梅雨には洗濯物がなかなか乾きません。","romaji":"Tsuyu ni wa sentakumono ga nakanaka kawakimasen.","german":"During the rainy season, laundry hardly dries.","furigana":"つゆ|せんたくもの|かわ"}],"notes":"梅雨 is read つゆ and means the East Asian rainy season, not each individual rainy day. 梅雨の季節 means \"the season of the rainy season\". 梅雨には sets this period as the frame for a statement, for example that laundry hardly dries.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どの季節が好きですか。","romaji":"Dono kisetsu ga suki desu ka.","german":"Which season do you like?","furigana":"きせつ|す"},{"japanese":"日本には四つの季節があります。","romaji":"Nihon ni wa yottsu no kisetsu ga arimasu.","german":"There are four seasons in Japan.","furigana":"にほん|よっ|きせつ"}],"notes":"季節 is a season or a period of the year. どの季節 asks which one out of several is meant. For four seasons use 四つの季節; 四つ is read よっつ. With 季節の you can assign other things to the respective season.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 12"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"酢を少し入れます。","romaji":"Su o sukoshi iremasu.","german":"I add a little vinegar.","furigana":"す|すこ|い"},{"japanese":"酢は棚の上にあります。","romaji":"Su wa tana no ue ni arimasu.","german":"The vinegar is on top of the shelf.","furigana":"す|たな|うえ"}],"notes":"酢 is vinegar as an ingredient. In 酢を少し入れる, 酢 is what is added, and 少し describes the small amount. When talking about where it is kept, a place takes に and あります. The short reading す consists of only one mora.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"小麦粉でパンを作ります。","romaji":"Komugiko de pan o tsukurimasu.","german":"I make bread with wheat flour.","furigana":"こむぎこ|つく"},{"japanese":"小麦粉を一袋買いました。","romaji":"Komugiko o hitofukuro kaimashita.","german":"I bought a bag of wheat flour.","furigana":"こむぎこ|ひとふくろか"}],"notes":"小麦粉 is wheat flour. With 小麦粉で you name it as the material something is made from. When shopping, the amount can be given with 袋: 一袋 is read ひとふくろ and means \"one bag\". The whole word is read こむぎこ.","levelNote":"Compound: 小麦 (N3) + 粉 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ケーキを食べましょう。","romaji":"Kēki o tabemashō.","german":"Let's eat cake.","furigana":"た"},{"japanese":"誕生日にケーキを作りました。","romaji":"Tanjōbi ni kēki o tsukurimashita.","german":"I made a cake for his/her birthday.","furigana":"たんじょうび|つく"}],"notes":"ケーキ is cake; the exact kind depends on the context. The ー makes the e long: kēki. ケーキを食べましょう invites people to eat together; 誕生日にケーキを作る describes making a cake for a birthday.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 19"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"味噌汁が温かいです。","romaji":"Misoshiru ga atatakai desu.","german":"The miso soup is warm.","furigana":"みそしる|あたた"},{"japanese":"毎朝味噌汁を飲みます。","romaji":"Maiasa misoshiru o nomimasu.","german":"I drink miso soup every morning.","furigana":"まいあさみそしる|の"}],"notes":"味噌汁 is soup made with miso, that is, fermented soybean paste. For drinking or eating this soup, 飲む is often used. 味噌汁が温かい comments on its temperature; 毎朝味噌汁を飲む describes the habit of having it in the morning.","levelNote":"Compound: 味噌 (N4) + 汁 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"おかずを作りました。","romaji":"Okazu o tsukurimashita.","german":"I made side dishes to go with the rice.","furigana":"つく"},{"japanese":"今日のおかずは何ですか。","romaji":"Kyō no okazu wa nan desu ka.","german":"What is today's side dish?","furigana":"きょう|なん"}],"notes":"おかず are dishes eaten with rice, such as vegetables, fish or meat. The word can mean one such dish or several side dishes together. 今日のおかずは何ですか asks about the side dish of the day; before です, 何 is read なん here. お菜 is a rare kanji spelling; 御数 is listed in the dictionary only as a search form. For writing, the usual kana form おかず is suitable.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"飴をなめます。","romaji":"Ame o namemasu.","german":"I suck on a candy.","furigana":"あめ"},{"japanese":"子供に飴をあげました。","romaji":"Kodomo ni ame o agemashita.","german":"I gave the child a candy.","furigana":"こども|あめ"}],"notes":"飴 is a hard candy that you can suck on. 飴をなめる means \"to suck on a candy\". In 子供に飴をあげる, に marks the person who receives the candy. The same reading あめ also belongs to 雨 \"rain\"; the script and the context tell the words apart.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"煎餅を食べます。","romaji":"Senbei o tabemasu.","german":"I eat rice crackers.","furigana":"せんべい|た"},{"japanese":"煎餅をお土産に買いました。","romaji":"Senbei o omiyage ni kaimashita.","german":"I bought rice crackers as a souvenir.","furigana":"せんべい|みやげ|か"}],"notes":"煎餅 are Japanese rice crackers; the hiragana spelling せんべい is also often seen. 煎餅を食べる describes eating them, 煎餅をお土産に買う describes buying them as a souvenir. The に after お土産 names the purpose of the purchase.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お正月に餅を食べます。","romaji":"Oshōgatsu ni mochi o tabemasu.","german":"People eat mochi at New Year.","furigana":"しょうがつ|もち|た"},{"japanese":"焼いた餅に醤油をつけて食べます。","romaji":"Yaita mochi ni shōyu o tsukete tabemasu.","german":"I eat grilled mochi with soy sauce.","furigana":"や|もち|しょうゆ|た"}],"notes":"餅 is mochi, a small rice cake made from glutinous rice. お正月に names the New Year festival as the occasion for eating it. In 焼いた餅, 焼いた describes how the mochi is prepared; 醤油をつけて食べる means to eat it with soy sauce.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"林檎を一つください。","romaji":"Ringo o hitotsu kudasai.","german":"One apple, please.","furigana":"りんご|ひと"},{"japanese":"林檎は赤いです。","romaji":"Ringo wa akai desu.","german":"The apple is red.","furigana":"りんご|あか"}],"notes":"林檎 is the apple as a fruit; the word is often written りんご. You can count an apple with 一つ: 林檎を一つください. 赤い describes the color of the fruit under discussion; the reading of the whole word remains りんご.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"冬は蜜柑がおいしいです。","romaji":"Fuyu wa mikan ga oishii desu.","german":"Mandarin oranges are delicious in winter.","furigana":"ふゆ|みかん"},{"japanese":"蜜柑の皮をむきました。","romaji":"Mikan no kawa o mukimashita.","german":"I peeled the mandarin orange.","furigana":"みかん|かわ"}],"notes":"蜜柑 are mandarin oranges, especially the satsuma mandarins common in Japan. The common spelling みかん reads the same. 蜜柑の皮 refers to the peel; 皮をむく means to peel the fruit. The word 蜜柑 alone does not fix a number of pieces.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"苺が好きです。","romaji":"Ichigo ga suki desu.","german":"I like strawberries.","furigana":"いちご|す"},{"japanese":"苺のケーキを食べたいです。","romaji":"Ichigo no kēki o tabetai desu.","german":"I want to eat strawberry cake.","furigana":"いちご|た"}],"notes":"苺 means strawberry; いちご is often written instead. In 苺が好き, 苺 is what one likes. In 苺のケーキ, の connects the strawberries with a cake; 食べたい expresses the wish to eat it.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"夏は西瓜を食べます。","romaji":"Natsu wa suika o tabemasu.","german":"I eat watermelon in summer.","furigana":"なつ|すいか|た"},{"japanese":"西瓜を半分に切りました。","romaji":"Suika o hanbun ni kirimashita.","german":"I cut the watermelon in half.","furigana":"すいか|はんぶん|き"}],"notes":"西瓜 is a watermelon; すいか or スイカ is often seen. In 夏は西瓜を食べる, 夏 is the time frame. 西瓜を半分に切る, on the other hand, describes preparation: the watermelon is divided into two halves.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"葡萄を買いました。","romaji":"Budō o kaimashita.","german":"I bought grapes.","furigana":"ぶどう|か"},{"japanese":"秋には葡萄がおいしいです。","romaji":"Aki ni wa budō ga oishii desu.","german":"Grapes are delicious in autumn.","furigana":"あき|ぶどう"}],"notes":"葡萄 are grapes; the common kana spelling is ぶどう. The last o in budō is long. 葡萄を買う describes shopping, and 葡萄がおいしい is a judgment of taste. Whether it means one bunch or several comes from context.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"桃がおいしいです。","romaji":"Momo ga oishii desu.","german":"The peach is delicious.","furigana":"もも"},{"japanese":"桃の皮をむいてください。","romaji":"Momo no kawa o muite kudasai.","german":"Please peel the peach.","furigana":"もも|かわ"}],"notes":"桃 is a peach. 桃がおいしい describes its taste. With 桃の皮 you name the skin of the fruit; 桃の皮をむいてください asks someone to peel the peach. Both syllables in もも are short.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"梨の季節です。","romaji":"Nashi no kisetsu desu.","german":"It is nashi pear season.","furigana":"なし|きせつ"},{"japanese":"この梨はとても大きいです。","romaji":"Kono nashi wa totemo ōkii desu.","german":"This nashi pear is very big.","furigana":"なし|おお"}],"notes":"梨 is the nashi pear, an Asian pear; it is often written なし. 梨の季節 means \"pear season\". With この梨は… you describe a specific fruit, for example its size. The identically pronounced なし meaning \"without/not existing\" is a different word.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人参を切ります。","romaji":"Ninjin o kirimasu.","german":"I cut the carrot.","furigana":"にんじん|き"},{"japanese":"カレーに人参を入れます。","romaji":"Karē ni ninjin o iremasu.","german":"I put carrots in the curry.","furigana":"にんじん|い"}],"notes":"人参 means carrot here and is read にんじん; you also often see にんじん or ニンジン. When cutting, the carrot takes を. In カレーに人参を入れる, に names the dish into which the carrot goes as an ingredient.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"玉葱を炒めます。","romaji":"Tamanegi o itamemasu.","german":"I sauté the onion.","furigana":"たまねぎ|いた"},{"japanese":"玉葱を切ると涙が出ます。","romaji":"Tamanegi o kiru to namida ga demasu.","german":"When you cut onions, tears come to your eyes.","furigana":"たまねぎ|き|なみだ|で"}],"notes":"玉葱 is an onion; the reading is たまねぎ. 玉葱を炒める means to sauté onions. In 玉葱を切ると涙が出る, と links the cutting to the reaction that follows: your eyes water.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 34"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大根おろしを作ります。","romaji":"Daikon oroshi o tsukurimasu.","german":"I make grated daikon.","furigana":"だいこん|つく"},{"japanese":"大根を一本買いました。","romaji":"Daikon o ippon kaimashita.","german":"I bought a daikon radish.","furigana":"だいこん|いっぽんか"}],"notes":"大根 is the large white daikon radish. 大根おろし is grated daikon. When shopping, the long root is often counted with 本: 一本 is read いっぽん. In the plain word 大根, the reading is だいこん.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"茄子を焼きます。","romaji":"Nasu o yakimasu.","german":"I grill the eggplant.","furigana":"なす|や"},{"japanese":"茄子の天ぷらが好きです。","romaji":"Nasu no tenpura ga suki desu.","german":"I like eggplant tempura.","furigana":"なす|てん|す"}],"notes":"茄子 is an eggplant; the common kana spelling is なす. 茄子を焼く describes preparing it by pan-frying or grilling. In 茄子の天ぷら, の connects the eggplant with the dish tempura. In Modified Hepburn, tenpura is written with n before p.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"胡瓜のサラダを作ります。","romaji":"Kyūri no sarada o tsukurimasu.","german":"I make cucumber salad.","furigana":"きゅうり|つく"},{"japanese":"胡瓜を二本買いました。","romaji":"Kyūri o nihon kaimashita.","german":"I bought two cucumbers.","furigana":"きゅうり|にほんか"}],"notes":"胡瓜 means cucumber; きゅうり or キュウリ is often used instead. The u in kyūri is long. 胡瓜のサラダ is cucumber salad. When shopping, cucumbers can be counted with 本 as long objects: 二本 is read にほん.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"豆腐を買います。","romaji":"Tōfu o kaimasu.","german":"I buy tofu.","furigana":"とうふ|か"},{"japanese":"味噌汁に豆腐を入れます。","romaji":"Misoshiru ni tōfu o iremasu.","german":"I put tofu in the miso soup.","furigana":"みそしる|とうふ|い"}],"notes":"豆腐 is tofu. In tōfu the first o is pronounced long. 豆腐を買う describes shopping; 味噌汁に豆腐を入れる names tofu as an ingredient of miso soup. Here 豆腐 is the object with を and 味噌汁 is the receiving dish with に.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"海苔でおにぎりを包みます。","romaji":"Nori de onigiri o tsutsumimasu.","german":"I wrap the onigiri in nori.","furigana":"のり|つつ"},{"japanese":"海苔を細く切りました。","romaji":"Nori o hosoku kirimashita.","german":"I cut the nori into thin strips.","furigana":"のり|ほそ|き"}],"notes":"海苔 means nori, edible seaweed often used as thin sheets. 海苔でおにぎりを包む names nori as the material for wrapping. 海苔を細く切る describes cutting it into thin strips; を then marks nori as the object being worked on.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"南瓜の煮物を作ります。","romaji":"Kabocha no nimono o tsukurimasu.","german":"I make simmered pumpkin.","furigana":"かぼちゃ|にもの|つく"},{"japanese":"この南瓜は重いです。","romaji":"Kono kabocha wa omoi desu.","german":"This pumpkin is heavy.","furigana":"かぼちゃ|おも"}],"notes":"南瓜 is a pumpkin; the reading かぼちゃ cannot be derived from the individual characters. It is often written かぼちゃ or カボチャ. 南瓜の煮物 is a dish of cooked pumpkin. With この南瓜は... you describe a particular fruit, for example its weight.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"白菜を鍋に入れます。","romaji":"Hakusai o nabe ni iremasu.","german":"I put napa cabbage in the pot.","furigana":"はくさい|なべ|い"},{"japanese":"白菜は冷蔵庫にあります。","romaji":"Hakusai wa reizōko ni arimasu.","german":"The napa cabbage is in the refrigerator.","furigana":"はくさい|れいぞうこ"}],"notes":"白菜 is napa cabbage and is read はくさい. 白菜を鍋に入れる describes its use in a hot pot dish. When storage is the point, 冷蔵庫に names the place; あります says the napa cabbage is there.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"葱を刻みます。","romaji":"Negi o kizamimasu.","german":"I chop the green onions.","furigana":"ねぎ|きざ"},{"japanese":"ラーメンに葱をのせます。","romaji":"Rāmen ni negi o nosemasu.","german":"I put green onions on the ramen.","furigana":"ねぎ"}],"notes":"葱 is a green onion or spring onion, often also written ねぎ. 葱を刻む describes fine slicing or chopping. In ラーメンに葱をのせる, the green onion is the garnish placed on the ramen.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"蓮根を薄く切ります。","romaji":"Renkon o usuku kirimasu.","german":"I slice the lotus root thinly.","furigana":"れんこん|うす|き"},{"japanese":"蓮根の天ぷらがおいしいです。","romaji":"Renkon no tenpura ga oishii desu.","german":"Lotus root tempura is delicious.","furigana":"れんこん|てん"}],"notes":"蓮根 is lotus root as a vegetable; れんこん is often written. The reading ends in ん. 蓮根を薄く切る describes thin slices, 蓮根の天ぷら is a tempura dish made with lotus root. In Hepburn the n remains visible in renkon and tenpura.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"牛蒡をサラダにします。","romaji":"Gobō o sarada ni shimasu.","german":"I make a salad with burdock root.","furigana":"ごぼう"},{"japanese":"牛蒡はどこで買えますか。","romaji":"Gobō wa doko de kaemasu ka.","german":"Where can I buy burdock root?","furigana":"ごぼう|か"}],"notes":"牛蒡 is gobo, burdock root as a vegetable; ごぼう is often written. The final o of gobō is long. 牛蒡をサラダにする describes preparing it as a salad. 牛蒡はどこで買えますか asks where it can be bought.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"椎茸を焼きます。","romaji":"Shiitake o yakimasu.","german":"I grill shiitake mushrooms.","furigana":"しいたけ|や"},{"japanese":"スーパーで椎茸を買いました。","romaji":"Sūpā de shiitake o kaimashita.","german":"I bought shiitake mushrooms at the supermarket.","furigana":"しいたけ|か"}],"notes":"椎茸 is the shiitake mushroom; the reading is しいたけ, with a long i at the start. For shopping, 椎茸を買う is used, for preparation 椎茸を焼く. When buying, で names the place, for example スーパーで for \"at the supermarket\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鍋に水を入れます。","romaji":"Nabe ni mizu o iremasu.","german":"I put water in the pot.","furigana":"なべ|みず|い"},{"japanese":"家族と鍋を食べました。","romaji":"Kazoku to nabe o tabemashita.","german":"I ate a Japanese hot pot with my family.","furigana":"かぞく|なべ|た"}],"notes":"鍋 can mean the cooking pot or a Japanese stew-style dish prepared in it. In 鍋に水を入れる the vessel is meant. With 鍋を食べる you eat the dish, not the pot. 鍋料理 is the fuller term for such a dish.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"フライパンを洗いました。","romaji":"Furaipan o araimashita.","german":"I washed the frying pan.","furigana":"あら"},{"japanese":"フライパンで卵を焼きます。","romaji":"Furaipan de tamago o yakimasu.","german":"I fry an egg in the frying pan.","furigana":"たまご|や"}],"notes":"フライパン is a frying pan. フライパンで names the cooking utensil used for frying. When you clean the pan itself, it takes を: フライパンを洗う. The word is written entirely in katakana.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"まな板を買いました。","romaji":"Manaita o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a cutting board.","furigana":"いた|か"},{"japanese":"まな板の上で野菜を切ります。","romaji":"Manaita no ue de yasai o kirimasu.","german":"I cut vegetables on the cutting board.","furigana":"いた|うえ|やさい|き"}],"notes":"まな板 is a cutting board; 板 is read いた here. まな板の上で names the work surface on which you cut. With まな板を買う, by contrast, the board itself is the object of the purchase. The reading is まないた, with no additional consonant between な and い.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"包丁で切ります。","romaji":"Hōchō de kirimasu.","german":"I cut with the kitchen knife.","furigana":"ほうちょう|き"},{"japanese":"包丁で魚を切ります。","romaji":"Hōchō de sakana o kirimasu.","german":"I cut the fish with the kitchen knife.","furigana":"ほうちょう|さかな|き"},{"japanese":"料理が終わったら、包丁を洗って片付けます。","romaji":"Ryōri ga owattara, hōchō o aratte katazukemasu.","german":"When I'm done cooking, I wash the kitchen knife and put it away.","furigana":"りょうり|お|ほうちょう|あら|かたづ"}],"notes":"包丁 is a kitchen knife for cutting and preparing food. で names it as the tool, を the thing cut. 包丁を洗う, on the other hand, describes cleaning the knife itself. The reading ほうちょう contains two long o: hōchō. The knife is meant here, not the older meaning \"cooking\". 庖丁 is also listed as a spelling for this kitchen knife; the reading stays ほうちょう.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お玉でスープをすくいます。","romaji":"Otama de sūpu o sukuimasu.","german":"I scoop soup with the ladle.","furigana":"たま"},{"japanese":"お玉でスープをよそいます。","romaji":"Otama de sūpu o yosoimasu.","german":"I serve soup with the ladle.","furigana":"たま"},{"japanese":"使ったお玉を洗って、引き出しにしまいました。","romaji":"Tsukatta otama o aratte, hikidashi ni shimaimashita.","german":"I washed the ladle I used and put it in the drawer.","furigana":"つか|たま|あら|ひ|だ"}],"notes":"お玉 is a ladle for portioning soup or other liquid food. With で you name it as the tool: お玉でスープをすくう. お玉を洗う makes the ladle itself the object. The word is often written in kana as おたま. In these examples the kitchen tool is meant, not a personal name or the also attested meaning \"egg\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"栓抜きはありますか。","romaji":"Sennuki wa arimasu ka.","german":"Do you have a bottle opener?","furigana":"せんぬ"},{"japanese":"栓抜きでビンを開けます。","romaji":"Sennuki de bin o akemasu.","german":"I open the bottle with a bottle opener.","furigana":"せんぬ|あ"}],"notes":"栓抜き is a bottle opener. ありますか asks whether one is available; で names it as the tool for opening a bottle. The reading せんぬき has an n at the end of the first syllable and an n at the start of the next: sennuki. Before this n, Hepburn needs no separating apostrophe, unlike before a vowel.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 42"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"蛇口を閉めてください。","romaji":"Jaguchi o shimete kudasai.","german":"Please turn off the faucet.","furigana":"じゃぐち|し"},{"japanese":"蛇口から水が出ません。","romaji":"Jaguchi kara mizu ga demasen.","german":"No water comes out of the faucet.","furigana":"じゃぐち|みず|で"}],"notes":"蛇口 is the faucet. 蛇口を閉める means to turn it off; から names the starting point of the water. 蛇口から水が出ない therefore describes that no water comes out of the tap. The word is read as a whole as じゃぐち; the literal meaning of the individual kanji does not reliably explain the present reading.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"電球を替えます。","romaji":"Denkyū o kaemasu.","german":"I change the light bulb.","furigana":"でんきゅう|か"},{"japanese":"電球が切れました。","romaji":"Denkyū ga kiremashita.","german":"The light bulb has burned out.","furigana":"でんきゅう|き"}],"notes":"電球 is a light bulb. 電球を替える means to replace the bulb. 電球が切れる is the usual expression for it no longer lighting up, that is, having burned out. を marks the thing being replaced; が marks the bulb in which the failure occurs. The reading ends with a long ū: denkyū.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コンセントに差してください。","romaji":"Konsento ni sashite kudasai.","german":"Please plug it into the outlet.","furigana":"さ"},{"japanese":"この部屋にはコンセントが二つしかありません。","romaji":"Kono heya ni wa konsento ga futatsu shika arimasen.","german":"There are only two outlets in this room.","furigana":"へや|ふた"}],"notes":"コンセント is the word for an electrical outlet in Japanese. に names the target when plugging in a plug; the thing being plugged in can be omitted in conversation. コンセントが二つしかない means \"there are only two outlets\". It is not the English \"consent\" meaning agreement. For the plug itself, プラグ is used, for example.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"スイッチを入れてください。","romaji":"Suitchi o irete kudasai.","german":"Please turn it on.","furigana":"い"},{"japanese":"スイッチを押してください。","romaji":"Suitchi o oshite kudasai.","german":"Please press the switch.","furigana":"お"},{"japanese":"このスイッチは壊れていて、押しても電気がつきません。","romaji":"Kono suitchi wa kowarete ite, oshite mo denki ga tsukimasen.","german":"This switch is broken; the light doesn't come on even when I press it.","furigana":"こわ|お|でんき"}],"notes":"スイッチ is a switch for operating a device here. スイッチを入れる means \"to turn on\", スイッチを切る \"to turn off\". 押す describes pressing; not every switch is operated by pressing. In these examples the word does not mean a change between topics or a game console.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 10"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"掃除機をかけます。","romaji":"Sōjiki o kakemasu.","german":"I vacuum.","furigana":"そうじき"},{"japanese":"掃除機で部屋を掃除します。","romaji":"Sōjiki de heya o sōji shimasu.","german":"I clean the room with the vacuum cleaner.","furigana":"そうじき|へや|そうじ"},{"japanese":"掃除機の音が大きくて、電話の声が聞こえません。","romaji":"Sōjiki no oto ga ōkikute, denwa no koe ga kikoemasen.","german":"The vacuum cleaner is so loud that I can't hear the voice on the phone.","furigana":"そうじき|おと|おお|でんわ|こえ|き"}],"notes":"掃除機 is the vacuum cleaner. 掃除機をかける is the set expression for \"to vacuum\". 掃除機で掃除する names the appliance with で as the means. The word contains 掃除 \"cleaning\" and 機 \"machine\", but is read as the name of the appliance as a whole. The first syllable is long: sōjiki.","levelNote":"Compound: 掃除 (N5) + 機 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"アイロンをかけます。","romaji":"Airon o kakemasu.","german":"I do the ironing."},{"japanese":"シャツにアイロンをかけます。","romaji":"Shatsu ni airon o kakemasu.","german":"I iron the shirt."},{"japanese":"アイロンが冷めてから、箱に入れました。","romaji":"Airon ga samete kara, hako ni iremashita.","german":"After the iron had cooled down, I put it in the box.","furigana":"さ|はこ|い"}],"notes":"アイロン here is the iron for clothes. アイロンをかける means \"to iron\"; the garment can come before it with に, as in シャツにアイロンをかける. The word refers to the appliance and not to the metal iron in general. The combination シャツをかける alone can mean \"to hang up a shirt\" and is inaccurate for \"to iron\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"電子レンジで温めます。","romaji":"Denshi renji de atatamemasu.","german":"I heat it in the microwave.","furigana":"でんし|あたた"},{"japanese":"電子レンジで温めてください。","romaji":"Denshi renji de atatamete kudasai.","german":"Please heat it in the microwave.","furigana":"でんし|あたた"},{"japanese":"電子レンジの中に、お皿を忘れました。","romaji":"Denshi renji no naka ni, osara o wasuremashita.","german":"I left a plate in the microwave.","furigana":"でんし|なか|さら|わす"}],"notes":"電子レンジ is the microwave oven. With で you name it as the appliance or place of heating: 電子レンジで温める. In suitable conversation it is often shortened to レンジ. The examples do not say that you use an oven for baking. 電子レンジの中 means the interior of the appliance.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"炊飯器でご飯を炊きます。","romaji":"Suihanki de gohan o takimasu.","german":"I cook rice in the rice cooker.","furigana":"すいはんき|はん|た"},{"japanese":"新しい炊飯器だと、ご飯がとてもおいしく炊けます。","romaji":"Atarashii suihanki da to, gohan ga totemo oishiku takemasu.","german":"With the new rice cooker, the rice turns out very delicious.","furigana":"あたら|すいはんき|はん|た"}],"notes":"炊飯器 is a rice cooker. 炊飯器でご飯を炊く names the appliance as the means for cooking rice. 炊ける can describe a possible or successful cooking result; ご飯が炊ける makes the rice the subject. 炊飯器 refers to the appliance, not to a pot for just any dish. The reading is suihanki.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 35"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"物干しに洗濯物を干します。","romaji":"Monohoshi ni sentakumono o hoshimasu.","german":"I hang the laundry on the rack.","furigana":"ものほ|せんたくもの|ほ"},{"japanese":"雨が降ってきたので、物干しの洗濯物を中に入れました。","romaji":"Ame ga futte kita node, monohoshi no sentakumono o naka ni iremashita.","german":"Because it started to rain, I brought the laundry in from the drying rack.","furigana":"あめ|ふ|ものほ|せんたくもの|なか|い"}],"notes":"物干し is a device for drying laundry, such as a drying rack. に names the place where the laundry is hung. 物干しの洗濯物 means the laundry hanging there. 物干し is the device; 洗濯物 is what is being dried. The term does not always have to mean a freestanding folding rack.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"洗面台で顔を洗います。","romaji":"Senmendai de kao o araimasu.","german":"I wash my face at the washbasin.","furigana":"せんめんだい|かお|あら"},{"japanese":"洗面台の前で歯を磨きます。","romaji":"Senmendai no mae de ha o migakimasu.","german":"I brush my teeth in front of the washbasin.","furigana":"せんめんだい|まえ|は|みが"}],"notes":"洗面台 is the washing area with a basin, often also with a shelf or mirror. 洗面台で顔を洗う names it as the place where the face is washed. 洗面台の前で describes the spot in front of it. It is not the kitchen sink where dishes are usually washed. The whole word is read senmendai.","levelNote":"Compound: 洗面 (N2) + 台 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"浴槽にお湯を入れます。","romaji":"Yokusō ni oyu o iremasu.","german":"I fill the bathtub with hot water.","furigana":"よくそう|ゆ|い"},{"japanese":"浴槽は毎日洗ったほうがいいです。","romaji":"Yokusō wa mainichi aratta hō ga ii desu.","german":"You should wash the bathtub every day.","furigana":"よくそう|まいにちあら"}],"notes":"浴槽 is the bathtub as a container, not the bathroom as a room. 浴槽にお湯を入れる means to run hot water into it. 浴槽を洗う means cleaning the tub. たほうがいい gives advice; the example sentence explicitly mentions daily cleaning, without that being part of the word's meaning.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"便器を掃除します。","romaji":"Benki o sōji shimasu.","german":"I clean the toilet bowl.","furigana":"べんき|そうじ"},{"japanese":"トイレの便器を掃除しました。","romaji":"Toire no benki o sōji shimashita.","german":"I cleaned the toilet bowl.","furigana":"べんき|そうじ"},{"japanese":"便器のふたを閉めてください。","romaji":"Benki no futa o shimete kudasai.","german":"Please close the toilet lid.","furigana":"べんき|し"}],"notes":"便器 refers to the toilet bowl, the sanitary fixture, not the whole toilet room. 便器を掃除する means to clean this fixture. 便器のふた is the toilet lid. トイレ is broader in everyday use and can mean either the toilet or the toilet room; 便器 names the bowl more precisely.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ベランダで花を育てます。","romaji":"Beranda de hana o sodatemasu.","german":"I grow flowers on the balcony.","furigana":"はな|そだ"},{"japanese":"ベランダで洗濯物を干します。","romaji":"Beranda de sentakumono o hoshimasu.","german":"I hang the laundry out on the balcony.","furigana":"せんたくもの|ほ"}],"notes":"ベランダ here means the balcony of an apartment. With で you name it as the place of an activity: tending flowers or hanging laundry. The word can also be used for a veranda; in the examples a balcony is meant. It describes the place, not automatically a garden. In the flower example, 育てる means tending and letting them grow.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"カーテンを開けてください。","romaji":"Kāten o akete kudasai.","german":"Bitte öffnen Sie den Vorhang.","furigana":"あ"},{"japanese":"新しい部屋に青いカーテンをつけました。","romaji":"Atarashii heya ni aoi kāten o tsukemashita.","german":"Im neuen Zimmer habe ich blaue Vorhänge aufgehängt.","furigana":"あたら|へや|あお"}],"notes":"カーテン ist ein Vorhang. カーテンを開ける heißt, ihn aufzuziehen; カーテンをつける, ihn anzubringen oder aufzuhängen. Die Farbe steht vor dem Nomen, etwa 青いカーテン. Auf Deutsch kann im zweiten Beispiel „Vorhänge“ passen, obwohl das japanische Nomen keine eigene Mehrzahlform hat. Die Länge wird kāten geschrieben.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 35"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"絨毯の上に座ります。","romaji":"Jūtan no ue ni suwarimasu.","german":"I sit on the carpet.","furigana":"じゅうたん|うえ|すわ"},{"japanese":"新しい絨毯を買いました。","romaji":"Atarashii jūtan o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a new carpet.","furigana":"あたら|じゅうたん|か"}],"notes":"絨毯 is a carpet, either covering a large area or as a single runner or rug. の上に names its top side as a place to sit. 絨毯を買う, on the other hand, concerns acquiring one. In everyday writing the kanji can also be replaced by the kana spelling じゅうたん. The long u is rendered as ū: jūtan.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"花瓶に花を活けます。","romaji":"Kabin ni hana o ikemasu.","german":"I arrange flowers in the vase.","furigana":"かびん|はな|い"},{"japanese":"花瓶に花を入れました。","romaji":"Kabin ni hana o iremashita.","german":"I put flowers in the vase.","furigana":"かびん|はな|い"},{"japanese":"花瓶が割れたので、新しい物を買いました。","romaji":"Kabin ga wareta node, atarashii mono o kaimashita.","german":"The vase broke, so I bought a new one.","furigana":"かびん|わ|あたら|もの|か"}],"notes":"花瓶 is a flower vase. 花瓶に花を活ける means to arrange flowers in it; 入れる is more general, \"to put in\". The vase takes に as the container, the flowers take を as the object. 花瓶が割れる, by contrast, describes the vase itself breaking. The spelling combines \"flower\" with \"bottle\", but the whole word is read kabin.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 30"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"目覚まし時計をセットします。","romaji":"Mezamashi dokei o setto shimasu.","german":"I set the alarm clock.","furigana":"めざ|どけい"},{"japanese":"目覚まし時計が鳴りました。","romaji":"Mezamashi dokei ga narimashita.","german":"The alarm clock rang.","furigana":"めざ|どけい|な"}],"notes":"目覚まし時計 is an alarm clock. をセットする means to set it to a wake-up time; が鳴る describes its ringing. In this compound, 時計 is read どけい, with d instead of t. The full reading is めざましどけい. The word refers to the device, not just the time of waking up.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 16"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"体温計で熱を測ります。","romaji":"Taionkei de netsu o hakarimasu.","german":"Ich messe mit dem Fieberthermometer meine Körpertemperatur.","furigana":"たいおんけい|ねつ|はか"},{"japanese":"すみません、体温計はどこにありますか。","romaji":"Sumimasen, taionkei wa doko ni arimasu ka.","german":"Entschuldigung, wo ist das Fieberthermometer?","furigana":"たいおんけい"}],"notes":"体温計 ist ein Thermometer für die Körpertemperatur, auf Deutsch oft „Fieberthermometer“. で nennt das Messgerät in 体温計で熱を測る. 熱を測る ist eine übliche Alltagsverbindung für das Temperaturmessen und bedeutet nicht, dass Fieber schon feststeht. どこにありますか fragt nach dem Aufbewahrungsort des Geräts.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 42"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"体重計に乗ります。","romaji":"Taijūkei ni norimasu.","german":"I step on the scale.","furigana":"たいじゅうけい|の"},{"japanese":"毎朝体重計に乗ります。","romaji":"Maiasa taijūkei ni norimasu.","german":"I step on the scale every morning.","furigana":"まいあさたいじゅうけい|の"},{"japanese":"体重計の数字が小さくて、よく見えません。","romaji":"Taijūkei no sūji ga chiisakute, yoku miemasen.","german":"The numbers on the scale are small, so I can't see them well.","furigana":"たいじゅうけい|すうじ|ちい|み"}],"notes":"体重計 is a scale for measuring body weight. 体重計に乗る means to stand on the scale; に marks the destination of the movement. 体重計の数字 means the numbers on the display; 見る can refer to reading that display. The word means the measuring device, not the weight itself. The reading is taijūkei.","levelNote":"Compound: 体重 (N3) + 計 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"毎日日記を書きます。","romaji":"Mainichi nikki o kakimasu.","german":"I write in my diary every day.","furigana":"まいにちにっき|か"},{"japanese":"毎日日記を書いています。","romaji":"Mainichi nikki o kaite imasu.","german":"I write in my diary every day.","furigana":"まいにちにっき|か"},{"japanese":"古い日記を読んで、学生のころを思い出しました。","romaji":"Furui nikki o yonde, gakusei no koro o omoidashimashita.","german":"Reading an old diary, I remembered my student days.","furigana":"ふる|にっき|よ|がくせい|おも|だ"}],"notes":"日記 is a diary or a personal daily record. 日記を書く means \"to write a diary\". 毎日 gives the frequency; 書いています can express an ongoing habit here. 古い日記を読む concerns rereading one's own memories later. The small っ in にっき is written as a double k: nikki.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 18"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"漫画を読むのが好きです。","romaji":"Manga o yomu no ga suki desu.","german":"I like reading manga.","furigana":"まんが|よ|す"},{"japanese":"日本の漫画は面白いです。","romaji":"Nihon no manga wa omoshiroi desu.","german":"Japanese manga are interesting.","furigana":"にほん|まんが|おもしろ"}],"notes":"漫画 are drawn stories, comics or manga. 漫画を読むのが好き means that you like reading them; の turns reading into an activity that you can evaluate. 日本の漫画 means manga from Japan. The word describes the medium and says nothing by itself about whether it is meant for children or adults.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 27"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"小説を読んでいます。","romaji":"Shōsetsu o yonde imasu.","german":"I'm reading a novel.","furigana":"しょうせつ|よ"},{"japanese":"この小説を読んでいます。","romaji":"Kono shōsetsu o yonde imasu.","german":"I'm reading this novel.","furigana":"しょうせつ|よ"},{"japanese":"友達は、仕事の後で短い小説を書いています。","romaji":"Tomodachi wa, shigoto no ato de mijikai shōsetsu o kaite imasu.","german":"After work, my friend is writing a short story.","furigana":"ともだち|しごと|あと|みじか|しょうせつ|か"}],"notes":"小説 is a literary narrative text, often a novel, but also a shorter story. It is not used only for a very long book. 小説を読む refers to reading; 小説を書く to writing such texts. 書いている can describe work currently in progress. The first syllable is long: shōsetsu.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 28"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"葉書を送ります。","romaji":"Hagaki o okurimasu.","german":"I send a postcard.","furigana":"はがき|おく"},{"japanese":"友達に葉書を送りました。","romaji":"Tomodachi ni hagaki o okurimashita.","german":"I sent my friend a postcard.","furigana":"ともだち|はがき|おく"},{"japanese":"葉書に住所を書いてから、切手を貼りました。","romaji":"Hagaki ni jūsho o kaite kara, kitte o harimashita.","german":"I wrote the address on the postcard and then put on a stamp.","furigana":"はがき|じゅうしょ|か|きって|は"}],"notes":"葉書 is the postcard. In everyday writing it is very often spelled はがき. 葉書を送る names the sending; に can give the recipient. 葉書に住所を書く, by contrast, makes the card the place of writing. Here the word does not mean a short note slip, even though that further meaning is in the dictionary.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"小包を送りたいです。","romaji":"Kozutsumi o okuritai desu.","german":"I want to send a parcel.","furigana":"こづつみ|おく"},{"japanese":"小包が届きました。","romaji":"Kozutsumi ga todokimashita.","german":"A parcel has arrived.","furigana":"こづつみ|とど"}],"notes":"小包 is a package or parcel for sending. Despite 小, it does not necessarily have to be a very small shipment. を marks the parcel being sent; が marks the parcel that arrives. The reading こづつみ is written kozutsumi in Hepburn. The づ becomes zu here, not dzu.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"印鑑を押してください。","romaji":"Inkan o oshite kudasai.","german":"Bitte stempeln Sie mit Ihrem Namensstempel.","furigana":"いんかん|お"},{"japanese":"書類に印鑑を押してください。","romaji":"Shorui ni inkan o oshite kudasai.","german":"Bitte bringen Sie Ihren Stempel auf dem Dokument an.","furigana":"しょるい|いんかん|お"},{"japanese":"印鑑を家に忘れたので、取りに帰りました。","romaji":"Inkan o ie ni wasureta node, tori ni kaerimashita.","german":"Weil ich meinen Namensstempel zu Hause vergessen hatte, bin ich zurückgegangen, um ihn zu holen.","furigana":"いんかん|いえ|わす|と|かえ"}],"notes":"印鑑 ist ein persönlicher Namensstempel beziehungsweise ein Siegel. 印鑑を押す heißt, einen Stempelabdruck anzubringen; auf Deutsch klingt „stempeln“ natürlicher als wörtliches „den Stempel drücken“. に nennt das Dokument, auf das gestempelt wird. Der Satz allein sagt nicht, dass ein bestimmtes Formular rechtlich immer einen Stempel verlangt.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"両替をお願いします。","romaji":"Ryōgae o onegai shimasu.","german":"Please change this money.","furigana":"りょうがえ|ねが"},{"japanese":"銀行で両替をしました。","romaji":"Ginkō de ryōgae o shimashita.","german":"I exchanged money at the bank.","furigana":"ぎんこう|りょうがえ"},{"japanese":"千円札を百円玉に両替しました。","romaji":"Sen'en-satsu o hyakuen-dama ni ryōgae shimashita.","german":"I changed a thousand-yen bill into hundred-yen coins.","furigana":"せんえんさつ|ひゃくえんだま|りょうがえ"}],"notes":"両替 means \"money exchange\". It can refer to exchange between currencies or into other bills and coins of the same currency. 両替する is the verb for it. 両替をお願いします is a polite request for this service. For paying for a purchase you don't simply use 両替する.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お守りを買いました。","romaji":"Omamori o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a lucky charm.","furigana":"まも|か"},{"japanese":"神社でお守りを買いました。","romaji":"Jinja de omamori o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a lucky charm at the shrine.","furigana":"じんじゃ|まも|か"},{"japanese":"旅行のときは、このお守りを持ち歩いています。","romaji":"Ryokō no toki wa, kono omamori o mochiaruite imasu.","german":"When I travel, I carry this lucky charm with me.","furigana":"りょこう|まも|も|ある"}],"notes":"お守り is an amulet or lucky charm meant to symbolize protection or luck. You receive or buy such items at a shrine, for example. お守りを持ち歩く means to carry it with you. The word's meaning describes the object and its attributed function, not a proven protective effect.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"絵葉書を送りました。","romaji":"Ehagaki o okurimashita.","german":"I sent a picture postcard.","furigana":"えはがき|おく"},{"japanese":"旅行先から絵葉書を送りました。","romaji":"Ryokōsaki kara ehagaki o okurimashita.","german":"I sent a picture postcard from my travel destination.","furigana":"りょこうさき|えはがき|おく"},{"japanese":"この絵葉書の写真を見ると、旅行を思い出します。","romaji":"Kono ehagaki no shashin o miru to, ryokō o omoidasu.","german":"When I see the photo on this picture postcard, I remember the trip."}],"notes":"絵葉書 is a picture postcard with an image on it. It is a special kind of 葉書, but an ordinary postcard need not have such a picture. を marks the card being sent; から can name the place you send it from. 絵葉書の写真, by contrast, refers to the picture on the card. The reading is ehagaki.","levelNote":"Compound: 絵 (N5) + 葉書 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自動販売機でジュースを買います。","romaji":"Jidō hanbaiki de jūsu o kaimasu.","german":"I buy juice from the vending machine.","furigana":"じどうはんばいき|か"},{"japanese":"自動販売機で飲み物を買いました。","romaji":"Jidō hanbaiki de nomimono o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a drink from the vending machine.","furigana":"じどうはんばいき|の|もの|か"},{"japanese":"自動販売機のおつりを取り忘れました。","romaji":"Jidō hanbaiki no otsuri o toriwasuremashita.","german":"I forgot to take my change from the vending machine.","furigana":"じどうはんばいき|と|わす"}],"notes":"自動販売機 is a vending machine. It often sells drinks, but the word is not limited to juice or drinks. で names the machine as the place of purchase. 自動販売機のおつり means the change from the machine. The full reading is じどうはんばいき; the first o is long.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大きいブランケットをロフトに上げる。","romaji":"Ōkii buranketto o rofuto ni ageru.","german":"I put the big blanket up on the loft.","furigana":"おお|あ"},{"japanese":"手を上げてください。","romaji":"Te o agete kudasai.","german":"Please raise your hand.","furigana":"て|あ"}],"notes":"上げる here means to lift something or bring it to a higher place. The thing lifted takes を; に can name the higher destination. It is a transitive ichidan verb: 上げます, 上げて. ロフト here means a raised living level or mezzanine. Distinguish this \"lifting\" from the also-read あげる \"to give\" by context.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"力いっぱいレバーを下げて！","romaji":"Chikaraippai rebā o sagete!","german":"Push the lever down as hard as you can!","furigana":"ちから|さ"},{"japanese":"少し温度を下げてください。","romaji":"Sukoshi ondo o sagete kudasai.","german":"Please lower the temperature a little.","furigana":"すこ|おんど|さ"}],"notes":"下げる here means \"to lower, bring down, reduce\". The thing lowered takes を, for example a lever or a temperature. It is a transitive ichidan verb: 下げます, 下げて. 力いっぱい下げて is a direct, forceful command; 少し下げてください is a polite request for a small change. The word does not require physical force in every context.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"みそラーメン下さい。","romaji":"Miso rāmen kudasai.","german":"One miso ramen, please.","furigana":"くだ"},{"japanese":"お水を下さい。","romaji":"Omizu o kudasai.","german":"Please give me some water.","furigana":"みず|くだ"},{"japanese":"この紙に名前を書いて下さい。","romaji":"Kono kami ni namae o kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write your name on this paper.","furigana":"かみ|なまえ|か|くだ"}],"notes":"下さい is the polite request \"please give me ...\". In orders, を can be omitted in conversation: みそラーメン下さい. It is usually written ください in kana. It is the special imperative form of くださる and is practiced here as a ready-made expression. After the て-form of another verb, ください instead means \"please do ...\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"入り口は、どこ？","romaji":"Iriguchi wa, doko?","german":"Where is the entrance?","furigana":"い|ぐち"},{"japanese":"入り口はどこですか。","romaji":"Iriguchi wa doko desu ka.","german":"Where is the entrance?","furigana":"い|ぐち"},{"japanese":"入り口で友達を待っています。","romaji":"Iriguchi de tomodachi o matte imasu.","german":"I am waiting for a friend at the entrance.","furigana":"い|ぐち|ともだち|ま"}],"notes":"入り口 is the entrance. This reading contains g: iriguchi, not irikuchi. 入り口はどこ？ is a short, casual question; どこですか is more polite. 入り口で names the entrance as the place of an activity, such as waiting. The spelling 入口 is also common and is read the same way in the right context.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大したことじゃないよ。","romaji":"Taishita koto ja nai yo.","german":"It's no big deal.","furigana":"たい"},{"japanese":"大したことありません。","romaji":"Taishita koto arimasen.","german":"It's nothing special.","furigana":"たい"},{"japanese":"一人で全部作ったなんて、大したものですね。","romaji":"Hitori de zenbu tsukutta nante, taishita mono desu ne.","german":"To think you made everything by yourself, that's really something.","furigana":"ひとり|ぜんぶつく|たい"}],"notes":"大した comes before a noun and means \"significant, considerable, remarkable\". It very often appears with a negative: 大したことじゃない, \"it's no big deal\". In a positive sentence it can express recognition. It is used here as an attributive expression; you do not simply attach な to it as with an ordinary な-adjective.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あっ、ふじ山だ！","romaji":"A, Fujisan da!","german":"Oh, that's Mt. Fuji!"},{"japanese":"いつかふじ山に登りたいです。","romaji":"Itsuka Fujisan ni noboritai desu.","german":"Someday I want to climb Mt. Fuji.","furigana":"さん|のぼ"}],"notes":"ふじ山 is a learner-friendly mixed spelling of 富士山, Mt. Fuji. In this name 山 is read さん; this is not the polite name suffix さん. In normal kanji text 富士山 is usual. あっ expresses a sudden discovery; に names the destination when climbing. The proper name is written Fujisan.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この公園には人工の池があります。","romaji":"Kono kōen ni wa jinkō no ike ga arimasu.","german":"There is an artificial pond in this park.","furigana":"こうえん|じんこう|いけ"},{"japanese":"これは人工の花です。","romaji":"Kore wa jinkō no hana desu.","german":"This is an artificial flower.","furigana":"じんこう|はな"}],"notes":"人工 means \"artificial, man-made\". Before a noun it often takes の: 人工の花, an artificial flower. In fixed compounds の can be missing, for example 人工芝 for artificial turf. It refers to human production as opposed to natural origin. The identically read 人口 means \"population\" and is a different word.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"力いっぱいがんばりました！","romaji":"Chikaraippai ganbarimashita!","german":"I gave it my all!","furigana":"ちから"},{"japanese":"力いっぱい走りました。","romaji":"Chikaraippai hashirimashita.","german":"I ran with all my strength.","furigana":"ちから|はし"},{"japanese":"重いドアを力いっぱい押しましたが、開きませんでした。","romaji":"Omoi doa o chikaraippai oshimashita ga, akimasen deshita.","german":"I pushed the heavy door with all my strength, but it didn't open.","furigana":"おも|ちから|お|あ"}],"notes":"力いっぱい means \"with all one's strength, as hard as possible\". It stands before an action as an expression of manner. It can describe physical effort or stress strong effort in trying. The small っ in いっぱい is written as a doubled p. It does not merely mean repeating a task very often.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ルームメイトはロフトに上る。","romaji":"Rūmumeito wa rofuto ni noboru.","german":"My roommate climbs up to the loft.","furigana":"のぼ"},{"japanese":"階段を上ります。","romaji":"Kaidan o noborimasu.","german":"I go up the stairs.","furigana":"かいだん|のぼ"}],"notes":"上る here means to go up or climb. It is intransitive, even though the path covered takes を: 階段を上る. に can name the destination, such as a higher living level. This godan verb forms 上ります and 上って. The spelling 登る is especially common when climbing a mountain; both can express \"to climb up\" depending on the context.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"夕べ、月をみた。","romaji":"Yūbe, tsuki o mita.","german":"I saw the moon last night.","furigana":"ゆう|つき"},{"japanese":"夕べ友達と食事をしました。","romaji":"Yūbe tomodachi to shokuji o shimashita.","german":"Last night I had a meal with a friend.","furigana":"ゆう|ともだち|しょくじ"},{"japanese":"夕べよく眠れなかったので、今日は眠いです。","romaji":"Yūbe yoku nemurenakatta node, kyō wa nemui desu.","german":"I couldn't sleep well last night, so I'm sleepy today.","furigana":"ゆう|ねむ|きょう|ねむ"}],"notes":"夕べ here means \"yesterday evening, last night\" and can stand at the start of a sentence as a time expression. It refers to the evening before today. In other contexts the word can also simply mean \"evening\", for instance in event titles with の; that meaning is not the one in these examples. The long first syllable is written yūbe. 昨夜 can also refer to yesterday evening or last night with the reading ゆうべ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"白のドレスの女の子がレイチェルです。","romaji":"Shiro no doresu no onna no ko ga Reicheru desu.","german":"The girl in the white dress is Rachel.","furigana":"しろ|おんな|こ"},{"japanese":"女の子が歌を歌っています。","romaji":"Onna no ko ga uta o utatte imasu.","german":"A girl is singing a song.","furigana":"おんな|こ|うた|うた"}],"notes":"女の子 means \"girl\". It can refer to a female child; whether a daughter is meant depends on the context. 女の子が names a girl as an identified person or as the one acting. の belongs to the fixed compound 女の子. For adult women the term can sound too familiar or diminutive depending on the situation.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 10"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"そっちは女子トイレですよ。","romaji":"Sotchi wa joshi toire desu yo.","german":"The women's restroom is over there.","furigana":"じょし"},{"japanese":"女子トイレはどこですか。","romaji":"Joshi toire wa doko desu ka.","german":"Where is the women's restroom?","furigana":"じょし"},{"japanese":"このクラスは女子が十人、男子が八人です。","romaji":"Kono kurasu wa joshi ga jūnin, danshi ga hachinin desu.","german":"In this class there are ten girls and eight boys.","furigana":"じょし|じゅうにん|だんし|はちにん"}],"notes":"女子 means girls or women and often appears in categories, such as 女子トイレ or 女子学生. In direct personal conversation it is not always the most fitting term for an adult woman. そっち is a casual direction word, while どこですか politely asks about the place. The counterpart 男子 appears in corresponding categories.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"わたしは五才です。","romaji":"Watashi wa gosai desu.","german":"I am five years old.","furigana":"ごさい"},{"japanese":"おばあさんは九十才です。","romaji":"Obāsan wa kyūjussai desu.","german":"My grandmother is ninety years old.","furigana":"きゅうじゅっさい"},{"japanese":"この教室には、六才から通えます。","romaji":"Kono kyōshitsu ni wa, rokusai kara kayoemasu.","german":"You can attend this class from age six.","furigana":"きょうしつ|ろくさい|かよ"}],"notes":"〜才 is the age counter \"... years old\". The wavy line marks a place for a number and is not pronounced. 才 is here the simplified spelling of the age character 歳. With numbers the pronunciation sometimes changes: 一才 is いっさい, 十才 is じゅっさい or じっさい; 二十才 has the special reading はたち. The examples practice concrete statements of age.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このカレー、中々おいしいですね。","romaji":"Kono karē, nakanaka oishii desu ne.","german":"This curry is quite delicious.","furigana":"なかなか"},{"japanese":"中々面白い本ですね。","romaji":"Nakanaka omoshiroi hon desu ne.","german":"Quite an interesting book, isn't it?","furigana":"なかなかおもしろ|ほん"},{"japanese":"鍵を探していますが、中々見つかりません。","romaji":"Kagi o sagashite imasu ga, nakanaka mitsukarimasen.","german":"I'm looking for my key, but I just can't find it.","furigana":"かぎ|さが|なかなかみ"}],"notes":"中々 is usually written なかなか. In a positive evaluation it means \"quite, fairly, considerably\". With a negated verb it often means that something does not come easily despite effort or waiting: なかなか見つからない, \"not easy to find\". So it is not always a mere intensifier like \"very\". 中中 is also listed as a spelling; the kana form なかなか is more common.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 18"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"五月一日にニューヨークへかえりました。","romaji":"Gogatsu tsuitachi ni Nyūyōku e kaerimashita.","german":"I returned to New York on May 1st.","furigana":"ごがつついたち"},{"japanese":"五月は過ごしやすいです。","romaji":"Gogatsu wa sugoshiyasui desu.","german":"May is pleasant.","furigana":"ごがつ|す"}],"notes":"五月 is the month of May and is read ごがつ. A month can take に; as the topic of a statement it is followed by は. 五月一日 is May 1st: ごがつついたち, not ごがついちにち. 過ごしやすい here evaluates the pleasant weather or conditions; this is a statement in the given context, not a worldwide weather rule.","levelNote":"Compound: 月 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"六月にアメリカにいきました。","romaji":"Rokugatsu ni Amerika ni ikimashita.","german":"I went to the United States in June.","furigana":"ろくがつ"},{"japanese":"この町では、六月は雨が多いです。","romaji":"Kono machi de wa, rokugatsu wa ame ga ōi desu.","german":"In this town it rains a lot in June.","furigana":"まち|ろくがつ|あめ|おお"}],"notes":"六月 is the month of June, read ろくがつ. に links it as a point in time to an activity; は makes it the topic of a statement. 雨が多い means it rains a lot. With この町では it is clear which place the weather statement refers to. In month names, 月 is read がつ, not つき.","levelNote":"Compound: 月 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このドレスは五千円でした。","romaji":"Kono doresu wa gosen'en deshita.","german":"Dieses Kleid hat 5.000 Yen gekostet.","furigana":"ごせんえん"},{"japanese":"これは五百円です。","romaji":"Kore wa gohyaku en desu.","german":"Das kostet fünfhundert Yen.","furigana":"ごひゃくえん"},{"japanese":"千円払って、二百円のおつりをもらいました。","romaji":"Sen'en haratte, nihyaku en no otsuri o moraimashita.","german":"Ich habe tausend Yen bezahlt und zweihundert Yen Wechselgeld bekommen.","furigana":"せんえんはら|にひゃくえん"}],"notes":"〜円 folgt hier auf eine Zahl und nennt einen Betrag in Yen. Die Wellenlinie markiert den Platz für die Zahl; ausgesprochen wird nur der Betrag, etwa 五百円, gohyaku en. Nach einem n kann in Hepburn ein Trennapostroph stehen: 五千円, gosen'en. Auf Deutsch bleibt „Yen“ auch bei mehreren Einheiten unverändert. 円 ist auch ohne die Platzhaltermarkierung die Schreibung der Währungseinheit; hier üben Sie damit Geldbeträge in Yen.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"十月に五十才になります。","romaji":"Jūgatsu ni gojussai ni narimasu.","german":"I will turn fifty in October.","furigana":"じゅうがつ|ごじゅっさい"},{"japanese":"この山では、十月は紅葉の季節です。","romaji":"Kono yama de wa, jūgatsu wa kōyō no kisetsu desu.","german":"On this mountain, October is the season of autumn leaves.","furigana":"やま|じゅうがつ|こうよう|きせつ"}],"notes":"十月 is October, read じゅうがつ. に names the month as a point in time, for example for a birthday. 五十才になる means \"to turn fifty\"; the common reading is ごじゅっさい. 紅葉の季節 describes the season of changing leaves at the given place. 十月 is a month name, not a statement of \"ten months long\".","levelNote":"Compound: 月 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ウェディングパーティーは一月でした。","romaji":"Wedingu pātī wa ichigatsu deshita.","german":"The wedding party was in January.","furigana":"いちがつ"},{"japanese":"この町では、一月はとても寒いです。","romaji":"Kono machi de wa, ichigatsu wa totemo samui desu.","german":"In this town it is very cold in January.","furigana":"まち|いちがつ|さむ"}],"notes":"一月 is January and is read いちがつ as a month name. The same spelling with a different reading can mean \"one month\": ひとつき. The examples mean the calendar name. A celebration can have taken place in January; the cold in the second sentence describes a specific place. In month names, 月 is read がつ.","levelNote":"Compound: 月 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"バレンタインデーは二月だよ。","romaji":"Barentain dē wa nigatsu da yo.","german":"Valentine's Day is in February.","furigana":"にがつ"},{"japanese":"二月は一番短い月です。","romaji":"Nigatsu wa ichiban mijikai tsuki desu.","german":"February is the shortest month.","furigana":"にがつ|いちばんみじか|つき"}],"notes":"二月 is February, read にがつ. Not every number has the same reading before 月 as when counting; this combination is learned as a month name. は can make the month the topic. 一番短い月 means \"the shortest month\"; here 月 is read as the independent word つき. The examples use the modern calendar.","levelNote":"Compound: 月 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子犬は、かわいい。","romaji":"Koinu wa, kawaii.","german":"The puppy is cute.","furigana":"こいぬ"},{"japanese":"かわいい子犬ですね。","romaji":"Kawaii koinu desu ne.","german":"What a cute puppy!","furigana":"こいぬ"},{"japanese":"子犬に水をあげてから、散歩に行きました。","romaji":"Koinu ni mizu o agete kara, sanpo ni ikimashita.","german":"I gave the puppy some water and then went for a walk.","furigana":"こいぬ|みず|さんぽ|い"}],"notes":"子犬 is a puppy, that is, a young dog. 子 here names the young of an animal. かわいい子犬 judges it as cute; 子犬に水をあげる, by contrast, describes caring for it. With に you name the animal as the recipient. The reading is koinu with the vowels o and i kept separate.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「おはよう！」と、王子がいった。","romaji":"\"Ohayou!\" to, ouji ga itta.","german":"\"Good morning!\" said the prince.","furigana":"おうじ"},{"japanese":"王子様がお城に住んでいます。","romaji":"Ōjisama ga oshiro ni sunde imasu.","german":"The prince lives in the castle.","furigana":"おうじさま|しろ|す"},{"japanese":"イギリスの王子が日本を訪れました。","romaji":"Igirisu no ouji ga Nihon o otozuremashita.","german":"The British prince visited Japan.","furigana":"おうじ|にほん|おとず"}],"notes":"王子 is a prince as the son of a king; the female counterpart is 王女, in fairy tales usually お姫様. In fairy tales and children's language you almost always hear 王子様, and 白馬の王子様 (\"prince on a white horse\") stands for the dream man. For the Japanese imperial family one does not use 王子 but separate words such as 皇太子 (\"crown prince\") or 親王.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"それは、女王のネックレスです。","romaji":"Sore wa, joō no nekkuresu desu.","german":"That is the queen's necklace.","furigana":"じょおう"},{"japanese":"女王はとても美しいです。","romaji":"Joō wa totemo utsukushii desu.","german":"The queen is very beautiful.","furigana":"じょおう|うつく"}],"notes":"女王 means \"queen\", here a female monarch. 女王のネックレス means her necklace; 女王は makes her the topic of a description. It is a different term from 王女 \"princess\". The reading is じょおう: joō, with the syllable jo before a long o. You do not insert an extra syllable じょう.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この話では、王女はいつでも正しいです。","romaji":"Kono hanashi de wa, ōjo wa itsu demo tadashii desu.","german":"In this story, the princess is always right.","furigana":"はなし|おうじょ|ただ"},{"japanese":"王女は優しい人です。","romaji":"Ōjo wa yasashii hito desu.","german":"The princess is a kind person.","furigana":"おうじょ|やさ|ひと"},{"japanese":"王女は町を訪れ、子供たちと話しました。","romaji":"Ōjo wa machi o otozure, kodomotachi to hanashimashita.","german":"The princess visited the town and talked with the children.","furigana":"おうじょ|まち|おとず|こども|はな"}],"notes":"王女 is a princess, especially a daughter from a royal house. 女王, on the other hand, is the queen. The words have similar kanji in reverse order but are not interchangeable. In the example sentence この話では a claim is limited to the story being told. The reading おうじょ is written ōjo.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"四月一日はエイプリルフールです。","romaji":"Shigatsu tsuitachi wa eipuriru fūru desu.","german":"April 1st is April Fools' Day.","furigana":"しがつついたち"},{"japanese":"四月から新学期が始まります。","romaji":"Shigatsu kara shingakki ga hajimarimasu.","german":"The new term starts in April.","furigana":"しがつ|しんがっき|はじ"}],"notes":"四月 is April and is read しがつ, not よんがつ. 四月一日 is しがつついたち, \"April 1st\". に can name a point in time, から a start. 新学期 is the new school term or new academic period; it is not in every context the whole new school year. The examples mean the modern calendar.","levelNote":"Compound: 月 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本のカレーはおいしいです。","romaji":"Nihon no karē wa oishii desu.","german":"Japanese curry is delicious.","furigana":"にほん"},{"japanese":"日本に行ったことがありますか。","romaji":"Nihon ni itta koto ga arimasu ka.","german":"Have you ever been to Japan?","furigana":"にほん|い"}],"notes":"日本 is Japan. The name has the common readings にほん and にっぽん; this entry practices にほん. 日本のカレー names Japanese curry, 日本に行く names Japan as the travel destination. 行ったことがありますか asks about past experience. の shows origin or affiliation here, not ownership of a country.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"タスクはシステムエラーを正すことです。","romaji":"Tasuku wa shisutemu erā o tadasu koto desu.","german":"My task is to correct system errors.","furigana":"ただ"},{"japanese":"間違いを正しました。","romaji":"Machigai o tadashimashita.","german":"I corrected the mistake.","furigana":"まちが|ただ"},{"japanese":"先生の話を聞く前に、姿勢を正しました。","romaji":"Sensei no hanashi o kiku mae ni, shisei o tadashimashita.","german":"Before listening to the teacher, I straightened my posture.","furigana":"せんせい|はなし|き|まえ|しせい|ただ"}],"notes":"正す means \"to correct, to put right\". A mistake takes を; 姿勢を正す also means to straighten one's posture. This godan verb forms 正します and 正して. For an ordinary repair, 直す is often more fitting. The similarly read 質す \"to inquire\" is a different dictionary entry and not the meaning of the examples.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"なんだ、この玉？","romaji":"Nan da, kono tama?","german":"What is this ball?","furigana":"たま"},{"japanese":"子供が玉で遊んでいます。","romaji":"Kodomo ga tama de asonde imasu.","german":"The child is playing with a ball.","furigana":"こども|たま|あそ"}],"notes":"玉 here means \"ball\" or a small round object. なんだ、この玉？ is a casual question about the ball; 玉で遊ぶ names it as something to play with. The reading is たま. With coins you often find the altered ending だま, such as 百円玉; that is not simply the reading of the standalone word in these examples. 球 is also a spelling of たま for a sphere or ball. The other meanings of this word depend on the spelling and the context.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その丸い玉ねぎ下さい。","romaji":"Sono marui tamanegi kudasai.","german":"Please give me that round onion.","furigana":"まる|たま|くだ"},{"japanese":"玉ねぎを買ってきてください。","romaji":"Tamanegi o katte kite kudasai.","german":"Please buy some onions and bring them back.","furigana":"たま|か"},{"japanese":"玉ねぎを切ると、涙が出ます。","romaji":"Tamanegi o kiru to, namida ga demasu.","german":"When I cut onions, tears come to my eyes.","furigana":"たま|き|なみだ|で"}],"notes":"玉ねぎ is the onion. The word part ねぎ does not make it an ordinary long spring onion; 玉ねぎ is its own vegetable name. を names it as the object bought or prepared. 買ってきてください asks someone to buy them and bring them back. In conversation, を can be omitted when ordering.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 34"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本で白人のパートナーとくらしてます。","romaji":"Nihon de hakujin no pātonā to kurashitemasu.","german":"I live in Japan with a white partner.","furigana":"にほん|はくじん"},{"japanese":"あの白人の女性はアメリカ人です。","romaji":"Ano hakujin no josei wa Amerikajin desu.","german":"That white woman over there is American.","furigana":"はくじん|じょせい|じん"}],"notes":"白人 refers to a white person as a population or ancestry category. の can precede another noun, as in 白人のパートナー. By itself it says nothing about nationality; 白人 and アメリカ人 are different pieces of information. 女性 explicitly names a woman in the second example. The modern meaning practiced here is not one of the older dictionary meanings.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"サーモンは目玉もおいしいですよ。","romaji":"Sāmon wa medama mo oishii desu yo.","german":"Salmon eyes are tasty too.","furigana":"めだま"},{"japanese":"目玉焼きを作りました。","romaji":"Medamayaki o tsukurimashita.","german":"I made a fried egg.","furigana":"めだまや|つく"},{"japanese":"このお祭りの目玉は、夜の花火です。","romaji":"Kono omatsuri no medama wa, yoru no hanabi desu.","german":"The main attraction of this festival is the fireworks at night.","furigana":"まつ|めだま|よる|はなび"}],"notes":"目玉 literally means the eyeball. Figuratively it can name the main attraction or special highlight of an event. The fixed compound 目玉焼き, by contrast, means \"fried egg (sunny-side up)\"; no eyeball is meant. The examples show a food with eyes, the fixed dish name and an event highlight. The reading is medama.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"出口にたくさんの人々がいた。","romaji":"Deguchi ni takusan no hitobito ga ita.","german":"Am Ausgang waren viele Menschen.","furigana":"でぐち|ひとびと"},{"japanese":"たくさんの人々が来ました。","romaji":"Takusan no hitobito ga kimashita.","german":"Viele Menschen sind gekommen.","furigana":"ひとびと|き"},{"japanese":"この町の人々は、毎年お祭りを一緒に準備します。","romaji":"Kono machi no hitobito wa, maitoshi omatsuri o issho ni junbi shimasu.","german":"Die Menschen dieser Stadt bereiten das Fest jedes Jahr gemeinsam vor.","furigana":"まち|ひとびと|まいとし|まつ|いっしょ|じゅんび"}],"notes":"人々 heißt „Menschen, Leute“ als Gruppe und klingt oft schriftlicher als 人. Das Wiederholungszeichen 々 zeigt die Wiederholung des Kanji; in der Lesung wird das zweite ひと zu びと. Mit が kann die Gruppe als vorhandene oder kommende Menschen erscheinen. Die Form wird nicht wie ein frei verwendbares deutsches Mehrzahlsuffix an jedes Nomen angehängt.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 33"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"水中にコイがたくさんいる。","romaji":"Suichū ni koi ga takusan iru.","german":"Es gibt viele Koi-Fische im Wasser.","furigana":"すいちゅう"},{"japanese":"水中で泳ぎます。","romaji":"Suichū de oyogimasu.","german":"Ich schwimme unter Wasser.","furigana":"すいちゅう|およ"}],"notes":"水中 bedeutet den Bereich im oder unter Wasser. Es ist ein Nomen: 水中に nennt einen Aufenthaltsort, 水中で den Ort einer Tätigkeit. 水中で泳ぐ meint Schwimmen unter Wasser; bei den Koi kann auf Deutsch „im Wasser“ natürlicher sein. Die Lesung endet mit langem u: suichū.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ジャケットの左右にはポケットがあります。","romaji":"Jaketto no sayū ni wa poketto ga arimasu.","german":"The jacket has pockets on the left and right sides.","furigana":"さゆう"},{"japanese":"左右を確認してください。","romaji":"Sayū o kakunin shite kudasai.","german":"Please look left and right.","furigana":"さゆう|かくにん"},{"japanese":"道路を渡る前に、左右をよく見ます。","romaji":"Dōro o wataru mae ni, sayū o yoku mimasu.","german":"Before I cross the road, I look carefully left and right.","furigana":"どうろ|わた|まえ|さゆう|み"}],"notes":"左右 names left and right together. ジャケットの左右 describes both sides of the jacket. 左右を確認する means to look at or check both sides, for example before crossing a road. In 道路を渡る, 道路 is the path being crossed. The reading さゆう has a long u: sayū.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ミホちゃん、おはよう！","romaji":"Miho-chan, ohayō!","german":"Good morning, Miho-chan!"},{"japanese":"友達に「おはよう」と言いました。","romaji":"Tomodachi ni \"ohayō\" to iimashita.","german":"I said \"good morning\" to my friend.","furigana":"ともだち|い"},{"japanese":"朝、先生に「おはようございます」と挨拶しました。","romaji":"Asa, sensei ni \"ohayō gozaimasu\" to aisatsu shimashita.","german":"In the morning I greeted the teacher with \"good morning\".","furigana":"あさ|せんせい|あいさつ"}],"notes":"おはよう is the casual morning greeting. To people you address more politely you usually say おはようございます. With friends the short form fits. In some occupations it is also used at the start of work later in the day; that is a usage in particular environments, not a general greeting for every time of day. お早う is a kanji spelling of the same short greeting; normally おはよう is written in kana.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"アメリカ人ですか？","romaji":"Amerikajin desu ka?","german":"Are you American?","furigana":"じん"},{"japanese":"クラスにアメリカ人の学生がいます。","romaji":"Kurasu ni Amerikajin no gakusei ga imasu.","german":"There is an American student in the class.","furigana":"じん|がくせい"}],"notes":"アメリカ人 is a person from the USA, that is, a U.S. American. In this nationality term, 人 is read じん. の can precede another noun: アメリカ人の学生. The compound term is learned as a whole and does not by itself say which language a person is currently using.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二人のフランス人がいる。","romaji":"Futari no Furansujin ga iru.","german":"There are two French people.","furigana":"ふたり|じん"},{"japanese":"フランス人の友達がいます。","romaji":"Furansujin no tomodachi ga imasu.","german":"I have a French friend.","furigana":"じん|ともだち"}],"notes":"フランス人 is a French man or woman. In this combination 人 is read じん. When two people are counted, 二人 is read ふたり, not ににん. フランス人の友達 describes the nationality of a friend. The expression does not fix the person's gender.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ビー玉は小さい丸い玉です。","romaji":"Bīdama wa chiisai marui tama desu.","german":"A marble is a small, round ball.","furigana":"だま|ちい|まる|たま"},{"japanese":"子供がビー玉で遊んでいます。","romaji":"Kodomo ga bīdama de asonde imasu.","german":"The child is playing with marbles.","furigana":"こども|だま|あそ"}],"notes":"ビー玉 is a small glass marble for playing. The word refers to a particular round plaything, not any small ball of any material. With で you name the marbles as the thing played with. The long first syllable is written bī; 玉 is read だま in this compound. The general ball, on the other hand, is 玉, tama.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今月は大切なテストがあります。","romaji":"Kongetsu wa taisetsu na tesuto ga arimasu.","german":"There is an important test this month.","furigana":"こんげつ|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"今月は忙しいです。","romaji":"Kongetsu wa isogashii desu.","german":"I am busy this month.","furigana":"こんげつ|いそが"},{"japanese":"今月の予定を手帳に書きました。","romaji":"Kongetsu no yotei o techō ni kakimashita.","german":"I wrote this month's schedule in my planner.","furigana":"こんげつ|よてい|てちょう|か"}],"notes":"今月 is the current month, the month in which you are speaking. As a time expression it often needs no に; with は the month becomes the topic. 今月の予定 means the appointments of this month. It is not always \"the next thirty days\": the expression is based on the calendar month.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"マイケルさんは、父の友人です。","romaji":"Maikeru-san wa, chichi no yūjin desu.","german":"Michael is a friend of my father's.","furigana":"ちち|ゆうじん"},{"japanese":"友人に会いました。","romaji":"Yūjin ni aimashita.","german":"I met a friend.","furigana":"ゆうじん|あ"}],"notes":"友人 means \"friend\" and sounds more factual or formal than 友達. It suits reports, for example, or introducing a person to others. 父の友人 names a friend of the father; 友人に会う names the friend as the person one meets. The word alone does not indicate a romantic relationship and does not specify gender.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このアパート、戸口のデザインがかわいいですね。","romaji":"Kono apāto, toguchi no dezain ga kawaii desu ne.","german":"The design of this apartment's doorway is cute.","furigana":"とぐち"},{"japanese":"戸口に立っている人は誰ですか。","romaji":"Toguchi ni tatte iru hito wa dare desu ka.","german":"Who is the person standing at the doorway?","furigana":"とぐち|た|ひと|だれ"}],"notes":"戸口 is the door opening, or the entrance right at the door. 戸口のデザイン describes its design; 戸口に立つ means to stand there. It is narrower than any access to a large building or site. 入り口 is often more general for \"entrance\" in everyday use. The item practiced here is not a family name.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このベンツは中古です。","romaji":"Kono Bentsu wa chūko desu.","german":"This Benz is secondhand.","furigana":"ちゅうこ"},{"japanese":"中古の車を買いました。","romaji":"Chūko no kuruma o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a used car.","furigana":"ちゅうこ|くるま|か"},{"japanese":"中古ですが、傷はありません。","romaji":"Chūko desu ga, kizu wa arimasen.","german":"It's used, but it has no scratches.","furigana":"ちゅうこ|きず"}],"notes":"中古 means \"used, secondhand\" and before a noun is usually followed by の: 中古の車. It describes previous use or resale, not automatically a bad condition. 中古です is also possible as a predicate. The use practiced here is not the historical meaning \"Middle Ages\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"外人カップルとダブルデートをした。","romaji":"Gaijin kappuru to daburu dēto o shita.","german":"We went on a double date with a foreign couple.","furigana":"がいじん"},{"japanese":"このクラスに外人がいます。","romaji":"Kono kurasu ni gaijin ga imasu.","german":"There is a foreigner in this class.","furigana":"がいじん"}],"notes":"外人 is a colloquial term for a foreign person and can sound exclusionary or derogatory. The usually more neutral form is 外国人. The examples help you understand the shorter form in conversation; it is not a generally polite form of address. By itself the word does not specify a concrete nationality.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"市立プールは三ドルで入れるよ。","romaji":"Shiritsu pūru wa sandoru de haireru yo.","german":"You can get into the municipal pool for three dollars.","furigana":"しりつ|さん|はい"},{"japanese":"市立の図書館に行きます。","romaji":"Shiritsu no toshokan ni ikimasu.","german":"I am going to the municipal library.","furigana":"しりつ|としょかん|い"}],"notes":"市立 means that a facility is run or established by a city. It appears in compounds like 市立プール or before a noun with の. The reading しりつ sounds exactly like 私立 \"private\"; to tell them apart, people sometimes say いちりつ in speech. The examples concern municipal facilities, not general visits to the city.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今はシアトル市にすんでいます。","romaji":"Ima wa Shiatoru-shi ni sunde imasu.","german":"I live in the city of Seattle now.","furigana":"いま|し"},{"japanese":"シアトル市のウェブサイトで地図を見ました。","romaji":"Shiatoru-shi no webusaito de chizu o mimashita.","german":"I looked at a map on the City of Seattle website.","furigana":"し|ちず|み"}],"notes":"シアトル市 is the city of Seattle. 市 is read し as the ending of a city name. シアトル市に住む names the place of residence; シアトル市のウェブサイト, by contrast, refers to the city's website. The name シアトル is written in katakana.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これはフィットネストレーニングに用いるマシンです。","romaji":"Kore wa fittonesu torēningu ni mochiiru mashin desu.","german":"This is a machine used for fitness training.","furigana":"もち"},{"japanese":"この道具を用いてください。","romaji":"Kono dōgu o mochiite kudasai.","german":"Please use this tool.","furigana":"どうぐ|もち"}],"notes":"用いる means \"to use\" and is especially useful in explanations or instructions. It is a transitive Ichidan verb: 用います, 用いて. The means used takes を, the purpose often takes に. Before a noun, 用いる describes what the thing is used for.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人生ってすばらしい。","romaji":"Jinsei tte subarashii.","german":"Life is wonderful.","furigana":"じんせい"},{"japanese":"人生で初めて外国に行きました。","romaji":"Jinsei de hajimete gaikoku ni ikimashita.","german":"I traveled abroad for the first time in my life.","furigana":"じんせい|はじ|がいこく|い"}],"notes":"人生 means life as a personal path through life. 人生って... is a casual way of making life the topic of conversation. 人生で初めて means \"for the first time in one's life\"; で names the frame of experience here, not a place of residence.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ジェニーさんはイギリス人です。","romaji":"Jenī-san wa Igirisujin desu.","german":"Jenny is British.","furigana":"じん"},{"japanese":"イギリス人の友達と英語で話しました。","romaji":"Igirisujin no tomodachi to eigo de hanashimashita.","german":"I talked in English with a British friend.","furigana":"じん|ともだち|えいご|はな"}],"notes":"イギリス人 is a British person. The ending 人 is read じん in nationality terms. イギリス人です states nationality; イギリス人の友達 links this information with a person, such as a friend.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日は、お元気ですか。","romaji":"Konnichiwa, ogenki desu ka.","german":"Hello, how are you?","furigana":"こんにち|げんき"},{"japanese":"友達へのメールを「今日は」で始めました。","romaji":"Tomodachi e no mēru o konnichiwa de hajimemashita.","german":"I started an email to a friend with \"hello\".","furigana":"ともだち|こんにち|はじ"}],"notes":"Here 今日は means the greeting: it is read こんにちは, with wa at the end. It is usually written in hiragana. It suits a daytime greeting in conversation and a friendly opening for a message. The same spelling can otherwise mean きょうは \"as for today ...\"; this card practices the greeting.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 0"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大人しい犬ですね。","romaji":"Otonashii inu desu ne.","german":"That's a quiet dog, isn't it?","furigana":"おとな|いぬ"},{"japanese":"図書館では大人しくしてください。","romaji":"Toshokan de wa otonashiku shite kudasai.","german":"Please be quiet in the library.","furigana":"としょかん|おとな"}],"notes":"大人しい describes quiet, gentle or good-natured behavior, including in animals. As an i-adjective it stands directly before the noun: 大人しい犬. With 大人しくする you talk about behaving quietly or well; for this, い becomes く.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 38"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"戸をノックした。","romaji":"To o nokku shita.","german":"I knocked on the door.","furigana":"と"},{"japanese":"戸を閉めてください。","romaji":"To o shimete kudasai.","german":"Please close the door.","furigana":"と|し"}],"notes":"戸 means a door, especially one in Japanese-style construction, such as a sliding door. In 戸をノックする and 戸を閉める the door is the object with を. The reading と is short; 戸 by itself does not specify which way the door opens.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの女の人？マミさんだよ。","romaji":"Ano onna no hito? Mami-san da yo.","german":"That woman? That's Mami.","furigana":"おんな|ひと"},{"japanese":"女の人に道を教えてもらいました。","romaji":"Onna no hito ni michi o oshiete moraimashita.","german":"A woman explained the way to me.","furigana":"おんな|ひと|みち|おし"}],"notes":"女の人 means \"woman\" and is a term for a person made of 女＋の＋人. In あの女の人, あの points to a woman farther away. When you get something explained by her, に is used: 女の人に教えてもらう. The reading also includes the ん in おんな.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 10"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"友達の輪を広げたいです。","romaji":"Tomodachi no wa o hirogetai desu.","german":"I want to widen my circle of friends.","furigana":"ともだち|わ|ひろ"},{"japanese":"地図を広げて見ました。","romaji":"Chizu o hirogete mimashita.","german":"I spread out the map and looked at it.","furigana":"ちず|ひろ|み"}],"notes":"広げる is a transitive Ichidan verb: 広げます, 広げて. With it you can unfold something, such as a map, or expand an area. 友達の輪を広げる means to widen one's circle of friends. What is spread out or expanded takes を.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"山びこだ！きこえる？","romaji":"Yamabiko da! Kikoeru?","german":"It's an echo! Can you hear it?","furigana":"やま"},{"japanese":"山びこを聞きたくて、大きな声で呼びました。","romaji":"Yamabiko o kikitakute, ōkina koe de yobimashita.","german":"Because I wanted to hear an echo, I called out loudly.","furigana":"やま|き|おお|こえ|よ"}],"notes":"山びこ is an echo, especially one in the mountains. 聞こえる describes that one can hear it without actively listening for it. 山びこを聞きたくて, on the other hand, names the wish to hear an echo as the reason for a call.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"本はテーブルの上にあります。","romaji":"Hon wa tēburu no ue ni arimasu.","german":"The book is on the table.","furigana":"ほん|うえ"},{"japanese":"テーブルの上をふきました。","romaji":"Tēburu no ue o fukimashita.","german":"I wiped the tabletop.","furigana":"うえ"}],"notes":"テーブルの上 means the tabletop or the place on the table. の links テーブル with 上, which is read うえ here. For a location, に follows; when you wipe the surface, it takes を as the area being worked on.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ベッドの下にはマンガがたくさんあります。","romaji":"Beddo no shita ni wa manga ga takusan arimasu.","german":"There are lots of manga under the bed.","furigana":"した"},{"japanese":"ベッドの下を掃除しました。","romaji":"Beddo no shita o sōji shimashita.","german":"I cleaned under the bed.","furigana":"した|そうじ"}],"notes":"ベッドの下 is the area under the bed. の links the bed with the position word 下, read した. With ベッドの下に you name the place of objects; with ベッドの下を掃除する, the area being cleaned. The small ッ doubles the d in beddo.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"そして、ふじ山から日の出をみました。","romaji":"Soshite, Fujisan kara hinode o mimashita.","german":"Then I watched the sunrise from Mt. Fuji.","furigana":"さん|ひ|で"},{"japanese":"日の出の時間は何時ですか。","romaji":"Hinode no jikan wa nanji desu ka.","german":"What time is sunrise?","furigana":"ひ|で|じかん|なんじ"}],"notes":"日の出 is sunrise. The part の belongs to the fixed term; the reading is ひので. With 日の出を見る you report watching it. With 日の出の時間は何時ですか, on the other hand, you ask for the time of sunrise.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"生ビールを一杯ください。","romaji":"Namabīru o ippai kudasai.","german":"One draft beer, please.","furigana":"なま|いっぱい"},{"japanese":"生ビールのメニューを見せてください。","romaji":"Namabīru no menyū o misete kudasai.","german":"Please show me the draft beer menu.","furigana":"なま|み"}],"notes":"生ビール is nama beer, often ordered as draft beer in restaurants. In this meaning, 生 is read なま, and ビール has a long i. To count glasses of beer use 杯: 一杯 is いっぱい. 生ビールのメニュー names the corresponding drinks menu.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"北口にいるよ！","romaji":"Kitaguchi ni iru yo!","german":"I'm at the north exit!","furigana":"きたぐち"},{"japanese":"北口で待ち合わせしましょう。","romaji":"Kitaguchi de machiawase shimashō.","german":"Let's meet at the north exit.","furigana":"きたぐち|ま|あ"}],"notes":"北口 is the northern entrance or exit, for example at a train station. 口 is read ぐち here. 北口にいる says where a person is; 北口で待ち合わせする names the meeting point. Whether an entrance or an exit is meant depends on the situation.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これが富士山の写真です。","romaji":"Kore ga Fujisan no shashin desu.","german":"This is a photo of Mt. Fuji.","furigana":"ふじさん|しゃしん"},{"japanese":"これは何ですか。","romaji":"Kore wa nan desu ka.","german":"What is this?","furigana":"なん"},{"japanese":"先ほど話した計画ですが、これについてどう思いますか。","romaji":"Sakihodo hanashita keikaku desu ga, kore ni tsuite dō omoimasu ka.","german":"About the plan we just talked about, what do you think of it?","furigana":"さき|はな|けいかく|おも"}],"notes":"これ is an independent demonstrative pronoun: \"this one\". It refers to something near the speaker or the thing just pointed out. Unlike この, これ needs no following noun. これが… identifies the thing, これは何ですか asks about it. これ can also pick up the thing or idea just discussed. 此れ is a rare kanji spelling; in everyday writing これ is usual.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"リンゴジュース、二つ下さい。","romaji":"Ringo jūsu, futatsu kudasai.","german":"Two apple juices, please.","furigana":"ふた|くだ"},{"japanese":"リンゴを洗ってから食べます。","romaji":"Ringo o aratte kara tabemasu.","german":"I wash the apple and then eat it.","furigana":"あら|た"}],"notes":"リンゴ is the katakana spelling for apple. In リンゴジュース it forms the first part of \"apple juice\". In an order, 二つ counts two servings. リンゴを洗ってから食べる, by contrast, describes a sequence: wash the apple first, then eat it.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ランプの明かりで本を読みました。","romaji":"Ranpu no akari de hon o yomimashita.","german":"I read a book by lamplight.","furigana":"あ|ほん|よ"},{"japanese":"部屋の明かりをつけました。","romaji":"Heya no akari o tsukemashita.","german":"I turned on the light in the room.","furigana":"へや|あ"}],"notes":"明かり is light or a light source. ランプの明かり means the light of a lamp; 明かりで本を読む names it as an aid for reading. 部屋の明かりをつける means to switch on the light in the room. Here つける is used as the verb for switching on.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"右手に力が入らないんです。","romaji":"Migite ni chikara ga hairanai n desu.","german":"I have no strength in my right hand.","furigana":"みぎて|ちから|はい"},{"japanese":"右手でペンを持ちます。","romaji":"Migite de pen o mochimasu.","german":"I hold the pen in my right hand.","furigana":"みぎて|も"}],"notes":"右手 is the right hand and is a noun. With 右手で you name the hand as the means of an action, such as holding a pen. 右手に力が入らない describes having no strength in the right hand. In this word, 手 is read て.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"左手でスプーンをもった。","romaji":"Hidarite de supūn o motta.","german":"I held a spoon in my left hand.","furigana":"ひだりて"},{"japanese":"左手に指輪をしています。","romaji":"Hidarite ni yubiwa o shite imasu.","german":"I wear a ring on my left hand.","furigana":"ひだりて|ゆびわ"}],"notes":"左手 is the left hand and is a noun. 左手で names it as the means of holding or using an object. 左手に指輪をする, by contrast, describes the place where one wears a ring.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"百歳まで生きたいです。","romaji":"Hyakusai made ikitai desu.","german":"I want to live to be a hundred years old.","furigana":"ひゃくさい|い"},{"japanese":"魚は水の中で生きています。","romaji":"Sakana wa mizu no naka de ikite imasu.","german":"Fish live in water.","furigana":"さかな|みず|なか|い"}],"notes":"生きる is an intransitive ichidan verb: 生きます, 生きて. It speaks of living, that is, being alive. 生きている describes this state. In 百歳まで生きたい, まで names the desired age as the limit; for the place where one lives, 住む is usually used instead.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"しばらく、このクラスは休止です。","romaji":"Shibaraku, kono kurasu wa kyūshi desu.","german":"This class is suspended for a while.","furigana":"きゅうし"},{"japanese":"工事を一時休止しました。","romaji":"Kōji o ichiji kyūshi shimashita.","german":"I temporarily suspended the construction work.","furigana":"こうじ|いちじきゅうし"}],"notes":"休止 is the suspension or interruption of an activity or operation. It is not simply a rest break like 休憩. このクラスは休止です describes a class that is suspended. With 工事を休止する you say that you are interrupting the construction work; the project affected takes を.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"休日はワインをのみます。","romaji":"Kyūjitsu wa wain o nomimasu.","german":"On days off I drink wine.","furigana":"きゅうじつ"},{"japanese":"明日は休日です。","romaji":"Ashita wa kyūjitsu desu.","german":"Tomorrow is a day off.","furigana":"あした|きゅうじつ"}],"notes":"休日 is a day off or a holiday; the word need not mean a legal public holiday. 休日は… describes what one does on such days. 明日は休日です says that tomorrow is a day off. The u in kyūjitsu is long.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先ず、トマトを切ります。","romaji":"Mazu, tomato o kirimasu.","german":"First, I cut the tomatoes.","furigana":"ま|き"},{"japanese":"先ず手を洗ってください。","romaji":"Mazu te o aratte kudasai.","german":"Please wash your hands first.","furigana":"ま|て|あら"}],"notes":"先ず means \"first\" here and is mostly written まず. It places an action before the following steps. The adverb needs no particle: 先ずトマトを切る. A request can also begin with 先ず to highlight the first step.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先々月、キューバにいきました。","romaji":"Sensengetsu, Kyūba ni ikimashita.","german":"The month before last, I went to Cuba.","furigana":"せんせんげつ"},{"japanese":"先々月の家賃はいくらでしたか。","romaji":"Sensengetsu no yachin wa ikura deshita ka.","german":"How much was the rent the month before last?","furigana":"せんせんげつ|やちん"}],"notes":"先々月 is the month before last, that is, two months before the current month. You can use it as a time expression without a particle. With 先々月の you connect this period to a noun, such as 先々月の家賃 for the rent of the month before last.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先月は六月でした。","romaji":"Sengetsu wa rokugatsu deshita.","german":"Last month was June.","furigana":"せんげつ|ろくがつ"},{"japanese":"先月日本に行きました。","romaji":"Sengetsu Nihon ni ikimashita.","german":"I went to Japan last month.","furigana":"せんげつにほん|い"}],"notes":"先月 is the month immediately before the month in which you are speaking. As a time expression it often stands before the verb without a particle: 先月日本に行きました. With 先月は you make the past month the topic; 先月の写真 would be photos from that month.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先日はどうもありがとうございました。","romaji":"Senjitsu wa dōmo arigatō gozaimashita.","german":"Thank you very much for the other day.","furigana":"せんじつ"},{"japanese":"先日買った本を読みました。","romaji":"Senjitsu katta hon o yomimashita.","german":"I read the book I bought the other day.","furigana":"せんじつか|ほん|よ"}],"notes":"先日 refers to a certain day not long ago: \"the other day\" or \"a few days ago\". Unlike 先月, it does not specify a whole calendar month. 先日はありがとうございました thanks someone for an earlier meeting or help. In 先日買った本, the time expression belongs to the purchase, not to the later reading.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 41"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"父はフリスビーの名人です。","romaji":"Chichi wa furisubī no meijin desu.","german":"My father is a Frisbee expert.","furigana":"ちち|めいじん"},{"japanese":"料理の名人にコツを教えてもらいました。","romaji":"Ryōri no meijin ni kotsu o oshiete moraimashita.","german":"I had a master of cooking teach me some tricks.","furigana":"りょうり|めいじん|おし"}],"notes":"名人 is someone who masters an activity superbly. With activity + の名人 you name the field, such as 料理の名人. The word can also be used admiringly in daily life and does not claim any particular professional qualification. 名人に教えてもらう describes having such a person teach you something.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"名字は「田中」です。","romaji":"Myōji wa Tanaka desu.","german":"My surname is \"Tanaka\".","furigana":"みょうじ|たなか"},{"japanese":"名字を教えてください。","romaji":"Myōji o oshiete kudasai.","german":"Please tell me your surname.","furigana":"みょうじ|おし"}],"notes":"名字 is the family name and is a noun. With 名字は田中です you state your surname; 名字を教えてください asks for this information. The whole reading is みょうじ, with a long ō. It does not refer to the given name, which is called 下の名前, for example.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"女の子は、おこって口を一文字にむすびました。","romaji":"Onnanoko wa, okotte kuchi o ichimonji ni musubimashita.","german":"The girl got angry and pressed her lips into a straight line.","furigana":"おんな|こ|くち|いちもんじ"},{"japanese":"電線の上に鳥が一文字に並んでいました。","romaji":"Densen no ue ni tori ga ichimonji ni narande imashita.","german":"Birds were lined up in a straight line on the power line.","furigana":"でんせん|うえ|とり|いちもんじ|なら"}],"notes":"With the reading いちもんじ, 一文字 means \"straight line\", like the shape of the character 一. 口を一文字に結ぶ describes lips pressed firmly together into a straight line; 一文字に並ぶ describes a straight-line arrangement. The reading ひともじ for \"one character\" belongs to a different usage and is not practiced here.","levelNote":"Compound: 文字 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"２０１１年に、日本にいきました。","romaji":"Nisen jūichi-nen ni, Nihon ni ikimashita.","german":"I went to Japan in 2011.","furigana":"ねん|にほん"},{"japanese":"２０１１年の写真を見ました。","romaji":"Nisen jūichi-nen no shashin o mimashita.","german":"I looked at photos from 2011.","furigana":"ねん|しゃしん|み"}],"notes":"２０１１年 refers to the calendar year 2011 and is read にせんじゅういちねん. After this year number, 年 is read ねん. With ２０１１年に you name the year of an event; with ２０１１年の写真 you assign photos to this year. The full-width digits change neither the number nor the reading.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"年内に牛の赤ちゃんが生まれます。","romaji":"Nennai ni ushi no akachan ga umaremasu.","german":"A calf will be born within this year.","furigana":"ねんない|うし|あか|う"},{"japanese":"年内に終わらせます。","romaji":"Nennai ni owarasemasu.","german":"I will finish it by the end of the year.","furigana":"ねんない|お"}],"notes":"年内 means the part of the year in question that is still left. 年内に means \"within this year\" or \"by the end of the year\": the event should happen before the turn of the year, not necessarily exactly on the last day. 終わらせます is \"I will bring it to an end\"; this differs from the intransitive 終わります \"it ends\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一年生の子は手を上げて下さい。","romaji":"Ichinensei no ko wa te o agete kudasai.","german":"First-year students, please raise your hands.","furigana":"いちねんせい|こ|て|あ|くだ"},{"japanese":"私は大学の一年生です。","romaji":"Watashi wa daigaku no ichinensei desu.","german":"I am a first-year university student.","furigana":"わたし|だいがく|いちねんせい"}],"notes":"一年生 refers to a person in the first year of a school or college. For elementary school children \"first grader\" fits, for 大学の一年生 \"first-year student\". It is about the year of schooling, not age. 一年生の子 connects the grade level with the children.","levelNote":"Compound: 年生 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"早々にメールをくれて、ありがとう。","romaji":"Sōsō ni mēru o kurete, arigatō.","german":"Thank you for your prompt email.","furigana":"そうそう"},{"japanese":"帰宅早々、電話が鳴りました。","romaji":"Kitaku sōsō, denwa ga narimashita.","german":"The phone rang right after I got home.","furigana":"きたくそうそう|でんわ|な"}],"notes":"早々に means \"quickly\" or \"promptly\". In 早々にメールをくれて、ありがとう you thank someone for a quick message. After a noun, 早々 can also mean \"right after ...\": 帰宅早々 is immediately after getting home. The reading そうそう contains two long ō sounds; here the word is an adverb, not a particle.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 23"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"父はテーブルの上のチョコレートを一気にたべた。","romaji":"Chichi wa tēburu no ue no chokorēto o ikki ni tabeta.","german":"My father ate the chocolate on the table in one go.","furigana":"ちち|うえ|いっき"},{"japanese":"気温が一気に下がりました。","romaji":"Kion ga ikki ni sagarimashita.","german":"The temperature dropped all at once.","furigana":"きおん|いっき|さ"}],"notes":"一気に describes an action done without interruption, such as eating something up in one go. With a change of state it can mean \"all at once\" or \"suddenly\": 気温が一気に下がる. The っ is rendered as a doubled k in ikki. に belongs to the expression practiced here.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日の母は気分がいいです。","romaji":"Kyō no haha wa kibun ga ii desu.","german":"My mother is in a good mood today.","furigana":"きょう|はは|きぶん"},{"japanese":"今日は映画を見る気分ではありません。","romaji":"Kyō wa eiga o miru kibun de wa arimasen.","german":"I'm not in the mood to watch a movie today.","furigana":"きょう|えいが|み|きぶん"}],"notes":"気分 refers to one's mood or state of feeling. 気分がいい can mean \"to be in a good mood\" or \"to feel well\", depending on context. With 映画を見る気分ではありません you say that you are not in the mood for a movie right now; it is not about the ability to watch a movie.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いつになったら、本気を出すんですか。","romaji":"Itsu ni nattara, honki o dasu n desu ka.","german":"When are you finally going to get serious?","furigana":"ほんき|だ"},{"japanese":"本気で言っていますか。","romaji":"Honki de itte imasu ka.","german":"Do you mean that seriously?","furigana":"ほんき|い"}],"notes":"本気 speaks of seriousness and genuine commitment. 本気で言う means to mean something seriously; 本気を出す means to really exert oneself or to get seriously started. The question いつになったら、本気を出すんですか can sound reproachful. 本気で describes the attitude toward the action that follows.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"よく平気でいられますね。","romaji":"Yoku heiki de iraremasu ne.","german":"It's amazing that you can stay so calm.","furigana":"へいき"},{"japanese":"大丈夫、平気ですよ。","romaji":"Daijōbu, heiki desu yo.","german":"Don't worry, I'm fine.","furigana":"だいじょうぶ|へいき"}],"notes":"平気 describes calmness or a lack of worry. In casual speech 平気です can also assure that everything is fine. Depending on tone, 平気でいる sounds admiring or critical. よく平気でいられますね expresses astonishment; よく here does not mean \"often\" or \"always\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ニックって一本気なところがあるよね。","romaji":"Nikku tte ippongi na tokoro ga aru yo ne.","german":"Nick has something of a single-minded streak, doesn't he?","furigana":"いっぽんぎ"},{"japanese":"彼は一本気な人です。","romaji":"Kare wa ippongi na hito desu.","german":"He is a straightforward, single-minded person.","furigana":"かれ|いっぽんぎ|ひと"},{"japanese":"祖父は一本気な性格で、一度決めたことは絶対に曲げなかった。","romaji":"Sofu wa ippongi na seikaku de, ichido kimeta koto wa zettai ni magenakatta.","german":"My grandfather had a single-minded character and never went back on anything he had once decided.","furigana":"そふ|いっぽんぎ|せいかく|いちどき|ぜったい|ま"}],"notes":"一本気 describes a character that is \"straightforward\" and \"unwavering\" in devotion to one thing: honest, without ulterior motives, but also not very willing to compromise. Typical patterns are 一本気な人, 一本気な性格 and 一本気なところがある; less often as a noun with の (一本気の男). The tone is usually appreciative, unlike 頑固 (\"stubborn\"), which is clearly negative; 一途, by contrast, stresses devotion to a person or goal, for example in love. The word sounds somewhat old-fashioned.","levelNote":"Compound: 本気 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あ！竹の子が土から出てる！","romaji":"A! Takenoko ga tsuchi kara deteru!","german":"Oh! A bamboo shoot is poking out of the ground!","furigana":"たけ|こ|つち|で"},{"japanese":"竹の子を煮ました。","romaji":"Takenoko o nimashita.","german":"I simmered bamboo shoots.","furigana":"たけ|こ|に"}],"notes":"竹の子 is a bamboo shoot, not literally a \"bamboo child\". It can mean the growing plant or the food. 出てる is the casual short form of 出ている, \"is coming out / has grown out\". 土 has the reading つち here; the whole reading of the card word remains たけのこ.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一人の村人が川に入った。","romaji":"Hitori no murabito ga kawa ni haitta.","german":"A villager went into the river.","furigana":"ひとり|むらびと|かわ|はい"},{"japanese":"村人がお祭りを準備しています。","romaji":"Murabito ga omatsuri o junbi shite imasu.","german":"The villagers are preparing the festival.","furigana":"むらびと|まつ|じゅんび"}],"notes":"村人 are people who live in a village. In this word, 人 is read びと: むらびと. 一人の村人 names a single person; here 一人 is read ひとり. Without a number, 村人 can also mean several villagers depending on the context, such as the helpers at a festival.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人に見せるほどのキッチンじゃないですよ。","romaji":"Hito ni miseru hodo no kitchin ja nai desu yo.","german":"It's not a kitchen worth showing to other people.","furigana":"ひと|み"},{"japanese":"写真を見せてください。","romaji":"Shashin o misete kudasai.","german":"Please show me the photo.","furigana":"しゃしん|み"}],"notes":"見せる means \"to show\" and is a transitive ichidan verb: 見せます, 見せて. The thing shown takes を, the person it is shown to takes に. 人に見せるほどの… describes something worth showing to others. 見る, by contrast, means \"to see/look at\" and distributes the roles differently.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 16"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このチャートの見方、分かる？","romaji":"Kono chāto no mikata, wakaru?","german":"Do you know how to read this chart?","furigana":"みかた|わ"},{"japanese":"見方を変えてみましょう。","romaji":"Mikata o kaete mimashō.","german":"Let's try changing our way of looking at it.","furigana":"みかた|か"}],"notes":"見方 can refer to a viewpoint or to the way of reading or understanding something. このチャートの見方 asks how to read the chart. 見方を変える, on the other hand, means to take a different perspective. In 変えてみましょう, てみる invites trying that once.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"川には貝がたくさんいる。","romaji":"Kawa ni wa kai ga takusan iru.","german":"There are lots of shellfish in the river.","furigana":"かわ|かい"},{"japanese":"海辺で貝を拾いました。","romaji":"Umibe de kai o hiroimashita.","german":"I picked up shells at the seaside.","furigana":"うみべ|かい|ひろ"}],"notes":"貝 can refer to the living shellfish or to its shell. For living shellfish, いる fits as with other animals. 貝を拾う at the beach often means picking up shells; there 拾う is the verb of the action. The reading かい stays the same in both meanings.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このエリアでは去年、水が不足しました。","romaji":"Kono eria de wa kyonen, mizu ga fusoku shimashita.","german":"Last year this area had a water shortage.","furigana":"きょねん|みず|ふそく"},{"japanese":"人手が不足しています。","romaji":"Hitode ga fusoku shite imasu.","german":"There is a shortage of staff.","furigana":"ひとで|ふそく"}],"notes":"不足 means shortage or scarcity. As a する-verb, 不足する means \"to be lacking / to be insufficient\" and is intransitive: 水が不足する, not 水を. 不足しています describes an existing shortage. The card shows the noun stem 不足; the verb forms belong to 不足する.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"車内に人々がたくさんいます。","romaji":"Shanai ni hitobito ga takusan imasu.","german":"There are lots of people inside the car.","furigana":"しゃない|ひとびと"},{"japanese":"車内ではお静かにお願いします。","romaji":"Shanai de wa oshizuka ni onegai shimasu.","german":"Please be quiet inside the vehicle.","furigana":"しゃない|しず|ねが"}],"notes":"車内 is the interior of a vehicle, including a train or bus. It is a noun: 車内に人がいる names the place where someone is, and 車内ではお静かにお願いします asks for quiet behavior there. Vehicle + の車内 can specify which vehicle is meant.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日系二世の友達に日本語を教えました。","romaji":"Nikkei nisei no tomodachi ni nihongo o oshiemashita.","german":"I taught Japanese to a friend who is second-generation Japanese-descended.","furigana":"にっけいにせい|ともだち|にほんご|おし"},{"japanese":"彼は日系二世です。","romaji":"Kare wa nikkei nisei desu.","german":"He is a second-generation Japanese emigrant descendant.","furigana":"かれ|にっけいにせい"}],"notes":"二世 here means the second generation of a family with a migration background, such as 日系二世 for people of Japanese descent. It is not a statement of age. The addition 日系 makes clear which origin is meant. The statement of origin alone says nothing about which language the individual person speaks.","levelNote":"Compound: 世 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の友達は日系三世です。","romaji":"Watashi no tomodachi wa nikkei sansei desu.","german":"My friend is third-generation Japanese-descended.","furigana":"わたし|ともだち|にっけいさんせい"},{"japanese":"ヘンリー三世についての本を読みました。","romaji":"Henrī sansei ni tsuite no hon o yomimashita.","german":"I read a book about Henry III.","furigana":"さんせい|ほん|よ"}],"notes":"三世 can refer to the third generation of a family with a migration background, such as 日系三世. After a name it also means \"the Third\", as in ヘンリー三世. Here the reading is さんせい. The statement of origin and the number in a ruler's name are different uses of the same word.","levelNote":"Compound: 世 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"週末は主に家で過ごします。","romaji":"Shūmatsu wa omoni ie de sugoshimasu.","german":"On weekends I mainly spend my time at home.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|おも|いえ|す"},{"japanese":"この会社は主に車を作っています。","romaji":"Kono kaisha wa omoni kuruma o tsukutte imasu.","german":"This company mainly makes cars.","furigana":"かいしゃ|おも|くるま|つく"}],"notes":"主に means \"mainly\" or \"chiefly\". It names the main emphasis without excluding other possibilities. 主に家で過ごす says where one mostly spends time; 主に車を作る names the most important product. The adverb comes before the statement it qualifies.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この人は、わたしの主人です。","romaji":"Kono hito wa, watashi no shujin desu.","german":"This person is my husband.","furigana":"ひと|しゅじん"},{"japanese":"この店の主人は料理が上手です。","romaji":"Kono mise no shujin wa ryōri ga jōzu desu.","german":"The owner of this shop is good at cooking.","furigana":"みせ|しゅじん|りょうり|じょうず"}],"notes":"主人 is a noun. In わたしの主人 one's own husband is meant; in この店の主人 the owner of the shop. The context therefore decides the meaning. For another person's husband the polite form ご主人 is often encountered; this card practices the form without ご.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"左がマミ、中央がジェニー、右がマイケルです。","romaji":"Hidari ga Mami, chūō ga Jenī, migi ga Maikeru desu.","german":"On the left is Mami, in the middle is Jenny, and on the right is Michael.","furigana":"ひだり|ちゅうおう|みぎ"},{"japanese":"部屋の中央にテーブルがあります。","romaji":"Heya no chūō ni tēburu ga arimasu.","german":"There is a table in the middle of the room.","furigana":"へや|ちゅうおう"}],"notes":"中央 is the middle or center and is a noun. 部屋の中央に names a place within the room. In 中央がジェニーです, on the other hand, が identifies the person in the middle position. In ちゅうおう both ū and ō are long; as a noun, 中央 often needs の before another noun.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの山、平らですね。","romaji":"Ano yama, taira desu ne.","german":"That mountain is flat, isn't it?","furigana":"やま|たい"},{"japanese":"平らな場所にテントを立てました。","romaji":"Taira na basho ni tento o tatemashita.","german":"I pitched the tent on a level spot.","furigana":"たい|ばしょ|た"}],"notes":"平ら describes a level or flat surface. As a na-adjective it takes な before a noun: 平らな場所. In a sentence you say この道は平らです. 平らにする describes making something level; ら is part of the word and not a sign of an i-adjective.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"平日のディナーは主にカレーです。","romaji":"Heijitsu no dinā wa omoni karē desu.","german":"On weekdays, dinner is mainly curry.","furigana":"へいじつ|おも"},{"japanese":"平日は仕事があります。","romaji":"Heijitsu wa shigoto ga arimasu.","german":"I have work on weekdays.","furigana":"へいじつ|しごと"}],"notes":"平日 refers to the ordinary days as opposed to days off or holidays. In the usual weekly schedule this typically means Monday to Friday; the actual schedule determines the boundary. 平日のディナー uses の for dinner on such days. 平日は makes these days the topic of a habit or work arrangement.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 26"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"力いっぱいの一打でしたね。","romaji":"Chikara ippai no ichida deshita ne.","german":"That was a hit with all your strength, wasn't it?","furigana":"ちから|いちだ"},{"japanese":"一打で勝負が決まりました。","romaji":"Ichida de shōbu ga kimarimashita.","german":"A single blow decided the match.","furigana":"いちだ|しょうぶ|き"}],"notes":"一打 is a single blow or hit, for example in sports. The reading is いちだ. 力いっぱいの一打 evaluates a powerful blow; 一打で names a single blow as the means or the decisive cause. Here the word counts the act of striking, not a person.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"バーのテーブルの上には小皿がたくさんあった。","romaji":"Bā no tēburu no ue ni wa kozara ga takusan atta.","german":"There were many small plates on the table in the bar.","furigana":"うえ|こざら"},{"japanese":"小皿に醤油を入れます。","romaji":"Kozara ni shōyu o iremasu.","german":"I put soy sauce in the small dish.","furigana":"こざら|しょうゆ|い"}],"notes":"小皿 is a small plate or small dish for serving. The reading こざら has a z: 皿 alone is read さら. 小皿がある describes tableware that is there; 小皿に醤油を入れる names the small dish as the destination and the soy sauce as the contents.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お礼にビールをもらいました。","romaji":"Orei ni bīru o moraimashita.","german":"I got a beer as a thank-you.","furigana":"れい"},{"japanese":"お礼を言いたいです。","romaji":"Orei o iitai desu.","german":"I want to express my thanks.","furigana":"れい|い"}],"notes":"お礼 is a polite word for thanks. お礼に means \"as a thank-you\" and names the occasion for a gift or action. お礼を言う means to express one's thanks; お礼を言いたい expresses the wish to do so. The initial お is part of the word form practiced here.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 50"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これは不正な方法です。","romaji":"Kore wa fusei na hōhō desu.","german":"This is an unfair method.","furigana":"ふせい|ほうほう"},{"japanese":"不正は許されません。","romaji":"Fusei wa yurusaremasen.","german":"Fraud is not tolerated.","furigana":"ふせい|ゆる"}],"notes":"不正 describes dishonest or unlawful behavior. As a noun it can mean \"fraud / wrongdoing\" and as a na-adjective it can modify a noun: 不正な方法. 不正は許されません rejects such behavior. The meaning comes from the context, not from one particular kind of crime alone.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この子牛は、まだまだ小さい赤ちゃんです。","romaji":"Kono kōshi wa, madamada chīsai akachan desu.","german":"This calf is still a very small baby.","furigana":"こうし|ちい|あか"},{"japanese":"牧場に子牛がいます。","romaji":"Bokujō ni kōshi ga imasu.","german":"There are calves on the farm.","furigana":"ぼくじょう|こうし"}],"notes":"子牛 is a calf. The whole reading is こうし, with a long ō; it is not split into ko ushi. 子 here describes the young animal. For living calves you use いる, as in 牧場に子牛がいます for where they are.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これは母の大切な宝石です。","romaji":"Kore wa haha no taisetsu na hōseki desu.","german":"This is a precious gem of my mother's.","furigana":"はは|たいせつ|ほうせき"},{"japanese":"指輪に宝石がついています。","romaji":"Yubiwa ni hōseki ga tsuite imasu.","german":"The ring is set with a gem.","furigana":"ゆびわ|ほうせき"}],"notes":"宝石 is a gem or jewel and is a noun. 母の大切な宝石 can mean a stone that means a great deal to the mother personally; 大切 does not have to claim a high monetary value. 宝石がついている describes a piece of jewelry fitted with a gem. The reading ほうせき has a long ō.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"半分じゃなくて丸ごと一つ下さい。","romaji":"Hanbun ja nakute marugoto hitotsu kudasai.","german":"Please give me one whole piece, not just half.","furigana":"はんぶん|まる|ひと|くだ"},{"japanese":"フォルダーを丸ごとコピーしました。","romaji":"Forudā o marugoto kopī shimashita.","german":"I copied the whole folder.","furigana":"まる"}],"notes":"丸ごと means \"whole / in its entirety\". It refers to the extent, not to how quickly something happens. 丸ごと一つ orders a complete piece instead of a half. A folder can also be 丸ごとコピーする, that is, copied together with all its contents. The word is used as an adverb.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大気の汚れについて調べました。","romaji":"Taiki no yogore ni tsuite shirabemashita.","german":"I looked into air pollution.","furigana":"たいき|よご|しら"},{"japanese":"大気がきれいです。","romaji":"Taiki ga kirei desu.","german":"The air is clean.","furigana":"たいき"}],"notes":"大気 refers to the atmosphere or the air as part of the environment. You encounter the word in connection with weather or environmental topics. 大気の汚れ means \"pollution of the air\"; 大気がきれい describes its cleanliness. The reading たいき stays short, with no long vowel.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"母は、よく「大丈夫」と口にします。","romaji":"Haha wa, yoku daijōbu to kuchi ni shimasu.","german":"My mother often says \"It's okay\".","furigana":"はは|だいじょうぶ|くち"},{"japanese":"彼はその話を口にしません。","romaji":"Kare wa sono hanashi o kuchi ni shimasen.","german":"He doesn't mention that story.","furigana":"かれ|はなし|くち"}],"notes":"Here 口にする means to utter or mention something. A topic takes を: その話を口にする. For words quoted literally, use と: 「大丈夫」と口にする. You change the する part, for example to 口にします or 口にしません; 口 stays くち in this expression.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"カナダは北アメリカにあります。","romaji":"Kanada wa kita Amerika ni arimasu.","german":"Canada is in North America.","furigana":"きた"},{"japanese":"北アメリカの地図を見ました。","romaji":"Kita Amerika no chizu o mimashita.","german":"I looked at a map of North America.","furigana":"きた|ちず|み"}],"notes":"北アメリカ is North America as a continent. 北 is read きた here; アメリカ is in katakana. 北アメリカにある names a geographic location; 北アメリカの地図 is a map of the continent. It does not merely mean the north of the USA.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"プレゼントのネクタイ、どれにする？","romaji":"Purezento no nekutai, dore ni suru?","german":"Which tie shall we get as a present?"},{"japanese":"どれがあなたの本ですか。","romaji":"Dore ga anata no hon desu ka.","german":"Which one is your book?","furigana":"ほん"},{"japanese":"どれ、温かいうちにスープをいただこう。","romaji":"Dore, atatakai uchi ni sūpu o itadakō.","german":"Well then, I'll have the soup while it's warm.","furigana":"あたた"}],"notes":"どれ asks independently about one thing out of several possibilities: どれがあなたの本ですか. With どれにする you choose something; directly before a noun, however, どの is used. As an interjection, どれ can also mean \"well then\" or \"let's see\" and introduce an action. 何れ is a rare kanji spelling for this reading; どれ is usually in hiragana. 何れ can also be read いずれ, a different word.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 8"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"うん、そうしよう！","romaji":"Un, sō shiyō!","german":"Yeah, let's do that!"},{"japanese":"「もう食べた？」「うん、食べたよ。」","romaji":"Mō tabeta? Un, tabeta yo.","german":"\"Have you eaten yet?\" \"Yeah, I have.\"","furigana":"た|た"}],"notes":"うん is a casual affirmative reply: \"yes\". It suits familiar conversations, for instance among friends or in the family. With うん、そうしよう you agree to a suggestion; in response to a question, うん can confirm a fact. In more polite situations はい is usually the better reply.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 20"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"毎日ボトルに水を入れる。","romaji":"Mainichi botoru ni mizu o ireru.","german":"I put water in the bottle every day.","furigana":"まいにち|みず|い"},{"japanese":"子供を部屋に入れました。","romaji":"Kodomo o heya ni iremashita.","german":"I let the child into the room.","furigana":"こども|へや|い"}],"notes":"入れる is a transitive ichidan verb: 入れます, 入れて. With を you name what goes in, with に the destination. With water it means \"to fill\"; 子供を部屋に入れる can mean to let the child into the room. The intransitive 入る, by contrast, describes someone or something going in by itself.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 16"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"リサイクルのゴミを出す日です。","romaji":"Risaikuru no gomi o dasu hi desu.","german":"Today is the day to put out the recycling.","furigana":"だ|ひ"},{"japanese":"かばんから財布を出しました。","romaji":"Kaban kara saifu o dashimashita.","german":"I took my wallet out of my bag.","furigana":"さいふ|だ"}],"notes":"出す is a transitive godan verb: 出します, 出して. It describes taking something out or putting it outside. The thing takes を, and the place it comes from can follow with から: かばんから財布を出す. 出す日 is the day of the action; 出る, by contrast, describes going out without a direct object.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 16"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この計画は新しい仕事を生みました。","romaji":"Kono keikaku wa atarashii shigoto o umimashita.","german":"This plan created new jobs.","furigana":"けいかく|あたら|しごと|う"},{"japanese":"猫が子猫を生みました。","romaji":"Neko ga koneko o umimashita.","german":"The cat gave birth to kittens.","furigana":"ねこ|こねこ|う"}],"notes":"生む can mean \"to give birth to\" or \"to bring forth\". A living being or a thing takes を as what comes into being: 子猫を生む, 仕事を生む. It is a transitive Godan verb: 生みます, 生んで. The intransitive 生まれる, on the other hand, says that something is born or comes into being.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ブックスタンドは本を立てるためのものです。","romaji":"Bukku sutando wa hon o tateru tame no mono desu.","german":"A book stand is for standing books upright.","furigana":"ほん|た"},{"japanese":"テントを立てました。","romaji":"Tento o tatemashita.","german":"I set up a tent.","furigana":"た"}],"notes":"立てる here means to set something upright or to put it up. It is a transitive ichidan verb: 立てます, 立てて. The object that is set up takes を. In 本を立てるためのもの, ための names the purpose of the object; テントを立てる is setting up a tent. 立つ, by contrast, describes standing itself.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この字は太字にしてください。","romaji":"Kono ji wa futoji ni shite kudasai.","german":"Please make this character bold.","furigana":"じ|ふとじ"},{"japanese":"太字の見出しを読みました。","romaji":"Futoji no midashi o yomimashita.","german":"I read the headline in bold type.","furigana":"ふとじ|みだ|よ"}],"notes":"太字 is bold type or thick lettering and is a noun. 太字にする switches the text to bold; 太字の見出し links the style with a headline. 太字で書く names the lettering as the manner of writing. The reading is ふとじ, with no long vowel.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"カードをよく交ぜて下さい。","romaji":"Kādo o yoku mazete kudasai.","german":"Please shuffle the cards well.","furigana":"ま|くだ"},{"japanese":"大人のゲームに子供も交ぜました。","romaji":"Otona no gēmu ni kodomo mo mazemashita.","german":"I let the child join in the adults' game too.","furigana":"おとな|こども|ま"}],"notes":"交ぜる is a transitive Ichidan verb: 交ぜます, 交ぜて. It fits shuffling cards together and including a person in a group or a game. In カードをよく交ぜる, よく means \"thoroughly\". With に you can name the group or game that someone is added to.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"夕べは、月光がとてもきれいでした。","romaji":"Yūbe wa, gekkō ga totemo kirei deshita.","german":"The moonlight was very beautiful last night.","furigana":"ゆう|げっこう"},{"japanese":"月光で道が見えました。","romaji":"Gekkō de michi ga miemashita.","german":"I could see the road by moonlight.","furigana":"げっこう|みち|み"}],"notes":"月光 is moonlight and is read げっこう: the doubled k for っ and ō for こう. 月光がきれい evaluates the light; 月光で道が見える describes being able to see the road by moonlight. で names the illumination here, が the thing that is visible.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このアパートは日光がよく入る。","romaji":"Kono apāto wa nikkō ga yoku hairu.","german":"This apartment gets a lot of sunlight.","furigana":"にっこう|はい"},{"japanese":"日光を避けて、日陰に座りました。","romaji":"Nikkō o sakete, hikage ni suwarimashita.","german":"I avoided the sunlight and sat in the shade.","furigana":"にっこう|さ|ひかげ|すわ"}],"notes":"日光 is sunlight here. In このアパートは日光がよく入る you say that a lot of light comes into the apartment. 日光を避ける means to avoid direct sunlight. っ gives kk and こう a long ō. The identically written city Nikkō is a different meaning.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 35"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"DVDとCDが同日にリリースされた。","romaji":"Dībuidī to shīdī ga dōjitsu ni rirīsu sareta.","german":"The DVD and the CD were released on the same day.","furigana":"どうじつ"},{"japanese":"同日に二つの試験があるので、困っています。","romaji":"Dōjitsu ni futatsu no shiken ga aru node, komatte imasu.","german":"I have a problem because two exams fall on the same day.","furigana":"どうじつ|ふた|しけん|こま"}],"notes":"同日 means \"on the same day\". The events concerned can still take place at different times; 同日 does not claim simultaneity to the minute. 同日に names the shared day as a time expression. You often meet the word in announcements or when planning several appointments.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"テーブルの上で、コインが回る。","romaji":"Tēburu no ue de, koin ga mawaru.","german":"The coin spins on the table.","furigana":"うえ|まわ"},{"japanese":"地球は太陽の周りを回っています。","romaji":"Chikyū wa taiyō no mawari o mawatte imasu.","german":"The Earth revolves around the Sun.","furigana":"ちきゅう|たいよう|まわ|まわ"}],"notes":"回る is an intransitive godan verb: 回ります, 回って. It can describe turning in place or circling around something. In コインが回る the coin is the subject. 太陽の周りを回る names with を the path traveled; を does not make the verb transitive here.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今回は、やめておきます。","romaji":"Konkai wa, yamete okimasu.","german":"I'll pass this time.","furigana":"こんかい"},{"japanese":"今回のテストは難しかったです。","romaji":"Konkai no tesuto wa muzukashikatta desu.","german":"The test this time was difficult.","furigana":"こんかい|むずか"}],"notes":"今回 means the present occasion or \"this time\". 今回は、やめておきます is a polite decision to forgo something this time. 今回のテスト connects the expression to the test in question. Even when the exam is already over, 今回 can still mean the current round that was discussed most recently.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あのミーティングに行くのは毎回この三人です。","romaji":"Ano mītingu ni iku no wa maikai kono sannin desu.","german":"The same three people go to that meeting every time.","furigana":"い|まいかい|さんにん"},{"japanese":"毎回遅刻しないでください。","romaji":"Maikai chikoku shinaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't be late every time.","furigana":"まいかいちこく"}],"notes":"毎回 means \"every time\" and refers to recurring occasions, not necessarily fixed days. In a sentence it often stands without a particle. 毎回この三人 stresses that the same three people always take part. 毎回遅刻しないでください objects to repeated lateness; it does not describe a single exception.","levelNote":"Compound: 回 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"テレビが当たるらしいよ。","romaji":"Terebi ga ataru rashii yo.","german":"Apparently you can win a TV.","furigana":"あ"},{"japanese":"宝くじが当たりました。","romaji":"Takarakuji ga atarimashita.","german":"I won the lottery.","furigana":"たから|あ"},{"japanese":"ボールが窓に当たりました。","romaji":"Bōru ga mado ni atarimashita.","german":"The ball hit the window.","furigana":"まど|あ"}],"notes":"当たる means, among other things, \"to hit\" and \"to win\". In these meanings it is intransitive: the ball or the prize takes が; the target that is hit can take に. 宝くじが当たる means to win a lottery prize. The Godan verb forms 当たります and 当たって. An object that someone hits deliberately is expressed differently with the related 当てる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「当たり！」という文字が目に入った。","romaji":"\"Atari!\" to iu moji ga me ni haitta.","german":"The word \"Winner!\" caught my eye.","furigana":"あ|もじ|め|はい"},{"japanese":"今日は当たりの日ですね。","romaji":"Kyō wa atari no hi desu ne.","german":"Today is a lucky day, isn't it?","furigana":"きょう|あ|ひ"},{"japanese":"このくじは当たりなので、景品をもらえます。","romaji":"Kono kuji wa atari na node, keihin o moraemasu.","german":"This ticket is a winner, so I get a prize.","furigana":"あ|けいひん"}],"notes":"当たり here is a hit or a lucky success. On a ticket or package, 当たり can indicate a win; as a short exclamation \"Bingo!\" fits. 当たりの日 names an especially successful day. Do not confuse it with the identically read place word 辺り \"area\". In these examples the form is a noun, not the verb form 当たり or the ending \"per\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このワークショップは毎月ありますか？","romaji":"Kono wākushoppu wa maitsuki arimasu ka?","german":"Is this workshop held every month?","furigana":"まいつき"},{"japanese":"毎月本を三冊読みます。","romaji":"Maitsuki hon o sansatsu yomimasu.","german":"I read three books every month.","furigana":"まいつきほん|さんさつよ"}],"notes":"毎月 means \"every month\", read まいつき here. It names a regular repetition; 来月, by contrast, refers only to the next month. For an event, 毎月ありますか asks about the monthly occurrence. 毎月本を三冊読む states a monthly quantity; 三冊 counts books. Before 毎月 you usually need no に.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 36"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"毎年四月に花見をします。","romaji":"Maitoshi shigatsu ni hanami o shimasu.","german":"Every April I go cherry blossom viewing.","furigana":"まいとししがつ|はなみ"},{"japanese":"毎年お花見に行きます。","romaji":"Maitoshi ohanami ni ikimasu.","german":"I go to see the cherry blossoms every year.","furigana":"まいとし|はなみ|い"},{"japanese":"毎年、誕生日に家族写真を撮ります。","romaji":"Maitoshi, tanjōbi ni kazoku shashin o torimasu.","german":"Every year we take a family photo on his/her birthday.","furigana":"まいとし|たんじょうび|かぞくしゃしん|と"}],"notes":"毎年 is \"every year\", here with the reading まいとし. It describes habits or regularly recurring events. 毎年四月に links the yearly repetition with a month; the に belongs to 四月 here. お花見 is viewing cherry blossoms and does not have to be an organized festival. The second common reading まいねん does not change the meaning.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"牛肉は平気ですか？","romaji":"Gyūniku wa heiki desu ka?","german":"Is beef okay for you?","furigana":"ぎゅうにく|へいき"},{"japanese":"牛肉が高くなりました。","romaji":"Gyūniku ga takaku narimashita.","german":"Beef has gotten more expensive.","furigana":"ぎゅうにく|たか"}],"notes":"牛肉 is beef. The noun names the meat, not the living animal 牛. 牛肉は平気ですか asks in a food context whether someone can eat beef without a problem or finds it acceptable. 高くなる means \"to become more expensive\" here. The long first syllable is written gyū; 牛 and 肉 together form the fixed food name.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 12"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"男は自分だけだった。","romaji":"Otoko wa jibun dake datta.","german":"I was the only man there.","furigana":"おとこ|じぶん"},{"japanese":"自分のことは自分でします。","romaji":"Jibun no koto wa jibun de shimasu.","german":"I take care of my own things myself.","furigana":"じぶん|じぶん"}],"notes":"自分 refers to the person in question, oneself; who is meant is clear from the sentence. In the examples it is the speaker. 自分のこと names one's own affairs, 自分でする means \"to do it oneself\". It does not automatically stand for \"I\" in every conversation: in narration it can refer to another person mentioned earlier.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 27"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの水色のバッグがほしいんですが。","romaji":"Ano mizuiro no baggu ga hoshii n desu ga.","german":"I'd like that light blue bag.","furigana":"みずいろ"},{"japanese":"水色のシャツを着ています。","romaji":"Mizuiro no shatsu o kite imasu.","german":"I am wearing a light blue shirt.","furigana":"みずいろ|き"}],"notes":"水色 is light blue or a pale shade of blue. Before a noun, の is usually used: 水色のバッグ, 水色のシャツ. The word is not an i-adjective; 水色い is not formed. ほしいんですが is a reserved way to introduce a wish, for example in a shop. The color term names only the shade, not the material of the bag or shirt.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このバスはシアトル行きです。","romaji":"Kono basu wa Shiatoru-yuki desu.","german":"This bus goes to Seattle.","furigana":"ゆ"},{"japanese":"東京行きの電車に乗ります。","romaji":"Tōkyō-yuki no densha ni norimasu.","german":"I take the train bound for Tokyo.","furigana":"とうきょうゆ|でんしゃ|の"},{"japanese":"東京行きの荷物は、この箱に入れてください。","romaji":"Tōkyō-yuki no nimotsu wa, kono hako ni irete kudasai.","german":"Please put the items bound for Tokyo in this box.","furigana":"とうきょうゆ|にもつ|はこ|い"}],"notes":"行き is used after the name of a destination and means \"heading to ...\" or \"destined for ...\". This entry practices the reading ゆき, as in 東京行き. You also hear いき; the reading shown here remains ゆき. After the compound, の can come before the vehicle. It is a destination label attached to a noun, not an ordinary sentence particle like に.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"何千回もエラーが出た。","romaji":"Nanzenkai mo erā ga deta.","german":"The error occurred thousands of times.","furigana":"なんぜんかい|で"},{"japanese":"何千人もの人が来ました。","romaji":"Nanzennin mo no hito ga kimashita.","german":"Thousands of people came.","furigana":"なんぜんにん|ひと|き"}],"notes":"何千 means \"many thousands\" and is often combined with a counter: 何千回 \"thousands of times\", 何千人 \"thousands of people\". も emphasizes the large number. Here 何 is not a question about a specific number; the sentence asserts a vaguely large quantity. With 人 you say にん, with 回 かい.","levelNote":"Compound: 千 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"何月生まれですか？","romaji":"Nangatsu umare desu ka?","german":"What month were you born?","furigana":"なんがつう"},{"japanese":"何月に日本に来ましたか。","romaji":"Nangatsu ni Nihon ni kimashita ka.","german":"What month did you come to Japan?","furigana":"なんがつ|にほん|き"}],"notes":"何月 asks \"which month?\" and is read なんがつ. The combination 何月生まれ asks about the birth month, not the birthday as a calendar date. 何月に names the month of an event. In this calendar question, 月 is read がつ; \"how many months?\" needs a different combination, such as 何か月.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人生は学ぶことが多いです。","romaji":"Jinsei wa manabu koto ga ōi desu.","german":"There is a lot to learn in life.","furigana":"じんせい|まな|おお"},{"japanese":"大学で経済学を学びました。","romaji":"Daigaku de keizaigaku o manabimashita.","german":"I studied economics at university.","furigana":"だいがく|けいざいがく|まな"}],"notes":"学ぶ means \"to learn, to study\" and takes the subject of learning with を: 経済学を学ぶ. It can refer both to instruction and to learning from experience. 大学で names the place of study here. The Godan verb ends in ぶ and forms 学びます, 学んで and 学んだ. 勉強する is another common form for focused study.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"兄は一つ上の学年です。","romaji":"Ani wa hitotsu ue no gakunen desu.","german":"My brother is one year above me.","furigana":"あに|ひと|うえ|がくねん"},{"japanese":"学年が変わる前に、新しい教科書を買います。","romaji":"Gakunen ga kawaru mae ni, atarashii kyōkasho o kaimasu.","german":"Before the new school year begins, I buy new textbooks.","furigana":"がくねん|か|まえ|あたら|きょうかしょ|か"}],"notes":"学年 can refer to the school year as a period of time or to a student's grade level. 一つ上の学年 means \"one grade higher\". For \"I am in third grade\" one usually says 三年生; 学年, on the other hand, can be compared directly or made the topic. The reading is がくねん, gakunen.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ロボット工学のクラスに入りたい。","romaji":"Robotto kōgaku no kurasu ni hairitai.","german":"I want to take a robotics class.","furigana":"こうがく|はい"},{"japanese":"工学を勉強しています。","romaji":"Kōgaku o benkyō shite imasu.","german":"I am studying engineering.","furigana":"こうがく|べんきょう"},{"japanese":"工学の知識を使って、新しい機械を作ります。","romaji":"Kōgaku no chishiki o tsukatte, atarashii kikai o tsukurimasu.","german":"I use knowledge of engineering to build a new machine.","furigana":"こうがく|ちしき|つか|あたら|きかい|つく"}],"notes":"工学 is engineering, or the engineering sciences. It names a field of study and takes を as a subject of study. ロボット工学 is robotics; の can attach a course to it. クラスに入りたい means wanting to take part in this course. Identically read words such as 高額 \"large sum of money\" have different kanji and meanings.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"それは金玉ではありません。","romaji":"Sore wa kintama de wa arimasen.","german":"That is not a testicle.","furigana":"きんたま"},{"japanese":"その言い方は下品なので、「金玉」ではなく「睾丸」と言います。","romaji":"Sono iikata wa gehin na node, \"kintama\" de wa naku \"kōgan\" to iimasu.","german":"That way of putting it is vulgar, so I say 「睾丸」 rather than 「金玉」.","furigana":"い|かた|げひん|きんたま||い"}],"notes":"金玉, read きんたま, is a vulgar colloquial term for the testicles. In a medical or polite description, 睾丸 is the appropriate neutral term. The individual kanji do not literally mean \"golden balls\" here. The reading きんぎょく belongs to a different dictionary entry; this entry explains the vulgar form so that you can understand such conversations.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"汗と一緒に、水分が体内から出ます。","romaji":"Ase to issho ni, suibun ga tainai kara demasu.","german":"Along with sweat, fluid leaves the body.","furigana":"あせ|いっしょ|すいぶん|たいない|で"},{"japanese":"体内の水分が大切です。","romaji":"Tainai no suibun ga taisetsu desu.","german":"The fluid in the body is important.","furigana":"たいない|すいぶん|たいせつ"}],"notes":"体内 means the inside of the body. As a noun it is combined with particles: 体内から \"from inside the body\", 体内の水分 \"the fluid in the body\". It is not an i-adjective. The examples describe a process and a component inside the body; for the surface of the body you need a different expression.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「サイコ」はヒッチコックの大作だ。","romaji":"\"Saiko\" wa Hitchikokku no taisaku da.","german":"\"Psycho\" is Hitchcock's masterpiece.","furigana":"たいさく"},{"japanese":"この映画は大作ですね。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa taisaku desu ne.","german":"This film is a masterpiece, isn't it?","furigana":"えいが|たいさく"},{"japanese":"この大作を完成するまでに、五年かかりました。","romaji":"Kono taisaku o kansei suru made ni, gonen kakarimashita.","german":"It took five years to complete this large-scale work.","furigana":"たいさく|かんせい|ごねん"}],"notes":"大作 can refer to a substantial, large-scale work or an important masterpiece. For films it can stress the large scale of production without praising every single scene. の names the creator, as in ヒッチコックの大作. In the second sentence, ね is an invitation to agree with an evaluation. Do not confuse it with 対策 \"countermeasure\", which is read the same way.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"つい大声が出てしまった。","romaji":"Tsui ōgoe ga dete shimatta.","german":"I raised my voice without meaning to.","furigana":"おおごえ|で"},{"japanese":"大声で話さないでください。","romaji":"Ōgoe de hanasanaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't speak in a loud voice.","furigana":"おおごえ|はな"}],"notes":"大声 is a loud voice. With で it becomes the manner of speaking: 大声で話す \"to speak loudly\". 大声が出る describes a loud utterance slipping out. つい and てしまった stress the unintentional occurrence in the first sentence. As a polite notice, the request 大声で話さないでください can be used.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの二人は兄弟です。","romaji":"Ano futari wa kyōdai desu.","german":"Those two are brothers.","furigana":"ふたり|きょうだい"},{"japanese":"兄弟は二人います。","romaji":"Kyōdai wa futari imasu.","german":"I have two siblings.","furigana":"きょうだい|ふたり"},{"japanese":"姉妹で小さな店を経営しています。","romaji":"Kyōdai de chīsana mise o keiei shite imasu.","german":"We sisters run a small shop together.","furigana":"きょうだい|ちい|みせ|けいえい"},{"japanese":"姉妹のうち、姉は東京に住み、妹は大阪に住んでいます。","romaji":"Kyōdai no uchi, ane wa Tōkyō ni sumi, imōto wa Ōsaka ni sunde imasu.","german":"Of the two sisters, the elder lives in Tokyo and the younger in Osaka.","furigana":"きょうだい|あね|とうきょう|す|いもうと|おおさか|す"}],"notes":"兄弟 can mean brothers or siblings in general; sisters are not excluded in the general meaning. The kanji do name an older and a younger brother, but the overall usage is broader. 二人 is read ふたり. With 兄弟は二人います the speaker usually means two siblings besides themselves; for the total number of siblings including oneself you can phrase it differently, for example 三人兄弟です. The spelling 姉妹 can also be read きょうだい as a special meaning reading (gikun) when sisters are explicitly meant. Normally, however, 姉妹 is read しまい. The examples with 姉妹 deliberately use the special reading きょうだい here.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 11"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ピアノのコンクールは来月ですか？","romaji":"Piano no konkūru wa raigetsu desu ka?","german":"Is the piano competition next month?","furigana":"らいげつ"},{"japanese":"来月から新しい仕事を始めます。","romaji":"Raigetsu kara atarashii shigoto o hajimemasu.","german":"I will start a new job next month.","furigana":"らいげつ|あたら|しごと|はじ"}],"notes":"来月 is the next calendar month after the current one. As a time expression it often stands without に. 来月ですか asks about a date, and 来月から marks the start of an activity that lasts for some time. The expression does not mean exactly \"in thirty days\"; the distance depends on today's date.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この地方には、古来の習慣が残っています。","romaji":"Kono chihō ni wa, korai no shūkan ga nokotte imasu.","german":"Customs from olden times remain in this region.","furigana":"ちほう|こらい|しゅうかん|のこ"},{"japanese":"この伝統は古来から続いています。","romaji":"Kono dentō wa korai kara tsuzuite imasu.","german":"This tradition has continued since ancient times.","furigana":"でんとう|こらい|つづ"}],"notes":"古来 means \"since ancient times, for a long time\" and usually sounds written or elevated. It suits traditional customs and traditions. 古来の習慣 is \"a custom that has existed since ancient times\". You also find 古来から; the から underlines the starting point in time. For a short personal \"for a while now\", 昔から is often more everyday.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"マイケルは、父の三十年来の友人です。","romaji":"Maikeru wa, chichi no sanjūnenrai no yūjin desu.","german":"Michael has been a friend of my father's for thirty years.","furigana":"ちち|さんじゅうねんらい|ゆうじん"},{"japanese":"十年来の友人です。","romaji":"Jūnenrai no yūjin desu.","german":"He has been a friend for ten years.","furigana":"じゅうねんらい|ゆうじん"},{"japanese":"十年来の夢が、やっと実現しました。","romaji":"Jūnenrai no yume ga, yatto jitsugen shimashita.","german":"A dream I have held for ten years has finally come true.","furigana":"じゅうねんらい|ゆめ|じつげん"}],"notes":"〜年来 links a number of years with a relationship or thing that has existed over that period: 三十年来の友人 \"a friend of thirty years\". The sign 〜 shows the place for a number and is not pronounced. の often follows. Even without a number, 年来 means \"existing for a long time\". Here it names the duration up to now, not a calendar year or a future deadline.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"社内にデザイナーがいます。","romaji":"Shanai ni dezainā ga imasu.","german":"The company has in-house designers.","furigana":"しゃない"},{"japanese":"社内のルールを守ってください。","romaji":"Shanai no rūru o mamotte kudasai.","german":"Please follow the company's internal rules.","furigana":"しゃない|まも"}],"notes":"社内 means \"inside the company, in-house\". 社内に names the internal area as a place, 社内のルール the company's internal rules. The word does not by itself say whether the whole operation is in a single building. Contrast 社外 \"outside the company\"; the identically read 車内, by contrast, means the interior of a vehicle.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"姉は来月その会社に入社します。","romaji":"Ane wa raigetsu sono kaisha ni nyūsha shimasu.","german":"My older sister joins that company next month.","furigana":"あね|らいげつ|かいしゃ|にゅうしゃ"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"四月に新卒社員が一斉に入社した。","romaji":"Shigatsu ni shinsotsu shain ga issei ni nyūsha shita.","german":"The new graduates all joined the company at the same time in April.","furigana":"しがつ|しんそつしゃいん|いっせい|にゅうしゃ"},{"japanese":"入社してから、もう五年になります。","romaji":"Nyuusha shite kara, mou go-nen ni narimasu.","german":"It has already been five years since I joined the company.","furigana":"にゅうしゃ|ごねん"}],"notes":"入社 means joining a company as an employee; as an intransitive する-verb, the company takes に: 会社に入社する. It stresses the formal start of employment, often in connection with 新卒 and the 入社式 in April. 就職 is more general, \"finding a job\", and is not limited to companies. The counterpart is 退社 (\"leaving the company\", but also \"finishing work for the day\") or 退職. Common compounds: 入社試験, 入社式, 中途入社.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"そのチーズは三角でした。","romaji":"Sono chīzu wa sankaku deshita.","german":"The cheese was triangular.","furigana":"さんかく"},{"japanese":"この形は三角です。","romaji":"Kono katachi wa sankaku desu.","german":"This shape is a triangle.","furigana":"かたち|さんかく"},{"japanese":"紙を三角に折ってください。","romaji":"Kami o sankaku ni otte kudasai.","german":"Please fold the paper into a triangle.","furigana":"かみ|さんかく|お"}],"notes":"三角 is a triangle or a triangular shape. You describe something with 三角です or use 三角の before a noun. With the cheese the shape is meant; the second statement names a geometric figure. 三角に折る means to fold something into a triangle. The word says nothing about equal sides: a triangle does not have to be equilateral.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"わたしのキーボードのキーは四角です。","romaji":"Watashi no kībōdo no kī wa shikaku desu.","german":"The keys on my keyboard are square.","furigana":"しかく"},{"japanese":"この箱は四角です。","romaji":"Kono hako wa shikaku desu.","german":"This box is square.","furigana":"はこ|しかく"},{"japanese":"ノートに四角を描いて、その中に名前を書きました。","romaji":"Nōto ni shikaku o kaite, sono naka ni namae o kakimashita.","german":"I drew a square in my notebook and wrote my name inside it.","furigana":"しかく|か|なか|なまえ|か"}],"notes":"四角 refers to a four-cornered shape and, depending on the object, can also mean a square. For exactly \"square\", 正方形 is clearer. 四角です describes the shape; 四角い is the related i-adjective. In this entry the reading is しかく, not the identically read 資格 \"qualification\". The examples show objects and the drawing of a figure.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"スタッフは「ありがとうございました」と、言う。","romaji":"Sutaffu wa \"arigatō gozaimashita\" to, iu.","german":"The staff member says, \"Thank you very much.\"","furigana":"い"},{"japanese":"はっきり言ってください。","romaji":"Hakkiri itte kudasai.","german":"Please say it clearly.","furigana":"い"}],"notes":"言う means \"to say\". Direct speech comes before と, as in 「ありがとう」と言う. This Godan verb forms 言います, 言って and 言った; despite its short reading, it is not an Ichidan verb. はっきり言う asks for a clear statement. In conversation the dictionary form often sounds like ゆう, but the dictionary reading practiced here remains いう.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 8"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"走行スピードを下げてください。","romaji":"Sōkō supīdo o sagete kudasai.","german":"Please reduce your driving speed.","furigana":"そうこう|さ"},{"japanese":"高速道路を走行しています。","romaji":"Kōsoku dōro o sōkō shite imasu.","german":"We are driving on the expressway.","furigana":"こうそくどうろ|そうこう"}],"notes":"走行 refers to driving or the movement of a vehicle. As a noun it appears in 走行スピード, for example; with する it becomes a verb: 走行しています. 高速道路を here names the route being driven; を is not marking an object that is changed. In everyday speech 車で走る often fits as well, while 走行 sounds more technical.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このレストランは近日オープンする。","romaji":"Kono resutoran wa kinjitsu ōpun suru.","german":"This restaurant will open soon.","furigana":"きんじつ"},{"japanese":"近日中にお知らせします。","romaji":"Kinjitsuchū ni oshirase shimasu.","german":"We will notify you shortly.","furigana":"きんじつちゅう|し"}],"notes":"近日 means \"soon, within the next few days\" and is often used in announcements. It does not yet name an exact date. 近日中に limits an announced action to the near future. オープンする means \"to open\" for a restaurant. For a binding appointment, a specific day or time is clearer.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"近年、日本のウィスキーがトレンドです。","romaji":"Kinnen, Nihon no uisukī ga torendo desu.","german":"In recent years, Japanese whisky has been trending.","furigana":"きんねん|にほん"},{"japanese":"近年、外国人観光客が増えました。","romaji":"Kinnen, gaikokujin kankōkyaku ga fuemashita.","german":"In recent years, the number of foreign tourists has increased.","furigana":"きんねん|がいこくじんかんこうきゃく|ふ"},{"japanese":"近年の研究で、新しいことが分かりました。","romaji":"Kinnen no kenkyū de, atarashii koto ga wakarimashita.","german":"Research in recent years has revealed new things.","furigana":"きんねん|けんきゅう|あたら|わ"}],"notes":"近年 means \"in recent years\". It suits developments over a longer recent period, not just yesterday or today. 近年の before a noun can refer, for example, to recent research. 最近 is more general in everyday speech for \"lately\". The reading きんねん contains two consecutive n sounds.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"２０１８年に男の子が生まれました。","romaji":"Nisen jūhachi-nen ni otoko no ko ga umaremashita.","german":"A boy was born in 2018.","furigana":"ねん|おとこ|こ|う"},{"japanese":"男の子が二人遊んでいます。","romaji":"Otoko no ko ga futari asonde imasu.","german":"Two boys are playing.","furigana":"おとこ|こ|ふたりあそ"}],"notes":"男の子 is a boy or a male baby. The fixed compound contains の, but in ordinary usage it does not simply mean \"a man's child\". With 生まれる, the boy is the child who was born; 二人 counts two boys and is read ふたり. For an adult man, 男の人 is the usual other term.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 10"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"キャベツの中に毛虫がいた。","romaji":"Kyabetsu no naka ni kemushi ga ita.","german":"There was a hairy caterpillar in the cabbage.","furigana":"なか|けむし"},{"japanese":"庭に毛虫がいます。","romaji":"Niwa ni kemushi ga imasu.","german":"There are hairy caterpillars in the garden.","furigana":"にわ|けむし"},{"japanese":"毛虫が葉を食べています。","romaji":"Kemushi ga ha o tabete imasu.","german":"A hairy caterpillar is eating a leaf.","furigana":"けむし|は|た"}],"notes":"毛虫 is a hairy caterpillar. 毛 means hair; there are other words for caterpillars in general, such as イモムシ. がいる names the animal in a place. 毛虫が葉を食べる describes its feeding, not its effect on people. The figurative dictionary meaning for an annoying person is not practiced here.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"父は下町で生まれた。","romaji":"Chichi wa shitamachi de umareta.","german":"My father was born in the old downtown district.","furigana":"ちち|したまち|う"},{"japanese":"下町を散歩しました。","romaji":"Shitamachi o sanpo shimashita.","german":"I took a walk through the old downtown district.","furigana":"したまち|さんぽ"}],"notes":"下町 traditionally refers to the lower-lying part of a city, characterized by shops, crafts and residential houses. It is not simply any city center or the modern business district. In Tokyo the term can especially mean the traditional eastern districts. The examples mention a place of origin and a walking route, without specifying a particular Tokyo district.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"田中先生は男の人です。","romaji":"Tanaka sensei wa otoko no hito desu.","german":"Tanaka-sensei is a man.","furigana":"たなかせんせい|おとこ|ひと"},{"japanese":"あの男の人は先生です。","romaji":"Ano otoko no hito wa sensei desu.","german":"That man is a teacher.","furigana":"おとこ|ひと|せんせい"},{"japanese":"男の人が駅で道を教えてくれました。","romaji":"Otoko no hito ga eki de michi o oshiete kuremashita.","german":"A man showed me the way at the station.","furigana":"おとこ|ひと|えき|みち|おし"}],"notes":"男の人 means a man and is a common, neutral description of a person. 男の子, by contrast, means \"boy\". The の belongs to the fixed compound; the whole term is understood as a unit. A sentence can identify the person or ask about what he does. When addressing someone directly, one usually uses a name or an appropriate role title.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 10"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今まで、ありがとう。","romaji":"Ima made, arigatō.","german":"Thank you for everything until now.","furigana":"いま"},{"japanese":"今まで何をしていましたか。","romaji":"Ima made nani o shite imashita ka.","german":"What have you been doing until now?","furigana":"いま|なに"}],"notes":"今まで means \"until now, so far\" and sets the present as the endpoint of a period. 今までありがとう can be a farewell thanks for the time spent together so far. 今まで何をしていましたか asks about activities up to this moment. With に one can refer to past experiences; that is a separate construction, not the meaning \"from now on\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ピンク色の毛糸を下さい。","romaji":"Pinkuiro no keito o kudasai.","german":"Please give me some pink yarn.","furigana":"いろ|けいと|くだ"},{"japanese":"毛糸でマフラーを編みました。","romaji":"Keito de mafurā o amimashita.","german":"I knitted a scarf with yarn.","furigana":"けいと|あ"}],"notes":"毛糸 is wool yarn for knitting or crocheting. It means a processed thread and not just any raw wool. ピンク色の毛糸 names the color of the yarn; 毛糸で編む names the material used. 編む forms the past tense 編みました. For a scarf, マフラー is the usual word in the example.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今すぐスーパーにいってきます。","romaji":"Ima sugu sūpā ni itte kimasu.","german":"I'm going to the supermarket right now and will be back.","furigana":"いま"},{"japanese":"今すぐ来てください。","romaji":"Ima sugu kite kudasai.","german":"Please come right away.","furigana":"いま|き"}],"notes":"今すぐ means \"right now\" and stresses that an action should begin without delay. It is often used as a time expression without any further particle. 今すぐ来てください can sound urgent depending on the situation. 行ってきます announces leaving with a later return; the return is implied in this phrase.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 18"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この里では、昔から米を作っています。","romaji":"Kono sato de wa, mukashi kara kome o tsukutte imasu.","german":"In this village, rice has been grown since long ago.","furigana":"さと|むかし|こめ|つく"},{"japanese":"お盆に里帰りします。","romaji":"Obon ni satogaeri shimasu.","german":"I go back to my hometown for Obon.","furigana":"ぼん|さとがえ"},{"japanese":"夏休みに、久しぶりに里に帰りました。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi ni, hisashiburi ni sato ni kaerimashita.","german":"During summer vacation, I went back to my hometown for the first time in a long while.","furigana":"なつやす|ひさ|さと|かえ"}],"notes":"里 can mean a village or one's home, that is, the family's home. It often sounds more traditional than 村 or 故郷. 里に帰る describes returning there. The fixed compound 里帰り means \"homecoming, visit to one's hometown\". 里 is not normally used on its own for just any animal habitat.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ちょっと里心がついちゃったんです。","romaji":"Chotto satogokoro ga tsuichatta n desu.","german":"I'm feeling a little homesick.","furigana":"さとごころ"},{"japanese":"里心がついて泣きました。","romaji":"Satogokoro ga tsuite nakimashita.","german":"I got homesick and cried.","furigana":"さとごころ|な"},{"japanese":"家族の写真を見ると、里心がつきます。","romaji":"Kazoku no shashin o miru to, satogokoro ga tsukimasu.","german":"When I look at family photos, I get homesick.","furigana":"かぞく|しゃしん|み|さとごころ"}],"notes":"里心 is homesickness or longing for one's hometown. The fixed expression 里心がつく means that this feeling arises. ついちゃった is the casual contraction of ついてしまった and describes the feeling that has arisen. With が the homesickness is named as a state that arises; 里心 is not itself a verb.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"それは本当ですか？","romaji":"Sore wa hontō desu ka?","german":"Is that true?","furigana":"ほんとう"},{"japanese":"本当のことを言ってください。","romaji":"Hontō no koto o itte kudasai.","german":"Please tell me the truth.","furigana":"ほんとう|い"}],"notes":"本当 means \"truth\", and 本当です means \"it is true\". Before a noun, の often follows: 本当のこと means \"the truth, what really happened\". 本当に strengthens a statement as \"really\"; by contrast, 本当ですか is a follow-up question about whether something is true. It is not an i-adjective and forms no 本当い here. The kana form ほんとう refers here to the same truth or reality.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 21"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あのコインパーキング、空車あるかな？","romaji":"Ano koin pākingu, kūsha aru ka na?","german":"I wonder if that coin parking lot has a free space.","furigana":"くうしゃ"},{"japanese":"空車のタクシーが来ました。","romaji":"Kūsha no takushī ga kimashita.","german":"A vacant taxi came.","furigana":"くうしゃ|き"}],"notes":"空車 can indicate an unoccupied taxi or free spaces in a parking lot. For a taxi, the sign means it can take passengers. For a parking facility, the available parking area is meant. The counterpart 満車 means complete occupancy in a parking context. The examples show these two different everyday situations.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"主人は一生のパートナーです。","romaji":"Shujin wa isshō no pātonā desu.","german":"My husband is my partner for life.","furigana":"しゅじん|いっしょう"},{"japanese":"一生の思い出になりました。","romaji":"Isshō no omoide ni narimashita.","german":"It became a memory for a lifetime.","furigana":"いっしょう|おも|で"},{"japanese":"この日のことは、一生忘れません。","romaji":"Kono hi no koto wa, isshō wasuremasen.","german":"I will never forget what happened on this day as long as I live.","furigana":"ひ|いっしょうわす"}],"notes":"一生 is a person's whole life. With の it describes a lifelong relationship or memory: 一生のパートナー, 一生の思い出. It can also limit an action in time without a particle, as in 一生忘れない. The small っ appears in the romanization as an extra s: isshō; 一緒 \"together\" is a different word.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの子の仕草は、まるで大人の仕草だ。","romaji":"Ano ko no shigusa wa, marude otona no shigusa da.","german":"That child's gestures are just like an adult's.","furigana":"こ|しぐさ|おとな|しぐさ"},{"japanese":"猫の仕草がかわいいです。","romaji":"Neko no shigusa ga kawaii desu.","german":"The cat's mannerisms are cute.","furigana":"ねこ|しぐさ"},{"japanese":"彼は、手を振る仕草で「こちらへ」と伝えました。","romaji":"Kare wa, te o furu shigusa de \"kochira e\" to tsutaemashita.","german":"He signaled \"Over here\" by waving his hand.","furigana":"かれ|て|ふ|しぐさ|つた"}],"notes":"仕草 is a visible movement, gesture or typical way of moving. It can describe people or animals. まるで大人の仕草だ compares the behavior to that of an adult. 仕草で shows that a gesture conveys something even without words. The focus here is on observed movements, not a full theatrical performance.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「出る」の「て形」は「出て」です。","romaji":"\"Deru\" no \"te-kei\" wa \"dete\" desu.","german":"The te-form of 「出る」 is 「出て」.","furigana":"で|けい|で"},{"japanese":"作文では「ます形」を使ってください。","romaji":"Sakubun de wa \"masu-kei\" o tsukatte kudasai.","german":"Please use the \"masu form\" in your essay.","furigana":"さくぶん|けい|つか"}],"notes":"〜形, read けい, denotes a form in compounds, especially a grammatical verb form: て形, ます形. The mark 〜 shows the place of the preceding element and is not pronounced. The reading here is not the standalone word for shape, かたち. For a round plate, 丸い形 with かたち is therefore correct, not this attached grammatical term. Without the placeholder mark, the element is written 形; in て形 and ます形 the reading is けい.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"わたしは日本人ですが、生まれはサンフランシスコです。","romaji":"Watashi wa Nihonjin desu ga, umare wa Sanfuranshisuko desu.","german":"I am Japanese, but I was born in San Francisco.","furigana":"にほんじん|う"},{"japanese":"生まれはどちらですか。","romaji":"Umare wa dochira desu ka.","german":"Where were you born?","furigana":"う"},{"japanese":"私の生まれは小さな村ですが、今は大きな町に住んでいます。","romaji":"Watashi no umare wa chiisana mura desu ga, ima wa ōkina machi ni sunde imasu.","german":"I was born in a small village, but now I live in a big city.","furigana":"わたし|う|ちい|むら|いま|おお|まち|す"}],"notes":"生まれ here is birth, or the birthplace, as a noun. 生まれはサンフランシスコです names the place, while 日本人 names the nationality; the two do not have to be the same country. 生まれはどちらですか asks specifically about the place of birth. In these sentences the form is not simply a truncated verb form of 生まれる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その他のゴミはリサイクルして下さい。","romaji":"Sonohoka no gomi wa risaikuru shite kudasai.","german":"Please recycle the rest of the trash.","furigana":"ほか|くだ"},{"japanese":"「その他のご質問はありますか。」","romaji":"Sonohoka no goshitsumon wa arimasu ka?","german":"Do you have any other questions?","furigana":"ほか|しつもん"},{"japanese":"店では、パンを売っています。その外、飲み物も買えます。","romaji":"Mise de wa, pan o utte imasu. Sonohoka, nomimono mo kaemasu.","german":"The shop sells bread. Besides that, you can also buy drinks.","furigana":"みせ|う|ほか|の|もの|か"}],"notes":"その他 means \"the rest\" or \"in addition\". This entry practices そのほか; especially in written lists そのた is also usual. Before a noun it takes の: その他のご質問, further questions. As an addition, そのほか can also introduce a further item. The spelling その外 is an attested variant, while そのほか explicitly shows the reading practiced here. What is left over or added comes from the context.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"エレベーターは西口にあります。","romaji":"Erebētā wa nishiguchi ni arimasu.","german":"The elevator is at the west exit.","furigana":"にしぐち"},{"japanese":"西口で待ち合わせしましょう。","romaji":"Nishiguchi de machiawase shimashō.","german":"Let's meet at the west exit.","furigana":"にしぐち|ま|あ"}],"notes":"西口 is the west exit or west entrance, often at a train station. The compound names a specific side of the building. 西口にあります gives the location of an elevator; 西口で待ち合わせる gives the meeting point of an appointment. In this compound, 口 is read ぐち. A family name written the same way is not part of the location usage practiced here.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日は三日月です。","romaji":"Kyō wa mikazuki desu.","german":"Today there is a crescent moon.","furigana":"きょう|みかづき"},{"japanese":"三日月がきれいですね。","romaji":"Mikazuki ga kirei desu ne.","german":"The crescent moon is beautiful, isn't it?","furigana":"みかづき"},{"japanese":"絵の隅に、小さな三日月を描きました。","romaji":"E no sumi ni, chīsana mikazuki o kakimashita.","german":"I drew a small crescent moon in the corner of the picture.","furigana":"え|すみ|ちい|みかづき|か"}],"notes":"三日月 is a slender crescent moon. The name contains 三日, but in ordinary use it names the visible shape of the moon, not a freely counted period of three days. The reading is みかづき, mikazuki in Hepburn. It can refer to the moon in the sky or to a drawn crescent; 月 remains part of the fixed word here.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"それ、おもしろそうだね！","romaji":"Sore, omoshirosō da ne!","german":"That looks interesting!"},{"japanese":"それはいくらですか。","romaji":"Sore wa ikura desu ka.","german":"How much is that?"}],"notes":"それ means \"that\" for something near the listener or something just mentioned in the conversation. It stands alone; before a noun you need その instead. それはいくらですか asks for the price of that item. 面白そう describes an impression. 其れ is a rare kanji spelling of the same word; それ in hiragana is the usual form. The examples practice the demonstrative, not an exclamation or a verb form.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今、デパートのトイレにいる。","romaji":"Ima, depāto no toire ni iru.","german":"I'm in the department store restroom right now.","furigana":"いま"},{"japanese":"デパートで服を買いました。","romaji":"Depāto de fuku o kaimashita.","german":"I bought clothes at the department store.","furigana":"ふく|か"}],"notes":"デパート is a department store with various departments. This shortened English loanword does not refer to a single department of a company here. デパートのトイレ names a facility in the department store; デパートで服を買う gives the place of shopping. に fits the location of presence in the first sentence, で the action carried out there in the second.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これ、あなたのブログですか？","romaji":"Kore, anata no burogu desu ka?","german":"Is this your blog?"},{"japanese":"あなたのお名前は何ですか。","romaji":"Anata no onamae wa nan desu ka.","german":"What is your name?","furigana":"なまえ|なん"}],"notes":"あなた means \"you\", but it is not automatically a polite form of address. In direct conversation, people often use the name, the job title, or omit the person altogether. あなたの here marks possession, that is, the person in question. The questions are understandable but can seem distant with unfamiliar people. The special intimate address between spouses is not the meaning of these examples. 貴方 and 貴女 are recorded kanji spellings of あなた. The kana form is the usual one; the kanji do not guarantee a polite tone.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"パパはテニスが上手い。","romaji":"Papa wa tenisu ga umai.","german":"Dad is good at tennis.","furigana":"うま"},{"japanese":"上手いですね！","romaji":"Umai desu ne!","german":"Well done!","furigana":"うま"},{"japanese":"姉は料理が上手いので、作り方を教えてもらいました。","romaji":"Ane wa ryōri ga umai node, tsukurikata o oshiete moraimashita.","german":"Since my older sister is good at cooking, she taught me how to make it.","furigana":"あね|りょうり|うま|つく|かた|おし"}],"notes":"上手い describes an ability here: テニスが上手い means \"to be good at tennis\". The reading is うまい, not じょうず; unlike 上手, 上手い is an i-adjective and forms 上手かった and 上手く. In casual conversation, 上手いですね can praise a skillful action. For this spelling we practice \"skilled\"; the other possible meaning \"delicious\" is often written うまい or 旨い.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの男は三十代です。","romaji":"Ano otoko wa sanjūdai desu.","german":"That man over there is in his thirties.","furigana":"おとこ|さんじゅうだい"},{"japanese":"彼は三十代です。","romaji":"Kare wa sanjūdai desu.","german":"He is in his thirties.","furigana":"かれ|さんじゅうだい"},{"japanese":"三十代になってから、ピアノを習い始めました。","romaji":"Sanjūdai ni natte kara, piano o naraihajimemashita.","german":"After I turned thirty, I started learning piano.","furigana":"さんじゅうだい|なら|はじ"}],"notes":"三十代 means the life stage from 30 to 39 years. The suffix 代 forms an age group here; \"exactly thirty years old\" is 三十歳. 三十代です places a person in this group, 三十代になってから looks back at the start of this life stage. The pronunciation is run together: さんじゅうだい.","levelNote":"Compound: 代 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"本の代金はカードではらいます。","romaji":"Hon no daikin wa kādo de haraimasu.","german":"I pay for the book by card.","furigana":"ほん|だいきん"},{"japanese":"代金を払ってください。","romaji":"Daikin o haratte kudasai.","german":"Please pay the amount.","furigana":"だいきん|はら"},{"japanese":"代金には送料が含まれています。","romaji":"Daikin ni wa sōryō ga fukumarete imasu.","german":"Shipping is included in the price.","furigana":"だいきん|そうりょう|ふく"}],"notes":"代金 is the amount one pays for goods or a service. 本の代金 names the purchase price of the book, 代金を払う the payment of this amount. With カードで the means of payment is stated. 代金に含まれる explains what is included in the amount; the shipping costs are called 送料. General expenses or financial outlay are more often described with 費用.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この糸は、すぐに切れる。","romaji":"Kono ito wa, sugu ni kireru.","german":"This thread snaps easily.","furigana":"いと|き"},{"japanese":"糸が切れてしまいました。","romaji":"Ito ga kirete shimaimashita.","german":"The thread snapped.","furigana":"いと|き"},{"japanese":"ビザの期限が来月切れます。","romaji":"Biza no kigen ga raigetsu kiremasu.","german":"My visa expires next month.","furigana":"きげん|らいげつき"}],"notes":"切れる is an intransitive Ichidan verb here: 糸が切れる means \"the thread snaps\". The affected thing takes が; someone who cuts something uses 切る with を instead. With 期限が切れる a deadline or validity expires. The forms are 切れます, 切れて and 切れない. 切れてしまった stresses the resulting, often undesired state.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"タイトルは、全て大文字でおねがいします。","romaji":"Taitoru wa, subete ōmoji de onegai shimasu.","german":"Please write the whole title in capital letters.","furigana":"すべ|おおもじ"},{"japanese":"名前は大文字で書いてください。","romaji":"Namae wa ōmoji de kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write your name in capital letters.","furigana":"なまえ|おおもじ|か"},{"japanese":"シフトキーを押しながら、大文字を入力します。","romaji":"Shifuto kī o oshinagara, ōmoji o nyūryoku shimasu.","german":"I type capital letters while holding down the Shift key.","furigana":"お|おおもじ|にゅうりょく"}],"notes":"大文字 is read おおもじ here and means capital letters, such as A instead of a. It is about letter case, not a larger font size. 大文字で書く uses で to indicate how something is written; when typing, the Shift key can help. The opposite term is 小文字. The first example's romanization therefore uses ōmoji.","levelNote":"Compound: 大 (N3) + 文字 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「ありがとう」と耳打ちした。","romaji":"\"Arigatō\" to mimiuchi shita.","german":"I whispered \"thank you\" in his ear.","furigana":"みみう"},{"japanese":"友達に秘密を耳打ちしました。","romaji":"Tomodachi ni himitsu o mimiuchi shimashita.","german":"I whispered a secret in my friend's ear.","furigana":"ともだち|ひみつ|みみう"}],"notes":"耳打ち refers to whispering directly into someone's ear. With する it becomes a verb: 耳打ちしました. The person is marked with に, and the whispered content with を or as a quotation before と. So you can whisper a short \"thank you\" or tell someone a secret. Any quiet voice without this closeness to the ear is not automatically 耳打ち.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"サワークリームをヨーグルトで代用しました。","romaji":"Sawākurīmu o yōguruto de daiyō shimashita.","german":"I substituted yogurt for sour cream.","furigana":"だいよう"},{"japanese":"バターの代用にマーガリンを使います。","romaji":"Batā no daiyō ni māgarin o tsukaimasu.","german":"I use margarine as a substitute for butter.","furigana":"だいよう|つか"},{"japanese":"枕がなかったので、タオルで代用しました。","romaji":"Makura ga nakatta node, taoru de daiyō shimashita.","german":"There was no pillow, so I used a towel instead.","furigana":"まくら|だいよう"}],"notes":"代用 means using something else as a substitute. In サワークリームをヨーグルトで代用する, the product being replaced comes before を and the substitute before で: yogurt replaces sour cream. Xの代用にYを使う expresses the same relationship as a noun. The word also fits outside recipes, for example when a towel temporarily replaces a pillow.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"父はビデオに写るのがきらいです。","romaji":"Chichi wa bideo ni utsuru no ga kirai desu.","german":"My father does not like appearing in videos.","furigana":"ちち|うつ"},{"japanese":"写真にきれいに写りました。","romaji":"Shashin ni kirei ni utsurimashita.","german":"I came out nicely in the photo.","furigana":"しゃしん|うつ"}],"notes":"写る means to appear in a photo or video. It is intransitive: the person shown is the subject, and 写真に or ビデオに names the medium. 写す, by contrast, is the verb for photographing or depicting someone or something. 写る ends in る but is a godan verb: 写ります, 写って, 写らない. The identically read 移る describes a change of place.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ホテルでミーティングを行うつもりです。","romaji":"Hoteru de mītingu o okonau tsumori desu.","german":"I intend to hold the meeting at the hotel.","furigana":"おこな"},{"japanese":"来月イベントを行います。","romaji":"Raigetsu ibento o okonaimasu.","german":"We will hold an event next month.","furigana":"らいげつ|おこな"},{"japanese":"実験を行って、結果をノートに書きました。","romaji":"Jikken o okonatte, kekka o nōto ni kakimashita.","german":"I conducted an experiment and wrote the results in my notebook.","furigana":"じっけん|おこな|けっか|か"}],"notes":"行う means \"to carry out\" or \"to conduct\" and sounds more formal than plain する. The action performed takes を: ミーティングを行う, 実験を行う. 場所で names the place of the activity. Despite the character 行, it is read おこなう here, not いく. This godan verb forms 行います, 行って and 行わない; the te-form is pronounced おこなって.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その車両は地下に入った。","romaji":"Sono sharyō wa chika ni haitta.","german":"The vehicle entered the underground area.","furigana":"しゃりょう|ちか|はい"},{"japanese":"地下に駐車場があります。","romaji":"Chika ni chūshajō ga arimasu.","german":"There is a parking lot in the basement.","furigana":"ちか|ちゅうしゃじょう"}],"notes":"地下 is a noun for the area beneath the ground or the basement level. 地下に入る names a destination, 地下にある a location. Before another noun, の is used, as in 地下の駐車場. The examples use the spatial meaning; political or secret activities are not described with it here.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"両手を上げて、バンザイをしました。","romaji":"Ryōte o agete, banzai o shimashita.","german":"They raised both hands and shouted banzai.","furigana":"りょうて|あ"},{"japanese":"両手で持ってください。","romaji":"Ryōte de motte kudasai.","german":"Please hold it with both hands.","furigana":"りょうて|も"}],"notes":"両手 means the two hands of a person. In 両手を上げる they are the object of the movement; 両手で持つ, on the other hand, names the means by which one holds something. 両 stands for both parts of a pair, not for any number of hands. The word is a noun; before a following noun it needs の.","levelNote":"Compound: 手 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"両日のスケジュールをメールして下さい。","romaji":"Ryōjitsu no sukejūru o mēru shite kudasai.","german":"Please email me the schedule for both days.","furigana":"りょうじつ|くだ"},{"japanese":"両日とも天気がよかったです。","romaji":"Ryōjitsu tomo tenki ga yokatta desu.","german":"The weather was good on both days.","furigana":"りょうじつ|てんき"}],"notes":"両日 refers to two already known days and means \"both days\" or \"on both days\". The word suits schedules and announcements in particular. 両日のスケジュール sums up the planning of both days, 両日とも stresses that a statement applies to both. The days need not be consecutive; 二日間, by contrast, describes a duration of two days.","levelNote":"Compound: 日 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"父も母も、両方ともフランス人です。","romaji":"Chichi mo haha mo, ryōhō tomo Furansujin desu.","german":"My father and my mother are both French.","furigana":"ちち|はは|りょうほう|じん"},{"japanese":"両方食べたいです。","romaji":"Ryōhō tabetai desu.","german":"I want to eat both.","furigana":"りょうほうた"}],"notes":"両方 refers to both members or sides of a group of two. 父も母も names the two people explicitly; 両方とも confirms that the statement applies to both. With two dishes, 両方食べたい can mean that you want both. Before another noun, の is used, as in 両方の店. For more than two things, 全部 is usually more suitable.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"玉ねぎは全て切りますか？","romaji":"Tamanegi wa subete kirimasu ka?","german":"Shall I cut all the onions?","furigana":"たま|すべ|き"},{"japanese":"全て終わりました。","romaji":"Subete owarimashita.","german":"Everything is finished.","furigana":"すべ|お"}],"notes":"全て refers to everything within a given group or to the full extent of an action. 全ての本 means \"all the books\": の is used before a noun. In 全て終わりました, the whole process in question is complete. 玉ねぎは全て切りますか asks whether all the onions on hand should be cut completely. すべて is often written in hiragana; it is not a na-adjective.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"会社まで全力で走りました。","romaji":"Kaisha made zenryoku de hashirimashita.","german":"I ran to the office with all my strength.","furigana":"かいしゃ|ぜんりょく|はし"},{"japanese":"全力で走りました。","romaji":"Zenryoku de hashirimashita.","german":"I ran as hard as I could.","furigana":"ぜんりょく|はし"},{"japanese":"友達の引っ越しを、全力で手伝いました。","romaji":"Tomodachi no hikkoshi o, zenryoku de tetsudaimashita.","german":"I helped my friend move with all my might.","furigana":"ともだち|ひ|こ|ぜんりょく|てつだ"}],"notes":"全力 is all of one's strength or the greatest possible effort. 全力で describes the manner of an action: doing it with everything one has. This can apply to physical running or committed help. The common pattern is 全力で plus a verb. The word is a noun, not a na-adjective.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"全日本柔道連盟から、お知らせが届きました。","romaji":"Zennihon jūdō renmei kara, oshirase ga todokimashita.","german":"A notice has arrived from the All Japan Judo Federation.","furigana":"ぜんにほんじゅうどうれんめい|し|とど"},{"japanese":"全日本選手権が始まります。","romaji":"Zennihon senshuken ga hajimarimasu.","german":"The All Japan Championships are about to begin.","furigana":"ぜんにほんせんしゅけん|はじ"}],"notes":"全日本 can mean \"all-Japan, nationwide\" before names of competitions or associations. 全日本選手権 is a national championship, 全日本柔道連盟 the All Japan Judo Federation. In both examples, 全日本 is part of a name with nationwide scope. Event titles may have a set pronunciation; here the dictionary reading ぜんにほん is kept.","levelNote":"Compound: 全 (N3) + 日本 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日は一日、平安な日でした。","romaji":"Kyō wa ichinichi, heian na hi deshita.","german":"Today was a peaceful day.","furigana":"きょう|いちにち|へいあん|ひ"},{"japanese":"平安な時代でした。","romaji":"Heian na jidai deshita.","german":"It was a peaceful era.","furigana":"へいあん|じだい"},{"japanese":"家族の平安を願っています。","romaji":"Kazoku no heian o negatte imasu.","german":"I wish peace and well-being for my family.","furigana":"かぞく|へいあん|ねが"}],"notes":"平安 means peace, tranquility or well-being and sounds rather solemn or literary. As a na-adjective it appears in 平安な日; as a noun one can say 家族の平安を願う, that is, to wish one's family peace and well-being. In everyday descriptions, 穏やか or 平和 often fit better. 平安な時代 here means a peaceful era, not the name of the historical 平安時代.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"九州に行ったことはありますか？","romaji":"Kyūshū ni itta koto wa arimasu ka?","german":"Have you ever been to Kyushu?","furigana":"きゅうしゅう|い"},{"japanese":"九州に旅行に行きました。","romaji":"Kyūshū ni ryokō ni ikimashita.","german":"I took a trip to Kyushu.","furigana":"きゅうしゅう|りょこう|い"},{"japanese":"九州の地図を買いました。","romaji":"Kyūshū no chizu o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a map of Kyushu.","furigana":"きゅうしゅう|ちず|か"}],"notes":"九州 is the name of one of Japan's four main islands. As a place name it takes に for a travel destination and の as in 九州の地図. 行ったことがありますか asks about past travel experience, while 旅行に行きました reports a specific trip. The long vowels in きゅうしゅう are rendered as Kyūshū.","levelNote":"Compound: 州 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"本州の人口は何人ですか？","romaji":"Honshū no jinkō wa nannin desu ka?","german":"What is the population of Honshu?","furigana":"ほんしゅう|じんこう|なんにん"},{"japanese":"本州は、日本で一番大きい島です。","romaji":"Honshū wa, Nihon de ichiban ōkii shima desu.","german":"Honshu is the largest island in Japan.","furigana":"ほんしゅう|にほん|いちばんおお|しま"}],"notes":"本州 is Honshu, the largest of Japan's four main islands. 本州の人口 asks about the population of this island. In size comparisons, 一番 needs a recognizable frame of reference; 日本で一番大きい島 therefore explicitly means \"the largest island in Japan\". 本州に行く names Honshu as a travel destination.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これはジャズの名曲です。","romaji":"Kore wa jazu no meikyoku desu.","german":"This is a jazz classic.","furigana":"めいきょく"},{"japanese":"この名曲を聴いてください。","romaji":"Kono meikyoku o kiite kudasai.","german":"Please listen to this masterpiece.","furigana":"めいきょく|き"}],"notes":"名曲 is a famous or especially successful piece of music. The word can honor a song as well as an instrumental piece; it is not a general word for masterpieces of painting or literature. ジャズの名曲 assigns the piece to a music genre. この名曲を聴いてください explicitly recommends a particular piece for listening.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"マヨネーズなら、まだ少し有る。","romaji":"Mayonēzu nara, mada sukoshi aru.","german":"Wir haben noch etwas Mayonnaise.","furigana":"すこ|あ"},{"japanese":"冷蔵庫にまだ牛乳が有ります。","romaji":"Reizōko ni mada gyūnyū ga arimasu.","german":"Im Kühlschrank ist noch Milch.","furigana":"れいぞうこ|ぎゅうにゅう|あ"},{"japanese":"今日は、話す時間が少し有ります。","romaji":"Kyō wa, hanasu jikan ga sukoshi arimasu.","german":"Heute habe ich ein wenig Zeit zum Reden.","furigana":"きょう|はな|じかん|すこ|あ"}],"notes":"有る beschreibt das Vorhandensein unbelebter Dinge und wird im Alltag meist ある geschrieben. Das Vorhandene steht mit が, der Ort mit に: 冷蔵庫に牛乳がある. Auch verfügbare Zeit kann man mit 時間がある ausdrücken; die deutsche Übersetzung lautet dann oft „Zeit haben“. Für Menschen oder Tiere verwendet man in ihrer gewöhnlichen Existenz いる. Die Verneinung ist unregelmäßig ない, höflich ありません, nicht あらない.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二人の死体は、山で見つかった。","romaji":"Futari no shitai wa, yama de mitsukatta.","german":"The bodies of the two people were found in the mountains.","furigana":"ふたり|したい|やま|み"},{"japanese":"道に死体がありました。","romaji":"Michi ni shitai ga arimashita.","german":"There was a corpse on the road.","furigana":"みち|したい"},{"japanese":"警察が死体を調べています。","romaji":"Keisatsu ga shitai o shirabete imasu.","german":"The police are examining the body.","furigana":"けいさつ|したい|しら"}],"notes":"死体 is a noun for a dead body, that is, a corpse. In reports you can say 死体が見つかった; 見つかる means that something is found. Because the body is no longer alive, 死体がある is the fitting phrase here. 死亡, by contrast, refers to dying or death as an event. For two people, the counter 二人 is read ふたり here.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今回のフライトで、死亡した人はいませんでした。","romaji":"Konkai no furaito de, shibō shita hito wa imasen deshita.","german":"Nobody died on this flight.","furigana":"こんかい|しぼう|ひと"},{"japanese":"事故で一人が死亡しました。","romaji":"Jiko de hitori ga shibō shimashita.","german":"One person died in the accident.","furigana":"じこ|ひとり|しぼう"},{"japanese":"書類に、死亡した日を記入します。","romaji":"Shorui ni, shibō shita hi o kinyū shimasu.","german":"I write the date of death on the document.","furigana":"しょるい|しぼう|ひ|きにゅう"}],"notes":"死亡 means death and sounds factual, for example in news or documents. With する it becomes the intransitive 死亡する: 事故で一人が死亡した reports that someone died in an accident. The person takes が, and 事故で names the circumstance. In everyday personal speech you often hear 死ぬ; 死亡した日 fits, for example, giving a date on a form.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"クリスマスプレゼントに、羊毛のブランケットをもらいました。","romaji":"Kurisumasu purezento ni, yōmō no buranketto o moraimashita.","german":"I received a wool blanket as a Christmas present.","furigana":"ようもう"},{"japanese":"このセーターは羊毛でできています。","romaji":"Kono sētā wa yōmō de dekite imasu.","german":"This sweater is made of wool.","furigana":"ようもう"},{"japanese":"羊毛を洗ってから、糸にします。","romaji":"Yōmō o aratte kara, ito ni shimasu.","german":"I wash the wool and then spin it into thread.","furigana":"ようもう|あら|いと"}],"notes":"羊毛 is wool from sheep. As a material it can modify a product with の: 羊毛のブランケット. 羊毛でできている explains what an object is made of. The word names the fiber itself; 毛糸, by contrast, means yarn, as used for knitting. When processed, 羊毛 can become 糸.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"中国の人口は日本よりも大きい。","romaji":"Chūgoku no jinkō wa Nihon yori mo ōkii.","german":"China's population is larger than Japan's.","furigana":"ちゅうごく|じんこう|にほん|おお"},{"japanese":"中国は大きい国です。","romaji":"Chūgoku wa ōkii kuni desu.","german":"China is a big country.","furigana":"ちゅうごく|おお|くに"},{"japanese":"中国の友達から、手紙が来ました。","romaji":"Chūgoku no tomodachi kara, tegami ga kimashita.","german":"I got a letter from a friend in China.","furigana":"ちゅうごく|ともだち|てがみ|き"}],"notes":"中国 means the country China in this entry. 中国の人口 gives the population, 中国の友達 links the country to a person. The same word with the same reading can also refer to Japan's Chūgoku region; for travel plans, the context must therefore be clear. Here the examples refer to the country.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"全国の図書館を地図で調べました。","romaji":"Zenkoku no toshokan o chizu de shirabemashita.","german":"Ich habe Bibliotheken im ganzen Land auf einer Karte gesucht.","furigana":"ぜんこく|としょかん|ちず|しら"},{"japanese":"全国で雨が降っています。","romaji":"Zenkoku de ame ga futte imasu.","german":"Im ganzen Land regnet es.","furigana":"ぜんこく|あめ|ふ"}],"notes":"全国 bezeichnet das ganze Land und ist ein Nomen. 全国で nennt das Land als Gebiet einer Handlung oder eines Geschehens; 全国の図書館 meint Bibliotheken im ganzen Land. Als deutsche Übersetzung passt je nach Satz auch „landesweit“. Das Wort selbst sagt nicht, um welches Land es geht.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"四国にはまだ行ったことがありません。","romaji":"Shikoku ni wa mada itta koto ga arimasen.","german":"I have never been to Shikoku.","furigana":"しこく|い"},{"japanese":"四国の地図を買いました。","romaji":"Shikoku no chizu o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a map of Shikoku.","furigana":"しこく|ちず|か"}],"notes":"四国 is the name of one of Japan's four main islands. The whole word is read しこく. 四国に行く names the island as a travel destination; 四国の地図 links the name to a map. With 行ったことがありません, one says that one has not yet had this trip experience.","levelNote":"Compound: 国 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"まだ外国に行ったことがありません。","romaji":"Mada gaikoku ni itta koto ga arimasen.","german":"I have never been abroad.","furigana":"がいこく|い"},{"japanese":"外国の切手を集めています。","romaji":"Gaikoku no kitte o atsumete imasu.","german":"I collect foreign stamps.","furigana":"がいこく|きって|あつ"}],"notes":"外国 is another country, or abroad, from the speaker's point of view. It is a noun: 外国に行く names a travel destination, 外国の切手 describes stamps from other countries. 外国に行ったことがない reports a lack of experience abroad; がいこく has no long vowel.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 11"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"六月に外国人の友人が日本に来ます。","romaji":"Rokugatsu ni gaikokujin no yūjin ga Nihon ni kimasu.","german":"In June, a friend from abroad is coming to Japan.","furigana":"ろくがつ|がいこくじん|ゆうじん|にほん|き"},{"japanese":"この町には外国人が多いです。","romaji":"Kono machi ni wa gaikokujin ga ōi desu.","german":"There are many foreigners in this town.","furigana":"まち|がいこくじん|おお"}],"notes":"外国人 is a person from another country; which country counts as \"foreign\" depends on the context. 人 is read じん here. 外国人の友人 links the origin to a friend. The word does not describe a particular language or region of origin.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 15"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"主人は米国にいます。","romaji":"Shujin wa Beikoku ni imasu.","german":"My husband is in the United States.","furigana":"しゅじん|べいこく"},{"japanese":"米国の大学のパンフレットを読みました。","romaji":"Beikoku no daigaku no panfuretto o yomimashita.","german":"I read the brochure of a university in the United States.","furigana":"べいこく|だいがく|よ"}],"notes":"米国 is a term for the United States and is a noun. It is read べいこく; 米 has the reading べい here. 米国にいる names a person's location, 米国の大学 a university there. The expression is often found in written texts and news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの二人って姉妹みたいだよね。","romaji":"Ano futari tte shimai mitai da yo ne.","german":"Those two look like sisters, don't they?","furigana":"ふたり|しまい"},{"japanese":"姉妹がいますか。","romaji":"Shimai ga imasu ka.","german":"Do you have sisters?","furigana":"しまい"}],"notes":"姉妹 refers to sisters collectively, without naming each one as an older or younger sister. The reading is しまい. 姉妹みたい describes an impression or comparison, not necessarily a family relationship. 姉妹がいますか, by contrast, asks about sisters that actually exist.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 38"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"後でホテルに来て下さい。","romaji":"Ato de hoteru ni kite kudasai.","german":"Please come to the hotel later.","furigana":"あと|き|くだ"},{"japanese":"後で電話します。","romaji":"Ato de denwa shimasu.","german":"I'll call you later.","furigana":"あと|でんわ"}],"notes":"後で means \"later\" or \"afterwards\". Without further detail, it leaves the time open: 後で電話します. 食事の後で would explicitly be \"after the meal\". In 後でホテルに来てください, で belongs to the time expression; ホテルに names the destination of the coming.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 6"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"海外に行きたいなぁ。","romaji":"Kaigai ni ikitai nā.","german":"I'd like to go abroad.","furigana":"かいがい|い"},{"japanese":"海外のニュースを読みました。","romaji":"Kaigai no nyūsu o yomimashita.","german":"I read news from overseas.","furigana":"かいがい|よ"}],"notes":"海外 refers to foreign countries, that is, regions beyond one's own country. It is a noun: 海外に行く names the travel destination, and 海外のニュース means news from abroad. Although 海 means \"sea\", present-day usage does not presuppose any particular trip by ship.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 35"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私のカバンは茶色です。","romaji":"Watashi no kaban wa chairo desu.","german":"My bag is brown.","furigana":"わたし|ちゃいろ"},{"japanese":"茶色の靴を買いました。","romaji":"Chairo no kutsu o kaimashita.","german":"I bought brown shoes.","furigana":"ちゃいろ|くつ|か"}],"notes":"茶色 is the color brown. As a color noun, it takes の before a thing: 茶色の靴. In 私のカバンは茶色です, the color is stated with です. The reading is ちゃいろ; ちゃ forms a single syllable. The related i-adjective 茶色い has an additional い at the end.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 34"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先月、会社を首になりました。","romaji":"Sengetsu, kaisha o kubi ni narimashita.","german":"Last month, I got fired from the company.","furigana":"せんげつ|かいしゃ|くび"},{"japanese":"首にならないように、仕事を丁寧にしています。","romaji":"Kubi ni naranai yō ni, shigoto o teinei ni shite imasu.","german":"I do my work carefully so that I don't get fired.","furigana":"くび|しごと|ていねい"}],"notes":"首になる is colloquial for being dismissed or \"fired\". In 会社を首になる, を marks the company one has to leave; it is not the object of an action on the neck. The なる part conjugates as a Godan verb: 首になりました, 首にならない. In formal conversations, \"fired\" as a translation may sound correspondingly harsh.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"去年、母が亡くなった。","romaji":"Kyonen, haha ga nakunatta.","german":"My mother passed away last year.","furigana":"きょねん|はは|な"},{"japanese":"亡くなった祖父の写真を見ました。","romaji":"Nakunatta sofu no shashin o mimashita.","german":"I looked at a photo of my late grandfather.","furigana":"な|そふ|しゃしん|み"}],"notes":"亡くなる is a gentle term for \"to die\", especially when talking about people. This Godan verb is intransitive: the deceased person takes が. 亡くなった祖父 describes the late grandfather. The same reading なくなる, when written 無くなる, can instead mean the disappearance of a thing.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 23"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"メールアドレスは、全て小文字で入力して下さい。","romaji":"Mēru adoresu wa, subete komoji de nyūryoku shite kudasai.","german":"Please enter the whole email address in lowercase.","furigana":"すべ|こもじ|にゅうりょく|くだ"},{"japanese":"この文字は小文字のエーです。","romaji":"Kono moji wa komoji no ē desu.","german":"This character is a lowercase a.","furigana":"もじ|こもじ"}],"notes":"小文字 here means lowercase letters such as a, as opposed to capital letters such as A. It is about the letter form, not merely the font size. 小文字で入力する states the form when typing; 小文字のエー describes a small a. The full reading is こもじ.","levelNote":"Compound: 小 (N3) + 文字 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"冬休みは来週からです。","romaji":"Fuyuyasumi wa raishū kara desu.","german":"Winter vacation starts next week.","furigana":"ふゆやす|らいしゅう"},{"japanese":"冬休みにスキーに行きます。","romaji":"Fuyuyasumi ni sukī ni ikimasu.","german":"I'm going skiing during winter vacation.","furigana":"ふゆやす|い"}],"notes":"冬休み is the winter vacation, or a wintry holiday period. 冬休みは来週からです gives its start; 冬休みにスキーに行く names it as the period of a planned activity. The word itself does not fix particular months or vacation dates.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この漢字を読むことが出来ます。","romaji":"Kono kanji o yomu koto ga dekimasu.","german":"I can read this kanji.","furigana":"かんじ|よ|でき"},{"japanese":"料理が出来ました。","romaji":"Ryōri ga dekimashita.","german":"The meal is ready.","furigana":"りょうり|でき"}],"notes":"出来る is an intransitive Ichidan verb: 出来ます, 出来て. With verb + ことが出来る, one talks about ability or possibility. In 料理が出来ました, by contrast, it means the meal is finished. These two meanings depend on the sentence. The word is often written in hiragana: できる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"兄は、とても早口です。","romaji":"Ani wa, totemo hayakuchi desu.","german":"My older brother talks very fast.","furigana":"あに|はやくち"},{"japanese":"早口で話さないでください。","romaji":"Hayakuchi de hanasanaide kudasai.","german":"Please do not speak so fast.","furigana":"はやくち|はな"}],"notes":"早口 means fast speech or a fast way of speaking. It is a noun: 兄は早口です describes the brother's way of speaking. 早口で話す states the manner of speaking; 早口で話さないでください asks for a slower pace. The word does not by itself mean the person as a \"fast talker\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お金が十円足りないんです。","romaji":"Okane ga jūen tarinai n desu.","german":"I'm ten yen short.","furigana":"かね|じゅうえんた"},{"japanese":"時間が足りないので、続きは明日読みます。","romaji":"Jikan ga tarinai node, tsuzuki wa ashita yomimasu.","german":"There isn't enough time, so I'll read the rest tomorrow.","furigana":"じかん|た|つづ|あしたよ"}],"notes":"足りない means \"to be not enough, to be lacking\". The thing that is insufficient takes が: お金が足りない. An amount such as 十円 says how much is missing. The expression is the negative form of 足りる; politely it is 足りません. ので can give the missing supply or time as a reason.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"元パートナーは、私より年上です。","romaji":"Moto pātonā wa, watashi yori toshiue desu.","german":"My former partner is older than me.","furigana":"もと|わたし|としうえ"},{"japanese":"年上の友達に相談しました。","romaji":"Toshiue no tomodachi ni sōdan shimashita.","german":"I asked an older friend for advice.","furigana":"としうえ|ともだち|そうだん"}],"notes":"年上 describes a higher age relative to another person. 私より年上 means \"older than me\". As a noun, it modifies a person with の: 年上の友達. It refers to age, not automatically to professional experience or a higher position.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今年はダイエットをしたいです。","romaji":"Kotoshi wa daietto o shitai desu.","german":"This year I want to go on a diet.","furigana":"ことし"},{"japanese":"今年は暑い夏でした。","romaji":"Kotoshi wa atsui natsu deshita.","german":"This year the summer was hot.","furigana":"ことし|あつ|なつ"}],"notes":"今年 is the calendar year in which one is speaking. 今年は sets this year as the topic of an intention or a retrospective. The reading ことし must be learned as a whole. A statement about a past summer can still contain 今年 if that summer belongs to the current year.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 10"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このクラスは私より年下の子が多いです。","romaji":"Kono kurasu wa watashi yori toshishita no ko ga ōi desu.","german":"There are many children younger than me in this class.","furigana":"わたし|としした|こ|おお"},{"japanese":"弟は三歳年下です。","romaji":"Otōto wa sansai toshishita desu.","german":"My younger brother is three years younger than me.","furigana":"おとうと|さんさいとしした"}],"notes":"年下 describes a lower age compared with another person. 年下の子 links the age relationship to a child. In 三歳年下, 三歳 gives the difference: three years younger. Who is meant as the point of comparison is stated with より or is clear from the situation.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"東口にはコンビニがありますか？","romaji":"Higashiguchi ni wa konbini ga arimasu ka?","german":"Is there a convenience store at the east exit?","furigana":"ひがしぐち"},{"japanese":"東口から出てください。","romaji":"Higashiguchi kara dete kudasai.","german":"Please go out through the east exit.","furigana":"ひがしぐち|で"}],"notes":"東口 is the entrance or exit on the east side, for example of a train station. 東 is read ひがし and 口 is read ぐち here. 東口にある names something at this place; 東口から出る describes where one exits. Whether one goes in or out is determined by the sentence.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"こっちを向いてください。","romaji":"Kotchi o muite kudasai.","german":"Please turn this way.","furigana":"む"},{"japanese":"この窓は南を向いています。","romaji":"Kono mado wa minami o muite imasu.","german":"This window faces south.","furigana":"まど|みなみ|む"}],"notes":"向く can describe a person turning or the orientation of an object. This godan verb forms 向きます, 向いて. こっちを向く means to turn this way; 南を向く means to face south. In these sentences, を marks the direction of the gaze or orientation.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私生活のストレスが大きい。","romaji":"Shiseikatsu no sutoresu ga ōkii.","german":"The stress in my private life is great.","furigana":"しせいかつ|おお"},{"japanese":"私生活を大切にしたいです。","romaji":"Shiseikatsu o taisetsu ni shitai desu.","german":"I want to value my private life.","furigana":"しせいかつ|たいせつ"}],"notes":"私生活 is private life, such as personal relationships and everyday life outside work. In this compound, 私 is read し. 私生活のストレス links an area of strain to private life. 私生活を大切にする means giving importance and attention to this part of life.","levelNote":"Compound: 私 (N5) + 生活 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私立の大学に入りたいです。","romaji":"Shiritsu no daigaku ni hairitai desu.","german":"I want to enter a private university.","furigana":"しりつ|だいがく|はい"},{"japanese":"これは私立の学校です。","romaji":"Kore wa shiritsu no gakkō desu.","german":"This is a private school.","furigana":"しりつ|がっこう"}],"notes":"私立 refers to an institution under private management, especially schools and universities. As a noun it connects with の: 私立の学校. The reading しりつ sounds like 市立 \"municipal\"; to tell them apart, 私立 is sometimes also called わたくしりつ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"目の前に青い海が広がっています。","romaji":"Me no mae ni aoi umi ga hirogatte imasu.","german":"The blue sea stretches out before my eyes.","furigana":"め|まえ|あお|うみ|ひろ"},{"japanese":"噂が広がりました。","romaji":"Uwasa ga hirogarimashita.","german":"The rumor spread.","furigana":"うわさ|ひろ"}],"notes":"広がる is an intransitive godan verb: 広がります, 広がって. It describes spatial expansion or spreading, including of rumors. With が you name what expands or spreads. The transitive 広げる, by contrast, describes someone extending or spreading something.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は豚肉を食べません。","romaji":"Watashi wa butaniku o tabemasen.","german":"I don't eat pork.","furigana":"わたし|ぶたにく|た"},{"japanese":"豚肉でカツを作りましょう。","romaji":"Butaniku de katsu o tsukurimashō.","german":"Let's make cutlets from pork.","furigana":"ぶたにく|つく"}],"notes":"豚肉 is pork and a noun. When eating, it takes を as an object: 豚肉を食べる. In 豚肉でカツを作る, on the other hand, で names the meat as an ingredient or material. The reading ぶたにく combines ぶた \"pig\" with にく \"meat\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 12"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"宝くじを買いました。","romaji":"Takarakuji o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a lottery ticket.","furigana":"たから|か"},{"japanese":"宝くじに当たりました。","romaji":"Takarakuji ni atarimashita.","german":"I won the lottery.","furigana":"たから|あ"}],"notes":"宝くじ can refer to the lottery or to a single lottery ticket. With 宝くじを買う you buy a ticket; 宝くじに当たる means to win the lottery. The reading is たからくじ. Having bought a ticket says nothing yet about winning.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 32"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あと一歩でゴールです。","romaji":"Ato ippo de gōru desu.","german":"The goal is just one step away.","furigana":"いっぽ"},{"japanese":"一歩ずつ前に進みましょう。","romaji":"Ippo zutsu mae ni susumimashō.","german":"Let's move forward step by step.","furigana":"いっぽ|まえ|すす"}],"notes":"一歩 is one step. The reading いっぽ contains a small っ and a p, not an h. あと一歩 names the one remaining step; 一歩ずつ means \"step by step\". 前に進む states the direction in which one keeps going.","levelNote":"Compound: 歩 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この車両は大きいですね。","romaji":"Kono sharyō wa ōkii desu ne.","german":"This car is big, isn't it?","furigana":"しゃりょう|おお"},{"japanese":"車両のドアが閉まりました。","romaji":"Sharyō no doa ga shimarimashita.","german":"The door of the car closed.","furigana":"しゃりょう|し"}],"notes":"車両 means a vehicle or a car of a train. The reading しゃりょう ends with a long o. この車両は大きいです describes a particular car; 車両のドア links the word with its door. When counting railway cars, 両 is usually used, as in 十両.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"平日は早く会社に行きます。","romaji":"Heijitsu wa hayaku kaisha ni ikimasu.","german":"On weekdays I go to the office early.","furigana":"へいじつ|はや|かいしゃ|い"},{"japanese":"早く来てください。","romaji":"Hayaku kite kudasai.","german":"Please come quickly.","furigana":"はや|き"}],"notes":"早く can mean \"early\" or \"quickly\". 早く会社に行く can refer to an early time; with 早く来てください one usually asks someone to come quickly. It is the ku-form of 早い. For speed, the spelling 速く is also seen. The situation shows which meaning is intended.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 10"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"社長室のドアをノックした。","romaji":"Shachōshitsu no doa o nokku shita.","german":"I knocked on the door of the president's office.","furigana":"しゃちょうしつ"},{"japanese":"会議室はどこですか。","romaji":"Kaigishitsu wa doko desu ka.","german":"Where is the meeting room?","furigana":"かいぎしつ"}],"notes":"〜室 is a word element at the end of a room name: 会議室 \"meeting room\", 社長室 \"president's office\". 〜 marks the spot for the preceding part of the word. 室 is read しつ. The particle follows the whole room word, as in 会議室は or 社長室のドア. The spelling without the placeholder is 室; 〜 stands here for the preceding part of the word.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"草地に大きな木があった。","romaji":"Kusachi ni ōkina ki ga atta.","german":"There was a big tree in the meadow.","furigana":"くさち|おお|き"},{"japanese":"草地に座りましょう。","romaji":"Kusachi ni suwarimashō.","german":"Let's sit on the grass.","furigana":"くさち|すわ"}],"notes":"草地 is an area covered with grass, such as a meadow. 草地に names it as a place; 草地の木 is a tree on that area. The reading is くさち. 座りましょう suggests sitting down together, without saying that one is already seated.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"国王の写真を見ました。","romaji":"Kokuō no shashin o mimashita.","german":"I looked at a photo of the king.","furigana":"こくおう|しゃしん|み"},{"japanese":"この物語では、国王が城に来ます。","romaji":"Kono monogatari de wa, kokuō ga shiro ni kimasu.","german":"In this story, the king comes to the castle.","furigana":"ものがたり|こくおう|しろ|き"}],"notes":"国王 refers to the head of a monarchy, usually translated as \"king\". It is a noun: 国王の写真 is a photo of the king, and 国王が来る names him as the one acting. こくおう is read with a long o at the end. By itself, the word does not name a particular country.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"月は、水星より小さい。","romaji":"Tsuki wa, suisei yori chīsai.","german":"The moon is smaller than Mercury.","furigana":"つき|すいせい|ちい"},{"japanese":"水星の絵を描きました。","romaji":"Suisei no e o kakimashita.","german":"I drew a picture of Mercury.","furigana":"すいせい|え|か"}],"notes":"水星 is the name of the planet Mercury. It is read すいせい; here it does not mean the \"comet\" 彗星, which is also read すいせい. 水星より names Mercury as the basis of comparison. 水星の絵 connects the planet's name with a drawing.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あそこに見えるのが、金星です。","romaji":"Asoko ni mieru no ga, kinsei desu.","german":"That thing you can see over there is Venus.","furigana":"み|きんせい"},{"japanese":"金星の写真を見ました。","romaji":"Kinsei no shashin o mimashita.","german":"I looked at a photo of Venus.","furigana":"きんせい|しゃしん|み"}],"notes":"金星 is the planet Venus and is read きんせい. The same spelling can mean a special victory when read きんぼし; this card practices the planet name. あそこに見えるのが金星です identifies a visible celestial body. 金星の写真, on the other hand, names a picture of it.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この木にはキノコが生える。","romaji":"Kono ki ni wa kinoko ga haeru.","german":"Mushrooms grow on this tree.","furigana":"き|は"},{"japanese":"赤ちゃんに歯が生えました。","romaji":"Akachan ni ha ga haemashita.","german":"The baby got a tooth.","furigana":"あか|は|は"}],"notes":"生える describes something growing out of a surface or emerging. It is an intransitive ichidan verb: 生えます, 生えて. With が you name what grows, and with に the place: 木にキノコが生える. For the first emergence of teeth, too, you say 歯が生える.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"図鑑で土星を調べました。","romaji":"Zukan de dosei o shirabemashita.","german":"I looked up Saturn in a reference book.","furigana":"ずかん|どせい|しら"},{"japanese":"土星には輪があります。","romaji":"Dosei ni wa wa ga arimasu.","german":"Saturn has rings.","furigana":"どせい|わ"}],"notes":"土星 is the planet Saturn. 土 is read ど here, and 星 せい. 土星を調べる makes the planet the object of the research; 土星には輪がある describes its rings. The spelling is a planet name and not a free description of earth or stars.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この町は、とても活気があります。","romaji":"Kono machi wa, totemo kakki ga arimasu.","german":"This town is very lively.","furigana":"まち|かっき"},{"japanese":"祭りの日、店に活気が戻りました。","romaji":"Matsuri no hi, mise ni kakki ga modorimashita.","german":"On the day of the festival, life returned to the shop.","furigana":"まつ|ひ|みせ|かっき|もど"}],"notes":"活気 means liveliness or vigorous activity, for example in a town or a shop. With 活気がある you say that a place is lively. 活気が戻る describes the liveliness returning. The small っ in かっき lengthens the k sound.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地中には、たくさんの虫がいます。","romaji":"Chichū ni wa, takusan no mushi ga imasu.","german":"There are many insects in the ground.","furigana":"ちちゅう|むし"},{"japanese":"地中にケーブルを埋めました。","romaji":"Chichū ni kēburu o umemashita.","german":"I buried a cable in the ground.","furigana":"ちちゅう|う"}],"notes":"地中 is the area inside the soil. As a place noun, it combines with に: 地中に虫がいる. 地中に埋める names the ground as the destination of burying. It is read ちちゅう with a long u; the word describes the position below the surface of the earth.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日は一人で出社ですか？","romaji":"Kyō wa hitori de shussha desu ka?","german":"Are you going to the office alone today?","furigana":"きょう|ひとり|しゅっしゃ"},{"japanese":"毎朝八時に出社します。","romaji":"Maiasa hachiji ni shussha shimasu.","german":"I go to work at eight every morning.","furigana":"まいあさはちじ|しゅっしゃ"},{"japanese":"在宅勤務が終わり、来月から毎日出社することになった。","romaji":"Zaitaku kinmu ga owari, raigetsu kara mainichi shussha suru koto ni natta.","german":"Working from home is over, and from next month I have to go to the office every day.","furigana":"ざいたくきんむ|お|らいげつ|まいにちしゅっしゃ"}],"notes":"出社 means \"to come to the office/company\" and is used almost only in a work context. As an intransitive する-verb, the time takes に: 九時に出社する. Nearly synonymous is 出勤 (\"to report for duty\"), which also fits work outside an office, whereas 出社 refers to the company; 会社に出社する is therefore redundant. Since the pandemic it is often set against 在宅勤務 or リモートワーク. The opposite is 退社 - but note that 退社 can also mean \"to leave the company\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"元パートナーからテキストがきた。","romaji":"Moto pātonā kara tekisuto ga kita.","german":"I got a text from my ex-partner.","furigana":"もと"},{"japanese":"元パートナーの本を返しました。","romaji":"Moto pātonā no hon o kaeshimashita.","german":"I returned my ex-partner's book.","furigana":"もと|ほん|かえ"}],"notes":"元パートナー is a former partner. 元 is read もと here and comes before パートナー. It can mean a former personal or business partnership; the context clarifies which. から names the sender of a message. の links the person to a thing, as in 元パートナーの本 \"the former partner's book\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"オレンジ色の石ころが川に落ちた。","romaji":"Orenji iro no ishikoro ga kawa ni ochita.","german":"An orange stone fell into the river.","furigana":"いろ|いし|かわ|お"},{"japanese":"子供が石ころを拾いました。","romaji":"Kodomo ga ishikoro o hiroimashita.","german":"The child picked up a pebble.","furigana":"こども|いし|ひろ"}],"notes":"石ころ is a small stone or pebble. In 石ころが落ちる the stone is the subject of falling; in 石ころを拾う someone picks it up. The reading is いしころ. The word does not refer to a particular type or color of stone.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いいよ、他の人にしてもらうから！","romaji":"Ii yo, hoka no hito ni shite morau kara!","german":"That's fine, I'll have someone else do it!","furigana":"ほか|ひと"},{"japanese":"他の人のカバンを間違えて持って帰りました。","romaji":"Hoka no hito no kaban o machigaete motte kaerimashita.","german":"I took someone else's bag home by mistake.","furigana":"ほか|ひと|まちが|も|かえ"}],"notes":"他の人 means another person or other people. の links 他 with 人; in this phrase it is read ほかのひと. The other people need not be unknown strangers. 他の人にしてもらう means to have someone else do something; 他の人のカバン names the owner of a bag.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一千万ドルがほしいなぁ。","romaji":"Issenman doru ga hoshii nā.","german":"I wish I had ten million dollars.","furigana":"いっせんまん"},{"japanese":"この国には一千万人が住んでいます。","romaji":"Kono kuni ni wa issenman nin ga sunde imasu.","german":"Ten million people live in this country.","furigana":"くに|いっせんまんにん|す"}],"notes":"一千万 is ten million: 千 \"thousand\" is combined here with 万 \"ten thousand\". The reading is いっせんまん with a small っ. The unit follows the number, as in 一千万ドル or 一千万人. With 一千万円, an audible n comes before えん.","levelNote":"Compound: 万 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この人、だれ？","romaji":"Kono hito, dare?","german":"Who is this person?","furigana":"ひと"},{"japanese":"この映画は面白いです。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa omoshiroi desu.","german":"This movie is interesting.","furigana":"えいが|おもしろ"}],"notes":"この comes directly before a noun and points to something near the speaker: この人, この映画. It is not connected with な and, unlike これ, does not stand alone here. この人、だれ？ is a shortened, colloquial question. この映画は makes the movie the topic of an evaluation. 此の is a rare kanji spelling with the same reading この. This pointer to a person, thing or idea is usually written in kana.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あれはエミリーじゃない？","romaji":"Are wa Emirī ja nai?","german":"Isn't that Emily over there?"},{"japanese":"あれは何ですか。","romaji":"Are wa nan desu ka.","german":"What is that over there?","furigana":"なん"},{"japanese":"昨日のあれは、うまくいきましたか。","romaji":"Kinō no are wa, umaku ikimashita ka.","german":"Did that thing from yesterday go well?","furigana":"きのう"}],"notes":"あれ stands alone and refers to something over there, away from both speaker and listener. In familiar conversation it can also mean a person; for polite naming, あの人 or あの方 fit better. Before a noun, use あの: あの本. あれは何ですか asks about a distant thing. In familiar conversation, あれ can also mean a thing that both people already know, without naming it. 彼 is a rare kanji spelling for it; it is usually written あれ. The same kanji meaning \"he\" is read differently: かれ.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このパン、５ドルだって。","romaji":"Kono pan, go doru datte.","german":"This bread is supposedly five dollars."},{"japanese":"百ドルは何円ですか。","romaji":"Hyaku doru wa nan en desu ka.","german":"How many yen is a hundred dollars?","furigana":"ひゃく|なんえん"}],"notes":"ドル is the currency unit dollar and follows a number: 五ドル, 百ドル. It is not a sentence particle. Which dollar is meant depends on the country or context. だって can colloquially report a price one has heard; 何円 asks for an amount in yen.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どの子がマリアちゃん？","romaji":"Dono ko ga Maria-chan?","german":"Which child is Maria?","furigana":"こ"},{"japanese":"どの本が欲しいですか。","romaji":"Dono hon ga hoshii desu ka.","german":"Which book do you want?","furigana":"ほん|ほ"}],"notes":"どの asks directly before a noun which one from a selection is meant: どの子, どの本. It connects to the noun without な. If the noun is missing, ask with どれ. どの本が欲しいですか asks about the book the person wants. 何の is a rare kanji spelling for どの; in everyday use the hiragana spelling is usual. The same characters can be read なんの in other phrases, but this card explicitly practices どの.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 16"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お金がぜんぜんない。","romaji":"Okane ga zenzen nai.","german":"I have no money at all.","furigana":"かね"},{"japanese":"今日は時間がないです。","romaji":"Kyō wa jikan ga nai desu.","german":"I don't have any time today.","furigana":"きょう|じかん"}],"notes":"ない negates the existence of a thing here: お金がない, 時間がない. What is lacking takes が. It behaves like an i-adjective: ないです is more polite, and なかった describes the lack in the past. ありません is also possible. 無い is the kanji spelling for this lack of existence; hiragana is very common. The ない attached directly to a verb, as in 行かない, is an auxiliary form and is usually written without kanji.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あ、カバンがない！","romaji":"A, kaban ga nai!","german":"Oh, my bag isn't here!"},{"japanese":"カバンを忘れてきました。","romaji":"Kaban o wasurete kimashita.","german":"I left my bag behind.","furigana":"わす"}],"notes":"カバン means a bag, often for carrying books or personal belongings. It can also be written 鞄. カバンがない states that the bag is missing; カバンを忘れてきた says that one left it behind when leaving. が marks the missing thing, を the forgotten object.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は西ヨーロッパに行きたいです。","romaji":"Watashi wa Nishi Yōroppa ni ikitai desu.","german":"I want to go to Western Europe.","furigana":"わたし|にし|い"},{"japanese":"西ヨーロッパの地図を買いました。","romaji":"Nishi Yōroppa no chizu o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a map of Western Europe.","furigana":"にし|ちず|か"}],"notes":"西ヨーロッパ is Western Europe. 西 is read にし here; ヨーロッパ contains a long o and a small っ before p. に names the region as a travel destination. 西ヨーロッパの地図 links the region name to a map; the exact division of Europe may depend on the context.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"パスタを二人で分ける。","romaji":"Pasuta o futari de wakeru.","german":"We share the pasta between the two of us.","furigana":"ふたり|わ"},{"japanese":"ケーキを三つに分けましょう。","romaji":"Kēki o mittsu ni wakemashō.","german":"Let's divide the cake into three pieces.","furigana":"みっ|わ"}],"notes":"分ける is a transitive ichidan verb: 分けます, 分けて. The thing that is divided or shared takes を. 二人で names the two people involved; 三つに names the three resulting parts. 二人で分ける speaks of sharing together, not of two parts alone.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"母に代わって、私が電話に出ました。","romaji":"Haha ni kawatte, watashi ga denwa ni demashita.","german":"I answered the phone in place of my mother.","furigana":"はは|か|わたし|でんわ|で"},{"japanese":"先生が代わりました。","romaji":"Sensei ga kawarimashita.","german":"The teacher has changed.","furigana":"せんせい|か"}],"notes":"代わる is an intransitive godan verb: 代わります, 代わって. 先生が代わる says that another person takes over as teacher. 母に代わって names the mother as the person whose place one temporarily takes. The spelling 代わる often emphasizes substitution; the meaning comes from the whole sentence. 代る is another recorded spelling for this meaning of substitution and replacement.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大学に入学する考えはありません。","romaji":"Daigaku ni nyūgaku suru kangae wa arimasen.","german":"I have no intention of enrolling in a university.","furigana":"だいがく|にゅうがく|かんが"},{"japanese":"いい考えがあります。","romaji":"Ii kangae ga arimasu.","german":"I have a good idea.","furigana":"かんが"}],"notes":"考え is a noun: a thought, an idea or an intention. In いい考えがある it is about an idea. Verb + 考えがある can express an intention, such as 入学する考え. It is not the full verb form 考える \"to think\". かんがえ is read with a short e at the end.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"田中氏の本を読みました。","romaji":"Tanaka-shi no hon o yomimashita.","german":"I read Mr. Tanaka's book.","furigana":"たなかし|ほん|よ"},{"japanese":"山田氏がスピーチをしました。","romaji":"Yamada-shi ga supīchi o shimashita.","german":"Mr. Yamada gave a speech.","furigana":"やまだし"}],"notes":"〜氏 is a formal suffix after a name, especially in written reports: 田中氏. It can refer to men or women. 〜 marks the spot for the name here. In normal direct address, さん is used more often. After the whole name including 氏, particles such as が or の follow. The spelling without the placeholder is 氏; 〜 stands here for the preceding name. The independent meaning \"clan\" belongs to a different use of 氏.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"氏名を入力して下さい。","romaji":"Shimei o nyūryoku shite kudasai.","german":"Please enter your full name.","furigana":"しめい|にゅうりょく|くだ"},{"japanese":"その人の氏名は分かりません。","romaji":"Sono hito no shimei wa wakarimasen.","german":"I do not know that person's full name.","furigana":"ひと|しめい|わ"}],"notes":"氏名 is the full name, usually family name and given name. It often appears on forms. 氏名を入力する means to enter one's name; 氏名が分かる says that one knows it. The reading is しめい, not うじめい.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"財布に千円札が二枚あります。","romaji":"Saifu ni sen’ensatsu ga nimai arimasu.","german":"I have two 1,000-yen bills in my wallet.","furigana":"さいふ|せんえんさつ|にまい"},{"japanese":"一万円札を崩してください。","romaji":"Ichiman’ensatsu o kuzushite kudasai.","german":"Please break this 10,000-yen bill.","furigana":"いちまんえんさつ|くず"}],"notes":"〜札 after an amount of money denotes a bill: 千円札 \"1,000-yen bill\", 一万円札 \"10,000-yen bill\". This word element is read さつ. The finished bills are counted with 枚: 二枚. 〜 is a placeholder for the stated value, not a spoken syllable.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"千円札が足りません。","romaji":"Sen’ensatsu ga tarimasen.","german":"I'm short of 1,000-yen bills.","furigana":"せんえんさつ|た"},{"japanese":"千円札で払いました。","romaji":"Sen’ensatsu de haraimashita.","german":"I paid with a 1,000-yen bill.","furigana":"せんえんさつ|はら"}],"notes":"千円札 is a single bill worth a thousand yen. In せんえんさつ there is an audible n between せん and えん. To count the bills, use 枚. 千円札で払う names the bill as the means of payment; 千円札が足りない means there are too few such bills.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この辺は、人が少ないです。","romaji":"Kono hen wa, hito ga sukunai desu.","german":"There are few people in this area.","furigana":"へん|ひと|すく"},{"japanese":"この辺にコンビニはありますか。","romaji":"Kono hen ni konbini wa arimasu ka.","german":"Is there a convenience store around here?","furigana":"へん"}],"notes":"この辺 is the area nearby. With は you make it the topic of a description; with に you ask about something at this place. The card uses the reading このへん. この辺 is a place noun, not a freely conjugating i- or na-adjective.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 29"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ホットドッグにケチャップ付ける？","romaji":"Hottodoggu ni kechappu tsukeru?","german":"Do you want ketchup on your hot dog?","furigana":"つ"},{"japanese":"名札を付けてください。","romaji":"Nafuda o tsukete kudasai.","german":"Please put on your name tag.","furigana":"なふだ|つ"}],"notes":"付ける is a transitive Ichidan verb: 付けます, 付けて. You name with を what you attach or add, and with に the place. With ケチャップを付ける, it is about adding a sauce; 名札を付ける can mean putting on a name tag. In casual conversation, を can be omitted.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一万円以上のホテルでもいいですよ。","romaji":"Ichiman’en ijō no hoteru demo ii desu yo.","german":"A hotel that costs 10,000 yen or more is fine too.","furigana":"いちまんえんいじょう"},{"japanese":"十八歳以上の方だけ入れます。","romaji":"Jūhassai ijō no kata dake hairemasu.","german":"Only people aged eighteen or older may enter.","furigana":"じゅうはっさいいじょう|かた|はい"}],"notes":"After a number, 〜以上 means \"at least\" or \"or more\". The stated number is included: 十八歳以上 also covers eighteen-year-olds. The reading is いじょう with a long o. 一万円以上のホテル links the minimum amount to the hotel. 以上 on its own can have other meanings in other contexts.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は友達の信頼を失いました。","romaji":"Kare wa tomodachi no shinrai o ushinaimashita.","german":"He lost his friend's trust.","furigana":"かれ|ともだち|しんらい|うしな"},{"japanese":"大切なものを失いたくないです。","romaji":"Taisetsu na mono o ushinaitakunai desu.","german":"I do not want to lose anything important.","furigana":"たいせつ|うしな"}],"notes":"失う is a transitive godan verb: 失います, 失って. What is lost takes を. It is used for important things or trust. 失いたくない expresses not wanting to lose something. The passive form 失われる can say that something is lost.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"なんて失礼な男だ。","romaji":"Nante shitsurei na otoko da.","german":"What a rude man.","furigana":"しつれい|おとこ"},{"japanese":"失礼ですが、お名前は何ですか。","romaji":"Shitsurei desu ga, onamae wa nan desu ka.","german":"Excuse me, what is your name?","furigana":"しつれい|なまえ|なん"}],"notes":"失礼 as a na-adjective means \"rude\": 失礼な人. With 失礼ですが, on the other hand, you politely introduce a possibly personal question, similar to \"Excuse me, but ...\". The form does not say that the person addressed is rude. The reading しつれい ends in えい.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お母さんは、ダイエットに必死だ。","romaji":"Okāsan wa, daietto ni hisshi da.","german":"Mom is working desperately at dieting.","furigana":"かあ|ひっし"},{"japanese":"必死な顔で助けを求めました。","romaji":"Hisshi na kao de tasuke o motomemashita.","german":"I asked for help with a desperate look on my face.","furigana":"ひっし|かお|たす|もと"}],"notes":"必死 describes very intense, often desperate effort. It is a na-adjective: 必死な顔. With に it describes the manner of an action: 必死に勉強する. 必死 does not have to mean that someone has lost all hope; strenuous effort can also be meant. The small っ in ひっし is part of the reading.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"未来のことは分かりません。","romaji":"Mirai no koto wa wakarimasen.","german":"I do not know about the future.","furigana":"みらい|わ"},{"japanese":"子供たちの未来を考えています。","romaji":"Kodomotachi no mirai o kangaete imasu.","german":"I am thinking about the children's future.","furigana":"こども|みらい|かんが"}],"notes":"未来 is the future, especially the time still ahead of us. It is a noun: 未来のこと refers to future matters, and 未来を考える makes the future the object of thought. みらい is read with short vowels. The word itself does not give a specific date.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"年末に家族でソバを食べます。","romaji":"Nenmatsu ni kazoku de soba o tabemasu.","german":"At the end of the year, my family eats soba.","furigana":"ねんまつ|かぞく|た"},{"japanese":"年末は忙しいです。","romaji":"Nenmatsu wa isogashii desu.","german":"I'm busy at the end of the year.","furigana":"ねんまつ|いそが"}],"notes":"年末 is the end of the year. 年末に names it as the time of an action; 年末は can make it the topic of a description. It denotes a period around the end of the year and need not mean only the last day. The reading is ねんまつ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"月末までに、このアパートを出ます。","romaji":"Getsumatsu made ni, kono apāto o demasu.","german":"I will move out of this apartment by the end of the month.","furigana":"げつまつ|で"},{"japanese":"月末に給料をもらいます。","romaji":"Getsumatsu ni kyūryō o moraimasu.","german":"I get my salary at the end of the month.","furigana":"げつまつ|きゅうりょう"}],"notes":"月末 is the end of a month. 月末までに names a deadline: by the end of the month at the latest. 月末に, by contrast, names a point in time around the end of the month. The reading is げつまつ. 末 means \"end\" in this word, not \"weekend\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"高校までは車で十分かかります。","romaji":"Kōkō made wa kuruma de juppun kakarimasu.","german":"It takes ten minutes by car to get to high school.","furigana":"こうこう|くるま|じゅっぷん"},{"japanese":"高校の友達に会いました。","romaji":"Kōkō no tomodachi ni aimashita.","german":"I met a friend from high school.","furigana":"こうこう|ともだち|あ"}],"notes":"高校 is the short form of 高等学校, an upper secondary school. 高校まで names it as the destination or endpoint of a route. 高校の友達 can mean friends from one's high school days. こうこう has two long o sounds. In the route sentence, 十分 is read じゅっぷん \"ten minutes\" here.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 15"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"夏休みは来週からです。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi wa raishū kara desu.","german":"Summer vacation starts next week.","furigana":"なつやす|らいしゅう"},{"japanese":"夏休みに海に行きたいです。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi ni umi ni ikitai desu.","german":"I want to go to the sea during summer vacation.","furigana":"なつやす|うみ|い"}],"notes":"夏休み is the summer vacation or a summery holiday period. 夏休みは来週からです gives the start; 夏休みに海に行く names the vacation as the period of a trip. The word itself does not fix particular vacation months. It is read なつやすみ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"数学は私の弱点です。","romaji":"Sūgaku wa watashi no jakuten desu.","german":"Math is my weak point.","furigana":"すうがく|わたし|じゃくてん"},{"japanese":"この計画の弱点を見つけました。","romaji":"Kono keikaku no jakuten o mitsukemashita.","german":"I found the weak point of this plan.","furigana":"けいかく|じゃくてん|み"}],"notes":"弱点 is a weakness or weak point, for example in a person, a device or a plan. 私の弱点 names one's own weakness. 弱点を直す describes working on a weak spot. The reading is じゃくてん; じゃ forms a single mora.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今、もう二時半ですよ。","romaji":"Ima, mō nijihan desu yo.","german":"It's already half past two now.","furigana":"いま|にじはん"},{"japanese":"二時半に会いましょう。","romaji":"Nijihan ni aimashō.","german":"Let's meet at half past two.","furigana":"にじはん|あ"}],"notes":"二時半 is half past two, that is, 2:30. 半 adds half an hour; here it does not mean \"half past one\". 二時半です states the time, 二時半に会う the time of a meeting. Whether morning or afternoon is meant must be clarified by the context or by a word like 午後.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二つのライトが同時に明るくなった。","romaji":"Futatsu no raito ga dōji ni akaruku natta.","german":"The two lights became bright at the same time.","furigana":"ふた|どうじ|あか"},{"japanese":"話すのと書くのを同時にするのは難しいです。","romaji":"Hanasu no to kaku no o dōji ni suru no wa muzukashii desu.","german":"It is difficult to speak and write at the same time.","furigana":"はな|か|どうじ|むずか"}],"notes":"同時 is the same point in time. With に it becomes \"at the same time\": 同時に明るくなる. Before another noun, 同時の is possible, as in 同時の到着. Two events on the same day are not necessarily 同時. The reading どうじ has a long o.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このヘアドライヤーは、かなり強力だ。","romaji":"Kono hea doraiyā wa, kanari kyōryoku da.","german":"This hair dryer is quite powerful.","furigana":"きょうりょく"},{"japanese":"暗い道では強力なライトを使います。","romaji":"Kurai michi de wa kyōryoku na raito o tsukaimasu.","german":"I use a powerful light on dark roads.","furigana":"くら|みち|きょうりょく|つか"}],"notes":"強力 means \"strong\" or \"powerful\", for example for a device or an effect. It is a na-adjective: 強力なライト. With です or だ you describe the strength of something. The reading きょうりょく has a long o in the first part.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地理を学ぶために大学へ行きました。","romaji":"Chiri o manabu tame ni daigaku e ikimashita.","german":"I went to university to study geography.","furigana":"ちり|まな|だいがく|い"},{"japanese":"地理の授業が好きです。","romaji":"Chiri no jugyō ga suki desu.","german":"I like geography class.","furigana":"ちり|じゅぎょう|す"}],"notes":"地理 is geography, as a field of knowledge or a school subject. 地理を学ぶ names it as the subject of study; 地理の授業 is a class in this subject. ために can name the purpose of attending a university. ちり has two short syllables; it is not the country name チリ (Chile).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"空に、たくさんの風船が見えます。","romaji":"Sora ni, takusan no fūsen ga miemasu.","german":"I can see many balloons in the sky.","furigana":"そら|ふうせん|み"},{"japanese":"子供が風船を持っています。","romaji":"Kodomo ga fūsen o motte imasu.","german":"The child is holding a balloon.","furigana":"こども|ふうせん|も"}],"notes":"風船 is a balloon, for example as a toy or decoration. 風 is read ふう here, with a long u. 風船が見える describes that balloons can be seen; 風船を持つ names them as objects being held. For a hot-air balloon, the word usually encountered is 気球.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先週は、私の会社はお休みでした。","romaji":"Senshū wa, watashi no kaisha wa oyasumi deshita.","german":"Last week my company was closed.","furigana":"せんしゅう|わたし|かいしゃ|やす"},{"japanese":"先週の写真を見ました。","romaji":"Senshū no shashin o mimashita.","german":"I looked at the photos from last week.","furigana":"せんしゅう|しゃしん|み"}],"notes":"先週 is the week before the current week. It is read せんしゅう with a long u. 先週は frames a retrospective description; 先週の写真 means photos from that week. An action with 先週 refers to the previous week and is often in a past form.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今週は大きなテストがあります。","romaji":"Konshū wa ōkina tesuto ga arimasu.","german":"There is a big test this week.","furigana":"こんしゅう|おお"},{"japanese":"今週の金曜日は暇ですか。","romaji":"Konshū no kin’yōbi wa hima desu ka.","german":"Are you free this Friday?","furigana":"こんしゅう|きんようび|ひま"}],"notes":"今週 is the current week. 今週は makes it the topic of a plan or announcement. 今週の金曜日 means the Friday of this week; の links the week with the day here. It is read こんしゅう with a long u. 今週 alone does not yet name a single date.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"週末は、ベッドかソファの上にいます。","romaji":"Shūmatsu wa, beddo ka sofa no ue ni imasu.","german":"On the weekend, I stay on my bed or sofa.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|うえ"},{"japanese":"週末に何をしますか。","romaji":"Shūmatsu ni nani o shimasu ka.","german":"What are you doing on the weekend?","furigana":"しゅうまつ|なに"}],"notes":"週末 is the weekend. As a topic, 週末は describes a habit or a plan, while 週末に names the time of an action. For a single upcoming weekend, 今週末 helps; for the past weekend, 先週末. The second question leaves open which activity is planned; it does not require naming a particular day of the week.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 13"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これは、金魚ですか？コイですか？","romaji":"Kore wa, kingyo desu ka? Koi desu ka?","german":"Is this a goldfish? Or a carp?","furigana":"きんぎょ"},{"japanese":"お祭りで金魚すくいをしました。","romaji":"Omatsuri de kingyo sukui o shimashita.","german":"I played goldfish scooping at the festival.","furigana":"まつ|きんぎょ"}],"notes":"金魚 is a goldfish. It is an animal name; for a living animal you use いる. The first question distinguishes 金魚 from コイ, a carp. 金魚すくい names a game at festivals in which you scoop goldfish out of the water with a small scoop. Here 金魚 is part of a compound game name.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"白鳥の羽は、白くてきれいです。","romaji":"Hakuchō no hane wa, shirokute kirei desu.","german":"Swan feathers are white and beautiful.","furigana":"はくちょう|はね|しろ"},{"japanese":"湖に白鳥がいます。","romaji":"Mizuumi ni hakuchō ga imasu.","german":"There are swans on the lake.","furigana":"みずうみ|はくちょう"}],"notes":"白鳥 means \"swan\" here. Although the characters mean \"white\" and \"bird\", 白鳥 usually names this group of birds and not every white bird. 白鳥の羽 describes the feathers, 湖に白鳥がいる the location of living animals. The reading はくちょう ends with a long vowel: hakuchō.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私のレインコートは黄色です。","romaji":"Watashi no reinkōto wa kiiro desu.","german":"My raincoat is yellow.","furigana":"わたし|きいろ"},{"japanese":"黄色い花がきれいです。","romaji":"Kiiroi hana ga kirei desu.","german":"The yellow flowers are beautiful.","furigana":"きいろ|はな"},{"japanese":"入口の黄色の看板が目印です。","romaji":"Iriguchi no kiiro no kanban ga mejirushi desu.","german":"The yellow sign at the entrance is the landmark.","furigana":"いりぐち|きいろ|かんばん|めじるし"}],"notes":"黄色 is the color name yellow and can stand as a noun with です: 黄色です. Before a noun, 黄色の is possible; the usual i-adjective form is 黄色い, as in 黄色い花. The form 黄色い thus belongs to the related adjective form, while the entry names the color 黄色. In directions, you can use the color as a landmark.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 34"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"トロントには、大きな黒人のコミュニティがあります。","romaji":"Toronto ni wa, ōkina kokujin no komyunitī ga arimasu.","german":"There is a large Black community in Toronto."},{"japanese":"クラスに黒人の学生がいます。","romaji":"Kurasu ni kokujin no gakusei ga imasu.","german":"There is a Black student in the class.","furigana":"こくじん|がくせい"}],"notes":"黒人 is a noun for a Black person. Before another noun, の is used, as in 黒人の学生 or 黒人のコミュニティ. The word does not describe any particular nationality; a person can at the same time be, for example, カナダ人. The examples name a community and an individual student, without deriving further characteristics from the term.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これは私の妹の人形です。","romaji":"Kore wa watashi no imōto no ningyō desu.","german":"This is my little sister's doll.","furigana":"わたし|いもうと|にんぎょう"},{"japanese":"娘に人形を買ってあげました。","romaji":"Musume ni ningyō o katte agemashita.","german":"I bought my daughter a doll.","furigana":"むすめ|にんぎょう|か"}],"notes":"人形 is a doll or a figure that represents a human being. 人形です identifies the object; 娘に人形を買ってあげた names a gift for the daughter. あげる emphasizes that you did something for someone else. For living people 人 is used; for a doll, ある is the usual existence verb.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 30"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このテーブルは、林さんの手作りです。","romaji":"Kono tēburu wa, Hayashi-san no tezukuri desu.","german":"This table was handmade by Hayashi.","furigana":"はやし|てづく"},{"japanese":"これは手作りのお菓子です。","romaji":"Kore wa tezukuri no okashi desu.","german":"These are handmade sweets.","furigana":"てづく|かし"}],"notes":"手作り as a noun means \"made by hand\" or \"homemade\". Before a product, の is used: 手作りのお菓子. 林さんの手作りです names the person who made the item himself or herself. It can describe both crafted items and self-prepared food; the examples make no statement about whether they are sold.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"南口から出るとすぐにお茶の店があります。","romaji":"Minamiguchi kara deru to sugu ni ocha no mise ga arimasu.","german":"As soon as you leave through the south exit, there is a tea shop.","furigana":"みなみぐち|で|ちゃ|みせ"},{"japanese":"南口で会いましょう。","romaji":"Minamiguchi de aimashō.","german":"Let's meet at the south exit.","furigana":"みなみぐち|あ"}],"notes":"南口 denotes the entrance or exit on the south side, often at a train station. 南口から出る names the starting point of a movement, 南口で会う the meeting point. The reading みなみぐち contains the voiced form ぐち of 口. Naming the exact exit is helpful for meetings when a station has several entrances.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ここのスタッフは大体、大学生です。","romaji":"Koko no sutaffu wa daitai, daigakusei desu.","german":"Die meisten Mitarbeiter hier sind Studenten.","furigana":"だいたい|だいがくせい"},{"japanese":"姉は大学生です。","romaji":"Ane wa daigakusei desu.","german":"Meine ältere Schwester ist Studentin.","furigana":"あね|だいがくせい"},{"japanese":"大学生の時、図書館でアルバイトをしました。","romaji":"Daigakusei no toki, toshokan de arubaito o shimashita.","german":"Während meines Studiums habe ich in einer Bibliothek gejobbt.","furigana":"だいがくせい|とき|としょかん"}],"notes":"大学生 ist jemand, der an einer Hochschule oder Universität studiert. Das Nomen ist unabhängig vom Geschlecht; bei 姉 wird es im Deutschen als „Studentin“ übersetzt. 大学生です nennt den Ausbildungsstatus. Nicht jede Person, die etwas lernt, ist 大学生: Für einen allgemeinen Lernenden gibt es 学習者, für Schülerinnen und Schüler passendere Schulbezeichnungen.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先生が「すみません」の言い方を正してくれました。","romaji":"Sensei ga \"sumimasen\" no iikata o tadashite kuremashita.","german":"The teacher corrected how I say 「すみません」.","furigana":"せんせい|い|かた|ただ"},{"japanese":"もっと丁寧な言い方をしてください。","romaji":"Motto teinei na iikata o shite kudasai.","german":"Please phrase it more politely.","furigana":"ていねい|い|かた"}],"notes":"言い方 is the way of saying something: the wording, the tone or the chosen manner of expression. 言う supplies the stem 言い, and 方 means the manner or way here. 丁寧な言い方 asks for a more polite wording, without necessarily changing the content. A teacher can also correct the way in which one says a particular expression.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"パトカーが先回りして、逃げる車の行く手をふさいだ。","romaji":"Patokaa ga sakimawari shite, nigeru kuruma no yukute o fusaida.","german":"The patrol car got ahead of the fleeing car and blocked its path.","furigana":"さきまわ|に|くるま|ゆ|て"},{"japanese":"先回りして待っていました。","romaji":"Sakimawari shite matte imashita.","german":"I got there ahead of time and was waiting.","furigana":"さきまわ|ま"},{"japanese":"母は私が言う前に、先回りして必要な物を用意してくれた。","romaji":"Haha wa watashi ga iu mae ni, sakimawari shite hitsuyou na mono o youi shite kureta.","german":"My mother prepared everything I needed before I even said anything.","furigana":"はは|わたし|い|まえ|さきまわ|ひつよう|もの|ようい"}],"notes":"先回り means to beat someone to a place by taking a different or faster route and arrive at the destination before them; 先回りして待つ often follows. Figuratively, it means to anticipate something, e.g. 相手の考えを先回りする or 話を先回りする (\"to jump ahead\"). It is an intransitive する-verb, and the destination takes に. Unlike 先に行く (\"to go on ahead\"), 先回り carries the nuance of intercepting someone or getting there before them.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"近々、会いましょう。","romaji":"Chikajika, aimashō.","german":"Let's meet soon.","furigana":"ちかぢか|あ"},{"japanese":"近々引っ越す予定です。","romaji":"Chikajika hikkosu yotei desu.","german":"I plan to move soon.","furigana":"ちかぢかひ|こ|よてい"}],"notes":"近々 means \"soon\" or \"in the near future\" here. It does not fix an exact day and fits plans or invitations: 近々会いましょう. It is used directly before the action, without adding する or な. The reading is ちかぢか, chikajika in Hepburn. A concrete deadline such as 明日 is more precise than this open-ended time expression.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日は青空が広がっている。","romaji":"Kyō wa aozora ga hirogatte iru.","german":"The blue sky stretches out overhead today.","furigana":"きょう|あおぞら|ひろ"},{"japanese":"今日は青空が広がっています。","romaji":"Kyō wa aozora ga hirogatte imasu.","german":"The blue sky stretches out overhead today.","furigana":"きょう|あおぞら|ひろ"},{"japanese":"青空の下で、お弁当を食べました。","romaji":"Aozora no shita de, obentō o tabemashita.","german":"I ate my bento under the blue sky.","furigana":"あおぞら|した|べんとう|た"}],"notes":"青空 is a blue, clear-looking sky. 青空が広がる describes the visible sky above a place; 青空の下で names the surroundings of an outdoor activity. The word is a noun, so の comes before 下. What is emphasized is not the size but the blue of the sky; 大空 emphasizes its vastness more.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その兄弟は、まだ二人とも小学生です。","romaji":"Sono kyōdai wa, mada futari tomo shōgakusei desu.","german":"Both of those brothers are still elementary school students.","furigana":"きょうだい|ふたり|しょうがくせい"},{"japanese":"弟は小学生です。","romaji":"Otōto wa shōgakusei desu.","german":"My younger brother is an elementary school student.","furigana":"おとうと|しょうがくせい"},{"japanese":"小学生の時、毎日歩いて学校に行きました。","romaji":"Shōgakusei no toki, mainichi aruite gakkō ni ikimashita.","german":"When I was in elementary school, I walked to school every day.","furigana":"しょうがくせい|とき|まいにちある|がっこう|い"}],"notes":"小学生 refers to a child who attends elementary school. The noun is gender-neutral; in one example, 弟 makes clear that the younger brother is meant. 二人とも says that both people attend this school level, and まだ stresses \"still\". 小学生の時 can place an activity in one's own elementary school years.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 48"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の子は、今中学生です。","romaji":"Watashi no ko wa, ima chūgakusei desu.","german":"My child is a junior high school student now.","furigana":"わたし|こ|いまちゅうがくせい"},{"japanese":"姉は中学生の時テニスをしていました。","romaji":"Ane wa chūgakusei no toki tenisu o shite imashita.","german":"My older sister played tennis when she was in junior high school.","furigana":"あね|ちゅうがくせい|とき"}],"notes":"中学生 is a student at a Japanese junior high school, that is, the school level between elementary school and high school. 中学生です states the current status. 中学生の時, by contrast, looks back on the junior high school years. In the first example, 今 is read いま; it means \"now\" and is not part of the word 中学生.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 19"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今年、不人気だったアニメは有りますか？","romaji":"Kotoshi, funinki datta anime wa arimasu ka?","german":"Were there any anime that were unpopular this year?","furigana":"ことし|ふにんき|あ"},{"japanese":"この歌手は不人気になりました。","romaji":"Kono kashu wa funinki ni narimashita.","german":"This singer has become unpopular.","furigana":"かしゅ|ふにんき"}],"notes":"不人気 means \"unpopular\" or \"not very popular\". As a na-adjective it can stand before a noun, as in 不人気な番組. 不人気だった describes a past state; 不人気になる describes a change toward less popularity. The word first of all says something about the reception by the public, not about whether a work is good or bad on its merits.","levelNote":"Compound: 不 (N3) + 人気 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"こうやってハートの形を切り取るんです。","romaji":"Kō yatte hāto no katachi o kiritoru n desu.","german":"This is how you cut out a heart shape.","furigana":"かたち|き|と"},{"japanese":"新聞の記事を切り取りました。","romaji":"Shinbun no kiji o kiritorimashita.","german":"I cut out the newspaper article.","furigana":"しんぶん|きじ|き|と"}],"notes":"切り取る means to detach a part by cutting or separating it. The cut-out piece takes を: ハートの形を切り取る, 記事を切り取る. Unlike plain 切る, the word emphasizes removing or detaching. It is a godan verb with 切り取ります and 切り取って. The two examples show crafting and keeping a newspaper clipping.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"男が、マイケルのカバンからスマホを取り出すのを見たよ。","romaji":"Otoko ga, Maikeru no kaban kara sumaho o toridasu no o mita yo.","german":"I saw a man take a smartphone out of Michael's bag.","furigana":"おとこ|と|だ|み"},{"japanese":"箱から本を取り出しました。","romaji":"Hako kara hon o toridashimashita.","german":"I took a book out of the box.","furigana":"はこ|ほん|と|だ"}],"notes":"取り出す means to take something out of a container or an enclosed area. The object takes を and the place it comes from takes から: 箱から本を取り出す. This godan verb forms 取り出します and 取り出して. のを見た can present an observed action as a whole clause: you saw someone taking something out.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どうして象のハナは長いの？","romaji":"Dōshite zō no hana wa nagai no?","german":"Why do elephants have long trunks?","furigana":"ぞう|なが"},{"japanese":"動物園で象を見ました。","romaji":"Dōbutsuen de zō o mimashita.","german":"I saw an elephant at the zoo.","furigana":"どうぶつえん|ぞう|み"}],"notes":"象 means \"elephant\". For a living animal, use いる; when observing, 象 becomes the object of 見る. 鼻 can mean the trunk of an elephant, whereas for humans it means the nose. The first question asks about the long trunk, the second tells of one's own observation at the zoo.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 10"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一丁目のコンビニはオープンしたばかりです。","romaji":"Itchōme no konbini wa ōpun shita bakari desu.","german":"The convenience store in 1-chome has just opened.","furigana":"いっちょうめ"},{"japanese":"三丁目の交差点を右に曲がります。","romaji":"Sanchōme no kōsaten o migi ni magarimasu.","german":"I turn right at the intersection in 3-chome.","furigana":"さんちょうめ|こうさてん|みぎ|ま"}],"notes":"〜丁目 is an address element after a number and denotes a numbered section of a city district. 一丁目 is read いっちょうめ here, 三丁目 さんちょうめ. It is neither a standalone place designation without a number nor a grammatical conversation particle. In place names, 一丁目の店 names a shop in the first block, and 三丁目の交差点 an intersection in the third block. The spelling without the placeholder is 丁目; 〜 stands here for the preceding number.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"三日の午後三時に、ここで会いましょう。","romaji":"Mikka no gogo sanji ni, koko de aimashō.","german":"Let's meet here at 3 p.m. on the 3rd.","furigana":"みっか|ごごさんじ|あ"},{"japanese":"今何時ですか。","romaji":"Ima nanji desu ka.","german":"What time is it now?","furigana":"いまなんじ"}],"notes":"〜時 follows a number and gives a clock time: 三時 is さんじ, 午後三時 is 3 p.m. 何時 is read なんじ and asks \"what time is it?\". For a duration, use 時間, not just 時. In the first example, 三日 is a calendar day and is read みっか; the time follows as 午後三時. The spelling without the placeholder is 時; 〜 stands here for the preceding number.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"タクシーを見たら、右手を大きく上げて下さいね。","romaji":"Takushī o mitara, migite o ōkiku agete kudasai ne.","german":"When you see a taxi, please raise your right hand high.","furigana":"み|みぎて|おお|あ|くだ"},{"japanese":"字を大きく書いてください。","romaji":"Ji o ōkiku kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write the characters large.","furigana":"じ|おお|か"}],"notes":"大きく describes how an action is carried out. It is the ku-form of 大きい: 字を大きく書く means \"to write large\". With 大きく手を上げる, the movement is made clearly and broadly. The form attaches directly to the verb; before a noun, the adjective form 大きい is used instead, as in 大きい字.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"船体は大きかった。","romaji":"Sentai wa ōkikatta.","german":"The hull was big.","furigana":"せんたい|おお"},{"japanese":"船体が大きいですね。","romaji":"Sentai ga ōkii desu ne.","german":"The hull is big, isn't it?","furigana":"せんたい|おお"},{"japanese":"港で、船体に傷がないか調べました。","romaji":"Minato de, sentai ni kizu ga nai ka shirabemashita.","german":"At the harbor, I checked whether the hull was damaged.","furigana":"みなと|せんたい|きず|しら"}],"notes":"船体 is the body or hull of a ship. The word is more technical than 船, which means the whole ship. 船体が大きい describes the hull, 船体を調べる an inspection of this part. As a noun, it is worked into the sentence with が or を; it is not a size specification on its own.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"こちらが302号室です。","romaji":"Kochira ga sanmaruni-gōshitsu desu.","german":"This is room 302.","furigana":"ごうしつ"},{"japanese":"三〇五号室はどこですか。","romaji":"Sanmarugo-gōshitsu wa doko desu ka.","german":"Where is room 305?"}],"notes":"〜号室 follows a room or apartment number. 三〇五号室 is read さんまるごごうしつ here; the zero can be read まる when digits are read one by one. こちらが302号室です identifies a room, どこですか asks for its location. The 〜 in the entry is a placeholder for the number and is not part of the spoken reading.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"こんな日は、大空を見上げよう。","romaji":"Konna hi wa, ōzora o miageyō.","german":"On a day like this, let's look up at the sky.","furigana":"ひ|おおぞら|みあ"},{"japanese":"大空を見ると気持ちがいいです。","romaji":"Ōzora o miru to kimochi ga ii desu.","german":"It feels good to look at the wide sky.","furigana":"おおぞら|み|きも"},{"japanese":"飛行機が大空を飛んでいます。","romaji":"Hikōki ga ōzora o tonde imasu.","german":"An airplane is flying through the big sky.","furigana":"ひこうき|おおぞら|と"}],"notes":"大空 is the wide, open sky. It sounds more vivid than plain 空 and emphasizes vastness. 大空を見上げる describes looking up, 大空を飛ぶ a movement through the sky. 青空, by contrast, emphasizes the blue color. The beginning おお is pronounced long: ōzora.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"駅の近くに、安いホテルが見当たりました。","romaji":"Eki no chikaku ni, yasui hoteru ga miatarimashita.","german":"I found a cheap hotel near the station.","furigana":"えき|ちか|やす|みあ"},{"japanese":"鍵がどこにも見当たりません。","romaji":"Kagi ga doko ni mo miatarimasen.","german":"The key is nowhere to be found.","furigana":"かぎ|みあ"}],"notes":"見当たる means to be found or spotted while searching or looking around. It is intransitive: 鍵が見当たらない means \"the key can't be found\". どこにも with a negative reinforces \"nowhere\". The positive form can report that one has found a suitable thing. This godan verb forms 見当たります and 見当たらない.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これが、三角形のおにぎりの作り方です。","romaji":"Kore ga, sankakukei no onigiri no tsukurikata desu.","german":"This is how to make a triangular rice ball.","furigana":"さんかくけい|つく|かた"},{"japanese":"この看板は三角形です。","romaji":"Kono kanban wa sankakukei desu.","german":"This sign is triangular.","furigana":"かんばん|さんかくけい"}],"notes":"三角形 is a triangle and is read さんかくけい here. As a noun it can modify another thing with の: 三角形のおにぎり is a triangular rice ball. 三角形です describes the shape of an object. The also common pronunciation さんかっけい is a shortened variant; this entry's romanizations follow the given reading さんかくけい.","levelNote":"Compound: 三角 (N3) + 形 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「立つ」の活用形が分かりません。","romaji":"\"Tatsu\" no katsuyōkei ga wakarimasen.","german":"I do not know the conjugated forms of 「立つ」.","furigana":"た|かつようけい|わ"},{"japanese":"動詞の活用形を覚えてください。","romaji":"Dōshi no katsuyōkei o oboete kudasai.","german":"Please learn the conjugated forms of verbs.","furigana":"どうし|かつようけい|おぼ"},{"japanese":"「書か」は「書く」の活用形で、「書かない」で使います。","romaji":"\"Kaka\" wa \"kaku\" no katsuyōkei de, \"kakanai\" de tsukaimasu.","german":"「書か」 is a conjugated form of 「書く」 and is used in 「書かない」.","furigana":"か|か|かつようけい|か|つか"}],"notes":"活用形 names a form of an inflecting word. In Japanese school grammar it refers to forms such as 未然形 and 連用形. For verbs, what matters is which form comes before an ending: 書か is used, for example, in 書かない. The reading ends in けい, not がた. 分かりません reports a comprehension problem here, and 覚えてください is a request to learn.","levelNote":"Compound: 活用 (N3) + 形 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一体どうなっているんだ。","romaji":"Ittai dō natte iru n da.","german":"Was zum Teufel ist hier los?","furigana":"いったい"},{"japanese":"一体何が起きたのですか。","romaji":"Ittai nani ga okita no desu ka.","german":"Was um alles in der Welt ist passiert?","furigana":"いったいなに|お"},{"japanese":"一体どうして約束を忘れたのですか。","romaji":"Ittai dōshite yakusoku o wasureta no desu ka.","german":"Warum haben Sie denn bloß die Verabredung vergessen?","furigana":"いったい|やくそく|わす"}],"notes":"一体 verstärkt hier eine Frage und zeigt Verwunderung, Ungeduld oder den Wunsch nach einer Erklärung. Es steht vor einem Fragewort, etwa 一体どう oder 一体何が. „Zum Teufel“ kann im Deutschen passen, ist aber stärker als manche japanische Verwendung; „denn bloß“ ist oft neutraler. Die Form mit んだ klingt direkter als die höfliche Frage mit のですか.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「ありがとう」と小声で言いました。","romaji":"\"Arigatō\" to kogoe de iimashita.","german":"\"Thank you,\" I said in a low voice.","furigana":"こごえ|い"},{"japanese":"小声で話してください。","romaji":"Kogoe de hanashite kudasai.","german":"Please speak quietly.","furigana":"こごえ|はな"}],"notes":"小声 is a quiet voice. With で, the noun describes how someone speaks: 小声で言う or 小声で話す. The first phrase can render a softly spoken word of thanks, the second can ask someone to lower their volume. Unlike 耳打ち, 小声 does not require closeness to another person's ear. Before a noun, の is used, as in 小声の会話.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"海王星は色が青いです。","romaji":"Kaiōsei wa iro ga aoi desu.","german":"Neptune is blue in color.","furigana":"かいおうせい|いろ|あお"},{"japanese":"海王星は太陽から遠いです。","romaji":"Kaiōsei wa taiyō kara tōi desu.","german":"Neptune is far from the sun.","furigana":"かいおうせい|たいよう|とお"}],"notes":"海王星 is the planet Neptune. 太陽から遠い states its great distance from the sun; 海王星の色 refers to its color. The name is read かいおうせい, with a long おう. It names a planet, not a general type of star. In the examples, visible color and spatial distance are distinguished.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"五千円以下の中古のテレビはありますか？","romaji":"Gosen en ika no chūko no terebi wa arimasu ka?","german":"Do you have a used TV for 5,000 yen or less?","furigana":"ごせんえんいか|ちゅうこ"},{"japanese":"十歳以下の子供は無料です。","romaji":"Jussai ika no kodomo wa muryō desu.","german":"Children aged ten and under are free.","furigana":"じゅっさいいか|こども|むりょう"}],"notes":"〜以下 sets an upper limit and includes the stated value. 五千円以下 means at most 5,000 yen, and 十歳以下 means up to and including ten years. For a limit that excludes the stated value, with numbers you would use something like 未満. After 以下, の can connect a noun: 五千円以下のテレビ. The examples show a price limit and an age condition.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"町民は毎年みんなで花見に行く。","romaji":"Chōmin wa maitoshi minna de hanami ni iku.","german":"Every year the townspeople all go cherry blossom viewing together.","furigana":"ちょうみん|まいとし|はなみ|い"},{"japanese":"町民がお祭りに参加します。","romaji":"Chōmin ga omatsuri ni sanka shimasu.","german":"The townspeople take part in the festival.","furigana":"ちょうみん|まつ|さんか"},{"japanese":"町民だけが、この施設を使えます。","romaji":"Chōmin dake ga, kono shisetsu o tsukaemasu.","german":"Only town residents can use this facility.","furigana":"ちょうみん|しせつ|つか"}],"notes":"町民 are the residents of a town or small municipality. 町民が names them as an acting group, 町民だけ stresses a rule that applies only to this group. 町民 is not a nationality. For residents of a city called 市, 市民 is used; in the entry, the municipality is designated with 町.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"次のクラスは来週です。","romaji":"Tsugi no kurasu wa raishū desu.","german":"The next class is next week.","furigana":"つぎ|らいしゅう"},{"japanese":"来週の予定を教えてください。","romaji":"Raishū no yotei o oshiete kudasai.","german":"Please tell me the plans for next week.","furigana":"らいしゅう|よてい|おし"}],"notes":"来週 is the week after the current week. The word can give the time directly or specify a plan with の: 来週の予定. 来週です answers the question of when something takes place. For an appointment exactly one week later, 一週間後 is more precise; that is not always the same date as \"next week\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"以後は、このアドレスにメールして下さい。","romaji":"Igo wa, kono adoresu ni mēru shite kudasai.","german":"From now on, please send emails to this address.","furigana":"いご|くだ"},{"japanese":"以後気をつけます。","romaji":"Igo ki o tsukemasu.","german":"I will be careful from now on.","furigana":"いごき"},{"japanese":"卒業して以後、先生に会っていません。","romaji":"Sotsugyō shite igo, sensei ni atte imasen.","german":"I have not seen my teacher since I graduated.","furigana":"そつぎょう|いご|せんせい|あ"}],"notes":"以後 refers to the time from a reference point onward. Without an earlier point in time, it often means \"from now on\", for example with a changed email address or a resolution. After a te-form it can mean \"since then\": 卒業して以後 looks back at the time after graduation. The word sounds more formal than これから and can stand directly before the action as a time expression.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一斤のパンは6枚切りか8枚切りで売られていることが多い。","romaji":"Ikkin no pan wa rokumaigiri ka hachimaigiri de urarete iru koto ga ōi.","german":"A loaf of bread is often sold sliced into six or eight slices.","furigana":"いっきん|まいぎ|まいぎ|う|おお"},{"japanese":"食パンを一斤ください。","romaji":"Shokupan o ikkin kudasai.","german":"One loaf of sandwich bread, please.","furigana":"しょく|いっきん"}],"notes":"一斤 is a quantity word for one loaf of bread. The number of slices is stated separately: 6枚切り means this amount of bread is cut into six slices, 8枚切り accordingly into eight. When ordering, the quantity can stand directly after the object: 食パンを一斤ください. What is practiced here is the loaf of bread, not the older weight unit 斤.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このパンは、二斤で五百円です。","romaji":"Kono pan wa, nikin de gohyaku en desu.","german":"This bread is 500 yen for two loaves.","furigana":"にきん|ごひゃくえん"},{"japanese":"パンを二斤注文しました。","romaji":"Pan o nikin chūmon shimashita.","german":"I ordered two loaves of bread.","furigana":"にきんちゅうもん"},{"japanese":"パンを二斤焼いて、隣の人に一斤あげました。","romaji":"Pan o nikin yaite, tonari no hito ni ikkin agemashita.","german":"I baked two loaves of bread and gave one to my neighbor.","furigana":"にきんや|となり|ひと|いっきん"}],"notes":"二斤 gives a bread quantity of two loaves and is read にきん. 斤 is a counter for bread here, not a statement of the number of slices. 二斤で五百円 gives the price for the whole quantity of two loaves. When ordering or baking, the quantity can stand after the object without any further particle: パンを二斤注文する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"火星には水がありますか？","romaji":"Kasei ni wa mizu ga arimasu ka?","german":"Is there water on Mars?","furigana":"かせい|みず"},{"japanese":"火星は赤い星です。","romaji":"Kasei wa akai hoshi desu.","german":"Mars is a red planet.","furigana":"かせい|あか|ほし"}],"notes":"火星 is the planet Mars. With 火星には you ask about something on this planet, such as water. 星 can also mean a planet in general usage; astronomically more precise is 惑星. The name is a noun. The の in 火星の写真 would denote a picture of this planet.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"木星を見たことありますか？","romaji":"Mokusei o mita koto arimasu ka?","german":"Have you ever seen Jupiter?","furigana":"もくせい|み"},{"japanese":"木星は、太陽系で一番大きい惑星です。","romaji":"Mokusei wa, taiyōkei de ichiban ōkii wakusei desu.","german":"Jupiter is the largest planet in the solar system.","furigana":"もくせい|たいようけい|いちばんおお|わくせい"}],"notes":"木星 is Jupiter, a planet of our solar system. 木星を見たことがありますか asks about a past observation. With \"the largest planet\", the frame of reference must be clear: 太陽系で一番大きい惑星 limits the statement to the solar system. 木星 is a name; the general term for a group of planets is 惑星.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"山を下りて、やっと人里に着きました。","romaji":"Yama o orite, yatto hitozato ni tsukimashita.","german":"After coming down the mountain, I finally reached a populated area.","furigana":"やま|お|ひとざと|つ"},{"japanese":"人里離れた場所です。","romaji":"Hitozato hanareta basho desu.","german":"It is a place far from human settlements.","furigana":"ひとざとはな|ばしょ"}],"notes":"人里 refers to a place inhabited by people, especially in contrast to mountains, forests or wilderness. 人里に着く describes reaching a settlement. 人里離れた場所 is a place away from human settlement; 離れた connects directly to 場所 here. The word sounds more descriptive than a specific municipality term such as 町.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大体、二分くらいかかります。","romaji":"Daitai, nifun kurai kakarimasu.","german":"It takes about two minutes.","furigana":"だいたい|にふん"},{"japanese":"あと十分で着きます。","romaji":"Ato juppun de tsukimasu.","german":"I will arrive in ten minutes.","furigana":"じゅっぷん|つ"}],"notes":"〜分 expresses minutes. The pronunciation changes after numbers: 二分 is にふん, and 十分 is read じゅっぷん here. For a duration, you ask how many minutes an action takes; あと十分で, by contrast, gives the time remaining until arrival. 十分 can also be read じゅうぶん and mean \"sufficient\"; in the example, the time expression shows the minutes reading. The spelling without the placeholder is 分; 〜 stands here for the preceding number.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このスーツのジャケットには内ポケットがありません。","romaji":"Kono sūtsu no jaketto ni wa uchipoketto ga arimasen.","german":"The jacket of this suit has no inside pocket.","furigana":"うち"},{"japanese":"内ポケットに財布を入れています。","romaji":"Uchipoketto ni saifu o irete imasu.","german":"I keep my wallet in the inside pocket.","furigana":"うち|さいふ|い"}],"notes":"内ポケット is a pocket on the inside of a garment. The word combines 内 with the loanword ポケット and is pronounced うちポケット. 内ポケットがない describes a property of a jacket; 内ポケットに財布を入れる names the place something is put into. It means the pocket inside a garment, not a separate handbag.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日は、ナポレオン三世について学びましょう。","romaji":"Kyō wa, Naporeon sansei ni tsuite manabimashō.","german":"Today, let's learn about Napoleon III.","furigana":"きょう|さんせい|まな"},{"japanese":"ナポレオン三世は有名な皇帝でした。","romaji":"Naporeon sansei wa yūmei na kōtei deshita.","german":"Napoleon III was a famous emperor.","furigana":"さんせい|ゆうめい|こうてい"}],"notes":"ナポレオン三世 is the personal name Napoleon III. The ending 三世 is read さんせい and distinguishes him from other bearers of the same name. について names the topic of a lesson, は introduces the person as the topic of a description. The entry practices this name; 三世 is not translated here as \"three worlds\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その人、有名人だよ。","romaji":"Sono hito, yūmeijin da yo.","german":"That person is a celebrity.","furigana":"ひと|ゆうめいじん"},{"japanese":"その本を見せてください。","romaji":"Sono hon o misete kudasai.","german":"Please show me that book.","furigana":"ほん|み"},{"japanese":"その話は、昨日も聞きました。","romaji":"Sono hanashi wa, kinō mo kikimashita.","german":"I heard that story yesterday as well.","furigana":"はなし|きのう|き"}],"notes":"その comes directly before a noun. It often refers to something near the listener or something already mentioned: その本, その話. It cannot replace a thing on its own; それ exists for that. The demonstrative use should be distinguished from the hesitating filler その…. In conversation, the context helps recognize what is being pointed at or referred to. 其の is the rare kanji spelling of the demonstrative その. In normal usage, kana is used here; this spelling does not denote a family name.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの人、どこかで見たことある気がする。","romaji":"Ano hito, dokoka de mita koto aru ki ga suru.","german":"I feel like I've seen that person over there somewhere before.","furigana":"ひと|み|き"},{"japanese":"あの山は富士山です。","romaji":"Ano yama wa Fujisan desu.","german":"That mountain is Mt. Fuji.","furigana":"やま|ふじさん"}],"notes":"あの comes before a noun and refers to something distant from both conversation partners, such as あの山. It can also mean a person known to both whom one points out. To stand alone as a substitute for a thing, use あれ. The filler あの… at the start of a conversation is a different use; in the examples, あの belongs to 人 or 山 respectively. The rare kanji spelling 彼の belongs here to the reading あの; this demonstrative is usually written in kana. In another context, 彼の can be read かれの \"his\"; that is a different use.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"カクレクマノミは海水魚です。","romaji":"Kakurekumanomi wa kaisuigyo desu.","german":"The clownfish is a saltwater fish.","furigana":"かいすいぎょ"},{"japanese":"水族館で海水魚を見ました。","romaji":"Suizokukan de kaisuigyo o mimashita.","german":"I saw saltwater fish at the aquarium.","furigana":"すいぞくかん|かいすいぎょ|み"}],"notes":"海水魚 are fish that live in the sea, that is, in salt water. The opposite is 淡水魚 for freshwater fish. 海水魚です assigns a species of fish to this group; 水族館で海水魚を見る tells of an observation in an aquarium. The ending 魚 is read ぎょ here and belongs to the compound word for the animal group.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"北国のくらしはどうですか？","romaji":"Kitaguni no kurashi wa dō desu ka?","german":"What is life like in the northern region?","furigana":"きたぐに"},{"japanese":"北国の冬は寒いです。","romaji":"Kitaguni no fuyu wa samui desu.","german":"Winters in the north are cold.","furigana":"きたぐに|ふゆ|さむ"}],"notes":"北国 can denote a northern country or region. In a Japanese context it often means an area in the north; it is not a fixed country name. 北国のくらし asks about life there, and 北国の冬 describes a season in that area. The reading きたぐに contains ぐに as the voiced form of 国.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"西日がまぶしくて、テレビが見えません。","romaji":"Nishibi ga mabushikute, terebi ga miemasen.","german":"The evening sun is so bright that I cannot see the TV.","furigana":"にしび|み"},{"japanese":"西日が眩しいです。","romaji":"Nishibi ga mabushii desu.","german":"The evening sun is dazzling.","furigana":"にしび|まぶ"},{"japanese":"西日を避けるため、カーテンを閉めました。","romaji":"Nishibi o sakeru tame, kāten o shimemashita.","german":"I closed the curtains to keep out the western sun.","furigana":"にしび|さ|し"}],"notes":"西日 is sunlight coming in from the west, especially in the afternoon or toward evening. 西日がまぶしい describes this light being dazzling. It can interfere with watching TV or be a reason to close curtains. The word is read にしび; it does not refer only to a sunset on the horizon.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"東アジアにはたくさんの山があります。","romaji":"Higashi Ajia ni wa takusan no yama ga arimasu.","german":"There are many mountains in East Asia.","furigana":"ひがし|やま"},{"japanese":"東アジアの文化に興味があります。","romaji":"Higashi Ajia no bunka ni kyōmi ga arimasu.","german":"I'm interested in the culture of East Asia.","furigana":"ひがし|ぶんか|きょうみ"}],"notes":"東アジア denotes the geographic region of East Asia. The reading here is ひがしアジア: 東 is read ひがし, and アジア is a loanword. 東アジアには names something within the region; 東アジアの文化 relates something to this region. It is a region name and not the nationality of an individual person.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"もうすぐラマダーンが明ける。","romaji":"Mō sugu ramadān ga akeru.","german":"Ramadan will be over soon.","furigana":"あ"},{"japanese":"夜が明けました。","romaji":"Yoru ga akemashita.","german":"Night has given way to dawn.","furigana":"よる|あ"}],"notes":"明ける is an intransitive Ichidan verb in these examples. 夜が明ける means that the night ends and it gets light. With a longer period like ラマダーンが明ける, it means \"to come to an end\". The subject takes が. The spelling 明ける emphasizes this change of time; 開ける often describes opening an object.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"円いフリスビーを子犬にプレゼントした。","romaji":"Marui furisubī o koinu ni purezento shita.","german":"I gave a round frisbee to the puppy as a present.","furigana":"まる|こいぬ"},{"japanese":"この石は円い形です。","romaji":"Kono ishi wa marui katachi desu.","german":"This stone has a round shape.","furigana":"いし|まる|かたち"},{"japanese":"円い紙を半分に折りました。","romaji":"Marui kami o hanbun ni orimashita.","german":"I folded the round piece of paper in half.","furigana":"まる|かみ|はんぶん|お"}],"notes":"円い means \"round\" here, especially with a circular, flat shape. The spelling 丸い, read the same, is often used for ball-shaped things. As an i-adjective, 円い stands directly before the noun: 円い紙. Before an action, 円く can describe the manner; the past is 円かった. The examples look at shapes and at handling a round piece of paper.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"兄弟を目の色で見分ける。","romaji":"Kyōdai o me no iro de miwakeru.","german":"I tell the brothers apart by their eye color.","furigana":"きょうだい|め|いろ|みわ"},{"japanese":"双子を見分けるのは難しいです。","romaji":"Futago o miwakeru no wa muzukashii desu.","german":"It is difficult to tell twins apart.","furigana":"ふたご|みわ|むずか"},{"japanese":"この機械は、赤い玉と青い玉を見分けます。","romaji":"Kono kikai wa, akai tama to aoi tama o miwakemasu.","german":"This machine distinguishes red balls from blue balls.","furigana":"きかい|あか|たま|あお|たま|みわ"}],"notes":"見分ける means to tell things or people apart by recognizable features. The thing distinguished takes を, the feature often で: 目の色で見分ける. 見分けるのは makes the action the topic. This transitive Ichidan verb forms 見分けます and 見分けない; the spelling should not be equated with 分ける in the sense of dividing.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"次回は、いつですか？","romaji":"Jikai wa, itsu desu ka?","german":"When is the next time?","furigana":"じかい"},{"japanese":"次回も頑張りましょう。","romaji":"Jikai mo ganbarimashō.","german":"Let's do our best next time too.","furigana":"じかい|がんば"}],"notes":"次回 denotes the next issue, session or occasion of a repeated thing. It sounds more formal than 今度 and does not fix a particular day. 次回は、いつですか asks for the date; 次回も connects the next occasion to the previous one. With の it can modify a noun, as in 次回の授業.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"さっきテレビで中東のニュースを見ました。","romaji":"Sakki terebi de Chūtō no nyūsu o mimashita.","german":"I just saw news about the Middle East on TV.","furigana":"ちゅうとう|み"},{"japanese":"中東に行ったことがありますか。","romaji":"Chūtō ni itta koto ga arimasu ka.","german":"Have you ever been to the Middle East?","furigana":"ちゅうとう|い"}],"notes":"中東 is the region name \"Middle East\". With の it combines with topics like 中東のニュース; with 行く, に names the travel destination. The word is a noun, not an adjective that can be used independently. The boundaries of the region so named can be drawn differently depending on the context; the entry therefore gives no fixed list of countries.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日が出て、辺りが明るくなってきました。","romaji":"Hi ga dete, atari ga akaruku natte kimashita.","german":"The sun came up, and it is gradually getting lighter all around.","furigana":"ひ|で|あた|あか"},{"japanese":"この辺りは静かですね。","romaji":"Kono atari wa shizuka desu ne.","german":"It's quiet around here, isn't it?","furigana":"あた|しず"}],"notes":"辺り here refers to the immediate surroundings without drawing an exact boundary. この辺り is \"around here\"; 辺りが明るくなる describes a change in the surroundings. The word can also appear in approximate statements, but the examples practice its spatial meaning. For a precisely defined address, you name the place more concretely.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"たくさんの車が通る。","romaji":"Takusan no kuruma ga tōru.","german":"Lots of cars pass by.","furigana":"くるま|とお"},{"japanese":"この道を通って学校に行きます。","romaji":"Kono michi o tōtte gakkō ni ikimasu.","german":"I go to school by this road.","furigana":"みち|とお|がっこう|い"}],"notes":"通る here means passing by, driving past, or using a route. Although the verb is intransitive, を in この道を通る marks the route traveled. The destination follows with に: 学校に行く. This godan verb ending in る forms 通ります, 通って and 通らない; it therefore does not belong to the ichidan group like 見る.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このエンジンは百馬力です。","romaji":"Kono enjin wa hyaku bariki desu.","german":"Dieser Motor hat 100 PS.","furigana":"ひゃくばりき"},{"japanese":"この車は馬力がありますね。","romaji":"Kono kuruma wa bariki ga arimasu ne.","german":"Dieses Auto hat viel Leistung, nicht wahr?","furigana":"くるま|ばりき"}],"notes":"馬力 bezeichnet die Motorleistung, die man in Pferdestärken angeben kann. Zahl plus 馬力 nennt den Leistungswert: 百馬力 sind 100 PS. 馬力がある beschreibt einen leistungsstarken Motor ohne eine genaue Zahl. Die Wörter 高い馬力 oder 馬力がある werden im Deutschen meist natürlicher mit „hohe Leistung“ oder „leistungsstark“ wiedergegeben als mit einer einzelnen „Pferdestärke“.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このアパートには、たくさんの住人がいます。","romaji":"Kono apāto ni wa, takusan no jūnin ga imasu.","german":"Many people live in this apartment building.","furigana":"じゅうにん"},{"japanese":"このマンションの住人です。","romaji":"Kono manshon no jūnin desu.","german":"I am a resident of this apartment building.","furigana":"じゅうにん"}],"notes":"住人 is a person who lives in a place. このマンションの住人 assigns someone to a residential building, while 住人がいます names the people who live there. The word concerns the place of residence, not nationality. アパート refers to the residential building in the examples; for a single apartment, 部屋 would be more precise.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"医大に入るにはお金がかかります。","romaji":"Idai ni hairu ni wa okane ga kakarimasu.","german":"Getting into a medical university costs money.","furigana":"いだい|はい|かね"},{"japanese":"医大に入りたいです。","romaji":"Idai ni hairitai desu.","german":"I want to get into a medical university.","furigana":"いだい|はい"}],"notes":"医大 is the short form for a medical college or university. 医大に入る here means to be admitted there and start studying; it is not merely about entering a building. 入るには names the requirement or cost of this admission. With 入りたい, one expresses one's own wish to study at such a university.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大学では医学を学びます。","romaji":"Daigaku de wa igaku o manabimasu.","german":"I study medicine at university.","furigana":"だいがく|いがく|まな"},{"japanese":"医学を勉強しています。","romaji":"Igaku o benkyō shite imasu.","german":"I'm studying medicine.","furigana":"いがく|べんきょう"},{"japanese":"この本には、医学の歴史が書いてあります。","romaji":"Kono hon ni wa, igaku no rekishi ga kaite arimasu.","german":"This book describes the history of medicine.","furigana":"ほん|いがく|れきし|か"}],"notes":"医学 is medicine as a science or field of study. 医学を学ぶ and 医学を勉強する describe studying this subject; の connects it with, for example, 発展, the development of the field. Neither a single medication nor medical treatment is meant here. For a medication 薬 is used, and for treatment something like 治療.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 47"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この国は、かつて君主国だったそうです。","romaji":"Kono kuni wa, katsute kunshukoku datta sō desu.","german":"I hear this country used to be a monarchy.","furigana":"くに|くんしゅこく"},{"japanese":"イギリスは君主国です。","romaji":"Igirisu wa kunshukoku desu.","german":"The United Kingdom is a monarchy.","furigana":"くんしゅこく"}],"notes":"君主国 is a state with a monarch as head of state. The word refers to the state, not to the individual ruler. By itself it does not say how much political power that person holds. かつて and だったそうです report a former form of government, while 君主国です gives a present classification.","levelNote":"Compound: 君主 (N1) + 国 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"マイケル君には兄弟がいません。","romaji":"Maikeru-kun ni wa kyōdai ga imasen.","german":"Michael has no siblings.","furigana":"くん|きょうだい"},{"japanese":"山田君、おはようございます。","romaji":"Yamada-kun, ohayō gozaimasu.","german":"Good morning, Yamada!","furigana":"やまだくん"}],"notes":"〜君 is read くん and attached to a name, for example 山田君. It is often used to address boys or younger men who are familiar to the speaker and of equal or lower status. The form is not a general respectful address for just anyone; when in doubt, さん is often the better choice. It can occur both in direct address and when speaking about someone. The symbol 〜 is only a placeholder for the name. The spelling without the placeholder is 君; 〜 stands here for the preceding name.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ドルに対する円のレートは、今いくらですか？","romaji":"Doru ni taisuru en no rēto wa, ima ikura desu ka?","german":"What is the exchange rate of the yen against the dollar right now?","furigana":"たい|えん|いま"},{"japanese":"この問題に対して意見があります。","romaji":"Kono mondai ni taishite iken ga arimasu.","german":"I have an opinion on this problem.","furigana":"もんだい|たい|いけん"}],"notes":"In these examples, 対する connects a topic or a standard of comparison with a statement. 名詞に対する stands before a noun: ドルに対する円のレート. 名詞に対して stands before the predicate, for example when expressing an opinion about a problem. The te-form is 対して, the polite form 対します. \"Against\" here means a relationship or comparison, not necessarily a spatial position.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"東京支局からポートランドの本社にもどって下さい。","romaji":"Tōkyō shikyoku kara Pōtorando no honsha ni modotte kudasai.","german":"Please return from the Tokyo branch office to the headquarters in Portland.","furigana":"とうきょうしきょく|ほんしゃ|くだ"},{"japanese":"新聞社の支局で働いています。","romaji":"Shinbunsha no shikyoku de hataraite imasu.","german":"I work at a branch office of a newspaper company.","furigana":"しんぶんしゃ|しきょく|はたら"}],"notes":"支局 is a branch office, especially of an agency, news agency or newspaper editorial office. 本社, by contrast, denotes the headquarters of a company. 支局から names the starting point of a return; 支局で names the workplace. For an ordinary bank or store branch, 支店 is the more common word.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日、役人が来ましたよ。","romaji":"Kyō, yakunin ga kimashita yo.","german":"Heute kam ein Beamter.","furigana":"きょう|やくにん|き"},{"japanese":"あの役人はとても真面目です。","romaji":"Ano yakunin wa totemo majime desu.","german":"Jener Beamte ist sehr gewissenhaft.","furigana":"やくにん|まじめ"}],"notes":"役人 bezeichnet jemanden, der für eine staatliche oder kommunale Verwaltung arbeitet. Im Deutschen passt oft „Beamter“, ohne dass damit eine genaue deutsche beamtenrechtliche Stellung behauptet wird. 役人が来る berichtet von einem Besuch; あの役人 bestimmt eine bestimmte Person, deren Verhalten man beurteilt. In förmlichen Berufsangaben hört man auch 公務員.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この学校の先生になることは、大役ですよ。","romaji":"Kono gakkō no sensei ni naru koto wa, taiyaku desu yo.","german":"Becoming a teacher at this school is a big responsibility.","furigana":"がっこう|せんせい|たいやく"},{"japanese":"大役を任されました。","romaji":"Taiyaku o makasaremashita.","german":"I was entrusted with an important task.","furigana":"たいやく|まか"}],"notes":"大役 is a particularly important or responsible task. This entry's reading is たいやく. 大役を任される means that someone entrusts you with this responsibility; 大役です assesses an activity as a great responsibility. The word means more than merely long-lasting work. The examples use the general meaning of a task, not a special role in the theater.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"心身のために毎日ヨガをしましょう。","romaji":"Shinshin no tame ni mainichi yoga o shimashō.","german":"Let's do yoga every day for mind and body.","furigana":"しんしん|まいにち"},{"japanese":"心身ともに健康です。","romaji":"Shinshin tomo ni kenkō desu.","german":"I am healthy in both mind and body.","furigana":"しんしん|けんこう"}],"notes":"心身 combines mind and body. 心身のために names both as the purpose of an action; 心身ともに emphasizes that a statement applies to both. The word itself is a noun and connects to another noun with の. しんしん is pronounced without long vowels; the components 心 and 身 are not separate words in this compound.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一日に一回、全身にスプレーして下さい。","romaji":"Ichinichi ni ikkai, zenshin ni supurē shite kudasai.","german":"Please spray your whole body once a day.","furigana":"いちにち|いっかい|ぜんしん|くだ"},{"japanese":"全身が筋肉痛です。","romaji":"Zenshin ga kinnikutsū desu.","german":"My whole body has sore muscles.","furigana":"ぜんしん|きんにくつう"}],"notes":"全身 means the whole body. With に it can name the target of an application, with が the body as the bearer of a sensation: 全身が筋肉痛です. Before a noun, の is often used, such as 全身の写真 for a photo showing the whole person. 全身 is itself a noun, not an i- or na-adjective.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"両者の考えが同じじゃない。","romaji":"Ryōsha no kangae ga onaji ja nai.","german":"The two sides' views are not the same.","furigana":"りょうしゃ|かんが|おな"},{"japanese":"両者の意見を聞きました。","romaji":"Ryōsha no iken o kikimashita.","german":"I listened to the opinions of both sides.","furigana":"りょうしゃ|いけん|き"}],"notes":"両者 sums up two people, groups or sides that are already known. It sounds more formal than 二人 and can also refer to two parties. 両者の考え compares their views; 両者の意見を聞く describes letting both have their say. For more than two parties, 両者 is not the right collective term.","levelNote":"Compound: 者 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この本の作者に会いたいです。","romaji":"Kono hon no sakusha ni aitai desu.","german":"I want to meet the author of this book.","furigana":"ほん|さくしゃ|あ"},{"japanese":"この本の作者は有名です。","romaji":"Kono hon no sakusha wa yūmei desu.","german":"The author of this book is famous.","furigana":"ほん|さくしゃ|ゆうめい"},{"japanese":"この絵の作者は、私の友達です。","romaji":"Kono e no sakusha wa, watashi no tomodachi desu.","german":"The creator of this picture is a friend of mine.","furigana":"え|さくしゃ|わたし|ともだち"}],"notes":"作者 is the person who created a work. For a book it means \"author\"; for a picture it can denote its creator or artist. 作品の作者 assigns the person to the work. 作者に会う names them as the target of a meeting, and 作者が書く or 描く names them as the acting person. Not every artist is automatically the 作者 of the work currently being looked at.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの男の人は有名な工学者です。","romaji":"Ano otoko no hito wa yūmei na kōgakusha desu.","german":"That man is a famous engineer.","furigana":"おとこ|ひと|ゆうめい|こうがくしゃ"},{"japanese":"工学者が新しい機械を作りました。","romaji":"Kōgakusha ga atarashii kikai o tsukurimashita.","german":"The engineer built a new machine.","furigana":"こうがくしゃ|あたら|きかい|つく"}],"notes":"工学者 is someone who works professionally in engineering. The word is built from 工学 and 者 but is used as a whole noun. 有名な工学者 describes a well-known specialist; 工学者が names them as the actor in developing a machine. The examples use \"engineer\" without specifying a particular academic degree or job title.","levelNote":"Compound: 工学 (N3) + 者 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"土の中から古いお皿が見つかったのが、この場所です。","romaji":"Tsuchi no naka kara furui osara ga mitsukatta no ga, kono basho desu.","german":"This is the place where an old plate was found in the soil.","furigana":"つち|なか|ふる|さら|み|ばしょ"},{"japanese":"待ち合わせの場所はどこですか。","romaji":"Machiawase no basho wa doko desu ka.","german":"Where is the meeting place?","furigana":"ま|あ|ばしょ"}],"notes":"場所 names a place or a specific spot. この場所です can point to a spot where something happened. 待ち合わせの場所 identifies it as the meeting point; the question どこですか asks for the location. This entry practices the spatial meaning, not the special use for a sumo tournament.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 26"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"入場する前にチケットを買って下さい。","romaji":"Nyūjō suru mae ni chiketto o katte kudasai.","german":"Please buy a ticket before you enter.","furigana":"にゅうじょう|まえ|か|くだ"},{"japanese":"入場料は五百円です。","romaji":"Nyūjōryō wa gohyaku en desu.","german":"The admission fee is 500 yen.","furigana":"にゅうじょうりょう|ごひゃくえん"}],"notes":"入場 refers to entering an event hall or venue. With する it becomes the intransitive 入場する, \"to enter\"; no direct object with を is needed. 入場料, by contrast, is the compound noun for the admission fee. In the first example, buying the ticket counts as a step before entering; in the second, the price is stated.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ここじゃなくて、どこか他所の店に行こうよ。","romaji":"Koko ja nakute, dokoka yoso no mise ni ikō yo.","german":"Let's go to some other store instead of this one.","furigana":"よそ|みせ|い"},{"japanese":"他所のことは気にしないでください。","romaji":"Yoso no koto wa ki ni shinaide kudasai.","german":"Please do not worry about other people's affairs.","furigana":"よそ|き"}],"notes":"他所 is read よそ here and is often written only in kana. 他所の店 is a store other than the current one; 他所のこと here means the affairs of other people or groups. The word contrasts something outside one's own place or circle. The reading よそ must not be replaced by the other possible character readings as different words. 余所 is another kanji spelling of よそ; the kana form is common here as well.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"毎日の朝ごはんはベーコンとパンです。","romaji":"Mainichi no asagohan wa bēkon to pan desu.","german":"My daily breakfast is bacon and bread.","furigana":"まいにち|あさ"},{"japanese":"毎朝朝ごはんを食べます。","romaji":"Maiasa asagohan o tabemasu.","german":"I eat breakfast every morning.","furigana":"まいあさあさ|た"},{"japanese":"今朝は時間がなくて、朝ごはんを食べませんでした。","romaji":"Kesa wa jikan ga nakute, asagohan o tabemasen deshita.","german":"I had no time this morning, so I did not eat breakfast.","furigana":"けさ|じかん|あさ|た"}],"notes":"朝ごはん is breakfast as a meal. In everyday use, ごはん here is not limited to rice: breakfast can also consist of bread. 朝ごはんは describes the content of the meal, 朝ごはんを食べる the eating. In the additional example, 時間がなくて gives the reason the meal was skipped.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 6"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"明るい朝日の中、ヨガをします。","romaji":"Akarui asahi no naka, yoga o shimasu.","german":"I do yoga in the bright morning sun.","furigana":"あか|あさひ|なか"},{"japanese":"朝日がきれいですね。","romaji":"Asahi ga kirei desu ne.","german":"The morning sun is beautiful, isn't it?","furigana":"あさひ"},{"japanese":"この部屋には、東の窓から朝日が入ります。","romaji":"Kono heya ni wa, higashi no mado kara asahi ga hairimasu.","german":"Morning sunlight comes into this room through the east window.","furigana":"へや|ひがし|まど|あさひ|はい"}],"notes":"朝日 is the sun, or its light, in the morning. 朝日の中 names the light as the surroundings of an activity; 朝日がきれい describes the sight. 朝日が入る can express that morning sunlight falls in through a window. The nature word is meant here, not a family, place or company name with the same characters.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"休みの日は、茶道の教室に行きます。","romaji":"Yasumi no hi wa, sadō no kyōshitsu ni ikimasu.","german":"On my days off, I go to a tea ceremony class.","furigana":"やす|ひ|さどう|きょうしつ|い"},{"japanese":"柔道の試合を見ました。","romaji":"Jūdō no shiai o mimashita.","german":"I watched a judo match.","furigana":"じゅうどう|しあい|み"}],"notes":"〜道 serves as a word element for a way or a practiced art, as in 茶道 \"tea ceremony\" and 柔道 \"judo\". Here it is read どう with a long o. 〜 shows the place of the preceding word part. The standalone 道 \"road, way\" with the reading みち is a different use.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"試験まで、あと一日。時間が足りません。","romaji":"Shiken made, ato ichinichi. Jikan ga tarimasen.","german":"One day until the exam. I don't have enough time.","furigana":"しけん|いちにち|じかん|た"},{"japanese":"友達と楽しい時間を過ごしました。","romaji":"Tomodachi to tanoshii jikan o sugoshimashita.","german":"I had a nice time with a friend.","furigana":"ともだち|たの|じかん|す"}],"notes":"時間 is time that one has or needs for something. 時間が足りない means that it is not enough. With a number, 時間 also gives a duration in hours: 三時間 \"three hours\". 楽しい時間 is a pleasantly spent time. For a clock time, 時 without 間 is used, as in 三時.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 9"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"図書館で三時間勉強しました。","romaji":"Toshokan de sanjikan benkyō shimashita.","german":"I studied for three hours at the library.","furigana":"としょかん|さんじかんべんきょう"},{"japanese":"二週間、旅行しました。","romaji":"Nishūkan, ryokō shimashita.","german":"I traveled for two weeks.","furigana":"にしゅうかん|りょこう"}],"notes":"〜間 after a time expression denotes a duration: 三時間 \"three hours\", 二週間 \"two weeks\". Here it is read かん. Three o'clock is 三時, a duration of three hours is 三時間. 〜 is a placeholder for the preceding word part; the whole duration can stand with the verb without any further particle. The spelling without the placeholder is 間; 〜 stands here for the preceding time expression.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人間は言葉で考えを伝えます。","romaji":"Ningen wa kotoba de kangae o tsutaemasu.","german":"Humans convey their thoughts with words.","furigana":"にんげん|ことば|かんが|つた"},{"japanese":"この物語では、人間と動物が一緒に暮らしています。","romaji":"Kono monogatari de wa, ningen to dōbutsu ga issho ni kurashite imasu.","german":"In this story, humans and animals live together.","furigana":"ものがたり|にんげん|どうぶつ|いっしょ|く"}],"notes":"人間 is a human being or humankind as a whole. 人 is read にん here, 間 げん. 人間が names humans as the subject; 人間と動物 connects humans with animals. In a general statement about 人間, a single person is not necessarily meant.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の家は、安心できる空間です。","romaji":"Watashi no ie wa, anshin dekiru kūkan desu.","german":"My home is a space where I can feel at ease.","furigana":"わたし|いえ|あんしん|くうかん"},{"japanese":"机と机の間に空間を作りました。","romaji":"Tsukue to tsukue no aida ni kūkan o tsukurimashita.","german":"I made some space between the two desks.","furigana":"つくえ|つくえ|あいだ|くうかん|つく"}],"notes":"空間 is a space or free room, not necessarily an enclosed room. It can also denote an area perceived as pleasant. 安心できる空間 describes a place where one can feel secure. 空間を作る means to make room. くうかん has a long u.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"数字を入力するのが下手です。","romaji":"Sūji o nyūryoku suru no ga heta desu.","german":"I am bad at entering numbers.","furigana":"すうじ|にゅうりょく|へた"},{"japanese":"この数字は六です。","romaji":"Kono sūji wa roku desu.","german":"This digit is a six.","furigana":"すうじ|ろく"}],"notes":"数字 are numerals or numbers, for example when reading or entering them. この数字は六です identifies a digit. 数字を入力する makes them the content being entered. The expression 下手です judges a person's ability, not the number itself. すうじ begins with a long u.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この会社のスタッフの人数は二十人です。","romaji":"Kono kaisha no sutaffu no ninzū wa nijūnin desu.","german":"The number of staff at this company is twenty.","furigana":"かいしゃ|にんずう|にじゅうにん"},{"japanese":"参加者の人数を教えてください。","romaji":"Sankasha no ninzū o oshiete kudasai.","german":"Please tell me the number of participants.","furigana":"さんかしゃ|にんずう|おし"}],"notes":"人数 is the number of people. A number of people is stated with 人: 二十人 \"twenty people\". 人数 stands for the count itself, for example when asking for the number of participants. The reading is にんずう with a long u; 人数 is not translated as a single person.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今回の点数は百点でした。","romaji":"Konkai no tensū wa hyakuten deshita.","german":"This time my score was 100 points.","furigana":"こんかい|てんすう|ひゃくてん"},{"japanese":"前より点数が上がりました。","romaji":"Mae yori tensū ga agarimashita.","german":"My score has gone up compared to before.","furigana":"まえ|てんすう|あ"}],"notes":"点数 is a score, for example on a test or in a game. 百点 gives one hundred points; 点数 says what kind of figure is involved. 点数が上がる describes improved performance. The reading てんすう ends with a long u. The number of points can be stated explicitly in the sentence or left open.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"東京は大きい町です。","romaji":"Tōkyō wa ōkii machi desu.","german":"Tokyo is a big city.","furigana":"とうきょう|おお|まち"},{"japanese":"東京に住んでいます。","romaji":"Tōkyō ni sunde imasu.","german":"I live in Tokyo.","furigana":"とうきょう|す"}],"notes":"東京 is Tokyo. It is read とうきょう with two long o sounds; きょ forms a single mora. 東京に住む names the city as the place of residence. With 東京は大きい町です, a property of the city is described. The name itself is a noun and is not inflected like an adjective.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"山の向こうには何が見えますか？","romaji":"Yama no mukō ni wa nani ga miemasu ka?","german":"What can you see on the other side of the mountains?","furigana":"やま|む|なに|み"},{"japanese":"向こうに病院があります。","romaji":"Mukō ni byōin ga arimasu.","german":"There is a hospital over there.","furigana":"む|びょういん"}],"notes":"向こう can mean the other side or a place over there. 山の向こう is the other side of the mountains. With 向こうに, one asks or reports about something at a distant place. It is a place noun here; the reading むこう has a long o.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 35"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この映画は、全米で人気があります。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa, zenbei de ninki ga arimasu.","german":"This movie is popular across the U.S.","furigana":"えいが|ぜんべい|にんき"},{"japanese":"全米の天気を調べました。","romaji":"Zenbei no tenki o shirabemashita.","german":"I checked the weather across the U.S.","furigana":"ぜんべい|てんき|しら"}],"notes":"全米 here means the whole United States. 全米で names the U.S. as the area of an action or a widespread property. 全米の天気 refers to the weather in the whole country. 米 is read べい; the reading of the whole word is ぜんべい.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"それ以上、川に近づくな！","romaji":"Sore ijō, kawa ni chikazuku na!","german":"Do not go any closer to the river!","furigana":"いじょう|かわ|ちか"},{"japanese":"春が近づいてきました。","romaji":"Haru ga chikazuite kimashita.","german":"Spring is approaching.","furigana":"はる|ちか"},{"japanese":"仕事で何度も会ううちに、二人は少しずつ近づいていった。","romaji":"Shigoto de nando mo au uchi ni, futari wa sukoshi zutsu chikazuite itta.","german":"As the two kept meeting through work, they gradually grew closer.","furigana":"しごと|なんど|あ|ふたり|すこ|ちか"}],"notes":"近づく is an intransitive godan verb: 近づきます, 近づいて. With に you name the target of the approach. Seasons can also figuratively come closer: 春が近づく. Verb + な is a harsh prohibition and sounds much stronger than a polite request. The reading ちかづく is written with z in Hepburn (chikazuku). 近付く is another spelling with the same reading ちかづく. With people, the verb can also mean getting closer to each other or getting to know someone better; the social context makes this meaning clear.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"田中先生はお休みなので、代わりの先生が来ます。","romaji":"Tanaka-sensei wa oyasumi na node, kawari no sensei ga kimasu.","german":"Mr. Tanaka is off today, so a substitute teacher is coming.","furigana":"たなかせんせい|やす|か|せんせい|き"},{"japanese":"その代わりに何をしましょうか。","romaji":"Sono kawari ni nani o shimashō ka.","german":"What shall we do instead?","furigana":"か|なに"}],"notes":"代わり is a replacement or substitute, and a noun. 代わりの先生 refers to a substitute teacher. その代わりに can mean \"instead\" and suggest a different action. The reading is かわり; the related verb 代わる, by contrast, ends in る.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あなたと全く同じ考えです。","romaji":"Anata to mattaku onaji kangae desu.","german":"I have exactly the same opinion as you.","furigana":"まった|おな|かんが"},{"japanese":"全く問題ありません。","romaji":"Mattaku mondai arimasen.","german":"There is no problem at all.","furigana":"まった|もんだい"}],"notes":"全く intensifies a statement: \"completely\", \"entirely\" or \"at all\". 全く同じ考え emphasizes complete agreement; 全く問題ありません emphasizes that there is no problem. It is not limited to negative sentences. まったく exclaimed on its own can also express annoyance; here the examples practice the intensifying meaning.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この前、木の上に毛虫がいたんです。","romaji":"Kono mae, ki no ue ni kemushi ga ita n desu.","german":"The other day, there was a caterpillar on the tree.","furigana":"まえ|き|うえ|けむし"},{"japanese":"この前の日曜日に映画を見ました。","romaji":"Kono mae no nichiyōbi ni eiga o mimashita.","german":"Last Sunday I watched a movie.","furigana":"まえ|にちようび|えいが|み"}],"notes":"この前 is a time expression here for \"the other day\" or an earlier point in time. The distance from the present remains open. この前の日曜日 means the most recent Sunday. 前 is read まえ on this card. The phrase can place the sentence in time directly or, with の, specify a time expression.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お知らせの紙がポストに入ってました。","romaji":"Oshirase no kami ga posuto ni haittemashita.","german":"A notice was in the mailbox.","furigana":"し|かみ|はい"},{"japanese":"大切なお知らせがあります。","romaji":"Taisetsu na oshirase ga arimasu.","german":"I have an important announcement.","furigana":"たいせつ|し"}],"notes":"お知らせ is a notice or announcement. The お belongs to the polite word form. お知らせの紙 means a sheet of paper with a notice; お知らせがあります announces that there is something to report. The meaning does not specify whether the notice is printed, spoken or electronic.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 28"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"工作が出来上がるのに、三日かかりました。","romaji":"Kōsaku ga dekiagaru no ni, mikka kakarimashita.","german":"It took three days for the craft project to be finished.","furigana":"こうさく|できあ|みっか"},{"japanese":"料理が出来上がりました。","romaji":"Ryōri ga dekiagarimashita.","german":"The meal is ready.","furigana":"りょうり|できあ"}],"notes":"出来上がる is an intransitive godan verb: 出来上がります, 出来上がって. With が you name what is finished, such as a craft project or a meal. In 出来上がるのに三日かかる, のに names the time required here, not a contrast. Three days is read みっか.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"白い毛の中に黒い毛が交じる。","romaji":"Shiroi ke no naka ni kuroi ke ga majiru.","german":"Black hairs are mixed in among the white ones.","furigana":"しろ|け|なか|くろ|け|ま"},{"japanese":"子供が大人に交じって遊んでいます。","romaji":"Kodomo ga otona ni majitte asonde imasu.","german":"The child is playing, mingling with the adults.","furigana":"こども|おとな|ま|あそ"}],"notes":"交じる is an intransitive godan verb even though it ends in いる: 交じります, 交じって. It can mean being mixed in or mingling among people. With が you name who or what is among them; に names the group or area. For material mixtures, the spelling 混じる is often seen.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"うちの犬は、雪を見ると走り回る。","romaji":"Uchi no inu wa, yuki o miru to hashirimawaru.","german":"Our dog runs around when it sees snow.","furigana":"いぬ|ゆき|み|はし|まわ"},{"japanese":"子供たちが公園で走り回っています。","romaji":"Kodomotachi ga kōen de hashirimawatte imasu.","german":"The children are running around in the park.","furigana":"こども|こうえん|はし|まわ"}],"notes":"走り回る means to run around in various places. The whole verb is intransitive and takes godan endings: 走り回ります, 走り回って. With が you name the person or animal running, with で the place. 雪を見ると can describe an event that regularly triggers something.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"父は、お茶の値段を当てるのが上手です。","romaji":"Chichi wa, ocha no nedan o ateru no ga jōzu desu.","german":"My father is good at guessing the price of tea.","furigana":"ちち|ちゃ|ねだん|あ|じょうず"},{"japanese":"答えを当ててください。","romaji":"Kotae o atete kudasai.","german":"Please guess the answer.","furigana":"こた|あ"}],"notes":"当てる here means to guess something correctly. The sought answer or detail takes を. It is a transitive ichidan verb: 当てます, 当てて. お茶の値段を当てる means to guess the price of the tea; 答えを当ててください is a request to give the right answer.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"メイクで別人に化けました。","romaji":"Meiku de betsujin ni bakemashita.","german":"I transformed into a different person with makeup.","furigana":"べつじん|ば"},{"japanese":"この物語では、狐が人に化けます。","romaji":"Kono monogatari de wa, kitsune ga hito ni bakemasu.","german":"In this story, a fox turns into a person.","furigana":"ものがたり|きつね|ひと|ば"}],"notes":"化ける describes a transformation, for example in stories, or figuratively a greatly changed appearance. It is an intransitive ichidan verb: 化けます, 化けて. With に you name the assumed form: 人に化ける, 別人に化ける. メイクで names the means of the change.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ノックの後、ドアノブを回す音がしました。","romaji":"Nokku no ato, doanobu o mawasu oto ga shimashita.","german":"After the knock, I heard the sound of a doorknob turning.","furigana":"あと|まわ|おと"},{"japanese":"ハンドルを右に回してください。","romaji":"Handoru o migi ni mawashite kudasai.","german":"Please turn the steering wheel to the right.","furigana":"みぎ|まわ"}],"notes":"回す is a transitive godan verb: 回します, 回して. With を you name what is turned, such as a doorknob or steering wheel. ドアノブを回す音 contains a whole action as a description of the sound. 右に gives the direction of the turn. The intransitive 回る means that something turns by itself.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私自身はコーヒーもお茶も両方のみます。","romaji":"Watashi jishin wa kōhī mo ocha mo ryōhō nomimasu.","german":"I myself drink both coffee and tea.","furigana":"わたしじしん|ちゃ|りょうほう"},{"japanese":"私自身がやります。","romaji":"Watashi jishin ga yarimasu.","german":"I will do it myself.","furigana":"わたしじしん"}],"notes":"私自身 emphasizes \"I myself\" or \"I personally\". 私自身は can highlight one's own habit; 私自身が emphasizes that I am the one taking on the action. 私自身の考え highlights one's own opinion. The reading on this card is わたしじしん.","levelNote":"Compound: 私 (N5) + 自身 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私立大学に入学したいです。","romaji":"Shiritsu daigaku ni nyūgaku shitai desu.","german":"I want to enroll in a private university.","furigana":"しりつだいがく|にゅうがく"},{"japanese":"この私立大学の学費は高いです。","romaji":"Kono shiritsu daigaku no gakuhi wa takai desu.","german":"The tuition at this private university is high.","furigana":"しりつだいがく|がくひ|たか"}],"notes":"私立大学 is a privately run university. The word is read しりつだいがく. 私立大学に入学する means to enroll or be admitted there. 私立大学の学費 refers to its tuition; the term alone does not fix any particular fee level.","levelNote":"Compound: 私立 (N3) + 大学 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その活用形は、前回のクラスで学びました。","romaji":"Sono katsuyōkei wa, zenkai no kurasu de manabimashita.","german":"I learned that conjugation form in the last class.","furigana":"かつようけい|ぜんかい|まな"},{"japanese":"前回はここで昼ご飯を食べました。","romaji":"Zenkai wa koko de hirugohan o tabemashita.","german":"Last time, I ate lunch here.","furigana":"ぜんかい|ひる|はん|た"}],"notes":"前回 is the last time, that is, the previous episode or session in a series. 前回のクラス names the previous class. 前回は can set an earlier occasion as the topic. The reading is ぜんかい; it means the previous repetition, not automatically yesterday.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地上五階、地下二階のビルです。","romaji":"Chijō gokai, chika nikai no biru desu.","german":"It's a building with five floors above ground and two below.","furigana":"ちじょうごかい|ちかにかい"},{"japanese":"飛行機から地上の町が見えます。","romaji":"Hikōki kara chijō no machi ga miemasu.","german":"From the plane, you can see the town on the ground.","furigana":"ひこうき|ちじょう|まち|み"}],"notes":"地上 denotes the area above the ground or the surface of the earth. With の it describes a noun: 地上のフロア \"an above-ground floor\". 地上に names a place above the ground. In building specifications, 地上 comes before the number of above-ground floors. ちじょう ends with a long o.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"もう少し後ろに向けることは出来ますか？","romaji":"Mō sukoshi ushiro ni mukeru koto wa dekimasu ka?","german":"Can you point it a little further back?","furigana":"すこ|うし|む|でき"},{"japanese":"私にカメラを向けないでください。","romaji":"Watashi ni kamera o mukenaide kudasai.","german":"Please do not point the camera at me.","furigana":"わたし|む"}],"notes":"向ける is a transitive ichidan verb: 向けます, 向けて. You aim an object or body part in a direction. The object takes を, the target takes に. 向けないでください asks that something not be pointed. The intransitive 向く describes the orientation of the person or thing itself.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お前は一体ここで何してるんだ？","romaji":"Omae wa ittai koko de nani shiteru n da?","german":"What on earth are you doing here?","furigana":"まえ|いったい|なに"},{"japanese":"お前はどう思う？","romaji":"Omae wa dō omou?","german":"What do you think?","furigana":"まえ|おも"}],"notes":"お前 is a very informal \"you\", often in male-coded speech. Depending on the relationship, it sounds familiar, rough or condescending. You also hear it in arguments. As a form of address it is unsuitable for polite conversation with unfamiliar people. It is a pronoun here; before は or が it behaves like a noun. おまえ is the kana spelling of the same pronoun. Another dictionary meaning refers to the presence of a person of high rank or a deity; it should be distinguished from the familiar pronoun on this card.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地中海にはキレイなビーチが広がっています。","romaji":"Chichūkai ni wa kirei na bīchi ga hirogatte imasu.","german":"A beautiful beach stretches along the Mediterranean.","furigana":"ちちゅうかい|ひろ"},{"japanese":"夏休みに地中海でクルーズをしました。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi ni chichūkai de kurūzu o shimashita.","german":"During summer vacation, I took a cruise on the Mediterranean.","furigana":"なつやす|ちちゅうかい"}],"notes":"地中海 is the proper name of the Mediterranean Sea. It is read ちちゅうかい with a long u; ちゅ is pronounced together. 地中海で names it as the place of an activity, 地中海の as a link to a noun. 地中海でクルーズをする means to take a cruise on the Mediterranean.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"水道の水が出ません。","romaji":"Suidō no mizu ga demasen.","german":"No water is coming out of the tap.","furigana":"すいどう|みず|で"},{"japanese":"水道の水をボトルに入れました。","romaji":"Suidō no mizu o botoru ni iremashita.","german":"I put tap water in a bottle.","furigana":"すいどう|みず|い"}],"notes":"水道 is the water supply or water pipe system. 水道の水 is tap water, and 水道から水が出る describes water coming out of it. 水道から names the source of the water. すいどう ends in a long o; the beginning すい stays sui.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 32"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"夜は車のヘッドライトを点ける。","romaji":"Yoru wa kuruma no heddoraito o tsukeru.","german":"At night I turn on the car's headlights.","furigana":"よる|くるま|つ"},{"japanese":"テレビを点けてください。","romaji":"Terebi o tsukete kudasai.","german":"Please turn on the TV.","furigana":"つ"}],"notes":"点ける is a transitive ichidan verb: 点けます, 点けて. It is used to switch on a light or a device; the device takes を. The verb is also often written つける. 点けてください is a request to turn something on. The spelling 点ける makes the meaning \"turn on/light\" clear here.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"毎日、ディナーを作るのは、お父さんの役目です。","romaji":"Mainichi, dinā o tsukuru no wa, otōsan no yakume desu.","german":"Cooking dinner every day is Dad's job.","furigana":"まいにち|つく|とう|やくめ"},{"japanese":"大切な役目を任されました。","romaji":"Taisetsu na yakume o makasaremashita.","german":"I was entrusted with an important role.","furigana":"たいせつ|やくめ|まか"}],"notes":"役目 is a task, role or function that someone takes on. お父さんの役目 names the person to whom the task is assigned. 役目を任される means to be given a task. A regular responsibility can be a 役目 just as much as a newly assumed task.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このシャツはコットン生地で出来ています。","romaji":"Kono shatsu wa kotton kiji de dekite imasu.","german":"This shirt is made of cotton fabric.","furigana":"きじ|でき"},{"japanese":"この生地は柔らかいです。","romaji":"Kono kiji wa yawarakai desu.","german":"This fabric is soft.","furigana":"きじ|やわ"}],"notes":"生地 here is fabric, that is, textile material. コットン生地 is cotton fabric. In 生地で出来ている, で names the material. この生地 can point to a fabric one is looking at or feeling. The reading on this card is きじ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の学校では九科目学びます。","romaji":"Watashi no gakkō de wa kyūkamoku manabimasu.","german":"At my school, we study nine subjects.","furigana":"わたし|がっこう|きゅうかもくまな"},{"japanese":"好きな科目は何ですか。","romaji":"Suki na kamoku wa nan desu ka.","german":"What is your favorite subject?","furigana":"す|かもく|なん"}],"notes":"科目 is a teaching or study subject. 好きな科目 asks about a favorite subject. With a number, you state the number of subjects: 九科目 \"nine subjects\". 科目を学ぶ names the subject as what is learned. The number of subjects depends on the particular curriculum.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本に来てから、数年がたちました。","romaji":"Nihon ni kite kara, sūnen ga tachimashita.","german":"Several years have passed since I came to Japan.","furigana":"にほん|き|すうねん"},{"japanese":"数年後にまた会いましょう。","romaji":"Sūnengo ni mata aimashō.","german":"Let's meet again in a few years.","furigana":"すうねんご|あ"}],"notes":"数年 is an imprecise expression for a few years. 数年前 means \"a few years ago\", 数年後 \"in a few years\". 来てから数年がたつ describes the time that has passed since an arrival. The reading is すうねん with a long u; the exact number of years remains open.","levelNote":"Compound: 年 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"絵文字を交ぜて、メッセージを入力しましょう。","romaji":"Emoji o mazete, messēji o nyūryoku shimashō.","german":"Let's type a message with some emoji mixed in.","furigana":"えもじ|ま|にゅうりょく"},{"japanese":"この絵文字はどういう意味ですか。","romaji":"Kono emoji wa dō iu imi desu ka.","german":"What does this emoji mean?","furigana":"えもじ|いみ"}],"notes":"絵文字 are emoji, small pictographic characters in messages. 絵文字を入れる or 使う describes inserting or using such characters. You can ask the meaning of a single emoji with この絵文字はどういう意味ですか. It is read えもじ; the j in the romanization represents じ.","levelNote":"Compound: 絵 (N5) + 文字 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"父は、プロの投手です。","romaji":"Chichi wa, puro no tōshu desu.","german":"My father is a professional pitcher.","furigana":"ちち|とうしゅ"},{"japanese":"あの投手は速い球を投げます。","romaji":"Ano tōshu wa hayai tama o nagemasu.","german":"That pitcher throws fast balls.","furigana":"とうしゅ|はや|たま|な"},{"japanese":"今日の試合の先発投手は誰ですか。","romaji":"Kyō no shiai no senpatsu tōshu wa dare desu ka?","german":"Who is today's starting pitcher?","furigana":"きょう|しあい|せんぱつとうしゅ|だれ"}],"notes":"投手 is the technical term for the pitcher in baseball; in conversation and sports broadcasts you hear the loanword ピッチャー just as often. Typical compounds are 先発投手 \"starting pitcher\", 救援投手 \"reliever\" and エース投手. The opponent is the batter 打者 (バッター), and the catcher is 捕手 (キャッチャー). The related verb is 投げる: 投手が球を投げる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"でも、できないよ。","romaji":"Demo, dekinai yo.","german":"But I can't do it."},{"japanese":"高いです。でも、おいしいです。","romaji":"Takai desu. Demo, oishii desu.","german":"It's expensive. But it's delicious.","furigana":"たか"}],"notes":"でも links two statements here with \"but\" and stands at the beginning of the second statement. It can introduce an objection or a contrasting evaluation. でも、できないよ is a personal objection; 高いです。でも、おいしいです contrasts price and taste. This use is common in conversation.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 12"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ここで食べよう！","romaji":"Koko de tabeyō!","german":"Let's eat here!","furigana":"た"},{"japanese":"ここに座ってください。","romaji":"Koko ni suwatte kudasai.","german":"Please sit here.","furigana":"すわ"}],"notes":"ここ points to a place near the speaker: \"here\". ここで names the place of an action, ここに a destination or position. ここで食べよう suggests eating at this place. ここに座ってください asks someone to sit at the indicated spot. 此処 is a rare kanji spelling of ここ. For the ordinary location \"here\", kana is mostly used.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日はおやつにマフィンを食べた。","romaji":"Kyō wa oyatsu ni mafin o tabeta.","german":"Today I had a muffin as a snack.","furigana":"きょう|た"},{"japanese":"三時のおやつを買いに行きます。","romaji":"Sanji no oyatsu o kai ni ikimasu.","german":"I am going to buy a three o'clock snack.","furigana":"さんじ|か|い"},{"japanese":"昼休みに、小さなおやつを食べた。","romaji":"Hiruyasumi ni, chiisana oyatsu o tabeta.","german":"I ate a small snack during my lunch break.","furigana":"ひるやす|ちい|た"}],"notes":"おやつ is a between-meal snack, often in the afternoon, but also at lunchtime. It does not have to be exactly at three o'clock. おやつに names something eaten as a snack; 三時のおやつ specifies a planned snack by time. The お belongs to the usual word form and is read together with やつ. お八つ is a rare kanji spelling; お八 without つ is also attested as a historical or rare spelling for this snack meaning. Today おやつ in kana is the usual form. 八つ historically belongs to a time designation; the meaning learned here is a between-meal snack, not a quantity of eight snacks and not an old term for a time of day.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"もしもし、山川です。","romaji":"Moshimoshi, Yamakawa desu.","german":"Hello, this is Yamakawa.","furigana":"やまかわ"},{"japanese":"もしもし、聞こえますか。","romaji":"Moshimoshi, kikoemasu ka.","german":"Hello, can you hear me?","furigana":"き"}],"notes":"もしもし is used on the phone to make contact or to check whether the other person is still listening. Afterwards, one can state one's own name: もしもし、山川です. もしもし、聞こえますか asks whether one can be heard. The phrase is read as a fixed greeting.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 9"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二人のライバルがついに対決することになった。","romaji":"Futari no raibaru ga tsuini taiketsu suru koto ni natta.","german":"The two rivals are finally going to face off.","furigana":"ふたり|たいけつ"},{"japanese":"対決を前に、二つのチームは練習しています。","romaji":"Taiketsu o mae ni, futatsu no chīmu wa renshū shite imasu.","german":"Ahead of the showdown, the two teams are practicing.","furigana":"たいけつ|まえ|ふた|れんしゅう"}],"notes":"対決 is a confrontation or direct encounter; with する it becomes an intransitive verb. 対決します and 対決して are regular する forms. The participants can be named together with が, an opponent also with と. 対決することになった describes that the encounter is now scheduled.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の友達は南アフリカに住んでいます。","romaji":"Watashi no tomodachi wa Minami Afurika ni sunde imasu.","german":"My friend lives in South Africa.","furigana":"わたし|ともだち|みなみ|す"},{"japanese":"南アフリカに行ってみたいです。","romaji":"Minami Afurika ni itte mitai desu.","german":"I'd like to go to South Africa sometime.","furigana":"みなみ|い"}],"notes":"南アフリカ here denotes the state of South Africa. 南 is read みなみ, アフリカ afurika. 南アフリカに住む names the country as a place of residence, 南アフリカに行く as a travel destination. The proper name stays unchanged even before に or の.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"つめたい北風がふいています。","romaji":"Tsumetai kitakaze ga fuite imasu.","german":"A cold north wind is blowing.","furigana":"きたかぜ"},{"japanese":"北風が強いので、窓を閉めました。","romaji":"Kitakaze ga tsuyoi node, mado o shimemashita.","german":"The north wind is strong, so I closed the window.","furigana":"きたかぜ|つよ|まど|し"}],"notes":"北風 is wind from a northern direction. 北 is read きた here, 風 かぜ. 北風が吹く names the wind as what blows. With 北風が強いので you can give the wind strength as a reason for an action. 北風 names the direction, not automatically a particular temperature.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日の夜明けの空はピンクでした。","romaji":"Kyō no yoake no sora wa pinku deshita.","german":"The sky at dawn today was pink.","furigana":"きょう|よあ|そら"},{"japanese":"夜明けに目が覚めました。","romaji":"Yoake ni me ga samemashita.","german":"I woke up at daybreak.","furigana":"よあ|め|さ"}],"notes":"夜明け is dawn or daybreak. 夜明けの空 describes the sky at that time, and 夜明けに names the time of an event. It is read よあけ without a long vowel. Time expressions can specify which dawn is meant more precisely, such as 今日の夜明け.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ハート形の風船を買いました。","romaji":"Hātogata no fūsen o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a heart-shaped balloon.","furigana":"がた|ふうせん|か"},{"japanese":"この紙をハート形に切ってください。","romaji":"Kono kami o hātogata ni kitte kudasai.","german":"Please cut this paper into a heart shape.","furigana":"かみ|がた|き"}],"notes":"ハート形 is a heart shape. It is read はーとがた with a long a and がた. ハート形の describes an object as heart-shaped; ハート形に names the desired shape in a transformation, for example when cutting out. The word can describe balloons, pieces of paper or other shapes.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先ず、本の目次を見て下さい。","romaji":"Mazu, hon no mokuji o mite kudasai.","german":"First, please look at the table of contents of the book.","furigana":"ま|ほん|もくじ|み|くだ"},{"japanese":"目次で読みたい章を探しました。","romaji":"Mokuji de yomitai shō o sagashimashita.","german":"I looked in the table of contents for the chapter I wanted to read.","furigana":"もくじ|よ|しょう|さが"}],"notes":"目次 is the table of contents of a book or document. 本の目次 names the book it belongs to. 目次を見る means to look into it; 目次で章を探す describes searching for a chapter using the table. It is read もくじ, not もくつぎ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"サケは海魚ですか？それとも川魚ですか？","romaji":"Sake wa umizakana desu ka? Soretomo kawazakana desu ka?","german":"Is salmon a saltwater fish? Or a freshwater fish?","furigana":"うみざかな|かわざかな"},{"japanese":"この市場には海魚が多いです。","romaji":"Kono ichiba ni wa umizakana ga ōi desu.","german":"There are many sea fish at this market.","furigana":"いちば|うみざかな|おお"}],"notes":"海魚 are sea fish, that is, saltwater fish. The reading on this card is うみざかな; 魚 is read ざかな there. The word is often contrasted with 川魚 \"river fish\". A question with それとも offers two possible classifications and does not answer it yet.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私、和風スイーツがすきなんです。","romaji":"Watashi, wafū suītsu ga suki na n desu.","german":"I like Japanese-style sweets.","furigana":"わたし|わふう"},{"japanese":"和風の部屋に泊まりました。","romaji":"Wafū no heya ni tomarimashita.","german":"I stayed in a Japanese-style room.","furigana":"わふう|へや|と"}],"notes":"和風 is a Japanese style. It can stand directly before a noun, as in 和風スイーツ, or with の: 和風の部屋. For food it concerns its design or manner of preparation; for a room, its furnishings. わふう ends with a long u.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この和室にアンティークのソファを買いたいと思っています。","romaji":"Kono washitsu ni antīku no sofa o kaitai to omotte imasu.","german":"I'm thinking of buying an antique sofa for this Japanese-style room.","furigana":"わしつ|か|おも"},{"japanese":"この旅館には和室があります。","romaji":"Kono ryokan ni wa washitsu ga arimasu.","german":"This inn has a Japanese-style room.","furigana":"りょかん|わしつ"}],"notes":"和室 is a room in the traditional Japanese style. 和室があります states that such a room exists. 和室に can indicate it as the destination of furnishing or of a stay. The reading わしつ has no long vowels.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 22"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"キモノなどの和服は、日本の文化の一部です。","romaji":"Kimono nado no wafuku wa, Nippon no bunka no ichibu desu.","german":"Traditional Japanese clothing such as the kimono is part of Japanese culture.","furigana":"わふく|にっぽん|ぶんか|いちぶ"},{"japanese":"結婚式で和服を着ました。","romaji":"Kekkonshiki de wafuku o kimashita.","german":"I wore traditional Japanese clothing at the wedding.","furigana":"けっこんしき|わふく|き"}],"notes":"和服 is traditional Japanese clothing, such as a kimono. 和服を着る means to put on or wear such clothing. キモノなどの和服 names the kimono as an example. The word denotes a clothing style and says nothing by itself about when or by whom it is worn.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"九州の名物は何ですか？","romaji":"Kyūshū no meibutsu wa nan desu ka?","german":"What are the regional specialties of Kyushu?","furigana":"きゅうしゅう|めいぶつ|なん"},{"japanese":"この町の名物をお土産に買いました。","romaji":"Kono machi no meibutsu o omiyage ni kaimashita.","german":"I bought a specialty of this town as a souvenir.","furigana":"まち|めいぶつ|みやげ|か"}],"notes":"名物 is a well-known specialty or typical product of a place. It can be something edible, but does not have to be. 九州の名物 or この町の名物 names the regional connection with の. 名物を買う describes buying such a product. めいぶつ is romanized as meibutsu.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"食べすぎて苦しい。","romaji":"Tabesugite kurushii.","german":"I feel uncomfortable because I ate too much.","furigana":"た|くる"},{"japanese":"友達がいなくて、苦しい時期がありました。","romaji":"Tomodachi ga inakute, kurushii jiki ga arimashita.","german":"I went through a hard time because I had no friends.","furigana":"ともだち|くる|じき"}],"notes":"苦しい is an i-adjective for physical discomfort or a burdensome situation. The forms are 苦しかった and 苦しくない. 食べすぎて苦しい describes discomfort after eating too much. 苦しい時期 can mean a difficult period of life. Which burden is meant depends on the context.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"客室は広く、ベッドが二つありました。","romaji":"Kyakushitsu wa hiroku, beddo ga futatsu arimashita.","german":"The guest room was spacious and had two beds.","furigana":"きゃくしつ|ひろ|ふた"},{"japanese":"係の人が客室に案内してくれました。","romaji":"Kakari no hito ga kyakushitsu ni annai shite kuremashita.","german":"A staff member showed me to the guest room.","furigana":"かかり|ひと|きゃくしつ|あんない"}],"notes":"客室 is a guest room here, for example in a hotel. 客室は広い describes its size; 客室に案内する means to guide someone to the room. きゃ forms a single mora. In other contexts the word can also mean a passenger cabin; in these examples it is a room.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"小さい時は、大人になったらケーキ屋さんになりたいと思ってました。","romaji":"Chīsai toki wa, otona ni nattara kēkiyasan ni naritai to omottemashita.","german":"When I was little, I thought I wanted to become a cake shop owner when I grew up.","furigana":"ちい|とき|おとな|や|おも"},{"japanese":"あの花屋はきれいです。","romaji":"Ano hanaya wa kirei desu.","german":"That flower shop is pretty.","furigana":"はなや"}],"notes":"〜屋 is attached to a word and denotes a shop or a person with the corresponding trade: 花屋 \"flower shop\", 肉屋 \"butcher shop/butcher\". さん can make the term more polite. With ケーキ屋さんになりたい it is about a career wish, with 花屋 about the shop. 〜 is a placeholder and is not pronounced. The spelling without the placeholder is 屋.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"肉屋さんで牛肉を四百グラム買いました。","romaji":"Nikuyasan de gyūniku o yonhyakuguramu kaimashita.","german":"I bought 400 grams of beef at the butcher.","furigana":"にくや|ぎゅうにく|よんひゃく|か"},{"japanese":"駅の近くに肉屋があります。","romaji":"Eki no chikaku ni nikuya ga arimasu.","german":"There is a butcher shop near the station.","furigana":"えき|ちか|にくや"}],"notes":"肉屋 denotes a butcher shop or a butcher. 肉屋で買う names the shop as the place of purchase. With さん one can make the term more polite: 肉屋さん. 牛肉 is beef; 四百グラム names a concrete purchased amount and is read よんひゃくグラム.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この町には古い茶屋があります。","romaji":"Kono machi ni wa furui chaya ga arimasu.","german":"This town has an old teahouse.","furigana":"まち|ふる|ちゃや"},{"japanese":"京都の茶屋でお茶を飲みました。","romaji":"Kyōto no chaya de ocha o nomimashita.","german":"I drank tea at a teahouse in Kyōto.","furigana":"きょうと|ちゃや|ちゃ|の"}],"notes":"茶屋 here means a teahouse where one can take a break and drink tea. 茶屋があります states that a teahouse exists. 茶屋でお茶を飲む describes it as the place of drinking. The reading is ちゃや; ちゃ forms a single mora.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"魚屋さんで生のサーモンを買いました。","romaji":"Sakanayasan de nama no sāmon o kaimashita.","german":"I bought raw salmon at the fish shop.","furigana":"さかなや|なま|か"},{"japanese":"魚屋の前で友達を待っています。","romaji":"Sakanaya no mae de tomodachi o matte imasu.","german":"I am waiting for a friend in front of the fish shop.","furigana":"さかなや|まえ|ともだち|ま"}],"notes":"魚屋 is a fish shop or fishmonger. 魚 is read さかな here; with 屋 it becomes さかなや. 魚屋で names the place of shopping, 魚屋の前 the area in front of the shop. 魚屋さん is a more polite term for the shop or the seller.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の友人は名古屋でデザイナーをしています。","romaji":"Watashi no yūjin wa Nagoya de dezainā o shite imasu.","german":"My friend works as a designer in Nagoya.","furigana":"わたし|ゆうじん|なごや"},{"japanese":"名古屋に行ったことがありますか。","romaji":"Nagoya ni itta koto ga arimasu ka.","german":"Have you ever been to Nagoya?","furigana":"なごや|い"}],"notes":"名古屋 is the name of the city of Nagoya. It is read なごや without long vowels. 名古屋で names the city as the place of an activity, 名古屋に as a travel destination. 行ったことがありますか asks about a past visit, not about a current travel plan.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"毎度、同じエラーが出るんですよ。","romaji":"Maido, onaji erā ga deru n desu yo.","german":"I get the same error every time.","furigana":"まいど|おな|で"},{"japanese":"毎度ありがとうございます。","romaji":"Maido arigatō gozaimasu.","german":"Thank you for your continued patronage.","furigana":"まいど"}],"notes":"毎度 means \"every time\" and refers to repeated occasions. 毎度、同じエラーが出る describes a regularly recurring problem. In 毎度ありがとうございます, it becomes the usual thanks to repeat customers or for continued support. It does not name a specific number.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日は三十度です。","romaji":"Kyō wa sanjūdo desu.","german":"Today it is thirty degrees.","furigana":"きょう|さんじゅうど"},{"japanese":"この角度は九十度です。","romaji":"Kono kakudo wa kyūjūdo desu.","german":"This angle is ninety degrees.","furigana":"かくど|きゅうじゅうど"},{"japanese":"この映画は二度見ました。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa nido mimashita.","german":"I have seen this movie twice.","furigana":"えいが|にどみ"}],"notes":"〜度 follows a number. For temperatures and angles it means \"degree\": 三十度 \"thirty degrees\", 九十度 \"ninety degrees\". As a counter for occurrences it means \"time(s)\": 二度 \"twice\". In the third example, 二度 counts how often the movie was seen. The temperature scale must be clear from the context. The reading on this card is ど; 〜 marks the place for the number and is not pronounced. The spelling without the placeholder is 度.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい本は明日発売されます。","romaji":"Atarashii hon wa ashita hatsubai saremasu.","german":"The new book goes on sale tomorrow.","furigana":"あたら|ほん|あしたはつばい"},{"japanese":"限定版は発売日に売り切れた。","romaji":"Genteiban wa hatsubai-bi ni urikireta.","german":"The limited edition sold out on the release day.","furigana":"げんていばん|はつばいび|う|き"}],"notes":"発売 is the start of sales of a product; 発売する means to put it on the market. 発売される is the passive form \"to be released/to go on sale\". 発売日 names the release or sales start day. The verb with する is transitive; the product can be named with を.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"何県の出身ですか。","romaji":"Nani ken no shusshin desu ka.","german":"Which prefecture are you from?","furigana":"なにけん|しゅっしん"},{"japanese":"大阪府と京都府と兵庫県に行きました。","romaji":"Ōsaka-fu to Kyōto-fu to Hyōgo-ken ni ikimashita.","german":"I went to Osaka Prefecture, Kyoto Prefecture and Hyogo Prefecture.","furigana":"おおさかふ|きょうとふ|ひょうごけん|い"},{"japanese":"この県で一番高い山は何ですか。","romaji":"Kono ken de ichiban takai yama wa nan desu ka.","german":"What is the highest mountain in this prefecture?","furigana":"けん|いちばんたか|やま|なん"}],"notes":"〜県 is attached to place names and forms the names of prefectures: 千葉県, 兵庫県. Japan has 47 administrative units (都道府県), of which 43 are 県; exceptions are 東京都, 北海道 and 大阪府 and 京都府. You ask about someone's origin with 何県 or どこの県; 県 also stands alone as a noun (この県, 県の代表). Common compounds are 県庁 (\"prefectural government\") and 県民 (\"prefectural resident\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの女の人は、とてもエレガントな美人ですね。","romaji":"Ano onna no hito wa, totemo ereganto na bijin desu ne.","german":"That woman is a very elegant beauty, isn't she?","furigana":"おんな|ひと|びじん"},{"japanese":"美人の絵を描きました。","romaji":"Bijin no e o kakimashita.","german":"I painted a picture of a beautiful woman.","furigana":"びじん|え|か"}],"notes":"美人 denotes a beautiful woman as a person. It is a noun: 美人です can describe a person, and 美人の絵 a picture of a beautiful woman. The meaning is an assessment of appearance. びじん has no long vowels; じん is written jin.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"勝負は引き分けだった。","romaji":"Shōbu wa hikiwake datta.","german":"The match ended in a draw.","furigana":"しょうぶ|ひ|わ"},{"japanese":"友達とゲームで勝負しました。","romaji":"Tomodachi to gēmu de shōbu shimashita.","german":"I played a game against my friend.","furigana":"ともだち|しょうぶ"}],"notes":"勝負 here means a contest or game in which people compete against each other. 勝負する is an intransitive する verb; 勝負します and 勝負して are regular forms. 勝負は引き分けだった describes the result. With と you can name the opponent, as in 友達と勝負する.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このレースの勝者はお金がもらえますよ。","romaji":"Kono rēsu no shōsha wa okane ga moraemasu yo.","german":"The winner of this race gets money.","furigana":"しょうしゃ|かね"},{"japanese":"勝者にトロフィーをあげます。","romaji":"Shōsha ni torofī o agemasu.","german":"I'll give the winner a trophy.","furigana":"しょうしゃ"}],"notes":"勝者 is the person or group that has won a contest. このレースの勝者 names the competition in question with の. 勝者に names the recipient when an award is handed over. しょうしゃ contains two syllables pronounced together, しょう and しゃ, the first with a long o.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"必勝を願って、お守りを買いました。","romaji":"Hisshō o negatte, omamori o kaimashita.","german":"Hoping for certain victory, I bought a good-luck charm.","furigana":"ひっしょう|ねが|まも|か"},{"japanese":"旗に「必勝」と書きました。","romaji":"Hata ni “hisshō” to kakimashita.","german":"I wrote 「必勝」 (\"certain victory\") on the flag.","furigana":"はた|ひっしょう|か"}],"notes":"必勝 is a certain victory. The word is encountered as a motto or wish before a contest. 必勝を願う means to wish for a certain victory. The word expresses confidence or a goal; a written motto does not by itself say how the contest will end. ひっしょう contains a small っ and a long o.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今回のテストは、楽勝だったね。","romaji":"Konkai no tesuto wa, rakushō datta ne.","german":"This test was a piece of cake, wasn't it?","furigana":"こんかい|らくしょう"},{"japanese":"私たちのチームは楽勝しました。","romaji":"Watashitachi no chīmu wa rakushō shimashita.","german":"Our team won easily.","furigana":"わたし|らくしょう"}],"notes":"楽勝 is an easy victory and is also used figuratively for something very easy. 楽勝だった can therefore say of a test that it was a piece of cake. In 楽勝する, the する verb describes an effortless victory. らくしょう ends with a long o.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"アンナさんの体重は、何キロですか？","romaji":"Anna-san no taijū wa, nankiro desu ka?","german":"How many kilograms does Anna weigh?","furigana":"たいじゅう|なん"},{"japanese":"最近体重が増えました。","romaji":"Saikin taijū ga fuemashita.","german":"I have gained weight recently.","furigana":"さいきんたいじゅう|ふ"}],"notes":"体重 is body weight. With 何キロ you ask about weight in kilograms. 体重が増える means that weight increases. The word alone does not judge a number as good or bad. たいじゅう ends with a long u; じゅ is pronounced as one unit.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"分からない文字の下に、二重のラインを引いて下さい。","romaji":"Wakaranai moji no shita ni, nijū no rain o hiite kudasai.","german":"Please draw a double line under the characters you do not understand.","furigana":"わ|もじ|した|にじゅう|ひ|くだ"},{"japanese":"二重の意味があります。","romaji":"Nijū no imi ga arimasu.","german":"It has a double meaning.","furigana":"にじゅう|いみ"}],"notes":"二重 is a doubling or a double version. With の it modifies a noun: 二重のライン \"a double line\". 二重に can name a double arrangement or action. The reading on this card is にじゅう with a long u. 二重の意味 can mean a double meaning.","levelNote":"Compound: 重 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"もう全ての仮名を学びましたか？","romaji":"Mō subete no kana o manabimashita ka?","german":"Have you already learned all the kana?","furigana":"すべ|かな|まな"},{"japanese":"仮名で名前を書いてください。","romaji":"Kana de namae o kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write your name in kana.","furigana":"かな|なまえ|か"}],"notes":"仮名 refers to kana, that is, the Japanese scripts hiragana and katakana. 仮名で書く names the script used with で. 仮名を学ぶ names it as what is learned. The reading here is かな. In a specific writing task, the context can determine which of the two kana scripts is meant.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ペルーは南米の国の一つです。","romaji":"Perū wa Nanbei no kuni no hitotsu desu.","german":"Peru is one of the countries in South America.","furigana":"なんべい|くに|ひと"},{"japanese":"南米を旅行したいです。","romaji":"Nanbei o ryokō shitai desu.","german":"I want to travel around South America.","furigana":"なんべい|りょこう"}],"notes":"南米 is South America; it is a short form of 南アメリカ. 南米の国 names a country of this continent. In 南米を旅行する, を marks the area traveled. なんべい is written Nanbei; the n before b is kept in this romanization.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"パーティで色々な人と交わるのが下手なんです。","romaji":"Pāti de iroiro na hito to majiwaru no ga heta na n desu.","german":"I am bad at mingling with all kinds of people at parties.","furigana":"いろいろ|ひと|まじ|へた"},{"japanese":"二つの道が交わっています。","romaji":"Futatsu no michi ga majiwatte imasu.","german":"Two roads intersect.","furigana":"ふた|みち|まじ"}],"notes":"交わる is an intransitive godan verb: 交わります, 交わって. Paths can cross; people can associate with each other or mingle among others. With が you name the crossing paths, with と the people someone has contact with. The meaning depends on the context.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は小学校の先生です。","romaji":"Watashi wa shōgakkō no sensei desu.","german":"I am an elementary school teacher.","furigana":"わたし|しょうがっこう|せんせい"},{"japanese":"小学校の近くに住んでいます。","romaji":"Shōgakkō no chikaku ni sunde imasu.","german":"I live near an elementary school.","furigana":"しょうがっこう|ちか|す"}],"notes":"小学校 is an elementary school. 小学校の先生 means a teacher at this type of school. 小学校の近くに describes a place near the school. しょうがっこう has two long o and a small っ; the romanization is shōgakkō.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 38"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ストーブの火に気を付けて下さい。","romaji":"Sutōbu no hi ni ki o tsukete kudasai.","german":"Please be careful with the fire of the heater.","furigana":"ひ|き|つ|くだ"},{"japanese":"気を付けて帰ってください。","romaji":"Ki o tsukete kaette kudasai.","german":"Please be careful on your way home.","furigana":"き|つ|かえ"}],"notes":"気を付けて is a fixed request to be careful. It contains the て-form of 付ける; with ください the request becomes more polite. 火に気を付けて names the danger with に, 気を付けて帰って names careful behavior on the way home. 気をつけて without the character 付 is also common.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の弟は高校生です。","romaji":"Watashi no otōto wa kōkōsei desu.","german":"My younger brother is a high school student.","furigana":"わたし|おとうと|こうこうせい"},{"japanese":"高校生の時、図書館でよく勉強しました。","romaji":"Kōkōsei no toki, toshokan de yoku benkyō shimashita.","german":"When I was a high school student, I often studied in the library.","furigana":"こうこうせい|とき|としょかん|べんきょう"}],"notes":"高校生 is a student at an upper secondary school. 高校生です states the current student status. 高校生の時 can look back on one's own earlier school days. こうこうせい is written kōkōsei: two long o sounds, followed by the vowel sequence ei.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このコンビニには中学校の生徒がよく来ます。","romaji":"Kono konbini ni wa chūgakkō no seito ga yoku kimasu.","german":"Junior high school students often come to this convenience store.","furigana":"ちゅうがっこう|せいと|き"},{"japanese":"弟は中学校に通っています。","romaji":"Otōto wa chūgakkō ni kayotte imasu.","german":"My younger brother attends junior high school.","furigana":"おとうと|ちゅうがっこう|かよ"}],"notes":"中学校 refers to a junior high school, that is, a lower secondary school. 中学校の生徒 refers to its students. 中学校に通う means to attend this school regularly. ちゅうがっこう has a long u, a long o and a small っ.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 38"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この手紙には、まだ切手が付いてないですよ。","romaji":"Kono tegami ni wa, mada kitte ga tsuite nai desu yo.","german":"This letter doesn't have a stamp on it yet.","furigana":"てがみ|きって|つ"},{"japanese":"電気が付いていますね。","romaji":"Denki ga tsuite imasu ne.","german":"The light is on, isn't it?","furigana":"でんき|つ"}],"notes":"付く is intransitive here: 切手が付く describes a stamp that is attached, and 電気が付く a light that comes on. The te-form with いる describes the existing state. The place of attachment can be named with に, as in 手紙に. For actively attaching something, the transitive 付ける is used. For lights, 点く is also written; the entry keeps the spelling given here.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の母を見付けることが出来ますか？","romaji":"Watashi no haha o mitsukeru koto ga dekimasu ka?","german":"Can you find my mother?","furigana":"わたし|はは|みつ|でき"},{"japanese":"やっと仕事を見付けました。","romaji":"Yatto shigoto o mitsukemashita.","german":"I finally found a job.","furigana":"しごと|みつ"}],"notes":"見付ける means to find someone or something. The object found or sought takes を. The also common spelling 見つける has the same reading; here 見付ける is kept. This ichidan verb forms 見付けます and 見付けない. In the second example, やっと stresses that the search took longer or was harder than hoped.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"弟は弱虫だと言われている。","romaji":"Otōto wa yowamushi da to iwarete iru.","german":"They say my younger brother is a wimp.","furigana":"おとうと|よわむし|い"},{"japanese":"あの子は弱虫ですね。","romaji":"Ano ko wa yowamushi desu ne.","german":"That child is a wimp, isn't it?","furigana":"こ|よわむし"},{"japanese":"弱虫と呼ばないでください。","romaji":"Yowamushi to yobanaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't call me a wimp.","furigana":"よわむし|よ"}],"notes":"弱虫 is a derogatory term for a \"coward\" or a fearful person. 虫 in this compound does not mean an actual insect. 弱虫だ calls the person that; 弱虫だと言われる reports being called that by others. The word can be used as a noun; the additional sentence shows how to object to this hurtful label.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"主人は弱々しい体ながら全力で走った。","romaji":"Shujin wa yowayowashii karada nagara zenryoku de hashitta.","german":"My husband ran with all his strength despite his frail body.","furigana":"しゅじん|よわよわ|からだ|ぜんりょく|はし"},{"japanese":"弱々しい光が見えます。","romaji":"Yowayowashii hikari ga miemasu.","german":"I can see a faint light.","furigana":"よわよわ|ひかり|み"}],"notes":"弱々しい describes something that looks weak or powerless: a body, a voice or even a light. This i-adjective stands before a noun without な: 弱々しい光. In the first example, 体ながら means \"despite the body being in this state\". It is about the impression of lacking strength; the word does not specify any particular medical cause.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"マグカップの口の所が欠けてしまった。","romaji":"Magukappu no kuchi no tokoro ga kakete shimatta.","german":"The rim of the mug got chipped.","furigana":"くち|ところ|か"},{"japanese":"お皿の端が欠けました。","romaji":"Osara no hashi ga kakemashita.","german":"The edge of the plate got chipped.","furigana":"さら|はし|か"},{"japanese":"この計画には、具体的な説明が欠けています。","romaji":"Kono keikaku ni wa, gutaiteki na setsumei ga kakete imasu.","german":"This plan lacks a concrete explanation.","furigana":"けいかく|ぐたいてき|せつめい|か"}],"notes":"欠ける is an intransitive ichidan verb here. The damaged edge takes が: 端が欠ける. It can also express that something essential is missing; the thing it concerns then often follows with に. The form 欠けてしまった emphasizes the undesired result. The examples show material damage and a missing explanation, without claiming that anyone caused the damage on purpose.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"祖母の家の古いテレビは白黒でした。","romaji":"Sobo no ie no furui terebi wa shirokuro deshita.","german":"The old TV at my grandmother's house was black and white.","furigana":"そぼ|いえ|ふる|しろくろ"},{"japanese":"白黒の写真です。","romaji":"Shirokuro no shashin desu.","german":"It is a black-and-white photo.","furigana":"しろくろ|しゃしん"},{"japanese":"この資料は白黒で印刷してください。","romaji":"Kono shiryō wa shirokuro de insatsu shite kudasai.","german":"Please print these materials in black and white.","furigana":"しりょう|しろくろ|いんさつ"}],"notes":"白黒 means black and white here, that is, an image without colors. The noun connects to another noun with の: 白黒の写真. 白黒で印刷する names the printing mode, 白黒でした an earlier state. The examples use the image and color meaning; in other contexts, the word can also refer to a decision about right and wrong.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"すごくいい映像ですね。","romaji":"Sugoku ii eizō desu ne.","german":"That is really nice footage, isn't it?","furigana":"えいぞう"},{"japanese":"テレビで映像を見ました。","romaji":"Terebi de eizō o mimashita.","german":"I saw the footage on TV.","furigana":"えいぞう|み"}],"notes":"映像 here refers to film or video footage. It can comprise several moving images and is not limited to a single movie. 映像を見る describes watching it, and いい映像 evaluates the footage. The word is a noun; in 映像の編集, の connects it with \"editing\", for example. The examples do not use a reflection or a mere mental image.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"おもしろい画像ですね。","romaji":"Omoshiroi gazō desu ne.","german":"That's an interesting image.","furigana":"がぞう"},{"japanese":"画像をダウンロードしました。","romaji":"Gazō o daunrōdo shimashita.","german":"I downloaded the image.","furigana":"がぞう"}],"notes":"画像 is an image, here especially a digital image file or its visible content. 画像をダウンロードする means downloading this file. おもしろい画像, by contrast, evaluates what can be seen in the image. With の one can add a property, for example 画像の大きさ for the image size; the word is a noun.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"さっき生保のセールスマネジャーが家に来ましたよ。","romaji":"Sakki seiho no sērusu manejā ga ie ni kimashita yo.","german":"A sales manager from a life insurance company came to our house earlier.","furigana":"せいほ|いえ|き"},{"japanese":"生保に入っていますか。","romaji":"Seiho ni haitte imasu ka.","german":"Do you have life insurance?","furigana":"せいほ|はい"}],"notes":"生保 is an abbreviation here for 生命保険, life insurance. 生保に入っている asks about an existing insurance contract, not about entering an office. In the first example, 生保の refers to the field of the sales manager. The same characters can form a different abbreviation in other contexts; here, sales and insurance make clear that life insurance is meant.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"体力を保つために、毎朝歩いています。","romaji":"Tairyoku o tamotsu tame ni, maiasa aruite imasu.","german":"To keep up my physical strength, I walk every morning.","furigana":"たいりょく|たも|まいあさある"},{"japanese":"きれいな状態を保ってください。","romaji":"Kirei na jōtai o tamotte kudasai.","german":"Please keep it in a clean condition.","furigana":"じょうたい|たも"}],"notes":"保つ here means to keep something in its state or at its previous level. What is preserved takes を: 体力を保つ or 状態を保つ. This transitive godan verb forms 保ちます, 保って and 保たない. In the first example, daily walking is a personal habit; the second sentence asks to maintain a clean condition.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"友人の説得を試みたが、うまくいかなかった。","romaji":"Yūjin no settoku o kokoromita ga, umaku ikanakatta.","german":"I tried to persuade my friend, but it did not work out.","furigana":"ゆうじん|せっとく|こころ"},{"japanese":"新しいレシピでパン作りを試みました。","romaji":"Atarashii reshipi de panzukuri o kokoromimashita.","german":"I attempted to make bread with a new recipe.","furigana":"あたら|づく|こころ"}],"notes":"試みる means to make an attempt. It sounds more written or formal than the everyday やってみる. The undertaking takes を, as in 説得を試みる. This ichidan verb forms 試みます and 試みた. The examples contrast trying out a new cooking idea with trying to persuade someone; an attempt does not guarantee success.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先ずは何も言わないで試食してみて。","romaji":"Mazu wa nani mo iwanaide shishoku shite mite.","german":"First, try it without saying anything.","furigana":"ま|なに|い|ししょく"},{"japanese":"デパートで試食しました。","romaji":"Depāto de shishoku shimashita.","german":"I tried a sample at the department store.","furigana":"ししょく"}],"notes":"試食 is tasting food, often before a purchase or as a sample of a new dish. With する it becomes the transitive 試食する; the dish tried can take を. 試食してみて is a familiar request to try it once. The word concerns food and not testing devices or an exam.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この本は都会と田舎を対比しています。","romaji":"Kono hon wa tokai to inaka o taihi shiteimasu.","german":"This book contrasts the city and the countryside.","furigana":"ほん|とかい|いなか|たいひ"},{"japanese":"明るい場面と暗い場面が強く対比されている。","romaji":"Akarui bamen to kurai bamen ga tsuyoku taihi sareteiru.","german":"Bright scenes and dark scenes are strongly contrasted.","furigana":"あか|ばめん|くら|ばめん|つよ|たいひ"},{"japanese":"白い雪と赤い椿の対比がとても美しい。","romaji":"Shiroi yuki to akai tsubaki no taihi ga totemo utsukushii.","german":"The contrast between the white snow and the red camellias is very beautiful.","furigana":"しろ|ゆき|あか|つばき|たいひ|うつく"}],"notes":"対比 means placing two things side by side to make their differences clear - more \"juxtaposition, contrast\" than a mere \"comparison\". Typical: AとBを対比する, Aと対比して, 〜の対比が鮮やかだ (for colors, light and shadow, novel characters). 比較 is the general word for \"to compare\", often with an evaluation of which is better; 対照, like 対比, stresses the contrast. Rather literary, often found in texts on art, literature and statistics.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"それでは、発表します。スピーチコンテストの勝者は田中さんです！","romaji":"Sore de wa, happyō shimasu. Supīchi kontesuto no shōsha wa Tanaka-san desu!","german":"Now I will announce it. The winner of the speech contest is Ms. Tanaka!","furigana":"はっぴょう|しょうしゃ|たなか"},{"japanese":"新商品を発表しました。","romaji":"Shinshōhin o happyō shimashita.","german":"I presented a new product.","furigana":"しんしょうひん|はっぴょう"}],"notes":"発表 is announcing or publicly presenting something. 発表する takes the content with を, for example 新商品を発表する. With an announcement, the content can instead follow in the next sentence. The word also means a presentation; it does not require that the content was secret. 発表 itself is the noun, and 発表します is the polite verb form.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 31"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このグラフは、町の人口を表しています。","romaji":"Kono gurafu wa, machi no jinkō o arawashite imasu.","german":"This graph represents the population of the town.","furigana":"まち|じんこう|あらわ"},{"japanese":"この絵は何を表していますか。","romaji":"Kono e wa nani o arawashite imasu ka.","german":"What does this picture express?","furigana":"え|なに|あらわ"}],"notes":"表す can mean to represent what something stands for, or to express a meaning. What is represented follows with を: 人口を表す. With この絵は何を表していますか you ask about the content or message of a picture. This transitive godan verb ending in す forms 表します and 表して. The examples use the representing and expressing meanings that go with this spelling.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"出発まで五分です。","romaji":"Shuppatsu made gofun desu.","german":"There are five minutes until departure.","furigana":"しゅっぱつ|ごふん"},{"japanese":"電車は九時に出発します。","romaji":"Densha wa kuji ni shuppatsu shimasu.","german":"The train departs at nine o'clock.","furigana":"でんしゃ|くじ|しゅっぱつ"}],"notes":"出発 means departing or setting off. With する it becomes an intransitive verb: 電車が出発する. 出発まで names the time that remains until departure; 九時に, by contrast, names the point in time itself. The word can also refer to a journey on foot and is not limited to trains.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"やっぱりオデンには辛子でしょ。","romaji":"Yappari oden ni wa karashi desho.","german":"Oden really calls for mustard, doesn't it?","furigana":"からし"},{"japanese":"辛子をつけて食べますか。","romaji":"Karashi o tsukete tabemasu ka.","german":"Do you eat it with mustard?","furigana":"からし|た"}],"notes":"辛子 is mustard, in Japanese everyday life often the hot yellow mustard served with dishes like oden. The word is often written からし; this entry keeps its kanji spelling. 辛子をつける means to put some of it on the food. In the first sentence, the familiar でしょ asks for agreement on a preference, while the second asks about eating habits.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先日、友人と本屋に行きました。","romaji":"Senjitsu, yūjin to hon'ya ni ikimashita.","german":"The other day, I went to the bookstore with a friend.","furigana":"せんじつ|ゆうじん|ほんや|い"},{"japanese":"本屋で新しい小説を買いました。","romaji":"Hon'ya de atarashii shōsetsu o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a new novel at the bookstore.","furigana":"ほんや|あたら|しょうせつ|か"}],"notes":"本屋 is a bookstore here. With 行く, に marks the destination of a visit; with 買う, で marks the place of purchase. The reading ほんや contains ん before や, not a combined syllable にゃ; in romanization it is therefore written hon'ya. The examples concern the shop, not a publisher or a main building, which the word can denote in other contexts.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 10"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子犬は天使みたいでした。","romaji":"Koinu wa tenshi mitai deshita.","german":"The puppy was like an angel.","furigana":"こいぬ|てんし"},{"japanese":"あの絵の天使はきれいですね。","romaji":"Ano e no tenshi wa kirei desu ne.","german":"The angel in that painting is beautiful, isn't it?","furigana":"え|てんし"}],"notes":"天使 is an angel as a religious or pictorially represented figure. 天使みたい can affectionately describe an especially lovable being as a comparison; the puppy does not literally become an angel. あの絵の天使, by contrast, names the depicted figure in a picture. The word is used as a noun.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新年に大きいセールを行います。","romaji":"Shinnen ni ōkii sēru o okonaimasu.","german":"We will hold a big sale in the new year.","furigana":"しんねん|おお|おこな"},{"japanese":"新年おめでとうございます。","romaji":"Shinnen omedetō gozaimasu.","german":"Happy New Year!","furigana":"しんねん"}],"notes":"新年 is the new year, or the time of its beginning. 新年に names it as the time of an event. 新年おめでとうございます is a polite New Year's greeting. The final とう in おめでとう is pronounced as a long tō. The ん of しん and the following ね of ねん remain recognizable in shinnen instead of merging into a single syllable.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"工学者と心理学者の対談を聞きに行きました。","romaji":"Kōgakusha to shinrigakusha no taidan o kiki ni ikimashita.","german":"I went to hear a conversation between an engineer and a psychologist.","furigana":"こうがくしゃ|しんりがくしゃ|たいだん|き|い"},{"japanese":"テレビで対談を見ました。","romaji":"Terebi de taidan o mimashita.","german":"I watched a conversation on TV.","furigana":"たいだん|み"}],"notes":"対談 is a conversation or dialogue between two people, often before an audience or for publication. 対談を聞く and 対談を見る treat the conversation as an event or media content. With する it can also express that someone holds such a dialogue with another person. It means more than a brief greeting in passing.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"母は足が不自由なので、車を使うことが多いです。","romaji":"Haha wa ashi ga fujiyū na node, kuruma o tsukau koto ga ōi desu.","german":"My mother has difficulty walking, so she often uses a car.","furigana":"はは|あし|ふじゆう|くるま|つか|おお"},{"japanese":"足が不自由な方もいます。","romaji":"Ashi ga fujiyū na kata mo imasu.","german":"There are also people with difficulty walking.","furigana":"あし|ふじゆう|かた"},{"japanese":"耳が不自由なので、紙に書いて伝えてください。","romaji":"Mimi ga fujiyū na node, kami ni kaite tsutaete kudasai.","german":"I am hard of hearing, so please write it on paper to tell me.","furigana":"みみ|ふじゆう|かみ|か|つた"}],"notes":"不自由 can express that a bodily function is limited. 足が不自由 describes an impairment of the legs or of walking. As a na-adjective it connects to a person with な: 足が不自由な方. なので gives the reason for choosing the car in the first example. Here a specific limitation is described, not a trait of the whole person.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「毎日全力で生きる」というのは、父からの教えです。","romaji":"„Mainichi zenryoku de ikiru“ to iu no wa, chichi kara no oshie desu.","german":"\"Live with all your strength every day\" is a teaching from my father.","furigana":"まいにちぜんりょく|い|ちち|おし"},{"japanese":"先生の教えを大切にしています。","romaji":"Sensei no oshie o taisetsu ni shite imasu.","german":"I treasure my teacher's teachings.","furigana":"せんせい|おし|たいせつ"}],"notes":"教え is a teaching, instruction or a principle that has been passed on. 父からの教え names the person it comes from; 先生の教え names the source. 教えを大切にする means to value and preserve this teaching. Here 教え is the noun, not the identically written verb form of 教える.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この付近に、コンビニはありますか？","romaji":"Kono fukin ni, konbini wa arimasu ka?","german":"Is there a convenience store in this area?","furigana":"ふきん"},{"japanese":"駅の付近にホテルがあります。","romaji":"Eki no fukin ni hoteru ga arimasu.","german":"There is a hotel near the station.","furigana":"えき|ふきん"},{"japanese":"駅の付近では、夜も車の音が聞こえます。","romaji":"Eki no fukin de wa, yoru mo kuruma no oto ga kikoemasu.","german":"Near the station, you can hear car noise even at night.","furigana":"えき|ふきん|よる|くるま|おと|き"}],"notes":"付近 is the immediate surroundings of a place. この付近 names the area around the current reference point; 駅の付近 names the station as the starting point of orientation. The term does not presuppose a fixed distance in meters. In the examples, に marks the place of existing shops or buildings; 付近 itself is a noun.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"マイケルは魚よりも泳ぎが上手です。","romaji":"Maikeru wa sakana yori mo oyogi ga jōzu desu.","german":"Michael is a better swimmer than a fish!","furigana":"さかな|およ|じょうず"},{"japanese":"彼は泳ぎが上手です。","romaji":"Kare wa oyogi ga jōzu desu.","german":"He is good at swimming.","furigana":"かれ|およ|じょうず"},{"japanese":"今日はプールで泳ぎを練習します。","romaji":"Kyō wa pūru de oyogi o renshū shimasu.","german":"Today I will practice swimming at the pool.","furigana":"きょう|およ|れんしゅう"}],"notes":"泳ぎ is swimming or the way someone swims. In 泳ぎが上手, the swimming ability becomes the object of an evaluation with が. 泳ぎを練習する treats swimming as a practiced activity. The word is a noun here; the identically written connective form of 泳ぐ is a separate grammatical use.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ガラスは光を通します。","romaji":"Garasu wa hikari o tōshimasu.","german":"Glass lets light through.","furigana":"ひかり|とお"},{"japanese":"お客様をお部屋に通してください。","romaji":"Okyakusama o oheya ni tōshite kudasai.","german":"Please show the guest into the room.","furigana":"きゃくさま|へや|とお"}],"notes":"通す is transitive here. 光を通す means to let light through; お客様を部屋に通す means to show a guest into the room. を marks what is let through or shown in, に the destination. This godan verb ending in す forms 通します and 通して. Unlike 通る, here it describes someone or something making the passage possible.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"すみません。このクーポンの使用の仕方が分からないんです。","romaji":"Sumimasen. Kono kūpon no shiyō no shikata ga wakaranai n desu.","german":"Excuse me, I do not know how to use this coupon.","furigana":"しよう|しかた|わ"},{"japanese":"この機械は使用中です。","romaji":"Kono kikai wa shiyōchū desu.","german":"This machine is in use.","furigana":"きかい|しようちゅう"}],"notes":"使用 is the use of an object or means and sounds more formal than 使う. 使用する forms the transitive verb; 使用の仕方 asks as a noun about the way of using something. 使用中 means that something is currently being used, not merely that it is usable. The よう in しよう is pronounced long; it is not the shortened sound sequence しょう.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"カレーはいつも辛口を買います。","romaji":"Karē wa itsumo karakuchi o kaimasu.","german":"Beim Curry kaufe ich immer die scharfe Variante.","furigana":"からくち|か"},{"japanese":"辛口のカレーが好きです。","romaji":"Karakuchi no karē ga suki desu.","german":"Ich mag scharfes Curry.","furigana":"からくち|す"},{"japanese":"このワインは辛口です。","romaji":"Kono wain wa karakuchi desu.","german":"Dieser Wein ist trocken.","furigana":"からくち"}],"notes":"辛口 bezeichnet bei Curry die scharfe Variante, bei Wein oder Sake dagegen einen trockenen Geschmack. Der Bezug entscheidet also, welche deutsche Übersetzung passt. Als Nomen kann es direkt mit を stehen; vor einem anderen Nomen verbindet es sich mit の: 辛口のカレー. Die Beispiele vergleichen eine Schärfewahl beim Essen mit einer Geschmacksangabe für ein Getränk.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私はマレーシア出身です。","romaji":"Watashi wa Marēshia shusshin desu.","german":"I'm from Malaysia.","furigana":"わたし|しゅっしん"},{"japanese":"出身はどこですか。","romaji":"Shusshin wa doko desu ka.","german":"Where are you from?","furigana":"しゅっしん"},{"japanese":"彼は、この大学の出身です。","romaji":"Kare wa, kono daigaku no shusshin desu.","german":"He is a graduate of this university.","furigana":"かれ|だいがく|しゅっしん"}],"notes":"出身 names a person's origin, for example a place, a country or a school. マレーシア出身です means that one comes from Malaysia; it does not automatically determine nationality. 出身はどこですか asks about origin, not about the current place of residence. A place name often stands before 出身 without an additional particle.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 11"},
{"examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"ピッチャーが交代します。","romaji":"Picchā ga kōtai shimasu.","german":"The pitcher is being replaced.","furigana":"こうたい"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"途中で担当者が交代した。","romaji":"Tochū de tantōsha ga kōtai shita.","german":"The person in charge changed partway through.","furigana":"とちゅう|たんとうしゃ|こうたい"}],"notes":"交代 is replacing, changing or taking turns in a role. With する, the person who takes over or hands over the place can take が. In sports it concerns, for example, the pitcher; in a work situation, the person in charge. 途中で here means a change during the process; it need not mean a journey \"on the way\". 交替 is another spelling for this change or replacement; with する it also forms a verb.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"そこで先生に会ったよ。","romaji":"Soko de sensei ni atta yo.","german":"I met my teacher there.","furigana":"せんせい|あ"},{"japanese":"そこに座ってください。","romaji":"Soko ni suwatte kudasai.","german":"Please sit over there.","furigana":"すわ"}],"notes":"そこ denotes a place near the listener or a place just mentioned. そこで names the place of an action; そこに with 座る the spot where one sits down. For a place far from both conversation partners, あそこ is often used. 其処 is a rare kanji spelling; in everyday writing, そこ is usually used. It is the place demonstrative that is meant, not 底, \"bottom\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私のうちは、あそこだよ。","romaji":"Watashi no uchi wa, asoko da yo.","german":"My house is over there.","furigana":"わたし"},{"japanese":"あそこにお店があります。","romaji":"Asoko ni omise ga arimasu.","german":"There's a shop over there.","furigana":"みせ"}],"notes":"あそこ is \"over there\", that is, a place that is distant from the speaker and the listener. The demonstrative needs no additional noun after it: あそこだ indicates the place directly. With に, it names the place where a building exists. あの, by contrast, comes before a noun, such as あの店; the two forms are therefore built into the sentence differently. 彼処 is a rare kanji spelling of this place demonstrative. Normally, あそこ is written in kana.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一ヶ月で五キロやせました。","romaji":"Ikkagetsu de go kiro yasemashita.","german":"I lost five kilos in one month.","furigana":"いっかげつ|ご"},{"japanese":"五キロ走りました。","romaji":"Go kiro hashirimashita.","german":"I ran five kilometers.","furigana":"ご|はし"}],"notes":"キロ is the short form of キログラム or キロメートル. The topic decides whether weight or distance is meant: やせる refers to body weight, 走る to the distance covered. A number before キロ gives the amount. If the context allows both, the full term is clearer. In other compounds, キロ can also be the prefix for thousand; the two examples practice the units as nouns.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あとはそのまま来た道を下るだけだ。","romaji":"Ato wa sono mama kita michi o kudaru dake da.","german":"All that's left is to go back down the same way we came.","furigana":"き|みち|くだ"},{"japanese":"坂を下りました。","romaji":"Saka o kudarimashita.","german":"I went down the slope.","furigana":"さか|くだ"},{"japanese":"バスで、山道をゆっくり下りました。","romaji":"Basu de, yamamichi o yukkuri kudarimashita.","german":"We slowly went down the mountain road by bus.","furigana":"やまみち|くだ"}],"notes":"下る here means going or driving down along a route. 坂を下る uses を for the path covered; the verb is nonetheless intransitive. This godan verb ending in る forms 下ります and 下って. In the romanizations, this entry's reading くだる is kept; the related 下りる, おりる, is a different verb.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"海外から年金を受け取る方法ってありますか？","romaji":"Kaigai kara nenkin o uketoru hōhō tte arimasu ka?","german":"Is there a way to receive a pension from abroad?","furigana":"かいがい|ねんきん|う|と|ほうほう"},{"japanese":"荷物を受け取りました。","romaji":"Nimotsu o uketorimashita.","german":"I received the package.","furigana":"にもつ|う|と"}],"notes":"受け取る means to receive or accept something. What is received takes を, and the source can be named with から. A payment can be 受け取る just like a package. This transitive godan verb forms 受け取ります and 受け取って. The examples deal with actually receiving, not interpreting a statement, which is also possible with this word.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ここで十分間休みましょう。","romaji":"Koko de juppunkan yasumimashō.","german":"Let's rest here for ten minutes.","furigana":"じゅっぷんかんやす"},{"japanese":"十分間休憩しましょう。","romaji":"Juppunkan kyūkei shimashō.","german":"Let's take a ten-minute break.","furigana":"じゅっぷんかんきゅうけい"},{"japanese":"この問題は十分間で解けました。","romaji":"Kono mondai wa juppunkan de tokemashita.","german":"I was able to solve this problem in ten minutes.","furigana":"もんだい|じゅっぷんかん|と"}],"notes":"十分間 means a duration of ten minutes and is read じゅっぷんかん here. The also common reading じっぷんかん is not used in the entry. 間 makes the duration explicit; 十分間で names a span of time within which something is accomplished. The characters 十分 are the number with the counter for minutes here and are not read じゅうぶん, \"sufficient\".","levelNote":"Compound: 十分 (N4) + 間 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"去年は、暖かい南国で冬を過ごしました。","romaji":"Kyonen wa, atatakai nangoku de fuyu o sugoshimashita.","german":"Last year, I spent the winter in a warm southern country.","furigana":"きょねん|あたた|なんごく|ふゆ|す"},{"japanese":"この花は、南国から来た植物です。","romaji":"Kono hana wa, nangoku kara kita shokubutsu desu.","german":"This flower is a plant from a southern region.","furigana":"はな|なんごく|き|しょくぶつ"}],"notes":"南国 denotes a southern country or region, often with the idea of a warm climate. It is a noun and connects to another noun with の. It fixes no particular national border and no exact climate type. The examples name a personal travel memory and the origin of a plant from a southern area.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あたたかい南風がふいています。","romaji":"Atatakai minamikaze ga fuite imasu.","german":"A warm south wind is blowing.","furigana":"みなみかぜ"},{"japanese":"今日は南風が吹いています。","romaji":"Kyō wa minamikaze ga fuite imasu.","german":"A south wind is blowing today.","furigana":"きょう|みなみかぜ|ふ"},{"japanese":"南風で、旗が揺れています。","romaji":"Minamikaze de, hata ga yurete imasu.","german":"The flag is fluttering in the south wind.","furigana":"みなみかぜ|はた|ゆ"}],"notes":"南風 is wind coming from the south. This entry's reading is みなみかぜ; the wind direction names where the wind comes from, not where it goes. 南風が吹く describes the blowing. 南風で, by contrast, can give the cause of a movement, such as a fluttering flag. Whether the wind is warm depends on the place and the weather.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"車は、南東の方に行った。","romaji":"Kuruma wa, nantō no hō ni itta.","german":"The car went toward the southeast.","furigana":"くるま|なんとう|ほう|い"},{"japanese":"この部屋は南東向きです。","romaji":"Kono heya wa nantōmuki desu.","german":"This room faces southeast.","furigana":"へや|なんとうむ"}],"notes":"南東 is the compass direction southeast. With の it connects to 方, the direction, while 向き gives the orientation of a room. The word is a noun, not a na-adjective. 方 is read ほう here; 南東の方に行く means moving in this direction without naming a particular destination.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本では、犬をタクシーに乗せることは出来ますか？","romaji":"Nippon de wa, inu o takushī ni noseru koto wa dekimasu ka?","german":"In Japan, can you take a dog in a taxi?","furigana":"にっぽん|いぬ|の|でき"},{"japanese":"子供を車に乗せました。","romaji":"Kodomo o kuruma ni nosemashita.","german":"I put the child in the car.","furigana":"こども|くるま|の"}],"notes":"乗せる here means to let someone board a vehicle or take them along in it. The person or animal takes を, the vehicle takes に. It is the transitive counterpart of 乗る: the child gets in, while someone else puts or takes it in the car. This ichidan verb forms 乗せます and 乗せて; the examples state no general rule about taking animals in taxis.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"インターネットの速度がおそすぎてイライラしています。","romaji":"Intānetto no sokudo ga ososugite iraira shite imasu.","german":"My internet speed is so slow that it irritates me.","furigana":"そくど"},{"japanese":"速度を落としてください。","romaji":"Sokudo o otoshite kudasai.","german":"Please reduce your speed.","furigana":"そくど|お"}],"notes":"速度 is the speed or pace of a process. 速度が遅い describes a low speed; 速度を落とす means to reduce it. The word can refer to traffic and to technical processes such as an internet connection. The examples state no particular unit of measurement, so speed is judged qualitatively.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は、この学院で心理学を学んでいます。","romaji":"Watashi wa, kono gakuin de shinrigaku o manande imasu.","german":"Ich studiere an dieser Akademie Psychologie.","furigana":"わたし|がくいん|しんりがく|まな"},{"japanese":"この学院で日本語を学びます。","romaji":"Kono gakuin de nihongo o manabimasu.","german":"Ich lerne an dieser Akademie Japanisch.","furigana":"がくいん|にほんご|まな"},{"japanese":"学院の受付で、入学の手続きをしました。","romaji":"Gakuin no uketsuke de, nyūgaku no tetsuzuki o shimashita.","german":"Ich habe an der Anmeldung der Akademie die Einschreibeformalitäten erledigt.","furigana":"がくいん|うけつけ|にゅうがく|てつづ"}],"notes":"学院 ist ein Institut oder eine Akademie als Bildungseinrichtung. Das Wort erscheint häufig als Teil eines Schulnamens, legt aber allein keinen bestimmten deutschen Hochschultyp fest. 学院で nennt den Ort des Lernens; 学院の受付 nennt die Anmeldung beziehungsweise Rezeption der Einrichtung. Die Beispiele verwenden eine nicht näher benannte Akademie.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この電車は終点まで走行します。","romaji":"Kono densha wa shūten made sōkō shimasu.","german":"This train runs to the last stop.","furigana":"でんしゃ|しゅうてん|そうこう"},{"japanese":"このバスの終点は東京駅です。","romaji":"Kono basu no shūten wa Tōkyō eki desu.","german":"The last stop of this bus is Tokyo Station.","furigana":"しゅうてん|とうきょうえき"},{"japanese":"終点で、乗客が全員降りました。","romaji":"Shūten de, jōkyaku ga zen'in orimashita.","german":"At the terminal, all the passengers got off.","furigana":"しゅうてん|じょうきゃく|ぜんいんお"}],"notes":"終点 is the last station or the end of a transport route. 終点まで names the end point to which a train runs. 終点は東京駅です identifies the specific terminal of a bus route under discussion. 終点で names the place of getting off. It concerns the route of this service, not the general end of every journey.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ミーティングは終了しました。","romaji":"Mītingu wa shūryō shimashita.","german":"The meeting has ended.","furigana":"しゅうりょう"},{"japanese":"試合が終了しました。","romaji":"Shiai ga shūryō shimashita.","german":"The game has ended.","furigana":"しあい|しゅうりょう"},{"japanese":"保存してから、作業を終了してください。","romaji":"Hozon shite kara, sagyō o shūryō shite kudasai.","german":"Please save first and then finish your work.","furigana":"ほぞん|さぎょう|しゅうりょう"}],"notes":"終了 is the end or conclusion of a process. 終了する can express that something comes to an end, or that someone ends something; the sentence structure decides. 試合が終了する names the game as ended, 作業を終了する the activity someone finishes. 終了しました reports an end that has occurred, while the additional sentence asks for a deliberate conclusion.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"最終のバスはいつ出発ですか？","romaji":"Saishū no basu wa itsu shuppatsu desu ka?","german":"When does the last bus leave?","furigana":"さいしゅう|しゅっぱつ"},{"japanese":"これが最終のバスです。","romaji":"Kore ga saishū no basu desu.","german":"This is the last bus.","furigana":"さいしゅう"},{"japanese":"最終の確認が終わったら、送ってください。","romaji":"Saishū no kakunin ga owattara, okutte kudasai.","german":"Please send it once the final check is finished.","furigana":"さいしゅう|かくにん|お|おく"}],"notes":"最終 denotes the last position or phase in a series. Before a noun, の often follows, as in 最終のバス or 最終の確認; compounds such as 最終確認 are also common. The bus examples refer to the last scheduled service, the additional example to a final checking step. The word itself is a noun, not a na-adjective.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ボールが転がる音がした。","romaji":"Bōru ga korogaru oto ga shita.","german":"I heard the sound of a ball rolling.","furigana":"ころ|おと"},{"japanese":"ボールが転がっています。","romaji":"Bōru ga korogatte imasu.","german":"The ball is rolling.","furigana":"ころ"},{"japanese":"床に空き缶が転がっています。","romaji":"Yuka ni akikan ga korogatte imasu.","german":"Empty cans are lying around on the floor.","furigana":"ゆか|あ|かん|ころ"}],"notes":"転がる is intransitive: the ball is described as rolling, without naming the cause of this movement. The verb forms 転がります and 転がって. 転がっている can also mean \"lying around\" in a suitable context, such as empty cans on the floor. The examples therefore show both a movement and objects lying scattered.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"父は毎日、会社と家の間を車かバイクで運転する。","romaji":"Chichi wa mainichi, kaisha to ie no aida o kuruma ka baiku de unten suru.","german":"My father drives a car or motorcycle between the office and home every day.","furigana":"ちち|まいにち|かいしゃ|いえ|あいだ|くるま|うんてん"},{"japanese":"父が車を運転しています。","romaji":"Chichi ga kuruma o unten shite imasu.","german":"My father is driving the car.","furigana":"ちち|くるま|うんてん"},{"japanese":"雨の日は、ゆっくり車を運転してください。","romaji":"Ame no hi wa, yukkuri kuruma o unten shite kudasai.","german":"Please drive the car slowly on rainy days.","furigana":"あめ|ひ|くるま|うんてん"}],"notes":"運転する here means to operate a vehicle. 車を運転する names the vehicle being driven with を; a route driven can also be given with を. 間 is read あいだ in the route between the company and the house. The suru-form gives 運転します and 運転して. Merely riding along without steering oneself is not meant.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 8"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"車は、走る時にタイヤが回転します。","romaji":"Kuruma wa, hashiru toki ni taiya ga kaiten shimasu.","german":"When a car drives, its tires rotate.","furigana":"くるま|はし|とき|かいてん"},{"japanese":"この椅子は回転します。","romaji":"Kono isu wa kaiten shimasu.","german":"This chair swivels.","furigana":"いす|かいてん"},{"japanese":"この取っ手を一回転させてください。","romaji":"Kono totte o ikkaiten sasete kudasai.","german":"Please turn this handle one full turn.","furigana":"と|て|いっかいてん"}],"notes":"回転 is a rotation around an axis or a turning movement. 回転する is intransitive here: the tires or the chair turn. 一回転 denotes one complete revolution. 回転させる makes something turn; the handle being moved then follows with を. The examples use the physical rotating movement, not the turnover rate of goods and not mental agility.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"もう少し前に進んで下さい。","romaji":"Mō sukoshi mae ni susunde kudasai.","german":"Please move forward a little more.","furigana":"すこ|まえ|すす|くだ"},{"japanese":"まっすぐ前に進んでください。","romaji":"Massugu mae ni susunde kudasai.","german":"Please go straight ahead.","furigana":"まえ|すす"},{"japanese":"今日は、仕事がよく進みました。","romaji":"Kyō wa, shigoto ga yoku susumimashita.","german":"I made good progress on my work today.","furigana":"きょう|しごと|すす"}],"notes":"進む is intransitive and can describe spatial movement forward or progress in a piece of work. 前に names the direction for movement; 仕事が names the process that is progressing. This godan verb forms 進みます and 進んで. For a direct object that someone deliberately moves forward, 進める would be used rather than 進む in this contrast.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"道の工事が進行しています。","romaji":"Michi no kōji ga shinkō shite imasu.","german":"The road construction is progressing.","furigana":"みち|こうじ|しんこう"},{"japanese":"工事が順調に進行しています。","romaji":"Kōji ga junchō ni shinkō shite imasu.","german":"The construction work is progressing smoothly.","furigana":"こうじ|じゅんちょう|しんこう"},{"japanese":"会議の進行について、先に確認しましょう。","romaji":"Kaigi no shinkō ni tsuite, saki ni kakunin shimashō.","german":"Let's first confirm how the meeting will proceed.","furigana":"かいぎ|しんこう|さき|かくにん"}],"notes":"進行 here denotes the progress of a piece of work or an event. In the examples, 進行する is used for a process that moves forward. 順調に additionally says that this happens smoothly; 進行 alone does not yet judge the course positively. 会議の進行 names, as a noun, the course of a meeting. The entry practices this meaning for work and events.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"去年の夏、青葉が美しい山へ行きました。","romaji":"Kyonen no natsu, aoba ga utsukushii yama e ikimashita.","german":"Last summer, we went to a mountain with beautiful fresh green leaves.","furigana":"きょねん|なつ|あおば|うつく|やま|い"},{"japanese":"初夏の青葉がきれいです。","romaji":"Shoka no aoba ga kirei desu.","german":"The fresh green leaves of early summer are beautiful.","furigana":"しょか|あおば"},{"japanese":"窓を開けると、青葉の香りがしました。","romaji":"Mado o akeru to, aoba no kaori ga shimashita.","german":"When I opened the window, I smelled the scent of fresh green leaves.","furigana":"まど|あ|あおば|かお"}],"notes":"青葉 are fresh green leaves, or young foliage. In this nature word, 青 is used for green; the leaves are not blue. 青葉が美しい describes their sight, 青葉の香り their smell. The plant word is meant here and not a personal or place name with the same characters.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"宝くじが当たるなんて、運がいいよ。","romaji":"Takarakuji ga ataru nante, un ga ii yo.","german":"Winning the lottery, you're lucky.","furigana":"たから|あ|うん"},{"japanese":"今日は運がいいですね。","romaji":"Kyō wa un ga ii desu ne.","german":"You're lucky today, aren't you?","furigana":"きょう|うん"},{"japanese":"運がいいことに、最後の一枚の切符を買えました。","romaji":"Un ga ii koto ni, saigo no ichimai no kippu o kaemashita.","german":"Luckily, I was able to buy the last ticket.","furigana":"うん|さいご|いちまい|きっぷ|か"}],"notes":"運がいい means to have luck: a favorable coincidence benefits someone. 運 takes が and いい evaluates this coincidence; it is not simply about the feeling of being happy. 運がいいことに introduces a fortunate circumstance and can be translated as \"luckily\". The examples show an unexpected win and the luck of still getting a ticket.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"その映画は来週公開されます。","romaji":"Sono eiga wa raishū kōkai saremasu.","german":"That movie will be released next week.","furigana":"えいが|らいしゅうこうかい"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"会議の記録は後で公開される予定です。","romaji":"Kaigi no kiroku wa ato de kōkai sareru yotei desu.","german":"The minutes of the meeting are scheduled to be made public later.","furigana":"かいぎ|きろく|あと|こうかい|よてい"},{"japanese":"この寺の庭は春と秋だけ一般に公開されている。","romaji":"Kono tera no niwa wa haru to aki dake ippan ni koukai sarete iru.","german":"The garden of this temple is open to the public only in spring and autumn.","furigana":"てら|にわ|はる|あき|いっぱん|こうかい"}],"notes":"公開 means making something accessible to the public: films (映画が公開される = \"is released in theaters\"), information, data, gardens or buildings. As a transitive する-verb the object takes を: 情報を公開する; the passive 公開される is very common. 公表 stresses the official announcement of facts, 発表 announcing or presenting, while 公開 puts making accessible in the foreground. Opposite: 非公開 (\"not open to the public\"). Common: 一般公開, 公開日, 情報公開.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は社長ですが、開業したばかりで、お金がありません。","romaji":"Watashi wa shachō desu ga, kaigyō shita bakari de, okane ga arimasen.","german":"Ich bin Firmenchef, aber ich habe den Betrieb gerade erst eröffnet und habe kein Geld.","furigana":"わたし|しゃちょう|かいぎょう|かね"},{"japanese":"この店は来月開業します。","romaji":"Kono mise wa raigetsu kaigyō shimasu.","german":"Dieses Geschäft eröffnet nächsten Monat.","furigana":"みせ|らいげつかいぎょう"}],"notes":"開業 bezeichnet den Beginn eines Geschäftsbetriebs oder einer Praxis. Mit する kann eine Person eine Tätigkeit aufnehmen oder ein Geschäft eröffnen: 店が開業する nennt das Geschäft als Subjekt. 開業したばかり bedeutet, dass die Eröffnung erst kurz zurückliegt. Das Wort beschreibt hier die Aufnahme des Betriebs; die deutsche Übersetzung „gründen“ wäre ohne weiteren Zusammenhang weniger genau.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日は集金の日です。","romaji":"Kyō wa shūkin no hi desu.","german":"Today is the day for collecting money.","furigana":"きょう|しゅうきん|ひ"},{"japanese":"毎月集金に来ます。","romaji":"Maitsuki shūkin ni kimasu.","german":"Someone comes to collect the money every month.","furigana":"まいつきしゅうきん|き"},{"japanese":"集金したお金を、封筒に入れました。","romaji":"Shūkin shita okane o, fūtō ni iremashita.","german":"I put the collected money in an envelope.","furigana":"しゅうきん|かね|ふうとう|い"}],"notes":"集金 is the collecting or gathering of money, such as payments that are due. 集金の日 names a day set aside for it, and 集金に来る the purpose of a visit. With する it forms a verb; 集金したお金 is money that has already been collected. It concerns collecting a payment, not automatically a voluntary donation, for which 寄付, for example, is used.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"学校で、むかしの農民のくらしを学びました。","romaji":"Gakkō de, mukashi no nōmin no kurashi o manabimashita.","german":"At school, we learned about the lives of farmers in the past.","furigana":"がっこう|のうみん|まな"},{"japanese":"あの農民はお米を作っています。","romaji":"Ano nōmin wa okome o tsukutte imasu.","german":"That farmer grows rice.","furigana":"のうみん|こめ|つく"}],"notes":"農民 refers to people who engage in agriculture and also appears in historical descriptions. As a noun it can be described with の: むかしの農民 are farmers of earlier times. 農民のくらし means their way of life, 農民が米を作る their activity. The word names a group of people and is not an adjective; it does not claim that all these people grow the same crops.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"うちの社長はトライアスロンの後に出社するような鉄人です。","romaji":"Uchi no shachō wa toraiasuron no nochi ni shussha suru yō na tetsujin desu.","german":"Our company president is an ironman who comes to work even after a triathlon.","furigana":"しゃちょう|のち|しゅっしゃ|てつじん"},{"japanese":"あの人は鉄人のように強いです。","romaji":"Ano hito wa tetsujin no yō ni tsuyoi desu.","german":"That person is as strong as an iron man.","furigana":"ひと|てつじん|つよ"}],"notes":"鉄人 is here, figuratively, a person with extraordinary strength or endurance. The fact that the company president still works after a triathlon is the basis for this comparison; he is not made of iron. 鉄人のように means \"like an iron man\" and compares a trait. The noun can also denote a robot in fictional contexts; this meaning is not used in the examples.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大人になったら、電鉄会社に入社したいです。","romaji":"Otona ni nattara, dentetsu kaisha ni nyūsha shitai desu.","german":"When I grow up, I want to join an electric railway company.","furigana":"おとな|でんてつかいしゃ|にゅうしゃ"},{"japanese":"この電鉄は便利です。","romaji":"Kono dentetsu wa benri desu.","german":"This railway line is convenient.","furigana":"でんてつ|べんり"}],"notes":"電鉄 is an electric railway and often appears as part of the name of a railway company. 電鉄会社 names the company where someone wants to work; この電鉄 refers, in the second example, to the railway line under discussion. 会社に入社する means to join a company. The characters 電 and 鉄 help in recognizing the word, but do not replace its full reading でんてつ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"算数の先生は、田中先生です。","romaji":"Sansū no sensei wa, Tanaka sensei desu.","german":"The arithmetic teacher is Mr. Tanaka.","furigana":"さんすう|せんせい|たなかせんせい"},{"japanese":"算数の宿題をしましょう。","romaji":"Sansū no shukudai o shimashō.","german":"Let's do our arithmetic homework.","furigana":"さんすう|しゅくだい"}],"notes":"算数 is arithmetic, or elementary mathematics as a school subject. 算数の先生 names the teacher for this subject, 算数の宿題 the corresponding homework. The word is a noun and connects here to the following noun with の. The more general term for mathematics is 数学; the two words are not used in the same way in every educational context.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"週に一回、日本語の教室に通っています。","romaji":"Shū ni ikkai, nihongo no kyōshitsu ni kayotte imasu.","german":"I attend a Japanese class once a week.","furigana":"しゅう|いっかい|にほんご|きょうしつ|かよ"},{"japanese":"日本語を勉強しています。","romaji":"Nihongo o benkyō shite imasu.","german":"I'm studying Japanese.","furigana":"にほんご|べんきょう"},{"japanese":"名前を日本語で書いてください。","romaji":"Namae o nihongo de kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write your name in Japanese.","furigana":"なまえ|にほんご|か"}],"notes":"日本語 is the Japanese language and is used as a noun. 日本語を勉強する names it as the object of study; 日本語で書く names with で the language in which something is written. 日本語の教室 denotes a Japanese course. 一回 is read いっかい, so 週に一回 means \"once a week\". A subject of study and the linguistic means of an action fulfill different roles in the sentence.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"フランス語を学ぶために大学に行きました。","romaji":"Furansugo o manabu tame ni daigaku ni ikimashita.","german":"I went to university to study French.","furigana":"ご|まな|だいがく|い"},{"japanese":"フランス語が少し話せます。","romaji":"Furansugo ga sukoshi hanasemasu.","german":"I can speak a little French.","furigana":"ご|すこ|はな"}],"notes":"フランス語 denotes the French language; 語 is read ご in this language name. フランス語を学ぶ names the subject of study, フランス語が話せる the language one can speak. ために explains the purpose of going to university in the first example. In the usual writing, the syllables of the country name are in katakana; the entry's continuous hiragana reading gives the same pronunciation.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"友人にスペイン語を教えてあげました。","romaji":"Yūjin ni Supeingo o oshiete agemashita.","german":"I taught Spanish to a friend.","furigana":"ゆうじん|ご|おし"},{"japanese":"スペイン語を勉強したいです。","romaji":"Supeingo o benkyō shitai desu.","german":"I want to study Spanish.","furigana":"ご|べんきょう"}],"notes":"スペイン語 is Spanish as a language. 人にスペイン語を教える names the learner with に and the subject taught with を. 教えてあげる presents the teaching as a favor to this person; the form suits a familiar relationship better than a neutral statement about teaching. 勉強したい, by contrast, expresses one's own wish to study. The 語 of the language name is read ご.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日の調子はどうですか？","romaji":"Kyō no chōshi wa dō desu ka?","german":"How are you doing today?","furigana":"きょう|ちょうし"},{"japanese":"体の調子はどうですか。","romaji":"Karada no chōshi wa dō desu ka.","german":"How are you feeling physically?","furigana":"からだ|ちょうし"},{"japanese":"このパソコンは、最近調子が悪いです。","romaji":"Kono pasokon wa, saikin chōshi ga warui desu.","german":"This computer hasn't been working well lately.","furigana":"さいきんちょうし|わる"}],"notes":"調子 here means the condition of a person or the functioning of a device. 体の調子 asks explicitly about physical well-being; without this addition, the context can clarify the reference. 調子がいい and 調子が悪い evaluate how well something is going or working. For a computer, 調子が悪い describes a malfunction, not an illness. Other meanings such as a musical tone are not practiced here.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 19"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先生は、重要なポイントを強調しました。","romaji":"Sensei wa, jūyō na pointo o kyōchō shimashita.","german":"The teacher emphasized the important points.","furigana":"せんせい|じゅうよう|きょうちょう"},{"japanese":"この言葉を強調してください。","romaji":"Kono kotoba o kyōchō shite kudasai.","german":"Please emphasize this word.","furigana":"ことば|きょうちょう"},{"japanese":"赤い線で、注意する所を強調しました。","romaji":"Akai sen de, chūi suru tokoro o kyōchō shimashita.","german":"I highlighted the places to watch out for with red lines.","furigana":"あか|せん|ちゅうい|ところ|きょうちょう"}],"notes":"強調 is the special emphasizing or highlighting of a statement. With する it is transitive: the emphasized word or important point takes を. 強調 can happen in speech or through visible markings; in the additional example, a red line is the means of directing attention. Here it does not mean greater loudness as such, but the special weighting of a content.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"まだ親しい友人にしか話してないの。","romaji":"Mada shitashii yūjin ni shika hanashite nai no.","german":"I have only told close friends so far.","furigana":"した|ゆうじん|はな"},{"japanese":"彼とは親しい友達です。","romaji":"Kare to wa shitashii tomodachi desu.","german":"He and I are close friends.","furigana":"かれ|した|ともだち"}],"notes":"親しい here describes a close, familiar relationship with people. 親しい友人 are close friends; 人と親しい names with と the person one is close to. It is an i-adjective and stands before 友人 or 友達 without な. しか with a negative form limits the circle of those in the know to close friends in the first example. Physical closeness would be expressed with 近い instead.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この前より、顔付きが穏やかになったね。","romaji":"Kono mae yori, kaotsuki ga odayaka ni natta ne.","german":"Your expression has become calmer than last time.","furigana":"まえ|かおつ|おだ"},{"japanese":"怖い顔付きをしていますね。","romaji":"Kowai kaotsuki o shite imasu ne.","german":"You have a frightening look on your face, don't you?","furigana":"こわ|かおつ"},{"japanese":"役者は、顔付きを変えて別の人を演じました。","romaji":"Yakusha wa, kaotsuki o kaete betsu no hito o enjimashita.","german":"The actor changed his expression and played a different person.","furigana":"やくしゃ|かおつ|か|べつ|ひと|えん"}],"notes":"顔付き denotes the facial expression or the outward appearance of the face. 顔付きが穏やかになる describes a change to a calmer expression; 怖い顔付きをしている describes the visible impression. With 顔付きを変える, someone can deliberately change their expression, for example when acting. The first sentence compares with an earlier meeting, not with another person's face.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"アルツハイマー病のせいで、色々な事を思い出せない時があります。","romaji":"Arutsuhaimābyō no sei de, iroiro na koto o omoidasenai toki ga arimasu.","german":"Because of Alzheimer's disease, there are times when I cannot remember various things.","furigana":"びょう|いろいろ|こと|おも|だ|とき"},{"japanese":"祖父は心臓病で入院しています。","romaji":"Sofu wa shinzōbyō de nyūin shite imasu.","german":"My grandfather is in the hospital with heart disease.","furigana":"そふ|しんぞうびょう|にゅういん"},{"japanese":"父は糖尿病なので、甘いものを控えています。","romaji":"Chichi wa tounyoubyou na node, amai mono o hikaete imasu.","german":"My father has diabetes, so he cuts back on sweets.","furigana":"ちち|とうにょうびょう|あま|ひか"}],"notes":"〜病 is a suffix for names of diseases: 心臓病 (\"heart disease\"), 糖尿病 (\"diabetes\"), アルツハイマー病. On its own, 病 is not read びょう; the everyday word for illness is 病気, and literary there is 病（やまい）. For syndromes and complaints, 〜症 is often used instead, such as 花粉症 (\"hay fever\") or 熱中症 (\"heatstroke\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"病人や、ケガ人はいますか？","romaji":"Byōnin ya, keganin wa imasu ka?","german":"Is anyone sick or injured?","furigana":"びょうにん|にん"},{"japanese":"病人を病院に連れて行きました。","romaji":"Byōnin o byōin ni tsurete ikimashita.","german":"I took the sick person to the hospital.","furigana":"びょうにん|びょういん|つ|い"}],"notes":"病人 is a sick person, not necessarily someone already being treated in a hospital. 病人がいる states the presence of such a person with いる, which is used for living beings. 病人を病院に連れて行く names the accompanied person with を and the destination with に. ケガ人 is read けがにん and, in contrast, denotes injured people in the first example.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"かき氷は夏に食べるのが最高です。","romaji":"Kakigōri wa natsu ni taberu no ga saikō desu.","german":"Shaved ice is best eaten in summer.","furigana":"ごおり|なつ|た|さいこう"},{"japanese":"今日の天気は最高です。","romaji":"Kyō no tenki wa saikō desu.","german":"The weather is wonderful today.","furigana":"きょう|てんき|さいこう"},{"japanese":"今日の最高気温は三十度でした。","romaji":"Kyō no saikō kion wa sanjū do deshita.","german":"Today's highest temperature was thirty degrees.","furigana":"きょう|さいこうきおん|さんじゅうど"}],"notes":"最高 can express an enthusiastic evaluation such as \"wonderful\" or name the highest value of a quantity. 天気は最高です praises the weather; 最高気温 is the highest temperature. Before a noun, compounds such as 最高気温 and connections with の are common. The examples therefore distinguish a personal judgment from a measurable maximum. かき氷 is shaved ice.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"パンダが次々に竹を食べました。","romaji":"Panda ga tsugitsugi ni take o tabemashita.","german":"The panda ate bamboo one piece after another.","furigana":"つぎつぎ|たけ|た"},{"japanese":"問題が次々と出てきました。","romaji":"Mondai ga tsugitsugi to dete kimashita.","german":"Problems came up one after another.","furigana":"もんだい|つぎつぎ|で"}],"notes":"次々 describes a series of events in which the next one keeps being added. It can be used adverbially with に or と: 次々に竹を食べる and 問題が次々と出てくる. It emphasizes the sequence and not simply a large total quantity. In the second example, problems arise one after another; 出てくる means \"to crop up\" here, not people coming out of a room.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このパーツ、金に代える？","romaji":"Kono pātsu, kin ni kaeru?","german":"Shall we replace this part with gold?","furigana":"きん|か"},{"japanese":"古い部品を新しいものに代えました。","romaji":"Furui buhin o atarashii mono ni kaemashita.","german":"I replaced the old part with a new one.","furigana":"ふる|ぶひん|あたら|か"},{"japanese":"今日の試合では、選手を一人代えました。","romaji":"Kyō no shiai de wa, senshu o hitori kaemashita.","german":"In today's game, we substituted one player.","furigana":"きょう|しあい|せんしゅ|ひとりか"}],"notes":"代える is a transitive ichidan verb: what or who is replaced can take を, the replacement に. For things, this word family is often written 替える, and for another person as a stand-in or replacement, often 代える. When replacing things, the spelling 代える also appears next to 替える. The player substitution shows the typical use with a person. The forms are 代えます and 代えて, not a godan form ending in って.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"町民の中には、目医者に通っている人も多いです。","romaji":"Chōmin no naka ni wa, meisha ni kayotte iru hito mo ōi desu.","german":"Many of the town's residents go to the eye doctor.","furigana":"ちょうみん|なか|めいしゃ|かよ|ひと|おお"},{"japanese":"目医者に行かなければなりません。","romaji":"Meisha ni ikanakereba narimasen.","german":"I have to go to the eye doctor.","furigana":"めいしゃ|い"}],"notes":"目医者 is an understandable term for an eye doctor. 目医者に通う describes repeated visits; 目医者に行く can mean a single trip to this practice. に names the destination. The first sentence describes the behavior of many local residents, the second a personal necessity. The reading めいしゃ is romanized as meisha; the vowel sequence remains recognizable.","levelNote":"Compound: 目 (N5) + 医者 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"右に曲がって一番目の部屋に入ってください。","romaji":"Migi ni magatte ichibanme no heya ni haitte kudasai.","german":"Please turn right and enter the first room.","furigana":"みぎ|ま|いちばんめ|へや|はい"},{"japanese":"一番目に名前を呼ばれました。","romaji":"Ichibanme ni namae o yobaremashita.","german":"My name was called first.","furigana":"いちばんめ|なまえ|よ"}],"notes":"一番目 is the first position in a sequence. 一番目の部屋 identifies the first room with の; 一番目に names the rank of an event, such as in the calling of names. 目 makes it an ordinal number explicitly. Being first in the order is not a claim that this room or person is qualitatively the best. The reading is いちばんめ without a small っ.","levelNote":"Compound: 番目 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"明日のデートが楽しみです。","romaji":"Ashita no dēto ga tanoshimi desu.","german":"I'm looking forward to the date tomorrow.","furigana":"あした|たの"},{"japanese":"旅行が楽しみです。","romaji":"Ryokō ga tanoshimi desu.","german":"I'm looking forward to the trip.","furigana":"りょこう|たの"},{"japanese":"毎朝、庭で花を見るのが私の楽しみです。","romaji":"Maiasa, niwa de hana o miru no ga watashi no tanoshimi desu.","german":"Looking at the flowers in the garden every morning is my pleasure.","furigana":"まいあさ|にわ|はな|み|わたし|たの"}],"notes":"楽しみ can be anticipation of an event or a regular joy in everyday life. デートが楽しみです means that you are looking forward to the date; the noun is combined with です here. 毎朝花を見るのが楽しみです, by contrast, names a recurring pleasant activity. 楽しみにしている is also a common form for anticipation. The entry is the noun 楽しみ, not the identically written continuative form of the verb 楽しむ.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 35"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このカレーは、日本人向けのスパイスを使ってます。","romaji":"Kono karē wa, Nipponjinmuke no supaisu o tsukattemasu.","german":"This curry uses spices intended for Japanese people.","furigana":"にっぽんじんむ|つか"},{"japanese":"この本は子供向けです。","romaji":"Kono hon wa kodomomuke desu.","german":"This book is intended for children.","furigana":"ほん|こどもむ"},{"japanese":"この車は、海外向けに作られています。","romaji":"Kono kuruma wa, kaigaimuke ni tsukurarete imasu.","german":"This car is made for the overseas market.","furigana":"くるま|かいがいむ|つく"}],"notes":"〜向け attaches to a noun and denotes the target group or the intended market. 子供向けです means \"intended for children\"; 子供向けの本 uses の before the following noun. 海外向けに作る names, with に, the market for which something is made. It is a word suffix, not an independent sentence particle. The sign 〜 only shows the attachment point and is not pronounced. The spelling without the placeholder is 向け.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"きっと助かるよ。平気。","romaji":"Kitto tasukaru yo. Heiki.","german":"You will surely be saved. Don't worry.","furigana":"たす|へいき"},{"japanese":"みんなが助かりました。","romaji":"Minna ga tasukarimashita.","german":"Everyone was saved.","furigana":"たす"},{"japanese":"荷物を持ってくれると助かります。","romaji":"Nimotsu o motte kureru to tasukarimasu.","german":"It would help me if you carried the luggage.","furigana":"にもつ|も|たす"}],"notes":"助かる is intransitive: it describes the person who is rescued or the one who benefits from help. It can mean being rescued from danger, but also \"that would be a help to me\". 荷物を持ってくれると助かります asks for support indirectly and says that carrying would be a relief. The active \"to save or support someone\" is 助ける. As a godan verb, 助かる forms 助かります and 助かって.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 18"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"夏は、かき氷を食べたり、花火を楽しんだりする季節です。","romaji":"Natsu wa, kakigōri o tabetari, hanabi o tanoshindari suru kisetsu desu.","german":"Summer is the season for eating shaved ice and enjoying fireworks.","furigana":"なつ|ごおり|た|はなび|たの|きせつ"},{"japanese":"旅行を楽しみましょう。","romaji":"Ryokō o tanoshimimashō.","german":"Let's enjoy the trip.","furigana":"りょこう|たの"}],"notes":"楽しむ means to take pleasure in an activity or experience. The thing enjoyed takes を: 花火を楽しむ or 旅行を楽しむ. This transitive godan verb ending in む forms 楽しみます and 楽しんで; the invitation 楽しみましょう suggests enjoying something together. Unlike 楽しみです, which often expresses anticipation, the verb here describes experiencing something pleasant. 楽む is an additional dictionary and search spelling with the same reading たのしむ. 楽しむ remains the usual form; the variant is not a different verb.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人に対して小石を投げ付けるなんて、失礼だろ！","romaji":"Hito ni taishite koishi o nagetsukeru nante, shitsurei daro!","german":"Throwing pebbles at people is rude!","furigana":"ひと|たい|こいし|な|つ|しつれい"},{"japanese":"ボールを投げ付けないでください。","romaji":"Bōru o nagetsukenaide kudasai.","german":"Please do not throw the ball at anyone.","furigana":"な|つ"},{"japanese":"相手にひどい言葉を投げ付けてしまった。","romaji":"Aite ni hidoi kotoba o nagetsukete shimatta.","german":"I ended up hurling hurtful words at the other person.","furigana":"あいて|ことば|な|つ"}],"notes":"投げ付ける is a forceful throw or one aimed at someone. The thrown object takes を, and the target can be named with に. With 言葉 or insults as the object, it is figurative: one hurls hurtful words at someone. This ichidan verb forms 投げ付けます and 投げ付けて. The negative 投げ付けないでください asks to refrain from this action; an unnamed target is not added in translation.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この昔話では、タヌキが人を化かします。","romaji":"Kono mukashibanashi de wa, tanuki ga hito o bakashimasu.","german":"In this folktale, a tanuki bewitches people.","furigana":"むかしばなし|ひと|ば"},{"japanese":"狐に化かされたような気分です。","romaji":"Kitsune ni bakasareta yō na kibun desu.","german":"I feel as if I had been bewitched by a fox.","furigana":"きつね|ば|きぶん"}],"notes":"化かす is a transitive godan verb: 化かします, 化かして. In stories it can mean \"to bewitch\" or \"to deceive by magic\". With を you name the person deceived. 狐に化かされたような気分 means figuratively to feel as if bewitched or completely confused; 化かされた is the passive form.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コウイチの赤ちゃんの名前が決定したら、すぐにお知らせしますね。","romaji":"Kōichi no akachan no namae ga kettei shitara, sugu ni oshirase shimasu ne.","german":"As soon as Koichi's baby's name is decided, I will let you know right away.","furigana":"あか|なまえ|けってい|し"},{"japanese":"会議で計画を決定しました。","romaji":"Kaigi de keikaku o kettei shimashita.","german":"We decided on the plan at the meeting.","furigana":"かいぎ|けいかく|けってい"}],"notes":"決定する means \"to decide\" or \"to determine\" and forms する forms: 決定します, 決定して. With を you name what someone decides. With が you can say that something is settled: 名前が決定する. 決定したら names the settling as the condition for the next action. けってい contains a small っ and the vowel sequence ei.","levelNote":"Derived from 決定 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"旅行先で友達に出会いました。","romaji":"Ryokōsaki de tomodachi ni deaimashita.","german":"I ran into a friend on my trip.","furigana":"りょこうさき|ともだち|であ"},{"japanese":"図書館ですてきな本に出会いました。","romaji":"Toshokan de suteki na hon ni deaimashita.","german":"I came across a wonderful book at the library.","furigana":"としょかん|ほん|であ"},{"japanese":"この谷で二つの川が出合う。","romaji":"Kono tani de futatsu no kawa ga deau.","german":"Two rivers flow together in this valley.","furigana":"たに|ふた|かわ|であ"}],"notes":"出会う is an intransitive godan verb: 出会います, 出会って. It means a chance encounter or discovering something. With に you name the person or thing you come across. With すてきな本に出会う, it is figuratively about discovering an appealing book. 出合う is another spelling with the same reading であう. For rivers flowing together or paths meeting, 出合う is especially typical. An older \"rendezvous\" is a secret meeting in dictionary examples; for a normal appointment, one uses, for example, 待ち合わせる instead.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"四月に私鉄の会社に入社しました。","romaji":"Shigatsu ni shitetsu no kaisha ni nyūsha shimashita.","german":"In April, I joined a private railway company.","furigana":"しがつ|してつ|かいしゃ|にゅうしゃ"},{"japanese":"私鉄で通勤しています。","romaji":"Shitetsu de tsūkin shite imasu.","german":"I commute by private railway.","furigana":"してつ|つうきん"}],"notes":"私鉄 is a private railway. 私鉄の会社 names a corresponding railway company, 私鉄で the means of transport. 入社する describes joining a company. 私鉄で通勤する means to commute to work by this railway. してつ is read without a long vowel.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"うちの犬は要求が多い。","romaji":"Uchi no inu wa yōkyū ga ōi.","german":"Our dog makes a lot of demands.","furigana":"いぬ|ようきゅう|おお"},{"japanese":"労働者が要求を出しました。","romaji":"Rōdōsha ga yōkyū o dashimashita.","german":"The workers made a demand.","furigana":"ろうどうしゃ|ようきゅう|だ"}],"notes":"要求 is a demand or an insistent request. 要求する is a transitive する verb: 要求します, 要求して. With を you name what is demanded. 要求が多い describes many wishes or demands; 要求を出す means to make a demand. The reading ようきゅう has a long o and a long u.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私大に入学したいと思っています。","romaji":"Shidai ni nyūgaku shitai to omotte imasu.","german":"I'd like to enroll at a private university.","furigana":"しだい|にゅうがく|おも"},{"japanese":"姉は私大に通っています。","romaji":"Ane wa shidai ni kayotte imasu.","german":"My older sister attends a private university.","furigana":"あね|しだい|かよ"}],"notes":"私大 is an abbreviation of 私立大学, i.e. a private university. 私大に入学する means entering such a university, 私大に通う means attending it regularly. The reading is しだい with no long vowel. The word by itself says nothing about tuition or quality.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"イベントの開始時間は何時ですか？","romaji":"Ibento no kaishi jikan wa nanji desu ka?","german":"What time does the event start?","furigana":"かいしじかん|なんじ"},{"japanese":"授業が開始しました。","romaji":"Jugyō ga kaishi shimashita.","german":"The class has started.","furigana":"じゅぎょう|かいし"}],"notes":"開始 is the start of an action or event. 開始する forms a する-verb and can be transitive or intransitive: 作業を開始する \"to start the work\", 授業が開始する \"the class starts\". 開始時間 asks for the start time. 開始します and 開始して are regular する-forms.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このドレスは、体の曲線をキレイに見せてくれる。","romaji":"Kono doresu wa, karada no kyokusen o kirei ni misete kureru.","german":"This dress shows off the curves of the body beautifully.","furigana":"からだ|きょくせん|み"},{"japanese":"紙に曲線を描きました。","romaji":"Kami ni kyokusen o kakimashita.","german":"I drew a curved line on the paper.","furigana":"かみ|きょくせん|か"}],"notes":"曲線 is a curved line or curve. 曲線を描く describes drawing one, 体の曲線 the contours of a body. 見せてくれる describes the impression something gives. きょ forms a single mora; きょくせん has no long vowels.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"色々な言語を学ぶために海外へ行きました。","romaji":"Iroiro na gengo o manabu tame ni kaigai e ikimashita.","german":"I went abroad to learn various languages.","furigana":"いろいろ|げんご|まな|かいがい|い"},{"japanese":"この本は三つの言語で書かれています。","romaji":"Kono hon wa mittsu no gengo de kakarete imasu.","german":"This book is written in three languages.","furigana":"ほん|みっ|げんご|か"}],"notes":"言語 is a language as a system. 言語を学ぶ names it as the subject being learned; 色々な言語 means various languages. 学ぶために explains the purpose of an action. The reading on this card is げんご. Here the word refers to languages in general, not to a single spoken sentence.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"東京ディズニーリゾートは、千葉県にあります。","romaji":"Tōkyō Dizunī Rizōto wa, Chiba-ken ni arimasu.","german":"The Tokyo Disney Resort is in Chiba Prefecture.","furigana":"とうきょう|ちばけん"},{"japanese":"千葉に住んでいる友達がいます。","romaji":"Chiba ni sunde iru tomodachi ga imasu.","german":"I have a friend who lives in Chiba.","furigana":"ちば|す|ともだち"}],"notes":"千葉 can mean the city or the prefecture of Chiba. 千葉県 explicitly names the prefecture, and 千葉市 explicitly names the city. 千葉に住む gives Chiba as a place of residence; without an addition, the context determines the exact reference. ちば has no long vowels.","levelNote":"Compound: 葉 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"思わず本音を口にしてしまいました。","romaji":"Omowazu honne o kuchi ni shite shimaimashita.","german":"I blurted out what I really think.","furigana":"おも|ほんね|くち"},{"japanese":"思わず笑ってしまいました。","romaji":"Omowazu waratte shimaimashita.","german":"I couldn't help laughing.","furigana":"おも|わら"}],"notes":"思わず describes a reaction that happens involuntarily or without prior intention. It stands before the action as an adverb. 思わず本音を口にする means letting one's true thoughts slip out. 思わず笑ってしまう describes laughter that escapes you. してしまいました can emphasize that the action was completed unintentionally.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この立ち飲み屋は、生ビールが安くて、おつまみもおいしいですよ。","romaji":"Kono tachinomiya wa, namabīru ga yasukute, otsumami mo oishii desu yo.","german":"This standing bar has cheap draft beer and tasty snacks.","furigana":"た|の|や|なま|やす"},{"japanese":"ここでは立ち飲みができます。","romaji":"Koko de wa tachinomi ga dekimasu.","german":"You can drink standing up here.","furigana":"た|の"}],"notes":"立ち飲み is drinking while standing. 立ち飲み屋 or 立ち飲みの店 refers to a shop or bar where people drink standing. 立ち飲みができます is about the possibility of drinking in this way. The reading is たちのみ, with no long vowels.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これからクリーニングの作業をします。","romaji":"Kore kara kurīningu no sagyō o shimasu.","german":"I'm going to do the cleaning work now.","furigana":"さぎょう"},{"japanese":"作業が終わりました。","romaji":"Sagyō ga owarimashita.","german":"The work is finished.","furigana":"さぎょう|お"}],"notes":"作業 is a concrete piece of work or task. 作業する forms an intransitive する-verb; 作業をする is also common. 作業が終わる describes completion. 作業をします can announce an upcoming task. さぎょう contains a long o and the jointly pronounced sequence ぎょ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"長い間病んでいました。","romaji":"Nagai aida yande imashita.","german":"I was ill for a long time.","furigana":"なが|あいだや"},{"japanese":"この物語では、主人公は心を病んでいます。","romaji":"Kono monogatari de wa, shujinkō wa kokoro o yande imasu.","german":"In this story, the protagonist is mentally troubled.","furigana":"ものがたり|しゅじんこう|こころ|や"}],"notes":"病む is a Godan verb: 病みます, 病んで. It can refer to illness or suffering. 病んでいる describes a continuing state. In 心を病む, 心 is named with を as the affected area. This expression denotes mental suffering; the exact condition must be inferred from the context.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日の朝、役場に行ってきました。","romaji":"Kyō no asa, yakuba ni itte kimashita.","german":"I went to the town hall this morning.","furigana":"きょう|あさ|やくば|い"},{"japanese":"役場に届を出しました。","romaji":"Yakuba ni todoke o dashimashita.","german":"I submitted a notification to the town hall.","furigana":"やくば|とどけ|だ"}],"notes":"役場 is a town hall, i.e. the local government office. 役場に行く names it as the destination of a visit. 役場に届を出す describes submitting a notification there. 行ってきました expresses that one went there and came back. The reading is やくば.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"姉に対する父の口調は強かった。","romaji":"Ane ni taisuru chichi no kuchō wa tsuyokatta.","german":"My father's tone toward my older sister was harsh.","furigana":"あね|たい|ちち|くちょう|つよ"},{"japanese":"優しい口調で話してください。","romaji":"Yasashii kuchō de hanashite kudasai.","german":"Please speak in a gentle tone.","furigana":"やさ|くちょう|はな"}],"notes":"口調 is the way someone speaks, i.e. their tone of voice. 人に対する口調 states who the tone is directed at. 優しい口調で describes a gentle tone as the manner of speaking. The reading is くちょう with a long o; here 口 is read only as く.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"青森の農場で生のトウモロコシを食べました。","romaji":"Aomori no nōjō de nama no tōmorokoshi o tabemashita.","german":"I ate raw corn at a farm in Aomori.","furigana":"あおもり|のうじょう|なま|た"},{"japanese":"大きな農場で働いています。","romaji":"Ōkina nōjō de hataraite imasu.","german":"I work on a large farm.","furigana":"おお|のうじょう|はたら"}],"notes":"農場 is an agricultural operation or farm. 農場で names it as the place of an activity. 青森の農場 places it in a region; 大きな農場 describes its size. のうじょう has two long o sounds and is written nōjō.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今朝になってから「あ！テスト今日じゃん！」って思い出したんです。","romaji":"Kesa ni natte kara “A! Tesuto kyō jan!” tte omoidashita n desu.","german":"It was only this morning that I remembered, \"Oh! The test is today!\"","furigana":"けさ|きょう|おも|だ"},{"japanese":"今朝は寒かったですね。","romaji":"Kesa wa samukatta desu ne.","german":"It was cold this morning, wasn't it?","furigana":"けさ|さむ"}],"notes":"今朝 means \"this morning\" and is read けさ. It can stand as a time expression without any particle, or become the topic with は. 今朝になってから stresses that one only noticed something this very morning. The expression refers to the morning of the day on which it is spoken.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 8"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の兄は、足し算が上手で早いです。","romaji":"Watashi no ani wa, tashizan ga jōzu de hayai desu.","german":"My older brother is good and fast at addition.","furigana":"わたし|あに|た|ざん|じょうず|はや"},{"japanese":"まず足し算を習いましょう。","romaji":"Mazu tashizan o naraimashō.","german":"Let's learn addition first.","furigana":"た|ざん|なら"}],"notes":"足し算 is addition, i.e. adding numbers together. 足し算が上手 describes good arithmetic skill. 足し算を習う names addition as what is being learned. In this word 算 is read ざん; たしざん has no long vowels.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"もちろん、わたしは引き算ができます！","romaji":"Mochiron, watashi wa hikizan ga dekimasu!","german":"Of course I can do subtraction!","furigana":"ひ|ざん"},{"japanese":"次は引き算を練習しましょう。","romaji":"Tsugi wa hikizan o renshū shimashō.","german":"Next, let's practice subtraction.","furigana":"つぎ|ひ|ざん|れんしゅう"}],"notes":"引き算 is subtraction, i.e. taking numbers away. 引き算ができる describes the ability to do it; 引き算を練習する names what is practiced. 算 is read ざん here. With 次は you can announce subtraction as the next learning step. 引算 is a spelling variant of 引き算 with the same reading ひきざん and the same meaning. The usual spelling on this card is 引き算.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人が来ると、セキュリティのためのライトが点くんです。","romaji":"Hito ga kuru to, sekyuriti no tame no raito ga tsuku n desu.","german":"When someone comes, the security light comes on.","furigana":"ひと|く|つ"},{"japanese":"電気が点いたので、本を読み始めました。","romaji":"Denki ga tsuita node, hon o yomihajimemashita.","german":"Because the light came on, I started reading a book.","furigana":"でんき|つ|ほん|よ|はじ"}],"notes":"点く is an intransitive godan verb: 点きます, 点いて. It describes a light or device coming on; it takes が. With the transitive 点ける, someone switches something on. 点いたので can name the coming on as the reason for a following action. The spelling つく is also often seen.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"山道を歩くのは体力が必要だ。","romaji":"Yamamichi o aruku no wa tairyoku ga hitsuyō da.","german":"Walking on a mountain path takes physical stamina.","furigana":"やまみち|ある|たいりょく|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"山道の途中で休みました。","romaji":"Yamamichi no tochū de yasumimashita.","german":"I rested partway along the mountain path.","furigana":"やまみち|とちゅう|やす"}],"notes":"山道 is a path or road in the mountains. The reading on this card is やまみち. In 山道を歩く, を marks the path that is traversed. 山道の途中 names a point along the way on this path. The word alone does not say how difficult or steep the path is.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あ、タバコのにおいがする。","romaji":"A, tabako no nioi ga suru.","german":"Oh, I smell tobacco."},{"japanese":"ここでタバコを吸わないでください。","romaji":"Koko de tabako o suwanaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't smoke cigarettes here.","furigana":"す"}],"notes":"タバコ means tobacco or a cigarette. タバコのにおい describes the smell that is perceived. タバコを吸う means \"to smoke\"; 吸わないでください asks someone not to. The word is often written in kana. All vowels in タバコ are short. 煙草（たばこ） is a kanji spelling of the same word; タバコ or たばこ is commonly used. The characters are read as a whole word, たばこ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あのレストランのサービス、ビミョーじゃない？","romaji":"Ano resutoran no sābisu, bimyō ja nai?","german":"The service at that restaurant is so-so, don't you think?"},{"japanese":"店員さんのサービスに感謝しています。","romaji":"Ten’in-san no sābisu ni kansha shite imasu.","german":"I am grateful for the staff member's service.","furigana":"てんいん|かんしゃ"}],"notes":"サービス here means service, i.e. attendance or care. レストランのサービス refers to what the restaurant provides. サービスに感謝する describes thanks for this care. ビミョーじゃない？ is a colloquial, skeptical assessment. サービス is written sābisu, with a long a.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 16"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"しかし、それはウソだった。","romaji":"Shikashi, sore wa uso datta.","german":"However, that was a lie."},{"japanese":"日本語は難しいです。しかし面白いです。","romaji":"Nihongo wa muzukashii desu. Shikashi omoshiroi desu.","german":"Japanese is difficult. However, it is interesting.","furigana":"にほんご|むずか|おもしろ"}],"notes":"しかし is a conjunction meaning \"however/but\". It introduces a statement that contrasts with the preceding one. しかし、それはウソだった corrects an earlier account; 難しいです。しかし面白いです sets two evaluations against each other. It is often used in explanatory or written texts. 然し is a rare kanji spelling of しかし. The usual spelling of the conjunction is in kana.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 19"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"うちの会社は、あそこのオフィスビルに入ってるんだよ。","romaji":"Uchi no kaisha wa, asoko no ofisu biru ni haitteru n da yo.","german":"My company is in that office building over there.","furigana":"かいしゃ|はい"},{"japanese":"あのビルは高いですね。","romaji":"Ano biru wa takai desu ne.","german":"That building is tall, isn't it?","furigana":"たか"}],"notes":"ビル is a short form of ビルディング and refers to a multi-story building. オフィスビル is an office building. 会社がビルに入っている describes the company as being housed there. 入ってる is the colloquial short form of 入っている. ビル has two short vowels; ビール, by contrast, means beer.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 10"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"アップルパイのレシピは、おばあちゃんに教わるといいよ。","romaji":"Appurupai no reshipi wa, obāchan ni osowaru to ii yo.","german":"You should learn the apple pie recipe from Grandma.","furigana":"おそ"},{"japanese":"先生に日本語を教わりました。","romaji":"Sensei ni nihongo o osowarimashita.","german":"I learned Japanese from my teacher.","furigana":"せんせい|にほんご|おそ"}],"notes":"教わる is a transitive Godan verb: 教わります, 教わって. に names the person you learn from, and を the thing learned. The focus is on the learner. 教える, in contrast, describes teaching from the teacher's point of view. 教わるといいよ is friendly advice to have someone teach you something.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どうせすぐブームは去るよ。","romaji":"Dōse sugu būmu wa saru yo.","german":"The fad will be over soon anyway.","furigana":"さ"},{"japanese":"友達が去年この町を去りました。","romaji":"Tomodachi ga kyonen kono machi o sarimashita.","german":"My friend left this town last year.","furigana":"ともだち|きょねん|まち|さ"}],"notes":"去る is an intransitive Godan verb here: 去ります, 去って. In 町を去る, を marks the place that is left. ブームが去る means a trend is over. どうせ can express a resigned or dismissive expectation: \"anyway\". The reading さる has short vowels.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"明後日の予定を教えてもらえますか？","romaji":"Asatte no yotei o oshiete moraemasu ka?","german":"Could you tell me the plans for the day after tomorrow?","furigana":"あさって|よてい|おし"},{"japanese":"明後日から旅行に行きます。","romaji":"Asatte kara ryokō ni ikimasu.","german":"I'm going on a trip starting the day after tomorrow.","furigana":"あさって|りょこう|い"}],"notes":"明後日（あさって）is the day after tomorrow, counted from today. As a time expression it often stands without に; with の it can modify a noun: 明後日の予定. The more formal reading みょうごにち belongs to a separate reading entry. The fixed phrase あさっての方向, on the other hand, means a completely wrong direction.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"テストで一ヶ所だけミスをしてしまった。","romaji":"Tesuto de ikkasho dake misu o shite shimatta.","german":"I made a mistake in only one place on the test.","furigana":"いっかしょ"},{"japanese":"一ヶ所に集まってください。","romaji":"Ikkasho ni atsumatte kudasai.","german":"Please gather in one place.","furigana":"いっかしょ|あつ"}],"notes":"一ヶ所 means \"one place\" or \"one spot\". The reading is いっかしょ with a small っ; the small ヶ is read か here. 一ヶ所だけ limits something to exactly one place. 一ヶ所に集まる means that everyone comes together in one place. 一箇所 and 一カ所 are also common spellings.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"紙に直線を引いてください。","romaji":"Kami ni chokusen o hiite kudasai.","german":"Please draw a straight line on the paper.","furigana":"かみ|ちょくせん|ひ"},{"japanese":"この道は地図では直線になっています。","romaji":"Kono michi wa chizu de wa chokusen ni natte imasu.","german":"On the map, this road is shown as a straight line.","furigana":"みち|ちず|ちょくせん"}],"notes":"直線 is a straight line. 直線を引く means to draw such a line; に names the surface and で a tool used. 直線になる describes a line as straight. The sequence ちょ forms a single mora. 曲線, by contrast, means a curved line.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"車はゆっくり直進していた。","romaji":"Kuruma wa yukkuri chokushin shite ita.","german":"The car was driving straight ahead slowly.","furigana":"くるま|ちょくしん"},{"japanese":"この道を直進してください。","romaji":"Kono michi o chokushin shite kudasai.","german":"Please go straight along this road.","furigana":"みち|ちょくしん"},{"japanese":"交差点では、右折する車より直進する車が優先です。","romaji":"Kōsaten de wa, usetsu suru kuruma yori chokushin suru kuruma ga yūsen desu.","german":"At intersections, cars going straight have priority over cars turning right.","furigana":"こうさてん|うせつ|くるま|ちょくしん|くるま|ゆうせん"}],"notes":"直進 means \"to go straight ahead\" and is typical of traffic language, navigation announcements and physics (光は直進する). It is intransitive; the route you travel along takes を, as with other verbs of movement: この道を直進する. In everyday speech people often simply say まっすぐ行く; 直進 sounds more matter-of-fact. At intersections, 直進 is contrasted with 右折/左折, e.g. 直進車 (\"vehicle going straight ahead\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"父 is the humble term for one's own father when speaking about him to others. The reading is ちち. 父と話す names him as the conversation partner, 父は introduces him as the topic. For another person's father, polite everyday speech usually uses お父さん.","examples":[{"japanese":"父は東京に住んでいます。","romaji":"Chichi wa Tōkyō ni sunde imasu.","german":"My father lives in Tokyo.","furigana":"ちち|とうきょう|す"},{"japanese":"きのう父と電話で話しました。","romaji":"Kinō chichi to denwa de hanashimashita.","german":"Yesterday I talked with my father on the phone.","furigana":"ちち|でんわ|はな"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 7"},
{"notes":"母 is the humble term for one's own mother when talking with others. The reading はは has two short a's. In 母に花をあげる, she is marked with に as the recipient and the flowers with を as the gift. For another person's mother, one usually says お母さん politely.","examples":[{"japanese":"母は学校で働いています。","romaji":"Haha wa gakkō de hataraite imasu.","german":"My mother works at a school.","furigana":"はは|がっこう|はたら"},{"japanese":"母に花をあげました。","romaji":"Haha ni hana o agemashita.","german":"I gave flowers to my mother.","furigana":"はは|はな"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 7"},
{"notes":"兄 means one's own older brother when speaking about him. Age is part of the meaning; 弟 is the younger brother. 兄と映画を見る names the brother as a companion. The reading is あに. For another person's older brother, お兄さん is often used politely.","examples":[{"japanese":"兄は大学生です。","romaji":"Ani wa daigakusei desu.","german":"My older brother is a university student.","furigana":"あに|だいがくせい"},{"japanese":"日曜日に兄と映画を見ました。","romaji":"Nichiyōbi ni ani to eiga o mimashita.","german":"On Sunday I watched a movie with my older brother.","furigana":"にちようび|あに|えいが|み"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 11"},
{"notes":"姉 is the term for one's own older sister when telling others about her. 姉から names her as the source of a letter or gift. 姉は日本語を教えています describes her occupation. The reading あね contains no long vowels. A younger sister is 妹.","examples":[{"japanese":"姉は日本語を教えています。","romaji":"Ane wa nihongo o oshiete imasu.","german":"My older sister teaches Japanese.","furigana":"あね|にほんご|おし"},{"japanese":"姉から手紙が来ました。","romaji":"Ane kara tegami ga kimashita.","german":"I received a letter from my older sister.","furigana":"あね|てがみ|き"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 11"},
{"notes":"両親 bezeichnet beide Eltern zusammen. Die Lesung dieser Karte ist りょうしん mit langem o. 両親は stellt die Eltern als Thema vor; 両親に会う nennt sie mit に als Personen, die man trifft. 両親に mit einem anderen Verb kann auch eine Empfänger- oder Zielrolle haben, die das Verb bestimmt.","examples":[{"japanese":"両親はドイツに住んでいます。","romaji":"Ryōshin wa Doitsu ni sunde imasu.","german":"Meine Eltern wohnen in Deutschland.","furigana":"りょうしん|す"},{"japanese":"来月、両親に会います。","romaji":"Raigetsu, ryōshin ni aimasu.","german":"Nächsten Monat treffe ich meine Eltern.","furigana":"らいげつ|りょうしん|あ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 11"},
{"notes":"おじさん can mean an uncle or, informally, a middle-aged man. Among relatives, 伯父さん is also written for an older brother and 叔父さん for a younger brother of a parent. For a stranger, the other meaning is clear from the context. おじさん has a short i; おじいさん has a long i.","examples":[{"japanese":"私のおじさんは大阪に住んでいます。","romaji":"Watashi no ojisan wa Ōsaka ni sunde imasu.","german":"My uncle lives in Osaka.","furigana":"わたし|おおさか|す"},{"japanese":"知らないおじさんに駅までの道を聞きました。","romaji":"Shiranai ojisan ni eki made no michi o kikimashita.","german":"I asked a man I didn't know the way to the station.","furigana":"し|えき|みち|き"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 41"},
{"notes":"In the family, おばさん means \"aunt\". 伯母さん refers to an older sister and 叔母さん to a younger sister of a parent. In informal speech, おばさん can also mean a middle-aged woman. The context shows whether a relative is meant. ば has a short a; おばあさん has a long a.","examples":[{"japanese":"私のおばさんとお茶を飲みました。","romaji":"Watashi no obasan to ocha o nomimashita.","german":"I drank tea with my aunt.","furigana":"わたし|ちゃ|の"},{"japanese":"知らないおばさんに道を聞きました。","romaji":"Shiranai obasan ni michi o kikimashita.","german":"I asked a woman I didn't know the way.","furigana":"し|みち|き"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 41"},
{"notes":"おじいさん can mean a grandfather or an elderly man. 私のおじいさん makes it clear that one's own grandfather is meant. The i-sequence in じい is pronounced long; the shorter おじさん means uncle or middle-aged man. With あのおじいさん, the context decides whether a relative is meant.","examples":[{"japanese":"私のおじいさんは毎朝、散歩します。","romaji":"Watashi no ojiisan wa maiasa, sanpo shimasu.","german":"My grandfather takes a walk every morning.","furigana":"わたし|まいあさ|さんぽ"},{"japanese":"あのおじいさんはとても元気です。","romaji":"Ano ojiisan wa totemo genki desu.","german":"That old man is very energetic.","furigana":"げんき"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 13"},
{"notes":"青 ist ein Nomen für die Farbe Blau. 好きな色は青です nennt sie als Lieblingsfarbe. Bei einer Ampel wird das grüne Signal im Japanischen als 青 bezeichnet; deshalb kann 信号が青になる auf Deutsch „die Ampel wird grün“ heißen. 青い ist die zugehörige Adjektivform. あお enthält zwei getrennte kurze Vokale.","examples":[{"japanese":"好きな色は青です。","romaji":"Suki na iro wa ao desu.","german":"Meine Lieblingsfarbe ist Blau.","furigana":"す|いろ|あお"},{"japanese":"信号が青になりました。","romaji":"Shingō ga ao ni narimashita.","german":"Die Ampel ist grün geworden.","furigana":"しんごう|あお"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 34"},
{"notes":"赤 is the noun for red. Before another noun it is connected with の: 赤のペン. 赤い, on the other hand, is an adjective and stands directly before the noun. 信号は赤です states the state of a traffic light. This card uses the reading あか and the color meaning.","examples":[{"japanese":"赤のペンを使ってください。","romaji":"Aka no pen o tsukatte kudasai.","german":"Please use the red pen.","furigana":"あか|つか"},{"japanese":"信号は赤です。","romaji":"Shingō wa aka desu.","german":"The traffic light is red.","furigana":"しんごう|あか"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 34"},
{"notes":"白 is a noun for the color white. 白です can state the color as a predicate; 白のシャツ connects the noun with a piece of clothing. The adjective 白い needs no の before シャツ. The reading しろ has short vowels. The color meaning is intended here.","examples":[{"japanese":"この紙は白です。","romaji":"Kono kami wa shiro desu.","german":"This paper is white.","furigana":"かみ|しろ"},{"japanese":"白のシャツを買いました。","romaji":"Shiro no shatsu o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a white shirt.","furigana":"しろ|か"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 34"},
{"notes":"黒 is the noun for black. 好きな色は黒です states a color preference. 黒のかばん uses の between the color noun and the bag. 私のです omits the noun that was already mentioned and here means \"it's mine\". 黒い is the adjective form.","examples":[{"japanese":"好きな色は黒です。","romaji":"Suki na iro wa kuro desu.","german":"My favorite color is black.","furigana":"す|いろ|くろ"},{"japanese":"黒のかばんは私のです。","romaji":"Kuro no kaban wa watashi no desu.","german":"The black bag is mine.","furigana":"くろ|わたし"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 34"},
{"notes":"黄色い is an i-adjective. It stands directly before the noun it describes: 黄色い花, 黄色いかばん. As a predicate you can use 黄色いです; the negative form is 黄色くないです. The color noun is 黄色. The sequence きい contains a long i.","examples":[{"japanese":"黄色い花が咲いています。","romaji":"Kiiroi hana ga saite imasu.","german":"Yellow flowers are blooming.","furigana":"きいろ|はな|さ"},{"japanese":"妹は黄色いかばんを持っています。","romaji":"Imōto wa kiiroi kaban o motte imasu.","german":"My younger sister has a yellow bag.","furigana":"いもうと|きいろ|も"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"池 is a pond. 池があります states its existence; に marks the place. 池のそば refers to the area right by the pond. The reading is いけ. Bodies of water take あります, while fish in the water are expressed with います.","examples":[{"japanese":"公園に小さな池があります。","romaji":"Kōen ni chīsana ike ga arimasu.","german":"There is a small pond in the park.","furigana":"こうえん|ちい|いけ"},{"japanese":"池のそばでお弁当を食べました。","romaji":"Ike no soba de obentō o tabemashita.","german":"I ate my packed lunch by the pond.","furigana":"いけ|べんとう|た"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 30"},
{"notes":"北 here is the noun for north and is read きた. 日本の北に gives the location in the north of Japan. 北へ names a direction of movement; the particle へ is pronounced e. 北という方角 can be used without a specific place name. In compounds, 北 is often read ほく.","examples":[{"japanese":"北海道は日本の北にあります。","romaji":"Hokkaidō wa Nihon no kita ni arimasu.","german":"Hokkaido is in the north of Japan.","furigana":"ほっかいどう|にほん|きた"},{"japanese":"駅から北へ歩いてください。","romaji":"Eki kara kita e aruite kudasai.","german":"Please walk north from the station.","furigana":"えき|きた|ある"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 32"},
{"notes":"南 is the noun for south. The reading on this card is みなみ. 日本の南に describes the location in the south of Japan. 南を向いている describes something as facing south. With 南へ you can name a direction of movement; the verb that follows determines the sentence structure.","examples":[{"japanese":"沖縄は日本の南にあります。","romaji":"Okinawa wa Nihon no minami ni arimasu.","german":"Okinawa is in the south of Japan.","furigana":"おきなわ|にほん|みなみ"},{"japanese":"部屋の窓は南を向いています。","romaji":"Heya no mado wa minami o muite imasu.","german":"The window of the room faces south.","furigana":"へや|まど|みなみ|む"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 32"},
{"notes":"東 is the noun for east and is read ひがし here. 東から出る names the east as the starting direction. 駅の東に describes a location east of the station. In compounds, 東 can also be read とう, as in 東京; the reading on this card remains ひがし.","examples":[{"japanese":"太陽は東から出ます。","romaji":"Taiyō wa higashi kara demasu.","german":"The sun rises in the east.","furigana":"たいよう|ひがし|で"},{"japanese":"学校は駅の東にあります。","romaji":"Gakkō wa eki no higashi ni arimasu.","german":"The school is east of the station.","furigana":"がっこう|えき|ひがし"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 32"},
{"notes":"西 is the noun for west. 西に沈む describes the sun setting in the west. 西へ names the direction of movement; へ is pronounced e. 十分 as a length of time is read じゅっぷん here, i.e. ten minutes. The reading にし contains short vowels.","examples":[{"japanese":"太陽は西に沈みます。","romaji":"Taiyō wa nishi ni shizumimasu.","german":"The sun sets in the west.","furigana":"たいよう|にし|しず"},{"japanese":"駅から西へ十分歩きます。","romaji":"Eki kara nishi e juppun arukimasu.","german":"It's a ten-minute walk west from the station.","furigana":"えき|にし|じゅっぷんある"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 29"},
{"notes":"On this card, 後 is read あと. For the time meaning, a noun is connected with の後: ご飯の後 \"after the meal\". 後で can mean \"later\" without an explicitly stated reference point. Spatially, 後 can mean the area behind someone: 私の後について来る means to follow me, walking behind me. あと contains two short vowels.","examples":[{"japanese":"ご飯の後で薬を飲みます。","romaji":"Gohan no ato de kusuri o nomimasu.","german":"I take medicine after the meal.","furigana":"はん|あと|くすり|の"},{"japanese":"後で電話します。","romaji":"Ato de denwa shimasu.","german":"I'll call you later.","furigana":"あと|でんわ"},{"japanese":"私の後について来てください。","romaji":"Watashi no ato ni tsuite kite kudasai.","german":"Please follow me.","furigana":"わたし|あと|き"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 11"},
{"notes":"先 is read さき here. 先に means \"first\" or \"before the others\" in terms of time. この道の先に describes, spatially, a point further ahead on this road. 帰ってもいいですか asks for permission. The rest of the sentence shows whether 先 is meant in terms of time or space.","examples":[{"japanese":"先に帰ってもいいですか。","romaji":"Saki ni kaette mo ii desu ka.","german":"May I go home before the others?","furigana":"さき|かえ"},{"japanese":"この道の先に店があります。","romaji":"Kono michi no saki ni mise ga arimasu.","german":"There is a shop further down this road.","furigana":"みち|さき|みせ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"次 is a noun for the next one in a sequence. Before another noun it is connected with の: 次の電車. 次は makes the next step or the next person the topic. 次に can mean \"next\". The reading is つぎ, with short vowels.","examples":[{"japanese":"次の電車は何時ですか。","romaji":"Tsugi no densha wa nanji desu ka.","german":"What time is the next train?","furigana":"つぎ|でんしゃ|なんじ"},{"japanese":"次は私が読みます。","romaji":"Tsugi wa watashi ga yomimasu.","german":"I'll read next.","furigana":"つぎ|わたし|よ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 6"},
{"notes":"今晩 means \"this evening\" or \"tonight\". It refers to the evening of the day on which it is spoken. As a time expression it can stand without a particle; 今晩は makes the evening the topic, with は read wa. 今晩、電話してもいいですか asks permission for a call this evening.","examples":[{"japanese":"今晩は家で食べます。","romaji":"Konban wa ie de tabemasu.","german":"I'm eating at home this evening.","furigana":"こんばん|いえ|た"},{"japanese":"今晩、電話してもいいですか。","romaji":"Konban, denwa shite mo ii desu ka.","german":"May I call you this evening?","furigana":"こんばん|でんわ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 4"},
{"notes":"夕方 refers to late afternoon through early evening, roughly the time around dusk. It does not name a fixed time of day. 夕方までに means \"by early evening\" and sets a latest point in time. The reading ゆうがた has a long u; 方 is read がた here.","examples":[{"japanese":"夕方、犬と散歩します。","romaji":"Yūgata, inu to sanpo shimasu.","german":"In the early evening I go for a walk with my dog.","furigana":"ゆうがた|いぬ|さんぽ"},{"japanese":"夕方までに帰ります。","romaji":"Yūgata made ni kaerimasu.","german":"I'll be back by early evening.","furigana":"ゆうがた|かえ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 32"},
{"notes":"夕飯 is the evening meal. The reading on this card is ゆうはん, with a long u. 夕飯は七時です gives the planned mealtime. 夕飯を作る names dinner as something that is prepared. 作りませんか is a polite invitation to cook together.","examples":[{"japanese":"夕飯は七時です。","romaji":"Yūhan wa shichiji desu.","german":"Dinner is at seven.","furigana":"ゆうはん|しちじ"},{"japanese":"一緒に夕飯を作りませんか。","romaji":"Issho ni yūhan o tsukurimasen ka.","german":"Shall we cook dinner together?","furigana":"いっしょ|ゆうはん|つく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"晩 refers to the evening or the night. 朝から晩まで covers the span from morning to evening. その晩 refers to an evening that has already been mentioned. With a time expression and は, that evening can become the topic. The reading ばん contains the moraic n.","examples":[{"japanese":"朝から晩まで雨でした。","romaji":"Asa kara ban made ame deshita.","german":"It rained from morning till night.","furigana":"あさ|ばん|あめ"},{"japanese":"その晩は早く寝ました。","romaji":"Sono ban wa hayaku nemashita.","german":"That evening I went to bed early.","furigana":"ばん|はや|ね"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"時 is read とき here and names a point in time or an occasion. After a noun, の comes before 時: 子供の時. A na-adjective takes な before it: 暇な時. With a verb, the plain form is used, e.g. 出かける時. The whole expression states when something happens.","examples":[{"japanese":"子供の時、東京に住んでいました。","romaji":"Kodomo no toki, Tōkyō ni sunde imashita.","german":"When I was a child, I lived in Tokyo.","furigana":"こども|とき|とうきょう|す"},{"japanese":"暇な時、本を読みます。","romaji":"Hima na toki, hon o yomimasu.","german":"When I have free time, I read books.","furigana":"ひま|とき|ほん|よ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 4"},
{"notes":"話 is a noun for a conversation, a speech or a story. 話を聞く means to listen to what is said; 先生の話 names the teacher as the source. The reading is はなし. The verb 話す \"to speak/to tell\", on the other hand, has a す; the spelling on this noun card ends with the kanji.","examples":[{"japanese":"先生の話を聞いてください。","romaji":"Sensei no hanashi o kiite kudasai.","german":"Please listen to what the teacher says.","furigana":"せんせい|はなし|き"},{"japanese":"その話はおもしろいです。","romaji":"Sono hanashi wa omoshiroi desu.","german":"That story is interesting.","furigana":"はなし"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 21"},
{"notes":"暇 can mean \"free time\" as a noun and \"not busy\" as a na-adjective. 暇です describes that someone has time. Before a noun, な is used: 暇な時. With 暇がある, free time is named as time that is available. The reading ひま has two short vowels.","examples":[{"japanese":"日曜日は暇です。","romaji":"Nichiyōbi wa hima desu.","german":"I'm free on Sunday.","furigana":"にちようび|ひま"},{"japanese":"暇な時に来てください。","romaji":"Hima na toki ni kite kudasai.","german":"Please come when you have time.","furigana":"ひま|とき|き"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 25"},
{"notes":"物 is a general noun for things or objects. An adjective can stand directly before it: 重い物, 古い物. With を you name objects at which an action is directed; with が you can describe their presence. The reading on this card is もの. In this usage, 物 is not used for people.","examples":[{"japanese":"重い物を持たないでください。","romaji":"Omoi mono o motanaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't carry heavy things.","furigana":"おも|もの|も"},{"japanese":"この箱には古い物が入っています。","romaji":"Kono hako ni wa furui mono ga haitte imasu.","german":"There are old things in this box.","furigana":"はこ|ふる|もの|はい"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 10"},
{"notes":"所 here is a place or spot and is read ところ. 静かな所 connects a na-adjective with the place; 所で gives the place of an action. どんな所 asks what kind of place something is. The spelling ところ in kana is also common, especially in grammatical expressions.","examples":[{"japanese":"静かな所で勉強したいです。","romaji":"Shizuka na tokoro de benkyō shitai desu.","german":"I want to study in a quiet place.","furigana":"しず|ところ|べんきょう"},{"japanese":"ここはどんな所ですか。","romaji":"Koko wa donna tokoro desu ka.","german":"What kind of place is this?","furigana":"ところ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 8"},
{"notes":"In these examples, 辺 means \"area/vicinity\". この辺 refers to the area here; あの辺 to an area over there. With に you ask about something that exists there. With で you name the place of an activity. The reading is へん; the word can mean an approximate area rather than an exact point.","examples":[{"japanese":"この辺に銀行はありますか。","romaji":"Kono hen ni ginkō wa arimasu ka.","german":"Is there a bank around here?","furigana":"へん|ぎんこう"},{"japanese":"この辺で少し休みましょう。","romaji":"Kono hen de sukoshi yasumimashō.","german":"Let's take a short break around here.","furigana":"へん|すこ|やす"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 29"},
{"notes":"横 can refer to the side, the place next to something, or a horizontal direction. 銀行の横に means \"next to the bank\". 紙を横に置く describes laying the paper sideways. 箱の横の面 names the side surface of a box. With の you connect 横 with its reference point. The reading よこ has short vowels.","examples":[{"japanese":"銀行の横に本屋があります。","romaji":"Ginkō no yoko ni hon’ya ga arimasu.","german":"There is a bookstore next to the bank.","furigana":"ぎんこう|よこ|ほんや"},{"japanese":"紙を横に置いてください。","romaji":"Kami o yoko ni oite kudasai.","german":"Please lay the paper sideways.","furigana":"かみ|よこ|お"},{"japanese":"箱の横の面に名前を書いてください。","romaji":"Hako no yoko no men ni namae o kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write your name on the side of the box.","furigana":"はこ|よこ|めん|なまえ|か"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 26"},
{"notes":"村 is a village. The reading on this card is むら. 村に住む names the village as the place of residence; この村には makes it the place and topic of a further statement. 駅がありません means that there is no station there. With 小さな you describe a small village.","examples":[{"japanese":"祖母は小さな村に住んでいます。","romaji":"Sobo wa chīsana mura ni sunde imasu.","german":"My grandmother lives in a small village.","furigana":"そぼ|ちい|むら|す"},{"japanese":"この村には駅がありません。","romaji":"Kono mura ni wa eki ga arimasen.","german":"This village has no train station.","furigana":"むら|えき"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 31"},
{"notes":"門 is a gate, e.g. the entrance of a school ground. 門の前 names the area in front of the gate; 門を閉める names it as the thing that is closed. 閉めます is a transitive verb, because someone closes the gate. もん ends in a moraic n.","examples":[{"japanese":"学校の門の前で待っています。","romaji":"Gakkō no mon no mae de matte imasu.","german":"I'm waiting in front of the school gate.","furigana":"がっこう|もん|まえ|ま"},{"japanese":"夜は門を閉めます。","romaji":"Yoru wa mon o shimemasu.","german":"We close the gate at night.","furigana":"よる|もん|し"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"箱 is a box. 箱の中に describes the interior as a place. 箱を開ける names the box as the object of opening; 開けてください is a polite request. The exact size depends on the context. はこ has short vowels.","examples":[{"japanese":"箱の中に本があります。","romaji":"Hako no naka ni hon ga arimasu.","german":"There are books in the box.","furigana":"はこ|なか|ほん"},{"japanese":"この箱を開けてください。","romaji":"Kono hako o akete kudasai.","german":"Please open this box.","furigana":"はこ|あ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 10"},
{"notes":"上着 is a jacket or a garment for the upper body. To put it on, 着る is used: 上着を着ます. 上着を椅子に掛ける marks the jacket with を and the chair with に. The reading is うわぎ; in this compound 着 is read ぎ.","examples":[{"japanese":"寒いので上着を着ます。","romaji":"Samui node uwagi o kimasu.","german":"It's cold, so I'll put on a jacket.","furigana":"さむ|うわぎ|き"},{"japanese":"上着を椅子に掛けました。","romaji":"Uwagi o isu ni kakemashita.","german":"I hung my jacket over the chair.","furigana":"うわぎ|いす|か"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 17"},
{"notes":"カレンダー is a calendar. カレンダーがあります names it as an existing object; カレンダーに書く uses に for the surface or place of the entry. 休みの日 refers to days off. The stroke ー lengthens the last a: karendā.","examples":[{"japanese":"壁にカレンダーがあります。","romaji":"Kabe ni karendā ga arimasu.","german":"There is a calendar on the wall.","furigana":"かべ"},{"japanese":"カレンダーに休みの日を書きます。","romaji":"Karendā ni yasumi no hi o kakimasu.","german":"I write the days off on the calendar.","furigana":"やす|ひ|か"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 30"},
{"notes":"シャワー means a shower. シャワーを浴びる is the expression for \"to take a shower\"; 浴びる is an ichidan verb: 浴びます, 浴びて. シャワーがあります names an existing shower. シャ is pronounced as a single sequence, and ー lengthens the final a.","examples":[{"japanese":"朝、シャワーを浴びます。","romaji":"Asa, shawā o abimasu.","german":"I take a shower in the morning.","furigana":"あさ|あ"},{"japanese":"この部屋にはシャワーがあります。","romaji":"Kono heya ni wa shawā ga arimasu.","german":"This room has a shower.","furigana":"へや"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 16"},
{"notes":"テーブル is a table. テーブルに置く names it as the destination of placing; テーブルの下 refers to the space beneath it. テーブルの上に explicitly names the tabletop as the place. The first syllable has a long e because of ー: tēburu.","examples":[{"japanese":"テーブルにお皿を置きます。","romaji":"Tēburu ni osara o okimasu.","german":"I put plates on the table.","furigana":"さら|お"},{"japanese":"テーブルの下に猫がいます。","romaji":"Tēburu no shita ni neko ga imasu.","german":"There is a cat under the table.","furigana":"した|ねこ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 10"},
{"notes":"ボールペン is a ballpoint pen. ボールペンで書く names the writing tool with で. ボールペンを買う names it as the object bought. 青い describes the color of the item. ー lengthens the o; at the end is a moraic n: bōrupen.","examples":[{"japanese":"ボールペンで名前を書いてください。","romaji":"Bōrupen de namae o kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write your name with a ballpoint pen.","furigana":"なまえ|か"},{"japanese":"青いボールペンを買いました。","romaji":"Aoi bōrupen o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a blue ballpoint pen.","furigana":"あお|か"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 2"},
{"notes":"カップ here means a cup or mug. カップに入れる names the vessel with に and the contents with を. カップは小さいです describes the size of the vessel. The small ッ is written as a double p: kappu. コーヒー, by contrast, has long vowels.","examples":[{"japanese":"カップにコーヒーを入れます。","romaji":"Kappu ni kōhī o iremasu.","german":"I pour coffee into the cup.","furigana":"い"},{"japanese":"このカップは小さいです。","romaji":"Kono kappu wa chīsai desu.","german":"This cup is small.","furigana":"ちい"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 42"},
{"notes":"カレー refers to curry or a curry dish and can also be short for カレーライス. 昼ご飯にカレーを食べる names curry as the lunch. あまり辛くない describes the dish as not particularly spicy. あまり is used here with a negative form. ー lengthens the e in karē.","examples":[{"japanese":"昼ご飯にカレーを食べました。","romaji":"Hirugohan ni karē o tabemashita.","german":"I ate curry for lunch.","furigana":"ひる|はん|た"},{"japanese":"このカレーはあまり辛くないです。","romaji":"Kono karē wa amari karakunai desu.","german":"This curry is not very spicy.","furigana":"から"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 13"},
{"notes":"シャツ here means a shirt as an upper garment. To wear it, you say シャツを着る. 着ています describes the state of having it on. シャツを洗う names the garment as the object being washed. シャ is pronounced together as sha.","examples":[{"japanese":"新しいシャツを着ています。","romaji":"Atarashii shatsu o kite imasu.","german":"I'm wearing a new shirt.","furigana":"あたら|き"},{"japanese":"このシャツを洗ってください。","romaji":"Kono shatsu o aratte kudasai.","german":"Please wash this shirt.","furigana":"あら"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 7"},
{"notes":"スカート is a skirt. 青いスカート connects the color adjective directly with the garment. スカートは長いです judges its length. With the degree word 少し the statement becomes \"a little long\". The stroke ー lengthens the a: sukāto.","examples":[{"japanese":"青いスカートを買いました。","romaji":"Aoi sukāto o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a blue skirt.","furigana":"あお|か"},{"japanese":"このスカートは少し長いです。","romaji":"Kono sukāto wa sukoshi nagai desu.","german":"This skirt is a little long.","furigana":"すこ|なが"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 18"},
{"notes":"ズボン means trousers and is usually written in katakana. For putting on or wearing them, 履く is used: 履きます, 履いて. 大きすぎます means \"too big\"; before すぎる, the final い of the i-adjective 大きい drops. ズボン has short vowels and a moraic n at the end.","examples":[{"japanese":"黒いズボンを履いています。","romaji":"Kuroi zubon o haite imasu.","german":"I'm wearing black trousers.","furigana":"くろ|は"},{"japanese":"このズボンは大きすぎます。","romaji":"Kono zubon wa ōkisugimasu.","german":"These trousers are too big.","furigana":"おお"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 40"},
{"notes":"スリッパ are light house shoes, i.e. slippers. Footwear is put on with 履く; スリッパを履く names them as the object with を. トイレのスリッパ refers to the slippers intended for the toilet. The second sentence asks to put this pair in a particular place. The small ッ briefly stops the airflow before p: surippa, not suripa.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"玄関でスリッパを履きます。","romaji":"Genkan de surippa o hakimasu.","german":"I put on slippers in the entrance area.","furigana":"げんかん|は"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"トイレのスリッパはここに置いてください。","romaji":"Toire no surippa wa koko ni oite kudasai.","german":"Please put the toilet slippers here.","furigana":"お"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"ストーブ here is a heater, i.e. a room heating device. For an appliance, つける means \"to turn on\" and 消す accordingly \"to turn off\". 寒いので gives the reason for turning it on; 寝る前に fixes the time for turning it off as before going to sleep. In these examples the word means the room heater. A kitchen stove would be called something like コンロ in another context.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"寒いのでストーブをつけました。","romaji":"Samui node sutōbu o tsukemashita.","german":"It's cold, so I turned on the heater.","furigana":"さむ"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"寝る前にストーブを消してください。","romaji":"Neru mae ni sutōbu o keshite kudasai.","german":"Please turn off the heater before going to bed.","furigana":"ね|まえ|け"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 17"},
{"notes":"ポケット is a pocket in or on a garment. ポケットにある names the location of an object, whereas ポケットから出す names taking it out of the pocket. に and から therefore express different spatial relations. For a carried handbag or shopping bag, かばん or バッグ fits better. The small ッ before t belongs to the pronunciation poketto.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"鍵はポケットにあります。","romaji":"Kagi wa poketto ni arimasu.","german":"The key is in my pocket.","furigana":"かぎ"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"ポケットからお金を出しました。","romaji":"Poketto kara okane o dashimashita.","german":"I took money out of my pocket.","furigana":"かね|だ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 27"},
{"notes":"ポスト here is a mailbox for posting mail. 手紙をポストに入れる names the letter with を and the destination with に. 駅の前にポストがある, by contrast, describes where a mailbox stands. The noun has other meanings, such as a professional position; the reference to a letter and to the red box makes the mail meaning clear here.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"手紙をポストに入れます。","romaji":"Tegami o posuto ni iremasu.","german":"I put the letter in the mailbox.","furigana":"てがみ|い"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"駅の前に赤いポストがあります。","romaji":"Eki no mae ni akai posuto ga arimasu.","german":"There is a red postbox in front of the station.","furigana":"えき|まえ|あか"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 10"},
{"notes":"ペット is an animal kept as a pet. ペットを飼う uses を for the animal and 飼う for keeping animals; 飼えます is the potential form. In connection with a house, it can express that keeping pets is allowed there. 私のペットは犬です, by contrast, identifies one's own animal. The word meant here is the animal word borrowed from English, not the identically sounding abbreviation PET for plastic.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"この家ではペットを飼えます。","romaji":"Kono ie de wa petto o kaemasu.","german":"You can keep pets in this house.","furigana":"いえ|か"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"私のペットは小さな犬です。","romaji":"Watashi no petto wa chiisana inu desu.","german":"My pet is a small dog.","furigana":"わたし|ちい|いぬ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 27"},
{"notes":"八百屋 is a vegetable and fruit shop or the person who runs it. The reading やおや is learned as a whole; in this word, 八百 is not pronounced as the number はっぴゃく. 八百屋で names the place of shopping, 八百屋がある the existence of a shop. The examples deal with the actual shop and not with a figurative term for someone who does many different things.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"八百屋で野菜を買います。","romaji":"Yaoya de yasai o kaimasu.","german":"I buy vegetables at the greengrocer.","furigana":"やおや|やさい|か"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"駅の近くに八百屋があります。","romaji":"Eki no chikaku ni yaoya ga arimasu.","german":"There is a greengrocer near the station.","furigana":"えき|ちか|やおや"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"大きな stands directly before a noun. With 犬 it describes physical size, with 声 loudness: 大きな声で means \"in a loud voice\". It is a fixed prenominal form and does not form a predicate such as 大きなです. For a stand-alone statement, 大きいです is used. The な belongs to the whole form 大きな here; no further な is added.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"大きな犬がいます。","romaji":"Ōkina inu ga imasu.","german":"There's a big dog.","furigana":"おお|いぬ"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"大きな声で読んでください。","romaji":"Ōkina koe de yonde kudasai.","german":"Please read in a loud voice.","furigana":"おお|こえ|よ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 38"},
{"notes":"小さな is a fixed prenominal form that stands directly before a noun: 小さな店 or 小さな花. A stand-alone statement is formed with 小さいです instead. The word can also express little importance or a small extent, as in 小さな間違い. In the extra example, でも means \"even\" and shows that even a small mistake gets corrected. No additional な is inserted after 小さな.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"小さな店でパンを買いました。","romaji":"Chiisana mise de pan o kaimashita.","german":"I bought bread at a small shop.","furigana":"ちい|みせ|か"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"庭に小さな花が咲いています。","romaji":"Niwa ni chiisana hana ga saite imasu.","german":"Small flowers are blooming in the garden.","furigana":"にわ|ちい|はな|さ"},{"japanese":"小さな間違いでも、すぐに直します。","romaji":"Chiisana machigai de mo, sugu ni naoshimasu.","german":"I'll fix even small mistakes right away.","furigana":"ちい|まちが|なお"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 38"},
{"notes":"嫌 can express one's own dislike or an unpleasant quality. 雨の日は嫌です means that one doesn't like rainy days; 嫌なこと are unpleasant things. Before a noun, as a na-adjective, it takes な; at the end of a sentence, です can follow. 嫌です said on its own can sound quite clearly like a refusal. The examples thus contrast a personal dislike with the description of unpleasant experiences.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"雨の日は嫌です。","romaji":"Ame no hi wa iya desu.","german":"I don't like rainy days.","furigana":"あめ|ひ|いや"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"嫌なことは忘れましょう。","romaji":"Iya na koto wa wasuremashō.","german":"Let's forget the unpleasant things.","furigana":"いや|わす"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"ぬるい describes a lukewarm temperature, for example of tea or bath water. It can imply that more warmth was expected. As an i-adjective, it forms ぬるく before なる: ぬるくなった means \"has become lukewarm\". The first example describes an existing temperature, the second a change. Laxity or an overly lenient attitude is a different usage and is not practiced here.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"このお茶はぬるいです。","romaji":"Kono ocha wa nurui desu.","german":"This tea is lukewarm.","furigana":"ちゃ"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"お風呂のお湯がぬるくなりました。","romaji":"Ofuro no oyu ga nuruku narimashita.","german":"The bath water has become lukewarm.","furigana":"ふろ|ゆ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"もう一度 asks for or describes another performance of the same action. With 言ってください it is a request for repetition, with 見たいです the wish to watch a movie one has already seen again. もう is pronounced long here and together with 一度 means \"once more\". It does not refer to an additional time of day, and on its own it says nothing about how often the action is repeated in total.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"もう一度言ってください。","romaji":"Mō ichido itte kudasai.","german":"Please say it once more.","furigana":"いちどい"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"あの映画をもう一度見たいです。","romaji":"Ano eiga o mō ichido mitai desu.","german":"I want to see that movie once more.","furigana":"えいが|いちどみ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"どうぞ can accompany an invitation, a permission, or the friendly handing over of something. Before 座ってください it turns the request into an invitation to take a seat. このお菓子をどうぞ offers sweets, even without an explicit verb. In the extra example, it agrees to a request to use a pen. It therefore means \"please\", \"here you are\" or \"go ahead\", depending on the situation.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"どうぞ、座ってください。","romaji":"Dōzo, suwatte kudasai.","german":"Please, have a seat.","furigana":"すわ"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"このお菓子をどうぞ。","romaji":"Kono okashi o dōzo.","german":"Please have some of these sweets.","furigana":"かし"},{"japanese":"「このペンを使ってもいいですか。」「どうぞ。」","romaji":"“Kono pen o tsukatte mo ii desu ka.” “Dōzo.”","german":"\"May I use this pen?\" \"Go ahead.\"","furigana":"つか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"どうも can strengthen thanks or be used on its own as a brief thank-you. どうもありがとうございます is fuller and more polite than the brief いつもどうも. In another context, どうも means \"somehow\" and expresses an impression that is hard to explain exactly. So the translation \"thanks\" does not fit everywhere. The examples distinguish explicit thanks from the subjective impression of a hard-to-understand explanation.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"どうもありがとうございます。","romaji":"Dōmo arigatō gozaimasu.","german":"Thank you very much."},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"いつもどうも。","romaji":"Itsumo dōmo.","german":"Thanks, as always."},{"japanese":"どうもこの説明は分かりにくいです。","romaji":"Dōmo kono setsumei wa wakarinikui desu.","german":"Somehow this explanation is hard to understand.","furigana":"せつめい|わ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 2"},
{"notes":"ええ is an affirmative \"yes\" in conversation. It can confirm a statement or introduce the answer to a question. In casual conversation it often sounds softer than a curt はい; in formal procedures, はい is the natural answer. The reading has a long e sound. What is meant here is agreement, not a hesitant えー or the regionally used adjective ええ meaning \"good\".","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"ええ、そうです。","romaji":"Ē, sō desu.","german":"Yes, that's right."},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"ええ、明日は休みです。","romaji":"Ē, ashita wa yasumi desu.","german":"Yes, I have tomorrow off.","furigana":"あした|やす"},{"japanese":"「この席は空いていますか。」「ええ、どうぞ。」","romaji":"“Kono seki wa aite imasu ka.” “Ē, dōzo.”","german":"\"Is this seat free?\" \"Yes, go ahead.\"","furigana":"せき|あ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 6"},
{"notes":"さあ can prompt action or introduce an uncertain answer. さあ、始めましょう invites everyone to begin together; さあ、よく分かりません can be rendered as \"Hmm, I don't really know\". The speaking situation and intonation distinguish these functions. As an introductory exclamation, the word is not inflected like an ordinary adjective or verb.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"さあ、始めましょう。","romaji":"Sā, hajimemashō.","german":"Well then, let's begin.","furigana":"はじ"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"さあ、よく分かりません。","romaji":"Sā, yoku wakarimasen.","german":"Hmm, I don't really know.","furigana":"わ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 14"},
{"notes":"こんな stands before a noun and refers to this kind of thing. A speaker can use it to refer to a book being shown or to a place just experienced. こんな本 is therefore \"a book like this\", not necessarily exactly this one book. こんな所に can additionally express surprise at the place. The word already stands as a complete form before the noun; no further な is inserted.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"こんな本を探しています。","romaji":"Konna hon o sagashite imasu.","german":"I'm looking for a book like this.","furigana":"ほん|さが"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"こんな所に店があるんですね。","romaji":"Konna tokoro ni mise ga aru n desu ne.","german":"So there's a shop in a place like this.","furigana":"ところ|みせ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 26"},
{"notes":"どんな asks before a noun about its kind or characteristics: どんな音楽 or どんな仕事. It does not simply pick a particular item from a series, as どの can. With でも a different function arises: どんな仕事でも means \"for any job, no matter what kind\". The extra example makes this all-encompassing statement clear and is not a question.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"どんな音楽が好きですか。","romaji":"Donna ongaku ga suki desu ka.","german":"What kind of music do you like?","furigana":"おんがく|す"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"どんな仕事をしていますか。","romaji":"Donna shigoto o shite imasu ka.","german":"What kind of work do you do?","furigana":"しごと"},{"japanese":"どんな仕事でも、最後まで頑張ります。","romaji":"Donna shigoto de mo, saigo made ganbarimasu.","german":"Whatever the job, I do my best until the end.","furigana":"しごと|さいご|がんば"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 8"},
{"notes":"あまり is used here with a negative form and weakens a quantity or a degree. あまり食べません means \"don't eat much\", あまり寒くない \"not particularly cold\". In these sentences it does not mean that nothing at all is eaten or that there is no cold at all. The negation can stand with the verb or be formed with くない for an i-adjective.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"肉はあまり食べません。","romaji":"Niku wa amari tabemasen.","german":"I don't eat much meat.","furigana":"にく|た"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"今日はあまり寒くないです。","romaji":"Kyō wa amari samukunai desu.","german":"It's not very cold today.","furigana":"きょう|さむ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 8"},
{"notes":"いい describes something good or pleasant and is also used for permission. いい天気 praises the weather; ～てもいいですか asks whether an action is permitted. The usual inflected forms come from よい: よくない and よかった. They are not formed simply by adding くない or かった to いい. The second example is about permission to sit, not about the quality of sitting.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"今日はいい天気です。","romaji":"Kyō wa ii tenki desu.","german":"The weather is nice today.","furigana":"きょう|てんき"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"ここに座ってもいいですか。","romaji":"Koko ni suwatte mo ii desu ka.","german":"May I sit here?","furigana":"すわ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 3"},
{"notes":"じゃあ is the spoken form of では and introduces a next step in the conversation. じゃあ、また明日 prepares for a farewell; じゃあ、これをください shows the decision after a choice. It therefore means \"well then\", \"in that case\" or \"okay then\", depending on the context. In more formal speech, では is often more suitable. The long a sound belongs to the form じゃあ used here.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"じゃあ、また明日。","romaji":"Jā, mata ashita.","german":"Well then, see you tomorrow.","furigana":"あした"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"じゃあ、これをください。","romaji":"Jā, kore o kudasai.","german":"In that case, I'll take this one, please."}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 2"},
{"notes":"では here introduces a new step in the conversation or a farewell. では、始めます announces the start; では、また来週 prepares for the end of the meeting. In such transitions it usually sounds a little more formal than じゃあ. The written は is pronounced wa in this combination. What is meant here is the conversational transition, not the identically written combination of a place expression with で and the topic marker は.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"では、始めます。","romaji":"Dewa, hajimemasu.","german":"Well then, let's begin.","furigana":"はじ"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"では、また来週。","romaji":"Dewa, mata raishū.","german":"Well then, see you next week.","furigana":"らいしゅう"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 22"},
{"notes":"それから connects a temporal sequence or supplements a list. After the breakfast sentence it means \"after that\" and places the trip to school in time. In an order, it can add a further desired item, i.e. \"and also\". It picks up the previous content of the conversation. It should therefore be translated according to the context and not rendered in every sentence with a bare \"from there\".","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"朝ご飯を食べます。それから学校に行きます。","romaji":"Asagohan o tabemasu. Sorekara gakkō ni ikimasu.","german":"I eat breakfast. After that I go to school.","furigana":"あさ|はん|た|がっこう|い"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"パンと、それから牛乳をください。","romaji":"Pan to, sorekara gyūnyū o kudasai.","german":"Bread, and also milk, please.","furigana":"ぎゅうにゅう"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 6"},
{"notes":"そして connects additional statements or successive actions. Two qualities of the same room are joined as \"and\"; for reading and then sleeping, \"and then\" fits. The word stands as a link between the statements and is not inflected like a verb. In the second example, the temporal order follows from the completed actions and their context.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"この部屋は広いです。そして明るいです。","romaji":"Kono heya wa hiroi desu. Soshite akarui desu.","german":"This room is spacious. And it's bright.","furigana":"へや|ひろ|あか"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"本を読みました。そして寝ました。","romaji":"Hon o yomimashita. Soshite nemashita.","german":"I read a book and then went to sleep.","furigana":"ほん|よ|ね"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 8"},
{"notes":"それでは picks up what has been discussed so far and leads to the next step in the conversation. It can introduce a polite farewell or formulate an agreement that follows from it. After a stated situation, it can also be translated as \"in that case\": if the shop is closed today, a visit tomorrow is proposed. 失礼します is a polite farewell in the first example. The は is pronounced wa here; the shorter spoken form is それじゃあ.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"それでは、失礼します。","romaji":"Soredewa, shitsurei shimasu.","german":"Well then, I'll take my leave.","furigana":"しつれい"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"それでは、駅で会いましょう。","romaji":"Soredewa, eki de aimashō.","german":"Then let's meet at the station.","furigana":"えき|あ"},{"japanese":"「今日は店が休みです。」「それでは、明日行きましょう。」","romaji":"“Kyō wa mise ga yasumi desu.” “Soredewa, ashita ikimashō.”","german":"\"The shop is closed today.\" \"In that case, let's go tomorrow.\"","furigana":"きょう|みせ|やす|あしたい"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 49"},
{"notes":"どうして here asks for a reason or motivation. The question about the absence calls for an explanation of a past event; the question about studying Japanese can ask about the personal goal. In a sentence with どうして～のか, the why-question is embedded, for example as the subject of an explanation. The word can also ask about a method; the examples specifically practice its \"why\" usage.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"どうして学校を休みましたか。","romaji":"Dōshite gakkō o yasumimashita ka.","german":"Why were you absent from school?","furigana":"がっこう|やす"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"どうして日本語を勉強していますか。","romaji":"Dōshite nihongo o benkyō shite imasu ka.","german":"Why are you studying Japanese?","furigana":"にほんご|べんきょう"},{"japanese":"どうして遅れたのか、説明してください。","romaji":"Dōshite okureta no ka, setsumei shite kudasai.","german":"Please explain why you were late.","furigana":"おく|せつめい"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 9"},
{"notes":"どなた is the polite question about a person and corresponds to 誰. It stands as a question word itself: どなたですか asks about identity, どなたと names the companion with と. 方 and the respectful verb form いらっしゃる fit the polite tone of the examples. The word does not ask about an object; 何 would be used for that. The politeness concerns the person addressed or talked about.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"あの方はどなたですか。","romaji":"Ano kata wa donata desu ka.","german":"Who is that person?","furigana":"かた"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"どなたといらっしゃいましたか。","romaji":"Donata to irasshaimashita ka.","german":"Who did you come with?"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"どちら asks about a direction or place, about a choice, and also politely about a person. お茶とコーヒーはどちらがいいですか contrasts two possibilities. お国はどちらですか politely asks about the country of origin. どちら様ですか asks about a person's identity; 様 makes the question additionally respectful. For more than two items, どれ is often used for the choice.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"お茶とコーヒーはどちらがいいですか。","romaji":"Ocha to kōhī wa dochira ga ii desu ka.","german":"Which would you prefer, tea or coffee?","furigana":"ちゃ"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"お国はどちらですか。","romaji":"Okuni wa dochira desu ka.","german":"Where are you from?","furigana":"くに"},{"japanese":"こちらの方は、どちら様ですか。","romaji":"Kochira no kata wa, dochirasama desu ka.","german":"May I ask who this person is?","furigana":"かた|さま"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 3"},
{"notes":"こちら politely refers to the speaker's side or area and can also introduce a nearby person. こちらへ来てください names the direction toward the speaker with へ. こちらは山田さんです, by contrast, introduces Yamada as a person. The reference depends on the situation; こちら is not just a place word in every sentence. In casual conversation, the form こっち is used.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"こちらへ来てください。","romaji":"Kochira e kite kudasai.","german":"Please come this way.","furigana":"き"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"こちらは山田さんです。","romaji":"Kochira wa Yamada-san desu.","german":"This is Mr./Ms. Yamada.","furigana":"やまだ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 3"},
{"notes":"そちら refers to the area of the person addressed. It can mean a direction toward the other person's area, a seat there, or the place where the other person is. In そちらの会社 it can politely refer to the other person's company. そちらに座る names a destination seat with に, whereas そちらの天気 refers to the weather at the other location. The casual form is そっち.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"そちらに座ってください。","romaji":"Sochira ni suwatte kudasai.","german":"Please sit over there.","furigana":"すわ"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"そちらの天気はどうですか。","romaji":"Sochira no tenki wa dō desu ka.","german":"How is the weather where you are?","furigana":"てんき"},{"japanese":"そちらの会社では、日本語を使いますか。","romaji":"Sochira no kaisha de wa, nihongo o tsukaimasu ka.","german":"Is Japanese used at your company?","furigana":"かいしゃ|にほんご|つか"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 3"},
{"notes":"あちら refers to a place or direction that is distant from both speakers. あちらです points to the entrance over there. あちらの店 specifies with の a shop in that distant area; で names it as the place of shopping. The form sounds more polite than あっち. Here it indicates spatial distance, not temporal order.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"入口はあちらです。","romaji":"Iriguchi wa achira desu.","german":"The entrance is over there.","furigana":"いりぐち"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"あちらの店で買いました。","romaji":"Achira no mise de kaimashita.","german":"I bought it at the shop over there.","furigana":"みせ|か"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 3"},
{"notes":"こっち is the casual form of こちら and refers to the speaker's side. こっちに来て calls someone over; こっちの道 selects the road on this side. の connects the demonstrative with 道. A request without ください suits close conversation partners. The small っ before ち is written tch in Modified Hepburn: kotchi.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"こっちに来て。","romaji":"Kotchi ni kite.","german":"Come here.","furigana":"き"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"こっちの道が近いです。","romaji":"Kotchi no michi ga chikai desu.","german":"This road is shorter.","furigana":"みち|ちか"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 20"},
{"notes":"そっち ist die lockere Form von そちら und meint den Bereich beim Gegenüber. そっちに行く fragt im ersten Satz nach dem Gehen dorthin; deutsch passt aus Sicht des Gegenübers „zu dir kommen“. そっちのかばん bezeichnet mit の die Tasche auf dessen Seite. Die Frage 誰の lässt das Wort für den Besitz weg und bedeutet „wem gehört sie?“. Die Form passt zu einem vertrauten Gesprächston.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"そっちに行ってもいい？","romaji":"Sotchi ni itte mo ii?","german":"Darf ich zu dir kommen?","furigana":"い"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"そっちのかばんは誰の？","romaji":"Sotchi no kaban wa dare no?","german":"Wem gehört die Tasche dort bei dir?","furigana":"だれ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 20"},
{"notes":"あっち is the casual form of あちら: \"over there\" or \"in that direction\". 駅はあっちです shows the location or direction of the station. あっちで待っていて names with で the place where someone should wait. In the described situation, the place is distant from both speakers. The small っ belongs to the whole reading atchi; it is not left out.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"駅はあっちです。","romaji":"Eki wa atchi desu.","german":"The station is over there.","furigana":"えき"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"あっちで待っていて。","romaji":"Atchi de matte ite.","german":"Wait over there.","furigana":"ま"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 20"},
{"notes":"どっち is the casual form of どちら. It can ask about a choice between two possibilities or about a direction. 赤と青、どっちが好き？ offers colors to choose from; 駅はどっち？ asks for the direction to the station. Short questions without ですか suit casual conversation here. In Modified Hepburn, the small っ before ち is written tch: dotchi.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"赤と青、どっちが好き？","romaji":"Aka to ao, dotchi ga suki?","german":"Which do you like better, red or blue?","furigana":"あか|あお|す"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"駅はどっち？","romaji":"Eki wa dotchi?","german":"Which way is the station?","furigana":"えき"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 20"},
{"notes":"つまらない describes something boring or uninteresting and can also mean \"trivial\". For the movie it is about a lack of interest, for つまらないこと about a minor matter. The word is often written in hiragana and is a separate i-adjective here. It does not have to be understood literally as the negation of 詰まる in every context. ので gives the reason in the second example for why friends were called.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"この映画はつまらないです。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa tsumaranai desu.","german":"This movie is boring.","furigana":"えいが"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"一人ではつまらないので、友達を呼びました。","romaji":"Hitori de wa tsumaranai node, tomodachi o yobimashita.","german":"It's boring alone, so I invited friends.","furigana":"ひとり|ともだち|よ"},{"japanese":"つまらないことでけんかをしないでください。","romaji":"Tsumaranai koto de kenka o shinaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't fight over trivial things."}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 5"},
{"notes":"みんな refers to all the people of a group known from the context. みんなで invites a joint action; みんな来ましたか asks whether the whole expected group has come. The situation determines who belongs to it. みんな is less formal and more emphatic than みな. Before さん, the usual form of address みなさん is used, not simply みんなさん.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"みんなで写真を撮りましょう。","romaji":"Minna de shashin o torimashō.","german":"Let's take a photo all together.","furigana":"しゃしん|と"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"みんな、もう来ましたか。","romaji":"Minna, mō kimashita ka.","german":"Has everyone arrived yet?","furigana":"き"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 11"},
{"notes":"皆 is read みな here and refers to all members of a group. It is somewhat more formal than みんな. 学生は皆 names the complete group of students, and 皆で相談する a joint discussion. The following で describes acting together. The form みな is also in the address 皆さん; its full pronunciation is みなさん.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"学生は皆、教室にいます。","romaji":"Gakusei wa mina, kyōshitsu ni imasu.","german":"The students are all in the classroom.","furigana":"がくせい|みな|きょうしつ"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"皆で相談しましょう。","romaji":"Mina de sōdan shimashō.","german":"Let's discuss it all together.","furigana":"みな|そうだん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"また often indicates a repetition or a renewed meeting. また明日会いましょう looks ahead to another meeting the next day; また雨が降ってきた to rain setting in again. As a link between statements, また can also mean \"in addition\". In the extra example, it adds the closeness to the station to the room's spaciousness. The context decides between repetition and additional information.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"また明日会いましょう。","romaji":"Mata ashita aimashō.","german":"Let's meet again tomorrow.","furigana":"あしたあ"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"また雨が降ってきました。","romaji":"Mata ame ga futte kimashita.","german":"It has started raining again.","furigana":"あめ|ふ"},{"japanese":"この部屋は広いです。また、駅にも近いです。","romaji":"Kono heya wa hiroi desu. Mata, eki ni mo chikai desu.","german":"This room is spacious. It is also close to the station.","furigana":"へや|ひろ|えき|ちか"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 14"},
{"notes":"そう picks up content that has already been expressed or understood. そうです confirms the other person's statement; そう思います agrees with a thought. The word means \"so\" or \"that's right\" here. It is not the identically written そう that attaches to a verb stem and forms an impression like \"looks like\". The long o sound is romanized as sō.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"そうです。私は学生です。","romaji":"Sō desu. Watashi wa gakusei desu.","german":"That's right. I'm a student.","furigana":"わたし|がくせい"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"私もそう思います。","romaji":"Watashi mo sō omoimasu.","german":"I think so too.","furigana":"わたし|おも"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 2"},
{"notes":"方 is read かた here and is a respectful term for a person. あの方 politely describes a distant person; どの方 asks about a person from a selection. The construction is noun plus particle, such as 方は or 方が. The same character is often read ほう in expressions of direction and comparison. There is also the attached form ～方 for a method; these roles are to be distinguished from the polite word for a person.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"あの方は先生です。","romaji":"Ano kata wa sensei desu.","german":"That person is a teacher.","furigana":"かた|せんせい"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"どの方が山田さんですか。","romaji":"Dono kata ga Yamada-san desu ka.","german":"Which person is Mr./Ms. Yamada?","furigana":"かた|やまだ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 14"},
{"notes":"方 is read ほう here and refers to a direction or a side in a comparison. 駅の方へ歩く means to walk in the direction of the station; the station need not be the destination reached. こちらの方が安いです compares the alternative here with another one. の connects the reference with 方, が marks the compared alternative. The polite person reading かた belongs to a different usage.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"こちらの方が安いです。","romaji":"Kochira no hō ga yasui desu.","german":"This one is cheaper.","furigana":"ほう|やす"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"駅の方へ歩きます。","romaji":"Eki no hō e arukimasu.","german":"I walk toward the station.","furigana":"えき|ほう|ある"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 29"},
{"notes":"側 is read そば here and indicates spatial proximity. 駅の側 is the vicinity of the station, 私の側 the place right next to me. の establishes the reference; に names the place of residence with 住む and the destination seat with 座る. In this meaning the word is often written そば. The identically sounding buckwheat noodles are a different word and not a place expression.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"駅の側に住んでいます。","romaji":"Eki no soba ni sunde imasu.","german":"I live near the station.","furigana":"えき|そば|す"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"私の側に座ってください。","romaji":"Watashi no soba ni suwatte kudasai.","german":"Please sit next to me.","furigana":"わたし|そば|すわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"他（ほか）refers to something else or additional. Before a noun, の is used: 他の本 \"another book\". 他に often means \"in addition, besides\", 他で \"elsewhere\". The kana spelling ほか is also common; the reading ほか is to be distinguished from 他（た）in other compounds.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"他の本を見せてください。","romaji":"Hoka no hon o misete kudasai.","german":"Please show me another book.","furigana":"ほか|ほん|み"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"他に質問はありますか。","romaji":"Hoka ni shitsumon wa arimasu ka.","german":"Do you have any other questions?","furigana":"ほか|しつもん"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"この店にはないので、他で探します。","romaji":"Kono mise ni wa nai node, hoka de sagashimasu.","german":"They don't have it in this shop, so I'll look elsewhere.","furigana":"みせ|ほか|さが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"被る is read かぶる here and means to put something on the head or wear it there. The hat is the object, marked with を. 被ってください asks someone to put it on, 被っています describes the resulting state of wearing it. Despite its る ending it is a Godan verb and forms 被って. The other reading こうむる belongs to meanings such as \"to suffer\" and is not used here.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"帽子を被ってください。","romaji":"Bōshi o kabutte kudasai.","german":"Please put on your hat.","furigana":"ぼうし|かぶ"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"子供は赤い帽子を被っています。","romaji":"Kodomo wa akai bōshi o kabutte imasu.","german":"The child is wearing a red hat.","furigana":"こども|あか|ぼうし|かぶ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"やる here means to do a task or activity, and in usage it feels more casual than する. 宿題をやる names the task as the object; やってから places the playing after finishing the homework. The polite ending in やります makes the sentence more polite, while the verb itself remains rather colloquial. This godan verb forms やります and やって.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"宿題をやってから遊びます。","romaji":"Shukudai o yatte kara asobimasu.","german":"I'll play after I do my homework.","furigana":"しゅくだい|あそ"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"今日は何をやりますか。","romaji":"Kyō wa nani o yarimasu ka.","german":"What are we doing today?","furigana":"きょう|なに"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 5"},
{"notes":"火 is fire or a flame. 火を消す names with を the fire that is to be extinguished or switched off. 火の近く, by contrast, names a place near the fire. The form 行かないでください asks you to refrain from approaching. The word is read ひ like 日, \"day\", but has different characters and a different meaning.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"火を消してください。","romaji":"Hi o keshite kudasai.","german":"Please put out the fire.","furigana":"ひ|け"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"火の近くに行かないでください。","romaji":"Hi no chikaku ni ikanaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't go near the fire.","furigana":"ひ|ちか|い"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 34"},
{"notes":"日 is read ひ here and can mean a day, the bright part of the day, or sunlight. いい日でした evaluates a day as a good experience; 日が長くなる describes longer daylight. 日が当たる means that the sun shines on a place. The extra room example uses this sunlight meaning. Dates and compounds have their own readings; the single reading ひ is not simply used everywhere.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"今日はいい日でした。","romaji":"Kyō wa ii hi deshita.","german":"Today was a good day.","furigana":"きょう|ひ"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"日が長くなりました。","romaji":"Hi ga nagaku narimashita.","german":"The days have gotten longer.","furigana":"ひ|なが"},{"japanese":"この部屋は、午前中によく日が当たります。","romaji":"Kono heya wa, gozenchū ni yoku hi ga atarimasu.","german":"This room gets a lot of sun in the morning.","furigana":"へや|ごぜんちゅう|ひ|あ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 19"},
{"notes":"一人 is read irregularly as ひとり when counting people. 学生が一人います counts a single person; 一人で says that someone acts without company. In the Japan example this means \"come to Japan alone\", not necessarily being unmarried. In the second sentence the number of people stands after the subject and before います. Another common counting form is 二人, ふたり.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"一人で日本に来ました。","romaji":"Hitori de Nihon ni kimashita.","german":"I came to Japan alone.","furigana":"ひとり|にほん|き"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"教室に学生が一人います。","romaji":"Kyōshitsu ni gakusei ga hitori imasu.","german":"There is one student in the classroom.","furigana":"きょうしつ|がくせい|ひとり"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 7"},
{"notes":"二人 means two people and is read ふたり. 子供が二人います counts the children, 二人で旅行する describes a trip as a pair. 友達と二人で in the first example means the speaker and a friend, i.e. two people in total. The most common reading is ふたり; ににん is also attested. The word alone does not claim a romantic relationship between the two people.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"友達と二人で旅行します。","romaji":"Tomodachi to futari de ryokō shimasu.","german":"I'm traveling with a friend, just the two of us.","furigana":"ともだち|ふたり|りょこう"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"部屋に子供が二人います。","romaji":"Heya ni kodomo ga futari imasu.","german":"There are two children in the room.","furigana":"へや|こども|ふたり"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 7"},
{"notes":"二十歳 is read はたち when giving this age and means twenty years old. 二十歳になる describes reaching this age; 二十歳です states the current age. 今年 places the birthday in the current year without naming a specific date. The special reading applies to the age; the number 二十 alone is usually read にじゅう.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"私は今年、二十歳になります。","romaji":"Watashi wa kotoshi, hatachi ni narimasu.","german":"I turn twenty this year.","furigana":"わたし|ことし|はたち"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"兄は二十歳です。","romaji":"Ani wa hatachi desu.","german":"My older brother is twenty years old.","furigana":"あに|はたち"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"半 bedeutet eine Hälfte und ergänzt Mengen oder Uhrzeiten. 七時半 ist 7:30 Uhr, also auf Deutsch „halb acht“, nicht „halb sieben“. 一時間半 ist eine Stunde plus eine halbe Stunde, insgesamt anderthalb Stunden. Die erste Angabe nennt einen Zeitpunkt mit に, die zweite eine Dauer als Aussage. Die Lesung des einzelnen Wortes ist はん; ganze Zahlverbindungen werden als vollständige Ausdrücke gelesen.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"毎朝、七時半に起きます。","romaji":"Maiasa, shichiji han ni okimasu.","german":"Ich stehe jeden Morgen um halb acht auf.","furigana":"まいあさ|しちじはん|お"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"授業は一時間半です。","romaji":"Jugyō wa ichijikan han desu.","german":"Der Unterricht dauert anderthalb Stunden.","furigana":"じゅぎょう|いちじかんはん"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 4"},
{"notes":"As a duration, 一日 is read いちにち. 一日中 covers the whole day, whereas 一日に三回 gives a frequency per day. The first day of a month, by contrast, is read ついたち. So pay attention to 中 or the following frequency expression before choosing the reading.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"一日中、家にいました。","romaji":"Ichinichijū, ie ni imashita.","german":"I was at home all day.","furigana":"いちにちじゅう|いえ"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"この薬は一日に三回飲みます。","romaji":"Kono kusuri wa ichinichi ni sankai nomimasu.","german":"You take this medicine three times a day.","furigana":"くすり|いちにち|さんかいの"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 13"},
{"notes":"ついたち is the fixed calendar reading for the first day of a month. It remains after the month name: 五月一日 is read ごがつついたち. A duration of one day, by contrast, is read いちにち. A stand-alone 一日 can, in connection with an appointment, mean the 1st of the month in question.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"今日は五月一日です。","romaji":"Kyō wa gogatsu tsuitachi desu.","german":"Today is May 1st.","furigana":"きょう|ごがつついたち"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"店は一日が休みです。","romaji":"Mise wa tsuitachi ga yasumi desu.","german":"The shop is closed on the 1st.","furigana":"みせ|ついたち|やす"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"パーティー here means a celebration with several guests. パーティーをする describes hosting or celebrating, パーティーに友達を呼ぶ inviting friends to it. に marks the event as the destination of the invitation. The ending ティー is pronounced with a long i; パーティ is also attested.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"土曜日にパーティーをします。","romaji":"Doyōbi ni pātī o shimasu.","german":"We're having a party on Saturday.","furigana":"どようび"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"パーティーに友達を呼びました。","romaji":"Pātī ni tomodachi o yobimashita.","german":"I invited friends to the party.","furigana":"ともだち|よ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 12"},
{"notes":"ページ refers to a page of a book and also follows a number. 十ページを開く names the page that is to be opened; 五ページ読む the number of pages read. The verb helps to distinguish a page number from an amount of pages. The kanji spelling 頁 is also read ページ in this usage; you find it in books and indexes, for example. For ten, じっページ and じゅっページ are both in use.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"十ページを開いてください。","romaji":"Juppēji o hiraite kudasai.","german":"Please open to page ten.","furigana":"じゅっ|ひら"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"毎日、五ページ読みます。","romaji":"Mainichi, go pēji yomimasu.","german":"I read five pages every day.","furigana":"まいにち|ご|よ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 6"},
{"notes":"ボタン can refer to a button on clothing or a button on a device. With ボタンを押す you press a button; ボタンが取れる describes a button that has come off. The rare kanji spelling 釦 is read ボタン in both meanings. In everyday writing, the katakana form is usual. The identically sounding plant name 牡丹 is a different word.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"このボタンを押してください。","romaji":"Kono botan o oshite kudasai.","german":"Please press this button.","furigana":"お"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"シャツのボタンが取れました。","romaji":"Shatsu no botan ga toremashita.","german":"A button came off the shirt.","furigana":"と"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 16"},
{"notes":"クラス can mean a group of learners or a particular course. クラスには二十人います describes how many people belong to the group; 日本語のクラスは月曜日です gives the date of the course. For people, います is used here. The similarly sounding verb 暮らす, by contrast, means to live or to spend one's daily life.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"私のクラスには二十人います。","romaji":"Watashi no kurasu ni wa nijū nin imasu.","german":"There are twenty people in my class.","furigana":"わたし|にじゅうにん"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"日本語のクラスは月曜日です。","romaji":"Nihongo no kurasu wa getsuyōbi desu.","german":"The Japanese class is on Monday.","furigana":"にほんご|げつようび"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 11"},
{"notes":"すぐに places the start of an action close to the present or a previously mentioned point in time. Before 帰ります it announces a soon return; after 分からない時は it urges one to ask without waiting long. に stands before the verb and can be omitted in many such sentences. It is about the point in time here, not the speed of the action.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"すぐに帰ります。","romaji":"Sugu ni kaerimasu.","german":"I'll be back right away.","furigana":"かえ"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"分からない時はすぐに聞いてください。","romaji":"Wakaranai toki wa sugu ni kiite kudasai.","german":"If you don't understand, please ask right away.","furigana":"わ|とき|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"など shows that the things mentioned are examples from a larger selection. In りんごやバナナなど it closes an open-ended list. After just one noun, as in 本など, it can be rendered as \"for example\". A following case particle remains possible: などを marks the things named as examples as the object.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"りんごやバナナなどを買いました。","romaji":"Ringo ya banana nado o kaimashita.","german":"I bought apples, bananas and so on.","furigana":"か"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"休みの日は本などを読みます。","romaji":"Yasumi no hi wa hon nado o yomimasu.","german":"On days off I read books and the like.","furigana":"やす|ひ|ほん|よ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"だけ limits a selection or quantity to what is named. 水だけ excludes other drinks, 五分だけ limits the waiting time to five minutes. Unlike しか, だけ does not require a negated verb: 飲みました and 待ってください stay affirmative here. The limitation stands directly after the noun or quantity in question.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"水だけ飲みました。","romaji":"Mizu dake nomimashita.","german":"I only drank water.","furigana":"みず|の"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"五分だけ待ってください。","romaji":"Gofun dake matte kudasai.","german":"Please wait just five minutes.","furigana":"ごふん|ま"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"より follows the standard of a comparison. In 今日は昨日より暖かい, 昨日 is the reference point: today is warmer than yesterday. AはBより～ thus describes that the quality is more pronounced in A than in B. The same comparison can stand before a noun, as in ここより静かな所 for a place that is quieter than this one.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"今日は昨日より暖かいです。","romaji":"Kyō wa kinō yori atatakai desu.","german":"It's warmer today than yesterday.","furigana":"きょう|きのう|あたた"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"電車はバスより速いです。","romaji":"Densha wa basu yori hayai desu.","german":"The train is faster than the bus.","furigana":"でんしゃ|はや"},{"japanese":"ここより静かな所に住みたいです。","romaji":"Koko yori shizuka na tokoro ni sumitai desu.","german":"I want to live in a place quieter than here.","furigana":"しず|ところ|す"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"さん wird als höflicher Zusatz hinter den Namen einer Person gesetzt. Es unterscheidet nicht zwischen Männern und Frauen; die deutsche Wiedergabe richtet sich nach dem bekannten Zusammenhang. Bei einer Selbstvorstellung lässt man さん normalerweise weg. Der Zusatz kann sowohl bei der Erwähnung einer Person als auch in der direkten Anrede stehen, etwa 山田さん、… .","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"田中さんは先生です。","romaji":"Tanaka-san wa sensei desu.","german":"Tanaka ist eine Lehrkraft.","furigana":"たなか|せんせい"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"山田さんに電話しました。","romaji":"Yamada-san ni denwa shimashita.","german":"Ich habe Yamada angerufen.","furigana":"やまだ|でんわ"},{"japanese":"山田さん、お元気ですか。","romaji":"Yamada-san, ogenki desu ka.","german":"Yamada, wie geht es Ihnen?","furigana":"やまだ|げんき"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 1"},
{"notes":"お巡りさん is a familiar everyday term for a police officer. The word refers to the person: お巡りさんに道を聞く means to ask them the way. 交番, by contrast, is the small police box where the person may work. お and さん are part of the fixed term here and are kept when using it.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"お巡りさんに道を聞きました。","romaji":"Omawarisan ni michi o kikimashita.","german":"I asked a police officer the way.","furigana":"まわ|みち|き"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"お巡りさんは交番にいます。","romaji":"Omawarisan wa kōban ni imasu.","german":"The police officer is at the police box.","furigana":"まわ|こうばん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"どういたしまして ist eine feste Antwort auf einen ausgesprochenen Dank. Die Person, der gedankt wurde, sagt damit gern geschehen. Die Formel lässt sich allein verwenden oder mit einer weiteren freundlichen Bemerkung verbinden. Sie ist keine Bitte um etwas: Das deutsche bitte hat hier die Bedeutung einer Antwort auf danke.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"「ありがとう。」「どういたしまして。」","romaji":"“Arigatō.” “Dō itashimashite.”","german":"„Danke.“ – „Gern geschehen.“"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"「手伝ってくれてありがとう。」「どういたしまして。」","romaji":"“Tetsudatte kurete arigatō.” “Dō itashimashite.”","german":"„Danke für deine Hilfe.“ – „Gern geschehen.“","furigana":"てつだ"},{"japanese":"「本を貸してくれてありがとう。」「どういたしまして。ゆっくり読んでね。」","romaji":"“Hon o kashite kurete arigatō.” “Dō itashimashite. Yukkuri yonde ne.”","german":"„Danke, dass du mir das Buch geliehen hast.“ – „Gern geschehen. Lies es in Ruhe.“","furigana":"ほん|か|よ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"失礼します is a polite formula for announcing a small interruption or entering or leaving a room. Before 入ってもいいですか it serves as an introduction to the request to enter. At the end of a conversation it can express farewell. The ending します belongs to the polite form; the formula need not be understood as an actual apology for a mistake.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"失礼します。入ってもいいですか。","romaji":"Shitsurei shimasu. Haitte mo ii desu ka.","german":"Excuse me. May I come in?","furigana":"しつれい|はい"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"それでは、失礼します。","romaji":"Soredewa, shitsurei shimasu.","german":"Well then, I'll be going.","furigana":"しつれい"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 16"},
{"notes":"おやすみなさい is the greeting when going to bed and when parting at night. You can use it to announce that you yourself are going to sleep, or to wish the other person a good night. It does not replace the evening greeting こんばんは. The shorter form おやすみ suits close friends; おやすみなさい is the more polite full formula.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"もう寝ます。おやすみなさい。","romaji":"Mō nemasu. Oyasumi nasai.","german":"I'm going to bed now. Good night.","furigana":"ね"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"おやすみなさい。また明日。","romaji":"Oyasumi nasai. Mata ashita.","german":"Good night. See you tomorrow.","furigana":"あした"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 0"},
{"notes":"風呂 refers to the bath, the bathtub or bathing. The common phrase 風呂に入る means to take a bath; に is used here with 入る. In everyday life you often encounter the more polite form お風呂. この部屋には風呂がありません, by contrast, is about the fact that a bath is not part of the room's equipment.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"毎晩、お風呂に入ります。","romaji":"Maiban, ofuro ni hairimasu.","german":"I take a bath every evening.","furigana":"まいばん|ふろ|はい"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"この部屋には風呂がありません。","romaji":"Kono heya ni wa furo ga arimasen.","german":"This room doesn't have a bath.","furigana":"へや|ふろ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"縦 refers to the vertical direction or the lengthwise direction of an object; the opposite is 横. With に it becomes a direction expression for the action: 縦に折る means to fold lengthwise, 縦に書く to write vertically. With paper and writing, the intended direction depends on how the surface lies in front of you or is viewed.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"紙を縦に半分に折ってください。","romaji":"Kami o tate ni hanbun ni otte kudasai.","german":"Please fold the paper in half lengthwise.","furigana":"かみ|たて|はんぶん|お"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"名前を縦に書きました。","romaji":"Namae o tate ni kakimashita.","german":"I wrote the name vertically.","furigana":"なまえ|たて|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"This entry practices メートル as a unit of length. The word follows a number: 幅が三メートルあります describes a width, 百メートル先 a distance ahead. The rare kanji spelling 米 is read メートル in this meaning and is to be distinguished from the rice word 米（こめ）. メートル or the symbol m is usual. The secondary meaning as a measuring device is practiced separately; for that, メーター is mostly used today.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"この部屋は幅が三メートルあります。","romaji":"Kono heya wa haba ga san mētoru arimasu.","german":"This room is three meters wide.","furigana":"へや|はば|さん"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"百メートル先に駅があります。","romaji":"Hyaku mētoru saki ni eki ga arimasu.","german":"There is a station a hundred meters ahead.","furigana":"ひゃく|さき|えき"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"キロメートル refers to longer distances and distances covered. 学校まで二キロメートルあります gives the distance to the school; 五キロメートル歩く the distance one walks. In conversation, キロ can suffice when a distance is meant. The rare kanji spelling 粁 has the same reading; the katakana form and the symbol km are usual.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"学校まで二キロメートルあります。","romaji":"Gakkō made ni kiromētoru arimasu.","german":"It's two kilometers to the school.","furigana":"がっこう|に"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"今日は五キロメートル歩きました。","romaji":"Kyō wa go kiromētoru arukimashita.","german":"Today I walked five kilometers.","furigana":"きょう|ご|ある"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"キログラム follows a number and gives weight. この荷物は十キログラムです describes the weight of a piece of luggage; 米を二キログラム買う the quantity measured out at purchase. The short form キロ is enough when the context is clear. The rare kanji spelling 瓩 is also read キログラム; in everyday life you mostly encounter the katakana form or the symbol kg.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"この荷物は十キログラムです。","romaji":"Kono nimotsu wa jū kiroguramu desu.","german":"This luggage weighs ten kilograms.","furigana":"にもつ|じゅう"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"米を二キログラム買いました。","romaji":"Kome o ni kiroguramu kaimashita.","german":"I bought two kilograms of rice.","furigana":"こめ|に|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"グラム gives smaller weights when shopping and cooking. 肉を二百グラムください orders a specific amount; 砂糖を五十グラム入れる states the quantity of an ingredient. The rare kanji spelling 瓦 can also mean グラム. Here it belongs to the unit word and is to be distinguished from the ordinary word 瓦（かわら）, roof tile. In recipes and on packaging, グラム or g is usual.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"肉を二百グラムください。","romaji":"Niku o nihyaku guramu kudasai.","german":"Two hundred grams of meat, please.","furigana":"にく|にひゃく"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"砂糖を五十グラム入れます。","romaji":"Satō o gojū guramu iremasu.","german":"I add fifty grams of sugar.","furigana":"さとう|ごじゅう|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"ワイシャツ refers to a dress shirt, as worn for example to work or with a suit. 着る is used for this; 着て行く additionally describes going somewhere in these clothes. ワイシャツにアイロンを掛ける means to iron the shirt. The spelling Ｙシャツ is also read ワイシャツ. Although the term goes back to the English \"white shirt\", the shirt can have a different color.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"仕事にはワイシャツを着て行きます。","romaji":"Shigoto ni wa waishatsu o kite ikimasu.","german":"I wear a dress shirt to work.","furigana":"しごと|き|い"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"ワイシャツにアイロンを掛けました。","romaji":"Waishatsu ni airon o kakemashita.","german":"I ironed the dress shirt.","furigana":"か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"テープ means tape; the object and the verb show which kind is meant. When sealing a box or with よく付く, it is adhesive tape. With 声を聞く, a recording tape may be meant. テープで names the tape as an aid or medium, while テープは makes the tape itself the topic.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"テープで箱を閉じました。","romaji":"Tēpu de hako o tojimashita.","german":"I sealed the box with tape.","furigana":"はこ|と"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"このテープはよく付きます。","romaji":"Kono tēpu wa yoku tsukimasu.","german":"This tape sticks well.","furigana":"つ"},{"japanese":"古いテープで、祖父の声を聞きました。","romaji":"Furui tēpu de, sofu no koe o kikimashita.","german":"I heard my grandfather's voice on an old tape.","furigana":"ふる|そふ|こえ|き"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 2"},
{"notes":"こちらこそ answers a friendly remark by turning it back on oneself: \"likewise\" or \"the pleasure/thanks is mine\". After よろしくお願いします it returns the wish for good cooperation. After ありがとうございました it stresses that one is grateful too. In this fixed phrase, こそ emphasizes one's own side.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"「よろしくお願いします。」「こちらこそ。」","romaji":"“Yoroshiku onegai shimasu.” “Kochira koso.”","german":"\"Pleased to meet you.\" \"Likewise.\"","furigana":"ねが"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"こちらこそ、ありがとうございました。","romaji":"Kochira koso, arigatō gozaimashita.","german":"Thank you, too."}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"一 as a stand-alone number is read いち. In 一円 this reading is kept: いちえん. With certain counters, however, the form changes, for example 一回（いっかい）or 一人（ひとり）. So learn a number and its counter together. In 答えは一です, the number itself is the answer, with no counted thing.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"答えは一です。","romaji":"Kotae wa ichi desu.","german":"The answer is 1.","furigana":"こた|いち"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"あと一円足りません。","romaji":"Ato ichi en tarimasen.","german":"I'm still one yen short.","furigana":"いちえんた"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 0"},
{"notes":"二 is the number two and is read に on this card. When counting flat things, it is combined, for example, with 枚: 二枚 \"two pieces\". In an answer, the number can stand alone before です. People and days of the month have their own forms such as 二人 and 二日, which are learned as whole words.","examples":[{"japanese":"答えは二です。","romaji":"Kotae wa ni desu.","german":"The answer is 2.","furigana":"こた|に"},{"japanese":"切符を二枚買いました。","romaji":"Kippu o nimai kaimashita.","german":"I bought two tickets.","furigana":"きっぷ|にまいか"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 0"},
{"notes":"三 is the number three and is read さん. The n is a separate sound at the end of the number. With 枚 you count photos, for example: 三枚. The number 三 is different from the honorific ending さん after a name. In larger numbers, it can likewise stand before other number words, such as 三十.","examples":[{"japanese":"答えは三です。","romaji":"Kotae wa san desu.","german":"The answer is 3.","furigana":"こた|さん"},{"japanese":"写真を三枚撮りました。","romaji":"Shashin o sanmai torimashita.","german":"I took three photos.","furigana":"しゃしん|さんまいと"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 0"},
{"notes":"四 means four; this card uses よん. The moraic n remains audible in 四枚 as well. In 四月, however, the same kanji is read し: しがつ. The right reading therefore depends on the whole expression. 四枚ください asks for four flat objects that were mentioned beforehand.","examples":[{"japanese":"答えは四です。","romaji":"Kotae wa yon desu.","german":"The answer is 4.","furigana":"こた|よん"},{"japanese":"切手を四枚ください。","romaji":"Kitte o yonmai kudasai.","german":"Four stamps, please.","furigana":"きって|よんまい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"五 is the number five and is read ご here. The vowel is short in the basic form. 五円玉 refers to a five-yen coin; 玉 is read だま in this compound. With other counters, other expressions are formed, such as 五枚 for five flat things. Number and counter are read together.","examples":[{"japanese":"答えは五です。","romaji":"Kotae wa go desu.","german":"The answer is 5.","furigana":"こた|ご"},{"japanese":"五円玉を見つけました。","romaji":"Go-endama o mitsukemashita.","german":"I found a five-yen coin.","furigana":"ごえんだま|み"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 0"},
{"notes":"六 is read ろく and means six. With 枚, 六枚 means \"six pieces\", for example of photos. With some other counters, the pronunciation changes: 六百 is read ろっぴゃく. Such combinations are learned as whole forms. ろく has two short vowels in its basic form.","examples":[{"japanese":"答えは六です。","romaji":"Kotae wa roku desu.","german":"The answer is 6.","furigana":"こた|ろく"},{"japanese":"写真を六枚見せてください。","romaji":"Shashin o rokumai misete kudasai.","german":"Please show me six photos.","furigana":"しゃしん|ろくまいみ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 0"},
{"notes":"七 means seven; the reading on this card is なな. 七枚 means, for example, \"seven cards/sheets\". Another common reading is しち, as in 七月. なな has two short a's and no long a. Which reading is used must be noted for each expression.","examples":[{"japanese":"答えは七です。","romaji":"Kotae wa nana desu.","german":"The answer is 7.","furigana":"こた|なな"},{"japanese":"七枚のカードを並べました。","romaji":"Nanamai no kādo o narabemashita.","german":"I laid out seven cards side by side.","furigana":"ななまい|なら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"八 is the number eight and is read はち. 八枚 counts, for example, eight envelopes. Before certain counters, shortened forms with a small っ arise, such as 八百 はっぴゃく. In 八枚, the basic reading はち is kept. はち has no long vowels.","examples":[{"japanese":"答えは八です。","romaji":"Kotae wa hachi desu.","german":"The answer is 8.","furigana":"こた|はち"},{"japanese":"封筒を八枚買いました。","romaji":"Fūtō o hachimai kaimashita.","german":"I bought eight envelopes.","furigana":"ふうとう|はちまいか"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 0"},
{"notes":"九 is the number nine. This card reads it きゅう with a long u; きゅ forms a sequence pronounced together. 九枚 means \"nine pieces\" for flat things. In 九月, however, the reading is く: くがつ. Number combinations are therefore read to match the counter.","examples":[{"japanese":"答えは九です。","romaji":"Kotae wa kyū desu.","german":"The answer is 9.","furigana":"こた|きゅう"},{"japanese":"九枚の写真を選びました。","romaji":"Kyūmai no shashin o erabimashita.","german":"I chose nine photos.","furigana":"きゅうまい|しゃしん|えら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"十 is the number ten and is read じゅう with a long u here. 十円玉 is a ten-yen coin. With some counters the form changes, for example 十分 じゅっぷん for ten minutes. とお appears in other number expressions such as 十日; this card uses じゅう.","examples":[{"japanese":"答えは十です。","romaji":"Kotae wa jū desu.","german":"The answer is 10.","furigana":"こた|じゅう"},{"japanese":"十円玉が落ちています。","romaji":"Jū-endama ga ochite imasu.","german":"A ten-yen coin is lying there.","furigana":"じゅうえんだま|お"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 0"},
{"notes":"百 is a hundred and is read ひゃく. ひゃ forms a sequence pronounced together. 百円 means one hundred yen. In larger numbers, some combinations change their reading: 三百 さんびゃく, 六百 ろっぴゃく, 八百 はっぴゃく. In the basic form 百, there is normally no 一 before ひゃく.","examples":[{"japanese":"答えは百です。","romaji":"Kotae wa hyaku desu.","german":"The answer is 100.","furigana":"こた|ひゃく"},{"japanese":"あと百円足りません。","romaji":"Ato hyaku-en tarimasen.","german":"I'm still 100 yen short.","furigana":"ひゃくえんた"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 3"},
{"notes":"千 is a thousand and is read せん. 千円 is the amount of a thousand yen; before 円, an apostrophe in sen'en separates the n from the following e. For exactly one thousand, one usually says 千, without 一 before it. In building larger numbers, forms such as 二千 and 三千 occur, where 三千 is read さんぜん.","examples":[{"japanese":"答えは千です。","romaji":"Kotae wa sen desu.","german":"The answer is 1000.","furigana":"こた|せん"},{"japanese":"この本は千円です。","romaji":"Kono hon wa sen’en desu.","german":"This book costs 1,000 yen.","furigana":"ほん|せんえん"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 3"},
{"notes":"万 is the unit for ten thousand. Exactly 10,000 is 一万 いちまん; here, the one is explicitly stated before 万. 二万 means 20,000. 一万円 is an amount of 10,000 yen. When reading larger numbers, grouping by four digits helps, since 万 bundles 10,000 each time.","examples":[{"japanese":"答えは一万です。","romaji":"Kotae wa ichiman desu.","german":"The answer is 10,000.","furigana":"こた|いちまん"},{"japanese":"この時計は一万円です。","romaji":"Kono tokei wa ichiman’en desu.","german":"This watch costs 10,000 yen.","furigana":"とけい|いちまんえん"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 3"},
{"notes":"On this card, 四 is read し. This reading appears in 四月 しがつ for April and when reciting the number sequence 一、二、三、四. Other expressions need よん, for example 四枚. The card therefore practices the reading し without carrying it over to all combinations with 四.","examples":[{"japanese":"四月に日本へ行きます。","romaji":"Shigatsu ni Nihon e ikimasu.","german":"I'm going to Japan in April.","furigana":"しがつ|にほん|い"},{"japanese":"一、二、三、四と声に出して数えました。","romaji":"Ichi, ni, san, shi to koe ni dashite kazoemashita.","german":"I counted out loud: \"one, two, three, four.\"","furigana":"いち|に|さん|し|こえ|だ|かぞ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"七 is read しち here. This is the reading in 七時 しちじ and 七月 しちがつ. With other counters, なな may be common, for example 七枚 ななまい. The two short syllables in しち should be read clearly. This card practices the しち combinations with time of day and month.","examples":[{"japanese":"七時に起きます。","romaji":"Shichiji ni okimasu.","german":"I get up at seven.","furigana":"しちじ|お"},{"japanese":"七月は暑いです。","romaji":"Shichigatsu wa atsui desu.","german":"July is hot.","furigana":"しちがつ|あつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"九 has the reading く on this card. It is used in 九時 くじ and 九月 くがつ. In 九枚, by contrast, it is read きゅうまい. く has a short u; the other basic reading きゅう has a long u. The whole word determines the right reading.","examples":[{"japanese":"九時に寝ます。","romaji":"Kuji ni nemasu.","german":"I go to bed at nine.","furigana":"くじ|ね"},{"japanese":"九月に学校が始まります。","romaji":"Kugatsu ni gakkō ga hajimarimasu.","german":"School starts in September.","furigana":"くがつ|がっこう|はじ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"二日 is read ふつか. As a date, it is the second day of a month: 五月二日 \"May 2nd\". In 二日かかる, by contrast, a duration of two days is meant. 二日間 also emphasizes the time span. The reading has neither long vowels nor a small っ.","examples":[{"japanese":"今日は五月二日です。","romaji":"Kyō wa gogatsu futsuka desu.","german":"Today is May 2nd.","furigana":"きょう|ごがつふつか"},{"japanese":"旅行は二日かかります。","romaji":"Ryokō wa futsuka kakarimasu.","german":"The trip takes 2 days.","furigana":"りょこう|ふつか"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 13"},
{"notes":"三日 is read みっか with a small っ. It can name the third day of a month or a duration of three days. With a month before it, such as 五月三日, the date is clear. In 三日かかります, it gives the time needed. The reading is みっか in both cases.","examples":[{"japanese":"今日は五月三日です。","romaji":"Kyō wa gogatsu mikka desu.","german":"Today is May 3rd.","furigana":"きょう|ごがつみっか"},{"japanese":"旅行は三日かかります。","romaji":"Ryokō wa mikka kakarimasu.","german":"The trip takes 3 days.","furigana":"りょこう|みっか"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 13"},
{"notes":"四日 is よっか, with a small っ and a short o. It refers to the fourth day of a month or a duration of four days. 五月四日 is thus May 4th. For a duration, 四日間 can also be said. ようか with a long o, by contrast, is 八日, i.e. eight days or the eighth day.","examples":[{"japanese":"今日は五月四日です。","romaji":"Kyō wa gogatsu yokka desu.","german":"Today is May 4th.","furigana":"きょう|ごがつよっか"},{"japanese":"旅行は四日かかります。","romaji":"Ryokō wa yokka kakarimasu.","german":"The trip takes 4 days.","furigana":"りょこう|よっか"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 13"},
{"notes":"五日 is the fifth day of a month or a duration of five days and is read いつか. 五月五日 means May 5th. 五日かかる gives five days of time needed. The identically pronounced いつか can mean \"someday\"; with this kanji spelling 五日, it is about the number five.","examples":[{"japanese":"今日は五月五日です。","romaji":"Kyō wa gogatsu itsuka desu.","german":"Today is May 5th.","furigana":"きょう|ごがついつか"},{"japanese":"旅行は五日かかります。","romaji":"Ryokō wa itsuka kakarimasu.","german":"The trip takes 5 days.","furigana":"りょこう|いつか"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 13"},
{"notes":"六日 is read むいか. The u and the following i are pronounced as two vowels; there is no small っ. 五月六日 is May 6th. When you say something takes 六日, six days are meant. The duration can be explicitly marked as a time span with 六日間.","examples":[{"japanese":"今日は五月六日です。","romaji":"Kyō wa gogatsu muika desu.","german":"Today is May 6th.","furigana":"きょう|ごがつむいか"},{"japanese":"旅行は六日かかります。","romaji":"Ryokō wa muika kakarimasu.","german":"The trip takes 6 days.","furigana":"りょこう|むいか"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 13"},
{"notes":"七日 is the seventh day of a month or a duration of seven days. The reading is なのか, i.e. not なな plus か. 五月七日 names May 7th; 七日かかります describes the time needed of seven days. All vowels in なのか are short.","examples":[{"japanese":"今日は五月七日です。","romaji":"Kyō wa gogatsu nanoka desu.","german":"Today is May 7th.","furigana":"きょう|ごがつなのか"},{"japanese":"旅行は七日かかります。","romaji":"Ryokō wa nanoka kakarimasu.","german":"The trip takes 7 days.","furigana":"りょこう|なのか"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 13"},
{"notes":"八日 is read ようか and has a long o: yōka. It refers to the eighth day of a month or a duration of eight days. 五月八日 is May 8th. Four days, by contrast, is よっか with a small っ. So pay attention to the length of the o and the difference from っ.","examples":[{"japanese":"今日は五月八日です。","romaji":"Kyō wa gogatsu yōka desu.","german":"Today is May 8th.","furigana":"きょう|ごがつようか"},{"japanese":"旅行は八日かかります。","romaji":"Ryokō wa yōka kakarimasu.","german":"The trip takes 8 days.","furigana":"りょこう|ようか"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 13"},
{"notes":"九日 is the ninth day of a month or nine days' duration. The reading is ここのか with four short syllables: こ・こ・の・か. 五月九日 is May 9th. 九日かかる gives the duration; 九日間 explicitly highlights the time span. The reading stays the same in these usages.","examples":[{"japanese":"今日は五月九日です。","romaji":"Kyō wa gogatsu kokonoka desu.","german":"Today is May 9th.","furigana":"きょう|ごがつここのか"},{"japanese":"旅行は九日かかります。","romaji":"Ryokō wa kokonoka kakarimasu.","german":"The trip takes 9 days.","furigana":"りょこう|ここのか"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 13"},
{"notes":"十日 is read とおか with a long o: tōka. It is the tenth day of a month or a duration of ten days. 五月十日 means \"May 10th\". In 十日かかる, it is about a time span of ten days. The spelling とお has the same length here as a long o in other words.","examples":[{"japanese":"今日は五月十日です。","romaji":"Kyō wa gogatsu tōka desu.","german":"Today is May 10th.","furigana":"きょう|ごがつとおか"},{"japanese":"旅行は十日かかります。","romaji":"Ryokō wa tōka kakarimasu.","german":"The trip takes 10 days.","furigana":"りょこう|とおか"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 13"},
{"notes":"二十日 has the special reading はつか. It refers to the twentieth day of a month or a duration of twenty days. 五月二十日 means \"May 20th\". 二十日かかります gives twenty days of time needed. This form is learned as a whole; the basic numbers 二 and 十 are not read individually here.","examples":[{"japanese":"今日は五月二十日です。","romaji":"Kyō wa gogatsu hatsuka desu.","german":"Today is May 20th.","furigana":"きょう|ごがつはつか"},{"japanese":"旅行は二十日かかります。","romaji":"Ryokō wa hatsuka kakarimasu.","german":"The trip takes 20 days.","furigana":"りょこう|はつか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"四つ counts four things without a more specific counter. The reading is よっつ with a small っ; it is written yottsu. The quantity can follow the object: りんごを四つ. 四つあります states the quantity of existing objects. A construction before the noun is also possible: 四つの箱.","examples":[{"japanese":"りんごを四つください。","romaji":"Ringo o yottsu kudasai.","german":"Four apples, please.","furigana":"よっ"},{"japanese":"机の上に箱が四つあります。","romaji":"Tsukue no ue ni hako ga yottsu arimasu.","german":"There are 4 boxes on the table.","furigana":"つくえ|うえ|はこ|よっ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 9"},
{"notes":"五つ is the general counting form for five things and is read いつつ. The two つ are normal syllables; there is no small っ. You can count after the noun, as in りんごを五つ, or stand before a noun with 五つの. The plain number word 五, by contrast, is read ご.","examples":[{"japanese":"りんごを五つください。","romaji":"Ringo o itsutsu kudasai.","german":"Five apples, please.","furigana":"いつ"},{"japanese":"机の上に箱が五つあります。","romaji":"Tsukue no ue ni hako ga itsutsu arimasu.","german":"There are 5 boxes on the table.","furigana":"つくえ|うえ|はこ|いつ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 9"},
{"notes":"六つ counts six things in general. The reading むっつ contains a small っ and is written muttsu. This is a different form from the plain number word 六 ろく. In 箱が六つあります, the quantity stands between the object and あります. With 六つの箱, the quantity can also stand before the noun.","examples":[{"japanese":"りんごを六つください。","romaji":"Ringo o muttsu kudasai.","german":"Six apples, please.","furigana":"むっ"},{"japanese":"机の上に箱が六つあります。","romaji":"Tsukue no ue ni hako ga muttsu arimasu.","german":"There are 6 boxes on the table.","furigana":"つくえ|うえ|はこ|むっ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 9"},
{"notes":"七つ means seven things and is read ななつ. It has three short syllables and no small っ. The つ belongs to the general counting form, so no further counter such as 枚 is needed for this usage. In 七つの箱, の connects the quantity with the following noun.","examples":[{"japanese":"りんごを七つください。","romaji":"Ringo o nanatsu kudasai.","german":"Seven apples, please.","furigana":"なな"},{"japanese":"机の上に箱が七つあります。","romaji":"Tsukue no ue ni hako ga nanatsu arimasu.","german":"There are 7 boxes on the table.","furigana":"つくえ|うえ|はこ|なな"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 9"},
{"notes":"八つ is the general counting form for eight things. It is read やっつ with a small っ and written yattsu. The plain number word 八 is はち, whereas this counting form begins with や. 八つあります states the quantity present; 八つください asks for this quantity.","examples":[{"japanese":"りんごを八つください。","romaji":"Ringo o yattsu kudasai.","german":"Eight apples, please.","furigana":"やっ"},{"japanese":"机の上に箱が八つあります。","romaji":"Tsukue no ue ni hako ga yattsu arimasu.","german":"There are 8 boxes on the table.","furigana":"つくえ|うえ|はこ|やっ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 9"},
{"notes":"九つ counts nine things in general and is read ここのつ: こ・こ・の・つ. All vowels are short. ここのつ ends in つ, whereas the day form 九日 ここのか ends in か. When ordering, 九つ follows the noun you want, as in りんごを九つください.","examples":[{"japanese":"りんごを九つください。","romaji":"Ringo o kokonotsu kudasai.","german":"I'd like nine apples, please.","furigana":"ここの"},{"japanese":"机の上に箱が九つあります。","romaji":"Tsukue no ue ni hako ga kokonotsu arimasu.","german":"There are nine boxes on the desk.","furigana":"つくえ|うえ|はこ|ここの"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 9"},
{"notes":"零 is zero and is read れい. 零度 names a temperature of zero degrees; the unit is given with 度. 零から数える takes zero as the starting point of a count. Another common word for the number is ゼロ. The romanization of this reading stays rei.","examples":[{"japanese":"今日は零度です。","romaji":"Kyō wa reido desu.","german":"It's zero degrees today.","furigana":"きょう|れいど"},{"japanese":"零から数えてください。","romaji":"Rei kara kazoete kudasai.","german":"Please count from zero.","furigana":"れい|かぞ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"ゼロ names the number zero and can also mean that there is none of something or nothing left. 残りはゼロです says that nothing is left. ゼロ度 gives zero degrees. 零 れい is another word for the number. ゼロ has two short vowels.","examples":[{"japanese":"残りはゼロです。","romaji":"Nokori wa zero desu.","german":"There is nothing left.","furigana":"のこ"},{"japanese":"気温はゼロ度です。","romaji":"Kion wa zerodo desu.","german":"The temperature is zero degrees.","furigana":"きおん|ど"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"イクラ refers to the individual, usually salted or seasoned eggs of the salmon as food, for example on sushi or a rice bowl. The word comes from Russian and is usually written in katakana. Do not confuse it with いくら／幾ら (\"how much\").","examples":[{"japanese":"このすしにはイクラがのっています。","romaji":"Kono sushi ni wa ikura ga notte imasu.","german":"There is salmon roe on this sushi."},{"japanese":"イクラを小さな容器に入れて、冷蔵庫にしまいました。","romaji":"Ikura o chiisana yōki ni irete, reizōko ni shimaimashita.","german":"I put the salmon roe into a small container and stored it in the fridge.","furigana":"ちい|ようき|い|れいぞうこ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Question word with the counter 人（にん）. Do not confuse it with 何人（なにじん）\"what nationality\", which is written the same way.","examples":[{"japanese":"ご家族は何人ですか。","romaji":"Gokazoku wa nannin desu ka.","german":"How many people are in your family?","furigana":"かぞく|なんにん"},{"japanese":"パーティーには何人来ましたか。","romaji":"Pātī ni wa nannin kimashita ka.","german":"How many people came to the party?","furigana":"なんにんき"}],"levelNote":"Compound: 人 (N5)"},
{"notes":"Question word with 時（じ, o'clock）: 今何時ですか. The same spelling can be read いつ (\"when\"), but \"when\" is nowadays usually written in hiragana, so 何時 is almost always なんじ.","examples":[{"japanese":"すみません、今何時ですか。","romaji":"Sumimasen, ima nanji desu ka.","german":"Excuse me, what time is it now?","furigana":"いまなんじ"},{"japanese":"明日は何時に起きますか。","romaji":"Ashita wa nanji ni okimasu ka.","german":"What time are you getting up tomorrow?","furigana":"あした|なんじ|お"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 4"},
{"notes":"Month names = number + 月（がつ）. 三月 is the month of March; \"three months\", by contrast, is 三か月（さんかげつ）.","examples":[{"japanese":"三月に大学を卒業します。","romaji":"Sangatsu ni daigaku o sotsugyō shimasu.","german":"I graduate from university in March.","furigana":"さんがつ|だいがく|そつぎょう"},{"japanese":"日本では三月の終わりごろ、桜が咲き始めます。","romaji":"Nihon de wa sangatsu no owari goro, sakura ga sakihajimemasu.","german":"In Japan, the cherry trees begin to bloom around the end of March.","furigana":"にほん|さんがつ|お|さくら|さ|はじ"}],"levelNote":"Compound: 月 (N5)"},
{"notes":"Standard reading しちがつ. Because しち is easily confused with いち, you also hear なながつ colloquially; in exams and textbooks しちがつ is the standard.","examples":[{"japanese":"七月七日は七夕です。","romaji":"Shichigatsu nanoka wa Tanabata desu.","german":"July 7th is Tanabata.","furigana":"しちがつなのか|たなばた"},{"japanese":"七月から夏休みが始まります。","romaji":"Shichigatsu kara natsuyasumi ga hajimarimasu.","german":"Summer vacation starts in July.","furigana":"しちがつ|なつやす|はじ"}],"levelNote":"Compound: 月 (N5)"},
{"notes":"Read regularly: はち + がつ. The Obon festival（お盆）falls in August, when many people travel back to their hometowns.","examples":[{"japanese":"八月はとても暑いです。","romaji":"Hachigatsu wa totemo atsui desu.","german":"August is very hot.","furigana":"はちがつ|あつ"},{"japanese":"八月の中ごろに国へ帰ります。","romaji":"Hachigatsu no nakagoro ni kuni e kaerimasu.","german":"I go back to my home country in the middle of August.","furigana":"はちがつ|なか|くに|かえ"}],"levelNote":"Compound: 月 (N5)"},
{"notes":"Read くがつ, not きゅうがつ - one of the irregular month readings, along with 四月（しがつ）and 七月（しちがつ）.","examples":[{"japanese":"九月に日本へ留学します。","romaji":"Kugatsu ni Nihon e ryūgaku shimasu.","german":"I will study abroad in Japan in September.","furigana":"くがつ|にほん|りゅうがく"},{"japanese":"九月になっても、まだ暑い日が続いています。","romaji":"Kugatsu ni natte mo, mada atsui hi ga tsuzuite imasu.","german":"Even in September, hot days still continue.","furigana":"くがつ|あつ|ひ|つづ"}],"levelNote":"Compound: 月 (N5)"},
{"notes":"Composed of 十一（eleven）and 月（がつ）. Do not confuse it with 十一日（じゅういちにち, the eleventh）.","examples":[{"japanese":"十一月になると、山の木が赤や黄色になります。","romaji":"Jūichigatsu ni naru to, yama no ki ga aka ya kiiro ni narimasu.","german":"When November comes, the trees in the mountains turn red and yellow.","furigana":"じゅういちがつ|やま|き|あか|きいろ"},{"japanese":"誕生日は十一月三日です。","romaji":"Tanjōbi wa jūichigatsu mikka desu.","german":"My birthday is November 3rd.","furigana":"たんじょうび|じゅういちがつみっか"}],"levelNote":"Compound: 月 (N5)"},
{"notes":"The last month of the year; December 31st is called 大晦日（おおみそか）. An old, poetic name is 師走（しわす）.","examples":[{"japanese":"十二月は仕事がとても忙しいです。","romaji":"Jūnigatsu wa shigoto ga totemo isogashii desu.","german":"I am very busy at work in December.","furigana":"じゅうにがつ|しごと|いそが"},{"japanese":"十二月三十一日に、家族でそばを食べます。","romaji":"Jūnigatsu sanjūichinichi ni, kazoku de soba o tabemasu.","german":"On December 31st, my family eats soba noodles.","furigana":"じゅうにがつさんじゅういちにち|かぞく|た"}],"levelNote":"Compound: 月 (N5)"},
{"notes":"Loanword in katakana. Long, thin things are counted with 本（ほん／ぼん／ぽん）: バナナ一本.","examples":[{"japanese":"朝ご飯にバナナを一本食べました。","romaji":"Asagohan ni banana o ippon tabemashita.","german":"I ate a banana for breakfast.","furigana":"あさ|はん|いっぽんた"},{"japanese":"このバナナはまだ緑色で、かたいです。","romaji":"Kono banana wa mada midoriiro de, katai desu.","german":"This banana is still green and hard.","furigana":"みどりいろ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Ländername in Katakana (von niederländisch „Duits“). Sprache: ドイツ語, Person: ドイツ人. Die alte Kanji-Schreibung 独逸 lebt nur in Abkürzungen weiter, z. B. 独 in 日独（にちどく）„japanisch-deutsch“.","examples":[{"japanese":"私はドイツから来ました。","romaji":"Watashi wa Doitsu kara kimashita.","german":"Ich komme aus Deutschland.","furigana":"わたし|き"},{"japanese":"ドイツのビールはとても有名です。","romaji":"Doitsu no bīru wa totemo yūmei desu.","german":"Deutsches Bier ist sehr berühmt.","furigana":"ゆうめい"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 1"},
{"notes":"Country name in katakana. Language: フランス語, person: フランス人. Abbreviation in compounds: 仏（ふつ）, e.g. 日仏.","examples":[{"japanese":"夏休みにフランスへ旅行します。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi ni Furansu e ryokō shimasu.","german":"I'm traveling to France during the summer vacation.","furigana":"なつやす|りょこう"},{"japanese":"フランスのパンはおいしいですね。","romaji":"Furansu no pan wa oishii desu ne.","german":"French bread is delicious, isn't it?"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 1"},
{"notes":"The usual name for the United Kingdom (formally and in newspapers 英国（えいこく）). The language is not called \"イギリス語\" but 英語.","examples":[{"japanese":"兄はイギリスの大学で勉強しています。","romaji":"Ani wa Igirisu no daigaku de benkyō shite imasu.","german":"My older brother is studying at a British university.","furigana":"あに|だいがく|べんきょう"},{"japanese":"イギリスではよく雨が降ります。","romaji":"Igirisu de wa yoku ame ga furimasu.","german":"It often rains in Britain.","furigana":"あめ|ふ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 1"},
{"notes":"Country name in katakana. Language: イタリア語, Italian food: イタリア料理.","examples":[{"japanese":"イタリア料理が大好きです。","romaji":"Itaria ryōri ga daisuki desu.","german":"I love Italian food.","furigana":"りょうり|だいす"},{"japanese":"来年、イタリアに行きたいです。","romaji":"Rainen, Itaria ni ikitai desu.","german":"I want to go to Italy next year.","furigana":"らいねん|い"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 3"},
{"notes":"Country name in katakana. Language: スペイン語 (already N5).","examples":[{"japanese":"スペインは日本より暖かいです。","romaji":"Supein wa Nihon yori atatakai desu.","german":"Spain is warmer than Japan.","furigana":"にほん|あたた"},{"japanese":"友達はスペインでサッカーを見ました。","romaji":"Tomodachi wa Supein de sakkā o mimashita.","german":"My friend watched a soccer game in Spain.","furigana":"ともだち|み"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 7"},
{"notes":"Land + 語（ご）= Sprache. Aber: „Englisch“ ist 英語, nicht イギリス語.","examples":[{"japanese":"ドイツ語を少し話せます。","romaji":"Doitsugo o sukoshi hanasemasu.","german":"Ich kann ein bisschen Deutsch.","furigana":"ご|すこ|はな"},{"japanese":"この本はドイツ語で書いてあります。","romaji":"Kono hon wa doitsugo de kaite arimasu.","german":"Dieses Buch ist auf Deutsch geschrieben.","furigana":"ほん|ご|か"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Usually Standard Chinese (Mandarin) is meant. Many kanji are shared, but pronunciation and grammar are completely different.","examples":[{"japanese":"中国語の発音は難しいです。","romaji":"Chūgokugo no hatsuon wa muzukashii desu.","german":"Chinese pronunciation is difficult.","furigana":"ちゅうごくご|はつおん|むずか"},{"japanese":"父は仕事で中国語を使います。","romaji":"Chichi wa shigoto de chūgokugo o tsukaimasu.","german":"My father uses Chinese at work.","furigana":"ちち|しごと|ちゅうごくご|つか"}],"levelNote":"Compound: 中国 (N5) + 語 (N5)"},
{"notes":"The usual term for the language spoken in South Korea; the more neutral, linguistic term is 朝鮮語（ちょうせんご）.","examples":[{"japanese":"韓国のドラマを見て、韓国語を覚えました。","romaji":"Kankoku no dorama o mite, kankokugo o oboemashita.","german":"I learned Korean by watching Korean dramas.","furigana":"かんこく|み|かんこくご|おぼ"},{"japanese":"駅の案内は英語と韓国語でも書いてあります。","romaji":"Eki no annai wa eigo to kankokugo demo kaite arimasu.","german":"The station signs are also written in English and Korean.","furigana":"えき|あんない|えいご|かんこくご|か"}],"levelNote":"Compound: 韓国 (N5) + 語 (N5)"},
{"notes":"Country + 語（ご）= language. Music terms such as ピアノ or テンポ come from Italian.","examples":[{"japanese":"大学でイタリア語の授業を取っています。","romaji":"Daigaku de itariago no jugyō o totte imasu.","german":"I'm taking an Italian class at university.","furigana":"だいがく|ご|じゅぎょう|と"},{"japanese":"「チャオ」はイタリア語のあいさつです。","romaji":"“Chao” wa itariago no aisatsu desu.","german":"\"Ciao\" is an Italian greeting.","furigana":"ご"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"外国 (foreign country) + 語 (language). \"To learn a foreign language\": 外国語を勉強する／学ぶ.","examples":[{"japanese":"外国語を勉強するのは楽しいです。","romaji":"Gaikokugo o benkyō suru no wa tanoshii desu.","german":"Learning foreign languages is fun.","furigana":"がいこくご|べんきょう|たの"},{"japanese":"この学校では二つの外国語を習います。","romaji":"Kono gakkō de wa futatsu no gaikokugo o naraimasu.","german":"At this school, you learn two foreign languages.","furigana":"がっこう|ふた|がいこくご|なら"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 13"},
{"notes":"Land + 人（じん）= Staatsangehörige(r). Höflicher bei Personen: ドイツの方（かた）.","examples":[{"japanese":"私はドイツ人です。","romaji":"Watashi wa doitsujin desu.","german":"Ich bin Deutsche(r).","furigana":"わたし|じん"},{"japanese":"あのドイツ人の先生はとても親切です。","romaji":"Ano doitsujin no sensei wa totemo shinsetsu desu.","german":"Der deutsche Lehrer dort ist sehr freundlich.","furigana":"じん|せんせい|しんせつ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Country + 人（じん）= national. More polite when speaking of a person: 中国の方（かた）.","examples":[{"japanese":"ルームメイトは中国人です。","romaji":"Rūmumeito wa chūgokujin desu.","german":"My roommate is Chinese.","furigana":"ちゅうごくじん"},{"japanese":"この店には中国人の観光客がたくさん来ます。","romaji":"Kono mise ni wa chūgokujin no kankōkyaku ga takusan kimasu.","german":"Many Chinese tourists come to this store.","furigana":"みせ|ちゅうごくじん|かんこうきゃく|き"}],"levelNote":"Compound: 中国 (N5) + 人 (N5)"},
{"notes":"Country + 人（じん）= national. More polite when speaking of a person: 韓国の方（かた）.","examples":[{"japanese":"隣の部屋に韓国人の留学生が住んでいます。","romaji":"Tonari no heya ni kankokujin no ryūgakusei ga sunde imasu.","german":"A Korean exchange student lives in the next room.","furigana":"となり|へや|かんこくじん|りゅうがくせい|す"},{"japanese":"彼は韓国人ですが、日本で生まれました。","romaji":"Kare wa kankokujin desu ga, Nihon de umaremashita.","german":"He is Korean, but he was born in Japan.","furigana":"かれ|かんこくじん|にほん|う"}],"levelNote":"Compound: 韓国 (N5) + 人 (N5)"},
{"notes":"Country + 人（じん）. Also read にっぽんじん, but in everyday speech it is usually にほんじん.","examples":[{"japanese":"日本人の友達がたくさんいます。","romaji":"Nihonjin no tomodachi ga takusan imasu.","german":"I have a lot of Japanese friends.","furigana":"にほんじん|ともだち"},{"japanese":"日本人はよくお辞儀をします。","romaji":"Nihonjin wa yoku ojigi o shimasu.","german":"Japanese people often bow.","furigana":"にほんじん|じぎ"}],"levelNote":"Compound: 日本 (N5) + 人 (N5)"},
{"notes":"Usually the USA is meant (officially アメリカ合衆国, short 米国（べいこく）). In compounds: 米, e.g. 日米 \"Japanese-American\".","examples":[{"japanese":"兄はアメリカに住んでいます。","romaji":"Ani wa Amerika ni sunde imasu.","german":"My older brother lives in America.","furigana":"あに|す"},{"japanese":"アメリカの映画をよく見ます。","romaji":"Amerika no eiga o yoku mimasu.","german":"I often watch American movies.","furigana":"えいが|み"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 1"},
{"notes":"Noun; as a verb コピーする (already N4). A photocopy, but also a copy made on a computer. Advertising copy: キャッチコピー.","examples":[{"japanese":"会議の資料のコピーを十部お願いします。","romaji":"Kaigi no shiryō no kopī o jūbu onegai shimasu.","german":"Please make ten copies of the meeting materials.","furigana":"かいぎ|しりょう|じゅうぶ|ねが"},{"japanese":"このコピーは字が薄くて読めません。","romaji":"Kono kopī wa ji ga usukute yomemasen.","german":"The print on this copy is too faint to read.","furigana":"じ|うす|よ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"Follows a quantity: 一人一つずつ (one each), 少しずつ (little by little). Formerly also written づつ.","examples":[{"japanese":"りんごを一人二つずつ取ってください。","romaji":"Ringo o hitori futatsu zutsu totte kudasai.","german":"Please take two apples each.","furigana":"ひとりふた|と"},{"japanese":"日本語が少しずつ分かるようになってきた。","romaji":"Nihongo ga sukoshi zutsu wakaru yō ni natte kita.","german":"I'm gradually coming to understand Japanese little by little.","furigana":"にほんご|すこ|わ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"Casual short form of さようなら. It often sounds final (\"farewell\"); in everyday life people more often part with じゃあね or またね.","examples":[{"japanese":"先生、さよなら。また明日。","romaji":"Sensei, sayonara. Mata ashita.","german":"Goodbye, teacher. See you tomorrow.","furigana":"せんせい|あした"},{"japanese":"さよならも言わずに、彼は町を出ていった。","romaji":"Sayonara mo iwazu ni, kare wa machi o dete itta.","german":"He left the town without even saying goodbye.","furigana":"い|かれ|まち|で"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Hesitant \"Um, excuse me ...\" - used to politely address someone or to buy time to think. Shorter: あの.","examples":[{"japanese":"あのう、すみません。駅はどこですか。","romaji":"Anō, sumimasen. Eki wa doko desu ka.","german":"Um, excuse me. Where is the station?","furigana":"えき"},{"japanese":"あのう、ちょっとお願いがあるんですが……。","romaji":"Anō, chotto onegai ga aru n desu ga...","german":"Um, I have a small favor to ask ...","furigana":"ねが"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 2"},
{"notes":"A polite apology for a small disturbance or a mistake, also when leaving a room (for example, the staff room). The present tense 失礼します is used when entering.","examples":[{"japanese":"番号を間違えました。失礼しました。","romaji":"Bangō o machigaemashita. Shitsurei shimashita.","german":"I dialed the wrong number. I apologize.","furigana":"ばんごう|まちが|しつれい"},{"japanese":"「失礼しました」と言って、職員室を出た。","romaji":"\"Shitsurei shimashita\" to itte, shokuinshitsu o deta.","german":"I said \"Sorry to have disturbed you\" and left the staff room.","furigana":"しつれい|い|しょくいんしつ|で"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 20"},
{"notes":"\"See you then\" - somewhat more formal than じゃあ、また. Often used at the end of emails or conversations with acquaintances.","examples":[{"japanese":"ではまた、来週お会いしましょう。","romaji":"Dewa mata, raishū oai shimashō.","german":"Well then, let's meet next week.","furigana":"らいしゅう|あ"},{"japanese":"今日はありがとうございました。ではまた。","romaji":"Kyō wa arigatō gozaimashita. Dewa mata.","german":"Thank you for today. See you then.","furigana":"きょう"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"\"How are you?\" - only used when you haven't seen each other for a while, not as an everyday greeting. Answer: はい、元気です／おかげさまで.","examples":[{"japanese":"お久しぶりです。お元気ですか。","romaji":"Ohisashiburi desu. Ogenki desu ka.","german":"Long time no see. How are you?","furigana":"ひさ|げんき"},{"japanese":"手紙の最初に「お元気ですか」と書いた。","romaji":"Tegami no saisho ni \"ogenki desu ka\" to kaita.","german":"At the beginning of the letter I wrote \"How are you?\".","furigana":"てがみ|さいしょ|げんき|か"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Beginning, start. 初め is used more for points in time (月の初め = beginning of the month), 始め for the start of an action (仕事始め). 初めに = first of all; do not confuse it with 初めて (for the first time).","examples":[{"japanese":"来月の初めに引っ越します。","romaji":"Raigetsu no hajime ni hikkoshimasu.","german":"I'm moving at the beginning of next month.","furigana":"らいげつ|はじ|ひ|こ"},{"japanese":"初めはよく分からなかったが、だんだん慣れてきた。","romaji":"Hajime wa yoku wakaranakatta ga, dandan narete kita.","german":"At first I didn't understand much, but gradually I got used to it.","furigana":"はじ|わ|な"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 20"},
{"notes":"Match (the kanji 燐寸 is very rare); nowadays mostly replaced by lighters (ライター). Same-sounding words: マッチ \"contest\" (タイトルマッチ) and マッチする \"to match; go well together\".","examples":[{"japanese":"マッチでろうそくに火をつけた。","romaji":"Matchi de rōsoku ni hi o tsuketa.","german":"I lit the candle with a match.","furigana":"ひ"},{"japanese":"キャンプでマッチを使って火をおこした。","romaji":"Kyanpu de matchi o tsukatte hi o okoshita.","german":"While camping we started a fire using matches.","furigana":"つか|ひ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"Film for cameras, but also a thin protective film (保護フィルム = screen protector for a phone). A movie is called 映画.","examples":[{"japanese":"祖父の古いカメラにはフィルムが必要です。","romaji":"Sofu no furui kamera ni wa firumu ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"My grandfather's old camera needs film.","furigana":"そふ|ふる|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"スマホの画面に保護フィルムを貼った。","romaji":"Sumaho no gamen ni hogo firumu o hatta.","german":"I put a screen protector on my phone.","furigana":"がめん|ほご|は"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 10"},
{"notes":"Phonograph record; also a record in sports (but 世界記録 is more common than ワールドレコード).","examples":[{"japanese":"父は古いレコードをたくさん持っています。","romaji":"Chichi wa furui rekōdo o takusan motte imasu.","german":"My father has a lot of old records.","furigana":"ちち|ふる|も"},{"japanese":"最近またレコードで音楽を聴く人が増えている。","romaji":"Saikin mata rekōdo de ongaku o kiku hito ga fuete iru.","german":"Recently more people are listening to music on records again.","furigana":"さいきん|おんがく|き|ひと|ふ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"Fruit and color; as a color also オレンジ色. Mandarins are called みかん.","examples":[{"japanese":"朝ご飯にオレンジを一つ食べた。","romaji":"Asagohan ni orenji o hitotsu tabeta.","german":"I ate an orange for breakfast.","furigana":"あさ|はん|ひと|た"},{"japanese":"オレンジのセーターを着ている人が私の姉です。","romaji":"Orenji no sētā o kite iru hito ga watashi no ane desu.","german":"The person in the orange sweater is my older sister.","furigana":"き|ひと|わたし|あね"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"字引（じびき）, also written 字引き, is an older term for a dictionary. In everyday use today 辞書 is the more common choice. 字引で調べる means \"to look something up in a dictionary\". The word is still understood; the label does not mean it is unusable or purely historical.","examples":[{"japanese":"わからない言葉を字引で調べます。","romaji":"Wakaranai kotoba o jibiki de shirabemasu.","german":"I look up words I don't know in the dictionary.","furigana":"ことば|じびき|しら"},{"japanese":"祖父の字引は大きくて、重いです。","romaji":"Sofu no jibiki wa ōkikute, omoi desu.","german":"My grandfather's dictionary is big and heavy.","furigana":"そふ|じびき|おお|おも"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"私 is read わたくし here. This self-reference is more formal than わたし and suits, for example, a professional introduction or a public speech. It is used by people of all genders. As a pronoun, わたくし can be followed by は or が. In the first example someone introduces themselves; in the second, が emphasizes who wrote the letter. The reading わたし remains another common self-reference.","examples":[{"japanese":"わたくしは田中です。","romaji":"Watakushi wa Tanaka desu.","german":"My name is Tanaka.","furigana":"たなか"},{"japanese":"その手紙は、わたくしが書きました。","romaji":"Sono tegami wa, watakushi ga kakimashita.","german":"I wrote that letter.","furigana":"てがみ|か"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 50"},
{"notes":"一月 is read ひとつき here and denotes the duration of one month. The same kanji spelling can denote January when read いちがつ; the reading and context decide. The also attested spelling ひと月 makes the duration easier to recognize. 一月に一度 means once a month, while a span of time placed before an activity gives its duration.","examples":[{"japanese":"兄は一月、京都に住んでいました。","romaji":"Ani wa hitotsuki, Kyōto ni sunde imashita.","german":"My older brother lived in Kyoto for a month.","furigana":"あに|ひとつき|きょうと|す"},{"japanese":"一月に一度、部屋の窓を全部掃除します。","romaji":"Hitotsuki ni ichido, heya no mado o zenbu sōji shimasu.","german":"Once a month I clean all the windows of the room.","furigana":"ひとつき|いちど|へや|まど|ぜんぶそうじ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"回 counts how often an action or event occurs. After a number, 一回 means once and 三回 three times; 一回 is pronounced いっかい. 週に三回 gives the frequency per week. もう一回 asks for a repetition. This counter counts occurrences; the identically read words 階 and 貝 have other meanings.","examples":[{"japanese":"週に三回、図書館へ行きます。","romaji":"Shū ni sankai, toshokan e ikimasu.","german":"I go to the library three times a week.","furigana":"しゅう|さんかい|としょかん|い"},{"japanese":"もう一回、名前を言ってください。","romaji":"Mō ikkai, namae o itte kudasai.","german":"Please say your name one more time.","furigana":"いっかい|なまえ|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"Here くらい follows a quantity or time expression and means \"about\": 十五分くらい, 三千円くらい. The pronunciation ぐらい also occurs. This card practices the reading くらい and the approximate quantity; other uses for a degree or comparison are not covered here. Kana is the usual way to write it. Distinguish it from 暗い \"dark\" and the noun 位 \"rank/place value\".","examples":[{"japanese":"駅まで歩いて十五分くらいかかります。","romaji":"Eki made aruite jūgofun kurai kakarimasu.","german":"It takes about fifteen minutes to walk to the station.","furigana":"えき|ある|じゅうごふん"},{"japanese":"このかばんは三千円くらいでした。","romaji":"Kono kaban wa sanzen’en kurai deshita.","german":"This bag cost about three thousand yen.","furigana":"さんぜんえん"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"人 is read にん here as the counter for people: 三人 さんにん, 四人 よにん. One person is 一人 ひとり, two people 二人 ふたり. 何人 なんにん asks \"how many people?\". The number with 人 can follow the noun it belongs to: 学生が三人います. 何人で describes the size of a group doing something together. The standalone noun 人 \"person\" has the other reading ひと.","examples":[{"japanese":"この教室には学生が三人います。","romaji":"Kono kyōshitsu ni wa gakusei ga sannin imasu.","german":"There are three students in this classroom.","furigana":"きょうしつ|がくせい|さんにん"},{"japanese":"旅行には何人で行きますか。","romaji":"Ryokō ni wa nannin de ikimasu ka.","german":"How many people are you traveling with?","furigana":"りょこう|なんにん|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"冊 counts bound books, notebooks, or individual volumes of a work. 二冊 にさつ means \"two books/volumes\" and 三冊 さんさつ \"three books/volumes\". One is 一冊 いっさつ; the double s in the romanization stands for a geminate consonant. 本を二冊借りる states the number of books borrowed; 全部で三冊 states the total number of volumes. The similar-sounding 札 さつ belongs to banknotes and is a different word.","examples":[{"japanese":"図書館で本を二冊借りました。","romaji":"Toshokan de hon o nisatsu karimashita.","german":"I borrowed two books from the library.","furigana":"としょかん|ほん|にさつか"},{"japanese":"この小説は全部で三冊あります。","romaji":"Kono shōsetsu wa zenbu de sansatsu arimasu.","german":"This novel has three volumes in total.","furigana":"しょうせつ|ぜんぶ|さんさつ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 14"},
{"notes":"ゆく and いく can both mean \"to go\". ゆく sounds more literary than いく; this reading is deliberately practiced here. The destination takes へ or に, and a path traveled along takes を. The polite form is ゆきます. For the present-day て-form and simple past, use いって and いった, not ゆいて and ゆいた.","examples":[{"japanese":"私は北の町へ行く。","romaji":"Watashi wa kita no machi e yuku.","german":"I am going to the town in the north.","furigana":"わたし|きた|まち|ゆ"},{"japanese":"旅人は細い山道を行きます。","romaji":"Tabibito wa hosoi yamamichi o yukimasu.","german":"The traveler goes along a narrow mountain path.","furigana":"たびびと|ほそ|やまみち|ゆ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"背（せい）means body height here. 背が高い means \"to be tall\", 背を測る \"to measure one's height\". せい sounds longer than せ, another common reading of 背. The meaning \"back\" does not belong to the reading せい learned here.","examples":[{"japanese":"弟は私より背が高いです。","romaji":"Otōto wa watashi yori sei ga takai desu.","german":"My younger brother is taller than me.","furigana":"おとうと|わたし|せい|たか"},{"japanese":"子供の背を毎月測っています。","romaji":"Kodomo no sei o maigetsu hakatte imasu.","german":"I measure the child's height every month.","furigana":"こども|せい|まいげつはか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"まいげつ is a reading of 毎月 alongside まいつき and means \"every month\". As a time expression, the word can stand directly before the sentence. If you also name a specific day, に follows that day: 毎月十日に. \"For a month\", by contrast, is 一か月; 毎月 describes repetition.","examples":[{"japanese":"毎月、家賃を払います。","romaji":"Maigetsu, yachin o haraimasu.","german":"I pay rent every month.","furigana":"まいげつ|やちん|はら"},{"japanese":"この雑誌は毎月十日に届きます。","romaji":"Kono zasshi wa maigetsu tōka ni todokimasu.","german":"This magazine arrives on the tenth of every month.","furigana":"ざっし|まいげつとおか|とど"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"まいねん is a reading of 毎年 alongside まいとし. The word denotes something that repeats every year. Before a noun, の is used, as in 毎年の旅行 for \"the annual trip\". For a duration of one year, use 一年 or 一年間; 毎年 does not denote a duration.","examples":[{"japanese":"毎年、夏に海へ行きます。","romaji":"Mainen, natsu ni umi e ikimasu.","german":"I go to the sea every year in summer.","furigana":"まいねん|なつ|うみ|い"},{"japanese":"毎年の旅行を楽しみにしています。","romaji":"Mainen no ryokō o tanoshimi ni shite imasu.","german":"I'm looking forward to our annual trip.","furigana":"まいねん|りょこう|たの"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"うち often means one's own home or household and is frequently written in hiragana. 家で means \"at home\" here, and 私の家に \"to my place\". The family can also be meant as a unit: 家は四人家族です. The reading いえ often denotes the house as a building; うち often emphasizes the connection to the people living there.","examples":[{"japanese":"今日は家で夕ご飯を食べます。","romaji":"Kyō wa uchi de yūgohan o tabemasu.","german":"I'm having dinner at home today.","furigana":"きょう|うち|ゆう|はん|た"},{"japanese":"今度、私の家に遊びに来てください。","romaji":"Kondo, watashi no uchi ni asobi ni kite kudasai.","german":"Please come over to my place sometime soon.","furigana":"こんど|わたし|うち|あそ|き"},{"japanese":"家は四人家族です。","romaji":"Uchi wa yonin kazoku desu.","german":"My household is a family of four.","furigana":"うち|よにんかぞく"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 3"},
{"notes":"何ですか is usually read なんですか. Before a counter, なん asks for the number, as in 何回（なんかい）for \"how many times\". The reading depends on the combination, so you cannot replace なに with なん everywhere. 何を in a question about the object is normally read なにを.","examples":[{"japanese":"これは何ですか。","romaji":"Kore wa nan desu ka.","german":"What is this?","furigana":"なん"},{"japanese":"週に何回、運動しますか。","romaji":"Shū ni nankai, undō shimasu ka.","german":"How many times a week do you exercise?","furigana":"しゅう|なんかい|うんどう"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 2"},
{"notes":"年（ねん）follows numbers in years of the calendar and when counting years. 三年 is read さんねん. Without a preceding number, ねん appears, for example, in 年に一度: \"once a year\". For the year as an independent time noun, 年（とし）is also common. This card does not replace the different reading とし.","examples":[{"japanese":"年に一度、家族で写真を撮ります。","romaji":"Nen ni ichido, kazoku de shashin o torimasu.","german":"Once a year, my family takes a photo together.","furigana":"ねん|いちど|かぞく|しゃしん|と"},{"japanese":"日本に三年住んでいました。","romaji":"Nihon ni sannen sunde imashita.","german":"I lived in Japan for three years.","furigana":"にほん|さんねんす"},{"japanese":"二〇二五年に日本へ行きました。","romaji":"Nisen nijūgonen ni Nihon e ikimashita.","german":"I went to Japan in 2025.","furigana":"|にほん|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"がつ denotes the month within the calendar year: 四月（しがつ）is April, and 何月（なんがつ）asks \"which month?\". After a month name, に is often used to give a point in time. For the duration \"four months\", say 四か月（よんかげつ）. The moon, by contrast, is read 月（つき）.","examples":[{"japanese":"四月に新しい学校へ行きます。","romaji":"Shigatsu ni atarashii gakkō e ikimasu.","german":"I'm going to a new school in April.","furigana":"しがつ|あたら|がっこう|い"},{"japanese":"誕生日は何月ですか。","romaji":"Tanjōbi wa nangatsu desu ka.","german":"What month is your birthday?","furigana":"たんじょうび|なんがつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"中（ちゅう）denotes medium size or a middle rank here. For sizes, you can contrast 大・中・小（だい・ちゅう・しょう）. As a noun, 中 alone can name the chosen item, as in 中を一つ. This meaning is to be distinguished from the suffix in 工事中 or 授業中.","examples":[{"japanese":"コーヒーは中を一つください。","romaji":"Kōhī wa chū o hitotsu kudasai.","german":"One medium coffee, please.","furigana":"ちゅう|ひと"},{"japanese":"テストの成績は中でした。","romaji":"Tesuto no seiseki wa chū deshita.","german":"My test results were average.","furigana":"せいせき|ちゅう"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 33"},
{"notes":"Here がる attaches to the stem of an adjective and describes visible signs of a feeling, often in another person. With 寒い the い drops: 寒がる. With 嫌な the stem 嫌 remains: 嫌がる. The resulting verbs are godan verbs: 寒がります, 嫌がっている. It describes an observed reaction; you are not claiming to know the other person's thoughts for certain. With 得意がる the combination means that someone acts proud. The word part がる does not stand alone in this use.","examples":[{"japanese":"弟は寒がって、窓を閉めました。","romaji":"Otōto wa samugatte, mado o shimemashita.","german":"My younger brother acted cold and closed the window.","furigana":"おとうと|さむ|まど|し"},{"japanese":"妹はこの薬を飲むのを嫌がっています。","romaji":"Imōto wa kono kusuri o nomu no o iyagatte imasu.","german":"My younger sister is reluctant to take this medicine.","furigana":"いもうと|くすり|の|いや"},{"japanese":"彼は少し褒められると、すぐ得意がります。","romaji":"Kare wa sukoshi homerareru to, sugu tokuigarimasu.","german":"If he is praised even a little, he immediately acts proud.","furigana":"かれ|すこ|ほ|とくい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"番（ばん）follows a number and denotes a number within a series. 二番のバス is the bus with the number two; 問題の三番 names question number three. The number and 番 form a unit. For the order of people there is also 私の番 \"it's my turn\"; this separate meaning is not practiced here.","examples":[{"japanese":"二番のバスに乗ってください。","romaji":"Niban no basu ni notte kudasai.","german":"Please take bus number two.","furigana":"にばん|の"},{"japanese":"問題の三番がわかりません。","romaji":"Mondai no sanban ga wakarimasen.","german":"I don't understand question number three.","furigana":"もんだい|さんばん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"個（こ）counts small individual things, such as eggs or items in a box. It follows the number: 二個 is two pieces, and 一個 is read いっこ. The quantity can stand directly before を or follow the item named. For people, other counters are used. Only the counting of things is practiced here.","examples":[{"japanese":"卵を二個買いました。","romaji":"Tamago o niko kaimashita.","german":"I bought two eggs.","furigana":"たまご|にこか"},{"japanese":"箱には一個しか入っていません。","romaji":"Hako ni wa ikko shika haitte imasen.","german":"There is only one piece in the box.","furigana":"はこ|いっこ|はい"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 14"},
{"notes":"語（ご）as a noun denotes a word or a term. この語 names a particular word, for example when asking about its meaning; 別の語 means a different word. This use is common in explanations about language and text. The suffix for languages, as in 日本語, has its own card.","examples":[{"japanese":"この語の意味を教えてください。","romaji":"Kono go no imi o oshiete kudasai.","german":"Please tell me the meaning of this word.","furigana":"ご|いみ|おし"},{"japanese":"同じ語を何度も使わず、別の語に変えました。","romaji":"Onaji go o nando mo tsukawazu, betsu no go ni kaemashita.","german":"I changed the word so as not to keep using the same one.","furigana":"おな|ご|なんど|つか|べつ|ご|か"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 2"},
{"notes":"語（ご）follows the name of a country or language and forms the name of a language. 日本語 means Japanese, フランス語 French. The suffix describes the language, not the nationality of a person; 日本人 is a different word for that. The use as a free noun meaning \"word\" is practiced separately.","examples":[{"japanese":"日本語で名前を書いてください。","romaji":"Nihongo de namae o kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write your name in Japanese.","furigana":"にほんご|なまえ|か"},{"japanese":"この映画はフランス語ですが、日本語の字幕があります。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa Furansugo desu ga, Nihongo no jimaku ga arimasu.","german":"This movie is in French, but it has Japanese subtitles.","furigana":"えいが|ご|にほんご|じまく"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 2"},
{"notes":"ああ expresses a feeling as an exclamation, such as disappointment or relief. The tone and the following sentence show what is meant. 嗚呼 is a rare, rather literary spelling; in everyday writing, ああ is usually used. A short pause often follows the exclamation. Distinguish this use from ああ meaning \"that way\"; it does not replace a polite answer to a question.","examples":[{"japanese":"ああ、楽しみにしていた試合が中止になってしまった。","romaji":"Ā, tanoshimi ni shite ita shiai ga chūshi ni natte shimatta.","german":"Ah, the game I was looking forward to was canceled.","furigana":"たの|しあい|ちゅうし"},{"japanese":"ああ、無事に帰ってきてくれてよかった。","romaji":"Ā, buji ni kaette kite kurete yokatta.","german":"Ah, I'm so glad you came back safely.","furigana":"ぶじ|かえ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"週間 states a duration in weeks and follows a number: 一週間（いっしゅうかん）, 二週間（にしゅうかん）. It denotes a span of time, whereas 週 can also denote a particular calendar week. 一週間以内 means \"within a week\". The identically read 習慣 means \"habit\".","examples":[{"japanese":"日本に三週間いました。","romaji":"Nihon ni sanshūkan imashita.","german":"I was in Japan for three weeks.","furigana":"にほん|さんしゅうかん"},{"japanese":"この宿題は二週間以内に出してください。","romaji":"Kono shukudai wa nishūkan inai ni dashite kudasai.","german":"Please hand in this homework within two weeks.","furigana":"しゅくだい|にしゅうかんいない|だ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"台 denotes a base or platform on which something stands or on which you put something. With 台の上に you name a place on this base. In 作業台, for example, it denotes a work surface. The same spelling can also serve as a counter for machines and vehicles; that is a different use.","examples":[{"japanese":"この台の上に箱を置いてください。","romaji":"Kono dai no ue ni hako o oite kudasai.","german":"Please put the box on this stand.","furigana":"だい|うえ|はこ|お"},{"japanese":"この台は低いので、上の棚に手が届きません。","romaji":"Kono dai wa hikui node, ue no tana ni te ga todokimasen.","german":"This platform is too low, so I can't reach the upper shelf.","furigana":"だい|ひく|うえ|たな|て|とど"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 14"},
{"notes":"日 is used in calendars and opening hours as a short form of 日曜日, Sunday. This abbreviation appears together with the other days of the week, for example 土・日 for Saturday and Sunday. The reading にち belongs to the weekday name here; the word 日 meaning \"day\" or \"sun\" is read ひ in other contexts.","examples":[{"japanese":"この予定表では、「日」は日曜日を表しています。","romaji":"Kono yoteihyō de wa, \"nichi\" wa nichiyōbi o arawashite imasu.","german":"In this schedule, \"日\" stands for Sunday.","furigana":"よていひょう|にち|にちようび|あらわ"},{"japanese":"営業時間の欄に「日：休み」と書いてあります。","romaji":"Eigyō jikan no ran ni \"nichi: yasumi\" to kaite arimasu.","german":"The opening hours column says \"Sun: closed\".","furigana":"えいぎょうじかん|らん|にち|やす|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"側 is read がわ here and denotes a side. Combined with other words you get, for example, 右側（みぎがわ）, the right side, and 店の側, the store's side as a party involved. どちら側 asks \"which side?\". The reading そば of the same kanji, by contrast, means \"nearby\" or \"next to\".","examples":[{"japanese":"道の右側を歩いてください。","romaji":"Michi no migigawa o aruite kudasai.","german":"Please walk on the right side of the road.","furigana":"みち|みぎがわ|ある"},{"japanese":"店の側と客の側で、意見が違いました。","romaji":"Mise no gawa to kyaku no gawa de, iken ga chigaimashita.","german":"The store's side and the customer's side had different opinions.","furigana":"みせ|がわ|きゃく|がわ|いけん|ちが"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 29"},
{"notes":"御 is read ご in this use and is mostly written in kana. The prefix makes a word more polite or expresses respect: ご住所, ご家族, ご連絡. It often stands before words of Chinese origin, but it cannot be added to just any noun. In ご連絡ありがとうございます, the thanks go to the other person's message. お is a related prefix with other common combinations.","examples":[{"japanese":"ご住所とお名前を書いてください。","romaji":"Gojūsho to onamae o kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write your address and name.","furigana":"じゅうしょ|なまえ|か"},{"japanese":"ご連絡ありがとうございます。","romaji":"Gorenraku arigatō gozaimasu.","german":"Thank you for your message.","furigana":"れんらく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"人 wird als diese Nachsilbe じん gelesen. Nach einem Ländernamen nennt es die Herkunft oder Nationalität: ドイツ人, 日本人. Auch ein Tätigkeitsbereich kann davorstehen: 芸能人 bezeichnet jemanden aus der Unterhaltungsbranche. Es ist hier kein frei stehendes Wort für „Mensch“; dafür verwendet man 人（ひと）. Beim Zählen von Personen lautet die Grundlesung にん.","examples":[{"japanese":"私はドイツ人です。","romaji":"Watashi wa Doitsujin desu.","german":"Ich bin Deutscher beziehungsweise Deutsche.","furigana":"わたし|じん"},{"japanese":"あの芸能人は歌も演技も上手です。","romaji":"Ano geinōjin wa uta mo engi mo jōzu desu.","german":"Jener Star ist sowohl im Singen als auch im Schauspielern gut.","furigana":"げいのうじん|うた|えんぎ|じょうず"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 1"},
{"notes":"匹 counts smaller animals here, for example cats, dogs, or fish. After a number the initial sound often changes: 一匹（いっぴき）, 二匹（にひき）, 三匹（さんびき）, 六匹（ろっぴき）. The combination states the number; 猫が三匹います means that there are three cats. For people, use 人.","examples":[{"japanese":"庭に猫が三匹います。","romaji":"Niwa ni neko ga sanbiki imasu.","german":"There are three cats in the garden.","furigana":"にわ|ねこ|"},{"japanese":"魚を二匹買いました。","romaji":"Sakana o nihiki kaimashita.","german":"I bought two fish.","furigana":"さかな|にひきか"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 14"},
{"notes":"枚 counts thin, flat objects, such as sheets of paper, plates, or tickets. The number comes first: 一枚（いちまい）, 三枚（さんまい）. The initial sound of まい stays unchanged. 紙を二枚ください orders two sheets of paper; 切符が二枚あります describes two tickets being on hand.","examples":[{"japanese":"紙を三枚ください。","romaji":"Kami o sanmai kudasai.","german":"Please give me three sheets of paper.","furigana":"かみ|さんまい"},{"japanese":"切符は二枚買いましたが、友達が一人来られなくなりました。","romaji":"Kippu wa nimai kaimashita ga, tomodachi ga hitori korarenaku narimashita.","german":"I bought two tickets, but one friend can't come after all.","furigana":"きっぷ|にまいか|ともだち|ひとりこ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 5"},
{"notes":"In this use it is usually written ながら. It attaches to the stem before ます: 聞きます → 聞きながら. It connects two actions by the same person that take place at the same time. The action at the end of the sentence is usually the main statement: 音楽を聞きながら勉強する means that you listen to music while studying. Other meanings, such as \"although\", depend on the construction.","examples":[{"japanese":"音楽を聞きながら料理をします。","romaji":"Ongaku o kikinagara ryōri o shimasu.","german":"I cook while listening to music.","furigana":"おんがく|き|りょうり"},{"japanese":"父は新聞を読みながら朝ご飯を食べています。","romaji":"Chichi wa shinbun o yominagara asagohan o tabete imasu.","german":"My father eats breakfast while reading the newspaper.","furigana":"ちち|しんぶん|よ|あさ|はん|た"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"か月 counts a duration or number of months; it does not name a month of the year. It follows a number and also appears as ヶ月, カ月, or 箇月. Common forms are 一か月（いっかげつ）, 三か月（さんかげつ）, and 六か月（ろっかげつ）. 三月（さんがつ）, by contrast, means March. For a duration, 間 can follow: 三か月間.","examples":[{"japanese":"日本語を三か月勉強しました。","romaji":"Nihongo o sankagetsu benkyō shimashita.","german":"I studied Japanese for three months.","furigana":"にほんご|さん|げつべんきょう"},{"japanese":"この券は買った日から一か月間使えます。","romaji":"Kono ken wa katta hi kara ikkagetsukan tsukaemasu.","german":"You can use this ticket for one month from the day of purchase.","furigana":"けん|か|ひ|いっ|げつかんつか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"か marks a question at the end of a sentence here: 行きますか. In polite questions it often follows です or ます. It does not change the verb and needs no question mark in a Japanese sentence. In casual speech, rising intonation alone can also indicate a question. A curt か after a plain verb form can sound much more direct than a polite question, depending on the situation.","examples":[{"japanese":"この席は空いていますか。","romaji":"Kono seki wa aite imasu ka.","german":"Is this seat free?","furigana":"せき|あ"},{"japanese":"明日は何時に来ますか。","romaji":"Ashita wa nanji ni kimasu ka.","german":"What time are you coming tomorrow?","furigana":"あした|なんじ|き"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"どうもありがとうございます is a polite, complete expression of thanks. どうも intensifies the thanks compared with ありがとうございます. In letters and messages as well as in conversation, the kana spelling is usual; 有難う is also read ありがとう. With an occasion placed before it, the thanks becomes specific: ご招待どうもありがとうございます. For something already completed, ありがとうございました is also used.","examples":[{"japanese":"荷物を持ってくださって、どうもありがとうございます。","romaji":"Nimotsu o motte kudasatte, dōmo arigatō gozaimasu.","german":"Thank you very much for carrying my luggage.","furigana":"にもつ|も"},{"japanese":"ご招待どうもありがとうございます。ぜひ伺います。","romaji":"Goshōtai dōmo arigatō gozaimasu. Zehi ukagaimasu.","german":"Thank you very much for your invitation. I would be glad to come.","furigana":"しょうたい|うかが"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"鶏肉 can be read けいにく as well as とりにく. The reading けいにく consists of the Chinese readings of 鶏 and 肉 and denotes the same chicken meat. In spoken pronunciation, えい usually becomes a long e; the romanization of this reading is keiniku. The spelling 鳥肉 with the more general kanji 鳥 has other readings and also denotes the meat of other birds.","examples":[{"japanese":"この資料では、鶏肉の生産量を調べています。","romaji":"Kono shiryō de wa, keiniku no seisanryō o shirabete imasu.","german":"This document examines the production volume of chicken meat.","furigana":"しりょう|けいにく|せいさんりょう|しら"},{"japanese":"鶏肉と豚肉を別々の袋に入れてください。","romaji":"Keiniku to butaniku o betsubetsu no fukuro ni irete kudasai.","german":"Please put the chicken and the pork in separate bags.","furigana":"けいにく|ぶたにく|べつべつ|ふくろ|い"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"リッパー here means a seam ripper for cutting sewing thread. It is used, for example, to undo a wrongly sewn seam or to remove a button. リッパーで names the tool used. The small ッ doubles the following p sound, and ー lengthens the last vowel: rippā. Other fields use the same loanword for other devices or software.","examples":[{"japanese":"間違えた縫い目をリッパーでほどきました。","romaji":"Machigaeta nuime o rippā de hodokimashita.","german":"I undid the wrongly sewn seam with a seam ripper.","furigana":"まちが|ぬ|め"},{"japanese":"ボタンの糸を切るために、リッパーを貸してください。","romaji":"Botan no ito o kiru tame ni, rippā o kashite kudasai.","german":"Please lend me a seam ripper so I can cut the thread of the button.","furigana":"いと|き|か"}],"levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"notes":"パーティ is a form without the long final i of パーティー. The word can denote a celebration or a group doing something together, especially in mountain climbing: パーティを組む means to form such a group. The meaning \"political party\" is also attested; in present-day general usage, 政党 is clearer for that. Context decides which meaning is intended.","examples":[{"japanese":"友達の家でパーティを開きます。","romaji":"Tomodachi no ie de pāti o hirakimasu.","german":"We're having a party at a friend's house.","furigana":"ともだち|いえ|ひら"},{"japanese":"三人でパーティを組んで、山に登りました。","romaji":"Sannin de pāti o kunde, yama ni noborimashita.","german":"The three of us formed a party and climbed a mountain.","furigana":"さんにん|く|やま|のぼ"},{"japanese":"この本では、政党のことを「パーティ」と呼んでいます。","romaji":"Kono hon de wa, seitō no koto o \"pāti\" to yonde imasu.","german":"In this book, political parties are called \"パーティ\".","furigana":"ほん|せいとう|よ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"ラジカセ is the short form of ラジオカセット: a device combining a radio and a cassette recorder. ラジカセで音楽を聞く names the device as the means of listening. ラジカセで録音する describes a recording made with the device; 録音する means to record. An ordinary radio without a cassette function is called ラジオ.","examples":[{"japanese":"父の古いラジカセで音楽を聞いています。","romaji":"Chichi no furui rajikase de ongaku o kiite imasu.","german":"I listen to music on my father's old radio cassette player.","furigana":"ちち|ふる|おんがく|き"},{"japanese":"ラジカセで、自分の歌を録音しました。","romaji":"Rajikase de, jibun no uta o rokuon shimashita.","german":"I recorded my own singing on the radio cassette player.","furigana":"じぶん|うた|ろくおん"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 18"},
{"notes":"ラジオカセット is the full name for a combined radio and cassette recorder; in conversation it is often shortened to ラジカセ. カセットを入れる describes inserting a cassette, ラジオカセットが壊れる a malfunction of the device. The term refers to the whole device, whereas カセット alone denotes the cassette or a module.","examples":[{"japanese":"ラジオカセットにカセットを入れて、再生ボタンを押しました。","romaji":"Rajio kasetto ni kasetto o irete, saisei botan o oshimashita.","german":"I put a cassette into the radio cassette player and pressed the play button.","furigana":"い|さいせい|お"},{"japanese":"ラジオカセットが壊れたので、修理を頼みました。","romaji":"Rajio kasetto ga kowareta node, shūri o tanomimashita.","german":"Because the radio cassette player was broken, I asked for it to be repaired.","furigana":"こわ|しゅうり|たの"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"一昨日 is read いっさくじつ here and means the day before yesterday. This Sino-Japanese reading suits written notices or reports; in everyday conversation おととい is usual. Both readings refer to the same day. 一昨日の refers to something from that day, 一昨日から to a start on that day. The small っ doubles the s sound: issakujitsu.","examples":[{"japanese":"一昨日の会議で、新しい計画が決まりました。","romaji":"Issakujitsu no kaigi de, atarashii keikaku ga kimarimashita.","german":"At the meeting the day before yesterday, a new plan was decided.","furigana":"いっさくじつ|かいぎ|あたら|けいかく|き"},{"japanese":"一昨日から、図書館の利用時間が変わりました。","romaji":"Issakujitsu kara, toshokan no riyō jikan ga kawarimashita.","german":"Since the day before yesterday, the library's opening hours have changed.","furigana":"いっさくじつ|としょかん|りようじかん|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"一昨年 is read いっさくねん here and means the year before last year, i.e. two years ago. The reading suits reports and retrospectives; in everyday conversation おととし is usual. 一昨年の assigns something to that year. 一昨年から names a start two years ago. It refers to the calendar year, not necessarily a distance of exactly 24 months. The small っ doubles the s sound: issakunen.","examples":[{"japanese":"一昨年の売上は、昨年より多かったです。","romaji":"Issakunen no uriage wa, sakunen yori ōkatta desu.","german":"Sales two years ago were higher than last year.","furigana":"いっさくねん|うりあげ|さくねん|おお"},{"japanese":"この会社は一昨年から海外にも商品を売っています。","romaji":"Kono kaisha wa issakunen kara kaigai ni mo shōhin o utte imasu.","german":"This company has been selling its products overseas since the year before last.","furigana":"かいしゃ|いっさくねん|かいがい|しょうひん|う"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2 (source: N2; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"十 is read とお here, as the end of the native counting series 一つ、二つ、三つ…九つ、十. With it you can count ten things without naming a particular counter. Unlike 一つ through 九つ, とお is not followed by つ. In the Sino-Japanese number series, ten is じゅう. Both forms have a long vowel; the romanization of とお is tō.","examples":[{"japanese":"箱の中にみかんが十あります。","romaji":"Hako no naka ni mikan ga tō arimasu.","german":"There are ten mandarin oranges in the box.","furigana":"はこ|なか|とお"},{"japanese":"子供と一つから十まで数えました。","romaji":"Kodomo to hitotsu kara tō made kazoemashita.","german":"I counted with the child from one to ten in the native counting series.","furigana":"こども|ひと|とお|かぞ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 9"},
{"notes":"A polite prefix before nouns and adjectives, mostly before native Japanese words: お茶, お金, お名前, お元気. Before Sino-Japanese words ご (御/ご, separate card) is usual: ご家族, ご連絡; but exceptions such as お電話 and お時間 are common. It is also part of the humble form お + verb stem + する (お持ちします). Almost always written in kana; the kanji 御 is seen mainly in formal texts.","examples":[{"japanese":"お茶をもう一杯いかがですか。","romaji":"Ocha o mō ippai ikaga desu ka.","german":"Would you like another cup of tea?","furigana":"ちゃ|いっぱい"},{"japanese":"すみません、お名前をもう一度お願いします。","romaji":"Sumimasen, onamae o mō ichido onegai shimasu.","german":"Excuse me, could you give me your name once more?","furigana":"なまえ|いちど|ねが"},{"japanese":"先生、お元気ですか。","romaji":"Sensei, ogenki desu ka.","german":"Teacher, how are you?","furigana":"せんせい|げんき"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Basic particle that replaces は or が (usually also を; after other particles it is attached: にも, からも) and expresses \"also\": 私も学生です. Used twice, AもBも means \"both A and B\", and negated, \"neither A nor B\". After quantities, も stresses a surprisingly large amount (三時間も \"as long as three hours\"); after question words with a negative, it yields \"nothing / no one\" (何も...ない, 誰も...ない). In contrast to は (topic, contrast) and が (subject), も adds something of the same kind.","examples":[{"japanese":"田中さんは学生です。私も学生です。","romaji":"Tanaka-san wa gakusei desu. Watashi mo gakusei desu.","german":"Mr. Tanaka is a student. I am a student too.","furigana":"たなか|がくせい|わたし|がくせい"},{"japanese":"妹は肉も魚も食べません。","romaji":"Imouto wa niku mo sakana mo tabemasen.","german":"My younger sister eats neither meat nor fish.","furigana":"いもうと|にく|さかな|た"},{"japanese":"電車が止まって、三時間も待ちました。","romaji":"Densha ga tomatte, sanjikan mo machimashita.","german":"The train stopped, and I waited for as long as three hours.","furigana":"でんしゃ|と|さんじかん|ま"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"The voiced variant of the particle くらい (the same word, separate card くらい). After amounts and time expressions it means \"about, approximately\" (三十分ぐらい); after nouns \"at least\" (挨拶ぐらいしなさい); after verbs and adjectives \"so much that\" (泣きたいぐらい). After nouns and numbers ぐらい is somewhat more common, after この/その/あの くらい is more usual; mostly the two are interchangeable. For clock times, however, ごろ is used (三時ごろ).","examples":[{"japanese":"駅まで歩いて十分ぐらいです。","romaji":"Eki made aruite juppun gurai desu.","german":"It takes about ten minutes to walk to the station.","furigana":"えき|ある|じゅっぷん"},{"japanese":"自分の部屋ぐらい自分で掃除しなさい。","romaji":"Jibun no heya gurai jibun de sōji shinasai.","german":"You can at least clean your own room yourself.","furigana":"じぶん|へや|じぶん|そうじ"},{"japanese":"泣きたいぐらいうれしかった。","romaji":"Nakitai gurai ureshikatta.","german":"I was so happy I could have cried.","furigana":"な"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"社会は変わっています。","romaji":"Shakai wa kawatte imasu.","german":"Society is changing.","furigana":"しゃかい|か"},{"japanese":"社会の問題について話しましょう。","romaji":"Shakai no mondai ni tsuite hanashimashō.","german":"Let's talk about social problems.","furigana":"しゃかい|もんだい|はな"}],"notes":"社会 here means society or community. 社会が変わる describes social change; 社会の問題 names problems within society. With の, the noun can specify another term more closely. The abbreviated name for the school subject 社会科 is a different dictionary meaning and is not taught here.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 42"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"政治に興味があります。","romaji":"Seiji ni kyōmi ga arimasu.","german":"I'm interested in politics.","furigana":"せいじ|きょうみ"},{"japanese":"政治の話はやめましょう。","romaji":"Seiji no hanashi wa yamemashō.","german":"Let's stop talking about politics.","furigana":"せいじ|はなし"}],"notes":"政治 denotes politics or the exercise of government. 政治に興味がある means to be interested in politics; 政治の話 is a conversation about politics. The noun takes に as the object of interest and の before 話. This card deals with the field itself, not a personal name written the same way.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 21"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本の経済は強いです。","romaji":"Nihon no keizai wa tsuyoi desu.","german":"Japan's economy is strong.","furigana":"にほん|けいざい|つよ"},{"japanese":"経済を勉強しています。","romaji":"Keizai o benkyō shite imasu.","german":"I'm studying economics.","furigana":"けいざい|べんきょう"}],"notes":"経済 denotes the economy or the science of economics here. 国の経済 names the economy of a country; 経済を勉強する means to study economics. The first example contains an evaluation with 強い, the second an activity. Other uses for one's personal finances or thriftiness are not part of these examples.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 13"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本の文化に興味があります。","romaji":"Nihon no bunka ni kyōmi ga arimasu.","german":"I'm interested in Japanese culture.","furigana":"にほん|ぶんか|きょうみ"},{"japanese":"文化の違いを学びました。","romaji":"Bunka no chigai o manabimashita.","german":"I learned about cultural differences.","furigana":"ぶんか|ちが|まな"}],"notes":"文化 refers to the culture of a society or community. 国の文化 means the culture of a country; 文化の違い means cultural differences. 興味がある describes an interest, and 学ぶ describes learning about the topic. The identically written name of the historical Bunka period belongs to a different meaning.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 42"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本の歴史を勉強しています。","romaji":"Nihon no rekishi o benkyō shite imasu.","german":"I'm studying Japanese history.","furigana":"にほん|れきし|べんきょう"},{"japanese":"この建物には長い歴史があります。","romaji":"Kono tatemono ni wa nagai rekishi ga arimasu.","german":"This building has a long history.","furigana":"たてもの|なが|れきし"}],"notes":"歴史 means history: either as knowledge about the past or as the past of a place, building, or company. 歴史を勉強する means to study history; 長い歴史がある means to look back on a long history. In the second example the building is the topic whose past is described.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい技術を学びます。","romaji":"Atarashii gijutsu o manabimasu.","german":"Ich lerne eine neue Technik.","furigana":"あたら|ぎじゅつ|まな"},{"japanese":"日本の技術は世界で有名です。","romaji":"Nihon no gijutsu wa sekai de yūmei desu.","german":"Japanische Technologie ist weltweit berühmt.","furigana":"にほん|ぎじゅつ|せかい|ゆうめい"}],"notes":"技術 kann Technologie oder eine erlernte Technik und Fertigkeit bezeichnen. 技術を学ぶ beschreibt den Erwerb einer solchen Fähigkeit; 国の技術 die Technologie eines Landes. Die passende deutsche Bedeutung hängt vom Zusammenhang ab. Die beiden Beispiele zeigen Lernen und eine Bewertung der Bekanntheit von Technologie.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 37"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仕事と関係があります。","romaji":"Shigoto to kankei ga arimasu.","german":"It has to do with my work.","furigana":"しごと|かんけい"},{"japanese":"二人の関係はとてもいいです。","romaji":"Futari no kankei wa totemo ii desu.","german":"The two of them have a very good relationship.","furigana":"ふたり|かんけい"}],"notes":"関係 here means relationship or connection. Aと関係がある means to have to do with A; 二人の関係 is the relationship between two people. The noun can therefore refer to a factual connection or to people. With する, 関係する becomes the verb \"to be connected\"; the card form is the noun.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 35"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"旅行の準備をします。","romaji":"Ryokō no junbi o shimasu.","german":"I'm preparing for the trip.","furigana":"りょこう|じゅんび"},{"japanese":"パーティーの準備ができました。","romaji":"Pātī no junbi ga dekimashita.","german":"The preparations for the party are done.","furigana":"じゅんび"}],"notes":"準備 is preparation. 旅行の準備をする means to make travel preparations; 準備ができる means that everything is ready. With する it can also become a transitive verb: 必要なものを準備する. On this card the noun is the focus. The examples distinguish the act of preparing from the state reached.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 24"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"車に注意してください。","romaji":"Kuruma ni chūi shite kudasai.","german":"Watch out for cars.","furigana":"くるま|ちゅうい"},{"japanese":"テストの前に注意事項を読みます。","romaji":"Tesuto no mae ni chūi jikō o yomimasu.","german":"Before the test, I read the instructions.","furigana":"まえ|ちゅういじこう|よ"}],"notes":"注意 refers to attention, caution, or a notice. 人や物に注意する means to pay attention to a person or thing. 注意事項 are points that should be observed. The examples show attention in traffic and reading notices before a test. The combination 注意する is an intransitive suru-verb here; the card form 注意 is a noun.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本に興味があります。","romaji":"Nihon ni kyōmi ga arimasu.","german":"I'm interested in Japan.","furigana":"にほん|きょうみ"},{"japanese":"この映画に興味がありますか。","romaji":"Kono eiga ni kyōmi ga arimasu ka.","german":"Are you interested in this movie?","furigana":"えいが|きょうみ"},{"japanese":"読んでいる途中で、この本への興味を失いました。","romaji":"Yonde iru tochū de, kono hon e no kyōmi o ushinaimashita.","german":"While reading, I lost interest in this book.","furigana":"よ|とちゅう|ほん|きょうみ|うしな"}],"notes":"興味 is interest or curiosity in something. The topic takes に: 日本に興味がある. 興味を持つ means to show or develop interest; 興味を失う means to lose interest. The card shows a noun that is often used with these fixed verb combinations.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 41"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"残念ですが行けません。","romaji":"Zannen desu ga ikemasen.","german":"Unfortunately, I can't go.","furigana":"ざんねん|い"},{"japanese":"残念な結果でした。","romaji":"Zannen na kekka deshita.","german":"It was a disappointing result.","furigana":"ざんねん|けっか"}],"notes":"残念 is a na-adjective meaning regrettable or disappointing. 残念ですが introduces a refusal with \"unfortunately\"; 残念な結果 describes a disappointing result. Before a noun it takes な, and at the end of a sentence です. The word expresses an evaluation from the speaker's point of view.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"正確な時間を教えてください。","romaji":"Seikaku na jikan o oshiete kudasai.","german":"Please tell me the exact time.","furigana":"せいかく|じかん|おし"},{"japanese":"この時計は正確です。","romaji":"Kono tokei wa seikaku desu.","german":"This clock is accurate.","furigana":"とけい|せいかく"},{"japanese":"質問に正確に答えてください。","romaji":"Shitsumon ni seikaku ni kotaete kudasai.","german":"Please answer the question accurately.","furigana":"しつもん|せいかく|こた"}],"notes":"正確 is a na-adjective meaning accurate, correct or precise. 正確な時間 gives an exact time; 正確です judges the accuracy of a clock or of information. With に you describe the manner of an action, e.g. 正確に答える. It is about correctness or precision, not merely about doing something neatly.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ここは危険です。","romaji":"Koko wa kiken desu.","german":"It's dangerous here.","furigana":"きけん"},{"japanese":"夜一人で歩くのは危険です。","romaji":"Yoru hitori de aruku no wa kiken desu.","german":"It's dangerous to walk alone at night.","furigana":"よるひとり|ある|きけん"}],"notes":"危険 as a na-adjective describes something dangerous: 危険な場所. At the end of a sentence it is 危険です. An activity can become the topic with の, as in 夜一人で歩くのは危険です. As a noun, 危険 also means danger; the opposite is 安全. The examples are evaluations of a place and of an activity.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 33"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この町は安全です。","romaji":"Kono machi wa anzen desu.","german":"This town is safe.","furigana":"まち|あんぜん"},{"japanese":"安全な場所に避難してください。","romaji":"Anzen na basho ni hinan shite kudasai.","german":"Please evacuate to a safe place.","furigana":"あんぜん|ばしょ|ひなん"}],"notes":"安全 is a na-adjective meaning safe and, as a noun, safety. 安全な場所 names a safe place; 安全です judges a place as safe. The opposite is 危険. The two examples distinguish a general evaluation of a place from a request to go to a safe place.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 42"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"丁寧に書いてください。","romaji":"Teinei ni kaite kudasai.","german":"Bitte schreiben Sie sorgfältig.","furigana":"ていねい|か"},{"japanese":"あの店員さんはとても丁寧です。","romaji":"Ano ten'in-san wa totemo teinei desu.","german":"Dieser Verkäufer ist sehr höflich.","furigana":"てんいん|ていねい"}],"notes":"丁寧 kann höflich im Umgang mit Menschen oder sorgfältig bei einer Tätigkeit bedeuten. 丁寧に書く beschreibt sorgfältiges Schreiben; 店員さんは丁寧です ein höfliches Auftreten. Als na-Adjektiv steht es mit な vor Nomen und mit に vor einem Verb. Die deutsche Entsprechung richtet sich nach der beschriebenen Tätigkeit oder Person.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"素晴らしい景色ですね。","romaji":"Subarashii keshiki desu ne.","german":"What a wonderful view.","furigana":"すば|けしき"},{"japanese":"素晴らしい演奏でした。","romaji":"Subarashii ensō deshita.","german":"It was a wonderful performance.","furigana":"すば|えんそう"}],"notes":"素晴らしい is an i-adjective meaning wonderful or great. It can stand directly before a noun: 素晴らしい景色. At the end of a sentence, the polite past form is 素晴らしかったです. The expression is clear praise and suits both an impression and an achievement.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 27"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"予定を変えます。","romaji":"Yotei o kaemasu.","german":"I'm changing the plan.","furigana":"よてい|か"},{"japanese":"髪の色を変えました。","romaji":"Kami no iro o kaemashita.","german":"I changed my hair color.","furigana":"かみ|いろ|か"}],"notes":"変える is a transitive ichidan verb: someone changes something, which takes を. 予定を変える means to change a plan; 髪の色を変える means to change one's hair color. The polite forms are 変えます and 変えました. Its intransitive counterpart 変わる describes something changing on its own.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"インターネットで調べます。","romaji":"Intānetto de shirabemasu.","german":"I'll look it up on the internet.","furigana":"しら"},{"japanese":"辞書で言葉を調べます。","romaji":"Jisho de kotoba o shirabemasu.","german":"I look up words in the dictionary.","furigana":"じしょ|ことば|しら"},{"japanese":"事故の原因を調べました。","romaji":"Jiko no gen'in o shirabemashita.","german":"I investigated the cause of the accident.","furigana":"じこ|げんいん|しら"}],"notes":"With 調べる you check or investigate something in a targeted way; a word is looked up in a dictionary, and a cause is investigated. The object takes を, and the means takes で. It is a transitive ichidan verb.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 15"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"東京で育ちました。","romaji":"Tōkyō de sodachimashita.","german":"I grew up in Tokyo.","furigana":"とうきょう|そだ"},{"japanese":"この植物は日光で育ちます。","romaji":"Kono shokubutsu wa nikkō de sodachimasu.","german":"This plant grows in sunlight.","furigana":"しょくぶつ|にっこう|そだ"}],"notes":"育つ describes a child growing up or a plant growing. The living thing takes が, the place of growing up often takes で. For raising a child or growing plants, the transitive 育てる is used. 育つ is a Godan verb ending in つ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お金が足りません。","romaji":"Okane ga tarimasen.","german":"I don't have enough money.","furigana":"かね|た"},{"japanese":"時間が足りません。","romaji":"Jikan ga tarimasen.","german":"There isn't enough time.","furigana":"じかん|た"},{"japanese":"三人なら、この量で足ります。","romaji":"Sannin nara, kono ryō de tarimasu.","german":"For three people, this amount is enough.","furigana":"さんにん|りょう|た"}],"notes":"足りる says that money, time, or an amount is sufficient for a purpose. What is needed takes が; 足りない means \"not enough\". It is an intransitive ichidan verb. The more formal phrase ～に足りる in the sense of \"to be worthy of\" belongs to a different use.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 17"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この辞書は役に立ちます。","romaji":"Kono jisho wa yaku ni tachimasu.","german":"This dictionary is useful.","furigana":"じしょ|やく|た"},{"japanese":"この経験は将来役に立ちます。","romaji":"Kono keiken wa shōrai yaku ni tachimasu.","german":"This experience will be useful in the future.","furigana":"けいけん|しょうらいやく|た"}],"notes":"役に立つ is the fixed phrase for \"to be useful\" or \"to help\". The useful thing is the subject; with ～に you can name the purpose or the person. Only 立つ is conjugated: 役に立ちます, 役に立った. The phrase suits tools as well as experiences.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"勉強を続けます。","romaji":"Benkyō o tsuzukemasu.","german":"I'll keep studying.","furigana":"べんきょう|つづ"},{"japanese":"毎日運動を続けています。","romaji":"Mainichi undō o tsuzukete imasu.","german":"I keep exercising every day.","furigana":"まいにちうんどう|つづ"},{"japanese":"休憩の後で話を続けましょう。","romaji":"Kyūkei no ato de hanashi o tsuzukemashō.","german":"Let's continue the conversation after the break.","furigana":"きゅうけい|あと|はなし|つづ"}],"notes":"続ける means that someone continues an activity; the activity takes を. By contrast, 続く describes something continuing on its own. 続ける is a transitive ichidan verb. After a verb stem, ～続ける can also express \"to keep on ...ing\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 21"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"雨が続いています。","romaji":"Ame ga tsuzuite imasu.","german":"The rain continues.","furigana":"あめ|つづ"},{"japanese":"この道はまだ続きます。","romaji":"Kono michi wa mada tsuzukimasu.","german":"This road goes on further.","furigana":"みち|つづ"}],"notes":"続く describes, without a direct object, that something continues or extends further: 雨が続く, 道が続く. It is an intransitive godan verb ending in く. When a person deliberately continues something, it is ～を続ける instead.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人口が増えています。","romaji":"Jinkō ga fuete imasu.","german":"The population is increasing.","furigana":"じんこう|ふ"},{"japanese":"最近仕事が増えました。","romaji":"Saikin shigoto ga fuemashita.","german":"Recently my workload has increased.","furigana":"さいきんしごと|ふ"}],"notes":"増える describes an increase in people, things, or work; the thing that increases takes が. It is an intransitive ichidan verb. Someone who actively increases an amount uses 増やす with を. The spelling 殖える is used especially for multiplication or growth of capital.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お金が減りました。","romaji":"Okane ga herimashita.","german":"My money has run low.","furigana":"かね|へ"},{"japanese":"冬になると観光客が減ります。","romaji":"Fuyu ni naru to kankōkyaku ga herimasu.","german":"The number of tourists falls in winter.","furigana":"ふゆ|かんこうきゃく|へ"}],"notes":"減る describes a quantity or number becoming smaller. The thing that decreases takes が: お金が減る. It is an intransitive Godan verb ending in る: 減ります・減らない・減って; when someone reduces something, the transitive counterpart is 減らす.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"電車が込んでいます。","romaji":"Densha ga konde imasu.","german":"The train is crowded.","furigana":"でんしゃ|こ"},{"japanese":"休みの日はどこも込みます。","romaji":"Yasumi no hi wa doko mo komimasu.","german":"It's crowded everywhere on days off.","furigana":"やす|ひ|こ"},{"japanese":"朝早く行けば、道はまだ込んでいません。","romaji":"Asa hayaku ikeba, michi wa mada konde imasen.","german":"If you go early in the morning, the road is not yet congested.","furigana":"あさはや|い|みち|こ"}],"notes":"Here 込む is the independent intransitive verb for \"to be full or crowded\". For crowds and traffic, 混む is often written. It is a godan verb ending in む. The attached ～込む in compound verbs such as 申し込む has its own functions and should be distinguished from this use.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 17"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お腹が空きました。","romaji":"Onaka ga sukimashita.","german":"I'm hungry.","furigana":"なか|す"},{"japanese":"朝のラッシュが終わると、電車は空きます。","romaji":"Asa no rasshu ga owaru to, densha wa sukimasu.","german":"After the morning rush, the train gets less crowded.","furigana":"あさ|お|でんしゃ|す"}],"notes":"空く is read すく here. For a train or a shop it means becoming less crowded; 空いている（すいている）describes few crowds. お腹が空く means to get or be hungry. This intransitive godan verb forms すきます and すいて. For a free seat, 空いている with the other reading あいている is usually said; that reading belongs to 空く（あく）. すく is the kana spelling of exactly this reading and these meanings; it denotes neither the reading あく nor the homophone for combing, 梳く.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"車を売ります。","romaji":"Kuruma o urimasu.","german":"I'm selling my car.","furigana":"くるま|う"},{"japanese":"このお店はパンを売っています。","romaji":"Kono omise wa pan o utte imasu.","german":"This shop sells bread.","furigana":"みせ|う"}],"notes":"売る is a transitive godan verb: 売ります, 売って. With を you name the goods sold. 店がパンを売っている can describe a shop's regular offering. 売ります can also announce one's own plan to sell; it does not have to describe a sale currently in progress.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 15"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この本はよく売れています。","romaji":"Kono hon wa yoku urete imasu.","german":"This book is selling well.","furigana":"ほん|う"},{"japanese":"あの歌手のCDはよく売れます。","romaji":"Ano kashu no CD wa yoku uremasu.","german":"That singer's CDs sell well.","furigana":"かしゅ|う"},{"japanese":"古い自転車が千円で売れました。","romaji":"Furui jitensha ga sen'en de uremashita.","german":"The old bicycle sold for a thousand yen.","furigana":"ふる|じてんしゃ|せんえん|う"}],"notes":"売れる means that goods sell or are sold; the goods take が or は as topic. It is an intransitive Ichidan verb. 売る, in contrast, means actively selling something with を. よく売れる means \"to sell well\", not automatically \"to be famous\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ルールを守ってください。","romaji":"Rūru o mamotte kudasai.","german":"Please follow the rules.","furigana":"まも"},{"japanese":"子供を危険から守ります。","romaji":"Kodomo o kiken kara mamorimasu.","german":"I protect the children from danger.","furigana":"こども|きけん|まも"}],"notes":"守る can mean to protect people from something or to keep rules and promises. The thing protected or kept takes を; a danger can follow with から. It is a transitive Godan verb ending in る, not an Ichidan verb. The rare reading もる is not taught here.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"荷物を運びます。","romaji":"Nimotsu o hakobimasu.","german":"I carry the luggage.","furigana":"にもつ|はこ"},{"japanese":"テーブルを二階に運びました。","romaji":"Tēburu o nikai ni hakobimashita.","german":"I carried the table to the second floor.","furigana":"にかい|はこ"}],"notes":"運ぶ here means \"to carry\" or \"to transport\". The thing takes を, and the destination takes に. It is a transitive godan verb ending in ぶ; the て-form is 運んで. For floors, Japan counts the ground floor as 一階: 二階 is therefore the floor above the ground floor (the second floor in American usage, the first floor in British usage).","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 22"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"来月引っ越します。","romaji":"Raigetsu hikkoshimasu.","german":"I'm moving next month.","furigana":"らいげつひ|こ"},{"japanese":"大阪から東京に引っ越しました。","romaji":"Ōsaka kara Tōkyō ni hikkoshimashita.","german":"I moved from Osaka to Tokyo.","furigana":"おおさか|とうきょう|ひ|こ"}],"notes":"引っ越す is used for changing residence, not for merely moving an object. The previous place takes から, and the new home or city takes に. It is an intransitive godan verb ending in す: 引っ越して, 引っ越した.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 19"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コースに申し込みました。","romaji":"Kōsu ni mōshikomimashita.","german":"I signed up for the course.","furigana":"もう|こ"},{"japanese":"マラソン大会に申し込みました。","romaji":"Marason taikai ni mōshikomimashita.","german":"I signed up for the marathon.","furigana":"たいかい|もう|こ"},{"japanese":"申し込もうとしましたが、もう締め切られていました。","romaji":"Mōshikomō to shimashita ga, mō shimekirarete imashita.","german":"I tried to sign up, but the deadline had already passed.","furigana":"もう|こ|し|き"}],"notes":"申し込む means to submit a registration or an application. A course or event usually takes に; 参加する, in contrast, means actually taking part. It is a Godan verb ending in む: 申し込んで, 申し込んだ. A rejected or late application can also be described with it.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"パーティーに参加します。","romaji":"Pātī ni sanka shimasu.","german":"I'm taking part in the party.","furigana":"さんか"},{"japanese":"会議に参加してください。","romaji":"Kaigi ni sanka shite kudasai.","german":"Please attend the meeting.","furigana":"かいぎ|さんか"}],"notes":"参加する describes actually taking part in an event or activity; the event takes に. For the prior registration, 申し込む is used. It is an intransitive する-verb. The noun 参加 means \"participation\"; the example sentences use the full verb form.","levelNote":"Derived from 参加 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"来年大学を卒業します。","romaji":"Rainen daigaku o sotsugyō shimasu.","german":"I will graduate from university next year.","furigana":"らいねんだいがく|そつぎょう"},{"japanese":"高校を卒業してから働きます。","romaji":"Kōkō o sotsugyō shite kara hatarakimasu.","german":"I will work after graduating from high school.","furigana":"こうこう|そつぎょう|はたら"}],"notes":"卒業する means to complete a school or course of study. Despite the intransitive する-verb, the school takes を, because you leave it: 大学を卒業する. The mere start of an education is 入学する. Figurative uses, such as giving up a habit, belong to a further sense.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 15"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大学に入学しました。","romaji":"Daigaku ni nyūgaku shimashita.","german":"I entered university.","furigana":"だいがく|にゅうがく"},{"japanese":"四月に小学校に入学します。","romaji":"Shigatsu ni shōgakkō ni nyūgaku shimasu.","german":"He starts elementary school in April.","furigana":"しがつ|しょうがっこう|にゅうがく"}],"notes":"入学する means to begin an education as a pupil or student at a school or university. The institution takes に. It does not denote a single movement into a building; 入る is enough for that. It is an intransitive する-verb, with 入学 as the noun for the start of school or study.","levelNote":"Derived from 入学 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"明日の朝出発します。","romaji":"Ashita no asa shuppatsu shimasu.","german":"I'm setting off tomorrow morning.","furigana":"あした|あさしゅっぱつ"},{"japanese":"バスは九時に出発します。","romaji":"Basu wa kuji ni shuppatsu shimasu.","german":"The bus departs at nine o'clock.","furigana":"くじ|しゅっぱつ"}],"notes":"出発する means \"to set off\" or \"to depart\" and is intransitive. In the first example, a person announces their own departure for tomorrow morning; in the second, it is about the departure time of a bus. An exact time takes に: 九時に. The starting point is given with から, and a destination with へ or に. 出発します and 出発しました are forms of する; 出発 alone is the noun \"departure\" or \"setting off\".","levelNote":"Derived from 出発 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"飛行機が到着しました。","romaji":"Hikōki ga tōchaku shimashita.","german":"The plane has arrived.","furigana":"ひこうき|とうちゃく"},{"japanese":"電車は五分後に到着します。","romaji":"Densha wa gofun go ni tōchaku shimasu.","german":"The train arrives in five minutes.","furigana":"でんしゃ|ごふんご|とうちゃく"},{"japanese":"荷物が予定より早く到着しました。","romaji":"Nimotsu ga yotei yori hayaku tōchaku shimashita.","german":"The package arrived earlier than planned.","furigana":"にもつ|よてい|はや|とうちゃく"}],"notes":"到着する describes people, vehicles or shipments arriving at a destination. The destination takes に, the arriving thing が. It is an intransitive する-verb and sounds more formal than the short 着く. The noun 到着 means \"arrival\".","levelNote":"Derived from 到着 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本は車を輸出しています。","romaji":"Nihon wa kuruma o yushutsu shite imasu.","german":"Japan exports cars.","furigana":"にほん|くるま|ゆしゅつ"},{"japanese":"この工場は電子機器を輸出しています。","romaji":"Kono kōjō wa denshi kiki o yushutsu shite imasu.","german":"This factory exports electronic devices.","furigana":"こうじょう|でんしきき|ゆしゅつ"},{"japanese":"海外に輸出するため、書類を用意しました。","romaji":"Kaigai ni yushutsu suru tame, shorui o yōi shimashita.","german":"I prepared the documents for exporting abroad.","furigana":"かいがい|ゆしゅつ|しょるい|ようい"}],"notes":"輸出する means to send goods out of a country to a foreign country. The goods take を, and the destination country takes に or へ. It is a transitive する-verb; the usual reading is ゆしゅつする. The opposite term 輸入する denotes import. Biological uses of the noun are not part of the material here.","levelNote":"Derived from 輸出 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"食料品を輸入しています。","romaji":"Shokuryōhin o yunyū shite imasu.","german":"We import food products.","furigana":"しょくりょうひん|ゆにゅう"},{"japanese":"ワインをフランスから輸入しています。","romaji":"Wain o Furansu kara yunyū shite imasu.","german":"We import wine from France.","furigana":"ゆにゅう"},{"japanese":"輸入する前に、商品の品質を調べました。","romaji":"Yunyū suru mae ni, shōhin no hinshitsu o shirabemashita.","german":"Before importing, I checked the quality of the goods.","furigana":"ゆにゅう|まえ|しょうひん|ひんしつ|しら"}],"notes":"輸入する means to bring goods from abroad into one's own country. The goods take を, and the country of origin takes から. It is a transitive する-verb; the reading learned is ゆにゅうする. 輸出する denotes the opposite direction. \"Import\" is used here in the economic sense.","levelNote":"Derived from 輸入 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"工場で車を生産します。","romaji":"Kōjō de kuruma o seisan shimasu.","german":"Cars are produced at the factory.","furigana":"こうじょう|くるま|せいさん"},{"japanese":"この地域はお米を生産しています。","romaji":"Kono chiiki wa okome o seisan shite imasu.","german":"This region produces rice.","furigana":"ちいき|こめ|せいさん"}],"notes":"生産する means to manufacture goods or agricultural products. The product takes を, and the place of production can follow with で. It is a transitive する-verb; 生産 alone is the noun \"production\". The expression suits a factory as well as growing rice.","levelNote":"Derived from 生産 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"研究を発表します。","romaji":"Kenkyū o happyō shimasu.","german":"I present the research.","furigana":"けんきゅう|はっぴょう"},{"japanese":"新しい計画を発表しました。","romaji":"Atarashii keikaku o happyō shimashita.","german":"The new plan was announced.","furigana":"あたら|けいかく|はっぴょう"}],"notes":"発表する means to present results or to announce information publicly. The content takes を, and the setting of a presentation often takes で. It is a transitive する-verb. It describes making something known; publishing a printed work, by contrast, is 出版する.","levelNote":"Derived from 発表 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"実験に成功しました。","romaji":"Jikken ni seikō shimashita.","german":"The experiment succeeded.","furigana":"じっけん|せいこう"},{"japanese":"ビジネスに成功したいです。","romaji":"Bijinesu ni seikō shitai desu.","german":"I want to succeed in business.","furigana":"せいこう"}],"notes":"成功する describes an undertaking succeeding or a person being successful. The undertaking can follow with に: 実験に成功する. It is an intransitive する-verb. \"To pass a school exam\" is usually 試験に合格する; a successful technical trial is a different situation.","levelNote":"Derived from 成功 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"料理に失敗しました。","romaji":"Ryōri ni shippai shimashita.","german":"The cooking failed.","furigana":"りょうり|しっぱい"},{"japanese":"何度失敗しても諦めません。","romaji":"Nando shippai shite mo akiramemasen.","german":"No matter how many times I fail, I won't give up.","furigana":"なんどしっぱい|あきら"}],"notes":"失敗する means that something fails or that someone makes a mistake. An activity can follow with に, as in 料理に失敗する. The noun 失敗 means \"mistake\" or \"failure\". In the second sentence, ～ても shows that the person keeps going despite repeated failures.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 22"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"サイズが合います。","romaji":"Saizu ga aimasu.","german":"The size fits.","furigana":"あ"},{"japanese":"この靴は私に合いません。","romaji":"Kono kutsu wa watashi ni aimasen.","german":"These shoes don't fit me.","furigana":"くつ|わたし|あ"},{"japanese":"二人は意見が合います。","romaji":"Futari wa iken ga aimasu.","german":"The two of them agree in their opinions.","furigana":"ふたり|いけん|あ"}],"notes":"合う here means \"to fit\" or \"to match\". What fits takes が; a person can follow with に. Opinions can also agree: 意見が合う. It is an intransitive godan verb ending in う. \"To meet someone\" is usually written 会う.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"諦めないでください。","romaji":"Akiramenaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't give up.","furigana":"あきら"},{"japanese":"夢を諦めたくないです。","romaji":"Yume o akirametaku nai desu.","german":"I don't want to give up my dream.","furigana":"ゆめ|あきら"},{"japanese":"雨なので、山に登るのを諦めました。","romaji":"Ame na node, yama ni noboru no o akiramemashita.","german":"Because of the rain, I gave up climbing the mountain.","furigana":"あめ|やま|のぼ|あきら"}],"notes":"諦める means to give up a wish or plan or to resign oneself to a situation. The thing given up takes を; a planned activity can follow with ～のを. It is a transitive ichidan verb. 諦めないで is a request not to give up.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 14"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"友達が悲しんでいます。","romaji":"Tomodachi ga kanashinde imasu.","german":"My friend is sad.","furigana":"ともだち|かな"},{"japanese":"彼はペットの死を悲しんでいます。","romaji":"Kare wa petto no shi o kanashinde imasu.","german":"He is grieving over the death of his pet.","furigana":"かれ|し|かな"}],"notes":"悲しむ is the verb for grieving or feeling sadness. A cause can take を, as in ペットの死を悲しむ. It is a transitive Godan verb ending in む; 悲しい, in contrast, is the adjective \"sad\". Even without an explicitly named cause, someone can simply be 悲しんでいる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あなたを信じます。","romaji":"Anata o shinjimasu.","german":"Ich glaube Ihnen.","furigana":"しん"},{"japanese":"この話を信じますか。","romaji":"Kono hanashi o shinjimasu ka.","german":"Glauben Sie diese Geschichte?","furigana":"はなし|しん"}],"notes":"信じる kann heißen, einer Person zu vertrauen oder eine Aussage für wahr zu halten. Beides steht im Japanischen mit を: 人を信じる, 話を信じる. Es ist ein transitives Ichidan-Verb. Der deutsche Unterschied zwischen „jemandem glauben“ und „etwas glauben“ verlangt hier keine andere japanische Partikel.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"成功を祈ります。","romaji":"Seikō o inorimasu.","german":"I wish you success.","furigana":"せいこう|いの"},{"japanese":"家族の健康を祈っています。","romaji":"Kazoku no kenkō o inotte imasu.","german":"I pray for my family's health.","furigana":"かぞく|けんこう|いの"}],"notes":"祈る means \"to pray\", but it can also express a sincere wish in a situation that is not explicitly religious. What is wished for takes を: 成功を祈る, 健康を祈る. It is a transitive godan verb ending in る; the te-form is 祈って.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その計画に反対します。","romaji":"Sono keikaku ni hantai shimasu.","german":"I oppose that plan.","furigana":"けいかく|はんたい"},{"japanese":"両親は私の留学に反対しました。","romaji":"Ryōshin wa watashi no ryūgaku ni hantai shimashita.","german":"My parents opposed my studying abroad.","furigana":"りょうしん|わたし|りゅうがく|はんたい"}],"notes":"反対する means to be against a proposal or plan. What the objection is directed at takes に. It is an intransitive する-verb. The noun 反対 can also denote an opposite direction; this directional sense is not taught here as a verb.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 22"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その意見に賛成します。","romaji":"Sono iken ni sansei shimasu.","german":"I agree with that opinion.","furigana":"いけん|さんせい"},{"japanese":"みんなが賛成しました。","romaji":"Minna ga sansei shimashita.","german":"Everyone agreed.","furigana":"さんせい"}],"notes":"賛成する means to agree with an opinion or proposal. The content takes に, and the agreeing person takes が. It is an intransitive する-verb; the opposite is 反対する. 賛成 alone can be used as a brief expression of agreement, \"I'm for it!\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 22"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"図書館を利用します。","romaji":"Toshokan o riyō shimasu.","german":"I use the library.","furigana":"としょかん|りよう"},{"japanese":"図書館をよく利用します。","romaji":"Toshokan o yoku riyō shimasu.","german":"I often use the library.","furigana":"としょかん|りよう"},{"japanese":"空いた時間を利用して、本を読みます。","romaji":"Aita jikan o riyō shite, hon o yomimasu.","german":"I use my free time to read a book.","furigana":"あ|じかん|りよう|ほん|よ"}],"notes":"利用する means to use a facility, service, or opportunity. What is used takes を. It is a transitive する-verb and suits, for example, libraries or available time. The more general 使う is also used for simple tools and objects.","levelNote":"Derived from 利用 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本の生活は楽しいです。","romaji":"Nihon no seikatsu wa tanoshii desu.","german":"Life in Japan is enjoyable.","furigana":"にほん|せいかつ|たの"},{"japanese":"一人暮らしの生活に慣れました。","romaji":"Hitorigurashi no seikatsu ni naremashita.","german":"I've gotten used to life living alone.","furigana":"ひとりぐ|せいかつ|な"}],"notes":"生活 denotes everyday life and the way someone lives. It is a noun: 日本の生活, 一人暮らしの生活. With する it can mean \"to live\". It is about daily life here, not biological life or lifespan, which are expressed with 生命 or 寿命, for example.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 8"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"早起きの習慣があります。","romaji":"Hayaoki no shūkan ga arimasu.","german":"I have a habit of getting up early.","furigana":"はやお|しゅうかん"},{"japanese":"早起きの習慣をつけたいです。","romaji":"Hayaoki no shūkan o tsuketai desu.","german":"I want to get into the habit of getting up early.","furigana":"はやお|しゅうかん"},{"japanese":"この家には、玄関で靴を脱ぐ習慣があります。","romaji":"Kono ie ni wa, genkan de kutsu o nugu shūkan ga arimasu.","german":"In this house, it is customary to take off your shoes at the entrance.","furigana":"いえ|げんかん|くつ|ぬ|しゅうかん"}],"notes":"習慣 is a personal habit or a customary practice within a group. 習慣がある means to have a habit; 習慣をつける means to acquire one. The identically read 週間, which denotes a number of weeks, is written differently.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 28"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本の人口は減っています。","romaji":"Nihon no jinkō wa hette imasu.","german":"Japan's population is decreasing.","furigana":"にほん|じんこう|へ"},{"japanese":"この町の人口は少ないです。","romaji":"Kono machi no jinkō wa sukunai desu.","german":"This town's population is small.","furigana":"まち|じんこう|すく"}],"notes":"人口 refers to the number of people in an area. With ～の人口 you name the area; 多い and 少ない describe a large or small number of inhabitants. 増える and 減る describe how it changes. The identically read 人工 means \"artificial\" and is a different word.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 47"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"交通が便利です。","romaji":"Kōtsū ga benri desu.","german":"Die Verkehrsanbindung ist gut.","furigana":"こうつう|べんり"},{"japanese":"交通が便利な場所に住みたいです。","romaji":"Kōtsū ga benri na basho ni sumitai desu.","german":"Ich möchte an einem Ort mit guter Verkehrsanbindung wohnen.","furigana":"こうつう|べんり|ばしょ|す"},{"japanese":"朝は交通が激しくなります。","romaji":"Asa wa kōtsū ga hageshiku narimasu.","german":"Morgens wird der Verkehr dichter.","furigana":"あさ|こうつう|はげ"}],"notes":"交通 bezeichnet Verkehr oder Verkehrsanbindung. 交通が便利 heißt, dass man gut von einem Ort zum anderen gelangt; „bequemer Verkehr“ wäre im Deutschen unnatürlich. Hier wird das Nomen verwendet. Weitere Wörterbuchsinne wie der Austausch zwischen Menschen sind von diesem Verkehrsgebrauch zu unterscheiden.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 21"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"交通事故がありました。","romaji":"Kōtsū jiko ga arimashita.","german":"There was a traffic accident.","furigana":"こうつうじこ"},{"japanese":"交通事故に気をつけてください。","romaji":"Kōtsū jiko ni ki o tsukete kudasai.","german":"Please be careful to avoid traffic accidents.","furigana":"こうつうじこ|き"},{"japanese":"工場の事故で機械が止まりました。","romaji":"Kōjō no jiko de kikai ga tomarimashita.","german":"Because of an accident at the factory, the machine stopped.","furigana":"こうじょう|じこ|きかい|と"}],"notes":"事故 here refers to an accident or an unwanted disruption. 交通事故 is a traffic accident; 事故がある or 起こる describes the event. The noun is read じこ. The older reading ことゆえ and the broader sense \"circumstances\" do not belong to this card.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 39"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"事故の原因を調べます。","romaji":"Jiko no gen'in o shirabemasu.","german":"I'm investigating the cause of the accident.","furigana":"じこ|げんいん|しら"},{"japanese":"病気の原因がわかりました。","romaji":"Byōki no gen'in ga wakarimashita.","german":"The cause of the illness has been found.","furigana":"びょうき|げんいん"}],"notes":"原因 is the cause from which an event or problem arises. ～の原因 names the event that was caused; 原因を調べる means to investigate the cause. For a stated motive, 理由 is often used. In gen'in, the apostrophe separates ん from the following vowel.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 46"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"テストの結果はどうでしたか。","romaji":"Tesuto no kekka wa dō deshita ka.","german":"How was the test result?","furigana":"けっか"},{"japanese":"試験の結果はいつわかりますか。","romaji":"Shiken no kekka wa itsu wakarimasu ka.","german":"When will we find out the exam results?","furigana":"しけん|けっか"},{"japanese":"調査の結果を表にまとめました。","romaji":"Chōsa no kekka o hyō ni matomemashita.","german":"I summarized the results of the survey in a table.","furigana":"ちょうさ|けっか|ひょう"}],"notes":"結果 is the noun \"result\" or \"consequence\" here. A test or survey precedes it with の. 結果がわかる means to learn the result; 結果をまとめる, to summarize it. The construction ～した結果 in the sense of \"as a result of ...\" is another use.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"理由を教えてください。","romaji":"Riyū o oshiete kudasai.","german":"Please tell me the reason.","furigana":"りゆう|おし"},{"japanese":"遅刻の理由を教えてください。","romaji":"Chikoku no riyū o oshiete kudasai.","german":"Please tell me the reason for your lateness.","furigana":"ちこく|りゆう|おし"},{"japanese":"理由もなく怒らないでください。","romaji":"Riyū mo naku okoranaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't get angry for no reason.","furigana":"りゆう|おこ"}],"notes":"理由 refers to the reason or motive for a behavior. ～の理由 names what an explanation is being sought for; 理由を教える means to tell the reason. For the cause of an accident that is being investigated, 原因 often fits better. The reading learned here is りゆう, not the also recorded reading わけ.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 43"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"旅行の目的は何ですか。","romaji":"Ryokō no mokuteki wa nan desu ka.","german":"What is the purpose of your trip?","furigana":"りょこう|もくてき|なん"},{"japanese":"目的を持って勉強することが大切です。","romaji":"Mokuteki o motte benkyō suru koto ga taisetsu desu.","german":"It is important to study with a goal in mind.","furigana":"もくてき|も|べんきょう|たいせつ"}],"notes":"目的 is the purpose or intended goal of an action. With 旅行の目的 you ask what a trip is for; 目的を持って means to act with a particular goal. Unlike a destination, 目的 here describes the intention behind an activity.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 42"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あなたの意見を聞かせてください。","romaji":"Anata no iken o kikasete kudasai.","german":"Please tell me your opinion.","furigana":"いけん|き"},{"japanese":"会議で自分の意見をはっきり言いました。","romaji":"Kaigi de jibun no iken o hakkiri iimashita.","german":"In the meeting I clearly stated my own opinion.","furigana":"かいぎ|じぶん|いけん|い"}],"notes":"意見 means an opinion or a point of view here. 意見を言う means to express one's view; 意見を聞く or 聞かせてください asks for another person's view. This card practices this noun, not the other dictionary meaning of 意見する, to admonish someone.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 21"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"旅行の計画を立てます。","romaji":"Ryokō no keikaku o tatemasu.","german":"I'll make travel plans.","furigana":"りょこう|けいかく|た"},{"japanese":"夏休みの計画を立てましょう。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi no keikaku o tatemashō.","german":"Let's make plans for the summer vacation.","furigana":"なつやす|けいかく|た"},{"japanese":"天気が悪いので、計画を変えました。","romaji":"Tenki ga warui node, keikaku o kaemashita.","german":"Because the weather is bad, I changed the plan.","furigana":"てんき|わる|けいかく|か"}],"notes":"計画 is a worked-out plan or project. 計画を立てる means to draw up a plan; 計画を変える means to change it. The activity can precede it with の. With する, the noun can become the verb \"to plan\"; the card teaches the noun form.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いい方法を教えてください。","romaji":"Ii hōhō o oshiete kudasai.","german":"Please tell me a good method.","furigana":"ほうほう|おし"},{"japanese":"他の方法を考えましょう。","romaji":"Hoka no hōhō o kangaemashō.","german":"Let's think of other methods.","furigana":"ほか|ほうほう|かんが"}],"notes":"方法 refers to a method or procedure by which one achieves something. With ～の方法, the activity can precede it. 方法を教える means to explain a procedure; 他の方法を考える means to look for an alternative. The word does not mean a spatial path here.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 35"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この場合はどうしますか。","romaji":"Kono baai wa dō shimasu ka.","german":"What do you do in this case?","furigana":"ばあい"},{"japanese":"雨の場合は中止です。","romaji":"Ame no baai wa chūshi desu.","german":"In case of rain, it will be canceled.","furigana":"あめ|ばあい|ちゅうし"}],"notes":"場合 names a case or particular circumstances. After a noun, の precedes it, as in 雨の場合. Verbs stand directly before it in their plain form: 雨が降る場合. The expression is especially suited to explaining how to proceed in a possible situation.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"環境を守りましょう。","romaji":"Kankyō o mamorimashō.","german":"Let's protect the environment.","furigana":"かんきょう|まも"},{"japanese":"環境を守ることは大切です。","romaji":"Kankyō o mamoru koto wa taisetsu desu.","german":"It is important to protect the environment.","furigana":"かんきょう|まも|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"静かな環境で勉強したいです。","romaji":"Shizuka na kankyō de benkyō shitai desu.","german":"I want to study in a quiet environment.","furigana":"しず|かんきょう|べんきょう"}],"notes":"環境 refers to the environment or the circumstances in which someone lives or works. 環境を守る usually means environmental protection; 静かな環境 describes a quiet surrounding. It is a noun and can therefore stand before another noun with の.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 21"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"昨日地震がありました。","romaji":"Kinō jishin ga arimashita.","german":"There was an earthquake yesterday.","furigana":"きのうじしん"},{"japanese":"昨日大きな地震がありました。","romaji":"Kinō ōkina jishin ga arimashita.","german":"There was a big earthquake yesterday.","furigana":"きのうおお|じしん"},{"japanese":"地震で窓ガラスが割れました。","romaji":"Jishin de madogarasu ga waremashita.","german":"The window glass broke because of the earthquake.","furigana":"じしん|まど|わ"}],"notes":"地震 is the noun for an earthquake. 地震がある or 地震が起こる describes the event; 地震で can introduce its consequence. The identically read 自信 means \"self-confidence\" and is a different word.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 29"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大学で研究しています。","romaji":"Daigaku de kenkyū shite imasu.","german":"I do research at a university.","furigana":"だいがく|けんきゅう"},{"japanese":"この大学で研究をしたいです。","romaji":"Kono daigaku de kenkyū o shitai desu.","german":"I want to do research at this university.","furigana":"だいがく|けんきゅう"},{"japanese":"研究の結果を会議で説明しました。","romaji":"Kenkyū no kekka o kaigi de setsumei shimashita.","german":"I explained the research results at the meeting.","furigana":"けんきゅう|けっか|かいぎ|せつめい"}],"notes":"研究 refers to the systematic investigation of a topic. As a noun it appears, for example, in 研究の結果; with する it becomes the verb \"to research\". The research institution can follow with で. For general studying for school or exams, 勉強 is more usual.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"工場で働いています。","romaji":"Kōjō de hataraite imasu.","german":"I work at a factory.","furigana":"こうじょう|はたら"},{"japanese":"工場で新しい製品を作っています。","romaji":"Kōjō de atarashii seihin o tsukutte imasu.","german":"New products are being made at the factory.","furigana":"こうじょう|あたら|せいひん|つく"}],"notes":"工場 is a factory or production site. It is read こうじょう here; the same spelling also has the reading こうば, which is not automatically equated with this entry. The place of work or production takes で: 工場で働く, 工場で作る.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 36"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いい製品を作ります。","romaji":"Ii seihin o tsukurimasu.","german":"We make good products.","furigana":"せいひん|つく"},{"japanese":"この製品の品質はとてもいいです。","romaji":"Kono seihin no hinshitsu wa totemo ii desu.","german":"The quality of this product is very good.","furigana":"せいひん|ひんしつ"}],"notes":"製品 is a finished manufactured product, especially a good from production. 製品を作る denotes manufacturing; 製品の品質 the quality of the product. The expression fits less well with unprocessed raw materials, for which 原料, for example, is used.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 15"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お祭りに行きましょう。","romaji":"Omatsuri ni ikimashō.","german":"Let's go to the festival.","furigana":"まつ|い"},{"japanese":"夏のお祭りが楽しみです。","romaji":"Natsu no omatsuri ga tanoshimi desu.","german":"I'm looking forward to the summer festival.","furigana":"なつ|まつ|たの"},{"japanese":"雨のため、お祭りは中止になりました。","romaji":"Ame no tame, omatsuri wa chūshi ni narimashita.","german":"The festival was canceled because of the rain.","furigana":"あめ|まつ|ちゅうし"}],"notes":"お祭り refers to a festival, often a local matsuri. The お is part of the polite word form; the plain form is 祭り. Going to the festival is お祭りに行く. The expression can refer to the event visited as well as to an anticipated or canceled festival.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お正月は家族と過ごします。","romaji":"Oshōgatsu wa kazoku to sugoshimasu.","german":"I spend New Year with my family.","furigana":"しょうがつ|かぞく|す"},{"japanese":"お正月に家族で集まります。","romaji":"Oshōgatsu ni kazoku de atsumarimasu.","german":"My family gets together at New Year.","furigana":"しょうがつ|かぞく|あつ"},{"japanese":"お正月はこの店も休みです。","romaji":"Oshōgatsu wa kono mise mo yasumi desu.","german":"This shop is also closed at New Year.","furigana":"しょうがつ|みせ|やす"}],"notes":"お正月 denotes New Year, and especially the first days of the new year. The お belongs to the polite word form; 正月 is the plain base. This is about the New Year season, not the further dictionary meaning \"January\". The expression fits family visits as well as company holidays.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 11"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"特に和食が好きです。","romaji":"Toku ni washoku ga suki desu.","german":"I especially like Japanese food.","furigana":"とく|わしょく|す"},{"japanese":"この計画には特に問題はありません。","romaji":"Kono keikaku ni wa toku ni mondai wa arimasen.","german":"There are no particular problems with this plan.","furigana":"けいかく|とく|もんだい"}],"notes":"特に highlights something as special: 特に和食が好きです stresses a preference within a larger selection. Before a negated statement it can mean that there is nothing in particular, as in 特に問題はありません. As an adverb it modifies the statement that follows and does not take な.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 15"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"必ず来てください。","romaji":"Kanarazu kite kudasai.","german":"Please be sure to come.","furigana":"かなら|き"},{"japanese":"必ず宿題をしてください。","romaji":"Kanarazu shukudai o shite kudasai.","german":"Be sure to do your homework.","furigana":"かなら|しゅくだい"},{"japanese":"このバスは必ず駅の前に止まります。","romaji":"Kono basu wa kanarazu eki no mae ni tomarimasu.","german":"This bus always stops in front of the station.","furigana":"かなら|えき|まえ|と"}],"notes":"必ず is an adverb meaning \"without exception\" or \"in any case\". In a request it strengthens the obligation: 必ず来てください. In a statement it describes something that reliably always happens. It is used directly with the verb and takes no に.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 36"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"絶対に忘れません。","romaji":"Zettai ni wasuremasen.","german":"I will never forget.","furigana":"ぜったい|わす"},{"japanese":"絶対に遅刻しないでください。","romaji":"Zettai ni chikoku shinaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't be late under any circumstances.","furigana":"ぜったい|ちこく"}],"notes":"絶対に strengthens a statement: \"for sure\" or \"under no circumstances\". With a negative such as 忘れません, it means \"by no means\". Before a request it can sound very emphatic; the two examples involve a firm promise and a strict prohibition.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 36"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"やはり日本語は難しいです。","romaji":"Yahari nihongo wa muzukashii desu.","german":"Japanese is difficult after all.","furigana":"にほんご|むずか"},{"japanese":"やはり日本語は難しいですね。","romaji":"Yahari nihongo wa muzukashii desu ne.","german":"Japanese is difficult after all, isn't it?","furigana":"にほんご|むずか"},{"japanese":"予報どおり、やはり雨が降りました。","romaji":"Yohō dōri, yahari ame ga furimashita.","german":"As forecast, it did rain after all.","furigana":"よほう|あめ|ふ"}],"notes":"やはり means \"as expected\" or \"after all / in the end\". It often connects the statement with an earlier assumption or a reconfirmed judgment. やっぱり is the common colloquial form. やはり is an adverb here, not a form of the verb やはる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"なかなか上手ですね。","romaji":"Nakanaka jōzu desu ne.","german":"You're quite good, aren't you?","furigana":"じょうず"},{"japanese":"バスがなかなか来ません。","romaji":"Basu ga nakanaka kimasen.","german":"The bus just won't come.","furigana":"き"}],"notes":"なかなか has two important patterns. With a positive evaluation it means \"quite\" or \"surprisingly good\": なかなか上手. With a negation it shows that something does not happen easily or soon despite expectations: なかなか来ません means that the bus just won't come.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 18"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"そろそろ行きましょう。","romaji":"Sorosoro ikimashō.","german":"It's about time to go.","furigana":"い"},{"japanese":"そろそろ帰りましょう。","romaji":"Sorosoro kaerimashō.","german":"It's about time to head home.","furigana":"かえ"},{"japanese":"そろそろ電車が来るはずです。","romaji":"Sorosoro densha ga kuru hazu desu.","german":"The train should be coming soon.","furigana":"でんしゃ|く"}],"notes":"そろそろ can announce that something will happen soon, or say that it is gradually time for an action. そろそろ帰りましょう therefore means \"We should be heading home soon\". In these sentences it describes the right moment; the dictionary meaning \"carefully / slowly forward\" is a different use.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 23"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"しっかり頑張ります。","romaji":"Shikkari ganbarimasu.","german":"I will really do my best.","furigana":"がんば"},{"japanese":"しっかり食べてください。","romaji":"Shikkari tabete kudasai.","german":"Eat properly!","furigana":"た"},{"japanese":"手すりをしっかり握ってください。","romaji":"Tesuri o shikkari nigitte kudasai.","german":"Please hold the handrail firmly.","furigana":"て|にぎ"}],"notes":"しっかり refers to something firm or to a thorough, sufficient action. 手すりをしっかり握る means \"to grip the handrail firmly\"; しっかり食べる means \"to eat properly / enough\". It stands before the verb as an adverb and can also emphatically urge careful action.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"できるだけ早く来てください。","romaji":"Dekiru dake hayaku kite kudasai.","german":"Please come as soon as possible.","furigana":"はや|き"},{"japanese":"できるだけ野菜を食べるようにしています。","romaji":"Dekiru dake yasai o taberu yō ni shite imasu.","german":"I try to eat as many vegetables as possible.","furigana":"やさい|た"}],"notes":"できるだけ pushes an action to the limit of what is possible: \"as ... as possible\". A manner or quantity can follow, for example できるだけ早く for the greatest possible speed. In できるだけ野菜を食べる the target amount is meant; the phrase does not guarantee that the goal is fully achieved.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 12"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お互いに助け合いましょう。","romaji":"Otagai ni tasukeaimashō.","german":"Let's help each other.","furigana":"たが|たす|あ"},{"japanese":"二人はお互いに顔を見合わせました。","romaji":"Futari wa otagai ni kao o miawasemashita.","german":"The two looked at each other.","furigana":"ふたり|たが|かお|みあ"}],"notes":"お互いに means that an action takes place reciprocally between people. It can stand before the verb: お互いに顔を見合わせる means to look at each other. The お belongs to the more polite form of 互いに; in お互いに助け合う, the adverb and the verb both emphasize mutual help.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"それぞれ違います。","romaji":"Sorezore chigaimasu.","german":"Each one is different.","furigana":"ちが"},{"japanese":"それぞれの意見を聞きましょう。","romaji":"Sorezore no iken o kikimashō.","german":"Let's hear each person's opinion.","furigana":"いけん|き"}],"notes":"それぞれ means \"each individually\" or \"respectively\". It can itself stand for a group of people or things, as in それぞれ違います. Before a noun it takes の: それぞれの意見, \"the respective opinion / each person's opinion\". The dictionary lists this nominal use alongside the adverbial one.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"やっと終わりました。","romaji":"Yatto owarimashita.","german":"It's finally over.","furigana":"お"},{"japanese":"やっと仕事が終わりました。","romaji":"Yatto shigoto ga owarimashita.","german":"The work is finally finished.","furigana":"しごと|お"},{"japanese":"走って、やっと電車に間に合いました。","romaji":"Hashitte, yatto densha ni ma ni aimashita.","german":"I ran and just barely made the train.","furigana":"はし|でんしゃ|ま|あ"}],"notes":"やっと means either \"finally\" after effort or waiting, or \"just barely / with difficulty\". やっと終わりました emphasizes the completion achieved. With やっと間に合いました, the narrow time margin can come to the foreground. The situation shows which meaning fits.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 36"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"うまくいきました。","romaji":"Umaku ikimashita.","german":"It went well."},{"japanese":"うまく説明できません。","romaji":"Umaku setsumei dekimasen.","german":"I can't explain it well.","furigana":"せつめい"}],"notes":"うまく is the adverbial form of うまい. Here it describes successful or skillful action: うまくいく, \"to go well\", and うまく説明する, \"to explain well\". The statement concerns the execution or the result; the use of うまい for tasty food is not taught in these examples.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 44"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大変お世話になりました。","romaji":"Taihen osewa ni narimashita.","german":"Vielen Dank für alles.","furigana":"たいへん|せわ"},{"japanese":"留学中にたくさんの人にお世話になりました。","romaji":"Ryūgakuchū ni takusan no hito ni osewa ni narimashita.","german":"Während meines Auslandsstudiums war ich vielen Menschen zu Dank verpflichtet.","furigana":"りゅうがくちゅう|ひと|せわ"},{"japanese":"入院中は、看護師さんにお世話になります。","romaji":"Nyūinchū wa, kangoshi-san ni osewa ni narimasu.","german":"Während des Krankenhausaufenthalts werden mich die Pflegekräfte betreuen.","furigana":"にゅういんちゅう|かんごし|せわ"}],"notes":"お世話になる heißt, von jemandem betreut, unterstützt oder aufgenommen zu werden. Die helfende Person steht mit に. お世話になりました ist zugleich eine höfliche Dankesformel für erhaltene Hilfe; die wörtliche Übersetzung „in Ihrer Fürsorge gewesen sein“ wäre im Deutschen oft unnatürlich. Die Gegenwart kann auch eine bevorstehende Betreuung beschreiben.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 23"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お疲れ様でした。","romaji":"Otsukaresama deshita.","german":"Thank you for your hard work. / Good work today.","furigana":"つか|さま"},{"japanese":"お疲れ様でした。今日もいい仕事でしたね。","romaji":"Otsukaresama deshita. Kyō mo ii shigoto deshita ne.","german":"Thank you for your hard work. You did a good job today, too.","furigana":"つか|さま|きょう|しごと"},{"japanese":"先に帰ります。皆さん、お疲れ様です。","romaji":"Saki ni kaerimasu. Minasan, otsukaresama desu.","german":"I'm heading home first. Thanks for your hard work, everyone.","furigana":"さき|かえ|みな|つか|さま"}],"notes":"お疲れ様 acknowledges the effort of others. The common forms are お疲れ様です and お疲れ様でした. Among colleagues, the phrase can mean \"Have a good evening\" when parting; it is not a literal statement that someone looks tired. For unfamiliar customers or superiors, the appropriate address depends on the relationship and the situation.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"困っている人を助けます。","romaji":"Komatte iru hito o tasukemasu.","german":"I help people in trouble.","furigana":"こま|ひと|たす"},{"japanese":"困っている人を助けたいです。","romaji":"Komatte iru hito o tasuketai desu.","german":"I want to help people in need.","furigana":"こま|ひと|たす"},{"japanese":"川に落ちた子供を助けました。","romaji":"Kawa ni ochita kodomo o tasukemashita.","german":"I rescued a child who had fallen into the river.","furigana":"かわ|お|こども|たす"}],"notes":"助ける is a transitive ichidan verb for helping or rescuing. The person takes を: 人を助ける. It can denote everyday support as well as rescue from danger. The te-form is 助けて; 助かる, by contrast, describes someone receiving help or being saved.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 22"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"間違えたら謝りましょう。","romaji":"Machigaetara ayamarimashō.","german":"If you make a mistake, you should apologize.","furigana":"まちが|あやま"},{"japanese":"遅れたことを謝りました。","romaji":"Okureta koto o ayamarimashita.","german":"I apologized for being late.","furigana":"おく|あやま"}],"notes":"謝る means \"to apologize\". The reason can be expressed with ことを: 遅れたことを謝る. The person one apologizes to takes に. Despite the ending る, it is a godan verb: 謝ります, 謝って. The similar-sounding spelling 誤る means \"to make a mistake\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 18"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先生に褒められました。","romaji":"Sensei ni homeraremashita.","german":"I was praised by my teacher.","furigana":"せんせい|ほ"},{"japanese":"先生に褒められて嬉しかったです。","romaji":"Sensei ni homerarete ureshikatta desu.","german":"I was happy to be praised by my teacher.","furigana":"せんせい|ほ|うれ"},{"japanese":"子供の努力を褒めました。","romaji":"Kodomo no doryoku o homemashita.","german":"I praised the child's efforts.","furigana":"こども|どりょく|ほ"}],"notes":"褒める is the transitive ichidan verb for \"to praise\". A person or their achievement takes を. The passive form 褒められる means \"to be praised\"; the person who praises then takes に. In the active example, the child's effort is explicitly what is praised.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子供を叱りました。","romaji":"Kodomo o shikarimashita.","german":"Ich habe das Kind getadelt.","furigana":"こども|しか"},{"japanese":"母に叱られました。","romaji":"Haha ni shikararemashita.","german":"Ich wurde von meiner Mutter getadelt.","furigana":"はは|しか"}],"notes":"叱る bedeutet, jemanden wegen seines Verhaltens zu tadeln oder auszuschimpfen. Die Person steht mit を. Es ist ein transitives Godan-Verb mit る: 叱ります, 叱って. 叱られる ist das Passiv „getadelt werden“. Im Deutschen sagt man „das Kind tadeln“ oder „mit dem Kind schimpfen“.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"空港に迎えに行きます。","romaji":"Kūkō ni mukae ni ikimasu.","german":"I'm going to the airport to pick someone up.","furigana":"くうこう|むか|い"},{"japanese":"空港で友達を迎えます。","romaji":"Kūkō de tomodachi o mukaemasu.","german":"I pick up my friend at the airport.","furigana":"くうこう|ともだち|むか"},{"japanese":"家族で新年を迎えました。","romaji":"Kazoku de shinnen o mukaemashita.","german":"We welcomed the new year with our family.","furigana":"かぞく|しんねん|むか"}],"notes":"迎える is a transitive ichidan verb. It means, for example, to receive or pick up an arriving person; the person takes を. 迎えに行く stresses that you go in order to pick someone up. With time periods such as 新年, it can also mean to experience or welcome the beginning.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"友達を見送ります。","romaji":"Tomodachi o miokurimasu.","german":"I see my friend off.","furigana":"ともだち|みおく"},{"japanese":"駅で家族を見送りました。","romaji":"Eki de kazoku o miokurimashita.","german":"I saw my family off at the station.","furigana":"えき|かぞく|みおく"},{"japanese":"雨のため、出発を見送りました。","romaji":"Ame no tame, shuppatsu o miokurimashita.","german":"Because of the rain, we put off our departure for now.","furigana":"あめ|しゅっぱつ|みおく"}],"notes":"見送る means to say goodbye to someone as they set off, often by accompanying them part of the way. The person takes を. For a plan or a departure, 見送る can also mean \"to put off for now\". It is a transitive godan verb ending in る; 見送って is the て-form.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"パーティーに招待されました。","romaji":"Pātī ni shōtai saremashita.","german":"I was invited to a party.","furigana":"しょうたい"},{"japanese":"友達をパーティーに招待しました。","romaji":"Tomodachi o pātī ni shōtai shimashita.","german":"I invited my friends to the party.","furigana":"ともだち|しょうたい"}],"notes":"招待する means to invite someone explicitly. The invited person takes を, and the occasion takes に: 友達をパーティーに招待する. 招待される is the passive \"to be invited\". 招待 alone is the noun \"invitation\"; the verb form combines it with する.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 19"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先生を尊敬しています。","romaji":"Sensei o sonkei shite imasu.","german":"I respect my teacher.","furigana":"せんせい|そんけい"},{"japanese":"私が一番尊敬している人は祖母です。","romaji":"Watashi ga ichiban sonkei shite iru hito wa sobo desu.","german":"The person I respect most is my grandmother.","furigana":"わたし|いちばんそんけい|ひと|そぼ"},{"japanese":"彼の意見には反対ですが、彼の誠実さは尊敬しています。","romaji":"Kare no iken ni wa hantai desu ga, kare no seijitsusa wa sonkei shite imasu.","german":"I disagree with his opinion, but I respect his sincerity.","furigana":"かれ|いけん|はんたい|かれ|せいじつ|そんけい"}],"notes":"尊敬する means to hold someone in high regard because of their abilities, attitude or achievements. The person respected takes を; 尊敬しています describes an existing attitude. As a suru-verb it forms 尊敬して・尊敬しない. Respect does not rule out a differing opinion.","levelNote":"Derived from 尊敬 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"幸せを感じます。","romaji":"Shiawase o kanjimasu.","german":"I feel happiness.","furigana":"しあわ|かん"},{"japanese":"春の暖かさを感じます。","romaji":"Haru no atatakasa o kanjimasu.","german":"I feel the warmth of spring.","furigana":"はる|あたた|かん"}],"notes":"感じる means to perceive or feel, for instance warmth, happiness or a mood. The thing perceived takes を. It is an Ichidan verb; 感じます and 感じて are regular forms. The examples distinguish an inner feeling from a physically perceptible warmth.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"よく考えてください。","romaji":"Yoku kangaete kudasai.","german":"Please think carefully.","furigana":"かんが"},{"japanese":"将来のことを考えています。","romaji":"Shōrai no koto o kangaete imasu.","german":"I'm thinking about the future.","furigana":"しょうらい|かんが"}],"notes":"考える means \"to think / to consider\" and can also express planning an action. The topic can take のことを: 将来のことを考える. よく考えてください is a request to think thoroughly. It is a transitive ichidan verb; the te-form is 考えて.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 18"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"間違いに気がつきました。","romaji":"Machigai ni ki ga tsukimashita.","german":"I noticed the mistake.","furigana":"まちが|き"},{"japanese":"財布を忘れたことに気がつきました。","romaji":"Saifu o wasureta koto ni ki ga tsukimashita.","german":"I noticed that I had forgotten my wallet.","furigana":"さいふ|わす|き"},{"japanese":"しばらくして気がつくと、病院のベッドにいました。","romaji":"Shibaraku shite ki ga tsuku to, byōin no beddo ni imashita.","german":"When I came to after a while, I was in a hospital bed.","furigana":"き|びょういん"}],"notes":"気がつく is a fixed phrase for \"to notice / to become aware of something\". What is noticed takes に, also after a sentence with こと: 忘れたことに気がつく. In a different situation the phrase can mean \"to regain consciousness\". The verb component is a godan verb ending in く: 気がつきます, 気がついて.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"朝の光がきれいです。","romaji":"Asa no hikari ga kirei desu.","german":"The morning light is beautiful.","furigana":"あさ|ひかり"},{"japanese":"朝の光が窓から入ります。","romaji":"Asa no hikari ga mado kara hairimasu.","german":"The morning light comes in through the window.","furigana":"あさ|ひかり|まど|はい"},{"japanese":"懐中電灯の光で地図を読みました。","romaji":"Kaichū dentō no hikari de chizu o yomimashita.","german":"I read the map by the light of a flashlight.","furigana":"かいちゅうでんとう|ひかり|ちず|よ"}],"notes":"光 here means \"light\" or \"ray of light\". The reading is ひかり; the reading こう belongs to other uses, for example in compounds. With で, light can denote the means of an action: 光で地図を読む. The examples involve natural morning light and an artificial light source.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いろいろな種類があります。","romaji":"Iroiro na shurui ga arimasu.","german":"There are various kinds.","furigana":"しゅるい"},{"japanese":"この店にはたくさんの種類のケーキがあります。","romaji":"Kono mise ni wa takusan no shurui no kēki ga arimasu.","german":"This store has many kinds of cake.","furigana":"みせ|しゅるい"}],"notes":"種類 refers to a kind, type, or category. Before another noun, の is used: いろいろな種類のケーキ, \"various kinds of cake\". たくさんの種類 emphasizes variety, not the number of individual pieces. The independent noun is taught here; an attached counting use is a separate grammatical use.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 19"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"料理の材料を買います。","romaji":"Ryōri no zairyō o kaimasu.","german":"Ich kaufe Kochzutaten.","furigana":"りょうり|ざいりょう|か"},{"japanese":"料理の材料を買いに行きます。","romaji":"Ryōri no zairyō o kai ni ikimasu.","german":"Ich gehe die Zutaten fürs Kochen kaufen.","furigana":"りょうり|ざいりょう|か|い"},{"japanese":"この机の材料は木です。","romaji":"Kono tsukue no zairyō wa ki desu.","german":"Das Material dieses Tisches ist Holz.","furigana":"つくえ|ざいりょう|き"}],"notes":"材料 kann Zutaten für ein Gericht oder Material zur Herstellung eines Gegenstands meinen. Das Erzeugnis steht oft vor の: 料理の材料, 机の材料. Bei Holz oder Metall passt im Deutschen „Material“, beim Kochen „Zutaten“. Die Lesung ざいりょう wird zairyō geschrieben.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 34"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"道具を準備してください。","romaji":"Dōgu o junbi shite kudasai.","german":"Please prepare the tools.","furigana":"どうぐ|じゅんび"},{"japanese":"掃除の道具はどこですか。","romaji":"Sōji no dōgu wa doko desu ka.","german":"Where are the cleaning supplies?","furigana":"そうじ|どうぐ"}],"notes":"道具 is a general word for tools, devices, and utensils needed for an activity. The activity often precedes it with の: 掃除の道具, \"cleaning supplies\". Here it refers to concrete aids. The long first syllable どう is rendered with ō in dōgu.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 27"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いい機会です。","romaji":"Ii kikai desu.","german":"This is a good opportunity.","furigana":"きかい"},{"japanese":"またお会いする機会があれば嬉しいです。","romaji":"Mata oai suru kikai ga areba ureshii desu.","german":"I'd be happy if there is a chance to meet again.","furigana":"あ|きかい|うれ"}],"notes":"機会 means \"opportunity / chance\" and is read きかい. The possible action can precede the noun as a verb: お会いする機会, \"an opportunity to meet\". A favorable opportunity is いい機会. 機械, \"machine\", sounds the same but is a different word.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 35"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"例を挙げてください。","romaji":"Rei o agete kudasai.","german":"Please give an example.","furigana":"れい|あ"},{"japanese":"例を挙げて説明します。","romaji":"Rei o agete setsumei shimasu.","german":"I'll explain using examples.","furigana":"れい|あ|せつめい"}],"notes":"例 means \"example\" here. 例を挙げる is the fixed phrase for \"to give / cite an example\"; 例を使う means \"to use an example\". The reading is れい, written rei. The other dictionary meanings, \"precedent\" or \"custom\", are not used in these sentences.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"以上で終わります。","romaji":"Ijō de owarimasu.","german":"That concludes it.","furigana":"いじょう|お"},{"japanese":"十八歳以上の方が対象です。","romaji":"Jūhassai ijō no kata ga taishō desu.","german":"It is aimed at people aged 18 and over.","furigana":"じゅうはっさいいじょう|かた|たいしょう"},{"japanese":"以上の点に注意してください。","romaji":"Ijō no ten ni chūi shite kudasai.","german":"Please pay attention to the points above.","furigana":"いじょう|てん|ちゅうい"}],"notes":"以上 after a number includes that number: 十八歳以上 means \"from age 18\", that is, including exactly 18. Here it is a nominal postposition. As a closing formula, 以上で終わります means \"That concludes it\". \"More than 18\" without including the limit would be wrong in this numerical use. Before a noun, 以上の also refers to the points mentioned earlier.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 40"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"十歳以下は無料です。","romaji":"Jussai ika wa muryō desu.","german":"Free for children ten years old and under.","furigana":"じゅっさいいか|むりょう"},{"japanese":"五百円以下の商品を探しています。","romaji":"Gohyaku-en ika no shōhin o sagashite imasu.","german":"I'm looking for products costing 500 yen or less.","furigana":"ごひゃくえんいか|しょうひん|さが"},{"japanese":"以下の質問に答えてください。","romaji":"Ika no shitsumon ni kotaete kudasai.","german":"Please answer the following questions.","furigana":"いか|しつもん|こた"}],"notes":"以下 after a number includes the limit: 十歳以下 also covers ten-year-olds; 五百円以下 means \"500 yen at most\". It is a nominal suffix-like word placed after the noun. The dictionary meaning \"the following\" is a different usage. In these examples, \"under ten / under 500\" as an exclusive limit would be wrong. 以下の質問, on the other hand, refers to the questions that follow.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 40"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日曜日以外は働きます。","romaji":"Nichiyōbi igai wa hatarakimasu.","german":"I work except on Sundays.","furigana":"にちようびいがい|はたら"},{"japanese":"日本語以外の言語も話せます。","romaji":"Nihongo igai no gengo mo hanasemasu.","german":"I can also speak languages other than Japanese.","furigana":"にほんごいがい|げんご|はな"}],"notes":"以外 follows the excluded item: 日曜日以外 means \"except on Sundays\". With の it connects to a noun: 日本語以外の言語, \"languages other than Japanese\". Depending on the statement, it can mean \"except / apart from\" or \"in addition to\". It is a nominal postposed word and not a sentence particle.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"席が空いていますか。","romaji":"Seki ga aite imasu ka.","german":"Is this seat free?","furigana":"せき|あ"},{"japanese":"窓側の席をお願いします。","romaji":"Madogawa no seki o onegai shimasu.","german":"A window seat, please.","furigana":"まどがわ|せき|ねが"}],"notes":"席 means a seat, for example on a train, in a restaurant or in a class. 席が空いている means \"a seat is free\". For a window seat you say 窓側の席. It refers to the usable place, not necessarily the chair as an object; for that, 椅子 fits.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 33"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"庭で花を植えます。","romaji":"Niwa de hana o uemasu.","german":"I plant flowers in the garden.","furigana":"にわ|はな|う"},{"japanese":"庭の木に鳥がいます。","romaji":"Niwa no ki ni tori ga imasu.","german":"There is a bird in the tree in the garden.","furigana":"にわ|き|とり"}],"notes":"庭 is a garden or yard at a house. 庭で names it as the place of an activity, 庭に as the place where something exists. With の you connect the word to an object in the garden: 庭の木. The two vowels in にわ are short.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 6"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"部屋の隅に置いてください。","romaji":"Heya no sumi ni oite kudasai.","german":"Please put it in the corner of the room.","furigana":"へや|すみ|お"},{"japanese":"部屋の隅に本棚があります。","romaji":"Heya no sumi ni hondana ga arimasu.","german":"There is a bookshelf in the corner of the room.","furigana":"へや|すみ|ほんだな"},{"japanese":"地図の隅に、小さな説明が書いてあります。","romaji":"Chizu no sumi ni, chīsana setsumei ga kaite arimasu.","german":"A small explanation is written in one corner of the map.","furigana":"ちず|すみ|ちい|せつめい|か"}],"notes":"隅（すみ）means a corner or edge area within a surface or room. ～の隅に names the place, as in 部屋の隅に. The spelling 角 is also recorded for すみ; for the corner of a room, the ordinary spelling 隅 is suitable. The same kanji 角 can be read かど, つの or かく in other words. The meaning \"horn\" belongs to つの and is not the meaning of すみ practiced here. In the third example, a small explanation lies in one corner of the map.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 30"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の代わりに行ってください。","romaji":"Watashi no kawari ni itte kudasai.","german":"Please go in my place.","furigana":"わたし|か|い"},{"japanese":"コーヒーの代わりにお茶を飲みます。","romaji":"Kōhī no kawari ni ocha o nomimasu.","german":"I drink tea instead of coffee.","furigana":"か|ちゃ|の"},{"japanese":"手伝ってくれた代わりに、昼ご飯をごちそうします。","romaji":"Tetsudatte kureta kawari ni, hirugohan o gochisō shimasu.","german":"In return for your help, I'll treat you to lunch.","furigana":"てつだ|か|ひる|はん"}],"notes":"代わりに means \"instead of\" or \"in return for\". After a noun, の is used: 私の代わりに. It can refer to a substituting person or thing. After a clause such as 手伝ってくれた代わりに, it expresses compensation for the help given. The whole expression is taught here.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お湯を沸かします。","romaji":"Oyu o wakashimasu.","german":"I boil water.","furigana":"ゆ|わ"},{"japanese":"お風呂を沸かします。","romaji":"Ofuro o wakashimasu.","german":"I heat the bath water.","furigana":"ふろ|わ"}],"notes":"沸かす is a transitive godan verb ending in す: you heat a liquid, often until it boils. The thing heated takes を. お風呂を沸かす means to heat the bath water, not merely to run water into the tub. 沸く, by contrast, describes the water becoming hot or boiling.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"体が冷えました。","romaji":"Karada ga hiemashita.","german":"My body got cold.","furigana":"からだ|ひ"},{"japanese":"夜になると体が冷えます。","romaji":"Yoru ni naru to karada ga hiemasu.","german":"My body gets cold at night.","furigana":"よる|からだ|ひ"},{"japanese":"冷蔵庫でジュースが冷えました。","romaji":"Reizōko de jūsu ga hiemashita.","german":"The juice got cold in the refrigerator.","furigana":"れいぞうこ|ひ"}],"notes":"冷える describes something becoming cold or cooling down. It is an intransitive ichidan verb; the thing that cools takes が. It can refer to the body, a drink or a room. 冷やす, by contrast, means to actively cool something and requires an object with を.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"スープを温めます。","romaji":"Sūpu o atatamemasu.","german":"I'm heating up the soup.","furigana":"あたた"},{"japanese":"スープを電子レンジで温めます。","romaji":"Sūpu o denshi renji de atatamemasu.","german":"I heat the soup in the microwave.","furigana":"でんし|あたた"},{"japanese":"手を温めました。","romaji":"Te o atatamemashita.","german":"I warmed my hands.","furigana":"て|あたた"}],"notes":"温める means to warm something up or make it warmer. The object takes を. It is a transitive Ichidan verb; 温めて is the て-form. The reading あたためる is chosen here. Heating a liquid to boiling is described more precisely by 沸かす.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"足を踏まないでください。","romaji":"Ashi o fumanaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't step on my foot.","furigana":"あし|ふ"},{"japanese":"草を踏まないでください。","romaji":"Kusa o fumanaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't walk on the grass.","furigana":"くさ|ふ"}],"notes":"踏む here means \"to step on something\". The thing stepped on takes を: 足を踏む, 草を踏む. It is a transitive godan verb ending in む; the て-form is 踏んで. With ないでください you ask someone not to step on or walk on something.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 21"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"財布を盗まれました。","romaji":"Saifu o nusumaremashita.","german":"My wallet was stolen.","furigana":"さいふ|ぬす"},{"japanese":"誰かが自転車を盗みました。","romaji":"Dareka ga jitensha o nusumimashita.","german":"Somebody stole a bicycle.","furigana":"だれ|じてんしゃ|ぬす"}],"notes":"盗む is the transitive godan verb for \"to steal\". The stolen object takes を. 財布を盗まれました is a typical adversative passive: the speaker suffered the loss. The て-form is 盗んで; 盗まれる means \"to have something stolen / to be stolen\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 21"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"騒がないでください。","romaji":"Sawaganaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't make noise.","furigana":"さわ"},{"japanese":"夜中に騒がないでください。","romaji":"Yonaka ni sawaganaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't make noise at night.","furigana":"よなか|さわ"},{"japanese":"パーティーでみんなと騒ぎました。","romaji":"Pātī de minna to sawagimashita.","german":"I partied wildly with everyone at the party.","furigana":"さわ"}],"notes":"騒ぐ can mean \"to make noise\" or \"to celebrate boisterously\". It is an intransitive godan verb ending in ぐ: 騒いで, 騒ぎます. A request like 騒がないでください asks for quiet. In the party sentence, on the other hand, cheerful, loud celebrating is meant.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"誘いを断りました。","romaji":"Sasoi o kotowarimashita.","german":"I turned down the invitation.","furigana":"さそ|ことわ"},{"japanese":"丁寧に断りました。","romaji":"Teinei ni kotowarimashita.","german":"I declined politely.","furigana":"ていねい|ことわ"},{"japanese":"時間がなくて、仕事の依頼を断りました。","romaji":"Jikan ga nakute, shigoto no irai o kotowarimashita.","german":"I didn't have time, so I turned down the job request.","furigana":"じかん|しごと|いらい|ことわ"}],"notes":"断る here means to decline an invitation or request. The thing declined takes を: 誘いを断る. 丁寧に断る emphasizes a polite refusal. It is a transitive Godan verb ending in る; 断って is the て-form. The other meaning, \"to give notice in advance / ask permission\", is not used here.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"友達を映画に誘いました。","romaji":"Tomodachi o eiga ni sasoimashita.","german":"I invited my friend to the movies.","furigana":"ともだち|えいが|さそ"},{"japanese":"週末、山登りに誘われたので、一緒に行くことにしました。","romaji":"Shūmatsu, yamanobori ni sasowareta node, issho ni iku koto ni shimashita.","german":"I was invited to go hiking at the weekend, so I decided to go along.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|やまのぼ|さそ|いっしょ|い"},{"japanese":"甘い香りに誘われて、パン屋に入りました。","romaji":"Amai kaori ni sasowarete, pan'ya ni hairimashita.","german":"Lured by the sweet smell, I went into the bakery.","furigana":"あま|かお|さそ|や|はい"}],"notes":"誘う is a transitive godan verb ending in う: 誘います・誘って・誘わない. When inviting, the person takes を and the activity takes に: 友達を映画に誘う. It can also mean that something attracts someone; 誘われる is the passive form, \"to be invited or lured\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 15"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"会議に出席します。","romaji":"Kaigi ni shusseki shimasu.","german":"I will attend the meeting.","furigana":"かいぎ|しゅっせき"},{"japanese":"明日の会議に出席します。","romaji":"Ashita no kaigi ni shusseki shimasu.","german":"I will attend tomorrow's meeting.","furigana":"あした|かいぎ|しゅっせき"},{"japanese":"いとこの結婚式に出席しました。","romaji":"Itoko no kekkonshiki ni shusseki shimashita.","german":"I attended my cousin's wedding.","furigana":"けっこんしき|しゅっせき"}],"notes":"出席する means to be present at a scheduled event. The event takes に: 会議に出席する. 出席 alone is the noun \"attendance\". 欠席する is the opposite. The expression suits meetings, classes or a ceremony.","levelNote":"Derived from 出席 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"病気で欠席しました。","romaji":"Byōki de kesseki shimashita.","german":"I was absent because of illness.","furigana":"びょうき|けっせき"},{"japanese":"病気で学校を欠席しました。","romaji":"Byōki de gakkō o kesseki shimashita.","german":"I was absent from school because of illness.","furigana":"びょうき|がっこう|けっせき"},{"japanese":"来週の会議は欠席します。","romaji":"Raishū no kaigi wa kesseki shimasu.","german":"I will be absent from next week's meeting.","furigana":"らいしゅう|かいぎ|けっせき"}],"notes":"欠席する means to be absent from an expected appointment. School, class or a meeting can take を: 学校を欠席する. A cause often follows with で, such as 病気で. The verb describes the absence; by itself it does not say whether the absence is excused.","levelNote":"Derived from 欠席 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"学校に遅刻しました。","romaji":"Gakkō ni chikoku shimashita.","german":"I was late for school.","furigana":"がっこう|ちこく"},{"japanese":"電車が遅れて遅刻しました。","romaji":"Densha ga okurete chikoku shimashita.","german":"The train was delayed and I was late.","furigana":"でんしゃ|おく|ちこく"}],"notes":"遅刻する means to arrive later than the agreed or prescribed time. The destination takes に: 学校に遅刻する. It concerns a person's being late; the delay of a train is described by 電車が遅れる instead. 遅刻 alone is the noun \"being late\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 11"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"英語に翻訳してください。","romaji":"Eigo ni honyaku shite kudasai.","german":"Bitte übersetzen Sie ins Englische.","furigana":"えいご|ほんやく"},{"japanese":"この手紙を英語に翻訳してください。","romaji":"Kono tegami o eigo ni honyaku shite kudasai.","german":"Bitte übersetzen Sie diesen Brief ins Englische.","furigana":"てがみ|えいご|ほんやく"},{"japanese":"ドイツ語の説明書を日本語に翻訳しました。","romaji":"Doitsugo no setsumeisho o Nihongo ni honyaku shimashita.","german":"Ich habe die deutsche Anleitung ins Japanische übersetzt.","furigana":"ご|せつめいしょ|にほんご|ほんやく"}],"notes":"翻訳する heißt hier, einen schriftlichen Text in eine andere Sprache zu übertragen. Der Text steht mit を, die Zielsprache mit に: 手紙を英語に翻訳する. 翻訳 ist das Nomen „Übersetzung“. Mündliches Dolmetschen heißt gewöhnlich 通訳する; die biologische Fachbedeutung wird hier nicht gelehrt.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 22"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"貯金を増やしたいです。","romaji":"Chokin o fuyashitai desu.","german":"I want to increase my savings.","furigana":"ちょきん|ふ"},{"japanese":"語彙を増やしたいです。","romaji":"Goi o fuyashitai desu.","german":"I want to expand my vocabulary.","furigana":"ごい|ふ"}],"notes":"増やす is the transitive Godan verb for \"to increase\". The thing increased takes を: 貯金を増やす, 語彙を増やす. 増える, in contrast, describes an increase with no acting subject named. The て-form is 増やして; 減らす is the opposite.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ゴミを減らしましょう。","romaji":"Gomi o herashimashō.","german":"Let's reduce our garbage.","furigana":"へ"},{"japanese":"食費を減らしたいです。","romaji":"Shokuhi o herashitai desu.","german":"I want to cut down my food costs.","furigana":"しょくひ|へ"}],"notes":"減らす means to actively reduce an amount. The thing reduced takes を: ゴミを減らす, 食費を減らす. It is a transitive Godan verb ending in す. 減る, in contrast, means that something becomes less. The て-form is 減らして.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"怪我が治りました。","romaji":"Kega ga naorimashita.","german":"The injury has healed.","furigana":"けが|なお"},{"japanese":"風邪はもう治りましたか。","romaji":"Kaze wa mō naorimashita ka.","german":"Has your cold gotten better yet?","furigana":"かぜ|なお"}],"notes":"治る means recovering from or healing of an illness or injury. It is an intransitive godan verb ending in る; the thing that heals takes が. The て-form is 治って. 直る, with the same reading, often describes a mistake or a broken object being put right again.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"幸せを願います。","romaji":"Shiawase o negaimasu.","german":"I wish for happiness.","furigana":"しあわ|ねが"},{"japanese":"世界の平和を願います。","romaji":"Sekai no heiwa o negaimasu.","german":"I wish for world peace.","furigana":"せかい|へいわ|ねが"},{"japanese":"ここに名前を書いてくださるよう願います。","romaji":"Koko ni namae o kaite kudasaru yō negaimasu.","german":"I ask that you write your name here.","furigana":"なまえ|か|ねが"}],"notes":"願う can express a wish or denote a sincere request. The thing wished for takes を: 幸せを願う, 平和を願う. It is a transitive Godan verb ending in う; 願って is the て-form. お願いします is a common polite request that is translated idiomatically in many situations.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"服が汚れました。","romaji":"Fuku ga yogoremashita.","german":"My clothes got dirty.","furigana":"ふく|よご"},{"japanese":"服が泥で汚れました。","romaji":"Fuku ga doro de yogoremashita.","german":"My clothes got dirty from the mud.","furigana":"ふく|どろ|よご"},{"japanese":"雨で窓が汚れました。","romaji":"Ame de mado ga yogoremashita.","german":"The windows got dirty from the rain.","furigana":"あめ|まど|よご"}],"notes":"汚れる with the reading よごれる means \"to get dirty\". It is an intransitive ichidan verb: the clothes take が, and the cause takes, for example, で. 汚す describes someone making something dirty. The also possible reading けがれる belongs to a different usage and is not taught here.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 18"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"電話番号を間違えました。","romaji":"Denwa bangō o machigaemashita.","german":"I got the phone number wrong.","furigana":"でんわばんごう|まちが"},{"japanese":"名前を間違えてすみません。","romaji":"Namae o machigaete sumimasen.","german":"Sorry, I got your name wrong.","furigana":"なまえ|まちが"},{"japanese":"塩を砂糖と間違えました。","romaji":"Shio o satō to machigaemashita.","german":"I mistook salt for sugar.","furigana":"しお|さとう|まちが"}],"notes":"間違える means to do something wrongly or to mix up two things. The thing affected takes を, for example 名前を間違える. It is a transitive ichidan verb; 間違えて is the て-form. The construction と間違える names what one mistakes something for.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 21"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"教育は大切です。","romaji":"Kyōiku wa taisetsu desu.","german":"Education is important.","furigana":"きょういく|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"子供の教育はとても大切です。","romaji":"Kodomo no kyōiku wa totemo taisetsu desu.","german":"The education of children is very important.","furigana":"こども|きょういく|たいせつ"}],"notes":"教育 can mean school education and teaching, or the upbringing of a child. 子供の教育 can therefore mean \"education\" or \"raising children\", depending on the situation. Here the noun is taught; with する it can also form a verb. The reading きょういく is written kyōiku.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 42"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"成績が上がりました。","romaji":"Seiseki ga agarimashita.","german":"My grades have improved.","furigana":"せいせき|あ"},{"japanese":"今学期の成績がよかったです。","romaji":"Kongakki no seiseki ga yokatta desu.","german":"My grades this semester were good.","furigana":"こんがっき|せいせき"},{"japanese":"試験の成績を先生に見せました。","romaji":"Shiken no seiseki o sensei ni misemashita.","german":"I showed my exam results to the teacher.","furigana":"しけん|せいせき|せんせい|み"}],"notes":"成績 denotes evaluated results or performance. In school it usually means grades: 成績が上がる means \"one's grades improve\". 今学期の成績 names the results of this semester. Outside school it can also denote, for example, a competition result or a professional track record.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 12"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本に留学したいです。","romaji":"Nihon ni ryūgaku shitai desu.","german":"Ich möchte für ein Auslandsstudium nach Japan gehen.","furigana":"にほん|りゅうがく"},{"japanese":"ドイツに留学したいです。","romaji":"Doitsu ni ryūgaku shitai desu.","german":"Ich möchte für ein Auslandsstudium nach Deutschland gehen.","furigana":"りゅうがく"},{"japanese":"留学には多くのお金がかかります。","romaji":"Ryūgaku ni wa ōku no okane ga kakarimasu.","german":"Ein Auslandsstudium kostet viel Geld.","furigana":"りゅうがく|おお|かね"}],"notes":"留学 bedeutet Lernen oder Studieren im Ausland. Die Zielnation steht bei 留学する mit に: 日本に留学する. Das Wort setzt die Perspektive des Herkunftslands voraus; es heißt nicht bloß, im jeweiligen Land irgendeine Ausbildung zu absolvieren. Hier ist der Wortkopf das Nomen, das auch mit する ein Verb bilden kann.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"規則を守りましょう。","romaji":"Kisoku o mamorimashō.","german":"Let's follow the rules.","furigana":"きそく|まも"},{"japanese":"学校の規則を守ってください。","romaji":"Gakkō no kisoku o mamotte kudasai.","german":"Please follow the school rules.","furigana":"がっこう|きそく|まも"},{"japanese":"会議で新しい規則を決めました。","romaji":"Kaigi de atarashii kisoku o kimemashita.","german":"We decided on new rules at the meeting.","furigana":"かいぎ|あたら|きそく|き"}],"notes":"規則 means a rule or regulation, for example for a school, a company or a procedure. 規則を守る means \"to follow the rules\", 規則を決める \"to set rules\". The word sounds more formal than the loanword ルール. A の phrase names the area concerned, for example 学校の規則.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 33"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"駐車場はどこですか。","romaji":"Chūshajō wa doko desu ka.","german":"Where is the parking lot?","furigana":"ちゅうしゃじょう"},{"japanese":"駐車場は満車です。","romaji":"Chūshajō wa mansha desu.","german":"The parking lot is full.","furigana":"ちゅうしゃじょう|まんしゃ"}],"notes":"駐車場 is a place for parking cars: a parking lot, parking area, or parking garage. It does not mean the individual parked car. 満車 describes a completely full parking lot. The long syllables ちゅう and じょう are written with ū and ō in chūshajō.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 23"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"港に船があります。","romaji":"Minato ni fune ga arimasu.","german":"There is a ship in the harbor.","furigana":"みなと|ふね"},{"japanese":"港に大きな船が停まっています。","romaji":"Minato ni ōkina fune ga tomatte imasu.","german":"A large ship is docked in the harbor.","furigana":"みなと|おお|ふね|と"},{"japanese":"港で荷物を船に積みました。","romaji":"Minato de nimotsu o fune ni tsumimashita.","german":"I loaded cargo onto the ship at the harbor.","furigana":"みなと|にもつ|ふね|つ"}],"notes":"港 with the reading みなと means \"harbor\". It refers to a place where ships dock and take on cargo or passengers. に can name the location of a ship, で the place of an activity. The reading こう is used in compounds and is not the standalone head word here.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 35"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"火事がありました。","romaji":"Kaji ga arimashita.","german":"There was a fire.","furigana":"かじ"},{"japanese":"火事の時は119番に電話してください。","romaji":"Kaji no toki wa ichi-ichi-kyū-ban ni denwa shite kudasai.","german":"In case of fire, please call 119.","furigana":"かじ|とき|ばん|でんわ"}],"notes":"火事 means an uncontrolled fire, for example in a building; for fire as a substance or flame, 火 is usually used. 火事がある reports a fire incident, 火事になる its outbreak. The emergency number 119 is read as いち・いち・きゅう in this context.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 39"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"泥棒が捕まりました。","romaji":"Dorobō ga tsukamarimashita.","german":"The thief was caught.","furigana":"どろぼう|つか"},{"japanese":"泥棒が窓から入りました。","romaji":"Dorobō ga mado kara hairimashita.","german":"The thief came in through the window.","furigana":"どろぼう|まど|はい"}],"notes":"泥棒 here means a person who steals: a thief or burglar. 捕まる describes the thief being caught. The dictionary can also use the word for the act; in these sentences the person is meant in each case. The ending ぼう is rendered with ō in dorobō.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 37"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"警察に連絡しました。","romaji":"Keisatsu ni renraku shimashita.","german":"I contacted the police.","furigana":"けいさつ|れんらく"},{"japanese":"すぐに警察に電話しました。","romaji":"Sugu ni keisatsu ni denwa shimashita.","german":"I called the police right away.","furigana":"けいさつ|でんわ"},{"japanese":"警察が交差点で交通を整理しています。","romaji":"Keisatsu ga kōsaten de kōtsū o seiri shite imasu.","german":"The police are directing traffic at the intersection.","furigana":"けいさつ|こうさてん|こうつう|せいり"}],"notes":"警察 means the police as an institution or the responsible police office. 警察に連絡する means to get in touch with the police; this need not be by phone. 警察に電話する explicitly names a phone call. For an individual police officer, 警察官 is more precise.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 33"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"国際交流が大切です。","romaji":"Kokusai kōryū ga taisetsu desu.","german":"International exchange is important.","furigana":"こくさいこうりゅう|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"国際会議に参加しました。","romaji":"Kokusai kaigi ni sanka shimashita.","german":"I took part in an international conference.","furigana":"こくさいかいぎ|さんか"}],"notes":"国際 means \"international\" and is often used as the first part of a compound: 国際交流, \"international exchange\", 国際会議, \"international conference\". Here it is a nominal word component; it is not inflected like an い-adjective.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 18"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"世界の平和を願います。","romaji":"Sekai no heiwa o negaimasu.","german":"I wish for world peace.","furigana":"せかい|へいわ|ねが"},{"japanese":"平和な世界を作りたいです。","romaji":"Heiwa na sekai o tsukuritai desu.","german":"I want to create a peaceful world.","furigana":"へいわ|せかい|つく"}],"notes":"平和 means peace or peaceful coexistence. As a noun it can name the thing wished for with を: 平和を願う. Used adjectivally before a noun, it takes な: 平和な世界, \"a peaceful world\". The reading へいわ is written heiwa, not hēwa.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 42"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"都合が悪いです。","romaji":"Tsugō ga warui desu.","german":"It's not convenient for me.","furigana":"つごう|わる"},{"japanese":"明日は都合が悪いです。","romaji":"Ashita wa tsugō ga warui desu.","german":"Tomorrow is not convenient for me.","furigana":"あした|つごう|わる"},{"japanese":"ご都合のいい日を教えてください。","romaji":"Gotsugō no ii hi o oshiete kudasai.","german":"Please tell me a day that is convenient for you.","furigana":"つごう|ひ|おし"}],"notes":"都合 means personal circumstances or time availability here. 都合がいい means \"it suits me / I have time\", 都合が悪い \"it doesn't suit me\". In polite questions about appointments, ご都合 is often used. In this use the word is not simply a chance that happens to occur, like 機会.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 22"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二人は仲がいいです。","romaji":"Futari wa naka ga ii desu.","german":"The two of them get along well.","furigana":"ふたり|なか"},{"japanese":"あの二人は仲がいいです。","romaji":"Ano futari wa naka ga ii desu.","german":"Those two get along well.","furigana":"ふたり|なか"},{"japanese":"昔は兄と仲が悪かったです。","romaji":"Mukashi wa ani to naka ga warukatta desu.","german":"I used to be on bad terms with my older brother.","furigana":"むかし|あに|なか|わる"}],"notes":"仲 describes the relationship between people. 仲がいい means \"to get along well\", 仲が悪い \"to be on bad terms\". A partner in the relationship can take と: 兄と仲がいい. It is a noun; \"good\" or \"bad\" is expressed by the adjective that follows.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"間違いに気がつきました。","romaji":"Machigai ni ki ga tsukimashita.","german":"I noticed the mistake.","furigana":"まちが|き"},{"japanese":"このメールには間違いがたくさんあります。","romaji":"Kono mēru ni wa machigai ga takusan arimasu.","german":"There are many mistakes in this e-mail.","furigana":"まちが"}],"notes":"間違い here is a mistake or error. 間違いに気がつく means to notice a mistake; 間違いがある states that something contains errors. The noun can also be combined with する: 間違いをする. The card does not cover older meanings such as misconduct.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 19"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お見舞いに行きます。","romaji":"Omimai ni ikimasu.","german":"I'm going to visit someone who is ill.","furigana":"みま|い"},{"japanese":"入院した友達にお見舞いに行きます。","romaji":"Nyūin shita tomodachi ni omimai ni ikimasu.","german":"I'm going to visit my friend who was hospitalized.","furigana":"にゅういん|ともだち|みま|い"},{"japanese":"お見舞いの手紙を書きました。","romaji":"Omimai no tegami o kakimashita.","german":"I wrote a get-well letter.","furigana":"みま|てがみ|か"}],"notes":"お見舞い means a visit or an expression of sympathy during illness or after a misfortune. お見舞いに行く means \"to pay a sick visit\". It can also mean a get-well letter or a corresponding gift. The お gives the expression a polite form; the attack meaning of the dictionary belongs to a different usage.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お祝いの言葉をありがとう。","romaji":"Oiwai no kotoba o arigatō.","german":"Thank you for your words of congratulation.","furigana":"いわ|ことば"},{"japanese":"誕生日のお祝いをしましょう。","romaji":"Tanjōbi no oiwai o shimashō.","german":"Let's celebrate your birthday.","furigana":"たんじょうび|いわ"},{"japanese":"結婚のお祝いとして花を贈りました。","romaji":"Kekkon no oiwai to shite hana o okurimashita.","german":"I sent flowers as a wedding gift.","furigana":"けっこん|いわ|はな|おく"}],"notes":"お祝い can mean congratulations, a celebration or a gift for a joyful occasion. お祝いの言葉 are words of congratulation; 誕生日のお祝い is a birthday celebration. With お祝いとして, a gift can be named as a token of congratulation. The polite form with お is common in everyday speech.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 41"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この工場は車を生産します。","romaji":"Kono kōjō wa kuruma o seisan shimasu.","german":"This factory produces cars.","furigana":"こうじょう|くるま|せいさん"},{"japanese":"この地方は米の生産が盛んです。","romaji":"Kono chihō wa kome no seisan ga sakan desu.","german":"Rice production is thriving in this region.","furigana":"ちほう|こめ|せいさん|さか"}],"notes":"生産 is the noun for production or manufacturing, including in agriculture. With する it becomes the transitive verb \"to produce\": 車を生産する. 米の生産 means rice production. 盛んです here means that a lot is produced or that production is flourishing.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"申し込みは今日までです。","romaji":"Mōshikomi wa kyō made desu.","german":"Anmeldungen sind noch bis heute möglich.","furigana":"もう|こ|きょう"},{"japanese":"申し込みの締め切りは来週です。","romaji":"Mōshikomi no shimekiri wa raishū desu.","german":"Die Bewerbungsfrist endet nächste Woche.","furigana":"もう|こ|し|き|らいしゅう"},{"japanese":"料理教室の申し込みをしました。","romaji":"Ryōri kyōshitsu no mōshikomi o shimashita.","german":"Ich habe mich für einen Kochkurs angemeldet.","furigana":"りょうりきょうしつ|もう|こ"}],"notes":"申し込み ist das Nomen zu 申し込む. Es kann eine Anmeldung zu einer Veranstaltung, eine Bewerbung oder einen Antrag bezeichnen. 締め切り ist die Frist; 申し込みは今日まで heißt, dass die Anmeldung noch bis heute möglich ist. Die passende deutsche Übersetzung hängt davon ab, wofür man sich anmeldet oder bewirbt.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 40"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"手続きをしてください。","romaji":"Tetsuzuki o shite kudasai.","german":"Please complete the procedures.","furigana":"てつづ"},{"japanese":"パスポートの手続きをしました。","romaji":"Pasupōto no tetsuzuki o shimashita.","german":"I completed the formalities for my passport.","furigana":"てつづ"},{"japanese":"受付で手続きの方法を聞きました。","romaji":"Uketsuke de tetsuzuki no hōhō o kikimashita.","german":"I asked at the reception desk how the procedure works.","furigana":"うけつけ|てつづ|ほうほう|き"}],"notes":"手続き means a procedure or the formal steps required, for example for a passport. 手続きをする means \"to complete the formalities\". It refers to the prescribed process, not to practical handiwork. 手続きの方法 names the way of going about it, which you can ask about.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"受付は一階にあります。","romaji":"Uketsuke wa ikkai ni arimasu.","german":"The reception desk is on the first floor.","furigana":"うけつけ|いっかい"},{"japanese":"受付で名前を書いてください。","romaji":"Uketsuke de namae o kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write your name at the reception desk.","furigana":"うけつけ|なまえ|か"}],"notes":"受付 denotes the reception or front desk here, for example in a hotel, office, or clinic. 受付で names the place where you check in or write your name. The usual spelling 受付 is read うけつけ. The meaning \"acceptance of an application\" belongs to a further dictionary use.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"好きなテレビ番組は何ですか。","romaji":"Suki na terebi bangumi wa nan desu ka.","german":"What is your favorite TV program?","furigana":"す|ばんぐみ|なん"},{"japanese":"今朝、ラジオ番組で新しい曲を聞きました。","romaji":"Kesa, rajio bangumi de atarashii kyoku o kikimashita.","german":"This morning I heard a new song on a radio program.","furigana":"けさ|ばんぐみ|あたら|きょく|き"}],"notes":"番組 means an individual show or program on television or radio. 好きな番組 asks about a favorite show; 番組で says on which program something was heard or seen. Here it is not a computer program, which is usually called プログラム.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 28"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この記事を読みました。","romaji":"Kono kiji o yomimashita.","german":"Ich habe diesen Artikel gelesen.","furigana":"きじ|よ"},{"japanese":"新聞で面白い記事を読みました。","romaji":"Shinbun de omoshiroi kiji o yomimashita.","german":"Ich habe einen interessanten Artikel in der Zeitung gelesen.","furigana":"しんぶん|おもしろ|きじ|よ"},{"japanese":"旅行の記事を書きました。","romaji":"Ryokō no kiji o kakimashita.","german":"Ich habe einen Artikel über eine Reise geschrieben.","furigana":"りょこう|きじ|か"}],"notes":"記事 ist ein Artikel oder Bericht, etwa in einer Zeitung, Zeitschrift oder auf einer Nachrichtenseite. 記事を読む heißt „einen Artikel lesen“, 記事を書く „einen Artikel schreiben“. Es ist hier nicht der grammatische „Artikel“ wie „der / die / das“ im Deutschen.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"美術館に行きたいです。","romaji":"Bijutsukan ni ikitai desu.","german":"I want to go to the art museum.","furigana":"びじゅつかん|い"},{"japanese":"美術の授業が好きです。","romaji":"Bijutsu no jugyō ga suki desu.","german":"I like art class.","furigana":"びじゅつ|じゅぎょう|す"},{"japanese":"日本の美術に興味があります。","romaji":"Nihon no bijutsu ni kyōmi ga arimasu.","german":"I'm interested in Japanese art.","furigana":"にほん|びじゅつ|きょうみ"}],"notes":"美術 denotes the fine arts, such as painting or sculpture. 美術の授業 is art class. 美術館 is an art museum and contains the word as a component. For art and artistic activities in a broader sense, 芸術 is a more common umbrella term.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 13"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この肉は硬いです。","romaji":"Kono niku wa katai desu.","german":"This meat is tough.","furigana":"にく|かた"},{"japanese":"このパンは硬くて食べにくいです。","romaji":"Kono pan wa katakute tabenikui desu.","german":"This bread is hard and difficult to eat.","furigana":"かた|た"},{"japanese":"この石は硬くて、簡単には割れません。","romaji":"Kono ishi wa katakute, kantan ni wa waremasen.","german":"This stone is hard and does not break easily.","furigana":"いし|かた|かんたん|わ"}],"notes":"硬い means hard or tough and is often used for the properties of a material. Hard bread or tough meat is difficult to bite; 硬い石 describes the hardness of a stone. The opposite is 軟らかい. 固い can also express hardness or firmness, while 堅い often emphasizes a solid, reliable quality. The usual combinations help with choosing the spelling. As an i-adjective, it forms 硬くて and 硬くない.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 36"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この川は深いです。","romaji":"Kono kawa wa fukai desu.","german":"This river is deep.","furigana":"かわ|ふか"},{"japanese":"この川はとても深いです。","romaji":"Kono kawa wa totemo fukai desu.","german":"This river is very deep.","furigana":"かわ|ふか"},{"japanese":"この本には深い意味があります。","romaji":"Kono hon ni wa fukai imi ga arimasu.","german":"This book has a deeper meaning.","furigana":"ほん|ふか|いみ"}],"notes":"深い is an い-adjective for spatial depth, for example of a river. Before a noun it stands directly: 深い川. It can also mean \"profound\" in a figurative sense, for example 深い意味, \"a deeper meaning\". 深く is the adverbial form; 浅い is the opposite, \"shallow\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ここは浅いから安全です。","romaji":"Koko wa asai kara anzen desu.","german":"It's shallow here, so it's safe.","furigana":"あさ|あんぜん"},{"japanese":"子供用のプールは浅いです。","romaji":"Kodomo-yō no pūru wa asai desu.","german":"The children's pool is shallow.","furigana":"こどもよう|あさ"},{"japanese":"昨夜は眠りが浅かったです。","romaji":"Sakuya wa nemuri ga asakatta desu.","german":"I slept lightly last night.","furigana":"さくや|ねむ|あさ"}],"notes":"浅い means \"shallow\", for example of water, or \"superficial\". It is an い-adjective: 浅いプール, \"a shallow pool\". 浅い眠り, on the other hand, is light sleep. The adverbial form is 浅く; 深い means greater depth.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"懐かしい写真ですね。","romaji":"Natsukashii shashin desu ne.","german":"Das Foto weckt schöne Erinnerungen, nicht wahr?","furigana":"なつ|しゃしん"},{"japanese":"この曲を聞くと懐かしい気持ちになります。","romaji":"Kono kyoku o kiku to natsukashii kimochi ni narimasu.","german":"Wenn ich dieses Lied höre, werde ich nostalgisch.","furigana":"きょく|き|なつ|きも"}],"notes":"懐かしい beschreibt, dass etwas liebe Erinnerungen weckt oder einem aus früherer Zeit vertraut ist und fehlt. Im Deutschen passt je nach Satz „nostalgisch“ oder „das weckt Erinnerungen“. Es ist ein い-Adjektiv; 懐かしい写真 ist ein Foto, das dieses Gefühl auslöst, nicht bloß ein altes Foto.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"旅行に行けて羨ましいです。","romaji":"Ryokō ni ikete urayamashii desu.","german":"I envy you for being able to go on a trip.","furigana":"りょこう|い|うらや"},{"japanese":"海外旅行に行けるなんて羨ましいです。","romaji":"Kaigai ryokō ni ikeru nante urayamashii desu.","german":"I envy you for being able to go on an overseas trip.","furigana":"かいがいりょこう|い|うらや"},{"japanese":"長い休みがあって羨ましいです。","romaji":"Nagai yasumi ga atte urayamashii desu.","german":"I envy you for having such a long vacation.","furigana":"なが|やす|うらや"}],"notes":"羨ましい can express \"I'm envious\" or \"that is enviable\". It is an い-adjective. 羨ましいです often reveals the speaker's feeling even when \"I\" is not mentioned. It does not have to mean resentment; you can also use it in a friendly way to say you would like to have the opportunity yourself.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 17"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先生は厳しいです。","romaji":"Sensei wa kibishii desu.","german":"The teacher is strict.","furigana":"せんせい|きび"},{"japanese":"今年の冬は厳しいです。","romaji":"Kotoshi no fuyu wa kibishii desu.","german":"This winter is harsh.","furigana":"ことし|ふゆ|きび"}],"notes":"厳しい means \"strict\" for people or rules and \"harsh / severe\" for conditions. 先生は厳しいです describes a strict teacher; 冬が厳しい means a harsh winter. It is an い-adjective. The reading is きびしい; the identically written form いかめしい belongs to a different word usage.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 47"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"素敵な景色ですね。","romaji":"Suteki na keshiki desu ne.","german":"What a lovely view.","furigana":"すてき|けしき"},{"japanese":"素敵なプレゼントをありがとう。","romaji":"Suteki na purezento o arigatō.","german":"Thank you for the lovely present.","furigana":"すてき"}],"notes":"素敵 is a な-adjective for something beautiful, charming, or great. Before a noun, な is used: 素敵な景色, 素敵なプレゼント. It is a personal positive evaluation. The kanji are chosen as phonetic spelling (当て字); the reading is すてき.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 12"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"時間は十分あります。","romaji":"Jikan wa jūbun arimasu.","german":"There is plenty of time.","furigana":"じかん|じゅうぶん"},{"japanese":"もう十分食べました。","romaji":"Mō jūbun tabemashita.","german":"I've already eaten enough.","furigana":"じゅうぶんた"},{"japanese":"十分な準備ができました。","romaji":"Jūbun na junbi ga dekimashita.","german":"I made sufficient preparations.","furigana":"じゅうぶん|じゅんび"}],"notes":"十分 with the reading じゅうぶん means \"enough / sufficient\". As an adverb it stands before the verb, for example 十分食べる. Before a noun it takes な: 十分な準備, \"sufficient preparation\". The identically written time expression \"ten minutes\" is read じゅっぷん or じっぷん and does not belong to this card.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ずっと待っていました。","romaji":"Zutto matte imashita.","german":"I was waiting the whole time.","furigana":"ま"},{"japanese":"ずっとここに住んでいます。","romaji":"Zutto koko ni sunde imasu.","german":"I've been living here all along.","furigana":"す"},{"japanese":"この店のほうがずっと安いです。","romaji":"Kono mise no hō ga zutto yasui desu.","german":"This shop is much cheaper.","furigana":"みせ|やす"}],"notes":"ずっと can stress an uninterrupted duration or a large difference. ずっと待つ means \"to wait the whole time\"; ずっと安い means \"much cheaper\". With ずっと住んでいる a long period is usually meant, not necessarily one's whole life. The lengthened form ずーっと intensifies the statement.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 12"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"もうすぐ春です。","romaji":"Mōsugu haru desu.","german":"Spring is coming soon.","furigana":"はる"},{"japanese":"電車はもうすぐ出発します。","romaji":"Densha wa mōsugu shuppatsu shimasu.","german":"The train will depart shortly.","furigana":"でんしゃ|しゅっぱつ"},{"japanese":"宿題はもうすぐ終わります。","romaji":"Shukudai wa mōsugu owarimasu.","german":"I'll be finished with my homework soon.","furigana":"しゅくだい|お"}],"notes":"もうすぐ announces that an event is imminent in time: もうすぐ春です, もうすぐ終わります. Unlike すぐ (\"immediately\"), it is not suitable for requests or commands - \"Please come right away\" is すぐ来てください, not もうすぐ. It is almost always written in kana.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 19"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本語がどんどん上手になります。","romaji":"Nihongo ga dondon jōzu ni narimasu.","german":"My Japanese is getting better and better.","furigana":"にほんご|じょうず"},{"japanese":"遠慮しないで、どんどん食べてください。","romaji":"Enryo shinaide, dondon tabete kudasai.","german":"Don't hold back, please eat as much as you like.","furigana":"えんりょ|た"},{"japanese":"駅の近くに新しいビルがどんどんできています。","romaji":"Eki no chikaku ni atarashii biru ga dondon dekite imasu.","german":"New buildings are going up one after another near the station.","furigana":"えき|ちか|あたら"}],"notes":"どんどん describes a swift, vigorous development, often with ～くなる/～になる: どんどん上手になる. In requests it encourages someone to keep going without restraint: どんどん食べてください, \"Please help yourself freely\". Do not confuse it with だんだん (\"gradually\"); as an onomatopoeic word, どんどん also stands for loud knocking or drumming (ドアをどんどんたたく).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"うっかり忘れました。","romaji":"Ukkari wasuremashita.","german":"I forgot it by mistake.","furigana":"わす"},{"japanese":"うっかり駅を乗り過ごしてしまいました。","romaji":"Ukkari eki o norisugoshite shimaimashita.","german":"I carelessly missed my stop.","furigana":"えき|の|す"},{"japanese":"うっかりして、砂糖と塩を間違えました。","romaji":"Ukkari shite, satō to shio o machigaemashita.","german":"I wasn't paying attention and mixed up the sugar and the salt.","furigana":"さとう|しお|まちが"}],"notes":"うっかり describes a mishap caused by inattention, not by intention - the opposite of わざと. It often appears together with ～てしまう: うっかり忘れてしまう. As the verb うっかりする it means \"to be inattentive, not to pay attention\"; a careless mistake is called うっかりミス.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"わざとやったのですか。","romaji":"Wazato yatta no desu ka.","german":"Did you do it on purpose?"},{"japanese":"父は子供とのゲームでわざと負けました。","romaji":"Chichi wa kodomo to no gēmu de wazato makemashita.","german":"My father deliberately lost the game against his child.","furigana":"ちち|こども|ま"},{"japanese":"わざとじゃありません。ごめんなさい。","romaji":"Wazato ja arimasen. Gomennasai.","german":"It wasn't on purpose. I'm sorry."}],"notes":"わざと means \"on purpose, intentionally\" and usually concerns something undesirable or feigned: わざと負ける \"to lose on purpose\". To apologize, you say わざとじゃありません \"It wasn't on purpose\". Do not confuse it with わざわざ (\"go out of one's way, take the trouble specially\"); it is written in kana.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"まさか本当ですか。","romaji":"Masaka hontō desu ka.","german":"Is it really true?","furigana":"ほんとう"},{"japanese":"まさか彼が来るとは思いませんでした。","romaji":"Masaka kare ga kuru to wa omoimasen deshita.","german":"I never thought he would come.","furigana":"かれ|く|おも"},{"japanese":"まさか雪は降らないでしょう。","romaji":"Masaka yuki wa furanai deshō.","german":"It surely won't snow.","furigana":"ゆき|ふ"}],"notes":"まさか expresses that one considers something impossible or can hardly believe it. As an adverb it is usually combined with a negative or a supposition: まさか～ないでしょう \"surely not\", まさか～とは思わなかった \"I never thought that ...\". On its own as an exclamation, まさか！ means \"No way!/You can't be serious!\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"さすが先生ですね。","romaji":"Sasuga sensei desu ne.","german":"Just what you'd expect of a teacher.","furigana":"せんせい"},{"japanese":"三時間歩いたので、さすがに疲れました。","romaji":"San-jikan aruita node, sasuga ni tsukaremashita.","german":"After walking for three hours, I was tired after all.","furigana":"さんじかんある|つか"}],"notes":"さすが + person or noun praises someone for living up to expectations: さすが先生ですね \"Typical of a teacher - just what I'd expect\". On its own as an exclamation, さすが！ means roughly \"Impressive!\". さすがに, on the other hand, means \"as one would expect, even so\", often with a qualification: さすがに疲れました \"I was tired after all\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"かなり疲れました。","romaji":"Kanari tsukaremashita.","german":"I'm quite tired.","furigana":"つか"},{"japanese":"今日はかなり暑いです。","romaji":"Kyō wa kanari atsui desu.","german":"It's quite hot today.","furigana":"きょう|あつ"},{"japanese":"駅までかなりの距離があります。","romaji":"Eki made kanari no kyori ga arimasu.","german":"It's quite a distance to the station.","furigana":"えき|きょり"}],"notes":"かなり means \"quite, considerably\" and lies in strength between 少し and とても. Before nouns it takes の: かなりの距離, かなりの人. It sounds more matter-of-fact than とても and can intensify both positive and negative things; 割と, by contrast, is weaker and expresses more \"surprisingly, actually quite\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 36"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"割と簡単でした。","romaji":"Wari to kantan deshita.","german":"It was relatively easy.","furigana":"わり|かんたん"},{"japanese":"このレストランは割と安いです。","romaji":"Kono resutoran wa wari to yasui desu.","german":"This restaurant is relatively cheap.","furigana":"わり|やす"}],"notes":"割と (also 割に) means \"relatively, quite\" and often implies that something is better or different than expected: 割と簡単でした. It is more colloquial and weaker than かなり. It is often written in kana: わりと.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お陰様で元気です。","romaji":"Okagesama de genki desu.","german":"I'm well, thank you.","furigana":"かげさま|げんき"},{"japanese":"お陰様で、試験に合格しました。","romaji":"Okagesama de, shiken ni gōkaku shimashita.","german":"Thanks to your support, I passed the exam.","furigana":"かげさま|しけん|ごうかく"}],"notes":"お陰様で is a polite expression for gratefully attributing a good condition or success to someone's support. As a reply to a question about one's well-being, it can also be a friendly routine phrase without naming any concrete help. おかげさまで is often written in hiragana; a following sentence explains what went well.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 19"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"遠慮しないで食べてください。","romaji":"Enryo shinaide tabete kudasai.","german":"Please eat, don't hold back.","furigana":"えんりょ|た"},{"japanese":"今日は用事があるので、パーティーは遠慮します。","romaji":"Kyō wa yōji ga aru node, pātī wa enryo shimasu.","german":"I have something to do today, so I'll pass on the party.","furigana":"きょう|ようじ|えんりょ"},{"japanese":"ここでたばこはご遠慮ください。","romaji":"Koko de tabako wa go-enryo kudasai.","german":"Please refrain from smoking here.","furigana":"えんりょ"}],"notes":"遠慮する means to hold back out of consideration or to politely decline. 遠慮しないでください or ご遠慮なく encourages a guest to help themselves. With ～は遠慮します you politely decline, and on signs ～はご遠慮ください is a polite prohibition (\"please refrain\").","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 19"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"痛くても我慢してください。","romaji":"Itakutemo gaman shite kudasai.","german":"Even if it hurts, please bear it.","furigana":"いた|がまん"},{"japanese":"ダイエット中なので、甘い物を我慢しています。","romaji":"Daietto-chū na node, amai mono o gaman shite imasu.","german":"I'm on a diet, so I'm holding off on sweets.","furigana":"ちゅう|あま|もの|がまん"},{"japanese":"この暑さはもう我慢できません。","romaji":"Kono atsusa wa mō gaman dekimasen.","german":"I can't stand this heat any longer.","furigana":"あつ|がまん"}],"notes":"我慢する means to endure something unpleasant or to deny oneself something. What one endures or goes without takes を: 痛みを我慢する, 甘い物を我慢する. 我慢できない means \"cannot bear (any longer)\"; 我慢強い describes a patient, resilient person.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 23"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"文句ばかり言わないで。","romaji":"Monku bakari iwanaide.","german":"Stop complaining all the time.","furigana":"もんく|い"},{"japanese":"この店の料理は文句なしにおいしいです。","romaji":"Kono mise no ryōri wa monku nashi ni oishii desu.","german":"The food at this restaurant is flawlessly delicious.","furigana":"みせ|りょうり|もんく"},{"japanese":"お客さんに文句を言われました。","romaji":"Okyaku-san ni monku o iwaremashita.","german":"A customer complained to me.","furigana":"きゃく|もんく|い"}],"notes":"文句 is a personal complaint or grumbling; the typical combination is 文句を言う, \"to complain\". 文句ばかり言う means \"to grumble constantly\", and 文句なし/文句がない means \"flawless, nothing to complain about\". A formal complaint to a company is rather 苦情.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 21"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの人の噂は本当ですか。","romaji":"Ano hito no uwasa wa hontou desu ka.","german":"Is the rumor about that person true?","furigana":"ひと|うわさ|ほんとう"},{"japanese":"駅前に新しいスーパーができるという噂です。","romaji":"Ekimae ni atarashii sūpā ga dekiru to iu uwasa desu.","german":"The rumor is that a new supermarket is going to open in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|あたら|うわさ"},{"japanese":"噂をすれば、田中さんが来ましたよ。","romaji":"Uwasa o sureba, Tanaka-san ga kimashita yo.","german":"Speak of the devil - here comes Mr. Tanaka.","furigana":"うわさ|たなか|き"}],"notes":"噂 is a rumor or gossip about someone; it is often written in kana: うわさ. Typical are 噂を聞く, ～の噂をする \"to talk about someone\" and ～という噂です \"the rumor is that ...\". The fixed phrase 噂をすれば（影）corresponds to \"Speak of the devil\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"秘密にしてください。","romaji":"Himitsu ni shite kudasai.","german":"Please keep it secret.","furigana":"ひみつ"},{"japanese":"これは二人だけの秘密です。","romaji":"Kore wa futari dake no himitsu desu.","german":"This is a secret just between the two of us.","furigana":"ふたり|ひみつ"},{"japanese":"このパンがおいしい秘密は何ですか。","romaji":"Kono pan ga oishii himitsu wa nan desu ka.","german":"What is the secret of this delicious bread?","furigana":"ひみつ|なん"}],"notes":"秘密 is a secret. Important combinations: 秘密にする \"to keep secret\", 秘密を守る \"to keep a secret\" and 秘密を話す \"to reveal\". It can also refer to the \"secret\" of a success: おいしさの秘密. Colloquially, among friends, 内緒 is often used.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 17"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は優しい性格です。","romaji":"Kare wa yasashii seikaku desu.","german":"He has a kind personality.","furigana":"かれ|やさ|せいかく"},{"japanese":"兄と私は性格が全然違います。","romaji":"Ani to watashi wa seikaku ga zenzen chigaimasu.","german":"My older brother and I have completely different personalities.","furigana":"あに|わたし|せいかく|ぜんぜんちが"},{"japanese":"彼とは性格が合いません。","romaji":"Kare to wa seikaku ga aimasen.","german":"He and I don't get along in terms of personality.","furigana":"かれ|せいかく|あ"}],"notes":"性格 is a person's character or personality. You describe it with an adjective before it (明るい性格, 優しい性格) or with が: 性格がいい/悪い. 性格が合う means \"to get along personality-wise\", 性格が合わない \"not to be compatible\". For things, 性格 also means \"nature, character\" (問題の性格).","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 38"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"態度を改めてください。","romaji":"Taido o aratamete kudasai.","german":"Please change your attitude.","furigana":"たいど|あらた"},{"japanese":"店員の態度がとても悪かったです。","romaji":"Ten'in no taido ga totemo warukatta desu.","german":"The shop assistant's attitude was very bad.","furigana":"てんいん|たいど|わる"},{"japanese":"会社はこの問題についてまだ態度を決めていません。","romaji":"Kaisha wa kono mondai ni tsuite mada taido o kimete imasen.","german":"The company has not yet taken a position on this issue.","furigana":"かいしゃ|もんだい|たいど|き"}],"notes":"態度 is visible behavior or manner toward others: 態度がいい/悪い, 店員の態度. 態度が大きい means \"to act arrogantly\", 態度を改める \"to improve one's behavior\". 態度 also denotes the stance on an issue: 態度を決める \"to take a stand\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"我儘を言わないでください。","romaji":"Wagamama o iwanaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't be so selfish.","furigana":"わがまま|い"},{"japanese":"弟はわがままな子供でした。","romaji":"Otōto wa wagamama na kodomo deshita.","german":"My younger brother was a willful child.","furigana":"おとうと|こども"},{"japanese":"一つだけわがままを聞いてください。","romaji":"Hitotsu dake wagamama o kiite kudasai.","german":"Please grant me just one favor.","furigana":"ひと|き"}],"notes":"わがまま is almost always written in kana; the kanji 我儘 are rare. As a na-adjective it describes a selfish, spoiled person: わがままな子供. As a noun it appears in わがままを言う \"to make selfish demands, to whine\"; politely, you ask for a personal favor with わがままを聞いてください.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"勝手に使わないでください。","romaji":"Katte ni tsukawanaide kudasai.","german":"Please do not use it without permission.","furigana":"かって|つか"},{"japanese":"あの人は本当に勝手な人ですね。","romaji":"Ano hito wa hontō ni katte na hito desu ne.","german":"He really is a selfish person.","furigana":"ひと|ほんとう|かって|ひと"},{"japanese":"ドアが勝手に開きました。","romaji":"Doa ga katte ni akimashita.","german":"The door opened by itself.","furigana":"かって|あ"}],"notes":"勝手 expresses that someone acts only as they please. The most common form is 勝手に \"on one's own authority, without asking\": 勝手に使わないで. With things, 勝手に means \"by itself\": ドアが勝手に開く. As a na-adjective, 勝手な人 (or 自分勝手) describes a selfish person; 勝手にしなさい！ means \"Do whatever you like!\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"電話番号を交換しましょう。","romaji":"Denwa bangō o kōkan shimashō.","german":"Let's exchange phone numbers.","furigana":"でんわばんごう|こうかん"},{"japanese":"電池を交換してください。","romaji":"Denchi o kōkan shite kudasai.","german":"Please change the battery.","furigana":"でんち|こうかん"},{"japanese":"このシャツを大きいサイズと交換できますか。","romaji":"Kono shatsu o ōkii saizu to kōkan dekimasu ka.","german":"Can I exchange this shirt for a bigger size?","furigana":"おお|こうかん"}],"notes":"交換する means \"to exchange, to swap\" and also \"to replace, to swap out\" (電池を交換する). The patterns are AをBと交換する \"to exchange A for B\" and 友達と～を交換する \"to exchange ... with a friend\". For exchanging money you say 両替する; the Japanese synonym for \"to swap out\" is 取り替える.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 20"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"病気を予防するために手を洗います。","romaji":"Byouki o yobou suru tame ni te o araimasu.","german":"I wash my hands to prevent illness.","furigana":"びょうき|よぼう|て|あら"},{"japanese":"インフルエンザの予防注射を受けました。","romaji":"Infuruenza no yobō chūsha o ukemashita.","german":"I got a flu shot.","furigana":"よぼうちゅうしゃ|う"}],"notes":"予防する is a formal Sino-Japanese word for preventing illnesses, accidents or disasters: 病気を予防する. Compounds such as 予防注射 \"preventive vaccination\" are common. In everyday speech, the simpler 防ぐ is often used for \"to prevent, to ward off\".","levelNote":"Derived from 予防 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"料理を注文します。","romaji":"Ryōri o chūmon shimasu.","german":"I order a dish.","furigana":"りょうり|ちゅうもん"},{"japanese":"ネットで本を注文しました。","romaji":"Netto de hon o chūmon shimashita.","german":"I ordered a book online.","furigana":"ほん|ちゅうもん"}],"notes":"注文する means \"to order\", in a restaurant as well as online or from a dealer: 料理を注文する. Waiters politely ask ご注文はお決まりですか, \"Have you decided yet?\". In casual conversation people often simply say 頼む. As a noun, 注文 can also mean \"wish, demand\": 注文が多い \"to have many special requests\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 18"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"予約を確認します。","romaji":"Yoyaku o kakunin shimasu.","german":"I'm confirming the reservation.","furigana":"よやく|かくにん"},{"japanese":"出かける前に、ガスを止めたかどうか確認します。","romaji":"Dekakeru mae ni, gasu o tometa ka dō ka kakunin shimasu.","german":"Before leaving the house, I check whether I turned off the gas.","furigana":"で|まえ|と|かくにん"},{"japanese":"明日の時間をもう一度確認させてください。","romaji":"Ashita no jikan o mō ichido kakunin sasete kudasai.","german":"Let me confirm tomorrow's time once more.","furigana":"あした|じかん|いちどかくにん"}],"notes":"確認する means \"to make sure, to check, to verify\": 予約を確認する. With an embedded question, ～かどうか確認する means \"to check whether ...\". In professional life, 確認します and ご確認ください are heard constantly. Approval in the sense of \"to authorize\", by contrast, is 承認.","levelNote":"Derived from 確認 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お客様を案内します。","romaji":"Okyakusama o annai shimasu.","german":"I show the guest around.","furigana":"きゃくさま|あんない"},{"japanese":"お客様を会議室に案内します。","romaji":"Okyakusama o kaigishitsu ni annai shimasu.","german":"I take the guest to the conference room.","furigana":"きゃくさま|かいぎしつ|あんない"},{"japanese":"地図を見ながら、駅までの道を案内しました。","romaji":"Chizu o minagara, eki made no michi o annai shimashita.","german":"While looking at the map, I explained the way to the station.","furigana":"ちず|み|えき|みち|あんない"}],"notes":"案内する here means to guide someone or to show the way. The person takes を and the destination takes に: お客様を会議室に案内する. Giving directions can also be expressed with 道を案内する. お客様 is a polite term for a guest or customer. The forms of する change, for example in 案内します and 案内しました; 案内 alone is the noun. This card practices the meaning \"to guide\", not the further meanings \"to notify\" or \"to invite\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 16"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"エレベーターが故障しています。","romaji":"Erebeetaa ga koshou shite imasu.","german":"The elevator is out of order.","furigana":"こしょう"},{"japanese":"車が故障して、会社に遅れました。","romaji":"Kuruma ga koshō shite, kaisha ni okuremashita.","german":"My car broke down, so I was late for work.","furigana":"くるま|こしょう|かいしゃ|おく"},{"japanese":"この自動販売機は故障中です。","romaji":"Kono jidō hanbaiki wa koshō-chū desu.","german":"This vending machine is out of order.","furigana":"じどうはんばいき|こしょうちゅう"}],"notes":"故障する is said of machines and devices that no longer work: 車が故障する, エレベーターが故障している. For things that break or are physically damaged (glass, toys), 壊れる is used. The sign 故障中 means \"out of order\".","levelNote":"Derived from 故障 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"授業を録音します。","romaji":"Jugyō o rokuon shimasu.","german":"I record the lessons.","furigana":"じゅぎょう|ろくおん"},{"japanese":"自分の発音を録音して聞きます。","romaji":"Jibun no hatsuon o rokuon shite kikimasu.","german":"I record my pronunciation and listen to it.","furigana":"じぶん|はつおん|ろくおん|き"},{"japanese":"スマホで鳥の声を録音しました。","romaji":"Sumaho de tori no koe o rokuon shimashita.","german":"I recorded bird calls with my phone.","furigana":"とり|こえ|ろくおん"}],"notes":"録音する means \"to record sound\": 授業を録音する. For video recordings, 録画する is used. The device takes で (スマホで録音する); to ask for permission, use 録音してもいいですか.","levelNote":"Derived from 録音 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ニュースは毎日放送されます。","romaji":"Nyuusu wa mainichi housou saremasu.","german":"The news is broadcast every day.","furigana":"まいにちほうそう"},{"japanese":"地震のとき、学校で放送がありました。","romaji":"Jishin no toki, gakkō de hōsō ga arimashita.","german":"During the earthquake, there was an announcement at school.","furigana":"じしん|がっこう|ほうそう"}],"notes":"放送する means \"to broadcast\" (TV, radio) and also \"to announce over a loudspeaker\". Since the broadcaster is usually the one acting, it is often used in the passive: 毎日放送される. A program is 番組, a live broadcast 生放送; 校内放送 is the announcement at school.","levelNote":"Derived from 放送 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いろいろな経験をしました。","romaji":"Iroiro na keiken o shimashita.","german":"I had various experiences.","furigana":"けいけん"},{"japanese":"いい経験ができました。","romaji":"Ii keiken ga dekimashita.","german":"I was able to have a good experience.","furigana":"けいけん"},{"japanese":"留学中に、初めて一人暮らしを経験しました。","romaji":"Ryūgakuchū ni, hajimete hitorigurashi o keiken shimashita.","german":"While studying abroad, I experienced living alone for the first time.","furigana":"りゅうがくちゅう|はじ|ひとりぐ|けいけん"}],"notes":"経験する means to experience something oneself or to gain experience with it. What is experienced takes を: 一人暮らしを経験する. There is also the combination 経験をする with the noun 経験. いい経験ができました here means that one had the opportunity for a good experience. 留学中に names the time during studying abroad; 初めて marks a first experience. The verb forms are, for example, 経験します and 経験しました.","levelNote":"Derived from 経験 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"将来の生活を想像します。","romaji":"Shourai no seikatsu o souzou shimasu.","german":"I imagine my future life.","furigana":"しょうらい|せいかつ|そうぞう"},{"japanese":"日本の夏は想像していたより暑かったです。","romaji":"Nihon no natsu wa sōzō shite ita yori atsukatta desu.","german":"Summer in Japan was hotter than I had imagined.","furigana":"にほん|なつ|そうぞう|あつ"},{"japanese":"電気のない生活は想像できません。","romaji":"Denki no nai seikatsu wa sōzō dekimasen.","german":"I can't imagine life without electricity.","furigana":"でんき|せいかつ|そうぞう"}],"notes":"想像する means \"to imagine, to picture\"; the thing imagined takes を: 将来を想像する. When comparing with reality, you say 想像していたより or 想像以上 (\"more than expected\"). 想像できない or 想像もつかない means \"unimaginable\".","levelNote":"Derived from 想像 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この映画にとても感動しました。","romaji":"Kono eiga ni totemo kandou shimashita.","german":"I was very moved by this movie.","furigana":"えいが|かんどう"},{"japanese":"マラソンで最後まで走る選手を見て、感動しました。","romaji":"Marason de saigo made hashiru senshu o mite, kandō shimashita.","german":"I was moved to see the runners who kept going to the end of the marathon.","furigana":"さいご|はし|せんしゅ|み|かんどう"}],"notes":"感動する means to be deeply moved or touched inside, always in a positive sense. What one is moved by takes に (映画に感動する) or appears as the reason in the て-form (～を見て感動しました). The related adjective is 感動的な, \"moving, touching\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 13"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"無事で安心しました。","romaji":"Buji de anshin shimashita.","german":"I'm relieved that everyone is safe.","furigana":"ぶじ|あんしん"},{"japanese":"この水は安心して飲めます。","romaji":"Kono mizu wa anshin shite nomemasu.","german":"You can drink this water without worry.","furigana":"みず|あんしん|の"},{"japanese":"薬を飲めばすぐ治りますから、安心してください。","romaji":"Kusuri o nomeba sugu naorimasu kara, anshin shite kudasai.","german":"The medicine will make you better soon, so please don't worry.","furigana":"くすり|の|なお|あんしん"}],"notes":"安心する means \"to feel reassured, relieved\" when a worry disappears; the reason usually appears in the て-form or with で: 無事で安心しました. 安心して～ means \"without worry, with peace of mind ...\", and 安心してください reassures someone (\"Don't worry\"). The opposite is 心配する; as a na-adjective: 安心な町.","levelNote":"Derived from 安心 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子供は野菜を嫌がります。","romaji":"Kodomo wa yasai o iyagarimasu.","german":"The child does not like vegetables.","furigana":"こども|やさい|いや"},{"japanese":"弟は病院に行くのを嫌がっています。","romaji":"Otōto wa byōin ni iku no o iyagatte imasu.","german":"My younger brother is reluctant to go to the hospital.","furigana":"おとうと|びょういん|い|いや"},{"japanese":"人が嫌がることをしてはいけません。","romaji":"Hito ga iyagaru koto o shite wa ikemasen.","german":"You must not do things that other people dislike.","furigana":"ひと|いや"}],"notes":"嫌がる describes another person visibly disliking something or resisting it; for your own feelings, use 嫌だ or 嫌い. The thing disliked takes を, and an action takes ～のを: 病院に行くのを嫌がる. 人が嫌がることをしない means \"do not do things that make others uncomfortable\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"祖母は故郷を懐かしがっています。","romaji":"Sobo wa furusato o natsukashigatte imasu.","german":"My grandmother feels nostalgic about her hometown.","furigana":"そぼ|ふるさと|なつ"},{"japanese":"父は学生時代の歌を聞いて、とても懐かしがっていました。","romaji":"Chichi wa gakusei jidai no uta o kiite, totemo natsukashigatte imashita.","german":"When my father heard songs from his student days, he felt very nostalgic.","furigana":"ちち|がくせいじだい|うた|き|なつ"}],"notes":"懐かしがる describes someone else visibly looking back wistfully on the past; for your own feeling you say 懐かしい. What is missed takes を: 故郷を懐かしがる. It usually appears in the ～ている form or in the past tense.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子供が恥ずかしがっています。","romaji":"Kodomo ga hazukashigatte imasu.","german":"The child is shy.","furigana":"こども|は"},{"japanese":"妹は写真を撮られるのを恥ずかしがります。","romaji":"Imōto wa shashin o torareru no o hazukashigarimasu.","german":"My younger sister is embarrassed about having her picture taken.","furigana":"いもうと|しゃしん|と|は"},{"japanese":"恥ずかしがらないで、大きい声で話してください。","romaji":"Hazukashigaranaide, ōkii koe de hanashite kudasai.","german":"Don't be shy, please speak in a loud voice.","furigana":"は|おお|こえ|はな"}],"notes":"恥ずかしがる describes visible shyness or embarrassment in others; for yourself you say 恥ずかしい. The cause takes を or ～のを: 写真を撮られるのを恥ずかしがる. The negative request 恥ずかしがらないで encourages someone; a shy person is called 恥ずかしがり屋.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この柱は古いです。","romaji":"Kono hashira wa furui desu.","german":"This pillar is old.","furigana":"はしら|ふる"},{"japanese":"柱にぶつかって、頭が痛いです。","romaji":"Hashira ni butsukatte, atama ga itai desu.","german":"I bumped into a pillar and my head hurts.","furigana":"はしら|あたま|いた"},{"japanese":"このお寺には太い柱がたくさんあります。","romaji":"Kono otera ni wa futoi hashira ga takusan arimasu.","german":"This temple has many thick pillars.","furigana":"てら|ふと|はしら"}],"notes":"柱 is a supporting pillar or post, for example in houses and temples; it is counted with 本. Related words are 電柱 \"utility pole\" and 大黒柱 \"mainstay (of the family)\". You bump into a pillar with に: 柱にぶつかる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"布団を押し入れに入れます。","romaji":"Futon o oshiire ni iremasu.","german":"I put the futon in the closet.","furigana":"ふとん|お|い|い"},{"japanese":"押し入れを片付けたら、古いアルバムが出てきました。","romaji":"Oshiire o katazuketara, furui arubamu ga dete kimashita.","german":"When I tidied up the closet, an old photo album turned up.","furigana":"お|い|かたづ|ふる|で"}],"notes":"押し入れ is the built-in closet with sliding doors in a Japanese tatami room, in which mainly futons are stored during the day. A Western-style wardrobe is called クローゼット. You put something in with に: 布団を押し入れに入れる; the spelling 押入れ also occurs.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 22"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"毛布が必要ですか。","romaji":"Mōfu ga hitsuyō desu ka.","german":"Do you need a blanket?","furigana":"もうふ|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"寒いので毛布をもう一枚ください。","romaji":"Samui node mōfu o mō ichimai kudasai.","german":"It's cold, so please give me one more blanket.","furigana":"さむ|もうふ|いちまい"},{"japanese":"寝ている子供に毛布をかけてあげました。","romaji":"Nete iru kodomo ni mōfu o kakete agemashita.","german":"I covered the sleeping child with a blanket.","furigana":"ね|こども|もうふ"}],"notes":"毛布 is a (wool) blanket for covering oneself, for example on an airplane or as an extra cover in winter; it is counted with 枚. To cover someone is 毛布をかける. The thick comforter of a futon, in contrast, is called 掛け布団.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ガスを止めてください。","romaji":"Gasu o tomete kudasai.","german":"Please turn off the gas.","furigana":"と"},{"japanese":"冬はガス代が高くなります。","romaji":"Fuyu wa gasu-dai ga takaku narimasu.","german":"In winter the gas bill goes up.","furigana":"ふゆ|だい|たか"},{"japanese":"ガスのにおいがします。窓を開けてください。","romaji":"Gasu no nioi ga shimasu. Mado o akete kudasai.","german":"I smell gas. Please open the window.","furigana":"まど|あ"}],"notes":"ガス in everyday speech usually means the heating and cooking gas in a household. You turn it off with 止める; the gas bill is ガス代, the gas stove ガスコンロ. Gasoline, by contrast, is ガソリン.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 26"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"洗剤を買ってきてください。","romaji":"Senzai o katte kite kudasai.","german":"Please buy some detergent.","furigana":"せんざい|か"},{"japanese":"お皿を洗う洗剤がなくなりました。","romaji":"Osara o arau senzai ga nakunarimashita.","german":"We've run out of dish soap.","furigana":"さら|あら|せんざい"},{"japanese":"洗剤を入れすぎないでください。","romaji":"Senzai o iresuginaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't put in too much detergent.","furigana":"せんざい|い"}],"notes":"洗剤 is the general term for cleaning agents - laundry detergent for clothes as well as dish soap for dishes (食器用洗剤). You put it in with 入れる and use it with で: 洗剤で洗う. Soap, in contrast, is 石けん.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"寝る前に歯磨きをしてください。","romaji":"Neru mae ni hamigaki o shite kudasai.","german":"Please brush your teeth before going to bed.","furigana":"ね|まえ|はみが"},{"japanese":"昼ご飯の後、会社で歯磨きをします。","romaji":"Hirugohan no ato, kaisha de hamigaki o shimasu.","german":"After lunch, I brush my teeth at the office.","furigana":"ひる|はん|あと|かいしゃ|はみが"},{"japanese":"旅行用の歯磨きセットを買いました。","romaji":"Ryokō-yō no hamigaki setto o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a travel toothbrush set.","furigana":"りょこうよう|はみが|か"}],"notes":"歯磨き is tooth brushing; you say 歯磨きをする or use the verb 歯を磨く. Toothpaste is usually called 歯磨き粉 and the toothbrush 歯ブラシ. In Japan, many people also brush their teeth after lunch at work or school.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"体温を測ってください。","romaji":"Taion o hakatte kudasai.","german":"Please take your temperature.","furigana":"たいおん|はか"},{"japanese":"運動すると体温が上がります。","romaji":"Undō suru to taion ga agarimasu.","german":"Body temperature rises when you exercise.","furigana":"うんどう|たいおん|あ"}],"notes":"体温 is the temperature of the body, as opposed to the air temperature 気温. 体温を測る means to measure it; 体温が上がる describes a rise. The expression denotes the measured value, not automatically a fever or a diagnosis.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"注射が怖いです。","romaji":"Chūsha ga kowai desu.","german":"I'm afraid of injections.","furigana":"ちゅうしゃ|こわ"},{"japanese":"病院で注射を打ってもらいました。","romaji":"Byōin de chūsha o utte moraimashita.","german":"I got a shot at the hospital.","furigana":"びょういん|ちゅうしゃ|う"},{"japanese":"注射の後はお風呂に入らないでください。","romaji":"Chūsha no ato wa ofuro ni hairanaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't take a bath after the injection.","furigana":"ちゅうしゃ|あと|ふろ|はい"}],"notes":"注射 is a shot or injection. The doctor gives it with 注射をする or 注射を打つ; as a patient you say 注射をしてもらう or 注射を受ける; 予防注射 is a preventive vaccination. Caution: the similar-sounding 駐車 (ちゅうしゃ) means \"parking\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 46"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"来週手術をします。","romaji":"Raishū shujutsu o shimasu.","german":"I'm having surgery next week.","furigana":"らいしゅうしゅじゅつ"},{"japanese":"手術は無事に終わりました。","romaji":"Shujutsu wa buji ni owarimashita.","german":"The surgery went off without a hitch.","furigana":"しゅじゅつ|ぶじ|お"},{"japanese":"祖父は目の手術を受けました。","romaji":"Sofu wa me no shujutsu o ukemashita.","german":"My grandfather had eye surgery.","furigana":"そふ|め|しゅじゅつ|う"}],"notes":"手術 is a surgical operation. 手術をする can be used from the doctor's point of view (\"to operate\") as well as the patient's (\"to be operated on\"); to say it unambiguously as the patient, use 手術を受ける. The affected body part precedes it with の: 目の手術, 心臓の手術.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"母が二週間入院しました。","romaji":"Haha ga nishuukan nyuuin shimashita.","german":"My mother was in the hospital for two weeks.","furigana":"はは|にしゅうかんにゅういん"},{"japanese":"入院している友達のお見舞いに行きます。","romaji":"Nyūin shite iru tomodachi no omimai ni ikimasu.","german":"I'm going to visit a friend who is in the hospital.","furigana":"にゅういん|ともだち|みま|い"}],"notes":"入院する means \"to be admitted to a hospital as an inpatient\"; the hospital takes に: 病院に入院する. The duration stands without a particle (二週間入院する), 入院中 means \"being in the hospital\". You visit the sick for an お見舞い; the opposite is 退院する, outpatient treatment is 通院.","levelNote":"Derived from 入院 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"来週退院できるそうです。","romaji":"Raishuu taiin dekiru sou desu.","german":"I hear he can be discharged next week.","furigana":"らいしゅうたいいん"},{"japanese":"退院したら、温泉に行きたいです。","romaji":"Taiin shitara, onsen ni ikitai desu.","german":"When I get out of the hospital, I want to go to an onsen.","furigana":"たいいん|おんせん|い"}],"notes":"退院する means \"to be discharged from the hospital\"; the hospital being left can take を: 病院を退院する. 退院できる and plans with 退院したら \"as soon as I am discharged\" are common. Congratulations: 退院おめでとうございます. Opposite: 入院する.","levelNote":"Derived from 退院 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"患者さんに優しくしてください。","romaji":"Kanja-san ni yasashiku shite kudasai.","german":"Please be kind to the patients.","furigana":"かんじゃ|やさ"},{"japanese":"この病院には多くの患者がいます。","romaji":"Kono byōin ni wa ōku no kanja ga imasu.","german":"This hospital has many patients.","furigana":"びょういん|おお|かんじゃ"}],"notes":"患者 is a patient in the medical sense; in a hospital one politely says 患者さん. A sick person in general is 病人. Quantities: 多くの患者, 患者が増える.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"銀行でドルを円に両替しました。","romaji":"Ginkou de doru o en ni ryougae shimashita.","german":"I exchanged dollars for yen at the bank.","furigana":"ぎんこう|えん|りょうがえ"},{"japanese":"千円札を百円玉に両替してもらえますか。","romaji":"Sen'en-satsu o hyaku-en-dama ni ryōgae shite moraemasu ka.","german":"Could you change a 1,000-yen bill into 100-yen coins for me?","furigana":"せんえんさつ|ひゃくえんだま|りょうがえ"}],"notes":"両替する means \"to exchange money\", both into another currency and into small change. The pattern is AをBに両替する: ドルを円に両替する. Buses often have a 両替機 for changing bills into coins. For exchanging goods, 交換する is used.","levelNote":"Derived from 両替 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お荷物をお預かりします。","romaji":"Onimotsu o oazukari shimasu.","german":"I'll take care of your luggage.","furigana":"にもつ|あず"},{"japanese":"隣の人が子供を預かってくれました。","romaji":"Tonari no hito ga kodomo o azukatte kuremashita.","german":"The neighbor looked after my child for me.","furigana":"となり|ひと|こども|あず"}],"notes":"預かる means to take something or someone into temporary custody: 荷物を預かる, 子供を預かる. The counterpart is 預ける (\"to leave in someone's care\"): 荷物をフロントに預ける. In hotels and at the cash register you hear the polite form お預かりします, e.g. 一万円お預かりします \"I have received 10,000 yen from you\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"プリントを配ります。","romaji":"Purinto o kubarimasu.","german":"I hand out the worksheets.","furigana":"くば"},{"japanese":"チラシを駅前で配りました。","romaji":"Chirashi o ekimae de kubarimashita.","german":"I handed out flyers in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|くば"}],"notes":"配る means to distribute something to several people; the recipients take に: 学生にプリントを配る. Typical objects are handouts, flyers, cards or gifts. The expression 気を配る means \"to be attentive, to look out for others\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"道が滑りやすいです。","romaji":"Michi ga suberiyasui desu.","german":"The road is slippery.","furigana":"みち|すべ"},{"japanese":"氷の上で滑りました。","romaji":"Kōri no ue de suberimashita.","german":"I slipped on the ice.","furigana":"こおり|うえ|すべ"},{"japanese":"冬休みに、スキー場で一日中滑りました。","romaji":"Fuyuyasumi ni, sukījō de ichinichijū suberimashita.","german":"During winter vacation I skied all day at the ski resort.","furigana":"ふゆやす|じょう|いちにちじゅうすべ"}],"notes":"滑る means both \"to slip\" and \"to glide\" (on ice, on skis). 滑りやすい means \"slippery\", 手が滑る \"to slip out of one's hand\". Colloquially, 滑る also means \"to fail an exam\" or that a joke falls flat.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"階段で転んでしまいました。","romaji":"Kaidan de koronde shimaimashita.","german":"I fell on the stairs.","furigana":"かいだん|ころ"},{"japanese":"子供が転んで、ひざにけがをしました。","romaji":"Kodomo ga koronde, hiza ni kega o shimashita.","german":"The child fell down and hurt his knee.","furigana":"こども|ころ"},{"japanese":"雪の日は転ばないように気をつけてください。","romaji":"Yuki no hi wa korobanai yō ni ki o tsukete kudasai.","german":"On snowy days, please be careful not to fall.","furigana":"ゆき|ひ|ころ|き"}],"notes":"転ぶ means \"to fall down, to tumble\" when a person loses their balance; the place takes で: 階段で転ぶ. 倒れる, by contrast, means \"to fall over, to collapse\" (also for objects), 落ちる \"to fall down from a height\". Often used with ～てしまう or ～ないように気をつける.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 18"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"太陽が海に沈みます。","romaji":"Taiyou ga umi ni shizumimasu.","german":"The sun sets into the sea.","furigana":"たいよう|うみ|しず"},{"japanese":"嵐で船が沈みました。","romaji":"Arashi de fune ga shizumimashita.","german":"The ship sank in the storm.","furigana":"あらし|ふね|しず"},{"japanese":"雨の日は気分が沈みます。","romaji":"Ame no hi wa kibun ga shizumimasu.","german":"I feel down on rainy days.","furigana":"あめ|ひ|きぶん|しず"}],"notes":"沈む means \"to sink, to go down\" - for ships as well as for the sun (日が沈む; opposite: 昇る). Figuratively, 気分が沈む means \"to feel down\". The transitive counterpart is 沈める (\"to sink something\"), the opposite is 浮かぶ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"雲が空に浮かんでいます。","romaji":"Kumo ga sora ni ukande imasu.","german":"Clouds are floating in the sky.","furigana":"くも|そら|う"},{"japanese":"いい考えが浮かびました。","romaji":"Ii kangae ga ukabimashita.","german":"A good idea came to me.","furigana":"かんが|う"},{"japanese":"彼女の目に涙が浮かびました。","romaji":"Kanojo no me ni namida ga ukabimashita.","german":"Tears welled up in her eyes.","furigana":"かのじょ|め|なみだ|う"}],"notes":"浮かぶ means \"to float, to drift\" (雲が空に浮かぶ, 船が海に浮かぶ) and, figuratively, \"to come to mind, to surface\": いい考えが浮かぶ. The place takes に. The transitive counterpart is 浮かべる (涙を浮かべる \"to have tears in one's eyes\"); \"to swim\" in the sense of swimming as a sport is 泳ぐ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"空が曇っています。","romaji":"Sora ga kumotte imasu.","german":"The sky is cloudy.","furigana":"そら|くも"},{"japanese":"午後から曇るでしょう。","romaji":"Gogo kara kumoru deshō.","german":"It will probably become cloudy from the afternoon.","furigana":"ごご|くも"}],"notes":"曇る describes the sky becoming cloudy. It is an intransitive godan verb: 曇ります, 曇って. 空が曇っている states the cloudy condition. 曇るでしょう conveys a weather forecast. The subject takes が; you cannot cloud an object with を.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"雨がやんで、空が晴れました。","romaji":"Ame ga yande, sora ga haremashita.","german":"The rain stopped and the sky cleared.","furigana":"あめ|そら|は"},{"japanese":"明日は晴れるといいですね。","romaji":"Ashita wa hareru to ii desu ne.","german":"I hope it'll be sunny tomorrow.","furigana":"あした|は"}],"notes":"With the weather, 晴れる means \"to clear up\" or \"to become sunny\". The intransitive ichidan verb forms 晴れます and 晴れて. 空が晴れる names the sky as the subject. 晴れるといいですね expresses a wish, not a certain weather forecast. After rain, 晴れました can report that the clearing has already happened.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 19"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"冠婚葬祭にはマナーがあります。","romaji":"Kankonsousai ni wa manaa ga arimasu.","german":"There are rules of etiquette for ceremonial occasions.","furigana":"かんこんそうさい"},{"japanese":"冠婚葬祭にはお金がかかります。","romaji":"Kankonsōsai ni wa okane ga kakarimasu.","german":"Weddings, funerals and other family ceremonies cost a lot of money.","furigana":"かんこんそうさい|かね"}],"notes":"冠婚葬祭 is a four-character expression (四字熟語) for the important family ceremonies: 冠 coming of age, 婚 wedding, 葬 funeral, 祭 ancestral rites. It mostly stands as a collective term, for example in 冠婚葬祭のマナー or 冠婚葬祭の費用. The word is rather formal and written.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"来月友達の結婚式に出ます。","romaji":"Raigetsu tomodachi no kekkonshiki ni demasu.","german":"Next month I'm attending a friend's wedding.","furigana":"らいげつともだち|けっこんしき|で"},{"japanese":"二人は来年の春に結婚式を挙げます。","romaji":"Futari wa rainen no haru ni kekkonshiki o agemasu.","german":"The two of them will hold their wedding next spring.","furigana":"ふたり|らいねん|はる|けっこんしき|あ"},{"japanese":"結婚式でスピーチを頼まれました。","romaji":"Kekkonshiki de supīchi o tanomaremashita.","german":"I was asked to give a speech at the wedding.","furigana":"けっこんしき|たの"}],"notes":"結婚式 is the wedding ceremony. As a guest you attend with に: 結婚式に出る/出席する; the couple holds it: 結婚式を挙げる. The reception with food that follows is called 披露宴, the money gift from the guests ご祝儀.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 31"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"葬式に参加しました。","romaji":"Sōshiki ni sanka shimashita.","german":"I attended a funeral.","furigana":"そうしき|さんか"},{"japanese":"葬式に黒い服を着ます。","romaji":"Sōshiki ni kuroi fuku o kimasu.","german":"You wear black clothes to a funeral.","furigana":"そうしき|くろ|ふく|き"},{"japanese":"祖父のお葬式は家族だけで行いました。","romaji":"Sofu no osōshiki wa kazoku dake de okonaimashita.","german":"We held my grandfather's funeral with family only.","furigana":"そふ|そうしき|かぞく|おこな"}],"notes":"葬式 is the everyday word for a funeral or memorial service; the more polite form is usually お葬式. A person attending uses 葬式に出る or 葬式に行く, while the family uses 葬式をする or, more formally, 葬式を行う. In notices and official texts, 葬儀（そうぎ）is more common.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"毎年年賀状を書いています。","romaji":"Maitoshi nengajou o kaite imasu.","german":"I write New Year's cards every year.","furigana":"まいとしねんがじょう|か"},{"japanese":"元日に年賀状がたくさん届きました。","romaji":"Ganjitsu ni nengajō ga takusan todokimashita.","german":"Many New Year's cards arrived on New Year's Day.","furigana":"がんじつ|ねんがじょう|とど"},{"japanese":"年賀状は十二月二十五日までに出してください。","romaji":"Nengajō wa jūnigatsu nijūgonichi made ni dashite kudasai.","german":"Please mail the New Year's cards by December 25th.","furigana":"ねんがじょう|じゅうにがつにじゅうごにち|だ"}],"notes":"年賀状 are New Year's cards that you mail at the end of December and that the post office delivers together on January 1st. Typical verbs: 年賀状を書く, 年賀状を出す; to arrive: 年賀状が届く. They are counted with 枚: 年賀状を五十枚書きました. Those in a year of mourning send a mourning notice (喪中はがき) as early as November instead.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 7"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お盆に実家に帰ります。","romaji":"Obon ni jikka ni kaerimasu.","german":"During Obon I go back to my family home.","furigana":"ぼん|じっか|かえ"},{"japanese":"お盆にはお墓参りをします。","romaji":"Obon ni wa ohakamairi o shimasu.","german":"At Obon we visit the family grave.","furigana":"ぼん|はかまい"},{"japanese":"お盆の時期は新幹線がとても混みます。","romaji":"Obon no jiki wa shinkansen ga totemo komimasu.","german":"During the Obon period, the Shinkansen are very crowded.","furigana":"ぼん|じき|しんかんせん|こ"}],"notes":"お盆 is the Buddhist festival in mid-August (regionally mid-July) at which people welcome the souls of their ancestors. Many companies are then closed (お盆休み), people travel to their hometowns and visit the family graves, and trains and highways are overcrowded. As a time expression, お盆に is used. Note: in everyday speech お盆 also means \"tray\"; the context decides.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"公園で花見をしましょう。","romaji":"Kouen de hanami o shimashou.","german":"Let's go cherry blossom viewing in the park.","furigana":"こうえん|はなみ"},{"japanese":"花見の季節は、どこの公園も人でいっぱいです。","romaji":"Hanami no kisetsu wa, doko no kōen mo hito de ippai desu.","german":"In cherry blossom season, every park is full of people.","furigana":"はなみ|きせつ|こうえん|ひと"},{"japanese":"週末の花見は雨で中止になりました。","romaji":"Shūmatsu no hanami wa ame de chūshi ni narimashita.","german":"The weekend hanami was canceled because of rain.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|はなみ|あめ|ちゅうし"}],"notes":"花見 almost always means viewing the cherry blossoms (桜) in spring, usually as a picnic with food and drinks under the trees. You say 花見をする or 花見に行く; the polite form お花見 is very common. Popular spots fill up quickly, so people often reserve a place early. For autumn leaves, by contrast, you say 紅葉狩り.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"温泉に入りたいです。","romaji":"Onsen ni hairitai desu.","german":"I want to go to a hot spring.","furigana":"おんせん|はい"},{"japanese":"箱根は温泉で有名です。","romaji":"Hakone wa onsen de yūmei desu.","german":"Hakone is famous for its hot springs.","furigana":"はこね|おんせん|ゆうめい"},{"japanese":"週末、家族で温泉に行きました。","romaji":"Shūmatsu, kazoku de onsen ni ikimashita.","german":"On the weekend I went to a hot spring town with my family.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|かぞく|おんせん|い"}],"notes":"Bathing in a hot spring is 温泉に入る (not 泳ぐ). 温泉 denotes both the spring itself and the spa resort with inns: 温泉に行く usually means \"to go to an onsen town\". An open-air bath is 露天風呂. Before bathing you wash thoroughly, and you do not wear swimwear.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 9"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"旅館に泊まります。","romaji":"Ryokan ni tomarimasu.","german":"I'm staying at a ryokan.","furigana":"りょかん|と"},{"japanese":"旅館で和食を食べました。","romaji":"Ryokan de washoku o tabemashita.","german":"I ate Japanese food at the ryokan.","furigana":"りょかん|わしょく|た"},{"japanese":"旅館の部屋は畳で、とても静かでした。","romaji":"Ryokan no heya wa tatami de, totemo shizuka deshita.","german":"The room at the ryokan had tatami and was very quiet.","furigana":"りょかん|へや|たたみ|しず"}],"notes":"A 旅館 is a traditional Japanese inn with tatami rooms and futons; dinner and breakfast are usually included in the price. To stay overnight is 旅館に泊まる (with に). Unlike at a ホテル, you often wear a yukata in the building. Smaller, family-run guesthouses are called 民宿.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 49"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"京都のお寺を見に行きました。","romaji":"Kyouto no otera o mi ni ikimashita.","german":"I went to see the temples in Kyoto.","furigana":"きょうと|てら|み|い"},{"japanese":"祖父のお墓はお寺にあります。","romaji":"Sofu no ohaka wa otera ni arimasu.","german":"My grandfather's grave is on the grounds of a temple.","furigana":"そふ|はか|てら"},{"japanese":"毎朝お寺の鐘が聞こえます。","romaji":"Maiasa otera no kane ga kikoemasu.","german":"Every morning you can hear the temple bell.","furigana":"まいあさ|てら|かね|き"}],"notes":"お寺 is a Buddhist temple; a Shinto shrine, by contrast, is 神社 - do not confuse the two. In everyday speech people almost always say お寺 with the polite お. In proper names, 寺 is usually read じ: 金閣寺（きんかくじ）. To visit: お寺を見学する, お寺を見に行く.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"着物を着てみたいです。","romaji":"Kimono o kite mitai desu.","german":"I'd like to try wearing a kimono.","furigana":"きもの|き"},{"japanese":"お正月に着物を着ます。","romaji":"Oshōgatsu ni kimono o kimasu.","german":"I wear a kimono at New Year.","furigana":"しょうがつ|きもの|き"},{"japanese":"このお店で着物を借りることができます。","romaji":"Kono omise de kimono o kariru koto ga dekimasu.","german":"You can rent kimonos at this shop.","furigana":"みせ|きもの|か"}],"notes":"You wear or put on a kimono with 着る: 着物を着る; \"in a kimono\" is 着物で. Today it is mainly worn on special occasions such as New Year, weddings, or Coming of Age Day. In older language 着物 can also mean \"clothing\" in general. The opposite term is 洋服 (Western clothing); 和服 is a more formal word for Japanese clothing.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 20"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"花火大会に浴衣を着ていきます。","romaji":"Hanabi taikai ni yukata o kite ikimasu.","german":"I go to the fireworks festival wearing a yukata.","furigana":"はなびたいかい|ゆかた|き"},{"japanese":"旅館に泊まると、部屋に浴衣があります。","romaji":"Ryokan ni tomaru to, heya ni yukata ga arimasu.","german":"When you stay at a ryokan, there is a yukata in the room.","furigana":"りょかん|と|へや|ゆかた"}],"notes":"浴衣 is a light cotton kimono worn in summer to festivals and fireworks; in ryokan and onsen towns it serves as loungewear and a bathrobe. The reading ゆかた is irregular and has to be learned as a whole. To put on: 浴衣を着る, \"in a yukata\": 浴衣で. The left side goes over the right - the reverse is only done for the deceased.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"花火大会に行きます。","romaji":"Hanabi taikai ni ikimasu.","german":"I'm going to the fireworks festival.","furigana":"はなびたいかい|い"},{"japanese":"夏に花火を見るのが好きです。","romaji":"Natsu ni hanabi o miru no ga suki desu.","german":"I like watching fireworks in summer.","furigana":"なつ|はなび|み|す"},{"japanese":"子供たちと庭で花火をしました。","romaji":"Kodomotachi to niwa de hanabi o shimashita.","german":"I set off fireworks in the garden with the children.","furigana":"こども|にわ|はなび"}],"notes":"In Japan fireworks are a summer topic, not a New Year's Eve one: large fireworks festivals are called 花火大会. Watching is 花火を見る, setting off small hand-held fireworks yourself is 花火をする, launching rockets is 花火を上げる. The word is made up of 花 (\"flower\") and 火 (\"fire\").","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 27"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"夏祭りが楽しみです。","romaji":"Natsu matsuri ga tanoshimi desu.","german":"I'm looking forward to the summer festival.","furigana":"なつまつ|たの"},{"japanese":"この地域の祭りは有名です。","romaji":"Kono chiiki no matsuri wa yūmei desu.","german":"This region's festival is famous.","furigana":"ちいき|まつ|ゆうめい"},{"japanese":"今週の土曜日、神社でお祭りがあります。","romaji":"Konshū no doyōbi, jinja de omatsuri ga arimasu.","german":"This Saturday there is a festival at the shrine.","furigana":"こんしゅう|どようび|じんじゃ|まつ"}],"notes":"In everyday speech 祭り is often said お祭り. That a festival takes place is expressed with がある, and the place takes で: 神社で祭りがあります. In compounds it becomes ～まつり (夏祭り, 雪祭り); in fixed names the り is often dropped (祇園祭). Caution: school and university festivals such as 文化祭 are read ～さい.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本のアニメは世界中で人気です。","romaji":"Nihon no anime wa sekaijuu de ninki desu.","german":"Japanese anime is popular all over the world.","furigana":"にほん|せかいじゅう|にんき"},{"japanese":"子供のころ、毎週土曜日の朝にアニメを見ていました。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, maishū doyōbi no asa ni anime o mite imashita.","german":"As a child, I watched anime every Saturday morning.","furigana":"こども|まいしゅうどようび|あさ|み"}],"notes":"アニメ is the short form of アニメーション and in Japanese denotes any animated film or cartoon, not only Japanese productions. You say アニメを見る; a feature film is an アニメ映画. Printed comics, by contrast, are called 漫画（まんが）.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 21"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子供の頃から空手を習っています。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro kara karate o naratte imasu.","german":"I have been learning karate since I was a child.","furigana":"こども|ころ|からて|なら"},{"japanese":"空手は沖縄で生まれました。","romaji":"Karate wa Okinawa de umaremashita.","german":"Karate originated in Okinawa.","furigana":"からて|おきなわ|う"}],"notes":"空手 literally means \"empty hand\", that is, fighting without weapons. Taking lessons is 空手を習う, doing the sport is 空手をする or colloquially 空手をやる. Training takes place in a 道場; the black belt is 黒帯. The meaning \"with empty hands\" is rare; for that, 手ぶら is rather used.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 13"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"柔道は日本の武道です。","romaji":"Juudou wa Nihon no budou desu.","german":"Judo is a Japanese martial art.","furigana":"じゅうどう|にほん|ぶどう"},{"japanese":"柔道の試合を見ました。","romaji":"Jūdō no shiai o mimashita.","german":"I watched a judo match.","furigana":"じゅうどう|しあい|み"},{"japanese":"弟は学校のクラブで柔道をしています。","romaji":"Otōto wa gakkō no kurabu de jūdō o shite imasu.","german":"My younger brother does judo in his school club.","furigana":"おとうと|がっこう|じゅうどう"}],"notes":"You do judo with 柔道をする or 柔道をやる; taking lessons is 柔道を習う. The judo uniform is the 柔道着, a match is a 試合. Like 剣道, 茶道 or 書道, the word ends in 道（どう）, \"way\"; the umbrella term for the martial arts is 武道.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 26"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"相撲を見に行きました。","romaji":"Sumō o mi ni ikimashita.","german":"I went to watch sumo.","furigana":"すもう|み|い"},{"japanese":"相撲の力士はとても大きいです。","romaji":"Sumō no rikishi wa totemo ōkii desu.","german":"Sumo wrestlers are very big.","furigana":"すもう|りきし|おお"},{"japanese":"子供たちが公園で相撲を取っています。","romaji":"Kodomotachi ga kōen de sumō o totte imasu.","german":"The children are wrestling sumo-style in the park.","furigana":"こども|こうえん|すもう|と"}],"notes":"The reading すもう is irregular. To wrestle sumo is 相撲を取る (not する); to watch is 相撲を見る. The wrestlers are called 力士, colloquially and affectionately お相撲さん. A tournament is called 場所, for example 九州場所.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 19"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"書道を練習しています。","romaji":"Shodō o renshū shite imasu.","german":"I am practicing calligraphy.","furigana":"しょどう|れんしゅう"},{"japanese":"書道の授業で漢字を書きます。","romaji":"Shodō no jugyō de kanji o kakimasu.","german":"In calligraphy class, I write kanji.","furigana":"しょどう|じゅぎょう|かんじ|か"},{"japanese":"書道には筆と墨を使います。","romaji":"Shodō ni wa fude to sumi o tsukaimasu.","german":"You use a brush and ink for calligraphy.","furigana":"しょどう|ふで|すみ|つか"}],"notes":"書道 is the art of writing characters on paper with a brush (筆) and ink (墨). You say 書道を習う or 書道を練習する; classes are called 書道教室. In elementary school, the subject is usually called 習字（しゅうじ）, which is more like \"penmanship\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"茶道を体験してみたいです。","romaji":"Sadou o taiken shite mitai desu.","german":"I would like to try the tea ceremony.","furigana":"さどう|たいけん"},{"japanese":"茶道では、お茶を飲む前に茶碗を回します。","romaji":"Sadō de wa, ocha o nomu mae ni chawan o mawashimasu.","german":"In the tea ceremony, you turn the tea bowl before drinking.","furigana":"さどう|ちゃ|の|まえ|ちゃわん|まわ"}],"notes":"茶道 is read さどう or ちゃどう; both readings are correct. In everyday speech people often simply say お茶: お茶を習っています means \"I am learning the tea ceremony\". To try it out: 茶道を体験する. What is drunk is powdered green tea, 抹茶.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 34"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"母は生け花を習っています。","romaji":"Haha wa ikebana o naratte imasu.","german":"My mother is learning flower arranging.","furigana":"はは|い|ばな|なら"},{"japanese":"玄関に生け花を飾りました。","romaji":"Genkan ni ikebana o kazarimashita.","german":"I displayed an ikebana arrangement in the entryway.","furigana":"げんかん|い|ばな|かざ"}],"notes":"生け花 denotes both the art of flower arranging and the finished arrangement. Spellings: 生け花, 生花, or いけばな; as an art form it is also called 華道（かどう）. To learn: 生け花を習う; to display an arrangement: 生け花を飾る. The related verb is 花を生ける.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 12"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"折り紙で鶴を作りました。","romaji":"Origami de tsuru o tsukurimashita.","german":"I made a crane out of origami paper.","furigana":"お|がみ|つる|つく"},{"japanese":"折り紙を一枚もらえますか。","romaji":"Origami o ichimai moraemasu ka.","german":"Could I have a sheet of origami paper?","furigana":"お|がみ|いちまい"}],"notes":"折り紙 is both the art of paper folding and the square folding paper itself; sheets are counted with 枚. Folding is 折る: 鶴を折る. The material takes で: 折り紙で鶴を作る. The expression 折り紙付き (\"guaranteed, proven\") comes from the second meaning, \"certificate of authenticity\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"おじいさんは畑で野菜を作っています。","romaji":"Ojiisan wa hatake de yasai o tsukutte imasu.","german":"My grandfather grows vegetables in his field.","furigana":"はたけ|やさい|つく"},{"japanese":"家の裏に小さい畑があります。","romaji":"Ie no ura ni chiisai hatake ga arimasu.","german":"There is a small vegetable garden behind the house.","furigana":"いえ|うら|ちい|はたけ"}],"notes":"畑 is a dry field for vegetables, grain or fruit, as opposed to the irrigated rice field 田んぼ. To grow vegetables is 畑で野菜を作る. In compounds it becomes ～ばたけ: 麦畑, 茶畑. The kanji is a character created in Japan (国字).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"田んぼが広がっています。","romaji":"Tanbo ga hirogatte imasu.","german":"The rice paddies stretch out far and wide.","furigana":"た|ひろ"},{"japanese":"春になると田んぼに水が入ります。","romaji":"Haru ni naru to tanbo ni mizu ga hairimasu.","german":"In spring, water is let into the rice paddy.","furigana":"はる|た|みず|はい"},{"japanese":"夜、田んぼでカエルが鳴いています。","romaji":"Yoru, tanbo de kaeru ga naite imasu.","german":"At night, frogs croak in the rice paddy.","furigana":"よる|た|な"}],"notes":"田んぼ is the usual word for a (flooded) rice paddy; the single kanji 田（た）appears mainly in compounds and names such as 田中. A dry field for vegetables, by contrast, is called 畑. Planting rice seedlings in spring is called 田植え.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この坂は急です。","romaji":"Kono saka wa kyū desu.","german":"This slope is steep.","furigana":"さか|きゅう"},{"japanese":"坂を上ると、駅が見えます。","romaji":"Saka o noboru to, eki ga miemasu.","german":"If you go up the slope, you can see the station.","furigana":"さか|のぼ|えき|み"},{"japanese":"学校は坂の上にあります。","romaji":"Gakkō wa saka no ue ni arimasu.","german":"The school is at the top of the slope.","furigana":"がっこう|さか|うえ"}],"notes":"Up a slope is 坂を上る（のぼる）, down is 坂を下りる or 坂を下る（くだる）; the path takes を. An incline is an 上り坂, a descent a 下り坂, the latter also figuratively for \"things are going downhill\". \"Steep\" is 急: 急な坂. In place names it often becomes ～ざか.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この通りをまっすぐ行きます。","romaji":"Kono tōri o massugu ikimasu.","german":"Go straight along this street.","furigana":"とお|い"},{"japanese":"この通りにはお店がたくさんあります。","romaji":"Kono tōri ni wa omise ga takusan arimasu.","german":"There are many shops on this street.","furigana":"とお|みせ"},{"japanese":"夜は通りに人がほとんどいません。","romaji":"Yoru wa tōri ni hito ga hotondo imasen.","german":"At night there are hardly any people on the street.","furigana":"よる|とお|ひと"}],"notes":"通り is a busy street with houses and shops, whereas 道 generally means \"road, way\". Going along a street: この通りをまっすぐ行く (with を). In street names it becomes ～どおり: 中央通り. Do not confuse it with ～とおり \"as, just as\" (言ったとおり) and the counter for kinds (二通り).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"都会の生活は忙しいです。","romaji":"Tokai no seikatsu wa isogashii desu.","german":"Life in the city is busy.","furigana":"とかい|せいかつ|いそが"},{"japanese":"田舎より都会のほうが便利です。","romaji":"Inaka yori tokai no hō ga benri desu.","german":"The city is more convenient than the countryside.","furigana":"いなか|とかい|べんり"}],"notes":"都会 means a larger city or the urban environment, often in contrast to 田舎, the countryside. With の it can modify another noun: 都会の生活. The card uses the word for life in a big city, not for the rare identically written abbreviation of the Tokyo metropolitan assembly.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 31"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"田舎は静かです。","romaji":"Inaka wa shizuka desu.","german":"The countryside is quiet.","furigana":"いなか|しず"},{"japanese":"将来は田舎で暮らしたいです。","romaji":"Shōrai wa inaka de kurashitai desu.","german":"In the future I want to live in the countryside.","furigana":"しょうらい|いなか|く"},{"japanese":"お正月は田舎に帰ります。","romaji":"Oshōgatsu wa inaka ni kaerimasu.","german":"I go back to my hometown for New Year.","furigana":"しょうがつ|いなか|かえ"}],"notes":"田舎 is the countryside as opposed to the big city (都会). It also means one's own hometown: 田舎に帰る means \"to go back to one's hometown\". Depending on context, the word sounds slightly derogatory, as in 田舎者 (\"country bumpkin\"). The reading いなか is irregular.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 25"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"故郷が懐かしいです。","romaji":"Furusato ga natsukashii desu.","german":"I miss my hometown.","furigana":"ふるさと|なつ"},{"japanese":"故郷の料理が食べたいです。","romaji":"Furusato no ryōri ga tabetai desu.","german":"I want to eat food from my hometown.","furigana":"ふるさと|りょうり|た"},{"japanese":"私の故郷は北海道の小さな町です。","romaji":"Watashi no furusato wa Hokkaidō no chiisana machi desu.","german":"My hometown is a small town in Hokkaido.","furigana":"わたし|ふるさと|ほっかいどう|ちい|まち"}],"notes":"故郷 is read ふるさと in everyday use and often also written in kana; the reading こきょう sounds more literary. The word is emotional and fits homesickness: 故郷が懐かしい. To ask neutrally about someone's place of origin, use 出身: ご出身はどちらですか.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 18"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"夏は湿気が多くて大変です。","romaji":"Natsu wa shikke ga ookute taihen desu.","german":"The humidity is unpleasantly high in summer.","furigana":"なつ|しっけ|おお|たいへん"},{"japanese":"湿気で洗濯物がなかなか乾きません。","romaji":"Shikke de sentakumono ga nakanaka kawakimasen.","german":"Because of the humidity, the laundry hardly dries.","furigana":"しっけ|せんたくもの|かわ"}],"notes":"湿気 is the perceptible moisture in the air or in rooms. High or low levels are expressed with 湿気が多い and 湿気が少ない. For the measurable relative humidity in percent, 湿度（しつど）is used instead: 湿度は八十パーセントです. Muggy, hot weather is described as 蒸し暑い.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"紅葉を見に京都に行きます。","romaji":"Kouyou o mi ni Kyouto ni ikimasu.","german":"I am going to Kyoto to see the autumn leaves.","furigana":"こうよう|み|きょうと|い"},{"japanese":"今年は紅葉が遅いです。","romaji":"Kotoshi wa kōyō ga osoi desu.","german":"The autumn colors are late this year.","furigana":"ことし|こうよう|おそ"}],"notes":"紅葉 refers to the red and yellow autumn coloring of leaves; as a する-verb, 紅葉する means \"to turn autumn colors\". The same kanji are also read もみじ, which sounds more poetic and also means \"maple\". An outing to view autumn leaves is called 紅葉狩り（もみじがり）, the counterpart to spring 花見.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"桜が満開です。","romaji":"Sakura ga mankai desu.","german":"The cherry blossoms are in full bloom.","furigana":"さくら|まんかい"},{"japanese":"庭に桜を一本植えました。","romaji":"Niwa ni sakura o ippon uemashita.","german":"I planted a cherry tree in the garden.","furigana":"にわ|さくら|いっぽんう"}],"notes":"桜 can denote both the cherry tree and its blossoms. 桜が満開 means that the trees are in full bloom; 桜を植える speaks of planting a tree. This card practices this plant word, not the other meaning of サクラ for paid or fake audience members.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 8"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"目覚ましを六時にセットしました。","romaji":"Mezamashi o rokuji ni setto shimashita.","german":"I set the alarm clock for six o'clock.","furigana":"めざ|ろくじ"},{"japanese":"今朝は目覚ましが鳴らなくて、遅刻しました。","romaji":"Kesa wa mezamashi ga naranakute, chikoku shimashita.","german":"The alarm didn't ring this morning, so I was late.","furigana":"けさ|めざ|な|ちこく"}],"notes":"目覚まし is the short form of 目覚まし時計. You set the alarm with 目覚ましをセットする or 目覚ましをかける, and the time takes に: 六時に. The alarm ringing is 目覚ましが鳴る, and turning it off is 目覚ましを止める. In 目覚ましにコーヒーを飲む, the basic meaning \"to wake oneself up\" is at work.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"定期を更新しました。","romaji":"Teiki o kōshin shimashita.","german":"I renewed my commuter pass.","furigana":"ていき|こうしん"},{"japanese":"定期を忘れてしまいました。","romaji":"Teiki o wasurete shimaimashita.","german":"I forgot my commuter pass.","furigana":"ていき|わす"},{"japanese":"来週で定期が切れます。","romaji":"Raishū de teiki ga kiremasu.","german":"My commuter pass expires next week.","furigana":"らいしゅう|ていき|き"}],"notes":"定期 here is the short form of 定期券, a commuter pass for a fixed route between two stations, often for one, three or six months. Today it is usually stored on an IC card such as Suica. Typical: 定期を買う, 定期を更新する, 定期が切れる (\"to expire\"). In other words 定期 means \"regular\": 定期的に, 定期検診.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"渋滞で遅れました。","romaji":"Jūtai de okuremashita.","german":"I was late because of a traffic jam.","furigana":"じゅうたい|おく"},{"japanese":"高速道路で渋滞に巻き込まれました。","romaji":"Kōsoku dōro de jūtai ni makikomaremashita.","german":"I got caught in a traffic jam on the expressway.","furigana":"こうそくどうろ|じゅうたい|ま|こ"},{"japanese":"連休の初日は道路がとても渋滞します。","romaji":"Renkyū no shonichi wa dōro ga totemo jūtai shimasu.","german":"On the first day of a long weekend the roads are very congested.","furigana":"れんきゅう|しょにち|どうろ|じゅうたい"}],"notes":"渋滞 is a traffic jam; as an intransitive する-verb: 道路が渋滞している. The cause takes で: 渋滞で遅れる. To get caught in a traffic jam is 渋滞に巻き込まれる or, colloquially, 渋滞にはまる. For crowds of people at stations or in shops, you say not 渋滞 but 混雑 or 混む.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"運転は好きですか。","romaji":"Unten wa suki desu ka.","german":"Do you like driving?","furigana":"うんてん|す"},{"japanese":"運転中にスマホを使わないでください。","romaji":"Untenchū ni sumaho o tsukawanaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't use your phone while driving.","furigana":"うんてんちゅう|つか"},{"japanese":"大雪で電車の運転が止まりました。","romaji":"Ōyuki de densha no unten ga tomarimashita.","german":"Train service was suspended because of heavy snow.","furigana":"おおゆき|でんしゃ|うんてん|と"}],"notes":"Driving a vehicle is 車を運転する (vehicle with を); the driver is the 運転手, the driver's license 運転免許. For bicycles, by contrast, you say 自転車に乗る. 運転 also refers to the operation of trains and machines: 電車の運転が止まる. With 中 you get 運転中 (\"while driving\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"免許を取りました。","romaji":"Menkyo o torimashita.","german":"I got my license.","furigana":"めんきょ|と"},{"japanese":"免許を取るために教習所に通っています。","romaji":"Menkyo o toru tame ni kyōshūjo ni kayotte imasu.","german":"I go to driving school in order to get my license.","furigana":"めんきょ|と|きょうしゅうじょ|かよ"},{"japanese":"この仕事には特別な免許が必要です。","romaji":"Kono shigoto ni wa tokubetsu na menkyo ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"This job requires a special license.","furigana":"しごと|とくべつ|めんきょ|ひつよう"}],"notes":"免許 means an official permit or license. In everyday speech the driver's license is often meant: 免許を取る means to obtain it. The full expression 運転免許 makes this meaning clear; without context, 免許 can also mean another professional or technical qualification.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 22"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"手帳に予定を書きます。","romaji":"Techō ni yotei o kakimasu.","german":"I write my appointments in my planner.","furigana":"てちょう|よてい|か"},{"japanese":"もう来年の手帳を買いました。","romaji":"Mō rainen no techō o kaimashita.","german":"I've already bought next year's planner.","furigana":"らいねん|てちょう|か"},{"japanese":"手帳をなくしてしまいました。","romaji":"Techō o nakushite shimaimashita.","german":"I lost my pocket planner.","furigana":"てちょう"}],"notes":"手帳 is a small notebook or pocket planner in which you mainly enter appointments: 手帳に予定を書く (destination with に). A school notebook, by contrast, is ノート, and a notepad is メモ帳. 手帳 also stands for official booklets such as 母子手帳 (maternal and child health handbook).","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"契約を結びました。","romaji":"Keiyaku o musubimashita.","german":"We signed a contract.","furigana":"けいやく|むす"},{"japanese":"携帯電話の契約を変えました。","romaji":"Keitai denwa no keiyaku o kaemashita.","german":"I changed my mobile phone contract.","furigana":"けいたいでんわ|けいやく|か"},{"japanese":"こちらの契約書にサインをお願いします。","romaji":"Kochira no keiyakusho ni sain o onegai shimasu.","german":"Please sign this contract.","furigana":"けいやくしょ|ねが"}],"notes":"To conclude a contract is formally 契約を結ぶ or simply 契約する; the contracting partner takes と: 会社と契約する. The contract document is the 契約書. When a contract expires, you say 契約が切れる; 契約社員 are fixed-term employees.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"レポートの締め切りは金曜日です。","romaji":"Repooto no shimekiri wa kin'youbi desu.","german":"The deadline for the report is Friday.","furigana":"し|き|きんようび"},{"japanese":"締め切りに間に合いませんでした。","romaji":"Shimekiri ni maniaimasen deshita.","german":"I didn't make the deadline.","furigana":"し|き|ま|あ"}],"notes":"締め切り is the submission or registration deadline. Being on time is 締め切りに間に合う, extending a deadline is 締め切りを延ばす. The spellings 締切 and 〆切 are also seen. The related verb 締め切る means \"to close off, to stop accepting\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 33"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"父は来年退職します。","romaji":"Chichi wa rainen taishoku shimasu.","german":"My father retires next year.","furigana":"ちち|らいねんたいしょく"},{"japanese":"病気のため、会社を退職しました。","romaji":"Byōki no tame, kaisha o taishoku shimashita.","german":"I left the company because of illness.","furigana":"びょうき|かいしゃ|たいしょく"}],"notes":"退職 is leaving a job, whether through retirement (定年退職) or one's own resignation. The company takes を: 会社を退職する. In conversation, people often just say 会社を辞める. Dismissal by the employer, by contrast, is 解雇 or, colloquially, クビ; severance pay is 退職金.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"来年就職します。","romaji":"Rainen shūshoku shimasu.","german":"I'm starting a job next year.","furigana":"らいねんしゅうしょく"},{"japanese":"就職活動を始めました。","romaji":"Shūshoku katsudō o hajimemashita.","german":"I started job hunting.","furigana":"しゅうしょくかつどう|はじ"},{"japanese":"大学を卒業して、銀行に就職しました。","romaji":"Daigaku o sotsugyō shite, ginkō ni shūshoku shimashita.","german":"After graduating from university, I got a job at a bank.","furigana":"だいがく|そつぎょう|ぎんこう|しゅうしょく"}],"notes":"就職 means getting a permanent position, especially after university. The company takes に: 銀行に就職する. The job search itself is 就職活動, abbreviated 就活. The counterpart is 退職; changing jobs is 転職.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"面接の準備をしています。","romaji":"Mensetsu no junbi o shite imasu.","german":"I'm preparing for the interview.","furigana":"めんせつ|じゅんび"},{"japanese":"面接でいろいろな質問をされました。","romaji":"Mensetsu de iroiro na shitsumon o saremashita.","german":"I was asked various questions in the interview.","furigana":"めんせつ|しつもん"},{"japanese":"この大学の入試には面接もあります。","romaji":"Kono daigaku no nyūshi ni wa mensetsu mo arimasu.","german":"This university's entrance exam also includes an interview.","furigana":"だいがく|にゅうし|めんせつ"}],"notes":"面接 is a personal selection interview, for example for a job application, university admission or a visa application. As an applicant you take part with 面接を受ける; the person conducting it says 面接をする; the interviewer is the 面接官. An interview with journalists, by contrast, is an インタビュー.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 23"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"履歴書を書いて送りました。","romaji":"Rirekisho o kaite okurimashita.","german":"I wrote my resume and sent it in.","furigana":"りれきしょ|か|おく"},{"japanese":"履歴書には写真を貼ってください。","romaji":"Rirekisho ni wa shashin o hatte kudasai.","german":"Please attach a photo to your resume.","furigana":"りれきしょ|しゃしん|は"}],"notes":"A Japanese 履歴書 is a standardized form with a passport photo, education and career history; in the past it was often written by hand. Submitting it: 履歴書を出す or formally 履歴書を提出する. Detailed work experience is described additionally in the 職務経歴書.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 14"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"兄は病院で技師として働いています。","romaji":"Ani wa byouin de gishi to shite hataraite imasu.","german":"My older brother works as a technician at a hospital.","furigana":"あに|びょういん|ぎし|はたら"},{"japanese":"技師が機械を修理しに来ました。","romaji":"Gishi ga kikai o shūri shi ni kimashita.","german":"A technician came to repair the machine.","furigana":"ぎし|きかい|しゅうり|き"}],"notes":"技師 is a rather formal job title for technical specialists. In everyday speech 技術者 or エンジニア is more common. Today 技師 is encountered mainly in fixed titles such as 放射線技師 (radiology technologist). \"To work as a technician\" is 技師として働く.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"弁護士に相談しました。","romaji":"Bengoshi ni sōdan shimashita.","german":"I consulted a lawyer.","furigana":"べんごし|そうだん"},{"japanese":"姉は弁護士として東京の事務所で働いています。","romaji":"Ane wa bengoshi to shite Tōkyō no jimusho de hataraite imasu.","german":"My older sister works as a lawyer at a law office in Tokyo.","furigana":"あね|べんごし|とうきょう|じむしょ|はたら"}],"notes":"弁護士 denotes a person who works professionally as a lawyer. 弁護士に相談する means \"to get legal advice\"; 弁護士として働く describes the professional activity. The word distinguishes this person from a judge, 裁判官, and a prosecutor, 検察官.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"裁判に出ます。","romaji":"Saiban ni demasu.","german":"I'm appearing in court.","furigana":"さいばん|で"},{"japanese":"裁判は来月始まります。","romaji":"Saiban wa raigetsu hajimarimasu.","german":"The trial starts next month.","furigana":"さいばん|らいげつはじ"},{"japanese":"長い時間がかかりましたが、裁判に勝ちました。","romaji":"Nagai jikan ga kakarimashita ga, saiban ni kachimashita.","german":"It took a long time, but we won the case.","furigana":"なが|じかん|さいばん|か"}],"notes":"裁判 is the legal proceeding itself; the court as a building or institution is 裁判所, the judge 裁判官. To file a lawsuit is 裁判を起こす, to win or lose a case 裁判に勝つ or 裁判に負ける. As a party or witness you appear with 裁判に出る.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"法律を守らなければなりません。","romaji":"Houritsu o mamoranakereba narimasen.","german":"We must obey the law.","furigana":"ほうりつ|まも"},{"japanese":"姉は大学で法律を勉強しています。","romaji":"Ane wa daigaku de hōritsu o benkyō shite imasu.","german":"My older sister is studying law at university.","furigana":"あね|だいがく|ほうりつ|べんきょう"},{"japanese":"新しい法律ができました。","romaji":"Atarashii hōritsu ga dekimashita.","german":"A new law has been enacted.","furigana":"あたら|ほうりつ"}],"notes":"To obey the law is 法律を守る, to violate it 法律に違反する. \"Prohibited by law\" is expressed with 法律で禁止されている. As a field of study, 法律 means about \"law (jurisprudence)\": 法律を勉強する. For an individual law with a name, ～法 often stands: 労働基準法.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 42"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"選挙に行きます。","romaji":"Senkyo ni ikimasu.","german":"I'm going to vote.","furigana":"せんきょ|い"},{"japanese":"来月、市長の選挙があります。","romaji":"Raigetsu, shichō no senkyo ga arimasu.","german":"Next month there is a mayoral election.","furigana":"らいげつ|しちょう|せんきょ"},{"japanese":"日本では十八歳から選挙で投票できます。","romaji":"Nihon de wa jūhassai kara senkyo de tōhyō dekimasu.","german":"In Japan you can vote in elections from the age of 18.","furigana":"にほん|じゅうはっさい|せんきょ|とうひょう"}],"notes":"\"To go and vote\" is 選挙に行く; casting your vote is 投票する. That an election takes place is said with 選挙がある or formally 選挙が行われる. To run as a candidate is 選挙に出る. The simple everyday \"to choose\", by contrast, is 選ぶ.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 23"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"貿易会社で働いています。","romaji":"Bōeki gaisha de hataraite imasu.","german":"I work at a trading company.","furigana":"ぼうえきがいしゃ|はたら"},{"japanese":"日本は多くの国と貿易をしています。","romaji":"Nihon wa ōku no kuni to bōeki o shite imasu.","german":"Japan trades with many countries.","furigana":"にほん|おお|くに|ぼうえき"}],"notes":"貿易 is trade with foreign countries, that is, import (輸入) and export (輸出). The partner takes と: 多くの国と貿易をする. A trading company is a 貿易会社（ぼうえきがいしゃ）. For trade and business within the country, 商売 or 取引 is rather used.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 49"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"税金を払います。","romaji":"Zeikin o haraimasu.","german":"I pay taxes.","furigana":"ぜいきん|はら"},{"japanese":"この値段には税金が含まれていますか。","romaji":"Kono nedan ni wa zeikin ga fukumarete imasu ka.","german":"Is tax included in this price?","furigana":"ねだん|ぜいきん|ふく"},{"japanese":"来年から税金が上がるそうです。","romaji":"Rainen kara zeikin ga agaru sō desu.","german":"I hear taxes will go up from next year.","furigana":"らいねん|ぜいきん|あ"}],"notes":"税金 is the general term for taxes; individual kinds of tax end in ～税, for example 消費税 (consumption tax). To pay taxes is 税金を払う, formally 税金を納める. On price tags you read 税込み (\"tax included\") and 税抜き (\"excluding tax\").","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 15"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"将来のために貯金しています。","romaji":"Shourai no tame ni chokin shite imasu.","german":"I'm saving money for the future.","furigana":"しょうらい|ちょきん"},{"japanese":"貯金を全部使って、車を買いました。","romaji":"Chokin o zenbu tsukatte, kuruma o kaimashita.","german":"I spent all my savings and bought a car.","furigana":"ちょきん|ぜんぶつか|くるま|か"}],"notes":"貯金 means both saving and the savings themselves. As a する-verb: 貯金する (\"to put money aside\"); existing savings are expressed with 貯金がある. A piggy bank is a 貯金箱. The more formal 預金 refers specifically to bank deposits.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"借金を早く返したいです。","romaji":"Shakkin o hayaku kaeshitai desu.","german":"I want to pay off my debt quickly.","furigana":"しゃっきん|はや|かえ"},{"japanese":"家を買うために、銀行から借金をしました。","romaji":"Ie o kau tame ni, ginkō kara shakkin o shimashita.","german":"I took out a loan from the bank to buy a house.","furigana":"いえ|か|ぎんこう|しゃっきん"}],"notes":"借金 is a debt of money. To borrow money or go into debt is 借金をする; from whom is marked with から or に: 銀行から借金をする. To repay is 借金を返す, to be in debt is 借金がある. For borrowing objects, simply use 借りる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"保険に入っていますか。","romaji":"Hoken ni haitte imasu ka.","german":"Do you have insurance?","furigana":"ほけん|はい"},{"japanese":"病院に行くときは、保険証を持っていきます。","romaji":"Byōin ni iku toki wa, hokenshō o motte ikimasu.","german":"When I go to the doctor, I take my insurance card with me.","furigana":"びょういん|い|ほけんしょう|も"},{"japanese":"事故の修理代は保険で払いました。","romaji":"Jiko no shūridai wa hoken de haraimashita.","german":"I paid for the accident repairs through insurance.","furigana":"じこ|しゅうりだい|ほけん|はら"}],"notes":"Taking out insurance is 保険に入る (with に). Important types are 健康保険 (health insurance) and 生命保険 (life insurance); the insurance card is the 保険証. Do not confuse it with the similar-sounding 保健 (\"health care\", e.g. 保健室).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"年金を受け取ります。","romaji":"Nenkin o uketorimasu.","german":"I receive a pension.","furigana":"ねんきん|う|と"},{"japanese":"年金だけでは生活が大変です。","romaji":"Nenkin dake de wa seikatsu ga taihen desu.","german":"It's hard to live on the pension alone.","furigana":"ねんきん|せいかつ|たいへん"},{"japanese":"毎月、年金を払っています。","romaji":"Maitsuki, nenkin o haratte imasu.","german":"I pay into the pension every month.","furigana":"まいつき|ねんきん|はら"}],"notes":"年金 is the regular pension and also the pension system. To receive a pension is 年金をもらう or more formally 年金を受け取る; paying contributions is colloquially 年金を払う. Someone who lives on their pension leads a 年金生活. Leaving work itself, by contrast, is 退職 or 定年.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"出張の経費を計算しました。","romaji":"Shutchou no keihi o keisan shimashita.","german":"I calculated the expenses for the business trip.","furigana":"しゅっちょう|けいひ|けいさん"},{"japanese":"会社は経費を減らさなければなりません。","romaji":"Kaisha wa keihi o herasanakereba narimasen.","german":"The company has to cut expenses.","furigana":"かいしゃ|けいひ|へ"}],"notes":"経費 are costs in a business context, such as operating or travel costs. Private spending is more often called 費用 or 出費. Charging something to expenses is colloquially 経費で落とす; cutting costs is 経費を減らす or, formally, 経費を削減する.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"収入が増えました。","romaji":"Shūnyū ga fuemashita.","german":"My income has increased.","furigana":"しゅうにゅう|ふ"},{"japanese":"アルバイトの収入で生活しています。","romaji":"Arubaito no shūnyū de seikatsu shite imasu.","german":"I live on my income from a part-time job.","furigana":"しゅうにゅう|せいかつ"}],"notes":"収入 covers all earnings, not just salary (給料). You say 収入が多い or 収入が少ない, and changes with 収入が増える and 収入が減る. The counterpart is expenses, 支出. The source takes で: アルバイトの収入で生活する.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 43"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"毎月の支出を管理しています。","romaji":"Maitsuki no shishutsu o kanri shite imasu.","german":"I keep track of my monthly expenses.","furigana":"まいつき|ししゅつ|かんり"},{"japanese":"今月は収入より支出のほうが多かったです。","romaji":"Kongetsu wa shūnyū yori shishutsu no hō ga ōkatta desu.","german":"This month my expenses were higher than my income.","furigana":"こんげつ|しゅうにゅう|ししゅつ|おお"},{"japanese":"会社は今年、広告の支出を減らしました。","romaji":"Kaisha wa kotoshi, kōkoku no shishutsu o herashimashita.","german":"The company cut its advertising expenditure this year.","furigana":"かいしゃ|ことし|こうこく|ししゅつ|へ"}],"notes":"支出 is the formal word for expenses in a household account book, a company balance sheet or a national budget; the counterpart is 収入 (\"income\"). Typical combinations: 支出が増える, 支出を減らす, 毎月の支出. In casual everyday speech 出費 or simply 使ったお金 is more usual; as a する-verb (支出する) it sounds official.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"会社の利益が上がりました。","romaji":"Kaisha no rieki ga agarimashita.","german":"The company's profit has gone up.","furigana":"かいしゃ|りえき|あ"},{"japanese":"自分の利益だけを考えてはいけません。","romaji":"Jibun no rieki dake o kangaete wa ikemasen.","german":"You must not think only of your own interest.","furigana":"じぶん|りえき|かんが"}],"notes":"利益 is the profit of a business: 利益が出る (\"a profit arises\"), 利益を上げる (\"to make a profit\"). It also means \"benefit, interest\": 自分の利益 (\"one's own benefit\"). The opposite is 損 or 損失; for small personal advantages in daily life, 得 is used more often (得をする).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"台風で大きな損害が出ました。","romaji":"Taifuu de ookina songai ga demashita.","german":"The typhoon caused great damage.","furigana":"たいふう|おお|そんがい|で"},{"japanese":"間違った情報で、会社に大きな損害を与えてしまいました。","romaji":"Machigatta jōhō de, kaisha ni ōkina songai o ataete shimaimashita.","german":"Because of wrong information, I caused the company great damage.","furigana":"まちが|じょうほう|かいしゃ|おお|そんがい|あた"}],"notes":"損害 is material or financial damage, for example from storms, accidents or business mistakes. Typical: 損害が出る (\"damage occurs\"), 損害を受ける (\"to suffer damage\"), ～に損害を与える (\"to cause damage to someone\"). The related 被害 emphasizes the victim's side and is also used when people are injured; 損害 targets the loss of value or money.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この業界は競争が激しいです。","romaji":"Kono gyoukai wa kyousou ga hageshii desu.","german":"Competition is fierce in this industry.","furigana":"ぎょうかい|きょうそう|はげ"},{"japanese":"弟と駅まで競争しました。","romaji":"Otōto to eki made kyōsō shimashita.","german":"I raced my younger brother to the station.","furigana":"おとうと|えき|きょうそう"}],"notes":"競争 means competition and rivalry, but also quite concretely a race. Typical: 競争が激しい (\"fierce competition\"), 競争に勝つ／負ける; the one you compete with takes と: 友達と競争する. A competitor is a 競争相手. For a sports game or tournament, by contrast, you say 試合.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい商品の宣伝をしています。","romaji":"Atarashii shouhin no senden o shite imasu.","german":"We're advertising the new product.","furigana":"あたら|しょうひん|せんでん"},{"japanese":"テレビで宣伝しているお菓子を買ってみました。","romaji":"Terebi de senden shite iru okashi o katte mimashita.","german":"I tried the sweets that are being advertised on TV.","furigana":"せんでん|かし|か"}],"notes":"宣伝 is the activity of advertising, i.e. making something known - through ads, TV commercials or word of mouth; in a political context also \"propaganda\". As a する-verb the advertised thing takes を: 新商品を宣伝する; as a noun you say ～の宣伝をする. A single ad or advertising poster, in contrast, is a 広告.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"広告を見ました。","romaji":"Kōkoku o mimashita.","german":"I saw the advertisement.","furigana":"こうこく|み"},{"japanese":"インターネット広告が増えています。","romaji":"Intānetto kōkoku ga fuete imasu.","german":"Internet advertising is increasing.","furigana":"こうこく|ふ"},{"japanese":"新聞に店の広告を出しました。","romaji":"Shinbun ni mise no kōkoku o dashimashita.","german":"We placed an ad for our shop in the newspaper.","furigana":"しんぶん|みせ|こうこく|だ"}],"notes":"広告 is the concrete advertisement or advertising space: 新聞広告, ネット広告, 駅の広告. To place an ad is 広告を出す. In contrast to 宣伝 (advertising activity as a whole), 広告 means more the medium or the individual ad; TV commercials are colloquially called CM (シーエム).","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 13"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"資料を十部印刷してください。","romaji":"Shiryou o juubu insatsu shite kudasai.","german":"Please print ten copies of the materials.","furigana":"しりょう|じゅうぶいんさつ"},{"japanese":"年賀状を家のプリンターで印刷しました。","romaji":"Nengajō o ie no purintā de insatsu shimashita.","german":"I printed the New Year's cards on my home printer.","furigana":"ねんがじょう|いえ|いんさつ"}],"notes":"What is printed takes を: 書類を印刷する; the device or method takes で: プリンターで, カラーで印刷する. Copies are counted with 部: 資料を十部. In office life you also hear プリントする; 印刷 sounds more formal and is also used for print shops and books.","levelNote":"Derived from 印刷 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この書類をコピーしてください。","romaji":"Kono shorui o kopī shite kudasai.","german":"Please copy this document.","furigana":"しょるい"},{"japanese":"会議の前に資料を十部コピーしておきました。","romaji":"Kaigi no mae ni shiryō o jūbu kopī shite okimashita.","german":"Before the meeting I made ten copies of the materials.","furigana":"かいぎ|まえ|しりょう|じゅうぶ"},{"japanese":"写真のファイルを別のフォルダーにコピーしました。","romaji":"Shashin no fairu o betsu no forudā ni kopī shimashita.","german":"I copied the photo files to another folder.","furigana":"しゃしん|べつ"}],"notes":"コピーする means to make a copy of something. The thing copied takes を; for paper, you can count copies with 部. The action can also be copying a file. As a suru-verb it forms コピーして・コピーしない; an advertising slogan as the noun コピー is a different meaning.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"レストランの場所を検索しました。","romaji":"Resutoran no basho o kensaku shimashita.","german":"I searched for the restaurant's location.","furigana":"ばしょ|けんさく"},{"japanese":"知らない言葉は、スマホの辞書で検索します。","romaji":"Shiranai kotoba wa, sumaho no jisho de kensaku shimasu.","german":"I look up words I don't know in the dictionary on my smartphone.","furigana":"し|ことば|じしょ|けんさく"}],"notes":"The thing searched for takes を, the medium takes で: ネットで店の場所を検索する. 検索 is targeted searching in databases, dictionaries or on the internet; lost things or people, by contrast, are searched for with 探す (さがす). Colloquially, a Google search is also called ググる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい建物を設計します。","romaji":"Atarashii tatemono o sekkei shimasu.","german":"Ich entwerfe ein neues Gebäude.","furigana":"あたら|たてもの|せっけい"},{"japanese":"この車はドイツで設計されました。","romaji":"Kono kuruma wa Doitsu de sekkei saremashita.","german":"Dieses Auto wurde in Deutschland konstruiert.","furigana":"くるま|せっけい"}],"notes":"設計 ist der technische Entwurf von Gebäuden, Maschinen oder Systemen; was entworfen wird, steht mit を. Den Bauplan nennt man 設計図, die entwerfende Person 設計者. Für Mode oder Grafik sagt man eher デザインする; Vorhaben im Sinne von „planen“ macht man mit 計画する.","levelNote":"Derived from 設計 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"車を修理します。","romaji":"Kuruma o shūri shimasu.","german":"I'm repairing the car.","furigana":"くるま|しゅうり"},{"japanese":"壊れた時計を修理してもらいました。","romaji":"Kowareta tokei o shūri shite moraimashita.","german":"I had the broken watch repaired.","furigana":"こわ|とけい|しゅうり"},{"japanese":"エアコンの修理に一週間かかります。","romaji":"Eakon no shūri ni isshūkan kakarimasu.","german":"The repair of the air conditioner takes a week.","furigana":"しゅうり|いっしゅうかん"}],"notes":"What is repaired takes を: 車を修理する. To have something repaired, you say 修理してもらう or 修理に出す (\"to hand in for repair\"). In everyday speech 直す (なおす) is the more common word; 修理 sounds more formal and suits devices, cars and buildings in particular - not illnesses (for those, 治す).","levelNote":"Derived from 修理 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"机の上を整理します。","romaji":"Tsukue no ue o seiri shimasu.","german":"I'm tidying up the top of my desk.","furigana":"つくえ|うえ|せいり"},{"japanese":"引っ越しの前に、古い服を整理しました。","romaji":"Hikkoshi no mae ni, furui fuku o seiri shimashita.","german":"Before moving, I sorted through my old clothes.","furigana":"ひ|こ|まえ|ふる|ふく|せいり"},{"japanese":"話す前に、考えを整理しましょう。","romaji":"Hanasu mae ni, kangae o seiri shimashō.","german":"Let's sort out our thoughts before we speak.","furigana":"はな|まえ|かんが|せいり"}],"notes":"整理する means to put in order systematically, often sorting out what is unnecessary: 書類を整理する, 古い服を整理する. It also works abstractly: 考えを整理する (\"to organize one's thoughts\"). 片付ける is the everyday \"to tidy up, put away\"; the fixed phrase 整理整頓 means \"order and tidiness\".","levelNote":"Derived from 整理 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"水を節約しましょう。","romaji":"Mizu o setsuyaku shimashō.","german":"Let's save water.","furigana":"みず|せつやく"},{"japanese":"時間を節約するために、タクシーで行きました。","romaji":"Jikan o setsuyaku suru tame ni, takushī de ikimashita.","german":"To save time, I went by taxi.","furigana":"じかん|せつやく|い"},{"japanese":"毎日お弁当を作って、食費を節約しています。","romaji":"Mainichi obentō o tsukutte, shokuhi o setsuyaku shite imasu.","german":"I make a bento every day and save on food costs.","furigana":"まいにち|べんとう|つく|しょくひ|せつやく"}],"notes":"節約する means to use something sparingly, i.e. not to waste it: 水・電気・時間・お金を節約する. Putting money aside, in contrast, is 貯金する or お金をためる. The opposite is 無駄遣い (\"waste\").","levelNote":"Derived from 節約 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"台風のため運動会が延期されました。","romaji":"Taifuu no tame undoukai ga enki saremashita.","german":"The sports day was postponed because of the typhoon.","furigana":"たいふう|うんどうかい|えんき"},{"japanese":"会議を来週に延期しましょう。","romaji":"Kaigi o raishū ni enki shimashō.","german":"Let's postpone the meeting until next week.","furigana":"かいぎ|らいしゅう|えんき"}],"notes":"What is postponed takes を, the new date takes に: 会議を来週に延期する. In news reports and announcements the passive is common: 試合が延期されました. Unlike 中止 (\"cancellation\"), the event still takes place later; in conversation you also say 延ばす or ずらす.","levelNote":"Derived from 延期 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"工事が中止されました。","romaji":"Kōji ga chūshi saremashita.","german":"The construction work was halted.","furigana":"こうじ|ちゅうし"},{"japanese":"雨のためイベントを中止しました。","romaji":"Ame no tame ibento o chūshi shimashita.","german":"The event was canceled because of rain.","furigana":"あめ|ちゅうし"}],"notes":"中止する means to cancel something planned entirely or to stop something in progress: イベントを中止する, 工事が中止される. On posters you often see 雨天中止 (\"canceled in case of rain\"). Unlike 延期 (\"postponement\"), the event does not take place; giving up a habit, by contrast, is expressed with やめる.","levelNote":"Derived from 中止 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"建物が完成しました。","romaji":"Tatemono ga kansei shimashita.","german":"The building has been completed.","furigana":"たてもの|かんせい"},{"japanese":"この絵を来月までに完成させたいです。","romaji":"Kono e o raigetsu made ni kansei sasetai desu.","german":"I want to finish this painting by next month.","furigana":"え|らいげつ|かんせい"}],"notes":"完成する is mostly used intransitively: 建物が完成する (\"the building is completed\"). Someone who completes something themselves usually says 完成させる: 作品を完成させる. In everyday speech people often just say できる or できあがる; 完成 sounds like a larger, finished work.","levelNote":"Derived from 完成 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"防犯カメラを設置しました。","romaji":"Bouhan kamera o secchi shimashita.","german":"A security camera was installed.","furigana":"ぼうはん|せっち"},{"japanese":"駅に新しいエレベーターが設置されました。","romaji":"Eki ni atarashii erebētā ga secchi saremashita.","german":"A new elevator was installed at the station.","furigana":"えき|あたら|せっち"}],"notes":"設置する means to set up or install equipment or facilities permanently; the place takes に and the equipment takes を: 入口にカメラを設置する. In notices and news, the passive is common: ～が設置されています. For smaller things, everyday speech prefers つける or 置く; officially, 設置 also means establishing offices or committees.","levelNote":"Derived from 設置 (N1) (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お客様に丁寧に接してください。","romaji":"Okyakusama ni teinei ni sesshite kudasai.","german":"Please treat customers politely.","furigana":"きゃくさま|ていねい|せっ"},{"japanese":"この公園は海に接しています。","romaji":"Kono kōen wa umi ni sesshite imasu.","german":"This park borders the sea.","furigana":"こうえん|うみ|せっ"},{"japanese":"留学して、いろいろな文化に接しました。","romaji":"Ryūgaku shite, iroiro na bunka ni sesshimashita.","german":"While studying abroad, I came into contact with many cultures.","furigana":"りゅうがく|ぶんか|せっ"}],"notes":"接する always takes に. With people, it means \"to deal with or treat someone\": 子どもに優しく接する. With places, it means \"to border on\": 海に接する町; it also means \"to come into contact with something\": 外国の文化に接する. It sounds rather formal; for dealing with customers, everyday speech also uses 対応する.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"夜一人で歩くのは恐いです。","romaji":"Yoru hitori de aruku no wa kowai desu.","german":"Walking alone at night is scary.","furigana":"よるひとり|ある|こわ"},{"japanese":"子どものころ、犬が恐かったです。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, inu ga kowakatta desu.","german":"When I was a child, I was afraid of dogs.","furigana":"こ|いぬ|こわ"},{"japanese":"父は怒ると、とても恐いです。","romaji":"Chichi wa okoru to, totemo kowai desu.","german":"When my father gets angry, he is really scary.","furigana":"ちち|おこ|こわ"}],"notes":"こわい is today usually written 怖い; 恐い is a rarer spelling variant with the same meaning. What one is afraid of takes が: 犬が恐い - this is how you talk about your own feelings; for other people you say 怖がる (\"to be afraid\"). Said of people, it also means \"strict, intimidating\": 恐い先生.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼はコンピュータに詳しいです。","romaji":"Kare wa konpyūta ni kuwashii desu.","german":"He is knowledgeable about computers.","furigana":"かれ|くわ"},{"japanese":"詳しい説明をお願いします。","romaji":"Kuwashii setsumei o onegai shimasu.","german":"Please give a detailed explanation.","furigana":"くわ|せつめい|ねが"},{"japanese":"詳しくはホームページを見てください。","romaji":"Kuwashiku wa hōmupēji o mite kudasai.","german":"For details, please see the website.","furigana":"くわ|み"}],"notes":"詳しい has two usages: before a noun it means \"detailed, thorough\" (詳しい説明), and with に it means \"to be well versed\": 彼は車に詳しい. Adverbially it is 詳しく: 詳しく説明する. The phrase 詳しくは～ (\"for details ...\") often appears in announcements and notices.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 35"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"激しい雨が降っています。","romaji":"Hageshii ame ga futte imasu.","german":"It's raining heavily.","furigana":"はげ|あめ|ふ"},{"japanese":"この業界は競争が激しいです。","romaji":"Kono gyōkai wa kyōsō ga hageshii desu.","german":"Competition is fierce in this industry.","furigana":"ぎょうかい|きょうそう|はげ"}],"notes":"激しい describes a strong or violent intensity, for example of rain, movement or competition. It is an i-adjective: 激しい雨 modifies a noun, 競争が激しい evaluates the competition. The adverbial form is 激しく; here the word does not merely mean that something is big.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この地方では農業が盛んです。","romaji":"Kono chihō dewa nōgyō ga sakan desu.","german":"Agriculture is thriving in this region.","furigana":"ちほう|のうぎょう|さか"},{"japanese":"この地域ではサッカーが盛んです。","romaji":"Kono chiiki de wa sakkā ga sakan desu.","german":"Soccer is very popular in this area.","furigana":"ちいき|さか"},{"japanese":"授業では学生から盛んに質問が出ました。","romaji":"Jugyō de wa gakusei kara sakan ni shitsumon ga demashita.","german":"In class, the students asked questions eagerly.","furigana":"じゅぎょう|がくせい|さか|しつもん|で"}],"notes":"盛ん is a な-adjective and describes an activity flourishing or being widespread in a place: ～では～が盛んです. Typical subjects are industries, sports or exchange: 農業・スポーツ・交流が盛ん. Adverbially, 盛んに means \"eagerly, vigorously, frequently\". For individual people, 元気 or 活発 is rather used.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"市場で新鮮な魚を買いました。","romaji":"Ichiba de shinsen na sakana o kaimashita.","german":"I bought fresh fish at the market.","furigana":"いちば|しんせん|さかな|か"},{"japanese":"外国で生活すると、毎日が新鮮です。","romaji":"Gaikoku de seikatsu suru to, mainichi ga shinsen desu.","german":"When you live abroad, every day feels fresh.","furigana":"がいこく|せいかつ|まいにち|しんせん"}],"notes":"新鮮 is a な-adjective: 新鮮な魚, 新鮮な空気. It refers above all to the freshness of food, but figuratively also to impressions that feel new and stimulating: 毎日が新鮮だ. It does not fit freshly washed laundry or a refreshing feeling; there you say きれい or さわやか.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"台所はいつも清潔にしてください。","romaji":"Daidokoro wa itsumo seiketsu ni shite kudasai.","german":"Please always keep the kitchen clean.","furigana":"だいどころ|せいけつ"},{"japanese":"このホテルは古いですが、とても清潔です。","romaji":"Kono hoteru wa furui desu ga, totemo seiketsu desu.","german":"This hotel is old, but it's very clean.","furigana":"ふる|せいけつ"}],"notes":"清潔 means hygienic cleanliness and is a な-adjective: 清潔なタオル. With にする or に保つ you say \"to keep clean\". The everyday きれい also means \"beautiful\", whereas 清潔 clearly means \"hygienic\"; the opposite is 不潔. 清潔感がある praises a well-groomed appearance.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不思議な夢を見ました。","romaji":"Fushigi na yume o mimashita.","german":"I had a strange dream.","furigana":"ふしぎ|ゆめ|み"},{"japanese":"どうして彼が来なかったのか、不思議に思いました。","romaji":"Dōshite kare ga konakatta no ka, fushigi ni omoimashita.","german":"I wondered why he hadn't come.","furigana":"かれ|こ|ふしぎ|おも"}],"notes":"不思議 here means strange, puzzling or mysterious. As a na-adjective it stands before a noun with な: 不思議な夢. 不思議に思う describes finding something astonishing or wondering about it. The form 不思議と is another adverbial use and is not practiced here as a separate sentence pattern.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"退屈な授業でした。","romaji":"Taikutsu na jugyō deshita.","german":"It was a boring class.","furigana":"たいくつ|じゅぎょう"},{"japanese":"雨で外に出られないので、退屈しています。","romaji":"Ame de soto ni derarenai node, taikutsu shite imasu.","german":"I can't go outside because of the rain, so I'm bored.","furigana":"あめ|そと|で|たいくつ"}],"notes":"退屈 is a な-adjective (\"boring\": 退屈な授業) and at the same time a する-verb: 退屈する (\"to be bored\"), mostly as 退屈している. Colloquially people often say つまらない about boring things; 暇 only means \"having free time\" and is not necessarily negative.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"手続きが面倒です。","romaji":"Tetsuzuki ga mendou desu.","german":"The paperwork is a hassle.","furigana":"てつづ|めんどう"},{"japanese":"姉が子どもの面倒を見てくれました。","romaji":"Ane ga kodomo no mendō o mite kuremashita.","german":"My older sister looked after the children for me.","furigana":"あね|こ|めんどう|み"},{"japanese":"ご面倒をおかけして、すみません。","romaji":"Gomendō o okake shite, sumimasen.","german":"Sorry for the trouble.","furigana":"めんどう"}],"notes":"As a な-adjective, 面倒 means \"troublesome, bothersome\": 面倒な手続き; colloquially people say 面倒くさい or めんどくさい. The fixed phrase ～の面倒を見る means \"to take care of someone\". Politely, you apologize with ご面倒をおかけしました (\"Sorry for the trouble\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"家族と一緒にいると幸せです。","romaji":"Kazoku to issho ni iru to shiawase desu.","german":"I'm happy when I'm with my family.","furigana":"かぞく|いっしょ|しあわ"},{"japanese":"ご結婚おめでとうございます。どうぞお幸せに。","romaji":"Gokekkon omedetō gozaimasu. Dōzo oshiawase ni.","german":"Congratulations on your marriage! I wish you all the happiness.","furigana":"けっこん|しあわ"},{"japanese":"祖母は幸せな人生を送りました。","romaji":"Sobo wa shiawase na jinsei o okurimashita.","german":"My grandmother lived a happy life.","furigana":"そぼ|しあわ|じんせい|おく"}],"notes":"幸せ is a noun and a な-adjective: 幸せな家庭, 幸せを感じる. It means deeper, lasting happiness in life, whereas 嬉しい expresses joy over a specific occasion. At a wedding one says お幸せに; the more formal synonym is 幸福 (こうふく).","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 13"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不幸な事故がありました。","romaji":"Fukou na jiko ga arimashita.","german":"There was an unfortunate accident.","furigana":"ふこう|じこ"},{"japanese":"先週、親戚に不幸がありました。","romaji":"Senshū, shinseki ni fukō ga arimashita.","german":"Last week there was a death in the family of a relative.","furigana":"せんしゅう|しんせき|ふこう"},{"japanese":"けがが軽かったのは不幸中の幸いでした。","romaji":"Kega ga karukatta no wa fukōchū no saiwai deshita.","german":"It was a blessing in disguise that the injury was minor.","furigana":"かる|ふこうちゅう|さいわ"}],"notes":"不幸 is a noun and a な-adjective: 不幸な事故 (\"tragic accident\"). The phrase ～に不幸があった is a polite euphemism for a death in the family. 不幸中の幸い means \"a blessing in disguise\" (\"luck within misfortune\"). For \"I am unhappy\", people in everyday speech rather say 幸せじゃない or 悲しい.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"料理が得意です。","romaji":"Ryouri ga tokui desu.","german":"I'm good at cooking.","furigana":"りょうり|とくい"},{"japanese":"得意な科目は何ですか。","romaji":"Tokui na kamoku wa nan desu ka.","german":"What is your best subject?","furigana":"とくい|かもく|なん"},{"japanese":"弟はテストで百点を取って、得意そうな顔をしています。","romaji":"Otōto wa tesuto de hyakuten o totte, tokuisō na kao o shite imasu.","german":"My little brother got 100 points on the test and looks proud.","furigana":"おとうと|ひゃくてん|と|とくい|かお"}],"notes":"What one is good at takes が: 料理が得意です; before nouns: 得意な科目. Unlike 上手, 得意 can be said about oneself without boasting, since it means more \"my strong point\". The opposite is 苦手. 得意そう or 得意げ means \"proud, triumphant\", and お得意様 are regular customers.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"数学が苦手です。","romaji":"Suugaku ga nigate desu.","german":"I'm bad at math.","furigana":"すうがく|にがて"},{"japanese":"辛い食べ物はちょっと苦手です。","romaji":"Karai tabemono wa chotto nigate desu.","german":"I'm not very fond of spicy food.","furigana":"から|た|もの|にがて"},{"japanese":"人前で話すのが苦手です。","romaji":"Hitomae de hanasu no ga nigate desu.","german":"I'm not good at speaking in front of people.","furigana":"ひとまえ|はな|にがて"}],"notes":"What one finds difficult or doesn't suit one takes が: 数学が苦手です; activities are embedded with の: 人前で話すのが苦手. 苦手 is also a soft, polite way of saying you don't like something or someone - gentler than 嫌い. 下手, in contrast, describes objectively poor performance, including that of others; the opposite of 苦手 is 得意.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地味な服が好きです。","romaji":"Jimi na fuku ga suki desu.","german":"I like plain clothes.","furigana":"じみ|ふく|す"},{"japanese":"彼は地味ですが、まじめに働く人です。","romaji":"Kare wa jimi desu ga, majime ni hataraku hito desu.","german":"He is rather unassuming, but he works conscientiously.","furigana":"かれ|じみ|はたら|ひと"}],"notes":"地味 is the opposite of 派手 and describes colors, clothing or people as plain and restrained: 地味な色, 地味な人. Depending on the context it sounds neutral (\"understated\") or slightly negative (\"drab, dull\"). 地味な仕事 is unspectacular but often important work.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女の服装はいつも派手です。","romaji":"Kanojo no fukusou wa itsumo hade desu.","german":"Her clothes are always flashy.","furigana":"かのじょ|ふくそう|はで"},{"japanese":"結婚式は派手にしないで、家族だけでしました。","romaji":"Kekkonshiki wa hade ni shinaide, kazoku dake de shimashita.","german":"We didn't have a lavish wedding; it was just family.","furigana":"けっこんしき|はで|かぞく"}],"notes":"派手 is a な-adjective for anything loud, eye-catching or elaborate: 派手な色, 派手な服, 派手なパーティー. Adverbially, 派手に means \"on a large scale, lavishly\": 派手にお金を使う. The opposite is 地味. It can be meant admiringly or critically (\"gaudy\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"贅沢な食事をしました。","romaji":"Zeitaku na shokuji o shimashita.","german":"We had a luxurious meal.","furigana":"ぜいたく|しょくじ"},{"japanese":"給料が出たので、たまには贅沢しましょう。","romaji":"Kyūryō ga deta node, tama ni wa zeitaku shimashō.","german":"Payday has come, so let's treat ourselves for once.","furigana":"きゅうりょう|で|ぜいたく"},{"japanese":"仕事があるだけいいです。贅沢は言えません。","romaji":"Shigoto ga aru dake ii desu. Zeitaku wa iemasen.","german":"I'm lucky just to have a job. I can't be picky.","furigana":"しごと|ぜいたく|い"}],"notes":"贅沢 is a noun, a な-adjective and a する-verb: 贅沢な食事, 贅沢する (\"to treat oneself, to live in luxury\"). Depending on the context it sounds positive (\"luxury\") or reproachful (\"waste\"). 贅沢を言う means \"to make too high demands, to be picky\". Because of the difficult kanji it is often written ぜいたく.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"まるで夢のようです。","romaji":"Marude yume no yō desu.","german":"It's just like a dream.","furigana":"ゆめ"},{"japanese":"彼はまるで日本人のように日本語を話します。","romaji":"Kare wa marude Nihonjin no yō ni Nihongo o hanashimasu.","german":"He speaks Japanese as if he were Japanese.","furigana":"かれ|にほんじん|にほんご|はな"}],"notes":"まるで strengthens a comparison: \"just like\" or \"as if\". It is often followed by ようだ, ように or みたいだ: まるで夢のようです. Something seems like the thing compared, without necessarily being the same. In negative sentences まるで can also mean \"not at all\"; the examples on this card practice the comparison.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"たまに映画を見ます。","romaji":"Tamani eiga o mimasu.","german":"I watch movies once in a while.","furigana":"えいが|み"},{"japanese":"たまには休んだほうがいいですよ。","romaji":"Tamani wa yasunda hō ga ii desu yo.","german":"You should take a rest once in a while, too.","furigana":"やす"}],"notes":"たまに means \"occasionally, now and then\" and occurs less often than 時々. With は (たまには) it sounds like \"for once, for a change\" and often appears in suggestions: たまには休もう. Before nouns, you say たまの: たまの休み (\"the rare day off\"). It is almost always written in kana.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 49"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"めったに外食しません。","romaji":"Mettani gaishoku shimasen.","german":"I rarely eat out.","furigana":"がいしょく"},{"japanese":"彼はめったに怒りません。","romaji":"Kare wa mettani okorimasen.","german":"He rarely gets angry.","furigana":"かれ|おこ"},{"japanese":"こんなチャンスはめったにありません。","romaji":"Konna chansu wa mettani arimasen.","german":"You rarely get a chance like this."}],"notes":"めったに always appears with a negative: めったに～ない (\"hardly ever, rarely\"). It is stronger than あまり～ない. めったにない means \"rare, extraordinary\": めったにないチャンス. The kanji spelling 滅多に exists, but kana is more usual.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"せっかく来たのに閉まっていました。","romaji":"Sekkaku kita no ni shimatte imashita.","german":"I went all that way, but it was closed.","furigana":"き|し"},{"japanese":"せっかく京都に来たから、お寺を見に行きましょう。","romaji":"Sekkaku Kyōto ni kita kara, otera o mi ni ikimashō.","german":"Since we've come all the way to Kyoto, let's go see some temples.","furigana":"きょうと|き|てら|み|い"},{"japanese":"せっかくの休みなのに、ずっと雨でした。","romaji":"Sekkaku no yasumi na no ni, zutto ame deshita.","german":"It was a rare day off, and yet it rained the whole time.","furigana":"やす|あめ"}],"notes":"せっかく expresses that something took effort or is a valuable opportunity. With ～のに, disappointment follows (せっかく来たのに閉まっていた); with ～から or ～だから, it expresses the wish to make the most of the opportunity. Before nouns, せっかくの is used: せっかくの休み. You can politely decline with せっかくですが…; it is usually written in kana (rarely 折角).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"たとえ雨でも行きます。","romaji":"Tatoe ame demo ikimasu.","german":"Even if it rains, I'll go.","furigana":"あめ|い"},{"japanese":"たとえ難しくても頑張ります。","romaji":"Tatoe muzukashikutemo ganbarimasu.","german":"Even if it's difficult, I'll do my best.","furigana":"むずか|がんば"},{"japanese":"たとえお金がなくても、家族がいれば幸せです。","romaji":"Tatoe okane ga nakutemo, kazoku ga ireba shiawase desu.","german":"Even if I have no money, I'm happy as long as I have my family.","furigana":"かね|かぞく|しあわ"}],"notes":"たとえ reinforces a concessive clause and always appears together with ～ても or ～でも: たとえ雨でも, たとえ難しくても. On its own it makes no sense; if you leave it out, the sentence stays grammatical, only less emphatic. Do not confuse it with 例えば (\"for example\") or the noun 例え (\"comparison, analogy\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一応確認しておきます。","romaji":"Ichiou kakunin shite okimasu.","german":"I will check just to be sure.","furigana":"いちおうかくにん"},{"japanese":"雨が降るかもしれないので、一応傘を持っていきます。","romaji":"Ame ga furu kamo shirenai node, ichiō kasa o motte ikimasu.","german":"It might rain, so I'll take an umbrella just in case.","furigana":"あめ|ふ|いちおうかさ|も"},{"japanese":"レポートは一応書きましたが、まだ自信がありません。","romaji":"Repōto wa ichiō kakimashita ga, mada jishin ga arimasen.","german":"I have written the report, more or less, but I'm still not confident.","furigana":"いちおうか|じしん"}],"notes":"一応 has two main meanings: \"just in case, to be on the safe side\" (一応確認しておきます) and \"more or less, so far\" - you have done something but are not entirely satisfied. It is often combined with ～ておく. It sounds restrained and modest, and is very common both in daily life and at the office.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"まず自己紹介をします。","romaji":"Mazu jiko shōkai o shimasu.","german":"First, I'll introduce myself.","furigana":"じこしょうかい"},{"japanese":"まず手を洗いましょう。","romaji":"Mazu te o araimashō.","german":"First, let's wash our hands.","furigana":"て|あら"},{"japanese":"まずこのボタンを押して、次に番号を入れてください。","romaji":"Mazu kono botan o oshite, tsugi ni bangō o irete kudasai.","german":"First press this button, then enter the number.","furigana":"お|つぎ|ばんごう|い"}],"notes":"まず introduces the first step and is often continued with 次に or それから: まず～、次に～. 最初に is similar but emphasizes the chronological order more. まず also means \"most likely\", usually with a supposition or negation: まず大丈夫でしょう. It is almost always written in kana (rarely 先ず).","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 16"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"さっき電話がありました。","romaji":"Sakki denwa ga arimashita.","german":"There was a phone call a little while ago.","furigana":"でんわ"},{"japanese":"さっきの話をもう少し詳しく聞かせてください。","romaji":"Sakki no hanashi o mō sukoshi kuwashiku kikasete kudasai.","german":"Please tell me a bit more about what we were just talking about.","furigana":"はなし|すこ|くわ|き"}],"notes":"さっき refers, from the moment of speaking, to the short time before. It is common in conversation and means \"a little while ago\" or \"just now\", without naming a fixed number of minutes. With の it can modify a noun: さっきの話 is the conversation or topic from just now.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"相変わらず元気ですね。","romaji":"Aikawarazu genki desu ne.","german":"You're as energetic as ever.","furigana":"あいか|げんき"},{"japanese":"この店は相変わらず混んでいますね。","romaji":"Kono mise wa aikawarazu konde imasu ne.","german":"This shop is crowded as always.","furigana":"みせ|あいか|こ"}],"notes":"相変わらず says that a quality or state is unchanged from before. This can be friendly, as in 相変わらず元気, or denote a lasting burden, such as a shop that is still crowded. The expression does not automatically mean \"always\" on every occasion, but compares the present state with a known earlier one.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"何しろ初めてですから。","romaji":"Nanishiro hajimete desu kara.","german":"After all, it's my first time.","furigana":"なに|はじ"},{"japanese":"昨日のお祭りは、何しろ人が多くて大変でした。","romaji":"Kinō no omatsuri wa, nanishiro hito ga ōkute taihen deshita.","german":"The festival yesterday was exhausting - there were simply a huge number of people.","furigana":"きのう|まつ|なに|ひと|おお|たいへん"}],"notes":"何しろ stresses a reason that explains everything that follows - similar to \"after all, simply, the fact is\". It is often followed by ～から, ～ので or a て-form: 何しろ忙しくて…. It is similar to とにかく, but typically introduces a justification. It is also often written in kana: なにしろ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コーヒーですか、それとも紅茶ですか。","romaji":"Kōhī desu ka, soretomo kōcha desu ka.","german":"Kaffee oder Tee?","furigana":"こうちゃ"},{"japanese":"電車で行きますか、それともバスで行きますか。","romaji":"Densha de ikimasu ka, soretomo basu de ikimasu ka.","german":"Fahren Sie mit dem Zug oder mit dem Bus?","furigana":"でんしゃ|い|い"}],"notes":"それとも verbindet in einer Frage zwei Möglichkeiten: „oder alternativ“. Man kann beide als Fragen aussprechen, etwa コーヒーですか、それとも紅茶ですか. Die Wendung ist eine Konjunktion; sie ersetzt nicht jedes deutsche „oder“, etwa bei einer bloßen Aufzählung ohne Entscheidungsfrage.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"つまり無理ということですか。","romaji":"Tsumari muri to iu koto desu ka.","german":"So, you mean it's impossible?","furigana":"むり"},{"japanese":"母の姉、つまり私の伯母は京都に住んでいます。","romaji":"Haha no ane, tsumari watashi no oba wa Kyōto ni sunde imasu.","german":"My mother's older sister, that is, my aunt, lives in Kyoto.","furigana":"はは|あね|わたし|おば|きょうと|す"}],"notes":"つまり sums something up or rephrases it more clearly: \"that is\" or \"in other words\". You can also use it to check whether you have understood a conclusion correctly. In 母の姉、つまり私の伯母 the second part names the same person from a different perspective; the card does not cover the noun for a blockage.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ようやく電車が来ました。","romaji":"Youyaku densha ga kimashita.","german":"The train finally came.","furigana":"でんしゃ|き"},{"japanese":"先生の説明を聞いて、ようやくわかりました。","romaji":"Sensei no setsumei o kiite, yōyaku wakarimashita.","german":"After listening to the teacher's explanation, I finally understood.","furigana":"せんせい|せつめい|き"},{"japanese":"ようやく暖かくなってきました。","romaji":"Yōyaku atatakaku natte kimashita.","german":"It has finally started to get warm.","furigana":"あたた"}],"notes":"ようやく expresses that something finally happens after a long wait or effort: ようやく電車が来た. It is nearly synonymous with やっと, but sounds a little more matter-of-fact; やっと stresses the relief more. With ～てきた, it describes a gradual change. It is usually written in kana (rarely 漸く).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"たった一人で暮らしています。","romaji":"Tatta hitori de kurashite imasu.","german":"I live all by myself.","furigana":"ひとり|く"},{"japanese":"たった五分で宿題が終わりました。","romaji":"Tatta gofun de shukudai ga owarimashita.","german":"I finished my homework in just five minutes.","furigana":"ごふん|しゅくだい|お"},{"japanese":"たった今、家に着きました。","romaji":"Tatta ima, ie ni tsukimashita.","german":"I just got home.","furigana":"いま|いえ|つ"}],"notes":"たった stands before numbers and amounts and stresses how little it is: たった五分, たった千円. It is often reinforced with だけ or しか～ない: たった千円しかない. たった今 means \"just now, a moment ago\". It is more colloquial and more emotional than ただ or わずか.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"なんとか間に合いました。","romaji":"Nantoka maniaimashita.","german":"I somehow made it in time.","furigana":"ま|あ"},{"japanese":"お金はあまりないけど、なんとかなるでしょう。","romaji":"Okane wa amari nai kedo, nantoka naru deshō.","german":"We don't have much money, but we'll manage somehow.","furigana":"かね"},{"japanese":"山田なんとかさんという人から電話がありましたよ。","romaji":"Yamada nantoka-san to iu hito kara denwa ga arimashita yo.","german":"A person named Yamada something called.","furigana":"やまだ|ひと|でんわ"}],"notes":"なんとか means \"somehow (with effort)\": you cope with something despite difficulties. なんとか間に合う means \"to just manage to make it in time\", なんとかなる \"it will work out somehow\", なんとかする \"to do something about it, to find a solution\". なんとか also serves as a placeholder for something you can't think of at the moment: なんとかさん \"Mr./Ms. So-and-so\", 山田なんとかという人 \"a certain Yamada So-and-so\". Mostly written in kana; 何とか is the kanji spelling.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"さらに詳しく教えてください。","romaji":"Sarani kuwashiku oshiete kudasai.","german":"Please explain in even more detail.","furigana":"くわ|おし"},{"japanese":"夜になって、雨がさらに強くなりました。","romaji":"Yoru ni natte, ame ga sarani tsuyoku narimashita.","german":"As evening came, the rain got even stronger.","furigana":"よる|あめ|つよ"},{"japanese":"この店は安いです。さらに、駅からも近いです。","romaji":"Kono mise wa yasui desu. Sarani, eki kara mo chikai desu.","german":"This shop is cheap. Moreover, it's close to the station.","furigana":"みせ|やす|えき|ちか"}],"notes":"さらに intensifies an increase: \"even (more), still further\", often before adjectives or verbs of change: さらに強くなる, さらに詳しく. At the start of a sentence it works as a conjunction, \"moreover, in addition\", and sounds somewhat more formal than それに. It is mostly written in kana (rarely 更に).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今年の目標を決めました。","romaji":"Kotoshi no mokuhyou o kimemashita.","german":"I decided on my goal for this year.","furigana":"ことし|もくひょう|き"},{"japanese":"チームの目標まで、あと少しです。","romaji":"Chīmu no mokuhyō made, ato sukoshi desu.","german":"We're almost at our team's target.","furigana":"もくひょう|すこ"}],"notes":"目標 is a concrete, often measurable goal: 目標を立てる／決める, 目標を達成する (\"to achieve\"). 目的, in contrast, denotes the purpose or intention behind an action (旅行の目的). A travel destination is called 目的地, the goal or finish line in sports ゴール.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"努力すれば成功します。","romaji":"Doryoku sureba seikou shimasu.","german":"If you make an effort, you will succeed.","furigana":"どりょく|せいこう"},{"japanese":"彼の努力のおかげで、試合に勝ちました。","romaji":"Kare no doryoku no okage de, shiai ni kachimashita.","german":"Thanks to his efforts, we won the match.","furigana":"かれ|どりょく|しあい|か"}],"notes":"努力 is a noun and a する-verb: 努力する (\"to make an effort, to strive\"). Typical phrases are 努力が足りない, 努力が実る (\"the effort bears fruit\") and ～の努力のおかげで. A hard-working person is called 努力家. In conversation people often say 頑張る; 努力 sounds more formal and suits applications and speeches.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"希望を持ちましょう。","romaji":"Kibō o mochimashō.","german":"Let's keep hope.","furigana":"きぼう|も"},{"japanese":"ご希望の日にちを教えてください。","romaji":"Gokibō no hinichi o oshiete kudasai.","german":"Please tell us your preferred date.","furigana":"きぼう|ひ|おし"},{"japanese":"第一希望の大学に合格しました。","romaji":"Daiichi kibō no daigaku ni gōkaku shimashita.","german":"I passed the entrance exam at my first-choice university.","furigana":"だいいちきぼう|だいがく|ごうかく"}],"notes":"希望 means both \"hope\" (希望を持つ) and \"wish\" in formal contexts: ご希望の日時, 第一希望 (\"first choice\"). As a する-verb, the thing wished for takes を: 進学を希望する. For personal wishes in everyday life, ～たい or 願い is rather used.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 30"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いい案がありますか。","romaji":"Ii an ga arimasu ka.","german":"Do you have a good idea?","furigana":"あん"},{"japanese":"会議で私の案が採用されました。","romaji":"Kaigi de watashi no an ga saiyō saremashita.","german":"My proposal was adopted at the meeting.","furigana":"かいぎ|わたし|あん|さいよう"}],"notes":"案 is an idea, plan or proposal that can still be considered or worked out. 案がある means that someone has a proposal; 案を採用する means to adopt it. In compounds such as 改善案, 案 at the end stands for a proposal for improvement. The examples deal with a proposal, not the older meaning \"desk\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"メールの内容を確認しました。","romaji":"Meeru no naiyou o kakunin shimashita.","german":"I checked the content of the email.","furigana":"ないよう|かくにん"},{"japanese":"この本は内容が少し難しいです。","romaji":"Kono hon wa naiyō ga sukoshi muzukashii desu.","german":"The content of this book is a bit difficult.","furigana":"ほん|ないよう|すこ|むずか"}],"notes":"内容 is the substance of texts, conversations, courses or contracts: メールの内容, 仕事の内容. The physical contents of a container, in contrast, are called 中身 (なかみ): 箱の中身. Common combinations are 内容を確認する and 内容がわかる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"順番に並んでください。","romaji":"Junban ni narande kudasai.","german":"Please line up in order.","furigana":"じゅんばん|なら"},{"japanese":"次はあなたの順番です。","romaji":"Tsugi wa anata no junban desu.","german":"Next is your turn.","furigana":"つぎ|じゅんばん"}],"notes":"順番 denotes a fixed order or one's own place in it. 順番に means \"in order\"; あなたの順番です says that it is now your turn. It can be used when queuing, for tasks or in a game. With の, the person acting is assigned their respective turn.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"女性の割合が増えています。","romaji":"Josei no wariai ga fuete imasu.","german":"The proportion of women is increasing.","furigana":"じょせい|わりあい|ふ"},{"japanese":"水と砂糖を三対一の割合で混ぜます。","romaji":"Mizu to satō o san tai ichi no wariai de mazemasu.","german":"Mix water and sugar in a ratio of three to one.","furigana":"みず|さとう|さんたいいち|わりあい|ま"},{"japanese":"今日のテストは割合簡単でした。","romaji":"Kyō no tesuto wa wariai kantan deshita.","german":"Today's test was relatively easy.","furigana":"きょう|わりあいかんたん"}],"notes":"割合 is a proportion or ratio: 女性の割合, 三対一の割合で混ぜる. As an adverb, 割合（に） means \"relatively, comparatively\", similar to わりと or 比較的. Percentages are given with パーセント: 割合は三十パーセントです.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ある程度理解できます。","romaji":"Aru teido rikai dekimasu.","german":"I can understand to a certain extent.","furigana":"ていどりかい"},{"japanese":"一時間程度かかります。","romaji":"Ichijikan teido kakarimasu.","german":"It takes about an hour.","furigana":"いちじかんていど"},{"japanese":"けがはどの程度ですか。","romaji":"Kega wa dono teido desu ka.","german":"How serious is the injury?","furigana":"ていど"}],"notes":"程度 expresses extent or degree: けがの程度, どの程度 (\"how much, how far\"). ある程度 means \"to a certain degree\". After numbers it means \"about, approximately\" and sounds more formal than ぐらい: 一時間程度.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この料理の量は多いです。","romaji":"Kono ryouri no ryou wa ooi desu.","german":"The portion of this dish is big.","furigana":"りょうり|りょう|おお"},{"japanese":"今年は雨の量が少ないです。","romaji":"Kotoshi wa ame no ryō ga sukunai desu.","german":"There is little rainfall this year.","furigana":"ことし|あめ|りょう|すく"},{"japanese":"量より質が大切です。","romaji":"Ryō yori shitsu ga taisetsu desu.","german":"Quality is more important than quantity.","furigana":"りょう|しつ|たいせつ"}],"notes":"量 means a quantity that is measured rather than counted: 雨の量, 料理の量 (portion). For countable things you say 数 (かず). Typical are 量が多い／少ない and the saying 量より質 (\"quality over quantity\"). As a suffix it appears in words such as 雨量 or 運動量.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 44"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この建物の高さは何メートルですか。","romaji":"Kono tatemono no takasa wa nan mētoru desu ka.","german":"How tall is this building?","furigana":"たてもの|たか|なん"},{"japanese":"この机は高さを変えることができます。","romaji":"Kono tsukue wa takasa o kaeru koto ga dekimasu.","german":"The height of this desk can be adjusted.","furigana":"つくえ|たか|か"}],"notes":"高さ is the noun for 高い and means the height of buildings, mountains or furniture, but also pitch: 声の高さ. By the same pattern you form 長さ, 重さ, 大きさ. A person's body height, by contrast, is 身長 (しんちょう). You ask with 高さは何メートルですか or どのくらいですか.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 40"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"学生の数が増えました。","romaji":"Gakusei no kazu ga fuemashita.","german":"The number of students has increased.","furigana":"がくせい|かず|ふ"},{"japanese":"箱の中のりんごの数を数えてください。","romaji":"Hako no naka no ringo no kazu o kazoete kudasai.","german":"Please count how many apples are in the box.","furigana":"はこ|なか|かず|かぞ"}],"notes":"数 (かず) is the number of countable things: 学生の数, りんごの数. To count is 数を数える (かぞえる). For uncountable amounts such as water, 量 is used. As a prefix it is read すう: 数人 (\"several people\"); the number of people is usually 人数 (にんずう).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"去年の二倍になりました。","romaji":"Kyonen no nibai ni narimashita.","german":"It has doubled compared to last year.","furigana":"きょねん|にばい"},{"japanese":"東京の家賃は私の町の三倍ぐらいです。","romaji":"Tōkyō no yachin wa watashi no machi no sanbai gurai desu.","german":"Rent in Tokyo is about three times that of my town.","furigana":"とうきょう|やちん|わたし|まち|さんばい"},{"japanese":"試験の前なので、勉強の時間を倍にしました。","romaji":"Shiken no mae na node, benkyō no jikan o bai ni shimashita.","german":"Because the exam is coming up, I doubled my study time.","furigana":"しけん|まえ|べんきょう|じかん|ばい"}],"notes":"On its own, 倍 means \"double\": 倍になる (\"to double\"), 倍にする (\"to double something\"). With a number before it, it is a counter for \"-fold\": 二倍, 三倍, 十倍. Comparisons are formed with AはBの三倍です (\"A is three times B\"); 倍 and 二倍 both mean \"double\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"半分ください。","romaji":"Hanbun kudasai.","german":"Please give me half.","furigana":"はんぶん"},{"japanese":"ケーキの半分をあげます。","romaji":"Kēki no hanbun o agemasu.","german":"I'll give you half of the cake.","furigana":"はんぶん"}],"notes":"半分 is half of a whole. ケーキの半分 uses の to link the half and the whole it refers to. 半分ください asks for half; 半分をあげます announces giving it to someone. In hanbun the n is kept before b.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 42"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"全体の計画を見せてください。","romaji":"Zentai no keikaku o misete kudasai.","german":"Please show me the overall plan.","furigana":"ぜんたい|けいかく|み"},{"japanese":"クラス全体で京都へ旅行に行きました。","romaji":"Kurasu zentai de Kyōto e ryokō ni ikimashita.","german":"The whole class went on a trip to Kyoto.","furigana":"ぜんたい|きょうと|りょこう|い"},{"japanese":"雪が降って、町全体が白くなりました。","romaji":"Yuki ga futte, machi zentai ga shiroku narimashita.","german":"It snowed, and the whole town turned white.","furigana":"ゆき|ふ|まちぜんたい|しろ"}],"notes":"全体 regards something as a connected whole, whereas 全部 enumerates all the individual parts (\"everything\"). Placed after a noun, it forms \"the whole ...\": クラス全体, 町全体. Attributively it takes の (全体の計画), and adverbially it often appears as 全体的に (\"overall\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この部分が分かりません。","romaji":"Kono bubun ga wakarimasen.","german":"I don't understand this part.","furigana":"ぶぶん|わ"},{"japanese":"映画の最後の部分がとてもよかったです。","romaji":"Eiga no saigo no bubun ga totemo yokatta desu.","german":"The last part of the movie was very good.","furigana":"えいが|さいご|ぶぶん"},{"japanese":"椅子の壊れた部分を直しました。","romaji":"Isu no kowareta bubun o naoshimashita.","german":"I repaired the broken part of the chair.","furigana":"いす|こわ|ぶぶん|なお"}],"notes":"部分 denotes a particular section or spot within a whole, such as a text, a film or an object: この部分, 最後の部分. For \"a part of it, some\", 一部 is used more often: 一部の人. The adverb 部分的に means \"partially\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"残りの仕事は明日します。","romaji":"Nokori no shigoto wa ashita shimasu.","german":"I'll do the rest of the work tomorrow.","furigana":"のこ|しごと|あした"},{"japanese":"ケーキの残りは冷蔵庫に入れておきました。","romaji":"Kēki no nokori wa reizōko ni irete okimashita.","german":"I put the rest of the cake in the refrigerator.","furigana":"のこ|れいぞうこ|い"},{"japanese":"夏休みも残り三日です。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi mo nokori mikka desu.","german":"There are only three days left of summer vacation.","furigana":"なつやす|のこ|みっか"}],"notes":"残り is the noun of 残る (\"to be left over\") and denotes the rest. Attributively it takes の: 残りの仕事, 残りのお金. With a quantity it means \"... left\": 残り三日 (\"three days left\"). Leftover food is also called 残り物.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仕方がありません。","romaji":"Shikata ga arimasen.","german":"It can't be helped.","furigana":"しかた"},{"japanese":"挨拶の仕方は国によって違います。","romaji":"Aisatsu no shikata wa kuni ni yotte chigaimasu.","german":"How people greet each other differs from country to country.","furigana":"あいさつ|しかた|くに|ちが"},{"japanese":"雨でピクニックが中止になった。仕方ないね。","romaji":"Ame de pikunikku ga chūshi ni natta. Shikata nai ne.","german":"The picnic was canceled because of the rain. Oh well, it can't be helped.","furigana":"あめ|ちゅうし|しかた"}],"notes":"N + の仕方 means \"the way one does N\", especially with する-nouns: 勉強の仕方, 挨拶の仕方. With ordinary verbs, you use the verb stem + 方 instead: 使い方, 書き方. 仕方 is most often met in the fixed phrase 仕方がない / 仕方がありません (\"there's nothing to be done\"), colloquially shortened to 仕方ない or しょうがない.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"やり方を教えてください。","romaji":"Yarikata o oshiete kudasai.","german":"Please show me how to do it.","furigana":"かた|おし"},{"japanese":"彼のやり方には賛成できません。","romaji":"Kare no yarikata ni wa sansei dekimasen.","german":"I can't agree with his way of doing things.","furigana":"かれ|かた|さんせい"},{"japanese":"やり方を変えたら、仕事が早く終わりました。","romaji":"Yarikata o kaetara, shigoto ga hayaku owarimashita.","german":"When I changed my approach, I finished the work faster.","furigana":"かた|か|しごと|はや|お"}],"notes":"やり方 comes from やる, the casual variant of する, and is very common in everyday speech: やり方を教えてください. It can also refer to a person's personal style or approach, often with a critical undertone: あの人のやり方. Somewhat more formal is 方法 (\"method\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このアプリの使い方がわかりません。","romaji":"Kono apuri no tsukaikata ga wakarimasen.","german":"I don't understand how to use this app.","furigana":"つか|かた"},{"japanese":"お金の使い方に気をつけてください。","romaji":"Okane no tsukaikata ni ki o tsukete kudasai.","german":"Please be careful about how you spend your money.","furigana":"かね|つか|かた|き"},{"japanese":"この言葉の使い方は正しいですか。","romaji":"Kono kotoba no tsukaikata wa tadashii desu ka.","german":"Is this word used correctly?","furigana":"ことば|つか|かた|ただ"}],"notes":"使い方 (\"how to use something\") applies to devices and apps, but also to money, time and words: お金の使い方, 時間の使い方, 言葉の使い方. The object comes before it with の: このアプリの使い方. An instruction manual is a 説明書.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この漢字の読み方を教えてください。","romaji":"Kono kanji no yomikata o oshiete kudasai.","german":"Please tell me how to read this kanji.","furigana":"かんじ|よ|かた|おし"},{"japanese":"この地名の読み方はとても難しいです。","romaji":"Kono chimei no yomikata wa totemo muzukashii desu.","german":"The reading of this place name is very difficult.","furigana":"ちめい|よ|かた|むずか"},{"japanese":"同じ文章でも、読み方で印象が変わります。","romaji":"Onaji bunshō demo, yomikata de inshō ga kawarimasu.","german":"Even with the same text, the way it is read aloud changes the impression.","furigana":"おな|ぶんしょう|よ|かた|いんしょう|か"}],"notes":"読み方 is the way one reads something. With a kanji or place name, the word asks for the reading or pronunciation. With a whole text it can also denote the manner of reading aloud. It is formed from 読み, the verb stem of 読む, and 方, here かた \"way, manner\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 14"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"手紙の書き方を習います。","romaji":"Tegami no kakikata o naraimasu.","german":"Ich lerne, wie man Briefe schreibt.","furigana":"てがみ|か|かた|なら"},{"japanese":"この漢字の書き方を教えてください。","romaji":"Kono kanji no kakikata o oshiete kudasai.","german":"Bitte zeigen Sie mir, wie man dieses Kanji schreibt.","furigana":"かんじ|か|かた|おし"},{"japanese":"住所の書き方は日本とドイツで違います。","romaji":"Jūsho no kakikata wa Nihon to Doitsu de chigaimasu.","german":"Adressen schreibt man in Japan anders als in Deutschland.","furigana":"じゅうしょ|か|かた|にほん|ちが"}],"notes":"書き方 ist die Art, etwas zu schreiben: die Form eines Briefs (手紙の書き方), wie ein Kanji geschrieben wird (漢字の書き方) oder das Format eines Dokuments. Wie bei allen Wörtern auf Verbstamm + 方 steht das Objekt mit の davor. Für die Strichreihenfolge im engeren Sinn gibt es auch 書き順.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"味噌汁の作り方を教えてください。","romaji":"Misoshiru no tsukurikata o oshiete kudasai.","german":"Please tell me how to make miso soup.","furigana":"みそしる|つく|かた|おし"},{"japanese":"箱の後ろに作り方が書いてあります。","romaji":"Hako no ushiro ni tsukurikata ga kaite arimasu.","german":"The instructions are written on the back of the box.","furigana":"はこ|うし|つく|かた|か"},{"japanese":"子どもに紙飛行機の作り方を教えました。","romaji":"Kodomo ni kamihikōki no tsukurikata o oshiemashita.","german":"I taught the children how to make a paper airplane.","furigana":"こ|かみひこうき|つく|かた|おし"}],"notes":"作り方 is the way of making something; in cooking it corresponds to \"preparation\" or \"recipe\": カレーの作り方. On packaging, 作り方 often appears as a heading above the preparation instructions. It applies just as well to crafting and building: 紙飛行機の作り方.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"駅への行き方を教えてください。","romaji":"Eki e no ikikata o oshiete kudasai.","german":"Please tell me how to get to the station.","furigana":"えき|い|かた|おし"},{"japanese":"空港までの行き方をインターネットで調べました。","romaji":"Kūkō made no ikikata o intānetto de shirabemashita.","german":"I looked up on the internet how to get to the airport.","furigana":"くうこう|い|かた|しら"}],"notes":"行き方 here asks by which route one gets to a destination. The destination often precedes the noun with への or までの: 駅への行き方. 行き is the verb stem of 行く, and 方 in this compound is read かた, \"way, manner\". The card reads the word いきかた and deals with the concrete route, not the general procedure.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人によって考え方が違います。","romaji":"Hito ni yotte kangaekata ga chigaimasu.","german":"The way of thinking differs from person to person.","furigana":"ひと|かんが|かた|ちが"},{"japanese":"父は考え方が少し古いです。","romaji":"Chichi wa kangaekata ga sukoshi furui desu.","german":"My father's way of thinking is a bit old-fashioned.","furigana":"ちち|かんが|かた|すこ|ふる"},{"japanese":"考え方を変えたら、問題が小さく見えました。","romaji":"Kangaekata o kaetara, mondai ga chiisaku miemashita.","german":"When I changed my way of thinking, the problem seemed smaller.","furigana":"かんが|かた|か|もんだい|ちい|み"}],"notes":"考え方 means a person's way of thinking or attitude, but also the approach to a problem. Typical are 考え方が違う, 考え方が古い and 考え方を変える. 考え without 方, by contrast, is a single thought or idea: いい考えがあります.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自分の生き方を選びます。","romaji":"Jibun no ikikata o erabimasu.","german":"I choose my own way of life.","furigana":"じぶん|い|かた|えら"},{"japanese":"祖父の生き方を尊敬しています。","romaji":"Sofu no ikikata o sonkei shite imasu.","german":"I respect my grandfather's way of life.","furigana":"そふ|い|かた|そんけい"},{"japanese":"仕事をやめて、田舎で新しい生き方を始めました。","romaji":"Shigoto o yamete, inaka de atarashii ikikata o hajimemashita.","german":"I quit my job and started a new way of life in the countryside.","furigana":"しごと|いなか|あたら|い|かた|はじ"}],"notes":"生き方 means how one leads one's life: values, decisions, life plan. For daily living (housing, work, livelihood), 生活 or 暮らし fits better. Note: the similar-sounding 行き方 (いきかた) means \"way to get there\": 駅への行き方.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"占いを信じますか。","romaji":"Uranai o shinjimasu ka.","german":"Do you believe in fortune-telling?","furigana":"うらな|しん"},{"japanese":"駅前の占い師に手相を見てもらいました。","romaji":"Ekimae no uranaishi ni tesō o mite moraimashita.","german":"I had my palm read by a fortune teller in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|うらな|し|てそう|み"},{"japanese":"テレビの占いでは、今日はいい日だそうです。","romaji":"Terebi no uranai de wa, kyō wa ii hi da sō desu.","german":"According to the fortune-telling on TV, today is supposed to be a good day.","furigana":"うらな|きょう|ひ"}],"notes":"占い means fortune-telling or interpreting the future. It is not limited to horoscopes; palm reading is included too. 占いを信じる means to believe in such readings. A person who offers them can be called by the full occupational word 占い師.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人気のラーメン店に行列ができています。","romaji":"Ninki no raamen-ten ni gyouretsu ga dekite imasu.","german":"A line has formed at the popular ramen shop.","furigana":"にんき|てん|ぎょうれつ"},{"japanese":"行列に並んで、一時間待ちました。","romaji":"Gyōretsu ni narande, ichijikan machimashita.","german":"I stood in line and waited for an hour.","furigana":"ぎょうれつ|なら|いちじかんま"},{"japanese":"お祭りの行列が町の中を通りました。","romaji":"Omatsuri no gyōretsu ga machi no naka o tōrimashita.","german":"The festival procession passed through the town.","furigana":"まつ|ぎょうれつ|まち|なか|とお"}],"notes":"行列 is a line of people: 行列ができる (\"a line forms\"), 行列に並ぶ (\"to stand in line\"). 行列のできる店 is a shop so popular that you have to queue. 行列 also means \"parade, procession\", for example at festivals; for a simple row, 列 alone is often used.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"混雑を避けましょう。","romaji":"Konzatsu o sakemashō.","german":"Let's avoid the crowds.","furigana":"こんざつ|さ"},{"japanese":"朝の電車は混雑しています。","romaji":"Asa no densha wa konzatsu shite imasu.","german":"The morning trains are crowded.","furigana":"あさ|でんしゃ|こんざつ"}],"notes":"混雑 is the more formal word for crowding; in conversation people mostly say 混む: 電車が混んでいます. 混雑 is often heard in announcements and read on notices: 大変混雑しております. As a する-verb it is intransitive; the crowded place takes が or は.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"電車の遅れで遅刻しました。","romaji":"Densha no okure de chikoku shimashita.","german":"I was late because the train was delayed.","furigana":"でんしゃ|おく|ちこく"},{"japanese":"一週間休んだので、勉強の遅れを取り戻したいです。","romaji":"Isshūkan yasunda node, benkyō no okure o torimodoshitai desu.","german":"I was absent for a week, so I want to catch up on my studies.","furigana":"いっしゅうかんやす|べんきょう|おく|と|もど"},{"japanese":"工事に遅れが出ています。","romaji":"Kōji ni okure ga dete imasu.","german":"The construction work is running behind schedule.","furigana":"こうじ|おく|で"}],"notes":"遅れ is the noun of 遅れる and denotes a delay or being behind. For transport, it is 遅れが出る (\"delays occur\"); the cause takes で: 電車の遅れで. Falling behind in study or work is made up with 遅れを取り戻す.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大きな変化がありました。","romaji":"Ōkina henka ga arimashita.","german":"There was a big change.","furigana":"おお|へんか"},{"japanese":"季節の変化を感じます。","romaji":"Kisetsu no henka o kanjimasu.","german":"I feel the change of the seasons.","furigana":"きせつ|へんか|かん"},{"japanese":"この町は十年でずいぶん変化しました。","romaji":"Kono machi wa jūnen de zuibun henka shimashita.","german":"This town has changed a great deal in ten years.","furigana":"まち|じゅうねん|へんか"}],"notes":"変化 is a rather formal word for change; in conversation the verb 変わる is often used. Typical: 変化がある, 気温の変化, 変化する (intransitive). A deliberately made change, for example to a plan, is called 変更.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"天気の影響で遅れました。","romaji":"Tenki no eikyō de okuremashita.","german":"I was delayed because of the weather.","furigana":"てんき|えいきょう|おく"},{"japanese":"天気が気分に影響します。","romaji":"Tenki ga kibun ni eikyō shimasu.","german":"The weather affects my mood.","furigana":"てんき|きぶん|えいきょう"},{"japanese":"兄の影響で、ギターを始めました。","romaji":"Ani no eikyō de, gitā o hajimemashita.","german":"I started playing guitar because of my older brother's influence.","furigana":"あに|えいきょう|はじ"}],"notes":"影響 can be exerted and received: AがBに影響する / 影響を与える (\"to influence\"), Bの影響を受ける (\"to be influenced\"). With で it names a cause: 台風の影響で電車が止まりました. This form is very common in news reports and announcements.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この薬は効果があります。","romaji":"Kono kusuri wa kōka ga arimasu.","german":"This medicine is effective.","furigana":"くすり|こうか"},{"japanese":"毎日練習した効果が出てきました。","romaji":"Mainichi renshū shita kōka ga dete kimashita.","german":"The effect of practicing every day is starting to show.","furigana":"まいにちれんしゅう|こうか|で"}],"notes":"効果 is the effect or result of a measure. 効果がある says that something is effective; 効果が出る shows that the effect is becoming visible. The word can be used for a remedy as well as for practice or work. The card practices this success of an action, not specifically sound or film effects.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いい印象を受けました。","romaji":"Ii inshō o ukemashita.","german":"I got a good impression.","furigana":"いんしょう|う"},{"japanese":"第一印象は大切です。","romaji":"Daiichi inshō wa taisetsu desu.","german":"First impressions are important.","furigana":"だいいちいんしょう|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"日本の印象はどうですか。","romaji":"Nihon no inshō wa dō desu ka.","german":"What is your impression of Japan?","furigana":"にほん|いんしょう"}],"notes":"Typical phrases: いい印象を受ける (\"to get a good impression\"), 印象を与える (\"to make an impression\"), 第一印象 (\"first impression\"), 印象に残る (\"to stay in one's memory\"). Visitors to Japan very often hear the question 日本の印象はどうですか. The adjective 印象的な means \"impressive\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子供の頃の記憶がほとんどありません。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro no kioku ga hotondo arimasen.","german":"I have hardly any memories of my childhood.","furigana":"こども|ころ|きおく"},{"japanese":"この歌を聞くと、学生時代の記憶がよみがえります。","romaji":"Kono uta o kiku to, gakusei jidai no kioku ga yomigaerimasu.","german":"When I hear this song, memories of my student days come back.","furigana":"うた|き|がくせいじだい|きおく"},{"japanese":"祖父は九十歳ですが、記憶力がとてもいいです。","romaji":"Sofu wa kyūjussai desu ga, kiokuryoku ga totemo ii desu.","german":"My grandfather is ninety, but he has a very good memory.","furigana":"そふ|きゅうじゅっさい|きおくりょく"}],"notes":"記憶 is memory, or a stored recollection, and sounds rather formal: 記憶がない, 記憶にない (\"not to remember\"), 記憶力 (\"memory capacity\"). For happy personal memories people rather say 思い出: 楽しい思い出. \"To remember\" is usually 覚えている in everyday speech.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"おばあさんの知恵を借りましょう。","romaji":"Obaasan no chie o karimashou.","german":"Let's draw on grandmother's wisdom.","furigana":"ちえ|か"},{"japanese":"レモンで汚れを落とすのは生活の知恵です。","romaji":"Remon de yogore o otosu no wa seikatsu no chie desu.","german":"Removing stains with lemon is a household tip.","furigana":"よご|お|せいかつ|ちえ"},{"japanese":"三歳になって、息子は知恵がついてきました。","romaji":"Sansai ni natte, musuko wa chie ga tsuite kimashita.","german":"At three years old, my son is becoming quite clever.","furigana":"さんさい|むすこ|ちえ"}],"notes":"知恵 is practical cleverness and life wisdom, not learned knowledge (that would be 知識). Fixed phrases: 知恵を借りる (\"to ask someone for advice\"), 知恵を出し合う (\"to look for a solution together\"), 生活の知恵 (\"household tip, folk remedy\"). For children, 知恵がつく (\"to become smarter\") is used.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は高い能力を持っています。","romaji":"Kare wa takai nouryoku o motte imasu.","german":"He is very capable.","furigana":"かれ|たか|のうりょく|も"},{"japanese":"十二月に日本語能力試験を受けます。","romaji":"Jūnigatsu ni Nihongo Nōryoku Shiken o ukemasu.","german":"I'm taking the JLPT in December.","furigana":"じゅうにがつ|にほんごのうりょくしけん|う"},{"japanese":"この仕事には英語の能力が必要です。","romaji":"Kono shigoto ni wa eigo no nōryoku ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"This job requires English ability.","furigana":"しごと|えいご|のうりょく|ひつよう"}],"notes":"能力 is the general ability to do something, innate or acquired: 能力がある, 能力が高い, ～する能力. Very well known is 日本語能力試験, the JLPT. Distinction: 才能 is talent, 実力 is the ability actually demonstrated.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女には音楽の才能があります。","romaji":"Kanojo ni wa ongaku no sainou ga arimasu.","german":"She has a talent for music.","furigana":"かのじょ|おんがく|さいのう"},{"japanese":"才能より努力のほうが大切だと思います。","romaji":"Sainō yori doryoku no hō ga taisetsu da to omoimasu.","german":"I think effort is more important than talent.","furigana":"さいのう|どりょく|たいせつ|おも"},{"japanese":"先生は彼の絵の才能に気がつきました。","romaji":"Sensei wa kare no e no sainō ni ki ga tsukimashita.","german":"The teacher noticed his talent for drawing.","furigana":"せんせい|かれ|え|さいのう|き"}],"notes":"才能 is a mostly innate gift, especially in art, music or sports. Typical: Nの才能がある, 才能のある人, 才能を伸ばす (\"to develop a talent\"). The person who has the talent often takes には: 彼女には才能があります. General abilities, including learned ones, are rather called 能力.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"実力を見せましょう。","romaji":"Jitsuryoku o misemashō.","german":"Let's show our real strength.","furigana":"じつりょく|み"},{"japanese":"試験で自分の実力がよく分かりました。","romaji":"Shiken de jibun no jitsuryoku ga yoku wakarimashita.","german":"The exam showed me clearly where I really stand.","furigana":"しけん|じぶん|じつりょく|わ"},{"japanese":"彼は実力で社長になりました。","romaji":"Kare wa jitsuryoku de shachō ni narimashita.","german":"He became company president on his own merit.","furigana":"かれ|じつりょく|しゃちょう"}],"notes":"実力 is the actual ability demonstrated in practice, as opposed to 才能 (talent) or mere potential. Typical: 実力がある, 実力を出す (\"to show one's ability\"), 実力で (\"on one's own merit\"). Very common in sports and exams, e.g. 実力テスト.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"それは常識です。","romaji":"Sore wa jōshiki desu.","german":"That's common knowledge.","furigana":"じょうしき"},{"japanese":"約束の時間を守るのは社会人の常識です。","romaji":"Yakusoku no jikan o mamoru no wa shakaijin no jōshiki desu.","german":"Keeping appointment times is common sense for working adults.","furigana":"やくそく|じかん|まも|しゃかいじん|じょうしき"}],"notes":"常識 denotes generally known knowledge, common sense or usual social expectations. 常識です can present something as self-evident; 社会人の常識 speaks of expectations of adults in working life. What counts as 常識 depends on the surroundings. 常識がある describes a person with sensible judgment.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本の伝統を守ります。","romaji":"Nihon no dentō o mamorimasu.","german":"We preserve Japanese traditions.","furigana":"にほん|でんとう|まも"},{"japanese":"この店には百年の伝統があります。","romaji":"Kono mise ni wa hyakunen no dentō ga arimasu.","german":"This shop has a hundred-year tradition.","furigana":"みせ|ひゃくねん|でんとう"},{"japanese":"着物は日本の伝統的な服です。","romaji":"Kimono wa Nihon no dentōteki na fuku desu.","german":"The kimono is a traditional Japanese garment.","furigana":"きもの|にほん|でんとうてき|ふく"}],"notes":"伝統 is a tradition handed down through generations. Typical: 伝統を守る (\"to preserve\"), 伝統がある, 百年の伝統. As an adjective, 伝統的な (\"traditional\") is used: 伝統的な料理. For individual customs, for example on festival days, 習慣 or 風習 fit better.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地方の風習を知りたいです。","romaji":"Chihō no fūshū o shiritai desu.","german":"I want to learn about local customs.","furigana":"ちほう|ふうしゅう|し"},{"japanese":"その風習は今も続いています。","romaji":"Sono fūshū wa ima mo tsuzuite imasu.","german":"That custom continues even today.","furigana":"ふうしゅう|いま|つづ"},{"japanese":"日本には、お正月に子どもにお年玉をあげる風習があります。","romaji":"Nihon ni wa, oshōgatsu ni kodomo ni otoshidama o ageru fūshū ga arimasu.","german":"In Japan, there is a custom of giving children money at New Year.","furigana":"にほん|しょうがつ|こ|としだま|ふうしゅう"}],"notes":"風習 refers to customs handed down in a region or society, often with a regional or old-fashioned tone. Typical: ～する風習がある, 地方の風習, 古い風習. 習慣 is broader and can also mean personal habits; 伝統 stresses long transmission.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"宗教に興味がありますか。","romaji":"Shūkyō ni kyōmi ga arimasu ka.","german":"Are you interested in religion?","furigana":"しゅうきょう|きょうみ"},{"japanese":"日本にはいくつかの宗教があります。","romaji":"Nihon ni wa ikutsuka no shūkyō ga arimasu.","german":"There are several religions in Japan.","furigana":"にほん|しゅうきょう"},{"japanese":"宗教上の理由で、豚肉を食べません。","romaji":"Shūkyōjō no riyū de, butaniku o tabemasen.","german":"For religious reasons, I don't eat pork.","furigana":"しゅうきょうじょう|りゆう|ぶたにく|た"}],"notes":"宗教 means \"religion\" in general; individual religions are formed with 教: 仏教, キリスト教, イスラム教. Many Japanese people say of themselves 特に宗教はありません, even though they celebrate Shinto and Buddhist festivals. For dietary rules, you often hear 宗教上の理由で (\"for religious reasons\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この本は面白い思想を紹介しています。","romaji":"Kono hon wa omoshiroi shisou o shoukai shite imasu.","german":"This book introduces interesting ideas.","furigana":"ほん|おもしろ|しそう|しょうかい"},{"japanese":"この国では思想の自由が守られています。","romaji":"Kono kuni de wa shisō no jiyū ga mamorarete imasu.","german":"Freedom of thought is protected in this country.","furigana":"くに|しそう|じゆう|まも"}],"notes":"思想 is a weighty word for an entire system of thought: philosophy, worldview or political ideology, e.g. 東洋の思想 or 思想の自由. For a single thought or idea in everyday life, 考え is used. The word is encountered mainly in school, books and the news, hardly in small talk.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"道徳の授業があります。","romaji":"Dōtoku no jugyō ga arimasu.","german":"There is an ethics class.","furigana":"どうとく|じゅぎょう"},{"japanese":"お年寄りに席を譲るのは、道徳の基本です。","romaji":"Otoshiyori ni seki o yuzuru no wa, dōtoku no kihon desu.","german":"Giving up your seat to elderly people is a basic principle of morality.","furigana":"としよ|せき|ゆず|どうとく|きほん"},{"japanese":"うそをつくのは道徳的によくないです。","romaji":"Uso o tsuku no wa dōtokuteki ni yokunai desu.","german":"Lying is morally wrong.","furigana":"どうとくてき"}],"notes":"道徳 is social morality, i.e. generally accepted rules of right conduct. In Japanese schools, 道徳 is also a subject of its own (moral education). The adjective is 道徳的な (\"moral\"); philosophical or professional ethics is rather called 倫理.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"礼儀正しい人です。","romaji":"Reigi tadashii hito desu.","german":"He is a polite person.","furigana":"れいぎただ|ひと"},{"japanese":"人の家に入るときは、靴を脱ぐのが礼儀です。","romaji":"Hito no ie ni hairu toki wa, kutsu o nugu no ga reigi desu.","german":"When entering someone's house, it is proper to take off your shoes.","furigana":"ひと|いえ|はい|くつ|ぬ|れいぎ"},{"japanese":"目上の人には礼儀を守りましょう。","romaji":"Meue no hito ni wa reigi o mamorimashō.","german":"Let's observe proper manners toward our superiors.","furigana":"めうえ|ひと|れいぎ|まも"}],"notes":"礼儀 means good manners and decorum. The adjective 礼儀正しい (\"polite, well-mannered\") is very common. Other phrases: 礼儀を守る, 礼儀を知らない, ～のが礼儀です (\"it is proper to ...\"). A well-known proverb: 親しき仲にも礼儀あり (\"Even among friends, politeness is called for\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"敬語を使うのは難しいです。","romaji":"Keigo o tsukau no wa muzukashii desu.","german":"Using honorific language is difficult.","furigana":"けいご|つか|むずか"},{"japanese":"お客様には敬語で話してください。","romaji":"Okyakusama ni wa keigo de hanashite kudasai.","german":"Please speak to customers in honorific language.","furigana":"きゃくさま|けいご|はな"},{"japanese":"アルバイトで敬語を覚えました。","romaji":"Arubaito de keigo o oboemashita.","german":"I learned honorific language at my part-time job.","furigana":"けいご|おぼ"}],"notes":"敬語 is the general term for Japanese honorific language. It is divided into 尊敬語 (respectful language, which elevates the other person), 謙譲語 (humble language, which lowers oneself) and 丁寧語 (the です/ます form). Typical: 敬語を使う, 敬語で話す.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 19"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"関西の方言は面白いです。","romaji":"Kansai no hougen wa omoshiroi desu.","german":"The Kansai dialect is interesting.","furigana":"かんさい|ほうげん|おもしろ"},{"japanese":"祖母は方言で話すので、ときどき分かりません。","romaji":"Sobo wa hōgen de hanasu node, tokidoki wakarimasen.","german":"My grandmother speaks in dialect, so I sometimes can't understand her.","furigana":"そぼ|ほうげん|はな|わ"},{"japanese":"緊張すると、つい方言が出てしまいます。","romaji":"Kinchō suru to, tsui hōgen ga dete shimaimasu.","german":"When I'm nervous, my dialect slips out before I know it.","furigana":"きんちょう|ほうげん|で"}],"notes":"方言 is a regional dialect; its counterpart is 標準語 (\"standard language\"). Individual dialects are colloquially named with ～弁: 関西弁, 大阪弁. Typical: 方言で話す, 方言が出る (\"the dialect comes out\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"文法を勉強しています。","romaji":"Bunpō o benkyō shite imasu.","german":"Ich lerne Grammatik.","furigana":"ぶんぽう|べんきょう"},{"japanese":"この文は文法が間違っています。","romaji":"Kono bun wa bunpō ga machigatte imasu.","german":"In diesem Satz ist die Grammatik falsch.","furigana":"ぶん|ぶんぽう|まちが"},{"japanese":"日本語の文法はドイツ語とずいぶん違います。","romaji":"Nihongo no bunpō wa Doitsugo to zuibun chigaimasu.","german":"Die japanische Grammatik unterscheidet sich stark von der deutschen.","furigana":"にほんご|ぶんぽう|ご|ちが"}],"notes":"文法 wird ぶんぽう gelesen, nicht ぶんほう. Typisch: 文法を勉強する, 文法が正しい / 間違っている, 文法の練習. Ein Grammatikbuch heißt 文法書, und 文法的に正しい bedeutet „grammatisch korrekt“.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 41"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本語の発音は難しいですか。","romaji":"Nihongo no hatsuon wa muzukashii desu ka.","german":"Is Japanese pronunciation difficult?","furigana":"にほんご|はつおん|むずか"},{"japanese":"この言葉はどう発音しますか。","romaji":"Kono kotoba wa dō hatsuon shimasu ka.","german":"How do you pronounce this word?","furigana":"ことば|はつおん"},{"japanese":"先生に発音を直してもらいました。","romaji":"Sensei ni hatsuon o naoshite moraimashita.","german":"My teacher corrected my pronunciation.","furigana":"せんせい|はつおん|なお"}],"notes":"発音 is both the noun \"pronunciation\" and the transitive verb 発音する (\"to pronounce\", object with を). Typical: 発音がいい / きれい, 発音を直す (\"to correct the pronunciation\"). Important for learners: long vowels and the small っ can change the meaning.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 41"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"面白い表現を覚えました。","romaji":"Omoshiroi hyougen o oboemashita.","german":"I learned an interesting expression.","furigana":"おもしろ|ひょうげん|おぼ"},{"japanese":"気持ちを言葉で表現するのは難しいです。","romaji":"Kimochi o kotoba de hyōgen suru no wa muzukashii desu.","german":"It is hard to put feelings into words.","furigana":"きも|ことば|ひょうげん|むずか"},{"japanese":"この表現は目上の人には使わないでください。","romaji":"Kono hyōgen wa meue no hito ni wa tsukawanaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't use this expression with superiors.","furigana":"ひょうげん|めうえ|ひと|つか"}],"notes":"表現 is on the one hand a linguistic phrase (面白い表現, 丁寧な表現), on the other hand the act of expressing itself: 気持ちを表現する (transitive, with を). 表現力 means \"expressive ability\". In contrast to 言葉 (\"word\"), 表現 usually means a whole formulation.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 44"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"翻訳の仕事をしています。","romaji":"Honyaku no shigoto o shite imasu.","german":"I work as a translator.","furigana":"ほんやく|しごと"},{"japanese":"この小説は二十の言語に翻訳されています。","romaji":"Kono shōsetsu wa nijū no gengo ni honyaku sarete imasu.","german":"This novel has been translated into twenty languages.","furigana":"しょうせつ|にじゅう|げんご|ほんやく"}],"notes":"翻訳 means translating a text or its translated version. 翻訳の仕事 is translation work; with する it becomes the verb 翻訳する. The target language then takes に: 日本語に翻訳する. For oral interpreting, 通訳 is often used.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"通訳を頼みました。","romaji":"Tsūyaku o tanomimashita.","german":"I asked for an interpreter.","furigana":"つうやく|たの"},{"japanese":"会議で通訳をしました。","romaji":"Kaigi de tsūyaku o shimashita.","german":"I interpreted at the conference.","furigana":"かいぎ|つうやく"},{"japanese":"姉は病院で通訳として働いています。","romaji":"Ane wa byōin de tsūyaku to shite hataraite imasu.","german":"My older sister works as an interpreter at a hospital.","furigana":"あね|びょういん|つうやく|はたら"}],"notes":"通訳 is oral interpreting and at the same time the person who interprets (also 通訳者). Written translation, in contrast, is 翻訳. Typical: 通訳をする / 通訳する, 通訳を頼む, 同時通訳 (\"simultaneous interpreting\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"文章を書くのが好きです。","romaji":"Bunshō o kaku no ga suki desu.","german":"I like writing texts.","furigana":"ぶんしょう|か|す"},{"japanese":"この文章は短くて、分かりやすいです。","romaji":"Kono bunshō wa mijikakute, wakariyasui desu.","german":"This text is short and easy to understand.","furigana":"ぶんしょう|みじか|わ"}],"notes":"文章 denotes a written text or composition. With を you name it as the object of writing. 書くのが好き describes a liking for writing; 分かりやすい judges how easily a text can be understood. ぶんしょう is written bunshō with a long o.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"作文を書いてください。","romaji":"Sakubun o kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write an essay.","furigana":"さくぶん|か"},{"japanese":"日本語で作文を書きました。","romaji":"Nihongo de sakubun o kakimashita.","german":"I wrote an essay in Japanese.","furigana":"にほんご|さくぶん|か"}],"notes":"作文 is an essay or the writing of an essay. 作文を書く is a common combination. 作文を names the written text, 日本語で the language in which it is written. 書いてください asks for the writing; 書きました reports the finished essay.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 9"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本語の会話を練習したいです。","romaji":"Nihongo no kaiwa o renshuu shitai desu.","german":"I want to practice Japanese conversation.","furigana":"にほんご|かいわ|れんしゅう"},{"japanese":"電車の中で、隣の人たちの会話が聞こえました。","romaji":"Densha no naka de, tonari no hitotachi no kaiwa ga kikoemashita.","german":"On the train I could hear the conversation of the people next to me.","furigana":"でんしゃ|なか|となり|ひと|かいわ|き"},{"japanese":"最近、家族との会話が少なくなりました。","romaji":"Saikin, kazoku to no kaiwa ga sukunaku narimashita.","german":"Recently I talk less with my family.","furigana":"さいきん|かぞく|かいわ|すく"}],"notes":"会話 is a conversation between people, especially in language classes: 会話の練習, 英会話 (\"English conversation\"). The verb 会話する is possible, but in everyday speech people usually say 話す. The person you talk with takes と: 家族との会話.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 28"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大統領が演説をしました。","romaji":"Daitouryou ga enzetsu o shimashita.","german":"The president gave a speech.","furigana":"だいとうりょう|えんぜつ"},{"japanese":"選挙の前なので、駅前で演説をしている人がいます。","romaji":"Senkyo no mae na node, ekimae de enzetsu o shite iru hito ga imasu.","german":"Elections are coming up, so someone is giving a speech in front of the station.","furigana":"せんきょ|まえ|えきまえ|えんぜつ|ひと"}],"notes":"演説 is a public, often political speech before an audience: 演説をする, 演説を聞く. At weddings, school ceremonies or in class, people rather say スピーチ; a specialist lecture is called 講演. Before elections in Japan you often hear 街頭演説 (\"street speech\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"討論に参加します。","romaji":"Tōron ni sanka shimasu.","german":"I will take part in the debate.","furigana":"とうろん|さんか"},{"japanese":"環境問題について討論しました。","romaji":"Kankyō mondai ni tsuite tōron shimashita.","german":"We debated environmental problems.","furigana":"かんきょうもんだい|とうろん"},{"japanese":"昨日の夜、テレビで政治家の討論を見ました。","romaji":"Kinō no yoru, terebi de seijika no tōron o mimashita.","german":"Last night, I watched a debate between politicians on TV.","furigana":"きのう|よる|せいじか|とうろん|み"}],"notes":"討論 is an orderly debate in which different positions argue against each other, for example on TV or in class (討論会). The topic takes について: ～について討論する. 議論 is more general; an informal discussion is 話し合い.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"議論が白熱しました。","romaji":"Giron ga hakunetsu shimashita.","german":"The discussion became heated.","furigana":"ぎろん|はくねつ"},{"japanese":"この問題について議論しましょう。","romaji":"Kono mondai ni tsuite giron shimashō.","german":"Let's discuss this problem.","furigana":"もんだい|ぎろん"},{"japanese":"長い議論の結果、計画を変えることになりました。","romaji":"Nagai giron no kekka, keikaku o kaeru koto ni narimashita.","german":"After a long discussion, it was decided to change the plan.","furigana":"なが|ぎろん|けっか|けいかく|か"}],"notes":"議論 is a factual discussion or a dispute of opinions, but not a personal quarrel (that would be けんか or 口論). Typical: ～について議論する, 議論になる, 議論が白熱する (\"the discussion gets heated\"). 議論 is more general than 討論, which means an organized debate.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自分の主張を述べます。","romaji":"Jibun no shuchō o nobemasu.","german":"I'll state my own view.","furigana":"じぶん|しゅちょう|の"},{"japanese":"彼の主張は正しいと思います。","romaji":"Kare no shuchō wa tadashii to omoimasu.","german":"I think his claim is correct.","furigana":"かれ|しゅちょう|ただ|おも"},{"japanese":"彼は自分は悪くないと強く主張しました。","romaji":"Kare wa jibun wa warukunai to tsuyoku shuchō shimashita.","german":"He insisted strongly that he had done nothing wrong.","furigana":"かれ|じぶん|わる|つよ|しゅちょう"}],"notes":"主張 is an assertion or position that one emphatically upholds, i.e. stronger than 意見 (\"opinion\"). As the verb 主張する it takes を (権利を主張する) or a quotation with と (～と主張する). 自己主張 means \"asserting oneself, assertiveness\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"やっと結論が出ました。","romaji":"Yatto ketsuron ga demashita.","german":"We have finally reached a conclusion.","furigana":"けつろん|で"},{"japanese":"結論から言うと、この計画は無理です。","romaji":"Ketsuron kara iu to, kono keikaku wa muri desu.","german":"To start with the conclusion, this plan is impossible.","furigana":"けつろん|い|けいかく|むり"},{"japanese":"レポートの最後に結論を書いてください。","romaji":"Repōto no saigo ni ketsuron o kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write a conclusion at the end of the report.","furigana":"さいご|けつろん|か"}],"notes":"Fixed combinations: 結論を出す (\"to reach a conclusion\"), 結論が出る (\"a result is settled\"), 結論から言うと (\"to get straight to the point\"). In essays and reports, 結論 also denotes the final section, i.e. the conclusion.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二つの商品を比較しました。","romaji":"Futatsu no shouhin o hikaku shimashita.","german":"I compared the two products.","furigana":"ふた|しょうひん|ひかく"},{"japanese":"去年と比較すると、今年は雨が多いです。","romaji":"Kyonen to hikaku suru to, kotoshi wa ame ga ōi desu.","german":"Compared with last year, it rains a lot this year.","furigana":"きょねん|ひかく|ことし|あめ|おお"},{"japanese":"いつも兄と比較されるのが嫌です。","romaji":"Itsumo ani to hikaku sareru no ga iya desu.","german":"I hate always being compared to my older brother.","furigana":"あに|ひかく|いや"}],"notes":"比較 is the more formal word for \"comparison\"; in everyday speech people mostly say 比べる. As a verb: AとBを比較する. The adverb 比較的 (\"relatively\") is very common: 今日は比較的暖かいです.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"アンケート調査を行いました。","romaji":"Ankeeto chousa o okonaimashita.","german":"We conducted a questionnaire survey.","furigana":"ちょうさ|おこな"},{"japanese":"警察が事故の原因を調査しています。","romaji":"Keisatsu ga jiko no gen'in o chōsa shite imasu.","german":"The police are investigating the cause of the accident.","furigana":"けいさつ|じこ|げんいん|ちょうさ"}],"notes":"調査 is a systematic, often official investigation or survey: アンケート調査 (\"questionnaire survey\"), 世論調査 (\"opinion poll\"). As a verb: 調査する or formally 調査を行う. In everyday speech, \"to look up, to find out\" is simply 調べる.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 42"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"統計を見てみましょう。","romaji":"Tōkei o mite mimashō.","german":"Let's take a look at the statistics.","furigana":"とうけい|み"},{"japanese":"統計によると、日本の人口は減っています。","romaji":"Tōkei ni yoru to, Nihon no jinkō wa hette imasu.","german":"According to the statistics, Japan's population is declining.","furigana":"とうけい|にほん|じんこう|へ"},{"japanese":"店では毎月、売り上げの統計を取っています。","romaji":"Mise de wa maitsuki, uriage no tōkei o totte imasu.","german":"At the shop, we compile sales statistics every month.","furigana":"みせ|まいつき|う|あ|とうけい|と"}],"notes":"統計 means \"statistics\" in the sense of collected figures and their analysis. Important expressions: 統計によると (\"according to statistics\"), 統計を取る (\"to compile statistics\"). The academic subject is 統計学, and \"statistical\" is 統計的な.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"報告書を書きます。","romaji":"Hōkokusho o kakimasu.","german":"I'll write a report.","furigana":"ほうこくしょ|か"},{"japanese":"上司に報告しなければなりません。","romaji":"Jōshi ni hōkoku shinakereba narimasen.","german":"I have to report to my boss.","furigana":"じょうし|ほうこく"},{"japanese":"結婚したことを友達に報告しました。","romaji":"Kekkon shita koto o tomodachi ni hōkoku shimashita.","german":"I told my friends that I got married.","furigana":"けっこん|ともだち|ほうこく"}],"notes":"報告 is a notification or report: to whom with に, about what with を or について (上司に結果を報告する). A written report is called 報告書. One also \"reports\" private news, e.g. 結婚の報告. In Japanese companies the rule ほうれんそう applies: 報告・連絡・相談.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"連絡先を交換しましょう。","romaji":"Renrakusaki o koukan shimashou.","german":"Let's exchange contact information.","furigana":"れんらくさき|こうかん"},{"japanese":"緊急の連絡先をここに書いてください。","romaji":"Kinkyū no renrakusaki o koko ni kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write your emergency contact here.","furigana":"きんきゅう|れんらくさき|か"},{"japanese":"引っ越したので、連絡先が変わりました。","romaji":"Hikkoshita node, renrakusaki ga kawarimashita.","german":"I moved, so my contact information has changed.","furigana":"ひ|こ|れんらくさき|か"}],"notes":"連絡先 is composed of 連絡 (\"contact\") and 先 (\"destination\"): the address, phone number or e-mail at which someone can be reached. Typical: 連絡先を教える, 連絡先を交換する. On forms, 緊急連絡先 (\"emergency contact\") often appears.","levelNote":"Compound: 連絡 (N4) + 先 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"郵便で送ります。","romaji":"Yūbin de okurimasu.","german":"I'll send it by mail.","furigana":"ゆうびん|おく"},{"japanese":"今日はもう郵便が来ましたか。","romaji":"Kyō wa mō yūbin ga kimashita ka.","german":"Has the mail come yet today?","furigana":"きょう|ゆうびん|き"},{"japanese":"住所の前に郵便番号を書いてください。","romaji":"Jūsho no mae ni yūbin bangō o kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write the postal code before the address.","furigana":"じゅうしょ|まえ|ゆうびんばんごう|か"}],"notes":"郵便 is the mail as a service and as items sent: 郵便で送る (\"to send by mail\"), 郵便が来る (\"the mail arrives\"). Important compounds: 郵便局 (\"post office\"), 郵便番号 (\"postal code\"), 郵便受け (\"mailbox at the door\"). The symbol 〒 stands for the post.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"速達で送ってください。","romaji":"Sokutatsu de okutte kudasai.","german":"Please send it by express mail.","furigana":"そくたつ|おく"},{"japanese":"速達なら、明日には届きます。","romaji":"Sokutatsu nara, ashita ni wa todokimasu.","german":"With express mail, it will arrive by tomorrow.","furigana":"そくたつ|あした|とど"},{"japanese":"速達の料金はいくらですか。","romaji":"Sokutatsu no ryōkin wa ikura desu ka.","german":"How much is express delivery?","furigana":"そくたつ|りょうきん"}],"notes":"速達 is express postal delivery for an additional fee: 速達で送る (\"to send by express mail\"). Such items carry a red stripe or the stamp 速達. Do not confuse it with 書留 (\"registered mail\"), which is about secure, traceable delivery.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 11"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"教科書を開いてください。","romaji":"Kyōkasho o hiraite kudasai.","german":"Please open your textbook.","furigana":"きょうかしょ|ひら"},{"japanese":"教科書を忘れてしまいました。","romaji":"Kyōkasho o wasurete shimaimashita.","german":"I forgot my textbook.","furigana":"きょうかしょ|わす"},{"japanese":"この話は歴史の教科書に載っています。","romaji":"Kono hanashi wa rekishi no kyōkasho ni notte imasu.","german":"This story is in the history textbook.","furigana":"はなし|れきし|きょうかしょ|の"}],"notes":"教科書 is the official textbook for a class; additional study and practice books are called 参考書. Books are counted with 冊: 教科書を二冊. Figuratively, 教科書通り means \"by the book, exactly according to the rules\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 6"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"参考書を買いました。","romaji":"Sankōsho o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a study guide.","furigana":"さんこうしょ|か"},{"japanese":"試験のために、毎晩参考書で勉強しています。","romaji":"Shiken no tame ni, maiban sankōsho de benkyō shite imasu.","german":"For the exam, I study with a study guide every evening.","furigana":"しけん|まいばんさんこうしょ|べんきょう"},{"japanese":"先生にいい参考書を紹介してもらいました。","romaji":"Sensei ni ii sankōsho o shōkai shite moraimashita.","german":"My teacher recommended a good study guide to me.","furigana":"せんせい|さんこうしょ|しょうかい"}],"notes":"参考書 is a supplementary study or practice book that you use alongside the 教科書, typically for exam preparation. A reference work in the narrower sense, such as a dictionary, is called 辞書 or 辞典. It is counted with 冊: 参考書を三冊.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"来週の日程を確認しましょう。","romaji":"Raishuu no nittei o kakunin shimashou.","german":"Let's check next week's schedule.","furigana":"らいしゅう|にってい|かくにん"},{"japanese":"旅行の日程が決まりました。","romaji":"Ryokō no nittei ga kimarimashita.","german":"The itinerary for the trip has been decided.","furigana":"りょこう|にってい|き"},{"japanese":"会議の日程を変えてもいいですか。","romaji":"Kaigi no nittei o kaete mo ii desu ka.","german":"May we change the date of the meeting?","furigana":"かいぎ|にってい|か"}],"notes":"日程 is a schedule covering one or more days, for example for a trip, conference or event: 旅行の日程. Typical: 日程を決める, 日程を確認する, 日程を調整する (\"to coordinate dates\"). Personal plans are more often 予定; スケジュール is the casual loanword.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"試験期間は二週間です。","romaji":"Shiken kikan wa nishuukan desu.","german":"The exam period is two weeks.","furigana":"しけんきかん|にしゅうかん"},{"japanese":"この商品は期間限定です。","romaji":"Kono shōhin wa kikan gentei desu.","german":"This product is available for a limited time only.","furigana":"しょうひん|きかんげんてい"},{"japanese":"セールの期間中は、お店がとても混みます。","romaji":"Sēru no kikanchū wa, omise ga totemo komimasu.","german":"The shop gets very crowded during the sale period.","furigana":"きかんちゅう|みせ|こ"}],"notes":"期間 is a span of time with a beginning and an end, i.e. a duration: 試験期間 (\"exam period\"), 期間中 (\"during the period\"). 期間限定 (\"for a limited time only\") is very common for offers and products. Do not confuse it with 期限: 期限 is the end point, i.e. the deadline.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"期限までに提出してください。","romaji":"Kigen made ni teishutsu shite kudasai.","german":"Please submit it by the deadline.","furigana":"きげん|ていしゅつ"},{"japanese":"この薬の期限を確認してください。","romaji":"Kono kusuri no kigen o kakunin shite kudasai.","german":"Please check the expiration date of this medicine.","furigana":"くすり|きげん|かくにん"},{"japanese":"パスポートの期限が切れています。","romaji":"Pasupōto no kigen ga kirete imasu.","german":"My passport has expired.","furigana":"きげん|き"}],"notes":"期限 is the deadline or expiration date, i.e. the point in time until which something is valid or must be done: 期限までに (\"by the deadline\"). When the deadline has passed, you say 期限が切れる. Fixed compounds are 賞味期限 (\"best-before date\"), 有効期限 (\"validity period\") and 提出期限 (\"submission deadline\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このお寺は八世紀に建てられました。","romaji":"Kono otera wa hasseiki ni tateraremashita.","german":"This temple was built in the eighth century.","furigana":"てら|はっせいき|た"},{"japanese":"二十一世紀は技術の時代です。","romaji":"Nijūisseiki wa gijutsu no jidai desu.","german":"The 21st century is the age of technology.","furigana":"にじゅういっせいき|ぎじゅつ|じだい"},{"japanese":"この店は半世紀前からあります。","romaji":"Kono mise wa hanseiki mae kara arimasu.","german":"This shop has been here for half a century.","furigana":"みせ|はんせいきまえ"}],"notes":"世紀 is attached directly to the number: 二十一世紀 (\"the 21st century\"), 八世紀に (\"in the eighth century\"). The pronunciation of the number often changes: 一世紀 いっせいき, 八世紀 はっせいき. Other phrases are 半世紀 (\"half a century\") and 世紀の〜 (\"... of the century\"), e.g. 世紀の発見.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"江戸時代の文化に興味があります。","romaji":"Edo jidai no bunka ni kyoumi ga arimasu.","german":"I'm interested in the culture of the Edo period.","furigana":"えどじだい|ぶんか|きょうみ"},{"japanese":"学生時代はよく旅行しました。","romaji":"Gakusei jidai wa yoku ryokō shimashita.","german":"I traveled a lot during my student days.","furigana":"がくせいじだい|りょこう"},{"japanese":"時代が変わりました。","romaji":"Jidai ga kawarimashita.","german":"Times have changed.","furigana":"じだい|か"}],"notes":"時代 means an era or period: historical, as in 江戸時代 (\"Edo period\"), but also personal, as in 学生時代 (\"student days\") or 子供時代 (\"childhood\"). 時代が変わる means \"times change\", 時代遅れ means \"outdated, old-fashioned\". For a century as a unit of time, by contrast, 世紀 is used.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"現代の若者はスマホをよく使います。","romaji":"Gendai no wakamono wa sumaho o yoku tsukaimasu.","german":"Young people today use smartphones a lot.","furigana":"げんだい|わかもの|つか"},{"japanese":"現代美術の展覧会に行きました。","romaji":"Gendai bijutsu no tenrankai ni ikimashita.","german":"I went to an exhibition of contemporary art.","furigana":"げんだいびじゅつ|てんらんかい|い"},{"japanese":"現代では、多くの人が家で仕事をしています。","romaji":"Gendai de wa, ōku no hito ga ie de shigoto o shite imasu.","german":"Nowadays, many people work from home.","furigana":"げんだい|おお|ひと|いえ|しごと"}],"notes":"現代 means the present time or the modern era and mostly appears attributively with の: 現代の社会, 現代の若者. It is common in compounds such as 現代美術 (\"contemporary art\") or 現代日本語 (\"modern Japanese\"). For an everyday \"today, nowadays\", people rather say 今 or 最近; 現代では sounds more formal.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"古代エジプトの歴史を学びました。","romaji":"Kodai Ejiputo no rekishi o manabimashita.","german":"I studied the history of ancient Egypt.","furigana":"こだい|れきし|まな"},{"japanese":"博物館で古代の道具を見ました。","romaji":"Hakubutsukan de kodai no dōgu o mimashita.","german":"I saw tools from ancient times at the museum.","furigana":"はくぶつかん|こだい|どうぐ|み"},{"japanese":"古代ローマの人々もお風呂が好きでした。","romaji":"Kodai Rōma no hitobito mo ofuro ga suki deshita.","german":"The people of ancient Rome also loved baths.","furigana":"こだい|ひとびと|ふろ|す"}],"notes":"古代 refers to antiquity or the early period of a culture, and is usually placed before country names or used with の: 古代エジプト, 古代ローマ, 古代の文明. Its counterpart is 現代. For an everyday \"in the past, long ago\", you don't use 古代 but 昔.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"株を買いました。","romaji":"Kabu o kaimashita.","german":"I bought stocks.","furigana":"かぶ|か"},{"japanese":"株で利益を得ました。","romaji":"Kabu de rieki o emashita.","german":"I made a profit on stocks.","furigana":"かぶ|りえき|え"},{"japanese":"今日は株価が下がりました。","romaji":"Kyō wa kabuka ga sagarimashita.","german":"Share prices fell today.","furigana":"きょう|かぶか|さ"}],"notes":"株 is the everyday word for \"stock, share\": 株を買う／売る, 株で儲ける. The share price is 株価, a joint-stock company 株式会社 - often abbreviated (株) in company names. 株 also serves as a counter for plants with roots (一株 \"one clump\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不動産屋で部屋を探しました。","romaji":"Fudōsan'ya de heya o sagashimashita.","german":"I looked for a room at a real estate agency.","furigana":"ふどうさんや|へや|さが"},{"japanese":"不動産の値段が上がっています。","romaji":"Fudōsan no nedan ga agatte imasu.","german":"Real estate prices are rising.","furigana":"ふどうさん|ねだん|あ"},{"japanese":"父は不動産の会社で働いています。","romaji":"Chichi wa fudōsan no kaisha de hataraite imasu.","german":"My father works at a real estate company.","furigana":"ちち|ふどうさん|かいしゃ|はたら"}],"notes":"不動産 is the general term for land and buildings. In daily life, you meet the word mostly in 不動産屋 or 不動産会社 (\"real estate agent, real estate company\"), where you look for a rental apartment in Japan. A single listed unit, by contrast, is called 物件, not 不動産.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"東京の家賃は高いです。","romaji":"Toukyou no yachin wa takai desu.","german":"Rent in Tokyo is high.","furigana":"とうきょう|やちん|たか"},{"japanese":"毎月二十五日に家賃を払います。","romaji":"Maitsuki nijūgonichi ni yachin o haraimasu.","german":"I pay the rent on the 25th of every month.","furigana":"まいつきにじゅうごにち|やちん|はら"},{"japanese":"この部屋の家賃はいくらですか。","romaji":"Kono heya no yachin wa ikura desu ka.","german":"How much is the rent for this room?","furigana":"へや|やちん"}],"notes":"家賃 is the rent for an apartment or house. Typical are 家賃を払う (\"to pay rent\"), 家賃が高い／安い, and statements such as 家賃は月八万円. In Japan, 敷金 (\"deposit\") and 礼金 (\"key money\") often come on top when moving in.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 22"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"冬は光熱費が高くなります。","romaji":"Fuyu wa kounetsuhi ga takaku narimasu.","german":"Utility costs go up in winter.","furigana":"ふゆ|こうねつひ|たか"},{"japanese":"光熱費は家賃に含まれていますか。","romaji":"Kōnetsuhi wa yachin ni fukumarete imasu ka.","german":"Are utilities included in the rent?","furigana":"こうねつひ|やちん|ふく"},{"japanese":"光熱費を節約するために、使わない電気を消します。","romaji":"Kōnetsuhi o setsuyaku suru tame ni, tsukawanai denki o keshimasu.","german":"To save on utility costs, I turn off electricity I'm not using.","furigana":"こうねつひ|せつやく|つか|でんき|け"}],"notes":"光熱費 are a household's energy costs, mainly electricity and gas. Water is strictly counted separately as 水道代; together they are called 水道光熱費. Typical expressions are 光熱費を払う, 光熱費が上がる and 光熱費を節約する (\"to save on energy costs\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"国籍はどちらですか。","romaji":"Kokuseki wa dochira desu ka.","german":"What is your nationality?","furigana":"こくせき"},{"japanese":"彼は日本の国籍を取りました。","romaji":"Kare wa Nihon no kokuseki o torimashita.","german":"He acquired Japanese nationality.","furigana":"かれ|にほん|こくせき|と"},{"japanese":"書類に名前と国籍を書いてください。","romaji":"Shorui ni namae to kokuseki o kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write your name and nationality on the form.","furigana":"しょるい|なまえ|こくせき|か"}],"notes":"国籍 is nationality, e.g. on forms and at immigration. Politely you ask 国籍はどちらですか; the answer is the country: 国籍はドイツです. Acquiring and holding it are expressed with 国籍を取る／取得する and 国籍を持つ; 二重国籍 means \"dual nationality\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ビザを申請します。","romaji":"Biza o shinsei shimasu.","german":"I'm applying for a visa.","furigana":"しんせい"},{"japanese":"日本に行くためにビザが必要です。","romaji":"Nihon ni iku tame ni biza ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"You need a visa to go to Japan.","furigana":"にほん|い|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"ビザが切れる前に更新します。","romaji":"Biza ga kireru mae ni kōshin shimasu.","german":"I'll renew my visa before it expires.","furigana":"き|まえ|こうしん"}],"notes":"ビザ is the visa: ビザを申請する (\"to apply for\"), ビザを取る (\"to obtain\"), ビザを更新する (\"to renew\"). When it expires, you say ビザが切れる. The official term for visa is 査証; in everyday speech ビザ often also denotes residence status, such as 学生ビザ or 就労ビザ (\"work visa\").","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 20"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"入国審査を受けます。","romaji":"Nyūkoku shinsa o ukemasu.","german":"I go through immigration control.","furigana":"にゅうこくしんさ|う"},{"japanese":"入国カードに名前を書いてください。","romaji":"Nyūkoku kādo ni namae o kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write your name on the arrival card.","furigana":"にゅうこく|なまえ|か"},{"japanese":"ビザがないと、入国できません。","romaji":"Biza ga nai to, nyūkoku dekimasen.","german":"You can't enter the country without a visa.","furigana":"にゅうこく"}],"notes":"入国 is entry into a country; as the verb 入国する, the destination country takes に: 日本に入国する. Important compounds at the airport are 入国審査 (\"immigration control\") and 入国カード (\"arrival card\"). The opposite is 出国 (\"leaving a country\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"出国手続きをします。","romaji":"Shukkoku tetsuzuki o shimasu.","german":"I'm completing the departure formalities.","furigana":"しゅっこくてつづ"},{"japanese":"明日の朝、日本を出国します。","romaji":"Ashita no asa, Nihon o shukkoku shimasu.","german":"Tomorrow morning I leave Japan.","furigana":"あした|あさ|にほん|しゅっこく"},{"japanese":"出国する前に、空港で両親に電話しました。","romaji":"Shukkoku suru mae ni, kūkō de ryōshin ni denwa shimashita.","german":"Before leaving the country, I called my parents from the airport.","furigana":"しゅっこく|まえ|くうこう|りょうしん|でんわ"}],"notes":"出国 is leaving a country; as the verb 出国する, the country being left takes を: 日本を出国する. At the airport you encounter 出国手続き (\"departure formalities\") and 出国審査 (\"departure control\"). The opposite is 入国 (\"entering a country\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"税関で荷物を検査されました。","romaji":"Zeikan de nimotsu o kensa saremashita.","german":"My luggage was inspected at customs.","furigana":"ぜいかん|にもつ|けんさ"},{"japanese":"税関を通るのに三十分かかりました。","romaji":"Zeikan o tōru no ni sanjuppun kakarimashita.","german":"It took thirty minutes to get through customs.","furigana":"ぜいかん|とお|さんじゅっぷん"},{"japanese":"お酒をたくさん買ったので、税関で申告しました。","romaji":"Osake o takusan katta node, zeikan de shinkoku shimashita.","german":"Since I bought a lot of alcohol, I declared it at customs.","furigana":"さけ|か|ぜいかん|しんこく"}],"notes":"税関 is customs, or the customs office at an airport or port. Going through customs is 税関を通る; declaring goods is 申告する. The typical question there is 申告するものはありますか (\"Do you have anything to declare?\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"観光に来ました。","romaji":"Kankō ni kimashita.","german":"I came for sightseeing.","furigana":"かんこう|き"},{"japanese":"京都を観光しました。","romaji":"Kyōto o kankō shimashita.","german":"I did some sightseeing in Kyoto.","furigana":"きょうと|かんこう"},{"japanese":"この町は観光客が多いです。","romaji":"Kono machi wa kankōkyaku ga ōi desu.","german":"There are many tourists in this town.","furigana":"まち|かんこうきゃく|おお"}],"notes":"観光 is tourism or sightseeing. As the verb 観光する, the place visited takes を: 京都を観光する; the purpose of travel is expressed with に: 観光に来る. When asked about the purpose of your trip at immigration, you simply answer 観光です. Common compounds are 観光客 (\"tourist\") and 観光地 (\"tourist destination\").","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 15"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"有名な名所を回りました。","romaji":"Yūmei na meisho o mawarimashita.","german":"I visited famous sights.","furigana":"ゆうめい|めいしょ|まわ"},{"japanese":"この町には名所がたくさんあります。","romaji":"Kono machi ni wa meisho ga takusan arimasu.","german":"This town has many famous sights.","furigana":"まち|めいしょ"},{"japanese":"ここは桜の名所です。","romaji":"Koko wa sakura no meisho desu.","german":"This place is famous for its cherry blossoms.","furigana":"さくら|めいしょ"}],"notes":"名所 is a famous place worth seeing. 〜の名所 for \"known for ...\" is very typical: 桜の名所, 紅葉の名所. Going around the sights is 名所を回る or 名所を巡る. 観光地, by contrast, refers more to a whole tourist area, while 名所 is the individual spot.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どの方角ですか。","romaji":"Dono hōgaku desu ka.","german":"Which direction is it?","furigana":"ほうがく"},{"japanese":"北の方角に進んでください。","romaji":"Kita no hōgaku ni susunde kudasai.","german":"Please head north.","furigana":"きた|ほうがく|すす"},{"japanese":"方角が分からなくなりました。","romaji":"Hōgaku ga wakaranaku narimashita.","german":"I lost my sense of direction.","furigana":"ほうがく|わ"}],"notes":"方角 means a compass direction or a direction in the terrain, usually in terms of 東西南北: 北の方角, 駅の方角. Compared with 方向 (\"direction\" in general, also figurative), 方角 is more geographical. If you have lost your bearings, you say 方角が分からない.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コンビニが目印です。","romaji":"Konbini ga mejirushi desu.","german":"The convenience store is the landmark.","furigana":"めじるし"},{"japanese":"自分の傘に目印をつけました。","romaji":"Jibun no kasa ni mejirushi o tsukemashita.","german":"I put a mark on my umbrella.","furigana":"じぶん|かさ|めじるし"},{"japanese":"目印に赤い帽子をかぶって行きます。","romaji":"Mejirushi ni akai bōshi o kabutte ikimasu.","german":"I will wear a red hat so you can recognize me.","furigana":"めじるし|あか|ぼうし|い"}],"notes":"目印 is a feature by which you quickly recognize something: as a waypoint in directions (〜が目印です \"the landmark is ...\") or as a mark on your own belongings (目印をつける \"to mark\"). When arranging to meet, people also name a 目印 so they can find each other. An official traffic sign, by contrast, is 標識.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"標識に従ってください。","romaji":"Hyōshiki ni shitagatte kudasai.","german":"Please follow the signs.","furigana":"ひょうしき|したが"},{"japanese":"交通標識に注意してください。","romaji":"Kōtsū hyōshiki ni chūi shite kudasai.","german":"Please pay attention to the traffic signs.","furigana":"こうつうひょうしき|ちゅうい"},{"japanese":"この標識は「止まれ」という意味です。","romaji":"Kono hyōshiki wa \"tomare\" to iu imi desu.","german":"This sign means \"stop\".","furigana":"ひょうしき|と|いみ"}],"notes":"標識 are official signs and markers, especially 道路標識 or 交通標識 (\"road signs\"). Following a sign is 標識に従う. Shop signs and billboards, by contrast, are 看板, and information boards are 案内板.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"出口はどこですか。","romaji":"Deguchi wa doko desu ka.","german":"Where is the exit?","furigana":"でぐち"},{"japanese":"駅の出口で待っています。","romaji":"Eki no deguchi de matte imasu.","german":"I'm waiting at the station exit.","furigana":"えき|でぐち|ま"}],"notes":"出口 is the exit of a building or area. 出口はどこですか asks for its location. 駅の出口 assigns it to the station; 出口で待つ names it as a meeting point. 口 is read ぐち in this word, with a voiced g.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 33"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"入口はあちらです。","romaji":"Iriguchi wa achira desu.","german":"The entrance is over there.","furigana":"いりぐち"},{"japanese":"入口で切符を見せてください。","romaji":"Iriguchi de kippu o misete kudasai.","german":"Please show your ticket at the entrance.","furigana":"いりぐち|きっぷ|み"}],"notes":"入口 is the entrance. The spelling 入り口 is also common for the same reading いりぐち. あちら politely points to a place over there. 入口で切符を見せる describes an action at the entrance: で names the place, を the ticket shown.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 33"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"非常口の場所を確認してください。","romaji":"Hijouguchi no basho o kakunin shite kudasai.","german":"Please check where the emergency exit is.","furigana":"ひじょうぐち|ばしょ|かくにん"},{"japanese":"火事のときは、非常口から逃げてください。","romaji":"Kaji no toki wa, hijōguchi kara nigete kudasai.","german":"In case of fire, please escape through the emergency exit.","furigana":"かじ|ひじょうぐち|に"},{"japanese":"非常口の前に物を置かないでください。","romaji":"Hijōguchi no mae ni mono o okanaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't put anything in front of the emergency exit.","furigana":"ひじょうぐち|まえ|もの|お"}],"notes":"非常口 is the emergency exit, in Japan usually marked with the green sign showing a running figure. Typical are 非常口を確認する and 非常口から逃げる (\"to escape through the emergency exit\"). Related are 非常時 (\"emergency\") and 非常ベル (\"alarm bell\"); the normal exit is 出口.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 33"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"手洗いはどこですか。","romaji":"Tearai wa doko desu ka.","german":"Where is the restroom?","furigana":"てあら"},{"japanese":"食事の前には手洗いをしましょう。","romaji":"Shokuji no mae ni wa tearai o shimashō.","german":"We should wash our hands before eating.","furigana":"しょくじ|まえ|てあら"}],"notes":"手洗い means washing one's hands and can also mean the washroom or toilet. For polite questions about the toilet, お手洗い is more usual than the plain form 手洗い. 手洗いをする means \"to wash one's hands\". Context distinguishes this action from a room and from washing laundry by hand.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"リモコンが見つかりません。","romaji":"Rimokon ga mitsukarimasen.","german":"I can't find the remote control.","furigana":"み"},{"japanese":"リモコンでテレビを消してください。","romaji":"Rimokon de terebi o keshite kudasai.","german":"Please turn off the TV with the remote control.","furigana":"け"},{"japanese":"エアコンのリモコンで温度を下げました。","romaji":"Eakon no rimokon de ondo o sagemashita.","german":"I lowered the temperature with the air conditioner's remote control.","furigana":"おんど|さ"}],"notes":"リモコン is the short form of リモートコントロール and means the remote control. To distinguish, people say テレビのリモコン or エアコンのリモコン. The device used to operate something takes で: リモコンでテレビをつける／消す.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ドライバーを貸してください。","romaji":"Doraibā o kashite kudasai.","german":"Please lend me a screwdriver.","furigana":"か"},{"japanese":"ネジを締めるのにドライバーが必要です。","romaji":"Neji o shimeru no ni doraibā ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"I need a screwdriver to tighten the screw.","furigana":"し|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"タクシーのドライバーに道を聞きました。","romaji":"Takushī no doraibā ni michi o kikimashita.","german":"I asked the taxi driver for directions.","furigana":"みち|き"}],"notes":"ドライバー has two everyday meanings: \"screwdriver\" (ネジを締める／外す) and \"driver\". For professional drivers, 運転手 is more common, while ドライバー sounds more modern (タクシードライバー). It also refers to the golf club \"driver\" and the device driver on a computer.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 41"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"リモコンの電池が切れました。","romaji":"Rimokon no denchi ga kiremashita.","german":"The battery in the remote control has run out.","furigana":"でんち|き"},{"japanese":"電池を新しいのに替えました。","romaji":"Denchi o atarashii no ni kaemashita.","german":"I replaced the battery with a new one.","furigana":"でんち|あたら|か"},{"japanese":"単三電池を四本買いました。","romaji":"Tansan denchi o yonhon kaimashita.","german":"I bought four AA batteries.","furigana":"たんさんでんち|よんほんか"}],"notes":"電池 is a battery. When it is dead, you say 電池が切れる; to replace it is 電池を替える／交換する. Batteries are counted with 本: 単三電池を二本 (\"two AA batteries\"). For the rechargeable battery of a smartphone or laptop, バッテリー is usually used.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 10"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コンビニで買い物をします。","romaji":"Konbini de kaimono o shimasu.","german":"I shop at the convenience store.","furigana":"か|もの"},{"japanese":"コンビニで電気代を払いました。","romaji":"Konbini de denkidai o haraimashita.","german":"I paid the electricity bill at the convenience store.","furigana":"でんきだい|はら"},{"japanese":"駅の前にコンビニがあります。","romaji":"Eki no mae ni konbini ga arimasu.","german":"There is a convenience store in front of the station.","furigana":"えき|まえ"}],"notes":"コンビニ is the short form of コンビニエンスストア and means the small store, usually open around the clock, found on almost every corner in Japan. The place of an action takes で: コンビニで買う. There you can also pay bills, send packages, or withdraw money.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 10"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"百貨店でプレゼントを買いました。","romaji":"Hyakkaten de purezento o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a present at the department store.","furigana":"ひゃっかてん|か"},{"japanese":"その百貨店は午後八時まで開いています。","romaji":"Sono hyakkaten wa gogo hachiji made aite imasu.","german":"That department store is open until 8 p.m.","furigana":"ひゃっかてん|ごごはちじ|あ"}],"notes":"百貨店 is a department store with many merchandise sections, such as clothing, household goods and food. It is also called デパート. 百貨店で買う names the department store as the place of shopping; 開いている describes that it is open. The word does not mean a single small specialty shop.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"美容院に行きます。","romaji":"Biyōin ni ikimasu.","german":"I'm going to the hair salon.","furigana":"びよういん|い"},{"japanese":"美容院で髪を切りました。","romaji":"Biyōin de kami o kirimashita.","german":"I got my hair cut at the hair salon.","furigana":"びよういん|かみ|き"},{"japanese":"電話で美容院を予約しました。","romaji":"Denwa de biyōin o yoyaku shimashita.","german":"I booked the hair salon by phone.","furigana":"でんわ|びよういん|よやく"}],"notes":"美容院 (also 美容室) is a hair salon offering cuts, coloring, and styling; the classic men's barber is called 床屋 or 理容室. You go 美容院に行く and have your hair cut there: 美容院で髪を切る. Do not confuse it with 病院 (びょういん, \"hospital\"): 美容院 is pronounced び・よう・いん.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 10"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"歯が痛いので歯医者に行きます。","romaji":"Ha ga itai node haisha ni ikimasu.","german":"I have a toothache, so I'm going to the dentist.","furigana":"は|いた|はいしゃ|い"},{"japanese":"歯医者さんに、甘いものを食べすぎないように言われました。","romaji":"Haisha-san ni, amai mono o tabesuginai yō ni iwaremashita.","german":"The dentist told me not to eat too many sweets.","furigana":"はいしゃ|あま|た|い"},{"japanese":"歯医者の予約は三時です。","romaji":"Haisha no yoyaku wa sanji desu.","german":"My dentist appointment is at three o'clock.","furigana":"はいしゃ|よやく|さんじ"}],"notes":"歯医者 denotes the dentist and also the dental practice: 歯医者に行く (\"to go to the dentist\"). One speaks of the dentist personally in a friendly, polite way as 歯医者さん. On signs and in formal texts, 歯科 or 歯科医 appear instead.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 15"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子供を保育園に預けます。","romaji":"Kodomo o hoikuen ni azukemasu.","german":"I leave my child at daycare.","furigana":"こども|ほいくえん|あず"},{"japanese":"五時に保育園に子供を迎えに行きます。","romaji":"Goji ni hoikuen ni kodomo o mukae ni ikimasu.","german":"I go to pick up my child from daycare at five.","furigana":"ごじ|ほいくえん|こども|むか|い"},{"japanese":"近くの保育園は空きがありません。","romaji":"Chikaku no hoikuen wa aki ga arimasen.","german":"The daycare nearby has no vacancies.","furigana":"ちか|ほいくえん|あ"}],"notes":"保育園 is a daycare center or nursery for children from 0 to about 5 years old, mainly for working parents, with full-day care. Leaving a child there is 保育園に預ける, picking up is 保育園に迎えに行く. It should be distinguished from 幼稚園, the more education-oriented kindergarten from age 3 with shorter care hours.","levelNote":"Compound: 保育 (N1) + 園 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"幼稚園に通っています。","romaji":"Yōchien ni kayotte imasu.","german":"Er geht in den Kindergarten.","furigana":"ようちえん|かよ"},{"japanese":"息子は来年幼稚園に入ります。","romaji":"Musuko wa rainen yōchien ni hairimasu.","german":"Mein Sohn kommt nächstes Jahr in den Kindergarten.","furigana":"むすこ|らいねんようちえん|はい"},{"japanese":"幼稚園の先生になりたいです。","romaji":"Yōchien no sensei ni naritai desu.","german":"Ich möchte Erzieher im Kindergarten werden.","furigana":"ようちえん|せんせい"}],"notes":"幼稚園 ist der Kindergarten für Kinder ab etwa 3 Jahren bis zur Einschulung; er gilt als Bildungseinrichtung und hat meist kürzere Betreuungszeiten als das 保育園 („Kita“). Typisch sind 幼稚園に通う („in den Kindergarten gehen“) und 幼稚園に入る („in den Kindergarten kommen“). Die Übersetzung „Vorschule“ trifft es im Deutschen nicht.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 41"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"週三回塾に通っています。","romaji":"Shuu sankai juku ni kayotte imasu.","german":"I go to cram school three times a week.","furigana":"しゅうさんかいじゅく|かよ"},{"japanese":"塾が終わるのは夜九時です。","romaji":"Juku ga owaru no wa yoru kuji desu.","german":"Cram school doesn't end until nine in the evening.","furigana":"じゅく|お|よるくじ"},{"japanese":"塾の先生は教え方がとても上手です。","romaji":"Juku no sensei wa oshiekata ga totemo jōzu desu.","german":"The teacher at the cram school explains very well.","furigana":"じゅく|せんせい|おし|かた|じょうず"}],"notes":"塾 is a private tutoring or cram school that many Japanese students attend after regular classes. Regular attendance is 塾に通う, a single trip 塾に行く. Specifically for university entrance exams there is also the 予備校.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 48"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大学受験のために予備校に行きます。","romaji":"Daigaku juken no tame ni yobikou ni ikimasu.","german":"I go to a prep school for the university entrance exam.","furigana":"だいがくじゅけん|よびこう|い"},{"japanese":"予備校の授業は夜まであります。","romaji":"Yobikō no jugyō wa yoru made arimasu.","german":"Classes at the prep school go on into the evening.","furigana":"よびこう|じゅぎょう|よる"},{"japanese":"試験に落ちて、一年間予備校で勉強しました。","romaji":"Shiken ni ochite, ichinenkan yobikō de benkyō shimashita.","german":"After failing the exam, I studied at a prep school for a year.","furigana":"しけん|お|いちねんかんよびこう|べんきょう"}],"notes":"予備校 is a preparatory school that prepares students specifically for entrance exams, above all those for university (大学受験). In particular, 浪人 - school leavers who did not pass on the first attempt - often study there for a year. Unlike a 塾, a 予備校 is aimed mainly at high school students and school graduates.","levelNote":"Compound: 予備 (N2) + 校 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"奨学金をもらって留学しました。","romaji":"Shougakukin o moratte ryuugaku shimashita.","german":"I received a scholarship and studied abroad.","furigana":"しょうがくきん|りゅうがく"},{"japanese":"奨学金に申し込みました。","romaji":"Shōgakukin ni mōshikomimashita.","german":"I applied for a scholarship.","furigana":"しょうがくきん|もう|こ"},{"japanese":"大学を出てから、奨学金を返しています。","romaji":"Daigaku o dete kara, shōgakukin o kaeshite imasu.","german":"Since graduating from university, I have been repaying my student loan.","furigana":"だいがく|で|しょうがくきん|かえ"}],"notes":"奨学金 is financial support for studies: 奨学金をもらう (\"to receive\"), 奨学金に申し込む (\"to apply\"). Note: in Japan many 奨学金 are loans that must be repaid after the studies (奨学金を返す). A scholarship without repayment is more precisely called 給付型奨学金.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 16"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"アルバイトを探しています。","romaji":"Arubaito o sagashite imasu.","german":"Ich suche einen Nebenjob.","furigana":"さが"},{"japanese":"レストランでアルバイトをしています。","romaji":"Resutoran de arubaito o shite imasu.","german":"Ich habe einen Nebenjob in einem Restaurant."},{"japanese":"アルバイトの時給は千二百円です。","romaji":"Arubaito no jikyū wa sen nihyaku en desu.","german":"Der Stundenlohn im Nebenjob beträgt 1.200 Yen.","furigana":"じきゅう|せんにひゃくえん"}],"notes":"アルバイト (vom deutschen „Arbeit“) ist ein Nebenjob, vor allem von Schülern und Studenten; umgangssprachlich kurz バイト. Man sagt アルバイトをする und gibt den Arbeitsort mit で an: コンビニでアルバイトをする. Bezahlt wird meist pro Stunde (時給); Teilzeitarbeit von Erwachsenen, etwa Hausfrauen, heißt eher パート.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 9"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ボランティアに参加します。","romaji":"Borantia ni sanka shimasu.","german":"I'm taking part in volunteer work.","furigana":"さんか"},{"japanese":"週末にボランティアをしています。","romaji":"Shūmatsu ni borantia o shite imasu.","german":"I do volunteer work on weekends.","furigana":"しゅうまつ"},{"japanese":"大会でボランティアとして働きました。","romaji":"Taikai de borantia to shite hatarakimashita.","german":"I worked as a volunteer at the tournament.","furigana":"たいかい|はたら"}],"notes":"ボランティア means both the voluntary activity (ボランティアをする, ボランティアに参加する) and the volunteer person (ボランティアとして働く, ボランティアの人). Common compounds are ボランティア活動 (\"voluntary activity\") and ボランティア団体 (\"aid organization\"). It is always understood that the work is unpaid.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 26"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"夏にキャンプに行きます。","romaji":"Natsu ni kyanpu ni ikimasu.","german":"I'm going camping in the summer.","furigana":"なつ|い"},{"japanese":"山でキャンプをしました。","romaji":"Yama de kyanpu o shimashita.","german":"We camped in the mountains.","furigana":"やま"},{"japanese":"キャンプ場でカレーを作りました。","romaji":"Kyanpujō de karē o tsukurimashita.","german":"We cooked curry at the campsite.","furigana":"じょう|つく"}],"notes":"キャンプ is camping: キャンプに行く (\"to go camping\") or キャンプをする. The campground is キャンプ場, a campfire キャンプファイヤー. In sports, キャンプ also denotes a training camp, such as 春季キャンプ in professional baseball.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 11"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"父と一緒に釣りに行きました。","romaji":"Chichi to issho ni tsuri ni ikimashita.","german":"I went fishing with my father.","furigana":"ちち|いっしょ|つ|い"},{"japanese":"趣味は釣りです。","romaji":"Shumi wa tsuri desu.","german":"My hobby is fishing.","furigana":"しゅみ|つ"},{"japanese":"この川は釣り禁止です。","romaji":"Kono kawa wa tsuri kinshi desu.","german":"Fishing is prohibited at this river.","furigana":"かわ|つ|きんし"}],"notes":"釣り is fishing as an activity and hobby; with the purpose-に you say 釣りに行く (\"to go fishing\"). The related verb is 釣る: 魚を釣る (\"to catch a fish\"). Note: as おつり (usually in kana) the same word means \"change (money)\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 13"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"富士山に登山します。","romaji":"Fujisan ni tozan shimasu.","german":"I'm going to climb Mt. Fuji.","furigana":"ふじさん|とざん"},{"japanese":"週末に登山をする予定です。","romaji":"Shūmatsu ni tozan o suru yotei desu.","german":"I plan to go mountain climbing on the weekend.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|とざん|よてい"},{"japanese":"登山の前に、天気をよく調べてください。","romaji":"Tozan no mae ni, tenki o yoku shirabete kudasai.","german":"Please check the weather carefully before a climb.","furigana":"とざん|まえ|てんき|しら"}],"notes":"登山 covers mountaineering as well as hiking in the mountains. The verb 登山する sounds somewhat more elevated; in everyday speech people often simply say 山に登る. The mountain can be given with に: 富士山に登山する. 登山道 (\"mountain trail\") and 登山靴 (\"hiking boots\") go with it.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"水泳が得意です。","romaji":"Suiei ga tokui desu.","german":"I'm good at swimming.","furigana":"すいえい|とくい"},{"japanese":"水泳は体にいい運動です。","romaji":"Suiei wa karada ni ii undō desu.","german":"Swimming is good exercise for the body.","furigana":"すいえい|からだ|うんどう"},{"japanese":"毎週、水泳教室に通っています。","romaji":"Maishū, suiei kyōshitsu ni kayotte imasu.","german":"I go to a swimming class every week.","furigana":"まいしゅう|すいえいきょうしつ|かよ"}],"notes":"水泳 is swimming as a sport or school subject: 水泳が得意, 水泳教室 (\"swimming class\"), 水泳部 (\"swimming club\"). For the concrete activity \"to swim\", the verb 泳ぐ is usually used: プールで泳ぐ. Competitions are 水泳大会.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 36"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"陸上部に入っています。","romaji":"Rikujōbu ni haitte imasu.","german":"I'm in the track and field club.","furigana":"りくじょうぶ|はい"},{"japanese":"陸上の大会で百メートルを走りました。","romaji":"Rikujō no taikai de hyaku mētoru o hashirimashita.","german":"I ran the 100 meters at a track and field meet.","furigana":"りくじょう|たいかい|ひゃく|はし"}],"notes":"陸上 literally means \"on land\". In sports it is the short form of 陸上競技, that is, track and field; this card practices the sports usage. 陸上部 is a corresponding club or school group, 陸上の大会 a competition. The reading is りくじょう, with a long じょう.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"全国大会に出場します。","romaji":"Zenkoku taikai ni shutsujō shimasu.","german":"I'm taking part in the national tournament.","furigana":"ぜんこくたいかい|しゅつじょう"},{"japanese":"来月サッカーの大会があります。","romaji":"Raigetsu sakkā no taikai ga arimasu.","german":"Next month there is a soccer tournament.","furigana":"らいげつ|たいかい"},{"japanese":"夏に花火大会を見に行きました。","romaji":"Natsu ni hanabi taikai o mi ni ikimashita.","german":"In summer we went to see a fireworks festival.","furigana":"なつ|はなびたいかい|み|い"}],"notes":"大会 is a tournament or competition, but also a large event: サッカーの大会, 全国大会 (\"national tournament\"), 花火大会 (\"fireworks festival\"). To take part is 大会に出る or more formally 大会に出場する. The school sports festival, by contrast, is the 運動会.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 40"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"優勝おめでとうございます。","romaji":"Yūshō omedetō gozaimasu.","german":"Congratulations on the victory.","furigana":"ゆうしょう"},{"japanese":"チームが優勝しました。","romaji":"Chīmu ga yūshō shimashita.","german":"The team won the championship.","furigana":"ゆうしょう"},{"japanese":"一回戦には勝ちましたが、優勝はできませんでした。","romaji":"Ikkaisen ni wa kachimashita ga, yūshō wa dekimasen deshita.","german":"We won the first round, but we couldn't win the title.","furigana":"いっかいせん|か|ゆうしょう"}],"notes":"優勝 is the overall victory or title win in a tournament or league, not the win in a single game (for that, 勝つ). As a verb you say 大会で優勝する (\"to win the tournament\"). Congratulations are expressed with 優勝おめでとうございます; the winning team is the 優勝チーム.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"試合に勝ちました。","romaji":"Shiai ni kachimashita.","german":"I won the match.","furigana":"しあい|か"},{"japanese":"兄にテニスで勝ちました。","romaji":"Ani ni tenisu de kachimashita.","german":"I beat my older brother at tennis.","furigana":"あに|か"},{"japanese":"勝っても負けても、楽しかったです。","romaji":"Katte mo makete mo, tanoshikatta desu.","german":"Whether we won or lost, it was fun.","furigana":"か|ま|たの"}],"notes":"勝つ is intransitive: whom or what you defeat takes に, never を: 試合に勝つ, 相手に勝つ. The discipline is given with で: テニスで勝つ. The て-form is 勝って; the opposite is 負ける, and winning a tournament title is called 優勝する.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 22"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"試合に負けて悔しいです。","romaji":"Shiai ni makete kuyashii desu.","german":"I'm frustrated about losing the match.","furigana":"しあい|ま|くや"},{"japanese":"暑さに負けないで、頑張ってください。","romaji":"Atsusa ni makenaide, ganbatte kudasai.","german":"Don't let the heat get you down, keep going!","furigana":"あつ|ま|がんば"},{"japanese":"少し負けてくれませんか。","romaji":"Sukoshi makete kuremasen ka.","german":"Could you give me a little discount?","furigana":"すこ|ま"}],"notes":"負ける is intransitive: what or whom you lose to takes に: 試合に負ける, 相手に負ける. Figuratively it means \"to give in, to succumb\": 暑さに負けない (\"not to let the heat get one down\"). When shopping, 負ける also means \"to give a discount\": 少し負けてください.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 22"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"試合は引き分けでした。","romaji":"Shiai wa hikiwake deshita.","german":"The game ended in a draw.","furigana":"しあい|ひ|わ"},{"japanese":"引き分けなら、両チームに勝ち点が一つずつ入ります。","romaji":"Hikiwake nara, ryō chīmu ni kachiten ga hitotsu zutsu hairimasu.","german":"In case of a draw, each team gets one point.","furigana":"ひ|わ|りょう|か|てん|ひと|はい"}],"notes":"引き分け is the result of a contest in which neither side wins. 試合は引き分けでした reports a drawn game; 引き分けに終わる means that it ends in a draw. It is a noun; the verb 引き分ける has its own form and is not used here as a card reading.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"チームを応援します。","romaji":"Chīmu o ōen shimasu.","german":"I'm cheering for the team.","furigana":"おうえん"},{"japanese":"試験、頑張ってね。応援してるよ。","romaji":"Shiken, ganbatte ne. Ōen shiteru yo.","german":"Good luck on the exam! I'm rooting for you.","furigana":"しけん|がんば|おうえん"},{"japanese":"両親が私の留学を応援してくれました。","romaji":"Ryōshin ga watashi no ryūgaku o ōen shite kuremashita.","german":"My parents supported my study abroad.","furigana":"りょうしん|わたし|りゅうがく|おうえん"}],"notes":"応援する means \"to cheer on\" in sports, but also quite generally \"to support, to keep one's fingers crossed\". Whom or what one supports takes を: チームを応援する, 夢を応援する. Among friends you encourage with 応援してるよ (\"I'm rooting for you\"). An organized cheering section is called 応援団.","levelNote":"Derived from 応援 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新記録を出しました。","romaji":"Shinkiroku o dashimashita.","german":"A new record was set.","furigana":"しんきろく|だ"},{"japanese":"毎日、体重を記録しています。","romaji":"Mainichi, taijū o kiroku shite imasu.","german":"I record my weight every day.","furigana":"まいにち|たいじゅう|きろく"},{"japanese":"会議の内容を記録してください。","romaji":"Kaigi no naiyō o kiroku shite kudasai.","german":"Please take minutes of the meeting.","furigana":"かいぎ|ないよう|きろく"}],"notes":"記録 has two main meanings: \"record, minutes\" (記録する \"to record, to note down\") and \"record\" in sports. For records you say 記録を出す (\"to set\"), 記録を破る／更新する (\"to break, to improve\") and 新記録 (\"new record\"). 記録的な means \"record-breaking\", e.g. 記録的な大雨 (\"record rainfall\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本は後半に得点しました。","romaji":"Nihon wa kōhan ni tokuten shimashita.","german":"Japan scored in the second half.","furigana":"にほん|こうはん|とくてん"},{"japanese":"彼はチームで一番得点が多い選手です。","romaji":"Kare wa chīmu de ichiban tokuten ga ōi senshu desu.","german":"He is the player with the most points on the team.","furigana":"かれ|いちばんとくてん|おお|せんしゅ"}],"notes":"得点 is a point scored or the score achieved, such as a goal in soccer or a run in baseball. As a verb, 得点する means \"to score, to score a goal\"; its counterpart, a goal conceded, is 失点. For test results and school grades, everyday speech prefers 点数 or simply 点.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"審判の判定に従います。","romaji":"Shinpan no hantei ni shitagaimasu.","german":"I follow the referee's decision.","furigana":"しんぱん|はんてい|したが"},{"japanese":"兄はサッカーの試合で審判をしています。","romaji":"Ani wa sakkā no shiai de shinpan o shite imasu.","german":"My older brother referees soccer matches.","furigana":"あに|しあい|しんぱん"},{"japanese":"審判が笛を吹いて、試合が始まりました。","romaji":"Shinpan ga fue o fuite, shiai ga hajimarimashita.","german":"The referee blew the whistle, and the game started.","furigana":"しんぱん|ふえ|ふ|しあい|はじ"}],"notes":"審判 is the referee in sports and also the act of refereeing itself: 審判をする (\"to be a referee, to officiate\"). His decision is called 審判の判定. In a figurative sense 審判 means \"judgment\", e.g. 最後の審判 (\"the Last Judgment\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"有名な選手に会いました。","romaji":"Yūmei na senshu ni aimashita.","german":"I met a famous athlete.","furigana":"ゆうめい|せんしゅ|あ"},{"japanese":"あの選手はとても速いです。","romaji":"Ano senshu wa totemo hayai desu.","german":"That player is very fast.","furigana":"せんしゅ|はや"},{"japanese":"将来、サッカー選手になりたいです。","romaji":"Shōrai, sakkā senshu ni naritai desu.","german":"I want to become a soccer player in the future.","furigana":"しょうらい|せんしゅ"}],"notes":"選手 is an athlete or player in competitive sports: サッカー選手, 野球選手, 代表選手 (\"national team player\"); in general people say スポーツ選手. In the media, 選手 is added respectfully after names: 大谷選手. It is not used for musicians or actors.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"監督の指示に従います。","romaji":"Kantoku no shiji ni shitagaimasu.","german":"I follow the coach's instructions.","furigana":"かんとく|しじ|したが"},{"japanese":"映画の監督に会いました。","romaji":"Eiga no kantoku ni aimashita.","german":"I met the film director.","furigana":"えいが|かんとく|あ"},{"japanese":"先生が試験を監督しています。","romaji":"Sensei ga shiken o kantoku shite imasu.","german":"The teacher is proctoring the exam.","furigana":"せんせい|しけん|かんとく"}],"notes":"監督 is the person in charge: in film the director (映画監督), in sports the head coach or team manager; assistant coaches, by contrast, are called コーチ. As a suru-verb, 監督する means \"to supervise, oversee\": 試験を監督する (\"to proctor the exam\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は有名な歌手のファンです。","romaji":"Kanojo wa yuumei na kashu no fan desu.","german":"She is a fan of a famous singer.","furigana":"かのじょ|ゆうめい|かしゅ"},{"japanese":"サッカーのファンがたくさん集まりました。","romaji":"Sakkā no fan ga takusan atsumarimashita.","german":"Many soccer fans gathered.","furigana":"あつ"}],"notes":"ファン is a fan or supporter: 〜のファンです (\"I am a fan of ...\"), 〜のファンになる (\"to become a fan of ...\"). Organized fans meet in a ファンクラブ. Written the same way is another word for a fan or ventilator (パソコンのファン); this card means the fan (supporter).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この店は人気があります。","romaji":"Kono mise wa ninki ga arimasu.","german":"This shop is popular.","furigana":"みせ|にんき"},{"japanese":"彼女はクラスの人気者です。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kurasu no ninkimono desu.","german":"She is popular in her class.","furigana":"かのじょ|にんきもの"},{"japanese":"このゲームは子供たちの間で人気が出ました。","romaji":"Kono gēmu wa kodomotachi no aida de ninki ga demashita.","german":"This game has become popular among children.","furigana":"こども|あいだ|にんき|で"}],"notes":"人気 means \"popularity\": 人気がある (\"to be popular\"), 人気の店 (\"popular shop\"); among whom is given with に: 若い人に人気がある. To become popular is 人気が出る, a popular person is a 人気者. Note: read as ひとけ, 人気 means \"presence of people\", for example 人気のない道 (\"deserted road\").","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 28"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今年の流行は何ですか。","romaji":"Kotoshi no ryuukou wa nan desu ka.","german":"What is the trend this year?","furigana":"ことし|りゅうこう|なん"},{"japanese":"姉はいつも流行の服を着ています。","romaji":"Ane wa itsumo ryuukou no fuku o kite imasu.","german":"My older sister always wears fashionable clothes.","furigana":"あね|りゅうこう|ふく|き"},{"japanese":"今、学校でインフルエンザが流行しています。","romaji":"Ima, gakkou de infuruenza ga ryuukou shite imasu.","german":"The flu is going around at school right now.","furigana":"いま|がっこう|りゅうこう"}],"notes":"流行 refers to what is currently \"in fashion\" or \"trending\": 流行の服, 流行に乗る \"to follow the trend\", 流行遅れ \"out of fashion\". As an intransitive する-verb, 流行する means \"to be in fashion\", but also \"to be rampant\" for diseases (インフルエンザが流行する). In everyday speech the verb is often heard as the identically written 流行る (はやる), which sounds more colloquial. ブーム is a short-lived hype, while ファッション refers only to clothing.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"話題を変えましょう。","romaji":"Wadai o kaemashō.","german":"Let's change the subject.","furigana":"わだい|か"},{"japanese":"それは面白い話題ですね。","romaji":"Sore wa omoshiroi wadai desu ne.","german":"That is an interesting topic.","furigana":"おもしろ|わだい"},{"japanese":"駅前に話題のカフェができました。","romaji":"Ekimae ni wadai no kafe ga dekimashita.","german":"A café that everyone is talking about has opened in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|わだい"}],"notes":"話題 is the topic being talked about: 話題を変える \"to change the subject\", 話題になる \"to get people talking, make headlines\". With の before a noun it means \"much discussed, trendy\": 話題の映画, 話題の店. For the theme of an essay or lecture one says テーマ rather, for a title 題 or タイトル; 話 alone means \"talk, story\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"本音を言ってください。","romaji":"Honne o itte kudasai.","german":"Please tell me what you really think.","furigana":"ほんね|い"},{"japanese":"日本では、本音と建前を使い分けることがあります。","romaji":"Nihon de wa, honne to tatemae o tsukaiwakeru koto ga arimasu.","german":"In Japan, people sometimes show their real opinion or the position they present outwardly, depending on the situation.","furigana":"にほん|ほんね|たてまえ|つか|わ"}],"notes":"本音 is what someone actually thinks or intends. 本音を言う asks for an honest personal statement. The opposite 建前 refers to the position or reason presented outwardly; the two can diverge depending on the situation. This is not a claim that all people in Japan behave the same way.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"建前と本音は違います。","romaji":"Tatemae to honne wa chigaimasu.","german":"The façade and the real opinion are different.","furigana":"たてまえ|ほんね|ちが"},{"japanese":"日本では建前が大切です。","romaji":"Nihon de wa tatemae ga taisetsu desu.","german":"In Japan, the official façade is important.","furigana":"にほん|たてまえ|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"建前ではなく、本音を聞かせてください。","romaji":"Tatemae de wa naku, honne o kikasete kudasai.","german":"Don't give me the official version; tell me what you really think.","furigana":"たてまえ|ほんね|き"}],"notes":"建前 is the outwardly presented, socially expected position - as opposed to 本音, the real opinion. The pair 建前と本音 is a key concept in Japanese communication; 建前を言う means \"to say what one is supposed to say\", and 建前上 means \"officially, in form\". The word is not necessarily negative, but describes polite consideration. The second meaning, \"topping-out ceremony when building a house\", is rare today.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は空気を読むのが上手だ。","romaji":"Kare wa kūki o yomu no ga jōzu da.","german":"He is good at reading the room.","furigana":"かれ|くうき|よ|じょうず"},{"japanese":"みんな疲れていたので、空気を読んで話を短く切り上げた。","romaji":"Minna tsukarete ita node, kūki o yonde hanashi o mijikaku kiriageta.","german":"Everyone was tired, so I read the room and cut my talk short.","furigana":"つか|くうき|よ|はなし|みじか|き|あ"},{"japanese":"空気が読めない人だと思われたくない。","romaji":"Kūki ga yomenai hito da to omowaretaku nai.","german":"I don't want to be seen as someone who can't read the room.","furigana":"くうき|よ|ひと|おも"}],"notes":"Literally \"to read the air\": to grasp the unspoken mood and expectations of a group and adjust one's behavior accordingly, for example by staying quiet, giving in, or ending a conversation. Typical patterns are 空気を読んで〜する and 空気を読むのが上手／苦手. The potential form 空気が読めない criticizes a lack of tact and can be hurtful; the related 場の空気 means \"the atmosphere in the room\". Colloquial to neutral; it refers to a social judgment, not literal reading.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"目上の人に敬語を使います。","romaji":"Meue no hito ni keigo o tsukaimasu.","german":"I use honorific language with my superiors.","furigana":"めうえ|ひと|けいご|つか"},{"japanese":"目上の人より先に座らないほうがいいです。","romaji":"Meue no hito yori saki ni suwaranai hō ga ii desu.","german":"It is better not to sit down before your seniors.","furigana":"めうえ|ひと|さき|すわ"}],"notes":"目上 refers to people who rank higher in a given relationship through age or position. It is usually followed by の人: 目上の人. With such people one uses respectful language depending on the situation. The example sentences deal with linguistic and social manners; the expression alone does not set a fixed rule for every encounter.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"目下の人にも丁寧に接します。","romaji":"Meshita no hito ni mo teinei ni sesshimasu.","german":"I am polite even to those junior to me.","furigana":"めした|ひと|ていねい|せっ"},{"japanese":"目上の人と目下の人では話し方が違います。","romaji":"Meue no hito to meshita no hito de wa hanashikata ga chigaimasu.","german":"You speak differently to those above you and those below you.","furigana":"めうえ|ひと|めした|ひと|はな|かた|ちが"},{"japanese":"部長は目下の人の意見もよく聞きます。","romaji":"Buchou wa meshita no hito no iken mo yoku kikimasu.","german":"The department head also listens to the opinions of his subordinates.","furigana":"ぶちょう|めした|ひと|いけん|き"}],"notes":"目下 (めした) are people who are below the speaker in rank, age or position; the opposite is 目上. It usually appears with の人 or の者: 目下の人. The distinction determines which language you choose - for example, you say ご苦労さま only to a 目下, not to superiors. For subordinates in a company, 部下 is more concrete. Note: the same 目下 read as もっか means \"at present\" in written language.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"握手をしましょう。","romaji":"Akushu o shimashō.","german":"Let's shake hands.","furigana":"あくしゅ"},{"japanese":"会った時に握手をしました。","romaji":"Atta toki ni akushu o shimashita.","german":"We shook hands when we met.","furigana":"あ|とき|あくしゅ"},{"japanese":"有名な歌手と握手できて、うれしかったです。","romaji":"Yuumei na kashu to akushu dekite, ureshikatta desu.","german":"I was delighted to be able to shake hands with a famous singer.","furigana":"ゆうめい|かしゅ|あくしゅ"}],"notes":"握手 is the handshake; as a verb 握手する or 握手をする, with the partner marked by と: 先生と握手する. In Japan people usually greet each other with a bow (お辞儀); handshakes occur mostly in business with foreigners, in politics, in sports, and at fan events (握手会). Figuratively, 握手 can also mean \"reconciliation, cooperation\", for example between countries or companies.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ごちそうさまでした。とても美味しかったです。","romaji":"Gochisousama deshita. Totemo oishikatta desu.","german":"Thank you for the meal. It was delicious.","furigana":"おい"},{"japanese":"先輩、ごちそうさまでした。","romaji":"Senpai, gochisousama deshita.","german":"Thank you for the meal, senior colleague.","furigana":"せんぱい"},{"japanese":"お母さん、ごちそうさま！","romaji":"Okaasan, gochisousama!","german":"Thanks for the meal, Mom!","furigana":"かあ"}],"notes":"ごちそうさま is said after the meal, as the counterpart of いただきます before. The polite and most common form is ごちそうさまでした; among family and friends ごちそうさま is enough. You also use it to thank someone who invited you or paid, and to the staff when leaving small restaurants. The kanji spelling ご馳走様 is rare. It is also said jokingly when a couple is being lovey-dovey in front of you.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 0"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"行ってきます。","romaji":"Itte kimasu.","german":"I'm off. (said when leaving)","furigana":"い"},{"japanese":"行ってきます。夕方に帰ります。","romaji":"Itte kimasu. Yūgata ni kaerimasu.","german":"I'm off. I'll be back in the evening.","furigana":"い|ゆうがた|かえ"},{"japanese":"ちょっと郵便局まで行ってきます。","romaji":"Chotto yuubinkyoku made itte kimasu.","german":"I'm just going to the post office.","furigana":"ゆうびんきょく|い"}],"notes":"行ってきます is said when leaving home or the office when you will come back; literally \"I go and come back\" (行って + 来る). The reply from those staying behind is 行ってらっしゃい, and on returning ただいま and おかえりなさい follow. To superiors, 行ってまいります is more polite. Someone who leaves for good does not say 行ってきます but さようなら or 失礼します.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 0"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どうぞよろしくお願いします。","romaji":"Dōzo yoroshiku onegai shimasu.","german":"Ich freue mich auf die Zusammenarbeit.","furigana":"ねが"},{"japanese":"新入社員です。よろしくお願いします。","romaji":"Shinnyūshain desu. Yoroshiku onegai shimasu.","german":"Ich bin ein neuer Mitarbeiter. Freut mich.","furigana":"しんにゅうしゃいん|ねが"},{"japanese":"この書類、よろしくお願いします。","romaji":"Kono shorui, yoroshiku onegai shimasu.","german":"Würden Sie sich bitte um diese Unterlagen kümmern?","furigana":"しょるい|ねが"}],"notes":"よろしくお願いします hat kein genaues deutsches Gegenstück. Am Ende einer Vorstellung (はじめまして…) bedeutet es etwa „freut mich, auf gute Zusammenarbeit“; wenn man jemanden um etwas bittet, heißt es „ich verlasse mich auf Sie, bitte kümmern Sie sich darum“. Es steht auch am Ende fast jeder geschäftlichen E-Mail. Noch höflicher ist どうぞよろしくお願いいたします, unter Freunden reicht よろしく oder よろしくね.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 13"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"気をつけて行ってください。","romaji":"Ki o tsukete itte kudasai.","german":"Please be careful on your way.","furigana":"き|い"},{"japanese":"帰り道、気をつけてね。","romaji":"Kaerimichi, ki o tsukete ne.","german":"Take care on the way home.","furigana":"かえ|みち|き"},{"japanese":"階段が滑りやすいので、気をつけてください。","romaji":"Kaidan ga suberiyasui node, ki o tsukete kudasai.","german":"The stairs are slippery, so please be careful.","furigana":"かいだん|すべ|き"}],"notes":"気をつけて is the て-form of 気をつける \"to be careful\" and serves as a parting phrase (\"take care, get home safely\") and as a warning (\"Watch out!\"). More polite: 気をつけてください or お気をつけて, familiar: 気をつけてね. What one watches out for takes に: 車に気をつけて. The spelling 気を付けて is equally common. 注意する sounds more formal and appears more on signs.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お先に失礼します。また明日。","romaji":"Osaki ni shitsurei shimasu. Mata ashita.","german":"Excuse me for leaving ahead of you. See you tomorrow.","furigana":"さき|しつれい|あした"},{"japanese":"すみません、明日早いので、お先に失礼します。","romaji":"Sumimasen, ashita hayai node, osaki ni shitsurei shimasu.","german":"Sorry, I have to be up early tomorrow, so I'll be leaving before you.","furigana":"あしたはや|さき|しつれい"},{"japanese":"会議の途中ですが、お先に失礼します。","romaji":"Kaigi no tochuu desu ga, osaki ni shitsurei shimasu.","german":"The meeting is still going on, but I'm afraid I have to leave first.","furigana":"かいぎ|とちゅう|さき|しつれい"}],"notes":"お先に失礼します is said when you leave while others are still staying - typically when leaving the office at the end of the day. The usual reply is お疲れさまでした. Among colleagues of equal rank it is shortened to お先に or お先です. Someone who leaves a party or meeting early also uses the phrase. If everyone leaves together, you simply say 失礼します or お疲れさまでした.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 18"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"家具を組み立てます。","romaji":"Kagu o kumitatemasu.","german":"I assemble furniture.","furigana":"かぐ|く|た"},{"japanese":"棚を自分で組み立てました。","romaji":"Tana o jibun de kumitatemashita.","german":"I assembled the shelf myself.","furigana":"たな|じぶん|く|た"},{"japanese":"日本語で文を組み立てるのは難しいです。","romaji":"Nihongo de bun o kumitateru no wa muzukashii desu.","german":"Constructing sentences in Japanese is difficult.","furigana":"にほんご|ぶん|く|た|むずか"}],"notes":"組み立てる means \"to assemble from individual parts\": 家具を組み立てる, 棚を組み立てる. Figuratively, you can also \"build up\" sentences, plans or arguments: 文を組み立てる. The verb is transitive (を); there is no common intransitive counterpart, and the passive 組み立てられる is used instead. The noun 組み立て means \"assembly, structure\", and in compounds it is often written without okurigana: 組立工場. 作る is more general, and 建てる is used for buildings.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"電球を取り替えます。","romaji":"Denkyū o torikaemasu.","german":"I'm replacing the light bulb.","furigana":"でんきゅう|と|か"},{"japanese":"古い部品を新しいものに取り替えます。","romaji":"Furui buhin o atarashii mono ni torikaemasu.","german":"I'm replacing the old parts with new ones.","furigana":"ふる|ぶひん|あたら|と|か"},{"japanese":"友達と席を取り替えました。","romaji":"Tomodachi to seki o torikaemashita.","german":"I swapped seats with my friend.","furigana":"ともだち|せき|と|か"}],"notes":"取り替える means to exchange something for something else or to replace it with something else. The previous object takes を, the replacement takes に: 古い部品を新しいものに取り替える. It is a transitive ichidan verb: 取り替えます・取り替えて・取り替えない. A mutual exchange can be expressed with と.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ロープを強く引っ張ってください。","romaji":"Roopu o tsuyoku hippatte kudasai.","german":"Please pull the rope hard.","furigana":"つよ|ひ|ぱ"},{"japanese":"子供がお母さんの手を引っ張って、おもちゃ売り場に行きました。","romaji":"Kodomo ga okaasan no te o hippatte, omocha uriba ni ikimashita.","german":"The child pulled his mother by the hand to the toy section.","furigana":"こども|かあ|て|ひ|ぱ|う|ば|い"},{"japanese":"キャプテンがチームを引っ張っています。","romaji":"Kyaputen ga chiimu o hippatte imasu.","german":"The captain is leading the whole team.","furigana":"ひ|ぱ"}],"notes":"引っ張る emphasizes pulling strongly or persistently toward oneself: ロープを引っ張る, 袖を引っ張る. 引く is the more neutral base word; that is why doors are labeled 引く (or 引), not 引っ張る. Figuratively, チームを引っ張る means \"to lead the team, carry it along\", and 話を引っ張る \"to drag something out\". The verb is transitive (を); there is no intransitive counterpart, and the passive 引っ張られる is used instead.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"犬が猫を追いかけています。","romaji":"Inu ga neko o oikakete imasu.","german":"The dog is chasing the cat.","furigana":"いぬ|ねこ|お"},{"japanese":"財布を落とした人を追いかけて、渡しました。","romaji":"Saifu o otoshita hito o oikakete, watashimashita.","german":"I ran after the person who had dropped their wallet and gave it back.","furigana":"さいふ|お|ひと|お|わた"},{"japanese":"夢を追いかけて東京に来ました。","romaji":"Yume o oikakete Toukyou ni kimashita.","german":"I came to Tokyo to chase my dream.","furigana":"ゆめ|お|とうきょう|き"}],"notes":"追いかける means \"to run after, to pursue\"; the target takes を: 猫を追いかける. Figuratively, you pursue dreams or trends: 夢を追いかける, 流行を追いかける. 追う is shorter and more literary; 追いつく means \"to catch up with\" and 追い越す \"to overtake\". The spelling 追い掛ける exists, but 追いかける is usual. The person being chased is in the passive: 犬に追いかけられる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"後ろを振り返りました。","romaji":"Ushiro o furikaerimashita.","german":"I turned around.","furigana":"うし|ふ|かえ"},{"japanese":"誰かに呼ばれて振り返りました。","romaji":"Dareka ni yobarete furikaerimashita.","german":"Someone called me and I turned around.","furigana":"だれ|よ|ふ|かえ"},{"japanese":"この一年を振り返ってみましょう。","romaji":"Kono ichinen o furikaette mimashou.","german":"Let's look back on this past year.","furigana":"いちねん|ふ|かえ"}],"notes":"振り返る literally means \"to turn around and look back\": 後ろを振り返る. Figuratively, it means \"to look back on something, to reflect\": 一年を振り返る, 過去を振り返る. 振り向く is purely physical (turning one's head in a direction, e.g. when called), and 戻る means \"to go back\". The noun 振り返り is a \"review, retrospective\" in working life.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子供が道路に飛び出しました。","romaji":"Kodomo ga douro ni tobidashimashita.","german":"A child ran out onto the road.","furigana":"こども|どうろ|と|だ"},{"japanese":"母とけんかして、家を飛び出しました。","romaji":"Haha to kenka shite, ie o tobidashimashita.","german":"I had a fight with my mother and stormed out of the house.","furigana":"はは|いえ|と|だ"},{"japanese":"地震の時、すぐに外に飛び出すのは危ないです。","romaji":"Jishin no toki, sugu ni soto ni tobidasu no wa abunai desu.","german":"In an earthquake, it is dangerous to rush outside right away.","furigana":"じしん|とき|そと|と|だ|あぶ"}],"notes":"飛び出す means \"to suddenly jump out, dash out\"; the destination takes に (道路に飛び出す), the place left takes を (家を飛び出す). A typical traffic warning is 飛び出し注意 \"Caution, children may run into the road\". Figuratively, 家を飛び出す also means \"to run away from home\", and things can \"stick out\". 出る is neutral, while 飛び出す stresses suddenness and momentum. The verb is intransitive.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"なくした鍵が見つかりました。","romaji":"Nakushita kagi ga mitsukarimashita.","german":"Der verlorene Schlüssel wurde gefunden.","furigana":"かぎ|み"},{"japanese":"新しい仕事がなかなか見つかりません。","romaji":"Atarashii shigoto ga nakanaka mitsukarimasen.","german":"Ich finde einfach keinen neuen Job.","furigana":"あたら|しごと|み"},{"japanese":"授業中にスマホを使っていたら、先生に見つかりました。","romaji":"Jugyouchuu ni sumaho o tsukatte itara, sensei ni mitsukarimashita.","german":"Ich habe im Unterricht mein Handy benutzt und wurde vom Lehrer erwischt.","furigana":"じゅぎょうちゅう|つか|せんせい|み"}],"notes":"見つかる ist das intransitive Gegenstück zu 見つける „finden“: Das Gefundene ist Subjekt mit が (鍵が見つかる). Wo man im Deutschen „ich finde keinen Job“ sagt, klingt 仕事が見つからない im Japanischen natürlicher. Steht der Finder mit に, bedeutet es „erwischt werden“: 先生に見つかる. Im Gegensatz zu 見つける wird 見つかる nicht mit を verwendet.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"インターネットに繋がりません。","romaji":"Intānetto ni tsunagarimasen.","german":"I can't connect to the internet.","furigana":"つな"},{"japanese":"この道は駅まで繋がっています。","romaji":"Kono michi wa eki made tsunagatte imasu.","german":"This road leads all the way to the station.","furigana":"みち|えき|つな"}],"notes":"繋がる describes a connection that exists or is being formed. It is an intransitive godan verb: 繋がります・繋がって・繋がらない. The side that is connected to often takes に; for a road, まで can name the place reached. When someone connects things themselves, the transitive counterpart 繋ぐ is used.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ストレスが溜まっています。","romaji":"Sutoresu ga tamatte imasu.","german":"I have a lot of stress built up.","furigana":"た"},{"japanese":"洗濯物が溜まっています。","romaji":"Sentakumono ga tamatte imasu.","german":"The laundry is piling up.","furigana":"せんたくもの|た"},{"japanese":"雨の後、道に水が溜まっています。","romaji":"Ame no ato, michi ni mizu ga tamatte imasu.","german":"After the rain, water has collected on the road.","furigana":"あめ|あと|みち|みず|た"}],"notes":"溜まる (usually written in kana as たまる) describes something gradually accumulating; often in the ている-form as a state. Typical subjects are ストレス, 疲れ, 仕事, 宿題, ごみ, 洗濯物 and water. For money, one writes 貯まる (お金が貯まる \"money builds up\"). The transitive counterpart is 溜める or 貯める: ストレスを溜める, お金を貯める.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"部屋が片付きました。","romaji":"Heya ga katazukimashita.","german":"The room has been tidied up.","furigana":"へや|かたづ"},{"japanese":"仕事がやっと片付きました。","romaji":"Shigoto ga yatto katazukimashita.","german":"The work is finally done.","furigana":"しごと|かたづ"},{"japanese":"この問題はまだ片付いていません。","romaji":"Kono mondai wa mada katazuite imasen.","german":"This problem has not been settled yet.","furigana":"もんだい|かたづ"}],"notes":"片付く is the intransitive counterpart of 片付ける \"to tidy up, to finish\": 部屋を片付ける → 部屋が片付く \"the room is tidy\". It is also used for work or problems that are \"finished\" or \"solved\": 仕事が片付く, 問題が片付く. The ている-form for the state is common: 片付いている部屋. The spelling 片づく is also common. The old meaning \"to be married off (a daughter)\" is rare today.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"服を汚してしまいました。","romaji":"Fuku o yogoshite shimaimashita.","german":"I got my clothes dirty.","furigana":"ふく|よご"},{"japanese":"新しい服を汚さないでね。","romaji":"Atarashii fuku o yogosanaide ne.","german":"Don't get your new clothes dirty.","furigana":"あたら|ふく|よご"},{"japanese":"工場から出る水が川を汚しています。","romaji":"Koujou kara deru mizu ga kawa o yogoshite imasu.","german":"Wastewater from the factory is polluting the river.","furigana":"こうじょう|で|みず|かわ|よご"}],"notes":"汚す is transitive: \"to make something dirty, to pollute\" (服を汚す, 川を汚す). The intransitive counterpart is 汚れる \"to get dirty\" (服が汚れる), the adjective is 汚い \"dirty\". For a mishap, てしまう is often used: 汚してしまった. Note: the same kanji read けがす means, in elevated language, \"to dishonor, to stain\" (名誉を汚す).","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 18"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コーヒーが冷めてしまいました。","romaji":"Koohii ga samete shimaimashita.","german":"The coffee has gone cold.","furigana":"さ"},{"japanese":"冷めないうちに食べてください。","romaji":"Samenai uchi ni tabete kudasai.","german":"Please eat before it gets cold.","furigana":"さ|た"},{"japanese":"彼への気持ちが冷めてしまいました。","romaji":"Kare e no kimochi ga samete shimaimashita.","german":"My feelings for him have cooled.","furigana":"かれ|きも|さ"}],"notes":"冷める describes something warm cooling to normal temperature: スープが冷める, お茶が冷める. The transitive counterpart is 冷ます (お湯を冷ます). 冷える, by contrast, means \"to get cold, to be chilled\" (ビールが冷える, 体が冷える). Set phrase: 冷めないうちにどうぞ \"please, while it's still warm\". Figuratively, feelings or enthusiasm cool off: 気持ちが冷める. Not to be confused with 覚める \"to wake up\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"パンがきれいに焼けました。","romaji":"Pan ga kirei ni yakemashita.","german":"The bread baked nicely.","furigana":"や"},{"japanese":"海で一日遊んで、顔が真っ赤に焼けました。","romaji":"Umi de ichinichi asonde, kao ga makka ni yakemashita.","german":"After a day at the sea, my face got bright red from the sun.","furigana":"うみ|いちにちあそ|かお|ま|か|や"},{"japanese":"火事で古いお寺が焼けてしまいました。","romaji":"Kaji de furui otera ga yakete shimaimashita.","german":"An old temple burned down in a fire.","furigana":"かじ|ふる|てら|や"}],"notes":"焼ける is the intransitive counterpart of 焼く: パンを焼く → パンが焼ける \"the bread is done baking\". It also describes something burning down (火事で家が焼ける) or getting tanned or sunburned by the sun (日に焼ける, noun 日焼け). 燃える means \"to burn, to be in flames\", whereas 焼ける emphasizes the result. For meat: よく焼けている \"well done\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"天ぷらを揚げます。","romaji":"Tenpura o agemasu.","german":"I deep-fry tempura.","furigana":"てん|あ"},{"japanese":"鶏肉を油で揚げて、から揚げを作りました。","romaji":"Toriniku o abura de agete, karaage o tsukurimashita.","german":"I deep-fried chicken in oil and made karaage.","furigana":"とりにく|あぶら|あ|あ|つく"},{"japanese":"子供たちが公園でたこを揚げています。","romaji":"Kodomotachi ga kouen de tako o agete imasu.","german":"The children are flying kites in the park.","furigana":"こども|こうえん|あ"}],"notes":"揚げる means \"to deep-fry in oil\" in cooking: 天ぷらを揚げる, 油で揚げる. From it come 揚げ物 \"fried food\" and 揚げたて \"freshly fried\". The intransitive counterpart is 揚がる (天ぷらが揚がる). 揚げる is also used for flying kites or hoisting flags (たこを揚げる). Not to be confused with the homophones 上げる \"to raise\" and あげる \"to give\"; 焼く is \"to grill, to bake\", 炒める \"to stir-fry\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"野菜を蒸して食べます。","romaji":"Yasai o mushite tabemasu.","german":"I steam vegetables and eat them.","furigana":"やさい|む|た"},{"japanese":"もち米を蒸して、お餅を作ります。","romaji":"Mochigome o mushite, omochi o tsukurimasu.","german":"You steam glutinous rice to make mochi.","furigana":"ごめ|む|もち|つく"},{"japanese":"今日はずいぶん蒸しますね。","romaji":"Kyou wa zuibun mushimasu ne.","german":"It sure is muggy today, isn't it?","furigana":"きょう|む"}],"notes":"蒸す means \"to steam\" when transitive: 野菜を蒸す, 蒸し器 \"steamer\", 茶碗蒸し \"steamed egg custard\". Intransitively it means \"to be muggy\": 今日は蒸しますね; the adjective 蒸し暑い is more usual for this. 煮る is \"to simmer in liquid\", ゆでる \"to boil in water\". Related is 蒸れる \"to become hot and damp\" (e.g., feet in shoes).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"筋肉が痛いです。","romaji":"Kinniku ga itai desu.","german":"My muscles hurt.","furigana":"きんにく|いた"},{"japanese":"運動して筋肉をつけたいです。","romaji":"Undō shite kinniku o tsuketai desu.","german":"I want to build muscle by exercising.","furigana":"うんどう|きんにく"},{"japanese":"年を取ると筋肉が落ちます。","romaji":"Toshi o toru to kinniku ga ochimasu.","german":"When you get older, you lose muscle.","furigana":"とし|と|きんにく|お"}],"notes":"筋肉 is a muscle or the musculature. Muscles are \"put on\": 筋肉をつける (transitive) or 筋肉がつく (intransitive); losing them is 筋肉が落ちる. Sore muscles are 筋肉痛, and strength training is colloquially 筋トレ. 筋 (すじ) alone means \"tendon, fiber\" or the \"plot\" of a story.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"転んで骨を折りました。","romaji":"Koronde hone o orimashita.","german":"I fell and broke a bone.","furigana":"ころ|ほね|お"},{"japanese":"この魚は骨が多いので、気をつけて食べてください。","romaji":"Kono sakana wa hone ga ooi node, ki o tsukete tabete kudasai.","german":"This fish has many bones, so please eat carefully.","furigana":"さかな|ほね|おお|き|た"},{"japanese":"この仕事は骨が折れます。","romaji":"Kono shigoto wa hone ga oremasu.","german":"This job is quite a lot of work.","furigana":"しごと|ほね|お"}],"notes":"骨 is a bone; in fish also a fish bone (魚の骨). Breaking one is 足の骨を折る or, medically, 骨折する. Idiomatically, 骨が折れる means \"to be hard work\" and 骨を折る \"to go to great pains\"; context decides. Frames also have 骨, e.g., 傘の骨 \"umbrella ribs\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"胃が痛いです。","romaji":"I ga itai desu.","german":"My stomach hurts.","furigana":"い|いた"},{"japanese":"食べすぎて、胃の調子が悪いです。","romaji":"Tabesugite, i no chōshi ga warui desu.","german":"I ate too much, and my stomach feels bad.","furigana":"た|い|ちょうし|わる"},{"japanese":"図で胃の位置を確認しました。","romaji":"Zu de i no ichi o kakunin shimashita.","german":"I checked where the stomach is located in the diagram.","furigana":"ず|い|いち|かくにん"}],"notes":"胃 is the stomach as an internal organ; 腹 or お腹, by contrast, refers more generally to the abdominal area. 胃が痛い names stomach pain, 胃の調子が悪い an upset stomach. The reading consists of a single mora い. The card does not cover an astronomical meaning.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 32"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"肺のレントゲンを撮りました。","romaji":"Hai no rentogen o torimashita.","german":"I had a chest X-ray taken.","furigana":"はい|と"},{"japanese":"たばこは肺に悪いです。","romaji":"Tabako wa hai ni warui desu.","german":"Cigarettes are bad for the lungs.","furigana":"はい|わる"}],"notes":"肺 is the lung as a breathing organ. 肺のレントゲン refers to an X-ray of this organ. In 肺に悪い, に marks what is harmed by something. The identically sounding はい as the answer \"yes\" is a different word.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"心臓が速く動いています。","romaji":"Shinzō ga hayaku ugoite imasu.","german":"My heart is beating fast.","furigana":"しんぞう|はや|うご"},{"japanese":"祖父は心臓の手術を受けました。","romaji":"Sofu wa shinzō no shujutsu o ukemashita.","german":"My grandfather had heart surgery.","furigana":"そふ|しんぞう|しゅじゅつ|う"}],"notes":"心臓 refers here to the heart as a bodily organ. 心臓が動く describes its activity, 心臓の手術 an operation on this organ. For inner feelings or the mind, 心 is used. This card does not practice the figurative meanings \"nerve, cheek\" or \"center\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"血液検査を受けました。","romaji":"Ketsueki kensa o ukemashita.","german":"I had a blood test.","furigana":"けつえきけんさ|う"},{"japanese":"血液型は何型ですか。","romaji":"Ketsuekigata wa nanigata desu ka.","german":"What is your blood type?","furigana":"けつえきがた|なにがた"},{"japanese":"運動すると血液の流れがよくなります。","romaji":"Undou suru to ketsueki no nagare ga yoku narimasu.","german":"Exercise improves blood circulation.","furigana":"うんどう|けつえき|なが"}],"notes":"血液 is the technical, medical word for \"blood\" and appears mainly in compounds: 血液検査 \"blood test\", 血液型 \"blood type\". In daily life one says 血 (ち): 血が出る \"to bleed\", not 血液が出る. People in Japan like to ask about blood type because it is associated with personality. Donating blood is 献血.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"虫歯があるので歯医者に行きます。","romaji":"Mushiba ga aru node haisha ni ikimasu.","german":"I have a cavity, so I'm going to the dentist.","furigana":"むしば|はいしゃ|い"},{"japanese":"甘い物を食べすぎると虫歯になりますよ。","romaji":"Amai mono o tabesugiru to mushiba ni narimasu yo.","german":"If you eat too many sweets, you'll get cavities.","furigana":"あま|もの|た|むしば"},{"japanese":"歯医者さんに虫歯を一本抜いてもらいました。","romaji":"Haisha-san ni mushiba o ippon nuite moraimashita.","german":"The dentist pulled one bad tooth for me.","furigana":"はいしゃ|むしば|いっぽんぬ"}],"notes":"虫歯 means both tooth decay and the decayed tooth itself; it is counted with 本: 虫歯が一本ある. Typical are 虫歯になる \"to get a cavity\", 虫歯がある and 虫歯が痛い. It is treated at the 歯医者. Literally the word means \"insect tooth\", from the old belief that worms gnaw at the tooth.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"血圧が高いです。","romaji":"Ketsuatsu ga takai desu.","german":"My blood pressure is high.","furigana":"けつあつ|たか"},{"japanese":"毎朝血圧を測っています。","romaji":"Maiasa ketsuatsu o hakatte imasu.","german":"I measure my blood pressure every morning.","furigana":"まいあさけつあつ|はか"},{"japanese":"血圧が低くて、朝起きるのがつらいです。","romaji":"Ketsuatsu ga hikukute, asa okiru no ga tsurai desu.","german":"My blood pressure is low, so getting up in the morning is hard.","furigana":"けつあつ|ひく|あさお"}],"notes":"血圧 is blood pressure. It is 高い or 低い (not 大きい/小さい) and can 上がる \"rise\" or 下がる \"fall\". Measuring is 血圧を測る. Medical terms include 高血圧 \"high blood pressure\" and 低血圧 \"low blood pressure\". At the doctor's you often hear: 血圧を測りましょう.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"症状を教えてください。","romaji":"Shōjō o oshiete kudasai.","german":"Please tell me your symptoms.","furigana":"しょうじょう|おし"},{"japanese":"どんな症状がありますか。","romaji":"Donna shōjō ga arimasu ka.","german":"What symptoms do you have?","furigana":"しょうじょう"},{"japanese":"薬を飲んだら、症状が軽くなりました。","romaji":"Kusuri o nondara, shoujou ga karuku narimashita.","german":"After I took the medicine, my symptoms got milder.","furigana":"くすり|の|しょうじょう|かる"}],"notes":"症状 is the medical word for \"symptoms, complaints\": 風邪の症状, 症状がある, 症状が出る, 症状が重い/軽い. At the doctor's you are asked: どんな症状ですか. For general well-being one says 具合 or 調子 (具合が悪い). 症状 does not fit non-medical \"signs\"; for those one uses e.g. サイン.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"剣道の大会で優勝しました。","romaji":"Kendou no taikai de yuushou shimashita.","german":"I won the kendo tournament.","furigana":"けんどう|たいかい|ゆうしょう"},{"japanese":"中学校で三年間剣道をやっていました。","romaji":"Chuugakkou de sannenkan kendou o yatte imashita.","german":"I did kendo for three years in junior high school.","furigana":"ちゅうがっこう|さんねんかんけんどう"},{"japanese":"剣道の道場に週二回通っています。","romaji":"Kendou no doujou ni shuu nikai kayotte imasu.","german":"I go to the kendo dojo twice a week.","furigana":"けんどう|どうじょう|しゅうにかいかよ"}],"notes":"剣道 is the Japanese art of fencing with a bamboo sword (竹刀) and protective armor (防具). You say 剣道をする, 剣道をやる or 剣道を習う; schools have a 剣道部. Training takes place in the 道場, ranks are called 段. The suffix 道 \"way\" is also found in 柔道 and 茶道.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 36"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"毎朝体操をします。","romaji":"Maiasa taisō o shimasu.","german":"I do exercises every morning.","furigana":"まいあさたいそう"},{"japanese":"夏休みに公園でラジオ体操をしました。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi ni kouen de rajio taisou o shimashita.","german":"During summer vacation, we did radio calisthenics in the park.","furigana":"なつやす|こうえん|たいそう"},{"japanese":"体操の選手がオリンピックで金メダルを取りました。","romaji":"Taisou no senshu ga Orinpikku de kin medaru o torimashita.","german":"A gymnast won a gold medal at the Olympics.","furigana":"たいそう|せんしゅ|きん|と"}],"notes":"体操 means gymnastics or calisthenics exercises; usually you say 体操をする. Very well known is ラジオ体操, the morning exercises done to radio music, and before swimming you do 準備体操 \"warm-up exercises\". As a sport, 体操 means artistic gymnastics, and 新体操 is rhythmic gymnastics. 運動 is general \"movement, exercise\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この魚はぬるぬるしています。","romaji":"Kono sakana wa nurunuru shite imasu.","german":"This fish is slimy.","furigana":"さかな"},{"japanese":"川の石がぬるぬるして、滑りそうになりました。","romaji":"Kawa no ishi ga nurunuru shite, suberisou ni narimashita.","german":"The stones in the river were slimy, and I almost slipped.","furigana":"かわ|いし|すべ"},{"japanese":"オクラはぬるぬるしているので、苦手です。","romaji":"Okura wa nurunuru shite iru node, nigate desu.","german":"Okra is so slimy, I don't like it.","furigana":"にがて"}],"notes":"ぬるぬる describes a slippery, slimy surface: fish, eel, okra, mossy stones, soapy hands. It usually appears as ぬるぬるする / ぬるぬるしている, also ぬるぬるになる. ねばねば is \"sticky, stringy\" (natto), べたべた \"sticky\" (sweets, sweat), つるつる \"smooth\" (ice, skin). ぬるぬる sounds rather unpleasant.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"飴を触った手がべたべたします。","romaji":"Ame o sawatta te ga betabeta shimasu.","german":"My hands are sticky from touching candy.","furigana":"あめ|さわ|て"},{"japanese":"あのカップルは人前でもべたべたしています。","romaji":"Ano kappuru wa hitomae demo betabeta shite imasu.","german":"That couple clings to each other even in public.","furigana":"ひとまえ"},{"japanese":"壁にポスターがべたべた貼ってあります。","romaji":"Kabe ni posutaa ga betabeta hatte arimasu.","german":"Posters are plastered all over the wall.","furigana":"かべ|は"}],"notes":"べたべた describes stickiness: hands after sweets, skin in sweat and humidity (汗でべたべた). Mostly べたべたする or べたべたになる. It also means \"all over\" (ポスターをべたべた貼る) and, for couples, \"to cling together, be constantly lovey-dovey\". べとべと is even wetter and more unpleasant, ねばねば is stringy, and ぬるぬる is slimy.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ふわふわのタオルが好きです。","romaji":"Fuwafuwa no taoru ga suki desu.","german":"Ich mag flauschige Handtücher.","furigana":"す"},{"japanese":"このパンはふわふわで柔らかいです。","romaji":"Kono pan wa fuwafuwa de yawarakai desu.","german":"Dieses Brot ist locker und weich.","furigana":"やわ"}],"notes":"ふわふわ beschreibt etwas Leichtes, Luftiges oder Weiches mit wenig Widerstand. Für einen Gegenstand sind ふわふわのタオル und ふわふわで柔らかい möglich. Ein Handtuch ist dabei flauschig, Brot eher locker und weich; dieselbe deutsche Übersetzung passt nicht zu jedem Material. Diese Karte übt die weiche Beschaffenheit, nicht das unbeständige Verhalten einer Person.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"発表の前にどきどきしました。","romaji":"Happyou no mae ni dokidoki shimashita.","german":"My heart was pounding before the presentation.","furigana":"はっぴょう|まえ"},{"japanese":"好きな人と話すと、胸がどきどきします。","romaji":"Suki na hito to hanasu to, mune ga dokidoki shimasu.","german":"When I talk to the person I like, my heart pounds.","furigana":"す|ひと|はな|むね"},{"japanese":"走った後なので、まだ心臓がどきどきしています。","romaji":"Hashitta ato na node, mada shinzou ga dokidoki shite imasu.","german":"I just ran, so my heart is still pounding.","furigana":"はし|あと|しんぞう"}],"notes":"どきどき is the pounding of the heart - from excitement, nervousness, being in love, fear or after exertion. It is usually used as どきどきする, often with 胸が or 心臓が. It is also often written in katakana: ドキドキ. わくわく is only positive anticipation, and はらはら is worry while watching; どきどき can be positive or negative.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"旅行が楽しみでわくわくしています。","romaji":"Ryokou ga tanoshimi de wakuwaku shite imasu.","german":"I'm excited about the trip.","furigana":"りょこう|たの"},{"japanese":"プレゼントを開ける前は、いつもわくわくします。","romaji":"Purezento o akeru mae wa, itsumo wakuwaku shimasu.","german":"Before opening a present, I'm always excited.","furigana":"あ|まえ"},{"japanese":"次にどうなるか、わくわくしながら本を読みました。","romaji":"Tsugi ni dou naru ka, wakuwaku shinagara hon o yomimashita.","german":"I read the book with excitement, wondering what would happen next.","furigana":"つぎ|ほん|よ"}],"notes":"わくわく describes joyful excitement and anticipation of something nice: trips, presents, an exciting story. It usually appears as わくわくする, also ワクワク in katakana. Unlike どきどき, it is never used for fear or nervousness - before an exam you are 緊張する or どきどきする, not わくわく.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"渋滞でいらいらしました。","romaji":"Juutai de iraira shimashita.","german":"I was irritated by the traffic jam.","furigana":"じゅうたい"},{"japanese":"あまり寝ていない日は、すぐいらいらします。","romaji":"Amari nete inai hi wa, sugu iraira shimasu.","german":"On days when I haven't slept much, I get irritable quickly.","furigana":"ね|ひ"},{"japanese":"弟にいらいらして、大きい声を出してしまいました。","romaji":"Otouto ni iraira shite, ookii koe o dashite shimaimashita.","german":"My little brother annoyed me so much that I ended up raising my voice.","furigana":"おとうと|おお|こえ|だ"}],"notes":"いらいら describes inner irritation and impatience: いらいらする. The cause takes で (渋滞でいらいらする), a person who annoys one takes に (弟にいらいらする); \"to annoy someone\" is いらいらさせる. Often written イライラ too. 怒る shows anger outwardly, while むかつく is colloquial and stronger.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"道に迷ってうろうろしました。","romaji":"Michi ni mayotte urouro shimashita.","german":"I got lost and wandered around.","furigana":"みち|まよ"},{"japanese":"駅の前で怪しい人がうろうろしています。","romaji":"Eki no mae de ayashii hito ga urouro shite imasu.","german":"A suspicious person is loitering in front of the station.","furigana":"えき|まえ|あや|ひと"},{"japanese":"試験の結果が心配で、部屋の中をうろうろしていました。","romaji":"Shiken no kekka ga shinpai de, heya no naka o urouro shite imashita.","german":"I was worried about the exam results and was pacing around the room.","furigana":"しけん|けっか|しんぱい|へや|なか"}],"notes":"うろうろ describes aimless or restless walking around: when you have lost your way, pace back and forth nervously, or loiter. It is usually うろうろする; the place takes を (部屋の中をうろうろする) or で. It often carries a sense of perplexity or suspicion. ぶらぶら, by contrast, is leisurely strolling, and 迷う means \"to get lost\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"昨日はぐっすり眠れました。","romaji":"Kinō wa gussuri nemuremashita.","german":"I slept soundly last night.","furigana":"きのう|ねむ"},{"japanese":"赤ちゃんがぐっすり寝ているので、静かにしてください。","romaji":"Akachan ga gussuri nete iru node, shizuka ni shite kudasai.","german":"The baby is sleeping soundly, so please be quiet.","furigana":"あか|ね|しず"}],"notes":"ぐっすり is an adverb for deep, sound sleep. It goes with 寝る or 眠る and can also be combined with と. ぐっすり眠れた says that good sleep was possible; ぐっすり寝ている describes a person who is sleeping right now. It is not used for intense work or other activities.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"書類がばらばらになりました。","romaji":"Shorui ga barabara ni narimashita.","german":"The documents got scattered.","furigana":"しょるい"},{"japanese":"意見がばらばらです。","romaji":"Iken ga barabara desu.","german":"Opinions are divided.","furigana":"いけん"},{"japanese":"卒業してから、クラスのみんなはばらばらになりました。","romaji":"Sotsugyou shite kara, kurasu no minna wa barabara ni narimashita.","german":"Since graduating, everyone in our class has scattered in all directions.","furigana":"そつぎょう"}],"notes":"ばらばら means \"scattered, apart, in pieces\": ばらばらになる \"to fall apart, to scatter\", ばらばらにする \"to take apart\". For opinions, sizes or times it means \"inconsistent\": 意見がばらばらだ. ばらばらに帰る means \"to go home separately\". The opposite is そろう \"to be uniform, complete\"; ぱらぱら describes a light sprinkling or flipping through pages.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お母さんにそっくりですね。","romaji":"Okāsan ni sokkuri desu ne.","german":"You look just like your mother.","furigana":"かあ"},{"japanese":"このケーキ、本物の花にそっくりですね。","romaji":"Kono keeki, honmono no hana ni sokkuri desu ne.","german":"This cake looks just like a real flower!","furigana":"ほんもの|はな"},{"japanese":"泥棒にお金をそっくり盗まれました。","romaji":"Dorobou ni okane o sokkuri nusumaremashita.","german":"A thief stole all of my money.","furigana":"どろぼう|かね|ぬす"}],"notes":"そっくり has two uses. As a な-adjective it means \"strikingly similar, spitting image\": Xにそっくりだ, そっくりな. This is stronger than 似ている \"to be similar\". As an adverb it means \"completely, entirely\": お金をそっくり盗まれる, そっくりそのまま \"exactly as is\". そっくりさん is a \"look-alike, doppelgänger\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"試合に負けて悔しいです。","romaji":"Shiai ni makete kuyashii desu.","german":"I'm frustrated that I lost the match.","furigana":"しあい|ま|くや"},{"japanese":"あと少しで勝てたのに悔しいです。","romaji":"Ato sukoshi de kateta noni kuyashii desu.","german":"I was so close to winning, how frustrating.","furigana":"すこ|か|くや"},{"japanese":"友達にばかにされて、悔しかったです。","romaji":"Tomodachi ni baka ni sarete, kuyashikatta desu.","german":"I was furious when my friend made fun of me.","furigana":"ともだち|くや"}],"notes":"悔しい expresses the bitter frustration over one's own defeat, failure or humiliation, often combined with the wish to do better. 残念 is more neutral, \"too bad, regrettable\", and also fits things one could not influence; 悲しい is \"sad\". For other people's feelings one uses 悔しがる or 悔しそう. The noun is 悔しさ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"切ない映画を見ました。","romaji":"Setsunai eiga o mimashita.","german":"I watched a heartrending movie.","furigana":"せつ|えいが|み"},{"japanese":"別れの歌が切ないです。","romaji":"Wakare no uta ga setsunai desu.","german":"The farewell song is wistful.","furigana":"わか|うた|せつ"},{"japanese":"遠くに住んでいる恋人に会えなくて、切ないです。","romaji":"Tooku ni sunde iru koibito ni aenakute, setsunai desu.","german":"It hurts that I can't see my girlfriend because she lives far away.","furigana":"とお|す|こいびと|あ|せつ"}],"notes":"切ない describes a bittersweet, tugging pain in the chest - longing, unrequited love, parting, wistfulness. It is typical of songs, movies and manga, but is also common in everyday speech. 悲しい is simply \"sad\", 寂しい \"lonely\", 苦しい \"agonizing\". The noun is 切なさ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自分が情けないと思います。","romaji":"Jibun ga nasakenai to omoimasu.","german":"I think I'm pathetic.","furigana":"じぶん|なさ|おも"},{"japanese":"試合で一点も取れなくて、情けない結果でした。","romaji":"Shiai de itten mo torenakute, nasakenai kekka deshita.","german":"We couldn't score a single point in the match; it was a miserable result.","furigana":"しあい|いってん|と|なさ|けっか"},{"japanese":"すぐにあきらめるのは情けないよ。","romaji":"Sugu ni akirameru no wa nasakenai yo.","german":"Giving up right away is pathetic!","furigana":"なさ"}],"notes":"情けない means \"pathetic, miserable, shameful\". It is often directed at oneself (自分が情けない) when one is disappointed by one's own weakness; it expresses shame and frustration at the same time. Toward others it is a clear reproach. みっともない emphasizes how something looks to others, 恥ずかしい is one's own embarrassment, 悔しい the annoyance over a defeat.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"だらしない格好をしています。","romaji":"Darashinai kakkou o shite imasu.","german":"He is dressed sloppily.","furigana":"かっこう"},{"japanese":"彼は時間にだらしないので、いつも遅れて来ます。","romaji":"Kare wa jikan ni darashinai node, itsumo okurete kimasu.","german":"He is careless about time, so he always arrives late.","furigana":"かれ|じかん|おく|き"},{"japanese":"お金にだらしない人にはお金を貸しません。","romaji":"Okane ni darashinai hito ni wa okane o kashimasen.","german":"I don't lend money to people who are careless with it.","furigana":"かね|ひと|かね|か"}],"notes":"だらしない describes people who are sloppy and undisciplined - in clothing, lifestyle or behavior: だらしない格好, だらしない生活. With に you indicate the area: 時間にだらしない \"unpunctual\", お金にだらしない \"sloppy with money\". For a messy room, 散らかっている is preferred. いいかげん stresses irresponsibility. Colloquially also だらしがない.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"おとなしい子供です。","romaji":"Otonashii kodomo desu.","german":"He's a quiet child.","furigana":"こども"},{"japanese":"あの犬はおとなしいです。","romaji":"Ano inu wa otonashii desu.","german":"That dog is well-behaved.","furigana":"いぬ"},{"japanese":"派手な色より、おとなしい色の服が好きです。","romaji":"Hade na iro yori, otonashii iro no fuku ga suki desu.","german":"I prefer clothes in subdued colors to flashy ones.","furigana":"はで|いろ|いろ|ふく|す"}],"notes":"おとなしい means \"quiet, well-behaved, gentle\" and describes children, animals and reserved people. おとなしくする means \"to behave, to keep still\". For colors and patterns it means \"subdued, plain\". The kanji spelling 大人しい (\"like an adult\") is rare. 静か refers more to sounds and surroundings, 優しい to kindness.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 38"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"しつこく聞かないでください。","romaji":"Shitsukoku kikanaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't ask so persistently.","furigana":"き"},{"japanese":"セールスの電話がしつこくて困っています。","romaji":"Seerusu no denwa ga shitsukokute komatte imasu.","german":"The constant sales calls are bothering me.","furigana":"でんわ|こま"},{"japanese":"このラーメンは味がしつこいです。","romaji":"Kono raamen wa aji ga shitsukoi desu.","german":"The flavor of this ramen is too heavy.","furigana":"あじ"}],"notes":"しつこい describes annoying persistence: people, questions, sales calls, but also a cold that won't go away (しつこい風邪). As an adverb: しつこく聞く. With food, it means \"too heavy, too greasy\" (味がしつこい); the opposite is あっさり. Positive perseverance, by contrast, is described with 熱心 or 粘り強い.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ずるいことをしてはいけません。","romaji":"Zurui koto o shite wa ikemasen.","german":"You must not do unfair things."},{"japanese":"えー、一人だけケーキを二つ食べたの？ずるい！","romaji":"Ee, hitori dake keeki o futatsu tabeta no? Zurui!","german":"What, you're the only one who ate two pieces of cake? That's not fair!","furigana":"ひとり|ふた|た"},{"japanese":"あの人はずるくて、大変な仕事をいつも人にやらせます。","romaji":"Ano hito wa zurukute, taihen na shigoto o itsumo hito ni yarasemasu.","german":"He is cunning and always makes others do the hard work.","furigana":"ひと|たいへん|しごと|ひと"}],"notes":"ずるい means \"unfair, cunning, sly\". As an exclamation, ずるい！ protests an injustice or shows envy (\"that's not fair!\") - typical among children and friends. ずるをする means \"to cheat\". 卑怯 is stronger and morally condemning, and 不公平 is the objective \"unfairly distributed\". The kanji spelling 狡い is rare.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"風邪で体がだるいです。","romaji":"Kaze de karada ga darui desu.","german":"I feel sluggish because of a cold.","furigana":"かぜ|からだ"},{"japanese":"月曜の朝はだるくて、起きたくないです。","romaji":"Getsuyou no asa wa darukute, okitakunai desu.","german":"On Monday mornings I feel so sluggish that I don't want to get up.","furigana":"げつよう|あさ|お"},{"japanese":"宿題、だるいなあ。","romaji":"Shukudai, darui naa.","german":"Homework, ugh - can't be bothered.","furigana":"しゅくだい"}],"notes":"だるい describes a physical feeling of heaviness and listlessness, for example with a cold, fever, heat or exhaustion: 体がだるい, 足がだるい. Colloquially, especially among young people, it also means \"annoying, dull, can't be bothered\". 疲れた is \"tired after exertion\", and 眠い \"sleepy\". In technical language, 倦怠感 is used.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"くだらない冗談を言わないで。","romaji":"Kudaranai jōdan o iwanaide.","german":"Don't make silly jokes.","furigana":"じょうだん|い"},{"japanese":"くだらない話はやめましょう。","romaji":"Kudaranai hanashi wa yamemashō.","german":"Let's stop this silly talk.","furigana":"はなし"},{"japanese":"昨日見た映画はくだらなかったです。","romaji":"Kinou mita eiga wa kudaranakatta desu.","german":"The movie I saw yesterday was rubbish.","furigana":"きのうみ|えいが"}],"notes":"くだらない means \"worthless, silly, nonsense\" and sounds disparaging: くだらない話, くだらない冗談, くだらない番組. つまらない, by contrast, means \"boring, uninteresting\" - くだらない goes further and denies the thing any value. The kanji spelling 下らない is rare; colloquially also くだらん.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"みっともない行動はやめなさい。","romaji":"Mittomonai kōdō wa yamenasai.","german":"Stop that disgraceful behavior.","furigana":"こうどう"},{"japanese":"そんなことをするのはみっともないです。","romaji":"Sonna koto o suru no wa mittomonai desu.","german":"It is embarrassing to do something like that."},{"japanese":"シャツが出ていて、みっともないですよ。","romaji":"Shatsu ga dete ite, mittomonai desu yo.","german":"Your shirt is hanging out; it looks untidy.","furigana":"で"}],"notes":"みっともない describes what looks embarrassing or improper in the eyes of others - clothing, appearance, behavior in public. Parents often scold: みっともないからやめなさい. 恥ずかしい is one's own feeling of shame, and 情けない is disappointment over weakness. The kanji spelling 見っともない is rare.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"食べ物を捨てるのはもったいないです。","romaji":"Tabemono o suteru no wa mottainai desu.","german":"Throwing away food is wasteful.","furigana":"た|もの|す"},{"japanese":"天気がいいのに家にいるのはもったいないですよ。","romaji":"Tenki ga ii noni ie ni iru no wa mottainai desu yo.","german":"It's a waste to stay home when the weather is so nice.","furigana":"てんき|いえ"},{"japanese":"私にはもったいないお言葉です。","romaji":"Watashi ni wa mottainai okotoba desu.","german":"Those are kinder words than I deserve.","furigana":"わたし|ことば"}],"notes":"もったいない expresses that something valuable is being wasted: food, money, time, talent or an opportunity; as an exclamation もったいない！ \"What a waste!\". Typical: Xのはもったいない. In humble speech it means \"too good for me, undeserved\": 私にはもったいない. 残念, by contrast, is \"regrettable\" without the idea of waste.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"歩道を歩いてください。","romaji":"Hodō o aruite kudasai.","german":"Please walk on the sidewalk.","furigana":"ほどう|ある"},{"japanese":"この道は歩道が狭いので危ないです。","romaji":"Kono michi wa hodō ga semai node abunai desu.","german":"The sidewalk on this road is narrow, so it is dangerous.","furigana":"みち|ほどう|せま|あぶ"}],"notes":"歩道 is the path or sidewalk for pedestrians alongside a road. 歩道を歩く uses を for the path traveled. 歩道が狭い describes its small width. A marked crossing over the road, in contrast, is called 横断歩道.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"横断歩道を渡ります。","romaji":"Ōdan hodō o watarimasu.","german":"I cross at the crosswalk.","furigana":"おうだんほどう|わた"},{"japanese":"二つ目の横断歩道を渡って、右に曲がってください。","romaji":"Futatsume no oudan hodou o watatte, migi ni magatte kudasai.","german":"Cross the second crosswalk and then turn right.","furigana":"ふた|め|おうだんほどう|わた|みぎ|ま"},{"japanese":"車は横断歩道の前で止まらなければなりません。","romaji":"Kuruma wa oudan hodou no mae de tomaranakereba narimasen.","german":"Cars must stop in front of the crosswalk.","furigana":"くるま|おうだんほどう|まえ|と"}],"notes":"横断歩道 is any marked pedestrian crossing, both the classic zebra crossing and the crossing at a traffic light. Typical is 横断歩道を渡る, where を marks the stretch crossed. The traffic light for it is 信号, a pedestrian bridge is 歩道橋, and the sidewalk itself is 歩道. 横断 alone means \"crossing\": 道路を横断する \"to cross the road\".","levelNote":"Compound: 横断 (N3) + 歩道 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"近道を通りましょう。","romaji":"Chikamichi o tōrimashō.","german":"Let's take the shortcut.","furigana":"ちかみち|とお"},{"japanese":"近道を知っていますよ。","romaji":"Chikamichi o shitte imasu yo.","german":"I know a shortcut.","furigana":"ちかみち|し"},{"japanese":"日本語が上手になる近道はありますか。","romaji":"Nihongo ga jouzu ni naru chikamichi wa arimasu ka.","german":"Is there a quick way to get good at Japanese?","furigana":"にほんご|じょうず|ちかみち"}],"notes":"近道 is the shorter route itself and, as a する-verb, taking a shortcut: 近道をする or 近道を通る \"to take a shortcut\"; when a route becomes shorter, you say ～が近道になる. Figuratively it stands for the fastest way to a goal: 上手になる近道. The opposite is 遠回り \"detour\". Note: a linguistic \"abbreviation\" (of a word) is called not 近道 but 略語.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この道は一方通行です。","romaji":"Kono michi wa ippō tsūkō desu.","german":"This street is one-way.","furigana":"みち|いっぽうつうこう"},{"japanese":"彼の愛は一方通行だった。","romaji":"Kare no ai wa ippō tsūkō datta.","german":"His love was one-sided.","furigana":"かれ|あい|いっぽうつうこう"},{"japanese":"コミュニケーションが一方通行にならないように、質問の時間を作りましょう。","romaji":"Komyunikēshon ga ippō tsūkō ni naranai yō ni, shitsumon no jikan o tsukurimashō.","german":"Let's set aside time for questions so that communication doesn't become a one-way street.","furigana":"いっぽうつうこう|しつもん|じかん|つく"}],"notes":"一方通行 is composed of 一方 \"one side, one direction\" and 通行 \"traffic, passage\". Literally it is one-way traffic; road signs say 一方通行, and one says 一方通行の道 or 一方通行を逆走する \"to drive the wrong way down a one-way street\". Figuratively it describes something that runs in only one direction: 一方通行の恋 \"unrequited love\", 一方通行のコミュニケーション \"communication without feedback\". To be distinguished from this is the adjective 一方的 \"one-sided, unilateral\", which tends to judge a person's inconsiderate behavior.","levelNote":"Compound: 一方 (N3) + 通行 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"スピード違反で罰金を払いました。","romaji":"Supīdo ihan de bakkin o haraimashita.","german":"I paid a fine for speeding.","furigana":"いはん|ばっきん|はら"},{"japanese":"ここに車を止めると、罰金を取られます。","romaji":"Koko ni kuruma o tomeru to, bakkin o toraremasu.","german":"If you park a car here, you'll be fined.","furigana":"くるま|と|ばっきん|と"}],"notes":"罰金 is a monetary penalty for a violation. 罰金を払う names the payment; 罰金を取られる describes, from the point of view of the person affected, that they have to pay a penalty. The specific violation can be given with で. The amount and legal basis depend on the situation; the card practices the word's usage.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 33"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新宿で乗り換えます。","romaji":"Shinjuku de norikaemasu.","german":"I change trains at Shinjuku.","furigana":"しんじゅく|の|か"},{"japanese":"空港までは乗り換えが一回あります。","romaji":"Kuukou made wa norikae ga ikkai arimasu.","german":"There is one transfer on the way to the airport.","furigana":"くうこう|の|か|いっかい"},{"japanese":"乗り換えの時間が五分しかないので、急ぎましょう。","romaji":"Norikae no jikan ga gofun shika nai node, isogimashou.","german":"We only have five minutes to change trains, so let's hurry.","furigana":"の|か|じかん|ごふん|いそ"}],"notes":"乗り換え is the noun for the ichidan verb 乗り換える \"to transfer\". The transfer point takes で (新宿で乗り換える), and the new line takes に (山手線に乗り換える). As a noun you hear 乗り換えがある, 乗り換えなしで行ける or 乗り換えの時間; station signs often use the short form 乗換. Figuratively, it also means switching to another provider or system.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"片道切符を買いました。","romaji":"Katamichi kippu o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a one-way ticket.","furigana":"かたみちきっぷ|か"},{"japanese":"片道の切符をください。","romaji":"Katamichi no kippu o kudasai.","german":"A one-way ticket, please.","furigana":"かたみち|きっぷ"},{"japanese":"家から会社まで片道一時間かかります。","romaji":"Ie kara kaisha made katamichi ichijikan kakarimasu.","german":"It takes an hour each way from home to the office.","furigana":"いえ|かいしゃ|かたみちいちじかん"}],"notes":"片道 is one direction of a trip: 片道切符 \"one-way ticket\", 片道料金, and with time expressions \"each way\": 片道一時間. At the ticket counter, 片道でお願いします is often enough. Its counterpart is 往復 \"round trip\". Note: a one-way street is not 片道 but 一方通行.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"往復切符はいくらですか。","romaji":"Ōfuku kippu wa ikura desu ka.","german":"How much is a round-trip ticket?","furigana":"おうふくきっぷ"},{"japanese":"往復の切符を買いました。","romaji":"Ōfuku no kippu o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a round-trip ticket.","furigana":"おうふく|きっぷ|か"},{"japanese":"毎日、家と会社を往復するだけです。","romaji":"Mainichi, ie to kaisha o oufuku suru dake desu.","german":"Every day I just go back and forth between home and the office.","furigana":"まいにち|いえ|かいしゃ|おうふく"}],"notes":"往復 means \"there and back\": as a noun in 往復切符 or 往復の切符, and with prices using で: 往復で三万円. As a する-verb, the route takes を: 家と会社を往復する \"to commute between home and the company\". Its counterpart is 片道. In elevated language, 往復 also means an exchange of letters, e.g. 往復はがき \"postcard with a reply card\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"始発電車に乗ります。","romaji":"Shihatsu densha ni norimasu.","german":"I take the first train.","furigana":"しはつでんしゃ|の"},{"japanese":"終電に間に合わなかったので、始発を待ちました。","romaji":"Shuuden ni maniawanakatta node, shihatsu o machimashita.","german":"I had missed the last train, so I waited for the first train.","furigana":"しゅうでん|ま|あ|しはつ|ま"},{"japanese":"この電車は東京駅始発です。","romaji":"Kono densha wa Toukyou-eki shihatsu desu.","german":"This train starts its run at Tokyo Station.","furigana":"でんしゃ|とうきょうえきしはつ"}],"notes":"始発 is the first train or bus of the day; you travel 始発で (\"on the first train\") or wait for the 始発. Its counterpart is 終電 or 終バス. Second meaning: a train that begins its run at a station is 始発 there - 東京駅始発の電車; at such a 始発駅 you can usually get a seat easily.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"終電に間に合いました。","romaji":"Shūden ni maniaimashita.","german":"I made it to the last train.","furigana":"しゅうでん|ま|あ"},{"japanese":"終電は何時ですか。","romaji":"Shuuden wa nanji desu ka.","german":"What time is the last train?","furigana":"しゅうでん|なんじ"},{"japanese":"終電がなくなったので、タクシーで帰りました。","romaji":"Shuuden ga nakunatta node, takushii de kaerimashita.","german":"The last train had gone, so I took a taxi home.","furigana":"しゅうでん|かえ"}],"notes":"終電 is the short form of 最終電車, the last train of the day. Typical phrases: 終電に間に合う \"to catch the last train\", 終電に乗り遅れる or 終電を逃す \"to miss it\", and 終電がなくなる \"no more trains run\". The counterpart is 始発. For buses, people speak of the 終バス or 最終バス.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 21"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"定期券を更新します。","romaji":"Teikiken o kōshin shimasu.","german":"I renew my commuter pass.","furigana":"ていきけん|こうしん"},{"japanese":"定期券を家に忘れてしまいました。","romaji":"Teikiken o ie ni wasurete shimaimashita.","german":"I forgot my commuter pass at home.","furigana":"ていきけん|いえ|わす"},{"japanese":"会社が定期券代を払ってくれます。","romaji":"Kaisha ga teikikendai o haratte kuremasu.","german":"My company pays for my commuter pass.","furigana":"かいしゃ|ていきけんだい|はら"}],"notes":"定期券 is a pass for a fixed route, usually for one, three or six months; commuters have a 通勤定期, and school and university students a 通学定期. In everyday speech people often just say 定期. Today the pass is usually stored on an IC card. Typical: 定期券を買う/更新する; many companies pay their employees' 定期券代.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"座席を予約しました。","romaji":"Zaseki o yoyaku shimashita.","german":"I reserved a seat.","furigana":"ざせき|よやく"},{"japanese":"座席の下に荷物を置いてください。","romaji":"Zaseki no shita ni nimotsu o oite kudasai.","german":"Please put your luggage under the seat.","furigana":"ざせき|した|にもつ|お"}],"notes":"座席 is a seat, especially on a train, bus, or in a cinema or hall. 座席を予約する secures a specific seat; 座席の下 refers to the space under the seat. The word can mean the assigned place and the physical seat. For a freely movable seat one often says 椅子.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"改札を通ります。","romaji":"Kaisatsu o tōrimasu.","german":"I go through the ticket gate.","furigana":"かいさつ|とお"},{"japanese":"改札の前で待っています。","romaji":"Kaisatsu no mae de matte imasu.","german":"I am waiting in front of the ticket gate.","furigana":"かいさつ|まえ|ま"},{"japanese":"北口の改札を出て、右に行ってください。","romaji":"Kitaguchi no kaisatsu o dete, migi ni itte kudasai.","german":"Exit through the north exit ticket gate and go right.","furigana":"きたぐち|かいさつ|で|みぎ|い"}],"notes":"改札 today mainly means the ticket barrier in a station, also 改札口; originally, it meant the inspection of tickets. Typical: 改札を通る, 改札を出る/に入る and 改札で待ち合わせる. Large stations have several gates such as 中央改札 or 北口の改札; automatic gates are 自動改札. A railroad crossing barrier, by contrast, is 踏切.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"遅刻してすみません。","romaji":"Chikoku shite sumimasen.","german":"I'm sorry for being late.","furigana":"ちこく"},{"japanese":"遅刻は許されません。","romaji":"Chikoku wa yurusaremasen.","german":"Being late is not tolerated.","furigana":"ちこく|ゆる"},{"japanese":"寝坊して、学校に遅刻しました。","romaji":"Nebou shite, gakkou ni chikoku shimashita.","german":"I overslept and was late for school.","furigana":"ねぼう|がっこう|ちこく"}],"notes":"遅刻 means arriving late for a fixed appointment; the destination takes に: 学校に遅刻する, 会議に遅刻する. It is used only for people; late trains or buses 遅れる, not 遅刻する. To apologize one says 遅刻してすみません or 遅れてすみません. The counterpart for leaving early is 早退.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"体調が悪いので早退します。","romaji":"Taichō ga warui node sōtai shimasu.","german":"I'm not feeling well, so I'll leave early.","furigana":"たいちょう|わる|そうたい"},{"japanese":"今日は用事があるので、三時に早退してもいいですか。","romaji":"Kyou wa youji ga aru node, sanji ni soutai shite mo ii desu ka.","german":"I have something to take care of today; may I leave at three o'clock?","furigana":"きょう|ようじ|さんじ|そうたい"},{"japanese":"息子が熱で学校を早退しました。","romaji":"Musuko ga netsu de gakkou o soutai shimashita.","german":"My son came home early from school because of a fever.","furigana":"むすこ|ねつ|がっこう|そうたい"}],"notes":"早退 means leaving work or school before the regular end; the place takes を: 会社を早退する, 学校を早退する. To ask permission one uses 早退してもいいですか or 早退させてください. The counterpart is 遅刻; being absent altogether is 休む, or at school 欠席する.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"昇進おめでとうございます。","romaji":"Shōshin omedetō gozaimasu.","german":"Congratulations on your promotion.","furigana":"しょうしん"},{"japanese":"姉は来月、課長に昇進します。","romaji":"Ane wa raigetsu, kachō ni shōshin shimasu.","german":"My older sister will be promoted to section chief next month.","furigana":"あね|らいげつ|かちょう|しょうしん"}],"notes":"昇進 is advancement to a higher professional position or rank. With する it becomes an intransitive verb: 課長に昇進する means \"to be promoted to section chief\". The new position takes に. A pay raise is 昇給 and is not automatically the same thing.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"転職を考えています。","romaji":"Tenshoku o kangaeteimasu.","german":"I'm thinking about changing jobs.","furigana":"てんしょく|かんが"},{"japanese":"来月転職する予定です。","romaji":"Raigetsu tenshoku suru yotei desu.","german":"I plan to change jobs next month.","furigana":"らいげつてんしょく|よてい"},{"japanese":"銀行から旅行会社に転職しました。","romaji":"Ginkou kara ryokou gaisha ni tenshoku shimashita.","german":"I switched from a bank to a travel company.","furigana":"ぎんこう|りょこうがいしゃ|てんしょく"}],"notes":"転職 is a change of workplace or profession; the new job takes に: 旅行会社に転職する. To be distinguished are 就職 \"finding one's first employment\" and 退職 \"leaving one's job\". The job search when changing is called 転職活動. A move because of a transfer within the same company, by contrast, is 転勤.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"取引先を訪問します。","romaji":"Torihikisaki o hōmon shimasu.","german":"I visit a business partner.","furigana":"とりひきさき|ほうもん"},{"japanese":"取引先との打ち合わせがあります。","romaji":"Torihikisaki to no uchiawase ga arimasu.","german":"I have a meeting with a business partner.","furigana":"とりひきさき|う|あ"},{"japanese":"取引先に新しい商品を紹介しました。","romaji":"Torihikisaki ni atarashii shouhin o shoukai shimashita.","german":"We introduced a new product to our client.","furigana":"とりひきさき|あたら|しょうひん|しょうかい"}],"notes":"取引先 are companies or people with whom you regularly do business - customers as well as suppliers. It belongs to business language; when speaking politely about the people there, you say 取引先の方. For customers in a shop, by contrast, you say お客様. Typical: 取引先を訪問する, 取引先との打ち合わせ, 取引先に連絡する.","levelNote":"Compound: 取引 (N1) + 先 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"午後に打ち合わせがあります。","romaji":"Gogo ni uchiawase ga arimasu.","german":"I have a meeting in the afternoon.","furigana":"ごご|う|あ"},{"japanese":"打ち合わせの前に、資料を準備しておきます。","romaji":"Uchiawase no mae ni, shiryō o junbi shite okimasu.","german":"Before the meeting, I will prepare the materials.","furigana":"う|あ|まえ|しりょう|じゅんび"},{"japanese":"打ち合わせで、当日の役割を決めました。","romaji":"Uchiawase de, tōjitsu no yakuwari o kimemashita.","german":"At the planning meeting, we decided the roles for the day.","furigana":"う|あ|とうじつ|やくわり|き"}],"notes":"打ち合わせ is a meeting for preparation or coordination. 打ち合わせがある announces an appointment; 打ち合わせで決める names the meeting as the place of a joint decision. With する, it can also refer to the act of coordinating. This card does not deal with the identically written overlapping of a garment.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"来週出張に行きます。","romaji":"Raishū shutchō ni ikimasu.","german":"I'm going on a business trip next week.","furigana":"らいしゅうしゅっちょう|い"},{"japanese":"来週大阪に出張します。","romaji":"Raishū Ōsaka ni shutchō shimasu.","german":"Next week I'm on a business trip to Osaka.","furigana":"らいしゅうおおさか|しゅっちょう"},{"japanese":"部長は今、出張中です。","romaji":"Buchou wa ima, shutchouchuu desu.","german":"The department head is on a business trip right now.","furigana":"ぶちょう|いま|しゅっちょうちゅう"}],"notes":"出張 is a work-related trip; the destination takes に or へ: 大阪に出張する, or you say 出張に行く or 出張で大阪へ行く. On the phone you often hear 出張中です \"is on a business trip\". A private trip is 旅行, by contrast. In compounds such as 出張修理, 出張 also means \"on-site service at the customer's place\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 19"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"売上が伸びています。","romaji":"Uriage ga nobiteimasu.","german":"Sales are growing.","furigana":"うりあげ|の"},{"japanese":"今月の売上が良かったです。","romaji":"Kongetsu no uriage ga yokatta desu.","german":"This month's sales were good.","furigana":"こんげつ|うりあげ|よ"},{"japanese":"雨の日は店の売上が下がります。","romaji":"Ame no hi wa mise no uriage ga sagarimasu.","german":"On rainy days, the shop's sales drop.","furigana":"あめ|ひ|みせ|うりあげ|さ"}],"notes":"売上 is sales revenue, i.e. the total of goods or services sold - not the profit, which is 利益. Typical: 売上が伸びる/上がる \"to rise\", 売上が下がる/落ちる \"to fall\", 売上を伸ばす \"to increase\". It is also written 売り上げ; in bookkeeping, 売上高 is used.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"敷金は二ヶ月分です。","romaji":"Shikikin wa nikagetsubun desu.","german":"The deposit is two months' rent.","furigana":"しききん|にかげつぶん"},{"japanese":"引っ越すとき、敷金は返してもらえますか。","romaji":"Hikkosu toki, shikikin wa kaeshite moraemasu ka.","german":"Will I get the deposit back when I move out?","furigana":"ひ|こ|しききん|かえ"},{"japanese":"この部屋は敷金も礼金もいりません。","romaji":"Kono heya wa shikikin mo reikin mo irimasen.","german":"This apartment requires neither a deposit nor key money.","furigana":"へや|しききん|れいきん"}],"notes":"敷金 is the rental deposit paid on moving in; when moving out, cleaning and repairs are deducted and the rest is refunded. The amount is given in months of rent: 敷金二か月分. Not to be confused with 礼金 \"key money\", a gift to the landlord that is never returned. In western Japan the deposit is sometimes called 保証金.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"礼金はありません。","romaji":"Reikin wa arimasen.","german":"There is no key money.","furigana":"れいきん"},{"japanese":"この物件は礼金が必要です。","romaji":"Kono bukken wa reikin ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"This property requires key money.","furigana":"ぶっけん|れいきん|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"契約の時に礼金を払いました。","romaji":"Keiyaku no toki ni reikin o haraimashita.","german":"I paid key money when I signed the lease.","furigana":"けいやく|とき|れいきん|はら"}],"notes":"礼金 here means key money in connection with renting an apartment. It is a payment to the landlord and differs from the deposit 敷金. In apartment listings, 礼金なし says that this payment is waived; 礼金を払う describes paying it. This card practices this rental meaning and not a general monetary gift as thanks.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"間取りを見せてください。","romaji":"Madori o misete kudasai.","german":"Please show me the floor plan.","furigana":"まど|み"},{"japanese":"間取りを見てから決めます。","romaji":"Madori o mite kara kimemasu.","german":"I'll decide after I see the floor plan.","furigana":"まど|み|き"},{"japanese":"この部屋は間取りがいいですね。","romaji":"Kono heya wa madori ga ii desu ne.","german":"This apartment has a good layout, doesn't it?","furigana":"へや|まど"}],"notes":"間取り is the room layout of an apartment, i.e. its floor plan. In listings it is given with codes like 1K or 2LDK (L = living room, D = dining area, K = kitchen, the number = additional rooms). The drawing for it is called 間取り図. Typical: 間取りがいい, 使いやすい間取り, 間取りを見る.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大家さんに連絡します。","romaji":"Ōyasan ni renraku shimasu.","german":"I'll contact the landlord.","furigana":"おおや|れんらく"},{"japanese":"大家さんは一階に住んでいます。","romaji":"Ooya-san wa ikkai ni sunde imasu.","german":"The landlord lives on the first floor.","furigana":"おおや|いっかい|す"},{"japanese":"家賃は毎月大家さんに払います。","romaji":"Yachin wa maigetsu ooya-san ni haraimasu.","german":"I pay the rent to the landlord every month.","furigana":"やちん|まいげつおおや|はら"}],"notes":"大家 (おおや) is the landlord or landlady and is almost always used with さん: 大家さん. 家主 is somewhat more formal. The same kanji read たいか mean something quite different, namely \"master, authority\" (書道の大家). For larger buildings, contact often goes through a management company (管理会社) rather than directly through the 大家さん.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"来月引っ越しをします。","romaji":"Raigetsu hikkoshi o shimasu.","german":"I'm moving next month.","furigana":"らいげつひ|こ"},{"japanese":"引っ越しの準備で忙しいです。","romaji":"Hikkoshi no junbi de isogashii desu.","german":"I'm busy with preparations for the move.","furigana":"ひ|こ|じゅんび|いそが"},{"japanese":"引っ越しを手伝ってくれてありがとう。","romaji":"Hikkoshi o tetsudatte kurete arigatou.","german":"Thank you for helping me move.","furigana":"ひ|こ|てつだ"}],"notes":"引っ越し is the noun for the godan verb 引っ越す \"to move\" (東京に引っ越す, 新しい家へ引っ越す). As a noun you say 引っ越しをする, 引っ越しの準備, 引っ越しを手伝う. After moving in, it is customary to introduce yourself to the neighbors with a small gift (引っ越しの挨拶). On company signs you often see the shorter spelling 引越し.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 24"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"粗大ごみの回収日はいつですか。","romaji":"Sodai gomi no kaishūbi wa itsu desu ka.","german":"When is the bulky waste collection day?","furigana":"そだい|かいしゅうび"},{"japanese":"古いソファを粗大ごみに出します。","romaji":"Furui sofa o sodai gomi ni dashimasu.","german":"I'm putting the old sofa out as bulky waste.","furigana":"ふる|そだい|だ"},{"japanese":"粗大ごみを捨てるには、シールを買わなければなりません。","romaji":"Sodai gomi o suteru ni wa, shiiru o kawanakereba narimasen.","german":"To dispose of bulky waste, you have to buy a sticker.","furigana":"そだい|す|か"}],"notes":"粗大ごみ is bulky waste such as furniture, bicycles, or large appliances. In Japan you usually have to register it with the municipality beforehand and buy a paid ticket (粗大ごみ処理券, often simply シール); then you put it out on the agreed day: 粗大ごみに出す. It is also written 粗大ゴミ. Jokingly, the term is also used for a retired husband who is just in the way at home.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ごみの分別をしてください。","romaji":"Gomi no bunbetsu o shite kudasai.","german":"Please separate your garbage.","furigana":"ぶんべつ"},{"japanese":"この町では、ごみを八種類に分別します。","romaji":"Kono machi de wa, gomi o hasshurui ni bunbetsu shimasu.","german":"In this town, garbage is separated into eight types.","furigana":"まち|はっしゅるい|ぶんべつ"},{"japanese":"ペットボトルとかんは分別して捨ててください。","romaji":"Petto botoru to kan wa bunbetsu shite sutete kudasai.","german":"Please dispose of PET bottles and cans separately.","furigana":"ぶんべつ|す"}],"notes":"分別 (ぶんべつ) is separating and sorting, in everyday life mainly of garbage: ごみの分別, ごみを分別する. In Japan garbage is strictly separated into burnable waste, plastic, cans, bottles and so on. Note: the same kanji read ふんべつ mean \"discretion, good judgment\" (分別のある大人). For simple sorting, 分ける is also used.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"町内会の集まりがあります。","romaji":"Chōnaikai no atsumari ga arimasu.","german":"There is a neighborhood association meeting.","furigana":"ちょうないかい|あつ"},{"japanese":"町内会の集まりに参加しました。","romaji":"Chōnaikai no atsumari ni sanka shimashita.","german":"I took part in the neighborhood association meeting.","furigana":"ちょうないかい|あつ|さんか"},{"japanese":"町内会で、公園の掃除を手伝いました。","romaji":"Chōnaikai de, kōen no sōji o tetsudaimashita.","german":"At the neighborhood association, I helped clean the park.","furigana":"ちょうないかい|こうえん|そうじ|てつだ"}],"notes":"町内会 is an association of the residents of a neighborhood. It may organize, for example, meetings, local festivals or communal cleaning. 町内会の集まり means a meeting of this association; 町内会に入る means to join it. Which activities exist depends on the particular neighborhood.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"回覧板を回してください。","romaji":"Kairanban o mawashite kudasai.","german":"Please pass on the circular.","furigana":"かいらんばん|まわ"},{"japanese":"回覧板でお祭りのお知らせが来ました。","romaji":"Kairanban de omatsuri no oshirase ga kimashita.","german":"An announcement about the festival came through the circular.","furigana":"かいらんばん|まつ|し|き"},{"japanese":"回覧板にごみの出し方が書いてあります。","romaji":"Kairanban ni gomi no dashikata ga kaite arimasu.","german":"The circular explains how to put out the trash.","furigana":"かいらんばん|だ|かた|か"}],"notes":"回覧板 is a folder or clipboard with notices from the neighborhood association (町内会) that is passed from household to household. You read it, sign off on it, and pass it on: 回覧板を回す; when it arrives at your place, one says 回覧板が回ってくる. It contains e.g. festivals, garbage rules, or disaster drills. 回覧 alone refers to the circulation of documents, for instance in a company.","levelNote":"Compound: 回覧 (N3) + 板 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"駅で友達とすれ違いました。","romaji":"Eki de tomodachi to surechigaimashita.","german":"I passed a friend at the station.","furigana":"えき|ともだち|ちが"},{"japanese":"狭い道で車とすれ違いました。","romaji":"Semai michi de kuruma to surechigaimashita.","german":"I passed a car on the narrow road.","furigana":"せま|みち|くるま|ちが"},{"japanese":"母を駅まで迎えに行きましたが、すれ違ってしまいました。","romaji":"Haha o eki made mukae ni ikimashita ga, surechigatte shimaimashita.","german":"I went to the station to pick up my mother, but we missed each other.","furigana":"はは|えき|むか|い|ちが"}],"notes":"すれ違う means to pass each other going in opposite directions on foot or by vehicle; the other party takes と: 友達とすれ違う, 車とすれ違う. It also means \"to miss each other\" when you had meant to meet, and figuratively \"to talk past each other, not be in agreement\": 気持ちがすれ違う. The noun is すれ違い. Usually only 違う is written with kanji.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"石につまずきました。","romaji":"Ishi ni tsumazukimashita.","german":"I tripped over a stone.","furigana":"いし"},{"japanese":"暗い部屋で、子どものおもちゃにつまずきました。","romaji":"Kurai heya de, kodomo no omocha ni tsumazukimashita.","german":"In the dark room, I tripped over the children's toy.","furigana":"くら|へや|こ"},{"japanese":"漢字の勉強でつまずいています。","romaji":"Kanji no benkyou de tsumazuite imasu.","german":"I am struggling with my kanji studies.","furigana":"かんじ|べんきょう"}],"notes":"つまずく means \"to trip\"; the obstacle takes に: 石につまずく. If a fall follows, you say つまずいて転ぶ - 転ぶ is the falling itself. Figuratively, it means \"to run into difficulties, to fail\", and the area takes で: 仕事でつまずく. The kanji 躓く is difficult and rare; it is almost always written in kana.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子供が跳ねています。","romaji":"Kodomo ga haneteimasu.","german":"The children are jumping around.","furigana":"こども|は"},{"japanese":"油が跳ねるので、気をつけてください。","romaji":"Abura ga haneru node, ki o tsukete kudasai.","german":"Be careful, the oil splatters.","furigana":"あぶら|は|き"},{"japanese":"池で魚が跳ねました。","romaji":"Ike de sakana ga hanemashita.","german":"A fish jumped in the pond.","furigana":"いけ|さかな|は"}],"notes":"跳ねる means \"to hop, to jump up\" - of children, animals, fish or balls; lively jumping around is often 飛び跳ねる. Liquids can also splash: 油が跳ねる, 泥が跳ねる. For comparison: 跳ぶ is a deliberate jump (over something, a long way), and 飛ぶ means \"to fly\". Do not confuse it with the transitive はねる \"to hit with a car\" (車にはねられる), which is written 撥ねる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"海に潜って魚を見ました。","romaji":"Umi ni mogutte sakana o mimashita.","german":"I dove into the sea and saw fish.","furigana":"うみ|もぐ|さかな|み"},{"japanese":"寒いので、布団に潜って寝ました。","romaji":"Samui node, futon ni mogutte nemashita.","german":"Because it was cold, I crawled under the futon and slept.","furigana":"さむ|ふとん|もぐ|ね"},{"japanese":"猫がベッドの下に潜っています。","romaji":"Neko ga beddo no shita ni mogutte imasu.","german":"The cat is hiding under the bed.","furigana":"ねこ|した|もぐ"}],"notes":"潜る (もぐる) means \"to dive\", and the destination takes に: 海に潜る, 水に潜る. Equally often it means \"to crawl under something, to hide\": 布団に潜る, 机の下に潜る. Figuratively, 地下に潜る means \"to go underground\". Note: the same kanji read くぐる means \"to pass through something\" (門をくぐる).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ハンドルをしっかり握ります。","romaji":"Handoru o shikkari nigirimasu.","german":"I grip the steering wheel firmly.","furigana":"にぎ"},{"japanese":"おにぎりを握りました。","romaji":"Onigiri o nigirimashita.","german":"I made onigiri.","furigana":"にぎ"},{"japanese":"子どもの手を握って、道を渡りました。","romaji":"Kodomo no te o nigitte, michi o watarimashita.","german":"I held the child's hand and crossed the street.","furigana":"こ|て|にぎ|みち|わた"}],"notes":"握る means \"to grip firmly with the hand\": ハンドルを握る, 手を握る, つり革を握る. In the kitchen it means shaping rice or sushi by hand, hence the name おにぎり. Figuratively, one has something \"in hand\": 権力を握る, 秘密を握る. Neutral \"to hold, carry\" is 持つ, while sudden grabbing is more つかむ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"猫の頭を撫でます。","romaji":"Neko no atama o nademasu.","german":"I stroke the cat's head.","furigana":"ねこ|あたま|な"},{"japanese":"母は泣いている子どもの頭を優しく撫でました。","romaji":"Haha wa naite iru kodomo no atama o yasashiku nademashita.","german":"The mother gently stroked her crying child's head.","furigana":"はは|な|こ|あたま|やさ|な"},{"japanese":"犬を撫でてもいいですか。","romaji":"Inu o nadete mo ii desu ka.","german":"May I pet the dog?","furigana":"いぬ|な"}],"notes":"撫でる means \"to stroke, to gently pass a hand over something\": 頭を撫でる, 犬を撫でる, 髪を撫でる. If you rub something that hurts, you say さする; mere touching is 触る. Since 撫 is not a Jōyō kanji, it is often written なでる in kana. The idiom 胸をなで下ろす means \"to breathe a sigh of relief\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"本にしおりを挟みます。","romaji":"Hon ni shiori o hasamimasu.","german":"I put a bookmark in the book.","furigana":"ほん|はさ"},{"japanese":"ドアに指を挟んでしまいました。","romaji":"Doa ni yubi o hasande shimaimashita.","german":"I got my finger caught in the door.","furigana":"ゆび|はさ"},{"japanese":"銀行は道を挟んで郵便局の向かいにあります。","romaji":"Ginkou wa michi o hasande yuubinkyoku no mukai ni arimasu.","german":"The bank is across the street from the post office.","furigana":"ぎんこう|みち|はさ|ゆうびんきょく|む"}],"notes":"挟む means \"to put or clamp something between two things\": AをBに挟む, e.g. しおりを本に挟む, ハムをパンに挟む. This also applies unintentionally: 指をドアに挟む \"to get one's finger caught\". With a place: 道を挟んで \"across the street\". 口を挟む means \"to interrupt, to cut in\". The intransitive partner is 挟まる (指がドアに挟まる).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"シールを剥がします。","romaji":"Shīru o hagashimasu.","german":"I peel off the sticker.","furigana":"は"},{"japanese":"壁のポスターを剥がしました。","romaji":"Kabe no posutaa o hagashimashita.","german":"I took the poster off the wall.","furigana":"かべ|は"},{"japanese":"ばんそうこうを剥がすとき、少し痛かったです。","romaji":"Bansoukou o hagasu toki, sukoshi itakatta desu.","german":"It hurt a little when I peeled off the bandage.","furigana":"は|すこ|いた"}],"notes":"剥がす means \"to peel or remove something that is stuck or firmly attached\": シール/ポスター/ばんそうこうを剥がす. The intransitive partner is 剥がれる (ペンキが剥がれる \"the paint peels off\"). Peeling fruit or vegetables, by contrast, is 剥く (むく). The word is often written in kana: はがす.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"タオルを絞ります。","romaji":"Taoru o shiborimasu.","german":"I wring out the towel.","furigana":"しぼ"},{"japanese":"レモンを絞って、魚にかけます。","romaji":"Remon o shibotte, sakana ni kakemasu.","german":"I squeeze a lemon and pour the juice over the fish.","furigana":"しぼ|さかな"},{"japanese":"旅行先を二つに絞りました。","romaji":"Ryokousaki o futatsu ni shiborimashita.","german":"I narrowed down the travel destinations to two.","furigana":"りょこうさき|ふた|しぼ"}],"notes":"絞る means \"to press liquid out by twisting or squeezing\": タオルを絞る \"to wring out\", レモンを絞る \"to squeeze\". Figuratively, it means \"to narrow down, to reduce\": 候補を二つに絞る, 音を絞る \"to turn the volume down\", 知恵を絞る \"to rack one's brains\". When extracting milk or juice, 搾る is also written (牛乳を搾る).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"部屋を散らかさないで。","romaji":"Heya o chirakasanai de.","german":"Don't make a mess of the room.","furigana":"へや|ち"},{"japanese":"子どもがおもちゃを散らかしたまま、出かけてしまいました。","romaji":"Kodomo ga omocha o chirakashita mama, dekakete shimaimashita.","german":"The child went out leaving the toys scattered everywhere.","furigana":"こ|ち|で"}],"notes":"散らかす means to scatter things in disorder or to make a room messy. It is transitive: 部屋を散らかす or おもちゃを散らかす name the room or objects with を. As a godan verb ending in す, it forms 散らかします・散らかして・散らかさない. The state of something being messy can be described with the intransitive 散らかる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ボタンを縫い付けます。","romaji":"Botan o nuitsukemasu.","german":"I sew on the button.","furigana":"ぬ|つ"},{"japanese":"母が浴衣を縫ってくれました。","romaji":"Haha ga yukata o nutte kuremashita.","german":"My mother sewed a yukata for me.","furigana":"はは|ゆかた|ぬ"},{"japanese":"けがをして、病院で三針縫いました。","romaji":"Kega o shite, byouin de mihari nuimashita.","german":"I got injured and had three stitches at the hospital.","furigana":"びょういん|みはりぬ"}],"notes":"縫う means \"to sew\", by hand or with a sewing machine: 服を縫う, ミシンで縫う. Sewing something on is 縫い付ける (ボタンを縫い付ける). Doctors also stitch wounds: 三針縫う \"to stitch with three stitches\". Figuratively, 人ごみを縫って歩く means \"to weave through the crowd\". Knitting, by contrast, is 編む.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"マフラーを編みます。","romaji":"Mafurā o amimasu.","german":"I'm knitting a scarf.","furigana":"あ"},{"japanese":"祖母はセーターを編むのが上手です。","romaji":"Sobo wa seetaa o amu no ga jouzu desu.","german":"My grandmother is good at knitting sweaters.","furigana":"そぼ|あ|じょうず"},{"japanese":"妹の髪を編んであげました。","romaji":"Imouto no kami o ande agemashita.","german":"I braided my little sister's hair.","furigana":"いもうと|かみ|あ"}],"notes":"編む means to interlace threads or hair: knitting (棒針で編む), crocheting (かぎ針で編む) and braiding (髪を編む; a braid is 三つ編み). The result is called 編み物, hand-knitted items 手編み. In elevated language, 編む also means \"to compile, to edit\" (an anthology). Sewing, by contrast, is 縫う, weaving 織る.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 13"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"髪を茶色に染めました。","romaji":"Kami o chairo ni somemashita.","german":"I dyed my hair brown.","furigana":"かみ|ちゃいろ|そ"},{"japanese":"白いTシャツを青く染めました。","romaji":"Shiroi tii-shatsu o aoku somemashita.","german":"I dyed a white T-shirt blue.","furigana":"しろ|あお|そ"},{"japanese":"夕日が空を赤く染めていました。","romaji":"Yuuhi ga sora o akaku somete imashita.","german":"The setting sun was coloring the sky red.","furigana":"ゆうひ|そら|あか|そ"}],"notes":"染める means \"to dye\", especially hair and fabric: 髪を染める, 布を染める. The color takes に (茶色に染める) or appears as an adverb in く (赤く染める). The intransitive partner is 染まる (空が赤く染まる). Unlike 塗る (\"to paint\"), with 染める the color penetrates the material. Figuratively: 顔を赤く染める \"to blush\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"きゅうりを漬けました。","romaji":"Kyuuri o tsukemashita.","german":"I pickled cucumbers.","furigana":"つ"},{"japanese":"汚れたシャツを水に漬けておきます。","romaji":"Yogoreta shatsu o mizu ni tsukete okimasu.","german":"I leave the dirty shirt soaking in water.","furigana":"よご|みず|つ"},{"japanese":"肉をしょうゆに一時間漬けてから焼きます。","romaji":"Niku o shouyu ni ichijikan tsukete kara yakimasu.","german":"I marinate the meat in soy sauce for an hour and then grill it.","furigana":"にく|いちじかんつ|や"}],"notes":"漬ける means \"to put into a liquid\": AをBに漬ける - for soaking (シャツを水に漬ける) or marinating (肉をしょうゆに漬ける), and above all \"to pickle, to preserve\" as with pickles (きゅうりを漬ける). The result is called 漬物. The intransitive partner is 漬かる (よく漬かっている \"well pickled\"). Do not confuse with the similar-sounding 付ける \"to attach\" and つける \"to turn on\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"野菜を炒めます。","romaji":"Yasai o itamemasu.","german":"I stir-fry the vegetables.","furigana":"やさい|いた"},{"japanese":"まず、玉ねぎを茶色になるまで炒めます。","romaji":"Mazu, tamanegi o chairo ni naru made itamemasu.","german":"First, sauté the onions until they are brown.","furigana":"たま|ちゃいろ|いた"},{"japanese":"残ったご飯を卵と一緒に炒めました。","romaji":"Nokotta gohan o tamago to issho ni itamemashita.","german":"I fried the leftover rice together with egg.","furigana":"のこ|はん|たまご|いっしょ|いた"}],"notes":"炒める means \"to fry in a little oil while stirring\", typically in a pan or wok: 野菜を炒める, 油で炒める; a dish made this way is e.g. 野菜炒め. To be distinguished are 焼く (to pan-fry, grill, bake without stirring), 揚げる (to deep-fry) and 煮る (to simmer in liquid). Not to be confused with the identically sounding 痛める \"to hurt, injure\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"カレーをゆっくり煮込みます。","romaji":"Karee o yukkuri nikomimasu.","german":"I let the curry simmer slowly.","furigana":"にこ"},{"japanese":"牛肉と野菜を二時間煮込みました。","romaji":"Gyuuniku to yasai o nijikan nikomimashita.","german":"I stewed beef and vegetables for two hours.","furigana":"ぎゅうにく|やさい|にじかんにこ"},{"japanese":"この店のスープは十時間煮込んであるそうです。","romaji":"Kono mise no suupu wa juujikan nikonde aru sou desu.","german":"The soup at this restaurant is said to be simmered for ten hours.","furigana":"みせ|じゅうじかんにこ"}],"notes":"煮込む means \"to simmer long and slowly, to stew\"; compared with the general 煮る (\"to cook in liquid\"), 込む stresses duration and thoroughness. Typical: じっくり/ゆっくり煮込む, and for the result 煮込み料理 or 煮込みハンバーグ. Cooking various ingredients together is also 煮込む. Cooking only in water is ゆでる.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ご飯をお茶碗に盛ってください。","romaji":"Gohan o ochawan ni motte kudasai.","german":"Please serve the rice into the bowl.","furigana":"はん|ちゃわん|も"},{"japanese":"庭に土を盛って、花を植えました。","romaji":"Niwa ni tsuchi o motte, hana o uemashita.","german":"I piled up soil in the garden and planted flowers.","furigana":"にわ|つち|も|はな|う"},{"japanese":"彼の話はいつも少し盛っています。","romaji":"Kare no hanashi wa itsumo sukoshi motte imasu.","german":"His stories are always a bit exaggerated.","furigana":"かれ|はなし|すこ|も"}],"notes":"盛る mainly means \"to serve food, to fill a bowl\": ご飯を茶碗に盛る, サラダを皿に盛る; hence 大盛り (\"large portion\") and 盛り付け (\"plating\"). For rice and soup, everyday speech also uses よそう. In addition, 盛る means \"to heap up\": 土を盛る. Colloquially, 話を盛る means \"to exaggerate, to embellish\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コーヒーをこぼしてしまいました。","romaji":"Koohii o koboshite shimaimashita.","german":"I spilled the coffee."},{"japanese":"ご飯をこぼさないように食べてね。","romaji":"Gohan o kobosanai you ni tabete ne.","german":"Eat so that you don't drop rice everywhere.","furigana":"はん|た"},{"japanese":"父はよく仕事のぐちをこぼします。","romaji":"Chichi wa yoku shigoto no guchi o koboshimasu.","german":"My father often complains about his work.","furigana":"ちち|しごと"}],"notes":"こぼす means \"to spill, to drop bits\": liquids as well as crumbs or rice: 水/コーヒーをこぼす; often with てしまう because it is an accident. The intransitive partner is こぼれる (水がこぼれる). Set phrases: 涙をこぼす \"to shed tears\", ぐちをこぼす \"to grumble, complain\". It is almost always written in kana.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"窓から外を覗きました。","romaji":"Mado kara soto o nozokimashita.","german":"I peeked out of the window.","furigana":"まど|そと|のぞ"},{"japanese":"帰りに本屋を覗いてみます。","romaji":"Kaeri ni hon'ya o nozoite mimasu.","german":"On the way home I'll drop in at the bookstore.","furigana":"かえ|ほんや|のぞ"},{"japanese":"人の部屋を覗かないでください。","romaji":"Hito no heya o nozokanaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't peek into other people's rooms.","furigana":"ひと|へや|のぞ"}],"notes":"覗く means \"to look in through an opening or secretly\": 窓から覗く, 鍵穴から覗く; the thing looked at takes を, the opening takes から. Also \"to drop in briefly at a shop\": 本屋を覗く. Intransitively it means \"to peek out\": 雲の間から青空が覗く. Not to be confused with 除く (のぞく) \"to remove, to exclude\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"パンが膨らみました。","romaji":"Pan ga fukuramimashita.","german":"The bread has risen.","furigana":"ふく"},{"japanese":"春になると、桜のつぼみが膨らみます。","romaji":"Haru ni naru to, sakura no tsubomi ga fukuramimasu.","german":"In spring, the cherry blossom buds swell.","furigana":"はる|さくら|ふく"},{"japanese":"留学の話を聞いて、夢が膨らみました。","romaji":"Ryuugaku no hanashi o kiite, yume ga fukuramimashita.","german":"When I heard about studying abroad, my dreams grew.","furigana":"りゅうがく|はなし|き|ゆめ|ふく"}],"notes":"膨らむ means \"to puff up, to swell\", for example of dough, balloons or buds: パンが膨らむ. Figuratively, dreams, plans or costs grow: 夢が膨らむ, 費用が膨らむ. The transitive partners are 膨らます/膨らませる (風船を膨らます). The similar 膨れる often sounds negative (お腹が膨れる \"to be full\") and also means \"to sulk\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"靴紐をほどきます。","romaji":"Kutsuhimo o hodokimasu.","german":"I untie my shoelaces.","furigana":"くつひも"},{"japanese":"荷物をほどいて、服を片付けました。","romaji":"Nimotsu o hodoite, fuku o katazukemashita.","german":"I unpacked my luggage and put away the clothes.","furigana":"にもつ|ふく|かたづ"},{"japanese":"古いセーターをほどいて、マフラーを編みました。","romaji":"Furui seetaa o hodoite, mafuraa o amimashita.","german":"I unraveled an old sweater and knitted a scarf.","furigana":"ふる|あ"}],"notes":"ほどく means \"to loosen something knotted or tied\": 靴ひも/結び目/リボンをほどく. Knitted items can also be unraveled (セーターをほどく) and tied-up luggage unpacked (荷物をほどく). The opposite is 結ぶ \"to tie\"; the intransitive partner is ほどける (靴ひもがほどける \"come undone\"). The kanji 解く is usually read とく, which is why ほどく is written in kana.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"マフラーを巻きます。","romaji":"Mafurā o makimasu.","german":"I wrap a scarf around my neck.","furigana":"ま"},{"japanese":"寿司を海苔で巻きます。","romaji":"Sushi o nori de makimasu.","german":"I wrap sushi in nori seaweed.","furigana":"すし|のり|ま"},{"japanese":"足にけがをしたので、包帯を巻きました。","romaji":"Ashi ni kega o shita node, houtai o makimashita.","german":"I hurt my leg, so I put on a bandage.","furigana":"あし|ほうたい|ま"}],"notes":"巻く means \"to wind around something, to roll up\": マフラーを首に巻く, 包帯を巻く, 紙を巻く. The wrapping material takes で: 海苔で巻く, hence 巻き寿司. Other expressions: ねじを巻く \"to wind up\", 舌を巻く \"to be amazed\". Wrapping something up completely is 包む. As a counter, 巻 refers to volumes (第一巻).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ページをめくります。","romaji":"Pēji o mekurimasu.","german":"I turn the page."},{"japanese":"カードを一枚めくってください。","romaji":"Kaado o ichimai mekutte kudasai.","german":"Please flip over one card.","furigana":"いちまい"},{"japanese":"布団をめくると、猫が寝ていました。","romaji":"Futon o mekuru to, neko ga nete imashita.","german":"When I pulled back the futon, a cat was sleeping there.","furigana":"ふとん|ねこ|ね"}],"notes":"めくる means \"to turn pages or fold back something that covers\": ページ/カレンダーをめくる, カードをめくる \"to flip a card\", 布団をめくる \"to pull back the blanket\". The intransitive partner is めくれる (スカートがめくれる). The kanji 捲る is rare; it is written in kana. Opening a book is 開く.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"スープをかき混ぜます。","romaji":"Sūpu o kakimazemasu.","german":"I stir the soup.","furigana":"ま"},{"japanese":"納豆をよくかき混ぜてから食べます。","romaji":"Nattou o yoku kakimazete kara tabemasu.","german":"I stir natto well before eating it.","furigana":"なっとう|ま|た"},{"japanese":"お風呂のお湯をかき混ぜてから入ってください。","romaji":"Ofuro no oyu o kakimazete kara haitte kudasai.","german":"Please stir the bathwater before you get in.","furigana":"ふろ|ゆ|ま|はい"}],"notes":"かき混ぜる means \"to stir thoroughly, to mix\", e.g. with a spoon or chopsticks: スープ/卵/納豆をかき混ぜる. The prefix かき intensifies the simple 混ぜる (\"to mix\"). Typical is よくかき混ぜる. Intransitively, something blends with 混ざる. In older Japanese baths, one stirs the water before getting in because the hottest water is on top.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ギターを弾きます。","romaji":"Gitā o hikimasu.","german":"I play the guitar.","furigana":"ひ"},{"japanese":"姉はピアノを弾くのが上手です。","romaji":"Ane wa piano o hiku no ga jōzu desu.","german":"My older sister is good at playing the piano.","furigana":"あね|ひ|じょうず"}],"notes":"弾く denotes playing a stringed or keyboard instrument, such as guitar or piano. With を you name the instrument. The godan verb forms 弾きます and 弾いて, read hikimasu and hiite. 弾くのが上手 means being good at playing the instrument named.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 9"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"風景を描きます。","romaji":"Fūkei o egakimasu.","german":"I paint a landscape.","furigana":"ふうけい|えが"},{"japanese":"将来の夢を絵に描きました。","romaji":"Shōrai no yume o e ni egakimashita.","german":"I drew my dream for the future.","furigana":"しょうらい|ゆめ|え|えが"},{"japanese":"この映画は家族の愛を描いています。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa kazoku no ai o egaite imasu.","german":"This film portrays the love within a family.","furigana":"えいが|かぞく|あい|えが"}],"notes":"描く (えがく) means \"to draw, to paint\" and beyond that \"to depict\" (in film, novels) and \"to imagine\" (心に描く). The same kanji is also read かく; for everyday drawing one usually says 絵をかく, while えがく sounds somewhat more elevated and is the usual reading for \"to depict, to picture\". Shapes and paths are also \"described\": 円を描く.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"映画を撮影しています。","romaji":"Eiga o satsuei shiteimasu.","german":"They are filming a movie.","furigana":"えいが|さつえい"},{"japanese":"映画の撮影が始まりました。","romaji":"Eiga no satsuei ga hajimarimashita.","german":"The filming of the movie has begun.","furigana":"えいが|さつえい|はじ"},{"japanese":"ここは撮影禁止です。","romaji":"Koko wa satsuei kinshi desu.","german":"Photography is prohibited here.","furigana":"さつえいきんし"}],"notes":"撮影する means \"to photograph or film\" and sounds more formal than the everyday 写真を撮る. It is often found on signs: 撮影禁止 \"no photography\". As a noun: 映画の撮影, 撮影中 \"filming in progress\". The object takes を: 風景を撮影する. For movies, people also simply say 映画を撮る.","levelNote":"Derived from 撮影 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"栄養バランスを考えます。","romaji":"Eiyō baransu o kangaemasu.","german":"I think about the balance of nutrients.","furigana":"えいよう|かんが"},{"japanese":"病気のときは、栄養のあるものを食べてください。","romaji":"Byouki no toki wa, eiyou no aru mono o tabete kudasai.","german":"When you are sick, please eat something nutritious.","furigana":"びょうき|えいよう|た"},{"japanese":"最近忙しくて、栄養が足りていません。","romaji":"Saikin isogashikute, eiyou ga tarite imasen.","german":"I've been so busy lately that I'm not getting enough nutrition.","furigana":"さいきんいそが|えいよう|た"}],"notes":"栄養 is nutritional value or nutrient supply: 栄養がある \"to be nutritious\", 栄養をとる \"to take in nutrients\", 栄養が足りない, 栄養バランス. For \"nutrition\" in the sense of eating habits one rather says 食事 or 食生活. A nutritionist is a 栄養士.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"カロリーを控えています。","romaji":"Karorī o hikaeteimasu.","german":"I am cutting back on calories.","furigana":"ひか"},{"japanese":"このケーキはカロリーが高いです。","romaji":"Kono kēki wa karorī ga takai desu.","german":"This cake is high in calories.","furigana":"たか"},{"japanese":"この飲み物はカロリーゼロです。","romaji":"Kono nomimono wa karorii zero desu.","german":"This drink has zero calories.","furigana":"の|もの"}],"notes":"カロリー is the energy value of food; packages show kcal (キロカロリー). You say カロリーが高い/低い, less often 多い/少ない. Eating less is カロリーを控える or 抑える. Advertising likes カロリーゼロ, カロリーオフ or 低カロリー; the opposite is 高カロリー.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"食物繊維が豊富です。","romaji":"Shokumotsu sen'i ga hōfu desu.","german":"It is rich in dietary fiber.","furigana":"しょくもつせんい|ほうふ"},{"japanese":"食物繊維は健康にいいです。","romaji":"Shokumotsu sen'i wa kenkō ni ii desu.","german":"Dietary fiber is good for your health.","furigana":"しょくもつせんい|けんこう"},{"japanese":"食物繊維をとるために、毎朝野菜を食べています。","romaji":"Shokumotsu sen'i o toru tame ni, maiasa yasai o tabete imasu.","german":"To get dietary fiber, I eat vegetables every morning.","furigana":"しょくもつせんい|まいあさやさい|た"}],"notes":"食物繊維 is dietary fiber, i.e. indigestible plant components found in vegetables, beans, mushrooms or seaweed. Typical: 食物繊維が豊富/多い, 食物繊維をとる. 食物 is read しょくもつ here, not たべもの. 繊維 on its own means \"fiber\", also in textiles.","levelNote":"Compound: 食物 (N3) + 繊維 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仏壇にお花を供えます。","romaji":"Butsudan ni ohana o sonaemasu.","german":"I place flowers on the household altar.","furigana":"ぶつだん|はな|そな"},{"japanese":"毎朝仏壇にお線香をあげます。","romaji":"Maiasa butsudan ni osenkō o agemasu.","german":"Every morning I light incense at the altar.","furigana":"まいあさぶつだん|せんこう"},{"japanese":"祖母の家には古い仏壇があります。","romaji":"Sobo no ie ni wa furui butsudan ga arimasu.","german":"There is an old Buddhist altar in my grandmother's house.","furigana":"そぼ|いえ|ふる|ぶつだん"}],"notes":"仏壇 is the Buddhist household altar, a cabinet with doors in which memorial tablets (位牌) and photos of deceased family members stand. Typical actions are お花を供える \"to offer flowers\", お線香をあげる \"to light incense\" and 仏壇に手を合わせる \"to put one's hands together before the altar\". Do not confuse it with 神棚, the small Shinto household shrine, which usually hangs high up on the wall.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"盆踊りに参加しました。","romaji":"Bon odori ni sanka shimashita.","german":"I took part in the Bon dance.","furigana":"ぼんおど|さんか"},{"japanese":"盆踊りは夏の楽しみです。","romaji":"Bon odori wa natsu no tanoshimi desu.","german":"The Bon dance is a summer pleasure.","furigana":"ぼんおど|なつ|たの"},{"japanese":"浴衣を着て、近くの公園の盆踊りに行きました。","romaji":"Yukata o kite, chikaku no kouen no bon odori ni ikimashita.","german":"I put on a yukata and went to the Bon dance at a nearby park.","furigana":"ゆかた|き|ちか|こうえん|ぼんおど|い"}],"notes":"盆踊り is the folk dance for お盆, the summer festival commemorating the dead (mid-July or mid-August depending on the region). People dance in a circle around a wooden tower (やぐら) with drums, often in a 浴衣, in parks, at temples or shrines. The word refers both to the dance (盆踊りを踊る) and to the whole event (盆踊りに行く, 盆踊りに参加する).","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 26"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"七五三のお祝いをしました。","romaji":"Shichigosan no oiwai o shimashita.","german":"We celebrated Shichigosan.","furigana":"しちごさん|いわ"},{"japanese":"七五三の写真を撮りました。","romaji":"Shichigosan no shashin o torimashita.","german":"We took Shichigosan photos.","furigana":"しちごさん|しゃしん|と"},{"japanese":"今年は娘の七五三なので、着物を着て神社に行きます。","romaji":"Kotoshi wa musume no shichigosan na node, kimono o kite jinja ni ikimasu.","german":"Our daughter has her Shichigosan this year, so we're going to the shrine in kimono.","furigana":"ことし|むすめ|しちごさん|きもの|き|じんじゃ|い"}],"notes":"七五三 is the festival around November 15 for children aged three, five and seven (traditionally boys at three and five, girls at three and seven). The family visits a shrine in kimono, prays for health and growth, and has commemorative photos taken; the children receive long lucky candy (千歳飴). Typical are 七五三のお祝い and 七五三で神社にお参りする.","levelNote":"Compound: 三 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"節分に豆まきをします。","romaji":"Setsubun ni mamemaki o shimasu.","german":"On Setsubun we throw beans.","furigana":"せつぶん|まめ"},{"japanese":"節分の夜、父が鬼のお面をかぶりました。","romaji":"Setsubun no yoru, chichi ga oni no omen o kaburimashita.","german":"On the evening of Setsubun, my father put on a demon mask.","furigana":"せつぶん|よる|ちち|おに|めん"},{"japanese":"節分は毎年二月三日ごろです。","romaji":"Setsubun wa maitoshi nigatsu mikka goro desu.","german":"Setsubun is around February 3 every year.","furigana":"せつぶん|まいとしにがつみっか"}],"notes":"節分 is the day before the traditional start of spring (立春), usually February 3. People throw roasted soybeans (豆まき) and call out 鬼は外、福は内 \"Demons out, good luck in\"; often a family member in a mask plays the demon. Afterwards you eat as many beans as your age in years. You say 節分に豆をまく or 節分の日.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ひな祭りにひな人形を飾ります。","romaji":"Hinamatsuri ni hina ningyō o kazarimasu.","german":"For Girls' Day, we display dolls.","furigana":"まつ|にんぎょう|かざ"},{"japanese":"ひな祭りには、ちらし寿司を食べます。","romaji":"Hinamatsuri ni wa, chirashizushi o tabemasu.","german":"On Girls' Day, we eat chirashi sushi.","furigana":"まつ|ずし|た"},{"japanese":"三月三日はひな祭りで、娘のお祝いをします。","romaji":"Sangatsu mikka wa hinamatsuri de, musume no oiwai o shimasu.","german":"March 3 is Girls' Day, and we celebrate our daughter.","furigana":"さんがつみっか|まつ|むすめ|いわ"}],"notes":"ひな祭り (also 雛祭り or 桃の節句) is the festival for girls on March 3. Families display ひな人形, a tiered stand of dolls representing the imperial court, and eat things such as ちらし寿司. The dolls are set up a few weeks beforehand and put away right after. The counterpart for boys is こどもの日 on May 5.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"こどもの日に鯉のぼりを飾ります。","romaji":"Kodomo no hi ni koinobori o kazarimasu.","german":"On Children's Day, people hang up carp streamers.","furigana":"ひ|こい|かざ"},{"japanese":"こどもの日は五月五日で、国民の祝日です。","romaji":"Kodomo no hi wa gogatsu itsuka de, kokumin no shukujitsu desu.","german":"Children's Day is on May 5 and is a national holiday.","furigana":"ひ|ごがついつか|こくみん|しゅくじつ"}],"notes":"こどもの日 is Japan's Children's Day on May 5 and a national holiday. The name is read こども・の・ひ; の is part of the name. The examples show a typical custom and the day's place in the calendar. 子供の日 is also a dictionary spelling; the existing card uses the official spelling こどもの日.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 26"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"暑いので扇子で扇ぎます。","romaji":"Atsui node sensu de aogimasu.","german":"It is hot, so I fan myself with a folding fan.","furigana":"あつ|せんす|あお"},{"japanese":"京都のお土産に扇子を買いました。","romaji":"Kyouto no omiyage ni sensu o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a folding fan as a souvenir from Kyoto.","furigana":"きょうと|みやげ|せんす|か"},{"japanese":"夏は扇子をバッグに入れておくと便利です。","romaji":"Natsu wa sensu o baggu ni irete oku to benri desu.","german":"In summer it is handy to keep a folding fan in your bag.","furigana":"なつ|せんす|い|べんり"}],"notes":"扇子 is a collapsible folding fan that you open (扇子を広げる) to fan yourself and close again (扇子を閉じる). To fan yourself is 扇子であおぐ (あおぐ is usually written in kana). The flat, round fan often given away at summer festivals, by contrast, is called うちわ; an electric fan is 扇風機.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 20"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"風鈴の音が涼しげです。","romaji":"Fūrin no oto ga suzushige desu.","german":"The sound of the wind chime feels cool.","furigana":"ふうりん|おと|すず"},{"japanese":"夏になると、窓辺に風鈴をつるします。","romaji":"Natsu ni naru to, madobe ni fūrin o tsurushimasu.","german":"In summer, I hang a wind chime by the window.","furigana":"なつ|まどべ|ふうりん"}],"notes":"風鈴 is a wind chime, a small wind bell that rings when moved by the wind. It is often hung at a window in summer. 風鈴の音 refers to its sound; 涼しげ describes that this gives an impression of coolness. This does not mean that the wind chime actually cools the air.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"下駄を履いて歩きます。","romaji":"Geta o haite arukimasu.","german":"I walk in geta.","furigana":"げた|は|ある"},{"japanese":"浴衣に下駄を合わせます。","romaji":"Yukata ni geta o awasemasu.","german":"You wear geta with a yukata.","furigana":"ゆかた|げた|あ"},{"japanese":"夏祭りに下駄で行ったら、足が痛くなりました。","romaji":"Natsu matsuri ni geta de ittara, ashi ga itaku narimashita.","german":"I went to the summer festival in geta, and my feet hurt afterward.","furigana":"なつまつ|げた|い|あし|いた"}],"notes":"下駄 are traditional wooden sandals with two crosspieces under the sole; they clatter when you walk. Like all footwear, they are worn with 履く \"to put on\", nowadays mainly with a 浴衣 at summer festivals. With a kimono, flat 草履 are usually worn instead. The word also appears in 下駄箱, the shoe cabinet at the entrance, even though ordinary shoes are kept in it.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お店に招き猫が置いてあります。","romaji":"Omise ni manekineko ga oite arimasu.","german":"There is a beckoning cat in the shop.","furigana":"みせ|まね|ねこ|お"},{"japanese":"右手を上げている招き猫は、お金を招くと言われています。","romaji":"Migite o agete iru manekineko wa, okane o maneku to iwarete imasu.","german":"A beckoning cat with its right paw raised is said to beckon money.","furigana":"みぎて|あ|まね|ねこ|かね|まね|い"},{"japanese":"日本のお土産に小さい招き猫を買いました。","romaji":"Nihon no omiyage ni chiisai manekineko o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a small beckoning cat as a souvenir from Japan.","furigana":"にほん|みやげ|ちい|まね|ねこ|か"}],"notes":"招き猫 comes from 招く \"to beckon, to invite\": the cat is not waving goodbye but beckoning luck with a raised paw (the Japanese beckoning gesture with the palm facing down). A raised right paw is said to attract money, a left paw customers. You find them at the entrance or register of shops and restaurants; typical is 招き猫が置いてある.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"わざわざ来てくれてありがとう。","romaji":"Wazawaza kite kurete arigatō.","german":"Thank you for coming all this way.","furigana":"き"},{"japanese":"メールで送れるので、わざわざ来なくても大丈夫です。","romaji":"Mēru de okureru node, wazawaza konakute mo daijōbu desu.","german":"You can send it by email, so you don't need to come specially.","furigana":"おく|こ|だいじょうぶ"}],"notes":"わざわざ stresses that someone takes special trouble or an extra trip for an action. When thanking, it acknowledges this effort: わざわざ来てくれてありがとう. With a negative, it can say that the effort is not necessary. Depending on tone, the word can also critically highlight needless effort.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"なるほど、そういうことですか。","romaji":"Naruhodo, sō iu koto desu ka.","german":"I see, so that's how it is."},{"japanese":"なるほど、この方法なら早くできますね。","romaji":"Naruhodo, kono hōhō nara hayaku dekimasu ne.","german":"I see, this method would be faster.","furigana":"ほうほう|はや"}],"notes":"なるほど is a reaction to an explanation: \"I see\" or \"indeed\". It shows that something has become understandable, and it can introduce a conclusion that follows. In conversation, depending on tone, it can also come across as an assessment of the explanation; for very formal agreement, for example 承知しました or そうですか fit better.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"さっぱりわかりません。","romaji":"Sappari wakarimasen.","german":"I don't understand at all."},{"japanese":"シャワーを浴びてさっぱりしました。","romaji":"Shawā o abite sappari shimashita.","german":"I felt refreshed after taking a shower.","furigana":"あ"},{"japanese":"夏はさっぱりしたものが食べたいです。","romaji":"Natsu wa sappari shita mono ga tabetai desu.","german":"In summer I want to eat something light.","furigana":"なつ|た"}],"notes":"さっぱり has two quite different uses. With a negative it means \"not at all\": さっぱりわからない (colloquially stronger than 全然). With する it describes a fresh, clean, relieved feeling (お風呂に入ってさっぱりする), light, non-greasy food (さっぱりした料理, opposite こってり) or an open, uncomplicated character (さっぱりした性格). Compared with すっきり, さっぱり emphasizes freshness and cleanliness more.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"結果にがっかりしました。","romaji":"Kekka ni gakkari shimashita.","german":"I was disappointed with the result.","furigana":"けっか"},{"japanese":"楽しみにしていた旅行が中止になって、がっかりしました。","romaji":"Tanoshimi ni shite ita ryokou ga chuushi ni natte, gakkari shimashita.","german":"I was disappointed that the trip I had been looking forward to was canceled.","furigana":"たの|りょこう|ちゅうし"},{"japanese":"そんなにがっかりしないで。また来年があるよ。","romaji":"Sonna ni gakkari shinaide. Mata rainen ga aru yo.","german":"Don't be so disappointed. There's always next year.","furigana":"らいねん"}],"notes":"がっかり is almost always used as がっかりする \"to be disappointed, to feel let down\". The cause is marked with に (結果にがっかりする) or with a て-form (話を聞いてがっかりした). To disappoint someone is がっかりさせる. 残念 expresses more factual regret and is more polite, while がっかり describes the palpable emotional slump.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"シャワーを浴びてすっきりしました。","romaji":"Shawā o abite sukkiri shimashita.","german":"I felt refreshed after taking a shower.","furigana":"あ"},{"japanese":"部屋を掃除してすっきりしました。","romaji":"Heya o sōji shite sukkiri shimashita.","german":"I felt refreshed after cleaning the room.","furigana":"へや|そうじ"},{"japanese":"言いたいことを言ったら、気持ちがすっきりしました。","romaji":"Iitai koto o ittara, kimochi ga sukkiri shimashita.","german":"After I said what I wanted to say, I felt relieved.","furigana":"い|い|きも"}],"notes":"すっきり describes the feeling that something bothersome is gone: stress, tiredness, clutter, a stuffy nose. Typical are 気分がすっきりする, 頭がすっきりする \"to have a clear head\" and すっきりした部屋 \"a tidy, simple room\". Negated (すっきりしない天気, 気持ちがすっきりしない), it means \"gloomy, unsatisfying\". Similar to さっぱり, but it stresses relief and clarity more than freshness.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"きちんと片付けてください。","romaji":"Kichinto katazukete kudasai.","german":"Please tidy up properly.","furigana":"かたづ"},{"japanese":"毎日きちんと薬を飲んでください。","romaji":"Mainichi kichinto kusuri o nonde kudasai.","german":"Please take your medicine properly every day.","furigana":"まいにち|くすり|の"},{"japanese":"面接にはきちんとした服で行きましょう。","romaji":"Mensetsu ni wa kichinto shita fuku de ikimashou.","german":"Let's go to the interview in proper clothes.","furigana":"めんせつ|ふく|い"}],"notes":"きちんと means \"the way it should be\": neatly, cleanly, correctly, punctually, regularly. It stands before verbs (きちんと片付ける, きちんと説明する) or, as きちんとした, before nouns (きちんとした服, きちんとした人). ちゃんと is almost synonymous, but more casual and colloquial; きちんと puts more emphasis on order and precision.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 38"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"同じ話を何回も聞かされて、うんざりしました。","romaji":"Onaji hanashi o nankai mo kikasarete, unzari shimashita.","german":"I was sick of being made to listen to the same story again and again.","furigana":"おな|はなし|なんかい|き"},{"japanese":"毎日同じ仕事でうんざりです。","romaji":"Mainichi onaji shigoto de unzari desu.","german":"I'm sick of doing the same work every day.","furigana":"まいにちおな|しごと"},{"japanese":"梅雨の雨にはもううんざりです。","romaji":"Tsuyu no ame ni wa mou unzari desu.","german":"I'm fed up with the rain of the rainy season.","furigana":"つゆ|あめ"}],"notes":"うんざり expresses that you are thoroughly sick of something because it repeats too often or lasts too long. Typical: Xにうんざりする, Xにはもううんざりだ, うんざりした顔. Compared with 飽きる \"to get tired of something, to lose interest\", うんざり is stronger and contains annoyance or aversion.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ぼんやりしていました。","romaji":"Bon'yari shite imashita.","german":"I was spacing out."},{"japanese":"窓の外をぼんやり眺めています。","romaji":"Mado no soto o bon'yari nagamete imasu.","german":"I'm gazing absent-mindedly out the window.","furigana":"まど|そと|なが"},{"japanese":"眼鏡がないと、字がぼんやり見えます。","romaji":"Megane ga nai to, ji ga bon'yari miemasu.","german":"Without glasses, I see the letters only blurrily.","furigana":"めがね|じ|み"}],"notes":"ぼんやり has two main meanings. First, \"indistinct, blurry\": ぼんやり見える, ぼんやり覚えている \"to remember vaguely\"; the opposite is はっきり. Second, \"absent-minded, lost in thought\": ぼんやりする, ぼんやり眺める. A single careless slip from inattention is rather うっかり.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"慌ただしい毎日を送っています。","romaji":"Awatadashii mainichi o okutteimasu.","german":"I lead a hectic life.","furigana":"あわ|まいにち|おく"},{"japanese":"年末は慌ただしいです。","romaji":"Nenmatsu wa awatadashii desu.","german":"The end of the year is hectic.","furigana":"ねんまつ|あわ"},{"japanese":"朝は時間がなくて、慌ただしく家を出ました。","romaji":"Asa wa jikan ga nakute, awatadashiku ie o demashita.","german":"I had no time in the morning and left the house in a rush.","furigana":"あさ|じかん|あわ|いえ|で"}],"notes":"慌ただしい describes a time or situation as hectic and rushed: 慌ただしい毎日, 年末は慌ただしい, adverbially 慌ただしく出かける. It is related to 慌てる \"to get flustered\". 忙しい mainly says that you have a lot to do; 慌ただしい stresses the restless, rushed atmosphere and sounds somewhat more literary. Often also written in kana: あわただしい.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"勿体ぶらないで教えて。","romaji":"Mottaiburanaide oshiete.","german":"Stop being so mysterious and tell me.","furigana":"もったい|おし"},{"japanese":"彼はいつも勿体ぶった話し方をします。","romaji":"Kare wa itsumo mottaibutta hanashikata o shimasu.","german":"He always speaks in a pompous way.","furigana":"かれ|もったい|はな|かた"},{"japanese":"先生は勿体ぶって、答えをなかなか言いませんでした。","romaji":"Sensei wa mottaibutte, kotae o nakanaka iimasen deshita.","german":"The teacher made a show of it and didn't tell us the answer for a long time.","furigana":"せんせい|もったい|こた|い"}],"notes":"勿体ぶる (usually in kana もったいぶる) means \"to put on airs\" or \"to make it needlessly suspenseful\", e.g. when someone deliberately withholds information. Very common is the request 勿体ぶらないで教えて \"don't keep me in suspense\". As an attribute, 勿体ぶった話し方 describes a pompous, self-important manner. It is intransitive; ぶる means \"to act as if one were\" (cf. 偉ぶる). Despite the same root, it has nothing to do with もったいない \"a pity, wasteful\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"無駄遣いをやめましょう。","romaji":"Mudazukai o yamemashou.","german":"Let's stop wasting money.","furigana":"むだづか"},{"japanese":"時間を無駄にしないでください。","romaji":"Jikan o muda ni shinaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't waste time.","furigana":"じかん|むだ"},{"japanese":"いくら言っても無駄です。","romaji":"Ikura itte mo muda desu.","german":"No matter how often you say it, it's useless.","furigana":"い|むだ"}],"notes":"無駄 is a noun and a な-adjective: \"waste, futility\". Important patterns are Xを無駄にする \"to waste X\", 無駄な時間 \"wasted time\", 〜ても無駄だ \"it is useless to ...\" and 無駄遣い \"wasteful spending\". 無駄がない praises something as efficient. もったいない, by contrast, is the exclamation that something valuable is a pity because it is being wasted.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"当然のことです。","romaji":"Tōzen no koto desu.","german":"That goes without saying.","furigana":"とうぜん"},{"japanese":"約束したんだから、当然行きますよ。","romaji":"Yakusoku shita n da kara, touzen ikimasu yo.","german":"I promised, so of course I'll come.","furigana":"やくそく|とうぜんい"},{"japanese":"悪いことをしたら、怒られるのは当然です。","romaji":"Warui koto o shitara, okorareru no wa touzen desu.","german":"If you do something bad, it's natural that you get scolded.","furigana":"わる|おこ|とうぜん"}],"notes":"当然 means \"self-evident, natural, as expected\". As a noun or な-adjective it appears in 当然のこと and 〜のは当然だ \"it is only natural that ...\", as an adverb directly before the verb (当然行きます). もちろん is more everyday and also serves as an answer, \"but of course!\"; 当然 sounds more matter-of-fact, but as a brief answer (当然でしょう) it is slightly blunt. A colloquial synonym is 当たり前.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"万が一のために準備します。","romaji":"Mangaichi no tame ni junbi shimasu.","german":"I'm preparing for the worst case.","furigana":"まん|いち|じゅんび"},{"japanese":"万が一に備えて保険に入りました。","romaji":"Mangaichi ni sonaete hoken ni hairimashita.","german":"I took out insurance in case something happens.","furigana":"まん|いち|そな|ほけん|はい"},{"japanese":"万が一遅れる場合は、電話してください。","romaji":"Mangaichi okureru baai wa, denwa shite kudasai.","german":"If, against expectations, you are late, please call.","furigana":"まん|いちおく|ばあい|でんわ"}],"notes":"万が一 stresses that something is highly unlikely (\"one in ten thousand\"). As an adverb it goes with conditions: 万が一〜たら / 〜場合は \"in the unlikely event that ...\". As a noun it refers to an emergency: 万が一に備える, 万が一のとき, 万が一のために. The short form 万一 is more literary. Unlike もし, the case is explicitly unlikely; 念のため, by contrast, means \"to be on the safe side\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"たったいま着きました。","romaji":"Tatta ima tsukimashita.","german":"I just arrived.","furigana":"つ"},{"japanese":"たったいま届きました。","romaji":"Tatta ima todokimashita.","german":"It just arrived.","furigana":"とど"},{"japanese":"部長はたったいま出かけたところです。","romaji":"Buchou wa tatta ima dekaketa tokoro desu.","german":"The department head has just stepped out.","furigana":"ぶちょう|で"}],"notes":"たったいま (usually written たった今) is the emphasized form of 今: \"just now, only this very moment\". It is used with the past tense (たったいま着きました) and very often with 〜たところ. さっき means \"a little while ago\" and can already be several minutes or hours back; たったいま is truly immediate.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"もしかしたら明日は雨かもしれません。","romaji":"Moshikashitara ashita wa ame kamo shiremasen.","german":"It might rain tomorrow.","furigana":"あした|あめ"},{"japanese":"もしかしたら、財布を電車に忘れたかもしれない。","romaji":"Moshikashitara, saifu o densha ni wasureta kamo shirenai.","german":"I may have left my wallet on the train.","furigana":"さいふ|でんしゃ|わす"},{"japanese":"もしかしたら、パーティーに行けないかもしれません。","romaji":"Moshikashitara, paatii ni ikenai kamo shiremasen.","german":"I may not be able to go to the party.","furigana":"い"}],"notes":"もしかしたら \"maybe, possibly\" almost always goes with かもしれない or のではないか at the end of the sentence. It expresses a rather low probability, weaker than たぶん. もしかすると means the same and is a little more formal; もしかして is used mainly in questions: もしかして田中さんですか \"Are you Mr. Tanaka, by any chance?\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 32"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仕方がないですね。","romaji":"Shikata ga nai desu ne.","german":"It can't be helped.","furigana":"しかた"},{"japanese":"仕方がないから自分でやります。","romaji":"Shikata ga nai kara jibun de yarimasu.","german":"It can't be helped, so I'll do it myself.","furigana":"しかた|じぶん"},{"japanese":"新しいゲームがほしくて仕方がない。","romaji":"Atarashii geemu ga hoshikute shikata ga nai.","german":"I want the new game so badly.","furigana":"あたら|しかた"}],"notes":"仕方がない literally means \"there is no way (to do it)\" and expresses acceptance of a situation: \"nothing can be done about it\". More casual are 仕方ない and しょうがない; the polite form is 仕方がありません. 〜ても仕方がない means \"there is no point in ...\", and after the て-form of adjectives it means \"unbearably, extremely\": 眠くて仕方がない \"I'm dead tired\". It is conjugated like an い-adjective.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"気にしないでください。","romaji":"Ki ni shinaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't worry about it.","furigana":"き"},{"japanese":"彼女は人の目をとても気にします。","romaji":"Kanojo wa hito no me o totemo ki ni shimasu.","german":"She cares a lot about what other people think of her.","furigana":"かのじょ|ひと|め|き"},{"japanese":"値段は気にしないで、好きなものを頼んでください。","romaji":"Nedan wa ki ni shinaide, suki na mono o tanonde kudasai.","german":"Don't worry about the price; order whatever you like.","furigana":"ねだん|き|す|たの"}],"notes":"Xを気にする means \"to worry about X, to pay attention to X\", often with the nuance that one worries too much (人の目を気にする \"to worry about what others think\"). 気にしないで(ください) is the usual reassurance \"never mind, don't worry about it\". The intransitive counterpart is Xが気になる: something occupies your mind on its own or catches your attention, whereas 気にする is conscious attention.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"間違いに気が付いた。","romaji":"Machigai ni ki ga tsuita.","german":"I noticed the mistake.","furigana":"まちが|き|つ"},{"japanese":"駅に着いてから、財布がないことに気が付きました。","romaji":"Eki ni tsuite kara, saifu ga nai koto ni ki ga tsukimashita.","german":"After I arrived at the station, I realized that I didn't have my wallet.","furigana":"えき|つ|さいふ|き|つ"}],"notes":"気が付く means to notice something or become aware of it. The 気が is a fixed part of the phrase. With に you name what is noticed; a whole situation can be attached with ことに. The final part is a godan verb: 気が付きます, 気が付いて, 気が付いた.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 21"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"手伝いが必要ですか。","romaji":"Tetsudai ga hitsuyō desu ka.","german":"Do you need help?","furigana":"てつだ|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"引っ越しの手伝いをしました。","romaji":"Hikkoshi no tetsudai o shimashita.","german":"I helped with the move.","furigana":"ひ|こ|てつだ"},{"japanese":"子どものころ、よく家の手伝いをしました。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, yoku ie no tetsudai o shimashita.","german":"When I was a child, I often helped around the house.","furigana":"こ|いえ|てつだ"}],"notes":"手伝い is the noun of 手伝う and refers to helping with a task that someone else is doing: 引っ越しの手伝い, 家の手伝い \"helping around the house\". One says 手伝いをする, humbly お手伝いします / お手伝いしましょうか. 助け, by contrast, is help or rescue in an emergency. お手伝いさん also refers to a housekeeper.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"最初からやり直します。","romaji":"Saisho kara yarinaoshimasu.","german":"I'll start over from the beginning.","furigana":"さいしょ|なお"},{"japanese":"最初からやり直しましょう。","romaji":"Saisho kara yarinaoshimashō.","german":"Let's start over from the beginning.","furigana":"さいしょ|なお"},{"japanese":"計算に間違いがあったので、最後の部分だけやり直しました。","romaji":"Keisan ni machigai ga atta node, saigo no bubun dake yarinaoshimashita.","german":"There was a mistake in the calculation, so I redid only the last part.","furigana":"けいさん|まちが|さいご|ぶぶん|なお"}],"notes":"やり直す means to start an activity again or to carry it out once more, often to correct a result. 最初からやり直す stresses a fresh start from the beginning; one can also redo only a certain part. It is a transitive godan verb ending in す: やり直します・やり直して・やり直さない.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"計画を見直す必要があります。","romaji":"Keikaku o minaosu hitsuyou ga arimasu.","german":"We need to review the plan.","furigana":"けいかく|みなお|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"出す前に、もう一度答えを見直してください。","romaji":"Dasu mae ni, mou ichido kotae o minaoshite kudasai.","german":"Please look over your answers once more before handing them in.","furigana":"だ|まえ|いちどこた|みなお"},{"japanese":"困っているときに助けてくれて、彼を見直しました。","romaji":"Komatte iru toki ni tasukete kurete, kare o minaoshimashita.","german":"Because he helped me when I was in trouble, I now see him in a new light.","furigana":"こま|たす|かれ|みなお"}],"notes":"見直す (見る + 直す \"once more, anew\") has three common meanings: to look something over again (答えを見直す), to reconsider and revise plans or rules (計画を見直す, noun 見直し), and to see someone in a better light (彼を見直した). The last meaning is always positive; a change of opinion for the worse is not expressed by 見直す. Transitive with を.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"昔のことを思い出しました。","romaji":"Mukashi no koto o omoidashimashita.","german":"I remembered the old days.","furigana":"むかし|おも|だ"},{"japanese":"学生時代を思い出します。","romaji":"Gakusei jidai o omoidashimasu.","german":"I remember my student days.","furigana":"がくせいじだい|おも|だ"},{"japanese":"あの人の名前がなかなか思い出せません。","romaji":"Ano hito no namae ga nakanaka omoidasemasen.","german":"I just can't recall that person's name.","furigana":"ひと|なまえ|おも|だ"}],"notes":"Xを思い出す means \"to remember X\" in the sense of \"calling something (back) to mind\", so it is a process. That you still know something is expressed by 覚えている instead. When something won't come to mind, the potential form 思い出せない is used. To remind someone of something is 思い出させる; the noun 思い出 means \"memory\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"同じ間違いを繰り返さないように。","romaji":"Onaji machigai o kurikaesanai yō ni.","german":"Don't repeat the same mistake.","furigana":"おな|まちが|く|かえ"},{"japanese":"私の後について繰り返してください。","romaji":"Watashi no ato ni tsuite kurikaeshite kudasai.","german":"Please repeat after me.","furigana":"わたし|あと|く|かえ"},{"japanese":"歴史は繰り返すと言われています。","romaji":"Rekishi wa kurikaesu to iwarete imasu.","german":"They say history repeats itself.","furigana":"れきし|く|かえ|い"}],"notes":"繰り返す means \"to repeat\", often several times: 同じ間違いを繰り返す, 何度も繰り返して練習する. Mostly transitive with を, but it can also be used intransitively: 歴史は繰り返す \"history repeats itself\". The noun or adverb 繰り返し means \"repetition, over and over\". For a simple \"say it once more\", もう一度言う is enough in everyday speech.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"深呼吸をして落ち着いてください。","romaji":"Shinkokyuu o shite ochitsuite kudasai.","german":"Take a deep breath and calm down.","furigana":"しんこきゅう|お|つ"},{"japanese":"新しい町の生活にやっと落ち着きました。","romaji":"Atarashii machi no seikatsu ni yatto ochitsukimashita.","german":"I have finally settled into life in the new town.","furigana":"あたら|まち|せいかつ|お|つ"},{"japanese":"この喫茶店は静かで落ち着きます。","romaji":"Kono kissaten wa shizuka de ochitsukimasu.","german":"This café is quiet, and I can really relax here.","furigana":"きっさてん|しず|お|つ"}],"notes":"落ち着く is intransitive and means \"to come to rest\": to calm down (落ち着いてください), to subside (熱が落ち着く), to settle in (新しい生活に落ち着く), and to feel comfortable and relaxed in a place (この部屋は落ち着く). 落ち着いた色 or 落ち着いた人 means \"subdued\" or \"composed\" respectively. The transitive counterpart is 落ち着ける (気持ちを落ち着ける), and the opposite is 慌てる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女と三年付き合っています。","romaji":"Kanojo to sannen tsukiatte imasu.","german":"I have been going out with her for three years.","furigana":"かのじょ|さんねんつ|あ"},{"japanese":"買い物に付き合ってくれない？","romaji":"Kaimono ni tsukiatte kurenai?","german":"Will you come shopping with me?","furigana":"か|もの|つ|あ"},{"japanese":"仕事の後、上司に付き合って飲みに行きました。","romaji":"Shigoto no ato, joushi ni tsukiatte nomi ni ikimashita.","german":"After work, I went out drinking to keep my boss company.","furigana":"しごと|あと|じょうし|つ|あ|の|い"}],"notes":"付き合う has two main meanings. With と: \"to be together with someone, to be in a relationship\" or generally \"to associate with\" (彼女と付き合う, 付き合ってください as a love confession). With に: \"to accompany someone, to join in on something\" (買い物に付き合う). The noun 付き合い means \"association, social obligation\", e.g. 付き合いで飲みに行く. Often also written つきあう; intransitive.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 15"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"手を振って挨拶します。","romaji":"Te o futte aisatsu shimasu.","german":"I wave to greet someone.","furigana":"て|ふ|あいさつ"},{"japanese":"肉に塩を振ります。","romaji":"Niku ni shio o furimasu.","german":"I sprinkle salt on the meat.","furigana":"にく|しお|ふ"},{"japanese":"好きな人に告白したけど、振られました。","romaji":"Suki na hito ni kokuhaku shita kedo, furaremashita.","german":"I confessed to the person I like, but I got turned down.","furigana":"す|ひと|こくはく|ふ"}],"notes":"振る is a 五段動詞 (振って, 振らない) with the basic meaning \"to swing, to shake\": 手を振る \"to wave\", 首を横に振る \"to shake one's head\", バットを振る. It also means \"to sprinkle\" (塩を振る) and, in the passive 振られる, \"to get turned down, to be dumped\". Not to be confused with the identically sounding 降る \"to fall (rain, snow)\". The intransitive counterpart is 振れる \"to swing, to deflect\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"予定をずらしてもいいですか。","romaji":"Yotei o zurashite mo ii desu ka.","german":"May I shift the appointment?","furigana":"よてい"},{"japanese":"机を少し右にずらしてください。","romaji":"Tsukue o sukoshi migi ni zurashite kudasai.","german":"Please move the desk a little to the right.","furigana":"つくえ|すこ|みぎ"},{"japanese":"電車が混むので、会社に行く時間を一時間ずらしました。","romaji":"Densha ga komu node, kaisha ni iku jikan o ichijikan zurashimashita.","german":"Since the trains are crowded, I shifted my commuting time by an hour.","furigana":"でんしゃ|こ|かいしゃ|い|じかん|いちじかん"}],"notes":"ずらす means \"to shift something a little\", spatially (机を右にずらす) or in time (予定をずらす, 時間をずらす), without canceling it; it can be forward or backward. The intransitive counterpart is ずれる \"to slip, to shift\" (予定がずれる). 延期する, by contrast, explicitly means \"to postpone to later\" and is more formal; 動かす is a general \"to move\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"休日は家でごろごろしています。","romaji":"Kyuujitsu wa ie de gorogoro shite imasu.","german":"On days off I lie around at home.","furigana":"きゅうじつ|いえ"},{"japanese":"遠くで雷がごろごろ鳴っています。","romaji":"Tooku de kaminari ga gorogoro natte imasu.","german":"Thunder is rumbling in the distance.","furigana":"とお|かみなり|な"},{"japanese":"目にごみが入って、ごろごろします。","romaji":"Me ni gomi ga haitte, gorogoro shimasu.","german":"I got dirt in my eye and it feels gritty.","furigana":"め|はい"}],"notes":"ごろごろ is an onomatopoeic word with several meanings. Most common is ごろごろする \"to lie around lazily, to laze about\" (家でごろごろする). It also describes a dull rumbling (雷がごろごろ鳴る, also a growling stomach or a purring cat), the rolling of heavy things, and a scratchy foreign-body feeling in the eye (目がごろごろする). Small children also call thunder ごろごろ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"試験の結果が気になってそわそわしています。","romaji":"Shiken no kekka ga ki ni natte sowasowa shite imasu.","german":"I'm restless because I'm worried about my exam results.","furigana":"しけん|けっか|き"},{"japanese":"今日はデートなので、朝からそわそわしています。","romaji":"Kyou wa deeto na node, asa kara sowasowa shite imasu.","german":"I have a date today, so I've been jittery since the morning.","furigana":"きょう|あさ"},{"japanese":"そわそわしないで、座って待っていてください。","romaji":"Sowasowa shinaide, suwatte matte ite kudasai.","german":"Don't fidget; sit down and wait.","furigana":"すわ|ま"}],"notes":"そわそわ describes visible restlessness: you can't sit still because you are nervous or impatiently waiting for something, e.g. a result or a date. Mostly as そわそわする. どきどき, by contrast, refers to the inner pounding of the heart, and いらいら to irritable impatience. The opposite is 落ち着いている.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ぎりぎり電車に間に合いました。","romaji":"Girigiri densha ni maniaimashita.","german":"I just barely made the train.","furigana":"でんしゃ|ま|あ"},{"japanese":"締め切りぎりぎりにレポートを出しました。","romaji":"Shimekiri girigiri ni repooto o dashimashita.","german":"I handed in the report right at the deadline.","furigana":"し|き|だ"},{"japanese":"ぎりぎりの点数で試験に合格しました。","romaji":"Girigiri no tensuu de shiken ni goukaku shimashita.","german":"I passed the exam with a score right at the borderline.","furigana":"てんすう|しけん|ごうかく"}],"notes":"ぎりぎり means \"just barely, at the last minute, at the very limit\". Typical patterns are ぎりぎり間に合う, noun + ぎりぎり (締め切りぎりぎり, 時間ぎりぎり) and ぎりぎりの + noun (ぎりぎりの点数). Colloquially people shout ぎりぎりセーフ \"made it just in time\". Another word of the same form is the onomatopoeia for grinding teeth (歯をぎりぎりさせる).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いきなり雨が降り出しました。","romaji":"Ikinari ame ga furidashimashita.","german":"It suddenly started to rain.","furigana":"あめ|ふ|だ"},{"japanese":"知らない人にいきなり話しかけられて、びっくりしました。","romaji":"Shiranai hito ni ikinari hanashikakerarete, bikkuri shimashita.","german":"A stranger suddenly spoke to me, and I was startled.","furigana":"し|ひと|はな"},{"japanese":"いきなり難しい問題から始めないほうがいいですよ。","romaji":"Ikinari muzukashii mondai kara hajimenai hou ga ii desu yo.","german":"You shouldn't start with difficult problems right away.","furigana":"むずか|もんだい|はじ"}],"notes":"いきなり means \"suddenly, without warning\" and often also \"right away, without a preliminary step\" (いきなり難しい問題から始める). It stresses that something happens unexpectedly and abruptly, often through an action that one finds startling. 急に is more neutral and describes mainly sudden changes (急に寒くなる); 突然 is somewhat more formal. Almost always in kana.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"台風で部屋がめちゃくちゃになりました。","romaji":"Taifuu de heya ga mechakucha ni narimashita.","german":"The room was in a complete mess because of the typhoon.","furigana":"たいふう|へや"},{"japanese":"このケーキ、めちゃくちゃおいしい！","romaji":"Kono keeki, mechakucha oishii!","german":"This cake is super delicious!"},{"japanese":"彼の言っていることはめちゃくちゃです。","romaji":"Kare no itte iru koto wa mechakucha desu.","german":"What he is saying makes no sense at all.","furigana":"かれ|い"}],"notes":"めちゃくちゃ is colloquial. As a な-adjective it means \"completely messed up, wrecked\" (部屋がめちゃくちゃだ, 〜をめちゃくちゃにする) or \"nonsensical, absurd\" (めちゃくちゃな話). As an adverb it intensifies like \"totally, super\": めちゃくちゃおいしい; short forms are めちゃ and めっちゃ. In neutral or polite situations people say とても instead.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 21"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼はぶつぶつ文句を言っています。","romaji":"Kare wa butsubutsu monku o itte imasu.","german":"He is grumbling and complaining.","furigana":"かれ|もんく|い"},{"japanese":"おじいさんはテレビを見ながら、何かぶつぶつ言っています。","romaji":"Ojiisan wa terebi o minagara, nanika butsubutsu itte imasu.","german":"Grandpa is watching TV and muttering something.","furigana":"み|なに|い"},{"japanese":"顔にぶつぶつができました。","romaji":"Kao ni butsubutsu ga dekimashita.","german":"I got spots on my face.","furigana":"かお"}],"notes":"ぶつぶつ describes quiet, discontented muttering to oneself: ぶつぶつ言う, ぶつぶつ文句を言う \"to grumble\". With ひそひそ, by contrast, you whisper secretly with someone else. As a noun, ぶつぶつ also means \"pimples, rash\": 顔にぶつぶつができる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女はてきぱき仕事をします。","romaji":"Kanojo wa tekipaki shigoto o shimasu.","german":"She works briskly and efficiently.","furigana":"かのじょ|しごと"},{"japanese":"彼は質問にてきぱきと答えました。","romaji":"Kare wa shitsumon ni tekipaki to kotaemashita.","german":"He answered the questions promptly and clearly.","furigana":"かれ|しつもん|こた"},{"japanese":"てきぱきした店員さんで、気持ちがよかったです。","romaji":"Tekipaki shita ten'in-san de, kimochi ga yokatta desu.","german":"The clerk was quick and capable, which was pleasant.","furigana":"てんいん|きも"}],"notes":"てきぱき praises prompt, efficient and businesslike action: てきぱき(と)働く, てきぱき答える, てきぱきした人 \"a capable, brisk person\". It is about efficiency and decisiveness, not just speed. Opposites are のろのろ \"sluggish\" and ぐずぐず \"dawdling, hesitant\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"のろのろ運転はやめてください。","romaji":"Noronoro unten wa yamete kudasai.","german":"Please stop driving so slowly.","furigana":"うんてん"},{"japanese":"のろのろ歩かないでください。","romaji":"Noronoro arukanaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't walk so slowly.","furigana":"ある"},{"japanese":"朝は眠くて、のろのろ支度をしました。","romaji":"Asa wa nemukute, noronoro shitaku o shimashita.","german":"I was so sleepy in the morning that I got ready at a snail's pace.","furigana":"あさ|ねむ|したく"}],"notes":"のろのろ describes a sluggish, slow movement and usually sounds annoyed: のろのろ歩く, のろのろ運転 \"crawling along, stop-and-go traffic\". Related is the adjective のろい \"slow, sluggish\". ゆっくり, by contrast, is neutral or positive (ゆっくり休む). Opposites: てきぱき, さっさと.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"こっそり出かけました。","romaji":"Kossori dekakemashita.","german":"I went out secretly.","furigana":"で"},{"japanese":"こっそりお菓子を食べました。","romaji":"Kossori okashi o tabemashita.","german":"I secretly ate sweets.","furigana":"かし|た"},{"japanese":"いい店をこっそり教えてあげるね。","romaji":"Ii mise o kossori oshiete ageru ne.","german":"I'll tell you about a good shop in secret.","furigana":"みせ|おし"}],"notes":"こっそり means \"secretly, unnoticed\" and describes actions that others should not notice: こっそり出かける, こっそり食べる, こっそり教える \"to tell on the quiet\". It stands before the verb, often with と. ひそかに is more literary and can also refer to inner feelings (ひそかに思う); 内緒で stresses that you tell no one.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あっさりした味が好きです。","romaji":"Assari shita aji ga suki desu.","german":"I like a light flavor.","furigana":"あじ|す"},{"japanese":"彼はあっさり負けを認めました。","romaji":"Kare wa assari make o mitomemashita.","german":"He readily admitted defeat.","furigana":"かれ|ま|みと"}],"notes":"あっさり describes two kinds of lightness here. In あっさりした味, the flavor is mild, not heavy or rich. Before an action, it can mean that it happens quickly and without resistance, such as あっさり負けを認める. Both uses are possible; \"plain\" alone does not always fit the flavor well.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"こってりしたラーメンが好きです。","romaji":"Kotteri shita rāmen ga suki desu.","german":"I like rich ramen.","furigana":"す"},{"japanese":"夏はこってりした料理より、さっぱりしたものがいいです。","romaji":"Natsu wa kotteri shita ryouri yori, sappari shita mono ga ii desu.","german":"In summer I prefer light food to heavy dishes.","furigana":"なつ|りょうり"},{"japanese":"遅刻して、先生にこってり叱られました。","romaji":"Chikoku shite, sensei ni kotteri shikararemashita.","german":"I was late and got a thorough scolding from the teacher.","furigana":"ちこく|せんせい|しか"}],"notes":"こってり mainly describes heavy, oily, rich food: こってりしたラーメン, こってりした味. Opposites are あっさり and さっぱり \"light, not greasy\". It can also describe thickly applied makeup. A second meaning is \"thoroughly, good and proper\" with scolding: こってり叱られる \"to get a good talking-to\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"しぶしぶ賛成しました。","romaji":"Shibushibu sansei shimashita.","german":"I agreed reluctantly.","furigana":"さんせい"},{"japanese":"しぶしぶ引き受けました。","romaji":"Shibushibu hikiukemashita.","german":"I accepted it reluctantly.","furigana":"ひ|う"},{"japanese":"母に言われて、しぶしぶ部屋を片付けました。","romaji":"Haha ni iwarete, shibushibu heya o katazukemashita.","german":"My mother told me to, so I reluctantly tidied up my room.","furigana":"はは|い|へや|かたづ"}],"notes":"しぶしぶ (also 渋々, from 渋い \"astringent\") means \"reluctantly, out of necessity\": you do something because you must or were asked to, although you don't feel like it (しぶしぶ賛成する, しぶしぶ引き受ける). いやいや shows the aversion even more clearly, while 仕方なく stresses that there was no other choice.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二人がひそひそ話をしています。","romaji":"Futari ga hisohiso hanashi o shite imasu.","german":"The two of them are whispering to each other.","furigana":"ふたり|はなし"},{"japanese":"図書館なので、ひそひそ声で話しました。","romaji":"Toshokan na node, hisohiso goe de hanashimashita.","german":"We were in the library, so we spoke in whispers.","furigana":"としょかん|ごえ|はな"},{"japanese":"後ろの席で、誰かがひそひそと私のことを話しています。","romaji":"Ushiro no seki de, dareka ga hisohiso to watashi no koto o hanashite imasu.","german":"Someone in the seat behind me is whispering about me.","furigana":"うし|せき|だれ|わたし|はな"}],"notes":"ひそひそ describes quiet whispering so that others cannot hear, often with an undertone of muttering or secrecy: ひそひそ話をする, ひそひそ声で話す. ぶつぶつ, by contrast, is discontented muttering to oneself. The verb ささやく \"to whisper, to murmur\" is more neutral and can also be tender.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いつもにこにこしています。","romaji":"Itsumo nikoniko shiteimasu.","german":"He is always smiling."},{"japanese":"おばあさんがにこにこ笑っています。","romaji":"Obāsan ga nikoniko waratte imasu.","german":"The grandmother is smiling warmly.","furigana":"わら"},{"japanese":"店員さんがにこにこと迎えてくれました。","romaji":"Ten'in-san ga nikoniko to mukaete kuremashita.","german":"The shop assistant welcomed us with a smile.","furigana":"てんいん|むか"}],"notes":"にこにこ describes a friendly, warm smile without laughing: にこにこする, にこにこ(と)笑う, にこにこ顔. Distinguish it from にやにや \"to grin\" (smugly or suggestively), くすくす \"to giggle\" and げらげら \"to laugh loudly\". A single brief smile is にっこり.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"めそめそ泣かないで。","romaji":"Mesomeso nakanai de.","german":"Don't cry like that.","furigana":"な"},{"japanese":"失恋してから、姉は毎日めそめそしています。","romaji":"Shitsuren shite kara, ane wa mainichi mesomeso shite imasu.","german":"Since her heartbreak, my older sister has been weepy every day.","furigana":"しつれん|あね|まいにち"}],"notes":"めそめそ describes quiet, tearful sobbing or continuous whining with tears. It goes with 泣く or with する: めそめそしている. The expression can sound disparaging; めそめそ泣かないで is a direct, not very empathetic request and not a neutral description of sadness.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お腹がぺこぺこです。","romaji":"Onaka ga pekopeko desu.","german":"I'm starving.","furigana":"なか"},{"japanese":"彼はいつも上司にぺこぺこしています。","romaji":"Kare wa itsumo jōshi ni pekopeko shite imasu.","german":"He is always bowing and scraping to his boss.","furigana":"かれ|じょうし"}],"notes":"ぺこぺこ has two meanings practiced here. お腹がぺこぺこです means that you are very hungry; this describes a state. 人にぺこぺこする refers to repeated, often servile bowing toward someone and can sound critical. The listed base forms have different accents depending on the role: the hungry state 0, the adverbial bowing 1.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ぐずぐずしないで早く支度して。","romaji":"Guzuguzu shinaide hayaku shitaku shite.","german":"Don't dawdle; get ready quickly.","furigana":"はや|したく"},{"japanese":"ぐずぐずしていると、電車に遅れますよ。","romaji":"Guzuguzu shite iru to, densha ni okuremasu yo.","german":"If you dawdle, you'll miss the train.","furigana":"でんしゃ|おく"},{"japanese":"いつまでもぐずぐず悩んでいないで、早く決めなさい。","romaji":"Itsumade mo guzuguzu nayande inaide, hayaku kimenasai.","german":"Stop agonizing over it forever and decide already.","furigana":"なや|はや|き"}],"notes":"ぐずぐず describes dawdling, hesitating and indecision: ぐずぐずする, ぐずぐずしないで \"don't dawdle, come on\". With のろのろ it is about slow movement, with ぐずぐず rather about not getting going. There is also ぐずぐず言う \"to grumble\". Related is ぐずる \"to fuss\" (of children). Colloquial and almost always in kana.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"靴がぼろぼろです。","romaji":"Kutsu ga boroboro desu.","german":"My shoes are worn out.","furigana":"くつ"},{"japanese":"毎日残業で、体がぼろぼろです。","romaji":"Mainichi zangyou de, karada ga boroboro desu.","german":"With overtime every day, my body is completely wrecked.","furigana":"まいにちざんぎょう|からだ"},{"japanese":"映画を見て、涙がぼろぼろ出ました。","romaji":"Eiga o mite, namida ga boroboro demashita.","german":"Watching the movie, tears streamed down my face.","furigana":"えいが|み|なみだ|で"}],"notes":"ぼろぼろ comes from ぼろ \"rags\" and as a な-/の-adjective means \"worn out, tattered, broken\" (ぼろぼろの靴, 本がぼろぼろだ), figuratively also \"completely exhausted, at the end of one's rope\" (体がぼろぼろ). As an adverb it describes crumbs or drops falling in large quantities: 涙がぼろぼろ出る, パンがぼろぼろこぼれる. Smaller, lighter drops are described by ぽろぽろ.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"星がきらきら光っています。","romaji":"Hoshi ga kirakira hikatte imasu.","german":"The stars are twinkling.","furigana":"ほし|ひか"},{"japanese":"子どもの目がきらきら輝いていました。","romaji":"Kodomo no me ga kirakira kagayaite imashita.","german":"The child's eyes were sparkling.","furigana":"こ|め|かがや"}],"notes":"きらきら describes many small or changing reflections of light, that is, twinkling or sparkling. It is used with 光る and 輝く, for example; きらきらと is also possible. With stars, actual light is meant; with a child's eyes, the expression can convey enthusiasm at the same time.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"梅雨の時期はじめじめしています。","romaji":"Tsuyu no jiki wa jimejime shite imasu.","german":"It is damp during the rainy season.","furigana":"つゆ|じき"},{"japanese":"この部屋はじめじめしているので、洗濯物が乾きません。","romaji":"Kono heya wa jimejime shite iru node, sentakumono ga kawakimasen.","german":"This room is so damp that the laundry doesn't dry.","furigana":"へや|せんたくもの|かわ"},{"japanese":"じめじめした話はやめて、楽しい話をしましょう。","romaji":"Jimejime shita hanashi wa yamete, tanoshii hanashi o shimashou.","german":"Let's stop the gloomy talk and talk about something fun.","furigana":"はなし|たの|はなし"}],"notes":"じめじめ describes unpleasant, clammy dampness, as in the rainy season or in poorly ventilated rooms: じめじめした天気, 部屋がじめじめする. むしむし, by contrast, stresses muggy heat. Figuratively, じめじめ can also mean \"gloomy, dreary\" (じめじめした話, じめじめした性格). The opposite is からっと \"dry, crisp\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"太陽がかんかん照っています。","romaji":"Taiyō ga kankan tetteimasu.","german":"The sun is blazing down.","furigana":"たいよう|て"},{"japanese":"かんかん照りの暑い日です。","romaji":"Kankan deri no atsui hi desu.","german":"It's a scorching hot day.","furigana":"|あつ|ひ"},{"japanese":"約束を忘れたので、彼女はかんかんに怒っています。","romaji":"Yakusoku o wasureta node, kanojo wa kankan ni okotte imasu.","german":"I forgot our appointment, so she is absolutely furious.","furigana":"やくそく|わす|かのじょ|おこ"}],"notes":"かんかん has two common meanings. With the sun: \"merciless, blazing\" (日がかんかん照る, the noun かんかん照り \"scorching sun\", here read かんかんでり). With people: \"livid, hopping mad\", mostly about others (かんかんに怒る, 父はかんかんだ). As a sound (\"clang, ding\") for example of a railroad crossing, it is usually written in katakana カンカン.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"雨がしとしと降っています。","romaji":"Ame ga shitoshito futte imasu.","german":"It is drizzling softly.","furigana":"あめ|ふ"},{"japanese":"梅雨の時期は、しとしと降る雨の日が多いです。","romaji":"Tsuyu no jiki wa, shitoshito furu ame no hi ga ooi desu.","german":"The rainy season has many days of quiet drizzle.","furigana":"つゆ|じき|ふ|あめ|ひ|おお"},{"japanese":"しとしと降る雨の音を聞きながら、本を読みました。","romaji":"Shitoshito furu ame no oto o kikinagara, hon o yomimashita.","german":"I read while listening to the sound of the soft rain.","furigana":"ふ|あめ|おと|き|ほん|よ"}],"notes":"しとしと describes quiet, even, gentle rain that lasts a long time, and feels rather calm and atmospheric: 雨がしとしと降る, しとしと降る雨. It is used almost only for rain. In contrast to it, ざあざあ stands for pouring downpours and ぱらぱら for scattered drops.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ざあざあ降っています。","romaji":"Zāzā futteimasu.","german":"It's pouring.","furigana":"ふ"},{"japanese":"ざあざあ降りの雨で出かけられません。","romaji":"Zāzā buri no ame de dekakeraremasen.","german":"I can't go out in this pouring rain.","furigana":"ぶ|あめ|で"},{"japanese":"水道の水をざあざあ出したままにしないでください。","romaji":"Suidou no mizu o zaazaa dashita mama ni shinaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't leave the tap water running nonstop.","furigana":"すいどう|みず|だ"}],"notes":"ざあざあ imitates the roar of heavy rain or a lot of flowing water: 雨がざあざあ降る, ざあざあ降り \"downpour\" (like 土砂降り). Also for running water: 水をざあざあ流す. Often also in katakana ザーザー. Contrasts with gentle しとしと.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日はぽかぽか陽気です。","romaji":"Kyō wa pokapoka yōki desu.","german":"It's pleasantly warm weather today.","furigana":"きょう|ようき"},{"japanese":"日向はぽかぽかして気持ちいいです。","romaji":"Hinata wa pokapoka shite kimochi ii desu.","german":"It's nice and warm in the sun.","furigana":"ひなた|きも"},{"japanese":"お風呂に入って、体がぽかぽかしています。","romaji":"Ofuro ni haitte, karada ga pokapoka shite imasu.","german":"After the bath, my body is nice and warm.","furigana":"ふろ|はい|からだ"}],"notes":"ぽかぽか describes soothing, pleasant warmth. It is used for the weather, a sunny spot, or a body that has warmed up; with する it forms a description of a state. It does not mean scorching hot. The other dictionary meaning, for repeated hitting, is not meant here.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"朝はひんやりしています。","romaji":"Asa wa hin'yari shite imasu.","german":"It is chilly in the morning.","furigana":"あさ"},{"japanese":"ひんやりした風が気持ちいいです。","romaji":"Hin'yari shita kaze ga kimochi ii desu.","german":"The cool breeze feels good.","furigana":"かぜ|きも"},{"japanese":"冷たいコップに触ると、手がひんやりします。","romaji":"Tsumetai koppu ni sawaru to, te ga hin'yari shimasu.","german":"When I touch the cold glass, my hands feel cool.","furigana":"つめ|さわ|て"}],"notes":"ひんやり describes a noticeable coolness, for example in the air or when touching an object. It can be used with する or modify a noun with した: ひんやりした風. The coolness can be pleasant; it does not automatically mean severe cold. The ん before や is separated with an apostrophe in Hepburn: hin'yari.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日はむしむしします。","romaji":"Kyō wa mushimushi shimasu.","german":"It is muggy today.","furigana":"きょう"},{"japanese":"梅雨の時期は毎日むしむしして、洗濯物が乾きません。","romaji":"Tsuyu no jiki wa mainichi mushimushi shite, sentakumono ga kawakimasen.","german":"During the rainy season it's muggy every day, and the laundry won't dry.","furigana":"つゆ|じき|まいにち|せんたくもの|かわ"},{"japanese":"部屋の中がむしむしするので、窓を開けましょう。","romaji":"Heya no naka ga mushimushi suru node, mado o akemashou.","german":"It's stuffy in the room, so let's open the window.","furigana":"へや|なか|まど|あ"}],"notes":"むしむし describes humid, oppressive hot air, typical of the rainy season and the Japanese midsummer. It is mostly used as a する-verb: 今日はむしむしする \"it's muggy today\"; also for stuffy rooms: 部屋がむしむしする. The adjective 蒸し暑い has the same meaning but is more neutral. As an onomatopoeic word it is written in kana, in advertising and comics often as ムシムシ.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"スマホを弄っています。","romaji":"Sumaho o ijitteimasu.","german":"I am fiddling with my smartphone.","furigana":"いじ"},{"japanese":"パソコンの設定を勝手に弄らないでください。","romaji":"Pasokon no settei o katte ni ijiranaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't change the computer settings without asking.","furigana":"せってい|かって|いじ"},{"japanese":"緊張すると、髪を弄る癖があります。","romaji":"Kinchou suru to, kami o ijiru kuse ga arimasu.","german":"When I'm nervous, I have a habit of playing with my hair.","furigana":"きんちょう|かみ|いじ|くせ"}],"notes":"いじる means \"to fiddle with, to play with something\" and takes を: スマホをいじる, 髪をいじる. It also means \"to tinker with, to alter something\", often without permission or expertise: 設定をいじる. Colloquially, it also means \"to tease someone\". Unlike 触る \"to touch\", いじる stresses repeated, often unnecessary handling. The kanji 弄 is rare; in daily life it is mostly written いじる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"りんごをかじります。","romaji":"Ringo o kajirimasu.","german":"Ich beiße in den Apfel."},{"japanese":"犬が靴をかじるので困っています。","romaji":"Inu ga kutsu o kajiru node komatte imasu.","german":"Der Hund kaut ständig an den Schuhen herum, das ist ein echtes Problem.","furigana":"いぬ|くつ|こま"},{"japanese":"大学でドイツ語を少しかじりました。","romaji":"Daigaku de doitsugo o sukoshi kajirimashita.","german":"An der Uni habe ich ein bisschen Deutsch gelernt.","furigana":"だいがく|ご|すこ"}],"notes":"かじる bedeutet „in etwas Hartes hineinbeißen, (an)nagen“ und nimmt を: りんごをかじる, ネズミが壁をかじる. Anders als 噛む „kauen, beißen“ geht es um das Abbeißen kleiner Stücke von außen. Übertragen heißt es „ein bisschen von etwas verstehen, hineinschnuppern“: ドイツ語を少しかじった. Die Redewendung 親のすねをかじる bedeutet „den Eltern auf der Tasche liegen“. Meist in Kana geschrieben.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"りんごの皮をむきます。","romaji":"Ringo no kawa o mukimasu.","german":"I peel the apple.","furigana":"かわ"},{"japanese":"みかんをむいて、子供にあげました。","romaji":"Mikan o muite, kodomo ni agemashita.","german":"I peeled a mandarin and gave it to the child.","furigana":"こども"},{"japanese":"ジャガイモの皮をむくのを手伝ってくれる？","romaji":"Jagaimo no kawa o muku no o tetsudatte kureru?","german":"Can you help me peel the potatoes?","furigana":"かわ|てつだ"}],"notes":"むく (kanji 剥く, mostly in kana) means \"to peel\" and takes を: 皮をむく, or the fruit directly as the object, りんごをむく, みかんをむく. Do not confuse it with the identically sounding 向く \"to turn toward, to face\". The intransitive counterpart is むける \"to peel\": 日焼けで皮がむけた \"after the sunburn, my skin peeled\". Peeling with a knife is 包丁でむく.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"おつまみをつまみながら話しましょう。","romaji":"Otsumami o tsumami nagara hanashimashou.","german":"Let's chat while nibbling on snacks.","furigana":"はな"},{"japanese":"臭いので、鼻をつまみました。","romaji":"Kusai node, hana o tsumamimashita.","german":"It stank, so I pinched my nose.","furigana":"くさ|はな"},{"japanese":"豆を箸でつまむのは難しいです。","romaji":"Mame o hashi de tsumamu no wa muzukashii desu.","german":"It is hard to pick up beans with chopsticks.","furigana":"まめ|はし|むずか"}],"notes":"つまむ means \"to pick up with the fingertips or chopsticks\": 鼻をつまむ \"to hold one's nose\", 箸で豆をつまむ. From this comes \"to nibble, to snack on the side\" - the word おつまみ for small snacks with alcohol derives from it. Unlike つかむ \"to grab with the whole hand\", you pick up with just two fingers. The kanji 摘む is also read つむ \"to pick (flowers)\", which is why it is mostly written in kana.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ラーメンを啜ります。","romaji":"Rāmen o susurimasu.","german":"I slurp ramen.","furigana":"すす"},{"japanese":"お味噌汁を啜りました。","romaji":"Omisoshiru o susurimashita.","german":"I slurped the miso soup.","furigana":"みそしる|すす"},{"japanese":"風邪で一日中鼻を啜っていました。","romaji":"Kaze de ichinichijuu hana o susutte imashita.","german":"I had a cold and was sniffling all day.","furigana":"かぜ|いちにちじゅうはな|すす"}],"notes":"すする means \"to slurp\" and refers mainly to noodles and soups: そばをすする, みそ汁をすする. Loud slurping of noodles is not considered impolite in Japan. With 鼻 it means \"to sniffle, to snuffle\": 鼻をすする. For normal drinking, one says 飲む. The kanji 啜 is difficult; すする is mostly written in kana.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"スプーンですくいます。","romaji":"Supūn de sukuimasu.","german":"I scoop it up with a spoon."},{"japanese":"スプーンでスープをすくいます。","romaji":"Supūn de sūpu o sukuimasu.","german":"I scoop the soup with a spoon."},{"japanese":"両手で川の水をすくいました。","romaji":"Ryōte de kawa no mizu o sukuimashita.","german":"I scooped up river water with both hands.","furigana":"りょうて|かわ|みず"}],"notes":"すくう here means to pick up a liquid or small objects with a spoon, a ladle, the hand or a similar tool. The tool takes で, and the thing picked up takes を. It is a transitive godan verb ending in う: すくいます・すくって・すくわない. 救う \"to rescue\" and 巣くう \"to nest\" are other words with the same reading.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"パンをちぎって食べます。","romaji":"Pan o chigitte tabemasu.","german":"I tear the bread into pieces and eat it.","furigana":"た"},{"japanese":"いらない紙を小さくちぎって捨てました。","romaji":"Iranai kami o chiisaku chigitte sutemashita.","german":"I tore the paper I no longer needed into small pieces and threw it away.","furigana":"かみ|ちい|す"}],"notes":"ちぎる means to tear something into pieces with the hands or to tear off a piece. The thing torn takes を; 小さく says that small pieces result. It is a transitive godan verb ending in る: ちぎります・ちぎって・ちぎらない. The identically read 契る for a vow or promise does not belong to this card.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"水をあげなかったので花が萎れました。","romaji":"Mizu o agenakatta node hana ga shioremashita.","german":"The flower wilted because I didn't water it.","furigana":"みず|はな|しお"},{"japanese":"このレタスは少し萎れていますね。","romaji":"Kono retasu wa sukoshi shiorete imasu ne.","german":"This lettuce is a bit wilted, isn't it?","furigana":"すこ|しお"},{"japanese":"先生に叱られて、弟はすっかり萎れていました。","romaji":"Sensei ni shikararete, otouto wa sukkari shiorete imashita.","german":"After the teacher scolded him, my little brother was completely downcast.","furigana":"せんせい|しか|おとうと|しお"}],"notes":"しおれる describes plants that go limp from lack of water or heat and let their heads hang: 花がしおれる, 野菜がしおれる. Unlike 枯れる \"to wither, to die\", a しおれた plant can recover with water. Figuratively it is also said of people who are dejected after a reprimand or disappointment. Intransitive, with no transitive counterpart; mostly written in kana.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子供たちがプールではしゃいでいます。","romaji":"Kodomotachi ga puuru de hashaide imasu.","german":"The children are romping in the pool.","furigana":"こども"},{"japanese":"久しぶりの旅行で、母も子供のようにはしゃいでいました。","romaji":"Hisashiburi no ryokou de, haha mo kodomo no you ni hashaide imashita.","german":"On the first trip in a long time, even my mother was as playful as a child.","furigana":"ひさ|りょこう|はは|こども"},{"japanese":"電車の中ではしゃぐのはやめなさい。","romaji":"Densha no naka de hashagu no wa yamenasai.","german":"Stop horsing around on the train!","furigana":"でんしゃ|なか"}],"notes":"はしゃぐ means \"to be boisterous with joy, to romp around\" and is intransitive: 子供がはしゃぐ. It describes cheerful, loud excitement, for example on vacation or at a party; in adults it sounds slightly childish, especially はしゃぎすぎ \"to be overexcited\". Unlike 騒ぐ \"to make noise, to raise a racket\", the emphasis is on joy, not on the disturbance. Written in kana.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"弟がお菓子をもらえなくて拗ねています。","romaji":"Otouto ga okashi o moraenakute sunete imasu.","german":"My little brother is sulking because he didn't get any sweets.","furigana":"おとうと|かし|す"},{"japanese":"彼女は誕生日を忘れられて、拗ねてしまいました。","romaji":"Kanojo wa tanjoubi o wasurerarete, sunete shimaimashita.","german":"She sulked because her birthday was forgotten.","furigana":"かのじょ|たんじょうび|わす|す"},{"japanese":"そんなに拗ねないで、一緒に遊ぼうよ。","romaji":"Sonna ni sunenaide, issho ni asobou yo.","german":"Don't sulk like that; come on, let's play together.","furigana":"す|いっしょ|あそ"}],"notes":"すねる means \"to sulk, to be in a huff\": you show your hurt by stubbornly staying silent or not joining in, often to get attention. Typical for children or in relationships: 無視されてすねる. Unlike 怒る \"to get angry\", すねる is passive and rather childish. Intransitive; the kanji 拗 is rare, and すねる is mostly written in kana.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"最近ゲームにはまっています。","romaji":"Saikin geemu ni hamatte imasu.","german":"Lately I have been hooked on games.","furigana":"さいきん"},{"japanese":"車のタイヤが穴にはまって、動けません。","romaji":"Kuruma no taiya ga ana ni hamatte, ugokemasen.","german":"The car's tire is stuck in a hole, and it can't move.","furigana":"くるま|あな|うご"},{"japanese":"このパズルのピースは、ここにはまるはずです。","romaji":"Kono pazuru no piisu wa, koko ni hamaru hazu desu.","german":"This puzzle piece should fit here."}],"notes":"はまる is intransitive and has several meanings: \"to fit exactly\" (指輪がはまらない), \"to fall into, to get stuck\" (穴にはまる, 渋滞にはまる) and, colloquially and very commonly, \"to be gripped by something, to be addicted to\": ゲームにはまっている. The target takes に. The transitive counterpart is はめる \"to put in, to put on\" (指輪をはめる). Mostly written in kana.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"壁にぶつかりました。","romaji":"Kabe ni butsukarimashita.","german":"I bumped into the wall.","furigana":"かべ"},{"japanese":"角で人にぶつかりました。","romaji":"Kado de hito ni butsukarimashita.","german":"I ran into someone at the corner.","furigana":"かど|ひと"},{"japanese":"仕事で難しい問題にぶつかることもあります。","romaji":"Shigoto de muzukashii mondai ni butsukaru koto mo arimasu.","german":"At work, you sometimes run into difficult problems.","furigana":"しごと|むずか|もんだい"}],"notes":"ぶつかる means \"to collide, to bump against something\" and is intransitive: 壁にぶつかる; for a mutual collision と is used: 車と車がぶつかる. The transitive counterpart is ぶつける \"to bump something\": 頭をぶつける. Figuratively it means \"to run into a problem\" (問題にぶつかる), \"to clash\" (意見がぶつかる) and \"to coincide in time\" (予定がぶつかる).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"メガネがずれました。","romaji":"Megane ga zuremashita.","german":"My glasses slipped."},{"japanese":"台風で、旅行の予定が一日ずれました。","romaji":"Taifuu de, ryokou no yotei ga ichinichi zuremashita.","german":"Because of the typhoon, our trip schedule shifted by one day.","furigana":"たいふう|りょこう|よてい|いちにち"},{"japanese":"話がずれるので、その話はまた今度にしましょう。","romaji":"Hanashi ga zureru node, sono hanashi wa mata kondo ni shimashou.","german":"This is going off topic, so let's talk about it another time.","furigana":"はなし|はなし|こんど"}],"notes":"ずれる means \"to slip, to shift, to deviate from the right position\" and is intransitive: メガネがずれる, 予定が一日ずれる. Figuratively: 話がずれる \"to get off the topic\", 感覚がずれている \"to have an odd sense, to be off the mark\". The transitive counterpart is ずらす \"to shift\" (日程をずらす). Written in kana.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"シャツが体にくっついています。","romaji":"Shatsu ga karada ni kuttsuiteimasu.","german":"My shirt is sticking to my body.","furigana":"からだ"},{"japanese":"ガムが靴にくっつきました。","romaji":"Gamu ga kutsu ni kuttsukimashita.","german":"Chewing gum stuck to my shoe.","furigana":"くつ"},{"japanese":"子供がずっとお母さんにくっついて離れません。","romaji":"Kodomo ga zutto okaasan ni kuttsuite hanaremasen.","german":"The child keeps clinging to his mother and won't leave her side.","furigana":"こども|かあ|はな"}],"notes":"くっつく means \"to stick, to adhere\" and is intransitive; what something sticks to takes に: ガムが靴にくっつく. It is also used for people who cling closely to each other (子供が母親にくっつく), and colloquially for couples who get together. The transitive counterpart is くっつける \"to stick on, to join together\". Compared with the neutral 付く, くっつく sounds more colloquial and stresses firm adhesion.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"弟をからかいます。","romaji":"Otōto o karakaimasu.","german":"I tease my younger brother.","furigana":"おとうと"},{"japanese":"友達をからかうのはやめなさい。","romaji":"Tomodachi o karakau no wa yamenasai.","german":"Stop teasing your friend.","furigana":"ともだち"},{"japanese":"背が低いことをからかわれて、悲しかったです。","romaji":"Se ga hikui koto o karakawarete, kanashikatta desu.","german":"I was sad because I was teased about being short.","furigana":"せ|ひく|かな"}],"notes":"からかう means \"to tease, to make fun of, to pull someone's leg\" and takes the person with を: 弟をからかう. Someone who is teased says からかわれる. It is usually meant playfully, but can hurt; unlike いじめる \"to bully, to torment\", it lacks the malicious intent. You can also からかう animals. Written in kana.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"空港で友達を出迎えました。","romaji":"Kuukou de tomodachi o demukaemashita.","german":"I met my friend at the airport.","furigana":"くうこう|ともだち|でむか"},{"japanese":"玄関で家族全員がお客さんを出迎えました。","romaji":"Genkan de kazoku zen'in ga okyakusan o demukaemashita.","german":"The whole family welcomed the guest at the front door.","furigana":"げんかん|かぞくぜんいん|きゃく|でむか"},{"japanese":"ホテルに着くと、スタッフが笑顔で出迎えてくれました。","romaji":"Hoteru ni tsuku to, sutaffu ga egao de demukaete kuremashita.","german":"When we arrived at the hotel, the staff welcomed us with a smile.","furigana":"つ|えがお|でむか"}],"notes":"出迎える means \"to meet or receive someone on their arrival\" - at the station, airport or door: 空港で友達を出迎える. In everyday speech people often say the simpler 迎えに行く \"to go and pick up\"; 出迎える stresses going out to meet and greet someone and sounds somewhat more formal. The noun is 出迎え, politely お出迎え. The opposite is 見送る \"to see off\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子供を甘やかしすぎです。","romaji":"Kodomo o amayakashisugi desu.","german":"You spoil your child too much.","furigana":"こども|あま"},{"japanese":"たまには自分を甘やかして、ケーキを食べます。","romaji":"Tama ni wa jibun o amayakashite, keeki o tabemasu.","german":"Once in a while I pamper myself and eat cake.","furigana":"じぶん|あま|た"},{"japanese":"犬を甘やかすと、言うことを聞かなくなりますよ。","romaji":"Inu o amayakasu to, iu koto o kikanaku narimasu yo.","german":"If you spoil the dog, it will stop listening to you.","furigana":"いぬ|あま|い|き"}],"notes":"甘やかす means \"to spoil, to be too indulgent\" and takes を: 子供を甘やかす, 孫を甘やかす. It usually has a reproachful tone. With 自分, it jokingly means \"to treat oneself\". Do not confuse it with the intransitive 甘える \"to let oneself be pampered, to be dependent\": parents 甘やかす the child, and the child 甘える toward the parents.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"それが彼の口癖です。","romaji":"Sore ga kare no kuchiguse desu.","german":"That is his pet phrase.","furigana":"かれ|くちぐせ"},{"japanese":"彼の口癖は「まあまあ」です。","romaji":"Kare no kuchiguse wa \"māmā\" desu.","german":"His pet phrase is \"so-so\".","furigana":"かれ|くちぐせ"},{"japanese":"「忙しい」と言う口癖を直したいです。","romaji":"“Isogashii” to iu kuchiguse o naoshitai desu.","german":"I want to break my habit of saying \"I'm busy\".","furigana":"いそが|い|くちぐせ|なお"}],"notes":"口癖 is an expression that someone says over and over out of habit. It need not be a consciously chosen favorite saying. 人の口癖 attributes this verbal habit to a person; 口癖を直す describes trying to break it. The card does not mean just any phrase someone uses only once.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"友達に仕事の愚痴を聞いてもらいました。","romaji":"Tomodachi ni shigoto no guchi o kiite moraimashita.","german":"A friend listened to my complaints about work.","furigana":"ともだち|しごと|ぐち|き"},{"japanese":"父はお酒を飲むと、いつも愚痴を言います。","romaji":"Chichi wa osake o nomu to, itsumo guchi o iimasu.","german":"When my father drinks, he always complains.","furigana":"ちち|さけ|の|ぐち|い"},{"japanese":"愚痴ばかり言っていないで、何かしたらどう？","romaji":"Guchi bakari itte inaide, nanika shitara dou?","german":"Instead of just complaining, why don't you do something?","furigana":"ぐち|い|なに"}],"notes":"愚痴 is complaining about things you can't change anyway - work, the boss, the weather. Typical: 愚痴を言う or 愚痴をこぼす \"to complain\", 愚痴を聞く \"to listen to someone's complaints\". Unlike 文句 \"complaint, objection\", which is directed at someone, 愚痴 serves more to let off steam. Colloquially there is also the verb 愚痴る.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"近所の人と世間話をしました。","romaji":"Kinjo no hito to sekenbanashi o shimashita.","german":"I chatted with a neighbor.","furigana":"きんじょ|ひと|せけんばなし"},{"japanese":"美容院で美容師さんと世間話をするのが苦手です。","romaji":"Biyouin de biyoushi-san to sekenbanashi o suru no ga nigate desu.","german":"I'm bad at making small talk with the hairdresser at the salon.","furigana":"びよういん|びようし|せけんばなし|にがて"},{"japanese":"社長は世間話のあとで、仕事の話を始めました。","romaji":"Shachou wa sekenbanashi no ato de, shigoto no hanashi o hajimemashita.","german":"After some small talk, the president began to discuss business.","furigana":"しゃちょう|せけんばなし|しごと|はなし|はじ"}],"notes":"世間話 is casual chatting about everyday matters such as the weather, the neighborhood, or news, i.e. small talk: 近所の人と世間話をする. The conversation partner takes と. Similar is 雑談 \"informal conversation\", which is also common among colleagues; 世間話 sounds a bit older and neighborly. For gossip about specific people one rather says うわさ話.","levelNote":"Compound: 世間 (N3) + 話 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"冗談を言って笑わせます。","romaji":"Jōdan o itte warawasemasu.","german":"I make jokes to make people laugh.","furigana":"じょうだん|い|わら"},{"japanese":"えっ、会社を辞める？冗談でしょう？","romaji":"E? Kaisha o yameru? Joudan deshou?","german":"What, you're quitting the company? You're joking, right?"},{"japanese":"今のは冗談だよ。怒らないで。","romaji":"Ima no wa joudan da yo. Okoranaide.","german":"That was just a joke. Don't be angry.","furigana":"いま|じょうだん|おこ"}],"notes":"冗談 is a joke or jest in conversation: 冗談を言う \"to joke\". Very common are 冗談でしょう \"You're joking, right?\" and 冗談だよ \"Just kidding\". 冗談じゃない, by contrast, means \"This is no joke / No way!\". For a told joke with a punchline one rather says ジョーク or 笑い話; 冗談 is mainly the spontaneous jest.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"言い訳をしないでください。","romaji":"Iiwake o shinaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't make excuses.","furigana":"い|わけ"},{"japanese":"遅刻の言い訳を考えながら、駅まで走りました。","romaji":"Chikoku no iiwake o kangaenagara, eki made hashirimashita.","german":"I ran to the station while thinking up an excuse for being late.","furigana":"ちこく|い|わけ|かんが|えき|はし"},{"japanese":"忙しかったことは、宿題をしなかった言い訳になりません。","romaji":"Isogashikatta koto wa, shukudai o shinakatta iiwake ni narimasen.","german":"Being busy is not an excuse for not doing your homework.","furigana":"いそが|しゅくだい|い|わけ"}],"notes":"言い訳 is an excuse or justification that explains a failing - usually with a negative undertone: 言い訳をする, 言い訳ばかりする. ～は言い訳にならない means \"... is no excuse\". Note: a request for forgiveness is not 言い訳 but 謝る or すみません. Neutral in the sense of \"explanation\" is 説明.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"独り言を言っています。","romaji":"Hitorigoto o itteimasu.","german":"He is talking to himself.","furigana":"ひと|ごと|い"},{"japanese":"独り言を言う癖があります。","romaji":"Hitorigoto o iu kuse ga arimasu.","german":"I have a habit of talking to myself.","furigana":"ひと|ごと|い|くせ"},{"japanese":"気にしないでください。ただの独り言です。","romaji":"Ki ni shinaide kudasai. Tada no hitorigoto desu.","german":"Don't mind me. I'm just talking to myself.","furigana":"き|ひと|ごと"}],"notes":"独り言 means \"talking to oneself, muttering to oneself\"; the verb for it is 言う: 独り言を言う. It is often associated with ぶつぶつ \"to mutter\". The phrase ただの独り言です \"I'm just talking to myself\" signals that no answer is expected. Besides 独り言, the spelling ひとり言 is also seen.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"寝坊して遅刻しました。","romaji":"Nebō shite chikoku shimashita.","german":"I overslept and was late.","furigana":"ねぼう|ちこく"},{"japanese":"明日は大事な試験なので、寝坊しないように目覚まし時計を二つかけます。","romaji":"Ashita wa daiji na shiken na node, nebou shinai you ni mezamashi dokei o futatsu kakemasu.","german":"I have an important exam tomorrow, so I'm setting two alarm clocks so I don't oversleep.","furigana":"あした|だいじ|しけん|ねぼう|めざ|どけい|ふた"},{"japanese":"うちの息子は寝坊で、毎朝起こすのが大変です。","romaji":"Uchi no musuko wa nebou de, maiasa okosu no ga taihen desu.","german":"My son is a late riser, and waking him up every morning is hard work.","furigana":"むすこ|ねぼう|まいあさお|たいへん"}],"notes":"寝坊 is oversleeping; as a する-verb it is intransitive: 寝坊して遅刻した. 朝寝坊 has the same meaning but is more explicit. The word also refers to a person who likes to sleep late (お寝坊さん \"sleepyhead\"). Not to be confused with 寝過ごす, which stresses sleeping through a particular moment, such as your train stop.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は寝言を言うことがあります。","romaji":"Kare wa negoto o iu koto ga arimasu.","german":"He sometimes talks in his sleep.","furigana":"かれ|ねごと|い"},{"japanese":"昨日の夜、寝言で私の名前を呼んでいたよ。","romaji":"Kinou no yoru, negoto de watashi no namae o yonde ita yo.","german":"Last night you called my name in your sleep.","furigana":"きのう|よる|ねごと|わたし|なまえ|よ"},{"japanese":"寝言は寝てから言ってよ。","romaji":"Negoto wa nete kara itte yo.","german":"Dream on!","furigana":"ねごと|ね|い"}],"notes":"寝言 is talking in one's sleep; the verb for it is 言う: 寝言を言う. With で it means \"(say) in one's sleep\": 寝言で名前を呼ぶ. Figuratively it means \"nonsense, babbling\"; the cheeky expression 寝言は寝て言え means roughly \"Dream on!\". Not to be confused with いびき \"snoring\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"父のいびきがうるさいです。","romaji":"Chichi no ibiki ga urusai desu.","german":"My father's snoring is loud.","furigana":"ちち"},{"japanese":"夫は毎晩大きないびきをかいて寝ます。","romaji":"Otto wa maiban ookina ibiki o kaite nemasu.","german":"My husband snores loudly every night.","furigana":"おっと|まいばんおお|ね"},{"japanese":"疲れているときは、いびきをかくそうです。","romaji":"Tsukarete iru toki wa, ibiki o kaku sou desu.","german":"I'm told I snore when I'm tired.","furigana":"つか"}],"notes":"いびき is snoring. The verb for it is not する but かく: いびきをかく \"to snore\". Common: いびきがうるさい \"the snoring is loud\", 大きないびき. The kanji 鼾 is rare; it is almost always written in kana. Quiet breathing in sleep, by contrast, is 寝息.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"授業中に居眠りしました。","romaji":"Jugyōchū ni inemuri shimashita.","german":"I dozed off during class.","furigana":"じゅぎょうちゅう|いねむ"},{"japanese":"電車で居眠りして、降りる駅を過ぎてしまいました。","romaji":"Densha de inemuri shite, oriru eki o sugite shimaimashita.","german":"I dozed off on the train and went past my stop.","furigana":"でんしゃ|いねむ|お|えき|す"},{"japanese":"居眠り運転は、とても危ないです。","romaji":"Inemuri unten wa, totemo abunai desu.","german":"Drowsy driving is very dangerous.","furigana":"いねむ|うんてん|あぶ"}],"notes":"居眠り is unintentionally nodding off while sitting, for example in class, on a train, or in a meeting: 授業中に居眠りする. Unlike 昼寝 \"nap\", which one takes deliberately, 居眠り happens unintentionally. Important compound: 居眠り運転 \"falling asleep at the wheel\". As a する-verb it is intransitive, also 居眠りをする.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今朝は寝癖がひどいです。","romaji":"Kesa wa neguse ga hidoi desu.","german":"Heute Morgen sind meine Haare total verwuschelt.","furigana":"けさ|ねぐせ"},{"japanese":"後ろに寝癖がついていますよ。","romaji":"Ushiro ni neguse ga tsuite imasu yo.","german":"Hinten stehen dir die Haare vom Schlafen ab.","furigana":"うし|ねぐせ"},{"japanese":"出かける前に、水で寝癖を直しました。","romaji":"Dekakeru mae ni, mizu de neguse o naoshimashita.","german":"Bevor ich losging, habe ich meine zerzausten Haare mit Wasser glatt gemacht.","furigana":"で|まえ|みず|ねぐせ|なお"}],"notes":"寝癖 sind die Haare, die nach dem Schlafen in alle Richtungen abstehen. Typisch: 寝癖がつく „die Haare stehen ab“, 寝癖を直す „die Haare richten“, 寝癖がひどい. Seltener bedeutet es „unruhiges Schlafen“; dafür sagt man aber meist 寝相. Ein deutsches Einzelwort fehlt – „vom Schlafen verwuschelte Haare“ trifft es am besten.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日焼けして赤くなりました。","romaji":"Hiyake shite akaku narimashita.","german":"I got sunburned and turned red.","furigana":"ひや|あか"},{"japanese":"海で日焼けしました。","romaji":"Umi de hiyake shimashita.","german":"I got a tan at the beach.","furigana":"うみ|ひや"},{"japanese":"日焼けしないように、日焼け止めを塗ります。","romaji":"Hiyake shinai you ni, hiyakedome o nurimasu.","german":"I put on sunscreen so I won't get sunburned.","furigana":"ひや|ひや|ど|ぬ"}],"notes":"日焼け covers both suntan and sunburn; as a する-verb it is intransitive: 海で日焼けした. Whether brown or red is meant comes from the context (日焼けで赤くなった). Sunscreen is 日焼け止め. Paper, books or tatami that fade in sunlight also 日焼けする. In Japan, great importance is placed on sun protection.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あくびが止まりません。","romaji":"Akubi ga tomarimasen.","german":"I can't stop yawning.","furigana":"と"},{"japanese":"眠くてあくびが出ます。","romaji":"Nemukute akubi ga demasu.","german":"I'm sleepy and keep yawning.","furigana":"ねむ|で"},{"japanese":"会議中にあくびをしてしまい、部長に見られました。","romaji":"Kaigichuu ni akubi o shite shimai, buchou ni miraremashita.","german":"I yawned during the meeting, and my department head saw me.","furigana":"かいぎちゅう|ぶちょう|み"}],"notes":"あくび is a yawn. Typical are あくびが出る \"to have to yawn\" and あくびをする \"to yawn\". A suppressed yawn is あくびをかみ殺す. The kanji 欠伸 is rare; it is almost always written in kana. Yawning in front of others, for example in meetings, is considered impolite, as it is for us; you put your hand in front of your mouth.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"くしゃみが出ます。","romaji":"Kushami ga demasu.","german":"I have to sneeze.","furigana":"で"},{"japanese":"花粉症でくしゃみが止まりません。","romaji":"Kafunshō de kushami ga tomarimasen.","german":"I can't stop sneezing because of hay fever.","furigana":"かふんしょう|と"},{"japanese":"くしゃみをするときは、ハンカチで口を押さえましょう。","romaji":"Kushami o suru toki wa, hankachi de kuchi o osaemashou.","german":"When you sneeze, cover your mouth with a handkerchief.","furigana":"くち|お"}],"notes":"くしゃみ is a sneeze. You say くしゃみが出る \"to have to sneeze\" or くしゃみをする \"to sneeze\". Common causes are 風邪 and 花粉症. According to Japanese folk belief, you sneeze when someone is talking about you. The word is written in kana; the kanji spelling 嚔 is not in use.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"しゃっくりが止まりません。","romaji":"Shakkuri ga tomarimasen.","german":"I can't stop hiccupping.","furigana":"と"},{"japanese":"水を飲んだらしゃっくりが止まりました。","romaji":"Mizu o nondara shakkuri ga tomarimashita.","german":"When I drank water, the hiccups stopped.","furigana":"みず|の|と"},{"japanese":"笑いすぎて、しゃっくりが出てしまいました。","romaji":"Waraisugite, shakkuri ga dete shimaimashita.","german":"I laughed so much that I got the hiccups.","furigana":"わら|で"}],"notes":"しゃっくり is the hiccups. Typical: しゃっくりが出る \"to get the hiccups\", しゃっくりが止まらない \"the hiccups won't stop\", しゃっくりを止める \"to get rid of the hiccups\". As a する-verb, it is less common. Home remedies such as drinking water or holding your breath are known in Japan just the same. Always written in kana.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"パソコンの使いすぎで肩こりがひどいです。","romaji":"Pasokon no tsukaisugi de katakori ga hidoi desu.","german":"Vom vielen Arbeiten am Computer ist meine Nackenverspannung schlimm.","furigana":"つか|かた"},{"japanese":"肩こりには、お風呂にゆっくり入るのがいいですよ。","romaji":"Katakori ni wa, ofuro ni yukkuri hairu no ga ii desu yo.","german":"Gegen verspannte Schultern hilft ein ausgiebiges Bad.","furigana":"かた|ふろ|はい"},{"japanese":"肩こりで頭まで痛くなりました。","romaji":"Katakori de atama made itaku narimashita.","german":"Von der Verspannung habe ich sogar Kopfschmerzen bekommen.","furigana":"かた|あたま|いた"}],"notes":"肩こり ist die Verspannung von Schultern und Nacken, ein sehr verbreitetes Alltagsleiden in Japan. Das Verb dazu lautet 肩がこる „verspannte Schultern haben“. Häufig: 肩こりがひどい, 肩こりに効く „gegen Verspannungen wirksam“. Die Schreibung 肩凝り gibt es auch, üblicher ist 肩こり. Im Deutschen spricht man meist vom „verspannten Nacken“.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"腰痛に悩んでいます。","romaji":"Yōtsū ni nayande imasu.","german":"I suffer from lower back pain.","furigana":"ようつう|なや"},{"japanese":"腰痛で立てません。","romaji":"Yōtsū de tatemasen.","german":"I can't stand up because of lower back pain.","furigana":"ようつう|た"}],"notes":"腰痛 means pain in the lower back, that is, in the lumbar region. It is narrower than general back pain. 腰痛に悩む describes a persistent burden; 腰痛で names the pain as the reason for a limitation. The card names the symptom and gives no diagnosis.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"運動した後、筋肉痛になりました。","romaji":"Undou shita ato, kinnikutsuu ni narimashita.","german":"I got sore muscles after exercising.","furigana":"うんどう|あと|きんにくつう"},{"japanese":"久しぶりに山に登ったので、足が筋肉痛です。","romaji":"Hisashiburi ni yama ni nobotta node, ashi ga kinnikutsuu desu.","german":"I climbed a mountain for the first time in a long while, so my legs are sore.","furigana":"ひさ|やま|のぼ|あし|きんにくつう"},{"japanese":"年を取ると、筋肉痛が二日後に来るそうです。","romaji":"Toshi o toru to, kinnikutsuu ga futsukago ni kuru sou desu.","german":"They say that as you get older, muscle soreness only comes two days later.","furigana":"とし|と|きんにくつう|ふつかご|く"}],"notes":"筋肉痛 is, in everyday use, the muscle soreness after sports or unaccustomed exertion. One says 筋肉痛になる, or with a body part 足が筋肉痛だ \"my legs are sore\". Medically it can also mean muscle pain in general. It is composed of 筋肉 \"muscle\" and 痛 \"pain\", like 頭痛 or 腹痛.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"足首を捻挫しました。","romaji":"Ashikubi o nenza shimashita.","german":"I sprained my ankle.","furigana":"あしくび|ねんざ"},{"japanese":"捻挫が治るまで、サッカーの練習を休みます。","romaji":"Nenza ga naoru made, sakkā no renshū o yasumimasu.","german":"I'll skip soccer practice until the sprain heals.","furigana":"ねんざ|なお|れんしゅう|やす"}],"notes":"捻挫 is a sprain. With する it can mean that one has sprained a body part; in the example this is marked with を: 足首を捻挫する. As a noun it can stand for the injury in 捻挫が治る. The card distinguishes a sprain linguistically from a bone fracture, 骨折.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"腕に打ち身があります。","romaji":"Ude ni uchimi ga arimasu.","german":"I have a bruise on my arm.","furigana":"うで|う|み"},{"japanese":"階段で転んで打ち身ができました。","romaji":"Kaidan de koronde uchimi ga dekimashita.","german":"I fell on the stairs and got a bruise.","furigana":"かいだん|ころ|う|み"},{"japanese":"打ち身には、冷たい湿布を貼るといいですよ。","romaji":"Uchimi ni wa, tsumetai shippu o haru to ii desu yo.","german":"For a bruise, it helps to put on a cooling patch.","furigana":"う|み|つめ|しっぷ|は"}],"notes":"打ち身 is a bruise from a blow or fall, without an open wound: 打ち身ができる. The word is composed of 打つ \"to hit\" and 身 \"body\". The medical technical term is 打撲, and the visible bruise mark itself is called あざ. For treatment one cools it and often sticks on a 湿布.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"漆にかぶれました。","romaji":"Urushi ni kaburemashita.","german":"I got a rash from lacquer.","furigana":"うるし"},{"japanese":"新しい化粧品でかぶれました。","romaji":"Atarashii keshōhin de kaburemashita.","german":"I got a rash from the new cosmetics.","furigana":"あたら|けしょうひん"},{"japanese":"かぶれがひどいので、皮膚科に行きました。","romaji":"Kabure ga hidoi node, hifuka ni ikimashita.","german":"The rash was so bad that I went to a dermatologist.","furigana":"ひふか|い"}],"notes":"かぶれ is a skin rash as a reaction to something that touched the skin: plants, cosmetics, metal or plasters. More common than the noun is the intransitive verb かぶれる; the cause is marked with に or で: 化粧品でかぶれる. A general rash or eczema is 湿疹. As a suffix, 〜かぶれ means, pejoratively, \"enthusiastically influenced by something\", e.g. 西洋かぶれ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"夏はあせもができます。","romaji":"Natsu wa asemo ga dekimasu.","german":"I get heat rash in summer.","furigana":"なつ"},{"japanese":"赤ちゃんにあせもができました。","romaji":"Akachan ni asemo ga dekimashita.","german":"The baby got heat rash.","furigana":"あか"},{"japanese":"あせもがかゆくて、夜眠れません。","romaji":"Asemo ga kayukute, yoru nemuremasen.","german":"The heat rash itches so much that I can't sleep at night.","furigana":"よるねむ"}],"notes":"あせも is heat rash, that is, itchy red bumps caused by heavy sweating, especially in babies in summer. Typical: あせもができる, あせもがかゆい. The word is related to 汗 \"sweat\"; the kanji spelling 汗疹 is rare. Unlike かぶれ, あせも is not caused by contact with a substance but by sweat.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"腰に湿布を貼りました。","romaji":"Koshi ni shippu o harimashita.","german":"I put a cooling patch on my lower back.","furigana":"こし|しっぷ|は"},{"japanese":"お風呂に入る前に、湿布をはがしてください。","romaji":"Ofuro ni hairu mae ni, shippu o hagashite kudasai.","german":"Please peel off the patch before you take a bath.","furigana":"ふろ|はい|まえ|しっぷ"},{"japanese":"肩こりがひどいので、薬局で湿布を買いました。","romaji":"Katakori ga hidoi node, yakkyoku de shippu o kaimashita.","german":"My shoulders are so stiff that I bought some pain-relief patches at the pharmacy.","furigana":"かた|やっきょく|しっぷ|か"}],"notes":"湿布 in Japan is above all the medicated pain-relief patch from the pharmacy, cooling (冷湿布) or warming (温湿布). You stick it on with 貼る and peel it off with はがす. Typical uses are 肩こり, back pain, and bruises. For small wounds, 絆創膏 is used instead.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"絆創膏を貼ってください。","romaji":"Bansōkō o hatte kudasai.","german":"Please put a bandage on it.","furigana":"ばんそうこう|は"},{"japanese":"指を切ったので絆創膏を貼りました。","romaji":"Yubi o kitta node bansōkō o harimashita.","german":"I cut my finger and put on a bandage.","furigana":"ゆび|き|ばんそうこう|は"},{"japanese":"絆創膏を持っていますか。","romaji":"Bansoukou o motte imasu ka.","german":"Do you have a bandage with you?","furigana":"ばんそうこう|も"}],"notes":"絆創膏 is the adhesive bandage for small cuts and wounds. You stick it on with 貼る and peel it off with はがす. Colloquially it is often called by brand names such as バンドエイド or, regionally, カットバン. For pain-relief patches against stiffness, 湿布 is used instead. The difficult kanji mainly need to be recognized when reading.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"腕にほくろがあります。","romaji":"Ude ni hokuro ga arimasu.","german":"I have a mole on my arm.","furigana":"うで"},{"japanese":"背中のほくろが大きくなったので、病院で見てもらいました。","romaji":"Senaka no hokuro ga ookiku natta node, byouin de mite moraimashita.","german":"The mole on my back got bigger, so I had it looked at in the hospital.","furigana":"せなか|おお|びょういん|み"},{"japanese":"口の横のほくろが彼女のチャームポイントです。","romaji":"Kuchi no yoko no hokuro ga kanojo no chaamu pointo desu.","german":"The mole next to her mouth is her charm point.","furigana":"くち|よこ|かのじょ"}],"notes":"ほくろ is a small dark spot on the skin, i.e. a mole or beauty mark: 顔にほくろがある. A ほくろ under the eye is called 泣きぼくろ and is considered attractive. The kanji 黒子 is also read くろこ, so ほくろ is mostly written in kana. Flat pigment spots from age or sun, by contrast, are called しみ, and freckles そばかす.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"そばかすがかわいいです。","romaji":"Sobakasu ga kawaii desu.","german":"The freckles are cute."},{"japanese":"夏になるとそばかすが出ます。","romaji":"Natsu ni naru to sobakasu ga demasu.","german":"I get freckles in summer.","furigana":"なつ|で"},{"japanese":"子供のころから、鼻のまわりにそばかすがあります。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro kara, hana no mawari ni sobakasu ga arimasu.","german":"I've had freckles around my nose since I was a child.","furigana":"こども|はな"}],"notes":"そばかす are freckles, small light-brown spots mainly on the face. One says そばかすがある or, from the sun, そばかすができる / そばかすが出る. Distinction: しみ are larger pigment spots, ほくろ individual dark moles. The word is written in kana; originally it referred to buckwheat chaff, which the spots resemble.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"笑うとえくぼができます。","romaji":"Warau to ekubo ga dekimasu.","german":"I get dimples when I laugh.","furigana":"わら"},{"japanese":"あの赤ちゃんのえくぼはとてもかわいいです。","romaji":"Ano akachan no ekubo wa totemo kawaii desu.","german":"That baby's dimples are very cute.","furigana":"あか"}],"notes":"えくぼ is a small dimple, especially on the cheek, that can become visible when smiling. えくぼができる describes its appearance; 人のえくぼ names a person's dimple. The word is often written in hiragana. It refers to a small indentation and not a wrinkle.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"手のひらに汗をかいています。","romaji":"Te no hira ni ase o kaite imasu.","german":"My palms are sweating.","furigana":"て|あせ"},{"japanese":"手のひらを上にして、手を前に出してください。","romaji":"Te no hira o ue ni shite, te o mae ni dashite kudasai.","german":"Please hold your hand out in front with the palm up.","furigana":"て|うえ|て|まえ|だ"},{"japanese":"雪が手のひらの上ですぐに溶けました。","romaji":"Yuki ga te no hira no ue de sugu ni tokemashita.","german":"The snow melted instantly on my palm.","furigana":"ゆき|て|うえ|と"}],"notes":"手のひら is the palm of the hand; the back is called 手の甲 \"back of the hand\". The spellings 手のひら, 手の平 and 掌 exist. Typical: 手のひらに汗をかく \"to get sweaty palms\", 手のひらを上にする \"to turn the palm up\". The idiom 手のひらを返す means \"to suddenly change one's attitude\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"つま先で立ちます。","romaji":"Tsumasaki de tachimasu.","german":"I stand on tiptoe.","furigana":"さき|た"},{"japanese":"この靴はつま先が痛いです。","romaji":"Kono kutsu wa tsumasaki ga itai desu.","german":"These shoes hurt my toes.","furigana":"くつ|さき|いた"},{"japanese":"寒くて、つま先が冷たくなりました。","romaji":"Samukute, tsumasaki ga tsumetaku narimashita.","german":"It was so cold that my toes got cold.","furigana":"さむ|さき|つめ"}],"notes":"つま先 means the tips of the toes, or the front part of the foot, also of a shoe or sock: 靴のつま先. Typical: つま先で立つ \"to stand on tiptoe\", つま先立ち. The individual toes are called 足の指, the heel かかと. The spelling 爪先 is also correct.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二の腕を鍛えています。","romaji":"Ninoude o kitaeteimasu.","german":"I'm training my upper arms.","furigana":"に|うで|きた"},{"japanese":"半袖を着ると、二の腕が気になります。","romaji":"Hansode o kiru to, ninoude ga ki ni narimasu.","german":"When I wear short sleeves, I'm self-conscious about my upper arms.","furigana":"はんそで|き|に|うで|き"},{"japanese":"蚊に二の腕を刺されました。","romaji":"Ka ni ninoude o sasaremashita.","german":"A mosquito bit me on the upper arm.","furigana":"か|に|うで|さ"}],"notes":"二の腕 is the upper arm between shoulder and elbow. In everyday life the word often comes up in fitness and beauty topics, especially with regard to the soft back side: 二の腕を鍛える, 二の腕が気になる. For \"arm\" in general one says 腕; medically the upper arm is called 上腕.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ふくらはぎが張っています。","romaji":"Fukurahagi ga hatteimasu.","german":"My calves are tight.","furigana":"は"},{"japanese":"走った後、ふくらはぎが痛いです。","romaji":"Hashitta ato, fukurahagi ga itai desu.","german":"My calves hurt after running.","furigana":"はし|あと|いた"},{"japanese":"夜中にふくらはぎがつって、目が覚めました。","romaji":"Yonaka ni fukurahagi ga tsutte, me ga samemashita.","german":"In the middle of the night I got a cramp in my calf and woke up.","furigana":"よなか|め|さ"}],"notes":"ふくらはぎ is the calf. Common expressions: ふくらはぎが張る \"the calves are hard, tense\", ふくらはぎがつる \"to get a calf cramp\". The shin at the front, by contrast, is called すね. The kanji 脹脛 are rare; the word is written in kana.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"脇の下に汗をかきました。","romaji":"Waki no shita ni ase o kakimashita.","german":"I sweated under my arms.","furigana":"わき|した|あせ"},{"japanese":"本を脇に抱えて、図書館に行きました。","romaji":"Hon o waki ni kakaete, toshokan ni ikimashita.","german":"With a book tucked under my arm, I went to the library.","furigana":"ほん|わき|かか|としょかん|い"},{"japanese":"道の脇に自転車を止めました。","romaji":"Michi no waki ni jitensha o tomemashita.","german":"I parked my bicycle at the side of the road.","furigana":"みち|わき|じてんしゃ|と"}],"notes":"脇 means, first, \"armpit\", usually as 脇の下 \"underarm\": 脇の下に汗をかく. Second, it means \"side, edge, next to\": 道の脇 \"roadside\", 本を脇に抱える \"to tuck a book under one's arm\", 脇に置く \"to set aside\". For \"next to\", 横 or そば is more common in everyday speech. It is also written わき.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"顎が痛いです。","romaji":"Ago ga itai desu.","german":"My jaw hurts.","furigana":"あご|いた"},{"japanese":"考えるとき、彼はいつも顎に手を当てます。","romaji":"Kangaeru toki, kare wa itsumo ago ni te o atemasu.","german":"When he thinks, he always puts his hand to his chin.","furigana":"かんが|かれ|あご|て|あ"}],"notes":"顎 can refer to the jaw and, on the face, especially the chin. The reading on this card is あご. 顎に手を当てる speaks of putting a hand to the chin; in the case of pain, 顎 can also mean the jaw area depending on the situation. The meaning for a barb on a fishhook is not practiced here.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"頬が赤くなりました。","romaji":"Hō ga akaku narimashita.","german":"My cheeks turned red.","furigana":"ほお|あか"},{"japanese":"赤ちゃんの頬がふっくらしています。","romaji":"Akachan no hō ga fukkura shite imasu.","german":"The baby's cheeks are plump.","furigana":"あか|ほお"},{"japanese":"涙が頬を流れました。","romaji":"Namida ga hoo o nagaremashita.","german":"Tears ran down her cheeks.","furigana":"なみだ|ほお|なが"}],"notes":"頬 is the cheek. It has two readings: ほお (standard) and ほほ (also widespread). Colloquially and in child language one says ほっぺ or ほっぺた. Typical: 頬が赤くなる, 涙が頬を流れる, 頬杖をつく \"to rest one's chin on one's hand\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"眠くて瞼が重いです。","romaji":"Nemukute mabuta ga omoi desu.","german":"I'm sleepy and my eyelids are heavy.","furigana":"ねむ|まぶた|おも"},{"japanese":"たくさん泣いたので、朝起きたら瞼が腫れていました。","romaji":"Takusan naita node, asa okitara mabuta ga harete imashita.","german":"I cried a lot, so my eyelids were swollen when I got up in the morning.","furigana":"な|あさお|まぶた|は"},{"japanese":"瞼にアイシャドウを塗ります。","romaji":"Mabuta ni aishadou o nurimasu.","german":"I apply eye shadow to my eyelids.","furigana":"まぶた|ぬ"}],"notes":"瞼 is the eyelid and is mostly written in kana, まぶた. Common: まぶたが重い \"to have heavy lids, to be sleepy\", まぶたが腫れる \"to have swollen lids\". A lid with a crease is 二重まぶた, one without is 一重まぶた - a frequent topic in beauty matters.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"眉間にしわを寄せます。","romaji":"Miken ni shiwa o yosemasu.","german":"I furrow my brow.","furigana":"みけん|よ"},{"japanese":"疲れたときは、眉間を指で押すと楽になります。","romaji":"Tsukareta toki wa, miken o yubi de osu to raku ni narimasu.","german":"When you are tired, pressing the spot between your eyebrows with a finger helps you feel better.","furigana":"つか|みけん|ゆび|お|らく"},{"japanese":"眉間のしわが気になります。","romaji":"Miken no shiwa ga ki ni narimasu.","german":"The wrinkle between my eyebrows bothers me.","furigana":"みけん|き"}],"notes":"眉間 is the spot between the eyebrows above the bridge of the nose. An almost fixed phrase: 眉間にしわを寄せる \"to knit one's brow, to frown\", as a sign of anger, worry or thought. Also 眉間のしわ \"frown line\". The forehead as a whole is 額 or おでこ, the eyebrow is 眉.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"うなじが日焼けしました。","romaji":"Unaji ga hiyake shimashita.","german":"The nape of my neck got sunburned.","furigana":"ひや"},{"japanese":"髪を上げると、うなじが見えます。","romaji":"Kami o ageru to, unaji ga miemasu.","german":"When you put your hair up, you can see the nape of your neck.","furigana":"かみ|あ|み"}],"notes":"うなじ refers to the nape, especially the back of the neck below the hairline. One can describe that it becomes visible when the hair is put up, or that its skin got sunburned. The word is often written in hiragana. The identically sounding older term for a sea route is a different word.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"くるぶしが靴に当たって痛いです。","romaji":"Kurubushi ga kutsu ni atatte itai desu.","german":"The shoe rubs against my ankle and it hurts.","furigana":"くつ|あ|いた"},{"japanese":"くるぶしをぶつけました。","romaji":"Kurubushi o butsukemashita.","german":"I bumped my ankle."},{"japanese":"くるぶしまでの短い靴下をはいています。","romaji":"Kurubushi made no mijikai kutsushita o haite imasu.","german":"I'm wearing short socks that only reach the ankle.","furigana":"みじか|くつした"}],"notes":"くるぶし is the ankle bone, i.e. the protruding bone on the inside and outside of the ankle joint. The joint itself is called 足首: that is why \"to twist one's ankle\" is 足首をひねる, not くるぶしをひねる. For socks and trousers, くるぶし serves as a length specification (くるぶし丈). The word is not used for finger knuckles.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"つむじが二つあります。","romaji":"Tsumuji ga futatsu arimasu.","german":"I have two hair whorls.","furigana":"ふた"},{"japanese":"子どものつむじは頭の右側にあります。","romaji":"Kodomo no tsumuji wa atama no migigawa ni arimasu.","german":"My child's hair whorl is on the right side of the head.","furigana":"こ|あたま|みぎがわ"}],"notes":"つむじ refers to the hair whorl, i.e. the spot where the hair grows in a circular pattern. Such whorls are counted with 一つ・二つ and their position is described with phrases such as 頭の右側など. It refers to the direction of hair growth, not a hairstyle and not a whirlwind.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"最近白髪が増えました。","romaji":"Saikin shiraga ga fuemashita.","german":"I have been getting more gray hairs lately.","furigana":"さいきんしらが|ふ"},{"japanese":"白髪を染めるかどうか迷っています。","romaji":"Shiraga o someru ka dou ka mayotte imasu.","german":"I can't decide whether to dye my gray hair.","furigana":"しらが|そ|まよ"},{"japanese":"父の頭に白髪を一本見つけました。","romaji":"Chichi no atama ni shiraga o ippon mitsukemashita.","german":"I found a gray hair on my father's head.","furigana":"ちち|あたま|しらが|いっぽんみ"}],"notes":"白髪 means gray or white hair. In everyday life it is read しらが; the reading はくはつ is literary (白髪の老人). Typical: 白髪が増える, 白髪が生える, 白髪を抜く, 白髪を染める. Hair dye for covering gray hair is called 白髪染め.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"前髪を切りました。","romaji":"Maegami o kirimashita.","german":"I got my bangs cut.","furigana":"まえがみ|き"},{"japanese":"前髪が伸びて、目に入ります。","romaji":"Maegami ga nobite, me ni hairimasu.","german":"My bangs have grown out and get in my eyes.","furigana":"まえがみ|の|め|はい"},{"japanese":"前髪を上げると、印象が変わりますね。","romaji":"Maegami o ageru to, inshou ga kawarimasu ne.","german":"If you push your bangs up, the impression changes, doesn't it?","furigana":"まえがみ|あ|いんしょう|か"}],"notes":"前髪 is the hair at the front that falls onto the forehead, the bangs (fringe). Typical: 前髪を切る, 前髪を伸ばす, 前髪を上げる \"to sweep back or put up one's bangs\". The counterpart is 後ろ髪 \"hair at the back\", known from 後ろ髪を引かれる \"to find it hard to part\". For men, it is more natural to translate it as \"the hair at the front\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"枝毛が気になります。","romaji":"Edage ga ki ni narimasu.","german":"My split ends bother me.","furigana":"えだげ|き"},{"japanese":"枝毛を切るために、美容院に行きました。","romaji":"Edage o kiru tame ni, biyōin ni ikimashita.","german":"I went to the hair salon to get my split ends cut.","furigana":"えだげ|き|びよういん|い"}],"notes":"枝毛 is split ends at the tips of the hair. 枝毛が気になる describes being bothered by split ends; 枝毛を切る means to cut off the split tips. The noun refers to the hair damage, while 美容院 names the hair salon.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"くせ毛で困っています。","romaji":"Kusege de komatteimasu.","german":"My curly hair is a problem for me.","furigana":"げ|こま"},{"japanese":"くせ毛で髪がまとまりません。","romaji":"Kusege de kami ga matomarimasen.","german":"Because of my curls, my hair won't stay in place.","furigana":"げ|かみ"},{"japanese":"姉はくせ毛ですが、私はストレートです。","romaji":"Ane wa kusege desu ga, watashi wa sutoreeto desu.","german":"My older sister has curly hair, but mine is straight.","furigana":"あね|げ|わたし"}],"notes":"くせ毛 (also 癖毛) refers to naturally wavy, curly or unruly hair; 癖 means \"peculiarity, habit\". It usually means hair that is hard to manage, hence often with 困る or まとまらない. Colloquially one also says 天然パーマ, or 天パ for short. Pretty, intended curls are rather 巻き毛, an artificial perm is パーマ; the opposite is straight hair (ストレート, 直毛).","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"親知らずを抜きました。","romaji":"Oyashirazu o nukimashita.","german":"I had a wisdom tooth pulled.","furigana":"おやし|ぬ"},{"japanese":"親知らずが生えてきて、少し痛いです。","romaji":"Oyashirazu ga haete kite, sukoshi itai desu.","german":"My wisdom tooth is coming in and it hurts a little.","furigana":"おやし|は|すこ|いた"}],"notes":"親知らず means wisdom tooth on this card. 親知らずを抜く describes having such a tooth pulled; 生えてくる its coming through. The expression is often used in conversations about dentist visits. The other dictionary meaning, for unknown biological parents, does not belong to these examples.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"部屋の真ん中にテーブルがあります。","romaji":"Heya no mannaka ni tēburu ga arimasu.","german":"There is a table in the middle of the room.","furigana":"へや|ま|なか"},{"japanese":"道の真ん中に立たないでください。","romaji":"Michi no mannaka ni tatanaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't stand in the middle of the road.","furigana":"みち|ま|なか|た"},{"japanese":"写真の真ん中にいるのが父です。","romaji":"Shashin no mannaka ni iru no ga chichi desu.","german":"The one in the middle of the photo is my father.","furigana":"しゃしん|ま|なか|ちち"}],"notes":"真ん中 is the exact middle of a space or a row: Xの真ん中に \"right in the middle of X\", 三人の真ん中. It is more everyday and concrete than 中心 (\"center\", also abstract: 話の中心) and 中央 (formal, e.g. on signs or in 中央線). Not to be confused with 中 (\"in, inside\"): 箱の中 is \"in the box\", 箱の真ん中 \"exactly in the middle of the box\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 30"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"だんだん売り上げが落ちてきた。","romaji":"Dandan uriage ga ochite kita.","german":"Sales have gradually declined.","furigana":"う|あ|お"},{"japanese":"先月より売り上げが伸びました。","romaji":"Sengetsu yori uriage ga nobimashita.","german":"Sales increased compared with last month.","furigana":"せんげつ|う|あ|の"},{"japanese":"店長は毎晩その日の売り上げを計算します。","romaji":"Tenchou wa maiban sono hi no uriage o keisan shimasu.","german":"The store manager calculates the day's sales every evening.","furigana":"てんちょう|まいばん|ひ|う|あ|けいさん"}],"notes":"売り上げ is sales revenue, i.e. the total amount from sales, not the profit (利益). Typical verbs: 売り上げが伸びる/上がる (\"to rise\"), 売り上げが落ちる/下がる (\"to fall\"), 売り上げを伸ばす (\"to increase\"). In business documents, the shortened 売上 or 売上高 is often written; 売り上げ is the usual spelling in running text.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日のラーメンは全部売り切れです。","romaji":"Kyō no rāmen wa zenbu urikire desu.","german":"Today's ramen is all sold out.","furigana":"きょう|ぜんぶう|き"},{"japanese":"チケットは売り切れです。","romaji":"Chiketto wa urikire desu.","german":"The tickets are sold out.","furigana":"う|き"},{"japanese":"自動販売機のボタンに「売り切れ」と出ています。","romaji":"Jidou hanbaiki no botan ni 'urikire' to dete imasu.","german":"\"Sold out\" is showing on the button of the vending machine.","furigana":"じどうはんばいき|う|き|で"}],"notes":"売り切れ is the noun for the intransitive verb 売り切れる (\"to be sold out\"): Xは売り切れです, 売り切れになる, attributively 売り切れの商品. On vending machines and signs, 売切 or 売り切れ is often written. The more formal 完売 is used more as a positive success report (チケットは完売しました). Grammatically, 売り切れ is a noun, not an adjective - \"to be sold out\" is formed with です.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ネットで買ったカメラの売り手は、とても親切な人でした。","romaji":"Netto de katta kamera no urite wa, totemo shinsetsu na hito deshita.","german":"The seller of the camera I bought online was a very kind person.","furigana":"か|う|て|しんせつ|ひと"},{"japanese":"売り手と買い手が交渉しています。","romaji":"Urite to kaite ga kōshō shite imasu.","german":"The seller and the buyer are negotiating.","furigana":"う|て|か|て|こうしょう"},{"japanese":"今は売り手市場なので、仕事が見つけやすいです。","romaji":"Ima wa urite shijou na node, shigoto ga mitsukeyasui desu.","german":"It's a seller's market right now, so it's easy to find a job.","furigana":"いま|う|てしじょう|しごと|み"}],"notes":"売り手 is the selling party in a transaction, the counterpart to 買い手 (\"buyer\"). It is not an occupational title: a shop assistant in a store is called 店員 or 販売員. It is common in 売り手市場 (\"seller's market\"; in the job market: many jobs, few applicants) and 買い手市場, as well as in online purchases and negotiations.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ウェディングパーティーは今夜です。","romaji":"Wedingu pātī wa kon'ya desu.","german":"Die Hochzeitsfeier ist heute Abend.","furigana":"こんや"},{"japanese":"今夜は星がきれいです。","romaji":"Kon'ya wa hoshi ga kirei desu.","german":"Heute Nacht sind die Sterne schön.","furigana":"こんや|ほし"}],"notes":"今夜 bezeichnet den Abend oder die Nacht des heutigen Tages. Als Zeitangabe steht es häufig ohne に am Satzanfang; in 今夜です wird es zum Prädikat. Ob man im Deutschen heute Abend oder heute Nacht sagt, ergibt sich aus der beschriebenen Situation. Das ん vor や wird in Hepburn durch ein Apostroph getrennt: kon'ya.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 32"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"東北で、ボランティアをしていました。","romaji":"Tōhoku de, borantia o shite imashita.","german":"I was doing volunteer work in Tohoku.","furigana":"とうほく"},{"japanese":"東北の冬は寒いです。","romaji":"Tōhoku no fuyu wa samui desu.","german":"Winters in Tohoku are cold.","furigana":"とうほく|ふゆ|さむ"}],"notes":"東北 here means the Tōhoku region in the north of Japan's main island, Honshū. 東北で marks the place of an activity, 東北の冬 the winter of that region. The same written form can also express the direction northeast; this card and its pronunciation evidence refer to the region name.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"私は日本へ行くと決心しました。","romaji":"Watashi wa Nihon e iku to kesshin shimashita.","german":"I decided to go to Japan.","furigana":"わたし|にほん|い|けっしん"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"彼は会社を辞める決心がついた。","romaji":"Kare wa kaisha o yameru kesshin ga tsuita.","german":"He made up his mind to quit the company.","furigana":"かれ|かいしゃ|や|けっしん"},{"japanese":"今年こそたばこをやめようと決心したが、三日で決心が揺らいだ。","romaji":"Kotoshi koso tabako o yameyō to kesshin shita ga, mikka de kesshin ga yuraida.","german":"I resolved to quit smoking this year for real, but after three days my resolve wavered.","furigana":"ことし|けっしん|みっか|けっしん|ゆ"}],"notes":"決心 is a firm decision after inner struggle. Typical patterns: 〜と決心する, 〜ことを決心する and 〜決心がつく (\"to be able to make up one's mind\"), negated 決心がつかない. Other collocations: 決心が固い, 決心が揺らぐ. 決める is neutral for any decision, while 決意 sounds more solemn and stronger and often appears in speeches or written language. For everyday trifles such as choosing a dish, 決心 does not fit.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"祖父は来月その施設に入所します。","romaji":"Sofu wa raigetsu sono shisetsu ni nyūsho shimasu.","german":"My grandfather will be admitted to that facility next month.","furigana":"そふ|らいげつ|しせつ|にゅうしょ"},{"japanese":"希望者は面談のあとで入所が決まる。","romaji":"Kibōsha wa mendan no ato de nyūsho ga kimaru.","german":"For applicants, admission is decided after the interview.","furigana":"きぼうしゃ|めんだん|にゅうしょ|き"},{"japanese":"彼は詐欺罪で刑務所に入所した。","romaji":"Kare wa sagizai de keimusho ni nyūsho shita.","german":"He was sent to prison for fraud.","furigana":"かれ|さぎざい|けいむしょ|にゅうしょ"}],"notes":"入所 means \"to enter or be admitted to an institution (所)\": a nursing home (施設に入所する), a daycare (保育所), a research institute (研究所) or an office such as a law firm or talent agency (事務所). It is intransitive: 〜に入所する. For a prison it means \"beginning a prison term\", and the opposite is then 出所 (\"release from prison\"); otherwise the opposite is 退所. Other institutions have their own words: 入学 (school), 入社 (company), 入院 (hospital).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"始めに、プレゼンテーションをしたいと思います。","romaji":"Hajime ni, purezentēshon o shitai to omoimasu.","german":"First of all, I would like to give a presentation.","furigana":"はじ|おも"},{"japanese":"始めに自己紹介をしましょう。","romaji":"Hajime ni jiko shoukai o shimashou.","german":"First, let's introduce ourselves.","furigana":"はじ|じこしょうかい"},{"japanese":"始めに玉ねぎを切ってください。","romaji":"Hajime ni tamanegi o kitte kudasai.","german":"First, please cut the onions.","furigana":"はじ|たま|き"}],"notes":"はじめに introduces the first step of a sequence - in speeches, presentations, instructions and recipes: \"first of all, to begin with\". For this temporal meaning, the spelling 初めに or はじめに is more usual than 始めに; as a heading (\"introduction, preface\"), it is almost always はじめに. 最初に and まず are more everyday. Note: はじめは means \"at first (but later different)\": はじめは難しかったが….","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このレタス、もしかして本物なの？","romaji":"Kono retasu, moshikashite honmono na no?","german":"Is this lettuce real, by any chance?","furigana":"ほんもの"},{"japanese":"これは本物のダイヤモンドです。","romaji":"Kore wa honmono no daiyamondo desu.","german":"This is a real diamond.","furigana":"ほんもの"},{"japanese":"本物の富士山を見て、感動しました。","romaji":"Honmono no Fujisan o mite, kandou shimashita.","german":"Seeing the real Mt. Fuji moved me deeply.","furigana":"ほんもの|ふじさん|み|かんどう"}],"notes":"本物 means \"the real thing, the original\" and is grammatically a noun: attributive 本物の (本物の金), predicative 本物です. The opposite is 偽物 (\"fake, imitation\"). For true statements one uses 本当 (本当の話), not 本物. Figuratively, 本物 praises real ability: 彼の実力は本物だ (\"His ability is the real deal\").","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 37"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この辺は金持ちの家が多い。","romaji":"Kono hen wa kanemochi no ie ga ooi.","german":"There are many rich people's houses in this area.","furigana":"へん|かねも|いえ|おお"},{"japanese":"あの人は金持ちで有名です。","romaji":"Ano hito wa kanemochi de yūmei desu.","german":"That person is famous for being rich.","furigana":"ひと|かねも|ゆうめい"},{"japanese":"お金持ちになったら、世界中を旅行したいです。","romaji":"Okanemochi ni nattara, sekaijuu o ryokou shitai desu.","german":"If I get rich, I want to travel all over the world.","furigana":"かねも|せかいじゅう|りょこう"}],"notes":"金持ち is a noun (\"rich person\"); \"X is rich\" is therefore Xは金持ちです. In conversation the more polite form お金持ち is usually heard; bare 金持ち can sound blunt or even disparaging. The opposite is 貧乏 (\"poor\"). More formal and written is 裕福 (\"wealthy\"). Phrases: 金持ちになる (\"to get rich\"), お金持ちの家.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"わたしの事で争うのはよして！","romaji":"Watashi no koto de arasou no wa yoshite!","german":"Please don't fight over me!","furigana":"こと|あらそ"},{"japanese":"二つのチームが優勝を争っています。","romaji":"Futatsu no chiimu ga yuushou o arasotte imasu.","german":"Two teams are competing for the championship.","furigana":"ふた|ゆうしょう|あらそ"},{"japanese":"今度の選挙では三人が争います。","romaji":"Kondo no senkyo de wa sannin ga arasoimasu.","german":"Three people are running against each other in the upcoming election.","furigana":"こんど|せんきょ|さんにん|あらそ"}],"notes":"争う is intransitive: to quarrel or compete with someone is 〜と争う, the occasion takes で (お金のことで争う), but the prize fought for takes を (優勝を争う, 一位を争う). It sounds more serious and more literary than けんかする, which is used for everyday squabbles among children or friends. Related: 競う (\"to vie\", fair, sporting), 戦う (\"to fight\"); noun: 争い.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コウイチとは共通点が全くないから、何を話せばいいか分からないよ。","romaji":"Kouichi to wa kyoutsuuten ga mattaku nai kara, nani o hanaseba ii ka wakaranai yo.","german":"I have nothing at all in common with Koichi, so I don't know what to talk about.","furigana":"きょうつうてん|まった|なに|はな|わ"},{"japanese":"二人には共通点が多いです。","romaji":"Futari ni wa kyōtsūten ga ōi desu.","german":"The two of them have a lot in common.","furigana":"ふたり|きょうつうてん|おお"},{"japanese":"日本語と韓国語には共通点がたくさんあります。","romaji":"Nihongo to Kankokugo ni wa kyoutsuuten ga takusan arimasu.","german":"Japanese and Korean have many things in common.","furigana":"にほんご|かんこくご|きょうつうてん"}],"notes":"共通点 is a commonality between people or things: XとYの共通点, (XとYには)共通点がある/ない/多い, 共通点を見つける. The opposite is 違い or, formally, 相違点 (\"difference\"). 共通 alone means \"common\" and often stands attributively: 共通の趣味 (\"shared hobby\"), 共通の友達.","levelNote":"Compound: 共通 (N3) + 点 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"チームメイトとの共同作業は楽しいです。","romaji":"Chiimumeito to no kyoudou sagyou wa tanoshii desu.","german":"Working together with teammates is fun.","furigana":"きょうどうさぎょう|たの"},{"japanese":"共同で研究を進めます。","romaji":"Kyōdō de kenkyū o susumemasu.","german":"We are carrying out the research jointly.","furigana":"きょうどう|けんきゅう|すす"},{"japanese":"このアパートはトイレが共同です。","romaji":"Kono apaato wa toire ga kyoudou desu.","german":"This apartment building has a shared toilet.","furigana":"きょうどう"}],"notes":"共同 means \"joint, communal\" and mostly stands as a first element or adverbially: 共同作業 (\"collaboration\"), 共同研究, 共同で (\"jointly\"). It also refers to shared use: トイレが共同です (\"shared toilet\"). The verb 共同する (\"to cooperate\") is rare; in everyday speech one says 協力する or 一緒にする. Not to be confused with the identically read 協同 (e.g. 協同組合, \"cooperative\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このリスト、共有してもらえますか？","romaji":"Kono risuto, kyōyū shite moraemasu ka?","german":"Könnten Sie diese Liste mit mir teilen?","furigana":"きょうゆう"},{"japanese":"ファイルを共有してください。","romaji":"Fairu o kyōyū shite kudasai.","german":"Bitte teilen Sie die Datei.","furigana":"きょうゆう"},{"japanese":"兄と一台の車を共有しています。","romaji":"Ani to ichidai no kuruma o kyouyuu shite imasu.","german":"Ich teile mir mit meinem großen Bruder ein Auto.","furigana":"あに|いちだい|くるま|きょうゆう"}],"notes":"共有する ist transitiv: Xを(Yと)共有する – etwas gemeinsam besitzen oder nutzen, Informationen oder Dateien teilen (情報を共有する, 画面を共有する). Im IT-Alltag sagt man auch シェアする. Achtung bei deutschem „teilen“: Einen Kuchen oder die Rechnung aufteilen heißt 分ける bzw. 割り勘にする, nicht 共有する. Das Nomen erscheint in 共有ファイル, 共有スペース.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このキーホルダーは、二つの学校の友好のシンボルです。","romaji":"Kono kiihorudaa wa, futatsu no gakkou no yuukou no shinboru desu.","german":"Dieser Schlüsselanhänger ist ein Symbol der Freundschaft zwischen den beiden Schulen.","furigana":"ふた|がっこう|ゆうこう"},{"japanese":"友好関係を大切にしています。","romaji":"Yūkō kankei o taisetsu ni shite imasu.","german":"Wir pflegen die freundschaftlichen Beziehungen.","furigana":"ゆうこうかんけい|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"私の町はドイツの小さな町と友好都市になっています。","romaji":"Watashi no machi wa Doitsu no chiisana machi to yuukou toshi ni natte imasu.","german":"Meine Stadt ist Partnerstadt einer kleinen Stadt in Deutschland.","furigana":"わたし|まち|ちい|まち|ゆうこうとし"}],"notes":"友好 ist die Freundschaft zwischen Gruppen, Städten oder Staaten und klingt förmlich: 友好関係 („freundschaftliche Beziehungen“), 友好都市 („Partnerstadt“), 友好を深める, 友好的な態度 („freundliche Haltung“). Für die persönliche Freundschaft zwischen zwei Menschen sagt man 友情 oder einfach 友達; 私たちの友好 wäre zwischen Freunden unpassend.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"早く大人になりたいな。","romaji":"Hayaku otona ni naritai na.","german":"I want to become an adult soon.","furigana":"はや|おとな"},{"japanese":"夢が現実になりました。","romaji":"Yume ga genjitsu ni narimashita.","german":"The dream became reality.","furigana":"ゆめ|げんじつ"},{"japanese":"このクラスは二十人の学生から成っています。","romaji":"Kono kurasu wa nijuunin no gakusei kara natte imasu.","german":"This class consists of twenty students.","furigana":"にじゅうにん|がくせい|な"}],"notes":"なる (\"to become\") is almost always written in kana in everyday use. Connection: nouns and な-adjectives take に (医者になる, 元気になる), い-adjectives take く (寒くなる). The counterpart is する: 〜にする (\"to make into, to decide on\") versus 〜になる (\"to become\"). The kanji spelling 成る is seen mainly in 〜から成る (\"to consist of\", literary).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今年は何位だろう？","romaji":"Kotoshi wa nan'i darō?","german":"I wonder what place I'll get this year.","furigana":"ことし|なんい"},{"japanese":"三位に入りました。","romaji":"San'i ni hairimashita.","german":"I placed third.","furigana":"さんい|はい"},{"japanese":"マラソンで五位になりました。","romaji":"Marason de goi ni narimashita.","german":"I came in fifth in the marathon.","furigana":"ごい"}],"notes":"〜位 is a counter suffix for ranks and placings: 一位, 二位, 三位 (さんい), 何位 (なんい). Typical: 〜位になる, 〜位に入る (\"to finish among the top ...\"), 〜位を取る. Not to be confused with くらい／ぐらい (\"approximately\", usually written in kana) and the noun 位 (くらい, \"rank, dignity\"). In competitions, 〜番 is also said colloquially.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一位になったら、一週間毎日カレーを作ってあげる。","romaji":"Ichii ni nattara, isshūkan mainichi karē o tsukutte ageru.","german":"If you take first place, I'll make curry for you every day for a week.","furigana":"いちい|いっしゅうかんまいにち|つく"},{"japanese":"テストで一位を取りました。","romaji":"Tesuto de ichii o torimashita.","german":"I got first place on the test.","furigana":"いちい|と"},{"japanese":"人気ランキングで一位のラーメン屋に行きました。","romaji":"Ninki rankingu de ichii no raamen'ya ni ikimashita.","german":"I went to the ramen shop that is number one in the popularity ranking.","furigana":"にんき|いちい|や|い"}],"notes":"一位 is first place in a ranking or competition: 一位になる, 一位を取る, ランキング一位. Colloquially one often says 一番; 首位 is more formal (news, league standings), 優勝 is the overall victory in a tournament. Grammatically 一位 is a noun. With a different meaning, いちい is also the Japanese yew (mostly in kana).","levelNote":"Compound: 位 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"学力テストで首位に立った。","romaji":"Gakuryoku tesuto de shui ni tatta.","german":"I took first place in the academic achievement test.","furigana":"がくりょく|しゅい|た"},{"japanese":"チームが首位に立っています。","romaji":"Chīmu ga shui ni tatte imasu.","german":"The team is in first place.","furigana":"しゅい|た"},{"japanese":"この本は三週間ずっと売り上げ首位です。","romaji":"Kono hon wa sanshuukan zutto uriage shui desu.","german":"This book has been at the top of the sales ranking for three weeks.","furigana":"ほん|さんしゅうかん|う|あ|しゅい"}],"notes":"首位 means \"top position, first place\" and sounds more formal than 一位 or トップ, typical of sports and business news. Fixed combinations: 首位に立つ (\"to move into first place\"), 首位を守る (\"to defend the lead\"), 首位争い (\"battle for the top of the table\"). In conversation, people usually say 一位 or トップ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"低い声の男の人が好きです。","romaji":"Hikui koe no otoko no hito ga suki desu.","german":"I like men with a deep voice.","furigana":"ひく|こえ|おとこ|ひと|す"},{"japanese":"今日は気温が低いです。","romaji":"Kyō wa kion ga hikui desu.","german":"The temperature is low today.","furigana":"きょう|きおん|ひく"}],"notes":"低い is an i-adjective: 低くない, 低かった. With 気温 it denotes a low temperature, with 声 a low pitch. A deep voice does not have to be quiet. Before a noun it stands directly: 低い声. 好き names a personal preference here.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 8"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あのドラマ、最低だった！","romaji":"Ano dorama, saitei datta!","german":"That drama was the worst!","furigana":"さいてい"},{"japanese":"明日の最低気温は三度だそうです。","romaji":"Ashita no saitei kion wa san do da sou desu.","german":"The lowest temperature tomorrow is supposed to be three degrees.","furigana":"あした|さいていきおん|さんど"},{"japanese":"最低でも一日七時間は寝たいです。","romaji":"Saitei demo ichinichi nanajikan wa netai desu.","german":"I want to sleep at least seven hours a day.","furigana":"さいてい|いちにちななじかん|ね"}],"notes":"最低 is the opposite of 最高 and has two faces: factual \"lowest, minimum\" (最低気温, 最低料金) and, as a judgment, \"the worst, lousy, unacceptable\", very harsh about people: あの人は最低だ. Adverbially, 最低(でも) means \"at least\". As a な-adjective: 最低な人; the noun 最低限 means \"the absolute minimum\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 17"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"初回の授業では自己紹介をします。","romaji":"Shokai no jugyou de wa jiko shoukai o shimasu.","german":"In the first class, we introduce ourselves.","furigana":"しょかい|じゅぎょう|じこしょうかい"},{"japanese":"初回は無料です。","romaji":"Shokai wa muryō desu.","german":"The first time is free.","furigana":"しょかい|むりょう"},{"japanese":"ドラマの初回を見ることができませんでした。","romaji":"Dorama no shokai o miru koto ga dekimasen deshita.","german":"I couldn't watch the first episode of the drama.","furigana":"しょかい|み"}],"notes":"初回 is the first occasion in a series of appointments, episodes or deliveries, typical of service and advertising language: 初回無料, 初回限定 (\"first edition only\"), 初回の授業, ドラマの初回 (\"first episode\"). For \"to do (something) for the first time\", the adverb 初めて is used instead: 初めて来ました, not 初回来ました. The counterpart is 最終回 (\"final episode\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"別のアプローチをしてみない？","romaji":"Betsu no apurōchi o shite minai?","german":"Shall we try a different approach?","furigana":"べつ"},{"japanese":"すみません、別の色はありますか。","romaji":"Sumimasen, betsu no iro wa arimasu ka.","german":"Excuse me, do you have this in a different color?","furigana":"べつ|いろ"},{"japanese":"今日は忙しいので、別の日にしませんか。","romaji":"Kyou wa isogashii node, betsu no hi ni shimasen ka.","german":"I'm busy today, so shall we make it another day?","furigana":"きょう|いそが|べつ|ひ"}],"notes":"別の stands before a noun and means \"another, a separate\": 別の日, 別の方法, 別の人. Unlike ほかの (\"other, remaining\", more additive: ほかの人も来ます), 別の stresses that it is something different and separate. 違う can also mean \"different\", but also \"wrong\" (違う人 = \"the wrong person\"). Related: 別に (adverb) and 別々.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 26"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ブロッコリーとシチューのタッパーは別にしてもらえますか？","romaji":"Burokkorī to shichū no tappā wa betsu ni shite moraemasu ka?","german":"Could you put the broccoli and the stew in separate containers?","furigana":"べつ"},{"japanese":"別に気にしていません。","romaji":"Betsu ni ki ni shite imasen.","german":"It doesn't particularly bother me.","furigana":"べつ|き"},{"japanese":"「どうしたの？」「別に。」","romaji":"'Dou shita no?' 'Betsu ni.'","german":"\"What's wrong?\" \"Nothing really.\"","furigana":"べつ"}],"notes":"別に has two uses. With a negative it means \"not particularly, not really\": 別に困っていない. Alone as an answer, 「別に。」 means \"nothing much, whatever\" and can come across as indifferent or curt. Without a negative it means \"separately, apart\": 別にする, 別に払う. For \"each separately\" with several parties, 別々に is more usual.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コウイチはお酒をやめてから、すっかり別人になったね。","romaji":"Kouichi wa osake o yamete kara, sukkari betsujin ni natta ne.","german":"Koichi has become a completely different person since he quit drinking.","furigana":"さけ|べつじん"},{"japanese":"髪を切ったら別人みたいです。","romaji":"Kami o kittara betsujin mitai desu.","german":"With my hair cut, he looks like a different person.","furigana":"かみ|き|べつじん"},{"japanese":"写真の人は兄ではなく、別人でした。","romaji":"Shashin no hito wa ani de wa naku, betsujin deshita.","german":"The person in the photo was not my older brother but someone else.","furigana":"しゃしん|ひと|あに|べつじん"}],"notes":"別人 (reading べつじん) is \"a different person, someone else\". Often figurative for a major change: 別人になる, まるで別人のようだ (\"like a different person\"). Literally it stands for a person other than the one assumed: 犯人は別人だった. Not to be confused with 他人 (\"stranger, unrelated person\"); mistaking one person for another is 人違い.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"友人とのスケジュールが合わないのでハワイまで別々に行きます。","romaji":"Yūjin to no sukejūru ga awanai node Hawai made betsubetsu ni ikimasu.","german":"My schedule doesn't match my friend's, so we are going to Hawaii separately.","furigana":"ゆうじん|あ|べつべつ|い"},{"japanese":"お会計は別々でお願いします。","romaji":"Okaikei wa betsubetsu de onegai shimasu.","german":"Separate checks, please.","furigana":"かいけい|べつべつ|ねが"},{"japanese":"白い服と黒い服は別々に洗います。","romaji":"Shiroi fuku to kuroi fuku wa betsubetsu ni araimasu.","german":"I wash white and black clothes separately.","furigana":"しろ|ふく|くろ|ふく|べつべつ|あら"}],"notes":"別々 means \"separately, each on its own\" and presupposes several people or things involved. Mostly adverbial: 別々に行く, 別々に払う; attributively 別々の部屋. In a restaurant: お会計は別々で(お願いします) - \"separate checks\". The opposite is 一緒に. Unlike 別に, 別々に stresses that each part is treated individually.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自転車のブレーキが利かない。","romaji":"Jitensha no burēki ga kikanai.","german":"The brakes on my bicycle don't work.","furigana":"じてんしゃ|き"},{"japanese":"鼻が利く人ですね。","romaji":"Hana ga kiku hito desu ne.","german":"You have a good nose.","furigana":"はな|き|ひと"},{"japanese":"弟とけんかして、三日も口を利いていません。","romaji":"Otouto to kenka shite, mikka mo kuchi o kiite imasen.","german":"I had a fight with my little brother and haven't spoken a word to him for three days.","furigana":"おとうと|みっか|くち|き"}],"notes":"利く means \"to function, to work (well)\" in the sense of an ability: ブレーキが利く, 鼻が利く (\"to have a keen nose\"), 気が利く (\"to be attentive\"), 口を利く (\"to talk with someone\"). To be distinguished from this is 効く for the effect of remedies: 薬が効く, エアコンが効く. Both sound the same and are often simply written in kana as きく; not to be confused with 聞く (\"to hear\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この仕事、労働時間がすごく長いんです。","romaji":"Kono shigoto, rōdō jikan ga sugoku nagai n desu.","german":"The working hours at this job are very long.","furigana":"しごと|ろうどうじかん|なが"},{"japanese":"労働条件を改善する必要があります。","romaji":"Rōdō jōken o kaizen suru hitsuyō ga arimasu.","german":"Working conditions need to be improved.","furigana":"ろうどうじょうけん|かいぜん|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"一日十二時間も労働するのは体によくないです。","romaji":"Ichinichi juuni jikan mo roudou suru no wa karada ni yoku nai desu.","german":"Working twelve hours a day is not good for the body.","furigana":"いちにちじゅうにじかん|ろうどう|からだ"}],"notes":"労働 is \"work\" as an economic and legal concept (gainful work, labor) and is common mainly in compounds: 労働時間, 労働条件, 労働者, 肉体労働 (\"manual labor\"). For everyday \"to work\" one says 働く, for \"work, job\" 仕事; 労働する sounds stiff and official.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コウイチ社長は、日本の労働者不足についてどうお考えなんですか？","romaji":"Kouichi shachou wa, Nihon no roudousha busoku ni tsuite dou okangae na n desu ka?","german":"Mr. Koichi, as a company president, what do you think about the labor shortage in Japan?","furigana":"しゃちょう|にほん|ろうどうしゃぶそく|かんが"},{"japanese":"労働者の権利を守ります。","romaji":"Rōdōsha no kenri o mamorimasu.","german":"We protect workers' rights.","furigana":"ろうどうしゃ|けんり|まも"},{"japanese":"この工場では二百人の労働者が働いています。","romaji":"Kono koujou de wa nihyakunin no roudousha ga hataraite imasu.","german":"Two hundred workers work in this factory.","furigana":"こうじょう|にひゃくにん|ろうどうしゃ|はたら"}],"notes":"労働者 is \"worker, employee\" as an economic and legal concept: 労働者の権利, 外国人労働者, 労働者不足 (ろうどうしゃぶそく, \"labor shortage\"). In everyday life people rather introduce themselves as 会社員 (\"company employee\") or say 働く人; 労働者 sounds like newspapers, politics or a trade union. Sense 2 refers specifically to people doing physical work (factory, construction site).","levelNote":"Compound: 労働 (N3) + 者 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの日、ここであなたと会えたのは運命ですよ。","romaji":"Ano hi, koko de anata to aeta no wa unmei desu yo.","german":"It was fate that I met you here on that day.","furigana":"ひ|あ|うんめい"},{"japanese":"運命を信じますか。","romaji":"Unmei o shinjimasu ka.","german":"Do you believe in fate?","furigana":"うんめい|しん"},{"japanese":"その事故が彼の運命を変えました。","romaji":"Sono jiko ga kare no unmei o kaemashita.","german":"That accident changed his destiny.","furigana":"じこ|かれ|うんめい|か"}],"notes":"運命 is fate, felt to be predetermined: 運命を信じる, 運命を変える, 運命の人 (\"the one, the right person\"), 運命的な出会い (\"a fateful encounter\"). It should be distinguished from 運 (\"luck, chance\"): 運がいい means \"to be lucky\", not \"to have a good fate\". 宿命 sounds even more inescapable and is literary.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"夜はオフィスをバーとして開放しています。","romaji":"Yoru wa ofisu o baa toshite kaihou shite imasu.","german":"In the evening, we open our office as a bar.","furigana":"よる|かいほう"},{"japanese":"窓を開放して空気を入れましょう。","romaji":"Mado o kaihō shite kūki o iremashō.","german":"Let's open the windows and let in some air.","furigana":"まど|かいほう|くうき|い"},{"japanese":"夏休みの間、学校のプールを子どもたちに開放します。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi no aida, gakkou no puuru o kodomotachi ni kaihou shimasu.","german":"During summer vacation, the school pool is open to the children.","furigana":"なつやす|あいだ|がっこう|こ|かいほう"}],"notes":"開放する is transitive: Xを開放する - to leave something open or make it accessible to others (校庭を市民に開放する, 窓を開放する). For the everyday opening of a door or window, 開ける is enough; 開放 stresses keeping something open or releasing it for use. Beware of the identically read 解放する (\"to free\", e.g. hostages or stress). Also: 開放的 (\"open, open-minded\").","levelNote":"Derived from 開放 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私はラーメンを注文しました。","romaji":"Watashi wa rāmen o chūmon shimashita.","german":"I ordered ramen.","furigana":"わたし|ちゅうもん"},{"japanese":"この店ではスマホで注文できます。","romaji":"Kono mise de wa sumaho de chūmon dekimasu.","german":"At this shop you can order with a smartphone.","furigana":"みせ|ちゅうもん"},{"japanese":"あのお客さんはいつも注文が多いです。","romaji":"Ano okyakusan wa itsumo chuumon ga ooi desu.","german":"That customer always has lots of special requests.","furigana":"きゃく|ちゅうもん|おお"}],"notes":"注文する is transitive: Xを注文する (\"to order X\") at a restaurant, in a shop or online (ネットで注文する). Waiters ask ご注文は？ or ご注文はお決まりですか. When eating, one colloquially also says 頼む; a reservation, by contrast, is 予約. Sense 2: 注文 as \"request, demand\", e.g. 注文が多い (\"to be demanding, picky\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"みんなが集中すればこの仕事は五時までに終わるはずだよ。","romaji":"Minna ga shuuchuu sureba kono shigoto wa goji made ni owaru hazu da yo.","german":"If everyone concentrates, this work should be finished by five o'clock.","furigana":"しゅうちゅう|しごと|ごじ|お"},{"japanese":"勉強に集中できません。","romaji":"Benkyō ni shūchū dekimasen.","german":"I can't concentrate on studying.","furigana":"べんきょう|しゅうちゅう"},{"japanese":"人口が東京に集中しています。","romaji":"Jinkou ga Toukyou ni shuuchuu shite imasu.","german":"The population is concentrated in Tokyo.","furigana":"じんこう|とうきょう|しゅうちゅう"}],"notes":"集中する means \"to concentrate\" and is mostly intransitive with に: 勉強に集中する. Adverbially: 集中して勉強する (\"to study with concentration\"). Transitive use is possible when bringing something together: 気持ちを集中する. It also means \"to be concentrated, to gather\": 人口が都市に集中する. Related: 集中力 (\"ability to concentrate\").","levelNote":"Derived from 集中 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ミーティングの場所は、お客さまが指定しました。","romaji":"Miitingu no basho wa, okyakusama ga shitei shimashita.","german":"The customer specified the place for the meeting.","furigana":"ばしょ|きゃく|してい"},{"japanese":"日時を指定してください。","romaji":"Nichiji o shitei shite kudasai.","german":"Please specify the date and time.","furigana":"にちじ|してい"},{"japanese":"新幹線の指定席を予約しました。","romaji":"Shinkansen no shiteiseki o yoyaku shimashita.","german":"I reserved a reserved seat on the Shinkansen.","furigana":"しんかんせん|していせき|よやく"}],"notes":"指定する means \"to specify, to state, to designate\" - usually the place, time or kind of something: 日時を指定する, 指定された場所. It sounds matter-of-fact and is often encountered in forms, online orders (配達日時指定) and instructions. Known from 指定席 (\"reserved seat\") as opposed to 自由席. General \"to decide\" is 決める.","levelNote":"Derived from 指定 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"洋服を買う前にサイズをチェックするのは大切です。","romaji":"Yōfuku o kau mae ni saizu o chekku suru no wa taisetsu desu.","german":"It's important to check the size before buying clothes.","furigana":"ようふく|か|まえ|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"新しい洋服を買いました。","romaji":"Atarashii yōfuku o kaimashita.","german":"I bought new clothes.","furigana":"あたら|ようふく|か"}],"notes":"洋服 denotes Western-style clothing, as opposed to 和服 such as a kimono. With を you name the clothing bought. 買う前に means \"before buying\"; the dictionary form 買う precedes it. サイズをチェックする checks the size with a single を. ようふく has a long o.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 39"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この家には小さな洋室が三つあります。","romaji":"Kono ie ni wa chiisana yōshitsu ga mittsu arimasu.","german":"This house has three small Western-style rooms.","furigana":"いえ|ちい|ようしつ|みっ"},{"japanese":"このホテルには洋室と和室があります。","romaji":"Kono hoteru ni wa yōshitsu to washitsu ga arimasu.","german":"This hotel has Western-style and Japanese-style rooms.","furigana":"ようしつ|わしつ"},{"japanese":"私の部屋は洋室で、ベッドと机があります。","romaji":"Watashi no heya wa youshitsu de, beddo to tsukue ga arimasu.","german":"My room is Western-style, with a bed and a desk.","furigana":"わたし|へや|ようしつ|つくえ"}],"notes":"洋室 is a Western-style room with wooden or carpeted floor, a bed, and doors, in contrast to the 和室 with tatami, futon, and sliding doors. The word is ubiquitous in apartment listings (洋室6畳) and hotel bookings. 洋 stands for \"Western\" as in 洋服 and 洋食.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コウイチにラインしたら秒で返事きたw","romaji":"Kōichi ni rain shitara byō de henji kita.","german":"I sent Koichi a LINE message and got a reply in seconds, lol."},{"japanese":"あと三十秒待ってください。","romaji":"Ato sanjū-byō matte kudasai.","german":"Please wait another thirty seconds.","furigana":"さんじゅうびょうま"}],"notes":"秒 is the unit of time \"second\" and follows a number directly in quantities: 三十秒, thirty seconds. It is a noun with a counting function. 秒で is also used in casual messages as an exaggeration for \"instantly\". The final w in the first example is a written laughter mark; it is not part of the spoken pronunciation of the sentence.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 46"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"来週、初級から中級にレベルが上がります。","romaji":"Raishū, shokyū kara chūkyū ni reberu ga agarimasu.","german":"Next week I move up from the beginner to the intermediate level.","furigana":"らいしゅう|しょきゅう|ちゅうきゅう|あ"},{"japanese":"初級のクラスに入りました。","romaji":"Shokyū no kurasu ni hairimashita.","german":"I joined a beginner class.","furigana":"しょきゅう|はい"},{"japanese":"この本は初級の学習者向けです。","romaji":"Kono hon wa shokyuu no gakushuusha muke desu.","german":"This book is intended for beginner-level learners.","furigana":"ほん|しょきゅう|がくしゅうしゃむ"}],"notes":"初級 is the beginner level in the series 初級・中級・上級 - in language courses, textbooks, exams or sports. It is used as a noun with の or as the first element: 初級クラス, 初級の文法. A person at that level is a 初級者; 初心者 is more general, \"newcomer\" (also in driving or hobbies). Grammatically, 初級 is a noun, not an adjective.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"中級とは、どういったレベルですか？","romaji":"Chūkyū to wa, dō itta reberu desu ka?","german":"What kind of level is intermediate?","furigana":"ちゅうきゅう"},{"japanese":"中級のレベルに上がりました。","romaji":"Chūkyū no reberu ni agarimashita.","german":"I moved up to the intermediate level.","furigana":"ちゅうきゅう|あ"},{"japanese":"中級のスキーコースはちょっと難しかったです。","romaji":"Chuukyuu no sukii koosu wa chotto muzukashikatta desu.","german":"The intermediate ski course was a little difficult.","furigana":"ちゅうきゅう|むずか"}],"notes":"中級 is the middle level between 初級 and 上級, in language courses, exams, ski slopes or cooking classes. It is used as a noun with の or as the first element of a compound: 中級クラス, 中級者 (\"intermediate-level learner\"). Grammatically it is a noun. Not to be confused with 中学 (\"middle school\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どうしても上級クラスについていけません。","romaji":"Doushitemo joukyuu kurasu ni tsuite ikemasen.","german":"I just can't keep up with the advanced class.","furigana":"じょうきゅう"},{"japanese":"上級の試験を受けます。","romaji":"Jōkyū no shiken o ukemasu.","german":"I am taking the advanced exam.","furigana":"じょうきゅう|しけん|う"},{"japanese":"姉はテニスの上級者です。","romaji":"Ane wa tenisu no joukyuusha desu.","german":"My older sister is an advanced tennis player.","furigana":"あね|じょうきゅうしゃ"}],"notes":"上級 is the highest level in 初級・中級・上級: 上級クラス, 上級の試験, 上級者 (\"advanced learner, expert\"). Outside courses, it also means \"higher-ranking\": 上級生 (\"student in a higher grade\") as opposed to 下級生. For people who are very good at something, 上手な人 or ベテラン is also used.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"警察が犯人を追っています。","romaji":"Keisatsu ga hannin o otte imasu.","german":"The police are chasing the culprit.","furigana":"けいさつ|はんにん|お"},{"japanese":"夢を追い続けます。","romaji":"Yume o oitsuzukemasu.","german":"I will keep pursuing my dream.","furigana":"ゆめ|お|つづ"},{"japanese":"毎日仕事に追われています。","romaji":"Mainichi shigoto ni owarete imasu.","german":"I am hounded by work every day.","furigana":"まいにちしごと|お"}],"notes":"追う is transitive: Xを追う, to go after someone or pursue something (犯人を追う), also figuratively: 夢を追う, 流行を追う. For physically running after, 追いかける is more everyday. In the passive, 〜に追われる means \"to be driven by something\": 仕事に追われる. Related: 追いつく (\"to catch up\"), 追い越す (\"to overtake\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"父は、いつも母との戦いに負ける。","romaji":"Chichi wa, itsumo haha to no tatakai ni makeru.","german":"Dad always loses his battles with Mom.","furigana":"ちち|はは|たたか|ま"},{"japanese":"長い戦いが終わりました。","romaji":"Nagai tatakai ga owarimashita.","german":"The long battle is over.","furigana":"なが|たたか|お"},{"japanese":"病気との戦いは一年続きました。","romaji":"Byouki to no tatakai wa ichinen tsuzukimashita.","german":"The fight against the illness lasted a year.","furigana":"びょうき|たたか|いちねんつづ"}],"notes":"戦い is the noun of 戦う: battle and war, but also competition and figurative struggle (時間との戦い). Typical: 〜との戦い, 戦いに勝つ/負ける. For the fight against illness or poverty it is also written 闘い. A war as a whole is 戦争, an everyday quarrel けんか.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"明日のゲームのための作戦を立てました。","romaji":"Ashita no gēmu no tame no sakusen o tatemashita.","german":"I made a plan for tomorrow's game.","furigana":"あした|さくせん|た"},{"japanese":"母の誕生日のサプライズ作戦は大成功でした。","romaji":"Haha no tanjoubi no sapuraizu sakusen wa daiseikou deshita.","german":"Our surprise plan for Mom's birthday was a great success.","furigana":"はは|たんじょうび|さくせん|だいせいこう"},{"japanese":"夏までのダイエット作戦を始めました。","romaji":"Natsu made no daietto sakusen o hajimemashita.","german":"I started a diet plan for losing weight by summer.","furigana":"なつ|さくせん|はじ"}],"notes":"作戦 is a tactic or a concrete plan for reaching a goal: 作戦を立てる (\"to work out a tactic\"), 作戦を変える, 作戦が成功する. Originally military (\"operation\"), in everyday life it is often used jokingly: ダイエット作戦, サプライズ作戦. A long-term overall strategy is more often 戦略, and a general plan is 計画.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"なんでこんな所を戦車が走ってるんだろう？","romaji":"Nande konna tokoro o sensha ga hashitteru n darou?","german":"Why is a tank driving around in a place like this?","furigana":"ところ|せんしゃ|はし"},{"japanese":"博物館で戦車を見ました。","romaji":"Hakubutsukan de sensha o mimashita.","german":"I saw a tank at the museum.","furigana":"はくぶつかん|せんしゃ|み"},{"japanese":"弟は戦車のプラモデルを作るのが好きです。","romaji":"Otouto wa sensha no puramoderu o tsukuru no ga suki desu.","german":"My younger brother likes building tank models.","furigana":"おとうと|せんしゃ|つく|す"}],"notes":"戦車 is a tank (tracked military vehicle); historically also the war chariot. It is counted with 台 or 両. One says 戦車に乗る, 戦車が走る. The word is composed of 戦 (\"battle\") and 車 (\"vehicle\"); the English \"tank\" as タンク, by contrast, usually means a container in Japanese.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"すみません。今、戦場にいるので電話に出られないんです。","romaji":"Sumimasen. Ima, senjou ni iru node denwa ni derarenai n desu.","german":"Sorry. I'm on the battlefield right now, so I can't answer the phone.","furigana":"いま|せんじょう|でんわ|で"},{"japanese":"戦場は危険な場所です。","romaji":"Senjō wa kiken na basho desu.","german":"A battlefield is a dangerous place.","furigana":"せんじょう|きけん|ばしょ"},{"japanese":"お昼の時間、この店のキッチンはまるで戦場です。","romaji":"Ohiru no jikan, kono mise no kitchin wa marude senjou desu.","german":"At lunchtime, this restaurant's kitchen is a real battlefield.","furigana":"ひる|じかん|みせ|せんじょう"}],"notes":"戦場 is the battlefield. In everyday speech it is often used figuratively for a hectic place: 昼の台所はまるで戦場だ (\"the kitchen at noon is a real battlefield\"). Typical combinations: 戦場に行く, 戦場で戦う. Not to be confused with 会場 (\"venue\") or 工場 (\"factory\"), which can also end in 〜じょう.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あなたとチェスで競うつもりはないです。","romaji":"Anata to chesu de kisou tsumori wa nai desu.","german":"I have no intention of competing with you at chess.","furigana":"きそ"},{"japanese":"世界中の選手と競います。","romaji":"Sekaijū no senshu to kisoimasu.","german":"I compete against athletes from all over the world.","furigana":"せかいじゅう|せんしゅ|きそ"},{"japanese":"二人はクラスで成績を競っています。","romaji":"Futari wa kurasu de seiseki o kisotte imasu.","german":"The two are competing for the best grades in the class.","furigana":"ふたり|せいせき|きそ"}],"notes":"競う is intransitive and means \"to compete, to vie\": 〜と競う (with someone), 〜で競う (in a discipline), 〜を競う (for something: 速さを競う). It sounds rather literary and has a sporting, fair-play connotation. In conversation 競争する is more common; 争う, by contrast, stresses quarrel or fierce rivalry.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"やばい。便所サンダルで学校に来ちゃった！","romaji":"Yabai. Benjo sandaru de gakkou ni kichatta!","german":"Oh no. I came to school wearing toilet slippers!","furigana":"べんじょ|がっこう|き"},{"japanese":"祖父はトイレのことを今でも「便所」と言います。","romaji":"Sofu wa toire no koto o ima demo 'benjo' to iimasu.","german":"My grandfather still calls the toilet 「便所」.","furigana":"そふ|いま|べんじょ|い"},{"japanese":"昔の家では、便所は外にありました。","romaji":"Mukashi no ie de wa, benjo wa soto ni arimashita.","german":"In old houses, the toilet was outside.","furigana":"むかし|いえ|べんじょ|そと"}],"notes":"便所 is an old-fashioned, crude word for toilet - today mostly heard from older men or in fixed expressions such as 便所サンダル (\"plastic slippers\"). The neutral word is トイレ, the polite one is お手洗い, and the very formal one is 化粧室. In polite speech (あちらです), 便所 therefore does not fit.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あそこのスーパー、今週はトマトの安売りセールをやってるよ。","romaji":"Asoko no suupaa, konshuu wa tomato no yasuuri seeru o yatteru yo.","german":"That supermarket over there is having a tomato sale this week.","furigana":"こんしゅう|やすう"},{"japanese":"スーパーで安売りをしています。","romaji":"Sūpā de yasuuri o shite imasu.","german":"The supermarket is having a sale.","furigana":"やすう"},{"japanese":"自分を安売りしないでください。","romaji":"Jibun o yasuuri shinaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't sell yourself short.","furigana":"じぶん|やすう"}],"notes":"安売り is selling at low prices: 安売りをする, 安売りの店, 大安売り (\"big clearance sale\"). As a する-verb it is transitive: 野菜を安売りする. Similar are 特売 (supermarket special offer) and セール. Figuratively, 自分を安売りする means \"to sell oneself short\", and 〜を安売りしない means \"not to treat something lightly\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ムカつくから、なんか仕返ししたいな。","romaji":"Mukatsuku kara, nanka shikaeshi shitai na.","german":"It's annoying; I want to get back at them somehow.","furigana":"しかえ"},{"japanese":"仕返しはよくないです。","romaji":"Shikaeshi wa yoku nai desu.","german":"Revenge is not good.","furigana":"しかえ"},{"japanese":"弟はおもちゃを取られた仕返しに、兄のお菓子を食べてしまった。","romaji":"Otōto wa omocha o torareta shikaeshi ni, ani no okashi o tabete shimatta.","german":"In retaliation for having his toy taken, the younger brother ate his older brother's sweets.","furigana":"おとうと|と|しかえ|あに|かし|た"}],"notes":"仕返し is retaliation or revenge for wrongs suffered; as a する-verb, the person takes に: 相手に仕返しする. Common are 〜の仕返しに (\"as revenge for ...\") and 仕返しを恐れる. 仕返し sounds everyday and can also be childishly harmless, whereas 復讐 is serious, often bloody revenge and more literary; colloquially there is also やり返す. The rare meaning \"to do something over again\" hardly occurs in modern Japanese.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"メールが返ってくるまで会社にいます。","romaji":"Mēru ga kaette kuru made kaisha ni imasu.","german":"I will stay at the office until an email comes back.","furigana":"かえ|かいしゃ"},{"japanese":"元の状態に返りました。","romaji":"Moto no jōtai ni kaerimashita.","german":"It has returned to its original state.","furigana":"もと|じょうたい|かえ"},{"japanese":"歩いているうちに、ズボンの裾が反った。","romaji":"Aruite iru uchi ni, zubon no suso ga kaetta.","german":"While I was walking, the hem of my trousers folded back.","furigana":"ある|すそ|かえ"}],"notes":"返る／かえる describes here a return or the return to a former state. For turning over or folding back, the spelling 反る with this reading is also attested, for instance for a folded-back hem. This is an intransitive process: 裾が反る. The reading そる of 反る, by contrast, refers to bending or curving and is learned separately. かえる is a godan verb: かえります, かえって, かえった. The transitive 返す means to give something back or to turn it over.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ゴンドラ乗り場はどこですか？","romaji":"Gondora noriba wa doko desu ka?","german":"Where is the gondola station?","furigana":"の|ば"},{"japanese":"タクシーの乗り場はどこですか。","romaji":"Takushī no noriba wa doko desu ka.","german":"Where is the taxi stand?","furigana":"の|ば"},{"japanese":"空港行きのバスは三番乗り場から出ます。","romaji":"Kuukou iki no basu wa sanban noriba kara demasu.","german":"The bus to the airport leaves from stop number 3.","furigana":"くうこうい|さんばんの|ば|で"}],"notes":"乗り場 is the place where you board a means of transport: バス乗り場, タクシー乗り場, 〜番乗り場 (\"stop/platform no. ...\"), also for ferries and cable cars. A bus stop along the route is more often バス停 or 停留所; the platform in a train station is ホーム. The counterpart is 降り場 (\"drop-off point\").","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 10"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私はあなたのことを負け犬だとは思わないよ。","romaji":"Watashi wa anata no koto o makeinu da to wa omowanai yo.","german":"I don't think you are a loser.","furigana":"わたし|ま|いぬ|おも"},{"japanese":"負け犬にはなりたくないです。","romaji":"Makeinu ni wa naritaku nai desu.","german":"I don't want to be a loser.","furigana":"ま|いぬ"},{"japanese":"試合の後で文句を言っても、負け犬の遠吠えだよ。","romaji":"Shiai no ato de monku o itte mo, makeinu no tooboe da yo.","german":"Complaining after the game is just the howling of a loser.","furigana":"しあい|あと|もんく|い|ま|いぬ|"}],"notes":"負け犬 (\"beaten dog\") is a derogatory word for a loser or failure. Well known is the saying 負け犬の遠吠え, the \"howling of a beaten dog\", i.e. complaining after a defeat. In the 2000s it was also used as slang for older unmarried women without children (now felt to be insulting). Neutral is 負けた人, formal 敗者.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日のゲームは日本チームの勝ちでした。","romaji":"Kyō no gēmu wa Nippon chīmu no kachi deshita.","german":"Today's game was won by the Japanese team.","furigana":"きょう|にっぽん|か"},{"japanese":"じゃんけんで私の勝ち！","romaji":"Janken de watashi no kachi!","german":"I win at rock-paper-scissors!","furigana":"わたし|か"},{"japanese":"先に着いた人の勝ちです。","romaji":"Saki ni tsuita hito no kachi desu.","german":"Whoever arrives first wins.","furigana":"さき|つ|ひと|か"}],"notes":"勝ち is the noun of 勝つ and appears mainly in the form Xの勝ち (\"X has won\"): 日本チームの勝ち, 私の勝ち！ The opposite is 負け; 勝ち負け means \"victory or defeat\". 勝利 is more formal (news, speeches). Expressions like 早い者勝ち (\"first come, first served\") show the pattern \"... wins\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ビエト、今日はちょっと不良っぽいヘアスタイルだね。","romaji":"Bieto, kyō wa chotto furyō ppoi heasutairu da ne.","german":"Viet, your hairstyle looks a bit delinquent today.","furigana":"きょう|ふりょう"},{"japanese":"製品に不良がありました。","romaji":"Seihin ni furyō ga arimashita.","german":"The product had a defect.","furigana":"せいひん|ふりょう"},{"japanese":"体調不良で会社を休みました。","romaji":"Taichou furyou de kaisha o yasumimashita.","german":"I stayed home from work because I wasn't feeling well.","furigana":"たいちょうふりょう|かいしゃ|やす"}],"notes":"不良 has two meanings. First, \"defective, faulty, bad\" - almost only in compounds: 不良品 (\"defective goods\"), 体調不良 (\"feeling unwell\"), 消化不良. Second, \"hoodlum, delinquent\": 不良少年, 不良っぽい (\"delinquent-looking\"). For general \"bad\", you say 悪い; 不良 does not fit weather or food.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このブランケット、気持ちいい。","romaji":"Kono buranketto, kimochi ii.","german":"This blanket feels nice.","furigana":"きも"},{"japanese":"マッサージが気持ちいいです。","romaji":"Massaaji ga kimochi ii desu.","german":"The massage feels good.","furigana":"きも"},{"japanese":"今日は風が気持ちいいですね。","romaji":"Kyou wa kaze ga kimochi ii desu ne.","german":"The breeze is pleasant today, isn't it?","furigana":"きょう|かぜ|きも"}],"notes":"気持ちいい describes a pleasant physical feeling or a refreshing mood: 風が気持ちいい, お風呂が気持ちいい. It conjugates like いい: 気持ちよかった, 気持ちよくない. Before nouns people often say 気持ちのいい朝. The opposite 気持ち悪い means \"disgusting\" or \"I feel sick\". For enjoyment of activities, 楽しい is used instead.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このサプリ、試して。","romaji":"Kono sapuri, tameshite.","german":"Try this supplement.","furigana":"ため"},{"japanese":"新しいレシピを試してみます。","romaji":"Atarashii reshipi o tameshite mimasu.","german":"I'll try a new recipe.","furigana":"あたら|ため"},{"japanese":"自分の力を試したくて、試験を受けました。","romaji":"Jibun no chikara o tameshitakute, shiken o ukemashita.","german":"I wanted to test my abilities, so I took the exam.","furigana":"じぶん|ちから|ため|しけん|う"}],"notes":"試す is transitive: Xを試す, to try something out or put it to the test (新しい方法を試す, 自分の力を試す). For \"to try doing something\" one mostly uses the form 〜てみる; the two are often combined: 試してみる. Trying on clothes is 試着する. Also: 試しに (\"on a trial basis\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"入学試験まで、あと二ヶ月です。","romaji":"Nyūgaku shiken made, ato nikagetsu desu.","german":"There are only two months left until the entrance exam.","furigana":"にゅうがくしけん|にかげつ"},{"japanese":"入学試験に向けて勉強しています。","romaji":"Nyūgaku shiken ni mukete benkyō shite imasu.","german":"I'm studying for the entrance exam.","furigana":"にゅうがくしけん|む|べんきょう"},{"japanese":"兄は大学の入学試験に合格しました。","romaji":"Ani wa daigaku no nyuugaku shiken ni goukaku shimashita.","german":"My older brother passed the university entrance exam.","furigana":"あに|だいがく|にゅうがくしけん|ごうかく"}],"notes":"入学試験 is the entrance exam of a school or university, often abbreviated 入試 (大学入試). Typical verbs: 入学試験を受ける (\"to take\"), 入学試験に合格する (\"to pass\"), 入学試験に落ちる (\"to fail\"). For companies it is 入社試験; a final exam is 卒業試験.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 31"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"正解です！","romaji":"Seikai desu!","german":"That's correct!","furigana":"せいかい"},{"japanese":"正解は三番です。","romaji":"Seikai wa sanban desu.","german":"The correct answer is number three.","furigana":"せいかい|さんばん"},{"japanese":"傘を持ってきて正解でした。","romaji":"Kasa o motte kite seikai deshita.","german":"It was the right call to bring an umbrella.","furigana":"かさ|も|せいかい"}],"notes":"正解 is a noun: \"the correct answer, the right solution\". Typical are 正解は〜です, the quiz exclamation 正解！ and the opposite 不正解 \"wrong\". As a する-verb, 問題に正解する means \"to answer a question correctly\". Colloquially, 〜て正解だった means \"it was the right decision to ...\". Do not confuse it with the adjective 正しい \"right, correct\", which stands before nouns (正しい答え).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このスプーンはケース付きですよ。","romaji":"Kono supūn wa kēsu-tsuki desu yo.","german":"This spoon comes with a case.","furigana":"つ"},{"japanese":"朝食付きのホテルを予約しました。","romaji":"Chōshoku-tsuki no hoteru o yoyaku shimashita.","german":"I booked a hotel with breakfast.","furigana":"ちょうしょくつ|よやく"}],"notes":"〜付き here means \"equipped with ...\" or \"including ...\". The component in question stands directly before it: ケース付き, 朝食付き. When the whole combination describes another noun, の follows, as in 朝食付きのホテル. The sign 〜 is an unspoken placeholder. This card practices this attached component, not the identically written verb form of 付く or ツキ in the sense of \"luck\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"年末ジャンボ宝くじはまだ発売中ですか？","romaji":"Nenmatsu Janbo takarakuji wa mada hatsubaichū desu ka?","german":"Is the year-end Jumbo lottery still on sale?","furigana":"ねんまつ|たから|はつばいちゅう"},{"japanese":"新しいゲームが発売中です。","romaji":"Atarashii gēmu ga hatsubaichū desu.","german":"A new game is on sale.","furigana":"あたら|はつばいちゅう"},{"japanese":"この本は全国の本屋で発売中です。","romaji":"Kono hon wa zenkoku no hon'ya de hatsubaichuu desu.","german":"This book is on sale at bookstores nationwide.","furigana":"ほん|ぜんこく|ほんや|はつばいちゅう"}],"notes":"発売中 is composed of 発売 \"market launch, start of sales\" and the suffix 〜中 \"in progress\", and means \"(newly) on the market, available now\". It appears mainly on posters, in advertising and in announcements, often as the set phrase 好評発売中 \"now available - in great demand\". Before a noun it is 発売中の: 発売中の雑誌. Caution: 発売中 does not mean \"on special offer, discounted\"; for that one says セール中 or 安売り.","levelNote":"Compound: 発売 (N3) + 中 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"安田さん、食べ物を持って来てください。","romaji":"Yasuda-san, tabemono o motte kite kudasai.","german":"Yasuda-san, please bring some food.","furigana":"やすだ|た|もの|も|き"},{"japanese":"日本の食べ物は美味しいです。","romaji":"Nihon no tabemono wa oishii desu.","german":"Japanese food is delicious.","furigana":"にほん|た|もの|おい"}],"notes":"食べ物 denotes food or something edible. 食べ物を持って来る means to bring food; 持って来てください asks for it. 日本の食べ物 uses の to link the food and its origin. Unlike 食事, 食べ物 denotes the edible thing, not the act or occasion of a meal.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 8"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大きすぎず、小さすぎず、本当に丁度いいサイズです。","romaji":"Ōkisugizu, chiisasugizu, hontō ni chōdo ii saizu desu.","german":"It is neither too big nor too small; it's really just the right size.","furigana":"おお|ちい|ほんとう|ちょうど"},{"japanese":"お風呂のお湯、ちょうどいいよ。","romaji":"Ofuro no oyu, chōdo ii yo.","german":"The bath water is just right.","furigana":"ふろ|ゆ"},{"japanese":"ちょうどよかった。今、電話しようと思っていたんです。","romaji":"Chōdo yokatta. Ima, denwa shiyou to omotte ita n desu.","german":"Perfect timing. I was just thinking of calling you now.","furigana":"いま|でんわ|おも"}],"notes":"ちょうどいい means \"just right, fitting\" for size, temperature, amount or timing. It is mostly written in kana; the kanji spelling 丁度いい is less common. It conjugates like いい via よい: ちょうどよかった, ちょうどよくない. ちょうどよかった is also a common reaction, \"Perfect timing!\", when someone shows up at just the right moment. Similar is ぴったり \"a perfect fit\", which stresses the exact fit.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 28"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"アメリカに行ったら高い日本語力を保持できるか不安です。","romaji":"Amerika ni ittara takai nihongoryoku o hoji dekiru ka fuan desu.","german":"I'm worried whether I can maintain a high level of Japanese if I go to America.","furigana":"い|たか|にほんごりょく|ほじ|ふあん"},{"japanese":"健康を保持するために運動します。","romaji":"Kenkō o hoji suru tame ni undō shimasu.","german":"I exercise in order to maintain my health.","furigana":"けんこう|ほじ|うんどう"},{"japanese":"その選手は今も百メートル走の日本記録を保持している。","romaji":"Sono senshu wa ima mo hyaku-mētoru-sō no Nihon kiroku o hoji shite iru.","german":"That athlete still holds the Japanese record in the 100-meter dash.","furigana":"せんしゅ|いま|ひゃく|そう|にほんきろく|ほじ"}],"notes":"保持する means \"to keep something, to preserve (in a certain state)\" and is a transitive, rather literary する-verb: 記録を保持する, 能力を保持する, 健康を保持する. In conversation 保つ is more natural; for maintaining a level or state one also says 維持する (日本語力を維持する). Common compounds: 記録保持者 (\"record holder\"), 秘密保持 (\"confidentiality\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"えっ、東京から九州まで毎日通ってるの？","romaji":"E, Tōkyō kara Kyūshū made mainichi kayotteru no?","german":"What, you commute from Tokyo to Kyushu every day?"},{"japanese":"毎日電車で会社に通っています。","romaji":"Mainichi densha de kaisha ni kayotte imasu.","german":"I commute to work by train every day.","furigana":"まいにちでんしゃ|かいしゃ|かよ"},{"japanese":"子どものころ、ピアノ教室に通っていました。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, piano kyōshitsu ni kayotte imashita.","german":"When I was a child, I went to piano lessons regularly.","furigana":"こ|きょうしつ|かよ"}],"notes":"通う is intransitive and means \"to go somewhere regularly\", to school, to work, to the hospital, to the gym. The destination is marked with に: 学校に通う, ジムに通う; the means of transport with で. Unlike 行く, 通う stresses repeated, habitual back-and-forth travel, which is why it is mostly used in the ている form. Related nouns are 通勤 \"commuting to work\" and 通学 \"commuting to school\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"中学の時は、バレーボール部でした。","romaji":"Chūgaku no toki wa, barēbōrubu deshita.","german":"In middle school, I was in the volleyball club.","furigana":"ちゅうがく|とき|ぶ"},{"japanese":"営業部で働いています。","romaji":"Eigyōbu de hataraite imasu.","german":"I work in the sales department.","furigana":"えいぎょうぶ|はたら"},{"japanese":"この資料を三部コピーしてください。","romaji":"Kono shiryō o sanbu kopii shite kudasai.","german":"Please make three copies of these materials.","furigana":"しりょう|さんぶ"}],"notes":"〜部 is a suffix after nouns. In companies, it denotes a department (営業部 \"sales\", 人事部 \"personnel department\"); 部 is bigger than 課. In schools and universities, it is the club: 野球部, テニス部, and the activity is called 部活. After numbers, 部 counts copies of documents or newspapers: 一部, 三部. As a noun on its own, 部 also means \"part\" (第一部 \"Part 1\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日、屋上でバーベキューをします。","romaji":"Kyō, okujō de bābekyū o shimasu.","german":"We're having a barbecue on the rooftop today.","furigana":"きょう|おくじょう"},{"japanese":"ビルの屋上からの景色がきれいです。","romaji":"Biru no okujō kara no keshiki ga kirei desu.","german":"The view from the rooftop of the building is beautiful.","furigana":"おくじょう|けしき"},{"japanese":"デパートの屋上に小さい遊園地がありました。","romaji":"Depāto no okujō ni chiisai yūenchi ga arimashita.","german":"There was a small amusement park on the roof of the department store.","furigana":"おくじょう|ちい|ゆうえんち"}],"notes":"屋上 is the walkable flat roof of a building, for example at schools, department stores or office buildings. One says 屋上で (place of action), 屋上に上がる \"to go up on the roof\" and 屋上から \"from the roof\". The sloped tile roof or house roof, by contrast, is 屋根; you normally don't stand on a 屋根.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 35"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"毎日の生活にヨガは欠かせません。","romaji":"Mainichi no seikatsu ni yoga wa kakasemasen.","german":"Yoga is indispensable in my daily life.","furigana":"まいにち|せいかつ|か"},{"japanese":"朝食は欠かさず食べます。","romaji":"Chōshoku wa kakasazu tabemasu.","german":"I never skip breakfast.","furigana":"ちょうしょく|か|た"},{"japanese":"祖父は毎朝の散歩を一日も欠かしたことがありません。","romaji":"Sofu wa maiasa no sanpo o ichinichi mo kakashita koto ga arimasen.","german":"My grandfather has never missed his morning walk even for a single day.","furigana":"そふ|まいあさ|さんぽ|いちにち|か"}],"notes":"欠かす (transitive) means \"to omit, to neglect\" and occurs almost only in the negative. 欠かさず means \"without exception, regularly\" (毎日欠かさず運動する), and the potential form 欠かせない means \"indispensable\" (X に欠かせない). The intransitive counterpart is 欠ける \"to be lacking, to chip\"; 欠く is the more literary form with a similar meaning.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"昔の町を見物するのが楽しみです。","romaji":"Mukashi no machi o kenbutsu suru no ga tanoshimi desu.","german":"I'm looking forward to seeing the old town.","furigana":"むかし|まち|けんぶつ|たの"},{"japanese":"お祭りを見物に行きましょう。","romaji":"Omatsuri o kenbutsu ni ikimashō.","german":"Let's go and watch the festival.","furigana":"まつ|けんぶつ|い"},{"japanese":"家族で花火を見物しました。","romaji":"Kazoku de hanabi o kenbutsu shimashita.","german":"We watched the fireworks with the family.","furigana":"かぞく|はなび|けんぶつ"}],"notes":"見物 (見物する) means \"to look at something as a spectator\": sights, festivals, fireworks or sporting events: X を見物する, X を見物に行く. The spectators are 見物人. To be distinguished are 観光 \"tourism, traveling to see sights\" and 見学 \"a visit for learning\" (factory, school). With the reading みもの, the same kanji word means \"something worth seeing\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"平仮名は読めますか？","romaji":"Hiragana wa yomemasu ka?","german":"Can you read hiragana?","furigana":"ひらがな|よ"},{"japanese":"まず平仮名を覚えましょう。","romaji":"Mazu hiragana o oboemashō.","german":"Let's learn hiragana first.","furigana":"ひらがな|おぼ"},{"japanese":"名前をひらがなで書いてください。","romaji":"Namae o hiragana de kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write your name in hiragana.","furigana":"なまえ|か"}],"notes":"平仮名 is the rounded Japanese syllabary used for particles, verb endings and many native words. The word itself is almost always written in kana: ひらがな. The angular script for loanwords is カタカナ; both together are 仮名 (かな). Typical: ひらがなで書く \"to write in hiragana\"; the small reading aids above kanji are called ふりがな.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 9"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この子は文字通り「天才」だ。","romaji":"Kono ko wa mojidōri tensai da.","german":"This child is literally a \"genius\".","furigana":"こ|もじどお|てんさい"},{"japanese":"文字通りの意味ではありません。","romaji":"Mojidōri no imi de wa arimasen.","german":"It is not meant literally.","furigana":"もじどお|いみ"},{"japanese":"大雪で、町は文字通り真っ白になりました。","romaji":"Ōyuki de, machi wa mojidōri masshiro ni narimashita.","german":"Because of the heavy snow, the town literally turned white.","furigana":"おおゆき|まち|もじどお|ま|しろ"}],"notes":"文字通り means \"literally, word for word\". As an adverb it stands directly before the predicate (文字通り真っ白); before nouns it is 文字通りの (文字通りの意味 \"the literal meaning\"). It is often used to stress that a strong expression is actually true; the word is then often put in 「」. It sounds somewhat more literary than the everyday 本当に.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"パソコンの使い方の初歩を教えるビデオを作りました。","romaji":"Pasokon no tsukaikata no shoho o oshieru bideo o tsukurimashita.","german":"I made a video teaching the basics of how to use a computer.","furigana":"つか|かた|しょほ|おし|つく"},{"japanese":"初歩から学び始めます。","romaji":"Shoho kara manabi hajimemasu.","german":"I will start learning from the basics.","furigana":"しょほ|まな|はじ"},{"japanese":"これは初歩的なミスですね。","romaji":"Kore wa shohoteki na misu desu ne.","german":"That's a beginner's mistake, isn't it.","furigana":"しょほてき"}],"notes":"初歩 is a noun for the \"first steps, the rudiments\" of a subject: 初歩から学ぶ \"to learn from the basics\", 初歩の日本語. Very common is the na-adjective 初歩的 \"elementary, beginner-like\", e.g. 初歩的なミス \"beginner's mistake\". Similar are 基本 \"basics (which always remain important)\" and 基礎 \"foundation\"; 初歩, by contrast, stresses the beginner level.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"レベルアップすると、新たなステージに行くことができますよ。","romaji":"Reberuappu suru to, arata na sutēji ni iku koto ga dekimasu yo.","german":"If you level up, you can reach a new stage.","furigana":"あら|い"},{"japanese":"新たな挑戦を始めます。","romaji":"Arata na chōsen o hajimemasu.","german":"I'm starting a new challenge.","furigana":"あら|ちょうせん|はじ"},{"japanese":"駅前に新たにスーパーができました。","romaji":"Ekimae ni arata ni sūpā ga dekimashita.","german":"A new supermarket has opened in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|あら"}],"notes":"新た is a na-adjective with a literary tone: 新たな + noun \"new, fresh\" and adverbially 新たに \"newly, anew\". It is used mainly with abstract things like challenge, problem or beginning (新たな問題, 新たな一歩) and is common in the news. For concrete everyday objects (new shoes, a new phone) one says 新しい. Set phrase: 気持ちを新たにする \"to start afresh, to renew one's motivation\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"西洋の国にはまだ行ったことがないんです。","romaji":"Seiyou no kuni ni wa mada itta koto ga nai n desu.","german":"I have never been to a Western country.","furigana":"せいよう|くに|い"},{"japanese":"西洋の文化を学びました。","romaji":"Seiyō no bunka o manabimashita.","german":"I studied Western culture.","furigana":"せいよう|ぶんか|まな"},{"japanese":"このレストランでは西洋料理も食べられます。","romaji":"Kono resutoran de wa seiyō ryōri mo taberaremasu.","german":"At this restaurant you can also eat Western food.","furigana":"せいようりょうり|た"}],"notes":"西洋 means \"the West, the Occident\" (Europe and North America) and is the counterpart of 東洋 \"the Orient, East Asia\". It is mostly used with の or in compounds: 西洋の文化, 西洋人, 西洋料理, 西洋風. In everyday speech today, people often say 欧米 \"Europe and America\" or simply 海外. The short character 洋 appears in 洋食 and 洋服.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"何事もポジティブな心持ちが大切だよね。","romaji":"Nanigoto mo pojitibu na kokoromochi ga taisetsu da yo ne.","german":"A positive mindset is important in everything, isn't it?","furigana":"なにごと|こころも|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"嬉しい心持ちです。","romaji":"Ureshii kokoromochi desu.","german":"I have a happy feeling.","furigana":"うれ|こころも"}],"notes":"心持ち refers to an inner feeling, a mood, or the attitude with which one approaches something. ポジティブな心持ち concerns the attitude toward a task, 嬉しい心持ち the joyful feeling. The adverbial use meaning \"a little\", which is also possible, is not meant here.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"見事な木ですね。","romaji":"Migoto na ki desu ne.","german":"That's a magnificent tree.","furigana":"みごと|き"},{"japanese":"見事な演技でした。","romaji":"Migoto na engi deshita.","german":"It was a superb performance.","furigana":"みごと|えんぎ"},{"japanese":"天気予報は見事に外れました。","romaji":"Tenki yohō wa migoto ni hazuremashita.","german":"The weather forecast was completely wrong.","furigana":"てんきよほう|みごと|はず"}],"notes":"見事 is a na-adjective: 見事な \"splendid, magnificent, masterly\" (見事な桜, 見事な演技). Adverbially, 見事に means \"perfectly, skillfully\" - often also ironically for a complete failure: 見事に失敗した \"failed thoroughly\". As praise one calls out お見事！ \"Bravo!\". Compared with すばらしい, 見事 emphasizes craftsmanship or an impressive sight.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"図書館で生物学の本を借りました。","romaji":"Toshokan de seibutsugaku no hon o karimashita.","german":"I borrowed a biology book from the library.","furigana":"としょかん|せいぶつがく|ほん|か"},{"japanese":"大学で生物学を専攻しています。","romaji":"Daigaku de seibutsugaku o senkō shite imasu.","german":"I am majoring in biology at university.","furigana":"だいがく|せいぶつがく|せんこう"},{"japanese":"兄は生物学の研究をしています。","romaji":"Ani wa seibutsugaku no kenkyū o shite imasu.","german":"My older brother does research in biology.","furigana":"あに|せいぶつがく|けんきゅう"}],"notes":"生物学 is the science \"biology\"; 〜学 forms names of academic fields (数学, 経済学). At school the subject is usually just called 生物; 生物学 sounds like university and research. A biologist is a 生物学者. Typical combinations: 生物学を勉強する, 大学で生物学を専攻する.","levelNote":"Compound: 生物 (N3) + 学 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの投手の伝記、読んだ？","romaji":"Ano tōshu no denki, yonda?","german":"Have you read that pitcher's biography?","furigana":"とうしゅ|でんき|よ"},{"japanese":"有名な科学者の伝記を読みました。","romaji":"Yūmei na kagakusha no denki o yomimashita.","german":"I read a biography of a famous scientist.","furigana":"ゆうめい|かがくしゃ|でんき|よ"},{"japanese":"祖父の伝記を書きたいと思っています。","romaji":"Sofu no denki o kakitai to omotte imasu.","german":"I would like to write a biography of my grandfather.","furigana":"そふ|でんき|か|おも"}],"notes":"伝記 is a \"biography, life story\", usually as a book about a famous person: 〜の伝記を読む/書く. An autobiography is 自伝, and a biographical film is 伝記映画. Beware when listening: 伝記 sounds exactly like 電気 \"electricity, light\" - context decides.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"石を転がした。","romaji":"Ishi o korogashita.","german":"I rolled a stone.","furigana":"いし|ころ"},{"japanese":"ボールを転がして遊びます。","romaji":"Bōru o korogashite asobimasu.","german":"We play by rolling the ball around.","furigana":"ころ|あそ"},{"japanese":"サイコロを転がしてください。","romaji":"Saikoro o korogashite kudasai.","german":"Please roll the dice.","furigana":"ころ"}],"notes":"転がす is transitive: \"to roll something, make it roll\" - a ball, a stone, dice (サイコロを転がす) or a rolling suitcase. The intransitive counterpart is 転がる \"to roll\" (ボールが転がる). Do not confuse it with 転ぶ \"to fall down, tumble\", which is used only for people and animals.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どうぞ、インタビューを進めて下さい。","romaji":"Dōzo, intabyū o susumete kudasai.","german":"Please go ahead and continue with the interview.","furigana":"すす|くだ"},{"japanese":"計画を進めましょう。","romaji":"Keikaku o susumemashō.","german":"Let's move the plan forward.","furigana":"けいかく|すす"},{"japanese":"時計を五分進めておきました。","romaji":"Tokei o gofun susumete okimashita.","german":"I set the clock ahead by five minutes.","furigana":"とけい|ごふんすす"}],"notes":"進める is transitive: \"to move forward, to carry on\" - 計画を進める, 仕事を進める, 話を進める. Literally also \"to move forward\", e.g. 時計を進める \"to set a clock ahead\". The intransitive counterpart is 進む \"to advance, to progress\" (仕事が進む). Read the same but with a different meaning: 勧める \"to recommend, to advise\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"バナナだけの食事はバランスを欠いていると思いますよ。","romaji":"Banana dake no shokuji wa baransu o kaite iru to omoimasu yo.","german":"I think a diet of only bananas lacks balance.","furigana":"しょくじ|か|おも"},{"japanese":"礼儀を欠く行動はよくないです。","romaji":"Reigi o kaku kōdō wa yoku nai desu.","german":"Behavior lacking in courtesy is not good.","furigana":"れいぎ|か|こうどう"},{"japanese":"お皿を落として、ふちを欠いてしまいました。","romaji":"Osara o otoshite, fuchi o kaite shimaimashita.","german":"I dropped the plate and chipped its rim.","furigana":"さら|お|か"}],"notes":"欠く is transitive and rather literary. It means \"to lack, to not have\" (バランスを欠く, 常識を欠く) and concretely \"to chip off a corner\" (皿を欠く). In everyday speech one more often hears the intransitive 欠ける \"to be missing, to chip\" (お皿が欠けた) or the phrase 欠かせない \"indispensable\" from 欠かす. Caution: read the same as 書く \"to write\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あのイタリアンレストランは一代で店を閉めてしまった。","romaji":"Ano itarian resutoran wa ichidai de mise o shimete shimatta.","german":"That Italian restaurant closed after only one generation.","furigana":"いちだい|みせ|し"},{"japanese":"この会社は祖父が一代で大きくしました。","romaji":"Kono kaisha wa sofu ga ichidai de ookiku shimashita.","german":"My grandfather built this company up to a large one within his own lifetime.","furigana":"かいしゃ|そふ|いちだい|おお"}],"notes":"一代 means \"one generation, one human life\" in the sense of one's own lifetime or period of activity. Typical is 一代で \"within a single generation, by one's own efforts\", for example when someone builds up a company on their own. The founding generation is 初代, the following ones 二代目, 三代目. For a generation as an age group (the young generation), 世代 is used instead.","levelNote":"Compound: 代 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"北西に見える山が、ふじ山です。","romaji":"Hokusei ni mieru yama ga, Fujisan desu.","german":"Der Berg, den man im Nordwesten sehen kann, ist der Fuji.","furigana":"ほくせい|み|やま|さん"},{"japanese":"風は北西から吹いています。","romaji":"Kaze wa hokusei kara fuite imasu.","german":"Der Wind weht aus Nordwesten.","furigana":"かぜ|ほくせい|ふ"},{"japanese":"東京の北西にある町に住んでいます。","romaji":"Tōkyō no hokusei ni aru machi ni sunde imasu.","german":"Ich wohne in einer Stadt nordwestlich von Tokio.","furigana":"とうきょう|ほくせい|まち|す"}],"notes":"北西 heißt „Nordwesten“. Wie im Deutschen steht 北 bzw. 南 zuerst: 北西, 北東, 南西, 南東. Typisch sind 北西の風 „Nordwestwind“, 〜の北西に „nordwestlich von …“ und 北西から „aus Nordwesten“. Das umgekehrte 西北 gibt es auch, es ist aber seltener und klingt altmodisch.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"入り口に直行して下さい。","romaji":"Irikuchi ni chokkō shite kudasai.","german":"Please go straight to the entrance.","furigana":"い|くち|ちょっこう|くだ"},{"japanese":"空港に直行します。","romaji":"Kūkō ni chokkō shimasu.","german":"I'm going straight to the airport.","furigana":"くうこう|ちょっこう"},{"japanese":"成田からパリまで直行便で行きました。","romaji":"Narita kara Pari made chokkōbin de ikimashita.","german":"I flew from Narita to Paris on a direct flight.","furigana":"なりた|ちょっこうびん|い"}],"notes":"直行 (直行する) means \"to go directly, without a stopover\"; the destination is marked with に: 空港に直行する. Very common is 直行便 \"direct flight, direct service\". In office language there is also 直行 \"going directly to a client instead of to the office\" and 直帰 \"going directly home\". More colloquially, one says まっすぐ行く.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの二人、仲良しのふりをしているだけで、本当は仲が悪いと思うよ。","romaji":"Ano futari, nakayoshi no furi o shite iru dake de, hontō wa naka ga warui to omou yo.","german":"I think those two are only pretending to be good friends and actually don't get along.","furigana":"ふたり|なかよ|ほんとう|なか|わる|おも"},{"japanese":"あの二人は仲良しです。","romaji":"Ano futari wa nakayoshi desu.","german":"Those two are good friends.","furigana":"ふたり|なかよ"}],"notes":"仲良し refers to a good friend or a friendly relationship. 二人は仲良しです means that two people are good friends. Before another noun, の is used here: 仲良しのふり, pretending to be friends. This card covers the friendship meaning, not the euphemistic use of 仲良しする.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いつかは温かい家庭を作れたらな、と思ってます。","romaji":"Itsuka wa atatakai katei o tsukuretara na, to omottemasu.","german":"I hope that someday I can build a warm family.","furigana":"あたた|かてい|つく|おも"},{"japanese":"家庭では日本語を話しています。","romaji":"Katei de wa nihongo o hanashite imasu.","german":"We speak Japanese at home.","furigana":"かてい|にほんご|はな"}],"notes":"家庭 is a noun for the family or the home as a domestic sphere of life. 家庭の connects it to a noun; 家庭で names the domestic setting of an activity. 温かい家庭 figuratively means a warm, loving family. 作れたら expresses here the hoped-for possibility of building such a family.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"息子は元気です。","romaji":"Musuko wa genki desu.","german":"My son is doing well.","furigana":"むすこ|げんき"},{"japanese":"息子が来月結婚します。","romaji":"Musuko ga raigetsu kekkon shimasu.","german":"My son is getting married next month.","furigana":"むすこ|らいげつけっこん"},{"japanese":"息子さんはおいくつですか。","romaji":"Musuko-san wa oikutsu desu ka.","german":"How old is your son?","furigana":"むすこ"}],"notes":"息子 is one's own son when talking about one's own family. For someone else's son one politely says 息子さん, very formally ご子息. The counterpart is 娘 \"daughter\" or 娘さん. Small children are often simply called 子ども or by name in daily life.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 28"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"すぐに利息が大きくなってしまった。","romaji":"Sugu ni risoku ga ōkiku natte shimatta.","german":"Die Zinsen sind schnell gewachsen.","furigana":"りそく|おお"},{"japanese":"銀行の利息は低いです。","romaji":"Ginkō no risoku wa hikui desu.","german":"Die Zinsen der Bank sind niedrig.","furigana":"ぎんこう|りそく|ひく"},{"japanese":"借りたお金は利息をつけて返します。","romaji":"Karita okane wa risoku o tsukete kaeshimasu.","german":"Das geliehene Geld zahle ich mit Zinsen zurück.","furigana":"か|かね|りそく|かえ"}],"notes":"利息 sind „Zinsen“ auf Kredite oder Guthaben. Typische Verbindungen: 利息がつく „es fallen Zinsen an“, 利息を払う „Zinsen zahlen“, 利息をつけて返す „mit Zinsen zurückzahlen“. 利子 bedeutet praktisch dasselbe; der Zinssatz heißt dagegen 金利. Wie im Deutschen ist 利息 ein Wort aus Bank- und Geldsprache.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私も父も根本の考え方は同じです。","romaji":"Watashi mo chichi mo konpon no kangaekata wa onaji desu.","german":"My father and I share the same basic way of thinking.","furigana":"わたし|ちち|こんぽん|かんが|かた|おな"},{"japanese":"根本から考え直しましょう。","romaji":"Konpon kara kangaenaoshimashō.","german":"Let's rethink it from the ground up.","furigana":"こんぽん|かんが|なお"},{"japanese":"この問題は根本的に解決しなければなりません。","romaji":"Kono mondai wa konponteki ni kaiketsu shinakereba narimasen.","german":"We have to solve this problem fundamentally.","furigana":"もんだい|こんぽんてき|かいけつ"}],"notes":"根本 means \"root, foundation, the fundamental part\" of something. Common are 根本から \"from the ground up\", 根本の考え方, and above all the na-adjective 根本的 \"fundamental\", or adverbially 根本的に. 基本 \"basis, basics\" is similar but refers more to basic rules. With the reading ねもと, 根本 (also written 根元) denotes the lower part of a tree or object.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"トイレは使ったら水を流しましょう。","romaji":"Toire wa tsukattara mizu o nagashimashō.","german":"Please flush the toilet after use.","furigana":"つか|みず|なが"},{"japanese":"悲しい映画を見て、涙を流しました。","romaji":"Kanashii eiga o mite, namida o nagashimashita.","german":"I shed tears watching the sad movie.","furigana":"かな|えいが|み|なみだ|なが"},{"japanese":"店ではいつも静かな音楽を流しています。","romaji":"Mise de wa itsumo shizuka na ongaku o nagashite imasu.","german":"Quiet music is always playing in the shop.","furigana":"みせ|しず|おんがく|なが"}],"notes":"流す is transitive: \"to let flow\". In everyday life, トイレの水を流す means \"to flush the toilet\"; also 涙を流す \"to shed tears\", 汗を流す \"to sweat; to work hard\" and 音楽を流す \"to play music\". The intransitive counterpart is 流れる \"to flow, to be playing\" (水が流れる, 音楽が流れる). As a suffix, 〜流す means \"to do in passing, without attention\": 聞き流す \"to let go in one ear and out the other\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"さすが、一流のピアニストは違うね。","romaji":"Sasuga, ichiryū no pianisuto wa chigau ne.","german":"As expected, a first-rate pianist is something else.","furigana":"いちりゅう|ちが"},{"japanese":"一流のレストランで食事しました。","romaji":"Ichiryū no resutoran de shokuji shimashita.","german":"I ate at a first-class restaurant.","furigana":"いちりゅう|しょくじ"},{"japanese":"兄は一流の会社で働いています。","romaji":"Ani wa ichiryū no kaisha de hataraite imasu.","german":"My older brother works at a top company.","furigana":"あに|いちりゅう|かいしゃ|はたら"}],"notes":"一流 means \"first-class, top-\" and usually appears as 一流の before nouns: 一流のホテル, 一流企業, 一流大学. The lower grades 二流 and 三流 mean \"second-rate\" and \"third-rate\" and sound derogatory; at the very top is 超一流 \"absolute world class\". An advanced use is 彼一流の… \"typical of him\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"アマゾン川の本流は水が茶色いそうですよ。","romaji":"Amazongawa no honryuu wa mizu ga chairoi sou desu yo.","german":"Apparently the water in the main stream of the Amazon is brown.","furigana":"がわ|ほんりゅう|みず|ちゃいろ"},{"japanese":"川の本流は深いです。","romaji":"Kawa no honryū wa fukai desu.","german":"The main stream of the river is deep.","furigana":"かわ|ほんりゅう|ふか"},{"japanese":"この小さい川は、この先で本流と一つになります。","romaji":"Kono chiisai kawa wa, kono saki de honryū to hitotsu ni narimasu.","german":"This small river joins the main stream farther ahead.","furigana":"ちい|かわ|さき|ほんりゅう|ひと"}],"notes":"本流 is the \"main stream\" of a river as opposed to its tributaries (支流). Figuratively it also means the \"main direction, mainstream\" of a school of thought or party; but this sounds literary. For \"mainstream\" in everyday speech, one rather says 主流. The word has nothing to do with 本 \"book\"; here 本 means \"main\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"年末までに売り上げを去年の五倍にしたいんです。","romaji":"Nenmatsu made ni uriage o kyonen no gobai ni shitai n desu.","german":"I want to increase sales to five times last year's by the end of the year.","furigana":"ねんまつ|う|あ|きょねん|ごばい"},{"japanese":"値段が二倍になりました。","romaji":"Nedan ga nibai ni narimashita.","german":"The price doubled.","furigana":"ねだん|にばい"},{"japanese":"この部屋は私の部屋の三倍ぐらい広いです。","romaji":"Kono heya wa watashi no heya no sanbai gurai hiroi desu.","german":"This room is about three times as large as my room.","furigana":"へや|わたし|へや|さんばい|ひろ"}],"notes":"〜倍 is a counter for multiples: 二倍 \"double\", 三倍 \"triple\", 十倍 \"tenfold\". Typical patterns are X の三倍 \"three times as much as X\", 〜倍になる \"to become x times as much\" and 〜倍にする \"to multiply by x\". Used alone, 倍 already means \"double\" (値段が倍になった). Note: \"twice\" in the sense of frequency is 二回 or 二度, not 二倍.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いつもの二倍の大きさのカップでコーヒーを飲みました。","romaji":"Itsumo no nibai no ōkisa no kappu de kōhī o nomimashita.","german":"I drank coffee from a cup twice the size of my usual one.","furigana":"にばい|おお|の"},{"japanese":"去年の二倍売れました。","romaji":"Kyonen no nibai uremashita.","german":"It sold twice as much as last year.","furigana":"きょねん|にばいう"},{"japanese":"このジュースは量が二倍なのに、値段は同じです。","romaji":"Kono jūsu wa ryō ga nibai na no ni, nedan wa onaji desu.","german":"This juice has double the amount, but the price is the same.","furigana":"りょう|にばい|ねだん|おな"}],"notes":"二倍 means \"double, twice as much\". The comparison is made with の: いつもの二倍 \"twice as much as usual\"; before nouns it is 二倍の (二倍の量). With なる/する it expresses doubling: 二倍になる, 二倍にする. In conversation people often say simply 倍. Not to be confused with 二回 \"twice (frequency)\".","levelNote":"Compound: 倍 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"バンドメンバーの全員が男です。","romaji":"Bando menbā no zen'in ga otoko desu.","german":"All of the band members are men.","furigana":"ぜんいん|おとこ"},{"japanese":"全員集まりましたか。","romaji":"Zen'in atsumarimashita ka.","german":"Has everyone gathered?","furigana":"ぜんいんあつ"},{"japanese":"家族全員で旅行に行きました。","romaji":"Kazoku zen'in de ryokō ni ikimashita.","german":"We went on a trip with the whole family.","furigana":"かぞくぜんいん|りょこう|い"}],"notes":"全員 means \"all (members of a group)\" and applies only to people. Typical are 全員が, 全員で \"all together\" and combinations like 家族全員, クラス全員. It can also stand adverbially without a particle (全員集まりました). In casual conversation one often says みんな; for things one uses 全部.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先日、新しい社員が入社しました。","romaji":"Senjitsu, atarashii shain ga nyūsha shimashita.","german":"A new employee joined the company the other day.","furigana":"せんじつ|あたら|しゃいん|にゅうしゃ"},{"japanese":"私はこの会社の社員じゃありません。アルバイトです。","romaji":"Watashi wa kono kaisha no shain ja arimasen. Arubaito desu.","german":"I'm not an employee of this company. I'm a part-timer.","furigana":"わたし|かいしゃ|しゃいん"},{"japanese":"社員食堂は安くておいしいです。","romaji":"Shain shokudō wa yasukute oishii desu.","german":"The company cafeteria is cheap and good.","furigana":"しゃいんしょくどう|やす"}],"notes":"社員 is an \"employee, staff member\" of a particular company: この会社の社員, 新入社員 \"new employee\", 正社員 \"full-time employee\" as opposed to アルバイト or パート. When stating your occupation in general, 会社員です is used instead. Set compounds are 社員食堂 \"cafeteria\" and 社員旅行 \"company trip\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"広島はカキで有名です。","romaji":"Hiroshima wa kaki de yūmei desu.","german":"Hiroshima is famous for oysters.","furigana":"ひろしま|ゆうめい"},{"japanese":"広島の平和記念公園に行きました。","romaji":"Hiroshima no Heiwa Kinen Kōen ni ikimashita.","german":"I went to the Peace Memorial Park in Hiroshima.","furigana":"ひろしま|へいわきねんこうえん|い"},{"japanese":"広島風のお好み焼きを食べたことがありますか。","romaji":"Hiroshima-fū no okonomiyaki o tabeta koto ga arimasu ka.","german":"Have you ever eaten Hiroshima-style okonomiyaki?","furigana":"ひろしまふう|この|や|た"}],"notes":"広島 is a major city and prefecture in the west of Honshū (広島市, 広島県). It is known for the Peace Memorial Park, the Atomic Bomb Dome (原爆ドーム), oysters (カキ) and Hiroshima-style okonomiyaki (広島風お好み焼き). In reference to the atomic bombing, the name is sometimes written in katakana, ヒロシマ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"第一章はおもしろかったけど、第二章はいまいちだった。","romaji":"Dai isshō wa omoshirokatta kedo, dai nishō wa imaichi datta.","german":"Das erste Kapitel war gut, aber das zweite war mittelmäßig.","furigana":"だいいっしょう|だいにしょう"},{"japanese":"第二章から読み始めてください。","romaji":"Dainishō kara yomihajimete kudasai.","german":"Bitte fangen Sie ab dem zweiten Kapitel an zu lesen.","furigana":"だいにしょう|よ|はじ"},{"japanese":"結婚して、人生の第二章が始まりました。","romaji":"Kekkon shite, jinsei no dai nishō ga hajimarimashita.","german":"Mit der Hochzeit hat ein neues Kapitel in meinem Leben begonnen.","furigana":"けっこん|じんせい|だいにしょう|はじ"}],"notes":"第二章 heißt „zweites Kapitel“ und ist zusammengesetzt aus 第 (Ordinalpräfix „-te“), 二 und dem Zähler 章 „Kapitel“. Genauso bildet man 第一章, 第三章 usw. Übertragen spricht man wie im Deutschen vom 人生の第二章 „neuen Kapitel im Leben“. In Büchern steht oft auch 第2章 mit arabischer Ziffer.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自分の安全を第一に考えて下さい。","romaji":"Jibun no anzen o dai ichi ni kangaete kudasai.","german":"Please put your own safety first.","furigana":"じぶん|あんぜん|だいいち|かんが|くだ"},{"japanese":"安全第一です。","romaji":"Anzen daiichi desu.","german":"Safety first.","furigana":"あんぜんだいいち"},{"japanese":"旅行には行けないよ。第一、お金がないし。","romaji":"Ryokō ni wa ikenai yo. Daiichi, okane ga nai shi.","german":"I can't go on the trip. For one thing, I have no money.","furigana":"りょこう|い|だいいち|かね"}],"notes":"第一 is first of all a noun: \"the first thing, the most important thing\". After a noun, 〜第一 expresses top priority (安全第一 \"safety first\", 健康第一). 第一に means \"in the first place, first of all\". At the beginning of a sentence, 第一、… works as an adverb \"for one thing, to begin with\" when adding another, decisive argument.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この作品は作文コンクールで第一位でした。","romaji":"Kono sakuhin wa sakubun konkūru de dai ichii deshita.","german":"This work took first place in the essay contest.","furigana":"さくひん|さくぶん|だいいちい"},{"japanese":"今月の売り上げで、うちの店が第一位になりました。","romaji":"Kongetsu no uriage de, uchi no mise ga daiichii ni narimashita.","german":"Our shop took first place in sales this month.","furigana":"こんげつ|う|あ|みせ|だいいちい"},{"japanese":"人気ランキングで、この歌が第一位です。","romaji":"Ninki rankingu de, kono uta ga daiichii desu.","german":"This song is number one in the popularity ranking.","furigana":"にんき|うた|だいいちい"}],"notes":"第一位 is \"first place\" in a ranking or competition: 第一位になる \"to become number one\", 〜で第一位 \"first place in ...\". In conversation people usually say the shorter 一位, likewise 二位, 三位. The prefix 第 turns the number into an ordinal and sounds somewhat more official.","levelNote":"Compound: 第一 (N2) + 位 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"住所は東京都新宿区です。","romaji":"Jūsho wa Tōkyō-to Shinjuku-ku desu.","german":"My address is in Shinjuku Ward, Tokyo.","furigana":"じゅうしょ|とうきょうとしんじゅくく"},{"japanese":"東京都に住んでいます。","romaji":"Tōkyōto ni sunde imasu.","german":"I live in Tokyo.","furigana":"とうきょうと|す"},{"japanese":"東京都の人口は千四百万人ぐらいです。","romaji":"Tōkyō-to no jinkō wa sen'yonhyakuman-nin gurai desu.","german":"The Tokyo Metropolis has a population of about 14 million.","furigana":"とうきょうと|じんこう|せんよんひゃくまんにん"}],"notes":"東京都 is the administrative unit \"Tokyo Metropolis\". 都 is a type of prefecture of its own next to 道 (北海道), 府 (大阪府, 京都府) and 県, and there is only one 都. 東京都 is used in addresses, forms and official language (都知事, 都庁); in everyday speech people simply say 東京.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"京都は昔、日本の都でした。","romaji":"Kyōto wa mukashi, Nihon no miyako deshita.","german":"Kyoto used to be the capital of Japan.","furigana":"きょうと|むかし|にほん|みやこ"},{"japanese":"京都には美しい庭園があります。","romaji":"Kyōto ni wa utsukushii teien ga arimasu.","german":"There are beautiful gardens in Kyoto.","furigana":"きょうと|うつく|ていえん"},{"japanese":"東京から京都まで新幹線で二時間ぐらいです。","romaji":"Tōkyō kara Kyōto made shinkansen de nijikan gurai desu.","german":"It takes about two hours from Tokyo to Kyoto by Shinkansen.","furigana":"とうきょう|きょうと|しんかんせん|にじかん"}],"notes":"京都 is both a city and a prefecture (京都市, 京都府) and was Japan's capital from 794 until the beginning of the Meiji period (1868/69). It is known for temples, shrines, gardens and traditional districts. The kanji 都 means \"capital\"; 東京 literally means \"eastern capital\". Typical: 京都に行く, 京都駅, 京都の人.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あそこで何か動いたよ。","romaji":"Asoko de nani ka ugoita yo.","german":"Something moved over there.","furigana":"なに|うご"},{"japanese":"時計が動いていません。","romaji":"Tokei ga ugoite imasen.","german":"The clock isn't running.","furigana":"とけい|うご"},{"japanese":"写真を撮りますから、動かないでください。","romaji":"Shashin o torimasu kara, ugokanaide kudasai.","german":"I'm going to take a photo, so please don't move.","furigana":"しゃしん|と|うご"}],"notes":"動く is intransitive: \"to move\", and for machines \"to run, to work\" (時計が動かない \"the clock isn't running\", 電車が動いていない \"the trains aren't running\"). Figuratively it also means \"to act, to become active\". The transitive counterpart is 動かす \"to move, to set in motion\" (机を動かす). For \"to move house\", one does not say 動く but 引っ越す.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"田中さんは商売で大きく成功しました。","romaji":"Tanakasan wa shōbai de ōkiku seikō shimashita.","german":"Mr. Tanaka had great success in business.","furigana":"たなか|しょうばい|おお|せいこう"},{"japanese":"商売がうまくいっています。","romaji":"Shōbai ga umaku itte imasu.","german":"Business is going well.","furigana":"しょうばい"},{"japanese":"兄は東京で新しい商売を始めました。","romaji":"Ani wa Tōkyō de atarashii shōbai o hajimemashita.","german":"My older brother started a new business in Tokyo.","furigana":"あに|とうきょう|あたら|しょうばい|はじ"}],"notes":"商売 is \"trade, business\" in the sense of buying and selling, often for small shops and self-employed people: 商売をする, 商売を始める, 商売がうまくいく. 商売繁盛 is the wish for \"a thriving business\". Colloquially 商売 can also mean \"occupation, job\" (商売道具 \"tools of the trade\"). For companies and industries, ビジネス or 事業 sound more modern.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ここは昔、商人の家でした。","romaji":"Koko wa mukashi, shōnin no ie deshita.","german":"This used to be a merchant's house.","furigana":"むかし|しょうにん|いえ"},{"japanese":"昔の商人は船で旅をしました。","romaji":"Mukashi no shōnin wa fune de tabi o shimashita.","german":"Merchants of the past traveled by ship.","furigana":"むかし|しょうにん|ふね|たび"},{"japanese":"大阪の商人は話がうまいと言われています。","romaji":"Ōsaka no shōnin wa hanashi ga umai to iwarete imasu.","german":"Osaka merchants are said to be good talkers.","furigana":"おおさか|しょうにん|はなし|い"}],"notes":"商人 is a \"merchant, trader\" and often sounds historical, for instance for merchants of the Edo period or the proverbial 大阪商人. For today's businesspeople one rather says 商売人, ビジネスマン or 店の人. Easily confused when listening: 証人 \"witness\" is read the same way, and 職人 is a \"craftsman\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あいつは悪人だが、花が大好きなガーデニングのプロなんだ。","romaji":"Aitsu wa akunin da ga, hana ga daisuki na gādeningu no puro na n da.","german":"He's a villain, but also a gardening pro who loves flowers.","furigana":"あくにん|はな|だいす"},{"japanese":"映画の悪人は怖かったです。","romaji":"Eiga no akunin wa kowakatta desu.","german":"The villain in the movie was scary.","furigana":"えいが|あくにん|こわ"},{"japanese":"彼は悪人ではありません。ただ少し口が悪いだけです。","romaji":"Kare wa akunin de wa arimasen. Tada sukoshi kuchi ga warui dake desu.","german":"He is not a bad person. He just has a bit of a sharp tongue.","furigana":"かれ|あくにん|すこ|くち|わる"}],"notes":"悪人 is a \"bad person, villain\" and sounds somewhat literary or like stories and films. In everyday speech people mostly say 悪い人. The opposite is 善人 \"good person\". The villain role in film or theater is 悪役.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの悪女、耳だけはいいんだよな。","romaji":"Ano akujo, mimi dake wa ii n da yo na.","german":"That wicked woman has good ears, at least.","furigana":"あくじょ|みみ"},{"japanese":"このドラマの悪女が面白いです。","romaji":"Kono dorama no akujo ga omoshiroi desu.","german":"The wicked woman in this drama is interesting.","furigana":"あくじょ|おもしろ"},{"japanese":"その小説の主人公は、男をだます悪女です。","romaji":"Sono shōsetsu no shujinkō wa, otoko o damasu akujo desu.","german":"The protagonist of that novel is a femme fatale who deceives men.","furigana":"しょうせつ|しゅじんこう|おとこ|あくじょ"}],"notes":"悪女 refers to a \"wicked, scheming woman\", often in the sense of a femme fatale in novels, dramas and gossip. Used of real people, it is a strong insult. The gender-neutral word is 悪人. The old secondary meaning \"ugly woman\" is hardly used today.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日は最悪な一日だったよ。","romaji":"Kyō wa saiaku na ichinichi datta yo.","german":"Today was the worst day.","furigana":"きょう|さいあく|いちにち"},{"japanese":"財布を落とした。最悪！","romaji":"Saifu o otoshita. Saiaku!","german":"I lost my wallet. Ugh, the worst!","furigana":"さいふ|お|さいあく"},{"japanese":"最悪の場合、歩いて帰ればいいよ。","romaji":"Saiaku no baai, aruite kaereba ii yo.","german":"In the worst case, we can just walk home.","furigana":"さいあく|ばあい|ある|かえ"}],"notes":"最悪 means \"the worst\" and is the opposite of 最高. Before nouns it is 最悪の or, colloquially, 最悪な (最悪の場合 \"in the worst case\"). As an exclamation, 最悪！ means \"Damn! Terrible!\" and is very colloquial. At the start of a sentence, 最悪 can work adverbially as \"at worst\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"こんな深夜に、どうしたの？","romaji":"Konna shin'ya ni, dō shita no?","german":"What's the matter, at this hour of the night?","furigana":"しんや"},{"japanese":"深夜まで仕事をしました。","romaji":"Shin'ya made shigoto o shimashita.","german":"I worked until late at night.","furigana":"しんや|しごと"},{"japanese":"深夜バスで大阪へ行きます。","romaji":"Shin'ya basu de Ōsaka e ikimasu.","german":"I'm going to Osaka by night bus.","furigana":"しんや|おおさか|い"}],"notes":"深夜 is the \"late night\", roughly from midnight until early morning. Typical are 深夜に, 深夜まで, 深夜バス \"night bus\", 深夜番組 \"late-night show\" and 深夜料金 \"late-night surcharge\" for taxis. 夜中 has the same meaning but is more colloquial; 夜遅く only means \"late in the evening\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ここは水深100フィートです。","romaji":"Koko wa suishin hyaku fīto desu.","german":"The water is a hundred feet deep here.","furigana":"すいしん"},{"japanese":"このプールの水深は二メートルです。","romaji":"Kono pūru no suishin wa ni mētoru desu.","german":"The water depth of this pool is two meters.","furigana":"すいしん|に"},{"japanese":"飛び込む前に、水深を確認してください。","romaji":"Tobikomu mae ni, suishin o kakunin shite kudasai.","german":"Please check the water depth before jumping in.","furigana":"と|こ|まえ|すいしん|かくにん"}],"notes":"水深 is the depth of the water at a certain spot. A measurement is stated with 水深は二メートルです or checked with 水深を確認する. The word is a noun; 深い, by contrast, is the adjective \"deep\". The measurements in the first two examples refer to specific, mutually unrelated places.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"水深は最深で３００mもある。","romaji":"Suishin wa saishin de sanbyaku mētoru mo aru.","german":"The water depth is as much as 300 meters at the deepest point."},{"japanese":"海の最深部はとても暗いです。","romaji":"Umi no saishinbu wa totemo kurai desu.","german":"The deepest part of the sea is very dark.","furigana":"うみ|さいしんぶ|くら"},{"japanese":"地図には最深の場所が示されています。","romaji":"Chizu ni wa saishin no basho ga shimesarete imasu.","german":"The map shows the deepest spot.","furigana":"ちず|さいしん|ばしょ|しめ"}],"notes":"最深 here refers to the greatest depth or the deepest spot. The noun appears in 最深で, at the deepest point, and combines with の: 最深の場所. In 最深部, 部 is the area or part. In a dictionary the word can also have an adjectival role; the examples and the accent evidence of this card deal with the nominal use. Do not confuse it with 最新, which means newest.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"四十二階まで競争よ！","romaji":"Yonjūnikai made kyōsō yo!","german":"Lass uns zum zweiundvierzigsten Stock um die Wette laufen!","furigana":"よんじゅうにかい|きょうそう"},{"japanese":"オフィスは四十二階にあります。","romaji":"Ofisu wa yonjūnikai ni arimasu.","german":"Das Büro befindet sich im 42. Stock.","furigana":"よんじゅうにかい"},{"japanese":"四十二階のレストランから富士山が見えます。","romaji":"Yonjūnikai no resutoran kara Fujisan ga miemasu.","german":"Vom Restaurant im 42. Stock aus kann man den Fuji sehen.","furigana":"よんじゅうにかい|ふじさん|み"}],"notes":"四十二階 setzt sich aus der Zahl 四十二 und dem Zähler 〜階 „Stockwerk“ zusammen; in der Praxis schreibt man meist 42階. Achtung bei der Zählung: In Japan ist 一階 das Erdgeschoss, 四十二階 entspricht also dem 41. Obergeschoss nach deutscher Zählung. Die Lesung von 階 ändert sich bei manchen Zahlen: 三階 (さんがい), 何階 (なんがい), 一階 (いっかい).","levelNote":"Compound: 階 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"学期が終わると、夏休みが始まります。","romaji":"Gakki ga owaru to, natsuyasumi ga hajimarimasu.","german":"When the term ends, summer vacation begins.","furigana":"がっき|お|なつやす|はじ"},{"japanese":"新しい学期が始まります。","romaji":"Atarashii gakki ga hajimarimasu.","german":"The new term begins.","furigana":"あたら|がっき|はじ"},{"japanese":"二学期の成績が上がりました。","romaji":"Ni-gakki no seiseki ga agarimashita.","german":"My grades in the second term improved.","furigana":"にがっき|せいせき|あ"}],"notes":"学期 is a division of the school year. Japanese schools usually have three 学期 (一学期 from April, 二学期 from September, 三学期 from January), which corresponds more to trimesters. At universities one speaks of 前期/後期 or likewise of 学期 \"semester\". Common are 新学期 \"new school year/term\" and 学期末 \"end of the term\". Read the same: 楽器 \"musical instrument\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"やっと今年もスノーボードとスキーの時期が来ましたね。","romaji":"Yatto kotoshi mo sunōbōdo to sukī no jiki ga kimashita ne.","german":"The snowboarding and skiing season has finally come around again this year.","furigana":"ことし|じき|き"},{"japanese":"桜の時期が一番好きです。","romaji":"Sakura no jiki ga ichiban suki desu.","german":"I like the cherry blossom season best.","furigana":"さくら|じき|いちばんす"},{"japanese":"引っ越しの時期はまだ決めていません。","romaji":"Hikkoshi no jiki wa mada kimete imasen.","german":"We haven't decided yet when we will move.","furigana":"ひ|こ|じき|き"}],"notes":"時期 is a \"period, point in time, the time for something\": この時期 \"at this time of year, at this stage\", 桜の時期, 時期が早い \"it's still too early\". Unlike 季節 (the four seasons), 時期 refers to a particular phase for an event or activity. 時代, by contrast, refers to an era, and ころ to an approximate point in time.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"茶の湯は日本の文化の大切な一部です。","romaji":"Chanoyu wa Nippon no bunka no taisetsu na ichibu desu.","german":"The tea ceremony is an important part of Japanese culture.","furigana":"ちゃ|ゆ|にっぽん|ぶんか|たいせつ|いちぶ"},{"japanese":"祖母は若いころから茶の湯を習っています。","romaji":"Sobo wa wakai koro kara chanoyu o naratte imasu.","german":"My grandmother has been learning the tea ceremony since she was young.","furigana":"そぼ|わか|ちゃ|ゆ|なら"},{"japanese":"茶の湯の道具は、どれも古くて高いです。","romaji":"Chanoyu no dōgu wa, dore mo furukute takai desu.","german":"The utensils for the tea ceremony are all old and expensive.","furigana":"ちゃ|ゆ|どうぐ|ふる|たか"}],"notes":"茶の湯 is a traditional term for the Japanese tea ceremony, literally \"hot water for tea\". Today 茶道 (さどう) is more common; in everyday speech also simply お茶を習う \"to learn the tea ceremony\". Typical are 茶の湯の道具 \"tea utensils\" and 茶の湯を習う. The word sounds cultivated and somewhat old-fashioned.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ケトルから湯気が出てるよ。","romaji":"Ketoru kara yuge ga deteru yo.","german":"Steam is coming out of the kettle.","furigana":"ゆげ|で"},{"japanese":"お風呂の湯気で鏡が曇りました。","romaji":"Ofuro no yuge de kagami ga kumorimashita.","german":"The mirror fogged up from the steam of the bath.","furigana":"ふろ|ゆげ|かがみ|くも"},{"japanese":"湯気が立っているラーメンはおいしそうです。","romaji":"Yuge ga tatte iru rāmen wa oishisō desu.","german":"Steaming ramen looks delicious.","furigana":"ゆげ|た"}],"notes":"湯気 is the visible steam rising from hot water, food or the bath. Typical are 湯気が出る and 湯気が立つ \"it steams\". Technical steam (steam engine) is 蒸気, smoke is 煙 and fog is 霧. Figuratively, 頭から湯気を出す means \"to boil with rage\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その短刀、どこで買ったの？","romaji":"Sono tantō, doko de katta no?","german":"Where did you buy that dagger?","furigana":"たんとう|か"},{"japanese":"博物館に古い短刀が展示されています。","romaji":"Hakubutsukan ni furui tantō ga tenji sarete imasu.","german":"An old dagger is on display at the museum.","furigana":"はくぶつかん|ふる|たんとう|てんじ"},{"japanese":"侍は腰に刀と短刀を差していました。","romaji":"Samurai wa koshi ni katana to tantou o sashite imashita.","german":"The samurai wore a sword and a short sword at his waist.","furigana":"さむらい|こし|かたな|たんとう|さ"}],"notes":"短刀 is a short knife or short sword with a blade of at most about 30 cm, especially the traditional Japanese short sword of the samurai era. The word is encountered mainly in museums, history, and samurai films; in everyday speech one says ナイフ for a knife and 包丁 for a kitchen knife. The long samurai sword is called 刀. Wearing it at the waist is expressed with 差す: 刀を差す.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"短期の実験が終わりました。","romaji":"Tanki no jikken ga owarimashita.","german":"The short-term experiment is finished.","furigana":"たんき|じっけん|お"},{"japanese":"短期のアルバイトを探しています。","romaji":"Tanki no arubaito o sagashite imasu.","german":"I'm looking for a short-term part-time job.","furigana":"たんき|さが"},{"japanese":"夏休みに三週間の短期留学をしました。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi ni sanshuukan no tanki ryuugaku o shimashita.","german":"During summer vacation I did a three-week short study stay abroad.","furigana":"なつやす|さんしゅうかん|たんきりゅうがく"}],"notes":"短期 means \"short duration, short-term\" and is a noun, not an い-adjective: it takes の before a noun (短期のアルバイト) or forms compounds such as 短期留学 \"short stay abroad for study\" or 短期間 \"short period of time\". The opposite is 長期 \"long-term\". For everyday \"short\" (a short time, a short way), 短い is used. Do not confuse it with the identically pronounced 短気 \"short-tempered, impatient\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ねえママ、今日の晩ご飯はもう決まった？","romaji":"Nee mama, kyou no bangohan wa mou kimatta?","german":"Hey Mom, has it been decided what's for dinner tonight?","furigana":"きょう|ばん|はん|き"},{"japanese":"会議の日程が決まりました。","romaji":"Kaigi no nittei ga kimarimashita.","german":"The date for the meeting has been decided.","furigana":"かいぎ|にってい|き"},{"japanese":"この寮では、夜十時までに帰ることに決まっています。","romaji":"Kono ryou de wa, yoru juuji made ni kaeru koto ni kimatte imasu.","german":"In this dormitory, it is the rule that you must be back by ten at night.","furigana":"りょう|よるじゅうじ|かえ|き"}],"notes":"決まる is the intransitive counterpart of 決める \"to decide, to fix\": 日程を決める → 日程が決まる \"the date is set\". The result is marked with に: 行き先が北海道に決まる. ～ことに決まる or ～ことに決まっている means \"it is fixed / it is the rule that ...\". In the ている form, 決まっている expresses a fixed state or something taken for granted. Colloquially, 決まってる also means \"looks perfect, looks great\" (clothes, hairstyle).","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 19"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の父親も酒飲みなんですよ。","romaji":"Watashi no chichioya mo sakenomi na n desu yo.","german":"My father is a drinker too.","furigana":"わたし|ちちおや|さけの"},{"japanese":"この店は酒飲みに人気があります。","romaji":"Kono mise wa sakenomi ni ninki ga arimasu.","german":"This pub is popular with people who like to drink.","furigana":"みせ|さけの|にんき"},{"japanese":"彼は酒飲みだが、仕事はまじめだ。","romaji":"Kare wa sakenomi da ga, shigoto wa majime da.","german":"He drinks a lot, but he is conscientious at work.","furigana":"かれ|さけの|しごと"}],"notes":"酒飲み refers to someone who likes to drink a lot of alcohol regularly; 酒 here stands for alcohol in general, not just sake. The word is colloquial and often sounds self-deprecating or mildly reproachful, but does not necessarily mean alcohol addiction (that would be アルコール依存症). Intensified, it is 大酒飲み \"heavy drinker\". 酒好き, by contrast, stresses only the liking for alcohol.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"キリンの鳴き声を聞いたことがありますか。","romaji":"Kirin no nakigoe o kiita koto ga arimasu ka.","german":"Have you ever heard the sound a giraffe makes?","furigana":"な|ごえ|き"},{"japanese":"鳥の鳴き声が聞こえます。","romaji":"Tori no nakigoe ga kikoemasu.","german":"I can hear birds singing.","furigana":"とり|な|ごえ|き"},{"japanese":"夜中に猫の鳴き声で目が覚めました。","romaji":"Yonaka ni neko no nakigoe de me ga samemashita.","german":"I woke up in the middle of the night to a cat meowing.","furigana":"よなか|ねこ|な|ごえ|め|さ"}],"notes":"鳴き声 is the sound of an animal: the chirping of birds, meowing, barking, also the chirping of insects (虫の鳴き声). It comes from the verb 鳴く, which is used for animal sounds. The identically read 泣き声 with 泣く, by contrast, means the \"crying\" of a person, such as a baby. A human voice is simply 声. Typical: 鳴き声が聞こえる, 鳴き声を聞く.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"心配事があるときは、友人や親に相談するのが一番です。","romaji":"Shinpaigoto ga aru toki wa, yūjin ya oya ni sōdan suru no ga ichiban desu.","german":"When you have worries, it is best to talk to friends or parents.","furigana":"しんぱいごと|ゆうじん|おや|そうだん|いちばん"},{"japanese":"何か心配事がありますか。","romaji":"Nanika shinpaigoto ga arimasu ka.","german":"Do you have any worries?","furigana":"なに|しんぱいごと"},{"japanese":"心配事が多くて、夜よく眠れません。","romaji":"Shinpaigoto ga ookute, yoru yoku nemuremasen.","german":"I have so many worries that I can't sleep well at night.","furigana":"しんぱいごと|おお|よる|ねむ"}],"notes":"心配事 is a concrete thing that worries you, a single \"worry\" or problem. 心配 alone, by contrast, is the feeling or the act of worrying (心配する, 心配だ). Typical are 心配事がある and 何か心配事がありますか. Through compounding, 事 becomes ごと; it is also written 心配ごと. Similar is 悩み, which means more of an inner problem you wrestle with.","levelNote":"Compound: 心配 (N4) + 事 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"落ち葉を集めて火を起こそう。","romaji":"Ochiba o atsumete hi o okosō.","german":"Let's gather fallen leaves and make a fire.","furigana":"お|ば|あつ|ひ|お"},{"japanese":"秋になると落ち葉が増えます。","romaji":"Aki ni naru to ochiba ga fuemasu.","german":"In autumn there are more falling leaves.","furigana":"あき|お|ば|ふ"},{"japanese":"公園の道が落ち葉でいっぱいです。","romaji":"Kouen no michi ga ochiba de ippai desu.","german":"The path in the park is full of fallen leaves.","furigana":"こうえん|みち|お|ば"}],"notes":"落ち葉 are leaves that have fallen from the tree - a compound of 落ちる and 葉, which becomes ば here. Typical combinations: 落ち葉を集める, 落ち葉を掃く \"to sweep leaves\", 落ち葉を踏む. Colored leaves still on the tree are 紅葉 (こうよう/もみじ); dried-up leaves are called 枯れ葉. The word is a noun, not an adjective.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"父親はいつも私たちに助言をしてくれます。","romaji":"Chichioya wa itsumo watashitachi ni jogen o shite kuremasu.","german":"Our father always gives us advice.","furigana":"ちちおや|わたし|じょげん"},{"japanese":"父親に似ているとよく言われます。","romaji":"Chichioya ni nite iru to yoku iwaremasu.","german":"People often tell me I look like my father.","furigana":"ちちおや|に|い"},{"japanese":"父親として、子どもと過ごす時間を大切にしたいです。","romaji":"Chichioya to shite, kodomo to sugosu jikan o taisetsu ni shitai desu.","german":"As a father, I want to cherish the time I spend with my children.","furigana":"ちちおや|こ|す|じかん|たいせつ"}],"notes":"父親 is the matter-of-fact, neutral word for \"father\" as the male parent, often in contrast to 母親. It is used descriptively, e.g. 父親として \"as a father\" or 父親に似ている. To outsiders one politely refers to one's own father as 父, and to other people's fathers as お父さん; a father is addressed as お父さん, never as 父親. When speaking of oneself as a \"father\" in a role, one also uses 父親.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"うちの母親はいつも元気です。","romaji":"Uchi no hahaoya wa itsumo genki desu.","german":"My mother is always full of energy.","furigana":"ははおや|げんき"},{"japanese":"母親の手料理が恋しいです。","romaji":"Hahaoya no teryōri ga koishii desu.","german":"I miss my mother's home cooking.","furigana":"ははおや|てりょうり|こい"},{"japanese":"その子は母親の手をしっかり握っていました。","romaji":"Sono ko wa hahaoya no te o shikkari nigitte imashita.","german":"The child was gripping her mother's hand tightly.","furigana":"こ|ははおや|て|にぎ"}],"notes":"母親 is the matter-of-fact, neutral word for \"mother\" as the female parent, the counterpart of 父親. It is used descriptively, e.g. 母親の手料理 or 母親として. Politely about one's own mother one says 母, about other people's mothers お母さん; she is addressed as お母さん. うちの母親 sounds casual and colloquial and is common among friends.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この曲、ビートルズの中で三番目くらいにすき。","romaji":"Kono kyoku, bītoruzu no naka de sanbanme kurai ni suki.","german":"I probably like this song third best among the Beatles songs.","furigana":"きょく|なか|さんばんめ"},{"japanese":"三番目の駅で降りてください。","romaji":"Sanbanme no eki de orite kudasai.","german":"Please get off at the third station.","furigana":"さんばんめ|えき|お"},{"japanese":"私は三人兄弟の三番目です。","romaji":"Watashi wa sannin kyoudai no sanbanme desu.","german":"I am the third of three brothers.","furigana":"わたし|さんにんきょうだい|さんばんめ"}],"notes":"With the suffix 番目, numbers become ordinal numbers: 一番目, 二番目, 三番目 \"the third\". Before a noun, の is used: 三番目の駅; the counting direction is given with から: 右から三番目. 三番 alone, on the other hand, denotes a number or rank (\"number three\", 三番線 \"platform 3\"). For things you also hear 三つ目, and in formal written language 第三.","levelNote":"Compound: 番目 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの人の話し方には本当にイライラするのよね。","romaji":"Ano hito no hanashikata ni wa hontou ni iraira suru no yo ne.","german":"His way of talking really gets on my nerves.","furigana":"ひと|はな|かた|ほんとう"},{"japanese":"彼の話し方はわかりやすいです。","romaji":"Kare no hanashikata wa wakariyasui desu.","german":"His way of speaking is easy to understand.","furigana":"かれ|はな|かた"},{"japanese":"面接の前に、話し方を練習しました。","romaji":"Mensetsu no mae ni, hanashikata o renshuu shimashita.","german":"I practiced how I speak before the interview.","furigana":"めんせつ|まえ|はな|かた|れんしゅう"}],"notes":"話し方 is the way someone speaks: tempo, tone, word choice and politeness. It follows the pattern verb stem + 方 \"way of doing\", like 書き方 or 読み方. Typical: 話し方が丁寧だ, 話し方が速い, 話し方を練習する. 言い方 refers more narrowly to the phrasing of a specific utterance (その言い方はひどい), whereas 話し方 refers more to a person's general speaking style.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これで終わりだ。","romaji":"Kore de owari da.","german":"That's all.","furigana":"お"},{"japanese":"映画の終わりが感動的でした。","romaji":"Eiga no owari ga kandōteki deshita.","german":"The ending of the movie was moving.","furigana":"えいが|お|かんどうてき"},{"japanese":"今月の終わりに引っ越します。","romaji":"Kongetsu no owari ni hikkoshimasu.","german":"I'm moving at the end of this month.","furigana":"こんげつ|お|ひ|こ"}],"notes":"終わり is the noun for 終わる and means \"end, finish\". With の it gives the point in time: 今月の終わりに \"at the end of this month\", 映画の終わり. これで終わりです or 今日はこれで終わりにしましょう end a class or a meeting. The opposite is 始まり or 初め. Casually you also say おしまい; as the closing word of a film, often just 終 appears.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 20"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このお店は何時に開きますか。","romaji":"Kono omise wa nanji ni akimasu ka.","german":"What time does this shop open?","furigana":"みせ|なんじ|あ"},{"japanese":"ドアが開きません。","romaji":"Doa ga akimasen.","german":"The door won't open.","furigana":"あ"},{"japanese":"この席は空いていますか。","romaji":"Kono seki wa aite imasu ka.","german":"Is this seat free?","furigana":"せき|あ"},{"japanese":"午後なら時間が空いています。","romaji":"Gogo nara jikan ga aite imasu.","german":"I have time in the afternoon.","furigana":"ごご|じかん|あ"}],"notes":"開く is read あく here: a door opens or a shop opens. For an empty or free seat as well as available time, the spelling 空く with the same reading is used. 空いています describes the state of being free or available. The intransitive godan verb forms あきます and あいて; what opens or becomes free takes が. 空く with the reading すく, by contrast, often describes less crowding or hunger.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 18"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"まさかこんなことが起こるなんて、思ってもみなかったよ。","romaji":"Masaka konna koto ga okoru nante, omotte mo minakatta yo.","german":"I never thought something like this would happen.","furigana":"お|おも"},{"japanese":"何が起こったのですか。","romaji":"Nani ga okotta no desu ka.","german":"What happened?","furigana":"なに|お"},{"japanese":"地震が起こったら、机の下に入ってください。","romaji":"Jishin ga okottara, tsukue no shita ni haitte kudasai.","german":"If an earthquake occurs, please get under a desk.","furigana":"じしん|お|つくえ|した|はい"}],"notes":"起こる is intransitive: an event \"happens, occurs\" - 事故が起こる, 地震が起こる, 問題が起こる. The transitive counterpart is 起こす \"to cause\" (事故を起こす). In spoken Japanese, 起きる is just as common in this meaning (事故が起きる). Caution: the identically read 怒る means \"to get angry, to scold\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この手紙で母の気持ちは伝わりました。","romaji":"Kono tegami de haha no kimochi wa tsutawarimashita.","german":"My mother's feelings came across through this letter.","furigana":"てがみ|はは|きも|つた"},{"japanese":"そのニュースはすぐに町中に伝わりました。","romaji":"Sono nyuusu wa sugu ni machijuu ni tsutawarimashita.","german":"The news spread quickly throughout the town.","furigana":"まちじゅう|つた"},{"japanese":"これはこの村に昔から伝わる話です。","romaji":"Kore wa kono mura ni mukashi kara tsutawaru hanashi desu.","german":"This is a story that has been handed down in this village since long ago.","furigana":"むら|むかし|つた|はなし"}],"notes":"伝わる is the intransitive counterpart of 伝える \"to communicate, to convey\": 気持ちを伝える → 気持ちが伝わる \"the feelings get across\". It also means that news or rumors spread (ニュースが伝わる), that customs are handed down (昔から伝わる話) and that something reaches a country (仏教が日本に伝わった). Often in the negative: 言いたいことが伝わらない \"they don't understand what I mean\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"スクリーンにスライドを映してもらえますか？","romaji":"Sukurīn ni suraido o utsushite moraemasu ka?","german":"Could you project the slides onto the screen?","furigana":"うつ"},{"japanese":"出かける前に、鏡に姿を映してチェックします。","romaji":"Dekakeru mae ni, kagami ni sugata o utsushite chekku shimasu.","german":"Before going out, I check my appearance in the mirror.","furigana":"で|まえ|かがみ|すがた|うつ"},{"japanese":"静かな湖が青い空を映していました。","romaji":"Shizuka na mizuumi ga aoi sora o utsushite imashita.","german":"The quiet lake reflected the blue sky.","furigana":"しず|みずうみ|あお|そら|うつ"}],"notes":"映す is transitive and means \"to cast an image onto something\": X を スクリーン/テレビ に映す \"to project, to display\" or X を鏡に映す \"to look at in the mirror\"; a water surface also \"reflects\" something. The intransitive counterpart is 映る \"to be reflected, to appear\" (鏡に顔が映る). Not to be confused with 写す \"to copy, to photograph\" and 移す \"to move, to transfer\", which are read the same way.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ヨーグルトは食べ物の消化を助けてくれます。","romaji":"Yōguruto wa tabemono no shōka o tasukete kuremasu.","german":"Yogurt helps with the digestion of food.","furigana":"た|もの|しょうか|たす"},{"japanese":"食べ物の消化には時間がかかります。","romaji":"Tabemono no shōka ni wa jikan ga kakarimasu.","german":"Digesting food takes time.","furigana":"た|もの|しょうか|じかん"},{"japanese":"一度にたくさん習っても、消化できません。","romaji":"Ichido ni takusan naratte mo, shouka dekimasen.","german":"Even if you learn a lot at once, you can't absorb it.","furigana":"いちど|なら|しょうか"}],"notes":"消化 is \"digestion\"; as a transitive する-verb: 食べ物を消化する. Common are 消化がいい/悪い \"easy/hard to digest\". Figuratively it means to \"absorb\" knowledge (習ったことを消化する), or to \"use up, work through\" something (休みを消化する, 予定を消化する). Do not confuse it with the identically read 消火 \"extinguishing a fire\" (消火器 \"fire extinguisher\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ボタンが取れてしまいました。","romaji":"Botan ga torete shimaimashita.","german":"The button came off.","furigana":"と"},{"japanese":"このしみは洗ってもなかなか取れません。","romaji":"Kono shimi wa aratte mo nakanaka toremasen.","german":"This stain won't come out even after washing.","furigana":"あら|と"},{"japanese":"やっと夏休みが取れました。","romaji":"Yatto natsuyasumi ga toremashita.","german":"I was finally able to take my summer vacation.","furigana":"なつやす|と"}],"notes":"取れる is the intransitive counterpart of 取る. It means \"to come off, to come loose\" (ボタンが取れる), \"to disappear\" for stains, pain or fatigue (しみが取れる, 痛みが取れる) and \"to be harvested/caught\" (then often written 採れる/獲れる). At the same time it is the potential form of 取る: 休みが取れる \"to be able to take time off\", 予約が取れる \"to get a reservation\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼らはみんな、カナダ人なんですよ。","romaji":"Karera wa minna, Kanadajin na n desu yo.","german":"They are all Canadians.","furigana":"かれ|じん"},{"japanese":"彼らは一緒に来ました。","romaji":"Karera wa issho ni kimashita.","german":"They came together.","furigana":"かれ|いっしょ|き"},{"japanese":"彼らとは大学で知り合いました。","romaji":"Karera to wa daigaku de shiriaimashita.","german":"I got to know them at university.","furigana":"かれ|だいがく|し|あ"}],"notes":"彼ら is the plural of 彼 and means \"they\" for a group of men or a mixed group; for all-female groups one says 彼女たち. In spoken Japanese, third-person pronouns tend to be avoided; people use names or say あの人たち. 彼ら therefore sounds somewhat distant and literary; it is not used for superiors or customers. The ending ら is less polite than たち.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「あ、あそこに誰かいる。」「え、誰もいないよ？」","romaji":"“A, asoko ni dareka iru.” “E, dare mo inai yo?”","german":"\"Oh, there's someone over there.\" \"Huh, there's no one there, is there?\"","furigana":"だれ|だれ"},{"japanese":"誰か助けてください。","romaji":"Dareka tasukete kudasai.","german":"Can someone help me?","furigana":"だれ|たす"}],"notes":"誰か is an indefinite pronoun for \"someone\". 誰かいる states that someone is there; 誰か助けてください calls for help from any person. 誰もいない, by contrast, means \"no one is there\", with the negative form いない. The か here belongs to the expression and is not a sentence-final question particle.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 31"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「何階ですか？」「四階をおねがいします。」「このエレベーターは四階には止まりませんよ。」","romaji":"Nangai desu ka? Yonkai o onegai shimasu. Kono erebeetaa wa yonkai ni wa tomarimasen yo.","german":"\"Which floor?\" \"The fourth, please.\" \"This elevator doesn't stop at the fourth floor.\"","furigana":"なんがい|よんかい|よんかい|と"},{"japanese":"二階に上がってください。","romaji":"Nikai ni agatte kudasai.","german":"Please go up to the second floor.","furigana":"にかい|あ"},{"japanese":"トイレは三階にあります。","romaji":"Toire wa sangai ni arimasu.","german":"The toilets are on the third floor.","furigana":"さんがい"}],"notes":"階 is the counter for floors of a building. Japanese counts the ground floor as 一階, so 二階 is the \"first floor\" in the British sense (second floor in the American sense); basement floors are 地下一階 etc. Sound changes: いっかい, にかい, さんがい, よんかい, ろっかい, はっかい, じゅっかい; 何階 is read なんがい or なんかい. Do not confuse it with the counter 回 \"-times\", which is also read かい.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"まずは図書館の会員になったらどうですか？","romaji":"Mazu wa toshokan no kaiin ni nattara dou desu ka?","german":"Why not become a library member first?","furigana":"としょかん|かいいん"},{"japanese":"会員カードをお持ちですか。","romaji":"Kaiin kaado o omochi desu ka.","german":"Do you have a membership card?","furigana":"かいいん|も"},{"japanese":"このジムは会員しか使えません。","romaji":"Kono jimu wa kaiin shika tsukaemasen.","german":"Only members can use this gym.","furigana":"かいいん|つか"}],"notes":"会員 is a \"member\" of a society, club, gym or a service with registration. Typical are 会員になる \"to become a member\", 会員カード, 会員登録 \"registration\" (e.g. on websites) and 会員制 \"members only\". For members of a band or team, メンバー is more common, for school clubs 部員, for company employees 社員.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 43"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は昔この船の船員でした。","romaji":"Watashi wa mukashi kono fune no sen'in deshita.","german":"I used to be a sailor on this ship.","furigana":"わたし|むかし|ふね|せんいん"},{"japanese":"船員として世界を回りました。","romaji":"Sen'in to shite sekai o mawarimashita.","german":"I traveled around the world as a sailor.","furigana":"せんいん|せかい|まわ"},{"japanese":"船員は長い間、家族に会えません。","romaji":"Sen'in wa nagai aida, kazoku ni aemasen.","german":"Sailors cannot see their families for a long time.","furigana":"せんいん|なが|あいだ|かぞく|あ"}],"notes":"船員 is the general term for a crew member of a ship, that is, a \"sailor\". The captain is 船長; the entire crew (also on aircraft) is 乗組員. Colloquial and a bit more romantic is 船乗り \"seafarer\". Navy soldiers, by contrast, are 水兵. The word is a noun: 船員として働く, 船員になる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"落ちのある話が好きなんです。","romaji":"Ochi no aru hanashi ga suki na n desu.","german":"I like stories with a punchline.","furigana":"お|はなし|す"},{"japanese":"この話の落ちが面白いです。","romaji":"Kono hanashi no ochi ga omoshiroi desu.","german":"The punchline of this story is funny.","furigana":"はなし|お|おもしろ"},{"japanese":"今から行っても、遅れるのが落ちだよ。","romaji":"Ima kara itte mo, okureru no ga ochi da yo.","german":"Even if we leave now, all we'll end up doing is being late.","furigana":"いま|い|おく|お"}],"notes":"落ち here means the \"punchline\" of a joke or story and is usually written in katakana as オチ. It comes from the storytelling art 落語; オチのない話 \"a story without a punchline\" is a typical criticism. It also means \"outcome, the end of the story\": ～のが落ちだ \"all that comes of it is ...\" (mostly negative). Not to be confused with the verb 落ちる \"to fall\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は毎日同じ時間に同じ行動をします。","romaji":"Kare wa mainichi onaji jikan ni onaji kōdō o shimasu.","german":"Er tut jeden Tag zur selben Zeit genau dasselbe.","furigana":"かれ|まいにちおな|じかん|おな|こうどう"},{"japanese":"すぐに行動しましょう。","romaji":"Sugu ni kōdō shimashō.","german":"Lass uns sofort handeln.","furigana":"こうどう"},{"japanese":"地震のときは、落ち着いて行動してください。","romaji":"Jishin no toki wa, ochitsuite koudou shite kudasai.","german":"Bewahren Sie bei einem Erdbeben Ruhe und handeln Sie besonnen.","furigana":"じしん|お|つ|こうどう"}],"notes":"行動 bedeutet „Handeln, Verhalten“ und ist als する-Verb intransitiv: 行動する „handeln, sich verhalten“, etwa 落ち着いて行動する. Typisch sind 行動を起こす „aktiv werden“ und 行動が早い „schnell handeln“. 活動 meint dagegen eine organisierte Tätigkeit (クラブ活動), 動作 eine einzelne körperliche Bewegung, 行為 eine bestimmte Tat in förmlicher Sprache. Das deutsche „Handlung“ im Sinn von „Romanhandlung“ ist nicht gemeint.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日は研究室の集まりがある。","romaji":"Kyō wa kenkyūshitsu no atsumari ga aru.","german":"Today there is a gathering of people from the lab.","furigana":"きょう|けんきゅうしつ|あつ"},{"japanese":"友達の集まりに参加しました。","romaji":"Tomodachi no atsumari ni sanka shimashita.","german":"I took part in a gathering of friends.","furigana":"ともだち|あつ|さんか"},{"japanese":"雨のせいで、今日は客の集まりが悪いです。","romaji":"Ame no sei de, kyou wa kyaku no atsumari ga warui desu.","german":"Because of the rain, few customers are coming today.","furigana":"あめ|きょう|きゃく|あつ|わる"}],"notes":"集まり is the noun of 集まる and refers to a rather informal \"gathering, get-together\": 友達の集まり, 家族の集まり, 研究室の集まり. A formal meeting is a 会議, a larger assembly a 集会. 集まりがいい/悪い describes the \"turnout\", i.e. whether many or few people come. Typical: 集まりがある, 集まりに参加する/出る.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地下道でヤクザの親分とすれちがったんだ。","romaji":"Chikadō de yakuza no oyabun to surechigatta n da.","german":"I passed a yakuza boss in the underpass.","furigana":"ちかどう|おやぶん"},{"japanese":"親分の命令に従います。","romaji":"Oyabun no meirei ni shitagaimasu.","german":"I follow the boss's orders.","furigana":"おやぶん|めいれい|したが"},{"japanese":"兄は子どものころ、近所の子どもたちの親分でした。","romaji":"Ani wa kodomo no koro, kinjo no kodomotachi no oyabun deshita.","german":"When he was a child, my older brother was the leader of the neighborhood kids.","furigana":"あに|こ|きんじょ|こ|おやぶん"}],"notes":"親分 is the \"boss, leader\" of a group, above all a yakuza gang or traditional craftsmen; the followers are 子分. Casually, the leader of a gang of kids can also be called this. For the boss in a company, one does not say 親分 but 上司 or 社長. 親分肌 describes someone with a natural, caring leadership style. Similar is 親方 (master in a craft or sumo).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今の気持ちを顔文字で表してください。","romaji":"Ima no kimochi o kaomoji de arawashite kudasai.","german":"Please express your current feelings with kaomoji.","furigana":"いま|きも|かおもじ|あらわ"},{"japanese":"メッセージに顔文字を使います。","romaji":"Messēji ni kaomoji o tsukaimasu.","german":"I use kaomoji in messages.","furigana":"かおもじ|つか"},{"japanese":"仕事のメールで顔文字を使うのはやめたほうがいいですよ。","romaji":"Shigoto no meeru de kaomoji o tsukau no wa yameta hou ga ii desu yo.","german":"You should avoid using kaomoji in business emails.","furigana":"しごと|かおもじ|つか"}],"notes":"顔文字 are faces built from characters such as (^_^) or (T_T); unlike Western emoticons, they are upright and are not read sideways. Literally the word means \"face characters\" (顔 + 文字). To be distinguished from them are 絵文字 \"emoji\" (pictograms) and スタンプ \"stickers\" in chat apps. They are used in casual messages, rather not in business emails.","levelNote":"Compound: 顔 (N5) + 文字 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"メディア部の部室には、たくさんのCDがあります。","romaji":"Media-bu no bushitsu ni wa, takusan no shī dī ga arimasu.","german":"There are lots of CDs in the media club's clubroom.","furigana":"ぶ|ぶしつ"},{"japanese":"放課後に部室で練習します。","romaji":"Hōkago ni bushitsu de renshū shimasu.","german":"We practice in the clubroom after school.","furigana":"ほうかご|ぶしつ|れんしゅう"}],"notes":"部室 is the room of a school or university club. 部室に describes what is located there; 部室で what one does there. The word means the clubroom and not the club itself, which is expressed by 部 or クラブ, for example.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この図書館は大学院生に人気です。","romaji":"Kono toshokan wa daigakuinsei ni ninki desu.","german":"This library is popular with graduate students.","furigana":"としょかん|だいがくいんせい|にんき"},{"japanese":"大学院生として研究しています。","romaji":"Daigakuinsei to shite kenkyū shite imasu.","german":"I do research as a graduate student.","furigana":"だいがくいんせい|けんきゅう"},{"japanese":"大学を卒業したあと、大学院生になりました。","romaji":"Daigaku o sotsugyou shita ato, daigakuinsei ni narimashita.","german":"After graduating from university, I became a graduate student.","furigana":"だいがく|そつぎょう|だいがくいんせい"}],"notes":"大学院生 is someone studying at a 大学院 (\"graduate school\") - this includes master's (修士) and doctoral (博士) students. In short, 院生 is often used. Undergraduate students are called 大学生 or 学部生. Typical: 大学院生として研究する, 大学院生になる; moving on after the bachelor's degree is 大学院に進む.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私のガールフレンドは、週末はコスプレイヤーとして活動しています。","romaji":"Watashi no gaarufurendo wa, shuumatsu wa kosupureiyaa to shite katsudou shite imasu.","german":"My girlfriend is active as a cosplayer on weekends.","furigana":"わたし|しゅうまつ|かつどう"},{"japanese":"ボランティア活動に参加しています。","romaji":"Borantia katsudō ni sanka shite imasu.","german":"I take part in volunteer activities.","furigana":"かつどう|さんか"},{"japanese":"この火山は今も活動しています。","romaji":"Kono kazan wa ima mo katsudou shite imasu.","german":"This volcano is still active.","furigana":"かざん|いま|かつどう"}],"notes":"活動 is an \"activity\" (usually organized or regular); as a する-verb it is intransitive: 活動する \"to be active\". Typical compounds are ボランティア活動, クラブ活動 or 部活動 (short: 部活) and 就職活動 (short: 就活, \"job hunting\"). It also applies to organizations, animals and volcanoes. 行動, by contrast, means the actions or behavior of an individual, and 運動 physical exercise or sport.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 49"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日は祭日だからいつものカフェがお休みだったよ。","romaji":"Kyō wa saijitsu da kara itsumo no kafe ga oyasumi datta yo.","german":"Today is a holiday, so my usual cafe was closed.","furigana":"きょう|さいじつ|やす"},{"japanese":"祭日は銀行が休みです。","romaji":"Saijitsu wa ginkō ga yasumi desu.","german":"Banks are closed on public holidays.","furigana":"さいじつ|ぎんこう|やす"}],"notes":"祭日 denotes a festival day or public holiday. As a noun it can be used with です or だ; 祭日の休み links it to another noun via の. In the examples, the holiday explains why shops or banks are closed. The official Japanese national holidays are called 国民の祝日.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その会社員は、いつも着々と仕事をこなしていた。","romaji":"Sono kaishain wa, itsumo chakuchaku to shigoto o konashite ita.","german":"That office worker always got his work done steadily.","furigana":"かいしゃいん|ちゃくちゃく|しごと"},{"japanese":"計画が着々と進んでいます。","romaji":"Keikaku ga chakuchaku to susunde imasu.","german":"The plan is progressing steadily.","furigana":"けいかく|ちゃくちゃく|すす"},{"japanese":"来月の試合のために、着々と準備をしています。","romaji":"Raigetsu no shiai no tame ni, chakuchaku to junbi o shite imasu.","german":"I'm preparing step by step for next month's match.","furigana":"らいげつ|しあい|ちゃくちゃく|じゅんび"}],"notes":"着々 almost always appears as 着々と and describes something moving toward a goal step by step and according to plan - typically with 進む: 準備が着々と進む, 工事が着々と進む. It sounds rather objective and is common in news and reports. だんだん, by contrast, means a gradual change, and どんどん rapid, vigorous progress. The mark 々 repeats the preceding kanji.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これ以上、私を苦しめないで！","romaji":"Kore ijō, watashi o kurushimenaide!","german":"Don't torment me any more!","furigana":"いじょう|わたし|くる"},{"japanese":"病気が彼を苦しめています。","romaji":"Byōki ga kare o kurushimete imasu.","german":"The illness is making him suffer.","furigana":"びょうき|かれ|くる"},{"japanese":"動物を苦しめるのはやめてください。","romaji":"Doubutsu o kurushimeru no wa yamete kudasai.","german":"Please stop tormenting the animals.","furigana":"どうぶつ|くる"}],"notes":"苦しめる is transitive: X を苦しめる \"to torment someone, make someone suffer\". The subject is often a cause such as illness, heat or a problem: 病気が彼を苦しめる. The intransitive counterpart is 苦しむ \"to suffer\" (病気に苦しむ), which is related to the adjective 苦しい. For deliberately bullying people, for example at school, いじめる is used instead. The word sounds serious and emotional.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"では、解答を見てみましょう。","romaji":"Dewa, kaitou o mite mimashou.","german":"Well then, let's look at the answers.","furigana":"かいとう|み"},{"japanese":"テストの解答を確認しました。","romaji":"Tesuto no kaitō o kakunin shimashita.","german":"I checked the answers of the test.","furigana":"かいとう|かくにん"},{"japanese":"次の質問に日本語で解答してください。","romaji":"Tsugi no shitsumon ni nihongo de kaitou shite kudasai.","german":"Please answer the following questions in Japanese.","furigana":"つぎ|しつもん|にほんご|かいとう"}],"notes":"解答 is the \"solution, answer\" to an exam or exercise question: 解答用紙 \"answer sheet\", 解答欄 \"answer box\", 問題に解答する. Note: the identically read 回答 is the \"reply\" to a survey, inquiry or complaint. In everyday speech people simply say 答え or 答える; 解答 is more academic and literary.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"なんでグラスで飲み物を飲む時に小指を立てるの？","romaji":"Nande gurasu de nomimono o nomu toki ni koyubi o tateru no?","german":"Why do you stick out your little finger when you drink from a glass?","furigana":"の|もの|の|とき|こゆび|た"},{"japanese":"小指をぶつけて痛いです。","romaji":"Koyubi o butsukete itai desu.","german":"I bumped my little finger and it hurts.","furigana":"こゆび|いた"},{"japanese":"子どもと小指を出して指切りをしました。","romaji":"Kodomo to koyubi o dashite yubikiri o shimashita.","german":"I made a pinky promise with the child.","furigana":"こ|こゆび|だ|ゆびき"}],"notes":"小指 is the little finger; on the foot, more precisely 足の小指 \"little toe\". The other fingers are 親指, 人差し指, 中指 and 薬指. In the childhood promise 指切り, the little fingers are hooked together. 小指を立てる (sticking out the little finger) used to be a gesture for \"girlfriend, lover\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"バターを入れて、うどんを洋風にアレンジしました。","romaji":"Batā o irete, udon o yōfū ni arenji shimashita.","german":"I added butter and gave the udon a Western twist.","furigana":"い|ようふう"},{"japanese":"洋風の建物が好きです。","romaji":"Yōfū no tatemono ga suki desu.","german":"I like Western-style buildings.","furigana":"ようふう|たてもの|す"},{"japanese":"朝ごはんは和風と洋風、どちらがいいですか。","romaji":"Asagohan wa wafuu to youfuu, dochira ga ii desu ka.","german":"Which would you prefer for breakfast, Japanese or Western?","furigana":"あさ|わふう|ようふう"}],"notes":"洋風 means \"in the Western style\" and is the counterpart of 和風 \"in the Japanese style\". It usually takes の before a noun (洋風の建物, 洋風の朝ごはん); 洋風な is also possible; 洋風にする/アレンジする means \"to give a Western twist\". Related are 洋食 \"Western food\" and 洋室 \"Western-style room\" (as opposed to 和室).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"さとみちゃんとは、仲良くなれそう？","romaji":"Satomi-chan to wa, nakayoku naresou?","german":"Do you think you'll get along with Satomi?","furigana":"なかよ"},{"japanese":"みんなで仲良くしましょう。","romaji":"Minna de nakayoku shimashō.","german":"Let's all get along.","furigana":"なかよ"},{"japanese":"けんかしないで、仲良く遊びなさい。","romaji":"Kenka shinaide, nakayoku asobinasai.","german":"Don't fight; play nicely together.","furigana":"なかよ|あそ"}],"notes":"仲良く is the adverbial form of 仲がいい / 仲良い and means \"on good terms, harmoniously\". Very common are 仲良くする \"to get along well\" and 仲良くなる \"to become friends\", each with と: 友達と仲良くする. It also stands directly before verbs: 仲良く遊ぶ, 仲良く暮らす. The noun 仲良し means \"good friends, best friend\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今、成田空港に着きました。","romaji":"Ima, Narita kūkō ni tsukimashita.","german":"I just arrived at Narita Airport.","furigana":"いま|なりたくうこう|つ"},{"japanese":"東京に何時に着きますか。","romaji":"Tōkyō ni nanji ni tsukimasu ka.","german":"What time will we arrive in Tokyo?","furigana":"とうきょう|なんじ|つ"}],"notes":"着く means \"to arrive\" and is an intransitive godan verb: 着きます, 着いて. With に you name the destination of arrival. 着きました reports that you have already arrived; 何時に着きますか asks for the arrival time. It differs from 着る, \"to put on (clothes)\", with a different reading and conjugation.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 15"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の彼氏は酒好きです。","romaji":"Watashi no kareshi wa sakezuki desu.","german":"My boyfriend likes to drink.","furigana":"わたし|かれし|さけず"},{"japanese":"彼は酒好きで有名です。","romaji":"Kare wa sakezuki de yūmei desu.","german":"He is known for liking alcohol.","furigana":"かれ|さけず|ゆうめい"},{"japanese":"酒好きの父のために、日本酒をプレゼントしました。","romaji":"Sakezuki no chichi no tame ni, nihonshu o purezento shimashita.","german":"I gave sake to my father, who likes to drink.","furigana":"さけず|ちち|にほんしゅ"}],"notes":"酒好き describes someone who likes to drink alcohol; in compounds 好き becomes ずき (本好き, 犬好き). It is used as a noun or な-adjective: 酒好きの人, 彼は酒好きだ, intensified 大の酒好き. Compared with 酒飲み (\"a drinker who drinks a lot\"), it emphasizes the fondness more and sounds friendlier. How much someone can handle is expressed with 酒に強い/弱い.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"え、この絵、一億円もするんですか？","romaji":"E, kono e, ichiokuen mo suru n desu ka?","german":"What, does this painting really cost a hundred million yen?","furigana":"え|いちおくえん"},{"japanese":"宝くじで一億円当たりました。","romaji":"Takarakuji de ichiokuen atarimashita.","german":"I won a hundred million yen in the lottery.","furigana":"たから|いちおくえんあ"},{"japanese":"もし一億円あったら、何をしますか。","romaji":"Moshi ichiokuen attara, nani o shimasu ka.","german":"What would you do if you had a hundred million yen?","furigana":"いちおくえん|なに"}],"notes":"一億円 is 100,000,000 yen. Japanese counts large numbers in steps of four digits: 万 (10,000) and 億 (100,000,000); so \"one million\" is 百万 and \"one billion\" is 十億. As with 万, 一 must stand before 億: いちおく, never just おく. 一億円 is proverbial as a huge sum, for example for a lottery win (宝くじで一億円当たる). With も one stresses the size: 一億円もする.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コウイチが本当に肉を食べられないかは疑問です。","romaji":"Kouichi ga hontou ni niku o taberarenai ka wa gimon desu.","german":"It is doubtful whether Koichi really can't eat meat.","furigana":"ほんとう|にく|た|ぎもん"},{"japanese":"この結果に疑問を持っています。","romaji":"Kono kekka ni gimon o motte imasu.","german":"I have doubts about this result.","furigana":"けっか|ぎもん|も"},{"japanese":"何か疑問があれば、いつでも聞いてください。","romaji":"Nanika gimon ga areba, itsudemo kiite kudasai.","german":"If you have any questions, please ask at any time.","furigana":"なに|ぎもん|き"}],"notes":"疑問 is a doubt or an open question that one asks oneself: 疑問を持つ, 疑問に思う \"to wonder, to doubt\", ～かは疑問だ \"it is questionable whether ...\". 何か疑問があれば means \"if there are any questions\". 質問, by contrast, is a question that one asks someone (質問する), and 問題 is a task or a problem. Grammar: 疑問文 \"interrogative sentence\", 疑問詞 \"question word\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この器は母のです。","romaji":"Kono utsuwa wa haha no desu.","german":"This bowl is my mother's.","furigana":"うつわ|はは"},{"japanese":"美しい器に料理を盛りました。","romaji":"Utsukushii utsuwa ni ryōri o morimashita.","german":"I served the food in a beautiful vessel.","furigana":"うつく|うつわ|りょうり|も"},{"japanese":"彼は社長の器ではありません。","romaji":"Kare wa shachou no utsuwa de wa arimasen.","german":"He doesn't have what it takes to be company president.","furigana":"かれ|しゃちょう|うつわ"}],"notes":"器 is a vessel, especially tableware such as bowls and plates, often with an aesthetic undertone (器にこだわる \"to care about beautiful tableware\"). In general, \"tableware\" is 食器, a container for packaging is 容器. Figuratively, 器 means a person's \"caliber\": 器が大きい \"big-hearted\", 社長の器 \"having what it takes to be a boss\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この食器をテーブルの上にならべてくれませんか。","romaji":"Kono shokki o tēburu no ue ni narabete kuremasen ka.","german":"Could you set this tableware out on the table?","furigana":"しょっき|うえ"},{"japanese":"食器を洗ってください。","romaji":"Shokki o aratte kudasai.","german":"Please wash the dishes.","furigana":"しょっき|あら"},{"japanese":"引っ越しのとき、食器を新聞紙で包みました。","romaji":"Hikkoshi no toki, shokki o shinbunshi de tsutsumimashita.","german":"When I moved, I wrapped the dishes in newspaper.","furigana":"ひ|こ|しょっき|しんぶんし|つつ"}],"notes":"食器 is the collective term for dishes and cutlery: plates, bowls, cups, often also chopsticks. Typical are 食器を洗う \"to wash the dishes\", 食器を並べる \"to set the table\", 食器棚 \"dish cabinet\" and 食器洗い機 \"dishwasher\". Individual pieces are called specifically 皿, 茶わん or コップ; 器 sounds more refined and emphasizes the beauty of a single vessel.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の彼氏は力士です。","romaji":"Watashi no kareshi wa rikishi desu.","german":"My boyfriend is a sumo wrestler.","furigana":"わたし|かれし|りきし"},{"japanese":"力士の体はとても大きいです。","romaji":"Rikishi no karada wa totemo ōkii desu.","german":"A sumo wrestler's body is very big.","furigana":"りきし|からだ|おお"},{"japanese":"力士はちゃんこ鍋をよく食べます。","romaji":"Rikishi wa chankonabe o yoku tabemasu.","german":"Sumo wrestlers often eat chanko stew.","furigana":"りきし|なべ|た"}],"notes":"力士 is the official term for a professional sumo wrestler. In everyday speech people affectionately say お相撲さん; the sport itself is 相撲, the highest rank 横綱. Wrestlers in the upper divisions are called 関取. For wrestlers in other combat sports (pro wrestling), レスラー is used.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この新宿のホテルのシャワーは、水かぬるま湯しか出ない。","romaji":"Kono Shinjuku no hoteru no shawā wa, mizu ka nurumayu shika denai.","german":"The shower in this hotel in Shinjuku only gives cold or lukewarm water.","furigana":"しんじゅく|みず|ゆ|で"},{"japanese":"新宿で買い物をしました。","romaji":"Shinjuku de kaimono o shimashita.","german":"I went shopping in Shinjuku.","furigana":"しんじゅく|か|もの"},{"japanese":"新宿駅はとても大きくて、よく迷います。","romaji":"Shinjuku-eki wa totemo ookikute, yoku mayoimasu.","german":"Shinjuku Station is so big that I often get lost.","furigana":"しんじゅくえき|おお|まよ"}],"notes":"新宿 is a ward (新宿区) and a business and entertainment district in Tokyo. 新宿駅 is considered the busiest station in the world; also located there are the Tokyo Metropolitan Government Building (東京都庁), the nightlife district 歌舞伎町 and the park 新宿御苑. Watch the reading: しんじゅく with voiced じゅ, not しゅく. As a place name it takes で/に/へ: 新宿で買い物する, 新宿に行く.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 29"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この映画のこの回想のシーンがすごく好きなんだよね。","romaji":"Kono eiga no kono kaisō no shīn ga sugoku suki na n da yo ne.","german":"I really like this flashback scene in the movie.","furigana":"えいが|かいそう|す"},{"japanese":"昔のことを回想しました。","romaji":"Mukashi no koto o kaisō shimashita.","german":"I looked back on the past.","furigana":"むかし|かいそう"},{"japanese":"祖母は戦争中の生活を回想して、本に書きました。","romaji":"Sobo wa sensouchuu no seikatsu o kaisou shite, hon ni kakimashita.","german":"My grandmother recalled her life during the war and wrote a book about it.","furigana":"そぼ|せんそうちゅう|せいかつ|かいそう|ほん|か"}],"notes":"回想 is the deliberate \"looking back, recalling\" of the past and sounds rather literary; as a する-verb it is transitive: 昔のことを回想する. Common are 回想シーン \"flashback\" in film and manga and 回想録 \"memoirs\". In everyday speech people more often say 思い出す \"to remember\" or 振り返る \"to look back\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"うちの会社の飲み会はとても楽しいですよ！","romaji":"Uchi no kaisha no nomikai wa totemo tanoshii desu yo!","german":"The drinking parties at my company are a lot of fun!","furigana":"かいしゃ|の|かい|たの"},{"japanese":"金曜日に飲み会があります。","romaji":"Kin'yōbi ni nomikai ga arimasu.","german":"We have a drinking party on Friday.","furigana":"きんようび|の|かい"},{"japanese":"飲み会に誘われたけど、断りました。","romaji":"Nomikai ni sasowareta kedo, kotowarimashita.","german":"I was invited to a drinking party, but I declined.","furigana":"の|かい|さそ|ことわ"}],"notes":"飲み会 is a social drinking party among colleagues, fellow students or friends, mostly in a pub (居酒屋). It is an important social ritual and not a binge; people who don't drink alcohol take part anyway. Special forms are 歓迎会 \"welcome party\", 送別会 \"farewell party\" and 忘年会 \"year-end party\"; the second round is 二次会. Typical: 飲み会がある, 飲み会に誘う/参加する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"チラリと見返ると、彼もまだ私のことを見ていました。","romaji":"Chirari to mikaeru to, kare mo mada watashi no koto o mite imashita.","german":"When I glanced back, he was still looking at me.","furigana":"みかえ|かれ|わたし|み"},{"japanese":"歩きながら見返りました。","romaji":"Aruki nagara mikaerimashita.","german":"I looked back while walking.","furigana":"ある|みかえ"},{"japanese":"後ろから名前を呼ばれて見返ると、先生が立っていました。","romaji":"Ushiro kara namae o yobarete mikaeru to, sensei ga tatte imashita.","german":"When someone called my name from behind and I turned around, my teacher was standing there.","furigana":"うし|なまえ|よ|みかえ|せんせい|た"}],"notes":"見返る means \"to turn around and look back\" - literally, over one's shoulder. It sounds somewhat literary; in everyday speech one mostly says 振り返る, which also figuratively means \"to look back on the past\". Well known is the woodblock-print motif 見返り美人. Not to be confused with 見返す \"to look at again; to show someone up\" and the noun 見返り \"return, reward\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"花さんとは、4月の初めに出会いました。","romaji":"Hana-san to wa, shigatsu no hajime ni deaimashita.","german":"I met Hana at the beginning of April.","furigana":"はな|がつ|はじ|であ"},{"japanese":"初めに目標を決めましょう。","romaji":"Hajime ni mokuhyou o kimemashou.","german":"First, let's decide on the goal.","furigana":"はじ|もくひょう|き"},{"japanese":"初めに、自己紹介をさせてください。","romaji":"Hajime ni, jiko shoukai o sasete kudasai.","german":"First, let me introduce myself.","furigana":"はじ|じこしょうかい"}],"notes":"初めに means \"first, at the beginning\" and opens a sequence, often followed by 次に and 最後に. With の before it, it means \"at the beginning of\": 四月の初めに \"at the beginning of April\". Not to be confused with 初めて \"for the first time\". For the start of an action, the spelling 始めに is also found. As a heading (mostly in kana: はじめに) it stands for \"introduction, preface\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 35"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「風の神様」が、神風を起こしたんだよ。","romaji":"\"Kaze no kamisama\" ga, kamikaze o okoshita n da yo.","german":"The \"wind god\" created the divine wind.","furigana":"かぜ|かみさま|かみかぜ|お"},{"japanese":"神風は歴史の授業で学びました。","romaji":"Kamikaze wa rekishi no jugyō de manabimashita.","german":"I learned about the kamikaze in history class.","furigana":"かみかぜ|れきし|じゅぎょう|まな"},{"japanese":"モンゴルの軍が来たとき、大きな台風が吹きました。人々はそれを神風と呼びました。","romaji":"Mongoru no gun ga kita toki, ookina taifuu ga fukimashita. Hitobito wa sore o kamikaze to yobimashita.","german":"When the Mongol army came, a huge typhoon blew. People called it the \"divine wind\".","furigana":"ぐん|き|おお|たいふう|ふ|ひとびと|かみかぜ|よ"}],"notes":"神風 literally means \"divine wind\". Originally it refers to the typhoons that destroyed the Mongol invasion fleets in the 13th century and thus supposedly saved Japan. In the West the word is known mainly through the suicide pilots of World War II; in Japan these are usually called 特攻 or 特攻隊. The word is a noun and rare in everyday life, mainly found in history and literature.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は左利きです。","romaji":"Watashi wa hidarikiki desu.","german":"I am left-handed.","furigana":"わたし|ひだりき"},{"japanese":"左利きの人には、このはさみは使いにくいです。","romaji":"Hidarikiki no hito ni wa, kono hasami wa tsukainikui desu.","german":"These scissors are hard to use for left-handed people.","furigana":"ひだりき|ひと|つか"},{"japanese":"子どものころ、左利きを右利きに直されました。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, hidarikiki o migikiki ni naosaremashita.","german":"As a child, I was trained out of being left-handed into being right-handed.","furigana":"こ|ひだりき|みぎき|なお"}],"notes":"左利き refers both to left-handedness and to a left-handed person; 利き comes from 利く \"to work well\". It is a noun: 私は左利きです, 左利きの人, 左利き用のはさみ. The opposite is 右利き, ambidextrous is 両利き, the dominant hand is 利き手. An older, humorous meaning is \"someone who likes to drink\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"明日は体育があります。","romaji":"Ashita wa taiiku ga arimasu.","german":"We have P.E. tomorrow.","furigana":"あした|たいいく"},{"japanese":"体育の授業で走りました。","romaji":"Taiiku no jugyō de hashirimashita.","german":"We ran in P.E. class.","furigana":"たいいく|じゅぎょう|はし"},{"japanese":"雨の日は体育館でバスケットボールをします。","romaji":"Ame no hi wa taiikukan de basukettobooru o shimasu.","german":"On rainy days we play basketball in the gym.","furigana":"あめ|ひ|たいいくかん"}],"notes":"体育 is the school subject \"sports, physical education\": 体育の授業, 体育の先生, 明日は体育がある. The gym is called 体育館. The holiday 体育の日 was renamed スポーツの日 in 2020. For sports as a leisure activity one says スポーツ, for physical exercise 運動.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の息子は右利きではありません。","romaji":"Watashi no musuko wa migikiki de wa arimasen.","german":"My son is not right-handed.","furigana":"わたし|むすこ|みぎき"},{"japanese":"日本人のほとんどは右利きです。","romaji":"Nihonjin no hotondo wa migikiki desu.","german":"Most Japanese people are right-handed.","furigana":"にほんじん|みぎき"},{"japanese":"その店には右利き用のギターしかありませんでした。","romaji":"Sono mise ni wa migikikiyou no gitaa shika arimasen deshita.","german":"That shop only had guitars for right-handed players.","furigana":"みせ|みぎき|よう"}],"notes":"右利き means right-handedness or a right-handed person and is a noun: 右利きです, 右利きの人, 右利き用 \"for right-handers\". The opposite is 左利き, ambidextrous is 両利き, the preferred hand 利き手. 利き comes from 利く \"to work well\"; it is always written with kanji.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あと五分くらいで追い付くと思う。","romaji":"Ato gofun kurai de oitsuku to omou.","german":"I think I'll catch up with you in about five minutes.","furigana":"ごふん|お|つ|おも"},{"japanese":"走って友達に追い付きました。","romaji":"Hashitte tomodachi ni oitsukimashita.","german":"I ran and caught up with my friend.","furigana":"はし|ともだち|お|つ"},{"japanese":"勉強をがんばって、クラスのみんなに追い付きたいです。","romaji":"Benkyou o ganbatte, kurasu no minna ni oitsukitai desu.","german":"I want to study hard and catch up with everyone in my class.","furigana":"べんきょう|お|つ"}],"notes":"追い付く is intransitive; the person caught up with takes に: 友達に追い付く. It is also used figuratively, for example for level of study or performance (クラスのみんなに追い付く), and in the negative for \"can't keep up\" (生産が追い付かない). 追い越す, by contrast, means \"to overtake\" (with を), and 追いかける \"to chase, to run after\". It is often also written 追いつく.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一人で戦うつもりなの？","romaji":"Hitori de tatakau tsumori na no?","german":"Do you intend to fight alone?","furigana":"ひとり|たたか"},{"japanese":"最後まで戦いましょう。","romaji":"Saigo made tatakaimashō.","german":"Let's fight to the end.","furigana":"さいご|たたか"},{"japanese":"祖父は長い間、病気と戦っています。","romaji":"Sofu wa nagai aida, byouki to tatakatte imasu.","german":"My grandfather has been fighting his illness for a long time.","furigana":"そふ|なが|あいだ|びょうき|たたか"}],"notes":"戦う is intransitive: the opponent is marked with と (敵と戦う, 強いチームと戦う), not with を. Besides war and combat, it also means \"to compete against someone\" in sports and \"to struggle against\" difficulties (病気と戦う). For the struggle with illness or poverty, the spelling 闘う is often seen; the noun is 戦い \"fight\". For a personal quarrel among friends, one says けんかする instead.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あなたが大好きです。","romaji":"Anata ga daisuki desu.","german":"I love you.","furigana":"だいす"},{"japanese":"チョコレートが大好きです。","romaji":"Chokorēto ga daisuki desu.","german":"I love chocolate.","furigana":"だいす"}],"notes":"大好き expresses strong affection or a great liking. The object is named with が: あなたが大好き, チョコレートが大好き. It behaves like a na-adjective: 大好きな食べ物. With people it can mean \"to be very fond of\"; with things, \"to like very much\". It is not a transitive verb with を.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"風船が女の子の手から放れた。","romaji":"Fūsen ga onnanoko no te kara hanareta.","german":"The balloon slipped out of the girl's hand.","furigana":"ふうせん|おんな|こ|て|はな"},{"japanese":"鳥がかごから放れました。","romaji":"Tori ga kago kara hanaremashita.","german":"The bird got out of the cage.","furigana":"とり|はな"},{"japanese":"散歩中に犬がひもから放れてしまいました。","romaji":"Sanpochuu ni inu ga himo kara hanarete shimaimashita.","german":"During the walk, the dog broke loose from its leash.","furigana":"さんぽちゅう|いぬ|はな"}],"notes":"放れる is a rare spelling of はなれる and emphasizes \"tearing oneself loose, getting free\" from a grip or a restraint: 手から放れる, ひもから放れる. In everyday writing, 離れる (\"to move away, to be separated\") is used almost always, and for animals that escape, 逃げる is the usual word. The transitive counterpart is 放す \"to let go, to release\" (鳥を放す), which is encountered far more often.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"入館料はいくらですか？","romaji":"Nyūkanryō wa ikura desu ka?","german":"How much is the admission fee?","furigana":"にゅうかんりょう"},{"japanese":"美術館の入館料は大人千円です。","romaji":"Bijutsukan no nyuukanryou wa otona sen'en desu.","german":"Admission to the art museum is 1,000 yen for adults.","furigana":"びじゅつかん|にゅうかんりょう|おとなせんえん"},{"japanese":"毎月第一日曜日は入館料が無料になります。","romaji":"Maitsuki daiichi nichiyoubi wa nyuukanryou ga muryou ni narimasu.","german":"On the first Sunday of every month, admission is free.","furigana":"まいつきだいいちにちようび|にゅうかんりょう|むりょう"}],"notes":"入館料 is the admission fee for a building such as a museum, library, aquarium or art gallery; 館 means \"building, hall\". For events, stadiums or parks, 入場料 is used, for zoos and gardens 入園料. Typical are 入館料を払う, 入館料がかかる and 入館料は無料です; on signs it often appears with price tiers such as 大人・子ども.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本はガス料金が高いですか。","romaji":"Nihon wa gasu ryoukin ga takai desu ka.","german":"Are gas prices high in Japan?","furigana":"にほん|りょうきん|たか"},{"japanese":"駐車場の料金を払いました。","romaji":"Chūshajō no ryōkin o haraimashita.","german":"I paid the parking fee.","furigana":"ちゅうしゃじょう|りょうきん|はら"},{"japanese":"タクシーの料金は夜になると高くなります。","romaji":"Takushii no ryoukin wa yoru ni naru to takaku narimasu.","german":"Taxi fares go up at night.","furigana":"りょうきん|よる|たか"}],"notes":"料金 is the charge for a service or use: 電気料金, 電話料金, タクシーの料金, 駐車料金. For the price of goods, one says 値段 instead; colloquially, 料金 is often replaced by the suffix 〜代 (電気代, ガス代). Typical combinations are 料金を払う, 料金が高い/安い and 料金が上がる; 運賃 is specifically the fare, 料金所 the toll booth.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あ、新しい映画館ができてる。","romaji":"A, atarashii eigakan ga dekiteru.","german":"Oh, a new movie theater has been built.","furigana":"あたら|えいがかん"},{"japanese":"映画館で新しい映画を見ました。","romaji":"Eigakan de atarashii eiga o mimashita.","german":"I saw a new movie at the movie theater.","furigana":"えいがかん|あたら|えいが|み"}],"notes":"映画館 is the movie theater as a building or place, 映画 the film. 映画館で names the place where a film is seen. できてる is the spoken short form of できている and here describes a newly built movie theater. It is about the finished state, not a personal ability.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 31"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"そのヤクザの組長は暗殺されてしまいました。","romaji":"Sono yakuza no kumichou wa ansatsu sarete shimaimashita.","german":"That yakuza boss ended up being assassinated.","furigana":"くみちょう|あんさつ"},{"japanese":"大統領が暗殺されました。","romaji":"Daitōryō ga ansatsu saremashita.","german":"The president was assassinated.","furigana":"だいとうりょう|あんさつ"},{"japanese":"リンカーンは1865年に暗殺されました。","romaji":"Rinkaan wa 1865-nen ni ansatsu saremashita.","german":"Lincoln was assassinated in 1865.","furigana":"ねん|あんさつ"}],"notes":"暗殺 is the targeted, usually politically motivated murder of a well-known person, that is, an \"assassination\". As a verb it appears almost always in the passive: 大統領が暗殺される. For killing in general, 殺す is used; for murder as a crime, 殺人; the assassin is 暗殺者, the case 暗殺事件. The word belongs mainly in news and history books.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"暗記は苦手なんです。","romaji":"Anki wa nigate na n desu.","german":"I'm not good at memorizing.","furigana":"あんき|にがて"},{"japanese":"漢字を暗記するのは大変です。","romaji":"Kanji o anki suru no wa taihen desu.","german":"Memorizing kanji is hard work.","furigana":"かんじ|あんき|たいへん"},{"japanese":"テストの前に単語を全部暗記しました。","romaji":"Tesuto no mae ni tango o zenbu anki shimashita.","german":"I memorized all the vocabulary before the test.","furigana":"まえ|たんご|ぜんぶあんき"}],"notes":"暗記 is deliberate memorization, word for word: 単語を暗記する, 九九を暗記する. The general 覚える means \"to remember, to learn\" and is less technical; 暗記 emphasizes mechanical memorization, often before exams. Related are 丸暗記 \"rote memorization\", 暗記カード \"flashcards\" and 暗記が得意/苦手.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"田中様、お電話です。","romaji":"Tanaka-sama, odenwa desu.","german":"Mr. Tanaka, you have a phone call.","furigana":"たなかさま|でんわ"},{"japanese":"封筒に「山田花子様」と書きました。","romaji":"Fuutou ni \"Yamada Hanako-sama\" to kakimashita.","german":"I wrote \"Hanako Yamada\" with 様 on the envelope.","furigana":"ふうとう|やまだはなこさま|か"},{"japanese":"お客様、こちらの席へどうぞ。","romaji":"Okyakusama, kochira no seki e douzo.","german":"Sir/Madam, please come this way to your seat.","furigana":"きゃくさま|せき"}],"notes":"〜様 is the more polite form of 〜さん and is attached to names when addressing customers, guests or business partners, as well as on envelopes and in e-mails (山田様). Service staff constantly say お客様. You never use it for yourself or your own company. It is also found in fixed words such as 神様, 王様, 皆様, お疲れ様 and ご馳走様.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あのハエ、どうやって殺すつもりなの？","romaji":"Ano hae, dō yatte korosu tsumori na no?","german":"How do you plan to kill that fly?","furigana":"ころ"},{"japanese":"虫を殺さないでください。","romaji":"Mushi o korosanaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't kill insects.","furigana":"むし|ころ"},{"japanese":"息を殺して、ドアの後ろに隠れていました。","romaji":"Iki o koroshite, doa no ushiro ni kakurete imashita.","german":"I held my breath and hid behind the door.","furigana":"いき|ころ|うし|かく"}],"notes":"殺す is transitive (\"to kill\", 虫を殺す); the passive 殺される means \"to be killed\", and the intransitive counterpart is 死ぬ \"to die\". Figuratively it means \"to suppress\": 息を殺す \"to hold one's breath\", 声を殺す \"to muffle one's voice\", 才能を殺す \"to let a talent wither\". Colloquially, 殺される is also used jokingly and exaggeratedly (母に殺される \"Mom's going to kill me\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私はドラマの殺人のシーンとかも苦手なんです。","romaji":"Watashi wa dorama no satsujin no shīn toka mo nigate na n desu.","german":"I also can't stand murder scenes in dramas.","furigana":"わたし|さつじん|にがて"},{"japanese":"殺人事件が起きました。","romaji":"Satsujin jiken ga okimashita.","german":"A murder case occurred.","furigana":"さつじんじけん|お"},{"japanese":"殺人は重い罪です。","romaji":"Satsujin wa omoi tsumi desu.","german":"Murder is a serious crime.","furigana":"さつじん|おも|つみ"}],"notes":"殺人 is murder or killing as an act and a crime; it mostly appears in compounds such as 殺人事件 \"murder case\", 殺人犯 \"murderer\" or 殺人を犯す \"to commit murder\". For the action itself in a sentence, the verb 殺す is used more often. Colloquially, 殺人的 means \"murderous\": 殺人的な暑さ, 殺人的な混雑.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自殺をしてはいけません。","romaji":"Jisatsu o shite wa ikemasen.","german":"You must not commit suicide.","furigana":"じさつ"},{"japanese":"自殺を防ぐ活動をしています。","romaji":"Jisatsu o fusegu katsudō o shite imasu.","german":"We are working on suicide prevention.","furigana":"じさつ|ふせ|かつどう"},{"japanese":"この雪の中を山に登るのは自殺行為ですよ。","romaji":"Kono yuki no naka o yama ni noboru no wa jisatsu koui desu yo.","german":"Climbing a mountain in this snow is suicidal, you know.","furigana":"ゆき|なか|やま|のぼ|じさつこうい"}],"notes":"自殺 is an intransitive する-verb: 自殺する \"to take one's own life\", with no を-object. An attempt is 自殺を図る, prevention is 自殺防止 or 自殺予防. News reports often use more cautious wording, such as 自ら命を絶つ. Figuratively, 自殺行為 means \"sheer suicide\", i.e. an extremely reckless act.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"え、全然そんなことないよ。","romaji":"E, zenzen sonna koto nai yo.","german":"Huh, that's not true at all.","furigana":"ぜんぜん"},{"japanese":"全然わかりません。","romaji":"Zenzen wakarimasen.","german":"I don't understand at all.","furigana":"ぜんぜん"},{"japanese":"「待った？」「ううん、全然大丈夫。」","romaji":"\"Matta?\" \"Uun, zenzen daijoubu.\"","german":"\"Did you wait long?\" \"No, it's totally fine.\"","furigana":"ま|ぜんぜんだいじょうぶ"}],"notes":"全然 normally goes with a negation: 全然わからない \"not to understand at all\", 全然おいしくない. It also fits words with a negative meaning such as 全然だめ. In colloquial speech today you often hear it with positive statements as an intensifier (全然大丈夫 \"totally okay\"), which is avoided in formal texts. あまり〜ない means only \"not very\", and 全く is the more formal counterpart of 全然.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 9"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は熱いお茶が好きです。","romaji":"Watashi wa atsui ocha ga suki desu.","german":"I like hot tea.","furigana":"わたし|あつ|ちゃ|す"},{"japanese":"このお茶は熱いので気をつけて。","romaji":"Kono ocha wa atsui node ki o tsukete.","german":"This tea is hot, so be careful.","furigana":"ちゃ|あつ|き"}],"notes":"熱い is an i-adjective for a hot object or hot liquid: 熱くない, 熱かった. Hot tea is 熱いお茶. For hot weather, the other spelling 暑い is used. 熱いので can give the heat as the reason for the warning 気をつけて.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"それは皆さんが決めてください。","romaji":"Sore wa minasan ga kimete kudasai.","german":"Please decide that among yourselves.","furigana":"みな|き"},{"japanese":"皆さん、おはようございます。","romaji":"Minasan, ohayō gozaimasu.","german":"Good morning, everyone.","furigana":"みな"}],"notes":"皆さん politely addresses a group as \"all of you\" or \"everyone\". It can be used as a form of address: 皆さん、おはようございます. With が it marks the group that does or decides something. The casual みんな is a different form; 皆さん suits, for example, a greeting to a group.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このネコ、目の色が緑なんだよ。","romaji":"Kono neko, me no iro ga midori nan da yo.","german":"This cat has green eyes.","furigana":"め|いろ|みどり"},{"japanese":"春になると木が緑になります。","romaji":"Haru ni naru to ki ga midori ni narimasu.","german":"In spring the trees turn green.","furigana":"はる|き|みどり"}],"notes":"緑 is a noun for the color green. Before a noun it usually appears as 緑の, as in 緑の目. 緑になる describes a change to this color. 緑なんだ explains or stresses a fact in conversation. The word is read みどり and is not an i-adjective.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 16"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この玉子、なんで緑色なの！？","romaji":"Kono tamago, nande midoriiro na no!?","german":"Why is this egg green?!","furigana":"たまご|みどりいろ"},{"japanese":"緑色のセーターを着ています。","romaji":"Midoriiro no sētā o kite imasu.","german":"I'm wearing a green sweater.","furigana":"みどりいろ|き"},{"japanese":"日本では緑色の信号も「青」と言います。","romaji":"Nihon de wa midoriiro no shingou mo \"ao\" to iimasu.","german":"In Japan, a green traffic light is also called \"blue\".","furigana":"にほん|みどりいろ|しんごう|あお|い"}],"notes":"緑色 is a noun and is placed before nouns with の (緑色のセーター) or used as a predicate with だ/です; there is no い-form as with 赤い or 青い. Often 緑 alone is enough, which additionally means \"greenery, plants\". Note: traffic lights and some vegetables are traditionally called 青 (\"blue\") even though they are green. 黄緑 is light green.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子どものころ、詩人になりたかったです。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, shijin ni naritakatta desu.","german":"When I was a child, I wanted to become a poet.","furigana":"こ|しじん"},{"japanese":"有名な詩人の作品を読みました。","romaji":"Yūmei na shijin no sakuhin o yomimashita.","german":"I read the works of a famous poet.","furigana":"ゆうめい|しじん|さくひん|よ"},{"japanese":"この詩人の詩は教科書にも載っています。","romaji":"Kono shijin no shi wa kyoukasho ni mo notte imasu.","german":"This poet's poems are even included in textbooks.","furigana":"しじん|し|きょうかしょ|の"}],"notes":"詩人 is \"poet\" - someone who writes 詩 (poems): 詩を書く, 詩人になる. For novelists one says 作家 or 小説家; someone who writes traditional tanka is a 歌人, and someone who writes haiku is a 俳人. Jokingly, someone who expresses themselves in a very emotional way is also called 詩人, as in 詩人だね.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先月、課長になりました。","romaji":"Sengetsu, kachō ni narimashita.","german":"Last month I became a section chief.","furigana":"せんげつ|かちょう"},{"japanese":"課長に報告しました。","romaji":"Kachō ni hōkoku shimashita.","german":"I reported to the section chief.","furigana":"かちょう|ほうこく"},{"japanese":"田中課長、会議の資料ができました。","romaji":"Tanaka kachou, kaigi no shiryou ga dekimashita.","german":"Section Chief Tanaka, the materials for the meeting are ready.","furigana":"たなかかちょう|かいぎ|しりょう"}],"notes":"課長 is the head of a 課 (\"section, department\"), ranking below 部長 and above 係長 in the company hierarchy. The title is used directly as a form of address instead of さん: 課長、ちょっといいですか / 田中課長. When talking to outsiders about your own boss, you drop the honorific address and say 課長の田中.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 18"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"実はコウイチの首には賞金がかけられているって知っていましたか。","romaji":"Jitsu wa Kouichi no kubi ni wa shoukin ga kakerarete iru tte shitte imashita ka.","german":"Did you know that there is actually a bounty on Koichi's head?","furigana":"じつ|くび|しょうきん|し"},{"japanese":"大会の賞金は百万円です。","romaji":"Taikai no shoukin wa hyakuman'en desu.","german":"The prize money for the tournament is one million yen.","furigana":"たいかい|しょうきん|ひゃくまんえん"},{"japanese":"クイズ番組で優勝して、賞金をもらいました。","romaji":"Kuizu bangumi de yuushou shite, shoukin o moraimashita.","german":"I won a quiz show and received prize money.","furigana":"ばんぐみ|ゆうしょう|しょうきん"}],"notes":"賞金 is money you receive as a prize or reward: 賞金をもらう, 賞金を獲得する. A prize in kind, by contrast, is 賞品, the prize as an honor 賞. 賞金をかける means \"to put up prize money / a bounty\"; for criminals one also speaks of 懸賞金.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 50"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"バイクの車輪が外れてしまいました。","romaji":"Baiku no sharin ga hazurete shimaimashita.","german":"The wheel of my motorcycle came off.","furigana":"しゃりん|はず"},{"japanese":"自転車の車輪が曲がってしまいました。","romaji":"Jitensha no sharin ga magatte shimaimashita.","german":"The wheel of my bicycle got bent.","furigana":"じてんしゃ|しゃりん|ま"},{"japanese":"このスーツケースは車輪が四つ付いています。","romaji":"Kono suutsukeesu wa sharin ga yottsu tsuite imasu.","german":"This suitcase has four wheels.","furigana":"しゃりん|よっ|つ"}],"notes":"車輪 is the whole wheel of a vehicle (rim with tire), on cars, bicycles, trains or suitcases. In everyday speech people often say タイヤ for cars or bicycles; so it is the タイヤ that goes flat (タイヤがパンクする), not the 車輪. The expression 車の両輪 denotes two things that work only together.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一部の魚には水銀がたまります。","romaji":"Ichibu no sakana ni wa suigin ga tamarimasu.","german":"Mercury accumulates in some fish.","furigana":"いちぶ|さかな|すいぎん"},{"japanese":"水銀は体に有害です。","romaji":"Suigin wa karada ni yūgai desu.","german":"Mercury is harmful to the body.","furigana":"すいぎん|からだ|ゆうがい"}],"notes":"水銀 is the chemical element mercury. It is a substance name and a noun: 水銀がたまる describes an accumulation, 水銀を使う its use. The character 銀 alone means silver; the whole word refers to a different element. The examples talk about accumulation in some fish and about harmfulness to the body.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このキャラの目の色は銀色がいいと思います。","romaji":"Kono kyara no me no iro wa gin'iro ga ii to omoimasu.","german":"I think silver would be a good eye color for this character.","furigana":"め|いろ|ぎんいろ|おも"},{"japanese":"銀色の車が好きです。","romaji":"Gin'iro no kuruma ga suki desu.","german":"I like silver cars.","furigana":"ぎんいろ|くるま|す"},{"japanese":"このスプーンは銀色ですが、本物の銀ではありません。","romaji":"Kono supuun wa gin'iro desu ga, honmono no gin de wa arimasen.","german":"This spoon is silver-colored, but it is not real silver.","furigana":"ぎんいろ|ほんもの|ぎん"}],"notes":"銀色 is a noun (\"silver color\") and stands before nouns with の (銀色の車) or with に (銀色に光る); there is no い-form. It describes only the color; the metal itself is 銀. For cars and electrical appliances people also often say シルバー; the counterpart is 金色 \"gold-colored\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"食べ放題に行くといつも食べすぎてしまいます。","romaji":"Tabehōdai ni iku to itsumo tabe sugite shimaimasu.","german":"Whenever I go to an all-you-can-eat place, I always eat too much.","furigana":"た|ほうだい|い|た"},{"japanese":"カラオケの歌い放題を楽しみました。","romaji":"Karaoke no utaihōdai o tanoshimimashita.","german":"I enjoyed all-you-can-sing karaoke.","furigana":"うた|ほうだい|たの"},{"japanese":"弟は親がいないと、やりたい放題です。","romaji":"Otouto wa oya ga inai to, yaritaihoudai desu.","german":"When their parents aren't around, my younger brother does whatever he wants.","furigana":"おとうと|おや|ほうだい"}],"notes":"〜放題 is attached to the verb stem and means \"as much as you like\": 食べ放題 \"all-you-can-eat\", 飲み放題, 使い放題 (flat rate). With the たい form it sounds negative: 言いたい放題 / やりたい放題 \"to say or do whatever one wants, without consideration\". Also negative is 荒れ放題 \"completely overgrown\", when something is left to itself.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"駅長の子は、どうやら養子らしいです。","romaji":"Ekichō no ko wa, dōyara yōshi rashiidesu.","german":"I heard that the stationmaster's child is apparently adopted.","furigana":"えきちょう|こ|ようし"},{"japanese":"養子を迎えました。","romaji":"Yōshi o mukaemashita.","german":"We took in an adopted child.","furigana":"ようし|むか"},{"japanese":"彼は子どものころ、おじの養子になりました。","romaji":"Kare wa kodomo no koro, oji no youshi ni narimashita.","german":"When he was a child, he was adopted by his uncle.","furigana":"かれ|こ|ようし"}],"notes":"養子 is an adopted child, traditionally mostly a son; for a daughter there is 養女. Common are 養子にする \"to adopt\", 養子になる \"to be adopted\" and 養子をもらう/迎える \"to take in a child\". A Japanese peculiarity is the 婿養子: a son-in-law who is adopted into the wife's family and takes her name.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"駅前のマンションに住んでいます。","romaji":"Ekimae no manshon ni sunde imasu.","german":"I live in an apartment in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|す"},{"japanese":"駅前にカフェがあります。","romaji":"Ekimae ni kafe ga arimasu.","german":"There is a cafe in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ"},{"japanese":"駅前で友達と待ち合わせをしました。","romaji":"Ekimae de tomodachi to machiawase o shimashita.","german":"I met a friend in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|ともだち|ま|あ"}],"notes":"駅前 is the area directly in front of the station with shops, bus stops and taxi stands, not only the square itself, which is called 駅前広場. One says 駅前に住む, 駅前で待ち合わせる; 駅前通り is the street in front of the station. For the exact position \"right in front of the station building\", people more often say 駅の前.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 29"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あそこの駅員さんに聞いてみましょう。","romaji":"Asoko no ekiinsan ni kiite mimashō.","german":"Let's ask the station attendant over there.","furigana":"えきいん|き"},{"japanese":"駅員に道を聞きました。","romaji":"Ekiin ni michi o kikimashita.","german":"I asked a station attendant for directions.","furigana":"えきいん|みち|き"},{"japanese":"父は駅員として三十年働きました。","romaji":"Chichi wa ekiin to shite sanjuunen hatarakimashita.","german":"My father worked as a station attendant for thirty years.","furigana":"ちち|えきいん|さんじゅうねんはたら"}],"notes":"駅員 are the employees of a station, for example at the ticket gate or platform; politely one says 駅員さん. The conductor on the train is 車掌, the train driver 運転士, the head of the station 駅長. When you have questions, you turn to the 駅員: 駅員に聞く.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 29"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日は好きなアイドルがこの駅で一日駅長をしているんです。","romaji":"Kyō wa suki na aidoru ga kono eki de ichinichi ekichō o shite iru n desu.","german":"Today my favorite idol is honorary stationmaster for a day at this station.","furigana":"きょう|す|えき|いちにちえきちょう"},{"japanese":"駅長が挨拶をしました。","romaji":"Ekichō ga aisatsu o shimashita.","german":"The stationmaster gave a welcoming speech.","furigana":"えきちょう|あいさつ"},{"japanese":"和歌山の駅にはネコの駅長がいました。","romaji":"Wakayama no eki ni wa neko no ekichou ga imashita.","german":"At a station in Wakayama there was a cat as stationmaster.","furigana":"わかやま|えき|えきちょう"}],"notes":"駅長 is the stationmaster, i.e. the head of a station; his office is the 駅長室. The regular staff are 駅員. In promotional events a celebrity is often appointed 一日駅長 \"honorary stationmaster for a day\"; the cat Tama also became famous as the 駅長 of a small station.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"机の上を整理します。","romaji":"Tsukue no ue o seiri shimasu.","german":"I'm tidying up the desk.","furigana":"つくえ|うえ|せいり"},{"japanese":"旅行の写真を整理してアルバムに入れた。","romaji":"Ryokō no shashin o seiri shite arubamu ni ireta.","german":"I sorted the travel photos and put them in an album.","furigana":"りょこう|しゃしん|せいり|い"},{"japanese":"ちょっと頭の中を整理してから話します。","romaji":"Chotto atama no naka o seiri shite kara hanashimasu.","german":"Let me sort out my thoughts for a moment, then I'll tell you.","furigana":"あたま|なか|せいり|はな"}],"notes":"整理 means \"to put in order, to sort\", often involving getting rid of what is unnecessary: 書類を整理する, 写真を整理する; also abstractly, 考えを整理する. In 整理整頓 it stands next to 整頓 \"to put neatly in place\"; 片付ける is more general, \"to tidy up, to put away\". Other meanings are \"to regulate\" (交通整理) and \"to cut down\" (人員整理 \"staff reduction\"); 整理券 is a numbered ticket.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あれ、カオルの彼氏じゃない？","romaji":"Are, Kaoru no kareshi ja nai?","german":"Hey, isn't that Kaoru's boyfriend?","furigana":"かれし"},{"japanese":"彼氏と映画を見に行きました。","romaji":"Kareshi to eiga o mi ni ikimashita.","german":"I went to see a movie with my boyfriend.","furigana":"かれし|えいが|み|い"},{"japanese":"親戚に彼氏がいるかどうか聞かれました。","romaji":"Shinseki ni kareshi ga iru ka dou ka kikaremashita.","german":"My relatives asked me whether I have a boyfriend.","furigana":"しんせき|かれし|き"}],"notes":"彼氏 is a steady boyfriend in a romantic relationship: 彼氏がいる, 彼氏ができる, 彼氏と別れる. A man who is merely a friend is 男友達 or simply 友達. The counterpart is 彼女 \"girlfriend\"; the ex is colloquially 元彼. The word is casual-neutral; more formally one says 交際相手 or 恋人.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女はハナさんです。","romaji":"Kanojo wa Hanasan desu.","german":"Sie ist Hana.","furigana":"かのじょ"},{"japanese":"彼女はとても優しいです。","romaji":"Kanojo wa totemo yasashii desu.","german":"Sie ist sehr nett.","furigana":"かのじょ|やさ"},{"japanese":"彼女と付き合って、もう三年になります。","romaji":"Kanojo to tsukiatte, mou sannen ni narimasu.","german":"Ich bin jetzt schon drei Jahre mit meiner Freundin zusammen.","furigana":"かのじょ|つ|あ|さんねん"}],"notes":"彼女 hat zwei Bedeutungen: das Pronomen „sie“ und – sehr häufig – „(feste) Freundin“ (彼女がいる, 僕の彼女). Als Pronomen wird es seltener gebraucht als im Deutschen; meist nennt man den Namen oder sagt あの人, und über Anwesende 彼女 zu sagen kann distanziert wirken. Eine bloße Freundin ohne Beziehung ist 女友達 oder 友達; das männliche Gegenstück ist 彼 bzw. 彼氏.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"俺は生ビールにするよ。","romaji":"Ore wa namabīru ni suru yo.","german":"I'll have a draft beer.","furigana":"おれ|なま"},{"japanese":"俺がやるよ。","romaji":"Ore ga yaru yo.","german":"I'll do it.","furigana":"おれ"},{"japanese":"俺の傘、知らない？","romaji":"Ore no kasa, shiranai?","german":"Have you seen my umbrella?","furigana":"おれ|かさ|し"}],"notes":"俺 is a markedly masculine, casual \"I\" used among friends, in the family or toward younger people. At work, toward superiors or strangers it sounds rough; there people say 私 or 僕. 僕 sounds more boyish and softer. In manga it is often written in katakana as オレ; the plural is 俺たち or 俺ら.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"俺たち、一年生です。","romaji":"Oretachi, ichinensei desu.","german":"We are first-years.","furigana":"おれ|いちねんせい"},{"japanese":"俺たちは負けない。","romaji":"Oretachi wa makenai.","german":"We won't lose.","furigana":"おれ|ま"},{"japanese":"俺たちの町にも新しい駅ができたよ。","romaji":"Oretachi no machi ni mo atarashii eki ga dekita yo.","german":"Our town has a new station now, too.","furigana":"おれ|まち|あたら|えき"}],"notes":"俺たち is the plural of 俺 and means \"we\" in casual, masculine speech among friends or in a team. Even more casual is 俺ら; more neutral are 僕たち and 私たち, formal 私ども. The ending is usually written in hiragana たち, less often 達.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これはジュースじゃないです。缶ビールです。","romaji":"Kore wa jūsu ja nai desu. Kanbīru desu.","german":"This is not juice. It's canned beer.","furigana":"かん"},{"japanese":"缶ビールを冷蔵庫に入れました。","romaji":"Kanbīru o reizōko ni iremashita.","german":"I put the canned beer in the refrigerator.","furigana":"かん|れいぞうこ|い"}],"notes":"缶ビール is beer in a can. 缶 names the packaging; ビール the drink. With 缶ビールです you identify the contents, with 缶ビールを冷蔵庫に入れる you describe chilling it. The card refers to the product, not a counting unit; individual cans are usually counted with 本.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"空き缶はここに入れてください。","romaji":"Akikan wa koko ni irete kudasai.","german":"Please put empty cans in here.","furigana":"あ|かん|い"},{"japanese":"空き缶はリサイクルに出してください。","romaji":"Akikan wa risaikuru ni dashite kudasai.","german":"Please put empty cans out for recycling.","furigana":"あ|かん|だ"},{"japanese":"公園に空き缶が落ちていたので、拾いました。","romaji":"Kouen ni akikan ga ochite ita node, hiroimashita.","german":"There was an empty can lying in the park, so I picked it up.","furigana":"こうえん|あ|かん|お|ひろ"}],"notes":"空き缶 is an empty beverage can; 空き (from 空く \"to become empty\") also appears in 空き瓶 \"empty bottle\", 空き箱 and 空き家. In Japanese waste separation, 空き缶 usually belong to the recyclables category \"缶・びん\". Cans thoughtlessly thrown away are called 空き缶のポイ捨て.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"うちの犬はおじいちゃんなので、すぐ疲れるんです。","romaji":"Uchi no inu wa ojiichan na node, sugu tsukareru n desu.","german":"My dog is old, so he gets tired quickly.","furigana":"いぬ|つか"},{"japanese":"長い一日で疲れました。","romaji":"Nagai ichinichi de tsukaremashita.","german":"I got tired after a long day.","furigana":"なが|いちにち|つか"}],"notes":"疲れる is an intransitive ichidan verb: 疲れます, 疲れて. It describes someone becoming tired. 疲れました can report present tiredness after exertion. すぐ疲れる explains that someone tires quickly. んです adds an explanatory tone in conversation.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 11"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ネコは疲れをいやしてくれるよ。","romaji":"Neko wa tsukare o iyashite kureru yo.","german":"Cats help you relieve your tiredness.","furigana":"つか"},{"japanese":"温泉で疲れを取りましょう。","romaji":"Onsen de tsukare o torimashō.","german":"Let's get rid of our tiredness in the hot spring.","furigana":"おんせん|つか|と"},{"japanese":"旅行の疲れがまだ残っています。","romaji":"Ryokou no tsukare ga mada nokotte imasu.","german":"I'm still worn out from the trip.","furigana":"りょこう|つか|のこ"}],"notes":"疲れ is the noun form of 疲れる and means tiredness or exhaustion. Typical expressions are 疲れが取れる \"to recover\", 疲れを取る \"to get rid of fatigue\", 疲れがたまる \"exhaustion builds up\" and 疲れが出る \"the exhaustion starts to show\". 疲労 is the formal word; the greeting お疲れ様 comes from the same root.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"北海道は日本の北にあります。","romaji":"Hokkaidou wa Nihon no kita ni arimasu.","german":"Hokkaido is in the north of Japan.","furigana":"ほっかいどう|にほん|きた"},{"japanese":"北海道のラベンダー畑を見ました。","romaji":"Hokkaidō no rabendā batake o mimashita.","german":"I saw the lavender fields in Hokkaido.","furigana":"ほっかいどう|ばたけ|み"},{"japanese":"冬休みに北海道へスキーに行きます。","romaji":"Fuyuyasumi ni Hokkaidou e sukii ni ikimasu.","german":"I'm going skiing in Hokkaido during the winter vacation.","furigana":"ふゆやす|ほっかいどう|い"}],"notes":"北海道 is the northernmost of the four main islands and also an administrative unit of its own: the only 道 among the 都道府県, so one never says 北海道県. Its capital is 札幌. One goes 北海道へ/に, and residents are called 道民. It is known for snow, skiing, dairy products and lavender fields.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"そのデータはどうやって共有するつもりですか？","romaji":"Sono dēta wa dō yatte kyōyū suru tsumori desu ka?","german":"How are you planning to share that data?","furigana":"きょうゆう"},{"japanese":"情報を共有しましょう。","romaji":"Jōhō o kyōyū shimashō.","german":"Let's share the information.","furigana":"じょうほう|きょうゆう"},{"japanese":"ルームメイトと台所を共有しています。","romaji":"Ruumumeito to daidokoro o kyouyuu shite imasu.","german":"I share the kitchen with my roommate.","furigana":"だいどころ|きょうゆう"}],"notes":"共有する means \"to own or use jointly, to share\": 情報を共有する, ファイルを共有する, 部屋を友達と共有する. It stresses that several people have the same thing at their disposal; \"to divide\" in the sense of distributing is 分ける instead. In apps and social media, シェアする is also often heard. Compounds are 共有スペース and 情報共有.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"田代島の人口は百人以下です。","romaji":"Tashirojima no jinkō wa hyakunin ika desu.","german":"The population of Tashirojima is no more than a hundred people.","furigana":"たしろじま|じんこう|ひゃくにんいか"},{"japanese":"田代島は猫の島として有名です。","romaji":"Tashirojima wa neko no shima to shite yūmei desu.","german":"Tashirojima is famous as a cat island.","furigana":"たしろじま|ねこ|しま|ゆうめい"}],"notes":"田代島 is the name of Tashirojima, an island in Miyagi Prefecture that belongs to the city of Ishinomaki. 地名 (place name) + の人口 talks about the population; 猫の島として有名です talks about its fame as a cat island. 以下 includes the stated limit: 百人以下 means at most a hundred people. The limit given in the example is supported by the city's population figures from 2026.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このイラストの女の子の表情、めっちゃいいね。","romaji":"Kono irasuto no onnanoko no hyōjō, metcha ii ne.","german":"I really like the expression on the girl's face in this illustration.","furigana":"おんな|こ|ひょうじょう"},{"japanese":"彼女の表情が変わりました。","romaji":"Kanojo no hyōjō ga kawarimashita.","german":"Her expression changed.","furigana":"かのじょ|ひょうじょう|か"},{"japanese":"写真を撮るので、もっと明るい表情をしてください。","romaji":"Shashin o toru node, motto akarui hyoujou o shite kudasai.","german":"I'm going to take a photo, so please look a bit more cheerful.","furigana":"しゃしん|と|あか|ひょうじょう"}],"notes":"表情 is the facial expression: 表情が明るい/暗い, 表情が豊か \"expressive\", 表情が硬い \"to look tense\", 表情を変える. 無表情 means \"expressionless\". In an extended sense it also describes the mood or atmosphere of a place (町の表情), whereas 顔 is the face itself.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ネコに中指をなめられました。","romaji":"Neko ni nakayubi o nameraremashita.","german":"A cat licked my middle finger.","furigana":"なかゆび"},{"japanese":"中指にリングをつけています。","romaji":"Nakayubi ni ringu o tsukete imasu.","german":"I'm wearing a ring on my middle finger.","furigana":"なかゆび"},{"japanese":"中指をドアにはさんでしまいました。","romaji":"Nakayubi o doa ni hasande shimaimashita.","german":"I got my middle finger caught in the door.","furigana":"なかゆび"}],"notes":"中指 is the middle finger, also the middle toe (足の中指). The fingers are, in order, 親指, 人差し指, 中指, 薬指, 小指. The insulting gesture 中指を立てる was adopted from the West and is less common in Japan.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女はオレの女神様なんだ！","romaji":"Kanojo wa ore no megamisama na n da!","german":"She is my goddess!","furigana":"かのじょ|めがみさま"},{"japanese":"ギリシャ神話の女神を勉強しました。","romaji":"Girisha shinwa no megami o benkyō shimashita.","german":"I studied the goddesses of Greek mythology.","furigana":"しんわ|めがみ|べんきょう"},{"japanese":"自由の女神を見にニューヨークへ行きました。","romaji":"Jiyuu no megami o mi ni Nyuuyooku e ikimashita.","german":"I went to New York to see the Statue of Liberty.","furigana":"じゆう|めがみ|み|い"}],"notes":"女神 is a goddess, that is, a female 神; with 様 (女神様) it sounds reverent or besotted, for example when admiring a woman. Well-known phrases are 自由の女神 \"Statue of Liberty\" and 勝利の女神 \"goddess of victory\" (勝利の女神がほほえむ \"luck is on someone's side\"). For gods in general, 神 or 神様 is used.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"王様は料理が大好きです。","romaji":"Ōsama wa ryōri ga daisuki desu.","german":"The king loves cooking.","furigana":"おうさま|りょうり|だいす"},{"japanese":"昔々、王様がいました。","romaji":"Mukashimukashi, ōsama ga imashita.","german":"Once upon a time, there was a king.","furigana":"むかしむかし|おうさま"},{"japanese":"この国には今も王様がいます。","romaji":"Kono kuni ni wa ima mo ousama ga imasu.","german":"This country still has a king today.","furigana":"くに|いま|おうさま"}],"notes":"王様 is the everyday, child-friendly word for \"king\", typical in fairy tales (昔々、王様が…). In news and official texts one says 国王 or simply 王. Related words are 女王 \"queen\", 王子 \"prince\" and お姫様 \"princess\". Figuratively, someone who behaves in a domineering way is also called 王様.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"親指を放してくれ！","romaji":"Oyayubi o hanashite kure!","german":"Let go of my thumb!","furigana":"おやゆび|はな"},{"japanese":"親指を上げてOKの合図をしました。","romaji":"Oyayubi o agete OK no aizu o shimashita.","german":"I gave the OK sign with my thumb up.","furigana":"おやゆび|あ|あいず"},{"japanese":"足の親指をぶつけて、とても痛いです。","romaji":"Ashi no oyayubi o butsukete, totemo itai desu.","german":"I bumped my big toe and it hurts a lot.","furigana":"あし|おやゆび|いた"}],"notes":"親指 is the thumb; on the foot it is the big toe (足の親指). The \"thumbs up\" gesture is 親指を立てる. The other fingers are 人差し指, 中指, 薬指 and 小指. On a smartphone you usually tap with the 親指.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼はサッカーの神様と呼ばれています。","romaji":"Kare wa sakkaa no kamisama to yobarete imasu.","german":"He is called the god of soccer.","furigana":"かれ|かみさま|よ"},{"japanese":"神様にお願いをしました。","romaji":"Kamisama ni onegai o shimashita.","german":"I made a request to God.","furigana":"かみさま|ねが"},{"japanese":"日本ではトイレにも神様がいると言われています。","romaji":"Nihon de wa toire ni mo kamisama ga iru to iwarete imasu.","german":"In Japan, it is said that there is a god even in the toilet.","furigana":"にほん|かみさま|い"}],"notes":"神様 is the respectful form of 神 and the usual word for gods in everyday speech; in Shintō there are countless gods, for example in mountains or houses; the Christian God is also called this. One prays to a god with 神様にお願いする / 祈る. Figuratively, someone is the \"god\" of their field: サッカーの神様. Also well known is the saying お客様は神様です.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 20"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"母は指先が器用で、何でも作れます。","romaji":"Haha wa yubisaki ga kiyou de, nandemo tsukuremasu.","german":"My mother is very good with her hands and can make anything.","furigana":"はは|ゆびさき|きよう|なん|つく"},{"japanese":"冬は指先が冷たくなります。","romaji":"Fuyu wa yubisaki ga tsumetaku narimasu.","german":"In winter my fingertips get cold.","furigana":"ふゆ|ゆびさき|つめ"},{"japanese":"画面を指先で軽くさわってください。","romaji":"Gamen o yubisaki de karuku sawatte kudasai.","german":"Please touch the screen lightly with your fingertip.","furigana":"がめん|ゆびさき|かる"}],"notes":"指先 is the fingertip: 指先が冷たい, 指先で触る. 指先が器用 means \"nimble-fingered, skillful\". It can also mean the tips of the toes, but つま先 is the usual word for that. 先 here means \"tip, end\", as in 鼻先.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"親分の絵が、コンクールで特選に入賞したぞ！","romaji":"Oyabun no e ga, konkūru de tokusen ni nyūshō shita zo!","german":"The boss's painting won the special selection prize at the competition!","furigana":"おやぶん|え|とくせん|にゅうしょう"},{"japanese":"特選の食材を使っています。","romaji":"Tokusen no shokuzai o tsukatte imasu.","german":"We use specially selected ingredients.","furigana":"とくせん|しょくざい|つか"},{"japanese":"この店の特選和牛はとても高いです。","romaji":"Kono mise no tokusen wagyuu wa totemo takai desu.","german":"This restaurant's premium wagyu is very expensive.","furigana":"みせ|とくせんわぎゅう|たか"}],"notes":"特選 has two uses: on products it stands for \"selected, premium quality\" (特選品, 特選和牛, 特選しょうゆ), and at art exhibitions and competitions it is a high distinction (特選に選ばれる, 特選に入賞する). It is not a \"special offer\" in the sense of a reduced price; that is 特売 or セール. The word is rather literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"やった！シアトル市の大学に受かったよ！","romaji":"Yatta! Shiatorushi no daigaku ni ukatta yo!","german":"Yes! I got into a university in Seattle!","furigana":"し|だいがく|う"},{"japanese":"三回目でやっと運転免許の試験に受かりました。","romaji":"Sankaime de yatto unten menkyo no shiken ni ukarimashita.","german":"I finally passed the driving license test on the third try.","furigana":"さんかいめ|うんてんめんきょ|しけん|う"},{"japanese":"アルバイトの面接に受かったので、来週から働きます。","romaji":"Arubaito no mensetsu ni ukatta node, raishuu kara hatarakimasu.","german":"I passed the interview for the part-time job, so I'll start working next week.","furigana":"めんせつ|う|らいしゅう|はたら"}],"notes":"受かる is intransitive and means \"to pass (an exam), be accepted\"; the target takes に: 試験に受かる, 大学に受かる. The opposite is 落ちる \"to fail\". The transitive 受ける means \"to take (an exam)\", the more formal synonym is 合格する. 受かる is colloquial-neutral and very common in conversation.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これは天王星の画像です。","romaji":"Kore wa Tennousei no gazou desu.","german":"This is an image of Uranus.","furigana":"てんのうせい|がぞう"},{"japanese":"天王星は太陽系の惑星です。","romaji":"Tennōsei wa taiyōkei no wakusei desu.","german":"Uranus is a planet of the solar system.","furigana":"てんのうせい|たいようけい|わくせい"},{"japanese":"理科の授業で天王星について調べました。","romaji":"Rika no jugyou de Tennousei ni tsuite shirabemashita.","german":"In science class we looked up information about Uranus.","furigana":"りか|じゅぎょう|てんのうせい|しら"}],"notes":"天王星 is the planet Uranus, the seventh planet from the sun. The planet names end in 星: 水星, 金星, 地球, 火星, 木星, 土星, 天王星, 海王星. When reading, てん and おう merge into てんのう; do not confuse it with 天皇 \"emperor\", which is also read てんのう.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"そのワンピース、めっちゃかわいいね！","romaji":"Sono wanpīsu, metcha kawaii ne!","german":"That dress is so cute!"},{"japanese":"新しいワンピースを買いました。","romaji":"Atarashii wanpīsu o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a new dress.","furigana":"あたら|か"},{"japanese":"夏はワンピース一枚で出かけられるので楽です。","romaji":"Natsu wa wanpiisu ichimai de dekakerareru node raku desu.","german":"In summer it's easy because you can go out in just a dress.","furigana":"なつ|いちまい|で|らく"}],"notes":"ワンピース is a wasei-eigo word from \"one-piece\" and means a (summer) dress made in one piece, that is, top and skirt together. A formal evening dress is more likely called ドレス. You say ワンピースを着る; for swimwear it denotes the one-piece swimsuit. In capitals, \"ONE PIECE\" is also the famous manga.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人を待たせるのは良くないよ。","romaji":"Hito o mataseru no wa yokunai yo.","german":"It's not good to keep people waiting.","furigana":"ひと|ま|よ"},{"japanese":"お待たせしてすみません。","romaji":"Omatase shite sumimasen.","german":"I'm sorry to have kept you waiting.","furigana":"ま"},{"japanese":"寝坊して、彼女を一時間も待たせてしまいました。","romaji":"Nebou shite, kanojo o ichijikan mo matasete shimaimashita.","german":"I overslept and kept my girlfriend waiting for a whole hour.","furigana":"ねぼう|かのじょ|いちじかん|ま"}],"notes":"待たせる is the causative of 待つ: \"to make someone wait\" (人を待たせる). Very common are the fixed polite formulas お待たせしました (staff or someone arriving), humble お待たせしてすみません, and casual お待たせ！. The opposite perspective is 待たされる \"to be kept waiting\" (causative-passive).","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 20"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"下着売り場はあちらです。","romaji":"Shitagi uriba wa achira desu.","german":"The underwear section is over there.","furigana":"したぎう|ば"},{"japanese":"新しい下着を買いに行きます。","romaji":"Atarashii shitagi o kai ni ikimasu.","german":"I'm going to buy new underwear.","furigana":"あたら|したぎ|か|い"},{"japanese":"旅行には下着を三日分持っていきます。","romaji":"Ryokou ni wa shitagi o mikkabun motte ikimasu.","german":"I'm taking three days' worth of underwear on the trip.","furigana":"りょこう|したぎ|みっかぶんも"}],"notes":"下着 is underwear of all kinds, including undershirts; its counterpart is 上着 \"jacket\". Note: パンツ in Japanese usually means \"underpants\", and 肌着 is the undershirt worn directly against the skin. In department stores the section is called 下着売り場; for women's underwear you also hear ランジェリー.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 17"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"水着、ちゃんと持ってきた？","romaji":"Mizugi, chanto motte kita?","german":"Did you bring your swimsuit?","furigana":"みずぎ|も"},{"japanese":"プールに行くので水着を持っていきます。","romaji":"Puuru ni iku node mizugi o motte ikimasu.","german":"I'm taking a swimsuit because I'm going to the pool.","furigana":"い|みずぎ|も"},{"japanese":"海に着いてから水着に着替えました。","romaji":"Umi ni tsuite kara mizugi ni kigaemashita.","german":"After arriving at the sea, I changed into my swimsuit.","furigana":"うみ|つ|みずぎ|きが"}],"notes":"水着 is any kind of swimwear: swimsuit, swim trunks or bikini (ビキニ). You say 水着を着る and 水着に着替える \"to change into\". Colloquially, men's swim trunks are also called 海パン. The word is composed of 水 \"water\" and 着 \"clothing\", like 下着 and 上着.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一時間も待ちぼうけを食いました。","romaji":"Ichijikan mo machibouke o kuimashita.","german":"Ich habe eine Stunde lang vergeblich gewartet.","furigana":"いちじかん|ま|く"},{"japanese":"約束の時間を間違えて、友達に待ちぼうけを食わせてしまいました。","romaji":"Yakusoku no jikan o machigaete, tomodachi ni machibouke o kuwasete shimaimashita.","german":"Ich habe mich in der Uhrzeit vertan und meinen Freund umsonst warten lassen.","furigana":"やくそく|じかん|まちが|ともだち|ま|く"},{"japanese":"雨の中、二時間も待ちぼうけでした。","romaji":"Ame no naka, nijikan mo machibouke deshita.","german":"Ich habe im Regen zwei Stunden lang umsonst gewartet.","furigana":"あめ|なか|にじかん|ま"}],"notes":"待ちぼうけ ist das vergebliche Warten auf jemanden, der nicht kommt – auf Deutsch „versetzt werden“. Die feste Wendung ist 待ちぼうけを食う „vergeblich warten“; jemanden so warten lassen heißt 待ちぼうけを食わせる. Das Wort ist umgangssprachlich und wird praktisch immer mit ぼうけ in Hiragana geschrieben.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの人感じ悪いよね。","romaji":"Ano hito kanji warui yo ne.","german":"That person gives a bad impression, don't you think?","furigana":"ひとかん|わる"},{"japanese":"いい感じですね。","romaji":"Ii kanji desu ne.","german":"That looks good.","furigana":"かん"},{"japanese":"もう春が来た感じがします。","romaji":"Mou haru ga kita kanji ga shimasu.","german":"It feels like spring has already come.","furigana":"はる|き|かん"}],"notes":"感じ is the noun form of 感じる and means \"feeling, impression\". Typical are 〜感じがする \"to feel like, to have the impression that\", 感じがいい/悪い \"likeable/unlikeable\", and colloquially いい感じ \"going well\" or こんな感じ \"something like this\". Do not confuse it with the identically read 漢字; 気持ち is more the inner feeling.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私が今ここにいるのは、皆様のおかげです。","romaji":"Watashi ga ima koko ni iru no wa, minasama no okage desu.","german":"I am here now thanks to all of you.","furigana":"わたし|いま|みなさま"},{"japanese":"皆様のご協力に感謝します。","romaji":"Minasama no gokyōryoku ni kansha shimasu.","german":"I thank you all for your cooperation.","furigana":"みなさま|きょうりょく|かんしゃ"},{"japanese":"乗客の皆様にお知らせします。","romaji":"Joukyaku no minasama ni oshirase shimasu.","german":"An announcement for all passengers.","furigana":"じょうきゃく|みなさま|し"}],"notes":"皆様 is the most polite form of \"everyone\": みんな is casual, 皆さん polite, 皆様 especially respectful. It is heard in speeches, announcements, business letters and customer service. Typical are 皆様のおかげで \"thanks to all of you\" and 皆様、こんにちは.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 37"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あれ、本館にはどうやって行けばいいんだっけ？","romaji":"Are, honkan ni wa dou yatte ikeba ii n da kke?","german":"Um, how do I get to the main building again?","furigana":"ほんかん|い"},{"japanese":"本館は改装中です。","romaji":"Honkan wa kaisōchū desu.","german":"The main building is under renovation.","furigana":"ほんかん|かいそうちゅう"},{"japanese":"本館と新館は二階でつながっています。","romaji":"Honkan to shinkan wa nikai de tsunagatte imasu.","german":"The main building and the new building are connected on the second floor.","furigana":"ほんかん|しんかん|にかい"}],"notes":"本館 is the main building as opposed to 別館 \"annex\" or 新館 \"new building\" - typical for department stores, hotels, ryokan, schools and hospitals. On signs, 本館 also means \"this building, our building\", for example 本館は全館禁煙です.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ごめんなさい、悪気はなかったんです。","romaji":"Gomennasai, warugi wa nakatta n desu.","german":"I'm sorry, I didn't mean any harm.","furigana":"わるぎ"},{"japanese":"彼に悪気がないのは分かっていますが、少し悲しかったです。","romaji":"Kare ni warugi ga nai no wa wakatte imasu ga, sukoshi kanashikatta desu.","german":"I know he means no harm, but I felt a little sad.","furigana":"かれ|わるぎ|わ|すこ|かな"},{"japanese":"子どもが言ったことだから、悪気はないよ。","romaji":"Kodomo ga itta koto da kara, warugi wa nai yo.","german":"A child said it, so there's no ill intent.","furigana":"こ|い|わるぎ"}],"notes":"悪気 (read わるぎ) means \"ill intent\" and is almost always used in the negative: 悪気はない/なかった \"it wasn't meant maliciously\". It is used to apologize or to defend someone. The more formal word for \"malice\", also a legal term, is 悪意.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"山田さんって、山登りするんですか？","romaji":"Yamadasan tte, yamanobori suru n desu ka?","german":"Do you go mountain climbing, Mr. Yamada?","furigana":"やまだ|やまのぼ"},{"japanese":"週末に山登りをします。","romaji":"Shūmatsu ni yamanobori o shimasu.","german":"I go hiking in the mountains on weekends.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|やまのぼ"},{"japanese":"山登りの時は、水をたくさん持っていきましょう。","romaji":"Yamanobori no toki wa, mizu o takusan motte ikimashou.","german":"When you go hiking, bring plenty of water.","furigana":"やまのぼ|とき|みず|も"}],"notes":"山登り is the everyday word for \"climbing a mountain\" and usually means hiking in the mountains, not technical climbing. One says 山登りをする or 山登りに行く; with a place name: 富士山に登る. The Sino-Japanese 登山 is more matter-of-fact and sounds more like sport or alpinism, ハイキング like an easier hike without a summit.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 35"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"え、「流行歌」って言葉、ちょっと古くない？おじいちゃんとかおばあちゃんっぽい。","romaji":"E, ryuukouka tte kotoba, chotto furukunai? Ojiichan toka obaachan ppoi.","german":"Huh, isn't the word 「流行歌」 a bit old-fashioned? It sounds like something grandparents would say.","furigana":"りゅうこうか|ことば|ふる"},{"japanese":"祖母は若い頃の流行歌をよく歌っています。","romaji":"Sobo wa wakai koro no ryuukouka o yoku utatte imasu.","german":"My grandmother often sings the hit songs of her youth.","furigana":"そぼ|わか|ころ|りゅうこうか|うた"},{"japanese":"ラジオから懐かしい流行歌が流れてきました。","romaji":"Rajio kara natsukashii ryuukouka ga nagarete kimashita.","german":"A nostalgic old hit came on the radio.","furigana":"なつ|りゅうこうか|なが"}],"notes":"流行歌 refers to a popular song that was sung everywhere in a certain period, especially the hits of the Showa era. The word sounds old-fashioned today; for current hits one says ヒット曲 or simply 流行っている歌. Typical combinations are 昭和の流行歌 or 昔の流行歌.","levelNote":"Compound: 流行 (N3) + 歌 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このレストランの料理は例外なくどれもおいしいです。","romaji":"Kono resutoran no ryouri wa reigai naku dore mo oishii desu.","german":"The dishes at this restaurant are all delicious without exception.","furigana":"りょうり|れいがい"},{"japanese":"すべてのルールには例外があります。","romaji":"Subete no rūru ni wa reigai ga arimasu.","german":"Every rule has exceptions.","furigana":"れいがい"},{"japanese":"今回だけは例外として、明日出してもいいですよ。","romaji":"Konkai dake wa reigai to shite, ashita dashite mo ii desu yo.","german":"Just this once, as an exception, you may hand it in tomorrow.","furigana":"こんかい|れいがい|あしただ"}],"notes":"例外 is a noun: 例外がある, 例外を認める. Very common are the set phrase 例外なく \"without exception\" and 例外として \"as an exception\". As an adjective, 例外的な means \"exceptional, as an exception\". The opposite would be the rule: ルール or 原則.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これはほんの一例にすぎません。","romaji":"Kore wa honno ichirei ni sugimasen.","german":"This is just one example.","furigana":"いちれい"},{"japanese":"一例を挙げると、日本では家に入る時に靴を脱ぎます。","romaji":"Ichirei o ageru to, Nihon de wa ie ni hairu toki ni kutsu o nugimasu.","german":"To give one example, in Japan you take off your shoes when entering a house.","furigana":"いちれい|あ|にほん|いえ|はい|とき|くつ|ぬ"},{"japanese":"一例として、私の場合をお話しします。","romaji":"Ichirei to shite, watashi no baai o ohanashi shimasu.","german":"As an example, let me tell you about my own case.","furigana":"いちれい|わたし|ばあい|はな"}],"notes":"一例 means \"one example, a single case\" and sounds somewhat more formal than 例. Typical are ほんの一例 \"just one example (of many)\", 一例を挙げる \"to give an example\" and 一例として \"as an example\". In everyday speech one usually just says 例えば or 例を出す.","levelNote":"Compound: 例 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この例文、ちょっとおかしくない？","romaji":"Kono reibun, chotto okashikunai?","german":"Isn't this example sentence a bit strange?","furigana":"れいぶん"},{"japanese":"教科書の例文を読みました。","romaji":"Kyōkasho no reibun o yomimashita.","german":"I read the example sentences in the textbook.","furigana":"きょうかしょ|れいぶん|よ"},{"japanese":"この言葉を使って、例文を作ってみましょう。","romaji":"Kono kotoba o tsukatte, reibun o tsukutte mimashou.","german":"Let's try making an example sentence using this word.","furigana":"ことば|つか|れいぶん|つく"}],"notes":"例文 is an \"example sentence\" as found in textbooks and dictionaries. Typical combinations are 例文を読む, 例文を作る and 辞書の例文. Not to be confused with 例 \"example\" in general or 文 \"sentence\"; 作文 is a composition one writes oneself.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あのランナーは、最後の一周で十位から一位になった。","romaji":"Ano rannā wa, saigo no isshū de jūi kara ichii ni natta.","german":"That runner went from tenth to first place in the last lap.","furigana":"さいご|いっしゅう|じゅうい|いちい"},{"japanese":"公園を一周しましょう。","romaji":"Kōen o isshū shimashō.","german":"Let's go once around the park.","furigana":"こうえん|いっしゅう"},{"japanese":"いつか自転車で日本を一周したいです。","romaji":"Itsuka jitensha de Nihon o isshuu shitai desu.","german":"Someday I want to cycle all the way around Japan.","furigana":"じてんしゃ|にほん|いっしゅう"}],"notes":"一周 is \"one lap, one circuit\". As a する-verb, the place circled takes を: 公園を一周する, 世界一周する \"to make a trip around the world\". As a noun it denotes a lap in sports: 最後の一周. For further laps you count 二周, 三周. Do not confuse it with 一週 (one week, 一週間), which is pronounced the same.","levelNote":"Compound: 周 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの女性の周囲にはいつもヤクザ風の男がいる。","romaji":"Ano josei no shūi ni wa itsumo yakuzafū no otoko ga iru.","german":"There are always yakuza-looking men around that woman.","furigana":"じょせい|しゅうい|ふう|おとこ"},{"japanese":"周囲の環境がとても静かです。","romaji":"Shūi no kankyō ga totemo shizuka desu.","german":"The surrounding environment is very quiet.","furigana":"しゅうい|かんきょう|しず"},{"japanese":"この湖の周囲は十キロぐらいあります。","romaji":"Kono mizuumi no shuui wa juukiro gurai arimasu.","german":"The circumference of this lake is about ten kilometers.","furigana":"みずうみ|しゅうい|じゅう"}],"notes":"周囲 means \"surroundings\", often also \"the people around\" (周囲の人), and also the \"circumference\" of an object: 池の周囲は二キロ. It is more formal and literary than the everyday 周り, which is usually preferred in conversation. Typical: 周囲を見回す, 周囲に気をつける.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"周年記念イベントの準備で忙しい。","romaji":"Shūnen kinen ibento no junbi de isogashii.","german":"I'm busy preparing for the anniversary event.","furigana":"しゅうねんきねん|じゅんび|いそが"},{"japanese":"会社の十周年を祝いました。","romaji":"Kaisha no jusshuunen o iwaimashita.","german":"We celebrated the company's tenth anniversary.","furigana":"かいしゃ|じゅっしゅうねん|いわ"},{"japanese":"私たちは来年、結婚五周年です。","romaji":"Watashitachi wa rainen, kekkon goshuunen desu.","german":"Next year is our fifth wedding anniversary.","furigana":"わたし|らいねん|けっこんごしゅうねん"}],"notes":"周年 almost always follows a number and refers to a round anniversary: 創立十周年 \"tenth anniversary of the founding\", 結婚五周年. Watch the pronunciation: 十周年 is read じゅっしゅうねん or じっしゅうねん. The yearly recurring day itself is called 記念日 (結婚記念日); 周年 counts the years and is often combined with 記念.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このカフェ、いい感じの雰囲気だね。","romaji":"Kono kafe, ii kanji no fun'iki da ne.","german":"This café has a nice atmosphere, doesn't it?","furigana":"かん|ふんいき"},{"japanese":"彼の一言で、会議の雰囲気が悪くなりました。","romaji":"Kare no hitokoto de, kaigi no fun'iki ga waruku narimashita.","german":"One remark from him made the mood in the meeting turn bad.","furigana":"かれ|ひとこと|かいぎ|ふんいき|わる"},{"japanese":"新しい先生は優しい雰囲気の人です。","romaji":"Atarashii sensei wa yasashii fun'iki no hito desu.","german":"The new teacher has a gentle air about her.","furigana":"あたら|せんせい|やさ|ふんいき|ひと"}],"notes":"雰囲気 is the \"atmosphere, mood\" of a place or situation, but also the \"aura\" of a person: 優しい雰囲気の人. Typical: 雰囲気がいい／悪い, 雰囲気のある店, 雰囲気を壊す. The correct reading is ふんいき; the often heard ふいんき is considered a mistake.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本には四季があります。","romaji":"Nippon ni wa shiki ga arimasu.","german":"Japan has four seasons.","furigana":"にっぽん|しき"},{"japanese":"日本の四季は美しいです。","romaji":"Nihon no shiki wa utsukushii desu.","german":"Japan's four seasons are beautiful.","furigana":"にほん|しき|うつく"},{"japanese":"この公園では四季の花を楽しむことができます。","romaji":"Kono kouen de wa shiki no hana o tanoshimu koto ga dekimasu.","german":"In this park you can enjoy the flowers of each season throughout the year.","furigana":"こうえん|しき|はな|たの"}],"notes":"四季 means the four seasons as a whole and sounds somewhat more elevated than 季節, which refers to a single season (好きな季節は秋です). A popular cliche is 日本には四季がある or 四季がはっきりしている. Set combinations: 四季の花, 四季折々 \"in every season\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"君には完全に負けたよ。","romaji":"Kimi ni wa kanzen ni maketa yo.","german":"You totally beat me.","furigana":"きみ|かんぜん|ま"},{"japanese":"完全に理解しました。","romaji":"Kanzen ni rikai shimashita.","german":"I understood it completely.","furigana":"かんぜん|りかい"},{"japanese":"完全な人間なんていません。","romaji":"Kanzen na ningen nante imasen.","german":"There's no such thing as a perfect person.","furigana":"かんぜん|にんげん"}],"notes":"完全 is a な-adjective: 完全な答え \"a complete answer\". Very common is the adverb 完全に \"completely, totally\", also used colloquially as an intensifier: 完全に忘れてた. Unlike 完璧 \"flawless, perfect\", 完全 emphasizes that nothing is missing. Opposite: 不完全.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"完成前に起こしてもらえますか？","romaji":"Kansei mae ni okoshite moraemasu ka?","german":"Can you wake me up before it's finished?","furigana":"かんせいまえ|お"},{"japanese":"作品がやっと完成しました。","romaji":"Sakuhin ga yatto kansei shimashita.","german":"The work is finally finished.","furigana":"さくひん|かんせい"},{"japanese":"新しい駅ビルは来年完成する予定です。","romaji":"Atarashii eki biru wa rainen kansei suru yotei desu.","german":"The new station building is scheduled to be completed next year.","furigana":"あたら|えき|らいねんかんせい|よてい"}],"notes":"完成 is the \"completion\" of a work, building or plan. It is mostly intransitive: 作品が完成する \"the work gets completed\"; transitively one more often says 作品を完成させる. In everyday speech, for small things one rather says できた; 完成 sounds like a bigger project. Nouns: 完成品, 完成まであと一週間.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人性について考えています。","romaji":"Jinsei ni tsuite kangaete imasu.","german":"I'm thinking about human nature.","furigana":"じんせい|かんが"},{"japanese":"この小説は人性の弱さを書いています。","romaji":"Kono shousetsu wa jinsei no yowasa o kaite imasu.","german":"This novel depicts the weakness of human nature.","furigana":"しょうせつ|じんせい|よわ|か"},{"japanese":"怒りや悲しみも人性の一部です。","romaji":"Ikari ya kanashimi mo jinsei no ichibu desu.","german":"Anger and sadness are also part of human nature.","furigana":"いか|かな|じんせい|いちぶ"}],"notes":"人性 \"human nature\" is a rare, literary word from philosophy and literature. In normal Japanese one says 人間性 or 人間の本性 instead. Do not confuse it with the identically pronounced and very common 人生 \"life, course of life\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"性病にかかってしまいました。","romaji":"Seibyō ni kakatte shimaimashita.","german":"I caught a sexually transmitted disease.","furigana":"せいびょう"},{"japanese":"性病の検査を受けました。","romaji":"Seibyō no kensa o ukemashita.","german":"I got tested for sexually transmitted diseases.","furigana":"せいびょう|けんさ|う"},{"japanese":"性病を防ぐために、コンドームを使いましょう。","romaji":"Seibyou o fusegu tame ni, kondoomu o tsukaimashou.","german":"Use condoms to prevent sexually transmitted diseases.","furigana":"せいびょう|ふせ|つか"}],"notes":"性病 is the general term for \"venereal diseases\". One says 性病にかかる \"to catch\" and 性病の検査を受ける \"to get tested\". In medicine and educational texts, the more neutral technical term 性感染症 is used today.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"せっかくのアドバイスを無駄にするつもりか？","romaji":"Sekkaku no adobaisu o muda ni suru tsumori ka?","german":"Are you going to waste the advice I took the trouble to give you?","furigana":"むだ"},{"japanese":"せっかく作ったのに、誰も食べてくれなかった。","romaji":"Sekkaku tsukutta noni, dare mo tabete kurenakatta.","german":"I went to the trouble of making it, but nobody ate it.","furigana":"つく|だれ|た"},{"japanese":"せっかく京都に来たんだから、お寺を見に行こう。","romaji":"Sekkaku Kyouto ni kita n da kara, otera o mi ni ikou.","german":"Since we've come all the way to Kyoto, let's go see a temple.","furigana":"きょうと|き|てら|み|い"}],"notes":"せっかく is almost always written in kana. Three patterns: せっかく〜のに \"went to the trouble of ..., but in vain\" (regret), せっかく〜から \"since we're here ..., (let's make use of the opportunity)\" and せっかくの＋noun \"precious, long-awaited\", often with disappointment: せっかくの休みが雨だ. The polite formula せっかくですが introduces a refusal.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"君には失望したよ。","romaji":"Kimi ni wa shitsubō shita yo.","german":"I'm disappointed in you.","furigana":"きみ|しつぼう"},{"japanese":"彼の態度に失望しました。","romaji":"Kare no taido ni shitsubō shimashita.","german":"I was disappointed by his attitude.","furigana":"かれ|たいど|しつぼう"},{"japanese":"試合に負けて、ファンの失望はとても大きかった。","romaji":"Shiai ni makete, fan no shitsubou wa totemo ookikatta.","german":"After losing the match, the fans' disappointment was enormous.","furigana":"しあい|ま|しつぼう|おお"}],"notes":"失望 is \"disappointment\" and sounds stronger and more formal than the everyday がっかりする. The person or thing one is disappointed in is marked with に: 彼の態度に失望する. To disappoint someone is 失望させる; as a noun: 失望が大きい.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あなたの望みは何ですか。","romaji":"Anata no nozomi wa nani desu ka.","german":"What are your wishes?","furigana":"のぞ|なに"},{"japanese":"まだ望みはあります。","romaji":"Mada nozomi wa arimasu.","german":"There is still hope.","furigana":"のぞ"},{"japanese":"やっと長年の望みがかないました。","romaji":"Yatto naganen no nozomi ga kanaimashita.","german":"My long-held wish has finally come true.","furigana":"ながねん|のぞ"}],"notes":"望み is the noun of 望む and means \"wish\" as well as \"hope, prospect\". Typical: 望みがかなう \"the wish comes true\", 望みを捨てない \"not to give up hope\", まだ望みがある. By comparison, 願い sounds more personal and emotional, 希望 more matter-of-fact (e.g. on forms).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"両親は私が医者になることを望んでいます。","romaji":"Ryoushin wa watashi ga isha ni naru koto o nozonde imasu.","german":"My parents wish for me to become a doctor.","furigana":"りょうしん|わたし|いしゃ|のぞ"},{"japanese":"平和を望んでいます。","romaji":"Heiwa o nozonde imasu.","german":"I wish for peace.","furigana":"へいわ|のぞ"},{"japanese":"このホテルの部屋からは富士山を望むことができます。","romaji":"Kono hoteru no heya kara wa Fujisan o nozomu koto ga dekimasu.","german":"From the rooms of this hotel you can see Mt. Fuji.","furigana":"へや|ふじさん|のぞ"}],"notes":"望む \"to wish, to hope for\" is more formal than 〜たい or 欲しい and often appears in speeches or texts: 平和を望む. One can also expect something from others: 親は子供が医者になることを望んでいる. In written language it also means \"to look out at something\": 富士山を望む部屋. Colloquially: 望むところだ \"Bring it on, that suits me just fine!\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"明日、ホームセンターに木材を買いに行く予定です。","romaji":"Ashita, hōmusentā ni mokuzai o kai ni iku yotei desu.","german":"I plan to go to the home improvement store tomorrow to buy lumber.","furigana":"あした|もくざい|か|い|よてい"},{"japanese":"木材で家を建てます。","romaji":"Mokuzai de ie o tatemasu.","german":"I'm building a house out of wood.","furigana":"もくざい|いえ|た"},{"japanese":"日本は外国から木材をたくさん輸入しています。","romaji":"Nihon wa gaikoku kara mokuzai o takusan yunyuu shite imasu.","german":"Japan imports a lot of lumber from abroad.","furigana":"にほん|がいこく|もくざい|ゆにゅう"}],"notes":"木材 is \"wood\" as a material and commodity, for example in home improvement stores, industry or statistics. In everyday speech, for objects people prefer 木でできた \"made of wood\"; 木 alone also means \"tree\". The closely related 材木 refers specifically to cut construction timber such as beams and boards.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"古い材木を使って、テーブルを作りました。","romaji":"Furui zaimoku o tsukatte, teeburu o tsukurimashita.","german":"I made a table out of old lumber.","furigana":"ふる|ざいもく|つか|つく"},{"japanese":"材木を運んでいます。","romaji":"Zaimoku o hakonde imasu.","german":"I'm transporting lumber.","furigana":"ざいもく|はこ"},{"japanese":"祖父は町で小さな材木屋をやっていました。","romaji":"Sofu wa machi de chiisana zaimokuya o yatte imashita.","german":"My grandfather ran a small lumber yard in town.","furigana":"そふ|まち|ちい|ざいもくや"}],"notes":"材木 is cut \"construction timber\" such as beams and boards, as you buy at a 材木屋 \"lumber yard\". It sounds more traditional and concrete than 木材, which generally denotes \"wood as a raw material\". Typical: 材木を運ぶ, 材木を切る.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"へー、松の葉っぱのこと「松葉」って言うんだ。知らなかった。","romaji":"Hē, matsu no happa no koto matsuba tte iu n da. Shiranakatta.","german":"Oh, so the leaves of a pine are called 「松葉」. I didn't know that.","furigana":"まつ|は|まつば|い|し"},{"japanese":"松葉が地面に落ちています。","romaji":"Matsuba ga jimen ni ochite imasu.","german":"Pine needles are lying on the ground.","furigana":"まつば|じめん|お"},{"japanese":"冬になっても、松葉は緑のままです。","romaji":"Fuyu ni natte mo, matsuba wa midori no mama desu.","german":"Even in winter, pine needles stay green.","furigana":"ふゆ|まつば|みどり"}],"notes":"松葉 is the \"pine needle\", composed of 松 \"pine\" and 葉 \"leaf\", which here becomes voiced to ば. In conversation one also says 松の葉. Well-known compounds are 松葉杖 \"crutch\" (after its forked shape) and 松葉ガニ, a regional name for the snow crab.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あんなことを言うなんて、彼の人格を疑います。","romaji":"Anna koto o iu nante, kare no jinkaku o utagaimasu.","german":"Saying such a thing makes me doubt his character.","furigana":"い|かれ|じんかく|うたが"},{"japanese":"彼は立派な人格の持ち主です。","romaji":"Kare wa rippa na jinkaku no mochinushi desu.","german":"He is a person of noble character.","furigana":"かれ|りっぱ|じんかく|も|ぬし"},{"japanese":"お酒を飲むと人格が変わる人がいます。","romaji":"Osake o nomu to jinkaku ga kawaru hito ga imasu.","german":"Some people become a completely different person when they drink.","furigana":"さけ|の|じんかく|か|ひと"}],"notes":"人格 is \"character\" in the moral sense, the personality as a whole, and sounds serious. Typical: 人格を疑う \"to doubt someone's decency\", 人格者 \"person of noble character\", 二重人格. For everyday temperament people say 性格 (明るい性格), for a person's likable manner 人柄.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ゼッタイ合格するぞ！","romaji":"Zettai gōkaku suru zo!","german":"I'm definitely going to pass!","furigana":"ごうかく"},{"japanese":"試験に合格できて嬉しいです。","romaji":"Shiken ni gōkaku dekite ureshii desu.","german":"I'm glad I was able to pass the exam.","furigana":"しけん|ごうかく|うれ"},{"japanese":"兄は東京の大学に合格しました。","romaji":"Ani wa Toukyou no daigaku ni goukaku shimashita.","german":"My older brother got into a university in Tokyo.","furigana":"あに|とうきょう|だいがく|ごうかく"}],"notes":"合格する means \"to pass an exam\" or \"to be admitted\"; the exam or school is marked with に: 試験に合格する, 大学に合格する. Colloquially one also says 受かる; the opposite is 落ちる or 不合格になる. Related: 合格点 \"passing mark\", 合格発表 \"announcement of results\".","levelNote":"Derived from 合格 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ステキな格好だね。","romaji":"Suteki na kakkō da ne.","german":"That's a great outfit.","furigana":"かっこう"},{"japanese":"格好いい服を着ていますね。","romaji":"Kakkoii fuku o kite imasu ne.","german":"You're wearing stylish clothes.","furigana":"|ふく|き"},{"japanese":"その格好では寒くないですか。","romaji":"Sono kakkō de wa samukunai desu ka.","german":"Aren't you cold in that getup?","furigana":"かっこう|さむ"}],"notes":"格好 here means appearance, getup or clothing. 素敵な格好 praises the outfit; その格好 refers to a person's actual clothes. 格好いい is a fixed combination for good-looking or stylish, which is pronounced kakkoii in the example. The standalone base form of the card, by contrast, is the noun kakkō. かっこう is the kana spelling of this noun for appearance or getup; it is not the short form かっこ or the noun かっこう \"cuckoo\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ガッシリした骨格の男性がタイプです。","romaji":"Gasshiri shita kokkaku no dansei ga taipu desu.","german":"I like men with a sturdy build.","furigana":"こっかく|だんせい"},{"japanese":"骨格がしっかりしています。","romaji":"Kokkaku ga shikkari shite imasu.","german":"He has a sturdy bone structure.","furigana":"こっかく"},{"japanese":"やっと新しい計画の骨格ができました。","romaji":"Yatto atarashii keikaku no kokkaku ga dekimashita.","german":"At last the framework of the new plan is in place.","furigana":"あたら|けいかく|こっかく"}],"notes":"骨格 is the \"skeleton\" or the \"bone structure, physique\": 骨格がしっかりしている \"to be solidly built\". Figuratively it means the \"framework\" of a plan or text: 計画の骨格. For the general build of the body, 体格 is also used in daily life; 骨格 stresses the bone structure.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私はここに残るよ。","romaji":"Watashi wa koko ni nokoru yo.","german":"I'll stay here.","furigana":"わたし|のこ"},{"japanese":"仕事が残っています。","romaji":"Shigoto ga nokotte imasu.","german":"There is still work left.","furigana":"しごと|のこ"},{"japanese":"あの旅行のことは、今でも心に残っています。","romaji":"Ano ryokou no koto wa, ima demo kokoro ni nokotte imasu.","german":"That trip has stayed in my heart to this day.","furigana":"りょこう|いま|こころ|のこ"}],"notes":"残る is intransitive: \"to remain, to be left over, to stay behind\"; the transitive counterpart is 残す \"to leave over\". The place is marked with に: ここに残る, 会社に残る. Something left over takes が: 仕事が残っている, ご飯が残る. Figuratively: 心に残る, 記憶に残る \"to stay in one's memory\". For \"to stay\" in general, いる is often enough.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自分的には上手にアメリカ的なスピーチができたと思ってたんだけどな。","romaji":"Jibunteki ni wa jouzu ni Amerikateki na supiichi ga dekita to omotteta n da kedo na.","german":"Ich dachte eigentlich, dass ich eine recht gute Rede im amerikanischen Stil gehalten habe.","furigana":"じぶんてき|じょうず|てき|おも"},{"japanese":"日本的な考え方です。","romaji":"Nihonteki na kangaekata desu.","german":"Das ist eine typisch japanische Denkweise.","furigana":"にほんてき|かんが|かた"},{"japanese":"もっと健康的な生活をしたいです。","romaji":"Motto kenkouteki na seikatsu o shitai desu.","german":"Ich möchte gesünder leben.","furigana":"けんこうてき|せいかつ"}],"notes":"〜的 hängt man an sino-japanische Nomen und bildet so な-Adjektive: 日本的な考え方, 健康的な生活, adverbial 経済的に. Es entspricht deutschen Endungen wie „-isch, -lich, -artig“. Jugendsprachlich bedeutet 私的には oder 自分的には „was mich betrifft“; das gilt als sehr salopp. Alleinstehend wird 的 als まと gelesen und heißt „Zielscheibe“.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"めっちゃ日本的な考え方だよね。","romaji":"Metcha Nihonteki na kangaekata da yo ne.","german":"That's a very Japanese way of thinking, isn't it?","furigana":"にほんてき|かんが|かた"},{"japanese":"この庭園はとても日本的です。","romaji":"Kono teien wa totemo nihonteki desu.","german":"This garden is very typically Japanese.","furigana":"ていえん|にほんてき"},{"japanese":"何でもみんなで決めるのは、日本的な会社の特徴です。","romaji":"Nan demo minna de kimeru no wa, Nihonteki na kaisha no tokuchou desu.","german":"Deciding everything together is a characteristic of Japanese companies.","furigana":"なん|き|にほんてき|かいしゃ|とくちょう"}],"notes":"日本的 \"typically Japanese\" describes above all ways of thinking, values and culture: 日本的な考え方, 日本的な会社. For the look and style of rooms, food or design, 和風 or 日本風 is used more often. 日本らしい, by contrast, usually sounds appreciative: \"just the way you would expect from Japan\".","levelNote":"Compound: 日本 (N5) + 的 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私、芸能界に入りたいんです。","romaji":"Watashi, geinōkai ni hairitai n desu.","german":"I want to get into show business.","furigana":"わたし|げいのうかい|はい"},{"japanese":"彼女は十五歳で芸能界にデビューしました。","romaji":"Kanojo wa juugosai de geinoukai ni debyuu shimashita.","german":"She made her debut in show business at fifteen.","furigana":"かのじょ|じゅうごさい|げいのうかい"},{"japanese":"芸能界のニュースにはあまり興味がありません。","romaji":"Geinoukai no nyuusu ni wa amari kyoumi ga arimasen.","german":"I'm not very interested in entertainment news.","furigana":"げいのうかい|きょうみ"}],"notes":"芸能界 is the \"show business, entertainment industry\" with actors, singers and TV stars. Typical are 芸能界に入る, 芸能界デビュー and 芸能界を引退する. A person from this world is called 芸能人; gossip about it is 芸能ニュース.","levelNote":"Compound: 芸能 (N2) + 界 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの芸人、めっちゃおもしろいね！","romaji":"Ano geinin, metcha omoshiroi ne!","german":"That comedian is really funny, isn't he?","furigana":"げいにん"},{"japanese":"将来はお笑い芸人になるのが夢です。","romaji":"Shourai wa owarai geinin ni naru no ga yume desu.","german":"My dream is to become a comedian in the future.","furigana":"しょうらい|わら|げいにん|ゆめ"},{"japanese":"お祭りで大道芸人がジャグリングをしていました。","romaji":"Omatsuri de daidou geinin ga jaguringu o shite imashita.","german":"A street performer was juggling at the festival.","furigana":"まつ|だいどうげいにん"}],"notes":"芸人 today mainly refers to comedians on television, in full お笑い芸人. Originally it means \"performer, entertainer\" in general, such as 大道芸人 \"street performer\". Not to be confused with 芸能人 (celebrity, star in general) or 芸者 (geisha).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"宴会で芸者さんが踊りを見せてくれました。","romaji":"Enkai de geishasan ga odori o misete kuremashita.","german":"A geisha performed a dance for us at the banquet.","furigana":"えんかい|げいしゃ|おど|み"},{"japanese":"京都で芸者を見ました。","romaji":"Kyōto de geisha o mimashita.","german":"I saw a geisha in Kyoto.","furigana":"きょうと|げいしゃ|み"},{"japanese":"芸者は歌や踊りでお客さんを楽しませる仕事です。","romaji":"Geisha wa uta ya odori de okyakusan o tanoshimaseru shigoto desu.","german":"Geisha's work is to entertain guests with song and dance.","furigana":"げいしゃ|うた|おど|きゃく|たの|しごと"}],"notes":"芸者 is a traditional female entertainer who entertains at banquets with dance, song and conversation; respectfully one says 芸者さん. In Kyoto she is called 芸妓, the apprentice 舞妓. The image of the geisha as a prostitute that is widespread in the West is false.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これは消化するのに時間がかかると思うよ。","romaji":"Kore wa shōka suru no ni jikan ga kakaru to omou yo.","german":"I think this will take some time to digest.","furigana":"しょうか|じかん|おも"},{"japanese":"脂っこい食べ物は消化しにくいです。","romaji":"Aburakkoi tabemono wa shōka shinikui desu.","german":"Greasy food is hard to digest.","furigana":"あぶら|た|もの|しょうか"},{"japanese":"今月中に残りの有給を消化しなければなりません。","romaji":"Kongetsu chuu ni nokori no yuukyuu o shouka shinakereba narimasen.","german":"I have to use up my remaining paid leave this month.","furigana":"こんげつちゅう|のこ|ゆうきゅう|しょうか"}],"notes":"消化する means \"to digest\" (食べ物を消化する) and, figuratively, \"to absorb material\": 授業の内容を消化する. In working life it also means \"to use up, work through\": 有給を消化する \"to take remaining leave\". Common: 消化にいい／悪い \"easy／hard to digest\". Do not confuse it with the identically read 消火 \"extinguishing a fire\".","levelNote":"Derived from 消化 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これ、消しゴムで消えるよ。","romaji":"Kore, keshigomu de kieru yo.","german":"This will come off with an eraser.","furigana":"け|き"},{"japanese":"電気が消えました。","romaji":"Denki ga kiemashita.","german":"The light went out.","furigana":"でんき|き"}],"notes":"消える is an intransitive ichidan verb: 消えます, 消えて. A light can go out, and writing can disappear. With が you name the thing affected. 消しゴムで names the eraser as the means. The transitive 消す, by contrast, describes someone erasing or switching something off.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 18"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この絵、めっちゃ気持ち悪い。","romaji":"Kono e, metcha kimochi warui.","german":"This picture is really creepy.","furigana":"え|きも|わる"},{"japanese":"乗り物酔いで気持ち悪いです。","romaji":"Norimono yoi de kimochiwarui desu.","german":"I feel sick from motion sickness.","furigana":"の|ものよ|きも|わる"},{"japanese":"雨で靴下がぬれて、気持ち悪い。","romaji":"Ame de kutsushita ga nurete, kimochi warui.","german":"My socks got wet in the rain, and it feels yucky.","furigana":"あめ|くつした|きも|わる"}],"notes":"気持ち悪い covers two areas: physically \"I feel sick\" (乗り物酔いで気持ち悪い) and emotionally \"disgusting, creepy, repulsive\" (虫が気持ち悪い). It also expresses an unpleasant feeling on the skin: 汗で気持ち悪い. In youth slang it is shortened to キモい, which is very hurtful when said about people. Opposite: 気持ちいい.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私一人でこのピアノを動かすのはムリです。","romaji":"Watashi hitori de kono piano o ugokasu no wa muri desu.","german":"It's impossible for me to move this piano by myself.","furigana":"わたしひとり|うご"},{"japanese":"この箱を動かしてください。","romaji":"Kono hako o ugokashite kudasai.","german":"Please move this box.","furigana":"はこ|うご"},{"japanese":"その映画は多くの人の心を動かしました。","romaji":"Sono eiga wa ooku no hito no kokoro o ugokashimashita.","german":"That movie moved many people.","furigana":"えいが|おお|ひと|こころ|うご"}],"notes":"動かす is transitive: \"to move something, to shift, to set in motion\"; the intransitive counterpart is 動く \"to move\". Typical are 机を動かす, 体を動かす \"to move around, to exercise\" and 機械を動かす \"to operate a machine\". Figuratively, 心を動かす means \"to move, to touch someone\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"こういうイヤリング、着ける？","romaji":"Kōiu iyaringu, tsukeru?","german":"Do you wear earrings like these?","furigana":"つ"},{"japanese":"ネクタイを着けましょう。","romaji":"Nekutai o tsukemashō.","german":"Let's put on a necktie.","furigana":"つ"},{"japanese":"電車の中ではマスクを着けてください。","romaji":"Densha no naka de wa masuku o tsukete kudasai.","german":"Please wear a mask on the train.","furigana":"でんしゃ|なか|つ"}],"notes":"着ける is \"to put on, to wear\" for accessories and things fastened to the body: マスク, ネックレス, 指輪, イヤリング. It is mostly just written つける. For clothing on the upper body 着る applies, for trousers and shoes はく, for hats かぶる, for ties and belts more often しめる. Fixed phrase: 身に着ける \"to wear on the body; to acquire (a skill)\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ちゃんと毎日勉強するんだよ。","romaji":"Chanto mainichi benkyō suru n da yo.","german":"You have to study properly every day.","furigana":"まいにちべんきょう"},{"japanese":"毎日三時間勉強します。","romaji":"Mainichi sanjikan benkyō shimasu.","german":"I study three hours every day.","furigana":"まいにちさんじかんべんきょう"},{"japanese":"試験の前に、友達と図書館で勉強しました。","romaji":"Shiken no mae ni, tomodachi to toshokan de benkyō shimashita.","german":"Before the exam, I studied at the library with a friend.","furigana":"しけん|まえ|ともだち|としょかん|べんきょう"}],"notes":"勉強する means \"to study\" or \"to work on a subject of learning\" here. The subject or language can take を: 日本語を勉強する. The duration comes without に, as in 三時間勉強する. The phrase 〜するんだよ can be an emphatic instruction in this context, not just a description. In the third example, と names the study partner and で the place of study. For repeatedly practicing a skill, 練習する often fits. This card practices the meaning \"to study\", not a price discount.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いつ旅行する予定なんですか？","romaji":"Itsu ryokō suru yotei na n desu ka?","german":"When are you planning to travel?","furigana":"りょこう|よてい"},{"japanese":"夏にヨーロッパを旅行します。","romaji":"Natsu ni Yōroppa o ryokō shimasu.","german":"I'm traveling around Europe in the summer.","furigana":"なつ|りょこう"},{"japanese":"一人で旅行するのが好きです。","romaji":"Hitori de ryokou suru no ga suki desu.","german":"I like traveling alone.","furigana":"ひとり|りょこう|す"}],"notes":"旅行する means \"to travel, to take a trip\". The area traveled takes を (ヨーロッパを旅行する \"to travel around Europe\"), and the destination can take に or へ. In everyday speech 旅行に行く is even more common. 旅 sounds more poetic, and 出張 is a business trip.","levelNote":"Derived from 旅行 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ゼッタイ起こすなよ。","romaji":"Zettai okosu na yo.","german":"Whatever you do, don't wake him up.","furigana":"お"},{"japanese":"朝七時に起こしてください。","romaji":"Asa shichiji ni okoshite kudasai.","german":"Please wake me up at seven in the morning.","furigana":"あさしちじ|お"},{"japanese":"あの運転手は去年も事故を起こしました。","romaji":"Ano untenshu wa kyonen mo jiko o okoshimashita.","german":"That driver caused an accident last year, too.","furigana":"うんてんしゅ|きょねん|じこ|お"}],"notes":"起こす is the transitive counterpart of 起きる and means \"to wake someone\" or \"to set upright\": 子供を起こす, 倒れた自転車を起こす. Also very common is \"to cause, to trigger\": 事故を起こす, 問題を起こす. To ask to be woken: 七時に起こしてください.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 16"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このスキー場に来るのは、ほとんど外国人旅行者です。","romaji":"Kono sukījō ni kuru no wa, hotondo gaikokujin ryokōsha desu.","german":"Most of the visitors to this ski resort are foreign tourists.","furigana":"じょう|く|がいこくじんりょこうしゃ"},{"japanese":"多くの旅行者が京都を訪れます。","romaji":"Ōku no ryokōsha ga Kyōto o otozuremasu.","german":"Many travelers visit Kyoto.","furigana":"おお|りょこうしゃ|きょうと|おとず"},{"japanese":"旅行者のための案内所は駅の中にあります。","romaji":"Ryokousha no tame no annaijo wa eki no naka ni arimasu.","german":"The information center for travelers is inside the station.","furigana":"りょこうしゃ|あんないじょ|えき|なか"}],"notes":"旅行者 is a \"traveler\" in general, rather matter-of-fact, as in news or on signs: 外国人旅行者. Someone traveling to see the sights is a 観光客 \"tourist\", the more common word in conversation. 旅人 sounds literary and romantic.","levelNote":"Compound: 旅行 (N5) + 者 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの人、人の悪口ばっかり言うから苦手なんだよね。","romaji":"Ano hito, hito no waruguchi bakkari iu kara nigate na n da yo ne.","german":"I don't like that person because he's always badmouthing others.","furigana":"ひと|ひと|わるぐち|い|にがて"},{"japanese":"人の悪口を言わないでください。","romaji":"Hito no waruguchi o iwanaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't speak ill of others.","furigana":"ひと|わるぐち|い"},{"japanese":"陰で悪口を言われていたと知って、悲しくなりました。","romaji":"Kage de waruguchi o iwarete ita to shitte, kanashiku narimashita.","german":"I was sad to learn that people had been badmouthing me behind my back.","furigana":"かげ|わるぐち|い|し|かな"}],"notes":"悪口 means \"gossip, disparaging remarks\" about someone; almost always in the phrase 悪口を言う, often 人の悪口. Behind someone's back: 陰で悪口を言う. The reading is わるぐち; the Sino-Japanese reading あっこう is literary. A complaint about something, by contrast, is 文句.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"良好なコミュニケーションをとるために必要なこととは何だと思いますか？","romaji":"Ryōkō na komyunikēshon o toru tame ni hitsuyō na koto to wa nani da to omoimasu ka?","german":"What do you think is necessary to have good communication?","furigana":"りょうこう|ひつよう|なに|おも"},{"japanese":"健康状態は良好です。","romaji":"Kenkō jōtai wa ryōkō desu.","german":"My state of health is good.","furigana":"けんこうじょうたい|りょうこう"},{"japanese":"両国の関係は今も良好です。","romaji":"Ryoukoku no kankei wa ima mo ryoukou desu.","german":"Relations between the two countries are still good.","furigana":"りょうこく|かんけい|いま|りょうこう"}],"notes":"良好 is a formal な-adjective for \"good, satisfactory\" and typically appears in reports, news and medical findings: 経過は良好です, 良好な関係. In everyday conversation people simply say いい or よい. It is often used predicatively with です.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"え、これ全自動なんですか？","romaji":"E, kore zen jidō na n desu ka?","german":"What, this is fully automatic?","furigana":"ぜんじどう"},{"japanese":"このドアは自動で開きます。","romaji":"Kono doa wa jidō de akimasu.","german":"This door opens automatically.","furigana":"じどう|あ"},{"japanese":"駅の前に自動販売機があります。","romaji":"Eki no mae ni jidou hanbaiki ga arimasu.","german":"There is a vending machine in front of the station.","furigana":"えき|まえ|じどうはんばいき"}],"notes":"自動 means \"automatic\" and is not a な-adjective: one says 自動で開く or 自動の機械; as an adverb also 自動的に. It appears in many compounds: 自動ドア, 自動販売機, 自動車, 全自動. The opposite is 手動 \"manual\". In grammar, 自動 is the short form of 自動詞 \"intransitive verb\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"消化不良で気持ち悪い。","romaji":"Shōka furyō de kimochi warui.","german":"I feel sick from indigestion.","furigana":"しょうかふりょう|きも|わる"},{"japanese":"食べ過ぎて消化不良になりました。","romaji":"Tabesugite shōkafuryō ni narimashita.","german":"I ate too much and got indigestion.","furigana":"た|す|しょうかふりょう"},{"japanese":"説明が多すぎて、消化不良のまま授業が終わりました。","romaji":"Setsumei ga oosugite, shouka furyou no mama jugyou ga owarimashita.","german":"There were so many explanations that class ended before I had absorbed everything.","furigana":"せつめい|おお|しょうかふりょう|じゅぎょう|お"}],"notes":"消化不良 is \"indigestion\": 消化不良になる, 消化不良を起こす. Figuratively it means that one has not properly absorbed material or information: 授業が消化不良のまま終わった. In everyday speech, for stomach trouble people also say お腹の調子が悪い or 胃がもたれる.","levelNote":"Compound: 消化 (N2) + 不良 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しく回転ずしのお店がオープンした。","romaji":"Atarashiku kaitenzushi no omise ga oopun shita.","german":"A new conveyor-belt sushi restaurant has opened.","furigana":"あたら|かいてん|みせ"},{"japanese":"家族で回転ずしに行きました。","romaji":"Kazoku de kaitenzushi ni ikimashita.","german":"We went to a conveyor-belt sushi restaurant as a family.","furigana":"かぞく|かいてん|い"},{"japanese":"この回転ずしは、どの皿も百円です。","romaji":"Kono kaitenzushi wa, dono sara mo hyakuen desu.","german":"At this conveyor-belt sushi place, every plate costs one hundred yen.","furigana":"かいてん|さら|ひゃくえん"}],"notes":"回転ずし is sushi from a conveyor belt, literally \"rotating sushi\"; 寿司 becomes voiced ずし here. It is also written 回転寿司. One says 回転ずしに行く and usually pays by plate (一皿百円). Colloquially also 回るお寿司.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"この店は九時に閉店します。","romaji":"Kono mise wa kuji ni heiten shimasu.","german":"This store closes at nine o'clock.","furigana":"みせ|くじ|へいてん"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"閉店前にパンを買いました。","romaji":"Heiten mae ni pan o kaimashita.","german":"I bought bread before closing time.","furigana":"へいてんまえ|か"},{"japanese":"駅前の本屋が、来月で閉店するそうだ。","romaji":"Ekimae no hon'ya ga, raigetsu de heiten suru sō da.","german":"I hear the bookstore in front of the station will close for good next month.","furigana":"えきまえ|ほんや|らいげつ|へいてん"}],"notes":"閉店 means two things: the daily closing of a shop (閉店時間, 閉店は九時) and the permanent closure of a business (来月閉店する, 閉店セール). As a する-verb it is mostly intransitive, with the shop as the topic: この店は九時に閉店する. The opposite is 開店 \"opening, grand opening\". In conversation, for the daily closing one often simply says 店が閉まる; 閉店 sounds more businesslike and typically appears on signs and in announcements.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このドアは自動で開閉します。","romaji":"Kono doa wa jidō de kaihei shimasu.","german":"This door opens and closes automatically.","furigana":"じどう|かいへい"},{"japanese":"係員が一日に何度も窓を開閉した。","romaji":"Kakariin ga ichinichi ni nando mo mado o kaihei shita.","german":"The attendant opened and closed the window many times a day.","furigana":"かかりいん|いちにち|なんど|まど|かいへい"},{"japanese":"電車のドアの開閉にご注意ください。","romaji":"Densha no doa no kaihei ni go-chūi kudasai.","german":"Please be careful of the train doors opening and closing.","furigana":"でんしゃ|かいへい|ちゅうい"}],"notes":"開閉 combines \"to open and close\" in one word and is a する-verb of written and technical language, for example in instructions and announcements: ドアの開閉にご注意ください. It can be used both transitively (窓を開閉する) and intransitively (ドアが自動で開閉する). Common in compounds: 開閉式の屋根 (\"retractable roof\"), 開閉ボタン. In everyday speech people prefer 開け閉め (あけしめ): 窓の開け閉め.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"よかったら、今から俺らとカラオケ行かない？","romaji":"Yokattara, ima kara orera to karaoke ikanai?","german":"If you feel like it, how about going to karaoke with us now?","furigana":"いま|おれ|い"},{"japanese":"俺らで何とかしよう。","romaji":"Orera de nantoka shiyō.","german":"Let's sort it out ourselves somehow.","furigana":"おれ|なん"},{"japanese":"俺らの学校は駅からかなり遠いんだ。","romaji":"Orera no gakkou wa eki kara kanari tooi n da.","german":"Our school is pretty far from the station.","furigana":"おれ|がっこう|えき|とお"}],"notes":"俺ら is the casual masculine form for \"we, us\", formed from 俺 and the casual plural suffix ら. It sounds rougher than 俺たち and much more informal than 僕ら or 私たち; it is used only among friends. Rarely, it also simply means \"I\" (dialectal).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あそこに井戸があるよ。","romaji":"Asoko ni ido ga aru yo.","german":"There's a well over there.","furigana":"いど"},{"japanese":"昔はこの井戸から水を汲みました。","romaji":"Mukashi wa kono ido kara mizu o kumimashita.","german":"In the old days we drew water from this well.","furigana":"むかし|いど|みず|く"},{"japanese":"井戸の水は夏でも冷たいです。","romaji":"Ido no mizu wa natsu demo tsumetai desu.","german":"The water from the well is cold even in summer.","furigana":"いど|みず|なつ|つめ"}],"notes":"井戸 is a \"well\" for drawing water, not an ornamental fountain (that would be 噴水). Typical: 井戸を掘る, 井戸の水 (井戸水), 井戸から水をくむ. The expression 井戸端会議 \"chat at the well\" denotes gossip among neighbors.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は相手のペースに合わせるのが上手いよね。","romaji":"Kare wa aite no pēsu ni awaseru no ga umai yo ne.","german":"He's good at adapting to the other person's pace, isn't he?","furigana":"かれ|あいて|あ|うま"},{"japanese":"時計を正確な時間に合わせました。","romaji":"Tokei o seikaku na jikan ni awasemashita.","german":"I set the clock to the correct time.","furigana":"とけい|せいかく|じかん|あ"},{"japanese":"みんなで力を合わせて頑張りましょう。","romaji":"Minna de chikara o awasete ganbarimashou.","german":"Let's all join forces and do our best.","furigana":"ちから|あ|がんば"}],"notes":"合わせる is the transitive counterpart of 合う \"to fit\": \"to adjust, to coordinate, to put together\". With に you state what one adjusts to: 相手に合わせる, 時計を時間に合わせる. Set phrases: 力を合わせる \"to work together\", 手を合わせる \"to put one's hands together\", 間に合わせる \"to make it in time\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"相手の動きをよく見て！","romaji":"Aite no ugoki o yoku mite!","german":"Watch your opponent's movements closely!","furigana":"あいて|うご|み"},{"japanese":"猫の動きが速いです。","romaji":"Neko no ugoki ga hayai desu.","german":"The cat's movements are fast.","furigana":"ねこ|うご|はや"},{"japanese":"毎朝ニュースで物価の動きをチェックしています。","romaji":"Maiasa nyuusu de bukka no ugoki o chekku shite imasu.","german":"Every morning I check price trends in the news.","furigana":"まいあさ|ぶっか|うご"}],"notes":"動き is the noun of 動く: the \"movement\" of a body or object (猫の動き) and, figuratively, \"development, trend\" (物価の動き, 新しい動き). Typical: 動きが速い／遅い, 動きを見る, 動きがない \"nothing is happening\". For athletic movement or physical exercise, 運動 is used instead.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"周りがギザギザになっているコインを集めています。","romaji":"Mawari ga gizagiza ni natte iru koin o atsumete imasu.","german":"I collect coins with serrated edges.","furigana":"まわ|あつ"},{"japanese":"周りを見てから道を渡りましょう。","romaji":"Mawari o mite kara michi o watarimashō.","german":"Let's look around before crossing the street.","furigana":"まわ|み|みち|わた"},{"japanese":"周りの人に迷惑をかけないようにしましょう。","romaji":"Mawari no hito ni meiwaku o kakenai you ni shimashou.","german":"Let's be careful not to bother the people around us.","furigana":"まわ|ひと|めいわく"}],"notes":"周り is a noun: \"surroundings\" (家の周り), \"circumference, edge\" (池の周り) and often \"the people around one\" (周りの人). Place expressions are formed with 〜の周りに／で. It is the everyday word; 周囲 is more formal. It is also written 回り; 身の回り means \"personal belongings, one's immediate surroundings\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 30"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このプールの深さを教えて下さい。","romaji":"Kono pūru no fukasa o oshiete kudasai.","german":"Please tell me the depth of this pool.","furigana":"ふか|おし|くだ"},{"japanese":"この湖の深さは五十メートルです。","romaji":"Kono mizuumi no fukasa wa gojū mētoru desu.","german":"The depth of this lake is fifty meters.","furigana":"みずうみ|ふか|ごじゅう"},{"japanese":"大人になってから、先生の言葉の深さが分かりました。","romaji":"Otona ni natte kara, sensei no kotoba no fukasa ga wakarimashita.","german":"Only after I became an adult did I understand the depth of my teacher's words.","furigana":"おとな|せんせい|ことば|ふか|わ"}],"notes":"深さ is the noun for 深い and refers to the measurable \"depth\": 深さは五メートル, 深さを測る. Figuratively it also means the \"depth\" of feelings or thoughts: 愛の深さ. The variant 深み stresses more a quality (taste, character) and is not measurable.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の生理周期はいつも大体２８日です。","romaji":"Watashi no seiri shuuki wa itsumo daitai nijuuhachinichi desu.","german":"My menstrual cycle is always about 28 days.","furigana":"わたし|せいりしゅうき|だいたい|にち"},{"japanese":"流行には周期があると言われています。","romaji":"Ryuukou ni wa shuuki ga aru to iwarete imasu.","german":"Fashions are said to go in cycles.","furigana":"りゅうこう|しゅうき|い"},{"japanese":"地球は一年の周期で太陽の周りを回っています。","romaji":"Chikyuu wa ichinen no shuuki de taiyou no mawari o mawatte imasu.","german":"The Earth orbits the Sun in a cycle of one year.","furigana":"ちきゅう|いちねん|しゅうき|たいよう|まわ|まわ"}],"notes":"周期 is the \"cycle, period\" of a regularly recurring process: 生理周期, 公転周期, 〜年の周期で. It sounds technical or scientific. For intervals between buses or appointments, 間隔 is used instead; for a single lap, 一周.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"待合室で待ってるね！","romaji":"Machiaishitsu de matteru ne!","german":"I'll wait in the waiting room!","furigana":"まちあいしつ|ま"},{"japanese":"駅の待合室で友達を待ちました。","romaji":"Eki no machiaishitsu de tomodachi o machimashita.","german":"I waited for my friend in the station waiting room.","furigana":"えき|まちあいしつ|ともだち|ま"},{"japanese":"病院の待合室はとても混んでいました。","romaji":"Byouin no machiaishitsu wa totemo konde imashita.","german":"The hospital waiting room was very crowded.","furigana":"びょういん|まちあいしつ|こ"}],"notes":"待合 on its own is a short form; almost always 待合室 \"waiting room, waiting hall\" is used, in doctors' offices, hospitals and stations. One waits 待合室で. The spelling 待ち合い室 also occurs. Do not confuse it with 待ち合わせ \"appointment, meeting point\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"声は子どもの声だったが、言葉つきはまるで大人だった。","romaji":"Koe wa kodomo no koe datta ga, kotobatsuki wa marude otona datta.","german":"The voice was a child's, but the way of speaking was completely adult.","furigana":"こえ|こ|こえ|ことば|おとな"},{"japanese":"彼の言葉つきは丁寧です。","romaji":"Kare no kotobatsuki wa teinei desu.","german":"His manner of speaking is polite.","furigana":"かれ|ことば|ていねい"},{"japanese":"彼は怒ると、急に言葉つきが乱暴になる。","romaji":"Kare wa okoru to, kyuu ni kotobatsuki ga ranbou ni naru.","german":"When he gets angry, his way of speaking suddenly turns rough.","furigana":"かれ|おこ|きゅう|ことば|らんぼう"}],"notes":"言葉つき (also written 言葉付き) describes how someone speaks, the tone and manner that come through in speech: 丁寧な言葉つき \"polite manner of speaking\", 乱暴な言葉つき \"rough manner of speaking\", 言葉つきが変わる. It is rather literary; in daily life people more often say 口調 \"tone of voice\" or 言葉遣い \"choice of words, language use\". 言葉遣い stresses the chosen words (keigo or not), 言葉つき more the impression the way of speaking makes.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"母の具合があまりよくないんです。","romaji":"Haha no guai ga amari yokunai n desu.","german":"My mother isn't feeling very well.","furigana":"はは|ぐあい"},{"japanese":"今日は具合が悪いです。","romaji":"Kyō wa guai ga warui desu.","german":"I don't feel well today.","furigana":"きょう|ぐあい|わる"},{"japanese":"紙をこんな具合に折ってください。","romaji":"Kami o konna guai ni otte kudasai.","german":"Please fold the paper like this.","furigana":"かみ|ぐあい|お"}],"notes":"具合 refers to the condition of something, but above all to one's state of health: 具合が悪い \"to feel unwell, to be sick\", politely お具合はいかがですか. Devices also have a 具合: パソコンの具合がおかしい \"something is wrong with the PC\". In こんな具合に / そういう具合に it means \"in this way\". 調子 is similar, but stresses form and performance (調子がいい); 気分が悪い means more nausea or a bad mood.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 46"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"それは意図的に作り出されたプロパガンダの画像じゃないでしょうか。","romaji":"Sore wa itoteki ni tsukuridasareta puropaganda no gazō ja nai deshō ka.","german":"Isn't that an image of propaganda that was created deliberately?","furigana":"いとてき|つく|だ|がぞう"},{"japanese":"意図的に無視しました。","romaji":"Itoteki ni mushi shimashita.","german":"I ignored it on purpose.","furigana":"いとてき|むし"}],"notes":"意図的 describes something that happens on purpose. Before a noun it takes な, as in 意図的な行動; as an adverbial it takes に: 意図的に無視する. It stresses the contrast with an oversight. The question in the first example expresses a conjecture about how the image came about, not an established fact.","levelNote":"Compound: 意図 (N1) + 的 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"とても近代的ですね。","romaji":"Totemo kindaiteki desu ne.","german":"This is really modern, isn't it?","furigana":"きんだいてき"},{"japanese":"この建物は近代的なデザインです。","romaji":"Kono tatemono wa kindaiteki na dezain desu.","german":"This building has a modern design.","furigana":"たてもの|きんだいてき"},{"japanese":"昔の小さな村が、近代的な町に変わりました。","romaji":"Mukashi no chiisana mura ga, kindaiteki na machi ni kawarimashita.","german":"The small village of old has turned into a modern town.","furigana":"むかし|ちい|むら|きんだいてき|まち|か"}],"notes":"近代的 is a な-adjective: 近代的な建物, 近代的に発展する. It means \"modern\" in the sense of \"modernized, up to date\", often in contrast to traditional or old-fashioned - typical for cities, buildings, facilities and ways of thinking. 近代 is the modern era (in Japan from the Meiji period on); 現代的 emphasizes \"contemporary, of today\", and モダン is often used for stylish, modern design.","levelNote":"Compound: 近代 (N3) + 的 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私が合図をしたら、大きくジャンプをしてください。","romaji":"Watashi ga aizu o shitara, ōkiku janpu o shite kudasai.","german":"When I give the signal, please jump high.","furigana":"わたし|あいず|おお"},{"japanese":"先生が合図を出しました。","romaji":"Sensei ga aizu o dashimashita.","german":"The teacher gave the signal.","furigana":"せんせい|あいず|だ"},{"japanese":"出発の合図を待ちましょう。","romaji":"Shuppatsu no aizu o machimashō.","german":"Let's wait for the signal to depart.","furigana":"しゅっぱつ|あいず|ま"},{"japanese":"相図をしたら、スクワットを始めて下さい。","romaji":"Aizu o shitara, sukuwatto o hajimete kudasai.","german":"When I give the signal, please start the squats.","furigana":"あいず|はじ|くだ"},{"japanese":"合図を送ってください。","romaji":"Aizu o okutte kudasai.","german":"Please send the signal.","furigana":"あいず|おく"}],"notes":"合図 is an agreed-upon sign or signal. 合図をする and 合図を出す mean to give a signal; 合図を待つ means to wait for it. The bare form is a noun that is also used with する. In the first example the action begins only after the signal. 相図 is a rare spelling of the same word.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"本能的にヤバイって感じたんだよね。","romaji":"Honnōteki ni yabai tte kanjita n da yo ne.","german":"I instinctively felt that it was bad news.","furigana":"ほんのうてき|かん"},{"japanese":"動物は本能で行動します。","romaji":"Dōbutsu wa honnō de kōdō shimasu.","german":"Animals act on instinct.","furigana":"どうぶつ|ほんのう|こうどう"},{"japanese":"子どもを守るのは母親の本能です。","romaji":"Kodomo o mamoru no wa hahaoya no honnou desu.","german":"Protecting one's children is a mother's instinct.","furigana":"こ|まも|ははおや|ほんのう"}],"notes":"本能 is a noun: \"instinct, innate drive\". Typical are 本能で行動する \"to act instinctively\" and the adverb 本能的に \"instinctively\"; also compounds such as 母性本能 \"maternal instinct\" or 生存本能 \"survival instinct\". 直感, on the other hand, is \"intuition, gut feeling\" in a specific situation, while 本能 means the innate behavior of humans and animals.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"明日は運動会です。","romaji":"Ashita wa undōkai desu.","german":"Tomorrow is sports day.","furigana":"あした|うんどうかい"},{"japanese":"秋に運動会があります。","romaji":"Aki ni undōkai ga arimasu.","german":"There is a sports day in autumn.","furigana":"あき|うんどうかい"},{"japanese":"運動会で息子がリレーに出ました。","romaji":"Undoukai de musuko ga rirei ni demashita.","german":"At sports day, my son ran in the relay.","furigana":"うんどうかい|むすこ|で"}],"notes":"運動会 is the sports day at Japanese kindergartens and schools, usually in autumn (sometimes in spring), with relay races, tug-of-war and 玉入れ; families come to watch and bring bento. One says 運動会がある, 運動会に出る, 運動会で走る. At middle and high schools the event is often called 体育祭; companies and communities also hold 運動会.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 26"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"シャワーの水が温まるまで、ここで待ちます。","romaji":"Shawā no mizu ga atatamaru made, koko de machimasu.","german":"I'll wait here until the shower water gets warm.","furigana":"みず|あたた|ま"},{"japanese":"お風呂に入って温まりました。","romaji":"Ofuro ni haitte atatamarimashita.","german":"I got into the bath and warmed up.","furigana":"ふろ|はい|あたた"},{"japanese":"その話を聞いて、心が温まりました。","romaji":"Sono hanashi o kiite, kokoro ga atatamarimashita.","german":"Hearing that story warmed my heart.","furigana":"はなし|き|こころ|あたた"}],"notes":"温まる is intransitive: something or someone gets warm (体が温まる, スープが温まる); the transitive counterpart is 温める \"to warm up\" (スープを温める). For rooms, air and weather one usually writes 暖まる (部屋が暖まる), for liquids, food and the body 温まる. Figuratively: 心が温まる \"it warms one's heart\", 心温まる話 \"heartwarming story\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「赤ずきん」には悪い狼が出てきます。","romaji":"'Akazukin' ni wa warui ookami ga dete kimasu.","german":"There is a bad wolf in \"Little Red Riding Hood\".","furigana":"あか|わる|おおかみ|で"},{"japanese":"森に狼がいました。","romaji":"Mori ni ōkami ga imashita.","german":"There was a wolf in the forest.","furigana":"もり|おおかみ"},{"japanese":"彼はクラスの一匹狼です。","romaji":"Kare wa kurasu no ippiki ookami desu.","german":"He is the lone wolf of the class.","furigana":"かれ|いっぴきおおかみ"}],"notes":"狼 is the wolf; in Japan the native wolf (ニホンオオカミ) is extinct, and in zoo and nonfiction texts オオカミ is often written in katakana. Wolves howl: 狼が遠吠えする. Fixed expressions: 一匹狼 \"lone wolf, loner\" and, colloquially, 狼 or 送り狼 for a man who is after women. They are counted with 匹 or 頭.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"また狼男が出たらしい。","romaji":"Mata ookamiotoko ga deta rashii.","german":"A werewolf has apparently shown up again.","furigana":"おおかみおとこ|で"},{"japanese":"狼男の映画を見ました。","romaji":"Ōkamiotoko no eiga o mimashita.","german":"I watched a werewolf movie.","furigana":"おおかみおとこ|えいが|み"},{"japanese":"狼男は満月の夜に変身すると言われています。","romaji":"Ookamiotoko wa mangetsu no yoru ni henshin suru to iwarete imasu.","german":"It is said that werewolves transform on full-moon nights.","furigana":"おおかみおとこ|まんげつ|よる|へんしん|い"}],"notes":"狼男 is the werewolf of Western legends and horror films, who turns into a wolf on full-moon nights (満月の夜に狼に変身する). The word is mostly encountered in movies, games and Halloween costumes. For a man who pursues women, one does not say 狼男 but simply 狼.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの男の人は、有名なベストセラー作家です。","romaji":"Ano otoko no hito wa, yūmei na besutoserā sakka desu.","german":"That man is a famous bestselling author.","furigana":"おとこ|ひと|ゆうめい|さっか"},{"japanese":"将来作家になりたいです。","romaji":"Shōrai sakka ni naritai desu.","german":"I want to become a writer in the future.","furigana":"しょうらいさっか"},{"japanese":"好きな作家の新しい本が出ました。","romaji":"Suki na sakka no atarashii hon ga demashita.","german":"A new book by my favorite author has come out.","furigana":"す|さっか|あたら|ほん|で"}],"notes":"作家 is a writer or author as a profession (人気作家, 作家になる); in the arts also an artist (陶芸作家 \"ceramic artist\"). 小説家 is specifically \"novelist\". The author of a particular work is 作者 (この本の作者), the author of the present text, formally, 筆者. Manga artists, by contrast, are 漫画家.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 49"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼はクラシック音楽を好む人です。","romaji":"Kare wa kurashikku ongaku o konomu hito desu.","german":"He is a person who prefers classical music.","furigana":"かれ|おんがく|この|ひと"},{"japanese":"静かな場所を好みます。","romaji":"Shizuka na basho o konomimasu.","german":"I prefer quiet places.","furigana":"しず|ばしょ|この"},{"japanese":"この料理は若い人に好まれています。","romaji":"Kono ryouri wa wakai hito ni konomarete imasu.","german":"This dish is popular with young people.","furigana":"りょうり|わか|ひと|この"}],"notes":"好む is transitive (X を好む) and much more literary than 好き; in conversation one says 静かな所が好きです instead of 静かな所を好みます. Common are the passive 好まれる \"to be popular\" (若い人に好まれる), the negative 好まない, and 好んで～する \"to do something gladly, of one's own will\". Related are the noun 好み \"taste, preference\" and お好み焼き.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"色んな人がいるので、様々な意見が出るのは当たり前です。","romaji":"Ironna hito ga iru node, samazama na iken ga deru no wa atarimae desu.","german":"Since there are all kinds of people, it is only natural that different opinions come up.","furigana":"いろ|ひと|さまざま|いけん|で|あ|まえ"},{"japanese":"様々な国の料理が食べられます。","romaji":"Samazama na kuni no ryōri ga taberaremasu.","german":"You can eat dishes from various countries.","furigana":"さまざま|くに|りょうり|た"},{"japanese":"好きな食べ物は人によって様々です。","romaji":"Suki na tabemono wa hito ni yotte samazama desu.","german":"Favorite foods differ from person to person.","furigana":"す|た|もの|ひと|さまざま"}],"notes":"様々 is a な-adjective: 様々な国, 様々な意見; as a predicate 人によって様々だ \"it differs from person to person\". It means the same as いろいろ or, colloquially, 色んな, but sounds a little more formal and is typical of writing, news and talks. 々 is the repetition mark; さまざま is also often written in hiragana.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このバンドのボーカルは、福島の出身です。","romaji":"Kono bando no bōkaru wa, Fukushima no shusshin desu.","german":"This band's vocalist is from Fukushima.","furigana":"ふくしま|しゅっしん"},{"japanese":"福島の果物は美味しいです。","romaji":"Fukushima no kudamono wa oishii desu.","german":"The fruit from Fukushima is delicious.","furigana":"ふくしま|くだもの|おい"},{"japanese":"夏休みに福島県の祖母の家に行きます。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi ni Fukushima-ken no sobo no ie ni ikimasu.","german":"In summer vacation I'm going to my grandmother's house in Fukushima Prefecture.","furigana":"なつやす|ふくしまけん|そぼ|いえ|い"}],"notes":"福島 is a proper name: the prefecture 福島県 in the Tōhoku region and its capital 福島市; also a common family name. To distinguish, one says 福島県 or 福島市. Fukushima is known for peaches and other fruit, sake and onsen; internationally also for the nuclear accident of 2011 (福島第一原発).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仏教は古代インドで始まりました。","romaji":"Bukkyou wa kodai Indo de hajimarimashita.","german":"Buddhism began in ancient India.","furigana":"ぶっきょう|こだい|はじ"},{"japanese":"仏教の教えについて学びました。","romaji":"Bukkyō no oshie ni tsuite manabimashita.","german":"I learned about the teachings of Buddhism.","furigana":"ぶっきょう|おし|まな"},{"japanese":"日本では、お葬式を仏教のやり方でする家が多いです。","romaji":"Nihon de wa, osoushiki o bukkyou no yarikata de suru ie ga ooi desu.","german":"In Japan, many families hold funerals in the Buddhist way.","furigana":"にほん|そうしき|ぶっきょう|かた|いえ|おお"}],"notes":"仏教 is Buddhism; a Buddhist is 仏教徒, Buddhist temples are called お寺 (as opposed to the Shintō shrines 神社). Buddhism came to Japan via Korea in the 6th century. Many Japanese combine both religions: weddings and New Year are often Shintō, funerals mostly Buddhist (仏式).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その大仏の高さは約15メートルです。","romaji":"Sono daibutsu no takasa wa yaku juugo meetoru desu.","german":"That great Buddha statue is about 15 meters tall.","furigana":"だいぶつ|たか|やく"},{"japanese":"鎌倉の大仏を見に行きましょう。","romaji":"Kamakura no daibutsu o mi ni ikimashō.","german":"Let's go see the Great Buddha in Kamakura.","furigana":"かまくら|だいぶつ|み|い"},{"japanese":"奈良の大仏は東大寺にあります。","romaji":"Nara no daibutsu wa Toudaiji ni arimasu.","german":"The Great Buddha of Nara is at Tōdai-ji Temple.","furigana":"なら|だいぶつ|とうだいじ"}],"notes":"大仏 is a large Buddha statue, usually with a place name: 奈良の大仏 at the temple 東大寺 and 鎌倉の大仏 are the best known. A Buddha statue in general is 仏像; 大仏 are only the especially large ones. One says 大仏を見る, 大仏にお参りする.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 37"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の母は信心深い人です。","romaji":"Watashi no haha wa shinjinbukai hito desu.","german":"My mother is a very devout person.","furigana":"わたし|はは|しんじんぶか|ひと"},{"japanese":"「鰯の頭も信心から」ということわざがあります。","romaji":"'Iwashi no atama mo shinjin kara' to iu kotowaza ga arimasu.","german":"There is a proverb, \"Even a sardine head becomes sacred through faith\".","furigana":"|あたま|しんじん"},{"japanese":"若い人は昔ほど信心がありません。","romaji":"Wakai hito wa mukashi hodo shinjin ga arimasen.","german":"Young people are not as devout as they used to be.","furigana":"わか|ひと|むかし|しんじん"}],"notes":"信心 is religious faith, piety toward gods or Buddha. It is most often encountered in 信心深い（しんじんぶかい）\"pious, devout\" and in the proverb 鰯の頭も信心から. More general and formal is 信仰 \"faith, religion\" (信仰する); trust in people, by contrast, is 信用 or 信頼.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"正直、その店員の言葉には、少し不信感を持ちました。","romaji":"Shoujiki, sono ten'in no kotoba ni wa, sukoshi fushinkan o mochimashita.","german":"Honestly, I felt a little distrust toward that shop assistant's words.","furigana":"しょうじき|てんいん|ことば|すこ|ふしんかん|も"},{"japanese":"政治家への不信が高まっています。","romaji":"Seijika e no fushin ga takamatte imasu.","german":"Distrust of politicians is growing.","furigana":"せいじか|ふしん|たか"},{"japanese":"何度もうそをつかれて、人間不信になりました。","romaji":"Nando mo uso o tsukarete, ningen fushin ni narimashita.","german":"After being lied to so many times, I came to distrust people.","furigana":"なんど|にんげんふしん"}],"notes":"不信 is a noun: \"distrust, disbelief\". It rarely stands alone, but mostly in compounds: 不信感 \"feeling of distrust\" (不信感を持つ／抱く), 政治不信 \"disillusionment with politics\", 人間不信 \"distrust of people\". Beware of the identically pronounced 不審 \"suspicious\" (不審な人 \"suspicious person\"). In daily life, people often simply say 信じられない or 信用できない.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いや、まだ返信してない。","romaji":"Iya, mada henshin shitenai.","german":"No, I haven't replied yet.","furigana":"へんしん"},{"japanese":"メールの返信をお願いします。","romaji":"Mēru no henshin o onegai shimasu.","german":"Please reply to the email.","furigana":"へんしん|ねが"},{"japanese":"返信が遅くなってすみません。","romaji":"Henshin ga osoku natte sumimasen.","german":"Sorry for the late reply.","furigana":"へんしん|おそ"}],"notes":"返信 is the written reply to an email, letter or message: メールに返信する, 返信が来る, 返信を待つ. 返事 is more general and also applies to spoken replies (\"Yes!\" in response to a call); 答え is the answer to a question, 回答 to a survey or inquiry. Not to be confused with the homophone 変身 \"transformation\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この地区はファミリー向けで住みやすいです。","romaji":"Kono chiku wa famirii-muke de sumiyasui desu.","german":"This district is family-oriented and comfortable to live in.","furigana":"ちく|む|す"},{"japanese":"この地区は静かです。","romaji":"Kono chiku wa shizuka desu.","german":"This district is quiet.","furigana":"ちく|しず"},{"japanese":"野球部が地区大会で優勝しました。","romaji":"Yakyuubu ga chiku taikai de yuushou shimashita.","german":"The baseball club won the district tournament.","furigana":"やきゅうぶ|ちくたいかい|ゆうしょう"}],"notes":"地区 is a noun for a delimited area, neighborhood or district, often with a function: 住宅地区 \"residential area\", 商業地区 \"business district\", 地区大会 \"district tournament\". It sounds somewhat official. 地域 is broader and means \"area, region, community\", 地方 \"region, province\"; 区 is the administrative ward of a large city (新宿区).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本語の単語学習には、ワニカニがいいですよ。","romaji":"Nihongo no tango gakushuu ni wa, WaniKani ga ii desu yo.","german":"WaniKani is good for studying Japanese vocabulary.","furigana":"にほんご|たんごがくしゅう"},{"japanese":"毎日新しい単語を覚えます。","romaji":"Mainichi atarashii tango o oboemasu.","german":"I learn new words every day.","furigana":"まいにちあたら|たんご|おぼ"},{"japanese":"この単語の意味が分かりません。","romaji":"Kono tango no imi ga wakarimasen.","german":"I don't understand the meaning of this word.","furigana":"たんご|いみ|わ"}],"notes":"単語 is the individual word as a unit of learning: 単語を覚える \"to learn vocabulary\", 英単語 \"English vocabulary word\", 単語帳 \"vocabulary notebook\". 言葉 is much broader (\"word, language, utterance\"), and 語彙 (\"vocabulary\") means the whole stock of words. When asking about the meaning of a word, you say この単語の意味 or simply この言葉の意味.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 9"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この町には変人が多いらしい。","romaji":"Kono machi ni wa henjin ga ooi rashii.","german":"There seem to be a lot of oddballs in this town.","furigana":"まち|へんじん|おお"},{"japanese":"彼は少し変人です。","romaji":"Kare wa sukoshi henjin desu.","german":"He is a bit of an oddball.","furigana":"かれ|すこ|へんじん"},{"japanese":"天才はよく変人だと言われます。","romaji":"Tensai wa yoku henjin da to iwaremasu.","german":"Geniuses are often said to be eccentrics.","furigana":"てんさい|へんじん|い"}],"notes":"変人 refers to an oddball or eccentric, slightly derogatory, but among friends also jokingly affectionate. More neutral are 変わった人 or 変わり者; 変な人 is the plain description \"strange person\" and can also mean \"suspicious person\". Typical: 変人扱いされる \"to be treated as a weirdo\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"山の中で男性の変死体が見つかりました。","romaji":"Yama no naka de dansei no henshitai ga mitsukarimashita.","german":"The body of a man was found in the mountains under unexplained circumstances.","furigana":"やま|なか|だんせい|へんしたい|み"},{"japanese":"変死の原因を調査しています。","romaji":"Henshi no gen'in o chōsa shite imasu.","german":"The cause of the unnatural death is being investigated.","furigana":"へんし|げんいん|ちょうさ"},{"japanese":"その歌手は旅行先で変死しました。","romaji":"Sono kashu wa ryokousaki de henshi shimashita.","german":"The singer died under mysterious circumstances while traveling.","furigana":"かしゅ|りょこうさき|へんし"}],"notes":"変死 is a term from police and news reports for an unnatural or unexplained death (accident, suicide, crime or unknown cause). Common are 変死体 \"body found under unexplained circumstances\" and, in writing, 変死を遂げる; 変死する is intransitive. In everyday speech one rather says 不自然な死に方 or names the cause directly (事故で亡くなる).","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"上海事変は何年に起こりましたか。","romaji":"Shanhai jihen wa nannen ni okorimashita ka.","german":"In what year did the Shanghai Incident occur?","furigana":"|なんねん|お"},{"japanese":"歴史の授業で満州事変について学びました。","romaji":"Rekishi no jugyou de Manshuu jihen ni tsuite manabimashita.","german":"In history class, we learned about the Manchurian Incident.","furigana":"れきし|じゅぎょう|まんしゅうじへん|まな"},{"japanese":"当時、日本政府は戦争を「事変」と呼びました。","romaji":"Touji, Nihon seifu wa sensou o 'jihen' to yobimashita.","german":"At that time, the Japanese government called the war an \"incident\".","furigana":"とうじ|にほんせいふ|せんそう|じへん|よ"}],"notes":"事変 is a historical term for an armed conflict or incident without a formal declaration of war, above all in names such as 満州事変 (Mukden Incident, 1931) or 上海事変. The Japanese government of the time euphemistically called wars 事変. In everyday speech, 事件 \"incident, crime\", 事故 \"accident\" or 出来事 \"event\" are used instead.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"春休みには家族で日本に行く予定です。","romaji":"Haruyasumi ni wa kazoku de Nippon ni iku yotei desu.","german":"During spring break, we're planning to go to Japan with the family.","furigana":"はるやす|かぞく|にっぽん|い|よてい"},{"japanese":"春休みに旅行します。","romaji":"Haruyasumi ni ryokō shimasu.","german":"I'm traveling during spring break.","furigana":"はるやす|りょこう"},{"japanese":"春休みが終わったら、新しい学年が始まります。","romaji":"Haruyasumi ga owattara, atarashii gakunen ga hajimarimasu.","german":"When spring break is over, the new school year begins.","furigana":"はるやす|お|あたら|がくねん|はじ"}],"notes":"春休み is the spring vacation; in Japan it falls between two school years (roughly the end of March to the beginning of April), and at universities it lasts from February to March. One says 春休みに～する or 春休み中. Parallel to it there are 夏休み and 冬休み; time off from work, by contrast, is 休暇 or 休み.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"昨今は、SNS中毒の人が多くなっています。","romaji":"Sakkon wa, SNS chūdoku no hito ga ōku natte imasu.","german":"These days, more and more people are addicted to social media.","furigana":"さっこん|ちゅうどく|ひと|おお"},{"japanese":"昨今の経済状況は厳しいです。","romaji":"Sakkon no keizai jōkyō wa kibishii desu.","german":"The economic situation these days is tough.","furigana":"さっこん|けいざいじょうきょう|きび"},{"japanese":"物が高い昨今、自分で弁当を作る人が増えています。","romaji":"Mono ga takai sakkon, jibun de bentou o tsukuru hito ga fuete imasu.","german":"In these times when everything is expensive, more and more people make their own bento.","furigana":"もの|たか|さっこん|じぶん|べんとう|つく|ひと|ふ"}],"notes":"昨今 is a formal, literary word for \"nowadays, recently\"; it is used as a noun (昨今の経済状況, 昨今は) and often in news, speeches and letters, with a critical undertone about current trends. In conversation, people say 最近, この頃 or 近頃.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今晩は。こんな所で何してるの？","romaji":"Konbanwa. Konna tokoro de nani shiteru no?","german":"Good evening. What are you doing in a place like this?","furigana":"こんばん|ところ|なに"},{"japanese":"今晩は、お元気ですか。","romaji":"Konbanwa, ogenki desu ka.","german":"Good evening, how are you?","furigana":"こんばん|げんき"},{"japanese":"夜、近所の人に会ったら「今晩は」とあいさつします。","romaji":"Yoru, kinjo no hito ni attara 'konbanwa' to aisatsu shimasu.","german":"When I meet neighbors in the evening, I greet them with \"Good evening\".","furigana":"よる|きんじょ|ひと|あ|こんばん"}],"notes":"今晩は is the greeting in the evening, once it is dark; it is mostly written in hiragana こんばんは, and the は is pronounced \"wa\" (the spelling こんばんわ is considered wrong). It is used to greet acquaintances, neighbors or guests, but not one's own family. When parting in the evening, one does not say 今晩は but お休みなさい or 失礼します.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 0"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は食中毒で入院することになりました。","romaji":"Kare wa shokuchuudoku de nyuuin suru koto ni narimashita.","german":"He ended up being hospitalized for food poisoning.","furigana":"かれ|しょくちゅうどく|にゅういん"},{"japanese":"食中毒に気をつけてください。","romaji":"Shokuchūdoku ni ki o tsukete kudasai.","german":"Please be careful about food poisoning.","furigana":"しょくちゅうどく|き"},{"japanese":"弟はゲーム中毒で、一日中ゲームをしています。","romaji":"Otouto wa geemu chuudoku de, ichinichijuu geemu o shite imasu.","german":"My younger brother is addicted to video games and plays them all day.","furigana":"おとうと|ちゅうどく|いちにちじゅう"}],"notes":"中毒 is first of all poisoning: 食中毒 \"food poisoning\", 一酸化炭素中毒 \"carbon monoxide poisoning\"; you say 中毒になる or 中毒を起こす, 中毒する is rare. As a suffix it colloquially means \"-addiction\": ゲーム中毒, スマホ中毒, アルコール中毒. In formal medical texts, addiction is more often 依存症 (アルコール依存症).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ガラガラヘビって有毒なんですか？","romaji":"Garagarahebi tte yūdoku na n desu ka?","german":"Are rattlesnakes poisonous?","furigana":"ゆうどく"},{"japanese":"このきのこは有毒です。","romaji":"Kono kinoko wa yūdoku desu.","german":"This mushroom is poisonous.","furigana":"ゆうどく"},{"japanese":"火事の煙には有毒なガスが含まれています。","romaji":"Kaji no kemuri ni wa yuudoku na gasu ga fukumarete imasu.","german":"Smoke from fires contains toxic gases.","furigana":"かじ|けむり|ゆうどく|ふく"}],"notes":"有毒 is a formal, technical な-adjective or noun for \"poisonous, toxic\": 有毒な植物, 有毒ガス, 有毒物質; the opposite is 無毒 \"non-toxic\". In daily life people mostly say 毒がある (このきのこには毒がある). It appears mainly in warnings, news and nonfiction texts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼のオナラはいつも毒ガスのようにくさい。","romaji":"Kare no onara wa itsumo dokugasu no yō ni kusai.","german":"His farts always stink like poison gas.","furigana":"かれ|どく"},{"japanese":"毒ガスは非常に危険です。","romaji":"Dokugasu wa hijō ni kiken desu.","german":"Poison gas is extremely dangerous.","furigana":"どく|ひじょう|きけん"},{"japanese":"戦争で毒ガスが使われたことがあります。","romaji":"Sensou de dokugasu ga tsukawareta koto ga arimasu.","german":"Poison gas has been used in wars.","furigana":"せんそう|どく|つか"}],"notes":"毒ガス is \"poison gas\", above all as a chemical weapon (毒ガス兵器) or in historical and news contexts. For toxic gases from accidents, fires or volcanoes, 有毒ガス is more common; but foul smells are also jokingly compared to 毒ガス. ガス (from Dutch) is written in katakana.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ビザが切れたあとも日本にいると、不法滞在になります。","romaji":"Biza ga kireta ato mo Nihon ni iru to, fuhou taizai ni narimasu.","german":"Anyone who stays in Japan after their visa has expired is in the country illegally.","furigana":"き|にほん|ふほうたいざい"},{"japanese":"不法駐車は罰金の対象です。","romaji":"Fuhō chūsha wa bakkin no taishō desu.","german":"Illegal parking is subject to a fine.","furigana":"ふほうちゅうしゃ|ばっきん|たいしょう"},{"japanese":"公園にごみを不法に捨てる人がいて困ります。","romaji":"Kouen ni gomi o fuhou ni suteru hito ga ite komarimasu.","german":"It is annoying that some people dump their trash illegally in the park.","furigana":"こうえん|ふほう|す|ひと|こま"}],"notes":"不法 means \"unlawful, illegal\" and mostly appears as the first element of compounds: 不法駐車 \"illegal parking\", 不法滞在 \"illegal stay\", 不法投棄 \"illegal dumping of waste\", 不法入国; adverbial 不法に. The closely related 違法 is more common in media and technology: 違法コピー, 違法ダウンロード. Both are rather formal; in colloquial speech people say 法律違反 or だめ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この子猫はまだお母さんのミルクを飲んでいます。","romaji":"Kono koneko wa mada okaasan no miruku o nonde imasu.","german":"This kitten is still drinking its mother's milk.","furigana":"こねこ|かあ|の"},{"japanese":"かわいい子猫を拾いました。","romaji":"Kawaii koneko o hiroimashita.","german":"I found a cute kitten.","furigana":"こねこ|ひろ"},{"japanese":"うちの猫が子猫を四匹産みました。","romaji":"Uchi no neko ga koneko o yonhiki umimashita.","german":"Our cat had four kittens.","furigana":"ねこ|こねこ|よんひきう"}],"notes":"子猫 is a kitten, i.e. a young cat; also written 仔猫 or こねこ. The prefix 子 forms many words for young animals: 子犬, 子馬, 子牛. It is counted with 匹 (子猫が三匹). Affectionately one says 子猫ちゃん; a cat giving birth to kittens: 子猫を産む.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"紀元後64年にローマで大火事が発生したのを知っていますか？","romaji":"Kigengo rokujūyon-nen ni Rōma de ōkaji ga hassei shita no o shitte imasu ka?","german":"Do you know that a great fire broke out in Rome in AD 64?","furigana":"きげんご|ねん|おおかじ|はっせい|し"},{"japanese":"西暦では、紀元前と紀元後を区別します。","romaji":"Seireki de wa, kigenzen to kigengo o kubetsu shimasu.","german":"In the calendar, 紀元前 and 紀元後 are distinguished (BC and AD).","furigana":"せいれき|きげんぜん|きげんご|くべつ"}],"notes":"紀元後 refers to the period after the start of a calendar era. In the Christian calendar, 紀元後64年 corresponds to the year AD 64. Year numbers with 西暦 are more common. The examples show a historical year date and the distinction between 紀元前 and 紀元後; they do not claim a precisely known birth year of Jesus.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は英語を勉強しています。","romaji":"Watashi wa eigo o benkyō shite imasu.","german":"I'm studying English.","furigana":"わたし|えいご|べんきょう"},{"japanese":"この手紙は英語で書かれています。","romaji":"Kono tegami wa eigo de kakarete imasu.","german":"This letter is written in English.","furigana":"てがみ|えいご|か"}],"notes":"英語 is the noun for the English language. 英語を勉強する names it as what is being studied; 英語で names it as the language of a statement or text. 英語の本 is a book in English. The reading is えいご; ei stays ei in romanization: eigo.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の兄は、英会話は上手だけど、計算は苦手なんです。","romaji":"Watashi no ani wa, eikaiwa wa jōzu da kedo, keisan wa nigate na n desu.","german":"My older brother is good at English conversation, but bad at math.","furigana":"わたし|あに|えいかいわ|じょうず|けいさん|にがて"},{"japanese":"英会話教室に通っています。","romaji":"Eikaiwa kyōshitsu ni kayotte imasu.","german":"I attend an English conversation class.","furigana":"えいかいわきょうしつ|かよ"},{"japanese":"仕事で使うので、英会話を習い始めました。","romaji":"Shigoto de tsukau node, eikaiwa o naraihajimemashita.","german":"I use it for work, so I started learning English conversation.","furigana":"しごと|つか|えいかいわ|なら|はじ"}],"notes":"英会話 is English conversation as a skill and a subject of study: 英会話を習う, 英会話が得意. 英会話教室 and 英会話スクール are very common; in short, 英会話 can also mean the language school itself (英会話に通う). 英語 is the language in general, while 英会話 emphasizes speaking as opposed to reading and grammar.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 22"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女の英文は分かりやすくて読みやすいです。","romaji":"Kanojo no eibun wa wakariyasukute yomiyasui desu.","german":"Her English writing is easy to understand and easy to read.","furigana":"かのじょ|えいぶん|わ|よ"},{"japanese":"英文を日本語に訳しました。","romaji":"Eibun o nihongo ni yakushimashita.","german":"I translated the English text into Japanese.","furigana":"えいぶん|にほんご|やく"},{"japanese":"姉は大学で英文を専攻しています。","romaji":"Ane wa daigaku de eibun o senkou shite imasu.","german":"My older sister majors in English literature at university.","furigana":"あね|だいがく|えいぶん|せんこう"}],"notes":"英文 is an English text or sentence in written form: 英文を読む, 英文を書く, 英文を訳す; 英文法 is English grammar. At universities, 英文 is short for 英文学（科）\"English literature (department)\". The counterpart is 和文 \"Japanese text\"; the language itself is 英語.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は英国出身です。","romaji":"Kare wa Eikoku shusshin desu.","german":"He is from Britain.","furigana":"かれ|えいこくしゅっしん"},{"japanese":"英国に留学したことがあります。","romaji":"Eikoku ni ryūgaku shita koto ga arimasu.","german":"I once studied in Britain.","furigana":"えいこく|りゅうがく"},{"japanese":"ニュースでは「イギリス」ではなく「英国」と言うことが多いです。","romaji":"Nyuusu de wa 'Igirisu' de wa naku 'Eikoku' to iu koto ga ooi desu.","german":"In the news, people often say \"Eikoku\" rather than \"Igirisu\".","furigana":"えいこく|い|おお"}],"notes":"英国 is the formal, written-language term for the United Kingdom or Great Britain, not just England. It appears in news, official documents and company names; in conversation people almost always say イギリス. The abbreviation 英 appears in 英語, 日英 \"Japanese-British\" and 英首相 \"British prime minister\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これ、英和だっけ？","romaji":"Kore, eiwa da kke?","german":"Is this the English-Japanese one?","furigana":"えいわ"},{"japanese":"英和辞典を使って調べました。","romaji":"Eiwa jiten o tsukatte shirabemashita.","german":"I looked it up using an English-Japanese dictionary.","furigana":"えいわじてん|つか|しら"},{"japanese":"和英辞典と英和辞典の両方を持っています。","romaji":"Waei jiten to eiwa jiten no ryouhou o motte imasu.","german":"I have both a Japanese-English and an English-Japanese dictionary.","furigana":"わえいじてん|えいわじてん|りょうほう|も"}],"notes":"英和 means \"English-Japanese\" and almost always refers to dictionaries: an 英和辞典 (or simply 英和) looks up English words and explains them in Japanese. The opposite direction is 和英 \"Japanese-English\" (和英辞典). 和 stands for Japan here, as in 和食 or 和室.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"合計で三千円になります。","romaji":"Goukei de sanzen'en ni narimasu.","german":"That comes to 3,000 yen in total.","furigana":"ごうけい|さんぜんえん"},{"japanese":"全部合計すると、いくらになりますか。","romaji":"Zenbu goukei suru to, ikura ni narimasu ka.","german":"How much does it come to altogether?","furigana":"ぜんぶごうけい"},{"japanese":"テストの点数を合計してください。","romaji":"Tesuto no tensuu o goukei shite kudasai.","german":"Please add up the test scores.","furigana":"てんすう|ごうけい"}],"notes":"合計 is the total sum: 合計で三千円 \"3,000 yen in total\", 合計金額. As a する-verb it is transitive: 点数を合計する \"to add up the points\". In conversation one often simply says 全部で; on receipts it appears as 合計 or, short, 計, and the subtotal is 小計.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は計算が速いです。","romaji":"Kare wa keisan ga hayai desu.","german":"He calculates quickly.","furigana":"かれ|けいさん|はや"},{"japanese":"計算を間違えました。","romaji":"Keisan o machigaemashita.","german":"I made a mistake in the calculation.","furigana":"けいさん|まちが"},{"japanese":"雨の日のことも計算に入れて、計画を立てましょう。","romaji":"Ame no hi no koto mo keisan ni irete, keikaku o tatemashou.","german":"Let's make a plan that also takes rainy days into account.","furigana":"あめ|ひ|けいさん|い|けいかく|た"}],"notes":"計算 is calculating or a calculation: 計算が速い／得意, 計算を間違える \"to miscalculate\", 計算ミス; as a する-verb it is transitive (費用を計算する). Figuratively it means \"taking into account, calculation\": 計算に入れる \"to factor in\", 計算通り \"as planned\", 計算高い \"calculating\". A pocket calculator is usually called 電卓.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"うちの父は元軍人です。","romaji":"Uchi no chichi wa moto gunjin desu.","german":"My father is a former soldier.","furigana":"ちち|もとぐんじん"},{"japanese":"祖父は軍人でした。","romaji":"Sofu wa gunjin deshita.","german":"My grandfather was a soldier.","furigana":"そふ|ぐんじん"},{"japanese":"自衛隊の人は「軍人」ではなく「自衛官」と呼ばれます。","romaji":"Jieitai no hito wa 'gunjin' de wa naku 'jieikan' to yobaremasu.","german":"Members of the Self-Defense Forces are called \"Jieikan\", not \"soldiers\".","furigana":"じえいたい|ひと|ぐんじん|じえいかん|よ"}],"notes":"軍人 is a member of the armed forces, whether officer or soldier, usually as an occupation; 元軍人 \"former soldier\". Ordinary soldiers are called 兵士 or 兵隊. Members of the Japanese Self-Defense Forces (自衛隊) are officially called not 軍人 but 自衛官.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"僕のおじいちゃんは、海軍の軍人だったそうです。","romaji":"Boku no ojiichan wa, kaigun no gunjin datta sō desu.","german":"I heard that my grandfather was a navy man.","furigana":"ぼく|かいぐん|ぐんじん"},{"japanese":"海軍の船が港に入りました。","romaji":"Kaigun no fune ga minato ni hairimashita.","german":"A navy ship entered the harbor.","furigana":"かいぐん|ふね|みなと|はい"},{"japanese":"この町の近くにアメリカ海軍の基地があります。","romaji":"Kono machi no chikaku ni Amerika kaigun no kichi ga arimasu.","german":"There is a US Navy base near this town.","furigana":"まち|ちか|かいぐん|きち"}],"notes":"海軍 is a noun: \"navy, naval forces\"; related are 陸軍 \"army\" and 空軍 \"air force\". One says 海軍に入る, 海軍の基地, アメリカ海軍. Japan itself officially has no 海軍, but the 海上自衛隊 \"Maritime Self-Defense Force\"; 海軍 therefore appears mainly for foreign armed forces and in history.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お願い！手伝って！","romaji":"Onegai! Tetsudatte!","german":"Please! Help me!","furigana":"ねが|てつだ"},{"japanese":"一つお願いがあります。","romaji":"Hitotsu onegai ga arimasu.","german":"I have a favor to ask.","furigana":"ひと|ねが"},{"japanese":"友だちのお願いを断れませんでした。","romaji":"Tomodachi no onegai o kotowaremasen deshita.","german":"I couldn't turn down my friend's request.","furigana":"とも|ねが|ことわ"}],"notes":"お願い is the polite form of 願い \"wish, request\": お願いがあります \"I have a favor to ask\", お願いを聞く \"to grant a request\", お願いを断る \"to turn down\". As a verb: お願いする (humble, X を Y にお願いする), and in the set phrase お願いします \"please\". On its own, as an exclamation, お願い！ is a casual, urgent plea among close friends.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このレストランは飲み放題があるみたいだよ。","romaji":"Kono resutoran wa nomihōdai ga aru mitai da yo.","german":"This restaurant seems to have an all-you-can-drink option.","furigana":"の|ほうだい"},{"japanese":"飲み放題のプランにしましょう。","romaji":"Nomihōdai no puran ni shimashō.","german":"Let's go with the all-you-can-drink plan.","furigana":"の|ほうだい"},{"japanese":"飲み放題は二時間までです。","romaji":"Nomihoudai wa nijikan made desu.","german":"The all-you-can-drink option is limited to two hours.","furigana":"の|ほうだい|にじかん"}],"notes":"飲み放題 means \"drink as much as you want\" for a fixed price, typical in izakaya and at company parties, usually with a time limit (2時間飲み放題). One says 飲み放題がある, 飲み放題付きのコース, 飲み放題にする. The suffix ～放題 \"as much as one likes\" also appears in 食べ放題 and 言いたい放題 \"saying whatever one wants\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 33"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このホテルの朝食は食べ放題です。","romaji":"Kono hoteru no choushoku wa tabehoudai desu.","german":"Breakfast at this hotel is all-you-can-eat.","furigana":"ちょうしょく|た|ほうだい"},{"japanese":"あのレストランは食べ放題で人気です。","romaji":"Ano resutoran wa tabehōdai de ninki desu.","german":"That restaurant is popular for its all-you-can-eat deal.","furigana":"た|ほうだい|にんき"},{"japanese":"食べ放題だからって、食べすぎないでね。","romaji":"Tabehoudai da kara tte, tabesuginaide ne.","german":"Just because it's all-you-can-eat, don't eat too much!","furigana":"た|ほうだい|た"}],"notes":"食べ放題 is an offer where you can eat as much as you like for a fixed price, often with a time limit (90分食べ放題) and for particular dishes: 焼肉食べ放題, ケーキ食べ放題. A buffet is also called バイキング in Japan. As in 飲み放題, ～放題 means \"as much as one likes\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その男は人殺しです。","romaji":"Sono otoko wa hitogoroshi desu.","german":"That man is a murderer.","furigana":"おとこ|ひとごろ"},{"japanese":"人殺しは許されません。","romaji":"Hitogoroshi wa yurusaremasen.","german":"Murder cannot be justified.","furigana":"ひとごろ|ゆる"},{"japanese":"映画の中で女の人が「人殺し！」と叫びました。","romaji":"Eiga no naka de onna no hito ga 'hitogoroshi!' to sakebimashita.","german":"In the movie, a woman screamed, \"Murderer!\"","furigana":"えいが|なか|おんな|ひと|ひとごろ|さけ"}],"notes":"人殺し is the native Japanese word for \"murder\" and \"murderer\" and sounds direct and emotional, for example as an accusation or a scream 「人殺し！」. In news and legal language, 殺人 \"murder\" (殺人事件) and 殺人犯 or 殺人者 \"murderer\" are used. The underlying verb is 殺す \"to kill\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この殺人者は、有名なシリアルキラーです。","romaji":"Kono satsujinsha wa, yūmei na shiriaru kirā desu.","german":"This murderer is a famous serial killer.","furigana":"さつじんしゃ|ゆうめい"},{"japanese":"殺人者が逮捕されました。","romaji":"Satsujinsha ga taiho saremashita.","german":"The murderer was arrested.","furigana":"さつじんしゃ|たいほ"},{"japanese":"その小説では、最後まで殺人者が誰か分かりません。","romaji":"Sono shousetsu de wa, saigo made satsujinsha ga dare ka wakarimasen.","german":"In that novel, you don't find out who the murderer is until the end.","furigana":"しょうせつ|さいご|さつじんしゃ|だれ|わ"}],"notes":"殺人者 is a formal, rather literary word for \"murderer\". In news and criminal law, 殺人犯 (convicted or wanted murderer) or 犯人 \"culprit\" is heard more often; 殺人 alone is the act (殺人事件 \"murder case\"). Colloquially and emotionally, 人殺し is used.","levelNote":"Compound: 殺人 (N2) + 者 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この輪ゴムはとても丈夫です。","romaji":"Kono wagomu wa totemo jōbu desu.","german":"This rubber band is very durable.","furigana":"わ|じょうぶ"},{"japanese":"書類を輪ゴムで束ねました。","romaji":"Shorui o wagomu de tabanemashita.","german":"I bundled the documents together with a rubber band.","furigana":"しょるい|わ|たば"},{"japanese":"すみません、輪ゴムを一つもらえますか。","romaji":"Sumimasen, wagomu o hitotsu moraemasu ka.","german":"Excuse me, could I have a rubber band?","furigana":"わ|ひと"}],"notes":"輪ゴム is a rubber band: 輪 \"ring\" plus ゴム \"rubber\" (from Dutch). Typical are 輪ゴムで留める \"to fasten with a rubber band\" and 輪ゴムで束ねる \"to bundle with a rubber band\"; it is usually counted with 本 or 個. A hair tie, by contrast, is a ヘアゴム, and an eraser is a 消しゴム.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どこで練習するの？","romaji":"Doko de renshū suru no?","german":"Where will you practice?","furigana":"れんしゅう"},{"japanese":"ピアノを毎日練習しています。","romaji":"Piano o mainichi renshū shite imasu.","german":"I practice the piano every day.","furigana":"まいにちれんしゅう"}],"notes":"練習する means to practice a skill through repetition. What is practiced can be named with を: ピアノを練習する. どこで asks about the place; the first example can refer to a planned practice location. 毎日練習しています, by contrast, describes a regular habit. The forms 練習します, 練習した and 練習して show how する changes; 練習 alone is the noun \"practice\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 10"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その年に、変事が起きたんです。","romaji":"Sono toshi ni, henji ga okita n desu.","german":"An unusual incident occurred that year.","furigana":"とし|へんじ|お"},{"japanese":"変事が起きたらすぐ連絡してください。","romaji":"Henji ga okitara sugu renraku shite kudasai.","german":"If an incident occurs, please contact us immediately.","furigana":"へんじ|お|れんらく"},{"japanese":"古い記録には、その年の変事が書かれています。","romaji":"Furui kiroku ni wa, sono toshi no henji ga kakarete imasu.","german":"Old records describe the unusual events of that year.","furigana":"ふる|きろく|とし|へんじ|か"}],"notes":"変事 is a rare, literary word for an unusual, disturbing event or misfortune, for example in chronicles or formal texts. Caution: it sounds exactly like the very common 返事 \"reply\". In everyday speech one says 事件, 事故, 異変 or 大変なこと instead.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ごめん、今データ同期中だからちょっと待って。","romaji":"Gomen, ima deeta doukichuu da kara chotto matte.","german":"Sorry, the data is syncing right now, so wait a moment.","furigana":"いま|どうきちゅう|ま"},{"japanese":"データは同期中です。","romaji":"Dēta wa dōkichū desu.","german":"The data is syncing.","furigana":"どうきちゅう"},{"japanese":"スマホの画面に「同期中」と出ています。","romaji":"Sumaho no gamen ni 'doukichuu' to dete imasu.","german":"\"Syncing\" is displayed on the phone screen.","furigana":"がめん|どうきちゅう|で"}],"notes":"同期中 is made up of 同期 \"synchronization\" and the suffix ～中（ちゅう）\"in progress\", like 会議中 or 電話中; it is seen mostly as a status display on phones and computers (同期中です, 同期中…). Note: 同期 also means \"same year group\" (会社の同期 \"colleague who joined the company in the same year\"). The verb is 同期する \"to synchronize\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"それに比例して、コストも上がります。","romaji":"Sore ni hirei shite, kosuto mo agarimasu.","german":"The costs rise in proportion to that.","furigana":"ひれい|あ"},{"japanese":"努力に比例して結果が出ます。","romaji":"Doryoku ni hirei shite kekka ga demasu.","german":"Results come in proportion to effort.","furigana":"どりょく|ひれい|けっか|で"},{"japanese":"数学の授業で、比例と反比例を習いました。","romaji":"Suugaku no jugyou de, hirei to hanpirei o naraimashita.","german":"In math class I learned about direct and inverse proportion.","furigana":"すうがく|じゅぎょう|ひれい|はんぴれい|なら"}],"notes":"比例 is proportionality; as an intransitive する-verb it takes に: X に比例する \"to be proportional to X\", adverbially X に比例して \"in proportion to X\". The opposite is 反比例 \"inversely proportional\". 割合 and 比率, on the other hand, denote a concrete ratio or share. In politics, 比例 is short for 比例代表 \"proportional representation\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"もしここにマリアがいたら、こんな数学の問題五秒で解けちゃうよ。マリアにとってはちょちょいのちょいだね。","romaji":"Moshi koko ni Maria ga itara, konna suugaku no mondai gobyou de tokechau yo. Maria ni totte wa chochoi no choi da ne.","german":"If Maria were here, she'd solve a math problem like this in five seconds. It's a piece of cake for her.","furigana":"すうがく|もんだいごびょう|と"},{"japanese":"この問題が解けました。","romaji":"Kono mondai ga tokemashita.","german":"I was able to solve this problem.","furigana":"もんだい|と"},{"japanese":"靴のひもが解けていますよ。","romaji":"Kutsu no himo ga tokete imasu yo.","german":"Your shoelace is untied.","furigana":"くつ|と"}],"notes":"解ける is intransitive: a problem \"gets solved\" or \"can be solved\" (問題が解ける); the transitive counterpart is 解く (問題を解く). Since the potential form of 解く is also 解ける, 問題が解けた covers both readings: \"I was able to solve the problem\". Other meanings: ひもが解ける \"to come loose, to come undone\", 誤解が解ける \"a misunderstanding gets cleared up\". Do not confuse it with 溶ける \"to melt, to dissolve\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この料理はとても美味しいです。","romaji":"Kono ryōri wa totemo oishii desu.","german":"This dish is very delicious.","furigana":"りょうり|おい"},{"japanese":"美味しい料理を作ってくれて、ありがとう。","romaji":"Oishii ryōri o tsukutte kurete, arigatō.","german":"Thank you for cooking me a delicious meal.","furigana":"おい|りょうり|つく"}],"notes":"美味しい is an i-adjective for good taste: 美味しくない, 美味しかった. It comes directly before a noun: 美味しい料理. It is often written in hiragana as おいしい. 作ってくれてありがとう thanks someone for having cooked for you. In oishii there is no gap inside the word; desu stays separate.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 8"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"了解しました。すぐにやります。","romaji":"Ryoukai shimashita. Sugu ni yarimasu.","german":"Understood. I'll do it right away.","furigana":"りょうかい"},{"japanese":"「明日は九時集合ね。」「了解！」","romaji":"'Ashita wa kuji shuugou ne.' 'Ryoukai!'","german":"\"We meet at nine tomorrow, OK?\" \"Got it!\"","furigana":"あした|くじしゅうごう|りょうかい"},{"japanese":"社長の了解を得てから、始めましょう。","romaji":"Shachou no ryoukai o ete kara, hajimemashou.","german":"Let's start after obtaining the president's approval.","furigana":"しゃちょう|りょうかい|え|はじ"}],"notes":"了解 means \"understanding, consent\"; 了解しました is the usual acknowledgment \"Understood, will do\". Toward superiors and customers, 承知しました or かしこまりました is considered more polite, as 了解 seems too casual to some. Among friends, 了解！ is enough (in chats even りょ). Formal usage: 了解を得る \"to obtain approval\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"土台はできるだけ頑丈にしておいた方がいいですよ。","romaji":"Dodai wa dekiru dake ganjou ni shite oita hou ga ii desu yo.","german":"It's better to make the foundation as sturdy as possible.","furigana":"どだい|がんじょう|ほう"},{"japanese":"この机は頑丈にできています。","romaji":"Kono tsukue wa ganjō ni dekite imasu.","german":"This desk is built sturdily.","furigana":"つくえ|がんじょう"},{"japanese":"父は体が頑丈で、めったに風邪をひきません。","romaji":"Chichi wa karada ga ganjou de, metta ni kaze o hikimasen.","german":"My father has a robust body and rarely catches a cold.","furigana":"ちち|からだ|がんじょう|かぜ"}],"notes":"頑丈 is a な-adjective for massive, solid things and strong bodies: 頑丈な箱, 頑丈な体, 頑丈にできている \"solidly built\". 丈夫 also means \"durable\", but is also used for health (体が丈夫) and sounds less hefty; 頑丈 emphasizes heavy, stable construction. Opposite: 壊れやすい \"fragile\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"テストを最後に見返すクセをつけた方がいいですよ。","romaji":"Tesuto o saigo ni mikaesu kuse o tsuketa hou ga ii desu yo.","german":"It's a good idea to get into the habit of going over your test again at the end.","furigana":"さいご|みかえ|ほう"},{"japanese":"レポートをもう一度見返しました。","romaji":"Repōto o mō ichido mikaeshimashita.","german":"I checked the report once more.","furigana":"いちどみかえ"},{"japanese":"いつか、ばかにした人たちを見返してやりたい。","romaji":"Itsuka, baka ni shita hitotachi o mikaeshite yaritai.","german":"Someday I want to show up the people who looked down on me.","furigana":"ひと|みかえ"}],"notes":"見返す takes を and mostly means \"to look through once more\": to review notes, photos or an exam before handing it in. Unlike 見直す, which tends to mean \"to review and improve\" or \"to reassess\", 見返す is simply looking at it again. A second common meaning is \"to show someone\": a person who was underestimated wants to prove the others wrong through success, typically as 見返してやる. It can also mean \"to look back, to stare back\" (相手の顔を見返す).","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの選手はすごいホームランを放つらしいですよ。","romaji":"Ano senshu wa sugoi hoomuran o hanatsu rashii desu yo.","german":"I heard that this player hits some really big home runs.","furigana":"せんしゅ|はな"},{"japanese":"矢を放ちました。","romaji":"Ya o hanachimashita.","german":"I shot the arrow.","furigana":"や|はな"},{"japanese":"暗い部屋の中で、時計が青い光を放っていました。","romaji":"Kurai heya no naka de, tokei ga aoi hikari o hanatte imashita.","german":"In the dark room, the clock was giving off a blue light.","furigana":"くら|へや|なか|とけい|あお|ひかり|はな"}],"notes":"放つ is rather elevated and literary; it appears in sports reports, news and literature: 矢を放つ \"to shoot an arrow\", ホームランを放つ \"to hit a home run\", 光を放つ \"to emit light\", 火を放つ \"to set fire\". In everyday speech, 放す is usually used for \"to release\" (魚を川に放す) and 撃つ for \"to shoot\". The passive 放たれる is also common (矢が放たれた).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大変だ！あと五分で昼休みだ。","romaji":"Taihen da! Ato gofun de hiruyasumi da.","german":"Oh no! The lunch break starts in five minutes.","furigana":"たいへん|ごふん|ひるやす"},{"japanese":"昼休みに散歩します。","romaji":"Hiruyasumi ni sanpo shimasu.","german":"I take a walk during the lunch break.","furigana":"ひるやす|さんぽ"},{"japanese":"この会社の昼休みは十二時から一時までです。","romaji":"Kono kaisha no hiruyasumi wa juuniji kara ichiji made desu.","german":"At this company the lunch break is from twelve to one.","furigana":"かいしゃ|ひるやす|じゅうにじ|いちじ"}],"notes":"昼休み is the lunch break at school, office or factory. As a time expression it takes に (昼休みに散歩する) or から/まで for the period. You say 昼休みを取る \"to take a lunch break\" and 昼休み中 \"during the lunch break\". Distinguish it from 昼ご飯 or, colloquially, お昼 (the lunch meal itself) and from 休憩, any short break.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この二十四時間スーパーは、昨年オープンしました。","romaji":"Kono nijuuyojikan suupaa wa, sakunen oopun shimashita.","german":"This 24-hour supermarket opened last year.","furigana":"にじゅうよじかん|さくねん"},{"japanese":"昨年日本に行きました。","romaji":"Sakunen Nihon ni ikimashita.","german":"I went to Japan last year.","furigana":"さくねんにほん|い"},{"japanese":"昨年は大変お世話になりました。","romaji":"Sakunen wa taihen osewa ni narimashita.","german":"Thank you very much for all your support over the past year.","furigana":"さくねん|たいへん|せわ"}],"notes":"昨年 is the formal equivalent of 去年 \"last year\". It appears in business letters, news, speeches and especially in New Year greetings (昨年は大変お世話になりました). In ordinary conversation people almost always say 去年. Like other time words, it is used adverbially without a particle (昨年オープンした) or with の before a noun (昨年の夏).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"おっマスク外すと別人だね。","romaji":"O masuku hazusu to betsujin da ne.","german":"Wow, you look like a different person without your mask."},{"japanese":"メガネを外しました。","romaji":"Megane o hazushimashita.","german":"I took off my glasses.","furigana":"はず"},{"japanese":"田中はただいま席を外しております。","romaji":"Tanaka wa tadaima seki o hazushite orimasu.","german":"Mr. Tanaka is away from his desk at the moment.","furigana":"たなか|せき|はず"}],"notes":"外す is transitive (X を外す); the intransitive partner is 外れる (ボタンが外れる, 予報が外れる). It means \"to take off, to detach\" things that are fastened: メガネ・マスク・時計・ボタンを外す. Clothes and shoes, on the other hand, are taken off with 脱ぐ. Other common uses: 席を外す \"to be away from one's seat for a moment\" (typical on the phone in an office), 的を外す \"to miss the target\", メンバーから外す \"to drop from the team\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ええっ、それは初耳です。","romaji":"Ee, sore wa hatsumimi desu.","german":"What? That's news to me."},{"japanese":"彼が結婚していたなんて、初耳だよ。","romaji":"Kare ga kekkon shite ita nante, hatsumimi da yo.","german":"It's news to me that he was married.","furigana":"かれ|けっこん|はつみみ"},{"japanese":"え、来週テストがあるの？初耳なんだけど。","romaji":"E, raishuu tesuto ga aru no? Hatsumimi nan da kedo.","german":"Huh, there's a test next week? That's the first I've heard of it.","furigana":"らいしゅう|はつみみ"}],"notes":"初耳 means \"to hear something for the first time\" and almost always stands as a predicate: それは初耳です / 初耳だ is the typical surprised reaction to a piece of news. Often a sentence with なんて or とは precedes: 彼が結婚していたなんて初耳だ. It refers to information, not a sound; for music or noises, 初めて聞く is used. The word is used both colloquially and politely.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今朝、乗馬をしました。","romaji":"Kesa, jōba o shimashita.","german":"I went horseback riding this morning.","furigana":"けさ|じょうば"},{"japanese":"休みの日に乗馬を楽しみます。","romaji":"Yasumi no hi ni jōba o tanoshimimasu.","german":"On my days off I enjoy horseback riding.","furigana":"やす|ひ|じょうば|たの"},{"japanese":"北海道の牧場で初めて乗馬を体験しました。","romaji":"Hokkaidou no bokujou de hajimete jouba o taiken shimashita.","german":"I tried horseback riding for the first time at a ranch in Hokkaido.","furigana":"ほっかいどう|ぼくじょう|はじ|じょうば|たいけん"}],"notes":"乗馬 means horseback riding as a sport or hobby: 乗馬をする / 乗馬する, 乗馬クラブ, 乗馬体験. The horse itself does not take を; to say \"to get on a horse, to ride a horse\", use 馬に乗る. 乗馬 therefore sounds somewhat matter-of-fact and suits leisure offerings, hobbies and courses. More rarely it also refers to the riding horse itself.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これは、やわらかいイメージを表すオノマトペですね。","romaji":"Kore wa, yawarakai imēji o arawasu onomatope desu ne.","german":"This is an onomatopoeia that expresses an impression of softness.","furigana":"あらわ"},{"japanese":"日本語にはオノマトペが多いです。","romaji":"Nihongo ni wa onomatope ga ōi desu.","german":"Japanese has a lot of onomatopoeia.","furigana":"にほんご|おお"},{"japanese":"漫画を読むと、たくさんのオノマトペが出てきます。","romaji":"Manga o yomu to, takusan no onomatope ga dete kimasu.","german":"When you read manga, you come across many onomatopoeic expressions.","furigana":"まんが|よ|で"}],"notes":"オノマトペ comes from French onomatopée and in Japanese covers two groups: 擬音語, which imitate actual sounds (ワンワン, ドンドン), and 擬態語, which describe states or feelings (キラキラ, ふわふわ, イライラ). Japanese has a great many of them; they are everywhere in manga, advertising and everyday speech. They mostly appear with と or する as an adverb or verb (ふわふわする).","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ポーカーするからトランプ買ってきて！","romaji":"Pookaa suru kara toranpu katte kite!","german":"We're going to play poker, so go buy some playing cards!","furigana":"か"},{"japanese":"トランプで遊びましょう。","romaji":"Toranpu de asobimashō.","german":"Let's play cards.","furigana":"あそ"},{"japanese":"修学旅行の夜、みんなでトランプをしました。","romaji":"Shuugaku ryokou no yoru, minna de toranpu o shimashita.","german":"On the night of the school trip, we all played cards together.","furigana":"しゅうがくりょこう|よる"}],"notes":"トランプ in Japanese refers to Western playing cards or the card deck as a whole, not a particular game. \"To play cards\" is トランプをする or トランプで遊ぶ; a single card is called カード. Well-known games are ババ抜き and 七並べ. Do not confuse it with かるた, the traditional Japanese cards.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"なんで私ばかり疑うの？","romaji":"Nande watashi bakari utagau no?","german":"Warum verdächtigst du nur mich?","furigana":"わたし|うたが"},{"japanese":"彼の言葉を疑っています。","romaji":"Kare no kotoba o utagatte imasu.","german":"Ich zweifle an seinen Worten.","furigana":"かれ|ことば|うたが"},{"japanese":"テストの点を見て、自分の目を疑いました。","romaji":"Tesuto no ten o mite, jibun no me o utagaimashita.","german":"Als ich meine Testnote sah, traute ich meinen Augen nicht.","furigana":"てん|み|じぶん|め|うたが"}],"notes":"疑う ist transitiv (X を疑う) und heißt „misstrauen, verdächtigen, bezweifeln“. Achtung beim Muster mit と: 彼が犯人だと疑う bedeutet „ich verdächtige ihn, der Täter zu sein“, also das Gegenteil des deutschen „bezweifeln, dass …“. Feste Wendung: 自分の目を疑う „seinen Augen nicht trauen“. Gegenteil ist 信じる; das Nomen lautet 疑い (疑いがある).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ハシゴを下りるのがこわいんです。","romaji":"Hashigo o oriru no ga kowai n desu.","german":"I'm scared of going down the ladder.","furigana":"お"},{"japanese":"山を下りましょう。","romaji":"Yama o orimashō.","german":"Let's go down the mountain.","furigana":"やま|お"},{"japanese":"やっとビザが下りました。","romaji":"Yatto biza ga orimashita.","german":"My visa has finally been granted.","furigana":"お"}],"notes":"下りる is intransitive; the path or place one goes down takes を (階段を下りる, 山を下りる). For getting off vehicles, 降りる is usually written (電車を降りる); 下りる suits stairs, mountains and ladders, as well as 幕が下りる \"the curtain falls\" and 許可・ビザが下りる \"is granted\". The transitive partner is 下ろす (荷物を下ろす). Do not confuse with 下がる (to fall, drop) and 下る (くだる, e.g. to go down a river).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"社会と人間のあり方を問うテーマです。","romaji":"Shakai to ningen no arikata o tou tēma desu.","german":"It is a theme that questions the way society and people should be.","furigana":"しゃかい|にんげん|かた|と"},{"japanese":"責任を問われました。","romaji":"Sekinin o towaremashita.","german":"I was held responsible.","furigana":"せきにん|と"},{"japanese":"年齢を問わず、だれでも参加できます。","romaji":"Nenrei o towazu, dare demo sanka dekimasu.","german":"Anyone can take part, regardless of age.","furigana":"ねんれい|と|さんか"}],"notes":"問う is elevated and literary; in everyday speech one asks with 聞く or 尋ねる. Typical are set patterns: 責任を問う \"to hold accountable\" (passive 責任を問われる), ～を問わず \"regardless of, no matter whether\" (年齢を問わず) and ～を問う \"to call into question, to raise as an issue\" in media and criticism. Note: the te-form is 問うて, not 問って.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は全てを受け入れる心の広さを持っています。","romaji":"Kare wa subete o ukeireru kokoro no hirosa o motte imasu.","german":"He has the generosity of spirit to accept everything.","furigana":"かれ|すべ|う|い|こころ|ひろ|も"},{"japanese":"現実を受け入れなければなりません。","romaji":"Genjitsu o ukeirenakereba narimasen.","german":"We have to accept reality.","furigana":"げんじつ|う|い"},{"japanese":"この大学は毎年たくさんの留学生を受け入れています。","romaji":"Kono daigaku wa maitoshi takusan no ryuugakusei o ukeirete imasu.","german":"This university takes in many international students every year.","furigana":"だいがく|まいとし|りゅうがくせい|う|い"}],"notes":"受け入れる is transitive (X を受け入れる) and has two main meanings: \"to accept\" (a proposal, request, criticism, reality) and \"to take in\" people (refugees, exchange students, patients). Distinguish it from 受け取る (to receive an object), 受ける (to take an exam, to receive a call) and 認める (to recognize, to admit). The noun is 受け入れ; in official documents also written 受入れ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コンビニでペットボトルの緑茶買って来てくれない？","romaji":"Konbini de pettobotoru no ryokucha katte kite kurenai?","german":"Could you buy me a bottle of green tea at the convenience store?","furigana":"りょくちゃか|き"},{"japanese":"毎朝緑茶を飲みます。","romaji":"Maiasa ryokucha o nomimasu.","german":"I drink green tea every morning.","furigana":"まいあさりょくちゃ|の"},{"japanese":"緑茶と紅茶、どちらがいいですか。","romaji":"Ryokucha to koucha, dochira ga ii desu ka.","german":"Which would you like, green tea or black tea?","furigana":"りょくちゃ|こうちゃ"}],"notes":"緑茶 is the general term for green tea. In daily life, Japanese people usually just say お茶, since that already means green tea; 緑茶 is used to distinguish it from 紅茶 (black tea) or ウーロン茶, and on labels and menus. Well-known varieties are 煎茶, ほうじ茶 and 抹茶.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"赤ちゃんは何でも舐める。","romaji":"Akachan wa nani demo nameru.","german":"Babies lick everything.","furigana":"あか|なに|な"},{"japanese":"アイスクリームを舐めました。","romaji":"Aisukurīmu o namemashita.","german":"I licked an ice cream.","furigana":"な"},{"japanese":"あのチームをなめていたら、負けてしまいました。","romaji":"Ano chiimu o namete itara, makete shimaimashita.","german":"I was taking that team lightly, and we lost.","furigana":"ま"}],"notes":"舐める is transitive (X を舐める) and first means \"to lick\": アイスを舐める, 犬が手を舐める; with candy also \"to suck\" (飴を舐める). Very common is the figurative meaning \"to underestimate, not take seriously\": 相手をなめる, なめるな! \"Don't underestimate me!\". In this meaning the word is almost always written in kana; the kanji variant 嘗める is rare, and in general なめる is more common than 舐める.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"こちらは、外来を担当している先生です。","romaji":"Kochira wa, gairai o tantou shite iru sensei desu.","german":"This is the doctor in charge of the outpatient department.","furigana":"がいらい|たんとう|せんせい"},{"japanese":"外来の患者が多いです。","romaji":"Gairai no kanja ga ōi desu.","german":"There are many outpatients.","furigana":"がいらい|かんじゃ|おお"},{"japanese":"「パン」は外来語です。","romaji":"'Pan' wa gairaigo desu.","german":"\"Pan\" (bread) is a loanword.","furigana":"がいらいご"}],"notes":"外来 has two areas: in a hospital it means \"outpatient, outpatient department\" (外来の患者, 外来受付, 外来で診てもらう) as opposed to 入院 \"inpatient\". As a prefix it means \"introduced from outside or from abroad\": 外来語 \"loanword\" (usually in katakana), 外来種 \"invasive species\". It is not a true adjective, but is used with の or as part of a compound.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このホテルは、オーナーが不明です。","romaji":"Kono hoteru wa, oonaa ga fumei desu.","german":"The owner of this hotel is unknown.","furigana":"ふめい"},{"japanese":"原因は不明です。","romaji":"Gen'in wa fumei desu.","german":"The cause is unknown.","furigana":"げんいん|ふめい"},{"japanese":"不明な点があれば、いつでも聞いてください。","romaji":"Fumei na ten ga areba, itsu demo kiite kudasai.","german":"If there is anything unclear, please ask at any time.","furigana":"ふめい|てん|き"}],"notes":"不明 means \"unknown, unclear\" and sounds objective to formal; you read it in news and forms: 原因不明 \"cause unknown\", 行方不明 \"missing\", 身元不明 \"unidentified\". As a predicate it is 不明だ/です, before a noun 不明な (不明な点があれば…). Colloquially, 意味不明 means \"makes no sense\". In conversation people otherwise prefer 分からない.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今、歯医者の待合室にいます。","romaji":"Ima, haisha no machiaishitsu ni imasu.","german":"I'm in the dentist's waiting room right now.","furigana":"いま|はいしゃ|まちあいしつ"},{"japanese":"病院の待合室で待っています。","romaji":"Byōin no machiaishitsu de matte imasu.","german":"I'm waiting in the hospital waiting room.","furigana":"びょういん|まちあいしつ|ま"},{"japanese":"雪で電車が遅れたので、駅の待合室で休みました。","romaji":"Yuki de densha ga okureta node, eki no machiaishitsu de yasumimashita.","german":"The train was delayed by snow, so I rested in the station waiting room.","furigana":"ゆき|でんしゃ|おく|えき|まちあいしつ|やす"}],"notes":"待合室 is the waiting room at a doctor's office, hospital, train station or airport. You are 待合室にいる or wait 待合室で. Note the spelling without ち: 待合室, although the verbal noun 待ち合わせ \"appointment, meeting place\" is written with it. For hotels people say ロビー, for performers or speakers 控え室.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"わぁ、びっくりした！何で仮面なんてかぶってるの？","romaji":"Wā, bikkuri shita! Nande kamen nante kabutteru no?","german":"Wow, you scared me! Why are you wearing a mask?","furigana":"なん|かめん"},{"japanese":"仮面をつけて踊りました。","romaji":"Kamen o tsukete odorimashita.","german":"I danced wearing a mask.","furigana":"かめん|おど"},{"japanese":"彼は親切の仮面をかぶっていました。","romaji":"Kare wa shinsetsu no kamen o kabutte imashita.","german":"He was wearing a mask of kindness.","furigana":"かれ|しんせつ|かめん"}],"notes":"仮面 is a mask that covers the face, for example in a performance. 仮面をつける or かぶる means to put it on or wear it. The word can also figuratively mean a pretended attitude: 親切の仮面 is a mask of kindness. An ordinary medical face mask, on the other hand, is called マスク.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の通っていた高校は、とても保守的な学校でした。","romaji":"Watashi no kayotte ita kōkō wa, totemo hoshuteki na gakkō deshita.","german":"The high school I attended was a very conservative school.","furigana":"わたし|かよ|こうこう|ほしゅてき|がっこう"},{"japanese":"彼は保守的な考え方をします。","romaji":"Kare wa hoshuteki na kangaekata o shimasu.","german":"He has a conservative way of thinking.","furigana":"かれ|ほしゅてき|かんが|かた"},{"japanese":"父は保守的で、新しい物をなかなか使いません。","romaji":"Chichi wa hoshuteki de, atarashii mono o nakanaka tsukaimasen.","german":"My father is conservative and hardly ever uses new things.","furigana":"ちち|ほしゅてき|あたら|もの|つか"}],"notes":"保守的 is a な-adjective: 保守的な人, 保守的な考え方, 彼は保守的だ. It describes people, institutions or attitudes that stick to what is proven and are cautious about the new. Opposites are 革新的 and 進歩的. The base word 保守 appears in politics (保守派, 保守政党) and in technology also means \"maintenance\" (システムの保守).","levelNote":"Compound: 保守 (N1) + 的 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ずいぶん古典的なギャグだね。","romaji":"Zuibun kotenteki na gyagu da ne.","german":"That's quite a classic gag.","furigana":"こてんてき"},{"japanese":"古典的な方法ですが、今でもよく使われています。","romaji":"Kotenteki na houhou desu ga, ima demo yoku tsukawarete imasu.","german":"It's a classic method, but it is still widely used today.","furigana":"こてんてき|ほうほう|いま|つか"},{"japanese":"この映画は古典的なラブストーリーです。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa kotenteki na rabu sutoorii desu.","german":"This movie is a classic love story.","furigana":"えいが|こてんてき"}],"notes":"古典的 is a な-adjective and means \"classical, traditional, time-tested\": 古典的な方法, 古典的なラブストーリー. It often also sounds ironic, like \"old-fashioned, hackneyed\", for example with 古典的なギャグ or 古典的な手口 \"an ancient trick\". Classical music is called クラシック(音楽), not 古典的な音楽. The base word 古典 denotes the classics of literature (古典文学).","levelNote":"Compound: 古典 (N2) + 的 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この言葉は、仏典の中に登場します。","romaji":"Kono kotoba wa, butten no naka ni tōjō shimasu.","german":"This word appears in Buddhist scriptures.","furigana":"ことば|ぶってん|なか|とうじょう"},{"japanese":"仏典を読んで勉強しています。","romaji":"Butten o yonde benkyō shite imasu.","german":"I'm studying Buddhist scriptures.","furigana":"ぶってん|よ|べんきょう"},{"japanese":"古い仏典が、このお寺に大切に残されています。","romaji":"Furui butten ga, kono otera ni taisetsu ni nokosarete imasu.","german":"Old Buddhist scriptures are carefully preserved in this temple.","furigana":"ふる|ぶってん|てら|たいせつ|のこ"}],"notes":"仏典 is the collective term for Buddhist scriptures and sounds technical or literary. The individual sutras that are recited are called お経 in everyday speech. The kanji 典 means \"scripture, work\" and is also in 辞典, 字典, 事典 and 古典; 仏 stands for Buddha or Buddhism. Typical combinations are 仏典を読む and 仏典に出てくる.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この字典には、五万以上の漢字がのっています。","romaji":"Kono jiten ni wa, goman ijou no kanji ga notte imasu.","german":"This kanji dictionary contains over 50,000 kanji.","furigana":"じてん|ごまんいじょう|かんじ"},{"japanese":"字典で漢字を調べました。","romaji":"Jiten de kanji o shirabemashita.","german":"I looked up the kanji in a kanji dictionary.","furigana":"じてん|かんじ|しら"},{"japanese":"書道の先生は、古い字典で字の形を確かめていました。","romaji":"Shodou no sensei wa, furui jiten de ji no katachi o tashikamete imashita.","german":"The calligraphy teacher was checking the shape of the character in an old kanji dictionary.","furigana":"しょどう|せんせい|ふる|じてん|じ|かたち|たし"}],"notes":"字典 is a character dictionary that explains individual kanji with their readings, meanings and forms. There are three homophonous じてん: 辞典 (dictionary of words), 字典 (of written characters) and 事典 (encyclopedia of things). To tell them apart, people say, half-jokingly and practically, もじてん, ことばてん and ことてん. In everyday speech one usually just says 辞書 or 漢字辞典; 字典 is more of a technical or title word.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"それ、何の事典？","romaji":"Sore, nani no jiten?","german":"What kind of encyclopedia is that?","furigana":"なに|じてん"},{"japanese":"百科事典で調べました。","romaji":"Hyakka jiten de shirabemashita.","german":"I looked it up in the encyclopedia.","furigana":"ひゃっかじてん|しら"},{"japanese":"この動物事典には、写真がたくさんのっています。","romaji":"Kono doubutsu jiten ni wa, shashin ga takusan notte imasu.","german":"This animal encyclopedia has a lot of photos.","furigana":"どうぶつじてん|しゃしん"}],"notes":"事典 is a subject dictionary or encyclopedia that explains things and matters, not words. It mostly appears in compounds: 百科事典 \"encyclopedia\", 動物事典, 歴史事典. To be distinguished from it are 辞典 or 辞書 (dictionary) and 字典 (character dictionary); since all are read じてん, ことてん is also said for clarity.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"明日からの冒険のために、力を残しておいた方がいいよ。","romaji":"Ashita kara no bōken no tame ni, chikara o nokoshite oita hō ga ii yo.","german":"We should save our strength for the adventure starting tomorrow.","furigana":"あした|ぼうけん|ちから|のこ|ほう"},{"japanese":"冒険の物語が好きです。","romaji":"Bōken no monogatari ga suki desu.","german":"I like adventure stories.","furigana":"ぼうけん|ものがたり|す"},{"japanese":"今の仕事を辞めて店を始めるのは、ちょっと冒険だと思う。","romaji":"Ima no shigoto o yamete mise o hajimeru no wa, chotto bouken da to omou.","german":"Quitting my current job and opening a shop seems like quite a gamble to me.","furigana":"いま|しごと|や|みせ|はじ|ぼうけん|おも"}],"notes":"冒険 is an adventure: 冒険に出る \"to set out on an adventure\", 冒険する, 冒険物語, 冒険家 \"adventurer\". Figuratively it also refers to a risky undertaking: それはちょっと冒険だ \"that's quite a gamble\". Unlike 危険 (\"danger\"), 冒険 emphasizes knowingly taking a risk. For games and films, アドベンチャー is also common.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このジーパンは新品です。","romaji":"Kono jīpan wa shinpin desu.","german":"These jeans are brand new.","furigana":"しんぴん"},{"japanese":"新品のスマホを買いました。","romaji":"Shinpin no sumaho o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a brand-new smartphone.","furigana":"しんぴん|か"},{"japanese":"この自転車は中古ですが、新品同様です。","romaji":"Kono jitensha wa chuuko desu ga, shinpin douyou desu.","german":"This bicycle is secondhand, but as good as new.","furigana":"じてんしゃ|ちゅうこ|しんぴんどうよう"}],"notes":"新品 means a new, unused article, i.e. new goods as opposed to 中古 (secondhand). It is used as a noun (これは新品です) or with の before a noun (新品の靴). For things that are simply \"new\" in the sense of \"newly released\" or \"different from before\", 新しい is used. 新品同様 \"like new, as good as new\" is common for secondhand goods.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"生存者は一人だけでした。","romaji":"Seizonsha wa hitori dake deshita.","german":"There was only one survivor.","furigana":"せいぞんしゃ|ひとり"},{"japanese":"生存者が見つかりました。","romaji":"Seizonsha ga mitsukarimashita.","german":"A survivor has been found.","furigana":"せいぞんしゃ|み"},{"japanese":"砂漠で生存するためには、水が一番大切です。","romaji":"Sabaku de seizon suru tame ni wa, mizu ga ichiban taisetsu desu.","german":"To survive in the desert, water is the most important thing.","furigana":"さばく|せいぞん|みず|いちばんたいせつ"}],"notes":"生存 is an objective, rather formal word for \"survival, being alive\" and appears mainly in news, science and reports. Very common are compounds such as 生存者 \"survivors\", 生存率 \"survival rate\" and 生存確認 \"confirmation of being alive\". As a する-verb it is intransitive: 生存する. In everyday speech people more often say 生きる, 生き残る or 助かる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コウイチの家に行ってみたけど、留守でした。","romaji":"Kouichi no ie ni itte mita kedo, rusu deshita.","german":"I went to Koichi's house, but he wasn't in.","furigana":"いえ|い|るす"},{"japanese":"今日は留守にします。","romaji":"Kyō wa rusu ni shimasu.","german":"I won't be home today.","furigana":"きょう|るす"},{"japanese":"留守の間、猫の世話をお願いできますか。","romaji":"Rusu no aida, neko no sewa o onegai dekimasu ka.","german":"Could you take care of the cat while I'm away?","furigana":"るす|あいだ|ねこ|せわ|ねが"}],"notes":"留守 means that someone is not at home (or not at the office). Typical patterns: 留守だ \"is not in\", 家を留守にする \"to leave the house empty, to be away\", 留守の間 / 留守中 \"during one's absence\". Related are 留守番 \"house-sitting\", 留守番電話 or 留守電 \"answering machine\" and 居留守を使う \"to pretend not to be home\". For absence from school or work, 欠席 or 休み is used instead.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 33"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"真実はいつも一つしかありません。","romaji":"Shinjitsu wa itsumo hitotsu shika arimasen.","german":"There is always only one truth.","furigana":"しんじつ|ひと"},{"japanese":"真実を知りたいです。","romaji":"Shinjitsu o shiritai desu.","german":"I want to know the truth.","furigana":"しんじつ|し"},{"japanese":"彼が話したことは真実ではありませんでした。","romaji":"Kare ga hanashita koto wa shinjitsu de wa arimasen deshita.","german":"What he said was not the truth.","furigana":"かれ|はな|しんじつ"}],"notes":"真実 is \"the truth\", that is, how something really is, and sounds weightier than the everyday 本当 (本当のこと). It is often used with を: 真実を知る, 真実を話す, 真実を明らかにする. 事実, on the other hand, is an objectively verifiable fact, while 真実 means the truth behind things. Register: rather literary, common in crime stories and news.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"それは私の専門外です。","romaji":"Sore wa watashi no senmongai desu.","german":"That's outside my field of expertise.","furigana":"わたし|せんもんがい"},{"japanese":"彼の専門は物理学です。","romaji":"Kare no senmon wa butsurigaku desu.","german":"His specialty is physics.","furigana":"かれ|せんもん|ぶつりがく"},{"japanese":"大学では何を専門に勉強していますか。","romaji":"Daigaku de wa nani o senmon ni benkyou shite imasu ka.","german":"What are you majoring in at university?","furigana":"だいがく|なに|せんもん|べんきょう"}],"notes":"専門 is a person's field of specialization: 私の専門は経済です, ～を専門にする, 専門外 \"not my field\". There are many compounds: 専門家 \"expert\", 専門学校 \"vocational school\", 専門用語 \"technical term\". As a suffix, ～専門 means \"exclusively for\" (女性専門のジム). Specifically for a university major there is also 専攻.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 15"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その時、政府は市民を助けてくれないんだってことだけ学びました。","romaji":"Sono toki, seifu wa shimin o tasukete kurenai n datte koto dake manabimashita.","german":"At that time, I learned only that the government would not help its citizens.","furigana":"とき|せいふ|しみん|たす|まな"},{"japanese":"政府が新しい政策を発表しました。","romaji":"Seifu ga atarashii seisaku o happyō shimashita.","german":"The government announced a new policy.","furigana":"せいふ|あたら|せいさく|はっぴょう"},{"japanese":"政府は来年から税金を上げると言っています。","romaji":"Seifu wa rainen kara zeikin o ageru to itte imasu.","german":"The government says it will raise taxes from next year.","furigana":"せいふ|らいねん|ぜいきん|あ|い"}],"notes":"政府 is the government as the state apparatus: 日本政府, 政府の発表, 政府が決める. To be distinguished from it are 政治 \"politics\", 内閣 \"cabinet\" and 首相 \"prime minister\". For local governments, one does not say 政府 but 自治体 or 市役所. Typical combinations: 政府が発表する, 政府に求める, 政府の方針.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 21"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"東京弁は共通語とほとんど同じですよ。","romaji":"Tōkyōben wa kyōtsūgo to hotondo onaji desu yo.","german":"The Tokyo dialect is almost the same as the standard language.","furigana":"とうきょうべん|きょうつうご|おな"},{"japanese":"関西の友だちに「東京弁は冷たく聞こえる」と言われました。","romaji":"Kansai no tomodachi ni 'Toukyouben wa tsumetaku kikoeru' to iwaremashita.","german":"A friend from Kansai told me that the Tokyo dialect sounds cold.","furigana":"かんさい|とも|とうきょうべん|つめ|き|い"},{"japanese":"落語では、昔の東京弁がよく使われます。","romaji":"Rakugo de wa, mukashi no Toukyouben ga yoku tsukawaremasu.","german":"In rakugo, old Tokyo speech is often used.","furigana":"らくご|むかし|とうきょうべん|つか"}],"notes":"東京弁 is the Tokyo speech. Because today's 標準語 or 共通語 is based on the language of Tokyo, many Tokyoites do not perceive their speech as a dialect at all; people from other regions, especially Kansai, are the ones who often say 東京弁. In a narrower sense, it also means the old Tokyo Japanese of the old town (下町), as heard in rakugo. Formed with the suffix ～弁, like 大阪弁 or 関西弁.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"それって何弁？","romaji":"Sore tte naniben?","german":"What dialect is that?","furigana":"なにべん"},{"japanese":"おばあちゃんは今でも広島弁で話します。","romaji":"Obaachan wa ima demo Hiroshimaben de hanashimasu.","german":"My grandmother still speaks in the Hiroshima dialect.","furigana":"いま|ひろしまべん|はな"},{"japanese":"兄は弁が立つから、けんかしてもいつも負けてしまう。","romaji":"Ani wa ben ga tatsu kara, kenka shite mo itsumo makete shimau.","german":"My older brother is so eloquent that I always lose when we argue.","furigana":"あに|べん|た|ま"}],"notes":"～弁 attaches as a suffix to place or region names and denotes their dialect: 関西弁, 大阪弁, 東北弁, 博多弁. With 何弁？ one asks which dialect something is; in a factual or scholarly register, 方言 is used. As a word in its own right, 弁 means \"speech, eloquence\", above all in the phrase 弁が立つ \"to be eloquent, to speak well\". The kanji 弁 also appears in quite different words such as 弁当 or 弁護士.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ごめん、ちょっと急ぐから先に行くね！","romaji":"Gomen, chotto isogu kara saki ni iku ne!","german":"Sorry, I'm in a bit of a hurry, so I'm going ahead!","furigana":"いそ|さき|い"},{"japanese":"時間がないので急ぎましょう。","romaji":"Jikan ga nai node isogimashō.","german":"We have no time, so let's hurry.","furigana":"じかん|いそ"},{"japanese":"急いで食べると、体によくないですよ。","romaji":"Isoide taberu to, karada ni yokunai desu yo.","german":"Eating in a hurry isn't good for your body.","furigana":"いそ|た|からだ"}],"notes":"急ぐ means \"to hurry, to be in a hurry\" and is mostly intransitive: 駅へ急ぐ, and with a route also 道を急ぐ. Transitively it can mean \"to speed something up\" (仕事を急ぐ). Very common are 急いで＋verb \"to do something quickly, in a rush\" and 急いでいる \"to be in a hurry\". Unlike 慌てる (to get flustered, lose one's head), 急ぐ is neutral. Proverb: 急がば回れ \"more haste, less speed\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 6"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"父が急死した時、私は高校生でした。","romaji":"Chichi ga kyūshi shita toki, watashi wa kōkōsei deshita.","german":"I was a high school student when my father died suddenly.","furigana":"ちち|きゅうし|とき|わたし|こうこうせい"},{"japanese":"急死のニュースに驚きました。","romaji":"Kyūshi no nyūsu ni odorokimashita.","german":"I was shocked by the news of the sudden death.","furigana":"きゅうし|おどろ"},{"japanese":"有名な歌手が四十歳で急死しました。","romaji":"Yuumei na kashu ga yonjussai de kyuushi shimashita.","german":"A famous singer died suddenly at the age of forty.","furigana":"ゆうめい|かしゅ|よんじゅっさい|きゅうし"}],"notes":"急死 is a sudden, unexpected death, for example from heart failure or illness, and as a する-verb it is intransitive: 急死する. The word is matter-of-fact and typical of news and reports. For accidents one rather speaks of 事故死. When talking about one's own relatives in conversation, 急に亡くなりました sounds more considerate.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"京都行き急行は三番乗り場から出るみたいだよ。","romaji":"Kyouto-iki kyuukou wa sanban noriba kara deru mitai da yo.","german":"The express to Kyoto seems to leave from platform three.","furigana":"きょうとい|きゅうこう|さんばんの|ば|で"},{"japanese":"急行電車に乗りました。","romaji":"Kyūkō densha ni norimashita.","german":"I got on an express train.","furigana":"きゅうこうでんしゃ|の"},{"japanese":"事故の知らせを聞いて、警察が現場に急行しました。","romaji":"Jiko no shirase o kiite, keisatsu ga genba ni kyuukou shimashita.","german":"Hearing the report of the accident, the police rushed to the scene.","furigana":"じこ|し|き|けいさつ|げんば|きゅうこう"}],"notes":"急行 is above all the express train: it stops only at the more important stations. The usual ranking is 普通 or 各駅停車 < 快速 < 急行 < 特急; on private railways the 急行 usually costs no extra fare. As a する-verb, 急行する means \"to rush (to a place)\" and is typical of news reports: 現場に急行する. Common phrases are 急行に乗る and ～行きの急行.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"全治六ヶ月みたい。","romaji":"Zenchi rokkagetsu mitai.","german":"It seems it will take about six months to heal completely.","furigana":"ぜんちろっかげつ"},{"japanese":"全治三ヶ月の怪我です。","romaji":"Zenchi sankagetsu no kega desu.","german":"It's an injury that takes three months to heal.","furigana":"ぜんちさんかげつ|けが"},{"japanese":"けがが全治するまで、サッカーは休みます。","romaji":"Kega ga zenchi suru made, sakkaa wa yasumimasu.","german":"I'll sit out soccer until the injury has fully healed.","furigana":"ぜんち|やす"}],"notes":"全治 means \"complete recovery\" and appears mainly in medical statements and news: 全治三か月のけが \"an injury expected to take three months to heal\". The time span after 全治 thus gives the expected time until recovery. As a する-verb it is intransitive: けがが全治する. In everyday speech one rather says 治る or 完治する.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その病気は今はまだ不治の病とされています。","romaji":"Sono byouki wa ima wa mada fuji no yamai to sarete imasu.","german":"That disease is still considered incurable at present.","furigana":"びょうき|いま|ふじ|やまい"},{"japanese":"不治の病と診断されました。","romaji":"Fuji no yamai to shindan saremashita.","german":"He was diagnosed with an incurable disease.","furigana":"ふじ|やまい|しんだん"},{"japanese":"医学が進んで、不治の病は少しずつ減っています。","romaji":"Igaku ga susunde, fuji no yamai wa sukoshi zutsu hette imasu.","german":"As medicine advances, incurable diseases are gradually decreasing.","furigana":"いがく|すす|ふじ|やまい|すこ|へ"}],"notes":"不治 means \"incurability\" and occurs practically only in the set phrase 不治の病 \"incurable disease\". The word is elevated or literary; in conversation one rather says 治らない病気. Besides ふじ, the reading ふち is also possible, but rarer. Do not confuse it with the similar-sounding 富士 (Mt. Fuji).","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私のひいおばあちゃんは明治生まれでした。","romaji":"Watashi no hiiobaachan wa Meiji umare deshita.","german":"My great-grandmother was born in the Meiji era.","furigana":"わたし|めいじう"},{"japanese":"明治時代に日本は大きく変わりました。","romaji":"Meiji jidai ni Nihon wa ōkiku kawarimashita.","german":"Japan changed greatly in the Meiji period.","furigana":"めいじじだい|にほん|おお|か"},{"japanese":"この建物は明治四十年に建てられました。","romaji":"Kono tatemono wa Meiji yonjuu-nen ni tateraremashita.","german":"This building was built in the 40th year of Meiji.","furigana":"たてもの|めいじよんじゅうねん|た"}],"notes":"明治 is the Meiji era (1868-1912), in which Japan modernized greatly. Japanese eras (元号) are also used to count years: 明治四十年, 明治生まれ, 明治時代. The order of the modern eras is 明治 → 大正 → 昭和 → 平成 → 令和. An important term is 明治維新 \"Meiji Restoration\"; 明治 is also a well-known company name (chocolate, dairy products).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ウチの犬はシャワーを浴びるのが好きです。","romaji":"Uchi no inu wa shawā o abiru no ga suki desu.","german":"Our dog likes being showered.","furigana":"いぬ|あ|す"},{"japanese":"朝シャワーを浴びます。","romaji":"Asa shawā o abimasu.","german":"I take a shower in the morning.","furigana":"あさ|あ"},{"japanese":"歌手は観客の拍手を浴びた。","romaji":"Kashu wa kankyaku no hakushu o abita.","german":"The singer was showered with applause from the audience.","furigana":"かしゅ|かんきゃく|はくしゅ|あ"}],"notes":"With 浴びる, the thing that hits the body takes を: シャワーを浴びる (to take a shower), 日光を浴びる (to get sunlight). Figurative uses are also common: 拍手を浴びる (to be showered with applause) and 批判を浴びる (to draw criticism). It is a transitive ichidan verb: 浴びます・浴びて・浴びない. For a bath in a bathtub, you usually say お風呂に入る.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"トトロの大きなおなかの上に乗って日光浴がしたいです。","romaji":"Totoro no ookina onaka no ue ni notte nikkouyoku ga shitai desu.","german":"I want to climb onto Totoro's big belly and sunbathe.","furigana":"おお|うえ|の|にっこうよく"},{"japanese":"公園で日光浴をしました。","romaji":"Kōen de nikkōyoku o shimashita.","german":"I sunbathed in the park.","furigana":"こうえん|にっこうよく"},{"japanese":"猫が窓のそばで日光浴をしています。","romaji":"Neko ga mado no soba de nikkouyoku o shite imasu.","german":"The cat is sunbathing by the window.","furigana":"ねこ|まど|にっこうよく"}],"notes":"日光浴 means \"sunbath, to sunbathe\" and is used with をする or as a する-verb: 日光浴(を)する. It also fits animals (猫が日光浴をしている). To be distinguished from 日焼け \"suntan, sunburn\". 浴 means \"to bathe\", as in 森林浴 \"forest bathing\" and 海水浴 \"sea bathing\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ノリで特急に乗ることに決めました。","romaji":"Nori de tokkyū ni noru koto ni kimemashita.","german":"We decided on a whim to take the limited express.","furigana":"とっきゅう|の|き"},{"japanese":"特急で大阪まで行きます。","romaji":"Tokkyū de Ōsaka made ikimasu.","german":"I'm going to Osaka by limited express.","furigana":"とっきゅう|おおさか|い"},{"japanese":"特急に乗るには、特急券が必要です。","romaji":"Tokkyuu ni noru ni wa, tokkyuuken ga hitsuyou desu.","german":"To take the limited express, you need a limited express ticket.","furigana":"とっきゅう|の|とっきゅうけん|ひつよう"}],"notes":"特急 is the short form of 特別急行 and refers to the fastest train below the Shinkansen. On JR you need a 特急券 (limited express ticket) in addition to your fare; on some private railways the 特急 costs no extra. Ranking: 普通 < 快速 < 急行 < 特急. Figuratively, 特急で also means \"at top speed\" (特急で仕上げる).","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コウイチは真っ黒なニンニクが好きなんです。","romaji":"Kouichi wa makkuro na ninniku ga suki na n desu.","german":"Koichi likes black garlic.","furigana":"ま|くろ|す"},{"japanese":"日焼けで真っ黒になりました。","romaji":"Hiyake de makkuro ni narimashita.","german":"I got deeply tanned from the sun.","furigana":"ひや|ま|くろ"},{"japanese":"トーストを焼きすぎて、真っ黒になってしまった。","romaji":"Toosuto o yakisugite, makkuro ni natte shimatta.","german":"I toasted the bread too long and it turned completely black.","furigana":"や|ま|くろ"}],"notes":"真っ黒 means \"deep black, pitch black\" and is used with な or の before nouns and with になる: 真っ黒な髪, 真っ黒になる. It also means \"heavily sun-tanned\" (日焼けで真っ黒) and \"dirty, charred\"; colloquially it even means \"clearly guilty\", opposite 真っ白 \"innocent\". The pattern 真っ＋color also exists in 真っ白, 真っ赤 and 真っ青 (also \"deathly pale\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ヤグルマギクの花言葉は「幸福」です。","romaji":"Yagurumagiku no hanakotoba wa kōfuku desu.","german":"In the language of flowers, the cornflower stands for 「幸福」.","furigana":"はなことば|こうふく"},{"japanese":"幸福な生活を送りたいです。","romaji":"Kōfuku na seikatsu o okuritai desu.","german":"I want to lead a happy life.","furigana":"こうふく|せいかつ|おく"},{"japanese":"家族の幸福を一番に考えています。","romaji":"Kazoku no koufuku o ichiban ni kangaete imasu.","german":"I put my family's happiness first.","furigana":"かぞく|こうふく|いちばん|かんが"}],"notes":"幸福 means \"happiness\" in the sense of a lasting state of well-being and sounds rather literary or elevated; in everyday speech one mostly says 幸せ. As a な-adjective: 幸福な人生, 幸福な家庭. Luck in the sense of chance or good fortune, on the other hand, is 運 or 幸運 (運がいい). Typical combinations: 幸福を祈る, 人々の幸福.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"君の笑顔がマジで大好きなんだよ！","romaji":"Kimi no egao ga maji de daisuki na n da yo!","german":"I really love your smile!","furigana":"きみ|えがお|だいす"},{"japanese":"彼女はいつも笑顔です。","romaji":"Kanojo wa itsumo egao desu.","german":"She always has a smile on her face.","furigana":"かのじょ|えがお"},{"japanese":"店員さんが笑顔で「いらっしゃいませ」と言いました。","romaji":"Ten'insan ga egao de 'irasshaimase' to iimashita.","german":"The shop assistant said 「いらっしゃいませ」 with a smile.","furigana":"てんいん|えがお|い"}],"notes":"笑顔 is a smiling face, i.e. \"smile\" as a facial expression. Typical combinations: 笑顔で～ \"with a smile\", 笑顔になる \"to start smiling\", 笑顔を見せる, 笑顔がすてき. To be distinguished from 笑い \"laughter\" and the more literary ほほえみ \"gentle smile\". In service and advertising, 笑顔 is a very common word.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"え、会社辞めるの？","romaji":"E, kaisha yameru no?","german":"What, you're quitting the company?","furigana":"かいしゃや"},{"japanese":"会社を辞める決意をしました。","romaji":"Kaisha o yameru ketsui o shimashita.","german":"I made up my mind to leave the company.","furigana":"かいしゃ|や|けつい"},{"japanese":"部活を辞めて、アルバイトを始めました。","romaji":"Bukatsu o yamete, arubaito o hajimemashita.","german":"I quit the school club and started a part-time job.","furigana":"ぶかつ|や|はじ"}],"notes":"辞める is transitive (X を辞める) and means to give up a job, school, club or office: 会社を辞める, 学校を辞める, 部活を辞める. 止める/やめる \"to stop an action or habit\" (たばこをやめる) is read the same way; it is usually written in kana. The more formal word for leaving one's occupation is 退職する.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コウイチは心にもないお世辞を言うのが下手です。","romaji":"Kouichi wa kokoro ni mo nai oseji o iu no ga heta desu.","german":"Koichi is bad at giving compliments he doesn't mean.","furigana":"こころ|せじ|い|へた"},{"japanese":"お世辞を言わないでください。","romaji":"Oseji o iwanaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't flatter me.","furigana":"せじ|い"},{"japanese":"お世辞でもうれしいです。","romaji":"Oseji demo ureshii desu.","german":"I'm happy even if it's just flattery.","furigana":"せじ"}],"notes":"お世辞 is a compliment that is not entirely sincere, that is, flattery: お世辞を言う, お世辞がうまい. In reply to praise, one answers modestly with お世辞でもうれしいです, and お世辞抜きで means \"honestly, without flattering\". A sincere compliment, on the other hand, is 褒め言葉 or the verb 褒める. The お is a fixed part of the word.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"まずは入門コースから始めましょう。","romaji":"Mazu wa nyuumon koosu kara hajimemashou.","german":"Let's start with the introductory course first.","furigana":"にゅうもん|はじ"},{"japanese":"日本語入門のクラスに入りました。","romaji":"Nihongo nyūmon no kurasu ni hairimashita.","german":"I joined a beginning Japanese class.","furigana":"にほんごにゅうもん|はい"},{"japanese":"彼は十五歳で相撲部屋に入門しました。","romaji":"Kare wa juugosai de sumou beya ni nyuumon shimashita.","german":"He entered a sumo stable at the age of fifteen.","furigana":"かれ|じゅうごさい|すもうべや|にゅうもん"}],"notes":"入門 now mostly means \"introduction\": 入門書 \"introductory book\", 入門コース, 入門編, 日本語入門. The original meaning is \"to enter as a pupil under a master\", for example in sumo, martial arts or rakugo: 相撲部屋に入門する. In contrast to 初級 \"beginner level\", 入門 emphasizes the very first step into a field.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この問題に関するあなたの意見を教えてください。","romaji":"Kono mondai ni kansuru anata no iken o oshiete kudasai.","german":"Please tell me your opinion on this issue.","furigana":"もんだい|かん|いけん|おし"},{"japanese":"日本の文化に関する本を読みました。","romaji":"Nihon no bunka ni kansuru hon o yomimashita.","german":"I read a book about Japanese culture.","furigana":"にほん|ぶんか|かん|ほん|よ"},{"japanese":"この件に関して、何か質問はありますか。","romaji":"Kono ken ni kanshite, nanika shitsumon wa arimasu ka.","german":"Do you have any questions regarding this matter?","furigana":"けん|かん|なに|しつもん"}],"notes":"関する occurs practically only in two forms: ～に関する + noun \"regarding, about\" (環境に関する本) and adverbial ～に関して (この件に関して). It is more formal and literary than ～について, which is more common in conversation. Typical in news, reports and business emails.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"阪神電車で行く予定です。","romaji":"Hanshin densha de iku yotei desu.","german":"I'm planning to go by Hanshin train.","furigana":"はんしんでんしゃ|い|よてい"},{"japanese":"阪神タイガースのファンです。","romaji":"Hanshin Taigāsu no fan desu.","german":"I'm a fan of the Hanshin Tigers.","furigana":"はんしん"},{"japanese":"大阪と神戸のあたりを「阪神」と呼びます。","romaji":"Oosaka to Koube no atari o 'Hanshin' to yobimasu.","german":"The area around Osaka and Kobe is called \"Hanshin\".","furigana":"おおさか||はんしん|よ"}],"notes":"阪神 combines 阪 (大阪) and 神 (神戸) and refers to the region between Osaka and Kobe. It is best known as a company name: 阪神電車, 阪神百貨店 and the baseball team 阪神タイガース. Following the same pattern there are 京阪 (Kyoto-Osaka) and 京浜 (Tokyo-Yokohama). As a proper name, it is used without an article or any addition.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このTシャツは前面のデザインがすごく良い。","romaji":"Kono tiishatsu wa zenmen no dezain ga sugoku yoi.","german":"The design on the front of this T-shirt is really good.","furigana":"ぜんめん|よ"},{"japanese":"建物の前面を修理しています。","romaji":"Tatemono no zenmen o shūri shite imasu.","german":"The front of the building is being repaired.","furigana":"たてもの|ぜんめん|しゅうり"},{"japanese":"このポスターは、商品の安さを前面に出しています。","romaji":"Kono posutaa wa, shouhin no yasusa o zenmen ni dashite imasu.","german":"This poster puts the low price of the products front and center.","furigana":"しょうひん|やす|ぜんめん|だ"}],"notes":"前面 is the front side or front of a building, vehicle or garment and sounds somewhat matter-of-fact. To be distinguished from it are 正面 (the side facing you; \"straight ahead\") and 表 (the front side of a sheet). Figuratively, ～を前面に出す means \"to put in the foreground\". Do not confuse with the identically read 全面 \"entire surface, completely\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地面にはたくさんの葉っぱが落ちていました。","romaji":"Jimen ni wa takusan no happa ga ochite imashita.","german":"Lots of leaves had fallen on the ground.","furigana":"じめん|は|お"},{"japanese":"雨で地面が濡れています。","romaji":"Ame de jimen ga nurete imasu.","german":"The ground is wet from the rain.","furigana":"あめ|じめん|ぬ"},{"japanese":"ボールが地面に落ちる前に取りました。","romaji":"Booru ga jimen ni ochiru mae ni torimashita.","german":"I caught the ball before it fell to the ground.","furigana":"じめん|お|まえ|と"}],"notes":"地面 is the ground outdoors, whether soil or asphalt: 地面に座る, 地面に落ちる, 地面が濡れる. The floor inside buildings is 床 (ゆか), and 土 denotes soil as a material. Colloquially people also say 地べた (地べたに座る). The particle に for movement onto the ground is typical.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"外面はきれいですが中は古いです。","romaji":"Gaimen wa kirei desu ga naka wa furui desu.","german":"The outside looks nice, but the inside is old.","furigana":"がいめん|なか|ふる"},{"japanese":"このボールの外面は革でできています。","romaji":"Kono booru no gaimen wa kawa de dekite imasu.","german":"The outer surface of this ball is made of leather.","furigana":"がいめん|かわ"},{"japanese":"外面的な美しさより、心の美しさが大切です。","romaji":"Gaimenteki na utsukushisa yori, kokoro no utsukushisa ga taisetsu desu.","german":"Inner beauty is more important than outward beauty.","furigana":"がいめんてき|うつく|こころ|うつく|たいせつ"}],"notes":"外面（がいめん） refers to the outer surface or outward appearance of a thing: 建物の外面, ボールの外面. It sounds rather matter-of-fact and written; in everyday speech one usually says 外側 \"outside\" or 見た目 \"appearance\". Note: with the reading そとづら the same word means \"the face one shows to outsiders\" (外面がいい \"friendly toward outsiders\"). The derivation 外面的 \"outward, superficial\" is also common.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"有名な料理人がこの店で働いています。","romaji":"Yuumei na ryourinin ga kono mise de hataraite imasu.","german":"A famous chef works at this restaurant.","furigana":"ゆうめい|りょうりにん|みせ|はたら"},{"japanese":"将来は料理人になりたいです。","romaji":"Shourai wa ryourinin ni naritai desu.","german":"I want to become a cook in the future.","furigana":"しょうらい|りょうりにん"},{"japanese":"父は料理人なので、家でもおいしいものを作ってくれます。","romaji":"Chichi wa ryourinin na node, ie de mo oishii mono o tsukutte kuremasu.","german":"My father is a cook, so he makes delicious food at home too.","furigana":"ちち|りょうりにん|いえ|つく"}],"notes":"料理人 is the general term for someone who cooks professionally, from restaurant cooks to top chefs; the reading is りょうりにん, not りょうりじん. In everyday life the loanwords コック \"cook\" and シェフ \"head chef\" are often heard, which suggest Western cuisine more; a cook of Japanese cuisine is traditionally called 板前. Someone who cooks well at home is not a 料理人 but 料理が上手な人.","levelNote":"Compound: 料理 (N5) + 人 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は有名な芸術家です。","romaji":"Kare wa yuumei na geijutsuka desu.","german":"He is a famous artist.","furigana":"かれ|ゆうめい|げいじゅつか"},{"japanese":"この町には若い芸術家がたくさん住んでいます。","romaji":"Kono machi ni wa wakai geijutsuka ga takusan sunde imasu.","german":"Many young artists live in this town.","furigana":"まち|わか|げいじゅつか|す"},{"japanese":"芸術家として生活するのは簡単ではありません。","romaji":"Geijutsuka to shite seikatsu suru no wa kantan de wa arimasen.","german":"Making a living as an artist is not easy.","furigana":"げいじゅつか|せいかつ|かんたん"}],"notes":"芸術家 is the general term for creators of art of any kind; more specific are 画家 \"painter\", 彫刻家 \"sculptor\" or 音楽家 \"musician\". The suffix 家 here denotes someone who practices an art or a field (cf. 作家 \"writer\"). The loanword アーティスト is also used in everyday speech for pop singers and bands, whereas 芸術家 tends to mean serious, \"high\" art and sounds somewhat more elevated.","levelNote":"Compound: 芸術 (N3) + 家 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この電車のドアは、自動的には開きません。","romaji":"Kono densha no doa wa, jidōteki ni wa hirakimasen.","german":"The doors of this train do not open automatically.","furigana":"でんしゃ|じどうてき|ひら"},{"japanese":"外が暗くなると、電気が自動的につきます。","romaji":"Soto ga kuraku naru to, denki ga jidouteki ni tsukimasu.","german":"When it gets dark outside, the light comes on automatically.","furigana":"そと|くら|でんき|じどうてき"},{"japanese":"このパソコンは夜中に自動的にアップデートされます。","romaji":"Kono pasokon wa yonaka ni jidouteki ni appudeeto saremasu.","german":"This computer is updated automatically at night.","furigana":"よなか|じどうてき"}],"notes":"自動的 is a な-adjective and appears almost always adverbially as 自動的に \"automatically, by itself\": ドアが自動的に閉まる. Before a noun it is 自動的な. The base word 自動 forms compounds such as 自動ドア or 自動販売機; the opposite is 手動 \"manual\". Figuratively, 自動的に also describes consequences that occur without any action of one's own, e.g. 会員になると自動的に割引になります.","levelNote":"Compound: 自動 (N3) + 的 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は基本的にはスピリチュアルな事は信じません。","romaji":"Watashi wa kihonteki ni wa supirichuaru na koto wa shinjimasen.","german":"Basically, I don't believe in spiritual things.","furigana":"わたし|きほんてき|こと|しん"},{"japanese":"基本的なことから始めましょう。","romaji":"Kihonteki na koto kara hajimemashō.","german":"Let's start with the basics.","furigana":"きほんてき|はじ"},{"japanese":"基本的に土曜日は休みですが、忙しい時は働きます。","romaji":"Kihonteki ni doyoubi wa yasumi desu ga, isogashii toki wa hatarakimasu.","german":"Basically I have Saturdays off, but when it's busy, I work.","furigana":"きほんてき|どようび|やす|いそが|とき|はたら"}],"notes":"基本的 is a な-adjective: 基本的な + noun means \"basic, elementary\" (基本的なルール); adverbially, 基本的に means \"basically, in principle\". 基本的に often hints that there are exceptions - similar to \"as a rule\". The base noun 基本 \"basis\" also stands alone (基本が大切). For \"from the ground up, radical\" one uses 根本的 instead.","levelNote":"Compound: 基本 (N3) + 的 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は絶対に本に折り目をつけません。","romaji":"Kare wa zettai ni hon ni orime o tsukemasen.","german":"He never dog-ears his books.","furigana":"かれ|ぜったい|ほん|お|め"},{"japanese":"紙に折り目をつけました。","romaji":"Kami ni orime o tsukemashita.","german":"I made a fold in the paper.","furigana":"かみ|お|め"},{"japanese":"このズボンにはきれいな折り目がついています。","romaji":"Kono zubon ni wa kirei na orime ga tsuite imasu.","german":"These trousers have a neat pressed crease.","furigana":"お|め"}],"notes":"折り目 is the line that forms when paper or cloth is folded: a fold, crease, or the pressed crease of trousers. Typical are 折り目をつける \"to make a crease\" and 折り目がつく \"to get a crease\". Unlike しわ \"wrinkle, crinkle\", 折り目 is a straight, usually deliberate fold line. In the set phrase 折り目正しい it means \"correct, well-mannered\" in behavior.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"明日は私の卒業式です。","romaji":"Ashita wa watashi no sotsugyōshiki desu.","german":"Tomorrow is my graduation ceremony.","furigana":"あした|わたし|そつぎょうしき"},{"japanese":"卒業式で泣きました。","romaji":"Sotsugyōshiki de nakimashita.","german":"I cried at the graduation ceremony.","furigana":"そつぎょうしき|な"},{"japanese":"卒業式には両親も来てくれました。","romaji":"Sotsugyoushiki ni wa ryoushin mo kite kuremashita.","german":"My parents also came to my graduation ceremony.","furigana":"そつぎょうしき|りょうしん|き"}],"notes":"卒業式 is the graduation ceremony of a school or university; in Japan it usually takes place in March, and its April counterpart is 入学式. One says 卒業式に出る／出席する \"to attend the ceremony\" and 卒業式で泣く \"to cry at the ceremony\". 卒業 applies to any school graduation, including elementary and junior high school, not only university.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 15"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人間はみんな不完全です。","romaji":"Ningen wa minna fukanzen desu.","german":"All human beings are imperfect.","furigana":"にんげん|ふかんぜん"},{"japanese":"このレポートはまだ不完全です。","romaji":"Kono repōto wa mada fukanzen desu.","german":"This report is still incomplete.","furigana":"ふかんぜん"},{"japanese":"不完全な情報で決めるのは危ないです。","romaji":"Fukanzen na jouhou de kimeru no wa abunai desu.","german":"Making decisions based on incomplete information is dangerous.","furigana":"ふかんぜん|じょうほう|き|あぶ"}],"notes":"不完全 is the opposite of 完全 \"complete, perfect\" and works as a な-adjective and noun: 不完全な情報, まだ不完全です. It covers both \"incomplete\" and \"imperfect, flawed\". For a piece of work that is simply not finished yet, one often says 未完成; for \"insufficient\", 不十分 fits better. The tone is matter-of-fact and slightly literary.","levelNote":"Compound: 不 (N3) + 完全 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"希望する大学に入学できるといいですね！","romaji":"Kibō suru daigaku ni nyūgaku dekiru to iidesu ne!","german":"I hope you can get into the university you want!","furigana":"きぼう|だいがく|にゅうがく"},{"japanese":"転職を希望しています。","romaji":"Tenshoku o kibō shite imasu.","german":"I'm hoping to change jobs.","furigana":"てんしょく|きぼう"},{"japanese":"飛行機の席は窓側を希望します。","romaji":"Hikouki no seki wa madogawa o kibou shimasu.","german":"For the airplane seat, I would like a window seat.","furigana":"ひこうき|せき|まどがわ|きぼう"}],"notes":"希望する means \"to wish, to hope for something\" and is transitive: 〜を希望する. It sounds more formal than 〜たい and typically appears in applications, forms and when stating preferences (窓側の席を希望します). The derivatives ご希望 \"your wish\" and 希望者 \"applicant, interested person\" are very common. The noun 希望 alone also means \"hope\" (希望を持つ). For personal wishes in daily life, 〜たい or 欲しい is more usual.","levelNote":"Derived from 希望 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"食べ物を残すのは良くないですよ。","romaji":"Tabemono o nokosu no wa yokunaidesu yo.","german":"It's not good to leave food uneaten.","furigana":"た|もの|のこ|よ"},{"japanese":"母は机の上にメモを残して出かけました。","romaji":"Haha wa tsukue no ue ni memo o nokoshite dekakemashita.","german":"My mother left a note on the table and went out.","furigana":"はは|つくえ|うえ|のこ|で"},{"japanese":"来月のために、お金を少し残しておきます。","romaji":"Raigetsu no tame ni, okane o sukoshi nokoshite okimasu.","german":"I'll set aside a little money for next month.","furigana":"らいげつ|かね|すこ|のこ"}],"notes":"残す is transitive and means \"to leave over, to leave behind\": 食べ物を残す, メモを残す. The intransitive partner is 残る \"to remain\" (ご飯が残っている). With 〜ておく it means \"to keep, to put aside\" (お金を残しておく); figuratively also \"to leave behind\" (記録を残す, 名前を残す). Leaving something behind by accident, on the other hand, is 忘れる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ゼリーを冷蔵庫で固めます。","romaji":"Zerii o reizouko de katamemasu.","german":"I set the jelly in the refrigerator.","furigana":"れいぞうこ|かた"},{"japanese":"雪を固めて、雪だるまを作りました。","romaji":"Yuki o katamete, yukidaruma o tsukurimashita.","german":"I packed the snow firmly and made a snowman.","furigana":"ゆき|かた|ゆき|つく"},{"japanese":"決意を固めました。","romaji":"Ketsui o katamemashita.","german":"I firmed up my resolve.","furigana":"けつい|かた"}],"notes":"固める is transitive and means \"to make firm, harden, solidify\": 雪を固める, ゼリーを冷蔵庫で固める. The intransitive partner is 固まる \"to become firm, to set\". Figuratively it stands for firming up plans and decisions: 決意を固める, 考えを固める. The expression 身を固める means \"to settle down, get married\". The underlying adjective is 固い \"hard, firm\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日は何曜日ですか。","romaji":"Kyou wa nan'youbi desu ka.","german":"What day of the week is it today?","furigana":"きょう|なんようび"},{"japanese":"この店は曜日によって閉まる時間が違います。","romaji":"Kono mise wa youbi ni yotte shimaru jikan ga chigaimasu.","german":"The closing time of this shop differs depending on the day of the week.","furigana":"みせ|ようび|し|じかん|ちが"},{"japanese":"ゴミを出す曜日を間違えてしまいました。","romaji":"Gomi o dasu youbi o machigaete shimaimashita.","german":"I got the day for putting out the garbage wrong.","furigana":"だ|ようび|まちが"}],"notes":"曜日 means \"day of the week\" and is in all the day names: 月曜日, 火曜日 etc. The question is asked with 何曜日（なんようび）; do not confuse it with 何日 \"which date\". In conversation the days are often shortened to 月曜, 火曜. On its own, 曜日 appears mainly in phrases such as 曜日によって \"depending on the day of the week\" or 曜日を間違える \"to get the day of the week wrong\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は生まれは大阪です。","romaji":"Watashi wa umare wa Ōsaka desu.","german":"I was born in Osaka.","furigana":"わたし|う|おおさか"},{"japanese":"大阪はたこ焼きが有名です。","romaji":"Oosaka wa takoyaki ga yuumei desu.","german":"Osaka is famous for takoyaki.","furigana":"おおさか|や|ゆうめい"},{"japanese":"東京から大阪まで新幹線で二時間半ぐらいです。","romaji":"Toukyou kara Oosaka made shinkansen de nijikan han gurai desu.","german":"From Tokyo to Osaka it takes about two and a half hours by Shinkansen.","furigana":"とうきょう|おおさか|しんかんせん|にじかんはん"}],"notes":"大阪 is Japan's second-largest metropolitan area and is both a city (大阪市) and a prefecture (大阪府). It is regarded as the center of 関西 and is known for foods such as たこ焼き and お好み焼き as well as for the 大阪弁 dialect. In Latin script the long vowel is usually omitted (\"Osaka\"). A person from Osaka is called 大阪の人 or 大阪出身の人.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"アイツは他人に関心がないんだよ。","romaji":"Aitsu wa tanin ni kanshin ga nai n da yo.","german":"That guy has no interest in other people, you know.","furigana":"たにん|かんしん"},{"japanese":"環境問題に関心があります。","romaji":"Kankyō mondai ni kanshin ga arimasu.","german":"I am interested in environmental issues.","furigana":"かんきょうもんだい|かんしん"},{"japanese":"若い人の政治への関心が低くなっています。","romaji":"Wakai hito no seiji e no kanshin ga hikuku natte imasu.","german":"Young people's interest in politics is declining.","furigana":"わか|ひと|せいじ|かんしん|ひく"}],"notes":"関心 means \"interest, concern\" and takes に: 〜に関心がある／ない, 〜に関心を持つ. It sounds more matter-of-fact than 興味, which rather expresses personal curiosity and enjoyment (映画に興味がある). 関心 suits social topics in particular and is rated with 高い／低い (政治への関心が低い). Do not confuse it with the identically read 感心 \"to admire, to be impressed\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ボールが顔面に当たりました。","romaji":"Booru ga ganmen ni atarimashita.","german":"The ball hit me in the face.","furigana":"がんめん|あ"},{"japanese":"転んで顔面を強く打ちました。","romaji":"Koronde ganmen o tsuyoku uchimashita.","german":"I fell and hit my face hard.","furigana":"ころ|がんめん|つよ|う"},{"japanese":"このヘルメットは顔面も守ります。","romaji":"Kono herumetto wa ganmen mo mamorimasu.","german":"This helmet also protects the face.","furigana":"がんめん|まも"}],"notes":"顔面 is a technical word for the face as a body part, as it appears in medicine, sports and accident reports: 顔面にボールが当たる, 顔面を打つ. In everyday speech people simply say 顔. 顔面 is not used for facial expression or for \"face\" in the sense of reputation (顔が広い). A well-known phrase is 顔面蒼白 \"deathly pale\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"記念日には、彼とディナーに行く予定です。","romaji":"Kinenbi ni wa, kare to dinā ni iku yotei desu.","german":"On our anniversary, I plan to go out for dinner with him.","furigana":"きねんび|かれ|い|よてい"},{"japanese":"結婚記念日にレストランに行きます。","romaji":"Kekkon kinenbi ni resutoran ni ikimasu.","german":"We go to a restaurant on our wedding anniversary.","furigana":"けっこんきねんび|い"},{"japanese":"今日は学校の創立記念日です。","romaji":"Kyō wa gakkō no sōritsu kinenbi desu.","german":"Today is the school's founding anniversary.","furigana":"きょう|がっこう|そうりつきねんび"}],"notes":"記念日 is an anniversary or day of remembrance that commemorates a particular event. 結婚記念日 is a wedding anniversary, 創立記念日 the founding day of an institution. 記念日に indicates what one does on that day. The word does not automatically mean a public holiday with the day off.","levelNote":"Compound: 記念 (N3) + 日 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これ、色んな場面で活用できてすごく便利ですよ！","romaji":"Kore, ironna bamen de katsuyō dekite sugoku benri desu yo!","german":"This is very convenient - you can use it in all kinds of situations!","furigana":"いろ|ばめん|かつよう|べんり"},{"japanese":"この映画の最後の場面が好きです。","romaji":"Kono eiga no saigo no bamen ga suki desu.","german":"I like the last scene of this movie.","furigana":"えいが|さいご|ばめん|す"},{"japanese":"大事な場面になると、いつも緊張してしまいます。","romaji":"Daiji na bamen ni naru to, itsumo kinchou shite shimaimasu.","german":"In important moments, I always get nervous.","furigana":"だいじ|ばめん|きんちょう"}],"notes":"場面 refers on the one hand to a scene in a film, play or book (最後の場面), and on the other hand to a concrete situation or occasion in life: いろいろな場面で使える, 大事な場面. In a film context the loanword シーン is also common. 場合 sounds similar, but means a supposed case (\"in case\"): 雨の場合は中止です. For a general state of affairs one rather uses 状況.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"幸運を呼ぶおまじないよ。","romaji":"Kouun o yobu omajinai yo.","german":"It's a charm that brings good luck.","furigana":"こううん|よ"},{"japanese":"幸運を祈ります。","romaji":"Kōun o inorimasu.","german":"I wish you good luck.","furigana":"こううん|いの"},{"japanese":"幸運にも、電車に間に合いました。","romaji":"Kouun ni mo, densha ni maniaimashita.","german":"Luckily, I made it in time for the train.","furigana":"こううん|でんしゃ|ま|あ"}],"notes":"幸運 means \"luck\" in the sense of fortunate chance, not in the sense of contentment - for that one says 幸せ or 幸福. It is a noun and a な-adjective: 幸運な人, adverbially 幸運にも \"luckily\". Set phrases are 幸運を祈ります \"I wish you good luck\" and 幸運を呼ぶ \"to bring good luck\". In everyday speech one mostly says 運がいい or, colloquially, ラッキー.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"そんなことを言われると、心が折れるよ。","romaji":"Sonna koto o iwareru to, kokoro ga oreru yo.","german":"When you say things like that, I lose heart.","furigana":"い|こころ|お"},{"japanese":"強い風で枝が折れました。","romaji":"Tsuyoi kaze de eda ga oremashita.","german":"A branch broke in the strong wind.","furigana":"つよ|かぜ|えだ|お"},{"japanese":"サッカーの試合で足の骨が折れました。","romaji":"Sakkaa no shiai de ashi no hone ga oremashita.","german":"I broke a bone in my leg in a soccer match.","furigana":"しあい|あし|ほね|お"}],"notes":"折れる is intransitive: something long or thin breaks or snaps (枝が折れる, 骨が折れる). The transitive partner is 折る \"to break, to fold\". Glass or plates, by contrast, are 割れる, devices 壊れる, threads 切れる. Figuratively, 心が折れる means \"to lose heart\" and 骨が折れる also means \"to be laborious\". In directions, 右に折れる means \"to turn right\" (more common in everyday speech: 曲がる).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"週末には食料品を買いにコストコに行きます。","romaji":"Shūmatsu ni wa shokuryōhin o kai ni kosutoko ni ikimasu.","german":"On weekends I go to Costco to buy groceries.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|しょくりょうひん|か|い"},{"japanese":"このデパートの食料品売り場は地下にあります。","romaji":"Kono depaato no shokuryouhin uriba wa chika ni arimasu.","german":"The grocery section of this department store is in the basement.","furigana":"しょくりょうひんう|ば|ちか"},{"japanese":"最近、食料品の値段が上がっています。","romaji":"Saikin, shokuryouhin no nedan ga agatte imasu.","german":"Grocery prices have been rising recently.","furigana":"さいきん|しょくりょうひん|ねだん|あ"}],"notes":"食料品 means food as merchandise, i.e. what you buy at the supermarket; typical are 食料品を買う, 食料品売り場 and 食料品店. For food in general, 食べ物 is used in daily life. 食品 is similar but sounds more technical (食品の表示), and 食料 means food as nourishment rather than as stock or supply (食料が足りない).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"旅行カバンに付ける荷札をさがしています。","romaji":"Ryokō kaban ni tsukeru nifuda o sagashite imasu.","german":"I'm looking for a luggage tag to put on my travel bag.","furigana":"りょこう|つ|にふだ"},{"japanese":"荷物に荷札をつけました。","romaji":"Nimotsu ni nifuda o tsukemashita.","german":"I attached a tag to the luggage.","furigana":"にもつ|にふだ"},{"japanese":"荷札に名前と住所を書いてください。","romaji":"Nifuda ni namae to juusho o kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write your name and address on the tag.","furigana":"にふだ|なまえ|じゅうしょ|か"}],"notes":"荷札 is a tag or label attached to luggage or parcels, usually with a name and address: 荷物に荷札をつける. For suitcases, ネームタグ is also often said today. Related words with 札 \"sign, slip\" are 名札 \"name tag\" on clothing and 値札 \"price tag\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"釣った小さい魚は海に帰します。","romaji":"Tsutta chiisai sakana wa umi ni kaeshimasu.","german":"I put the small fish I catch back into the sea.","furigana":"つ|ちい|さかな|うみ|かえ"},{"japanese":"子供を早く帰しました。","romaji":"Kodomo o hayaku kaeshimashita.","german":"I sent the child home early.","furigana":"こども|はや|かえ"},{"japanese":"こんな遅い時間に、一人で帰すのは心配です。","romaji":"Konna osoi jikan ni, hitori de kaesu no wa shinpai desu.","german":"I'm worried about letting you go home alone at such a late hour.","furigana":"おそ|じかん|ひとり|かえ|しんぱい"}],"notes":"帰す is transitive and means \"to send someone home, to let someone return\"; the intransitive partner is 帰る. It refers to people or animals going back to their place: 子供を家に帰す, 魚を海に帰す. Objects, on the other hand, are given back with the identically read 返す (本を返す). Typical is 早く帰す \"to send home early\", for example when someone is ill.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ストレスで病気が悪化することがあります。","romaji":"Sutoresu de byouki ga akka suru koto ga arimasu.","german":"Stress can make an illness worse.","furigana":"びょうき|あっか"},{"japanese":"天気が悪化しています。","romaji":"Tenki ga akka shite imasu.","german":"The weather is getting worse.","furigana":"てんき|あっか"},{"japanese":"あの二人の関係は最近悪化しています。","romaji":"Ano futari no kankei wa saikin akka shite imasu.","german":"The relationship between those two has been getting worse lately.","furigana":"ふたり|かんけい|さいきんあっか"}],"notes":"悪化 is a Sino-Japanese noun used as an intransitive する-verb: 〜が悪化する \"to worsen, to get worse\". It sounds objective and is typical for news, medical and weather reports (病気が悪化する, 天気が悪化する, 関係が悪化する). In conversation people usually just say 悪くなる. The opposite is 改善する \"to improve\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"メーカーに問い合わせるよ。","romaji":"Mēkā ni toiawaseru yo.","german":"I'll ask the manufacturer.","furigana":"と|あ"},{"japanese":"詳しい情報を問い合わせました。","romaji":"Kuwashii jōhō o toiawasemashita.","german":"I inquired about detailed information.","furigana":"くわ|じょうほう|と|あ"},{"japanese":"駅に電話して、忘れ物について問い合わせました。","romaji":"Eki ni denwa shite, wasuremono ni tsuite toiawasemashita.","german":"I called the station and inquired about a lost item.","furigana":"えき|でんわ|わす|もの|と|あ"}],"notes":"問い合わせる means \"to inquire, make an inquiry\" - usually to a company or office: 〜に〜を問い合わせる or 〜について問い合わせる. It is more official than 聞く and typical for calls to customer service or authorities. Very common is the noun 問い合わせ, for example お問い合わせ \"inquiry, contact\" on websites or 問い合わせ先 \"contact point\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この病気を治すには薬が必要です。","romaji":"Kono byōki o naosu ni wa kusuri ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"Medicine is needed to cure this illness.","furigana":"びょうき|なお|くすり|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"医者が病気を治します。","romaji":"Isha ga byōki o naoshimasu.","german":"A doctor cures illnesses.","furigana":"いしゃ|びょうき|なお"},{"japanese":"早く風邪を治して、学校に行きたいです。","romaji":"Hayaku kaze o naoshite, gakkou ni ikitai desu.","german":"I want to get over my cold quickly and go to school.","furigana":"はや|かぜ|なお|がっこう|い"}],"notes":"治す is transitive and means \"to cure, heal\" - for illnesses and injuries: 病気を治す, けがを治す, 風邪を治す. The subject can be the doctor or the patient himself. The intransitive partner is 治る \"to get well\" (風邪が治った). Note: the identically read 直す means \"to repair, correct\" (時計を直す, 間違いを直す).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この地域には紀元前何年ごろから人が住んでいたのですか。","romaji":"Kono chiiki ni wa kigenzen nannen goro kara hito ga sunde ita no desu ka.","german":"Since about what year BC have people lived in this region?","furigana":"ちいき|きげんぜんなんねん|ひと|す"},{"japanese":"この遺跡は紀元前三千年のものです。","romaji":"Kono iseki wa kigenzen sanzen-nen no mono desu.","german":"These ruins date from 3000 BC.","furigana":"いせき|きげんぜんさんぜんねん"}],"notes":"紀元前 refers, in the Christian calendar, to years before Christ, also called before the common era. It stands directly before the year number: 紀元前三千年. 年ごろ makes the dating approximate. This form is suitable for historical dating; for the very distant history of the Earth, expressions such as 何万年前 are usually used.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"弟は泣き虫で、ちょっとしたことですぐ泣きます。","romaji":"Otouto wa nakimushi de, chotto shita koto de sugu nakimasu.","german":"My younger brother is a crybaby and cries right away over little things.","furigana":"おとうと|な|むし|な"},{"japanese":"子供の頃は泣き虫でした。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro wa nakimushi deshita.","german":"I was a crybaby when I was a child.","furigana":"こども|ころ|な|むし"},{"japanese":"転んだくらいで泣くなんて、泣き虫だなあ。","romaji":"Koronda kurai de naku nante, nakimushi da naa.","german":"Crying just because you fell down, what a crybaby you are.","furigana":"ころ|な|な|むし"}],"notes":"泣き虫 is a teasing expression for someone who cries easily, especially for children. It usually sounds affectionately mocking, but it can also hurt. 虫 is used here as a suffix for people with a typical trait, cf. 弱虫 \"weakling, coward\" and 本の虫 \"bookworm\". As a predicate it takes だ／です: 子供の頃は泣き虫でした.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"赤ちゃんの泣き声が聞こえます。","romaji":"Akachan no nakigoe ga kikoemasu.","german":"I can hear a baby crying.","furigana":"あか|な|ごえ|き"},{"japanese":"隣の部屋から泣き声がして、心配になりました。","romaji":"Tonari no heya kara nakigoe ga shite, shinpai ni narimashita.","german":"I heard crying from the next room and became worried.","furigana":"となり|へや|な|ごえ|しんぱい"},{"japanese":"母は泣き声で事情を説明しました。","romaji":"Haha wa nakigoe de jijō o setsumei shimashita.","german":"My mother explained the circumstances in a tearful voice.","furigana":"はは|な|ごえ|じじょう|せつめい"}],"notes":"泣き声 is the audible sound of crying or a crying voice. 泣き声が聞こえる describes hearing the crying; 泣き声で話す means someone speaks in a tearful or tear-choked voice. The word names the sound and is not the verb 泣く.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は、一番星に願い事をしました。","romaji":"Kanojo wa, ichibanboshi ni negaigoto o shimashita.","german":"She made a wish on the first star.","furigana":"かのじょ|いちばんぼし|ねが|ごと"},{"japanese":"七夕に願い事を書きました。","romaji":"Tanabata ni negaigoto o kakimashita.","german":"I wrote a wish on Tanabata.","furigana":"たなばた|ねが|ごと|か"},{"japanese":"あなたの願い事がかなうといいですね。","romaji":"Anata no negaigoto ga kanau to ii desu ne.","german":"I hope your wish comes true.","furigana":"ねが|ごと"}],"notes":"願い事 is a concrete wish addressed to gods, stars or fate. Typical are 願い事をする \"to make a wish\" and 願い事がかなう \"a wish comes true\"; at 七夕 it is written on colorful strips of paper. The more general 願い also includes requests to people (お願い), while 希望 expresses a more matter-of-fact hope or preference.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あのコンビは関西の芸人です。","romaji":"Ano konbi wa Kansai no geinin desu.","german":"That comedy duo is from Kansai.","furigana":"かんさい|げいにん"},{"japanese":"関西に旅行に行きたいです。","romaji":"Kansai ni ryokō ni ikitai desu.","german":"I want to travel to Kansai.","furigana":"かんさい|りょこう|い"},{"japanese":"関西弁は東京の言葉とアクセントがかなり違います。","romaji":"Kansaiben wa Toukyou no kotoba to akusento ga kanari chigaimasu.","german":"The Kansai dialect has quite a different accent from Tokyo speech.","furigana":"かんさいべん|とうきょう|ことば|ちが"}],"notes":"関西 refers to the region around Osaka, Kyoto and Kobe in the west of the main island; its counterpart is 関東 around Tokyo. You say 関西に住む, 関西出身, 関西の人. The dialect is called 関西弁, and many comedians come from this area. Geographically and officially people also speak of 近畿地方; 関西 is the culturally everyday term.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本の自動車のハンドルは通常右側にあります。","romaji":"Nippon no jidōsha no handoru wa tsūjō migigawa ni arimasu.","german":"The steering wheel of Japanese cars is usually on the right side.","furigana":"にっぽん|じどうしゃ|つうじょうみぎがわ"},{"japanese":"右側を歩いてください。","romaji":"Migigawa o aruite kudasai.","german":"Please walk on the right side.","furigana":"みぎがわ|ある"},{"japanese":"写真の右側にいるのが兄です。","romaji":"Shashin no migigawa ni iru no ga ani desu.","german":"The one on the right side of the photo is my older brother.","furigana":"しゃしん|みぎがわ|あに"}],"notes":"右側 means the right side of something: 〜の右側に／を. For the direction of walking or turning, however, only 右 is used: 右に曲がる, not 右側に曲がる. Typical combinations are 右側通行 \"right-hand traffic\" and 写真の右側 \"on the right in the photo\". The counterpart is 左側; 側 also forms 内側, 外側 and 両側.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このエスカレーターでは、みんな左側に立っていますね。","romaji":"Kono esukarētā de wa, minna hidarigawa ni tatte imasu ne.","german":"Everyone is standing on the left side of this escalator, aren't they?","furigana":"ひだりがわ|た"},{"japanese":"左側に学校があります。","romaji":"Hidarigawa ni gakkō ga arimasu.","german":"There is a school on the left side.","furigana":"ひだりがわ|がっこう"},{"japanese":"日本では、車は道の左側を走ります。","romaji":"Nihon de wa, kuruma wa michi no hidarigawa o hashirimasu.","german":"In Japan, cars drive on the left side of the road.","furigana":"にほん|くるま|みち|ひだりがわ|はし"}],"notes":"左側 is the left side of something: 〜の左側に／を. In Japan cars drive on the left (道の左側を走る); on escalators people stand on the left in Tokyo, but on the right in Osaka. For the bare direction, 左 suffices (左に曲がる). The counterpart is 右側, and the traffic rule is called 左側通行 \"left-hand traffic\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ゾンビを中に入れないために、シャッターを下ろして、内側からカギをかけました。","romaji":"Zonbi o naka ni irenai tame ni, shattā o oroshite, uchigawa kara kagi o kakemashita.","german":"To keep the zombies from getting in, I lowered the shutter and locked it from the inside.","furigana":"なか|い|お|うちがわ"},{"japanese":"ドアの内側に鍵があります。","romaji":"Doa no uchigawa ni kagi ga arimasu.","german":"The key is on the inside of the door.","furigana":"うちがわ|かぎ"},{"japanese":"黄色い線の内側までお下がりください。","romaji":"Kiiroi sen no uchigawa made osagari kudasai.","german":"Please stand back inside the yellow line.","furigana":"きいろ|せん|うちがわ|さ"}],"notes":"内側 is the inner side or inner area of something with a clear boundary: ドアの内側, 線の内側. For \"inside\" in the sense of \"in a room\", 中 is usually said (部屋の中). Well known is the platform announcement 黄色い線の内側までお下がりください \"Please step back behind the yellow line\". The opposite is 外側; 内側から鍵をかける means \"to lock from the inside\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"外側に開くと思ったら、内側に開くドアだったんだね。","romaji":"Sotogawa ni hiraku to omottara, uchigawa ni hiraku doa datta n da ne.","german":"I thought it would open outward, but it was a door that opens inward.","furigana":"そとがわ|ひら|おも|うちがわ|ひら"},{"japanese":"箱の外側に名前を書きました。","romaji":"Hako no sotogawa ni namae o kakimashita.","german":"I wrote my name on the outside of the box.","furigana":"はこ|そとがわ|なまえ|か"},{"japanese":"この窓は外側が汚れていて、よく見えません。","romaji":"Kono mado wa sotogawa ga yogorete ite, yoku miemasen.","german":"The outside of this window is dirty, so I can't see well.","furigana":"まど|そとがわ|よご|み"}],"notes":"外側 is the outer side or outer area of something: 箱の外側, 外側に開く. General \"outdoors, outside\" is 外, on the other hand: 外で遊ぶ, not 外側で遊ぶ. The opposite is 内側; the two are often used together, for example with doors, clothing or traffic lanes (外側の車線 \"outer lane\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"道の両側に木が植えてあります。","romaji":"Michi no ryougawa ni ki ga uete arimasu.","german":"Trees are planted on both sides of the road.","furigana":"みち|りょうがわ|き|う"},{"japanese":"ベッドの両側に小さいランプがあります。","romaji":"Beddo no ryougawa ni chiisai ranpu ga arimasu.","german":"There are small lamps on both sides of the bed.","furigana":"りょうがわ|ちい"},{"japanese":"このバスは両側にドアがあります。","romaji":"Kono basu wa ryougawa ni doa ga arimasu.","german":"This bus has doors on both sides.","furigana":"りょうがわ"}],"notes":"両側 means \"both sides\" of something, in a spatial sense: 道の両側, ベッドの両側. For \"both\" in the general sense, 両方 is used (両方とも好き). With body parts, 両 + noun such as 両手 or 両耳 is preferred. Typical is the structure 〜の両側に〜がある／並んでいる; similar is 左右 \"left and right\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"東側にはふじ山が見えます。","romaji":"Higashigawa ni wa Fujisan ga miemasu.","german":"You can see Mount Fuji on the east side.","furigana":"ひがしがわ|さん|み"},{"japanese":"東側の窓から朝日が見えます。","romaji":"Higashigawa no mado kara asahi ga miemasu.","german":"You can see the morning sun from the east-side window.","furigana":"ひがしがわ|まど|あさひ|み"},{"japanese":"駅の東側に新しいホテルができました。","romaji":"Eki no higashigawa ni atarashii hoteru ga dekimashita.","german":"A new hotel has opened on the east side of the station.","furigana":"えき|ひがしがわ|あたら"}],"notes":"東側 is the east side of something: 駅の東側, 東側の窓. For the pure compass direction, 東 suffices (東へ行く). Correspondingly there are 西側, 南側 and 北側. Historically, 東側 also meant the Eastern Bloc during the Cold War, as opposed to 西側 \"the West\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"もうあの国には、兵員はほとんど残っていません。","romaji":"Mō ano kuni ni wa, heiin wa hotondo nokotte imasen.","german":"There are hardly any troops left in that country anymore.","furigana":"くに|へいいん|のこ"},{"japanese":"兵員が集められました。","romaji":"Heiin ga atsumeraremashita.","german":"The troops were assembled.","furigana":"へいいん|あつ"},{"japanese":"兵員を船で島に運びました。","romaji":"Heiin o fune de shima ni hakobimashita.","german":"The troops were transported to the island by ship.","furigana":"へいいん|ふね|しま|はこ"}],"notes":"兵員 refers to soldiers as personnel or troop strength, i.e. as a number or group rather than an individual. It is a literary word from news and military reports (兵員を送る, 兵員が不足する). A single soldier is called 兵士; colloquial and somewhat old-fashioned is 兵隊.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これが俺の最終兵器だ！","romaji":"Kore ga ore no saishū heiki da!","german":"This is my ultimate weapon!","furigana":"おれ|さいしゅうへいき"},{"japanese":"核兵器は危険です。","romaji":"Kaku heiki wa kiken desu.","german":"Nuclear weapons are dangerous.","furigana":"かくへいき|きけん"},{"japanese":"その国は新しい兵器を作っています。","romaji":"Sono kuni wa atarashii heiki o tsukutte imasu.","german":"That country is developing new weapons.","furigana":"くに|あたら|へいき|つく"}],"notes":"兵器 denotes military weapons and weapon systems, especially in compounds such as 核兵器 \"nuclear weapons\" or 化学兵器 \"chemical weapons\". 武器 is more general and includes any weapon from a knife to a rifle; figuratively people say 笑顔が武器だ, not 兵器. Jokingly, 最終兵器 \"ultimate weapon\" is heard for a last trump card.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は元兵士です。","romaji":"Kare wa moto heishi desu.","german":"He is a former soldier.","furigana":"かれ|もとへいし"},{"japanese":"兵士たちが訓練しています。","romaji":"Heishitachi ga kunren shite imasu.","german":"The soldiers are training.","furigana":"へいし|くんれん"},{"japanese":"戦争で多くの兵士が亡くなりました。","romaji":"Sensou de ooku no heishi ga nakunarimashita.","german":"Many soldiers died in the war.","furigana":"せんそう|おお|へいし|な"}],"notes":"兵士 is the neutral word for an individual soldier, often in the plural 兵士たち. 兵隊 is colloquial and somewhat old-fashioned; 軍人 emphasizes the profession as a member of the military, including officers. Members of the Japan Self-Defense Forces are officially called 自衛官, not 兵士.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"米兵が基地にいます。","romaji":"Beihei ga kichi ni imasu.","german":"There are US soldiers at the base.","furigana":"べいへい|きち"},{"japanese":"米兵の事件がニュースになりました。","romaji":"Beihei no jiken ga nyuusu ni narimashita.","german":"An incident involving a US soldier made the news.","furigana":"べいへい|じけん"},{"japanese":"その米兵は日本語がとても上手です。","romaji":"Sono beihei wa nihongo ga totemo jouzu desu.","german":"That US soldier is very good at Japanese.","furigana":"べいへい|にほんご|じょうず"}],"notes":"米兵 means \"US soldier\"; 米 stands for the USA in compounds (米国, 日米). The short word is typical of news, especially in connection with the bases on Okinawa. In conversation people more often say アメリカ兵 or アメリカ軍の兵士. 英 for Great Britain (英国) is formed similarly.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"マンガが原作の映画は、原作の良さを失ってしまうことが多いです。","romaji":"Manga ga gensaku no eiga wa, gensaku no yosa o ushinatte shimau koto ga ōidesu.","german":"Movies based on manga often lose the appeal of the original.","furigana":"げんさく|えいが|げんさく|よ|うしな|おお"},{"japanese":"映画の原作を読みました。","romaji":"Eiga no gensaku o yomimashita.","german":"I read the original work of the movie.","furigana":"えいが|げんさく|よ"},{"japanese":"このアニメの原作は小説です。","romaji":"Kono anime no gensaku wa shousetsu desu.","german":"The original of this anime is a novel.","furigana":"げんさく|しょうせつ"}],"notes":"原作 is the original work on which an adaptation is based - typically a novel or manga that is made into a film or anime. Typical phrases are 〜が原作の映画 \"a film based on ...\", 原作を読む and 原作者 \"author of the original\". 原作通り means \"true to the original\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"インフレの主因って何だと思いますか？","romaji":"Infure no shuin tte nani da to omoimasu ka?","german":"What do you think is the main cause of inflation?","furigana":"しゅいん|なに|おも"},{"japanese":"事故の主因が判明しました。","romaji":"Jiko no shuin ga hanmei shimashita.","german":"The main cause of the accident has been determined.","furigana":"じこ|しゅいん|はんめい"},{"japanese":"医者によると、寝不足が頭痛の主因だそうです。","romaji":"Isha ni yoru to, nebusoku ga zutsuu no shuin da sou desu.","german":"According to the doctor, lack of sleep is the main cause of my headaches.","furigana":"いしゃ|ねぶそく|ずつう|しゅいん"}],"notes":"主因 means \"main cause\" and is a literary word from reports, news and analyses: 事故の主因, 〜が主因となる. In conversation one mostly says 一番の原因 or 主な原因. It almost always follows の after the event whose cause is named.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お昼ごはん、食堂で食べない？","romaji":"Ohiru gohan, shokudō de tabenai?","german":"Want to eat lunch at the cafeteria?","furigana":"ひる|しょくどう|た"},{"japanese":"学校の食堂で昼ご飯を食べます。","romaji":"Gakkō no shokudō de hirugohan o tabemasu.","german":"I eat lunch in the school cafeteria.","furigana":"がっこう|しょくどう|ひる|はん|た"}],"notes":"食堂 is a dining hall or cafeteria. With で you name it as the place of eating. 食べない？ in an invitation is a familiar question in the negative present, used to suggest eating together. 学校の食堂 assigns the cafeteria to a school. しょくどう has a long o at the end.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"海の水は塩水なので、そのままでは飲めません。","romaji":"Umi no mizu wa shiomizu na node, sono mama de wa nomemasen.","german":"Seawater is salt water, so you can't drink it as it is.","furigana":"うみ|みず|しおみず|の"},{"japanese":"パスタを塩水で茹でます。","romaji":"Pasuta o shiomizu de yudemasu.","german":"I boil the pasta in salt water.","furigana":"しおみず|ゆ"},{"japanese":"切ったりんごを塩水につけると、色が変わりません。","romaji":"Kitta ringo o shiomizu ni tsukeru to, iro ga kawarimasen.","german":"If you soak cut apples in salt water, they won't change color.","furigana":"き|しおみず|いろ|か"}],"notes":"塩水（しおみず） is the everyday word for salt water, for example in the kitchen: 塩水でゆでる, 塩水につける. In technical texts the same word is also read えんすい. Seawater is more precisely 海水, fresh water 真水. A well-known kitchen trick: cut apples are briefly put in 塩水 so that they do not turn brown.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"塩味が少し足りない気がします。","romaji":"Shioaji ga sukoshi tarinai ki ga shimasu.","german":"I feel it's lacking a little saltiness.","furigana":"しおあじ|すこ|た|き"},{"japanese":"このポテトチップスは塩味です。","romaji":"Kono potetochippusu wa shioaji desu.","german":"These potato chips are salt-flavored.","furigana":"しおあじ"},{"japanese":"ラーメンは塩味としょうゆ味、どちらにしますか。","romaji":"Raamen wa shioaji to shouyu aji, dochira ni shimasu ka.","german":"For ramen, which would you like, salt or soy sauce?","furigana":"しおあじ|あじ"}],"notes":"塩味（しおあじ） means \"salty taste\" and is often used as a flavor variety: 塩味のポテトチップス, 塩味のラーメン. Similar formations are しょうゆ味 and みそ味. To say that something is too salty, the adjectives しょっぱい or 塩辛い are used. In technical texts the word is also read えんみ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ゼッタイに官金に手を出すんじゃないぞ！","romaji":"Zettai ni kankin ni te o dasu n ja nai zo!","german":"Never lay a hand on government funds!","furigana":"かんきん|て|だ"},{"japanese":"官金の管理は厳しいです。","romaji":"Kankin no kanri wa kibishii desu.","german":"The management of government funds is strict.","furigana":"かんきん|かんり|きび"},{"japanese":"その役人は官金を自分のために使いました。","romaji":"Sono yakunin wa kankin o jibun no tame ni tsukaimashita.","german":"That official used government funds for himself.","furigana":"やくにん|かんきん|じぶん|つか"}],"notes":"官金 means \"government funds, public money\" and is an outdated, literary word; 官 stands for government and authorities (官庁, 官僚). Today one mostly says 公金 or speaks concretely of 税金. The phrase 官金に手を出す means \"to misappropriate government funds\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は元外交官です。","romaji":"Kare wa moto gaikōkan desu.","german":"He is a former diplomat.","furigana":"かれ|もとがいこうかん"},{"japanese":"彼女は外交官として働いています。","romaji":"Kanojo wa gaikōkan to shite hataraite imasu.","german":"She works as a diplomat.","furigana":"かのじょ|がいこうかん|はたら"},{"japanese":"外交官になるために、外国語を三つ勉強しています。","romaji":"Gaikoukan ni naru tame ni, gaikokugo o mittsu benkyou shite imasu.","german":"To become a diplomat, I am studying three foreign languages.","furigana":"がいこうかん|がいこくご|みっ|べんきょう"}],"notes":"外交官 is composed of 外交 \"diplomacy\" and 官 \"official\" and refers to diplomats in the foreign service. An ambassador is more precisely 大使. 官 forms many civil-service occupations, such as 警察官 \"police officer\" or 裁判官 \"judge\". Typical are 外交官になる and 外交官として働く.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人の気持ちを察する前に、自分の気持ちを大切にするべきです。","romaji":"Hito no kimochi o sassuru mae ni, jibun no kimochi o taisetsu ni surubekidesu.","german":"Before sensing other people's feelings, you should value your own feelings.","furigana":"ひと|きも|さっ|まえ|じぶん|きも|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"彼女の気持ちを察しました。","romaji":"Kanojo no kimochi o sasshimashita.","german":"I sensed her feelings.","furigana":"かのじょ|きも|さっ"},{"japanese":"大変でしたね。お気持ち、お察しします。","romaji":"Taihen deshita ne. Okimochi, osasshi shimasu.","german":"That must have been hard. You have my sympathy.","furigana":"たいへん|きも|さっ"}],"notes":"察する means \"to sense, to intuit\" - above all the feelings or intentions of others without their being spoken: 気持ちを察する. It is transitive and sounds more refined than 気づく or わかる. The polite phrase お察しします expresses sympathy (\"I can imagine how you feel\"). The noun 察し appears in 察しがいい \"to catch on quickly, to be perceptive\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は危険察知能力が高い。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kiken satchi nōryoku ga takai.","german":"She has a high ability to sense danger.","furigana":"かのじょ|きけんさっちのうりょく|たか"},{"japanese":"危険を察知しました。","romaji":"Kiken o satchi shimashita.","german":"I sensed danger.","furigana":"きけん|さっち"},{"japanese":"犬は地震を前もって察知できると言われています。","romaji":"Inu wa jishin o maemotte satchi dekiru to iwarete imasu.","german":"Dogs are said to be able to sense earthquakes in advance.","furigana":"いぬ|じしん|まえ|さっち|い"}],"notes":"察知 means to sense in time something hidden or imminent, especially danger, intentions or changes: 危険を察知する. It is a transitive する-verb with a matter-of-fact, written sound. 察する refers more to the feelings of others, and 気づく is the everyday \"to notice\". The combination 危険察知能力 \"sense for danger\" is common.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このフライトに空席はありません。","romaji":"Kono furaito ni kūseki wa arimasen.","german":"There are no vacant seats on this flight.","furigana":"くうせき"},{"japanese":"空席はありますか。","romaji":"Kūseki wa arimasu ka.","german":"Do you have any seats available?","furigana":"くうせき"},{"japanese":"部長の席は、まだ空席のままです。","romaji":"Buchou no seki wa, mada kuuseki no mama desu.","german":"The manager's seat is still vacant.","furigana":"ぶちょう|せき|くうせき"}],"notes":"空席 refers to a free, unoccupied seat, above all with reservations on trains, planes, cinemas or restaurants: 空席がある, 空席はありますか. On displays you read 空席あり; the opposite is 満席 \"fully booked\". In daily life people also simply say 空いている席. 空席 can also refer to a vacant post.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は常に笑顔です。","romaji":"Kare wa tsuneni egao desu.","german":"He is always smiling.","furigana":"かれ|つね|えがお"},{"japanese":"運転中は常に安全に注意してください。","romaji":"Unten-chū wa tsuneni anzen ni chūi shite kudasai.","german":"Please always pay attention to safety while driving.","furigana":"うんてんちゅう|つね|あんぜん|ちゅうい"}],"notes":"常に means always or constantly and emphasizes the constancy of a state or behavior. It stands before the predicate it describes and is especially common in explanations or general instructions. 運転中は常に states a requirement for the whole drive; 常に笑顔 describes a permanently friendly appearance.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は日本語で日常会話が出来るようになりたいです。","romaji":"Watashi wa nihongo de nichijō kaiwa ga dekiru yō ni naritaidesu.","german":"I want to be able to have everyday conversations in Japanese.","furigana":"わたし|にほんご|にちじょうかいわ|でき"},{"japanese":"日常生活で日本語を使います。","romaji":"Nichijō seikatsu de nihongo o tsukaimasu.","german":"I use Japanese in my daily life.","furigana":"にちじょうせいかつ|にほんご|つか"},{"japanese":"旅行が終わって、また日常に戻りました。","romaji":"Ryokou ga owatte, mata nichijou ni modorimashita.","german":"The trip is over, and I'm back to my daily routine.","furigana":"りょこう|お|にちじょう|もど"}],"notes":"日常 means \"everyday life, the ordinary\" and mostly appears in compounds or with の: 日常生活, 日常会話, 日常の仕事. Adverbially and adjectivally, 日常的 is often used (日常的に使う \"to use regularly, in daily life\"). 毎日, by contrast, specifically means \"every day\", and 普段 \"usually, normally\". 日常に戻る means \"to return to everyday life\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この神社の池には、どんな干天でもゼッタイに水があったんだって。","romaji":"Kono jinja no ike ni wa, donna kanten de mo zettai ni mizu ga atta n da tte.","german":"They say the pond of this shrine always had water, no matter how dry the weather got.","furigana":"じんじゃ|いけ|かんてん|みず"},{"japanese":"干天が続いて水不足です。","romaji":"Kanten ga tsuzuite mizubusoku desu.","german":"The dry spell continues and there is a water shortage.","furigana":"かんてん|つづ|みずぶそく"},{"japanese":"その知らせは、まさに干天の慈雨でした。","romaji":"Sono shirase wa, masani kanten no jiu deshita.","german":"That news was truly a welcome rain after a long drought.","furigana":"し|かんてん|じう"}],"notes":"干天 refers to a period of dry weather without rain and is a literary, written word; it is also written 旱天. In everyday speech one says 日照り or 雨が降らない日が続く, and for drought in the news 干ばつ. Today 干天 is known mainly from the saying 干天の慈雨 \"longed-for rain after a drought\", used figuratively for welcome help in need.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"それ、ただの幻覚じゃない？","romaji":"Sore, tada no genkaku ja nai?","german":"Isn't that just a hallucination?","furigana":"げんかく"},{"japanese":"熱で幻覚を見ました。","romaji":"Netsu de genkaku o mimashita.","german":"I had hallucinations from the fever.","furigana":"ねつ|げんかく|み"},{"japanese":"この薬を飲むと、幻覚が起こることがあります。","romaji":"Kono kusuri o nomu to, genkaku ga okoru koto ga arimasu.","german":"Taking this medicine can sometimes cause hallucinations.","furigana":"くすり|の|げんかく|お"}],"notes":"幻覚 is a hallucination, i.e. a perception without a real stimulus. Typical are 幻覚を見る and 幻覚が見える; the cause takes で (熱で幻覚を見る). Auditory hallucinations are more precisely 幻聴. 錯覚, by contrast, is a misperception of something real, and 幻 more poetically means \"illusion, phantom\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"クツの底にガムがついてるよ。","romaji":"Kutsu no soko ni gamu ga tsuiteru yo.","german":"You have chewing gum stuck to the sole of your shoe.","furigana":"そこ"},{"japanese":"海の底に宝物があるかもしれません。","romaji":"Umi no soko ni takaramono ga aru kamo shiremasen.","german":"There might be treasure at the bottom of the sea.","furigana":"うみ|そこ|たからもの"},{"japanese":"心の底から感謝しています。","romaji":"Kokoro no soko kara kansha shite imasu.","german":"I am grateful from the bottom of my heart.","furigana":"こころ|そこ|かんしゃ"}],"notes":"底 is the bottom or lowest surface of a container or body of water (鍋の底, 海の底) and also the sole of a shoe (靴の底). 下, by contrast, means \"under\": 箱の下 is under the box, 箱の底 is its bottom. The floor of a room is called 床. Figuratively: 心の底から \"from the bottom of one's heart\", 底をつく \"to run out\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"心底嬉しかったです。","romaji":"Shinsoko ureshikatta desu.","german":"I was truly happy.","furigana":"しんそこうれ"},{"japanese":"試験に合格したと聞いて、心底ほっとしました。","romaji":"Shiken ni goukaku shita to kiite, shinsoko hotto shimashita.","german":"Hearing that I passed the exam, I felt deeply relieved.","furigana":"しけん|ごうかく|き|しんそこ"},{"japanese":"ああいう人は心底嫌いです。","romaji":"Aa iu hito wa shinsoko kirai desu.","german":"I truly hate people like that.","furigana":"ひと|しんそこきら"}],"notes":"心底 is an adverb that intensifies feelings: 心底うれしい \"happy from the heart\", 心底ほっとする \"to be deeply relieved\", 心底驚く. It sounds more emotional and emphatic than the neutral 心から (心から感謝します) and is often used with negative feelings too: 心底嫌いだ, 心底うんざりする. As a noun it means \"the deepest part of the heart, one's true intention\" (心底から); in this meaning there is also the literary reading しんてい.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"川底には水草がたくさん生えていました。","romaji":"Kawazoko ni wa mizukusa ga takusan haete imashita.","german":"Many water plants were growing on the riverbed.","furigana":"かわぞこ|みずくさ|は"},{"japanese":"水がきれいなので、川底の石まではっきり見えます。","romaji":"Mizu ga kirei na node, kawazoko no ishi made hakkiri miemasu.","german":"The water is so clear that you can even see the stones on the riverbed clearly.","furigana":"みず|かわぞこ|いし|み"},{"japanese":"落としたかぎが川底に沈んでしまいました。","romaji":"Otoshita kagi ga kawazoko ni shizunde shimaimashita.","german":"The key I dropped sank to the bottom of the river.","furigana":"お|かわぞこ|しず"}],"notes":"川底 is the bottom of a river, i.e. what lies beneath the water (底 \"bottom\", read here with voicing as ぞこ). Typical are 川底に沈む \"to sink to the bottom of the river\", 川底が見える and 川底の石. The bank, on the other hand, is 川岸 or 川辺; the sea floor is 海底 (かいてい) with the Sino-Japanese reading. In technical texts (geography, hydraulic engineering), 河床 is also used for \"riverbed\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は愛国心が強い人です。","romaji":"Kare wa aikokushin ga tsuyoi hito desu.","german":"He is a very patriotic person.","furigana":"かれ|あいこくしん|つよ|ひと"},{"japanese":"オリンピックを見ると、愛国心がわいてきます。","romaji":"Orinpikku o miru to, aikokushin ga waite kimasu.","german":"Watching the Olympics makes me feel patriotic.","furigana":"み|あいこくしん"},{"japanese":"学校で愛国心を教えるべきかどうか、意見が分かれています。","romaji":"Gakkou de aikokushin o oshieru beki ka dou ka, iken ga wakarete imasu.","german":"Opinions are divided on whether patriotism should be taught at school.","furigana":"がっこう|あいこくしん|おし|いけん|わ"}],"notes":"愛国心 is love for one's own country, \"patriotism\" as a feeling or attitude. Typical combinations are 愛国心が強い, 愛国心を持つ and 愛国心がわく; the person is called 愛国者. In Japan the word is politically sensitive because of the history of the war, for example in the debate about patriotism education at schools. For exaggerated nationalism, ナショナリズム or 国家主義 is more usual.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"寒くなりましたので、くれぐれもご自愛ください。","romaji":"Samuku narimashita node, kuregure mo gojiai kudasai.","german":"It has gotten cold, so please take good care of yourself.","furigana":"さむ|じあい"},{"japanese":"お忙しい日が続くと思いますが、どうぞご自愛ください。","romaji":"Oisogashii hi ga tsuzuku to omoimasu ga, douzo gojiai kudasai.","german":"I imagine you will continue to have busy days, but please take care of yourself.","furigana":"いそが|ひ|つづ|おも|じあい"},{"japanese":"先生へのメールの最後に「ご自愛ください」と書きました。","romaji":"Sensei e no meeru no saigo ni 'gojiai kudasai' to kakimashita.","german":"At the end of my email to my teacher, I wrote 「ご自愛ください」.","furigana":"せんせい|さいご|じあい|か"}],"notes":"自愛 is found almost only in the polite closing formula ご自愛ください \"please take care of your health\" in letters, emails and greeting cards to superiors, customers or older people, often reinforced with どうぞ or くれぐれも. Since 自愛 already means \"to take care of oneself\", お体をご自愛ください is considered redundant, but it is widespread. One does not say it about oneself; among friends one rather says 体に気をつけてね, and to someone already ill one says お大事に. The meaning \"self-love\" is rare.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"文学には性愛のテーマが多いです。","romaji":"Bungaku ni wa seiai no teema ga ooi desu.","german":"There are many themes of sexual love in literature.","furigana":"ぶんがく|せいあい|おお"},{"japanese":"古代ギリシャの人々は、友情と性愛を分けて考えていました。","romaji":"Kodai Girisha no hitobito wa, yuujou to seiai o wakete kangaete imashita.","german":"The ancient Greeks thought of friendship and sexual love as separate.","furigana":"こだい|ひとびと|ゆうじょう|せいあい|わ|かんが"},{"japanese":"性愛の場面が多いので、この映画は子どもには向きません。","romaji":"Seiai no bamen ga ooi node, kono eiga wa kodomo ni wa mukimasen.","german":"This movie has many love scenes, so it is not suitable for children.","furigana":"せいあい|ばめん|おお|えいが|こ|む"}],"notes":"性愛 is sexual, physical love, \"eros\", and belongs to written and technical language (literature, philosophy, psychology). Everyday romantic love is 恋愛, affection in general 愛情. 性愛 is also known as a component of 異性愛 \"heterosexuality\" and 同性愛 \"homosexuality\". Typical are 性愛を描く, 性愛のテーマ and 性愛の描写.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"愛知県に行ったことがありますか？","romaji":"Aichiken ni itta koto ga arimasu ka?","german":"Have you ever been to Aichi Prefecture?","furigana":"あいちけん|い"},{"japanese":"愛知県は自動車産業で有名です。","romaji":"Aichiken wa jidōsha sangyō de yūmei desu.","german":"Aichi Prefecture is famous for its automobile industry.","furigana":"あいちけん|じどうしゃさんぎょう|ゆうめい"},{"japanese":"名古屋は愛知県にあります。","romaji":"Nagoya wa Aichiken ni arimasu.","german":"Nagoya is in Aichi Prefecture.","furigana":"なごや|あいちけん"}],"notes":"愛知県 is a prefecture in the Chūbu region with the capital 名古屋. It is the center of the Japanese car industry; Toyota has its headquarters here. In conversation people often say just 愛知 (愛知の出身です); in addresses the full form comes before the place name: 愛知県名古屋市…. The local dialect is called 名古屋弁.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"決勝で敗れました。","romaji":"Kesshou de yaburemashita.","german":"We were defeated in the final.","furigana":"けっしょう|やぶ"},{"japanese":"日本は一対二でブラジルに敗れました。","romaji":"Nihon wa ittai ni de Burajiru ni yaburemashita.","german":"Japan lost to Brazil 2-1.","furigana":"にほん|いったいに|やぶ"},{"japanese":"選挙に敗れた彼は、政治家をやめました。","romaji":"Senkyo ni yabureta kare wa, seijika o yamemashita.","german":"After losing the election, he quit politics.","furigana":"せんきょ|やぶ|かれ|せいじか"}],"notes":"敗れる \"to be defeated, to lose\" is intransitive; the opponent takes に (ブラジルに敗れる), the contest takes で or に (決勝で敗れる, 選挙に敗れる). It is more formal than the everyday 負ける and typical of sports reports and news. The transitive counterpart is 破る \"to defeat\" (相手を破る). Do not confuse it with the identically read 破れる \"to tear\" (紙が破れる).","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"勝敗は最後までわかりません。","romaji":"Shouhai wa saigo made wakarimasen.","german":"You can't know the outcome until the end.","furigana":"しょうはい|さいご"},{"japanese":"最後のゴールで勝敗が決まりました。","romaji":"Saigo no gooru de shouhai ga kimarimashita.","german":"The final goal decided the outcome.","furigana":"さいご|しょうはい|き"},{"japanese":"子どものサッカーでは、勝敗よりも楽しむことが大切です。","romaji":"Kodomo no sakkaa de wa, shouhai yori mo tanoshimu koto ga taisetsu desu.","german":"In children's soccer, having fun is more important than winning or losing.","furigana":"こ|しょうはい|たの|たいせつ"}],"notes":"勝敗 denotes the outcome of a contest, literally \"victory and defeat\". Typical are 勝敗が決まる/つく \"to be decided\", 勝敗を決める and 勝敗を分ける \"to be decisive\". Colloquially people say 勝ち負け with the same meaning. Do not confuse it with 勝負, the contest or duel itself (勝負する \"to compete against each other\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は敗者にはなりたくありません。","romaji":"Watashi wa haisha ni wa naritaku arimasen.","german":"I don't want to be a loser.","furigana":"わたし|はいしゃ"},{"japanese":"敗者にも拍手を送りましょう。","romaji":"Haisha ni mo hakushu o okurimashō.","german":"Let's give applause to the losers too.","furigana":"はいしゃ|はくしゅ|おく"},{"japanese":"試合の後、勝者と敗者が握手をしました。","romaji":"Shiai no ato, shousha to haisha ga akushu o shimashita.","german":"After the match, the winner and the loser shook hands.","furigana":"しあい|あと|しょうしゃ|はいしゃ|あくしゅ"}],"notes":"敗者 \"loser\" is a rather formal word from sports, politics and news; the counterpart is 勝者 \"winner\". In daily life people mostly say 負けた人 or 負けたほう. Well known is 敗者復活戦 \"repechage round\" in tournaments. Note, homophones: 歯医者 \"dentist\" and 廃車 \"scrapped car\" are also read はいしゃ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"梅酒みたいでおいしい！","romaji":"Umeshu mitai de oishii!","german":"Es schmeckt lecker, wie Pflaumenwein!","furigana":"うめしゅ"},{"japanese":"自家製の梅酒を作りました。","romaji":"Jikasei no umeshu o tsukurimashita.","german":"Ich habe selbstgemachten Pflaumenwein hergestellt.","furigana":"じかせい|うめしゅ|つく"},{"japanese":"梅酒をソーダで割って飲むのが好きです。","romaji":"Umeshu o sooda de watte nomu no ga suki desu.","german":"Ich trinke Pflaumenwein gern mit Soda gemischt.","furigana":"うめしゅ|わ|の|す"}],"notes":"梅酒 entsteht, indem man unreife Ume-Früchte (japanische Aprikose) mit Kandiszucker in Shōchū oder anderem Schnaps einlegt; streng genommen ist es also ein Likör, im Deutschen heißt es aber meist „Pflaumenwein“. Viele Familien setzen ihn im Frühsommer selbst an: 梅酒を漬ける/作る. Getrunken wird er pur, auf Eis (梅酒のロック), mit Soda (梅酒ソーダ) oder mit heißem Wasser (お湯割り). 酒 wird hier しゅ gelesen, wie in 日本酒.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"親は無力ですよ。子が大きくなると、してあげられることなんて、ほとんどありません。","romaji":"Oya wa muryoku desu yo. Ko ga ookiku naru to, shite agerareru koto nante, hotondo arimasen.","german":"Parents are powerless. Once the kids grow up, there's hardly anything you can do for them.","furigana":"おや|むりょく|こ|おお"},{"japanese":"大きな地震の前では、人間は無力だと感じました。","romaji":"Ookina jishin no mae de wa, ningen wa muryoku da to kanjimashita.","german":"Faced with a big earthquake, I felt that humans are powerless.","furigana":"おお|じしん|まえ|にんげん|むりょく|かん"},{"japanese":"友達を助けられなくて、無力な自分が悔しかったです。","romaji":"Tomodachi o tasukerarenakute, muryoku na jibun ga kuyashikatta desu.","german":"I was frustrated at being powerless, unable to help my friend.","furigana":"ともだち|たす|むりょく|じぶん|くや"}],"notes":"無力 \"powerless, helpless\" is a noun and な-adjective: 無力な自分, 〜の前では無力だ. It describes a lack of power or ability to change something, not physical weakness (for that, 弱い). The feeling of helplessness is called 無力感, often in 無力感を覚える. The opposite 有力 means rather \"influential, promising\" (有力な候補).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コウイチは無休で働いている。","romaji":"Kouichi wa mukyuu de hataraite iru.","german":"Koichi works without a day off.","furigana":"むきゅう|はたら"},{"japanese":"この店は年中無休です。","romaji":"Kono mise wa nenjū mukyū desu.","german":"This store is open every day of the year.","furigana":"みせ|ねんじゅうむきゅう"},{"japanese":"お正月も無休で営業します。","romaji":"Oshougatsu mo mukyuu de eigyou shimasu.","german":"We will be open on New Year's too.","furigana":"しょうがつ|むきゅう|えいぎょう"}],"notes":"無休 means \"without a closing day\" and appears above all on shop signs and in business information: 年中無休 \"open all year round\", 土日無休 \"open on weekends too\", 無休で営業する. The opposite is 定休日 \"regular closing day\". For people, 無休で働く \"to work without a day off\" is said. Careful when listening: the identically read 無給 means \"unpaid\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"無名の画家の絵を買いました。","romaji":"Mumei no gaka no e o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a painting by an unknown painter.","furigana":"むめい|がか|え|か"},{"japanese":"無名の新人が大会で優勝しました。","romaji":"Mumei no shinjin ga taikai de yuushou shimashita.","german":"An unknown newcomer won the tournament.","furigana":"むめい|しんじん|たいかい|ゆうしょう"},{"japanese":"彼も昔は無名だったが、今では誰でも知っている俳優だ。","romaji":"Kare mo mukashi wa mumei datta ga, ima de wa dare demo shitte iru haiyuu da.","german":"He too was unknown once, but now he is an actor everyone knows.","furigana":"かれ|むかし|むめい|いま|だれ|し|はいゆう"}],"notes":"無名 \"unknown, not famous\" mostly stands attributively with の (無名の歌手, 無名の新人) or predicatively as 無名だ; the opposite is 有名. The original meaning \"nameless\" lives on in set expressions such as 無名戦士の墓 \"Tomb of the Unknown Soldier\". For an anonymous letter or sender, 匿名 is used instead, and a stranger is not called 無名 but 知らない人.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"皆さんが無事で本当に良かったです。","romaji":"Minasan ga buji de hontou ni yokatta desu.","german":"I'm really glad everyone is safe.","furigana":"みな|ぶじ|ほんとう|よ"},{"japanese":"無事に帰って来てください。","romaji":"Buji ni kaette kite kudasai.","german":"Please come back safely.","furigana":"ぶじ|かえ|き"},{"japanese":"荷物は無事に届きましたか。","romaji":"Nimotsu wa buji ni todokimashita ka.","german":"Did the package arrive safely?","furigana":"にもつ|ぶじ|とど"}],"notes":"無事 means \"safe and sound, without incident\". As a state it appears as 無事だ or 無事で (家族が無事でよかった), as an adverb 無事に \"safely, happily, smoothly\" with verbs such as 着く, 帰る, 終わる, 届く. Unlike 安全 (\"safe\" as a property, 安全な場所), 無事 emphasizes the outcome: nothing happened. Set phrases are ご無事で \"Arrive safely\" and 無事を祈る.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は経済について無知なので、ニュースがよく分かりません。","romaji":"Watashi wa keizai ni tsuite muchi na node, nyuusu ga yoku wakarimasen.","german":"I'm ignorant about economics, so I often don't understand the news.","furigana":"わたし|けいざい|むち|わ"},{"japanese":"無知は恥ずかしいことではありません。","romaji":"Muchi wa hazukashii koto de wa arimasen.","german":"Ignorance is nothing to be ashamed of.","furigana":"むち|は"},{"japanese":"無知な人をばかにしてはいけません。","romaji":"Muchi na hito o baka ni shite wa ikemasen.","german":"You must not make fun of ignorant people.","furigana":"むち|ひと"}],"notes":"無知 \"ignorance, ignorant\" is a noun and a な-adjective: 無知な人, 〜について無知だ, 自分の無知を恥じる. The word is judgmental and can come across as insulting; to say politely that you are not familiar with something, 〜に詳しくない or よく知らない is preferable. Do not confuse it with the identically read むち \"whip\" (鞭).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"無茶なこと言わないでよ。","romaji":"Mucha na koto iwanaide yo.","german":"Don't say unreasonable things.","furigana":"むちゃ|い"},{"japanese":"熱があるのに走るなんて無茶だよ。","romaji":"Netsu ga aru noni hashiru nante mucha da yo.","german":"Running when you have a fever is reckless.","furigana":"ねつ|はし|むちゃ"},{"japanese":"若いころは無茶をして、よく親に怒られました。","romaji":"Wakai koro wa mucha o shite, yoku oya ni okoraremashita.","german":"When I was young I did reckless things and was often scolded by my parents.","furigana":"わか|むちゃ|おや|おこ"}],"notes":"無茶 describes something unreasonable, reckless or excessive: 無茶を言う \"to demand the impossible\", 無茶をする \"to act recklessly\", 無茶な計画. It is colloquial and often sounds reproachful or worried (無茶しないでね). In contrast to 無理 (\"impossible; to overexert oneself\", 無理をする), 無茶 emphasizes recklessness. Intensified, it becomes 無茶苦茶 or めちゃくちゃ \"completely chaotic, extreme\"; the kanji are chosen only for their sound.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私はこの大学の薬学部に入りたいんです。","romaji":"Watashi wa kono daigaku no yakugakubu ni hairitai n desu.","german":"I want to get into the pharmacy faculty of this university.","furigana":"わたし|だいがく|やくがくぶ|はい"},{"japanese":"大学で薬学を学んでいます。","romaji":"Daigaku de yakugaku o manande imasu.","german":"I am studying pharmacy at university.","furigana":"だいがく|やくがく|まな"},{"japanese":"姉は会社で薬学の研究をしています。","romaji":"Ane wa kaisha de yakugaku no kenkyuu o shite imasu.","german":"My older sister does pharmaceutical research at her company.","furigana":"あね|かいしゃ|やくがく|けんきゅう"}],"notes":"薬学 is pharmacy as a science and field of study: 薬学を学ぶ/専攻する, 薬学部 \"faculty of pharmacy\", 薬学の研究. The profession is called 薬剤師 \"pharmacist\", the pharmacy itself 薬局. Alongside 医学 \"medicine\", it is a subject of its own. The word is a noun; \"pharmaceutical\" is expressed with 薬学の or 薬学的.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今すぐ薬物をやめろ！","romaji":"Ima sugu yakubutsu o yamero!","german":"Stop the drugs right now!","furigana":"いま|やくぶつ"},{"japanese":"薬物の使用は禁止されています。","romaji":"Yakubutsu no shiyō wa kinshi sarete imasu.","german":"The use of drugs is prohibited.","furigana":"やくぶつ|しよう|きんし"},{"japanese":"薬物アレルギーがある人は、医者に言ってください。","romaji":"Yakubutsu arerugii ga aru hito wa, isha ni itte kudasai.","german":"If you have a drug allergy, please tell the doctor.","furigana":"やくぶつ|ひと|いしゃ|い"}],"notes":"薬物 is the formal, technical word for a substance with a medicinal effect. In the news it usually means illegal drugs: 薬物乱用, 薬物依存, 薬物事件, 薬物をやめる. In medicine it is neutral for pharmaceutical substances: 薬物アレルギー, 薬物療法. In daily life people simply say 薬 for medicine; colloquially ドラッグ for drugs, and 麻薬 for narcotics in the narrower sense.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"セールになってたから薬用ローションを買ってみました。","romaji":"Seeru ni natteta kara yakuyou roushon o katte mimashita.","german":"It was on sale, so I tried buying a medicated lotion.","furigana":"やくよう|か"},{"japanese":"薬用のハンドクリームを買いました。","romaji":"Yakuyō no handokurīmu o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a medicated hand cream.","furigana":"やくよう|か"},{"japanese":"このせっけんは薬用なので、にきびに効くそうです。","romaji":"Kono sekken wa yakuyou na node, nikibi ni kiku sou desu.","german":"This soap is medicated, so it's said to work on acne.","furigana":"やくよう|き"}],"notes":"薬用 \"for medicinal purposes\" usually stands directly before product names: 薬用石けん, 薬用リップクリーム, 薬用ハンドクリーム, more rarely with の. In Japan it marks care products with an active ingredient (legally 医薬部外品, \"quasi-drugs\"). Other combinations are 薬用植物 \"medicinal plant\" and 薬用酒 \"medicinal liquor\". \"Medical\" in the sense of \"doctor's, relating to medicine\" is 医学的 or 医療の instead.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"寒くて指の感覚がなくなりました。","romaji":"Samukute yubi no kankaku ga nakunarimashita.","german":"It was so cold that I lost all feeling in my fingers.","furigana":"さむ|ゆび|かんかく"},{"japanese":"彼女は色の感覚がとてもいいです。","romaji":"Kanojo wa iro no kankaku ga totemo ii desu.","german":"She has a very good sense of color.","furigana":"かのじょ|いろ|かんかく"},{"japanese":"日本に長く住んで、時間の感覚が変わりました。","romaji":"Nihon ni nagaku sunde, jikan no kankaku ga kawarimashita.","german":"After living in Japan for a long time, my sense of time has changed.","furigana":"にほん|なが|す|じかん|かんかく|か"}],"notes":"感覚 is sensory perception and, figuratively, a feel for something: 指の感覚がない \"to have no feeling in one's fingers\", 色の感覚がいい \"to have a good sense of color\", 時間の感覚 \"sense of time\", 金銭感覚 \"attitude toward money\". Popular is 〜感覚で \"as if it were ...\" (ゲーム感覚で). For a vague impression one rather says 感じ, for emotions 気持ち or 感情. Despite the dictionary entry, 感覚する is practically never used.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"図書館で『図説 日本の城』という本を借りました。","romaji":"Toshokan de 'Zusetsu Nihon no shiro' to iu hon o karimashita.","german":"I borrowed a book called 『図説 日本の城』 from the library.","furigana":"としょかん|ずせつ|にほん|しろ|ほん|か"},{"japanese":"この本は、体のしくみを分かりやすく図説しています。","romaji":"Kono hon wa, karada no shikumi o wakariyasuku zusetsu shite imasu.","german":"This book explains the structure of the body in an easy-to-understand way with illustrations.","furigana":"ほん|からだ|わ|ずせつ"},{"japanese":"歴史の授業で、図説の資料集を使っています。","romaji":"Rekishi no jugyou de, zusetsu no shiryoushuu o tsukatte imasu.","german":"In history class, we use an illustrated reference book.","furigana":"れきし|じゅぎょう|ずせつ|しりょうしゅう|つか"}],"notes":"図説 is an explanation with illustrations, plates and diagrams. It is found mainly in titles of richly illustrated nonfiction books and reference volumes (『図説 日本の歴史』). As a する-verb (〜を図説する) it is literary. More common in everyday speech is 図解 (図解付き \"with explanatory graphics\"); a single picture or drawing is called イラスト or 挿絵.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この非常ブレーキ、こわれてるかもしれないよ。","romaji":"Kono hijou bureeki, kowareteru kamo shirenai yo.","german":"This emergency brake might be broken.","furigana":"ひじょう"},{"japanese":"非常に大切な会議です。","romaji":"Hijō ni taisetsu na kaigi desu.","german":"It's an extremely important meeting.","furigana":"ひじょう|たいせつ|かいぎ"},{"japanese":"非常口はあちらです。","romaji":"Hijouguchi wa achira desu.","german":"The emergency exit is over there.","furigana":"ひじょうぐち"}],"notes":"非常 has two faces. As a noun it means \"emergency\" and appears mainly in compounds: 非常口 \"emergency exit\", 非常ベル, 非常ブレーキ, 非常食, 非常時. As a な-adjective it means \"extraordinary, enormous\" and appears most often as the adverb 非常に \"extremely, very\", which sounds more formal than とても. For an acute emergency, everyday speech rather uses 緊急 (緊急の場合).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鼻くそ、指でほじるとかありえないんだけど。ティッシュ使って取りなよ。","romaji":"Hanakuso, yubi de hojiru toka arienai n da kedo. Tisshu tsukatte tori na yo.","german":"Picking your nose with your fingers is not okay. Use a tissue to get it out.","furigana":"はな|ゆび|つか|と"},{"japanese":"鼻くそをほじらないで。","romaji":"Hanakuso o hojiranaide.","german":"Don't pick your nose.","furigana":"はな"},{"japanese":"子どもの鼻くそをティッシュで取りました。","romaji":"Kodomo no hanakuso o tisshu de torimashita.","german":"I wiped my child's booger away with a tissue.","furigana":"こ|はな|と"}],"notes":"鼻くそ is a colloquial, rather crude word for a booger or nasal mucus. 鼻くそをほじる describes picking one's nose. In the first example, the reproach is followed by a casual request with 取りなよ; the expression is suited to familiar conversation, not to a formal description.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鼻先にマスタードがついてるよ。","romaji":"Hanasaki ni masutādo ga tsuiteru yo.","german":"You have mustard on the tip of your nose.","furigana":"はなさき"},{"japanese":"鼻先が冷たいです。","romaji":"Hanasaki ga tsumetai desu.","german":"The tip of my nose is cold.","furigana":"はなさき|つめ"},{"japanese":"店に着いたら、鼻先でドアを閉められてしまいました。","romaji":"Mise ni tsuitara, hanasaki de doa o shimerarete shimaimashita.","german":"When I got to the shop, the door was shut in my face.","furigana":"みせ|つ|はなさき|し"}],"notes":"鼻先 is the tip of the nose (in everyday speech also 鼻の先 or 鼻の頭). Figuratively it means \"right under one's nose\": 鼻先でドアを閉められる \"to have the door shut in one's face\". The phrase 鼻先で笑う means \"to laugh contemptuously, to laugh at someone scornfully\". For \"before one's eyes\" in general, 目の前 is more common.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"料理をしながら鼻歌を歌っています。","romaji":"Ryouri o shinagara hanauta o utatte imasu.","german":"I hum while cooking.","furigana":"りょうり|はなうた|うた"},{"japanese":"父は機嫌がいいと、鼻歌を歌います。","romaji":"Chichi wa kigen ga ii to, hanauta o utaimasu.","german":"My father hums when he's in a good mood.","furigana":"ちち|きげん|はなうた|うた"},{"japanese":"電車の中で思わず鼻歌を歌ってしまって、恥ずかしかったです。","romaji":"Densha no naka de omowazu hanauta o utatte shimatte, hazukashikatta desu.","german":"I hummed in the train without thinking, and it was embarrassing.","furigana":"でんしゃ|なか|おも|はなうた|うた|は"}],"notes":"鼻歌 is quietly singing or humming to oneself, usually out of good mood and while doing something else. One says 鼻歌を歌う; 鼻歌まじりに means \"cheerfully humming, completely relaxed\". Unlike ハミング, pure humming with the mouth closed, 鼻歌 can also be half-aloud with lyrics. The verb 口ずさむ \"to hum or sing a tune to oneself\" is similar.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は鼻血がよく出ます。","romaji":"Watashi wa hanaji ga yoku demasu.","german":"I often get nosebleeds.","furigana":"わたし|はなぢ|で"},{"japanese":"急に鼻血が出ました。","romaji":"Kyū ni hanaji ga demashita.","german":"I suddenly got a nosebleed.","furigana":"きゅう|はなぢ|で"},{"japanese":"鼻血が止まらないときは、下を向いて鼻を押さえてください。","romaji":"Hanaji ga tomaranai toki wa, shita o muite hana o osaete kudasai.","german":"When a nosebleed won't stop, please lean forward and pinch your nose.","furigana":"はなぢ|と|した|む|はな|お"}],"notes":"鼻血 is written with a voiced ぢ (はなぢ), because 血 on its own is read ち. Typical are 鼻血が出る \"to have a nosebleed\", 鼻血が止まる/止まらない and 鼻血を出す. In manga and anime, a nosebleed often stands jokingly for sexual arousal. Bleeding in general is 出血.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"証明書は発行できません。","romaji":"Shōmeisho wa hakkō dekimasen.","german":"We cannot issue a certificate.","furigana":"しょうめいしょ|はっこう"},{"japanese":"身分証明書を見せてください。","romaji":"Mibun shōmeisho o misete kudasai.","german":"Please show your ID.","furigana":"みぶんしょうめいしょ|み"},{"japanese":"新しい会社に出すために、卒業証明書が必要です。","romaji":"Atarashii kaisha ni dasu tame ni, sotsugyou shoumeisho ga hitsuyou desu.","german":"I need a graduation certificate to submit to my new company.","furigana":"あたら|かいしゃ|だ|そつぎょうしょうめいしょ|ひつよう"}],"notes":"証明書 is a document that officially certifies something. Common compounds are 身分証明書 \"ID card\", 卒業証明書 \"graduation certificate\", 在学証明書 \"certificate of enrollment\" and 印鑑証明書 \"seal registration certificate\". Typical verbs: 証明書を発行する \"to issue\", もらう/取る \"to get one issued\", 提出する \"to submit\". The base word 証明 means \"proof, evidence\" (証明する); a driver's license and a membership card are, by contrast, 免許証 and 会員証.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"水兵さんのセーラー服ってどこで買えるんですか？","romaji":"Suiheisan no sērāfuku tte doko de kaeru n desu ka?","german":"Where can you buy a sailor's uniform?","furigana":"すいへい|ふく|か"},{"japanese":"水兵が船の上で働いています。","romaji":"Suihei ga fune no ue de hataraite imasu.","german":"The sailors are working on the ship.","furigana":"すいへい|ふね|うえ|はたら"},{"japanese":"祖父は若いころ、海軍の水兵でした。","romaji":"Sofu wa wakai koro, kaigun no suihei deshita.","german":"When he was young, my grandfather was a sailor in the navy.","furigana":"そふ|わか|かいぐん|すいへい"}],"notes":"水兵 is a sailor of the navy (海軍), i.e. an ordinary soldier on a warship. For seafarers on civilian ships one says 船員 or 船乗り. The Japanese school uniform セーラー服 derives from the sailor uniform; 水兵さん sounds childlike and affectionate, as in children's songs. Do not confuse it with the identically read 水平 \"horizontal\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の年齢は不詳です。","romaji":"Kare no nenrei wa fushou desu.","german":"His age is unknown.","furigana":"かれ|ねんれい|ふしょう"},{"japanese":"これは作者不詳の古い歌です。","romaji":"Kore wa sakusha fushou no furui uta desu.","german":"This is an old song by an unknown author.","furigana":"さくしゃふしょう|ふる|うた"},{"japanese":"書類の住所のところに「不詳」と書いてありました。","romaji":"Shorui no juusho no tokoro ni 'fushou' to kaite arimashita.","german":"\"Unknown\" was written in the address field of the document.","furigana":"しょるい|じゅうしょ|ふしょう|か"}],"notes":"不詳 means \"not known in detail, not determined\" and belongs to official written language: on forms, in news and in source citations. Typical are 作者不詳, 年齢不詳, 身元不詳 and 生年不詳. Colloquially, someone whose age is hard to guess is jokingly called 年齢不詳. The broader 不明, by contrast, is used for anything unclear: 原因不明, 行方不明.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大阪で吉本新喜劇を見てきました。","romaji":"Oosaka de Yoshimoto Shinkigeki o mite kimashita.","german":"I went to see Yoshimoto Shinkigeki in Osaka.","furigana":"おおさか|よしもとしんきげき|み"},{"japanese":"チャップリンの喜劇は今見ても笑えます。","romaji":"Chappurin no kigeki wa ima mite mo waraemasu.","german":"Chaplin's comedies are still funny to watch today.","furigana":"きげき|いまみ|わら"},{"japanese":"私は悲劇より喜劇のほうが好きです。","romaji":"Watashi wa higeki yori kigeki no hou ga suki desu.","german":"I like comedies better than tragedies.","furigana":"わたし|ひげき|きげき|す"}],"notes":"喜劇 is comedy as a genre in theater and film; the opposite is 悲劇. Related are 喜劇映画, 喜劇役者 and 喜劇的 \"comic\". Well known is the Osaka comedy theater 吉本新喜劇. In everyday speech, for comedy shows and comedians people prefer お笑い, and for Western films and series often コメディー. The word is a noun, not an adjective.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"それが悲劇の始まりだった。","romaji":"Sore ga higeki no hajimari datta.","german":"That was the beginning of the tragedy.","furigana":"ひげき|はじ"},{"japanese":"これは悲劇的な事件です。","romaji":"Kore wa higekiteki na jiken desu.","german":"This is a tragic incident.","furigana":"ひげきてき|じけん"},{"japanese":"授業でシェイクスピアの悲劇を読みました。","romaji":"Jugyou de Sheikusupia no higeki o yomimashita.","german":"In class we read a tragedy by Shakespeare.","furigana":"じゅぎょう|ひげき|よ"}],"notes":"悲劇 means, first, a tragedy as a stage play, and second, a tragic event in real life: 悲劇が起きる, 悲劇を繰り返す. The corresponding adjective is 悲劇的 \"tragic\" (悲劇的な事件). The opposite is 喜劇. Colloquially it is also used in an exaggerated way for small mishaps: それは悲劇だね.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日は皆さんに、電気代を劇的に安くする方法をお教えします。","romaji":"Kyou wa minasan ni, denkidai o gekiteki ni yasuku suru houhou o o-oshie shimasu.","german":"Today I will teach you all a way to dramatically lower your electricity bill.","furigana":"きょう|みな|でんきだい|げきてき|やす|ほうほう|おし"},{"japanese":"試合は劇的な結末でした。","romaji":"Shiai wa gekiteki na ketsumatsu deshita.","german":"The match had a dramatic ending.","furigana":"しあい|げきてき|けつまつ"},{"japanese":"その本を読んで、私の人生は劇的に変わりました。","romaji":"Sono hon o yonde, watashi no jinsei wa gekiteki ni kawarimashita.","german":"Reading that book changed my life dramatically.","furigana":"ほん|よ|わたし|じんせい|げきてき|か"}],"notes":"劇的 is a な-adjective: 劇的な + noun means \"dramatic, thrilling, moving\" (劇的な勝利, 劇的な再会); the adverb 劇的に means \"drastically, suddenly\" with verbs of change such as 変わる, 増える, 減る, 良くなる. Unlike ドラマチック, which sounds rather romantic and emotional, 劇的に often describes a strong change soberly. With figures in formal texts, 大幅に is also used.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"皆さんは歌劇を見たことがありますか？","romaji":"Minasan wa kageki o mita koto ga arimasu ka?","german":"Have you ever seen an opera?","furigana":"みな|かげき|み"},{"japanese":"歌劇を見に行きましょう。","romaji":"Kageki o mi ni ikimashō.","german":"Let's go see an opera.","furigana":"かげき|み|い"},{"japanese":"宝塚歌劇団は女性だけの劇団です。","romaji":"Takarazuka Kagekidan wa josei dake no gekidan desu.","german":"The Takarazuka Revue is an all-female theater company.","furigana":"たからづかかげきだん|じょせい|げきだん"}],"notes":"歌劇 is the older, formal word for \"opera\"; in daily life today people almost always say オペラ. 歌劇 lives on in names and titles: 宝塚歌劇団 (the famous all-female revue theater), 歌劇場 \"opera house\", 歌劇「カルメン」. Typical verbs are 歌劇を見る and 歌劇を上演する \"to perform\". The word is a noun, not an adjective.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このやり方は不味いと思います。","romaji":"Kono yarikata wa mazui to omoimasu.","german":"I think this approach is unwise.","furigana":"かた|まず|おも"},{"japanese":"この料理は不味いです。","romaji":"Kono ryōri wa mazui desu.","german":"This dish tastes bad.","furigana":"りょうり|まず"}],"notes":"不味い is an i-adjective. For food it means \"bad-tasting\", for a course of action \"unwise\" or \"problematic\". It is often written in hiragana as まずい. 不味くない is the negative form. 不味いと思います marks a personal judgment. Directly judging taste with this word can sound blunt.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このブドウはタネ無しだから食べやすいですよ。","romaji":"Kono budou wa tanenashi da kara tabeyasui desu yo.","german":"These grapes are seedless, so they're easy to eat.","furigana":"な|た"},{"japanese":"理由無しに休みました。","romaji":"Riyū nashi ni yasumimashita.","german":"I was absent without a reason.","furigana":"りゆうな|やす"},{"japanese":"砂糖なしのコーヒーをください。","romaji":"Satou nashi no koohii o kudasai.","german":"A coffee without sugar, please.","furigana":"さとう"}],"notes":"無し \"without, not existing\" is almost always written in kana and attaches to nouns as 〜なし: 砂糖なしのコーヒー, 予約なしで入れる, 理由なしに. The counterpart is 〜あり (有り), as in 駐車場あり. With verbs one uses 〜ないで instead (朝ご飯を食べないで). Colloquially, ナシ means \"out of the question\", as the opposite of アリ \"acceptable\" (それはナシでしょ).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このマンガはまだ完結していません。","romaji":"Kono manga wa mada kanketsu shite imasen.","german":"This manga hasn't been completed yet.","furigana":"かんけつ"},{"japanese":"このドラマは全十回で完結します。","romaji":"Kono dorama wa zen jukkai de kanketsu shimasu.","german":"This drama will conclude in ten episodes.","furigana":"ぜんじゅっかい|かんけつ"},{"japanese":"このシリーズの完結編は来年公開されます。","romaji":"Kono shiriizu no kanketsuhen wa rainen koukai saremasu.","german":"The final installment of this series will be released next year.","furigana":"かんけつへん|らいねんこうかい"}],"notes":"完結 is intransitive: a story, a serialized manga or a series 完結する \"comes to its conclusion\". Typical are 連載が完結する, 全十巻で完結, 完結編 \"final part\" as well as 完結済み and 未完結. To actively bring something to a conclusion, one says 完結させる. In contrast to 終わる (\"to end\" in general), 完結 emphasizes completion in terms of content; a finished product or building is 完成 instead.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"毎晩無言電話がかかってくるんです。","romaji":"Maiban mugon denwa ga kakatte kuru n desu.","german":"I get silent phone calls every night.","furigana":"まいばんむごんでんわ"},{"japanese":"彼は無言で立っていました。","romaji":"Kare wa mugon de tatte imashita.","german":"He stood there without saying a word.","furigana":"かれ|むごん|た"},{"japanese":"けんかの後、二人は無言のまま駅まで歩きました。","romaji":"Kenka no ato, futari wa mugon no mama eki made arukimashita.","german":"After the quarrel, the two walked silently to the station.","furigana":"あと|ふたり|むごん|えき|ある"}],"notes":"無言 means \"without saying a word\": 無言で \"silently\", 無言のまま, 無言の圧力 \"silent pressure\". 無言電話 is a call in which the caller says nothing, usually as harassment. 沈黙 is silence as a state and rather literary (沈黙が続く); \"to fall silent, be quiet\" as a verb is 黙る. For a quiet environment, 静か is used, not 無言.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"愛する人と一緒にいられて、幸せです。","romaji":"Aisuru hito to issho ni irarete, shiawase desu.","german":"I'm happy to be able to be with the person I love.","furigana":"あい|ひと|いっしょ|しあわ"},{"japanese":"家族を愛しています。","romaji":"Kazoku o aishite imasu.","german":"I love my family.","furigana":"かぞく|あい"},{"japanese":"彼は音楽を心から愛していました。","romaji":"Kare wa ongaku o kokoro kara aishite imashita.","german":"He loved music with all his heart.","furigana":"かれ|おんがく|こころ|あい"}],"notes":"愛する \"to love\" is transitive (〜を愛する) and belongs to a special する-class: 愛して, negative 愛さない, potential 愛せる. In everyday speech, feelings are usually expressed with 好き or 大好き; 愛してる sounds very heavy and appears more in songs, films and letters. Very common is the attributive form 愛する + noun: 愛する人, 愛する家族, 愛する故郷. The object can also be music, nature or one's own country.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その政治家に愛人がいることが、週刊誌に書かれました。","romaji":"Sono seijika ni aijin ga iru koto ga, shuukanshi ni kakaremashita.","german":"A weekly magazine wrote that the politician has a lover.","furigana":"せいじか|あいじん|しゅうかんし|か"},{"japanese":"ドラマの中で、社長は愛人と旅行に出かけました。","romaji":"Dorama no naka de, shachou wa aijin to ryokou ni dekakemashita.","german":"In the drama, the company president went on a trip with his mistress.","furigana":"なか|しゃちょう|あいじん|りょこう|で"},{"japanese":"中国語の「愛人」は「妻」や「夫」という意味ですが、日本語では違います。","romaji":"Chuugokugo no 'aijin' wa 'tsuma' ya 'otto' to iu imi desu ga, Nihongo de wa chigaimasu.","german":"The Chinese word \"愛人\" means \"wife\" or \"husband\", but in Japanese it is different.","furigana":"ちゅうごくご|あいじん|つま|おっと|いみ|にほんご|ちが"}],"notes":"愛人 in present-day Japanese means the secret lover outside a marriage, i.e. an affair, and sounds negative. For a boyfriend or girlfriend in a normal relationship, 恋人 or 彼氏/彼女 is said. Typical are 愛人がいる and 愛人を作る. Caution for those who know Chinese: in the People's Republic of China, the same written word means \"spouse\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一日中歩いて、疲れ果ててしまいました。","romaji":"Ichinichijuu aruite, tsukarehatete shimaimashita.","german":"I walked all day and was completely exhausted.","furigana":"いちにちじゅうある|つか|は"},{"japanese":"久しぶりに帰ったら、ふるさとの町は変わり果てていました。","romaji":"Hisashiburi ni kaettara, furusato no machi wa kawarihatete imashita.","german":"When I returned after a long time, my hometown had changed beyond recognition.","furigana":"ひさ|かえ|まち|か|は"},{"japanese":"道に迷って困り果てていたら、親切な人が助けてくれました。","romaji":"Michi ni mayotte komarihatete itara, shinsetsu na hito ga tasukete kuremashita.","german":"I got lost and was at my wits' end, but a kind person helped me.","furigana":"みち|まよ|こま|は|しんせつ|ひと|たす"}],"notes":"果てる on its own is rather literary for \"to come to an end\" or \"to die\" (命が果てる). In everyday speech it is encountered mainly after a verb stem meaning \"completely, to the extreme\": 疲れ果てる \"to be completely exhausted\", 困り果てる \"to be at a total loss\", 変わり果てる \"to change beyond recognition\", あきれ果てる \"to be utterly dumbfounded\". It is intransitive; related are the noun 果て \"end, farthest edge\" (世界の果て) and 果てしない \"endless\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一昨日から熱が出ています。","romaji":"Ototoi kara netsu ga dete imasu.","german":"I've had a fever since the day before yesterday.","furigana":"おととい|ねつ|で"},{"japanese":"一昨日映画を見ました。","romaji":"Ototoi eiga o mimashita.","german":"The day before yesterday I watched a movie.","furigana":"おとといえいが|み"}],"notes":"一昨日 is read おととい here and means the day before yesterday. おととい is the usual hiragana spelling of the same time expression. The word can stand without a particle; 一昨日から stresses a start on that day that continues up to the time of speaking. The attested reading いっさくじつ is more written and formal and is not practiced here. おととい has four short morae and is not おととし, \"the year before last\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これは青春時代の思い出の写真です。","romaji":"Kore wa seishun jidai no omoide no shashin desu.","german":"This is a photo of memories from my youth.","furigana":"せいしゅんじだい|おも|で|しゃしん"},{"japanese":"この映画は高校生の青春を描いています。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa koukousei no seishun o egaite imasu.","german":"This movie depicts the youth of high school students.","furigana":"えいが|こうこうせい|せいしゅん|えが"},{"japanese":"もう四十歳ですが、気持ちはまだ青春です。","romaji":"Mou yonjussai desu ga, kimochi wa mada seishun desu.","german":"I'm already forty, but I still feel young at heart.","furigana":"よんじゅっさい|きも|せいしゅん"}],"notes":"青春 is youth as a phase of life, usually emotionally or nostalgically colored: school days, club activities, first love, dreams. Typical are 青春時代, 青春の思い出, 青春を楽しむ and 青春ドラマ/青春映画; literally it means \"blue spring\". For the quality of \"youthfulness\" people say 若さ, for young people 若者; childhood is 子どものころ. The word is a noun, not an adverb.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は英語がペラペラです。","romaji":"Kare wa eigo ga perapera desu.","german":"He is fluent in English.","furigana":"かれ|えいご"},{"japanese":"彼女は友達の秘密をペラペラしゃべってしまいました。","romaji":"Kanojo wa tomodachi no himitsu o perapera shabette shimaimashita.","german":"She blabbed her friend's secret.","furigana":"かのじょ|ともだち|ひみつ"},{"japanese":"この紙はペラペラで、すぐ破れます。","romaji":"Kono kami wa perapera de, sugu yaburemasu.","german":"This paper is flimsy and tears easily.","furigana":"かみ|やぶ"}],"notes":"ペラペラ is onomatopoeic and colloquial. Best known is \"to speak fluently\": 英語がペラペラだ, ペラペラ話す, ペラペラになる. But it can also have the negative sense \"to chatter away, to blab\" (秘密をペラペラしゃべる), as well as \"thin, flimsy\" for paper or cloth (ペラペラの紙) and \"to flip through quickly\" (本をペラペラめくる). It is often written in hiragana too; formally, \"fluent\" is 流暢.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 22"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は、サッカーで頭部にケガをしたようです。","romaji":"Kare wa, sakkā de tōbu ni kega o shita yō desu.","german":"It seems he injured his head playing soccer.","furigana":"かれ|とうぶ"},{"japanese":"頭部を保護するためにヘルメットをかぶります。","romaji":"Tōbu o hogo suru tame ni herumetto o kaburimasu.","german":"I wear a helmet to protect my head.","furigana":"とうぶ|ほご"},{"japanese":"病院で頭部のレントゲンを撮りました。","romaji":"Byouin de toubu no rentogen o torimashita.","german":"I had an X-ray of my head taken at the hospital.","furigana":"びょういん|とうぶ|と"}],"notes":"頭部 is the formal, technical word for the head as a body region and appears in medicine, news and police reports: 頭部を打つ, 頭部のけが, 頭部のレントゲン. In daily life people simply say 頭. By the same pattern there are 胸部 \"chest area\" and 腹部 \"abdominal area\". For the \"head\" of an organization, one does not use 頭部 but トップ or 〜長.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"家の西側に黄色いものがあると金運がアップするそうです。","romaji":"Ie no nishigawa ni kiiroi mono ga aru to kin'un ga appu suru sō desu.","german":"I hear that having something yellow on the west side of your house increases your financial luck.","furigana":"いえ|にしがわ|きいろ|きんうん"},{"japanese":"西側に窓があります。","romaji":"Nishigawa ni mado ga arimasu.","german":"There is a window on the west side.","furigana":"にしがわ|まど"}],"notes":"西側 refers to the western side of a house, room or area. As a noun it takes に for the place and の before another noun: 西側の窓. 金運がアップするそうです in the first example reports a belief that was heard about luck and is not an established fact. The reading にしがわ here refers to the spatial west side.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大阪弁を教えてください。","romaji":"Ōsakaben o oshiete kudasai.","german":"Please teach me the Osaka dialect.","furigana":"おおさかべん|おし"},{"japanese":"大阪弁は面白いです。","romaji":"Ōsakaben wa omoshiroi desu.","german":"The Osaka dialect is interesting.","furigana":"おおさかべん|おもしろ"},{"japanese":"大阪弁では「ありがとう」を「おおきに」と言うこともあります。","romaji":"Oosakaben de wa 'arigatou' o 'ookini' to iu koto mo arimasu.","german":"In the Osaka dialect, \"arigatou\" is sometimes said as \"ookini\".","furigana":"おおさかべん|い"}],"notes":"大阪弁 is the dialect of Osaka, the best-known variety of the Kansai dialect (関西弁). Typical are ほんま \"really\", あかん \"no good\", おおきに \"thanks\" and negation in 〜へん (分からへん). One says 大阪弁で話す or 大阪弁をしゃべる. The suffix 〜弁 forms dialect names (博多弁, 東北弁); \"dialect\" in general is 方言.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は関西弁がしゃべれます。","romaji":"Watashi wa Kansaiben ga shaberemasu.","german":"I can speak the Kansai dialect.","furigana":"わたし|かんさいべん"},{"japanese":"友達が関西弁で話します。","romaji":"Tomodachi ga kansaiben de hanashimasu.","german":"My friend speaks in the Kansai dialect.","furigana":"ともだち|かんさいべん|はな"},{"japanese":"関西弁の「あかん」は「だめ」という意味です。","romaji":"Kansaiben no 'akan' wa 'dame' to iu imi desu.","german":"\"Akan\" in the Kansai dialect means \"dame\" (no good).","furigana":"かんさいべん|いみ"}],"notes":"関西弁 is the umbrella term for the dialects of the Kansai region around 大阪, 京都 and 神戸, with sub-varieties such as 大阪弁 and 京都弁. Thanks to comedians on television, it is known all over Japan; typical are あかん, ほんま, なんでやねん and a pitch accent of its own. The counterpart is 標準語 or 共通語 \"standard language\". One says 関西弁を話す/しゃべる or 関西弁で話す.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今週は土曜日も仕事です。","romaji":"Konshū wa doyōbi mo shigoto desu.","german":"This week I have to work on Saturday too.","furigana":"こんしゅう|どようび|しごと"},{"japanese":"土曜日に買い物に行きます。","romaji":"Doyōbi ni kaimono ni ikimasu.","german":"I go shopping on Saturday.","furigana":"どようび|か|もの|い"}],"notes":"土曜日 is Saturday. 土曜日に names a point in time for an action; 土曜日も adds this day as well. 今週は土曜日も仕事です says that you work on Saturdays this week too. In どようび, よう is a long o, hence doyōbi.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"来週の日曜日は予定があります。","romaji":"Raishū no nichiyōbi wa yotei ga arimasu.","german":"Next Sunday I have plans.","furigana":"らいしゅう|にちようび|よてい"},{"japanese":"日曜日は家でゆっくりします。","romaji":"Nichiyōbi wa ie de yukkuri shimasu.","german":"On Sunday I relax at home.","furigana":"にちようび|いえ"}],"notes":"日曜日 is Sunday. 来週の日曜日 means the Sunday of next week. 日曜日は makes the day the topic. 予定があります says that you have plans; ゆっくりします can describe taking your time and relaxing. にちようび is written nichiyōbi with a long o.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今度の月曜日、仕事の後、飲みに行かない？","romaji":"Kondo no getsuyōbi, shigoto no ato, nomi ni ikanai?","german":"Want to go for a drink after work this coming Monday?","furigana":"こんど|げつようび|しごと|あと|の|い"},{"japanese":"月曜日から仕事が始まります。","romaji":"Getsuyōbi kara shigoto ga hajimarimasu.","german":"Work starts on Monday.","furigana":"げつようび|しごと|はじ"}],"notes":"月曜日 is Monday. 今度の月曜日 names the coming Monday. 仕事の後 places an action in time after work. 飲みに行かない？ is a familiar invitation to go for a drink. 月曜日から names Monday as the start; げつようび has a long o.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"木曜日は六時からピアノなんです。","romaji":"Mokuyōbi wa rokuji kara piano nan desu.","german":"On Thursday I have piano from six o'clock.","furigana":"もくようび|ろくじ"},{"japanese":"木曜日にテストがあります。","romaji":"Mokuyōbi ni tesuto ga arimasu.","german":"There is a test on Thursday.","furigana":"もくようび"}],"notes":"木曜日 is Thursday. 木曜日は names it as the topic, 木曜日に as the date of an event. 六時から means \"from six o'clock\". ピアノなんです can stand in conversation for a piano-lesson appointment; んです explains your own schedule. もくようび is written mokuyōbi.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"水曜日にするべきことは、大きく分けて三つあります。","romaji":"Suiyōbi ni suru beki koto wa, ōkiku wakete mittsu arimasu.","german":"What we should do on Wednesday can be roughly divided into three things.","furigana":"すいようび|おお|わ|みっ"},{"japanese":"水曜日は早く帰ります。","romaji":"Suiyōbi wa hayaku kaerimasu.","german":"I go home early on Wednesday.","furigana":"すいようび|はや|かえ"}],"notes":"水曜日 is Wednesday. 水曜日にするべきこと means things one should do on that day. 大きく分けて sorts them roughly into groups; 三つ names three tasks here. 水曜日は can make the day the topic of a personal plan. すいようび has the vowel sequence ui followed by a long o.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女はカナダからの留学生です。","romaji":"Kanojo wa Kanada kara no ryūgakusei desu.","german":"She is an international student from Canada.","furigana":"かのじょ|りゅうがくせい"},{"japanese":"大学にたくさんの留学生がいます。","romaji":"Daigaku ni takusan no ryūgakusei ga imasu.","german":"There are many international students at the university.","furigana":"だいがく|りゅうがくせい"}],"notes":"留学生 is someone who studies abroad, such as an exchange student. The word does not necessarily imply an exchange program. カナダからの留学生 gives the origin with からの. 大学に留学生がいる names foreign students at a university. りゅう is a long u after the jointly pronounced sequence りゅ.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 11"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は日本のお笑いが好きです。","romaji":"Watashi wa Nippon no owarai ga suki desu.","german":"I like Japanese comedy.","furigana":"わたし|にっぽん|わら|す"},{"japanese":"お笑い番組を見て笑いました。","romaji":"Owarai bangumi o mite waraimashita.","german":"I watched a comedy show and laughed.","furigana":"わら|ばんぐみ|み|わら"},{"japanese":"弟は将来、お笑い芸人になりたいそうです。","romaji":"Otouto wa shourai, owarai geinin ni naritai sou desu.","german":"My younger brother says he wants to become a comedian in the future.","furigana":"おとうと|しょうらい|わら|げいにん"}],"notes":"お笑い is Japanese comedy and entertainment humor, i.e. 漫才, sketches (コント) and 落語 on television and on stage. Typical are お笑い番組 \"comedy show\", お笑い芸人 \"comedian\" and お笑いコンビ \"comedy duo\". 喜劇, by contrast, is comedy as a theater or film genre, and コメディー refers rather to Western comedies. Laughter itself is 笑い, not お笑い.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本ではクリスマスディナーにケンタッキーを食べると言ったら、なぜか笑いが起きました。","romaji":"Nippon de wa kurisumasu dinā ni Kentakkī o taberu to ittara, naze ka warai ga okimashita.","german":"When I said that in Japan people eat KFC for Christmas dinner, for some reason laughter broke out.","furigana":"にっぽん|た|い|わら|お"},{"japanese":"笑いは健康にいいです。","romaji":"Warai wa kenkō ni ii desu.","german":"Laughter is good for your health.","furigana":"わら|けんこう"}],"notes":"笑い is the noun for laughter. 笑いが起きる means that laughter arises; 笑い声を聞く means to hear a laughing voice. The verb is 笑う. In the first example, a remark becomes an occasion for laughter; the sentence does not say that every person in Japan chooses this Christmas meal.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ちゃんと借用書を書いてくれるんだったら、お金をかしてもいいよ。","romaji":"Chanto shakuyōsho o kaite kureru n dattara, okane o kashite mo ii yo.","german":"If you write me a proper IOU, I'm happy to lend you the money.","furigana":"しゃくようしょ|か|かね"},{"japanese":"「パン」はポルトガル語からの借用語です。","romaji":"'Pan' wa Porutogarugo kara no shakuyougo desu.","german":"「パン」 is a loanword from Portuguese.","furigana":"ご|しゃくようご"},{"japanese":"市のホールを借用するには、申し込みが必要です。","romaji":"Shi no hooru o shakuyou suru ni wa, moushikomi ga hitsuyou desu.","german":"To use the city hall, you need to submit an application.","furigana":"し|しゃくよう|もう|こ|ひつよう"}],"notes":"借用 is the formal word for \"to borrow\" and appears in contracts, applications and technical texts: 〜を借用する, 借用書 \"IOU\", 借用語 \"loanword\". In everyday speech one almost always says 借りる; \"to borrow a book from the library\" is 本を借りる, not 借用する. The counterpart \"to lend\" is formally 貸与, in everyday speech 貸す.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コストメリットは借家の方が大きいんですよ。","romaji":"Kosuto meritto wa shakuya no hou ga ookii n desu yo.","german":"Cost-wise, a rented house is more advantageous.","furigana":"しゃくや|ほう|おお"},{"japanese":"借家に住んでいます。","romaji":"Shakuya ni sunde imasu.","german":"I live in a rented house.","furigana":"しゃくや|す"},{"japanese":"結婚してから十年間、ずっと借家暮らしです。","romaji":"Kekkon shite kara juunenkan, zutto shakuya gurashi desu.","german":"For the ten years since we got married, we have always lived in rented housing.","furigana":"けっこん|じゅうねんかん|しゃくやぐ"}],"notes":"借家 is a rented house, usually a detached house (一戸建て), as opposed to an owned home, 持ち家. Typical are 借家に住む and 借家暮らし \"to live in rented housing\". For rental apartments in general one says 賃貸 (賃貸マンション) or アパート. In legal terms such as 借地借家法, 借家 is also read しゃっか.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ナーランダ僧院は仏教のメッカでした。","romaji":"Nāranda sōin wa bukkyō no mekka deshita.","german":"The Nalanda monastery was the Mecca of Buddhism.","furigana":"そういん|ぶっきょう"},{"japanese":"山の中に古い僧院があります。","romaji":"Yama no naka ni furui sōin ga arimasu.","german":"There is an old monastery in the mountains.","furigana":"やま|なか|ふる|そういん"},{"japanese":"その僧院では、お坊さんたちが毎朝五時に起きます。","romaji":"Sono souin de wa, obousantachi ga maiasa goji ni okimasu.","german":"In that monastery, the monks get up at five o'clock every morning.","furigana":"そういん|ぼう|まいあさごじ|お"}],"notes":"僧院 is a monastery where monks (僧, お坊さん) live and practice religion. It sounds literary and is used mainly for monasteries abroad, for example in Tibet, India or Europe. For Japanese Buddhist temples one says 寺 or お寺 in everyday speech, for Christian monasteries mostly 修道院. Typical are 僧院で暮らす and 古い僧院.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この本は元仏僧の方が書かれた本です。","romaji":"Kono hon wa moto bussō no kata ga kakareta hon desu.","german":"This book was written by a former Buddhist monk.","furigana":"ほん|もとぶっそう|かた|か|ほん"},{"japanese":"仏僧が修行をしています。","romaji":"Bussō ga shugyō o shite imasu.","german":"A Buddhist monk is doing religious training.","furigana":"ぶっそう|しゅぎょう"}],"notes":"仏僧 refers to a Buddhist monk or cleric. 元仏僧 means a former Buddhist monk; 仏僧が修行をする describes religious practice. 方 and 書かれた in the first example express respect toward the author. Do not confuse ぶっそう with the identically read 物騒, \"dangerous\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"シーッ！それは禁句ですよ！","romaji":"Shī! Sore wa kinku desu yo!","german":"Shh! You can't say that!"},{"japanese":"彼女の前で年齢の話は禁句だよ。","romaji":"Kanojo no mae de nenrei no hanashi wa kinku da yo.","german":"Talking about age in front of her is taboo.","furigana":"かのじょ|まえ|ねんれい|はなし|きんく"},{"japanese":"結婚式のスピーチでは「別れる」や「切れる」は禁句です。","romaji":"Kekkonshiki no supiichi de wa 'wakareru' ya 'kireru' wa kinku desu.","german":"In wedding speeches, words like 「別れる」 or 「切れる」 are taboo.","furigana":"けっこんしき|わか|き|きんく"}],"notes":"禁句 is a word or topic that one should not bring up in a particular situation or in front of a particular person: 〜は禁句だ, 〜の前では禁句. A well-known example is wedding speeches, in which words like 別れる \"to part\" or 切れる \"to be cut\" are considered unlucky (忌み言葉). Broader is タブー \"taboo\" for actions and topics in general; words that are not broadcast on radio and TV are called 放送禁止用語.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この土地は可分の財産として扱われる。","romaji":"Kono tochi wa kabun no zaisan to shite atsukawareru.","german":"This land is treated as divisible property.","furigana":"とち|かぶん|ざいさん|あつか"},{"japanese":"法律上、可分債務と不可分債務は区別される。","romaji":"Hōritsujō, kabun saimu to fukabun saimu wa kubetsu sareru.","german":"Legally, divisible obligations and indivisible obligations are distinguished.","furigana":"ほうりつじょう|かぶんさいむ|ふかぶんさいむ|くべつ"},{"japanese":"お金のように分けても価値が変わらない物を、民法では可分物という。","romaji":"Okane no yō ni waketemo kachi ga kawaranai mono o, minpō de wa kabunbutsu to iu.","german":"Things like money, whose value does not change when divided, are called divisible things in the Civil Code.","furigana":"かね|わ|かち|か|もの|みんぽう|かぶんぶつ"}],"notes":"可分 means \"divisible\" and is a technical term, mainly in civil law: 可分債務 (divisible obligation), 可分物 (divisible thing), 可分給付. It usually appears as a prefix in compounds or with の (可分の財産). In everyday life one far more often meets the opposite 不可分, for example in 不可分の関係 (\"inseparable relationship\"). For \"can be divided\" in ordinary usage one says 分けられる or 分割できる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"僕には告白する勇気はありません。","romaji":"Boku ni wa kokuhaku suru yūki wa arimasen.","german":"I don't have the courage to confess.","furigana":"ぼく|こくはく|ゆうき"},{"japanese":"好きな人に告白しました。","romaji":"Suki na hito ni kokuhaku shimashita.","german":"I confessed my feelings to the person I like.","furigana":"す|ひと|こくはく"},{"japanese":"彼は昔ついたうそを、母に告白しました。","romaji":"Kare wa mukashi tsuita uso o, haha ni kokuhaku shimashita.","german":"He confessed to his mother a lie he had told long ago.","furigana":"かれ|むかし|はは|こくはく"}],"notes":"告白 in everyday life today mainly means \"declaration of love\": 好きな人に告白する, 告白される; afterwards one is often only officially a couple in Japan (付き合う). In youth slang it is shortened to 告る. It also means \"to confess, reveal\" with を: 秘密を告白する, 罪を告白する. A confession of a crime to the police, on the other hand, is usually 自白.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あのドラマの次回の予告、見た？","romaji":"Ano dorama no jikai no yokoku, mita?","german":"Did you see the preview for the next episode of that drama?","furigana":"じかい|よこく|み"},{"japanese":"会社は予告なしに工場を閉めました。","romaji":"Kaisha wa yokoku nashi ni koujou o shimemashita.","german":"The company closed the factory without notice.","furigana":"かいしゃ|よこく|こうじょう|し"},{"japanese":"先生は、来週テストをすると予告しました。","romaji":"Sensei wa, raishuu tesuto o suru to yokoku shimashita.","german":"The teacher announced in advance that there would be a test next week.","furigana":"せんせい|らいしゅう|よこく"}],"notes":"予告 is an announcement in advance: 〜を予告する or 〜と予告する, 予告なしに or 予告なく \"without warning\". In film and television it means \"preview\": 次回予告 \"preview of the next episode\", 予告編 \"trailer\". Do not confuse with 予報 \"forecast\" (天気予報) and 予約 \"reservation\"; a general notice is お知らせ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"喫煙は体に良くないです。","romaji":"Kitsuen wa karada ni yokunai desu.","german":"Smoking is bad for your health.","furigana":"きつえん|からだ|よ"},{"japanese":"ここは喫煙禁止です。","romaji":"Koko wa kitsuen kinshi desu.","german":"Smoking is prohibited here.","furigana":"きつえんきんし"},{"japanese":"すみません、喫煙所はどこにありますか。","romaji":"Sumimasen, kitsuenjo wa doko ni arimasu ka?","german":"Excuse me, where is the smoking area?","furigana":"きつえんじょ"}],"notes":"喫煙 is the formal word for \"smoking\" and is encountered above all on signs and in official texts: 喫煙所 \"smoking area\", 喫煙席 \"smoking seat\", 喫煙者 \"smoker\". In conversation people almost always say たばこを吸う; 喫煙する sounds official. The counterpart is 禁煙 \"no smoking; quitting smoking\" - on signs it is usually simply 禁煙 rather than 喫煙禁止.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は喫茶店でモーニングを食べるのが好きです。","romaji":"Watashi wa kissaten de mōningu o taberu no ga suki desu.","german":"I like eating the morning set at a coffee shop.","furigana":"わたし|きっさてん|た|す"},{"japanese":"駅の近くの喫茶店で休みましょう。","romaji":"Eki no chikaku no kissaten de yasumimashō.","german":"Let's take a break at the coffee shop near the station.","furigana":"えき|ちか|きっさてん|やす"}],"notes":"喫茶店 refers to a café or tea room. 喫茶店で names the place of a break or meal. モーニング here means a breakfast offer at the café. 駅の近くの喫茶店 describes a café near the station. The small っ in きっさてん doubles the s: kissaten.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 10"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新聞は、駅の近くに新しい病院ができると報じています。","romaji":"Shinbun wa, eki no chikaku ni atarashii byouin ga dekiru to houjite imasu.","german":"The newspaper reports that a new hospital will be built near the station.","furigana":"しんぶん|えき|ちか|あたら|びょういん|ほう"},{"japanese":"その事故はテレビのニュースでも報じられました。","romaji":"Sono jiko wa terebi no nyuusu demo houjiraremashita.","german":"The accident was also reported on the TV news.","furigana":"じこ|ほう"},{"japanese":"メディアが報じることを、全部信じてはいけません。","romaji":"Media ga houjiru koto o, zenbu shinjite wa ikemasen.","german":"You shouldn't believe everything the media report.","furigana":"ほう|ぜんぶしん"}],"notes":"報じる \"to report, announce\" is a literary verb of news language: 新聞が〜と報じる \"the newspaper reports that ...\", 事故を報じる. Very common is the passive 報じられる \"it is reported\". In everyday speech people more often say 伝える or ニュースで言っていた; the related noun is 報道 or 報道する. The older variant 報ずる has the same meaning; the sense \"to repay\" is nowadays usually expressed with 報いる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一日二万円で留守番の仕事を引き受けた。","romaji":"Ichinichi ni man'en de rusuban no shigoto o hikiuketa.","german":"I took a job house-sitting for 20,000 yen a day.","furigana":"いちにちにまんえん|るすばん|しごと|ひ|う"},{"japanese":"両親が出かけている間、留守番をしました。","romaji":"Ryōshin ga dekakete iru aida, rusuban o shimashita.","german":"I stayed home while my parents were out.","furigana":"りょうしん|で|あいだ|るすばん"},{"japanese":"留守番電話にメッセージを残してください。","romaji":"Rusuban denwa ni messeeji o nokoshite kudasai.","german":"Please leave a message on the answering machine.","furigana":"るすばんでんわ|のこ"}],"notes":"留守番 means staying at home and looking after the house or apartment while the others are away: 留守番をする / 留守番する, typical with children: 一人で留守番する. To children one says kindly お留守番. 留守 alone is absence from home (留守にする \"to be away\"). The answering machine is 留守番電話, short 留守電.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 33"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今は銀座にいます。","romaji":"Ima wa Ginza ni imasu.","german":"I'm in Ginza now.","furigana":"いま|ぎんざ"},{"japanese":"銀座で買い物を楽しみました。","romaji":"Ginza de kaimono o tanoshimimashita.","german":"I enjoyed shopping in Ginza.","furigana":"ぎんざ|か|もの|たの"},{"japanese":"日本には「銀座」という名前のついた商店街がたくさんあります。","romaji":"Nihon ni wa 'Ginza' to iu namae no tsuita shoutengai ga takusan arimasu.","german":"In Japan there are many shopping streets with the name \"Ginza\".","furigana":"にほん|ぎんざ|なまえ|しょうてんがい"}],"notes":"銀座 is the famous shopping and entertainment district in Tokyo with luxury shops and department stores: 銀座に行く, 銀座で買い物をする. Because Ginza is regarded as the epitome of a lively shopping street, many shopping streets throughout Japan are called 〜銀座 (e.g. 戸越銀座). As a place name it is always written in kanji.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"キオスクで駅弁を買いました。","romaji":"Kiosuku de ekiben o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a station bento at the kiosk.","furigana":"えきべん|か"},{"japanese":"新幹線で駅弁を食べました。","romaji":"Shinkansen de ekiben o tabemashita.","german":"I ate a station bento on the Shinkansen.","furigana":"しんかんせん|えきべん|た"},{"japanese":"デパートで、全国の駅弁を売っています。","romaji":"Depaato de, zenkoku no ekiben o utte imasu.","german":"The department store sells ekiben from all over the country.","furigana":"ぜんこく|えきべん|う"}],"notes":"駅弁 is a short form of 駅 and 弁当: a lunch box sold at train stations, often with regional specialties. You typically buy one before a longer trip (駅弁を買う) and eat it on the train, especially on the 新幹線. Department stores hold popular 駅弁大会 with bentos from all over Japan. A bento from a supermarket or convenience store is, by contrast, not a 駅弁.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コウイチの弓がどこにも見当たらないんだ。","romaji":"Kouichi no yumi ga doko ni mo miataranai n da.","german":"I can't find Koichi's bow anywhere.","furigana":"ゆみ|みあ"},{"japanese":"弓で的を射ました。","romaji":"Yumi de mato o imashita.","german":"I hit the target with a bow.","furigana":"ゆみ|まと|い"},{"japanese":"バイオリンの弓が古くなったので、新しいのを買いました。","romaji":"Baiorin no yumi ga furuku natta node, atarashii no o kaimashita.","german":"My violin bow got old, so I bought a new one.","furigana":"ゆみ|ふる|あたら|か"}],"notes":"弓 is the bow as a weapon or sports equipment; the arrow is 矢. Typical combinations: 弓を引く \"to draw the bow\", 弓で矢を射る \"to shoot with a bow\". Traditional Japanese archery is called 弓道. 弓 also denotes the bow for string instruments: バイオリンの弓.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いい記事を書くためには下書きをすることが大事です。","romaji":"Ii kiji o kaku tame ni wa shitagaki o suru koto ga daiji desu.","german":"To write a good article, it is important to make a draft.","furigana":"きじ|か|したが|だいじ"},{"japanese":"メールの下書きを保存しました。","romaji":"Mēru no shitagaki o hozon shimashita.","german":"I saved the draft of the e-mail.","furigana":"したが|ほぞん"},{"japanese":"鉛筆で下書きしてから、ペンで描きます。","romaji":"Enpitsu de shitagaki shite kara, pen de kakimasu.","german":"I sketch in pencil first and then draw with a pen.","furigana":"えんぴつ|したが|か"}],"notes":"下書き is a draft or preliminary sketch - for texts as well as for pictures (first sketch in pencil, then ink). You say 下書きをする / 下書きする or 〜の下書きを書く. In e-mail and apps, 下書き is the drafts folder: 下書きに保存する. The counterpart, the clean final version, is 清書.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いつも予定ができたら、すぐにカレンダーに書き入れるようにしています。","romaji":"Itsumo yotei ga dekitara, sugu ni karendā ni kakiireru yō ni shite imasu.","german":"Whenever I have a new appointment, I make a point of writing it into my calendar right away.","furigana":"よてい|か|い"},{"japanese":"名前を書類に書き入れました。","romaji":"Namae o shorui ni kakiiremashita.","german":"I wrote my name in the document.","furigana":"なまえ|しょるい|か|い"},{"japanese":"空いているところに、正しい答えを書き入れてください。","romaji":"Aite iru tokoro ni, tadashii kotae o kakiirete kudasai.","german":"Please fill in the correct answer in the blank.","furigana":"あ|ただ|こた|か|い"}],"notes":"書き入れる means to write something into a designated place: the place with に, the content with を - カレンダーに予定を書き入れる, 空欄に答えを書き入れる. Considerably more common is the synonymous 書き込む, which is also used for posts on the internet; on forms you read the formal 記入する. There is no intransitive counterpart.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ステッカーの注文枚数は、百枚からです。","romaji":"Sutekkā no chūmon maisū wa, hyakumai kara desu.","german":"The minimum order quantity for stickers is 100 sheets.","furigana":"ちゅうもんまいすう|ひゃくまい"},{"japanese":"コピーの枚数を確認してください。","romaji":"Kopī no maisū o kakunin shite kudasai.","german":"Please check the number of copies.","furigana":"まいすう|かくにん"},{"japanese":"一人が買えるチケットの枚数には制限があります。","romaji":"Hitori ga kaeru chiketto no maisuu ni wa seigen ga arimasu.","german":"There is a limit on the number of tickets one person can buy.","furigana":"ひとり|か|まいすう|せいげん"}],"notes":"枚数 is the number of things counted with the counter 枚: sheets, copies, photos, tickets, shirts, plates. Typical are 枚数を数える / 確認する and 枚数制限 \"limit on the number of pieces\". When asking directly for the number, you say 何枚; for other things 数 or 個数, 人数 etc. is used.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ガソリンスタンドで洗車してもらいました。","romaji":"Gasorin sutando de sensha shite moraimashita.","german":"I had my car washed at the gas station.","furigana":"せんしゃ"},{"japanese":"週末に洗車をしました。","romaji":"Shūmatsu ni sensha o shimashita.","german":"I washed my car on the weekend.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|せんしゃ"},{"japanese":"明日は雨だから、洗車するのはやめておこう。","romaji":"Ashita wa ame da kara, sensha suru no wa yamete okou.","german":"It's going to rain tomorrow, so I'll skip washing the car.","furigana":"あした|あめ|せんしゃ"}],"notes":"洗車 is washing a car; as a する-verb it is intransitive: 週末に洗車する. To name the car as an object, one says 車を洗う. Related are 洗車場 \"car wash, washing area\" and 洗車機 \"automatic car wash\"; at the gas station one often has the car washed: 洗車してもらう.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日は焼き肉が食べたい気分です。","romaji":"Kyō wa yakiniku ga tabetai kibun desu.","german":"I feel like eating yakiniku today.","furigana":"きょう|や|にく|た|きぶん"},{"japanese":"友達と焼き肉を食べに行きました。","romaji":"Tomodachi to yakiniku o tabe ni ikimashita.","german":"I went to eat yakiniku with my friends.","furigana":"ともだち|や|にく|た|い"},{"japanese":"焼き肉屋では、自分で肉を焼きます。","romaji":"Yakinikuya de wa, jibun de niku o yakimasu.","german":"At a yakiniku restaurant, you grill the meat yourself.","furigana":"や|にくや|じぶん|にく|や"}],"notes":"焼き肉 is the grilling of bite-sized pieces of meat at the table, very popular in Japan and similar to Korean barbecue; one goes 焼き肉に行く or to a 焼き肉屋 and grills the meat oneself. In restaurant names it is often written 焼肉 without き. In general it can also simply mean \"grilled meat\". Grilling outdoors is, by contrast, バーベキュー.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"黒煙が見えると思ったら、あそこのマンションで火事だって！","romaji":"Kokuen ga mieru to omottara, asoko no manshon de kaji datte!","german":"I thought I saw black smoke, and it turns out there's a fire in that apartment building over there!","furigana":"こくえん|み|おも|かじ"},{"japanese":"工場から黒煙が上がっています。","romaji":"Kōjō kara kokuen ga agatte imasu.","german":"Black smoke is rising from the factory.","furigana":"こうじょう|こくえん|あ"},{"japanese":"古いバスが黒煙を出しながら走っていました。","romaji":"Furui basu ga kokuen o dashinagara hashitte imashita.","german":"An old bus was driving along belching black smoke.","furigana":"ふる|こくえん|だ|はし"}],"notes":"黒煙 is a written-language word for \"black smoke\", as known from news about fires and accidents: 黒煙が上がる \"black smoke rises\", 黒煙を出す. In conversation people usually just say 黒い煙. White smoke is correspondingly 白煙.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの禅寺の名前は何だっけ？","romaji":"Ano zendera no namae wa nan dakke?","german":"What was the name of that Zen temple again?","furigana":"ぜんでら|なまえ|なん"},{"japanese":"京都の禅寺を訪れました。","romaji":"Kyōto no zendera o otozuremashita.","german":"I visited a Zen temple in Kyoto.","furigana":"きょうと|ぜんでら|おとず"},{"japanese":"禅寺で座禅を体験しました。","romaji":"Zendera de zazen o taiken shimashita.","german":"I experienced zazen at a Zen temple.","furigana":"ぜんでら|ざぜん|たいけん"}],"notes":"禅寺 is a temple of Zen Buddhism; note the reading でら (voicing of てら). In general, 寺 or politely お寺 is said for a temple. At many 禅寺, visitors can try 座禅 (seated meditation). Well-known examples are many temples in Kyoto and Kamakura.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は禅僧です。","romaji":"Kare wa zensō desu.","german":"He is a Zen monk.","furigana":"かれ|ぜんそう"},{"japanese":"禅僧が静かに座禅をしています。","romaji":"Zensō ga shizuka ni zazen o shite imasu.","german":"The Zen monk is meditating quietly.","furigana":"ぜんそう|しず|ざぜん"},{"japanese":"その禅僧は毎朝四時に起きて、お寺を掃除します。","romaji":"Sono zensou wa maiasa yoji ni okite, otera o souji shimasu.","german":"That Zen monk gets up at four every morning and cleans the temple.","furigana":"ぜんそう|まいあさよじ|お|てら|そうじ"}],"notes":"禅僧 refers to a monk or priest of Zen Buddhism; 僧 alone is a somewhat formal word for Buddhist monks. In everyday speech one says お坊さん. 禅僧 is encountered mainly in books, documentaries and explanations about temples, often together with 座禅 and 修行 \"ascetic training\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この町には、色々な人種の人がいます。","romaji":"Kono machi ni wa, iroiro na jinshu no hito ga imasu.","german":"People of many different races live in this town.","furigana":"まち|いろいろ|じんしゅ|ひと"},{"japanese":"人は人種に関係なく平等です。","romaji":"Hito wa jinshu ni kankei naku byoudou desu.","german":"All people are equal regardless of race.","furigana":"ひと|じんしゅ|かんけい|びょうどう"},{"japanese":"人種差別は許されません。","romaji":"Jinshu sabetsu wa yurusaremasen.","german":"Racial discrimination is not acceptable.","furigana":"じんしゅさべつ|ゆる"}],"notes":"人種 is the (biologically conceived) human race, today mainly in expressions such as 人種差別 \"racism, racial discrimination\" and 人種に関係なく \"regardless of race\". For ethnic groups one says 民族, for nationality 国籍. Colloquially, 人種 is used jokingly for a \"type of person\": あの人たちは別の人種だ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"それは、ほぼやってないに等しいんじゃない？","romaji":"Sore wa, hobo yattenai ni hitoshii n ja nai?","german":"Isn't that practically the same as not having done it?","furigana":"ひと"},{"japanese":"二つの量は等しいです。","romaji":"Futatsu no ryō wa hitoshii desu.","german":"The two quantities are equal.","furigana":"ふた|りょう|ひと"},{"japanese":"この学校では、全員に等しくチャンスがあります。","romaji":"Kono gakkou de wa, zen'in ni hitoshiku chansu ga arimasu.","german":"At this school, everyone has an equal chance.","furigana":"がっこう|ぜんいん|ひと"}],"notes":"等しい is a rather literary い-adjective for \"equal\", especially for quantities, lengths and values: 長さが等しい, AはBに等しい. Very common is the figurative phrase 〜に等しい \"as good as, tantamount to\": ないに等しい \"practically nothing\". The adverb form 等しく means \"equally\". In everyday speech, \"equal, the same\" is usually 同じ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「イコール」って「等号」って言うんだ！知らなかった。","romaji":"'Ikooru' tte 'tougou' tte iu n da! Shiranakatta.","german":"So 「イコール」 is also called 「等号」! I didn't know that.","furigana":"とうごう|い|し"},{"japanese":"数式に等号を使います。","romaji":"Sūshiki ni tōgō o tsukaimasu.","german":"We use the equals sign in mathematical formulas.","furigana":"すうしき|とうごう|つか"},{"japanese":"テストで等号を書き忘れて、点を引かれました。","romaji":"Tesuto de tougou o kakiwasurete, ten o hikaremashita.","german":"I forgot to write the equals sign on the test and lost points.","furigana":"とうごう|か|わす|てん|ひ"}],"notes":"等号 is the technical term for the equals sign \"=\" from math class; in everyday speech the sign is usually read as イコール. The inequality signs < and > are called 不等号. Typical: 等号で結ぶ \"to connect with an equals sign\", 等号を書く.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この宝くじの一等の賞金はいくらですか？","romaji":"Kono takarakuji no ittō no shōkin wa ikura desu ka?","german":"How much is the first prize in this lottery?","furigana":"たから|いっとう|しょうきん"},{"japanese":"フェリーの一等の部屋を予約しました。","romaji":"Ferii no ittou no heya o yoyaku shimashita.","german":"I booked a first-class room on the ferry.","furigana":"いっとう|へや|よやく"},{"japanese":"運動会のかけっこで一等になりました。","romaji":"Undoukai no kakekko de ittou ni narimashita.","german":"I came in first in the foot race at the sports day.","furigana":"うんどうかい|いっとう"}],"notes":"一等 means \"first rank, first class, first prize\": 宝くじの一等 \"jackpot of a lottery\", 一等賞, 一等になる \"to come first\". On ferries there are still 一等 and 二等 as classes; on trains the first class is now called グリーン車. 一等地 is a \"prime location\". For placement in a competition, 一位 or colloquially 一番 is more common.","levelNote":"Compound: 等 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"学校で避難訓練がありました。","romaji":"Gakkou de hinan kunren ga arimashita.","german":"We had an evacuation drill at school.","furigana":"がっこう|ひなんくんれん"},{"japanese":"毎日厳しい訓練をしています。","romaji":"Mainichi kibishii kunren o shite imasu.","german":"We do tough training every day.","furigana":"まいにちきび|くんれん"},{"japanese":"盲導犬は、長い時間をかけて訓練されます。","romaji":"Moudouken wa, nagai jikan o kakete kunren saremasu.","german":"Guide dogs are trained over a long period of time.","furigana":"もうどうけん|なが|じかん|くんれん"}],"notes":"訓練 is systematic, often physical training or a drill to practice an ability or behavior: 避難訓練 \"evacuation drill\", 軍事訓練, 犬を訓練する \"to train a dog\". As a する-verb it is transitive; the passive 訓練される is common. For practicing sports, music or languages one rather says 練習, for fitness トレーニング, for in-house company training 研修.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"脳死かどうかは、どうやって判断するんですか？","romaji":"Noushi ka dou ka wa, dou yatte handan suru n desu ka?","german":"How do they determine whether it's brain death?","furigana":"のうし|はんだん"},{"japanese":"脳死と判定されました。","romaji":"Nōshi to hantei saremashita.","german":"I was pronounced brain dead.","furigana":"のうし|はんてい"},{"japanese":"脳死を人の死と考えるかどうかは、国によって違います。","romaji":"Noushi o hito no shi to kangaeru ka dou ka wa, kuni ni yotte chigaimasu.","german":"Whether brain death is considered death of a person differs from country to country.","furigana":"のうし|ひと|し|かんが|くに|ちが"}],"notes":"脳死 is the medical and legal term for brain death: 脳死と判定される \"to be declared brain dead\", 脳死の状態. It plays a role above all in discussions about organ donation, because in Japan it was long disputed whether brain death counts as the death of a person. Cardiac death is 心臓死; \"to die\" in general is 死ぬ / 亡くなる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"少ない脳みそを使ってよく考えてみろ！","romaji":"Sukunai nōmiso o tsukatte yoku kangaete miro!","german":"Use your tiny brain and think hard!","furigana":"すく|のう|つか|かんが"},{"japanese":"テストの後は、脳みそが疲れて何も考えられない。","romaji":"Tesuto no ato wa, noumiso ga tsukarete nani mo kangaerarenai.","german":"After the exam, my brain is so tired I can't think about anything.","furigana":"あと|のう|つか|なに|かんが"},{"japanese":"ゾンビ映画で脳みそが出てくるシーンは苦手です。","romaji":"Zonbi eiga de noumiso ga dete kuru shiin wa nigate desu.","german":"I can't stand scenes in zombie movies where brains come out.","furigana":"えいが|のう|で|にがて"}],"notes":"脳みそ (also 脳味噌) is a colloquial word for the brain or brain matter and figuratively for \"brains\": 脳みそを使え \"Use your head!\" - this often sounds rude or mocking. In neutral or medical contexts, 脳 is used. With 疲れる it jokingly describes mental exhaustion.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"それってメディアの洗脳だよ。","romaji":"Sore tte media no sennou da yo.","german":"That's media brainwashing.","furigana":"せんのう"},{"japanese":"洗脳されないように気をつけてください。","romaji":"Sennō sarenai yō ni ki o tsukete kudasai.","german":"Please be careful not to be brainwashed.","furigana":"せんのう|き"},{"japanese":"毎日このCMを見ていたら、洗脳されて歌を覚えてしまいました。","romaji":"Mainichi kono shiiemu o mite itara, sennou sarete uta o oboete shimaimashita.","german":"I watched this commercial every day, got brainwashed, and ended up memorizing the song.","furigana":"まいにち|み|せんのう|うた|おぼ"}],"notes":"洗脳 is \"brainwashing\"; as a する-verb it is transitive (人を洗脳する), and very often used in the passive 洗脳される. It is spoken of seriously in connection with cults or propaganda, but colloquially also in an exaggerated, joking way, for example when an advertising jingle won't leave your head or you are completely taken in by someone's opinion.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ここに落書きをしたのは誰だ？","romaji":"Koko ni rakugaki o shita no wa dare da?","german":"Who scribbled here?","furigana":"らくが|だれ"},{"japanese":"壁に落書きをしてはいけません。","romaji":"Kabe ni rakugaki o shite wa ikemasen.","german":"You must not draw graffiti on the wall.","furigana":"かべ|らくが"},{"japanese":"授業中、ノートに猫の落書きをしていました。","romaji":"Jugyouchuu, nooto ni neko no rakugaki o shite imashita.","german":"During class, I was doodling cats in my notebook.","furigana":"じゅぎょうちゅう|ねこ|らくが"}],"notes":"落書き covers everything that is scribbled without permission or on the side: graffiti on walls, scribbles in textbooks and harmless doodles in a notebook. The place takes に: 壁に落書きする, ノートに落書きをする. Do not confuse with 下書き \"draft\". For graffiti as an art form, グラフィティ is also said.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"僕のお父さんはピザの配達員です。誰よりも速くピザを配達することができます。","romaji":"Boku no otousan wa piza no haitatsuin desu. Dare yori mo hayaku piza o haitatsu suru koto ga dekimasu.","german":"My father is a pizza delivery person. He can deliver pizzas faster than anyone.","furigana":"ぼく|とう|はいたついん|だれ|はや|はいたつ"},{"japanese":"荷物を配達してもらいました。","romaji":"Nimotsu o haitatsu shite moraimashita.","german":"I had the package delivered.","furigana":"にもつ|はいたつ"},{"japanese":"新聞は毎朝五時ごろ配達されます。","romaji":"Shinbun wa maiasa goji goro haitatsu saremasu.","german":"The newspaper is delivered around five o'clock every morning.","furigana":"しんぶん|まいあさごじ|はいたつ"}],"notes":"配達する means to bring goods or mail to the recipient: 新聞を配達する, ピザの配達, 郵便配達; the person is the 配達員. From the customer's point of view one says 配達してもらう, from the goods' point of view 配達される. More general is 届ける \"to bring, to deliver\"; 送る is \"sending\" from the sender's side, and 配送 is more the logistical delivery.","levelNote":"Derived from 配達 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"伝達ミスで、ピンクの車になってしまったんです。","romaji":"Dentatsu misu de, pinku no kuruma ni natte shimatta n desu.","german":"Because of a miscommunication, it turned out to be a pink car.","furigana":"でんたつ|くるま"},{"japanese":"情報の伝達が大切です。","romaji":"Jōhō no dentatsu ga taisetsu desu.","german":"The transmission of information is important.","furigana":"じょうほう|でんたつ|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"部長の指示を、チームのみんなに伝達しました。","romaji":"Buchou no shiji o, chiimu no minna ni dentatsu shimashita.","german":"I passed the manager's instructions on to everyone on the team.","furigana":"ぶちょう|しじ|でんたつ"}],"notes":"伝達 is the formal passing on of information, instructions or signals, especially in companies and organizations: 情報伝達, 伝達事項 \"announcements\", 伝達ミス \"communication error\". As a する-verb: 指示を伝達する. In everyday speech people simply say 伝える. In biology and technology it also denotes the transmission of signals (神経の伝達).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"まずは静止画をアップします。","romaji":"Mazu wa seishiga o appu shimasu.","german":"First, I'll upload the still images.","furigana":"せいしが"},{"japanese":"カメラの前で静止してください。","romaji":"Kamera no mae de seishi shite kudasai.","german":"Please hold still in front of the camera.","furigana":"まえ|せいし"},{"japanese":"車が完全に静止してから、ドアを開けてください。","romaji":"Kuruma ga kanzen ni seishi shite kara, doa o akete kudasai.","german":"Please open the door only after the car has come to a complete stop.","furigana":"くるま|かんぜん|せいし|あ"}],"notes":"静止 is a rather literary, technical word for \"standstill, rest\"; as a する-verb it is intransitive: 静止する \"to stand still, come to a standstill\". Well known are 静止画 \"still image\" (opposite 動画) and 静止衛星 \"geostationary satellite\". In everyday speech people say 止まる or じっとする; 静止 is heard more in announcements, instructions and technology.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"こんなにカワイイ猫たちがいっぱいだと、平静を保てる自信がありません。","romaji":"Konnani kawaii nekotachi ga ippai da to, heisei o tamoteru jishin ga arimasen.","german":"With so many cute cats like this, I'm not confident I can keep my composure.","furigana":"ねこ|へいせい|たも|じしん"},{"japanese":"平静を保ってください。","romaji":"Heisei o tamotte kudasai.","german":"Please stay calm.","furigana":"へいせい|たも"},{"japanese":"彼は平静な顔で、うそをつきました。","romaji":"Kare wa heisei na kao de, uso o tsukimashita.","german":"He lied with a perfectly calm expression.","furigana":"かれ|へいせい|かお"}],"notes":"平静 means \"calm, composure, self-possession\" and is mostly used as a noun: 平静を保つ \"to keep calm\", 平静を失う \"to lose one's composure\"; as a な-adjective 平静な態度 / 平静な顔. It sounds somewhat elevated. In everyday speech one rather says 落ち着く; 冷静, by contrast, emphasizes a cool, level head. Do not confuse it with the identically read era name 平成.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は全人類の希望です。","romaji":"Kare wa zen jinrui no kibō desu.","german":"He is the hope of all humankind.","furigana":"かれ|ぜんじんるい|きぼう"},{"japanese":"人類の未来について考えます。","romaji":"Jinrui no mirai ni tsuite kangaemasu.","german":"I think about the future of humankind.","furigana":"じんるい|みらい|かんが"},{"japanese":"人類が初めて月に行ったのは、1969年です。","romaji":"Jinrui ga hajimete tsuki ni itta no wa, senkyuuhyaku rokujuukyuu nen desu.","german":"Humans first went to the moon in 1969.","furigana":"じんるい|はじ|つき|い|ねん"}],"notes":"人類 is humankind as a whole or as a biological species: 人類の歴史, 人類の未来, 全人類. It sounds matter-of-fact to solemn. 人間, by contrast, means the individual human or people in general, and 人々 \"people\". The science of humankind is called 人類学.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"それ、親類の会社なんです。","romaji":"Sore, shinrui no kaisha na n desu.","german":"That's a relative's company.","furigana":"しんるい|かいしゃ"},{"japanese":"お正月に親類が集まります。","romaji":"Oshōgatsu ni shinrui ga atsumarimasu.","german":"Relatives gather at New Year.","furigana":"しょうがつ|しんるい|あつ"},{"japanese":"あの人は、私の遠い親類にあたります。","romaji":"Ano hito wa, watashi no tooi shinrui ni atarimasu.","german":"That person is a distant relative of mine.","furigana":"ひと|わたし|とお|しんるい"}],"notes":"親類 means \"relatives, kin\" and is somewhat more formal than 親戚, which is much more common in everyday speech. Typical: 遠い親類 \"distant relatives\", 親類が集まる, 親類の家. In fixed expressions such as 親類縁者 \"relatives and kin\" only 親類 occurs. For one's own immediate family, 家族 is used.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"説明書通りにやれば大丈夫ですよ。","romaji":"Setsumeisho doori ni yareba daijoubu desu yo.","german":"If you follow the instructions, you'll be fine.","furigana":"せつめいしょどお|だいじょうぶ"},{"japanese":"説明書を読んでから使ってください。","romaji":"Setsumeisho o yonde kara tsukatte kudasai.","german":"Please read the manual before using it.","furigana":"せつめいしょ|よ|つか"},{"japanese":"薬の説明書に、一日三回飲むと書いてあります。","romaji":"Kusuri no setsumeisho ni, ichinichi sankai nomu to kaite arimasu.","german":"The medicine's instruction sheet says to take it three times a day.","furigana":"くすり|せつめいしょ|いちにちさんかいの|か"}],"notes":"説明書 is a written instruction or description that comes with a product: 説明書を読む, 説明書どおりに組み立てる. For appliances it is more precisely 取扱説明書, colloquially 取説; for medicines it is the package insert. Work instructions within a company are more often called マニュアル.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 34"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この町では、今でも列車のことを「汽車」と言う人がいます。","romaji":"Kono machi de wa, ima demo ressha no koto o 'kisha' to iu hito ga imasu.","german":"In this town, some people still call trains \"kisha\".","furigana":"まち|いま|れっしゃ|きしゃ|い|ひと"},{"japanese":"昔は汽車で旅をしました。","romaji":"Mukashi wa kisha de tabi o shimashita.","german":"In the past, I traveled by steam train.","furigana":"むかし|きしゃ|たび"},{"japanese":"週末だけ、観光用の汽車が走っています。","romaji":"Shuumatsu dake, kankouyou no kisha ga hashitte imasu.","german":"A sightseeing steam train runs only on weekends.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|かんこうよう|きしゃ|はし"}],"notes":"汽車 originally meant the steam train and today sounds old-fashioned or nostalgic, for example in children's songs and memories. In regions without electrified lines (such as Hokkaido or Shikoku), older people still call diesel trains 汽車 too. The normal expression otherwise is 電車, or 列車 in general. Do not confuse with the homophone 記者 \"journalist\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 36"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大島へは、東海汽船の船で行けます。","romaji":"Ooshima e wa, Toukai Kisen no fune de ikemasu.","german":"You can get to Oshima on a Tokai Kisen ship.","furigana":"おおしま|とうかいきせん|ふね|い"},{"japanese":"港に大きな汽船が停まっています。","romaji":"Minato ni ōkina kisen ga tomatte imasu.","german":"A large steamship is docked in the harbor.","furigana":"みなと|おお|きせん|と"},{"japanese":"1853年に、アメリカの汽船が日本に来ました。","romaji":"Senhappyaku gojuusan nen ni, Amerika no kisen ga Nihon ni kimashita.","german":"In 1853, an American steamship came to Japan.","furigana":"ねん|きせん|にほん|き"}],"notes":"汽船 is the steamship; today the word belongs mainly to historical contexts (the arrival of American steamships in 1853) and lives on in the names of shipping companies, e.g. 東海汽船. In daily life people simply say 船, or フェリー for car ferries. The counterpart on rails is 汽車.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 36"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"火曜日の夜に、料理教室に通っています。","romaji":"Kayōbi no yoru ni, ryōri kyōshitsu ni kayotte imasu.","german":"On Tuesday evenings I attend a cooking class.","furigana":"かようび|よる|りょうりきょうしつ|かよ"},{"japanese":"火曜日に病院に行きます。","romaji":"Kayōbi ni byōin ni ikimasu.","german":"I go to the hospital on Tuesday.","furigana":"かようび|びょういん|い"}],"notes":"火曜日 is Tuesday. 火曜日の夜 connects the day and the evening with の. 通っています describes the regular attendance of a cooking class here. 火曜日に gives a single Tuesday appointment. かようび contains a long o and is written kayōbi.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ようやく金曜日です。","romaji":"Yōyaku kin’yōbi desu.","german":"It's finally Friday.","furigana":"きんようび"},{"japanese":"金曜日の夜は映画を見ます。","romaji":"Kin’yōbi no yoru wa eiga o mimasu.","german":"On Friday evening I watch a movie.","furigana":"きんようび|よる|えいが|み"}],"notes":"金曜日 is Friday. ようやく金曜日です expresses relief that this day has finally arrived. 金曜日の夜 means Friday evening. The n before y is separated by an apostrophe in romanization: kin'yōbi. よう contains a long o.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"金曜の夜の七時に三人で予約って可能ですか？","romaji":"Kin'you no yoru no shichiji ni sannin de yoyaku tte kanou desu ka?","german":"Is it possible to make a reservation for three people on Friday evening at seven o'clock?","furigana":"きんよう|よる|しちじ|さんにん|よやく|かのう"},{"japanese":"それは可能です。","romaji":"Sore wa kanō desu.","german":"That is possible.","furigana":"かのう"},{"japanese":"このホテルでは、カードでの支払いが可能です。","romaji":"Kono hoteru de wa, kaado de no shiharai ga kanou desu.","german":"Card payment is possible at this hotel.","furigana":"しはら|かのう"}],"notes":"可能 is a な-adjective/noun for \"possible, feasible\" and sounds more formal than できる: 予約は可能ですか, 〜することが可能です, 可能な限り \"as far as possible\". The opposite is 不可能. \"Possibility, probability\" is 可能性 (〜の可能性がある). In conversation one usually simply asks できますか.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このフタ、うまく閉まらないんだけど。","romaji":"Kono futa, umaku shimaranai n da kedo.","german":"This lid won't close properly.","furigana":"し"},{"japanese":"お店は十時に閉まります。","romaji":"Omise wa jūji ni shimarimasu.","german":"The shop closes at ten o'clock.","furigana":"みせ|じゅうじ|し"}],"notes":"閉まる is an intransitive godan verb: 閉まります, 閉まって. A lid or door closes; a shop shuts. With が you name the thing that closes. 閉める, by contrast, describes actively closing an object. 閉まらないんだけど reports a problem in casual speech.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 18"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コウイチ達、一体どこに行ったんだろう。","romaji":"Kōichi-tachi, ittai doko ni itta n darō.","german":"Where on earth did Koichi and the others go?","furigana":"たち|いったい|い"},{"japanese":"子供達が遊んでいます。","romaji":"Kodomotachi ga asonde imasu.","german":"The children are playing.","furigana":"こどもたち|あそ"}],"notes":"〜達 is usually written as たち and mainly marks a group of people or animals. 子供達 means \"the children\". After a person's name, on the other hand, it means that person together with others: コウイチ達 means \"Koichi and the others\". It is not a plural marker that is automatically required for every noun. The sign 〜 shows the place of the preceding expression and is not pronounced; 達 is the kanji spelling of this suffix.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"なんか警告文が出てきたんだけど…どうしよう？","romaji":"Nanka keikokubun ga dete kita n da kedo... dou shiyou?","german":"Some kind of warning message popped up... what should I do?","furigana":"けいこくぶん|で"},{"japanese":"医者は彼に、たばこをやめるように警告しました。","romaji":"Isha wa kare ni, tabako o yameru you ni keikoku shimashita.","german":"The doctor warned him to quit smoking.","furigana":"いしゃ|かれ|けいこく"},{"japanese":"サッカーの試合で、選手が審判から警告を受けました。","romaji":"Sakkaa no shiai de, senshu ga shinpan kara keikoku o ukemashita.","german":"In the soccer match, a player received a caution from the referee.","furigana":"しあい|せんしゅ|しんぱん|けいこく|う"}],"notes":"警告 is an explicit warning or caution: 警告する, 〜に〜ように警告する, 警告を受ける / 出す. Common are 警告文 \"warning notice\" and 警告音 \"warning sound\"; in sports, 警告 is the caution (yellow card). 注意 is milder (\"notice, admonition\"). Official severe-weather warnings, on the other hand, are 警報 (大雨警報) or, weaker, 注意報.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"そろそろプリンが固まるころかな。","romaji":"Sorosoro purin ga katamaru koro ka na.","german":"The pudding should be setting about now.","furigana":"かた"},{"japanese":"セメントが固まりました。","romaji":"Semento ga katamarimashita.","german":"The cement has hardened.","furigana":"かた"},{"japanese":"パソコンが急に固まって、何もできません。","romaji":"Pasokon ga kyuu ni katamatte, nani mo dekimasen.","german":"My computer suddenly froze and I can't do anything.","furigana":"きゅう|かた|なに"}],"notes":"固まる is intransitive: something becomes hard or firm by itself (セメントが固まる, プリンが固まる); the transitive counterpart is 固める \"to harden, firm up\". Figuratively, plans or opinions \"firm up\" (予定が固まる), people huddle together (みんなで固まる) or freeze in fright. Colloquially it is also said of computers and apps that hang: パソコンが固まった.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今はメガネをかけてないので、遠くの物がよく見えません。","romaji":"Ima wa megane o kaketenai node, tooku no mono ga yoku miemasen.","german":"I'm not wearing my glasses now, so I can't see distant things well.","furigana":"いま|とお|もの|み"},{"japanese":"遠くに山が見えます。","romaji":"Tōku ni yama ga miemasu.","german":"I can see mountains in the distance.","furigana":"とお|やま|み"},{"japanese":"遠くから来てくれて、ありがとう。","romaji":"Tooku kara kite kurete, arigatou.","german":"Thank you for coming from far away.","furigana":"とお|き"}],"notes":"遠く is the noun and adverb form of 遠い and means \"the distance, far away\": 遠くに見える, 遠くの町, 遠くへ行く, 遠くから来る. The counterpart is 近く. Before a noun, 遠く needs の (遠くの山), whereas 遠い stands directly before it (遠い山). Figuratively, 遠く及ばない means \"to be far from matching\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 36"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これは日本の子守歌です。","romaji":"Kore wa Nihon no komoriuta desu.","german":"This is a Japanese lullaby.","furigana":"にほん|こもりうた"},{"japanese":"母が子守歌を歌ってくれました。","romaji":"Haha ga komoriuta o utatte kuremashita.","german":"My mother sang me a lullaby.","furigana":"はは|こもりうた|うた"},{"japanese":"先生の声は、まるで子守歌のようで眠くなります。","romaji":"Sensei no koe wa, marude komoriuta no you de nemuku narimasu.","german":"The teacher's voice is just like a lullaby and makes me sleepy.","furigana":"せんせい|こえ|こもりうた|ねむ"}],"notes":"子守歌 is a lullaby; 子守 means \"looking after children\". One says 子守歌を歌う; the spelling 子守唄 is equally common. Jokingly, a monotonous speech or a boring lesson is called 子守歌, because one falls asleep during it. Well-known traditional examples are 江戸の子守歌 or 五木の子守歌.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「食べた」の辞書形は「食べる」です。","romaji":"'Tabeta' no jishokei wa 'taberu' desu.","german":"The dictionary form of 「食べた」 is 「食べる」.","furigana":"た|じしょけい|た"},{"japanese":"動詞の辞書形を覚えましょう。","romaji":"Dōshi no jishokei o oboemashō.","german":"Let's learn the dictionary forms of verbs.","furigana":"どうし|じしょけい|おぼ"},{"japanese":"辞書形に「ことができる」をつけると、「できる」という意味になります。","romaji":"Jishokei ni 'koto ga dekiru' o tsukeru to, 'dekiru' to iu imi ni narimasu.","german":"If you add 「ことができる」 to the dictionary form, it means \"can\".","furigana":"じしょけい|いみ"}],"notes":"辞書形 is a term from teaching Japanese to foreigners: the basic form of a verb as it appears in the dictionary (食べる, 行く). Many patterns attach to it, e.g. 〜ことができる or 〜前に. In Japanese school grammar the same form is called 終止形 or 基本形. Related terms are ます形, て形 and ない形.","levelNote":"Compound: 辞書 (N5) + 形 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"幸いなことに、ちょうど空車のタクシーがやって来ました。","romaji":"Saiwai na koto ni, choudo kuusha no takushii ga yatte kimashita.","german":"Fortunately, an empty taxi came by just then.","furigana":"さいわ|くうしゃ|き"},{"japanese":"幸い、怪我はありませんでした。","romaji":"Saiwai, kega wa arimasen deshita.","german":"Fortunately, there were no injuries.","furigana":"さいわ|けが"},{"japanese":"お返事をいただければ幸いです。","romaji":"Ohenji o itadakereba saiwai desu.","german":"I would be grateful if you could send a reply.","furigana":"へんじ|さいわ"}],"notes":"幸い is used today mainly adverbially: 幸い / 幸いにも / 幸いなことに \"fortunately, luckily\" - somewhat more formal than 運よく or よかったことに. In business letters, 〜ていただければ幸いです \"I would be grateful if ...\" is a set polite request. 不幸中の幸い means \"a blessing in disguise\". For a personal feeling of happiness one says 幸せ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"関係者以外の立ち入りを禁じる。","romaji":"Kankeisha igai no tachiiri o kinjiru.","german":"Entry by anyone other than authorized persons is prohibited.","furigana":"かんけいしゃいがい|た|い|きん"},{"japanese":"この場所では写真撮影を禁じています。","romaji":"Kono basho de wa shashin satsuei o kinjite imasu.","german":"Taking photographs is prohibited in this place.","furigana":"ばしょ|しゃしんさつえい|きん"},{"japanese":"この学校では、アルバイトが禁じられています。","romaji":"Kono gakkou de wa, arubaito ga kinjirarete imasu.","german":"Part-time jobs are prohibited at this school.","furigana":"がっこう|きん"}],"notes":"禁じる \"to prohibit\" is literary and often appears on signs and in rules: 〜を禁じる, especially in the passive 禁じられている \"is prohibited\". The older variant 禁ずる has the same meaning. In everyday speech people say 禁止する, 禁止です or simply だめ. Figuratively it also means \"to suppress feelings\", for example in 涙を禁じ得ない.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"喉頭は、声を出すために大切なところです。","romaji":"Koutou wa, koe o dasu tame ni taisetsu na tokoro desu.","german":"The larynx is an important part for producing the voice.","furigana":"こうとう|こえ|だ|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"祖父は喉頭がんの手術を受けました。","romaji":"Sofu wa koutou gan no shujutsu o ukemashita.","german":"My grandfather had surgery for laryngeal cancer.","furigana":"そふ|こうとう|しゅじゅつ|う"},{"japanese":"医者が小さいカメラで喉頭を見ました。","romaji":"Isha ga chiisai kamera de koutou o mimashita.","german":"The doctor looked at my larynx with a small camera.","furigana":"いしゃ|ちい|こうとう|み"}],"notes":"喉頭 is the medical technical term for the larynx, e.g. in 喉頭がん \"laryngeal cancer\" or 喉頭炎 \"laryngitis\". In everyday speech one simply says のど (のどが痛い). The ear, nose and throat department is called 耳鼻咽喉科 (not 耳鼻喉頭科); the pharynx is 咽頭.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の父は飴細工の名人です。","romaji":"Watashi no chichi wa amezaiku no meijin desu.","german":"My father is a master of candy sculpture.","furigana":"わたし|ちち|あめざいく|めいじん"},{"japanese":"祭りで飴細工を買いました。","romaji":"Matsuri de amezaiku o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a candy sculpture at the festival.","furigana":"まつ|あめざいく|か"},{"japanese":"職人がはさみを使って、あっという間にうさぎの飴細工を作りました。","romaji":"Shokunin ga hasami o tsukatte, atto iu ma ni usagi no amezaiku o tsukurimashita.","german":"The craftsman used scissors to make a rabbit candy sculpture in no time.","furigana":"しょくにん|つか|ま|あめざいく|つく"}],"notes":"飴細工 is a traditional craft: soft, hot sugar mass is shaped into animals and figures with the hands and scissors at lightning speed; you often see it at stalls at temple festivals. 飴細工 denotes both the technique and the finished figure; the craftsman is 飴細工の職人. Figuratively, something that is impressive only on the outside is also called 飴細工.","levelNote":"Compound: 飴 (N5) + 細工 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日子ちゃんが私を苛めるの。","romaji":"Kyouko-chan ga watashi o ijimeru no.","german":"Kyoko is picking on me.","furigana":"きょうこ|わたし|いじ"},{"japanese":"弱い者を苛めてはいけません。","romaji":"Yowai mono o ijimete wa ikemasen.","german":"You must not bully the weak.","furigana":"よわ|もの|いじ"},{"japanese":"弟は学校でいじめられているようです。","romaji":"Otouto wa gakkou de ijimerarete iru you desu.","german":"It seems my younger brother is being bullied at school.","furigana":"おとうと|がっこう"}],"notes":"苛める is almost always written in kana: いじめる \"to bully, to torment, to harass\". It ranges from teasing among children to systematic bullying; the noun いじめ \"bullying\" is a major social issue. Typical: 〜をいじめる, passive いじめられる. Harmless teasing is rather からかう. Figuratively: 体をいじめる \"to demand a lot of one's body\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 21"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あなたの初恋ってどんな感じだった？","romaji":"Anata no hatsukoi tte donna kanji datta?","german":"What was your first love like?","furigana":"はつこい|かん"},{"japanese":"初恋の思い出は忘れません。","romaji":"Hatsukoi no omoide wa wasuremasen.","german":"I won't forget the memories of my first love.","furigana":"はつこい|おも|で|わす"},{"japanese":"同窓会で、初恋の人と二十年ぶりに会いました。","romaji":"Dousoukai de, hatsukoi no hito to nijuunen buri ni aimashita.","german":"At a class reunion, I met my first love for the first time in twenty years.","furigana":"どうそうかい|はつこい|ひと|にじゅうねん|あ"}],"notes":"初恋 is the first love, both the experience and the person: 初恋の人 / 初恋の相手 \"my first love\". One says 初恋をする or speaks of 初恋の思い出. A well-known saying is 初恋は実らない \"First love never comes true\". To fall in love in general is 恋をする or 好きになる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"やばい、めっちゃドキドキする。","romaji":"Yabai, metcha dokidoki suru.","german":"Wow, my heart is pounding like crazy."},{"japanese":"告白する前にドキドキしました。","romaji":"Kokuhaku suru mae ni dokidoki shimashita.","german":"My heart was pounding before I confessed.","furigana":"こくはく|まえ"},{"japanese":"走ったあとで、胸がドキドキしています。","romaji":"Hashitta ato de, mune ga dokidoki shite imasu.","german":"My heart is pounding after running.","furigana":"はし|むね"}],"notes":"ドキドキ imitates the pounding of the heart and describes excitement, nervousness, fear or being in love: ドキドキする, 胸がドキドキする. It is also written in hiragana as どきどき. ワクワク, by contrast, emphasizes joyful anticipation, and ハラハラ anxious watching when something might go wrong. As an adverb: ドキドキしながら待つ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"五感のうちどれか一つを失ってしまうとしたら、どれを選びますか？また、その理由も教えてください。","romaji":"Gokan no uchi dore ka hitotsu o ushinatte shimau to shitara, dore o erabimasu ka? Mata, sono riyū mo oshiete kudasai.","german":"If you had to lose one of the five senses, which would you choose? Please also tell me why.","furigana":"ごかん|ひと|うしな|えら|りゆう|おし"},{"japanese":"五感を使って料理を楽しみます。","romaji":"Gokan o tsukatte ryōri o tanoshimimasu.","german":"I enjoy cooking using all five senses.","furigana":"ごかん|つか|りょうり|たの"},{"japanese":"年を取ると、五感が少しずつ弱くなります。","romaji":"Toshi o toru to, gokan ga sukoshi zutsu yowaku narimasu.","german":"As you get older, your five senses gradually weaken.","furigana":"とし|と|ごかん|すこ|よわ"}],"notes":"五感 are the five senses: 視覚 (sight), 聴覚 (hearing), 嗅覚 (smell), 味覚 (taste) and 触覚 (touch). Typical are 五感を使う, 五感で楽しむ \"to enjoy with all the senses\" - popular in advertising and travel brochures. The \"sixth sense\", intuition, is called 第六感.","levelNote":"Compound: 感 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"政治家達の二枚舌にはもうウンザリだよ。","romaji":"Seijikatachi no nimaijita ni wa mō unzari da yo.","german":"I'm sick and tired of the politicians' double-talk.","furigana":"せいじかたち|にまいじた"},{"japanese":"二枚舌を使う人は信用できません。","romaji":"Nimaijita o tsukau hito wa shin'yō dekimasen.","german":"You can't trust someone who is double-tongued.","furigana":"にまいじた|つか|ひと|しんよう"},{"japanese":"彼は上司と部下に違うことを言う二枚舌の男だ。","romaji":"Kare wa jōshi to buka ni chigau koto o iu nimaijita no otoko da.","german":"He is a two-faced man who tells his boss and his subordinates different things.","furigana":"かれ|じょうし|ぶか|ちが|い|にまいじた|おとこ"}],"notes":"二枚舌 literally means \"two tongues\" and refers to someone who tells different people contradictory things or does not keep promises. The fixed phrase is 二枚舌を使う (\"to speak with a forked tongue\"); attributively: 二枚舌の政治家, 二枚舌外交. It is clearly derogatory; similar are 裏表がある (\"to have two faces\") and 嘘つき (\"liar\"), though 二枚舌 specifically stresses the contradiction.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの試合はまれに見る乱戦だった。","romaji":"Ano shiai wa mare ni miru ransen datta.","german":"That game was a rarely seen free-for-all.","furigana":"しあい|み|らんせん"},{"japanese":"試合は乱戦になりました。","romaji":"Shiai wa ransen ni narimashita.","german":"The game turned into a wild fight.","furigana":"しあい|らんせん"},{"japanese":"今度の選挙は候補者が多くて、乱戦になりそうです。","romaji":"Kondo no senkyo wa kouhosha ga ookute, ransen ni narisou desu.","german":"The next election has many candidates, so it looks like it will be a wide-open race.","furigana":"こんど|せんきょ|こうほしゃ|おお|らんせん"}],"notes":"乱戦 is a chaotic fight, a melee in which everyone fights in confusion. In sports reporting it refers to a wild game with many goals or constant changes of lead (試合が乱戦になる); in elections, an open race with many candidates. The word is rather journalistic and literary. A simple fight is 戦い, a close-quarters man-to-man fight rather 接近戦.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"雑誌が、有名人の乱交パーティーについて書いていました。","romaji":"Zasshi ga, yuumeijin no rankou paatii ni tsuite kaite imashita.","german":"A magazine wrote about a celebrity orgy party.","furigana":"ざっし|ゆうめいじん|らんこう|か"},{"japanese":"乱交パーティーを開いた男たちが、警察に捕まりました。","romaji":"Rankou paatii o hiraita otokotachi ga, keisatsu ni tsukamarimashita.","german":"The men who held an orgy party were arrested by the police.","furigana":"らんこう|ひら|おとこ|けいさつ|つか"}],"notes":"乱交 refers to sexual promiscuity with changing partners, or group sex; it is most often encountered in 乱交パーティー \"sex party\" in tabloid media and police reports. The word is crude and matter-of-fact and does not belong in everyday conversation. As a する-verb it is intransitive, but it is rarely used that way. The similar-looking 乱戦, by contrast, means \"melee\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私はこのコミックの別冊を集めています。","romaji":"Watashi wa kono komikku no bessatsu o atsumete imasu.","german":"I'm collecting the special editions of this comic.","furigana":"わたし|べっさつ|あつ"},{"japanese":"雑誌の別冊を買いました。","romaji":"Zasshi no bessatsu o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a special issue of the magazine.","furigana":"ざっし|べっさつ|か"},{"japanese":"問題の答えは、別冊にのっています。","romaji":"Mondai no kotae wa, bessatsu ni notte imasu.","german":"The answers to the questions are in the separate booklet.","furigana":"もんだい|こた|べっさつ"}],"notes":"別冊 is a separate volume or supplement: 別冊付録 \"enclosed special booklet\" of a magazine, 別冊 as a special edition of a magazine (also in titles of manga magazines such as 別冊マーガレット). In textbooks and workbooks, the solutions are often in the 別冊: 答えは別冊にあります.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あんないい加減な人、見たことありません。","romaji":"Anna iikagen na hito, mita koto arimasen.","german":"I've never seen such an irresponsible person.","furigana":"かげん|ひと|み"},{"japanese":"いい加減な仕事はしないでください。","romaji":"Iikagen na shigoto wa shinaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't do sloppy work.","furigana":"かげん|しごと"},{"japanese":"もう、いい加減にしなさい！","romaji":"Mou, iikagen ni shinasai!","german":"Come on, that's enough!","furigana":"かげん"}],"notes":"いい加減 as a na-adjective means \"sloppy, careless, irresponsible\": いい加減な人, いい加減な仕事, いい加減な返事. Very common is the exclamation いい加減にしなさい / いい加減にして \"That's enough now!\". As an adverb it means \"quite, really getting to the point of\": いい加減疲れた. Similarly derogatory, 適当 is used colloquially. The literal meaning \"just right\" is nowadays heard almost only in 湯加減 \"bath water temperature\" (いい湯加減).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"さすがにこの年なら善悪は分かるでしょ。","romaji":"Sasuga ni kono toshi nara zen'aku wa wakaru desho.","german":"At this age, surely you know right from wrong.","furigana":"とし|ぜんあく|わ"},{"japanese":"善悪の判断ができます。","romaji":"Zen'aku no handan ga dekimasu.","german":"I can tell right from wrong.","furigana":"ぜんあく|はんだん"},{"japanese":"子どもに善悪を教えるのは親の仕事です。","romaji":"Kodomo ni zen'aku o oshieru no wa oya no shigoto desu.","german":"Teaching children right and wrong is the parents' job.","furigana":"こ|ぜんあく|おし|おや|しごと"}],"notes":"善悪 means \"good and evil\" or \"right and wrong\" and is a rather literary, abstract word. Typical are 善悪の判断 \"moral judgment\", 善悪を区別する or 善悪が分かる \"to be able to tell good from evil\". In casual conversation people often say the simpler いいことと悪いこと. It is used almost only as a noun with の, を or が.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"集団で行動するのが苦手なんです。","romaji":"Shūdan de kōdō suru no ga nigate na n desu.","german":"I'm not good at acting in a group.","furigana":"しゅうだん|こうどう|にがて"},{"japanese":"小学生は毎朝集団で学校に行きます。","romaji":"Shougakusei wa maiasa shuudan de gakkou ni ikimasu.","german":"Elementary school students go to school in groups every morning.","furigana":"しょうがくせい|まいあさしゅうだん|がっこう|い"},{"japanese":"寮での集団生活にはもう慣れました。","romaji":"Ryou de no shuudan seikatsu ni wa mou naremashita.","german":"I've already gotten used to communal life in the dormitory.","furigana":"りょう|しゅうだんせいかつ|な"}],"notes":"集団 is a (usually larger) group or mass of people or animals and sounds more matter-of-fact than グループ. Typical are 集団で行動する \"to act as a group\", 集団生活 \"communal life\" (school, dormitory, army) and 集団登校 \"going to school together in groups\". An organized group with a common purpose, for example for trips, is rather 団体 (団体旅行). In everyday speech, for groups of friends one simply says グループ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このチームには、入団テストを受けて合格した人だけが入団できます。","romaji":"Kono chīmu ni wa, nyūdan tesuto o ukete gōkaku shita hito dake ga nyūdan dekimasu.","german":"Only people who pass the entrance test can join this team.","furigana":"にゅうだん|う|ごうかく|ひと|にゅうだん"},{"japanese":"サッカーチームに入団しました。","romaji":"Sakkā chīmu ni nyūdan shimashita.","german":"I joined a soccer team.","furigana":"にゅうだん"},{"japanese":"妹は去年、地元の劇団に入団しました。","romaji":"Imouto wa kyonen, jimoto no gekidan ni nyuudan shimashita.","german":"My younger sister joined a local theater troupe last year.","furigana":"いもうと|きょねん|じもと|げきだん|にゅうだん"}],"notes":"入団する means \"to join a group whose name ends in 団\": sports teams (especially professional teams, 球団), theater troupes (劇団), orchestras (楽団) or scouts. The destination takes に: チームに入団する. The opposite is 退団 \"leaving\". For school clubs, 入部 is said, for companies 入社 and for schools 入学; in daily life, simply 入る also works.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼のお父さんは警官だよ。","romaji":"Kare no otōsan wa keikan da yo.","german":"His father is a police officer.","furigana":"かれ|とう|けいかん"},{"japanese":"警官が交通整理をしています。","romaji":"Keikan ga kōtsū seiri o shite imasu.","german":"A police officer is directing traffic.","furigana":"けいかん|こうつうせいり"}],"notes":"警官 is a noun for a police officer. It can name an occupation with です or だ. 警官が names the person as the one acting. 警官の links the word to a noun. けいかん contains the vowel sequence ei; it is written keikan.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 37"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの男に殺人の容疑がかかっているらしい。","romaji":"Ano otoko ni satsujin no yougi ga kakatte iru rashii.","german":"That man seems to be suspected of murder.","furigana":"おとこ|さつじん|ようぎ"},{"japanese":"容疑者が逮捕されました。","romaji":"Yōgisha ga taiho saremashita.","german":"The suspect was arrested.","furigana":"ようぎしゃ|たいほ"},{"japanese":"その男は容疑を認めているそうです。","romaji":"Sono otoko wa yougi o mitomete iru sou desu.","german":"I hear the man admits to the charges.","furigana":"おとこ|ようぎ|みと"}],"notes":"容疑 is \"suspicion of a crime\" in the police and legal sense and is typical of news language. Set patterns are Xの容疑で逮捕される \"to be arrested on suspicion of X\", Xに容疑がかかる \"to be under suspicion\", 容疑を認める／否認する \"to admit/deny the charges\". 容疑者 is the \"suspect\". For everyday suspecting or distrust, 疑い or the verb 疑う is used instead.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"弟は今日は青いズボンを履いています。","romaji":"Otōto wa kyō wa aoi zubon o haite imasu.","german":"My younger brother is wearing blue pants today.","furigana":"おとうと|きょう|あお|は"},{"japanese":"新しい靴を履きました。","romaji":"Atarashii kutsu o hakimashita.","german":"I put on new shoes.","furigana":"あたら|くつ|は"},{"japanese":"部屋に入る前に、ここでスリッパをはいてください。","romaji":"Heya ni hairu mae ni, koko de surippa o haite kudasai.","german":"Please put on slippers here before entering the room.","furigana":"へや|はい|まえ"}],"notes":"履く is used for shoes and clothing on the lower body, such as pants; the kana spelling はく is also possible. For pants and skirts you also see 穿く, while 履く is especially used for shoes. This transitive godan verb forms 履きます and 履いて, read hakimasu and haite. 履いている describes the state of wearing. With を you name the garment. For a shirt you use 着る; the identically read 掃く means \"to sweep\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"わかめスープを一つください。","romaji":"Wakame sūpu o hitotsu kudasai.","german":"Could I have one wakame soup, please?","furigana":"ひと"},{"japanese":"みそ汁にわかめを入れます。","romaji":"Misoshiru ni wakame o iremasu.","german":"I put wakame in the miso soup.","furigana":"しる|い"},{"japanese":"乾燥わかめは水で戻してから使います。","romaji":"Kansou wakame wa mizu de modoshite kara tsukaimasu.","german":"Dried wakame is soaked in water before use.","furigana":"かんそう|みず|もど|つか"}],"notes":"わかめ is an edible brown seaweed and a standard ingredient in みそ汁, salads and soups. The word is almost always written in hiragana (on packages also ワカメ); the kanji 若布 is rarely seen. Usually one buys 乾燥わかめ, which is soaked in water before use. Do not confuse it with 海苔 (dried seaweed sheets for sushi) and 昆布 (kombu for broth).","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"生徒がロボットだったら、宿題もちゃんと出すし、先生も楽だろうな。","romaji":"Seito ga robotto dattara, shukudai mo chanto dasu shi, sensei mo raku darō na.","german":"If the students were robots, they'd hand in their homework properly, and it would probably be easier for the teachers.","furigana":"せいと|しゅくだい|だ|せんせい|らく"},{"japanese":"この学校には五百人の生徒がいます。","romaji":"Kono gakkō ni wa gohyakunin no seito ga imasu.","german":"There are five hundred students at this school.","furigana":"がっこう|ごひゃくにん|せいと"}],"notes":"生徒 is a noun for a pupil or student. 生徒が names them as the subject; 生徒の links them to a noun. 五百人の生徒 gives the number with the person counter 人. だったら can introduce a hypothetical condition. The reading せいと is seito, with no additional な before a noun.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 48"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私たちは仏教徒です。","romaji":"Watashitachi wa bukkyōto desu.","german":"We are Buddhists.","furigana":"わたし|ぶっきょうと"},{"japanese":"日本人の多くは仏教徒です。","romaji":"Nihonjin no ōku wa bukkyōto desu.","german":"Many Japanese people are Buddhists.","furigana":"にほんじん|おお|ぶっきょうと"},{"japanese":"この町には仏教徒もキリスト教徒も住んでいます。","romaji":"Kono machi ni wa bukkyouto mo kirisutokyouto mo sunde imasu.","german":"Both Buddhists and Christians live in this town.","furigana":"まち|ぶっきょうと|きょうと|す"}],"notes":"仏教徒 is made up of 仏教 \"Buddhism\" and 徒 \"follower\" and means \"Buddhist\". 'キリスト教徒' and 'イスラム教徒' are formed the same way. When talking about one's own family, one often rather says うちは仏教です, because many Japanese do not see themselves as strictly religious, although funerals are mostly Buddhist. More general for \"believers\" are 信者 and 信徒.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この宗教には世界中に信徒がいます。","romaji":"Kono shuukyou ni wa sekaijuu ni shinto ga imasu.","german":"This religion has followers all over the world.","furigana":"しゅうきょう|せかいじゅう|しんと"},{"japanese":"教会にはたくさんの信徒がいます。","romaji":"Kyōkai ni wa takusan no shinto ga imasu.","german":"The church has many believers.","furigana":"きょうかい|しんと"},{"japanese":"お寺の信徒が集まって、お祭りの準備をしました。","romaji":"Otera no shinto ga atsumatte, omatsuri no junbi o shimashita.","german":"The temple's followers gathered and prepared for the festival.","furigana":"てら|しんと|あつ|まつ|じゅんび"}],"notes":"信徒 refers to the believers or lay members of a religious community, often as opposed to priests, and sounds rather formal. More common in daily life is 信者, which is also used figuratively for fervent fans. In compounds for individual religions, 教徒 is usually used (仏教徒, キリスト教徒). Do not confuse with 神道 (しんとう, with a long o) \"Shinto\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大学では知識だけでなく、多くの人間関係を得ることもできます。","romaji":"Daigaku de wa chishiki dake denaku, ōku no ningen kankei o eru koto mo dekimasu.","german":"At university you can gain not only knowledge but also many human relationships.","furigana":"だいがく|ちしき|おお|にんげんかんけい|え"},{"japanese":"良い結果を得ることができました。","romaji":"Yoi kekka o eru koto ga dekimashita.","german":"I was able to obtain good results.","furigana":"よ|けっか|え"},{"japanese":"親の許可を得てから、留学を決めました。","romaji":"Oya no kyoka o ete kara, ryuugaku o kimemashita.","german":"I decided on studying abroad only after getting my parents' permission.","furigana":"おや|きょか|え|りゅうがく|き"}],"notes":"得る is a formal, rather literary verb for \"to obtain, to gain, to receive\". Typical objects are 知識, 経験, 情報, 結果 and 許可 (許可を得る \"to obtain permission\"). In everyday speech one usually says もらう or 手に入れる. In written language the dictionary form is also read うる, especially as an auxiliary verb meaning \"to be possible\": あり得る \"to be possible\", but the negative is always あり得ない.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は上司を説得するためにプレゼンをしました。","romaji":"Kanojo wa jōshi o settoku suru tame ni purezen o shimashita.","german":"She gave a presentation to persuade her boss.","furigana":"かのじょ|じょうし|せっとく"},{"japanese":"両親を説得しました。","romaji":"Ryōshin o settoku shimashita.","german":"I persuaded my parents.","furigana":"りょうしん|せっとく"},{"japanese":"彼の話には説得力があります。","romaji":"Kare no hanashi ni wa settokuryoku ga arimasu.","german":"His talk is persuasive.","furigana":"かれ|はなし|せっとくりょく"}],"notes":"説得する means \"to talk someone into or out of something, to convince (with words)\" so that they do or don't do something: 親を説得する, 〜するように説得する. When one is persuaded oneself, the passive 説得される is used. Do not confuse it with 納得する, which means \"to be satisfied oneself, to accept something\". 説得力 is \"persuasiveness\": 説得力がある \"convincing\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"そのプロジェクトは大失敗に終わりました。","romaji":"Sono purojekuto wa daishippai ni owarimashita.","german":"The project ended in a complete failure.","furigana":"だいしっぱい|お"},{"japanese":"プレゼンテーションが大失敗でした。","romaji":"Purezentaashon ga daishippai deshita.","german":"The presentation was a total flop."},{"japanese":"初めてケーキを作ったけど、大失敗しちゃった。","romaji":"Hajimete keeki o tsukutta kedo, daishippai shichatta.","german":"I made a cake for the first time, but it was a total failure.","furigana":"はじ|つく|だいしっぱい"}],"notes":"大失敗 is a \"huge flop, fiasco\" - 失敗 intensified by 大. Typical are 〜は大失敗に終わる \"ended in a fiasco\", 〜が大失敗だった and, as a する-verb, 大失敗する \"to go totally wrong\". In casual conversation one often hears 大失敗しちゃった. For small mistakes, ミス or 間違い is said, not 大失敗.","levelNote":"Compound: 大 (N3) + 失敗 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"そんな事は容易に想像できますよね？","romaji":"Sonna koto wa yōi ni sōzō dekimasu yo ne?","german":"You can easily imagine something like that, right?","furigana":"こと|ようい|そうぞう"},{"japanese":"この問題は容易ではありません。","romaji":"Kono mondai wa yōi de wa arimasen.","german":"This problem is not easy.","furigana":"もんだい|ようい"},{"japanese":"もっと容易な方法はありませんか。","romaji":"Motto youi na houhou wa arimasen ka.","german":"Isn't there an easier method?","furigana":"ようい|ほうほう"}],"notes":"容易 is a formal な-adjective for \"easy, simple\"; in everyday speech one usually says 簡単 or やさしい. Typical are 容易に想像できる \"easily imaginable\" and 容易ではない \"anything but easy\". Note: 用意 \"preparation\" (用意する) is read exactly the same, ようい, but has nothing to do with it.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"暴力には反対です。","romaji":"Bōryoku ni wa hantai desu.","german":"I am against violence.","furigana":"ぼうりょく|はんたい"},{"japanese":"暴力は許されません。","romaji":"Bōryoku wa yurusaremasen.","german":"Violence is not tolerated.","furigana":"ぼうりょく|ゆる"},{"japanese":"彼は人に暴力をふるったことがありません。","romaji":"Kare wa hito ni bouryoku o furutta koto ga arimasen.","german":"He has never used violence against anyone.","furigana":"かれ|ひと|ぼうりょく"}],"notes":"暴力 is (usually physical) \"violence\". To use violence is 暴力をふるう, not 暴力をする. Common compounds are 家庭内暴力 \"domestic violence\" and 暴力団 \"gang, yakuza\". For rough, inconsiderate behavior or speech, on the other hand, the adjective 乱暴な is used.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どこでそんな乱暴な言葉を覚えてきたの？","romaji":"Doko de sonna ranbō na kotoba o oboete kita no?","german":"Where did you learn such rough language?","furigana":"らんぼう|ことば|おぼ"},{"japanese":"荷物を乱暴に扱わないでください。","romaji":"Nimotsu o ranbou ni atsukawanaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't handle the luggage roughly.","furigana":"にもつ|らんぼう|あつか"},{"japanese":"彼は運転が乱暴なので、一緒に乗りたくないです。","romaji":"Kare wa unten ga ranbou na node, issho ni noritakunai desu.","german":"He drives recklessly, so I don't want to ride with him.","furigana":"かれ|うんてん|らんぼう|いっしょ|の"}],"notes":"乱暴 is used mainly as a な-adjective: \"rough, rude, inconsiderate\" - 乱暴な言葉 \"rough language\", 乱暴に扱う \"to handle roughly\", 運転が乱暴だ \"to drive recklessly\". The verb 乱暴する \"to become violent\" is rare and can euphemistically also mean sexual violence, so it is better avoided. The noun for \"violence\" itself is 暴力.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"アイツ最近ちょっと暴走気味だよね。","romaji":"Aitsu saikin chotto bōsōgimi da yo ne.","german":"Der dreht in letzter Zeit ein bisschen durch, oder?","furigana":"さいきん|ぼうそうぎみ"},{"japanese":"車が暴走しました。","romaji":"Kuruma ga bōsō shimashita.","german":"Das Auto ist außer Kontrolle geraten.","furigana":"くるま|ぼうそう"},{"japanese":"夜中に暴走族のバイクの音がうるさかったです。","romaji":"Yonaka ni bousouzoku no baiku no oto ga urusakatta desu.","german":"Mitten in der Nacht war der Lärm der Raser-Motorräder furchtbar laut.","furigana":"よなか|ぼうそうぞく|おと"}],"notes":"暴走する ist intransitiv und heißt „außer Kontrolle geraten, wild losrasen“: von Fahrzeugen (車が暴走する, 暴走車), von Maschinen oder Programmen („durchdrehen“) und übertragen von Menschen, die eigenmächtig über die Stränge schlagen (暴走気味 „etwas zu weit gehen“). 暴走族 sind lärmende Raser- oder Motorradbanden. Mit dem deutschen „Amoklauf“ hat das Wort nichts zu tun.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は梅干しのおにぎりが大好きです。","romaji":"Watashi wa umeboshi no onigiri ga daisuki desu.","german":"I love onigiri with pickled plum.","furigana":"わたし|うめぼ|だいす"},{"japanese":"お弁当に梅干しを入れました。","romaji":"Obentō ni umeboshi o iremashita.","german":"I put a pickled plum in my lunch box.","furigana":"べんとう|うめぼ|い"},{"japanese":"梅干しはすっぱいですが、体にいいそうです。","romaji":"Umeboshi wa suppai desu ga, karada ni ii sou desu.","german":"Pickled plums are sour, but they are said to be good for you.","furigana":"うめぼ|からだ"}],"notes":"梅干し are ume fruits pickled in salt and dried (more a type of apricot than plums), very salty and sour (すっぱい). They are eaten in おにぎり, in the お弁当 (for example one umeboshi in the middle of the rice) or with rice porridge. The second part comes from 干す \"to dry\"; it is also written 梅干. Often just 梅 is said for short: 梅のおにぎり.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"減法の記号は「−」です。","romaji":"Genpou no kigou wa \"−\" desu.","german":"The symbol for subtraction is 「−」.","furigana":"げんぽう|きごう"},{"japanese":"算数で減法を習いました。","romaji":"Sansū de genpō o naraimashita.","german":"I learned subtraction in arithmetic class.","furigana":"さんすう|げんぽう|なら"},{"japanese":"この問題は減法ではなく、加法を使います。","romaji":"Kono mondai wa genpou de wa naku, kahou o tsukaimasu.","german":"This problem calls for addition, not subtraction.","furigana":"もんだい|げんぽう|かほう|つか"}],"notes":"減法 is the mathematical technical term for \"subtraction\" and appears in textbooks alongside 加法 \"addition\", 乗法 \"multiplication\" and 除法 \"division\". In everyday speech and in elementary school lessons one almost always says 引き算 (and 足し算, 掛け算, 割り算). The matching verb is 引く \"to subtract\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"もうすぐ桃の節句なので、ひな人形を出しました。","romaji":"Mōsugu momo no sekku na node, hinaningyō o dashimashita.","german":"The Girls' Festival is coming soon, so I put out the hina dolls.","furigana":"もも|せっく|にんぎょう|だ"},{"japanese":"五月の節句にちまきを食べます。","romaji":"Gogatsu no sekku ni chimaki o tabemasu.","german":"On the Boys' Festival in May, people eat chimaki.","furigana":"ごがつ|せっく|た"},{"japanese":"日本には五つの節句があります。","romaji":"Nihon ni wa itsutsu no sekku ga arimasu.","german":"Japan has five traditional seasonal festivals.","furigana":"にほん|いつ|せっく"}],"notes":"節句 are the traditional seasonal festivals; the five classical ones (五節句) fall on 1.1./7.1., 3.3., 5.5., 7.7. and 9.9. Still alive today are mainly 桃の節句 on March 3 (ひな祭り, the Girls' Festival with hina dolls) and 端午の節句 on May 5 (Boys' Festival, today こどもの日). The word is almost always used as X の節句.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"オフィスの出口から社員が続々と出てきた。","romaji":"Ofisu no deguchi kara shain ga zokuzoku to dete kita.","german":"Employees came streaming out of the office exit one after another.","furigana":"でぐち|しゃいん|ぞくぞく|で"},{"japanese":"お客さんが続々と来ています。","romaji":"Okyakusan ga zokuzoku to kite imasu.","german":"Customers are arriving one after another.","furigana":"きゃく|ぞくぞく|き"},{"japanese":"駅の近くに新しい店が続々とオープンしています。","romaji":"Eki no chikaku ni atarashii mise ga zokuzoku to oopun shite imasu.","german":"New stores are opening one after another near the station.","furigana":"えき|ちか|あたら|みせ|ぞくぞく"}],"notes":"続々(と) means \"one after another, in rapid succession\" and is used with verbs of movement or events such as 来る, 集まる, 出てくる, 届く or オープンする. It emphasizes that many come one after another in a short time, and sounds somewhat like a news report; more neutral and more common is 次々(と). The identically pronounced ぞくぞく in kana, on the other hand, describes a shiver or tingle from cold or excitement.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ごめんなさい。私、猫舌なんです。","romaji":"Gomennasai. Watashi, nekojita na n desu.","german":"Tut mir leid. Ich kann nichts Heißes essen.","furigana":"わたし|ねこじた"},{"japanese":"猫舌の人のために、スープを少し冷ましておきました。","romaji":"Nekojita no hito no tame ni, suupu o sukoshi samashite okimashita.","german":"Für alle, die nichts Heißes vertragen, habe ich die Suppe etwas abkühlen lassen.","furigana":"ねこじた|ひと|すこ|さ"},{"japanese":"猫舌なので、ラーメンを食べるのに時間がかかります。","romaji":"Nekojita na node, raamen o taberu no ni jikan ga kakarimasu.","german":"Weil ich heißes Essen schlecht vertrage, brauche ich lange für eine Schüssel Ramen.","furigana":"ねこじた|た|じかん"}],"notes":"猫舌 („Katzenzunge“) ist ein Nomen und beschreibt jemanden, der sehr heißes Essen oder Getränke nicht verträgt. Typisch ist 私、猫舌なんです oder 猫舌なので熱いものが苦手です. Es geht nur um Hitze im Mund, nicht um allgemeine Hitzeempfindlichkeit (dafür 暑さに弱い). Im Deutschen gibt es kein festes Gegenstück, man umschreibt es.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ここから歌詞が思い出せないんだよね。","romaji":"Koko kara kashi ga omoidasenai n da yo ne.","german":"I can't remember the lyrics from here.","furigana":"かし|おも|だ"},{"japanese":"この歌の歌詞が素敵です。","romaji":"Kono uta no kashi ga suteki desu.","german":"The lyrics of this song are lovely.","furigana":"うた|かし|すてき"},{"japanese":"歌詞を見ながら歌ってもいいですか。","romaji":"Kashi o minagara utatte mo ii desu ka.","german":"May I sing while looking at the lyrics?","furigana":"かし|み|うた"}],"notes":"歌詞 are the \"lyrics\". The counterpart is 曲 \"melody, piece of music\"; correspondingly, 作詞 means \"to write the lyrics\" and 作曲 \"to compose\". Typical are 歌詞を覚える, 歌詞を見ながら歌う and 歌詞カード (the lyrics booklet of a CD). Do not confuse with the homophone お菓子 (かし, \"sweets\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"マイクさんは、どうやって数詞を覚えたんですか？","romaji":"Maikusan wa, dō yatte sūshi o oboeta n desu ka?","german":"How did Mike learn the numerals?","furigana":"すうし|おぼ"},{"japanese":"日本語の数詞は複雑です。","romaji":"Nihongo no sūshi wa fukuzatsu desu.","german":"Japanese numerals are complicated.","furigana":"にほんご|すうし|ふくざつ"},{"japanese":"「三本」の「本」は数詞ではなく、助数詞です。","romaji":"\"Sanbon\" no \"hon\" wa suushi de wa naku, josuushi desu.","german":"The 本 in 「三本」 is not a numeral but a counter.","furigana":"さんぼん|ほん|すうし|じょすうし"}],"notes":"数詞 is the grammatical technical term \"numeral\" (一, 二, 百, ひとつ ...). To be distinguished from it is 助数詞, the \"counter\" or counting unit word such as 本, 枚 or 匹, which is attached to the number. In everyday speech people rather speak of 数 \"number\" or 数字 \"digit\"; 数詞 is encountered mainly in grammar lessons.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は無意識にその曲を口ずさんでいました。","romaji":"Kanojo wa muishiki ni sono kyoku o kuchizusande imashita.","german":"She was humming that song without realizing it.","furigana":"かのじょ|むいしき|きょく|くち"},{"japanese":"無意識のうちにやっていました。","romaji":"Muishiki no uchi ni yatte imashita.","german":"I did it without being aware of it.","furigana":"むいしき"},{"japanese":"考えごとをしていたら、無意識に家の前まで来ていました。","romaji":"Kangaegoto o shite itara, muishiki ni ie no mae made kite imashita.","german":"I was lost in thought, and before I knew it I had arrived in front of my house.","furigana":"かんが|むいしき|いえ|まえ|き"}],"notes":"無意識 is mostly used adverbially as 無意識に or 無意識のうちに \"unconsciously, without noticing\" and attributively with の: 無意識の行動, 無意識のくせ. The opposite is 意識的に \"consciously, deliberately\". For \"unconscious\" in the medical sense, everyday speech rather says 意識がない or 意識不明. In psychology, 無意識 also means \"the unconscious\".","levelNote":"Compound: 無 (N3) + 意識 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"社長は私財を使って会社を助けました。","romaji":"Shachou wa shizai o tsukatte kaisha o tasukemashita.","german":"The company president helped the company with his private funds.","furigana":"しゃちょう|しざい|つか|かいしゃ|たす"},{"japanese":"私財を投じて学校を建てました。","romaji":"Shizai o tōjite gakkō o tatemashita.","german":"He used his own fortune to build a school.","furigana":"しざい|とう|がっこう|た"},{"japanese":"会社のお金と私財は、きちんと分けてください。","romaji":"Kaisha no okane to shizai wa, kichinto wakete kudasai.","german":"Please keep company money and private funds strictly separate.","furigana":"かいしゃ|かね|しざい|わ"}],"notes":"私財 is a person's \"private property, own money\", as opposed to company or public funds. The word is formal and often appears in the set phrase 私財を投じる \"to invest one's own fortune\", usually for a charitable purpose. In everyday speech one simply says 自分のお金 or 財産.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"返せるかどうか分からないのに、借財をするのはよくありません。","romaji":"Kaeseru ka dou ka wakaranai noni, shakuzai o suru no wa yoku arimasen.","german":"It is not good to take on debt when you don't know whether you can repay it.","furigana":"かえ|わ|しゃくざい"},{"japanese":"借財を返済しなければなりません。","romaji":"Shakuzai o hensai shinakereba narimasen.","german":"The debt must be repaid.","furigana":"しゃくざい|へんさい"},{"japanese":"店がうまくいかなくて、大きな借財が残りました。","romaji":"Mise ga umaku ikanakute, ookina shakuzai ga nokorimashita.","german":"The shop didn't do well, and a large debt was left behind.","furigana":"みせ|おお|しゃくざい|のこ"}],"notes":"借財 is a literary, somewhat old-fashioned word for \"debt, loan\". In everyday speech one almost always says 借金. Typical are 借財をする \"to take on debt\", 借財を返す／返済する \"to repay debt\" and 借財が残る \"debt remains\". In casual speech, 借財 therefore sounds stiff.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この道では事故が連続して起きています。","romaji":"Kono michi de wa jiko ga renzoku shite okite imasu.","german":"Accidents keep happening one after another on this road.","furigana":"みち|じこ|れんぞく|お"},{"japanese":"三日連続で雨が降りました。","romaji":"Mikka renzoku de ame ga furimashita.","german":"It rained for three days in a row.","furigana":"みっかれんぞく|あめ|ふ"},{"japanese":"毎朝、連続ドラマを見るのが楽しみです。","romaji":"Maiasa, renzoku dorama o miru no ga tanoshimi desu.","german":"I look forward to the TV series every morning.","furigana":"まいあさ|れんぞく|み|たの"}],"notes":"連続 means \"succession, series\". Very common is number + time unit + 連続(で): 三日連続で \"three days in a row\", 二年連続優勝 \"champion two years in a row\". As a verb, 連続する is mostly intransitive: 事故が連続している \"accidents are piling up\". Compounds include 連続ドラマ \"TV series\". More neutral for \"to go on, to continue\" is 続く.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このイベントでは、赤ちゃんを連れた人がほとんどでした。","romaji":"Kono ibento de wa, akachan o tsureta hito ga hotondo deshita.","german":"Most people at this event were carrying babies.","furigana":"あか|つ|ひと"},{"japanese":"子供を公園に連れて行きます。","romaji":"Kodomo o kōen ni tsurete ikimasu.","german":"I take my child to the park.","furigana":"こども|こうえん|つ|い"},{"japanese":"友達を家に連れて来てもいい？","romaji":"Tomodachi o ie ni tsurete kite mo ii?","german":"Is it okay to bring a friend to the house?","furigana":"ともだち|いえ|つ|き"}],"notes":"連れる means \"to take someone along, to be accompanied by\" and takes only people or animals as its object. It almost always appears in the て-form: 連れて行く \"to take (there)\", 連れて来る \"to bring (here)\", 連れて帰る \"to take home\". Attributively one says 子どもを連れた人 \"people with a child\". For objects, 持って行く／持って来る is used instead.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの財閥は戦前、日本経済を支配していた。","romaji":"Ano zaibatsu wa senzen, Nihon keizai o shihai shite ita.","german":"That zaibatsu dominated the Japanese economy before the war.","furigana":"ざいばつ|せんぜん|にほんけいざい|しはい"},{"japanese":"戦後、GHQの指令で財閥は解体された。","romaji":"Sengo, GHQ no shirei de zaibatsu wa kaitai sareta.","german":"After the war, the zaibatsu were dissolved on the orders of GHQ.","furigana":"せんご|しれい|ざいばつ|かいたい"},{"japanese":"韓国では財閥系の大企業が経済の中心を担っている。","romaji":"Kankoku de wa zaibatsu-kei no daikigyō ga keizai no chūshin o ninatte iru.","german":"In South Korea, large chaebol-affiliated companies are at the center of the economy.","furigana":"かんこく|ざいばつけい|だいきぎょう|けいざい|ちゅうしん|にな"}],"notes":"財閥 refers to the large conglomerates controlled by a family through a holding company, such as 三井, 三菱 or 住友, which dominated the Japanese economy before 1945; after the war they were broken up in the 財閥解体 (\"dissolution of the zaibatsu\"). Their successors are today more often called 企業グループ or 系列. The word is still alive for the Korean chaebol (韓国の財閥) and colloquially for \"filthy-rich people\": 財閥の御曹司 (\"son of a rich family\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"順位ってそんなに大事？","romaji":"Jun'i tte sonnani daiji?","german":"Is ranking really that important?","furigana":"じゅんい|だいじ"},{"japanese":"テストの順位が発表されました。","romaji":"Tesuto no jun'i ga happyō saremashita.","german":"The test rankings were announced.","furigana":"じゅんい|はっぴょう"},{"japanese":"リーグでのチームの順位が三位に上がりました。","romaji":"Riigu de no chiimu no jun'i ga san'i ni agarimashita.","german":"The team's position in the league rose to third place.","furigana":"じゅんい|さんい|あ"}],"notes":"順位 is the \"placement, rank\" in a competition or ranking. Typical are 順位が上がる／下がる, 順位をつける \"to make a ranking\" and 優先順位 \"priority\". The concrete place is given with 〜位: 一位, 三位. For a simple order, for example when queuing, 順番 is used instead.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"荷物は大きい順に車に積みましょう。","romaji":"Nimotsu wa ookii jun ni kuruma ni tsumimashou.","german":"Let's load the luggage into the car in order of size, largest first.","furigana":"にもつ|おお|じゅん|くるま|つ"},{"japanese":"数字を大きい順に並べてください。","romaji":"Sūji o ōkiijun ni narabete kudasai.","german":"Please arrange the numbers from largest to smallest.","furigana":"すうじ|おお|じゅん|なら"},{"japanese":"ファイルをサイズの大きい順に並べ替えました。","romaji":"Fairu o saizu no ookii jun ni narabekaemashita.","german":"I sorted the files by size, largest first.","furigana":"おお|じゅん|なら|か"}],"notes":"大きい順 is a productive formation from adjective + 順 \"order\": \"in order of size, from the largest to the smallest\" or, for numbers, \"descending\". It mostly takes に and verbs such as 並べる, 並ぶ or 並べ替える; the criterion comes before it with の: サイズの大きい順. The counterpart is 小さい順; formed in the same way are 高い順, 安い順, 多い順, 新しい順 and 五十音順.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"答えの数字は、小さい順に書くのがルールです。","romaji":"Kotae no sūji wa, chiisai jun ni kaku no ga rūru desu.","german":"The rule is to write the answer numbers from smallest to largest.","furigana":"こた|すうじ|ちい|じゅん|か"},{"japanese":"小さい順に番号をつけました。","romaji":"Chiisaijun ni bangō o tsukemashita.","german":"I numbered them from smallest to largest.","furigana":"ちい|じゅん|ばんごう"},{"japanese":"写真を撮るので、背の小さい順に前から並んでください。","romaji":"Shashin o toru node, se no chiisai jun ni mae kara narande kudasai.","german":"We're taking a photo, so please line up from the front, shortest first.","furigana":"しゃしん|と|せ|ちい|じゅん|まえ|なら"}],"notes":"小さい順 means \"in ascending order of size, starting from the smallest\" or, for numbers, \"ascending\". As with 大きい順, it takes に and verbs such as 並べる, 並ぶ or 書く; the criterion comes before it with の: 背の小さい順. With children, 小さい can also mean \"young\": 小さい順 \"the smallest (youngest) first\". For body height, 背の低い順 is somewhat more correct.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"席順はあいうえお順にしました。","romaji":"Sekijun wa aiueo jun ni shimashita.","german":"The seating order was set in the order of the Japanese syllabary.","furigana":"せきじゅん|じゅん"},{"japanese":"席順を決めましょう。","romaji":"Sekijun o kimemashō.","german":"Let's decide the seating arrangement.","furigana":"せきじゅん|き"},{"japanese":"結婚式の席順を考えるのは大変です。","romaji":"Kekkonshiki no sekijun o kangaeru no wa taihen desu.","german":"Planning the seating arrangement for a wedding is difficult.","furigana":"けっこんしき|せきじゅん|かんが|たいへん"}],"notes":"席順 is the \"seating order\" at celebrations, meetings or in a classroom: 席順を決める, 席順表 \"seating chart\". On formal occasions it often reflects the hierarchy (seat of honor 上座, lower seat 下座). Do not confuse it with 席替え, the regular swapping of seats in Japanese school classes.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このミュージアムでは、アーティストの作品が年代順にディスプレイされています。","romaji":"Kono myūjiamu de wa, ātisuto no sakuhin ga nendaijun ni disupurei sarete imasu.","german":"In this museum, the artists' works are displayed in chronological order.","furigana":"さくひん|ねんだいじゅん"},{"japanese":"写真を年代順に並べました。","romaji":"Shashin o nendaijun ni narabemashita.","german":"I arranged the photos in chronological order.","furigana":"しゃしん|ねんだいじゅん|なら"},{"japanese":"年代順の一覧を作ってください。","romaji":"Nendaijun no ichiran o tsukutte kudasai.","german":"Please make a list in chronological order.","furigana":"ねんだいじゅん|いちらん|つく"}],"notes":"年代順 is chronological order. 年代順に並べる means to arrange things by the time of their origin; 年代順の展示 is a chronological exhibition. The noun combines here with に to indicate the arrangement. It is about the order in time, not about the age or rank of the people viewing.","levelNote":"Compound: 年代 (N3) + 順 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"町名は五十音順にならんでいます。","romaji":"Chōmei wa gojūonjun ni narande imasu.","german":"The town names are arranged in gojuon order.","furigana":"ちょうめい|ごじゅうおんじゅん"},{"japanese":"名簿を五十音順に並べました。","romaji":"Meibo o gojūonjun ni narabemashita.","german":"I arranged the roster in gojuon order.","furigana":"めいぼ|ごじゅうおんじゅん|なら"},{"japanese":"先生は五十音順に名前を呼びました。","romaji":"Sensei wa gojuuonjun ni namae o yobimashita.","german":"The teacher called the names in gojuon order.","furigana":"せんせい|ごじゅうおんじゅん|なまえ|よ"}],"notes":"五十音順 is the Japanese \"alphabetical\" order following the kana table あいうえお・かきくけこ ... up to ん. Dictionaries, name lists and indexes are sorted this way; usually with に + 並ぶ／並べる. Colloquially people also say あいうえお順. For Latin letters it is アルファベット順.","levelNote":"Compound: 五十音 (N2) + 順 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は細かいことはあまり気にしない性格です。","romaji":"Watashi wa komakai koto wa amari ki ni shinai seikaku desu.","german":"I'm the type who doesn't worry much about details.","furigana":"わたし|こま|き|せいかく"},{"japanese":"玉ねぎを細かく切ってください。","romaji":"Tamanegi o komakaku kitte kudasai.","german":"Please chop the onion finely.","furigana":"たま|こま|き"},{"japanese":"すみません、細かいお金がないんです。","romaji":"Sumimasen, komakai okane ga nai n desu.","german":"Sorry, I don't have small change.","furigana":"こま|かね"}],"notes":"細かい has several meanings: \"small, fine\" for many small pieces (細かく切る \"to cut small\", 細かい雨 \"drizzle\"), 細かいお金 \"small change\", \"precise, detailed\" (細かい説明) and \"petty, picky\" (お金に細かい). 細かいことは気にしない means \"not to worry about trifles\". In contrast to 小さい, which describes a single small object, 細かい emphasizes fineness or detail. Opposite: 粗い \"coarse\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 44"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この近くに警察署はありますか？","romaji":"Kono chikaku ni keisatsusho wa arimasu ka?","german":"Is there a police station near here?","furigana":"ちか|けいさつしょ"},{"japanese":"財布を拾ったので、警察署に届けました。","romaji":"Saifu o hirotta node, keisatsusho ni todokemashita.","german":"I found a wallet, so I turned it in to the police station.","furigana":"さいふ|ひろ|けいさつしょ|とど"},{"japanese":"事故のことで、警察署で話を聞かれました。","romaji":"Jiko no koto de, keisatsusho de hanashi o kikaremashita.","german":"I was questioned at the police station about the accident.","furigana":"じこ|けいさつしょ|はなし|き"}],"notes":"警察署 is the larger police station of a district (e.g. 新宿警察署). For lost property, directions or small matters, people usually go to a 交番, the small police box; in the countryside there is the 駐在所. The suffix 署 also appears in 消防署 \"fire station\" and 税務署 \"tax office\". In conversation people often just say 警察: 警察に行く.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"窓の外から、汽車の汽笛が聞こえてきました。","romaji":"Mado no soto kara, kisha no kiteki ga kikoete kimashita.","german":"From outside the window, I could hear the whistle of a steam train.","furigana":"まど|そと|きしゃ|きてき|き"},{"japanese":"汽笛が鳴りました。","romaji":"Kiteki ga narimashita.","german":"The steam whistle sounded.","furigana":"きてき|な"},{"japanese":"船が汽笛を鳴らして港を出ていきました。","romaji":"Fune ga kiteki o narashite minato o dete ikimashita.","german":"The ship sounded its horn and left the harbor.","furigana":"ふね|きてき|な|みなと|で"}],"notes":"汽笛 is the steam whistle or signal horn of a train (汽車) or ship; today it is used mainly for ships and often has a nostalgic ring. The pair is 汽笛が鳴る (intransitive) and 汽笛を鳴らす (transitive). A trill whistle or flute is called 笛, a car horn クラクション.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ここで少し塩を加えると味が引き立ちます。","romaji":"Koko de sukoshi shio o kuwaeru to aji ga hikitachimasu.","german":"Adding a little salt here brings out the flavor.","furigana":"すこ|しお|くわ|あじ|ひ|た"},{"japanese":"新しいメンバーを一人チームに加えました。","romaji":"Atarashii menbaa o hitori chiimu ni kuwaemashita.","german":"We added one new member to the team.","furigana":"あたら|ひとり|くわ"},{"japanese":"3に5を加えると8になります。","romaji":"San ni go o kuwaeru to hachi ni narimasu.","german":"Three plus five makes eight.","furigana":"くわ"}],"notes":"加える is transitive: XにYを加える \"to add Y to X\". It is used in cooking (塩を加える, more everyday 入れる), in arithmetic (more everyday 足す), for people (仲間に加える \"to take into the group\") and formally for 説明を加える \"to add an explanation\" or 危害を加える \"to inflict harm\". The intransitive counterpart is 加わる \"to join, to be added\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"参加方法はこちらです。","romaji":"Sanka hōhō wa kochira desu.","german":"Here is how to participate.","furigana":"さんかほうほう"},{"japanese":"大会に参加する予定です。","romaji":"Taikai ni sanka suru yotei desu.","german":"I plan to take part in the tournament.","furigana":"たいかい|さんか|よてい"},{"japanese":"日曜日のパーティーに参加しませんか。","romaji":"Nichiyoubi no paatii ni sanka shimasen ka.","german":"Won't you come to the party on Sunday?","furigana":"にちようび|さんか"}],"notes":"参加する is intransitive; the event takes に, not を: パーティーに参加する, 大会に参加する. Common derivations are 参加者 \"participant\", 参加費 \"participation fee\" and 不参加 \"non-participation\". 出席する emphasizes attendance at classes or meetings, whereas 参加 means active participation; joining a club, on the other hand, is 入る.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"焼き芋屋さんのトラックが家の前を通りました。","romaji":"Yakiimoya-san no torakku ga ie no mae o toorimashita.","german":"The sweet potato vendor's truck passed in front of our house.","furigana":"や|いもや|いえ|まえ|とお"},{"japanese":"冬に焼き芋を食べるのが好きです。","romaji":"Fuyu ni yakiimo o taberu no ga suki desu.","german":"I like eating roasted sweet potatoes in winter.","furigana":"ふゆ|や|いも|た|す"},{"japanese":"落ち葉を集めて、焼き芋を作りました。","romaji":"Ochiba o atsumete, yakiimo o tsukurimashita.","german":"We gathered fallen leaves and made roasted sweet potatoes.","furigana":"お|ば|あつ|や|いも|つく"}],"notes":"焼き芋 is a sweet potato (さつまいも) roasted in an oven or on hot stones, a typical snack in autumn and winter. Street vendors drive around with small trucks and call out \"石焼き芋\". It has nothing to do with an ordinary baked potato made from じゃがいも. It is also written 焼きいも or やきいも.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"じゃが芋のスープを作りました。","romaji":"Jagaimo no sūpu o tsukurimashita.","german":"I made potato soup.","furigana":"いも|つく"},{"japanese":"じゃが芋をカレーに入れます。","romaji":"Jagaimo o karē ni iremasu.","german":"I put potatoes in the curry.","furigana":"いも|い"},{"japanese":"じゃがいもの皮をむいてください。","romaji":"Jagaimo no kawa o muite kudasai.","german":"Please peel the potatoes.","furigana":"かわ"}],"notes":"じゃがいも is the \"potato\" and is mostly written in kana (じゃがいも or ジャガイモ); じゃが芋 is a mixed spelling, and the technical word is 馬鈴薯. The name goes back to Jakarta (old ジャガタラ). Typical dishes are 肉じゃが, カレー and ポテトサラダ. The sweet potato, on the other hand, is さつまいも, and french fries are フライドポテト.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は新しい劇団を立ち上げました。","romaji":"Kare wa atarashii gekidan o tachiagemashita.","german":"He started a new theater company.","furigana":"かれ|あたら|げきだん|た|あ"},{"japanese":"劇団に入って演技を始めました。","romaji":"Gekidan ni haitte engi o hajimemashita.","german":"I joined a theater company and started acting.","furigana":"げきだん|はい|えんぎ|はじ"},{"japanese":"週末に、有名な劇団の芝居を見に行きました。","romaji":"Shuumatsu ni, yuumei na gekidan no shibai o mi ni ikimashita.","german":"On the weekend I went to see a play by a famous theater company.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|ゆうめい|げきだん|しばい|み|い"}],"notes":"劇団 is a \"theater troupe, theater ensemble\", for example 劇団四季. Typical are 劇団に入る／入団する \"to join a troupe\", 劇団を作る／立ち上げる \"to found\" and 劇団員 \"ensemble member\". Do not confuse it with 劇場 \"theater (building)\" and 劇 \"play\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの連中はいつもくだらない話ばかりしている。","romaji":"Ano renchū wa itsumo kudaranai hanashi bakari shite iru.","german":"Those guys are always talking nonsense.","furigana":"れんちゅう|はなし"},{"japanese":"あの連中は信用できません。","romaji":"Ano renchū wa shin'yō dekimasen.","german":"You can't trust those guys.","furigana":"れんちゅう|しんよう"},{"japanese":"今夜は会社の連中と飲みに行ってくるね。","romaji":"Kon'ya wa kaisha no renchuu to nomi ni itte kuru ne.","german":"I'm going out drinking with the guys from work tonight.","furigana":"こんや|かいしゃ|れんちゅう|の|い"}],"notes":"連中 is colloquial for \"people, guys, bunch\". With あの or その it often sounds derogatory (あの連中は信用できない), but among friends it can also stand casually and familiarly for one's own group (会社の連中). It is inappropriate for people one respects; politely, one says 皆さん or 方々.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"犬はしっぽをふって喜びを表します。","romaji":"Inu wa shippo o futte yorokobi o arawashimasu.","german":"Dogs express joy by wagging their tails.","furigana":"いぬ|よろこ|あらわ"},{"japanese":"合格の喜びを家族と分かち合いました。","romaji":"Goukaku no yorokobi o kazoku to wakachiaimashita.","german":"I shared the joy of passing with my family.","furigana":"ごうかく|よろこ|かぞく|わ|あ"},{"japanese":"子どもが生まれた時の喜びは忘れられません。","romaji":"Kodomo ga umareta toki no yorokobi wa wasureraremasen.","german":"I will never forget the joy when my child was born.","furigana":"こ|う|とき|よろこ|わす"}],"notes":"喜び is the noun of 喜ぶ \"to be glad\" and means \"joy\", often about an event: 合格の喜び, 優勝の喜び. Typical are 喜びを感じる, 喜びを表す and 喜びの声. 嬉しさ emphasizes the subjective feeling of happiness, 楽しみ anticipation or enjoyment. In congratulations, the formal お喜び申し上げます is used. Opposite: 悲しみ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"マスコミはそのニュースを連日報道しました。","romaji":"Masukomi wa sono nyūsu o renjitsu hōdō shimashita.","german":"The media reported that news day after day.","furigana":"れんじつほうどう"},{"japanese":"連日暑い日が続いています。","romaji":"Renjitsu atsui hi ga tsuzuite imasu.","german":"Hot days continue day after day.","furigana":"れんじつあつ|ひ|つづ"},{"japanese":"連日の残業で疲れています。","romaji":"Renjitsu no zangyou de tsukarete imasu.","german":"I'm tired from working overtime day after day.","furigana":"れんじつ|ざんぎょう|つか"}],"notes":"連日 means \"day after day, on several consecutive days\" and usually emphasizes something extraordinary that repeats (heat, reports, overtime). It is used adverbially without a particle (連日報道する) or attributively with の (連日の雨). It sounds like news language; in conversation people prefer 毎日 or 何日も続けて. 毎日, by contrast, describes a normal daily routine.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"悲しみを乗りこえるには時間が必要です。","romaji":"Kanashimi o norikoeru ni wa jikan ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"It takes time to get over sadness.","furigana":"かな|の|じかん|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"深い悲しみを感じています。","romaji":"Fukai kanashimi o kanjite imasu.","german":"I am feeling deep sorrow.","furigana":"ふか|かな|かん"},{"japanese":"ペットが死んだ悲しみで、何も食べられませんでした。","romaji":"Petto ga shinda kanashimi de, nani mo taberaremasen deshita.","german":"I couldn't eat anything because of the grief of my pet's death.","furigana":"し|かな|なに|た"}],"notes":"悲しみ is the noun of 悲しい and means \"grief, sorrow, sadness\". Typical are 深い悲しみ, 悲しみを乗り越える \"to overcome grief\" and Xの悲しみ (別れの悲しみ). 悲しさ describes more the degree of being sad, 寂しさ loneliness. Opposite: 喜び.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 33"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その国には、混血の人がたくさん住んでいます。","romaji":"Sono kuni ni wa, konketsu no hito ga takusan sunde imasu.","german":"In diesem Land leben viele Menschen gemischter Abstammung.","furigana":"くに|こんけつ|ひと|す"},{"japanese":"彼は混血です。","romaji":"Kare wa konketsu desu.","german":"Er ist gemischter Herkunft.","furigana":"かれ|こんけつ"},{"japanese":"日本では、混血の人を「ハーフ」と呼ぶことが多いです。","romaji":"Nihon de wa, konketsu no hito o \"haafu\" to yobu koto ga ooi desu.","german":"In Japan nennt man Menschen gemischter Herkunft oft „Hāfu“.","furigana":"にほん|こんけつ|ひと|よ|おお"}],"notes":"混血 bezeichnet „gemischte Abstammung“ (混血の人, 日本人とXの混血) und ist ein sachlich-schriftliches Wort, das heute leicht distanziert oder diskriminierend wirken kann. Im Alltag sagt man meist ハーフ, inzwischen auch ミックス oder ダブル. Das Verb 混血する ist selten. Auch das deutsche „Mischling“ ist abwertend und sollte nicht als Übersetzung dienen.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日を改めることにしました。","romaji":"Hi o aratameru koto ni shimashita.","german":"I decided to postpone it to another day.","furigana":"ひ|あらた"},{"japanese":"生活習慣を改めます。","romaji":"Seikatsu shūkan o aratamemasu.","german":"I will change my lifestyle habits.","furigana":"せいかつしゅうかん|あらた"},{"japanese":"では、改めてご連絡します。","romaji":"Dewa, aratamete gorenraku shimasu.","german":"Then, I will contact you again.","furigana":"あらた|れんらく"}],"notes":"改める is transitive and means \"to change (for the better), correct, revise\"; it sounds more formal than 変える. Typical objects are 態度, 考え, 習慣 and 規則; 日を改める means \"to postpone to another day\". The intransitive counterpart is 改まる (改まった場 \"formal occasion\"). Very common is the adverb 改めて \"once more, again\": 改めてご連絡します.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"飲酒をやめようと思った一番の理由は何ですか？","romaji":"Inshu o yameyou to omotta ichiban no riyuu wa nan desu ka?","german":"What was the main reason you decided to quit drinking?","furigana":"いんしゅ|おも|いちばん|りゆう|なん"},{"japanese":"飲酒運転は絶対にダメです。","romaji":"Inshu unten wa zettai ni dame desu.","german":"Drunk driving is absolutely not okay.","furigana":"いんしゅうんてん|ぜったい"},{"japanese":"二十歳になるまで、飲酒は法律で禁止されています。","romaji":"Hatachi ni naru made, inshu wa houritsu de kinshi sarete imasu.","german":"Until you turn twenty, drinking alcohol is prohibited by law.","furigana":"はたち|いんしゅ|ほうりつ|きんし"}],"notes":"飲酒 is the official, written word for \"alcohol consumption, drinking alcohol\". Typical are 飲酒運転 \"drunk driving\", 未成年の飲酒 and 飲酒量. In daily life people simply say お酒を飲む; 飲酒する is possible but sounds stiff. In Japan, alcohol is allowed only from the age of 20.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お持ち帰りでお願いします。","romaji":"Omochikaeri de onegai shimasu.","german":"I'd like it to go, please.","furigana":"も|かえ|ねが"},{"japanese":"店内でお召し上がりですか、お持ち帰りですか。","romaji":"Tennai de omeshiagari desu ka, omochikaeri desu ka.","german":"Will you eat in, or take out?","furigana":"てんない|め|あ|も|かえ"},{"japanese":"このケーキはお持ち帰りできますか。","romaji":"Kono keeki wa omochikaeri dekimasu ka.","german":"Can I take this cake to go?","furigana":"も|かえ"}],"notes":"お持ち帰り is the polite word for \"to take away\" and comes from the verb 持ち帰る. Customers say お持ち帰りでお願いします, while staff ask 店内でお召し上がりですか、お持ち帰りですか. Synonymous but more casual is テイクアウト. Colloquially, お持ち帰り can also mean taking someone home with you after a party.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一昨年の市長って誰だったっけ？","romaji":"Ototoshi no shichō tte dare datta kke?","german":"Wer war noch mal vor zwei Jahren Bürgermeister?","furigana":"おととし|しちょう|だれ"},{"japanese":"一昨年ドイツに行きました。","romaji":"Ototoshi Doitsu ni ikimashita.","german":"Vor zwei Jahren war ich in Deutschland.","furigana":"おととし|い"}],"notes":"一昨年 wird hier おととし gelesen: das Jahr vor dem letzten Jahr, also vor zwei Jahren. おととし ist die Hiraganaschreibung derselben Zeitangabe. 一昨年の ordnet etwas diesem Jahr zu; ohne Partikel kann das Wort den Zeitpunkt einer Handlung nennen. だったっけ fragt vertraut nach einer Erinnerung. Die formellere Lesung いっさくねん gehört nicht zu den Beispielen dieser Karte. おととし ist nicht おととい, vorgestern.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 38"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この言い方でも意味は通じるけど、ちょっと不自然かな。","romaji":"Kono iikata de mo imi wa tsūjiru kedo, chotto fushizen ka na.","german":"This phrasing gets the meaning across, but it sounds a bit unnatural.","furigana":"い|かた|いみ|つう|ふしぜん"},{"japanese":"英語が通じませんでした。","romaji":"Eigo ga tsūjimasen deshita.","german":"English wasn't understood.","furigana":"えいご|つう"},{"japanese":"この道は駅に通じています。","romaji":"Kono michi wa eki ni tsuujite imasu.","german":"This road leads to the station.","furigana":"みち|えき|つう"}],"notes":"通じる is mostly intransitive with が and at its core means \"to go through, to be connected\": 道が駅に通じる \"the road leads to the station\", 電話が通じる \"the call gets through\", 英語が通じる \"English is understood\", 気持ちが通じる \"feelings get across\". Unlike 分かる, here the language or meaning is the subject, not the person. Xに通じている also means \"to be well versed in X\"; 〜を通じて means \"via, through (someone/something)\". Literary variant: 通ずる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"すごーい！流石ですね。","romaji":"Sugōi! Sasuga desu ne.","german":"Wow! You're amazing, as expected.","furigana":"さすが"},{"japanese":"流石先輩ですね。","romaji":"Sasuga senpai desu ne.","german":"As expected of a senior.","furigana":"さすがせんぱい"},{"japanese":"流石、経験のある先生は説明が分かりやすいですね。","romaji":"Sasuga, keiken no aru sensei wa setsumei ga wakariyasui desu ne.","german":"As expected, an experienced teacher explains things in an easy-to-understand way.","furigana":"さすが|けいけん|せんせい|せつめい|わ"}],"notes":"流石 is usually written さすが and here praises an achievement that one expected from the person: typical for you or \"as expected\". さすがですね expresses appreciation. The identically written personal name and other sentence patterns such as さすがに with \"even so\" are separate usages; in these examples the praise is at the center.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ここをクリックすると、文字が上に重なるから読めなくなるんだ。バグかな？","romaji":"Koko o kurikku suru to, moji ga ue ni kasanaru kara yomenaku naru n da. Bagu ka na?","german":"When I click here, the text overlaps and becomes unreadable. Is it a bug?","furigana":"もじ|うえ|かさ|よ"},{"japanese":"予定が重なってしまいました。","romaji":"Yotei ga kasanatte shimaimashita.","german":"My appointments ended up overlapping.","furigana":"よてい|かさ"},{"japanese":"悪いことが重なって、今週は本当に大変でした。","romaji":"Warui koto ga kasanatte, konshuu wa hontou ni taihen deshita.","german":"Bad things piled up, and this week was really tough.","furigana":"わる|かさ|こんしゅう|ほんとう|たいへん"}],"notes":"重なる is intransitive: things lie on top of each other (本が重なっている), appointments or events coincide (予定が重なる \"to overlap\", 祝日と日曜日が重なる) or pile up (悪いことが重なる \"one misfortune after another\"). The transitive counterpart is 重ねる \"to stack, to repeat\" (皿を重ねる).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"東西南北どこへ行っても人がいる。","romaji":"Tōzai nanboku doko e itte mo hito ga iru.","german":"Egal in welche Himmelsrichtung man geht, überall sind Menschen.","furigana":"とうざいなんぼく|い|ひと"},{"japanese":"地図を見ても、東西南北が分かりません。","romaji":"Chizu o mite mo, tōzai nanboku ga wakarimasen.","german":"Selbst mit Karte weiß ich nicht, wo welche Himmelsrichtung ist.","furigana":"ちず|み|とうざいなんぼく|わ"},{"japanese":"この駅には東西南北に四つの出口があります。","romaji":"Kono eki ni wa tōzai nanboku ni yottsu no deguchi ga arimasu.","german":"Dieser Bahnhof hat vier Ausgänge, in jede Himmelsrichtung einen.","furigana":"えき|とうざいなんぼく|よっ|でぐち"}],"notes":"Wörtlich: 東 Osten, 西 Westen, 南 Süden, 北 Norden – die Reihenfolge ist anders als im deutschen „Norden, Süden, Osten, Westen“. Wörtlich nennt es die vier Himmelsrichtungen, übertragen heißt es „in alle Richtungen, überall“. Typische Rahmen sind 東西南北が分からない „keine Orientierung haben“, 東西南北に広がる und 東西南北どこへ行っても. Das Register ist neutral.","levelNote":"Compound: 東西 (N2) + 南北 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私もその意見に同感ですね。","romaji":"Watashi mo sono iken ni dōkan desu ne.","german":"I agree with that opinion, too.","furigana":"わたし|いけん|どうかん"},{"japanese":"「この映画、ちょっと長いね。」「うん、同感。」","romaji":"\"Kono eiga, chotto nagai ne.\" \"Un, doukan.\"","german":"\"This movie is a bit long.\" \"Yeah, I agree.\"","furigana":"えいが|なが|どうかん"},{"japanese":"その点については、私も全く同感です。","romaji":"Sono ten ni tsuite wa, watashi mo mattaku doukan desu.","german":"On that point, I completely agree as well.","furigana":"てん|わたし|まった|どうかん"}],"notes":"同感 expresses that one is \"of the same opinion\" or feels the same way: Xに同感です, 全く同感です. In casual conversation a short 同感！ \"I think so too!\" is enough. 賛成, by contrast, means \"to be in favor of a proposal\", 共感 \"to empathize, understand emotionally\". 同感する is possible, but usually you hear 同感だ／同感です.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"もう少し社会人としての自覚を持った方がいいよ。","romaji":"Mou sukoshi shakaijin toshite no jikaku o motta hou ga ii yo.","german":"You should have a bit more awareness of being a working adult.","furigana":"すこ|しゃかいじん|じかく|も|ほう"},{"japanese":"自覚を持って行動してください。","romaji":"Jikaku o motte kōdō shite kudasai.","german":"Please act with awareness.","furigana":"じかく|も|こうどう"},{"japanese":"彼には、自分が悪いという自覚がないようです。","romaji":"Kare ni wa, jibun ga warui to iu jikaku ga nai you desu.","german":"He doesn't seem to be aware that he is in the wrong.","furigana":"かれ|じぶん|わる|じかく"}],"notes":"自覚 is awareness of one's own situation, role or condition - not \"self-confidence\" in the sense of self-assurance (that would be 自信). Typical are 〜としての自覚を持つ \"to be aware of one's role as ...\" and 〜という自覚がない \"not to be aware that ...\". As a する-verb, what is recognized takes を: 疲れを自覚する \"to realize that one is exhausted\". A slightly elevated but commonly used expression, for example in admonitions.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"レストランで皿洗いのバイトを始めました。","romaji":"Resutoran de saraarai no baito o hajimemashita.","german":"I started a part-time job washing dishes at a restaurant.","furigana":"さらあら|はじ"},{"japanese":"毎日皿洗いをしています。","romaji":"Mainichi saraarai o shite imasu.","german":"I wash the dishes every day.","furigana":"まいにちさらあら"},{"japanese":"夕ご飯の後、皿洗いを手伝ってくれる？","romaji":"Yuugohan no ato, saraarai o tetsudatte kureru?","german":"Can you help me with the dishes after dinner?","furigana":"ゆう|はん|あと|さらあら|てつだ"}],"notes":"皿洗い means \"dishwashing\" (literally \"plate washing\") and is used with する: 皿洗いをする. It also refers to the job of dishwasher: 皿洗いのバイト. Synonymous and somewhat more general is 食器洗い; the dishwasher is 食器洗い機. In conversation one often simply says お皿を洗う.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"年号が改まって、新しい時代が始まりました。","romaji":"Nengou ga aratamatte, atarashii jidai ga hajimarimashita.","german":"The era name changed and a new era began.","furigana":"ねんごう|あらた|あたら|じだい|はじ"},{"japanese":"態度が改まりました。","romaji":"Taido ga aratamarimashita.","german":"His attitude improved.","furigana":"たいど|あらた"},{"japanese":"改まった場所では、敬語を使いましょう。","romaji":"Aratamatta basho de wa, keigo o tsukaimashou.","german":"In formal settings, let's use honorific language.","furigana":"あらた|ばしょ|けいご|つか"}],"notes":"改まる is intransitive: something \"renews itself\" or \"changes for the better\", for example 年が改まる \"a new year begins\", 態度が改まる \"one's behavior improves\". The transitive counterpart is 改める \"to change, improve\" (規則を改める). Very common is the form 改まった \"formal, solemn\": 改まった場 \"an official occasion\"; 改まって何？ colloquially asks \"Why so formal?\". Rather elevated vocabulary.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"水と油って、混ざるんだっけ？混ざらないんだっけ？","romaji":"Mizu to abura tte, mazaru n da kke? Mazaranai n da kke?","german":"Water and oil - do they mix, or don't they?","furigana":"みず|あぶら|ま|ま"},{"japanese":"色が混ざってきれいです。","romaji":"Iro ga mazatte kirei desu.","german":"The colors blend and look pretty.","furigana":"いろ|ま"},{"japanese":"子どもたちの中に、大人が一人混ざって遊んでいました。","romaji":"Kodomotachi no naka ni, otona ga hitori mazatte asonde imashita.","german":"One adult was playing mixed in among the children.","furigana":"こ|なか|おとな|ひとりま|あそ"}],"notes":"混ざる is intransitive: things \"mix together\" or something is \"mixed in\" somewhere - AとBが混ざる or AにBが混ざる. The transitive counterpart is 混ぜる \"to mix, to stir\". Almost synonymous is 混じる; for things that remain distinguishable (people in a group), 交ざる is also written. Do not confuse it with 混む \"to be crowded\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"釣った魚が余っているので、よかったら少しいかがですか？","romaji":"Tsutta sakana ga amatte iru node, yokattara sukoshi ikaga desu ka?","german":"I have leftover fish that I caught, so would you like some?","furigana":"つ|さかな|あま|すこ"},{"japanese":"お金が余りました。","romaji":"Okane ga amarimashita.","german":"I had money left over.","furigana":"かね|あま"},{"japanese":"テストの時間が余ったので、もう一度見直しました。","romaji":"Tesuto no jikan ga amatta node, mou ichido minaoshimashita.","german":"I had time left over on the test, so I checked it again.","furigana":"じかん|あま|いちどみなお"}],"notes":"余る is intransitive and means \"to be left over, to be in excess\" - more than is needed: お金が余る, 時間が余る. In contrast to 残る (\"to remain, to still be there\"), 余る emphasizes the surplus. In arithmetic, 10を3で割ると1余る means \"remainder 1\"; the related noun is 余り. Fixed expression: 手に余る \"to be beyond one's control\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このイス、一つ余計だったね。","romaji":"Kono isu, hitotsu yokei datta ne.","german":"We had one chair too many.","furigana":"ひと|よけい"},{"japanese":"余計なことを言わないで。","romaji":"Yokei na koto o iwanaide.","german":"Don't say unnecessary things.","furigana":"よけい|い"},{"japanese":"やめてと言われると、余計にやりたくなります。","romaji":"Yamete to iwareru to, yokei ni yaritaku narimasu.","german":"When I'm told to stop, I want to do it all the more.","furigana":"い|よけい"}],"notes":"余計 is a な-adjective for \"superfluous, too much, unnecessary\": 余計なこと \"unnecessary things\", 一つ余計だ \"one too many\". The phrase 余計なお世話 means \"Mind your own business!\". As an adverb (余計に / 余計) it means \"all the more, even more\": 言われると余計にやりたくなる. It often sounds slightly reproachful; more neutral are 不要 and いらない.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これは私個人の意見です。","romaji":"Kore wa watashi kojin no iken desu.","german":"This is my personal opinion.","furigana":"わたしこじん|いけん"},{"japanese":"個人情報を守ってください。","romaji":"Kojin jōhō o mamotte kudasai.","german":"Please protect personal information.","furigana":"こじんじょうほう|まも"},{"japanese":"このツアーは個人でも申し込めます。","romaji":"Kono tsuaa wa kojin demo moushikomemasu.","german":"You can also sign up for this tour as an individual.","furigana":"こじん|もう|こ"}],"notes":"個人 is a noun: \"individual, private person\" - as opposed to a group, company or state. It is used attributively with の or in compounds: 個人の意見, 個人情報 \"personal data\", 個人で \"as an individual\". The adjective \"personal\" is 個人的(な): 個人的には \"personally, in my own opinion\". 私個人の意見 emphasizes that one speaks only for oneself.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"カフェで個室を予約して、友達とゆっくりおしゃべりを楽しみました。","romaji":"Kafe de koshitsu o yoyaku shite, tomodachi to yukkuri oshaberi o tanoshimimashita.","german":"I reserved a private room at a cafe and enjoyed a leisurely chat with my friend.","furigana":"こしつ|よやく|ともだち|たの"},{"japanese":"レストランの個室を予約しました。","romaji":"Resutoran no koshitsu o yoyaku shimashita.","german":"I reserved a private room at the restaurant.","furigana":"こしつ|よやく"},{"japanese":"入院中は個室を希望しています。","romaji":"Nyūin-chū wa koshitsu o kibō shite imasu.","german":"I would like a single room during my hospital stay.","furigana":"にゅういんちゅう|こしつ|きぼう"}],"notes":"個室 is a room of one's own or a separated room. In a restaurant it means a separate dining room or private booth; in a hospital it can be a single room. 個室を予約する means to reserve such a room. The two restaurant examples do not describe reserving the entire restaurant.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"やばい、このクッパ強すぎる。誰か倒す方法知ってたら、教えて！","romaji":"Yabai, kono Kuppa tsuyosugiru. Dareka taosu houhou shittetara, oshiete!","german":"Man, this Bowser is too strong. If anyone knows how to defeat him, tell me!","furigana":"つよ|だれ|たお|ほうほうし|おし"},{"japanese":"ゲームでボスを倒しました。","romaji":"Gēmu de bosu o taoshimashita.","german":"I defeated the boss in the game.","furigana":"たお"},{"japanese":"すみません、座席を倒してもいいですか？","romaji":"Sumimasen, zaseki o taoshite mo ii desu ka?","german":"Excuse me, may I recline my seat?","furigana":"ざせき|たお"}],"notes":"倒す is transitive: to \"knock over, topple, tip down\" something (コップを倒す, 木を倒す, 座席を倒す) and, figuratively, to \"defeat\" an opponent (敵を倒す, ボスを倒す). The intransitive counterpart is 倒れる \"to fall over, to collapse\". On a plane or bus one politely asks 座席を倒してもいいですか before reclining the seat.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あのマンションは、お化けが出るというウワサのせいで借り手がつかない。","romaji":"Ano manshon wa, obake ga deru to iu uwasa no sei de karite ga tsukanai.","german":"That apartment can't find a tenant because of the rumor that it's haunted.","furigana":"ば|で|か|て"},{"japanese":"この物件には借り手がいません。","romaji":"Kono bukken ni wa karite ga imasen.","german":"There is no tenant for this property.","furigana":"ぶっけん|か|て"},{"japanese":"お金の借り手は、毎月少しずつ返さなければなりません。","romaji":"Okane no karite wa, maitsuki sukoshi zutsu kaesanakereba narimasen.","german":"A person who borrows money has to pay it back in small installments every month.","furigana":"かね|か|て|まいつきすこ|かえ"}],"notes":"借り手 is \"someone who borrows or rents something\": the tenant of an apartment, but also a borrower of a loan or an item. The opposite is 貸し手 \"landlord, lender, creditor\". Typical: 借り手がつく / 見つかる \"to find a tenant\", 借り手がいない. In contracts, 借主 is more common; in everyday speech one also simply says 借りる人.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"クズリって、実在するんですね。","romaji":"Kuzuri tte, jitsuzai suru n desu ne.","german":"So wolverines really exist, huh.","furigana":"じつざい"},{"japanese":"この映画の主人公は、実在した人物です。","romaji":"Kono eiga no shujinkou wa, jitsuzai shita jinbutsu desu.","german":"The protagonist of this movie is a person who really existed.","furigana":"えいが|しゅじんこう|じつざい|じんぶつ"},{"japanese":"この物語はフィクションで、実在の人物とは関係ありません。","romaji":"Kono monogatari wa fikushon de, jitsuzai no jinbutsu to wa kankei arimasen.","german":"This story is fiction and has no relation to real persons.","furigana":"ものがたり|じつざい|じんぶつ|かんけい"}],"notes":"実在 means \"to actually exist\", as opposed to invented things, legends or fiction. As an intransitive する-verb: 実在する動物, 実在した人物 \"a person who really lived\". Attributively one says 実在の: 実在の人物・団体. Well known is the note at the end of films and novels that the story has nothing to do with real persons. Neutral to slightly formal; in daily life one can also say 本当にいる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"在外選挙では、海外に住んでいる日本人も投票できます。","romaji":"Zaigai senkyo de wa, kaigai ni sunde iru Nihonjin mo touhyou dekimasu.","german":"Bei der Auslandswahl können auch Japaner abstimmen, die im Ausland leben.","furigana":"ざいがいせんきょ|かいがい|す|にほんじん|とうひょう"},{"japanese":"在外日本人の数が増えています。","romaji":"Zaigai nihonjin no kazu ga fuete imasu.","german":"Die Zahl der Japaner im Ausland nimmt zu.","furigana":"ざいがいにほんじん|かず|ふ"},{"japanese":"父は大学の在外研究で、一年間ドイツに行きます。","romaji":"Chichi wa daigaku no zaigai kenkyuu de, ichinenkan Doitsu ni ikimasu.","german":"Mein Vater geht für ein Forschungsjahr seiner Uni ein Jahr lang nach Deutschland.","furigana":"ちち|だいがく|ざいがいけんきゅう|いちねんかん|い"}],"notes":"在外 bedeutet „im Ausland befindlich“ und ist ein Amts- und Schriftwort, das fast nur vor anderen Nomen steht: 在外日本人 / 在外邦人 „im Ausland lebende Japaner“, 在外選挙 „Auslandswahl“, 在外研究 „Forschungsaufenthalt im Ausland“. Allein oder prädikativ wird es nicht verwendet. Im Alltag sagt man 海外に住んでいる, 海外生活 oder 海外在住.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"夜中に悪夢で目が覚めました。","romaji":"Yonaka ni akumu de me ga samemashita.","german":"I woke up in the middle of the night from a nightmare.","furigana":"よなか|あくむ|め|さ"},{"japanese":"昨夜悪夢を見ました。","romaji":"Sakuya akumu o mimashita.","german":"I had a nightmare last night.","furigana":"さくやあくむ|み"},{"japanese":"あの一日は悪夢のようでした。","romaji":"Ano ichinichi wa akumu no yō deshita.","german":"That day was like a nightmare.","furigana":"いちにち|あくむ"}],"notes":"悪夢 is a nightmare. 悪夢を見る means to have a nightmare; 悪夢で目が覚める, to wake up from one. The noun also appears in 悪夢のような for a figurative comparison with a very unpleasant experience.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの夫妻は顔がそっくりです。","romaji":"Ano fusai wa kao ga sokkuri desu.","german":"That couple look exactly alike.","furigana":"ふさい|かお"},{"japanese":"田中夫妻が引っ越しました。","romaji":"Tanaka fusai ga hikkoshimashita.","german":"Mr. and Mrs. Tanaka have moved.","furigana":"たなかふさい|ひ|こ"},{"japanese":"結婚式には、社長ご夫妻もいらっしゃいました。","romaji":"Kekkonshiki ni wa, shachou go-fusai mo irasshaimashita.","german":"The company president and his wife also attended the wedding.","furigana":"けっこんしき|しゃちょう|ふさい"}],"notes":"夫妻 is \"married couple\" in formal language and typically follows names or titles: 田中夫妻 \"Mr. and Mrs. Tanaka\", politely 社長ご夫妻. For one's own couple or in everyday speech, 夫婦 is used (私たち夫婦, 夫婦げんか); you never say 夫妻 about yourself. Frequent in news, invitations and celebratory speeches.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"妻子がいるのに、他の女性と二人でご飯に行くのはダメでしょ。","romaji":"Saishi ga iru noni, hoka no josei to futari de gohan ni iku no wa dame desho.","german":"Even though you have a wife and children, you shouldn't go out to eat alone with another woman.","furigana":"さいし|ほか|じょせい|ふたり|はん|い"},{"japanese":"彼は妻子を養っています。","romaji":"Kare wa saishi o yashinatte imasu.","german":"He supports his wife and children.","furigana":"かれ|さいし|やしな"},{"japanese":"彼は妻子を国に残して、一人で日本に働きに来ました。","romaji":"Kare wa saishi o kuni ni nokoshite, hitori de Nihon ni hataraki ni kimashita.","german":"He left his wife and children in his home country and came to Japan alone to work.","furigana":"かれ|さいし|くに|のこ|ひとり|にほん|はたら|き"}],"notes":"妻子 (read さいし, not つまこ) means \"wife and children\" as a man's family. It sounds somewhat literary; typical are 妻子を養う \"to support wife and children\", 妻子を残す and 妻子持ち \"family man, married man\". In daily life people rather say 奥さんと子ども or 家族.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"たとえ一夫多妻がOKになっても、お金がなければ少子化は止められないでしょ。","romaji":"Tatoe ipputasai ga OK ni natte mo, okane ga nakereba shoushika wa tomerarenai desho.","german":"Even if polygamy became acceptable, without money the declining birthrate couldn't be stopped, right?","furigana":"いっぷたさい|かね|しょうしか|と"},{"japanese":"一夫多妻は日本では認められていません。","romaji":"Ipputasai wa Nihon de wa mitomerarete imasen.","german":"Polygamy is not recognized in Japan.","furigana":"いっぷたさい|にほん|みと"},{"japanese":"この国では、昔は一夫多妻が普通でした。","romaji":"Kono kuni de wa, mukashi wa ipputasai ga futsuu deshita.","german":"In this country, polygamy used to be normal.","furigana":"くに|むかし|いっぷたさい|ふつう"}],"notes":"一夫多妻 is a four-character word: literally \"one husband - many wives\", i.e. polygyny or plural marriage. The system is called 一夫多妻制; antonyms are 一夫一婦(制) \"monogamy\" and the rare 一妻多夫. It is a matter-of-fact, formal expression from society, law and ethnology; used with が/は as subject or attributively with の. It is read いっぷ with a doubled consonant.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日のお母さんの機嫌はどう？","romaji":"Kyō no okāsan no kigen wa dō?","german":"How is Mom's mood today?","furigana":"きょう|かあ|きげん"},{"japanese":"今日は機嫌がいいですね。","romaji":"Kyō wa kigen ga ii desu ne.","german":"You're in a good mood today.","furigana":"きょう|きげん"},{"japanese":"父は朝から機嫌が悪くて、誰とも話しません。","romaji":"Chichi wa asa kara kigen ga warukute, dare to mo hanashimasen.","german":"My father has been in a bad mood since morning and isn't talking to anyone.","furigana":"ちち|あさ|きげん|わる|だれ|はな"}],"notes":"機嫌 is the outwardly visible \"mood\", mostly as seen by others: 機嫌がいい / 悪い \"in a good / bad mood\". 機嫌を取る means \"to keep someone in a good mood, to flatter\", 機嫌を損ねる \"to put someone in a bad mood\". ご機嫌 is also a な-adjective \"in high spirits\" (ご機嫌だね). Unlike 気分 (one's own condition, also physical), 機嫌 mainly describes the mood that others sense.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その男は盗みの嫌疑で警察に呼ばれました。","romaji":"Sono otoko wa nusumi no kengi de keisatsu ni yobaremashita.","german":"That man was summoned by the police on suspicion of theft.","furigana":"おとこ|ぬす|けんぎ|けいさつ|よ"},{"japanese":"彼に嫌疑がかかっています。","romaji":"Kare ni kengi ga kakatte imasu.","german":"He is under suspicion.","furigana":"かれ|けんぎ"},{"japanese":"調べた結果、彼の嫌疑は晴れました。","romaji":"Shirabeta kekka, kare no kengi wa haremashita.","german":"As a result of the investigation, the suspicion against him was cleared.","furigana":"しら|けっか|かれ|けんぎ|は"}],"notes":"嫌疑 is a formal, legal expression for \"suspicion\" of having committed a crime or wrong. Typical: 〜の嫌疑で \"on suspicion of ...\", 嫌疑がかかる \"to be under suspicion\", 嫌疑が晴れる \"to be cleared of suspicion\". In police reports 容疑 (容疑者 \"suspect\") is more common; in daily life people say 疑い or 疑われる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"留守番電話にメッセージを残しました。","romaji":"Rusubandenwa ni messēji o nokoshimashita.","german":"I left a message on the answering machine.","furigana":"るすばんでんわ|のこ"},{"japanese":"電話に出られないときは、留守番電話にしています。","romaji":"Denwa ni derarenai toki wa, rusubandenwa ni shite imasu.","german":"When I can't answer the phone, I set it to voicemail.","furigana":"でんわ|で|るすばんでんわ"}],"notes":"留守番電話 is an answering machine or the corresponding function on a telephone. 留守番電話にメッセージを残す describes leaving a message; 留守番電話にする, switching to this function. 留守番 means looking after the house, but the whole compound refers to a telephone device or a service.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 18"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"最近、色んな加工食品に使われているシリコーンって、本当に無害なのかな？コーヒーにも入ってるんだけど。","romaji":"Saikin, ironna kakou shokuhin ni tsukawarete iru shirikoon tte, hontou ni mugai na no ka na? Koohii ni mo haitteru n da kedo.","german":"Silicone, which is used in all sorts of processed foods lately - is it really harmless? It's in coffee too, you know.","furigana":"さいきん|いろ|かこうしょくひん|つか|ほんとう|むがい|はい"},{"japanese":"この虫は無害です。","romaji":"Kono mushi wa mugai desu.","german":"This insect is harmless.","furigana":"むし|むがい"},{"japanese":"彼は無害そうに見えますが、怒るととても怖いです。","romaji":"Kare wa mugai sou ni miemasu ga, okoru to totemo kowai desu.","german":"He looks harmless, but he's very scary when he gets angry.","furigana":"かれ|むがい|み|おこ|こわ"}],"notes":"無害 is a な-adjective (also with の) meaning \"harmless, innocuous\" and is the opposite of 有害 \"harmful\". Typical: 人体に無害 \"harmless to humans\", 無害な虫. Figuratively, 無害そう describes a person who seems harmless. The tone is rather matter-of-fact and technical (products, chemicals, animals); in daily life people also say 安全 or 体に悪くない.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お尻にイボができました。","romaji":"Oshiri ni ibo ga dekimashita.","german":"I got a wart on my bottom.","furigana":"しり"},{"japanese":"長く座ってお尻が痛いです。","romaji":"Nagaku suwatte oshiri ga itai desu.","german":"I've been sitting for a long time and my bottom hurts.","furigana":"なが|すわ|しり|いた"},{"japanese":"転んでお尻を打ちました。","romaji":"Koronde oshiri o uchimashita.","german":"I fell and hit my bottom.","furigana":"ころ|しり|う"}],"notes":"お尻 is the usual, polite everyday word for \"bottom, butt\" and is also used with children and at the doctor's: お尻が痛い, お尻を打つ. The form without お, 尻, sounds cruder and lives on mainly in idioms (尻に敷く \"to have under one's thumb\"). On Japanese toilets, おしり marks the bidet function. Often also written in kana.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このアイヌのダンスでは弓矢が使われます。","romaji":"Kono Ainu no dansu de wa yumiya ga tsukawaremasu.","german":"Bei diesem Ainu-Tanz werden Pfeil und Bogen verwendet.","furigana":"ゆみや|つか"},{"japanese":"弓矢を使って狩りをしました。","romaji":"Yumiya o tsukatte kari o shimashita.","german":"Ich habe mit Pfeil und Bogen gejagt.","furigana":"ゆみや|つか|か"},{"japanese":"博物館で昔の弓矢を見ました。","romaji":"Hakubutsukan de mukashi no yumiya o mimashita.","german":"Im Museum habe ich alte Bögen und Pfeile gesehen.","furigana":"はくぶつかん|むかし|ゆみや|み"}],"notes":"弓矢 setzt sich aus 弓 „Bogen“ und 矢 „Pfeil“ zusammen und bezeichnet die Waffe als Paar, deutsch „Pfeil und Bogen“. Mit を使う, で狩りをする oder 弓矢で射る. In älteren Texten steht 弓矢 auch allgemein für „Waffen, Kriegswesen“. Das japanische Bogenschießen als Kampfkunst heißt 弓道.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このお守りが、きっとあなたを災いから守ってくれるでしょう。","romaji":"Kono omamori ga, kitto anata o wazawai kara mamotte kureru deshou.","german":"This amulet will surely protect you from misfortune.","furigana":"まも|わざわ|まも"},{"japanese":"余計なことを言うな。口は災いのもとだよ。","romaji":"Yokei na koto o iu na. Kuchi wa wazawai no moto da yo.","german":"Don't say unnecessary things. The mouth is the source of calamity.","furigana":"よけい|い|くち|わざわ"},{"japanese":"電車に遅れたおかげで事故にあわなかった。災い転じて福となす、だね。","romaji":"Densha ni okureta okage de jiko ni awanakatta. Wazawai tenjite fuku to nasu, da ne.","german":"Being late for the train kept me from having an accident. Turning misfortune into blessing, as they say.","furigana":"でんしゃ|おく|じこ|わざわ|てん|ふく"}],"notes":"災い is \"misfortune, calamity\" in an abstract, somewhat literary sense - that which brings harm. Typical: 災いから守る, 災いを招く \"to invite misfortune\". Well-known proverbs: 口は災いのもと \"Talking gets you into trouble\" and 災い転じて福となす \"a blessing in disguise, bad turns into good\". Concrete disasters are 災害, personal bad luck 災難. Often written in kana as わざわい.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"明け方、あのビルで火災が発生したそうです。","romaji":"Akegata, ano biru de kasai ga hassei shita sō desu.","german":"I hear a fire broke out in that building at dawn.","furigana":"あ|がた|かさい|はっせい"},{"japanese":"火災のときは、エレベーターを使わないでください。","romaji":"Kasai no toki wa, erebeetaa o tsukawanaide kudasai.","german":"In case of fire, please do not use the elevator.","furigana":"かさい|つか"},{"japanese":"家を買ったので、火災保険に入りました。","romaji":"Ie o katta node, kasai hoken ni hairimashita.","german":"I bought a house, so I took out fire insurance.","furigana":"いえ|か|かさいほけん|はい"}],"notes":"火災 is the formal word for \"fire\" - in news, announcements and compounds: 火災が発生する, 火災報知器 \"fire alarm\", 火災保険 \"fire insurance\". In conversation one mostly says 火事 (火事だ！). \"Conflagration\" would be too dramatic: 火災 can also be a small fire. 火災 refers to a damaging fire, not an intended fire like 火 or たき火.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"それはとんだ災難でしたね。","romaji":"Sore wa tonda sainan deshita ne.","german":"That was quite a misfortune.","furigana":"さいなん"},{"japanese":"財布をなくして、電車も止まって、今日は災難続きです。","romaji":"Saifu o nakushite, densha mo tomatte, kyou wa sainan tsuzuki desu.","german":"I lost my wallet and the trains stopped too, so today has been one mishap after another.","furigana":"さいふ|でんしゃ|と|きょう|さいなんつづ"},{"japanese":"旅行先でかばんを盗まれるという災難にあいました。","romaji":"Ryokousaki de kaban o nusumareru to iu sainan ni aimashita.","german":"I suffered the misfortune of having my bag stolen on my trip.","furigana":"りょこうさき|ぬす|さいなん"}],"notes":"災難 is a personal \"misfortune, mishap\" that befalls someone through no fault of their own: 災難にあう \"to have bad luck, to meet with misfortune\". Very common is the sympathetic phrase とんだ災難でしたね \"That was really bad luck!\"; 災難続き means \"a run of bad luck\". In contrast to 災害 (natural disaster), 災難 usually affects individuals, and it sounds more everyday than 災い.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"震災にあった日のことが、今でも忘れられないトラウマになっています。","romaji":"Shinsai ni atta hi no koto ga, ima demo wasurerarenai torauma ni natte imasu.","german":"The day of the earthquake disaster is still an unforgettable trauma for me.","furigana":"しんさい|ひ|いま|わす"},{"japanese":"震災から十年が経ちました。","romaji":"Shinsai kara jūnen ga tachimashita.","german":"Ten years have passed since the earthquake disaster.","furigana":"しんさい|じゅうねん|た"},{"japanese":"震災に備えて、家に水と食べ物を用意しています。","romaji":"Shinsai ni sonaete, ie ni mizu to tabemono o youi shite imasu.","german":"I keep water and food at home in preparation for an earthquake disaster.","furigana":"しんさい|そな|いえ|みず|た|もの|ようい"}],"notes":"震災 is the \"earthquake disaster\", i.e. the damage and consequences of a large earthquake - the quake itself is 地震. Major events bear names with 大震災, such as 東日本大震災 (2011) or 阪神・淡路大震災 (1995). Typical: 震災にあう \"to experience an earthquake\", 震災から〜年, 震災に備える \"to prepare for an earthquake\". Serious, rather written tone.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"なんか煙いと思ったら、近所で火事が起きてたらしい。","romaji":"Nanka kemui to omottara, kinjo de kaji ga okiteta rashii.","german":"I thought it was smoky somehow, and apparently there was a fire in the neighborhood.","furigana":"けむ|おも|きんじょ|かじ|お"},{"japanese":"部屋でたばこを吸われると、煙くて目が痛くなります。","romaji":"Heya de tabako o suwareru to, kemukute me ga itaku narimasu.","german":"When someone smokes in the room, it's smoky and my eyes hurt.","furigana":"へや|す|けむ|め|いた"},{"japanese":"たき火の近くは煙いので、少し離れて座りましょう。","romaji":"Takibi no chikaku wa kemui node, sukoshi hanarete suwarimashou.","german":"It's smoky near the bonfire, so let's sit a bit farther away.","furigana":"び|ちか|けむ|すこ|はな|すわ"}],"notes":"煙い is an い-adjective and describes that smoke is bothersome to you - it burns the eyes or scratches the throat: 部屋が煙い, たき火が煙い. It does not mean \"smoky\" in the sense of taste (that would be スモーキー). 煙たい has the same meaning and, figuratively, also describes an unpleasant, awe-inspiring person (煙たい上司). Redundant phrases such as 煙が煙い are avoided.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"共犯者は国外にいる可能性が高いそうだ。","romaji":"Kyōhansha wa kokugai ni iru kanōsei ga takai sō da.","german":"The accomplice is said to be likely abroad.","furigana":"きょうはんしゃ|こくがい|かのうせい|たか"},{"japanese":"共犯者が逮捕されました。","romaji":"Kyōhansha ga taiho saremashita.","german":"The accomplice was arrested.","furigana":"きょうはんしゃ|たいほ"},{"japanese":"ケーキを食べたのは私だけじゃないよ。妹も共犯者だよ。","romaji":"Keeki o tabeta no wa watashi dake ja nai yo. Imouto mo kyouhansha da yo.","german":"I'm not the only one who ate the cake. My little sister is an accomplice too.","furigana":"た|わたし|いもうと|きょうはんしゃ"}],"notes":"共犯者 is the \"accomplice\" in a crime; 共犯 alone means \"complicity\". The main perpetrator is 主犯, the perpetrator in general 犯人. Typical in news: 共犯者が逮捕される, 〜の共犯者として. In daily life the word is also used jokingly for someone who joined in a small prank.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"うちの会社の男女比率は、5:2です。","romaji":"Uchi no kaisha no danjo hiritsu wa, go tai ni desu.","german":"The male-to-female ratio at our company is 5 to 2."},{"japanese":"ご飯を炊くときは、米と水の比率が大切です。","romaji":"Gohan o taku toki wa, kome to mizu no hiritsu ga taisetsu desu.","german":"When cooking rice, the ratio of rice to water is important.","furigana":"はん|た|こめ|みず|ひりつ|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"給料に対する家賃の比率が高すぎます。","romaji":"Kyuuryou ni taisuru yachin no hiritsu ga takasugimasu.","german":"The ratio of rent to salary is too high.","furigana":"きゅうりょう|たい|やちん|ひりつ|たか"}],"notes":"比率 is the \"ratio\" or \"proportion\" of two quantities to each other: AとBの比率, 男女比率. Numerical ratios are read with 対 (5:2 → 五対二). Typical: 比率が高い / 低い \"a high / low proportion\". The more everyday word is 割合, which can also mean \"relatively, fairly\"; 比率 sounds more objective and statistical.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"スーパーのバックヤードは立入禁止です。","romaji":"Sūpā no bakku yādo wa tachiirikinshi desu.","german":"The supermarket's back area is off limits.","furigana":"たちいりきんし"},{"japanese":"この区域は立入禁止です。","romaji":"Kono kuiki wa tachiirikinshi desu.","german":"This area is off limits.","furigana":"くいき|たちいりきんし"},{"japanese":"子どもたちが立入禁止の場所で遊んでいて、注意されました。","romaji":"Kodomotachi ga tachiirikinshi no basho de asonde ite, chuui saremashita.","german":"The children were playing in a restricted area and got warned.","furigana":"こ|たちいりきんし|ばしょ|あそ|ちゅうい"}],"notes":"立入禁止 (also 立ち入り禁止) is the typical sign text \"No entry, no trespassing\", composed of 立入 \"entering\" and 禁止 \"prohibition\". In a sentence it is a noun with です (ここは立入禁止です) or attributive with の (立入禁止の場所). Common variants: 関係者以外立入禁止 \"authorized personnel only\", 芝生内立入禁止 \"keep off the grass\". Official, terse tone.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 33"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ここは、フグ料理の穴場なんですよ。","romaji":"Koko wa, fugu ryōri no anaba na n desu yo.","german":"This place is a hidden gem for pufferfish dishes.","furigana":"りょうり|あなば"},{"japanese":"花火を見るなら、この公園が穴場ですよ。","romaji":"Hanabi o miru nara, kono kouen ga anaba desu yo.","german":"If you want to watch fireworks, this park is a hidden gem.","furigana":"はなび|み|こうえん|あなば"},{"japanese":"週末でも空いている、穴場のスキー場を探しています。","romaji":"Shuumatsu demo suite iru, anaba no sukiijou o sagashite imasu.","german":"I'm looking for a hidden-gem ski resort that's empty even on weekends.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|す|あなば|じょう|さが"}],"notes":"穴場 (literally \"hole place\") is a \"hidden gem\": a good place that few people know - a restaurant, viewpoint, beach. Typical: 〜の穴場, 穴場スポット, 穴場の店; \"X is a hidden gem for Y\" is XはYの穴場だ. Colloquial and very common in travel and gourmet magazines. Similar is 隠れ家的なお店 \"a place like a hideaway\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鼻血が出たので、鼻の穴にティッシュを詰めました。","romaji":"Hanaji ga deta node, hana no ana ni tisshu o tsumemashita.","german":"My nose was bleeding, so I stuffed tissue in my nostril.","furigana":"はなぢ|で|はな|あな|つ"},{"japanese":"弟は鼻の穴をふくらませて、得意そうに話しました。","romaji":"Otouto wa hana no ana o fukuramasete, tokui sou ni hanashimashita.","german":"My younger brother flared his nostrils and spoke proudly.","furigana":"おとうと|はな|あな|とくい|はな"},{"japanese":"お医者さんが、ライトで鼻の穴の中を見ました。","romaji":"Oishasan ga, raito de hana no ana no naka o mimashita.","german":"The doctor looked inside my nostril with a light.","furigana":"いしゃ|はな|あな|なか|み"}],"notes":"鼻の穴 is literally \"the hole of the nose\" and the normal everyday word for \"nostril\"; the technical word is 鼻孔. Typical expressions: 鼻の穴をふくらませる \"to flare one's nostrils\" (from excitement, pride or anger) and 鼻の穴にティッシュを詰める for a nosebleed. \"To pick one's nose\" is 鼻をほじる, \"stuffy nose\" is 鼻が詰まる - without 穴.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日はお昼に穴子の天ぷらを食べました。","romaji":"Kyō wa ohiru ni anago no tenpura o tabemashita.","german":"Today for lunch I had conger eel tempura.","furigana":"きょう|ひる|あなご|てん|た"},{"japanese":"穴子のお寿司が好きです。","romaji":"Anago no osushi ga suki desu.","german":"I like conger eel sushi.","furigana":"あなご|すし|す"},{"japanese":"うなぎより穴子のほうが、あっさりしています。","romaji":"Unagi yori anago no hou ga, assari shite imasu.","german":"Conger eel is lighter than freshwater eel.","furigana":"あなご"}],"notes":"穴子 is the \"conger eel\" (sea eel), named for hiding in holes (穴). On menus it is often written アナゴ in katakana. Popular as 穴子の天ぷら and as sushi with sweet sauce. Do not confuse it with うなぎ, the fattier freshwater eel, usually eaten grilled as うな重.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このチョコレート、くつ下みたいに臭い。","romaji":"Kono chokoreeto, kutsushita mitai ni kusai.","german":"This chocolate stinks like socks.","furigana":"した|くさ"},{"japanese":"ゴミが臭いです。","romaji":"Gomi ga kusai desu.","german":"The garbage smells bad.","furigana":"くさ"},{"japanese":"あの人の話、なんか臭いな。","romaji":"Ano hito no hanashi, nanka kusai na.","german":"What he's saying smells fishy somehow.","furigana":"ひと|はなし|くさ"}],"notes":"臭い (くさい) is an い-adjective for \"stinking, foul-smelling\": ゴミが臭い, 足が臭い. Colloquially it also means \"suspicious, fishy\" (あの話は臭い). As a suffix, 〜くさい forms words such as 面倒くさい \"troublesome\" or 古くさい \"old-fashioned\". Note: the same kanji read as a noun, におい, means \"smell, stench\" (臭いがする); pleasant smells are written 匂い.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"僕も裕福な家庭に生まれたかったな。","romaji":"Boku mo yūfuku na katei ni umaretakatta na.","german":"I wish I had been born into a wealthy family too.","furigana":"ぼく|ゆうふく|かてい|う"},{"japanese":"あの町には、裕福な人がたくさん住んでいます。","romaji":"Ano machi ni wa, yuufuku na hito ga takusan sunde imasu.","german":"Many wealthy people live in that town.","furigana":"まち|ゆうふく|ひと|す"},{"japanese":"昔は裕福ではありませんでしたが、毎日楽しかったです。","romaji":"Mukashi wa yuufuku de wa arimasen deshita ga, mainichi tanoshikatta desu.","german":"We weren't well-off in the past, but every day was fun.","furigana":"むかし|ゆうふく|まいにちたの"}],"notes":"裕福 is a な-adjective meaning \"wealthy, well-off\" and sounds somewhat more refined than お金持ち. Typical: 裕福な家庭 / 家に生まれる, 裕福に暮らす. Negated: 裕福ではない. In contrast to 豊か (\"rich in something\", also nature or feelings: 自然が豊か), 裕福 refers only to money and standard of living.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このコミュニティフォーラムでは、ユーザー同士のトラブル防止のため、DMは禁止にしています。","romaji":"Kono komyuniti fōramu de wa, yūzā dōshi no toraburu bōshi no tame, dī emu wa kinshi ni shite imasu.","german":"On this community forum, DMs are prohibited to prevent trouble between users.","furigana":"どうし|ぼうし|きんし"},{"japanese":"事故を防止するために気をつけましょう。","romaji":"Jiko o bōshi suru tame ni ki o tsukemashō.","german":"Let's be careful in order to prevent accidents.","furigana":"じこ|ぼうし|き"}],"notes":"防止 is the prevention of an undesirable event. As a noun it appears in トラブル防止のため; with する it becomes the transitive verb \"to prevent\": 事故を防止する. The standalone form on this card is the noun. It refers to preventing an event, not to solving it afterwards.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地元のゴーストタウン化を防ぐためにも、ゼッタイにこのビジネスを成功させます。","romaji":"Jimoto no goosuto taun-ka o fusegu tame ni mo, zettai ni kono bijinesu o seikou sasemasu.","german":"I will definitely make this business a success, also to prevent my hometown from turning into a ghost town.","furigana":"じもと|か|ふせ|せいこう"},{"japanese":"日焼けを防ぐために帽子をかぶります。","romaji":"Hiyake o fusegu tame ni bōshi o kaburimasu.","german":"I wear a hat to prevent sunburn.","furigana":"ひや|ふせ|ぼうし"},{"japanese":"風邪を防ぐには、手を洗うのが一番です。","romaji":"Kaze o fusegu ni wa, te o arau no ga ichiban desu.","german":"The best way to prevent colds is to wash your hands.","furigana":"かぜ|ふせ|て|あら|いちばん"}],"notes":"防ぐ is transitive: one \"prevents\" or \"fends off\" what would cause harm - the unwanted thing takes を: 事故を防ぐ, 日焼けを防ぐ, 風邪を防ぐ. Very common is 〜を防ぐため(に) \"in order to prevent ...\". Unlike 守る (to protect something valuable: 子どもを守る), 防ぐ is directed against the danger itself. The noun \"prevention\" is 予防.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私、仕事で困難に直面すると、ワクワクするんです。","romaji":"Watashi, shigoto de konnan ni chokumen suru to, wakuwaku suru n desu.","german":"I get excited when I face difficulties at work.","furigana":"わたし|しごと|こんなん|ちょくめん"},{"japanese":"困難を乗り越えましょう。","romaji":"Konnan o norikoemashou.","german":"Let's overcome the difficulties.","furigana":"こんなん|の|こ"},{"japanese":"大雪で、車での移動は困難です。","romaji":"Ooyuki de, kuruma de no idou wa konnan desu.","german":"Because of the heavy snow, traveling by car is difficult.","furigana":"おおゆき|くるま|いどう|こんなん"}],"notes":"困難 is used as a noun \"difficulty, hardship\" and as a な-adjective \"difficult\", but sounds more formal than 難しい. Typical: 困難に直面する \"to face difficulties\", 困難を乗り越える \"to overcome difficulties\", 〜は困難だ \"... is hardly possible\". In bureaucratic language, 困難です is often a polite way of saying \"it can't be done\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その記事の内容には、たくさんの非難の声が上がりました。","romaji":"Sono kiji no naiyō ni wa, takusan no hinan no koe ga agarimashita.","german":"Many voices of criticism were raised about the content of that article.","furigana":"きじ|ないよう|ひなん|こえ|あ"},{"japanese":"彼の行動が非難されました。","romaji":"Kare no kōdō ga hinan saremashita.","german":"His actions were condemned.","furigana":"かれ|こうどう|ひなん"},{"japanese":"人を非難する前に、自分のことを考えてください。","romaji":"Hito o hinan suru mae ni, jibun no koto o kangaete kudasai.","german":"Before you blame others, think about yourself.","furigana":"ひと|ひなん|まえ|じぶん|かんが"}],"notes":"非難 is sharp \"criticism, reproach, condemnation\" - one reproaches someone morally. As a transitive する-verb: 人を非難する; passive 非難される, often also 非難を浴びる \"to be heavily criticized\" and 非難の声が上がる. 批判, by contrast, is objective, also constructive criticism. Note: pronounced the same is 避難 \"evacuation, fleeing to safety\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の専門は文化人類学です。","romaji":"Watashi no senmon wa bunka jinruigaku desu.","german":"My major is cultural anthropology.","furigana":"わたし|せんもん|ぶんかじんるいがく"},{"japanese":"大学で人類学を勉強しています。","romaji":"Daigaku de jinruigaku o benkyō shite imasu.","german":"I'm studying anthropology at university.","furigana":"だいがく|じんるいがく|べんきょう"},{"japanese":"人類学の授業で、昔の人の生活について学びました。","romaji":"Jinruigaku no jugyou de, mukashi no hito no seikatsu ni tsuite manabimashita.","german":"In anthropology class, we learned about the lives of people in the past.","furigana":"じんるいがく|じゅぎょう|むかし|ひと|せいかつ|まな"}],"notes":"人類学 is made up of 人類 \"humankind\" and 〜学 \"science, study\": \"anthropology\". It is common in names of fields such as 文化人類学 \"cultural anthropology\"; the researcher is called 人類学者. Typical: 人類学を勉強する / 専攻する. Related fields are 考古学 \"archaeology\" and 社会学 \"sociology\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"朝からカツカレーなんて、胸焼けするわ！","romaji":"Asa kara katsu karē nante, muneyake suru wa!","german":"Katsu curry for breakfast gives me heartburn!","furigana":"あさ|むねや"},{"japanese":"食べ過ぎて胸焼けがします。","romaji":"Tabesugite muneyake ga shimasu.","german":"I have heartburn from eating too much.","furigana":"た|す|むねや"},{"japanese":"胸焼けがひどいので、胃薬を飲みました。","romaji":"Muneyake ga hidoi node, igusuri o nomimashita.","german":"I had bad heartburn, so I took stomach medicine.","furigana":"むねや|いぐすり|の"}],"notes":"胸焼け (literally \"chest burning\", also written 胸やけ) is \"heartburn\". One says 胸焼けがする or, as an intransitive verb, 胸焼けする. Colloquially it is also used to express that very greasy or sweet food makes you feel sick just by looking at it. The remedy is 胃薬 \"stomach medicine\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今って、ネットとリアルはほぼ不可分になってしまっていますよね？","romaji":"Ima tte, netto to riaru wa hobo fukabun ni natte shimatte imasu yo ne?","german":"These days, the internet and the real world have become almost inseparable, haven't they?","furigana":"いま|ふかぶん"},{"japanese":"この二つの問題は不可分です。","romaji":"Kono futatsu no mondai wa fukabun desu.","german":"These two problems are inseparable.","furigana":"ふた|もんだい|ふかぶん"},{"japanese":"言葉と文化は、不可分の関係にあります。","romaji":"Kotoba to bunka wa, fukabun no kankei ni arimasu.","german":"Language and culture are inseparably linked.","furigana":"ことば|ぶんか|ふかぶん|かんけい"}],"notes":"不可分 (不 \"not\" + 可 \"possible\" + 分 \"to divide\") means \"inseparable, indivisible\". It is a literary word, typical in set patterns: AとBは不可分の関係にある \"A and B are inseparably linked\", 不可分になる. In conversation one rather says 切り離せない or 分けられない.","levelNote":"Compound: 不可 (N3) + 分 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"漢字の音訓の見分け方を教えてください。","romaji":"Kanji no onkun no miwakekata o oshiete kudasai.","german":"Please tell me how to tell on readings from kun readings in kanji.","furigana":"かんじ|おんくん|みわ|かた|おし"},{"japanese":"漢字には音訓の読みがあります。","romaji":"Kanji ni wa onkun no yomi ga arimasu.","german":"Kanji have on and kun readings.","furigana":"かんじ|おんくん|よ"},{"japanese":"この辞書では、音訓から漢字を探すことができます。","romaji":"Kono jisho de wa, onkun kara kanji o sagasu koto ga dekimasu.","german":"In this dictionary, you can look up kanji by their readings.","furigana":"じしょ|おんくん|かんじ|さが"}],"notes":"音訓 combines the two types of kanji readings: 音読み (the reading adopted from Chinese) and 訓読み (the Japanese reading). The word is a technical term from dictionaries and teaching, as in 音訓索引 \"index by readings\" or 音訓の区別. In conversation one usually names 音読み and 訓読み separately.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の近所に住む人達は、パーティが大好きな人達ばかりです。","romaji":"Watashi no kinjo ni sumu hitotachi wa, paatii ga daisuki na hitotachi bakari desu.","german":"The people who live in my neighborhood are all people who love parties."},{"japanese":"あの人達は親切です。","romaji":"Ano hitotachi wa shinsetsu desu.","german":"Those people are kind.","furigana":"ひとたち|しんせつ"},{"japanese":"困っている人達を助けたいです。","romaji":"Komatte iru hitotachi o tasuketai desu.","german":"I want to help people who are in trouble.","furigana":"こま|ひとたち|たす"}],"notes":"人達 is the plural form of 人, \"people\", and is mostly written 人たち, because 達 as a plural suffix is usually in kana. It almost always needs a modifier: あの人たち, 若い人たち, 困っている人たち. More polite is 方々 (あの方々), more general and written is 人々.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"京都旅行で行った小料理屋では、静々と流れる小川を見下ろしながら、アユの塩焼きをいただきました。","romaji":"Kyōto ryokō de itta koryōriya de wa, shizushizu to nagareru ogawa o mioroshinagara, ayu no shioyaki o itadakimashita.","german":"At a small restaurant on my Kyoto trip, I enjoyed salt-grilled sweetfish while looking down at a quietly flowing stream.","furigana":"きょうとりょこう|い|こりょうりや|しずしず|なが|おがわ|みお|しおや"},{"japanese":"静々と部屋を出ました。","romaji":"Shizushizu to heya o demashita.","german":"I left the room quietly and slowly.","furigana":"しずしず|へや|で"}],"notes":"静々 is usually written しずしず and describes a calm, slow or dignified movement. It often takes と before the verb: 静々と部屋を出る. The expression has a narrative feel; it describes the manner of movement and not merely the absence of noise.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"意見を言っただけなのに、上司に「文句を言うな」と怒鳴られました。","romaji":"Iken o itta dake na noni, jōshi ni \"monku o iu na\" to donararemashita.","german":"I only gave my opinion, but my boss yelled at me, \"Stop complaining!\"","furigana":"いけん|い|じょうし|もんく|い|どな"},{"japanese":"いつも文句を言わないでください。","romaji":"Itsumo monku o iwanaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't always complain.","furigana":"もんく|い"}],"notes":"文句を言う means to complain or grumble. 文句 here is the noun \"complaint\" with を; only 言う is conjugated: 文句を言います, 文句を言わない. 文句を言うな is a curt prohibition. This card treats the whole combination, not the other meaning of 文句 as wording or phrasing.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 21"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この部屋への電子機器持ち込みは禁止されています。","romaji":"Kono heya e no denshi kiki mochikomi wa kinshi sarete imasu.","german":"Bringing electronic devices into this room is prohibited.","furigana":"へや|でんしききも|こ|きんし"},{"japanese":"飛行機内では電子機器の使用を控えてください。","romaji":"Hikōki nai de wa denshi kiki no shiyō o hikaete kudasai.","german":"Please refrain from using electronic devices on the airplane.","furigana":"ひこうきない|でんしきき|しよう|ひか"},{"japanese":"電子機器は水に弱いので、気をつけてください。","romaji":"Denshi kiki wa mizu ni yowai node, ki o tsukete kudasai.","german":"Electronic devices are sensitive to water, so please be careful.","furigana":"でんしきき|みず|よわ|き"}],"notes":"電子機器 (電子 \"electronics\" + 機器 \"equipment, apparatus\") is the matter-of-fact umbrella term for \"electronic devices\" such as smartphones, laptops or tablets. Typical in notices: 電子機器の使用 / 持ち込み \"use / bringing of electronic devices\". Household appliances are more often called 電化製品 or 家電; in daily life people also simply say スマホやパソコン.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"事前にGoogleマップで目的地までの経路をチェックしておいてくださいね！","romaji":"Jizen ni Google mappu de mokutekichi made no keiro o chekku shite oite kudasai ne!","german":"Please check the route to your destination on Google Maps in advance!","furigana":"じぜん|もくてきち|けいろ"},{"japanese":"最短の経路を教えてください。","romaji":"Saitan no keiro o oshiete kudasai.","german":"Please tell me the shortest route.","furigana":"さいたん|けいろ|おし"},{"japanese":"ウイルスの感染経路は、まだ分かっていません。","romaji":"Uirusu no kansen keiro wa, mada wakatte imasen.","german":"The route of infection of the virus is still not known.","furigana":"かんせんけいろ|わ"}],"notes":"経路 is an objective word for the \"route, path\" that something or someone takes, and also for the \"course\" of a process. Typical: 経路を調べる / 検索する (in navigation apps), 避難経路 \"escape route\", 感染経路 \"route of infection\". In conversation people more often say 道, 道順 or ルート.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"スマホでブラウザってどうやって閉じるんですか？","romaji":"Sumaho de burauza tte dō yatte tojiru n desu ka?","german":"How do you close the browser on a smartphone?","furigana":"と"},{"japanese":"目を閉じてください。","romaji":"Me o tojite kudasai.","german":"Please close your eyes.","furigana":"め|と"},{"japanese":"本を閉じて、先生の話を聞いてください。","romaji":"Hon o tojite, sensei no hanashi o kiite kudasai.","german":"Close your books and listen to the teacher.","furigana":"ほん|と|せんせい|はなし|き"}],"notes":"閉じる is an ichidan verb, mostly transitive (Xを閉じる): to \"close\" eyes, books, umbrellas, apps or windows on a computer. For doors, windows and shutters, on the other hand, 閉める is usually used. Less often it is intransitive (幕が閉じる \"the curtain falls\") or means \"to end\" (会を閉じる). The opposite is 開く (ひらく).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は、読者にとって分かりやすい文章を書くことを心がけています。","romaji":"Kare wa, dokusha ni totte wakariyasui bunshou o kaku koto o kokorogakete imasu.","german":"He makes a point of writing text that is easy for readers to understand.","furigana":"かれ|どくしゃ|わ|ぶんしょう|か|こころ"},{"japanese":"この小説には多くの読者がいます。","romaji":"Kono shōsetsu ni wa ōku no dokusha ga imasu.","german":"This novel has many readers.","furigana":"しょうせつ|おお|どくしゃ"},{"japanese":"この雑誌の読者は、ほとんどが二十代の女性です。","romaji":"Kono zasshi no dokusha wa, hotondo ga nijuudai no josei desu.","german":"Most readers of this magazine are women in their twenties.","furigana":"ざっし|どくしゃ|にじゅうだい|じょせい"}],"notes":"読者 is the \"reader\" of a book, newspaper or magazine, usually as a group or target audience: 読者が多い, 若い読者, 読者の声 \"letters from readers, readers' opinions\". A loyal reader is 愛読者. 読み手 sounds more abstract (as the counterpart of 書き手 \"writer\"); someone who is reading right now is simply 読んでいる人.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本在留のアメリカ人と友達になりました。","romaji":"Nippon zairyū no Amerikajin to tomodachi ni narimashita.","german":"I became friends with an American residing in Japan.","furigana":"にっぽんざいりゅう|じん|ともだち"},{"japanese":"在留カードを更新しました。","romaji":"Zairyū kādo o kōshin shimashita.","german":"I renewed my residence card.","furigana":"ざいりゅう|こうしん"},{"japanese":"在留期間が切れる前に、入管で手続きをしてください。","romaji":"Zairyuu kikan ga kireru mae ni, nyuukan de tetsuzuki o shite kudasai.","german":"Please complete the procedure at the immigration office before your period of stay expires.","furigana":"ざいりゅうきかん|き|まえ|にゅうかん|てつづ"}],"notes":"在留 is the official term for \"residence\", especially the legal residence of foreigners: 在留カード \"residence card\", 在留資格 \"status of residence\", 在留期間 \"period of stay\". As an intransitive する-verb: 日本に在留する. Similar is 在住 \"resident of\" (東京在住); in daily life people simply say 住む.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ヴァンパイアは人の心を自在にコントロールすることができる。","romaji":"Vanpaia wa hito no kokoro o jizai ni kontorooru suru koto ga dekiru.","german":"Vampires can control people's minds at will.","furigana":"ひと|こころ|じざい"},{"japanese":"彼は三カ国語を自在に操ります。","romaji":"Kare wa sankakokugo o jizai ni ayatsurimasu.","german":"He commands three languages with ease.","furigana":"かれ|さん|こくご|じざい|あやつ"},{"japanese":"このカメラは、角度を自在に変えられます。","romaji":"Kono kamera wa, kakudo o jizai ni kaeraremasu.","german":"This camera can change its angle freely.","furigana":"かくど|じざい|か"}],"notes":"自在 means \"at will, freely and effortlessly\" and almost always appears adverbially as 自在に + verb: 自在に操る \"to master skillfully\", 自在に変える \"to change at will\". Much more common than the single word is the four-character 自由自在 \"completely at will\". In contrast to 自由 (\"free, freedom\"), 自在 emphasizes the ability to fully control something.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"列の最後尾はこちらです。","romaji":"Retsu no saikoubi wa kochira desu.","german":"The end of the line is here.","furigana":"れつ|さいこうび"},{"japanese":"列の最後尾に並んでください。","romaji":"Retsu no saikōbi ni narande kudasai.","german":"Please line up at the end of the line.","furigana":"れつ|さいこうび|なら"},{"japanese":"電車の最後尾の車両は、空いていることが多いです。","romaji":"Densha no saikoubi no sharyou wa, suite iru koto ga ooi desu.","german":"The last car of the train is often empty.","furigana":"でんしゃ|さいこうび|しゃりょう|す|おお"}],"notes":"最後尾 (最後 \"last\" + 尾 \"tail\") is the \"rearmost end\" of a row, above all a waiting line or a train. At events, staff carry signs saying 最後尾 \"end of the line\"; you queue up with 最後尾に並ぶ. The opposite is 先頭 \"head, front end\". In daily life people also say 列の一番後ろ.","levelNote":"Compound: 最後 (N4) + 尾 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"議長、今日の議題は何ですか？","romaji":"Gichō, kyō no gidai wa nani desu ka?","german":"Mr. Chairman, what is on today's agenda?","furigana":"ぎちょう|きょう|ぎだい|なに"},{"japanese":"議長が会議を開きました。","romaji":"Gichō ga kaigi o hirakimashita.","german":"The chairman opened the meeting.","furigana":"ぎちょう|かいぎ|ひら"},{"japanese":"彼女は、生徒会の会議で議長に選ばれました。","romaji":"Kanojo wa, seitokai no kaigi de gichou ni erabaremashita.","german":"She was elected chairperson at the student council meeting.","furigana":"かのじょ|せいとかい|かいぎ|ぎちょう|えら"}],"notes":"議長 is the \"chairperson\" of a meeting or assembly, the person who leads the discussion: 議長を務める \"to preside, to chair\", 議長に選ばれる. The title is also used for presidents of parliaments (衆議院議長). Do not confuse it with 会長 (head of a club or corporation), 委員長 (committee chair) and 司会 (host or emcee of an event). 議長 can be used directly as a form of address.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"履歴書には、学校の名前を「高等学校」まで正しく書きましょう。","romaji":"Rirekisho ni wa, gakkou no namae o \"koutou gakkou\" made tadashiku kakimashou.","german":"On your résumé, write the school's name correctly, all the way to \"高等学校\".","furigana":"りれきしょ|がっこう|なまえ|こうとうがっこう|ただ|か"},{"japanese":"高等学校を卒業しました。","romaji":"Kōtō gakkō o sotsugyō shimashita.","german":"I graduated from high school.","furigana":"こうとうがっこう|そつぎょう"},{"japanese":"日本では、中学校を卒業すると、ほとんどの人が高等学校に進みます。","romaji":"Nihon de wa, chuugakkou o sotsugyou suru to, hotondo no hito ga koutou gakkou ni susumimasu.","german":"In Japan, after graduating from junior high school, most people go on to high school.","furigana":"にほん|ちゅうがっこう|そつぎょう|ひと|こうとうがっこう|すす"}],"notes":"高等学校 is the official name of the three-year \"high school\" after junior high school (中学校), roughly grades 10 to 12. In daily life people almost always use the short form 高校 (高校生 \"high school student\"); the full form appears in school names, résumés and documents. Typical: 高等学校を卒業する, 高等学校に進む / 入学する.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"喉飴持ってない？","romaji":"Nodoame motte nai?","german":"Do you have a cough drop?","furigana":"のどあめも"},{"japanese":"喉が痛いので喉飴を舐めます。","romaji":"Nodo ga itai node nodoame o namemasu.","german":"My throat hurts, so I'll suck on a cough drop.","furigana":"のど|いた|のどあめ|な"},{"japanese":"このハーブの喉飴は、すっきりした味がします。","romaji":"Kono haabu no nodoame wa, sukkiri shita aji ga shimasu.","german":"This herbal cough drop has a refreshing taste.","furigana":"のどあめ|あじ"}],"notes":"喉飴 (usually written のど飴) is a \"throat lozenge, cough drop\", from 喉 \"throat\" and 飴 \"candy\". Candies are sucked, hence 喉飴をなめる and not 食べる. People take them when 喉が痛い or with a scratchy throat (喉がイガイガする), for example before karaoke.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いつ産まれる予定なの？","romaji":"Itsu umareru yotei na no?","german":"When is the baby due?","furigana":"う|よてい"},{"japanese":"赤ちゃんが産まれました。","romaji":"Akachan ga umaremashita.","german":"A baby was born.","furigana":"あか|う"},{"japanese":"この子犬は先月産まれたばかりです。","romaji":"Kono koinu wa sengetsu umareta bakari desu.","german":"This puppy was born only last month.","furigana":"こいぬ|せんげつう"}],"notes":"産まれる is the intransitive counterpart of 産む \"to give birth\" and refers to the physical birth of a person or animal: 赤ちゃんが産まれる. The more general and more common spelling is 生まれる; only it is also used figuratively (新しいアイデアが生まれる \"a new idea arises\"). Place and time of birth take で and に respectively: 東京で生まれた, 二〇〇〇年に生まれた. The person who has the child takes に: 姉に男の子が産まれた \"my sister had a boy\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その町なら、大阪経由でも京都経由でも行けますよ。","romaji":"Sono machi nara, Oosaka keiyu de mo Kyouto keiyu de mo ikemasu yo.","german":"If it's that town, you can get there either via Osaka or via Kyoto.","furigana":"まち|おおさかけいゆ|きょうとけいゆ|い"},{"japanese":"名古屋経由で大阪に行きます。","romaji":"Nagoya keiyu de Ōsaka ni ikimasu.","german":"I'm going to Osaka via Nagoya.","furigana":"なごやけいゆ|おおさか|い"},{"japanese":"チケットは旅行会社経由で予約しました。","romaji":"Chiketto wa ryokougaisha keiyu de yoyaku shimashita.","german":"I booked the tickets through a travel agency.","furigana":"りょこうがいしゃけいゆ|よやく"}],"notes":"経由 means \"via, by way of\" and comes directly after a place name: 名古屋経由で大阪へ行く. As a する-verb it is 〜を経由して: ドバイを経由してパリへ行く. By extension it also names the route through an office or an intermediary: 旅行会社経由で予約する \"to book through a travel agency\". Unlike 〜を通って \"passing through ...\", 経由 emphasizes the intermediate stop of a route; it is typical in timetables, flight information and business language.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あ、トイレットペーパー、もうすぐ無くなるよ。","romaji":"A, toirettopēpā, mōsugu nakunaru yo.","german":"Oh, we're almost out of toilet paper.","furigana":"な"},{"japanese":"財布が無くなりました。どこかで落としたかもしれません。","romaji":"Saifu ga nakunarimashita. Dokoka de otoshita kamo shiremasen.","german":"My wallet is gone. I may have dropped it somewhere.","furigana":"さいふ|な|お"},{"japanese":"薬を飲んだら、頭の痛みが無くなりました。","romaji":"Kusuri o nondara, atama no itami ga nakunarimashita.","german":"After I took the medicine, the headache went away.","furigana":"くすり|の|あたま|いた|な"}],"notes":"無くなる means that something \"runs out\" (supplies, time), \"gets lost\" or \"disappears\" (pain, desire): 時間が無くなる, 財布が無くなった, 痛みが無くなる. It is mostly written in kana: なくなる. The transitive counterpart is 無くす \"to lose\" (財布を無くす). Do not confuse with 亡くなる \"to pass away\" and with the ending 〜なくなる \"no longer ...\" (食べられなくなる), which consists of ない＋なる.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 23"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"手を繋いで歩きましょう。","romaji":"Te o tsunaide arukimashou.","german":"Let's walk holding hands.","furigana":"て|つな|ある"},{"japanese":"パソコンをテレビに繋いで、写真を見ました。","romaji":"Pasokon o terebi ni tsunaide, shashin o mimashita.","german":"I connected the computer to the TV and looked at photos.","furigana":"つな|しゃしん|み"},{"japanese":"担当の者にお繋ぎします。少々お待ちください。","romaji":"Tantou no mono ni otsunagi shimasu. Shoushou omachi kudasai.","german":"I will put you through to the person in charge. Please wait a moment.","furigana":"たんとう|もの|つな|しょうしょう|ま"}],"notes":"繋ぐ means \"to connect, to tie up\" and is mostly written in kana: つなぐ. Typical uses are 手を繋ぐ \"to hold hands\", AをBに繋ぐ \"to connect A to B\" (devices, cables, also tying a dog to a post) and, on the phone, 担当者にお繋ぎします \"I'll put you through to the person in charge\". The intransitive counterpart is 繋がる: 電話が繋がらない \"I can't get through\", インターネットに繋がる \"to be connected to the internet\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地元が水害で大変なことになっているようだ。","romaji":"Jimoto ga suigai de taihen na koto ni natte iru yō da.","german":"It seems my hometown is in serious trouble because of flood damage.","furigana":"じもと|すいがい|たいへん"},{"japanese":"台風で水害が発生しました。","romaji":"Taifū de suigai ga hassei shimashita.","german":"Flood damage occurred because of the typhoon.","furigana":"たいふう|すいがい|はっせい"},{"japanese":"この町は昔から水害が多いです。","romaji":"Kono machi wa mukashi kara suigai ga ooi desu.","german":"This town has had a lot of flood damage since long ago.","furigana":"まち|むかし|すいがい|おお"}],"notes":"水害 is \"flood damage\" or a \"flood disaster\", i.e. damage caused by water after heavy rain, a typhoon or rivers overflowing their banks. The flood itself is 洪水. Typical are 水害が起きる／発生する, 水害に遭う \"to be hit by a flood\" and 水害で被害を受ける. The word is matter-of-fact and is encountered mainly in news; the umbrella term is 災害 \"disaster\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"こちらに回答を入力してください。","romaji":"Kochira ni kaitō o nyūryoku shite kudasai.","german":"Please enter your answer here.","furigana":"かいとう|にゅうりょく"},{"japanese":"アンケートに回答してください。","romaji":"Ankēto ni kaitō shite kudasai.","german":"Please respond to the survey.","furigana":"かいとう"},{"japanese":"会社からの回答を待っています。","romaji":"Kaisha kara no kaitou o matte imasu.","german":"I'm waiting for a reply from the company.","furigana":"かいしゃ|かいとう|ま"}],"notes":"回答 is the \"answer\" to a survey, inquiry or official question: アンケートに回答する, 質問に回答する, 回答を待つ. It sounds more matter-of-fact than the everyday 答え. Do not confuse with the identically read 解答 \"solution\" to an exam problem. For the response to a letter, an invitation or a call, 返事 is said.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 45"},
{"notes":"夫 is the neutral term for one's own husband when talking about him with others, and the standard word in forms and news. 主人 sounds more traditional (literally \"master of the house\"), 旦那 colloquial. Another person's husband is politely called ご主人 or 旦那さん, not 夫. The married couple is 夫婦.","examples":[{"japanese":"夫は今、仕事中です。","romaji":"Otto wa ima, shigotochū desu.","german":"My husband is at work right now.","furigana":"おっと|いま|しごとちゅう"},{"japanese":"夫と家事を分けています。","romaji":"Otto to kaji o wakete imasu.","german":"My husband and I share the housework.","furigana":"おっと|かじ|わ"},{"japanese":"夫の両親は北海道に住んでいます。","romaji":"Otto no ryoushin wa Hokkaidou ni sunde imasu.","german":"My husband's parents live in Hokkaido.","furigana":"おっと|りょうしん|ほっかいどう|す"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"妻 is the neutral term for one's own wife toward others and the standard word in forms. 家内 sounds older and humble, 嫁 is colloquial and actually means \"daughter-in-law\". Another person's wife is politely called 奥さん or 奥様, not 妻. In a matter-of-fact way, 夫と妻 stand side by side.","examples":[{"japanese":"妻は図書館で働いています。","romaji":"Tsuma wa toshokan de hataraite imasu.","german":"My wife works at a library.","furigana":"つま|としょかん|はたら"},{"japanese":"妻と旅行の計画を立てました。","romaji":"Tsuma to ryokō no keikaku o tatemashita.","german":"I made travel plans with my wife.","furigana":"つま|りょこう|けいかく|た"},{"japanese":"妻の誕生日に花を買いました。","romaji":"Tsuma no tanjoubi ni hana o kaimashita.","german":"I bought flowers for my wife's birthday.","furigana":"つま|たんじょうび|はな|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"彼 ist das Pronomen „er“, wird im Gespräch aber sparsamer benutzt als im Deutschen: Oft nennt man einfach den Namen oder sagt あの人. Für Vorgesetzte oder Ältere ist 彼 unpassend. In 私の彼 oder 彼ができた bedeutet es „mein Freund“ im Sinne von Partner; umgangssprachlich häufiger ist dafür 彼氏. Die Mehrzahl lautet 彼ら.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は私と同じ大学に通っています。","romaji":"Kare wa watashi to onaji daigaku ni kayotte imasu.","german":"Er besucht dieselbe Universität wie ich.","furigana":"かれ|わたし|おな|だいがく|かよ"},{"japanese":"彼と映画を見に行きました。","romaji":"Kare to eiga o mi ni ikimashita.","german":"Ich bin mit meinem Freund ins Kino gegangen.","furigana":"かれ|えいが|み|い"},{"japanese":"田中さんですか。彼なら、もう帰りましたよ。","romaji":"Tanaka-san desu ka. Kare nara, mou kaerimashita yo.","german":"Herr Tanaka? Der ist schon nach Hause gegangen.","furigana":"たなか|かれ|かえ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 5"},
{"notes":"僕 is an \"I\" used mainly by boys and men in daily life; it sounds friendlier and softer than the rougher 俺, but less formal than 私. In meetings or job interviews men usually switch to 私. Adults sometimes address small boys as 僕 themselves (\"hey there, little guy\"). Plural: 僕たち or 僕ら.","examples":[{"japanese":"僕はサッカーが好きです。","romaji":"Boku wa sakkā ga suki desu.","german":"I like soccer.","furigana":"ぼく|す"},{"japanese":"そのかばんは僕のです。","romaji":"Sono kaban wa boku no desu.","german":"That bag is mine.","furigana":"ぼく"},{"japanese":"僕、お名前は？","romaji":"Boku, onamae wa?","german":"Hey there, what's your name?","furigana":"ぼく|なまえ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 20"},
{"notes":"君 is a familiar \"you\" among friends, toward younger people, or from superiors to subordinates; in song lyrics it is the typical \"you\". Toward superiors or strangers it sounds impolite; there you use the name with さん. Do not confuse it with the name suffix 〜君, which is read くん.","examples":[{"japanese":"君はどう思う？","romaji":"Kimi wa dō omou?","german":"What do you think?","furigana":"きみ|おも"},{"japanese":"君と話せてよかった。","romaji":"Kimi to hanasete yokatta.","german":"I'm glad I got to talk with you.","furigana":"きみ|はな"},{"japanese":"君のことが好きだ。","romaji":"Kimi no koto ga suki da.","german":"I like you.","furigana":"きみ|す"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 20"},
{"notes":"娘 refers to one's own daughter when talking about her with others: うちの娘. About another person's daughter, one politely says 娘さん or お嬢さん. In addition, 娘 can mean a \"young, unmarried woman\", for example in 村の娘; this meaning sounds rather old-fashioned today. The counterpart for sons is 息子.","examples":[{"japanese":"娘は来年、小学校に入ります。","romaji":"Musume wa rainen, shōgakkō ni hairimasu.","german":"My daughter will enter elementary school next year.","furigana":"むすめ|らいねん|しょうがっこう|はい"},{"japanese":"娘と一緒にケーキを作りました。","romaji":"Musume to issho ni kēki o tsukurimashita.","german":"I made a cake with my daughter.","furigana":"むすめ|いっしょ|つく"},{"japanese":"娘さんはおいくつですか。","romaji":"Musume-san wa oikutsu desu ka.","german":"How old is your daughter?","furigana":"むすめ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 28"},
{"notes":"子 means \"child\", but rarely stands alone; it comes with a modifier: あの子, この子, 男の子, 女の子, うちの子. Alone and neutral, 子供 is mostly said. 子 also refers to young animals (パンダの子) and colloquially to young women (かわいい子). あの子 sounds familiar and is used only for children or clearly younger people.","examples":[{"japanese":"あの子はよく本を読みます。","romaji":"Ano ko wa yoku hon o yomimasu.","german":"That child reads a lot of books.","furigana":"こ|ほん|よ"},{"japanese":"小さい子にも分かるように説明しました。","romaji":"Chiisai ko ni mo wakaru yō ni setsumei shimashita.","german":"I explained it so that even a small child could understand.","furigana":"ちい|こ|わ|せつめい"},{"japanese":"動物園でパンダの子が生まれました。","romaji":"Doubutsuen de panda no ko ga umaremashita.","german":"A baby panda was born at the zoo.","furigana":"どうぶつえん|こ|う"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"お宅 is the respectful term for the other person's home or family: お宅に伺う \"to visit you at home\", お宅の犬 \"your dog\". For one's own home, うち is used instead. As a form of address \"you\" (お宅は…), it sounds distant and sometimes cool, for example among neighbors or between companies. The fan meaning オタク developed from this form of address and is written in katakana.","examples":[{"japanese":"明日、お宅に伺ってもいいですか。","romaji":"Ashita, otaku ni ukagatte mo ii desu ka.","german":"May I visit your home tomorrow?","furigana":"あした|たく|うかが"},{"japanese":"お宅は駅から近いですか。","romaji":"Otaku wa eki kara chikai desu ka.","german":"Is your home close to the station?","furigana":"たく|えき|ちか"},{"japanese":"お宅の犬は大きいですね。","romaji":"Otaku no inu wa ookii desu ne.","german":"Your dog is big, isn't it?","furigana":"たく|いぬ|おお"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 41"},
{"notes":"ご主人 is a traditional polite term for another person's husband: \"your husband\" or \"her husband\". You can use it to ask how he is or to mention his participation. For your own husband you usually use 夫 instead of the honorific ご主人. 御主人 is the variant written more with kanji; ご主人 is also common. The meaning learned here refers to the husband, not to the also possible meaning of master of the house or employer. The examples distinguish a question about his well-being from a question about accompanying you.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"ご主人はお元気ですか。","romaji":"Goshujin wa ogenki desu ka.","german":"How is your husband?","furigana":"しゅじん|げんき"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"ご主人も一緒にいらっしゃいますか。","romaji":"Goshujin mo issho ni irasshaimasu ka.","german":"Is your husband coming along too?","furigana":"しゅじん|いっしょ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 9"},
{"notes":"会場 is the \"venue\" - the hall or grounds where something takes place: 試験会場, 結婚式の会場, コンサート会場. Typical are 会場に入る, 会場に着く and, on signs, 会場はこちら \"this way to the venue\". An ordinary meeting room is 会議室; 場所 is the general \"place\" without reference to an event.","examples":[{"japanese":"試験会場は駅の近くです。","romaji":"Shiken kaijō wa eki no chikaku desu.","german":"The exam venue is near the station.","furigana":"しけんかいじょう|えき|ちか"},{"japanese":"会場に入る前に名前を書いてください。","romaji":"Kaijō ni hairu mae ni namae o kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write your name before entering the venue.","furigana":"かいじょう|はい|まえ|なまえ|か"},{"japanese":"コンサートの会場は人でいっぱいでした。","romaji":"Konsaato no kaijou wa hito de ippai deshita.","german":"The concert venue was packed with people.","furigana":"かいじょう|ひと"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 39"},
{"notes":"帰り is the noun of 帰る and means the \"way back\" or \"return\". 帰りに means \"on the way back\" (学校の帰りに, 仕事の帰りに), 帰りの電車 is the train back. The counterpart is 行き \"way there\": 行きはバス、帰りは電車. 帰りが遅い means \"to come home late\", and with お帰りなさい one greets someone who comes home.","examples":[{"japanese":"帰りにスーパーに寄ります。","romaji":"Kaeri ni sūpā ni yorimasu.","german":"I'll stop by the supermarket on the way home.","furigana":"かえ|よ"},{"japanese":"帰りの電車は混んでいました。","romaji":"Kaeri no densha wa konde imashita.","german":"The train home was crowded.","furigana":"かえ|でんしゃ|こ"},{"japanese":"最近、夫の帰りが遅いです。","romaji":"Saikin, otto no kaeri ga osoi desu.","german":"My husband has been coming home late lately.","furigana":"さいきん|おっと|かえ|おそ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 31"},
{"notes":"一度 means \"once\" and sounds a little more refined than 一回. Typical are 一度〜たことがある \"to have ... once\", 一度も〜ない \"not a single time\", もう一度 \"once more\" and 一度に \"at once, at the same time\". As a friendly invitation, 一度遊びに来てください means \"please come visit sometime\". With temperatures, however, 一度 means \"one degree\".","examples":[{"japanese":"一度、日本に行ったことがあります。","romaji":"Ichido, Nihon ni itta koto ga arimasu.","german":"I have been to Japan once.","furigana":"いちど|にほん|い"},{"japanese":"この店には一度も来たことがありません。","romaji":"Kono mise ni wa ichido mo kita koto ga arimasen.","german":"I have never once been to this store.","furigana":"みせ|いちど|き"},{"japanese":"すみません、もう一度言ってください。","romaji":"Sumimasen, mou ichido itte kudasai.","german":"Excuse me, please say that once more.","furigana":"いちどい"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 19"},
{"notes":"以内 follows a specification of time, quantity or distance and means \"within, at most\" - the limit value is included: 三日以内に \"within three days\", 千円以内で \"for at most 1000 yen\", 駅から歩いて十分以内. With deadlines it usually takes に, with budgets で. Similar is 以下 \"... or less\"; 未満, by contrast, excludes the limit value.","examples":[{"japanese":"一時間以内に戻ります。","romaji":"Ichijikan inai ni modorimasu.","german":"I'll be back within an hour.","furigana":"いちじかんいない|もど"},{"japanese":"五百円以内で昼ご飯を買いたいです。","romaji":"Gohyaku en inai de hirugohan o kaitai desu.","german":"I want to buy lunch for 500 yen or less.","furigana":"ごひゃくえんいない|ひる|はん|か"},{"japanese":"駅から歩いて十分以内のアパートを探しています。","romaji":"Eki kara aruite juppun inai no apaato o sagashite imasu.","german":"I'm looking for an apartment within a ten-minute walk of the station.","furigana":"えき|ある|じゅっぷんいない|さが"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 33"},
{"notes":"表 (おもて) is the \"front side\" or \"outer side\", the counterpart is 裏 \"back side\": 紙の表と裏, コインの表 \"heads\". It can also mean \"outside, in front of the house\": 表で待つ, 表に出る. In the meaning \"table, chart\", the same character is read ひょう (時刻表 \"timetable\"). Figuratively, 表 stands for the official, visible side, for example in 表と裏がある人 \"a person with two faces\".","examples":[{"japanese":"紙の表に名前を書いてください。","romaji":"Kami no omote ni namae o kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write your name on the front of the paper.","furigana":"かみ|おもて|なまえ|か"},{"japanese":"店の表で待っています。","romaji":"Mise no omote de matte imasu.","german":"I'm waiting outside the shop.","furigana":"みせ|おもて|ま"},{"japanese":"コインの表が出たら、私の勝ちです。","romaji":"Koin no omote ga detara, watashi no kachi desu.","german":"If the coin comes up heads, I win.","furigana":"おもて|で|わたし|か"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 40"},
{"notes":"校長 is the head of an elementary, junior high or high school. About the person and in addressing them, 校長先生 is mostly said in school life; 校長 alone sounds matter-of-fact (letters, news) or appears in compounds such as 校長室 \"principal's office\". The head of a university is, by contrast, 学長.","examples":[{"japanese":"校長先生が体育館で話しました。","romaji":"Kōchō-sensei ga taiikukan de hanashimashita.","german":"The principal spoke in the gym.","furigana":"こうちょうせんせい|たいいくかん|はな"},{"japanese":"校長に手紙を書きました。","romaji":"Kōchō ni tegami o kakimashita.","german":"I wrote a letter to the principal.","furigana":"こうちょう|てがみ|か"},{"japanese":"校長室はどこですか。","romaji":"Kouchoushitsu wa doko desu ka.","german":"Where is the principal's office?","furigana":"こうちょうしつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"部長 is the \"department head\" in a company, above 課長 and below 社長 in the usual hierarchy. As a form of address you use the title instead of さん: 部長 or 山田部長. To customers you refer to your own boss without an honorific: 部長の山田. In schools, 部長 is also the leader of a club, such as テニス部の部長.","examples":[{"japanese":"部長に予定を確認しました。","romaji":"Buchō ni yotei o kakunin shimashita.","german":"I checked the schedule with the department head.","furigana":"ぶちょう|よてい|かくにん"},{"japanese":"部長は今、会議に出ています。","romaji":"Buchō wa ima, kaigi ni dete imasu.","german":"The department head is in a meeting right now.","furigana":"ぶちょう|いま|かいぎ|で"},{"japanese":"兄はテニス部の部長です。","romaji":"Ani wa tenisubu no buchou desu.","german":"My older brother is the captain of the tennis club.","furigana":"あに|ぶ|ぶちょう"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 18"},
{"notes":"新聞社 is the company that publishes a newspaper, i.e. a \"newspaper publisher\" or its editorial office: 新聞社に勤める, 新聞社の記者 \"newspaper reporter\". The newspaper itself is called 新聞, a book publisher is 出版社 and a TV station is テレビ局. In company names it appears as 〜新聞社.","examples":[{"japanese":"兄は新聞社で働いています。","romaji":"Ani wa shinbunsha de hataraite imasu.","german":"My older brother works at a newspaper company.","furigana":"あに|しんぶんしゃ|はたら"},{"japanese":"新聞社から電話がありました。","romaji":"Shinbunsha kara denwa ga arimashita.","german":"There was a phone call from the newspaper company.","furigana":"しんぶんしゃ|でんわ"},{"japanese":"新聞社の人が学校に話を聞きに来ました。","romaji":"Shinbunsha no hito ga gakkou ni hanashi o kiki ni kimashita.","german":"People from the newspaper came to the school to interview us.","furigana":"しんぶんしゃ|ひと|がっこう|はなし|き|き"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 26"},
{"notes":"米 is uncooked rice as a grain and foodstuff: 米を炊く \"to cook rice\", 米を研ぐ \"to wash rice\". In everyday speech the polite form お米 is usually used. Cooked rice is called ご飯, in a Western restaurant ライス; the rice plant is called 稲. As an abbreviation for \"USA\" the same character is read べい (米国).","examples":[{"japanese":"米を洗ってから炊きます。","romaji":"Kome o aratte kara takimasu.","german":"I wash the rice before cooking it.","furigana":"こめ|あら|た"},{"japanese":"この米は日本で作られました。","romaji":"Kono kome wa Nihon de tsukuraremashita.","german":"This rice was grown in Japan.","furigana":"こめ|にほん|つく"},{"japanese":"スーパーでお米を五キロ買いました。","romaji":"Suupaa de okome o go kiro kaimashita.","german":"I bought five kilos of rice at the supermarket.","furigana":"こめ|ご|か"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 37"},
{"notes":"毛 refers to hair in general, animal fur, wool and feathers: 猫の毛, 毛のセーター \"wool sweater\". Hair on the head is called 髪 or 髪の毛. Typical phrases are 毛が抜ける \"hair falls out\" and 毛が生える \"hair grows\". For people, 毛 alone sounds rather physical and matter-of-fact; for hairstyle and haircut, use 髪.","examples":[{"japanese":"この猫の毛は柔らかいです。","romaji":"Kono neko no ke wa yawarakai desu.","german":"This cat's fur is soft.","furigana":"ねこ|け|やわ"},{"japanese":"服に犬の毛が付いています。","romaji":"Fuku ni inu no ke ga tsuite imasu.","german":"Dog hair is stuck to my clothes.","furigana":"ふく|いぬ|け|つ"},{"japanese":"最近、髪の毛がよく抜けます。","romaji":"Saikin, kami no ke ga yoku nukemasu.","german":"Lately a lot of my hair has been falling out.","furigana":"さいきん|かみ|け|ぬ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"小鳥 means \"small birds\", especially songbirds such as sparrows or pet birds such as budgerigars. Typical phrases are 小鳥が鳴く \"birds chirp\", 小鳥を飼う \"to keep a bird\" and 小鳥にえさをあげる. The general term is 鳥; 小鳥 sounds friendly and a little cute. Chicks, on the other hand, are called ひな.","examples":[{"japanese":"庭で小鳥が鳴いています。","romaji":"Niwa de kotori ga naite imasu.","german":"Small birds are singing in the garden.","furigana":"にわ|ことり|な"},{"japanese":"窓から小鳥が見えました。","romaji":"Mado kara kotori ga miemashita.","german":"I could see a small bird from the window.","furigana":"まど|ことり|み"},{"japanese":"毎朝、小鳥にえさをあげています。","romaji":"Maiasa, kotori ni esa o agete imasu.","german":"I feed the small birds every morning.","furigana":"まいあさ|ことり"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"力 is \"strength\" in both the physical and the figurative sense: 力が強い, 力を入れる \"to put effort in, to give it your all\", 力を合わせる \"to join forces\". It also means \"ability\" (日本語の力 \"Japanese skills\"), and 力になる means \"to help someone, to be a support\". As the second part of compounds it is usually read りょく: 能力, 体力.","examples":[{"japanese":"この箱を持つには力が必要です。","romaji":"Kono hako o motsu ni wa chikara ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"You need strength to carry this box.","furigana":"はこ|も|ちから|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"皆で力を合わせましょう。","romaji":"Mina de chikara o awasemashō.","german":"Let's join forces, everyone.","furigana":"みな|ちから|あ"},{"japanese":"困ったときは、いつでも力になりますよ。","romaji":"Komatta toki wa, itsudemo chikara ni narimasu yo.","german":"Whenever you're in trouble, I'm always here to help.","furigana":"こま|ちから"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 28"},
{"notes":"線 is a \"line\" or a \"stroke\": 線を引く \"to draw a line\", 白い線の内側 on a station platform. As a suffix it denotes railway and transport lines: 山手線 \"Yamanote Line\", 二番線 \"Track 2\". It also stands for wires and cables (電話の線). For a line of people, use 列 instead.","examples":[{"japanese":"赤い線の後ろで待ってください。","romaji":"Akai sen no ushiro de matte kudasai.","german":"Please wait behind the red line.","furigana":"あか|せん|うし|ま"},{"japanese":"紙にまっすぐな線を引きます。","romaji":"Kami ni massugu na sen o hikimasu.","german":"I draw a straight line on the paper.","furigana":"かみ|せん|ひ"},{"japanese":"次の駅で山手線に乗り換えてください。","romaji":"Tsugi no eki de Yamanote-sen ni norikaete kudasai.","german":"Please change to the Yamanote Line at the next station.","furigana":"つぎ|えき||の|か"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 34"},
{"notes":"点 has several basic meanings: a \"dot\" as a mark, the \"score\" on tests (九十点を取る, 百点満点), \"points\" or goals in a game, and, figuratively, an \"aspect\": この点 \"in this respect\", いい点 \"good point, merit\". For the score alone, 点数 is also used. As a counter, 点 also counts items and articles, for example in a shop (三点で千円).","examples":[{"japanese":"試験で九十点を取りました。","romaji":"Shiken de kyūjūten o torimashita.","german":"I got 90 points on the exam.","furigana":"しけん|きゅうじゅうてん|と"},{"japanese":"分からない点を質問してください。","romaji":"Wakaranai ten o shitsumon shite kudasai.","german":"Please ask about the points you don't understand.","furigana":"わ|てん|しつもん"},{"japanese":"サッカーの試合で二点入れました。","romaji":"Sakkaa no shiai de niten iremashita.","german":"I scored two goals in the soccer match.","furigana":"しあい|にてんい"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 45"},
{"notes":"別 means \"different, separate\". Before a noun, 別の is used: 別の日 \"another day\", 別の人. 別々に means \"separately from each other\" (別々に払う \"to pay separately\"). Very common is 別に with a negative: 別に問題ない \"no particular problem\"; a curt 別に… as an answer can sound dismissive. As a suffix, 〜別 sorts by category: 国別 \"by country\".","examples":[{"japanese":"別の日に会いましょう。","romaji":"Betsu no hi ni aimashō.","german":"Let's meet on another day.","furigana":"べつ|ひ|あ"},{"japanese":"これは別の問題です。","romaji":"Kore wa betsu no mondai desu.","german":"That is a different problem.","furigana":"べつ|もんだい"},{"japanese":"お会計は別々にお願いします。","romaji":"Okaikei wa betsubetsu ni onegai shimasu.","german":"Separate checks, please.","furigana":"かいけい|べつべつ|ねが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"変 is a な-adjective: 変な音, 変な味, この文は変だ. It means \"strange, odd\" and often has a slightly negative tone; said about people (変な人) it can sound derogatory. As an adverb it is 変に \"in a strange way\". Do not confuse it with 辺 \"vicinity\"; 大変 also has a different meaning (\"terrible, very\").","examples":[{"japanese":"外で変な音がしました。","romaji":"Soto de hen na oto ga shimashita.","german":"I heard a strange noise outside.","furigana":"そと|へん|おと"},{"japanese":"この文章は少し変です。","romaji":"Kono bunshō wa sukoshi hen desu.","german":"This sentence is a little odd.","furigana":"ぶんしょう|すこ|へん"},{"japanese":"この牛乳、ちょっと変な味がする。","romaji":"Kono gyuunyuu, chotto hen na aji ga suru.","german":"This milk tastes a bit strange.","furigana":"ぎゅうにゅう|へん|あじ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 22"},
{"notes":"昔 refers to a time far in the past, \"in the old days\" or \"long ago\": 昔の友達, 昔はよく泳いだ, 昔から \"since time immemorial\". Fairy tales begin with 昔々 \"Once upon a time\". For the more recent past (\"a moment ago, the other day\") 前 or この前 fit better. As a time adverb, 昔 stands at the start of the sentence without a particle.","examples":[{"japanese":"昔、この町に住んでいました。","romaji":"Mukashi, kono machi ni sunde imashita.","german":"I used to live in this town long ago.","furigana":"むかし|まち|す"},{"japanese":"祖父から昔の話を聞きました。","romaji":"Sofu kara mukashi no hanashi o kikimashita.","german":"I heard stories about the old days from my grandfather.","furigana":"そふ|むかし|はなし|き"},{"japanese":"昔々、ある所におじいさんとおばあさんがいました。","romaji":"Mukashi mukashi, aru tokoro ni ojiisan to obaasan ga imashita.","german":"Once upon a time, there lived an old man and an old woman.","furigana":"むかしむかし|ところ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 27"},
{"notes":"昼間 is the bright part of the day from morning to evening, as opposed to 夜: 昼間は暖かい. 昼, by contrast, rather means noon or lunch (昼ご飯). 日中 is somewhat more formal. 昼間から stresses that something already happens during the day, often with mild disapproval or a wink: 昼間からお酒を飲む.","examples":[{"japanese":"昼間は家にいません。","romaji":"Hiruma wa ie ni imasen.","german":"I'm not home during the day.","furigana":"ひるま|いえ"},{"japanese":"昼間は暖かいですが、夜は寒いです。","romaji":"Hiruma wa atatakai desu ga, yoru wa samui desu.","german":"It's warm during the day, but cold at night.","furigana":"ひるま|あたた|よる|さむ"},{"japanese":"昼間からビールを飲むのは気持ちがいいですね。","romaji":"Hiruma kara biiru o nomu no wa kimochi ga ii desu ne.","german":"It feels nice to drink beer in broad daylight, doesn't it?","furigana":"ひるま|の|きも"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 27"},
{"notes":"林 is a \"grove\" or \"copse\", smaller and sparser than 森 \"forest\", often consisting of one kind of tree: 松林 \"pine grove\", 竹林 \"bamboo grove\". Typical phrases are 林の中を歩く and 林がある. The character consists of two trees (木), 森 of three. 林 is also a common family name.","examples":[{"japanese":"家の後ろに林があります。","romaji":"Ie no ushiro ni hayashi ga arimasu.","german":"There is a grove behind the house.","furigana":"いえ|うし|はやし"},{"japanese":"林の中を歩きました。","romaji":"Hayashi no naka o arukimashita.","german":"I walked through the woods.","furigana":"はやし|なか|ある"},{"japanese":"秋になると、林の木が赤や黄色になります。","romaji":"Aki ni naru to, hayashi no ki ga aka ya kiiro ni narimasu.","german":"In autumn, the trees in the grove turn red and yellow.","furigana":"あき|はやし|き|あか|きいろ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"喉 is the throat seen from the inside, i.e. \"windpipe, pharynx\", and is often written in kana as のど. Important phrases are 喉が痛い \"to have a sore throat\", 喉が渇く \"to be thirsty\" (literally \"the throat dries out\") and 喉がかれる \"to become hoarse\". The neck seen from the outside, or the nape, is called 首 instead.","examples":[{"japanese":"喉が痛いので、今日は話せません。","romaji":"Nodo ga itai node, kyō wa hanasemasen.","german":"My throat hurts, so I can't talk today.","furigana":"のど|いた|きょう|はな"},{"japanese":"喉が渇きました。水をください。","romaji":"Nodo ga kawakimashita. Mizu o kudasai.","german":"I'm thirsty. Please give me some water.","furigana":"のど|かわ|みず"},{"japanese":"カラオケで歌いすぎて、喉がかれました。","romaji":"Karaoke de utaisugite, nodo ga karemashita.","german":"I sang too much at karaoke and lost my voice.","furigana":"うた|のど"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"ご存じ is the respectful counterpart of 知っている and is used only for the knowledge of others: 〜をご存じですか \"Do you know ...?\". About oneself, one answers humbly with 存じております or 存じません, never with ご存じです. The phrase ご存じのとおり \"as you know\" is common. The spelling ご存知 is just as widespread.","examples":[{"japanese":"この方をご存じですか。","romaji":"Kono kata o gozonji desu ka.","german":"Do you know this person?","furigana":"かた|ぞん"},{"japanese":"先生はその話をご存じです。","romaji":"Sensei wa sono hanashi o gozonji desu.","german":"The teacher knows that story.","furigana":"せんせい|はなし|ぞん"},{"japanese":"ご存じのとおり、来月から料金が変わります。","romaji":"Gozonji no toori, raigetsu kara ryoukin ga kawarimasu.","german":"As you know, the fees will change from next month.","furigana":"ぞん|らいげつ|りょうきん|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"引き出す literally means \"to pull out\": 箱を引き出す. In everyday life, お金を引き出す \"to withdraw money\" is very common; colloquially お金を下ろす is also said. Figuratively it means \"to bring out, to elicit\": 力を引き出す \"to bring out one's potential\", 答えを引き出す. The noun 引き出し is a \"drawer\" or a \"withdrawal\".","examples":[{"japanese":"銀行でお金を引き出しました。","romaji":"Ginkō de okane o hikidashimashita.","german":"I withdrew money at the bank.","furigana":"ぎんこう|かね|ひ|だ"},{"japanese":"机の下から箱を引き出しました。","romaji":"Tsukue no shita kara hako o hikidashimashita.","german":"I pulled a box out from under the desk.","furigana":"つくえ|した|はこ|ひ|だ"},{"japanese":"先生は生徒の力を上手に引き出します。","romaji":"Sensei wa seito no chikara o jouzu ni hikidashimasu.","german":"The teacher is good at bringing out the students' abilities.","furigana":"せんせい|せいと|ちから|じょうず|ひ|だ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"降り出す means \"to start raining or snowing\"; the subject is 雨 or 雪: 雨が降り出した. The ending 〜出す stresses a sudden, unplanned start, whereas 降り始める sounds more neutral. Typical are 今にも降り出しそうだ \"it could start any moment\" and 降り出す前に. The verb is intransitive and is also written 降りだす.","examples":[{"japanese":"帰る途中で雨が降り出しました。","romaji":"Kaeru tochū de ame ga furidashimashita.","german":"It started to rain on the way home.","furigana":"かえ|とちゅう|あめ|ふ|だ"},{"japanese":"雪が降り出す前に出発しましょう。","romaji":"Yuki ga furidasu mae ni shuppatsu shimashō.","german":"Let's leave before it starts to snow.","furigana":"ゆき|ふ|だ|まえ|しゅっぱつ"},{"japanese":"空が暗くなってきた。今にも降り出しそうだ。","romaji":"Sora ga kuraku natte kita. Ima ni mo furidashisou da.","german":"The sky is getting dark. It looks like it could start raining any moment.","furigana":"そら|くら|いま|ふ|だ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"向かう means \"to head for\" (destination with に or へ): 駅に向かう, 東京へ向かう途中. It also means \"to face, to sit opposite\": 机に向かう \"to sit down at one's desk\". 〜に向かって means \"toward, facing\". Related are 向く \"to turn, to look toward\" and the transitive 向ける \"to direct at\"; 向かい is \"opposite\".","examples":[{"japanese":"今、駅に向かっています。","romaji":"Ima, eki ni mukatte imasu.","german":"I'm on my way to the station now.","furigana":"いま|えき|む"},{"japanese":"学生は黒板に向かって座っています。","romaji":"Gakusei wa kokuban ni mukatte suwatte imasu.","german":"The students are sitting facing the blackboard.","furigana":"がくせい|こくばん|む|すわ"},{"japanese":"台風が九州に向かっています。","romaji":"Taifuu ga Kyuushuu ni mukatte imasu.","german":"A typhoon is heading toward Kyushu.","furigana":"たいふう|きゅうしゅう|む"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"申し上げる is the humble form (謙譲語) of 言う, used when addressing a statement directly to a person of higher status: お礼を申し上げる \"to express one's thanks\". 申す is plainly humble, for example with one's name: 田中と申します. In the form お／ご〜申し上げる it makes other verbs very polite: お願い申し上げます, お詫び申し上げます, typical of speeches and business letters.","examples":[{"japanese":"心からお礼を申し上げます。","romaji":"Kokoro kara orei o mōshiagemasu.","german":"I thank you from the bottom of my heart.","furigana":"こころ|れい|もう|あ"},{"japanese":"詳しいことは明日申し上げます。","romaji":"Kuwashii koto wa ashita mōshiagemasu.","german":"I will tell you the details tomorrow.","furigana":"くわ|あしたもう|あ"},{"japanese":"今後ともよろしくお願い申し上げます。","romaji":"Kongo tomo yoroshiku onegai moushiagemasu.","german":"We look forward to your continued support.","furigana":"こんご|ねが|もう|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"だから at the start of a sentence links a previously stated reason with its consequence: \"therefore, so\". It sounds colloquial; ですから is more polite, なので or そのため more neutral. In an annoyed tone, だから means \"I'm telling you ...\": だから言ったでしょう. Do not confuse it with 雨だから, where だ＋から gives the reason within a sentence.","examples":[{"japanese":"今日は休みです。だから、家にいます。","romaji":"Kyō wa yasumi desu. Dakara, ie ni imasu.","german":"Today is my day off. So I'm staying home.","furigana":"きょう|やす|いえ"},{"japanese":"この店はいつも混んでいる。だから、予約したほうがいい。","romaji":"Kono mise wa itsumo konde iru. Dakara, yoyaku shita hou ga ii.","german":"This shop is always crowded. So you should make a reservation.","furigana":"みせ|こ|よやく"},{"japanese":"だから、言ったでしょう。","romaji":"Dakara, itta deshou.","german":"That's what I told you, isn't it?","furigana":"い"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 4"},
{"notes":"すると introduces what happened immediately afterward, often something surprising: 窓を開けた。すると… \"Thereupon ...\". It is rather narrative and written; the following sentence describes an event, not one's own intention or a request. Besides that, すると draws a conclusion from what was said: \"so then, that means\". In conversation, そうしたら or そしたら is often used for the temporal sequence.","examples":[{"japanese":"窓を開けました。すると、猫が入ってきました。","romaji":"Mado o akemashita. Suruto, neko ga haitte kimashita.","german":"I opened the window. Then a cat came in.","furigana":"まど|あ|ねこ|はい"},{"japanese":"ボタンを押します。すると、ドアが開きます。","romaji":"Botan o oshimasu. Suruto, doa ga akimasu.","german":"You press the button. Then the door opens.","furigana":"お|あ"},{"japanese":"すると、田中さんは来ないということですか。","romaji":"Suruto, Tanaka-san wa konai to iu koto desu ka.","german":"So, you mean Mr. Tanaka isn't coming?","furigana":"たなか|こ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 30"},
{"notes":"それで ties a consequence to a reason: \"so, therefore\", for example in excuses. Unlike だから, what follows is not a request or command but a fact. As a question それで？ or それで、どうなったの？ you ask someone to go on with their story. Do not confuse it with それでは \"well then\", used to close or to move on.","examples":[{"japanese":"電車が止まりました。それで遅れました。","romaji":"Densha ga tomarimashita. Sorede okuremashita.","german":"The train stopped. So I was late.","furigana":"でんしゃ|と|おく"},{"japanese":"それで、どうなりましたか。","romaji":"Sorede, dō narimashita ka.","german":"And how did it turn out?"},{"japanese":"道が分からなくなりました。それで、交番で聞きました。","romaji":"Michi ga wakaranaku narimashita. Sorede, kouban de kikimashita.","german":"I got lost. So I asked at the police box.","furigana":"みち|わ|こうばん|き"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 28"},
{"notes":"それに adds a further point pointing in the same direction: \"besides, moreover\". It is often used to list reasons, frequently together with 〜し: 安いし、それにおいしい. Unlike そして \"and\" or それから \"after that\", it is not about a sequence but about an addition. その上 is somewhat more formal.","examples":[{"japanese":"この店は安いです。それに、おいしいです。","romaji":"Kono mise wa yasui desu. Soreni, oishii desu.","german":"This restaurant is cheap. Besides, the food is good.","furigana":"みせ|やす"},{"japanese":"今日は寒いです。それに風も強いです。","romaji":"Kyō wa samui desu. Soreni kaze mo tsuyoi desu.","german":"It's cold today. Besides, the wind is strong.","furigana":"きょう|さむ|かぜ|つよ"},{"japanese":"今日は行けません。宿題が多いし、それに少し熱もあるんです。","romaji":"Kyou wa ikemasen. Shukudai ga ooi shi, soreni sukoshi netsu mo aru n desu.","german":"I can't go today. I have a lot of homework, and besides, I have a bit of a fever.","furigana":"きょう|い|しゅくだい|おお|すこ|ねつ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 28"},
{"notes":"そんな stands before a noun and means \"that kind of, such\", referring to something the other person said or something just mentioned. The series: こんな, そんな, あんな, どんな. そんなことはありません is the usual modest way to deflect a compliment, and exclaimed on its own, そんな！ expresses dismay (\"That can't be!\"). Before adjectives and verbs, そんなに is used instead.","examples":[{"japanese":"そんな話は聞いたことがありません。","romaji":"Sonna hanashi wa kiita koto ga arimasen.","german":"I've never heard such a story.","furigana":"はなし|き"},{"japanese":"そんなことを言わないでください。","romaji":"Sonna koto o iwanaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't say such things.","furigana":"い"},{"japanese":"いいえ、そんなことはありません。","romaji":"Iie, sonna koto wa arimasen.","german":"No, that's not the case."}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 26"},
{"notes":"あんな means \"that kind of (over there)\" and refers to something distant or something both speakers know: あんな家. It often sounds judgmental, both positive (あんな人になりたい) and negative (あんな人とは話したくない). Before adjectives and verbs, あんなに \"so much\" is used. The series: こんな, そんな, あんな, どんな.","examples":[{"japanese":"あんな家に住みたいです。","romaji":"Anna ie ni sumitai desu.","german":"I'd like to live in a house like that.","furigana":"いえ|す"},{"japanese":"あんなに大きい犬は初めて見ました。","romaji":"Anna ni ōkii inu wa hajimete mimashita.","german":"I've never seen such a big dog before.","furigana":"おお|いぬ|はじ|み"},{"japanese":"あんな人とは二度と話したくない。","romaji":"Anna hito to wa nido to hanashitaku nai.","german":"I never want to talk to a person like that again.","furigana":"ひと|にど|はな"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 26"},
{"notes":"そんなに means \"so much, that much\" and refers to an extent that the other person mentioned or showed: そんなに急がなくてもいい. Very common is そんなに＋negation \"not particularly, not that much\": そんなにおいしくない. Correspondingly there are こんなに and あんなに. It stands before adjectives and verbs, whereas before nouns you use そんな.","examples":[{"japanese":"そんなに急がなくてもいいです。","romaji":"Sonna ni isoganakute mo ii desu.","german":"You don't have to hurry that much.","furigana":"いそ"},{"japanese":"そんなに高いとは思いませんでした。","romaji":"Sonna ni takai to wa omoimasen deshita.","german":"I didn't think it would be that expensive.","furigana":"たか|おも"},{"japanese":"この映画はそんなにおもしろくなかった。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa sonna ni omoshiroku nakatta.","german":"This movie wasn't all that interesting.","furigana":"えいが"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 21"},
{"notes":"だいぶ (also written 大分) means \"considerably, quite\" and mostly describes a clear change or a large gap: だいぶ慣れた, だいぶよくなった, だいぶ寒くなった. かなり is similar; ずいぶん expresses surprise more strongly. With ある or かかる it describes distance or duration: まだだいぶある \"there is still quite a way to go\".","examples":[{"japanese":"日本語がだいぶ分かるようになりました。","romaji":"Nihongo ga daibu wakaru yō ni narimashita.","german":"I've come to understand Japanese quite well.","furigana":"にほんご|わ"},{"japanese":"風邪はだいぶよくなりました。","romaji":"Kaze wa daibu yoku narimashita.","german":"My cold has gotten much better.","furigana":"かぜ"},{"japanese":"駅まではまだだいぶありますよ。","romaji":"Eki made wa mada daibu arimasu yo.","german":"It's still quite a way to the station.","furigana":"えき"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"もし at the beginning of a sentence announces a condition: \"if, in case\". It does not replace the conditional form but reinforces it; the verb still ends in 〜たら, 〜ば, 〜なら or 〜ても: もし雨が降ったら. Typical for hypothetical questions (もし〜たら、どうしますか). Related is もしかしたら \"perhaps\".","examples":[{"japanese":"もし雨が降ったら、家にいます。","romaji":"Moshi ame ga futtara, ie ni imasu.","german":"If it rains, I'll stay home.","furigana":"あめ|ふ|いえ"},{"japanese":"もし時間があれば、手伝ってください。","romaji":"Moshi jikan ga areba, tetsudatte kudasai.","german":"If you have time, please help.","furigana":"じかん|てつだ"},{"japanese":"もし宝くじが当たったら、何をしたいですか。","romaji":"Moshi takarakuji ga atattara, nani o shitai desu ka.","german":"What would you like to do if you won the lottery?","furigana":"たから|あ|なに"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"または connects alternatives: \"or\". It sounds objective and appears mainly in instructions, forms and announcements: ペンまたは鉛筆. In conversation, simply か (AかB) is usually used. When asking about a choice, use それとも: コーヒーにしますか、それとも紅茶にしますか. It is almost always written in kana, rarely 又は.","examples":[{"japanese":"ペンまたは鉛筆で書いてください。","romaji":"Pen mata wa enpitsu de kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write with a pen or a pencil.","furigana":"えんぴつ|か"},{"japanese":"月曜日または火曜日に来てください。","romaji":"Getsuyōbi mata wa kayōbi ni kite kudasai.","german":"Please come on Monday or Tuesday.","furigana":"げつようび|かようび|き"},{"japanese":"雨または雪の日は、試合は中止です。","romaji":"Ame mata wa yuki no hi wa, shiai wa chuushi desu.","german":"The game is canceled on days of rain or snow.","furigana":"あめ|ゆき|ひ|しあい|ちゅうし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"ため (rarely 為) has two main meanings: purpose and benefit (noun＋のために \"for\", verb＋ために \"in order to\", with the same subject and a volitional action) and reason in factual texts: 事故のため \"because of an accident\". ためになる means \"to be useful, instructive\". For goals that cannot be controlled, ように is used rather than ために (聞こえるように).","examples":[{"japanese":"家族のために料理を作ります。","romaji":"Kazoku no tame ni ryōri o tsukurimasu.","german":"I cook for my family.","furigana":"かぞく|りょうり|つく"},{"japanese":"試験に合格するために勉強しています。","romaji":"Shiken ni gōkaku suru tame ni benkyō shite imasu.","german":"I'm studying in order to pass the exam.","furigana":"しけん|ごうかく|べんきょう"},{"japanese":"事故のため、電車が遅れています。","romaji":"Jiko no tame, densha ga okurete imasu.","german":"The train is delayed because of an accident.","furigana":"じこ|でんしゃ|おく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"はず expresses the speaker's well-founded expectation: \"should be, ought to be (according to plan or logic)\". It follows the plain form (来るはず), noun + のはず and な-adjective + なはず, and is almost always written in hiragana (筈 is rare). はずがない means \"cannot possibly be\", はずだった or はずなのに expresses a disappointed expectation. Unlike べき (moral duty) or でしょう (mere supposition), はず rests on concrete grounds such as timetables, arrangements or experience.","examples":[{"japanese":"田中さんはもう家にいるはずです。","romaji":"Tanaka-san wa mō ie ni iru hazu desu.","german":"Mr./Ms. Tanaka should be home by now.","furigana":"たなか|いえ"},{"japanese":"今日は店が開いているはずです。","romaji":"Kyō wa mise ga aite iru hazu desu.","german":"The shop should be open today.","furigana":"きょう|みせ|あ"},{"japanese":"鍵はかばんに入れたはずなのに、見つかりません。","romaji":"Kagi wa kaban ni ireta hazu na noni, mitsukarimasen.","german":"I'm sure I put the key in my bag, but I can't find it.","furigana":"かぎ|い|み"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"訳（わけ）refers to the reason or background of a matter; in conversation it sounds more personal than the matter-of-fact 理由. Fixed expressions are 訳が分からない \"can't make sense of something, to not make sense\" and 言い訳 \"excuse\". In grammar patterns it is usually written in hiragana: わけではない \"not necessarily\", わけがない \"no way\". With the reading やく the same kanji means \"translation\" (英訳, 翻訳).","examples":[{"japanese":"遅れた訳を教えてください。","romaji":"Okureta wake o oshiete kudasai.","german":"Please tell me the reason you were late.","furigana":"おく|わけ|おし"},{"japanese":"高い物がいつもいい訳ではありません。","romaji":"Takai mono ga itsumo ii wake de wa arimasen.","german":"Expensive things are not always good.","furigana":"たか|もの|わけ"},{"japanese":"この説明は難しくて、訳が分かりません。","romaji":"Kono setsumei wa muzukashikute, wake ga wakarimasen.","german":"This explanation is so difficult that I can't make any sense of it.","furigana":"せつめい|むずか|わけ|わ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"おかげ stands for a favorable influence one is grateful for: noun + のおかげで or plain verb form + おかげで (毎日練習したおかげで). For negative causes, のせいで is used instead. The polite phrase おかげさまで \"thanks for asking\" answers questions about one's well-being, even if the other person did not directly help. The kanji spelling お陰 is possible, but hiragana is usual.","examples":[{"japanese":"先生のおかげで合格できました。","romaji":"Sensei no okage de gōkaku dekimashita.","german":"Thanks to my teacher, I was able to pass.","furigana":"せんせい|ごうかく"},{"japanese":"薬のおかげでよく眠れました。","romaji":"Kusuri no okage de yoku nemuremashita.","german":"Thanks to the medicine, I slept well.","furigana":"くすり|ねむ"},{"japanese":"おかげさまで、母は元気になりました。","romaji":"Okagesama de, haha wa genki ni narimashita.","german":"Thank you for asking; my mother has recovered.","furigana":"はは|げんき"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"おかしい has two sides: \"funny, laughable\" and, more common in everyday use, \"strange, not right, suspicious\". Typical are 体の調子がおかしい \"something is wrong with my body\" or 〜のはおかしい \"it is strange that ...\". 面白い, by contrast, rather means \"interesting, entertaining\", and 変 is a close synonym for \"odd, peculiar\". As an い-adjective: おかしくて, おかしかった; the kanji spelling 可笑しい is rare.","examples":[{"japanese":"時計の音がおかしいです。","romaji":"Tokei no oto ga okashii desu.","german":"The clock is making a strange noise.","furigana":"とけい|おと"},{"japanese":"彼の話がおかしくて笑いました。","romaji":"Kare no hanashi ga okashikute waraimashita.","german":"His story was so funny that I laughed.","furigana":"かれ|はなし|わら"},{"japanese":"彼がまだ来ないのはおかしいですね。","romaji":"Kare ga mada konai no wa okashii desu ne.","german":"It's odd that he hasn't come yet.","furigana":"かれ|こ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 32"},
{"notes":"おもちゃ is the everyday word for toy and is almost always written in hiragana; the kanji word 玩具（がんぐ）appears more in shops and on signs. Typical are おもちゃで遊ぶ, おもちゃを片付ける and おもちゃ屋 \"toy store\". Figuratively, 人をおもちゃにする means \"to toy with someone\".","examples":[{"japanese":"子供がおもちゃで遊んでいます。","romaji":"Kodomo ga omocha de asonde imasu.","german":"The child is playing with a toy.","furigana":"こども|あそ"},{"japanese":"おもちゃを箱に片付けてください。","romaji":"Omocha o hako ni katazukete kudasai.","german":"Please put the toys away in the box.","furigana":"はこ|かたづ"},{"japanese":"誕生日に子供におもちゃを買ってあげました。","romaji":"Tanjoubi ni kodomo ni omocha o katte agemashita.","german":"I bought my child a toy for their birthday.","furigana":"たんじょうび|こども|か"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 11"},
{"notes":"この間 refers to a specific point in the recent past, \"the other day, a few days ago\", and so usually takes the past tense. It is often written in hiragana and colloquially shortened to こないだ. With の it forms attributes (この間の写真), and この間はありがとうございました is a set phrase of thanks when meeting again. For an ongoing state, \"lately\", you use このごろ or 最近 instead.","examples":[{"japanese":"この間、駅で先生に会いました。","romaji":"Kono aida, eki de sensei ni aimashita.","german":"The other day I met my teacher at the station.","furigana":"あいだ|えき|せんせい|あ"},{"japanese":"この間の写真を送ります。","romaji":"Kono aida no shashin o okurimasu.","german":"I'll send you the photos from the other day.","furigana":"あいだ|しゃしん|おく"},{"japanese":"この間はありがとうございました。","romaji":"Kono aida wa arigatou gozaimashita.","german":"Thank you for the other day.","furigana":"あいだ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 20"},
{"notes":"アクセサリー meint vor allem Modeschmuck wie Ketten, Ohrringe, Ringe und Armbänder und ist damit enger als das deutsche „Accessoires“ (Taschen oder Schals zählen meist nicht dazu). Schmuck anlegen heißt アクセサリーを付ける, ablegen アクセサリーを外す. Daneben bezeichnet das Wort Zubehör für Geräte, etwa für Kameras, Autos oder Smartphones. Echte Edelsteine nennt man eher 宝石, hochwertigen Schmuck auch ジュエリー.","examples":[{"japanese":"旅行先でアクセサリーを買いました。","romaji":"Ryokōsaki de akusesarī o kaimashita.","german":"Ich habe auf der Reise Schmuck gekauft.","furigana":"りょこうさき|か"},{"japanese":"仕事中はアクセサリーを外します。","romaji":"Shigotochū wa akusesarī o hazushimasu.","german":"Während der Arbeit lege ich den Schmuck ab.","furigana":"しごとちゅう|はず"},{"japanese":"この店にはカメラのアクセサリーがたくさんあります。","romaji":"Kono mise ni wa kamera no akusesarii ga takusan arimasu.","german":"In diesem Laden gibt es viel Kamerazubehör.","furigana":"みせ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"アナウンサー in Japan are usually salaried broadcasters of a television or radio station who read the news, host programs or commentate on sports. The announcement itself is called アナウンス (駅のアナウンス), and the person who hosts a show or event is usually called 司会者. Typical are アナウンサーになる and ニュースを読む. The profession is considered prestigious and demands especially clean pronunciation.","examples":[{"japanese":"アナウンサーがニュースを読んでいます。","romaji":"Anaunsā ga nyūsu o yonde imasu.","german":"The announcer is reading the news.","furigana":"よ"},{"japanese":"将来はアナウンサーになりたいです。","romaji":"Shōrai wa anaunsā ni naritai desu.","german":"I want to become an announcer in the future.","furigana":"しょうらい"},{"japanese":"あのアナウンサーは話し方がとてもきれいです。","romaji":"Ano anaunsaa wa hanashikata ga totemo kirei desu.","german":"That announcer speaks very beautifully.","furigana":"はな|かた"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"アルコール refers both to the chemical substance and to alcoholic drinks in general. For drinks, however, お酒 is usually used in everyday speech; アルコール sounds more matter-of-fact and appears on labels and notices (アルコール度数, ノンアルコールビール). Since the pandemic, アルコール often also means the hand sanitizer at the entrance of shops. Typical are アルコールを飲む and アルコールが入っている.","examples":[{"japanese":"この飲み物にはアルコールが入っていません。","romaji":"Kono nomimono ni wa arukōru ga haitte imasen.","german":"This drink does not contain alcohol.","furigana":"の|もの|はい"},{"japanese":"運転するのでアルコールは飲みません。","romaji":"Unten suru node arukōru wa nomimasen.","german":"I'm driving, so I won't drink alcohol.","furigana":"うんてん|の"},{"japanese":"入り口のアルコールで手をきれいにしてください。","romaji":"Iriguchi no arukooru de te o kirei ni shite kudasai.","german":"Please clean your hands with the alcohol at the entrance.","furigana":"い|ぐち|て"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"エスカレーター ist die Rolltreppe und darf nicht mit エレベーター „Aufzug“ verwechselt werden. Man sagt エスカレーターに乗る oder エスカレーターで上がる/下りる; die Richtung unterscheiden 上りのエスカレーター und 下りのエスカレーター. In Tokio steht man traditionell links, in Osaka rechts, offiziell wird aber gebeten, nicht zu laufen. Beachte: Das japanische 二階 entspricht dem deutschen ersten Stock.","examples":[{"japanese":"エスカレーターで二階に上がります。","romaji":"Esukarētā de nikai ni agarimasu.","german":"Ich fahre mit der Rolltreppe in den ersten Stock.","furigana":"にかい|あ"},{"japanese":"エスカレーターでは走らないでください。","romaji":"Esukarētā de wa hashiranaide kudasai.","german":"Laufen Sie bitte nicht auf der Rolltreppe.","furigana":"はし"},{"japanese":"下りのエスカレーターはどこですか。","romaji":"Kudari no esukareetaa wa doko desu ka.","german":"Wo ist die Rolltreppe nach unten?","furigana":"くだ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 3"},
{"notes":"オートバイ is a word coined in Japan (from \"auto\" and \"bicycle\") for a motorcycle. In everyday speech バイク is much more common, which also includes scooters; オートバイ sounds more matter-of-fact and a little older. One says オートバイに乗る, and as a means of transport オートバイで行く. Do not confuse it with 自転車 \"bicycle\".","examples":[{"japanese":"兄はオートバイで会社に通っています。","romaji":"Ani wa ōtobai de kaisha ni kayotte imasu.","german":"My older brother commutes to work by motorcycle.","furigana":"あに|かいしゃ|かよ"},{"japanese":"オートバイに乗る時はヘルメットを被ります。","romaji":"Ōtobai ni noru toki wa herumetto o kaburimasu.","german":"I wear a helmet when I ride a motorcycle.","furigana":"の|とき|かぶ"},{"japanese":"オートバイの免許を取りたいです。","romaji":"Ootobai no menkyo o toritai desu.","german":"I want to get a motorcycle license.","furigana":"めんきょ|と"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 40"},
{"notes":"ガソリン is gasoline. To fill up is ガソリンを入れる, the gas station is ガソリンスタンド, a Japanese coinage that does not exist in English in this form. Diesel is called 軽油, and fuel costs are ガソリン代. For price changes one says ガソリンの値段が上がる/下がる.","examples":[{"japanese":"車にガソリンを入れました。","romaji":"Kuruma ni gasorin o iremashita.","german":"I put gas in the car.","furigana":"くるま|い"},{"japanese":"ガソリンの値段が上がりました。","romaji":"Gasorin no nedan ga agarimashita.","german":"The price of gasoline has gone up.","furigana":"ねだん|あ"},{"japanese":"近くにガソリンスタンドはありますか。","romaji":"Chikaku ni gasorin sutando wa arimasu ka.","german":"Is there a gas station nearby?","furigana":"ちか"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 43"},
{"notes":"サラダ refers to the prepared dish, not the plant: lettuce is レタス. Typical are サラダを作る, ドレッシングをかける and compounds such as ポテトサラダ or 野菜サラダ. In Japan, a small salad often comes as a side dish with set menus, including breakfast.","examples":[{"japanese":"野菜を切ってサラダを作ります。","romaji":"Yasai o kitte sarada o tsukurimasu.","german":"I cut vegetables and make a salad.","furigana":"やさい|き|つく"},{"japanese":"昼ご飯はサラダとパンでした。","romaji":"Hirugohan wa sarada to pan deshita.","german":"Lunch was a salad and bread.","furigana":"ひる|はん"},{"japanese":"サラダにドレッシングをかけますか。","romaji":"Sarada ni doresshingu o kakemasu ka.","german":"Would you like dressing on your salad?"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"サンドイッチ is a sandwich, in Japan usually made with soft white bread with the crusts cut off. The spelling サンドウィッチ also occurs, and in everyday speech it is often shortened to サンド: 卵サンド, カツサンド. Putting the filling between the slices is called 挟む. In convenience stores, sandwiches usually come cut into triangles in a two-pack.","examples":[{"japanese":"駅でサンドイッチを買いました。","romaji":"Eki de sandoitchi o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a sandwich at the station.","furigana":"えき|か"},{"japanese":"卵のサンドイッチを二つください。","romaji":"Tamago no sandoitchi o futatsu kudasai.","german":"Two egg sandwiches, please.","furigana":"たまご|ふた"},{"japanese":"パンにハムとチーズを挟んで、サンドイッチを作りました。","romaji":"Pan ni hamu to chiizu o hasande, sandoitchi o tsukurimashita.","german":"I put ham and cheese between slices of bread and made sandwiches.","furigana":"はさ|つく"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 11"},
{"notes":"ジャム entspricht dem deutschen „Marmelade“ bzw. „Konfitüre“ aus beliebigen Früchten: いちごジャム, りんごのジャム. マーマレード bezeichnet im Japanischen nur Zitrusmarmelade. Aufstreichen heißt パンにジャムを塗る, das Glas ジャムの瓶. Achtung: Ein Verkehrsstau ist nicht ジャム, sondern 渋滞.","examples":[{"japanese":"パンにいちごのジャムを塗ります。","romaji":"Pan ni ichigo no jamu o nurimasu.","german":"Ich streiche Erdbeermarmelade aufs Brot.","furigana":"ぬ"},{"japanese":"このジャムはあまり甘くありません。","romaji":"Kono jamu wa amari amaku arimasen.","german":"Diese Marmelade ist nicht besonders süß.","furigana":"あま"},{"japanese":"祖母は毎年りんごでジャムを作ります。","romaji":"Sobo wa maitoshi ringo de jamu o tsukurimasu.","german":"Meine Großmutter kocht jedes Jahr Marmelade aus Äpfeln.","furigana":"そぼ|まいとし|つく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"ステーキ without any addition almost always means a beef steak (ビーフステーキ); other kinds of meat are named, for example ポークステーキ. To fry or grill is ステーキを焼く. In a restaurant you state the doneness with レア, ミディアム or ウェルダン, or simply say よく焼いてください \"well done, please\". Do not confuse it with ハンバーグ, a kind of meat patty, which is sometimes called ハンバーグステーキ on menus.","examples":[{"japanese":"夕飯にステーキを焼きました。","romaji":"Yūhan ni sutēki o yakimashita.","german":"I grilled a steak for dinner.","furigana":"ゆうはん|や"},{"japanese":"この店のステーキは柔らかいです。","romaji":"Kono mise no sutēki wa yawarakai desu.","german":"The steak at this restaurant is tender.","furigana":"みせ|やわ"},{"japanese":"ステーキはよく焼いてください。","romaji":"Suteeki wa yoku yaite kudasai.","german":"I'd like my steak well done, please.","furigana":"や"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"スーツ refers to a suit made up of a jacket and matching pants or skirt, for men and women alike. You say スーツを着る or スーツで会社に行く; the older word for a man's suit is 背広. For job hunting, students wear a plain, usually black リクルートスーツ. Do not confuse it with スーツケース \"suitcase\".","examples":[{"japanese":"面接にはスーツを着て行きます。","romaji":"Mensetsu ni wa sūtsu o kite ikimasu.","german":"I'll wear a suit to the interview.","furigana":"めんせつ|き|い"},{"japanese":"新しいスーツを買う必要があります。","romaji":"Atarashii sūtsu o kau hitsuyō ga arimasu.","german":"I need to buy a new suit.","furigana":"あたら|か|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"父は毎日スーツで会社に行きます。","romaji":"Chichi wa mainichi suutsu de kaisha ni ikimasu.","german":"My father goes to work in a suit every day.","furigana":"ちち|まいにち|かいしゃ|い"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 22"},
{"notes":"サンダル means sandals and other open shoes. As with all footwear, one says サンダルを履く (not 着る) and, to take them off, サンダルを脱ぐ. Flip-flops are called ビーチサンダル, colloquially ビーサン. With 歩きやすい or 歩きにくい you describe how comfortable it is to walk in them.","examples":[{"japanese":"夏はよくサンダルを履きます。","romaji":"Natsu wa yoku sandaru o hakimasu.","german":"I often wear sandals in summer.","furigana":"なつ|は"},{"japanese":"このサンダルは歩きやすいです。","romaji":"Kono sandaru wa arukiyasui desu.","german":"These sandals are easy to walk in.","furigana":"ある"},{"japanese":"海に行くので、ビーチサンダルを買いました。","romaji":"Umi ni iku node, biichi sandaru o kaimashita.","german":"I'm going to the sea, so I bought flip-flops.","furigana":"うみ|い|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"テキスト in class usually means the textbook or course material; it is somewhat more general than 教科書, which mainly stands for official school textbooks. In computer language it also means \"text\" in the sense of text data (テキストファイル). Typical are テキストを開く and テキストの十ページ. For the text of an article or book, 文章 or 本文 is more common.","examples":[{"japanese":"授業の前にテキストを読んでください。","romaji":"Jugyō no mae ni tekisuto o yonde kudasai.","german":"Please read the textbook before class.","furigana":"じゅぎょう|まえ|よ"},{"japanese":"テキストを家に忘れてしまいました。","romaji":"Tekisuto o ie ni wasurete shimaimashita.","german":"I forgot my textbook at home.","furigana":"いえ|わす"},{"japanese":"このテキストは説明が分かりやすいです。","romaji":"Kono tekisuto wa setsumei ga wakariyasui desu.","german":"The explanations in this textbook are easy to understand.","furigana":"せつめい|わ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"タイプ bezeichnet eine Art oder Ausführung von Dingen (どんなタイプの部屋) und besonders einen Menschentyp: 静かなタイプの人 „ein eher ruhiger Mensch“. 私のタイプ heißt wie im Deutschen „mein Typ“ im romantischen Sinn und wird oft verneint: タイプじゃない. Das Verb タイプする „tippen“ ist veraltet; heute sagt man 入力する oder 打つ.","examples":[{"japanese":"どんなタイプの部屋を探していますか。","romaji":"Donna taipu no heya o sagashite imasu ka.","german":"Was für eine Art Zimmer suchen Sie?","furigana":"へや|さが"},{"japanese":"彼は静かなタイプの人です。","romaji":"Kare wa shizuka na taipu no hito desu.","german":"Er ist ein eher ruhiger Mensch.","furigana":"かれ|しず|ひと"},{"japanese":"あの人は全然私のタイプじゃありません。","romaji":"Ano hito wa zenzen watashi no taipu ja arimasen.","german":"Er ist überhaupt nicht mein Typ.","furigana":"ひと|ぜんぜんわたし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"パート is the short form of パートタイム and means part-time work or the part-time worker themselves: パートで働く, パートをしている, パートの仕事. Unlike アルバイト, which is mainly associated with students and side jobs, パート typically stands for longer-term part-time positions of adults, often housewives. パート also means \"part\" or \"voice\" in music (ソプラノのパート).","examples":[{"japanese":"母は近くの店でパートをしています。","romaji":"Haha wa chikaku no mise de pāto o shite imasu.","german":"My mother works part-time at a shop nearby.","furigana":"はは|ちか|みせ"},{"japanese":"パートの仕事を探しています。","romaji":"Pāto no shigoto o sagashite imasu.","german":"I'm looking for a part-time job.","furigana":"しごと|さが"},{"japanese":"コーラスで私はアルトのパートを歌います。","romaji":"Koorasu de watashi wa aruto no paato o utaimasu.","german":"In the chorus, I sing the alto part.","furigana":"わたし|うた"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"二階建て describes a building with two floors; with 〜階建て you count the stories (三階建て, 十階建て), where たて becomes だて through sound change. Since the ground floor counts as 一階 in Japanese, a 二階建ての家 has a ground floor and an upper floor. As a modifier it takes の: 二階建てのバス \"double-decker bus\".","examples":[{"japanese":"二階建ての家に住んでいます。","romaji":"Nikaidate no ie ni sunde imasu.","german":"I live in a two-story house.","furigana":"にかいだ|いえ|す"},{"japanese":"駅の前に二階建ての店があります。","romaji":"Eki no mae ni nikaidate no mise ga arimasu.","german":"There is a two-story shop in front of the station.","furigana":"えき|まえ|にかいだ|みせ"},{"japanese":"ロンドンで二階建てのバスに乗りました。","romaji":"Rondon de nikaidate no basu ni norimashita.","german":"I rode a double-decker bus in London.","furigana":"にかいだ|の"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"おいでになる is a respectful form (尊敬語) for いる, 来る and 行く and refers only to actions of people of higher status, never to oneself. It sounds slightly more formal than the more common いらっしゃる; for one's own actions one humbly uses 参る, 伺う or おる. The ます-form is regularly おいでになります, and the request is おいでください \"please come\". It is almost always written in hiragana.","examples":[{"japanese":"先生はもうおいでになりました。","romaji":"Sensei wa mō oide ni narimashita.","german":"The teacher has already arrived.","furigana":"せんせい"},{"japanese":"明日もこちらにおいでになりますか。","romaji":"Ashita mo kochira ni oide ni narimasu ka.","german":"Will you come here tomorrow as well?","furigana":"あした"},{"japanese":"先生が今どちらにおいでになるか、ご存じですか。","romaji":"Sensei ga ima dochira ni oide ni naru ka, gozonji desu ka.","german":"Do you know where the teacher is right now?","furigana":"せんせい|いま|ぞん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"なさる is the respectful form (尊敬語) of する and describes actions of others: 何をなさいますか, ゴルフをなさる. The ます-form is the irregular なさいます, not なさります, and the imperative なさい appears in gentle commands from parents or teachers such as 早く寝なさい. When ordering, the staff ask 何になさいますか \"What will you have?\". For one's own actions, the humble いたす is used instead.","examples":[{"japanese":"先生は何をなさっていますか。","romaji":"Sensei wa nani o nasatte imasu ka.","german":"What is the teacher doing?","furigana":"せんせい|なに"},{"japanese":"週末は何をなさいますか。","romaji":"Shūmatsu wa nani o nasaimasu ka.","german":"What will you do on the weekend?","furigana":"しゅうまつ|なに"},{"japanese":"社長はゴルフをなさるそうです。","romaji":"Shachou wa gorufu o nasaru sou desu.","german":"I hear the company president plays golf.","furigana":"しゃちょう"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 19"},
{"notes":"びっくり is an onomatopoeic word and is almost always used as びっくりする \"to be startled, to be surprised\"; the trigger is marked by に (音にびっくりする) or a て-form (値段を見てびっくりした). To surprise someone is the causative びっくりさせる. The synonymous 驚く is more neutral and also used in writing; びっくり sounds more colloquial and lively. Spontaneously one exclaims びっくりした！ \"You scared me!\"; びっくり箱 is a jack-in-the-box.","examples":[{"japanese":"大きな音にびっくりしました。","romaji":"Ōkina oto ni bikkuri shimashita.","german":"I was startled by the loud noise.","furigana":"おお|おと"},{"japanese":"値段を見てびっくりしました。","romaji":"Nedan o mite bikkuri shimashita.","german":"I was surprised when I saw the price.","furigana":"ねだん|み"},{"japanese":"後ろから急に声をかけて、友達をびっくりさせました。","romaji":"Ushiro kara kyuu ni koe o kakete, tomodachi o bikkuri sasemashita.","german":"I called out to my friend suddenly from behind and startled him.","furigana":"うし|きゅう|こえ|ともだち"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"ほど is mostly written in hiragana and has three main functions. After quantities it means \"about, approximately\" and sounds somewhat more formal than ぐらい (十分ほど). In the comparison A は B ほど〜ない it means \"A is not as ... as B\" and always takes a negative. The pattern 〜ば〜ほど means \"the more ..., the more ...\", and after expressions of degree ほど means \"so much that\" (泣きたいほど).","examples":[{"japanese":"十分程待ちました。","romaji":"Juppun hodo machimashita.","german":"I waited about ten minutes.","furigana":"じゅっぷんほどま"},{"japanese":"今日は昨日ほど寒くありません。","romaji":"Kyō wa kinō hodo samuku arimasen.","german":"It isn't as cold today as yesterday.","furigana":"きょう|きのう|さむ"},{"japanese":"日本語は勉強すればするほど面白くなります。","romaji":"Nihongo wa benkyou sureba suru hodo omoshiroku narimasu.","german":"The more I study Japanese, the more interesting it becomes.","furigana":"にほんご|べんきょう|おもしろ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"内（うち）means \"inside\", but as an independent word it is now mostly used in a temporal or selective sense and then often written in hiragana: 今日のうちに \"before today is over\", 若いうちに \"while one is young\", 三つのうち \"of the three\". For the spatial inside, 中 is more usual in everyday speech, or compounds such as 内側 are used. The same reading うち (mostly as 家 or in kana) also stands for \"home, family\" and \"our\" (うちの会社).","examples":[{"japanese":"箱の内側を見てください。","romaji":"Hako no uchigawa o mite kudasai.","german":"Please look at the inside of the box.","furigana":"はこ|うちがわ|み"},{"japanese":"今日の内に返事をします。","romaji":"Kyō no uchi ni henji o shimasu.","german":"I'll reply within today.","furigana":"きょう|うち|へんじ"},{"japanese":"この三つの内、どれが一番好きですか。","romaji":"Kono mittsu no uchi, dore ga ichiban suki desu ka.","german":"Which of these three do you like best?","furigana":"みっ|うち|いちばんす"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"ちゃん is a familiar, endearing name suffix, used above all for children, girls, close friends and pets; it often attaches to short names or nicknames (ゆうちゃん). In the family it appears in おばあちゃん, お母ちゃん or お兄ちゃん. Compared with the neutral さん and くん, which is used more for boys, ちゃん is very familiar and unsuitable toward superiors or strangers. Only small children use it for their own name.","examples":[{"japanese":"花ちゃんはもう寝ました。","romaji":"Hana-chan wa mō nemashita.","german":"Little Hana is already asleep.","furigana":"はな|ね"},{"japanese":"ゆきちゃんと公園で遊びました。","romaji":"Yuki-chan to kōen de asobimashita.","german":"I played with Yuki in the park.","furigana":"こうえん|あそ"},{"japanese":"おばあちゃん、お誕生日おめでとう。","romaji":"Obaachan, otanjoubi omedetou.","german":"Grandma, happy birthday!","furigana":"たんじょうび"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 1"},
{"notes":"ひげ covers every kind of facial hair (beard, mustache, sideburns) and also the whiskers of cats or the antennae of insects. It is mostly written in hiragana; strictly speaking the kanji 髭 denotes the mustache and 鬚 the chin beard. Typical verbs are ひげをそる \"to shave\", ひげを伸ばす \"to grow a beard\" and ひげが生える \"a beard grows\".","examples":[{"japanese":"父は毎朝、髭をそります。","romaji":"Chichi wa maiasa, hige o sorimasu.","german":"My father shaves every morning.","furigana":"ちち|まいあさ|ひげ"},{"japanese":"兄は髭を伸ばしています。","romaji":"Ani wa hige o nobashite imasu.","german":"My older brother is growing a beard.","furigana":"あに|ひげ|の"},{"japanese":"猫のひげはとても長いです。","romaji":"Neko no hige wa totemo nagai desu.","german":"The cat's whiskers are very long.","furigana":"ねこ|なが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"こと refers to abstract things, matters and events, whereas 物（もの）stands for tangible objects. It is written predominantly in hiragana, especially in grammar patterns: た-form + ことがある \"to have done something once\", ことができる \"can\", ことにする \"to decide to\". With adjectives and verbs it forms expressions such as 大事なこと \"something important\" or 分からないこと \"something one doesn't understand\".","examples":[{"japanese":"大事な事を忘れていました。","romaji":"Daiji na koto o wasurete imashita.","german":"I had forgotten something important.","furigana":"だいじ|こと|わす"},{"japanese":"分からない事があれば聞いてください。","romaji":"Wakaranai koto ga areba kiite kudasai.","german":"If there is anything you don't understand, please ask.","furigana":"わ|こと|き"},{"japanese":"日本へ行ったことがありますか。","romaji":"Nihon e itta koto ga arimasu ka.","german":"Have you ever been to Japan?","furigana":"にほん|い"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 21"},
{"notes":"湯 ist heißes oder warmes Wasser; im Alltag sagt man fast immer お湯. Anders als im Deutschen unterscheidet das Japanische mit eigenen Wörtern: 水 ist kaltes Wasser, und „heißes Wasser“ heißt nicht 熱い水, sondern お湯. Typisch sind お湯を沸かす „Wasser aufkochen“, お湯が出る und お風呂のお湯. In Badehäusern kennzeichnen 男湯 und 女湯 die Bereiche, und 湯のみ ist eine Teetasse.","examples":[{"japanese":"お湯を沸かしてください。","romaji":"Oyu o wakashite kudasai.","german":"Kochen Sie bitte Wasser auf.","furigana":"ゆ|わ"},{"japanese":"お風呂のお湯が熱いです。","romaji":"Ofuro no oyu ga atsui desu.","german":"Das Badewasser ist heiß.","furigana":"ふろ|ゆ|あつ"},{"japanese":"お湯が出ないので、シャワーが使えません。","romaji":"Oyu ga denai node, shawaa ga tsukaemasen.","german":"Es kommt kein warmes Wasser, deshalb kann ich nicht duschen.","furigana":"ゆ|で|つか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"止む is intransitive and describes something stopping by itself, especially weather and noises: 雨が止む, 風が止む, 音が止む. It is mostly written in hiragana (やむ). The transitive counterpart 止める（やめる）means \"to stop doing something\" as a deliberate action. Do not confuse it with 止まる（とまる）\"to stop\" for movement and machines, or with 病む \"to be ill\".","examples":[{"japanese":"雨が止んだら出かけます。","romaji":"Ame ga yandara dekakemasu.","german":"When the rain stops, I'll go out.","furigana":"あめ|や|で"},{"japanese":"夜になって風が止みました。","romaji":"Yoru ni natte kaze ga yamimashita.","german":"The wind died down in the evening.","furigana":"よる|かぜ|や"},{"japanese":"隣の部屋の音がやっと止みました。","romaji":"Tonari no heya no oto ga yatto yamimashita.","german":"The noise from the next room finally stopped.","furigana":"となり|へや|おと|や"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"やめる is transitive: you deliberately stop an activity or drop a plan (たばこをやめる, 行くのをやめる). It is mostly written in hiragana because 止める is also read とめる \"to stop, to halt\" (a car, a machine). For quitting a job or leaving a school, 辞める is usual. The intransitive counterpart is 止む（やむ）\"to stop\" for rain or noise.","examples":[{"japanese":"たばこを止めました。","romaji":"Tabako o yamemashita.","german":"I quit smoking.","furigana":"や"},{"japanese":"雨なので試合を止めました。","romaji":"Ame na node shiai o yamemashita.","german":"Because of the rain, we called off the game.","furigana":"あめ|しあい|や"},{"japanese":"けんかはやめてください。","romaji":"Kenka wa yamete kudasai.","german":"Please stop fighting."}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"開く（ひらく）means \"to open\", especially for things that unfold: 本を開く, 傘を開く, to open a file. It also means \"to hold\" (パーティーを開く, 会議を開く) and \"to open for business\" (店を開く); intransitively it can mean that something opens (花が開く). The same kanji is also read あく: 開く（あく）\"to open (by itself)\" forms a pair with 開ける（あける）and is used more for doors, windows and lids.","examples":[{"japanese":"テキストを開いてください。","romaji":"Tekisuto o hiraite kudasai.","german":"Please open your textbook.","furigana":"ひら"},{"japanese":"来月、小さな店を開きます。","romaji":"Raigetsu, chiisana mise o hirakimasu.","german":"I'm opening a small shop next month.","furigana":"らいげつ|ちい|みせ|ひら"},{"japanese":"週末に友達の誕生日パーティーを開きます。","romaji":"Shuumatsu ni tomodachi no tanjoubi paatii o hirakimasu.","german":"We're holding a birthday party for a friend on the weekend.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|ともだち|たんじょうび|ひら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"用 means \"business, errand\": 用がある \"to have something to do\", 何か用？ \"What's up?\" (colloquial). In conversation the synonymous 用事 is more common; politely it is ご用 (ご用の方は… \"Those who have business, ...\"). As a suffix, 〜用 means \"intended for ...\": 子供用, 女性用, 仕事用.","examples":[{"japanese":"今日は用があるので、先に帰ります。","romaji":"Kyō wa yō ga aru node, saki ni kaerimasu.","german":"I have something to do today, so I'll go home first.","furigana":"きょう|よう|さき|かえ"},{"japanese":"何か私に用がありますか。","romaji":"Nanika watashi ni yō ga arimasu ka.","german":"Do you need anything from me?","furigana":"なに|わたし|よう"},{"japanese":"これは子供用の自転車です。","romaji":"Kore wa kodomoyou no jitensha desu.","german":"This is a bicycle for children.","furigana":"こどもよう|じてんしゃ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"すり refers both to the pickpocket and to pickpocketing and is written in hiragana (the kanji 掏摸 are rare). Typical are すりに遭う \"to be pickpocketed\", すりに財布を取られる and すりに気を付ける. A thief in general is 泥棒, shoplifting is 万引き, and snatching a handbag in passing is ひったくり.","examples":[{"japanese":"駅ですりに財布を取られました。","romaji":"Eki de suri ni saifu o toraremashita.","german":"My wallet was stolen by a pickpocket at the station.","furigana":"えき|さいふ|と"},{"japanese":"人が多い所ではすりに気を付けてください。","romaji":"Hito ga ōi tokoro de wa suri ni ki o tsukete kudasai.","german":"Please watch out for pickpockets in crowded places.","furigana":"ひと|おお|ところ|き|つ"},{"japanese":"警察がすりを捕まえました。","romaji":"Keisatsu ga suri o tsukamaemashita.","german":"The police arrested a pickpocket.","furigana":"けいさつ|つか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"床屋 is the colloquial word for a men's barber, where hair is cut and often the face is shaved. The shop is more formally called 理容室 or 理髪店; a salon with coloring and perms, mainly for women, is a 美容院. Typical are 床屋に行く and 床屋で髪を切る, even when you have your hair cut by someone.","examples":[{"japanese":"駅前の床屋で髪を切りました。","romaji":"Ekimae no tokoya de kami o kirimashita.","german":"I had my hair cut at the barber near the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|とこや|かみ|き"},{"japanese":"父は毎月、床屋に行きます。","romaji":"Chichi wa maitsuki, tokoya ni ikimasu.","german":"My father goes to the barber every month.","furigana":"ちち|まいつき|とこや|い"},{"japanese":"床屋でひげもそってもらいました。","romaji":"Tokoya de hige mo sotte moraimashita.","german":"At the barber's I also had my beard shaved.","furigana":"とこや"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 15"},
{"notes":"下宿 refers to a room rented in someone else's household, traditionally often with meals provided by the landlord's family, especially for students. As a verb, 下宿する is intransitive: 大学の近くで下宿する. The lodging itself is called 下宿 or 下宿先, the rent 下宿代. Today students more often live in their own アパート or in a dormitory (寮).","examples":[{"japanese":"大学の近くで下宿しています。","romaji":"Daigaku no chikaku de geshuku shite imasu.","german":"I'm lodging near the university.","furigana":"だいがく|ちか|げしゅく"},{"japanese":"新しい下宿を探しています。","romaji":"Atarashii geshuku o sagashite imasu.","german":"I'm looking for new lodging.","furigana":"あたら|げしゅく|さが"},{"japanese":"下宿のおばさんが毎朝ご飯を作ってくれます。","romaji":"Geshuku no obasan ga maiasa gohan o tsukutte kuremasu.","german":"The landlady makes me breakfast every morning.","furigana":"げしゅく|まいあさ|はん|つく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"生命 means \"life\" in a biological or abstract sense and sounds more objective and more literary than the everyday 命. Typical set phrases are 生命保険 \"life insurance\", 生命の危険 \"danger to life\" and 生命の起源 \"origin of life\". For life in the sense of one's life story or daily life, use 人生 or 生活 instead.","examples":[{"japanese":"水は生命に欠かせません。","romaji":"Mizu wa seimei ni kakasemasen.","german":"Water is essential to life.","furigana":"みず|せいめい|か"},{"japanese":"生命の始まりについて学びました。","romaji":"Seimei no hajimari ni tsuite manabimashita.","german":"I learned about the origin of life.","furigana":"せいめい|はじ|まな"},{"japanese":"生命保険に入りました。","romaji":"Seimei hoken ni hairimashita.","german":"I took out life insurance.","furigana":"せいめいほけん|はい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"こう means \"this way, like this\" and refers to something the speaker is currently showing or about to carry out. It belongs to the こ・そ・あ・ど series: そう \"that way (as you say, as mentioned)\", ああ \"that way (as over there, as is well known)\", どう \"how?\". Typical are こうする, こう言う and こうすれば; before nouns, こういう is used (こういう本 \"a book like this\").","examples":[{"japanese":"この道具はこう使います。","romaji":"Kono dōgu wa kō tsukaimasu.","german":"You use this tool like this.","furigana":"どうぐ|つか"},{"japanese":"私はこう考えています。","romaji":"Watashi wa kō kangaete imasu.","german":"This is how I see it.","furigana":"わたし|かんが"},{"japanese":"こうすれば、もっと早く終わりますよ。","romaji":"Kou sureba, motto hayaku owarimasu yo.","german":"If you do it like this, you'll finish faster.","furigana":"はや|お"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"これから means \"in a moment, from now\" or \"from now on, in the future\". Without a particle it often stands for an action that follows immediately (これから出かけます), with は or も for the further future (これからは気を付けます, これからもよろしく). これからの + noun means \"future, coming\". 今から stresses the starting point now more strongly, and 今後 is the formal equivalent.","examples":[{"japanese":"これから買い物に行きます。","romaji":"Korekara kaimono ni ikimasu.","german":"I'm going shopping now.","furigana":"か|もの|い"},{"japanese":"これからもよろしくお願いします。","romaji":"Korekara mo yoroshiku onegai shimasu.","german":"I look forward to continuing to work with you.","furigana":"ねが"},{"japanese":"これからは毎日日本語を勉強します。","romaji":"Korekara wa mainichi nihongo o benkyou shimasu.","german":"From now on I will study Japanese every day.","furigana":"まいにちにほんご|べんきょう"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 7"},
{"notes":"このごろ describes a period up to the present, \"lately, nowadays\", and mostly appears with the present tense or the ている form for habits and trends. In contrast, この間 refers to a single point in time, \"the other day\", and 最近 can express both trends and individual recent events. The kanji spelling この頃 is possible; hiragana is common.","examples":[{"japanese":"このごろ、よく雨が降ります。","romaji":"Konogoro, yoku ame ga furimasu.","german":"It rains often these days.","furigana":"あめ|ふ"},{"japanese":"このごろは毎朝走っています。","romaji":"Konogoro wa maiasa hashitte imasu.","german":"Lately I run every morning.","furigana":"まいあさはし"},{"japanese":"このごろの若い人はあまり新聞を読みません。","romaji":"Konogoro no wakai hito wa amari shinbun o yomimasen.","german":"Young people these days hardly read newspapers.","furigana":"わか|ひと|しんぶん|よ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 10"},
{"notes":"いっぱい means \"full\" (お腹がいっぱい \"full (of food)\", 〜でいっぱい \"full of\") and, colloquially, \"a lot, plenty\"; たくさん is more neutral. In kanji, 一杯 means \"a cup, a glass\" (コーヒーを一杯) and also \"a drink\" (一杯飲みに行く). As a suffix it means \"until the end of\" (今月いっぱい), and 精いっぱい means \"with all one's might\".","examples":[{"japanese":"お腹がいっぱいです。","romaji":"Onaka ga ippai desu.","german":"I'm full.","furigana":"なか"},{"japanese":"電車は人でいっぱいでした。","romaji":"Densha wa hito de ippai deshita.","german":"The train was full of people.","furigana":"でんしゃ|ひと"},{"japanese":"公園に桜の花がいっぱい咲いています。","romaji":"Kouen ni sakura no hana ga ippai saite imasu.","german":"Lots of cherry blossoms are in bloom in the park.","furigana":"こうえん|さくら|はな|さ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"おる is the humble or polite-formal form of いる and is almost always written in hiragana. It is used for oneself or one's own side, mostly as おります and in the pattern 〜ております instead of 〜ています, typically in business language, on the phone and in e-mails (お世話になっております). For people of higher status, use いらっしゃる instead. In western Japanese dialects, however, おる is the ordinary, non-humble form for \"to be there\".","examples":[{"japanese":"担当者は今、席を外しております。","romaji":"Tantōsha wa ima, seki o hazushite orimasu.","german":"The person in charge is away from his desk at the moment.","furigana":"たんとうしゃ|いま|せき|はず"},{"japanese":"私は午後もこちらにおります。","romaji":"Watashi wa gogo mo kochira ni orimasu.","german":"I will be here in the afternoon as well.","furigana":"わたし|ごご"},{"japanese":"いつも大変お世話になっております。","romaji":"Itsumo taihen osewa ni natte orimasu.","german":"Thank you always for your kind support.","furigana":"たいへん|せわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"ベル refers to a bell or buzzer that gives a signal: 玄関のベル, 自転車のベル, formerly also a telephone or school bell. Intransitively it is ベルが鳴る, transitively ベルを鳴らす, or ベルを押す for buttons. For door intercoms and chimes, インターホン and チャイム are often said today; large temple bells are 鐘, small bells 鈴.","examples":[{"japanese":"玄関のベルが鳴りました。","romaji":"Genkan no beru ga narimashita.","german":"The doorbell rang.","furigana":"げんかん|な"},{"japanese":"御用の方はベルを押してください。","romaji":"Goyō no kata wa beru o oshite kudasai.","german":"If you need anything, please press the bell.","furigana":"ごよう|かた|お"},{"japanese":"自転車のベルを鳴らしました。","romaji":"Jitensha no beru o narashimashita.","german":"I rang my bicycle bell.","furigana":"じてんしゃ|な"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 46"},
{"notes":"ハンドバッグ is a small (usually women's) handbag. For bags in general, バッグ or かばん is used; other types are ショルダーバッグ and トートバッグ. The spelling ハンドバック with ク is a common mistake. Typical are ハンドバッグに入れる and ハンドバッグを持つ.","examples":[{"japanese":"財布はハンドバッグに入っています。","romaji":"Saifu wa handobaggu ni haitte imasu.","german":"My wallet is in my handbag.","furigana":"さいふ|はい"},{"japanese":"母にハンドバッグを贈りました。","romaji":"Haha ni handobaggu o okurimashita.","german":"I gave my mother a handbag.","furigana":"はは|おく"},{"japanese":"電車の中にハンドバッグを忘れてしまいました。","romaji":"Densha no naka ni handobaggu o wasurete shimaimashita.","german":"I left my handbag on the train.","furigana":"でんしゃ|なか|わす"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"スクリーン mainly means a projection surface: the cinema screen or the screen in a classroom (スクリーンに映す). For the display of a computer, TV or phone, 画面 is the usual word; スクリーン appears there mainly in loanwords such as スクリーンショット. 大きなスクリーンで見る stresses the cinema experience.","examples":[{"japanese":"スクリーンに写真を映しました。","romaji":"Sukurīn ni shashin o utsushimashita.","german":"I projected photos onto the screen.","furigana":"しゃしん|うつ"},{"japanese":"大きなスクリーンで映画を見ました。","romaji":"Ōkina sukurīn de eiga o mimashita.","german":"I watched a movie on a big screen.","furigana":"おお|えいが|み"},{"japanese":"スマホのスクリーンショットを送ってください。","romaji":"Sumaho no sukuriinshotto o okutte kudasai.","german":"Please send me a screenshot of your phone.","furigana":"おく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"As a full verb, しまう means \"to put away in its place\" (usually written in hiragana, kanji 仕舞う). After the て-form (〜てしまう, colloquially 〜ちゃう) it expresses completion or regret; that grammar is covered in the grammar section.","examples":[{"japanese":"使ったはさみは引き出しにしまってください。","romaji":"Tsukatta hasami wa hikidashi ni shimatte kudasai.","german":"Please put the scissors you used back in the drawer.","furigana":"つか|ひ|だ"},{"japanese":"雨が強くなったので、今日は早めに店をしまいました。","romaji":"Ame ga tsuyoku natta node, kyō wa hayame ni mise o shimaimashita.","german":"The rain got heavy, so we closed the shop early today.","furigana":"あめ|つよ|きょう|はや|みせ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"After times of day and dates it is read ごろ and often written in hiragana: 十時ごろ \"around ten o'clock\". After nouns with の or after clauses it is ころ: 子どもの頃 \"when I was a child\". Related: この頃（このごろ）\"these days, lately\".","examples":[{"japanese":"子どもの頃、よく川で泳ぎました。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, yoku kawa de oyogimashita.","german":"When I was a child, I often swam in the river.","furigana":"こ|ころ|かわ|およ"},{"japanese":"明日は十時頃に駅に着きます。","romaji":"Ashita wa jūji goro ni eki ni tsukimasu.","german":"Tomorrow I will arrive at the station around ten o'clock.","furigana":"あした|じゅうじごろ|えき|つ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"The Japanese number system groups in steps of four digits: 万 = 10,000, 億 = 100,000,000, 兆 = 1,000,000,000,000. 一億 is \"one hundred million\", 十億 \"one billion\".","examples":[{"japanese":"日本の人口は約一億二千万人です。","romaji":"Nihon no jinkō wa yaku ichioku nisenman nin desu.","german":"Japan's population is about 120 million.","furigana":"にほん|じんこう|やくいちおくにせんまんにん"},{"japanese":"この絵は三億円で売れました。","romaji":"Kono e wa san-oku en de uremashita.","german":"This painting sold for 300 million yen.","furigana":"え|さんおくえん|う"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 25"},
{"notes":"Mostly email or mobile messages; as a verb, メールする means \"to send a message\". Postal mail is called 郵便 or 手紙.","examples":[{"japanese":"あとで詳しいことをメールで送ります。","romaji":"Ato de kuwashii koto o mēru de okurimasu.","german":"I'll send the details later by email.","furigana":"くわ|おく"},{"japanese":"昨日の夜、友達にメールしました。","romaji":"Kinō no yoru, tomodachi ni mēru shimashita.","german":"Last night I messaged a friend.","furigana":"きのう|よる|ともだち"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 7"},
{"notes":"インターネット refers to the Internet and is also shortened to ネット. インターネットで names the means of an action, such as an online booking. インターネットが使える or 使えない describes whether you can use an Internet connection. The long syllable ター is rendered with ā in intānetto.","examples":[{"japanese":"インターネットでホテルを予約しました。","romaji":"Intānetto de hoteru o yoyaku shimashita.","german":"I booked the hotel on the Internet.","furigana":"よやく"},{"japanese":"この部屋ではインターネットが使えません。","romaji":"Kono heya de wa intānetto ga tsukaemasen.","german":"The Internet can't be used in this room.","furigana":"へや|つか"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 18"},
{"notes":"Colloquial short form of スマートフォン; much more common in everyday speech than the long form. Older mobile phones are called 携帯（けいたい）.","examples":[{"japanese":"電車の中でスマホを見ている人が多いです。","romaji":"Densha no naka de sumaho o mite iru hito ga ōi desu.","german":"Many people look at their smartphones on the train.","furigana":"でんしゃ|なか|み|ひと|おお"},{"japanese":"スマホで地図を調べました。","romaji":"Sumaho de chizu o shirabemashita.","german":"I looked at the map on my smartphone.","furigana":"ちず|しら"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"The reply to 行ってきます: the person who stays says this to bid farewell to someone who is leaving and will come back. More polite: 行ってらっしゃいませ (for example in a hotel). On the return, ただいま and おかえりなさい follow.","examples":[{"japanese":"「行ってきます。」「行ってらっしゃい。気をつけてね。」","romaji":"\"Itte kimasu.\" \"Itterasshai. Ki o tsukete ne.\"","german":"\"I'm off.\" \"See you later. Be careful.\"","furigana":"い|い|き"},{"japanese":"ホテルのスタッフが「行ってらっしゃいませ」と言ってくれました。","romaji":"Hoteru no sutaffu ga \"itterasshaimase\" to itte kuremashita.","german":"The hotel staff said 「行ってらっしゃいませ」 to me.","furigana":"い|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Polite form of おめでとう. The occasion comes first, often with ご／お: 誕生日おめでとうございます, ご結婚おめでとうございます, at New Year 明けましておめでとうございます.","examples":[{"japanese":"ご結婚おめでとうございます。","romaji":"Gokekkon omedetō gozaimasu.","german":"Congratulations on your marriage.","furigana":"けっこん"},{"japanese":"明けましておめでとうございます。今年もよろしくお願いします。","romaji":"Akemashite omedetō gozaimasu. Kotoshi mo yoroshiku onegai shimasu.","german":"Happy New Year. I look forward to your continued support this year as well.","furigana":"あ|ことし|ねが"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 17"},
{"notes":"General term for a computer; for your own machine you usually say パソコン in daily life. The spelling without the long-vowel mark（コンピュータ）is widespread in technology and administration.","examples":[{"japanese":"このデータはコンピューターで計算しました。","romaji":"Kono dēta wa konpyūtā de keisan shimashita.","german":"I calculated this data on a computer.","furigana":"けいさん"},{"japanese":"父は会社でコンピューターのプログラムを作っています。","romaji":"Chichi wa kaisha de konpyūtā no puroguramu o tsukutte imasu.","german":"My father writes computer programs at his company.","furigana":"ちち|かいしゃ|つく"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 2"},
{"notes":"Short form of スーパーマーケット. As a prefix, スーパー also means \"super-\"; in daily life \"supermarket\" is the most common meaning.","examples":[{"japanese":"帰りにスーパーで牛乳を買います。","romaji":"Kaeri ni sūpā de gyūnyū o kaimasu.","german":"On the way home I buy milk at the supermarket.","furigana":"かえ|ぎゅうにゅう|か"},{"japanese":"このスーパーは夜十時まで開いています。","romaji":"Kono sūpā wa yoru jūji made aite imasu.","german":"This supermarket is open until 10 p.m.","furigana":"よるじゅうじ|あ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 5"},
{"notes":"Country name in katakana. Language: ロシア語. In compounds it is abbreviated as 露（ろ）, e.g. 日露.","examples":[{"japanese":"ロシアは世界で一番大きい国です。","romaji":"Roshia wa sekai de ichiban ōkii kuni desu.","german":"Russia is the largest country in the world.","furigana":"せかい|いちばんおお|くに"},{"japanese":"ロシアの冬はとても寒いそうです。","romaji":"Roshia no fuyu wa totemo samui sō desu.","german":"I hear the winters in Russia are very cold.","furigana":"ふゆ|さむ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 13"},
{"notes":"インド is the country name India. インドの… assigns something to the country; インドに住む describes living there. A person from India is called インド人. インドネシア, by contrast, means Indonesia, a different country. What is learned is the usual katakana spelling, not the kanji spelling 印度.","examples":[{"japanese":"インドのカレーは辛いです。","romaji":"Indo no karē wa karai desu.","german":"Indian curry is spicy.","furigana":"から"},{"japanese":"インドには人がたくさん住んでいます。","romaji":"Indo ni wa hito ga takusan sunde imasu.","german":"A lot of people live in India.","furigana":"ひと|す"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 1"},
{"notes":"カナダ is the country name Canada. カナダで names the place of an activity, such as a language course. カナダの… assigns something to the country, such as its mountains. The usual katakana spelling is the one to learn; the kanji spelling 加奈陀 is rare.","examples":[{"japanese":"カナダで英語を勉強しました。","romaji":"Kanada de eigo o benkyō shimashita.","german":"I studied English in Canada.","furigana":"えいご|べんきょう"},{"japanese":"カナダの山はとてもきれいでした。","romaji":"Kanada no yama wa totemo kirei deshita.","german":"The mountains in Canada were very beautiful.","furigana":"やま"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"ブラジル is the country name Brazil. ブラジルでは introduces a statement about the country; ブラジルのコーヒー names the origin of a product. The official language is called ポルトガル語. The word is written in katakana and is a noun.","examples":[{"japanese":"ブラジルではポルトガル語を話します。","romaji":"Burajiru de wa porutogarugo o hanashimasu.","german":"In Brazil they speak Portuguese.","furigana":"ご|はな"},{"japanese":"ブラジルのコーヒーを買いました。","romaji":"Burajiru no kōhī o kaimashita.","german":"I bought Brazilian coffee.","furigana":"か"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 1"},
{"notes":"Country name in katakana. Note: オーストリア is Austria, a difference of just one ラ. In news also 豪州（ごうしゅう）.","examples":[{"japanese":"オーストラリアにはカンガルーがいます。","romaji":"Ōsutoraria ni wa kangarū ga imasu.","german":"There are kangaroos in Australia."},{"japanese":"十二月のオーストラリアは夏です。","romaji":"Jūnigatsu no Ōsutoraria wa natsu desu.","german":"December is summer in Australia.","furigana":"じゅうにがつ|なつ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 11"},
{"notes":"Country name in katakana. Similar-sounding are タイ (necktie) and 鯛（たい）(sea bream); context decides.","examples":[{"japanese":"タイは一年中暑いです。","romaji":"Tai wa ichinenjū atsui desu.","german":"It's hot all year round in Thailand.","furigana":"いちねんじゅうあつ"},{"japanese":"タイ料理のレストランで晩ご飯を食べました。","romaji":"Tai ryōri no resutoran de bangohan o tabemashita.","german":"I had dinner at a Thai restaurant.","furigana":"りょうり|ばん|はん|た"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 1"},
{"notes":"ベトナム is the country name Vietnam. ベトナムの学生 describes the origin of a student; ベトナムのコーヒー the origin or national style of the coffee. For a person from Vietnam you say ベトナム人. The noun is written in katakana.","examples":[{"japanese":"同じクラスにベトナムの学生がいます。","romaji":"Onaji kurasu ni Betonamu no gakusei ga imasu.","german":"There is a Vietnamese student in my class.","furigana":"おな|がくせい"},{"japanese":"ベトナムのコーヒーは甘いです。","romaji":"Betonamu no kōhī wa amai desu.","german":"Vietnamese coffee is sweet.","furigana":"あま"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 16"},
{"notes":"Country + 語（ご）= language. The Cyrillic script is called キリル文字.","examples":[{"japanese":"ロシア語の文字は読めません。","romaji":"Roshiago no moji wa yomemasen.","german":"I can't read Russian script.","furigana":"ご|もじ|よ"},{"japanese":"彼女はロシア語と日本語を話します。","romaji":"Kanojo wa roshiago to nihongo o hanashimasu.","german":"She speaks Russian and Japanese.","furigana":"かのじょ|ご|にほんご|はな"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"ダウンロード is the downloading of digital data. ダウンロードする is a transitive verb: the app or file takes を. As a noun it can take に to name the activity that requires time. Transferring in the opposite direction is アップロード.","examples":[{"japanese":"このアプリを無料でダウンロードしました。","romaji":"Kono apuri o muryō de daunrōdo shimashita.","german":"I downloaded this app for free.","furigana":"むりょう"},{"japanese":"ダウンロードに時間がかかっています。","romaji":"Daunrōdo ni jikan ga kakatte imasu.","german":"The download is taking a long time.","furigana":"じかん"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"\"To enter a password\": パスワードを入力する. The PIN of a bank card, on the other hand, is called 暗証番号.","examples":[{"japanese":"パスワードを忘れてしまいました。","romaji":"Pasuwādo o wasurete shimaimashita.","german":"I forgot my password.","furigana":"わす"},{"japanese":"パスワードは八文字以上にしてください。","romaji":"Pasuwādo wa hachimoji ijō ni shite kudasai.","german":"Please make your password at least eight characters long.","furigana":"はちもじいじょう"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"As a verb with する: サイトにログインする. Opposite: ログアウト.","examples":[{"japanese":"メールアドレスとパスワードでログインします。","romaji":"Mēruadoresu to pasuwādo de roguin shimasu.","german":"You log in with your email address and password."},{"japanese":"朝からログインできなくて困っています。","romaji":"Asa kara roguin dekinakute komatte imasu.","german":"I haven't been able to log in since this morning and I'm at a loss.","furigana":"あさ|こま"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"アカウント here means a user account with a digital service. アカウントを作る means to create it; 仕事用です means it is intended for work purposes. A bank account is usually 口座. The noun アカウント refers to the access or the account, not to the associated password.","examples":[{"japanese":"新しいアカウントを作りました。","romaji":"Atarashii akaunto o tsukurimashita.","german":"I created a new account.","furigana":"あたら|つく"},{"japanese":"このアカウントは仕事用です。","romaji":"Kono akaunto wa shigotoyō desu.","german":"This account is for work.","furigana":"しごとよう"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Short for ウェブサイト. Also \"site, grounds\" (e.g. キャンプサイト).","examples":[{"japanese":"このサイトで安いホテルを探せます。","romaji":"Kono saito de yasui hoteru o sagasemasu.","german":"You can find cheap hotels on this website.","furigana":"やす|さが"},{"japanese":"詳しいことは会社のサイトを見てください。","romaji":"Kuwashii koto wa kaisha no saito o mite kudasai.","german":"Please see the company's website for details.","furigana":"くわ|かいしゃ|み"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"ホームページ can mean the start page, a web page or the whole website. In the examples it means the web presence of a shop or a school. ホームページで調べる describes looking something up there; ホームページに出ている says that something can be seen there. Both long vowels appear in hōmupēji.","examples":[{"japanese":"お店のホームページで営業時間を調べました。","romaji":"Omise no hōmupēji de eigyō jikan o shirabemashita.","german":"I checked the opening hours on the shop's website.","furigana":"みせ|えいぎょうじかん|しら"},{"japanese":"学校のホームページに写真が出ています。","romaji":"Gakkō no hōmupēji ni shashin ga dete imasu.","german":"There are photos on the school's website.","furigana":"がっこう|しゃしん|で"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"As a verb with する (to link). \"To send a link\": リンクを送る. Same sound: リンク (skating rink).","examples":[{"japanese":"会議のリンクをメールで送ります。","romaji":"Kaigi no rinku o mēru de okurimasu.","german":"I'll send the link to the meeting by email.","furigana":"かいぎ|おく"},{"japanese":"このリンクをクリックしてください。","romaji":"Kono rinku o kurikku shite kudasai.","german":"Please click this link."}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"As a verb with する: ボタンをクリックする. Double-click: ダブルクリック. On a smartphone, タップ is used more.","examples":[{"japanese":"右上のボタンをクリックしてください。","romaji":"Migiue no botan o kurikku shite kudasai.","german":"Please click the button at the top right.","furigana":"みぎうえ"},{"japanese":"クリック一つで注文できます。","romaji":"Kurikku hitotsu de chūmon dekimasu.","german":"You can order with a single click.","furigana":"ひと|ちゅうもん"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Full form of スマホ; common in manuals and news, but in conversation almost always スマホ.","examples":[{"japanese":"スマートフォンの使い方を祖母に教えました。","romaji":"Sumātofon no tsukaikata o sobo ni oshiemashita.","german":"I taught my grandmother how to use a smartphone.","furigana":"つか|かた|そぼ|おし"},{"japanese":"スマートフォンをお持ちの方は、画面を見せてください。","romaji":"Sumātofon o omochi no kata wa, gamen o misete kudasai.","german":"If you have a smartphone, please show the screen.","furigana":"も|かた|がめん|み"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"チャット here means a digital chat. With する it becomes a verb, \"to chat\"; 友達と names the conversation partner. チャットで describes the communication channel, for example for questions to a service. The identically sounding word for the currency kyat is a different meaning.","examples":[{"japanese":"夜は友達とチャットしています。","romaji":"Yoru wa tomodachi to chatto shite imasu.","german":"I chat with my friends in the evening.","furigana":"よる|ともだち"},{"japanese":"質問はチャットでも受け付けています。","romaji":"Shitsumon wa chatto demo uketsukete imasu.","german":"We also accept questions by chat.","furigana":"しつもん|う|つ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Tablet computer. In a medical context it also means \"tablet\" (pill), but for that 錠剤（じょうざい）is mostly said.","examples":[{"japanese":"子どもはタブレットでゲームをしています。","romaji":"Kodomo wa taburetto de gēmu o shite imasu.","german":"The child is playing games on a tablet.","furigana":"こ"},{"japanese":"会議にはタブレットを持って行きます。","romaji":"Kaigi ni wa taburetto o motte ikimasu.","german":"I take a tablet to meetings.","furigana":"かいぎ|も|い"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Often shortened to メアド or アドレス. The @ sign is called アットマーク.","examples":[{"japanese":"メールアドレスを教えてください。","romaji":"Mēruadoresu o oshiete kudasai.","german":"Please tell me your email address.","furigana":"おし"},{"japanese":"メールアドレスを変えたので、連絡します。","romaji":"Mēruadoresu o kaeta node, renraku shimasu.","german":"I'm contacting you because I changed my email address.","furigana":"か|れんらく"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 50"},
{"notes":"キーボード means \"keyboard\" on a computer and, in a band, usually the \"keyboard\" as an electronic keyed instrument. For typing, 打つ or 入力する fits; for playing, 弾く. The two long vowels are written with ī and ō in kībōdo. Which object is meant follows from the situation.","examples":[{"japanese":"このキーボードは打ちやすいです。","romaji":"Kono kībōdo wa uchiyasui desu.","german":"This keyboard is easy to type on.","furigana":"う"},{"japanese":"キーボードにコーヒーをこぼしてしまいました。","romaji":"Kībōdo ni kōhī o koboshite shimaimashita.","german":"I spilled coffee on the keyboard."},{"japanese":"姉はバンドでキーボードを弾いています。","romaji":"Ane wa bando de kībōdo o hiite imasu.","german":"My older sister plays keyboard in a band.","furigana":"あね|ひ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"The computer mouse. The animal is called ネズミ in everyday speech; マウス otherwise refers to laboratory mice.","examples":[{"japanese":"マウスが動かなくなりました。","romaji":"Mausu ga ugokanaku narimashita.","german":"The mouse stopped working.","furigana":"うご"},{"japanese":"マウスでファイルを選んでください。","romaji":"Mausu de fairu o erande kudasai.","german":"Please select the file with the mouse.","furigana":"えら"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 49"},
{"notes":"Short form of インターネット (ネットで買う = to buy online). Also a real net, e.g. in tennis.","examples":[{"japanese":"この本はネットで買いました。","romaji":"Kono hon wa netto de kaimashita.","german":"I bought this book online.","furigana":"ほん|か"},{"japanese":"ボールがネットに当たりました。","romaji":"Bōru ga netto ni atarimashita.","german":"The ball hit the net.","furigana":"あ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Also written プリンタ. \"To print out\": 印刷する or, colloquially, プリントする.","examples":[{"japanese":"プリンターの紙がなくなりました。","romaji":"Purintā no kami ga nakunarimashita.","german":"The printer is out of paper.","furigana":"かみ"},{"japanese":"コンビニのプリンターで資料を印刷しました。","romaji":"Konbini no purintā de shiryō o insatsu shimashita.","german":"I printed the documents on the printer at the convenience store.","furigana":"しりょう|いんさつ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"豚 here means the animal pig. 豚を飼う means to keep pigs; 豚の鼻 means the snout or the nose of the animal. Pork is unambiguously called 豚肉. As a term for a person, 豚 can be strongly insulting; the examples speak exclusively about animals.","examples":[{"japanese":"農場で豚と牛を見ました。","romaji":"Nōjō de buta to ushi o mimashita.","german":"I saw pigs and cows on the farm.","furigana":"のうじょう|ぶた|うし|み"},{"japanese":"豚は鼻がとてもいい動物です。","romaji":"Buta wa hana ga totemo ii dōbutsu desu.","german":"Pigs are animals with a very good sense of smell.","furigana":"ぶた|はな|どうぶつ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"A zoo animal, written in katakana. The lion as a zodiac sign: しし座.","examples":[{"japanese":"動物園でライオンが寝ていました。","romaji":"Dōbutsuen de raion ga nete imashita.","german":"The lion was sleeping at the zoo.","furigana":"どうぶつえん|ね"},{"japanese":"ライオンは「百獣の王」と呼ばれます。","romaji":"Raion wa “hyakujū no ō” to yobaremasu.","german":"The lion is called the \"king of beasts\".","furigana":"ひゃくじゅう|おう|よ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"イルカ means a dolphin. The katakana spelling is usual here; 海豚 is a rare kanji spelling with the reading いるか. イルカのショー names a performance with dolphins, イルカは… a statement about the animals. 頭がいい in the second example means that they are intelligent.","examples":[{"japanese":"水族館でイルカのショーを見ました。","romaji":"Suizokukan de iruka no shō o mimashita.","german":"I saw a dolphin show at the aquarium.","furigana":"すいぞくかん|み"},{"japanese":"イルカはとても頭がいい動物です。","romaji":"Iruka wa totemo atama ga ii dōbutsu desu.","german":"Dolphins are very intelligent animals.","furigana":"あたま|どうぶつ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"A zoo animal, written in katakana. Penguins live almost exclusively in the Southern Hemisphere; in Japan you see them in zoos and aquariums (水族館).","examples":[{"japanese":"ペンギンは鳥ですが、空を飛べません。","romaji":"Pengin wa tori desu ga, sora o tobemasen.","german":"Penguins are birds, but they can't fly.","furigana":"とり|そら|と"},{"japanese":"子どもたちはペンギンが歩くのを見て笑いました。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa pengin ga aruku no o mite waraimashita.","german":"The children laughed when they saw the penguins walk.","furigana":"こ|ある|み|わら"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"A zoo animal in katakana; very popular in Japan. A police patrol car is jokingly called パンダ because of its black-and-white paint job.","examples":[{"japanese":"動物園でパンダの赤ちゃんを見ました。","romaji":"Dōbutsuen de panda no akachan o mimashita.","german":"I saw a baby panda at the zoo.","furigana":"どうぶつえん|あか|み"},{"japanese":"パンダは一日中竹を食べています。","romaji":"Panda wa ichinichijū take o tabete imasu.","german":"Pandas eat bamboo all day long.","furigana":"いちにちじゅうたけ|た"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 10"},
{"notes":"Mostly in katakana; the kanji 麒麟 also denote the Chinese mythical creature qilin (logo of the Kirin brewery).","examples":[{"japanese":"キリンは首がとても長いです。","romaji":"Kirin wa kubi ga totemo nagai desu.","german":"Giraffes have very long necks.","furigana":"くび|なが"},{"japanese":"キリンが高い木の葉を食べています。","romaji":"Kirin ga takai ki no ha o tabete imasu.","german":"The giraffe is eating the leaves of a tall tree.","furigana":"たか|き|は|た"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"百円ショップ is the name of a type of store associated with the base price of 100 yen. It does not follow that every single product there has the same price. 百円ショップで買う describes a purchase there; 百円ショップには…もある names a further part of the assortment. Colloquially such a store is also called 百均（ひゃっきん）.","examples":[{"japanese":"このお皿は百円ショップで買いました。","romaji":"Kono osara wa hyakuen shoppu de kaimashita.","german":"I bought this plate at a 100-yen shop.","furigana":"さら|ひゃくえん|か"},{"japanese":"百円ショップには文房具もあります。","romaji":"Hyakuen shoppu ni wa bunbōgu mo arimasu.","german":"The 100-yen shop also has stationery.","furigana":"ひゃくえん|ぶんぼうぐ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"ドラッグストア is a store for over-the-counter medicines and other everyday goods. ドラッグストアで買う names the place of purchase; 駅前のドラッグストア refers to a specific branch at the station. 薬局 is also used for a pharmacy; the name alone does not say whether a branch processes prescriptions.","examples":[{"japanese":"ドラッグストアで風邪薬を買いました。","romaji":"Doraggusutoa de kazegusuri o kaimashita.","german":"I bought cold medicine at the drugstore.","furigana":"かぜぐすり|か"},{"japanese":"駅前のドラッグストアは夜十時まで開いています。","romaji":"Ekimae no doraggusutoa wa yoru jūji made aite imasu.","german":"The drugstore in front of the station is open until ten at night.","furigana":"えきまえ|よるじゅうじ|あ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"ドライヤー here means a hair dryer. ドライヤーで髪を乾かす describes drying the hair with the device; ドライヤーはありますか asks whether there is one. The long final syllable is written doraiyā. The card shows the name of the device, not a verb.","examples":[{"japanese":"ドライヤーで髪を乾かします。","romaji":"Doraiyā de kami o kawakashimasu.","german":"I dry my hair with a hair dryer.","furigana":"かみ|かわ"},{"japanese":"ホテルの部屋にドライヤーはありますか。","romaji":"Hoteru no heya ni doraiyā wa arimasu ka.","german":"Is there a hair dryer in the hotel room?","furigana":"へや"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Continent in katakana (from Portuguese \"Europa\"). In compounds: 欧（おう）, e.g. 欧米 \"Europe and America, the West\".","examples":[{"japanese":"夏休みにヨーロッパを旅行しました。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi ni Yōroppa o ryokō shimashita.","german":"I traveled around Europe during the summer vacation.","furigana":"なつやす|りょこう"},{"japanese":"ヨーロッパには古い町がたくさんあります。","romaji":"Yōroppa ni wa furui machi ga takusan arimasu.","german":"There are many old towns in Europe.","furigana":"ふる|まち"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 7"},
{"notes":"アジア is the continent Asia. アジアの国 refers to a country in Asia; アジアの料理 dishes from Asian cuisines. 東南 in 東南アジア means southeast. To express origin or relation to the continent, the noun is often linked with の.","examples":[{"japanese":"日本はアジアの国です。","romaji":"Nihon wa Ajia no kuni desu.","german":"Japan is an Asian country.","furigana":"にほん|くに"},{"japanese":"アジアの料理はどれも好きです。","romaji":"Ajia no ryōri wa dore mo suki desu.","german":"I like all Asian cuisines.","furigana":"りょうり|す"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 16"},
{"notes":"アフリカ is the continent of Africa and is written in katakana. アフリカで names the place of an activity; アフリカには describes what exists on the continent. The origin is expressed with アフリカの, for example for goods. 五十以上 includes fifty and means at least fifty.","examples":[{"japanese":"アフリカで野生のライオンを見ました。","romaji":"Afurika de yasei no raion o mimashita.","german":"I saw wild lions in Africa.","furigana":"やせい|み"},{"japanese":"アフリカには五十以上の国があります。","romaji":"Afurika ni wa gojū ijō no kuni ga arimasu.","german":"There are more than fifty countries in Africa.","furigana":"ごじゅういじょう|くに"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 35"},
{"notes":"ごめんください is here the polite call at the entrance of a house or shop: you draw attention to yourself and, in effect, ask for admission. With どなたかいらっしゃいますか you ask whether anyone is there. It is not a general greeting for every encounter. The spelling ご免ください is attested; the usual kana form is what is learned.","examples":[{"japanese":"ごめんください。どなたかいらっしゃいますか。","romaji":"Gomen kudasai. Donata ka irasshaimasu ka.","german":"Hello. Is anyone there?"},{"japanese":"店の入り口で「ごめんください」と声をかけた。","romaji":"Mise no iriguchi de \"gomen kudasai\" to koe o kaketa.","german":"I called out 「ごめんください」 at the entrance of the shop.","furigana":"みせ|い|ぐち|こえ"},{"japanese":"「ごめんください」と何度呼んでも、返事がありませんでした。","romaji":"\"Gomen kudasai\" to nando yonde mo, henji ga arimasen deshita.","german":"No matter how many times I called 「ごめんください」, there was no answer.","furigana":"なんどよ|へんじ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"ああ means \"in that way\" or \"like that\" and points to something that speaker and listener regard as distant or known. ああなりたい names a role model; ああ言われると reacts to the way something was said. This card covers the adverb of the series こう・そう・ああ・どう, not the identically sounding exclamation \"Ah!\".","examples":[{"japanese":"私も将来ああなりたい。","romaji":"Watashi mo shōrai ā naritai.","german":"I want to become like that in the future, too.","furigana":"わたし|しょうらい"},{"japanese":"ああ言われると、断れない。","romaji":"Ā iwareru to, kotowarenai.","german":"When someone says it like that, I can't refuse.","furigana":"い|ことわ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 9"},
{"notes":"家内 has three related meanings here with the same reading かない. As a humble term it means one's own wife when speaking to others; 妻 is another term for this, while 奥さん is used for another person's wife. In 家内一同, 家内 refers to the whole family. The spatial meaning \"inside the house\" also appears as a component of 家内工業, home industry. For an everyday statement of location, 家の中 is used. The respective combination shows which meaning is intended.","examples":[{"japanese":"家内は今、出かけております。","romaji":"Kanai wa ima, dekakete orimasu.","german":"My wife is out at the moment.","furigana":"かない|いま|で"},{"japanese":"家内と二人で温泉に行きました。","romaji":"Kanai to futari de onsen ni ikimashita.","german":"My wife and I went to a hot spring, just the two of us.","furigana":"かない|ふたり|おんせん|い"},{"japanese":"家内一同、心から感謝しております。","romaji":"Kanai ichidō, kokoro kara kansha shite orimasu.","german":"My whole family is deeply grateful.","furigana":"かないいちどう|こころ|かんしゃ"},{"japanese":"家内工業の仕事を自宅でしていました。","romaji":"Kanai kōgyō no shigoto o jitaku de shite imashita.","german":"I did cottage-industry work at home.","furigana":"かないこうぎょう|しごと|じたく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"Several meanings: soft, gentle (ソフトな声); software (ゲームソフト); soft-serve ice cream (ソフトクリーム).","examples":[{"japanese":"新しいゲームソフトを買いました。","romaji":"Atarashii gēmu sofuto o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a new video game.","furigana":"あたら|か"},{"japanese":"彼女はソフトな声で話す。","romaji":"Kanojo wa sofuto na koe de hanasu.","german":"She speaks in a soft voice.","furigana":"かのじょ|こえ|はな"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 15"},
{"notes":"\"So much, to that extent.\" Mostly with a negative: それほど難しくない = \"not that difficult\". Similar: そんなに.","examples":[{"japanese":"今日はそれほど寒くないです。","romaji":"Kyō wa sorehodo samukunai desu.","german":"It's not that cold today.","furigana":"きょう|さむ"},{"japanese":"それほど言うなら、一度やってみよう。","romaji":"Sorehodo iu nara, ichido yatte miyō.","german":"If you insist that much, let's try it once.","furigana":"い|いちど"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"飛行場 refers to an airfield or landing field where aircraft take off and land. 空港 is the term for an airport; both words can refer to the same facility. 飛行場まで names the way there, 飛行場がある the presence of such a facility. The reading has two long vowels: hikōjō.","examples":[{"japanese":"飛行場まで父を迎えに行きました。","romaji":"Hikōjō made chichi o mukae ni ikimashita.","german":"I went to the airfield to pick up my father.","furigana":"ひこうじょう|ちち|むか|い"},{"japanese":"この島には小さな飛行場があります。","romaji":"Kono shima ni wa chiisa na hikōjō ga arimasu.","german":"There is a small airfield on this island.","furigana":"しま|ちい|ひこうじょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"ファックス refers to a fax and also to the fax machine. ファックスで送る means to send something by fax; ファックスを使う can refer to the machine or to the transmission. With する it becomes a verb for faxing. The stand-alone form on this card is a noun; the spelling FAX is also common.","examples":[{"japanese":"申込書をファックスで送ってください。","romaji":"Mōshikomisho o fakkusu de okutte kudasai.","german":"Please send the application form by fax.","furigana":"もうしこみしょ|おく"},{"japanese":"この病院はまだファックスを使っている。","romaji":"Kono byōin wa mada fakkusu o tsukatte iru.","german":"This hospital still uses fax.","furigana":"びょういん|つか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"The capital prefecture Tokyo (東京都) as an administrative unit; mostly in compounds: 都内 (within Tokyo), 都庁 (prefectural government), 都知事 (governor).","examples":[{"japanese":"都の図書館で本を借りた。","romaji":"To no toshokan de hon o karita.","german":"I borrowed a book from the Metropolitan library.","furigana":"と|としょかん|ほん|か"},{"japanese":"都内の電車はとても便利です。","romaji":"Tonai no densha wa totemo benri desu.","german":"The trains in Tokyo are very convenient.","furigana":"とない|でんしゃ|べんり"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"\"But, however\", at the beginning of a sentence or after a sentence. More polite and more written than けど, more casual than しかし. Also as a soft introduction: すみませんけれども… (Excuse me, but ...).","examples":[{"japanese":"この店は高いけれども、とてもおいしい。","romaji":"Kono mise wa takai keredomo, totemo oishii.","german":"This restaurant is expensive, but very delicious.","furigana":"みせ|たか"},{"japanese":"雨が降っていた。けれども、試合は行われた。","romaji":"Ame ga futte ita. Keredomo, shiai wa okonawareta.","german":"It had been raining. Nevertheless, the match was held.","furigana":"あめ|ふ|しあい|おこな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"\"Not at all, not a bit\", only with a negative. More colloquial than 少しも or 全然.","examples":[{"japanese":"この映画はちっとも面白くなかった。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa chittomo omoshirokunakatta.","german":"This movie wasn't interesting at all.","furigana":"えいが|おもしろ"},{"japanese":"何度説明しても、ちっとも分かってくれない。","romaji":"Nando setsumei shite mo, chittomo wakatte kurenai.","german":"No matter how many times I explain, he doesn't understand at all.","furigana":"なんどせつめい|わ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 49"},
{"notes":"\"At last, finally\", after a long wait or as a final result, positive or negative. The kanji 到頭 is rare. Similar: ついに, やっと (positive only, after effort).","examples":[{"japanese":"三年かかって、とうとう家が完成した。","romaji":"Sannen kakatte, tōtō ie ga kansei shita.","german":"After three years, the house was finally completed.","furigana":"さんねん|いえ|かんせい"},{"japanese":"待ったけれど、彼はとうとう来なかった。","romaji":"Matta keredo, kare wa tōtō konakatta.","german":"I waited, but he didn't come in the end.","furigana":"ま|かれ|こ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"色んな stands directly before a noun and means \"various\" or \"all sorts of\". It is a colloquial prenominal word（連体詞）; neither な nor だ is attached to it. In familiar conversation it is often written いろんな. 色々な has a different form. The examples show different countries of origin and various kinds of bread.","examples":[{"japanese":"旅行でいろんな国の人と友達になった。","romaji":"Ryokō de ironna kuni no hito to tomodachi ni natta.","german":"On the trip I made friends with people from various countries.","furigana":"りょこう|くに|ひと|ともだち"},{"japanese":"この店には色んな種類のパンがある。","romaji":"Kono mise ni wa ironna shurui no pan ga aru.","german":"This shop has all kinds of bread.","furigana":"みせ|いろ|しゅるい"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Polite for other people's children; about one's own children, 子ども or うちの子 is said.","examples":[{"japanese":"お子さんは何歳ですか。","romaji":"Okosan wa nansai desu ka.","german":"How old is your child?","furigana":"こ|なんさい"},{"japanese":"田中さんのお子さんは大学生だそうです。","romaji":"Tanaka-san no okosan wa daigakusei da sō desu.","german":"I hear Mr./Ms. Tanaka's child is a university student.","furigana":"たなか|こ|だいがくせい"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 30"},
{"notes":"Child-language \"Dad\"; adults also use it within the family. Formal: 父, 父親; as a form of address: お父さん.","examples":[{"japanese":"パパ、見て見て！","romaji":"Papa, mite mite!","german":"Dad, look, look!","furigana":"み|み"},{"japanese":"うちのパパは料理が上手です。","romaji":"Uchi no papa wa ryōri ga jōzu desu.","german":"Our dad is good at cooking.","furigana":"りょうり|じょうず"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"ハンバーグ is a hamburger steak, i.e. the meat dish without a burger bun. The hamburger in a bun is called ハンバーガー. ハンバーグです can name the dish on the menu; ハンバーグを焼く describes its preparation. The long syllable バー is rendered with ā in hanbāgu.","examples":[{"japanese":"今日の晩ご飯はハンバーグです。","romaji":"Kyō no bangohan wa hanbāgu desu.","german":"Today's dinner is hamburger steak.","furigana":"きょう|ばん|はん"},{"japanese":"子どもはハンバーグが大好きです。","romaji":"Kodomo wa hanbāgu ga daisuki desu.","german":"Kids love hamburger steak.","furigana":"こ|だいす"},{"japanese":"フライパンでハンバーグを焼いています。","romaji":"Furaipan de hanbāgu o yaite imasu.","german":"I'm cooking hamburger steaks in a frying pan.","furigana":"や"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"Very polite form of ある (\"there is, to have\") and, in でございます, of です. Typical in service situations: こちらにございます. The kanji 御座います is rare.","examples":[{"japanese":"お手洗いは二階にございます。","romaji":"Otearai wa nikai ni gozaimasu.","german":"The restroom is on the second floor.","furigana":"てあら|にかい"},{"japanese":"何かご質問はございますか。","romaji":"Nanika goshitsumon wa gozaimasu ka.","german":"Do you have any questions?","furigana":"なに|しつもん"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 50"},
{"notes":"いくらでも expresses a freely chosen or very large amount. For refills of rice it means \"as much as you like\"; 時間ならいくらでもある stresses that there is plenty of time. The expression is used here as an adverb and does not denote a number. The meaning follows from the thing mentioned and the situation of the conversation.","examples":[{"japanese":"ご飯はいくらでもおかわりできます。","romaji":"Gohan wa ikura demo okawari dekimasu.","german":"You can have as many refills of rice as you like.","furigana":"はん"},{"japanese":"時間ならいくらでもあるよ。","romaji":"Jikan nara ikura demo aru yo.","german":"As for time, I have plenty.","furigana":"じかん"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"〜代 here is an attached word element for costs or a fee: 電気代 is the electricity cost, タクシー代 the taxi fare. The wavy line shows that another word comes before it. This suffix is not an independent sentence particle. Other uses of 代, such as decades of age or generations, do not belong to this card.","examples":[{"japanese":"今月は電気代が高かった。","romaji":"Kongetsu wa denkidai ga takakatta.","german":"The electricity bill was high this month.","furigana":"こんげつ|でんきだい|たか"},{"japanese":"タクシー代は会社が払います。","romaji":"Takushīdai wa kaisha ga haraimasu.","german":"The company pays the taxi fare.","furigana":"だい|かいしゃ|はら"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"行ってまいります is said by the person leaving when departing temporarily. まいります is the humble polite form; the greeting corresponds functionally to 行ってきます and implies a return is expected. In the office it can be used to announce going to an appointment. The person staying can reply with 行ってらっしゃい.","examples":[{"japanese":"では、銀行へ行ってまいります。","romaji":"Dewa, ginkō e itte mairimasu.","german":"Well then, I'll go to the bank.","furigana":"ぎんこう|い"},{"japanese":"「行ってまいります」と言って、部長に頭を下げた。","romaji":"\"Itte mairimasu\" to itte, buchō ni atama o sageta.","german":"Saying 「行ってまいります」, he bowed to the department head.","furigana":"い|い|ぶちょう|あたま|さ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"\"Sorry to keep you waiting\" - very common in restaurants and shops; among friends, simply お待たせ！ Even more polite: 大変お待たせいたしました.","examples":[{"japanese":"お待たせしました。コーヒーでございます。","romaji":"Omatase shimashita. Kōhī de gozaimasu.","german":"Sorry to keep you waiting. Here is your coffee.","furigana":"ま"},{"japanese":"すみません、お待たせしました。","romaji":"Sumimasen, omatase shimashita.","german":"Excuse me, sorry to keep you waiting.","furigana":"ま"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Association, club (テニスクラブ; at schools also クラブ活動 = 部活), nightclub, golf club. The card suit of clubs is also called クラブ.","examples":[{"japanese":"高校では写真クラブに入っていました。","romaji":"Kōkō de wa shashin kurabu ni haitte imashita.","german":"I was in the photography club in high school.","furigana":"こうこう|しゃしん|はい"},{"japanese":"週末はテニスクラブで練習しています。","romaji":"Shūmatsu wa tenisu kurabu de renshū shite imasu.","german":"I practice at a tennis club on weekends.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|れんしゅう"},{"japanese":"ゴルフのクラブを借りました。","romaji":"Gorufu no kurabu o karimashita.","german":"I borrowed a golf club.","furigana":"か"},{"japanese":"クラブのエースを引きました。","romaji":"Kurabu no ēsu o hikimashita.","german":"I drew the ace of clubs.","furigana":"ひ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 14"},
{"notes":"チェック here means checking or a check pattern. チェックする describes checking, for example a weather forecast. チェックのシャツ denotes a checked shirt; in this meaning the noun connects with の. To check in is チェックイン and is not taught on this card. In financial language, チェック can also mean a cheque, for example in Japanese information about American banks. 小切手 is the unambiguous Japanese term for this means of payment. In このチェックに署名する it is about signing a cheque, not about checking.","examples":[{"japanese":"出かける前に天気予報をチェックする。","romaji":"Dekakeru mae ni tenki yohō o chekku suru.","german":"I check the weather forecast before going out.","furigana":"で|まえ|てんきよほう"},{"japanese":"彼はいつもチェックのシャツを着ている。","romaji":"Kare wa itsumo chekku no shatsu o kite iru.","german":"He always wears checked shirts.","furigana":"かれ|き"},{"japanese":"このチェックに署名してください。","romaji":"Kono chekku ni shomei shite kudasai.","german":"Please sign this check.","furigana":"しょめい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"糸 means thread, yarn or a thin string. 針に糸を通す means to pull a thread through the eye of a needle; 糸で縫う describes sewing with thread. With the 三味線, 糸 can mean the string. The identically read 意図, by contrast, means \"intention\" and is a different word.","examples":[{"japanese":"針に糸を通すのは難しい。","romaji":"Hari ni ito o tōsu no wa muzukashii.","german":"Threading a needle is difficult.","furigana":"はり|いと|とお|むずか"},{"japanese":"赤い糸で名前を縫いました。","romaji":"Akai ito de namae o nuimashita.","german":"I sewed my name with red thread.","furigana":"あか|いと|なまえ|ぬ"},{"japanese":"三味線の糸が切れました。","romaji":"Shamisen no ito ga kiremashita.","german":"A string on the shamisen broke.","furigana":"しゃみせん|いと|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"Cloud. Cloudy: 曇り（くもり）. Same sound: 蜘蛛 (spider), in standard Japanese even with the same pitch pattern.","examples":[{"japanese":"空に白い雲が浮かんでいる。","romaji":"Sora ni shiroi kumo ga ukande iru.","german":"White clouds are floating in the sky.","furigana":"そら|しろ|くも|う"},{"japanese":"黒い雲が出てきたから、雨が降りそうだ。","romaji":"Kuroi kumo ga dete kita kara, ame ga furisō da.","german":"Dark clouds have appeared, so it looks like rain.","furigana":"くろ|くも|で|あめ|ふ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"気 bezeichnet hier das innere Empfinden, Gemüt oder die Stimmung und steht häufig in festen Verbindungen. 気がする heißt, ein Gefühl oder einen Eindruck zu haben; 気が強い beschreibt eine durchsetzungsstarke Art. 気が重い drückt ein belastetes Gemüt aus. 気をつける heißt „aufpassen“. Die passende deutsche Übersetzung hängt jeweils von der Verbindung ab; die philosophische Bedeutung „Qi“ wird hier nicht behandelt.","examples":[{"japanese":"雨が降りそうな気がする。","romaji":"Ame ga furisō na ki ga suru.","german":"Ich habe das Gefühl, dass es regnen wird.","furigana":"あめ|ふ|き"},{"japanese":"彼女は気が強いけれど、優しい人だ。","romaji":"Kanojo wa ki ga tsuyoi keredo, yasashii hito da.","german":"Sie ist willensstark, aber ein herzlicher Mensch.","furigana":"かのじょ|き|つよ|やさ|ひと"},{"japanese":"そんな話を聞くと、気が重くなります。","romaji":"Sonna hanashi o kiku to, ki ga omoku narimasu.","german":"Wenn ich so etwas höre, wird mir schwer ums Herz.","furigana":"はなし|き|き|おも"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"な-adjective: sudden (急な雨), urgent (急な用事) or steep (急な坂). Adverb: 急に (suddenly). Same sound: 九.","examples":[{"japanese":"急な用事ができて、行けなくなりました。","romaji":"Kyū na yōji ga dekite, ikenaku narimashita.","german":"Something urgent came up, so I can't go.","furigana":"きゅう|ようじ|い"},{"japanese":"この坂はとても急だから、気をつけて。","romaji":"Kono saka wa totemo kyū da kara, ki o tsukete.","german":"This slope is very steep, so be careful.","furigana":"さか|きゅう|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"City as an administrative unit: 横浜市, 市役所 (city hall), 市長 (mayor). The reading いち means \"market\" (朝市).","examples":[{"japanese":"市の図書館は日曜日も開いています。","romaji":"Shi no toshokan wa nichiyōbi mo aite imasu.","german":"The city library is open on Sundays too.","furigana":"し|としょかん|にちようび|あ"},{"japanese":"この市には大きな公園が三つある。","romaji":"Kono shi ni wa ōki na kōen ga mittsu aru.","german":"This city has three large parks.","furigana":"し|おお|こうえん|みっ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"Written character, letter; also handwriting: 字がきれい (beautiful writing). Kanji in general: 漢字; written characters in general: 文字.","examples":[{"japanese":"この字は何と読みますか。","romaji":"Kono ji wa nan to yomimasu ka.","german":"How do you read this character?","furigana":"じ|なん|よ"},{"japanese":"彼女は字がとてもきれいです。","romaji":"Kanojo wa ji ga totemo kirei desu.","german":"She has very beautiful handwriting.","furigana":"かのじょ|じ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 9"},
{"notes":"Ward (of a large city), e.g. the 23 wards of Tokyo (渋谷区). Ward office: 区役所.","examples":[{"japanese":"私は東京の世田谷区に住んでいます。","romaji":"Watashi wa Tōkyō no Setagaya-ku ni sunde imasu.","german":"I live in Setagaya Ward in Tokyo.","furigana":"わたし|とうきょう||す"},{"japanese":"この区には外国人がたくさん住んでいます。","romaji":"Kono ku ni wa gaikokujin ga takusan sunde imasu.","german":"Many foreigners live in this ward.","furigana":"く|がいこくじん|す"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"Suffix meaning \"made in / made of\": 日本製 (made in Japan), ドイツ製, プラスチック製 (made of plastic).","examples":[{"japanese":"この時計はスイス製です。","romaji":"Kono tokei wa Suisusei desu.","german":"This watch is made in Switzerland.","furigana":"とけい|せい"},{"japanese":"プラスチック製のコップは割れにくい。","romaji":"Purasuchikkusei no koppu wa warenikui.","german":"Plastic cups are hard to break.","furigana":"せい|わ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"Auditorium, assembly hall of a school or university. Same sound: 行動 (action).","examples":[{"japanese":"入学式は講堂で行われます。","romaji":"Nyūgakushiki wa kōdō de okonawaremasu.","german":"The entrance ceremony is held in the auditorium.","furigana":"にゅうがくしき|こうどう|おこな"},{"japanese":"全校生徒が講堂に集まった。","romaji":"Zenkō seito ga kōdō ni atsumatta.","german":"All the students gathered in the auditorium.","furigana":"ぜんこうせいと|こうどう|あつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"運動する means here to move physically or to do sports. The place takes で; a duration like 三十分 comes without に. 毎朝 describes a habit, while 〜ので in the second example gives the reason for the choice of place. The verb is intransitive; place and duration describe the activity. The forms are, for example, 運動します, 運動した and 運動して. 運動 alone is a noun; the meanings \"campaign\" and \"motion of a body\" are not practiced here.","examples":[{"japanese":"毎朝、公園で三十分運動します。","romaji":"Maiasa, kōen de sanjuppun undō shimasu.","german":"I exercise in the park for thirty minutes every morning.","furigana":"まいあさ|こうえん|さんじゅっぷんうんどう"},{"japanese":"昨日は雨だったので、家で運動しました。","romaji":"Kinō wa ame datta node, ie de undō shimashita.","german":"It rained yesterday, so I exercised at home.","furigana":"きのう|あめ|いえ|うんどう"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 9"},
{"notes":"食事する means to have a meal. 食事 alone is the noun \"meal\"; 食事をする is also possible. The full verb 食事する is intransitive. For a particular food as an object, one uses something like パンを食べる. With 〜しませんか you can politely invite someone to eat together. 一緒に means \"together\", と names the companion and で the place. The forms of する change, for example in 食事します, 食事しました and 食事して.","examples":[{"japanese":"映画の前に、一緒に食事しませんか。","romaji":"Eiga no mae ni, issho ni shokuji shimasen ka.","german":"Shall we have a meal together before the movie?","furigana":"えいが|まえ|いっしょ|しょくじ"},{"japanese":"家族とレストランで食事しました。","romaji":"Kazoku to resutoran de shokuji shimashita.","german":"I had a meal with my family at a restaurant.","furigana":"かぞく|しょくじ"}],"levelNote":"Derived from 食事 (N4)"},
{"notes":"明日 is read あす on this card and means \"tomorrow\". There is also the everyday reading あした. A time expression like 明日 stands without に in these sentences. 明日は names the topic of the weather report; 〜そうです here reports information that was heard. 明日までに sets a deadline: the documents are to be handed in tomorrow at the latest.","examples":[{"japanese":"明日は雨が降るそうです。","romaji":"Asu wa ame ga furu sō desu.","german":"I hear it will rain tomorrow.","furigana":"あす|あめ|ふ"},{"japanese":"この書類は明日までに出してください。","romaji":"Kono shorui wa asu made ni dashite kudasai.","german":"Please submit these documents by tomorrow.","furigana":"しょるい|あす|だ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 33"},
{"notes":"や connects nouns in a list that allows for further, unnamed items. 本やノート names, for example, books and notebooks among other objects. と, by contrast, presents the listed items as a complete list in the given context. The whole group of words can stand before が or を: 本やノートが, 料理や掃除を. This use is to be distinguished from 〜屋 for shops or occupations and from the regional copula や.","examples":[{"japanese":"机の上に、本やノートがあります。","romaji":"Tsukue no ue ni, hon ya nōto ga arimasu.","german":"On the desk there are books, notebooks and so on.","furigana":"つくえ|うえ|ほん"},{"japanese":"休日は、料理や掃除をします。","romaji":"Kyūjitsu wa, ryōri ya sōji o shimasu.","german":"On days off, I do things like cooking and cleaning.","furigana":"きゅうじつ|りょうり|そうじ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"じゅう covers an entire period, place or group of people: 一日中 \"all day\", 世界中 \"all over the world\", 親戚中 \"all the relatives\". With に, a time limit can be meant: 今日中に means \"within today, before today is over\". Which reading 中 takes depends on the combination and meaning; 工事中, by contrast, is read こうじちゅう.","examples":[{"japanese":"昨日は一日中雨が降っていました。","romaji":"Kinō wa ichinichi jū ame ga futte imashita.","german":"It rained all day yesterday.","furigana":"きのう|いちにちじゅうあめ|ふ"},{"japanese":"今日中にこの仕事を終わらせます。","romaji":"Kyō jū ni kono shigoto o owarasemasu.","german":"I will finish this work within today.","furigana":"きょうじゅう|しごと|お"},{"japanese":"この歌は世界中で知られています。","romaji":"Kono uta wa sekai jū de shirarete imasu.","german":"This song is known all over the world.","furigana":"うた|せかいじゅう|し"},{"japanese":"親戚中からお祝いの手紙が届きました。","romaji":"Shinseki jū kara oiwai no tegami ga todokimashita.","german":"Congratulatory letters arrived from all my relatives.","furigana":"しんせきじゅう|いわ|てがみ|とど"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 37"},
{"notes":"家（か）forms a word for a person after a noun. 写真家 is a photographer; 努力家 names someone who makes great efforts. The suffix can thus express an occupation or a pronounced trait. It is attached directly to the preceding element. 家（いえ）for \"house\" and 家（け）after a family name are other readings.","examples":[{"japanese":"写真家が海の写真を撮っています。","romaji":"Shashinka ga umi no shashin o totte imasu.","german":"A photographer is taking pictures of the sea.","furigana":"しゃしんか|うみ|しゃしん|と"},{"japanese":"弟は毎日練習する努力家です。","romaji":"Otōto wa mainichi renshū suru doryokuka desu.","german":"My younger brother is a hard worker who practices every day.","furigana":"おとうと|まいにちれんしゅう|どりょくか"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 28"},
{"notes":"店（てん） stands at the end of names for shops or restaurants, such as 書店（しょてん）, bookstore, and 喫茶店（きっさてん）, cafe. The first part of the word says what kind of shop it is. As a standalone word for a shop, 店 is usually read みせ: 店に入る. Practice the compound names each with their full reading.","examples":[{"japanese":"駅の前の書店で辞書を買いました。","romaji":"Eki no mae no shoten de jisho o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a dictionary at the bookstore in front of the station.","furigana":"えき|まえ|しょてん|じしょ|か"},{"japanese":"この喫茶店は朝七時から開いています。","romaji":"Kono kissaten wa asa shichiji kara aite imasu.","german":"This coffee shop is open from seven in the morning.","furigana":"きっさてん|あさしちじ|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"易い means easy or simple here. Standing alone it is found, for example, in 易い仕事 or in contrast with 難しい. For easily understood tasks and texts, やさしい is also common. 易い（やすい）must be distinguished from 安い（やすい）\"cheap\": the same pronunciation means a different meaning and kanji spelling here. After a verb stem such as 読み in 読みやすい, やすい acts as a suffix; the examples on this card practice the standalone adjective. Its negation is 易くない, the past tense 易かった.","examples":[{"japanese":"説明するのは易いですが、実際にやるのは難しいです。","romaji":"Setsumei suru no wa yasui desu ga, jissai ni yaru no wa muzukashii desu.","german":"Explaining is easy, but actually doing it is hard.","furigana":"せつめい|やす|じっさい|むずか"},{"japanese":"この程度の修理なら、私にも易い仕事です。","romaji":"Kono teido no shūri nara, watashi ni mo yasui shigoto desu.","german":"If it's a repair of this scale, it's an easy job even for me.","furigana":"ていど|しゅうり|わたし|やす|しごと"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"杯／はい can denote a cup for alcoholic drinks, especially in fixed or elevated phrases. 杯を挙げる means to raise the cup to drink or toast; 杯を重ねる means to drink repeatedly. The spelling 盃 is also attested. The common reading さかずき is learned separately. As a counter in 一杯／いっぱい, 杯 has a different function; this card shows the drinking cup in the phrases mentioned.","examples":[{"japanese":"祝宴で、皆が杯を挙げて二人の幸せを祈った。","romaji":"Shukuen de, minna ga hai o agete futari no shiawase o inotta.","german":"At the banquet, everyone raised their cups and prayed for the couple's happiness.","furigana":"しゅくえん|みんな|はい|あ|ふたり|しあわ|いの"},{"japanese":"宴が終わっても、二人は杯を重ねていた。","romaji":"Utage ga owatte mo, futari wa hai o kasanete ita.","german":"Even after the banquet ended, the two kept refilling their cups.","furigana":"うたげ|お|ふたり|はい|かさ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"死（し）is the noun for death, as in 死を悼む \"to mourn a death\" or 死を恐れる \"to fear death\". The verb is 死ぬ（しぬ）. The reading し applies here to the independent noun; it is not derived from the polite title 氏. The special use in baseball is not part of this card.","examples":[{"japanese":"祖父の死を家族みんなで悼んだ。","romaji":"Sofu no shi o kazoku minna de itanda.","german":"We, the whole family, mourned grandfather's death.","furigana":"そふ|し|かぞく|いた"},{"japanese":"この小説の主人公は死を恐れていない。","romaji":"Kono shōsetsu no shujinkō wa shi o osorete inai.","german":"The protagonist of this novel does not fear death.","furigana":"しょうせつ|しゅじんこう|し|おそ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"難い（かたい） is a standalone i-adjective meaning \"difficult\". It sounds literary and appears, for example, in 想像するに難くない \"not hard to imagine\". The form 難く（かたく） stands before ない. The attached suffix ～難い（がたい） in 信じ難い and ～難い（にくい） in 読み難い have their own readings and are not equated here. The old proverbial form 難し（かたし） is not a modern base form. 固い, 硬い and 堅い denote other kinds of hardness or firmness.","examples":[{"japanese":"この城は守るに易く、攻めるに難い。","romaji":"Kono shiro wa mamoru ni yasuku, semeru ni katai.","german":"This castle is easy to defend and hard to attack.","furigana":"しろ|まも|やす|せ|かた"},{"japanese":"彼が落胆したことは想像するに難くない。","romaji":"Kare ga rakutan shita koto wa sōzō suru ni kataku nai.","german":"It is not hard to imagine that he was disappointed.","furigana":"かれ|らくたん|そうぞう|かた"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"し here attaches to a completed statement and names one of several possible reasons. You can link reasons with …し、…し; not all of them need to be spoken. After nouns and na-adjectives, the plain form だし is used, for example 雨だし; after polite statements ですし is also possible. At the end of a sentence, し often leaves the conclusion unspoken and sounds conversational. The identically sounding number names and nouns belong to their own cards.","examples":[{"japanese":"雨だし、もう遅いし、今日は帰ろう。","romaji":"Ame da shi, mō osoi shi, kyō wa kaerō.","german":"It's raining and it's already late, so let's go home today.","furigana":"あめ|おそ|きょう|かえ"},{"japanese":"その店に行きたいけれど、今月はお金もないし。","romaji":"Sono mise ni ikitai keredo, kongetsu wa okane mo nai shi.","german":"I'd like to go to that shop, but I have no money this month either.","furigana":"みせ|い|こんげつ|かね"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"ああ here is a short affirmative reply: \"yes\", \"right\" or \"exactly\". You use it to confirm a question or agree with a statement. This reply belongs in casual conversation and, depending on tone, can sound calm or a bit curt; for a polite answer you would rather use はい or ええ. It is pronounced with a long a. The emotional exclamation \"ah!\" and the adverb ああ \"in that way\" have their own cards. The examples distinguish confirming a completed action from agreeing with a shared assessment.","examples":[{"japanese":"「もう予約した？」と聞かれて、「ああ、昨日したよ」と答えた。","romaji":"\"Mō yoyaku shita?\" to kikarete, \"Ā, kinō shita yo\" to kotaeta.","german":"Asked \"Have you made the reservation yet?\", I answered, \"Yeah, yesterday.\"","furigana":"よやく|き|きのう|こた"},{"japanese":"友達が「あの映画は面白かったね」と言うので、「ああ、本当にそうだ」とうなずいた。","romaji":"Tomodachi ga \"Ano eiga wa omoshirokatta ne\" to iu node, \"Ā, hontō ni sō da\" to unazuita.","german":"My friend said, \"That movie was good, wasn't it?\" so I nodded and said, \"Yeah, it really was.\"","furigana":"ともだち|えいが|おもしろ|い|ほんとう"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 9"},
{"notes":"あ is a short exclamation used when you suddenly notice or remember something: \"ah!\" or \"oh!\". It can stand before a whole sentence. In writing, a comma often follows. あっ is a related form, abruptly cut off; this card practices the short あ. The long ああ has its own meanings and cards. The examples show remembering a forgotten item and the surprising discovery of an animal.","examples":[{"japanese":"あ、傘を電車に忘れた。","romaji":"A, kasa o densha ni wasureta.","german":"Ah, I left my umbrella on the train.","furigana":"かさ|でんしゃ|わす"},{"japanese":"あ、庭に子猫がいるよ。","romaji":"A, niwa ni koneko ga iru yo.","german":"Oh, there's a kitten in the garden!","furigana":"にわ|こねこ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"電報 is a telegram: a message delivered as a written document. 電報を送る means to send a telegram, 電報を受け取る to receive one; 電報を打つ is also a fixed expression for sending. The word appears in older stories for urgent messages and, in Japan, also for formal congratulations or condolences. The examples contrast receiving an urgent summons with a wedding congratulation.","examples":[{"japanese":"祖父は「すぐ帰れ」という電報を受け取った。","romaji":"Sofu wa \"Sugu kaere\" to iu denpō o uketotta.","german":"My grandfather received a telegram saying \"Come home at once\".","furigana":"そふ|かえ|でんぽう|う|と"},{"japanese":"結婚式に出席できなかったので、友人にお祝いの電報を送った。","romaji":"Kekkonshiki ni shusseki dekinakatta node, yūjin ni oiwai no denpō o okutta.","german":"Since I couldn't attend the wedding, I sent my friend a congratulatory telegram.","furigana":"けっこんしき|しゅっせき|ゆうじん|いわ|でんぽう|おく"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 33"},
{"notes":"看護婦 means a nurse and is an older job title referring to women. It appears in earlier stories and occasionally still colloquially. The common job title today is 看護師, regardless of gender. 看護婦として働く describes the work; a 看護婦 can also appear as a character in an older story. The examples contrast a former profession with the care of a patient in a story.","examples":[{"japanese":"昭和の頃、祖母は病院で看護婦として働いていた。","romaji":"Shōwa no koro, sobo wa byōin de kangofu to shite hataraite ita.","german":"In the Showa era, my grandmother worked as a nurse in a hospital.","furigana":"しょうわ|ころ|そぼ|びょういん|かんごふ|はたら"},{"japanese":"その物語では、夜勤の看護婦が不安そうな患者を励ます。","romaji":"Sono monogatari de wa, yakin no kangofu ga fuansō na kanja o hagemasu.","german":"In that story, a night-shift nurse encourages an anxious patient.","furigana":"ものがたり|やきん|かんごふ|ふあん|かんじゃ|はげ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"泳ぎ方 is the way one swims, or a swimming technique. The noun combines the stem form 泳ぎ with 方 \"manner, way\". 泳ぎ方を教える means to teach someone swimming or a technique; 泳ぎ方を変える means to change one's way of swimming. It denotes the method and can refer to a particular stroke such as クロール. The examples contrast learning a technique with adapting it to tire less quickly.","examples":[{"japanese":"水泳の先生に、クロールの泳ぎ方を教えてもらった。","romaji":"Suiei no sensei ni, kurōru no oyogikata o oshiete moratta.","german":"My swimming teacher taught me how to do the crawl.","furigana":"すいえい|せんせい|およ|かた|おし"},{"japanese":"疲れにくい泳ぎ方に変えたら、前より長く泳げた。","romaji":"Tsukarenikui oyogikata ni kaetara, mae yori nagaku oyogeta.","german":"When I switched to a less tiring way of swimming, I could swim longer than before.","furigana":"つか|およ|かた|か|まえ|なが|およ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"利用 is the noun for the use or utilization of something. One speaks, for example, of 学生の利用 \"use by students\" or 車の利用 \"use of a car\". 利用が多い describes frequent use; 利用を控える means to limit or forgo use. With する it becomes the verb 利用する \"to use\", which has its own card. Here the examples show the frequent use of a facility and the deliberate renunciation of a means of transport.","examples":[{"japanese":"この図書館は、学生の利用が多い。","romaji":"Kono toshokan wa, gakusei no riyō ga ōi.","german":"This library is used a lot by students.","furigana":"としょかん|がくせい|りよう|おお"},{"japanese":"大雪の日は、車の利用を控えた。","romaji":"Ōyuki no hi wa, kuruma no riyō o hikaeta.","german":"On a day of heavy snow, I refrained from using the car.","furigana":"おおゆき|ひ|くるま|りよう|ひか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"ステレオ denotes stereo sound with separate left and right channels and also a stereo system. ステレオで録音する means to record in stereo; ステレオを修理する to repair a stereo system. The opposite in sound format is モノラル \"mono\". This card practices the independent noun for sound and device. The word element ステレオ in terms for spatial vision has a different use.","examples":[{"japanese":"この録音はステレオなので、左右から違う音が聞こえる。","romaji":"Kono rokuon wa sutereo na node, sayū kara chigau oto ga kikoeru.","german":"This recording is in stereo, so you hear different sounds from the left and right.","furigana":"ろくおん|さゆう|ちが|おと|き"},{"japanese":"古いステレオを修理して、またレコードを聴けるようになった。","romaji":"Furui sutereo o shūri shite, mata rekōdo o kikeru yō ni natta.","german":"After repairing the old stereo, I was able to listen to records again.","furigana":"ふる|しゅうり|き"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 46"},
{"notes":"昨夜 with the reading さくや means \"yesterday evening\" or \"last night\". It seems more written or formal than ゆうべ, which can also be written 昨夜. 昨夜遅く means late yesterday evening or late last night; 昨夜から describes the beginning of a state during last night. As a time expression, 昨夜 often stands without a particle before the verb.","examples":[{"japanese":"昨夜遅く、兄から電話があった。","romaji":"Sakuya osoku, ani kara denwa ga atta.","german":"My older brother called late last night.","furigana":"さくやおそ|あに|でんわ"},{"japanese":"昨夜から雨が降り続いている。","romaji":"Sakuya kara ame ga furitsuzuite iru.","german":"It has been raining continuously since last night.","furigana":"さくや|あめ|ふ|つづ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"けど joins two clauses with a contrast in spoken language: \"but\" or \"although\". It is a short form of けれども and stands directly after the first clause, for example 高いけど \"it's expensive, but ...\" or 練習したけど \"although I practiced ...\". After a noun or な-adjective, だけど is usually used. In formal writing, けれども and が are more common. The independent けど at the start of a new sentence has its own card.","examples":[{"japanese":"値段は高いけど、長く使える。","romaji":"Nedan wa takai kedo, nagaku tsukaeru.","german":"The price is high, but you can use it for a long time.","furigana":"ねだん|たか|なが|つか"},{"japanese":"練習したけど、試合には勝てなかった。","romaji":"Renshū shita kedo, shiai ni wa katenakatta.","german":"I practiced, but I couldn't win the match.","furigana":"れんしゅう|しあい|か"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"As an independent conjunction, けど can stand at the start of a new sentence: \"but\" or \"however\". It picks up a previously stated fact and contrasts it with a qualification or an unexpected result. This short form of けれども is colloquial; in more formal writing, けれども or しかし fit. The particle けど, which directly joins two clauses, is practiced on its own card.","examples":[{"japanese":"今日は忙しい。けど、少しなら話せる。","romaji":"Kyō wa isogashii. Kedo, sukoshi nara hanaseru.","german":"I'm busy today. But I can talk for a little while.","furigana":"きょう|いそが|すこ|はな"},{"japanese":"彼は道を間違えた。けど、約束の時間には間に合った。","romaji":"Kare wa michi o machigaeta. Kedo, yakusoku no jikan ni wa ma ni atta.","german":"He took the wrong road. But he still made it by the agreed time.","furigana":"かれ|みち|まちが|やくそく|じかん|ま|あ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 20"},
{"notes":"挨拶する means \"to greet\"; the person greeted takes に (先生に挨拶する), and the form 挨拶をする is equivalent. At celebrations it also means \"to give a short speech\". The noun 挨拶 \"greeting, words of welcome\" has its own card. The kanji are difficult, so in daily life it is often written in kana as あいさつする.","examples":[{"japanese":"朝、会社に着いたら、みんなに挨拶します。","romaji":"Asa, kaisha ni tsuitara, minna ni aisatsu shimasu.","german":"When I arrive at the office in the morning, I greet everyone.","furigana":"あさ|かいしゃ|つ|あいさつ"},{"japanese":"新しい隣の人にまだ挨拶していません。","romaji":"Atarashii tonari no hito ni mada aisatsu shite imasen.","german":"I haven't greeted my new neighbor yet.","furigana":"あたら|となり|ひと|あいさつ"},{"japanese":"結婚式では、最初に社長が挨拶しました。","romaji":"Kekkonshiki de wa, saisho ni shachō ga aisatsu shimashita.","german":"At the wedding, the company president gave a speech first.","furigana":"けっこんしき|さいしょ|しゃちょう|あいさつ"}],"levelNote":"Derived from 挨拶 (N5)"},
{"notes":"The polite, softer form of 金持ち（かねもち）\"rich person\", with the prefix お. In conversation お金持ち sounds more natural and friendly; 金持ち is neutral to somewhat blunt (金持ちのくせに) and appears in compounds like 大金持ち. Common in お金持ちになる \"to become rich\" and お金持ちの家 \"wealthy family\".","examples":[{"japanese":"将来はお金持ちになって、大きな家に住みたい。","romaji":"Shourai wa okanemochi ni natte, ookina ie ni sumitai.","german":"In the future I want to become rich and live in a big house.","furigana":"しょうらい|かねも|おお|いえ|す"},{"japanese":"あの人はお金持ちなのに、いつも安い服を着ている。","romaji":"Ano hito wa okanemochi na noni, itsumo yasui fuku o kite iru.","german":"That person is rich, yet always wears cheap clothes.","furigana":"ひと|かねも|やす|ふく|き"},{"japanese":"彼女はお金持ちの家に生まれた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa okanemochi no ie ni umareta.","german":"She was born into a wealthy family.","furigana":"かのじょ|かねも|いえ|う"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 10"},
{"notes":"計画する is transitive: the thing planned takes を (旅行を計画する), whole undertakings take 〜ことを計画する. With the noun 計画 (own card) one also says 計画を立てる \"to draw up a plan\". 予定, by contrast, is a fixed appointment or an item in the calendar, whereas 計画 stresses preparation and organization.","examples":[{"japanese":"夏休みに家族で旅行を計画しています。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi ni kazoku de ryokō o keikaku shite imasu.","german":"We are planning a family trip for the summer vacation.","furigana":"なつやす|かぞく|りょこう|けいかく"},{"japanese":"友だちの誕生日パーティーをこっそり計画しました。","romaji":"Tomodachi no tanjōbi pātī o kossori keikaku shimashita.","german":"I secretly planned a friend's birthday party.","furigana":"とも|たんじょうび|けいかく"},{"japanese":"会社は来年、新しい工場を建てることを計画している。","romaji":"Kaisha wa rainen, atarashii kōjō o tateru koto o keikaku shite iru.","german":"The company is planning to build a new factory next year.","furigana":"かいしゃ|らいねん|あたら|こうじょう|た|けいかく"}],"levelNote":"Derived from 計画 (N4)"},
{"notes":"Verb form of the noun card 怪我（けが）\"injury\". In everyday use, 怪我をする is just as common as 怪我する; the injured body part takes を (足を怪我する). It means \"to injure oneself (unintentionally)\"; to injure someone else is 怪我をさせる. Often written in kana (けがする); compare 傷（きず）\"wound\" and 痛める \"to hurt, to strain something\".","examples":[{"japanese":"階段から落ちて、足を怪我しました。","romaji":"Kaidan kara ochite, ashi o kega shimashita.","german":"I fell down the stairs and hurt my leg.","furigana":"かいだん|お|あし|けが"},{"japanese":"事故で三人が怪我したそうです。","romaji":"Jiko de sannin ga kega shita sou desu.","german":"I hear three people were injured in the accident.","furigana":"じこ|さんにん|けが"},{"japanese":"包丁を使うときは、怪我しないように気をつけてね。","romaji":"Houchou o tsukau toki wa, kega shinai you ni ki o tsukete ne.","german":"When you use a kitchen knife, be careful not to hurt yourself.","furigana":"ほうちょう|つか|けが|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"支度する means practical preparation in daily life: getting ready to go out, preparing food, packing luggage. The form with the noun is also common: 出かける支度をする, 夕飯の支度をする (the noun 支度 has its own card). 準備する is more general and more formal (also for plans, events), 用意する stresses laying things out ready. The spelling 仕度する has the same meaning.","examples":[{"japanese":"早く支度しなさい。学校に遅れるよ。","romaji":"Hayaku shitaku shinasai. Gakkō ni okureru yo.","german":"Get ready quickly! You'll be late for school.","furigana":"はや|したく|がっこう|おく"},{"japanese":"五分で支度しますから、ちょっと待ってください。","romaji":"Gofun de shitaku shimasu kara, chotto matte kudasai.","german":"I'll be ready in five minutes, so please wait a moment.","furigana":"ごふん|したく|ま"},{"japanese":"旅行に必要な物はもう全部支度しておきました。","romaji":"Ryokō ni hitsuyō na mono wa mō zenbu shitaku shite okimashita.","german":"I have already prepared everything needed for the trip.","furigana":"りょこう|ひつよう|もの|ぜんぶしたく"}],"levelNote":"Derived from 支度 (N4)"},
{"notes":"承知する is a formal \"to take note of, to consent\". Very common at work is the humble-polite reply 承知しました \"Understood, will do\"; even more humble is かしこまりました, more casual 分かりました or 了解. ご承知のとおり means \"as you know\". With an object: X を承知する or X は承知している \"to be aware of something\". The noun 承知 has its own card.","examples":[{"japanese":"はい、承知しました。少々お待ちください。","romaji":"Hai, shōchi shimashita. Shōshō omachi kudasai.","german":"Yes, understood. Please wait a moment.","furigana":"しょうち|しょうしょう|ま"},{"japanese":"危険なことは十分承知しています。","romaji":"Kiken na koto wa jūbun shōchi shite imasu.","german":"I am fully aware of the danger.","furigana":"きけん|じゅうぶんしょうち"},{"japanese":"父は私の留学をなかなか承知しなかった。","romaji":"Chichi wa watashi no ryūgaku o nakanaka shōchi shinakatta.","german":"My father wouldn't agree to my studying abroad for a long time.","furigana":"ちち|わたし|りゅうがく|しょうち"}],"levelNote":"Derived from 承知 (N4)"},
{"notes":"Onomatopoeic adverb for a smooth, effortless and often sudden movement: すっと立ち上がる, 痛みがすっと消える, 手をすっと出す. With する, 気持ちがすっとする / 胸がすっとする means \"to feel relieved, liberated\". Do not confuse it with ずっと \"all the time; much more\" and そっと \"quietly, gently\"; すっと stresses the smooth and quick. In speech it is often drawn out as すーっと.","examples":[{"japanese":"名前を呼ばれると、彼はすっと立ち上がった。","romaji":"Namae o yobareru to, kare wa sutto tachiagatta.","german":"When his name was called, he stood up smoothly.","furigana":"なまえ|よ|かれ|た|あ"},{"japanese":"薬を飲んだら、頭の痛みがすっと消えた。","romaji":"Kusuri o nondara, atama no itami ga sutto kieta.","german":"After I took the medicine, the headache vanished all at once.","furigana":"くすり|の|あたま|いた|き"},{"japanese":"言いたいことを全部言ったら、気持ちがすっとした。","romaji":"Iitai koto o zenbu ittara, kimochi ga sutto shita.","german":"After I said everything I wanted to say, I felt relieved.","furigana":"い|ぜんぶい|きも"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"Suru-verb for the noun 生活 \"life, daily life\"; the place takes で (東京で生活する), and so do the means (年金で生活する). It stresses the way of life and livelihood, whereas 暮らす sounds somewhat warmer and more everyday, and 住む means only \"to reside\". For \"to be alive\", 生きる is used instead.","examples":[{"japanese":"大学に入ってから、一人で生活しています。","romaji":"Daigaku ni haitte kara, hitori de seikatsu shite imasu.","german":"I've been living on my own since I entered university.","furigana":"だいがく|はい|ひとり|せいかつ"},{"japanese":"月十万円で生活するのは大変です。","romaji":"Tsuki juuman-en de seikatsu suru no wa taihen desu.","german":"Living on 100,000 yen a month is hard.","furigana":"つきじゅうまんえん|せいかつ|たいへん"},{"japanese":"電気のない島で一週間生活した。","romaji":"Denki no nai shima de isshuukan seikatsu shita.","german":"I lived for a week on an island without electricity.","furigana":"でんき|しま|いっしゅうかんせいかつ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 20"},
{"notes":"Most common is the form 〜の世話をする (犬の世話をする \"to take care of the dog\"); X を世話する is also possible but sounds a little more written. The person who receives help, on the other hand, says 世話になる or politely お世話になる (own cards). In the meaning \"to arrange, to find for someone\", one also says 仕事を世話する \"to arrange a job\".","examples":[{"japanese":"毎日、弟が犬の世話をしています。","romaji":"Mainichi, otōto ga inu no sewa o shite imasu.","german":"My younger brother looks after the dog every day.","furigana":"まいにち|おとうと|いぬ|せわ"},{"japanese":"母は入院中の祖母を毎日世話している。","romaji":"Haha wa nyūinchū no sobo o mainichi sewa shite iru.","german":"My mother takes care of my grandmother, who is in the hospital, every day.","furigana":"はは|にゅういんちゅう|そぼ|まいにちせわ"},{"japanese":"旅行の間、猫の世話をしてくれませんか。","romaji":"Ryokō no aida, neko no sewa o shite kuremasen ka.","german":"Could you look after the cat while I'm on my trip?","furigana":"りょこう|あいだ|ねこ|せわ"}],"levelNote":"Derived from 世話 (N4)"},
{"notes":"The base word without the polite prefix お. When speaking on its own, people almost always say お菓子（おかし）in everyday life; 菓子 alone sounds written or technical and appears mainly in compounds: 菓子パン \"sweet bun\", 菓子店 \"confectionery shop\", 和菓子（わがし）\"Japanese sweets\", 洋菓子（ようがし）\"Western sweets\" (with voiced が there). Do not confuse it with the homophones 歌詞 \"song lyrics\" and 貸し \"lending\".","examples":[{"japanese":"駅前に新しい菓子店ができました。","romaji":"Ekimae ni atarashii kashiten ga dekimashita.","german":"A new confectionery shop opened in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|あたら|かしてん"},{"japanese":"朝ご飯に、コンビニで菓子パンを買った。","romaji":"Asagohan ni, konbini de kashipan o katta.","german":"I bought a sweet bun at the convenience store for breakfast.","furigana":"あさ|はん|かし|か"},{"japanese":"客間には、お茶と菓子が用意してあった。","romaji":"Kyakuma ni wa, ocha to kashi ga youi shite atta.","german":"Tea and sweets had been prepared in the guest room.","furigana":"きゃくま|ちゃ|かし|ようい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Counts houses, shops, restaurants and other buildings: 一軒 (いっけん), 二軒, 三軒 (さんげん), 六軒 (ろっけん), 八軒 (はっけん), 十軒 (じゅっけん), question word 何軒 (なんげん). 一軒家 means \"detached house\". The card 軒/のき, on the other hand, teaches the noun \"eaves\"; do not confuse it with 〜件 (cases, matters) and 〜県 (prefecture).","examples":[{"japanese":"駅の近くに本屋が一軒あります。","romaji":"Eki no chikaku ni hon'ya ga ikken arimasu.","german":"There is one bookstore near the station.","furigana":"えき|ちか|ほんや|いっけん"},{"japanese":"この通りにはコンビニが三軒もある。","romaji":"Kono tōri ni wa konbini ga sangen mo aru.","german":"There are as many as three convenience stores on this street.","furigana":"とお|さんげん"},{"japanese":"昨日は友達と居酒屋を何軒も回った。","romaji":"Kinō wa tomodachi to izakaya o nangen mo mawatta.","german":"Yesterday I went around many izakaya with my friends.","furigana":"きのう|ともだち|いざかや|なんげん|まわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"招待 is the formal invitation as a noun: 招待を受ける \"to accept an invitation\", 招待を断る \"to decline\", politely ご招待. The invitation card is called 招待状. The verb \"to invite\" is 招待する (own card); 正体 \"true identity\" is read the same but is a different word.","examples":[{"japanese":"友だちから結婚式の招待状が届きました。","romaji":"Tomodachi kara kekkonshiki no shōtaijō ga todokimashita.","german":"An invitation to a wedding arrived from a friend.","furigana":"とも|けっこんしき|しょうたいじょう|とど"},{"japanese":"ご招待、ありがとうございます。","romaji":"Go-shōtai, arigatō gozaimasu.","german":"Thank you for the invitation.","furigana":"しょうたい"},{"japanese":"忙しかったので、パーティーへの招待を断りました。","romaji":"Isogashikatta node, pātī e no shōtai o kotowarimashita.","german":"I was busy, so I declined the invitation to the party.","furigana":"いそが|しょうたい|ことわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"The second, equally valid reading of 頬 \"cheek\" besides ほお (its own card). ほお is regarded as the standard and dictionary form and appears in the fixed words 頬杖（ほおづえ）and 頬張る（ほおばる）; ほほ is very common in spoken Japanese and is found in ほほえむ \"to smile\" (微笑む). In free use (頬が赤い) both readings are correct; ほほ sounds somewhat softer and more colloquial.","examples":[{"japanese":"寒さで子どもの頬が赤くなっている。","romaji":"Samusa de kodomo no hoho ga akaku natte iru.","german":"The child's cheeks are red from the cold.","furigana":"さむ|こ|ほほ|あか"},{"japanese":"映画を見ながら、涙が頬を流れた。","romaji":"Eiga o minagara, namida ga hoho o nagareta.","german":"While watching the movie, tears ran down my cheeks.","furigana":"えいが|み|なみだ|ほほ|なが"},{"japanese":"祖母は私の頬を両手で包んで、「大きくなったね」と言った。","romaji":"Sobo wa watashi no hoho o ryoute de tsutsunde, \"Ookiku natta ne\" to itta.","german":"My grandmother held my cheeks in both hands and said, \"You've grown big.\"","furigana":"そぼ|わたし|ほほ|りょうて|つつ|おお|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"A formal noun that is almost always written in kana and never occurs alone: ようだ \"seems\", ような \"like a ...\", ように \"so that / as\"; after a verb stem it denotes the manner (言いようがない \"there are no words for it\"). Do not confuse it with 〜様 (さま, polite name suffix), 様子 (ようす, state, appearance) or 用 (よう, business).","examples":[{"japanese":"田中さんは今日とても疲れているようだ。","romaji":"Tanaka-san wa kyou totemo tsukarete iru you da.","german":"Mr./Ms. Tanaka seems very tired today.","furigana":"たなか|きょう|つか"},{"japanese":"日本語が上手に話せるように、毎日練習しています。","romaji":"Nihongo ga jouzu ni hanaseru you ni, mainichi renshuu shite imasu.","german":"I practice every day so that I can speak Japanese well.","furigana":"にほんご|じょうず|はな|まいにちれんしゅう"},{"japanese":"あの時のうれしさは言いようがない。","romaji":"Ano toki no ureshisa wa iiyou ga nai.","german":"The joy I felt then is beyond words.","furigana":"とき|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"悪口 has two equally standard readings, わるくち and わるぐち (its own card); meaning and usage are identical, and わるくち is somewhat more common and often listed first in dictionaries. Typical is 〜の悪口を言う \"to badmouth someone\", passive 悪口を言われる. The Sino-Japanese reading あっこう occurs only in written language and set phrases such as 悪口雑言.","examples":[{"japanese":"人の悪口を言うのはよくないよ。","romaji":"Hito no warukuchi o iu no wa yoku nai yo.","german":"It's not good to speak ill of people.","furigana":"ひと|わるくち|い"},{"japanese":"彼女はいつも陰で上司の悪口を言っている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa itsumo kage de jōshi no warukuchi o itte iru.","german":"She is always badmouthing her boss behind his back.","furigana":"かのじょ|かげ|じょうし|わるくち|い"},{"japanese":"クラスで悪口を言われて、とても悲しかった。","romaji":"Kurasu de warukuchi o iwarete, totemo kanashikatta.","german":"I was very sad when I was badmouthed in class.","furigana":"わるくち|い|かな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"The noun of the verb 開放する (to open, make accessible); typical in 校庭開放 \"opening of the schoolyard\", 市場の開放 \"opening of the market\" and 開放感 \"feeling of spaciousness\". Do not confuse it with the homophones 解放 (liberation from coercion or captivity) and 介抱 (nursing a sick person): 開放 opens doors, spaces and markets, 解放 frees people.","examples":[{"japanese":"毎週土曜日は校庭開放の日です。","romaji":"Maishuu doyoubi wa koutei kaihou no hi desu.","german":"Every Saturday is a day when the schoolyard is open to the public.","furigana":"まいしゅうどようび|こうていかいほう|ひ"},{"japanese":"政府は農産物市場の開放を進めている。","romaji":"Seifu wa nousanbutsu shijou no kaihou o susumete iru.","german":"The government is pushing ahead with opening up the agricultural products market.","furigana":"せいふ|のうさんぶつしじょう|かいほう|すす"},{"japanese":"この部屋は天井が高くて開放感がある。","romaji":"Kono heya wa tenjou ga takakute kaihoukan ga aru.","german":"This room has a high ceiling and a feeling of spaciousness.","furigana":"へや|てんじょう|たか|かいほうかん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Suffix for persons who belong to an organization or work there: 会社員 \"company employee\", 店員 \"shop clerk\", 駅員 \"station employee\", 会員 \"member\", 公務員 \"civil servant\". 員 practically never stands alone; as a standalone word people say メンバー or 一員（いちいん）. Unlike 〜者（しゃ） \"person who does ...\", 〜員 emphasizes belonging to a group or staff. Do not confuse it with the homophone 韻（いん） \"rhyme\".","examples":[{"japanese":"兄は銀行員で、毎朝早く家を出ます。","romaji":"Ani wa ginkouin de, maiasa hayaku ie o demasu.","german":"My older brother is a bank employee and leaves the house early every morning.","furigana":"あに|ぎんこういん|まいあさはや|いえ|で"},{"japanese":"道がわからなかったので、駅員に聞きました。","romaji":"Michi ga wakaranakatta node, ekiin ni kikimashita.","german":"I didn't know the way, so I asked a station employee.","furigana":"みち|えきいん|き"},{"japanese":"このジムの会員になると、プールも使えます。","romaji":"Kono jimu no kaiin ni naru to, puuru mo tsukaemasu.","german":"If you become a member of this gym, you can also use the pool.","furigana":"かいいん|つか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"As a time expression, 十分 is read じっぷん or, in conversation nowadays more often, じゅっぷん \"ten minutes\". The same spelling read じゅうぶん means \"enough, sufficient\" (its own card); for this reason minutes are mostly written with numerals as 10分. With 〜間, 十分間 emphasizes the duration.","examples":[{"japanese":"駅まで歩いて十分ぐらいです。","romaji":"Eki made aruite jippun gurai desu.","german":"It's about ten minutes on foot to the station.","furigana":"えき|ある|じっぷん"},{"japanese":"あと十分で会議が始まります。","romaji":"Ato jippun de kaigi ga hajimarimasu.","german":"The meeting starts in ten minutes.","furigana":"じっぷん|かいぎ|はじ"},{"japanese":"授業と授業の間に十分の休憩があります。","romaji":"Jugyō to jugyō no aida ni jippun no kyūkei ga arimasu.","german":"There is a ten-minute break between classes.","furigana":"じゅぎょう|じゅぎょう|あいだ|じっぷん|きゅうけい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"ばかり follows nouns or verb forms: 肉ばかり食べる \"to eat only meat\", 遊んでばかりいる \"to do nothing but play\", 食べたばかり \"just ate\". After quantities it means \"about\" (一時間ばかり), similar to ぐらい. Unlike だけ, ばかり often sounds reproachful (\"always only\"). Colloquially often ばっかり; written almost always in kana, the kanji form 許り is rare.","examples":[{"japanese":"弟はゲームばかりしています。","romaji":"Otouto wa geemu bakari shite imasu.","german":"My little brother does nothing but play games.","furigana":"おとうと"},{"japanese":"さっき昼ご飯を食べたばかりです。","romaji":"Sakki hirugohan o tabeta bakari desu.","german":"I just ate lunch a little while ago.","furigana":"ひる|はん|た"},{"japanese":"駅で十分ばかり待ちました。","romaji":"Eki de juppun bakari machimashita.","german":"I waited about ten minutes at the station.","furigana":"えき|じゅっぷん|ま"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"A formal noun that expresses an unchanged state: verb in た-form + まま (窓を開けたまま寝る \"to sleep with the window open\"), noun + のまま (昔のまま), also 言われるままに \"as one is told\". Unlike 〜ながら, まま does not describe a second action but a state that remains, often unexpectedly. そのまま has its own card; do not confuse it with ママ \"mama\" and 間々 \"occasionally\". Almost always in kana.","examples":[{"japanese":"靴を履いたまま部屋に入らないでください。","romaji":"Kutsu o haita mama heya ni hairanaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't enter the room with your shoes on.","furigana":"くつ|は|へや|はい"},{"japanese":"窓を開けたまま寝てしまって、風邪をひいた。","romaji":"Mado o aketa mama nete shimatte, kaze o hiita.","german":"I fell asleep with the window open and caught a cold.","furigana":"まど|あ|ね|かぜ"},{"japanese":"この町には昔のままの古い家がたくさん残っている。","romaji":"Kono machi ni wa mukashi no mama no furui ie ga takusan nokotte iru.","german":"Many old houses remain in this town just as they were in the past.","furigana":"まち|むかし|ふる|いえ|のこ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"〜によると names the source of information and usually stands at the beginning of the sentence: 天気予報によると \"according to the weather forecast\". The sentence then typically ends with a hearsay form such as 〜そうです or 〜ということです. Equivalent and somewhat more written is 〜によれば. Do not confuse it with ことによると \"possibly\" and with the verb 拠る \"to be based on\"; the kanji spelling に依ると is rare.","examples":[{"japanese":"天気予報によると、明日は雨が降るそうです。","romaji":"Tenki yohou ni yoru to, ashita wa ame ga furu sou desu.","german":"According to the weather forecast, it will rain tomorrow.","furigana":"てんきよほう|あした|あめ|ふ"},{"japanese":"ニュースによると、駅の近くで事故があったそうです。","romaji":"Nyuusu ni yoru to, eki no chikaku de jiko ga atta sou desu.","german":"According to the news, there was an accident near the station.","furigana":"えき|ちか|じこ"},{"japanese":"田中さんの話によると、あの店は来月閉まるそうです。","romaji":"Tanaka-san no hanashi ni yoru to, ano mise wa raigetsu shimaru sou desu.","german":"According to what Mr./Ms. Tanaka says, that shop will close next month.","furigana":"たなか|はなし|みせ|らいげつし"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 47"},
{"notes":"Polite form of いけない; central in …てはいけません \"you must not\" (prohibition) and …なければいけません \"you must\" (obligation). On its own, それはいけませんね expresses sympathy: \"That's terrible.\" Almost always written in kana, because 行けません is at the same time the potential form of 行く, \"cannot go\". Casually one says …ちゃだめ or …てはだめ.","examples":[{"japanese":"美術館の中で写真を撮ってはいけません。","romaji":"Bijutsukan no naka de shashin o totte wa ikemasen.","german":"You must not take photographs inside the museum.","furigana":"びじゅつかん|なか|しゃしん|と"},{"japanese":"明日までにレポートを出さなければいけません。","romaji":"Ashita made ni repōto o dasanakereba ikemasen.","german":"I have to submit the report by tomorrow.","furigana":"あした|だ"},{"japanese":"えっ、熱があるんですか。それはいけませんね。","romaji":"E, netsu ga aru n desu ka. Sore wa ikemasen ne.","german":"What, you have a fever? That's not good."}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"The plain (non-polite) form of いけません (its own card); it is the core of the patterns …てはいけない \"must not\" and …なければいけない \"must\". On its own, いけない means \"bad, wrong\" (いけない子) or serves as an exclamation \"Damn!, Oh no!\" when one notices a mistake. Almost always in kana; when written 行けない it is more likely read as \"cannot go\" (potential of 行く).","examples":[{"japanese":"図書館で大きな声で話してはいけない。","romaji":"Toshokan de ōkina koe de hanashite wa ikenai.","german":"You must not talk loudly in the library.","furigana":"としょかん|おお|こえ|はな"},{"japanese":"明日までにレポートを出さなければいけない。","romaji":"Ashita made ni repōto o dasanakereba ikenai.","german":"I have to hand in the report by tomorrow.","furigana":"あした|だ"},{"japanese":"いけない、傘を電車に忘れてきた！","romaji":"Ikenai, kasa o densha ni wasurete kita!","german":"Oh no, I left my umbrella on the train!","furigana":"かさ|でんしゃ|わす"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Refers to an indefinite point in time, mostly in the future (\"someday\"), more rarely in the past (\"the other day, once\"). Almost always in kana; 何時か is a rare spelling. Do not confuse it with 五日/いつか \"the 5th of the month; five days\" (own card), which sounds the same. Also to be distinguished from いつでも \"any time\" and いつも \"always\".","examples":[{"japanese":"いつか富士山に登ってみたい。","romaji":"Itsuka Fujisan ni nobotte mitai.","german":"I want to climb Mt. Fuji someday.","furigana":"ふじさん|のぼ"},{"japanese":"いつかまたどこかで会いましょう。","romaji":"Itsuka mata doko ka de aimashō.","german":"Let's meet again somewhere sometime.","furigana":"あ"},{"japanese":"いつか話したラーメン屋に行ってみた。","romaji":"Itsuka hanashita rāmen'ya ni itte mita.","german":"I tried going to the ramen shop I talked about the other day.","furigana":"はな|や|い"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 27"},
{"notes":"Expresses a possibility and stands at the end of a sentence after the plain form of verbs and i-adjectives, or directly after nouns and na-adjectives (本当かもしれない, 静かかもしれない). Polite: かもしれません; colloquially shortened: かも. It is less certain than でしょう/だろう \"probably\". Usually in kana; かも知れない is rarer.","examples":[{"japanese":"午後から雨が降るかもしれない。","romaji":"Gogo kara ame ga furu kamoshirenai.","german":"It might rain from this afternoon.","furigana":"ごご|あめ|ふ"},{"japanese":"道が混んでいるので、少し遅れるかもしれません。","romaji":"Michi ga konde iru node, sukoshi okureru kamoshiremasen.","german":"The road is crowded, so I might be a little late.","furigana":"みち|こ|すこ|おく"},{"japanese":"彼の言っていることは本当かもしれない。","romaji":"Kare no itte iru koto wa hontō kamoshirenai.","german":"What he is saying may be true.","furigana":"かれ|い|ほんとう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"土曜 is the short form of 土曜日 (its own card) and is very common in conversation, timetables and opening hours: 土曜の夜, 土曜休み; 土日 means \"weekend\". The meaning is the same; the short form just sounds a little more casual, while 土曜日 is preferred in letters and announcements. Do not confuse it with the homophone 土用 \"dog days\".","examples":[{"japanese":"土曜の夜は友達と飲みに行く。","romaji":"Doyō no yoru wa tomodachi to nomi ni iku.","german":"On Saturday night I'm going out for drinks with friends.","furigana":"どよう|よる|ともだち|の|い"},{"japanese":"土曜も午前中だけ営業しています。","romaji":"Doyō mo gozenchū dake eigyō shite imasu.","german":"We are open only in the morning on Saturdays too.","furigana":"どよう|ごぜんちゅう|えいぎょう"},{"japanese":"次の土曜、空いてる？","romaji":"Tsugi no doyō, aiteru?","german":"Are you free next Saturday?","furigana":"つぎ|どよう|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"日曜 is the short form of 日曜日 (its own card) with the same meaning, typical of conversation, timetables and opening hours (日曜休み, 日曜の朝). It is fixed in compounds such as 日曜大工 \"DIY\" and 日曜版 \"Sunday edition\". In formal texts and announcements, the full form 日曜日 is preferred.","examples":[{"japanese":"日曜の朝はゆっくり寝ている。","romaji":"Nichiyō no asa wa yukkuri nete iru.","german":"On Sunday mornings I sleep in.","furigana":"にちよう|あさ|ね"},{"japanese":"父の趣味は日曜大工だ。","romaji":"Chichi no shumi wa nichiyō daiku da.","german":"My father's hobby is DIY carpentry.","furigana":"ちち|しゅみ|にちようだいく"},{"japanese":"その店は日曜が定休日です。","romaji":"Sono mise wa nichiyō ga teikyūbi desu.","german":"That shop's regular closing day is Sunday.","furigana":"みせ|にちよう|ていきゅうび"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"As a greeting, the casual short form of お帰りなさい (own card): family and friends answer ただいま with it, whereas お帰りなさい sounds somewhat more polite and complete. As a polite noun, お帰り means the \"homecoming, way home\" of a respected person, for example 社長のお帰り, or on signs お帰りはこちら \"Exit\". In the greeting it is often written in kana (おかえり).","examples":[{"japanese":"「ただいま！」「お帰り。今日は早かったね。」","romaji":"\"Tadaima!\" \"Okaeri. Kyou wa hayakatta ne.\"","german":"\"I'm home!\" \"Welcome back. You're early today.\"","furigana":"かえ|きょう|はや"},{"japanese":"お帰りはあちらの出口をご利用ください。","romaji":"Okaeri wa achira no deguchi o goriyou kudasai.","german":"Please use the exit over there on your way out.","furigana":"かえ|でぐち|りよう"},{"japanese":"社長のお帰りは何時ごろになりますか。","romaji":"Shachou no okaeri wa nanji goro ni narimasu ka.","german":"About what time will the company president be back?","furigana":"しゃちょう|かえ|なんじ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 0"},
{"notes":"Short form of 水曜日 (its own card), especially in schedules, notices and casual conversation (水曜の夜, 毎週水曜, 水曜休み). In calendars often just 水. In formal texts and announcements, 水曜日 is more usual; all weekdays have such short forms (月曜, 金曜).","examples":[{"japanese":"水曜は定休日なので、店は閉まっています。","romaji":"Suiyou wa teikyuubi na node, mise wa shimatte imasu.","german":"Wednesday is the regular closing day, so the shop is closed.","furigana":"すいよう|ていきゅうび|みせ|し"},{"japanese":"毎週水曜の夜にヨガ教室に通っている。","romaji":"Maishuu suiyou no yoru ni yoga kyoushitsu ni kayotte iru.","german":"I go to a yoga class every Wednesday night.","furigana":"まいしゅうすいよう|よる|きょうしつ|かよ"},{"japanese":"会議は来週の水曜に延期になった。","romaji":"Kaigi wa raishuu no suiyou ni enki ni natta.","german":"The meeting was postponed to next Wednesday.","furigana":"かいぎ|らいしゅう|すいよう|えんき"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Suffix after a number for the grade level or year of study: 小学三年生, 高校二年生, 大学四年生; you ask with 何年生ですか. The separate card 一年生 additionally covers \"beginner, newcomer\". Do not confuse it with 〜年 (years, year number) and the identically pronounced 粘性 \"viscosity\".","examples":[{"japanese":"弟は小学三年生です。","romaji":"Otōto wa shōgaku sannensei desu.","german":"My younger brother is a third-year elementary school student.","furigana":"おとうと|しょうがくさんねんせい"},{"japanese":"「何年生ですか。」「大学二年生です。」","romaji":"\"Nannensei desu ka.\" \"Daigaku ninensei desu.\"","german":"\"What year are you in?\" \"I'm a second-year university student.\"","furigana":"なんねんせい|だいがくにねんせい"},{"japanese":"六年生は来月、修学旅行に行きます。","romaji":"Rokunensei wa raigetsu, shūgaku ryokō ni ikimasu.","german":"The sixth-graders go on a school trip next month.","furigana":"ろくねんせい|らいげつ|しゅうがくりょこう|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"Suffix attached after a number for the position in a sequence: 二番目, 三番目, 何番目; with に for rankings such as 二番目に高い \"the second highest\". Unlike 一番 (\"most; number one\"), 〜番目 counts neutrally; the prefixed 第〜 is more literary, 〜回目 counts repetitions. 一番目 and 三番目 have their own cards.","examples":[{"japanese":"右から二番目の人が私の兄です。","romaji":"Migi kara ni-banme no hito ga watashi no ani desu.","german":"The second person from the right is my older brother.","furigana":"みぎ|にばんめ|ひと|わたし|あに"},{"japanese":"日本で二番目に高い山は何ですか。","romaji":"Nihon de ni-banme ni takai yama wa nan desu ka.","german":"What is the second highest mountain in Japan?","furigana":"にほん|にばんめ|たか|やま|なん"},{"japanese":"名簿の上から何番目に名前がありますか。","romaji":"Meibo no ue kara nan-banme ni namae ga arimasu ka.","german":"Which position from the top is your name in the roster?","furigana":"めいぼ|うえ|なんばんめ|なまえ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1 (source: N1; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"みたい is the colloquial equivalent of ようだ: noun + みたい \"like, similar to\" (子供みたい), sentence + みたい \"apparently\" (雨みたいだ). It inflects like a na-adjective: みたいな + noun, みたいに + verb. Compared with らしい, みたい expresses more one's own impression. Do not confuse it with 〜てみたい \"want to try\" (from みる).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は子供みたいに無邪気に笑った。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kodomo mitai ni mujaki ni waratta.","german":"She laughed innocently like a child.","furigana":"かのじょ|こども|むじゃき|わら"},{"japanese":"外が暗くなってきた。雨が降るみたいだ。","romaji":"Soto ga kuraku natte kita. Ame ga furu mitai da.","german":"It's getting dark outside. It looks like it will rain.","furigana":"そと|くら|あめ|ふ"},{"japanese":"宝くじが当たるなんて、夢みたいな話だ。","romaji":"Takarakuji ga ataru nante, yume mitai na hanashi da.","german":"Winning the lottery would be like a dream.","furigana":"たから|あ|ゆめ|はなし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A polite request to sit down, formed from お + stem of 掛ける (here \"to sit down\") + ください; usually written in kana. You hear it at reception, at a doctor's office or in a job interview. Simpler is 座ってください, even more polite おかけになってください. The card 掛ける teaches only \"to hang\"; this sense \"to sit down\" lives mainly in this phrase.","examples":[{"japanese":"どうぞ、こちらにおかけください。","romaji":"Dōzo, kochira ni okake kudasai.","german":"Please, have a seat here."},{"japanese":"順番にお呼びしますので、そちらの椅子におかけください。","romaji":"Junban ni oyobi shimasu node, sochira no isu ni okake kudasai.","german":"I will call you in order, so please have a seat on the chairs over there.","furigana":"じゅんばん|よ|いす"},{"japanese":"面接官：「どうぞおかけください。」学生：「失礼します。」","romaji":"Mensetsukan: \"Dōzo okake kudasai.\" Gakusei: \"Shitsurei shimasu.\"","german":"Interviewer: \"Please have a seat.\" Student: \"Excuse me.\" (thank you)","furigana":"めんせつかん|がくせい|しつれい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Concessive particle attached to the te-form of verbs and i-adjectives: 雨が降っても \"even if it rains\", 高くても \"even if it's expensive\". After nouns and na-adjectives, and with the voiced te-form, でも is used (学生でも, 読んでも). With いくら, どんなに or 何度 it means \"no matter how (much/often)\". Not to be confused with the conjunction でも \"but\" (vocab-n5:1195).","examples":[{"japanese":"雨が降っても、試合は行われる。","romaji":"Ame ga futte mo, shiai wa okonawareru.","german":"Even if it rains, the match will be held.","furigana":"あめ|ふ|しあい|おこな"},{"japanese":"高くても、このかばんが欲しい。","romaji":"Takakute mo, kono kaban ga hoshii.","german":"Even though it's expensive, I want this bag.","furigana":"たか|ほ"},{"japanese":"いくら食べても太らない人がうらやましい。","romaji":"Ikura tabete mo futoranai hito ga urayamashii.","german":"I envy people who don't gain weight no matter how much they eat.","furigana":"た|ふと|ひと"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Attaches to the stem of i- and na-adjectives and turns it into a noun for degree or extent: 高い → 高さ (\"height\"), 寒い → 寒さ (\"cold\"), 便利 → 便利さ (\"convenience\"). Very productive and neutrally measurable, whereas 〜み (e.g. 甘み, 痛み) rather expresses a felt quality and works only with a few adjectives. Do not confuse it with the sentence-final particle さ or with 差/さ (\"difference\").","examples":[{"japanese":"この山の高さは三千メートルぐらいです。","romaji":"Kono yama no takasa wa sanzen mētoru gurai desu.","german":"The height of this mountain is about three thousand meters.","furigana":"やま|たか|さんぜん"},{"japanese":"北海道の冬の寒さには、まだ慣れません。","romaji":"Hokkaidō no fuyu no samusa ni wa, mada naremasen.","german":"I'm still not used to the cold of winter in Hokkaido.","furigana":"ほっかいどう|ふゆ|さむ|な"},{"japanese":"スマホの便利さを知ると、もう手放せない。","romaji":"Sumaho no benrisa o shiru to, mō tebanasenai.","german":"Once you know how convenient a smartphone is, you can't give it up.","furigana":"べんり|し|てばな"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"主な stands only directly before a noun and is not conjugated: 主な原因 \"main cause\", 主な目的 \"main purpose\", 主な産業 \"most important industry\". As a predicate (～は主だ) it is uncommon; for that, 中心だ or 一番大事だ is rather said. The related adverb is 主に \"mainly\"; 主なる is the stiff, literary variant of the same word. Do not confuse it with 主（しゅ）\"lord\" or 主（ぬし）\"owner\".","examples":[{"japanese":"この町の主な産業は観光です。","romaji":"Kono machi no omo na sangyō wa kankō desu.","german":"The main industry of this town is tourism.","furigana":"まち|おも|さんぎょう|かんこう"},{"japanese":"事故の主な原因はスピードの出しすぎだった。","romaji":"Jiko no omo na gen'in wa supīdo no dashisugi datta.","german":"The main cause of the accident was excessive speed.","furigana":"じこ|おも|げんいん|だ"},{"japanese":"最初に会議の主な目的を説明します。","romaji":"Saisho ni kaigi no omo na mokuteki o setsumei shimasu.","german":"First, I will explain the main purpose of the meeting.","furigana":"さいしょ|かいぎ|おも|もくてき|せつめい"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"条件を確認してください。","romaji":"Jōken o kakunin shite kudasai.","german":"Please check the conditions.","furigana":"じょうけん|かくにん"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"家賃が安いことを条件に、この部屋を選んだ。","romaji":"Yachin ga yasui koto o jōken ni, kono heya o eranda.","german":"I chose this room on the condition that the rent is cheap.","furigana":"やちん|やす|じょうけん|へや|えら"},{"japanese":"給料はいいけど、労働条件が厳しすぎる。","romaji":"Kyuuryou wa ii kedo, roudou jouken ga kibishisugiru.","german":"The pay is good, but the working conditions are too harsh.","furigana":"きゅうりょう|ろうどうじょうけん|きび"}],"notes":"条件 is a condition or prerequisite that must be met: 条件を満たす \"to meet the conditions\", 条件をつける \"to set conditions\". With 〜を条件に it means \"on the condition that ...\". Common compounds are 労働条件 \"working conditions\" and 応募条件 \"application requirements\". Do not confuse it with 状況, the overall situation.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"状況が変わりました。","romaji":"Jōkyō ga kawarimashita.","german":"The situation has changed.","furigana":"じょうきょう|か"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"道路の状況によって、到着時間が変わる。","romaji":"Dōro no jōkyō ni yotte, tōchaku jikan ga kawaru.","german":"The arrival time changes depending on road conditions.","furigana":"どうろ|じょうきょう|とうちゃくじかん|か"},{"japanese":"今どういう状況なのか、詳しく説明してください。","romaji":"Ima dou iu joukyou na no ka, kuwashiku setsumei shite kudasai.","german":"Please explain in detail what the situation is now.","furigana":"いま|じょうきょう|くわ|せつめい"}],"notes":"状況 refers to the overall situation, that is, how things currently stand around you: 状況が変わる, 状況を説明する, 状況を把握する \"to grasp the situation\". 状態, by contrast, is the condition of an individual person or thing (体の状態, 機械の状態). Common combinations are 現在の状況, 交通状況 and 〜な状況で \"in a ... situation\". The word is neutral and very common in the news as well as in the office.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"健康状態はいいです。","romaji":"Kenkō jōtai wa ii desu.","german":"My health is good.","furigana":"けんこうじょうたい"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"冷蔵庫のドアが開いた状態になっていた。","romaji":"Reizōko no doa ga aita jōtai ni natte ita.","german":"The refrigerator door was left open.","furigana":"れいぞうこ|あ|じょうたい"},{"japanese":"この中古の自転車は、状態がとてもいいです。","romaji":"Kono chuuko no jitensha wa, joutai ga totemo ii desu.","german":"This used bicycle is in very good condition.","furigana":"ちゅうこ|じてんしゃ|じょうたい"}],"notes":"状態 is the state of something at a given time: 健康状態, 体の状態, 部屋の状態. After a verb in the た-form, 〜た状態 describes a state that continues after an action: ドアが開いた状態, 電気をつけた状態で寝る. Fitting expressions are 状態がいい / 悪い and 〜状態になる. 状況, by contrast, means the external overall situation, and 様子 more the visible appearance or behavior.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"対策を考えましょう。","romaji":"Taisaku o kangaemashō.","german":"Let's think of a countermeasure.","furigana":"たいさく|かんが"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"暑さ対策として、窓に日よけを付けた。","romaji":"Atsusa taisaku to shite, mado ni hiyoke o tsuketa.","german":"As a measure against the heat, I put a sunshade on the window.","furigana":"あつ|たいさく|まど|ひ|つ"},{"japanese":"本屋で日本語能力試験の対策の本を買った。","romaji":"Hon'ya de Nihongo Nouryoku Shiken no taisaku no hon o katta.","german":"I bought a Japanese-Language Proficiency Test prep book at the bookstore.","furigana":"ほんや|にほんごのうりょくしけん|たいさく|ほん|か"}],"notes":"対策 is a targeted measure against a problem or in preparation for something. Typical are 対策を立てる \"to plan measures\" and 対策を取る \"to take measures\". As a suffix, 〜対策 names the problem being addressed: 暑さ対策, 地震対策, 風邪対策 - and 試験対策 means \"exam preparation\". A general plan with no problem behind it is, by contrast, 計画.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"責任を取ります。","romaji":"Sekinin o torimasu.","german":"I will take responsibility.","furigana":"せきにん|と"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"この仕事は最後まで責任を持ってやります。","romaji":"Kono shigoto wa saigo made sekinin o motte yarimasu.","german":"I will take responsibility for this job until the end.","furigana":"しごと|さいご|せきにん|も"},{"japanese":"事故の責任は、運転していた私にあります。","romaji":"Jiko no sekinin wa, unten shite ita watashi ni arimasu.","german":"The responsibility for the accident lies with me, since I was driving.","furigana":"じこ|せきにん|うんてん|わたし"}],"notes":"責任 is responsibility in the sense of duty and liability. The verbs are important: 責任を持つ \"to take responsibility, to look after something responsibly\", 責任を取る \"to take the consequences\" (often up to resigning) and 責任がある \"to be responsible\". 〜の責任だ means \"it is ...'s fault\". The feeling of being responsible, on the other hand, is 責任感.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"教育制度が変わります。","romaji":"Kyōiku seido ga kawarimasu.","german":"The education system will change.","furigana":"きょういくせいど|か"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"育児休暇の制度を利用して、三か月休んだ。","romaji":"Ikuji kyūka no seido o riyō shite, sankagetsu yasunda.","german":"I used the childcare leave system and took three months off.","furigana":"いくじきゅうか|せいど|りよう|さん|げつやす"},{"japanese":"来年から、会社に在宅勤務の制度が導入される。","romaji":"Rainen kara, kaisha ni zaitaku kinmu no seido ga dounyuu sareru.","german":"From next year, a work-from-home system will be introduced at the company.","furigana":"らいねん|かいしゃ|ざいたくきんむ|せいど|どうにゅう"}],"notes":"制度 is an institutional system or official arrangement, such as 教育制度, 社会保障制度 or 育児休暇の制度. Typical are 制度を導入する \"to introduce\", 制度を利用する \"to use\" and 制度が変わる. A single rule is 規則 or ルール; 仕組み describes more the mechanism of how something works. The word is neutral but especially common in the news and official language.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"最近の傾向を調べます。","romaji":"Saikin no keikō o shirabemasu.","german":"I will look into recent trends.","furigana":"さいきん|けいこう|しら"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"疲れると甘い物を食べたくなる傾向がある。","romaji":"Tsukareru to amai mono o tabetaku naru keikō ga aru.","german":"When I'm tired, I tend to want to eat sweet things.","furigana":"つか|あま|もの|た|けいこう"},{"japanese":"この町の人口は、減る傾向にある。","romaji":"Kono machi no jinkou wa, heru keikou ni aru.","german":"The population of this town is on the decline.","furigana":"まち|じんこう|へ|けいこう"}],"notes":"傾向 is a recognizable tendency or inclination. Very common is 〜傾向がある \"to tend to\" after a verb, and 〜傾向にある \"to be in the process of ...\": 増える傾向にある. In reports you read 最近の傾向 or 〜の傾向が見られる. Unlike ブーム or 流行 (\"boom, fad\"), 傾向 is sober and descriptive and can also refer to personal quirks; an observed tendency is not an exceptionless rule.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不思議な現象です。","romaji":"Fushigi na genshō desu.","german":"It's a mysterious phenomenon.","furigana":"ふしぎ|げんしょう"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"空に光の輪が見える珍しい現象が起きた。","romaji":"Sora ni hikari no wa ga mieru mezurashii genshō ga okita.","german":"A rare phenomenon occurred in which a ring of light could be seen in the sky.","furigana":"そら|ひかり|わ|み|めずら|げんしょう|お"},{"japanese":"その映画は大ヒットして、社会現象になった。","romaji":"Sono eiga wa daihitto shite, shakai genshou ni natta.","german":"The movie was a huge hit and became a social phenomenon.","furigana":"えいが|だい|しゃかいげんしょう"}],"notes":"現象 is an observable occurrence in nature or society: 自然現象, 社会現象. Typical are 現象が起きる / 起こる and 現象が見られる. The word describes what happens, not why; the cause is 原因. 社会現象 is also used for a huge hype, for example when a film captivates all of Japan. Do not confuse it with the identically read 減少 \"decrease, decline\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"テストの範囲を教えてください。","romaji":"Tesuto no han'i o oshiete kudasai.","german":"Please tell me the scope of the test.","furigana":"はんい|おし"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"無理のない範囲で運動を続けている。","romaji":"Muri no nai han'i de undō o tsuzukete iru.","german":"I keep exercising within a range that isn't too much for me.","furigana":"むり|はんい|うんどう|つづ"},{"japanese":"明日は広い範囲で雨が降るでしょう。","romaji":"Ashita wa hiroi han'i de ame ga furu deshou.","german":"Tomorrow it will rain over a wide area.","furigana":"あした|ひろ|はんい|あめ|ふ"}],"notes":"範囲 is the range or extent within which something applies: テストの範囲 \"exam material\", 予算の範囲 \"budget limits\". Typical are 範囲内 / 範囲外 and 〜の範囲で \"within the scope of\"; 無理のない範囲で means \"as far as possible without overdoing it\". It is also used spatially: 広い範囲で雨が降る \"it rains over a wide area\". A field of expertise or knowledge is rather 分野.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"基準を満たしています。","romaji":"Kijun o mitashite imasu.","german":"It meets the standard.","furigana":"きじゅん|み"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"値段だけを基準にして商品を選ばない。","romaji":"Nedan dake o kijun ni shite shōhin o erabanai.","german":"I don't choose products based on price alone.","furigana":"ねだん|きじゅん|しょうひん|えら"},{"japanese":"部屋がきれいかどうかの基準は、人によって違う。","romaji":"Heya ga kirei ka dou ka no kijun wa, hito ni yotte chigau.","german":"The criteria for whether a room is clean differ from person to person.","furigana":"へや|きじゅん|ひと|ちが"}],"notes":"基準 is a yardstick or criterion against which something is measured: 基準を満たす \"to meet the standard\", 基準に合う, 基準を決める. 〜を基準にする means \"to take something as a yardstick\". Common compounds are 安全基準 \"safety standard\" and 判断基準 \"decision criterion\". 基本, by contrast, means \"foundation, basis\" and is not a measured value.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"基本を大切にしましょう。","romaji":"Kihon o taisetsu ni shimashō.","german":"Let's value the basics.","furigana":"きほん|たいせつ"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"このソースを基本にして、好みの味に変えられる。","romaji":"Kono sōsu o kihon ni shite, konomi no aji ni kaerareru.","german":"Using this sauce as a base, you can change it to your preferred taste.","furigana":"きほん|この|あじ|か"},{"japanese":"うちの会社は、基本的に残業はありません。","romaji":"Uchi no kaisha wa, kihonteki ni zangyou wa arimasen.","german":"At our company, there is basically no overtime.","furigana":"かいしゃ|きほんてき|ざんぎょう"}],"notes":"基本 means the basics or fundamentals of something: 基本を学ぶ / 身につける \"to learn the basics\", 基本に戻る \"to return to the basics\". With 〜を基本にする it means \"to take something as a basis\". Very common is the adverb 基本的に \"basically, in principle\", which in conversation often leaves room for exceptions. 基礎 is similar but emphasizes the foundation on which one builds (基礎知識).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"製造過程を見学します。","romaji":"Seizō katei o kengaku shimasu.","german":"We will tour the manufacturing process.","furigana":"せいぞうかてい|けんがく"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"結果だけでなく、そこに至る過程も大切だ。","romaji":"Kekka dake de naku, soko ni itaru katei mo taisetsu da.","german":"Not only the result but also the process leading to it is important.","furigana":"けっか|いた|かてい|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"子どもの成長の過程を写真に残している。","romaji":"Kodomo no seichou no katei o shashin ni nokoshite iru.","german":"I keep photos of my child's growth process.","furigana":"こ|せいちょう|かてい|しゃしん|のこ"}],"notes":"過程 is the course or process through which something comes about or develops: 製造過程, 成長の過程, 〜に至る過程. Typical is 〜の過程で \"in the course of\". 段階, by contrast, refers to a single stage within such a sequence; プロセス is the loanword with almost the same meaning. Watch out for homophones: かてい is also 家庭 \"family, household\" and 仮定 \"assumption\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"次の段階に進みます。","romaji":"Tsugi no dankai ni susumimasu.","german":"We will proceed to the next stage.","furigana":"つぎ|だんかい|すす"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"この計画はまだ準備の段階にある。","romaji":"Kono keikaku wa mada junbi no dankai ni aru.","german":"This plan is still in the preparation stage.","furigana":"けいかく|じゅんび|だんかい"},{"japanese":"今の段階では、まだ何も決まっていません。","romaji":"Ima no dankai de wa, mada nani mo kimatte imasen.","german":"At this stage, nothing has been decided yet.","furigana":"いま|だんかい|なに|き"}],"notes":"段階 is a step or phase in a development: 次の段階に進む, 準備の段階, 最終段階. With 〜の段階で it means \"at the stage of, at the time of\": 今の段階では \"at the present stage\". The adverb 段階的に means \"step by step\". In contrast to 過程, the whole course of events, 段階 counts the individual sections; it also stands for gradations as in 五段階評価 \"rating on a five-point scale\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"相手の立場を考えましょう。","romaji":"Aite no tachiba o kangaemashō.","german":"Let's think about the other person's position.","furigana":"あいて|たちば|かんが"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"親の立場から見ると、その決定には不安がある。","romaji":"Oya no tachiba kara miru to, sono kettei ni wa fuan ga aru.","german":"From a parent's standpoint, that decision is worrying.","furigana":"おや|たちば|み|けってい|ふあん"},{"japanese":"私の立場では、その質問には答えられません。","romaji":"Watashi no tachiba de wa, sono shitsumon ni wa kotaeraremasen.","german":"In my position, I can't answer that question.","furigana":"わたし|たちば|しつもん|こた"}],"notes":"立場 literally means \"the place where one stands\": the position someone is in, either socially (教師という立場) or as a point of view (〜の立場から見ると). Fixed expressions are 相手の立場に立つ / 相手の立場になって考える \"to put oneself in the other person's shoes\" and 立場が弱い \"to be in a weak position\". In discussions, 立場 also denotes one's fundamental stance, whereas 意見 is a single opinion.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"専門の知識が必要です。","romaji":"Senmon no chishiki ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"Specialized knowledge is required.","furigana":"せんもん|ちしき|ひつよう"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"本で得た知識を実際の仕事で使ってみた。","romaji":"Hon de eta chishiki o jissai no shigoto de tsukatte mita.","german":"I tried using the knowledge I gained from books in actual work.","furigana":"ほん|え|ちしき|じっさい|しごと|つか"},{"japanese":"彼は歴史について豊富な知識を持っている。","romaji":"Kare wa rekishi ni tsuite houfu na chishiki o motte iru.","german":"He has extensive knowledge of history.","furigana":"かれ|れきし|ほうふ|ちしき|も"}],"notes":"知識 is acquired knowledge: 知識がある / ない, 知識を得る, 知識を身につける \"to acquire knowledge\". Common combinations are 専門知識 \"specialized knowledge\", 基礎知識 \"basic knowledge\" and 〜に関する知識; someone who knows a lot has 豊富な知識. 知恵, by contrast, denotes practical wisdom and resourcefulness, and 情報 individual pieces of information.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 46"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この絵には価値があります。","romaji":"Kono e ni wa kachi ga arimasu.","german":"This painting has value.","furigana":"え|かち"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"古い日記には、お金では測れない価値がある。","romaji":"Furui nikki ni wa, okane de wa hakarenai kachi ga aru.","german":"Old diaries have a value that cannot be measured in money.","furigana":"ふる|にっき|かね|はか|かち"},{"japanese":"あの美術館は、一度は行ってみる価値があるよ。","romaji":"Ano bijutsukan wa, ichido wa itte miru kachi ga aru yo.","german":"That museum is worth visiting at least once.","furigana":"びじゅつかん|いちど|い|かち"}],"notes":"価値 is the value of a thing, material or ideal: 価値がある / ない, 価値が上がる / 下がる. After a verb, 〜価値がある means \"it is worth ...ing\": 一度行く価値がある \"worth a visit\". Do not confuse it with 価格 or 値段, the concrete price in money. Well-known compounds are 価値観 \"values\" and 付加価値 \"added value\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"感情を表現します。","romaji":"Kanjō o hyōgen shimasu.","german":"I express my emotions.","furigana":"かんじょう|ひょうげん"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"怒りの感情をそのまま相手にぶつけないようにした。","romaji":"Ikari no kanjō o sono mama aite ni butsukenai yō ni shita.","german":"I tried not to just unleash my anger on the other person.","furigana":"いか|かんじょう|あいて"},{"japanese":"会議で感情的になってしまい、後で反省した。","romaji":"Kaigi de kanjouteki ni natte shimai, ato de hansei shita.","german":"I became emotional in the meeting and reflected on it afterwards.","furigana":"かいぎ|かんじょうてき|あと|はんせい"}],"notes":"感情 means feelings and emotions such as joy, anger or sadness: 感情を表す / 表現する, 感情を抑える \"to suppress emotions\", 感情的になる \"to become emotional\". 気持ち is more everyday and also covers mood or intention (感謝の気持ち); 感情 sounds more objective. 感想, on the other hand, is an impression or opinion about something experienced, and 感覚 is a sensory perception.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"感謝の気持ちを伝えます。","romaji":"Kansha no kimochi o tsutaemasu.","german":"I will convey my feelings of gratitude.","furigana":"かんしゃ|きも|つた"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"手伝ってくれた友人に、感謝を込めて手紙を書いた。","romaji":"Tetsudatte kureta yūjin ni, kansha o komete tegami o kaita.","german":"I wrote a letter full of gratitude to the friend who helped me.","furigana":"てつだ|ゆうじん|かんしゃ|こ|てがみ|か"},{"japanese":"両親には、本当に感謝しています。","romaji":"Ryoushin ni wa, hontou ni kansha shite imasu.","german":"I am truly grateful to my parents.","furigana":"りょうしん|ほんとう|かんしゃ"}],"notes":"感謝 is gratitude. As a する-verb, the person takes に: 先生に感謝する \"to be grateful to the teacher\", and so does the occasion: ご協力に感謝します \"we thank you for your cooperation\". Fixed expressions are 感謝の気持ち, 感謝を込めて \"with gratitude\" and 感謝してもしきれない \"one can never be grateful enough\". In daily life people usually just say ありがとう; 感謝しています sounds more serious and more elevated.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"相手の意見を尊重します。","romaji":"Aite no iken o sonchō shimasu.","german":"I respect the other person's opinion.","furigana":"あいて|いけん|そんちょう"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"意見が違っても、相手の選択を尊重したい。","romaji":"Iken ga chigatte mo, aite no sentaku o sonchō shitai.","german":"Even if our opinions differ, I want to respect the other person's choice.","furigana":"いけん|ちが|あいて|せんたく|そんちょう"},{"japanese":"この国では、個人の自由が尊重されている。","romaji":"Kono kuni de wa, kojin no jiyuu ga sonchou sarete iru.","german":"In this country, individual freedom is respected.","furigana":"くに|こじん|じゆう|そんちょう"}],"notes":"尊重する means \"to respect, to hold in regard\" and mostly refers to abstract things: 意見を尊重する, 意思を尊重する, 人権を尊重する. It is transitive with を and sounds objective to formal; a common pattern is 〜の気持ちを尊重する \"to take someone's feelings seriously\". 尊敬する, by contrast, means to admire and revere a person (先生を尊敬する). The passive is 尊重される \"to be respected\".","levelNote":"Derived from 尊重 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自分の意見を主張します。","romaji":"Jibun no iken o shuchō shimasu.","german":"I assert my own opinion.","furigana":"じぶん|いけん|しゅちょう"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"彼女は会議で、休憩時間を増やすべきだと主張した。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kaigi de, kyūkei jikan o fuyasu beki da to shuchō shita.","german":"At the meeting, she insisted that break times should be increased.","furigana":"かのじょ|かいぎ|きゅうけいじかん|ふ|しゅちょう"},{"japanese":"男は最後まで、自分は犯人ではないと主張した。","romaji":"Otoko wa saigo made, jibun wa hannin de wa nai to shuchou shita.","german":"The man insisted to the end that he was not the culprit.","furigana":"おとこ|さいご|じぶん|はんにん|しゅちょう"}],"notes":"主張する means \"to advocate an opinion emphatically, to claim\". The content takes を (意見を主張する, 権利を主張する \"to assert a right\") or is quoted with と (無実だと主張する \"to maintain one's innocence\"). It is neutral but more forceful than 言う; for stubborn insistence one rather says 言い張る. The noun 主張 appears in 自己主張が強い \"assertive, very sure of oneself\".","levelNote":"Derived from 主張 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仕事を評価されました。","romaji":"Shigoto o hyōka saremashita.","german":"My work was appreciated.","furigana":"しごと|ひょうか"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"価格だけでなく、使いやすさも評価して選んだ。","romaji":"Kakaku dake de naku, tsukaiyasusa mo hyōka shite eranda.","german":"I chose it by evaluating not only the price but also the ease of use.","furigana":"かかく|つか|ひょうか|えら"},{"japanese":"先生は、学生の努力を高く評価している。","romaji":"Sensei wa, gakusei no doryoku o takaku hyouka shite iru.","german":"The teacher thinks highly of the students' efforts.","furigana":"せんせい|がくせい|どりょく|たか|ひょうか"}],"notes":"評価する means \"to evaluate, to assess\" and, especially with 高く, \"to appreciate, to value\": 努力を高く評価する. The passive 評価される therefore often means \"to receive recognition\", not just \"to be graded\": 仕事が評価された. The noun 評価 stands for ratings and grades (評価が高い, 五段階評価), and on the internet also for customer reviews.","levelNote":"Derived from 評価 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"夢を実現したいです。","romaji":"Yume o jitsugen shitai desu.","german":"I want to make my dream come true.","furigana":"ゆめ|じつげん"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"家族で海外に住むという希望がようやく実現した。","romaji":"Kazoku de kaigai ni sumu to iu kibō ga yōyaku jitsugen shita.","german":"The hope of living abroad as a family finally came true.","furigana":"かぞく|かいがい|す|きぼう|じつげん"},{"japanese":"新しい駅の計画は、予算の問題で実現しなかった。","romaji":"Atarashii eki no keikaku wa, yosan no mondai de jitsugen shinakatta.","german":"The plan for the new station was not realized because of budget problems.","furigana":"あたら|えき|けいかく|よさん|もんだい|じつげん"}],"notes":"実現する means \"to realize\" or \"to become reality\" and is used both transitively and intransitively: 夢を実現する \"to realize a dream\", 夢が実現する \"a dream comes true\". Typical objects are 夢, 希望, 計画, アイデア. For measurable goals such as sales figures, 達成する fits better. In everyday speech, for dreams one also says 夢をかなえる / 夢がかなう; 実現 sounds somewhat more objective.","levelNote":"Derived from 実現 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"目標を達成しました。","romaji":"Mokuhyō o tassei shimashita.","german":"I achieved my goal.","furigana":"もくひょう|たっせい"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"毎日少しずつ走って、月に百キロの目標を達成した。","romaji":"Mainichi sukoshi zutsu hashitte, tsuki ni hyakkiro no mokuhyō o tassei shita.","german":"By running a little every day, I reached my goal of one hundred kilometers a month.","furigana":"まいにちすこ|はし|つき|ひゃっ|もくひょう|たっせい"},{"japanese":"チーム全員で協力して、売り上げ目標を達成できた。","romaji":"Chiimu zenin de kyouryoku shite, uriage mokuhyou o tassei dekita.","german":"The whole team worked together and was able to meet the sales target.","furigana":"ぜんいん|きょうりょく|う|あ|もくひょう|たっせい"}],"notes":"達成する means \"to reach a goal\", almost always with a clearly defined goal as the object: 目標を達成する, ノルマを達成する, 記録を達成する. From it comes 達成感 \"sense of accomplishment, feeling of having done something\". 実現する fits better for dreams and plans that become reality; 到達する means reaching a place or level. In the office you often hear the noun 目標達成.","levelNote":"Derived from 達成 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しいことに挑戦します。","romaji":"Atarashii koto ni chōsen shimasu.","german":"I will take on something new.","furigana":"あたら|ちょうせん"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"失敗を恐れず、初めてパン作りに挑戦した。","romaji":"Shippai o osorezu, hajimete panzukuri ni chōsen shita.","german":"Without fearing failure, I tried baking bread for the first time.","furigana":"しっぱい|おそ|はじ|づく|ちょうせん"},{"japanese":"若い選手が、チャンピオンに挑戦することになった。","romaji":"Wakai senshu ga, chanpion ni chousen suru koto ni natta.","german":"A young player is going to challenge the champion.","furigana":"わか|せんしゅ|ちょうせん"}],"notes":"挑戦する means \"to dare to try something, to take on a challenge\"; the target takes に: 新しいことに挑戦する, 世界記録に挑戦する. It can also mean to challenge a stronger opponent: チャンピオンに挑戦する. Unlike the English \"challenge\", 挑戦 does not mean a mere difficulty; for that one says 課題. The loanword チャレンジする is just as common in everyday speech and somewhat more casual.","levelNote":"Derived from 挑戦 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"困難を克服しました。","romaji":"Konnan o kokufuku shimashita.","german":"I overcame the difficulties.","furigana":"こんなん|こくふく"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"人前で話す練習を重ねて、苦手意識を克服した。","romaji":"Hitomae de hanasu renshū o kasanete, nigate ishiki o kokufuku shita.","german":"By practicing speaking in front of people repeatedly, I overcame my discomfort with it.","furigana":"ひとまえ|はな|れんしゅう|かさ|にがていしき|こくふく"},{"japanese":"彼は大きな病気を克服して、また舞台に立った。","romaji":"Kare wa ookina byouki o kokufuku shite, mata butai ni tatta.","german":"He overcame a serious illness and stood on the stage again.","furigana":"かれ|おお|びょうき|こくふく|ぶたい|た"}],"notes":"克服する means \"to overcome, to master\" and takes を for a difficulty one defeats by one's own strength: 困難を克服する, 病気を克服する, 苦手意識 / 弱点を克服する. It sounds somewhat elevated and emphasizes long effort. In everyday speech, people often say 乗り越える, which also fits grief and crises (\"to get over something\"); 克服 fits weaknesses and illnesses better.","levelNote":"Derived from 克服 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"問題を解決します。","romaji":"Mondai o kaiketsu shimasu.","german":"I will solve the problem.","furigana":"もんだい|かいけつ"},{"japanese":"話し合った結果、トラブルが解決した。","romaji":"Hanashiatta kekka, toraburu ga kaiketsu shita.","german":"As a result of talking it over, the trouble was resolved.","furigana":"はな|あ|けっか|かいけつ"},{"japanese":"その事件はまだ解決していない。","romaji":"Sono jiken wa mada kaiketsu shite inai.","german":"That case has not been solved yet.","furigana":"じけん|かいけつ"}],"notes":"解決する means to get a real problem, dispute or case out of the world. It is both transitive and intransitive: 問題を解決する \"to solve a problem\", 問題が解決する \"a problem gets solved\". A math or exam question, on the other hand, is \"solved\" with 解く (問題を解く). Also common are 解決策 \"solution\" and 解決に向けて \"toward a solution\".","levelNote":"Derived from 解決 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"経済が発展しています。","romaji":"Keizai ga hatten shite imasu.","german":"The economy is developing.","furigana":"けいざい|はってん"},{"japanese":"小さな相談が、町全体の計画へと発展した。","romaji":"Chiisana sōdan ga, machi zentai no keikaku e to hatten shita.","german":"A small consultation developed into a plan for the whole town.","furigana":"ちい|そうだん|まちぜんたい|けいかく|はってん"},{"japanese":"この町は昔、港町として発展しました。","romaji":"Kono machi wa mukashi, minatomachi to shite hatten shimashita.","german":"This town prospered as a port town in the past.","furigana":"まち|むかし|みなとまち|はってん"}],"notes":"発展する is intransitive and describes the growth and unfolding of economies, cities, technology or culture: 経済が発展する, 町が発展する. With 〜に／〜へと発展する it means \"to expand into something\", often also negatively: 小さなけんかが大きな問題に発展する. Well known is 発展途上国 \"developing country\". By contrast, 進歩 emphasizes qualitative improvement (technology, skill), while 発展 is more about growth and spread.","levelNote":"Derived from 発展 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"技術が進歩しました。","romaji":"Gijutsu ga shinpo shimashita.","german":"Technology has advanced.","furigana":"ぎじゅつ|しんぽ"},{"japanese":"練習を録音すると、自分がどれだけ進歩したか分かる。","romaji":"Renshū o rokuon suru to, jibun ga dore dake shinpo shita ka wakaru.","german":"If you record your practice, you can tell how much you have improved.","furigana":"れんしゅう|ろくおん|じぶん|しんぽ|わ"},{"japanese":"医学が進歩したおかげで、多くの命が救われている。","romaji":"Igaku ga shinpo shita okage de, ooku no inochi ga sukuwarete iru.","german":"Thanks to advances in medicine, many lives are being saved.","furigana":"いがく|しんぽ|おお|いのち|すく"}],"notes":"進歩する is intransitive and means that something becomes qualitatively better or more advanced: 技術が進歩する, 医学の進歩. It fits abstract fields well, as well as a person's general progress (少しずつ進歩している). For skills such as language or sports, people often say 上達する (日本語が上達する). 発展, by contrast, is more about growth and spread, for example of an economy or city.","levelNote":"Derived from 進歩 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"病気から回復しました。","romaji":"Byōki kara kaifuku shimashita.","german":"I recovered from my illness.","furigana":"びょうき|かいふく"},{"japanese":"午後には天気が回復して、外で遊べた。","romaji":"Gogo ni wa tenki ga kaifuku shite, soto de asobeta.","german":"The weather improved in the afternoon, so we could play outside.","furigana":"ごご|てんき|かいふく|そと|あそ"},{"japanese":"景気がなかなか回復しない。","romaji":"Keiki ga nakanaka kaifuku shinai.","german":"The economy is not recovering easily.","furigana":"けいき|かいふく"}],"notes":"回復する means to return to an earlier good state. Usually intransitive with が: 体力・景気・天気が回復する; one recovers from an illness with から (病気から回復する). Transitive with を it means \"to regain\": 意識を回復する, 信用を回復する. Unlike 治る (the illness itself heals), 回復 usually focuses on the person, the strength or the situation.","levelNote":"Derived from 回復 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"問題に対応します。","romaji":"Mondai ni taiō shimasu.","german":"I will deal with the problem.","furigana":"もんだい|たいおう"},{"japanese":"このアプリは古い機種にも対応している。","romaji":"Kono apuri wa furui kishu ni mo taiō shite iru.","german":"This app also supports older models.","furigana":"ふる|きしゅ|たいおう"},{"japanese":"この表では、数字がそれぞれの色に対応しています。","romaji":"Kono hyou de wa, suuji ga sorezore no iro ni taiou shite imasu.","german":"In this table, each number corresponds to a particular color.","furigana":"ひょう|すうじ|いろ|たいおう"}],"notes":"対応する always takes に and has three main meanings: \"to take care of something, to respond to something\" (客・問題・変化に対応する, 対応が早い), \"to correspond to something\" (AはBに対応する) and, in technology, \"to support, to be compatible\" (古い機種にも対応している, 日本語対応). In everyday work the first meaning is the most common. 応じる, by contrast, stresses complying with a request or demand.","levelNote":"Derived from 対応 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい計画を提案します。","romaji":"Atarashii keikaku o teian shimasu.","german":"I will propose a new plan.","furigana":"あたら|けいかく|ていあん"},{"japanese":"友達に、週末みんなでキャンプに行こうと提案した。","romaji":"Tomodachi ni, shuumatsu minna de kyanpu ni ikou to teian shita.","german":"I suggested to my friends that we all go camping on the weekend.","furigana":"ともだち|しゅうまつ|い|ていあん"},{"japanese":"部長は私の提案を受け入れてくれた。","romaji":"Buchou wa watashi no teian o ukeirete kureta.","german":"The department head accepted my proposal.","furigana":"ぶちょう|わたし|ていあん|う|い"}],"notes":"提案する is transitive: you propose something with を, and the person addressed takes に. The content can also be quoted: 〜ようと提案する, 〜ことを提案する. As a noun, 提案 goes with 出す, 受け入れる or 断る. Unlike 勧める (\"to recommend something to someone\", for their benefit), 提案 puts an idea up for decision; it is neutral to businesslike.","levelNote":"Derived from 提案 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は世界で活躍しています。","romaji":"Kare wa sekai de katsuyaku shite imasu.","german":"He is active on the world stage.","furigana":"かれ|せかい|かつやく"},{"japanese":"新しい選手が試合で大活躍した。","romaji":"Atarashii senshu ga shiai de daikatsuyaku shita.","german":"The new player played a great part in the game.","furigana":"あたら|せんしゅ|しあい|だいかつやく"},{"japanese":"このロボットは災害の現場で活躍している。","romaji":"Kono robotto wa saigai no genba de katsuyaku shite iru.","german":"This robot is playing an active role at disaster sites.","furigana":"さいがい|げんば|かつやく"}],"notes":"活躍する does not merely mean \"to be active\", but \"to stand out, to work successfully\": someone achieves something outstanding in a field. The field takes で, the role takes として: 世界で活躍する, 歌手として活躍する. Things can also 活躍する when they render useful service. Intensified, it is 大活躍. Mere \"being active\" without praise would be 活動する.","levelNote":"Derived from 活躍 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"社会に貢献したいです。","romaji":"Shakai ni kōken shitai desu.","german":"I want to contribute to society.","furigana":"しゃかい|こうけん"},{"japanese":"彼のゴールがチームの勝利に貢献した。","romaji":"Kare no gooru ga chiimu no shouri ni kouken shita.","german":"His goal contributed to the team's victory.","furigana":"かれ|しょうり|こうけん"},{"japanese":"この研究は環境問題の解決に大きく貢献しました。","romaji":"Kono kenkyuu wa kankyou mondai no kaiketsu ni ookiku kouken shimashita.","german":"This research contributed greatly to solving environmental problems.","furigana":"けんきゅう|かんきょうもんだい|かいけつ|おお|こうけん"}],"notes":"貢献する is intransitive; what one contributes to takes に: 社会に貢献する, 勝利に貢献する. As a noun, one says 〜への貢献 or 社会貢献. The word is rather formal and suggests a meaningful contribution toward a larger goal. In everyday speech, for \"to be useful\" people rather say 役に立つ; 協力する means \"to cooperate, to help out\".","levelNote":"Derived from 貢献 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"健康を維持します。","romaji":"Kenkō o iji shimasu.","german":"I maintain my health.","furigana":"けんこう|いじ"},{"japanese":"古い家を維持するには、お金がかかる。","romaji":"Furui ie o iji suru ni wa, okane ga kakaru.","german":"Maintaining an old house costs money.","furigana":"ふる|いえ|いじ|かね"},{"japanese":"このチームは三年間、一位を維持している。","romaji":"Kono chiimu wa sannenkan, ichii o iji shite iru.","german":"This team has maintained first place for three years.","furigana":"さんねんかん|いちい|いじ"}],"notes":"維持する is transitive and means to keep a state unchanged or to keep something in good repair: 健康・平和・体重・品質を維持する. Fixed words are 現状維持 \"status quo\" and 維持費 \"maintenance costs\". The Japanese verb 保つ is very similar but sounds more everyday (清潔を保つ). 続ける, by contrast, means to continue an action, not to preserve a state.","levelNote":"Derived from 維持 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自分で判断してください。","romaji":"Jibun de handan shite kudasai.","german":"Please judge for yourself.","furigana":"じぶん|はんだん"},{"japanese":"見た目だけで人を判断してはいけない。","romaji":"Mitame dake de hito o handan shite wa ikenai.","german":"You must not judge people by their looks alone.","furigana":"み|め|ひと|はんだん"},{"japanese":"危ないと判断して、試合を中止しました。","romaji":"Abunai to handan shite, shiai o chuushi shimashita.","german":"Judging it to be dangerous, we called off the match.","furigana":"あぶ|はんだん|しあい|ちゅうし"}],"notes":"判断する means to assess a situation and draw a conclusion from it. The object takes を, the result is quoted with と (危ないと判断する), and the basis takes で or から (見た目で判断する). Common expressions are 判断に迷う \"to be undecided\" and 判断がつかない \"cannot judge\". 決める is simply \"to decide on something\", whereas 判断 emphasizes weighing things up.","levelNote":"Derived from 判断 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"状況を把握します。","romaji":"Jōkyō o haaku shimasu.","german":"I will get a grip on the situation.","furigana":"じょうきょう|はあく"},{"japanese":"新しい仕事の内容を、まだ把握していません。","romaji":"Atarashii shigoto no naiyou o, mada haaku shite imasen.","german":"I still haven't grasped what my new job involves.","furigana":"あたら|しごと|ないよう|はあく"},{"japanese":"地震の後、市はまだ被害の大きさを把握できていない。","romaji":"Jishin no ato, shi wa mada higai no ookisa o haaku dekite inai.","german":"After the earthquake, the city has not yet been able to grasp the extent of the damage.","furigana":"じしん|あと|し|ひがい|おお|はあく"}],"notes":"把握する is transitive and means to fully take in facts or a situation and keep them in view: 状況・内容・現状・人数を把握する. It is typical of the working world and the news, for example 被害の状況を把握する. Unlike 理解する (understanding the meaning or reasons), 把握 is about having an overview of the facts. In everyday conversation people tend to say 分かる.","levelNote":"Derived from 把握 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"適切な対応をお願いします。","romaji":"Tekisetsu na taiō o onegai shimasu.","german":"Please respond appropriately.","furigana":"てきせつ|たいおう|ねが"},{"japanese":"この言葉は、ここでは適切ではない。","romaji":"Kono kotoba wa, koko de wa tekisetsu de wa nai.","german":"This word is not appropriate here.","furigana":"ことば|てきせつ"},{"japanese":"薬は医者の指示に従って、適切に使ってください。","romaji":"Kusuri wa isha no shiji ni shitagatte, tekisetsu ni tsukatte kudasai.","german":"Please use the medicine appropriately, following the doctor's instructions.","furigana":"くすり|いしゃ|しじ|したが|てきせつ|つか"}],"notes":"適切 is a な-adjective: 適切な対応, 適切に使う; its negative is 不適切. It sounds objective and formal and often appears in instructions, official texts and at work. 適当 also actually means \"suitable\", but colloquially it is often used for \"careless, any old way\"; 適切 never has this negative undertone. ふさわしい emphasizes that something suits a person or an occasion.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これは重要な問題です。","romaji":"Kore wa jūyō na mondai desu.","german":"This is an important issue.","furigana":"じゅうよう|もんだい"},{"japanese":"重要な書類なので、なくさないでください。","romaji":"Juuyou na shorui na node, nakusanai de kudasai.","german":"It's an important document, so please don't lose it.","furigana":"じゅうよう|しょるい"},{"japanese":"毎日続けることが一番重要です。","romaji":"Mainichi tsuzukeru koto ga ichiban juuyou desu.","german":"Continuing every day is the most important thing.","furigana":"まいにちつづ|いちばんじゅうよう"}],"notes":"重要 is a な-adjective meaning \"important, of great significance\": 重要な会議, 重要な書類, 重要性 \"importance\". It is more objective and more formal than 大切 and 大事. 大切 often expresses personal, emotional appreciation: 大切な友達, not 重要な友達. In news and texts, 重要 is the usual choice, for example in 重要人物 \"key person\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"深刻な問題です。","romaji":"Shinkoku na mondai desu.","german":"It's a serious problem.","furigana":"しんこく|もんだい"},{"japanese":"そんなに深刻な顔をして、どうしたの？","romaji":"Sonna ni shinkoku na kao o shite, dou shita no?","german":"You have such a serious look on your face. What's the matter?","furigana":"しんこく|かお"},{"japanese":"この地域では水不足が深刻になっている。","romaji":"Kono chiiki de wa mizubusoku ga shinkoku ni natte iru.","german":"The water shortage is becoming serious in this region.","furigana":"ちいき|みずぶそく|しんこく"}],"notes":"深刻 is a な-adjective for serious, worrying situations: 深刻な問題, 深刻な被害, 水不足が深刻になる. It also describes a grave expression or attitude: 深刻な顔, 深刻に考える. Do not confuse it with 真剣 (\"serious, in earnest\": 真剣に勉強する). 重大 emphasizes great consequence, as in 重大なミス.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"目標を明確にしましょう。","romaji":"Mokuhyō o meikaku ni shimashō.","german":"Let's make our goals clear.","furigana":"もくひょう|めいかく"},{"japanese":"彼は質問に明確に答えなかった。","romaji":"Kare wa shitsumon ni meikaku ni kotaenakatta.","german":"He did not answer the question clearly.","furigana":"かれ|しつもん|めいかく|こた"},{"japanese":"この会社には明確なルールがない。","romaji":"Kono kaisha ni wa meikaku na ruuru ga nai.","german":"This company has no clear rules.","furigana":"かいしゃ|めいかく"}],"notes":"明確 is a な-adjective for things that are clearly outlined and unambiguously defined: 明確な目標, 明確な理由, 明確に答える. 明確にする means \"to clarify, to define\". It sounds formal. 明らか, by contrast, emphasizes that something is obvious (〜のは明らかだ); はっきり is the everyday word and also applies to seeing and hearing.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自然が豊かな土地です。","romaji":"Shizen ga yutaka na tochi desu.","german":"It's a land rich in nature.","furigana":"しぜん|ゆた|とち"},{"japanese":"昔に比べて、生活が豊かになった。","romaji":"Mukashi ni kurabete, seikatsu ga yutaka ni natta.","german":"Compared with the past, life has become more prosperous.","furigana":"むかし|くら|せいかつ|ゆた"},{"japanese":"彼女は表情が豊かで、見ていて楽しい。","romaji":"Kanojo wa hyoujou ga yutaka de, mite ite tanoshii.","german":"She is very expressive, and it's fun to watch her.","furigana":"かのじょ|ひょうじょう|ゆた|み|たの"}],"notes":"豊か is a な-adjective with three main uses: abundance (自然が豊かな土地, 経験豊か), material prosperity of a society or a life (豊かな国, 生活が豊かになる) and richness of inner qualities (表情・想像力が豊か, 心が豊か). For \"He is rich\" one does not say 豊か but お金持ち. 豊か sounds positive and somewhat elevated.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"貴重な経験をしました。","romaji":"Kichō na keiken o shimashita.","german":"I had a valuable experience.","furigana":"きちょう|けいけん"},{"japanese":"貴重品はフロントに預けてください。","romaji":"Kichouhin wa furonto ni azukete kudasai.","german":"Please leave valuables at the front desk.","furigana":"きちょうひん|あず"},{"japanese":"お忙しいところ、貴重なお時間をいただき、ありがとうございます。","romaji":"Oisogashii tokoro, kichou na ojikan o itadaki, arigatou gozaimasu.","german":"Thank you for giving me your valuable time despite how busy you are.","furigana":"いそが|きちょう|じかん"}],"notes":"貴重 is a な-adjective meaning \"valuable, precious\", mainly because something is rare or hard to obtain: 貴重な経験, 貴重な資料, 貴重なご意見. 貴重品 are \"valuables\". In polite thank-you phrases, one often says 貴重なお時間をいただき. 大切, by contrast, emphasizes personal value, and 高価 the high price.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"巨大な建物です。","romaji":"Kyodai na tatemono desu.","german":"It's a huge building.","furigana":"きょだい|たてもの"},{"japanese":"海で巨大な魚が釣れた。","romaji":"Umi de kyodai na sakana ga tsureta.","german":"I caught a huge fish in the sea.","furigana":"うみ|きょだい|さかな|つ"},{"japanese":"巨大な台風が日本に近づいている。","romaji":"Kyodai na taifuu ga Nihon ni chikazuite iru.","german":"A huge typhoon is approaching Japan.","furigana":"きょだい|たいふう|にほん|ちか"}],"notes":"巨大 is a な-adjective for things far larger than normal: 巨大な建物, 巨大な魚. It usually comes before a noun and also forms compounds such as 巨大地震 or 巨大企業. It is stronger and more literary than 大きい／大きな. For quantities (data, costs, time), 膨大 is more common, since 巨大 mainly describes spatial size.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"膨大なデータを分析します。","romaji":"Bōdai na dēta o bunseki shimasu.","german":"We will analyze an enormous amount of data.","furigana":"ぼうだい|ぶんせき"},{"japanese":"この工事には膨大な費用がかかる。","romaji":"Kono kouji ni wa boudai na hiyou ga kakaru.","german":"This construction work will cost an enormous amount.","furigana":"こうじ|ぼうだい|ひよう"},{"japanese":"この図書館には膨大な数の本がある。","romaji":"Kono toshokan ni wa boudai na kazu no hon ga aru.","german":"This library has an enormous number of books.","furigana":"としょかん|ぼうだい|かず|ほん"}],"notes":"膨大 is a な-adjective for huge quantities or volumes: 膨大なデータ, 膨大な時間, 膨大な費用, 膨大な数の本. It almost always stands before the noun and sounds literary. For the spatial size of a single object, 巨大 is used (巨大な建物), not 膨大. In conversation it corresponds roughly to すごくたくさんの.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"柔軟に対応します。","romaji":"Jūnan ni taiō shimasu.","german":"I will respond flexibly.","furigana":"じゅうなん|たいおう"},{"japanese":"彼は考え方がとても柔軟だ。","romaji":"Kare wa kangaekata ga totemo juunan da.","german":"He thinks very flexibly.","furigana":"かれ|かんが|かた|じゅうなん"},{"japanese":"毎朝ストレッチをして、体を柔軟にしている。","romaji":"Maiasa sutoretchi o shite, karada o juunan ni shite iru.","german":"I stretch every morning to keep my body supple.","furigana":"まいあさ|からだ|じゅうなん"}],"notes":"柔軟 is a な-adjective for flexibility in thinking and acting (柔軟な考え方, 柔軟に対応する) and for the suppleness of the body (体が柔軟だ, 柔軟性). 柔軟剤 is \"fabric softener\". 柔らかい is the everyday word for \"soft\" to the touch; 柔軟 sounds more formal. The opposite in thinking is, for example, 頭が固い or 頑固.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"確実な方法を選びます。","romaji":"Kakujitsu na hōhō o erabimasu.","german":"I will choose a reliable method.","furigana":"かくじつ|ほうほう|えら"},{"japanese":"このままなら、彼の合格はほぼ確実だ。","romaji":"Kono mama nara, kare no goukaku wa hobo kakujitsu da.","german":"If things continue like this, his passing is almost certain.","furigana":"かれ|ごうかく|かくじつ"},{"japanese":"書類は確実に本人に渡してください。","romaji":"Shorui wa kakujitsu ni honnin ni watashite kudasai.","german":"Please be sure to hand the documents to the person themselves.","furigana":"しょるい|かくじつ|ほんにん|わた"}],"notes":"確実 is a な-adjective with two main focuses: \"reliable\" (確実な方法) and \"certain, settled\" (合格はほぼ確実だ). As an adverb, 確実に means \"definitely, reliably\" or \"steadily\" (確実に上達している). The opposite is 不確実. 確か expresses more subjective certainty and, at the start of a sentence, means \"if I remember correctly\"; 安全 means \"safe, harmless\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"具体的に説明してください。","romaji":"Gutaiteki ni setsumei shite kudasai.","german":"Please explain specifically.","furigana":"ぐたいてき|せつめい"},{"japanese":"具体的な例を挙げてください。","romaji":"Gutaiteki na rei o agete kudasai.","german":"Please give a concrete example.","furigana":"ぐたいてき|れい|あ"},{"japanese":"旅行の計画は、まだ具体的に決まっていない。","romaji":"Ryokou no keikaku wa, mada gutaiteki ni kimatte inai.","german":"The travel plans have not been decided in concrete terms yet.","furigana":"りょこう|けいかく|ぐたいてき|き"}],"notes":"具体的 is a な-adjective with -的: 具体的な例, 具体的に説明する. The opposite is 抽象的 \"abstract\". Related are 具体例 \"concrete example\" and 具体化する \"to make concrete\". The word is very common in school and at work when asking for details: もう少し具体的に言うと…","levelNote":"Compound: 具体 (N3) + 的 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"積極的に参加しましょう。","romaji":"Sekkyokuteki ni sanka shimashō.","german":"Let's participate actively.","furigana":"せっきょくてき|さんか"},{"japanese":"彼女は誰にでも積極的に話しかける。","romaji":"Kanojo wa dare ni demo sekkyokuteki ni hanashikakeru.","german":"She proactively talks to everyone.","furigana":"かのじょ|だれ|せっきょくてき|はな"},{"japanese":"うちの会社は外国人の採用に積極的です。","romaji":"Uchi no kaisha wa gaikokujin no saiyou ni sekkyokuteki desu.","german":"Our company is proactive about hiring foreigners.","furigana":"かいしゃ|がいこくじん|さいよう|せっきょくてき"}],"notes":"積極的 is a な-adjective meaning \"active on one's own initiative, proactive\": 積極的に参加する, 積極的な性格. With に, 〜に積極的だ means \"to be engaged in, open to something\". It is usually meant positively and does not mean \"aggressive\". 熱心 emphasizes zeal, whereas 積極的 emphasizes that one takes the initiative oneself. The opposite is 消極的.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"消極的な態度はよくないです。","romaji":"Shōkyokuteki na taido wa yokunai desu.","german":"A passive attitude is not good.","furigana":"しょうきょくてき|たいど"},{"japanese":"彼は会議ではいつも消極的で、あまり発言しない。","romaji":"Kare wa kaigi de wa itsumo shoukyokuteki de, amari hatsugen shinai.","german":"He is always passive in meetings and hardly speaks up.","furigana":"かれ|かいぎ|しょうきょくてき|はつげん"},{"japanese":"政府はその計画に消極的だ。","romaji":"Seifu wa sono keikaku ni shoukyokuteki da.","german":"The government is reluctant about that plan.","furigana":"せいふ|けいかく|しょうきょくてき"}],"notes":"消極的 is a な-adjective for a lack of initiative or a hesitant attitude: 消極的な態度, 消極的な性格. 〜に消極的だ means \"to be reluctant, not very willing toward something\". It is usually a negative evaluation. A quiet character alone is more likely 大人しい, shyness 恥ずかしがり屋. The opposite is 積極的.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"効率的に働きましょう。","romaji":"Kōritsuteki ni hatarakimashō.","german":"Let's work efficiently.","furigana":"こうりつてき|はたら"},{"japanese":"時間を効率的に使うために、毎朝予定を立てている。","romaji":"Jikan o kouritsuteki ni tsukau tame ni, maiasa yotei o tatete iru.","german":"To use my time efficiently, I make a plan every morning.","furigana":"じかん|こうりつてき|つか|まいあさよてい|た"},{"japanese":"この方法は効率的だが、少しお金がかかる。","romaji":"Kono houhou wa kouritsuteki da ga, sukoshi okane ga kakaru.","german":"This method is efficient, but it costs a little money.","furigana":"ほうほう|こうりつてき|すこ|かね"}],"notes":"効率的 is a な-adjective formed with -的 from 効率 \"efficiency\": 効率的な方法, 効率的に働く. The opposite is 非効率的. In conversation one also very often hears 効率がいい／悪い. 便利, by contrast, means \"handy, convenient\" and does not refer to the ratio of effort to result.","levelNote":"Compound: 効率 (N1) + 的 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"合理的な判断をしてください。","romaji":"Gōriteki na handan o shite kudasai.","german":"Please make a rational judgment.","furigana":"ごうりてき|はんだん"},{"japanese":"この部屋は狭いけど、収納が合理的に作られている。","romaji":"Kono heya wa semai kedo, shuunou ga gouriteki ni tsukurarete iru.","german":"This room is small, but the storage is designed efficiently.","furigana":"へや|せま|しゅうのう|ごうりてき|つく"},{"japanese":"彼の説明は合理的で、みんな納得した。","romaji":"Kare no setsumei wa gouriteki de, minna nattoku shita.","german":"His explanation was rational and everyone was convinced.","furigana":"かれ|せつめい|ごうりてき|なっとく"}],"notes":"合理的 is a な-adjective and means \"reasonable, rationally justified\" (合理的な判断, 合理的な説明), but also \"efficient, without waste\" (合理的な設計). Related is 合理化 \"rationalization\"; the opposite is 不合理. 論理的, by contrast, refers to the logic of an argument, 合理的 more to practical reason and efficiency.","levelNote":"Compound: 合理 (N2) + 的 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"意外な結果でした。","romaji":"Igai na kekka deshita.","german":"It was an unexpected result.","furigana":"いがい|けっか"},{"japanese":"彼が料理が上手だなんて、意外だった。","romaji":"Kare ga ryouri ga jouzu da nante, igai datta.","german":"I was surprised that he is good at cooking.","furigana":"かれ|りょうり|じょうず|いがい"},{"japanese":"このお店、安いのに意外とおいしいね。","romaji":"Kono omise, yasui noni igai to oishii ne.","german":"This restaurant is cheap, yet surprisingly tasty, isn't it?","furigana":"みせ|やす|いがい"}],"notes":"意外 is a な-adjective for things that differ from what was expected: 意外な結果, 〜なんて意外だ. Very common in conversation is the adverb 意外と／意外に \"surprisingly, actually quite\": 意外とおいしい. The surprise can be positive or negative. Do not confuse it with the similar-sounding 以外 \"except\". Similar is 予想外 \"unforeseen\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"問題は明らかです。","romaji":"Mondai wa akiraka desu.","german":"The problem is obvious.","furigana":"もんだい|あき"},{"japanese":"調査で、事故の原因が明らかになった。","romaji":"Chousa de, jiko no gen'in ga akiraka ni natta.","german":"The investigation revealed the cause of the accident.","furigana":"ちょうさ|じこ|げんいん|あき"},{"japanese":"彼がうそをついているのは明らかだ。","romaji":"Kare ga uso o tsuite iru no wa akiraka da.","german":"It is obvious that he is lying.","furigana":"かれ|あき"}],"notes":"明らか is a な-adjective for facts that are obvious and undeniable: 〜のは明らかだ, 明らかに間違っている. Very common in the news are 明らかになる \"to come to light\" and 明らかにする \"to reveal, to clarify\". 明確, by contrast, emphasizes that something is precisely defined (明確な目標); in everyday speech people often just say はっきりしている.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"急速に発展しています。","romaji":"Kyūsoku ni hatten shite imasu.","german":"It is developing rapidly.","furigana":"きゅうそく|はってん"},{"japanese":"スマホは急速に広まった。","romaji":"Sumaho wa kyuusoku ni hiromatta.","german":"Smartphones spread rapidly.","furigana":"きゅうそく|ひろ"},{"japanese":"社会の急速な変化についていけない人もいる。","romaji":"Shakai no kyuusoku na henka ni tsuite ikenai hito mo iru.","german":"Some people cannot keep up with the rapid changes in society.","furigana":"しゃかい|きゅうそく|へんか|ひと"}],"notes":"急速 is a な-adjective for fast change or development over time, not for speed of movement: 急速に発展する／広まる／増える, 急速な変化. A fast car is 速い, not 急速. 急 emphasizes suddenness (急に雨が降る), whereas 急速 emphasizes the high pace of a development. The word sounds literary and is typical of the news; compare also 急速冷凍 \"flash freezing\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"単純な問題ではありません。","romaji":"Tanjun na mondai dewa arimasen.","german":"It's not a simple problem.","furigana":"たんじゅん|もんだい"},{"japanese":"この機械は仕組みが単純なので、壊れにくい。","romaji":"Kono kikai wa shikumi ga tanjun na node, kowarenikui.","german":"This machine has a simple mechanism, so it is hard to break.","furigana":"きかい|しく|たんじゅん|こわ"},{"japanese":"彼は単純だから、ほめるとすぐ機嫌がよくなる。","romaji":"Kare wa tanjun dakara, homeru to sugu kigen ga yoku naru.","german":"He is simple-minded, so he's in a good mood right away when you praise him.","furigana":"かれ|たんじゅん|きげん"}],"notes":"単純 is a な-adjective meaning \"simply built, not complex\" (opposite 複雑): 単純な仕組み, 単純作業, 単純ミス \"careless mistake\". Applied to people it means \"naive, simple-minded\" and sounds slightly mocking. 簡単, by contrast, means \"easy to do\": 簡単な問題 is an easy task, 単純な問題 an uncomplicated matter. For plain design, one positively says シンプル.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"共通の話題を見つけましょう。","romaji":"Kyōtsū no wadai o mitsukemashō.","german":"Let's find a topic we have in common.","furigana":"きょうつう|わだい|み"},{"japanese":"私たちは共通の友達を通して知り合った。","romaji":"Watashitachi wa kyoutsuu no tomodachi o tooshite shiriatta.","german":"We got to know each other through a mutual friend.","furigana":"わたし|きょうつう|ともだち|とお|し|あ"},{"japanese":"この問題は多くの国に共通している。","romaji":"Kono mondai wa ooku no kuni ni kyoutsuu shite iru.","german":"This problem is common to many countries.","furigana":"もんだい|おお|くに|きょうつう"}],"notes":"共通 is mostly used with の before nouns: 共通の話題, 共通の友達, 共通の趣味. As a する-verb, AはBに共通する means \"A is common to B\"; 共通している describes the state. Common words are 共通点 \"common point\" and 共通語 \"common language\". The form 共通な is rare.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ルールに従ってください。","romaji":"Rūru ni shitagatte kudasai.","german":"Please follow the rules.","furigana":"したが"},{"japanese":"案内に従って、二階の会場へ行ってください。","romaji":"Annai ni shitagatte, nikai no kaijou e itte kudasai.","german":"Please follow the guidance and go to the venue on the second floor.","furigana":"あんない|したが|にかい|かいじょう|い"},{"japanese":"年をとるに従って、体力が落ちてきた。","romaji":"Toshi o toru ni shitagatte, tairyoku ga ochite kita.","german":"As I get older, my physical strength has declined.","furigana":"とし|したが|たいりょく|お"}],"notes":"従う is intransitive; what one follows takes に: ルール・指示・命令に従う, 親に従う. 守る (ルールを守る, with を) emphasizes keeping to something, whereas 従う emphasizes following and submitting. In instructions, 案内に従って means \"following the signs\". As N3 grammar, 〜に従って means \"as ..., the more ...\" or \"with increasing ...\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"要求に応じます。","romaji":"Yōkyū ni ōjimasu.","german":"I will comply with the request.","furigana":"ようきゅう|おう"},{"japanese":"インタビューに応じてくださって、ありがとうございます。","romaji":"Intabyuu ni oujite kudasatte, arigatou gozaimasu.","german":"Thank you for agreeing to the interview.","furigana":"おう"},{"japanese":"料金は人数に応じて変わります。","romaji":"Ryoukin wa ninzuu ni oujite kawarimasu.","german":"The fee changes depending on the number of people.","furigana":"りょうきん|にんずう|おう|か"}],"notes":"応じる is intransitive; what one responds to takes に: 要求・質問・誘い・インタビューに応じる. It means \"to comply with a request, to agree\" and sounds rather formal; plain \"to answer\" is 答える. Very common is 〜に応じて \"depending on, in accordance with\": 人数に応じて料金が変わる. The literary variant is 応ずる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"税金を含んでいます。","romaji":"Zeikin o fukunde imasu.","german":"Tax is included.","furigana":"ぜいきん|ふく"},{"japanese":"このジュースはビタミンCを多く含んでいる。","romaji":"Kono juusu wa bitamin shii o ooku fukunde iru.","german":"This juice contains a lot of vitamin C.","furigana":"おお|ふく"},{"japanese":"日本を含むアジアの国々で、この歌は人気がある。","romaji":"Nihon o fukumu Ajia no kuniguni de, kono uta wa ninki ga aru.","german":"This song is popular in Asian countries, including Japan.","furigana":"にほん|ふく|くにぐに|うた|にんき"}],"notes":"含む is transitive; the whole is the subject and the component takes を: このジュースはビタミンを含む. 〜を含む + noun means \"including, among them\". The passive is very common: 料金に税金が含まれている. Do not confuse it with 含める (ichidan), which expresses actively including: 私を含めて五人. In the sense of \"to hold in the mouth\", one says 水を口に含む.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"結果を示します。","romaji":"Kekka o shimeshimasu.","german":"I will show the results.","furigana":"けっか|しめ"},{"japanese":"このグラフは、人口の変化を示している。","romaji":"Kono gurafu wa, jinkou no henka o shimeshite iru.","german":"This graph shows the change in population.","furigana":"じんこう|へんか|しめ"},{"japanese":"彼は新しい計画に強い関心を示した。","romaji":"Kare wa atarashii keikaku ni tsuyoi kanshin o shimeshita.","german":"He showed strong interest in the new plan.","furigana":"かれ|あたら|けいかく|つよ|かんしん|しめ"}],"notes":"示す is transitive and means \"to show, to point out, to indicate\", especially for data, examples, directions and attitudes: グラフが変化を示す, 例を示す, 関心・態度を示す, 矢印が示す方向. It sounds more literary than 見せる, which is used for physically showing something (写真を見せる). So you do not show a photo with 示す.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"チャンスを与えてください。","romaji":"Chansu o ataete kudasai.","german":"Please give me a chance.","furigana":"あた"},{"japanese":"台風は農業に大きな被害を与えた。","romaji":"Taifuu wa nougyou ni ookina higai o ataeta.","german":"The typhoon caused great damage to agriculture.","furigana":"たいふう|のうぎょう|おお|ひがい|あた"},{"japanese":"公園の鳩にえさを与えないでください。","romaji":"Kouen no hato ni esa o ataenaide kudasai.","german":"Please do not feed the pigeons in the park.","furigana":"こうえん|はと|あた"}],"notes":"与える is transitive: AにBを与える. Toward people it sounds formal or condescending; in conversation, people rather say あげる／くれる. Very common with abstract objects: 影響・被害・印象・ショック・チャンスを与える. Signs often read えさを与えないでください \"Please do not feed (the animals)\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"完璧を求めすぎないでください。","romaji":"Kanpeki o motomesuginaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't demand too much perfection.","furigana":"かんぺき|もと"},{"japanese":"店員に説明を求めましたが、何も答えてくれませんでした。","romaji":"Ten'in ni setsumei o motomemashita ga, nani mo kotaete kuremasen deshita.","german":"I asked the clerk for an explanation, but he didn't answer anything.","furigana":"てんいん|せつめい|もと|なに|こた"},{"japanese":"彼は仕事を求めて東京に引っ越しました。","romaji":"Kare wa shigoto o motomete Toukyou ni hikkoshimashita.","german":"He moved to Tokyo in search of work.","furigana":"かれ|しごと|もと|とうきょう|ひ|こ"}],"notes":"求める means \"to want, to seek, to demand\" and takes を: 助けを求める, 意見を求める. The person asked takes に: 店員に説明を求める. Unlike 探す (to search for something concrete), 求める aims more at abstract things such as help, peace, work or solutions and sounds somewhat more formal. The passive 求められる means \"to be expected\"; in shops, polite お求め means \"purchase\" (お求めやすい価格).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"間違いを認めます。","romaji":"Machigai o mitomemasu.","german":"I admit my mistake.","furigana":"まちが|みと"},{"japanese":"両親は私たちの結婚を認めてくれた。","romaji":"Ryoushin wa watashitachi no kekkon o mitomete kureta.","german":"My parents approved of our marriage.","furigana":"りょうしん|わたし|けっこん|みと"},{"japanese":"彼の努力は会社にも認められた。","romaji":"Kare no doryoku wa kaisha ni mo mitomerareta.","german":"His efforts were also recognized by the company.","furigana":"かれ|どりょく|かいしゃ|みと"}],"notes":"認める is transitive and has several meanings: \"to admit\" (間違い・罪を認める), \"to permit, to approve\" (結婚・入学を認める) and \"to recognize, to appreciate\", often in the passive (努力・才能が認められる). In reports it also means \"to find, to detect\": 異常は認められない. With と it expresses an assessment: 本物と認める. 許す, by contrast, means \"to forgive\" or \"to allow\" and sounds more personal.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"意見を述べてください。","romaji":"Iken o nobete kudasai.","german":"Please state your opinion.","furigana":"いけん|の"},{"japanese":"首相は記者会見で、今後の方針を述べた。","romaji":"Shushou wa kisha kaiken de, kongo no houshin o nobeta.","german":"At the press conference, the Prime Minister stated the future policy.","furigana":"しゅしょう|きしゃかいけん|こんご|ほうしん|の"},{"japanese":"前の章で述べたように、この問題には二つの原因がある。","romaji":"Mae no shou de nobeta you ni, kono mondai ni wa futatsu no gen'in ga aru.","german":"As stated in the previous chapter, this problem has two causes.","furigana":"まえ|しょう|の|もんだい|ふた|げんいん"}],"notes":"述べる is transitive and means \"to state, to set out\" in formal situations: in speeches, essays, meetings and the news. Typical are 意見・理由・感想を述べる and, in texts, 〜と述べた \"stated that ...\" or 前に述べたように \"as stated above\". For ordinary speech, use 言う or 話す.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"役割を果たしました。","romaji":"Yakuwari o hatashimashita.","german":"I fulfilled my role.","furigana":"やくわり|は"},{"japanese":"約束を果たすために、彼はもう一度日本に来た。","romaji":"Yakusoku o hatasu tame ni, kare wa mou ichido Nihon ni kita.","german":"To keep his promise, he came to Japan once more.","furigana":"やくそく|は|かれ|いちどにほん|き"},{"japanese":"そのチームは初めて優勝を果たした。","romaji":"Sono chiimu wa hajimete yuushou o hatashita.","german":"The team won the championship for the first time.","furigana":"はじ|ゆうしょう|は"}],"notes":"果たす is transitive and means to carry out a task or duty or to achieve a goal: 役割・責任・義務・約束・目的を果たす. In sports news it often appears as 優勝を果たす \"to win the title\". For a promise, 約束を守る is more everyday, while 約束を果たす sounds weightier. As a second element, 〜果たす means \"completely\": お金を使い果たす.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"問題が生じました。","romaji":"Mondai ga shōjimashita.","german":"A problem has arisen.","furigana":"もんだい|しょう"},{"japanese":"二人の間に誤解が生じてしまった。","romaji":"Futari no aida ni gokai ga shoujite shimatta.","german":"A misunderstanding arose between the two of them.","furigana":"ふたり|あいだ|ごかい|しょう"},{"japanese":"予定に変更が生じた場合は、すぐにご連絡ください。","romaji":"Yotei ni henkou ga shoujita baai wa, sugu ni gorenraku kudasai.","german":"If there is a change to the schedule, please contact us immediately.","furigana":"よてい|へんこう|しょう|ばあい|れんらく"}],"notes":"生じる is mostly intransitive and means \"to arise, to occur\": 問題・誤解・疑問・差が生じる. It is the formal, written counterpart of 起こる or 出る and often appears in business e-mails and reports (変更が生じた場合). Transitively, \"to cause\", it is rare and literary. The variant 生ずる is even more formal.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大きな影響を及ぼします。","romaji":"Ōkina eikyō o oyoboshimasu.","german":"It has a great influence.","furigana":"おお|えいきょう|およ"},{"japanese":"たばこは健康に悪い影響を及ぼす。","romaji":"Tabako wa kenkou ni warui eikyou o oyobosu.","german":"Cigarettes have a bad effect on health.","furigana":"けんこう|わる|えいきょう|およ"},{"japanese":"今回の地震は、広い地域に被害を及ぼした。","romaji":"Konkai no jishin wa, hiroi chiiki ni higai o oyoboshita.","german":"This earthquake caused damage over a wide area.","furigana":"こんかい|じしん|ひろ|ちいき|ひがい|およ"}],"notes":"及ぼす is transitive and appears almost always in the combination Xに影響・被害・害を及ぼす \"to affect X, to harm X\". It is literary and typical of the news. The intransitive counterpart is 及ぶ \"to reach, to extend\": 被害が広い地域に及ぶ. 与える (影響を与える) is nearly synonymous, but 及ぼす emphasizes reaching out to something.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"全体の半分を占めています。","romaji":"Zentai no hanbun o shimete imasu.","german":"It makes up half of the whole.","furigana":"ぜんたい|はんぶん|し"},{"japanese":"この会社では、女性が社員の六割を占めている。","romaji":"Kono kaisha de wa, josei ga shain no rokuwari o shimete iru.","german":"At this company, women account for sixty percent of the employees.","furigana":"かいしゃ|じょせい|しゃいん|ろくわり|し"},{"japanese":"上位三位を日本の選手が占めた。","romaji":"Joui san'i o Nihon no senshu ga shimeta.","german":"Japanese athletes took the top three places.","furigana":"じょういさんい|にほん|せんしゅ|し"}],"notes":"占める is transitive and describes above all proportions: 全体の半分を占める, 六割を占める \"to account for sixty percent\". It also stands for taking positions: 上位を占める, 多数を占める. The word is typical of statistics and the news. Do not confuse it with the similar-sounding 閉める \"to close\" and 締める \"to tighten\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"礼儀正しく振る舞いましょう。","romaji":"Reigi tadashiku furumaimashō.","german":"Let's behave politely.","furigana":"れいぎただ|ふ|ま"},{"japanese":"彼は何もなかったかのように振る舞った。","romaji":"Kare wa nani mo nakatta ka no you ni furumatta.","german":"He acted as if nothing had happened.","furigana":"かれ|なに|ふ|ま"},{"japanese":"祭りの日には、近所の人に料理を振る舞う。","romaji":"Matsuri no hi ni wa, kinjo no hito ni ryouri o furumau.","german":"On festival days, we treat our neighbors to food.","furigana":"まつ|ひ|きんじょ|ひと|りょうり|ふ|ま"}],"notes":"振る舞う means \"to behave\" and needs an expression of manner: 礼儀正しく振る舞う, 大人らしく振る舞う, 何もなかったかのように振る舞う. Used transitively it means \"to treat someone (to food or drink)\": 客に料理を振る舞う. The noun 振る舞い means \"behavior, conduct\". 行動する, by contrast, means \"to act, to take action\". It is also often written in kana.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"天気に恵まれました。","romaji":"Tenki ni megumaremashita.","german":"We were blessed with good weather.","furigana":"てんき|めぐ"},{"japanese":"私はいい友達に恵まれて、本当に幸せです。","romaji":"Watashi wa ii tomodachi ni megumarete, hontou ni shiawase desu.","german":"I am really happy to be blessed with good friends.","furigana":"わたし|ともだち|めぐ|ほんとう|しあわ"},{"japanese":"この国は石油などの資源に恵まれている。","romaji":"Kono kuni wa sekiyu nado no shigen ni megumarete iru.","german":"This country is blessed with resources such as oil.","furigana":"くに|せきゆ|しげん|めぐ"}],"notes":"恵まれる is intransitive; what one is blessed with is marked with に: 天気・才能・友達・資源に恵まれる. Formally it is the passive of 恵む, but it is used as a verb in its own right. 恵まれた環境／家庭 means \"a privileged environment/family\", and 恵まれない子どもたち means \"underprivileged children\". It often expresses gratitude for good fortune that one had no control over.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本では、家に入るときに靴を脱ぐのが一般的です。","romaji":"Nihon de wa, ie ni hairu toki ni kutsu o nugu no ga ippanteki desu.","german":"In Japan, it is common to take off your shoes when entering a house.","furigana":"にほん|いえ|はい|くつ|ぬ|いっぱんてき"},{"japanese":"一般的に、女性のほうが男性より長生きだ。","romaji":"Ippanteki ni, josei no hou ga dansei yori nagaiki da.","german":"In general, women live longer than men.","furigana":"いっぱんてき|じょせい|だんせい|ながい"},{"japanese":"これはごく一般的な名前なので、同じ名前の人がたくさんいる。","romaji":"Kore wa goku ippanteki na namae na node, onaji namae no hito ga takusan iru.","german":"This is a very common name, so there are many people with the same name.","furigana":"いっぱんてき|なまえ|おな|なまえ|ひと"}],"notes":"一般的 is a な-adjective meaning \"usual, widespread\". Typical patterns are 〜のが一般的だ \"it is common that ...\", 一般的に（言えば）\"in general\" and 一般的な + noun \"ordinary, common\". The noun 一般 appears in 一般の人 \"the general public, laypeople\". 普通 is the more everyday word for \"normal\".","levelNote":"Compound: 一般 (N3) + 的 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"実際に行ってみました。","romaji":"Jissai ni itte mimashita.","german":"I actually went and tried it.","furigana":"じっさい|い"},{"japanese":"写真で見るより、実際に見たほうがずっときれいだった。","romaji":"Shashin de miru yori, jissai ni mita hou ga zutto kirei datta.","german":"Seeing it in person was much prettier than seeing it in photos.","furigana":"しゃしん|み|じっさい|み"},{"japanese":"このアプリを実際に使っている人の感想を聞きたい。","romaji":"Kono apuri o jissai ni tsukatte iru hito no kansou o kikitai.","german":"I want to hear the impressions of people who actually use this app.","furigana":"じっさい|つか|ひと|かんそう|き"}],"notes":"実際に contrasts real experience or reality with theory, imagination or hearsay: 実際に行ってみる, 実際に使ってみる. 実際は／実際には means \"in reality, though\" and contradicts an expectation; 実際の + noun means \"real, actual\". Unlike 本当に, 実際に is not an intensifier: \"really pretty\" is 本当にきれい, not 実際にきれい.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"結局行きませんでした。","romaji":"Kekkyoku ikimasen deshita.","german":"In the end, I didn't go.","furigana":"けっきょくい"},{"japanese":"いろいろ悩んだけど、結局いつもの店で買った。","romaji":"Iroiro nayanda kedo, kekkyoku itsumo no mise de katta.","german":"I agonized over many things, but in the end I bought it at my usual shop.","furigana":"なや|けっきょく|みせ|か"},{"japanese":"結局、何が言いたいの？","romaji":"Kekkyoku, nani ga iitai no?","german":"So, what is it that you want to say?","furigana":"けっきょく|なに|い"}],"notes":"結局 sums up the result after some back and forth, often with an outcome different from what was planned: 結局行かなかった. At the start of a sentence it also means \"when all is said and done, in the final analysis\" (結局は自分次第); 結局、何が言いたいの? impatiently asks for the point. ついに／とうとう, by contrast, emphasize the long-awaited occurrence, and 最後に means \"lastly\" in a sequence.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仕事は忙しい。一方、やりがいがある。","romaji":"Shigoto wa isogashii. Ippō, yarigai ga aru.","german":"Work is busy. On the other hand, it is rewarding.","furigana":"しごと|いそが|いっぽう"},{"japanese":"物価が上がる一方で、給料は上がらない。","romaji":"Bukka ga agaru ippou de, kyuuryou wa agaranai.","german":"While prices rise, salaries do not.","furigana":"ぶっか|あ|いっぽう|きゅうりょう|あ"},{"japanese":"最近、仕事が増える一方だ。","romaji":"Saikin, shigoto ga fueru ippou da.","german":"Recently, my work has been increasing more and more.","furigana":"さいきん|しごと|ふ|いっぽう"}],"notes":"一方 has three important uses: at the start of a sentence (一方、／一方で) it introduces the other side, \"on the other hand, in contrast\". After a clause, 〜一方（で）means \"while, at the same time\". After the dictionary form, 〜一方だ means \"it just keeps ...ing\", mostly for negative developments (増える一方だ). As a noun it means \"one side, one of the two\" (一方の意見). It is rather literary; in conversation people often say でも.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"やがて春が来ます。","romaji":"Yagate haru ga kimasu.","german":"Spring will come soon.","furigana":"はる|き"},{"japanese":"雨はやがて雪に変わった。","romaji":"Ame wa yagate yuki ni kawatta.","german":"The rain eventually turned to snow.","furigana":"あめ|ゆき|か"},{"japanese":"小さな店だったが、やがて全国に知られるようになった。","romaji":"Chiisana mise datta ga, yagate zenkoku ni shirareru you ni natta.","german":"It was a small shop, but before long it became known across the country.","furigana":"ちい|みせ|ぜんこく|し"}],"notes":"やがて describes that something occurs \"after a while, in time\" in the natural course of time, without anyone helping it along: やがて春が来る, やがて雪に変わる. It is rather narrative and literary and is almost always written in kana. When talking about one's own plans in conversation, people say もうすぐ or すぐ; いずれ means \"someday\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"むしろこちらの方がいいです。","romaji":"Mushiro kochira no hō ga ii desu.","german":"This one is rather better.","furigana":"ほう"},{"japanese":"夏の暑さは苦手で、むしろ冬のほうが好きだ。","romaji":"Natsu no atsusa wa nigate de, mushiro fuyu no hou ga suki da.","german":"I can't stand the summer heat; if anything, I prefer winter.","furigana":"なつ|あつ|にがて|ふゆ|す"},{"japanese":"彼は怒るどころか、むしろ喜んでいた。","romaji":"Kare wa okoru dokoro ka, mushiro yorokonde ita.","german":"Far from getting angry, he was actually happy.","furigana":"かれ|おこ|よろこ"}],"notes":"むしろ compares two possibilities and says that the second is more accurate, often contrary to expectation: AよりむしろB, むしろ〜ほうがいい. With 〜どころか it means \"quite the opposite\". かえって, by contrast, emphasizes that a result runs counter to the intention (手伝ったらかえって邪魔になった). It is mostly written in kana and sounds somewhat elevated; in conversation people also say どちらかというと.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仮に失敗してもやり直します。","romaji":"Kari ni shippai shite mo yarinaoshimasu.","german":"Even if I fail, I will start over.","furigana":"かり|しっぱい|なお"},{"japanese":"仮に宝くじが当たったら、何をしますか。","romaji":"Kari ni takarakuji ga atattara, nani o shimasu ka.","german":"Supposing you won the lottery, what would you do?","furigana":"かり|たから|あ|なに"},{"japanese":"会議の日は仮に金曜日にしておきましょう。","romaji":"Kaigi no hi wa kari ni kin'youbi ni shite okimashou.","german":"Let's tentatively set the meeting day for Friday.","furigana":"かいぎ|ひ|かり|きんようび"}],"notes":"仮に introduces a purely hypothetical assumption and usually stands at the start of a sentence together with 〜ても, 〜としても, 〜たら or 〜とすれば: 仮にそうだとしても \"even if that were so\". Compared with the everyday もし, 仮に sounds more objective and stresses \"just for the sake of argument\". The second meaning is \"provisionally, temporarily\": 仮に決めておく \"to set provisionally\" (cf. 仮の \"provisional\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"直接会って話しましょう。","romaji":"Chokusetsu atte hanashimashō.","german":"Let's meet and talk in person.","furigana":"ちょくせつあ|はな"},{"japanese":"書類は担当者に直接渡してください。","romaji":"Shorui wa tantousha ni chokusetsu watashite kudasai.","german":"Please hand the documents directly to the person in charge.","furigana":"しょるい|たんとうしゃ|ちょくせつわた"},{"japanese":"事故の直接の原因はまだ分かっていません。","romaji":"Jiko no chokusetsu no gen'in wa mada wakatte imasen.","german":"The direct cause of the accident is not yet known.","furigana":"じこ|ちょくせつ|げんいん|わ"}],"notes":"直接 is used as an adverb without に directly before the verb: 直接会う, 直接話す, 直接渡す. Before nouns it is connected with の: 直接の原因 \"direct cause\", 直接の関係はない. The opposite is 間接 or 間接的; the na-adjective form is 直接的. 直接会って話す means \"to talk in person (not by mail or phone)\"; for a trip without a detour, 直行する is rather said.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 26"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"間接的に伝えました。","romaji":"Kansetsuteki ni tsutaemashita.","german":"I conveyed it indirectly.","furigana":"かんせつてき|つた"},{"japanese":"彼の話は間接的な表現が多くて、分かりにくいです。","romaji":"Kare no hanashi wa kansetsuteki na hyougen ga ookute, wakarinikui desu.","german":"He often expresses himself indirectly, so he is hard to understand.","furigana":"かれ|はなし|かんせつてき|ひょうげん|おお|わ"},{"japanese":"円安は私たちの生活にも間接的に影響しています。","romaji":"En'yasu wa watashitachi no seikatsu ni mo kansetsuteki ni eikyou shite imasu.","german":"The weak yen also has an indirect effect on our daily lives.","furigana":"えんやす|わたし|せいかつ|かんせつてき|えいきょう"}],"notes":"間接的 is a na-adjective formed with 〜的: 間接的な表現 \"indirect expression\", adverbially 間接的に伝える/影響する. Opposite: 直接的, or simply 直接. For a message deliberately put tactfully or in a roundabout way, 遠回しに is also common in conversation; 間接的 sounds more matter-of-fact and is also used for effects and causes (間接的な原因).","levelNote":"Compound: 間接 (N2) + 的 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"最も重要な問題です。","romaji":"Mottomo jūyō na mondai desu.","german":"It is the most important problem.","furigana":"もっと|じゅうよう|もんだい"},{"japanese":"富士山は日本で最も高い山です。","romaji":"Fujisan wa Nihon de mottomo takai yama desu.","german":"Mt. Fuji is the highest mountain in Japan.","furigana":"ふじさん|にほん|もっと|たか|やま"},{"japanese":"一年で最も寒いのは二月です。","romaji":"Ichinen de mottomo samui no wa nigatsu desu.","german":"The coldest time of the year is February.","furigana":"いちねん|もっと|さむ|にがつ"}],"notes":"最も forms the superlative before adjectives: 最も高い山, 最も重要な問題. It is the formal, written counterpart of 一番; in conversation, 一番 is mostly said. The frame of comparison is marked by で or の中で: 日本で最も…. Do not confuse it with もっと \"more\" and with the identically sounding もっとも (尤も) \"however, understandably\", which is mostly written in kana.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"極めて難しい問題です。","romaji":"Kiwamete muzukashii mondai desu.","german":"It is an extremely difficult problem.","furigana":"きわ|むずか|もんだい"},{"japanese":"その地域では、雨が降ることは極めてまれです。","romaji":"Sono chiiki de wa, ame ga furu koto wa kiwamete mare desu.","german":"In that region, it rains extremely rarely.","furigana":"ちいき|あめ|ふ|きわ"},{"japanese":"医者によると、手術が成功する可能性は極めて高いそうです。","romaji":"Isha ni yoru to, shujutsu ga seikou suru kanousei wa kiwamete takai sou desu.","german":"According to the doctor, the chance that the operation will succeed is extremely high.","furigana":"いしゃ|しゅじゅつ|せいこう|かのうせい|きわ|たか"}],"notes":"極めて is a formal intensifying adverb (\"extremely, highly\"), stronger than とても and more formal than 非常に. It stands before adjectives and na-adjectives, as in 極めて重要, 極めて危険, 極めてまれ, and is typical of news, reports and technical texts. For everyday feelings it sounds stiff (instead of 極めておいしい one says すごくおいしい). It comes from the verb 極める \"to push to the utmost\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"問題はますます深刻になっています。","romaji":"Mondai wa masumasu shinkoku ni natte imasu.","german":"The problem is becoming more and more serious.","furigana":"もんだい|しんこく"},{"japanese":"説明を聞いたら、ますます分からなくなりました。","romaji":"Setsumei o kiitara, masumasu wakaranaku narimashita.","german":"After listening to the explanation, I understood even less.","furigana":"せつめい|き|わ"},{"japanese":"練習するほど、ますます上手になりますよ。","romaji":"Renshuu suru hodo, masumasu jouzu ni narimasu yo.","german":"The more you practice, the better you will get.","furigana":"れんしゅう|じょうず"}],"notes":"ますます (rarely 益々) describes that an already existing state intensifies further; typically with 〜くなる/〜になる and 〜ている/〜てきた. With a negative it means \"less and less\": ますます分からなくなる. Compared with だんだん (\"gradually\", a neutral change), ますます emphasizes the increase. In letters and speeches there is the formula ますますのご活躍をお祈りします.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"次第に慣れてきました。","romaji":"Shidai ni narete kimashita.","german":"I have gradually gotten used to it.","furigana":"しだい|な"},{"japanese":"夕方になると、空は次第に暗くなっていきました。","romaji":"Yuugata ni naru to, sora wa shidai ni kuraku natte ikimashita.","german":"In the evening, the sky gradually grew dark.","furigana":"ゆうがた|そら|しだい|くら"},{"japanese":"薬を飲んだら、熱は次第に下がりました。","romaji":"Kusuri o nondara, netsu wa shidai ni sagarimashita.","german":"After I took the medicine, the fever gradually went down.","furigana":"くすり|の|ねつ|しだい|さ"}],"notes":"次第に describes a slow, steady change and is used with verbs of change such as 慣れる, 暗くなる, 下がる, often with 〜てくる/〜ていく. It sounds somewhat more literary than the everyday だんだん; it does not fit one-off, intentional actions. Do not confuse it with the suffix 〜次第: 着き次第 \"as soon as I arrive\", 天気次第 \"depending on the weather\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"チームの構成を考えます。","romaji":"Chīmu no kōsei o kangaemasu.","german":"I will think about the composition of the team.","furigana":"こうせい|かんが"},{"japanese":"この本は三つの章で構成されています。","romaji":"Kono hon wa mittsu no shou de kousei sarete imasu.","german":"This book is composed of three chapters.","furigana":"ほん|みっ|しょう|こうせい"},{"japanese":"申込書に家族構成を書く欄があります。","romaji":"Moushikomisho ni kazoku kousei o kaku ran ga arimasu.","german":"The application form has a field for writing the family composition.","furigana":"もうしこみしょ|かぞくこうせい|か|らん"}],"notes":"構成 refers to how a whole is put together from its parts: 家族構成, チームの構成, 文章の構成, 番組の構成. As a transitive する-verb it mostly appears in the passive: AはBで構成されている \"A consists of B\". Unlike 構造 (fixed inner structure, for example of a building), 構成 is about the arrangement or selection of elements, which can be planned and changed.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"建物の構造を調べます。","romaji":"Tatemono no kōzō o shirabemasu.","german":"I will examine the structure of the building.","furigana":"たてもの|こうぞう|しら"},{"japanese":"日本語と英語は文の構造がかなり違います。","romaji":"Nihongo to eigo wa bun no kouzou ga kanari chigaimasu.","german":"Japanese and English differ quite a lot in sentence structure.","furigana":"にほんご|えいご|ぶん|こうぞう|ちが"},{"japanese":"この家は地震に強い構造になっています。","romaji":"Kono ie wa jishin ni tsuyoi kouzou ni natte imasu.","german":"This house is built to withstand earthquakes.","furigana":"いえ|じしん|つよ|こうぞう"}],"notes":"構造 is the internal structure or construction of something: 建物の構造, 文の構造 \"sentence structure\", 社会の構造, 体の構造. Common patterns are 〜な構造になっている and 構造上 \"structurally\". Distinction: 構成 emphasizes the composition from parts, 仕組み rather how something works. 構造 is not a する-verb.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この機械の仕組みを説明します。","romaji":"Kono kikai no shikumi o setsumei shimasu.","german":"I will explain the mechanism of this machine.","furigana":"きかい|しく|せつめい"},{"japanese":"このドアは、カードをかざすと開く仕組みになっています。","romaji":"Kono doa wa, kaado o kazasu to aku shikumi ni natte imasu.","german":"This door is set up so that it opens when you hold a card up to it.","furigana":"あ|しく"},{"japanese":"日本の選挙の仕組みがよく分かりません。","romaji":"Nihon no senkyo no shikumi ga yoku wakarimasen.","german":"I don't really understand the Japanese election system.","furigana":"にほん|せんきょ|しく|わ"}],"notes":"仕組み (also written しくみ) describes how something works or is set up: 機械の仕組み, 社会の仕組み, 選挙の仕組み. Very common is 〜仕組みになっている \"is set up so that ...\". Unlike 構造 (physical structure), it is about processes and mechanisms. The related verb 仕組む, by contrast, mostly means \"to scheme, to engineer\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"成功の要素を分析します。","romaji":"Seikō no yōso o bunseki shimasu.","german":"I will analyze the elements of success.","furigana":"せいこう|ようそ|ぶんせき"},{"japanese":"この映画には、恋愛とミステリーの両方の要素があります。","romaji":"Kono eiga ni wa, ren'ai to misuterii no ryouhou no youso ga arimasu.","german":"This movie has elements of both romance and mystery.","furigana":"えいが|れんあい|りょうほう|ようそ"},{"japanese":"料理では、味だけでなく見た目も大切な要素です。","romaji":"Ryouri de wa, aji dake de naku mitame mo taisetsu na youso desu.","german":"In cooking, not only taste but also appearance is an important element.","furigana":"りょうり|あじ|み|め|たいせつ|ようそ"}],"notes":"要素 is a component or element that belongs to a whole: 重要な要素, 〜の要素がある \"has something of ...\". Unlike 要因 (a factor that causes a result), 要素 is more an \"ingredient\" of the whole. In IT it also denotes elements of an array or a list. Neutral to slightly formal.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本語の特徴を説明します。","romaji":"Nihongo no tokuchō o setsumei shimasu.","german":"I will explain the characteristics of Japanese.","furigana":"にほんご|とくちょう|せつめい"},{"japanese":"犯人の特徴を教えてください。","romaji":"Hannin no tokuchou o oshiete kudasai.","german":"Please tell me the characteristics of the culprit.","furigana":"はんにん|とくちょう|おし"},{"japanese":"この町の特徴は、古い建物が多いことです。","romaji":"Kono machi no tokuchou wa, furui tatemono ga ooi koto desu.","german":"The feature of this town is that it has many old buildings.","furigana":"まち|とくちょう|ふる|たてもの|おお"}],"notes":"特徴 is a conspicuous, distinguishing feature: 〜の特徴は…ことだ, 特徴がある, 特徴的な. It can be neutral, for example in descriptions of persons (犯人の特徴). The identically read 特長, by contrast, means \"strength, merit\" and is positive; 特色 emphasizes the distinctive character of a region or institution.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"成功の要因は努力です。","romaji":"Seikō no yōin wa doryoku desu.","german":"The key to success is hard work.","furigana":"せいこう|よういん|どりょく"},{"japanese":"事故の要因はいくつか考えられます。","romaji":"Jiko no youin wa ikutsu ka kangaeraremasu.","german":"Several causes can be considered for the accident.","furigana":"じこ|よういん|かんが"},{"japanese":"売り上げが下がった要因を調べています。","romaji":"Uriage ga sagatta youin o shirabete imasu.","german":"We are investigating the factors behind the drop in sales.","furigana":"う|あ|さ|よういん|しら"}],"notes":"要因 is a (main) factor that contributes to a result: 〜の要因, 主な要因, 〜が要因となる. It sounds more analytical than 原因: 原因 is the direct cause (for example of an accident), 要因 is one of several contributing factors. Typical of reports, news and analyses; compare also 要素 \"component\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"事件の背景を調べます。","romaji":"Jiken no haikei o shirabemasu.","german":"I will look into the background of the incident.","furigana":"じけん|はいけい|しら"},{"japanese":"富士山を背景に写真を撮りました。","romaji":"Fujisan o haikei ni shashin o torimashita.","german":"We took a photo with Mt. Fuji in the background.","furigana":"ふじさん|はいけい|しゃしん|と"},{"japanese":"この問題の背景には、人手不足があります。","romaji":"Kono mondai no haikei ni wa, hitode busoku ga arimasu.","german":"Behind this problem lies a labor shortage.","furigana":"もんだい|はいけい|ひとでぶそく"}],"notes":"背景 is on the one hand the visible background of a picture or stage (〜を背景に写真を撮る), on the other the background of an event: 事件の背景, 〜の背景には…がある \"behind ... lies ...\". For a person's personal career one does not say 背景 but 経歴; for photos, バック is also common colloquially.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"事業を海外に展開します。","romaji":"Jigyō o kaigai ni tenkai shimasu.","german":"We will expand the business overseas.","furigana":"じぎょう|かいがい|てんかい"},{"japanese":"このドラマは展開が速くて、目が離せません。","romaji":"Kono dorama wa tenkai ga hayakute, me ga hanasemasen.","german":"The plot of this drama moves so fast that you can't look away.","furigana":"てんかい|はや|め|はな"},{"japanese":"試合は予想外の展開になりました。","romaji":"Shiai wa yosougai no tenkai ni narimashita.","german":"The match took an unexpected turn.","furigana":"しあい|よそうがい|てんかい"}],"notes":"展開 means, first, the development of events: 話の展開, 意外な展開, 展開が速い. Second, the expansion of an activity: 事業を展開する, 全国展開. As a する-verb it is both transitive (〜を展開する) and intransitive (話が展開する). In IT it means \"to unpack/extract\" (ファイルを展開する), and in mathematics \"to expand\" (an expression).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"研究の成果を発表します。","romaji":"Kenkyū no seika o happyō shimasu.","german":"I will present the results of my research.","furigana":"けんきゅう|せいか|はっぴょう"},{"japanese":"毎日練習した成果が出て、試合に勝てました。","romaji":"Mainichi renshuu shita seika ga dete, shiai ni katemashita.","german":"The results of daily practice showed, and we won the match.","furigana":"まいにちれんしゅう|せいか|で|しあい|か"},{"japanese":"新しい方法を試しましたが、あまり成果はありませんでした。","romaji":"Atarashii houhou o tameshimashita ga, amari seika wa arimasen deshita.","german":"I tried a new method, but it didn't produce much result.","furigana":"あたら|ほうほう|ため|せいか"}],"notes":"成果 is the positive result of an effort, the \"fruit of one's work\": 成果を上げる/出す, 成果が出る, 研究成果. Unlike 結果, which is neutral and can also turn out badly, 成果 is always a success; 成果がない means \"without yield\". Compare 実績 \"proven track record\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この会社には素晴らしい実績があります。","romaji":"Kono kaisha ni wa subarashii jisseki ga arimasu.","german":"This company has an outstanding track record.","furigana":"かいしゃ|すば|じっせき"},{"japanese":"入社したばかりなので、まだ実績がありません。","romaji":"Nyuusha shita bakari na node, mada jisseki ga arimasen.","german":"I just joined the company, so I don't have a track record yet.","furigana":"にゅうしゃ|じっせき"},{"japanese":"彼は実績を積んで、課長になりました。","romaji":"Kare wa jisseki o tsunde, kachou ni narimashita.","german":"He built up a track record and became a section manager.","furigana":"かれ|じっせき|つ|かちょう"}],"notes":"実績 means results actually achieved and verifiable, a track record: 実績がある/ない, 実績を積む, 実績を上げる, 販売実績. Typical in business and job applications. 成果 is the result of a specific effort, and 業績 is a company's business performance or scholarly achievements.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"会社の業績が良くなりました。","romaji":"Kaisha no gyōseki ga yoku narimashita.","german":"The company's business results improved.","furigana":"かいしゃ|ぎょうせき|よ"},{"japanese":"業績が悪化したため、ボーナスが減りました。","romaji":"Gyouseki ga akka shita tame, boonasu ga herimashita.","german":"Because performance worsened, bonuses were reduced.","furigana":"ぎょうせき|あっか|へ"},{"japanese":"先生は医学の分野で大きな業績を残しました。","romaji":"Sensei wa igaku no bun'ya de ookina gyouseki o nokoshimashita.","german":"The professor left great achievements in the field of medicine.","furigana":"せんせい|いがく|ぶんや|おお|ぎょうせき|のこ"}],"notes":"業績 refers above all to the business performance of a company: 業績が上がる/伸びる/悪化する, 業績不振 \"poor business performance\". It also refers to the lasting accomplishments of a person, especially in science: 業績を残す, 研究業績. Unlike 実績 (verifiable track record), the emphasis here is on the overall result or life's work.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"実態を把握します。","romaji":"Jittai o haaku shimasu.","german":"I will get a grip on the actual state of affairs.","furigana":"じったい|はあく"},{"japanese":"調査で、長時間労働の実態が明らかになりました。","romaji":"Chousa de, choujikan roudou no jittai ga akiraka ni narimashita.","german":"The survey revealed the reality of long working hours.","furigana":"ちょうさ|ちょうじかんろうどう|じったい|あき"},{"japanese":"広告と違って、その会社の実態はひどいものでした。","romaji":"Koukoku to chigatte, sono kaisha no jittai wa hidoi mono deshita.","german":"Unlike the advertisement, the reality of that company was terrible.","furigana":"こうこく|ちが|かいしゃ|じったい"}],"notes":"実態 is the actual state of affairs, often in contrast to appearance, advertising or official statements: 実態を調査する/把握する, 〜の実態が明らかになる. Typical of news and reports. Not to be confused with the identically pronounced 実体 \"substance, essence\"; 現状 is simply \"the current situation\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"現実を受け入れましょう。","romaji":"Genjitsu o ukeiremashō.","german":"Let's accept reality.","furigana":"げんじつ|う|い"},{"japanese":"夢と現実は違うと、社会人になってわかりました。","romaji":"Yume to genjitsu wa chigau to, shakaijin ni natte wakarimashita.","german":"I realized once I became a working adult that dreams and reality are different.","furigana":"ゆめ|げんじつ|ちが|しゃかいじん"},{"japanese":"その計画は面白いけど、あまり現実的じゃないね。","romaji":"Sono keikaku wa omoshiroi kedo, amari genjitsuteki ja nai ne.","german":"That plan is interesting, but it isn't very realistic.","furigana":"けいかく|おもしろ|げんじつてき"}],"notes":"現実 is \"reality\", often in contrast to 夢 or 理想: 夢と現実. Typical combinations are 現実を受け入れる, 現実を見る and 現実から逃げる; escapism is 現実逃避. The adjective 現実的な means \"realistic\", 非現実的な \"unrealistic\". To be distinguished are 事実 (\"a fact\") and the adverb 実際に (\"actually\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"事実を確認してください。","romaji":"Jijitsu o kakunin shite kudasai.","german":"Please confirm the facts.","furigana":"じじつ|かくにん"},{"japanese":"この映画は事実に基づいた話です。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa jijitsu ni motozuita hanashi desu.","german":"This movie is a story based on fact.","furigana":"えいが|じじつ|もと|はなし"},{"japanese":"彼は自分が嘘をついたという事実を認めました。","romaji":"Kare wa jibun ga uso o tsuita to iu jijitsu o mitomemashita.","german":"He admitted the fact that he had lied.","furigana":"かれ|じぶん|うそ|じじつ|みと"}],"notes":"事実 is a fact: 事実を確認する/認める, 事実に基づく, 〜という事実. 事実上 means \"in fact, de facto\". At the start of a sentence, 事実、… can also be adverbial, meaning \"in fact\". 真実 \"truth\" sounds more emotional and is often opposed to lies, whereas 事実 is sober and objective.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"根拠を示してください。","romaji":"Konkyo o shimeshite kudasai.","german":"Please show your grounds.","furigana":"こんきょ|しめ"},{"japanese":"それは根拠のないうわさです。","romaji":"Sore wa konkyo no nai uwasa desu.","german":"That is a groundless rumor.","furigana":"こんきょ"},{"japanese":"何を根拠にそう言っているんですか。","romaji":"Nani o konkyo ni sou itte iru n desu ka.","german":"What are you basing that on?","furigana":"なに|こんきょ|い"}],"notes":"根拠 is the basis on which a claim or opinion rests: 根拠がある/ない, 根拠を示す, 〜を根拠に, 根拠のない噂 \"groundless rumor\". 証拠, by contrast, proves a fact (for example a crime), and 理由 is simply \"reason\". Typical in discussions and argumentation.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"証拠がありません。","romaji":"Shōko ga arimasen.","german":"There is no evidence.","furigana":"しょうこ"},{"japanese":"警察は現場で証拠を集めています。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa genba de shouko o atsumete imasu.","german":"The police are collecting evidence at the scene.","furigana":"けいさつ|げんば|しょうこ|あつ"},{"japanese":"よく笑うのは、元気な証拠ですよ。","romaji":"Yoku warau no wa, genki na shouko desu yo.","german":"Laughing a lot is proof that you're doing well.","furigana":"わら|げんき|しょうこ"}],"notes":"証拠 is proof, especially in the legal sense: 証拠がある/ない, 証拠を集める/残す/隠す, 動かぬ証拠 \"irrefutable evidence\". In everyday use, 〜のは…証拠だ means \"is a sign that ...\". Distinction: 根拠 is the justification of an opinion, 証拠 is what proves a fact.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"資料を準備してください。","romaji":"Shiryō o junbi shite kudasai.","german":"Please prepare the materials.","furigana":"しりょう|じゅんび"},{"japanese":"会議の前に資料を配ります。","romaji":"Kaigi no mae ni shiryou o kubarimasu.","german":"I'll hand out the materials before the meeting.","furigana":"かいぎ|まえ|しりょう|くば"},{"japanese":"卒業論文のために図書館で資料を集めました。","romaji":"Sotsugyou ronbun no tame ni toshokan de shiryou o atsumemashita.","german":"I gathered materials at the library for my graduation thesis.","furigana":"そつぎょうろんぶん|としょかん|しりょう|あつ"}],"notes":"資料 are documents or materials collected for a purpose: handouts in meetings (会議の資料), sources for research, informational material. Typical verbs: 資料を作る/配る/集める/準備する. Distinctions: 書類 are forms and official papers to fill out or submit, 材料 are raw materials or ingredients, and データ is raw data.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 15"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"データを分析します。","romaji":"Dēta o bunseki shimasu.","german":"I analyze the data.","furigana":"ぶんせき"},{"japanese":"パソコンが壊れて、データが全部消えてしまいました。","romaji":"Pasokon ga kowarete, deeta ga zenbu kiete shimaimashita.","german":"My computer broke and all the data disappeared.","furigana":"こわ|ぜんぶき"},{"japanese":"写真のデータをメールで送ってもらえますか。","romaji":"Shashin no deeta o meeru de okutte moraemasu ka.","german":"Could you send me the photo data by email?","furigana":"しゃしん|おく"}],"notes":"データ (from English) means data and, in everyday use, also digital files: データを集める/分析する/保存する, データが消える, 写真のデータ. With mobile phones, データ often means the data volume (データ通信, データ量). As usual in Japanese, no plural is marked; singular and plural follow from the context.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 42"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"正確な情報が必要です。","romaji":"Seikaku na jōhō ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"Accurate information is necessary.","furigana":"せいかく|じょうほう|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"インターネットで旅行の情報を調べました。","romaji":"Intaanetto de ryokou no jouhou o shirabemashita.","german":"I looked up travel information on the Internet.","furigana":"りょこう|じょうほう|しら"},{"japanese":"個人情報の取り扱いには注意してください。","romaji":"Kojin jouhou no toriatsukai ni wa chuui shite kudasai.","german":"Please be careful in handling personal information.","furigana":"こじんじょうほう|と|あつか|ちゅうい"}],"notes":"情報 is \"information\" as an uncountable noun: 情報を集める/得る/調べる, 情報が入る, 最新情報, 個人情報 \"personal data\", 情報交換. For a concrete notice or announcement, お知らせ is rather said, and for news on TV ニュース. As a school subject, 情報 means \"computer science\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 41"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"目的を達成する手段を考えます。","romaji":"Mokuteki o tassei suru shudan o kangaemasu.","german":"I will think about ways to achieve the goal.","furigana":"もくてき|たっせい|しゅだん|かんが"},{"japanese":"田舎では車が主な交通手段です。","romaji":"Inaka de wa kuruma ga omo na koutsuu shudan desu.","german":"In the countryside, cars are the main means of transportation.","furigana":"いなか|くるま|おも|こうつうしゅだん"},{"japanese":"彼は勝つためなら手段を選びません。","romaji":"Kare wa katsu tame nara shudan o erabimasen.","german":"If it is to win, he will stop at nothing.","furigana":"かれ|か|しゅだん|えら"}],"notes":"手段 is the means by which one reaches a goal: 〜する手段, 交通手段 \"means of transport\", 連絡手段, 最後の手段 \"last resort\". The phrase 手段を選ばない means \"to stop at nothing\". Distinction: 方法 is the way or procedure of doing something; 手段 emphasizes the tool or route to an end.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい手法を導入します。","romaji":"Atarashii shuhō o dōnyū shimasu.","german":"We will introduce a new technique.","furigana":"あたら|しゅほう|どうにゅう"},{"japanese":"この監督は独特な手法で映画を撮ります。","romaji":"Kono kantoku wa dokutoku na shuhou de eiga o torimasu.","german":"This director makes films with a very distinctive technique.","furigana":"かんとく|どくとく|しゅほう|えいが|と"},{"japanese":"最近は、ネットを使った販売手法が増えています。","romaji":"Saikin wa, netto o tsukatta hanbai shuhou ga fuete imasu.","german":"Recently there are more and more sales methods using the internet.","furigana":"さいきん|つか|はんばいしゅほう|ふ"}],"notes":"手法 is a method or technique, especially in work, research, art and business: 新しい手法, 研究手法, 販売手法, 独特な手法. It sounds more technical than the general 方法. 手段 is the means to an end, 技法 the craft-and-artistic technique. Typical verbs: 手法を使う/取り入れる/導入する.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"絵画の技法を学びます。","romaji":"Kaiga no gihō o manabimasu.","german":"I will learn painting techniques.","furigana":"かいが|ぎほう|まな"},{"japanese":"この器は昔ながらの技法で作られています。","romaji":"Kono utsuwa wa mukashinagara no gihou de tsukurarete imasu.","german":"This vessel is made with traditional techniques.","furigana":"うつわ|むかし|ぎほう|つく"},{"japanese":"小説を書く技法についての本を読んでいます。","romaji":"Shousetsu o kaku gihou ni tsuite no hon o yonde imasu.","german":"I am reading a book about techniques for writing novels.","furigana":"しょうせつ|か|ぎほう|ほん|よ"}],"notes":"技法 is a craft or artistic technique: 絵画の技法, 写真の技法, 伝統的な技法, 〜の技法を学ぶ. It is narrower than 手法 (a more general method, also in business and research) and more concrete than 技術 (\"technique, technology, skill\"). Rather written and technical.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"専門分野は何ですか。","romaji":"Senmon bunya wa nan desu ka.","german":"What is your field of specialization?","furigana":"せんもんぶんや|なん"},{"japanese":"彼女は医療の分野で働いています。","romaji":"Kanojo wa iryou no bun'ya de hataraite imasu.","german":"She works in the medical field.","furigana":"かのじょ|いりょう|ぶんや|はたら"},{"japanese":"数学は私の得意な分野ではありません。","romaji":"Suugaku wa watashi no tokui na bun'ya de wa arimasen.","german":"Mathematics is not my strong area.","furigana":"すうがく|わたし|とくい|ぶんや"}],"notes":"分野 is a field of expertise or activity: 専門分野, 得意分野 \"strength, specialty\", 〜の分野で働く/活躍する, IT分野. It is the everyday word; 領域 sounds more formal and academic. 範囲, by contrast, means \"scope, range\" (for example 試験の範囲).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい領域に挑戦します。","romaji":"Atarashii ryōiki ni chōsen shimasu.","german":"I will take on a challenge in a new field.","furigana":"あたら|りょういき|ちょうせん"},{"japanese":"この研究は、医学と工学の両方の領域にまたがっています。","romaji":"Kono kenkyuu wa, igaku to kougaku no ryouhou no ryouiki ni matagatte imasu.","german":"This research spans both the fields of medicine and engineering.","furigana":"けんきゅう|いがく|こうがく|りょうほう|りょういき"},{"japanese":"他国の領域に無断で入ることは許されません。","romaji":"Takoku no ryouiki ni mudan de hairu koto wa yurusaremasen.","german":"Entering another country's territory without permission is not allowed.","furigana":"たこく|りょういき|むだん|はい|ゆる"}],"notes":"領域 means on the one hand a field of expertise or research (研究領域, 新しい領域に挑戦する), on the other the sovereign territory of a state (land, sea and airspace together). It sounds more formal and academic than 分野. Common phrases: 〜の領域に入る/踏み込む \"to venture into the realm of ...\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"異なる視点から考えましょう。","romaji":"Kotonaru shiten kara kangaemashō.","german":"Let's think from a different perspective.","furigana":"こと|してん|かんが"},{"japanese":"視点を変えると、新しいアイデアが生まれます。","romaji":"Shiten o kaeru to, atarashii aidea ga umaremasu.","german":"If you change your perspective, new ideas are born.","furigana":"してん|か|あたら|う"},{"japanese":"この小説は犬の視点で書かれています。","romaji":"Kono shousetsu wa inu no shiten de kakarete imasu.","german":"This novel is written from a dog's perspective.","furigana":"しょうせつ|いぬ|してん|か"}],"notes":"視点 is the standpoint from which one views something: 〜の視点から, 視点を変える, 新しい視点, 子どもの視点. In narratives it also means \"narrative perspective\". Distinction: 観点 is more the viewpoint or criterion of an evaluation (経済の観点から); 立場 is one's own position or situation.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"経済の観点から見ると重要です。","romaji":"Keizai no kanten kara miru to jūyō desu.","german":"From an economic point of view, it is important.","furigana":"けいざい|かんてん|み|じゅうよう"},{"japanese":"安全の観点から、この道は夜通行止めになっています。","romaji":"Anzen no kanten kara, kono michi wa yoru tsuukoudome ni natte imasu.","german":"From the standpoint of safety, this road is closed to traffic at night.","furigana":"あんぜん|かんてん|みち|よるつうこうど"},{"japanese":"先生は、いくつかの観点から作文を評価します。","romaji":"Sensei wa, ikutsu ka no kanten kara sakubun o hyouka shimasu.","german":"The teacher evaluates essays from several viewpoints.","furigana":"せんせい|かんてん|さくぶん|ひょうか"}],"notes":"観点 is the viewpoint from which one judges something; almost always in the form 〜の観点から(見ると/考えると): 安全の観点から, 環境の観点から. It sounds more formal and analytical than 視点, which describes more one's own angle of view. Typical of reports, justifications and official statements.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この概念を理解しましょう。","romaji":"Kono gainen o rikai shimashō.","german":"Let's understand this concept.","furigana":"がいねん|りかい"},{"japanese":"昔の日本には「週末」という概念がありませんでした。","romaji":"Mukashi no Nihon ni wa \"shuumatsu\" to iu gainen ga arimasen deshita.","german":"In old Japan there was no concept of a \"weekend\".","furigana":"むかし|にほん|しゅうまつ|がいねん"},{"japanese":"幸せの概念は人によって違います。","romaji":"Shiawase no gainen wa hito ni yotte chigaimasu.","german":"The notion of happiness differs from person to person.","furigana":"しあわ|がいねん|ひと|ちが"}],"notes":"概念 is an abstract concept or idea: 〜という概念, 概念がない, 新しい概念, 時間の概念. It is a rather academic word; in everyday speech one often says 考え方 \"way of thinking\". Typical combinations: 概念を理解する, 概念が広がる, 〜の概念を変える.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この言葉の定義は何ですか。","romaji":"Kono kotoba no teigi wa nan desu ka.","german":"What is the definition of this word?","furigana":"ことば|ていぎ|なん"},{"japanese":"この調査では、六十五歳以上を高齢者と定義しています。","romaji":"Kono chousa de wa, rokujuugosai ijou o koureisha to teigi shite imasu.","german":"This survey defines people aged 65 and over as the elderly.","furigana":"ちょうさ|ろくじゅうごさいいじょう|こうれいしゃ|ていぎ"},{"japanese":"「成功」の定義は人それぞれです。","romaji":"\"Seikou\" no teigi wa hito sorezore desu.","german":"The definition of \"success\" differs for everyone.","furigana":"せいこう|ていぎ|ひと"}],"notes":"定義 is a definition: 〜の定義, 定義がはっきりしない/あいまいだ. As a transitive する-verb the pattern is AをBと定義する \"to define A as B\". Typical in science, statistics and discussions about what exactly a word means; in everyday speech people often ask more simply 〜ってどういう意味？","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"それを前提に話しましょう。","romaji":"Sore o zentei ni hanashimashō.","german":"Let's talk on that premise.","furigana":"ぜんてい|はな"},{"japanese":"結婚を前提にお付き合いしています。","romaji":"Kekkon o zentei ni otsukiai shite imasu.","german":"We are going out together with a view to marriage.","furigana":"けっこん|ぜんてい|つ|あ"},{"japanese":"この仕事は、英語が話せることが前提です。","romaji":"Kono shigoto wa, eigo ga hanaseru koto ga zentei desu.","german":"For this job, it is a prerequisite that you can speak English.","furigana":"しごと|えいご|はな|ぜんてい"}],"notes":"前提 is a prerequisite or starting point that is tacitly taken for granted: 〜を前提に(して)/〜を前提として, 〜が前提だ, 大前提 \"basic prerequisite\". Well known is 結婚を前提に付き合う \"to date with the intention of marrying\". Distinction: 条件 are negotiated conditions, whereas 前提 is presupposed.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"矛盾があります。","romaji":"Mujun ga arimasu.","german":"There is a contradiction.","furigana":"むじゅん"},{"japanese":"彼の話は前に言ったことと矛盾しています。","romaji":"Kare no hanashi wa mae ni itta koto to mujun shite imasu.","german":"What he says contradicts what he said before.","furigana":"かれ|はなし|まえ|い|むじゅん"},{"japanese":"「痩せたい」と言いながらケーキを食べるのは矛盾していますよ。","romaji":"\"Yasetai\" to iinagara keeki o taberu no wa mujun shite imasu yo.","german":"Saying \"I want to lose weight\" while eating cake is contradictory, you know.","furigana":"や|い|た|むじゅん"}],"notes":"矛盾 is a contradiction; literally \"spear and shield\", from the Chinese story of a merchant who boasted of a spear that nothing could withstand and a shield that nothing could pierce at the same time. Common are 矛盾がある and the intransitive する-verb: AがBと矛盾する \"A contradicts B\", 矛盾した態度 \"contradictory behavior\". It is not used for a person contradicting someone: that is 反論する or 言い返す.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仮説を立てて検証します。","romaji":"Kasetsu o tatete kenshō shimasu.","german":"I will form a hypothesis and test it.","furigana":"かせつ|た|けんしょう"},{"japanese":"その仮説は実験で正しいことが証明されました。","romaji":"Sono kasetsu wa jikken de tadashii koto ga shoumei saremashita.","german":"That hypothesis was proven correct by experiment.","furigana":"かせつ|じっけん|ただ|しょうめい"},{"japanese":"これはまだ仮説にすぎません。","romaji":"Kore wa mada kasetsu ni sugimasen.","german":"This is still nothing more than a hypothesis.","furigana":"かせつ"}],"notes":"仮説 is a scientific hypothesis: 仮説を立てる \"to form a hypothesis\", 仮説を検証する \"to test\", 仮説が正しい/証明される. Distinction: 仮定 is a mere assumption in a line of thought (\"assuming that ...\"), 推測 a conjecture. 仮説 belongs to research, business and analysis.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"理論と実践は違います。","romaji":"Riron to jissen wa chigaimasu.","german":"Theory and practice are different.","furigana":"りろん|じっせん|ちが"},{"japanese":"理論上は可能ですが、実際には難しいです。","romaji":"Rironjou wa kanou desu ga, jissai ni wa muzukashii desu.","german":"It is possible in theory, but difficult in reality.","furigana":"りろんじょう|かのう|じっさい|むずか"},{"japanese":"アインシュタインの理論を分かりやすく説明した本です。","romaji":"Ainshutain no riron o wakariyasuku setsumei shita hon desu.","german":"This is a book that explains Einstein's theory in an easy-to-understand way.","furigana":"りろん|わ|せつめい|ほん"}],"notes":"理論 is a theory: 理論を立てる, 〜の理論, 理論的な. Often in contrast to practice: 理論と実践, 理論上は \"theoretically\". 理屈, by contrast, means \"logic, reasoning\" and often sounds derogatory (理屈を言う \"to quibble\"), and 説 is a doctrine or hypothesis.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"基本原理を説明します。","romaji":"Kihon genri o setsumei shimasu.","german":"I will explain the basic principles.","furigana":"きほんげんり|せつめい"},{"japanese":"飛行機が飛ぶ原理を知っていますか。","romaji":"Hikouki ga tobu genri o shitte imasu ka.","german":"Do you know the principle by which airplanes fly?","furigana":"ひこうき|と|げんり|し"},{"japanese":"価格は市場原理によって決まります。","romaji":"Kakaku wa shijou genri ni yotte kimarimasu.","german":"Prices are determined by market forces.","furigana":"かかく|しじょうげんり|き"}],"notes":"原理 is the fundamental principle by which something works: 〜の原理, 基本原理, 〜が動く原理, 市場原理 \"market mechanism\". Distinction: 原則 is a rule one adheres to (原則として), 法則 is an observed regularity (物理の法則). So 原理 explains the \"why\" of a mechanism.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"物理の法則を学びます。","romaji":"Butsuri no hōsoku o manabimasu.","german":"I study the laws of physics.","furigana":"ぶつり|ほうそく|まな"},{"japanese":"ニュートンは万有引力の法則を発見しました。","romaji":"Nyuuton wa ban'yuu inryoku no housoku o hakken shimashita.","german":"Newton discovered the law of universal gravitation.","furigana":"ばんゆういんりょく|ほうそく|はっけん"},{"japanese":"人気の店には、ある法則があるそうです。","romaji":"Ninki no mise ni wa, aru housoku ga aru sou desu.","german":"Apparently, popular shops follow a certain pattern.","furigana":"にんき|みせ|ほうそく"}],"notes":"法則 is a regularity, above all in nature and science: 自然の法則, 物理の法則, 万有引力の法則. Used loosely, it also refers to recurring patterns in everyday life (成功の法則). Do not confuse it with 法律 \"law (legal)\" and 規則 \"regulation\"; 原則 is a rule one adheres to, whereas 法則 is one that simply holds.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"原則として賛成です。","romaji":"Gensoku toshite sansei desu.","german":"In principle, I agree.","furigana":"げんそく|さんせい"},{"japanese":"当店では、原則として返品はできません。","romaji":"Touten de wa, gensoku toshite henpin wa dekimasen.","german":"In principle, our store does not accept returns.","furigana":"とうてん|げんそく|へんぴん"},{"japanese":"「一人一票」は選挙の基本的な原則です。","romaji":"\"Hitori ippyou\" wa senkyo no kihonteki na gensoku desu.","german":"\"One person, one vote\" is a basic principle of elections.","furigana":"ひとりいっぴょう|せんきょ|きほんてき|げんそく"}],"notes":"原則 is a principle or general rule. Very common is 原則として or 原則的に \"in principle, as a rule\", which hints at exceptions; in notices also simply 原則、… at the start of a sentence. Other combinations: 原則を守る, 基本原則. Distinction: 原理 explains how something works, 原則 lays down how one should act.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人口の推移を見ます。","romaji":"Jinkō no suii o mimasu.","german":"I will look at the population trend.","furigana":"じんこう|すいい|み"},{"japanese":"今年の売り上げは順調に推移しています。","romaji":"Kotoshi no uriage wa junchou ni suii shite imasu.","german":"This year's sales are progressing smoothly.","furigana":"ことし|う|あ|じゅんちょう|すいい"},{"japanese":"グラフで気温の推移を確認してください。","romaji":"Gurafu de kion no suii o kakunin shite kudasai.","german":"Please check the temperature trend on the graph.","furigana":"きおん|すいい|かくにん"}],"notes":"推移 is the development or change of something over time, typically for figures and statistics: 人口の推移, 売り上げの推移. As an intransitive する-verb: 順調に推移する, 高い水準で推移する. It is formal and belongs in reports and the news; the more general word is 変化, and for the course of a process, 経過.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"市場の動向を調べます。","romaji":"Shijō no dōkō o shirabemasu.","german":"I am looking into market trends.","furigana":"しじょう|どうこう|しら"},{"japanese":"台風の動向に注意してください。","romaji":"Taifuu no doukou ni chuui shite kudasai.","german":"Please keep a close eye on the typhoon's movements.","furigana":"たいふう|どうこう|ちゅうい"},{"japanese":"各社は若者の消費動向に注目しています。","romaji":"Kakusha wa wakamono no shouhi doukou ni chuumoku shite imasu.","german":"Each company is paying attention to young people's spending trends.","furigana":"かくしゃ|わかもの|しょうひどうこう|ちゅうもく"}],"notes":"動向 are the movements and developments of a group, a market or a situation: 市場の動向, 世論の動向, 台風の動向, 消費動向. Typical verbs: 動向を調べる/見守る, 動向に注目する/注意する. Formal, typical of news and business. 傾向, by contrast, is a lasting inclination or tendency.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"会社の方針に従います。","romaji":"Kaisha no hōshin ni shitagaimasu.","german":"I will follow the company's policy.","furigana":"かいしゃ|ほうしん|したが"},{"japanese":"うちの教育方針は、子どもの好きなことをさせることです。","romaji":"Uchi no kyouiku houshin wa, kodomo no suki na koto o saseru koto desu.","german":"Our educational policy is to let children do what they like.","furigana":"きょういくほうしん|こ|す"},{"japanese":"会議で来年の方針が決まりました。","romaji":"Kaigi de rainen no houshin ga kimarimashita.","german":"Next year's course of action was decided at the meeting.","furigana":"かいぎ|らいねん|ほうしん|き"}],"notes":"方針 is the set course or guideline by which an organization or person acts: 方針を決める/立てる/変える, 会社の方針, 教育方針, 〜という方針だ. Note: the English word \"policy\" is a common translation, but 方針 means \"course of action, guiding principle\"; government policy is 政策.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい戦略を考えます。","romaji":"Atarashii senryaku o kangaemasu.","german":"I will think of a new strategy.","furigana":"あたら|せんりゃく|かんが"},{"japanese":"試合に勝つために、チームで戦略を立てました。","romaji":"Shiai ni katsu tame ni, chiimu de senryaku o tatemashita.","german":"To win the match, the team made a strategy.","furigana":"しあい|か|せんりゃく|た"},{"japanese":"この会社は海外市場を重視する戦略をとっています。","romaji":"Kono kaisha wa kaigai shijou o juushi suru senryaku o totte imasu.","german":"This company is pursuing a strategy that emphasizes overseas markets.","furigana":"かいしゃ|かいがいしじょう|じゅうし|せんりゃく"}],"notes":"戦略 is a long-term strategy, originally military, today mainly in business, politics and sports: 戦略を立てる/練る, 経営戦略, 販売戦略, 戦略的な. Distinction: 戦術 is the concrete tactic in an individual case, 作戦 the plan for a particular operation (also used casually in daily life).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"多くの課題があります。","romaji":"Ōku no kadai ga arimasu.","german":"There are many challenges.","furigana":"おお|かだい"},{"japanese":"夏休みの課題を早く終わらせたいです。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi no kadai o hayaku owarasetai desu.","german":"I want to finish my summer vacation assignment early.","furigana":"なつやす|かだい|はや|お"},{"japanese":"発音が今後の課題です。","romaji":"Hatsuon ga kongo no kadai desu.","german":"Pronunciation is my challenge for the future.","furigana":"はつおん|こんご|かだい"}],"notes":"課題 is a task to be solved or a challenge: 課題が多い/残る, 課題を解決する, 今後の課題 \"what one must work on in the future\". In school and university it also means \"homework, assignment\" (夏休みの課題, レポートの課題). Compared with 問題, 課題 sounds more constructive; 宿題 is homework in the narrower school sense.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"調査の対象は学生です。","romaji":"Chōsa no taishō wa gakusei desu.","german":"The subjects of the survey are students.","furigana":"ちょうさ|たいしょう|がくせい"},{"japanese":"このイベントは小学生を対象にしています。","romaji":"Kono ibento wa shougakusei o taishou ni shite imasu.","german":"This event is aimed at elementary school children.","furigana":"しょうがくせい|たいしょう"},{"japanese":"この商品はセールの対象外です。","romaji":"Kono shouhin wa seeru no taishougai desu.","german":"This product is excluded from the sale.","furigana":"しょうひん|たいしょうがい"}],"notes":"対象 is the target or object at which something is directed: 調査の対象, 〜を対象に(した) \"for, aimed at\", 〜の対象になる, 対象外 \"excluded\". Very common in notices and offers (小学生を対象にしたイベント). Do not confuse it with the identically read 対照 \"contrast\" and 対称 \"symmetry\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"天気の予測は難しいです。","romaji":"Tenki no yosoku wa muzukashii desu.","german":"Weather forecasting is difficult.","furigana":"てんき|よそく|むずか"},{"japanese":"専門家は、来年も物価が上がると予測しています。","romaji":"Senmonka wa, rainen mo bukka ga agaru to yosoku shite imasu.","german":"Experts predict that prices will rise next year as well.","furigana":"せんもんか|らいねん|ぶっか|あ|よそく"},{"japanese":"子どもが何をするかは予測がつきません。","romaji":"Kodomo ga nani o suru ka wa yosoku ga tsukimasen.","german":"There's no predicting what children will do.","furigana":"こ|なに|よそく"}],"notes":"予測 is a prediction based on data or analysis: 〜と予測する (transitive する-verb), 予測が当たる/外れる, 予測がつかない \"unpredictable\". Distinction: 予想 is an everyday expectation or guess, 予報 specifically a weather forecast (天気予報). 予測 sounds more objective and scientific.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"最悪の場合を想定します。","romaji":"Saiaku no baai o sōtei shimasu.","german":"I assume the worst case.","furigana":"さいあく|ばあい|そうてい"},{"japanese":"地震を想定した避難訓練が行われました。","romaji":"Jishin o soutei shita hinan kunren ga okonawaremashita.","german":"An evacuation drill assuming an earthquake was held.","furigana":"じしん|そうてい|ひなんくんれん|おこな"},{"japanese":"こんなに人が来るとは、想定外でした。","romaji":"Konna ni hito ga kuru to wa, souteigai deshita.","german":"I hadn't anticipated that this many people would come.","furigana":"ひと|く|そうていがい"}],"notes":"想定 is the mental playing through of a possible case: 〜を想定する, 最悪の場合を想定する, 〜を想定した訓練. Very common are 想定外 \"unforeseen, not planned for\" and 想定内 \"within expectations\". Distinction: 予想 is an expectation of what will probably happen; 想定 is the assumption of a scenario one prepares for.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"経済の見通しは明るいです。","romaji":"Keizai no mitōshi wa akarui desu.","german":"The economic outlook is bright.","furigana":"けいざい|みとお|あか"},{"japanese":"このカーブは見通しが悪いので、気をつけてください。","romaji":"Kono kaabu wa mitooshi ga warui node, ki o tsukete kudasai.","german":"This curve has poor visibility, so please be careful.","furigana":"みとお|わる|き"},{"japanese":"新しい駅は来年完成する見通しです。","romaji":"Atarashii eki wa rainen kansei suru mitooshi desu.","german":"The new station is expected to be completed next year.","furigana":"あたら|えき|らいねんかんせい|みとお"}],"notes":"見通し (from 見通す \"to see through, to survey\") has three meanings: clear visibility (見通しがいい/悪い道), outlook or forecast (〜の見通しが立つ, 〜する見通しだ \"is expected to\", typical in news) and foresight (見通しが甘い \"to be too optimistic in one's assessment\"). Compared with 予測, 見通し sounds less technical.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"需要が増えています。","romaji":"Juyō ga fuete imasu.","german":"Demand is increasing.","furigana":"じゅよう|ふ"},{"japanese":"夏はエアコンの需要が高くなります。","romaji":"Natsu wa eakon no juyou ga takaku narimasu.","german":"In summer, demand for air conditioners rises.","furigana":"なつ|じゅよう|たか"},{"japanese":"価格は需要と供給で決まります。","romaji":"Kakaku wa juyou to kyoukyuu de kimarimasu.","german":"Prices are determined by supply and demand.","furigana":"かかく|じゅよう|きょうきゅう|き"}],"notes":"需要 is (economic) demand and usually appears as the counterpart of 供給 \"supply\": 需要と供給. Typical are 需要が高い/多い, 需要が増える/減る and 〜の需要がある \"there is demand for ...\". For personal needs in daily life, people rather say 必要 or ニーズ; 需要 sounds like markets and economics. Do not confuse it with the identically read 重要 \"important\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"供給が足りません。","romaji":"Kyōkyū ga tarimasen.","german":"The supply is insufficient.","furigana":"きょうきゅう|た"},{"japanese":"この発電所は町全体に電気を供給しています。","romaji":"Kono hatsudensho wa machi zentai ni denki o kyoukyuu shite imasu.","german":"This power plant supplies electricity to the whole town.","furigana":"はつでんしょ|まちぜんたい|でんき|きょうきゅう"},{"japanese":"地震の後、水の供給が止まりました。","romaji":"Jishin no ato, mizu no kyoukyuu ga tomarimashita.","german":"After the earthquake, the water supply stopped.","furigana":"じしん|あと|みず|きょうきゅう|と"}],"notes":"供給 is supply in the economic sense, or provision and delivery; as a transitive する-verb: AにBを供給する \"to supply A with B\" (町に電気を供給する). The counterpart is 需要 \"demand\". Typical: 供給が足りない, 供給が止まる, 安定した供給. A special offer in a shop, however, is not 供給 but セール or 特売.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"天然資源を大切にしましょう。","romaji":"Tennen shigen o taisetsu ni shimashō.","german":"Let's value natural resources.","furigana":"てんねんしげん|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"日本は資源が少ない国です。","romaji":"Nihon wa shigen ga sukunai kuni desu.","german":"Japan is a country with few resources.","furigana":"にほん|しげん|すく|くに"},{"japanese":"資源ごみは水曜日に出してください。","romaji":"Shigen gomi wa suiyoubi ni dashite kudasai.","german":"Please put out recyclable waste on Wednesday.","furigana":"しげん|すいようび|だ"}],"notes":"資源 refers to raw materials and resources: 天然資源, 地下資源, エネルギー資源. Typical are 資源が豊か/少ない, 資源を大切にする and 資源を無駄にする. In daily life the word is met in waste separation: 資源ごみ is recyclable waste such as bottles, cans and paper. Figuratively there is also 人的資源 \"human resources\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"エネルギー消費を減らします。","romaji":"Enerugī shōhi o herashimasu.","german":"I will reduce energy consumption.","furigana":"しょうひ|へ"},{"japanese":"この冷蔵庫は電気をあまり消費しません。","romaji":"Kono reizouko wa denki o amari shouhi shimasen.","german":"This refrigerator doesn't use much electricity.","furigana":"れいぞうこ|でんき|しょうひ"},{"japanese":"消費期限が昨日までの牛乳を捨てました。","romaji":"Shouhi kigen ga kinou made no gyuunyuu o sutemashita.","german":"I threw away the milk whose use-by date was yesterday.","furigana":"しょうひきげん|きのう|ぎゅうにゅう|す"}],"notes":"消費 is consumption or use; as a transitive する-verb: 電気を消費する \"to consume electricity\". Fixed words are 消費者 \"consumer\", 消費税 \"consumption tax\" and 消費期限 \"use-by date\". The counterpart is 生産 \"production\". For \"spending money\" in daily life, people rather say お金を使う; 消費 sounds objective and economic.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"海外に投資します。","romaji":"Kaigai ni tōshi shimasu.","german":"I invest overseas.","furigana":"かいがい|とうし"},{"japanese":"兄はボーナスの一部を株に投資しました。","romaji":"Ani wa boonasu no ichibu o kabu ni toushi shimashita.","german":"My brother invested part of his bonus in stocks.","furigana":"あに|いちぶ|かぶ|とうし"},{"japanese":"英語の勉強は自分への投資だと思っています。","romaji":"Eigo no benkyou wa jibun e no toushi da to omotte imasu.","german":"I think of studying English as an investment in myself.","furigana":"えいご|べんきょう|じぶん|とうし|おも"}],"notes":"投資 is an investment, and as a する-verb \"to invest\". What is invested in takes に (株に投資する, 海外に投資する), the amount or capital takes を. Figuratively it is also used for time and education: 自分への投資 \"investment in oneself\". Related is 投資家 \"investor\"; saving in a bank account, by contrast, is 貯金.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"収益が上がりました。","romaji":"Shūeki ga agarimashita.","german":"Earnings have increased.","furigana":"しゅうえき|あ"},{"japanese":"イベントの収益は全部寄付します。","romaji":"Ibento no shuueki wa zenbu kifu shimasu.","german":"We will donate all the proceeds of the event.","furigana":"しゅうえき|ぜんぶきふ"},{"japanese":"新しいサービスはまだ収益が出ていません。","romaji":"Atarashii saabisu wa mada shuueki ga dete imasen.","german":"The new service is not yet generating revenue.","furigana":"あたら|しゅうえき|で"}],"notes":"収益 are the earnings or revenue from a business activity, so it is a word of business and corporate language. Typical: 収益が上がる/増える, 収益を上げる, 収益が出る \"to turn a profit\". Distinction: 収入 is a person's income, 利益 the profit after deducting costs; 収益 is often used similarly to 利益 but sounds like a company or project.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"予算を超えてしまいました。","romaji":"Yosan o koete shimaimashita.","german":"I went over budget.","furigana":"よさん|こ"},{"japanese":"ご予算はどのくらいですか。","romaji":"Go-yosan wa dono kurai desu ka.","german":"What is your budget?","furigana":"よさん"},{"japanese":"旅行の予算は一人五万円です。","romaji":"Ryokou no yosan wa hitori goman en desu.","german":"The travel budget is 50,000 yen per person.","furigana":"りょこう|よさん|ひとりごまんえん"}],"notes":"予算 is a budget, both the national budget (国の予算) and a private budget for shopping or travel. Typical: 予算を立てる \"to draw up a budget\", 予算を超える, 予算が足りない, 予算内で \"within the budget\". In shops or at real estate agencies, staff politely ask ご予算はどのくらいですか.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コスト削減が必要です。","romaji":"Kosuto sakugen ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"Cost reduction is necessary.","furigana":"さくげん|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"会社は残業時間を削減することにしました。","romaji":"Kaisha wa zangyou jikan o sakugen suru koto ni shimashita.","german":"The company decided to reduce overtime hours.","furigana":"かいしゃ|ざんぎょうじかん|さくげん"},{"japanese":"来年度は予算が一割削減されます。","romaji":"Rainendo wa yosan ga ichiwari sakugen saremasu.","german":"Next fiscal year, the budget will be cut by ten percent.","furigana":"らいねんど|よさん|いちわりさくげん"}],"notes":"削減 is the deliberate cutting of quantities and expenses; as a transitive する-verb: 経費を削減する. Typical objects are コスト, 経費, 予算, 人員 and 残業時間; often also passive: 予算が削減される. The word sounds official and businesslike - in everyday speech one says 減らす. For lowering prices 下げる fits, not 削減.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"所得格差が広がっています。","romaji":"Shotoku kakusa ga hirogatte imasu.","german":"Income inequality is widening.","furigana":"しょとくかくさ|ひろ"},{"japanese":"都会と田舎の格差が問題になっています。","romaji":"Tokai to inaka no kakusa ga mondai ni natte imasu.","german":"The gap between city and countryside has become a problem.","furigana":"とかい|いなか|かくさ|もんだい"},{"japanese":"男女の給料の格差をなくすべきです。","romaji":"Danjo no kyuuryou no kakusa o nakusu beki desu.","german":"The gap in pay between men and women should be eliminated.","furigana":"だんじょ|きゅうりょう|かくさ"}],"notes":"格差 is a gap or inequality between groups, usually with a social overtone: 所得格差, 地域格差, 男女の格差, 格差社会. Typical: 格差が広がる/縮まる, 格差をなくす. Neutral differences, for example in taste, are called 違い or 差; 格差 always implies a higher and a lower.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"景気が回復しています。","romaji":"Keiki ga kaifuku shite imasu.","german":"The economy is recovering.","furigana":"けいき|かいふく"},{"japanese":"最近、景気はどうですか。","romaji":"Saikin, keiki wa dou desu ka.","german":"How is business these days?","furigana":"さいきん|けいき"},{"japanese":"景気が悪くて、ボーナスが出ませんでした。","romaji":"Keiki ga warukute, boonasu ga demasen deshita.","german":"Business is bad, so we didn't get a bonus.","furigana":"けいき|わる|で"}],"notes":"景気 is the state of the economy or the business cycle: 景気がいい/悪い, 景気が回復する, 景気が悪くなる. Among businesspeople, 景気はどうですか is a stock phrase for \"How is business going?\". A slump is 不景気 or, more formally, 不況. In 景気よく or 景気づけ, the secondary meaning \"vigor, good spirits\" comes through.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不況が続いています。","romaji":"Fukyō ga tsuzuite imasu.","german":"The recession continues.","furigana":"ふきょう|つづ"},{"japanese":"不況の影響で、多くの店が閉まりました。","romaji":"Fukyou no eikyou de, ooku no mise ga shimarimashita.","german":"Because of the recession, many shops closed.","furigana":"ふきょう|えいきょう|おお|みせ|し"},{"japanese":"この仕事は不況に強いと言われています。","romaji":"Kono shigoto wa fukyou ni tsuyoi to iwarete imasu.","german":"This job is said to be resistant to recessions.","furigana":"しごと|ふきょう|つよ|い"}],"notes":"不況 is a recession, a prolonged economic slump. Typical: 不況が続く, 不況の影響で, 不況に強い \"recession-proof\". More colloquial is 不景気; the opposite is 好況 or 景気がいい. 不況 always refers to the economy as a whole or an industry, never to one's own financial situation - for that one says お金がない.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"失業率が上がっています。","romaji":"Shitsugyōritsu ga agatte imasu.","german":"The unemployment rate is rising.","furigana":"しつぎょうりつ|あ"},{"japanese":"会社がつぶれて、兄は失業しました。","romaji":"Kaisha ga tsuburete, ani wa shitsugyou shimashita.","german":"The company went under, and my older brother lost his job.","furigana":"かいしゃ|あに|しつぎょう"},{"japanese":"失業中は失業保険をもらっていました。","romaji":"Shitsugyouchuu wa shitsugyou hoken o moratte imashita.","german":"While I was unemployed, I received unemployment insurance.","furigana":"しつぎょうちゅう|しつぎょうほけん"}],"notes":"失業 is unemployment; as an intransitive する-verb, 失業する means \"to become unemployed\". Compounds: 失業者, 失業率, 失業保険 \"unemployment insurance\". Colloquially people also say 仕事がない or 無職; voluntarily quitting, on the other hand, is 仕事を辞める or 退職.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"雇用を増やす必要があります。","romaji":"Koyō o fuyasu hitsuyō ga arimasu.","german":"We need to increase employment.","furigana":"こよう|ふ|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"新しい工場は三百人の雇用を生みました。","romaji":"Atarashii koujou wa sanbyaku nin no koyou o umimashita.","german":"The new factory created 300 jobs.","furigana":"あたら|こうじょう|さんびゃくにん|こよう|う"},{"japanese":"この会社は外国人を多く雇用しています。","romaji":"Kono kaisha wa gaikokujin o ooku koyou shite imasu.","german":"This company employs many foreigners.","furigana":"かいしゃ|がいこくじん|おお|こよう"}],"notes":"雇用 is employment from the point of view of the employer or the labor market; as a transitive する-verb: 人を雇用する \"to employ someone\". In everyday speech 雇う is usually used instead. Typical: 雇用を生む/増やす/守る, 雇用契約, 正規雇用 and 非正規雇用. From the employee's point of view, starting a job is 就職する.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"福祉サービスが充実しています。","romaji":"Fukushi sābisu ga jūjitsu shite imasu.","german":"Welfare services are well developed.","furigana":"ふくし|じゅうじつ"},{"japanese":"姉は大学で福祉を勉強しています。","romaji":"Ane wa daigaku de fukushi o benkyou shite imasu.","german":"My older sister is studying social welfare at university.","furigana":"あね|だいがく|ふくし|べんきょう"},{"japanese":"この町は高齢者の福祉に力を入れています。","romaji":"Kono machi wa koureisha no fukushi ni chikara o irete imasu.","german":"This town focuses on the welfare of the elderly.","furigana":"まち|こうれいしゃ|ふくし|ちから|い"}],"notes":"福祉 is welfare or social services, above all as a field of state and social support: 社会福祉, 老人福祉, 福祉施設, 福祉の仕事. Typical: 福祉が充実している \"social services are well developed\", 福祉に力を入れる. It is a factual word from politics and administration; personal well-being is rather 幸せ or 健康.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"介護の仕事をしています。","romaji":"Kaigo no shigoto o shite imasu.","german":"I work in caregiving.","furigana":"かいご|しごと"},{"japanese":"父の介護のために、仕事を辞めました。","romaji":"Chichi no kaigo no tame ni, shigoto o yamemashita.","german":"I quit my job to take care of my father.","furigana":"ちち|かいご|しごと|や"},{"japanese":"祖母は自宅で家族に介護されています。","romaji":"Sobo wa jitaku de kazoku ni kaigo sarete imasu.","german":"My grandmother is cared for at home by her family.","furigana":"そぼ|じたく|かぞく|かいご"}],"notes":"介護 is the care and support of elderly, sick or disabled people in daily life (eating, bathing, moving about); as a transitive する-verb: 母を介護する. Compounds: 介護士, 介護施設, 親の介護. Distinction: 看護 is medical nursing (看護師 \"nurse in a hospital\"), 育児 is childcare.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"少子化が問題になっています。","romaji":"Shōshika ga mondai ni natte imasu.","german":"The declining birthrate has become a problem.","furigana":"しょうしか|もんだい"},{"japanese":"少子化のため、村の小学校がなくなりました。","romaji":"Shoushika no tame, mura no shougakkou ga nakunarimashita.","german":"Because of the declining birthrate, the village elementary school closed.","furigana":"しょうしか|むら|しょうがっこう"},{"japanese":"政府は新しい少子化対策を発表しました。","romaji":"Seifu wa atarashii shoushika taisaku o happyou shimashita.","german":"The government announced new measures against the declining birthrate.","furigana":"せいふ|あたら|しょうしかたいさく|はっぴょう"}],"notes":"少子化 refers to the decrease in the number of children or the birthrate, a central social issue in Japan. It is often combined with 高齢化 to form 少子高齢化. Typical: 少子化が進む, 少子化対策 \"measures against the declining birthrate\". The suffix 化 expresses a change; the birthrate itself is 出生率.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本では高齢化が進んでいます。","romaji":"Nihon de wa koureika ga susunde imasu.","german":"In Japan, the population is aging.","furigana":"にほん|こうれいか|すす"},{"japanese":"村の人口が高齢化して、若い人がほとんどいません。","romaji":"Mura no jinkou ga koureika shite, wakai hito ga hotondo imasen.","german":"The village population is aging, and there are hardly any young people.","furigana":"むら|じんこう|こうれいか|わか|ひと"},{"japanese":"少子高齢化は日本の大きな課題です。","romaji":"Shoushi koureika wa Nihon no ookina kadai desu.","german":"The declining birthrate and aging population is a major challenge for Japan.","furigana":"しょうしこうれいか|にほん|おお|かだい"}],"notes":"高齢化 is aging, usually of the population or society; as an intransitive する-verb: 人口が高齢化する. Typical: 高齢化が進む, 高齢化社会, 少子高齢化. 高齢 is the polite word for \"advanced age\" (高齢者 \"seniors\"), and 化 denotes the change. The aging of an individual person is not 高齢化 but 年を取る.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"若い世代の意見を聞きます。","romaji":"Wakai sedai no iken o kikimasu.","german":"I listen to the opinions of the younger generation.","furigana":"わか|せだい|いけん|き"},{"japanese":"私と父は世代が違うので、考え方も違います。","romaji":"Watashi to chichi wa sedai ga chigau node, kangaekata mo chigaimasu.","german":"My father and I are of different generations, so we also think differently.","furigana":"わたし|ちち|せだい|ちが|かんが|かた|ちが"},{"japanese":"この歌は世代を超えて愛されています。","romaji":"Kono uta wa sedai o koete aisarete imasu.","german":"This song is loved across generations.","furigana":"うた|せだい|こ|あい"}],"notes":"世代 is a generation: 若い世代, 親の世代, 次の世代, 同じ世代. Typical: 世代が違う, 世代を超えて \"across generations\", 世代交代 \"generational change\". Product generations are also counted this way: 第三世代. Distinction: 時代 is an era, not an age group.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"環境意識が高まっています。","romaji":"Kankyō ishiki ga takamatte imasu.","german":"Environmental awareness is growing.","furigana":"かんきょういしき|たか"},{"japanese":"事故のあと、彼はしばらく意識がありませんでした。","romaji":"Jiko no ato, kare wa shibaraku ishiki ga arimasen deshita.","german":"After the accident, he was unconscious for a while.","furigana":"じこ|かれ|いしき"},{"japanese":"最近は健康を意識して、毎日歩いています。","romaji":"Saikin wa kenkou o ishiki shite, mainichi aruite imasu.","german":"Recently I've been walking every day, mindful of my health.","furigana":"さいきん|けんこう|いしき|まいにちある"}],"notes":"意識 has two main meanings: consciousness in the medical sense (意識を失う \"to lose consciousness\", 意識が戻る \"to regain consciousness\") and awareness of something (環境意識, 意識が高まる). As a する-verb, 〜を意識する means \"to pay attention to, to be aware of\", for example 人の目を意識する \"to be conscious of how one appears to others\". 意識が高い describes someone with high awareness of issues, colloquially also used ironically.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"問題の認識が甘いです。","romaji":"Mondai no ninshiki ga amai desu.","german":"Awareness of the problem is too weak.","furigana":"もんだい|にんしき|あま"},{"japanese":"その点について、私たちの認識は少し違うようです。","romaji":"Sono ten ni tsuite, watashitachi no ninshiki wa sukoshi chigau you desu.","german":"On that point, our understandings seem to differ a little.","furigana":"てん|わたし|にんしき|すこ|ちが"},{"japanese":"このカメラは人の顔を自動で認識します。","romaji":"Kono kamera wa hito no kao o jidou de ninshiki shimasu.","german":"This camera automatically recognizes people's faces.","furigana":"ひと|かお|じどう|にんしき"}],"notes":"認識 is recognizing and understanding something, that is, how one assesses a situation: 問題を認識する, 認識が甘い \"to underestimate the situation\", 認識が違う \"to see things somewhat differently\". In technology it means \"recognition\": 顔認識, 音声認識. Distinction: 意識 is consciousness or awareness, 認識 is the factual grasping and comprehension; both sound rather literary.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"誤解を解きましょう。","romaji":"Gokai o tokimashō.","german":"Let's clear up the misunderstanding.","furigana":"ごかい|と"},{"japanese":"誤解しないでください。怒っているわけではありません。","romaji":"Gokai shinaide kudasai. Okotte iru wake de wa arimasen.","german":"Please don't misunderstand. I'm not angry.","furigana":"ごかい|おこ"},{"japanese":"誤解を招くような言い方は避けたほうがいいです。","romaji":"Gokai o maneku you na iikata wa saketa hou ga ii desu.","german":"It's better to avoid phrasing that could cause misunderstanding.","furigana":"ごかい|まね|い|かた|さ"}],"notes":"誤解 is a misunderstanding or false notion; as a する-verb: 〜を誤解する \"to misunderstand something/someone\". Fixed combinations: 誤解を招く \"to lead to misunderstandings\", 誤解を解く \"to clear up a misunderstanding\", 誤解される \"to be misunderstood\". Compared with 勘違い (one's own error, more everyday), 誤解 mostly emphasizes a wrong picture of a person or statement.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"偏見を持たないでください。","romaji":"Henken o motanaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't be prejudiced.","furigana":"へんけん|も"},{"japanese":"外国人に対する偏見はまだ残っています。","romaji":"Gaikokujin ni taisuru henken wa mada nokotte imasu.","german":"Prejudice against foreigners still remains.","furigana":"がいこくじん|たい|へんけん|のこ"},{"japanese":"実際に会ってみて、自分の偏見に気づきました。","romaji":"Jissai ni atte mite, jibun no henken ni kizukimashita.","german":"When I actually met them, I noticed my own prejudice.","furigana":"じっさい|あ|じぶん|へんけん|き"}],"notes":"偏見 is a prejudice, a one-sided, biased view. Typical: 偏見を持つ, 〜に対する偏見, 偏見をなくす, 偏見の目で見る. It is not a する-verb; \"to be prejudiced\" is expressed with 偏見がある or 偏見を持っている. Related: 先入観 is a (more neutral) preconceived notion, 差別 is the discrimination that can follow from prejudice.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"差別はよくないです。","romaji":"Sabetsu wa yokunai desu.","german":"Discrimination is bad.","furigana":"さべつ"},{"japanese":"彼女は女性だという理由で差別されました。","romaji":"Kanojo wa josei da to iu riyuu de sabetsu saremashita.","german":"She was discriminated against because she is a woman.","furigana":"かのじょ|じょせい|りゆう|さべつ"},{"japanese":"人種差別をなくすために、教育が大切です。","romaji":"Jinshu sabetsu o nakusu tame ni, kyouiku ga taisetsu desu.","german":"Education is important for eliminating racial discrimination.","furigana":"じんしゅさべつ|きょういく|たいせつ"}],"notes":"差別 is discrimination, that is, unfair disadvantaging of people; as a transitive する-verb: 人を差別する, passive 差別される. Typical: 人種差別, 男女差別, 差別をなくす. The older neutral meaning \"distinction\" lives on in 差別化 \"differentiation (of products)\". For neutral distinctions, people otherwise say 区別: 公私を区別する.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"男女平等は大切です。","romaji":"Danjo byōdō wa taisetsu desu.","german":"Gender equality is important.","furigana":"だんじょびょうどう|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"ケーキを三人で平等に分けました。","romaji":"Keeki o sannin de byoudou ni wakemashita.","german":"We divided the cake equally among the three of us.","furigana":"さんにん|びょうどう|わ"},{"japanese":"すべての人は法の下に平等です。","romaji":"Subete no hito wa hou no moto ni byoudou desu.","german":"All people are equal under the law.","furigana":"ひと|ほう|もと|びょうどう"}],"notes":"平等 means equality or equal rights and is used as a noun and as a な-adjective: 平等な社会, 平等に分ける \"to divide fairly\". Typical: 男女平等, 法の下の平等; the opposite is 不平等. Distinction: 公平 emphasizes fairness and impartiality; 平等 emphasizes equal treatment or equal rights for all.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"権利を守ります。","romaji":"Kenri o mamorimasu.","german":"I will protect my rights.","furigana":"けんり|まも"},{"japanese":"18歳になると、選挙で投票する権利があります。","romaji":"Juuhassai ni naru to, senkyo de touhyou suru kenri ga arimasu.","german":"When you turn 18, you have the right to vote in elections.","furigana":"さい|せんきょ|とうひょう|けんり"},{"japanese":"説明と商品が違ったので、お金を返してもらう権利があるはずです。","romaji":"Setsumei to shouhin ga chigatta node, okane o kaeshite morau kenri ga aru hazu desu.","german":"Since the product differed from the description, I should be entitled to get my money back.","furigana":"せつめい|しょうひん|ちが|かね|かえ|けんり"}],"notes":"権利 is a right in the legal sense, an entitlement: 〜する権利がある, 権利を守る/主張する/失う. The counterpart is 義務 \"duty\" (権利と義務). Combinations: 人権 \"human rights\", 選挙権 \"right to vote\". \"To be right\" in the sense of being correct, on the other hand, is 正しい, and \"law\" as a legal order is 法律 or 法.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"義務を果たしましょう。","romaji":"Gimu o hatashimashō.","german":"Let's fulfill our duties.","furigana":"ぎむ|は"},{"japanese":"日本では中学校までが義務教育です。","romaji":"Nihon de wa chuugakkou made ga gimu kyouiku desu.","german":"In Japan, education up to junior high school is compulsory.","furigana":"にほん|ちゅうがっこう|ぎむきょういく"},{"japanese":"車に乗るときは、シートベルトをする義務があります。","romaji":"Kuruma ni noru toki wa, shiitoberuto o suru gimu ga arimasu.","german":"When riding in a car, you are required to wear a seat belt.","furigana":"くるま|の|ぎむ"}],"notes":"義務 is a duty, especially a legal or moral one: 義務を果たす \"to fulfill one's duty\", 〜する義務がある, 〜が義務づけられている \"is required\". Fixed: 義務教育 \"compulsory education\", 納税の義務 \"duty to pay taxes\". The counterpart is 権利 \"right\". For personal tasks in daily life, people rather say 〜なければならない; 責任 is responsibility.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"規制が厳しくなりました。","romaji":"Kisei ga kibishiku narimashita.","german":"The regulations have become stricter.","furigana":"きせい|きび"},{"japanese":"マラソン大会のため、駅前で交通規制が行われています。","romaji":"Marason taikai no tame, ekimae de koutsuu kisei ga okonawarete imasu.","german":"Because of the marathon event, traffic restrictions are in effect in front of the station.","furigana":"たいかい|えきまえ|こうつうきせい|おこな"},{"japanese":"政府はたばこの広告を規制しています。","romaji":"Seifu wa tabako no koukoku o kisei shite imasu.","german":"The government regulates tobacco advertising.","furigana":"せいふ|こうこく|きせい"}],"notes":"規制 is regulation or restriction by laws and authorities; as a transitive する-verb: 〜を規制する. Typical: 規制が厳しい, 規制を緩める \"to loosen\", 規制を強化する \"to tighten\", 交通規制 \"traffic restriction, road closure\". Distinction: 規則 is a single rule or regulation, 制限 a limit such as 年齢制限 \"age restriction\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"教育改革が必要です。","romaji":"Kyōiku kaikaku ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"Educational reform is necessary.","furigana":"きょういくかいかく|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"新しい社長は会社の組織を大きく改革しました。","romaji":"Atarashii shachou wa kaisha no soshiki o ookiku kaikaku shimashita.","german":"The new president thoroughly reformed the company's organization.","furigana":"あたら|しゃちょう|かいしゃ|そしき|おお|かいかく"},{"japanese":"働き方改革で、残業が少なくなりました。","romaji":"Hatarakikata kaikaku de, zangyou ga sukunaku narimashita.","german":"Thanks to work style reform, there is less overtime.","furigana":"はたら|かたかいかく|ざんぎょう|すく"}],"notes":"改革 is a fundamental reform of systems and organizations; as a transitive する-verb: 制度を改革する. Typical: 教育改革, 政治改革, 働き方改革 \"reform of working styles\", 改革を進める. Distinction: 改善 is a (gradual) improvement, 改正 is the amendment of a law, 革命 is a revolution.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"生活環境の改善を図ります。","romaji":"Seikatsu kankyō no kaizen o hakarimasu.","german":"We will aim to improve the living environment.","furigana":"せいかつかんきょう|かいぜん|はか"},{"japanese":"医者に生活習慣を改善するように言われました。","romaji":"Isha ni seikatsu shuukan o kaizen suru you ni iwaremashita.","german":"The doctor told me to improve my lifestyle habits.","furigana":"いしゃ|せいかつしゅうかん|かいぜん|い"},{"japanese":"新しい薬を飲んでから、症状が少し改善しました。","romaji":"Atarashii kusuri o nonde kara, shoujou ga sukoshi kaizen shimashita.","german":"Since I started taking the new medicine, my symptoms have improved a little.","furigana":"あたら|くすり|の|しょうじょう|すこ|かいぜん"}],"notes":"改善 is the improvement of a bad or inadequate state; as a する-verb it is both transitive (〜を改善する) and intransitive (状況が改善する \"the situation improves\"). Typical: 改善を図る, 改善点, 生活習慣を改善する. In daily life people often say more simply よくする/よくなる. Distinction: 改良 is the technical improvement of a product, 改革 a fundamental reform; as a loanword, \"kaizen\" denotes the Japanese management philosophy.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"スケジュールを調整します。","romaji":"Sukejūru o chōsei shimasu.","german":"I will adjust the schedule.","furigana":"ちょうせい"},{"japanese":"椅子の高さを調整してください。","romaji":"Isu no takasa o chousei shite kudasai.","german":"Please adjust the height of the chair.","furigana":"いす|たか|ちょうせい"},{"japanese":"会議の日程は今、調整中です。","romaji":"Kaigi no nittei wa ima, chousei chuu desu.","german":"The date of the meeting is currently being coordinated.","furigana":"かいぎ|にってい|いま|ちょうせいちゅう"}],"notes":"調整 means adjusting, coordinating or setting something so that it fits; it is a transitive する-verb. Typical: 日程/スケジュールを調整する \"to coordinate dates\", 温度を調整する, 意見を調整する. In the office you often hear 調整中 \"currently being coordinated\". Distinction: 調節 is rather the mechanical regulation of amount or degree (温度調節), while 調整 also includes coordination between people.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"データを処理します。","romaji":"Dēta o shori shimasu.","german":"I will process the data.","furigana":"しょり"},{"japanese":"今日中にこの書類を処理しなければなりません。","romaji":"Kyou juu ni kono shorui o shori shinakereba narimasen.","german":"I have to deal with these documents today.","furigana":"きょうじゅう|しょるい|しょり"},{"japanese":"このパソコンは処理が速いです。","romaji":"Kono pasokon wa shori ga hayai desu.","german":"This computer processes quickly.","furigana":"しょり|はや"}],"notes":"処理 means to handle, take care of or dispose of something properly; a transitive する-verb. Typical: 仕事/書類を処理する, データ処理, ゴミ処理, 事故の処理. For computers it stands for \"processing\" (処理速度, 処理が速い). Distinction: 処分 is throwing away or a punishment, 対応 is responding to a situation or person.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"品質を管理します。","romaji":"Hinshitsu o kanri shimasu.","german":"I manage quality.","furigana":"ひんしつ|かんり"},{"japanese":"自分の健康は自分で管理しましょう。","romaji":"Jibun no kenkou wa jibun de kanri shimashou.","german":"Let's manage our own health ourselves.","furigana":"じぶん|けんこう|じぶん|かんり"},{"japanese":"パスワードはきちんと管理してください。","romaji":"Pasuwaado wa kichinto kanri shite kudasai.","german":"Please manage your passwords properly.","furigana":"かんり"}],"notes":"管理 is administration, supervision or control over things, buildings, money or people; a transitive する-verb. Typical: 健康管理, 品質管理, 時間を管理する, 管理人 \"building manager, caretaker\", 管理職 \"managerial position\". Distinction: 経営 is running a company as a business, 運営 the operation of an organization or event.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"会社を運営しています。","romaji":"Kaisha o un'ei shite imasu.","german":"I run a company.","furigana":"かいしゃ|うんえい"},{"japanese":"生徒たちが自分で文化祭を運営しています。","romaji":"Seitotachi ga jibun de bunkasai o un'ei shite imasu.","german":"The students run the school festival themselves.","furigana":"せいと|じぶん|ぶんかさい|うんえい"},{"japanese":"このサイトの運営にはお金がかかります。","romaji":"Kono saito no un'ei ni wa okane ga kakarimasu.","german":"Running this website costs money.","furigana":"うんえい|かね"}],"notes":"運営 is the operation or management of an organization, facility or event; transitive する-verb: 〜を運営する. Typical: 店/学校/サイト/イベントを運営する, 運営会社, 運営費. Distinction: 経営 stresses the business management of a company, 運営 the practical organizing and keeping things running, also for clubs and volunteer work; 管理 is administering and supervising.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先生の指導を受けます。","romaji":"Sensei no shidō o ukemasu.","german":"I receive the teacher's guidance.","furigana":"せんせい|しどう|う"},{"japanese":"田中さんは新入社員の指導を担当しています。","romaji":"Tanaka-san wa shinnyuu shain no shidou o tantou shite imasu.","german":"Mr. Tanaka is in charge of training new employees.","furigana":"たなか|しんにゅうしゃいん|しどう|たんとう"},{"japanese":"コーチは子どもたちを優しく指導しています。","romaji":"Koochi wa kodomotachi o yasashiku shidou shite imasu.","german":"The coach gently guides the children.","furigana":"こ|やさ|しどう"}],"notes":"指導 is guidance, supervision or instruction by an experienced person such as a teacher, trainer or superior; a transitive する-verb: 学生を指導する. Typical: 指導を受ける, 〜の指導のもとで, 指導者, 生活指導. Distinction: 教える is \"to teach\" in general; 指導 sounds more formal and emphasizes the guiding, correcting role.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"給料の交渉をします。","romaji":"Kyūryō no kōshō o shimasu.","german":"I will negotiate my salary.","furigana":"きゅうりょう|こうしょう"},{"japanese":"市場で値段を交渉して、少し安くしてもらいました。","romaji":"Ichiba de nedan o koushou shite, sukoshi yasuku shite moraimashita.","german":"I negotiated the price at the market and got it a bit cheaper.","furigana":"いちば|ねだん|こうしょう|すこ|やす"},{"japanese":"二つの会社の交渉はまだまとまっていません。","romaji":"Futatsu no kaisha no koushou wa mada matomatte imasen.","german":"The negotiations between the two companies have not yet been settled.","furigana":"ふた|かいしゃ|こうしょう"}],"notes":"交渉 is a negotiation; as a する-verb: 〜と交渉する \"to negotiate with someone\", 値段を交渉する \"to negotiate the price\". Typical: 交渉がまとまる \"to reach an agreement\", 給料交渉, 値下げ交渉. Distinction: 相談 is a consultation or arrangement, 話し合い a discussion to reach agreement; 交渉 stresses bargaining over differing interests.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ご協力をお願いします。","romaji":"Go-kyōryoku o onegai shimasu.","german":"I ask for your cooperation.","furigana":"きょうりょく|ねが"},{"japanese":"アンケートにご協力ありがとうございました。","romaji":"Ankeeto ni go-kyouryoku arigatou gozaimashita.","german":"Thank you for your cooperation with the survey.","furigana":"きょうりょく"},{"japanese":"みんなで協力して、部屋を片付けました。","romaji":"Minna de kyouryoku shite, heya o katazukemashita.","german":"We all worked together and tidied up the room.","furigana":"きょうりょく|へや|かたづ"}],"notes":"協力 is cooperation or help; an intransitive する-verb with に or と: 〜に協力する \"to take part in, to support\", 〜と協力する \"to work together with\". The polite formulas ご協力をお願いします and ご協力ありがとうございます are heard in announcements and read on signs. Distinction: 手伝う is concrete help with an activity; 協力 sounds more formal and means working together toward a common goal.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"他の部署と連携します。","romaji":"Hoka no busho to renkei shimasu.","german":"I will coordinate with other departments.","furigana":"ほか|ぶしょ|れんけい"},{"japanese":"病院と学校が連携して、子どもの健康を守っています。","romaji":"Byouin to gakkou ga renkei shite, kodomo no kenkou o mamotte imasu.","german":"The hospital and the school are working together to protect children's health.","furigana":"びょういん|がっこう|れんけい|こ|けんこう|まも"},{"japanese":"このアプリはカレンダーと連携できます。","romaji":"Kono apuri wa karendaa to renkei dekimasu.","german":"This app can be linked with the calendar.","furigana":"れんけい"}],"notes":"連携 is cooperation and coordination between organizations, departments or systems, mostly with と: 〜と連携する. Typical: 連携を取る, 連携を強める, 地域と連携する, アプリ連携 \"app integration\". Distinction: 協力 is general helping out; 連携 emphasizes that different parties coordinate and link their work; the word is rather formal and businesslike.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"文化が異なります。","romaji":"Bunka ga kotonarimasu.","german":"The cultures are different.","furigana":"ぶんか|こと"},{"japanese":"料金は季節によって異なります。","romaji":"Ryoukin wa kisetsu ni yotte kotonarimasu.","german":"The fee differs depending on the season.","furigana":"りょうきん|きせつ|こと"},{"japanese":"二人は異なる意見を持っています。","romaji":"Futari wa kotonaru iken o motte imasu.","german":"The two have different opinions.","furigana":"ふたり|こと|いけん|も"}],"notes":"異なる is the literary, more formal counterpart of 違う \"to be different\": AとBは異なる, 〜によって異なる \"differs depending on ...\". It often appears in manuals, the news and texts, also attributively: 異なる意見. Unlike 違う, 異なる never means \"to be wrong\"; \"That's not right\" remains 違います. The verb is intransitive and has no transitive counterpart.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"事実に基づいて判断します。","romaji":"Jijitsu ni motozuite handan shimasu.","german":"I will judge based on the facts.","furigana":"じじつ|もと|はんだん"},{"japanese":"この映画は実話に基づいています。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa jitsuwa ni motozuite imasu.","german":"This movie is based on a true story.","furigana":"えいが|じつわ|もと"},{"japanese":"規則に基づいて、遅刻した人は参加できません。","romaji":"Kisoku ni motozuite, chikoku shita hito wa sanka dekimasen.","german":"In accordance with the rules, people who are late cannot participate.","furigana":"きそく|もと|ちこく|ひと|さんか"}],"notes":"基づく is almost always used as 〜に基づく / 〜に基づいて \"based on, in accordance with\": データに基づいて判断する, 法律に基づく. Attributively: 事実に基づいた話 or 事実に基づく話 \"a story based on facts\". The verb is intransitive and rather literary; in daily life people also say 〜をもとに(して).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"リスクを伴います。","romaji":"Risuku o tomonaimasu.","german":"It involves risks.","furigana":"ともな"},{"japanese":"この手術は少し痛みを伴います。","romaji":"Kono shujutsu wa sukoshi itami o tomonaimasu.","german":"This operation involves some pain.","furigana":"しゅじゅつ|すこ|いた|ともな"},{"japanese":"台風に伴う大雨で、電車が止まりました。","romaji":"Taifuu ni tomonau ooame de, densha ga tomarimashita.","german":"Heavy rain accompanying the typhoon stopped the trains.","furigana":"たいふう|ともな|おおあめ|でんしゃ|と"}],"notes":"伴う has two main patterns: 〜を伴う \"to bring something with it, to be accompanied by something\" (危険を伴う, 痛みを伴う) and 〜に伴って / 〜に伴い / 〜に伴う \"along with, as a result of\" (人口の増加に伴って). The word is literary and formal and common in news and official announcements. The meaning \"to take someone along\" (家族を伴って) is rarer.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"皆さんの意見を計画に反映します。","romaji":"Minasan no iken o keikaku ni han'ei shimasu.","german":"We will reflect everyone's opinions in the plan.","furigana":"みな|いけん|けいかく|はんえい"},{"japanese":"この小説には当時の社会が反映されています。","romaji":"Kono shousetsu ni wa touji no shakai ga han'ei sarete imasu.","german":"This novel reflects the society of that time.","furigana":"しょうせつ|とうじ|しゃかい|はんえい"},{"japanese":"設定を変えたのに、まだ画面に反映されません。","romaji":"Settei o kaeta noni, mada gamen ni han'ei saremasen.","german":"I changed the settings, but they are not yet reflected on the screen.","furigana":"せってい|か|がめん|はんえい"}],"notes":"反映 means that something is reflected in, or taken into account in, something else. Transitive: AをBに反映する / 反映させる \"to incorporate A into B\" (意見を計画に反映させる); very often passive: 〜に反映される \"to be reflected in …\". In an IT context it means \"to apply (a change)\": 変更が反映されない \"the change is not applied\". For the physical reflection of light, 反射 is usually used.","levelNote":"Derived from 反映 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しいルールを適用します。","romaji":"Atarashii rūru o tekiyō shimasu.","german":"I will apply the new rules.","furigana":"あたら|てきよう"},{"japanese":"この治療には保険が適用されます。","romaji":"Kono chiryou ni wa hoken ga tekiyou saremasu.","german":"Insurance applies to this treatment.","furigana":"ちりょう|ほけん|てきよう"},{"japanese":"学生割引は、この商品には適用されません。","romaji":"Gakusei waribiki wa, kono shouhin ni wa tekiyou saremasen.","german":"The student discount does not apply to this product.","furigana":"がくせいわりびき|しょうひん|てきよう"}],"notes":"適用 means to apply a rule, law or rate to a case: AをBに適用する; very often passive: 〜が適用される \"applies, is in effect\". Typical: 保険が適用される \"is covered by insurance\", 割引が適用される, 法律を適用する. Distinction: 応用 is the practical application of knowledge or technology, 使う is general using. Do not confuse it with 適応 \"adaptation\".","levelNote":"Derived from 適用 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しいシステムを導入します。","romaji":"Atarashii shisutemu o dōnyū shimasu.","german":"We will introduce a new system.","furigana":"あたら|どうにゅう"},{"japanese":"この店ではセルフレジを導入しました。","romaji":"Kono mise de wa serufu reji o dounyuu shimashita.","german":"This store has introduced self-checkout registers.","furigana":"みせ|どうにゅう"},{"japanese":"来年から制服が導入されることになりました。","romaji":"Rainen kara seifuku ga dounyuu sareru koto ni narimashita.","german":"School uniforms will be introduced from next year.","furigana":"らいねん|せいふく|どうにゅう"}],"notes":"導入 means introducing or acquiring something new, such as a system, technology or procedure: 〜を導入する; passive 〜が導入される. Typical: システム/制度/機械を導入する, 導入費用. In texts and teaching, 導入 also means \"introduction\". Distinction: 紹介 is introducing a person or thing, 輸入 is importing goods.","levelNote":"Derived from 導入 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"調査を実施します。","romaji":"Chōsa o jisshi shimasu.","german":"We will conduct a survey.","furigana":"ちょうさ|じっし"},{"japanese":"来月、避難訓練を実施します。","romaji":"Raigetsu, hinan kunren o jisshi shimasu.","german":"We will hold an evacuation drill next month.","furigana":"らいげつ|ひなんくんれん|じっし"},{"japanese":"ただいまポイント2倍キャンペーンを実施中です。","romaji":"Tadaima pointo nibai kyanpeen o jisshi chuu desu.","german":"A double-points campaign is currently being held.","furigana":"ばい|じっしちゅう"}],"notes":"実施 means to officially carry out plans, measures, exams or campaigns: 〜を実施する; passive 〜が実施される. Typical: 調査/試験/訓練/キャンペーンを実施する, 実施中 \"currently under way\". It sounds formal and official; in daily life people say 行う or する. Distinction: 実行 emphasizes a person carrying out a plan or resolution, 実施 the regulated carrying out by an organization.","levelNote":"Derived from 実施 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"環境保護を推進します。","romaji":"Kankyō hogo o suishin shimasu.","german":"We promote environmental protection.","furigana":"かんきょうほご|すいしん"},{"japanese":"市はリサイクル運動を推進しています。","romaji":"Shi wa risaikuru undou o suishin shite imasu.","german":"The city is promoting a recycling campaign.","furigana":"し|うんどう|すいしん"},{"japanese":"会社は女性の管理職を増やす計画を推進しています。","romaji":"Kaisha wa josei no kanrishoku o fuyasu keikaku o suishin shite imasu.","german":"The company is pushing ahead with a plan to increase the number of women in management positions.","furigana":"かいしゃ|じょせい|かんりしょく|ふ|けいかく|すいしん"}],"notes":"推進 means actively driving forward a project, policy or movement: 〜を推進する. Typical objects are 計画, 政策, 改革, 環境保護 and デジタル化. The word is formal and occurs mainly in politics, administration and companies. Distinction: 促進 stresses speeding up a process (成長を促進する), 推進 the active pushing forward of an undertaking; technically 推進 also means \"propulsion\" (ships, rockets).","levelNote":"Derived from 推進 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"経済成長を促進します。","romaji":"Keizai seichō o sokushin shimasu.","german":"We promote economic growth.","furigana":"けいざいせいちょう|そくしん"},{"japanese":"適度な運動は消化を促進します。","romaji":"Tekido na undou wa shouka o sokushin shimasu.","german":"Moderate exercise aids digestion.","furigana":"てきど|うんどう|しょうか|そくしん"},{"japanese":"両国の文化交流を促進するイベントが開かれました。","romaji":"Ryoukoku no bunka kouryuu o sokushin suru ibento ga hirakaremashita.","german":"An event promoting cultural exchange between the two countries was held.","furigana":"りょうこく|ぶんかこうりゅう|そくしん|ひら"}],"notes":"促進 means promoting or accelerating a process: 〜を促進する. Typical: 経済成長/販売/交流/消化を促進する, 販売促進 (short 販促) \"sales promotion\". It usually concerns processes that are already under way and are meant to become faster or stronger. Distinction: 推進 is the active pushing forward of a plan by people or organizations. The word is formal and common in business and medicine.","levelNote":"Derived from 促進 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コストを抑制します。","romaji":"Kosuto o yokusei shimasu.","german":"We will keep costs in check.","furigana":"よくせい"},{"japanese":"この薬は食欲を抑制する効果があります。","romaji":"Kono kusuri wa shokuyoku o yokusei suru kouka ga arimasu.","german":"This medicine has the effect of suppressing appetite.","furigana":"くすり|しょくよく|よくせい|こうか"},{"japanese":"政府は物価の上昇を抑制しようとしています。","romaji":"Seifu wa bukka no joushou o yokusei shiyou to shite imasu.","german":"The government is trying to curb the rise in prices.","furigana":"せいふ|ぶっか|じょうしょう|よくせい"}],"notes":"抑制 means holding back, braking or suppressing something undesirable: 〜を抑制する. Typical: 物価の上昇/食欲/感情を抑制する, 抑制効果. The word is formal and literary; in everyday speech one says 抑える (コストを抑える, 気持ちを抑える). Distinction: 削減 is cutting in numerical terms, 抑制 is limiting or braking an increase.","levelNote":"Derived from 抑制 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"問題点を指摘します。","romaji":"Mondaiten o shiteki shimasu.","german":"I will point out the problem areas.","furigana":"もんだいてん|してき"},{"japanese":"上司に資料の間違いを指摘されました。","romaji":"Joushi ni shiryou no machigai o shiteki saremashita.","german":"My boss pointed out a mistake in the materials.","furigana":"じょうし|しりょう|まちが|してき"},{"japanese":"ご指摘ありがとうございます。すぐに直します。","romaji":"Go-shiteki arigatou gozaimasu. Sugu ni naoshimasu.","german":"Thank you for pointing that out. I will fix it right away.","furigana":"してき|なお"}],"notes":"指摘 means explicitly pointing out a mistake, a problem or an important point: 〜を指摘する, 〜と指摘する. Typical: 間違いを指摘する, 問題点を指摘する; passive: 〜に指摘される \"to have something pointed out by someone\". ご指摘ありがとうございます is a polite reaction to criticism. Distinction: 注意する is \"to warn, to caution\", 指す is literally pointing at something.","levelNote":"Derived from 指摘 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"提案を検討します。","romaji":"Teian o kentō shimasu.","german":"I will consider the proposal.","furigana":"ていあん|けんとう"},{"japanese":"引っ越しを検討しています。","romaji":"Hikkoshi o kentou shite imasu.","german":"I am considering moving.","furigana":"ひ|こ|けんとう"},{"japanese":"その件については、社内で前向きに検討いたします。","romaji":"Sono ken ni tsuite wa, shanai de maemuki ni kentou itashimasu.","german":"We will look into that matter positively within the company.","furigana":"けん|しゃない|まえむ|けんとう"}],"notes":"検討 means to examine and weigh something thoroughly before deciding: 〜を検討する, 〜するかどうか検討する. In business language, 前向きに検討します \"we will consider it favorably\" is very common, and often a polite way of not agreeing right away. Typical: 検討中, 再検討. Distinction: 考える is \"to think\" in general, 検査 a technical or medical examination.","levelNote":"Derived from 検討 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"結果を分析します。","romaji":"Kekka o bunseki shimasu.","german":"I will analyze the results.","furigana":"けっか|ぶんせき"},{"japanese":"失敗の原因をしっかり分析しましょう。","romaji":"Shippai no gen'in o shikkari bunseki shimashou.","german":"Let's analyze the cause of the failure thoroughly.","furigana":"しっぱい|げんいん|ぶんせき"},{"japanese":"就職活動の前に、自己分析をしました。","romaji":"Shuushoku katsudou no mae ni, jiko bunseki o shimashita.","german":"Before job hunting, I did a self-analysis.","furigana":"しゅうしょくかつどう|まえ|じこぶんせき"}],"notes":"分析 means to break something into its components and examine it systematically: 〜を分析する. Typical: データ/結果/原因/市場を分析する, 分析結果, 自己分析 \"self-analysis\" (in job hunting). Distinction: 調査 is gathering information, 分析 is the subsequent evaluation, 検討 is weighing before a decision.","levelNote":"Derived from 分析 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二つの案を比較します。","romaji":"Futatsu no an o hikaku shimasu.","german":"I will compare the two proposals.","furigana":"ふた|あん|ひかく"},{"japanese":"去年と比較して、今年は雨が少ないです。","romaji":"Kyonen to hikaku shite, kotoshi wa ame ga sukunai desu.","german":"Compared with last year, there has been less rain this year.","furigana":"きょねん|ひかく|ことし|あめ|すく"},{"japanese":"買う前に、ネットで値段を比較しました。","romaji":"Kau mae ni, netto de nedan o hikaku shimashita.","german":"Before buying, I compared prices online.","furigana":"か|まえ|ねだん|ひかく"}],"notes":"比較する means \"to compare\", mostly in the form AをBと比較する or AとBを比較する. Very common is the phrase 〜と比較して \"in comparison with\". In daily life people mostly use the native verb 比べる; 比較する sounds more objective and suits reports, statistics and price comparisons. Related: 比較的 \"relatively, comparatively\".","levelNote":"Derived from 比較 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二つを区別してください。","romaji":"Futatsu o kubetsu shite kudasai.","german":"Please distinguish between the two.","furigana":"ふた|くべつ"},{"japanese":"仕事とプライベートを区別するのは大切です。","romaji":"Shigoto to puraibeeto o kubetsu suru no wa taisetsu desu.","german":"It is important to separate work and private life.","furigana":"しごと|くべつ|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"双子なので、どちらがどちらか区別がつきません。","romaji":"Futago na node, dochira ga dochira ka kubetsu ga tsukimasen.","german":"They are twins, so I can't tell which is which.","furigana":"ふたご|くべつ"}],"notes":"区別する means \"to distinguish, to tell apart\": AとBを区別する or AをBと区別する. Very common are 区別がつく \"one can tell them apart\" and 区別がつかない \"one cannot tell them apart\". Do not confuse it with 差別 \"discrimination\", which means unfair disadvantaging of people. 見分ける, by contrast, emphasizes recognizing with the eyes.","levelNote":"Derived from 区別 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"データを分類します。","romaji":"Dēta o bunrui shimasu.","german":"I will classify the data.","furigana":"ぶんるい"},{"japanese":"図書館の本はテーマごとに分類されています。","romaji":"Toshokan no hon wa teema goto ni bunrui sarete imasu.","german":"The books in the library are classified by theme.","furigana":"としょかん|ほん|ぶんるい"},{"japanese":"トマトは野菜に分類されることが多いです。","romaji":"Tomato wa yasai ni bunrui sareru koto ga ooi desu.","german":"Tomatoes are often classified as vegetables.","furigana":"やさい|ぶんるい|おお"}],"notes":"分類する means \"to classify, to sort into groups\": AをBに分類する \"to classify A into B\". The passive 〜に分類される \"is classified as …\" is very common. In everyday speech one says more simply 分ける. For sorting trash, 分別（ぶんべつ） is used rather than 分類: ゴミの分別.","levelNote":"Derived from 分類 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"原因を特定します。","romaji":"Gen'in o tokutei shimasu.","german":"I will identify the cause.","furigana":"げんいん|とくてい"},{"japanese":"警察は防犯カメラの映像から犯人を特定しました。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa bouhan kamera no eizou kara hannin o tokutei shimashita.","german":"The police identified the culprit from security camera footage.","furigana":"けいさつ|ぼうはん|えいぞう|はんにん|とくてい"},{"japanese":"写真から場所を特定されることもあるので、気をつけてください。","romaji":"Shashin kara basho o tokutei sareru koto mo aru node, ki o tsukete kudasai.","german":"Please be careful, since your location can sometimes be identified from photos.","furigana":"しゃしん|ばしょ|とくてい|き"}],"notes":"特定する means \"to determine precisely, to pinpoint, to identify\" - to find the one correct answer among several possibilities: 原因を特定する, 犯人を特定する, 場所を特定する. Typical for news, police and technology. As an adjective, 特定の means \"certain, specific\" (特定の人). Do not confuse it with 指定する \"to designate, to specify\".","levelNote":"Derived from 特定 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"最悪のケースを想定します。","romaji":"Saiaku no kēsu o sōtei shimasu.","german":"I will assume the worst case.","furigana":"さいあく|そうてい"},{"japanese":"地震を想定して、避難訓練を行いました。","romaji":"Jishin o soutei shite, hinan kunren o okonaimashita.","german":"Assuming an earthquake, we held an evacuation drill.","furigana":"じしん|そうてい|ひなんくんれん|おこな"},{"japanese":"こんなに人が来るとは想定していませんでした。","romaji":"Konna ni hito ga kuru to wa soutei shite imasen deshita.","german":"I hadn't anticipated that this many people would come.","furigana":"ひと|く|そうてい"}],"notes":"想定する means \"to assume (a case), to proceed from, to factor in\": one imagines a situation in advance in order to prepare for it: 〜を想定して準備する. Very common are 想定外 \"unexpected, not planned for\" and 想定内 \"within expectations\". 予想, by contrast, is the expectation of an outcome, and 仮定 a purely mental assumption.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"将来を予測するのは難しいです。","romaji":"Shourai o yosoku suru no wa muzukashii desu.","german":"It is difficult to predict the future.","furigana":"しょうらい|よそく|むずか"},{"japanese":"専門家は、来年も物価が上がると予測しています。","romaji":"Senmonka wa, rainen mo bukka ga agaru to yosoku shite imasu.","german":"Experts predict that prices will rise next year as well.","furigana":"せんもんか|らいねん|ぶっか|あ|よそく"},{"japanese":"子どもの行動は予測できません。","romaji":"Kodomo no koudou wa yosoku dekimasen.","german":"Children's behavior cannot be predicted.","furigana":"こ|こうどう|よそく"}],"notes":"予測する means \"to predict, to forecast\", usually on the basis of data or analysis: 売り上げを予測する, 〜と予測している. Often negated: 予測できない / 予測がつかない \"unpredictable\". 予想 is more general and also used for personal guesses (試合の結果を予想する); 予報 is specifically a weather forecast.","levelNote":"Derived from 予測 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"被害額を推定します。","romaji":"Higaigaku o suitei shimasu.","german":"I will estimate the amount of damage.","furigana":"ひがいがく|すいてい"},{"japanese":"この寺は約八百年前に建てられたと推定されています。","romaji":"Kono tera wa yaku happyaku nen mae ni taterareta to suitei sarete imasu.","german":"This temple is estimated to have been built about 800 years ago.","furigana":"てら|やくはっぴゃくねんまえ|た|すいてい"},{"japanese":"警察は、男の年齢を四十歳ぐらいと推定しています。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa, otoko no nenrei o yonjussai gurai to suitei shite imasu.","german":"The police estimate the man's age at about forty.","furigana":"けいさつ|おとこ|ねんれい|よんじゅっさい|すいてい"}],"notes":"推定する means \"to estimate, to presume (on the basis of evidence)\". Typical in news and science, often in the passive: 〜と推定される \"it is estimated that …\", for example for age, damage or time. 推測 is rather a conjecture without firm grounds, and 見積もる is estimating costs (a cost estimate). Also well known is 推定無罪 \"presumption of innocence\".","levelNote":"Derived from 推定 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"温度を測定します。","romaji":"Ondo o sokutei shimasu.","german":"I will measure the temperature.","furigana":"おんど|そくてい"},{"japanese":"部屋の騒音レベルを測定しました。","romaji":"Heya no souon reberu o sokutei shimashita.","german":"We measured the noise level in the room.","furigana":"へや|そうおん|そくてい"},{"japanese":"四月に、学校で身長と体重を測定します。","romaji":"Shigatsu ni, gakkou de shinchou to taijuu o sokutei shimasu.","german":"In April, height and weight are measured at school.","furigana":"しがつ|がっこう|しんちょう|たいじゅう|そくてい"}],"notes":"測定する means \"to measure (with instruments)\" and sounds technical: 温度・距離・騒音を測定する, 測定値 \"measured value\". In everyday speech one usually says 測る (length, temperature), 計る (time) or 量る (weight). At school there are 身体測定 (measuring height and weight) and 体力測定 (fitness test).","levelNote":"Derived from 測定 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"動物を観察します。","romaji":"Dōbutsu o kansatsu shimasu.","german":"I observe animals.","furigana":"どうぶつ|かんさつ"},{"japanese":"夏休みに、アサガオを毎日観察して日記を書きました。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi ni, asagao o mainichi kansatsu shite nikki o kakimashita.","german":"During summer vacation, I observed morning glories every day and wrote a diary.","furigana":"なつやす|まいにちかんさつ|にっき|か"},{"japanese":"人の行動を観察するのが好きです。","romaji":"Hito no koudou o kansatsu suru no ga suki desu.","german":"I like observing people's behavior.","furigana":"ひと|こうどう|かんさつ|す"}],"notes":"観察する means \"to observe attentively\", usually over a longer period, in order to learn or understand something: 植物・動物・様子を観察する. Japanese elementary school children often keep an observation diary (観察日記) during vacations. 観測 is scientific observation with measurements (weather, stars), 監視 is surveillance. Simply \"looking at\" is just 見る.","levelNote":"Derived from 観察 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい方法が効果的かどうか実験してみます。","romaji":"Atarashii houhou ga koukateki ka dou ka jikken shite mimasu.","german":"I'll test through an experiment whether the new method is effective.","furigana":"あたら|ほうほう|こうかてき|じっけん"},{"japanese":"理科の授業で、水を使った実験をしました。","romaji":"Rika no jugyou de, mizu o tsukatta jikken o shimashita.","german":"In science class, we did an experiment using water.","furigana":"りか|じゅぎょう|みず|つか|じっけん"},{"japanese":"マウスで実験した結果、薬の効果が確認されました。","romaji":"Mausu de jikken shita kekka, kusuri no kouka ga kakunin saremashita.","german":"As a result of experiments on mice, the effect of the drug was confirmed.","furigana":"じっけん|けっか|くすり|こうか|かくにん"}],"notes":"実験する means \"to carry out an experiment, to experiment\". It often appears as 実験をする, with で for the material or test subject (マウスで実験する), or as 〜かどうか実験する \"to test whether ...\". For simple trying out in daily life, 試す is rather said. Related: 実験室 \"laboratory\", 実験的 \"experimental\".","levelNote":"Derived from 実験 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自分の力を証明しました。","romaji":"Jibun no chikara o shōmei shimashita.","german":"I proved my ability.","furigana":"じぶん|ちから|しょうめい"},{"japanese":"身分を証明できるものをお持ちですか。","romaji":"Mibun o shoumei dekiru mono o o-mochi desu ka.","german":"Do you have something that can prove your identity?","furigana":"みぶん|しょうめい|も"},{"japanese":"この理論が正しいことを実験で証明しました。","romaji":"Kono riron ga tadashii koto o jikken de shoumei shimashita.","german":"I proved by experiment that this theory is correct.","furigana":"りろん|ただ|じっけん|しょうめい"}],"notes":"証明する means \"to prove, to demonstrate\" a fact, theory or ability: 〜ことを証明する, 力を証明する. It also means \"to certify (officially)\": 身分を証明する \"to identify oneself\", 証明書 \"certificate, proof\". Do not confuse it with 説明する \"to explain\" and the identically read 照明 \"lighting\".","levelNote":"Derived from 証明 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい星を発見しました。","romaji":"Atarashii hoshi o hakken shimashita.","german":"I discovered a new star.","furigana":"あたら|ほし|はっけん"},{"japanese":"健康診断でがんが早期に発見されました。","romaji":"Kenkou shindan de gan ga souki ni hakken saremashita.","german":"The cancer was detected at an early stage in a health checkup.","furigana":"けんこうしんだん|そうき|はっけん"},{"japanese":"旅行すると、いつも新しい発見があります。","romaji":"Ryokou suru to, itsumo atarashii hakken ga arimasu.","german":"When I travel, I always make new discoveries.","furigana":"りょこう|あたら|はっけん"}],"notes":"発見する means \"to discover\": to find something previously unknown (新しい星, 遺跡) or to detect something hidden (病気の早期発見 \"early detection\"). For everyday things such as lost keys, people say 見つける. 発明する, by contrast, is \"to invent\", that is, to create something new. As a noun: 新しい発見がある \"to make a new discovery\".","levelNote":"Derived from 発見 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい装置を発明しました。","romaji":"Atarashii sōchi o hatsumei shimashita.","german":"I invented a new device.","furigana":"あたら|そうち|はつめい"},{"japanese":"カップラーメンを発明したのは日本人です。","romaji":"Kappu raamen o hatsumei shita no wa nihonjin desu.","german":"It was a Japanese person who invented instant cup noodles.","furigana":"はつめい|にほんじん"},{"japanese":"子どものころ、空を飛ぶ機械を発明したいと思っていました。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, sora o tobu kikai o hatsumei shitai to omotte imashita.","german":"When I was a child, I wanted to invent a machine that flies through the sky.","furigana":"こ|そら|と|きかい|はつめい|おも"}],"notes":"発明する means \"to invent\": to create technically something new that did not exist before: 機械・道具を発明する. Related: 発明品 \"invention\" and 発明家 \"inventor\". Opposite: 発見する \"to discover\" (to find something already present). 開発する is the planned development of products in companies.","levelNote":"Derived from 発明 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新製品を開発します。","romaji":"Shinseihin o kaihatsu shimasu.","german":"We will develop a new product.","furigana":"しんせいひん|かいはつ"},{"japanese":"この会社はスマホのアプリを開発しています。","romaji":"Kono kaisha wa sumaho no apuri o kaihatsu shite imasu.","german":"This company develops smartphone apps.","furigana":"かいしゃ|かいはつ"},{"japanese":"駅の周りが開発されて、高いビルが増えました。","romaji":"Eki no mawari ga kaihatsu sarete, takai biru ga fuemashita.","german":"The area around the station was developed, and there are now many more tall buildings.","furigana":"えき|まわ|かいはつ|たか|ふ"}],"notes":"開発する means \"to develop\" (products, technology, software: 新製品・アプリを開発する) and \"to develop (land), to exploit\" (land, resources: 駅前を開発する, 都市開発). Related: 開発者 \"developer\" and 開発途上国 \"developing country\". Not to be confused with 発展する \"to develop, to grow\" (city, economy), which is intransitive.","levelNote":"Derived from 開発 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"スマホが世界中に普及しました。","romaji":"Sumaho ga sekaijū ni fukyū shimashita.","german":"Smartphones have spread all over the world.","furigana":"せかいじゅう|ふきゅう"},{"japanese":"電気自動車はまだ十分に普及していません。","romaji":"Denki jidousha wa mada juubun ni fukyuu shite imasen.","german":"Electric cars are not yet sufficiently widespread.","furigana":"でんきじどうしゃ|じゅうぶん|ふきゅう"},{"japanese":"政府はリサイクルの考え方を普及させようとしています。","romaji":"Seifu wa risaikuru no kangaekata o fukyuu saseyou to shite imasu.","german":"The government is trying to spread the idea of recycling.","furigana":"せいふ|かんが|かた|ふきゅう"}],"notes":"普及する means \"to spread, to become generally common\" - technology, products or ideas adopted by many people: スマホが普及する, 〜に普及している. It is mostly intransitive with が; to actively spread something is 普及させる. Related: 普及率 \"penetration rate\". 広まる also fits rumors, and 流行る short-lived trends.","levelNote":"Derived from 普及 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"著しい進歩を遂げました。","romaji":"Ichijirushii shinpo o togemashita.","german":"Remarkable progress was made.","furigana":"いちじる|しんぽ|と"},{"japanese":"この十年で、町の人口に著しい変化がありました。","romaji":"Kono juunen de, machi no jinkou ni ichijirushii henka ga arimashita.","german":"Over the past ten years, there has been a marked change in the town's population.","furigana":"じゅうねん|まち|じんこう|いちじる|へんか"},{"japanese":"最近、気温の差が著しいので、体調に気をつけてください。","romaji":"Saikin, kion no sa ga ichijirushii node, taichou ni ki o tsukete kudasai.","german":"The temperature differences have been striking lately, so please take care of your health.","furigana":"さいきん|きおん|さ|いちじる|たいちょう|き"}],"notes":"著しい is a literary い-adjective: a change or difference is \"strikingly large, considerable\". Typical: 著しい変化・成長・進歩・差. It evaluates only the degree, not whether something is good - 著しい低下 \"a drastic decline\" is also possible. Adverbial: 著しく. In conversation one tends to say すごい, 大きい or かなり.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"顕著な効果がありました。","romaji":"Kencho na kōka ga arimashita.","german":"It had a remarkable effect.","furigana":"けんちょ|こうか"},{"japanese":"若者の読書離れが顕著になっています。","romaji":"Wakamono no dokusho banare ga kencho ni natte imasu.","german":"Young people's turning away from reading has become pronounced.","furigana":"わかもの|どくしょばな|けんちょ"},{"japanese":"この傾向は、特に都市部で顕著です。","romaji":"Kono keikou wa, toku ni toshibu de kencho desu.","german":"This tendency is particularly pronounced in urban areas.","furigana":"けいこう|とく|としぶ|けんちょ"}],"notes":"顕著 is a な-adjective of written and technical language: \"clearly noticeable, pronounced\". Typical: 顕著な効果・傾向・例, 〜が顕著だ, 〜が顕著に現れる. Close to 著しい, but 顕著 emphasizes clear visibility, 著しい the large extent. In conversation people rather say はっきりした or 目立つ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"微妙な問題です。","romaji":"Bimyō na mondai desu.","german":"It's a delicate issue.","furigana":"びみょう|もんだい"},{"japanese":"「この服、どう？」「うーん、ちょっと微妙かな。」","romaji":"\"Kono fuku, dou?\" \"Uun, chotto bimyou ka na.\"","german":"\"How's this outfit?\" \"Hmm, it's kind of so-so.\"","furigana":"ふく|びみょう"},{"japanese":"今から出ても、会議に間に合うかどうか微妙です。","romaji":"Ima kara dete mo, kaigi ni maniau ka dou ka bimyou desu.","german":"Even if I leave now, it's iffy whether I'll make it to the meeting.","furigana":"いま|で|かいぎ|ま|あ|びみょう"}],"notes":"微妙 has several meanings: \"fine, subtle\" (微妙な違い \"a subtle difference\"), \"delicate\" (微妙な問題・立場) and \"questionable, uncertain\" (間に合うか微妙だ). Colloquially, 微妙 is also a polite evasive answer for \"well, not so great\": \"どう？\" - \"うーん、微妙。\". This avoids a direct no.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"厳密に言えば違います。","romaji":"Genmitsu ni ieba chigaimasu.","german":"Strictly speaking, that is different.","furigana":"げんみつ|い|ちが"},{"japanese":"薬の品質は厳密に管理されています。","romaji":"Kusuri no hinshitsu wa genmitsu ni kanri sarete imasu.","german":"The quality of the medicine is strictly controlled.","furigana":"くすり|ひんしつ|げんみつ|かんり"},{"japanese":"厳密なルールはないので、自由に書いてください。","romaji":"Genmitsu na ruuru wa nai node, jiyuu ni kaite kudasai.","german":"There are no strict rules, so please feel free to write however you like.","furigana":"げんみつ|じゆう|か"}],"notes":"厳密 is a な-adjective for \"exact, precise, strict\" in the sense of thorough precision: 厳密な検査, 厳密に管理する. Very common is 厳密に言えば／言うと \"strictly speaking\". 厳しい, by contrast, means \"strict, harsh\" toward people (厳しい先生), and 正確 simply \"correct, accurate\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"妥当な価格です。","romaji":"Datō na kakaku desu.","german":"It is a reasonable price.","furigana":"だとう|かかく"},{"japanese":"今回は、計画を延期するのが妥当だと思います。","romaji":"Konkai wa, keikaku o enki suru no ga datou da to omoimasu.","german":"This time, I think it is reasonable to postpone the plan.","furigana":"こんかい|けいかく|えんき|だとう|おも"},{"japanese":"その結論が妥当かどうか、もう一度考えてみましょう。","romaji":"Sono ketsuron ga datou ka dou ka, mou ichido kangaete mimashou.","german":"Let's think once more about whether that conclusion is valid.","furigana":"けつろん|だとう|いちどかんが"}],"notes":"妥当 is a na-adjective meaning \"appropriate, reasonable, valid\" - correct after weighing the circumstances: 妥当な判断・価格・結論, 〜とするのが妥当だ. It sounds matter-of-fact and suits business and discussion. Be careful with 適当: in everyday speech it often means \"sloppy, half-hearted\"; 適切 means \"suitable, fitting\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この方法は有効です。","romaji":"Kono hōhō wa yūkō desu.","german":"This method is effective.","furigana":"ほうほう|ゆうこう"},{"japanese":"このチケットは発行日から三日間有効です。","romaji":"Kono chiketto wa hakkoubi kara mikkakan yuukou desu.","german":"This ticket is valid for three days from the date of issue.","furigana":"はっこうび|みっかかんゆうこう"},{"japanese":"通勤時間を有効に使って、英語を勉強しています。","romaji":"Tsuukin jikan o yuukou ni tsukatte, eigo o benkyou shite imasu.","german":"I'm studying English by making effective use of my commuting time.","furigana":"つうきんじかん|ゆうこう|つか|えいご|べんきょう"}],"notes":"有効 has two main meanings: \"effective\" (有効な方法, 時間を有効に使う \"to use time wisely\") and \"valid\" (チケットは三日間有効, 有効期限 \"period of validity, expiration date\"). The opposite is 無効. 効果的 emphasizes only effectiveness, not validity.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この切符は無効です。","romaji":"Kono kippu wa mukō desu.","german":"This ticket is invalid.","furigana":"きっぷ|むこう"},{"japanese":"期限が切れたクーポンは無効になります。","romaji":"Kigen ga kireta kuupon wa mukou ni narimasu.","german":"Expired coupons become invalid.","furigana":"きげん|き|むこう"},{"japanese":"設定で通知を無効にしました。","romaji":"Settei de tsuuchi o mukou ni shimashita.","german":"I disabled notifications in the settings.","furigana":"せってい|つうち|むこう"}],"notes":"無効 means \"invalid, void\" - for tickets, contracts, coupons or votes (無効票): 無効になる \"to become invalid\". In IT, 無効にする means \"to disable\" (opposite 有効にする). For \"ineffective\", everyday speech tends to use 効果がない. Not to be confused with the identically pronounced 向こう \"over there\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"チームワークは不可欠です。","romaji":"Chīmuwāku wa fukaketsu desu.","german":"Teamwork is indispensable.","furigana":"ふかけつ"},{"japanese":"植物が育つには、水と光が不可欠です。","romaji":"Shokubutsu ga sodatsu ni wa, mizu to hikari ga fukaketsu desu.","german":"For plants to grow, water and light are essential.","furigana":"しょくぶつ|そだ|みず|ひかり|ふかけつ"},{"japanese":"今の生活に、スマホは必要不可欠なものになりました。","romaji":"Ima no seikatsu ni, sumaho wa hitsuyou fukaketsu na mono ni narimashita.","german":"Smartphones have become something indispensable in life today.","furigana":"いま|せいかつ|ひつようふかけつ"}],"notes":"不可欠 is composed of 不 \"not\", 可 \"possible\" and 欠 \"to lack\": \"must not be missing, essential\". Typical: 〜に不可欠だ, 〜にとって不可欠な, and the fixed combination 必要不可欠 \"absolutely necessary\". It is stronger and more formal than 必要; colloquially one says 欠かせない.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"多様な意見があります。","romaji":"Tayō na iken ga arimasu.","german":"There are diverse opinions.","furigana":"たよう|いけん"},{"japanese":"この町には、多様な文化を持つ人々が住んでいます。","romaji":"Kono machi ni wa, tayou na bunka o motsu hitobito ga sunde imasu.","german":"People with diverse cultures live in this town.","furigana":"まち|たよう|ぶんか|も|ひとびと|す"},{"japanese":"働き方が多様になってきました。","romaji":"Hatarakikata ga tayou ni natte kimashita.","german":"Ways of working have become more diverse.","furigana":"はたら|かた|たよう"}],"notes":"多様 is a な-adjective for \"diverse, varied\" and sounds rather written: 多様な文化・価値観・働き方. Related: 多様化 \"diversification\", 多様性 \"diversity\" and the set phrase 多種多様 \"of all kinds\". In conversation one usually says いろいろな or さまざまな.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"慎重に行動しましょう。","romaji":"Shinchō ni kōdō shimashō.","german":"Let's act carefully.","furigana":"しんちょう|こうどう"},{"japanese":"大事なことなので、慎重に考えてから決めます。","romaji":"Daiji na koto na node, shinchou ni kangaete kara kimemasu.","german":"It's an important matter, so I will decide after thinking it over carefully.","furigana":"だいじ|しんちょう|かんが|き"},{"japanese":"割れやすいので、慎重に運んでください。","romaji":"Wareyasui node, shinchou ni hakonde kudasai.","german":"It breaks easily, so please carry it carefully.","furigana":"わ|しんちょう|はこ"}],"notes":"慎重 is a な-adjective meaning \"careful, circumspect, deliberate\": one acts or decides only after thorough consideration: 慎重に検討する, 慎重に選ぶ, 慎重な性格. The opposite is 軽率 \"rash\". 気をつける is more about watchfulness toward dangers, 丁寧 about care and politeness. Do not confuse it with the identically read 身長 \"body height\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大胆な計画ですね。","romaji":"Daitan na keikaku desu ne.","german":"That's a bold plan.","furigana":"だいたん|けいかく"},{"japanese":"彼女は髪を大胆に短く切りました。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kami o daitan ni mijikaku kirimashita.","german":"She boldly had her hair cut short.","furigana":"かのじょ|かみ|だいたん|みじか|き"},{"japanese":"赤と緑を使った大胆なデザインが人気です。","romaji":"Aka to midori o tsukatta daitan na dezain ga ninki desu.","german":"A bold design using red and green is popular.","furigana":"あか|みどり|つか|だいたん|にんき"}],"notes":"大胆 is a な-adjective for \"bold, daring, fearless\" (literally \"big gall bladder\", in the sense of great courage). It can praise (大胆なアイデア, 大胆なデザイン) or sound slightly critical, \"audacious, reckless\". Often adverbial: 大胆に変える. Opposite: 慎重 or 臆病. 勇敢, by contrast, is bravery in the face of danger.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"画期的な発明です。","romaji":"Kakkiteki na hatsumei desu.","german":"It is a groundbreaking invention.","furigana":"かっきてき|はつめい"},{"japanese":"これは家事を楽にする画期的な商品です。","romaji":"Kore wa kaji o raku ni suru kakkiteki na shouhin desu.","german":"This is a groundbreaking product that makes housework easier.","furigana":"かじ|らく|かっきてき|しょうひん"},{"japanese":"インターネットの登場は、情報の伝え方を画期的に変えました。","romaji":"Intaanetto no toujou wa, jouhou no tsutaekata o kakkiteki ni kaemashita.","german":"The arrival of the internet changed the way information is conveyed in a revolutionary way.","furigana":"とうじょう|じょうほう|つた|かた|かっきてき|か"}],"notes":"画期的 is a な-adjective for \"groundbreaking, epoch-making\" - 画期 literally means \"to mark off an epoch\". It is always positive and typical of technology, inventions and advertising: 画期的な発明・技術・商品. Adverbial 画期的に変わる \"to change fundamentally\". Stronger than 新しい, similar to 革命的.","levelNote":"Compound: 画期 (N1) + 的 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"総合的に判断します。","romaji":"Sōgōteki ni handan shimasu.","german":"I will judge taking everything into account.","furigana":"そうごうてき|はんだん"},{"japanese":"総合的に見ると、この案がいちばんいいと思います。","romaji":"Sougouteki ni miru to, kono an ga ichiban ii to omoimasu.","german":"Taking everything into account, I think this proposal is the best.","furigana":"そうごうてき|み|あん|おも"},{"japanese":"会社は、試験と面接の結果を総合的に評価します。","romaji":"Kaisha wa, shiken to mensetsu no kekka o sougouteki ni hyouka shimasu.","german":"The company evaluates the results of the exam and interview comprehensively.","furigana":"かいしゃ|しけん|めんせつ|けっか|そうごうてき|ひょうか"}],"notes":"総合的 means \"comprehensive, holistic\" - looking at all factors together. Very common: 総合的に判断する／評価する／見る \"to judge all things considered\". Without 的, 総合 appears in compounds such as 総合病院 \"general hospital\" or 総合大学 \"comprehensive university\". 全体的 describes more the overall impression (\"on the whole\").","levelNote":"Compound: 総合 (N1) + 的 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"客観的に見てください。","romaji":"Kyakkanteki ni mite kudasai.","german":"Please look at it objectively.","furigana":"きゃっかんてき|み"},{"japanese":"自分の意見ではなく、客観的なデータを示してください。","romaji":"Jibun no iken de wa naku, kyakkanteki na deeta o shimeshite kudasai.","german":"Please present objective data, not your own opinion.","furigana":"じぶん|いけん|きゃっかんてき|しめ"},{"japanese":"友達に客観的な意見を聞いてみました。","romaji":"Tomodachi ni kyakkanteki na iken o kiite mimashita.","german":"I asked a friend for an objective opinion.","furigana":"ともだち|きゃっかんてき|いけん|き"}],"notes":"客観的 means \"objective, impartial, unbiased\": 客観的に見る／判断する, 客観的な事実・データ. The opposite is 主観的 \"subjective\". Related: 客観視する \"to view something from a distance\". The word sounds somewhat elevated, but is also common in everyday life, for example when asking for a neutral opinion.","levelNote":"Compound: 客観 (N1) + 的 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"それは主観的な意見です。","romaji":"Sore wa shukanteki na iken desu.","german":"That is a subjective opinion.","furigana":"しゅかんてき|いけん"},{"japanese":"味の好みは主観的なものです。","romaji":"Aji no konomi wa shukanteki na mono desu.","german":"Taste in flavor is a subjective matter.","furigana":"あじ|この|しゅかんてき"},{"japanese":"評価が主観的にならないように、基準を決めました。","romaji":"Hyouka ga shukanteki ni naranai you ni, kijun o kimemashita.","german":"We set criteria so that the evaluation would not be subjective.","furigana":"ひょうか|しゅかんてき|きじゅん|き"}],"notes":"主観的 means \"subjective, colored by personal feeling\": 主観的な意見・判断. Often slightly critical (主観的すぎる \"too subjective\"). Opposite: 客観的. Without 的 one often hears あくまで主観ですが \"this is only my personal impression\" as a modest introduction.","levelNote":"Compound: 主観 (N1) + 的 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"段々寒くなってきました。","romaji":"Dandan samuku natte kimashita.","german":"It has gradually gotten colder.","furigana":"だんだんさむ"},{"japanese":"日本語の勉強がだんだん楽しくなってきました。","romaji":"Nihongo no benkyou ga dandan tanoshiku natte kimashita.","german":"Studying Japanese has gradually become fun.","furigana":"にほんご|べんきょう|たの"},{"japanese":"雨はだんだん強くなるでしょう。","romaji":"Ame wa dandan tsuyoku naru deshou.","german":"The rain will gradually get stronger.","furigana":"あめ|つよ"}],"notes":"段々 means \"gradually, step by step, more and more\" and is mostly written in kana だんだん. Typical with verbs of change: だんだん〜くなる, 〜てくる／〜ていく. Spoken and neutral; more literary is 徐々に. どんどん, by contrast, emphasizes quick, rapid increase. As a noun, 段々 means \"steps, terraces\" (段々畑).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"徐々に回復しています。","romaji":"Jojo ni kaifuku shite imasu.","german":"I am gradually recovering.","furigana":"じょじょ|かいふく"},{"japanese":"新しい仕事にも徐々に慣れてきました。","romaji":"Atarashii shigoto ni mo jojo ni narete kimashita.","german":"I have gradually gotten used to my new job.","furigana":"あたら|しごと|じょじょ|な"},{"japanese":"薬の量を徐々に減らしていきます。","romaji":"Kusuri no ryou o jojo ni herashite ikimasu.","german":"I will gradually reduce the dose of the medicine.","furigana":"くすり|りょう|じょじょ|へ"}],"notes":"徐々に means \"gradually, step by step\" and is somewhat more formal than だんだん. It is used for slow, steady changes: 徐々に回復する, 徐々に増える／減らす, 徐々に慣れる. The に is an integral part. Opposite: 急に or 急激に. Typical in reports, medicine and the news, but also in polite conversation.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"温度が急激に下がりました。","romaji":"Ondo ga kyūgeki ni sagarimashita.","german":"The temperature dropped sharply.","furigana":"おんど|きゅうげき|さ"},{"japanese":"円安で、輸入品の値段が急激に上がりました。","romaji":"En'yasu de, yunyuuhin no nedan ga kyuugeki ni agarimashita.","german":"Because of the weak yen, the price of imported goods rose sharply.","furigana":"えんやす|ゆにゅうひん|ねだん|きゅうげき|あ"},{"japanese":"急激に体重を減らすのは体によくありません。","romaji":"Kyuugeki ni taijuu o herasu no wa karada ni yoku arimasen.","german":"Losing weight rapidly is not good for the body.","furigana":"きゅうげき|たいじゅう|へ|からだ"}],"notes":"急激に is the adverbial form of the な-adjective 急激 and means \"abruptly and strongly, drastically\". It is used for changes in numbers and states: 急激に増える／減る／上がる／下がる, also 急激な変化. 急に means only \"suddenly\" (starting without warning), and 急速に emphasizes the high speed of a development. Rather news and written language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"業績が著しく向上しました。","romaji":"Gyōseki ga ichijirushiku kōjō shimashita.","german":"Business performance improved markedly.","furigana":"ぎょうせき|いちじる|こうじょう"},{"japanese":"睡眠不足が続くと、集中力が著しく低下します。","romaji":"Suimin busoku ga tsuzuku to, shuuchuuryoku ga ichijirushiku teika shimasu.","german":"If lack of sleep continues, concentration declines markedly.","furigana":"すいみんぶそく|つづ|しゅうちゅうりょく|いちじる|ていか"},{"japanese":"二人の意見は著しく異なっていました。","romaji":"Futari no iken wa ichijirushiku kotonatte imashita.","german":"The two people's opinions differed markedly.","furigana":"ふたり|いけん|いちじる|こと"}],"notes":"著しく is the adverbial form of 著しい: \"considerably, strikingly strongly\". It is used with verbs of change and with adjectives: 著しく向上する／低下する, 著しく異なる. It only evaluates the extent and can therefore be positive or negative. Literary; in conversation people rather say かなり, すごく or 大きく.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"おおよそ千人が参加しました。","romaji":"Ōyoso sennin ga sanka shimashita.","german":"Approximately a thousand people took part.","furigana":"せんにん|さんか"},{"japanese":"駅から会場まで、おおよそ十分かかります。","romaji":"Eki kara kaijou made, ooyoso juppun kakarimasu.","german":"It takes roughly ten minutes from the station to the venue.","furigana":"えき|かいじょう|じゅっぷん"},{"japanese":"話のおおよその内容は分かりました。","romaji":"Hanashi no ooyoso no naiyou wa wakarimashita.","german":"I understood the general content of the talk.","furigana":"はなし|ないよう|わ"}],"notes":"おおよそ means \"approximately, about\" with numbers and quantities: おおよそ千人, おおよそ十分. As a noun with の it means \"the rough outline, the gist\": おおよその内容, おおよその見当がつく \"to have a rough idea\". Mostly written in kana (rarely 大凡). およそ is synonymous but somewhat more common; in conversation だいたい is usually used.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"少なくとも三日かかります。","romaji":"Sukunaku tomo mikka kakarimasu.","german":"It will take at least three days.","furigana":"すく|みっか"},{"japanese":"他の人は知りませんが、少なくとも私は賛成です。","romaji":"Hoka no hito wa shirimasen ga, sukunaku tomo watashi wa sansei desu.","german":"I don't know about the others, but I, at least, agree.","furigana":"ほか|ひと|し|すく|わたし|さんせい"},{"japanese":"少なくとも一日一回は窓を開けて、空気を入れ替えましょう。","romaji":"Sukunaku tomo ichinichi ikkai wa mado o akete, kuuki o irekaemashou.","german":"Let's open the window at least once a day to air out the room.","furigana":"すく|いちにちいっかい|まど|あ|くうき|い|か"}],"notes":"少なくとも means \"at least\" with quantities and times (少なくとも三日) and \"at any rate, at least\" as a qualification (少なくとも私は賛成だ \"I, at least, am in favor\"). In conversation you also hear 最低（でも）. せめて, by contrast, expresses a modest wish (\"at least ..., please\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"せいぜい十人くらいです。","romaji":"Seizei jūnin kurai desu.","german":"There are about ten people at most.","furigana":"じゅうにん"},{"japanese":"アルバイトでは、せいぜい月に五万円しか稼げません。","romaji":"Arubaito de wa, seizei tsuki ni goman'en shika kasegemasen.","german":"With a part-time job, you can earn at most 50,000 yen a month.","furigana":"つき|ごまんえん|かせ"},{"japanese":"今さら急いでも、せいぜい五分早く着くくらいだよ。","romaji":"Imasara isoide mo, seizei gofun hayaku tsuku kurai da yo.","german":"Even if you hurry now, you'll arrive five minutes earlier at best.","furigana":"いま|いそ|ごふんはや|つ"}],"notes":"せいぜい names an upper limit: \"at most, at best\", often with 〜ぐらい／程度 or 〜しか and a slightly disparaging tone (\"that's all you can get\"). In its second meaning, \"as best as one can\", せいぜい頑張って often sounds ironic or condescending. The kanji 精々 is rare. More neutral are 多くても or 最大で.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"やや難しいです。","romaji":"Yaya muzukashii desu.","german":"It is somewhat difficult.","furigana":"むずか"},{"japanese":"明日は、やや雲が多い天気になるでしょう。","romaji":"Ashita wa, yaya kumo ga ooi tenki ni naru deshou.","german":"Tomorrow will be a somewhat cloudy day.","furigana":"あした|くも|おお|てんき"},{"japanese":"このズボンは私にはやや大きいです。","romaji":"Kono zubon wa watashi ni wa yaya ookii desu.","german":"These trousers are a little big for me.","furigana":"わたし|おお"}],"notes":"やや means \"somewhat, a little\" and sounds objective to written, typical of weather reports (やや強い雨, 雲がやや多い) and of reports. In conversation, people mostly say ちょっと or 少し. Do not confuse it with ややこしい \"complicated, tricky\", which is a separate word.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"かえって悪くなりました。","romaji":"Kaette waruku narimashita.","german":"It got worse instead.","furigana":"わる"},{"japanese":"近道をしたら、かえって時間がかかってしまいました。","romaji":"Chikamichi o shitara, kaette jikan ga kakatte shimaimashita.","german":"I took a shortcut and ended up taking even more time.","furigana":"ちかみち|じかん"},{"japanese":"気を使われると、かえって緊張します。","romaji":"Ki o tsukawareru to, kaette kinchou shimasu.","german":"When people go out of their way for me, I actually get more nervous.","furigana":"き|つか|きんちょう"}],"notes":"かえって expresses that an action brings about the opposite of what was expected: \"on the contrary, all the more, instead\". Typical after 〜たら／〜と／〜ので: 手伝ったら、かえって迷惑だった. Mostly written in kana (rarely 却って). 逆に is more general, \"conversely\", and むしろ means \"rather, preferably\" in comparisons and preferences.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"たちまち有名になりました。","romaji":"Tachimachi yūmei ni narimashita.","german":"He became famous in no time.","furigana":"ゆうめい"},{"japanese":"新しいゲームは、発売日にたちまち売り切れました。","romaji":"Atarashii geemu wa, hatsubaibi ni tachimachi urikiremashita.","german":"The new game sold out in no time on its release day.","furigana":"あたら|はつばいび|う|き"},{"japanese":"空が暗くなったと思ったら、たちまち大雨になりました。","romaji":"Sora ga kuraku natta to omottara, tachimachi ooame ni narimashita.","german":"I thought the sky had gone dark, and then it was immediately pouring.","furigana":"そら|くら|おも|おおあめ"}],"notes":"たちまち means \"in an instant, in no time\" and mostly describes, after the fact, how quickly something happened: たちまち売り切れる, たちまち有名になる. It sounds narrative and somewhat literary. Unlike すぐ, it cannot be used for requests or plans (not: たちまち来てください). 突然 emphasizes the unexpected, not the speed.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いずれ分かるでしょう。","romaji":"Izure wakaru deshō.","german":"You will find out sooner or later.","furigana":"わ"},{"japanese":"いずれまた、ゆっくりお話ししましょう。","romaji":"Izure mata, yukkuri ohanashi shimashou.","german":"Let's talk at length again sometime.","furigana":"はな"},{"japanese":"いずれにしても、明日までに返事をください。","romaji":"Izure ni shite mo, ashita made ni henji o kudasai.","german":"In any case, please reply by tomorrow.","furigana":"あした|へんじ"}],"notes":"As an adverb, いずれ means \"sooner or later, someday\" (いずれ分かる) and often appears in polite farewells: いずれまた \"until we meet again sometime\". いずれにしても／いずれにせよ means \"in any case, at any rate\". In written language it is also a pronoun, \"which (of several)\": いずれの場合も \"in either case\", いずれも \"all, both\" (いずれも人気の商品です). いつか is vaguer and sounds more like a wish; the kanji spelling 何れ is rare and is also read どれ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あくまで個人の意見です。","romaji":"Akumade kojin no iken desu.","german":"It is only my personal opinion.","furigana":"こじん|いけん"},{"japanese":"これはあくまで予定なので、変わるかもしれません。","romaji":"Kore wa akumade yotei na node, kawaru kamo shiremasen.","german":"This is only a plan, so it may change.","furigana":"よてい|か"},{"japanese":"彼はあくまで自分の意見を変えませんでした。","romaji":"Kare wa akumade jibun no iken o kaemasen deshita.","german":"He never changed his opinion to the very end.","furigana":"かれ|じぶん|いけん|か"}],"notes":"あくまで (also あくまでも, rarely 飽くまで) has two uses: as a limitation, \"only, merely\" (あくまで個人の意見, あくまで予定です) - very often as a caveat - and \"to the end, persistently\" (あくまで反対する, あくまで戦う). In the first sense it resembles ただ／単に, but stresses that something should not be misunderstood.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"そもそも何が問題ですか。","romaji":"Somosomo nani ga mondai desu ka.","german":"What is the problem in the first place?","furigana":"なに|もんだい"},{"japanese":"そもそも、あなたが約束を忘れたのが悪いんですよ。","romaji":"Somosomo, anata ga yakusoku o wasureta no ga warui n desu yo.","german":"To begin with, it's your fault for forgetting the appointment.","furigana":"やくそく|わす|わる"},{"japanese":"事故のそもそもの原因は、確認不足でした。","romaji":"Jiko no somosomo no gen'in wa, kakunin busoku deshita.","german":"The original cause of the accident was a lack of checking.","furigana":"じこ|げんいん|かくにんぶそく"}],"notes":"そもそも goes back to the origin or basic premise: \"in the first place, actually, from the outset\". It often questions a premise and then sounds slightly accusatory (そもそもあなたが…). As a noun with の: そもそもの原因 \"the real cause\". 最初に, by contrast, only means \"first\" in a sequence.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"問題は依然として残っています。","romaji":"Mondai wa izen toshite nokotte imasu.","german":"The problem still remains.","furigana":"もんだい|いぜん|のこ"},{"japanese":"事故の原因は、依然として分かっていません。","romaji":"Jiko no gen'in wa, izen toshite wakatte imasen.","german":"The cause of the accident is still not known.","furigana":"じこ|げんいん|いぜん|わ"},{"japanese":"景気は回復してきたが、失業率は依然として高い。","romaji":"Keiki wa kaifuku shite kita ga, shitsugyouritsu wa izen toshite takai.","german":"The economy has recovered, but the unemployment rate is still high.","furigana":"けいき|かいふく|しつぎょうりつ|いぜん|たか"}],"notes":"依然として means \"still, as before\" and sounds written; it is typical of the news and reports, often with unchanged, mostly unpleasant conditions: 依然として続く, 依然として分からない. In conversation people say まだ or 相変わらず. Do not confuse it with the identically read 以前 \"earlier, before\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"明日伺います。","romaji":"Ashita ukagaimasu.","german":"I will visit tomorrow.","furigana":"あしたうかが"},{"japanese":"お名前を伺ってもよろしいですか。","romaji":"Onamae o ukagatte mo yoroshii desu ka.","german":"May I ask your name?","furigana":"なまえ|うかが"},{"japanese":"お話は田中様から伺っております。","romaji":"Ohanashi wa Tanaka-sama kara ukagatte orimasu.","german":"I have already heard about this from Mr. Tanaka.","furigana":"はなし|たなかさま|うかが"}],"notes":"伺う is humble language (謙譲語) and replaces 訪ねる \"to visit\" as well as 聞く \"to ask\" and \"to hear\" when the action concerns a respected person: 先生のお宅に伺う, お名前を伺う, お話を伺う. ちょっと伺いますが is a polite way to introduce a question. For simply going somewhere with no reference to the other person, 参る fits better, and 伺う is never used for the actions of others. Do not confuse it with 窺う \"to peek, to wait for an opening\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 20"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"田中と申します。","romaji":"Tanaka to mōshimasu.","german":"My name is Tanaka.","furigana":"たなか|もう"},{"japanese":"父がよろしくと申しておりました。","romaji":"Chichi ga yoroshiku to moushite orimashita.","german":"My father sends his best regards.","furigana":"ちち|もう"},{"japanese":"先ほど申しましたように、会議は三時からです。","romaji":"Sakihodo moushimashita you ni, kaigi wa sanji kara desu.","german":"As I said earlier, the meeting starts at three o'clock.","furigana":"さき|もう|かいぎ|さんじ"}],"notes":"申す is the humble form of 言う and is used for one's own speech or that of one's own family or company: 田中と申します \"my name is Tanaka\", 父がよろしくと申しておりました. 申し上げる is even more deferential and is addressed directly to the respected person (お願い申し上げます). For the speech of superiors, one uses おっしゃる. The word is also often found in 申し訳ありません \"I am very sorry\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 20"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"すぐに参ります。","romaji":"Sugu ni mairimasu.","german":"Ich komme sofort.","furigana":"まい"},{"japanese":"まもなく、二番線に電車が参ります。","romaji":"Mamonaku, nibansen ni densha ga mairimasu.","german":"Auf Gleis 2 fährt in Kürze ein Zug ein.","furigana":"にばんせん|でんしゃ|まい"},{"japanese":"ドイツから参りました、シュミットと申します。","romaji":"Doitsu kara mairimashita, Shumitto to moushimasu.","german":"Ich komme aus Deutschland und heiße Schmidt.","furigana":"まい|もう"}],"notes":"参る ist die bescheidene bzw. höflich-förmliche Form von 行く und 来る für das eigene Kommen und Gehen (すぐに参ります, ドイツから参りました). Im Unterschied zu 伺う, das einen Besuch bei der respektierten Person ausdrückt, macht 参る eine Aussage einfach höflicher, etwa in Bahnsteigdurchsagen (電車が参ります). Umgangssprachlich bedeutet 参った „ich gebe auf, das hat mich geschafft“. Für Höhergestellte verwendet man いらっしゃる.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 20"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"社長はいらっしゃいますか。","romaji":"Shachō wa irasshaimasu ka.","german":"Is the president in?","furigana":"しゃちょう"},{"japanese":"先生は来週京都へいらっしゃるそうです。","romaji":"Sensei wa raishuu Kyouto e irassharu sou desu.","german":"I hear the teacher is going to Kyoto next week.","furigana":"せんせい|らいしゅうきょうと"},{"japanese":"お客様がいらっしゃいました。","romaji":"Okyakusama ga irasshaimashita.","german":"A customer has arrived.","furigana":"きゃくさま"}],"notes":"いらっしゃる is the honorific verb (尊敬語) for 行く, 来る and いる – it elevates the person who goes, comes or is present, never oneself. The ます-form is irregular: いらっしゃいます (not いらっしゃります); in the て-form, besides いらっしゃって, the shorter いらして is often heard. As an auxiliary verb it replaces ている: 読んでいらっしゃる \"is reading\". The humble counterparts for one's own side are 参る (\"to go, to come\") and おる (\"to be\"); the shop greeting いらっしゃいませ also comes from this verb.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 19"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どうぞ召し上がってください。","romaji":"Dōzo meshiagatte kudasai.","german":"Please help yourself.","furigana":"め|あ"},{"japanese":"お飲み物は何を召し上がりますか。","romaji":"Onomimono wa nani o meshiagarimasu ka.","german":"What would you like to drink?","furigana":"の|もの|なに|め|あ"},{"japanese":"先生はお酒を召し上がりません。","romaji":"Sensei wa osake o meshiagarimasen.","german":"Our teacher does not drink alcohol.","furigana":"せんせい|さけ|め|あ"}],"notes":"召し上がる is the honorific verb (尊敬語) for 食べる and 飲む; the food or drink takes を. It is used for guests, customers and superiors, never for oneself – for one's own eating one says humble いただく. The invitations どうぞ召し上がってください and, even more politely, お召し上がりください are very common, for example in restaurants or on packaging. 何を召し上がりますか is the polite version of 何を食べますか.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 19"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"存じております。","romaji":"Zonjite orimasu.","german":"I am aware of it.","furigana":"ぞん"},{"japanese":"申し訳ございませんが、その件については存じません。","romaji":"Moushiwake gozaimasen ga, sono ken ni tsuite wa zonjimasen.","german":"I am very sorry, but I do not know about that matter.","furigana":"もう|わけ|けん|ぞん"},{"japanese":"ご協力いただければ幸いに存じます。","romaji":"Go-kyouryoku itadakereba saiwai ni zonjimasu.","german":"We would be grateful for your cooperation.","furigana":"きょうりょく|さいわ|ぞん"}],"notes":"存じる is the humble verb (謙譲語) for 知る \"to know\" and 思う \"to think\". It is used almost only in fixed forms: 存じております \"I know\", 存じません \"I am not aware of it\" (more polite than 知りません), 〜と存じます \"I think\" in business letters. When one knows a respected person, one says 存じ上げております. The respectful counterpart for others is ご存じ: ご存じですか.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お写真を拝見しました。","romaji":"Oshashin o haiken shimashita.","german":"I have looked at your photos.","furigana":"しゃしん|はいけん"},{"japanese":"切符を拝見します。","romaji":"Kippu o haiken shimasu.","german":"May I see your ticket?","furigana":"きっぷ|はいけん"},{"japanese":"メールを拝見しました。すぐにお返事します。","romaji":"Meeru o haiken shimashita. Sugu ni ohenji shimasu.","german":"I have read your e-mail. I will reply right away.","furigana":"はいけん|へんじ"}],"notes":"拝見する is the humble verb (謙譲語) for \"to look at, to read\" when one looks at something that belongs to the person one is talking to or to a superior: お手紙を拝見しました, 切符を拝見します \"your ticket, please\". It is used only for one's own action, never for the other person's; for that one says honorific ご覧になる. In neutral language it corresponds to 見る or 読む. It is transitive with を.","levelNote":"Derived from 拝見 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先生はもうこの資料をご覧になりましたか。","romaji":"Sensei wa mō kono shiryō o goran ni narimashita ka.","german":"Has the teacher already looked at these materials?","furigana":"せんせい|しりょう|らん"},{"japanese":"どうぞ、こちらの絵をご覧になってください。","romaji":"Dōzo, kochira no e o goran ni natte kudasai.","german":"Please take a look at this picture here.","furigana":"え|らん"}],"notes":"ご覧になる is the honorific form of 見る. It is used for seeing or looking by a person one wants to show respect to, not for one's own action. The object takes を. The expression is conjugated via なる like a Godan verb: ご覧になりました, ご覧になってください. The meaning \"to try something\" after a te-form does not belong to these examples.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 19"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"チャンスを掴みました。","romaji":"Chansu o tsukamimashita.","german":"I seized the chance.","furigana":"つか"},{"japanese":"電車が揺れたので、近くの人の腕を掴んでしまいました。","romaji":"Densha ga yureta node, chikaku no hito no ude o tsukande shimaimashita.","german":"The train shook, so I ended up grabbing the arm of a person nearby.","furigana":"でんしゃ|ゆ|ちか|ひと|うで|つか"},{"japanese":"何度か練習して、やっとコツを掴みました。","romaji":"Nando ka renshuu shite, yatto kotsu o tsukamimashita.","german":"After practicing several times, I finally got the knack.","furigana":"なんど|れんしゅう|つか"}],"notes":"掴む (often つかむ) means \"to grab, to grasp\" (腕を掴む), figuratively \"to seize, to obtain\" (チャンスを掴む), \"to grasp\" (要点・コツを掴む \"to get the hang of it\") and \"to win\" (人の心を掴む). 握る is to hold firmly in the hand, 捕まえる to catch something that is fleeing. Intransitive partner: 掴まる \"to hold on\" (つり革に掴まる).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"多くの問題を抱えています。","romaji":"Ōku no mondai o kakaete imasu.","german":"I have many problems.","furigana":"おお|もんだい|かか"},{"japanese":"彼は大きな荷物を両手に抱えて、駅に向かいました。","romaji":"Kare wa ookina nimotsu o ryoute ni kakaete, eki ni mukaimashita.","german":"He headed to the station carrying a large piece of luggage in both arms.","furigana":"かれ|おお|にもつ|りょうて|かか|えき|む"},{"japanese":"テストの結果を見て、頭を抱えました。","romaji":"Tesuto no kekka o mite, atama o kakaemashita.","german":"Seeing the test results, I held my head in my hands.","furigana":"けっか|み|あたま|かか"}],"notes":"抱える first means \"to hold in the arms, to carry under the arm\" (荷物を抱える); 頭を抱える means \"to rack one's brains, to despair\". Figuratively: \"to have (problems, debts, worries), to be burdened with\": 問題・借金・悩みを抱える. Also \"to employ, to have to support\" (社員を抱える). Do not confuse it with 抱く（だく）\"to embrace\" - same kanji, different reading.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"家族を支えます。","romaji":"Kazoku o sasaemasu.","german":"I support my family.","furigana":"かぞく|ささ"},{"japanese":"倒れそうなおばあさんの体を支えました。","romaji":"Taoresou na obaasan no karada o sasaemashita.","german":"I supported the body of an elderly woman who was about to fall.","furigana":"たお|からだ|ささ"},{"japanese":"辛いとき、友達の言葉が私を支えてくれました。","romaji":"Tsurai toki, tomodachi no kotoba ga watashi o sasaete kuremashita.","german":"When times were hard, my friend's words supported me.","furigana":"つら|ともだち|ことば|わたし|ささ"}],"notes":"支える means physically \"to support, to hold\" (倒れないように支える) and figuratively \"to support, to sustain\" - emotionally, financially or as a foundation: 家族を支える, 経済を支える. The noun 支え means \"support, backing\" (心の支え). 助ける is \"to help, to rescue\", 手伝う \"to help with a task\", 応援する \"to cheer on, to support\". Often in the passive: 〜に支えられる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"家族を養っています。","romaji":"Kazoku o yashinatte imasu.","german":"I support my family.","furigana":"かぞく|やしな"},{"japanese":"毎日走って体力を養っています。","romaji":"Mainichi hashitte tairyoku o yashinatte imasu.","german":"I run every day to build up my stamina.","furigana":"まいにちはし|たいりょく|やしな"},{"japanese":"本を読むと、考える力が養われます。","romaji":"Hon o yomu to, kangaeru chikara ga yashinawaremasu.","german":"Reading cultivates the power of thought.","furigana":"ほん|よ|かんが|ちから|やしな"}],"notes":"養う means \"to support (someone), to provide for their livelihood\": 家族を養う, 妻と子供を養う. It is also used for developing abilities and qualities: 体力を養う, 想像力を養う, 考える力を養う - often in the passive 養われる. It is somewhat more elevated than 育てる \"to raise\"; keeping pets is 飼う. Also fixed is 英気を養う \"to recharge one's energy\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"信頼関係を築きます。","romaji":"Shinrai kankei o kizukimasu.","german":"I will build a relationship of trust.","furigana":"しんらいかんけい|きず"},{"japanese":"二人は幸せな家庭を築きました。","romaji":"Futari wa shiawase na katei o kizukimashita.","german":"The two of them built a happy home.","furigana":"ふたり|しあわ|かてい|きず"},{"japanese":"この城は四百年前に築かれました。","romaji":"Kono shiro wa yonhyakunen mae ni kizukaremashita.","german":"This castle was built four hundred years ago.","furigana":"しろ|よんひゃくねんまえ|きず"}],"notes":"築く literally means \"to build (walls, dikes, castles)\": 城を築く, 石垣を築く. Much more common today is the figurative meaning: to build up relationships, trust, a fortune or a position step by step - 信頼関係を築く, 家庭を築く, 地位を築く, 財産を築く. For ordinary buildings, people say 建てる; 築く emphasizes that something is built up laboriously and lastingly.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"常識を覆しました。","romaji":"Jōshiki o kutsugaeshimashita.","german":"It overturned conventional wisdom.","furigana":"じょうしき|くつがえ"},{"japanese":"裁判所は前の判決を覆しました。","romaji":"Saibansho wa mae no hanketsu o kutsugaeshimashita.","german":"The court overturned the earlier ruling.","furigana":"さいばんしょ|まえ|はんけつ|くつがえ"},{"japanese":"弱いチームが予想を覆して優勝しました。","romaji":"Yowai chiimu ga yosou o kutsugaeshite yuushou shimashita.","german":"The weak team defied expectations and won the championship.","furigana":"よわ|よそう|くつがえ|ゆうしょう"}],"notes":"覆す means \"to turn over, to tip over\" and above all, figuratively, \"to overturn, to throw out\". Typical combinations: 常識を覆す \"to turn conventional wisdom upside down\", 判決を覆す \"to overturn a verdict\", 予想を覆す \"to defy all predictions\", 政権を覆す \"to topple a government\". The intransitive counterpart is 覆る (判決が覆る). For tipped-over objects, everyday speech tends to use ひっくり返す; 覆す sounds literary.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"交通を妨げないでください。","romaji":"Kōtsū o samatagenaide kudasai.","german":"Please do not obstruct traffic.","furigana":"こうつう|さまた"},{"japanese":"近所の工事の音が睡眠を妨げています。","romaji":"Kinjo no kouji no oto ga suimin o samatagete imasu.","german":"The noise from nearby construction is disturbing my sleep.","furigana":"きんじょ|こうじ|おと|すいみん|さまた"},{"japanese":"スマホの使いすぎは勉強の妨げになります。","romaji":"Sumaho no tsukaisugi wa benkyou no samatage ni narimasu.","german":"Using your smartphone too much gets in the way of studying.","furigana":"つか|べんきょう|さまた"}],"notes":"妨げる means \"to hinder, to disturb\" and takes を: 交通を妨げる, 睡眠を妨げる. The word sounds written and formal and is typical for signs, news and regulations; in conversation 邪魔する is more usual. The noun 妨げ is very common in 〜の妨げになる \"to be a hindrance to\". Do not confuse it with 防ぐ (\"to prevent, to ward off\", e.g. accidents or illnesses).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"危険を避けましょう。","romaji":"Kiken o sakemashō.","german":"Let's avoid danger.","furigana":"きけん|さ"},{"japanese":"混雑を避けるために、朝早く出かけました。","romaji":"Konzatsu o sakeru tame ni, asa hayaku dekakemashita.","german":"To avoid the crowds, I left early in the morning.","furigana":"こんざつ|さ|あさはや|で"},{"japanese":"最近、彼女は私を避けているみたいです。","romaji":"Saikin, kanojo wa watashi o sakete iru mitai desu.","german":"Recently she seems to be avoiding me.","furigana":"さいきん|かのじょ|わたし|さ"}],"notes":"避ける (さける) means \"to avoid, to steer clear of\", especially with situations, topics and people: 危険を避ける, 人混みを避ける, その話題を避ける, 彼は私を避けている. With 〜のを避ける an action is avoided; 避けられない means \"unavoidable\". The same kanji is also read よける - that means physically dodging (車をよける, 水たまりをよける) and is mostly written in kana. In notices, 〜は避けてください serves as a polite prohibition.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい課題に取り組みます。","romaji":"Atarashii kadai ni torikumimasu.","german":"I will tackle a new challenge.","furigana":"あたら|かだい|と|く"},{"japanese":"市は環境問題に積極的に取り組んでいます。","romaji":"Shi wa kankyou mondai ni sekkyokuteki ni torikunde imasu.","german":"The city is actively tackling environmental problems.","furigana":"し|かんきょうもんだい|せっきょくてき|と|く"},{"japanese":"試験に向けて、毎日真剣に勉強に取り組んでいます。","romaji":"Shiken ni mukete, mainichi shinken ni benkyou ni torikunde imasu.","german":"I am studying seriously every day for the exam.","furigana":"しけん|む|まいにちしんけん|べんきょう|と|く"}],"notes":"取り組む means \"to devote oneself seriously to a task, to tackle something\" and takes に: 課題に取り組む, 環境問題に取り組む, 研究に取り組む. It stresses active, persistent commitment and is very common in school, work and politics; the noun 取り組み \"initiative, effort\" is just as widespread. Originally it means \"to grapple with each other\" (in sumo: 取組 \"bout\"). Not to be confused with 取り込む \"to take in, to incorporate\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"困難を乗り越えました。","romaji":"Konnan o norikoemashita.","german":"I overcame the difficulties.","furigana":"こんなん|の|こ"},{"japanese":"泥棒は塀を乗り越えて逃げました。","romaji":"Dorobou wa hei o norikoete nigemashita.","german":"The thief climbed over the wall and ran away.","furigana":"どろぼう|へい|の|こ|に"},{"japanese":"家族の支えがあったから、つらい時期を乗り越えられました。","romaji":"Kazoku no sasae ga atta kara, tsurai jiki o norikoeraremashita.","german":"Thanks to my family's support, I got through the hard times.","furigana":"かぞく|ささ|じき|の|こ"}],"notes":"乗り越える literally means \"to climb over something\" (塀を乗り越える) and figuratively \"to overcome, to get through\": 困難を乗り越える, 壁を乗り越える, 悲しみを乗り越える. Although it is considered intransitive, the obstacle takes を, as with verbs of movement. Unlike 克服する, which is more literary and often used for weaknesses or illnesses, 乗り越える sounds more vivid and emotional; it often appears with みんなで or 力を合わせて.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"売上の増加を見込んでいます。","romaji":"Uriage no zōka o mikonde imasu.","german":"We anticipate an increase in sales.","furigana":"うりあげ|ぞうか|みこ"},{"japanese":"渋滞を見込んで、一時間早く家を出ました。","romaji":"Juutai o mikonde, ichijikan hayaku ie o demashita.","german":"I allowed for a traffic jam and left home an hour early.","furigana":"じゅうたい|みこ|いちじかんはや|いえ|で"},{"japanese":"君の力を見込んで、この仕事を頼みたいんだ。","romaji":"Kimi no chikara o mikonde, kono shigoto o tanomitai n da.","german":"I have confidence in your ability, so I'd like to ask you to take this job.","furigana":"きみ|ちから|みこ|しごと|たの"}],"notes":"見込む means \"to expect in advance, to factor in, to estimate\" and belongs to the language of business and planning: 売上の増加を見込む, 渋滞を見込んで早めに出る. It also means \"to have confidence in someone\": 君を見込んで頼む. The noun 見込み \"prospect, forecast\" is very common (〜する見込みだ, 見込みがない). Unlike 期待する \"to hope\", 見込む is sober and calculating.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"結果を踏まえて判断します。","romaji":"Kekka o fumaete handan shimasu.","german":"I will make a judgment based on the results.","furigana":"けっか|ふ|はんだん"},{"japanese":"皆さんの意見を踏まえて、計画を見直します。","romaji":"Minasan no iken o fumaete, keikaku o minaoshimasu.","german":"Taking everyone's opinions into account, we will review the plan.","furigana":"みな|いけん|ふ|けいかく|みなお"},{"japanese":"去年の失敗を踏まえた対策が必要です。","romaji":"Kyonen no shippai o fumaeta taisaku ga hitsuyou desu.","german":"Measures that take last year's failure into account are necessary.","furigana":"きょねん|しっぱい|ふ|たいさく|ひつよう"}],"notes":"踏まえる means \"to build on something, to take something into account\" and appears almost always as 〜を踏まえて or attributively as 〜を踏まえた: 結果を踏まえて, 経験を踏まえた提案. It belongs to formal usage in meetings, reports and news. Unlike 〜に基づいて \"on the basis of\", 踏まえる stresses that one considers what is known and draws conclusions from it. The literal meaning \"to step firmly\" is rare.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"経験を活かします。","romaji":"Keiken o ikashimasu.","german":"I will make use of my experience.","furigana":"けいけん|い"},{"japanese":"自分の長所を活かせる仕事を探しています。","romaji":"Jibun no chousho o ikaseru shigoto o sagashite imasu.","german":"I am looking for a job where I can make use of my strengths.","furigana":"じぶん|ちょうしょ|い|しごと|さが"},{"japanese":"この料理は野菜の味を活かしています。","romaji":"Kono ryouri wa yasai no aji o ikashite imasu.","german":"This dish brings out the flavor of the vegetables.","furigana":"りょうり|やさい|あじ|い"}],"notes":"活かす is a variant spelling of 生かす in the meaning \"to make use of, to bring out\": 経験を活かす, 特技を活かす, 素材の味を活かす. The spelling with 活 is often chosen when it is about putting abilities and resources to use; for \"to keep alive\" only 生かす is written. Unlike 利用する \"to use\", 活かす stresses that potential is fully realized. Typical is 〜を活かして \"making use of ...\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"才能を生かす仕事をしたいです。","romaji":"Sainō o ikasu shigoto o shitai desu.","german":"I want a job that makes use of my talent.","furigana":"さいのう|い|しごと"},{"japanese":"釣った魚を水に入れて、生かしておきました。","romaji":"Tsutta sakana o mizu ni irete, ikashite okimashita.","german":"I put the fish I caught in water and kept them alive.","furigana":"つ|さかな|みず|い|い"},{"japanese":"大学で学んだことを仕事に生かしたいです。","romaji":"Daigaku de mananda koto o shigoto ni ikashitai desu.","german":"I want to put what I learned at university to use in my work.","furigana":"だいがく|まな|しごと|い"}],"notes":"生かす is the transitive counterpart of 生きる \"to live\" and first means \"to let live, to keep alive\": 魚を生かしておく. In everyday life, however, \"to make use of, to bring out\" is far more common: 才能を生かす, 経験を仕事に生かす. In this meaning the spelling 活かす is also widespread, but 生かす is regarded as the standard spelling. The figurative opposite is 殺す: 才能を殺す \"to let a talent go to waste\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"全ての人に当てはまるわけではありません。","romaji":"Subete no hito ni atehamaru wake dewa arimasen.","german":"It does not apply to everyone.","furigana":"すべ|ひと|あ"},{"japanese":"次の質問で、当てはまるものに丸をつけてください。","romaji":"Tsugi no shitsumon de, atehamaru mono ni maru o tsukete kudasai.","german":"In the following questions, please circle the ones that apply.","furigana":"つぎ|しつもん|あ|まる"},{"japanese":"この説明は日本の若者にも当てはまると思います。","romaji":"Kono setsumei wa Nihon no wakamono ni mo atehamaru to omoimasu.","german":"I think this explanation also applies to young people in Japan.","furigana":"せつめい|にほん|わかもの|あ|おも"}],"notes":"当てはまる means \"to apply, to fit, to be applicable\" and takes に: この規則はすべての場合に当てはまる. Typical for questionnaires is 当てはまるものに丸をつけてください \"circle what applies\". The transitive counterpart is 当てはめる \"to apply, to fit in\" (公式に数字を当てはめる). It is often written in kana as well (あてはまる); that something physically fits (clothes, a key) is expressed with 合う or はまる instead.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"それは当たり前のことです。","romaji":"Sore wa atarimae no koto desu.","german":"That goes without saying.","furigana":"あ|まえ"},{"japanese":"「手伝ってくれる？」「当たり前だよ！」","romaji":"\"Tetsudatte kureru?\" \"Atarimae da yo!\"","german":"\"Will you help me?\" \"Of course!\"","furigana":"てつだ|あ|まえ"},{"japanese":"毎日ご飯が食べられるのは当たり前ではありません。","romaji":"Mainichi gohan ga taberareru no wa atarimae de wa arimasen.","german":"Being able to eat every day is not something to be taken for granted.","furigana":"まいにち|はん|た|あ|まえ"}],"notes":"当たり前 means \"obvious, natural, a matter of course\" and is used as a な-adjective or with の: 当たり前のこと, 〜のは当たり前だ. In casual speech people answer with 当たり前だよ! or 当たり前じゃん \"Of course!\". It sounds more everyday and sometimes blunter than the literary 当然. It can also mean \"ordinary, normal\": 当たり前の生活 \"a perfectly normal life\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この仕事に相応しい人を探します。","romaji":"Kono shigoto ni fusawashii hito o sagashimasu.","german":"I will look for a person suited to this job.","furigana":"しごと|ふさわ|ひと|さが"},{"japanese":"結婚式にふさわしい服を買いに行きます。","romaji":"Kekkonshiki ni fusawashii fuku o kai ni ikimasu.","german":"I'm going to buy clothes appropriate for a wedding.","furigana":"けっこんしき|ふく|か|い"},{"japanese":"彼の態度は先生としてふさわしくないと思います。","romaji":"Kare no taido wa sensei to shite fusawashiku nai to omoimasu.","german":"I think his attitude is not fitting for a teacher.","furigana":"かれ|たいど|せんせい|おも"}],"notes":"ふさわしい means \"suitable, appropriate, worthy\" and takes に: この仕事にふさわしい人, 場にふさわしい服装. It is mostly written in kana; the kanji 相応しい are correct but rarer. Compared with 合う or 適切な, ふさわしい is more elevated and carries a judgment (\"worthy of something\"). The negative ふさわしくない means \"unsuitable, inappropriate\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"望ましい結果が得られました。","romaji":"Nozomashii kekka ga eraremashita.","german":"Desirable results were obtained.","furigana":"のぞ|けっか|え"},{"japanese":"薬は食後に飲むことが望ましいです。","romaji":"Kusuri wa shokugo ni nomu koto ga nozomashii desu.","german":"It is advisable to take the medicine after meals.","furigana":"くすり|しょくご|の|のぞ"},{"japanese":"この仕事は、経験のある人が望ましいです。","romaji":"Kono shigoto wa, keiken no aru hito ga nozomashii desu.","german":"For this job, someone with experience is preferable.","furigana":"しごと|けいけん|ひと|のぞ"}],"notes":"望ましい means \"desirable, preferable\" and comes from 望む \"to wish\". Typical patterns are 〜ことが望ましい and 〜のが望ましい, often in rules, recommendations and job ads (経験者が望ましい \"experience preferred\"). It sounds factual and formal; in conversation one tends to say 〜たほうがいい. Not to be confused with 好ましい, which expresses more \"likable, pleasant\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"好ましい変化がありました。","romaji":"Konomashii henka ga arimashita.","german":"There was a favorable change.","furigana":"この|へんか"},{"japanese":"彼の明るい性格はとても好ましいです。","romaji":"Kare no akarui seikaku wa totemo konomashii desu.","german":"His cheerful personality is very likable.","furigana":"かれ|あか|せいかく|この"},{"japanese":"授業中にスマホを使うのは好ましくありません。","romaji":"Jugyouchuu ni sumaho o tsukau no wa konomashiku arimasen.","german":"Using a smartphone during class is not desirable.","furigana":"じゅぎょうちゅう|つか|この"}],"notes":"好ましい means \"pleasant, likable, welcome\" and describes a positive impression: 好ましい人物, 好ましい変化. The negative 好ましくない \"undesirable, frowned upon\" is very common, for example for behavior: その態度は好ましくない. Compared with 望ましい (\"desirable as a goal\"), 好ましい stresses the assessment as pleasant. It is more formal than 好きな or いい.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"やむを得ない事情があります。","romaji":"Yamu o enai jijō ga arimasu.","german":"There are unavoidable circumstances.","furigana":"え|じじょう"},{"japanese":"雨のため、試合はやむを得ず中止になりました。","romaji":"Ame no tame, shiai wa yamu o ezu chuushi ni narimashita.","german":"Because of the rain, the match was unavoidably canceled.","furigana":"あめ|しあい|え|ちゅうし"},{"japanese":"遅刻はやむを得ない場合を除き、認められません。","romaji":"Chikoku wa yamu o enai baai o nozoki, mitomeraremasen.","german":"Lateness is not accepted except in unavoidable cases.","furigana":"ちこく|え|ばあい|のぞ|みと"}],"notes":"やむを得ない means \"unavoidable, cannot be changed\", literally something like \"one cannot stop it\". Typical are やむを得ない事情 \"unavoidable circumstances\" and the adverbial form やむを得ず \"out of necessity\". It is more formal than 仕方がない or しょうがない and often appears in apologies and official notices: やむを得ない場合を除き …. The spelling 止むを得ない is less common.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"否定的な意見が多いです。","romaji":"Hiteiteki na iken ga ōi desu.","german":"There are many negative opinions.","furigana":"ひていてき|いけん|おお"},{"japanese":"社長はその計画に否定的です。","romaji":"Shachou wa sono keikaku ni hiteiteki desu.","german":"The president is negative about the plan.","furigana":"しゃちょう|けいかく|ひていてき"},{"japanese":"彼は何でも否定的に考えてしまいます。","romaji":"Kare wa nan demo hiteiteki ni kangaete shimaimasu.","german":"He tends to see everything negatively.","furigana":"かれ|なん|ひていてき|かんが"}],"notes":"否定的 is a な-adjective meaning \"negative, dismissive\": 否定的な意見, 否定的な態度, 〜に否定的だ \"to take a negative view of something\". The suffix 的 turns the noun 否定 \"denial\" into an adjective; the opposite is 肯定的. In conversation you often hear the loanword ネガティブ, which tends to describe a generally pessimistic attitude, while 否定的 refers to a negative stance toward a particular matter.","levelNote":"Compound: 否定 (N3) + 的 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"肯定的に考えましょう。","romaji":"Kōteiteki ni kangaemashō.","german":"Let's think positively.","furigana":"こうていてき|かんが"},{"japanese":"アンケートでは、肯定的な意見が多かったです。","romaji":"Ankeeto de wa, kouteiteki na iken ga ookatta desu.","german":"In the survey, there were many positive opinions.","furigana":"こうていてき|いけん|おお"},{"japanese":"両親は私の留学に肯定的でした。","romaji":"Ryoushin wa watashi no ryuugaku ni kouteiteki deshita.","german":"My parents were positive about my studying abroad.","furigana":"りょうしん|わたし|りゅうがく|こうていてき"}],"notes":"肯定的 is a na-adjective: \"affirmative, approving, positive\". Typical are 肯定的な意見, 肯定的な評価 and 〜に肯定的だ \"to be positive about something\"; adverbially 肯定的に考える/捉える \"to see positively\". The opposite is 否定的. For an optimistic attitude, everyday speech prefers 前向き or ポジティブ; 肯定的 sounds more matter-of-fact and is typical for surveys, reports and discussions.","levelNote":"Compound: 肯定 (N2) + 的 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"根本的な解決が必要です。","romaji":"Konponteki na kaiketsu ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"A fundamental solution is needed.","furigana":"こんぽんてき|かいけつ|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"二人の考え方は根本的に違います。","romaji":"Futari no kangaekata wa konponteki ni chigaimasu.","german":"The two of them think fundamentally differently.","furigana":"ふたり|かんが|かた|こんぽんてき|ちが"},{"japanese":"事故の根本的な原因を調べています。","romaji":"Jiko no konponteki na gen'in o shirabete imasu.","german":"They are investigating the root cause of the accident.","furigana":"じこ|こんぽんてき|げんいん|しら"}],"notes":"根本的 is a na-adjective: \"fundamental, from the ground up\", derived from 根本 \"root, foundation\". Common are 根本的な問題, 根本的な原因, 根本的な解決 and adverbially 根本的に違う/見直す. It emphasizes going to the root of a matter - as opposed to superficial or temporary solutions (表面的, 一時的). Similar, but not the same, is 基本的 \"basically, in principle\".","levelNote":"Compound: 根本 (N2) + 的 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"抽象的な表現は避けましょう。","romaji":"Chūshōteki na hyōgen wa sakemashō.","german":"Let's avoid abstract expressions.","furigana":"ちゅうしょうてき|ひょうげん|さ"},{"japanese":"彼の説明は抽象的すぎて、よくわかりませんでした。","romaji":"Kare no setsumei wa chuushouteki sugite, yoku wakarimasen deshita.","german":"His explanation was too abstract, and I hardly understood it.","furigana":"かれ|せつめい|ちゅうしょうてき"},{"japanese":"美術館で抽象的な絵を見ました。","romaji":"Bijutsukan de chuushouteki na e o mimashita.","german":"I saw abstract paintings at the museum.","furigana":"びじゅつかん|ちゅうしょうてき|え|み"}],"notes":"抽象的 is a na-adjective: \"abstract, not concrete\". Typical are 抽象的な表現, 抽象的な説明 and 抽象的すぎてわからない. The opposite is 具体的 \"concrete\" - the pair is often heard when someone asks for examples: もっと具体的に説明してください. In art, abstract painting is 抽象画.","levelNote":"Compound: 抽象 (N2) + 的 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"従来の方法を改善します。","romaji":"Jūrai no hōhō o kaizen shimasu.","german":"We will improve the conventional method.","furigana":"じゅうらい|ほうほう|かいぜん"},{"japanese":"新しいモデルは従来より三割軽くなりました。","romaji":"Atarashii moderu wa juurai yori sanwari karuku narimashita.","german":"The new model is 30 percent lighter than before.","furigana":"あたら|じゅうらい|さんわりかる"},{"japanese":"会議は従来どおり月曜日に行います。","romaji":"Kaigi wa juurai doori getsuyoubi ni okonaimasu.","german":"The meeting will be held on Monday as usual.","furigana":"かいぎ|じゅうらい|げつようび|おこな"}],"notes":"従来 means \"up to now, so far\" and with の \"conventional, previous\": 従来の方法, 従来の製品, 従来どおり \"as before\". It belongs to formal and written style, for example in business reports and product descriptions (従来より軽い \"lighter than before\"). In conversation one tends to say 今までの or これまでの. Although it is listed as an adverb, it is mostly used like a noun with の or より.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今後も努力します。","romaji":"Kongo mo doryoku shimasu.","german":"I will keep making an effort from now on, too.","furigana":"こんご|どりょく"},{"japanese":"今後このようなことがないよう、気をつけます。","romaji":"Kongo kono you na koto ga nai you, ki o tsukemasu.","german":"I will be careful so that nothing like this happens again.","furigana":"こんご|き"},{"japanese":"今後ともよろしくお願いいたします。","romaji":"Kongo tomo yoroshiku onegai itashimasu.","german":"We look forward to your continued support.","furigana":"こんご|ねが"}],"notes":"今後 means \"from now on, in the future\" and is more formal than これから. It stands alone as an adverb (今後気をつけます) or with の before nouns (今後の予定, 今後の課題). It is very common in apologies and business correspondence, e.g. 今後ともよろしくお願いいたします \"we look forward to your continued support\". Unlike 将来 \"the (more distant) future\", 今後 means the time from now onward.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"当面はこのままで行きます。","romaji":"Tōmen wa kono mama de ikimasu.","german":"For now, we will carry on as we are.","furigana":"とうめん|い"},{"japanese":"店は当面の間、休業いたします。","romaji":"Mise wa toumen no aida, kyuugyou itashimasu.","german":"The shop will remain closed for the time being.","furigana":"みせ|とうめん|あいだ|きゅうぎょう"},{"japanese":"当面の生活費はアルバイトで何とかします。","romaji":"Toumen no seikatsuhi wa arubaito de nantoka shimasu.","german":"I will somehow manage my immediate living expenses with a part-time job.","furigana":"とうめん|せいかつひ|なん"}],"notes":"当面 as an adverb means \"for the time being, until further notice, for now\": 当面は休業します, 当面の間. With の before nouns it means \"current, pressing\": 当面の課題, 当面の生活費. It is more formal than とりあえず or しばらく and is typical of announcements and news. Its use as a する-verb meaning \"to face\" is rare; people usually say 直面する instead.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一概には言えません。","romaji":"Ichigai ni wa iemasen.","german":"You can't say that across the board.","furigana":"いちがい|い"},{"japanese":"ゲームが一概に悪いとは言えません。","romaji":"Geemu ga ichigai ni warui to wa iemasen.","german":"You can't say categorically that games are bad.","furigana":"いちがい|わる|い"},{"japanese":"値段が高いから一概に良い物だとは限りません。","romaji":"Nedan ga takai kara ichigai ni yoi mono da to wa kagirimasen.","german":"Just because the price is high doesn't mean it is necessarily good.","furigana":"ねだん|たか|いちがい|よ|もの|かぎ"}],"notes":"一概に means \"across the board, without distinction\" and is almost always used with a negation: 一概には言えない \"you can't say that so generally\", 一概に悪いとは言えない. は is often inserted (一概には). Similar is 必ずしも〜ない; 一概に, however, stresses that one cannot lump all cases together. Neutral to formal and very common in discussions.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"必ずしも正しいとは限りません。","romaji":"Kanarazu shimo tadashii to wa kagirimasen.","german":"It is not necessarily correct.","furigana":"かなら|ただ|かぎ"},{"japanese":"お金があれば必ずしも幸せになれるわけではありません。","romaji":"Okane ga areba kanarazu shimo shiawase ni nareru wake de wa arimasen.","german":"Having money does not necessarily make you happy.","furigana":"かね|かなら|しあわ"},{"japanese":"高い薬が必ずしもよく効くとは限りません。","romaji":"Takai kusuri ga kanarazu shimo yoku kiku to wa kagirimasen.","german":"Expensive medicine does not necessarily work better.","furigana":"たか|くすり|かなら|き|かぎ"}],"notes":"必ずしも means \"not necessarily\" and is always combined with a negation, usually 〜とは限らない or 〜わけではない: 必ずしも正しいとは限らない. Without a negation it is wrong - do not confuse it with 必ず \"certainly, without fail\". It sounds somewhat more formal than いつも〜とは限らない and is very common in discussions and texts.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"果たして成功するでしょうか。","romaji":"Hatashite seikō suru deshō ka.","german":"Will it really succeed?","furigana":"は|せいこう"},{"japanese":"果たしてこの方法で問題が解決するのだろうか。","romaji":"Hatashite kono houhou de mondai ga kaiketsu suru no darou ka.","german":"I wonder whether this method will really solve the problem.","furigana":"は|ほうほう|もんだい|かいけつ"},{"japanese":"雨が降るかもしれないと思っていたら、果たして午後から降り出した。","romaji":"Ame ga furu kamo shirenai to omotte itara, hatashite gogo kara furidashita.","german":"I thought it might rain, and sure enough, it started raining in the afternoon.","furigana":"あめ|ふ|おも|は|ごご|ふ|だ"}],"notes":"果たして is mostly used in questions or doubts and strengthens the uncertainty: 果たして〜だろうか \"I wonder whether … really …?\". Less often it means \"as expected, sure enough\": 果たして雨になった. It sounds literary and elevated; in conversation one says 本当に〜かな instead. Do not confuse it with the て-form of 果たす \"to fulfill\" (約束を果たして).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"改めてお礼を申し上げます。","romaji":"Aratamete orei o mōshiagemasu.","german":"I would like to express my thanks once again.","furigana":"あらた|れい|もう|あ"},{"japanese":"今日は時間がないので、また改めて伺います。","romaji":"Kyou wa jikan ga nai node, mata aratamete ukagaimasu.","german":"I don't have time today, so I will visit again another time.","furigana":"きょう|じかん|あらた|うかが"},{"japanese":"病気になって、健康の大切さを改めて感じました。","romaji":"Byouki ni natte, kenkou no taisetsusa o aratamete kanjimashita.","german":"Falling ill made me realize anew how important health is.","furigana":"びょうき|けんこう|たいせつ|あらた|かん"}],"notes":"改めて means \"once more, again (on another occasion)\" and also \"expressly, specially\": 改めて連絡します \"I will contact you again\", 改めてお礼を申し上げます. Also common is 改めて〜を感じる/実感する \"to become aware of something anew\". It comes from 改める \"to renew, to change\" and sounds more polite and formal than もう一度 or また.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一層の努力が必要です。","romaji":"Issō no doryoku ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"Even greater effort is needed.","furigana":"いっそう|どりょく|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"夜になると、風が一層強くなりました。","romaji":"Yoru ni naru to, kaze ga issou tsuyoku narimashita.","german":"When night fell, the wind grew even stronger.","furigana":"よる|かぜ|いっそうつよ"},{"japanese":"今後もより一層努力してまいります。","romaji":"Kongo mo yori issou doryoku shite mairimasu.","german":"We will continue to strive even harder in the future.","furigana":"こんご|いっそうどりょく"}],"notes":"一層 means \"even more, all the more\" and intensifies a comparison: 一層寒くなる, より一層努力する. With の it stands before nouns: 一層の努力 \"even greater effort\". It is more formal than もっと or さらに and is common in speeches and letters (今後より一層頑張ります). Do not confuse it with いっそ \"might as well\", which is usually written in kana; the meaning \"one layer\" is rare.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"経済的に自立したいです。","romaji":"Keizaiteki ni jiritsu shitai desu.","german":"I want to be financially independent.","furigana":"けいざいてき|じりつ"},{"japanese":"大学を卒業したら、親から自立するつもりです。","romaji":"Daigaku o sotsugyou shitara, oya kara jiritsu suru tsumori desu.","german":"After I graduate from university, I intend to become independent of my parents.","furigana":"だいがく|そつぎょう|おや|じりつ"},{"japanese":"子どもの自立を助けるのも親の役目です。","romaji":"Kodomo no jiritsu o tasukeru no mo oya no yakume desu.","german":"Helping children become independent is also the role of parents.","furigana":"こ|じりつ|たす|おや|やくめ"}],"notes":"自立 means \"independence\" in the sense of \"standing on one's own feet\", financially or in daily life: 経済的に自立する, 親から自立する. As a する-verb it is intransitive; what one breaks away from takes から. 独立, by contrast, means the independence of states or the step into professional self-employment (独立して店を開く). Related: 自立心 \"drive for independence\"; the opposite is 依存.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"スマホ依存が問題です。","romaji":"Sumaho izon ga mondai desu.","german":"Smartphone addiction is a problem.","furigana":"いぞん|もんだい"},{"japanese":"日本はエネルギーの多くを輸入に依存しています。","romaji":"Nihon wa enerugii no ooku o yunyuu ni izon shite imasu.","german":"Japan depends on imports for much of its energy.","furigana":"にほん|おお|ゆにゅう|いぞん"},{"japanese":"彼は親に依存しすぎています。","romaji":"Kare wa oya ni izon shisugite imasu.","german":"He depends too much on his parents.","furigana":"かれ|おや|いぞん"}],"notes":"依存 means \"dependence\" and, as a する-verb, \"to depend on\": 〜に依存する, 輸入に依存する. As part of a word it denotes addictions: スマホ依存, ゲーム依存, アルコール依存症. Today the reading いぞん is standard; いそん is regarded as traditional. The opposite is 自立; for an everyday \"to rely on\" one rather says 頼る.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼のスピーチは多くの人の共感を得ました。","romaji":"Kare no supiichi wa ooku no hito no kyoukan o emashita.","german":"His speech won the sympathy of many people.","furigana":"かれ|おお|ひと|きょうかん|え"},{"japanese":"この歌の歌詞にとても共感します。","romaji":"Kono uta no kashi ni totemo kyoukan shimasu.","german":"I really relate to the lyrics of this song.","furigana":"うた|かし|きょうかん"},{"japanese":"主人公の気持ちに共感できませんでした。","romaji":"Shujinkou no kimochi ni kyoukan dekimasen deshita.","german":"I couldn't relate to the protagonist's feelings.","furigana":"しゅじんこう|きも|きょうかん"}],"notes":"共感 means \"empathy, agreement\" in the sense of \"feeling the same, being able to identify with something\": 〜に共感する, 共感を得る/呼ぶ \"to resonate\". One feels with someone because one shares feelings or views. 同情, by contrast, is \"pity\" for someone in a worse situation. Very common for films, books, songs and in social media (共感できる \"relatable\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"被害者の家族に深く同情します。","romaji":"Higaisha no kazoku ni fukaku doujou shimasu.","german":"I deeply sympathize with the victims' families.","furigana":"ひがいしゃ|かぞく|ふか|どうじょう"},{"japanese":"彼の話を聞いて、みんな同情しました。","romaji":"Kare no hanashi o kiite, minna doujou shimashita.","german":"Hearing his story, everyone felt sorry for him.","furigana":"かれ|はなし|き|どうじょう"},{"japanese":"同情なんかいらない。助けてほしいんだ。","romaji":"Doujou nanka iranai. Tasukete hoshii n da.","german":"I don't need your pity. I want help.","furigana":"どうじょう|たす"}],"notes":"同情 means \"pity, compassion\" for someone who is suffering or unlucky: 〜に同情する, 同情を集める. Unlike 共感 (\"feeling with someone because one feels the same\"), 同情 carries a sense of a gap; 同情なんかいらない \"I don't need pity\" shows that it can also come across as condescending. For condolences on a death one says お悔やみ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"将来に不安を感じます。","romaji":"Shōrai ni fuan o kanjimasu.","german":"I feel anxious about the future.","furigana":"しょうらい|ふあん|かん"},{"japanese":"初めての一人暮らしで、少し不安です。","romaji":"Hajimete no hitorigurashi de, sukoshi fuan desu.","german":"I'm living alone for the first time and feel a little uneasy.","furigana":"はじ|ひとりぐ|すこ|ふあん"},{"japanese":"試験の結果が不安で、夜も眠れません。","romaji":"Shiken no kekka ga fuan de, yoru mo nemuremasen.","german":"I'm so worried about my exam results that I can't sleep at night.","furigana":"しけん|けっか|ふあん|よる|ねむ"}],"notes":"不安 means \"unease, worry, anxiety\" about something uncertain and is both a noun and a な-adjective: 将来が不安だ, 〜に不安を感じる, 不安になる, 不安な気持ち. Unlike 心配 (\"to worry about something or someone\", also about others), 不安 describes more of a diffuse, personal feeling of uncertainty; concrete fear is 怖い. The opposite is 安心.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"面接で緊張しました。","romaji":"Mensetsu de kinchō shimashita.","german":"I was nervous at the interview.","furigana":"めんせつ|きんちょう"},{"japanese":"緊張しないで、いつもどおりにやれば大丈夫ですよ。","romaji":"Kinchou shinaide, itsumo doori ni yareba daijoubu desu yo.","german":"Don't be nervous - if you do it like you always do, you'll be fine.","furigana":"きんちょう|だいじょうぶ"},{"japanese":"二つの国の関係が緊張しています。","romaji":"Futatsu no kuni no kankei ga kinchou shite imasu.","german":"Relations between the two countries are tense.","furigana":"ふた|くに|かんけい|きんちょう"}],"notes":"緊張 means \"tension, nervousness\" and as an intransitive する-verb \"to be nervous, tense\": 面接で緊張する, 緊張して手が震える. Someone who gets nervous easily is 緊張しやすい; tension eases with 緊張がほぐれる/とける. It can also refer to political tension (両国の関係が緊張している). Unlike 不安 (\"worry about the uncertain\"), 緊張 means the physical and mental tension in the moment.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"色々苦労しました。","romaji":"Iroiro kurō shimashita.","german":"I went through a lot of hardship.","furigana":"いろいろくろう"},{"japanese":"母は私たちを育てるのに苦労しました。","romaji":"Haha wa watashitachi o sodateru no ni kurou shimashita.","german":"My mother had a hard time raising us.","furigana":"はは|わたし|そだ|くろう"},{"japanese":"苦労して買った家なので、大切にしています。","romaji":"Kurou shite katta ie na node, taisetsu ni shite imasu.","german":"We worked hard to buy this house, so we treasure it.","furigana":"くろう|か|いえ|たいせつ"}],"notes":"苦労 means \"hardship, toil, troubles\" and as a する-verb \"to struggle, to go through a lot\": 仕事で苦労する, 〜のに苦労する, 苦労して〜 \"with great effort ...\". 苦労をかける means \"to cause someone trouble\". The phrase ご苦労さま is really directed at subordinates; to superiors it is better to say お疲れさまです. Compared with 努力 (\"purposeful effort\"), 苦労 emphasizes the hardship suffered.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仕事と家庭の間で葛藤しています。","romaji":"Shigoto to katei no aida de kattou shite imasu.","german":"I am torn between work and family.","furigana":"しごと|かてい|あいだ|かっとう"},{"japanese":"夢をあきらめるかどうか、長い間葛藤しました。","romaji":"Yume o akirameru ka dou ka, nagai aida kattou shimashita.","german":"I struggled for a long time over whether to give up my dream.","furigana":"ゆめ|なが|あいだかっとう"},{"japanese":"この映画は親子の葛藤を描いています。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa oyako no kattou o egaite imasu.","german":"This film depicts conflict between parents and children.","furigana":"えいが|おやこ|かっとう|えが"}],"notes":"葛藤 mainly denotes an inner conflict, being torn between two desires or duties: 心の葛藤, 〜と〜の間で葛藤する. It can also mean conflicts between people (親子の葛藤). The kanji stand for kudzu and wisteria - two climbing plants that entwine with each other. The word sounds literary; in conversation people often say 迷う or 悩む.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"達成感を味わいました。","romaji":"Tasseikan o ajiwaimashita.","german":"I savored the sense of accomplishment.","furigana":"たっせいかん|あじ"},{"japanese":"富士山の頂上に着いたとき、すごい達成感がありました。","romaji":"Fujisan no choujou ni tsuita toki, sugoi tasseikan ga arimashita.","german":"When I reached the summit of Mt. Fuji, I felt a tremendous sense of accomplishment.","furigana":"ふじさん|ちょうじょう|つ|たっせいかん"},{"japanese":"この仕事は大変ですが、達成感があります。","romaji":"Kono shigoto wa taihen desu ga, tasseikan ga arimasu.","german":"This job is tough, but it is rewarding.","furigana":"しごと|たいへん|たっせいかん"}],"notes":"達成感 is the feeling of having accomplished something - a \"sense of achievement\". It is composed of 達成 \"achievement\" and 感 \"feeling\". Typical combinations: 達成感を味わう, 達成感を得る, 達成感がある. Unlike 満足感 \"feeling of satisfaction\", 達成感 stresses that a goal was reached through one's own effort; related is 充実感 \"sense of fulfillment\".","levelNote":"Compound: 達成 (N1) + 感 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"充実した一日でした。","romaji":"Jūjitsu shita ichinichi deshita.","german":"It was a fulfilling day.","furigana":"じゅうじつ|いちにち"},{"japanese":"このホテルは設備が充実しています。","romaji":"Kono hoteru wa setsubi ga juujitsu shite imasu.","german":"This hotel is well equipped.","furigana":"せつび|じゅうじつ"},{"japanese":"市は子育て支援を充実させる予定です。","romaji":"Shi wa kosodate shien o juujitsu saseru yotei desu.","german":"The city plans to expand its child-rearing support.","furigana":"し|こそだ|しえん|じゅうじつ|よてい"}],"notes":"充実 means \"fulfillment, fullness, good provision\". As a する-verb it describes either a fulfilled, full life (充実した毎日, 毎日が充実している) or that something is rich and well developed (設備が充実している, 品ぞろえが充実している). \"To expand\" is expressed with 〜を充実させる. Related is 充実感 \"sense of fulfillment\"; satisfaction with a result, by contrast, is 満足.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"結果に満足しています。","romaji":"Kekka ni manzoku shite imasu.","german":"I am satisfied with the result.","furigana":"けっか|まんぞく"},{"japanese":"忙しくて、満足に寝る時間もありません。","romaji":"Isogashikute, manzoku ni neru jikan mo arimasen.","german":"I'm so busy that I don't even have enough time to sleep properly.","furigana":"いそが|まんぞく|ね|じかん"},{"japanese":"お客様に満足していただけるよう、頑張ります。","romaji":"Okyakusama ni manzoku shite itadakeru you, ganbarimasu.","german":"We will do our best so that our customers are satisfied.","furigana":"きゃくさま|まんぞく|がんば"}],"notes":"満足 means \"satisfaction\" and as a する-verb \"to be satisfied\": 結果に満足する, 現状に満足している. As a な-adjective it also means \"sufficient, proper\" - mostly negated: 満足な食事もできない, 満足に〜できない \"cannot even ... properly\". The opposite is 不満. Common in business: 顧客満足 \"customer satisfaction\", 満足度 \"degree of satisfaction\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不満があります。","romaji":"Fuman ga arimasu.","german":"I have a complaint.","furigana":"ふまん"},{"japanese":"今の給料に不満を持っている社員が多いです。","romaji":"Ima no kyuuryou ni fuman o motte iru shain ga ooi desu.","german":"Many employees are dissatisfied with their current salary.","furigana":"いま|きゅうりょう|ふまん|も|しゃいん|おお"},{"japanese":"子どもは不満そうな顔をしていました。","romaji":"Kodomo wa fuman sou na kao o shite imashita.","german":"The child had a dissatisfied look on his face.","furigana":"こ|ふまん|かお"}],"notes":"不満 means \"dissatisfaction, discontent\" and is both a noun and a な-adjective: 〜に不満がある, 不満を言う \"to complain\", 不満そうな顔. The opposite is 満足. Unlike 文句 (\"grumbling, a complaint\" as spoken words), 不満 refers to the feeling itself; 不平 is similar but more literary (不平不満).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"問題の本質を見極めます。","romaji":"Mondai no honshitsu o mikiwamemasu.","german":"I will discern the essence of the problem.","furigana":"もんだい|ほんしつ|みきわ"},{"japanese":"人間の本質は変わらないと思います。","romaji":"Ningen no honshitsu wa kawaranai to omoimasu.","german":"I think human nature doesn't change.","furigana":"にんげん|ほんしつ|か|おも"},{"japanese":"話が本質からずれていますよ。","romaji":"Hanashi ga honshitsu kara zurete imasu yo.","german":"The discussion is straying from the essence.","furigana":"はなし|ほんしつ"}],"notes":"本質 means the \"essence, core, true nature\" of a thing: 問題の本質, 本質をつかむ, 本質から外れる. It is used above all in discussions, analyses and texts and sounds rather formal. In contrast to 表面 \"surface\" or 見た目 \"appearance\", 本質 means what makes a thing what it is at its innermost. The な-adjective 本質的 means \"essential, fundamental\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"別の側面から見てみましょう。","romaji":"Betsu no sokumen kara mite mimashō.","german":"Let's look at it from another angle.","furigana":"べつ|そくめん|み"},{"japanese":"彼には優しい側面もあります。","romaji":"Kare ni wa yasashii sokumen mo arimasu.","german":"He also has a gentle side.","furigana":"かれ|やさ|そくめん"},{"japanese":"箱の側面に名前を書いてください。","romaji":"Hako no sokumen ni namae o kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write your name on the side of the box.","furigana":"はこ|そくめん|なまえ|か"}],"notes":"側面 literally means \"side, lateral surface\" (箱の側面, 建物の側面) and figuratively \"aspect, side of a matter or person\": 意外な側面, 経済的な側面, いろいろな側面から考える. In the figurative sense it is more formal than 面 or 部分. For the side next to something in everyday speech one says 横; 側面 means the surface or the aspect itself.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"面白い発想ですね。","romaji":"Omoshiroi hassō desu ne.","german":"That is an interesting idea.","furigana":"おもしろ|はっそう"},{"japanese":"子どもは大人にない自由な発想を持っています。","romaji":"Kodomo wa otona ni nai jiyuu na hassou o motte imasu.","german":"Children have a free way of thinking that adults lack.","furigana":"こ|おとな|じゆう|はっそう|も"},{"japanese":"発想を変えれば、問題はすぐ解決できるかもしれません。","romaji":"Hassou o kaereba, mondai wa sugu kaiketsu dekiru kamo shiremasen.","german":"If you change your way of thinking, you may be able to solve the problem quickly.","furigana":"はっそう|か|もんだい|かいけつ"}],"notes":"発想 means \"idea, inspiration, way of thinking\" - above all the way one comes up with something: 面白い発想, 自由な発想, 発想を変える \"to rethink\". The word emphasizes the creative approach; for a concrete idea, everyday speech often uses アイデア, and for a spontaneous notion 思いつき. Important compounds are 発想力 \"inventiveness\" and 発想の転換 \"change of perspective\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この点に着目しました。","romaji":"Kono ten ni chakumoku shimashita.","german":"I focused on this point.","furigana":"てん|ちゃくもく"},{"japanese":"彼女は子どもの睡眠時間に着目して研究しています。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kodomo no suimin jikan ni chakumoku shite kenkyuu shite imasu.","german":"She is doing research focusing on children's sleeping hours.","furigana":"かのじょ|こ|すいみんじかん|ちゃくもく|けんきゅう"},{"japanese":"値段だけでなく、品質にも着目するべきです。","romaji":"Nedan dake de naku, hinshitsu ni mo chakumoku suru beki desu.","german":"One should pay attention not only to the price but also to the quality.","furigana":"ねだん|ひんしつ|ちゃくもく"}],"notes":"着目する means \"to direct one's attention to something, to deliberately take an aspect into view\" and takes に: この点に着目する, 〜に着目して研究する. It is literary and typical of analyses, reports and science. Unlike 注目する, which generally means \"to pay/attract attention\" (注目を集める), 着目 stresses the deliberate singling out of a specific point as a starting point.","levelNote":"Derived from 着目 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"世界中が注目しています。","romaji":"Sekaijū ga chūmoku shite imasu.","german":"The whole world is watching.","furigana":"せかいじゅう|ちゅうもく"},{"japanese":"皆さん、こちらに注目してください。","romaji":"Minasan, kochira ni chuumoku shite kudasai.","german":"Everyone, please look this way.","furigana":"みな|ちゅうもく"},{"japanese":"その新しい商品は若者の間で注目を集めています。","romaji":"Sono atarashii shouhin wa wakamono no aida de chuumoku o atsumete imasu.","german":"That new product is attracting attention among young people.","furigana":"あたら|しょうひん|わかもの|あいだ|ちゅうもく|あつ"}],"notes":"注目する means \"to pay attention to, to follow closely\" and takes に: 〜に注目する, 世界中が注目している. Very common expressions are 注目を集める/浴びる \"to attract attention\" and 注目の〜 \"much-watched\" (注目の新人). Teachers say to the class: こちらに注目してください. Compared with 着目する (focusing on one particular aspect), 注目する is more general; being careful about dangers is 注意する instead.","levelNote":"Derived from 注目 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"良い結果を期待しています。","romaji":"Yoi kekka o kitai shite imasu.","german":"I am hoping for good results.","furigana":"よ|けっか|きたい"},{"japanese":"両親の期待に応えたいです。","romaji":"Ryoushin no kitai ni kotaetai desu.","german":"I want to live up to my parents' expectations.","furigana":"りょうしん|きたい|こた"},{"japanese":"その映画は期待していたほど面白くなかった。","romaji":"Sono eiga wa kitai shite ita hodo omoshiroku nakatta.","german":"The movie wasn't as interesting as I had expected.","furigana":"えいが|きたい|おもしろ"}],"notes":"期待する means \"to expect, to hope, to look forward to something\" and has a positive tone: 良い結果を期待する, 〜に期待する \"to count on someone\". Set phrases are 期待に応える \"to meet expectations\", 期待を裏切る \"to disappoint\" and 期待外れ \"disappointing\". For a neutral forecast (including of negative things) use 予想する or 見込む; \"to wait for someone\" is 待つ.","levelNote":"Derived from 期待 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"給料の引き上げを要求します。","romaji":"Kyūryō no hikiage o yōkyū shimasu.","german":"I demand a pay raise.","furigana":"きゅうりょう|ひ|あ|ようきゅう"},{"japanese":"客は店に返金を要求しました。","romaji":"Kyaku wa mise ni henkin o youkyuu shimashita.","german":"The customer demanded a refund from the store.","furigana":"きゃく|みせ|へんきん|ようきゅう"},{"japanese":"この仕事では高い語学力が要求されます。","romaji":"Kono shigoto de wa takai gogakuryoku ga youkyuu saremasu.","german":"This job requires high language skills.","furigana":"しごと|たか|ごがくりょく|ようきゅう"}],"notes":"要求する means \"to demand, to require\" - often emphatically, as a right or condition: 賃上げを要求する, 〜に〜を要求する, 説明を要求する. It is much more demanding than 頼む or お願いする \"to ask\"; 求める is similar but more neutral. Often used in the passive: 〜が要求される \"is required\". As a noun: 要求に応じる \"to comply with a demand\".","levelNote":"Derived from 要求 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"重要性を強調します。","romaji":"Jūyōsei o kyōchō shimasu.","german":"I emphasize the importance.","furigana":"じゅうようせい|きょうちょう"},{"japanese":"社長は安全が第一だと強調しました。","romaji":"Shachou wa anzen ga daiichi da to kyouchou shimashita.","german":"The president stressed that safety comes first.","furigana":"しゃちょう|あんぜん|だいいち|きょうちょう"},{"japanese":"大事なところは赤い字で強調しておきました。","romaji":"Daiji na tokoro wa akai ji de kyouchou shite okimashita.","german":"I highlighted the important parts in red.","furigana":"だいじ|あか|じ|きょうちょう"}],"notes":"強調する means \"to emphasize, to highlight\", in speech as well as in writing: 重要性を強調する, 〜と強調する \"to say emphatically that ...\". Also visually: 赤い字で強調する, 太字で強調する. Do not confuse it with the similar-sounding 協調 \"cooperation, coordination\". Literally it means \"strong tone\".","levelNote":"Derived from 強調 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その意見を否定します。","romaji":"Sono iken o hitei shimasu.","german":"I reject that opinion.","furigana":"いけん|ひてい"},{"japanese":"大臣はそのうわさを否定しました。","romaji":"Daijin wa sono uwasa o hitei shimashita.","german":"The minister denied the rumor.","furigana":"だいじん|ひてい"},{"japanese":"人の夢を頭から否定するのはよくないです。","romaji":"Hito no yume o atama kara hitei suru no wa yoku nai desu.","german":"It is not good to dismiss other people's dreams out of hand.","furigana":"ひと|ゆめ|あたま|ひてい"}],"notes":"否定する means \"to deny, to dispute, to reject\": うわさを否定する, 事実を否定する, 自分を否定する. It means to portray something as untrue or wrong - unlike 断る \"to decline (a request)\" or 反対する \"to oppose\". In the news, 〜を否定した often means \"denied\". The opposite is 肯定する; in grammar, 否定形 is the negative form.","levelNote":"Derived from 否定 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その考えを肯定します。","romaji":"Sono kangae o kōtei shimasu.","german":"I affirm that idea.","furigana":"かんが|こうてい"},{"japanese":"彼は質問に対して、肯定も否定もしませんでした。","romaji":"Kare wa shitsumon ni taishite, koutei mo hitei mo shimasen deshita.","german":"In response to the question, he neither confirmed nor denied it.","furigana":"かれ|しつもん|たい|こうてい|ひてい"},{"japanese":"失敗した自分も肯定できるようになりたいです。","romaji":"Shippai shita jibun mo koutei dekiru you ni naritai desu.","german":"I want to be able to accept myself even when I have failed.","furigana":"しっぱい|じぶん|こうてい"}],"notes":"肯定する means \"to affirm, to acknowledge as correct, to approve\": 意見を肯定する, 自分を肯定する. The opposite is 否定する; together they appear in 肯定も否定もしない \"neither confirm nor deny\". Also common is 自己肯定感 \"self-esteem\". For simple agreement in conversation, 賛成する is more usual; 肯定する is formal and abstract. In grammar, 肯定文 is an affirmative sentence.","levelNote":"Derived from 肯定 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"政策を批判しました。","romaji":"Seisaku o hihan shimashita.","german":"He criticized the policy.","furigana":"せいさく|ひはん"},{"japanese":"その発言はネットで多くの人に批判されました。","romaji":"Sono hatsugen wa netto de ooku no hito ni hihan saremashita.","german":"That remark was criticized by many people online.","furigana":"はつげん|おお|ひと|ひはん"},{"japanese":"人の意見を批判するだけでなく、自分の案も出してください。","romaji":"Hito no iken o hihan suru dake de naku, jibun no an mo dashite kudasai.","german":"Don't just criticize other people's opinions; offer your own proposal too.","furigana":"ひと|いけん|ひはん|じぶん|あん|だ"}],"notes":"批判する means \"to criticize\" in the sense of a (often public) assessment of opinions, policies or actions; the thing criticized takes を: 政府を批判する. The passive 批判される \"to be criticized\" is especially common in the news. For personal grumbling about trivial things, 文句を言う fits better; for scolding by parents or superiors, 注意する or 叱る. The opposite in the sense of recognition is 評価する or 褒める.","levelNote":"Derived from 批判 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"色々な解釈ができます。","romaji":"Iroiro na kaishaku ga dekimasu.","german":"There are various interpretations.","furigana":"いろいろ|かいしゃく"},{"japanese":"彼の沈黙を「賛成」と解釈しました。","romaji":"Kare no chinmoku o sansei to kaishaku shimashita.","german":"I interpreted his silence as \"agreement\".","furigana":"かれ|ちんもく|さんせい|かいしゃく"},{"japanese":"この詩はどう解釈すればいいですか。","romaji":"Kono shi wa dou kaishaku sureba ii desu ka.","german":"How should this poem be interpreted?","furigana":"し|かいしゃく"}],"notes":"解釈する means \"to interpret\": one understands a text, a law, a statement or behavior in a certain way, typically with 〜と解釈する (\"to take as ...\") or 〜に解釈する (\"to interpret in a ... way\"): 好意的に解釈する. The noun often appears in 解釈が分かれる \"interpretations differ\". Do not confuse it with 通訳する (to interpret orally) or 翻訳する (to translate) - 解釈 refers to interpreting the content.","levelNote":"Derived from 解釈 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"状況を理解しました。","romaji":"Jōkyō o rikai shimashita.","german":"I understood the situation.","furigana":"じょうきょう|りかい"},{"japanese":"彼がなぜ怒ったのか、まだ理解できません。","romaji":"Kare ga naze okotta no ka, mada rikai dekimasen.","german":"I still can't understand why he got angry.","furigana":"かれ|おこ|りかい"},{"japanese":"工事中はご不便をおかけしますが、ご理解ください。","romaji":"Koujichuu wa gofuben o okake shimasu ga, gorikai kudasai.","german":"There will be some inconvenience during construction; we ask for your understanding.","furigana":"こうじちゅう|ふべん|りかい"}],"notes":"理解する is \"to understand, to comprehend\" in the sense of grasping content or having understanding for someone: 内容を理解する, 相手の気持ちを理解する. In everyday conversation people usually just say 分かる (with が), whereas 理解する takes its object with を and sounds more objective and a bit more formal. Set expressions: 理解できない \"cannot comprehend\", 理解が早い \"quick to understand\" and, in announcements, ご理解ください \"we ask for your understanding\".","levelNote":"Derived from 理解 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"説明に納得しました。","romaji":"Setsumei ni nattoku shimashita.","german":"I was convinced by the explanation.","furigana":"せつめい|なっとく"},{"japanese":"この結果にはどうしても納得できません。","romaji":"Kono kekka ni wa doushite mo nattoku dekimasen.","german":"I just can't accept this result.","furigana":"けっか|なっとく"},{"japanese":"先生の話を聞いて、やっと納得がいきました。","romaji":"Sensei no hanashi o kiite, yatto nattoku ga ikimashita.","german":"After listening to the teacher, I finally understood and accepted it.","furigana":"せんせい|はなし|き|なっとく"}],"notes":"納得する means to accept something inwardly because it makes sense to you: \"to be convinced, to be satisfied\". Typical are 説明に納得する and, negated, 結果に納得できない; the object usually takes に. Unlike 理解する (purely understanding), 納得 emphasizes agreement - you can understand something without agreeing with it. Set expressions: 納得がいく／いかない \"to make / not make sense to one\", 納得のいく説明 \"a convincing explanation\".","levelNote":"Derived from 納得 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"条件に合意しました。","romaji":"Jōken ni gōi shimashita.","german":"We agreed on the terms.","furigana":"じょうけん|ごうい"},{"japanese":"両国は貿易のルールについて合意しました。","romaji":"Ryoukoku wa boueki no ruuru ni tsuite goui shimashita.","german":"The two countries agreed on trade rules.","furigana":"りょうこく|ぼうえき|ごうい"},{"japanese":"大家さんと合意して、家賃を少し下げてもらいました。","romaji":"Ooya-san to goui shite, yachin o sukoshi sagete moraimashita.","german":"I came to an agreement with the landlord and got the rent lowered a little.","furigana":"おおや|ごうい|やちん|すこ|さ"}],"notes":"合意する means \"to agree, to come to terms\" - usually between parties, companies or states, and therefore rather formal. What one agrees on takes に (条件に合意する) or 〜について, with whom takes と (相手と合意する). Also common are 合意に達する／合意に至る \"to reach an agreement\" and 合意を得る \"to obtain consent\". In private everyday life one rather says 話がまとまる or 賛成する.","levelNote":"Derived from 合意 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お互いに妥協しましょう。","romaji":"Otagai ni dakyō shimashō.","german":"Let's compromise with each other.","furigana":"たが|だきょう"},{"japanese":"部屋探しで、駅からの距離は妥協しました。","romaji":"Heyasagashi de, eki kara no kyori wa dakyou shimashita.","german":"When looking for a room, I compromised on the distance from the station.","furigana":"へやさが|えき|きょり|だきょう"},{"japanese":"あのシェフは味について絶対に妥協しません。","romaji":"Ano shefu wa aji ni tsuite zettai ni dakyou shimasen.","german":"That chef never compromises on flavor.","furigana":"あじ|ぜったい|だきょう"}],"notes":"妥協する means \"to make a compromise\", often with the nuance that one departs from one's own standards: 〜で妥協する \"to settle for ...\" (この値段で妥協する), with whom takes と. Very common in the negative as an expression of uncompromising consistency: 妥協しない, 妥協を許さない \"to tolerate no compromise\". Related is 譲歩する, which stresses more one-sided giving way to the other party.","levelNote":"Derived from 妥協 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"少し譲歩しましょう。","romaji":"Sukoshi jōho shimashō.","german":"Let's give a little.","furigana":"すこ|じょうほ"},{"japanese":"会社側が譲歩して、給料が上がることになりました。","romaji":"Kaisha-gawa ga jouho shite, kyuuryou ga agaru koto ni narimashita.","german":"The company side made concessions, and salaries are going to rise.","furigana":"かいしゃがわ|じょうほ|きゅうりょう|あ"},{"japanese":"値段については、これ以上譲歩できません。","romaji":"Nedan ni tsuite wa, kore ijou jouho dekimasen.","german":"On the price, I can't concede any further.","furigana":"ねだん|いじょうじょうほ"}],"notes":"譲歩する means \"to give way, to make a concession\" - one grants the other side something in a negotiation or dispute. To whom takes に (相手に譲歩する), on which point takes 〜について. The word is rather formal and typical of negotiations, politics and business; in everyday life one often says 譲る (\"to hand over, to give way\"). Unlike 妥協する (both sides meet in the middle), 譲歩 stresses the accommodation of one side.","levelNote":"Derived from 譲歩 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"国際交流を促進します。","romaji":"Kokusai kōryū o sokushin shimasu.","german":"We promote international exchange.","furigana":"こくさいこうりゅう|そくしん"},{"japanese":"留学中は、地元の人ともっと交流したかったです。","romaji":"Ryuugakuchuu wa, jimoto no hito to motto kouryuu shitakatta desu.","german":"While studying abroad, I wanted to interact more with local people.","furigana":"りゅうがくちゅう|じもと|ひと|こうりゅう"},{"japanese":"このアプリで、世界中の日本語学習者と交流できます。","romaji":"Kono apuri de, sekaijuu no nihongo gakushuusha to kouryuu dekimasu.","german":"With this app, you can interact with Japanese learners from all over the world.","furigana":"せかいじゅう|にほんごがくしゅうしゃ|こうりゅう"}],"notes":"交流する means \"to interact, to keep in contact\" between people, groups or countries; the partner is marked with と (地元の人と交流する). The noun is very common in compounds: 国際交流, 文化交流, 交流会 (\"exchange meeting, mixer\"). Unlike 交換する (exchanging things), the focus is on encounters and relationships, not objects. In electrical engineering, 交流 also means \"alternating current\".","levelNote":"Derived from 交流 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"基準を統一します。","romaji":"Kijun o tōitsu shimasu.","german":"Wir vereinheitlichen die Standards.","furigana":"きじゅん|とういつ"},{"japanese":"資料の文字の大きさを統一してください。","romaji":"Shiryou no moji no ookisa o touitsu shite kudasai.","german":"Bitte vereinheitlichen Sie die Schriftgröße in den Unterlagen.","furigana":"しりょう|もじ|おお|とういつ"},{"japanese":"ドイツは1990年に統一されました。","romaji":"Doitsu wa sen kyuuhyaku kyuujuu nen ni touitsu saremashita.","german":"Deutschland wurde 1990 wiedervereinigt.","furigana":"ねん|とういつ"}],"notes":"統一する heißt „vereinheitlichen“ (Regeln, Formate, Farben) oder „vereinigen“ (ein Land, Gruppen): 書式を統一する, 国を統一する. Es ist transitiv mit を; „einheitlich sein“ wird oft mit 統一されている ausgedrückt. Häufige Wendungen: 意見を統一する „eine gemeinsame Linie finden“, 統一感 „einheitlicher Gesamteindruck“. Im Unterschied zu 統合する (mehrere Teile zu einem Ganzen zusammenlegen) geht es bei 統一 vor allem um Gleichheit und Einheitlichkeit.","levelNote":"Derived from 統一 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二つの部署を統合します。","romaji":"Futatsu no busho o tōgō shimasu.","german":"We will merge the two departments.","furigana":"ふた|ぶしょ|とうごう"},{"japanese":"子どもが減ったので、二つの小学校が統合されました。","romaji":"Kodomo ga hetta node, futatsu no shougakkou ga tougou saremashita.","german":"Because there are fewer children, two elementary schools were merged.","furigana":"こ|へ|ふた|しょうがっこう|とうごう"},{"japanese":"新しいアプリは、メールとカレンダーの機能を統合しています。","romaji":"Atarashii apuri wa, meeru to karendaa no kinou o tougou shite imasu.","german":"The new app integrates email and calendar functions.","furigana":"あたら|きのう|とうごう"}],"notes":"統合する means \"to merge, to integrate\": several organizations, systems or data are combined into one unit, e.g. 部署を統合する, 学校を統合する. Transitive with を; \"to merge with ...\" is 〜と統合する. Typical of business and administrative language (経営統合 \"corporate merger\"). Distinction: 統一する stresses uniformity, 合併する the legal merger of companies or municipalities.","levelNote":"Derived from 統合 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"事業を拡大します。","romaji":"Jigyō o kakudai shimasu.","german":"We will expand the business.","furigana":"じぎょう|かくだい"},{"japanese":"写真を拡大すると、顔がよく見えます。","romaji":"Shashin o kakudai suru to, kao ga yoku miemasu.","german":"If you enlarge the photo, you can see the faces well.","furigana":"しゃしん|かくだい|かお|み"},{"japanese":"火事の被害がどんどん拡大しています。","romaji":"Kaji no higai ga dondon kakudai shite imasu.","german":"The damage from the fire is spreading more and more.","furigana":"かじ|ひがい|かくだい"}],"notes":"拡大する means \"to enlarge, to expand\" and is both transitive and intransitive: 事業を拡大する (to expand something) vs. 被害が拡大する (to spread). Typical in news and business: 感染の拡大, 市場の拡大; in everyday life also for enlarging pictures: 写真を拡大する, 拡大コピー. The opposite is 縮小する. For everyday \"to get bigger\" people often simply say 大きくなる or 広がる.","levelNote":"Derived from 拡大 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"規模を縮小します。","romaji":"Kibo o shukushō shimasu.","german":"We will scale down the operation.","furigana":"きぼ|しゅくしょう"},{"japanese":"このページを縮小してコピーしてください。","romaji":"Kono peeji o shukushou shite kopii shite kudasai.","german":"Please copy this page reduced in size.","furigana":"しゅくしょう"},{"japanese":"人口が減って、国内の市場が縮小しています。","romaji":"Jinkou ga hette, kokunai no shijou ga shukushou shite imasu.","german":"As the population declines, the domestic market is shrinking.","furigana":"じんこう|へ|こくない|しじょう|しゅくしょう"}],"notes":"縮小する means \"to reduce, to scale down, to cut back\", both transitive (規模を縮小する) and intransitive (市場が縮小する). It sounds matter-of-fact and suits business, administration and technology: 事業の縮小, 縮小コピー. The opposite is 拡大する. For quantities getting smaller, everyday speech prefers 減る／減らす; 縮小 emphasizes scope and extent.","levelNote":"Derived from 縮小 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"障害を排除します。","romaji":"Shōgai o haijo shimasu.","german":"We will eliminate obstacles.","furigana":"しょうがい|はいじょ"},{"japanese":"警察がデモの参加者を道路から排除しました。","romaji":"Keisatsu ga demo no sankasha o douro kara haijo shimashita.","german":"The police removed the demonstrators from the road.","furigana":"けいさつ|さんかしゃ|どうろ|はいじょ"},{"japanese":"事故の可能性はまだ排除できません。","romaji":"Jiko no kanousei wa mada haijo dekimasen.","german":"The possibility of an accident cannot yet be ruled out.","furigana":"じこ|かのうせい|はいじょ"}],"notes":"排除する means \"to eliminate, to exclude, to remove\" - something disturbing or undesirable is actively removed or kept away: 障害を排除する, 可能性を排除する \"to rule out a possibility\". It is transitive with を and sounds formal, typical of news, law and politics; with people it sounds harsh (反対意見を排除する \"to shut out dissenting views\"). For everyday removal one rather says 取り除く; merely leaving out or not counting is 除く.","levelNote":"Derived from 排除 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"古い制度を廃止します。","romaji":"Furui seido o haishi shimasu.","german":"We will abolish the old system.","furigana":"ふる|せいど|はいし"},{"japanese":"利用者が少ないので、このバス路線は来年廃止されます。","romaji":"Riyousha ga sukunai node, kono basu rosen wa rainen haishi saremasu.","german":"Because there are few users, this bus route will be discontinued next year.","furigana":"りようしゃ|すく|ろせん|らいねんはいし"},{"japanese":"うちの学校では制服が廃止されました。","romaji":"Uchi no gakkou de wa seifuku ga haishi saremashita.","german":"School uniforms were abolished at our school.","furigana":"がっこう|せいふく|はいし"}],"notes":"廃止する means \"to abolish, to discontinue\" and refers to institutions, rules, systems or services: 制度を廃止する, 路線を廃止する. Transitive with を; very common in the passive: このバスは来年廃止されます. It sounds official and factual. Distinction: 中止する is cancelling a single plan or event (試合が中止になる), 廃止 the permanent abolition of something existing.","levelNote":"Derived from 廃止 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい会社を設立しました。","romaji":"Atarashii kaisha o setsuritsu shimashita.","german":"We established a new company.","furigana":"あたら|かいしゃ|せつりつ"},{"japanese":"この大学は百年前に設立されました。","romaji":"Kono daigaku wa hyakunen mae ni setsuritsu saremashita.","german":"This university was founded a hundred years ago.","furigana":"だいがく|ひゃくねんまえ|せつりつ"},{"japanese":"友達と一緒に、留学生を助けるサークルを設立しました。","romaji":"Tomodachi to issho ni, ryuugakusei o tasukeru saakuru o setsuritsu shimashita.","german":"Together with friends, I founded a club that helps international students.","furigana":"ともだち|いっしょ|りゅうがくせい|たす|せつりつ"}],"notes":"設立する means \"to found, to establish\" in an institutional sense: companies, associations, schools, foundations. Transitive with を, often in the passive or as a noun: 1950年に設立された, 会社の設立. In everyday speech one also simply says 作る; 創立 stresses the original founding (創立記念日), and 建設 is the building of structures - 設立 refers to the organization, not the building.","levelNote":"Derived from 設立 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"目標を設定します。","romaji":"Mokuhyō o settei shimasu.","german":"I will set a goal.","furigana":"もくひょう|せってい"},{"japanese":"毎朝6時にアラームを設定しています。","romaji":"Maiasa rokuji ni araamu o settei shite imasu.","german":"I set an alarm for 6 o'clock every morning.","furigana":"まいあさ|じ|せってい"},{"japanese":"新しいパスワードを設定してください。","romaji":"Atarashii pasuwaado o settei shite kudasai.","german":"Please set a new password.","furigana":"あたら|せってい"}],"notes":"設定する means \"to set, to configure\": fixing goals, deadlines or rules (目標を設定する, 期限を設定する) and above all configuring devices and software (アラームを設定する, パスワードを設定する). The noun 設定 also means \"settings\" in a menu (設定を変更する) as well as the setting of a story (物語の設定). For simply setting an alarm clock, one also colloquially says セットする or 目覚ましをかける.","levelNote":"Derived from 設定 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ブランドを確立しました。","romaji":"Burando o kakuritsu shimashita.","german":"The brand has been established.","furigana":"かくりつ"},{"japanese":"新しい治療法はまだ確立されていません。","romaji":"Atarashii chiryouhou wa mada kakuritsu sarete imasen.","german":"The new treatment has not yet been established.","furigana":"あたら|ちりょうほう|かくりつ"},{"japanese":"彼は30歳で作家としての地位を確立しました。","romaji":"Kare wa sanjussai de sakka to shite no chii o kakuritsu shimashita.","german":"At 30, he established his position as a writer.","furigana":"かれ|さい|さっか|ちい|かくりつ"}],"notes":"確立する means \"to establish firmly, to put on a secure foundation\": 地位を確立する, 方法を確立する, 信頼関係を確立する. It is mostly transitive with を, but also occurs intransitively (制度が確立する). The word is formal and typical of business, science and news. Not to be confused with the identically pronounced 確率 \"probability\".","levelNote":"Derived from 確立 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"知識を蓄積します。","romaji":"Chishiki o chikuseki shimasu.","german":"I accumulate knowledge.","furigana":"ちしき|ちくせき"},{"japanese":"長年蓄積したデータを分析しています。","romaji":"Naganen chikuseki shita deeta o bunseki shite imasu.","german":"We are analyzing data accumulated over many years.","furigana":"ながねんちくせき|ぶんせき"},{"japanese":"忙しい日が続いて、疲れが蓄積しています。","romaji":"Isogashii hi ga tsuzuite, tsukare ga chikuseki shite imasu.","german":"After a succession of busy days, fatigue is building up.","furigana":"いそが|ひ|つづ|つか|ちくせき"}],"notes":"蓄積する means \"to accumulate, to pile up (gradually)\", both transitive (データを蓄積する) and intransitive (疲労が蓄積する). Typical of factual texts about data, knowledge, experience, fatigue or pollutants. In everyday speech 溜める／溜まる sounds more natural: 疲れが溜まる. For money one says 貯める or 貯金する, not 蓄積する.","levelNote":"Derived from 蓄積 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"内容を充実させます。","romaji":"Naiyō o jūjitsu sasemasu.","german":"I will enrich the content.","furigana":"ないよう|じゅうじつ"},{"japanese":"今年の夏休みはとても充実していました。","romaji":"Kotoshi no natsuyasumi wa totemo juujitsu shite imashita.","german":"My summer vacation this year was really fulfilling.","furigana":"ことし|なつやす|じゅうじつ"},{"japanese":"この図書館は子ども向けの本が充実しています。","romaji":"Kono toshokan wa kodomomuke no hon ga juujitsu shite imasu.","german":"This library has a rich selection of children's books.","furigana":"としょかん|こ|む|ほん|じゅうじつ"}],"notes":"充実する is intransitive and means \"to be fulfilled, rich, well-equipped\": 設備が充実している, 充実した一日. It mostly appears as 充実している or attributively as 充実した〜. To actively expand or enrich something, one uses the causative 充実させる (内容を充実させる). The noun 充実感 \"sense of fulfillment\" is also common.","levelNote":"Derived from 充実 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この問題に関わっています。","romaji":"Kono mondai ni kakawatte imasu.","german":"I am involved in this problem.","furigana":"もんだい|かか"},{"japanese":"あの人とはあまり関わりたくないです。","romaji":"Ano hito to wa amari kakawaritakunai desu.","german":"I don't really want to have anything to do with that person.","furigana":"ひと|かか"},{"japanese":"これは命に関わる病気ではないので、安心してください。","romaji":"Kore wa inochi ni kakawaru byouki de wa nai node, anshin shite kudasai.","german":"This is not a life-threatening illness, so please rest assured.","furigana":"いのち|かか|びょうき|あんしん"}],"notes":"関わる means \"to have to do with something, to be involved\" and takes に: 事件に関わる, プロジェクトに関わる. With people, 〜と関わる means \"to have dealings with\": あの人とは関わらない方がいい. For serious matters, 命に関わる means \"to be life-threatening\". Unlike 携わる (to work professionally in a field), 関わる is broader and can also mean an unwanted entanglement; alternative spelling 係わる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"教育に携わっています。","romaji":"Kyōiku ni tazusawatte imasu.","german":"I am involved in education.","furigana":"きょういく|たずさ"},{"japanese":"父は三十年間、新聞の仕事に携わってきました。","romaji":"Chichi wa sanjuunenkan, shinbun no shigoto ni tazusawatte kimashita.","german":"My father has worked in the newspaper business for thirty years.","furigana":"ちち|さんじゅうねんかん|しんぶん|しごと|たずさ"},{"japanese":"このゲームの開発に携わった人は百人以上います。","romaji":"Kono geemu no kaihatsu ni tazusawatta hito wa hyakunin ijou imasu.","german":"More than a hundred people took part in developing this game.","furigana":"かいはつ|たずさ|ひと|ひゃくにんいじょう"}],"notes":"携わる means \"to be active in a field, to take part in something\" and takes に: 教育に携わる, 映画の制作に携わる. It usually refers to professional or serious, longer-term activities and sounds somewhat elevated, typical in résumés and self-introductions. Unlike 関わる (generally \"to be involved\", also negatively), 携わる always means active participation. Mostly in the form 携わっている or 携わってきた.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"社長と会長を兼ねています。","romaji":"Shachō to kaichō o kanete imasu.","german":"He serves as both president and chairman.","furigana":"しゃちょう|かいちょう|か"},{"japanese":"散歩を兼ねて、駅前のパン屋まで歩きました。","romaji":"Sanpo o kanete, ekimae no pan'ya made arukimashita.","german":"I walked to the bakery in front of the station, combining it with a stroll.","furigana":"さんぽ|か|えきまえ|や|ある"},{"japanese":"申し訳ございませんが、その質問にはお答えしかねます。","romaji":"Moushiwake gozaimasen ga, sono shitsumon ni wa okotae shikanemasu.","german":"I am very sorry, but I am unable to answer that question.","furigana":"もう|わけ|しつもん|こた"}],"notes":"兼ねる (transitive, with を) means that a person or thing fulfills two roles at once: 社長と会長を兼ねる, 趣味と実益を兼ねる; very common is 〜を兼ねて \"at the same time as …\" (散歩を兼ねて買い物に行く). After a verb stem, mostly in kana, 〜かねる means \"can hardly, to find oneself unable to\" - a polite refusal in business Japanese: お答えしかねます. Note: 〜かねない, by contrast, means \"might well (do something bad)\": 事故になりかねない.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"災害に備えましょう。","romaji":"Saigai ni sonaemashō.","german":"Let's prepare for disasters.","furigana":"さいがい|そな"},{"japanese":"明日の試験に備えて、早く寝ます。","romaji":"Ashita no shiken ni sonaete, hayaku nemasu.","german":"I'm going to bed early to prepare for tomorrow's exam.","furigana":"あした|しけん|そな|はや|ね"},{"japanese":"このホテルの部屋は、キッチンと洗濯機を備えています。","romaji":"Kono hoteru no heya wa, kitchin to sentakuki o sonaete imasu.","german":"The rooms in this hotel are equipped with a kitchen and a washing machine.","furigana":"へや|せんたくき|そな"}],"notes":"備える has two main meanings: \"to prepare for\" with に (災害に備える, 試験に備える) and \"to be equipped with, to possess\" with を (設備を備える, 才能を備える). In the first sense it is forward-looking: one takes precautions for a possible case, whereas 準備する means \"to prepare\" more generally and takes the thing prepared with を. Common forms: 〜に備えて \"in case of ...\", 〜を備えている \"is equipped with\". The intransitive counterpart 備わる means \"to be provided, to be equipped\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不足を補います。","romaji":"Fusoku o oginaimasu.","german":"I will make up for the shortage.","furigana":"ふそく|おぎな"},{"japanese":"野菜が足りないときは、サプリで栄養を補っています。","romaji":"Yasai ga tarinai toki wa, sapuri de eiyou o oginatte imasu.","german":"When I don't eat enough vegetables, I supplement my nutrition with supplements.","furigana":"やさい|た|えいよう|おぎな"},{"japanese":"経験が少ない分を努力で補いたいと思います。","romaji":"Keiken ga sukunai bun o doryoku de oginaitai to omoimasu.","german":"I want to make up for my lack of experience with effort.","furigana":"けいけん|すく|ぶん|どりょく|おぎな|おも"}],"notes":"補う means \"to supplement, to compensate, to make up for\": something missing is added or a weakness is offset. Transitive with を: 不足を補う, 栄養を補う; the means is marked with で: 経験不足を努力で補う. Also typical is お互いの弱点を補い合う \"to complement each other's weaknesses\". Unlike 足す (simply adding, also in arithmetic), 補う emphasizes filling a gap.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"雪が山を覆っています。","romaji":"Yuki ga yama o ōtte imasu.","german":"Snow covers the mountain.","furigana":"ゆき|やま|おお"},{"japanese":"怖い場面で、思わず手で目を覆いました。","romaji":"Kowai bamen de, omowazu te de me o ooimashita.","german":"At the scary scene, I covered my eyes with my hands without thinking.","furigana":"こわ|ばめん|おも|て|め|おお"},{"japanese":"今日は一日中、空が厚い雲に覆われていました。","romaji":"Kyou wa ichinichijuu, sora ga atsui kumo ni oowarete imashita.","german":"The sky was covered with thick clouds all day today.","furigana":"きょう|いちにちじゅう|そら|あつ|くも|おお"}],"notes":"覆う means \"to cover, to shroud\" and is transitive with を: 雪が山を覆う, 手で顔を覆う. It often describes something completely covering a surface, and sounds somewhat more literary than かぶせる (to put something over) or 隠す (to hide). Often used in the passive: 〜に覆われる \"to be covered with\": 空が雲に覆われる. Figuratively: 目を覆う \"to cover one's eyes, to look away (because something is terrible)\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この家はいつも子どもたちの笑顔に溢れています。","romaji":"Kono ie wa itsumo kodomotachi no egao ni afurete imasu.","german":"This house is always filled with the smiling faces of children.","furigana":"いえ|こ|えがお|あふ"},{"japanese":"大雨で川の水があふれました。","romaji":"Ooame de kawa no mizu ga afuremashita.","german":"The river overflowed because of the heavy rain.","furigana":"おおあめ|かわ|みず"},{"japanese":"週末の駅は観光客であふれていました。","romaji":"Shuumatsu no eki wa kankoukyaku de afurete imashita.","german":"The station was packed with tourists on the weekend.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|えき|かんこうきゃく"}],"notes":"溢れる means \"to overflow\": water spills over the banks (川があふれる), a place is overfull (駅は人であふれている), and figuratively one is \"full of\" a feeling or quality (自信にあふれる, 涙があふれる). It is intransitive; the content is marked with で or に (人であふれる, 元気にあふれる). It is mostly written in kana, あふれる. Related is こぼれる, which rather describes small amounts falling out or being spilled.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"危険が潜んでいます。","romaji":"Kiken ga hisonde imasu.","german":"Danger lurks.","furigana":"きけん|ひそ"},{"japanese":"犯人はまだこの近くに潜んでいるかもしれません。","romaji":"Hannin wa mada kono chikaku ni hisonde iru kamo shiremasen.","german":"The culprit may still be hiding somewhere near here.","furigana":"はんにん|ちか|ひそ"},{"japanese":"便利なサービスの裏には、思わぬ問題が潜んでいることもあります。","romaji":"Benri na saabisu no ura ni wa, omowanu mondai ga hisonde iru koto mo arimasu.","german":"Behind convenient services, unexpected problems can also lurk.","furigana":"べんり|うら|おも|もんだい|ひそ"}],"notes":"潜む means \"to hide, to lurk, to lie hidden\" - intransitive, the place takes に: 草むらに潜む, 心の奥に潜む. Besides living beings keeping themselves hidden, it is often used abstractly for dangers, problems or feelings lying beneath the surface: 危険が潜んでいる. It sounds somewhat literary; in everyday speech \"to hide\" is usually 隠れる. The same kanji appears in 潜る \"to dive\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"雲が空に漂っています。","romaji":"Kumo ga sora ni tadayotte imasu.","german":"Clouds are drifting in the sky.","furigana":"くも|そら|ただよ"},{"japanese":"台所からカレーのいい匂いが漂ってきました。","romaji":"Daidokoro kara karee no ii nioi ga tadayotte kimashita.","german":"A nice smell of curry drifted from the kitchen.","furigana":"だいどころ|にお|ただよ"},{"japanese":"会議室には重い空気が漂っていました。","romaji":"Kaigishitsu ni wa omoi kuuki ga tadayotte imashita.","german":"A heavy atmosphere hung in the meeting room.","furigana":"かいぎしつ|おも|くうき|ただよ"}],"notes":"漂う means \"to drift, to float\" (on water or in the air: 海に漂う, 空に漂う) and figuratively \"to hang in the air\" for smells and moods: いい香りが漂う, 緊張感が漂う. It is intransitive; the place takes に, the source of a smell takes から (〜から漂ってくる). The word sounds somewhat literary and is often used in descriptions. 浮かぶ, by contrast, means just \"to float on the surface, to hover\", without the aimless drifting.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"古い建物が朽ちています。","romaji":"Furui tatemono ga kuchite imasu.","german":"The old building is decaying.","furigana":"ふる|たてもの|く"},{"japanese":"森の中に、朽ちた木の橋がありました。","romaji":"Mori no naka ni, kuchita ki no hashi ga arimashita.","german":"In the forest there was a rotted wooden bridge.","furigana":"もり|なか|く|き|はし"},{"japanese":"彼の作品は、時代が変わっても朽ちることがありません。","romaji":"Kare no sakuhin wa, jidai ga kawatte mo kuchiru koto ga arimasen.","german":"His works do not fade even as times change.","furigana":"かれ|さくひん|じだい|か|く"}],"notes":"朽ちる means \"to rot, to decay, to become rotten\" - above all wood and buildings that fall apart over time: 朽ちた木, 橋が朽ちる. It is intransitive and very often appears as 朽ちた〜 or 朽ちている; intensified it is 朽ち果てる \"to decay completely\". Figuratively and literarily: 朽ちることのない名声 \"imperishable fame\". For food one says 腐る instead, for metal さびる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"合意に至りました。","romaji":"Gōi ni itarimashita.","german":"We reached an agreement.","furigana":"ごうい|いた"},{"japanese":"色々なことがあって、今に至ります。","romaji":"Iroiro na koto ga atte, ima ni itarimasu.","german":"A lot has happened, and that is how I came to be where I am now.","furigana":"いろいろ|いま|いた"},{"japanese":"この店では、子どもから大人に至るまで、みんな楽しめます。","romaji":"Kono mise de wa, kodomo kara otona ni itaru made, minna tanoshimemasu.","german":"At this shop, everyone from children to adults can enjoy themselves.","furigana":"みせ|こ|おとな|いた|たの"}],"notes":"至る means \"to arrive at, to lead to\" and takes に: 結論に至る, 合意に至る, 死に至る. It sounds literary and formal; in everyday speech people prefer 〜になる or 着く. Important patterns: 〜から〜に至るまで \"from ... all the way to\", 今に至る \"up to the present\", 至る所 \"everywhere\". Spatially it can also mean \"(to) lead (to a place)\", for example of roads.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"被害は広範囲に及びました。","romaji":"Higai wa kōhan'i ni oyobimashita.","german":"The damage extended over a wide area.","furigana":"ひがい|こうはんい|およ"},{"japanese":"会議は五時間に及びました。","romaji":"Kaigi wa gojikan ni oyobimashita.","german":"The meeting went on for five hours.","furigana":"かいぎ|ごじかん|およ"},{"japanese":"料理の腕では、私は母に及びません。","romaji":"Ryouri no ude de wa, watashi wa haha ni oyobimasen.","german":"When it comes to cooking, I can't compare with my mother.","furigana":"りょうり|うで|わたし|はは|およ"}],"notes":"及ぶ means \"to extend to, to reach, to amount to\" and takes に: 被害が全国に及ぶ, 会議が三時間に及ぶ. It stresses that something reaches a (often large) extent or area. Negated, 〜に及ばない means \"to not match\" (彼には及ばない), and 〜には及ばない also means \"to not be necessary\". It is literary and formal and very common in news; the transitive counterpart is 及ぼす (影響を及ぼす \"to exert influence\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コミュニケーションは欠かせません。","romaji":"Komyunikēshon wa kakasemasen.","german":"Communication is indispensable.","furigana":"か"},{"japanese":"私には毎朝のコーヒーが欠かせません。","romaji":"Watashi ni wa maiasa no koohii ga kakasemasen.","german":"I can't do without my coffee every morning.","furigana":"わたし|まいあさ|か"},{"japanese":"彼はこのチームに欠かせない選手です。","romaji":"Kare wa kono chiimu ni kakasenai senshu desu.","german":"He is an indispensable player for this team.","furigana":"かれ|か|せんしゅ"}],"notes":"欠かせない is the negative potential form of 欠く \"to lack, to omit\" and literally means \"cannot be left out\" - that is, \"indispensable, essential\". It conjugates like an i-adjective (欠かせなかった). Typical pattern: AにBは欠かせない \"B is indispensable for A\", attributively 欠かせない存在. A synonym is the more formal 不可欠 (必要不可欠); 欠かせない sounds more vivid and also suits personal habits: 毎朝のコーヒーが欠かせない.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"甚大な被害が出ました。","romaji":"Jindai na higai ga demashita.","german":"Enormous damage occurred.","furigana":"じんだい|ひがい|で"},{"japanese":"台風は農業に甚大な影響を与えました。","romaji":"Taifuu wa nougyou ni jindai na eikyou o ataemashita.","german":"The typhoon had a huge impact on agriculture.","furigana":"たいふう|のうぎょう|じんだい|えいきょう|あた"},{"japanese":"このミスによる会社の損害は甚大です。","romaji":"Kono misu ni yoru kaisha no songai wa jindai desu.","german":"The damage to the company from this mistake is enormous.","furigana":"かいしゃ|そんがい|じんだい"}],"notes":"甚大 means \"enormous, immense\", but is used almost only for negative magnitudes: 甚大な被害, 甚大な損害, 影響が甚大だ. It is a な-adjective with a strongly literary, formal tone, typical of news about disasters and accidents. It does not fit positive size such as a great success; there people say 大きな, or 莫大な for sums of money.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"些細なことで喧嘩しました。","romaji":"Sasai na koto de kenka shimashita.","german":"We quarreled over a trivial matter.","furigana":"ささい|けんか"},{"japanese":"些細なミスが大きな事故につながることもあります。","romaji":"Sasai na misu ga ookina jiko ni tsunagaru koto mo arimasu.","german":"Even a small mistake can lead to a serious accident.","furigana":"ささい|おお|じこ"},{"japanese":"彼女は些細なことでもすぐに気にします。","romaji":"Kanojo wa sasai na koto demo sugu ni ki ni shimasu.","german":"She worries right away even about trivial things.","furigana":"かのじょ|ささい|き"}],"notes":"些細 means \"trivial, insignificant\" and is a な-adjective: 些細なこと \"a trifle\", 些細なミス. It often appears in sentences stressing that something big arose from a small cause (些細なことで喧嘩する) or that one should not get upset over trifles. Synonyms are ちょっとした (more colloquial) and 小さな; 些細 sounds somewhat more elevated and is also written in kana as ささい.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"頻繁に連絡を取ります。","romaji":"Hinpan ni renraku o torimasu.","german":"I keep in contact frequently.","furigana":"ひんぱん|れんらく|と"},{"japanese":"この道は事故が頻繁に起こります。","romaji":"Kono michi wa jiko ga hinpan ni okorimasu.","german":"Accidents happen frequently on this road.","furigana":"みち|じこ|ひんぱん|お"},{"japanese":"予定の頻繁な変更は困ります。","romaji":"Yotei no hinpan na henkou wa komarimasu.","german":"Frequent changes to the schedule are a problem.","furigana":"よてい|ひんぱん|へんこう|こま"}],"notes":"頻繁 means \"frequent, at short intervals\" and is a na-adjective mostly used adverbially as 頻繁に: 頻繁に連絡する, 頻繁に起こる; attributively: 頻繁な変更. It sounds more factual than よく or the colloquial しょっちゅう and stresses a conspicuously high frequency, often with a slightly critical undertone. Opposite: まれ or めったに〜ない.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"こんなチャンスは稀です。","romaji":"Konna chansu wa mare desu.","german":"A chance like this is rare.","furigana":"まれ"},{"japanese":"この薬は、まれに頭痛が出ることがあります。","romaji":"Kono kusuri wa, mare ni zutsuu ga deru koto ga arimasu.","german":"With this medicine, headaches may occur in rare cases.","furigana":"くすり|ずつう|で"},{"japanese":"東京で雪が十センチも積もるのはまれです。","romaji":"Toukyou de yuki ga jussenchi mo tsumoru no wa mare desu.","german":"It is rare for ten centimeters of snow to pile up in Tokyo.","furigana":"とうきょう|ゆき|じゅっ|つ"}],"notes":"稀 (mostly written in kana, まれ) means \"rare\" and is a na-adjective: 稀なケース, 〜は稀だ. Adverbially, 稀に means \"rarely, in rare cases\": 稀に副作用が出る. It sounds somewhat more literary than めったに〜ない, which is more common in everyday speech. Fixed phrase: 稀に見る \"exceptional, such as is rarely seen\" (稀に見る才能). 珍しい, by contrast, emphasizes the unusual and surprising.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一方的な意見は良くないです。","romaji":"Ippōteki na iken wa yokunai desu.","german":"One-sided opinions are not good.","furigana":"いっぽうてき|いけん|よ"},{"japanese":"彼は一方的に話して、私の話を全然聞きませんでした。","romaji":"Kare wa ippouteki ni hanashite, watashi no hanashi o zenzen kikimasen deshita.","german":"He did all the talking and didn't listen to me at all.","furigana":"かれ|いっぽうてき|はな|わたし|はなし|ぜんぜんき"},{"japanese":"会社から一方的に契約を切られました。","romaji":"Kaisha kara ippouteki ni keiyaku o kiraremashita.","german":"The company terminated my contract unilaterally.","furigana":"かいしゃ|いっぽうてき|けいやく|き"}],"notes":"一方的 means \"one-sided\" - coming from one side only, without regard for the other: 一方的な意見, 一方的に話す, 一方的に決める. As a na-adjective it takes な attributively and に adverbially. It is usually meant critically, when someone talks or decides over the heads of others; in sports 一方的な試合 is a \"one-sided game\". Opposite: 相互の or 双方向の.","levelNote":"Compound: 一方 (N3) + 的 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"相互理解が大切です。","romaji":"Sōgo rikai ga taisetsu desu.","german":"Mutual understanding is important.","furigana":"そうごりかい|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"二つの国は相互に協力することを約束しました。","romaji":"Futatsu no kuni wa sougo ni kyouryoku suru koto o yakusoku shimashita.","german":"The two countries promised to cooperate with each other.","furigana":"ふた|くに|そうご|きょうりょく|やくそく"},{"japanese":"この二つの問題は相互に関係しています。","romaji":"Kono futatsu no mondai wa sougo ni kankei shite imasu.","german":"These two problems are related to each other.","furigana":"ふた|もんだい|そうご|かんけい"}],"notes":"相互 means \"mutual, reciprocal\" and is actually a noun that mostly stands before other nouns: 相互理解, 相互関係, 相互作用; with の: 相互の信頼. The adverbial 相互に is also possible (相互に協力する). It sounds literary and matter-of-fact; in conversation one says お互い(に) instead. It is not used as a na-adjective with な.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"永久に続くものはありません。","romaji":"Eikyū ni tsuzuku mono wa arimasen.","german":"Nothing lasts forever.","furigana":"えいきゅう|つづ"},{"japanese":"子どもの歯が抜けると、永久歯が生えてきます。","romaji":"Kodomo no ha ga nukeru to, eikyuushi ga haete kimasu.","german":"When children's baby teeth fall out, permanent teeth grow in.","furigana":"こ|は|ぬ|えいきゅうし|は"},{"japanese":"この会員カードは永久に使えます。","romaji":"Kono kaiin kaado wa eikyuu ni tsukaemasu.","german":"This membership card can be used permanently.","furigana":"かいいん|えいきゅう|つか"}],"notes":"永久 means \"eternity; eternal, permanent\". It is used as a noun and adjectivally, most often adverbially as 永久に \"forever\" and attributively with の or in compounds: 永久歯 \"permanent teeth\", 永久保存 \"permanent storage\". Compared with 永遠 (more poetic and emotional, 永遠の愛), 永久 sounds more factual and technical. In everyday speech people often say ずっと or いつまでも.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一時的な現象です。","romaji":"Ichijiteki na genshō desu.","german":"It is a temporary phenomenon.","furigana":"いちじてき|げんしょう"},{"japanese":"大雨のため、電車は一時的に止まっています。","romaji":"Ooame no tame, densha wa ichijiteki ni tomatte imasu.","german":"Trains are temporarily stopped because of heavy rain.","furigana":"おおあめ|でんしゃ|いちじてき|と"},{"japanese":"薬を飲むと痛みは一時的に消えますが、また戻ります。","romaji":"Kusuri o nomu to itami wa ichijiteki ni kiemasu ga, mata modorimasu.","german":"Taking medicine makes the pain disappear for a while, but it comes back.","furigana":"くすり|の|いた|いちじてき|き|もど"}],"notes":"一時的 means \"temporary, for a time\"; as a na-adjective it takes な attributively (一時的な現象) and に adverbially (一時的に止まる). Typical in announcements and news: 一時的に運転を見合わせる, 一時的な措置. Opposite: 恒常的 or 永久的. The base word 一時 (いちじ) means \"for a while, temporarily\", but also \"one o'clock\"; only with 〜的 does it become the adjective.","levelNote":"Compound: 一時 (N3) + 的 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"恒常的な問題です。","romaji":"Kōjōteki na mondai desu.","german":"It is a chronic problem.","furigana":"こうじょうてき|もんだい"},{"japanese":"この会社では恒常的に残業が続いています。","romaji":"Kono kaisha de wa koujouteki ni zangyou ga tsuzuite imasu.","german":"Overtime is a constant at this company.","furigana":"かいしゃ|こうじょうてき|ざんぎょう|つづ"},{"japanese":"地方の病院では、恒常的な医者不足が続いています。","romaji":"Chihou no byouin de wa, koujouteki na isha-busoku ga tsuzuite imasu.","german":"Hospitals in rural areas continue to suffer from a chronic shortage of doctors.","furigana":"ちほう|びょういん|こうじょうてき|いしゃぶそく|つづ"}],"notes":"恒常的 means \"constant, permanent, chronic\" - something exists not just temporarily but as a lasting state. As a na-adjective: 恒常的な人手不足, adverbially 恒常的に残業する. It is literary and formal and occurs mainly in reports, business and administration; in conversation people say いつも or ずっと. Opposite: 一時的.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"やむを得ず欠席しました。","romaji":"Yamu o ezu kesseki shimashita.","german":"I was unavoidably absent.","furigana":"え|けっせき"},{"japanese":"雨がひどかったので、やむを得ず試合を中止しました。","romaji":"Ame ga hidokatta node, yamu o ezu shiai o chuushi shimashita.","german":"Because it rained so hard, we had no choice but to cancel the match.","furigana":"あめ|え|しあい|ちゅうし"},{"japanese":"終電に乗り遅れて、やむを得ずタクシーで帰りました。","romaji":"Shuuden ni noriokurete, yamu o ezu takushii de kaerimashita.","german":"I missed the last train and had no choice but to go home by taxi.","furigana":"しゅうでん|の|おく|え|かえ"}],"notes":"やむを得ず means \"out of necessity, whether one likes it or not\" - one does something unwillingly because there is no other option. It consists of 止む \"to stop\" and the classical negative 得ず, literally something like \"without being able to stop\". It stands adverbially before the verb and sounds formal, often in apologies and notices: やむを得ず中止する. Related is the adjective form やむを得ない \"unavoidable\" (やむを得ない事情); colloquially one says しかたなく.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いわゆる常識です。","romaji":"Iwayuru jōshiki desu.","german":"Das ist das, was man gesunden Menschenverstand nennt.","furigana":"じょうしき"},{"japanese":"彼はいわゆる「天才」というタイプではありません。","romaji":"Kare wa iwayuru tensai to iu taipu de wa arimasen.","german":"Er ist nicht der Typ, den man ein „Genie“ nennt.","furigana":"かれ|てんさい"},{"japanese":"子どもが独立した後の、いわゆる「第二の人生」を楽しんでいます。","romaji":"Kodomo ga dokuritsu shita ato no, iwayuru daini no jinsei o tanoshinde imasu.","german":"Ich genieße mein sogenanntes zweites Leben, seit die Kinder aus dem Haus sind.","furigana":"こ|どくりつ|あと|だいに|じんせい|たの"}],"notes":"いわゆる bedeutet „sogenannt, was man … nennt“ und steht immer direkt vor einem Nomen; grammatisch ist es ein Pränominal (連体詞), kein Adverb. Man benutzt es, um einen bekannten Begriff oder ein Schlagwort einzuführen: いわゆる「ブラック企業」. Anders als das deutsche „sogenannt“ ist es meist nicht distanzierend oder abwertend, sondern erklärend im Sinne von „wie man so sagt“. Es wird fast immer in Kana geschrieben; die Kanji-Schreibung 所謂 ist selten.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一見簡単そうですが難しいです。","romaji":"Ikken kantan sō desu ga muzukashii desu.","german":"It looks easy at first glance, but it's difficult.","furigana":"いっけんかんたん|むずか"},{"japanese":"一見静かな町ですが、夜はとてもにぎやかです。","romaji":"Ikken shizuka na machi desu ga, yoru wa totemo nigiyaka desu.","german":"It looks like a quiet town at first glance, but it is very lively at night.","furigana":"いっけんしず|まち|よる"},{"japanese":"彼は一見怖そうですが、実はとても優しい人です。","romaji":"Kare wa ikken kowasou desu ga, jitsu wa totemo yasashii hito desu.","german":"He looks scary at first glance, but he is actually a very kind person.","furigana":"かれ|いっけんこわ|じつ|やさ|ひと"}],"notes":"一見 (いっけん) as an adverb means \"at first glance, apparently\" and usually announces a contrast: 一見〜だが、実は…. As a noun or する-verb it means \"a quick look, to glance at\" - known from the proverb 百聞は一見にしかず \"seeing once is better than hearing a hundred times\". Not to be confused with 一見 in the reading いちげん \"first-time customer without an introduction\" (一見さんお断り).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"要するに反対ということですか。","romaji":"Yō suru ni hantai to iu koto desu ka.","german":"In short, you're against it?","furigana":"よう|はんたい"},{"japanese":"色々説明しましたが、要するに時間が足りないんです。","romaji":"Iroiro setsumei shimashita ga, you suru ni jikan ga tarinain desu.","german":"I explained a lot, but in short, we don't have enough time.","furigana":"いろいろせつめい|よう|じかん|た"},{"japanese":"要するに、明日までにこれを全部やればいいんですね。","romaji":"You suru ni, ashita made ni kore o zenbu yareba iin desu ne.","german":"In short, I just have to get all of this done by tomorrow, right?","furigana":"よう|あした|ぜんぶ"}],"notes":"要するに means \"in short, in a word, basically\" and sums up or boils down what came before, often with 〜ということだ at the end of the sentence. When used to sum up someone else's words (\"So what you're saying is ...\"), it can come across as impatient or blunt. Related: つまり (more neutral, also used to clarify) and 簡単に言うと (friendlier). It comes from 要する \"to grasp the essentials\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"検査の結果は正常、すなわち問題ないということです。","romaji":"Kensa no kekka wa seijou, sunawachi mondai nai to iu koto desu.","german":"The test result is normal, that is, there is no problem.","furigana":"けんさ|けっか|せいじょう|もんだい"},{"japanese":"彼は母の弟、すなわち私のおじです。","romaji":"Kare wa haha no otouto, sunawachi watashi no oji desu.","german":"He is my mother's younger brother, that is, my uncle.","furigana":"かれ|はは|おとうと|わたし"},{"japanese":"1945年、すなわち戦争が終わった年に、祖父は生まれました。","romaji":"Sen kyuuhyaku yonjuugo nen, sunawachi sensou ga owatta toshi ni, sofu wa umaremashita.","german":"My grandfather was born in 1945, that is, in the year the war ended.","furigana":"ねん|せんそう|お|とし|そふ|う"}],"notes":"すなわち means \"that is, namely\" and equates two expressions, the second explaining or naming the first more precisely: A、すなわちB. It is literary and formal (specialist texts, speeches, reports); in conversation people usually say つまり. Grammatically it is a conjunction and does not stand alone at the start of a conversation but needs a preceding expression. It is almost always written in kana (kanji: 即ち).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"概ね同意します。","romaji":"Ōmune dōi shimasu.","german":"I largely agree.","furigana":"おおむ|どうい"},{"japanese":"明日はおおむね晴れるでしょう。","romaji":"Ashita wa oomune hareru deshou.","german":"Tomorrow will be mostly sunny.","furigana":"あした|は"},{"japanese":"プロジェクトはおおむね予定どおりに進んでいます。","romaji":"Purojekuto wa oomune yotei doori ni susunde imasu.","german":"The project is progressing largely as planned.","furigana":"よてい|すす"}],"notes":"概ね (usually in kana おおむね) means \"by and large, mostly\": something holds for the most part, with minor exceptions. It stands adverbially before a verb or adjective: おおむね良好, おおむね賛成. It sounds factual and formal (reports, weather forecasts, business language); in conversation people say だいたい or ほとんど instead. As a noun it rarely means \"gist, main content\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"売上が大幅に増えました。","romaji":"Uriage ga ōhaba ni fuemashita.","german":"Sales have increased substantially.","furigana":"うりあげ|おおはば|ふ"},{"japanese":"事故のため、電車が大幅に遅れています。","romaji":"Jiko no tame, densha ga oohaba ni okurete imasu.","german":"Trains are considerably delayed because of an accident.","furigana":"じこ|でんしゃ|おおはば|おく"},{"japanese":"旅行の計画を大幅に変更しました。","romaji":"Ryokou no keikaku o oohaba ni henkou shimashita.","german":"We changed our travel plans drastically.","furigana":"りょこう|けいかく|おおはば|へんこう"}],"notes":"大幅に means \"considerably, markedly, on a large scale\" and is the adverbial form of the na-adjective 大幅 (literally \"large width\"). It typically goes with changes in numbers, plans or times: 大幅に増える／減る, 大幅に遅れる, 大幅に変更する; attributively it is 大幅な値下げ. It sounds factual (news, business). かなり or ずいぶん also mean \"quite, very\", but are not limited to changes.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"相対的に見ると良いです。","romaji":"Sōtaiteki ni miru to ii desu.","german":"Viewed relatively, it is good.","furigana":"そうたいてき|み|い"},{"japanese":"この町は東京より相対的に家賃が安いです。","romaji":"Kono machi wa Toukyou yori soutaiteki ni yachin ga yasui desu.","german":"Rents in this town are relatively cheap compared to Tokyo.","furigana":"まち|とうきょう|そうたいてき|やちん|やす"},{"japanese":"学校の成績は、相対的な評価で決まります。","romaji":"Gakkou no seiseki wa, soutaiteki na hyouka de kimarimasu.","german":"School grades are determined by relative evaluation.","furigana":"がっこう|せいせき|そうたいてき|ひょうか|き"}],"notes":"相対的 means \"relative, in proportion\" - something is judged in comparison with other things, not on its own. As a na-adjective: 相対的な評価 \"relative evaluation\"; adverbially: 相対的に見ると \"comparatively speaking\", 相対的に安い \"relatively cheap\". The opposite is 絶対的. The word is factual and literary; in conversation one often says 比較的 or 〜と比べると.","levelNote":"Compound: 相対 (N1) + 的 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"絶対的な信頼があります。","romaji":"Zettaiteki na shinrai ga arimasu.","german":"There is absolute trust.","furigana":"ぜったいてき|しんらい"},{"japanese":"昔、王は絶対的な権力を持っていました。","romaji":"Mukashi, ou wa zettaiteki na kenryoku o motte imashita.","german":"In the past, the king held absolute power.","furigana":"むかし|おう|ぜったいてき|けんりょく|も"},{"japanese":"このチームには絶対的なエースがいません。","romaji":"Kono chiimu ni wa zettaiteki na eesu ga imasen.","german":"This team doesn't have an undisputed ace.","furigana":"ぜったいてき"}],"notes":"絶対的 means \"absolute, unconditional\" - something holds without comparison or restriction: 絶対的な権力, 絶対的な信頼, 絶対的な存在. As a na-adjective it is mostly used attributively with な. Unlike the adverb 絶対(に) \"definitely, guaranteed\", which is very common in everyday speech, 絶対的 describes a factual property. Opposite: 相対的.","levelNote":"Compound: 絶対 (N3) + 的 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"経済の安定が大切です。","romaji":"Keizai no antei ga taisetsu desu.","german":"Economic stability is important.","furigana":"けいざい|あんてい|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"もっと安定した仕事に就きたいです。","romaji":"Motto antei shita shigoto ni tsukitai desu.","german":"I want to get a more secure job.","furigana":"あんてい|しごと|つ"},{"japanese":"手術の後、父の体調はやっと安定してきました。","romaji":"Shujutsu no ato, chichi no taichou wa yatto antei shite kimashita.","german":"After the operation, my father's condition has finally stabilized.","furigana":"しゅじゅつ|あと|ちち|たいちょう|あんてい"}],"notes":"安定 means \"stability, steadiness\" and is often used as an intransitive する-verb: 収入が安定している \"income is stable\". Attributively 安定した comes before nouns (安定した仕事 \"a secure job\"); the opposite is 不安定(な). To stabilize something is 安定させる. Do not confuse it with 安心 \"relief, reassurance\", which describes a feeling, while 安定 refers to the state of things, a situation or health.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"現場は混乱しています。","romaji":"Genba wa konran shite imasu.","german":"The scene is in chaos.","furigana":"げんば|こんらん"},{"japanese":"説明が多すぎて、頭が混乱してしまいました。","romaji":"Setsumei ga oosugite, atama ga konran shite shimaimashita.","german":"There were so many explanations that my head got all muddled.","furigana":"せつめい|おお|あたま|こんらん"},{"japanese":"大雪で駅は大きな混乱になりました。","romaji":"Ooyuki de eki wa ookina konran ni narimashita.","german":"Heavy snow caused great chaos at the station.","furigana":"おおゆき|えき|おお|こんらん"}],"notes":"混乱 is \"confusion, disorder, chaos\" and is mostly used as an intransitive する-verb: 現場が混乱する, 頭が混乱する \"to be all mixed up in one's head\". Someone who causes chaos does 混乱を招く or 混乱させる. It describes situations, processes or thoughts - for a messy room, people say 散らかっている instead. Mild uncertainty or hesitation is better expressed with 迷う.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"経済危機が起こりました。","romaji":"Keizai kiki ga okorimashita.","german":"An economic crisis occurred.","furigana":"けいざいきき|お"},{"japanese":"この動物は絶滅の危機にあります。","romaji":"Kono doubutsu wa zetsumetsu no kiki ni arimasu.","german":"This animal is in danger of extinction.","furigana":"どうぶつ|ぜつめつ|きき"},{"japanese":"みんなで協力して、会社の危機を乗り越えました。","romaji":"Minna de kyouryoku shite, kaisha no kiki o norikoemashita.","german":"Everyone worked together and overcame the company's crisis.","furigana":"きょうりょく|かいしゃ|きき|の|こ"}],"notes":"危機 is a \"crisis, critical situation\" and often appears in compounds: 経済危機, 危機管理 \"crisis management\", 危機感 \"sense of crisis\". Typical are 危機に陥る \"to fall into a crisis\", 危機を乗り越える \"to overcome a crisis\" and 〜の危機にある \"to be threatened by ...\" (絶滅の危機). Unlike 危険 \"danger\", 危機 means a serious turning point; colloquially people often say ピンチ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"災害に備えましょう。","romaji":"Saigai ni sonaemashō.","german":"Let's prepare for disasters.","furigana":"さいがい|そな"},{"japanese":"日本は地震や台風などの自然災害が多い国です。","romaji":"Nihon wa jishin ya taifuu nado no shizen saigai ga ooi kuni desu.","german":"Japan is a country with many natural disasters such as earthquakes and typhoons.","furigana":"にほん|じしん|たいふう|しぜんさいがい|おお|くに"},{"japanese":"災害の時は、この学校が避難所になります。","romaji":"Saigai no toki wa, kono gakkou ga hinanjo ni narimasu.","german":"In the event of a disaster, this school becomes an evacuation shelter.","furigana":"さいがい|とき|がっこう|ひなんじょ"}],"notes":"災害 is a \"disaster, (natural) calamity\" such as an earthquake, typhoon or flood; 自然災害 stresses the natural origin. One says 災害が起きる, 災害に遭う \"to be hit by a disaster\" and 災害に備える \"to prepare for disasters\". Related are 防災 \"disaster prevention\" and 被災地 \"disaster area\". An ordinary accident is 事故, the resulting damage 被害.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"被害が広がっています。","romaji":"Higai ga hirogatte imasu.","german":"The damage is spreading.","furigana":"ひがい|ひろ"},{"japanese":"台風で多くの家が被害を受けました。","romaji":"Taifuu de ooku no ie ga higai o ukemashita.","german":"Many houses were damaged by the typhoon.","furigana":"たいふう|おお|いえ|ひがい|う"},{"japanese":"駅で財布を盗まれる被害に遭いました。","romaji":"Eki de saifu o nusumareru higai ni aimashita.","german":"I fell victim to having my wallet stolen at the station.","furigana":"えき|さいふ|ぬす|ひがい|あ"}],"notes":"被害 is the \"damage\" someone suffers - the character 被 marks the passive \"to suffer\". Typical are 被害を受ける \"to suffer damage\", 被害に遭う \"to become a victim of ...\" and 被害が出る/広がる. The injured person is 被害者 \"victim\", the perpetrator 加害者. Damage to an object in general (e.g. a scratch) is more 損害 or 傷; 被害 stresses that one was hit from outside (bad weather, theft, fraud).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"電力の復旧を急いでいます。","romaji":"Denryoku no fukkyū o isoide imasu.","german":"We are hurrying to restore the power.","furigana":"でんりょく|ふっきゅう|いそ"},{"japanese":"事故で止まっていた電車が一時間後に復旧しました。","romaji":"Jiko de tomatte ita densha ga ichijikan go ni fukkyuu shimashita.","german":"The trains that had stopped because of an accident resumed an hour later.","furigana":"じこ|と|でんしゃ|いちじかんご|ふっきゅう"},{"japanese":"サーバーの復旧にはまだ時間がかかりそうです。","romaji":"Saabaa no fukkyuu ni wa mada jikan ga kakarisou desu.","german":"It looks like it will still take time to restore the server.","furigana":"ふっきゅう|じかん"}],"notes":"復旧 means the \"restoration\" of infrastructure after a disruption: electricity, water, rail service, roads, computer systems. Mostly intransitive: 電車が復旧した \"the trains are running again\"; transitive use is rarer (システムを復旧する). Common are 復旧作業 \"repair work\" and 復旧の見込み \"prospect of restoration\". Distinction: 回復 for health or the economy, 復興 for the reconstruction of whole regions, 修理 for repairing an object.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"迅速な対応をお願いします。","romaji":"Jinsoku na taiō o onegai shimasu.","german":"Please respond promptly.","furigana":"じんそく|たいおう|ねが"},{"japanese":"あの店員さんはお客さんへの対応がとても丁寧です。","romaji":"Ano ten'in-san wa okyakusan e no taiou ga totemo teinei desu.","german":"That clerk is very polite in dealing with customers.","furigana":"てんいん|きゃく|たいおう|ていねい"},{"japanese":"このアプリは日本語に対応していません。","romaji":"Kono apuri wa nihongo ni taiou shite imasen.","german":"This app does not support Japanese.","furigana":"にほんご|たいおう"}],"notes":"対応 has several meanings: above all \"dealing with, response to, handling\" - 〜に対応する \"to respond to, to take care of\", 客の対応 \"customer service\", 対応が早い/遅い. In technology, 〜に対応している means \"supports ..., is compatible with\" (日本語に対応). It also means \"correspondence\": AとBが対応する. In contrast to 対策 \"countermeasure (preventive)\", 対応 describes the reaction to a concrete situation.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"正しい方向に進んでいます。","romaji":"Tadashii hōkō ni susunde imasu.","german":"We are heading in the right direction.","furigana":"ただ|ほうこう|すす"},{"japanese":"すみません、駅はどちらの方向ですか。","romaji":"Sumimasen, eki wa dochira no houkou desu ka.","german":"Excuse me, which direction is the station?","furigana":"えき|ほうこう"},{"japanese":"私は方向音痴なので、よく道に迷います。","romaji":"Watashi wa houkou onchi na node, yoku michi ni mayoimasu.","german":"I have no sense of direction, so I often get lost.","furigana":"わたし|ほうこうおんち|みち|まよ"}],"notes":"方向 is \"direction\", both spatially and figuratively: 駅の方向 \"toward the station\", 正しい方向に進む \"to go in the right direction\". Set expressions are 方向を変える, 反対方向 and, colloquially, 方向音痴 \"having no sense of direction\". In everyday speech people often ask more briefly どっち? or use 〜の方; 方面 refers more to an area or a destination (新宿方面), 向き to the orientation of an object (窓の向き).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一日一時間が目安です。","romaji":"Ichinichi ichijikan ga meyasu desu.","german":"One hour per day is a rough guide.","furigana":"いちにちいちじかん|めやす"},{"japanese":"ご飯は一人百グラムを目安にしてください。","romaji":"Gohan wa hitori hyaku guramu o meyasu ni shite kudasai.","german":"Please use about a hundred grams of rice per person as a guide.","furigana":"はん|ひとりひゃく|めやす"},{"japanese":"修理の料金の目安を教えていただけますか。","romaji":"Shuuri no ryoukin no meyasu o oshiete itadakemasu ka.","german":"Could you tell me roughly how much the repair will cost?","furigana":"しゅうり|りょうきん|めやす|おし"}],"notes":"目安 means \"rough guide, benchmark, approximate orientation\" - not a fixed rule but a yardstick for orientation. Typical: 〜が目安です, 〜を目安にする \"to go by …\", 料金の目安 \"approximate price\" and 目安がつく/立つ \"to have a rough idea\". Often found on packaging, in instructions and in price lists. A binding \"standard\", by contrast, is 基準.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"解決の手掛かりを見つけました。","romaji":"Kaiketsu no tegakari o mitsukemashita.","german":"I found a clue to the solution.","furigana":"かいけつ|てが|み"},{"japanese":"警察はまだ事件の手掛かりをつかんでいません。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa mada jiken no tegakari o tsukande imasen.","german":"The police have not yet picked up any leads in the case.","furigana":"けいさつ|じけん|てが"},{"japanese":"この古い写真が祖父の家を探す手掛かりになりました。","romaji":"Kono furui shashin ga sofu no ie o sagasu tegakari ni narimashita.","german":"This old photograph became a clue in the search for my grandfather's house.","furigana":"ふる|しゃしん|そふ|いえ|さが|てが"}],"notes":"手掛かり is a \"clue, lead, trace\" that leads to solving a riddle, case or problem; literally a \"handhold\" (also in climbing). Typical: 手掛かりを見つける/つかむ, 〜の手掛かりになる, 何の手掛かりもない. Very often also written 手がかり. A mere tip or piece of advice is rather ヒント; 手掛かり is more concrete, often in a detective or research context.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい取り組みを始めます。","romaji":"Atarashii torikumi o hajimemasu.","german":"We are starting a new initiative.","furigana":"あたら|と|く|はじ"},{"japanese":"この町はごみを減らす取り組みに力を入れています。","romaji":"Kono machi wa gomi o herasu torikumi ni chikara o irete imasu.","german":"This town is putting great effort into reducing waste.","furigana":"まち|へ|と|く|ちから|い"},{"japanese":"今日の相撲は横綱の取り組みが一番面白かったです。","romaji":"Kyou no sumou wa yokozuna no torikumi ga ichiban omoshirokatta desu.","german":"In today's sumo, the Yokozuna's bout was the most exciting.","furigana":"きょう|すもう|よこづな|と|く|いちばんおもしろ"}],"notes":"取り組み is the noun of 取り組む \"to tackle (a task) seriously\" and means \"effort, initiative, measure\". Typical: 〜への取り組み \"commitment to ...\" (環境問題への取り組み), 新しい取り組みを始める. Popular in corporate, school and government texts. In sumo, 取り組み also means \"bout, match\". 取組 without okurigana also appears in official texts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい枠組みを作ります。","romaji":"Atarashii wakugumi o tsukurimasu.","german":"We will create a new framework.","furigana":"あたら|わくぐ|つく"},{"japanese":"まず計画の大きな枠組みを決めてから、細かいことを話し合いましょう。","romaji":"Mazu keikaku no ookina wakugumi o kimete kara, komakai koto o hanashiaimashou.","german":"Let's first decide on the broad framework of the plan and then discuss the details.","furigana":"けいかく|おお|わくぐ|き|こま|はな|あ"},{"japanese":"今の法律の枠組みの中では、それはできません。","romaji":"Ima no houritsu no wakugumi no naka de wa, sore wa dekimasen.","german":"Within the framework of the current law, that is not possible.","furigana":"いま|ほうりつ|わくぐ|なか"}],"notes":"枠組み is literally a \"structure made of frames\" and usually means, figuratively, the \"framework, basic structure\" of a plan, system or agreement: 法律の枠組み, 〜の枠組みの中で \"within the framework of ...\", 枠組みを作る/決める. Concretely it can also be a wooden or metal frame. A single frame (picture, window, form field) is 枠; 枠組み emphasizes the overall construction.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"回復の見込みがあります。","romaji":"Kaifuku no mikomi ga arimasu.","german":"There is a prospect of recovery.","furigana":"かいふく|みこ"},{"japanese":"新しい駅は来年の春に完成する見込みです。","romaji":"Atarashii eki wa rainen no haru ni kansei suru mikomi desu.","german":"The new station is expected to be completed next spring.","furigana":"あたら|えき|らいねん|はる|かんせい|みこ"},{"japanese":"売り上げは見込みより少なかったです。","romaji":"Uriage wa mikomi yori sukunakatta desu.","german":"Sales were lower than expected.","furigana":"う|あ|みこ|すく"}],"notes":"見込み (from 見込む \"to estimate, to expect\") means \"prospect, likelihood\" and \"forecast, expectation\". Typical: 〜の見込みがある/ない \"there is/is no prospect of ...\", 見込みが外れる \"the expectation does not come true\", 卒業見込み \"expected graduation\". 見込みのある人 is someone \"with potential\". After verbs at the end of a sentence: 〜する見込みだ \"it is expected that ...\" - more factual than 予定 (firm plan).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"社会は信頼で成り立っています。","romaji":"Shakai wa shinrai de naritatte imasu.","german":"Society is built on trust.","furigana":"しゃかい|しんらい|な|た"},{"japanese":"この委員会は五人のメンバーから成り立っています。","romaji":"Kono iinkai wa gonin no menbaa kara naritatte imasu.","german":"This committee consists of five members.","furigana":"いいんかい|ごにん|な|た"},{"japanese":"客がこんなに少なくては、商売が成り立ちません。","romaji":"Kyaku ga konna ni sukunakute wa, shoubai ga naritachimasen.","german":"With so few customers, the business is not viable.","furigana":"きゃく|すく|しょうばい|な|た"}],"notes":"成り立つ is intransitive and has three core meanings: \"to consist of\" (〜から/で成り立つ), \"to come into being, to hold\" (契約が成り立つ, 理論が成り立つ) and \"to be viable, to work\" (商売が成り立たない \"the business doesn't pay\"). There is no transitive partner; one uses 成り立たせる. The noun 成り立ち means \"origin, structure\". In everyday speech, \"to consist of\" is often simply said 〜でできている.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"成り行きを見守ります。","romaji":"Nariyuki o mimamorimasu.","german":"I will watch how things develop.","furigana":"な|ゆ|みまも"},{"japanese":"話の成り行きで、私がパーティーの幹事をすることになりました。","romaji":"Hanashi no nariyuki de, watashi ga paatii no kanji o suru koto ni narimashita.","german":"As the conversation went, I ended up being in charge of organizing the party.","furigana":"はなし|な|ゆ|わたし|かんじ"},{"japanese":"細かい予定は決めないで、旅行は成り行きに任せることにしました。","romaji":"Komakai yotei wa kimenaide, ryokou wa nariyuki ni makaseru koto ni shimashita.","german":"We didn't make detailed plans and decided to leave the trip to chance.","furigana":"こま|よてい|き|りょこう|な|ゆ|まか"}],"notes":"成り行き is the \"course of things, progress, development\" of a situation, often without deliberate planning. Typical: 成り行きを見守る \"to wait and see how things develop\", 成り行きに任せる \"to let things take their course\", 話の成り行きで \"as the conversation went\". 当然の成り行き is \"the logical consequence\". In stock-market jargon it also means \"market order\". For planned progress, people say 進行 or 経過 instead.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仕事の傍ら勉強しています。","romaji":"Shigoto no katawara benkyō shite imasu.","german":"I study alongside my job.","furigana":"しごと|かたわ|べんきょう"},{"japanese":"彼女は会社に勤める傍ら、小説を書いています。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kaisha ni tsutomeru katawara, shousetsu o kaite imasu.","german":"While working at a company, she writes novels.","furigana":"かのじょ|かいしゃ|つと|かたわ|しょうせつ|か"},{"japanese":"母は眠っている子どもの傍らに静かに座っていた。","romaji":"Haha wa nemutte iru kodomo no katawara ni shizuka ni suwatte ita.","german":"The mother sat quietly beside her sleeping child.","furigana":"はは|ねむ|こ|かたわ|しず|すわ"}],"notes":"傍ら is a noun with two uses: spatially \"side, immediate vicinity\" (〜の傍らに座る \"to sit beside ...\", literary) and above all 〜の傍ら / verb + 傍ら \"alongside (a main activity), on the side\": 会社に勤める傍ら小説を書く. This construction describes ongoing dual activities, not simultaneous actions like 〜ながら. Literary; in conversation one rather says 〜の横に or 〜しながら.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"模範を示しましょう。","romaji":"Mohan o shimeshimashō.","german":"Let's set an example.","furigana":"もはん|しめ"},{"japanese":"先輩たちは後輩の模範にならなければなりません。","romaji":"Senpaitachi wa kouhai no mohan ni naranakereba narimasen.","german":"Senior students must be a model for the younger ones.","furigana":"せんぱい|こうはい|もはん"},{"japanese":"テストの後で模範解答が配られました。","romaji":"Tesuto no ato de mohan kaitou ga kubararemashita.","german":"Model answers were handed out after the test.","furigana":"あと|もはんかいとう|くば"}],"notes":"模範 is a \"model, pattern\" that others should follow. Typical: 模範を示す \"to set a good example\", 〜の模範となる \"to be a model for ...\", 模範的な学生 \"a model student\", 模範解答 \"model answer\". Somewhat more formal than お手本 / 手本, which is more common in everyday life (for example in calligraphy). 見本, by contrast, is a \"sample, specimen\" of a product.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これは典型的な例です。","romaji":"Kore wa tenkeiteki na rei desu.","german":"This is a typical example.","furigana":"てんけいてき|れい"},{"japanese":"毎日残業する彼は、典型的なサラリーマンです。","romaji":"Mainichi zangyou suru kare wa, tenkeiteki na sarariiman desu.","german":"He works overtime every day - a typical salaryman.","furigana":"まいにちざんぎょう|かれ|てんけいてき"},{"japanese":"この寺は昔の日本建築の典型だと言われています。","romaji":"Kono tera wa mukashi no nihon kenchiku no tenkei da to iwarete imasu.","german":"This temple is said to be a classic example of old Japanese architecture.","furigana":"てら|むかし|にほんけんちく|てんけい|い"}],"notes":"典型 means \"type, prime example\" and is most often used as the na-adjective 典型的(な) \"typical\": 典型的な例, 典型的な日本人. As a noun it appears in 〜の典型だ \"is a prime example of ...\". Compared with いかにも〜らしい (colloquial \"just so typical\"), 典型 sounds objective and analytical. 代表的, by contrast, stresses \"representative, best known\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"桜は日本の象徴です。","romaji":"Sakura wa nihon no shōchō desu.","german":"The cherry blossom is a symbol of Japan.","furigana":"さくら|にほん|しょうちょう"},{"japanese":"白い鳩は平和を象徴しています。","romaji":"Shiroi hato wa heiwa o shouchou shite imasu.","german":"The white dove symbolizes peace.","furigana":"しろ|はと|へいわ|しょうちょう"},{"japanese":"この古い橋は町の歴史を象徴する建物です。","romaji":"Kono furui hashi wa machi no rekishi o shouchou suru tatemono desu.","german":"This old bridge is a structure that symbolizes the town's history.","furigana":"ふる|はし|まち|れきし|しょうちょう|たてもの"}],"notes":"象徴 is a \"symbol, emblem\" for something abstract: 平和の象徴 \"symbol of peace\". As a transitive する-verb: AがBを象徴する \"A symbolizes B\", often in the form 象徴している. The adjective 象徴的(な) means \"symbolic, emblematic\". For signs such as traffic signs or chemical symbols, one uses 記号 or マーク instead; 象徴 always refers to a meaning beyond the thing itself.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"本来の目的を忘れないでください。","romaji":"Honrai no mokuteki o wasurenaide kudasai.","german":"Please do not forget the original purpose.","furigana":"ほんらい|もくてき|わす"},{"japanese":"本来なら今日は休みですが、急な仕事が入りました。","romaji":"Honrai nara kyou wa yasumi desu ga, kyuu na shigoto ga hairimashita.","german":"Normally I would have the day off today, but sudden work came up.","furigana":"ほんらい|きょう|やす|きゅう|しごと|はい"},{"japanese":"けがが治って、彼は本来の力を出せるようになりました。","romaji":"Kega ga naotte, kare wa honrai no chikara o daseru you ni narimashita.","german":"His injury has healed, and he can now show his true ability.","furigana":"なお|かれ|ほんらい|ちから|だ"}],"notes":"本来 means \"originally, properly, by nature\" and is used as an adverb and as a noun with の: 本来の目的 \"the original purpose\", 本来の力 \"one's true strength\". 本来なら/本来は \"normally, properly (it should be so)\" often introduces a contrast with reality. In everyday speech もともと is often used; 元来 is more literary and refers more to innate characteristics.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は元来真面目な人です。","romaji":"Kare wa ganrai majime na hito desu.","german":"He is by nature a serious person.","furigana":"かれ|がんらいまじめ|ひと"},{"japanese":"この地方は元来、米作りが盛んな所です。","romaji":"Kono chihou wa ganrai, komezukuri ga sakan na tokoro desu.","german":"This region has always been one where rice farming flourishes.","furigana":"ちほう|がんらい|こめづく|さか|ところ"},{"japanese":"元来、この祭りは豊作を祈るためのものでした。","romaji":"Ganrai, kono matsuri wa housaku o inoru tame no mono deshita.","german":"Originally, this festival was meant to pray for a good harvest.","furigana":"がんらい|まつ|ほうさく|いの"}],"notes":"元来 means \"by nature, originally, from the beginning\" and is a pure adverb: 彼は元来まじめな人だ. It describes qualities that something or someone has always had. The tone is literary to elevated; in conversation one mostly says もともと. Unlike 本来, it hardly forms phrases with の and is not used for \"actually it should ...\" (本来なら).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"単に知らなかっただけです。","romaji":"Tan ni shiranakatta dake desu.","german":"I just didn't know.","furigana":"たん|し"},{"japanese":"これは単にお金の問題ではありません。","romaji":"Kore wa tan ni okane no mondai de wa arimasen.","german":"This is not simply a matter of money.","furigana":"たん|かね|もんだい"},{"japanese":"日本に来たのは、単に仕事のためだけではなく、文化に興味があったからです。","romaji":"Nihon ni kita no wa, tan ni shigoto no tame dake de wa naku, bunka ni kyoumi ga atta kara desu.","german":"I came to Japan not only for work but also because I was interested in the culture.","furigana":"にほん|き|たん|しごと|ぶんか|きょうみ"}],"notes":"単に means \"merely, simply, just\" and is typically combined with だけ or にすぎない: 単に〜だけだ. The negation 単に〜ではない / 単に〜だけでなく \"not merely ..., but also\" is very common. It sounds somewhat more objective than ただ; in casual conversation people often say ただ〜だけ. Do not confuse it with 簡単に \"easily (= simply)\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"強いて言えば問題はここです。","romaji":"Shiite ieba mondai wa koko desu.","german":"If I had to say, the problem is here.","furigana":"し|い|もんだい"},{"japanese":"嫌なら、強いて参加する必要はありませんよ。","romaji":"Iya nara, shiite sanka suru hitsuyou wa arimasen yo.","german":"If you don't like it, you don't have to take part.","furigana":"いや|し|さんか|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"食べ物は何でも好きですが、強いて挙げればラーメンです。","romaji":"Tabemono wa nan demo suki desu ga, shiite agereba raamen desu.","german":"I like all kinds of food, but if I had to pick one, it would be ramen.","furigana":"た|もの|なん|す|し|あ"}],"notes":"強いて comes from 強いる \"to force\" and means \"with insistence, by force, necessarily\". Most common is the fixed phrase 強いて言えば / 強いて挙げれば \"if I had to say/name something\", with which one hesitantly gives an answer although nothing in particular comes to mind. Also in 強いて〜する必要はない \"you don't necessarily have to ...\". Slightly elevated; in conversation it is often replaced by 無理に.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"春の気配を感じます。","romaji":"Haru no kehai o kanjimasu.","german":"I can sense the signs of spring.","furigana":"はる|けはい|かん"},{"japanese":"誰もいないはずの部屋から、人の気配がしました。","romaji":"Dare mo inai hazu no heya kara, hito no kehai ga shimashita.","german":"I sensed someone's presence from a room that should have been empty.","furigana":"だれ|へや|ひと|けはい"},{"japanese":"雨はやむ気配がありません。","romaji":"Ame wa yamu kehai ga arimasen.","german":"There are no signs of the rain stopping.","furigana":"あめ|けはい"}],"notes":"気配 (read けはい, not きはい) is a \"sign, a feeling that something is there or coming\" - something one senses rather than clearly sees. Typical: 人の気配がする \"to sense that someone is there\", 春の気配を感じる, 〜する気配がない \"there are no signs that …\". Do not confuse it with 気配り \"attentiveness, consideration\". 兆し is more literary and refers to the first sign of a change.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"回復の兆しが見えます。","romaji":"Kaifuku no kizashi ga miemasu.","german":"There are signs of recovery.","furigana":"かいふく|きざ|み"},{"japanese":"梅の花が咲き始め、春の兆しが感じられます。","romaji":"Ume no hana ga sakihajime, haru no kizashi ga kanjiraremasu.","german":"The plum blossoms are starting to bloom, and you can feel the signs of spring.","furigana":"うめ|はな|さ|はじ|はる|きざ|かん"},{"japanese":"二人の関係に変化の兆しが表れてきました。","romaji":"Futari no kankei ni henka no kizashi ga arawarete kimashita.","german":"Signs of change have begun to appear in the two people's relationship.","furigana":"ふたり|かんけい|へんか|きざ|あらわ"}],"notes":"兆し is a \"sign, omen\" - the first signal that something is changing, often for the better: 回復の兆し, 景気回復の兆しが見える, 春の兆し. Typical verbs: 兆しが見える/表れる. It sounds a bit more literary than 気配 and is often used in news. The related verb is 兆す \"to announce itself\" (rare). A clearly ominous sign is rather called 前兆.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"安全への懸念があります。","romaji":"Anzen e no kenen ga arimasu.","german":"There are concerns about safety.","furigana":"あんぜん|けねん"},{"japanese":"大雨による川の増水が懸念されています。","romaji":"Ooame ni yoru kawa no zousui ga kenen sarete imasu.","german":"There are concerns that the river will rise because of the heavy rain.","furigana":"おおあめ|かわ|ぞうすい|けねん"},{"japanese":"社長はその計画に懸念を示しました。","romaji":"Shachou wa sono keikaku ni kenen o shimeshimashita.","german":"The president expressed concerns about the plan.","furigana":"しゃちょう|けいかく|けねん|しめ"}],"notes":"懸念 is \"concern, apprehension\" and sounds much more formal than 心配: it appears mainly in news, reports and business language. Typical: 〜への懸念 \"concerns about ...\", 懸念を示す/表明する \"to voice concerns\" and the passive 〜が懸念される \"it is feared that ...\". The verb 懸念する is transitive (〜を懸念する). In personal conversation people almost always say 心配する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"環境への配慮が必要です。","romaji":"Kankyō e no hairyo ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"Consideration for the environment is necessary.","furigana":"かんきょう|はいりょ|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"お年寄りに配慮して、駅にエレベーターが作られました。","romaji":"Otoshiyori ni hairyo shite, eki ni erebeetaa ga tsukuraremashita.","german":"With the elderly in mind, an elevator was built at the station.","furigana":"としよ|はいりょ|えき|つく"},{"japanese":"いつもご配慮いただき、ありがとうございます。","romaji":"Itsumo go-hairyo itadaki, arigatou gozaimasu.","german":"Thank you for always being so considerate.","furigana":"はいりょ"}],"notes":"配慮 is \"consideration, thoughtful care\" for people or concerns. Typical: 〜に配慮する \"to take ... into consideration\", 〜への配慮, 配慮が足りない \"not enough consideration\", polite ご配慮いただきありがとうございます. It sounds factual to formal; 気配り and 気遣い are more personal and everyday. Not to be confused with 遠慮 \"restraint\" - there one holds oneself back, whereas with 配慮 one thinks of others.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"色々な工夫をしました。","romaji":"Iroiro na kufū o shimashita.","german":"I came up with all sorts of ideas.","furigana":"いろいろ|くふう"},{"japanese":"狭い部屋でも、工夫すれば広く使えます。","romaji":"Semai heya demo, kufuu sureba hiroku tsukaemasu.","german":"Even in a small room, you can make it feel spacious with some ingenuity.","furigana":"せま|へや|くふう|ひろ|つか"},{"japanese":"子どもが野菜を食べるように、料理に工夫をしています。","romaji":"Kodomo ga yasai o taberu you ni, ryouri ni kufuu o shite imasu.","german":"I put thought into my cooking so that my children will eat vegetables.","furigana":"こ|やさい|た|りょうり|くふう"}],"notes":"工夫 (read くふう) means to \"come up with\" something clever, to work out a practical solution - and the result itself: \"a trick, an idea\". Typical: 工夫する, 工夫を凝らす \"to put a lot of thought into ideas\", 〜に工夫がある \"… is well thought out\", 工夫次第で \"depending on what you come up with\". Unlike 改善 \"improvement\", 工夫 emphasizes everyday creativity (cooking, saving, studying).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"緊急措置を取りました。","romaji":"Kinkyū sochi o torimashita.","german":"We took emergency measures.","furigana":"きんきゅうそち|と"},{"japanese":"学校はいじめに対して厳しい措置を取ることにしました。","romaji":"Gakkou wa ijime ni taishite kibishii sochi o toru koto ni shimashita.","german":"The school decided to take strict measures against bullying.","furigana":"がっこう|たい|きび|そち|と"},{"japanese":"地震の被害を受けた人には、特別な措置があります。","romaji":"Jishin no higai o uketa hito ni wa, tokubetsu na sochi ga arimasu.","german":"There are special measures for people affected by the earthquake damage.","furigana":"じしん|ひがい|う|ひと|とくべつ|そち"}],"notes":"措置 is a \"measure\" taken by authorities, companies or schools and sounds official. Typical combinations: 措置を取る \"to take measures\", formal 措置を講じる, 緊急措置, 特別措置, 必要な措置. Compared with 対策 \"countermeasure against a problem\", 措置 stresses the concrete administrative action; 処置, by contrast, rather means treatment (medical) or immediate action in an individual case.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい施策を実施します。","romaji":"Atarashii shisaku o jisshi shimasu.","german":"We will implement new measures.","furigana":"あたら|しさく|じっし"},{"japanese":"市は子育て家庭を支えるための施策を打ち出しました。","romaji":"Shi wa kosodate katei o sasaeru tame no shisaku o uchidashimashita.","german":"The city has announced measures to support families raising children.","furigana":"し|こそだ|かてい|ささ|しさく|う|だ"},{"japanese":"売り上げを伸ばすために、どんな施策が考えられますか。","romaji":"Uriage o nobasu tame ni, donna shisaku ga kangaeraremasu ka.","german":"What measures could be considered to boost sales?","furigana":"う|あ|の|しさく|かんが"}],"notes":"施策 is a planned \"measure, program\", above all by governments, municipalities or companies - used almost only in official, business and news language. Typical: 施策を実施する \"to implement\", 施策を打ち出す \"to announce, to launch\", 〜のための施策. The standard reading is しさく; せさく is sometimes heard but is considered less correct. More general is 政策 \"policy (as a line of action)\", more concrete 対策 \"countermeasure\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"サービスを提供します。","romaji":"Sābisu o teikyō shimasu.","german":"We provide a service.","furigana":"ていきょう"},{"japanese":"事件について情報を提供してください。","romaji":"Jiken ni tsuite jouhou o teikyou shite kudasai.","german":"Please provide information about the incident.","furigana":"じけん|じょうほう|ていきょう"},{"japanese":"このホテルでは朝食を無料で提供しています。","romaji":"Kono hoteru de wa choushoku o muryou de teikyou shite imasu.","german":"This hotel offers breakfast free of charge.","furigana":"ちょうしょく|むりょう|ていきょう"}],"notes":"提供 means \"provision, offering, making available\" of services, information, rooms or goods; as a transitive する-verb: 〜を提供する. Common in 情報提供, 無料で提供する and for donations: 臓器提供 \"organ donation\". On television 提供 also means \"sponsorship\" (この番組は〜の提供でお送りします). For personally offering something (for example help), one rather says 申し出る.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"インターネットの普及が進んでいます。","romaji":"Intānetto no fukyū ga susunde imasu.","german":"The spread of the internet is progressing.","furigana":"ふきゅう|すす"},{"japanese":"スマートフォンは十年ほどで急に普及しました。","romaji":"Sumaatofon wa juunen hodo de kyuu ni fukyuu shimashita.","german":"Smartphones spread rapidly in about ten years.","furigana":"じゅうねん|きゅう|ふきゅう"},{"japanese":"政府は電気自動車を普及させようとしています。","romaji":"Seifu wa denki jidousha o fukyuu saseyou to shite imasu.","german":"The government is trying to promote electric cars.","furigana":"せいふ|でんきじどうしゃ|ふきゅう"}],"notes":"普及 is the \"spread\" of technology, products, knowledge or habits in society. Mostly intransitive: スマホが普及する \"smartphones spread\"; transitively one says 普及させる (普及する with を is also possible). Typical: 普及率 \"penetration rate\", 〜の普及が進む. 普及 does not fit rumors or diseases - for those one says 広まる or 広がる; 浸透 emphasizes ideas penetrating deeply.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい考え方が浸透しています。","romaji":"Atarashii kangaekata ga shintō shite imasu.","german":"New ways of thinking are taking hold.","furigana":"あたら|かんが|かた|しんとう"},{"japanese":"雨水がゆっくりと地面に浸透していきます。","romaji":"Amamizu ga yukkuri to jimen ni shintou shite ikimasu.","german":"Rainwater slowly seeps into the ground.","furigana":"あまみず|じめん|しんとう"},{"japanese":"新しいルールを社員に浸透させるのは簡単ではありません。","romaji":"Atarashii ruuru o shain ni shintou saseru no wa kantan de wa arimasen.","german":"It is not easy to get new rules to really take root among employees.","furigana":"あたら|しゃいん|しんとう|かんたん"}],"notes":"浸透 literally means the \"seeping in\" of a liquid (水が土に浸透する) and, figuratively, that ideas, rules or brands \"take hold, become firmly rooted\" deep in a group: 〜が社会に浸透する. It is intransitive; the transitive form is 浸透させる (ルールを社員に浸透させる). Unlike 普及 \"spread (quantity, reach)\", 浸透 emphasizes depth - that something is truly accepted.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"健康の促進に努めます。","romaji":"Kenkō no sokushin ni tsutomemasu.","german":"We strive to promote health.","furigana":"けんこう|そくしん|つと"},{"japanese":"この薬には消化を促進する効果があります。","romaji":"Kono kusuri ni wa shouka o sokushin suru kouka ga arimasu.","german":"This medicine has the effect of promoting digestion.","furigana":"くすり|しょうか|そくしん|こうか"},{"japanese":"店は販売促進のために割引セールを行いました。","romaji":"Mise wa hanbai sokushin no tame ni waribiki seeru o okonaimashita.","german":"The store held a discount sale to promote sales.","furigana":"みせ|はんばいそくしん|わりびき|おこな"}],"notes":"促進 is the \"promotion, acceleration\" of a process, transitive as 〜を促進する. Typical in administration, business and medicine: 販売促進 (short 販促) \"sales promotion\", 消化を促進する \"to stimulate digestion\", 〜の促進を図る. It sounds formal; in conversation one rather says 進める or 早める. 推進 stresses pushing a project forward, 促進 accelerating a development.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"インフレの抑制が必要です。","romaji":"Infure no yokusei ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"Curbing inflation is necessary.","furigana":"よくせい|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"この薬は食欲を抑制する働きがあります。","romaji":"Kono kusuri wa shokuyoku o yokusei suru hataraki ga arimasu.","german":"This medicine works to suppress appetite.","furigana":"くすり|しょくよく|よくせい|はたら"},{"japanese":"彼は怒りを抑制できずに、大きな声を出してしまった。","romaji":"Kare wa ikari o yokusei dekizu ni, ookina koe o dashite shimatta.","german":"He could not restrain his anger and ended up raising his voice.","furigana":"かれ|いか|よくせい|おお|こえ|だ"}],"notes":"抑制 means \"curbing, suppression, inhibition\" - something is kept under control so that it does not increase. Transitive: 〜を抑制する (物価上昇を抑制する, 食欲を抑制する, 感情を抑制する). Typical in business, medicine and psychology. In everyday speech one mostly says the simpler 抑える. The political suppression of people, by contrast, is 弾圧 or 抑圧.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"規制の緩和が進んでいます。","romaji":"Kisei no kanwa ga susunde imasu.","german":"The easing of regulations is progressing.","furigana":"きせい|かんわ|すす"},{"japanese":"この薬は痛みを緩和してくれます。","romaji":"Kono kusuri wa itami o kanwa shite kuremasu.","german":"This medicine relieves pain.","furigana":"くすり|いた|かんわ"},{"japanese":"新しい道路ができて、駅前の渋滞が緩和されました。","romaji":"Atarashii douro ga dekite, ekimae no juutai ga kanwa saremashita.","german":"Since the new road was built, the congestion in front of the station has eased.","furigana":"あたら|どうろ|えきまえ|じゅうたい|かんわ"}],"notes":"緩和 means \"easing, relief, relaxation\" of rules, pain, tension or traffic. It is both transitive and intransitive: 規制を緩和する \"to relax regulations\", 痛みが緩和される \"the pain subsides\". Fixed terms are 規制緩和 \"deregulation\", 金融緩和 \"monetary easing\", 緩和ケア \"palliative care\". In everyday speech people often say 和らげる/和らぐ or ゆるめる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"セキュリティを強化します。","romaji":"Sekyuriti o kyōka shimasu.","german":"We will strengthen security.","furigana":"きょうか"},{"japanese":"警察は夜のパトロールを強化しています。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa yoru no patorooru o kyouka shite imasu.","german":"The police have stepped up their night patrols.","furigana":"けいさつ|よる|きょうか"},{"japanese":"来年に向けて、チームの守備を強化したい。","romaji":"Rainen ni mukete, chiimu no shubi o kyouka shitai.","german":"Looking ahead to next year, I want to strengthen the team's defense.","furigana":"らいねん|む|しゅび|きょうか"}],"notes":"強化 is \"strengthening, reinforcement\" and is mostly used as a transitive する-verb: X を強化する, passive 強化される. Typical are 対策を強化する, 警備を強化する, 規制を強化する, and compounds such as 強化ガラス \"tempered glass\" or 強化合宿 \"intensive training camp\". The opposite is 弱体化, or for rules 緩和 \"relaxation\". Compared with the general verb 強める, 強化 sounds matter-of-fact and suits organizations, systems and measures.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地域経済の活性化を図ります。","romaji":"Chiiki keizai no kasseika o hakarimasu.","german":"We aim to revitalize the regional economy.","furigana":"ちいきけいざい|かっせいか|はか"},{"japanese":"商店街を活性化するために、毎月イベントを開いています。","romaji":"Shoutengai o kasseika suru tame ni, maitsuki ibento o hiraite imasu.","german":"To revitalize the shopping street, we hold an event every month.","furigana":"しょうてんがい|かっせいか|まいつき|ひら"},{"japanese":"朝ご飯を食べると、脳が活性化すると言われています。","romaji":"Asagohan o taberu to, nou ga kasseika suru to iwarete imasu.","german":"It is said that eating breakfast activates the brain.","furigana":"あさ|はん|た|のう|かっせいか|い"}],"notes":"活性化 means \"revitalization, activation\": the economy, towns, organizations or brain cells are made lively again. With -化 it works as a する-verb, both transitive and intransitive: 町を活性化する / 経済が活性化する; often also 活性化させる. 地域の活性化 \"revitalization of the region\" and 〜の活性化を図る are very common. It is a typical word of politics and advertising; more personal is 元気にする.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"プロセスの最適化を行います。","romaji":"Purosesu no saitekika o okonaimasu.","german":"We will optimize the process.","furigana":"さいてきか|おこな"},{"japanese":"このサイトはスマホ用に最適化されています。","romaji":"Kono saito wa sumaho you ni saitekika sarete imasu.","german":"This website is optimized for smartphones.","furigana":"よう|さいてきか"},{"japanese":"パソコンの設定を最適化したら、動きが速くなりました。","romaji":"Pasokon no settei o saitekika shitara, ugoki ga hayaku narimashita.","german":"After I optimized my PC settings, it started running faster.","furigana":"せってい|さいてきか|うご|はや"}],"notes":"最適化 means \"optimization\" (最適 \"best suited\" + 化) and is a typical technical term in IT, engineering and business administration: 〜を最適化する \"to optimize\", 〜に最適化された \"tailored to ...\", 検索エンジン最適化 \"search engine optimization (SEO)\". Unlike 改善 \"improvement\", it means bringing something to the best possible state for a given purpose. In everyday speech people say 一番いい形にする instead.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"業務の効率化を進めます。","romaji":"Gyōmu no kōritsuka o susumemasu.","german":"We will push forward with improving operational efficiency.","furigana":"ぎょうむ|こうりつか|すす"},{"japanese":"新しいソフトを入れて、経理の仕事を効率化しました。","romaji":"Atarashii sofuto o irete, keiri no shigoto o kouritsuka shimashita.","german":"We installed new software and made accounting work more efficient.","furigana":"あたら|い|けいり|しごと|こうりつか"},{"japanese":"家事を効率化して、自分の時間を増やしたいです。","romaji":"Kaji o kouritsuka shite, jibun no jikan o fuyashitai desu.","german":"I want to make housework more efficient and have more time for myself.","furigana":"かじ|こうりつか|じぶん|じかん|ふ"}],"notes":"効率化 means \"improving efficiency, streamlining\": getting work done with less time and effort. Typical: 業務の効率化 \"streamlining of operations\", 〜を効率化する, 効率化を図る/進める. Popular in office and management language. The base word 効率 \"efficiency\" appears in 効率がいい/悪い; 効率的(な) means \"efficient\". Do not confuse it with 能率 \"efficiency (of a person's work)\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ニーズが多様化しています。","romaji":"Nīzu ga tayōka shite imasu.","german":"Needs are diversifying.","furigana":"たようか"},{"japanese":"最近は働き方が多様化して、家で働く人も増えました。","romaji":"Saikin wa hatarakikata ga tayouka shite, ie de hataraku hito mo fuemashita.","german":"Ways of working have become more varied recently, and more people work from home.","furigana":"さいきん|はたら|かた|たようか|いえ|はたら|ひと|ふ"},{"japanese":"若い人の価値観の多様化について話し合いました。","romaji":"Wakai hito no kachikan no tayouka ni tsuite hanashiaimashita.","german":"We discussed the diversification of young people's values.","furigana":"わか|ひと|かちかん|たようか|はな|あ"}],"notes":"多様化 means \"diversification, becoming more varied\" (多様 \"diverse\" + 化) and is mostly used intransitively: 価値観が多様化する \"values become more diverse\", ニーズの多様化. Often with 進む: 多様化が進む. It describes a social development; the result is called 多様性 \"diversity\". For deliberate spreading (e.g. of investments), people prefer 分散.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"国際化が進んでいます。","romaji":"Kokusaika ga susunde imasu.","german":"Internationalization is advancing.","furigana":"こくさいか|すす"},{"japanese":"大学の国際化のために、英語の授業が増えました。","romaji":"Daigaku no kokusaika no tame ni, eigo no jugyou ga fuemashita.","german":"English classes have increased as part of the university's internationalization.","furigana":"だいがく|こくさいか|えいご|じゅぎょう|ふ"},{"japanese":"この町も外国人の住民が増えて、国際化してきました。","romaji":"Kono machi mo gaikokujin no juumin ga fuete, kokusaika shite kimashita.","german":"This town, too, has become more international as the number of foreign residents has grown.","furigana":"まち|がいこくじん|じゅうみん|ふ|こくさいか"}],"notes":"国際化 means \"internationalization\" of society, companies, universities or cities; intransitive 国際化が進む / 社会が国際化する, and also transitive 大学を国際化する. In Japan it has been a buzzword since the 1980s, often in the sense of \"opening up to foreigners and other cultures\". Related: グローバル化 \"globalization\", which refers more to worldwide economic interconnection.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"社会の情報化が進んでいます。","romaji":"Shakai no jouhouka ga susunde imasu.","german":"The computerization of society is advancing.","furigana":"しゃかい|じょうほうか|すす"},{"japanese":"情報化社会では、個人情報を守ることがとても大切です。","romaji":"Jouhouka shakai de wa, kojin jouhou o mamoru koto ga totemo taisetsu desu.","german":"In an information society, protecting personal data is very important.","furigana":"じょうほうかしゃかい|こじんじょうほう|まも|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"学校の情報化で、生徒一人一人にタブレットが配られました。","romaji":"Gakkou no jouhouka de, seito hitori hitori ni taburetto ga kubararemashita.","german":"With the digitalization of schools, every student was given a tablet.","furigana":"がっこう|じょうほうか|せいとひとりひとり|くば"}],"notes":"情報化 refers to \"informatization, digitalization\" - the process by which computers and information networks permeate society and work. It occurs most often in fixed terms: 情報化社会 \"information society\", 情報化が進む, 学校の情報化. The word sounds somewhat dated (a buzzword of the 1980s/90s); today, for government offices and companies, people often speak of デジタル化 or DX.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"IT産業が成長しています。","romaji":"IT sangyō ga seichō shite imasu.","german":"The IT industry is growing.","furigana":"さんぎょう|せいちょう"},{"japanese":"この町の主な産業は観光です。","romaji":"Kono machi no omo na sangyou wa kankou desu.","german":"The main industry of this town is tourism.","furigana":"まち|おも|さんぎょう|かんこう"},{"japanese":"日本は自動車産業が盛んです。","romaji":"Nihon wa jidousha sangyou ga sakan desu.","german":"The automobile industry is thriving in Japan.","furigana":"にほん|じどうしゃさんぎょう|さか"}],"notes":"産業 is the general term for branches of the economy of all kinds, from agriculture to services; hence the many compounds such as 自動車産業, 観光産業 or IT産業. That an industry is strong in a region is expressed with 産業が盛んだ. Do not confuse it with 工業: 工業 means only manufacturing industry with factories, whereas 産業 covers every line of economic activity. The word is factual and typical of news, textbooks and economic topics.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"農業が盛んな地域です。","romaji":"Nōgyō ga sakan na chiiki desu.","german":"It is a region where agriculture thrives.","furigana":"のうぎょう|さか|ちいき"},{"japanese":"祖父は田舎で農業をしています。","romaji":"Sofu wa inaka de nougyou o shite imasu.","german":"My grandfather farms in the countryside.","furigana":"そふ|いなか|のうぎょう"},{"japanese":"会社を辞めて、農業を始める若い人が増えています。","romaji":"Kaisha o yamete, nougyou o hajimeru wakai hito ga fuete imasu.","german":"More and more young people are quitting their jobs and taking up farming.","furigana":"かいしゃ|や|のうぎょう|はじ|わか|ひと|ふ"}],"notes":"農業 is \"agriculture\" as an industry and an occupation. Typical: 農業をする/営む \"to engage in farming\", 農業が盛ん \"agriculture flourishes\", 農業で生活する. Related are 農家 \"farm, farming family\", 農民 \"farmer (historical)\" and 農作物 \"agricultural products\". The work in the field itself is 畑仕事 or 農作業.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"漁業が主な産業です。","romaji":"Gyogyō ga omo na sangyō desu.","german":"Fishing is the main industry.","furigana":"ぎょぎょう|おも|さんぎょう"},{"japanese":"この島の人の多くは漁業で生活しています。","romaji":"Kono shima no hito no ooku wa gyogyou de seikatsu shite imasu.","german":"Many people on this island make their living from fishing.","furigana":"しま|ひと|おお|ぎょぎょう|せいかつ"},{"japanese":"海の温度が上がって、漁業に大きな影響が出ています。","romaji":"Umi no ondo ga agatte, gyogyou ni ookina eikyou ga dete imasu.","german":"The rising sea temperature is having a major impact on the fishing industry.","furigana":"うみ|おんど|あ|ぎょぎょう|おお|えいきょう|で"}],"notes":"漁業 is \"fishing\" as an economic sector, including fish farming. Typical: 漁業が盛んな町, 漁業で生活する, 漁業関係者 \"people working in the fishing industry\". The fishermen themselves are 漁師, and the fishing port is 漁港. Angling as a hobby is 釣り. Note when reading: 漁 is read ぎょ here (but りょう in 漁師).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"商業施設が増えています。","romaji":"Shōgyō shisetsu ga fuete imasu.","german":"Commercial facilities are increasing.","furigana":"しょうぎょうしせつ|ふ"},{"japanese":"大阪は昔から商業の町として知られています。","romaji":"Oosaka wa mukashi kara shougyou no machi to shite shirarete imasu.","german":"Osaka has long been known as a commercial city.","furigana":"おおさか|むかし|しょうぎょう|まち|し"},{"japanese":"兄は商業高校を卒業して、銀行に就職しました。","romaji":"Ani wa shougyou koukou o sotsugyou shite, ginkou ni shuushoku shimashita.","german":"My older brother graduated from a commercial high school and got a job at a bank.","furigana":"あに|しょうぎょうこうこう|そつぎょう|ぎんこう|しゅうしょく"}],"notes":"商業 is \"commerce, trade\" as an economic sector - buying and selling goods. Typical in compounds: 商業施設 \"commercial facility, shopping complex\", 商業高校 \"commercial high school\", 商業地域. 商業的(な) means \"commercial\" (often slightly pejorative: 商業的な映画). The actual trade between countries is rather 貿易, and an individual's business 商売.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"製造コストを削減します。","romaji":"Seizō kosuto o sakugen shimasu.","german":"We will cut manufacturing costs.","furigana":"せいぞう|さくげん"},{"japanese":"この工場では車の部品を製造しています。","romaji":"Kono koujou de wa kuruma no buhin o seizou shite imasu.","german":"This factory manufactures car parts.","furigana":"こうじょう|くるま|ぶひん|せいぞう"},{"japanese":"パンの袋に製造年月日が書いてあります。","romaji":"Pan no fukuro ni seizou nengappi ga kaite arimasu.","german":"The manufacturing date is written on the bread bag.","furigana":"ふくろ|せいぞうねんがっぴ|か"}],"notes":"製造 is \"manufacturing, production\" on an industrial scale, transitive as 〜を製造する. Typical: 製造業 \"manufacturing industry\", 製造元 \"manufacturer\", 製造年月日 \"date of manufacture\", 製造コスト. The more general, everyday word is 作る; 生産 emphasizes the quantity produced (also in agriculture), 製作 the making of individual works (films, furniture).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"流通システムを改善します。","romaji":"Ryūtsū shisutemu o kaizen shimasu.","german":"We will improve the distribution system.","furigana":"りゅうつう|かいぜん"},{"japanese":"偽物のお金が流通しているので、注意してください。","romaji":"Nisemono no okane ga ryuutsuu shite iru node, chuui shite kudasai.","german":"Counterfeit money is in circulation, so please be careful.","furigana":"にせもの|かね|りゅうつう|ちゅうい"},{"japanese":"この部屋は窓が小さくて、空気の流通が悪いです。","romaji":"Kono heya wa mado ga chiisakute, kuuki no ryuutsuu ga warui desu.","german":"This room has small windows, and the air circulation is poor.","furigana":"へや|まど|ちい|くうき|りゅうつう|わる"}],"notes":"流通 means \"circulation, distribution\": goods travel from the manufacturer to the consumer (流通業 \"distribution and logistics industry\", 流通コスト), money is in circulation (お金が流通する). In its second meaning it is the \"circulating\" of air or water (空気の流通). As a する-verb it is intransitive: 〜が流通する \"is in circulation, is traded\". For the sale itself one says 販売.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい市場に参入します。","romaji":"Atarashii shijō ni sannyū shimasu.","german":"We will enter a new market.","furigana":"あたら|しじょう|さんにゅう"},{"japanese":"日本の株式市場は今日、大きく下がりました。","romaji":"Nihon no kabushiki shijou wa kyou, ookiku sagarimashita.","german":"The Japanese stock market fell sharply today.","furigana":"にほん|かぶしきしじょう|きょう|おお|さ"},{"japanese":"新商品を出す前に、市場調査をしましょう。","romaji":"Shin shouhin o dasu mae ni, shijou chousa o shimashou.","german":"Let's do market research before launching the new product.","furigana":"しんしょうひん|だ|まえ|しじょうちょうさ"}],"notes":"市場 has two readings: しじょう is the \"market\" in the economic sense (株式市場 \"stock market\", 市場調査 \"market research\", 市場に参入する \"to enter a market\"); いちば is a concrete market with stalls (魚市場 \"fish market\"). This card teaches しじょう. Typical: 市場が拡大する, 国内市場, 海外市場. A local market in everyday life is rather called いちば or マーケット.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大企業で働いています。","romaji":"Daikigyō de hataraite imasu.","german":"I work for a large company.","furigana":"だいきぎょう|はたら"},{"japanese":"日本の会社の多くは中小企業です。","romaji":"Nihon no kaisha no ooku wa chuushou kigyou desu.","german":"Most Japanese companies are small and medium-sized enterprises.","furigana":"にほん|かいしゃ|おお|ちゅうしょうきぎょう"},{"japanese":"その企業は環境に優しい製品で有名です。","romaji":"Sono kigyou wa kankyou ni yasashii seihin de yuumei desu.","german":"That company is famous for its eco-friendly products.","furigana":"きぎょう|かんきょう|やさ|せいひん|ゆうめい"}],"notes":"企業 is a \"company, enterprise\" as an economic organization and sounds more objective than 会社: 大企業 \"large company\", 中小企業 \"small and medium-sized enterprises\", 外資系企業 \"foreign-owned company\", 企業に就職する. In everyday speech people almost always say 会社 about their own firm; 企業 appears more in news, statistics and job applications. \"To found a company\" is 起業 (same reading!).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"会社を経営しています。","romaji":"Kaisha o keiei shite imasu.","german":"I run a company.","furigana":"かいしゃ|けいえい"},{"japanese":"おじは駅の近くで小さなレストランを経営しています。","romaji":"Oji wa eki no chikaku de chiisana resutoran o keiei shite imasu.","german":"My uncle runs a small restaurant near the station.","furigana":"えき|ちか|ちい|けいえい"},{"japanese":"客が減って、店の経営が苦しくなりました。","romaji":"Kyaku ga hette, mise no keiei ga kurushiku narimashita.","german":"With fewer customers, the shop's finances have become difficult.","furigana":"きゃく|へ|みせ|けいえい|くる"}],"notes":"経営 is the \"management, running\" of a company or business, transitive as 〜を経営する: 店を経営する \"to run a shop\". Typical: 経営者 \"business owner, executive\", 経営が苦しい \"the business is struggling\", 経営学 \"business administration\". For managing people or projects, people say 管理 or マネジメント; 運営 refers to running facilities, clubs or events.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"組織を改革します。","romaji":"Soshiki o kaikaku shimasu.","german":"We will reform the organization.","furigana":"そしき|かいかく"},{"japanese":"大きな組織の中で働くのは大変なこともあります。","romaji":"Ookina soshiki no naka de hataraku no wa taihen na koto mo arimasu.","german":"Working within a large organization can be hard at times.","furigana":"おお|そしき|なか|はたら|たいへん"},{"japanese":"彼らはボランティアの組織を作りました。","romaji":"Karera wa borantia no soshiki o tsukurimashita.","german":"They set up a volunteer organization.","furigana":"かれ|そしき|つく"}],"notes":"組織 refers to an organization as an entity (company, club, party) and also to its structure: 組織の一員 \"a member of the organization\", 大きな組織 \"a large organization\", 組織的 \"organized, systematic\". As a する-verb, 組織する means \"to form, to establish (a group)\" - to \"organize\" an event, by contrast, is 企画する or 準備する. In biology 組織 also means \"tissue\" (筋肉の組織).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"営業部門で働いています。","romaji":"Eigyō bumon de hataraite imasu.","german":"I work in the sales division.","furigana":"えいぎょうぶもん|はたら"},{"japanese":"この会社は来年、IT部門を大きくする予定です。","romaji":"Kono kaisha wa rainen, IT bumon o ookiku suru yotei desu.","german":"This company plans to expand its IT division next year.","furigana":"かいしゃ|らいねん|ぶもん|おお|よてい"},{"japanese":"彼女の写真はコンテストの風景部門で一位になりました。","romaji":"Kanojo no shashin wa kontesuto no fuukei bumon de ichii ni narimashita.","german":"Her photo took first place in the landscape category of the contest.","furigana":"かのじょ|しゃしん|ふうけいぶもん|いちい"}],"notes":"部門 is a sub-unit of a larger whole - in companies a division or business area (営業部門, 技術部門). One's own department is usually called 部 or 課 in everyday speech (営業部, 総務課); 部門 sounds more general and formal and often groups several departments together. A second important use: category in competitions and awards, e.g. 小説部門で優勝する \"to win in the novel category\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"優秀な人材を育てます。","romaji":"Yūshū na jinzai o sodatemasu.","german":"We train outstanding personnel.","furigana":"ゆうしゅう|じんざい|そだ"},{"japanese":"地方では若い人材が不足しています。","romaji":"Chihou de wa wakai jinzai ga fusoku shite imasu.","german":"There is a shortage of young talent in rural areas.","furigana":"ちほう|わか|じんざい|ふそく"},{"japanese":"彼はうちの会社にとって大切な人材です。","romaji":"Kare wa uchi no kaisha ni totte taisetsu na jinzai desu.","german":"He is a valuable asset to our company.","furigana":"かれ|かいしゃ|たいせつ|じんざい"}],"notes":"人材 refers to people as a resource of an organization, usually with a view to their abilities: 優秀な人材 \"highly qualified people\", 人材を育てる \"to train the next generation\", 人材が不足している \"there is a shortage of skilled people\". Mostly used collectively, but it can also mean a single person: 彼は貴重な人材だ \"he is a valuable asset\". Unlike 人員 (staffing level, headcount), 人材 stresses quality; 人材派遣 is \"temporary staffing, personnel placement\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"適切な報酬を支払います。","romaji":"Tekisetsu na hōshū o shiharaimasu.","german":"We will pay appropriate remuneration.","furigana":"てきせつ|ほうしゅう|しはら"},{"japanese":"ボランティアなので、報酬はもらっていません。","romaji":"Borantia na node, houshuu wa moratte imasen.","german":"It's volunteer work, so I don't receive any pay.","furigana":"ほうしゅう"},{"japanese":"翻訳の報酬は一ページ三千円です。","romaji":"Hon'yaku no houshuu wa ichi peeji sanzen en desu.","german":"The fee for the translation is 3,000 yen per page.","furigana":"ほんやく|ほうしゅう|いち|さんぜんえん"}],"notes":"報酬 is the compensation for work or a service, usually in money: 報酬を払う／受け取る, 成功報酬 \"success fee\", 役員報酬 \"executive compensation\". It sounds more factual and formal than 給料 (regular salary of employees) and fits especially fees for commissions, freelance work and side jobs. Also non-material: 努力の報酬 \"the reward of effort\". For a reward given to children one rather says ご褒美.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大阪に転勤になりました。","romaji":"Ōsaka ni tenkin ni narimashita.","german":"I was transferred to Osaka.","furigana":"おおさか|てんきん"},{"japanese":"父の転勤で、子どものころ何回も引っ越しました。","romaji":"Chichi no tenkin de, kodomo no koro nankai mo hikkoshimashita.","german":"Because of my father's job transfers, we moved many times when I was a child.","furigana":"ちち|てんきん|こ|なんかい|ひ|こ"},{"japanese":"転勤のない会社で働きたいです。","romaji":"Tenkin no nai kaisha de hatarakitai desu.","german":"I want to work for a company with no transfers.","furigana":"てんきん|かいしゃ|はたら"}],"notes":"転勤 is a transfer within the same company to a different location, usually with a move. Typical: 〜に転勤になる \"to be transferred to ...\" (decided by the employer) or 〜へ転勤する; 転勤族 are families who move often because of transfers. Do not confuse it with 転職 \"changing employers\" and 異動 (moving to a different department, even without a change of location). If you go to the new place without your family, it is called 単身赴任.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 25"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"有給休暇を取ります。","romaji":"Yūkyū kyūka o torimasu.","german":"I will take paid leave.","furigana":"ゆうきゅうきゅうか|と"},{"japanese":"来週、有給休暇を使って温泉に行きます。","romaji":"Raishuu, yuukyuu kyuuka o tsukatte onsen ni ikimasu.","german":"Next week I'm using paid leave to go to a hot spring.","furigana":"らいしゅう|ゆうきゅうきゅうか|つか|おんせん|い"},{"japanese":"今年は有給休暇がまだ十日残っています。","romaji":"Kotoshi wa yuukyuu kyuuka ga mada tooka nokotte imasu.","german":"I still have ten days of paid leave left this year.","furigana":"ことし|ゆうきゅうきゅうか|とおかのこ"}],"notes":"有給休暇 is the legally guaranteed paid leave; in conversation people almost always say 有給 or 有休 for short. One says 有給休暇を取る／使う, to apply for it is 申請する, and 有給がまだ残っている means \"I still have leave days left\". In Japan it is often taken by the day rather than as a long vacation. General \"time off, holidays\" is 休み or 休暇.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"残業手当が出ます。","romaji":"Zangyō teate ga demasu.","german":"Overtime pay is provided.","furigana":"ざんぎょうてあて|で"},{"japanese":"うちの会社は住宅手当がありません。","romaji":"Uchi no kaisha wa juutaku teate ga arimasen.","german":"Our company has no housing allowance.","furigana":"かいしゃ|じゅうたくてあて"},{"japanese":"先生がすぐにけがの手当をしてくれました。","romaji":"Sensei ga sugu ni kega no teate o shite kuremashita.","german":"The teacher treated the injury right away.","furigana":"せんせい|てあて"}],"notes":"手当 (also written 手当て) as a work term is an allowance in addition to the base salary: 残業手当 \"overtime pay\", 住宅手当 \"housing allowance\", 通勤手当 \"commuting allowance\", 児童手当 \"child benefit\". One says 手当が出る／つく \"there is an allowance\". The second common meaning is treating an injury: けがの手当をする \"to treat a wound\", 応急手当 \"first aid\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"海外に赴任します。","romaji":"Kaigai ni funin shimasu.","german":"I will be posted overseas.","furigana":"かいがい|ふにん"},{"japanese":"父は来月から名古屋に単身赴任します。","romaji":"Chichi wa raigetsu kara Nagoya ni tanshin funin shimasu.","german":"My father will go to Nagoya on a solo assignment from next month.","furigana":"ちち|らいげつ|なごや|たんしんふにん"},{"japanese":"新しい先生は四月にこの学校に赴任してきました。","romaji":"Atarashii sensei wa shigatsu ni kono gakkou ni funin shite kimashita.","german":"The new teacher took up her post at this school in April.","furigana":"あたら|せんせい|しがつ|がっこう|ふにん"}],"notes":"赴任する means to travel to a new place of duty in order to take up a position there; the destination takes に or へ: 海外に赴任する. It stresses the change of location and is used mainly for company employees, teachers and diplomats: 赴任先 \"new place of duty\", 海外赴任 \"overseas posting\". 単身赴任 means going to the new location without one's family - very common in Japan. 転勤 is the transfer itself, 赴任 the going there and taking up the post.","levelNote":"Derived from 赴任 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"社長が辞任しました。","romaji":"Shachō ga jinin shimashita.","german":"The company president resigned.","furigana":"しゃちょう|じにん"},{"japanese":"大臣は問題の責任を取って辞任しました。","romaji":"Daijin wa mondai no sekinin o totte jinin shimashita.","german":"The minister took responsibility for the problem and resigned.","furigana":"だいじん|もんだい|せきにん|と|じにん"},{"japanese":"彼女は体調を理由に会長を辞任しました。","romaji":"Kanojo wa taichou o riyuu ni kaichou o jinin shimashita.","german":"She resigned as chairperson for health reasons.","furigana":"かのじょ|たいちょう|りゆう|かいちょう|じにん"}],"notes":"辞任する means to step down voluntarily from an office or position of responsibility; the office takes を: 社長を辞任する, 大臣が辞任する. It is a word from the news and formal contexts, often in 責任を取って辞任する \"to take responsibility and resign\". An ordinary resignation as an employee is 仕事を辞める or 退職する in everyday speech; 辞退する, by contrast, means \"to decline an offer, to give something up\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい社員を採用します。","romaji":"Atarashii shain o saiyō shimasu.","german":"We will hire new employees.","furigana":"あたら|しゃいん|さいよう"},{"japanese":"面接を受けた会社に採用されました。","romaji":"Mensetsu o uketa kaisha ni saiyou saremashita.","german":"I was hired by the company where I had the interview.","furigana":"めんせつ|う|かいしゃ|さいよう"},{"japanese":"私の案が会議で採用されました。","romaji":"Watashi no an ga kaigi de saiyou saremashita.","german":"My proposal was adopted at the meeting.","furigana":"わたし|あん|かいぎ|さいよう"}],"notes":"採用する has two main meanings: to \"hire\" personnel (新卒を採用する, 採用試験 \"hiring exam\") and to \"adopt, accept\" ideas, proposals or methods (案を採用する). From the applicant's point of view one says the passive 採用される \"to be hired\"; the acceptance notice is 採用通知, the rejection 不採用. A more casual word for \"to employ\" is 雇う; the employer-side counterpart is 解雇する \"to dismiss\".","levelNote":"Derived from 採用 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不当に解雇されました。","romaji":"Futō ni kaiko saremashita.","german":"I was unfairly dismissed.","furigana":"ふとう|かいこ"},{"japanese":"会社は売り上げが下がって、社員を五十人解雇しました。","romaji":"Kaisha wa uriage ga sagatte, shain o gojuunin kaiko shimashita.","german":"Because sales dropped, the company laid off fifty employees.","furigana":"かいしゃ|う|あ|さ|しゃいん|ごじゅうにんかいこ"},{"japanese":"遅刻が多いと、解雇されることもありますよ。","romaji":"Chikoku ga ooi to, kaiko sareru koto mo arimasu yo.","german":"If you're late often, you might get fired, you know.","furigana":"ちこく|おお|かいこ"}],"notes":"解雇する is dismissal by the employer, a formal and legal word: 社員を解雇する, 不当解雇 \"unfair dismissal\". Those affected say 解雇される; colloquially it is クビになる \"to get the sack\", and with job cuts also リストラされる. If you quit yourself, you say 辞める or 退職する, never 解雇する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"外部に委託します。","romaji":"Gaibu ni itaku shimasu.","german":"We will outsource it.","furigana":"がいぶ|いたく"},{"japanese":"市はごみの収集を民間の会社に委託しています。","romaji":"Shi wa gomi no shuushuu o minkan no kaisha ni itaku shite imasu.","german":"The city has outsourced garbage collection to a private company.","furigana":"し|しゅうしゅう|みんかん|かいしゃ|いたく"},{"japanese":"この店では、作家から委託された作品を売っています。","romaji":"Kono mise de wa, sakka kara itaku sareta sakuhin o utte imasu.","german":"This store sells works that artists have placed on consignment.","furigana":"みせ|さっか|いたく|さくひん|う"}],"notes":"委託する means handing over a task or goods to another company or person to carry out; the recipient takes に, the thing takes を: 清掃を外部の会社に委託する \"to outsource the cleaning to an outside company\". Common in business and administration: 業務委託 \"commissioned work\", 委託販売 \"consignment sales\". In everyday speech one rather says 頼む or 任せる; 委託 sounds businesslike and contractual.","levelNote":"Derived from 委託 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい契約を結びました。","romaji":"Atarashii keiyaku o musubimashita.","german":"We concluded a new contract.","furigana":"あたら|けいやく|むす"},{"japanese":"新しいアパートを契約しました。","romaji":"Atarashii apaato o keiyaku shimashita.","german":"I signed a contract for a new apartment.","furigana":"あたら|けいやく"},{"japanese":"その選手はアメリカのチームと三年契約しました。","romaji":"Sono senshu wa Amerika no chiimu to sannen keiyaku shimashita.","german":"The player signed a three-year contract with an American team.","furigana":"せんしゅ|さんねんけいやく"}],"notes":"契約する means to conclude a contract; the contract partner takes と, the object takes を: 会社と契約する, スマホを契約する. Equivalent but somewhat more formal is 契約を結ぶ; to cancel a contract is 解約する, to renew it 契約を更新する. Important compounds: 契約書 \"contract document\", 契約社員 \"fixed-term employee\".","levelNote":"Derived from 契約 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"明日納品します。","romaji":"Ashita nōhin shimasu.","german":"We will deliver tomorrow.","furigana":"あしたのうひん"},{"japanese":"注文された商品は金曜日までに納品いたします。","romaji":"Chuumon sareta shouhin wa kin'youbi made ni nouhin itashimasu.","german":"We will deliver the ordered goods by Friday.","furigana":"ちゅうもん|しょうひん|きんようび|のうひん"},{"japanese":"デザインのデータを先週お客様に納品しました。","romaji":"Dezain no deeta o senshuu okyakusama ni nouhin shimashita.","german":"I delivered the design files to the client last week.","furigana":"せんしゅう|きゃくさま|のうひん"}],"notes":"納品する means to deliver ordered goods or a finished work product to the customer; the recipient takes に: 取引先に商品を納品する. It is business language from trade and production and also applies to services such as software, designs or translations. Related are 納品書 \"delivery note\" and 納期 \"delivery date\". Delivering a parcel is 届ける or 配達する in everyday speech; the ordering side is 発注する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"部品を発注しました。","romaji":"Buhin o hacchū shimashita.","german":"We ordered parts.","furigana":"ぶひん|はっちゅう"},{"japanese":"在庫が少ないので、紙を多めに発注してください。","romaji":"Zaiko ga sukunai node, kami o oome ni hacchuu shite kudasai.","german":"Stock is low, so please order a little more paper.","furigana":"ざいこ|すく|かみ|おお|はっちゅう"},{"japanese":"市は新しい橋の工事を地元の会社に発注しました。","romaji":"Shi wa atarashii hashi no kouji o jimoto no kaisha ni hacchuu shimashita.","german":"The city commissioned a local company to build the new bridge.","furigana":"し|あたら|はし|こうじ|じもと|かいしゃ|はっちゅう"}],"notes":"発注する means to place an order with a supplier or contractor, or to award a contract: 部品を工場に発注する. It is business language between companies and government offices; as a private customer in a shop or online, people say 注文する. The counterpart is 受注する \"to receive an order\"; 発注書 is the written order, 発注ミス an ordering error.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"在庫を確認します。","romaji":"Zaiko o kakunin shimasu.","german":"I will check the inventory.","furigana":"ざいこ|かくにん"},{"japanese":"すみません、このサイズの在庫はもうありません。","romaji":"Sumimasen, kono saizu no zaiko wa mou arimasen.","german":"I'm sorry, we no longer have this size in stock.","furigana":"ざいこ"},{"japanese":"人気の商品なので、ネットでもすぐに在庫切れになりました。","romaji":"Ninki no shouhin na node, netto demo sugu ni zaikogire ni narimashita.","german":"Because it's a popular product, it sold out online right away, too.","furigana":"にんき|しょうひん|ざいこぎ"}],"notes":"在庫 is the stock of goods in a warehouse or shop: 在庫がある／ない, 在庫を確認する, 在庫切れ \"out of stock, sold out\". In shops one often hears 在庫を確認してまいります \"I will check the stock\". Having too much stock is 在庫を抱える. For supplies at home one rather says 買い置き or ストック.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"品質を向上させます。","romaji":"Hinshitsu o kōjō sasemasu.","german":"We will improve quality.","furigana":"ひんしつ|こうじょう"},{"japanese":"値段は少し高いですが、品質がいいです。","romaji":"Nedan wa sukoshi takai desu ga, hinshitsu ga ii desu.","german":"The price is a little high, but the quality is good.","furigana":"ねだん|すこ|たか|ひんしつ"},{"japanese":"工場では毎日品質のチェックをしています。","romaji":"Koujou de wa mainichi hinshitsu no chekku o shite imasu.","german":"The factory checks quality every day.","furigana":"こうじょう|まいにちひんしつ"}],"notes":"品質 is the quality of products or services: 品質がいい／高い, 品質管理 \"quality control\", 品質を保証する \"to guarantee quality\". It is more factual and narrower than 質, which generally means \"quality, nature\" (睡眠の質, 人の質); 品質 is not used for people. Typical is the contrast with price: 値段より品質.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"車の性能が向上しました。","romaji":"Kuruma no seinō ga kōjō shimashita.","german":"The performance of the car has improved.","furigana":"くるま|せいのう|こうじょう"},{"japanese":"このパソコンは安いのに性能がいいです。","romaji":"Kono pasokon wa yasui noni seinou ga ii desu.","german":"This computer is cheap but performs well.","furigana":"やす|せいのう"},{"japanese":"カメラの性能を比べてから買います。","romaji":"Kamera no seinou o kurabete kara kaimasu.","german":"I will compare the performance of the cameras before buying one.","furigana":"せいのう|くら|か"}],"notes":"性能 is the technical performance of machines and devices: 性能がいい／高い, 高性能 \"high-performance\", エンジンの性能. It is not used for people; personal abilities are 能力, and results at school or work 成績. Distinction from 機能: 機能 is what a device can do (functions), 性能 is how well it does it.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい機能が追加されました。","romaji":"Atarashii kinō ga tsuika saremashita.","german":"A new feature has been added.","furigana":"あたら|きのう|ついか"},{"japanese":"このスマホにはカメラの便利な機能がたくさんあります。","romaji":"Kono sumaho ni wa kamera no benri na kinou ga takusan arimasu.","german":"This smartphone has many useful camera functions.","furigana":"べんり|きのう"},{"japanese":"新しいルールはまだうまく機能していません。","romaji":"Atarashii ruuru wa mada umaku kinou shite imasen.","german":"The new rule is not working properly yet.","furigana":"あたら|きのう"}],"notes":"機能 is the function of a device, system or organ: 便利な機能, 機能が多い, 機能を使う. As an intransitive する-verb, 機能する means \"to function, to fulfill its role\", especially for systems and rules: 制度がうまく機能していない. Whether a device simply runs is expressed in everyday speech more with 動く／動かない. Derived: 機能的 \"functional, practical\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この辺りは駅が近くて、利便性が高いです。","romaji":"Kono atari wa eki ga chikakute, ribensei ga takai desu.","german":"The area is very convenient, with the station close by.","furigana":"あた|えき|ちか|りべんせい|たか"},{"japanese":"利用者の利便性を考えて、アプリを新しくしました。","romaji":"Riyousha no ribensei o kangaete, apuri o atarashiku shimashita.","german":"We redesigned the app with user convenience in mind.","furigana":"りようしゃ|りべんせい|かんが|あたら"},{"japanese":"値段より利便性を重視して、コンビニで買い物をします。","romaji":"Nedan yori ribensei o juushi shite, konbini de kaimono o shimasu.","german":"I value convenience over price, so I shop at convenience stores.","furigana":"ねだん|りべんせい|じゅうし|か|もの"}],"notes":"利便性 refers to how convenient and practical something is to use, and is a typical word in advertising, housing listings and reports: 利便性が高い, 利便性を高める, 交通の利便性 \"good transport access\". It is the formal noun for 便利; in conversation people usually just say 便利だ. For software it often corresponds to \"usability\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"耐久性のある素材です。","romaji":"Taikyūsei no aru sozai desu.","german":"It is a durable material.","furigana":"たいきゅうせい|そざい"},{"japanese":"このかばんは軽くて耐久性も高いです。","romaji":"Kono kaban wa karukute taikyuusei mo takai desu.","german":"This bag is light and also very durable.","furigana":"かる|たいきゅうせい|たか"},{"japanese":"古い橋の耐久性を調べています。","romaji":"Furui hashi no taikyuusei o shirabete imasu.","german":"We are examining the durability of the old bridge.","furigana":"ふる|はし|たいきゅうせい|しら"}],"notes":"耐久性 is the ability of a material or product to withstand long use and stress: 耐久性がある／高い, 耐久性に優れる, 耐久性のある素材. It is used mainly in product descriptions, engineering and construction. In everyday speech people often simply say 丈夫だ \"sturdy\" or 長持ちする \"to last long\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"信頼性の高いシステムです。","romaji":"Shinraisei no takai shisutemu desu.","german":"It is a highly reliable system.","furigana":"しんらいせい|たか"},{"japanese":"インターネットの情報は、信頼性を確かめてから使いましょう。","romaji":"Intaanetto no jouhou wa, shinraisei o tashikamete kara tsukaimashou.","german":"Information from the internet should be checked for reliability before you use it.","furigana":"じょうほう|しんらいせい|たし|つか"},{"japanese":"この調査は人数が少ないので、結果の信頼性が低いです。","romaji":"Kono chousa wa ninzuu ga sukunai node, kekka no shinraisei ga hikui desu.","german":"This survey had few participants, so the reliability of its results is low.","furigana":"ちょうさ|にんずう|すく|けっか|しんらいせい|ひく"}],"notes":"信頼性 is the reliability or credibility of systems, products, data or sources: 信頼性が高い／低い, データの信頼性, 情報の信頼性を確かめる. It is the abstract noun of 信頼 \"trust\"; about people one says 信頼できる人 or 頼りになる人 instead. Related is 信用 \"trust in someone's word, creditworthiness\".","levelNote":"Compound: 信頼 (N3) + 性 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"生産性を向上させます。","romaji":"Seisansei o kōjō sasemasu.","german":"We will improve productivity.","furigana":"せいさんせい|こうじょう"},{"japanese":"会議が長すぎると、生産性が下がります。","romaji":"Kaigi ga nagasugiru to, seisansei ga sagarimasu.","german":"If meetings are too long, productivity drops.","furigana":"かいぎ|なが|せいさんせい|さ"},{"japanese":"在宅勤務にしてから、生産性が上がったと感じています。","romaji":"Zaitaku kinmu ni shite kara, seisansei ga agatta to kanjite imasu.","german":"Since I started working from home, I feel my productivity has gone up.","furigana":"ざいたくきんむ|せいさんせい|あ|かん"}],"notes":"生産性 is productivity, the ratio of output to the labor or time invested: 生産性が高い／低い, 生産性を上げる／向上させる. It comes from economics but is also used for personal work (今日は生産性が低い \"I'm unproductive today\"). Related is 効率 \"efficiency\", which stresses the economical use of resources; 生産 alone means \"production\".","levelNote":"Compound: 生産 (N4) + 性 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"持続可能な社会を目指します。","romaji":"Jizoku kanō na shakai o mezashimasu.","german":"We aim for a sustainable society.","furigana":"じぞくかのう|しゃかい|めざ"},{"japanese":"持続可能な漁業のために、魚をとりすぎないようにしています。","romaji":"Jizoku kanou na gyogyou no tame ni, sakana o torisuginai you ni shite imasu.","german":"For sustainable fishing, we take care not to catch too many fish.","furigana":"じぞくかのう|ぎょぎょう|さかな"},{"japanese":"今のやり方は持続可能ではありません。","romaji":"Ima no yarikata wa jizoku kanou de wa arimasen.","german":"The current way of doing things is not sustainable.","furigana":"いま|かた|じぞくかのう"}],"notes":"持続可能 means \"sustainable\" in the sense of \"able to be maintained over the long term\" and is a term of environmental, economic and social policy. As a な-adjective it usually stands before a noun: 持続可能な社会, 持続可能な開発目標 (the SDGs); predicatively it is 持続可能だ／持続可能ではない. The opposite is 持続不可能; the simple \"to last\" is 持続する or 続く.","levelNote":"Compound: 持続 (N1) + 可能 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"再生可能エネルギーを使います。","romaji":"Saisei kanō enerugī o tsukaimasu.","german":"We use renewable energy.","furigana":"さいせいかのう|つか"},{"japanese":"太陽や風は再生可能な資源です。","romaji":"Taiyou ya kaze wa saisei kanou na shigen desu.","german":"The sun and wind are renewable resources.","furigana":"たいよう|かぜ|さいせいかのう|しげん"},{"japanese":"この国では、電気の半分以上が再生可能エネルギーで作られています。","romaji":"Kono kuni de wa, denki no hanbun ijou ga saisei kanou enerugii de tsukurarete imasu.","german":"In this country, more than half of the electricity is produced from renewable energy.","furigana":"くに|でんき|はんぶんいじょう|さいせいかのう|つく"}],"notes":"再生可能 means \"renewable\" and appears almost only in fixed technical terms, above all 再生可能エネルギー (sun, wind, water), often shortened to 再エネ in the media. In this combination it stands directly before the noun without な; otherwise it is used as a な-adjective: 再生可能な資源. 再生 alone means \"playback, recycling, regeneration\" (動画を再生する \"to play a video\").","levelNote":"Compound: 再生 (N1) + 可能 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地球温暖化は深刻です。","romaji":"Chikyū ondanka wa shinkoku desu.","german":"Global warming is serious.","furigana":"ちきゅうおんだんか|しんこく"},{"japanese":"温暖化が進んで、夏がどんどん暑くなっています。","romaji":"Ondanka ga susunde, natsu ga dondon atsuku natte imasu.","german":"As warming progresses, summers keep getting hotter.","furigana":"おんだんか|すす|なつ|あつ"},{"japanese":"温暖化を防ぐために、車をあまり使わないようにしています。","romaji":"Ondanka o fusegu tame ni, kuruma o amari tsukawanai you ni shite imasu.","german":"To slow global warming, I try not to use the car much.","furigana":"おんだんか|ふせ|くるま|つか"}],"notes":"温暖化 is warming, almost always in the sense of climate warming: 地球温暖化 \"global warming\", 温暖化対策 \"measures against global warming\", 温暖化が進む \"warming progresses\", 温暖化を防ぐ \"to prevent warming\". The verb form 温暖化する is possible but rarer than these noun combinations. The broader term 気候変動 \"climate change\" also covers extreme weather.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"CO2の排出を減らします。","romaji":"CO2 no haishutsu o herashimasu.","german":"We will reduce CO2 emissions.","furigana":"はいしゅつ|へ"},{"japanese":"この車は排出ガスが少ないです。","romaji":"Kono kuruma wa haishutsu gasu ga sukunai desu.","german":"This car produces few exhaust emissions.","furigana":"くるま|はいしゅつ|すく"},{"japanese":"その工場は汚れた水を川に排出していました。","romaji":"Sono koujou wa yogoreta mizu o kawa ni haishutsu shite imashita.","german":"That factory was discharging dirty water into the river.","furigana":"こうじょう|よご|みず|かわ|はいしゅつ"}],"notes":"排出 means to release or expel something to the outside - especially exhaust gases, pollutants and wastewater: CO2の排出量 \"CO2 emissions\", 排出ガス \"exhaust gas\". As a transitive する-verb: 工場が有害な物質を排出する. It is a word from environmental science, engineering and medicine (excretion from the body). Taking out the garbage is ごみを出す in everyday speech, not 排出する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"水質汚染が問題です。","romaji":"Suishitsu osen ga mondai desu.","german":"Water pollution is a problem.","furigana":"すいしつおせん|もんだい"},{"japanese":"この地域の野菜が汚染されていないか調べました。","romaji":"Kono chiiki no yasai ga osen sarete inai ka shirabemashita.","german":"We checked whether the vegetables from this region are contaminated.","furigana":"ちいき|やさい|おせん|しら"},{"japanese":"大都市では大気汚染がひどい日もあります。","romaji":"Daitoshi de wa taiki osen ga hidoi hi mo arimasu.","german":"In big cities there are days when air pollution is severe.","furigana":"だいとし|たいきおせん|ひ"}],"notes":"汚染 is the pollution or contamination of the environment, food or substances by harmful substances: 大気汚染 \"air pollution\", 水質汚染 \"water pollution\", 土壌汚染 \"soil contamination\". As a する-verb it mostly appears in the passive or as a state: 川が汚染される／汚染されている. For everyday dirt on clothes or in a room, people say 汚れる or 汚い; 汚染 presupposes harmful substances and danger.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自然環境の保全に努めます。","romaji":"Shizen kankyō no hozen ni tsutomemasu.","german":"We strive to conserve the natural environment.","furigana":"しぜんかんきょう|ほぜん|つと"},{"japanese":"この森を保全するために、木を切ることが禁止されています。","romaji":"Kono mori o hozen suru tame ni, ki o kiru koto ga kinshi sarete imasu.","german":"Cutting down trees is prohibited in order to preserve this forest.","furigana":"もり|ほぜん|き|き|きんし"},{"japanese":"工場で機械の保全を担当しています。","romaji":"Koujou de kikai no hozen o tantou shite imasu.","german":"I am in charge of maintaining the machines at the factory.","furigana":"こうじょう|きかい|ほぜん|たんとう"}],"notes":"保全 means keeping something in good condition and safeguarding it - above all nature and the environment (環境保全, 自然保全) as well as facilities and machines (設備の保全 \"maintenance\"). It is a formal word from administration, law and engineering; in everyday speech people say 守る or 大切にする. Distinctions: 保護 \"protection\" from a danger (also of animals and people), 保存 \"storage, preservation\" (data, food).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"生態系を守りましょう。","romaji":"Seitaikei o mamorimashō.","german":"Let's protect the ecosystem.","furigana":"せいたいけい|まも"},{"japanese":"外国から来た魚が、この湖の生態系を壊しています。","romaji":"Gaikoku kara kita sakana ga, kono mizuumi no seitaikei o kowashite imasu.","german":"Fish from other countries are destroying the ecosystem of this lake.","furigana":"がいこく|き|さかな|みずうみ|せいたいけい|こわ"},{"japanese":"温暖化は海の生態系にも大きな影響を与えています。","romaji":"Ondanka wa umi no seitaikei ni mo ookina eikyou o ataete imasu.","german":"Global warming is also having a major impact on marine ecosystems.","furigana":"おんだんか|うみ|せいたいけい|おお|えいきょう|あた"}],"notes":"生態系 is the ecosystem, the interplay of all living things and their environment in an area: 海の生態系, 生態系を守る／壊す, 生態系に影響を与える. Originally a technical term, it is very common in news and environmental topics. Applied to business and IT, people usually say エコシステム. Related is 生態 \"way of life (of animals and plants)\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"絶滅危惧種を保護します。","romaji":"Zetsumetsu kigushu o hogo shimasu.","german":"We protect endangered species.","furigana":"ぜつめつきぐしゅ|ほご"},{"japanese":"恐竜は約六千六百万年前に絶滅しました。","romaji":"Kyouryuu wa yaku rokusen roppyakuman nen mae ni zetsumetsu shimashita.","german":"The dinosaurs went extinct about 66 million years ago.","furigana":"きょうりゅう|やくろくせんろっぴゃくまんねんまえ|ぜつめつ"},{"japanese":"公衆電話はもうすぐ絶滅するかもしれません。","romaji":"Koushuu denwa wa mou sugu zetsumetsu suru kamo shiremasen.","german":"Public telephones may soon die out.","furigana":"こうしゅうでんわ|ぜつめつ"}],"notes":"絶滅 is the complete extinction of a species or group; as an intransitive する-verb: 恐竜が絶滅した. Common combinations are 絶滅危惧種 \"endangered species\", 絶滅の危機にある and 絶滅寸前 \"on the verge of extinction\". Figuratively also used jokingly for things that are disappearing (絶滅しそうな職業). To actively wipe out is 絶滅させる; for diseases or crime, people more often say 撲滅.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"海の生物を研究しています。","romaji":"Umi no seibutsu o kenkyū shite imasu.","german":"I study marine life.","furigana":"うみ|せいぶつ|けんきゅう"},{"japanese":"火星に生物がいるかどうか、まだ分かっていません。","romaji":"Kasei ni seibutsu ga iru ka dou ka, mada wakatte imasen.","german":"It is still not known whether there is life on Mars.","furigana":"かせい|せいぶつ|わ"},{"japanese":"高校では生物の授業が一番好きでした。","romaji":"Koukou de wa seibutsu no jugyou ga ichiban suki deshita.","german":"In high school, biology class was my favorite.","furigana":"こうこう|せいぶつ|じゅぎょう|いちばんす"}],"notes":"生物 is the scientific, neutral umbrella term for all living things (animals, plants, microorganisms): 海の生物, 生物の多様性. In everyday speech people tend to say 生き物, which sounds warmer and more child-friendly. As a school subject, 生物 means \"biology\" (生物の授業). Note: read as なまもの, the same kanji mean \"raw, perishable food\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"細胞が分裂します。","romaji":"Saibō ga bunretsu shimasu.","german":"The cells divide.","furigana":"さいぼう|ぶんれつ"},{"japanese":"人間の体は何十兆もの細胞からできています。","romaji":"Ningen no karada wa nanjuuchou mo no saibou kara dekite imasu.","german":"The human body is made up of tens of trillions of cells.","furigana":"にんげん|からだ|なんじゅうちょう|さいぼう"},{"japanese":"寝不足は脳の細胞によくないそうです。","romaji":"Nebusoku wa nou no saibou ni yokunai sou desu.","german":"Lack of sleep is said to be bad for brain cells.","furigana":"ねぶそく|のう|さいぼう"}],"notes":"細胞 is the biological cell: 細胞が分裂する \"cells divide\", がん細胞 \"cancer cell\", 脳細胞 \"brain cell\". Other meanings of \"cell\" are not translated with 細胞: a prison cell is 独房, a spreadsheet cell セル, a battery cell 電池. Derogatory and colloquial, 単細胞 means \"simple-minded, simple\" (単細胞な人).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"遺伝子の研究が進んでいます。","romaji":"Idenshi no kenkyū ga susunde imasu.","german":"Genetic research is advancing.","furigana":"いでんし|けんきゅう|すす"},{"japanese":"この豆腐には遺伝子組み換えの大豆は使われていません。","romaji":"Kono toufu ni wa idenshi kumikae no daizu wa tsukawarete imasen.","german":"No genetically modified soybeans are used in this tofu.","furigana":"とうふ|いでんしく|か|だいず|つか"},{"japanese":"病気になりやすいかどうかには、遺伝子も関係しています。","romaji":"Byouki ni nariyasui ka dou ka ni wa, idenshi mo kankei shite imasu.","german":"Genes are also involved in whether someone gets sick easily.","furigana":"びょうき|いでんし|かんけい"}],"notes":"遺伝子 is the gene as the carrier of hereditary information: 遺伝子の研究, 遺伝子検査 \"genetic test\", 遺伝子組み換え食品 \"genetically modified food\" (on packages often 遺伝子組換え). To be distinguished from this is 遺伝 \"heredity\" (遺伝する \"to be inherited\"); \"It runs in the family\" is more 遺伝だ than 遺伝子だ. DNA is also called DNA in Japanese.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"免疫力を高めましょう。","romaji":"Men'ekiryoku o takamemashō.","german":"Let's boost our immune system.","furigana":"めんえきりょく|たか"},{"japanese":"一度はしかにかかると、免疫ができます。","romaji":"Ichido hashika ni kakaru to, men'eki ga dekimasu.","german":"Once you've had measles, you become immune.","furigana":"いちど|めんえき"},{"japanese":"兄は厳しい上司に慣れているので、怒られることに免疫があります。","romaji":"Ani wa kibishii joushi ni narete iru node, okorareru koto ni men'eki ga arimasu.","german":"My brother is used to strict bosses, so he is immune to being scolded.","furigana":"あに|きび|じょうし|な|おこ|めんえき"}],"notes":"免疫 is immunity, the body's defense against pathogens: 免疫がある／できる, 免疫力 \"immune strength\" (免疫力を高める, 免疫力が下がる). Figuratively, 〜に免疫がある means to be hardened against something, and 〜に免疫がない means to have no experience with something and therefore react sensitively. The vaccination itself is called 予防接種 or ワクチン.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"医師の診断を受けます。","romaji":"Ishi no shindan o ukemasu.","german":"I will get a doctor's diagnosis.","furigana":"いし|しんだん|う"},{"japanese":"検査の結果、インフルエンザと診断されました。","romaji":"Kensa no kekka, infuruenza to shindan saremashita.","german":"After the test, I was diagnosed with influenza.","furigana":"けんさ|けっか|しんだん"},{"japanese":"会社で毎年健康診断があります。","romaji":"Kaisha de maitoshi kenkou shindan ga arimasu.","german":"There is a health checkup at the company every year.","furigana":"かいしゃ|まいとしけんこうしんだん"}],"notes":"診断 is a medical diagnosis or assessment: 医者が診断する, 〜と診断される \"to be diagnosed with …\", 健康診断 \"health checkup\" (mandatory every year at companies), 診断書 \"medical certificate\". The examination in the consulting room itself is 診察; 診断 is the judgment that follows from it. Figuratively also used for tests and analyses: 性格診断 \"personality test\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"治療を受けています。","romaji":"Chiryō o ukete imasu.","german":"I am receiving treatment.","furigana":"ちりょう|う"},{"japanese":"今、歯医者で虫歯の治療をしています。","romaji":"Ima, haisha de mushiba no chiryou o shite imasu.","german":"I'm currently getting a cavity treated at the dentist.","furigana":"いま|はいしゃ|むしば|ちりょう"},{"japanese":"この病気は早く治療すれば治ります。","romaji":"Kono byouki wa hayaku chiryou sureba naorimasu.","german":"This illness can be cured if treated early.","furigana":"びょうき|はや|ちりょう|なお"}],"notes":"治療 is the medical treatment of an illness or injury: 治療を受ける \"to be treated\", 病気を治療する, 歯の治療 \"dental treatment\", 治療中 \"under treatment\". It refers to the treatment process; getting well itself is 治る, recovery 回復. Minor first-aid measures are more often called 手当て.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"病気の予防が大切です。","romaji":"Byōki no yobō ga taisetsu desu.","german":"Disease prevention is important.","furigana":"びょうき|よぼう|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"風邪の予防に、よく手を洗っています。","romaji":"Kaze no yobou ni, yoku te o aratte imasu.","german":"I wash my hands often to prevent colds.","furigana":"かぜ|よぼう|て|あら"},{"japanese":"子どもは来週、予防接種を受けます。","romaji":"Kodomo wa raishuu, yobou sesshu o ukemasu.","german":"My child will get vaccinated next week.","furigana":"こ|らいしゅう|よぼうせっしゅ|う"}],"notes":"予防 is prevention of illness, accidents or damage: 病気を予防する, 予防接種 \"vaccination\", 風邪の予防. Very common are 〜の予防に \"to prevent ...\" (予防にマスクをする) and 〜の予防になる \"helps prevent ...\". Distinction: 防止 \"prevention, stopping\" is used more for accidents and acts (事故防止, 犯罪防止), 予防 mainly for health.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"カロリーの摂取を減らします。","romaji":"Karorī no sesshu o herashimasu.","german":"I will reduce my calorie intake.","furigana":"せっしゅ|へ"},{"japanese":"暑い日は水分をこまめに摂取してください。","romaji":"Atsui hi wa suibun o komame ni sesshu shite kudasai.","german":"On hot days, please drink fluids frequently.","furigana":"あつ|ひ|すいぶん|せっしゅ"},{"japanese":"日本人は塩分の摂取量が多いと言われています。","romaji":"Nihonjin wa enbun no sesshuryou ga ooi to iwarete imasu.","german":"Japanese people are said to have a high salt intake.","furigana":"にほんじん|えんぶん|せっしゅりょう|おお|い"}],"notes":"摂取 is the intake of nutrients, fluids or other substances into the body: 栄養を摂取する, 水分を摂取する, 塩分の摂取量 \"salt intake\". It is a factual word from medicine, nutritional counseling and package labels; in everyday speech people say 食べる, 飲む or 栄養をとる. Formally and figuratively it is also used for absorbing culture and knowledge (外国の文化を摂取する).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"体力をつけましょう。","romaji":"Tairyoku o tsukemashō.","german":"Let's build up our stamina.","furigana":"たいりょく"},{"japanese":"年を取って、体力が落ちてきました。","romaji":"Toshi o totte, tairyoku ga ochite kimashita.","german":"As I get older, my physical strength has been declining.","furigana":"とし|と|たいりょく|お"},{"japanese":"この仕事は体力的にきついです。","romaji":"Kono shigoto wa tairyokuteki ni kitsui desu.","german":"This work is physically demanding.","furigana":"しごと|たいりょくてき"}],"notes":"体力 is physical strength and stamina, including resistance to illness: 体力がある／ない, 体力をつける \"to build up fitness\", 体力が落ちる \"to lose physical strength\", 体力的にきつい \"physically demanding\". Pure muscle strength is 力 or 筋力, mental strength 気力. Figuratively also for the financial strength of a company (企業の体力).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"精神的に強くなりたいです。","romaji":"Seishinteki ni tsuyoku naritai desu.","german":"I want to become mentally stronger.","furigana":"せいしんてき|つよ"},{"japanese":"彼はチャレンジ精神がある人です。","romaji":"Kare wa charenji seishin ga aru hito desu.","german":"He is someone with a spirit of challenge.","furigana":"かれ|せいしん|ひと"},{"japanese":"この町には助け合いの精神が残っています。","romaji":"Kono machi ni wa tasukeai no seishin ga nokotte imasu.","german":"The spirit of helping one another remains in this town.","furigana":"まち|たす|あ|せいしん|のこ"}],"notes":"精神 denotes the mind as opposed to the body (肉体) as well as inner attitude and mindset: 精神力 \"willpower\", チャレンジ精神 \"spirit of enterprise\", 助け合いの精神 \"spirit of mutual help\". The form 精神的(な) \"mental, psychological\" is very common (精神的に疲れる). For the everyday \"heart, mind\", people more often say 心; a \"ghost\" in the sense of a specter is 幽霊.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ストレスを解消しましょう。","romaji":"Sutoresu o kaishō shimashō.","german":"Bauen wir Stress ab.","furigana":"かいしょう"},{"japanese":"最近、仕事でストレスがたまっています。","romaji":"Saikin, shigoto de sutoresu ga tamatte imasu.","german":"In letzter Zeit staut sich bei mir viel Stress von der Arbeit an.","furigana":"さいきん|しごと"},{"japanese":"毎朝の満員電車が大きなストレスになっています。","romaji":"Maiasa no man'in densha ga ookina sutoresu ni natte imasu.","german":"Die überfüllten Züge jeden Morgen sind eine große Belastung für mich.","furigana":"まいあさ|まんいんでんしゃ|おお"}],"notes":"ストレス ist psychische (oder körperliche) Belastung: ストレスがたまる „Stress staut sich an“, ストレスを解消する／発散する „Stress abbauen“, ストレスを感じる, 〜がストレスになる „… wird zur Belastung“. Anders als das deutsche „Ich hab Stress“ meint ストレス nicht einfach viel zu tun – dafür sagt man 忙しい. ストレス ist immer die innere Belastung.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 32"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"リラックスすることが大切です。","romaji":"Rirakkusu suru koto ga taisetsu desu.","german":"Relaxing is important.","furigana":"たいせつ"},{"japanese":"面接の前に深呼吸して、リラックスしてください。","romaji":"Mensetsu no mae ni shinkokyuu shite, rirakkusu shite kudasai.","german":"Take a deep breath before the interview and relax.","furigana":"めんせつ|まえ|しんこきゅう"},{"japanese":"週末は家で音楽を聞いてリラックスします。","romaji":"Shuumatsu wa ie de ongaku o kiite rirakkusu shimasu.","german":"On weekends I relax at home listening to music.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|いえ|おんがく|き"}],"notes":"リラックス means relaxation and is almost always used as an intransitive する-verb: リラックスする \"to relax\", リラックスして \"nice and loose\", リラックスできる場所 \"a place to relax\". Often used as encouragement before exams or performances: もっとリラックスして. Related are くつろぐ \"to make oneself comfortable\" (more at home) and 落ち着く \"to calm down\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"メンタルが強い人です。","romaji":"Mentaru ga tsuyoi hito desu.","german":"He is mentally strong.","furigana":"つよ|ひと"},{"japanese":"スポーツ選手にとって、メンタルの強さは大切です。","romaji":"Supootsu senshu ni totte, mentaru no tsuyosa wa taisetsu desu.","german":"Mental strength is important for athletes.","furigana":"せんしゅ|つよ|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"会社はメンタルヘルスの相談窓口を作りました。","romaji":"Kaisha wa mentaru herusu no soudan madoguchi o tsukurimashita.","german":"The company set up a consultation desk for mental health.","furigana":"かいしゃ|そうだんまどぐち|つく"}],"notes":"メンタル is used in Japanese mainly as a noun for one's mental state and resilience: メンタルが強い／弱い \"mentally strong/weak\", メンタルの強さ \"mental strength\". As a first element it appears in メンタルヘルス \"mental health\" and メンタルケア. It sounds modern and rather colloquial; more formal are 精神的(な) or 心. The use as a な-adjective (メンタルな問題) is rarer.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"慢性的な痛みがあります。","romaji":"Manseiteki na itami ga arimasu.","german":"I have chronic pain.","furigana":"まんせいてき|いた"},{"japanese":"この病院は慢性的に看護師が足りません。","romaji":"Kono byouin wa manseiteki ni kangoshi ga tarimasen.","german":"This hospital is chronically short of nurses.","furigana":"びょういん|まんせいてき|かんごし|た"},{"japanese":"駅前の道では、毎朝慢性的な渋滞が起きています。","romaji":"Ekimae no michi de wa, maiasa manseiteki na juutai ga okite imasu.","german":"There is chronic traffic congestion every morning on the road in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|みち|まいあさまんせいてき|じゅうたい|お"}],"notes":"慢性的 means \"chronic\" - medically for long-lasting complaints (慢性的な頭痛, 慢性的な疲れ) and figuratively for persistent problems: 慢性的な人手不足 \"chronic staff shortage\", 慢性的な渋滞. It is used as a な-adjective before nouns or adverbially as 慢性的に. The base word 慢性 appears without な before disease names (慢性胃炎); the opposite is 急性 \"acute\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"革新的な技術です。","romaji":"Kakushinteki na gijutsu desu.","german":"It is an innovative technology.","furigana":"かくしんてき|ぎじゅつ"},{"japanese":"彼女の革新的なアイデアで、売り上げが二倍になりました。","romaji":"Kanojo no kakushinteki na aidea de, uriage ga nibai ni narimashita.","german":"Thanks to her innovative idea, sales doubled.","furigana":"かのじょ|かくしんてき|う|あ|にばい"},{"japanese":"その政党は保守的ではなく、革新的な考え方を持っています。","romaji":"Sono seitou wa hoshuteki de wa naku, kakushinteki na kangaekata o motte imasu.","german":"That party is not conservative but holds progressive views.","furigana":"せいとう|ほしゅてき|かくしんてき|かんが|かた|も"}],"notes":"革新的 means \"innovative, groundbreaking\" for technology, ideas and products (革新的な技術／アイデア) and politically \"reform-minded, progressive\" - the opposite is 保守的 \"conservative\". It is rather a word of written and media language; in conversation one often says 新しい, 画期的 \"groundbreaking\" or イノベーティブ. The noun 革新 means \"innovation, reform\" (技術革新).","levelNote":"Compound: 革新 (N1) + 的 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先端的な研究をしています。","romaji":"Sentanteki na kenkyū o shite imasu.","german":"We are doing cutting-edge research.","furigana":"せんたんてき|けんきゅう"},{"japanese":"この大学は先端的な医療技術で有名です。","romaji":"Kono daigaku wa sentanteki na iryou gijutsu de yuumei desu.","german":"This university is famous for its cutting-edge medical technology.","furigana":"だいがく|せんたんてき|いりょうぎじゅつ|ゆうめい"},{"japanese":"駅前に先端的なデザインのビルが建ちました。","romaji":"Ekimae ni sentanteki na dezain no biru ga tachimashita.","german":"A building with a futuristic design has been built in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|せんたんてき|た"}],"notes":"先端的 means \"state-of-the-art, at the forefront of development\", above all in research and technology. More common than 先端的, however, are 最先端 and 先端 in fixed combinations: 最先端の技術, 先端技術 \"cutting-edge technology\"; 先端的 sounds somewhat stiff and literary. 先端 itself originally means \"tip, extreme end\" (棒の先端). Related is 先進的 \"progressive, advanced\".","levelNote":"Compound: 先端 (N2) + 的 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"包括的な計画を立てます。","romaji":"Hōkatsuteki na keikaku o tatemasu.","german":"We will draw up a comprehensive plan.","furigana":"ほうかつてき|けいかく|た"},{"japanese":"二つの国は経済について包括的な合意をしました。","romaji":"Futatsu no kuni wa keizai ni tsuite houkatsuteki na goui o shimashita.","german":"The two countries reached a comprehensive agreement on the economy.","furigana":"ふた|くに|けいざい|ほうかつてき|ごうい"},{"japanese":"問題を一つずつではなく、包括的に考える必要があります。","romaji":"Mondai o hitotsu zutsu de wa naku, houkatsuteki ni kangaeru hitsuyou ga arimasu.","german":"We need to think about the problems comprehensively, not one at a time.","furigana":"もんだい|ひと|ほうかつてき|かんが|ひつよう"}],"notes":"包括的 means \"comprehensive, all-encompassing\" and belongs to administrative, legal and technical language: 包括的な計画, 包括的な合意 \"comprehensive agreement\", 包括的に検討する \"to examine comprehensively\". It stresses that many individual areas are covered together. In everyday speech people more often say 全体的(な) or 全部まとめて. It has nothing to do with \"inclusion\" in the social sense.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"段階的に実施します。","romaji":"Dankaiteki ni jisshi shimasu.","german":"We will implement it step by step.","furigana":"だんかいてき|じっし"},{"japanese":"電車の運賃は来年から段階的に上がります。","romaji":"Densha no unchin wa rainen kara dankaiteki ni agarimasu.","german":"Train fares will rise in stages from next year.","furigana":"でんしゃ|うんちん|らいねん|だんかいてき|あ"},{"japanese":"初心者向けに、段階的な練習メニューを作りました。","romaji":"Shoshinsha muke ni, dankaiteki na renshuu menyuu o tsukurimashita.","german":"For beginners, I made a training menu that builds up step by step.","furigana":"しょしんしゃむ|だんかいてき|れんしゅう|つく"}],"notes":"段階的 means \"step by step, in stages\" and is mostly used adverbially as 段階的に (段階的に進める／導入する／引き上げる) or attributively as 段階的な. It is typical for plans, reforms, price increases and relaxations in news and company announcements; in everyday speech one says 少しずつ or だんだん instead. 徐々に means \"gradually\" in a smooth transition, while 段階的に emphasizes clearly separate stages. The base word is 段階 \"step, phase\".","levelNote":"Compound: 段階 (N2) + 的 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"継続的な努力が必要です。","romaji":"Keizokuteki na doryoku ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"Continuous effort is necessary.","furigana":"けいぞくてき|どりょく|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"日本語は継続的に勉強しないと、すぐに忘れてしまいます。","romaji":"Nihongo wa keizokuteki ni benkyou shinai to, sugu ni wasurete shimaimasu.","german":"If you don't study Japanese continuously, you forget it quickly.","furigana":"にほんご|けいぞくてき|べんきょう|わす"},{"japanese":"この会社は地元の小学校を継続的に支援しています。","romaji":"Kono kaisha wa jimoto no shougakkou o keizokuteki ni shien shite imasu.","german":"This company has supported the local elementary school for a long time.","furigana":"かいしゃ|じもと|しょうがっこう|けいぞくてき|しえん"}],"notes":"継続的 means \"continuous, ongoing\", that is, carried on over a long period without interruption: 継続的な努力／支援, 継続的に運動する. The base word is 継続 \"continuation\" (継続する, proverb 継続は力なり \"persistence is power\"). In everyday speech one often says 続けて or ずっと. Distinctions: 連続 \"directly in a row\" (三日連続), 定期的 \"regularly, at fixed intervals\".","levelNote":"Compound: 継続 (N2) + 的 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"潜在的なリスクがあります。","romaji":"Senzaiteki na risuku ga arimasu.","german":"There are latent risks.","furigana":"せんざいてき"},{"japanese":"この地域には潜在的な需要がまだ多いと思います。","romaji":"Kono chiiki ni wa senzaiteki na juyou ga mada ooi to omoimasu.","german":"I think there is still a lot of untapped demand in this region.","furigana":"ちいき|せんざいてき|じゅよう|おお|おも"},{"japanese":"彼女には潜在的な才能があるが、本人は気づいていない。","romaji":"Kanojo ni wa senzaiteki na sainou ga aru ga, honnin wa kizuite inai.","german":"She has latent talent, but she herself hasn't noticed.","furigana":"かのじょ|せんざいてき|さいのう|ほんにん|き"}],"notes":"潜在的 means \"latent, potential\": something is present but (not yet) visible or used. As a な-adjective: 潜在的なリスク／需要／能力, adverbially 潜在的に. Typical in business, medicine and psychology, e.g. 潜在的な顧客 \"potential customers\"; related is 潜在意識 \"the subconscious\". The counterpart is 顕在化する \"to come to the surface\". It is rather written and formal; in conversation people often simply say 〜可能性がある.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"致命的な欠陥があります。","romaji":"Chimeiteki na kekkan ga arimasu.","german":"There is a fatal flaw.","furigana":"ちめいてき|けっかん"},{"japanese":"試合の最後に致命的なミスをしてしまった。","romaji":"Shiai no saigo ni chimeiteki na misu o shite shimatta.","german":"I made a critical mistake at the end of the game.","furigana":"しあい|さいご|ちめいてき"},{"japanese":"この病気は早く治療しないと致命的になることがある。","romaji":"Kono byouki wa hayaku chiryou shinai to chimeiteki ni naru koto ga aru.","german":"If this illness isn't treated early, it can become fatal.","furigana":"びょうき|はや|ちりょう|ちめいてき"}],"notes":"致命的 literally means \"fatal\" (cf. 致命傷 \"fatal wound\"), but is mostly used figuratively: a mistake or flaw that ruins everything - 致命的なミス, 致命的な欠陥, 致命的なダメージ. As a predicate it often goes with にとって: 〜にとって致命的だ \"fatal for ...\". Common in business, IT and sports. When real danger to life is meant in everyday speech, people more often say 命に関わる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"決定的な証拠が見つかりました。","romaji":"Ketteiteki na shōko ga mitsukarimashita.","german":"Conclusive evidence was found.","furigana":"けっていてき|しょうこ|み"},{"japanese":"この写真は決定的な瞬間をとらえている。","romaji":"Kono shashin wa ketteiteki na shunkan o toraete iru.","german":"This photo captures the decisive moment.","furigana":"しゃしん|けっていてき|しゅんかん"},{"japanese":"二人の考え方には決定的な違いがある。","romaji":"Futari no kangaekata ni wa ketteiteki na chigai ga aru.","german":"There is a decisive difference between their ways of thinking.","furigana":"ふたり|かんが|かた|けっていてき|ちが"}],"notes":"決定的 means \"decisive, conclusive, final\": something that clearly settles a matter and leaves no more doubt. Fixed combinations: 決定的な証拠 \"conclusive evidence\", 決定的な瞬間 \"the decisive moment\", 決定的な違い \"the fundamental difference\". As a predicate: 敗北が決定的になった \"defeat became all but certain\". Distinction: 重要 only means \"important\", whereas 決定的 means \"tipping the balance\".","levelNote":"Compound: 決定 (N3) + 的 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"象徴的な建物です。","romaji":"Shōchōteki na tatemono desu.","german":"It is a symbolic building.","furigana":"しょうちょうてき|たてもの"},{"japanese":"この事件は今の社会を象徴的に表している。","romaji":"Kono jiken wa ima no shakai o shouchouteki ni arawashite iru.","german":"This incident is emblematic of today's society.","furigana":"じけん|いま|しゃかい|しょうちょうてき|あらわ"},{"japanese":"彼はチームにとって象徴的な選手です。","romaji":"Kare wa chiimu ni totte shouchouteki na senshu desu.","german":"He is the symbolic player of the team.","furigana":"かれ|しょうちょうてき|せんしゅ"}],"notes":"象徴的 means \"symbolic, emblematic\": something stands for a larger idea. It is derived from 象徴 \"symbol\" (鳩は平和の象徴だ). Typical: 象徴的な建物／出来事／存在; adverbial 象徴的に表す \"to express symbolically\". Often it carries a nuance of \"telling, characteristic\": an event shows a larger development in miniature. Neutral to literary, frequent in news and commentary.","levelNote":"Compound: 象徴 (N1) + 的 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"典型的な日本の家です。","romaji":"Tenkeiteki na nihon no ie desu.","german":"It is a typical Japanese house.","furigana":"てんけいてき|にほん|いえ"},{"japanese":"それは初心者によくある典型的なミスです。","romaji":"Sore wa shoshinsha ni yoku aru tenkeiteki na misu desu.","german":"That is a typical mistake beginners make.","furigana":"しょしんしゃ|てんけいてき"},{"japanese":"彼は典型的な朝型の人間で、毎日五時に起きる。","romaji":"Kare wa tenkeiteki na asagata no ningen de, mainichi goji ni okiru.","german":"He is a typical morning person and gets up at five every day.","furigana":"かれ|てんけいてき|あさがた|にんげん|まいにちごじ|お"}],"notes":"典型的 means \"typical, classic\": something shows all the characteristics of a category and is a prime example. Common: 典型的な例, 典型的なパターン, 典型的な日本人. Often slightly critical or ironic: 典型的なミス \"a typical mistake\". Similar is 代表的 \"representative, leading\", which sounds more positive (代表的な料理); colloquially, \"typical\" is often expressed with 〜らしい or ありがち.","levelNote":"Compound: 典型 (N3) + 的 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"印象的なスピーチでした。","romaji":"Inshōteki na supīchi deshita.","german":"It was an impressive speech.","furigana":"いんしょうてき"},{"japanese":"彼女の明るい笑顔がとても印象的でした。","romaji":"Kanojo no akarui egao ga totemo inshouteki deshita.","german":"Her bright smile was very memorable.","furigana":"かのじょ|あか|えがお|いんしょうてき"},{"japanese":"映画の最後の場面が一番印象的だった。","romaji":"Eiga no saigo no bamen ga ichiban inshouteki datta.","german":"The last scene of the film was the most memorable.","furigana":"えいが|さいご|ばめん|いちばんいんしょうてき"}],"notes":"印象的 means \"impressive, memorable\": something leaves a strong, lasting impression (印象). Attributively: 印象的な場面／言葉／笑顔. As a predicate, 〜が印象的だった is a very typical phrase in storytelling: \"What stuck with me especially was ...\". Distinction: 印象がいい means \"to make a good impression\" (to seem likable), whereas 印象的 only means \"memorable\" and need not be positive.","levelNote":"Compound: 印象 (N3) + 的 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"魅力的な提案ですね。","romaji":"Miryokuteki na teian desu ne.","german":"That's an attractive proposal.","furigana":"みりょくてき|ていあん"},{"japanese":"彼は話し方がとても魅力的な人です。","romaji":"Kare wa hanashikata ga totemo miryokuteki na hito desu.","german":"He has a very charming way of speaking.","furigana":"かれ|はな|かた|みりょくてき|ひと"},{"japanese":"この町は古い建物が多くて魅力的です。","romaji":"Kono machi wa furui tatemono ga ookute miryokuteki desu.","german":"This town has many old buildings and is very appealing.","furigana":"まち|ふる|たてもの|おお|みりょくてき"}],"notes":"魅力的 means \"attractive, appealing, fascinating\" and comes from 魅力 \"charm, appeal\". It suits people (魅力的な人) as well as places, offers and conditions (魅力的な提案／条件／町). For people it refers more to their aura than to mere looks; for \"pretty\" people say きれい or かっこいい. Alternative: 〜に魅力がある. In business it also serves as a polite lead-in to a refusal: 魅力的なお話ですが...","levelNote":"Compound: 魅力 (N3) + 的 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"意欲的に取り組んでいます。","romaji":"Iyokuteki ni torikunde imasu.","german":"I am working on it with great motivation.","furigana":"いよくてき|と|く"},{"japanese":"新しい社員はとても意欲的で、何でも質問してくる。","romaji":"Atarashii shain wa totemo iyokuteki de, nan demo shitsumon shite kuru.","german":"The new employee is very eager and asks questions about everything.","furigana":"あたら|しゃいん|いよくてき|なん|しつもん"},{"japanese":"これは若い監督の意欲的な作品だ。","romaji":"Kore wa wakai kantoku no iyokuteki na sakuhin da.","german":"This is an ambitious work by a young director.","furigana":"わか|かんとく|いよくてき|さくひん"}],"notes":"意欲的 means \"motivated, engaged, ambitious\" and comes from 意欲 \"will, drive\". Very common adverbially: 意欲的に取り組む／学ぶ \"to approach something with enthusiasm\". Attributively: 意欲的な学生, 意欲的な作品 \"an ambitious work\". Typical in reports, evaluations and applications; in casual conversation one rather says やる気がある. 積極的 is similar, but stresses active behavior, whereas 意欲的 stresses the inner drive.","levelNote":"Compound: 意欲 (N1) + 的 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"届け出を提出します。","romaji":"Todokede o teishutsu shimasu.","german":"I will submit the notification.","furigana":"とど|で|ていしゅつ"},{"japanese":"引っ越したら、十四日以内に市役所に届け出をしてください。","romaji":"Hikkoshitara, juuyokka inai ni shiyakusho ni todokede o shite kudasai.","german":"When you move, please report it to the city office within 14 days.","furigana":"ひ|こ|じゅうよっかいない|しやくしょ|とど|で"},{"japanese":"会社を休むときは、事前に届け出が必要です。","romaji":"Kaisha o yasumu toki wa, jizen ni todokede ga hitsuyou desu.","german":"If you take time off from the company, prior notification is required.","furigana":"かいしゃ|やす|じぜん|とど|で|ひつよう"}],"notes":"届け出 is a formal notification to an authority, school or company, e.g. 出生届 \"birth registration\" or 転居届 \"change-of-address notification\". Typical: 届け出をする／出す／提出する, with the recipient marked by に: 市役所に届け出る. On forms the spelling 届出 without で is also common. Distinction: with 申請 you ask for approval, with 届け出 you merely report a fact. Handing in or reporting something to the police is 警察に届ける.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「助けて！」と叫びました。","romaji":"Tasukete! to sakebimashita.","german":"I shouted, \"Help!\"","furigana":"たす|さけ"},{"japanese":"子供たちが海に向かって大きな声で叫んでいる。","romaji":"Kodomotachi ga umi ni mukatte ookina koe de sakende iru.","german":"The children are shouting at the top of their voices toward the sea.","furigana":"こども|うみ|む|おお|こえ|さけ"},{"japanese":"最近、環境問題の大切さが強く叫ばれている。","romaji":"Saikin, kankyou mondai no taisetsusa ga tsuyoku sakebarete iru.","german":"Recently, the importance of environmental problems has been loudly stressed.","furigana":"さいきん|かんきょうもんだい|たいせつ|つよ|さけ"}],"notes":"叫ぶ means \"to shout, to cry out loudly\" - at high volume and often with strong emotion. What is shouted usually appears as a quotation with と: 「助けて！」と叫ぶ; the direction with に向かって. Distinctions: 大声を出す \"to raise one's voice\" is neutral, 怒鳴る \"to yell at\" is angry, 呼ぶ is \"to call someone\". Figuratively, 〜を叫ぶ means \"to demand loudly\", often in the passive: 改革の必要性が叫ばれている. Noun: 叫び（声） \"cry\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"家族で食卓を囲みます。","romaji":"Kazoku de shokutaku o kakomimasu.","german":"My family gathers around the dining table.","furigana":"かぞく|しょくたく|かこ"},{"japanese":"私の生まれた町は山に囲まれています。","romaji":"Watashi no umareta machi wa yama ni kakomarete imasu.","german":"The town where I was born is surrounded by mountains.","furigana":"わたし|う|まち|やま|かこ"},{"japanese":"正しい答えを丸で囲んでください。","romaji":"Tadashii kotae o maru de kakonde kudasai.","german":"Please circle the correct answer.","furigana":"ただ|こた|まる|かこ"}],"notes":"囲む means \"to surround, to encircle\" and is transitive: 〜を囲む. Typical: 食卓を囲む \"to sit around the table together\", 正しい答えを丸で囲む \"to circle the correct answer\" (the means takes で). The passive is very common: 山に囲まれた町 \"a town surrounded by mountains\", ファンに囲まれる \"to be surrounded by fans\". There is no separate intransitive counterpart; 囲まれる is used. Noun: 囲み \"border, box\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"穴を埋めます。","romaji":"Ana o umemasu.","german":"I will fill the hole.","furigana":"あな|う"},{"japanese":"犬が庭に骨を埋めました。","romaji":"Inu ga niwa ni hone o umemashita.","german":"The dog buried a bone in the garden.","furigana":"いぬ|にわ|ほね|う"},{"japanese":"空いているところを埋めて、文を完成させてください。","romaji":"Aite iru tokoro o umete, bun o kansei sasete kudasai.","german":"Fill in the blanks and complete the sentences.","furigana":"あ|う|ぶん|かんせい"}],"notes":"埋める means \"to bury; to fill up, to fill in\" and is transitive; the intransitive counterpart is 埋まる \"to be buried; to fill up\". Literally: 穴を埋める, 骨を土に埋める. Figuratively very common: 空欄を埋める \"to fill in the blanks\", 差を埋める \"to close a gap\", 会場を埋める \"to fill the hall\", 赤字を埋める \"to cover a deficit\". The reading うずめる exists but is elevated or literary.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"前の車を追い越しました。","romaji":"Mae no kuruma o oikoshimashita.","german":"I overtook the car in front.","furigana":"まえ|くるま|お|こ"},{"japanese":"息子はもう背で私を追い越しました。","romaji":"Musuko wa mou se de watashi o oikoshimashita.","german":"My son has already overtaken me in height.","furigana":"むすこ|せ|わたし|お|こ"},{"japanese":"マラソンで最後に三人を追い越した。","romaji":"Marason de saigo ni sannin o oikoshita.","german":"In the marathon, I passed three more runners at the end.","furigana":"さいご|さんにん|お|こ"}],"notes":"追い越す means \"to overtake, to pass\" and is transitive: 前の車を追い越す. It combines 追う \"to chase\" and 越す \"to exceed\". Figuratively it means to surpass someone, e.g. 背で父を追い越す \"to grow taller than one's father\". Distinction: 追いつく only means \"to catch up, to draw level\"; 追い越す goes beyond that. 追い抜く is almost synonymous and especially popular in sports. Traffic sign: 追い越し禁止 \"no overtaking\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"赤ちゃんを抱きました。","romaji":"Akachan o dakimashita.","german":"I held the baby in my arms.","furigana":"あか|だ"},{"japanese":"彼女は大きな花束を抱いて帰ってきた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa ookina hanataba o daite kaette kita.","german":"She came home holding a big bouquet of flowers.","furigana":"かのじょ|おお|はなたば|だ|かえ"},{"japanese":"猫を抱いて写真を撮りました。","romaji":"Neko o daite shashin o torimashita.","german":"I took a photo holding the cat.","furigana":"ねこ|だ|しゃしん|と"}],"notes":"抱く（だく） means \"to take in one's arms, to hold in one's arms\" and is transitive: 赤ちゃんを抱く. Common are 抱きしめる \"to hug tightly\" and the childish 抱っこする. For feelings and hopes, the same character is usually read いだく (夢を抱く, 不安を抱く) - a different, literary reading. Caution: between adults, だく can also mean \"to sleep with someone\"; a friendly hug is therefore more often ハグする or 抱き合う. For birds: 卵を抱く \"to brood\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地図を指して説明します。","romaji":"Chizu o sashite setsumei shimasu.","german":"I point at the map as I explain.","furigana":"ちず|さ|せつめい"},{"japanese":"時計の針がちょうど十二時を指しています。","romaji":"Tokei no hari ga choudo juuniji o sashite imasu.","german":"The clock hands are pointing exactly to twelve.","furigana":"とけい|はり|じゅうにじ|さ"},{"japanese":"この文の「それ」は何を指していますか。","romaji":"Kono bun no sore wa nani o sashite imasu ka.","german":"What does \"それ\" in this sentence refer to?","furigana":"ぶん|なに|さ"}],"notes":"指す means \"to point at; to refer to\" and is transitive. Literally: 指で地図を指す, 時計の針が三時を指す. In class: 先生に指される \"to be called on\". Abstractly very common: AはBを指す \"A refers to / means B\" - 「これ」は何を指していますか. Also in shogi: 将棋を指す. Do not confuse it with the homophones 刺す \"to stab\" and 差す (傘を差す \"to put up an umbrella\"). 指差す stresses pointing with the finger.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鳥を放しました。","romaji":"Tori o hanashimashita.","german":"I released the bird.","furigana":"とり|はな"},{"japanese":"危ないから、私の手を放さないでね。","romaji":"Abunai kara, watashi no te o hanasanaide ne.","german":"It's dangerous, so don't let go of my hand.","furigana":"あぶ|わたし|て|はな"},{"japanese":"公園では犬を放してはいけません。","romaji":"Kouen de wa inu o hanashite wa ikemasen.","german":"Dogs must not be let off the leash in the park.","furigana":"こうえん|いぬ|はな"}],"notes":"放す means \"to let go, to release\" and is transitive. Typical: 鳥を空に放す, 魚を川に放す \"to release\", 犬を放す \"to let a dog off the leash\", 手を放す \"to let go of a hand\". Be careful with the spelling: 離す (also はなす) means \"to separate, to create distance\" (目を離す \"to look away\"); with 手をはなす both spellings are common. 話す \"to speak\" is a pure homophone. Related: 手放す \"to part with, to give up\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子供が暴れています。","romaji":"Kodomo ga abarete imasu.","german":"The child is running wild.","furigana":"こども|あば"},{"japanese":"駅で酔った男が暴れて、警察が来ました。","romaji":"Eki de yotta otoko ga abarete, keisatsu ga kimashita.","german":"A drunk man rampaged at the station, and the police came.","furigana":"えき|よ|おとこ|あば|けいさつ|き"},{"japanese":"注射のとき、猫が暴れて大変だった。","romaji":"Chuusha no toki, neko ga abarete taihen datta.","german":"The cat thrashed wildly during the injection, which was quite a struggle.","furigana":"ちゅうしゃ|ねこ|あば|たいへん"}],"notes":"暴れる means \"to rampage, to run riot, to flail wildly\" and is intransitive (subject with が). It fits people (酔っ払いが暴れる), animals (馬が暴れる) and children who struggle violently or romp around. In sports it can also be positive: 試合で大暴れする \"to shine in a big way\". Distinction: 騒ぐ only means \"to be loud, to make noise\"; 暴れる includes violent movement or violence. Related: 暴力 \"violence\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"水が漏れています。","romaji":"Mizu ga morete imasu.","german":"Water is leaking.","furigana":"みず|も"},{"japanese":"会社の秘密がネットに漏れてしまいました。","romaji":"Kaisha no himitsu ga netto ni morete shimaimashita.","german":"The company's secrets leaked onto the internet.","furigana":"かいしゃ|ひみつ|も"},{"japanese":"隣の部屋から音楽が漏れて聞こえる。","romaji":"Tonari no heya kara ongaku ga morete kikoeru.","german":"Music can be heard leaking in from the next room.","furigana":"となり|へや|おんがく|も|き"}],"notes":"漏れる means \"to leak, to seep through, to escape\" and is intransitive (subject with が); the transitive counterpart is 漏らす. It applies to liquids, gas, light and sounds: 水が漏れる, ガスが漏れる, 隣から声が漏れる. Figuratively: 情報が漏れる \"information leaks\" (cf. 情報漏れ). It also means \"to be left out\": リストから名前が漏れる. In everyday writing it is often written in kana もれる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"時間を潰します。","romaji":"Jikan o tsubushimasu.","german":"I kill time.","furigana":"じかん|つぶ"},{"japanese":"ゆでたじゃがいもをフォークで潰してください。","romaji":"Yudeta jagaimo o fooku de tsubushite kudasai.","german":"Please mash the boiled potatoes with a fork.","furigana":"つぶ"},{"japanese":"父は経営が下手で、店を潰してしまった。","romaji":"Chichi wa keiei ga heta de, mise o tsubushite shimatta.","german":"My father was bad at management and ran the shop into the ground.","furigana":"ちち|けいえい|へた|みせ|つぶ"}],"notes":"潰す means \"to crush, to squash; to ruin\" and is transitive; the intransitive counterpart is 潰れる (\"to be crushed; to go bankrupt\"). Concretely: 空き缶を潰す, じゃがいもを潰す. Very common expressions: 時間を潰す \"to kill time\", 会社を潰す \"to ruin a company\", 顔を潰す \"to make someone lose face\". Distinction: 壊す is generally \"to break\", while 潰す emphasizes flattening or completely destroying something. Often also written in kana, つぶす.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"料理を味わいます。","romaji":"Ryōri o ajiwaimasu.","german":"I savor the food.","furigana":"りょうり|あじ"},{"japanese":"初めて負ける悔しさを味わいました。","romaji":"Hajimete makeru kuyashisa o ajiwaimashita.","german":"For the first time, I tasted the bitterness of losing.","furigana":"はじ|ま|くや|あじ"},{"japanese":"京都で日本の文化をじっくり味わいたいです。","romaji":"Kyouto de nihon no bunka o jikkuri ajiwaitai desu.","german":"In Kyoto, I would like to take in Japanese culture at leisure.","furigana":"きょうと|にほん|ぶんか|あじ"}],"notes":"味わう means \"to taste, to savor, to enjoy\" and is transitive. It emphasizes conscious, slow tasting: 料理をゆっくり味わう. Figuratively it means \"to experience something intensely\" - negative things too: 喜びを味わう, 悔しさを味わう, 苦しみを味わう. Unlike 食べる \"to eat\" and 楽しむ \"to have fun\", the focus is on conscious experience. Polite invitation: どうぞ味わってください. Noun: 味わい \"flavor, charm\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ネギを刻みます。","romaji":"Negi o kizamimasu.","german":"I chop the green onions.","furigana":"きざ"},{"japanese":"先生の言葉を心に刻んで、毎日頑張ります。","romaji":"Sensei no kotoba o kokoro ni kizande, mainichi ganbarimasu.","german":"I engrave my teacher's words in my heart and do my best every day.","furigana":"せんせい|ことば|こころ|きざ|まいにちがんば"},{"japanese":"木の幹に二人の名前が刻まれていた。","romaji":"Ki no miki ni futari no namae ga kizamarete ita.","german":"The names of the two were carved into the tree trunk.","furigana":"き|みき|ふたり|なまえ|きざ"}],"notes":"刻む means \"to cut finely, to chop; to carve\" and is transitive. In the kitchen: ネギ／キャベツを刻む \"to chop finely\" (cf. みじん切り \"finely minced\"). Engraving: 石に文字を刻む, 木に名前を刻む. Figuratively: 心に刻む \"to engrave in one's memory\", 時計が時を刻む \"the clock ticks away the time\", リズムを刻む. Distinction: 切る is generally \"to cut\", while 刻む means many small, even cuts or notches.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お茶を注ぎます。","romaji":"Ocha o sosogimasu.","german":"I pour the tea.","furigana":"ちゃ|そそ"},{"japanese":"彼は子どもの教育に力を注いでいる。","romaji":"Kare wa kodomo no kyouiku ni chikara o sosoide iru.","german":"He puts a lot of effort into his children's education.","furigana":"かれ|こ|きょういく|ちから|そそ"},{"japanese":"この川は太平洋に注いでいます。","romaji":"Kono kawa wa taiheiyou ni sosoide imasu.","german":"This river flows into the Pacific Ocean.","furigana":"かわ|たいへいよう|そそ"}],"notes":"注ぐ（そそぐ） means \"to pour\" and is mostly transitive: カップにお茶を注ぐ, 花に水を注ぐ. Intransitive for rivers: 川が海に注ぐ \"flows into the sea\". Figuratively very common: 〜に力を注ぐ \"to put a lot of effort into ...\", 愛情を注ぐ, 注目が注がれる. When pouring drinks, the same characters are often read つぐ (ビールをつぐ) - つぐ is more everyday, そそぐ somewhat more elevated and the usual one for all figurative meanings.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"水を零しました。","romaji":"Mizu o koboshimashita.","german":"I spilled some water.","furigana":"みず|こぼ"},{"japanese":"子供がテーブルにジュースをこぼした。","romaji":"Kodomo ga teeburu ni juusu o koboshita.","german":"The child spilled juice on the table.","furigana":"こども"},{"japanese":"父はいつも仕事の愚痴をこぼしている。","romaji":"Chichi wa itsumo shigoto no guchi o koboshite iru.","german":"My father is always complaining about work.","furigana":"ちち|しごと|ぐち"}],"notes":"零す means \"to spill, to scatter\" and is transitive; the intransitive counterpart is 零れる \"to overflow, to fall out\". It is almost always written in kana: こぼす. Typical: コーヒーをこぼす, ご飯をこぼす, 涙をこぼす \"to shed tears\". Second important meaning: 愚痴をこぼす \"to grumble, to complain\". Distinction: 落とす is \"to drop\" an object, while こぼす emphasizes sloshing out or scattering of liquid, crumbs, etc.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"グラスを傾けます。","romaji":"Gurasu o katamukemasu.","german":"I tilt the glass.","furigana":"かたむ"},{"japanese":"みんなが彼の話に耳を傾けました。","romaji":"Minna ga kare no hanashi ni mimi o katamukemashita.","german":"Everyone listened attentively to his story.","furigana":"かれ|はなし|みみ|かたむ"},{"japanese":"彼女は研究に情熱を傾けている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kenkyuu ni jounetsu o katamukete iru.","german":"She is devoting her passion to her research.","furigana":"かのじょ|けんきゅう|じょうねつ|かたむ"}],"notes":"傾ける means \"to tilt, to hold at an angle, to tip\" and is transitive; the intransitive counterpart is 傾く \"to lean, to stand crooked\". Concretely: 首を傾ける \"to tilt one's head to the side\", 瓶を傾ける. Very common figuratively: 耳を傾ける \"to listen attentively\", 〜に情熱／力を傾ける \"to pour one's energy into ...\". グラスを傾ける can, in elevated style, also mean \"to have a drink\". Tilting one's head in doubt is usually 首をかしげる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"体調を崩しました。","romaji":"Taichō o kuzushimashita.","german":"My health has worsened.","furigana":"たいちょう|くず"},{"japanese":"すみません、一万円札を崩してもらえますか。","romaji":"Sumimasen, ichiman'en satsu o kuzushite moraemasu ka.","german":"Excuse me, could you break a 10,000-yen bill for me?","furigana":"いちまんえんさつ|くず"},{"japanese":"どうぞ足を崩して、楽にしてください。","romaji":"Douzo ashi o kuzushite, raku ni shite kudasai.","german":"Please sit comfortably and make yourself at ease.","furigana":"あし|くず|らく"}],"notes":"崩す means \"to bring down, to demolish; to throw out of balance\" and is transitive; the intransitive counterpart is 崩れる. Very common expressions: 体調を崩す \"to fall ill\", バランスを崩す \"to lose one's balance\", 一万円札を崩す \"to break a 10,000-yen bill\", 足を崩す \"to sit comfortably\" (out of the formal seiza position). Concretely: 壁を崩す, 山を崩す. Also: 字を崩して書く \"to write in cursive style\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"建物が崩れました。","romaji":"Tatemono ga kuzuremashita.","german":"The building collapsed.","furigana":"たてもの|くず"},{"japanese":"午後から天気が崩れるそうです。","romaji":"Gogo kara tenki ga kuzureru sou desu.","german":"I hear the weather will turn bad from the afternoon.","furigana":"ごご|てんき|くず"},{"japanese":"汗で化粧が崩れてしまった。","romaji":"Ase de keshou ga kuzurete shimatta.","german":"My makeup smeared because of sweat.","furigana":"あせ|けしょう|くず"}],"notes":"崩れる means \"to collapse, to fall apart; to lose shape\" and is intransitive (subject with が); the transitive counterpart is 崩す. Concretely: 建物／崖が崩れる, cf. 土砂崩れ \"landslide\". Very common for weather: 天気が崩れる \"the weather turns bad\". Also: 化粧が崩れる \"makeup smears\", 形が崩れる, 計画が崩れる. Distinction: 壊れる is said of devices and objects, while 崩れる emphasizes the breakdown of a structure or order.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"経験を積みます。","romaji":"Keiken o tsumimasu.","german":"I gain experience.","furigana":"けいけん|つ"},{"japanese":"トラックに荷物を積んでから出発します。","romaji":"Torakku ni nimotsu o tsunde kara shuppatsu shimasu.","german":"We load the luggage onto the truck and then set off.","furigana":"にもつ|つ|しゅっぱつ"},{"japanese":"机の上に本が高く積んであります。","romaji":"Tsukue no ue ni hon ga takaku tsunde arimasu.","german":"Books are stacked high on the desk.","furigana":"つくえ|うえ|ほん|たか|つ"}],"notes":"積む means \"to pile up, to load; to accumulate\" and is transitive; the intransitive counterpart is 積もる \"to pile up\" (snow, dust). Literally: 箱を積む \"to stack boxes\", トラックに荷物を積む \"to load onto the truck\" (destination with に). Figuratively: 経験を積む \"to gain experience\", 練習を積む \"to practice a lot\". The resulting state with 〜てある: 本が積んである. Compounds: 積み重ねる \"to stack up, to build up step by step\", 積み木 \"building blocks\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"雪が積もりました。","romaji":"Yuki ga tsumorimashita.","german":"Snow has piled up.","furigana":"ゆき|つ"},{"japanese":"長い間掃除していないので、棚にほこりが積もっている。","romaji":"Nagai aida souji shite inai node, tana ni hokori ga tsumotte iru.","german":"Because it hasn't been cleaned for a long time, dust has accumulated on the shelf.","furigana":"なが|あいだそうじ|たな|つ"},{"japanese":"久しぶりに会ったので、積もる話がたくさんあった。","romaji":"Hisashiburi ni atta node, tsumoru hanashi ga takusan atta.","german":"We hadn't seen each other for a long time, so we had a lot to catch up on.","furigana":"ひさ|あ|つ|はなし"}],"notes":"積もる means \"to pile up, to settle\" and is intransitive (subject with が); the transitive counterpart is 積む. Typical for snow and dust: 雪が積もる, ほこりが積もる. Figuratively: 積もる話 \"a lot to talk about (after a long time)\" and the proverb 塵も積もれば山となる \"many a little makes a mickle\". The rarer transitive meaning \"to estimate\" lives on mainly in 見積もる／見積もり \"cost estimate\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"服が縮みました。","romaji":"Fuku ga chijimimashita.","german":"The clothes shrank.","furigana":"ふく|ちぢ"},{"japanese":"一位との差が少しずつ縮んできた。","romaji":"Ichii to no sa ga sukoshi zutsu chijinde kita.","german":"The gap with the first place has gradually narrowed.","furigana":"いちい|さ|すこ|ちぢ"},{"japanese":"車にぶつかりそうになって、寿命が縮んだよ。","romaji":"Kuruma ni butsukarisou ni natte, jumyou ga chijinda yo.","german":"I nearly got hit by a car, and it took years off my life.","furigana":"くるま|じゅみょう|ちぢ"}],"notes":"縮む means \"to shrink, to contract\" and is intransitive (subject with が); the transitive counterpart is 縮める \"to shorten\". Typical: 洗濯で服が縮む \"to shrink in the wash\", 差が縮む \"the gap narrows\". Idiomatic after a scare: 寿命が縮む \"it took years off my life\". Opposite: 伸びる \"to stretch, to become longer\". The formal Sino-Japanese word for reduction is 縮小.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"髪が伸びました。","romaji":"Kami ga nobimashita.","german":"My hair has grown.","furigana":"かみ|の"},{"japanese":"今年は売り上げが大きく伸びました。","romaji":"Kotoshi wa uriage ga ookiku nobimashita.","german":"Sales grew greatly this year.","furigana":"ことし|う|あ|おお|の"},{"japanese":"早く食べないと、ラーメンが伸びちゃうよ。","romaji":"Hayaku tabenai to, raamen ga nobichau yo.","german":"Eat quickly, or the ramen will get soggy.","furigana":"はや|た|の"}],"notes":"伸びる means \"to grow, to get longer, to stretch; to make progress\" and is intransitive (subject with が); the transitive counterpart is 伸ばす. Used for hair, nails, body height (背が伸びる) and plants. Figuratively: 売り上げ／成績が伸びる \"to rise, to improve\". Colloquially: ラーメンが伸びる \"the noodles get soggy\". For extending or postponing in time, the spelling 延びる is used (会議が延びる, 締め切りが延びる).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"手を伸ばします。","romaji":"Te o nobashimasu.","german":"I stretch out my hand.","furigana":"て|の"},{"japanese":"大学に入ってから、髪を伸ばしています。","romaji":"Daigaku ni haitte kara, kami o nobashite imasu.","german":"Since entering university, I've been growing my hair out.","furigana":"だいがく|はい|かみ|の"},{"japanese":"この先生は生徒のいいところを伸ばすのが上手だ。","romaji":"Kono sensei wa seito no ii tokoro o nobasu no ga jouzu da.","german":"This teacher is good at developing his students' strengths.","furigana":"せんせい|せいと|の|じょうず"}],"notes":"伸ばす means \"to stretch, to let grow longer; to develop\" and is transitive; the intransitive counterpart is 伸びる. Literally: 手を伸ばす \"to stretch out one's hand\", 髪を伸ばす \"to grow one's hair out\", 背筋を伸ばす \"to straighten up\". Figuratively: 才能／売り上げを伸ばす \"to develop, to increase\"; 足を伸ばす also means \"to go a bit farther\". For extending or postponing in time, the spelling 延ばす is mostly used (締め切りを延ばす).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"紐で縛ります。","romaji":"Himo de shibarimasu.","german":"I tie it with a string.","furigana":"ひも|しば"},{"japanese":"古い雑誌をまとめて縛ってから、ごみに出しました。","romaji":"Furui zasshi o matomete shibatte kara, gomi ni dashimashita.","german":"I bundled the old magazines together, tied them up, and put them out for disposal.","furigana":"ふる|ざっし|しば|だ"},{"japanese":"会社の規則に縛られるのは嫌だ。","romaji":"Kaisha no kisoku ni shibarareru no wa iya da.","german":"I don't like being bound by company rules.","furigana":"かいしゃ|きそく|しば|いや"}],"notes":"縛る means \"to tie, to bind, to tie up\" and is transitive: 〜を紐／縄で縛る. Concretely: 古新聞を紐で縛る \"to tie up old newspapers\", 犯人を縛る. Figuratively very common in the passive: 規則に縛られる \"to be bound by rules\", 時間に縛られない生活 \"a life not bound by time\". Distinction: 結ぶ means \"to knot, to tie together\" (a bow, shoelaces), while 縛る means wrapping firmly to hold something together or immobilize it.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"契約を結びます。","romaji":"Keiyaku o musubimasu.","german":"I will conclude a contract.","furigana":"けいやく|むす"},{"japanese":"靴ひもをしっかり結んでから走りましょう。","romaji":"Kutsuhimo o shikkari musunde kara hashirimashou.","german":"Let's tie our shoelaces tightly before we run.","furigana":"くつ|むす|はし"},{"japanese":"この橋は島と町を結んでいます。","romaji":"Kono hashi wa shima to machi o musunde imasu.","german":"This bridge connects the island with the town.","furigana":"はし|しま|まち|むす"}],"notes":"結ぶ means \"to tie, to knot; to connect; to conclude\" and is transitive. Literally: 靴ひも／リボンを結ぶ, 髪を結ぶ. Connecting: AとBを結ぶ, e.g. 東京と大阪を結ぶ新幹線. Concluding: 契約／条約を結ぶ. Other expressions: 実を結ぶ \"to bear fruit\" (also figuratively), 口を結ぶ \"to press one's lips together\". Opposite: ほどく \"to untie\". Distinction from 縛る: 結ぶ is knotting and linking, 縛る is tying up tightly.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"問題を解きます。","romaji":"Mondai o tokimasu.","german":"I solve the problem.","furigana":"もんだい|と"},{"japanese":"早く誤解を解きたいです。","romaji":"Hayaku gokai o tokitai desu.","german":"I want to clear up the misunderstanding quickly.","furigana":"はや|ごかい|と"},{"japanese":"このパズルは難しくて、なかなか解けない。","romaji":"Kono pazuru wa muzukashikute, nakanaka tokenai.","german":"This puzzle is hard, and I just can't solve it.","furigana":"むずか|と"}],"notes":"解く means \"to solve; to untie\" and is transitive; the intransitive counterpart is 解ける \"to be solved, to come undone\" (この問題は解けない). In everyday speech mainly for tasks with an answer: 問題／パズル／なぞを解く. Figuratively: 誤解を解く \"to clear up a misunderstanding\". For untying cords, ほどく is often read or written. Distinction: \"solving\" a problem in the sense of a difficulty is more 解決する.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"バターを溶かします。","romaji":"Batā o tokashimasu.","german":"I melt the butter.","furigana":"と"},{"japanese":"薬を水に溶かして飲んでください。","romaji":"Kusuri o mizu ni tokashite nonde kudasai.","german":"Please dissolve the medicine in water and drink it.","furigana":"くすり|みず|と|の"},{"japanese":"春の日差しが雪を溶かしていく。","romaji":"Haru no hizashi ga yuki o tokashite iku.","german":"The spring sunshine melts the snow little by little.","furigana":"はる|ひざ|ゆき|と"}],"notes":"溶かす means \"to melt, to dissolve\" and is transitive; the intransitive counterpart is 溶ける. It applies to solids through heat (バター／チョコレート／雪を溶かす) and to dissolving in a liquid, with the target marked by に: 砂糖をお湯に溶かす. For metal, 融かす is also seen in technical writing, but in everyday use 溶かす suffices. Do not confuse it with the homophone とかす \"to comb\" (髪をとかす, mostly in kana).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本語に英語が混じっています。","romaji":"Nihongo ni eigo ga majitte imasu.","german":"English is mixed into Japanese.","furigana":"にほんご|えいご|ま"},{"japanese":"父の髪に白いものが混じってきた。","romaji":"Chichi no kami ni shiroi mono ga majitte kita.","german":"Gray hair has started to appear in my father's hair.","furigana":"ちち|かみ|しろ|ま"},{"japanese":"雨に雪が混じり始めました。","romaji":"Ame ni yuki ga majiri hajimemashita.","german":"Snow has begun to mix with the rain.","furigana":"あめ|ゆき|ま|はじ"}],"notes":"混じる means \"to mix, to be mixed in\" and is intransitive: the added element takes が, the whole takes に - 日本語に英語が混じる, 髪に白髪が混じる. The transitive counterpart is 混ぜる \"to mix\". 混ざる is almost synonymous; 混じる often stresses that individual elements remain recognizable. For people, 交じる is usually written: 大人に交じって子どもも走る. As a suffix: 〜混じり (雨混じりの雪, 冗談混じりに).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"荷物をカバンに詰めます。","romaji":"Nimotsu o kaban ni tsumemasu.","german":"I pack my things into the bag.","furigana":"にもつ|つ"},{"japanese":"すみません、もう少し詰めていただけますか。","romaji":"Sumimasen, mou sukoshi tsumete itadakemasu ka.","german":"Excuse me, could you move over a little?","furigana":"すこ|つ"},{"japanese":"細かい点は来週の会議で詰めましょう。","romaji":"Komakai ten wa raishuu no kaigi de tsumemashou.","german":"Let's work out the details at next week's meeting.","furigana":"こま|てん|らいしゅう|かいぎ|つ"}],"notes":"詰める means \"to stuff in, to pack (full); to move closer together; to work out\" and is transitive; the intransitive counterpart is 詰まる. Literally: カバンに荷物を詰める, 箱にお菓子を詰める (destination with に). Making room: もう少し詰めてください \"Please move over a little\". Abstractly: 話／細かい点を詰める \"to sort out details\". As an auxiliary: 思い詰める \"to brood too much\", 問い詰める \"to press with questions\". Related: 缶詰 \"canned food\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"排水管が詰まりました。","romaji":"Haisuikan ga tsumarimashita.","german":"The drainpipe is clogged.","furigana":"はいすいかん|つ"},{"japanese":"今週は予定がぎっしり詰まっています。","romaji":"Konshuu wa yotei ga gisshiri tsumatte imasu.","german":"My schedule is packed this week.","furigana":"こんしゅう|よてい|つ"},{"japanese":"急に質問されて、答えに詰まってしまった。","romaji":"Kyuu ni shitsumon sarete, kotae ni tsumatte shimatta.","german":"I was asked suddenly and was lost for an answer.","furigana":"きゅう|しつもん|こた|つ"}],"notes":"詰まる means \"to be clogged; to be full; to be stuck\" and is intransitive (subject with が); the transitive counterpart is 詰める. Clogging: 排水管／トイレが詰まる, 鼻が詰まる \"stuffy nose\". Full: 予定がぎっしり詰まっている, 思い出が詰まったアルバム. Getting stuck: 言葉／答えに詰まる \"to be at a loss for an answer\", 息が詰まる \"to be unable to breathe; to feel suffocated\". Related: 行き詰まる \"to reach a dead end\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"壁にペンキを塗ります。","romaji":"Kabe ni penki o nurimasu.","german":"I am painting the wall.","furigana":"かべ|ぬ"},{"japanese":"パンにバターを塗って食べます。","romaji":"Pan ni bataa o nutte tabemasu.","german":"I spread butter on bread and eat it.","furigana":"ぬ|た"},{"japanese":"傷に薬を塗りました。","romaji":"Kizu ni kusuri o nurimashita.","german":"I applied ointment to the wound.","furigana":"きず|くすり|ぬ"}],"notes":"塗る means \"to apply a layer onto a surface\": the material takes を, the surface takes に – 壁にペンキを塗る, パンにバターを塗る, 顔にクリームを塗る, 傷に薬を塗る. For painting a picture, 描く is used instead; 塗る is only painting over or spreading on. There is no intransitive counterpart; \"is painted\" is 塗ってある. Related are 塗り薬 \"ointment\" and the sign ペンキ塗りたて \"wet paint\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"髪を乾かします。","romaji":"Kami o kawakashimasu.","german":"I dry my hair.","furigana":"かみ|かわ"},{"japanese":"雨で濡れた靴をストーブの前で乾かしました。","romaji":"Ame de nureta kutsu o sutoobu no mae de kawakashimashita.","german":"I dried my rain-soaked shoes in front of the heater.","furigana":"あめ|ぬ|くつ|まえ|かわ"},{"japanese":"洗濯物はいつも乾燥機で乾かしています。","romaji":"Sentakumono wa itsumo kansouki de kawakashite imasu.","german":"I always dry the laundry in the dryer.","furigana":"せんたくもの|かんそうき|かわ"}],"notes":"乾かす means \"to dry, to let dry\" and is transitive: 〜を乾かす; the intransitive counterpart is 乾く \"to become dry\". Typical: 髪をドライヤーで乾かす, 洗濯物を乾かす, 濡れた靴を乾かす. Distinction: 干す means \"to hang or lay out to dry\" (in sun or wind), whereas 乾かす stresses the result of becoming dry, also with a hair dryer or tumble dryer. Do not confuse it with 渇く \"to be thirsty\" (喉が渇く).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"桜の花が散りました。","romaji":"Sakura no hana ga chirimashita.","german":"The cherry blossoms have fallen.","furigana":"さくら|はな|ち"},{"japanese":"テレビがついていると気が散って勉強できない。","romaji":"Terebi ga tsuite iru to ki ga chitte benkyou dekinai.","german":"When the TV is on, I get distracted and can't study.","furigana":"き|ち|べんきょう"},{"japanese":"祭りが終わると、人々はそれぞれの家へ散っていった。","romaji":"Matsuri ga owaru to, hitobito wa sorezore no ie e chitte itta.","german":"When the festival ended, people dispersed, each to their own home.","furigana":"まつ|お|ひとびと|いえ|ち"}],"notes":"散る means \"to fall (off), to scatter\" and is intransitive (subject with が); the transitive counterpart is 散らす. Classic for blossoms and leaves: 桜が散る - strongly associated with transience. Also: 人々が散っていく \"to disperse\", 火花が散る \"sparks fly\" and, very common, 気が散る \"to be distracted\". Distinction: 落ちる is the general \"to fall down\", whereas 散る means many pieces scattering. Note: despite the -iru ending, it is a godan verb.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"努力が実りました。","romaji":"Doryoku ga minorimashita.","german":"My efforts paid off.","furigana":"どりょく|みの"},{"japanese":"今年は天気が良かったので、お米がよく実った。","romaji":"Kotoshi wa tenki ga yokatta node, okome ga yoku minotta.","german":"The weather was good this year, so the rice ripened well.","furigana":"ことし|てんき|よ|こめ|みの"},{"japanese":"長い片思いがやっと実りました。","romaji":"Nagai kataomoi ga yatto minorimashita.","german":"My long unrequited love has finally come true.","furigana":"なが|かたおも|みの"}],"notes":"実る means \"to bear fruit, to ripen; to pay off\" and is intransitive (subject with が); it comes from 実 \"fruit\". Literally: 稲が実る, リンゴが実る. Figuratively very common: 努力が実る \"effort pays off\", 恋が実る \"love is returned\". Well-known proverb: 実るほど頭を垂れる稲穂かな - the riper the ear of rice, the lower it bows (humility). Similar, somewhat more formal: 実を結ぶ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"芽が出ました。","romaji":"Me ga demashita.","german":"A sprout has come out.","furigana":"め|で"},{"japanese":"じゃがいもの芽は食べないでください。","romaji":"Jagaimo no me wa tabenaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't eat the sprouts of potatoes.","furigana":"め|た"},{"japanese":"彼は十年頑張ったが、なかなか芽が出なかった。","romaji":"Kare wa juunen ganbatta ga, nakanaka me ga denakatta.","german":"He worked hard for ten years, but his big break never came.","furigana":"かれ|じゅうねんがんば|め|で"}],"notes":"芽 is the \"sprout, shoot, bud\" - the first thing that grows from a seed or branch. Typical verbs: 芽が出る／芽を出す \"to sprout, to bud\", plus 新芽 \"young shoot\". Figuratively: 才能の芽 \"the seeds of talent\", 悪の芽を摘む \"to nip evil in the bud\", 芽が出ない \"to not make a breakthrough\" (especially artists, athletes). Distinction: つぼみ is the flower bud shortly before blooming. Homophone: 目 \"eye\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"木の根が深いです。","romaji":"Ki no ne ga fukai desu.","german":"The tree's roots are deep.","furigana":"き|ね|ふか"},{"japanese":"彼は口は悪いけど、根は優しい人だ。","romaji":"Kare wa kuchi wa warui kedo, ne wa yasashii hito da.","german":"He has a sharp tongue, but at heart he is a kind person.","furigana":"かれ|くち|わる|ね|やさ|ひと"},{"japanese":"まだ昔のことを根に持っているの？","romaji":"Mada mukashi no koto o ne ni motte iru no?","german":"Are you still holding a grudge about that old matter?","furigana":"むかし|ね|も"}],"notes":"根 is the \"root\" - literally of plants (木の根, 根を張る \"to put down roots\") and on the body (歯の根). Figuratively: 問題の根は深い \"the problem runs deep\", 根に持つ \"to hold a grudge\", 根はいい人 \"a good person at heart\". In compounds it is often read こん: 大根 \"daikon radish\", 根気 \"perseverance\". Colloquially, people also say 根っこ for plant roots.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"太い幹の木です。","romaji":"Futoi miki no ki desu.","german":"It is a tree with a thick trunk.","furigana":"ふと|みき|き"},{"japanese":"子供たちが木の幹に耳を当てている。","romaji":"Kodomotachi ga ki no miki ni mimi o atete iru.","german":"The children are pressing their ears against the tree trunk.","furigana":"こども|き|みき|みみ|あ"},{"japanese":"台風で木が倒れ、幹が二つに折れてしまった。","romaji":"Taifuu de ki ga taore, miki ga futatsu ni orete shimatta.","german":"The typhoon knocked a tree down, and the trunk broke in two.","furigana":"たいふう|き|たお|みき|ふた|お"}],"notes":"幹 is the \"tree trunk\" - the main part of a tree between the roots (根) and the branches (枝). Typical: 太い幹, 幹が太い. As a standalone word it is almost always literal; the figurative meaning \"core, main part\" is found mainly in compounds: 幹線道路 \"main road\", 新幹線 \"Shinkansen\" (literally \"new trunk line\"), 幹事 \"organizer (e.g. of a party)\", 根幹 \"foundation\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"森林を守りましょう。","romaji":"Shinrin o mamorimashō.","german":"Let's protect the forests.","furigana":"しんりん|まも"},{"japanese":"日本の国土の約三分の二は森林です。","romaji":"Nihon no kokudo no yaku sanbun no ni wa shinrin desu.","german":"About two thirds of Japan's land area is forest.","furigana":"にほん|こくど|やくさんぶん|に|しんりん"},{"japanese":"週末は森林浴をしにハイキングに行きます。","romaji":"Shuumatsu wa shinrin'yoku o shi ni haikingu ni ikimasu.","german":"On weekends, I go hiking to take a forest bath.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|しんりんよく|い"}],"notes":"森林 \"forest, woodland\" is a Sino-Japanese word made of 森 and 林 and is typical of news, statistics and environmental topics: 森林破壊 \"deforestation\", 森林資源, 森林浴 \"forest bathing\". In everyday speech one usually says 森 (a large, dense forest) or 林 (a small wood, grove). 森林 refers more to forest as an area or ecosystem. About two thirds of Japan is forested.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"滝を見に行きました。","romaji":"Taki o mi ni ikimashita.","german":"I went to see the waterfall.","furigana":"たき|み|い"},{"japanese":"走ったあと、滝のような汗をかいた。","romaji":"Hashitta ato, taki no you na ase o kaita.","german":"After running, sweat poured off me like a waterfall.","furigana":"はし|たき|あせ"},{"japanese":"この滝は高さが百メートル近くあります。","romaji":"Kono taki wa takasa ga hyaku meetoru chikaku arimasu.","german":"This waterfall is nearly a hundred meters high.","furigana":"たき|たか|ひゃく|ちか"}],"notes":"滝 is a \"waterfall\"; famous waterfalls have it in their names, such as 華厳の滝. Typical: 滝を見に行く, 滝が落ちる. Figuratively: 滝のような汗／雨 \"sweat or rain in torrents\". In a religious context there is 滝に打たれる \"to stand under a waterfall for purification\". The Sino-Japanese word 瀑布 is purely literary.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"深い谷があります。","romaji":"Fukai tani ga arimasu.","german":"There is a deep valley.","furigana":"ふか|たに"},{"japanese":"橋の上から谷を見下ろすと、少し怖かった。","romaji":"Hashi no ue kara tani o miorosu to, sukoshi kowakatta.","german":"Looking down into the valley from the bridge, I felt a little scared.","furigana":"はし|うえ|たに|みお|すこ|こわ"},{"japanese":"人生は山あり谷ありだ。","romaji":"Jinsei wa yama ari tani ari da.","german":"Life has its ups and downs.","furigana":"じんせい|やま|たに"}],"notes":"谷 is a \"valley\" or \"gorge\" - a depression between mountains, often with a river: 深い谷, 谷底 \"valley floor\", 谷川 \"mountain stream\". The opposite is 山. Figuratively: 山あり谷ありの人生 \"a life with ups and downs\", 景気の谷 \"an economic trough\". Common in place names and family names, where it is sometimes read や (渋谷). A wide basin surrounded by mountains, by contrast, is a 盆地.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"崖に気をつけてください。","romaji":"Gake ni ki o tsukete kudasai.","german":"Please be careful of the cliff.","furigana":"がけ|き"},{"japanese":"大雨のあと、道の近くで崖崩れがあった。","romaji":"Ooame no ato, michi no chikaku de gakekuzure ga atta.","german":"After the heavy rain, there was a landslide near the road.","furigana":"おおあめ|みち|ちか|がけくず"},{"japanese":"負けたらもう後がない、崖っぷちの試合だ。","romaji":"Maketara mou ato ga nai, gakeppuchi no shiai da.","german":"If we lose, we have nothing left - it's a match with our backs to the wall.","furigana":"ま|あと|がけ|しあい"}],"notes":"崖 is a \"cliff, steep face\" - an almost vertical wall of rock or earth. Typical: 崖から落ちる, 崖の上／下, 崖崩れ \"landslide\" (common in bad-weather news). Figuratively: 崖っぷち \"on the brink of the abyss\", e.g. 崖っぷちのチーム \"a team with its back to the wall\". Distinction: 坂 is a walkable slope, 崖 is almost vertical and dangerous; 断崖 is the literary \"sheer cliff\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"砂漠を横断しました。","romaji":"Sabaku o ōdan shimashita.","german":"We crossed the desert.","furigana":"さばく|おうだん"},{"japanese":"砂漠では昼と夜の気温の差がとても大きい。","romaji":"Sabaku de wa hiru to yoru no kion no sa ga totemo ookii.","german":"In the desert, the temperature difference between day and night is very large.","furigana":"さばく|ひる|よる|きおん|さ|おお"},{"japanese":"地球温暖化で砂漠が広がっている。","romaji":"Chikyuu ondanka de sabaku ga hirogatte iru.","german":"Deserts are spreading because of global warming.","furigana":"ちきゅうおんだんか|さばく|ひろ"}],"notes":"砂漠 is a \"desert\", made of 砂 \"sand\" and 漠 \"vast, barren\". Typical: サハラ砂漠, 砂漠を横断する, 砂漠化 \"desertification\". Figuratively: 都会の砂漠 \"the big city as a desert\" (loneliness). The older spelling 沙漠 is still occasionally seen. Do not confuse it with 砂浜 \"sandy beach\" and 砂場 \"sandbox\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本には火山が多いです。","romaji":"Nihon ni wa kazan ga ōi desu.","german":"There are many volcanoes in Japan.","furigana":"にほん|かざん|おお"},{"japanese":"火山が噴火して、町に灰が降りました。","romaji":"Kazan ga funka shite, machi ni hai ga furimashita.","german":"The volcano erupted, and ash fell on the town.","furigana":"かざん|ふんか|まち|はい|ふ"},{"japanese":"富士山も火山だということを知っていますか。","romaji":"Fujisan mo kazan da to iu koto o shitte imasu ka.","german":"Do you know that Mt. Fuji is also a volcano?","furigana":"ふじさん|かざん|し"}],"notes":"火山 is a \"volcano\", from 火 \"fire\" and 山 \"mountain\". Typical: 火山が噴火する \"to erupt\", 活火山 \"active volcano\", 火山灰 \"volcanic ash\". Japan lies on the Pacific Ring of Fire and has over a hundred active volcanoes; 富士山 is one of them. The country's many 温泉 \"hot springs\" are also due to volcanism. Neutral, used in everyday speech as well as in the news.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"火山が噴火しました。","romaji":"Kazan ga funka shimashita.","german":"The volcano erupted.","furigana":"かざん|ふんか"},{"japanese":"噴火の影響で、飛行機が欠航になりました。","romaji":"Funka no eikyou de, hikouki ga kekkou ni narimashita.","german":"Because of the eruption, flights were canceled.","furigana":"ふんか|えいきょう|ひこうき|けっこう"},{"japanese":"富士山が最後に噴火したのは1707年です。","romaji":"Fujisan ga saigo ni funka shita no wa 1707-nen desu.","german":"Mt. Fuji last erupted in 1707.","furigana":"ふじさん|さいご|ふんか|ねん"}],"notes":"噴火 is the eruption of a volcano. As an intransitive する-verb, the volcano is the subject with が: 火山が噴火する. Common compounds are 大噴火 \"huge eruption\", 噴火警報 \"eruption warning\" and 噴火口 \"crater\". For the outbreak of diseases, fire or conflict, 噴火 is not used; one says, for example, 発生する or 起こる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"津波警報が出ました。","romaji":"Tsunami keihō ga demashita.","german":"A tsunami warning was issued.","furigana":"つなみけいほう|で"},{"japanese":"この地震による津波の心配はありません。","romaji":"Kono jishin ni yoru tsunami no shinpai wa arimasen.","german":"There is no danger of a tsunami from this earthquake.","furigana":"じしん|つなみ|しんぱい"},{"japanese":"津波が来るかもしれないので、高い所へ逃げてください。","romaji":"Tsunami ga kuru kamo shirenai node, takai tokoro e nigete kudasai.","german":"A tsunami may come, so please flee to high ground.","furigana":"つなみ|く|たか|ところ|に"}],"notes":"津波 (literally \"harbor wave\") is the word that was adopted into other languages as \"tsunami\". Typical are 津波が来る／押し寄せる, 津波警報 \"tsunami warning\" and the weaker 津波注意報 \"tsunami advisory\". After every earthquake, the news says 津波の心配はありません \"there is no danger of a tsunami\". Ordinary high waves during a storm are called 高波 or 大波 instead.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 39"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"洪水で被害が出ました。","romaji":"Kōzui de higai ga demashita.","german":"The flood caused damage.","furigana":"こうずい|ひがい|で"},{"japanese":"大雨で川があふれて、町が洪水になりました。","romaji":"Ooame de kawa ga afurete, machi ga kouzui ni narimashita.","german":"The river overflowed in the heavy rain, and the town was flooded.","furigana":"おおあめ|かわ|まち|こうずい"},{"japanese":"現代人は情報の洪水の中で生きている。","romaji":"Gendaijin wa jouhou no kouzui no naka de ikite iru.","german":"Modern people live in a flood of information.","furigana":"げんだいじん|じょうほう|こうずい|なか|い"}],"notes":"洪水 is a flood, mostly because a river overflows its banks after heavy rain. Typical: 洪水が起きる, 町が洪水になる, with the cause marked by で: 洪水で家が流された. In the news one often hears 浸水 \"being under water\" (houses, streets) alongside it. Figuratively, 〜の洪水 means a flood of something, e.g. 情報の洪水 \"information overload\" or 車の洪水.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"干ばつが続いています。","romaji":"Kanbatsu ga tsuzuite imasu.","german":"The drought continues.","furigana":"かん|つづ"},{"japanese":"干ばつで野菜の値段が上がりました。","romaji":"Kanbatsu de yasai no nedan ga agarimashita.","german":"Vegetable prices rose because of the drought.","furigana":"かん|やさい|ねだん|あ"},{"japanese":"その国は去年ひどい干ばつに苦しみました。","romaji":"Sono kuni wa kyonen hidoi kanbatsu ni kurushimimashita.","german":"That country suffered a severe drought last year.","furigana":"くに|きょねん|かん|くる"}],"notes":"干ばつ is a long dry period in which rain fails to come and fields dry out. It is originally written 旱魃; because these kanji are not everyday kanji, newspapers write the mixed form 干ばつ. Typical: 干ばつが続く, 干ばつが起きる, 干ばつの被害, 干ばつに苦しむ. For merely dry weather, everyday speech uses 雨が降らない or 水不足 \"water shortage\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"避難場所を確認してください。","romaji":"Hinan basho o kakunin shite kudasai.","german":"Please check the evacuation site.","furigana":"ひなんばしょ|かくにん"},{"japanese":"台風が来たので、近くの小学校に避難しました。","romaji":"Taifuu ga kita node, chikaku no shougakkou ni hinan shimashita.","german":"Because a typhoon was coming, we took shelter in a nearby elementary school.","furigana":"たいふう|き|ちか|しょうがっこう|ひなん"},{"japanese":"会社では年に一回、避難訓練があります。","romaji":"Kaisha de wa nen ni ikkai, hinan kunren ga arimasu.","german":"At the company, there is an evacuation drill once a year.","furigana":"かいしゃ|ねん|いっかい|ひなんくんれん"}],"notes":"避難 is moving to a safe place to escape danger, especially in disasters. As an intransitive する-verb the destination takes に or へ: 学校に避難する \"to take shelter in the school\". Important compounds: 避難所 \"emergency shelter\", 避難場所 \"assembly point\", 避難訓練 \"evacuation drill\", 避難指示 \"evacuation order\". Not to be confused with the identically pronounced 非難 \"criticism, blame\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"救助隊が到着しました。","romaji":"Kyūjotai ga tōchaku shimashita.","german":"The rescue team has arrived.","furigana":"きゅうじょたい|とうちゃく"},{"japanese":"山で道に迷った人が、ヘリコプターで救助されました。","romaji":"Yama de michi ni mayotta hito ga, herikoputaa de kyuujo saremashita.","german":"A person who got lost in the mountains was rescued by helicopter.","furigana":"やま|みち|まよ|ひと|きゅうじょ"},{"japanese":"男性が海でおぼれている子どもを救助しました。","romaji":"Dansei ga umi de oborete iru kodomo o kyuujo shimashita.","german":"A man rescued a child who was drowning in the sea.","furigana":"だんせい|うみ|こ|きゅうじょ"}],"notes":"救助 is the rescue of people from acute danger, for example in accidents, at sea or after disasters. As a transitive する-verb: 人を救助する; from the viewpoint of those affected, usually in the passive: 救助される \"to be rescued\". Compounds: 救助隊 \"rescue team\", 人命救助 \"life-saving\"; 救助を求める means \"to call for help\". In everyday speech people say the simpler 助ける; 救助 sounds like an official operation and news reports. Do not confuse it with 救急 \"emergency\" as in 救急車.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"犯罪を防ぎます。","romaji":"Hanzai o fusegimasu.","german":"We prevent crime.","furigana":"はんざい|ふせ"},{"japanese":"この町では最近、犯罪が増えています。","romaji":"Kono machi de wa saikin, hanzai ga fuete imasu.","german":"Crime has been increasing in this town recently.","furigana":"まち|さいきん|はんざい|ふ"},{"japanese":"他人のパスワードを勝手に使うのは犯罪です。","romaji":"Tanin no pasuwaado o katte ni tsukau no wa hanzai desu.","german":"Using someone else's password without permission is a crime.","furigana":"たにん|かって|つか|はんざい"}],"notes":"犯罪 is the general term for criminal acts, often as a social phenomenon. Typical: 犯罪を犯す \"to commit a crime\", 犯罪を防ぐ, 犯罪が増える／減る, 犯罪に巻き込まれる \"to get caught up in a crime\"; the perpetrator is 犯罪者. For a specific individual case, people usually say 事件, and 罪 is more \"guilt, sin\" in the moral sense.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"容疑者が逮捕されました。","romaji":"Yōgisha ga taiho saremashita.","german":"The suspect was arrested.","furigana":"ようぎしゃ|たいほ"},{"japanese":"警察は容疑者の顔写真を公開しました。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa yougisha no kaojashin o koukai shimashita.","german":"The police released a photo of the suspect's face.","furigana":"けいさつ|ようぎしゃ|かおじゃしん|こうかい"},{"japanese":"容疑者は「お金に困っていた」と話しているそうです。","romaji":"Yougisha wa \"okane ni komatte ita\" to hanashite iru sou desu.","german":"The suspect is reported to have said, \"I was in financial trouble.\"","furigana":"ようぎしゃ|かね|こま|はな"}],"notes":"容疑者 is a person suspected of a crime who is under police investigation - a typical news word. In reports it even stands like a title directly after the name: 山田容疑者. Typical: 容疑者を逮捕する, 容疑者が逃げる, 容疑者が容疑を認める \"the suspect confesses\". After indictment, the same person is called 被告 \"defendant\"; 犯人, by contrast, is the actual perpetrator.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"被害者を助けます。","romaji":"Higaisha o tasukemasu.","german":"We help the victims.","furigana":"ひがいしゃ|たす"},{"japanese":"詐欺の被害者は高齢者が多いです。","romaji":"Sagi no higaisha wa koureisha ga ooi desu.","german":"Victims of fraud are often elderly people.","furigana":"さぎ|ひがいしゃ|こうれいしゃ|おお"},{"japanese":"事故の被害者の家族に話を聞きました。","romaji":"Jiko no higaisha no kazoku ni hanashi o kikimashita.","german":"We spoke with the family of the accident victim.","furigana":"じこ|ひがいしゃ|かぞく|はなし|き"}],"notes":"被害者 is someone who has suffered harm or wrong: a victim of crime, fraud, accidents or disasters. The opposite is 加害者 \"perpetrator, offender\". The underlying word is 被害 \"damage\": 被害に遭う \"to become a victim\", 被害を受ける. For fatalities in disasters, people more often say 犠牲者; 被害者 stresses the harm suffered, not necessarily death.","levelNote":"Compound: 被害 (N3) + 者 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その提案に賛成です。","romaji":"Sono teian ni sansei desu.","german":"I agree with that proposal.","furigana":"ていあん|さんせい"},{"japanese":"「今夜はラーメンにしない？」「賛成！」","romaji":"\"Kon'ya wa raamen ni shinai?\" \"Sansei!\"","german":"\"How about ramen tonight?\" \"Sounds good!\"","furigana":"こんや|さんせい"},{"japanese":"父は私の留学に賛成してくれませんでした。","romaji":"Chichi wa watashi no ryuugaku ni sansei shite kuremasen deshita.","german":"My father did not approve of my studying abroad.","furigana":"ちち|わたし|りゅうがく|さんせい"}],"notes":"賛成 means to agree with a proposal or an opinion. What one is in favor of takes に: 〜に賛成する, 〜に賛成です. The opposite is 反対: 賛成ですか、反対ですか \"Are you for or against?\" In casual conversation, a short 賛成！ \"Count me in!\" is enough. 賛成 is the neutral everyday word, also used in votes; 同意 \"consent\" sounds more formal and contractual.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 47"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"妥協点を見つけます。","romaji":"Dakyōten o mitsukemasu.","german":"We will find a compromise.","furigana":"だきょうてん|み"},{"japanese":"家探しでは、駅からの距離は妥協しました。","romaji":"Iesagashi de wa, eki kara no kyori wa dakyou shimashita.","german":"When looking for a home, I compromised on the distance from the station.","furigana":"いえさが|えき|きょり|だきょう"},{"japanese":"あのシェフは料理の味に絶対に妥協しない。","romaji":"Ano shefu wa ryouri no aji ni zettai ni dakyou shinai.","german":"That chef never compromises on the taste of his food.","furigana":"りょうり|あじ|ぜったい|だきょう"}],"notes":"妥協 means giving way and meeting in the middle to reach an agreement. As an intransitive する-verb: 〜と妥協する \"to compromise with someone\", 〜で妥協する \"to settle for something\". Fixed expression: 妥協点 \"common ground\". 妥協 often sounds slightly negative in the sense of \"making concessions\"; 妥協しない \"to make no compromises\" is therefore praise for high standards.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ビザを申請します。","romaji":"Biza o shinsei shimasu.","german":"I will apply for a visa.","furigana":"しんせい"},{"japanese":"有給休暇を申請したいんですが、どうすればいいですか。","romaji":"Yuukyuu kyuuka o shinsei shitai n desu ga, dou sureba ii desu ka.","german":"I'd like to apply for paid leave. What should I do?","furigana":"ゆうきゅうきゅうか|しんせい"},{"japanese":"申請書は市役所の窓口に出してください。","romaji":"Shinseisho wa shiyakusho no madoguchi ni dashite kudasai.","german":"Please submit the application form at the counter of City Hall.","furigana":"しんせいしょ|しやくしょ|まどぐち|だ"}],"notes":"申請 is an official application to an authority, company or institution, on which a decision is then made. As a transitive する-verb: ビザを申請する, with the recipient marked by に: 市役所に申請する. Compounds: 申請書 \"application form\", 申請中 \"application in progress\". For registrations to courses or events, one says 申し込み, and for applying for a job 応募 - 申請 is the formal application for permission or benefits.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"許可をもらいました。","romaji":"Kyoka o moraimashita.","german":"I got permission.","furigana":"きょか"},{"japanese":"許可なく、この部屋に入らないでください。","romaji":"Kyoka naku, kono heya ni hairanaide kudasai.","german":"Please do not enter this room without permission.","furigana":"きょか|へや|はい"},{"japanese":"学校はアルバイトを許可していません。","romaji":"Gakkou wa arubaito o kyoka shite imasen.","german":"The school does not allow part-time jobs.","furigana":"がっこう|きょか"}],"notes":"許可 is permission or official approval. Typical: 許可をもらう／得る \"to receive\", 許可を出す \"to grant\", 許可を求める \"to ask for permission\", 〜を許可する; \"without permission\" is 許可なく or 無断で. Compounds: 許可証 \"permit\", 営業許可 \"business license\". The opposite is 禁止. Between friends 許可 sounds very formal – there one simply asks いい？","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 35"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"撮影禁止です。","romaji":"Satsuei kinshi desu.","german":"Photography is prohibited.","furigana":"さつえいきんし"},{"japanese":"この公園では、ボール遊びが禁止されています。","romaji":"Kono kouen de wa, booru asobi ga kinshi sarete imasu.","german":"Playing ball games is prohibited in this park.","furigana":"こうえん|あそ|きんし"},{"japanese":"会社は今月から社内での喫煙を禁止しました。","romaji":"Kaisha wa kongetsu kara shanai de no kitsuen o kinshi shimashita.","german":"From this month, the company has banned smoking inside the building.","furigana":"かいしゃ|こんげつ|しゃない|きつえん|きんし"}],"notes":"禁止 is an explicit prohibition. On signs it usually comes without a particle after what is prohibited: 駐車禁止, 立入禁止, 撮影禁止, 喫煙禁止. As a verb: 〜を禁止する, often in the passive 〜は禁止されている. The opposite is 許可. In conversation, prohibitions are phrased more softly with 〜てはいけません or 〜ないでください; 禁止 sounds official and strict.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"速度制限があります。","romaji":"Sokudo seigen ga arimasu.","german":"There is a speed limit.","furigana":"そくどせいげん"},{"japanese":"このプランはデータ通信量に制限がありません。","romaji":"Kono puran wa deeta tsuushinryou ni seigen ga arimasen.","german":"This plan has no limit on data usage.","furigana":"つうしんりょう|せいげん"},{"japanese":"医者に塩分を制限するように言われました。","romaji":"Isha ni enbun o seigen suru you ni iwaremashita.","german":"The doctor told me to limit my salt intake.","furigana":"いしゃ|えんぶん|せいげん|い"}],"notes":"制限 is a limitation of quantity, time, age or speed. Typical: 制限がある, 制限を設ける, 〜を制限する; compounds: 速度制限, 年齢制限, 制限時間 \"time limit\", 無制限 \"unlimited\". Distinction: 禁止 forbids something entirely, 制限 allows it only up to a limit. The personal limit of endurance (\"I'm at my limit\") is 限界 instead.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"交通違反をしました。","romaji":"Kōtsū ihan o shimashita.","german":"I committed a traffic violation.","furigana":"こうつういはん"},{"japanese":"スピード違反で罰金を払いました。","romaji":"Supiido ihan de bakkin o haraimashita.","german":"I paid a fine for speeding.","furigana":"いはん|ばっきん|はら"},{"japanese":"試験中に辞書を使うのはルール違反です。","romaji":"Shiken-chuu ni jisho o tsukau no wa ruuru ihan desu.","german":"Using a dictionary during the exam is against the rules.","furigana":"しけんちゅう|じしょ|つか|いはん"}],"notes":"違反 is a violation of rules, laws or contracts. What is violated takes に: 法律に違反する, ルールに違反する. Very common as compounds: 交通違反, スピード違反, 駐車違反, 契約違反, ルール違反; colloquially: それ、ルール違反だよ \"That's against the rules!\" 違反 refers to rule-breaking; serious offenses are called 犯罪.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"罰を受けます。","romaji":"Batsu o ukemasu.","german":"I will receive punishment.","furigana":"ばつ|う"},{"japanese":"ゲームに負けた人は罰として歌を歌います。","romaji":"Geemu ni maketa hito wa batsu to shite uta o utaimasu.","german":"Whoever loses the game has to sing a song as a penalty.","furigana":"ま|ひと|ばつ|うた|うた"},{"japanese":"宿題を忘れた罰に、教室の掃除をさせられました。","romaji":"Shukudai o wasureta batsu ni, kyoushitsu no souji o saseraremashita.","german":"As punishment for forgetting my homework, I was made to clean the classroom.","furigana":"しゅくだい|わす|ばつ|きょうしつ|そうじ"}],"notes":"罰 is a punishment as a consequence of misbehavior, especially in upbringing, games or in the moral sense. Typical: 罰を受ける \"to be punished\", 罰を与える \"to punish\", 〜の罰として \"as punishment for ...\"; the verb is 罰する. 罰ゲーム is the forfeit for the loser of a game, and in 罰が当たる \"to suffer divine retribution\" it is read ばち. Fines are 罰金, judicial punishments 刑.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"信頼を築きます。","romaji":"Shinrai o kizukimasu.","german":"I build trust.","furigana":"しんらい|きず"},{"japanese":"彼は上司からとても信頼されています。","romaji":"Kare wa joushi kara totemo shinrai sarete imasu.","german":"He is highly trusted by his boss.","furigana":"かれ|じょうし|しんらい"},{"japanese":"一度失った信頼を取り戻すのは難しいです。","romaji":"Ichido ushinatta shinrai o torimodosu no wa muzukashii desu.","german":"It is hard to regain trust once it has been lost.","furigana":"いちどうしな|しんらい|と|もど|むずか"}],"notes":"信頼 is trust based on experience - one relies on someone. Typical: 信頼を得る \"to gain trust\", 信頼を築く \"to build\", 信頼を失う \"to lose\", 〜を信頼する, 信頼できる人, 信頼関係 \"relationship of trust\". The similar 信用 stresses credibility and creditworthiness (often in business), and 信じる is the general \"to believe\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先生を尊敬しています。","romaji":"Sensei o sonkei shite imasu.","german":"I respect my teacher.","furigana":"せんせい|そんけい"},{"japanese":"尊敬する人は父です。","romaji":"Sonkei suru hito wa chichi desu.","german":"The person I respect is my father.","furigana":"そんけい|ひと|ちち"},{"japanese":"困っている人をすぐ助けられるなんて、尊敬します。","romaji":"Komatte iru hito o sugu tasukerareru nante, sonkei shimasu.","german":"Being able to help people in need right away - I admire that.","furigana":"こま|ひと|たす|そんけい"}],"notes":"尊敬 is high regard for someone because of their character or achievements. As a transitive する-verb it mostly appears in the ている-form: 先生を尊敬している. Very typical is 尊敬する人 \"role model\", a classic question in job interviews. In grammar, 尊敬語 denotes respectful honorific language. The opposite is 軽蔑 \"contempt\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"嫉妬を感じます。","romaji":"Shitto o kanjimasu.","german":"I feel jealousy.","furigana":"しっと|かん"},{"japanese":"彼女は、彼が他の女の子と話すだけで嫉妬します。","romaji":"Kanojo wa, kare ga hoka no onna no ko to hanasu dake de shitto shimasu.","german":"She gets jealous just from him talking to other girls.","furigana":"かのじょ|かれ|ほか|おんな|こ|はな|しっと"},{"japanese":"同僚の成功に嫉妬してしまう自分が嫌です。","romaji":"Douryou no seikou ni shitto shite shimau jibun ga iya desu.","german":"I hate that I get envious of my colleague's success.","furigana":"どうりょう|せいこう|しっと|じぶん|いや"}],"notes":"嫉妬 is jealousy or resentful envy. As a する-verb the person or thing takes に: 〜に嫉妬する \"to be jealous/envious of someone\"; also 嫉妬心 and 嫉妬深い \"very jealous\". For jealousy in relationships one colloquially often says やきもちを焼く. うらやましい, by contrast, is harmless, benevolent envy (\"enviable\"); 嫉妬 sounds stronger and more negative.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"恨みを持っています。","romaji":"Urami o motte imasu.","german":"I hold a grudge.","furigana":"うら|も"},{"japanese":"彼に恨みはありませんが、今回のことは許せません。","romaji":"Kare ni urami wa arimasen ga, konkai no koto wa yurusemasen.","german":"I have nothing against him, but I cannot forgive what happened this time.","furigana":"かれ|うら|こんかい|ゆる"},{"japanese":"人の恨みを買うようなことはしないほうがいい。","romaji":"Hito no urami o kau you na koto wa shinai hou ga ii.","german":"You shouldn't do anything that would make others resent you.","furigana":"ひと|うら|か"}],"notes":"恨み is deep-seated resentment toward someone who has wronged you - longer-lasting than acute anger (怒り). Typical: 恨みを持つ／抱く \"to hold a grudge\", 〜に恨みがある, 恨みを買う \"to incur someone's resentment\", 恨みを晴らす \"to take revenge\". The related verb is 恨む. Jokingly one says 食べ物の恨みは恐ろしい: someone who takes your food will never be forgiven.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"後悔しています。","romaji":"Kōkai shite imasu.","german":"I regret it.","furigana":"こうかい"},{"japanese":"学生のころ、もっと勉強しなかったことを後悔しています。","romaji":"Gakusei no koro, motto benkyou shinakatta koto o koukai shite imasu.","german":"I regret not having studied more when I was a student.","furigana":"がくせい|べんきょう|こうかい"},{"japanese":"やらないで後悔するより、やって後悔したほうがいい。","romaji":"Yaranaide koukai suru yori, yatte koukai shita hou ga ii.","german":"It is better to do something and regret it than not to do it and regret it.","furigana":"こうかい|こうかい"}],"notes":"後悔 is regret about something one did or failed to do. As a する-verb: 〜を後悔する, often with こと: 言ったことを後悔している. In everyday speech the same feeling is expressed with 〜ばよかった (もっと勉強すればよかった). Proverb: 後悔先に立たず \"it is no use crying over spilt milk\". 後悔 does not fit regretful apologies to others - for that people say 残念です or 申し訳ありません.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"焦らないでください。","romaji":"Aseranaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't get impatient.","furigana":"あせ"},{"japanese":"試験の時間が足りなくなって、焦りました。","romaji":"Shiken no jikan ga tarinaku natte, aserimashita.","german":"I ran short of time during the exam and got flustered.","furigana":"しけん|じかん|た|あせ"},{"japanese":"電車の中で財布がないことに気づいて、本当に焦った。","romaji":"Densha no naka de saifu ga nai koto ni kizuite, hontou ni asetta.","german":"I realized on the train that my wallet was gone and I really panicked.","furigana":"でんしゃ|なか|さいふ|き|ほんとう|あせ"}],"notes":"焦る describes inner restlessness and hurry when time is short or something threatens to go wrong. It is intransitive and takes no object: 時間がなくて焦る. Very common as soothing advice: 焦らないで, 焦らなくていいよ \"no rush, take it easy\". Colloquially it can also mean a brief scare: 財布がなくて焦った. Distinction: 急ぐ is the outward act of hurrying, 焦る is the feeling; the noun is 焦り.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"怒鳴らないでください。","romaji":"Donaranaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't shout.","furigana":"どな"},{"japanese":"子どものころ、いたずらをして父に怒鳴られました。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, itazura o shite chichi ni donararemashita.","german":"As a child, I was yelled at by my father for misbehaving.","furigana":"こ|ちち|どな"},{"japanese":"店で客が店員に怒鳴っていて、びっくりしました。","romaji":"Mise de kyaku ga ten'in ni donatte ite, bikkuri shimashita.","german":"A customer was yelling at a clerk in the shop, and I was startled.","furigana":"みせ|きゃく|てんいん|どな"}],"notes":"怒鳴る means to shout at someone loudly and angrily. The person shouted at is marked with に: 部下に怒鳴る; from the victim's perspective, the passive: 父に怒鳴られた. Distinction: 叫ぶ is generally \"to scream, to cry out\" (also from fear or joy), 怒る is \"to get angry, to scold\" without necessarily being loud; 怒鳴る is always loud and aggressive. Intensified: 怒鳴りつける.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"呆れてしまいました。","romaji":"Akirete shimaimashita.","german":"I was appalled.","furigana":"あき"},{"japanese":"彼の言い訳には、呆れて何も言えませんでした。","romaji":"Kare no iiwake ni wa, akirete nani mo iemasen deshita.","german":"I was so appalled by his excuse that I could say nothing.","furigana":"かれ|い|わけ|あき|なに|い"},{"japanese":"母は散らかった部屋を見て、呆れた顔をしました。","romaji":"Haha wa chirakatta heya o mite, akireta kao o shimashita.","german":"When my mother saw the messy room, she gave me an exasperated look.","furigana":"はは|ち|へや|み|あき|かお"}],"notes":"呆れる expresses stunned amazement, almost always with criticism: one is dumbfounded, fed up, or can only shake one's head at someone's behavior. The trigger is marked with に: 彼の態度に呆れる. Typical: 呆れて物が言えない \"I am at a loss for words\", 呆れた顔 \"an exasperated look\". Often written in kana (あきれる). Unlike the neutral 驚く, 呆れる carries disapproval; do not confuse it with 飽きる \"to get tired of\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"惨めな気持ちです。","romaji":"Mijime na kimochi desu.","german":"I feel miserable.","furigana":"みじ|きも"},{"japanese":"みんなの前で失敗して、惨めな思いをしました。","romaji":"Minna no mae de shippai shite, mijime na omoi o shimashita.","german":"I failed in front of everyone and felt utterly humiliated.","furigana":"まえ|しっぱい|みじ|おも"},{"japanese":"雨の中、傘もなく一人で待っている自分が惨めに思えた。","romaji":"Ame no naka, kasa mo naku hitori de matte iru jibun ga mijime ni omoeta.","german":"Waiting alone in the rain without an umbrella, I felt pathetic.","furigana":"あめ|なか|かさ|ひとり|ま|じぶん|みじ|おも"}],"notes":"惨め is a na-adjective for a wretched, miserable state that arouses pity or shame. Typical: 惨めな気持ち, 惨めな思いをする \"to feel miserable and humiliated\", 惨めな生活, 自分が惨めになる. Often there is a sense of humiliation in front of others; 悲しい, by contrast, is simply \"sad\", and 可哀想 is \"pitiable\" from an observer's point of view. Frequently also written in kana: みじめ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"虚しい気持ちになりました。","romaji":"Munashii kimochi ni narimashita.","german":"I felt empty.","furigana":"むな|きも"},{"japanese":"一生懸命準備したのに試合が中止になって、虚しいです。","romaji":"Isshoukenmei junbi shita noni shiai ga chuushi ni natte, munashii desu.","german":"I prepared so hard, and then the match was cancelled, so it all feels pointless.","furigana":"いっしょうけんめいじゅんび|しあい|ちゅうし|むな"},{"japanese":"一日中ゲームをして過ごすと、夜に虚しくなる。","romaji":"Ichinichijuu geemu o shite sugosu to, yoru ni munashiku naru.","german":"If I spend the whole day playing games, I feel empty in the evening.","furigana":"いちにちじゅう|す|よる|むな"}],"notes":"虚しい describes a feeling of inner emptiness and meaninglessness, for example when effort was in vain or something has no real substance. Typical: 虚しい気持ち, 虚しくなる, 努力が虚しく終わる. The adverbial form 虚しく means \"in vain\": 声が虚しく響く. It is also written 空しい or in kana むなしい. Unlike 寂しい \"lonely\" (longing for people), 虚しい is about emptiness and futility.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"頼もしい人ですね。","romaji":"Tanomoshii hito desu ne.","german":"He is a reliable person.","furigana":"たの|ひと"},{"japanese":"新人なのに、何でも自分で解決できて頼もしいです。","romaji":"Shinjin na noni, nan demo jibun de kaiketsu dekite tanomoshii desu.","german":"Even though he is new, he can solve everything by himself, which is reassuring.","furigana":"しんじん|なん|じぶん|かいけつ|たの"},{"japanese":"息子が「僕が守るよ」と言って、頼もしく感じました。","romaji":"Musuko ga \"boku ga mamoru yo\" to itte, tanomoshiku kanjimashita.","german":"When my son said, \"I'll protect you,\" he seemed really strong to me.","furigana":"むすこ|ぼく|まも|い|たの|かん"}],"notes":"頼もしい describes someone you can rely on and who gives you a sense of security. It is mostly praise: 頼もしいね！, 頼もしい味方 \"a strong ally\". It is also used for younger people who grow beyond themselves: 頼もしく成長した. Almost synonymous, but more everyday, is 頼りになる; 信頼できる emphasizes trustworthiness, while 頼もしい emphasizes strength and support in difficult situations.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"誇らしい気持ちです。","romaji":"Hokorashii kimochi desu.","german":"I feel proud.","furigana":"ほこ|きも"},{"japanese":"娘が大会で優勝して、とても誇らしかったです。","romaji":"Musume ga taikai de yuushou shite, totemo hokorashikatta desu.","german":"My daughter won the tournament, and I was very proud.","furigana":"むすめ|たいかい|ゆうしょう|ほこ"},{"japanese":"彼は誇らしげに新しい車を見せてくれた。","romaji":"Kare wa hokorashige ni atarashii kuruma o misete kureta.","german":"He proudly showed me his new car.","furigana":"かれ|ほこ|あたら|くるま|み"}],"notes":"誇らしい expresses the feeling of being proud - of one's own achievement or that of people close to one. Typical: 誇らしい気持ち, 誇らしく思う, and with 〜げ: 誇らしげに \"with a proud look\". Related are 誇り \"pride\" (誇りに思う) and 誇る \"to boast of\". In present-day usage 誇らしい is positive; the meaning \"arrogant\" given in dictionaries is rare, and for that people say 偉そう.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"学問に励みます。","romaji":"Gakumon ni hagemimasu.","german":"I devote myself to my studies.","furigana":"がくもん|はげ"},{"japanese":"経済学は人の心も考える学問です。","romaji":"Keizaigaku wa hito no kokoro mo kangaeru gakumon desu.","german":"Economics is a discipline that also considers the human mind.","furigana":"けいざいがく|ひと|こころ|かんが|がくもん"},{"japanese":"大学は就職のためだけでなく、学問をする場所です。","romaji":"Daigaku wa shuushoku no tame dake de naku, gakumon o suru basho desu.","german":"University is not only for preparing for a job, but a place for scholarship.","furigana":"だいがく|しゅうしょく|がくもん|ばしょ"}],"notes":"学問 denotes serious, scholarly learning and erudition as well as an academic discipline. Typical: 学問に励む \"to devote oneself to study\", 学問の自由 \"academic freedom\", 学問的な \"scholarly\". It sounds more elevated and abstract than 勉強 (studying, e.g. for exams); 科学 means science as a method, especially the natural sciences.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"実験を行います。","romaji":"Jikken o okonaimasu.","german":"I will carry out an experiment.","furigana":"じっけん|おこな"},{"japanese":"理科の授業で、水を使った実験をしました。","romaji":"Rika no jugyou de, mizu o tsukatta jikken o shimashita.","german":"In science class we did an experiment with water.","furigana":"りか|じゅぎょう|みず|つか|じっけん"},{"japanese":"新しい薬の実験に成功したそうです。","romaji":"Atarashii kusuri no jikken ni seikou shita sou desu.","german":"I hear the experiment with the new drug was successful.","furigana":"あたら|くすり|じっけん|せいこう"}],"notes":"実験 is a scientific or practical experiment. Typical: 実験をする／行う, 〜を実験する, 実験に成功する／失敗する, 実験室 \"laboratory\", 実験結果, 動物実験. Figuratively it is also used for trial projects: 実験的な試み. Do not confuse it with 試験 \"exam, test\" (school, quality control).","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 47"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"論文を書いています。","romaji":"Ronbun o kaite imasu.","german":"I am writing a paper.","furigana":"ろんぶん|か"},{"japanese":"卒業論文のテーマがまだ決まりません。","romaji":"Sotsugyou ronbun no teema ga mada kimarimasen.","german":"I still haven't decided on the topic of my graduation thesis.","furigana":"そつぎょうろんぶん|き"},{"japanese":"先生の論文が有名な雑誌に載りました。","romaji":"Sensei no ronbun ga yuumei na zasshi ni norimashita.","german":"My professor's paper was published in a well-known journal.","furigana":"せんせい|ろんぶん|ゆうめい|ざっし|の"}],"notes":"論文 is an academic work: a journal article, graduation thesis or doctoral dissertation. Typical: 論文を書く, 論文を発表する \"to publish, to present\", 論文を提出する; 卒業論文 (short 卒論) \"graduation thesis\", 博士論文 \"doctoral dissertation\". A school essay, by contrast, is 作文, and a shorter seminar paper or assignment is レポート.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 42"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"学歴は関係ありません。","romaji":"Gakureki wa kankei arimasen.","german":"Educational background doesn't matter.","furigana":"がくれき|かんけい"},{"japanese":"履歴書に最終学歴を書いてください。","romaji":"Rirekisho ni saishuu gakureki o kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write your highest level of education on your résumé.","furigana":"りれきしょ|さいしゅうがくれき|か"},{"japanese":"日本は学歴社会だと言われることがあります。","romaji":"Nihon wa gakureki shakai da to iwareru koto ga arimasu.","german":"Japan is sometimes said to be a society that values educational credentials.","furigana":"にほん|がくれきしゃかい|い"}],"notes":"学歴 is a person's school and academic history, especially their highest qualification. Typical: 学歴が高い, 学歴社会 \"credentialist society\", 最終学歴 \"highest level of education\" (on a résumé), 学歴より実力 \"ability counts more than diplomas\". Do not confuse it with 経歴 \"career history\" and 資格 \"qualification, certificate\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"資格を取りました。","romaji":"Shikaku o torimashita.","german":"I obtained a qualification.","furigana":"しかく|と"},{"japanese":"看護師として働くには、国家資格が必要です。","romaji":"Kangoshi to shite hataraku ni wa, kokka shikaku ga hitsuyou desu.","german":"To work as a nurse, you need a national license.","furigana":"かんごし|はたら|こっかしかく|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"約束を守らない人に、文句を言う資格はない。","romaji":"Yakusoku o mamoranai hito ni, monku o iu shikaku wa nai.","german":"People who don't keep their promises have no right to complain.","furigana":"やくそく|まも|ひと|もんく|い|しかく"}],"notes":"資格 is a formal qualification or entitlement: a certificate, a license or the prerequisite for something. Typical: 資格を取る \"to obtain\", 資格がある／ない, 資格試験 \"qualification exam\", 受験資格 \"eligibility to take an exam\". Also moral: 君に文句を言う資格はない \"You have no right to complain\". Official permits such as a driver's license are called 免許 instead.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"解雇されました。","romaji":"Kaiko saremashita.","german":"I was dismissed.","furigana":"かいこ"},{"japanese":"会社は経営が悪化して、社員を百人解雇しました。","romaji":"Kaisha wa keiei ga akka shite, shain o hyakunin kaiko shimashita.","german":"As business deteriorated, the company laid off a hundred employees.","furigana":"かいしゃ|けいえい|あっか|しゃいん|ひゃくにんかいこ"},{"japanese":"理由もなく社員を解雇することはできません。","romaji":"Riyuu mo naku shain o kaiko suru koto wa dekimasen.","german":"You can't dismiss employees without a reason.","furigana":"りゆう|しゃいん|かいこ"}],"notes":"解雇 is dismissal by the employer - a formal word from contracts, news and labor law. From the viewpoint of those affected, usually in the passive: 会社に解雇される. Compounds: 不当解雇 \"unfair dismissal\", 解雇通知 \"notice of dismissal\". Colloquially people say クビになる \"to get fired\"; if you quit yourself, it is 辞める or, formally, 退職する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"給与が上がりました。","romaji":"Kyūyo ga agarimashita.","german":"My salary has gone up.","furigana":"きゅうよ|あ"},{"japanese":"給与は毎月二十五日に振り込まれます。","romaji":"Kyuuyo wa maitsuki nijuugo-nichi ni furikomaremasu.","german":"Salary is transferred on the 25th of every month.","furigana":"きゅうよ|まいつきにじゅうごにち|ふ|こ"},{"japanese":"給与明細をよく確認したほうがいいですよ。","romaji":"Kyuuyo meisai o yoku kakunin shita hou ga ii desu yo.","german":"You should check your pay slip carefully.","furigana":"きゅうよめいさい|かくにん"}],"notes":"給与 is pay for work, a formal word from contracts, pay slips and job ads; compounds include 給与明細 \"pay slip\" and 給与振込 \"salary transfer\". In conversation one usually says 給料 (給料日 \"payday\"); 月給 is monthly salary, 時給 hourly wage. The second meaning, \"granting, provision\" (as a verb 給与する), is rare.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい提案があります。","romaji":"Atarashii teian ga arimasu.","german":"I have a new proposal.","furigana":"あたら|ていあん"},{"japanese":"部長に新しい企画を提案しました。","romaji":"Buchou ni atarashii kikaku o teian shimashita.","german":"I proposed a new project to the department head.","furigana":"ぶちょう|あたら|きかく|ていあん"},{"japanese":"友達の提案で、週末にキャンプへ行くことになった。","romaji":"Tomodachi no teian de, shuumatsu ni kyanpu e iku koto ni natta.","german":"At a friend's suggestion, we're going camping on the weekend.","furigana":"ともだち|ていあん|しゅうまつ|い"}],"notes":"提案 is a proposal, especially in meetings and at work. Typical: 提案する, 提案を出す, 提案を受け入れる／断る, 人に提案する; 提案書 is a written proposal. In a meeting you announce: 一つ提案があるんですが.... For spontaneous private suggestions, 〜しようよ or 〜はどう？ usually suffices; 提案 sounds somewhat more formal.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"企画を立てます。","romaji":"Kikaku o tatemasu.","german":"I'm drawing up a plan.","furigana":"きかく|た"},{"japanese":"学園祭で何をするか、クラスで企画しています。","romaji":"Gakuensai de nani o suru ka, kurasu de kikaku shite imasu.","german":"Our class is planning what to do at the school festival.","furigana":"がくえんさい|なに|きかく"},{"japanese":"私の企画が会議で通りました。","romaji":"Watashi no kikaku ga kaigi de toorimashita.","german":"My proposal was approved at the meeting.","furigana":"わたし|きかく|かいぎ|とお"}],"notes":"企画 is the planning and conceiving of a project, event or product, and also the plan itself. Typical: 企画を立てる \"to develop a plan\", 企画書 \"project proposal\", 企画が通る \"to be approved\", 〜を企画する; 企画部 is the planning department. In contrast to 計画 (a general plan, e.g. a travel plan), 企画 stands for a creative idea, such as for an event, a show or a campaign.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大きな損失が出ました。","romaji":"Ōkina sonshitsu ga demashita.","german":"There was a big loss.","furigana":"おお|そんしつ|で"},{"japanese":"今回の事故で、会社は約一億円の損失を出しました。","romaji":"Konkai no jiko de, kaisha wa yaku ichioku-en no sonshitsu o dashimashita.","german":"Because of this accident, the company incurred a loss of about 100 million yen.","furigana":"こんかい|じこ|かいしゃ|やくいちおくえん|そんしつ|だ"},{"japanese":"あの選手のけがは、チームにとって大きな損失です。","romaji":"Ano senshu no kega wa, chiimu ni totte ookina sonshitsu desu.","german":"That player's injury is a big loss for the team.","furigana":"せんしゅ|おお|そんしつ"}],"notes":"損失 is a loss, usually financial; it is a formal word from business and the news. Typical: 損失が出る, 損失を出す, 損失を受ける, 〜円の損失. Figuratively it is also used for irreplaceable losses: チームにとって大きな損失だ. The opposite is 利益 \"profit\". In everyday speech people say the simpler 損 (損をした \"I lost out\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"銀行から融資を受けます。","romaji":"Ginkō kara yūshi o ukemasu.","german":"I'm getting a loan from the bank.","furigana":"ぎんこう|ゆうし|う"},{"japanese":"銀行は新しい工場のために三億円を融資しました。","romaji":"Ginkou wa atarashii koujou no tame ni san'oku-en o yuushi shimashita.","german":"The bank lent 300 million yen for the new factory.","furigana":"ぎんこう|あたら|こうじょう|さんおくえん|ゆうし"},{"japanese":"融資を申し込みましたが、断られました。","romaji":"Yuushi o moushikomimashita ga, kotowararemashita.","german":"I applied for a loan, but was turned down.","furigana":"ゆうし|もう|こ|ことわ"}],"notes":"融資 is financing or a loan granted by a bank or institution; it is a technical term from business and finance. Watch the perspective: the bank 融資する \"grants a loan\", the customer 融資を受ける \"receives\" or 融資を申し込む \"applies for\" it. In everyday speech people more often use ローン for loans, and 借りる／貸す for private lending; debts are called 借金.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"相続の手続きをします。","romaji":"Sōzoku no tetsuzuki o shimasu.","german":"I'm handling the inheritance procedures.","furigana":"そうぞく|てつづ"},{"japanese":"祖父の家を父が相続しました。","romaji":"Sofu no ie o chichi ga souzoku shimashita.","german":"My father inherited my grandfather's house.","furigana":"そふ|いえ|ちち|そうぞく"},{"japanese":"相続税が高くて、土地を売ることにしました。","romaji":"Souzokuzei ga takakute, tochi o uru koto ni shimashita.","german":"Because the inheritance tax was so high, we decided to sell the land.","furigana":"そうぞくぜい|たか|とち|う"}],"notes":"相続 is legal succession: inheriting assets (or debts) after a death. As a transitive する-verb: 財産を相続する, from whom with から. Compounds: 相続税 \"inheritance tax\", 相続人 \"heir\", 遺産相続 \"settlement of an estate\". For taking over tradition, a business or an office, people more often say 継ぐ／受け継ぐ (家業を継ぐ); 相続 has a legal flavor.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"素晴らしい発明です。","romaji":"Subarashii hatsumei desu.","german":"It's a wonderful invention.","furigana":"すば|はつめい"},{"japanese":"電話を発明したのはベルです。","romaji":"Denwa o hatsumei shita no wa Beru desu.","german":"It was Bell who invented the telephone.","furigana":"でんわ|はつめい"},{"japanese":"子どものころ、宿題をしてくれる機械を発明したかった。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, shukudai o shite kureru kikai o hatsumei shitakatta.","german":"As a child, I wanted to invent a machine that would do my homework for me.","furigana":"こ|しゅくだい|きかい|はつめい"}],"notes":"発明 is the invention of something new that did not exist before. As a transitive する-verb: 〜を発明する; plus 発明家 \"inventor\" and 発明品 \"invention (an object)\". The distinction from 発見 is important: one 発見する something that already existed (an element, a planet), but 発明する something newly created (the telephone). Proverb: 必要は発明の母 \"necessity is the mother of invention\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい発見がありました。","romaji":"Atarashii hakken ga arimashita.","german":"There was a new discovery.","furigana":"あたら|はっけん"},{"japanese":"北海道で恐竜の骨が発見されました。","romaji":"Hokkaidou de kyouryuu no hone ga hakken saremashita.","german":"Dinosaur bones were discovered in Hokkaido.","furigana":"ほっかいどう|きょうりゅう|ほね|はっけん"},{"japanese":"火事を早く発見したので、大きな被害はありませんでした。","romaji":"Kaji o hayaku hakken shita node, ookina higai wa arimasen deshita.","german":"Because the fire was discovered early, there was no major damage.","furigana":"かじ|はや|はっけん|おお|ひがい"}],"notes":"発見 is discovering or finding something that already existed but was unknown or hidden. As a transitive する-verb: 〜を発見する; in the news often passive: 〜が発見される. In everyday life also for small revelations: 新しい発見がある. Compared with 見つける, 発見 sounds more formal; 発明, by contrast, means inventing something new.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"産業革命が起きました。","romaji":"Sangyō kakumei ga okimashita.","german":"The Industrial Revolution took place.","furigana":"さんぎょうかくめい|お"},{"japanese":"フランス革命は1789年に始まりました。","romaji":"Furansu kakumei wa 1789-nen ni hajimarimashita.","german":"The French Revolution began in 1789.","furigana":"かくめい|ねん|はじ"},{"japanese":"スマートフォンは私たちの生活に革命を起こしました。","romaji":"Sumaatofon wa watashitachi no seikatsu ni kakumei o okoshimashita.","german":"Smartphones have revolutionized our lives.","furigana":"わたし|せいかつ|かくめい|お"}],"notes":"革命 is a revolution, either political (フランス革命) or figuratively a fundamental upheaval: 産業革命, IT革命. Typical: 革命が起きる／起こる, 〜に革命を起こす \"to revolutionize ...\", 革命的な \"revolutionary\" (also in advertising). Gradual reforms, by contrast, are 改革, and general change is 変化.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"技術が進化しています。","romaji":"Gijutsu ga shinka shite imasu.","german":"Technology is evolving.","furigana":"ぎじゅつ|しんか"},{"japanese":"鳥は恐竜から進化したと考えられています。","romaji":"Tori wa kyouryuu kara shinka shita to kangaerarete imasu.","german":"Birds are thought to have evolved from dinosaurs.","furigana":"とり|きょうりゅう|しんか|かんが"},{"japanese":"このゲームのキャラクターは、レベルが上がると進化します。","romaji":"Kono geemu no kyarakutaa wa, reberu ga agaru to shinka shimasu.","german":"The characters in this game evolve when they level up.","furigana":"あ|しんか"}],"notes":"進化 is biological evolution and, figuratively, further development toward something more advanced. As an intransitive する-verb: 〜が進化する; related is 進化論 \"theory of evolution\". Very common about technology and products: スマホは年々進化している. Distinctions: 進歩 \"progress\" (技術の進歩), 発展 \"development, growth\" (city, economy); the opposite is 退化. Do not confuse it with the identically read 深化 \"deepening\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"障子を開けました。","romaji":"Shōji o akemashita.","german":"I opened the shoji.","furigana":"しょうじ|あ"},{"japanese":"猫が障子に穴を開けてしまいました。","romaji":"Neko ga shouji ni ana o akete shimaimashita.","german":"The cat made a hole in the shoji paper.","furigana":"ねこ|しょうじ|あな|あ"},{"japanese":"年末に家族で障子を張り替えます。","romaji":"Nenmatsu ni kazoku de shouji o harikaemasu.","german":"At the end of the year, our family re-papers the shoji.","furigana":"ねんまつ|かぞく|しょうじ|は|か"}],"notes":"障子 is the traditional sliding door or window made of a fine wooden lattice covered with translucent Japanese paper that lets light in. Typical: 障子を開ける／閉める, 障子を張り替える \"to re-paper\" (often before New Year), 障子に穴を開ける (children, cats). Proverb: 壁に耳あり障子に目あり \"walls have ears\". The opaque counterpart that divides rooms is 襖.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"襖を閉めてください。","romaji":"Fusuma o shimete kudasai.","german":"Please close the fusuma.","furigana":"ふすま|し"},{"japanese":"布団は襖の奥の押し入れにしまってあります。","romaji":"Futon wa fusuma no oku no oshiire ni shimatte arimasu.","german":"The futons are stored in the closet behind the fusuma.","furigana":"ふとん|ふすま|おく|お|い"},{"japanese":"襖を外すと、二つの部屋が一つの広い部屋になります。","romaji":"Fusuma o hazusu to, futatsu no heya ga hitotsu no hiroi heya ni narimasu.","german":"If you take out the fusuma, two rooms become one large room.","furigana":"ふすま|はず|ふた|へや|ひと|ひろ|へや"}],"notes":"襖 is an opaque sliding door made of a wooden frame covered with thick paper or cloth. It separates rooms or closes off the closet (押し入れ). Typical: 襖を開ける／閉める, 襖を外す \"to take out\" (to join two rooms), 襖絵 \"paintings on sliding doors\" in temples. The kanji is rare, so it is often written in kana as ふすま. Unlike 障子, 襖 lets no light through.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"縁側でお茶を飲みます。","romaji":"Engawa de ocha o nomimasu.","german":"I drink tea on the veranda.","furigana":"えんがわ|ちゃ|の"},{"japanese":"祖母はよく縁側に座って庭を眺めていました。","romaji":"Sobo wa yoku engawa ni suwatte niwa o nagamete imashita.","german":"My grandmother often sat on the veranda looking at the garden.","furigana":"そぼ|えんがわ|すわ|にわ|なが"},{"japanese":"猫が縁側で気持ちよさそうに寝ています。","romaji":"Neko ga engawa de kimochi yosasou ni nete imasu.","german":"The cat is sleeping contentedly on the veranda.","furigana":"ねこ|えんがわ|きも|ね"}],"notes":"縁側 is the narrow, roofed wooden walkway on the outside of traditional Japanese houses, between the rooms and the garden. People sit there, drink tea, look at the garden or bask in the sun: 縁側に座る, 縁側でひなたぼっこする. The word evokes images of cozy life at the grandparents' house in the countryside. In the kitchen, えんがわ also refers to the fin-edge meat of flatfish, for example in sushi.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"廊下を走らないでください。","romaji":"Rōka o hashiranaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't run in the hallway.","furigana":"ろうか|はし"},{"japanese":"廊下に椅子が二つあります。","romaji":"Rōka ni isu ga futatsu arimasu.","german":"There are two chairs in the hallway.","furigana":"ろうか|いす|ふた"}],"notes":"廊下 is a hallway or corridor. In 廊下を走る, を marks the path traversed, not a direct object of running. 廊下に names the place where something is. 走らないでください asks you not to run there. ろうか is written rōka with a long o.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 30"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"煙突から煙が出ています。","romaji":"Entotsu kara kemuri ga dete imasu.","german":"Smoke is coming out of the chimney.","furigana":"えんとつ|けむり|で"},{"japanese":"昔の銭湯には高い煙突がありました。","romaji":"Mukashi no sentou ni wa takai entotsu ga arimashita.","german":"Public baths in the old days had tall chimneys.","furigana":"むかし|せんとう|たか|えんとつ"},{"japanese":"子どもたちは、サンタが煙突から来ると信じています。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa, Santa ga entotsu kara kuru to shinjite imasu.","german":"The children believe that Santa comes down the chimney.","furigana":"こ|えんとつ|く|しん"}],"notes":"煙突 is a chimney on houses, factories, public baths (銭湯) or ships. Typical: 煙突から煙が出る／上がる, 工場の煙突, 高い煙突. Since Japanese homes rarely have fireplaces, 煙突 is seen mainly at 銭湯 and factories. A well-known image from Christmas stories: Santa comes through the 煙突.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"塀を建てました。","romaji":"Hei o tatemashita.","german":"We built a fence.","furigana":"へい|た"},{"japanese":"猫が塀の上を歩いています。","romaji":"Neko ga hei no ue o aruite imasu.","german":"A cat is walking along the top of the wall.","furigana":"ねこ|へい|うえ|ある"},{"japanese":"地震のとき、ブロック塀の近くは危ないです。","romaji":"Jishin no toki, burokku-bei no chikaku wa abunai desu.","german":"In an earthquake, it is dangerous near concrete block walls.","furigana":"じしん|べい|ちか|あぶ"}],"notes":"塀 is a solid enclosure around a property: a wall of stone, concrete or concrete blocks, or a solid wooden fence. Typical: 塀を作る, 高い塀, 塀に囲まれた家, ブロック塀, 塀を乗り越える. Distinctions: 壁 is the wall of a building or room, 垣根 a hedge or light fence, フェンス a mesh or wire fence. Cats like to stroll along 塀の上 (the top of a wall).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"物置に道具をしまいます。","romaji":"Monooki ni dōgu o shimaimasu.","german":"I put the tools away in the shed.","furigana":"ものおき|どうぐ"},{"japanese":"庭に小さな物置を置きました。","romaji":"Niwa ni chiisana monooki o okimashita.","german":"We set up a small tool shed in the garden.","furigana":"にわ|ちい|ものおき|お"},{"japanese":"使っていない部屋を物置にしています。","romaji":"Tsukatte inai heya o monooki ni shite imasu.","german":"We use the unused room as a storeroom.","furigana":"つか|へや|ものおき"}],"notes":"物置 is a room or small shed where you keep things you do not need at the moment: tools, garden equipment, seasonal items. Typical are 物置にしまう \"to put away in the storeroom\" and 物置に入れる; a room used as a junk room is called 物置部屋. To be distinguished from 倉庫 \"warehouse, storehouse\" (larger, often commercial) and 押し入れ, the closet in a Japanese room for futons.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"築五年のマンションです。","romaji":"Chiku gonen no manshon desu.","german":"It's an apartment building five years old.","furigana":"ちくごねん"},{"japanese":"このアパートは築何年ですか。","romaji":"Kono apaato wa chiku nannen desu ka.","german":"How old is this apartment building?","furigana":"ちくなんねん"},{"japanese":"1990年築の家ですが、中はとてもきれいです。","romaji":"Sen kyuuhyaku kyuujuu nen chiku no ie desu ga, naka wa totemo kirei desu.","german":"The house was built in 1990, but it's very clean inside.","furigana":"ねんちく|いえ|なか"}],"notes":"築 appears almost only in fixed patterns about the age of a building: 築五年 \"five years old\" (before the number) or 1990年築 \"built in 1990\" (after the year). You mostly encounter it in housing listings and at viewings; the question about the building's age is 築何年ですか. Related are 築年数 \"age of the building\", 築浅 \"nearly new, only a few years old\" and 新築 \"newly built\". For people or things, one never says 築, but 〜歳 or 〜年前の.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"修理に出します。","romaji":"Shūri ni dashimasu.","german":"I'm sending it in for repair.","furigana":"しゅうり|だ"},{"japanese":"自転車のブレーキを自分で修理しました。","romaji":"Jitensha no bureeki o jibun de shuuri shimashita.","german":"I repaired the bicycle brakes myself.","furigana":"じてんしゃ|じぶん|しゅうり"},{"japanese":"エアコンの修理代は一万円ぐらいかかるそうです。","romaji":"Eakon no shuuridai wa ichiman'en gurai kakaru sou desu.","german":"The air conditioner repair is said to cost about 10,000 yen.","furigana":"しゅうりだい|いちまんえん"}],"notes":"修理 is the repair of devices, vehicles or machines; as a transitive する-verb: 車を修理する. Very common are 修理に出す \"to send in for repair\", 修理中 \"under repair\" and 修理代 \"repair costs\". In everyday speech people often simply say 直す; 修繕 is more formal and used more for buildings or clothing, and 修正 means \"correction\" of texts or data, not repair.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"工事中です。","romaji":"Kōjichū desu.","german":"Under construction.","furigana":"こうじちゅう"},{"japanese":"隣のビルの工事がうるさくて眠れません。","romaji":"Tonari no biru no kouji ga urusakute nemuremasen.","german":"The construction next door is so noisy that I can't sleep.","furigana":"となり|こうじ|ねむ"},{"japanese":"道路工事のため、この道は通れません。","romaji":"Douro kouji no tame, kono michi wa toremasen.","german":"This road is closed because of road construction.","furigana":"どうろこうじ|みち|"}],"notes":"工事 refers to construction or installation work of all kinds: 道路工事 \"road construction\", 水道工事, 配線工事. Typical are 工事中 \"under construction, due to construction work\", 工事が始まる/終わる and 工事現場 \"construction site\". Compared with 建築 (erecting buildings, also \"architecture\") and 建設 (large projects such as bridges or dams), 工事 means the actual work on site, with noise and barricades.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"建物を設計します。","romaji":"Tatemono o sekkei shimasu.","german":"I design a building.","furigana":"たてもの|せっけい"},{"japanese":"設計図を見ながら、機械を組み立てました。","romaji":"Sekkeizu o minagara, kikai o kumitatemashita.","german":"I assembled the machine while looking at the blueprint.","furigana":"せっけいず|み|きかい|く|た"},{"japanese":"結婚する前に、二人で将来の生活設計を考えました。","romaji":"Kekkon suru mae ni, futari de shourai no seikatsu sekkei o kangaemashita.","german":"Before getting married, the two of us thought about our future life plans.","furigana":"けっこん|まえ|ふたり|しょうらい|せいかつせっけい|かんが"}],"notes":"設計 is the technical planning and construction design of buildings, machines or systems; as a transitive する-verb: 家を設計する. It includes 設計図 \"blueprint, construction drawing\" and 設計士 \"designer, architect\". Unlike デザイン, which refers more to appearance, 設計 is about structure and function. Figuratively people also speak of 人生設計 or 生活設計 \"life planning\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本の建築は美しいです。","romaji":"Nihon no kenchiku wa utsukushii desu.","german":"Japanese architecture is beautiful.","furigana":"にほん|けんちく|うつく"},{"japanese":"兄は大学で建築を勉強しています。","romaji":"Ani wa daigaku de kenchiku o benkyou shite imasu.","german":"My older brother is studying architecture at university.","furigana":"あに|だいがく|けんちく|べんきょう"},{"japanese":"駅前で新しいホテルの建築が進んでいます。","romaji":"Ekimae de atarashii hoteru no kenchiku ga susunde imasu.","german":"Construction of a new hotel is progressing in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|あたら|けんちく|すす"}],"notes":"建築 means both the erecting of buildings and architecture as a field and style: 建築を学ぶ, 木造建築 \"wooden construction\", 建築家 \"architect\", 建築物 \"structure\". As a する-verb it is transitive (家を建築する) but sounds formal; in everyday speech people say 家を建てる. 建設 is used more for infrastructure such as roads or dams, 工事 for the construction work itself.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"実験器具を使います。","romaji":"Jikken kigu o tsukaimasu.","german":"I use laboratory equipment.","furigana":"じっけんきぐ|つか"},{"japanese":"一人暮らしを始めるので、調理器具をそろえました。","romaji":"Hitorigurashi o hajimeru node, chouri kigu o soroemashita.","german":"I'm starting to live alone, so I got together some cooking utensils.","furigana":"ひとりぐ|はじ|ちょうりきぐ"},{"japanese":"天井の照明器具を新しいものに替えました。","romaji":"Tenjou no shoumei kigu o atarashii mono ni kaemashita.","german":"I replaced the ceiling light with a new one.","furigana":"てんじょう|しょうめいきぐ|あたら|か"}],"notes":"器具 is a collective word for tools and instruments, mostly in compounds: 実験器具 \"laboratory equipment\", 調理器具 \"cooking utensils\", 医療器具, 照明器具 \"lighting fixtures\", 運動器具 \"exercise equipment\". On its own, it sounds somewhat matter-of-fact. 道具 is the general word for tool, 機械 is a (often motorized) machine, and 装置 is a larger technical installation.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"安全装置が作動しました。","romaji":"Anzen sōchi ga sadō shimashita.","german":"The safety device was activated.","furigana":"あんぜんそうち|さどう"},{"japanese":"この古いアパートには暖房装置がありません。","romaji":"Kono furui apaato ni wa danbou souchi ga arimasen.","german":"This old apartment has no heating system.","furigana":"ふる|だんぼうそうち"},{"japanese":"その劇は舞台装置がとてもきれいでした。","romaji":"Sono geki wa butai souchi ga totemo kirei deshita.","german":"The stage set in that play was very beautiful.","furigana":"げき|ぶたいそうち"}],"notes":"装置 is a technical device or installation that fulfills a particular function: 安全装置 \"safety device\", 暖房装置 \"heating system\", 防犯装置 \"anti-theft device\". Typical verbs are 装置が作動する \"to activate, to kick in\" and 装置を取り付ける \"to install\". In the theater, 舞台装置 means \"stage set\". 機械 stresses the machine itself, 器具 rather smaller hand tools, 設備 the entire equipment of a building.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"機械を操作します。","romaji":"Kikai o sōsa shimasu.","german":"I operate the machine.","furigana":"きかい|そうさ"},{"japanese":"父は機械に弱くて、スマホもうまく使えません。","romaji":"Chichi wa kikai ni yowakute, sumaho mo umaku tsukaemasen.","german":"My father is bad with technology and can't even use a smartphone properly.","furigana":"ちち|きかい|よわ|つか"},{"japanese":"この工場では機械が二十四時間動いています。","romaji":"Kono koujou de wa kikai ga nijuuyo jikan ugoite imasu.","german":"In this factory the machines run twenty-four hours a day.","furigana":"こうじょう|きかい|にじゅうよじかんうご"}],"notes":"機械 is a machine, especially one with a motor or mechanism: 機械を動かす/止める, 機械が故障する. Colloquially, 機械に弱い means \"to be bad with technology\" and 機械的 \"mechanical, without thinking\". Beware of the identically read 機会 \"opportunity\". For smaller devices you tend to use 器具, for household appliances 家電, and for large installations 装置.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 23"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"配線を確認します。","romaji":"Haisen o kakunin shimasu.","german":"I'll check the wiring.","furigana":"はいせん|かくにん"},{"japanese":"テレビの後ろの配線がごちゃごちゃしています。","romaji":"Terebi no ushiro no haisen ga gochagocha shite imasu.","german":"The wiring behind the TV is a tangled mess.","furigana":"うし|はいせん"},{"japanese":"古い家なので、電気の配線工事が必要です。","romaji":"Furui ie na node, denki no haisen kouji ga hitsuyou desu.","german":"Because it's an old house, electrical rewiring work is needed.","furigana":"ふる|いえ|でんき|はいせんこうじ|ひつよう"}],"notes":"配線 is electrical wiring or cable routing, in house installations as well as behind a TV or computer. Typical are 配線工事 \"electrical installation work\", 配線図 \"wiring diagram\" and 配線を確認する/つなぐ. As a する-verb (配線する) it means \"to wire\". A single cable is called コード or ケーブル; 配線 refers to the whole system.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"高速道路で行きます。","romaji":"Kōsokudōro de ikimasu.","german":"We'll go by expressway.","furigana":"こうそくどうろ|い"},{"japanese":"高速道路は料金が高いので、下の道で行きましょう。","romaji":"Kousoku douro wa ryoukin ga takai node, shita no michi de ikimashou.","german":"The expressway has expensive tolls, so let's take the regular roads.","furigana":"こうそくどうろ|りょうきん|たか|した|みち|い"},{"japanese":"次のインターで高速道路を降ります。","romaji":"Tsugi no intaa de kousoku douro o orimasu.","german":"We'll get off the expressway at the next interchange.","furigana":"つぎ|こうそくどうろ|お"}],"notes":"高速道路 is a highway or expressway; in Japan it almost always charges tolls. In everyday speech people say 高速 for short: 高速に乗る \"to get on the expressway\", 高速を降りる \"to get off\", 高速料金 \"expressway toll\". In contrast, the ordinary road is often called 下の道 or 一般道. Entrances and exits are called インター.","levelNote":"Compound: 高速 (N3) + 道路 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"指定席を予約しました。","romaji":"Shiteiseki o yoyaku shimashita.","german":"I reserved a seat.","furigana":"していせき|よやく"},{"japanese":"連休なので、新幹線の指定席はもう満席でした。","romaji":"Renkyuu na node, shinkansen no shiteiseki wa mou manseki deshita.","german":"Because of the consecutive holidays, the reserved seats on the Shinkansen were already full.","furigana":"れんきゅう|しんかんせん|していせき|まんせき"},{"japanese":"このコンサートは全席指定席です。","romaji":"Kono konsaato wa zenseki shiteiseki desu.","german":"All seats at this concert are reserved.","furigana":"ぜんせきしていせき"}],"notes":"指定席 is a reserved seat, above all on the Shinkansen and long-distance trains, but also at concerts and sports events. One says 指定席を取る or 指定席を予約する; the ticket is called 指定席券. The counterpart is 自由席 \"non-reserved seat\". 全席指定 means \"all seats reserved / only with a seat ticket\".","levelNote":"Compound: 指定 (N2) + 席 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自由席に座ります。","romaji":"Jiyūseki ni suwarimasu.","german":"I'll sit in an unreserved seat.","furigana":"じゆうせき|すわ"},{"japanese":"自由席は一号車から三号車です。","romaji":"Jiyuuseki wa ichigousha kara sangousha desu.","german":"The unreserved cars are cars 1 to 3.","furigana":"じゆうせき|いちごうしゃ|さんごうしゃ"},{"japanese":"自由席は混んでいて、東京までずっと立っていました。","romaji":"Jiyuuseki wa konde ite, Toukyou made zutto tatte imashita.","german":"The unreserved section was crowded, and I had to stand all the way to Tokyo.","furigana":"じゆうせき|こ|とうきょう|た"}],"notes":"自由席 is an unreserved seat, especially on the Shinkansen: in the 自由席 cars (自由席車両) you may sit anywhere you like, but have no guarantee of a seat. Typical are 自由席に座る, 自由席で行く and announcements like 自由席は一号車から三号車です. The counterpart is 指定席. Caution: \"a free seat\" in the sense of \"unoccupied\" is 空いている席 or 空席, not 自由席.","levelNote":"Compound: 自由 (N4) + 席 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"運賃はいくらですか。","romaji":"Unchin wa ikura desu ka.","german":"How much is the fare?","furigana":"うんちん"},{"japanese":"来月から電車の運賃が上がるそうです。","romaji":"Raigetsu kara densha no unchin ga agaru sou desu.","german":"I hear train fares will go up from next month.","furigana":"らいげつ|でんしゃ|うんちん|あ"},{"japanese":"子供の運賃は大人の半額です。","romaji":"Kodomo no unchin wa otona no hangaku desu.","german":"A child's fare is half the adult fare.","furigana":"こども|うんちん|おとな|はんがく"}],"notes":"運賃 is the fare in public transport (train, bus, taxi, plane) and, in freight transport, the shipping cost. Typical are 運賃を払う, 運賃が上がる \"fares rise\" and 片道運賃 \"one-way fare\". 料金 is more general \"fee, price\" (also for electricity or admission), and 交通費 is the total \"travel expenses\", such as those reimbursed by an employer.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"乗客が降ります。","romaji":"Jōkyaku ga orimasu.","german":"The passengers get off.","furigana":"じょうきゃく|お"},{"japanese":"その飛行機には二百人の乗客が乗っていました。","romaji":"Sono hikouki ni wa nihyakunin no joukyaku ga notte imashita.","german":"There were two hundred passengers on that plane.","furigana":"ひこうき|にひゃくにん|じょうきゃく|の"},{"japanese":"朝の電車はいつも乗客でいっぱいです。","romaji":"Asa no densha wa itsumo joukyaku de ippai desu.","german":"Morning trains are always full of passengers.","furigana":"あさ|でんしゃ|じょうきゃく"}],"notes":"乗客 are the passengers on a train, bus, plane or ship. The word is objective and typical of news and announcements: 乗客を乗せる \"to pick up passengers\", 乗客が乗る/降りる, 乗客数 \"number of passengers\". Staff address passengers directly as お客様. 旅客 is even more formal and appears in compounds such as 旅客機 \"passenger aircraft\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"電車が遅延しています。","romaji":"Densha ga chien shite imasu.","german":"The train is delayed.","furigana":"でんしゃ|ちえん"},{"japanese":"会社に出すために、駅で遅延証明書をもらいました。","romaji":"Kaisha ni dasu tame ni, eki de chien shoumeisho o moraimashita.","german":"For my company, I got a delay certificate at the station.","furigana":"かいしゃ|だ|えき|ちえんしょうめいしょ"},{"japanese":"大雪のため、多くの便に遅延が出ています。","romaji":"Ooyuki no tame, ooku no bin ni chien ga dete imasu.","german":"Because of heavy snow, many flights are delayed.","furigana":"おおゆき|おお|びん|ちえん|で"}],"notes":"遅延 is the formal word for \"delay\" and typical of train announcements, airport boards and official letters; as an intransitive する-verb: 電車が遅延する. In everyday life, 遅延証明書, the delay certificate you present at work, is important. In conversation people usually say 遅れ or 遅れる: 電車が十分遅れた. In IT, 遅延 also means \"latency\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人身事故で電車が止まっています。","romaji":"Jinshinjiko de densha ga tomatte imasu.","german":"Trains are stopped because of a passenger accident.","furigana":"じんしんじこ|でんしゃ|と"},{"japanese":"人身事故の影響で、電車が三十分遅れています。","romaji":"Jinshin jiko no eikyou de, densha ga sanjuppun okurete imasu.","german":"Because of a passenger accident, trains are running thirty minutes late.","furigana":"じんしんじこ|えいきょう|でんしゃ|さんじゅっぷんおく"},{"japanese":"交差点で車と自転車の人身事故がありました。","romaji":"Kousaten de kuruma to jitensha no jinshin jiko ga arimashita.","german":"There was an accident involving a car and a bicycle at the intersection, with injuries.","furigana":"こうさてん|くるま|じてんしゃ|じんしんじこ"}],"notes":"人身事故 is an accident involving personal injury (injured or dead), as opposed to 物損事故 \"accident with property damage only\". In road traffic it is a legal term; on the railways it is heard constantly in announcements like 人身事故の影響で..., often with a suicide behind it. Typical are 人身事故が起きる/発生する and 人身事故で電車が止まる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"運転見合わせが続いています。","romaji":"Untenmiawase ga tsuzuite imasu.","german":"The suspension of service continues.","furigana":"うんてんみあ|つづ"},{"japanese":"台風の影響で、新幹線が運転見合わせになりました。","romaji":"Taifuu no eikyou de, shinkansen ga unten miawase ni narimashita.","german":"Because of the typhoon, Shinkansen service has been suspended.","furigana":"たいふう|えいきょう|しんかんせん|うんてんみあ"},{"japanese":"運転見合わせの間、駅で一時間も待ちました。","romaji":"Unten miawase no aida, eki de ichijikan mo machimashita.","german":"During the suspension of service, I waited at the station for a whole hour.","furigana":"うんてんみあ|あいだ|えき|いちじかん|ま"}],"notes":"運転見合わせ is the temporary suspension of train or bus service, for example because of an accident, storm or earthquake; it comes from 運転を見合わせる \"to hold off operation for the time being\". Typical in announcements and on display boards: 運転見合わせ中, 〜線は運転見合わせとなっています. Unlike 運休 \"cancellation (of a train)\", it stresses that service will resume later (運転再開).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"振替輸送を利用します。","romaji":"Furikaeyusō o riyō shimasu.","german":"I use the substitute transport.","furigana":"ふりかえゆそう|りよう"},{"japanese":"ただいま、地下鉄で振替輸送を行っています。","romaji":"Tadaima, chikatetsu de furikae yusou o okonatte imasu.","german":"Substitute transport is currently being offered on the subway.","furigana":"ちかてつ|ふりかえゆそう|おこな"},{"japanese":"振替輸送を使うときは、改札で切符を見せてください。","romaji":"Furikae yusou o tsukau toki wa, kaisatsu de kippu o misete kudasai.","german":"When you use substitute transport, please show your ticket at the ticket gate.","furigana":"ふりかえゆそう|つか|かいさつ|きっぷ|み"}],"notes":"振替輸送 is substitute transport during a service disruption: your own ticket is then also valid on other lines or companies, at no extra charge. Typical phrases are 振替輸送を行う \"to offer substitute transport\" (railway company) and 振替輸送を利用する \"to use it\" (passenger); at the counter you often get a 振替乗車票 for it. You hear the word almost only in train announcements, together with 運転見合わせ or 人身事故.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"車掌に切符を見せます。","romaji":"Shashō ni kippu o misemasu.","german":"I show my ticket to the conductor.","furigana":"しゃしょう|きっぷ|み"},{"japanese":"車掌さんのアナウンスで、次の駅の名前がわかりました。","romaji":"Shashou-san no anaunsu de, tsugi no eki no namae ga wakarimashita.","german":"From the conductor's announcement, I learned the name of the next station.","furigana":"しゃしょう|つぎ|えき|なまえ"},{"japanese":"電車に傘を忘れたので、車掌さんに聞いてみました。","romaji":"Densha ni kasa o wasureta node, shashou-san ni kiite mimashita.","german":"I had left my umbrella on the train, so I asked the conductor.","furigana":"でんしゃ|かさ|わす|しゃしょう|き"}],"notes":"車掌 is the train conductor who operates the doors, makes announcements and checks tickets; politely one says 車掌さん. The train driver, by contrast, is 運転士, and station staff are 駅員. On many Japanese local trains there is no 車掌 any more (ワンマン運転), but on the Shinkansen there is.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"飛行機を操縦します。","romaji":"Hikōki o sōjū shimasu.","german":"I pilot the airplane.","furigana":"ひこうき|そうじゅう"},{"japanese":"ドローンの操縦は思ったより難しいです。","romaji":"Doroon no soujuu wa omotta yori muzukashii desu.","german":"Flying a drone is harder than I thought.","furigana":"そうじゅう|おも|むずか"},{"japanese":"彼女は夫をうまく操縦しているね。","romaji":"Kanojo wa otto o umaku soujuu shite iru ne.","german":"She really has her husband wrapped around her finger, doesn't she?","furigana":"かのじょ|おっと|そうじゅう"}],"notes":"操縦 is the operation of vehicles that require special skills: airplane, ship, helicopter, drone, robot. As a transitive する-verb: 飛行機を操縦する; the pilot is called 操縦士, the cockpit 操縦席. For a car or train you say 運転する, for operating devices 操作する. Figuratively, 操縦 also means to steer someone skillfully: 夫をうまく操縦する.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"車が衝突しました。","romaji":"Kuruma ga shōtotsu shimashita.","german":"The cars collided.","furigana":"くるま|しょうとつ"},{"japanese":"自転車が電柱に衝突して、乗っていた人がけがをしました。","romaji":"Jitensha ga denchuu ni shoutotsu shite, notte ita hito ga kega o shimashita.","german":"A bicycle hit a utility pole, and the rider was injured.","furigana":"じてんしゃ|でんちゅう|しょうとつ|の|ひと"},{"japanese":"会議で部長と意見が衝突してしまいました。","romaji":"Kaigi de buchou to iken ga shoutotsu shite shimaimashita.","german":"I ended up clashing with my department head over an opinion in the meeting.","furigana":"かいぎ|ぶちょう|いけん|しょうとつ"}],"notes":"衝突 is a violent collision of vehicles or objects; as an intransitive する-verb: AとBが衝突する or AがBに衝突する. Common are 正面衝突 \"head-on collision\" and 衝突事故. Figuratively it means \"conflict, quarrel\": 意見が衝突する. In everyday speech, ぶつかる is used for lighter cases; 衝突 sounds like the news or a serious matter.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"救急車を呼んでください。","romaji":"Kyūkyūsha o yonde kudasai.","german":"Please call an ambulance.","furigana":"きゅうきゅうしゃ|よ"},{"japanese":"祖母は救急車で病院に運ばれました。","romaji":"Sobo wa kyuukyuusha de byouin ni hakobaremashita.","german":"My grandmother was taken to the hospital by ambulance.","furigana":"そぼ|きゅうきゅうしゃ|びょういん|はこ"},{"japanese":"救急車が通るので、車を道の端に止めました。","romaji":"Kyuukyuusha ga tooru node, kuruma o michi no hashi ni tomemashita.","german":"An ambulance was coming through, so I pulled my car over to the side of the road.","furigana":"きゅうきゅうしゃ|とお|くるま|みち|はし|と"}],"notes":"救急車 is the ambulance; in Japan it is called on the number 119. Typical are 救急車を呼ぶ, 救急車で運ばれる \"to be taken by ambulance\" and 救急車が来る. The emergency medical team is 救急隊, the emergency room 救急外来. Do not confuse it with 消防車 \"fire engine\", which also comes via 119.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 47"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"消防車が来ました。","romaji":"Shōbōsha ga kimashita.","german":"The fire engine has arrived.","furigana":"しょうぼうしゃ|き"},{"japanese":"近所で火事があって、消防車が何台も来ました。","romaji":"Kinjo de kaji ga atte, shoubousha ga nandai mo kimashita.","german":"There was a fire in the neighborhood, and several fire engines came.","furigana":"きんじょ|かじ|しょうぼうしゃ|なんだい|き"},{"japanese":"息子は消防車のおもちゃが大好きです。","romaji":"Musuko wa shoubousha no omocha ga daisuki desu.","german":"My son loves his toy fire engine.","furigana":"むすこ|しょうぼうしゃ|だいす"}],"notes":"消防車 is the fire engine; the fire station is called 消防署, the firefighter 消防士. Typical phrases are 消防車が来る/出動する \"to turn out\" and 消防車のサイレン. In Japan you call the fire department at 119, the same number as for an ambulance. The fire itself is 火事, and \"to extinguish\" is 消す or, formally, 消火する.","levelNote":"Compound: 消防 (N3) + 車 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"警察に通報しました。","romaji":"Keisatsu ni tsūhō shimashita.","german":"I notified the police.","furigana":"けいさつ|つうほう"},{"japanese":"通報を受けて、警察がすぐに来ました。","romaji":"Tsuuhou o ukete, keisatsu ga sugu ni kimashita.","german":"The police came immediately after the report.","furigana":"つうほう|う|けいさつ|き"},{"japanese":"怪しい荷物を見つけたら、すぐに駅員に通報してください。","romaji":"Ayashii nimotsu o mitsuketara, sugu ni ekiin ni tsuuhou shite kudasai.","german":"If you find a suspicious piece of luggage, please report it to the station staff immediately.","furigana":"あや|にもつ|み|えきいん|つうほう"}],"notes":"通報 is a report to authorities such as the police or fire department, often in emergencies or cases of suspicion: 警察に通報する, 110番通報, 消防に通報する. The authority 通報を受ける \"receives the report\"; the person who reports is the 通報者. Unlike 報告 (a report, e.g. to a superior) and 連絡 (letting someone know in general), 通報 always goes to a competent body. A formal criminal complaint, on the other hand, is 被害届 or 告訴.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"事故を目撃しました。","romaji":"Jiko o mokugeki shimashita.","german":"I witnessed the accident.","furigana":"じこ|もくげき"},{"japanese":"警察は事件の目撃者を探しています。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa jiken no mokugekisha o sagashite imasu.","german":"The police are looking for witnesses to the incident.","furigana":"けいさつ|じけん|もくげきしゃ|さが"},{"japanese":"この近くの山でクマが目撃されたそうです。","romaji":"Kono chikaku no yama de kuma ga mokugeki sareta sou desu.","german":"A bear has reportedly been sighted in the mountains near here.","furigana":"ちか|やま|もくげき"}],"notes":"目撃 means to see an event by chance with one's own eyes, usually an accident, a crime or something unusual; as a transitive する-verb: 事故を目撃する. Very common are 目撃者 \"eyewitness\" and 目撃情報 \"witness reports, sighting information\". In the passive it describes sightings: クマが目撃された. For ordinary seeing or deliberate observing, use 見る or 観察する respectively.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"応急処置をしました。","romaji":"Ōkyūshochi o shimashita.","german":"I gave first aid.","furigana":"おうきゅうしょち"},{"japanese":"救急車が来るまで、駅員さんが応急処置をしてくれました。","romaji":"Kyuukyuusha ga kuru made, ekiin-san ga oukyuu shochi o shite kuremashita.","german":"A station employee gave first aid until the ambulance arrived.","furigana":"きゅうきゅうしゃ|く|えきいん|おうきゅうしょち"},{"japanese":"応急処置として、割れた窓にテープを貼りました。","romaji":"Oukyuu shochi to shite, wareta mado ni teepu o harimashita.","german":"As a stopgap, I taped up the broken window.","furigana":"おうきゅうしょち|わ|まど|は"}],"notes":"応急処置 is an emergency measure taken before the actual treatment or repair: for injured people \"first aid\", for objects a \"temporary repair\". People say 応急処置をする, 応急処置を受ける and 応急処置として… \"as a stopgap\". For first aid given to people 応急手当 is also common; a first-aid course is called 救命講習.","levelNote":"Compound: 応急 (N1) + 処置 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"包帯を巻きます。","romaji":"Hōtai o makimasu.","german":"I put on a bandage.","furigana":"ほうたい|ま"},{"japanese":"傷口が汚れないように、毎日包帯を替えてください。","romaji":"Kizuguchi ga yogorenai you ni, mainichi houtai o kaete kudasai.","german":"Please change the bandage every day so that the wound stays clean.","furigana":"きずぐち|よご|まいにちほうたい|か"},{"japanese":"包帯が取れるまで、二週間かかりました。","romaji":"Houtai ga toreru made, nishuukan kakarimashita.","german":"It took two weeks until the bandage came off.","furigana":"ほうたい|と|にしゅうかん"}],"notes":"包帯 is a bandage made of gauze; the typical verb is 巻く: 包帯を巻く \"to apply a bandage\", also 包帯を替える \"to change\" and 包帯を取る \"to take off\". A small adhesive plaster, by contrast, is 絆創膏 (mostly written ばんそうこう), and a gauze pad is ガーゼ. The older spelling 繃帯 is still occasionally found.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一週間入院しました。","romaji":"Isshūkan nyūin shimashita.","german":"I was in the hospital for a week.","furigana":"いっしゅうかんにゅういん"},{"japanese":"祖母は今、入院中です。","romaji":"Sobo wa ima, nyuuinchuu desu.","german":"My grandmother is in the hospital right now.","furigana":"そぼ|いま|にゅういんちゅう"},{"japanese":"足の手術のために入院することになりました。","romaji":"Ashi no shujutsu no tame ni nyuuin suru koto ni narimashita.","german":"I have to be hospitalized for an operation on my leg.","furigana":"あし|しゅじゅつ|にゅういん"}],"notes":"入院 is admission to or a stay in the hospital; as an intransitive する-verb one says 病院に入院する or simply 入院する. That someone is currently in the hospital is expressed with 入院している or 入院中. When a doctor sends someone to the hospital, it is 入院させる. The opposite is 退院 \"discharge\"; someone who only goes for outpatient treatment does 通院.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"来週退院します。","romaji":"Raishū taiin shimasu.","german":"I will be discharged next week.","furigana":"らいしゅうたいいん"},{"japanese":"父は昨日無事に退院しました。","romaji":"Chichi wa kinou buji ni taiin shimashita.","german":"My father was discharged from the hospital safely yesterday.","furigana":"ちち|きのうぶじ|たいいん"},{"japanese":"退院したら、一緒に旅行に行こう。","romaji":"Taiin shitara, issho ni ryokou ni ikou.","german":"When you get out of the hospital, let's go on a trip together.","furigana":"たいいん|いっしょ|りょこう|い"}],"notes":"退院 is discharge from the hospital; as an intransitive する-verb: 退院する, with the hospital as the starting point marked by を: 病院を退院する. It is the counterpart of 入院. Typical expressions are 無事に退院する \"to be discharged safely\" and the congratulation 退院おめでとう(ございます). For leaving a school or company, other words are used (卒業, 退職); 退院 applies only to hospitals.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"毎週通院しています。","romaji":"Maishū tsūin shite imasu.","german":"I go to the doctor every week as an outpatient.","furigana":"まいしゅうつういん"},{"japanese":"退院した後も、月に一回通院しています。","romaji":"Taiin shita ato mo, tsuki ni ikkai tsuuin shite imasu.","german":"Even after being discharged, I go to the hospital once a month.","furigana":"たいいん|あと|つき|いっかいつういん"},{"japanese":"通院のため、毎週水曜日は早く帰ります。","romaji":"Tsuuin no tame, maishuu suiyoubi wa hayaku kaerimasu.","german":"Because of my hospital visits, I go home early every Wednesday.","furigana":"つういん|まいしゅうすいようび|はや|かえ"}],"notes":"通院 means to go to a hospital or clinic regularly for treatment, i.e. to be treated as an outpatient; as an intransitive する-verb with に: 病院に通院する, often simply 通院している. Its counterpart is 入院 \"to be admitted to hospital\", followed by 退院. Common are 通院中 \"under medical treatment\" and 週に一回通院する. For a single doctor's visit, one says 病院に行く or 診察を受ける instead.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"処方箋をもらいました。","romaji":"Shohōsen o moraimashita.","german":"I got a prescription.","furigana":"しょほうせん"},{"japanese":"病院の帰りに、処方箋を薬局に持って行きました。","romaji":"Byouin no kaeri ni, shohousen o yakkyoku ni motte ikimashita.","german":"On the way home from the hospital, I took the prescription to the pharmacy.","furigana":"びょういん|かえ|しょほうせん|やっきょく|も|い"},{"japanese":"この薬は処方箋がないと買えません。","romaji":"Kono kusuri wa shohousen ga nai to kaemasen.","german":"You can't buy this medicine without a prescription.","furigana":"くすり|しょほうせん|か"}],"notes":"処方箋 is the doctor's prescription. In Japan the clinic issues it (処方箋を出す), and you bring it to the pharmacy (薬局に処方箋を出す/持って行く); pharmacies that fill prescriptions advertise 処方箋受付. Prescription-only medicines can only be bought 処方箋があれば. A cooking recipe, by contrast, is レシピ. Figuratively, 処方箋 is also used for \"cure-all, solution\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"副作用がありますか。","romaji":"Fukusayō ga arimasu ka.","german":"Are there any side effects?","furigana":"ふくさよう"},{"japanese":"この薬は副作用で眠くなることがあります。","romaji":"Kono kusuri wa fukusayou de nemuku naru koto ga arimasu.","german":"This medicine can cause drowsiness as a side effect.","furigana":"くすり|ふくさよう|ねむ"},{"japanese":"副作用が出たら、すぐに医者に相談してください。","romaji":"Fukusayou ga detara, sugu ni isha ni soudan shite kudasai.","german":"If side effects occur, please consult your doctor immediately.","furigana":"ふくさよう|で|いしゃ|そうだん"}],"notes":"副作用 is the side effect of a medicine or vaccination. Typical phrases are 副作用がある, 副作用が出る \"side effects appear\" and 〜の副作用で眠くなる. Figuratively, it also refers to negative side effects of a measure. Its counterpart, the desired effect, is 効果 or 効き目.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"花粉アレルギーがあります。","romaji":"Kafun arerugī ga arimasu.","german":"I have a pollen allergy.","furigana":"かふん"},{"japanese":"すみません、私はエビにアレルギーがあるんです。","romaji":"Sumimasen, watashi wa ebi ni arerugii ga aru n desu.","german":"Excuse me, I'm allergic to shrimp.","furigana":"わたし"},{"japanese":"弟は数学アレルギーで、数字を見るだけで嫌になるそうです。","romaji":"Otouto wa suugaku arerugii de, suuji o miru dake de iya ni naru sou desu.","german":"My younger brother is allergic to math, apparently; just seeing numbers puts him off.","furigana":"おとうと|すうがく|すうじ|み|いや"}],"notes":"アレルギー is an allergy, usually with the trigger in front: 花粉アレルギー, 卵アレルギー, 猫アレルギー. You say 〜アレルギーがある or 〜にアレルギーがある; the reaction is アレルギー反応, an allergic predisposition アレルギー体質. Colloquially it also stands for a strong aversion: 数学アレルギー. Hay fever is usually just called 花粉症.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ワクチンを接種しました。","romaji":"Wakuchin o sesshu shimashita.","german":"I got vaccinated.","furigana":"せっしゅ"},{"japanese":"子供にインフルエンザの予防接種を受けさせました。","romaji":"Kodomo ni infuruenza no yobou sesshu o ukesasemashita.","german":"I had my child vaccinated against the flu.","furigana":"こども|よぼうせっしゅ|う"},{"japanese":"二回目の接種は来月の予定です。","romaji":"Nikaime no sesshu wa raigetsu no yotei desu.","german":"The second shot is scheduled for next month.","furigana":"にかいめ|せっしゅ|らいげつ|よてい"}],"notes":"接種 is the administering of a vaccine; as a transitive する-verb: ワクチンを接種する. The everyday word for \"vaccination\" is 予防接種 (予防接種を受ける), colloquially also ワクチンを打つ. On its own, 接種 appears more in official texts and the news, e.g. 接種券 \"vaccination voucher\" or 二回目の接種 \"second dose\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"健康診断を受けます。","romaji":"Kenkō shindan o ukemasu.","german":"I will get a health checkup.","furigana":"けんこうしんだん|う"},{"japanese":"うちの会社では、年に一回健康診断があります。","romaji":"Uchi no kaisha de wa, nen ni ikkai kenkou shindan ga arimasu.","german":"At my company, there is a health checkup once a year.","furigana":"かいしゃ|ねん|いっかいけんこうしんだん"},{"japanese":"健康診断の結果、血圧が少し高いと言われました。","romaji":"Kenkou shindan no kekka, ketsuatsu ga sukoshi takai to iwaremashita.","german":"At the health checkup, I was told that my blood pressure is a little high.","furigana":"けんこうしんだん|けっか|けつあつ|すこ|たか|い"}],"notes":"健康診断 is the general health examination, which in Japan is usually organized annually by the employer for employees. Typical is 健康診断を受ける; abbreviated, one often says 健診, and the more comprehensive version is called 人間ドック. Results are in the 健康診断の結果, for example for 血圧 or 体重. An examination because of specific complaints is 診察.","levelNote":"Compound: 健康 (N4) + 診断 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"脈を測ります。","romaji":"Myaku o hakarimasu.","german":"I'm taking my pulse.","furigana":"みゃく|はか"},{"japanese":"面接の前は緊張して、脈が速くなりました。","romaji":"Mensetsu no mae wa kinchou shite, myaku ga hayaku narimashita.","german":"I was so nervous before the interview that my pulse sped up.","furigana":"めんせつ|まえ|きんちょう|みゃく|はや"},{"japanese":"毎日メッセージをくれるなら、脈があるかもしれないよ。","romaji":"Mainichi messeeji o kureru nara, myaku ga aru kamoshirenai yo.","german":"If she messages you every day, you might have a chance.","furigana":"まいにち|みゃく"}],"notes":"脈 is mainly the pulse: 脈を測る \"to measure the pulse\", 脈を取る \"to take the pulse\", 脈が速い/遅い. Colloquially, 脈がある means \"there is hope\", especially in love (\"he/she is interested\"), and 脈がない means the opposite. In compounds 脈 stands for veins and chains: 動脈 \"artery\", 山脈 \"mountain range\". 脈拍 is the more technical word for the pulse rate.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"足を骨折しました。","romaji":"Ashi o kossetsu shimashita.","german":"I broke my leg.","furigana":"あし|こっせつ"},{"japanese":"スキーで転んで、腕を骨折してしまいました。","romaji":"Sukii de koronde, ude o kossetsu shite shimaimashita.","german":"I fell while skiing and broke my arm.","furigana":"ころ|うで|こっせつ"},{"japanese":"骨折が完全に治るまで、三か月かかりました。","romaji":"Kossetsu ga kanzen ni naoru made, sankagetsu kakarimashita.","german":"It took three months for the fracture to heal completely.","furigana":"こっせつ|かんぜん|なお|さん|げつ"}],"notes":"骨折 is a bone fracture. As a する-verb, the body part takes を: 足を骨折する \"to break one's leg\", 腕を骨折した. Synonymous but more everyday is 骨が折れる \"a bone breaks\"; careful, 骨が折れる also means \"to be laborious\" figuratively. Also typical are 骨折が治る and ギプス \"cast\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"火傷をしました。","romaji":"Yakedo o shimashita.","german":"I got burned.","furigana":"やけど"},{"japanese":"お湯で手にやけどをしてしまいました。","romaji":"Oyu de te ni yakedo o shite shimaimashita.","german":"I burned my hand with hot water.","furigana":"ゆ|て"},{"japanese":"株に手を出して、大やけどをしました。","romaji":"Kabu ni te o dashite, ooyakedo o shimashita.","german":"I got into stocks and got badly burned.","furigana":"かぶ|て|だ|おお"}],"notes":"やけど is a burn or scald; it is mostly written in kana, and the kanji 火傷 is seen more in texts. Typical are やけどをする, 手にやけどをする and 舌をやけどする \"to burn one's tongue\". Figuratively, やけど(を)する means \"to burn one's fingers\", for example with stocks or risky relationships; 大やけど is a severe burn or a big loss.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"傷が治りました。","romaji":"Kizu ga naorimashita.","german":"The wound has healed.","furigana":"きず|なお"},{"japanese":"新しい車にもう傷がついてしまいました。","romaji":"Atarashii kuruma ni mou kizu ga tsuite shimaimashita.","german":"The new car already has a scratch.","furigana":"あたら|くるま|きず"},{"japanese":"子供の頃の心の傷は、なかなか消えません。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro no kokoro no kizu wa, nakanaka kiemasen.","german":"Emotional wounds from childhood do not disappear easily.","furigana":"こども|ころ|こころ|きず|き"}],"notes":"傷 is a wound, cut or scrape, but also a scratch on objects and, figuratively, emotional pain. Typical are 傷が治る, 傷が残る \"a scar remains\", 傷がつく \"to get scratched\" and 心の傷. Related verbs: 傷つく \"to get hurt\" and 傷つける \"to hurt\". けが refers to the accident or injury as a whole, 傷 to the concrete spot.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 40"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"足が腫れています。","romaji":"Ashi ga harete imasu.","german":"My foot is swollen.","furigana":"あし|は"},{"japanese":"蚊に刺されて、腕が赤く腫れました。","romaji":"Ka ni sasarete, ude ga akaku haremashita.","german":"I was bitten by a mosquito and my arm swelled up red.","furigana":"か|さ|うで|あか|は"},{"japanese":"昨日泣きすぎて、目が腫れてしまった。","romaji":"Kinou nakisugite, me ga harete shimatta.","german":"I cried too much yesterday and my eyes got puffy.","furigana":"きのうな|め|は"}],"notes":"腫れる is intransitive: a body part swells, usually from inflammation, a blow or an insect bite: 足が腫れる, 目が腫れる. It is often used in the form 腫れている \"is swollen\" and with 赤く: 赤く腫れる. The noun 腫れ appears in 腫れが引く \"the swelling goes down\". Do not confuse it with the identically read 晴れる \"to clear up (weather)\". A transitive counterpart exists only in expressions like 目を泣き腫らす \"to cry one's eyes red\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"足が痺れました。","romaji":"Ashi ga shibiremashita.","german":"My foot has gone numb.","furigana":"あし|しび"},{"japanese":"寒さで指先がしびれて、うまく字が書けません。","romaji":"Samusa de yubisaki ga shibirete, umaku ji ga kakemasen.","german":"My fingertips are numb from the cold, and I can't write well.","furigana":"さむ|ゆびさき|じ|か"},{"japanese":"あのギタリストの演奏には本当にしびれたよ。","romaji":"Ano gitarisuto no ensou ni wa hontou ni shibireta yo.","german":"That guitarist's performance really blew me away.","furigana":"えんそう|ほんとう"}],"notes":"しびれる is intransitive and mostly written in kana. The basic meaning is \"to go numb, to fall asleep\" of limbs, typically after sitting a long time in seiza: 正座で足がしびれる. It also describes tingling from electricity or cold (指先がしびれる). Colloquially, 〜にしびれる means \"to be captivated by something\": あの演奏にはしびれた. The noun しびれ appears in しびれが取れる \"the numbness goes away\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"眩暈がします。","romaji":"Memai ga shimasu.","german":"I feel dizzy.","furigana":"めまい"},{"japanese":"暑さでめまいを起こして、倒れてしまいました。","romaji":"Atsusa de memai o okoshite, taorete shimaimashita.","german":"I got dizzy from the heat and collapsed.","furigana":"あつ|お|たお"},{"japanese":"朝からめまいがひどいので、今日は会社を休みます。","romaji":"Asa kara memai ga hidoi node, kyou wa kaisha o yasumimasu.","german":"I've had severe dizziness since this morning, so I'm taking the day off work.","furigana":"あさ|きょう|かいしゃ|やす"}],"notes":"めまい is dizziness; mostly in kana, since the kanji 眩暈 (also 目眩) are hard and rather literary. The fixed pattern is めまいがする \"I feel dizzy\"; for severe dizziness one says めまいを起こす. The brief dizziness when standing up quickly is 立ちくらみ. For nausea, which often comes with it, 吐き気 is used.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"吐き気がします。","romaji":"Hakike ga shimasu.","german":"I feel nauseous.","furigana":"は|け"},{"japanese":"山道でバスに酔って、吐き気がしてきました。","romaji":"Yamamichi de basu ni yotte, hakike ga shite kimashita.","german":"I got carsick on the mountain road and started to feel nauseous.","furigana":"やまみち|よ|は|け"},{"japanese":"病院で吐き気止めの薬をもらいました。","romaji":"Byouin de hakikedome no kusuri o moraimashita.","german":"I got anti-nausea medicine at the hospital.","furigana":"びょういん|は|けど|くすり"}],"notes":"吐き気 is nausea, literally \"the feeling of having to vomit\". The standard pattern is 吐き気がする; formally it is 吐き気を催す. A remedy against it is 吐き気止め. In conversation, people often simply say 気持ち悪い, which can also mean \"disgusting\". For motion sickness, one hears 酔う: バスに酔う.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"下痢が続いています。","romaji":"Geri ga tsuzuite imasu.","german":"The diarrhea is continuing.","furigana":"げり|つづ"},{"japanese":"旅行先で水を飲んで、下痢をしてしまいました。","romaji":"Ryokousaki de mizu o nonde, geri o shite shimaimashita.","german":"I drank the water on my trip and got diarrhea.","furigana":"りょこうさき|みず|の|げり"},{"japanese":"すみません、下痢止めの薬はありますか。","romaji":"Sumimasen, geridome no kusuri wa arimasu ka.","german":"Excuse me, do you have any anti-diarrhea medicine?","furigana":"げりど|くすり"}],"notes":"下痢 is diarrhea, a plain, factual word used at the doctor's or the pharmacy: 下痢をする, 下痢が続く, 下痢止め \"anti-diarrhea medicine\". As a する-verb it is intransitive. In everyday speech it is often put more politely as お腹を壊す \"to upset one's stomach\" or お腹が緩い. The opposite is 便秘.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"便秘で困っています。","romaji":"Benpi de komatte imasu.","german":"I'm having trouble with constipation.","furigana":"べんぴ|こま"},{"japanese":"野菜をあまり食べないと、便秘になりやすいです。","romaji":"Yasai o amari tabenai to, benpi ni nariyasui desu.","german":"If you don't eat much vegetables, you tend to get constipated.","furigana":"やさい|た|べんぴ"},{"japanese":"最近、ちょっと便秘気味です。","romaji":"Saikin, chotto benpigimi desu.","german":"Lately I've been a little constipated.","furigana":"さいきん|べんぴぎみ"}],"notes":"便秘 is constipation. Typical phrases are 便秘になる, 便秘が続く, 便秘で困る and 便秘気味 \"slightly constipated\". The word is matter-of-fact and is used openly even in advertising for yogurt or tea; as an intransitive する-verb (便秘する) it is rarer. The opposite is 下痢, a remedy is called 便秘薬.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不眠症に悩んでいます。","romaji":"Fuminshō ni nayande imasu.","german":"I suffer from insomnia.","furigana":"ふみんしょう|なや"},{"japanese":"仕事のストレスで不眠症になりました。","romaji":"Shigoto no sutoresu de fuminshou ni narimashita.","german":"I developed insomnia from work stress.","furigana":"しごと|ふみんしょう"},{"japanese":"ただの寝不足ではなく、不眠症かもしれません。","romaji":"Tada no nebusoku de wa naku, fuminshou kamoshiremasen.","german":"It may not be just lack of sleep, but insomnia.","furigana":"ねぶそく|ふみんしょう"}],"notes":"不眠症 is insomnia as a (chronic) disorder, i.e. more than one bad night. Typical phrases are 不眠症になる, 不眠症に悩む \"to suffer from insomnia\" and 不眠症の治療. Someone who has simply slept too little is 寝不足; \"cannot fall asleep\" is 眠れない or 寝つけない.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"食欲がありません。","romaji":"Shokuyoku ga arimasen.","german":"I have no appetite.","furigana":"しょくよく"},{"japanese":"たくさん運動したら、食欲が出てきました。","romaji":"Takusan undou shitara, shokuyoku ga dete kimashita.","german":"After exercising a lot, I got an appetite.","furigana":"うんどう|しょくよく|で"},{"japanese":"秋は食欲の秋と言われるほど、食べ物がおいしい季節です。","romaji":"Aki wa shokuyoku no aki to iwareru hodo, tabemono ga oishii kisetsu desu.","german":"Autumn is such a good season for food that it is called the \"autumn of appetite\".","furigana":"あき|しょくよく|あき|い|た|もの|きせつ"}],"notes":"食欲 is the appetite for food. Typical are 食欲がある/ない, 食欲が出る \"to get an appetite\" and 食欲が落ちる \"the appetite decreases\". The well-known expression is 食欲の秋 \"autumn, the season of good eating\". Physical hunger, by contrast, is お腹が空く, and a craving for something specific is 〜が食べたい.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 46"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ダイエット中です。","romaji":"Daiettochū desu.","german":"I'm on a diet.","furigana":"ちゅう"},{"japanese":"夏までに三キロやせたいので、ダイエットを始めました。","romaji":"Natsu made ni sankiro yasetai node, daietto o hajimemashita.","german":"I want to lose three kilos by summer, so I started a diet.","furigana":"なつ|さん|はじ"},{"japanese":"ダイエットに成功した姉は、毎朝走っています。","romaji":"Daietto ni seikou shita ane wa, maiasa hashitte imasu.","german":"My sister, who succeeded in dieting, runs every morning.","furigana":"せいこう|あね|まいあさはし"}],"notes":"ダイエット in Japanese almost always means \"losing weight\", not a medical diet. You say ダイエットする, ダイエット中 \"I'm dieting right now\", ダイエットに成功する/失敗する. It also includes exercise for weight loss. A medically prescribed diet is called 食事制限 or 食事療法.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 19"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"関節が痛みます。","romaji":"Kansetsu ga itamimasu.","german":"My joints hurt.","furigana":"かんせつ|いた"},{"japanese":"寒くなると、膝の関節が痛くなります。","romaji":"Samuku naru to, hiza no kansetsu ga itaku narimasu.","german":"When it gets cold, my knee joints start to hurt.","furigana":"さむ|ひざ|かんせつ|いた"},{"japanese":"指の関節を鳴らすのはやめなさい。","romaji":"Yubi no kansetsu o narasu no wa yamenasai.","german":"Stop cracking your finger joints.","furigana":"ゆび|かんせつ|な"}],"notes":"関節 is the joint. Typical phrases are 関節が痛む/痛い, 膝の関節, 指の関節を鳴らす \"to crack one's knuckles\" and 関節が柔らかい \"flexible\". Medically you hear 関節炎 \"arthritis\" and 関節痛 \"joint pain\", e.g. as a flu symptom. The bone itself is 骨, the muscle 筋肉.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"臓器移植をしました。","romaji":"Zōki ishoku o shimashita.","german":"An organ transplant was carried out.","furigana":"ぞうきいしょく"},{"japanese":"運転免許証の裏には、臓器提供の意思を書く欄があります。","romaji":"Unten menkyoshou no ura ni wa, zouki teikyou no ishi o kaku ran ga arimasu.","german":"On the back of the driver's license there is a field for stating your wishes about organ donation.","furigana":"うんてんめんきょしょう|うら|ぞうきていきょう|いし|か|らん"},{"japanese":"お酒の飲みすぎは臓器に大きな負担をかけます。","romaji":"Osake no nomisugi wa zouki ni ookina futan o kakemasu.","german":"Drinking too much alcohol puts a heavy burden on the organs.","furigana":"さけ|の|ぞうき|おお|ふたん"}],"notes":"臓器 are the internal organs such as heart, liver or kidneys, mainly in a medical context. Most often you encounter 臓器移植 \"organ transplant\" and 臓器提供 \"organ donation\". 内臓 is the more general word for \"internal organs, entrails\"; 器官 is the biological umbrella term for organs by function (e.g. 感覚器官 \"sense organs\"). Individual organs often end in 臓: 心臓, 肝臓, 腎臓.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"抗体ができました。","romaji":"Kōtai ga dekimashita.","german":"Antibodies have formed.","furigana":"こうたい"},{"japanese":"昔はしかにかかったので、抗体があるはずです。","romaji":"Mukashi hashika ni kakatta node, koutai ga aru hazu desu.","german":"I had measles in the past, so I should have antibodies.","furigana":"むかし|こうたい"},{"japanese":"念のため、病院で抗体検査を受けました。","romaji":"Nen no tame, byouin de koutai kensa o ukemashita.","german":"Just to be safe, I had an antibody test at the hospital.","furigana":"ねん|びょういん|こうたいけんさ|う"}],"notes":"抗体 is the antibody that the immune system forms against a pathogen. Typical phrases are 抗体ができる \"antibodies form\", 抗体がある and 抗体検査 \"antibody test\". The word is technical but entered everyday language through vaccinations. Its counterpart, the triggering foreign substance, is 抗原 \"antigen\"; the immune system itself is 免疫.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"感染を防ぎます。","romaji":"Kansen o fusegimasu.","german":"We prevent infection.","furigana":"かんせん|ふせ"},{"japanese":"ウイルスに感染してしまいました。","romaji":"Uirusu ni kansen shite shimaimashita.","german":"I caught a virus.","furigana":"かんせん"},{"japanese":"インフルエンザの感染が学校で広がっています。","romaji":"Infuruenza no kansen ga gakkou de hirogatte imasu.","german":"The flu is spreading in the school.","furigana":"かんせん|がっこう|ひろ"}],"notes":"感染 is medical infection or contagion. As an intransitive する-verb, the pathogen takes に: ウイルスに感染する \"to be infected with a virus\". Common compounds are 感染症 \"infectious disease\", 感染者 \"infected person\" and 感染拡大 \"spread of infection\"; computer viruses also \"infect\" a computer. In everyday speech, for colds people more often say うつる/うつす: 風邪がうつった.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"隔離されました。","romaji":"Kakuri saremashita.","german":"I was put in quarantine.","furigana":"かくり"},{"japanese":"熱がある患者は別の部屋に隔離します。","romaji":"Netsu ga aru kanja wa betsu no heya ni kakuri shimasu.","german":"Patients with a fever are isolated in a separate room.","furigana":"ねつ|かんじゃ|べつ|へや|かくり"},{"japanese":"彼は社会から隔離されたような生活を送っています。","romaji":"Kare wa shakai kara kakuri sareta you na seikatsu o okutte imasu.","german":"He lives as if cut off from society.","furigana":"かれ|しゃかい|かくり|せいかつ|おく"}],"notes":"隔離 means to separate someone or something from others, especially the sick to prevent infection. As a transitive する-verb: AをBから隔離する; very common in the passive 隔離される. Well-known words are 自主隔離 \"self-isolation\" and 隔離病棟 \"isolation ward\". Official quarantine on entry to a country is actually 検疫, but in everyday speech people also say 隔離 for that.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"手を消毒してください。","romaji":"Te o shōdoku shite kudasai.","german":"Please disinfect your hands.","furigana":"て|しょうどく"},{"japanese":"転んでできた傷を消毒しました。","romaji":"Koronde dekita kizu o shoudoku shimashita.","german":"I disinfected the wound from the fall.","furigana":"ころ|きず|しょうどく"},{"japanese":"店の入口に消毒液が置いてあります。","romaji":"Mise no iriguchi ni shoudokueki ga oite arimasu.","german":"Disinfectant is placed at the entrance of the shop.","furigana":"みせ|いりぐち|しょうどくえき|お"}],"notes":"消毒 is disinfecting, that is, killing germs with alcohol, heat or chemicals. As a transitive する-verb: 手を消毒する, 傷口を消毒する. Common are 消毒液 \"disinfectant\" and アルコール消毒. 殺菌 is the more technical \"sterilizing, killing of germs\", and 除菌 appears mainly in advertising for cleaning products.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"衛生に気をつけます。","romaji":"Eisei ni ki o tsukemasu.","german":"I pay attention to hygiene.","furigana":"えいせい|き"},{"japanese":"この店は衛生管理がしっかりしています。","romaji":"Kono mise wa eisei kanri ga shikkari shite imasu.","german":"This restaurant has strict hygiene control.","furigana":"みせ|えいせいかんり"},{"japanese":"台所はいつも衛生的にしておきましょう。","romaji":"Daidokoro wa itsumo eiseiteki ni shite okimashou.","german":"Let's always keep the kitchen hygienic.","furigana":"だいどころ|えいせいてき"}],"notes":"衛生 means hygiene and health protection, but mostly appears in compounds: 衛生的 \"hygienic\" (な-adjective), 不衛生 \"unhygienic\", 衛生管理 \"hygiene control\", 公衆衛生 \"public health\", 衛生面 \"in terms of hygiene\". For everyday \"clean\" people more often say 清潔 or きれい. Do not confuse it with the identically read 衛星 \"satellite\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"酸素が必要です。","romaji":"Sanso ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"Oxygen is needed.","furigana":"さんそ|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"高い山の上は酸素が薄いです。","romaji":"Takai yama no ue wa sanso ga usui desu.","german":"The oxygen is thin at the top of high mountains.","furigana":"たか|やま|うえ|さんそ|うす"},{"japanese":"救急隊員が患者に酸素マスクをつけました。","romaji":"Kyuukyuu taiin ga kanja ni sanso masuku o tsukemashita.","german":"The paramedics put an oxygen mask on the patient.","furigana":"きゅうきゅうたいいん|かんじゃ|さんそ"}],"notes":"酸素 is oxygen; literally \"acid element\", following the same pattern as 水素 \"hydrogen\", 窒素 \"nitrogen\" and 炭素 \"carbon\". Typical phrases are 酸素が薄い \"the oxygen is thin\" (high-altitude air), 酸素不足 \"lack of oxygen\", 酸素マスク and 酸素ボンベ \"oxygen cylinder\". For thin mountain air, people also simply say 空気が薄い in everyday speech.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二酸化炭素を減らします。","romaji":"Nisankatanso o herashimasu.","german":"We reduce CO2.","furigana":"にさんかたんそ|へ"},{"japanese":"植物は二酸化炭素を吸って酸素を出します。","romaji":"Shokubutsu wa nisankatanso o sutte sanso o dashimasu.","german":"Plants take in carbon dioxide and release oxygen.","furigana":"しょくぶつ|にさんかたんそ|す|さんそ|だ"},{"japanese":"車から出る二酸化炭素が問題になっています。","romaji":"Kuruma kara deru nisankatanso ga mondai ni natte imasu.","german":"The CO2 coming from cars has become a problem.","furigana":"くるま|で|にさんかたんそ|もんだい"}],"notes":"二酸化炭素 is carbon dioxide, literally \"carbon dioxide\" (二 \"two\", 酸化 \"oxide\", 炭素 \"carbon\"). In environmental news it appears with 出す/排出する \"to emit\" and 減らす/削減する \"to reduce\"; it is often simply written CO2 (pronounced シーオーツー) or grouped under 温室効果ガス \"greenhouse gases\". In biology class: plants take in 二酸化炭素 and release 酸素.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"空気の大部分は窒素です。","romaji":"Kūki no daibubun wa chisso desu.","german":"Der Großteil der Luft ist Stickstoff.","furigana":"くうき|だいぶぶん|ちっそ"},{"japanese":"ポテトチップスの袋には窒素が入っています。","romaji":"Poteto chippusu no fukuro ni wa chisso ga haitte imasu.","german":"In Chipstüten ist Stickstoff.","furigana":"ふくろ|ちっそ|はい"},{"japanese":"窒素は植物が育つのに必要です。","romaji":"Chisso wa shokubutsu ga sodatsu no ni hitsuyou desu.","german":"Pflanzen brauchen Stickstoff zum Wachsen.","furigana":"ちっそ|しょくぶつ|そだ|ひつよう"}],"notes":"窒素 ist Stickstoff; das Zeichen 窒 bedeutet „verstopfen, ersticken“ (vgl. 窒息 „Ersticken“), ähnlich wie das deutsche Wort. Ein Fachwort, das im Alltag bei Luftzusammensetzung, Düngemitteln (窒素肥料), Lebensmittelverpackungen und 液体窒素 „flüssiger Stickstoff“ vorkommt. Es gehört zur Reihe der Elementnamen auf 素 wie 酸素 und 水素.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"水素エネルギーに注目しています。","romaji":"Suiso enerugī ni chūmoku shite imasu.","german":"Hydrogen energy is attracting attention.","furigana":"すいそ|ちゅうもく"},{"japanese":"水は水素と酸素からできています。","romaji":"Mizu wa suiso to sanso kara dekite imasu.","german":"Water is made of hydrogen and oxygen.","furigana":"みず|すいそ|さんそ"},{"japanese":"水素で走る車が少しずつ増えています。","romaji":"Suiso de hashiru kuruma ga sukoshi zutsu fuete imasu.","german":"The number of cars that run on hydrogen is gradually increasing.","furigana":"すいそ|はし|くるま|すこ|ふ"}],"notes":"水素 is hydrogen, literally \"water element\". Outside chemistry class, it is heard mainly in connection with energy: 水素エネルギー, 水素自動車 \"hydrogen car\", 燃料電池 \"fuel cell\". Do not confuse it with 水 \"water\" itself: water consists of 水素 and 酸素. 水素水 \"hydrogen water\" is an advertising term for beverages.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"有害な物質が含まれています。","romaji":"Yūgai na busshitsu ga fukumarete imasu.","german":"It contains harmful substances.","furigana":"ゆうがい|ぶっしつ|ふく"},{"japanese":"この物質は水に溶けません。","romaji":"Kono busshitsu wa mizu ni tokemasen.","german":"This substance does not dissolve in water.","furigana":"ぶっしつ|みず|と"},{"japanese":"物質的には豊かになったが、心は貧しくなったと言う人もいます。","romaji":"Busshitsuteki ni wa yutaka ni natta ga, kokoro wa mazushiku natta to iu hito mo imasu.","german":"Some people say we have become materially rich but poor in spirit.","furigana":"ぶっしつてき|ゆた|こころ|まず|い|ひと"}],"notes":"物質 is \"substance, matter\" in the scientific sense, typically in 化学物質 \"chemical\" and 有害物質 \"harmful substance\". To be distinguished from 材料 \"material (for making things)\" and the everyday 物 \"thing\". The adjective 物質的 \"material\" is often contrasted with 精神的 \"mental, spiritual\". Reading with small っ: ぶっしつ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"原子は非常に小さいです。","romaji":"Genshi wa hijō ni chiisai desu.","german":"Atoms are extremely small.","furigana":"げんし|ひじょう|ちい"},{"japanese":"水の分子は水素原子二つと酸素原子一つでできています。","romaji":"Mizu no bunshi wa suiso genshi futatsu to sanso genshi hitotsu de dekite imasu.","german":"A water molecule consists of two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom.","furigana":"みず|ぶんし|すいそげんしふた|さんそげんしひと"},{"japanese":"この町の近くに原子力発電所があります。","romaji":"Kono machi no chikaku ni genshiryoku hatsudensho ga arimasu.","german":"There is a nuclear power plant near this town.","furigana":"まち|ちか|げんしりょくはつでんしょ"}],"notes":"原子 is the atom; 分子 is the molecule, which consists of 原子. Very common is the compound 原子力 \"nuclear power\", e.g. 原子力発電所 (abbreviated 原発) \"nuclear power plant\"; 原子爆弾 is shortened to 原爆. Do not confuse it with the identically read 原始 \"primitive, primeval\" (原始時代 \"primeval age\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"水の分子は、水素原子二つと酸素原子一つからできています。","romaji":"Mizu no bunshi wa, suiso genshi futatsu to sanso genshi hitotsu kara dekite imasu.","german":"A water molecule consists of two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom.","furigana":"みず|ぶんし|すいそげんしふた|さんそげんしひと"},{"japanese":"空気の分子は、温度が高いほど速く動きます。","romaji":"Kuuki no bunshi wa, ondo ga takai hodo hayaku ugokimasu.","german":"The higher the temperature, the faster the air molecules move.","furigana":"くうき|ぶんし|おんど|たか|はや|うご"},{"japanese":"分数では、上の数を分子、下の数を分母と言います。","romaji":"Bunsuu de wa, ue no kazu o bunshi, shita no kazu o bunbo to iimasu.","german":"In a fraction, the upper number is called the numerator and the lower number the denominator.","furigana":"ぶんすう|うえ|かず|ぶんし|した|かず|ぶんぼ|い"}],"notes":"分子 is the \"molecule\" in chemistry; its building blocks are called 原子 (\"atom\"). In mathematics, 分子 is the \"numerator\" of a fraction; the denominator is 分母. In written language it also refers to \"elements\" of a group, usually pejoratively, as in 不満分子 (\"discontented elements\"). Which meaning is intended is clear from the context.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"再生可能エネルギーを使います。","romaji":"Saiseikanō enerugī o tsukaimasu.","german":"Wir nutzen erneuerbare Energie.","furigana":"さいせいかのう|つか"},{"japanese":"子供たちはエネルギーにあふれています。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa enerugii ni afurete imasu.","german":"Die Kinder strotzen nur so vor Energie.","furigana":"こども"},{"japanese":"日本はエネルギーの多くを輸入に頼っています。","romaji":"Nihon wa enerugii no ooku o yunyuu ni tayotte imasu.","german":"Japan ist bei der Energie stark von Importen abhängig.","furigana":"にほん|おお|ゆにゅう|たよ"}],"notes":"エネルギー stammt aus dem deutschen „Energie“ (daher -ギー statt englisch -ジー). Es bezeichnet physikalische Energie und Energiequellen: エネルギーを使う/節約する, 再生可能エネルギー „erneuerbare Energie“, 省エネ „Energiesparen“. Übertragen auch für menschliche Tatkraft: エネルギーにあふれる „vor Energie strotzen“, umgangssprachlich eher パワー oder 元気. Auf Lebensmitteletiketten steht エネルギー für den Kaloriengehalt.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"月の重力は小さいです。","romaji":"Tsuki no jūryoku wa chiisai desu.","german":"The moon's gravity is small.","furigana":"つき|じゅうりょく|ちい"},{"japanese":"宇宙船の中では重力をほとんど感じません。","romaji":"Uchuusen no naka de wa juuryoku o hotondo kanjimasen.","german":"Inside a spacecraft, you hardly feel gravity.","furigana":"うちゅうせん|なか|じゅうりょく|かん"},{"japanese":"リンゴが木から落ちるのは重力のためです。","romaji":"Ringo ga ki kara ochiru no wa juuryoku no tame desu.","german":"The apple falls from the tree because of gravity.","furigana":"き|お|じゅうりょく"}],"notes":"重力 is gravity, literally \"weight force\". There is also 引力 \"attractive force\" (more general, e.g. 万有引力 \"law of universal gravitation\", also the moon's pull on the tides). 無重力 means \"weightlessness\". Typical combinations: 重力がある/小さい, 重力を感じる, 重力に逆らう \"to defy gravity\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"磁石にくっつきます。","romaji":"Jishaku ni kuttsukimasu.","german":"It sticks to the magnet.","furigana":"じしゃく"},{"japanese":"冷蔵庫にメモを磁石で留めました。","romaji":"Reizouko ni memo o jishaku de tomemashita.","german":"I fastened the note to the fridge with a magnet.","furigana":"れいぞうこ|じしゃく|と"},{"japanese":"山の中で磁石を使って方角を調べました。","romaji":"Yama no naka de jishaku o tsukatte hougaku o shirabemashita.","german":"In the mountains, I used a compass to determine the direction.","furigana":"やま|なか|じしゃく|つか|ほうがく|しら"}],"notes":"磁石 is a magnet; things stick to it with に: 磁石にくっつく/つく. Refrigerator magnets are also called マグネット. The secondary meaning \"compass\" is preserved today mostly in 方位磁石; otherwise one says コンパス. Magnetic force is 磁力, and the magnetic field 磁場 or 磁界.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"実験室で研究します。","romaji":"Jikkenshitsu de kenkyū shimasu.","german":"I do research in the laboratory.","furigana":"じっけんしつ|けんきゅう"},{"japanese":"実験室では白衣を着なければなりません。","romaji":"Jikkenshitsu de wa hakui o kinakereba narimasen.","german":"You have to wear a white coat in the laboratory.","furigana":"じっけんしつ|はくい|き"},{"japanese":"化学の授業は実験室で行います。","romaji":"Kagaku no jugyou wa jikkenshitsu de okonaimasu.","german":"Chemistry class is held in the laboratory.","furigana":"かがく|じゅぎょう|じっけんしつ|おこな"}],"notes":"実験室 is a room for experiments, i.e. a laboratory (実験 \"experiment\" + 室 \"room\"). At schools, the science room is usually 理科室. 研究室 at universities often means a professor's research group or their office, not just the lab room; colloquially and in companies you also hear ラボ.","levelNote":"Compound: 実験 (N4) + 室 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"顕微鏡で観察します。","romaji":"Kenbikyō de kansatsu shimasu.","german":"I observe with a microscope.","furigana":"けんびきょう|かんさつ"},{"japanese":"細胞は顕微鏡でないと見えません。","romaji":"Saibou wa kenbikyou de nai to miemasen.","german":"You can't see cells without a microscope.","furigana":"さいぼう|けんびきょう|み"},{"japanese":"理科の授業で初めて顕微鏡をのぞきました。","romaji":"Rika no jugyou de hajimete kenbikyou o nozokimashita.","german":"In science class, I looked through a microscope for the first time.","furigana":"りか|じゅぎょう|はじ|けんびきょう"}],"notes":"顕微鏡 is a microscope. One \"peers into\" it (顕微鏡をのぞく) or observes with it (顕微鏡で見る/観察する). The character 鏡 \"mirror, lens\" links it with 望遠鏡 \"telescope\", 双眼鏡 \"binoculars\" and 眼鏡 \"glasses\". 電子顕微鏡 is the electron microscope.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"望遠鏡で星を見ます。","romaji":"Bōenkyō de hoshi o mimasu.","german":"I look at the stars through a telescope.","furigana":"ぼうえんきょう|ほし|み"},{"japanese":"望遠鏡をのぞくと、月のクレーターが見えました。","romaji":"Bouenkyou o nozoku to, tsuki no kureetaa ga miemashita.","german":"When I looked through the telescope, I could see the craters of the moon.","furigana":"ぼうえんきょう|つき|み"},{"japanese":"誕生日に父から望遠鏡を買ってもらいました。","romaji":"Tanjoubi ni chichi kara bouenkyou o katte moraimashita.","german":"For my birthday, my father bought me a telescope.","furigana":"たんじょうび|ちち|ぼうえんきょう|か"}],"notes":"望遠鏡 is a telescope or spyglass, literally \"mirror for looking into the distance\". You look through it (望遠鏡をのぞく) or view something with it (望遠鏡で見る). The astronomical telescope is more precisely called 天体望遠鏡; binoculars with two eyepieces, by contrast, are 双眼鏡.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"宇宙に行きたいです。","romaji":"Uchū ni ikitai desu.","german":"I want to go into space.","furigana":"うちゅう|い"},{"japanese":"宇宙飛行士になるのが子供のころからの夢です。","romaji":"Uchuu hikoushi ni naru no ga kodomo no koro kara no yume desu.","german":"Becoming an astronaut has been my dream since childhood.","furigana":"うちゅうひこうし|こども|ゆめ"},{"japanese":"宇宙はどこまで広がっているのでしょうか。","romaji":"Uchuu wa doko made hirogatte iru no deshou ka.","german":"I wonder how far the universe extends.","furigana":"うちゅう|ひろ"}],"notes":"宇宙 covers both \"outer space\" and \"the universe\". One goes 宇宙へ/に行く; common compounds are 宇宙飛行士 \"astronaut\", 宇宙人 \"alien\", 宇宙ステーション and 宇宙開発 \"space development\". 空, by contrast, is the visible sky, and 天 is the heavens in a religious or poetic sense.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 26"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"太陽系には八つの惑星があります。","romaji":"Taiyōkei ni wa yattsu no wakusei ga arimasu.","german":"There are eight planets in the solar system.","furigana":"たいようけい|やっ|わくせい"},{"japanese":"地球は太陽の周りを回る惑星です。","romaji":"Chikyuu wa taiyou no mawari o mawaru wakusei desu.","german":"The Earth is a planet that orbits the Sun.","furigana":"ちきゅう|たいよう|まわ|まわ|わくせい"},{"japanese":"夜空で一番明るく見える惑星は金星です。","romaji":"Yozora de ichiban akaruku mieru wakusei wa kinsei desu.","german":"The brightest planet in the night sky is Venus.","furigana":"よぞら|いちばんあか|み|わくせい|きんせい"}],"notes":"惑星 is a planet, literally a \"wandering star\" (惑 \"confused, to wander\"). Its counterparts are 恒星 \"fixed star\" and 衛星 \"moon, satellite\". The planets are called 水星, 金星, 火星, 木星, 土星 etc.; 星 alone is any star in the sky. Rarely, 惑星 also refers to a \"dark horse with a chance of winning\" in sports.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人工衛星を打ち上げました。","romaji":"Jinkou eisei o uchiagemashita.","german":"An artificial satellite was launched.","furigana":"じんこうえいせい|う|あ"},{"japanese":"月は地球のただ一つの衛星です。","romaji":"Tsuki wa chikyuu no tada hitotsu no eisei desu.","german":"The moon is the Earth's only satellite.","furigana":"つき|ちきゅう|ひと|えいせい"},{"japanese":"衛星放送で海外のサッカーの試合を見ました。","romaji":"Eisei housou de kaigai no sakkaa no shiai o mimashita.","german":"I watched an overseas soccer match on satellite broadcasting.","furigana":"えいせいほうそう|かいがい|しあい|み"}],"notes":"衛星 is a natural moon of a planet, but in everyday use it is mostly short for 人工衛星 \"artificial satellite\". Common compounds: 気象衛星 \"weather satellite\", 衛星写真 \"satellite image\", 衛星放送 \"satellite broadcasting\", 衛星都市 \"satellite city\". Satellites are 打ち上げる \"launched\". Read the same as 衛生 \"hygiene\"; only context tells them apart.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"銀河は美しいです。","romaji":"Ginga wa utsukushii desu.","german":"The Milky Way is beautiful.","furigana":"ぎんが|うつく"},{"japanese":"夏の夜、山の上で銀河がはっきり見えました。","romaji":"Natsu no yoru, yama no ue de ginga ga hakkiri miemashita.","german":"On a summer night, I could see the Milky Way clearly on the mountain.","furigana":"なつ|よる|やま|うえ|ぎんが|み"},{"japanese":"宇宙には数えきれないほどの銀河があります。","romaji":"Uchuu ni wa kazoekirenai hodo no ginga ga arimasu.","german":"There are countless galaxies in the universe.","furigana":"うちゅう|かぞ|ぎんが"}],"notes":"銀河, literally \"silver river\", refers to the Milky Way and, in general, a galaxy. For the Milky Way visible in the night sky, people usually say 天の川 in everyday speech, especially around the Tanabata festival. Our own galaxy is technically called 銀河系. In names and titles, 銀河 sounds romantic and poetic.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"衛星が地球の周りの軌道を回っています。","romaji":"Eisei ga chikyuu no mawari no kidou o mawatte imasu.","german":"The satellite is circling the Earth in orbit.","furigana":"えいせい|ちきゅう|まわ|きどう|まわ"},{"japanese":"新しい店の経営がやっと軌道に乗りました。","romaji":"Atarashii mise no keiei ga yatto kidou ni norimashita.","german":"The new shop's business has finally got on track.","furigana":"あたら|みせ|けいえい|きどう|の"},{"japanese":"計画の軌道修正が必要です。","romaji":"Keikaku no kidou shuusei ga hitsuyou desu.","german":"The plan needs a course correction.","furigana":"けいかく|きどうしゅうせい|ひつよう"}],"notes":"軌道 is an orbit or flight path (衛星が軌道を回る), and technically also the track of a railway. The figurative use is very common: 軌道に乗る \"to get going, to take off well\" (business, project) and 軌道修正 \"course correction\". For ordinary railway tracks, people say 線路 in everyday speech.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先生の発音を真似ます。","romaji":"Sensei no hatsuon o manemasu.","german":"I imitate the teacher's pronunciation.","furigana":"せんせい|はつおん|まね"},{"japanese":"子供は親のすることを何でも真似ます。","romaji":"Kodomo wa oya no suru koto o nan demo manemasu.","german":"Children imitate everything their parents do.","furigana":"こども|おや|なん|まね"},{"japanese":"有名な画家の絵を真似て描いてみました。","romaji":"Yuumei na gaka no e o manete kaite mimashita.","german":"I tried painting in imitation of a famous painter's picture.","furigana":"ゆうめい|がか|え|まね|か"}],"notes":"真似る is transitive: 〜を真似る \"to imitate something/someone\", e.g. pronunciation, style or behavior. In conversation, the form with the noun 真似 is even more common: 〜の真似をする; ものまね is imitation as an entertainment act. It is often written in kana, まねる. The verb 学ぶ historically goes back to まねぶ \"to imitate\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子供が母親に甘えています。","romaji":"Kodomo ga hahaoya ni amaete imasu.","german":"The child is acting spoiled with its mother.","furigana":"こども|ははおや|あま"},{"japanese":"では、お言葉に甘えてお先に失礼します。","romaji":"Dewa, okotoba ni amaete osaki ni shitsurei shimasu.","german":"Well then, if I may take you up on your kind offer, I'll leave first.","furigana":"ことば|あま|さき|しつれい"},{"japanese":"猫が甘えて膝の上に乗ってきました。","romaji":"Neko ga amaete hiza no ue ni notte kimashita.","german":"The cat snuggled up and climbed onto my lap.","furigana":"ねこ|あま|ひざ|うえ|の"}],"notes":"甘える is intransitive; the person one leans on takes に: 母親に甘える \"to snuggle up to one's mother, to let oneself be pampered\". Second meaning: to accept someone's kindness, as in the polite phrase お言葉に甘えて \"since you kindly offer\". The transitive counterpart 甘やかす means \"to pamper, to spoil someone\"; a clingy child is an 甘えん坊.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"怠けないで勉強しなさい。","romaji":"Namakenaide benkyō shinasai.","german":"Don't slack off and study.","furigana":"なま|べんきょう"},{"japanese":"彼は仕事を怠けてばかりいます。","romaji":"Kare wa shigoto o namakete bakari imasu.","german":"He does nothing but slack off at work.","furigana":"かれ|しごと|なま"},{"japanese":"練習を一日怠けると、取り戻すのが大変です。","romaji":"Renshuu o ichinichi namakeru to, torimodosu no ga taihen desu.","german":"If you skip practice for one day, it's hard to make up for it.","furigana":"れんしゅう|いちにちなま|と|もど|たいへん"}],"notes":"怠ける means \"to be lazy, to shirk\"; it can be used intransitively or with an object: 仕事を怠ける, 勉強を怠ける. A lazybones is a 怠け者. With the same kanji there is the more formal 怠る (おこたる) \"to neglect, to fail to do\" (注意を怠る); colloquially, for skipping or loafing, one says サボる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"威張らないでください。","romaji":"Ibaranaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't act so high and mighty.","furigana":"いば"},{"japanese":"部長はいつも部下に威張っています。","romaji":"Buchou wa itsumo buka ni ibatte imasu.","german":"The department head is always lording it over his subordinates.","furigana":"ぶちょう|ぶか|いば"},{"japanese":"一番になったからといって、威張るのはよくないよ。","romaji":"Ichiban ni natta kara to itte, ibaru no wa yoku nai yo.","german":"Just because you came in first, there is no need to be arrogant.","furigana":"いちばん|いば"}],"notes":"威張る means \"to act important, to play the big boss\" - a condescending, domineering manner, not boasting about something. The people one lords it over are marked with に: 部下に威張る. For \"to boast about something\", one says 〜を自慢する; similar to 威張る is 偉そうにする. Often written in kana: いばる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"友達を裏切りません。","romaji":"Tomodachi o uragirimasen.","german":"I won't betray my friends.","furigana":"ともだち|うらぎ"},{"japanese":"信じていた仲間に裏切られました。","romaji":"Shinjite ita nakama ni uragiraremashita.","german":"I was betrayed by a colleague I had trusted.","furigana":"しん|なかま|うらぎ"},{"japanese":"その映画はいい意味で期待を裏切りました。","romaji":"Sono eiga wa ii imi de kitai o uragirimashita.","german":"That movie exceeded my expectations in a good way.","furigana":"えいが|いみ|きたい|うらぎ"}],"notes":"裏切る is transitive: 友達を裏切る \"to betray a friend\", in the passive 〜に裏切られる \"to be betrayed by someone\". Also very common is 期待を裏切る \"to disappoint expectations\"; with いい意味で \"in a good sense\" it means that something exceeds expectations. The noun is 裏切り, a traitor is a 裏切り者.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子供を見守ります。","romaji":"Kodomo o mimamorimasu.","german":"I watch over the children.","furigana":"こども|みまも"},{"japanese":"みんなが息をのんで試合の行方を見守っています。","romaji":"Minna ga iki o nonde shiai no yukue o mimamotte imasu.","german":"Everyone is following the outcome of the game with bated breath.","furigana":"いき|しあい|ゆくえ|みまも"},{"japanese":"両親はいつも私の夢を温かく見守ってくれました。","romaji":"Ryoushin wa itsumo watashi no yume o atatakaku mimamotte kuremashita.","german":"My parents always supported my dream with warm encouragement.","furigana":"りょうしん|わたし|ゆめ|あたた|みまも"}],"notes":"見守る is transitive and describes protective, benevolent watching without intervening: 子供を見守る, 温かく見守る \"to look on warmly, to support lovingly\". In the news, 今後の動きを見守る means \"to follow further developments attentively\". Unlike 見張る \"to guard, to keep watch\" or 監視する \"to monitor\", it carries no distrust.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大事な場面を見逃しました。","romaji":"Daiji na bamen o minogashimashita.","german":"I missed an important scene.","furigana":"だいじ|ばめん|みのが"},{"japanese":"今回だけは見逃してあげます。","romaji":"Konkai dake wa minogashite agemasu.","german":"I'll let it go just this once.","furigana":"こんかい|みのが"},{"japanese":"このチャンスを見逃すわけにはいきません。","romaji":"Kono chansu o minogasu wake ni wa ikimasen.","german":"I can't afford to miss this chance.","furigana":"みのが"}],"notes":"見逃す is transitive and has three main meanings: to miss or overlook something (場面を見逃す), to deliberately let a mistake pass (今回は見逃す \"to turn a blind eye this time\") and to let an opportunity slip (チャンスを見逃す). 見落とす stresses the accidental overlooking of a detail, 見過ごす the tolerating of something. Online replays of TV programs are called 見逃し配信.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女を見つめました。","romaji":"Kanojo o mitsumemashita.","german":"I gazed at her.","furigana":"かのじょ|み"},{"japanese":"彼は黙って窓の外を見つめていました。","romaji":"Kare wa damatte mado no soto o mitsumete imashita.","german":"He was silently looking out the window.","furigana":"かれ|だま|まど|そと|み"},{"japanese":"一人旅で、自分自身を見つめ直しました。","romaji":"Hitoritabi de, jibun jishin o mitsumenaoshimashita.","german":"On a solo trip, I took a fresh look at myself.","furigana":"ひとりたび|じぶんじしん|み|なお"}],"notes":"見つめる is transitive: 〜を見つめる \"to gaze at, to fix one's eyes on something\". It describes a long, intense and often emotional gaze, as opposed to じろじろ見る \"to stare rudely\" or にらむ \"to glare\". Figuratively: 現実を見つめる \"to face reality\", 自分を見つめ直す \"to reexamine oneself\". The kanji form 見詰める is rare.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"駅で友達を見かけました。","romaji":"Eki de tomodachi o mikakemashita.","german":"I saw a friend at the station.","furigana":"えき|ともだち|み"},{"japanese":"最近、この辺りで外国人の観光客をよく見かけます。","romaji":"Saikin, kono atari de gaikokujin no kankoukyaku o yoku mikakemasu.","german":"Recently, you often see foreign tourists around here.","furigana":"さいきん|あた|がいこくじん|かんこうきゃく|み"},{"japanese":"この猫、よく見かけるけど、どこの猫だろう。","romaji":"Kono neko, yoku mikakeru kedo, doko no neko darou.","german":"I often see this cat; I wonder whose it is.","furigana":"ねこ|み|ねこ"}],"notes":"見かける means to see someone or something by chance and fleetingly, without making contact: 駅で友達を見かけた. With よく it expresses that you come across something often. By contrast, 会う means a real meeting and 見つける \"to find after searching\". Kanji form 見掛ける; the noun 見かけ means \"appearance, outward look\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いい考えが思いつきました。","romaji":"Ii kangae ga omoitsukimashita.","german":"I came up with a good idea.","furigana":"かんが|おも"},{"japanese":"プレゼントに何をあげたらいいか、何も思いつきません。","romaji":"Purezento ni nani o agetara ii ka, nani mo omoitsukimasen.","german":"I can't think of anything to give as a present.","furigana":"なに|なに|おも"},{"japanese":"思いついたことは、すぐにメモしておきます。","romaji":"Omoitsuita koto wa, sugu ni memo shite okimasu.","german":"I write down whatever comes to mind right away.","furigana":"おも"}],"notes":"思いつく means that an idea or solution suddenly occurs to you. The idea can take を or が: いい考えを/が思いつく. In the negative: 何も思いつかない \"nothing comes to mind\". The noun 思いつき is a spontaneous idea, often pejorative (思いつきで決める \"to decide on a whim\"). Do not confuse it with 思い出す \"to remember\"; similar is アイデアが浮かぶ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"正しいと思い込んでいました。","romaji":"Tadashii to omoikonde imashita.","german":"I was convinced that it was right.","furigana":"ただ|おも|こ"},{"japanese":"今日は日曜日だと思い込んで、寝坊してしまいました。","romaji":"Kyou wa nichiyoubi da to omoikonde, nebou shite shimaimashita.","german":"I was sure it was Sunday today and overslept.","furigana":"きょう|にちようび|おも|こ|ねぼう"},{"japanese":"それはただの思い込みですよ。","romaji":"Sore wa tada no omoikomi desu yo.","german":"That's just your assumption.","furigana":"おも|こ"}],"notes":"思い込む means to be firmly convinced of something - mostly wrongly: 〜と思い込む \"to wrongly assume, to talk oneself into\". The noun 思い込み means \"delusion, preconception\", e.g. 思い込みが激しい \"full of preconceptions, stubborn in one's assumptions\". Neutral \"to believe\" is 信じる; a mere misunderstanding is 勘違い.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"予約を取り消します。","romaji":"Yoyaku o torikeshimasu.","german":"I will cancel the reservation.","furigana":"よやく|と|け"},{"japanese":"さっきの発言を取り消します。","romaji":"Sakki no hatsugen o torikeshimasu.","german":"I take back my remark from a moment ago.","furigana":"はつげん|と|け"},{"japanese":"飲酒運転で免許を取り消されました。","romaji":"Inshu unten de menkyo o torikesaremashita.","german":"His license was revoked for drunk driving.","furigana":"いんしゅうんてん|めんきょ|と|け"}],"notes":"取り消す is transitive: to annul something already decided - a reservation, an order, a statement (発言を取り消す) or a permit. In everyday speech, for bookings one often says キャンセルする; 取り消す sounds somewhat more formal and also fits words and licenses (免許を取り消される \"to have one's license revoked\"). The noun is 取り消し.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"取り扱いに注意してください。","romaji":"Toriatsukai ni chūi shite kudasai.","german":"Please handle with care.","furigana":"と|あつか|ちゅうい"},{"japanese":"取り扱い説明書をよく読んでから使ってください。","romaji":"Toriatsukai setsumeisho o yoku yonde kara tsukatte kudasai.","german":"Please read the instruction manual carefully before use.","furigana":"と|あつか|せつめいしょ|よ|つか"},{"japanese":"申し訳ありませんが、当店ではこの商品の取り扱いはございません。","romaji":"Moushiwake arimasen ga, touten de wa kono shouhin no toriatsukai wa gozaimasen.","german":"I'm sorry, but our store does not carry this product.","furigana":"もう|わけ|とうてん|しょうひん|と|あつか"}],"notes":"取り扱い is the noun of 取り扱う \"to handle, to deal with, to carry (goods)\". Typical are 取り扱いに注意 \"handle with care\", 取り扱い説明書 (short 取説) \"instruction manual\" and, in shops, 〜の取り扱いがある/ない \"to (not) have something in stock\". On signs and parcels it is often written without okurigana: 取扱注意, 取扱説明書.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"色の組み合わせが素敵です。","romaji":"Iro no kumiawase ga suteki desu.","german":"The color combination is lovely.","furigana":"いろ|く|あ|すてき"},{"japanese":"トーナメントの組み合わせが決まりました。","romaji":"Toonamento no kumiawase ga kimarimashita.","german":"The pairings for the tournament have been decided.","furigana":"く|あ|き"},{"japanese":"パスワードは数字と文字の組み合わせにしてください。","romaji":"Pasuwaado wa suuji to moji no kumiawase ni shite kudasai.","german":"Please make the password a combination of numbers and letters.","furigana":"すうじ|もじ|く|あ"}],"notes":"組み合わせ is a combination of two or more things: AとBの組み合わせ, 色の組み合わせ. In sports it refers to the pairings of a tournament (試合の組み合わせ). The verb is 組み合わせる \"to combine\". Do not confuse it with 組合 (くみあい) \"union, cooperative\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"差し支えなければ教えてください。","romaji":"Sashitsukae nakereba oshiete kudasai.","german":"If you don't mind, please tell me.","furigana":"さ|つか|おし"},{"japanese":"遅くまで飲むと、明日の仕事に差し支えます。","romaji":"Osoku made nomu to, ashita no shigoto ni sashitsukaemasu.","german":"If I drink until late, it will affect tomorrow's work.","furigana":"おそ|の|あした|しごと|さ|つか"},{"japanese":"生活に差し支えるほどの痛みではありません。","romaji":"Seikatsu ni sashitsukaeru hodo no itami de wa arimasen.","german":"The pain is not so bad that it interferes with daily life.","furigana":"せいかつ|さ|つか|いた"}],"notes":"差し支える is intransitive: something has an adverse effect, with に for the affected area: 仕事に差し支える \"to affect work\". Most often you meet the noun 差し支え in polite formulas: 差し支えなければ \"if you don't mind\", 〜ても差し支えありません \"there is no objection to ...-ing\" (a formal version of 〜てもかまいません).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お問い合わせありがとうございます。","romaji":"Otoiawase arigatō gozaimasu.","german":"Thank you for your inquiry.","furigana":"と|あ"},{"japanese":"詳しくは電話でお問い合わせください。","romaji":"Kuwashiku wa denwa de otoiawase kudasai.","german":"For details, please inquire by phone.","furigana":"くわ|でんわ|と|あ"},{"japanese":"先週送った問い合わせの返事がまだ来ません。","romaji":"Senshuu okutta toiawase no henji ga mada kimasen.","german":"I still haven't received a reply to the inquiry I sent last week.","furigana":"せんしゅうおく|と|あ|へんじ|き"}],"notes":"問い合わせ is an inquiry to a company or office to obtain information. In business Japanese it is almost always with お: お問い合わせください \"please contact us\", 問い合わせ先 \"contact address\", お問い合わせフォーム. The verb is 問い合わせる: 〜に〜を問い合わせる. 質問, by contrast, is any question, e.g. in class.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"受け付けで聞いてください。","romaji":"Uketsuke de kiite kudasai.","german":"Please ask at the reception desk.","furigana":"う|つ|き"},{"japanese":"申し込みの受け付けは今月末までです。","romaji":"Moushikomi no uketsuke wa kongetsu matsu made desu.","german":"Applications are accepted until the end of this month.","furigana":"もう|こ|う|つ|こんげつまつ"},{"japanese":"病院に着いたら、まず受け付けを済ませてください。","romaji":"Byouin ni tsuitara, mazu uketsuke o sumasete kudasai.","german":"When you arrive at the hospital, please first check in at reception.","furigana":"びょういん|つ|う|つ|す"}],"notes":"受け付け is on the one hand the reception or check-in desk in companies, clinics or offices, and on the other hand the acceptance of applications or registrations (受付時間, 受付中, 受付終了). For the counter and on signs, the spelling without okurigana, 受付, is the most common. The verb is 受け付ける \"to accept, to receive\"; at a hotel the front desk is usually フロント.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"知り合いに会いました。","romaji":"Shiriai ni aimashita.","german":"I met an acquaintance.","furigana":"し|あ|あ"},{"japanese":"知り合いの紹介でこの会社に入りました。","romaji":"Shiriai no shoukai de kono kaisha ni hairimashita.","german":"I got this job through an acquaintance's introduction.","furigana":"し|あ|しょうかい|かいしゃ|はい"},{"japanese":"彼とは友達というより、ただの知り合いです。","romaji":"Kare to wa tomodachi to iu yori, tada no shiriai desu.","german":"He is just an acquaintance rather than a friend.","furigana":"かれ|ともだち|し|あ"}],"notes":"知り合い is an acquaintance, someone you know without being close friends; as opposed to 友達. The verb 知り合う means \"to get to know each other\" (〜と知り合う), 知り合いになる \"to become acquainted\". In formal language one says 知人. Typical is 知り合いの紹介 \"an introduction through an acquaintance\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"親戚が集まりました。","romaji":"Shinseki ga atsumarimashita.","german":"The relatives gathered.","furigana":"しんせき|あつ"},{"japanese":"夏休みに田舎の親戚の家に泊まりました。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi ni inaka no shinseki no ie ni tomarimashita.","german":"During summer vacation, I stayed at a relative's house in the countryside.","furigana":"なつやす|いなか|しんせき|いえ|と"},{"japanese":"彼とは遠い親戚です。","romaji":"Kare to wa tooi shinseki desu.","german":"He and I are distant relatives.","furigana":"かれ|とお|しんせき"}],"notes":"親戚 are the relatives outside the immediate family (家族), such as uncles, aunts and cousins. Traditionally they gather at New Year, Obon, weddings and 法事. 遠い親戚 means \"distant relative\", 親戚付き合い \"contact with the relatives\". 親類 is a more formal synonym.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"息子の嫁は優しい人です。","romaji":"Musuko no yome wa yasashii hito desu.","german":"My son's wife is a kind person.","furigana":"むすこ|よめ|やさ|ひと"},{"japanese":"姉は来月、農家に嫁に行きます。","romaji":"Ane wa raigetsu, nouka ni yome ni ikimasu.","german":"My sister is marrying into a farming family next month.","furigana":"あね|らいげつ|のうか|よめ|い"},{"japanese":"うちの嫁は料理が上手なんです。","romaji":"Uchi no yome wa ryouri ga jouzu nan desu.","german":"My wife is really good at cooking.","furigana":"よめ|りょうり|じょうず"}],"notes":"嫁 is traditionally the bride or the daughter-in-law from the viewpoint of the husband's family: 息子の嫁. 嫁に行く (or 嫁ぐ) means \"to marry into a family\", 花嫁 is the bride. Colloquially - especially in western Japan - men say うちの嫁 for their own wife; some consider this old-fashioned. The neutral word is 妻, and for someone else's wife 奥さん.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"娘の婿はいい人です。","romaji":"Musume no muko wa ii hito desu.","german":"My daughter's husband is a good person.","furigana":"むすめ|むこ|ひと"},{"japanese":"花婿が緊張した顔で入場しました。","romaji":"Hanamuko ga kinchou shita kao de nyuujou shimashita.","german":"The bridegroom entered with a nervous expression.","furigana":"はなむこ|きんちょう|かお|にゅうじょう"},{"japanese":"彼は婿養子になって、妻の家の名字を名乗っています。","romaji":"Kare wa muko youshi ni natte, tsuma no ie no myouji o nanotte imasu.","german":"He was adopted into his wife's family and uses her family's surname.","furigana":"かれ|むこようし|つま|いえ|みょうじ|なの"}],"notes":"婿 is the son-in-law (娘の婿, also 娘婿) and the bridegroom (花婿; formal 新郎). 婿養子 is a man who marries into his wife's family and takes on its family name; 婿入り is the marrying-in itself. The counterpart is 嫁.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"姑と仲がいいです。","romaji":"Shūtome to naka ga ii desu.","german":"I get along well with my mother-in-law.","furigana":"しゅうとめ|なか"},{"japanese":"嫁と姑の関係は難しいとよく言われます。","romaji":"Yome to shuutome no kankei wa muzukashii to yoku iwaremasu.","german":"It is often said that the relationship between a daughter-in-law and her mother-in-law is difficult.","furigana":"よめ|しゅうとめ|かんけい|むずか|い"},{"japanese":"週末は子供を連れて姑の家に行きます。","romaji":"Shuumatsu wa kodomo o tsurete shuutome no ie ni ikimasu.","german":"On weekends I take the kids to my mother-in-law's house.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|こども|つ|しゅうとめ|いえ|い"}],"notes":"姑 is traditionally the husband's mother from the wife's point of view; the father-in-law is 舅 (しゅうと). The word is closely tied to the cliché of the 嫁姑問題 \"conflict between daughter-in-law and mother-in-law\". Today 姑 sounds somewhat old-fashioned; more neutral are 義母 (ぎぼ) and, as a form of address, お義母さん.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は一人っ子です。","romaji":"Watashi wa hitorikko desu.","german":"I am an only child.","furigana":"わたし|ひとり|こ"},{"japanese":"一人っ子なので、子供のころは兄弟がほしかったです。","romaji":"Hitorikko na node, kodomo no koro wa kyoudai ga hoshikatta desu.","german":"Since I'm an only child, I wanted siblings when I was little.","furigana":"ひとり|こ|こども|きょうだい"},{"japanese":"最近は一人っ子の家庭が増えています。","romaji":"Saikin wa hitorikko no katei ga fuete imasu.","german":"Recently, families with only one child are increasing.","furigana":"さいきん|ひとり|こ|かてい|ふ"}],"notes":"一人っ子 is an only child. The ending っ子 marks children or people of a certain kind: 末っ子 \"youngest child\", 江戸っ子 \"native Tokyoite\", 都会っ子 \"city kid\". To be distinguished are 一人娘 \"only daughter\" and 一人息子 \"only son\". Typical: 一人っ子の家庭 \"one-child family\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は長男です。","romaji":"Kare wa chōnan desu.","german":"He is the eldest son.","furigana":"かれ|ちょうなん"},{"japanese":"父は長男として家を継ぎました。","romaji":"Chichi wa chounan toshite ie o tsugimashita.","german":"My father took over the family home as the eldest son.","furigana":"ちち|ちょうなん|いえ|つ"},{"japanese":"うちの長男は今年小学校に入ります。","romaji":"Uchi no chounan wa kotoshi shougakkou ni hairimasu.","german":"Our eldest son starts elementary school this year.","furigana":"ちょうなん|ことししょうがっこう|はい"}],"notes":"長男 is the eldest son – even if he is the only one. It belongs to the series 長男, 次男, 三男 or 長女, 次女 and is used objectively for birth order, also on forms. 兄, by contrast, means \"older brother\" from the speaker's point of view. Traditionally the 長男 inherits the family home (家を継ぐ).","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 49"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"次男が大学に入りました。","romaji":"Jinan ga daigaku ni hairimashita.","german":"My second son entered university.","furigana":"じなん|だいがく|はい"},{"japanese":"私は三人兄弟の次男です。","romaji":"Watashi wa sannin kyoudai no jinan desu.","german":"I am the second of three brothers.","furigana":"わたし|さんにんきょうだい|じなん"},{"japanese":"家は長男が継いで、次男は東京で働いています。","romaji":"Ie wa chounan ga tsuide, jinan wa Toukyou de hataraite imasu.","german":"The eldest son took over the family home, and the second son works in Tokyo.","furigana":"いえ|ちょうなん|つ|じなん|とうきょう|はたら"}],"notes":"次男 is the second son (次 \"next\"). In official documents it is also written 二男, likewise read じなん. The female counterparts are 長女 and 次女; the third son is 三男. It is used to describe birth order among siblings: 三人兄弟の次男.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"末っ子は甘えん坊です。","romaji":"Suekko wa amaenbō desu.","german":"The youngest child is very clingy.","furigana":"すえ|こ|あま|ぼう"},{"japanese":"私は五人兄弟の末っ子です。","romaji":"Watashi wa gonin kyoudai no suekko desu.","german":"I am the youngest of five siblings.","furigana":"わたし|ごにんきょうだい|すえ|こ"},{"japanese":"末っ子なので、家族みんなにかわいがられて育ちました。","romaji":"Suekko na node, kazoku minna ni kawaigararete sodachimashita.","german":"Being the youngest, I grew up doted on by the whole family.","furigana":"すえ|こ|かぞく|そだ"}],"notes":"末っ子 is the youngest child of a family (末 \"end\" + っ子). Youngest children are said to be 甘えん坊 \"clingy, affectionate\" and to be pampered by everyone (かわいがられる). It applies to boys and girls alike; contrasting terms are 長男/長女 and 一人っ子.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"披露宴は六時からです。","romaji":"Hirōen wa rokuji kara desu.","german":"The reception starts at six.","furigana":"ひろうえん|ろくじ"},{"japanese":"結婚式の後、ホテルで披露宴が開かれました。","romaji":"Kekkonshiki no ato, hoteru de hirouen ga hirakaremashita.","german":"After the ceremony, a wedding reception was held at the hotel.","furigana":"けっこんしき|あと|ひろうえん|ひら"},{"japanese":"友人の披露宴でスピーチを頼まれました。","romaji":"Yuujin no hirouen de supiichi o tanomaremashita.","german":"I was asked to give a speech at a friend's wedding reception.","furigana":"ゆうじん|ひろうえん|たの"}],"notes":"披露宴 is the reception with food and speeches, usually after the wedding ceremony itself (結婚式); in full 結婚披露宴. 披露 means \"to announce publicly, to present\" - the couple is presented to friends and relatives. Typical: 披露宴を開く/行う, 披露宴に招待される; guests bring ご祝儀, and often a more casual party, the 二次会, follows.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"法事に出席します。","romaji":"Hōji ni shusseki shimasu.","german":"I will attend the memorial service.","furigana":"ほうじ|しゅっせき"},{"japanese":"祖父の法事で、久しぶりに親戚が集まりました。","romaji":"Sofu no houji de, hisashiburi ni shinseki ga atsumarimashita.","german":"The relatives gathered for the first time in a long while at my grandfather's memorial service.","furigana":"そふ|ほうじ|ひさ|しんせき|あつ"},{"japanese":"来週の土曜日は法事があるので、行けません。","romaji":"Raishuu no doyoubi wa houji ga aru node, ikemasen.","german":"There is a memorial service next Saturday, so I can't come.","furigana":"らいしゅう|どようび|ほうじ|い"}],"notes":"法事 is a Buddhist memorial service for the deceased, held on fixed dates such as the 49th day (四十九日) or the anniversaries 一周忌 and 三回忌. A monk recites sutras, after which the relatives eat together. Typical: 法事がある, 法事に出る/出席する; more formal is 法要. The funeral itself is called 葬式.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"成人式に着物を着ます。","romaji":"Seijinshiki ni kimono o kimasu.","german":"I will wear a kimono to the coming-of-age ceremony.","furigana":"せいじんしき|きもの|き"},{"japanese":"多くの町では、成人の日に成人式が行われます。","romaji":"Ooku no machi de wa, seijin no hi ni seijinshiki ga okonawaremasu.","german":"In many towns, the coming-of-age ceremony is held on Coming-of-Age Day.","furigana":"おお|まち|せいじん|ひ|せいじんしき|おこな"},{"japanese":"成人式で久しぶりに中学の同級生に会いました。","romaji":"Seijinshiki de hisashiburi ni chuugaku no doukyuusei ni aimashita.","german":"At the coming-of-age ceremony, I saw my junior high classmates for the first time in a long while.","furigana":"せいじんしき|ひさ|ちゅうがく|どうきゅうせい|あ"}],"notes":"成人式 is the ceremony for young adults organized by local municipalities, usually on or around the holiday 成人の日 in January. Women traditionally wear a 振袖, men a suit or hakama. Since 2022 people in Japan are adults at 18, but most municipalities still celebrate twenty-year-olds, often under the name 二十歳の集い.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 39"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"正月は実家に帰ります。","romaji":"Shōgatsu wa jikka ni kaerimasu.","german":"I go back to my parents' home for New Year.","furigana":"しょうがつ|じっか|かえ"},{"japanese":"お正月休みは何日までですか。","romaji":"Oshougatsu yasumi wa nannichi made desu ka.","german":"Until what date are the New Year holidays?","furigana":"しょうがつやす|なんにち"},{"japanese":"正月にはおせち料理を食べる習慣があります。","romaji":"Shougatsu ni wa osechi ryouri o taberu shuukan ga arimasu.","german":"It is a custom to eat osechi dishes at New Year.","furigana":"しょうがつ|りょうり|た|しゅうかん"}],"notes":"正月 is the New Year season, especially the first three days (三が日), when many Japanese visit family, go to shrines and eat special dishes. In daily life people mostly say the polite お正月; typical are 正月休み \"New Year holidays\" and 正月に実家に帰る. January 1st itself is 元日 or 元旦; 新年 is more the \"new year\" in greetings and formal texts (新年あけましておめでとうございます).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お年玉をもらいました。","romaji":"Otoshidama o moraimashita.","german":"I received New Year's money.","furigana":"としだま"},{"japanese":"甥にお年玉を五千円あげました。","romaji":"Oi ni otoshidama o gosen-en agemashita.","german":"I gave my nephew 5,000 yen as a New Year's gift.","furigana":"おい|としだま|ごせんえん"},{"japanese":"お年玉で新しいゲームを買うつもりです。","romaji":"Otoshidama de atarashii geemu o kau tsumori desu.","german":"I am going to buy a new game with my New Year's money.","furigana":"としだま|あたら|か"}],"notes":"お年玉 is the money that children receive at New Year from parents, grandparents and relatives, usually in a small decorated envelope (ポチ袋). The typical verbs are お年玉をもらう and (to someone) お年玉をあげる; the お is firmly part of the word, and 年玉 alone is practically never said. Figuratively, companies also call New Year offers お年玉キャンペーン, and お年玉付き年賀はがき are New Year's cards with a lottery number.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 41"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"雛祭りにお雛様を飾ります。","romaji":"Hinamatsuri ni ohinasama o kazarimasu.","german":"We display the dolls for the Girls' Day festival.","furigana":"ひなまつ|ひなさま|かざ"},{"japanese":"雛祭りには家族でちらし寿司を食べます。","romaji":"Hinamatsuri ni wa kazoku de chirashizushi o tabemasu.","german":"On Girls' Day, our family eats chirashi sushi.","furigana":"ひなまつ|かぞく|ずし|た"},{"japanese":"娘が生まれて、今年が初めての雛祭りです。","romaji":"Musume ga umarete, kotoshi ga hajimete no hinamatsuri desu.","german":"Our daughter was born, and this year is her first Girls' Day.","furigana":"むすめ|う|ことし|はじ|ひなまつ"}],"notes":"雛祭り is the Girls' Day or Doll Festival on March 3 (also 桃の節句). Families with daughters set up ひな人形 or お雛様 on a tiered platform and wish the girls health and happiness; people eat things like ちらし寿司 and ひなあられ. It is often written partly in kana as ひな祭り. The dolls are set up with 飾る and put away soon afterward - otherwise, it is said, the daughter will marry late.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"端午の節句に鯉のぼりを飾ります。","romaji":"Tango no sekku ni koinobori o kazarimasu.","german":"We display carp streamers for Boys' Day.","furigana":"たんご|せっく|こい|かざ"},{"japanese":"端午の節句には柏餅を食べます。","romaji":"Tango no sekku ni wa kashiwamochi o tabemasu.","german":"On Boys' Day, people eat kashiwa mochi.","furigana":"たんご|せっく||た"},{"japanese":"端午の節句は今、「こどもの日」という祝日です。","romaji":"Tango no sekku wa ima, Kodomo no Hi to iu shukujitsu desu.","german":"Boys' Day is now a public holiday called \"Children's Day\".","furigana":"たんご|せっく|いま|ひ|しゅくじつ"}],"notes":"端午の節句 is the traditional festival for boys on May 5; 端午 originally meant the first \"horse day\" of the fifth month, and 節句 is a seasonal festival. Today the day is a public holiday for all children, こどもの日. Typical are carp streamers (鯉のぼりを飾る/揚げる), samurai helmets (兜) and 柏餅. The expression is somewhat traditional; in daily life people usually just say こどもの日.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"稲妻が光りました。","romaji":"Inazuma ga hikarimashita.","german":"Lightning flashed.","furigana":"いなずま|ひか"},{"japanese":"夜空に稲妻が走りました。","romaji":"Yozora ni inazuma ga hashirimashita.","german":"A bolt of lightning ran across the night sky.","furigana":"よぞら|いなずま|はし"},{"japanese":"彼は稲妻のような速さでゴールまで走った。","romaji":"Kare wa inazuma no you na hayasa de gooru made hashitta.","german":"He ran to the finish line as fast as lightning.","furigana":"かれ|いなずま|はや|はし"}],"notes":"稲妻 is the visible bolt of lightning; literally \"rice spouse\", from the old belief that lightning makes the rice ripen. Typical: 稲妻が光る or 稲妻が走る \"a bolt of lightning flashes\". 雷, by contrast, refers to the whole thunderstorm phenomenon and especially the thunder (雷が鳴る); in daily life people often say 雷 or 稲光 instead of 稲妻. Figuratively, 稲妻のように means \"lightning-fast\". Slightly elevated, vivid style.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"雷が鳴っています。","romaji":"Kaminari ga natte imasu.","german":"Thunder is rumbling.","furigana":"かみなり|な"},{"japanese":"近くの木に雷が落ちました。","romaji":"Chikaku no ki ni kaminari ga ochimashita.","german":"Lightning struck a tree nearby.","furigana":"ちか|き|かみなり|お"},{"japanese":"宿題をしなかったので、父の雷が落ちました。","romaji":"Shukudai o shinakatta node, chichi no kaminari ga ochimashita.","german":"Because I hadn't done my homework, my father exploded at me.","furigana":"しゅくだい|ちち|かみなり|お"}],"notes":"雷 is the thunderstorm as a whole – thunder and lightning. Thunder \"rumbles\" with 雷が鳴る, a lightning strike is 雷が落ちる; the pure flash of light is more often called 稲妻 or 稲光. Figuratively, 雷 is an angry outburst: 父の雷が落ちる \"Father blew up\". Children often say 雷さま affectionately; 雷雨 is a thunderstorm with rain.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 39"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"霧で前が見えません。","romaji":"Kiri de mae ga miemasen.","german":"I can't see ahead because of the fog.","furigana":"きり|まえ|み"},{"japanese":"朝は濃い霧が出ていましたが、昼までに晴れました。","romaji":"Asa wa koi kiri ga dete imashita ga, hiru made ni haremashita.","german":"There was thick fog in the morning, but it cleared by noon.","furigana":"あさ|こ|きり|で|ひる|は"},{"japanese":"霧のため、飛行機の出発が遅れています。","romaji":"Kiri no tame, hikouki no shuppatsu ga okurete imasu.","german":"Because of the fog, the flight's departure is delayed.","furigana":"きり|ひこうき|しゅっぱつ|おく"}],"notes":"霧 is fog. It \"forms\" with 霧が出る or 霧がかかる, is dense with 霧が濃い and clears with 霧が晴れる. As a cause, 霧で or, more formally, 霧のため is used. Light haze is もや, the milky spring haze かすみ. 霧 is also found in 霧雨 \"drizzle\" and 霧吹き \"spray bottle\" (in the sense of \"spray mist\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"霜が降りました。","romaji":"Shimo ga orimashita.","german":"Frost has formed.","furigana":"しも|お"},{"japanese":"冷凍庫の中に霜がたくさんついています。","romaji":"Reitouko no naka ni shimo ga takusan tsuite imasu.","german":"A lot of frost has built up inside the freezer.","furigana":"れいとうこ|なか|しも"},{"japanese":"霜が降りる前に、野菜を収穫しましょう。","romaji":"Shimo ga oriru mae ni, yasai o shuukaku shimashou.","german":"Let's harvest the vegetables before the frost comes.","furigana":"しも|お|まえ|やさい|しゅうかく"}],"notes":"霜 is rime or ground frost. The fixed phrase is 霜が降りる \"frost forms\" - not 降る as with rain. Related are 初霜 \"first frost\", 霜柱 \"needle ice in the ground\" and 霜焼け \"chilblains\". Ice in the freezer is also 霜 (霜がつく). Poetically, 霜 can refer to gray hair. For below-zero cold in general, 氷点下 is used more.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"吹雪で外に出られません。","romaji":"Fubuki de soto ni deraremasen.","german":"I can't go outside because of the snowstorm.","furigana":"ふぶき|そと|で"},{"japanese":"山の天気が急に変わって、吹雪になりました。","romaji":"Yama no tenki ga kyuu ni kawatte, fubuki ni narimashita.","german":"The weather in the mountains suddenly changed, and it turned into a snowstorm.","furigana":"やま|てんき|きゅう|か|ふぶき"},{"japanese":"公園で桜の花吹雪を見ました。","romaji":"Kouen de sakura no hanafubuki o mimashita.","german":"In the park, I saw a shower of cherry blossom petals.","furigana":"こうえん|さくら|はなふぶき|み"}],"notes":"吹雪 is a snowstorm, that is, snow with strong wind; literally \"blowing snow\". Typical: 吹雪になる, 吹雪が続く, intensified 猛吹雪. Pure heavy snowfall without wind is 大雪. Figuratively, one speaks of 花吹雪 when cherry blossom petals swirl through the air like snow. The related verb 吹雪く \"to storm and snow\" is rare.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"湿度が高いです。","romaji":"Shitsudo ga takai desu.","german":"The humidity is high.","furigana":"しつど|たか"},{"japanese":"今日の湿度は八十パーセントもあります。","romaji":"Kyou no shitsudo wa hachijuu paasento mo arimasu.","german":"Today's humidity is as high as 80 percent.","furigana":"きょう|しつど|はちじゅう"},{"japanese":"冬は湿度が低いので、加湿器を使っています。","romaji":"Fuyu wa shitsudo ga hikui node, kashitsuki o tsukatte imasu.","german":"The air is dry in winter, so I use a humidifier.","furigana":"ふゆ|しつど|ひく|かしつき|つか"}],"notes":"湿度 is the measured humidity of the air, usually in percent: 湿度が高い/低い, 湿度は80パーセント. For felt dampness or moisture indoors, one says 湿気 (湿気が多い, 湿気でカビが生える). The counterpart to temperature is 気温, together in weather reports: 気温と湿度. A neutral, somewhat technical term.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"気圧が下がっています。","romaji":"Kiatsu ga sagatte imasu.","german":"The atmospheric pressure is dropping.","furigana":"きあつ|さ"},{"japanese":"気圧の変化で頭が痛くなることがあります。","romaji":"Kiatsu no henka de atama ga itaku naru koto ga arimasu.","german":"Changes in air pressure sometimes give me a headache.","furigana":"きあつ|へんか|あたま|いた"},{"japanese":"山の上は気圧が低いので、お菓子の袋がふくらみます。","romaji":"Yama no ue wa kiatsu ga hikui node, okashi no fukuro ga fukuramimasu.","german":"The air pressure is low at the top of the mountain, so snack bags puff up.","furigana":"やま|うえ|きあつ|ひく|かし|ふくろ"}],"notes":"気圧 is atmospheric pressure: 気圧が高い/低い, 気圧が下がる/上がる. High- and low-pressure areas are 高気圧 and 低気圧; 低気圧 often stands for bad weather in everyday speech. In Japan, it is common to attribute headaches or tiredness to 気圧の変化. As a unit (\"atmosphere\"), 気圧 appears in physics, e.g. 1気圧.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"天気予報を見ます。","romaji":"Tenki yohō o mimasu.","german":"I watch the weather forecast.","furigana":"てんきよほう|み"},{"japanese":"天気予報によると、明日は雨が降るそうです。","romaji":"Tenki yohou ni yoru to, ashita wa ame ga furu sou desu.","german":"According to the weather forecast, it's going to rain tomorrow.","furigana":"てんきよほう|あした|あめ|ふ"},{"japanese":"今日は天気予報が外れて、一日中晴れました。","romaji":"Kyou wa tenki yohou ga hazurete, ichinichijuu haremashita.","german":"Today the weather forecast was wrong; it was sunny all day.","furigana":"きょう|てんきよほう|はず|いちにちじゅうは"}],"notes":"天気予報 is the weather forecast on TV, radio or in an app. You watch or hear it (天気予報を見る/聞く) and usually report its content with 天気予報によると…そうです or 天気予報では…. When the forecast is right, you say 天気予報が当たる; when it is wrong, 天気予報が外れる. In conversation it is often shortened to 予報.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 8"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"明日の予報は雨です。","romaji":"Ashita no yohō wa ame desu.","german":"Tomorrow's forecast is rain.","furigana":"あした|よほう|あめ"},{"japanese":"天気予報によると、午後から雪が降るそうです。","romaji":"Tenki yohou ni yoru to, gogo kara yuki ga furu sou desu.","german":"According to the weather forecast, it will snow from the afternoon.","furigana":"てんきよほう|ごご|ゆき|ふ"},{"japanese":"今日は予報が外れて、一日中晴れました。","romaji":"Kyou wa yohou ga hazurete, ichinichijuu haremashita.","german":"Today the forecast was wrong; it was sunny all day.","furigana":"きょう|よほう|はず|いちにちじゅうは"}],"notes":"予報 is an official forecast, above all in 天気予報 \"weather forecast\". Typical: 予報によると…, 予報が当たる \"the forecast is right\", 予報が外れる \"the forecast is wrong\". As a する-verb (〜を予報する) it sounds technical. To distinguish: 予想 is a general expectation or guess (試合の予想), 予測 a calculated prediction (経済の予測).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日差しが強いです。","romaji":"Hizashi ga tsuyoi desu.","german":"The sunlight is strong.","furigana":"ひざ|つよ"},{"japanese":"春の暖かい日差しを浴びて、散歩しました。","romaji":"Haru no atatakai hizashi o abite, sanpo shimashita.","german":"I went for a walk in the warm spring sunshine.","furigana":"はる|あたた|ひざ|あ|さんぽ"},{"japanese":"日差しを避けるために、帽子をかぶってください。","romaji":"Hizashi o sakeru tame ni, boushi o kabutte kudasai.","german":"Please wear a hat to avoid the sun.","furigana":"ひざ|さ|ぼうし"}],"notes":"日差し is direct incoming sunlight, as you feel it on your skin: 日差しが強い, 日差しがまぶしい, 日差しを浴びる \"to bask in the sun\", 日差しを避ける. Often also written 陽射し. 日光 is more neutral or technical \"sunlight\", 日当たり describes how sunny a home is (日当たりがいい), and 日なた is a sunny spot.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"木陰で休みましょう。","romaji":"Kokage de yasumimashō.","german":"Let's rest in the shade of the trees.","furigana":"こかげ|やす"},{"japanese":"木陰のベンチで本を読むのが好きです。","romaji":"Kokage no benchi de hon o yomu no ga suki desu.","german":"I like reading on a bench in the shade of the trees.","furigana":"こかげ|ほん|よ|す"},{"japanese":"車を木陰に止めました。","romaji":"Kuruma o kokage ni tomemashita.","german":"I parked the car in the shade of a tree.","furigana":"くるま|こかげ|と"}],"notes":"木陰 is the shade under a tree, a pleasantly cool place: 木陰で休む, 木陰に入る, 木陰のベンチ. The more general \"shade, where the sun doesn't reach\" is 日陰 (e.g. behind buildings). 影, by contrast, is the silhouette that an object casts. 木陰 sounds slightly picturesque but is quite common in everyday use.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"そよ風が気持ちいいです。","romaji":"Soyokaze ga kimochi ii desu.","german":"The gentle breeze feels good.","furigana":"かぜ|きも"},{"japanese":"窓を開けると、そよ風が入ってきました。","romaji":"Mado o akeru to, soyokaze ga haitte kimashita.","german":"When I opened the window, a gentle breeze came in.","furigana":"まど|あ|かぜ|はい"},{"japanese":"海辺でそよ風に吹かれながら、本を読みました。","romaji":"Umibe de soyokaze ni fukarenagara, hon o yomimashita.","german":"At the beach, I read a book with a light breeze blowing past me.","furigana":"うみべ|かぜ|ふ|ほん|よ"}],"notes":"そよ風 is a gentle, pleasant breeze; related to the sound-symbolic そよそよ \"blowing softly\". Typical: そよ風が吹く, そよ風が気持ちいい, そよ風に吹かれる \"to let the breeze blow over oneself\". The word has a positive, slightly poetic ring. 微風 is the literary equivalent, while strong wind is 強風.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日は蒸し暑いですね。","romaji":"Kyō wa mushiatsui desu ne.","german":"It's muggy today, isn't it?","furigana":"きょう|む|あつ"},{"japanese":"日本の夏は蒸し暑くて、夜もよく眠れません。","romaji":"Nihon no natsu wa mushiatsukute, yoru mo yoku nemuremasen.","german":"Japanese summers are so muggy that I can't sleep well even at night.","furigana":"にほん|なつ|む|あつ|よる|ねむ"},{"japanese":"蒸し暑い部屋で会議をするのはつらかったです。","romaji":"Mushiatsui heya de kaigi o suru no wa tsurakatta desu.","german":"Having the meeting in the muggy room was tough.","furigana":"む|あつ|へや|かいぎ"}],"notes":"蒸し暑い means \"muggy\", literally \"steaming hot\" - the typical weather of the Japanese summer and rainy season. A popular small-talk phrase is 蒸し暑いですね. Merely hot is 暑い; じめじめ describes damp, clammy wetness, 暑苦しい an oppressive, stuffy heat (also figuratively: overbearing). As an i-adjective: 蒸し暑くて, 蒸し暑かった.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今朝は肌寒いです。","romaji":"Kesa wa hadasamui desu.","german":"It's chilly this morning.","furigana":"けさ|はださむ"},{"japanese":"夜になると肌寒いので、上着を持っていったほうがいいですよ。","romaji":"Yoru ni naru to hadasamui node, uwagi o motte itta hou ga ii desu yo.","german":"It gets chilly at night, so you had better take a jacket.","furigana":"よる|はださむ|うわぎ|も"},{"japanese":"雨の日は、春でも少し肌寒く感じます。","romaji":"Ame no hi wa, haru demo sukoshi hadasamuku kanjimasu.","german":"On rainy days, it feels a bit chilly even in spring.","furigana":"あめ|ひ|はる|すこ|はださむ|かん"}],"notes":"肌寒い describes a slight, unpleasant coolness felt on the skin - typical for mornings and evenings in spring or autumn and on rainy days. It is weaker than 寒い and, unlike 涼しい \"pleasantly cool\", slightly negative. Common: 肌寒い朝, 肌寒く感じる, 少し肌寒い. In winter, with real cold, one does not say 肌寒い but 寒い.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"穏やかな天気です。","romaji":"Odayaka na tenki desu.","german":"The weather is mild.","furigana":"おだ|てんき"},{"japanese":"祖父はとても穏やかな人で、怒ったことがありません。","romaji":"Sofu wa totemo odayaka na hito de, okotta koto ga arimasen.","german":"My grandfather is a very gentle person and has never gotten angry.","furigana":"そふ|おだ|ひと|おこ"},{"japanese":"退職後は田舎で穏やかに暮らしたいです。","romaji":"Taishokugo wa inaka de odayaka ni kurashitai desu.","german":"After retirement, I want to live a quiet life in the countryside.","furigana":"たいしょくご|いなか|おだ|く"}],"notes":"穏やか is a な-adjective for anything calm without violent swings: weather and sea (穏やかな天気, 海が穏やかだ), character (穏やかな人 \"gentle person\") and way of life (穏やかに暮らす). In the second sense it means \"moderate, peaceful\": 穏やかに話し合う; 穏やかではない means \"disturbing, worrying\". 静か, by contrast, emphasizes silence above all, i.e. little noise.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"甚だしい誤解です。","romaji":"Hanahadashii gokai desu.","german":"It is a gross misunderstanding.","furigana":"はなは|ごかい"},{"japanese":"台風による被害は甚だしいものでした。","romaji":"Taifuu ni yoru higai wa hanahadashii mono deshita.","german":"The damage caused by the typhoon was enormous.","furigana":"たいふう|ひがい|はなは"},{"japanese":"自分が一番だと思うなんて、勘違いも甚だしい。","romaji":"Jibun ga ichiban da to omou nante, kanchigai mo hanahadashii.","german":"Thinking you are the best - what an extreme misjudgment.","furigana":"じぶん|いちばん|おも|かんちが|はなは"}],"notes":"甚だしい means \"to an extreme degree\" and is used almost always with something negative: 甚だしい誤解, 被害が甚だしい, 甚だしい違い. The pattern 〜も甚だしい is common as a sharp rebuke: 勘違いも甚だしい \"what a gross misjudgment\", 非常識も甚だしい. The style is literary to formal; in conversation one says ひどい or すごい instead. The related adverb is 甚だ \"extremely\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"雨が続いて鬱陶しいです。","romaji":"Ame ga tsuzuite uttōshii desu.","german":"The continuing rain is gloomy.","furigana":"あめ|つづ|うっとう"},{"japanese":"前髪が伸びて鬱陶しいので、切りに行きます。","romaji":"Maegami ga nobite uttoushii node, kiri ni ikimasu.","german":"My bangs have grown so long that they're annoying, so I'm going to get them cut.","furigana":"まえがみ|の|うっとう|き|い"},{"japanese":"何度も同じことを聞かれて、鬱陶しく感じた。","romaji":"Nando mo onaji koto o kikarete, uttoushiku kanjita.","german":"Being asked the same thing over and over felt irritating.","furigana":"なんど|おな|き|うっとう|かん"}],"notes":"鬱陶しい is usually written in kana as うっとうしい. It describes, on the one hand, what is gloomy and oppressive (梅雨の鬱陶しい天気, 気分が鬱陶しい) and, on the other, what gets on one's nerves: 前髪が鬱陶しい, ハエが鬱陶しい. Said about people (あの人、鬱陶しい) it is rather rude. 煩わしい aims more at tedious procedures, うるさい at noise or pushiness.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"煩わしい手続きです。","romaji":"Wazurawashii tetsuzuki desu.","german":"It's a tedious procedure.","furigana":"わずら|てつづ"},{"japanese":"近所付き合いが煩わしいと感じる人も多いです。","romaji":"Kinjo zukiai ga wazurawashii to kanjiru hito mo ooi desu.","german":"Many people find socializing with the neighbors troublesome.","furigana":"きんじょづ|あ|わずら|かん|ひと|おお"},{"japanese":"スマホの通知が煩わしいので、オフにしました。","romaji":"Sumaho no tsuuchi ga wazurawashii node, ofu ni shimashita.","german":"The smartphone notifications were bothersome, so I turned them off.","furigana":"つうち|わずら"}],"notes":"煩わしい means \"annoying, laborious\", especially for cumbersome procedures and obligations: 煩わしい手続き, 人間関係が煩わしい, 近所付き合いが煩わしい. It sounds more matter-of-fact and somewhat more refined than the colloquial 面倒くさい; 鬱陶しい, by contrast, is more emotional \"irritating\". Related is the verb 煩わす, e.g. in the polite phrase お手を煩わせてすみません \"sorry to trouble you\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"清々しい朝です。","romaji":"Sugasugashii asa desu.","german":"It's a refreshing morning.","furigana":"すがすが|あさ"},{"japanese":"山の上の空気は清々しいですね。","romaji":"Yama no ue no kuuki wa sugasugashii desu ne.","german":"The air up on the mountain is refreshing, isn't it?","furigana":"やま|うえ|くうき|すがすが"},{"japanese":"試験が全部終わって、清々しい気分です。","romaji":"Shiken ga zenbu owatte, sugasugashii kibun desu.","german":"All my exams are over, and I feel refreshed.","furigana":"しけん|ぜんぶお|すがすが|きぶん"}],"notes":"清々しい describes a clear, pure freshness that revives body and mind: 清々しい朝, 清々しい空気, and the liberated feeling after work is done (清々しい気分). People or their behavior can also come across as 清々しい, for example an open smile. さわやか is similar, but more everyday and broader (also taste, weather); 清々しい puts more emphasis on clarity and purity. Often written in kana as すがすがしい.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"気まずい雰囲気です。","romaji":"Kimazui fun'iki desu.","german":"The atmosphere is awkward.","furigana":"き|ふんいき"},{"japanese":"けんかしてから、彼とは気まずくなってしまいました。","romaji":"Kenka shite kara, kare to wa kimazuku natte shimaimashita.","german":"Since we fought, things have become awkward between him and me.","furigana":"かれ|き"},{"japanese":"エレベーターで昔の恋人に会って、気まずかったです。","romaji":"Erebeetaa de mukashi no koibito ni atte, kimazukatta desu.","german":"I ran into my ex in the elevator, and it was awkward.","furigana":"むかし|こいびと|あ|き"}],"notes":"気まずい describes the unpleasant, tense mood between people: 気まずい雰囲気, 気まずい沈黙, (〜と) 気まずくなる \"to become estranged from someone\". So it concerns the relationship, not personal shame - for one's own embarrassment, one says 恥ずかしい. Typical after quarrels, embarrassing encounters or when nobody says anything.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"素早い対応をありがとう。","romaji":"Subayai taiō o arigatō.","german":"Thank you for the quick response.","furigana":"すばや|たいおう"},{"japanese":"猫は素早くネズミを捕まえました。","romaji":"Neko wa subayaku nezumi o tsukamaemashita.","german":"The cat caught the mouse in a flash.","furigana":"ねこ|すばや|つか"},{"japanese":"事故のとき、運転手の素早い判断で、大きなけがをせずに済みました。","romaji":"Jiko no toki, untenshu no subayai handan de, ookina kega o sezu ni sumimashita.","german":"Thanks to the driver's quick decision, the accident ended without serious injuries.","furigana":"じこ|うんてんしゅ|すばや|はんだん|おお|す"}],"notes":"素早い means \"nimble, swift\" and describes quick movements or prompt reactions: 素早い動き, 素早い判断, 素早い対応; very often adverbial 素早く (素早く逃げる). 速い is generally \"fast\" (speed), 早い \"early\". A relative is すばしこい \"nimble, agile\", mostly for small animals or children. In business emails: 素早いご対応ありがとうございます.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"反応が鈍いです。","romaji":"Hannō ga nibui desu.","german":"The reaction is slow.","furigana":"はんのう|にぶ"},{"japanese":"朝から頭に鈍い痛みがあります。","romaji":"Asa kara atama ni nibui itami ga arimasu.","german":"I've had a dull pain in my head since this morning.","furigana":"あさ|あたま|にぶ|いた"},{"japanese":"彼は鈍いから、彼女の気持ちに全然気づいていない。","romaji":"Kare wa nibui kara, kanojo no kimochi ni zenzen kizuite inai.","german":"He is so dense that he doesn't notice her feelings at all.","furigana":"かれ|にぶ|かのじょ|きも|ぜんぜんき"}],"notes":"鈍い is the opposite of 鋭い and has several meanings: blunt (鈍い刃), dull (鈍い痛み, 鈍い音), sluggish (動きが鈍い, 反応が鈍い) and slow on the uptake, especially in matters of feeling (彼は鈍い). For people, 鈍感 \"insensitive\" is also common. Note: the same kanji is read のろい \"slow, sluggish\"; にぶい is the standard reading.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鋭い目つきです。","romaji":"Surudoi metsuki desu.","german":"He has a sharp look in his eyes.","furigana":"するど|め"},{"japanese":"猫の爪は鋭いので、気をつけてください。","romaji":"Neko no tsume wa surudoi node, ki o tsukete kudasai.","german":"Cat claws are sharp, so please be careful.","furigana":"ねこ|つめ|するど|き"},{"japanese":"母は勘が鋭くて、うそをついてもすぐにばれます。","romaji":"Haha wa kan ga surudokute, uso o tsuite mo sugu ni baremasu.","german":"My mother has sharp instincts; if I lie, she finds out right away.","furigana":"はは|かん|するど"}],"notes":"鋭い first means \"sharp, pointed\" for blades and claws (鋭いナイフ, 鋭い爪). Figuratively it stands for sharpness in expression - 鋭い痛み \"stabbing pain\", 鋭い批判, 鋭い質問, 鋭い目つき - and for a keen mind: 勘が鋭い \"to have good instincts\", 鋭い観察. The opposite is 鈍い. For food in the sense of \"spicy\", 辛い is used instead.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"粗い布です。","romaji":"Arai nuno desu.","german":"It is a coarse cloth.","furigana":"あら|ぬの"},{"japanese":"この写真は画像が粗くて、顔がよく見えません。","romaji":"Kono shashin wa gazou ga arakute, kao ga yoku miemasen.","german":"The image in this photo is coarse, and I can hardly see the face.","furigana":"しゃしん|がぞう|あら|かお|み"},{"japanese":"彼の仕事は速いけれど、少し粗いです。","romaji":"Kare no shigoto wa hayai keredo, sukoshi arai desu.","german":"He works fast, but he is a bit sloppy.","furigana":"かれ|しごと|はや|すこ|あら"}],"notes":"粗い means \"coarse\" in the sense of coarse-grained or loosely woven: 粒が粗い, 目が粗い布, 画像が粗い \"pixelated\"; figuratively also \"sloppy\" (仕事が粗い). Beware of the identically read 荒い \"rough, violent\": 波が荒い, 言葉遣いが荒い, 金遣いが荒い. Rule of thumb: 粗い = not fine, 荒い = wild, rough. The opposite of 粗い is 細かい.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"滑らかな肌です。","romaji":"Nameraka na hada desu.","german":"She has lovely smooth skin.","furigana":"なめ|はだ"},{"japanese":"彼は日本語を滑らかに話します。","romaji":"Kare wa nihongo o nameraka ni hanashimasu.","german":"He speaks Japanese fluently.","furigana":"かれ|にほんご|なめ|はな"},{"japanese":"このプリンはとても滑らかで、口の中ですぐに溶けます。","romaji":"Kono purin wa totemo nameraka de, kuchi no naka de sugu ni tokemasu.","german":"This pudding is so smooth that it melts in your mouth right away.","furigana":"なめ|くち|なか|と"}],"notes":"滑らか is a な-adjective and is often written in kana as なめらか. It describes smooth, even surfaces and textures (滑らかな肌, 滑らかなクリーム) as well as flowing processes: 滑らかに話す \"to speak fluently\", 動きが滑らか. Note: the same kanji is in 滑る \"to slip\". In everyday speech, for smooth-running processes people also say スムーズ, and for a smooth feel the onomatopoeic すべすべ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"脆い材料です。","romaji":"Moroi zairyō desu.","german":"It's a brittle material.","furigana":"もろ|ざいりょう"},{"japanese":"年を取ると骨が脆くなります。","romaji":"Toshi o toru to hone ga moroku narimasu.","german":"Bones become brittle with age.","furigana":"とし|と|ほね|もろ"},{"japanese":"彼女は涙もろくて、映画を見るとすぐ泣きます。","romaji":"Kanojo wa namidamorokute, eiga o miru to sugu nakimasu.","german":"She is soft-hearted and cries right away at movies.","furigana":"かのじょ|なみだ|えいが|み|な"}],"notes":"脆い, mostly in kana もろい, means \"brittle, easily broken\": 脆いガラス, 骨が脆くなる, 地盤が脆い. Figuratively it stands for emotional softness, especially in compounds: 涙もろい \"easily moved to tears\", 情にもろい \"easily touched\". In everyday speech, for objects people often just say 壊れやすい; 弱い is generally \"weak\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"曖昧な返事をしました。","romaji":"Aimai na henji o shimashita.","german":"I gave a vague reply.","furigana":"あいまい|へんじ"},{"japanese":"子どもの頃のことなので、記憶が曖昧です。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro no koto na node, kioku ga aimai desu.","german":"It was in my childhood, so my memory is hazy.","furigana":"こ|ころ|きおく|あいまい"},{"japanese":"大事な問題を曖昧なままにしないでください。","romaji":"Daiji na mondai o aimai na mama ni shinaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't leave important issues unresolved.","furigana":"だいじ|もんだい|あいまい"}],"notes":"曖昧 is a na-adjective for the unclear or ambiguous: 曖昧な返事, 曖昧な態度, 記憶が曖昧 \"hazy memory\"; 曖昧にする means \"to deliberately leave something open\". Often written in kana, あいまい. In conversation, はっきりしない is equivalent. 漠然, by contrast, describes a diffuse overall picture without clear contours (漠然とした不安), not an evasive statement.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"率直な意見をお願いします。","romaji":"Socchoku na iken o onegai shimasu.","german":"Please give me your frank opinion.","furigana":"そっちょく|いけん|ねが"},{"japanese":"率直に言うと、その計画には反対です。","romaji":"Socchoku ni iu to, sono keikaku ni wa hantai desu.","german":"To be frank, I am against that plan.","furigana":"そっちょく|い|けいかく|はんたい"},{"japanese":"彼女は率直な人なので、何でもはっきり言います。","romaji":"Kanojo wa socchoku na hito na node, nan demo hakkiri iimasu.","german":"She is a frank person, so she says everything clearly.","furigana":"かのじょ|そっちょく|ひと|なん|い"}],"notes":"率直 means \"open, candid, direct\" and is positive: 率直な意見, 率直な感想, 率直に話す. The fixed phrase 率直に言うと / 率直に言って introduces an open, often critical statement (\"frankly speaking\"). 正直, by contrast, emphasizes honesty in the sense of \"not lying\"; はっきり is more everyday and describes only clarity. Neutral to polite, common in meetings.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"素朴な疑問があります。","romaji":"Soboku na gimon ga arimasu.","german":"I have a simple question.","furigana":"そぼく|ぎもん"},{"japanese":"このお菓子は素朴な味がして、懐かしいです。","romaji":"Kono okashi wa soboku na aji ga shite, natsukashii desu.","german":"This sweet has a simple flavor and makes me nostalgic.","furigana":"かし|そぼく|あじ|なつ"},{"japanese":"村の人たちは素朴で親切でした。","romaji":"Mura no hitotachi wa soboku de shinsetsu deshita.","german":"The villagers were simple and kind.","furigana":"むら|ひと|そぼく|しんせつ"}],"notes":"素朴 describes what is simple and unaffected, with a positive undertone: 素朴な味 \"simple, honest flavor\", 素朴な人柄, 素朴な村. The set phrase 素朴な疑問 is a naive, very basic question that one simply asks oneself. 単純, by contrast, can mean \"simplistic\" in a negative sense, 質素 means \"frugal, modest\" (lifestyle), and シンプル stresses plain design.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"莫大な費用がかかります。","romaji":"Bakudai na hiyō ga kakarimasu.","german":"It will cost a fortune.","furigana":"ばくだい|ひよう"},{"japanese":"彼は親から莫大な財産を受け継ぎました。","romaji":"Kare wa oya kara bakudai na zaisan o uketsugimashita.","german":"He inherited a huge fortune from his parents.","furigana":"かれ|おや|ばくだい|ざいさん|う|つ"},{"japanese":"地震で莫大な損害が出ました。","romaji":"Jishin de bakudai na songai ga demashita.","german":"The earthquake caused enormous damage.","furigana":"じしん|ばくだい|そんがい|で"}],"notes":"莫大 refers to huge amounts, above all of money, costs, damage or data: 莫大な費用, 莫大な財産, 莫大な借金, 莫大な損害. For physical size (a huge building), 巨大 is used instead. The word almost always stands attributively with な before the noun and sounds matter-of-fact to written-language; in conversation people often say すごい額 or ものすごく.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"明白な事実です。","romaji":"Meihaku na jijitsu desu.","german":"It's an obvious fact.","furigana":"めいはく|じじつ"},{"japanese":"彼がうそをついていることは明白です。","romaji":"Kare ga uso o tsuite iru koto wa meihaku desu.","german":"It's obvious that he is lying.","furigana":"かれ|めいはく"},{"japanese":"これは明白なルール違反です。","romaji":"Kore wa meihaku na ruuru ihan desu.","german":"This is a clear violation of the rules.","furigana":"めいはく|いはん"}],"notes":"明白 means \"evident, unmistakable\" and sounds formal, for example in reports, debates or in court: 明白な事実, 明白な証拠, 明白なルール違反, 〜ことは明白だ. In everyday speech people usually say 明らか or, colloquially, はっきりしている. The opposite would be 曖昧 or 不明.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地道な努力が実を結びました。","romaji":"Jimichi na doryoku ga mi o musubimashita.","german":"Steady effort bore fruit.","furigana":"じみち|どりょく|み|むす"},{"japanese":"毎日地道に単語を覚えています。","romaji":"Mainichi jimichi ni tango o oboete imasu.","german":"I memorize vocabulary steadily every day.","furigana":"まいにちじみち|たんご|おぼ"},{"japanese":"彼は派手ではないが、地道に仕事をする人だ。","romaji":"Kare wa hade de wa nai ga, jimichi ni shigoto o suru hito da.","german":"He isn't flashy, but he is someone who works steadily.","furigana":"かれ|はで|じみち|しごと|ひと"}],"notes":"地道 praises persistent, unspectacular work step by step: 地道な努力, 地道な作業, 地道に続ける/働く. It has an appreciative tone - someone works without show, but reliably. Similar is こつこつ \"bit by bit\"; 着実 emphasizes sure progress. The opposite would be 派手 \"flashy\" or quick success.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"手軽に作れます。","romaji":"Tegaru ni tsukuremasu.","german":"It can be made easily.","furigana":"てがる|つく"},{"japanese":"スマホで手軽に写真を編集できます。","romaji":"Sumaho de tegaru ni shashin o henshuu dekimasu.","german":"You can easily edit photos on a smartphone.","furigana":"てがる|しゃしん|へんしゅう"},{"japanese":"昼ご飯は手軽にサンドイッチで済ませました。","romaji":"Hirugohan wa tegaru ni sandoicchi de sumasemashita.","german":"For lunch I kept it simple with a sandwich.","furigana":"ひる|はん|てがる|す"}],"notes":"手軽 means \"without much effort, uncomplicated, inexpensive\" and refers to effort, time or cost: 手軽に作れる, 手軽な料理, 手軽な値段; in advertising often お手軽. To be distinguished from 気軽, which describes the inner threshold (\"relaxed, without hesitation\"). So: 手軽に作れる料理 (little effort), but 気軽に聞いてください (without reserve).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"気軽に聞いてください。","romaji":"Kigaru ni kiite kudasai.","german":"Please feel free to ask.","furigana":"きがる|き"},{"japanese":"彼女は気軽に話しかけられる先輩です。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kigaru ni hanashikakerareru senpai desu.","german":"She is a senior colleague you can talk to casually.","furigana":"かのじょ|きがる|はな|せんぱい"},{"japanese":"このカフェは一人でも気軽に入れます。","romaji":"Kono kafe wa hitori demo kigaru ni hairemasu.","german":"You can go into this cafe easily, even alone.","furigana":"ひとり|きがる|はい"}],"notes":"気軽 means \"carefree, without reserve\" and describes the inner attitude: 気軽に話しかける, 気軽に参加する. Very common is the polite service phrase お気軽にどうぞ / お気軽にお問い合わせください \"feel free to contact us any time\". Do not confuse it with 手軽, which refers to little effort or a low price (手軽な料理).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"呆然と立ち尽くしました。","romaji":"Bōzen to tachitsukushimashita.","german":"I stood there stunned.","furigana":"ぼうぜん|た|つ"},{"japanese":"試験に落ちたと知って、呆然としました。","romaji":"Shiken ni ochita to shitte, bouzen to shimashita.","german":"When I found out I'd failed the exam, I was dumbfounded.","furigana":"しけん|お|し|ぼうぜん"},{"japanese":"家が火事で焼けてしまい、家族は呆然とした顔をしていた。","romaji":"Ie ga kaji de yakete shimai, kazoku wa bouzen to shita kao o shite ita.","german":"The family's house had burned down, and they had stunned looks on their faces.","furigana":"いえ|かじ|や|かぞく|ぼうぜん|かお"}],"notes":"呆然 describes the state of being paralyzed with shock or surprise. It is not used with な but with と: 呆然とする, 呆然と立ち尽くす, 呆然と見つめる, attributively 呆然とした顔. The spelling 茫然 is equivalent and often used interchangeably; 呆然 stresses the shock more. Slightly literary; in conversation people often say びっくりして何も言えなかった.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"茫然と外を見ています。","romaji":"Bōzen to soto o mite imasu.","german":"I'm gazing absent-mindedly outside.","furigana":"ぼうぜん|そと|み"},{"japanese":"突然の別れに、彼は茫然自失の状態だった。","romaji":"Totsuzen no wakare ni, kare wa bouzen jishitsu no joutai datta.","german":"After the sudden breakup, he was in a state of complete daze.","furigana":"とつぜん|わか|かれ|ぼうぜんじしつ|じょうたい"},{"japanese":"何をすればいいのか分からず、茫然としていました。","romaji":"Nani o sureba ii no ka wakarazu, bouzen to shite imashita.","german":"I didn't know what to do and was standing there in a daze.","furigana":"なに|わ|ぼうぜん"}],"notes":"茫然 is a spelling variant of 呆然 with the same reading; the kanji 茫 originally means \"vast, boundless\". Therefore 茫然 rather stresses the empty, absent-minded staring into space when everything slips away. It is used with と: 茫然とする, 茫然と眺める. The set phrase 茫然自失 means \"completely stunned, beside oneself\". More literary.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"漠然とした不安があります。","romaji":"Bakuzen to shita fuan ga arimasu.","german":"I have a vague sense of anxiety.","furigana":"ばくぜん|ふあん"},{"japanese":"将来のことは、まだ漠然としか考えていません。","romaji":"Shourai no koto wa, mada bakuzen to shika kangaete imasen.","german":"I've only thought about the future in vague terms so far.","furigana":"しょうらい|ばくぜん|かんが"},{"japanese":"質問が漠然としていて、どう答えればいいか分かりません。","romaji":"Shitsumon ga bakuzen to shite ite, dou kotaereba ii ka wakarimasen.","german":"The question is so vague that I don't know how to answer.","furigana":"しつもん|ばくぜん|こた|わ"}],"notes":"漠然 describes something indefinite without clear contours. It is used with と: 漠然とした不安 \"diffuse anxiety\", 漠然としたイメージ, 漠然と考える \"to think about it only vaguely\". Unlike 曖昧, which often means an evasive, ambiguous statement, 漠然 simply lacks concreteness: a feeling or plan is still blurry.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"さっさと片付けなさい。","romaji":"Sassato katazukenasai.","german":"Clean up quickly.","furigana":"かたづ"},{"japanese":"仕事をさっさと終わらせて、飲みに行こう。","romaji":"Shigoto o sassato owarasete, nomi ni ikou.","german":"Let's finish work quickly and go for a drink.","furigana":"しごと|お|の|い"},{"japanese":"彼は会議が終わると、さっさと帰ってしまった。","romaji":"Kare wa kaigi ga owaru to, sassato kaette shimatta.","german":"As soon as the meeting ended, he left right away.","furigana":"かれ|かいぎ|お|かえ"}],"notes":"さっさと means \"promptly, without dawdling\" and is colloquial. It often appears in commands with a slightly impatient tone (さっさとしなさい \"Hurry up!\") or describes someone doing something quickly and without consideration: さっさと帰る \"to make oneself scarce quickly\". 早く is neutral, すぐ emphasizes \"immediately\", and てきぱき praises efficient work.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"そっとドアを開けました。","romaji":"Sotto doa o akemashita.","german":"I opened the door quietly.","furigana":"あ"},{"japanese":"彼女は落ち込んでいるから、しばらくそっとしておこう。","romaji":"Kanojo wa ochikonde iru kara, shibaraku sotto shite okou.","german":"She's feeling down, so let's leave her alone for a while.","furigana":"かのじょ|お|こ"},{"japanese":"赤ちゃんを起こさないように、そっとベッドに寝かせました。","romaji":"Akachan o okosanai you ni, sotto beddo ni nekasemashita.","german":"I laid the baby gently in bed so as not to wake it.","furigana":"あか|お|ね"}],"notes":"そっと means \"quietly, gently\" (そっとドアを閉める, そっと置く) and \"secretly, unnoticed\" (そっと教える). Very common is そっとしておく \"to leave someone alone\", for example when someone is sad. こっそり stresses concealment more strongly, often with a guilty conscience; 静かに refers only to volume.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"じっと待っていました。","romaji":"Jitto matte imashita.","german":"I waited without moving.","furigana":"ま"},{"japanese":"注射のときは、じっとしていてね。","romaji":"Chuusha no toki wa, jitto shite ite ne.","german":"Hold still for the injection, okay?","furigana":"ちゅうしゃ"},{"japanese":"彼は何も言わずに、私の顔をじっと見つめた。","romaji":"Kare wa nani mo iwazu ni, watashi no kao o jitto mitsumeta.","german":"He looked me in the face fixedly, without saying a word.","furigana":"かれ|なに|い|わたし|かお|み"}],"notes":"じっと describes someone not moving (じっとする, じっと座る), looking at something fixedly or doing something intensely (じっと見つめる, じっと考える), or enduring patiently (じっと我慢する). With children you often hear じっとしていなさい \"Hold still!\". Not to be confused with ずっと \"the whole time\" - じっと stresses motionlessness, not duration.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"目がぐるぐる回ります。","romaji":"Me ga guruguru mawarimasu.","german":"My eyes are spinning.","furigana":"め|まわ"},{"japanese":"道に迷って、同じところをぐるぐる回っていました。","romaji":"Michi ni mayotte, onaji tokoro o guruguru mawatte imashita.","german":"I got lost and kept walking around in circles in the same place.","furigana":"みち|まよ|おな|まわ"},{"japanese":"けがをした足に包帯をぐるぐる巻きました。","romaji":"Kega o shita ashi ni houtai o guruguru makimashita.","german":"I wrapped a bandage around the injured leg several times.","furigana":"あし|ほうたい|ま"}],"notes":"ぐるぐる is onomatopoeic for repeated turning or circling: ぐるぐる回る, 同じところをぐるぐる歩く \"to walk in circles\", 目がぐるぐる回る \"to feel dizzy\". It also describes wrapping something around several times: 包帯をぐるぐる巻く, ぐるぐる巻き. Thoughts can also go ぐるぐる round in one's head. くるくる is lighter and faster, ぐるっと means \"once all the way around\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"街をぶらぶら歩きました。","romaji":"Machi o burabura arukimashita.","german":"I strolled around the town.","furigana":"まち|ある"},{"japanese":"椅子に座って、足をぶらぶらさせていました。","romaji":"Isu ni suwatte, ashi o burabura sasete imashita.","german":"She was sitting on the chair, swinging her legs.","furigana":"いす|すわ|あし"},{"japanese":"大学を卒業してから、仕事もしないでぶらぶらしています。","romaji":"Daigaku o sotsugyou shite kara, shigoto mo shinaide burabura shite imasu.","german":"Since graduating from university, he has been idling around without working.","furigana":"だいがく|そつぎょう|しごと"}],"notes":"ぶらぶら is onomatopoeic and has three main meanings: to stroll aimlessly (街をぶらぶら歩く, ぶらぶらする), to dangle (足をぶらぶらさせる) and to hang around idly, for example without a job (毎日ぶらぶらしている). Related is the verb ぶらつく \"to wander about\". うろうろ, by contrast, describes restless, searching wandering, often with a suspicious connotation.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"案外簡単でした。","romaji":"Angai kantan deshita.","german":"It was unexpectedly easy.","furigana":"あんがいかんたん"},{"japanese":"見た目は地味だけど、この店のラーメンは案外おいしいよ。","romaji":"Mitame wa jimi da kedo, kono mise no raamen wa angai oishii yo.","german":"The place looks plain, but the ramen here is surprisingly good.","furigana":"み|め|じみ|みせ|あんがい"},{"japanese":"心配していたけど、彼は案外早く新しい仕事に慣れた。","romaji":"Shinpai shite ita kedo, kare wa angai hayaku atarashii shigoto ni nareta.","german":"I was worried, but he got used to the new job surprisingly quickly.","furigana":"しんぱい|かれ|あんがいはや|あたら|しごと|な"}],"notes":"案外 expresses mild surprise when something is different from what was expected (usually better); it usually stands adverbially before an adjective: 案外簡単だった, 案外おいしい. Very similar is 意外と; 案外 sounds a bit more casual and conversational. 思ったより says the same in a completely neutral way. As a な-adjective (案外な結果) it is rarer.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一斉に立ち上がりました。","romaji":"Issei ni tachiagarimashita.","german":"Everyone stood up at the same time.","furigana":"いっせい|た|あ"},{"japanese":"信号が青になると、車が一斉に走り出した。","romaji":"Shingou ga ao ni naru to, kuruma ga issei ni hashiridashita.","german":"When the light turned green, all the cars set off at once.","furigana":"しんごう|あお|くるま|いっせい|はし|だ"},{"japanese":"暖かくなって、桜が一斉に咲き始めました。","romaji":"Atatakaku natte, sakura ga issei ni sakihajimemashita.","german":"It got warm, and the cherry trees all began to bloom at once.","furigana":"あたた|さくら|いっせい|さ|はじ"}],"notes":"一斉に means \"all at the same time, all at once\" and presupposes that many people or things do the same thing at the same moment: 一斉に立ち上がる, 一斉に咲く. 同時に, by contrast, can also concern two things or a single actor, and いっぺんに stresses \"everything at once instead of one after another\". As a noun, 一斉 appears in compounds such as 一斉送信 (\"mass email\") or 一斉休校 (\"nationwide school closure\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"生まれつき歌が上手です。","romaji":"Umaretsuki uta ga jōzu desu.","german":"He is a good singer by nature.","furigana":"う|うた|じょうず"},{"japanese":"私は生まれつき体が弱いです。","romaji":"Watashi wa umaretsuki karada ga yowai desu.","german":"I have been physically weak since birth.","furigana":"わたし|う|からだ|よわ"},{"japanese":"彼女の髪は生まれつきの茶色で、染めていません。","romaji":"Kanojo no kami wa umaretsuki no chairo de, somete imasen.","german":"Her hair is naturally brown; she doesn't dye it.","furigana":"かのじょ|かみ|う|ちゃいろ|そ"}],"notes":"生まれつき means \"from birth, by nature\". Adverbially it stands before the statement (生まれつき体が弱い), as an attribute with の (生まれつきの才能, 生まれつきの髪の色). It is often used to explain that something is not chosen or trained, e.g. hair color. More elevated is 生まれながら(に); 天性 stresses a special talent.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"おかげで助かりました。","romaji":"Okage de tasukarimashita.","german":"Thanks to you, I was saved.","furigana":"たす"},{"japanese":"先生のおかげで、試験に合格できました。","romaji":"Sensei no okage de, shiken ni goukaku dekimashita.","german":"Thanks to my teacher, I was able to pass the exam.","furigana":"せんせい|しけん|ごうかく"},{"japanese":"薬を飲んだおかげで、熱が下がりました。","romaji":"Kusuri o nonda okage de, netsu ga sagarimashita.","german":"Thanks to taking the medicine, my fever went down.","furigana":"くすり|の|ねつ|さ"}],"notes":"おかげで names a cause with a positive result: Nのおかげで, Vたおかげで (\"thanks to\"). Alone at the beginning of a sentence it means \"Thanks to you/that ...\". Related is the set phrase おかげさまで \"I'm fine, thank you\" as an answer to questions about one's well-being. For negative causes one uses せいで; but ironically おかげで can also assign blame (君のおかげで遅刻したよ).","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 18"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"雨のせいで遅れました。","romaji":"Ame no sei de okuremashita.","german":"I was late because of the rain.","furigana":"あめ|おく"},{"japanese":"寝坊したせいで、電車に乗り遅れました。","romaji":"Nebou shita sei de, densha ni noriokuremashita.","german":"I was late for the train because I overslept.","furigana":"ねぼう|でんしゃ|の|おく"},{"japanese":"隣の工事の音のせいで、ゆうべは全然眠れなかった。","romaji":"Tonari no kouji no oto no sei de, yuube wa zenzen nemurenakatta.","german":"I couldn't sleep at all last night because of the noise from the construction next door.","furigana":"となり|こうじ|おと|ぜんぜんねむ"}],"notes":"せいで names a cause with a negative result and often assigns blame: Nのせいで, Vたせいで (\"because of, it's the fault of\"). The counterpart for positive results is おかげで. Related are 〜のせいにする \"to blame someone\" (人のせいにする) and 〜せいか \"perhaps because\". The underlying noun せい (rarely written 所為) means \"fault, consequence\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"従って、変更が必要です。","romaji":"Shitagatte, henkō ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"Therefore, a change is necessary.","furigana":"したが|へんこう|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"この地域は雨が少ない。したがって、水が貴重だ。","romaji":"Kono chiiki wa ame ga sukunai. Shitagatte, mizu ga kichou da.","german":"This region gets little rain. Therefore, water is precious.","furigana":"ちいき|あめ|すく|みず|きちょう"},{"japanese":"申し込みの受付は本日で終了しました。したがって、これ以降のお申し込みはお受けできません。","romaji":"Moushikomi no uketsuke wa honjitsu de shuuryou shimashita. Shitagatte, kore ikou no omoushikomi wa ouke dekimasen.","german":"The application period ended today. Therefore, we cannot accept any applications after this.","furigana":"もう|こ|うけつけ|ほんじつ|しゅうりょう|いこう|もう|こ|う"}],"notes":"従って (mostly written in kana as したがって) is a conjunction at the beginning of a sentence: \"therefore, consequently\". It sounds literary and formal - typical in reports, announcements and arguments; in conversation people say だから or なので instead. Not to be confused with the て-form of the verb 従う: 〜に従って means \"in accordance with\" (指示に従って) or \"as ..., the more ...\" (年を取るに従って).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"更に詳しく説明します。","romaji":"Sara ni kuwashiku setsumei shimasu.","german":"I will explain in even more detail.","furigana":"さら|くわ|せつめい"},{"japanese":"明日はさらに気温が下がるでしょう。","romaji":"Ashita wa sara ni kion ga sagaru deshou.","german":"Tomorrow the temperature is expected to drop even further.","furigana":"あした|きおん|さ"},{"japanese":"この店は安い。さらに、駅から近くて便利だ。","romaji":"Kono mise wa yasui. Sara ni, eki kara chikakute benri da.","german":"This shop is cheap. Moreover, it's conveniently close to the station.","furigana":"みせ|やす|えき|ちか|べんり"}],"notes":"さらに intensifies something already there: \"even (more), still further\" before verbs and adjectives (さらに寒くなる, さらに詳しく). At the beginning of a sentence it acts as a conjunction \"moreover, in addition\" and adds another argument. Compared with the colloquial もっと, さらに sounds more objective and somewhat more literary. Mostly written in kana; the kanji spelling 更に is rarer.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"尚、詳細は後日連絡します。","romaji":"Nao, shōsai wa gojitsu renraku shimasu.","german":"Incidentally, we will contact you with details at a later date.","furigana":"なお|しょうさい|ごじつれんらく"},{"japanese":"薬を飲んだのに、熱がなお下がらない。","romaji":"Kusuri o nonda no ni, netsu ga nao sagaranai.","german":"Even though I took medicine, my fever still won't go down.","furigana":"くすり|の|ねつ|さ"},{"japanese":"駅から近ければなおいいです。","romaji":"Eki kara chikakereba nao ii desu.","german":"If it's close to the station, all the better.","furigana":"えき|ちか"}],"notes":"なお has two main uses. As a conjunction at the start of a sentence (なお、…) it introduces an additional note in announcements, emails and notices: \"incidentally, in addition, note:\". As an adverb it means \"still\" or \"even more\": なおいい \"all the better\", なお一層 \"even more strongly\". In everyday speech people more often say まだ for \"still\". Almost always in kana; related is なおさら \"all the more\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は母の弟、すなわち私のおじです。","romaji":"Kare wa haha no otouto, sunawachi watashi no oji desu.","german":"He is my mother's younger brother, that is, my uncle.","furigana":"かれ|はは|おとうと|わたし"},{"japanese":"返事がないということは、すなわち反対だということだ。","romaji":"Henji ga nai to iu koto wa, sunawachi hantai da to iu koto da.","german":"No reply means, in other words, that they are against it.","furigana":"へんじ|はんたい"},{"japanese":"試合は来週の土曜日、すなわち10月15日に行われます。","romaji":"Shiai wa raishuu no doyoubi, sunawachi juugatsu juugonichi ni okonawaremasu.","german":"The game will take place next Saturday, namely on October 15.","furigana":"しあい|らいしゅう|どようび|がつ|にち|おこな"}],"notes":"すなわち equates two expressions: \"that is, namely, in other words\". It stands between what has been said and its explanation or clarification (A、すなわちB) and sounds written and objective, typical of textbooks, reports and speeches. In conversation, つまり is used instead. Without preceding content, it cannot simply open a sentence. Mostly written in kana.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"敢えて言わせてもらいます。","romaji":"Aete iwasete moraimasu.","german":"I'll say it anyway, if you'll allow me.","furigana":"あ|い"},{"japanese":"彼はあえて難しい道を選んだ。","romaji":"Kare wa aete muzukashii michi o eranda.","german":"He deliberately chose the difficult path.","furigana":"かれ|むずか|みち|えら"},{"japanese":"あえて言うなら、色が少し地味かな。","romaji":"Aete iu nara, iro ga sukoshi jimi ka na.","german":"If I had to say something, the color is perhaps a little plain.","furigana":"い|いろ|すこ|じみ"}],"notes":"あえて means to do something deliberately although it seems unnecessary, risky or unusual: \"deliberately, on purpose, despite reservations\". Typical are あえて言うなら \"if I had to say something\" and あえて〜ない \"not necessarily, not particularly\". Unlike わざと (often negative, with malicious intent) and わざわざ (going out of one's way), あえて stresses a considered decision. Mostly in kana.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"辛うじて間に合いました。","romaji":"Karōjite ma ni aimashita.","german":"I barely made it in time.","furigana":"かろ|ま|あ"},{"japanese":"試験はかろうじて合格した。","romaji":"Shiken wa karoujite goukaku shita.","german":"I only just passed the exam.","furigana":"しけん|ごうかく"},{"japanese":"彼は事故に遭ったが、かろうじて命は助かった。","romaji":"Kare wa jiko ni atta ga, karoujite inochi wa tasukatta.","german":"He was in an accident, but narrowly survived.","furigana":"かれ|じこ|あ|いのち|たす"}],"notes":"かろうじて means \"just barely, by a narrow margin\": the result was achieved, but only very narrowly (間に合う, 合格する, 助かる). It sounds somewhat more literary than ぎりぎり, which is used colloquially mainly for time limits, and stresses the narrowness more than やっと, which stresses the long wait. Mostly in kana; the kanji 辛 is read unusually here.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"予め準備しておきます。","romaji":"Arakajime junbi shite okimasu.","german":"I'll prepare in advance.","furigana":"あらかじ|じゅんび"},{"japanese":"参加できない場合は、あらかじめご連絡ください。","romaji":"Sanka dekinai baai wa, arakajime gorenraku kudasai.","german":"If you cannot participate, please let us know beforehand.","furigana":"さんか|ばあい|れんらく"},{"japanese":"人気の店なので、あらかじめ席を予約しておいた。","romaji":"Ninki no mise na node, arakajime seki o yoyaku shite oita.","german":"Since the restaurant is popular, I reserved a table in advance.","furigana":"にんき|みせ|せき|よやく"}],"notes":"あらかじめ means \"in advance, beforehand\" and often appears with 〜ておく (あらかじめ準備しておく) or in polite requests such as あらかじめご了承ください \"we ask for your understanding in advance\". It sounds more formal than 前もって; in business language 事前に is also very common. Mostly written in kana. Unlike 前に, あらかじめ does not designate a point in time but stresses preparation before an event.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"滅多に風邪をひきません。","romaji":"Metta ni kaze o hikimasen.","german":"I rarely catch a cold.","furigana":"めった|かぜ"},{"japanese":"父はめったに怒らない。","romaji":"Chichi wa metta ni okoranai.","german":"My father rarely gets angry.","furigana":"ちち|おこ"},{"japanese":"こんなチャンスはめったにないよ。","romaji":"Konna chansu wa metta ni nai yo.","german":"You don't get a chance like this every day."}],"notes":"めったに always goes with a negative: めったに〜ない \"rarely, hardly ever\". It is stronger than あまり〜ない and stresses that something happens only exceptionally. Fixed phrase: めったにない \"rare, once in a lifetime\" (めったにないチャンス). Without negation, めったな means \"rash, careless\" (めったなことを言うな). Both the kanji 滅多 and kana are common.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"到底無理です。","romaji":"Tōtei muri desu.","german":"It's absolutely impossible.","furigana":"とうていむり"},{"japanese":"この量の仕事は、一日では到底終わらない。","romaji":"Kono ryou no shigoto wa, ichinichi de wa toutei owaranai.","german":"I can't possibly finish this amount of work in one day.","furigana":"りょう|しごと|いちにち|とうていお"},{"japanese":"彼の気持ちは私には到底理解できない。","romaji":"Kare no kimochi wa watashi ni wa toutei rikai dekinai.","german":"I can't possibly understand his feelings.","furigana":"かれ|きも|わたし|とうていりかい"}],"notes":"到底 intensifies an impossibility and is used with a negative or with words such as 無理, できない: \"by no means, not by any stretch\". It expresses that something objectively cannot be managed, whereas 決して〜ない rather stresses a will or a prohibition. とても〜ない is similar but more colloquial. Both kanji and kana are common; the tone is somewhat elevated.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ろくに寝ていません。","romaji":"Roku ni nete imasen.","german":"I've hardly slept.","furigana":"ね"},{"japanese":"ろくに勉強しないで試験を受けた。","romaji":"Roku ni benkyou shinaide shiken o uketa.","german":"I took the exam without studying properly.","furigana":"べんきょう|しけん|う"},{"japanese":"忙しくて、昼ご飯もろくに食べられなかった。","romaji":"Isogashikute, hirugohan mo roku ni taberarenakatta.","german":"I was so busy that I couldn't even eat lunch properly.","furigana":"いそが|ひる|はん|た"}],"notes":"ろくに is always used with a negative and means \"not properly, hardly decently\": ろくに寝ていない, ろくに勉強しない. It sounds colloquial and often reproachful or complaining, because something was not done to the necessary degree. Related is ろくな + noun + ない \"no decent ...\" (ろくなことがない). ほとんど〜ない is more neutral. Almost always in kana.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"しきりに電話が鳴ります。","romaji":"Shikiri ni denwa ga narimasu.","german":"The phone keeps ringing.","furigana":"でんわ|な"},{"japanese":"彼はしきりに時計を見ていた。","romaji":"Kare wa shikiri ni tokei o mite ita.","german":"He kept looking at his watch.","furigana":"かれ|とけい|み"},{"japanese":"友達がしきりにその映画を勧めてくる。","romaji":"Tomodachi ga shikiri ni sono eiga o susumete kuru.","german":"A friend keeps recommending that movie to me.","furigana":"ともだち|えいが|すす"}],"notes":"しきりに describes something that repeats conspicuously often: \"constantly, over and over\" (しきりに時計を見る, 電話がしきりに鳴る). It can also express an insistent attitude: しきりに勧める \"to recommend eagerly\". It sounds somewhat more descriptive and literary than 何度も or the colloquial しょっちゅう. Almost always in kana; the kanji 頻りに is rare.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"精々頑張ります。","romaji":"Seizei ganbarimasu.","german":"I'll do my best.","furigana":"せいぜいがんば"},{"japanese":"参加者はせいぜい20人ぐらいだろう。","romaji":"Sankasha wa seizei nijuunin gurai darou.","german":"At most about twenty participants will come.","furigana":"さんかしゃ|にん"},{"japanese":"駅まで歩いても、せいぜい10分です。","romaji":"Eki made aruite mo, seizei juppun desu.","german":"Even on foot, it's ten minutes to the station at most.","furigana":"えき|ある|ぷん"}],"notes":"せいぜい has two meanings. Mostly it means \"at most, at best\" and judges a quantity as rather small, often with ぐらい, 程度 or だろう (せいぜい20人ぐらい). It also means \"as well as one can\": せいぜい頑張ります. Said by others, せいぜい頑張って can sound condescending or ironic. A more neutral word for \"maximum\" is 多くても. Mostly written in kana.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"如何にも日本的ですね。","romaji":"Ika ni mo nihonteki desu ne.","german":"That's so typically Japanese, isn't it?","furigana":"いか|にほんてき"},{"japanese":"彼はいかにも眠そうな顔をしている。","romaji":"Kare wa ika ni mo nemusou na kao o shite iru.","german":"He looks really sleepy.","furigana":"かれ|ねむ|かお"},{"japanese":"いかにも彼らしい答えだ。","romaji":"Ika ni mo kare rashii kotae da.","german":"That's a typical answer for him.","furigana":"かれ|こた"}],"notes":"いかにも says that something looks exactly as one would expect: \"truly, typical, decidedly\". Typical combinations are いかにも〜らしい \"typical of\" and いかにも〜そう \"looks just like\". On its own as an answer, いかにも means \"indeed, exactly\", but sounds old-fashioned. Unlike 本当に, いかにも stresses the outward impression. Mostly in kana.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"何気なく言った一言で、彼女を傷つけてしまった。","romaji":"Nanigenaku itta hitokoto de, kanojo o kizutsukete shimatta.","german":"With a casual remark, I ended up hurting her.","furigana":"なにげ|い|ひとこと|かのじょ|きず"},{"japanese":"何気なく窓の外を見ると、雪が降っていた。","romaji":"Nanigenaku mado no soto o miru to, yuki ga futte ita.","german":"When I glanced out the window without thinking, it was snowing.","furigana":"なにげ|まど|そと|み|ゆき|ふ"},{"japanese":"何気なくつけたテレビで、面白い番組をやっていた。","romaji":"Nanigenaku tsuketa terebi de, omoshiroi bangumi o yatte ita.","german":"On the TV I had turned on without thinking, an interesting program was on.","furigana":"なにげ|おもしろ|ばんぐみ"}],"notes":"何気なく, the adverbial form of 何気ない, means \"without particular intention, just like that, casually\": 何気なく窓の外を見る, 何気なく言った一言. Unlike うっかり (by mistake, with an error), it stresses only the lack of intention; なんとなく means more \"for no clear reason, somehow\". The form 何気に \"actually quite\" is youth slang. Mostly written with kanji.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"多少時間がかかります。","romaji":"Tashō jikan ga kakarimasu.","german":"It takes a little time.","furigana":"たしょうじかん"},{"japanese":"多少の雨なら、試合は行われます。","romaji":"Tashou no ame nara, shiai wa okonawaremasu.","german":"If it's only light rain, the game will be held.","furigana":"たしょう|あめ|しあい|おこな"},{"japanese":"英語なら多少話せます。","romaji":"Eigo nara tashou hanasemasu.","german":"I can speak a little English.","furigana":"えいご|たしょうはな"}],"notes":"多少 as an adverb means \"somewhat, a little, to a certain degree\" and sounds more objective and polite than 少し or ちょっと, for example in announcements: 多少時間がかかります. With の it stands before nouns: 多少の雨, 多少の違い. As a noun it means \"quantity, much or little\", e.g. 金額の多少にかかわらず \"regardless of the amount\". Always written with kanji.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大幅に値上がりしました。","romaji":"Ōhaba ni neagari shimashita.","german":"Prices have risen substantially.","furigana":"おおはば|ねあ"},{"japanese":"電車が大幅に遅れている。","romaji":"Densha ga oohaba ni okurete iru.","german":"The train is running considerably late.","furigana":"でんしゃ|おおはば|おく"},{"japanese":"大幅な値下げセールが始まった。","romaji":"Oohaba na nesage seeru ga hajimatta.","german":"A sale with big price cuts has started.","furigana":"おおはば|ねさ|はじ"}],"notes":"大幅 describes large changes in quantities and values: \"considerable, drastic\". Adverbially it appears as 大幅に before verbs such as 増える, 減る, 上がる, 遅れる, 変更する; before nouns as 大幅な (大幅な値下げ). For a general increase in qualities people use かなり or ずいぶん, not 大幅. Opposite: 小幅 \"slight\". Typical of the news and business language.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大いに楽しみました。","romaji":"Ōi ni tanoshimimashita.","german":"I enjoyed it a great deal.","furigana":"おお|たの"},{"japanese":"その意見には大いに賛成です。","romaji":"Sono iken ni wa ooi ni sansei desu.","german":"I agree with that opinion wholeheartedly.","furigana":"いけん|おお|さんせい"},{"japanese":"今回の経験は、将来大いに役に立つと思う。","romaji":"Konkai no keiken wa, shourai ooi ni yaku ni tatsu to omou.","german":"I think this experience will be very useful in the future.","furigana":"こんかい|けいけん|しょうらいおお|やく|た|おも"}],"notes":"大いに means \"very much, to a great extent, to one's heart's content\" and mainly intensifies verbs: 大いに楽しむ, 大いに期待する, 大いに賛成する, 大いに役に立つ. Before adjectives, you normally use とても or 非常に, not 大いに. The tone is somewhat elevated and emphatic, typical of speeches and invitations such as 大いに飲みましょう. Always written with kanji.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"現に成功しています。","romaji":"Gen ni seikō shite imasu.","german":"In fact, it is succeeding.","furigana":"げん|せいこう"},{"japanese":"この薬はよく効く。現に私も一週間で治った。","romaji":"Kono kusuri wa yoku kiku. Gen ni watashi mo isshuukan de naotta.","german":"This medicine works well. In fact, I got better in a week myself.","furigana":"くすり|き|げん|わたし|いっしゅうかん|なお"},{"japanese":"あの交差点は危ない。現に先月も事故があった。","romaji":"Ano kousaten wa abunai. Gen ni sengetsu mo jiko ga atta.","german":"That intersection is dangerous. In fact, there was an accident there last month, too.","furigana":"こうさてん|あぶ|げん|せんげつ|じこ"}],"notes":"現に cites a concrete, actually existing fact as evidence for a claim: \"actually, as you can see\". The typical sequence is claim → 現に + example. 実際に is more general, and 実は rather introduces a revelation (\"to tell the truth\"). The tone is matter-of-fact, but it is as common in conversation as in texts.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"確か明日が締め切りです。","romaji":"Tashika ashita ga shimekiri desu.","german":"If I remember correctly, the deadline is tomorrow.","furigana":"たし|あした|し|き"},{"japanese":"確か、田中さんは大阪の出身だったと思います。","romaji":"Tashika, Tanaka-san wa Oosaka no shusshin datta to omoimasu.","german":"If I'm not mistaken, Mr. Tanaka is from Osaka.","furigana":"たし|たなか|おおさか|しゅっしん|おも"},{"japanese":"それは確かな情報ですか。","romaji":"Sore wa tashika na jouhou desu ka.","german":"Is that reliable information?","furigana":"たし|じょうほう"}],"notes":"As an adverb at the beginning of a sentence, 確か means \"if I remember correctly, as far as I know\" and marks a statement as not entirely certain; often ～と思います, ～はずです or a past form follows. As a な-adjective, on the other hand, it means \"certain, reliable\": 確かな情報, 確かな腕. Do not confuse it with 確かに \"indeed, truly\", which confirms a statement (確かにそうですね). In casual conversation the adverb is often followed by a short pause: 確か、….","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 29"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"密かに計画を立てました。","romaji":"Hisoka ni keikaku o tatemashita.","german":"I secretly made a plan.","furigana":"ひそ|けいかく|た"},{"japanese":"彼女は密かに彼のことが好きだった。","romaji":"Kanojo wa hisoka ni kare no koto ga suki datta.","german":"She was secretly in love with him.","furigana":"かのじょ|ひそ|かれ|す"},{"japanese":"週末の昼寝が私の密かな楽しみです。","romaji":"Shuumatsu no hirune ga watashi no hisoka na tanoshimi desu.","german":"The weekend nap is my little secret pleasure.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|ひるね|わたし|ひそ|たの"}],"notes":"密か is a na-adjective for \"secret, quietly\" and is mostly used as ひそかに (ひそかに計画する, ひそかに好きだ). Before nouns it appears as ひそかな: ひそかな楽しみ \"little secret pleasure\", ひそかな夢. It describes a quiet, inconspicuous hiding of feelings or plans; こっそり is more colloquial and stresses the furtiveness of an action. Both kana and kanji are common.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"健やかに成長しました。","romaji":"Sukoyaka ni seichō shimashita.","german":"He/She grew up healthy.","furigana":"すこ|せいちょう"},{"japanese":"お子様の健やかな成長をお祈りしております。","romaji":"Okosama no sukoyaka na seichou o oinori shite orimasu.","german":"We wish your child a healthy and strong upbringing.","furigana":"こさま|すこ|せいちょう|いの"},{"japanese":"祖父は九十歳になった今も健やかに暮らしている。","romaji":"Sofu wa kyuujussai ni natta ima mo sukoyaka ni kurashite iru.","german":"My grandfather is ninety now and still lives in good health.","furigana":"そふ|きゅうじゅっさい|いま|すこ|く"}],"notes":"健やか means \"healthy, robust, in good health\" and sounds elevated and warm. It is typical for children (健やかに育つ, 健やかな成長) as well as in congratulations and greeting formulas. In everyday speech people say 元気 or 健康 instead; 健康 is the neutral noun \"health\". 健やか stresses a healthy, undisturbed thriving of body and mind.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"爽やかな朝ですね。","romaji":"Sawayaka na asa desu ne.","german":"What a fresh, pleasant morning.","furigana":"さわ|あさ"},{"japanese":"彼はいつも笑顔で、爽やかな人だ。","romaji":"Kare wa itsumo egao de, sawayaka na hito da.","german":"He is always smiling and has a refreshingly pleasant manner.","furigana":"かれ|えがお|さわ|ひと"},{"japanese":"このお茶はミントの爽やかな香りがする。","romaji":"Kono ocha wa minto no sawayaka na kaori ga suru.","german":"This tea has a refreshing scent of mint.","furigana":"ちゃ|さわ|かお"}],"notes":"爽やか describes something pleasantly fresh without sultriness or heaviness: dry, clear weather (especially in autumn and spring), fresh scents and flavors (ミントの爽やかな香り) and likable, open people (爽やかな笑顔, 爽やかな青年). 涼しい, by contrast, refers only to pleasantly cool temperature. Written with kanji or in kana as さわやか.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"和やかな雰囲気です。","romaji":"Nagoyaka na fun'iki desu.","german":"There is a harmonious atmosphere.","furigana":"なご|ふんいき"},{"japanese":"会議は和やかに進んだ。","romaji":"Kaigi wa nagoyaka ni susunda.","german":"The meeting went along in a friendly atmosphere.","furigana":"かいぎ|なご|すす"},{"japanese":"家族で和やかに食事を楽しんだ。","romaji":"Kazoku de nagoyaka ni shokuji o tanoshinda.","german":"We enjoyed our meal in a pleasant family atmosphere.","furigana":"かぞく|なご|しょくじ|たの"}],"notes":"和やか describes the friendly, relaxed mood among people: 和やかな雰囲気, 和やかに話す, 会議が和やかに進む. It thus characterizes a situation or group, not the temperament of an individual; for that people use 穏やか \"calm, gentle\", which also applies to weather and sea. Typical of reports on meetings, celebrations and family meals.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"華やかなパーティーです。","romaji":"Hanayaka na pātī desu.","german":"It's a glamorous party.","furigana":"はな"},{"japanese":"彼女はいつも華やかな服を着ている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa itsumo hanayaka na fuku o kite iru.","german":"She always wears colorful, elegant clothes.","furigana":"かのじょ|はな|ふく|き"},{"japanese":"芸能界は華やかに見えるが、実は大変な世界だ。","romaji":"Geinoukai wa hanayaka ni mieru ga, jitsu wa taihen na sekai da.","german":"The entertainment industry looks glamorous, but it is actually a tough world.","furigana":"げいのうかい|はな|み|じつ|たいへん|せかい"}],"notes":"華やか means \"splendid, brilliant, colorful\" and is positive: 華やかなパーティー, 華やかな服, 華やかな世界. Unlike 派手 (flashy, conspicuous, often critical), 華やか praises the effect; 豪華 stresses luxury and high cost more. It can also describe a heyday: 華やかな時代 \"glorious era\". The kanji 華 means \"blossom, splendor\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"緩やかな坂道です。","romaji":"Yuruyaka na sakamichi desu.","german":"It's a gentle slope.","furigana":"ゆる|さかみち"},{"japanese":"景気は緩やかに回復しています。","romaji":"Keiki wa yuruyaka ni kaifuku shite imasu.","german":"The economy is recovering gradually.","furigana":"けいき|ゆる|かいふく"},{"japanese":"この学校は校則が緩やかだ。","romaji":"Kono gakkou wa kousoku ga yuruyaka da.","german":"This school's rules are lenient.","furigana":"がっこう|こうそく|ゆる"}],"notes":"緩やか means \"gentle, gradual, loose\": a gentle slope or a shallow curve (緩やかな坂, 緩やかなカーブ), a slow change (景気が緩やかに回復する) or mild rules (規則が緩やか). It sounds more matter-of-fact and more literary than the everyday word 緩い, which stands for example for shoes that are too loose or lax controls. Very common in the news.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"速やかに対応します。","romaji":"Sumiyaka ni taiō shimasu.","german":"We will respond promptly.","furigana":"すみ|たいおう"},{"japanese":"地震の際は、速やかに建物の外へ避難してください。","romaji":"Jishin no sai wa, sumiyaka ni tatemono no soto e hinan shite kudasai.","german":"In the event of an earthquake, evacuate the building immediately.","furigana":"じしん|さい|すみ|たてもの|そと|ひなん"},{"japanese":"書類は速やかにご提出ください。","romaji":"Shorui wa sumiyaka ni goteishutsu kudasai.","german":"Please submit the documents promptly.","furigana":"しょるい|すみ|ていしゅつ"}],"notes":"速やか means \"without delay, promptly\" and appears almost always as 速やかに in formal announcements, instructions and business texts: 速やかに避難してください, 速やかに対応いたします. In daily life, people say すぐに or 早く instead. It stresses acting without hesitation, not necessarily high speed; for speed, 速い is used.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"僅かな差で負けました。","romaji":"Wazuka na sa de makemashita.","german":"We lost by a narrow margin.","furigana":"わず|さ|ま"},{"japanese":"締め切りまで、残りわずか三日しかない。","romaji":"Shimekiri made, nokori wazuka mikka shika nai.","german":"There are only three days left until the deadline.","furigana":"し|き|のこ|みっか"},{"japanese":"貯金はほんのわずかしか残っていない。","romaji":"Chokin wa hon no wazuka shika nokotte inai.","german":"Only a tiny amount of my savings is left.","furigana":"ちょきん|のこ"}],"notes":"わずか means \"slight, little, narrow\": わずかな差, わずかなお金. Before numbers it means \"only, merely\" and often goes with しか or だけ: わずか三日しかない. In shops you read 残りわずか \"only a few left in stock\". It sounds somewhat more elevated than 少し or ちょっと and stresses how small the amount is. Mostly written in kana.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"皮肉を言いました。","romaji":"Hiniku o iimashita.","german":"He made a sarcastic remark.","furigana":"ひにく|い"},{"japanese":"皮肉なことに、傘を持って出かけた日に限って雨が降らない。","romaji":"Hiniku na koto ni, kasa o motte dekaketa hi ni kagitte ame ga furanai.","german":"Ironically, it never rains on the days I go out with an umbrella.","furigana":"ひにく|かさ|も|で|ひ|かぎ|あめ|ふ"},{"japanese":"彼のほめ言葉は皮肉に聞こえた。","romaji":"Kare no homekotoba wa hiniku ni kikoeta.","german":"His compliment sounded sarcastic.","furigana":"かれ|ことば|ひにく|き"}],"notes":"皮肉 means \"irony, sarcasm, a pointed remark\"; literally \"skin and flesh\", that is, something that only scratches the surface. Typical are 皮肉を言う \"to make snide remarks\", 皮肉っぽい \"sounding sarcastic\" and the verb 皮肉る. As a na-adjective it describes ironic coincidences: 皮肉な結果, 皮肉なことに \"ironically\". Unlike 冗談 (a joke), 皮肉 always contains a hidden reproach.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お世話になりました。","romaji":"Osewa ni narimashita.","german":"Thank you for all your help.","furigana":"せわ"},{"japanese":"毎朝、犬の世話をしています。","romaji":"Maiasa, inu no sewa o shite imasu.","german":"Every morning I take care of the dog.","furigana":"まいあさ|いぬ|せわ"},{"japanese":"余計なお世話だよ。","romaji":"Yokei na osewa da yo.","german":"Mind your own business!","furigana":"よけい|せわ"}],"notes":"世話 is \"care, looking after, help\". One takes care of someone with 〜の世話をする (犬の世話をする); receiving help is 世話になる. From this come the fixed polite formulas お世話になりました \"thank you for everything\" at parting and お世話になっております as the standard greeting in business e-mails. 余計なお世話 means \"mind your own business\", and 世話を焼く \"to fuss (too much) over someone\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"行儀よくしなさい。","romaji":"Gyōgi yoku shinasai.","german":"Behave yourself!","furigana":"ぎょうぎ"},{"japanese":"食事中に肘をつくのは行儀が悪いよ。","romaji":"Shokujichuu ni hiji o tsuku no wa gyougi ga warui yo.","german":"It's bad manners to rest your elbows on the table while eating.","furigana":"しょくじちゅう|ひじ|ぎょうぎ|わる"},{"japanese":"あの子はとてもお行儀がいいですね。","romaji":"Ano ko wa totemo ogyougi ga ii desu ne.","german":"That child is very well-behaved.","furigana":"こ|ぎょうぎ"}],"notes":"行儀 refers to a person's behavior, especially in daily life and at the table: 行儀がいい／悪い, 行儀よくする. With children, people like to say お行儀. マナー, by contrast, means the rules of etiquette in particular situations, and 礼儀 respectful politeness toward others. 他人行儀 means \"formal and distant, as with strangers\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"非常識な行動です。","romaji":"Hijōshiki na kōdō desu.","german":"That is thoughtless behavior.","furigana":"ひじょうしき|こうどう"},{"japanese":"夜中の二時に電話するなんて、非常識だよ。","romaji":"Yonaka no niji ni denwa suru nante, hijoushiki da yo.","german":"Calling at two in the morning is just not done!","furigana":"よなか|にじ|でんわ|ひじょうしき"},{"japanese":"結婚式に白いドレスで行くのは非常識だと言われた。","romaji":"Kekkonshiki ni shiroi doresu de iku no wa hijoushiki da to iwareta.","german":"I was told it's inappropriate to go to a wedding in a white dress.","furigana":"けっこんしき|しろ|い|ひじょうしき|い"}],"notes":"非常識 means \"without common sense, out of line, outrageous\" and is a な-adjective or noun: 非常識な人, 非常識な時間, 〜なんて非常識だ. It is a clear criticism of behavior that violates generally expected social rules. 失礼, by contrast, stresses rudeness toward a specific person. Opposite: 常識的, 常識がある.","levelNote":"Compound: 非 (N1) + 常識 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"マナーを守ってください。","romaji":"Manā o mamotte kudasai.","german":"Please observe the rules of etiquette.","furigana":"まも"},{"japanese":"電車の中では、携帯電話をマナーモードにしてください。","romaji":"Densha no naka de wa, keitai denwa o manaa moodo ni shite kudasai.","german":"On the train, please set your mobile phone to silent mode.","furigana":"でんしゃ|なか|けいたいでんわ"},{"japanese":"彼は食事のマナーが悪い。","romaji":"Kare wa shokuji no manaa ga warui.","german":"He has bad table manners.","furigana":"かれ|しょくじ|わる"}],"notes":"マナー refers to the rules of etiquette in particular situations: 食事のマナー, ビジネスマナー, 電車のマナー. Typical are マナーを守る \"to follow the rules\", マナーが悪い and マナー違反 \"violation of etiquette\". マナーモード is the silent mode of a mobile phone. Unlike English \"manner\", it never means \"way of doing something\". 行儀 is more personal behavior.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ご迷惑をおかけしました。","romaji":"Gomeiwaku o okake shimashita.","german":"I apologize for the trouble.","furigana":"めいわく"},{"japanese":"夜遅くに騒ぐと、近所の迷惑になります。","romaji":"Yoru osoku ni sawagu to, kinjo no meiwaku ni narimasu.","german":"If you make noise late at night, you disturb the neighbors.","furigana":"よるおそ|さわ|きんじょ|めいわく"},{"japanese":"迷惑メールが毎日たくさん届いて困っている。","romaji":"Meiwaku meeru ga mainichi takusan todoite komatte iru.","german":"I get lots of spam emails every day, and it's a nuisance.","furigana":"めいわく|まいにち|とど|こま"}],"notes":"迷惑 is \"nuisance, inconvenience, burden on others\". Central are 迷惑をかける \"to be a burden to someone\" (politely ご迷惑をおかけしました) and 〜の迷惑になる \"to disturb others\". As a な-adjective: 迷惑な人, 迷惑な話. 迷惑する means \"to be bothered, to suffer from\" (騒音に迷惑している). 迷惑メール is spam email. 邪魔, by contrast, means \"to be in the way, to hinder\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"遠慮しないでください。","romaji":"Enryo shinaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't hold back.","furigana":"えんりょ"},{"japanese":"ここではたばこはご遠慮ください。","romaji":"Koko de wa tabako wa goenryo kudasai.","german":"Please refrain from smoking here.","furigana":"えんりょ"},{"japanese":"すみません、今日の飲み会は遠慮しておきます。","romaji":"Sumimasen, kyou no nomikai wa enryo shite okimasu.","german":"Sorry, I'll pass on the drinking party today.","furigana":"きょう|の|かい|えんりょ"}],"notes":"遠慮 is the typically Japanese restraint out of consideration for others. 遠慮しないでください and 遠慮なく… invite someone to help themselves or speak up without hesitation. On signs and announcements, ご遠慮ください is a polite form of prohibition (\"Please refrain from this\"). With 遠慮しておきます one kindly declines an offer or invitation; the thing one gives up can take を or は.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"謙遜しすぎです。","romaji":"Kenson shisugi desu.","german":"You are being too modest.","furigana":"けんそん"},{"japanese":"日本では、褒められると謙遜するのが普通だ。","romaji":"Nihon de wa, homerareru to kenson suru no ga futsuu da.","german":"In Japan, it is normal to be modest when praised.","furigana":"にほん|ほ|けんそん|ふつう"},{"japanese":"彼は謙遜して、自分の功績をあまり話さない。","romaji":"Kare wa kenson shite, jibun no kouseki o amari hanasanai.","german":"He is modest and hardly talks about his achievements.","furigana":"かれ|けんそん|じぶん|こうせき|はな"}],"notes":"謙遜 is modestly playing down one's own accomplishments, especially in response to praise: 謙遜する, 謙遜して〜と言う. When someone is praised and deflects it, one politely replies ご謙遜を \"You are too modest\". 謙虚 (na-adjective), by contrast, describes a modest character in general. In Japan, deflecting praise is considered normal politeness.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"辛抱強く待ちます。","romaji":"Shinbōzuyoku machimasu.","german":"I'll wait patiently.","furigana":"しんぼうづよ|ま"},{"japanese":"もう少しの辛抱だから、頑張って。","romaji":"Mou sukoshi no shinbou da kara, ganbatte.","german":"Just a little more patience, so hang in there.","furigana":"すこ|しんぼう|がんば"},{"japanese":"痛いけど、少し辛抱してね。","romaji":"Itai kedo, sukoshi shinbou shite ne.","german":"It hurts a bit, but bear with it for a moment, okay?","furigana":"いた|すこ|しんぼう"}],"notes":"辛抱 is patiently enduring an unpleasant situation over some time: 辛抱する, 辛抱強い \"patient, persevering\", もう少しの辛抱だ \"just hang on a little longer\". 我慢 is more common and stresses suppressing wishes or pain in the moment; 忍耐 is more literary. 辛抱 sounds warm and encouraging, often toward children.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"根気が必要な仕事です。","romaji":"Konki ga hitsuyō na shigoto desu.","german":"It's a job that requires perseverance.","furigana":"こんき|ひつよう|しごと"},{"japanese":"根気よく練習を続けたら、上手になった。","romaji":"Konki yoku renshuu o tsuzuketara, jouzu ni natta.","german":"I kept practicing patiently and got good at it.","furigana":"こんき|れんしゅう|つづ|じょうず"},{"japanese":"私は根気がないので、何をやっても続かない。","romaji":"Watashi wa konki ga nai node, nani o yatte mo tsuzukanai.","german":"I have no perseverance, so I can't stick with anything I do.","furigana":"わたし|こんき|なに|つづ"}],"notes":"根気 is the perseverance to keep going calmly with a tedious or lengthy task: 根気がある／ない, 根気よく続ける, 根気強い, 根気のいる仕事. 辛抱 stresses enduring unpleasant things, 根性 rather fighting spirit and grit. 根気 suits patient detail work such as practicing, studying or crafting in particular.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"集中して勉強します。","romaji":"Shūchū shite benkyō shimasu.","german":"I study with concentration.","furigana":"しゅうちゅう|べんきょう"},{"japanese":"うるさくて、仕事に集中できない。","romaji":"Urusakute, shigoto ni shuuchuu dekinai.","german":"It's so noisy that I can't concentrate on my work.","furigana":"しごと|しゅうちゅう"},{"japanese":"日本では人口が東京に集中している。","romaji":"Nihon de wa jinkou ga Toukyou ni shuuchuu shite iru.","german":"In Japan, the population is concentrated in Tokyo.","furigana":"にほん|じんこう|とうきょう|しゅうちゅう"}],"notes":"集中 is \"concentration\" in two senses. Mental: 〜に集中する \"to concentrate on something\", 集中して勉強する, 集中力 \"ability to concentrate\". Spatial: people, buildings or power cluster in one place (人口が都市に集中する). Transitive with を: 意識を集中する. 集中豪雨 is a localized heavy downpour.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"油断しないでください。","romaji":"Yudan shinaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't let your guard down.","furigana":"ゆだん"},{"japanese":"ちょっと油断したら、財布を盗まれた。","romaji":"Chotto yudan shitara, saifu o nusumareta.","german":"I let my guard down for a moment, and my wallet was stolen.","furigana":"ゆだん|さいふ|ぬす"},{"japanese":"相手が弱くても油断は禁物だ。","romaji":"Aite ga yowakute mo yudan wa kinmotsu da.","german":"Even if the opponent is weak, you must not be careless.","furigana":"あいて|よわ|ゆだん|きんもつ"}],"notes":"油断 means letting one's vigilance slip because one feels too safe: 油断する, ちょっと油断したら…, 油断は禁物 \"one must not become careless\". The proverb 油断大敵 means \"carelessness is the greatest enemy\". 不注意, by contrast, emphasizes a specific careless mistake; 油断 is rather an attitude of carefree complacency.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"覚悟を決めました。","romaji":"Kakugo o kimemashita.","german":"I have steeled myself.","furigana":"かくご|き"},{"japanese":"失敗する覚悟で挑戦してみる。","romaji":"Shippai suru kakugo de chousen shite miru.","german":"I'll take on the challenge, prepared to fail.","furigana":"しっぱい|かくご|ちょうせん"},{"japanese":"留学するなら、苦労は覚悟しておいたほうがいい。","romaji":"Ryuugaku suru nara, kurou wa kakugo shite oita hou ga ii.","german":"If you study abroad, you should be prepared for hardship.","furigana":"りゅうがく|くろう|かくご"}],"notes":"覚悟 is the inner readiness to take on something hard or negative: 覚悟を決める \"to make up one's mind to face it\", 覚悟はできている, 〜は覚悟している \"to be prepared for something\", 〜覚悟で \"at the risk of\". 決心 and 決意 mean more the decision to do something; 覚悟 stresses accepting possible consequences.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は世界記録に挑んだ。","romaji":"Kare wa sekai kiroku ni idonda.","german":"He took on the world record.","furigana":"かれ|せかいきろく|いど"},{"japanese":"若い選手がチャンピオンに勝負を挑んだ。","romaji":"Wakai senshu ga chanpion ni shoubu o idonda.","german":"A young athlete challenged the champion to a match.","furigana":"わか|せんしゅ|しょうぶ|いど"},{"japanese":"冬の富士山に挑むのは危険だ。","romaji":"Fuyu no Fujisan ni idomu no wa kiken da.","german":"Taking on Mt. Fuji in winter is dangerous.","furigana":"ふゆ|ふじさん|いど|きけん"}],"notes":"挑む means \"to venture at something difficult\" or \"to challenge someone\". With a task it takes に: 記録に挑む, 冬山に挑む, 難問に挑む. With a person, the opponent takes に and the fight takes を: 相手に勝負を挑む. The word sounds elevated and often appears in news and sports reports; in everyday speech people usually say 挑戦する. 挑戦に挑む would be redundant.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"挑戦し続けます。","romaji":"Chōsen shitsuzukemasu.","german":"Ich stelle mich weiter Herausforderungen.","furigana":"ちょうせん|つづ"},{"japanese":"来年、フルマラソンに挑戦するつもりです。","romaji":"Rainen, furu marason ni chousen suru tsumori desu.","german":"Nächstes Jahr will ich mich an einem Marathon versuchen.","furigana":"らいねん|ちょうせん"},{"japanese":"週末、初めてケーキ作りに挑戦してみた。","romaji":"Shuumatsu, hajimete keeki-zukuri ni chousen shite mita.","german":"Am Wochenende habe ich mich zum ersten Mal ans Kuchenbacken gewagt.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|はじ|づく|ちょうせん"}],"notes":"挑戦 ist das Wagen eines schwierigen Versuchs: 〜に挑戦する „sich an etwas versuchen, etwas in Angriff nehmen“. Im Japanischen wird es viel breiter verwendet als das deutsche „Herausforderung“, etwa schon für ein neues Rezept oder eine neue Sportart. Das Lehnwort チャレンジ ist ein häufiges Synonym. Personen: 挑戦者 „Herausforderer“. Das Verb 挑む ist schriftlicher.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"困難を克服しました。","romaji":"Konnan o kokufuku shimashita.","german":"I overcame the difficulties.","furigana":"こんなん|こくふく"},{"japanese":"彼女は病気を克服して、仕事に戻った。","romaji":"Kanojo wa byouki o kokufuku shite, shigoto ni modotta.","german":"She overcame her illness and returned to work.","furigana":"かのじょ|びょうき|こくふく|しごと|もど"},{"japanese":"苦手な数学を克服するために、毎日練習している。","romaji":"Nigate na suugaku o kokufuku suru tame ni, mainichi renshuu shite iru.","german":"To overcome my weakness in math, I practice every day.","furigana":"にがて|すうがく|こくふく|まいにちれんしゅう"}],"notes":"克服 is overcoming a difficulty or weakness: 困難を克服する, 病気を克服する, 苦手を克服する. It is a transitive する-verb with を and sounds somewhat more formal than the synonymous 乗り越える. Typical of reports about illness, crises, fears and academic weaknesses.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"目標を達成しました。","romaji":"Mokuhyō o tassei shimashita.","german":"I achieved my goal.","furigana":"もくひょう|たっせい"},{"japanese":"山の頂上に着いたとき、大きな達成感を味わった。","romaji":"Yama no choujou ni tsuita toki, ookina tasseikan o ajiwatta.","german":"When I reached the summit of the mountain, I felt a great sense of accomplishment.","furigana":"やま|ちょうじょう|つ|おお|たっせいかん|あじ"},{"japanese":"ノルマを達成するまで帰れない。","romaji":"Noruma o tassei suru made kaerenai.","german":"I can't go home until I meet my quota.","furigana":"たっせい|かえ"}],"notes":"達成 means \"achieving, fulfilling\" a goal or target: 目標を達成する, 記録を達成する, ノルマを達成する. For arriving at a place, by contrast, one uses 着く or 到着する. Common is 達成感 \"sense of accomplishment, feeling of having achieved something\". 実現 rather means \"to realize\" (a dream, a plan).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大きな功績を残しました。","romaji":"Ōkina kōseki o nokoshimashita.","german":"He left behind great achievements.","furigana":"おお|こうせき|のこ"},{"japanese":"彼は環境保護の功績が認められて、賞を受けた。","romaji":"Kare wa kankyou hogo no kouseki ga mitomerarete, shou o uketa.","german":"His contributions to environmental protection were recognized, and he received an award.","furigana":"かれ|かんきょうほご|こうせき|みと|しょう|う"},{"japanese":"このプロジェクトの成功は、チーム全員の功績です。","romaji":"Kono purojekuto no seikou wa, chiimu zen'in no kouseki desu.","german":"The success of this project is an achievement of the whole team.","furigana":"せいこう|ぜんいん|こうせき"}],"notes":"功績 is a recognized, significant contribution to a community or a field: 功績を残す, 功績を上げる, 功績が認められる, 功績をたたえる. It is formal and often appears in speeches, obituaries and award citations. 業績 refers more to measurable results (company results, scholarly achievements), and 手柄 is colloquial \"credit, laurels\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"名誉ある賞です。","romaji":"Meiyo aru shō desu.","german":"It's an honorable award.","furigana":"めいよ|しょう"},{"japanese":"代表に選ばれて、とても名誉に思います。","romaji":"Daihyou ni erabarete, totemo meiyo ni omoimasu.","german":"I was chosen as a representative and feel very honored.","furigana":"だいひょう|えら|めいよ|おも"},{"japanese":"嘘の記事で名誉を傷つけられた。","romaji":"Uso no kiji de meiyo o kizutsukerareta.","german":"My reputation was damaged by a false article.","furigana":"うそ|きじ|めいよ|きず"}],"notes":"名誉 means \"honor\" in the sense of distinction (名誉ある賞, 名誉に思う) and \"good reputation\" (名誉を傷つける, 名誉を回復する). As a prefix it means \"honorary\": 名誉教授, 名誉市民. For the modest thank-you formula \"It is an honor for me\" people usually say 光栄です. 名誉 can also be used as a na-adjective: 名誉なこと.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本文化に誇りを持っています。","romaji":"Nihon bunka ni hokori o motte imasu.","german":"I am proud of Japanese culture.","furigana":"にほんぶんか|ほこ|も"},{"japanese":"息子が優勝して、誇りに思います。","romaji":"Musuko ga yuushou shite, hokori ni omoimasu.","german":"My son won, and I'm proud of him.","furigana":"むすこ|ゆうしょう|ほこ|おも"},{"japanese":"彼の言葉は私の誇りを傷つけた。","romaji":"Kare no kotoba wa watashi no hokori o kizutsuketa.","german":"His words hurt my pride.","furigana":"かれ|ことば|わたし|ほこ|きず"}],"notes":"誇り is positive pride in something valuable: 〜に誇りを持つ, 〜を誇りに思う, 誇りを傷つける. The verb is 誇る, the adjective 誇らしい \"proud, feeling proud\". プライド is often used for sensitive pride or ego (プライドが高い). Note: ほこり in kana usually means 埃 \"dust\", so 誇り is almost always written with kanji.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自信を持ってください。","romaji":"Jishin o motte kudasai.","german":"Please have confidence.","furigana":"じしん|も"},{"japanese":"料理には自信があります。","romaji":"Ryouri ni wa jishin ga arimasu.","german":"I'm confident about cooking.","furigana":"りょうり|じしん"},{"japanese":"試験に落ちて、自信をなくしてしまった。","romaji":"Shiken ni ochite, jishin o nakushite shimatta.","german":"After failing the exam, I lost my confidence.","furigana":"しけん|お|じしん"}],"notes":"自信 is confidence in one's own abilities: 自信がある／ない, 〜に自信がある \"to be confident in something\", 自信を持つ, 自信をなくす, 自信満々 \"full of confidence\", 自信過剰 \"overconfident\". Beware of the identically read 地震 \"earthquake\". 自身 (oneself, 私自身) is also a homophone.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"勇気を出してください。","romaji":"Yūki o dashite kudasai.","german":"Please have courage.","furigana":"ゆうき|だ"},{"japanese":"彼の言葉に勇気をもらった。","romaji":"Kare no kotoba ni yuuki o moratta.","german":"His words gave me courage.","furigana":"かれ|ことば|ゆうき"},{"japanese":"一人で海外に行くのは勇気がいる。","romaji":"Hitori de kaigai ni iku no wa yuuki ga iru.","german":"Going abroad alone takes courage.","furigana":"ひとり|かいがい|い|ゆうき"}],"notes":"勇気 is \"courage\": 勇気を出す \"to muster courage\", 勇気がある, 〜には勇気がいる \"it takes courage to do that\". Typically Japanese are 勇気をもらう \"to be encouraged\" and 勇気を与える \"to give courage\", often about athletes or artists; there is also the verb 勇気づける. 勇敢 (な-adjective) describes brave, valiant action, and 度胸 more nerve.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ゲームに夢中です。","romaji":"Gēmu ni muchū desu.","german":"I'm absorbed in the game.","furigana":"むちゅう"},{"japanese":"夢中になって本を読んでいたら、朝になっていた。","romaji":"Muchuu ni natte hon o yonde itara, asa ni natte ita.","german":"I was so absorbed in reading the book that suddenly it was morning.","furigana":"むちゅう|ほん|よ|あさ"},{"japanese":"子どもたちは夢中で虫を追いかけている。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa muchuu de mushi o oikakete iru.","german":"The children are chasing insects, completely absorbed.","furigana":"こ|むちゅう|むし|お"}],"notes":"夢中 means to be completely absorbed in something: 〜に夢中だ／夢中になる \"to be enthusiastic about, absorbed in, crazy about\" (ゲームに夢中, 彼女に夢中). 夢中で describes acting oblivious to oneself, without thinking of anything else. Intensified: 無我夢中 \"completely oblivious to oneself\". 熱中 is more matter-of-fact; 夢中 stresses more strongly forgetting one's surroundings. Grammatically a な-adjective or noun.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"熱心に勉強しています。","romaji":"Nesshin ni benkyō shite imasu.","german":"He is studying diligently.","furigana":"ねっしん|べんきょう"},{"japanese":"田中さんは仕事に熱心な人です。","romaji":"Tanaka-san wa shigoto ni nesshin na hito desu.","german":"Mr. Tanaka is a person who is dedicated to his work.","furigana":"たなか|しごと|ねっしん|ひと"},{"japanese":"子どもたちは先生の話を熱心に聞いていました。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa sensei no hanashi o nesshin ni kiite imashita.","german":"The children were listening to the teacher with rapt attention.","furigana":"こ|せんせい|はなし|ねっしん|き"}],"notes":"熱心 is a な-adjective: 熱心な学生 \"a diligent student\", adverbially 熱心に勉強する \"to study diligently\". What one is dedicated to takes に: 仕事に熱心だ; as a compound 教育熱心な親 \"parents who place great value on education\". In contrast to 一生懸命 (with all one's might, often in a concrete situation), 熱心 describes more a lasting attitude of interest and commitment. Almost always written in kanji.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"真剣に考えています。","romaji":"Shinken ni kangaete imasu.","german":"I'm thinking about it seriously.","furigana":"しんけん|かんが"},{"japanese":"彼は真剣な顔で話し始めた。","romaji":"Kare wa shinken na kao de hanashi hajimeta.","german":"He began to speak with a serious look.","furigana":"かれ|しんけん|かお|はな|はじ"},{"japanese":"遊びではなく、真剣に付き合いたい。","romaji":"Asobi de wa naku, shinken ni tsukiaitai.","german":"I don't want a fling; I want a serious relationship.","furigana":"あそ|しんけん|つ|あ"}],"notes":"真剣 describes a serious, fully concentrated attitude in a situation: 真剣に考える, 真剣な顔, 真剣に付き合う. まじめ, by contrast, is a character trait (\"diligent, conscientious\"), and 本気 stresses that something is meant seriously and is not a joke. Literally, 真剣 is a \"real sword\" instead of a practice sword; hence 真剣勝負 \"a life-and-death fight, a serious contest\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"堅実な経営です。","romaji":"Kenjitsu na keiei desu.","german":"The management is sound.","furigana":"けんじつ|けいえい"},{"japanese":"彼は派手さはないが、堅実な選手だ。","romaji":"Kare wa hadesa wa nai ga, kenjitsu na senshu da.","german":"He isn't flashy, but he's a dependable player.","furigana":"かれ|はで|けんじつ|せんしゅ"},{"japanese":"投資はしないで、堅実に貯金している。","romaji":"Toushi wa shinaide, kenjitsu ni chokin shite iru.","german":"I don't invest; I save steadily instead.","furigana":"とうし|けんじつ|ちょきん"}],"notes":"堅実 means \"solid, reliable, without risk\": 堅実な経営, 堅実な生き方, 堅実に貯金する. It praises a cautious, down-to-earth way that is not spectacular but reaches its goal safely. 着実, by contrast, stresses steady, step-by-step progress, and 確実 the certainty of a result. Typical of descriptions of business and people.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"正当な理由があります。","romaji":"Seitō na riyū ga arimasu.","german":"There is a legitimate reason.","furigana":"せいとう|りゆう"},{"japanese":"働いた分の正当な給料を求めた。","romaji":"Hataraita bun no seitou na kyuuryou o motometa.","german":"I demanded proper pay for the work I had done.","furigana":"はたら|ぶん|せいとう|きゅうりょう|もと"},{"japanese":"彼の主張は正当だと思う。","romaji":"Kare no shuchou wa seitou da to omou.","german":"I think his claim is justified.","furigana":"かれ|しゅちょう|せいとう|おも"}],"notes":"正当 means \"justified, legitimate, proper\" and mostly stands before nouns: 正当な理由, 正当な権利, 正当な評価, 正当な給料. 正当化する means \"to justify\", 正当防衛 \"self-defense\". Do not confuse it with the homophone 正統 \"orthodox, legitimate (succession)\". 妥当 rather means \"appropriate, plausible\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"公正な判断をします。","romaji":"Kōsei na handan o shimasu.","german":"We will make a fair judgment.","furigana":"こうせい|はんだん"},{"japanese":"選挙は公正に行われなければなりません。","romaji":"Senkyo wa kousei ni okonawarenakereba narimasen.","german":"Elections must be conducted fairly.","furigana":"せんきょ|こうせい|おこな"},{"japanese":"審判の判定は公正ではなかったと思う。","romaji":"Shinpan no hantei wa kousei de wa nakatta to omou.","german":"I don't think the referee's call was impartial.","furigana":"しんぱん|はんてい|こうせい|おも"}],"notes":"公正 means \"just, impartial\" in the sense of correct and free of favoritism; as a な-adjective: 公正な判断, 公正な選挙, 公正な裁判. The similar word 公平, by contrast, stresses equal treatment of everyone (\"everybody gets the same\"). 公正 sounds objective to formal and often appears in fixed terms such as 公正取引委員会 (Fair Trade Commission); adverbially it is 公正に扱う \"to treat fairly\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"対等な関係です。","romaji":"Taitō na kankei desu.","german":"It's a relationship between equals.","furigana":"たいとう|かんけい"},{"japanese":"新人でも、会議では対等に意見を言っていいですよ。","romaji":"Shinjin demo, kaigi de wa taitou ni iken o itte ii desu yo.","german":"Even as a newcomer, you can voice your opinion on equal terms in meetings.","furigana":"しんじん|かいぎ|たいとう|いけん|い"},{"japanese":"二つの会社は対等な立場で合併しました。","romaji":"Futatsu no kaisha wa taitou na tachiba de gappei shimashita.","german":"The two companies merged as equal partners.","furigana":"ふた|かいしゃ|たいとう|たちば|がっぺい"}],"notes":"対等 describes equality of rank between persons or parties: \"on an equal footing, with equal rights\". Typical are 対等な関係, 対等な立場 and adverbial 対等に話す／対等に扱う; it is also possible with の (対等の条件). Unlike 同じ \"same, identical\", 対等 is about status and power, not appearance or quantity. The opposite would be something like 上下関係 \"hierarchy\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"有能な人材です。","romaji":"Yūnō na jinzai desu.","german":"She is a capable professional.","furigana":"ゆうのう|じんざい"},{"japanese":"彼女は有能な秘書で、社長にとても信頼されている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa yuunou na hisho de, shachou ni totemo shinrai sarete iru.","german":"She is a competent secretary and is highly trusted by the president.","furigana":"かのじょ|ゆうのう|ひしょ|しゃちょう|しんらい"},{"japanese":"有能な人ほど、仕事を人に任せるのが上手です。","romaji":"Yuunou na hito hodo, shigoto o hito ni makaseru no ga jouzu desu.","german":"The more capable a person is, the better they are at delegating work.","furigana":"ゆうのう|ひと|しごと|ひと|まか|じょうず"}],"notes":"有能 means \"capable, competent\" and evaluates how well someone does their work; mostly a な-adjective before people: 有能な社員, 有能な秘書, 有能な人材. It sounds somewhat more objective and literary than できる人 or 仕事ができる, which you hear more often in daily life. The opposite is 無能; for outstanding performance in school or exams, 優秀 fits better.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"無能な上司です。","romaji":"Munō na jōshi desu.","german":"He is an incompetent boss.","furigana":"むのう|じょうし"},{"japanese":"何もできなくて、自分の無能さが嫌になった。","romaji":"Nani mo dekinakute, jibun no munousa ga iya ni natta.","german":"I couldn't do anything and grew sick of my own incompetence.","furigana":"なに|じぶん|むのう|いや"},{"japanese":"新聞は政府を無能だと厳しく批判した。","romaji":"Shinbun wa seifu o munou da to kibishiku hihan shita.","german":"The newspaper harshly criticized the government as incompetent.","furigana":"しんぶん|せいふ|むのう|きび|ひはん"}],"notes":"無能 means \"incapable, incompetent\" and is a harsh, derogatory judgment of a person: 無能な上司, 無能な政治家. Said to someone's face it is very insulting; in everyday speech people prefer to paraphrase with 仕事ができない or 頼りない. It can also be used self-critically (自分の無能さ \"one's own incompetence\"). Opposite: 有能.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"優秀な学生です。","romaji":"Yūshū na gakusei desu.","german":"He is an outstanding student.","furigana":"ゆうしゅう|がくせい"},{"japanese":"彼は優秀な成績で大学を卒業した。","romaji":"Kare wa yuushuu na seiseki de daigaku o sotsugyou shita.","german":"He graduated from university with excellent grades.","furigana":"かれ|ゆうしゅう|せいせき|だいがく|そつぎょう"},{"japanese":"この作品はコンテストで優秀賞をもらいました。","romaji":"Kono sakuhin wa kontesuto de yuushuushou o moraimashita.","german":"This work received an excellence award at the contest.","furigana":"さくひん|ゆうしゅうしょう"}],"notes":"優秀 means \"outstanding, excellent\" and praises performance and talent, especially in pupils, students and employees: 優秀な学生, 優秀な成績, 優秀な人材. Products or works can also be 優秀 (優秀賞 \"award for outstanding achievement\"). Unlike 有能 (\"competent on the job\"), 優秀 sounds like top performance and intelligence; colloquially people often say 頭がいい or すごくできる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この製品は他社の製品より品質が劣ります。","romaji":"Kono seihin wa tasha no seihin yori hinshitsu ga otorimasu.","german":"This product is inferior in quality to those of other companies.","furigana":"せいひん|たしゃ|せいひん|ひんしつ|おと"},{"japanese":"体力では若い選手に劣るが、経験では負けない。","romaji":"Tairyoku de wa wakai senshu ni otoru ga, keiken de wa makenai.","german":"I'm physically inferior to the young players, but I'm not beaten in experience.","furigana":"たいりょく|わか|せんしゅ|おと|けいけん|ま"},{"japanese":"安いけれど、味は高い店に劣らない。","romaji":"Yasui keredo, aji wa takai mise ni otoranai.","german":"It's cheap, but the taste is no worse than expensive restaurants.","furigana":"やす|あじ|たか|みせ|おと"}],"notes":"劣る is intransitive and means \"to be worse than, to be inferior\"; the point of comparison takes より or に, the area で or が: AはBより品質が劣る, 体力で劣る. It is rather objective and written; in conversation people more often say 負けている or よくない. The counterpart is 勝る; the fixed phrase 勝るとも劣らない means \"is in no way inferior\". Very often negated: 劣らない \"not inferior\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"健康に勝るものはありません。","romaji":"Kenkou ni masaru mono wa arimasen.","german":"Nothing is better than health.","furigana":"けんこう|まさ"},{"japanese":"この店のラーメンは、有名店に勝るとも劣らない味だ。","romaji":"Kono mise no raamen wa, yuumeiten ni masaru tomo otoranai aji da.","german":"The ramen at this shop tastes at least as good as that of famous shops.","furigana":"みせ|ゆうめいてん|まさ|おと|あじ"},{"japanese":"見た目は劣るが、性能ではこちらのほうが勝る。","romaji":"Mitame wa otoru ga, seinou de wa kochira no hou ga masaru.","german":"It's inferior in looks, but this one is superior in performance.","furigana":"み|め|おと|せいのう|まさ"}],"notes":"勝る (まさる) is intransitive and means \"to be superior, to surpass\"; the comparison is made with に or より: 健康に勝るものはない \"nothing surpasses health\". Do not confuse it with 勝つ (かつ) \"to win\": 勝る describes a lasting advantage in quality or ability, not the result of a competition. Because 勝って can be read both as かって and as まさって, 勝る is mostly seen in the dictionary form or in fixed phrases such as 勝るとも劣らない \"at least equal\". It sounds elevated.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女の実力はプロに匹敵します。","romaji":"Kanojo no jitsuryoku wa puro ni hitteki shimasu.","german":"Her ability rivals that of professionals.","furigana":"かのじょ|じつりょく|ひってき"},{"japanese":"その時計の値段は、私の給料三か月分に匹敵する。","romaji":"Sono tokei no nedan wa, watashi no kyuuryou sankagetsubun ni hitteki suru.","german":"The price of that watch is equivalent to three months of my salary.","furigana":"とけい|ねだん|わたし|きゅうりょうさん|げつぶん|ひってき"},{"japanese":"この町の夜景に匹敵する景色は、ほかにないと思う。","romaji":"Kono machi no yakei ni hitteki suru keshiki wa, hoka ni nai to omou.","german":"I don't think any other scenery rivals this town's night view.","furigana":"まち|やけい|ひってき|けしき|おも"}],"notes":"匹敵する is intransitive and means \"to be equal to, to be able to measure up to\"; the object of comparison always takes に: プロに匹敵する実力, 一年分の給料に匹敵する金額. It is mostly about abilities, sizes or amounts that equal something known, often something higher. It sounds matter-of-fact to literary; in conversation people more often say 〜と同じくらい or 〜に負けない. Negated (匹敵するものはない) it means \"incomparable\".","levelNote":"Derived from 匹敵 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"事業を拡大します。","romaji":"Jigyō o kakudai shimasu.","german":"We will expand the business.","furigana":"じぎょう|かくだい"},{"japanese":"字が小さいので、画面を拡大して見てください。","romaji":"Ji ga chiisai node, gamen o kakudai shite mite kudasai.","german":"The text is small, so please enlarge the display.","furigana":"じ|ちい|がめん|かくだい|み"},{"japanese":"台風の被害がさらに拡大しています。","romaji":"Taifuu no higai ga sara ni kakudai shite imasu.","german":"The damage from the typhoon is spreading further.","furigana":"たいふう|ひがい|かくだい"}],"notes":"拡大 means \"expansion, widening\" and also \"enlargement\" (optical). As a する-verb it is transitive (事業を拡大する, 画面を拡大する) and intransitive (被害が拡大する \"the damage spreads\"). Typical are 拡大コピー \"enlarged copy\" and 感染拡大 \"spread of an infection\". Opposite: 縮小; in daily life, for pictures people also simply say 大きくする.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"規模を縮小します。","romaji":"Kibo o shukushō shimasu.","german":"We will scale down the operation.","furigana":"きぼ|しゅくしょう"},{"japanese":"この地図をA4サイズに縮小してコピーしてください。","romaji":"Kono chizu o ee-yon saizu ni shukushou shite kopii shite kudasai.","german":"Please copy this map reduced to A4 size."},{"japanese":"人口が減って、国内の市場が縮小している。","romaji":"Jinkou ga hette, kokunai no shijou ga shukushou shite iru.","german":"As the population declines, the domestic market is shrinking.","furigana":"じんこう|へ|こくない|しじょう|しゅくしょう"}],"notes":"縮小 means \"reduction, scaling down, cutback\": 規模を縮小する, 予算を縮小する, 縮小コピー. Like 拡大, it is both transitive and intransitive (市場が縮小する \"the market shrinks\"). For money and quantities in general, 削減 \"cut\" often fits too; for clothes that have shrunk, one says 縮む instead. Opposite: 拡大.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人口が増加しています。","romaji":"Jinkō ga zōka shite imasu.","german":"The population is increasing.","furigana":"じんこう|ぞうか"},{"japanese":"外国人観光客の数は去年より二割増加した。","romaji":"Gaikokujin kankoukyaku no kazu wa kyonen yori niwari zouka shita.","german":"The number of foreign tourists rose by 20 percent from last year.","furigana":"がいこくじんかんこうきゃく|かず|きょねん|にわりぞうか"},{"japanese":"最近、ネットでの買い物の増加が目立ちます。","romaji":"Saikin, netto de no kaimono no zouka ga medachimasu.","german":"Recently, the increase in online shopping stands out.","furigana":"さいきん|か|もの|ぞうか|めだ"}],"notes":"増加 is the objective word for \"increase, rise\" in numbers and quantities: 人口の増加, 観光客が増加する. As a する-verb it is almost always used intransitively with が, like the everyday word 増える; but 増加 sounds like statistics, news and reports. Often with numbers: 前年より10％増加した. Opposite: 減少.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"出生率が減少しています。","romaji":"Shusseiritsu ga genshō shite imasu.","german":"The birth rate is declining.","furigana":"しゅっせいりつ|げんしょう"},{"japanese":"この村の人口は、ここ十年で半分に減少した。","romaji":"Kono mura no jinkou wa, koko juunen de hanbun ni genshou shita.","german":"This village's population has halved in the past ten years.","furigana":"むら|じんこう|じゅうねん|はんぶん|げんしょう"},{"japanese":"交通事故の件数は減少傾向にあります。","romaji":"Koutsuu jiko no kensuu wa genshou keikou ni arimasu.","german":"The number of traffic accidents is on a downward trend.","furigana":"こうつうじこ|けんすう|げんしょうけいこう"}],"notes":"減少 is the formal word for \"decrease, decline\": 人口の減少, 売り上げが減少する. As a する-verb it is mostly intransitive with が, corresponding to the everyday word 減る. Typical in the news and statistics, often with percentages or 〜傾向 (\"tendency\"): 減少傾向にある. Opposite: 増加.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"気温が上昇しています。","romaji":"Kion ga jōshō shite imasu.","german":"The temperature is rising.","furigana":"きおん|じょうしょう"},{"japanese":"物価の上昇に給料が追いつかない。","romaji":"Bukka no joushou ni kyuuryou ga oitsukanai.","german":"Salaries can't keep up with the rise in prices.","furigana":"ぶっか|じょうしょう|きゅうりょう|お"},{"japanese":"飛行機は離陸した後、ゆっくり上昇していった。","romaji":"Hikouki wa ririku shita ato, yukkuri joushou shite itta.","german":"After takeoff, the plane climbed slowly.","furigana":"ひこうき|りりく|あと|じょうしょう"}],"notes":"上昇 means \"rise, ascent\" and is used intransitively with が: 気温が上昇する, 物価が上昇する. It describes values, rates and temperatures, but also upward movement (飛行機が上昇する). In everyday speech people say 上がる instead; 上昇 sounds like weather reports and economic news. Opposite: 下降, for values also 低下.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"株価が下降しています。","romaji":"Kabuka ga kakō shite imasu.","german":"Stock prices are falling.","furigana":"かぶか|かこう"},{"japanese":"飛行機が雲の下まで下降すると、町が見えてきた。","romaji":"Hikouki ga kumo no shita made kakou suru to, machi ga miete kita.","german":"When the plane descended below the clouds, the town came into view.","furigana":"ひこうき|くも|した|かこう|まち|み"},{"japanese":"そのチームの人気は、ここ数年下降線をたどっている。","romaji":"Sono chiimu no ninki wa, koko suunen kakousen o tadotte iru.","german":"That team's popularity has been on the decline for the past few years.","furigana":"にんき|すうねんかこうせん"}],"notes":"下降 means \"descent, falling\" and is intransitive: 飛行機が下降する, 成績が下降する. It describes both downward movement and falling values; it is the counterpart of 上昇. For prices and rates, the news often uses 下落, and for performance and quality rather 低下. Typical is 下降線をたどる \"to be on the decline\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"為替の変動が激しいです。","romaji":"Kawase no hendō ga hageshii desu.","german":"Currency fluctuations are severe.","furigana":"かわせ|へんどう|はげ"},{"japanese":"野菜の値段は天気によって大きく変動する。","romaji":"Yasai no nedan wa tenki ni yotte ookiku hendou suru.","german":"Vegetable prices fluctuate greatly depending on the weather.","furigana":"やさい|ねだん|てんき|おお|へんどう"},{"japanese":"この仕事は月によって収入が変動するので、少し不安です。","romaji":"Kono shigoto wa tsuki ni yotte shuunyuu ga hendou suru node, sukoshi fuan desu.","german":"In this job my income fluctuates from month to month, so I'm a little worried.","furigana":"しごと|つき|しゅうにゅう|へんどう|すこ|ふあん"}],"notes":"変動 refers to fluctuations and changes of values that go up and down: 為替の変動, 価格が変動する, 気温の変動. As a する-verb it is intransitive. In contrast to the general 変化 \"change\", 変動 stresses the fluctuation of numbers, prices or conditions, often in economics and nature (地殻変動). Often with 激しい or 大きい.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"天気が不安定です。","romaji":"Tenki ga fuantei desu.","german":"The weather is unsettled.","furigana":"てんき|ふあんてい"},{"japanese":"ここはWi-Fiが不安定で、よく切れます。","romaji":"Koko wa waifai ga fuantei de, yoku kiremasu.","german":"The Wi-Fi is unstable here and often drops."},{"japanese":"アルバイトだけでは収入が不安定だ。","romaji":"Arubaito dake de wa shuunyuu ga fuantei da.","german":"With part-time jobs alone, the income is unstable.","furigana":"しゅうにゅう|ふあんてい"}],"notes":"不安定 is a な-adjective for \"unstable, unsteady, shaky\": 天気が不安定だ, 不安定な仕事, 気持ちが不安定になる. It fits weather, situations, income, connections (電波が不安定) and feelings. Not to be confused with 不安 \"worry, unease\": 不安定 describes a state that easily tips over, 不安 a feeling. Opposite: 安定した.","levelNote":"Compound: 不 (N3) + 安定 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"需要と供給の均衡を保ちます。","romaji":"Juyō to kyōkyū no kinkō o tamochimasu.","german":"We maintain the balance of supply and demand.","furigana":"じゅよう|きょうきゅう|きんこう|たも"},{"japanese":"後半20分、ゴールで試合の均衡が破れた。","romaji":"Kouhan nijuppun, gooru de shiai no kinkou ga yabureta.","german":"In the 20th minute of the second half, a goal broke the balance of the match.","furigana":"こうはん|ぷん|しあい|きんこう|やぶ"},{"japanese":"大国の間の力の均衡が崩れると、戦争の危険が高まる。","romaji":"Taikoku no aida no chikara no kinkou ga kuzureru to, sensou no kiken ga takamaru.","german":"When the balance of power between the great powers collapses, the danger of war rises.","furigana":"たいこく|あいだ|ちから|きんこう|くず|せんそう|きけん|たか"}],"notes":"均衡 means \"equilibrium\" between forces or quantities and is a rather formal word from economics, politics and sports: 需要と供給の均衡, 力の均衡. Typical verbs are 均衡を保つ \"to maintain the balance\" and 均衡が崩れる／均衡を破る \"the balance tips / is broken\", for example when the first goal is scored in a match. In everyday speech people mostly say バランス.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本から輸出します。","romaji":"Nihon kara yushutsu shimasu.","german":"We export from Japan.","furigana":"にほん|ゆしゅつ"},{"japanese":"この会社は自動車をアジアの国々に輸出している。","romaji":"Kono kaisha wa jidousha o ajia no kuniguni ni yushutsu shite iru.","german":"This company exports cars to Asian countries.","furigana":"かいしゃ|じどうしゃ|くにぐに|ゆしゅつ"},{"japanese":"日本のお米の輸出が少しずつ増えています。","romaji":"Nihon no okome no yushutsu ga sukoshi zutsu fuete imasu.","german":"Exports of Japanese rice are gradually increasing.","furigana":"にほん|こめ|ゆしゅつ|すこ|ふ"}],"notes":"輸出 means \"export\"; as a する-verb it is transitive: 車を輸出する, 海外へ輸出する. The destination country takes に／へ, the country of origin takes から. Common compounds are 輸出品 \"export goods\" and 輸出国 \"exporting country\". Opposite: 輸入; together they are called 輸出入 \"imports and exports\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"海外から輸入します。","romaji":"Kaigai kara yunyū shimasu.","german":"We import from overseas.","furigana":"かいがい|ゆにゅう"},{"japanese":"日本は石油のほとんどを輸入に頼っている。","romaji":"Nihon wa sekiyu no hotondo o yunyuu ni tayotte iru.","german":"Japan relies on imports for most of its oil.","furigana":"にほん|せきゆ|ゆにゅう|たよ"},{"japanese":"この店では輸入のお菓子やワインが買えます。","romaji":"Kono mise de wa yunyuu no okashi ya wain ga kaemasu.","german":"In this shop you can buy imported sweets and wine.","furigana":"みせ|ゆにゅう|かし|か"}],"notes":"輸入 means \"import\"; as a する-verb it is transitive: 石油を輸入する, 海外から輸入する. Common are 輸入品 \"imported goods\" and 輸入食品; in supermarkets one often reads 〜産 for the place of origin (アメリカ産). 輸入 can also refer to ideas or culture (文化の輸入). Opposite: 輸出.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"物価が上がっています。","romaji":"Bukka ga agatte imasu.","german":"Prices are rising.","furigana":"ぶっか|あ"},{"japanese":"東京は物価が高いので、生活が大変です。","romaji":"Toukyou wa bukka ga takai node, seikatsu ga taihen desu.","german":"Prices are high in Tokyo, so life there is hard.","furigana":"とうきょう|ぶっか|たか|せいかつ|たいへん"},{"japanese":"この国は物価が安くて、旅行しやすい。","romaji":"Kono kuni wa bukka ga yasukute, ryokou shiyasui.","german":"Prices are low in this country, so it's easy to travel.","furigana":"くに|ぶっか|やす|りょこう"}],"notes":"物価 is the general price level, that is, the overall cost of living, not the price of a single item (that is 値段 or 価格). Typical: 物価が高い／安い, 物価が上がる, 物価の上昇. It is used to compare cities and countries (東京は物価が高い). For rising prices in the news you also hear 物価高.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 20"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"外国の通貨に両替します。","romaji":"Gaikoku no tsūka ni ryōgae shimasu.","german":"I'll exchange into foreign currency.","furigana":"がいこく|つうか|りょうがえ"},{"japanese":"ヨーロッパの多くの国では、ユーロという共通の通貨が使われている。","romaji":"Yooroppa no ooku no kuni de wa, yuuro to iu kyoutsuu no tsuuka ga tsukawarete iru.","german":"In many European countries, a common currency, the euro, is used.","furigana":"おお|くに|きょうつう|つうか|つか"},{"japanese":"日本の通貨の単位は円です。","romaji":"Nihon no tsuuka no tan'i wa en desu.","german":"Japan's currency unit is the yen.","furigana":"にほん|つうか|たんい|えん"}],"notes":"通貨 is the \"currency\" as the system or means of payment of a country: 日本の通貨は円です, 外国の通貨, 共通通貨 \"common currency\". In daily life when exchanging money, people often simply say お金 or 外貨 \"foreign currency\"; 通貨 sounds more objective and belongs to economics and the news. Also well known is 仮想通貨 \"cryptocurrency\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"為替レートを確認します。","romaji":"Kawase rēto o kakunin shimasu.","german":"I will check the exchange rate.","furigana":"かわせ|かくにん"},{"japanese":"為替の影響で、輸入品の値段が上がった。","romaji":"Kawase no eikyou de, yunyuuhin no nedan ga agatta.","german":"Because of exchange rates, the prices of imported goods have risen.","furigana":"かわせ|えいきょう|ゆにゅうひん|ねだん|あ"},{"japanese":"昔は郵便為替でお金を送ることがよくあった。","romaji":"Mukashi wa yuubin kawase de okane o okuru koto ga yoku atta.","german":"In the past, people often sent money by postal money order.","furigana":"むかし|ゆうびんかわせ|かね|おく"}],"notes":"為替 has an irregular reading (かわせ) and means \"currency exchange\" or \"money order\". It is most often encountered in 為替レート／為替相場 \"exchange rate\" and 外国為替 \"foreign exchange\"; in the news one hears, for example, 為替が円安に動く. The older meaning \"money order\" lives on in 郵便為替. On its own, 為替 rarely stands for the rate itself - for that one usually says レート.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"預金を引き出します。","romaji":"Yokin o hikidashimasu.","german":"I'm withdrawing my savings.","furigana":"よきん|ひ|だ"},{"japanese":"給料の一部を毎月銀行に預金している。","romaji":"Kyuuryou no ichibu o maitsuki ginkou ni yokin shite iru.","german":"I deposit part of my salary in the bank every month.","furigana":"きゅうりょう|いちぶ|まいつきぎんこう|よきん"},{"japanese":"ATMで預金の残高を確認しました。","romaji":"Eetiiemu de yokin no zandaka o kakunin shimashita.","german":"I checked my account balance at the ATM.","furigana":"よきん|ざんだか|かくにん"}],"notes":"預金 is money deposited at a bank: \"bank deposit, savings\". Typical are 預金する \"to deposit\", 預金を引き出す \"to withdraw\" and 普通預金／定期預金 \"checking / time deposit account\". The everyday word 貯金 means \"savings\" in general (also in a piggy bank) and is used at the post office; 預金 is the bank's technical term. An account is called 口座.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"会社が倒産しました。","romaji":"Kaisha ga tōsan shimashita.","german":"The company went bankrupt.","furigana":"かいしゃ|とうさん"},{"japanese":"不景気で、町の小さな工場がいくつも倒産した。","romaji":"Fukeiki de, machi no chiisana koujou ga ikutsu mo tousan shita.","german":"Because of the recession, several small factories in town went under.","furigana":"ふけいき|まち|ちい|こうじょう|とうさん"},{"japanese":"その会社は倒産寸前だったが、新商品のヒットで助かった。","romaji":"Sono kaisha wa tousan sunzen datta ga, shinshouhin no hitto de tasukatta.","german":"The company was on the brink of bankruptcy but was saved by a hit new product.","furigana":"かいしゃ|とうさんすんぜん|しんしょうひん|たす"}],"notes":"倒産 is the bankruptcy of a company; as a する-verb it is intransitive: 会社が倒産する. For private individuals and as a legal term, 破産 (\"personal bankruptcy, insolvency\") is used. Typical are 倒産寸前 \"on the verge of collapse\" and 倒産の危機. Colloquially one also says 会社がつぶれる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"赤字が続いています。","romaji":"Akaji ga tsuzuite imasu.","german":"We keep running in the red.","furigana":"あかじ|つづ"},{"japanese":"今月は旅行に行ったので、家計が赤字になった。","romaji":"Kongetsu wa ryokou ni itta node, kakei ga akaji ni natta.","german":"We went on a trip this month, so the household budget is in the red.","furigana":"こんげつ|りょこう|い|かけい|あかじ"},{"japanese":"先生が作文に赤字で直しを入れてくれた。","romaji":"Sensei ga sakubun ni akaji de naoshi o irete kureta.","german":"My teacher corrected my composition in red ink.","furigana":"せんせい|さくぶん|あかじ|なお|い"}],"notes":"赤字 literally means \"red characters\" and stands for \"deficit, loss, being in the red\": 赤字になる, 赤字が続く, 赤字経営. It applies to companies, government budgets and also one's own wallet (今月は赤字だ). Opposite: 黒字. In another meaning, 赤字 also means \"corrections in red\" (赤字を入れる \"to correct\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"黒字に転換しました。","romaji":"Kuroji ni tenkan shimashita.","german":"We have turned a profit.","furigana":"くろじ|てんかん"},{"japanese":"新しい店は、開店して半年で黒字になった。","romaji":"Atarashii mise wa, kaiten shite hantoshi de kuroji ni natta.","german":"The new shop became profitable six months after opening.","furigana":"あたら|みせ|かいてん|はんとし|くろじ"},{"japanese":"今月は外食を減らしたので、家計は少し黒字だ。","romaji":"Kongetsu wa gaishoku o herashita node, kakei wa sukoshi kuroji da.","german":"We reduced eating out this month, so the household budget is slightly in the black.","furigana":"こんげつ|がいしょく|へ|かけい|すこ|くろじ"}],"notes":"黒字 literally means \"black characters\" and stands for \"profit, surplus, being in the black\": 黒字になる, 黒字に転換する \"to return to profit\", 黒字を出す. It applies to companies, the government budget (貿易黒字 \"trade surplus\") and the private household. Opposite: 赤字. For the concrete amount of profit, people more often say 利益 or もうけ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"納期を守ります。","romaji":"Nōki o mamorimasu.","german":"We will meet the delivery date.","furigana":"のうき|まも"},{"japanese":"このままでは納期に間に合わないので、今日は残業します。","romaji":"Kono mama de wa nouki ni maniawanai node, kyou wa zangyou shimasu.","german":"At this rate we won't make the delivery date, so I'll work overtime today.","furigana":"のうき|ま|あ|きょう|ざんぎょう"},{"japanese":"部品が届かず、お客様に納期を一週間延ばしてもらった。","romaji":"Buhin ga todokazu, okyakusama ni nouki o isshuukan nobashite moratta.","german":"Because parts didn't arrive, the customer let us extend the delivery date by a week.","furigana":"ぶひん|とど|きゃくさま|のうき|いっしゅうかんの"}],"notes":"納期 is the agreed delivery or submission date for goods and orders, more rarely also for payments. Typical in working life: 納期を守る \"to meet the deadline\", 納期に間に合う／間に合わない, 納期が遅れる, 納期を延ばす. Unlike the general 締め切り (\"submission deadline\", e.g. for applications or papers), 納期 concerns delivery to a customer.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"顧客に連絡します。","romaji":"Kokyaku ni renraku shimasu.","german":"I will contact the customer.","furigana":"こきゃく|れんらく"},{"japanese":"顧客情報の管理には十分注意してください。","romaji":"Kokyaku jouhou no kanri ni wa juubun chuui shite kudasai.","german":"Please be very careful in managing customer information.","furigana":"こきゃくじょうほう|かんり|じゅうぶんちゅうい"},{"japanese":"この会社は顧客の満足度を毎年調査している。","romaji":"Kono kaisha wa kokyaku no manzokudo o maitoshi chousa shite iru.","german":"This company surveys customer satisfaction every year.","furigana":"かいしゃ|こきゃく|まんぞくど|まいとしちょうさ"}],"notes":"顧客 is the business term for \"customer, clientele\", especially for regular customers and customer data: 顧客情報, 顧客満足度, 顧客リスト. In direct contact you never address customers as 顧客, but say お客様; in daily life it is 客 or お客さん. So 顧客 is an objective word from reports, marketing and company internals.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"取引先と会います。","romaji":"Torihikisaki to aimasu.","german":"I'm meeting a business partner.","furigana":"とりひきさき|あ"},{"japanese":"うちの会社は、その銀行と長年取引があります。","romaji":"Uchi no kaisha wa, sono ginkou to naganen torihiki ga arimasu.","german":"Our company has had dealings with that bank for many years.","furigana":"かいしゃ|ぎんこう|ながねんとりひき"},{"japanese":"犯人は警察に取引を持ちかけた。","romaji":"Hannin wa keisatsu ni torihiki o mochikaketa.","german":"The criminal offered the police a deal.","furigana":"はんにん|けいさつ|とりひき|も"}],"notes":"取引 (also written 取り引き) is a \"deal, trade, transaction\" between companies or persons. Typical are 取引先 \"business partner\", 取引がある \"to have business dealings\", 〜と取引する and 取引を始める／やめる. In crime stories and politics, 取引 can also mean a \"deal\" or horse-trading (取引に応じる). It is not used for simply buying something in a shop.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"市場を独占しています。","romaji":"Shijō o dokusen shite imasu.","german":"They monopolize the market.","furigana":"しじょう|どくせん"},{"japanese":"弟が一日中テレビを独占していて、見たい番組が見られない。","romaji":"Otouto ga ichinichijuu terebi o dokusen shite ite, mitai bangumi ga mirarenai.","german":"My younger brother hogs the TV all day, so I can't watch the program I want.","furigana":"おとうと|いちにちじゅう|どくせん|み|ばんぐみ|み"},{"japanese":"この雑誌には、あの俳優の独占インタビューが載っている。","romaji":"Kono zasshi ni wa, ano haiyuu no dokusen intabyuu ga notte iru.","german":"This magazine carries an exclusive interview with that actor.","furigana":"ざっし|はいゆう|どくせん|の"}],"notes":"独占 means \"monopoly, sole possession\": 市場を独占する. Besides the economic meaning, it is often used jokingly in everyday speech for \"to claim for oneself alone\": テレビを独占する, 部屋を独占する. Common compounds are 独占インタビュー \"exclusive interview\" and 独占販売 \"exclusive sales rights\". As a する-verb it is transitive with を.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"商品を展示します。","romaji":"Shōhin o tenji shimasu.","german":"We will display the goods.","furigana":"しょうひん|てんじ"},{"japanese":"申し訳ありません、この商品は売り切れです。","romaji":"Moushiwake arimasen, kono shouhin wa urikire desu.","german":"I'm sorry, this item is sold out.","furigana":"もう|わけ|しょうひん|う|き"},{"japanese":"コンビニでは毎週のように新しい商品が出ます。","romaji":"Konbini de wa maishuu no you ni atarashii shouhin ga demasu.","german":"At convenience stores, new products come out almost every week.","furigana":"まいしゅう|あたら|しょうひん|で"}],"notes":"商品 is the \"goods\" offered for sale: 商品を並べる, 新商品, 人気商品, 商品券 \"gift certificate\". Unlike 製品 (\"product\", from the manufacturer's viewpoint), 商品 stresses sale to customers. 品物 is more general, \"thing, article\", also without a trade connection. In shops you hear sentences like この商品は売り切れです.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"特許を取得しました。","romaji":"Tokkyo o shutoku shimashita.","german":"We obtained a patent.","furigana":"とっきょ|しゅとく"},{"japanese":"この技術については、もう特許を申請してあります。","romaji":"Kono gijutsu ni tsuite wa, mou tokkyo o shinsei shite arimasu.","german":"We have already applied for a patent on this technology.","furigana":"ぎじゅつ|とっきょ|しんせい"},{"japanese":"二つの会社が特許をめぐって裁判で争っている。","romaji":"Futatsu no kaisha ga tokkyo o megutte saiban de arasotte iru.","german":"Two companies are fighting in court over a patent.","furigana":"ふた|かいしゃ|とっきょ|さいばん|あらそ"}],"notes":"特許 is a \"patent\" on an invention. Typical: 特許を取る／取得する \"to obtain a patent\", 特許を申請する \"to apply for\", 特許を持つ. Whoever violates a patent commits 特許侵害. In an older meaning, 特許 also means \"special permission, license\"; in present-day usage, however, it almost always means the patent.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新製品を開発しています。","romaji":"Shinseihin o kaihatsu shite imasu.","german":"We are developing a new product.","furigana":"しんせいひん|かいはつ"},{"japanese":"兄はゲーム会社でアプリの開発をしています。","romaji":"Ani wa geemu gaisha de apuri no kaihatsu o shite imasu.","german":"My older brother develops apps at a game company.","furigana":"あに|がいしゃ|かいはつ"},{"japanese":"この辺りは開発が進んで、昔の森がなくなってしまった。","romaji":"Kono atari wa kaihatsu ga susunde, mukashi no mori ga nakunatte shimatta.","german":"Development has progressed in this area, and the old forest has disappeared.","furigana":"あた|かいはつ|すす|むかし|もり"}],"notes":"開発 means \"development\" of products, technology or software (新製品を開発する, アプリの開発) and also \"development\" of land or resources (土地の開発, 都市開発). As a する-verb it is transitive with を. A person's development or a child's growth, by contrast, is not called 開発 but 成長 or 発達. Common: 開発者, 研究開発.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"将来の展望を語ります。","romaji":"Shōrai no tenbō o katarimasu.","german":"I'll talk about the outlook for the future.","furigana":"しょうらい|てんぼう|かた"},{"japanese":"展望台から町全体を見渡せます。","romaji":"Tenboudai kara machi zentai o miwatasemasu.","german":"From the observation deck you can see the whole town.","furigana":"てんぼうだい|まちぜんたい|みわた"},{"japanese":"社長はインタビューで、来年の展望について話した。","romaji":"Shachou wa intabyuu de, rainen no tenbou ni tsuite hanashita.","german":"In the interview, the company president spoke about the prospects for next year.","furigana":"しゃちょう|らいねん|てんぼう|はな"}],"notes":"展望 literally means \"wide view\" (展望台 \"observation deck\", 展望が開ける) and figuratively \"prospect, outlook\" for the future: 将来の展望, 今後の展望. In the figurative meaning it sounds rather formal and often appears in speeches, interviews and reports. For the plain view from a window, people say 眺め or 景色 in everyday speech.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"効率を上げます。","romaji":"Kōritsu o agemasu.","german":"We'll increase efficiency.","furigana":"こうりつ|あ"},{"japanese":"夜遅くまで勉強するより、朝のほうが効率がいいよ。","romaji":"Yoru osoku made benkyou suru yori, asa no hou ga kouritsu ga ii yo.","german":"You study more efficiently in the morning than late at night.","furigana":"よるおそ|べんきょう|あさ|こうりつ"},{"japanese":"この古いエアコンはエネルギー効率が悪い。","romaji":"Kono furui eakon wa enerugii kouritsu ga warui.","german":"This old air conditioner is not energy efficient.","furigana":"ふる|こうりつ|わる"}],"notes":"効率 is \"efficiency\", the ratio of effort to result. Typical are 効率がいい／悪い, 効率を上げる, 効率よく勉強する and the な-adjective 効率的. 効率 concerns ways of working, machines and energy (エネルギー効率). Do not confuse it with 効果 \"effect\": 効果 asks whether something works, 効率 how economically it works.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"現地の実情を調べます。","romaji":"Genchi no jitsujō o shirabemasu.","german":"We'll look into the actual situation on site.","furigana":"げんち|じつじょう|しら"},{"japanese":"その規則は、学校の実情に合っていない。","romaji":"Sono kisoku wa, gakkou no jitsujou ni atte inai.","german":"That rule doesn't fit the actual situation at schools.","furigana":"きそく|がっこう|じつじょう|あ"},{"japanese":"介護の仕事の実情を知って、驚きました。","romaji":"Kaigo no shigoto no jitsujou o shitte, odorokimashita.","german":"I was surprised to learn what care work is really like.","furigana":"かいご|しごと|じつじょう|し|おどろ"}],"notes":"実情 is the \"actual state of affairs\", how things really are, often in contrast to expectations or official statements: 現場の実情, 実情に合わない規則. It sounds matter-of-fact and appears in reports, news and discussions. The similar 実態 rather stresses the (often hidden) reality of a problem; 事情, by contrast, are a person's \"circumstances, reasons\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仮説を検証します。","romaji":"Kasetsu o kenshō shimasu.","german":"We will verify the hypothesis.","furigana":"かせつ|けんしょう"},{"japanese":"新しい薬の効果を検証するために、実験を行った。","romaji":"Atarashii kusuri no kouka o kenshou suru tame ni, jikken o okonatta.","german":"An experiment was conducted to verify the effect of the new drug.","furigana":"あたら|くすり|こうか|けんしょう|じっけん|おこな"},{"japanese":"事故のあと、警察が現場検証をしていた。","romaji":"Jiko no ato, keisatsu ga genba kenshou o shite ita.","german":"After the accident, the police were conducting an on-site investigation.","furigana":"じこ|けいさつ|げんばけんしょう"}],"notes":"検証 means \"verification, proof\": you check with data or experiments whether something is true or works (仮説を検証する, 効果を検証する). As a する-verb it is transitive with を. Unlike 確認 (\"to take a quick look, to make sure\"), 検証 is a thorough, often scientific or official check; for accidents one speaks of 現場検証 \"on-site inspection\" by the police.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"データを分析します。","romaji":"Dēta o bunseki shimasu.","german":"We will analyze the data.","furigana":"ぶんせき"},{"japanese":"負けた試合のビデオを見て、原因を分析した。","romaji":"Maketa shiai no bideo o mite, gen'in o bunseki shita.","german":"I watched the video of the lost game and analyzed the causes.","furigana":"ま|しあい|み|げんいん|ぶんせき"},{"japanese":"就職活動の前に、自己分析をしておくといい。","romaji":"Shuushoku katsudou no mae ni, jiko bunseki o shite oku to ii.","german":"Before job hunting, it's good to do a self-analysis.","furigana":"しゅうしょくかつどう|まえ|じこぶんせき"}],"notes":"分析 is \"analysis\": breaking something down into its components and examining it closely. As a する-verb it is transitive: データを分析する, 原因を分析する. Common are 分析結果 and 自己分析 \"self-analysis\" (for job hunting). Unlike 調べる (\"to look up, to investigate\", very general), 分析 stresses systematic evaluation.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"高い評価を受けました。","romaji":"Takai hyōka o ukemashita.","german":"We received a high evaluation.","furigana":"たか|ひょうか|う"},{"japanese":"部長は彼の努力を高く評価している。","romaji":"Buchou wa kare no doryoku o takaku hyouka shite iru.","german":"The department head thinks highly of his efforts.","furigana":"ぶちょう|かれ|どりょく|たか|ひょうか"},{"japanese":"このレストランはネットの評価があまりよくない。","romaji":"Kono resutoran wa netto no hyouka ga amari yoku nai.","german":"This restaurant doesn't have very good ratings online.","furigana":"ひょうか"}],"notes":"評価 means \"evaluation, assessment\" and also \"appreciation\": 評価が高い／低い, 高く評価する \"to value highly\", 評価を受ける. As a する-verb it is transitive with を. It is used for grades, performance reviews at work, online ratings (星の評価) and reviews. With 高い or 高く, praise usually resonates; 評判, by contrast, is the \"reputation\" among people.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"重要な指標です。","romaji":"Jūyō na shihyō desu.","german":"It's an important indicator.","furigana":"じゅうよう|しひょう"},{"japanese":"失業率は、景気を知るための指標の一つです。","romaji":"Shitsugyouritsu wa, keiki o shiru tame no shihyou no hitotsu desu.","german":"The unemployment rate is one of the indicators for judging the economy.","furigana":"しつぎょうりつ|けいき|し|しひょう|ひと"},{"japanese":"血圧は健康状態の大切な指標になる。","romaji":"Ketsuatsu wa kenkou joutai no taisetsu na shihyou ni naru.","german":"Blood pressure is an important indicator of one's health.","furigana":"けつあつ|けんこうじょうたい|たいせつ|しひょう"}],"notes":"指標 is an \"indicator, benchmark value\" from which you can read a state or a development: 経済の指標, 健康の指標, 〜の指標になる. It sounds objective and occurs in economics, medicine and statistics. Unlike 目標 (\"goal\"), an 指標 only shows where you stand; a \"standard\" for judging is more 基準.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"対照的な結果です。","romaji":"Taishōteki na kekka desu.","german":"The results are contrasting.","furigana":"たいしょうてき|けっか"},{"japanese":"明るい姉とは対照的に、妹はとても静かだ。","romaji":"Akarui ane to wa taishouteki ni, imouto wa totemo shizuka da.","german":"In contrast to her cheerful older sister, the younger sister is very quiet.","furigana":"あか|あね|たいしょうてき|いもうと|しず"},{"japanese":"翻訳を原文と対照しながら、間違いを探した。","romaji":"Hon'yaku o genbun to taishou shinagara, machigai o sagashita.","german":"I compared the translation with the original and looked for errors.","furigana":"ほんやく|げんぶん|たいしょう|まちが|さが"}],"notes":"対照 means \"juxtaposition, comparison, contrast\". Very common is the な-adjective 対照的 \"contrasting\" (対照的な性格); as a する-verb it means \"to compare side by side\" (原文と訳文を対照する). Note, the same reading たいしょう: 対象 \"object, target group\" (子ども対象) and 対称 \"symmetry\" (左右対称) are different words.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"整合性を確認します。","romaji":"Seigōsei o kakunin shimasu.","german":"We will check the consistency.","furigana":"せいごうせい|かくにん"},{"japanese":"彼の説明は前の話と整合性がない。","romaji":"Kare no setsumei wa mae no hanashi to seigousei ga nai.","german":"His explanation is not consistent with what he said before.","furigana":"かれ|せつめい|まえ|はなし|せいごうせい"},{"japanese":"二つの資料の数字の整合性を取ってください。","romaji":"Futatsu no shiryou no suuji no seigousei o totte kudasai.","german":"Please reconcile the figures in the two documents.","furigana":"ふた|しりょう|すうじ|せいごうせい|と"}],"notes":"整合性 is \"consistency, freedom from contradiction\" - whether parts, data or statements fit together: データの整合性, 説明に整合性がない, 整合性を取る \"to bring into agreement\". The word is objective and typical of offices, IT and law. In conversation people say more simply つじつまが合う／合わない or 矛盾している \"contradictory\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"妥協点を見つけました。","romaji":"Dakyōten o mitsukemashita.","german":"We found a compromise.","furigana":"だきょうてん|み"},{"japanese":"労働組合と会社は、まだ妥協点を探っている。","romaji":"Roudou kumiai to kaisha wa, mada dakyouten o sagutte iru.","german":"The labor union and the company are still looking for common ground.","furigana":"ろうどうくみあい|かいしゃ|だきょうてん|さぐ"},{"japanese":"旅行先で意見が分かれたが、温泉のある海の町という妥協点に落ち着いた。","romaji":"Ryokousaki de iken ga wakareta ga, onsen no aru umi no machi to iu dakyouten ni ochitsuita.","german":"Opinions were divided about the travel destination, but we settled on a compromise: a seaside town with hot springs.","furigana":"りょこうさき|いけん|わ|おんせん|うみ|まち|だきょうてん|お|つ"}],"notes":"妥協点 is the point where both sides can meet: \"compromise solution, common ground\". Typical: 妥協点を探る／見つける, 妥協点に達する. The base word 妥協 (\"compromise, concession\") can also sound negative (妥協しない \"to make no concessions\"), whereas 妥協点 is neutral to positive. Common in negotiations, politics and everyday disputes.","levelNote":"Compound: 妥協 (N1) + 点 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"合意に達しました。","romaji":"Gōi ni tasshimashita.","german":"We reached an agreement.","furigana":"ごうい|たっ"},{"japanese":"両国は貿易のルールについて合意した。","romaji":"Ryoukoku wa boueki no ruuru ni tsuite goui shita.","german":"The two countries agreed on trade rules.","furigana":"りょうこく|ぼうえき|ごうい"},{"japanese":"家を売るには、家族全員の合意が必要だ。","romaji":"Ie o uru ni wa, kazoku zen'in no goui ga hitsuyou da.","german":"Selling the house requires the agreement of the whole family.","furigana":"いえ|う|かぞくぜんいん|ごうい|ひつよう"}],"notes":"合意 is an \"agreement, accord\" between two or more parties: 合意に達する, 合意を得る, 〜と合意する (intransitive, partner with と, content with で or に). It sounds formal and belongs to contracts, politics and negotiations. In everyday speech people more often say 話がまとまる or 意見が一致する. 同意, by contrast, is one side's consent to a proposal.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"正しい判断をします。","romaji":"Tadashii handan o shimasu.","german":"I will make the right decision.","furigana":"ただ|はんだん"},{"japanese":"空の色から判断すると、午後は雨になりそうだ。","romaji":"Sora no iro kara handan suru to, gogo wa ame ni narisou da.","german":"Judging from the color of the sky, it looks like rain this afternoon.","furigana":"そら|いろ|はんだん|ごご|あめ"},{"japanese":"行くかどうかは、あなたの判断に任せます。","romaji":"Iku ka dou ka wa, anata no handan ni makasemasu.","german":"Whether you go or not, I leave to your judgment.","furigana":"い|はんだん|まか"}],"notes":"判断 is \"judgment, assessment\": you assess a situation and decide what is right. Typical: 判断する, 判断を誤る \"to misjudge\", 〜から判断すると \"judging from ...\", 判断に迷う, 自分で判断する. Unlike 決定 (\"official decision\") and 決断 (\"bold resolution\"), 判断 stresses weighing and assessing.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大きな決断をしました。","romaji":"Ōkina ketsudan o shimashita.","german":"I made a big decision.","furigana":"おお|けつだん"},{"japanese":"会社を辞めて留学することを決断した。","romaji":"Kaisha o yamete ryuugaku suru koto o ketsudan shita.","german":"I decided to quit my job and study abroad.","furigana":"かいしゃ|や|りゅうがく|けつだん"},{"japanese":"リーダーには、すぐに決断する力が必要だ。","romaji":"Riidaa ni wa, sugu ni ketsudan suru chikara ga hitsuyou da.","german":"A leader needs the ability to decide quickly.","furigana":"けつだん|ちから|ひつよう"}],"notes":"決断 is a \"resolution\", especially a difficult or courageous one, after long hesitation: 決断する, 決断を下す, 大きな決断, 決断力 \"decisiveness\". Compared with 決める (everyday, also for trifles) and 判断 (matter-of-fact weighing), 決断 stresses determination. For trifles like lunch, 決断 would be exaggerated or jocular.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"難しい選択です。","romaji":"Muzukashii sentaku desu.","german":"It's a difficult choice.","furigana":"むずか|せんたく"},{"japanese":"次の四つの選択肢から、正しい答えを一つ選んでください。","romaji":"Tsugi no yottsu no sentakushi kara, tadashii kotae o hitotsu erande kudasai.","german":"Please choose the correct answer from the following four options.","furigana":"つぎ|よっ|せんたくし|ただ|こた|ひと|えら"},{"japanese":"大学を選択するときは、学費のことも考えたほうがいい。","romaji":"Daigaku o sentaku suru toki wa, gakuhi no koto mo kangaeta hou ga ii.","german":"When choosing a university, you should also think about the tuition.","furigana":"だいがく|せんたく|がくひ|かんが"}],"notes":"選択 is the \"choice, selection\" between possibilities: 選択する, 選択肢 \"option, answer choice\", 正しい選択, 選択を迫られる. It sounds somewhat more objective than the everyday verb 選ぶ. Note: 洗濯 \"doing laundry\" is also read せんたく - the kanji differ. For elections in the political sense, 選挙 is used.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"原因が判明しました。","romaji":"Gen'in ga hanmei shimashita.","german":"The cause has been established.","furigana":"げんいん|はんめい"},{"japanese":"調査の結果、データが間違っていたことが判明した。","romaji":"Chousa no kekka, deeta ga machigatte ita koto ga hanmei shita.","german":"The investigation revealed that the data was wrong.","furigana":"ちょうさ|けっか|まちが|はんめい"},{"japanese":"事故で亡くなった人の身元は、まだ判明していません。","romaji":"Jiko de nakunatta hito no mimoto wa, mada hanmei shite imasen.","german":"The identity of the person who died in the accident has not yet been established.","furigana":"じこ|な|ひと|みもと|はんめい"}],"notes":"判明 means \"to turn out, to become clear\" and is intransitive: 原因が判明する, 身元が判明する. Something unknown becomes known through investigation. The word is typical of the news and reports; in conversation people say 分かる instead. The difference from 解明 (transitive, \"to clear up\"): with 解明 someone actively clears something up, with 判明 it simply becomes known.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"謎を解明します。","romaji":"Nazo o kaimei shimasu.","german":"We will solve the mystery.","furigana":"なぞ|かいめい"},{"japanese":"科学者たちは、この病気の原因の解明に取り組んでいる。","romaji":"Kagakushatachi wa, kono byouki no gen'in no kaimei ni torikunde iru.","german":"Scientists are working on elucidating the cause of this disease.","furigana":"かがくしゃ|びょうき|げんいん|かいめい|と|く"},{"japanese":"警察は事件の真相を解明しようとしている。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa jiken no shinsou o kaimei shiyou to shite iru.","german":"The police are trying to uncover the truth of the case.","furigana":"けいさつ|じけん|しんそう|かいめい"}],"notes":"解明 means \"elucidation, deciphering\" of something mysterious or unknown: 謎を解明する, 原因を解明する, 真相の解明. As a する-verb it is transitive with を; someone (researchers, the police) clears it up actively. The word sounds objective and often appears in science and crime reports. When something becomes known \"on its own\", 判明する fits better.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"状況を把握します。","romaji":"Jōkyō o haaku shimasu.","german":"I'll get a grasp of the situation.","furigana":"じょうきょう|はあく"},{"japanese":"新しいリーダーは、まだチームのメンバーの名前を把握していない。","romaji":"Atarashii riidaa wa, mada chiimu no menbaa no namae o haaku shite inai.","german":"The new leader still doesn't know the names of the team members.","furigana":"あたら|なまえ|はあく"},{"japanese":"毎月いくら使っているか、きちんと把握したほうがいい。","romaji":"Maitsuki ikura tsukatte iru ka, kichinto haaku shita hou ga ii.","german":"You should keep a proper grasp of how much you spend every month.","furigana":"まいつき|つか|はあく"}],"notes":"把握 means \"to grasp, to have a handle on, to have an overview\": 状況を把握する, 内容を把握する, 全体を把握している. As a する-verb it is transitive with を. It is less about deep understanding (理解) than about knowing the facts and the current state. Typical for work and organization; in conversation people also say 分かっている or つかむ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"会社の理念を大切にします。","romaji":"Kaisha no rinen o taisetsu ni shimasu.","german":"We value the company's philosophy.","furigana":"かいしゃ|りねん|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"この学校は「自由と責任」という理念を掲げています。","romaji":"Kono gakkou wa \"jiyuu to sekinin\" to iu rinen o kakagete imasu.","german":"This school upholds the principle of \"freedom and responsibility\".","furigana":"がっこう|じゆう|せきにん|りねん|かか"},{"japanese":"その法律の理念はいいが、現実には難しい。","romaji":"Sono houritsu no rinen wa ii ga, genjitsu ni wa muzukashii.","german":"The principle of that law is good, but in reality it is difficult.","furigana":"ほうりつ|りねん|げんじつ|むずか"}],"notes":"理念 refers to the fundamental idea or guiding principle by which an organization or a person wants to act - especially common in 企業理念 \"corporate philosophy\" and 経営理念 \"management philosophy\". Typical combinations are 理念を掲げる \"to adopt a principle as one's banner\" and 理念に基づく \"to be based on a principle\". Unlike 考え \"thought\" or 思想 \"worldview, ideology\", 理念 sounds formal and goal-oriented; 理想, by contrast, means \"ideal, vision\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"データを分類します。","romaji":"Dēta o bunrui shimasu.","german":"We will classify the data.","furigana":"ぶんるい"},{"japanese":"図書館の本はテーマごとに分類されています。","romaji":"Toshokan no hon wa teema goto ni bunrui sarete imasu.","german":"The books in the library are classified by theme.","furigana":"としょかん|ほん|ぶんるい"},{"japanese":"トマトは野菜に分類されることが多いです。","romaji":"Tomato wa yasai ni bunrui sareru koto ga ooi desu.","german":"Tomatoes are mostly classified as vegetables.","furigana":"やさい|ぶんるい|おお"}],"notes":"分類 is dividing things into groups according to fixed characteristics; as a transitive する-verb: AをBに分類する \"to classify A into B\". The passive is very common: 〜に分類される \"is classified as …\". For trash sorting in daily life, one says rather 分別 (ぶんべつ) or simply 分ける; 分類 sounds more matter-of-fact and systematic, for instance in science, libraries or data processing.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"区別がつきません。","romaji":"Kubetsu ga tsukimasen.","german":"I can't tell them apart.","furigana":"くべつ"},{"japanese":"仕事とプライベートの区別をはっきりつけたいです。","romaji":"Shigoto to puraibeeto no kubetsu o hakkiri tsuketai desu.","german":"I want to clearly separate work and private life.","furigana":"しごと|くべつ"},{"japanese":"ゴミは燃えるものと燃えないものを区別して出してください。","romaji":"Gomi wa moeru mono to moenai mono o kubetsu shite dashite kudasai.","german":"Please separate garbage into burnable and non-burnable before putting it out.","furigana":"も|も|くべつ|だ"}],"notes":"区別 is distinguishing or telling two things apart: AとBを区別する or AとBの区別がつく/つかない \"to be able (or not) to tell A and B apart\"; 区別がつかない in particular is the most everyday form. 差別 looks similar but means \"discrimination\" - mixing them up is embarrassing. 区別 is value-neutral and only says that one recognizes or makes a difference.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本特有の文化です。","romaji":"Nihon tokuyū no bunka desu.","german":"It's a culture unique to Japan.","furigana":"にほんとくゆう|ぶんか"},{"japanese":"この魚には特有のにおいがあります。","romaji":"Kono sakana ni wa tokuyuu no nioi ga arimasu.","german":"This fish has a distinctive smell.","furigana":"さかな|とくゆう"},{"japanese":"急に熱が上がるのは、この病気に特有の症状です。","romaji":"Kyuu ni netsu ga agaru no wa, kono byouki ni tokuyuu no shoujou desu.","german":"A sudden rise in fever is a symptom characteristic of this illness.","furigana":"きゅう|ねつ|あ|びょうき|とくゆう|しょうじょう"}],"notes":"特有 means \"peculiar to a certain thing only, characteristic of\". It almost always appears as 〜特有の + noun or 〜に特有の: 日本特有の習慣, この病気に特有の症状. It is often about smells, tastes, symptoms or peculiarities that one recognizes at once: 特有のにおい. Similar is 独特 \"peculiar, unique\", which stresses the unusual more strongly; 固有 rather means something that belongs to a thing by nature.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"各国固有の問題があります。","romaji":"Kakkoku koyū no mondai ga arimasu.","german":"Each country has its own problems.","furigana":"かっこくこゆう|もんだい"},{"japanese":"「東京」は固有名詞です。","romaji":"\"Toukyou\" wa koyuu meishi desu.","german":"\"Tokyo\" is a proper noun.","furigana":"とうきょう|こゆうめいし"},{"japanese":"この島には固有の動物がたくさんいます。","romaji":"Kono shima ni wa koyuu no doubutsu ga takusan imasu.","german":"There are many animals unique to this island.","furigana":"しま|こゆう|どうぶつ"}],"notes":"固有 describes something that is inherent to a thing by nature or has been from the beginning: 〜固有の文化, 日本固有の植物 \"plants native only to Japan\". Fixed terms are 固有名詞 \"proper noun\" and 固有種 \"endemic species\". Compared with 特有 \"characteristic of\", 固有 stresses original belonging; as an adjective it almost always takes の, and the tone is matter-of-fact and rather literary.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"独自の方法を開発しました。","romaji":"Dokuji no hōhō o kaihatsu shimashita.","german":"We developed our own method.","furigana":"どくじ|ほうほう|かいはつ"},{"japanese":"彼は独自のスタイルで絵を描きます。","romaji":"Kare wa dokuji no sutairu de e o kakimasu.","german":"He paints in his own unique style.","furigana":"かれ|どくじ|え|か"},{"japanese":"これは新聞社が独自に調査した結果です。","romaji":"Kore wa shinbunsha ga dokuji ni chousa shita kekka desu.","german":"This is the result of the newspaper's own investigation.","furigana":"しんぶんしゃ|どくじ|ちょうさ|けっか"}],"notes":"独自 means \"one's own, developed independently of others\" and hence also \"original, distinctive\". It mostly stands as 独自の + noun (独自の方法, 独自のスタイル) or adverbially as 独自に \"independently, on one's own\": 独自に調査する. In contrast to 特有 \"characteristic of\", 独自 stresses that something was created by oneself and not adopted; 独特 \"peculiar\" rather describes the special character.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"双方の意見を聞きます。","romaji":"Sōhō no iken o kikimasu.","german":"We will hear both sides' opinions.","furigana":"そうほう|いけん|き"},{"japanese":"双方が合意して、契約が成立しました。","romaji":"Souhou ga goui shite, keiyaku ga seiritsu shimashita.","german":"Both parties agreed, and the contract was concluded.","furigana":"そうほう|ごうい|けいやく|せいりつ"},{"japanese":"事故の原因は双方にありました。","romaji":"Jiko no gen'in wa souhou ni arimashita.","german":"Both sides were to blame for the accident.","furigana":"じこ|げんいん|そうほう"}],"notes":"双方 means \"both sides, both parties\" and is used above all for counterparts who deal with each other - negotiating partners, parties to a dispute, contract partners: 双方の合意, 双方が納得する. In daily life, for \"both\" people more often say 両方 (for things) or 二人とも (for people); 双方 sounds formal and often appears in the news and business language. Related is 双方向 \"two-way, interactive\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一般的な意見です。","romaji":"Ippanteki na iken desu.","german":"It's a general opinion.","furigana":"いっぱんてき|いけん"},{"japanese":"この庭園は週末だけ一般に公開されています。","romaji":"Kono teien wa shuumatsu dake ippan ni koukai sarete imasu.","german":"This garden is open to the public only on weekends.","furigana":"ていえん|しゅうまつ|いっぱん|こうかい"},{"japanese":"一般の人にも分かるように説明してください。","romaji":"Ippan no hito ni mo wakaru you ni setsumei shite kudasai.","german":"Please explain it so that even laypeople can understand.","furigana":"いっぱん|ひと|わ|せつめい"}],"notes":"一般 means \"general, common\" and also refers to \"the general public, ordinary people\". As a noun it takes の: 一般の人 \"ordinary people, laypeople (not experts or celebrities)\", 一般公開 \"open to the public\". As an adjective people use 一般的な \"generally common, widespread\", as an adverb 一般に or 一般的に \"in general\"; a bare 一般な does not exist. Opposites are 特殊 \"special\" and 専門 \"specialized\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"普遍的な価値があります。","romaji":"Fuhenteki na kachi ga arimasu.","german":"It has universal value.","furigana":"ふへんてき|かち"},{"japanese":"愛は文学の普遍的なテーマです。","romaji":"Ai wa bungaku no fuhenteki na teema desu.","german":"Love is a universal theme of literature.","furigana":"あい|ぶんがく|ふへんてき"},{"japanese":"この考え方はどの国でも普遍的に通用します。","romaji":"Kono kangaekata wa dono kuni demo fuhenteki ni tsuuyou shimasu.","german":"This way of thinking is universally valid in every country.","furigana":"かんが|かた|くに|ふへんてき|つうよう"}],"notes":"普遍 means \"universally valid, applicable everywhere and at all times\". It almost always appears as 普遍的な + noun or 普遍的に: 普遍的な価値, 普遍的なテーマ. The tone is elevated and typical of essays, philosophy and art criticism. Compared with 一般的 \"generally common\", 普遍的 is stronger: it holds for all people and cultures without exception; the opposite is 特殊.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"特殊な技術が必要です。","romaji":"Tokushu na gijutsu ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"Special technology is required.","furigana":"とくしゅ|ぎじゅつ|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"うちの会社はちょっと特殊な事情があって、土曜日も働きます。","romaji":"Uchi no kaisha wa chotto tokushu na jijou ga atte, doyoubi mo hatarakimasu.","german":"My company has somewhat special circumstances, so we work on Saturdays too.","furigana":"かいしゃ|とくしゅ|じじょう|どようび|はたら"},{"japanese":"この映画は特殊メイクがすごいです。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa tokushu meiku ga sugoi desu.","german":"The special-effects makeup in this movie is amazing.","furigana":"えいが|とくしゅ"}],"notes":"特殊 means \"special, particular, unusual\" in the sense of \"different from the normal\": 特殊な技術, 特殊な事情, 特殊メイク \"special-effects makeup\". It is a na-adjective and often sounds matter-of-fact or technical. Unlike 特別 \"special (mostly positive, preferential)\", 特殊 is value-neutral and can also mean \"unusual, hard to handle\". Antonyms are 一般 and 普通.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"具体的に説明してください。","romaji":"Gutaiteki ni setsumei shite kudasai.","german":"Please explain concretely.","furigana":"ぐたいてき|せつめい"},{"japanese":"具体的な例を一つ挙げてもらえますか。","romaji":"Gutaiteki na rei o hitotsu agete moraemasu ka.","german":"Could you give one concrete example?","furigana":"ぐたいてき|れい|ひと|あ"},{"japanese":"旅行の計画はまだ具体的に決まっていません。","romaji":"Ryokou no keikaku wa mada gutaiteki ni kimatte imasen.","german":"The travel plans are not yet concretely decided.","furigana":"りょこう|けいかく|ぐたいてき|き"}],"notes":"具体 alone means \"concreteness\" and hardly occurs on its own; it is learned almost always as 具体的な \"concrete\" and 具体的に \"concretely, in detail\". Typical are 具体的に説明する, 具体的な例 and the compound 具体例 \"concrete example\". The opposite is 抽象的 \"abstract\". A bare 具体な is wrong - the adjective always needs 的.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"抽象的な概念です。","romaji":"Chūshōteki na gainen desu.","german":"It is an abstract concept.","furigana":"ちゅうしょうてき|がいねん"},{"japanese":"あなたの説明は抽象的すぎて、よく分かりません。","romaji":"Anata no setsumei wa chuushouteki sugite, yoku wakarimasen.","german":"Your explanation is too abstract, and I don't really understand it.","furigana":"せつめい|ちゅうしょうてき|わ"},{"japanese":"美術館で抽象画を見ました。","romaji":"Bijutsukan de chuushouga o mimashita.","german":"I saw abstract paintings at the museum.","furigana":"びじゅつかん|ちゅうしょうが|み"}],"notes":"抽象 means \"abstraction\"; in everyday speech it appears almost only as 抽象的な \"abstract\" or 抽象的に. It is often used to criticize statements that are too vague: 説明が抽象的すぎる. Fixed terms are 抽象画 \"abstract painting\" and 抽象化する \"to abstract\". The opposite is 具体的 \"concrete\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"客観的に判断します。","romaji":"Kyakkanteki ni handan shimasu.","german":"I judge objectively.","furigana":"きゃっかんてき|はんだん"},{"japanese":"自分のことを客観的に見るのは難しいです。","romaji":"Jibun no koto o kyakkanteki ni miru no wa muzukashii desu.","german":"It's hard to see oneself objectively.","furigana":"じぶん|きゃっかんてき|み|むずか"},{"japanese":"客観的なデータに基づいて話しましょう。","romaji":"Kyakkanteki na deeta ni motozuite hanashimashou.","german":"Let's talk based on objective data.","furigana":"きゃっかんてき|もと|はな"}],"notes":"客観 means \"objectivity\" and is used almost only as 客観的な \"objective\" or 客観的に \"objectively, factually\": 客観的に見る, 客観的なデータ, 客観的な事実. The counterpart is 主観 or 主観的 \"subjective\". Watch the pronunciation: きゃっかん with a double consonant.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"主観的な意見です。","romaji":"Shukanteki na iken desu.","german":"It's a subjective opinion.","furigana":"しゅかんてき|いけん"},{"japanese":"あくまで私の主観ですが、この店のラーメンが一番おいしいです。","romaji":"Akumade watashi no shukan desu ga, kono mise no raamen ga ichiban oishii desu.","german":"This is strictly my own view, but the ramen here is the best.","furigana":"わたし|しゅかん|みせ|いちばん"},{"japanese":"評価は主観ではなく、データで決めるべきです。","romaji":"Hyouka wa shukan de wa naku, deeta de kimeru beki desu.","german":"Evaluations should be decided by data, not by subjective impressions.","furigana":"ひょうか|しゅかん|き"}],"notes":"主観 is \"subjectivity\", that is, one's own personal view. It mostly appears as 主観的な \"subjective\" or as a noun as in 主観で判断する \"to judge by one's own feelings\"; 私の主観ですが… is a polite introduction to a personal opinion. The counterpart is 客観 or 客観的. Do not confuse it with the similar-sounding 習慣 \"habit\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"相対的な評価です。","romaji":"Sōtaiteki na hyōka desu.","german":"It's a relative evaluation.","furigana":"そうたいてき|ひょうか"},{"japanese":"この町は東京に比べると相対的に家賃が安いです。","romaji":"Kono machi wa Toukyou ni kuraberu to soutaiteki ni yachin ga yasui desu.","german":"Compared with Tokyo, rents in this town are relatively cheap.","furigana":"まち|とうきょう|くら|そうたいてき|やちん|やす"},{"japanese":"アインシュタインは相対性理論で有名です。","romaji":"Ainshutain wa soutaisei riron de yuumei desu.","german":"Einstein is famous for the theory of relativity.","furigana":"そうたいせいりろん|ゆうめい"}],"notes":"相対 means \"relativity\"; it is used almost always as 相対的な \"relative\" or 相対的に \"comparatively, by comparison\": 相対的に安い. The counterpart is 絶対 or 絶対的 \"absolute\". Fixed expressions are 相対評価 \"relative evaluation (compared with others)\" and 相対性理論 \"theory of relativity\". The tone is matter-of-fact and rather literary.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"絶対に間に合います。","romaji":"Zettai ni ma ni aimasu.","german":"We'll definitely make it in time.","furigana":"ぜったい|ま|あ"},{"japanese":"このことは絶対に誰にも言わないでね。","romaji":"Kono koto wa zettai ni dare ni mo iwanaide ne.","german":"Please don't tell this to anyone under any circumstances.","furigana":"ぜったい|だれ|い"},{"japanese":"社長は会社で絶対的な権力を持っています。","romaji":"Shachou wa kaisha de zettaiteki na kenryoku o motte imasu.","german":"The president has absolute power in the company.","furigana":"しゃちょう|かいしゃ|ぜったいてき|けんりょく|も"}],"notes":"絶対 is above all an adverb: 絶対(に) \"definitely, without fail\", in a negative sentence \"under no circumstances\": 絶対に行かない. With の or as 絶対的な it means \"absolute\": 絶対の自信, 絶対的な権力. Colloquially, 絶対 without に is very often used as a strong affirmation; in formal texts this seems too emotional, and 必ず or 確実に is better there. The opposite of 絶対的 is 相対的.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"合理的な考え方です。","romaji":"Gōriteki na kangaekata desu.","german":"It is a rational way of thinking.","furigana":"ごうりてき|かんが|かた"},{"japanese":"もっと合理的な方法で仕事を進めましょう。","romaji":"Motto gouriteki na houhou de shigoto o susumemashou.","german":"Let's carry out the work in a more rational way.","furigana":"ごうりてき|ほうほう|しごと|すす"},{"japanese":"そのルールは不合理だと思います。","romaji":"Sono ruuru wa fugouri da to omoimasu.","german":"I think that rule is unreasonable.","furigana":"ふごうり|おも"}],"notes":"合理 means \"rationality\" and is used practically only in derived forms: 合理的な \"rational, reasonable, efficient\" and 合理化 \"rationalization\". 合理的 often means less \"logical\" than \"practical, without unnecessary effort\": 合理的なやり方. The opposite is 不合理 \"unreasonable, absurd\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"論理的に考えます。","romaji":"Ronriteki ni kangaemasu.","german":"I think logically.","furigana":"ろんりてき|かんが"},{"japanese":"彼の話は論理が通っていません。","romaji":"Kare no hanashi wa ronri ga tootte imasen.","german":"His argument doesn't hold together.","furigana":"かれ|はなし|ろんり|とお"},{"japanese":"レポートは論理的な構成で書いてください。","romaji":"Repooto wa ronriteki na kousei de kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write the report with a logical structure.","furigana":"ろんりてき|こうせい|か"}],"notes":"論理 is \"logic\", that is, the consistent connection of thoughts. As an adjective one uses 論理的な \"logical\", as an adverb 論理的に: 論理的に考える, 論理的な文章. The noun alone is also common: 論理が通らない \"that is not coherent\", 論理の飛躍 \"leap in logic\". For \"logical!\" in the sense of \"of course\", one does not say 論理 but 当然 or もちろん.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"体系的に学びます。","romaji":"Taikeiteki ni manabimasu.","german":"I study systematically.","furigana":"たいけいてき|まな"},{"japanese":"この会社の給与体系は分かりにくいです。","romaji":"Kono kaisha no kyuuyo taikei wa wakarinikui desu.","german":"This company's salary structure is hard to understand.","furigana":"かいしゃ|きゅうよたいけい|わ"},{"japanese":"日本語の敬語は一つの体系になっています。","romaji":"Nihongo no keigo wa hitotsu no taikei ni natte imasu.","german":"Japanese honorific language forms a system of its own.","furigana":"にほんご|けいご|ひと|たいけい"}],"notes":"体系 is an ordered whole made of interrelated parts, that is, a \"system\" in the sense of an organization of knowledge or rules: 法体系 \"legal system\", 給与体系 \"pay structure\". Very common is 体系的な / 体系的に \"systematic(ally)\". For technical systems one usually says システム, and for institutional arrangements 制度.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"重要な役割を果たします。","romaji":"Jūyō na yakuwari o hatashimasu.","german":"It plays an important role.","furigana":"じゅうよう|やくわり|は"},{"japanese":"チームで役割を分担して準備しましょう。","romaji":"Chiimu de yakuwari o buntan shite junbi shimashou.","german":"Let's divide up the roles within the team and prepare.","furigana":"やくわり|ぶんたん|じゅんび"},{"japanese":"家庭での父親の役割も変わってきました。","romaji":"Katei de no chichioya no yakuwari mo kawatte kimashita.","german":"The role of the father in the family has also changed.","furigana":"かてい|ちちおや|やくわり|か"}],"notes":"役割 is the \"role\" or \"task\" that someone or something takes on in a given context. The most typical combination is 役割を果たす \"to play a role, to fulfill a function\"; also 役割を分担する \"to divide tasks\" and 役割分担 \"division of tasks\". For a theater or film role, one says 役 instead; 役目 is similar to 役割, but stresses duty more.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"事件の経緯を説明します。","romaji":"Jiken no keii o setsumei shimasu.","german":"I will explain the course of events of the incident.","furigana":"じけん|けいい|せつめい"},{"japanese":"二人が結婚した経緯を聞きました。","romaji":"Futari ga kekkon shita keii o kikimashita.","german":"I heard how the two came to marry.","furigana":"ふたり|けっこん|けいい|き"},{"japanese":"まず、これまでの経緯を簡単に説明させてください。","romaji":"Mazu, kore made no keii o kantan ni setsumei sasete kudasai.","german":"First, let me briefly explain how things have gone so far.","furigana":"けいい|かんたん|せつめい"}],"notes":"経緯 refers to the course of events, i.e. how a situation came about - the sequence of events with their circumstances: 事件の経緯, これまでの経緯を説明する. It is formal and typical of reports, apologies and the news. The same spelling is also read いきさつ, which sounds a bit more colloquial. The meaning \"longitude and latitude\" plays hardly any role in daily life.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい局面を迎えました。","romaji":"Atarashii kyokumen o mukaemashita.","german":"We have entered a new phase.","furigana":"あたら|きょくめん|むか"},{"japanese":"試合は大事な局面に入りました。","romaji":"Shiai wa daiji na kyokumen ni hairimashita.","german":"The game has entered a crucial phase.","furigana":"しあい|だいじ|きょくめん|はい"},{"japanese":"難しい局面でも、彼は冷静でした。","romaji":"Muzukashii kyokumen demo, kare wa reisei deshita.","german":"Even in the difficult situation, he stayed calm.","furigana":"むずか|きょくめん|かれ|れいせい"}],"notes":"局面 comes from board games such as 囲碁 or 将棋, where it means the \"position\"; figuratively it means the \"situation, phase, stage\" of a development. Typical are 新たな局面を迎える \"to enter a new phase\", 重要な局面 \"crucial moment\" and 難しい局面 \"difficult situation\". Unlike 状況 \"situation (in general)\", 局面 stresses a particular segment within a course of events; the tone is rather formal, as in the news and sports reports.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人生の転機を迎えました。","romaji":"Jinsei no tenki o mukaemashita.","german":"I've reached a turning point in my life.","furigana":"じんせい|てんき|むか"},{"japanese":"病気をしたことが、生活を見直す転機になりました。","romaji":"Byouki o shita koto ga, seikatsu o minaosu tenki ni narimashita.","german":"My illness became a turning point for reconsidering my lifestyle.","furigana":"びょうき|せいかつ|みなお|てんき"},{"japanese":"この新製品の成功が、会社にとって転機となりました。","romaji":"Kono shinseihin no seikou ga, kaisha ni totte tenki to narimashita.","german":"The success of this new product became a turning point for the company.","furigana":"しんせいひん|せいこう|かいしゃ|てんき"}],"notes":"転機 is a \"turning point\" at which life or a development changes fundamentally. It almost always appears in 転機を迎える \"to be at a turning point\", 転機になる / 転機となる \"to become a turning point\" or 人生の転機. It is often used in retrospect and sounds rather positive. Not to be confused with the identically read 天気 \"weather\"; 転換, by contrast, means \"switch, change of course\" as a process.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"社会の変革が進んでいます。","romaji":"Shakai no henkaku ga susunde imasu.","german":"Social transformation is advancing.","furigana":"しゃかい|へんかく|すす"},{"japanese":"インターネットは私たちの生活に大きな変革をもたらしました。","romaji":"Intaanetto wa watashitachi no seikatsu ni ookina henkaku o motarashimashita.","german":"The Internet has brought great change to our lives.","furigana":"わたし|せいかつ|おお|へんかく"},{"japanese":"会社が生き残るには、組織の変革が必要です。","romaji":"Kaisha ga ikinokoru ni wa, soshiki no henkaku ga hitsuyou desu.","german":"For the company to survive, the organization needs to be transformed.","furigana":"かいしゃ|い|のこ|そしき|へんかく|ひつよう"}],"notes":"変革 refers to a far-reaching restructuring of structures, in society, organizations or thinking: 社会の変革, 組織の変革, 変革をもたらす \"to bring about an upheaval\". 改革 \"reform\" usually means the deliberate improvement of a system (政治改革), 変革 rather the fundamental reshaping as a whole; 変化 is simply \"change\". The tone is formal; as a する-verb (変革する) it occurs less often than the noun.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"歴史の変遷を学びます。","romaji":"Rekishi no hensen o manabimasu.","german":"We study historical changes.","furigana":"れきし|へんせん|まな"},{"japanese":"この博物館では、日本の服の変遷を見ることができます。","romaji":"Kono hakubutsukan de wa, Nihon no fuku no hensen o miru koto ga dekimasu.","german":"In this museum you can see how clothing in Japan has changed.","furigana":"はくぶつかん|にほん|ふく|へんせん|み"},{"japanese":"時代とともに言葉の意味も変遷してきました。","romaji":"Jidai to tomo ni kotoba no imi mo hensen shite kimashita.","german":"The meanings of words have also changed with the times.","furigana":"じだい|ことば|いみ|へんせん"}],"notes":"変遷 describes how something has changed step by step over a longer period, the historical change seen in retrospect: 時代の変遷, 言葉の変遷, ファッションの変遷. It is a written-language word, typical of history books, exhibitions and specialist texts; as a する-verb it is intransitive (〜が変遷する). Unlike 変化 \"change\", 変遷 stresses the succession of several stages; 変革, by contrast, means a deliberately brought-about upheaval.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"言葉の起源を調べます。","romaji":"Kotoba no kigen o shirabemasu.","german":"I'm investigating the origin of the word.","furigana":"ことば|きげん|しら"},{"japanese":"生命の起源はまだよく分かっていません。","romaji":"Seimei no kigen wa mada yoku wakatte imasen.","german":"The origin of life is still not well understood.","furigana":"せいめい|きげん|わ"},{"japanese":"このスポーツの起源はイギリスにあると言われています。","romaji":"Kono supootsu no kigen wa Igirisu ni aru to iwarete imasu.","german":"This sport is said to have originated in England.","furigana":"きげん|い"}],"notes":"起源 is the \"origin\" in the sense of the very first beginning from which something arose: 生命の起源, 言葉の起源, 〜に起源がある. It sounds scientific and is used for big questions of history, biology or culture. 由来 is similar, but tends to tell the origin story of a name or custom; 原因 is the \"cause\" of an event. Note: read the same as 機嫌 \"mood\" and 期限 \"deadline\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"名前の由来を教えてください。","romaji":"Namae no yurai o oshiete kudasai.","german":"Please tell me the origin of the name.","furigana":"なまえ|ゆらい|おし"},{"japanese":"「パン」はポルトガル語に由来する言葉です。","romaji":"\"Pan\" wa Porutogarugo ni yurai suru kotoba desu.","german":"\"Pan\" (bread) is a word derived from Portuguese.","furigana":"ご|ゆらい|ことば"},{"japanese":"この化粧品は植物由来の成分で作られています。","romaji":"Kono keshouhin wa shokubutsu yurai no seibun de tsukurarete imasu.","german":"This cosmetic is made with plant-derived ingredients.","furigana":"けしょうひん|しょくぶつゆらい|せいぶん|つく"}],"notes":"由来 is the origin or the story of how something came about, above all of names, words, customs and festivals: 名前の由来, 地名の由来. As an intransitive する-verb, 〜に由来する means \"to go back to ..., to derive from ...\". 〜由来の is also common, e.g. 植物由来の \"of plant origin\". Compared with 起源 \"origin, the very first beginning\", 由来 tends to tell why something has its name or is the way it is.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"世界遺産に登録されました。","romaji":"Sekai isan ni tōroku saremashita.","german":"It was registered as a World Heritage site.","furigana":"せかいいさん|とうろく"},{"japanese":"祖父の遺産を兄弟で分けました。","romaji":"Sofu no isan o kyoudai de wakemashita.","german":"We siblings divided our grandfather's inheritance.","furigana":"そふ|いさん|きょうだい|わ"},{"japanese":"この祭りは町の大切な文化遺産です。","romaji":"Kono matsuri wa machi no taisetsu na bunka isan desu.","german":"This festival is an important cultural heritage of the town.","furigana":"まつ|まち|たいせつ|ぶんかいさん"}],"notes":"遺産 is, first, the \"inheritance\" in the legal sense, i.e. the estate of a deceased person: 遺産を相続する \"to receive an inheritance\", 遺産相続. Second, it means \"heritage, legacy\" in the cultural sense: 文化遺産, 世界遺産 \"World Heritage\". For inheriting as an action, 相続する is used; 遺産 is what is left behind. 財産, by contrast, means \"assets, property\" in general.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"古代の遺跡を見に行きます。","romaji":"Kodai no iseki o mi ni ikimasu.","german":"I'm going to see ancient ruins.","furigana":"こだい|いせき|み|い"},{"japanese":"工事中に古い遺跡が見つかりました。","romaji":"Koujichuu ni furui iseki ga mitsukarimashita.","german":"Old ruins were discovered during construction work.","furigana":"こうじちゅう|ふる|いせき|み"},{"japanese":"マチュピチュは世界で一番有名な遺跡の一つです。","romaji":"Machupichu wa sekai de ichiban yuumei na iseki no hitotsu desu.","german":"Machu Picchu is one of the most famous ruins in the world.","furigana":"せかい|いちばんゆうめい|いせき|ひと"}],"notes":"遺跡 are \"ruins, historical archaeological sites\", places where remains of earlier buildings or settlements are preserved: 古代の遺跡, 遺跡を発掘する \"to excavate a site\". It usually refers to an archaeological place; individual finds are called 遺物, an officially protected historic site 史跡. Do not confuse it with 遺産 \"heritage\" and 奇跡 \"miracle\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"古代文明について学びます。","romaji":"Kodai bunmei ni tsuite manabimasu.","german":"I'm learning about ancient civilizations.","furigana":"こだいぶんめい|まな"},{"japanese":"エジプト文明はナイル川の近くで生まれました。","romaji":"Ejiputo bunmei wa Nairu-gawa no chikaku de umaremashita.","german":"Egyptian civilization arose near the Nile.","furigana":"ぶんめい|がわ|ちか|う"},{"japanese":"文明が発達しても、人の悩みはあまり変わりません。","romaji":"Bunmei ga hattatsu shite mo, hito no nayami wa amari kawarimasen.","german":"Even as civilization advances, people's worries hardly change.","furigana":"ぶんめい|はったつ|ひと|なや|か"}],"notes":"文明 is \"civilization\", above all the technical and material level of development of a society: 古代文明, 文明が発達する, 文明社会. 文化 \"culture\" tends to stress art, customs and spiritual values, 文明 technology, cities and organization. Fixed terms are 四大文明 \"the four great early civilizations\" and 文明開化, the modernization of Japan in the 明治時代.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"様々な民族がいます。","romaji":"Samazama na minzoku ga imasu.","german":"There are various ethnic groups.","furigana":"さまざま|みんぞく"},{"japanese":"お祭りでは民族衣装を着て踊ります。","romaji":"Omatsuri de wa minzoku ishou o kite odorimasu.","german":"At the festival, people dance in traditional costume.","furigana":"まつ|みんぞくいしょう|き|おど"},{"japanese":"中国には五十以上の少数民族が住んでいます。","romaji":"Chuugoku ni wa gojuu ijou no shousuu minzoku ga sunde imasu.","german":"More than fifty ethnic minorities live in China.","furigana":"ちゅうごく|ごじゅういじょう|しょうすうみんぞく|す"}],"notes":"民族 is a group of people with a shared language, culture and history: \"people, ethnic group\". 少数民族 \"ethnic minority\", 多民族国家 \"multi-ethnic state\", 民族衣装 \"traditional costume\". It is not the same as 国民 \"nation, citizens\" or 人種 \"race\" (by physical characteristics). Beware of the similar-sounding 民俗 \"folk customs, folklore\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"移民が増えています。","romaji":"Imin ga fuete imasu.","german":"The number of immigrants is increasing.","furigana":"いみん|ふ"},{"japanese":"祖父は若いころブラジルに移民しました。","romaji":"Sofu wa wakai koro Burajiru ni imin shimashita.","german":"My grandfather emigrated to Brazil when he was young.","furigana":"そふ|わか|いみん"},{"japanese":"移民政策について議論が続いています。","romaji":"Imin seisaku ni tsuite giron ga tsuzuite imasu.","german":"The debate about immigration policy continues.","furigana":"いみんせいさく|ぎろん|つづ"}],"notes":"移民 means both emigration or immigration as a process and the person, the \"migrant\". As an intransitive する-verb: ブラジルに移民する \"to emigrate to Brazil\"; also typical are 移民政策 \"immigration policy\" and 移民を受け入れる \"to accept immigrants\". For an ordinary move abroad, people more often say 海外に移住する; 移民 sounds like permanent migration and is a word from politics and history. Unlike 難民 \"refugee\", migration is usually voluntary.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"難民を受け入れます。","romaji":"Nanmin o ukeiremasu.","german":"We will accept refugees.","furigana":"なんみん|う|い"},{"japanese":"戦争で多くの人が難民になりました。","romaji":"Sensou de ooku no hito ga nanmin ni narimashita.","german":"Many people became refugees because of the war.","furigana":"せんそう|おお|ひと|なんみん"},{"japanese":"地震の日、駅は帰宅難民でいっぱいでした。","romaji":"Jishin no hi, eki wa kitaku nanmin de ippai deshita.","german":"On the day of the earthquake, the station was full of stranded commuters.","furigana":"じしん|ひ|えき|きたくなんみん"}],"notes":"難民 is a \"refugee\" - someone who had to leave their homeland because of war, persecution or disasters: 難民を受け入れる, 難民キャンプ, 難民申請 \"asylum application\". Unlike 移民 \"migrant\", the flight is involuntary. In the media, 〜難民 also forms words for people stuck somewhere or unable to get something: 帰宅難民 \"stranded commuters\", 買い物難民 \"people without access to shopping\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"外交関係を強化します。","romaji":"Gaikō kankei o kyōka shimasu.","german":"We will strengthen diplomatic relations.","furigana":"がいこうかんけい|きょうか"},{"japanese":"兄は外交官として海外で働いています。","romaji":"Ani wa gaikoukan to shite kaigai de hataraite imasu.","german":"My older brother works abroad as a diplomat.","furigana":"あに|がいこうかん|かいがい|はたら"},{"japanese":"この問題は外交で解決するべきです。","romaji":"Kono mondai wa gaikou de kaiketsu suru beki desu.","german":"This problem should be solved through diplomacy.","furigana":"もんだい|がいこう|かいけつ"}],"notes":"外交 is \"diplomacy\", i.e. a state's relations with other states: 外交関係, 外交政策, 外交問題, 外交官 \"diplomat\". In the business world it also refers to outside sales, e.g. in 外交員 \"outside sales representative (e.g. in insurance)\". Careful: the identically read 外向的 means \"extroverted\" and has nothing to do with diplomacy.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"平和条約を結びました。","romaji":"Heiwa jōyaku o musubimashita.","german":"A peace treaty was concluded.","furigana":"へいわじょうやく|むす"},{"japanese":"日本はその条約にまだ加盟していません。","romaji":"Nihon wa sono jouyaku ni mada kamei shite imasen.","german":"Japan has not yet joined that treaty.","furigana":"にほん|じょうやく|かめい"},{"japanese":"歴史の授業で不平等条約について学びました。","romaji":"Rekishi no jugyou de fubyoudou jouyaku ni tsuite manabimashita.","german":"In history class we learned about the unequal treaties.","furigana":"れきし|じゅぎょう|ふびょうどうじょうやく|まな"}],"notes":"条約 is a \"treaty, agreement\" between states or international organizations: 条約を結ぶ \"to conclude a treaty\", 条約に加盟する \"to join a treaty\", 平和条約. For contracts between persons or companies one uses 契約 \"contract\"; 約束 is a personal \"promise, appointment\". 条約 belongs to the language of politics, history and the news.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"紛争を解決します。","romaji":"Funsō o kaiketsu shimasu.","german":"We will resolve the conflict.","furigana":"ふんそう|かいけつ"},{"japanese":"その地域では長い間紛争が続いています。","romaji":"Sono chiiki de wa nagai aida funsou ga tsuzuite imasu.","german":"Conflict has continued in that region for a long time.","furigana":"ちいき|なが|あいだふんそう|つづ"},{"japanese":"隣の会社と土地をめぐる紛争が起きました。","romaji":"Tonari no kaisha to tochi o meguru funsou ga okimashita.","german":"A dispute over land arose with the neighboring company.","furigana":"となり|かいしゃ|とち|ふんそう|お"}],"notes":"紛争 is a \"conflict\" or \"dispute\", usually between groups, states or parties and often over a longer period: 国際紛争, 地域紛争, 民族紛争, 紛争地域 \"conflict zone\". Typical are 紛争が起きる, 紛争が続く and 紛争を解決する. For a private quarrel between two people, people more often say けんか or もめごと; 戦争 is open \"war\", whereas 紛争 also covers smaller armed or legal disputes.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"和平交渉が始まりました。","romaji":"Wahei kōshō ga hajimarimashita.","german":"Peace negotiations have begun.","furigana":"わへいこうしょう|はじ"},{"japanese":"両国はついに和平に合意しました。","romaji":"Ryoukoku wa tsui ni wahei ni goui shimashita.","german":"The two countries finally agreed to peace.","furigana":"りょうこく|わへい|ごうい"},{"japanese":"国連は和平の実現に向けて努力しています。","romaji":"Kokuren wa wahei no jitsugen ni mukete doryoku shite imasu.","german":"The United Nations is working toward achieving a peace settlement.","furigana":"こくれん|わへい|じつげん|む|どりょく"}],"notes":"和平 refers to \"peace\" as the result of negotiations, i.e. the end of a war or conflict: 和平交渉 \"peace negotiations\", 和平合意 \"peace agreement\", 和平が実現する. By contrast, 平和 is the general state of peace, as in 平和を願う \"to wish for peace\". 和平 is a word of news and political language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"国の安全保障はとても重要です。","romaji":"Kuni no anzen hoshou wa totemo juuyou desu.","german":"The country's security is very important.","furigana":"くに|あんぜんほしょう|じゅうよう"},{"japanese":"安全保障理事会が緊急会議を開きました。","romaji":"Anzen hoshou rijikai ga kinkyuu kaigi o hirakimashita.","german":"The Security Council held an emergency meeting.","furigana":"あんぜんほしょうりじかい|きんきゅうかいぎ|ひら"},{"japanese":"今回の選挙では安全保障も大きなテーマになっています。","romaji":"Konkai no senkyo de wa anzen hoshou mo ookina teema ni natte imasu.","german":"In this election, security policy is also a big issue.","furigana":"こんかい|せんきょ|あんぜんほしょう|おお"}],"notes":"安全保障 refers to (above all national) \"security\" and the policy meant to ensure it - defense, alliances, crisis preparedness. It often appears in compounds: 安全保障政策 \"security policy\", 安全保障理事会 \"(UN) Security Council\", 日米安全保障条約, abbreviated 安保 \"Japan-US Security Treaty\". For everyday safety (road traffic, food), one says only 安全; 保障 alone means \"guarantee, safeguard\", as in 社会保障 \"social security\".","levelNote":"Compound: 安全 (N4) + 保障 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人権を守ります。","romaji":"Jinken o mamorimasu.","german":"Wir schützen die Menschenrechte.","furigana":"じんけん|まも"},{"japanese":"その国では人権問題が深刻です。","romaji":"Sono kuni de wa jinken mondai ga shinkoku desu.","german":"In diesem Land sind die Menschenrechtsprobleme gravierend.","furigana":"くに|じんけんもんだい|しんこく"},{"japanese":"子どもの人権も大切にしなければなりません。","romaji":"Kodomo no jinken mo taisetsu ni shinakereba narimasen.","german":"Auch die Rechte von Kindern müssen geachtet werden.","furigana":"こ|じんけん|たいせつ"}],"notes":"人権 sind die „Menschenrechte“ bzw. die Grundrechte jedes Einzelnen: 人権を守る / 尊重する, 人権問題, 基本的人権 „Grundrechte“. Das deutsche Pluralwort „Menschenrechte“ entspricht einfach 人権, ohne Pluralmarkierung. Der Ton ist sachlich-formell; das Wort erscheint in Politik, Recht und Schule, etwa in 人権教育 „Menschenrechtserziehung“.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"公平に扱います。","romaji":"Kōhei ni atsukaimasu.","german":"We treat everyone fairly.","furigana":"こうへい|あつか"},{"japanese":"ケーキを三人で公平に分けました。","romaji":"Keeki o sannin de kouhei ni wakemashita.","german":"We divided the cake fairly among the three of us.","furigana":"さんにん|こうへい|わ"},{"japanese":"あの先生は誰に対しても公平です。","romaji":"Ano sensei wa dare ni taishite mo kouhei desu.","german":"That teacher is fair to everyone.","furigana":"せんせい|だれ|たい|こうへい"}],"notes":"公平 means \"fair, just, impartial\" - everyone is treated equally, without favoritism: 公平な判断, 公平に分ける. It is a な-adjective, adverbially 公平に. The opposite 不公平 \"unfair\" is very common in daily life: それは不公平だ! 平等 \"equality\" stresses equal rights or shares, 公平 impartial treatment, and 公正 \"just, correct\" adherence to rules.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"多様性を尊重します。","romaji":"Tayōsei o sonchō shimasu.","german":"We respect diversity.","furigana":"たようせい|そんちょう"},{"japanese":"この森は生物の多様性が豊かです。","romaji":"Kono mori wa seibutsu no tayousei ga yutaka desu.","german":"This forest is rich in biodiversity.","furigana":"もり|せいぶつ|たようせい|ゆた"},{"japanese":"うちの会社は国籍や年齢の多様性を大切にしています。","romaji":"Uchi no kaisha wa kokuseki ya nenrei no tayousei o taisetsu ni shite imasu.","german":"Our company values diversity in nationality and age.","furigana":"かいしゃ|こくせき|ねんれい|たようせい|たいせつ"}],"notes":"多様性 is \"diversity\", the coexistence of many different species, people or opinions: 生物の多様性 or 生物多様性 \"biodiversity\", 文化の多様性, 多様性を認める / 尊重する. It is the noun of 多様な \"diverse\", formed with the suffix 性 \"-ness, -ity\". The word is matter-of-fact and frequent in politics, education and companies; there the loanword ダイバーシティ is also widespread.","levelNote":"Compound: 多様 (N1) + 性 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自然と共存します。","romaji":"Shizen to kyōzon shimasu.","german":"We coexist with nature.","furigana":"しぜん|きょうぞん"},{"japanese":"この町では古い建物と新しいビルが共存しています。","romaji":"Kono machi de wa furui tatemono to atarashii biru ga kyouzon shite imasu.","german":"In this town, old buildings and new skyscrapers coexist.","furigana":"まち|ふる|たてもの|あたら|きょうぞん"},{"japanese":"野生の動物と人間が共存するのは簡単ではありません。","romaji":"Yasei no doubutsu to ningen ga kyouzon suru no wa kantan de wa arimasen.","german":"It is not easy for wild animals and humans to coexist.","furigana":"やせい|どうぶつ|にんげん|きょうぞん|かんたん"}],"notes":"共存 means \"coexistence\", the peaceful side-by-side existence of different groups, living things or objects: AとBが共存する, 〜と共存する, 人間と自然の共存. The reading きょうぞん is usual; きょうそん is also correct. Similar is 共生 \"living together, symbiosis\", which stresses mutual dependence more strongly.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"社会に貢献します。","romaji":"Shakai ni kōken shimasu.","german":"I contribute to society.","furigana":"しゃかい|こうけん"},{"japanese":"彼のゴールがチームの勝利に貢献しました。","romaji":"Kare no gooru ga chiimu no shouri ni kouken shimashita.","german":"His goal contributed to the team's victory.","furigana":"かれ|しょうり|こうけん"},{"japanese":"御社の発展に貢献したいと考えております。","romaji":"Onsha no hatten ni kouken shitai to kangaete orimasu.","german":"I would like to contribute to the development of your company.","furigana":"おんしゃ|はってん|こうけん|かんが"}],"notes":"貢献 is a \"contribution\" to a goal or to the well-being of others; as an intransitive する-verb: 〜に貢献する \"to contribute to ...\". Typical are 社会に貢献する, チームの勝利に貢献する, 社会貢献 \"contribution to society\" and 大きな貢献をする. Unlike 寄付 \"donation (money, goods)\", 貢献 is any contribution through work, knowledge or effort. The tone is positive and rather formal, often in job applications and speeches.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地域に奉仕します。","romaji":"Chiiki ni hōshi shimasu.","german":"I serve the community.","furigana":"ちいき|ほうし"},{"japanese":"学生たちは週末に公園を掃除する奉仕活動をしました。","romaji":"Gakuseitachi wa shuumatsu ni kouen o souji suru houshi katsudou o shimashita.","german":"The students cleaned the park on the weekend as community service.","furigana":"がくせい|しゅうまつ|こうえん|そうじ|ほうしかつどう"},{"japanese":"本日はお客様への奉仕品を多数ご用意しております。","romaji":"Honjitsu wa okyakusama e no houshihin o tasuu goyoui shite orimasu.","german":"Today we have prepared a large number of special-offer items for our customers.","furigana":"ほんじつ|きゃくさま|ほうしひん|たすう|ようい"}],"notes":"奉仕 is selfless \"service\" for others or the community; as an intransitive する-verb with に: 社会に奉仕する, plus 奉仕活動 \"community service\". In everyday speech today people mostly say ボランティア for voluntary work; 奉仕 sounds older, more solemn or religious. In retail, 奉仕 also means \"special offer\", for example in 奉仕品 \"bargain goods\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"寄付をしました。","romaji":"Kifu o shimashita.","german":"I made a donation.","furigana":"きふ"},{"japanese":"地震の被害者のために寄付を集めています。","romaji":"Jishin no higaisha no tame ni kifu o atsumete imasu.","german":"We are collecting donations for the earthquake victims.","furigana":"じしん|ひがいしゃ|きふ|あつ"},{"japanese":"読まなくなった本を図書館に寄付しました。","romaji":"Yomanaku natta hon o toshokan ni kifu shimashita.","german":"I donated books I no longer read to the library.","furigana":"よ|ほん|としょかん|きふ"}],"notes":"寄付 is a \"donation\" of money or goods to an organization or for a good cause: 寄付をする, 寄付を集める \"to collect donations\", 寄付金. As a transitive する-verb: 学校に本を寄付する \"to donate books to the school\". In laws and at government offices, the spelling 寄附 is also found. 募金 refers specifically to collecting donation money, often on the street, while 貢献 is a general \"contribution\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"保育園に通っています。","romaji":"Hoikuen ni kayotte imasu.","german":"He/She goes to daycare.","furigana":"ほいくえん|かよ"},{"japanese":"姉は保育士として働いています。","romaji":"Ane wa hoikushi to shite hataraite imasu.","german":"My older sister works as a nursery teacher.","furigana":"あね|ほいくし|はたら"},{"japanese":"この町は保育の場所が足りなくて、困っている親が多いです。","romaji":"Kono machi wa hoiku no basho ga tarinakute, komatte iru oya ga ooi desu.","german":"This town lacks childcare places, and many parents are in trouble.","furigana":"まち|ほいく|ばしょ|た|こま|おや|おお"}],"notes":"保育 is the care and upbringing of small children as it takes place in daycare: 保育園 / 保育所 \"daycare center\", 保育士 \"childcare worker\", 保育料 \"daycare fee\". 保育園 (care for children of working parents, from infancy) differs from 幼稚園 \"kindergarten (educational institution from age three)\". As a する-verb, 保育する is rather technical; in everyday speech people say 子どもの面倒を見る or 子どもを預ける.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"過疎化が進んでいます。","romaji":"Kasoka ga susunde imasu.","german":"Depopulation is progressing.","furigana":"かそか|すす"},{"japanese":"祖母は過疎の村に一人で住んでいます。","romaji":"Sobo wa kaso no mura ni hitori de sunde imasu.","german":"My grandmother lives alone in a depopulated village.","furigana":"そぼ|かそ|むら|ひとり|す"},{"japanese":"過疎地域ではバスの本数が少なくて不便です。","romaji":"Kaso chiiki de wa basu no honsuu ga sukunakute fuben desu.","german":"In depopulated areas there are few buses, which is inconvenient.","furigana":"かそちいき|ほんすう|すく|ふべん"}],"notes":"過疎 means \"too sparse a population, depopulation\", above all of rural areas from which young people move to the cities. Typical are 過疎化 \"depopulation (as a process)\", 過疎化が進む, 過疎地 / 過疎地域 \"depopulated region\" and 過疎の村. The opposite is 過密 \"overcrowding, concentration\". The word belongs to the news and social studies; in conversation people also simply say 人が減っている.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"都市化が進んでいます。","romaji":"Toshika ga susunde imasu.","german":"Urbanization is progressing.","furigana":"としか|すす"},{"japanese":"都市化によって、この辺りの田んぼはほとんどなくなりました。","romaji":"Toshika ni yotte, kono atari no tanbo wa hotondo naku narimashita.","german":"Because of urbanization, the rice fields around here have almost all disappeared.","furigana":"としか|あた|た"},{"japanese":"都市化した地域では、夏の気温が上がりやすいです。","romaji":"Toshika shita chiiki de wa, natsu no kion ga agariyasui desu.","german":"In urbanized areas, summer temperatures rise more easily.","furigana":"としか|ちいき|なつ|きおん|あ"}],"notes":"都市化 is \"urbanization\", formed from 都市 \"city\" and the suffix 化 \"-ization, becoming something\". Typical are 都市化が進む, 急速な都市化 and the intransitive 〜が都市化する. It is the counterpart to 過疎化 \"depopulation\" in the countryside. The tone is objective, typical of geography and the news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"貧困をなくしたいです。","romaji":"Hinkon o nakushitai desu.","german":"I want to eliminate poverty.","furigana":"ひんこん"},{"japanese":"世界には貧困に苦しむ人がまだたくさんいます。","romaji":"Sekai ni wa hinkon ni kurushimu hito ga mada takusan imasu.","german":"There are still many people in the world suffering from poverty.","furigana":"せかい|ひんこん|くる|ひと"},{"japanese":"子どもの貧困は日本でも大きな問題です。","romaji":"Kodomo no hinkon wa Nihon demo ookina mondai desu.","german":"Child poverty is a big problem in Japan, too.","furigana":"こ|ひんこん|にほん|おお|もんだい"}],"notes":"貧困 is \"poverty\" as a social problem: 貧困問題, 貧困をなくす, 子どもの貧困, 貧困層 \"poor segment of the population\". An individual person is described in everyday speech more often with 貧乏 \"poor, broke\" (貧乏な学生); 貧困 is matter-of-fact and appears in the news, politics and statistics. Figuratively it can also mean \"lack\", for example 発想の貧困 \"poverty of ideas\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"飢餓で苦しんでいます。","romaji":"Kiga de kurushinde imasu.","german":"They are suffering from hunger.","furigana":"きが|くる"},{"japanese":"世界では今も多くの子どもが飢餓に直面しています。","romaji":"Sekai de wa ima mo ooku no kodomo ga kiga ni chokumen shite imasu.","german":"Many children around the world still face hunger today.","furigana":"せかい|いま|おお|こ|きが|ちょくめん"},{"japanese":"この団体は飢餓をなくすための活動をしています。","romaji":"Kono dantai wa kiga o nakusu tame no katsudou o shite imasu.","german":"This organization works to end hunger.","furigana":"だんたい|きが|かつどう"}],"notes":"飢餓 means \"hunger, famine\" in the serious sense - prolonged lack of food, often as a global problem: 飢餓に苦しむ, 飢餓をなくす, 飢餓状態. For ordinary hunger in daily life, people say お腹がすいた; 飢餓 would be far too strong for that. Related is 飢饉 \"famine (due to crop failure)\". 飢餓 is a written word from the news and the language of aid organizations.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"被災地を支援します。","romaji":"Hisaichi o shien shimasu.","german":"We will support the disaster area.","furigana":"ひさいち|しえん"},{"japanese":"留学生の生活を支援するプログラムがあります。","romaji":"Ryuugakusei no seikatsu o shien suru puroguramu ga arimasu.","german":"There is a program that supports international students in their daily lives.","furigana":"りゅうがくせい|せいかつ|しえん"},{"japanese":"皆様のご支援に心から感謝いたします。","romaji":"Minasama no goshien ni kokoro kara kansha itashimasu.","german":"We thank you all from the bottom of our hearts for your support.","furigana":"みなさま|しえん|こころ|かんしゃ"}],"notes":"支援 is organized, often material or financial support from the state, companies or groups: 〜を支援する, 支援を受ける, 支援金, 被災地支援. In thank-you formulas it takes ご: ご支援に感謝いたします. For cheering on or morally supporting a team, one says 応援; for personally helping with a task, 手伝う; 援助 stresses mainly financial and material aid. Rather formal, typical of the news and government offices.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"復興が進んでいます。","romaji":"Fukkō ga susunde imasu.","german":"Reconstruction is progressing.","furigana":"ふっこう|すす"},{"japanese":"地震から十年たって、町はほぼ復興した。","romaji":"Jishin kara juunen tatte, machi wa hobo fukkou shita.","german":"Ten years after the earthquake, the town has almost completely recovered.","furigana":"じしん|じゅうねん|まち|ふっこう"},{"japanese":"地域の復興のために、多くのボランティアが集まりました。","romaji":"Chiiki no fukkou no tame ni, ooku no borantia ga atsumarimashita.","german":"Many volunteers gathered for the reconstruction of the region.","furigana":"ちいき|ふっこう|おお|あつ"}],"notes":"復興 is the rebuilding of a whole area, a town, the economy or culture after a war or disaster: 復興が進む, 町が復興する, 震災からの復興, 復興支援. Mostly intransitive (町が復興する), more rarely transitive (町を復興する). For repairing individual things one says 修理, for the restoration of a building 修復, for rebuilding a house or company 再建; 回復 is recovery of health or the economy.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この動画は再生回数が百万回を超えました。","romaji":"Kono douga wa saisei kaisuu ga hyakuman kai o koemashita.","german":"This video has exceeded one million views.","furigana":"どうが|さいせいかいすう|ひゃくまんかい|こ"},{"japanese":"ボタンを押すと、音楽が再生されます。","romaji":"Botan o osu to, ongaku ga saisei saremasu.","german":"When you press the button, the music plays.","furigana":"お|おんがく|さいせい"},{"japanese":"このノートは再生紙で作られています。","romaji":"Kono nooto wa saiseishi de tsukurarete imasu.","german":"This notebook is made of recycled paper.","furigana":"さいせいし|つく"}],"notes":"In everyday speech today, 再生 mostly means playing video or sound: 動画を再生する, 再生ボタン, 再生回数 (\"view count\"), opposite 停止. It also stands for reuse (再生紙 \"recycled paper\", 再生プラスチック; in speech more often リサイクル) and for biological regeneration (細胞が再生する). It can be used transitively (〜を再生する) and intransitively or passively (音楽が再生される).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"血液が循環しています。","romaji":"Ketsueki ga junkan shite imasu.","german":"The blood is circulating.","furigana":"けつえき|じゅんかん"},{"japanese":"窓を開けて、部屋の空気を循環させましょう。","romaji":"Mado o akete, heya no kuuki o junkan sasemashou.","german":"Let's open the window and let the air in the room circulate.","furigana":"まど|あ|へや|くうき|じゅんかん"},{"japanese":"寝不足とストレスの悪循環から抜け出したい。","romaji":"Nebusoku to sutoresu no akujunkan kara nukedashitai.","german":"I want to escape from the vicious circle of lack of sleep and stress.","furigana":"ねぶそく|あくじゅんかん|ぬ|だ"}],"notes":"循環 is a cycle in which something keeps returning to its starting point: 血液が循環する, 空気・水・お金の循環. As a する-verb it is intransitive; to make something circulate, the causative 循環させる is used. Common compounds are 悪循環 \"vicious circle\", 好循環 \"virtuous circle\" and 循環バス \"loop bus\". For simple turning or flowing in daily life, one says rather 回る or 流れる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"賞味期限が切れた食品は廃棄します。","romaji":"Shoumi kigen ga kireta shokuhin wa haiki shimasu.","german":"Food past its best-before date is discarded.","furigana":"しょうみきげん|き|しょくひん|はいき"},{"japanese":"古いパソコンを廃棄する前に、データを消してください。","romaji":"Furui pasokon o haiki suru mae ni, deeta o keshite kudasai.","german":"Please delete the data before disposing of the old computer.","furigana":"ふる|はいき|まえ|け"},{"japanese":"工場の廃棄物が川を汚した。","romaji":"Koujou no haikibutsu ga kawa o yogoshita.","german":"Waste from the factory polluted the river.","furigana":"こうじょう|はいきぶつ|かわ|よご"}],"notes":"廃棄 is the official, often systematic disposal of things no longer needed: food, documents, devices, chemicals. Typical are 〜を廃棄する, 廃棄される, 廃棄物 \"waste (industrial)\" and 食品廃棄. In everyday life one simply throws garbage away (ゴミを捨てる); 廃棄 sounds official, or like a company and regulations. Rather formal and literary.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"水を節約しましょう。","romaji":"Mizu o setsuyaku shimashō.","german":"Let's save water.","furigana":"みず|せつやく"},{"japanese":"節約のために、毎日お弁当を作っています。","romaji":"Setsuyaku no tame ni, mainichi obentou o tsukutte imasu.","german":"To save money, I make my own lunch every day.","furigana":"せつやく|まいにち|べんとう|つく"},{"japanese":"地下鉄を使えば、時間が節約できますよ。","romaji":"Chikatetsu o tsukaeba, jikan ga setsuyaku dekimasu yo.","german":"If you take the subway, you can save time.","furigana":"ちかてつ|つか|じかん|せつやく"}],"notes":"節約 means to be sparing with something and use less: お金・電気・水・時間を節約する, 節約のために, 節約家 \"thrifty person\". Not to be confused with 貯金: 節約 means spending less, 貯金 (お金を貯める) putting money aside. 倹約 is similar, but stresses a frugal lifestyle and sounds more elevated. Opposite: 浪費 or, colloquially, 無駄遣い.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"時間を浪費しました。","romaji":"Jikan o rōhi shimashita.","german":"I wasted time.","furigana":"じかん|ろうひ"},{"japanese":"彼女はブランド品に給料を浪費している。","romaji":"Kanojo wa burandohin ni kyuuryou o rouhi shite iru.","german":"She squanders her salary on brand-name goods.","furigana":"かのじょ|ひん|きゅうりょう|ろうひ"},{"japanese":"電気の浪費を防ぐため、使わない部屋の電気は消しましょう。","romaji":"Denki no rouhi o fusegu tame, tsukawanai heya no denki wa keshimashou.","german":"To prevent wasting electricity, let's turn off the lights in unused rooms.","furigana":"でんき|ろうひ|ふせ|つか|へや|でんき|け"}],"notes":"浪費 is the pointless wasting of money, time or energy: 時間を浪費する, 〜に給料を浪費する (\"to squander one's pay on something\"), 浪費家 \"spendthrift\". It sounds somewhat more elevated and critical than the everyday 無駄遣い (お金を無駄遣いする), which is more common in conversation. The opposite is 節約. For food that gets thrown away, people more often say 無駄にする or 食品ロス.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"時間に余裕があります。","romaji":"Jikan ni yoyū ga arimasu.","german":"I have time to spare.","furigana":"じかん|よゆう"},{"japanese":"今月はお金に余裕がないので、旅行は来月にします。","romaji":"Kongetsu wa okane ni yoyuu ga nai node, ryokou wa raigetsu ni shimasu.","german":"I'm short on money this month, so I'll take the trip next month.","furigana":"こんげつ|かね|よゆう|りょこう|らいげつ"},{"japanese":"試験の前日なのに、彼は余裕のある顔をしている。","romaji":"Shiken no zenjitsu na no ni, kare wa yoyuu no aru kao o shite iru.","german":"Even though the exam is tomorrow, he looks completely relaxed.","furigana":"しけん|ぜんじつ|かれ|よゆう|かお"}],"notes":"余裕 is, first, the leeway that is left over: 時間・お金・スペースに余裕がある/ない, 余裕を持って \"with a buffer\" (余裕を持って家を出る). Second, it refers to inner composure: 余裕のある人, 余裕の表情. Colloquially, 余裕! or 余裕で合格した means \"easy, a piece of cake\". ゆとり is similar, but refers more to relaxed living conditions (ゆとりのある生活).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"限界に達しました。","romaji":"Genkai ni tasshimashita.","german":"I've reached my limit.","furigana":"げんかい|たっ"},{"japanese":"もう限界！少し休ませて。","romaji":"Mou genkai! Sukoshi yasumasete.","german":"I can't take any more! Let me rest a moment.","furigana":"げんかい|すこ|やす"},{"japanese":"一人でできることには限界があります。","romaji":"Hitori de dekiru koto ni wa genkai ga arimasu.","german":"There are limits to what one can do alone.","furigana":"ひとり|げんかい"}],"notes":"限界 is the utmost limit of strength, ability or capacity: 限界に達する, 限界を超える, 〜には限界がある, 体力の限界. Colloquially, もう限界! means \"I can't take it anymore!\". Not to be confused with 制限 (an imposed restriction, e.g. 速度制限), 限度 (permissible maximum, 限度額) and 境界 or 国境 for spatial borders between regions or countries.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"明日は雪が降る可能性があります。","romaji":"Ashita wa yuki ga furu kanousei ga arimasu.","german":"Morgen könnte es schneien.","furigana":"あした|ゆき|ふ|かのうせい"},{"japanese":"彼が犯人である可能性は低いと思います。","romaji":"Kare ga hannin de aru kanousei wa hikui to omoimasu.","german":"Ich halte es für unwahrscheinlich, dass er der Täter ist.","furigana":"かれ|はんにん|かのうせい|ひく|おも"},{"japanese":"若い人には無限の可能性がある。","romaji":"Wakai hito ni wa mugen no kanousei ga aru.","german":"Junge Menschen haben unbegrenztes Potenzial.","furigana":"わか|ひと|むげん|かのうせい"}],"notes":"可能性 drückt aus, wie wahrscheinlich etwas eintritt oder zutrifft: 〜可能性がある/高い/低い, direkt an die Grundform angehängt (雨が降る可能性). Außerdem bedeutet es „Potenzial“: 無限の可能性, 可能性を広げる. Achtung: Die deutsche „Möglichkeit“ im Sinn von Gelegenheit („Ich habe die Möglichkeit, in Japan zu studieren“) heißt 機会 oder チャンス, nicht 可能性. 可能 allein ist ein な-Adjektiv („möglich“).","levelNote":"Compound: 可能 (N3) + 性 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"危険性が高いです。","romaji":"Kikensei ga takai desu.","german":"The risk is high.","furigana":"きけんせい|たか"},{"japanese":"この薬には副作用の危険性があります。","romaji":"Kono kusuri ni wa fukusayou no kikensei ga arimasu.","german":"This medicine carries a risk of side effects.","furigana":"くすり|ふくさよう|きけんせい"},{"japanese":"専門家は大きな地震が起きる危険性を指摘している。","romaji":"Senmonka wa ookina jishin ga okiru kikensei o shiteki shite iru.","german":"Experts point out the risk of a major earthquake.","furigana":"せんもんか|おお|じしん|お|きけんせい|してき"}],"notes":"The suffix 〜性 forms abstract nouns; 危険性 is the risk, or degree of danger, that something bad will happen: 〜する危険性がある/高い, 〜の危険性を指摘する. 危険 alone is the danger itself and also a な-adjective (危険な場所). In conversation people more often say 危ない or リスク; 危険性 sounds objective and is typical of the news, reports and package inserts.","levelNote":"Compound: 危険 (N4) + 性 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"必要性を感じます。","romaji":"Hitsuyōsei o kanjimasu.","german":"I feel the necessity.","furigana":"ひつようせい|かん"},{"japanese":"会議で、新しいシステムの必要性について話し合いました。","romaji":"Kaigi de, atarashii shisutemu no hitsuyousei ni tsuite hanashiaimashita.","german":"In the meeting, we discussed the need for a new system.","furigana":"かいぎ|あたら|ひつようせい|はな|あ"},{"japanese":"今の段階では、手術の必要性はないそうです。","romaji":"Ima no dankai de wa, shujutsu no hitsuyousei wa nai sou desu.","german":"At this stage, apparently no surgery is necessary.","furigana":"いま|だんかい|しゅじゅつ|ひつようせい"}],"notes":"必要性 is necessity as an abstract topic that one talks about or judges: 〜の必要性を感じる/認める/訴える, 必要性について話し合う. In simple sentences, 必要 or 〜必要がある is enough: 行く必要がある is more natural than 行く必要性がある. Formal, typical of discussions, reports and justifications.","levelNote":"Compound: 必要 (N4) + 性 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"教育の重要性を訴えます。","romaji":"Kyōiku no jūyōsei o uttaemasu.","german":"Wir betonen die Wichtigkeit der Bildung.","furigana":"きょういく|じゅうようせい|うった"},{"japanese":"病気になって初めて、健康の重要性に気づきました。","romaji":"Byouki ni natte hajimete, kenkou no juuyousei ni kizukimashita.","german":"Erst als ich krank wurde, habe ich erkannt, wie wichtig Gesundheit ist.","furigana":"びょうき|はじ|けんこう|じゅうようせい|き"},{"japanese":"先生は毎日復習することの重要性を説明した。","romaji":"Sensei wa mainichi fukushuu suru koto no juuyousei o setsumei shita.","german":"Der Lehrer erklärte, wie wichtig es ist, jeden Tag zu wiederholen.","furigana":"せんせい|まいにちふくしゅう|じゅうようせい|せつめい"}],"notes":"重要性 ist die Wichtigkeit als abstraktes Nomen: 〜の重要性に気づく/を理解する/を訴える/を強調する. Im Gespräch klingt 大切さ (健康の大切さ) natürlicher; 重要性 ist typisch für Reden, Aufsätze und Nachrichten. 重要 allein ist ein な-Adjektiv. Achtung: Die deutsche „Bedeutung“ im Sinn von Wortbedeutung ist 意味, nicht 重要性.","levelNote":"Compound: 重要 (N3) + 性 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"安全性を確保します。","romaji":"Anzensei o kakuho shimasu.","german":"Wir gewährleisten die Sicherheit.","furigana":"あんぜんせい|かくほ"},{"japanese":"この車は安全性が高いことで知られています。","romaji":"Kono kuruma wa anzensei ga takai koto de shirarete imasu.","german":"Dieses Auto ist für seine hohe Sicherheit bekannt.","furigana":"くるま|あんぜんせい|たか|し"},{"japanese":"新しい薬の安全性を確かめるには、長い時間がかかる。","romaji":"Atarashii kusuri no anzensei o tashikameru ni wa, nagai jikan ga kakaru.","german":"Es dauert lange, die Sicherheit eines neuen Medikaments zu überprüfen.","furigana":"あたら|くすり|あんぜんせい|たし|なが|じかん"}],"notes":"安全性 ist der Grad an Sicherheit eines Produkts, Systems oder Verfahrens: 安全性が高い/低い, 〜の安全性を確認する/確保する/高める. Typisch bei Autos, Lebensmitteln, Medikamenten und Gebäuden. 安全 selbst ist der Zustand (安全な場所, 安全運転). Für Sicherheit im Sinne von Schutz vor Kriminalität oder Hackern sagt man eher 防犯 oder セキュリティ – das deutsche „Sicherheit“ deckt beides ab.","levelNote":"Compound: 安全 (N4) + 性 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"経済的に独立しています。","romaji":"Keizaiteki ni dokuritsu shite imasu.","german":"I'm financially independent.","furigana":"けいざいてき|どくりつ"},{"japanese":"十年働いた会社を辞めて、独立してカフェを開いた。","romaji":"Juunen hataraita kaisha o yamete, dokuritsu shite kafe o hiraita.","german":"After ten years, he quit the company he worked for, went independent and opened a cafe.","furigana":"じゅうねんはたら|かいしゃ|や|どくりつ|ひら"},{"japanese":"アメリカは1776年にイギリスから独立した。","romaji":"Amerika wa sen nanahyaku nanajuuroku nen ni Igirisu kara dokuritsu shita.","german":"America became independent from Britain in 1776.","furigana":"ねん|どくりつ"}],"notes":"独立 means independence and is used as an intransitive する-verb with から: 親から独立する, イギリスから独立する. It covers countries (独立国, 独立記念日), people (経済的に独立する) and work: 会社を辞めて独立する \"to quit a company and go independent\". 独立した部屋 is a separate room. 自立 is similar, but stresses getting by without outside help.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"束縛から解放されたいです。","romaji":"Sokubaku kara kaihō saretai desu.","german":"I want to be freed from constraints.","furigana":"そくばく|かいほう"},{"japanese":"彼氏の束縛が強くて、友達とも自由に会えない。","romaji":"Kareshi no sokubaku ga tsuyokute, tomodachi to mo jiyuu ni aenai.","german":"My boyfriend is so controlling that I can't even meet my friends freely.","furigana":"かれし|そくばく|つよ|ともだち|じゆう|あ"},{"japanese":"会社の規則に束縛されるのが嫌で、フリーランスになった。","romaji":"Kaisha no kisoku ni sokubaku sareru no ga iya de, furiiransu ni natta.","german":"I became a freelancer because I hated being bound by company rules.","furigana":"かいしゃ|きそく|そくばく|いや"}],"notes":"束縛 is a fetter in the figurative sense: constraints that take away someone's freedom. Transitive 人を束縛する, very often passive 〜に束縛される, and 束縛から解放される \"to be freed from constraints\". Colloquially, 束縛が強い/束縛する彼氏 describes a controlling, jealous partner. 制限, by contrast, is a matter-of-fact limitation, and 拘束 is rather detention in the legal sense.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ストレスから解放されました。","romaji":"Sutoresu kara kaihō saremashita.","german":"I was freed from stress.","furigana":"かいほう"},{"japanese":"三日後に、人質は全員解放された。","romaji":"Mikka go ni, hitojichi wa zen'in kaihou sareta.","german":"Three days later, all the hostages were released.","furigana":"みっかご|ひとじち|ぜんいんかいほう"},{"japanese":"週末くらいは仕事のことを忘れて、自分を解放したい。","romaji":"Shuumatsu kurai wa shigoto no koto o wasurete, jibun o kaihou shitai.","german":"At least on weekends, I want to forget about work and set myself free.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|しごと|わす|じぶん|かいほう"}],"notes":"解放 is freeing from compulsion, oppression or burden: 人質を解放する, 〜から解放される. The passive very often expresses relief: 仕事・試験・ストレスから解放される. Beware of the homophone: 開放 (also かいほう) means \"to open up, to make accessible\" (窓を開放する, 校庭を開放する). The opposite of 解放 is 束縛 or 拘束.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"時間の制約があります。","romaji":"Jikan no seiyaku ga arimasu.","german":"There are time constraints.","furigana":"じかん|せいやく"},{"japanese":"予算の制約で、計画を小さくしなければならなかった。","romaji":"Yosan no seiyaku de, keikaku o chiisaku shinakereba naranakatta.","german":"Because of budget constraints, we had to scale down the plan.","furigana":"よさん|せいやく|けいかく|ちい"},{"japanese":"字数の制約があるので、言いたいことを短くまとめてください。","romaji":"Jisuu no seiyaku ga aru node, iitai koto o mijikaku matomete kudasai.","german":"There is a character limit, so please summarize what you want to say briefly.","furigana":"じすう|せいやく|い|みじか"}],"notes":"制約 are circumstances or conditions that restrict one's room to act: 時間・予算・スペースの制約, 制約がある, 制約を受ける. 制限, by contrast, is an explicitly set limit (年齢制限, 速度制限); 制約 sounds more abstract and describes the situation rather than a rule. Do not confuse it with the homophones 誓約 \"pledge\" and 製薬 \"pharmaceuticals\". Rather formal.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"障害を乗り越えます。","romaji":"Shōgai o norikoemasu.","german":"We will overcome the obstacle.","furigana":"しょうがい|の|こ"},{"japanese":"障害のある人も使いやすい駅が増えています。","romaji":"Shougai no aru hito mo tsukaiyasui eki ga fuete imasu.","german":"There are more and more stations that are easy to use for people with disabilities, too.","furigana":"しょうがい|ひと|つか|えき|ふ"},{"japanese":"システム障害のため、電車が止まっています。","romaji":"Shisutemu shougai no tame, densha ga tomatte imasu.","german":"Because of a system failure, the trains have stopped.","furigana":"しょうがい|でんしゃ|と"}],"notes":"障害 has three main areas: an \"obstacle\" (障害を乗り越える), a technical \"disruption\" (システム障害, 通信障害) and a \"disability\" or \"disorder\" in the medical sense (障害者, 睡眠障害). A person with a disability is 障害のある人; because 害 means \"harm\", government offices and media often write 障がい. A physical obstacle in the way is usually called 障害物. In everyday speech, for minor things that get in the way, 邪魔 is more common.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 49"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"健康を維持します。","romaji":"Kenkō o iji shimasu.","german":"I maintain my health.","furigana":"けんこう|いじ"},{"japanese":"この古い家は維持費が高い。","romaji":"Kono furui ie wa ijihi ga takai.","german":"The upkeep costs of this old house are high.","furigana":"ふる|いえ|いじひ|たか"},{"japanese":"一位を維持するのは、一位になるより難しい。","romaji":"Ichii o iji suru no wa, ichii ni naru yori muzukashii.","german":"Keeping first place is harder than becoming first.","furigana":"いちい|いじ|いちい|むずか"}],"notes":"維持 means to keep a state as it is: 健康・体重・平和・関係を維持する, 現状維持 \"status quo\". 維持費 are the ongoing upkeep costs. The native verb 保つ is similar, but sounds less official. 保存 is the keeping of things or data, 整備 the practical maintenance of machines.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"計画を実施します。","romaji":"Keikaku o jisshi shimasu.","german":"We will carry out the plan.","furigana":"けいかく|じっし"},{"japanese":"来月、全社員を対象にアンケートを実施します。","romaji":"Raigetsu, zenshain o taishou ni ankeeto o jisshi shimasu.","german":"Next month, we will conduct a survey of all employees.","furigana":"らいげつ|ぜんしゃいん|たいしょう|じっし"},{"japanese":"雨の場合、運動会は翌日に実施されます。","romaji":"Ame no baai, undoukai wa yokujitsu ni jisshi saremasu.","german":"In case of rain, the sports festival will be held the next day.","furigana":"あめ|ばあい|うんどうかい|よくじつ|じっし"}],"notes":"実施 is the official carrying out of plans, surveys, exams, campaigns or rules: 〜を実施する, very often passive 〜が実施される and on notices 実施中. In conversation people mostly say やる or 行う. 実行 stresses the actual action of a person (計画を実行する), 実現 the coming true of a goal or dream.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"夢を実現しました。","romaji":"Yume o jitsugen shimashita.","german":"I realized my dream.","furigana":"ゆめ|じつげん"},{"japanese":"長年の計画がついに実現した。","romaji":"Naganen no keikaku ga tsui ni jitsugen shita.","german":"The long-held plan finally came true.","furigana":"ながねん|けいかく|じつげん"},{"japanese":"その案はいいけど、実現は難しいと思う。","romaji":"Sono an wa ii kedo, jitsugen wa muzukashii to omou.","german":"The idea is good, but I think it will be hard to realize.","furigana":"あん|じつげん|むずか|おも"}],"notes":"実現 means that a dream, plan or wish becomes reality. It can be transitive (夢を実現する) and intransitive (夢が実現する); also common are 実現可能 \"feasible\" and 実現は難しい. More emotional and more frequent in daily life is 夢が叶う/夢を叶える. 実施, by contrast, is the formal carrying out of a program.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"目標に達しました。","romaji":"Mokuhyō ni tasshimashita.","german":"We reached the goal.","furigana":"もくひょう|たっ"},{"japanese":"気温が40度に達した。","romaji":"Kion ga yonjuu do ni tasshita.","german":"The temperature reached 40 degrees.","furigana":"きおん|ど|たっ"},{"japanese":"話し合いは三時間続いたが、結論に達しなかった。","romaji":"Hanashiai wa sanjikan tsuzuita ga, ketsuron ni tasshinakatta.","german":"The discussion went on for three hours but did not reach a conclusion.","furigana":"はな|あ|さんじかんつづ|けつろん|たっ"}],"notes":"達する means \"to reach\" and takes に: 目標に達する, 40度に達する, 結論に達する. It is used for numbers, amounts, levels, places and stages and sounds written or formal; in conversation people say 届く, 着く or 〜になる instead. It is an irregular する-verb: 達して, 達した, negative 達しない. Close in meaning is the likewise elevated 至る.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"見積もりをお願いします。","romaji":"Mitsumori o onegai shimasu.","german":"Please give me an estimate.","furigana":"みつ|ねが"},{"japanese":"三つの会社から見積もりを取って比べました。","romaji":"Mittsu no kaisha kara mitsumori o totte kurabemashita.","german":"I got quotes from three companies and compared them.","furigana":"みっ|かいしゃ|みつ|と|くら"},{"japanese":"引っ越しの費用は、見積もりより二万円高かった。","romaji":"Hikkoshi no hiyou wa, mitsumori yori niman en takakatta.","german":"The moving cost was 20,000 yen more than the estimate.","furigana":"ひ|こ|ひよう|みつ|にまんえんたか"}],"notes":"見積もり is the noun of 見積もる \"to estimate, to appraise\" and mainly refers to a cost estimate: 見積もりを取る (to obtain), 見積もりを出す (to prepare), 見積もりを依頼する; the written quotation is called 見積書. It also means \"estimate\" in general: 見積もりが甘い \"to have estimated too optimistically\". The spellings 見積もり, 見積り and 見積 all occur.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"損得を考えます。","romaji":"Sontoku o kangaemasu.","german":"I weigh the pros and cons.","furigana":"そんとく|かんが"},{"japanese":"損得抜きで友達の引っ越しを手伝った。","romaji":"Sontoku nuki de tomodachi no hikkoshi o tetsudatta.","german":"I helped my friend move without thinking about my own advantage.","furigana":"そんとくぬ|ともだち|ひ|こ|てつだ"},{"japanese":"損得だけで仕事を選ぶと、後で後悔するかもしれない。","romaji":"Sontoku dake de shigoto o erabu to, ato de koukai suru kamo shirenai.","german":"If you choose a job based only on advantage, you may regret it later.","furigana":"そんとく|しごと|えら|あと|こうかい"}],"notes":"損得 consists of 損 \"loss\" and 得 \"gain\" and means personal advantage or disadvantage: 損得を考える, 損得勘定 \"cost-benefit calculation\". 損得抜きで means \"without thinking of one's own advantage, selflessly\". 利害, by contrast, is the constellation of interests of different parties, and 損益 is accounting profit and loss.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私はその会社と利害関係があるので、審査には参加できません。","romaji":"Watashi wa sono kaisha to rigai kankei ga aru node, shinsa ni wa sanka dekimasen.","german":"I have a conflict of interest with that company, so I cannot take part in the review.","furigana":"わたし|かいしゃ|りがいかんけい|しんさ|さんか"},{"japanese":"両国の利害が一致して、協定が結ばれた。","romaji":"Ryoukoku no rigai ga icchi shite, kyoutei ga musubareta.","german":"The interests of the two countries coincided, and an agreement was concluded.","furigana":"りょうこく|りがい|いっち|きょうてい|むす"},{"japanese":"利害が対立する話し合いは、なかなかまとまらない。","romaji":"Rigai ga tairitsu suru hanashiai wa, nakanaka matomaranai.","german":"Negotiations in which interests conflict are hard to settle.","furigana":"りがい|たいりつ|はな|あ"}],"notes":"利害 are the interests, that is, the benefit and harm, of the parties involved: 利害が一致する \"interests coincide\", 利害が対立する \"interests clash\", 利害関係 \"interest relationship, conflict of interest\", 利害関係者 \"stakeholder\". Typical of business, politics and law and rather formal. 損得, by contrast, is the personal calculation of whether one gains or loses.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"技術の恩恵を受けています。","romaji":"Gijutsu no onkei o ukete imasu.","german":"Wir profitieren von der Technologie.","furigana":"ぎじゅつ|おんけい|う"},{"japanese":"この町は観光の恩恵を受けて発展した。","romaji":"Kono machi wa kankou no onkei o ukete hatten shita.","german":"Diese Stadt hat sich dank des Tourismus entwickelt.","furigana":"まち|かんこう|おんけい|う|はってん"},{"japanese":"雨は農家にとって大切な自然の恩恵だ。","romaji":"Ame wa nouka ni totte taisetsu na shizen no onkei da.","german":"Für Bauern ist Regen ein wertvolles Geschenk der Natur.","furigana":"あめ|のうか|たいせつ|しぜん|おんけい"}],"notes":"恩恵 ist ein Nutzen oder Segen, den man von etwas Größerem erhält – Natur, Technik, Gesellschaft, Politik: 〜の恩恵を受ける, 〜の恩恵を被る, 自然の恩恵. Auf Deutsch passt oft „von etwas profitieren“. Gehoben und eher schriftlich. 利益 ist dagegen Gewinn im geschäftlichen Sinn, メリット ein Vorteil, und 恩 die persönliche Dankesschuld gegenüber einem Menschen (恩を返す).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"弊害が出ています。","romaji":"Heigai ga dete imasu.","german":"Negative effects are appearing.","furigana":"へいがい|で"},{"japanese":"スマホの使いすぎによる弊害が問題になっている。","romaji":"Sumaho no tsukaisugi ni yoru heigai ga mondai ni natte iru.","german":"The harmful effects of overusing smartphones are becoming a problem.","furigana":"つか|へいがい|もんだい"},{"japanese":"規則が厳しすぎると、かえって弊害が生まれることもある。","romaji":"Kisoku ga kibishisugiru to, kaette heigai ga umareru koto mo aru.","german":"Rules that are too strict can even produce harmful side effects.","furigana":"きそく|きび|へいがい|う"}],"notes":"弊害 is a harmful side effect of a system, rule, technology or habit: 〜による弊害, 弊害が出る/生じる, 弊害をもたらす. It usually presupposes that the thing itself is useful but has negative consequences. Written and objective; in daily life people more often say 悪影響 or 害. 副作用 are side effects of medicines, but are also used figuratively.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"男女の給料の差を是正する必要がある。","romaji":"Danjo no kyuuryou no sa o zesei suru hitsuyou ga aru.","german":"The pay gap between men and women needs to be corrected.","furigana":"だんじょ|きゅうりょう|さ|ぜせい|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"検査で指摘された問題点は、すぐに是正されました。","romaji":"Kensa de shiteki sareta mondaiten wa, sugu ni zesei saremashita.","german":"The problems pointed out in the inspection were corrected immediately.","furigana":"けんさ|してき|もんだいてん|ぜせい"},{"japanese":"長時間労働を是正するため、会社は残業を禁止した。","romaji":"Choujikan roudou o zesei suru tame, kaisha wa zangyou o kinshi shita.","german":"To curb excessive working hours, the company banned overtime.","furigana":"ちょうじかんろうどう|ぜせい|かいしゃ|ざんぎょう|きんし"}],"notes":"是正 means bringing something wrong or unjust into the right state: inequalities, abuses, violations (格差・不公平・長時間労働を是正する, 是正措置, 是正勧告). It is an official word from politics, administration and business. For correcting errors in text or speech, one says 訂正; for revising a draft, 修正; for general improvement, 改善.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"是非お越しください。","romaji":"Zehi okoshi kudasai.","german":"Please do come by all means.","furigana":"ぜひ|こ"},{"japanese":"日本に来たら、ぜひ京都に行ってみてください。","romaji":"Nihon ni kitara, zehi Kyouto ni itte mite kudasai.","german":"When you come to Japan, be sure to visit Kyoto.","furigana":"にほん|き|きょうと|い"},{"japanese":"「今度一緒に飲みに行きませんか。」「ぜひ！」","romaji":"\"Kondo issho ni nomi ni ikimasen ka.\" \"Zehi!\"","german":"\"Would you like to go for a drink together sometime?\" \"I'd love to!\"","furigana":"こんどいっしょ|の|い"}],"notes":"是非 as an adverb means \"by all means, definitely\" and strengthens wishes, requests and invitations: ぜひ来てください, ぜひ行きたいです. It therefore appears with ～てください, ～たい or ～てほしい, not in mere suppositions (for those use きっと) or statements of fact (use 必ず). As a short reply, ぜひ！ means \"I'd love to!\". As a noun, 是非 means \"right and wrong, pros and cons\" (written language); the adverb is usually written in kana as ぜひ in daily life.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 9"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ご気分は如何ですか。","romaji":"Gokibun wa ikaga desu ka.","german":"How are you feeling?","furigana":"きぶん|いかが"},{"japanese":"お茶はいかがですか。","romaji":"Ocha wa ikaga desu ka.","german":"Would you like some tea?","furigana":"ちゃ"}],"notes":"いかが is the polite equivalent of どう: ご気分はいかがですか asks about someone's condition; お茶はいかがですか offers tea. The kana spelling is usual. In いかがなものか there is a tone of doubt or polite criticism; here it does not simply mean \"how\". The kanji can also be read differently, for example 如何に（いかに）.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"何卒よろしくお願いします。","romaji":"Nanitozo yoroshiku onegai shimasu.","german":"I humbly ask for your kind consideration.","furigana":"なにとぞ|ねが"},{"japanese":"工事中はご迷惑をおかけしますが、何卒ご理解のほどよろしくお願い申し上げます。","romaji":"Koujichuu wa gomeiwaku o okake shimasu ga, nanitozo gorikai no hodo yoroshiku onegai moushiagemasu.","german":"We apologize for the inconvenience during construction and humbly ask for your understanding.","furigana":"こうじちゅう|めいわく|なにとぞ|りかい|ねが|もう|あ"},{"japanese":"返事が遅くなりましたこと、何卒お許しください。","romaji":"Henji ga osoku narimashita koto, nanitozo oyurushi kudasai.","german":"Please forgive me for my late reply.","furigana":"へんじ|おそ|なにとぞ|ゆる"}],"notes":"何卒 is a very formal \"please\" that makes a request especially humble and earnest. It appears almost only in business letters, emails, notices and speeches, mostly before よろしくお願い申し上げます or ご〜ください (何卒ご理解ください). It is clearly more formal than どうか or どうぞ and seems out of place in casual conversation. Often also written in kana: なにとぞ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"恐れ入りますが、お名前を伺ってもよろしいでしょうか。","romaji":"Osoreirimasu ga, onamae o ukagatte mo yoroshii deshou ka.","german":"Excuse me, may I ask your name?","furigana":"おそ|い|なまえ|うかが"},{"japanese":"お忙しいところ、わざわざ来ていただいて恐れ入ります。","romaji":"Oisogashii tokoro, wazawaza kite itadaite osoreirimasu.","german":"Thank you so much for going out of your way to come despite your busy schedule.","furigana":"いそが|き|おそ|い"},{"japanese":"彼の知識の深さには恐れ入りました。","romaji":"Kare no chishiki no fukasa ni wa osoreirimashita.","german":"I was amazed at the depth of his knowledge.","furigana":"かれ|ちしき|ふか|おそ|い"}],"notes":"恐れ入る is a very polite verb that appears almost only as 恐れ入ります. As 恐れ入りますが it introduces a request in customer service and business, and is more formal than すみませんが; for one's own mistake, by contrast, one says 申し訳ありません. On its own, 恐れ入ります can also express thanks for someone's trouble (\"much obliged\"). With に, 〜には恐れ入った means \"to take one's hat off to ...\", sometimes also ironically \"I'm speechless\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ご意見を頂きたいです。","romaji":"Goiken o itadakitai desu.","german":"I would like to hear your opinion.","furigana":"いけん|いただ"},{"japanese":"先生から手紙をいただきました。","romaji":"Sensei kara tegami o itadakimashita.","german":"I received a letter from my teacher.","furigana":"せんせい|てがみ"},{"japanese":"では、温かいうちにいただきます。","romaji":"Dewa, atatakai uchi ni itadakimasu.","german":"Well then, I will eat while it is still warm.","furigana":"あたた"}],"notes":"頂く is the humble counterpart (謙譲語) of もらう: 先生に本を頂きました \"I received a book from my teacher\"; the giver takes に or から. At the same time it is the polite word for \"to eat, to drink\", hence いただきます before a meal. As an auxiliary verb, ～ていただく means that someone kindly does something for one: 教えていただけますか \"could you show me?\" It is mostly written in kana, いただく, especially as an auxiliary verb; 戴く is a rare variant.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 20"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"プレゼントを差し上げます。","romaji":"Purezento o sashiagemasu.","german":"I will give you a present.","furigana":"さ|あ"},{"japanese":"お客様に新しいカタログを差し上げました。","romaji":"Okyakusama ni atarashii katarogu o sashiagemashita.","german":"I gave the customer the new catalog.","furigana":"きゃくさま|あたら|さ|あ"},{"japanese":"先生に旅行のお土産を差し上げたいと思っています。","romaji":"Sensei ni ryokou no omiyage o sashiagetai to omotte imasu.","german":"I would like to give my teacher a souvenir from my trip.","furigana":"せんせい|りょこう|みやげ|さ|あ|おも"}],"notes":"差し上げる is the humble form (謙譲語) of あげる: the speaker or their side gives something to a person of higher status – 先生にお土産を差し上げる. The recipient takes に, the gift takes を. As an auxiliary verb, ～て差し上げる \"to do for someone\" sounds slightly condescending when said directly to the other person; better then is お持ちします instead of 持って差し上げます. The basic meaning \"to lift up\" is rare today. The counterpart for the direction toward the speaker is くださる.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 20"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ご注文を承ります。","romaji":"Gochūmon o uketamawarimasu.","german":"I will take your order.","furigana":"ちゅうもん|うけたまわ"},{"japanese":"ご伝言は確かに承りました。担当の者に伝えておきます。","romaji":"Godengon wa tashika ni uketamawarimashita. Tantou no mono ni tsutaete okimasu.","german":"I have received your message. I will pass it on to the person in charge.","furigana":"でんごん|たし|うけたまわ|たんとう|もの|つた"},{"japanese":"お名前は以前から承っております。","romaji":"Onamae wa izen kara uketamawatte orimasu.","german":"I have long heard your name.","furigana":"なまえ|いぜん|うけたまわ"}],"notes":"承る is a humble verb (謙譲語) used by staff when speaking to customers. It replaces 受ける/引き受ける (to receive an order, reservation or message: ご予約を承ります, かしこまりました、承りました) and 聞く (to hear, to learn: お名前は承っております). It is used only for one's own action, never for the customer's. Do not confuse it with 承知する \"to understand, to agree\" (承知しました).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ご案内いたします。","romaji":"Goannai itashimasu.","german":"I will guide you.","furigana":"あんない"},{"japanese":"来週の会議の資料は私がご用意いたします。","romaji":"Raishuu no kaigi no shiryou wa watashi ga goyoui itashimasu.","german":"I will prepare the materials for next week's meeting.","furigana":"らいしゅう|かいぎ|しりょう|わたし|ようい"},{"japanese":"初めまして、山田と申します。どうぞよろしくお願い致します。","romaji":"Hajimemashite, Yamada to moushimasu. Douzo yoroshiku onegai itashimasu.","german":"Pleased to meet you, my name is Yamada. I look forward to working with you.","furigana":"はじ|やまだ|もう|ねが|いた"}],"notes":"致す is the humble form (謙譲語) of する and describes only one's own actions or those of one's own side. The pattern お/ご＋noun＋いたします is especially common: ご案内いたします, お送りいたします. Fixed polite formulas are 失礼いたします and よろしくお願いいたします. It is mostly written in kana; the kanji spelling 致します is seen mainly in business letters and e-mails. For the actions of others, honorific なさる is used.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 20"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"通信が途絶えました。","romaji":"Tsūshin ga todaemashita.","german":"Communication has been cut off.","furigana":"つうしん|とだ"},{"japanese":"通信料金を節約するため、家ではWi-Fiを使います。","romaji":"Tsuushin ryoukin o setsuyaku suru tame, ie de wa Wi-Fi o tsukaimasu.","german":"To save on data charges, I use Wi-Fi at home.","furigana":"つうしんりょうきん|せつやく|いえ|つか"},{"japanese":"大学の通信教育で英語を勉強しています。","romaji":"Daigaku no tsuushin kyouiku de eigo o benkyou shite imasu.","german":"I'm studying English through a university correspondence course.","furigana":"だいがく|つうしんきょういく|えいご|べんきょう"}],"notes":"通信 is the transmission of information over lines, radio or a network, today mostly data traffic: 通信環境, 通信速度, 通信料金, 通信制限 \"throttling\"; 通信が切れる/途絶える. In older words it stands for \"by mail\": 通信教育 \"distance learning\", 通信販売 or 通販 \"mail order\". For communication between people, one does not say 通信 but コミュニケーション or 連絡.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"回線が混んでいます。","romaji":"Kaisen ga konde imasu.","german":"The line is congested.","furigana":"かいせん|こ"},{"japanese":"新しい部屋に光回線を引きました。","romaji":"Atarashii heya ni hikari kaisen o hikimashita.","german":"I had a fiber-optic line installed in my new apartment.","furigana":"あたら|へや|ひかりかいせん|ひ"},{"japanese":"オンライン会議中に回線が切れてしまいました。","romaji":"Onrain kaigichuu ni kaisen ga kirete shimaimashita.","german":"The connection dropped during the online meeting.","furigana":"かいぎちゅう|かいせん|き"}],"notes":"回線 is the technical line for telephone or internet: 電話回線, 光回線 \"fiber-optic connection\", 回線速度. Typical verbs: 回線が混む (\"to be congested\"), 回線がつながる, 回線が切れる, 回線を引く (\"to have a line installed\"). 接続, by contrast, denotes the process or state of connecting, and ケーブル or コード the physical cable.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"インターネットに接続します。","romaji":"Intānetto ni setsuzoku shimasu.","german":"I connect to the internet.","furigana":"せつぞく"},{"japanese":"この電車は次の駅で特急に接続します。","romaji":"Kono densha wa tsugi no eki de tokkyuu ni setsuzoku shimasu.","german":"This train connects with the express at the next station.","furigana":"でんしゃ|つぎ|えき|とっきゅう|せつぞく"},{"japanese":"プリンターをパソコンに接続してください。","romaji":"Purintaa o pasokon ni setsuzoku shite kudasai.","german":"Please connect the printer to the computer.","furigana":"せつぞく"}],"notes":"接続 is connecting or hooking up: intransitive インターネットに接続する (\"to connect\"), transitive プリンターをパソコンに接続する (\"to hook up\"). In rail travel it means \"connection\": 特急に接続する, 接続駅. In grammar there is 接続詞 \"conjunction\". In everyday speech, one often simply says つなぐ/つながる; 接続 sounds more technical.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"端末を操作します。","romaji":"Tanmatsu o sōsa shimasu.","german":"I operate the terminal.","furigana":"たんまつ|そうさ"},{"japanese":"店員さんが注文を端末に入力した。","romaji":"Ten'insan ga chuumon o tanmatsu ni nyuuryoku shita.","german":"The clerk keyed the order into the terminal.","furigana":"てんいん|ちゅうもん|たんまつ|にゅうりょく"},{"japanese":"会社のパソコンやスマホなど、すべての端末にパスワードを設定してください。","romaji":"Kaisha no pasokon ya sumaho nado, subete no tanmatsu ni pasuwaado o settei shite kudasai.","german":"Please set a password on all devices, such as company PCs and smartphones.","furigana":"かいしゃ|たんまつ|せってい"}],"notes":"端末 (literally \"outermost end\") is short for 端末装置, a device at the end of a network: smartphone, tablet, PC, point-of-sale or ordering device, ticket machine. Typical are 携帯端末, 情報端末, 端末代 \"device cost\" when buying a phone. A technical, objective word from companies, government offices and contracts; in conversation people more often say スマホ, パソコン, 機械 or デバイス.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"画面をタッチしてください。","romaji":"Gamen o tatchi shite kudasai.","german":"Please touch the screen.","furigana":"がめん"},{"japanese":"画面が割れてしまったので、スマホを修理に出した。","romaji":"Gamen ga warete shimatta node, sumaho o shuuri ni dashita.","german":"The screen cracked, so I sent my smartphone in for repair.","furigana":"がめん|わ|しゅうり|だ"},{"japanese":"突然、画面にエラーメッセージが出た。","romaji":"Totsuzen, gamen ni eraa messeeji ga deta.","german":"Suddenly an error message appeared on the screen.","furigana":"とつぜん|がめん|で"}],"notes":"画面 is the screen surface and what is seen on it: テレビ・スマホの画面, 画面に表示される/映る, 画面をタッチする, ホーム画面, 画面が割れる (\"the display cracked\"). The device itself is more モニター or ディスプレイ, the cinema screen スクリーン. 画面 also refers to the frame of an image in a film or painting.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"書類を印刷します。","romaji":"Shorui o insatsu shimasu.","german":"I'll print the documents.","furigana":"しょるい|いんさつ"},{"japanese":"年賀状を家のプリンターで印刷した。","romaji":"Nengajou o ie no purintaa de insatsu shita.","german":"I printed the New Year's cards with the printer at home.","furigana":"ねんがじょう|いえ|いんさつ"},{"japanese":"インクが切れて、印刷できません。","romaji":"Inku ga kirete, insatsu dekimasen.","german":"The ink has run out, so I can't print.","furigana":"き|いんさつ"}],"notes":"印刷 is printing, both on a printer and at a print shop: 〜を印刷する, 印刷できない, カラー印刷, 両面印刷, 印刷物, 印刷所. In the office you also hear プリントする or プリントアウト; the noun プリント, however, mostly means a worksheet or handout. Photocopying is コピー (formally 複写), not 印刷.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"書類を複写します。","romaji":"Shorui o fukusha shimasu.","german":"I copy the documents.","furigana":"しょるい|ふくしゃ"},{"japanese":"この資料の無断複写は禁止されています。","romaji":"Kono shiryou no mudan fukusha wa kinshi sarete imasu.","german":"Unauthorized copying of these materials is prohibited.","furigana":"しりょう|むだんふくしゃ|きんし"},{"japanese":"申込書は複写式なので、強めに書いてください。","romaji":"Moushikomisho wa fukushashiki na node, tsuyome ni kaite kudasai.","german":"The application form is a carbon-copy type, so please press firmly when writing.","furigana":"もうしこみしょ|ふくしゃしき|つよ|か"}],"notes":"複写 is the formal, written word for copying or duplicating. It is found on notices (無断複写を禁じます \"unauthorized duplication prohibited\"), in 複写機 \"copier\" and 複写式 \"form with carbon copy\". In conversation, one almost always says コピーする. 写す is copying by hand, and 複製 is reproducing works of art or keys.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ファイルを保存します。","romaji":"Fairu o hozon shimasu.","german":"I'll save the file.","furigana":"ほぞん"},{"japanese":"開けた後は冷蔵庫で保存してください。","romaji":"Aketa ato wa reizouko de hozon shite kudasai.","german":"After opening, please store in the refrigerator.","furigana":"あ|あと|れいぞうこ|ほぞん"},{"japanese":"この寺は昔の姿のまま大切に保存されている。","romaji":"Kono tera wa mukashi no sugata no mama taisetsu ni hozon sarete iru.","german":"This temple has been carefully preserved in its original form.","furigana":"てら|むかし|すがた|たいせつ|ほぞん"}],"notes":"保存 means keeping something in good condition for later: food (冷蔵保存, 冷凍保存, 保存料 \"preservative\"), buildings and cultural properties (保存される), and data (ファイルを保存する, 上書き保存). 保管 is the safe keeping of objects or documents, 維持 the maintaining of a state.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"データを削除しました。","romaji":"Dēta o sakujo shimashita.","german":"I deleted the data.","furigana":"さくじょ"},{"japanese":"失礼なコメントは削除されました。","romaji":"Shitsurei na komento wa sakujo saremashita.","german":"Rude comments were deleted.","furigana":"しつれい|さくじょ"},{"japanese":"文章の最後の段落を削除してください。","romaji":"Bunshou no saigo no danraku o sakujo shite kudasai.","german":"Please delete the last paragraph of the text.","furigana":"ぶんしょう|さいご|だんらく|さくじょ"}],"notes":"削除 is deleting or striking from data, texts or lists: データ・メール・コメントを削除する, 削除される, 削除キー. It sounds technical or formal. The everyday 消す is broader (also wiping a blackboard, turning off a light), 取り消す means \"to cancel, to take back\", and 削る \"to shave off, to cut down\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ソフトを更新します。","romaji":"Sofuto o kōshin shimasu.","german":"I'll update the software.","furigana":"こうしん"},{"japanese":"来月、パスポートを更新しなければならない。","romaji":"Raigetsu, pasupooto o koushin shinakereba naranai.","german":"I have to renew my passport next month.","furigana":"らいげつ|こうしん"},{"japanese":"彼はマラソンの世界記録を更新した。","romaji":"Kare wa marason no sekai kiroku o koushin shita.","german":"He broke the marathon world record.","furigana":"かれ|せかいきろく|こうしん"}],"notes":"更新 means renewing or updating: extending contracts, IDs and visas (契約・免許・パスポートを更新する), updating software and websites (ブログを更新する, ページが更新される) and breaking records (記録を更新する). Both transitive and intransitive are possible. 延長, by contrast, is simply the extension of a deadline or duration.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"パスワードを入力してください。","romaji":"Pasuwādo o nyūryoku shite kudasai.","german":"Please enter your password.","furigana":"にゅうりょく"},{"japanese":"毎日、売り上げのデータをパソコンに入力するのが私の仕事です。","romaji":"Mainichi, uriage no deeta o pasokon ni nyuuryoku suru no ga watashi no shigoto desu.","german":"My job is to enter sales data into the computer every day.","furigana":"まいにち|う|あ|にゅうりょく|わたし|しごと"},{"japanese":"スマホで日本語を入力するのは、まだ慣れません。","romaji":"Sumaho de Nihongo o nyuuryoku suru no wa, mada naremasen.","german":"I'm still not used to typing Japanese on a smartphone.","furigana":"にほんご|にゅうりょく|な"}],"notes":"入力 is the input of data into a device: 〜を〜に入力する, 入力欄 \"input field\", 入力ミス, 音声入力. The opposite is 出力. For filling in a paper form by hand, one says 記入, not 入力. Colloquially, typing is also 打つ (メールを打つ).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"データを出力します。","romaji":"Dēta o shutsuryoku shimasu.","german":"We output the data.","furigana":"しゅつりょく"},{"japanese":"報告書をPDFで出力して、メールで送ってください。","romaji":"Houkokusho o PDF de shutsuryoku shite, meeru de okutte kudasai.","german":"Please export the report as a PDF and send it by email.","furigana":"ほうこくしょ|しゅつりょく|おく"},{"japanese":"このエンジンは出力が大きい。","romaji":"Kono enjin wa shutsuryoku ga ookii.","german":"This engine has a high output.","furigana":"しゅつりょく|おお"}],"notes":"出力 is the output of data to a screen, printer or file: データを出力する, PDFで出力する, プリンターに出力する (\"to print out\"). It also refers to the power of engines, speakers or power plants: 出力が大きい, 最大出力. The opposite is 入力; for plain printing, 印刷 is more common.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"漢字に変換します。","romaji":"Kanji ni henkan shimasu.","german":"I'll convert it into kanji.","furigana":"かんじ|へんかん"},{"japanese":"この動画をMP4に変換したい。","romaji":"Kono douga o MP4 ni henkan shitai.","german":"I want to convert this video to MP4.","furigana":"どうが|へんかん"},{"japanese":"ソーラーパネルは太陽の光を電気に変換する。","romaji":"Sooraa paneru wa taiyou no hikari o denki ni henkan suru.","german":"Solar panels convert sunlight into electricity.","furigana":"たいよう|ひかり|でんき|へんかん"}],"notes":"変換 means converting something into another form: 〜を〜に変換する. Very common in everyday IT: かな漢字変換 and the 変換キー of the keyboard, 変換ミス (wrong kanji from the input aid), converting file formats. Also in technology: 光を電気に変換する. 変化, by contrast, is a change of its own accord, 交換 an exchange, 換算 the conversion of units and 両替 currency exchange.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"インターネットで検索します。","romaji":"Intānetto de kensaku shimasu.","german":"I'll search on the internet.","furigana":"けんさく"},{"japanese":"分からない言葉は、辞書アプリで検索します。","romaji":"Wakaranai kotoba wa, jisho apuri de kensaku shimasu.","german":"For words I don't know, I look them up in a dictionary app.","furigana":"わ|ことば|じしょ|けんさく"},{"japanese":"「東京　ラーメン」で検索したら、たくさんの店が出てきた。","romaji":"\"Toukyou raamen\" de kensaku shitara, takusan no mise ga dete kita.","german":"When I searched for \"Tokyo ramen\", many shops came up.","furigana":"とうきょう|けんさく|みせ|で"}],"notes":"検索 is searching for information in a database, a dictionary or on the internet: 〜を検索する, 〜で検索する (search term or tool), 検索エンジン, 検索結果. Colloquially also ググる. For searching for lost things or people, one says 探す, for investigating in general 調べる; so you cannot 検索する a lost key.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"会員に登録します。","romaji":"Kaiin ni tōroku shimasu.","german":"I'm registering as a member.","furigana":"かいいん|とうろく"},{"japanese":"電話番号を登録しておくと、名前が表示されます。","romaji":"Denwa bangou o touroku shite oku to, namae ga hyouji saremasu.","german":"If you save the phone number, the name will be displayed.","furigana":"でんわばんごう|とうろく|なまえ|ひょうじ"},{"japanese":"富士山は2013年に世界遺産に登録された。","romaji":"Fujisan wa nisen juusan nen ni sekai isan ni touroku sareta.","german":"Mt. Fuji was registered as a World Heritage Site in 2013.","furigana":"ふじさん|ねん|せかいいさん|とうろく"}],"notes":"登録 is the official entering into a list, register or database: 会員登録, ユーザー登録, 〜に登録する (to register with something), 〜を登録する (to save a number, card or name). On YouTube, チャンネル登録 means \"to subscribe\", and 世界遺産に登録される means \"to be designated a World Heritage Site\". 申し込む is \"to apply, sign up for\", and registering your residence is called 転入届.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 13"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"設定を変更します。","romaji":"Settei o henkō shimasu.","german":"I'm changing the settings.","furigana":"せってい|へんこう"},{"japanese":"アラームを六時に設定しました。","romaji":"Araamu o rokuji ni settei shimashita.","german":"I set the alarm for six o'clock.","furigana":"ろくじ|せってい"},{"japanese":"この映画は、未来の東京という設定になっている。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa, mirai no Toukyou to iu settei ni natte iru.","german":"This movie is set in a future Tokyo.","furigana":"えいが|みらい|とうきょう|せってい"}],"notes":"設定 means, on the one hand, setting or configuring devices and programs: 〜を〜に設定する, 初期設定, 設定画面, パスワードを設定する. On the other hand, it refers to the starting situation of a story: 〜という設定 \"set in ..., the premise is ...\". Goals or deadlines are also set this way (目標を設定する). 設置, by contrast, is the physical setting up or installing.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"操作方法を教えてください。","romaji":"Sōsa hōhō o oshiete kudasai.","german":"Please tell me how to operate it.","furigana":"そうさほうほう|おし"},{"japanese":"この機械は操作が簡単で、子どもでも使えます。","romaji":"Kono kikai wa sousa ga kantan de, kodomo demo tsukaemasu.","german":"This machine is easy to operate; even children can use it.","furigana":"きかい|そうさ|かんたん|こ|つか"},{"japanese":"彼はデータを操作して、結果をよく見せた。","romaji":"Kare wa deeta o sousa shite, kekka o yoku miseta.","german":"He manipulated the data to make the results look good.","furigana":"かれ|そうさ|けっか|み"}],"notes":"操作 is the operation of machines and devices: 機械・パソコン・スマホを操作する, 操作方法, 操作ミス, 遠隔操作. Secondly, it means manipulation for one's own advantage: データ操作, 情報操作. Vehicles, by contrast, are driven with 運転する. Beware of the homophone: 捜査 (also そうさ) is a police investigation.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 35"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"エレベーターが故障しました。","romaji":"Erebētā ga koshō shimashita.","german":"The elevator has broken down.","furigana":"こしょう"},{"japanese":"この自動販売機は故障中です。","romaji":"Kono jidou hanbaiki wa koshouchuu desu.","german":"This vending machine is out of order.","furigana":"じどうはんばいき|こしょうちゅう"},{"japanese":"車が故障して、会社に遅れました。","romaji":"Kuruma ga koshou shite, kaisha ni okuremashita.","german":"My car broke down, so I was late for work.","furigana":"くるま|こしょう|かいしゃ|おく"}],"notes":"故障 refers to a technical failure of machines, devices or vehicles; as an intransitive する-verb: エレベーターが故障する. On signs you read 故障中 \"out of order\". 壊れる is more general and also covers mechanical breaking (glass, toys), whereas 故障 stresses that something no longer works. For people, 故障 is used only in sports for injuries (膝の故障); otherwise you say けが.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 23"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"建物を修復します。","romaji":"Tatemono o shūfuku shimasu.","german":"We will restore the building.","furigana":"たてもの|しゅうふく"},{"japanese":"壊れたデータを修復するソフトがあります。","romaji":"Kowareta deeta o shuufuku suru sofuto ga arimasu.","german":"There is software that repairs corrupted data.","furigana":"こわ|しゅうふく"},{"japanese":"けんかの後、二人の関係を修復するのに時間がかかった。","romaji":"Kenka no ato, futari no kankei o shuufuku suru no ni jikan ga kakatta.","german":"After the fight, it took a long time to mend the relationship between the two.","furigana":"あと|ふたり|かんけい|しゅうふく|じかん"}],"notes":"修復 means bringing something damaged back to its original state: historic buildings and works of art (文化財の修復), damaged data, and also relationships (関係を修復する \"to mend\"). For everyday repairs of devices, one says 修理; for restoring power, trains or networks after outages, 復旧; for rebuilding whole regions, 復興.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"車を点検に出しました。","romaji":"Kuruma o tenken ni dashimashita.","german":"I took my car in for inspection.","furigana":"くるま|てんけん|だ"},{"japanese":"毎朝、運転手はバスのブレーキを点検します。","romaji":"Maiasa, untenshu wa basu no bureeki o tenken shimasu.","german":"Every morning, the driver checks the bus's brakes.","furigana":"まいあさ|うんてんしゅ|てんけん"},{"japanese":"本日はエレベーターの点検のため、階段をご利用ください。","romaji":"Honjitsu wa erebeetaa no tenken no tame, kaidan o goriyou kudasai.","german":"Today the elevator is undergoing maintenance, so please use the stairs.","furigana":"ほんじつ|てんけん|かいだん|りよう"}],"notes":"点検 is a thorough check in which every part is examined for faults and safety: 車・機械・設備を点検する, 定期点検, 安全点検, 点検中. Taking a car in for inspection is 点検に出す. 検査 is a test or examination (also medical), 確認 a confirmation, 整備 the maintenance work itself; チェック is the casual equivalent.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"道路を整備します。","romaji":"Dōro o seibi shimasu.","german":"We will develop the roads.","furigana":"どうろ|せいび"},{"japanese":"飛行機は出発前に必ず整備されます。","romaji":"Hikouki wa shuppatsu mae ni kanarazu seibi saremasu.","german":"Airplanes are always serviced before departure.","furigana":"ひこうき|しゅっぱつまえ|かなら|せいび"},{"japanese":"子育てしやすい環境を整備することが大切だ。","romaji":"Kosodate shiyasui kankyou o seibi suru koto ga taisetsu da.","german":"It is important to create an environment in which it is easy to raise children.","furigana":"こそだ|かんきょう|せいび|たいせつ"}],"notes":"整備 has two main meanings. First, the maintenance of vehicles and machines: 車・飛行機の整備, 整備士 \"mechanic\", 整備不良. Second, the development and organization of infrastructure or framework conditions: 道路・環境・制度を整備する. 点検 is the inspection beforehand, 修理 is the repair after a defect, and 手入れ is everyday care of a garden or tools.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人工知能が発展しています。","romaji":"Jinkō chinō ga hatten shite imasu.","german":"Artificial intelligence is advancing.","furigana":"じんこうちのう|はってん"},{"japanese":"人工知能を使って、文章を翻訳しました。","romaji":"Jinkou chinou o tsukatte, bunshou o hon'yaku shimashita.","german":"I translated the text using AI.","furigana":"じんこうちのう|つか|ぶんしょう|ほんやく"},{"japanese":"将来、人工知能に仕事を取られるかもしれないと心配する人もいます。","romaji":"Shourai, jinkou chinou ni shigoto o torareru kamo shirenai to shinpai suru hito mo imasu.","german":"Some people worry that AI might take their jobs in the future.","furigana":"しょうらい|じんこうちのう|しごと|と|しんぱい|ひと"}],"notes":"人工知能 is the full technical term for \"artificial intelligence\"; in the news, advertising and daily life people mostly say AI for short (pronounced エーアイ). 人工 means \"artificial, man-made\" (cf. 人工衛星 \"satellite\"), 知能 \"intelligence\" (cf. 知能テスト). Typical combinations: 人工知能を活用する \"to use AI\", 人工知能が発達する／進歩する, 人工知能に仕事を奪われる \"to have one's job taken by AI\".","levelNote":"Compound: 人工 (N3) + 知能 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"業務を自動化します。","romaji":"Gyōmu o jidōka shimasu.","german":"We are automating the work processes.","furigana":"ぎょうむ|じどうか"},{"japanese":"工場では多くの作業が自動化されています。","romaji":"Koujou de wa ooku no sagyou ga jidouka sarete imasu.","german":"Many tasks are automated at the factory.","furigana":"こうじょう|おお|さぎょう|じどうか"},{"japanese":"毎月の請求書作りを自動化したら、とても楽になりました。","romaji":"Maitsuki no seikyuusho-zukuri o jidouka shitara, totemo raku ni narimashita.","german":"Since I automated the monthly invoice preparation, things have become much easier.","furigana":"まいつき|せいきゅうしょづく|じどうか|らく"}],"notes":"自動化 \"automation\" is a noun with the suffix 〜化 \"-ization\" (cf. 機械化, 国際化) and is mostly used as a transitive する-verb: 作業を自動化する \"to automate a workflow\". For the result you say 自動化される or 自動化が進む \"automation is progressing\". Do not confuse it with 自動 \"automatic\" on its own, which usually stands before nouns: 自動ドア, 自動販売機.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このプリンターを標準に設定します。","romaji":"Kono purintaa o hyoujun ni settei shimasu.","german":"I set this printer as the default.","furigana":"ひょうじゅん|せってい"},{"japanese":"彼は標準語ではなく、関西弁で話します。","romaji":"Kare wa hyoujungo de wa naku, Kansai-ben de hanashimasu.","german":"He speaks not standard Japanese but the Kansai dialect.","furigana":"かれ|ひょうじゅんご|かんさいべん|はな"},{"japanese":"この子の身長は標準より少し高いです。","romaji":"Kono ko no shinchou wa hyoujun yori sukoshi takai desu.","german":"This child's height is a little above average.","furigana":"こ|しんちょう|ひょうじゅん|すこ|たか"}],"notes":"標準 means \"standard, norm\" in the sense of the usual or average: 標準的な \"average, normal\", 標準より高い \"above average\". Frequent in compounds: 標準語 \"standard language\", 標準体重 \"standard weight\", 標準サイズ; for devices and software, 標準で means \"as standard\" and 標準に設定する \"to set as the default\". To be distinguished from 基準 \"benchmark, criterion (for judging)\" and 規格 \"technical standard\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"国際規格に準拠します。","romaji":"Kokusai kikaku ni junkyo shimasu.","german":"We comply with international standards.","furigana":"こくさいきかく|じゅんきょ"},{"japanese":"このプラグは日本の規格に合わないので、使えません。","romaji":"Kono puragu wa Nihon no kikaku ni awanai node, tsukaemasen.","german":"This plug doesn't conform to the Japanese standard, so it can't be used.","furigana":"にほん|きかく|あ|つか"},{"japanese":"規格外の野菜を安く買いました。","romaji":"Kikakugai no yasai o yasuku kaimashita.","german":"I bought non-standard vegetables cheaply.","furigana":"きかくがい|やさい|やす|か"}],"notes":"規格 is a fixed technical standard for the size, shape or quality of products: 国際規格, 日本工業規格, 規格に合う／合わない \"to conform / not conform to the standard\". Common in everyday speech is 規格外 \"outside the standard\", for example for crooked vegetables sold more cheaply, or jokingly for people with extraordinary abilities. Do not confuse it with the identically read 企画 \"planning, project\"; 標準 is more general \"standard, average\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仕様を確認します。","romaji":"Shiyō o kakunin shimasu.","german":"We will check the specifications.","furigana":"しよう|かくにん"},{"japanese":"この時計は防水仕様なので、お風呂でも使えます。","romaji":"Kono tokei wa bousui shiyou na node, ofuro de mo tsukaemasu.","german":"This watch is waterproof, so you can use it in the bath too.","furigana":"とけい|ぼうすいしよう|ふろ|つか"},{"japanese":"お客様の希望で、仕様が変更になりました。","romaji":"Okyakusama no kibou de, shiyou ga henkou ni narimashita.","german":"The specifications were changed at the customer's request.","furigana":"きゃくさま|きぼう|しよう|へんこう"}],"notes":"仕様 refers to the fixed design or technical specification of a product: 仕様書 \"specification document, requirements document\", 仕様を変更する \"to change the specification\". 〜仕様 is often used as a suffix meaning \"version, model\" (防水仕様 \"waterproof model\", 日本仕様), and それは仕様です means jokingly \"that's by design, not a bug\". The old meaning \"means, way\" lives on in 仕様がない, which today is usually written しょうがない. Do not confuse it with the identically read 使用 \"use\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"天然素材を使っています。","romaji":"Tennen sozai o tsukatte imasu.","german":"We use natural materials.","furigana":"てんねんそざい|つか"},{"japanese":"このシャツはどんな素材でできていますか。","romaji":"Kono shatsu wa donna sozai de dekite imasu ka.","german":"What material is this shirt made of?","furigana":"そざい"},{"japanese":"この料理は素材の味を生かしています。","romaji":"Kono ryouri wa sozai no aji o ikashite imasu.","german":"This dish brings out the natural flavor of the ingredients.","furigana":"りょうり|そざい|あじ|い"}],"notes":"素材 is the raw material something is made of, especially for clothing, furniture and cooking: 天然素材, 素材の味を生かす \"to bring out the natural flavor of the ingredients\". On the Internet, 素材 also means freely usable raw material such as images, music or icons (フリー素材). Compared with 材料 \"material, ingredients\" (general, also in recipes), 素材 stresses the quality of the raw material; 原料 is the industrial raw material.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"天然繊維の服です。","romaji":"Tennen sen'i no fuku desu.","german":"These are clothes made of natural fibers.","furigana":"てんねんせんい|ふく"},{"japanese":"ごぼうには食物繊維がたくさん含まれています。","romaji":"Gobou ni wa shokumotsu sen'i ga takusan fukumarete imasu.","german":"Burdock root contains a lot of dietary fiber.","furigana":"しょくもつせんい|ふく"},{"japanese":"この町は昔、繊維工業で栄えました。","romaji":"Kono machi wa mukashi, sen'i kougyou de sakaemashita.","german":"This town once prospered thanks to the textile industry.","furigana":"まち|むかし|せんいこうぎょう|さか"}],"notes":"繊維 means fibers, both textile fibers (天然繊維, 化学繊維, 繊維工業 \"textile industry\") and dietary fiber in nutrition: 食物繊維 \"dietary fiber\", 繊維が多い野菜. In everyday life, you meet the word most often in 食物繊維 on food packaging. For finished fabric, one says 布 or 生地, not 繊維.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"金属を加工します。","romaji":"Kinzoku o kakō shimasu.","german":"We process metal.","furigana":"きんぞく|かこう"},{"japanese":"金属アレルギーなので、安いピアスはつけられません。","romaji":"Kinzoku arerugii na node, yasui piasu wa tsukeraremasen.","german":"I have a metal allergy, so I can't wear cheap earrings.","furigana":"きんぞく|やす"},{"japanese":"空港の検査で、ポケットの金属を出すように言われました。","romaji":"Kuukou no kensa de, poketto no kinzoku o dasu you ni iwaremashita.","german":"At airport security I was told to take the metal objects out of my pockets.","furigana":"くうこう|けんさ|きんぞく|だ|い"}],"notes":"金属 is the general term \"metal\"; individual metals have their own words such as 鉄 \"iron\", 銅 \"copper\", 銀 \"silver\". Typical: 金属製の \"made of metal\", 金属アレルギー \"metal allergy\", 貴金属 \"precious metal\", and 金属ごみ in waste sorting. 金 on its own means \"gold\" or, as お金, \"money\", not \"metal\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本の陶器は有名です。","romaji":"Nihon no tōki wa yūmei desu.","german":"Japanese pottery is famous.","furigana":"にほん|とうき|ゆうめい"},{"japanese":"陶器のカップは落とすと割れやすいです。","romaji":"Touki no kappu wa otosu to wareyasui desu.","german":"Ceramic cups break easily if you drop them.","furigana":"とうき|お|わ"},{"japanese":"週末に陶器市でお皿を買いました。","romaji":"Shuumatsu ni toukiichi de osara o kaimashita.","german":"On the weekend, I bought plates at a pottery fair.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|とうきいち|さら|か"}],"notes":"陶器 is earthenware or pottery made of clay, fired at a relatively low temperature, and looks rather thick and warm; the harder, thinner porcelain is called 磁器. As a general term for ceramic tableware, one says 陶磁器 or simply 焼き物 (well-known kinds: 有田焼, 益子焼). Typical: 陶器の皿 \"ceramic plate\", 陶器市 \"pottery fair\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"漆塗りのお椀です。","romaji":"Urushi-nuri no owan desu.","german":"It is a lacquered bowl.","furigana":"うるしぬ|わん"},{"japanese":"漆の器は長く使うほど美しくなります。","romaji":"Urushi no utsuwa wa nagaku tsukau hodo utsukushiku narimasu.","german":"Lacquerware becomes more beautiful the longer you use it.","furigana":"うるし|うつわ|なが|つか|うつく"},{"japanese":"山で漆の木に触って、手がかぶれてしまいました。","romaji":"Yama de urushi no ki ni sawatte, te ga kaburete shimaimashita.","german":"I touched a lacquer tree in the mountains and got a rash on my hand.","furigana":"やま|うるし|き|さわ|て"}],"notes":"漆 is the traditional East Asian lacquer made from the sap of the lacquer tree, which is also called 漆 (often written in kana うるし). Lacquered objects are called 漆器 \"lacquerware\"; 漆塗り \"coated with Japanese lacquer\" describes bowls, chopsticks or trays (e.g. 輪島塗). The fresh sap irritates the skin: 漆にかぶれる \"to get a rash from the lacquer tree\". For modern lacquer or paint you say ペンキ or ニス, not 漆.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"陶芸教室に通っています。","romaji":"Tōgei kyōshitsu ni kayotte imasu.","german":"I attend a pottery class.","furigana":"とうげいきょうしつ|かよ"},{"japanese":"旅行先で陶芸体験をして、自分の茶碗を作りました。","romaji":"Ryokousaki de tougei taiken o shite, jibun no chawan o tsukurimashita.","german":"On a trip I tried pottery and made my own rice bowl.","furigana":"りょこうさき|とうげいたいけん|じぶん|ちゃわん|つく"},{"japanese":"祖父は定年後に陶芸を始めました。","romaji":"Sofu wa teinengo ni tougei o hajimemashita.","german":"My grandfather took up pottery after retiring.","furigana":"そふ|ていねんご|とうげい|はじ"}],"notes":"陶芸 is the art of pottery as an activity or hobby, i.e. shaping and firing ceramics; the product itself is called 陶器 or 焼き物. Typical combinations: 陶芸教室 \"pottery class\", 陶芸家 \"ceramic artist\", 陶芸体験 \"try-it-yourself pottery\" (a popular offering for tourists). As a verb you say 陶芸をする or, more concretely, 器を作る, ろくろを回す \"to turn the potter's wheel\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"華道を楽しんでいます。","romaji":"Kadō o tanoshinde imasu.","german":"I enjoy ikebana.","furigana":"かどう|たの"},{"japanese":"高校では華道部に入っていました。","romaji":"Koukou de wa kadoubu ni haitte imashita.","german":"In high school, I was in the ikebana club.","furigana":"こうこう|かどうぶ|はい"},{"japanese":"母は二十年以上、華道を習っています。","romaji":"Haha wa nijuunen ijou, kadou o naratte imasu.","german":"My mother has been studying ikebana for over twenty years.","furigana":"はは|にじゅうねんいじょう|かどう|なら"}],"notes":"華道 is the art of flower arranging as a \"way\" (道) with schools and rules, like 茶道 \"tea ceremony\" and 書道 \"calligraphy\". In everyday speech one usually says 生け花 or いけばな (いけばなを習う); 華道 sounds more formal and emphasizes discipline: 華道部 \"ikebana club\", 華道の先生, 華道の流派 \"ikebana school\". A single flower arrangement is 生け花, not 華道.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"武道を通して心を鍛えます。","romaji":"Budō o tōshite kokoro o kitaemasu.","german":"I train my mind through martial arts.","furigana":"ぶどう|とお|こころ|きた"},{"japanese":"日本の中学校では、体育の授業で武道を習います。","romaji":"Nihon no chuugakkou de wa, taiiku no jugyou de budou o naraimasu.","german":"In Japanese junior high schools, students learn martial arts in physical education class.","furigana":"にほん|ちゅうがっこう|たいいく|じゅぎょう|ぶどう|なら"},{"japanese":"武道は礼に始まり、礼に終わると言われています。","romaji":"Budou wa rei ni hajimari, rei ni owaru to iwarete imasu.","german":"It is said that martial arts begin and end with a bow.","furigana":"ぶどう|れい|はじ|れい|お|い"}],"notes":"武道 is the collective term for the Japanese martial arts as a \"way\" with mental training, e.g. 柔道, 剣道, 空手, 合気道, 弓道. Compared with 格闘技 \"combat sports\" (boxing etc.), 武道 stresses etiquette, discipline and character building; a well-known saying is 武道は礼に始まり礼に終わる \"martial arts begin and end with a bow\". Typical: 武道を習う, 武道館 \"martial arts hall\". The identically read ぶどう \"grapes\" exists, but it is usually written in kana.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"弓道を練習しています。","romaji":"Kyūdō o renshū shite imasu.","german":"I am practicing kyudo.","furigana":"きゅうどう|れんしゅう"},{"japanese":"高校の弓道部で三年間がんばりました。","romaji":"Koukou no kyuudoubu de sannenkan ganbarimashita.","german":"I worked hard for three years in the kyudo club at high school.","furigana":"こうこう|きゅうどうぶ|さんねんかん"},{"japanese":"弓道では、的に当てることより正しい姿勢が大切です。","romaji":"Kyuudou de wa, mato ni ateru koto yori tadashii shisei ga taisetsu desu.","german":"In kyudo, correct posture is more important than hitting the target.","furigana":"きゅうどう|まと|あ|ただ|しせい|たいせつ"}],"notes":"弓道 is traditional Japanese archery with the long bow 弓 and counts as one of the 武道; posture, breathing and concentration matter more than the number of hits. Olympic sport archery, by contrast, is called アーチェリー. Typical: 弓道部 (a popular school club), 弓道場 \"kyudo range\", 弓を引く \"to draw the bow\", 的に当たる \"to hit the target\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"歌舞伎を観ました。","romaji":"Kabuki o mimashita.","german":"I saw Kabuki.","furigana":"かぶき|み"},{"japanese":"歌舞伎では、女性の役も男性の役者が演じます。","romaji":"Kabuki de wa, josei no yaku mo dansei no yakusha ga enjimasu.","german":"In Kabuki, female roles are also played by male actors.","furigana":"かぶき|じょせい|やく|だんせい|やくしゃ|えん"},{"japanese":"初めて歌舞伎を見るなら、イヤホンガイドを借りるといいですよ。","romaji":"Hajimete kabuki o miru nara, iyahon gaido o kariru to ii desu yo.","german":"If you're watching Kabuki for the first time, it's a good idea to rent an earphone guide.","furigana":"はじ|かぶき|み|か"}],"notes":"歌舞伎 is the traditional popular theater of the Edo period, with magnificent costumes, striking makeup and exclusively male performers; female roles are played by 女形. Typical: 歌舞伎を観る／見る (観る stresses watching performances), 歌舞伎座 (the theater in Ginza), 歌舞伎役者 \"Kabuki actor\". The kanji 歌 \"song\", 舞 \"dance\", 伎 \"skill\" were assigned afterward to the old verb かぶく \"to behave eccentrically\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 9"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"落語を聞きに行きます。","romaji":"Rakugo o kiki ni ikimasu.","german":"I am going to listen to rakugo.","furigana":"らくご|き|い"},{"japanese":"落語家は一人でいろいろな役を演じます。","romaji":"Rakugoka wa hitori de iroiro na yaku o enjimasu.","german":"A rakugo performer plays many different roles all by himself.","furigana":"らくごか|ひとり|やく|えん"},{"japanese":"最近は英語で落語をする人もいます。","romaji":"Saikin wa eigo de rakugo o suru hito mo imasu.","german":"Recently there are also people who perform rakugo in English.","furigana":"さいきん|えいご|らくご|ひと"}],"notes":"落語 is traditional comic storytelling: a single 落語家 sits on a cushion in a kimono and, with only a fan and a hand towel, plays all the characters of a story that ends with a punch line (落ち／オチ), which is also where the name comes from. You usually say 落語を聞く, even though you also watch, and you experience it live in a 寄席 \"variety theater\". Do not confuse it with 漫才, comedy performed as a duo.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"能の舞台を観ました。","romaji":"Nō no butai o mimashita.","german":"I saw a Noh performance.","furigana":"のう|ぶたい|み"},{"japanese":"能では、役者が面をつけて演じます。","romaji":"Nou de wa, yakusha ga men o tsukete enjimasu.","german":"In Noh, the actors perform wearing masks.","furigana":"のう|やくしゃ|めん|えん"},{"japanese":"能ある鷹は爪を隠すと言いますね。","romaji":"Nou aru taka wa tsume o kakusu to iimasu ne.","german":"As they say, a skilled hawk hides its talons.","furigana":"のう|たか|つめ|かく|い"}],"notes":"能 as a stage form is the classical Japanese masked theater with slow, stylized movements, singing and music; it is often performed together with the comic 狂言 under the name 能楽. Typical: 能を観る, 能面 \"Noh mask\", 能舞台 \"Noh stage\". Otherwise 能 means \"ability, talent\" and appears almost only in compounds (能力, 才能, 可能) or in the proverb 能ある鷹は爪を隠す \"a capable hawk hides its talons\", said of skilled people who don't boast.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"狂言は面白いです。","romaji":"Kyōgen wa omoshiroi desu.","german":"Kyogen is entertaining.","furigana":"きょうげん|おもしろ"},{"japanese":"能と能の間に、短い狂言が演じられます。","romaji":"Nou to nou no aida ni, mijikai kyougen ga enjiraremasu.","german":"Between two Noh plays, a short kyogen is performed.","furigana":"のう|のう|あいだ|みじか|きょうげん|えん"},{"japanese":"その事件は、お金に困った男の狂言だったそうです。","romaji":"Sono jiken wa, okane ni komatta otoko no kyougen datta sou desu.","german":"They say the incident was staged by a man in need of money.","furigana":"じけん|かね|こま|おとこ|きょうげん"}],"notes":"狂言 is the classical comic stage form performed between the serious 能 plays; in old colloquial language, short dialogues show everyday scenes with masters, servants and farmers. Typical: 狂言を観る, 狂言師 \"kyogen performer\". Outside the theater, 狂言 means \"staged deception, a put-up job\", especially in 狂言強盗 \"faked robbery\" or 狂言誘拐 \"faked kidnapping\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"声優になりたいです。","romaji":"Seiyū ni naritai desu.","german":"I want to become a voice actor.","furigana":"せいゆう"},{"japanese":"このアニメの主人公の声は、有名な声優が担当しています。","romaji":"Kono anime no shujinkou no koe wa, yuumei na seiyuu ga tantou shite imasu.","german":"The main character of this anime is voiced by a famous voice actor.","furigana":"しゅじんこう|こえ|ゆうめい|せいゆう|たんとう"},{"japanese":"外国の映画を、日本の声優の吹き替えで見ました。","romaji":"Gaikoku no eiga o, Nihon no seiyuu no fukikae de mimashita.","german":"I watched a foreign film dubbed by Japanese voice actors.","furigana":"がいこく|えいが|にほん|せいゆう|ふ|か|み"}],"notes":"声優 are voice actors for anime, video games, radio dramas and dubbed versions of foreign films; in Japan they are often stars with their own fans, concerts and radio shows. Typical: 声優になる, 人気声優, 声優を目指す \"to aim to become a voice actor\", 〜の声を担当する \"to voice ...\". The dubbing itself is called 吹き替え; a narrator in documentaries is more likely a ナレーター.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"展示会に行きます。","romaji":"Tenjikai ni ikimasu.","german":"I'm going to the exhibition.","furigana":"てんじかい|い"},{"japanese":"来月、東京で新製品の展示会が開かれます。","romaji":"Raigetsu, Toukyou de shinseihin no tenjikai ga hirakaremasu.","german":"A trade show for new products will be held in Tokyo next month.","furigana":"らいげつ|とうきょう|しんせいひん|てんじかい|ひら"},{"japanese":"市役所のロビーで、子どもたちの絵の展示会をしています。","romaji":"Shiyakusho no robii de, kodomotachi no e no tenjikai o shite imasu.","german":"An exhibition of children's pictures is being held in the lobby of the city hall.","furigana":"しやくしょ|こ|え|てんじかい"}],"notes":"展示会 is an exhibition where things are shown, especially products at trade and sales fairs (新製品の展示会) or collections and works by amateurs. For art exhibitions, one more often says 展覧会 or, in short, 〜展 (ピカソ展); 展示会 sounds more matter-of-fact. Typical: 展示会を開く／開催する, 展示会に出展する \"to exhibit at a trade show\"; the verb 展示する \"to exhibit\" is transitive.","levelNote":"Compound: 展示 (N1) + 会 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"画家の個展を見ました。","romaji":"Gaka no koten o mimashita.","german":"I saw the painter's solo exhibition.","furigana":"がか|こてん|み"},{"japanese":"友達が来月、銀座で初めての個展を開きます。","romaji":"Tomodachi ga raigetsu, Ginza de hajimete no koten o hirakimasu.","german":"A friend of mine is holding his first solo exhibition in Ginza next month.","furigana":"ともだち|らいげつ|ぎんざ|はじ|こてん|ひら"},{"japanese":"いつか自分の写真の個展を開くのが夢です。","romaji":"Itsuka jibun no shashin no koten o hiraku no ga yume desu.","german":"My dream is to hold a solo exhibition of my own photographs someday.","furigana":"じぶん|しゃしん|こてん|ひら|ゆめ"}],"notes":"個展 is the short form of 個人展覧会 and means a solo exhibition showing only the works of a single artist; the counterpart is グループ展. Typical: 個展を開く \"to hold a solo exhibition\", 初個展 \"first solo exhibition\". Caution: the identically read 古典 \"classic, classical literature\" exists.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彫刻を鑑賞します。","romaji":"Chōkoku o kanshō shimasu.","german":"I am looking at sculptures.","furigana":"ちょうこく|かんしょう"},{"japanese":"公園の真ん中に大きな彫刻があります。","romaji":"Kouen no mannaka ni ookina choukoku ga arimasu.","german":"There is a large sculpture in the middle of the park.","furigana":"こうえん|ま|なか|おお|ちょうこく"},{"japanese":"美術の授業で、彫刻刀を使って木の板を彫りました。","romaji":"Bijutsu no jugyou de, choukokutou o tsukatte ki no ita o horimashita.","german":"In art class, I carved a wooden board using a carving knife.","furigana":"びじゅつ|じゅぎょう|ちょうこくとう|つか|き|いた|ほ"}],"notes":"彫刻 is both the work, \"sculpture, carving\", and the activity, \"sculpting, engraving\", and can be used as a する-verb: 木に模様を彫刻する. Typical: 彫刻家 \"sculptor\", 石の彫刻, 彫刻刀 \"carving knife\" (familiar from art class). For the action itself, everyday speech mostly uses the simple verb 彫る \"to carve, to engrave\"; 彫刻 sounds more artistic.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 37"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"絵画が趣味です。","romaji":"Kaiga ga shumi desu.","german":"Painting is my hobby.","furigana":"かいが|しゅみ"},{"japanese":"この美術館には西洋の絵画がたくさんあります。","romaji":"Kono bijutsukan ni wa seiyou no kaiga ga takusan arimasu.","german":"This museum has many Western paintings.","furigana":"びじゅつかん|せいよう|かいが"},{"japanese":"母は毎週、絵画教室で油絵を習っています。","romaji":"Haha wa maishuu, kaiga kyoushitsu de aburae o naratte imasu.","german":"My mother takes oil painting lessons at a painting class every week.","furigana":"はは|まいしゅう|かいがきょうしつ|あぶらえ|なら"}],"notes":"絵画 is the formal, objective word for \"painting\" as an art form; in everyday speech you simply say 絵: 絵を描く, 絵が好き. You encounter 絵画 in museums, catalogs and résumés: 西洋絵画, 日本の絵画, 絵画教室, 絵画展. Paintings are counted with 〜点 or 〜枚.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"作曲をしています。","romaji":"Sakkyoku o shite imasu.","german":"I am composing music.","furigana":"さっきょく"},{"japanese":"この歌は、作詞も作曲も彼女が一人でしました。","romaji":"Kono uta wa, sakushi mo sakkyoku mo kanojo ga hitori de shimashita.","german":"For this song, she wrote both the lyrics and the music all by herself.","furigana":"うた|さくし|さっきょく|かのじょ|ひとり"},{"japanese":"モーツァルトは五歳で作曲を始めたそうです。","romaji":"Moutsaruto wa gosai de sakkyoku o hajimeta sou desu.","german":"I hear Mozart began composing at the age of five.","furigana":"ごさい|さっきょく|はじ"}],"notes":"作曲 is the composing of music; as a する-verb: 曲を作曲する or simply 作曲する. For songs, a clear distinction is made between 作曲 \"music\", 作詞 \"lyrics\" and 編曲 \"arrangement\", as also written in song credits (作詞・作曲). The composer is called 作曲家; colloquially one also says 曲を作る.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 49"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ピアノを演奏します。","romaji":"Piano o ensō shimasu.","german":"I play the piano.","furigana":"えんそう"},{"japanese":"レストランでジャズの生演奏を聞きながら食事しました。","romaji":"Resutoran de jazu no nama-ensou o kikinagara shokuji shimashita.","german":"At the restaurant we ate while listening to live jazz.","furigana":"なまえんそう|き|しょくじ"},{"japanese":"演奏が終わると、会場から大きな拍手が起こりました。","romaji":"Ensou ga owaru to, kaijou kara ookina hakushu ga okorimashita.","german":"When the performance ended, loud applause rose from the hall.","furigana":"えんそう|お|かいじょう|おお|はくしゅ|お"}],"notes":"演奏 is the playing of a musical instrument, especially before an audience or at a higher level; as a transitive する-verb: ピアノを演奏する. In everyday speech, for individual instruments you say ピアノを弾く, 笛を吹く, 太鼓をたたく rather than this; 演奏 sounds more formal and fits all instruments. Typical: 演奏会 \"concert\", 生演奏 \"live music\", 演奏者 \"musician, performer\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"オーケストラを指揮します。","romaji":"Ōkesutora o shiki shimasu.","german":"I conduct the orchestra.","furigana":"しき"},{"japanese":"火事の現場では、隊長が消防士たちを指揮しました。","romaji":"Kaji no genba de wa, taichou ga shouboushitachi o shiki shimashita.","german":"At the scene of the fire, the captain commanded the firefighters.","furigana":"かじ|げんば|たいちょう|しょうぼうし|しき"},{"japanese":"合唱コンクールで、クラスの指揮者に選ばれました。","romaji":"Gasshou konkuuru de, kurasu no shikisha ni erabaremashita.","german":"At the choir contest, I was chosen as our class's conductor.","furigana":"がっしょう|しきしゃ|えら"}],"notes":"指揮 means \"leadership, command\" over a group and, specifically in music, \"conducting\". As a transitive する-verb: オーケストラを指揮する, 部隊を指揮する. Typical: 指揮者 \"conductor\", 〜の指揮で／〜の指揮のもとで \"under the direction of ...\", 指揮を執る \"to take command\". For everyday work instructions in the office you say 指示する \"to give instructions\" instead.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"美しい旋律です。","romaji":"Utsukushii senritsu desu.","german":"It is a beautiful melody.","furigana":"うつく|せんりつ"},{"japanese":"この曲は、ピアノの静かな旋律から始まります。","romaji":"Kono kyoku wa, piano no shizuka na senritsu kara hajimarimasu.","german":"This piece begins with a quiet piano melody.","furigana":"きょく|しず|せんりつ|はじ"},{"japanese":"合唱では、ソプラノが主旋律を歌います。","romaji":"Gasshou de wa, sopurano ga shusenritsu o utaimasu.","german":"In a choir, the sopranos sing the main melody.","furigana":"がっしょう|しゅせんりつ|うた"}],"notes":"旋律 is the elevated, technical word for \"melody\"; in everyday speech people mostly say メロディー. You find it in music class, in reviews and in literature: 美しい旋律, 旋律を奏でる \"to play a melody\", 主旋律 \"main melody\". Do not confuse it with the identically read 戦慄 \"shudder, horror\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"和音を弾きます。","romaji":"Waon o hikimasu.","german":"I play a chord.","furigana":"わおん|ひ"},{"japanese":"ピアノで左手は和音、右手はメロディーを弾きます。","romaji":"Piano de hidarite wa waon, migite wa merodii o hikimasu.","german":"On the piano, the left hand plays chords and the right hand plays the melody.","furigana":"ひだりて|わおん|みぎて|ひ"},{"japanese":"チームの中で不協和音が生まれています。","romaji":"Chiimu no naka de fukyouwaon ga umarete imasu.","german":"Discord is arising within the team.","furigana":"なか|ふきょうわおん|う"}],"notes":"和音 is a \"chord\" in music theory, that is, several tones sounding at the same time; 和 stands here for \"harmony\", not for \"Japanese\". Among musicians one often hears the loanword コード (コードを押さえる \"to play a chord\"), while 和音 is the term from school lessons and theory (三和音 \"triad\"). 不協和音 \"dissonance\" is also used figuratively for \"discord, disagreements\" in a group.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"拍子を取ります。","romaji":"Hyōshi o torimasu.","german":"I keep time.","furigana":"ひょうし|と"},{"japanese":"ワルツは三拍子の曲です。","romaji":"Warutsu wa sanbyoushi no kyoku desu.","german":"A waltz is a piece in triple time.","furigana":"さんびょうし|きょく"},{"japanese":"立ち上がった拍子に、机に頭をぶつけました。","romaji":"Tachiagatta hyoushi ni, tsukue ni atama o butsukemashita.","german":"As I stood up, I hit my head on the desk.","furigana":"た|あ|ひょうし|つくえ|あたま"}],"notes":"拍子 is \"time signature, meter\" in music (三拍子 \"triple time\", 四拍子 \"quadruple time\") and \"rhythm\": 手で拍子を取る \"to keep time with one's hands\". Also very common is 〜た拍子に \"just at the moment when ...\", usually with an unintended consequence: 転んだ拍子に眼鏡が落ちた. Related is 拍子抜け \"letdown because things turned out to be milder than expected\"; identically read but different is 表紙 \"book cover\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"俳句を詠みます。","romaji":"Haiku o yomimasu.","german":"I compose haiku.","furigana":"はいく|よ"},{"japanese":"俳句には季語を入れるのが普通です。","romaji":"Haiku ni wa kigo o ireru no ga futsuu desu.","german":"A haiku normally includes a seasonal word.","furigana":"はいく|きご|い|ふつう"},{"japanese":"授業で、夏をテーマに俳句を作りました。","romaji":"Jugyou de, natsu o teema ni haiku o tsukurimashita.","german":"In class, we wrote haiku on the theme of summer.","furigana":"じゅぎょう|なつ|はいく|つく"}],"notes":"俳句 is the shortest Japanese poem, with 5-7-5 morae, which traditionally contains a seasonal word (季語); famous poets are 松尾芭蕉 and 小林一茶. The typical verb is 詠む \"to compose (a poem)\", in daily speech also 俳句を作る; it is counted with 〜句 (一句詠む). A similarly structured but humorous poem without a seasonal word is called 川柳.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"短歌を詠みました。","romaji":"Tanka o yomimashita.","german":"I composed a tanka.","furigana":"たんか|よ"},{"japanese":"短歌は五・七・五・七・七の三十一音でできています。","romaji":"Tanka wa go, shichi, go, shichi, shichi no sanjuuichi-on de dekite imasu.","german":"A tanka consists of 31 syllables in the pattern 5-7-5-7-7.","furigana":"たんか|ご|しち|ご|しち|しち|さんじゅういちおん"},{"japanese":"祖母の短歌が新聞に載りました。","romaji":"Sobo no tanka ga shinbun ni norimashita.","german":"My grandmother's tanka was published in the newspaper.","furigana":"そぼ|たんか|しんぶん|の"}],"notes":"短歌 is the classical Japanese verse form with 5-7-5-7-7, that is, 31 morae, and thus longer than the 俳句; a seasonal word is not required. The form is already attested in the ancient collection 万葉集 and is still cultivated today in newspaper columns and contests. The verb is the same as for 俳句: 短歌を詠む or 作る; they are counted with 〜首 (一首).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"随筆を読んでいます。","romaji":"Zuihitsu o yonde imasu.","german":"I am reading an essay.","furigana":"ずいひつ|よ"},{"japanese":"「枕草子」は日本で最も古い随筆の一つです。","romaji":"'Makura no Soushi' wa Nihon de mottomo furui zuihitsu no hitotsu desu.","german":"\"The Pillow Book\" is one of the oldest essay works in Japan.","furigana":"|にほん|もっと|ふる|ずいひつ|ひと"},{"japanese":"作家の随筆を読むと、その人の毎日の生活が分かります。","romaji":"Sakka no zuihitsu o yomu to, sono hito no mainichi no seikatsu ga wakarimasu.","german":"Reading a writer's essays tells you a lot about their daily life.","furigana":"さっか|ずいひつ|よ|ひと|まいにち|せいかつ|わ"}],"notes":"随筆 is a freely written prose form in which the author records personal experiences, thoughts and observations; literally \"following the brush\" (随 \"to follow\", 筆 \"brush\"). Classic examples are 枕草子 and 徒然草. Today, in everyday speech people usually say エッセイ; 随筆 sounds more literary. A scholarly paper, by contrast, is 論文.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"丁寧語を使いましょう。","romaji":"Teineigo o tsukaimashō.","german":"Let's use polite language.","furigana":"ていねいご|つか"},{"japanese":"初めて会う人には、丁寧語で話したほうがいいです。","romaji":"Hajimete au hito ni wa, teineigo de hanashita hou ga ii desu.","german":"With people you meet for the first time, it is better to speak politely.","furigana":"はじ|あ|ひと|ていねいご|はな"},{"japanese":"「です」と「ます」は丁寧語です。","romaji":"'Desu' to 'masu' wa teineigo desu.","german":"\"desu\" and \"masu\" are polite language.","furigana":"ていねいご"}],"notes":"丁寧語 is the polite language with です／ます (and ございます), which makes the tone polite toward strangers, customers and superiors without elevating anyone or humbling oneself. It is one of the three main types of 敬語 alongside 尊敬語 and 謙譲語; sometimes 美化語 (お茶, ご飯) is counted as well. The counterpart is plain speech, 普通体, or colloquially タメ口.","levelNote":"Compound: 丁寧 (N4) + 語 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"尊敬語の使い方を学びます。","romaji":"Sonkeigo no tsukaikata o manabimasu.","german":"I am learning how to use honorific language.","furigana":"そんけいご|つか|かた|まな"},{"japanese":"「いらっしゃる」は「いる」の尊敬語です。","romaji":"'Irassharu' wa 'iru' no sonkeigo desu.","german":"\"irassharu\" is the honorific form of \"iru\".","furigana":"そんけいご"},{"japanese":"お客様に自分の会社の社長のことを話すとき、尊敬語を使ってはいけません。","romaji":"Okyakusama ni jibun no kaisha no shachou no koto o hanasu toki, sonkeigo o tsukatte wa ikemasen.","german":"When talking to customers about the president of your own company, you must not use honorific language.","furigana":"きゃくさま|じぶん|かいしゃ|しゃちょう|はな|そんけいご|つか"}],"notes":"尊敬語 is honorific language: it elevates the actions of other people (customers, superiors, teachers), e.g. いらっしゃる instead of いる／行く／来る, 召し上がる instead of 食べる, おっしゃる instead of 言う, or regularly with お〜になる and 〜られる. Important: for your own actions, and toward outsiders also for your own company, you never use 尊敬語 but 謙譲語.","levelNote":"Compound: 尊敬 (N3) + 語 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"謙譲語で話しました。","romaji":"Kenjōgo de hanashimashita.","german":"I spoke in humble language.","furigana":"けんじょうご|はな"},{"japanese":"「伺う」は「行く」や「聞く」の謙譲語です。","romaji":"'Ukagau' wa 'iku' ya 'kiku' no kenjougo desu.","german":"\"ukagau\" is the humble form of \"iku\" and \"kiku\".","furigana":"うかが|い|き|けんじょうご"},{"japanese":"面接では、謙譲語を正しく使えるかどうかも見られます。","romaji":"Mensetsu de wa, kenjougo o tadashiku tsukaeru ka dou ka mo miraremasu.","german":"In job interviews, they also look at whether you can use humble language correctly.","furigana":"めんせつ|けんじょうご|ただ|つか|み"}],"notes":"謙譲語 is humble language: you present your own actions (or those of your own group) modestly and thereby indirectly elevate the other person, e.g. 参る／伺う instead of 行く, いただく instead of もらう／食べる, 申す instead of 言う, regularly with お〜する. A typical mistake is to use 謙譲語 for the actions of others (wrong: 先生が申しました, right: 先生がおっしゃいました); there 尊敬語 belongs.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"語彙を増やしたいです。","romaji":"Goi o fuyashitai desu.","german":"I want to increase my vocabulary.","furigana":"ごい|ふ"},{"japanese":"彼女は語彙が豊富で、話がとても分かりやすいです。","romaji":"Kanojo wa goi ga houfu de, hanashi ga totemo wakariyasui desu.","german":"She has a rich vocabulary and explains things very clearly.","furigana":"かのじょ|ごい|ほうふ|はなし|わ"},{"japanese":"試験の前に、語彙の問題をたくさん練習しました。","romaji":"Shiken no mae ni, goi no mondai o takusan renshuu shimashita.","german":"Before the exam, I practiced a lot of vocabulary questions.","furigana":"しけん|まえ|ごい|もんだい|れんしゅう"}],"notes":"語彙 is the vocabulary of a person or language as a whole, not an individual word (that would be 単語 or 言葉). Typical: 語彙が豊富だ \"to have a rich vocabulary\", 語彙が少ない, 語彙を増やす, 語彙力 \"vocabulary skills\" (jokingly also 語彙力がない, when one is so excited that one can't find words). Because the kanji 彙 is rare, the spelling 語い is also seen.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"助詞の使い方が難しいです。","romaji":"Joshi no tsukaikata ga muzukashii desu.","german":"Die Verwendung von Partikeln ist schwer.","furigana":"じょし|つか|かた|むずか"},{"japanese":"「は」と「が」の違いは、助詞の中でも特に難しいです。","romaji":"'Wa' to 'ga' no chigai wa, joshi no naka de mo toku ni muzukashii desu.","german":"Der Unterschied zwischen „wa“ und „ga“ ist unter den Partikeln besonders schwierig.","furigana":"ちが|じょし|なか|とく|むずか"},{"japanese":"先生に作文の助詞の間違いを直してもらいました。","romaji":"Sensei ni sakubun no joshi no machigai o naoshite moraimashita.","german":"Der Lehrer hat mir die Partikelfehler in meinem Aufsatz korrigiert.","furigana":"せんせい|さくぶん|じょし|まちが|なお"}],"notes":"助詞 sind die Partikeln wie は, が, を, に, で, の, die hinter Wörter treten und deren Rolle im Satz anzeigen; im Deutschen entsprechen sie teils Präpositionen, teils Fällen. Typisch: 助詞を間違える „eine Partikel falsch verwenden“, 格助詞 „Kasuspartikel“, 終助詞 „Satzendpartikel“ (ね, よ). Nicht verwechseln mit dem gleich gelesenen 女子 „Mädchen, Frauen“ oder mit 助動詞 „Hilfsverb“ (z. B. たい, れる).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"動詞を活用します。","romaji":"Dōshi o katsuyō shimasu.","german":"I conjugate the verb.","furigana":"どうし|かつよう"},{"japanese":"日本語の動詞は文の最後に来ます。","romaji":"Nihongo no doushi wa bun no saigo ni kimasu.","german":"In Japanese, the verb comes at the end of the sentence.","furigana":"にほんご|どうし|ぶん|さいご|き"},{"japanese":"この動詞は自動詞ですか、他動詞ですか。","romaji":"Kono doushi wa jidoushi desu ka, tadoushi desu ka.","german":"Is this verb intransitive or transitive?","furigana":"どうし|じどうし|たどうし"}],"notes":"動詞 is the part of speech \"verb\". In Japanese classes a distinction is made between 五段動詞 (group 1, e.g. 書く), 一段動詞 (group 2, e.g. 食べる) and the irregular する and 来る; also 自動詞 \"intransitive verb\" and 他動詞 \"transitive verb\". Typical: 動詞を活用する \"to conjugate a verb\", 動詞のて形 \"the te-form of a verb\". The identically read 同志 \"comrade\" exists.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"形容詞を覚えます。","romaji":"Keiyōshi o oboemasu.","german":"I am learning the adjectives.","furigana":"けいようし|おぼ"},{"japanese":"「きれい」は「い」で終わりますが、い形容詞ではありません。","romaji":"'Kirei' wa 'i' de owarimasu ga, i-keiyoushi de wa arimasen.","german":"\"Kirei\" ends in \"i\", but it is not an i-adjective.","furigana":"お|けいようし"},{"japanese":"形容詞をたくさん使うと、文章が生き生きします。","romaji":"Keiyoushi o takusan tsukau to, bunshou ga ikiiki shimasu.","german":"Using plenty of adjectives makes a text vivid.","furigana":"けいようし|つか|ぶんしょう|い|い"}],"notes":"In Japanese school grammar, 形容詞 is the adjective ending in 〜い (高い, 新しい), which itself conjugates (高くない, 高かった). In classes for Japanese as a foreign language, people distinguish い形容詞 and な形容詞; the latter is called 形容動詞 in native Japanese grammar (静かだ, きれいだ). Typical: 形容詞を活用する, 形容詞の過去形 \"past form of the adjective\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"名詞の数が多いです。","romaji":"Meishi no kazu ga ōi desu.","german":"There are many nouns.","furigana":"めいし|かず|おお"},{"japanese":"「こと」を使うと、動詞を名詞のように使えます。","romaji":"'Koto' o tsukau to, doushi o meishi no you ni tsukaemasu.","german":"With \"koto\" you can use a verb like a noun.","furigana":"つか|どうし|めいし|つか"},{"japanese":"日本語の名詞には、ふつう複数形がありません。","romaji":"Nihongo no meishi ni wa, futsuu fukusuukei ga arimasen.","german":"Japanese nouns normally have no plural form.","furigana":"にほんご|めいし|ふくすうけい"}],"notes":"名詞 is the part of speech \"noun\", e.g. 本, 学校, 自由. Japanese nouns have neither articles nor grammatical gender and mostly no plural; they are linked with の (日本の車). Typical: 固有名詞 \"proper noun\", 名詞化 \"nominalization\" (with の or こと). Caution: the identically read 名刺 \"business card\" exists.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"副詞を正しく使います。","romaji":"Fukushi o tadashiku tsukaimasu.","german":"I use adverbs correctly.","furigana":"ふくし|ただ|つか"},{"japanese":"「早く」は形容詞「早い」から作られた副詞です。","romaji":"'Hayaku' wa keiyoushi 'hayai' kara tsukurareta fukushi desu.","german":"\"Hayaku\" is an adverb formed from the adjective \"hayai\".","furigana":"はや|けいようし|はや|つく|ふくし"},{"japanese":"副詞の位置を変えると、文の意味が少し変わることがあります。","romaji":"Fukushi no ichi o kaeru to, bun no imi ga sukoshi kawaru koto ga arimasu.","german":"Changing the position of an adverb can slightly change the meaning of a sentence.","furigana":"ふくし|いち|か|ぶん|いみ|すこ|か"}],"notes":"副詞 is the part of speech \"adverb\", which modifies verbs, adjectives or whole sentences: とても, ゆっくり, もう, たぶん. Many adverbs are formed from adjectives: 早い → 早く, 静か → 静かに. Some require a particular sentence ending, e.g. 全然 with negation, たぶん with でしょう, まるで with ようだ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"接続詞で文をつなぎます。","romaji":"Setsuzokushi de bun o tsunagimasu.","german":"I connect sentences with conjunctions.","furigana":"せつぞくし|ぶん"},{"japanese":"「しかし」は作文でよく使われる接続詞です。","romaji":"'Shikashi' wa sakubun de yoku tsukawareru setsuzokushi desu.","german":"「しかし」 is a conjunction often used in essays.","furigana":"さくぶん|つか|せつぞくし"},{"japanese":"接続詞が多すぎると、文章が読みにくくなります。","romaji":"Setsuzokushi ga oosugiru to, bunshou ga yominikuku narimasu.","german":"Too many conjunctions make a text hard to read.","furigana":"せつぞくし|おお|ぶんしょう|よ"}],"notes":"接続詞 are sentence-connecting words that in Japanese usually stand at the beginning of the new sentence: しかし, だから, それに, ところで. Particles such as から, けど, のに, which connect clauses directly, are grammatically called 接続助詞, not 接続詞. In writing, pay attention to style: しかし is literary and formal, でも is more spoken.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"慣用句を覚えましょう。","romaji":"Kan'yōku o oboemashō.","german":"Let's learn idioms.","furigana":"かんようく|おぼ"},{"japanese":"「顔が広い」は、知り合いが多いという意味の慣用句です。","romaji":"'Kao ga hiroi' wa, shiriai ga ooi to iu imi no kan'youku desu.","german":"「顔が広い」 is an idiom meaning that one knows many people.","furigana":"かお|ひろ|し|あ|おお|いみ|かんようく"},{"japanese":"慣用句を上手に使うと、日本語が自然に聞こえます。","romaji":"Kan'youku o jouzu ni tsukau to, Nihongo ga shizen ni kikoemasu.","german":"If you use idioms skillfully, your Japanese sounds natural.","furigana":"かんようく|じょうず|つか|にほんご|しぜん|き"}],"notes":"慣用句 is a fixed expression whose meaning does not follow from its individual words, for example 顔が広い \"to know many people\", 手を焼く \"to have a hard time with something\", 猫の手も借りたい \"to be extremely busy\". Many 慣用句 contain body parts such as 目, 口, 手, 足, 腹. To be distinguished are ことわざ \"proverb\", which expresses a piece of life wisdom, and 四字熟語 \"four-kanji expression\".","levelNote":"Compound: 慣用 (N1) + 句 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本の諺を教えてください。","romaji":"Nihon no kotowaza o oshiete kudasai.","german":"Please tell me some Japanese proverbs.","furigana":"にほん|ことわざ|おし"},{"japanese":"「猿も木から落ちる」という諺があります。","romaji":"'Saru mo ki kara ochiru' to iu kotowaza ga arimasu.","german":"There is a proverb \"Even monkeys fall from trees\".","furigana":"さる|き|お|ことわざ"},{"japanese":"諺にもあるように、急がば回れですよ。","romaji":"Kotowaza ni mo aru you ni, isogaba maware desu yo.","german":"As the proverb says, haste makes waste.","furigana":"ことわざ|いそ|まわ"}],"notes":"諺 is a proverb expressing a general piece of life wisdom or a lesson, e.g. 猿も木から落ちる \"even monkeys fall from trees\", meaning \"even experts make mistakes\". The kanji 諺 is rare; ことわざ is usually written in kana. Typical frames: 〜という諺がある, 諺にもあるように \"as the proverb says\". Unlike an 慣用句 \"idiom\", a 諺 is a whole statement with a lesson.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"四字熟語を勉強しています。","romaji":"Yojijukugo o benkyō shite imasu.","german":"I am studying four-character idioms.","furigana":"よじじゅくご|べんきょう"},{"japanese":"「一石二鳥」は、一つのことで二つの得をするという意味の四字熟語です。","romaji":"'Isseki nichou' wa, hitotsu no koto de futatsu no toku o suru to iu imi no yojijukugo desu.","german":"\"isseki nicho\" is a four-character idiom meaning to gain two benefits from one action.","furigana":"いっせきにちょう|ひと|ふた|とく|いみ|よじじゅくご"},{"japanese":"私の好きな四字熟語は「一期一会」です。","romaji":"Watashi no suki na yojijukugo wa 'ichigo ichie' desu.","german":"My favorite four-character idiom is \"ichigo ichie\".","furigana":"わたし|す|よじじゅくご|いちごいちえ"}],"notes":"四字熟語 are fixed expressions made of four kanji, often of Chinese origin, that sum up a lesson or situation concisely: 一石二鳥 \"kill two birds with one stone\", 一期一会 \"every encounter is unique\", 自業自得 \"you reap what you sow\". They are often used in speeches, essays and as life mottos and sound educated. 熟語 on its own refers to any kanji compound of two or more characters.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"誤りを訂正します。","romaji":"Ayamari o teisei shimasu.","german":"I correct the error.","furigana":"あやま|ていせい"},{"japanese":"先日のお知らせに誤りがありました。申し訳ございません。","romaji":"Senjitsu no oshirase ni ayamari ga arimashita. Moushiwake gozaimasen.","german":"There was an error in our recent notice. We apologize.","furigana":"せんじつ|し|あやま|もう|わけ"},{"japanese":"自分の判断の誤りを認めるのは難しいです。","romaji":"Jibun no handan no ayamari o mitomeru no wa muzukashii desu.","german":"It is hard to admit an error in your own judgment.","furigana":"じぶん|はんだん|あやま|みと|むずか"}],"notes":"誤り is the written, more formal word for \"mistake, error\", especially in texts, data and official notices: 記載に誤りがある \"the entry contains an error\", 誤りを訂正する, 誤りを認める. In conversation people mostly say 間違い. The related verb is 誤る \"to err\"; do not confuse it with the identically read 謝る \"to apologize\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"誤字を訂正しました。","romaji":"Goji o teisei shimashita.","german":"I corrected the typo.","furigana":"ごじ|ていせい"},{"japanese":"先ほどの発言を訂正させてください。","romaji":"Sakihodo no hatsugen o teisei sasete kudasai.","german":"Let me correct my earlier statement.","furigana":"さき|はつげん|ていせい"},{"japanese":"書類を訂正するときは、二本線を引いて印鑑を押してください。","romaji":"Shorui o teisei suru toki wa, nihonsen o hiite inkan o oshite kudasai.","german":"When you correct a document, draw two lines through it and put your seal next to it.","furigana":"しょるい|ていせい|にほんせん|ひ|いんかん|お"}],"notes":"訂正 is correcting wrong information in texts, documents or statements; as a transitive する-verb: 誤字を訂正する, 発言を訂正する. Typical: 訂正印 \"correction seal\", 訂正してお詫びします \"we correct this and apologize\" (newspapers, news). Compared with 修正 \"revision, touching up (plans, data, images)\", 訂正 stresses setting an error right; in everyday speech one simply says 直す.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"補足説明をします。","romaji":"Hosoku setsumei o shimasu.","german":"I'll give a supplementary explanation.","furigana":"ほそくせつめい"},{"japanese":"今の説明に少し補足させていただきます。","romaji":"Ima no setsumei ni sukoshi hosoku sasete itadakimasu.","german":"Let me add a little to the explanation just given.","furigana":"いま|せつめい|すこ|ほそく"},{"japanese":"詳しいデータは補足資料をご覧ください。","romaji":"Kuwashii deeta wa hosoku shiryou o goran kudasai.","german":"For detailed data, please see the supplementary materials.","furigana":"くわ|ほそくしりょう|らん"}],"notes":"補足 is an addition to something already said or written, supplying missing points; as a transitive する-verb: 説明を補足する. Very common in meetings: 補足させていただきます \"may I add something briefly\", 補足説明, 補足資料. Compared with 追加 \"adding (things, quantities)\", 補足 refers to information and explanations.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"内容を要約します。","romaji":"Naiyō o yōyaku shimasu.","german":"I will summarize the content.","furigana":"ないよう|ようやく"},{"japanese":"この記事を二百字以内で要約してください。","romaji":"Kono kiji o nihyakuji inai de youyaku shite kudasai.","german":"Please summarize this article in 200 characters or less.","furigana":"きじ|にひゃくじいない|ようやく"},{"japanese":"会議で話したことを要約して、メールで送りました。","romaji":"Kaigi de hanashita koto o youyaku shite, meeru de okurimashita.","german":"I summarized what was discussed in the meeting and sent it by email.","furigana":"かいぎ|はな|ようやく|おく"}],"notes":"要約 is summarizing the essentials of a text, talk or conversation in shorter form; as a transitive する-verb: 記事を要約する. Typical: 〜字以内で要約する \"to summarize in no more than ... characters\" (a common exam task), 要約文. Compared with まとめ (more everyday), 要約 is more objective; 概要 is more of a brief overview of a matter. The identically read ようやく \"finally\" exists.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"概要を説明します。","romaji":"Gaiyō o setsumei shimasu.","german":"I will explain the outline.","furigana":"がいよう|せつめい"},{"japanese":"プロジェクトの概要は、この資料にまとめてあります。","romaji":"Purojekuto no gaiyou wa, kono shiryou ni matomete arimasu.","german":"The project overview is summarized in this document.","furigana":"がいよう|しりょう"},{"japanese":"ニュースで事件の概要が報道されました。","romaji":"Nyuusu de jiken no gaiyou ga houdou saremashita.","german":"The outline of the incident was reported on the news.","furigana":"じけん|がいよう|ほうどう"}],"notes":"概要 is the rough overview of the essential points of a plan, project, event or text: 計画の概要, 事件の概要, 概要を説明する. In documents it often appears as a heading meaning \"overview\", and in academic papers it stands for the abstract. Unlike 要約 \"summary of an existing text\", 概要 describes the basic outline of a matter; the counterpart is 詳細 \"details\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"詳細をお知らせします。","romaji":"Shōsai o oshirase shimasu.","german":"We will let you know the details.","furigana":"しょうさい|し"},{"japanese":"詳細はホームページをご覧ください。","romaji":"Shousai wa hoomupeeji o goran kudasai.","german":"Please see the website for details.","furigana":"しょうさい|らん"},{"japanese":"事故の詳細はまだ分かっていません。","romaji":"Jiko no shousai wa mada wakatte imasen.","german":"The details of the accident are not yet known.","furigana":"じこ|しょうさい|わ"}],"notes":"詳細 refers to the \"details\" of a matter and sounds matter-of-fact and formal; typical in announcements and business: 詳細をお知らせします, 詳細はホームページをご覧ください, 事故の詳細. It also works as a na-adjective or adverb: 詳細な説明, 詳細に調べる. In everyday speech one more often says 詳しい (\"detailed, thorough\") or 詳しく. The counterpart is 概略 or 概要 (\"rough overview\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"概略を説明します。","romaji":"Gairyaku o setsumei shimasu.","german":"I'll explain the outline.","furigana":"がいりゃく|せつめい"},{"japanese":"計画の概略を一枚の紙にまとめました。","romaji":"Keikaku no gairyaku o ichimai no kami ni matomemashita.","german":"I summarized the outline of the plan on one sheet of paper.","furigana":"けいかく|がいりゃく|いちまい|かみ"},{"japanese":"詳細は後で話しますが、まず概略だけ聞いてください。","romaji":"Shousai wa ato de hanashimasu ga, mazu gairyaku dake kiite kudasai.","german":"I'll talk about the details later, but first just listen to the outline.","furigana":"しょうさい|あと|はな|がいりゃく|き"}],"notes":"概略 is the \"rough outline\" or \"main points\" of a plan, project or event, the counterpart to 詳細. It is a rather literary word; typical are 概略を説明する, 計画の概略, 概略図 (\"overview sketch\"). In business documents 概要 is even more common; in conversation one usually says だいたいの内容 or あらまし. A summary of the most important points would rather be 要点.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"要点をまとめます。","romaji":"Yōten o matomemasu.","german":"I will sum up the main points.","furigana":"ようてん"},{"japanese":"時間がないので、要点だけ話してください。","romaji":"Jikan ga nai node, youten dake hanashite kudasai.","german":"I don't have much time, so please tell me only the main points.","furigana":"じかん|ようてん|はな"},{"japanese":"先生の話は要点がはっきりしていて分かりやすい。","romaji":"Sensei no hanashi wa youten ga hakkiri shite ite wakariyasui.","german":"The teacher's talk has clear main points and is easy to understand.","furigana":"せんせい|はなし|ようてん|わ"}],"notes":"要点 is the \"main point\", i.e. the essential content of a speech, text or explanation. Typical combinations: 要点をまとめる \"to summarize the main points\", 要点を押さえる \"to grasp the essentials\", 要点だけ話す, 話の要点. In casual conversation people often say ポイント. 核心 is narrower and means the actual core of a problem, whereas 概略 is the rough overall overview.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"問題の核心に迫ります。","romaji":"Mondai no kakushin ni semarimasu.","german":"I am getting to the heart of the problem.","furigana":"もんだい|かくしん|せま"},{"japanese":"彼の質問は核心を突いていた。","romaji":"Kare no shitsumon wa kakushin o tsuite ita.","german":"His question hit the core of the matter.","furigana":"かれ|しつもん|かくしん|つ"},{"japanese":"彼女はなかなか話の核心に触れてくれない。","romaji":"Kanojo wa nakanaka hanashi no kakushin ni furete kurenai.","german":"She won't touch on the core of the story.","furigana":"かのじょ|はなし|かくしん|ふ"}],"notes":"核心 is the innermost \"core\" of a matter, especially of a problem or question. Set phrases: 核心に迫る \"to get closer to the core\", 核心を突く \"to hit the nail on the head\", 核心に触れる \"to touch on the core\", 問題の核心. For a spatial center you don't use 核心 but 中心. 要点 refers more to the most important points of a presentation, while 核心 is the decisive thing around which everything revolves.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本文化の真髄を学びます。","romaji":"Nihon bunka no shinzui o manabimasu.","german":"I study the quintessence of Japanese culture.","furigana":"にほんぶんか|しんずい|まな"},{"japanese":"この料理には和食の真髄が詰まっている。","romaji":"Kono ryouri ni wa washoku no shinzui ga tsumatte iru.","german":"This dish is packed with the essence of Japanese cuisine.","furigana":"りょうり|わしょく|しんずい|つ"},{"japanese":"先生は三十年かけて茶道の真髄を極めた。","romaji":"Sensei wa sanjuunen kakete sadou no shinzui o kiwameta.","german":"My teacher spent thirty years mastering the essence of the tea ceremony.","furigana":"せんせい|さんじゅうねん|さどう|しんずい|きわ"}],"notes":"真髄 (also written 神髄) is the \"quintessence\", the innermost essence of an art, doctrine or culture. It is an elevated, rather written-language word and almost always appears with の: 日本文化の真髄, 茶道の真髄. Typical verbs are 学ぶ, 味わう, 極める (\"to master completely\"). 本質 is the more neutral, analytical word for \"essence\"; 核心 means the core of a problem, not of an art.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"趣旨を説明します。","romaji":"Shushi o setsumei shimasu.","german":"I'll explain the purpose.","furigana":"しゅし|せつめい"},{"japanese":"この会の趣旨に賛成して参加しました。","romaji":"Kono kai no shushi ni sansei shite sanka shimashita.","german":"I took part because I support the aims of this group.","furigana":"かい|しゅし|さんせい|さんか"},{"japanese":"彼は「もっと休みが必要だ」という趣旨の発言をした。","romaji":"Kare wa \"motto yasumi ga hitsuyou da\" to iu shushi no hatsugen o shita.","german":"He said something to the effect that \"more time off is needed.\"","furigana":"かれ|やす|ひつよう|しゅし|はつげん"}],"notes":"趣旨 is the \"point and purpose\", or the aim, of an event, plan or statement. Typical: 会の趣旨を説明する, 趣旨に賛同する \"to support the aim\", 〜という趣旨の発言 \"a statement to the effect that ...\". The word is formal and often appears in speeches and writing. 目的 is the general word for \"goal\", 意図 a person's individual intention.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"意図が伝わりません。","romaji":"Ito ga tsutawarimasen.","german":"The intention doesn't come across.","furigana":"いと|つた"},{"japanese":"彼の質問の意図が分からなかった。","romaji":"Kare no shitsumon no ito ga wakaranakatta.","german":"I didn't understand what he was getting at with his question.","furigana":"かれ|しつもん|いと|わ"},{"japanese":"誰かを傷つける意図はありませんでした。","romaji":"Dareka o kizutsukeru ito wa arimasen deshita.","german":"I didn't intend to hurt anyone.","furigana":"だれ|きず|いと"}],"notes":"意図 is the conscious \"intention\" behind an action or statement; as a する-verb it is transitive: 〜を意図する. Common are 意図が伝わる \"the intention comes across\", 質問の意図, 〜する意図はない and the adjective 意図的 (\"intentional\"). In everyday speech intentions are mostly expressed with つもり (傷つけるつもりはなかった); 意図 sounds more matter-of-fact and analytical. 趣旨, by contrast, is the purpose of a thing, not of a person.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"高い志を持っています。","romaji":"Takai kokorozashi o motte imasu.","german":"He has high aspirations.","furigana":"たか|こころざし|も"},{"japanese":"医者になるという志を最後まで捨てなかった。","romaji":"Isha ni naru to iu kokorozashi o saigo made sutenakatta.","german":"He never gave up his resolve to become a doctor.","furigana":"いしゃ|こころざし|さいご|す"},{"japanese":"同じ志を持つ仲間と会社を作った。","romaji":"Onaji kokorozashi o motsu nakama to kaisha o tsukutta.","german":"I started a company with like-minded friends.","furigana":"おな|こころざし|も|なかま|かいしゃ|つく"}],"notes":"志 is a lofty, firmly held life goal or \"resolution\", often with an idealistic overtone: 志を持つ, 志を立てる \"to set oneself a goal\", 高い志, 志を果たす, 同じ志を持つ仲間 \"like-minded companions\". The related verb is 志す (医者を志す). More rarely, お志 means a \"kind gesture\" or a thank-you gift. 夢 is more general and less serious, 目標 a concrete, measurable goal.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"強い願望があります。","romaji":"Tsuyoi ganbō ga arimasu.","german":"I have a strong desire.","furigana":"つよ|がんぼう"},{"japanese":"彼女は結婚願望があまりないそうです。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kekkon ganbou ga amari nai sou desu.","german":"I hear she doesn't have much desire to get married.","furigana":"かのじょ|けっこんがんぼう"},{"japanese":"「有名になりたい」という願望は誰にでもある。","romaji":"\"Yuumei ni naritai\" to iu ganbou wa dare ni demo aru.","german":"Everyone has the desire to \"become famous\".","furigana":"ゆうめい|がんぼう|だれ"}],"notes":"願望 is a (often inner, deep-seated) \"wish\" or longing. Typical: 願望がある, 強い願望, 〜たいという願望, and compounds such as 結婚願望 \"desire to marry\" or 変身願望. 希望 is more neutral and often refers to concrete plans (希望の大学), while 願い sounds softer and more personal. 願望 is often used in psychological or analytical contexts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本に憧れています。","romaji":"Nihon ni akogarete imasu.","german":"I long for Japan.","furigana":"にほん|あこが"},{"japanese":"憧れの先輩と話せて、とてもうれしかった。","romaji":"Akogare no senpai to hanasete, totemo ureshikatta.","german":"I was very happy to talk with the senior student I admire.","furigana":"あこが|せんぱい|はな"},{"japanese":"子どものころ、パイロットは私の憧れの仕事だった。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, pairotto wa watashi no akogare no shigoto datta.","german":"When I was a child, being a pilot was my dream job.","furigana":"こ|わたし|あこが|しごと"}],"notes":"憧れ is a rapturous \"longing\" or admiration for something unattainable or for a role model. Very common is 憧れの + noun: 憧れの先輩, 憧れの職業 \"dream job\". The verb 憧れる is intransitive and takes に: 日本に憧れる. Expressions: 憧れを抱く, 憧れの的. Unlike 羨ましい \"envious\", 憧れ is purely positive; 尊敬, by contrast, stresses sober respect.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"過去に執着しないでください。","romaji":"Kako ni shūchaku shinaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't cling to the past.","furigana":"かこ|しゅうちゃく"},{"japanese":"彼はお金に強い執着がある。","romaji":"Kare wa okane ni tsuyoi shuuchaku ga aru.","german":"He has a strong attachment to money.","furigana":"かれ|かね|つよ|しゅうちゃく"},{"japanese":"勝ち負けに執着しすぎると、試合が楽しくなくなる。","romaji":"Kachimake ni shuuchaku shisugiru to, shiai ga tanoshiku naku naru.","german":"If you're too fixated on winning or losing, the game stops being fun.","furigana":"か|ま|しゅうちゃく|しあい|たの"}],"notes":"執着 is a compulsive \"clinging\" to something, usually negative: 〜に執着する (intransitive する-verb with に), 強い執着がある, 執着心. Typical objects are money, power, the past or an ex-partner. In contrast, こだわる can also be positive (\"to care about\": 素材にこだわる). A rarer reading is しゅうじゃく.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"政治に無関心です。","romaji":"Seiji ni mukanshin desu.","german":"I am indifferent to politics.","furigana":"せいじ|むかんしん"},{"japanese":"彼は周りの人に無関心な態度をとる。","romaji":"Kare wa mawari no hito ni mukanshin na taido o toru.","german":"He takes an indifferent attitude toward the people around him.","furigana":"かれ|まわ|ひと|むかんしん|たいど"},{"japanese":"子どもの勉強に無関心な親も少なくない。","romaji":"Kodomo no benkyou ni mukanshin na oya mo sukunaku nai.","german":"Not a few parents are indifferent to their children's studies.","furigana":"こ|べんきょう|むかんしん|おや|すく"}],"notes":"無関心 (\"indifference, lack of interest\") is a noun and a na-adjective: 〜に無関心だ, 無関心な態度, 政治的無関心. The counterpart is 関心がある \"to be interested\". In everyday speech people often simply say 興味がない; 無関心 sounds more objective and often critical, for example when it concerns politics, the environment or fellow human beings.","levelNote":"Compound: 無 (N3) + 関心 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"好奇心が旺盛です。","romaji":"Kōkishin ga ōsei desu.","german":"I am very curious.","furigana":"こうきしん|おうせい"},{"japanese":"好奇心から、その箱を開けてしまった。","romaji":"Koukishin kara, sono hako o akete shimatta.","german":"Out of curiosity, I opened that box.","furigana":"こうきしん|はこ|あ"},{"japanese":"子どもの好奇心を大切にしたい。","romaji":"Kodomo no koukishin o taisetsu ni shitai.","german":"I want to value children's curiosity.","furigana":"こ|こうきしん|たいせつ"}],"notes":"好奇心 is \"curiosity\" as a trait or driving force. Typical: 好奇心が強い, 好奇心が旺盛だ \"to be very eager to learn\", 好奇心から \"out of curiosity\", 好奇心をくすぐる \"to arouse curiosity\". The word is mostly positive; by contrast 好奇の目 \"curious, staring looks\" is derogatory. For \"I'm eager to see / interested\" people say 興味がある or 気になる in everyday speech.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"真理を探求します。","romaji":"Shinri o tankyū shimasu.","german":"I seek the truth.","furigana":"しんり|たんきゅう"},{"japanese":"彼は理想の味を探求して、世界中を旅した。","romaji":"Kare wa risou no aji o tankyuu shite, sekaijuu o tabi shita.","german":"He traveled all over the world in search of his ideal taste.","furigana":"かれ|りそう|あじ|たんきゅう|せかいじゅう|たび"},{"japanese":"知識の探求に終わりはない。","romaji":"Chishiki no tankyuu ni owari wa nai.","german":"There is no end to the pursuit of knowledge.","furigana":"ちしき|たんきゅう|お"}],"notes":"探求 is the \"search and striving\" for something desirable (knowledge, truth, flavor, happiness); as a transitive する-verb: 〜を探求する. Note: the identically read 探究 stresses scientific investigation and getting to the bottom of things (学問の探究, 真理の探究); 探求 stresses the searching. The similar 追求 means \"to strive for\" (利益を追求する). The word is rather literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"幸福を追求します。","romaji":"Kōfuku o tsuikyū shimasu.","german":"I pursue happiness.","furigana":"こうふく|ついきゅう"},{"japanese":"会社は利益だけを追求してはいけない。","romaji":"Kaisha wa rieki dake o tsuikyuu shite wa ikenai.","german":"A company must not pursue only profit.","furigana":"かいしゃ|りえき|ついきゅう"},{"japanese":"このカメラは使いやすさを徹底的に追求して作られた。","romaji":"Kono kamera wa tsukaiyasusa o tetteiteki ni tsuikyuu shite tsukurareta.","german":"This camera was made with a thorough pursuit of ease of use.","furigana":"つか|てっていてき|ついきゅう|つく"}],"notes":"追求 is the persistent \"pursuit of\" a goal or ideal; as a transitive する-verb: 利益を追求する, 幸福を追求する, 理想を追求する. Beware of the homophones: 追及 means \"to hold accountable, press for answers\" (責任を追及する), and 追究 \"to investigate thoroughly\" (真理を追究する). 追求 sounds objective and is often used in business and advertising (品質を追求する).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"苦悩に満ちた表情です。","romaji":"Kunō ni michita hyōjō desu.","german":"He has an expression full of anguish.","furigana":"くのう|み|ひょうじょう"},{"japanese":"彼は家族と仕事のどちらを選ぶか、長い間苦悩した。","romaji":"Kare wa kazoku to shigoto no dochira o erabu ka, nagai aida kunou shita.","german":"He agonized for a long time over whether to choose his family or his work.","furigana":"かれ|かぞく|しごと|えら|なが|あいだくのう"},{"japanese":"その小説は若者の苦悩を描いている。","romaji":"Sono shousetsu wa wakamono no kunou o egaite iru.","german":"That novel depicts the anguish of young people.","furigana":"しょうせつ|わかもの|くのう|えが"}],"notes":"苦悩 is deep mental \"suffering\" or inner struggle; the word is literary and often appears in novels, biographies and the news. Typical: 苦悩する (intransitive), 苦悩に満ちた表情, 苦悩の日々, 若者の苦悩. In everyday speech people say 悩む or 悩み; 苦しみ is more general and can also be physical, whereas 苦悩 is always mental.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"安堵しました。","romaji":"Ando shimashita.","german":"I was relieved.","furigana":"あんど"},{"japanese":"全員無事だと聞いて、安堵のため息をついた。","romaji":"Zen'in buji da to kiite, ando no tameiki o tsuita.","german":"When I heard that everyone was safe, I breathed a sigh of relief.","furigana":"ぜんいんぶじ|き|あんど|いき"},{"japanese":"試験に合格した息子の顔に安堵の表情が浮かんだ。","romaji":"Shiken ni goukaku shita musuko no kao ni ando no hyoujou ga ukanda.","german":"A look of relief appeared on the face of my son, who had passed the exam.","furigana":"しけん|ごうかく|むすこ|かお|あんど|ひょうじょう|う"}],"notes":"安堵 is the \"relief\" felt when a worry disappears; as an intransitive する-verb: 安堵する. Typical combinations: 安堵の表情, 安堵のため息をつく \"to breathe a sigh of relief\", with 胸をなで下ろす as an idiomatic parallel. The word is literary and often appears in the news; in conversation people say ほっとする or 安心する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"感動しました。","romaji":"Kandō shimashita.","german":"I was moved.","furigana":"かんどう"},{"japanese":"その映画を見て、涙が出るほど感動した。","romaji":"Sono eiga o mite, namida ga deru hodo kandou shita.","german":"I was so moved by that movie that tears came out.","furigana":"えいが|み|なみだ|で|かんどう"},{"japanese":"選手たちのプレーは多くの人に感動を与えた。","romaji":"Senshutachi no puree wa ooku no hito ni kandou o ataeta.","german":"The players' performance moved many people.","furigana":"せんしゅ|おお|ひと|かんどう|あた"}],"notes":"感動 is the state of being \"touched and moved\" by something beautiful or great; as an intransitive する-verb: 映画に感動する. Common are 感動を与える \"to move (someone)\", 感動的な \"moving\", and 感動して泣く. Distinctions: 感心 means \"to be impressed\" by someone's skill or behavior (偉いと思う \"thinking it admirable\"), and 感激 stresses overflowing joy or gratitude.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"反感を買いました。","romaji":"Hankan o kaimashita.","german":"He incurred resentment.","furigana":"はんかん|か"},{"japanese":"彼の偉そうな話し方に反感を持つ人も多い。","romaji":"Kare no erasou na hanashikata ni hankan o motsu hito mo ooi.","german":"Many people resent his arrogant way of speaking.","furigana":"かれ|えら|はな|かた|はんかん|も|ひと|おお"},{"japanese":"意見を無理に押しつけると、かえって反感を持たれますよ。","romaji":"Iken o muri ni oshitsukeru to, kaette hankan o motaremasu yo.","german":"If you force your opinion on people, you'll just provoke resentment.","furigana":"いけん|むり|お|はんかん|も"}],"notes":"反感 is an \"aversion\" or resentment toward a person or their behavior. The most important phrase is 反感を買う \"to make oneself unpopular, to draw resentment\"; also 反感を持つ / 抱く \"to feel an aversion\" and 反感を持たれる. 反対, by contrast, is matter-of-fact opposition to an opinion, and 嫌い is simply \"not liking\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"相手を侮ってはいけません。","romaji":"Aite o anadotte wa ikemasen.","german":"You must not underestimate your opponent.","furigana":"あいて|あなど"},{"japanese":"この店のラーメンは安いけど、味は侮れない。","romaji":"Kono mise no raamen wa yasui kedo, aji wa anadorenai.","german":"The ramen at this shop is cheap, but its taste is not to be underestimated.","furigana":"みせ|やす|あじ|あなど"},{"japanese":"山の天気を侮って、ひどい目にあった。","romaji":"Yama no tenki o anadotte, hidoi me ni atta.","german":"I underestimated the mountain weather and had a terrible time.","furigana":"やま|てんき|あなど|め"}],"notes":"侮る means \"to underestimate, to belittle\" and is transitive: 相手を侮る. The negated potential form is very common: 侮れない (\"not to be underestimated\"), often praising something unassuming. In warnings you get 〜を侮ってはいけない. Colloquially one says 甘く見る or なめる; 見下す emphasizes looking down on people, while 侮る is more about underestimating opponents, dangers or things.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人を蔑んではいけません。","romaji":"Hito o sagesunde wa ikemasen.","german":"You must not despise people.","furigana":"ひと|さげす"},{"japanese":"彼女は貧しい人々を蔑むような目で見た。","romaji":"Kanojo wa mazushii hitobito o sagesumu you na me de mita.","german":"She looked at the poor people with a contemptuous look.","furigana":"かのじょ|まず|ひとびと|さげす|め|み"},{"japanese":"学歴で人を蔑むのは間違っている。","romaji":"Gakureki de hito o sagesumu no wa machigatte iru.","german":"It is wrong to look down on people because of their education.","furigana":"がくれき|ひと|さげす|まちが"}],"notes":"蔑む means \"to despise, to look down on someone\" and is transitive: 人を蔑む. It is literary and written-style and is often written in kana (さげすむ); typical are 蔑むような目で見る and the noun 蔑み. In conversation people more often say 見下す or バカにする; 軽蔑する is the more neutral Sino-Japanese word. 侮る, by contrast, emphasizes underestimating.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先生を慕っています。","romaji":"Sensei o shitatte imasu.","german":"I look up to my teacher.","furigana":"せんせい|した"},{"japanese":"田中さんは優しいので、後輩たちに慕われている。","romaji":"Tanaka-san wa yasashii node, kouhaitachi ni shitawarete iru.","german":"Tanaka is kind, so the juniors look up to him.","furigana":"たなか|やさ|こうはい|した"},{"japanese":"子犬は母犬を慕って、どこへでもついて行く。","romaji":"Koinu wa hahainu o shitatte, doko e demo tsuite iku.","german":"The puppy adores its mother and follows her everywhere.","furigana":"こいぬ|ははいぬ|した|い"}],"notes":"慕う means to \"revere\" someone full of affection and respect, or to long for them; transitive: 先生を慕う. The passive is very common: 部下に慕われる上司 \"a boss who is looked up to by his subordinates\". It can also mean \"to follow someone devotedly\" (母を慕ってついて行く). 尊敬する stresses sober esteem, 憧れる rapturous admiration from afar; 慕う contains personal warmth.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"儚い夢でした。","romaji":"Hakanai yume deshita.","german":"It was a fleeting dream.","furigana":"はかな|ゆめ"},{"japanese":"桜の花は儚いからこそ美しいと言われる。","romaji":"Sakura no hana wa hakanai kara koso utsukushii to iwareru.","german":"It is said that cherry blossoms are beautiful precisely because they are fleeting.","furigana":"さくら|はな|はかな|うつく|い"},{"japanese":"優勝への儚い望みも、最後の試合で消えた。","romaji":"Yuushou e no hakanai nozomi mo, saigo no shiai de kieta.","german":"Even the faint hope of winning the championship vanished in the final match.","furigana":"ゆうしょう|はかな|のぞ|さいご|しあい|き"}],"notes":"儚い describes something \"transient, fleeting\": life, dreams, cherry blossoms, love. It has a poetic, wistful undertone and is mostly written in kana (はかない). Second meaning: \"vague, faint\" for hopes (儚い望み). Typical are 儚く消える and 人の命は儚い. Unlike 短い \"short\", 儚い stresses the wistfulness about passing away.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"愛しいわが子の寝顔を見ていると、疲れも忘れる。","romaji":"Itoshii waga ko no negao o mite iru to, tsukare mo wasureru.","german":"Looking at my beloved child's sleeping face, I forget my tiredness.","furigana":"いと|こ|ねがお|み|つか|わす"},{"japanese":"遠くに住む愛しい人に手紙を書いた。","romaji":"Tooku ni sumu itoshii hito ni tegami o kaita.","german":"I wrote a letter to a loved one who lives far away.","furigana":"とお|す|いと|ひと|てがみ|か"},{"japanese":"生まれたばかりの子犬が愛しくてたまらない。","romaji":"Umareta bakari no koinu ga itoshikute tamaranai.","german":"I find the newborn puppy unbearably endearing.","furigana":"う|こいぬ|いと"}],"notes":"愛しい expresses tender love for a person (or an animal): \"dear, beloved, precious\". It sounds emotional and poetic and is found mainly in songs, novels and letters (愛しい人, 愛しいわが子). In modern Japanese the variant 愛おしい (いとおしい) is more common. In everyday speech, for children or animals people say かわいい, and for a partner 大好きな人.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"負けて口惜しいです。","romaji":"Makete kuyashii desu.","german":"I am frustrated that I lost.","furigana":"ま|くや"},{"japanese":"あと一点で合格だったのに、本当に口惜しい。","romaji":"Ato itten de goukaku datta noni, hontou ni kuyashii.","german":"I missed passing by just one point, and it is really frustrating.","furigana":"いってん|ごうかく|ほんとう|くや"},{"japanese":"口惜しくて、その夜は眠れなかった。","romaji":"Kuyashikute, sono yoru wa nemurenakatta.","german":"I was so frustrated that I could not sleep that night.","furigana":"くや|よる|ねむ"}],"notes":"くやしい is the bitter feeling after a defeat, a mistake or an injustice: \"it really rankles\". The spelling 口惜しい is rare and archaic; today it is almost always written 悔しい. If 口惜しい is read くちおしい, it is a literary word for \"regrettable\". Typical: 負けて悔しい, 悔しくて泣く. 残念 is a more neutral regret, without the anger at oneself or others.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"少し物足りないです。","romaji":"Sukoshi monotarinai desu.","german":"It's a little unsatisfying.","furigana":"すこ|ものた"},{"japanese":"このスープは味が薄くて、何か物足りない。","romaji":"Kono suupu wa aji ga usukute, nanika monotarinai.","german":"This soup is bland; something is missing.","furigana":"あじ|うす|なに|ものた"},{"japanese":"上級者には、この練習は物足りないかもしれない。","romaji":"Joukyuusha ni wa, kono renshuu wa monotarinai kamo shirenai.","german":"This exercise might not be challenging enough for advanced learners.","furigana":"じょうきゅうしゃ|れんしゅう|ものた"}],"notes":"物足りない describes the feeling that \"something is missing\", not that the quantity is objectively insufficient (that would be 足りない). Typical: 何か物足りない, 味が物足りない \"too bland\", 〜には物足りない \"not demanding enough for ...\". It is used for food, explanations, performances or experiences and sounds milder than 不満 \"dissatisfaction\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"頼りない人です。","romaji":"Tayorinai hito desu.","german":"He is an unreliable person.","furigana":"たよ|ひと"},{"japanese":"新しいリーダーはまだ若くて、少し頼りない。","romaji":"Atarashii riidaa wa mada wakakute, sukoshi tayorinai.","german":"The new leader is still young and a bit unreliable.","furigana":"あたら|わか|すこ|たよ"},{"japanese":"「たぶん大丈夫…」という頼りない返事しか返ってこなかった。","romaji":"\"Tabun daijoubu...\" to iu tayorinai henji shika kaette konakatta.","german":"All I got back was an unconvincing reply: \"Probably fine...\"","furigana":"だいじょうぶ|たよ|へんじ|かえ"}],"notes":"頼りない literally means \"without anything to rely on\": a person or thing seems weak, unsure and not very trustworthy. Typical: 頼りない人, 頼りない返事, 頼りなさそうに見える. The opposite is 頼もしい \"reliable, inspiring confidence\". Unlike 信用できない (\"not trustworthy\", moral), 頼りない implies no dishonesty, only a lack of strength or competence.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"心強い味方です。","romaji":"Kokorozuyoi mikata desu.","german":"He is a reliable ally.","furigana":"こころづよ|みかた"},{"japanese":"あなたが一緒に来てくれると心強いです。","romaji":"Anata ga issho ni kite kureru to kokorozuyoi desu.","german":"It's reassuring if you come along with me.","furigana":"いっしょ|き|こころづよ"},{"japanese":"経験のある先輩がいるので、心強く思いました。","romaji":"Keiken no aru senpai ga iru node, kokorozuyoku omoimashita.","german":"There was an experienced senior colleague, so I felt reassured.","furigana":"けいけん|せんぱい|こころづよ|おも"}],"notes":"心強い describes the reassuring feeling of having support: \"encouraging, reassuring\". Typical: 〜がいてくれて心強い, 心強い味方, 心強く思う. The opposite is 心細い \"uneasy, forlorn\". 頼もしい describes a person rather as reliable and capable, whereas 心強い describes one's own feeling of security which this person evokes.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一人で心細いです。","romaji":"Hitori de kokorobosoi desu.","german":"I feel uneasy being alone.","furigana":"ひとり|こころぼそ"},{"japanese":"知らない町で道に迷って、心細くなった。","romaji":"Shiranai machi de michi ni mayotte, kokorobosoku natta.","german":"I got lost in an unfamiliar town and felt forlorn.","furigana":"し|まち|みち|まよ|こころぼそ"},{"japanese":"財布の中が心細いので、今日は外食をやめておこう。","romaji":"Saifu no naka ga kokorobosoi node, kyou wa gaishoku o yamete okou.","german":"My wallet is rather thin, so I'll skip eating out today.","furigana":"さいふ|なか|こころぼそ|きょう|がいしょく"}],"notes":"心細い is the \"uneasy, forlorn\" feeling when one is alone or without support: 一人で心細い, 心細くなる. Figuratively it also describes scarce supplies: 残りのお金が心細い. The opposite is 心強い. 寂しい stresses loneliness and longing for company, 不安 general worry; 心細い combines both with helplessness.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"名残惜しいですがお別れです。","romaji":"Nagorioshii desu ga owakare desu.","german":"I am sorry to leave, but it is time to say goodbye.","furigana":"なごりお|わか"},{"japanese":"楽しい旅行も今日で終わりだと思うと、名残惜しい。","romaji":"Tanoshii ryokou mo kyou de owari da to omou to, nagorioshii.","german":"When I think that this wonderful trip ends today, I hate to part.","furigana":"たの|りょこう|きょう|お|おも|なごりお"},{"japanese":"彼女は名残惜しそうに何度も手を振っていた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa nagorioshisou ni nando mo te o futte ita.","german":"She kept waving again and again, as if she did not want to leave.","furigana":"かのじょ|なごりお|なんど|て|ふ"}],"notes":"名残惜しい is composed of 名残 (\"lingering trace, memory\") and 惜しい (\"a pity\"): you part only reluctantly from a person, a place or a good time. A classic polite farewell formula is 名残惜しいですが、そろそろ… at the end of parties and speeches. The form 名残惜しそうに describes someone who is visibly reluctant to leave. 寂しい is more general and not tied to parting.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"有り難いお言葉です。","romaji":"Arigatai okotoba desu.","german":"Thank you very much for those kind words.","furigana":"あ|がた|ことば"},{"japanese":"手伝ってもらえると、本当にありがたいです。","romaji":"Tetsudatte moraeru to, hontou ni arigatai desu.","german":"If you could help me, I would be really grateful.","furigana":"てつだ|ほんとう"},{"japanese":"寒い日には温かいお茶がありがたい。","romaji":"Samui hi ni wa atatakai ocha ga arigatai.","german":"On cold days, a cup of warm tea is a blessing.","furigana":"さむ|ひ|あたた|ちゃ"}],"notes":"ありがたい (usually in kana; 有り難い is rare) describes something that you feel as a blessing: \"welcome, a blessing, something to be grateful for\". So it refers to the thing or help, not to the grateful person (that would be 感謝している). Typical: 〜てもらえるとありがたい (\"I would be grateful if ...\"), ありがたいことに (\"fortunately\"), ありがたく受け取る. ありがとう is derived from it.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"呆気ない最後でした。","romaji":"Akkenai saigo deshita.","german":"It was an anticlimactic ending.","furigana":"あっけ|さいご"},{"japanese":"優勝候補のチームが、一回戦であっけなく負けた。","romaji":"Yuushou kouho no chiimu ga, ikkaisen de akkenaku maketa.","german":"The favored team lost easily in the first round.","furigana":"ゆうしょうこうほ|いっかいせん|ま"},{"japanese":"楽しみにしていた夏休みも、あっけなく終わってしまった。","romaji":"Tanoshimi ni shite ita natsuyasumi mo, akkenaku owatte shimatta.","german":"The summer vacation I'd been looking forward to ended all too quickly.","furigana":"たの|なつやす|お"}],"notes":"あっけない (mostly in kana; 呆気ない is rare) describes something that is over \"disappointingly quickly\" or without drama: an ending, a victory, a defeat, the holidays. Very common is the adverb あっけなく: あっけなく負ける \"to lose without a fight\", あっけなく終わる. Related is あっけにとられる \"to be dumbfounded\". 物足りない stresses that something is missing, あっけない the shortness.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"見苦しい行動です。","romaji":"Migurushii kōdō desu.","german":"That is shameful behavior.","furigana":"みぐる|こうどう"},{"japanese":"負けたあとで言い訳をするのは見苦しい。","romaji":"Maketa ato de iiwake o suru no wa migurushii.","german":"Making excuses after losing is unseemly.","furigana":"ま|い|わけ|みぐる"},{"japanese":"お見苦しいところをお見せして、申し訳ありません。","romaji":"Omigurushii tokoro o omise shite, moushiwake arimasen.","german":"I apologize for letting you see me in such an unpresentable state.","furigana":"みぐる|み|もう|わけ"}],"notes":"見苦しい literally means \"painful to look at\": behavior or appearance is \"unsightly, embarrassing, undignified\". Typical: 見苦しい言い訳, 見苦しい負け方. In the polite formula お見苦しいところをお見せしました, one apologizes for having shown oneself in a messy or embarrassing state. Colloquially one says みっともない; 醜い rather means physical ugliness.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"図々しいお願いですが。","romaji":"Zūzūshii onegai desu ga.","german":"This is a brazen request, but...","furigana":"ずうずう|ねが"},{"japanese":"列に割り込むなんて、図々しい人だ。","romaji":"Retsu ni warikomu nante, zuuzuushii hito da.","german":"Cutting in line like that - what a brazen person!","furigana":"れつ|わ|こ|ずうずう|ひと"},{"japanese":"彼は図々しくも、私の弁当を勝手に食べた。","romaji":"Kare wa zuuzuushiku mo, watashi no bentou o katte ni tabeta.","german":"He had the nerve to eat my bento without asking.","furigana":"かれ|ずうずう|わたし|べんとう|かって|た"}],"notes":"図々しい (\"brazen, cheeky, impudent\") describes someone who takes or demands without consideration. It sounds more colloquial and sharper than the nearly synonymous 厚かましい. Typical: 図々しい人, 図々しい頼み, 図々しくも (\"had the cheek to ...\"). 図々しいお願いですが is heard as a modest opening to a request, but 厚かましいお願いですが is more common.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"厚かましいお願いですが。","romaji":"Atsukamashii onegai desu ga.","german":"This is an impudent request, but...","furigana":"あつ|ねが"},{"japanese":"一度会っただけなのに、お金を貸してと言うなんて厚かましい。","romaji":"Ichido atta dake na noni, okane o kashite to iu nante atsukamashii.","german":"We have only met once, and he asks to borrow money - how shameless.","furigana":"いちどあ|かね|か|い|あつ"},{"japanese":"厚かましいとは思いましたが、先生に推薦状をお願いしました。","romaji":"Atsukamashii to wa omoimashita ga, sensei ni suisenjou o onegai shimashita.","german":"I knew it was presumptuous, but I asked my teacher for a letter of recommendation.","furigana":"あつ|おも|せんせい|すいせんじょう|ねが"}],"notes":"厚かましい (\"shameless, brazen\", literally something like \"thick-skinned\") describes someone who asks for favors without shame. It is almost synonymous with 図々しい but sounds somewhat more composed. The modest opening 厚かましいお願いですが (\"it is presumptuous of me, but ...\") is very common, with which one makes oneself small. Related is 面の皮が厚い (\"to be thick-skinned\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"浅ましい行為です。","romaji":"Asamashii kōi desu.","german":"That is a despicable act.","furigana":"あさ|こうい"},{"japanese":"お金のために友達をだますなんて、浅ましい。","romaji":"Okane no tame ni tomodachi o damasu nante, asamashii.","german":"Deceiving friends for money is despicable.","furigana":"かね|ともだち|あさ"},{"japanese":"一番大きいケーキを取ろうとした自分が浅ましく思えた。","romaji":"Ichiban ookii keeki o torou to shita jibun ga asamashiku omoeta.","german":"I felt ashamed of myself for trying to take the biggest piece of cake.","furigana":"いちばんおお|と|じぶん|あさ|おも"}],"notes":"浅ましい condemns behavior as \"base, despicable, wretched\", usually because greed or self-interest lies behind it: 浅ましい行為, 浅ましい考え. It can also be used self-critically (自分が浅ましく思える). The word is strongly judgmental and rather literary. 情けない stresses pitifulness or weakness, ずるい unfairness, 浅ましい moral lowness.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"隣が騒がしいです。","romaji":"Tonari ga sawagashii desu.","german":"It is noisy next door.","furigana":"となり|さわ"},{"japanese":"教室が騒がしかったので、先生が注意した。","romaji":"Kyoushitsu ga sawagashikatta node, sensei ga chuui shita.","german":"The classroom was noisy, so the teacher gave a warning.","furigana":"きょうしつ|さわ|せんせい|ちゅうい"},{"japanese":"最近、世の中が何かと騒がしい。","romaji":"Saikin, yo no naka ga nanika to sawagashii.","german":"Lately the world has been rather unsettled.","furigana":"さいきん|よ|なか|なに|さわ"}],"notes":"騒がしい describes an environment as \"noisy, restless\" because of voices and commotion: 教室が騒がしい, 隣が騒がしい. Figuratively it means \"unsettled, turbulent\" (世の中が騒がしい). うるさい is more subjective and expresses annoyance (also about people), にぎやか is positive (\"bustling, lively\"). Related are 騒ぐ (\"to make noise\") and 騒ぎ (\"uproar\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"物騒な事件がありました。","romaji":"Bussō na jiken ga arimashita.","german":"There was a dangerous incident.","furigana":"ぶっそう|じけん"},{"japanese":"この辺りは夜になると物騒だから、一人で歩かないほうがいい。","romaji":"Kono atari wa yoru ni naru to bussou dakara, hitori de arukanai hou ga ii.","german":"This area is unsafe at night, so you had better not walk alone.","furigana":"あた|よる|ぶっそう|ひとり|ある"},{"japanese":"最近は物騒な世の中になったね。","romaji":"Saikin wa bussou na yo no naka ni natta ne.","german":"The world has become quite a dangerous place lately, hasn't it.","furigana":"さいきん|ぶっそう|よ|なか"}],"notes":"物騒 is a na-adjective for an \"unsafe, dangerous\" situation in which crime or violence is a threat: 物騒な世の中, 物騒な事件, この辺は夜物騒だ. Objects or talk can also be 物騒 (物騒なものを持ち歩く, 物騒な話). 危ない and 危険 are more general (also accidents, illnesses), while 物騒 refers mainly to crime and unrest.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"穏便に済ませましょう。","romaji":"Onbin ni sumasemashō.","german":"Let's settle it peacefully.","furigana":"おんびん|す"},{"japanese":"隣の人とのトラブルは、話し合いで穏便に解決しました。","romaji":"Tonari no hito to no toraburu wa, hanashiai de onbin ni kaiketsu shimashita.","german":"We settled the trouble with the neighbor amicably through discussion.","furigana":"となり|ひと|はな|あ|おんびん|かいけつ"},{"japanese":"会社としては、できるだけ穏便な処分にしたいと考えています。","romaji":"Kaisha to shite wa, dekiru dake onbin na shobun ni shitai to kangaete imasu.","german":"As a company, we would like to keep the disciplinary action as lenient as possible.","furigana":"かいしゃ|おんびん|しょぶん|かんが"}],"notes":"穏便 is a な-adjective meaning \"amicable, without fuss\", that is, without quarrel, scandal or legal action. It almost always appears adverbially as 穏便に with 済ませる, 解決する or 収める, or attributively in 穏便な方法 and 穏便な処分. Not to be confused with 穏やか, which describes a calm nature or mild weather. The word sounds somewhat formal and is typical when you want to settle a conflict discreetly.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"円満な家庭です。","romaji":"Enman na katei desu.","german":"It's a harmonious family.","furigana":"えんまん|かてい"},{"japanese":"話し合いで、問題は円満に解決した。","romaji":"Hanashiai de, mondai wa enman ni kaiketsu shita.","german":"The problem was settled amicably through discussion.","furigana":"はな|あ|もんだい|えんまん|かいけつ"},{"japanese":"彼女は会社を円満に退職した。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kaisha o enman ni taishoku shita.","german":"She left the company on good terms.","furigana":"かのじょ|かいしゃ|えんまん|たいしょく"}],"notes":"円満 (\"round and full\") describes relationships and solutions as \"harmonious, peaceful, amicable\". Typical: 円満な家庭, 夫婦円満, 円満に解決する \"to settle amicably\", 円満退社 \"to leave the company on good terms\". It refers to relationships without conflict; for a calm environment or a gentle character one more often says 穏やか.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"寛大な心を持ちましょう。","romaji":"Kandai na kokoro o mochimashō.","german":"Let's have a generous heart.","furigana":"かんだい|こころ|も"},{"japanese":"先生は、遅刻した学生に寛大だった。","romaji":"Sensei wa, chikoku shita gakusei ni kandai datta.","german":"The teacher was lenient with the students who came late.","furigana":"せんせい|ちこく|がくせい|かんだい"},{"japanese":"社長の寛大な判断のおかげで、彼は会社を辞めずに済んだ。","romaji":"Shachou no kandai na handan no okage de, kare wa kaisha o yamezu ni sunda.","german":"Thanks to the president's lenient decision, he did not have to quit the company.","furigana":"しゃちょう|かんだい|はんだん|かれ|かいしゃ|や|す"}],"notes":"寛大 means \"magnanimous, forgiving, tolerant\": generous in the sense of forgiving, not in the sense of being free with money (that would be 気前がいい). Typical: 寛大な心, 寛大な処置 \"lenient treatment\", 〜に寛大だ \"to be lenient toward ...\". In everyday speech people often say 心が広い; 寛大 sounds somewhat more formal.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"厳格な父親です。","romaji":"Genkaku na chichioya desu.","german":"He is a strict father.","furigana":"げんかく|ちちおや"},{"japanese":"この学校は校則が厳格で、髪を染めることも禁止されている。","romaji":"Kono gakkou wa kousoku ga genkaku de, kami o someru koto mo kinshi sarete iru.","german":"This school has strict rules, and even dyeing your hair is prohibited.","furigana":"がっこう|こうそく|げんかく|かみ|そ|きんし"},{"japanese":"個人情報は厳格に管理されています。","romaji":"Kojin jouhou wa genkaku ni kanri sarete imasu.","german":"Personal information is managed strictly.","furigana":"こじんじょうほう|げんかく|かんり"}],"notes":"厳格 (\"strict, rigorous\") describes people, rules and procedures that tolerate no exceptions: 厳格な父親, 厳格な規則, 厳格に管理する. It is more formal and objective than the everyday word 厳しい, which is also used for weather, situations or criticism (厳しい寒さ, not 厳格). The opposite is 寛大 \"lenient\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"素直な子供です。","romaji":"Sunao na kodomo desu.","german":"He is an obedient child.","furigana":"すなお|こども"},{"japanese":"素直に謝ったほうがいいよ。","romaji":"Sunao ni ayamatta hou ga ii yo.","german":"You had better just apologize honestly.","furigana":"すなお|あやま"},{"japanese":"褒められたら、素直に喜べばいい。","romaji":"Homeraretara, sunao ni yorokobeba ii.","german":"When you are praised, you can simply be happy.","furigana":"ほ|すなお|よろこ"}],"notes":"素直 describes an open, unpretentious character: \"obedient\" for children (素直な子), but above all \"honest about one's own feelings, not stubborn\". The adverb 素直に is very common: 素直に謝る, 素直に喜ぶ, 素直になれない (\"cannot bring oneself to be honest\"). 正直 means \"not lying\", whereas 素直 means \"without stubbornness and pretense\". For straight hair one also says 素直な髪.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"頑固なおじいさんです。","romaji":"Ganko na ojiisan desu.","german":"He's a stubborn old man.","furigana":"がんこ"},{"japanese":"父は頑固で、一度決めたら絶対に意見を変えない。","romaji":"Chichi wa ganko de, ichido kimetara zettai ni iken o kaenai.","german":"My father is stubborn; once he decides something, he never changes his mind.","furigana":"ちち|がんこ|いちどき|ぜったい|いけん|か"},{"japanese":"この洗剤は頑固な汚れもきれいに落とします。","romaji":"Kono senzai wa ganko na yogore mo kirei ni otoshimasu.","german":"This detergent removes even stubborn stains cleanly.","furigana":"せんざい|がんこ|よご|お"}],"notes":"頑固 (\"stubborn, headstrong\") describes people who do not change their opinion: 頑固な人, 頑固おやじ \"stubborn old coot\", 頑固に拒む. It can also be used appreciatively for loyalty to principles. Figuratively it means \"persistent\": 頑固な汚れ \"stubborn stain\", 頑固な肩こり. 強情 is similar but even more negative; 意地っ張り stresses defiance out of pride.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"強引なやり方です。","romaji":"Gōin na yarikata desu.","german":"It is a forceful way of doing things.","furigana":"ごういん|かた"},{"japanese":"彼は強引に話を進めようとした。","romaji":"Kare wa gouin ni hanashi o susumeyou to shita.","german":"He tried to push the matter through by force.","furigana":"かれ|ごういん|はなし|すす"},{"japanese":"友達に強引に誘われて、カラオケに行った。","romaji":"Tomodachi ni gouin ni sasowarete, karaoke ni itta.","german":"A friend pushed me into going, so I went to karaoke.","furigana":"ともだち|ごういん|さそ|い"}],"notes":"強引 describes an approach that overrides others and pushes something through \"by force\": \"pushy, overbearing\" - not physical violence (that would be 暴力的). The adverb 強引に is very common: 強引に誘う, 強引に話を進める, 強引に決める. Also 強引なセールス (\"pushy selling\"). 無理やり is similar and more colloquial; 横暴 is stronger and means abuse of power.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"横暴な態度です。","romaji":"Ōbō na taido desu.","german":"It is a tyrannical attitude.","furigana":"おうぼう|たいど"},{"japanese":"横暴な上司のせいで、部下が次々に辞めていった。","romaji":"Oubou na joushi no sei de, buka ga tsugitsugi ni yamete itta.","german":"Because of a tyrannical boss, his subordinates quit one after another.","furigana":"おうぼう|じょうし|ぶか|つぎつぎ|や"},{"japanese":"政府の横暴な決定に、多くの市民が反対した。","romaji":"Seifu no oubou na kettei ni, ooku no shimin ga hantai shita.","german":"Many citizens opposed the government's high-handed decision.","furigana":"せいふ|おうぼう|けってい|おお|しみん|はんたい"}],"notes":"横暴 refers to \"tyrannical, arbitrary\" behavior by someone who abuses their power: 横暴な上司, 横暴な態度, 権力の横暴. It is a noun and a na-adjective (横暴を許さない, 横暴に振る舞う). It is stronger than 強引 \"pushy, forceful\" and stresses abuse of power; 乱暴 refers more to rough, violent actions or language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"傲慢な態度はやめましょう。","romaji":"Gōman na taido wa yamemashō.","german":"Let's stop the arrogant attitude.","furigana":"ごうまん|たいど"},{"japanese":"成功してから、彼は少し傲慢になった。","romaji":"Seikou shite kara, kare wa sukoshi gouman ni natta.","german":"After becoming successful, he became a bit arrogant.","furigana":"せいこう|かれ|すこ|ごうまん"},{"japanese":"自分だけが正しいと思うのは傲慢だ。","romaji":"Jibun dake ga tadashii to omou no wa gouman da.","german":"It is arrogant to think that only you are right.","furigana":"じぶん|ただ|おも|ごうまん"}],"notes":"傲慢 (\"arrogant, haughty\") describes someone who considers themselves superior and treats others condescendingly: 傲慢な態度, 傲慢な人, 傲慢になる. It is a strong word close to the written language. In everyday speech people say 偉そう \"acting important\" instead; 生意気 \"cheeky, impertinent\" is used mainly for younger people or subordinates. The opposite is 謙虚 \"modest\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"謙虚な姿勢が大切です。","romaji":"Kenkyo na shisei ga taisetsu desu.","german":"A humble attitude is important.","furigana":"けんきょ|しせい|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"彼は有名になっても謙虚なままだ。","romaji":"Kare wa yuumei ni natte mo kenkyo na mama da.","german":"He has remained humble even after becoming famous.","furigana":"かれ|ゆうめい|けんきょ"},{"japanese":"先輩の意見を謙虚に聞くようにしています。","romaji":"Senpai no iken o kenkyo ni kiku you ni shite imasu.","german":"I try to listen to my seniors' opinions humbly.","furigana":"せんぱい|いけん|けんきょ|き"}],"notes":"謙虚 (\"humble, modest\") is a valued virtue: you do not put yourself in the foreground and you accept criticism openly. Typical: 謙虚な姿勢 / 態度, 謙虚に受け止める, 謙虚に学ぶ, 謙虚さを忘れない. 控えめ means more \"reserved, understated\", 遠慮 is holding back in a specific situation. The opposite is 傲慢.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"誠実な人です。","romaji":"Seijitsu na hito desu.","german":"He is a sincere person.","furigana":"せいじつ|ひと"},{"japanese":"お客様からの苦情には、誠実に対応することが大切だ。","romaji":"Okyakusama kara no kujou ni wa, seijitsu ni taiou suru koto ga taisetsu da.","german":"When handling customer complaints, it's important to respond sincerely.","furigana":"きゃくさま|くじょう|せいじつ|たいおう|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"彼の誠実な人柄に、みんなが信頼を寄せている。","romaji":"Kare no seijitsu na hitogara ni, minna ga shinrai o yosete iru.","german":"Everyone puts their trust in his sincere character.","furigana":"かれ|せいじつ|ひとがら|しんらい|よ"}],"notes":"誠実 (\"sincere, honest, faithful\") describes people and actions that deal with others honestly and conscientiously: 誠実な人, 誠実な対応, 誠実に対応する, 誠実さ. 正直 only means \"not lying\", 真面目 \"serious, dutiful\"; 誠実 stresses sincerity toward others. The word is very popular in business language and in choosing a partner.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"勤勉な学生です。","romaji":"Kinben na gakusei desu.","german":"He is a diligent student.","furigana":"きんべん|がくせい"},{"japanese":"日本人は勤勉だとよく言われる。","romaji":"Nihonjin wa kinben da to yoku iwareru.","german":"Japanese people are often said to be diligent.","furigana":"にほんじん|きんべん|い"},{"japanese":"祖父は一生勤勉に働いて、家族を支えた。","romaji":"Sofu wa isshou kinben ni hataraite, kazoku o sasaeta.","german":"My grandfather worked diligently all his life and supported the family.","furigana":"そふ|いっしょうきんべん|はたら|かぞく|ささ"}],"notes":"勤勉 (\"diligent, hardworking\") describes steady diligence in work or study and sounds rather formal and literary: 勤勉な学生, 勤勉に働く, 勤勉さ. Common in statements about national character (日本人は勤勉だ). In everyday speech one mostly says 真面目 or よく頑張る. The opposite is 怠惰 \"lazy\", colloquially 怠け者.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"怠惰な生活をしています。","romaji":"Taida na seikatsu o shite imasu.","german":"I live an idle life.","furigana":"たいだ|せいかつ"},{"japanese":"夏休みは怠惰に過ごしてしまった。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi wa taida ni sugoshite shimatta.","german":"I spent the summer vacation idly.","furigana":"なつやす|たいだ|す"},{"japanese":"彼の失敗は、能力ではなく怠惰が原因だ。","romaji":"Kare no shippai wa, nouryoku de wa naku taida ga gen'in da.","german":"His failure was caused not by lack of ability but by laziness.","furigana":"かれ|しっぱい|のうりょく|たいだ|げんいん"}],"notes":"怠惰 is a na-adjective and noun meaning \"sluggish, lazy, idle\" and sounds rather written or elevated: 怠惰な生活, 怠惰な性格, adverbially 怠惰に過ごす. In everyday speech people mostly use the verb 怠ける \"to slack off\", 怠け者 \"lazybones\" or だらしない. As a noun, 怠惰 names laziness itself, for example as a cause: 怠惰が原因だ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"貪欲に学びます。","romaji":"Don'yoku ni manabimasu.","german":"I learn eagerly.","furigana":"どんよく|まな"},{"japanese":"あの選手は勝利に貪欲だ。","romaji":"Ano senshu wa shouri ni don'yoku da.","german":"That player is hungry for victory.","furigana":"せんしゅ|しょうり|どんよく"},{"japanese":"金に貪欲な人とは付き合いたくない。","romaji":"Kane ni don'yoku na hito to wa tsukiaitakunai.","german":"I don't want to associate with people who are greedy for money.","furigana":"かね|どんよく|ひと|つ|あ"}],"notes":"貪欲 means \"greedy\", but today it is very often used positively for \"hungry, eager, ambitious\": 貪欲に学ぶ \"to learn avidly\", 勝利に貪欲だ \"to be hungry for victory\". The object of the greed is marked with に: 金に貪欲な人. It is morally negative only when it concerns money or possessions; colloquially 欲張り is also used for that. The word sounds somewhat elevated.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は子どものように純粋な心を持っている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kodomo no you ni junsui na kokoro o motte iru.","german":"She has a pure heart like a child.","furigana":"かのじょ|こ|じゅんすい|こころ|も"},{"japanese":"勝ち負けではなく、純粋にサッカーを楽しみたい。","romaji":"Kachimake de wa naku, junsui ni sakkā o tanoshimitai.","german":"I don't care about winning or losing; I just want to enjoy soccer.","furigana":"か|ま|じゅんすい|たの"},{"japanese":"純粋な水は電気をほとんど通さない。","romaji":"Junsui na mizu wa denki o hotondo tōsanai.","german":"Pure water hardly conducts electricity.","furigana":"じゅんすい|みず|でんき|とお"}],"notes":"純粋 has two main meanings: \"pure, unmixed\" for substances (純粋な水, 純粋な金) and \"sincere, unspoiled\" for people and feelings (純粋な心, 純粋な人), sometimes with an undertone of \"naive\". Adverbially, 純粋に means \"simply, purely out of\": 純粋に楽しみたい. For hygienically \"clean\", use 清潔 or きれい, not 純粋.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子どもたちの無邪気な笑顔を見ると、元気が出る。","romaji":"Kodomotachi no mujaki na egao o miru to, genki ga deru.","german":"Seeing the children's innocent smiles lifts my spirits.","furigana":"こ|むじゃき|えがお|み|げんき|で"},{"japanese":"公園で子犬が無邪気に遊んでいる。","romaji":"Kouen de koinu ga mujaki ni asonde iru.","german":"A puppy is playing innocently in the park.","furigana":"こうえん|こいぬ|むじゃき|あそ"},{"japanese":"彼は無邪気に人の秘密を話してしまう。","romaji":"Kare wa mujaki ni hito no himitsu o hanashite shimau.","german":"He naively blurts out other people's secrets.","furigana":"かれ|むじゃき|ひと|ひみつ|はな"}],"notes":"無邪気 (literally \"without malice\") describes above all children or childlike behavior: carefree, guileless, without ulterior motives, e.g. 無邪気な笑顔, 無邪気に遊ぶ. For adults it can also mean critically \"thoughtless, naive\": 無邪気に秘密を話す. 純粋 stresses inner purity more, 素直 openness and obedience.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"陰気な天気が続いていて、気分まで沈む。","romaji":"Inki na tenki ga tsuzuite ite, kibun made shizumu.","german":"The gloomy weather continues, and it even brings my mood down.","furigana":"いんき|てんき|つづ|きぶん|しず"},{"japanese":"地下の陰気な部屋には住みたくない。","romaji":"Chika no inki na heya ni wa sumitakunai.","german":"I don't want to live in a dismal basement room.","furigana":"ちか|いんき|へや|す"},{"japanese":"そんな陰気な顔をしないで、元気を出して。","romaji":"Sonna inki na kao o shinaide, genki o dashite.","german":"Don't make such a glum face; cheer up.","furigana":"いんき|かお|げんき|だ"}],"notes":"陰気 means \"gloomy, dismal, oppressive\" and describes people, faces, rooms or moods: 陰気な人, 陰気な部屋, 陰気な顔. The opposite is 陽気. In everyday speech, for people one more often simply says 暗い (暗い人); 陰気 sounds stronger and more negative. With weather it is possible (陰気な天気), but one mostly says どんよりした天気.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"陽気な人ですね。","romaji":"Yōki na hito desu ne.","german":"You are a cheerful person.","furigana":"ようき|ひと"},{"japanese":"パーティーでは陽気な音楽が流れていた。","romaji":"Pātī de wa yōki na ongaku ga nagarete ita.","german":"Cheerful music was playing at the party.","furigana":"ようき|おんがく|なが"},{"japanese":"今日は春らしい陽気ですね。","romaji":"Kyou wa haru rashii youki desu ne.","german":"Today the weather feels like spring.","furigana":"きょう|はる|ようき"}],"notes":"陽気 as a na-adjective means \"cheerful, merry, lively\": 陽気な人, 陽気な音楽, 陽気に歌う. The opposite is 陰気. As a noun it also means \"weather, climate of a season\", especially in phrases such as 春の陽気, 春らしい陽気 or いい陽気ですね. For people, 明るい is the more everyday, neutral word.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は話し方がとても上品だ。","romaji":"Kanojo wa hanashikata ga totemo jouhin da.","german":"She speaks very elegantly.","furigana":"かのじょ|はな|かた|じょうひん"},{"japanese":"このお菓子は甘さ控えめで上品な味がする。","romaji":"Kono okashi wa amasa hikaeme de jouhin na aji ga suru.","german":"This sweet is not too sugary and has a refined taste.","furigana":"かし|あま|ひか|じょうひん|あじ"},{"japanese":"パーティーには上品なドレスを着て行った。","romaji":"Pātī ni wa jōhin na doresu o kite itta.","german":"I went to the party in an elegant dress.","furigana":"じょうひん|き|い"}],"notes":"上品 means \"refined, cultured, elegant\" and refers to behavior, speech, clothing and also taste: 上品な話し方, 上品な人, 上品な味 (\"delicate, subtle in taste\"). The opposite is 下品. Unlike おしゃれ (\"stylish, chic\"), 上品 emphasizes restraint and good manners. Adverbial: 上品に食べる, 上品に笑う.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"下品な冗談はやめましょう。","romaji":"Gehin na jōdan wa yamemashō.","german":"Let's stop the vulgar jokes.","furigana":"げひん|じょうだん"},{"japanese":"電車の中で下品な言葉を使うのはやめてほしい。","romaji":"Densha no naka de gehin na kotoba o tsukau no wa yamete hoshii.","german":"I wish people would not use vulgar language on the train.","furigana":"でんしゃ|なか|げひん|ことば|つか"},{"japanese":"金色だらけの派手な服は、少し下品に見える。","romaji":"Kin'iro darake no hade na fuku wa, sukoshi gehin ni mieru.","german":"Flashy clothes that are covered in gold look a little tacky.","furigana":"きんいろ|はで|ふく|すこ|げひん|み"}],"notes":"下品 means \"vulgar, coarse, tasteless\" and is used for speech, jokes, behavior and flashy clothing: 下品な冗談, 下品な言葉, 下品に笑う. The opposite is 上品. It is a clear, disparaging judgment about a lack of decency; for mere impoliteness one rather says 失礼. Also often used as an exclamation in speech: 下品だなあ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この店の家具は、洗練されたデザインで人気がある。","romaji":"Kono mise no kagu wa, senren sareta dezain de ninki ga aru.","german":"The furniture in this shop is popular for its sophisticated design.","furigana":"みせ|かぐ|せんれん|にんき"},{"japanese":"彼女の服装はいつも洗練されている。","romaji":"Kanojo no fukusou wa itsumo senren sarete iru.","german":"Her clothes are always sophisticated.","furigana":"かのじょ|ふくそう|せんれん"},{"japanese":"何度も書き直して、文章を洗練させた。","romaji":"Nando mo kakinaoshite, bunshou o senren saseta.","german":"I rewrote the text many times and polished it.","furigana":"なんど|か|なお|ぶんしょう|せんれん"}],"notes":"洗練 is actually a noun (\"refinement, polish\") and not a na-adjective: *洗練な is wrong. As an adjective, the passive form 洗練された or 洗練されている is used almost always: 洗練されたデザイン, 洗練された人. In the active you say 文章を洗練する or, more often, 洗練させる \"to give a text the finishing touch\". Related: 上品 \"refined in manners\", おしゃれ \"fashionable\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"粋な着物ですね。","romaji":"Iki na kimono desu ne.","german":"That is a stylish kimono.","furigana":"いき|きもの"},{"japanese":"店長の粋な計らいで、誕生日にケーキが出てきた。","romaji":"Tenchō no iki na hakarai de, tanjōbi ni kēki ga dete kita.","german":"Thanks to the manager's tasteful gesture, a cake was brought out on my birthday.","furigana":"てんちょう|いき|はか|たんじょうび|で"},{"japanese":"祖父は昔から粋な人で、いつも帽子をかぶっていた。","romaji":"Sofu wa mukashi kara iki na hito de, itsumo boushi o kabutte ita.","german":"My grandfather has always been a stylish man and always wore a hat.","furigana":"そふ|むかし|いき|ひと|ぼうし"}],"notes":"粋 (いき) is an ideal of beauty from the Edo period: \"chic, stylish\" in a casual, unobtrusive way, e.g. 粋な着物, 粋な人. It can also mean \"sensitive, considerate\", especially in 粋な計らい \"a nice, tactful gesture\". The opposite is 野暮. Do not confuse it with the reading すい (粋 = \"essence, the best\", e.g. 技術の粋).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"そんなことを聞くのは野暮ですよ。","romaji":"Sonna koto o kiku no wa yabo desu yo.","german":"Asking such a thing is tactless.","furigana":"き|やぼ"},{"japanese":"野暮用があるので、今日は早めに帰ります。","romaji":"Yaboyou ga aru node, kyou wa hayame ni kaerimasu.","german":"I have an errand to run, so I'll go home a bit early today.","furigana":"やぼよう|きょう|はや|かえ"},{"japanese":"二人きりの時間を邪魔するなんて野暮だよ。","romaji":"Futarikiri no jikan o jama suru nante yabo da yo.","german":"Disturbing the two when they are alone together would be really tactless.","furigana":"ふたり|じかん|じゃま|やぼ"}],"notes":"野暮 is the opposite of 粋: \"unrefined, tactless, uncool\". Typical is 野暮なことを聞く/言う for questions or remarks that tact says to leave unsaid, often as a comment on oneself: 野暮なことは聞かないよ. Set expressions are 野暮用 (\"a bothersome errand\", used as a vague excuse) and 野暮ったい (\"frumpy, dowdy\" in appearance).","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"侘びの心を感じます。","romaji":"Wabi no kokoro o kanjimasu.","german":"I feel the spirit of wabi.","furigana":"わ|こころ|かん"},{"japanese":"茶道では侘びの精神が大切にされている。","romaji":"Sadou de wa wabi no seishin ga taisetsu ni sarete iru.","german":"In the tea ceremony, the spirit of wabi is cherished.","furigana":"さどう|わ|せいしん|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"この古い茶室には、侘び寂びの美しさがある。","romaji":"Kono furui chashitsu ni wa, wabisabi no utsukushisa ga aru.","german":"This old tea room has the beauty of wabi-sabi.","furigana":"ふる|ちゃしつ|わ|さ|うつく"}],"notes":"侘び is a concept of Japanese aesthetics: the quiet, plain, austere beauty that lies precisely in simplicity and imperfection. It is almost always heard in the pair 侘び寂び (often written わびさび) and in connection with the tea ceremony (茶道) and gardens. Not to be confused with お詫び (\"apology\"), which is read the same.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"侘び寂びは、日本人の美の感じ方を表す言葉です。","romaji":"Wabisabi wa, Nihonjin no bi no kanjikata o arawasu kotoba desu.","german":"Wabi-sabi is a term expressing the Japanese sense of beauty.","furigana":"わ|さ|にほんじん|び|かん|かた|あらわ|ことば"},{"japanese":"古い茶碗の色には、長い年月がつくった寂びがある。","romaji":"Furui chawan no iro ni wa, nagai nengetsu ga tsukutta sabi ga aru.","german":"The color of the old tea bowl has a patina created by long years.","furigana":"ふる|ちゃわん|いろ|なが|ねんげつ|さ"},{"japanese":"苔の生えた古い庭に、静かな寂びを感じた。","romaji":"Koke no haeta furui niwa ni, shizuka na sabi o kanjita.","german":"In the old, moss-covered garden, I felt a quiet, aged beauty.","furigana":"こけ|は|ふる|にわ|しず|さ|かん"}],"notes":"寂び (さび) denotes the beauty of age: patina, weathered surfaces and the quiet dignity of things that time has worked on. As a single word it is rare; it is met almost only in the pair 侘び寂び (わびさび). It is related to 寂しい (\"lonely\") and to 錆 (\"rust\"), which is read the same. JMdict lists the spelling 寂.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"風情のある街です。","romaji":"Fuzei no aru machi desu.","german":"It is a town with charm.","furigana":"ふぜい|まち"},{"japanese":"雨の日の京都も風情があっていいですね。","romaji":"Ame no hi no Kyouto mo fuzei ga atte ii desu ne.","german":"Kyoto on a rainy day has its charm too, doesn't it?","furigana":"あめ|ひ|きょうと|ふぜい"},{"japanese":"新しいビルばかりで、この辺りは風情がなくなった。","romaji":"Atarashii biru bakari de, kono atari wa fuzei ga nakunatta.","german":"With nothing but new buildings, this area has lost its charm.","furigana":"あたら|あた|ふぜい"}],"notes":"風情 refers to the atmospheric, often traditional appeal of a place or scene. It almost always appears with ある/ない: 風情がある, 風情のある街, 風情がない. Unlike the neutral 雰囲気 \"atmosphere\", 風情 is always positive and aesthetic. As a suffix (〜風情) it means derogatorily \"someone like ...\", for example the humble 私風情; that is rather literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"美しい風景です。","romaji":"Utsukushii fūkei desu.","german":"It is a beautiful landscape.","furigana":"うつく|ふうけい"},{"japanese":"窓から見える風景を絵に描いた。","romaji":"Mado kara mieru fuukei o e ni kaita.","german":"I drew the scenery I could see from the window in a picture.","furigana":"まど|み|ふうけい|え|か"},{"japanese":"朝の駅は、急ぐ人でいっぱいのいつもの風景だ。","romaji":"Asa no eki wa, isogu hito de ippai no itsumo no fuukei da.","german":"The morning station, full of hurrying people, is a familiar sight.","furigana":"あさ|えき|いそ|ひと|ふうけい"}],"notes":"風景 means \"landscape, scenery\" but also covers everyday scenes in cities: 日常の風景, 朝の駅の風景. In art: 風景画 \"landscape painting\". 景色 is the more everyday word for a beautiful view in nature, 眺め stresses the view from a particular point, and 光景 is a scene or event that one observes.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"素晴らしい眺めですね。","romaji":"Subarashii nagame desu ne.","german":"It's a wonderful view, isn't it?","furigana":"すば|なが"},{"japanese":"眺めのいい部屋を予約しました。","romaji":"Nagame no ii heya o yoyaku shimashita.","german":"I reserved a room with a nice view.","furigana":"なが|へや|よやく"},{"japanese":"隣にビルが建って、眺めが悪くなった。","romaji":"Tonari ni biru ga tatte, nagame ga waruku natta.","german":"A building went up next door, and the view got worse.","furigana":"となり|た|なが|わる"}],"notes":"眺め is the noun of 眺める (\"to look at, gaze into the distance\") and means \"view, outlook\" from a certain location. Typical: 眺めがいい/悪い, 眺めのいい部屋, 山頂からの眺め. 景色 refers more to the scenery itself, while 眺め is the view you get from here.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ここは紅葉の絶景スポットとして有名です。","romaji":"Koko wa kouyou no zekkei supotto to shite yuumei desu.","german":"This place is famous as a spot for spectacular autumn foliage views.","furigana":"こうよう|ぜっけい|ゆうめい"},{"japanese":"山頂からの絶景に言葉を失った。","romaji":"Sanchou kara no zekkei ni kotoba o ushinatta.","german":"The breathtaking view from the summit left me speechless.","furigana":"さんちょう|ぜっけい|ことば|うしな"},{"japanese":"ホテルの露天風呂から、海の絶景を楽しめます。","romaji":"Hoteru no rotenburo kara, umi no zekkei o tanoshimemasu.","german":"You can enjoy a spectacular view of the sea from the hotel's open-air bath.","furigana":"ろてんぶろ|うみ|ぜっけい|たの"}],"notes":"絶景 (from 絶 \"extremely\" and 景 \"view\") is a breathtaking, exceptionally beautiful view. It is very common in travel magazines and advertising: 絶景スポット, 絶景を楽しむ, 〜からの絶景. As an exclamation before a view you simply say 絶景だ!. It is stronger than 景色 or 眺め and is used only for truly impressive sights.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"有名な観光地です。","romaji":"Yūmei na kankōchi desu.","german":"It is a famous tourist destination.","furigana":"ゆうめい|かんこうち"},{"japanese":"夏休みは、どこの観光地も混んでいる。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi wa, doko no kankouchi mo konde iru.","german":"During summer vacation, every tourist spot is crowded.","furigana":"なつやす|かんこうち|こ"},{"japanese":"この村は最近、観光地として人気が出てきた。","romaji":"Kono mura wa saikin, kankouchi to shite ninki ga dete kita.","german":"This village has recently become popular as a tourist destination.","furigana":"むら|さいきん|かんこうち|にんき|で"}],"notes":"観光地 is a place or area visited by tourists: 有名な観光地, 人気の観光地, 観光地として知られる. A single famous building or sight is more likely called 名所 or 観光スポット. Slightly derogatory is 観光地化する \"to become overrun with tourism\".","levelNote":"Compound: 観光 (N4) + 地 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お土産を買いました。","romaji":"Omiyage o kaimashita.","german":"I bought souvenirs.","furigana":"みやげ|か"},{"japanese":"友達の家に行くとき、ケーキをお土産に持っていった。","romaji":"Tomodachi no ie ni iku toki, kēki o omiyage ni motte itta.","german":"When I went to a friend's house, I brought a cake as a gift.","furigana":"ともだち|いえ|い|みやげ|も"},{"japanese":"旅行の土産話を聞かせてください。","romaji":"Ryokou no miyagebanashi o kikasete kudasai.","german":"Please tell me stories from your trip.","furigana":"りょこう|みやげばなし|き"}],"notes":"土産 (special reading みやげ) is almost always used politely as お土産. It refers both to travel souvenirs bought for colleagues and family and to a gift for the host when visiting (then also 手土産). Typical: お土産を買う, 〜をお土産に持っていく. 土産話 are stories about the trip. An ordinary gift, by contrast, is プレゼント or 贈り物.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"民宿に泊まりました。","romaji":"Minshuku ni tomarimashita.","german":"I stayed at a minshuku.","furigana":"みんしゅく|と"},{"japanese":"海の近くの民宿で、新鮮な魚料理を食べた。","romaji":"Umi no chikaku no minshuku de, shinsen na sakana ryouri o tabeta.","german":"At a minshuku near the sea, I ate fresh fish dishes.","furigana":"うみ|ちか|みんしゅく|しんせん|さかなりょうり|た"},{"japanese":"民宿は旅館より安いが、トイレや風呂が共同のことが多い。","romaji":"Minshuku wa ryokan yori yasui ga, toire ya furo ga kyoudou no koto ga ooi.","german":"A minshuku is cheaper than a ryokan, but the toilet and bath are often shared.","furigana":"みんしゅく|りょかん|やす|ふろ|きょうどう|おお"}],"notes":"民宿 is a family-run guesthouse, often in the countryside, by the sea or in ski areas. It is cheaper and simpler than a 旅館: bath and toilet are often shared, and the food often comes from the house's own kitchen or from the day's catch. One stays at one with 民宿に泊まる. ペンション, by contrast, is a pension in the Western style.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日帰り旅行に行きます。","romaji":"Higaeri ryokō ni ikimasu.","german":"I am going on a day trip.","furigana":"ひがえ|りょこう|い"},{"japanese":"大阪なら、新幹線で日帰りできますよ。","romaji":"Oosaka nara, shinkansen de higaeri dekimasu yo.","german":"If it's Osaka, you can go and come back in a day by Shinkansen.","furigana":"おおさか|しんかんせん|ひがえ"},{"japanese":"日帰りの出張だったので、とても疲れた。","romaji":"Higaeri no shucchou datta node, totemo tsukareta.","german":"It was a one-day business trip, so I was very tired.","furigana":"ひがえ|しゅっちょう|つか"}],"notes":"日帰り means \"going and returning on the same day\", that is, without an overnight stay. It often stands before nouns: 日帰り旅行, 日帰り温泉, 日帰り出張, or adverbially with で: 日帰りで行く. As an intransitive する-verb: 日帰りする, 日帰りできる. The counterpart is e.g. 一泊二日 (\"two days, one night\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二週間滞在します。","romaji":"Nishūkan taizai shimasu.","german":"I will stay for two weeks.","furigana":"にしゅうかんたいざい"},{"japanese":"日本には、どのくらい滞在する予定ですか。","romaji":"Nihon ni wa, dono kurai taizai suru yotei desu ka.","german":"How long do you plan to stay in Japan?","furigana":"にほん|たいざい|よてい"},{"japanese":"滞在先のホテルの住所を書いてください。","romaji":"Taizaisaki no hoteru no juusho o kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write the address of the hotel where you are staying.","furigana":"たいざいさき|じゅうしょ|か"}],"notes":"滞在 is a \"stay\" in a place for a certain time, as an intransitive する-verb with に for the place: 日本に滞在する, ホテルに滞在する. Common compounds: 滞在期間, 滞在先 \"accommodation during the stay\", 長期滞在. 泊まる specifically means \"to stay overnight\" and 住む \"to live permanently\"; 滞在 lies in between and sounds somewhat more formal.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"宿泊料はいくらですか。","romaji":"Shukuhakuryō wa ikura desu ka.","german":"How much is the accommodation fee?","furigana":"しゅくはくりょう"},{"japanese":"会議の参加者は、駅前のホテルに宿泊します。","romaji":"Kaigi no sankasha wa, ekimae no hoteru ni shukuhaku shimasu.","german":"The meeting participants will stay at the hotel in front of the station.","furigana":"かいぎ|さんかしゃ|えきまえ|しゅくはく"},{"japanese":"当ホテルにご宿泊のお客様は、朝食が無料です。","romaji":"Tou hoteru ni goshukuhaku no okyakusama wa, choushoku ga muryou desu.","german":"Guests staying at our hotel get a free breakfast.","furigana":"とう|しゅくはく|きゃくさま|ちょうしょく|むりょう"}],"notes":"宿泊 is the formal word for \"to stay overnight, accommodation\" and is found mainly at hotels, travel agencies and on forms: ホテルに宿泊する, 宿泊施設, 宿泊客, 宿泊料/宿泊費. In conversation people mostly simply say 泊まる. In the polite language of hotels it is ご宿泊.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"搭乗手続きをします。","romaji":"Tōjō tetsuzuki o shimasu.","german":"I will go through the boarding procedure.","furigana":"とうじょうてつづ"},{"japanese":"搭乗券を見せてください。","romaji":"Toujouken o misete kudasai.","german":"Please show your boarding pass.","furigana":"とうじょうけん|み"},{"japanese":"まもなく搭乗を開始いたします。","romaji":"Mamonaku toujou o kaishi itashimasu.","german":"We will begin boarding shortly.","furigana":"とうじょう|かいし"}],"notes":"搭乗 is boarding an aircraft (more rarely a ship) and belongs to airport language: 搭乗券 \"boarding pass\", 搭乗口 \"gate\", 搭乗手続き \"check-in\", announcements such as ご搭乗の皆様. For trains, buses and cars you say 乗車, for ships also 乗船; in everyday speech simply 乗る.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"出入国カードを書きます。","romaji":"Shutsunyūkoku kādo o kakimasu.","german":"I fill out the entry/exit card.","furigana":"しゅつにゅうこく|か"},{"japanese":"空港の出入国審査に一時間もかかった。","romaji":"Kuukou no shutsunyuukoku shinsa ni ichijikan mo kakatta.","german":"Passport control at the airport took a whole hour.","furigana":"くうこう|しゅつにゅうこくしんさ|いちじかん"},{"japanese":"出入国の手続きは、パスポートがないとできません。","romaji":"Shutsunyūkoku no tetsuzuki wa, pasupōto ga nai to dekimasen.","german":"You can't complete the entry and exit procedures without a passport.","furigana":"しゅつにゅうこく|てつづ"}],"notes":"出入国 combines 出国 (\"departure from a country\") and 入国 (\"entry into a country\") and is an administrative word that occurs almost only in compounds: 出入国カード, 出入国審査 (\"passport control\"), 出入国管理. In conversation people usually speak of 出国 or 入国 separately, depending on what is meant.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本とドイツの時差は、夏は七時間です。","romaji":"Nihon to Doitsu no jisa wa, natsu wa nanajikan desu.","german":"Zwischen Japan und Deutschland beträgt der Zeitunterschied im Sommer sieben Stunden.","furigana":"にほん|じさ|なつ|ななじかん"},{"japanese":"まだ時差ぼけで、夜なかなか眠れません。","romaji":"Mada jisaboke de, yoru nakanaka nemuremasen.","german":"Ich habe noch Jetlag und kann nachts kaum schlafen.","furigana":"じさ|よる|ねむ"},{"japanese":"時差があるので、電話する時間に気をつけている。","romaji":"Jisa ga aru node, denwa suru jikan ni ki o tsukete iru.","german":"Wegen des Zeitunterschieds achte ich darauf, wann ich anrufe.","furigana":"じさ|でんわ|じかん|き"}],"notes":"時差 ist der „Zeitunterschied“ zwischen Zeitzonen: 日本とドイツの時差は八時間, 時差がある. Sehr häufig ist 時差ぼけ („Jetlag“), meist in 時差ぼけで眠い oder 時差ぼけがひどい. Außerdem gibt es 時差出勤 („gestaffelter Arbeitsbeginn“), wo 時差 einfach „zeitlich versetzt“ bedeutet.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"添乗員が案内します。","romaji":"Tenjōin ga annai shimasu.","german":"The tour conductor will guide us.","furigana":"てんじょういん|あんない"},{"japanese":"集合時間に遅れないよう、添乗員に注意された。","romaji":"Shuugou jikan ni okurenai you, tenjouin ni chuui sareta.","german":"The tour conductor warned me not to be late for the meeting time.","furigana":"しゅうごうじかん|おく|てんじょういん|ちゅうい"},{"japanese":"姉は旅行会社で添乗員として働いている。","romaji":"Ane wa ryokou gaisha de tenjouin to shite hataraite iru.","german":"My older sister works as a tour conductor at a travel agency.","furigana":"あね|りょこうがいしゃ|てんじょういん|はたら"}],"notes":"添乗員 accompanies a package or group tour (ツアー) from beginning to end and takes care of the schedule, hotels and organization. A ガイド (観光ガイド), by contrast, explains the sights on site. Colloquially you also hear ツアーコンダクター or, in short, ツアコン. The related verb is 添乗する \"to accompany a trip\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"神社を巡ります。","romaji":"Jinja o megurimasu.","german":"I visit shrines one after another.","furigana":"じんじゃ|めぐ"},{"japanese":"春が巡ってきて、桜の季節になった。","romaji":"Haru ga megutte kite, sakura no kisetsu ni natta.","german":"Spring has come around again, and it is the cherry blossom season.","furigana":"はる|めぐ|さくら|きせつ"},{"japanese":"新しい駅の建設をめぐって、住民の意見が分かれている。","romaji":"Atarashii eki no kensetsu o megutte, juumin no iken ga wakarete iru.","german":"Residents are divided over the construction of the new station.","furigana":"あたら|えき|けんせつ|じゅうみん|いけん|わ"}],"notes":"巡る is intransitive; を marks the route or the places visited: 名所を巡る, お寺を巡る (\"to make a tour\"). The noun 〜巡り (お寺巡り) fits with this. It also means \"to come around again\": 季節が巡る. The form 〜をめぐって (\"concerning, centering on ...\") is very common and is usually written in kana: 問題をめぐって議論する. 回る is the more everyday word for \"to go around\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"歴史を辿ります。","romaji":"Rekishi o tadorimasu.","german":"We trace history.","furigana":"れきし|たど"},{"japanese":"地図を片手に細い道をたどって、やっと村に着いた。","romaji":"Chizu o katate ni hosoi michi o tadotte, yatto mura ni tsuita.","german":"Map in hand, I followed the narrow path and finally reached the village.","furigana":"ちず|かたて|ほそ|みち|むら|つ"},{"japanese":"古い写真を見ながら、子どもの頃の記憶をたどった。","romaji":"Furui shashin o minagara, kodomo no koro no kioku o tadotta.","german":"Looking at old photos, I traced my childhood memories.","furigana":"ふる|しゃしん|み|こ|ころ|きおく"}],"notes":"辿る is mostly written in kana (たどる) and is transitive: 道をたどる \"to follow a path\", 記憶をたどる \"to trace back one's memory\", 歴史をたどる. Figuratively it describes a development: 悪化の一途をたどる \"to keep getting steadily worse\". Related is たどり着く \"to arrive after difficulty\". 追う, by contrast, means \"to chase, to pursue\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"街を彷徨いました。","romaji":"Machi o samayoimashita.","german":"I wandered around the town.","furigana":"まち|さまよ"},{"japanese":"道に迷って、一晩中山の中をさまよった。","romaji":"Michi ni mayotte, hitobanjuu yama no naka o samayotta.","german":"I lost my way and wandered in the mountains all night.","furigana":"みち|まよ|ひとばんじゅうやま|なか"},{"japanese":"事故の後、彼は三日間、生死の境をさまよった。","romaji":"Jiko no ato, kare wa mikkakan, seishi no sakai o samayotta.","german":"After the accident, he hovered between life and death for three days.","furigana":"じこ|あと|かれ|みっかかん|せいし|さかい"}],"notes":"彷徨う is almost always written in kana (さまよう) and means \"to wander aimlessly\". It is intransitive; を marks the area: 街をさまよう, 森の中をさまよう. Set phrase: 生死の境をさまよう \"to hover between life and death\". It sounds somewhat literary; in everyday speech people say うろうろする \"to walk around\" or 迷う \"to get lost\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"湖畔に佇んでいます。","romaji":"Kohan ni tatazunde imasu.","german":"He is standing still on the lakeshore.","furigana":"こはん|たたず"},{"japanese":"静かな森の中に、小さな神社がひっそりと佇んでいる。","romaji":"Shizuka na mori no naka ni, chiisana jinja ga hissori to tatazunde iru.","german":"A small shrine stands quietly in the middle of the silent forest.","furigana":"しず|もり|なか|ちい|じんじゃ|たたず"},{"japanese":"彼女は駅の前に、しばらく一人で佇んでいた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa eki no mae ni, shibaraku hitori de tatazunde ita.","german":"She stood alone in front of the station for a while.","furigana":"かのじょ|えき|まえ|ひとり|たたず"}],"notes":"佇む is a literary word for \"to stand still, to linger (pensively)\"; the place takes に: 湖畔に佇む, 駅の前に佇む. Mostly in the form 佇んでいる/佇んでいた. In travel writing it is also said of buildings: 森の中にひっそりと佇む神社. 立ち止まる, by contrast, is only \"to stop (walking)\", without the atmospheric note.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"寒くて震えています。","romaji":"Samukute furuete imasu.","german":"I am shivering from the cold.","furigana":"さむ|ふる"},{"japanese":"緊張して、スピーチの間ずっと声が震えていた。","romaji":"Kinchō shite, supīchi no aida zutto koe ga furuete ita.","german":"I was nervous, and my voice trembled throughout the speech.","furigana":"きんちょう|あいだ|こえ|ふる"},{"japanese":"窓ガラスが震えるほど大きな音がした。","romaji":"Mado garasu ga furueru hodo ookina oto ga shita.","german":"There was a noise so loud that the window glass shook.","furigana":"まど|ふる|おお|おと"}],"notes":"震える (\"to tremble, shake\") is intransitive; the cause is expressed with で or in the て-form: 寒さで震える, 怖くて震える. Typical: 手が震える, 声が震える, 体が震える. The transitive counterpart is 震わせる (肩を震わせる). 揺れる, by contrast, means \"to sway, wobble\", for example in an earthquake or in a moving train.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"雷が轟きました。","romaji":"Kaminari ga todorokimashita.","german":"The thunder roared.","furigana":"かみなり|とどろ"},{"japanese":"飛行機のエンジン音が空港に轟いた。","romaji":"Hikouki no enjin'on ga kuukou ni todoroita.","german":"The sound of the airplane engines roared through the airport.","furigana":"ひこうき|おん|くうこう|とどろ"},{"japanese":"彼の名前は世界中に轟いている。","romaji":"Kare no namae wa sekaijuu ni todoroite iru.","german":"His name is known all over the world.","furigana":"かれ|なまえ|せかいじゅう|とどろ"}],"notes":"轟く describes a mighty, booming sound that can be heard far away: 雷が轟く, エンジン音が轟く. Figuratively it means \"to be famous, to become widely known\": 名が世界に轟く; the transitive counterpart is 名を轟かせる. The word is rather literary and dramatic. 響く is more neutral (\"to resound, to echo\"), 鳴る is simply \"to sound\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"静かな夜の町に、お寺の鐘の音が響いた。","romaji":"Shizuka na yoru no machi ni, otera no kane no oto ga hibiita.","german":"The sound of the temple bell echoed through the quiet night town.","furigana":"しず|よる|まち|てら|かね|おと|ひび"},{"japanese":"先生の言葉が心に響いた。","romaji":"Sensei no kotoba ga kokoro ni hibiita.","german":"The teacher's words struck a chord in my heart.","furigana":"せんせい|ことば|こころ|ひび"},{"japanese":"夜更かしは、次の日の仕事に響く。","romaji":"Yofukashi wa, tsugi no hi no shigoto ni hibiku.","german":"Staying up late affects your work the next day.","furigana":"よふ|つぎ|ひ|しごと|ひび"}],"notes":"響く means \"to echo, to resound, to ring out\"; the space takes に: ホールに声が響く. Figuratively it means \"to touch one's heart\" (心に響く) and \"to affect\" (〜に響く): 睡眠不足が仕事に響く. The transitive counterpart is 響かせる. 鳴る means only \"to sound\" (bell, telephone), while 響く emphasizes the reverberation and the effect.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"耳元で囁きました。","romaji":"Mimimoto de sasayakimashita.","german":"He whispered in my ear.","furigana":"みみもと|ささや"},{"japanese":"図書館で友達が「もう帰ろう」とささやいた。","romaji":"Toshokan de tomodachi ga mou kaerou to sasayaita.","german":"In the library, my friend whispered, \"Let's go home.\"","furigana":"としょかん|ともだち|かえ"},{"japanese":"あの二人は付き合っているとささやかれている。","romaji":"Ano futari wa tsukiatte iru to sasayakarete iru.","german":"It is rumored that those two are going out.","furigana":"ふたり|つ|あ"}],"notes":"囁く is often written in kana (ささやく) and means \"to whisper\", usually directed at someone: 耳元でささやく, 小声でささやく, 「…」とささやく. In the passive it means \"to be rumored\": 〜とささやかれている. The noun is ささやき. つぶやく, by contrast, is \"to mutter to oneself\", with no addressee.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"何か呟いています。","romaji":"Nanika tsubuyaite imasu.","german":"He's muttering something.","furigana":"なに|つぶや"},{"japanese":"彼は「疲れた」と小さくつぶやいた。","romaji":"Kare wa tsukareta to chiisaku tsubuyaita.","german":"\"I'm tired,\" he muttered quietly.","furigana":"かれ|つか|ちい"},{"japanese":"毎日、SNSで今日の出来事をつぶやいている。","romaji":"Mainichi, SNS de kyou no dekigoto o tsubuyaite iru.","german":"I tweet about what happened that day on social media every day.","furigana":"まいにち|きょう|できごと"}],"notes":"呟く (mostly written つぶやく) means \"to murmur quietly to oneself\", often with no listener: 独り言をつぶやく, 「…」とつぶやく. In contrast to ささやく \"to whisper\", one is not addressing anyone. Since Twitter, つぶやく also means \"to post a message\", and the noun つぶやき also \"tweet\". It can be used transitively (〜をつぶやく) or with と.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"犬が唸っています。","romaji":"Inu ga unatte imasu.","german":"The dog is growling.","furigana":"いぬ|うな"},{"japanese":"腹痛で一晩中唸っていた。","romaji":"Fukutsuu de hitobanjuu unatte ita.","german":"I was groaning all night with a stomachache.","furigana":"ふくつう|ひとばんじゅううな"},{"japanese":"このシェフの料理は、グルメな客も唸らせる。","romaji":"Kono shefu no ryouri wa, gurume na kyaku mo unaraseru.","german":"This chef's cooking makes even gourmet customers marvel.","furigana":"りょうり|きゃく|うな"}],"notes":"唸る describes deep, dull sounds: groaning with pain (痛みで唸る), growling of dogs (犬が唸る) and the hum of engines or wind. Figuratively it means \"to marvel in admiration\"; very common is the causative 唸らせる \"to impress someone\": 客を唸らせる料理. Loud barking, by contrast, is 吠える.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"犬が吠えています。","romaji":"Inu ga hoete imasu.","german":"The dog is barking.","furigana":"いぬ|ほ"},{"japanese":"隣の犬は、知らない人を見るとすぐ吠える。","romaji":"Tonari no inu wa, shiranai hito o miru to sugu hoeru.","german":"The neighbor's dog barks right away when it sees a stranger.","furigana":"となり|いぬ|し|ひと|み|ほ"},{"japanese":"嵐の夜、風が吠えるように吹いていた。","romaji":"Arashi no yoru, kaze ga hoeru you ni fuite ita.","german":"On the stormy night, the wind blew as if howling.","furigana":"あらし|よる|かぜ|ほ|ふ"}],"notes":"吠える is the barking of dogs, but also the roaring or howling of other animals. The one barked at is marked with に: 犬が知らない人に吠える. Figuratively it is said of howling wind or a roaring sea, and derogatorily of people who shout and scold loudly (負け犬の遠吠え). A deep growl, by contrast, is 唸る.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鳥が鳴いています。","romaji":"Tori ga naite imasu.","german":"The birds are chirping.","furigana":"とり|な"},{"japanese":"うちの猫はお腹がすくと鳴きます。","romaji":"Uchi no neko wa onaka ga suku to nakimasu.","german":"Our cat meows when it gets hungry.","furigana":"ねこ|なか|な"}],"notes":"鳴く is an intransitive godan verb for animal sounds: 鳴きます, 鳴いて. Birds can chirp, cats meow; with が you name the animal. 鳴いている describes a sound being heard right now. The identically read spelling 泣く refers to human crying.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"猫を飼っています。","romaji":"Neko o katte imasu.","german":"I have a cat.","furigana":"ねこ|か"},{"japanese":"このマンションでは、犬を飼ってはいけません。","romaji":"Kono manshon de wa, inu o katte wa ikemasen.","german":"Keeping dogs is not allowed in this apartment building.","furigana":"いぬ|か"},{"japanese":"祖父は田舎で牛や鶏を飼っている。","romaji":"Sofu wa inaka de ushi ya niwatori o katte iru.","german":"My grandfather raises cows and chickens in the countryside.","furigana":"そふ|いなか|うし|にわとり|か"}],"notes":"飼う is transitive and means \"to keep (an animal)\": 猫を飼う, 犬を飼っている (mostly the ている form for the state). Related are 飼い主 (\"owner\") and 飼い犬. For children and plants you say 育てる, not 飼う. Watch out for the identically sounding 買う (\"to buy\"): ペットを買う means \"to buy a pet\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 11"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"犬の躾をします。","romaji":"Inu no shitsuke o shimasu.","german":"I'm training the dog.","furigana":"いぬ|しつけ"},{"japanese":"子どもの頃、親のしつけがとても厳しかった。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, oya no shitsuke ga totemo kibishikatta.","german":"When I was a child, my parents' discipline was very strict.","furigana":"こ|ころ|おや|きび"},{"japanese":"ちゃんと挨拶ができるのは、家庭のしつけがいい証拠だ。","romaji":"Chanto aisatsu ga dekiru no wa, katei no shitsuke ga ii shouko da.","german":"Being able to greet people properly is proof of a good upbringing at home.","furigana":"あいさつ|かてい|しょうこ"}],"notes":"躾 is mostly written in kana (しつけ) and refers to training in good behavior and manners, for children as well as pets: 犬のしつけ, しつけが厳しい, しつけのいい子. The verb is しつける. 教育, by contrast, is education in the school sense. The kanji 躾 (身 + 美) is a character created in Japan.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"魚に餌をやります。","romaji":"Sakana ni esa o yarimasu.","german":"I feed the fish.","furigana":"さかな|えさ"},{"japanese":"釣りの餌にミミズを使った。","romaji":"Tsuri no esa ni mimizu o tsukatta.","german":"I used earthworms as fishing bait.","furigana":"つ|えさ|つか"},{"japanese":"公園の鳩に餌をあげないでください。","romaji":"Kouen no hato ni esa o agenaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't feed the pigeons in the park.","furigana":"こうえん|はと|えさ"}],"notes":"餌 is \"feed\" for animals and \"bait\" in fishing. To feed is 餌をやる or, more politely, 餌をあげる (animal with に). Figuratively it stands for a lure: 餌に釣られる \"to fall for a bait\". Used for human food it is an insult. For pet food people also say ペットフード; the reading え occurs in 餌食 \"prey\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鳥の巣があります。","romaji":"Tori no su ga arimasu.","german":"There is a bird's nest.","furigana":"とり|す"},{"japanese":"ツバメが玄関の上に巣を作った。","romaji":"Tsubame ga genkan no ue ni su o tsukutta.","german":"Swallows built a nest above the entrance.","furigana":"げんかん|うえ|す|つく"},{"japanese":"部屋の隅にクモの巣が張っていた。","romaji":"Heya no sumi ni kumo no su ga hatte ita.","german":"A spider web was spun in the corner of the room.","furigana":"へや|すみ|す|は"}],"notes":"巣 is the nest or burrow of an animal: 鳥の巣, 蜂の巣, アリの巣, and also クモの巣 \"spider web\". Building a nest is 巣を作る, and a spider web is stretched: クモの巣が張る. Derived is 巣立つ \"to fledge\", also \"to leave the parental home\". Figuratively 巣 can be a \"hideout, shelter\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"白い羽が落ちていました。","romaji":"Shiroi hane ga ochite imashita.","german":"A white feather had fallen.","furigana":"しろ|はね|お"},{"japanese":"鳥が羽を広げて飛んでいった。","romaji":"Tori ga hane o hirogete tonde itta.","german":"The bird spread its wings and flew away.","furigana":"とり|はね|ひろ|と"},{"japanese":"蝶の羽はとても薄い。","romaji":"Chou no hane wa totemo usui.","german":"A butterfly's wings are very thin.","furigana":"ちょう|はね|うす"}],"notes":"羽 (はね) means both \"feather\" and \"wing\" (of birds and insects): 羽を広げる, 白い羽. 翼 (つばさ) is the larger, more poetic or technical \"wing\", for example of an airplane. For badminton shuttlecocks and rotor blades, 羽根 is often written. As a counter for birds, 羽 is read わ, with sound changes such as 三羽 (さんば).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"牙が鋭いです。","romaji":"Kiba ga surudoi desu.","german":"The fangs are sharp.","furigana":"きば|するど"},{"japanese":"象は長い牙を持っている。","romaji":"Zou wa nagai kiba o motte iru.","german":"Elephants have long tusks.","furigana":"ぞう|なが|きば|も"},{"japanese":"犬が牙をむいて唸った。","romaji":"Inu ga kiba o muite unatta.","german":"The dog bared its fangs and growled.","furigana":"いぬ|きば|うな"}],"notes":"牙 are the fangs of predators and the tusks of elephants or wild boars; ordinary teeth are 歯. Ivory is 象牙 (ぞうげ). The phrase 牙をむく \"to bare one's fangs\" is also used figuratively for \"to show hostility\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"蝶が飛んでいます。","romaji":"Chō ga tonde imasu.","german":"A butterfly is flying.","furigana":"ちょう|と"},{"japanese":"庭の花に蝶がとまっている。","romaji":"Niwa no hana ni chou ga tomatte iru.","german":"A butterfly is resting on a flower in the garden.","furigana":"にわ|はな|ちょう"},{"japanese":"結婚式に蝶ネクタイをして行った。","romaji":"Kekkonshiki ni chou nekutai o shite itta.","german":"I wore a bow tie to the wedding.","furigana":"けっこんしき|ちょう|い"}],"notes":"蝶 is \"butterfly\"; in everyday writing it often appears in kana (チョウ) or, in child language, as ちょうちょ. Moths, by contrast, are called 蛾 (が). Well-known expressions are 蝶ネクタイ (\"bow tie\") and 蝶よ花よと育てる (\"to spoil a child greatly\"). Butterflies land on flowers: 花にとまる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"蜂に刺されました。","romaji":"Hachi ni sasaremashita.","german":"I got stung by a bee.","furigana":"はち|さ"},{"japanese":"蜂蜜をパンに塗って食べた。","romaji":"Hachimitsu o pan ni nutte tabeta.","german":"I spread honey on bread and ate it.","furigana":"はちみつ|ぬ|た"},{"japanese":"山でハイキングをするときは、蜂に気をつけてください。","romaji":"Yama de haikingu o suru toki wa, hachi ni ki o tsukete kudasai.","german":"Please watch out for bees when hiking in the mountains.","furigana":"やま|はち|き"}],"notes":"蜂 (はち) is the general term for bees, wasps and hornets. More specific are ミツバチ \"honeybee\" and スズメバチ \"hornet\"; in compounds はち becomes ばち. To be stung is 蜂に刺される, and honey is 蜂蜜. Idiom: 蜂の巣をつついたよう \"like a stirred-up beehive\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"蛍が光っています。","romaji":"Hotaru ga hikatte imasu.","german":"Fireflies are glowing.","furigana":"ほたる|ひか"},{"japanese":"六月になると、この川に蛍を見に来る人が多い。","romaji":"Rokugatsu ni naru to, kono kawa ni hotaru o mi ni kuru hito ga ooi.","german":"When June comes, many people come to this river to see fireflies.","furigana":"ろくがつ|かわ|ほたる|み|く|ひと|おお"},{"japanese":"閉店の時間になると、店内に「蛍の光」が流れる。","romaji":"Heiten no jikan ni naru to, tennai ni Hotaru no Hikari ga nagareru.","german":"When closing time comes, \"Hotaru no Hikari\" plays inside the store.","furigana":"へいてん|じかん|てんない|ほたる|ひかり|なが"}],"notes":"蛍 is the \"firefly\", often written in kana ほたる/ホタル. It is a symbol of early summer (June-July) on clean rivers; watching fireflies is called 蛍狩り. The song 蛍の光 (to the melody of \"Auld Lang Syne\") plays in many shops shortly before closing time. In words such as 蛍光灯 and 蛍光ペン the kanji is read けい.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"蝉が鳴いています。","romaji":"Semi ga naite imasu.","german":"Cicadas are chirping.","furigana":"せみ|な"},{"japanese":"夏休みの朝は、蝉の声で目が覚める。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi no asa wa, semi no koe de me ga sameru.","german":"On summer vacation mornings, I wake up to the sound of cicadas.","furigana":"なつやす|あさ|せみ|こえ|め|さ"},{"japanese":"子どもの頃、よく公園で蝉を捕まえた。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, yoku kouen de semi o tsukamaeta.","german":"When I was a child, I often caught cicadas in the park.","furigana":"こ|ころ|こうえん|せみ|つか"}],"notes":"蝉 is the \"cicada\", often written in kana セミ. Its loud chirping (蝉が鳴く, 蝉の声) is the typical sound of high summer in Japan. Children like to catch cicadas in the park (蝉を捕まえる), and their empty larval shells are called 蝉の抜け殻. For the sound, as with all animals, the verb 鳴く is used.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"蟹を食べました。","romaji":"Kani o tabemashita.","german":"I ate crab.","furigana":"かに|た"},{"japanese":"冬になると、北海道へ蟹を食べに行く人が多い。","romaji":"Fuyu ni naru to, Hokkaidou e kani o tabe ni iku hito ga ooi.","german":"When winter comes, many people go to Hokkaido to eat crab.","furigana":"ふゆ|ほっかいどう|かに|た|い|ひと|おお"},{"japanese":"砂浜で小さい蟹が横に歩いていた。","romaji":"Sunahama de chiisai kani ga yoko ni aruite ita.","german":"A small crab was walking sideways on the sandy beach.","furigana":"すなはま|ちい|かに|よこ|ある"}],"notes":"蟹 is the \"crab\" with claws; on menus it is mostly written カニ or かに. It is eaten as a delicacy especially in winter, for example from Hokkaido; in compounds かに becomes がに (毛ガニ, タラバガニ). Walking sideways is jokingly called カニ歩き. Shrimp and lobster, by contrast, are 海老.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"海老の天ぷらが好きです。","romaji":"Ebi no tenpura ga suki desu.","german":"I like shrimp tempura.","furigana":"えび|てん|す"},{"japanese":"正月の料理には、長生きを願って海老を入れる。","romaji":"Shougatsu no ryouri ni wa, nagaiki o negatte ebi o ireru.","german":"Shrimp is included in New Year's dishes in the hope of a long life.","furigana":"しょうがつ|りょうり|ながい|ねが|えび|い"},{"japanese":"海老の殻をむくのは面倒だ。","romaji":"Ebi no kara o muku no wa mendou da.","german":"Peeling the shells off shrimp is a hassle.","furigana":"えび|から|めんどう"}],"notes":"海老 covers prawns, shrimp and also lobsters or spiny lobsters (伊勢海老); it is often written in kana エビ. The spelling literally means \"old person of the sea\" because the curved back resembles an old person, which is why shrimp in the New Year's meal stand for a wish for a long life. Popular are 海老フライ and 海老の天ぷら. Crabs with claws, by contrast, are 蟹.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"烏賊の刺身を食べます。","romaji":"Ika no sashimi o tabemasu.","german":"I eat squid sashimi.","furigana":"いか|さしみ|た"},{"japanese":"お祭りの屋台でいか焼きを買いました。","romaji":"Omatsuri no yatai de ikayaki o kaimashita.","german":"I bought grilled squid at a stall at the festival.","furigana":"まつ|やたい|や|か"},{"japanese":"いかとたこの違いは足の数です。","romaji":"Ika to tako no chigai wa ashi no kazu desu.","german":"The difference between squid and octopus is the number of legs.","furigana":"ちが|あし|かず"}],"notes":"烏賊 \"squid, cuttlefish\" is almost always written in kana in everyday life, as いか or イカ; the kanji is seen mainly on the menus of sushi restaurants. Typical combinations are いかの刺身, いか焼き (grilled squid at a festival stall) and イカリング (fried squid rings). Cuttlefish and squid are both called いか; the eight-armed octopus, by contrast, is たこ.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"たこ焼きが好きです。","romaji":"Takoyaki ga suki desu.","german":"I like takoyaki.","furigana":"や|す"},{"japanese":"スーパーでゆでたこを買って、サラダに入れました。","romaji":"Suupaa de yudedako o katte, sarada ni iremashita.","german":"I bought boiled octopus at the supermarket and put it in a salad."},{"japanese":"水族館で大きなたこを見ました。","romaji":"Suizokukan de ookina tako o mimashita.","german":"I saw a big octopus at the aquarium.","furigana":"すいぞくかん|おお|み"}],"notes":"蛸 \"octopus\" is normally written in kana: たこ or タコ. Best known is たこ焼き, batter balls with pieces of octopus from Osaka; in addition, ゆでだこ (boiled octopus) is eaten as sushi or in salads. Be careful when listening: the identically sounding 凧 (たこ) is a paper kite, and colloquially たこ is also used as an insult meaning \"fool\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鯛のお刺身です。","romaji":"Tai no osashimi desu.","german":"It is sea bream sashimi.","furigana":"たい|さしみ"},{"japanese":"お祝いの日には鯛を焼いて食べます。","romaji":"Oiwai no hi ni wa tai o yaite tabemasu.","german":"On celebration days we grill and eat sea bream.","furigana":"いわ|ひ|たい|や|た"},{"japanese":"たい焼きには魚は入っていません。","romaji":"Taiyaki ni wa sakana wa haitte imasen.","german":"Taiyaki has no fish in it.","furigana":"や|さかな|はい"}],"notes":"鯛 is the red sea bream and, in Japan, the lucky fish par excellence, because たい is reminiscent of めでたい \"auspicious\". It is served at weddings, at New Year and at celebrations, often whole, salted and grilled (鯛の塩焼き) or as 鯛めし. It is usually written 鯛 or たい; the sweet たい焼き only has the shape of the fish and contains no fish.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鮪の寿司が一番好きです。","romaji":"Maguro no sushi ga ichiban suki desu.","german":"Tuna sushi is my favorite.","furigana":"まぐろ|すし|いちばんす"},{"japanese":"回転寿司でまぐろを三皿も食べました。","romaji":"Kaitenzushi de maguro o sansara mo tabemashita.","german":"At the conveyor-belt sushi place, I ate as many as three plates of tuna.","furigana":"かいてんずし|さんさら|た"},{"japanese":"港でとれたばかりのまぐろを見ました。","romaji":"Minato de toreta bakari no maguro o mimashita.","german":"I saw freshly caught tuna at the harbor.","furigana":"みなと|み"}],"notes":"鮪 \"tuna\" is mostly written in kana (まぐろ / マグロ); only in sushi restaurants is the kanji often used. By fat content one distinguishes 赤身 (lean red meat), 中トロ and 大トロ (very fatty belly meat). For canned tuna, by contrast, one says ツナ, as in ツナ缶; まぐろ means the fresh fish.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鮭のおにぎりを食べます。","romaji":"Sake no onigiri o tabemasu.","german":"I eat salmon rice balls.","furigana":"さけ|た"},{"japanese":"朝ご飯に焼き鮭とみそ汁を食べました。","romaji":"Asagohan ni yakizake to misoshiru o tabemashita.","german":"For breakfast I had grilled salmon and miso soup.","furigana":"あさ|はん|や||しる|た"},{"japanese":"秋になると、鮭が川を上ってきます。","romaji":"Aki ni naru to, sake ga kawa o nobotte kimasu.","german":"When autumn comes, salmon swim up the rivers.","furigana":"あき|さけ|かわ|のぼ"}],"notes":"鮭 \"salmon\" is also pronounced しゃけ, especially colloquially and in dishes such as 鮭おにぎり or 焼き鮭. Salted, grilled salmon is part of the classic Japanese breakfast. As a sushi topping, especially for farmed salmon, people mostly say サーモン. Caution: お酒 (さけ) \"alcohol\" sounds the same but differs in pitch accent.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鰹節でだしを取ります。","romaji":"Katsuobushi de dashi o torimasu.","german":"I make dashi with bonito flakes.","furigana":"かつおぶし|と"},{"japanese":"高知でかつおのたたきを食べました。","romaji":"Kouchi de katsuo no tataki o tabemashita.","german":"In Kochi, I ate seared bonito.","furigana":"こうち|た"},{"japanese":"春から初夏にかけて、かつおがおいしくなります。","romaji":"Haru kara shoka ni kakete, katsuo ga oishiku narimasu.","german":"From spring to early summer, bonito tastes especially good.","furigana":"はる|しょか"}],"notes":"鰹 is the skipjack tuna (bonito), a close relative of tuna. Most important is its use as 鰹節 (かつおぶし): dried and shaved into flakes, it forms the base of many だし. Fresh, it is enjoyed as かつおのたたき, briefly seared on the outside. In everyday life it is often written かつお in kana; 初鰹, the first bonito of early summer, is regarded as a delicacy.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"出汁を作ります。","romaji":"Dashi o tsukurimasu.","german":"I make dashi.","furigana":"だし|つく"},{"japanese":"昆布とかつお節でだしを取りました。","romaji":"Konbu to katsuobushi de dashi o torimashita.","german":"I made stock with kelp and bonito flakes.","furigana":"こんぶ|ぶし|と"},{"japanese":"この店のうどんはだしがおいしいです。","romaji":"Kono mise no udon wa dashi ga oishii desu.","german":"The udon at this restaurant has delicious broth.","furigana":"みせ"}],"notes":"出汁 is the Japanese basic stock, usually made from kombu and 鰹節, and is almost always written in kana: だし. The fixed combination is だしを取る \"to extract a stock\"; ready-made granules are called だしの素. Unlike スープ (Western soup) or 汁 (soup as a dish), だし is the base from which miso soup, noodle broths and simmered dishes are made.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"味醂を入れます。","romaji":"Mirin o iremasu.","german":"I add mirin.","furigana":"みりん|い"},{"japanese":"照り焼きのたれは、しょうゆとみりんと砂糖で作ります。","romaji":"Teriyaki no tare wa, shouyu to mirin to satou de tsukurimasu.","german":"Teriyaki sauce is made from soy sauce, mirin and sugar.","furigana":"て|や|さとう|つく"},{"japanese":"みりんがなくなったので、スーパーで買ってきます。","romaji":"Mirin ga nakunatta node, suupaa de katte kimasu.","german":"We have run out of mirin, so I will go buy some at the supermarket.","furigana":"か"}],"notes":"味醂 \"mirin\" is sweet rice wine for cooking and is almost always written みりん. Together with しょうゆ, 砂糖 and 酒, it forms the base of many sauces, such as for 照り焼き or 煮物, and gives sweetness and gloss. It is not drunk; cheap substitutes with little alcohol are called みりん風調味料.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"薬味を添えます。","romaji":"Yakumi o soemasu.","german":"I add the condiments on the side.","furigana":"やくみ|そ"},{"japanese":"そばの薬味には、ねぎとわさびを使います。","romaji":"Soba no yakumi ni wa, negi to wasabi o tsukaimasu.","german":"For soba, scallions and wasabi are used as condiments.","furigana":"やくみ|つか"},{"japanese":"薬味はお好みで入れてください。","romaji":"Yakumi wa okonomi de irete kudasai.","german":"Please add the condiments as you like.","furigana":"やくみ|この|い"}],"notes":"薬味 are small, mostly fresh seasoning ingredients that are added to noodles, tofu or sashimi: chopped ねぎ, grated ginger, わさび, みょうが, 大葉 or 七味. So it is neither \"spice\" in general (スパイス, 香辛料) nor a seasoning such as soy sauce or salt (調味料). Typical are 薬味を添える, 薬味を入れる and, on menus, 薬味はお好みで.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"握り寿司を注文します。","romaji":"Nigirizushi o chūmon shimasu.","german":"I will order nigiri sushi.","furigana":"にぎ|ずし|ちゅうもん"},{"japanese":"この店の握り寿司は一皿に二貫ずつのっています。","romaji":"Kono mise no nigirizushi wa hitosara ni nikan zutsu notte imasu.","german":"At this restaurant, nigiri sushi comes two pieces per plate.","furigana":"みせ|にぎ|ずし|ひとさら|にかん"},{"japanese":"職人さんが目の前で握り寿司を作ってくれました。","romaji":"Shokunin-san ga me no mae de nigirizushi o tsukutte kuremashita.","german":"The chef made nigiri sushi for me right in front of my eyes.","furigana":"しょくにん|め|まえ|にぎ|ずし|つく"}],"notes":"握り寿司 is sushi in which a topping (ネタ) is placed on a hand-shaped ball of rice; the name comes from 握る \"to form by hand, press\". Note the voicing: すし becomes ずし in the compound, as in 巻き寿司 and ちらし寿司. In restaurants the pieces are counted with 〜貫 (一貫, 二貫); in everyday speech people often just say にぎり.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"巻き寿司を作りましょう。","romaji":"Makizushi o tsukurimashō.","german":"Let's make sushi rolls.","furigana":"ま|ずし|つく"},{"japanese":"お弁当に巻き寿司を入れました。","romaji":"Obentou ni makizushi o iremashita.","german":"I put sushi rolls in the lunch box.","furigana":"べんとう|ま|ずし|い"},{"japanese":"節分には太い巻き寿司を食べる習慣があります。","romaji":"Setsubun ni wa futoi makizushi o taberu shuukan ga arimasu.","german":"It is a custom to eat thick sushi rolls on Setsubun.","furigana":"せつぶん|ふと|ま|ずし|た|しゅうかん"}],"notes":"巻き寿司 is sushi rolled up with nori seaweed (海苔) and then cut into pieces; 巻く means \"to wrap, roll\". Thin rolls with one filling are called 細巻き (e.g. かっぱ巻き with cucumber), thick ones with many ingredients 太巻き. On 節分, in many regions people eat an uncut roll as 恵方巻き.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"年越し蕎麦を食べます。","romaji":"Toshikoshi soba o tabemasu.","german":"I eat toshikoshi soba (New Year's Eve noodles).","furigana":"としこ|そば|た"},{"japanese":"暑い日は冷たいざるそばが食べたくなります。","romaji":"Atsui hi wa tsumetai zarusoba ga tabetaku narimasu.","german":"On hot days I feel like eating cold zaru soba.","furigana":"あつ|ひ|つめ|た"},{"japanese":"駅の立ち食いそばで昼ご飯を済ませました。","romaji":"Eki no tachigui soba de hirugohan o sumasemashita.","german":"I had lunch at a standing soba shop in the station.","furigana":"えき|た|ぐ|ひる|はん|す"}],"notes":"蕎麦 \"buckwheat noodles\" is mostly written in kana (そば), but on restaurant signs you often see 蕎麦. They are eaten cold with a dipping sauce (ざるそば) or hot in broth (かけそば), and slurping is perfectly normal. On New Year's Eve people eat 年越しそば for a long life. Caution: 焼きそば are fried wheat noodles, and そば (側) \"beside\" is a different word.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"温かいうどんを食べます。","romaji":"Atatakai udon o tabemasu.","german":"I eat warm udon.","furigana":"あたた|た"},{"japanese":"風邪のときは、やわらかいうどんがいいですよ。","romaji":"Kaze no toki wa, yawarakai udon ga ii desu yo.","german":"When you have a cold, soft udon is just right.","furigana":"かぜ"},{"japanese":"香川は讃岐うどんで有名です。","romaji":"Kagawa wa Sanuki udon de yuumei desu.","german":"Kagawa is famous for its Sanuki udon.","furigana":"かがわ||ゆうめい"}],"notes":"饂飩 \"udon\" are thick wheat noodles; the difficult kanji is practically never used, and it is written うどん. Well-known varieties are きつねうどん (with sweetly simmered fried tofu), 天ぷらうどん and 讃岐うどん from Kagawa. Unlike そば (buckwheat), うどん are made of wheat flour and are thicker and softer; they are eaten hot or cold.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"和菓子と抹茶を頂きます。","romaji":"Wagashi to matcha o itadakimasu.","german":"I will have Japanese sweets and matcha.","furigana":"わがし|まっちゃ|いただ"},{"japanese":"京都のお土産に和菓子を買いました。","romaji":"Kyouto no omiyage ni wagashi o kaimashita.","german":"I bought Japanese sweets as a souvenir from Kyoto.","furigana":"きょうと|みやげ|わがし|か"},{"japanese":"洋菓子より和菓子のほうが好きです。","romaji":"Yougashi yori wagashi no hou ga suki desu.","german":"I like Japanese sweets better than Western sweets.","furigana":"ようがし|わがし|す"}],"notes":"和菓子 are traditional Japanese sweets such as 大福, 羊羹 or 団子, often made from sweet bean paste (あんこ) and rice flour. The opposite is 洋菓子 \"Western pastries\" such as cakes or cookies; 和 here stands for \"Japanese\", as in 和食. 和菓子 is often served with green tea, especially at the tea ceremony, and many kinds are available only in a certain season.","levelNote":"Compound: 和 (N2) + 菓子 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"抹茶が好きです。","romaji":"Matcha ga suki desu.","german":"I like matcha.","furigana":"まっちゃ|す"},{"japanese":"抹茶味のアイスを一つください。","romaji":"Matcha aji no aisu o hitotsu kudasai.","german":"One matcha-flavored ice cream, please.","furigana":"まっちゃあじ|ひと"},{"japanese":"茶道の教室で抹茶の点て方を習いました。","romaji":"Sadou no kyoushitsu de matcha no tatekata o naraimashita.","german":"I learned how to whisk matcha at a tea ceremony class.","furigana":"さどう|きょうしつ|まっちゃ|た|かた|なら"}],"notes":"抹茶 is finely ground green tea that is not steeped but whisked into a froth with hot water; in the tea ceremony (茶道) this is called 抹茶を点てる. In everyday life it appears mainly as a flavor: 抹茶アイス, 抹茶ラテ, 抹茶味. Ordinary leaf green tea, by contrast, is 緑茶 or simply お茶.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"番茶を飲みます。","romaji":"Bancha o nomimasu.","german":"I drink bancha.","furigana":"ばんちゃ|の"},{"japanese":"番茶はカフェインが少ないので、夜でも飲めます。","romaji":"Bancha wa kafein ga sukunai node, yoru demo nomemasu.","german":"Bancha has little caffeine, so you can drink it even in the evening.","furigana":"ばんちゃ|すく|よる|の"},{"japanese":"祖母の家では、食事のあとにいつも番茶が出ます。","romaji":"Sobo no ie de wa, shokuji no ato ni itsumo bancha ga demasu.","german":"At my grandmother's house, bancha is always served after meals.","furigana":"そぼ|いえ|しょくじ|ばんちゃ|で"}],"notes":"番茶 is a simple, inexpensive green tea made from late, coarser leaves; it tastes mild and contains little caffeine. It is drunk as an everyday tea with meals, in contrast to the finer 煎茶 or the premium 玉露. Roasted, it is often made into ほうじ茶. In conversation people usually just say お茶; 番茶 is used when the type matters.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"焼酎をロックで飲みます。","romaji":"Shōchū o rokku de nomimasu.","german":"I drink shochu on the rocks.","furigana":"しょうちゅう|の"},{"japanese":"寒い日は焼酎のお湯割りが一番です。","romaji":"Samui hi wa shouchuu no oyuwari ga ichiban desu.","german":"On cold days, shochu with hot water is the best.","furigana":"さむ|ひ|しょうちゅう|ゆわ|いちばん"},{"japanese":"鹿児島のお土産に芋焼酎をもらいました。","romaji":"Kagoshima no omiyage ni imojouchuu o moraimashita.","german":"I received sweet potato shochu as a souvenir from Kagoshima.","furigana":"かごしま|みやげ|いもじょうちゅう"}],"notes":"焼酎 is a Japanese distilled liquor made from sweet potatoes (芋焼酎), barley (麦焼酎), rice or other ingredients. Unlike 日本酒, which is brewed, it is a clear spirit, usually around 25% alcohol. It is drunk ロックで (on the rocks), 水割り (with water), お湯割り (with hot water) or mixed as チューハイ; shochu from Kyushu is especially famous.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本酒を一杯ください。","romaji":"Nihonshu o ippai kudasai.","german":"One glass of sake, please.","furigana":"にほんしゅ|いっぱい"},{"japanese":"この日本酒は冷やして飲むとおいしいです。","romaji":"Kono nihonshu wa hiyashite nomu to oishii desu.","german":"This sake tastes good chilled.","furigana":"にほんしゅ|ひ|の"},{"japanese":"日本酒は米と水から作られます。","romaji":"Nihonshu wa kome to mizu kara tsukuraremasu.","german":"Sake is made from rice and water.","furigana":"にほんしゅ|こめ|みず|つく"}],"notes":"日本酒 is Japanese rice wine brewed from rice, known internationally as \"sake\". In Japanese, however, お酒 means any \"alcoholic drink\" in general; so those who specifically mean rice wine say 日本酒 (formally also 清酒). It is drunk chilled (冷酒), at room temperature or hot (熱燗); the amount is counted with 一杯 or 一合.","levelNote":"Compound: 日本 (N5) + 酒 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"甘酒を温めて飲みます。","romaji":"Amazake o atatamete nomimasu.","german":"I warm up amazake and drink it.","furigana":"あまざけ|あたた|の"},{"japanese":"初詣のとき、神社で甘酒をもらいました。","romaji":"Hatsumoude no toki, jinja de amazake o moraimashita.","german":"At my New Year's shrine visit, I received amazake at the shrine.","furigana":"はつもうで|じんじゃ|あまざけ"},{"japanese":"この甘酒はアルコールが入っていないので、子どもも飲めます。","romaji":"Kono amazake wa arukooru ga haitte inai node, kodomo mo nomemasu.","german":"This amazake contains no alcohol, so children can drink it too.","furigana":"あまざけ|はい|こ|の"}],"notes":"甘酒 is a sweet, thick drink made from fermented rice (with 麹) or from sake lees (酒粕). Despite 酒 in the name, the koji version contains no alcohol and is also given to children; the sake-lees version contains only a trace. Traditionally it is drunk warm at New Year or at shrines, and in summer chilled as a tonic drink.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今週の献立を考えます。","romaji":"Konshū no kondate o kangaemasu.","german":"I am planning this week's menu.","furigana":"こんしゅう|こんだて|かんが"},{"japanese":"学校の給食の献立が配られました。","romaji":"Gakkou no kyuushoku no kondate ga kubararemashita.","german":"The school lunch menu was handed out.","furigana":"がっこう|きゅうしょく|こんだて|くば"},{"japanese":"冷蔵庫にある物で今夜の献立を決めました。","romaji":"Reizouko ni aru mono de kon'ya no kondate o kimemashita.","german":"I decided tonight's menu based on what was in the fridge.","furigana":"れいぞうこ|もの|こんや|こんだて|き"}],"notes":"献立 is the combination of dishes for a meal or a period, that is, the meal plan: 今日の献立, 一週間の献立, 給食の献立. Typical are 献立を考える and 献立を立てる \"to draw up a menu plan\". For the menu in a restaurant, by contrast, one says メニュー; 献立 sounds like the household, the school cafeteria or a cookbook.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"旬の野菜を食べます。","romaji":"Shun no yasai o tabemasu.","german":"I eat seasonal vegetables.","furigana":"しゅん|やさい|た"},{"japanese":"さんまは秋が旬です。","romaji":"Sanma wa aki ga shun desu.","german":"Pacific saury is in season in autumn.","furigana":"あき|しゅん"},{"japanese":"今、旬の俳優といえば彼でしょう。","romaji":"Ima, shun no haiyuu to ieba kare deshou.","german":"If we talk about the actor of the moment, it would be him.","furigana":"いま|しゅん|はいゆう|かれ"}],"notes":"旬 is the best time for a food, when it is freshest and tastiest: 旬の野菜, 旬の魚, 今が旬だ \"it is in season now\". It is narrower than 季節 \"season\", because 旬 stresses the peak of flavor. Figuratively, currently popular people or topics are also called 旬, for example 旬のタレント.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"産地直送の野菜です。","romaji":"Sanchi chokusō no yasai desu.","german":"These are vegetables direct from the producer.","furigana":"さんちちょくそう|やさい"},{"japanese":"買う前に、肉の産地を確認します。","romaji":"Kau mae ni, niku no sanchi o kakunin shimasu.","german":"Before buying meat, I check where it comes from.","furigana":"か|まえ|にく|さんち|かくにん"},{"japanese":"青森はりんごの産地として有名です。","romaji":"Aomori wa ringo no sanchi to shite yuumei desu.","german":"Aomori is famous as an apple-producing region.","furigana":"あおもり|さんち|ゆうめい"}],"notes":"産地 is the place where something is produced or grown: 米の産地, ワインの産地, みかんの産地. On packaging you find 産地 or 原産地 \"country of origin\", and 産地直送 means \"delivered directly from the producer\". The word sounds mainly like agriculture, fish and regional specialties; for industrial products people rather speak of 製造国 or 生産地.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"有機野菜を買います。","romaji":"Yūki yasai o kaimasu.","german":"I buy organic vegetables.","furigana":"ゆうきやさい|か"},{"japanese":"この店では有機栽培のコーヒーを売っています。","romaji":"Kono mise de wa yuuki saibai no koohii o utte imasu.","german":"This store sells organically grown coffee.","furigana":"みせ|ゆうきさいばい|う"},{"japanese":"祖父は畑で有機農業をしています。","romaji":"Sofu wa hatake de yuuki nougyou o shite imasu.","german":"My grandfather does organic farming in his field.","furigana":"そふ|はたけ|ゆうきのうぎょう"}],"notes":"有機 \"organic\" almost always stands as the first element before a noun: 有機野菜 \"organic vegetables\", 有機栽培 \"organic cultivation\", 有機農業. It is neither an い- nor a な-adjective: you don't say 有機な野菜 but 有機野菜 or, more rarely, 有機の野菜. In everyday speech you also often hear オーガニック for organic products; in chemistry the opposite is 無機 \"inorganic\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"添加物なしの食品です。","romaji":"Tenkabutsu nashi no shokuhin desu.","german":"It is a food without additives.","furigana":"てんかぶつ|しょくひん"},{"japanese":"子どものおやつは、なるべく添加物の少ないものを選んでいます。","romaji":"Kodomo no oyatsu wa, narubeku tenkabutsu no sukunai mono o erande imasu.","german":"For my children's snacks, I choose ones with as few additives as possible.","furigana":"こ|てんかぶつ|すく|えら"},{"japanese":"ラベルを見ると、たくさんの添加物が入っていました。","romaji":"Raberu o miru to, takusan no tenkabutsu ga haitte imashita.","german":"When I looked at the label, it contained many additives.","furigana":"み|てんかぶつ|はい"}],"notes":"添加物 are additives in food such as preservatives or colorings; the full term is 食品添加物. Typical expressions are 添加物が入っている, 添加物を避ける and 無添加 \"additive-free\", which is often printed large on packaging. The verb 添加する \"to add\" is technical; in everyday speech people simply say 入れる for ingredients.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"賞味期限を確認してください。","romaji":"Shōmikigen o kakunin shite kudasai.","german":"Please check the best-before date.","furigana":"しょうみきげん|かくにん"},{"japanese":"この牛乳、賞味期限が切れているよ。","romaji":"Kono gyuunyuu, shoumikigen ga kirete iru yo.","german":"This milk is past its best-before date.","furigana":"ぎゅうにゅう|しょうみきげん|き"},{"japanese":"賞味期限が近い商品は安くなっています。","romaji":"Shoumikigen ga chikai shouhin wa yasuku natte imasu.","german":"Products close to their best-before date are discounted.","furigana":"しょうみきげん|ちか|しょうひん|やす"}],"notes":"賞味期限 is the best-before date: until then a product is guaranteed to taste good, and afterwards it is often still edible. To be distinguished from it is 消費期限 \"use-by date\" for perishable goods such as meat or bento, after which you should not eat anything. Typical phrases are 賞味期限が切れる \"to expire\", 賞味期限切れ \"expired\" and 賞味期限が近い.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"冷凍食品を買います。","romaji":"Reitō shokuhin o kaimasu.","german":"I buy frozen food.","furigana":"れいとうしょくひん|か"},{"japanese":"残ったカレーを冷凍しておきました。","romaji":"Nokotta karee o reitou shite okimashita.","german":"I froze the leftover curry.","furigana":"のこ|れいとう"},{"japanese":"アイスは冷凍庫に入れてください。","romaji":"Aisu wa reitouko ni irete kudasai.","german":"Please put the ice cream in the freezer.","furigana":"れいとうこ|い"}],"notes":"冷凍 means \"freezing, deep-freezing\" and is common as a transitive する-verb: 肉を冷凍する, 残りを冷凍しておく. It often stands at the front of compounds such as 冷凍食品, 冷凍庫 \"freezer\" and 冷凍保存. The opposite is 解凍 \"to thaw\"; mere cooling in the refrigerator, by contrast, is 冷蔵 (冷蔵庫). When something freezes by itself, the intransitive 凍る is used.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"肉を解凍します。","romaji":"Niku o kaitō shimasu.","german":"I thaw the meat.","furigana":"にく|かいとう"},{"japanese":"冷凍のパンは電子レンジで解凍すると便利です。","romaji":"Reitou no pan wa denshi renji de kaitou suru to benri desu.","german":"It is convenient to thaw frozen bread in the microwave.","furigana":"れいとう|でんし|かいとう|べんり"},{"japanese":"ダウンロードしたファイルを解凍してください。","romaji":"Daunroodo shita fairu o kaitou shite kudasai.","german":"Please unzip the downloaded file.","furigana":"かいとう"}],"notes":"解凍 means \"to thaw\" and is very common as a transitive する-verb: 冷凍の肉を解凍する, 電子レンジで解凍する, 自然解凍 \"letting it thaw at room temperature\". The opposite is 冷凍 \"to freeze\". On a computer, 解凍 also means \"to unpack a compressed file\" (ZIPファイルを解凍する); the counterpart is 圧縮.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"食中毒に気をつけてください。","romaji":"Shokuchūdoku ni ki o tsukete kudasai.","german":"Please be careful about food poisoning.","furigana":"しょくちゅうどく|き"},{"japanese":"夏は食べ物が傷みやすいので、食中毒が増えます。","romaji":"Natsu wa tabemono ga itamiyasui node, shokuchuudoku ga fuemasu.","german":"In summer food spoils easily, so food poisoning increases.","furigana":"なつ|た|もの|いた|しょくちゅうどく|ふ"},{"japanese":"生の貝を食べて、食中毒になってしまいました。","romaji":"Nama no kai o tabete, shokuchuudoku ni natte shimaimashita.","german":"I ate raw shellfish and got food poisoning.","furigana":"なま|かい|た|しょくちゅうどく"}],"notes":"食中毒 is food poisoning caused by bacteria, viruses or toxins in food. Typical are 食中毒になる \"to get food poisoning\", 食中毒を起こす (said of foods or restaurants) and 食中毒に気をつける. Especially in the humid summer, news and schools warn about it. The shorter 中毒 means \"poisoning\" in general, but also \"addiction\" (ゲーム中毒).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"教壇に立ちます。","romaji":"Kyōdan ni tachimasu.","german":"I stand in front of the class as a teacher.","furigana":"きょうだん|た"},{"japanese":"父は三十年間、高校の教壇に立ちました。","romaji":"Chichi wa sanjuunenkan, koukou no kyoudan ni tachimashita.","german":"My father taught at a high school for thirty years.","furigana":"ちち|さんじゅうねんかん|こうこう|きょうだん|た"},{"japanese":"先生が教壇から下りて、生徒の席まで来ました。","romaji":"Sensei ga kyoudan kara orite, seito no seki made kimashita.","german":"The teacher stepped down from the platform and came over to the students' seats.","furigana":"せんせい|きょうだん|お|せいと|せき|き"}],"notes":"教壇 is the raised platform at the front of the classroom on which the teacher stands; the desk on it is called 教卓. Very common is the figurative phrase 教壇に立つ \"to teach as a teacher\", e.g. 三十年間教壇に立つ, as well as 教壇を去る \"to leave teaching\". The word sounds somewhat elevated; in modern schools the platform itself often no longer exists.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"辞典で調べます。","romaji":"Jiten de shirabemasu.","german":"I look it up in the dictionary.","furigana":"じてん|しら"},{"japanese":"図書館で国語辞典を借りました。","romaji":"Toshokan de kokugo jiten o karimashita.","german":"I borrowed a Japanese dictionary from the library.","furigana":"としょかん|こくごじてん|か"},{"japanese":"この辞典には例文がたくさん載っています。","romaji":"Kono jiten ni wa reibun ga takusan notte imasu.","german":"This dictionary contains many example sentences.","furigana":"じてん|れいぶん|の"}],"notes":"辞典 means \"dictionary\" and appears mainly in titles and fixed designations: 国語辞典 (Japanese dictionary), 英和辞典 (English-Japanese). In everyday speech, 辞書 is usually said; the same thing is meant. The identically pronounced 事典, on the other hand, is an encyclopedia of subjects (百科事典). To look something up is 辞典で調べる or 辞典を引く.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"百科事典で調べました。","romaji":"Hyakkajiten de shirabemashita.","german":"I looked it up in the encyclopedia.","furigana":"ひゃっかじてん|しら"},{"japanese":"図書館には全二十巻の百科事典があります。","romaji":"Toshokan ni wa zen nijukkan no hyakkajiten ga arimasu.","german":"The library has a twenty-volume encyclopedia.","furigana":"としょかん|ぜんにじゅっかん|ひゃっかじてん"},{"japanese":"兄は何でも知っていて、まるで歩く百科事典です。","romaji":"Ani wa nan demo shitte ite, marude aruku hyakkajiten desu.","german":"My older brother knows everything; he is a walking encyclopedia.","furigana":"あに|なん|し|ある|ひゃっかじてん"}],"notes":"百科事典 is an encyclopedia covering all fields of knowledge (百科 \"a hundred subjects\"). Typical is 百科事典で調べる \"to look something up in the encyclopedia\"; today it often also means online encyclopedias. Note the kanji: 事典 explains things, whereas 辞典 or 辞書 explains words. A person who knows everything is jokingly called 歩く百科事典 \"walking encyclopedia\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"講義を受けます。","romaji":"Kōgi o ukemasu.","german":"I attend a lecture.","furigana":"こうぎ|う"},{"japanese":"田中先生の講義はいつも面白いです。","romaji":"Tanaka sensei no kougi wa itsumo omoshiroi desu.","german":"Professor Tanaka's lectures are always interesting.","furigana":"たなかせんせい|こうぎ|おもしろ"},{"japanese":"寝坊して、朝の講義に出られませんでした。","romaji":"Nebou shite, asa no kougi ni deraremasen deshita.","german":"I overslept and couldn't attend the morning lecture.","furigana":"ねぼう|あさ|こうぎ|で"}],"notes":"講義 is a lecture at a university or college. Students attend it with 講義を受ける or 講義に出る, and the lecturer gives it with 講義をする. In school, 授業 is used instead; a public talk before an audience is 講演. The word is neutral and very common in student life (講義ノート, 講義室).","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 30"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"演習に参加します。","romaji":"Enshū ni sanka shimasu.","german":"I am taking part in the seminar.","furigana":"えんしゅう|さんか"},{"japanese":"教科書の後ろにある演習問題を解いてください。","romaji":"Kyoukasho no ushiro ni aru enshuu mondai o toite kudasai.","german":"Please solve the exercises at the back of the textbook.","furigana":"きょうかしょ|うし|えんしゅうもんだい|と"},{"japanese":"近くの海で軍の演習が行われました。","romaji":"Chikaku no umi de gun no enshuu ga okonawaremashita.","german":"A military exercise was held in the nearby sea.","furigana":"ちか|うみ|ぐん|えんしゅう|おこな"}],"notes":"演習 is a practical exercise for consolidating what has been learned, e.g. 演習問題 \"practice problems\". At universities it refers to a seminar or practice class as opposed to a lecture 講義, often in course names such as 英語演習. In the military, 演習 means maneuvers (軍事演習). For everyday \"to practice\" one more often says 練習する; 演習 sounds academic and formal.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"来年卒業します。","romaji":"Rainen sotsugyō shimasu.","german":"I will graduate next year.","furigana":"らいねんそつぎょう"},{"japanese":"大学を卒業したら、日本で働きたいです。","romaji":"Daigaku o sotsugyou shitara, Nihon de hatarakitai desu.","german":"After I graduate from university, I want to work in Japan.","furigana":"だいがく|そつぎょう|にほん|はたら"},{"japanese":"卒業式で両親が写真をたくさん撮ってくれました。","romaji":"Sotsugyoushiki de ryoushin ga shashin o takusan totte kuremashita.","german":"At the graduation ceremony, my parents took lots of photos.","furigana":"そつぎょうしき|りょうしん|しゃしん|と"}],"notes":"卒業 is graduation from a school or university. Although the verb is intransitive, the institution that is left takes を: 大学を卒業する. Common compounds are 卒業式 \"graduation ceremony\" and 卒業生 \"graduate\". Figuratively it also means \"to grow out of something\" (マンガを卒業する) or leaving a group, for example when an idol leaves their band. Counterpart: 入学.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大学に入学しました。","romaji":"Daigaku ni nyūgaku shimashita.","german":"I entered university.","furigana":"だいがく|にゅうがく"},{"japanese":"弟は来年の四月に小学校に入学します。","romaji":"Otouto wa rainen no shigatsu ni shougakkou ni nyuugaku shimasu.","german":"My younger brother will enter elementary school next April.","furigana":"おとうと|らいねん|しがつ|しょうがっこう|にゅうがく"},{"japanese":"入学試験は二月にあります。","romaji":"Nyuugaku shiken wa nigatsu ni arimasu.","german":"The entrance exam is in February.","furigana":"にゅうがくしけん|にがつ"}],"notes":"入学 is entry into a school or university; the institution takes に: 小学校に入学する, 大学に入学する. Since the Japanese school year begins in April, the entrance ceremony 入学式 usually takes place then. Further compounds are 入学試験 (short 入試) \"entrance exam\" and 入学祝い \"gift for starting school\". Counterpart: 卒業.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"部活に入りました。","romaji":"Bukatsu ni hairimashita.","german":"I joined a club.","furigana":"ぶかつ|はい"},{"japanese":"今日は部活があるので、帰りが遅くなります。","romaji":"Kyou wa bukatsu ga aru node, kaeri ga osoku narimasu.","german":"I have club activities today, so I will get home late.","furigana":"きょう|ぶかつ|かえ|おそ"},{"japanese":"中学時代、部活でバスケットボールをしていました。","romaji":"Chuugaku jidai, bukatsu de basukettobooru o shite imashita.","german":"In junior high, I played basketball in a club.","furigana":"ちゅうがくじだい|ぶかつ"}],"notes":"部活 is the short form of 部活動 and refers to club activities at junior high and high schools, such as サッカー部 or 吹奏楽部. You say 部活に入る \"to join a club\", 部活がある and 部活を休む; for a specific club usually 〜部に入る. A distinction is made between 運動部 (sports) and 文化部 (culture). The word is a very common, everyday student word.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"文化祭の準備をしています。","romaji":"Bunkasai no junbi o shite imasu.","german":"We are preparing for the cultural festival.","furigana":"ぶんかさい|じゅんび"},{"japanese":"文化祭でクラスのみんなと劇をしました。","romaji":"Bunkasai de kurasu no minna to geki o shimashita.","german":"At the cultural festival, I performed a play with my classmates.","furigana":"ぶんかさい|げき"},{"japanese":"妹の高校の文化祭に遊びに行きました。","romaji":"Imouto no koukou no bunkasai ni asobi ni ikimashita.","german":"I went to my younger sister's high school cultural festival.","furigana":"いもうと|こうこう|ぶんかさい|あそ|い"}],"notes":"文化祭 is a school's annual cultural festival, usually in autumn: classes run food stalls, perform plays or exhibit work, and outside visitors are welcome too. Typical are 文化祭の準備, 文化祭で劇をする and 文化祭に行く. At universities the festival is often called 学園祭 or 大学祭; the sporting counterpart is 体育祭.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"体育祭が楽しみです。","romaji":"Taiikusai ga tanoshimi desu.","german":"I am looking forward to the sports festival.","furigana":"たいいくさい|たの"},{"japanese":"体育祭のリレーでアンカーを走りました。","romaji":"Taiikusai no riree de ankaa o hashirimashita.","german":"I ran as the anchor in the relay at the sports festival.","furigana":"たいいくさい|はし"},{"japanese":"雨のため、体育祭は来週に延期されました。","romaji":"Ame no tame, taiikusai wa raishuu ni enki saremashita.","german":"Because of rain, the sports festival was postponed to next week.","furigana":"あめ|たいいくさい|らいしゅう|えんき"}],"notes":"体育祭 is the sports festival of a junior high or high school with races, relays and team games, often between teams such as 紅組 and 白組. In elementary schools the same event is usually called 運動会, a word that is also used for company or neighborhood events. Typical are 体育祭に出る, 体育祭のリレー and 体育祭が延期になる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"遠足に行きました。","romaji":"Ensoku ni ikimashita.","german":"I went on a school outing.","furigana":"えんそく|い"},{"japanese":"明日の遠足は動物園です。","romaji":"Ashita no ensoku wa doubutsuen desu.","german":"Tomorrow's outing is to the zoo.","furigana":"あした|えんそく|どうぶつえん"},{"japanese":"遠足のお弁当を母が作ってくれました。","romaji":"Ensoku no obentou o haha ga tsukutte kuremashita.","german":"My mother made my lunch box for the outing.","furigana":"えんそく|べんとう|はは|つく"}],"notes":"遠足 is a day trip that a kindergarten or school makes with the whole class, for example to a park, a zoo or a hill. Set expressions are 遠足に行く and 遠足のお弁当; legendary is the rule that sweets for a 遠足 may cost no more than 300 yen. Unlike 修学旅行 (a multi-day class trip), a 遠足 lasts only one day; a private excursion is rather called ハイキング or お出かけ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"修学旅行で京都に行きます。","romaji":"Shūgaku ryokō de Kyōto ni ikimasu.","german":"We are going to Kyoto on the school trip.","furigana":"しゅうがくりょこう|きょうと|い"},{"japanese":"修学旅行の夜は、友だちと遅くまで話しました。","romaji":"Shuugaku ryokou no yoru wa, tomodachi to osoku made hanashimashita.","german":"On the night of the school trip, I talked with my friends until late.","furigana":"しゅうがくりょこう|よる|とも|おそ|はな"},{"japanese":"修学旅行のお土産に八つ橋を買いました。","romaji":"Shuugaku ryokou no omiyage ni yatsuhashi o kaimashita.","german":"I bought yatsuhashi as a souvenir from the school trip.","furigana":"しゅうがくりょこう|みやげ|や|はし|か"}],"notes":"修学旅行 is the multi-day class trip that Japanese schools usually take in the last year of elementary, junior high or senior high school; popular destinations are Kyoto, Nara and Okinawa. One says 修学旅行に行く and 修学旅行で〜へ行く. The word is firmly tied to school: a training trip for company employees is called 研修旅行, and a one-day school outing is an 遠足.","levelNote":"Compound: 修学 (N1) + 旅行 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大学を受験します。","romaji":"Daigaku o juken shimasu.","german":"I am taking the university entrance exam.","furigana":"だいがく|じゅけん"},{"japanese":"受験勉強で毎日夜遅くまで起きています。","romaji":"Juken benkyou de mainichi yoru osoku made okite imasu.","german":"Because of exam preparation, I stay up late every night.","furigana":"じゅけんべんきょう|まいにちよるおそ|お"},{"japanese":"受験票を忘れないようにしてください。","romaji":"Jukenhyou o wasurenai you ni shite kudasai.","german":"Please make sure not to forget your exam admission ticket.","furigana":"じゅけんひょう|わす"}],"notes":"受験 means sitting an exam, especially an entrance exam for a school or university. As a する-verb, the school or exam takes を: 大学を受験する, 高校を受験する; for exams in general one also says 試験を受ける. Many compounds revolve around the dreaded exam period: 受験生 \"exam candidate\", 受験勉強, 受験票 \"exam admission ticket\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"試験に合格しました。","romaji":"Shiken ni gōkaku shimashita.","german":"I passed the exam.","furigana":"しけん|ごうかく"},{"japanese":"行きたかった大学に合格しました。","romaji":"Ikitakatta daigaku ni goukaku shimashita.","german":"I got into the university I wanted to go to.","furigana":"い|だいがく|ごうかく"},{"japanese":"この製品は安全検査に合格しています。","romaji":"Kono seihin wa anzen kensa ni goukaku shite imasu.","german":"This product has passed the safety inspection.","furigana":"せいひん|あんぜんけんさ|ごうかく"}],"notes":"合格 is passing an exam and is very common as an intransitive する-verb: 試験に合格する, 大学に合格する \"to be admitted to a university\". The goal is marked with に, not を. It also applies to inspections and standards, for example 検査に合格する. The opposite is 不合格; the announcement of results is 合格発表, and you can congratulate with 合格おめでとう.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"残念ながら不合格でした。","romaji":"Zannen nagara fugōkaku deshita.","german":"Unfortunately, I failed.","furigana":"ざんねん|ふごうかく"},{"japanese":"一点足りなくて、不合格になりました。","romaji":"Itten tarinakute, fugoukaku ni narimashita.","german":"I was one point short and failed.","furigana":"いってんた|ふごうかく"},{"japanese":"検査で不合格になった部品は使えません。","romaji":"Kensa de fugoukaku ni natta buhin wa tsukaemasen.","german":"Parts that fail the inspection cannot be used.","furigana":"けんさ|ふごうかく|ぶひん|つか"}],"notes":"不合格 is failing an exam or inspection. It is a pure noun without する: you say 不合格になる or 不合格だった, not 不合格する. In conversation, 試験に落ちる \"to fail an exam\" sounds more natural and personal; 不合格 appears more on notices and in objective reports. The opposite is 合格.","levelNote":"Compound: 不 (N3) + 合格 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"出席を取ります。","romaji":"Shusseki o torimasu.","german":"I will take attendance.","furigana":"しゅっせき|と"},{"japanese":"来週の会議には部長も出席します。","romaji":"Raishuu no kaigi ni wa buchou mo shusseki shimasu.","german":"The department head will also attend next week's meeting.","furigana":"らいしゅう|かいぎ|ぶちょう|しゅっせき"},{"japanese":"友だちの結婚式に出席しました。","romaji":"Tomodachi no kekkonshiki ni shusseki shimashita.","german":"I attended a friend's wedding.","furigana":"とも|けっこんしき|しゅっせき"}],"notes":"出席 is attendance at classes, meetings or celebrations; as an intransitive する-verb with に: 会議に出席する, 結婚式に出席する. 出席を取る means \"to check attendance\". In casual conversation people often simply say 出る (授業に出る); 出席 is more formal and polite and appears, for example, on invitation cards (ご出席). Opposite: 欠席.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"風邪で欠席します。","romaji":"Kaze de kesseki shimasu.","german":"I will be absent because of a cold.","furigana":"かぜ|けっせき"},{"japanese":"田中さんは今日の会議を欠席しています。","romaji":"Tanaka-san wa kyou no kaigi o kesseki shite imasu.","german":"Mr. Tanaka is absent from today's meeting.","furigana":"たなか|きょう|かいぎ|けっせき"},{"japanese":"欠席が多いと、単位がもらえません。","romaji":"Kesseki ga ooi to, tan'i ga moraemasen.","german":"If you have many absences, you can't get credit.","furigana":"けっせき|おお|たんい"}],"notes":"欠席 is being absent from classes, meetings or celebrations; as a する-verb, the missed event is marked with を: 授業を欠席する, 会議を欠席する, and the reason with で: 風邪で欠席する. For staying away from work, by contrast, people say 欠勤 or simply 休む. On reply cards to invitations you choose ご出席 or ご欠席; the opposite is 出席.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"来月から産休に入ります。","romaji":"Raigetsu kara sankyū ni hairimasu.","german":"I will start maternity leave next month.","furigana":"らいげつ|さんきゅう|はい"},{"japanese":"産休中の山田さんの仕事は、私が担当します。","romaji":"Sankyuu chuu no Yamada-san no shigoto wa, watashi ga tantou shimasu.","german":"While Ms. Yamada is on maternity leave, I will take over her work.","furigana":"さんきゅうちゅう|やまだ|しごと|わたし|たんとう"},{"japanese":"姉は産休が終わったあと、すぐに職場に戻りました。","romaji":"Ane wa sankyuu ga owatta ato, sugu ni shokuba ni modorimashita.","german":"After her maternity leave ended, my older sister returned to her workplace right away.","furigana":"あね|さんきゅう|お|しょくば|もど"}],"notes":"産休 is the short form of 産前産後休業, the statutory maternity leave before and after childbirth. Typical phrases are 産休に入る \"to go on maternity leave\", 産休を取る and 産休中. It is usually followed by 育休 (parental leave), which fathers can also take; in conversation the two are often mentioned together: 産休・育休.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"育休を取りました。","romaji":"Ikukyū o torimashita.","german":"I took parental leave.","furigana":"いくきゅう|と"},{"japanese":"夫も三か月の育休を取ることにしました。","romaji":"Otto mo sankagetsu no ikukyuu o toru koto ni shimashita.","german":"My husband has also decided to take three months of parental leave.","furigana":"おっと|さん|げつ|いくきゅう|と"},{"japanese":"来年の四月に育休を終えて、仕事に戻る予定です。","romaji":"Rainen no shigatsu ni ikukyuu o oete, shigoto ni modoru yotei desu.","german":"I plan to end my parental leave next April and return to work.","furigana":"らいねん|しがつ|いくきゅう|お|しごと|もど|よてい"}],"notes":"育休 is the short form of 育児休業 \"parental leave\". You say 育休を取る, 育休中 and 育休から復帰する \"to return from parental leave\". Unlike 産休, which concerns only the mother around childbirth, both mothers and fathers can take 育休; fathers on parental leave are often called 男性の育休.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"派遣社員として働いています。","romaji":"Haken shain to shite hataraite imasu.","german":"I work as a temp.","furigana":"はけんしゃいん|はたら"},{"japanese":"国はその地域に医者を派遣しました。","romaji":"Kuni wa sono chiiki ni isha o haken shimashita.","german":"The government sent doctors to the region.","furigana":"くに|ちいき|いしゃ|はけん"},{"japanese":"派遣会社に登録して、仕事を探しています。","romaji":"Haken gaisha ni touroku shite, shigoto o sagashite imasu.","german":"I registered with a temp agency and am looking for work.","furigana":"はけんがいしゃ|とうろく|しごと|さが"}],"notes":"派遣 originally means \"to send out\" and is then a transitive する-verb: 医者を被災地に派遣する. In working life it mainly stands for agency (temp) work: 派遣社員 \"temp worker\", 派遣会社 \"temp agency\" and, briefly, 派遣で働く. The opposite term in working life is 正社員 \"regular employee\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"正社員になりたいです。","romaji":"Seishain ni naritai desu.","german":"I want to become a regular employee.","furigana":"せいしゃいん"},{"japanese":"三年間アルバイトをして、やっと正社員として採用されました。","romaji":"Sannenkan arubaito o shite, yatto seishain to shite saiyou saremashita.","german":"After working part-time for three years, I was finally hired as a regular employee.","furigana":"さんねんかん|せいしゃいん|さいよう"},{"japanese":"この会社は正社員を募集しています。","romaji":"Kono kaisha wa seishain o boshuu shite imasu.","german":"This company is recruiting regular employees.","furigana":"かいしゃ|せいしゃいん|ぼしゅう"}],"notes":"正社員 is a permanent full-time employee with an open-ended contract, social benefits and bonus. You say 正社員になる, 正社員として働く or 正社員として採用される. The opposites are 契約社員 (fixed-term), 派遣社員 (temp agency work) as well as パート and アルバイト; together these are called 非正規 \"non-regular employees\".","levelNote":"Compound: 正 (N3) + 社員 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自営業を始めました。","romaji":"Jieigyō o hajimemashita.","german":"I started my own business.","furigana":"じえいぎょう|はじ"},{"japanese":"父は自営業で、小さなパン屋をやっています。","romaji":"Chichi wa jieigyou de, chiisana pan'ya o yatte imasu.","german":"My father is self-employed and runs a small bakery.","furigana":"ちち|じえいぎょう|ちい|や"},{"japanese":"申込書の職業のところに「自営業」と書きました。","romaji":"Moushikomisho no shokugyou no tokoro ni 'jieigyou' to kakimashita.","german":"In the occupation field of the application form, I wrote \"self-employed\".","furigana":"もうしこみしょ|しょくぎょう|じえいぎょう|か"}],"notes":"自営業 is self-employed work with one's own shop, business or practice, for example as a restaurant owner or craftsman. On forms, 自営業 appears as an occupation entry next to 会社員 or 学生. Typical are 自営業をしている, 自営業の家 and 実家は自営業です. In contrast to 起業 \"founding a company\", 自営業 stresses working independently as such, often on a small scale.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"起業を考えています。","romaji":"Kigyō o kangaete imasu.","german":"I am thinking about starting a business.","furigana":"きぎょう|かんが"},{"japanese":"彼は二十五歳で起業して、会社を大きくしました。","romaji":"Kare wa nijuugo sai de kigyou shite, kaisha o ookiku shimashita.","german":"He started a company at 25 and made it grow.","furigana":"かれ|にじゅうごさい|きぎょう|かいしゃ|おお"},{"japanese":"起業するには、まず資金が必要です。","romaji":"Kigyou suru ni wa, mazu shikin ga hitsuyou desu.","german":"To start a business, you first need funds.","furigana":"きぎょう|しきん|ひつよう"}],"notes":"起業 is founding one's own company; as an intransitive する-verb: 起業する, 大学を出てすぐ起業する. Related are 起業家 \"founder, entrepreneur\" and 起業資金. Caution, homophone: 企業 (きぎょう) means \"enterprise, company\" itself, whereas 起業 means the act of founding. More formal, especially for corporations, is 会社を設立する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"株式会社を設立しました。","romaji":"Kabushiki gaisha o setsuritsu shimashita.","german":"We established a stock corporation.","furigana":"かぶしきがいしゃ|せつりつ"},{"japanese":"お電話ありがとうございます。株式会社さくらでございます。","romaji":"Odenwa arigatou gozaimasu. Kabushiki gaisha Sakura de gozaimasu.","german":"Thank you for calling. This is Sakura Co., Ltd.","furigana":"でんわ|かぶしきがいしゃ"},{"japanese":"日本の会社の多くは株式会社です。","romaji":"Nihon no kaisha no ooku wa kabushiki gaisha desu.","german":"Most Japanese companies are stock corporations.","furigana":"にほん|かいしゃ|おお|かぶしきがいしゃ"}],"notes":"株式会社 is the Japanese joint-stock company (K.K.), the most common legal form for companies. It stands before or after the name in a company name, e.g. 株式会社さくら or さくら商事株式会社, and is often abbreviated as (株). Note the voicing change: かいしゃ becomes がいしゃ. In conversation one simply says 会社; 株式会社 is heard mainly in formal contexts such as contracts, on business cards and on the phone.","levelNote":"Compound: 株式 (N1) + 会社 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"本社は東京にあります。","romaji":"Honsha wa Tōkyō ni arimasu.","german":"The head office is in Tokyo.","furigana":"ほんしゃ|とうきょう"},{"japanese":"来月、大阪支社から本社に転勤します。","romaji":"Raigetsu, Oosaka shisha kara honsha ni tenkin shimasu.","german":"Next month I will be transferred from the Osaka branch office to the head office.","furigana":"らいげつ|おおさかししゃ|ほんしゃ|てんきん"},{"japanese":"この件は本社の判断を待たなければなりません。","romaji":"Kono ken wa honsha no handan o matanakereba narimasen.","german":"In this matter we have to wait for the decision of the head office.","furigana":"けん|ほんしゃ|はんだん|ま"}],"notes":"本社 is the head office or headquarters of a company: 本社は東京にある, 本社に転勤する, 本社ビル. Contrasting terms are 支社 (regional branch office) and 支店 (branch with customer traffic, e.g. for banks or shops). Less often, 本社 refers to the main shrine of a shrine complex, but in everyday use the meaning \"headquarters\" clearly dominates.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 31"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大阪に支社があります。","romaji":"Ōsaka ni shisha ga arimasu.","german":"We have a branch office in Osaka.","furigana":"おおさか|ししゃ"},{"japanese":"来年、シンガポールに新しい支社を作る予定です。","romaji":"Rainen, Shingapooru ni atarashii shisha o tsukuru yotei desu.","german":"Next year, we plan to set up a new branch office in Singapore.","furigana":"らいねん|あたら|ししゃ|つく|よてい"},{"japanese":"支社の人たちとオンラインで会議をしました。","romaji":"Shisha no hitotachi to onrain de kaigi o shimashita.","german":"I had an online meeting with the people at the branch office.","furigana":"ししゃ|ひと|かいぎ"}],"notes":"支社 is a branch office or subsidiary location of a company, usually responsible for a region: 大阪支社, 海外支社, 支社長. Compared with 支店 (a branch with customer traffic, e.g. a bank or shop), a 支社 is more of a regional administrative unit. The opposite is 本社 \"head office\"; you can be, for example, 支社に転勤する \"transferred to a branch office\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい部署に異動しました。","romaji":"Atarashii busho ni idō shimashita.","german":"I was transferred to a new department.","furigana":"あたら|ぶしょ|いどう"},{"japanese":"失礼ですが、どちらの部署の方ですか。","romaji":"Shitsurei desu ga, dochira no busho no kata desu ka.","german":"Excuse me, which department are you from?","furigana":"しつれい|ぶしょ|かた"},{"japanese":"この書類は担当の部署に出してください。","romaji":"Kono shorui wa tantou no busho ni dashite kudasai.","german":"Please submit this document to the department in charge.","furigana":"しょるい|たんとう|ぶしょ|だ"}],"notes":"部署 is the department or post to which one is assigned in a company or government agency. Typical are 部署に配属される \"to be assigned to a department\", 部署を異動する, 別の部署 and the question どちらの部署ですか. It is a general term for units such as 部, 課 or 係; for a specific one people usually give the name, such as 営業部 or 経理課.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人事異動がありました。","romaji":"Jinji idō ga arimashita.","german":"There was a personnel reshuffle.","furigana":"じんじいどう"},{"japanese":"来月から営業部に異動することになりました。","romaji":"Raigetsu kara eigyoubu ni idou suru koto ni narimashita.","german":"I have been assigned to be transferred to the sales department from next month.","furigana":"らいげつ|えいぎょうぶ|いどう"},{"japanese":"異動先の部署には、まだ知り合いがいません。","romaji":"Idousaki no busho ni wa, mada shiriai ga imasen.","german":"I don't know anyone yet in the department I am transferred to.","furigana":"いどうさき|ぶしょ|し|あ"}],"notes":"異動 is a transfer within a company or government agency, i.e. a change of department or post. Typical are 人事異動 \"personnel changes\" and 〜に異動する or 〜に異動になる \"to be transferred to ...\". Do not confuse it with the identically read 移動 \"movement, change of location\". 転勤, by contrast, stresses a transfer to a different workplace, often with a move.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新人研修に参加します。","romaji":"Shinjin kenshū ni sanka shimasu.","german":"I am taking part in the training for new employees.","furigana":"しんじんけんしゅう|さんか"},{"japanese":"来週から三日間、東京で研修を受けます。","romaji":"Raishuu kara mikkakan, Toukyou de kenshuu o ukemasu.","german":"From next week, I will have three days of training in Tokyo.","furigana":"らいしゅう|みっかかん|とうきょう|けんしゅう|う"},{"japanese":"名札に「研修中」と書いてある店員さんが、一生懸命説明してくれた。","romaji":"Nafuda ni kenshuuchuu to kaite aru ten'in-san ga, isshoukenmei setsumei shite kureta.","german":"A clerk with a name tag saying \"in training\" explained everything to me eagerly.","furigana":"なふだ|けんしゅうちゅう|か|てんいん|いっしょうけんめいせつめい"}],"notes":"研修 is professional training or induction, especially for employees: 新人研修, 社員研修, 海外研修. One says 研修を受ける or 研修に参加する; a person who is currently being trained often wears a badge saying 研修中. Unlike トレーニング (more sports) or 練習 (\"practice\"), it concerns organized further education on the job; 実習, by contrast, emphasizes practical exercises, for example during studies.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"朝礼があります。","romaji":"Chōrei ga arimasu.","german":"There is a morning assembly.","furigana":"ちょうれい"},{"japanese":"毎朝九時から、朝礼で今日の予定を確認します。","romaji":"Maiasa kuji kara, chourei de kyou no yotei o kakunin shimasu.","german":"Every morning at nine, we check the day's schedule at the morning meeting.","furigana":"まいあさくじ|ちょうれい|きょう|よてい|かくにん"},{"japanese":"月曜日の朝礼は、校長先生の話が長くて疲れた。","romaji":"Getsuyoubi no chourei wa, kouchou sensei no hanashi ga nagakute tsukareta.","german":"At the Monday assembly, the principal's talk was so long that I got tired.","furigana":"げつようび|ちょうれい|こうちょうせんせい|はなし|なが|つか"}],"notes":"朝礼 is the short assembly in the morning before work or school starts, at which announcements are made and the day's plan is discussed. One says 朝礼がある, 朝礼に出る and 朝礼で発表する. In schools 朝会 is also common; the short class meeting is called 朝の会 or ホームルーム. The word is neutral and common in both company and school life.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"忘年会を楽しみました。","romaji":"Bōnenkai o tanoshimimashita.","german":"I enjoyed the year-end party.","furigana":"ぼうねんかい|たの"},{"japanese":"今年の忘年会は、駅前の居酒屋でやります。","romaji":"Kotoshi no bounenkai wa, ekimae no izakaya de yarimasu.","german":"This year's year-end party will be held at the izakaya in front of the station.","furigana":"ことし|ぼうねんかい|えきまえ|いざかや"},{"japanese":"十二月は忘年会が続いて、ちょっと飲みすぎました。","romaji":"Juunigatsu wa bounenkai ga tsuzuite, chotto nomisugimashita.","german":"In December one year-end party followed another, and I drank a bit too much.","furigana":"じゅうにがつ|ぼうねんかい|つづ|の"}],"notes":"忘年会 literally means \"gathering to forget the year\": the year-end party in December, usually with colleagues, clubs or friends in an izakaya. Typical are 忘年会をする/やる, 忘年会を開く and 忘年会に出る. The counterpart in January is 新年会. The party is often followed by a 二次会.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 40"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新年会に参加します。","romaji":"Shinnenkai ni sanka shimasu.","german":"I will take part in the New Year's party.","furigana":"しんねんかい|さんか"},{"japanese":"会社の新年会で、ビンゴ大会がありました。","romaji":"Kaisha no shinnenkai de, bingo taikai ga arimashita.","german":"At the company's New Year's party, there was a bingo game.","furigana":"かいしゃ|しんねんかい|たいかい"},{"japanese":"新年会のお店は、もう予約しておきました。","romaji":"Shinnenkai no omise wa, mou yoyaku shite okimashita.","german":"I have already reserved the place for the New Year's party.","furigana":"しんねんかい|みせ|よやく"}],"notes":"新年会 is the New Year's party held in January among colleagues, in clubs or in neighborhoods, so it is not the New Year's Eve party. One says 新年会を開く, 新年会をする and 新年会に参加する/出る. The counterpart is the 忘年会 in December. As with other 〜会 words, an organizing person often takes care of the venue and reservation.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 40"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"歓迎会を開きます。","romaji":"Kangeikai o hirakimasu.","german":"We will hold a welcome party.","furigana":"かんげいかい|ひら"},{"japanese":"新しく入った田中さんの歓迎会は金曜日です。","romaji":"Atarashiku haitta Tanaka-san no kangeikai wa kin'youbi desu.","german":"The welcome party for Mr. Tanaka, who just joined, is on Friday.","furigana":"あたら|はい|たなか|かんげいかい|きんようび"},{"japanese":"サークルの歓迎会で、先輩たちとたくさん話せました。","romaji":"Saakuru no kangeikai de, senpaitachi to takusan hanasemashita.","german":"At the club's welcome party, I got to talk a lot with the seniors.","furigana":"かんげいかい|せんぱい|はな"}],"notes":"歓迎会 is a welcome party for new colleagues, students or guests. Typical: 〜さんの歓迎会, 歓迎会を開く/する, 新入生歓迎会 (often shortened to 新歓). The counterpart at a farewell is 送別会. It is based on 歓迎 \"welcome, reception\", which also occurs as the verb 歓迎する.","levelNote":"Compound: 歓迎 (N3) + 会 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"送別会に出席します。","romaji":"Sōbetsukai ni shusseki shimasu.","german":"I will attend the farewell party.","furigana":"そうべつかい|しゅっせき"},{"japanese":"転勤する山田さんの送別会で、花束を渡しました。","romaji":"Tenkin suru Yamada-san no soubetsukai de, hanataba o watashimashita.","german":"At the farewell party for Mr. Yamada, who is being transferred, we gave him a bouquet.","furigana":"てんきん|やまだ|そうべつかい|はなたば|わた"},{"japanese":"留学する友達のために、みんなで送別会を開いた。","romaji":"Ryuugaku suru tomodachi no tame ni, minna de soubetsukai o hiraita.","german":"We all held a farewell party for our friend who is going to study abroad.","furigana":"りゅうがく|ともだち|そうべつかい|ひら"}],"notes":"送別会 is the farewell party for someone who is being transferred, retiring, moving away or going abroad. One says 〜さんの送別会, 送別会を開く and 送別会に出る/出席する; 出席する sounds more formal. The counterpart is 歓迎会. Often flowers or a gift are presented on such occasions.","levelNote":"Compound: 送別 (N2) + 会 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二次会に行きますか。","romaji":"Nijikai ni ikimasu ka.","german":"Are you going to the after-party?","furigana":"にじかい|い"},{"japanese":"二次会はカラオケに行きましょう。","romaji":"Nijikai wa karaoke ni ikimashou.","german":"Let's go to karaoke for the second round.","furigana":"にじかい|い"},{"japanese":"結婚式の二次会で、大学時代の友達に久しぶりに会った。","romaji":"Kekkonshiki no nijikai de, daigaku jidai no tomodachi ni hisashiburi ni atta.","german":"At the after-party of the wedding, I saw friends from my university days for the first time in a long while.","furigana":"けっこんしき|にじかい|だいがくじだい|ともだち|ひさ|あ"}],"notes":"二次会 is the second round of an evening: after the first party (一次会), people move on, often to a karaoke bar or another venue; after that a 三次会 can even follow. Typical are 二次会に行く and 二次会に参加する. At weddings, the 二次会 is the casual party after the official reception, to which more friends are also invited.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 40"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"割り勘にしましょう。","romaji":"Warikan ni shimashō.","german":"Let's split the bill.","furigana":"わ|かん"},{"japanese":"五人で割り勘にすると、一人二千円です。","romaji":"Gonin de warikan ni suru to, hitori nisen'en desu.","german":"If the five of us split it, it is 2,000 yen per person.","furigana":"ごにん|わ|かん|ひとりにせんえん"},{"japanese":"今日は私がおごるから、割り勘じゃなくていいよ。","romaji":"Kyou wa watashi ga ogoru kara, warikan ja nakute ii yo.","german":"I'll treat you today, so we don't need to split it.","furigana":"きょう|わたし|わ|かん"}],"notes":"割り勘 means that everyone present pays the bill in equal shares. Typical are 割り勘にする and 割り勘で払う. The opposite is おごる \"to treat someone, to pay the bill\". The word is an abbreviation of 割り前勘定 and is used very often in everyday life; occasionally the spelling 割勘 is also seen.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ご馳走様でした。","romaji":"Gochisōsama deshita.","german":"Thank you for the meal. (after eating)","furigana":"ちそうさま"},{"japanese":"今日は私がごちそうします。","romaji":"Kyou wa watashi ga gochisou shimasu.","german":"Today it is my treat.","furigana":"きょう|わたし"},{"japanese":"お正月にはおいしいごちそうがたくさん並びます。","romaji":"Oshougatsu ni wa oishii gochisou ga takusan narabimasu.","german":"At New Year, lots of delicious dishes are laid out on the table.","furigana":"しょうがつ|なら"}],"notes":"ご馳走 is, on the one hand, an especially good, plentiful meal: すごいごちそうですね \"what a feast!\" On the other hand, ごちそうする means \"to invite someone (to a meal), to treat\", where the host pays: 友達に昼ご飯をごちそうする; the one who is treated says ごちそうになる. Well known is the phrase ごちそうさまでした after a meal or as thanks for an invitation. It is mostly written in kana, ごちそう, more rarely also 御馳走.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自己紹介をお願いします。","romaji":"Jikoshōkai o onegai shimasu.","german":"Please introduce yourself.","furigana":"じこしょうかい|ねが"},{"japanese":"初めての授業で、日本語で自己紹介をしました。","romaji":"Hajimete no jugyou de, Nihongo de jikoshoukai o shimashita.","german":"In the first class, I introduced myself in Japanese.","furigana":"はじ|じゅぎょう|にほんご|じこしょうかい"},{"japanese":"面接では、まず一分ぐらいで自己紹介してください。","romaji":"Mensetsu de wa, mazu ippun gurai de jikoshoukai shite kudasai.","german":"In the interview, please first introduce yourself in about one minute.","furigana":"めんせつ|いっぷん|じこしょうかい"}],"notes":"自己紹介 is introducing oneself, for example in the first class, in a job interview or at a meeting. You say 自己紹介をする or simply 自己紹介する; politely you ask with 自己紹介をお願いします. Those who introduce other people use 紹介する (〜を〜に紹介する) instead. Typical phrases are 簡単に自己紹介する \"to introduce oneself briefly\" and 自己紹介が苦手だ.","levelNote":"Compound: 自己 (N3) + 紹介 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"名簿を確認します。","romaji":"Meibo o kakunin shimasu.","german":"I will check the roster.","furigana":"めいぼ|かくにん"},{"japanese":"クラスの名簿に、私の名前が載っていません。","romaji":"Kurasu no meibo ni, watashi no namae ga notte imasen.","german":"My name is not on the class roster.","furigana":"めいぼ|わたし|なまえ|の"},{"japanese":"新しい会員の名簿を作りました。","romaji":"Atarashii kaiin no meibo o tsukurimashita.","german":"I made a directory for the new members.","furigana":"あたら|かいいん|めいぼ|つく"}],"notes":"名簿 is an official list of the names of members or participants: クラス名簿, 会員名簿, 社員名簿. Typical phrases are 名簿を作る, 名簿に載る \"to be on the list\" and 名簿から外す \"to remove from the list\". Unlike the general リスト or 一覧, a 名簿 always contains people, often with addresses or contact details.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"議事録を作成します。","romaji":"Gijiroku o sakusei shimasu.","german":"I will prepare the minutes.","furigana":"ぎじろく|さくせい"},{"japanese":"今日の会議の議事録は、誰が取りますか。","romaji":"Kyou no kaigi no gijiroku wa, dare ga torimasu ka.","german":"Who will take the minutes of today's meeting?","furigana":"きょう|かいぎ|ぎじろく|だれ|と"},{"japanese":"前回の議事録を読めば、決まったことがわかります。","romaji":"Zenkai no gijiroku o yomeba, kimatta koto ga wakarimasu.","german":"If you read the previous minutes, you can see what was decided.","furigana":"ぜんかい|ぎじろく|よ|き"}],"notes":"議事録 are the minutes of a meeting or conference, recording the discussion and the decisions. Typical are 議事録を取る \"to take minutes\", 議事録を作成する/まとめる and 前回の議事録. More general is 記録 \"record\"; a private メモ is not an official 議事録. The word belongs to office and administrative language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"見積書を送ります。","romaji":"Mitsumorisho o okurimasu.","german":"I will send the quotation.","furigana":"みつもりしょ|おく"},{"japanese":"引っ越しの前に、三つの会社から見積書をもらいました。","romaji":"Hikkoshi no mae ni, mittsu no kaisha kara mitsumorisho o moraimashita.","german":"Before moving, I got quotations from three companies.","furigana":"ひ|こ|まえ|みっ|かいしゃ|みつもりしょ"},{"japanese":"この見積書の金額は、税込みですか。","romaji":"Kono mitsumorisho no kingaku wa, zeikomi desu ka.","german":"Is the amount in this quotation tax included?","furigana":"みつもりしょ|きんがく|ぜいこ"}],"notes":"見積書 is a written offer with a cost estimate, for example from a moving company or a repair shop. It comes from 見積もる \"to estimate\"; in conversation people often say just 見積もり: 見積もりを取る/出す. Typical are 見積書を作成する, 見積書を送る and 見積書をもらう. Do not confuse it with 請求書 (invoice after the service) and 領収書 (receipt after payment).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"請求書を発行します。","romaji":"Seikyūsho o hakkō shimasu.","german":"I will issue the invoice.","furigana":"せいきゅうしょ|はっこう"},{"japanese":"電気代の請求書が届きました。","romaji":"Denkidai no seikyuusho ga todokimashita.","german":"The electricity bill has arrived.","furigana":"でんきだい|せいきゅうしょ|とど"},{"japanese":"請求書の金額を、月末までに振り込んでください。","romaji":"Seikyuusho no kingaku o, getsumatsu made ni furikonde kudasai.","german":"Please transfer the invoiced amount by the end of the month.","furigana":"せいきゅうしょ|きんがく|げつまつ|ふ|こ"}],"notes":"請求書 is the invoice by which a payment is requested, for example from companies, or electricity or telephone providers. You say 請求書を発行する/送る, 請求書が届く and often pay by bank transfer (振り込む). To be distinguished from it are 領収書 (receipt for a payment already made) and, in a restaurant, お会計 \"the bill, please\". It is based on 請求する \"to demand, bill\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"領収書をください。","romaji":"Ryōshūsho o kudasai.","german":"A receipt, please.","furigana":"りょうしゅうしょ"},{"japanese":"会社の経費なので、領収書をもらっておいてください。","romaji":"Kaisha no keihi na node, ryoushuusho o moratte oite kudasai.","german":"This is a company expense, so please get a receipt.","furigana":"かいしゃ|けいひ|りょうしゅうしょ"},{"japanese":"タクシーを降りるとき、運転手さんに領収書をもらいました。","romaji":"Takushii o oriru toki, untenshu-san ni ryoushuusho o moraimashita.","german":"When I got out of the taxi, I got a receipt from the driver.","furigana":"お|うんてんしゅ|りょうしゅうしょ"}],"notes":"領収書 is a formal receipt, often with the recipient's name and the purpose, which is needed, for example, for expense reports. The simple cash-register receipt is called レシート. Typical are 領収書をください, 領収書をもらう and 領収書を書く; there is also the spelling 領収証. By comparison: 請求書 demands a payment, 領収書 confirms it.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 45"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"稟議を通します。","romaji":"Ringi o tōshimasu.","german":"I put the proposal through the approval process.","furigana":"りんぎ|とお"},{"japanese":"新しいパソコンを買うには、稟議書を出さなければなりません。","romaji":"Atarashii pasokon o kau ni wa, ringisho o dasanakereba narimasen.","german":"To buy a new computer, you have to submit an approval request.","furigana":"あたら|か|りんぎしょ|だ"},{"japanese":"稟議がなかなか通らなくて、プロジェクトが始められない。","romaji":"Ringi ga nakanaka tooranakute, purojekuto ga hajimerarenai.","german":"The proposal just isn't getting approved, so we can't start the project.","furigana":"りんぎ|とお|はじ"}],"notes":"稟議 is the typically Japanese approval procedure in companies: a proposal (稟議書) circulates from office to office, and all responsible persons give their consent, often with their seal. Typical phrases are 稟議を上げる/回す \"to put a proposal into circulation\" and 稟議が通る \"to be approved\". The character 稟 is rare and occurs practically only in this word. It is purely office and business language.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"上司の決裁が必要です。","romaji":"Jōshi no kessai ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"The superior's approval is required.","furigana":"じょうし|けっさい|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"部長の決裁が下りたので、来月から工事を始めます。","romaji":"Buchou no kessai ga orita node, raigetsu kara kouji o hajimemasu.","german":"The department head has given his approval, so construction will begin next month.","furigana":"ぶちょう|けっさい|お|らいげつ|こうじ|はじ"},{"japanese":"社長が出張中で、書類を決裁してもらえません。","romaji":"Shachou ga shutchouchuu de, shorui o kessai shite moraemasen.","german":"The president is away on a business trip, so I can't get the documents approved.","furigana":"しゃちょう|しゅっちょうちゅう|しょるい|けっさい"}],"notes":"決裁 is the formal approval by a person with decision-making authority, e.g. a department head or managing director. Typical are 決裁をもらう/受ける, 決裁が下りる \"the approval is granted\" and 〜を決裁する. Note: the identically read 決済 means \"payment, settlement\" (e.g. カード決済) and is often confused with it. The word belongs to business and administrative language.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"判子を持ってきてください。","romaji":"Hanko o motte kite kudasai.","german":"Please bring your seal.","furigana":"はんこ|も"},{"japanese":"ここに判子を押してください。","romaji":"Koko ni hanko o oshite kudasai.","german":"Please put your seal here.","furigana":"はんこ|お"},{"japanese":"宅配便を受け取るとき、判子の代わりにサインしました。","romaji":"Takuhaibin o uketoru toki, hanko no kawari ni sain shimashita.","german":"When I received the parcel, I signed instead of using a seal.","furigana":"たくはいびん|う|と|はんこ|か"}],"notes":"判子 is the personal name seal that is used in Japan instead of a signature on forms, contracts or for package deliveries. One says 判子を押す \"to stamp\"; often it is written just はんこ or ハンコ. The more formal word is 印鑑, especially for an officially registered seal. A stamp for pictures or dates, by contrast, is called スタンプ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"届けを出します。","romaji":"Todoke o dashimasu.","german":"I will submit a notification.","furigana":"とど|だ"},{"japanese":"財布をなくしたので、交番に届けを出しました。","romaji":"Saifu o nakushita node, kouban ni todoke o dashimashita.","german":"Since I lost my wallet, I filed a report at the police box.","furigana":"さいふ|こうばん|とど|だ"},{"japanese":"学校を休むときは、欠席届を出してください。","romaji":"Gakkou o yasumu toki wa, kessekitodoke o dashite kudasai.","german":"When you miss school, please submit an absence notice.","furigana":"がっこう|やす|けっせきとどけ|だ"}],"notes":"届け (often also written 届) is a written notification to an authority, school or company. It often appears as a suffix: 欠席届 \"absence notice\", 退職届 \"resignation letter\", 婚姻届 \"marriage registration\"; in such compounds the okurigana is usually omitted. Typical is 届けを出す. The related verb is 届ける \"to submit, report\", and the formal noun is 届け出.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"パスポートを紛失しました。","romaji":"Pasupōto o funshitsu shimashita.","german":"I lost my passport.","furigana":"ふんしつ"},{"japanese":"カードを紛失した場合は、すぐにご連絡ください。","romaji":"Kaado o funshitsu shita baai wa, sugu ni gorenraku kudasai.","german":"If you lose your card, please contact us immediately.","furigana":"ふんしつ|ばあい|れんらく"},{"japanese":"会議の資料が一部、紛失しているようです。","romaji":"Kaigi no shiryou ga ichibu, funshitsu shite iru you desu.","german":"Some of the meeting materials seem to be missing.","furigana":"かいぎ|しりょう|いちぶ|ふんしつ"}],"notes":"紛失 is the formal word for \"loss\" of objects and often appears on forms and in announcements: 紛失届, 紛失物. As a する-verb it can be used transitively (パスポートを紛失する) or intransitively (書類が紛失する). In everyday speech one usually says なくす or なくなる. It is not used for people or for abstract things such as trust.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"拾得物の届け出をしました。","romaji":"Shūtokubutsu no todokede o shimashita.","german":"I reported the found item.","furigana":"しゅうとくぶつ|とど|で"},{"japanese":"拾得した日時と場所を、この用紙に書いてください。","romaji":"Shuutoku shita nichiji to basho o, kono youshi ni kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write the date, time and place of the find on this form.","furigana":"しゅうとく|にちじ|ばしょ|ようし|か"},{"japanese":"駅で見つかった拾得物は、警察に届けられます。","romaji":"Eki de mitsukatta shuutokubutsu wa, keisatsu ni todokeraremasu.","german":"Items found at the station are handed over to the police.","furigana":"えき|み|しゅうとくぶつ|けいさつ|とど"}],"notes":"拾得 is the official word for \"finding (a lost item)\" and appears almost only in forms, notices and police language: 拾得物 \"found item\", 拾得者 \"finder\", 拾得する. In everyday speech one simply says 拾う, and instead of 拾得物 mostly 落とし物. The counterpart on the loss side is 紛失 or 遺失.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"忘れ物をしました。","romaji":"Wasuremono o shimashita.","german":"I forgot something.","furigana":"わす|もの"},{"japanese":"電車を降りるときは、忘れ物に気をつけてください。","romaji":"Densha o oriru toki wa, wasuremono ni ki o tsukete kudasai.","german":"When you get off the train, please be careful not to leave anything behind.","furigana":"でんしゃ|お|わす|もの|き"},{"japanese":"お客様、お忘れ物ですよ。","romaji":"Okyakusama, owasuremono desu yo.","german":"Excuse me, you forgot something!","furigana":"きゃくさま|わす|もの"}],"notes":"忘れ物 is an object that has been left behind, or the act of leaving something behind itself; \"to leave something behind\" is 忘れ物をする. Politely with お: お忘れ物ですよ \"you forgot something\", and on trains one hears お忘れ物のないようご注意ください. It should be distinguished from 落とし物: that is something lost along the way, whereas 忘れ物 is something one has left standing somewhere.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 29"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"黒い財布の落とし物は届いていませんか。","romaji":"Kuroi saifu no otoshimono wa todoite imasen ka.","german":"Has a black wallet been turned in as lost property?","furigana":"くろ|さいふ|お|もの|とど"},{"japanese":"電車の中で落とし物をしてしまいました。","romaji":"Densha no naka de otoshimono o shite shimaimashita.","german":"I lost something on the train.","furigana":"でんしゃ|なか|お|もの"},{"japanese":"道で拾った落とし物を、交番に届けた。","romaji":"Michi de hirotta otoshimono o, kouban ni todoketa.","german":"I took the item I found on the street to the police box.","furigana":"みち|ひろ|お|もの|こうばん|とど"}],"notes":"落とし物 is an item that has been lost or dropped somewhere, that is, lost property from the point of view of the loser or the finder. Typical are 落とし物をする \"to lose something\", 落とし物を交番に届ける and 落とし物センター. To be distinguished from this is 忘れ物: something that you have left lying somewhere or forgotten. The related verb is 落とす \"to drop, to lose\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"貴重品に気をつけてください。","romaji":"Kichōhin ni ki o tsukete kudasai.","german":"Please be careful with your valuables.","furigana":"きちょうひん|き"},{"japanese":"貴重品はフロントでお預かりします。","romaji":"Kichouhin wa furonto de oazukari shimasu.","german":"We will keep your valuables at the front desk.","furigana":"きちょうひん|あず"},{"japanese":"プールに入る前に、貴重品をロッカーに入れた。","romaji":"Puuru ni hairu mae ni, kichouhin o rokkaa ni ireta.","german":"Before going into the pool, I put my valuables in a locker.","furigana":"はい|まえ|きちょうひん|い"}],"notes":"貴重品 are valuables such as a wallet, passport, jewelry or mobile phone. The word often appears on notices in hotels, baths and trains: 貴重品はお手元に, 貴重品ロッカー. Typical are 貴重品を預ける \"to leave valuables in safekeeping\" and 貴重品に気をつける. It is based on the adjective 貴重な \"valuable, precious\", which is also used for experiences (貴重な体験).","levelNote":"Compound: 貴重 (N3) + 品 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"所持品を確認してください。","romaji":"Shojihin o kakunin shite kudasai.","german":"Please check your belongings.","furigana":"しょじひん|かくにん"},{"japanese":"空港で所持品の検査を受けました。","romaji":"Kuukou de shojihin no kensa o ukemashita.","german":"I had my belongings inspected at the airport.","furigana":"くうこう|しょじひん|けんさ|う"},{"japanese":"警察は男の所持品を調べた。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa otoko no shojihin o shirabeta.","german":"The police searched the man's belongings.","furigana":"けいさつ|おとこ|しょじひん|しら"}],"notes":"所持品 are the things someone carries with them; the word is formal and appears mainly in police, security and exam contexts: 所持品検査, 所持品を調べる. In everyday speech one says 持ち物, e.g. on packing lists for an outing. It is based on 所持する \"to carry, possess\".","levelNote":"Compound: 所持 (N1) + 品 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"手荷物を預けます。","romaji":"Tenimotsu o azukemasu.","german":"I will check my baggage.","furigana":"てにもつ|あず"},{"japanese":"機内に持ち込める手荷物は、一つだけです。","romaji":"Kinai ni mochikomeru tenimotsu wa, hitotsu dake desu.","german":"You can bring only one piece of baggage into the cabin.","furigana":"きない|も|こ|てにもつ|ひと"},{"japanese":"手荷物受取所で、スーツケースが出てくるのを待っています。","romaji":"Tenimotsu uketorijo de, suutsukeesu ga dete kuru no o matte imasu.","german":"I'm waiting at baggage claim for my suitcase to come out.","furigana":"てにもつうけとりじょ|で|ま"}],"notes":"手荷物 is the luggage a traveler has with them. At the airport it includes both checked baggage (手荷物を預ける, 手荷物受取所 \"baggage claim\") and carry-on baggage; the latter is precisely called 機内持ち込み手荷物. In everyday speech people mostly simply say 荷物. The word is rather formal and often appears on signs and in announcements.","levelNote":"Compound: 手 (N5) + 荷物 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"荷造りをしましょう。","romaji":"Nizukuri o shimashō.","german":"Let's pack.","furigana":"にづく"},{"japanese":"引っ越しの荷造りが、まだ終わっていません。","romaji":"Hikkoshi no nizukuri ga, mada owatte imasen.","german":"I haven't finished packing for the move yet.","furigana":"ひ|こ|にづく|お"},{"japanese":"旅行の前の晩に、急いで荷造りした。","romaji":"Ryokou no mae no ban ni, isoide nizukuri shita.","german":"On the night before the trip, I packed in a hurry.","furigana":"りょこう|まえ|ばん|いそ|にづく"}],"notes":"荷造り means \"to pack up things, to pack luggage or boxes\", for example before a trip or a move. Typical phrases are 荷造りをする, 引っ越しの荷造り and 旅行の荷造り. In everyday speech one also says 荷物をまとめる or パッキング. The opposite, unpacking after a move, is 荷ほどき. The spelling 荷作り is occasionally seen.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"外出の支度をしています。","romaji":"Gaishutsu no shitaku o shite imasu.","german":"I am getting ready to go out.","furigana":"がいしゅつ|したく"},{"japanese":"早く支度しないと、学校に遅れるよ。","romaji":"Hayaku shitaku shinai to, gakkou ni okureru yo.","german":"If you don't get ready quickly, you'll be late for school.","furigana":"はや|したく|がっこう|おく"},{"japanese":"旅行の支度は、もうできましたか。","romaji":"Ryokou no shitaku wa, mou dekimashita ka.","german":"Have you finished packing for the trip yet?","furigana":"りょこう|したく"}],"notes":"支度 is everyday preparation, especially getting ready and preparing meals: 出かける支度, 食事の支度, 身支度 \"getting dressed and groomed\". One says 支度をする or, shortened, 支度する. 準備 is more general and more formal (also for projects and exams), and 用意 emphasizes making things available. 仕度 is a rarer spelling variant of the same word.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"身支度を整えます。","romaji":"Mijitaku o totonoemasu.","german":"I get ready.","furigana":"みじたく|ととの"},{"japanese":"お客さんが来る前に、身支度をしておきなさい。","romaji":"Okyakusan ga kuru mae ni, mijitaku o shite okinasai.","german":"Get ready before the guests arrive.","furigana":"きゃく|く|まえ|みじたく"},{"japanese":"寝坊したので、五分で身支度をして家を出た。","romaji":"Nebou shita node, gofun de mijitaku o shite ie o deta.","german":"I overslept, so I got ready in five minutes and left the house.","furigana":"ねぼう|ごふん|みじたく|いえ|で"}],"notes":"身支度 means \"getting oneself ready\": dressing, doing one's hair, putting on makeup, i.e. preparing one's appearance for going out. Typical are 身支度をする, 身支度を整える and 身支度を済ませる. 支度 or 準備 on their own mean general preparations (夕食の支度), and 着替える only changing clothes. The word is neutral to slightly written; in conversation people often simply say 準備する.","levelNote":"Compound: 身 (N3) + 支度 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"下準備をしておきます。","romaji":"Shitajunbi o shite okimasu.","german":"I will do the preparation in advance.","furigana":"したじゅんび"},{"japanese":"野菜を切るなど、料理の下準備は前の日にしておきました。","romaji":"Yasai o kiru nado, ryouri no shitajunbi wa mae no hi ni shite okimashita.","german":"I did the preparation for cooking, such as cutting the vegetables, the day before.","furigana":"やさい|き|りょうり|したじゅんび|まえ|ひ"},{"japanese":"しっかり下準備をしたので、会議でうまく説明できた。","romaji":"Shikkari shitajunbi o shita node, kaigi de umaku setsumei dekita.","german":"I prepared thoroughly, so I was able to explain well in the meeting.","furigana":"したじゅんび|かいぎ|せつめい"}],"notes":"下準備 is the preliminary work done beforehand so that the actual work goes smoothly: in cooking cutting and marinating, at work gathering material. Typical are 下準備をする and 下準備をしておく; 〜ておく fits especially well because it is about preparing in advance. 準備 is the general term, whereas 下準備 stresses the basic first steps.","levelNote":"Compound: 下 (N5) + 準備 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"段取りよく進めます。","romaji":"Dandori yoku susumemasu.","german":"I will proceed in an organized way.","furigana":"だんど|すす"},{"japanese":"段取りが悪くて、準備に時間がかかってしまった。","romaji":"Dandori ga warukute, junbi ni jikan ga kakatte shimatta.","german":"The arrangements were poor, so preparations took a long time.","furigana":"だんど|わる|じゅんび|じかん"},{"japanese":"明日のイベントの段取りを、今日中に決めておきましょう。","romaji":"Ashita no ibento no dandori o, kyoujuu ni kimete okimashou.","german":"Let's decide the arrangements for tomorrow's event by the end of today.","furigana":"あした|だんど|きょうじゅう|き"}],"notes":"段取り is the planned sequence of work steps, i.e. how one organizes and prepares something. Typical phrases: 段取りがいい/悪い \"well/badly organized\", 段取りよく \"in an efficiently planned way\", 段取りをつける/決める \"to set the procedures\". In contrast to 手順 (the fixed order of a procedure), 段取り is about forward-looking planning; 手際, by contrast, describes skill in carrying something out.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ホテルの手配をします。","romaji":"Hoteru no tehai o shimasu.","german":"I will arrange the hotel.","furigana":"てはい"},{"japanese":"タクシーを手配しておきましたので、玄関でお待ちください。","romaji":"Takushii o tehai shite okimashita node, genkan de omachi kudasai.","german":"I have arranged a taxi for you, so please wait at the entrance.","furigana":"てはい|げんかん|ま"},{"japanese":"その男は、全国に指名手配されている。","romaji":"Sono otoko wa, zenkoku ni shimei tehai sarete iru.","german":"That man is wanted nationwide.","furigana":"おとこ|ぜんこく|しめいてはい"}],"notes":"手配 means \"to organize, procure, arrange\", e.g. a hotel, tickets, a taxi or staff: 〜を手配する, 〜の手配をする. It sounds more businesslike than 用意する or 準備する and stresses that one orders something from third parties or sets it in motion. A second meaning is a police manhunt, especially in 指名手配 \"wanted (by the police)\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"手際よく料理をします。","romaji":"Tegiwa yoku ryōri o shimasu.","german":"I cook skillfully.","furigana":"てぎわ|りょうり"},{"japanese":"新人なのに、彼は手際がいいですね。","romaji":"Shinjin na noni, kare wa tegiwa ga ii desu ne.","german":"Even though he is new, he works quite efficiently.","furigana":"しんじん|かれ|てぎわ"},{"japanese":"手際が悪くて、片付けに二時間もかかった。","romaji":"Tegiwa ga warukute, katazuke ni nijikan mo kakatta.","german":"I was so clumsy that cleaning up took a full two hours.","furigana":"てぎわ|わる|かたづ|にじかん"}],"notes":"手際 refers to the skill and efficiency with which someone gets a job done in practice. It almost always appears in fixed combinations: 手際がいい/悪い and adverbial 手際よく \"quickly and skillfully\". The opposite 不手際 means \"clumsiness, oversight\" and is often used in apologies. While 段取り concerns planning in advance, 手際 describes the execution itself.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"手順を確認します。","romaji":"Tejun o kakunin shimasu.","german":"I will check the procedure.","furigana":"てじゅん|かくにん"},{"japanese":"説明書の手順通りに組み立ててください。","romaji":"Setsumeisho no tejun doori ni kumitatete kudasai.","german":"Please assemble it step by step according to the instructions.","furigana":"せつめいしょ|てじゅんどお|く|た"},{"japanese":"手順を一つ間違えたので、最初からやり直しです。","romaji":"Tejun o hitotsu machigaeta node, saisho kara yarinaoshi desu.","german":"I got one step wrong, so I have to start over from the beginning.","furigana":"てじゅん|ひと|まちが|さいしょ|なお"}],"notes":"手順 is the fixed sequence of steps, i.e. the procedure: 手順を守る, 手順を確認する, 手順通りに \"according to the procedure, step by step\", 手順を間違える. Compared with 順番 (\"order, whose turn it is\"), 手順 refers to work steps. 段取り, by contrast, stresses the planning and organization of the whole.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"見本をお見せします。","romaji":"Mihon o omise shimasu.","german":"I will show you a sample.","furigana":"みほん|み"},{"japanese":"店の前に、料理の見本が並んでいます。","romaji":"Mise no mae ni, ryouri no mihon ga narande imasu.","german":"Replicas of the dishes are displayed in front of the restaurant.","furigana":"みせ|まえ|りょうり|みほん|なら"},{"japanese":"遅刻ばかりする彼は、悪い見本だ。","romaji":"Chikoku bakari suru kare wa, warui mihon da.","german":"He is always late and is a bad example.","furigana":"ちこく|かれ|わる|みほん"}],"notes":"見本 is a sample or specimen that shows what something looks like: 商品の見本, 料理の見本 (the plastic food displays in front of restaurants), 見本市 \"trade fair\". It also means \"example, model case\", as in いい見本 or 悪い見本. A model to imitate is rather 手本; in commerce people today often also say サンプル.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先生の手本を見て書きます。","romaji":"Sensei no tehon o mite kakimasu.","german":"I write by looking at the teacher's model.","furigana":"せんせい|てほん|み|か"},{"japanese":"先輩を手本にして、仕事を覚えました。","romaji":"Senpai o tehon ni shite, shigoto o oboemashita.","german":"I took my senior colleague as a model and learned the job.","furigana":"せんぱい|てほん|しごと|おぼ"},{"japanese":"親は子どもに、いいお手本を見せなければならない。","romaji":"Oya wa kodomo ni, ii otehon o misenakereba naranai.","german":"Parents have to set a good example for their children.","furigana":"おや|こ|てほん|み"}],"notes":"手本 is a model or template to imitate: originally a writing or drawing model, today also a person with exemplary behavior. Typical are 〜を手本にする \"to take ... as a model\", 手本を見せる and the polite everyday お手本. Unlike 見本 (sample, specimen), 手本 always carries the idea of imitation.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"事情があって行けません。","romaji":"Jijō ga atte ikemasen.","german":"I have circumstances and cannot go.","furigana":"じじょう|い"},{"japanese":"遅れた事情を説明してください。","romaji":"Okureta jijou o setsumei shite kudasai.","german":"Please explain the circumstances of your delay.","furigana":"おく|じじょう|せつめい"},{"japanese":"東京の住宅事情は、なかなか厳しい。","romaji":"Toukyou no juutaku jijou wa, nakanaka kibishii.","german":"The housing situation in Tokyo is quite tough.","furigana":"とうきょう|じゅうたくじじょう|きび"}],"notes":"事情 are the circumstances or background of a situation. Very common is the politely vague reason 事情があって \"for certain reasons\", with which one need not name private reasons; similar is 家庭の事情 \"family reasons\". Other phrases: 事情を説明する, 事情が変わる, and as a suffix 住宅事情 or 交通事情 \"housing/traffic situation\". 理由, by contrast, names the concrete reason directly.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"貴重な体験でした。","romaji":"Kichō na taiken deshita.","german":"It was a valuable experience.","furigana":"きちょう|たいけん"},{"japanese":"京都で、着物を着る体験をしました。","romaji":"Kyouto de, kimono o kiru taiken o shimashita.","german":"In Kyoto, I tried wearing a kimono.","furigana":"きょうと|きもの|き|たいけん"},{"japanese":"このジムは、一回無料で体験できます。","romaji":"Kono jimu wa, ikkai muryou de taiken dekimasu.","german":"At this gym, you can try it out once for free.","furigana":"いっかいむりょう|たいけん"}],"notes":"体験 is a firsthand, direct experience you have had yourself: 〜を体験する, 体験談 \"personal account\", 無料体験 \"free trial session\", 職場体験 \"work experience placement\". 経験 is more general and also means accumulated experience and knowledge, e.g. 仕事の経験がある. For something one-off that you experienced yourself, 体験 fits better; for work experience, always 経験.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"感想を聞かせてください。","romaji":"Kansō o kikasete kudasai.","german":"Please tell me your impressions.","furigana":"かんそう|き"},{"japanese":"映画を見た感想を、みんなで話し合った。","romaji":"Eiga o mita kansou o, minna de hanashiatta.","german":"We all discussed our impressions after watching the movie.","furigana":"えいが|み|かんそう|はな|あ"},{"japanese":"夏休みの宿題で、本の感想文を書きました。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi no shukudai de, hon no kansoubun o kakimashita.","german":"For summer vacation homework, I wrote a book report.","furigana":"なつやす|しゅくだい|ほん|かんそうぶん|か"}],"notes":"感想 are one's personal impressions and thoughts after an experience, for example after a film, book or event. Typical are 感想を言う/述べる/書く, 感想を聞かせてください and 読書感想文 \"book report as a school essay\". 意見, by contrast, is an opinion or statement on a question; 印象 is the impression something or someone makes.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"評判がいいレストランです。","romaji":"Hyōban ga ii resutoran desu.","german":"It is a restaurant with a good reputation.","furigana":"ひょうばん"},{"japanese":"あの映画は、つまらないと評判だ。","romaji":"Ano eiga wa, tsumaranai to hyouban da.","german":"That movie is said to be boring.","furigana":"えいが|ひょうばん"},{"japanese":"駅前に新しくできたパン屋が、評判になっている。","romaji":"Ekimae ni atarashiku dekita pan'ya ga, hyouban ni natte iru.","german":"The newly opened bakery in front of the station is the talk of the town.","furigana":"えきまえ|あたら|や|ひょうばん"}],"notes":"評判 is the reputation or what people say about something: 評判がいい/悪い, 評判の店 \"a shop everyone is talking about\". With 〜と評判だ you convey the general assessment (\"is reputed to be ...\"), and 評判になる means \"to become the talk of the town, become known\". 噂 refers more to unconfirmed rumors about people, 人気 to popularity itself.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"世間の目が気になります。","romaji":"Seken no me ga ki ni narimasu.","german":"I am concerned about what other people think.","furigana":"せけん|め|き"},{"japanese":"こんな所で会うなんて、世間は狭いですね。","romaji":"Konna tokoro de au nante, seken wa semai desu ne.","german":"Meeting in a place like this, it's a small world, isn't it?","furigana":"ところ|あ|せけん|せま"},{"japanese":"彼は世間知らずで、よくだまされる。","romaji":"Kare wa seken shirazu de, yoku damasareru.","german":"He is naive about the world and often gets deceived.","furigana":"かれ|せけんし"}],"notes":"世間 is \"the people, the world out there\", i.e. society from the individual's point of view, especially as a judging public: 世間の目 \"what people think\", 世間体 \"reputation, appearances\". Other set expressions: 世間話 \"small talk\", 世間知らず \"naive about the world\", 世間は狭い \"the world is small\". 社会 is more abstract and objective, and 世論 means measurable public opinion.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"世論調査をしました。","romaji":"Yoron chōsa o shimashita.","german":"We conducted an opinion poll.","furigana":"よろんちょうさ"},{"japanese":"世論の多くは、新しい法律に反対しています。","romaji":"Yoron no ooku wa, atarashii houritsu ni hantai shite imasu.","german":"Much of public opinion is against the new law.","furigana":"よろん|おお|あたら|ほうりつ|はんたい"},{"japanese":"政府は世論を気にして、計画を変更した。","romaji":"Seifu wa yoron o ki ni shite, keikaku o henkou shita.","german":"The government changed its plan out of concern for public opinion.","furigana":"せいふ|よろん|き|けいかく|へんこう"}],"notes":"世論 is the public opinion of the population on political or social issues; it is read よろん or せろん. Typical are 世論調査 \"opinion poll\", 世論が高まる and 世論を気にする. The word belongs mainly to news and politics. 世間, by contrast, generally means \"the people\" and their judgment in everyday life.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"最近の風潮です。","romaji":"Saikin no fūchō desu.","german":"It is a recent trend.","furigana":"さいきん|ふうちょう"},{"japanese":"早く結婚しなくてもいいという風潮が広がっている。","romaji":"Hayaku kekkon shinakute mo ii to iu fuuchou ga hirogatte iru.","german":"The tendency to think that you don't need to marry early is spreading.","furigana":"はや|けっこん|ふうちょう|ひろ"},{"japanese":"何でもお金で考える風潮には、疑問を感じます。","romaji":"Nan demo okane de kangaeru fuuchou ni wa, gimon o kanjimasu.","german":"I have doubts about the tendency to think of everything in terms of money.","furigana":"なん|かね|かんが|ふうちょう|ぎもん|かん"}],"notes":"風潮 is a social current or tendency, often with a slightly critical undertone: 〜という風潮がある, 風潮が広がる, 社会の風潮. In contrast to 流行 (fashion, what is currently \"in\"), it is about ways of thinking and behaving; 傾向 is more neutral and is also used for data or individuals. The word is rather literary and common in commentaries or the news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"食べ物が腐りました。","romaji":"Tabemono ga kusarimashita.","german":"The food went bad.","furigana":"た|もの|くさ"},{"japanese":"牛乳が腐って、変なにおいがする。","romaji":"Gyuunyuu ga kusatte, hen na nioi ga suru.","german":"The milk has gone bad and smells strange.","furigana":"ぎゅうにゅう|くさ|へん"},{"japanese":"一回失敗したくらいで腐るなよ。","romaji":"Ikkai shippai shita kurai de kusaru na yo.","german":"Don't get discouraged just because you failed once.","furigana":"いっかいしっぱい|くさ"}],"notes":"腐る is intransitive: food spoils or rots (牛乳が腐る), wood or metal decays. Figuratively it means \"to lose heart, sulk\" (腐るなよ) or \"to be morally corrupt\" (腐った政治). The transitive partner is 腐らせる \"to let spoil\". Well known is the proverb 腐っても鯛 \"a sea bream is still a sea bream even when it has spoiled\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"花が萎みました。","romaji":"Hana ga shibomimashita.","german":"The flower withered.","furigana":"はな|しぼ"},{"japanese":"風船がしぼんで、小さくなってしまった。","romaji":"Fuusen ga shibonde, chiisaku natte shimatta.","german":"The balloon deflated and became small.","furigana":"ふうせん|ちい"},{"japanese":"試合に負けて、優勝の夢はしぼんだ。","romaji":"Shiai ni makete, yuushou no yume wa shibonda.","german":"After losing the match, the dream of winning the title shriveled away.","furigana":"しあい|ま|ゆうしょう|ゆめ"}],"notes":"萎む is mostly written in kana しぼむ and is intransitive: flowers wilt, a balloon or tire loses air, and in a figurative sense hopes or dreams fade (夢がしぼむ). 枯れる, by contrast, means that a plant dries out completely and dies; 縮む means \"to shrink\" (e.g. clothing). The transitive form is しぼませる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい環境に馴染みました。","romaji":"Atarashii kankyō ni najimimashita.","german":"I got used to the new environment.","furigana":"あたら|かんきょう|なじ"},{"japanese":"この靴は、やっと足になじんできた。","romaji":"Kono kutsu wa, yatto ashi ni najinde kita.","german":"These shoes have finally molded to my feet.","furigana":"くつ|あし"},{"japanese":"転校してきた妹は、すぐにクラスになじんだ。","romaji":"Tenkou shite kita imouto wa, sugu ni kurasu ni najinda.","german":"My little sister, who had transferred schools, quickly fit in with the class.","furigana":"てんこう|いもうと"}],"notes":"馴染む (often in kana なじむ) is intransitive and takes に: 新しい環境になじむ \"to settle in\", 職場になじむ. With things it means \"to fit, to blend in\": 靴が足になじむ, 化粧が肌になじむ. The noun なじみ appears in なじみの店 \"regular haunt\" and 幼なじみ \"childhood friend\". 慣れる emphasizes getting used to an activity, なじむ harmonious belonging.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"味が染み込みました。","romaji":"Aji ga shimikomimashita.","german":"The flavor has soaked in.","furigana":"あじ|し|こ"},{"japanese":"雨水が、ゆっくり地面に染み込んでいく。","romaji":"Amamizu ga, yukkuri jimen ni shimikonde iku.","german":"The rainwater slowly seeps into the ground.","furigana":"あまみず|じめん|し|こ"},{"japanese":"子どものころの習慣が、体に染み込んでいる。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro no shuukan ga, karada ni shimikonde iru.","german":"The habits from my childhood are ingrained in me.","furigana":"こ|しゅうかん|からだ|し|こ"}],"notes":"染み込む is intransitive and means \"to soak into, to seep into something\": liquid or flavor penetrates deeply (味が染み込む, 雨が地面に染み込む). Figuratively, habits or experiences are said to \"become second nature\" (体に染み込む). Compared with 染みる, 〜込む emphasizes penetrating to the inside. The transitive form is 染み込ませる; it is also often written しみ込む.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"心に染みる歌です。","romaji":"Kokoro ni shimiru uta desu.","german":"It is a song that touches the heart.","furigana":"こころ|し|うた"},{"japanese":"玉ねぎを切ると、目にしみる。","romaji":"Tamanegi o kiru to, me ni shimiru.","german":"When I cut onions, my eyes sting.","furigana":"たま|き|め"},{"japanese":"冷たい水が歯にしみて痛い。","romaji":"Tsumetai mizu ga ha ni shimite itai.","german":"Cold water makes my teeth hurt.","furigana":"つめ|みず|は|いた"}],"notes":"染みる is usually written in kana as しみる and is intransitive with に. It means \"to seep in\", but very often \"to sting, burn\": 目にしみる (onions, smoke), 傷にしみる, 歯にしみる (sensitive teeth). Figuratively it means \"to touch the heart\": 心にしみる, 身にしみる \"to feel something keenly\"; the spelling 沁みる is also seen for this. The noun 染み means \"stain\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"涙で字が滲みました。","romaji":"Namida de ji ga nijimimashita.","german":"The writing blurred because of tears.","furigana":"なみだ|じ|にじ"},{"japanese":"転んだひざに、血がにじんでいる。","romaji":"Koronda hiza ni, chi ga nijinde iru.","german":"Blood is seeping out of the knee I fell on.","furigana":"ころ|ち"},{"japanese":"彼の手紙には、家族への思いがにじんでいた。","romaji":"Kare no tegami ni wa, kazoku e no omoi ga nijinde ita.","german":"His love for his family showed through in his letter.","furigana":"かれ|てがみ|かぞく|おも"}],"notes":"滲む (usually in kana as にじむ) is intransitive: ink or color bleeds, outlines blur, blood, sweat or tears seep out (額に汗がにじむ, 血がにじむ). Figuratively, a feeling shows indirectly in words or gestures, often as にじみ出る. Typical patterns are 〜で滲む for the cause and 〜に滲む for the place. Distinguish it from 染みる \"to soak in, sting\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"睨まないでください。","romaji":"Niramanaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't glare at me.","furigana":"にら"},{"japanese":"先生は、騒いでいる生徒をじっとにらんだ。","romaji":"Sensei wa, sawaide iru seito o jitto niranda.","german":"The teacher glared at the noisy students.","furigana":"せんせい|さわ|せいと"},{"japanese":"警察は、あの男が犯人だとにらんでいる。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa, ano otoko ga hannin da to nirande iru.","german":"The police suspect that man is the culprit.","furigana":"けいさつ|おとこ|はんにん"}],"notes":"睨む (often in kana にらむ) is transitive: 〜をにらむ \"to glare at someone, to look at them sternly\"; intensified, にらみつける. With 〜とにらむ it means \"to suspect, to have one's suspicions\", for example with police or detectives. 見つめる, by contrast, is a neutral, attentive look without hostility. The phrase 上司ににらまれる means \"to be on the boss's bad side\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"景色を眺めています。","romaji":"Keshiki o nagamete imasu.","german":"I am gazing at the scenery.","furigana":"けしき|なが"},{"japanese":"電車の窓から、ぼんやり外を眺めていた。","romaji":"Densha no mado kara, bonyari soto o nagamete ita.","german":"I was vacantly looking outside from the train window."},{"japanese":"子どもたちが遊ぶのを、ベンチに座って眺めていた。","romaji":"Kodomotachi ga asobu no o, benchi ni suwatte nagamete ita.","german":"I sat on a bench and watched the children play.","furigana":"こ|あそ|すわ|なが"}],"notes":"眺める is transitive (〜を眺める) and means \"to look at calmly, let one's gaze wander\", especially at landscapes, the sky or the goings-on outside. It can also mean to watch something idly from the outside. 見る is general, 見つめる is a concentrated stare, and 見渡す is surveying a wide area. The noun 眺め means \"view\": 眺めがいい.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"美しい景色に見惚れました。","romaji":"Utsukushii keshiki ni mitoremashita.","german":"I was captivated by the beautiful scenery.","furigana":"うつく|けしき|みと"},{"japanese":"彼女の笑顔に思わず見惚れてしまった。","romaji":"Kanojo no egao ni omowazu mitorete shimatta.","german":"I couldn't help being captivated by her smile.","furigana":"かのじょ|えがお|おも|みと"},{"japanese":"花火に見惚れていて、写真を撮るのを忘れました。","romaji":"Hanabi ni mitorete ite, shashin o toru no o wasuremashita.","german":"I was so spellbound by the fireworks that I forgot to take photos.","furigana":"はなび|みと|しゃしん|と|わす"}],"notes":"見惚れる is intransitive: what one is spellbound by takes に - 景色に見惚れる, 笑顔に見惚れる. Typical are 思わず見惚れる and つい見惚れてしまう for an involuntary, admiring gaze. Unlike 見つめる (\"to stare at\", with を), 見惚れる emphasizes the fascination in which one forgets everything else. It is often written half in kana as 見とれる; the reading みほれる is rare.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"朝六時に目覚めました。","romaji":"Asa rokuji ni mezamemashita.","german":"I woke up at six in the morning.","furigana":"あさろくじ|めざ"},{"japanese":"高校生のときに音楽に目覚めました。","romaji":"Koukousei no toki ni ongaku ni mezamemashita.","german":"I discovered a passion for music when I was in high school.","furigana":"こうこうせい|おんがく|めざ"},{"japanese":"手術の後、患者はなかなか目覚めなかった。","romaji":"Shujutsu no ato, kanja wa nakanaka mezamenakatta.","german":"After the operation, the patient did not wake up for a long time.","furigana":"しゅじゅつ|あと|かんじゃ|めざ"}],"notes":"目覚める means \"to wake up\", but sounds more elevated and literary than the everyday 目が覚める; \"to get up\" is 起きる, by contrast. Figuratively, 〜に目覚める means \"to awaken to something, to discover something in oneself\": 音楽に目覚める, 才能に目覚める. The verb is intransitive; \"to wake someone\" is 起こす, \"to make someone awaken\" is 目覚めさせる. Related are the noun 目覚め and 目覚まし時計 \"alarm clock\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"夜更かししないでください。","romaji":"Yofukashi shinaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't stay up late.","furigana":"よふ"},{"japanese":"昨日は夜更かしして、今日はとても眠いです。","romaji":"Kinou wa yofukashi shite, kyou wa totemo nemui desu.","german":"I stayed up late yesterday and am very sleepy today.","furigana":"きのう|よふ|きょう|ねむ"},{"japanese":"夜更かしは肌に良くないそうです。","romaji":"Yofukashi wa hada ni yokunai sou desu.","german":"I hear that staying up late is bad for the skin.","furigana":"よふ|はだ|よ"}],"notes":"夜更かし is staying up late at night, usually as an intransitive する-verb: 夜更かしする. It is not about going out or partying, but simply about going to bed late (e.g. because of games, studying, TV series). Someone who stays up all night says 徹夜する. The counterpart is 早寝早起き; it is also often written in a kana mix as 夜ふかし.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は朝寝坊です。","romaji":"Kare wa asanebō desu.","german":"He is a late riser.","furigana":"かれ|あさねぼう"},{"japanese":"今朝は朝寝坊して、会社に遅刻しました。","romaji":"Kesa wa asanebou shite, kaisha ni chikoku shimashita.","german":"I overslept this morning and was late for work.","furigana":"けさ|あさねぼう|かいしゃ|ちこく"},{"japanese":"日曜日はゆっくり朝寝坊したいです。","romaji":"Nichiyoubi wa yukkuri asanebou shitai desu.","german":"On Sunday I want to sleep in leisurely.","furigana":"にちようび|あさねぼう"}],"notes":"朝寝坊 refers above all to oversleeping or sleeping late in the morning: 朝寝坊する, often with the consequence 遅刻する. Shortened, one also says simply 寝坊する. It can also denote a person: 彼は朝寝坊だ \"He is a late sleeper\". The opposite is 早起き; 寝過ごす also means to oversleep, including riding past your stop on a train.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"早起きは三文の徳です。","romaji":"Hayaoki wa sanmon no toku desu.","german":"Der frühe Vogel fängt den Wurm.","furigana":"はやお|さんもん|とく"},{"japanese":"明日は旅行なので、早起きしなければなりません。","romaji":"Ashita wa ryokou na node, hayaoki shinakereba narimasen.","german":"Morgen geht's auf Reise, also muss ich früh aufstehen.","furigana":"あした|りょこう|はやお"},{"japanese":"祖父はとても早起きで、毎朝四時に起きます。","romaji":"Sofu wa totemo hayaoki de, maiasa yoji ni okimasu.","german":"Mein Großvater ist ein echter Frühaufsteher und steht jeden Morgen um vier auf.","furigana":"そふ|はやお|まいあさよじ|お"}],"notes":"早起き ist das frühe Aufstehen, als intransitives する-Verb 早起きする. Es kann auch die Person bezeichnen: 祖父は早起きだ „Großvater ist Frühaufsteher“. Fest verbunden sind 早寝早起き („früh ins Bett, früh raus“) und das Sprichwort 早起きは三文の徳 (auch 得 geschrieben), das dem deutschen „Morgenstund hat Gold im Mund“ entspricht. Gegenteil ist 朝寝坊.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ジョギングが日課です。","romaji":"Jogingu ga nikka desu.","german":"Jogging is my daily routine.","furigana":"にっか"},{"japanese":"寝る前に日記を書くのを日課にしています。","romaji":"Neru mae ni nikki o kaku no o nikka ni shite imasu.","german":"I make it a daily habit to write in my diary before going to bed.","furigana":"ね|まえ|にっき|か|にっか"},{"japanese":"犬の散歩は父の日課です。","romaji":"Inu no sanpo wa chichi no nikka desu.","german":"Walking the dog is my father's daily task.","furigana":"いぬ|さんぽ|ちち|にっか"}],"notes":"日課 is something that one does habitually every day. Typical patterns are 〜が日課だ, 〜のが日課です and 〜を日課にする (\"to make something a daily habit\"). Unlike 習慣 (\"habit\" in general, also bad ones), 日課 is a daily task that is consciously kept up. A daily plan with times, by contrast, is called スケジュール.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"習い事をしています。","romaji":"Naraigoto o shite imasu.","german":"I take lessons.","furigana":"なら|ごと"},{"japanese":"子どもの頃、ピアノと水泳の習い事に通っていました。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, piano to suiei no naraigoto ni kayotte imashita.","german":"As a child, I attended piano and swimming lessons.","furigana":"こ|ころ|すいえい|なら|ごと|かよ"},{"japanese":"大人になってから、新しい習い事を始める人も多いです。","romaji":"Otona ni natte kara, atarashii naraigoto o hajimeru hito mo ooi desu.","german":"Many people also start new lessons after becoming adults.","furigana":"おとな|あたら|なら|ごと|はじ|ひと|おお"}],"notes":"習い事 are courses or lessons outside school, especially hobbies and skills such as piano, swimming, calligraphy or ballet. You say 習い事をする, 習い事に通う or ask 何か習い事をしていますか. Tutoring in school subjects, by contrast, is 塾; teaching at school is 授業. The word is typical for children but is also used by adults.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"毎週土曜日にお茶のお稽古に通っています。","romaji":"Maishuu doyoubi ni ocha no okeiko ni kayotte imasu.","german":"I go to tea ceremony lessons every Saturday.","furigana":"まいしゅうどようび|ちゃ|けいこ|かよ"},{"japanese":"道場で毎日剣道の稽古をしています。","romaji":"Doujou de mainichi kendou no keiko o shite imasu.","german":"I train in kendo at the dojo every day.","furigana":"どうじょう|まいにちけんどう|けいこ"},{"japanese":"本番の前に、役者たちは何度も稽古を重ねた。","romaji":"Honban no mae ni, yakushatachi wa nando mo keiko o kasaneta.","german":"Before the actual performance, the actors rehearsed over and over.","furigana":"ほんばん|まえ|やくしゃ|なんど|けいこ|かさ"}],"notes":"稽古 is practice in traditional arts and martial arts (tea ceremony, ikebana, kendo, sumo) as well as rehearsal in the theater. One says 稽古をする, 稽古に通う, 稽古を重ねる; 稽古をつける means \"to teach, to train someone\". The polite form お稽古 often stands for lessons in tea, flowers or piano. For ordinary practice in sports or music, 練習 is the neutral word.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"何年も修行を積んで、一人前の職人になった。","romaji":"Nannen mo shugyou o tsunde, ichininmae no shokunin ni natta.","german":"After years of hard training, he became a full-fledged craftsman.","furigana":"なんねん|しゅぎょう|つ|いちにんまえ|しょくにん"},{"japanese":"お寺で一週間修行しました。","romaji":"Otera de isshuukan shugyou shimashita.","german":"I trained at a temple for a week.","furigana":"てら|いっしゅうかんしゅぎょう"},{"japanese":"彼はまだ修行中なので、料理を作らせてもらえない。","romaji":"Kare wa mada shugyouchuu na node, ryouri o tsukurasete moraenai.","german":"He is still in training, so he is not allowed to cook yet.","furigana":"かれ|しゅぎょうちゅう|りょうり|つく"}],"notes":"修行 is hard, demanding training: originally religious asceticism (お寺で修行する, 山で修行する), today also the strict apprenticeship in a craft or kitchen (修行を積む, 修行中). The similar 修業（しゅうぎょう） refers more to learning a trade; for craft apprenticeships both spellings are found. Compared with 練習 or 稽古, 修行 sounds much more serious and longer-term. As a する-verb it is usually used without an object: 〜で修行する.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"体を鍛錬します。","romaji":"Karada o tanren shimasu.","german":"I train my body.","furigana":"からだ|たんれん"},{"japanese":"毎朝の鍛錬を欠かしません。","romaji":"Maiasa no tanren o kakashimasen.","german":"I never skip my morning training.","furigana":"まいあさ|たんれん|か"},{"japanese":"武道では、心と体の鍛錬が大切だと言われています。","romaji":"Budou de wa, kokoro to karada no tanren ga taisetsu da to iwarete imasu.","german":"In martial arts, it is said that training of mind and body is important.","furigana":"ぶどう|こころ|からだ|たんれん|たいせつ|い"}],"notes":"鍛錬 (also 鍛練) is steeling body and mind through hard, persistent training; originally it meant forging metal. It sounds written and serious: 心身を鍛錬する, 日々の鍛錬, 鍛錬を怠らない. In everyday speech people prefer the verb 鍛える (体を鍛える) or トレーニングする. The word is typical in the context of martial arts and sports.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本語が上達しました。","romaji":"Nihongo ga jōtatsu shimashita.","german":"My Japanese has improved.","furigana":"にほんご|じょうたつ"},{"japanese":"毎日練習すれば、すぐに上達しますよ。","romaji":"Mainichi renshuu sureba, sugu ni joutatsu shimasu yo.","german":"If you practice every day, you'll improve quickly.","furigana":"まいにちれんしゅう|じょうたつ"},{"japanese":"彼女はテニスの上達がとても早い。","romaji":"Kanojo wa tenisu no joutatsu ga totemo hayai.","german":"She improves at tennis very quickly.","furigana":"かのじょ|じょうたつ|はや"}],"notes":"上達 is progress in a skill: X が上達する (intransitive), for example 日本語が上達する, ピアノが上達する. Common are 上達が早い and 上達のコツ. Colloquially people simply say うまくなる; 進歩 is more general (also technology, science), and 向上 stresses the rise of a level. 上達 doesn't fit health or a situation; there you say 良くなる or 改善する.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"熟練した技術者です。","romaji":"Jukuren shita gijutsusha desu.","german":"He is a skilled engineer.","furigana":"じゅくれん|ぎじゅつしゃ"},{"japanese":"この作業には熟練が必要です。","romaji":"Kono sagyou ni wa jukuren ga hitsuyou desu.","german":"This work requires skill.","furigana":"さぎょう|じゅくれん|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"熟練の職人が一つずつ手で作っています。","romaji":"Jukuren no shokunin ga hitotsu zutsu te de tsukutte imasu.","german":"Skilled craftsmen make them by hand one by one.","furigana":"じゅくれん|しょくにん|ひと|て|つく"}],"notes":"熟練 is skill acquired through long experience. It mostly stands attributively: 熟練した職人, 熟練の技, or in compounds such as 熟練者 and 熟練工. It sounds somewhat formal; colloquially such people are called ベテラン. In contrast to 上手 \"skillful\", 熟練 stresses the long practice behind it.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"料理の腕前が上がりました。","romaji":"Ryōri no udemae ga agarimashita.","german":"My cooking skills have improved.","furigana":"りょうり|うでまえ|あ"},{"japanese":"彼のゴルフの腕前はプロ並みです。","romaji":"Kare no gorufu no udemae wa puro nami desu.","german":"His golf skills are at a professional level.","furigana":"かれ|うでまえ|な"},{"japanese":"今日はお客さんに自慢の腕前を見せるチャンスだ。","romaji":"Kyou wa okyakusan ni jiman no udemae o miseru chansu da.","german":"Today is a chance to show the guests my proud skills.","furigana":"きょう|きゃく|じまん|うでまえ|み"}],"notes":"腕前 is concrete, showable skill in an activity such as cooking, golf or craftwork; 腕 (\"arm\") stands for skill here. Typical are 〜の腕前, 腕前が上がる, 腕前を見せる/披露する and 腕前はプロ並み. The shorter and more colloquial 腕 (腕がいい) also exists. 能力, by contrast, means abstract ability, and 技術 means technique.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"特技はピアノです。","romaji":"Tokugi wa piano desu.","german":"My special skill is piano.","furigana":"とくぎ"},{"japanese":"面接で「特技は何ですか」と聞かれました。","romaji":"Mensetsu de \"tokugi wa nan desu ka\" to kikaremashita.","german":"In the interview, I was asked, \"What is your special skill?\"","furigana":"めんせつ|とくぎ|なん|き"},{"japanese":"彼の特技は、一度聞いた歌をすぐに覚えることです。","romaji":"Kare no tokugi wa, ichido kiita uta o sugu ni oboeru koto desu.","german":"His special skill is remembering a song immediately after hearing it once.","furigana":"かれ|とくぎ|いちどき|うた|おぼ"}],"notes":"特技 is a concrete ability that one can demonstrate, such as an instrument, a language or a trick. On a résumé and in job interviews, the classic question is about 趣味 and 特技 (\"hobbies and special skills\"). 趣味 is what you like to do, 得意 (な-adjective) means \"to be good at\" (ピアノが得意だ), and 才能 is more of an innate talent. The pattern 特技は〜です is typical.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"スポーツの素質があります。","romaji":"Supōtsu no soshitsu ga arimasu.","german":"He has an aptitude for sports.","furigana":"そしつ"},{"japanese":"この子には音楽家としての素質があると先生に言われた。","romaji":"Kono ko ni wa ongakuka to shite no soshitsu ga aru to sensei ni iwareta.","german":"The teacher said this child has what it takes to be a musician.","furigana":"こ|おんがくか|そしつ|せんせい|い"},{"japanese":"素質だけでなく、努力も大切です。","romaji":"Soshitsu dake de naku, doryoku mo taisetsu desu.","german":"Not only aptitude but also effort is important.","furigana":"そしつ|どりょく|たいせつ"}],"notes":"素質 is an innate disposition or potential for something: 〜の素質がある/ない, 素質に恵まれる, 〜としての素質. Unlike 才能, which also means developed talent, 素質 emphasizes the predisposition that still has to be nurtured. Not to be confused with 性質 (\"property, nature\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は天才です。","romaji":"Kare wa tensai desu.","german":"He is a genius.","furigana":"かれ|てんさい"},{"japanese":"彼女は数学の天才と呼ばれています。","romaji":"Kanojo wa suugaku no tensai to yobarete imasu.","german":"She is called a math genius.","furigana":"かのじょ|すうがく|てんさい|よ"},{"japanese":"こんなアイデアを思いつくなんて、君は天才だね！","romaji":"Konna aidea o omoitsuku nante, kimi wa tensai da ne!","german":"To come up with an idea like this, you're a genius!","furigana":"おも|きみ|てんさい"}],"notes":"天才 is a genius or prodigy: 〜の天才 (数学の天才), 天才児, adjectivally 天才的な. Colloquially it is often used as exaggerated praise: お前、天才だな! In contrast, 秀才 is someone who achieves very good results through diligence; the opposite of 天才 is 凡人.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 21"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は凡人です。","romaji":"Watashi wa bonjin desu.","german":"I am an ordinary person.","furigana":"わたし|ぼんじん"},{"japanese":"天才と凡人の違いは何だと思いますか。","romaji":"Tensai to bonjin no chigai wa nan da to omoimasu ka.","german":"What do you think is the difference between a genius and an ordinary person?","furigana":"てんさい|ぼんじん|ちが|なん|おも"},{"japanese":"凡人の私には、彼の考えはよく理解できない。","romaji":"Bonjin no watashi ni wa, kare no kangae wa yoku rikai dekinai.","german":"As an ordinary person, I can't really understand his thinking.","furigana":"ぼんじん|わたし|かれ|かんが|りかい"}],"notes":"凡人 is an ordinary person without special talent, often in contrast to 天才. It is often used humbly about oneself: 私のような凡人には.... The word sounds somewhat literary; neutrally one says 普通の人. 一般人, by contrast, means the \"ordinary citizen\" as opposed to celebrities or experts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"料理の達人です。","romaji":"Ryōri no tatsujin desu.","german":"He is a master of cooking.","furigana":"りょうり|たつじん"},{"japanese":"祖父は剣道の達人だった。","romaji":"Sofu wa kendou no tatsujin datta.","german":"My grandfather was a master of kendo.","furigana":"そふ|けんどう|たつじん"},{"japanese":"彼女は節約の達人で、毎月たくさん貯金している。","romaji":"Kanojo wa setsuyaku no tatsujin de, maitsuki takusan chokin shite iru.","german":"She is a master of saving money and puts away a lot every month.","furigana":"かのじょ|せつやく|たつじん|まいつき|ちょきん"}],"notes":"達人 is someone who masters an art or skill brilliantly: 剣の達人, 料理の達人. Casually it is also used for everyday talents, such as 節約の達人 \"master of thrift\". 名人 sounds more like a title (e.g. in shogi), 専門家 is the specialist with expert knowledge, and プロ the professional who lives off it.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"弟子を取ります。","romaji":"Deshi o torimasu.","german":"I take on a disciple.","furigana":"でし|と"},{"japanese":"有名な料理人の弟子になりました。","romaji":"Yuumei na ryourinin no deshi ni narimashita.","german":"I became an apprentice of a famous chef.","furigana":"ゆうめい|りょうりにん|でし"},{"japanese":"師匠は弟子たちにとても厳しかった。","romaji":"Shishou wa deshitachi ni totemo kibishikatta.","german":"The master was very strict with his disciples.","furigana":"ししょう|でし|きび"}],"notes":"弟子 is the pupil or apprentice of a master (師匠) in traditional arts, crafts, martial arts or religion. Typical are 弟子になる, 弟子入りする (\"to become someone's apprentice\") and 弟子を取る (\"to take on a pupil\"); the first and most important disciple is the 一番弟子. For pupils of a school, by contrast, one says 生徒 or 学生.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"師匠に教わります。","romaji":"Shishō ni osowarimasu.","german":"I learn from my master.","furigana":"ししょう|おそ"},{"japanese":"落語家の師匠に弟子入りを頼んだ。","romaji":"Rakugoka no shishou ni deshiiri o tanonda.","german":"I asked a rakugo master to take me on as an apprentice.","furigana":"らくごか|ししょう|でしい|たの"},{"japanese":"師匠、今日もよろしくお願いします。","romaji":"Shishou, kyou mo yoroshiku onegai shimasu.","german":"Master, I look forward to working with you again today.","furigana":"ししょう|きょう|ねが"}],"notes":"師匠 is the master or teacher in traditional arts, crafts, martial arts or rakugo; the counterpart is 弟子. It is also used as a form of address: 師匠! In sumo it denotes the stable master. 先生 is more general (teacher, doctor, lawyer), whereas 恩師 means a former teacher to whom one is grateful.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"恩師に会いに行きます。","romaji":"Onshi ni ai ni ikimasu.","german":"I am going to visit my former teacher.","furigana":"おんし|あ|い"},{"japanese":"結婚式で恩師がスピーチをしてくれました。","romaji":"Kekkonshiki de onshi ga supiichi o shite kuremashita.","german":"At the wedding, my former teacher gave a speech for me.","furigana":"けっこんしき|おんし"},{"japanese":"今の私があるのは恩師のおかげです。","romaji":"Ima no watashi ga aru no wa onshi no okage desu.","german":"I am who I am today thanks to my former teacher.","furigana":"いま|わたし|おんし"}],"notes":"恩師 is a former teacher to whom one owes a great deal; the word expresses respect and gratitude. It is used about one's own former teachers, for example at class reunions, in wedding speeches or in thank-you notes: 恩師に会う, 恩師のおかげ. As a direct form of address, 先生 is used, not 恩師. 師匠, by contrast, is the master of an art.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"年末に得意先へ挨拶に行きます。","romaji":"Nenmatsu ni tokuisaki e aisatsu ni ikimasu.","german":"At the end of the year, I go to greet our clients.","furigana":"ねんまつ|とくいさき|あいさつ|い"},{"japanese":"営業部の田中さんは毎日得意先を回っています。","romaji":"Eigyoubu no Tanaka-san wa mainichi tokuisaki o mawatte imasu.","german":"Mr. Tanaka from the sales department visits clients every day.","furigana":"えいぎょうぶ|たなか|まいにちとくいさき|まわ"},{"japanese":"大切な得意先を失うわけにはいかない。","romaji":"Taisetsu na tokuisaki o ushinau wake ni wa ikanai.","german":"We can't afford to lose an important client.","furigana":"たいせつ|とくいさき|うしな"}],"notes":"得意先 is a business term for a company's regular customers, mostly in the B2B field. Typical are 得意先を回る \"to make the rounds of clients\", 得意先に挨拶に行く and, politely, お得意様. 取引先 is more general \"business partner\" (also suppliers), and 顧客 is the formal \"customer\". Regulars at shops or restaurants are rather called 常連(客).","levelNote":"Compound: 得意 (N3) + 先 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"顔見知りに会いました。","romaji":"Kaomishiri ni aimashita.","german":"I met an acquaintance.","furigana":"かおみし|あ"},{"japanese":"同じ駅を使っているので、彼とは顔見知りです。","romaji":"Onaji eki o tsukatte iru node, kare to wa kaomishiri desu.","german":"We use the same station, so I know him by sight.","furigana":"おな|えき|つか|かれ|かおみし"},{"japanese":"犯人は被害者の顔見知りだったらしい。","romaji":"Hannin wa higaisha no kaomishiri datta rashii.","german":"The culprit seems to have been an acquaintance of the victim.","furigana":"はんにん|ひがいしゃ|かおみし"}],"notes":"顔見知り is someone you know by sight and perhaps greet, but are not really acquainted with. Typical are 顔見知りになる and 〜とは顔見知りだ. 知り合い is an acquaintance you have already talked to, and 友達 a friend. Do not confuse it with 人見知り \"shyness toward strangers\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"馴染みのお店に行きます。","romaji":"Najimi no omise ni ikimasu.","german":"I go to my regular shop.","furigana":"なじ|みせ|い"},{"japanese":"日本の伝統音楽には、あまり馴染みがありません。","romaji":"Nihon no dentou ongaku ni wa, amari najimi ga arimasen.","german":"I am not very familiar with traditional Japanese music.","furigana":"にほん|でんとうおんがく|なじ"},{"japanese":"テレビでお馴染みの俳優が駅前に来ていた。","romaji":"Terebi de onajimi no haiyuu ga ekimae ni kite ita.","german":"An actor familiar from TV was at the station.","furigana":"なじ|はいゆう|えきまえ|き"}],"notes":"馴染み (often written なじみ) means familiarity through frequent contact. 馴染みの店 is a regular haunt, 馴染みの客 a regular customer, 幼馴染み a friend from childhood. 〜に馴染みがある/ない means \"to be familiar / not familiar with something\", and お馴染み means \"well-known, familiar\" (お馴染みの曲). The verb 馴染む \"to get used to, become familiar\" belongs here.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"住民に説明します。","romaji":"Jūmin ni setsumei shimasu.","german":"I will explain to the residents.","furigana":"じゅうみん|せつめい"},{"japanese":"市役所で住民票を取ってきました。","romaji":"Shiyakusho de juuminhyou o totte kimashita.","german":"I got a certificate of residence at the city hall.","furigana":"しやくしょ|じゅうみんひょう|と"},{"japanese":"新しい道路の計画に、多くの住民が反対している。","romaji":"Atarashii douro no keikaku ni, ooku no juumin ga hantai shite iru.","german":"Many residents are opposed to the new road plan.","furigana":"あたら|どうろ|けいかく|おお|じゅうみん|はんたい"}],"notes":"住民 are the inhabitants of a certain area or place, above all in administrative and news contexts: 地域住民, 住民税, 住民票 \"certificate of residence\", 住民の反対. 市民 stresses the citizen of a city with rights, 国民 the people of a nation. 住人 is more the occupant of a specific house or apartment.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"市民の声を聞きます。","romaji":"Shimin no koe o kikimasu.","german":"We listen to the voices of the citizens.","furigana":"しみん|こえ|き"},{"japanese":"市民プールは夏の間、毎日開いています。","romaji":"Shimin puuru wa natsu no aida, mainichi aite imasu.","german":"The municipal pool is open every day during the summer.","furigana":"しみん|なつ|あいだ|まいにちあ"},{"japanese":"私は十年前から横浜市民です。","romaji":"Watashi wa juunen mae kara Yokohama shimin desu.","german":"I have been a citizen of Yokohama for ten years.","furigana":"わたし|じゅうねんまえ|よこはましみん"}],"notes":"市民 first of all refers to the inhabitants of a city (市): 横浜市民, 市民プール, 市民会館. It is also used more generally for \"citizen\" in the political sense, for example 市民の声 or 市民運動. 国民, by contrast, are the nationals of a country, and 住民 the residents of a particular area or building. The word is neutral and common in administration and news.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"国民の義務です。","romaji":"Kokumin no gimu desu.","german":"It is a duty of the citizens.","furigana":"こくみん|ぎむ"},{"japanese":"五月五日は国民の祝日です。","romaji":"Gogatsu itsuka wa kokumin no shukujitsu desu.","german":"May 5 is a national holiday.","furigana":"ごがついつか|こくみん|しゅくじつ"},{"japanese":"首相は国民に協力を呼びかけた。","romaji":"Shushou wa kokumin ni kyouryoku o yobikaketa.","german":"The prime minister called on the people to cooperate.","furigana":"しゅしょう|こくみん|きょうりょく|よ"}],"notes":"国民 are the people or citizens of a country as a whole, typical in politics, law and the news: 国民の祝日 \"national holiday\", 国民健康保険, 国民性 \"national character\". In contrast, 民族 denotes an ethnic group, 市民 the citizens of a city and 住民 the residents of an area. In everyday speech people rather say 人々 or 日本人.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"漫画は日本の大衆文化の一つです。","romaji":"Manga wa Nihon no taishuu bunka no hitotsu desu.","german":"Manga is part of Japan's popular culture.","furigana":"まんが|にほん|たいしゅうぶんか|ひと"},{"japanese":"駅の近くに安くておいしい大衆食堂があります。","romaji":"Eki no chikaku ni yasukute oishii taishuu shokudou ga arimasu.","german":"Near the station there is an inexpensive and tasty casual restaurant.","furigana":"えき|ちか|やす|たいしゅうしょくどう"},{"japanese":"この雑誌は一般大衆向けに分かりやすく書かれている。","romaji":"Kono zasshi wa ippan taishuu muke ni wakariyasuku kakarete iru.","german":"This magazine is written in an easy-to-understand way for the general public.","furigana":"ざっし|いっぱんたいしゅうむ|わ|か"}],"notes":"大衆 is the broad masses, the general public, especially as consumers and a target audience. It most often appears in compounds: 大衆文化 (\"popular culture\"), 大衆食堂 (\"simple, cheap eatery\"), 大衆向け, and adjectival 大衆的な. 群衆, by contrast, is a concrete crowd of people in one place. On its own, 大衆 sounds somewhat sociological and literary.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"群衆が集まっています。","romaji":"Gunshū ga atsumatte imasu.","german":"A crowd has gathered.","furigana":"ぐんしゅう|あつ"},{"japanese":"群衆の中から子どもの泣き声が聞こえた。","romaji":"Gunshuu no naka kara kodomo no nakigoe ga kikoeta.","german":"The crying of a child could be heard from within the crowd.","furigana":"ぐんしゅう|なか|こ|な|ごえ|き"},{"japanese":"警察が群衆に道を空けるよう呼びかけた。","romaji":"Keisatsu ga gunshuu ni michi o akeru you yobikaketa.","german":"The police called on the crowd to clear the way.","furigana":"けいさつ|ぐんしゅう|みち|あ|よ"}],"notes":"群衆 is a crowd of people that has physically gathered in one place: 群衆が集まる, 群衆の中, 群衆を押しのける. The word is rather literary and typical of the news; in everyday speech one says 人込み or 大勢の人. Unlike 大衆 (\"the broad masses\" as a social stratum), 群衆 always means a concrete crowd.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"年齢を聞いてもいいですか。","romaji":"Nenrei o kiite mo ii desu ka.","german":"May I ask your age?","furigana":"ねんれい|き"},{"japanese":"このアルバイトは年齢を問わず応募できます。","romaji":"Kono arubaito wa nenrei o towazu oubo dekimasu.","german":"You can apply for this part-time job regardless of age.","furigana":"ねんれい|と|おうぼ"},{"japanese":"申込書に名前と年齢を書いてください。","romaji":"Moushikomisho ni namae to nenrei o kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write your name and age on the application form.","furigana":"もうしこみしょ|なまえ|ねんれい|か"}],"notes":"年齢 is the formal word for \"age\" and typically appears in forms, rules and statistics: 年齢制限, 年齢を問わず (\"regardless of age\"), 年齢層. In conversation one says 年（とし） more often, asks 何歳ですか, or politely おいくつですか. Asking directly about someone's 年齢 sounds somewhat official.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 38"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"性別は関係ありません。","romaji":"Seibetsu wa kankei arimasen.","german":"Gender does not matter.","furigana":"せいべつ|かんけい"},{"japanese":"用紙に名前、年齢、性別を記入してください。","romaji":"Youshi ni namae, nenrei, seibetsu o kinyuu shite kudasai.","german":"Please enter your name, age and gender on the form.","furigana":"ようし|なまえ|ねんれい|せいべつ|きにゅう"},{"japanese":"生まれる前に赤ちゃんの性別が分かりました。","romaji":"Umareru mae ni akachan no seibetsu ga wakarimashita.","german":"We knew the baby's sex before it was born.","furigana":"う|まえ|あか|せいべつ|わ"}],"notes":"性別 is gender as a category, for example in forms, statistics and rules: 性別欄, 性別を問わず (\"regardless of gender\"), 性別による差別. In conversation people ask 男の子？女の子？ rather than about the 性別. For man/woman there are 男性 and 女性.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"血液型は何型ですか。","romaji":"Ketsuekigata wa nanigata desu ka.","german":"What is your blood type?","furigana":"けつえきがた|なにがた"},{"japanese":"日本では血液型で性格を占う人が多い。","romaji":"Nihon de wa ketsuekigata de seikaku o uranau hito ga ooi.","german":"In Japan, many people tell fortunes by blood type.","furigana":"にほん|けつえきがた|せいかく|うらな|ひと|おお"},{"japanese":"手術の前に血液型を調べます。","romaji":"Shujutsu no mae ni ketsuekigata o shirabemasu.","german":"They check your blood type before surgery.","furigana":"しゅじゅつ|まえ|けつえきがた|しら"}],"notes":"血液型 is the blood type; the types are called A型, B型, O型 and AB型, and one asks 何型ですか. In Japan, blood type is a popular small-talk topic because many people believe it reveals character (血液型占い, 血液型性格診断). Medically it appears, for example, in 血液型を調べる.","levelNote":"Compound: 血液 (N3) + 型 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"星座は何座ですか。","romaji":"Seiza wa naniza desu ka.","german":"What is your star sign?","furigana":"せいざ|なにざ"},{"japanese":"冬の夜空にはオリオン座などの星座がよく見えます。","romaji":"Fuyu no yozora ni wa Orionza nado no seiza ga yoku miemasu.","german":"In the winter night sky, you can see constellations such as Orion well.","furigana":"ふゆ|よぞら|ざ|せいざ|み"},{"japanese":"朝のテレビで星座占いを見るのが好きです。","romaji":"Asa no terebi de seiza uranai o miru no ga suki desu.","german":"I like watching the horoscope on TV in the morning.","furigana":"あさ|せいざうらな|み|す"}],"notes":"星座 means both the constellation in the sky and the zodiac sign in astrology. Individual constellations/signs are formed with 〜座: オリオン座, しし座, おうし座; the question is 何座ですか. The horoscope by star sign is called 星座占い and often airs on morning TV in Japan.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"縁起がいいですね。","romaji":"Engi ga ii desu ne.","german":"That's a good omen, isn't it?","furigana":"えんぎ"},{"japanese":"縁起でもないことを言わないでください。","romaji":"Engi demo nai koto o iwanaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't say such ominous things.","furigana":"えんぎ|い"},{"japanese":"日本では四は「死」と同じ音なので、縁起が悪い数字とされている。","romaji":"Nihon de wa shi wa \"shi\" to onaji oto na node, engi ga warui suuji to sarete iru.","german":"In Japan, four is considered an unlucky number because it sounds the same as \"death\".","furigana":"にほん|し|し|おな|おと|えんぎ|わる|すうじ"}],"notes":"縁起 refers to an omen of good or bad luck: 縁起がいい/悪い, 縁起物 \"lucky charm\", 縁起を担ぐ \"to pay attention to good omens\". The set phrase 縁起でもない wards off ominous words, as in \"Don't tempt fate!\". A second, written meaning is the origin story of a temple or shrine (寺の縁起). 運, by contrast, is luck itself.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"それは迷信です。","romaji":"Sore wa meishin desu.","german":"That is a superstition.","furigana":"めいしん"},{"japanese":"夜に爪を切ってはいけないという迷信があります。","romaji":"Yoru ni tsume o kitte wa ikenai to iu meishin ga arimasu.","german":"There is a superstition that you must not cut your nails at night.","furigana":"よる|つめ|き|めいしん"},{"japanese":"祖母は今でも古い迷信を信じています。","romaji":"Sobo wa ima demo furui meishin o shinjite imasu.","german":"My grandmother still believes in old superstitions.","furigana":"そぼ|いま|ふる|めいしん|しん"}],"notes":"迷信 is superstition, that is, a belief without rational basis: 迷信を信じる, ただの迷信, 〜という迷信がある, 迷信深い (\"superstitious\"). Unlike 縁起 (a concrete sign of good or bad luck) and 言い伝え (tradition, not necessarily false), 迷信 dismisses the matter as unfounded.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"古い言い伝えがあります。","romaji":"Furui iitsutae ga arimasu.","german":"There is an old legend.","furigana":"ふる|い|つた"},{"japanese":"この池には竜が住んでいるという言い伝えがある。","romaji":"Kono ike ni wa ryuu ga sunde iru to iu iitsutae ga aru.","german":"According to legend, a dragon lives in this pond.","furigana":"いけ|りゅう|す|い|つた"},{"japanese":"祖父から聞いた言い伝えを、子どもたちに話しました。","romaji":"Sofu kara kiita iitsutae o, kodomotachi ni hanashimashita.","german":"I told the children a legend I had heard from my grandfather.","furigana":"そふ|き|い|つた|こ|はな"}],"notes":"言い伝え is something passed down orally from generation to generation: 昔からの言い伝え, 〜という言い伝えがある, 言い伝えによると. It can be a legend, a piece of folk wisdom or a folk belief. 伝説 is more of an embellished legend about places or heroes, and 迷信 a belief judged to be unfounded. The related verb is 言い伝える.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 37"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本の昔話を読みます。","romaji":"Nihon no mukashibanashi o yomimasu.","german":"I read Japanese folk tales.","furigana":"にほん|むかしばなし|よ"},{"japanese":"「桃太郎」は有名な昔話です。","romaji":"\"Momotarou\" wa yuumei na mukashibanashi desu.","german":"\"Momotaro\" is a famous folk tale.","furigana":"ももたろう|ゆうめい|むかしばなし"},{"japanese":"同窓会で昔話に花が咲いた。","romaji":"Dousoukai de mukashibanashi ni hana ga saita.","german":"At the class reunion, we got caught up in reminiscing.","furigana":"どうそうかい|むかしばなし|はな|さ"}],"notes":"昔話 are, on the one hand, folk tales such as 桃太郎 or 浦島太郎, which begin with むかしむかし. On the other hand, it means telling about the past: 昔話をする, 昔話に花が咲く (\"to indulge in reminiscences\"). 童話 is more of a children's story (often written by an author), and 物語 means a story or tale in general.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 41"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"美しい物語です。","romaji":"Utsukushii monogatari desu.","german":"It is a beautiful story.","furigana":"うつく|ものがたり"},{"japanese":"これは小さな村で育った少年の物語です。","romaji":"Kore wa chiisana mura de sodatta shounen no monogatari desu.","german":"This is the story of a boy who grew up in a small village.","furigana":"ちい|むら|そだ|しょうねん|ものがたり"},{"japanese":"『源氏物語』は千年以上前に書かれた。","romaji":"\"Genji monogatari\" wa sennen ijou mae ni kakareta.","german":"\"The Tale of Genji\" was written more than a thousand years ago.","furigana":"げんじものがたり|せんねんいじょうまえ|か"}],"notes":"物語 is a story or tale, fictional or true: 〜の物語, 物語を書く/語る. Many classical works carry it in the title, for example 源氏物語 and 竹取物語. 話 is more general (\"conversation, story\"), ストーリー refers more to the plot, and 小説 to the novel. The verb 物語る figuratively means \"to show clearly\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"古い伝説があります。","romaji":"Furui densetsu ga arimasu.","german":"There is an old legend.","furigana":"ふる|でんせつ"},{"japanese":"この湖には人魚の伝説が残っています。","romaji":"Kono mizuumi ni wa ningyo no densetsu ga nokotte imasu.","german":"A legend of a mermaid remains about this lake.","furigana":"みずうみ|にんぎょ|でんせつ|のこ"},{"japanese":"彼は伝説のサッカー選手として今も人気がある。","romaji":"Kare wa densetsu no sakkaa senshu to shite ima mo ninki ga aru.","german":"He is still popular today as a legendary soccer player.","furigana":"かれ|でんせつ|せんしゅ|いま|にんき"}],"notes":"伝説 is a legend about places, heroes or events: 〜の伝説, 〜という伝説がある, 伝説が残る; 都市伝説 is the \"urban legend\". In modern use, 伝説の is also used for \"legendary\": 伝説の選手, 伝説のバンド. 言い伝え stresses oral tradition, and 神話 stresses myths of the gods.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本神話を学びます。","romaji":"Nihon shinwa o manabimasu.","german":"I study Japanese mythology.","furigana":"にほんしんわ|まな"},{"japanese":"ギリシャ神話には多くの神が登場します。","romaji":"Girisha shinwa ni wa ooku no kami ga toujou shimasu.","german":"Many gods appear in Greek mythology.","furigana":"しんわ|おお|かみ|とうじょう"},{"japanese":"地震で、この国の「安全神話」は崩れた。","romaji":"Jishin de, kono kuni no \"anzen shinwa\" wa kuzureta.","german":"The earthquake shattered this country's \"myth of safety\".","furigana":"じしん|くに|あんぜんしんわ|くず"}],"notes":"神話 is a myth or the mythology of a people: ギリシャ神話, 日本神話 (handed down in the 古事記, among others). Figuratively, 〜神話 denotes a widespread but unfounded belief, such as 安全神話 (\"myth of safety\"); typical is 神話が崩れる. Legends about people and places, by contrast, are called 伝説.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"妖怪の話が好きです。","romaji":"Yōkai no hanashi ga suki desu.","german":"I like stories about yokai.","furigana":"ようかい|はなし|す"},{"japanese":"河童や天狗は有名な妖怪です。","romaji":"Kappa ya tengu wa yuumei na youkai desu.","german":"Kappa and tengu are famous yokai.","furigana":"かっぱ|てんぐ|ゆうめい|ようかい"},{"japanese":"夜の山には妖怪が出ると、子どもの頃よく言われた。","romaji":"Yoru no yama ni wa youkai ga deru to, kodomo no koro yoku iwareta.","german":"When I was a child, I was often told that yokai appear in the mountains at night.","furigana":"よる|やま|ようかい|で|こ|ころ|い"}],"notes":"妖怪 are supernatural beings of Japanese folklore, such as 河童, 天狗 or 雪女; you say 妖怪が出る. 幽霊, by contrast, is the ghost of a dead person, and お化け is the colloquial umbrella term for haunting creatures. Through manga and anime such as ゲゲゲの鬼太郎 or 妖怪ウォッチ, 妖怪 are also very present in pop culture.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"幽霊を見ましたか。","romaji":"Yūrei o mimashita ka.","german":"Have you seen a ghost?","furigana":"ゆうれい|み"},{"japanese":"この古い旅館には幽霊が出るといううわさがある。","romaji":"Kono furui ryokan ni wa yuurei ga deru to iu uwasa ga aru.","german":"There is a rumor that this old inn is haunted.","furigana":"ふる|りょかん|ゆうれい|で"},{"japanese":"彼はテニス部の幽霊部員で、練習に一度も来ない。","romaji":"Kare wa tenisubu no yuurei buin de, renshuu ni ichido mo konai.","german":"He is a ghost member of the tennis club and never comes to practice.","furigana":"かれ|ぶ|ゆうれいぶいん|れんしゅう|いちど|こ"}],"notes":"幽霊 is the spirit of a dead person; in the Japanese image, in a white robe and without feet. Typical are 幽霊が出る (\"it is haunted\"), 幽霊を見る and 幽霊屋敷 (\"haunted house\"). Figuratively, 幽霊〜 stands for something that exists only on paper: 幽霊部員 (inactive club member), 幽霊会社 (shell company). 妖怪, by contrast, are legendary creatures, and お化け is the casual everyday word.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"節分に鬼が来ます。","romaji":"Setsubun ni oni ga kimasu.","german":"The oni comes on Setsubun.","furigana":"せつぶん|おに|き"},{"japanese":"鬼ごっこで、次はぼくが鬼だ。","romaji":"Onigokko de, tsugi wa boku ga oni da.","german":"In the game of tag, I'm \"it\" next.","furigana":"おに|つぎ|おに"},{"japanese":"部長は仕事になると鬼のように厳しい。","romaji":"Buchou wa shigoto ni naru to oni no you ni kibishii.","german":"When it comes to work, the department manager is demonically strict.","furigana":"ぶちょう|しごと|おに|きび"}],"notes":"鬼 is the horned ogre of Japanese folklore, often red or blue, with an iron club and tiger-skin loincloth. At 節分, families throw beans and shout 鬼は外、福は内. In tag (鬼ごっこ) or hide-and-seek, the 鬼 is the chaser or seeker. Figuratively it stands for relentlessness: 鬼のように厳しい, 仕事の鬼, 鬼コーチ; in youth slang it is also an intensifier (鬼かわいい).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"天狗の伝説があります。","romaji":"Tengu no densetsu ga arimasu.","german":"There is a legend of the tengu.","furigana":"てんぐ|でんせつ"},{"japanese":"天狗は赤い顔と長い鼻を持つ妖怪です。","romaji":"Tengu wa akai kao to nagai hana o motsu youkai desu.","german":"The tengu is a yokai with a red face and a long nose.","furigana":"てんぐ|あか|かお|なが|はな|も|ようかい"},{"japanese":"一度試合に勝ったくらいで天狗になってはいけない。","romaji":"Ichido shiai ni katta kurai de tengu ni natte wa ikenai.","german":"You shouldn't get conceited just because you won a game once.","furigana":"いちどしあい|か|てんぐ"}],"notes":"天狗 is a mountain spirit of Japanese folklore with a red face, long nose, wings and a feather fan; a variant with a bird's beak is called 烏天狗. Because of the long nose (cf. 鼻が高い \"to be proud\"), 天狗 figuratively stands for arrogance: 天狗になる \"to become conceited\", often after a success. The word is usually written in kanji.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"河童は日本の妖怪です。","romaji":"Kappa wa Nihon no youkai desu.","german":"The kappa is a Japanese yokai.","furigana":"かっぱ|にほん|ようかい"},{"japanese":"河童はきゅうりが好きだと言われています。","romaji":"Kappa wa kyuuri ga suki da to iwarete imasu.","german":"It is said that kappa like cucumbers.","furigana":"かっぱ|す|い"},{"japanese":"「河童の川流れ」とは、名人でも失敗することがあるという意味です。","romaji":"\"Kappa no kawanagare\" to wa, meijin demo shippai suru koto ga aru to iu imi desu.","german":"\"Kappa no kawanagare\" means that even masters sometimes make mistakes.","furigana":"かっぱ|かわなが|めいじん|しっぱい|いみ"}],"notes":"河童 is a water creature of rivers and ponds with a water-filled dish (皿) on its head, a beak and a shell; it is often also written カッパ. Because it supposedly loves cucumbers, the cucumber sushi roll is called かっぱ巻き. The proverb 河童の川流れ (\"even a kappa is swept away by the river\") means that even masters make mistakes.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"請求書を送ります。","romaji":"Seikyūsho o okurimasu.","german":"I will send the invoice.","furigana":"せいきゅうしょ|おく"},{"japanese":"修理代として一万円を請求されました。","romaji":"Shuuridai to shite ichiman'en o seikyuu saremashita.","german":"I was charged 10,000 yen for the repair.","furigana":"しゅうりだい|いちまんえん|せいきゅう"},{"japanese":"会社に交通費を請求するのを忘れていた。","romaji":"Kaisha ni koutsuuhi o seikyuu suru no o wasurete ita.","german":"I forgot to claim my transportation costs from the company.","furigana":"かいしゃ|こうつうひ|せいきゅう|わす"}],"notes":"請求 is demanding money or a right; as a transitive する-verb: (人)に料金を請求する, passive 〜を請求される \"to be billed\". Very common are 請求書 \"invoice\", 請求額 and 損害賠償を請求する. 要求 is a more general \"to demand\", 依頼 a polite request; the receipt after payment is called 領収書.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"納品日を確認します。","romaji":"Nōhinbi o kakunin shimasu.","german":"I will check the delivery date.","furigana":"のうひんび|かくにん"},{"japanese":"注文された商品は来週の月曜日に納品します。","romaji":"Chuumon sareta shouhin wa raishuu no getsuyoubi ni nouhin shimasu.","german":"The ordered goods will be delivered next Monday.","furigana":"ちゅうもん|しょうひん|らいしゅう|げつようび|のうひん"},{"japanese":"納品書と請求書を一緒にお送りします。","romaji":"Nouhinsho to seikyuusho o issho ni ookuri shimasu.","german":"We will send the delivery note and the invoice together.","furigana":"のうひんしょ|せいきゅうしょ|いっしょ|おく"}],"notes":"納品 is the delivery of ordered goods to a business customer; as a する-verb it is mostly transitive: 商品を納品する. Typical are 納品日, 納品書 \"delivery note\" and 期日までに納品する. 配達 is delivery (mail, parcels, pizza) to private individuals, and 出荷 shipping from a warehouse. The word clearly belongs to business language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"株式を買いました。","romaji":"Kabushiki o kaimashita.","german":"I bought shares.","furigana":"かぶしき|か"},{"japanese":"父は小さな株式会社を経営しています。","romaji":"Chichi wa chiisana kabushiki gaisha o keiei shite imasu.","german":"My father runs a small joint-stock company.","furigana":"ちち|ちい|かぶしきがいしゃ|けいえい"},{"japanese":"最近、株式市場が大きく動いている。","romaji":"Saikin, kabushiki shijou ga ookiku ugoite iru.","german":"Recently, the stock market has been moving sharply.","furigana":"さいきん|かぶしきしじょう|おお|うご"}],"notes":"株式 is the share as a formal economic term: 株式市場, 株式投資, 株式を発行する. It is most often met in 株式会社 (\"joint-stock company\", read かぶしきがいしゃ), which appears in almost every company name. In everyday speech, shares are usually just 株: 株を買う.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"届出を提出します。","romaji":"Todokede o teishutsu shimasu.","german":"I will submit the notification.","furigana":"とどけで|ていしゅつ"},{"japanese":"引っ越したら、十四日以内に市役所に届出が必要です。","romaji":"Hikkoshitara, juuyokka inai ni shiyakusho ni todokede ga hitsuyou desu.","german":"When you move, you need to file a notification at the city hall within 14 days.","furigana":"ひ|こ|じゅうよっかいない|しやくしょ|とどけで|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"財布をなくしたので、警察に届出をしました。","romaji":"Saifu o nakushita node, keisatsu ni todokede o shimashita.","german":"I lost my wallet, so I filed a report with the police.","furigana":"さいふ|けいさつ|とどけで"}],"notes":"届出 (also 届け出) is an official notification to authorities, the police or an employer: 届出をする, 届出を提出する, 届出が必要だ. Certain notifications are named with the ending 〜届（とどけ）: 婚姻届, 出生届, 転居届. The verb is 届け出る. 申請, by contrast, is an application for permission, and 申込 a registration for something.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"申込書を書いてください。","romaji":"Mōshikomisho o kaite kudasai.","german":"Please fill in the application form.","furigana":"もうしこみしょ|か"},{"japanese":"申込の締め切りは今月の三十日です。","romaji":"Moushikomi no shimekiri wa kongetsu no sanjuunichi desu.","german":"The application deadline is the 30th of this month.","furigana":"もうしこみ|し|き|こんげつ|さんじゅうにち"},{"japanese":"このセミナーはインターネットで申込ができます。","romaji":"Kono seminaa wa intaanetto de moushikomi ga dekimasu.","german":"You can sign up for this seminar online.","furigana":"もうしこみ"}],"notes":"申込 is the application, sign-up or order for something. In running text one usually writes 申し込み; the short form 申込 appears mainly in compounds and forms: 申込書, 申込用紙, 申込締切. The verb is 申し込む (〜に申し込む). 申請 is an application to an authority, 応募 is applying for a job, competition or prize draw, and 予約 is a reservation.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"上司の承認が必要です。","romaji":"Jōshi no shōnin ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"Your boss's approval is required.","furigana":"じょうし|しょうにん|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"計画は会議で承認されました。","romaji":"Keikaku wa kaigi de shounin saremashita.","german":"The plan was approved at the meeting.","furigana":"けいかく|かいぎ|しょうにん"},{"japanese":"新しい薬が国に承認された。","romaji":"Atarashii kusuri ga kuni ni shounin sareta.","german":"The new drug was approved by the government.","furigana":"あたら|くすり|くに|しょうにん"}],"notes":"承認 is formal approval by an authority or a superior: 承認を得る, 承認が下りる, 〜を承認する, passive 承認される. 許可 is permission to do something, and 賛成 agreement with an opinion. In addition, 承認 means \"recognition\", for example in 承認欲求 \"need for recognition\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"提案が却下されました。","romaji":"Teian ga kyakka saremashita.","german":"The proposal was rejected.","furigana":"ていあん|きゃっか"},{"japanese":"ビザの申請が却下されてしまいました。","romaji":"Biza no shinsei ga kyakka sarete shimaimashita.","german":"My visa application was rejected.","furigana":"しんせい|きゃっか"},{"japanese":"裁判所は訴えを却下した。","romaji":"Saibansho wa uttae o kyakka shita.","german":"The court dismissed the lawsuit.","furigana":"さいばんしょ|うった|きゃっか"}],"notes":"却下 is the formal rejection of an application, proposal or lawsuit by a competent body; mostly passive: 申請が却下される, 提案が却下される. The word is official or legal; in everyday speech people say 断る or 認められない. 拒否 is a firm refusal, and 不採用 a rejection in a job application.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"採用が決まりました。","romaji":"Saiyō ga kimarimashita.","german":"The hiring has been decided.","furigana":"さいよう|き"},{"japanese":"私の案が会議で採用されました。","romaji":"Watashi no an ga kaigi de saiyou saremashita.","german":"My proposal was adopted at the meeting.","furigana":"わたし|あん|かいぎ|さいよう"},{"japanese":"この会社は毎年三十人ぐらい新しい社員を採用します。","romaji":"Kono kaisha wa maitoshi sanjuunin gurai atarashii shain o saiyou shimasu.","german":"This company hires about thirty new employees every year.","furigana":"かいしゃ|まいとしさんじゅうにん|あたら|しゃいん|さいよう"}],"notes":"採用 has two main meanings: \"hiring\" of personnel (社員を採用する, 採用試験, 採用が決まる \"to get an offer\") and \"adoption, acceptance\" of ideas, proposals or methods (案を採用する, 新しいシステムを採用する). It is a transitive する-verb; from the viewpoint of the applicant or the proposal one says 採用される, and a rejection is 不採用. The everyday 雇う \"to employ\" is more general; 採用 sounds more official and refers to the selection process.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"会議室を予約しました。","romaji":"Kaigishitsu o yoyaku shimashita.","german":"I reserved the conference room.","furigana":"かいぎしつ|よやく"},{"japanese":"会議室にプロジェクターを準備しておいてください。","romaji":"Kaigishitsu ni purojekutaa o junbi shite oite kudasai.","german":"Please set up the projector in the conference room.","furigana":"かいぎしつ|じゅんび"},{"japanese":"部長はまだ会議室にいますか。","romaji":"Buchou wa mada kaigishitsu ni imasu ka.","german":"Is the department head still in the conference room?","furigana":"ぶちょう|かいぎしつ"}],"notes":"会議室 is the conference or meeting room in a company or government office. Typical are 会議室を予約する or, colloquially, 会議室を押さえる \"to block the room\"; meetings take place 会議室で. When there are several rooms, they are numbered: 第一会議室. The loanword ミーティングルーム is also common.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"業務提携を結びました。","romaji":"Gyōmu teikei o musubimashita.","german":"We concluded a business partnership.","furigana":"ぎょうむていけい|むす"},{"japanese":"うちの会社は韓国の会社と提携しています。","romaji":"Uchi no kaisha wa Kankoku no kaisha to teikei shite imasu.","german":"Our company has a partnership with a Korean company.","furigana":"かいしゃ|かんこく|かいしゃ|ていけい"},{"japanese":"提携しているホテルなら、会員は割引料金で泊まれます。","romaji":"Teikei shite iru hoteru nara, kaiin wa waribiki ryoukin de tomaremasu.","german":"At partner hotels, members can stay at discounted rates.","furigana":"ていけい|かいいん|わりびきりょうきん|と"}],"notes":"提携 refers to contractually regulated cooperation between companies, organizations or universities. Typical are 業務提携 \"business partnership\", 提携を結ぶ \"to enter into a partnership\" and 〜と提携する \"to partner with ...\". As a first element: 提携先 \"partner company\", 提携校 \"partner school\", 提携店 \"partner shop\". For personal cooperation in everyday life one rather says 協力; 提携 belongs to business and news language.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大規模なプロジェクトです。","romaji":"Daikibo na purojekuto desu.","german":"It is a large-scale project.","furigana":"だいきぼ"},{"japanese":"この地震は規模が大きかったです。","romaji":"Kono jishin wa kibo ga ookikatta desu.","german":"The scale of this earthquake was large.","furigana":"じしん|きぼ|おお"},{"japanese":"会社の規模は小さいですが、給料はいいです。","romaji":"Kaisha no kibo wa chiisai desu ga, kyuuryou wa ii desu.","german":"The company is small in scale, but the pay is good.","furigana":"かいしゃ|きぼ|ちい|きゅうりょう"}],"notes":"規模 means \"scale, extent, magnitude\" of projects, companies, events or disasters. Very common in compounds: 大規模 \"large-scale\", 小規模 \"small-scale\", 世界規模 \"worldwide\", attributively with な or の (大規模な工事, 世界規模の問題). Used freely, it appears with が大きい/小さい or 規模を拡大する \"to expand\". For the mere physical size of an object, one uses 大きさ or サイズ instead, not 規模.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"検査を受けます。","romaji":"Kensa o ukemasu.","german":"I will have an examination.","furigana":"けんさ|う"},{"japanese":"空港で荷物の検査がありました。","romaji":"Kuukou de nimotsu no kensa ga arimashita.","german":"There was a baggage inspection at the airport.","furigana":"くうこう|にもつ|けんさ"},{"japanese":"工場では、お店に送る前に全部の製品を検査します。","romaji":"Koujou de wa, omise ni okuru mae ni zenbu no seihin o kensa shimasu.","german":"At the factory, all products are inspected before being sent to stores.","furigana":"こうじょう|みせ|おく|まえ|ぜんぶ|せいひん|けんさ"}],"notes":"検査 is a systematic check or examination: in a hospital (血液検査 \"blood test\", 検査を受ける \"to undergo an examination\", 検査結果 \"test result\"), in a factory (製品を検査する) or at checks (荷物検査). It differs from the similar 試験 in that 試験 is usually an exam on knowledge or skills; 点検 is more the regular maintenance check of equipment, and 診察 the medical examination in the consulting room.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"処方箋をもらいました。","romaji":"Shohōsen o moraimashita.","german":"I got a prescription.","furigana":"しょほうせん"},{"japanese":"医者に風邪薬を処方してもらいました。","romaji":"Isha ni kazegusuri o shohou shite moraimashita.","german":"The doctor prescribed me cold medicine.","furigana":"いしゃ|かぜぐすり|しょほう"},{"japanese":"処方された薬は全部飲んでください。","romaji":"Shohou sareta kusuri wa zenbu nonde kudasai.","german":"Please take all of the prescribed medicine.","furigana":"しょほう|くすり|ぜんぶの"}],"notes":"処方 is the medical prescription of drugs: 医者が薬を処方する, from the patient's view 薬を処方してもらう or 処方される. The prescription as a document is called 処方箋; with it you pick up the medicine at the pharmacy (薬局). Over-the-counter drugs are called 市販の薬. In a broader sense, 処方 can also mean \"formulation, formula\"; a cooking recipe, by contrast, is レシピ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"体調が悪いです。","romaji":"Taichō ga warui desu.","german":"I'm not feeling well.","furigana":"たいちょう|わる"},{"japanese":"季節の変わり目は体調を崩しやすいです。","romaji":"Kisetsu no kawarime wa taichou o kuzushiyasui desu.","german":"It is easy to get sick when the seasons change.","furigana":"きせつ|か|め|たいちょう|くず"},{"japanese":"試合の前は体調管理に気をつけています。","romaji":"Shiai no mae wa taichou kanri ni ki o tsukete imasu.","german":"Before a match, I am careful about managing my physical condition.","furigana":"しあい|まえ|たいちょうかんり|き"}],"notes":"体調 is one's current physical condition. Typical: 体調がいい/悪い, 体調を崩す \"to get sick, not feel well\", 体調を整える \"to get in shape\", 体調管理 \"taking care of one's health\"; politely one asks 体調はいかがですか. In contrast to 健康 (\"health\" in general, long-term), 体調 describes the state on a particular day or in a phase; 具合 is more colloquial and can also refer to things.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"疲労がたまっています。","romaji":"Hirō ga tamatte imasu.","german":"Fatigue has built up.","furigana":"ひろう"},{"japanese":"温泉は疲労回復にいいそうです。","romaji":"Onsen wa hirou kaifuku ni ii sou desu.","german":"Hot springs are said to be good for recovering from fatigue.","furigana":"おんせん|ひろうかいふく"},{"japanese":"事故の原因は運転手の疲労だと考えられています。","romaji":"Jiko no gen'in wa untenshu no hirou da to kangaerarete imasu.","german":"The cause of the accident is thought to be the driver's fatigue.","furigana":"じこ|げんいん|うんてんしゅ|ひろう|かんが"}],"notes":"疲労 is the Sino-Japanese, rather factual word for \"fatigue, exhaustion\". Typical collocations: 疲労がたまる \"to build up\", 疲労がとれる \"to go away\", 疲労回復 \"recovery\" (often in ads for baths and drinks). In everyday speech one usually says 疲れ or 疲れた; 疲労 sounds medical, newsy or technical. Technically it also refers to material fatigue (金属疲労).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一日も早いご回復をお祈りしています。","romaji":"Ichinichi mo hayai go-kaifuku o oinori shite imasu.","german":"I pray for your speedy recovery.","furigana":"いちにち|はや|かいふく|いの"},{"japanese":"午後から天気が回復するでしょう。","romaji":"Gogo kara tenki ga kaifuku suru deshou.","german":"The weather will improve from the afternoon.","furigana":"ごご|てんき|かいふく"},{"japanese":"手術のあと、父は順調に回復しています。","romaji":"Shujutsu no ato, chichi wa junchou ni kaifuku shite imasu.","german":"After the surgery, my father is recovering well.","furigana":"しゅじゅつ|ちち|じゅんちょう|かいふく"}],"notes":"回復 means that a former good state returns: health (病気から回復する, 体力が回復する), but also weather (天気が回復する), the economy (景気の回復) or trust. It is mostly intransitive with が, but also transitive with を (信頼を回復する \"to restore trust\"). A polite get-well wish is 一日も早いご回復をお祈りしています. 治る refers specifically to the healing of an illness or wound, whereas 回復 is broader.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"応急処置をします。","romaji":"Ōkyū shochi o shimasu.","german":"I give first aid.","furigana":"おうきゅうしょち"},{"japanese":"病院で傷の処置をしてもらいました。","romaji":"Byouin de kizu no shochi o shite moraimashita.","german":"I had my wound treated at the hospital.","furigana":"びょういん|きず|しょち"},{"japanese":"会社はすぐに適切な処置をとりました。","romaji":"Kaisha wa sugu ni tekisetsu na shochi o torimashita.","german":"The company immediately took appropriate measures.","furigana":"かいしゃ|てきせつ|しょち"}],"notes":"処置 is the concrete measure with which one reacts to a situation. In the medical field: 応急処置 \"first aid\", 傷の処置 \"wound care\", 処置室 \"treatment room\". In general: 適切な処置をとる \"to take appropriate measures\". Similar are 対策 (countermeasure, often preventive) and 治療 (medical therapy over a longer period); 処置 stresses immediate action in an individual case.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"判決が出ました。","romaji":"Hanketsu ga demashita.","german":"The verdict has come out.","furigana":"はんけつ|で"},{"japanese":"裁判所は男に有罪の判決を言い渡しました。","romaji":"Saibansho wa otoko ni yuuzai no hanketsu o iiwatashimashita.","german":"The court sentenced the man as guilty.","furigana":"さいばんしょ|おとこ|ゆうざい|はんけつ|い|わた"},{"japanese":"判決に納得できないので、もう一度裁判をすることにしました。","romaji":"Hanketsu ni nattoku dekinai node, mou ichido saiban o suru koto ni shimashita.","german":"Because they could not accept the ruling, they decided to go to court again.","furigana":"はんけつ|なっとく|いちどさいばん"}],"notes":"判決 is the judgment of a court. Typical: 判決が出る/下る \"a verdict is handed down\", 判決を言い渡す \"to pronounce a verdict\", 有罪判決 \"guilty verdict\", 無罪判決 \"acquittal\". It belongs to news and legal language. For a personal judgment or assessment in everyday life, one does not say 判決 but 判断 or 評価.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"命令に従います。","romaji":"Meirei ni shitagaimasu.","german":"I follow the order.","furigana":"めいれい|したが"},{"japanese":"上司に命令されて、休日も出勤しました。","romaji":"Joushi ni meirei sarete, kyuujitsu mo shukkin shimashita.","german":"I was ordered by my boss and went to work even on my day off.","furigana":"じょうし|めいれい|きゅうじつ|しゅっきん"},{"japanese":"「行け」は「行く」の命令形です。","romaji":"\"Ike\" wa \"iku\" no meireikei desu.","german":"\"ike\" is the imperative form of \"iku\".","furigana":"い|い|めいれいけい"}],"notes":"命令 is an \"order\" from a higher authority: 命令する, 命令を出す, 命令に従う \"to obey\", 命令に背く \"to defy\". It sounds harsh and hierarchical (military, authorities, strict boss); in normal office life people rather say 指示 \"instruction\". In grammar the imperative is called 命令形 (行け, 食べろ), and in computer science 命令 is a program instruction.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"指示に従ってください。","romaji":"Shiji ni shitagatte kudasai.","german":"Please follow the instructions.","furigana":"しじ|したが"},{"japanese":"部長の指示で、資料をもう一度作り直しました。","romaji":"Buchou no shiji de, shiryou o mou ichido tsukurinaoshimashita.","german":"On the department head's instructions, I remade the materials once more.","furigana":"ぶちょう|しじ|しりょう|いちどつく|なお"},{"japanese":"薬は医者の指示どおりに飲んでください。","romaji":"Kusuri wa isha no shiji doori ni nonde kudasai.","german":"Please take the medicine exactly as the doctor instructed.","furigana":"くすり|いしゃ|しじ|の"}],"notes":"指示 is an \"instruction\", the neutral word in the office, at school and at the doctor's. Typical: 指示を出す \"to give instructions\", 指示に従う \"to follow\", 指示を受ける, 〜の指示で \"on the instructions of\", 医者の指示 \"doctor's orders\"; as a する-verb: 部下に〜するように指示する. Compared with 命令, 指示 sounds more matter-of-fact and less authoritative. In exams one often reads 指示に従って答えなさい \"Answer according to the instructions\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"援助を求めます。","romaji":"Enjo o motomemasu.","german":"I ask for help.","furigana":"えんじょ|もと"},{"japanese":"大学のとき、親から経済的な援助を受けていました。","romaji":"Daigaku no toki, oya kara keizaiteki na enjo o ukete imashita.","german":"When I was in college, I received financial support from my parents.","furigana":"だいがく|おや|けいざいてき|えんじょ|う"},{"japanese":"日本は多くの国に食料の援助をしています。","romaji":"Nihon wa ooku no kuni ni shokuryou no enjo o shite imasu.","german":"Japan provides food aid to many countries.","furigana":"にほん|おお|くに|しょくりょう|えんじょ"}],"notes":"援助 is \"help, support\", mostly in material or financial form, often from states, organizations or parents: 経済的な援助, 資金援助, 援助を受ける, 〜に援助する. It sounds more formal than 手伝い (practical help) or 助け (help in general). 支援 is very similar but stresses supporting activities (reconstruction, projects), whereas with 援助 one thinks above all of money or goods.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"意見が対立しています。","romaji":"Iken ga tairitsu shite imasu.","german":"Opinions are divided.","furigana":"いけん|たいりつ"},{"japanese":"新しいルールのことで、親と子どもが対立しています。","romaji":"Atarashii ruuru no koto de, oya to kodomo ga tairitsu shite imasu.","german":"Parents and children are at odds over the new rules.","furigana":"あたら|おや|こ|たいりつ"},{"japanese":"二つの国の対立がますます深まっています。","romaji":"Futatsu no kuni no tairitsu ga masumasu fukamatte imasu.","german":"The confrontation between the two countries is deepening more and more.","furigana":"ふた|くに|たいりつ|ふか"}],"notes":"対立 denotes an opposition or conflict between two sides whose opinions or interests are opposed: 意見が対立する, AとBが対立する, Aと対立する, 対立が深まる \"to intensify\". Attributively: 対立する意見, 対立関係. It describes a situation rather than a single quarrel; for a concrete quarrel one says 喧嘩 or 争い. Typical of politics, news and discussions.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"問題を解決しました。","romaji":"Mondai o kaiketsu shimashita.","german":"I solved the problem.","furigana":"もんだい|かいけつ"},{"japanese":"その事件はまだ解決していません。","romaji":"Sono jiken wa mada kaiketsu shite imasen.","german":"That case has not been solved yet.","furigana":"じけん|かいけつ"},{"japanese":"いい解決方法が見つかりません。","romaji":"Ii kaiketsu houhou ga mitsukarimasen.","german":"I can't find a good solution.","furigana":"かいけつほうほう|み"}],"notes":"解決 is the \"solution, settlement\" of a problem or conflict. As a する-verb it is used both transitively (問題を解決する) and intransitively (問題が解決する). Typical: 解決策 \"solution\", 解決方法, 円満に解決する \"to settle amicably\", 事件が解決する \"a case is solved\". Caution: the solution of a math problem or exam question is called 答え or 解答, not 解決.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"忍耐が必要です。","romaji":"Nintai ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"Patience is needed.","furigana":"にんたい|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"彼女は忍耐強く患者の話を聞きます。","romaji":"Kanojo wa nintaizuyoku kanja no hanashi o kikimasu.","german":"She listens to her patients patiently.","furigana":"かのじょ|にんたいづよ|かんじゃ|はなし|き"},{"japanese":"外国語の勉強で忍耐力がつきました。","romaji":"Gaikokugo no benkyou de nintairyoku ga tsukimashita.","german":"Studying a foreign language has built up my endurance.","furigana":"がいこくご|べんきょう|にんたいりょく"}],"notes":"忍耐 means \"patience, perseverance\" in the sense of enduring hardship or suffering. Typical: 忍耐が必要だ, 忍耐強い \"patient, persevering\", 忍耐力 \"staying power\". It sounds rather written and serious; in everyday speech people more often say 我慢 (to put up with something, hold back) or 辛抱. As a verb (忍耐する) it is rare.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"固い決意を持っています。","romaji":"Katai ketsui o motte imasu.","german":"I have a firm resolution.","furigana":"かた|けつい|も"},{"japanese":"彼は会社を辞めて留学することを決意しました。","romaji":"Kare wa kaisha o yamete ryuugaku suru koto o ketsui shimashita.","german":"He decided to quit the company and study abroad.","furigana":"かれ|かいしゃ|や|りゅうがく|けつい"},{"japanese":"新年の決意をノートに書きました。","romaji":"Shinnen no ketsui o nooto ni kakimashita.","german":"I wrote my New Year's resolutions in a notebook.","furigana":"しんねん|けつい|か"}],"notes":"決意 is a firm \"resolution\", an inner, often emotionally charged determination. Typical: 〜ことを決意する, 決意を固める \"to make up one's mind firmly\", 固い決意, 決意表明 \"statement of intent\". The very similar 決心 is somewhat more everyday; 決意 sounds more solemn and stresses determination. 決定, by contrast, is a matter-of-fact decision, for example by a company or committee.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"信念を貫きます。","romaji":"Shinnen o tsuranukimasu.","german":"I stand by my convictions.","furigana":"しんねん|つらぬ"},{"japanese":"父は「正直に生きる」という信念を持っています。","romaji":"Chichi wa \"shoujiki ni ikiru\" to iu shinnen o motte imasu.","german":"My father holds the conviction that one should live honestly.","furigana":"ちち|しょうじき|い|しんねん|も"},{"japanese":"どんなに反対されても、彼女は信念を曲げませんでした。","romaji":"Donna ni hantai sarete mo, kanojo wa shinnen o magemasen deshita.","german":"No matter how much people opposed her, she did not give up her convictions.","furigana":"はんたい|かのじょ|しんねん|ま"}],"notes":"信念 is a deep personal \"conviction\" that one acts on. Typical: 信念を持つ, 信念を貫く \"to stand by one's convictions\", 信念を曲げる \"to give up one's convictions\", 強い信念. Religious faith, by contrast, is 信仰. Compared with 意見 (\"opinion\"), 信念 is much stronger and more lasting; 自信 means \"self-confidence\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人の前で転んで、恥をかきました。","romaji":"Hito no mae de koronde, haji o kakimashita.","german":"I fell in front of people and was embarrassed.","furigana":"ひと|まえ|ころ|はじ"},{"japanese":"間違えることは恥ではありません。","romaji":"Machigaeru koto wa haji de wa arimasen.","german":"Making mistakes is not a disgrace.","furigana":"まちが|はじ"},{"japanese":"部下の前で恥をかかせないでください。","romaji":"Buka no mae de haji o kakasenaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't embarrass me in front of my subordinates.","furigana":"ぶか|まえ|はじ"}],"notes":"恥 is the noun \"shame, disgrace\". The most important phrase is 恥をかく \"to be embarrassed, lose face\"; to embarrass someone is 恥をかかせる. Others: 〜は恥ではない \"is no disgrace\", 恥を知れ \"Shame on you!\" (very harsh). The related adjective 恥ずかしい \"embarrassing, shy\" is a separate word, as is the literary verb 恥じる \"to feel ashamed\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"羨望の眼差しを向けます。","romaji":"Senbō no manazashi o mukemasu.","german":"He casts envious glances.","furigana":"せんぼう||む"},{"japanese":"彼女の新しい車はみんなの羨望の的です。","romaji":"Kanojo no atarashii kuruma wa minna no senbou no mato desu.","german":"Her new car is the envy of everyone.","furigana":"かのじょ|あたら|くるま|せんぼう|まと"},{"japanese":"留学する友達を、羨望の気持ちで見送りました。","romaji":"Ryuugaku suru tomodachi o, senbou no kimochi de miokurimashita.","german":"I saw off my friend who was going to study abroad with a feeling of envy.","furigana":"りゅうがく|ともだち|せんぼう|きも|みおく"}],"notes":"羨望 is an elevated, written word for \"envy\" in the sense of admiring envy - you wish for what another person has. It is almost always in set phrases: 羨望の眼差し \"envious, admiring looks\", 羨望の的 \"object of general envy\". In everyday life people say うらやましい or うらやむ instead. Jealousy (e.g. in relationships) is called 嫉妬 or やきもち; 羨望 is less negative.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"郷愁を感じます。","romaji":"Kyōshū o kanjimasu.","german":"I feel nostalgia.","furigana":"きょうしゅう|かん"},{"japanese":"古い町並みが郷愁を誘います。","romaji":"Furui machinami ga kyoushuu o sasoimasu.","german":"The old streetscape evokes nostalgia.","furigana":"ふる|まちな|きょうしゅう|さそ"},{"japanese":"外国で故郷の料理を食べたら、郷愁を覚えました。","romaji":"Gaikoku de furusato no ryouri o tabetara, kyoushuu o oboemashita.","german":"When I ate a dish from my hometown abroad, I felt homesick.","furigana":"がいこく|ふるさと|りょうり|た|きょうしゅう|おぼ"}],"notes":"郷愁 is a literary word for \"homesickness, nostalgia\", the wistful longing for one's homeland or for times gone by. Typical: 郷愁を感じる, 郷愁を覚える, 郷愁を誘う \"to evoke nostalgia\" (for example an old street or a song). In everyday speech one rather says ホームシック (homesickness abroad) or 懐かしい \"familiar, nostalgic\". 郷愁 is met mainly in literature, song lyrics and arts-and-culture columns.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大きな野望を抱いています。","romaji":"Ōkina yabō o idaite imasu.","german":"He harbors great ambitions.","furigana":"おお|やぼう|いだ"},{"japanese":"彼の野望は、自分の会社を世界一にすることです。","romaji":"Kare no yabou wa, jibun no kaisha o sekaiichi ni suru koto desu.","german":"His big ambition is to make his own company number one in the world.","furigana":"かれ|やぼう|じぶん|かいしゃ|せかいいち"},{"japanese":"映画の悪役は、世界を自分のものにするという野望を持っていました。","romaji":"Eiga no akuyaku wa, sekai o jibun no mono ni suru to iu yabou o motte imashita.","german":"The villain in the movie had the ambition to make the world his own.","furigana":"えいが|あくやく|せかい|じぶん|やぼう|も"}],"notes":"野望 is a big, often boundless \"ambition\" - an ambitious plan, for example for power or wealth. It often has a slightly negative or dramatic coloring (villains in movies have 野望), but it can also be used with a wink for big dreams. Typical: 野望を抱く \"to harbor\", 野望を実現する, 野望を持つ. Neutral to positive alternatives are 夢 \"dream\", 目標 \"goal\" and 抱負 \"resolution\"; 野心 is very similar but puts more emphasis on the ambitious trait of character.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"動揺しています。","romaji":"Dōyō shite imasu.","german":"He/she is shaken.","furigana":"どうよう"},{"japanese":"突然の知らせに、彼女はかなり動揺していました。","romaji":"Totsuzen no shirase ni, kanojo wa kanari douyou shite imashita.","german":"She was quite upset by the sudden news.","furigana":"とつぜん|し|かのじょ|どうよう"},{"japanese":"面接で難しい質問をされても、動揺を見せないようにしました。","romaji":"Mensetsu de muzukashii shitsumon o sarete mo, douyou o misenai you ni shimashita.","german":"Even when I was asked difficult questions in the interview, I tried not to show any agitation.","furigana":"めんせつ|むずか|しつもん|どうよう|み"}],"notes":"動揺 means to be thrown inwardly off balance: \"shock, loss of composure, agitation\". As an intransitive する-verb: 知らせを聞いて動揺する, also 動揺を隠す \"to not let it show\", 動揺を見せる. It can also refer to unrest in markets or in society. Compared with 驚く (\"to be surprised\"), 動揺 stresses that one loses one's composure; 慌てる describes more frantic, headless action.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"焦りを感じています。","romaji":"Aseri o kanjite imasu.","german":"I can feel myself getting anxious.","furigana":"あせ|かん"},{"japanese":"焦りから、簡単な問題を間違えてしまいました。","romaji":"Aseri kara, kantan na mondai o machigaete shimaimashita.","german":"Out of sheer nervousness, I got an easy question wrong.","furigana":"あせ|かんたん|もんだい|まちが"},{"japanese":"友達がみんな就職して、少し焦りが出てきました。","romaji":"Tomodachi ga minna shuushoku shite, sukoshi aseri ga dete kimashita.","german":"All my friends have found jobs, and I am gradually getting restless.","furigana":"ともだち|しゅうしょく|すこ|あせ|で"}],"notes":"焦り is the noun of 焦る \"to get nervous, to feel under pressure\". It means the inner restlessness when time is short or when one is falling behind others: 焦りを感じる, 焦りが出る, 焦りから失敗する \"to make mistakes out of nervousness\". In everyday speech the verb is often used: 焦らないで \"don't panic, take your time\". Not to be confused with 焦げる \"to burn (food)\", which has the same kanji.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"戸惑いを隠せません。","romaji":"Tomadoi o kakusemasen.","german":"I can't hide my bewilderment.","furigana":"とまど|かく"},{"japanese":"初めて日本に来たとき、生活習慣の違いに戸惑いを感じました。","romaji":"Hajimete Nihon ni kita toki, seikatsu shuukan no chigai ni tomadoi o kanjimashita.","german":"When I first came to Japan, I felt bewildered by the differences in lifestyle customs.","furigana":"はじ|にほん|き|せいかつしゅうかん|ちが|とまど|かん"},{"japanese":"急に予定が変わって、みんなの顔に戸惑いの表情が見えました。","romaji":"Kyuu ni yotei ga kawatte, minna no kao ni tomadoi no hyoujou ga miemashita.","german":"When the plan suddenly changed, everyone looked perplexed.","furigana":"きゅう|よてい|か|かお|とまど|ひょうじょう|み"}],"notes":"戸惑い is the noun of 戸惑う \"to be confused, at a loss\": the feeling of not knowing how to react or behave, for example in a new environment or when something unexpected happens. Typical: 戸惑いを感じる, 戸惑いを隠せない, 戸惑いの表情. The verb is even more common: 習慣の違いに戸惑う. In contrast to 混乱 (\"chaos, disorder\"), 戸惑い is a mild, personal uncertainty; 迷う means more \"to waver, to be unable to decide\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"達観した態度です。","romaji":"Takkan shita taido desu.","german":"He has a composed attitude.","furigana":"たっかん|たいど"},{"japanese":"祖父は人生を達観しているように見えます。","romaji":"Sofu wa jinsei o takkan shite iru you ni miemasu.","german":"My grandfather seems to take a detached view of life.","furigana":"そふ|じんせい|たっかん|み"},{"japanese":"彼はまだ二十歳なのに、ずいぶん達観しています。","romaji":"Kare wa mada hatachi na noni, zuibun takkan shite imasu.","german":"Although he is only twenty, he is remarkably composed about everything.","furigana":"かれ|はたち|たっかん"}],"notes":"達観 means looking at things with distance and composure - \"to stand above things\", also \"broad vision\". Mostly as a する-verb in the form 達観している \"sees everything calmly\" or 人生を達観する. It is often said of older, experienced people, sometimes slightly ironically also of young people (若いのに達観している). It is rather written; more everyday would be 落ち着いている \"calm\", while あきらめている \"resigned\" sounds negative.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"友達を慰めました。","romaji":"Tomodachi o nagusamemashita.","german":"I comforted my friend.","furigana":"ともだち|なぐさ"},{"japanese":"試合に負けて泣いている息子を慰めました。","romaji":"Shiai ni makete naite iru musuko o nagusamemashita.","german":"I comforted my son, who was crying after losing the game.","furigana":"しあい|ま|な|むすこ|なぐさ"},{"japanese":"落ち込んだとき、この歌に何度も慰められました。","romaji":"Ochikonda toki, kono uta ni nando mo nagusameraremashita.","german":"When I was feeling down, this song comforted me many times.","furigana":"お|こ|うた|なんど|なぐさ"}],"notes":"慰める means \"to comfort\": to lift up someone who is sad or disappointed. Construction: 人を慰める, 〜と言って慰める; passive 慰められる \"to be comforted\", also by things (音楽に慰められる). The noun is 慰め \"comfort\" (慰めの言葉). Unlike 励ます \"to encourage\" (looking ahead, giving strength), 慰める is directed at grief already suffered. There is no intransitive counterpart.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼を励ましました。","romaji":"Kare o hagemashimashita.","german":"I encouraged him.","furigana":"かれ|はげ"},{"japanese":"試験の前に、先生が「大丈夫、できるよ」と励ましてくれました。","romaji":"Shiken no mae ni, sensei ga \"Daijoubu, dekiru yo\" to hagemashite kuremashita.","german":"Before the exam, my teacher encouraged me by saying, \"Don't worry, you can do it.\"","furigana":"しけん|まえ|せんせい|だいじょうぶ|はげ"},{"japanese":"病気のとき、友達からの手紙に励まされました。","romaji":"Byouki no toki, tomodachi kara no tegami ni hagemasaremashita.","german":"When I was ill, I was encouraged by letters from my friends.","furigana":"びょうき|ともだち|てがみ|はげ"}],"notes":"励ます means \"to encourage, cheer up\": to give someone courage so that they keep going. Construction: 人を励ます, 〜と言って励ます; passive 励まされる \"to be encouraged\". The noun is 励まし (励ましの言葉). Related is the intransitive 励む \"to strive diligently\" (仕事に励む). Unlike 慰める (comforting after grief), 励ます looks forward; 応援する is \"to cheer on, support\", for example a team.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仲直りしましょう。","romaji":"Nakanaori shimashō.","german":"Let's make up.","furigana":"なかなお"},{"japanese":"昨日けんかした友達と、今日やっと仲直りできました。","romaji":"Kinou kenka shita tomodachi to, kyou yatto nakanaori dekimashita.","german":"Today I was finally able to make up with the friend I had quarreled with yesterday.","furigana":"きのう|ともだち|きょう|なかなお"},{"japanese":"二人はもう仲直りしたみたいですね。","romaji":"Futari wa mou nakanaori shita mitai desu ne.","german":"It looks like the two of them have already made up.","furigana":"ふたり|なかなお"}],"notes":"仲直り means to be reconciled again after a quarrel - literally \"to set the relationship (仲) right again (直る)\". As an intransitive する-verb: 〜と仲直りする, AとBが仲直りする. Typical of friends, couples, siblings and children; the word is colloquial and warm. For formal reconciliation, for example between countries or parties to a lawsuit, one rather says 和解. The opposite is 喧嘩する.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"口喧嘩をしました。","romaji":"Kuchigenka o shimashita.","german":"We had a quarrel.","furigana":"くちげんか"},{"japanese":"小さなことで妻と口げんかになりました。","romaji":"Chiisa na koto de tsuma to kuchigenka ni narimashita.","german":"I ended up in a quarrel with my wife over a trivial matter.","furigana":"ちい|つま|くち"},{"japanese":"兄弟の口喧嘩は毎日のことです。","romaji":"Kyoudai no kuchigenka wa mainichi no koto desu.","german":"Quarrels between siblings are an everyday occurrence.","furigana":"きょうだい|くちげんか|まいにち"}],"notes":"口喧嘩 is a quarrel with words only - as opposed to 喧嘩 in general, which can also be a physical fight. Typical: 口喧嘩をする, 〜と口喧嘩になる \"it comes to a quarrel\", 夫婦の口喧嘩. Because the kanji are difficult, it is often written 口げんか. The word is colloquial; more formal are 口論 or 言い争い.","levelNote":"Compound: 口 (N5) + 喧嘩 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"陰口を言ってはいけません。","romaji":"Kageguchi o itte wa ikemasen.","german":"You should not talk behind people's backs.","furigana":"かげぐち|い"},{"japanese":"同僚が私の陰口をたたいているのを聞いてしまいました。","romaji":"Douryou ga watashi no kageguchi o tataite iru no o kiite shimaimashita.","german":"I happened to overhear a colleague badmouthing me behind my back.","furigana":"どうりょう|わたし|かげぐち|き"},{"japanese":"彼女は人の陰口を絶対に言わないので、みんなに信頼されています。","romaji":"Kanojo wa hito no kageguchi o zettai ni iwanai node, minna ni shinrai sarete imasu.","german":"She never talks behind other people's backs, so everyone trusts her.","furigana":"かのじょ|ひと|かげぐち|ぜったい|い|しんらい"}],"notes":"陰口 is malicious talk about someone behind their back - literally \"shadow mouth\". Typical: 陰口を言う, very common colloquially 陰口をたたく, 〜の陰口, 陰口を言われる. Unlike 悪口 (\"to run someone down\", also directly to their face), 陰口 emphasizes that the person is not present. うわさ is more neutral, \"rumor, gossip\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"世界文化遺産に登録されました。","romaji":"Sekai bunka isan ni tōroku saremashita.","german":"It was registered as a World Cultural Heritage site.","furigana":"せかいぶんかいさん|とうろく"},{"japanese":"京都には多くの文化遺産が残っています。","romaji":"Kyouto ni wa ooku no bunka isan ga nokotte imasu.","german":"Many cultural heritage sites remain in Kyoto.","furigana":"きょうと|おお|ぶんかいさん|のこ"},{"japanese":"地域のお祭りも、次の世代に伝えたい文化遺産です。","romaji":"Chiiki no omatsuri mo, tsugi no sedai ni tsutaetai bunka isan desu.","german":"Local festivals, too, are cultural heritage that we want to pass on to the next generation.","furigana":"ちいき|まつ|つぎ|せだい|つた|ぶんかいさん"}],"notes":"文化遺産 is \"cultural heritage\": buildings, art and traditions handed down from earlier generations. Very common is 世界文化遺産 (UNESCO World Cultural Heritage), often shortened to 世界遺産, with 〜に登録される \"to be registered as\". Natural heritage is 自然遺産. 遺産 alone also means \"inheritance\" in the private sense (親の遺産).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"露天風呂が気持ちいいです。","romaji":"Rotenburo ga kimochi ii desu.","german":"The open-air bath feels great.","furigana":"ろてんぶろ|きも"},{"japanese":"雪を見ながら露天風呂に入りました。","romaji":"Yuki o minagara rotenburo ni hairimashita.","german":"I soaked in the open-air bath while looking at the snow.","furigana":"ゆき|み|ろてんぶろ|はい"},{"japanese":"このホテルには露天風呂付きの部屋があります。","romaji":"Kono hoteru ni wa rotenburo-tsuki no heya ga arimasu.","german":"This hotel has rooms with a private open-air bath.","furigana":"ろてんぶろつ|へや"}],"notes":"露天風呂 is a bath under the open sky, usually at a 温泉 or ryokan: 露天 means \"open-air\", 風呂 \"bath\". One says 露天風呂に入る \"to bathe in the open-air bath\", 露天風呂付きの部屋 \"room with a private open-air bath\", 貸切露天風呂 \"open-air bath that can be reserved privately\". The counterpart is 内風呂 or 内湯 \"indoor bath\". As usual at a 温泉, you wash beforehand and bathe without swimwear.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"土産物屋に行きました。","romaji":"Miyagemonoya ni ikimashita.","german":"I went to a souvenir shop.","furigana":"みやげものや|い"},{"japanese":"駅の土産物売り場で、会社の人にお菓子を買いました。","romaji":"Eki no miyagemono uriba de, kaisha no hito ni okashi o kaimashita.","german":"I bought sweets for my coworkers at the souvenir stand in the station.","furigana":"えき|みやげものう|ば|かいしゃ|ひと|かし|か"},{"japanese":"観光地の土産物は、少し値段が高いです。","romaji":"Kankouchi no miyagemono wa, sukoshi nedan ga takai desu.","german":"Souvenirs at tourist spots are a little expensive.","furigana":"かんこうち|みやげもの|すこ|ねだん|たか"}],"notes":"土産物 are \"souvenirs, gifts brought back\" as merchandise, especially the products sold at tourist destinations. Typical: 土産物屋 \"souvenir shop\", 土産物売り場 \"souvenir stand\", 土産物を買う. In conversation one mostly says (お)土産; 土産物 sounds more matter-of-fact and more like goods in a shop. In Japan, people often bring back edible souvenirs such as お菓子 for coworkers and family from trips.","levelNote":"Compound: 土産 (N3) + 物 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"来月帰国します。","romaji":"Raigetsu kikoku shimasu.","german":"I will return to my home country next month.","furigana":"らいげつきこく"},{"japanese":"夏休みに一時帰国して、家族に会いました。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi ni ichiji kikoku shite, kazoku ni aimashita.","german":"During the summer vacation I made a short trip home and saw my family.","furigana":"なつやす|いちじきこく|かぞく|あ"},{"japanese":"帰国の日が近づいて、少し寂しくなってきました。","romaji":"Kikoku no hi ga chikazuite, sukoshi sabishiku natte kimashita.","german":"The day of my return home is getting closer, and I am feeling a little lonely.","furigana":"きこく|ひ|ちか|すこ|さび"}],"notes":"帰国 means \"to return to one's home country\". As an intransitive する-verb: 帰国する, 〜から帰国する. Typical: 一時帰国 \"short visit home\", 帰国子女 \"children who grew up abroad and have returned\", 帰国の日. For trips home within Japan, one says 帰省 (to one's hometown) or simply 帰る. Leaving the country is 出国.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"渡航の準備をします。","romaji":"Tokō no junbi o shimasu.","german":"I am preparing for my trip overseas.","furigana":"とこう|じゅんび"},{"japanese":"渡航先によっては、ビザが必要です。","romaji":"Tokousaki ni yotte wa, biza ga hitsuyou desu.","german":"Depending on the destination, you need a visa.","furigana":"とこうさき|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"政府はその国への渡航を控えるように呼びかけています。","romaji":"Seifu wa sono kuni e no tokou o hikaeru you ni yobikakete imasu.","german":"The government is urging people to refrain from traveling to that country.","furigana":"せいふ|くに|とこう|ひか|よ"}],"notes":"渡航 originally referred to a journey across the sea; today it is practically \"travel abroad\" in the official sense. It appears mainly in official texts: 渡航する, 海外渡航, 渡航先 \"travel destination abroad\", 渡航者, 渡航費 \"travel costs\". In everyday speech one says 海外旅行 or 海外に行く instead. Returning to one's own country is 帰国.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ここに署名してください。","romaji":"Koko ni shomei shite kudasai.","german":"Please sign here.","furigana":"しょめい"},{"japanese":"契約書に署名する前に、よく読んでください。","romaji":"Keiyakusho ni shomei suru mae ni, yoku yonde kudasai.","german":"Please read the contract carefully before signing.","furigana":"けいやくしょ|しょめい|まえ|よ"},{"japanese":"駅前で、公園を守るための署名を集めていました。","romaji":"Ekimae de, kouen o mamoru tame no shomei o atsumete imashita.","german":"People were collecting signatures in front of the station to protect the park.","furigana":"えきまえ|こうえん|まも|しょめい|あつ"}],"notes":"署名 is a handwritten \"signature\". Typical: 署名する, 書類に署名する, 署名をもらう, 署名欄 \"signature field\", 署名を集める \"to collect signatures\". It is the word for contracts and forms; the everyday サイン is used for parcels, credit cards and autographs of stars. In Japan a seal is often added as well: 署名捺印 \"signature and seal\". Merely writing out the name is 記名.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"捺印をお願いします。","romaji":"Natsuin o onegai shimasu.","german":"Please affix your seal.","furigana":"なついん|ねが"},{"japanese":"書類に署名と捺印をして、送り返してください。","romaji":"Shorui ni shomei to natsuin o shite, okurikaeshite kudasai.","german":"Please sign and seal the document and send it back.","furigana":"しょるい|しょめい|なついん|おく|かえ"},{"japanese":"契約書の二か所に捺印しました。","romaji":"Keiyakusho no nikasho ni natsuin shimashita.","german":"I sealed the contract in two places.","furigana":"けいやくしょ|に|しょ|なついん"}],"notes":"捺印 is the placing of the personal name seal (印鑑, はんこ) on a document. Typical: 捺印する, 捺印をお願いします, 署名捺印 \"signature and seal\", 捺印欄. It is an official word that one reads on forms and in contracts; in conversation one usually says はんこを押す or 印鑑を押す. 押印 means practically the same and is more common in official language today.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"レポートを提出します。","romaji":"Repōto o teishutsu shimasu.","german":"I will hand in the report.","furigana":"ていしゅつ"},{"japanese":"申込書は金曜日までに窓口に提出してください。","romaji":"Moushikomisho wa kin'youbi made ni madoguchi ni teishutsu shite kudasai.","german":"Please submit the application form at the counter by Friday.","furigana":"もうしこみしょ|きんようび|まどぐち|ていしゅつ"},{"japanese":"宿題の提出期限を忘れていました。","romaji":"Shukudai no teishutsu kigen o wasurete imashita.","german":"I had forgotten the submission deadline for the homework.","furigana":"しゅくだい|ていしゅつきげん|わす"}],"notes":"提出 means \"submitting, handing in\" documents: homework, reports, applications, receipts. Transitive する-verb: 〜を〜に提出する (レポートを先生に提出する). Typical: 提出期限 \"submission deadline\", 提出物 \"documents to be submitted\", 書類を提出する. Among students one also hears simply 出す; 提出 sounds more official. 申請, by contrast, is the applying itself (ビザを申請する \"to apply for a visa\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"締切は来週です。","romaji":"Shimekiri wa raishū desu.","german":"The deadline is next week.","furigana":"しめきり|らいしゅう"},{"japanese":"なんとか締切に間に合いました。","romaji":"Nantoka shimekiri ni maniaimashita.","german":"I managed to make the deadline somehow.","furigana":"しめきり|ま|あ"},{"japanese":"申し込みの締切は今日の五時です。","romaji":"Moushikomi no shimekiri wa kyou no goji desu.","german":"The application deadline is five o'clock today.","furigana":"もう|こ|しめきり|きょう|ごじ"}],"notes":"締切 (often also written 締め切り) is the \"deadline\" - the last date for submission, registration or application. Typical: 締切に間に合う \"to meet the deadline\", 締切を過ぎる, 締切を延ばす \"to extend\", 締切日. The related verb is 締め切る \"to close, to cut off\" (受付を締め切る). For submission deadlines one also says 期限 (提出期限).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"会議が延期されました。","romaji":"Kaigi ga enki saremashita.","german":"The meeting has been postponed.","furigana":"かいぎ|えんき"},{"japanese":"雨のため、運動会は来週に延期になりました。","romaji":"Ame no tame, undoukai wa raishuu ni enki ni narimashita.","german":"Because of rain, the sports day was postponed to next week.","furigana":"あめ|うんどうかい|らいしゅう|えんき"},{"japanese":"出張を一週間延期してもらえませんか。","romaji":"Shutchou o isshuukan enki shite moraemasen ka.","german":"Could you postpone the business trip by a week?","furigana":"しゅっちょう|いっしゅうかんえんき"}],"notes":"延期 is the \"postponing\" of an appointment to a later date. As a transitive する-verb: 会議を延期する; for the event itself mostly 延期される or 延期になる. Typical: 〜まで延期する, 雨のため延期, 無期限延期 \"postponed indefinitely\". In contrast to 中止 (\"cancellation\", it does not take place), with 延期 the event is made up for later. Colloquially one also says 延ばす.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"試合が中止になりました。","romaji":"Shiai ga chūshi ni narimashita.","german":"The game was canceled.","furigana":"しあい|ちゅうし"},{"japanese":"台風が来るので、明日の旅行は中止します。","romaji":"Taifuu ga kuru node, ashita no ryokou wa chuushi shimasu.","german":"A typhoon is coming, so we are canceling tomorrow's trip.","furigana":"たいふう|く|あした|りょこう|ちゅうし"},{"japanese":"体に合わないので、その薬を飲むのを中止しました。","romaji":"Karada ni awanai node, sono kusuri o nomu no o chuushi shimashita.","german":"Because it didn't agree with me, I stopped taking that medicine.","furigana":"からだ|あ|くすり|の|ちゅうし"}],"notes":"中止 means \"cancellation\" (a planned event does not take place) or \"discontinuation, halt\" (something ongoing is stopped). Typical: 中止になる \"to be canceled\", 中止する \"to cancel\", 雨天中止 \"canceled in case of rain\". In contrast to 延期 (\"postponement\"), nothing is made up later; 中断 is only a temporary interruption. For private appointments one says キャンセルする or やめる more often.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 45"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"営業を再開しました。","romaji":"Eigyō o saikai shimashita.","german":"We have resumed business.","furigana":"えいぎょう|さいかい"},{"japanese":"事故で止まっていた電車が、一時間後に運転を再開しました。","romaji":"Jiko de tomatte ita densha ga, ichijikan go ni unten o saikai shimashita.","german":"The train that had been stopped by an accident resumed service an hour later.","furigana":"じこ|と|でんしゃ|いちじかんご|うんてん|さいかい"},{"japanese":"雨がやんだので、試合が再開されました。","romaji":"Ame ga yanda node, shiai ga saikai saremashita.","german":"Since the rain stopped, the game was resumed.","furigana":"あめ|しあい|さいかい"}],"notes":"再開 means \"resumption, reopening\" after a pause or interruption. It can be used transitively (営業を再開する) and intransitively (試合が再開する); often also 再開される. Typical: 運転再開 \"resumption of train service\", 営業再開, 試合再開. Note: 再会 \"reunion\" with people (友達と再会する) is read the same way.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「継続は力なり」と言いますから、毎日少しずつ練習しましょう。","romaji":"\"Keizoku wa chikara nari\" to iimasu kara, mainichi sukoshi zutsu renshuu shimashou.","german":"They say \"persistence is power,\" so let's practice a little every day.","furigana":"けいぞく|ちから|い|まいにちすこ|れんしゅう"},{"japanese":"この契約を来年も継続するかどうか、考えています。","romaji":"Kono keiyaku o rainen mo keizoku suru ka dou ka, kangaete imasu.","german":"I am thinking about whether to renew this contract next year as well.","furigana":"けいやく|らいねん|けいぞく|かんが"},{"japanese":"調査はまだ継続中です。","romaji":"Chousa wa mada keizokuchuu desu.","german":"The investigation is still ongoing.","furigana":"ちょうさ|けいぞくちゅう"}],"notes":"継続 means \"continuation, carrying on\" of something that is in progress. Transitive: 契約を継続する \"to renew\", 研究を継続する; intransitive: 〜が継続する. Typical: 継続中 \"ongoing\", 継続的に \"continuously\", 継続して \"continuing to\". Well known is the proverb 継続は力なり \"persistence is power\". Compared with the everyday 続ける/続く, 継続 sounds more formal (contracts, projects, memberships).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"作業が中断されました。","romaji":"Sagyō ga chūdan saremashita.","german":"The work was interrupted.","furigana":"さぎょう|ちゅうだん"},{"japanese":"大雨で試合が三十分ほど中断しました。","romaji":"Ooame de shiai ga sanjuppun hodo chuudan shimashita.","german":"The game was suspended for about thirty minutes because of heavy rain.","furigana":"おおあめ|しあい|さんじゅっぷん|ちゅうだん"},{"japanese":"電話がかかってきたので、食事を中断しました。","romaji":"Denwa ga kakatte kita node, shokuji o chuudan shimashita.","german":"The phone rang, so I interrupted my meal.","furigana":"でんわ|しょくじ|ちゅうだん"}],"notes":"中断 is an \"interruption\": something in progress is stopped temporarily and can continue later. Transitive: 作業を中断する; intransitive: 試合が中断する; also often passive 中断される. Typical: 一時中断, 雨で中断, 話を中断する \"to interrupt the conversation\". Unlike 中止 (definitive cancellation or abort), 中断 implies a continuation; resuming is then 再開.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"工事が完了しました。","romaji":"Kōji ga kanryō shimashita.","german":"The construction work has been completed.","furigana":"こうじ|かんりょう"},{"japanese":"登録が完了すると、メールが届きます。","romaji":"Touroku ga kanryou suru to, meeru ga todokimasu.","german":"When the registration is complete, you will receive an e-mail.","furigana":"とうろく|かんりょう|とど"},{"japanese":"引っ越しの手続きはもう完了しましたか。","romaji":"Hikkoshi no tetsuzuki wa mou kanryou shimashita ka.","german":"Have you already finished the paperwork for the move?","furigana":"ひ|こ|てつづ|かんりょう"}],"notes":"完了 means \"completion, finishing\" - something is fully done. As a する-verb it is intransitive (工事が完了する) and transitive (手続きを完了する). Very common on screens and in system messages: 登録完了 \"registration complete\", 送信完了, 準備完了. In conversation one mostly says 終わる; 完了 sounds matter-of-fact and official. In grammar, 完了 also denotes the perfect.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"進捗を報告します。","romaji":"Shinchoku o hōkoku shimasu.","german":"I will report on the progress.","furigana":"しんちょく|ほうこく"},{"japanese":"毎週金曜日に、プロジェクトの進捗状況を確認します。","romaji":"Maishuu kin'youbi ni, purojekuto no shinchoku joukyou o kakunin shimasu.","german":"Every Friday we check the progress of the project.","furigana":"まいしゅうきんようび|しんちょくじょうきょう|かくにん"},{"japanese":"「進捗はどうですか。」「予定より少し遅れています。」","romaji":"\"Shinchoku wa dou desu ka.\" \"Yotei yori sukoshi okurete imasu.\"","german":"\"How is the progress?\" \"We are a little behind schedule.\"","furigana":"しんちょく|よてい|すこ|おく"}],"notes":"進捗 refers to the \"progress\" of a task or project and belongs mainly to business language. Typical: 進捗状況 \"status of things\", 進捗を報告する, 進捗を確認する, 進捗管理. In the office, colleagues like to ask briefly 進捗どうですか \"How far along are you?\". In contrast to 進歩 (\"progress\" of technology or abilities) and 上達 (\"progress in learning\"), 進捗 is only about how far a task has been completed.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"運行状況を確認します。","romaji":"Unkō jōkyō o kakunin shimasu.","german":"I will check the operating status.","furigana":"うんこうじょうきょう|かくにん"},{"japanese":"このバスは一時間に二本運行しています。","romaji":"Kono basu wa ichijikan ni nihon unkou shite imasu.","german":"This bus runs twice an hour.","furigana":"いちじかん|にほんうんこう"},{"japanese":"大雪のため、電車の運行が止まっています。","romaji":"Ooyuki no tame, densha no unkou ga tomatte imasu.","german":"Because of heavy snow, train service has stopped.","furigana":"おおゆき|でんしゃ|うんこう|と"}],"notes":"運行 is the scheduled \"operation\" of buses, trains or ferries. Typical: 運行する, 運行状況 \"current operating situation\", 運行情報 \"traffic information\", 運行を見合わせる \"to temporarily suspend service\". For trains, announcements often use 運転 too (運転を見合わせる); for ships and aircraft 運航 (same reading) is mostly written. In astronomy, 運行 also refers to the movement of celestial bodies.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"時刻表を見てください。","romaji":"Jikokuhyō o mite kudasai.","german":"Please look at the timetable.","furigana":"じこくひょう|み"},{"japanese":"時刻表によると、次のバスは十時十五分です。","romaji":"Jikokuhyou ni yoru to, tsugi no basu wa juuji juugofun desu.","german":"According to the timetable, the next bus is at 10:15.","furigana":"じこくひょう|つぎ|じゅうじじゅうごふん"},{"japanese":"日本の電車はたいてい時刻表どおりに来ます。","romaji":"Nihon no densha wa taitei jikokuhyou doori ni kimasu.","german":"Japanese trains mostly arrive on time, as per the timetable.","furigana":"にほん|でんしゃ|じこくひょう|き"}],"notes":"時刻表 is the \"timetable\" for trains, buses, ferries or flights, the list of departure and arrival times (時刻 \"time of day, point in time\" + 表 \"table\"). Typical: 時刻表を見る/調べる, 時刻表によると \"according to the timetable\", 時刻表どおり \"on schedule\". A class schedule at school, by contrast, is a 時間割, and a personal agenda is a スケジュール or 予定表.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 15"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"防災訓練をします。","romaji":"Bōsai kunren o shimasu.","german":"We hold a disaster drill.","furigana":"ぼうさいくんれん"},{"japanese":"地震に備えて、防災グッズを用意しました。","romaji":"Jishin ni sonaete, bousai guzzu o youi shimashita.","german":"In case of an earthquake, I have prepared emergency supplies.","furigana":"じしん|そな|ぼうさい|ようい"},{"japanese":"市の防災アプリで、避難場所を確認できます。","romaji":"Shi no bousai apuri de, hinan basho o kakunin dekimasu.","german":"With the city's disaster prevention app, you can check the evacuation sites.","furigana":"し|ぼうさい|ひなんばしょ|かくにん"}],"notes":"防災 means \"disaster prevention, disaster preparedness\" - measures against damage from earthquakes, typhoons, floods, etc. It appears almost always in compounds: 防災訓練 \"disaster drill\", 防災グッズ \"emergency supplies\", 防災の日 (September 1), 防災意識 \"disaster awareness\". Related are 災害 \"disaster\" and 避難 \"evacuation\". Because of frequent earthquakes, the topic is very present in Japan.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"豪雨で川が氾濫しました。","romaji":"Gōu de kawa ga hanran shimashita.","german":"The river overflowed because of the torrential rain.","furigana":"ごうう|かわ|はんらん"},{"japanese":"昨夜の豪雨で、電車が止まってしまいました。","romaji":"Sakuya no gouu de, densha ga tomatte shimaimashita.","german":"Because of the heavy rain last night, the trains stopped.","furigana":"さくや|ごうう|でんしゃ|と"},{"japanese":"天気予報によると、明日は九州で豪雨のおそれがあります。","romaji":"Tenki yohou ni yoru to, ashita wa Kyuushuu de gouu no osore ga arimasu.","german":"According to the weather forecast, there is a risk of torrential rain in Kyushu tomorrow.","furigana":"てんきよほう|あした|きゅうしゅう|ごうう"}],"notes":"豪雨 refers to very heavy rain, often lasting for hours, that can cause damage. The word belongs mainly to weather reports and news; in everyday speech one mostly says simply 大雨. Typical are 豪雨で〜 (\"because of the heavy rain\"), 豪雨による被害 and 豪雨に見舞われる. Fixed compounds: 集中豪雨 \"localized torrential rain\" and ゲリラ豪雨 \"sudden downpour\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"虹が出ています。","romaji":"Niji ga dete imasu.","german":"There is a rainbow out.","furigana":"にじ|で"},{"japanese":"雨が上がって、空に大きな虹がかかりました。","romaji":"Ame ga agatte, sora ni ookina niji ga kakarimashita.","german":"After the rain stopped, a big rainbow stretched across the sky.","furigana":"あめ|あ|そら|おお|にじ"},{"japanese":"日本では、虹は七色だと言われています。","romaji":"Nihon de wa, niji wa nanairo da to iwarete imasu.","german":"In Japan, the rainbow is said to have seven colors.","furigana":"にほん|にじ|なないろ|い"}],"notes":"虹 is the rainbow. One says 虹が出る (\"a rainbow appears\") or, somewhat more pictorially, 虹がかかる (\"a rainbow arches across the sky\"). In Japan traditionally seven colors (七色) are counted, hence also 虹色 \"rainbow-colored, colorful\". The word is almost always written with kanji; in children's books and song lyrics にじ is also used.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日照りが続きます。","romaji":"Hideri ga tsuzukimasu.","german":"The dry spell continues.","furigana":"ひで|つづ"},{"japanese":"日照りで田んぼの水が足りなくなりました。","romaji":"Hideri de tanbo no mizu ga tarinaku narimashita.","german":"Because of the drought, water for the rice paddies ran short.","furigana":"ひで|た|みず|た"},{"japanese":"今年の夏は日照りのせいで、野菜の値段が上がっています。","romaji":"Kotoshi no natsu wa hideri no sei de, yasai no nedan ga agatte imasu.","german":"This summer, vegetable prices have risen because of the drought.","furigana":"ことし|なつ|ひで|やさい|ねだん|あ"}],"notes":"日照り is a longer period without rain, especially in summer, that dries out fields and water supplies. Typical: 日照りが続く, 日照りで〜 (\"because of the drought\"), 日照り続き. In the news, 干ばつ or 水不足 is used more often. Not to be confused with 日照 (にっしょう), which means the duration of sunshine, e.g. 日照時間.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"猛暑が続いています。","romaji":"Mōsho ga tsuzuite imasu.","german":"The extreme heat continues.","furigana":"もうしょ|つづ"},{"japanese":"猛暑のため、エアコンの売り上げが伸びています。","romaji":"Mousho no tame, eakon no uriage ga nobite imasu.","german":"Because of the heat wave, air conditioner sales keep rising.","furigana":"もうしょ|う|あ|の"},{"japanese":"気温が三十五度を超える日を猛暑日と言います。","romaji":"Kion ga sanjuugo do o koeru hi o moushobi to iimasu.","german":"A day with temperatures above 35 degrees is called a 猛暑日.","furigana":"きおん|さんじゅうごど|こ|ひ|もうしょび|い"}],"notes":"猛暑 is extreme, oppressive summer heat, a word from weather reports and the news; in conversation one says more すごく暑い. Typical: 猛暑が続く, 記録的な猛暑, 猛暑のため〜. Meteorologically, a day with at least 35 degrees is called 猛暑日, with at least 30 degrees 真夏日. Synonymous but rarer is 酷暑.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"気温が上がりました。","romaji":"Kion ga agarimashita.","german":"The temperature has risen.","furigana":"きおん|あ"},{"japanese":"今日の最高気温は三十二度だそうです。","romaji":"Kyou no saikou kion wa sanjuuni do da sou desu.","german":"They say today's high will be 32 degrees.","furigana":"きょう|さいこうきおん|さんじゅうにど"},{"japanese":"山の上は気温が低いので、上着を持っていきましょう。","romaji":"Yama no ue wa kion ga hikui node, uwagi o motte ikimashou.","german":"It's cooler up on the mountain, so let's take a jacket.","furigana":"やま|うえ|きおん|ひく|うわぎ|も"}],"notes":"気温 is the air temperature, as given in the weather report. You say 気温が高い/低い and 気温が上がる/下がる; also 最高気温 and 最低気温. Other temperatures use other words: 温度 in general (room, water, oven), 体温 \"body temperature\", 水温 \"water temperature\". Note: not 気温が暑い, but 気温が高い or simply 暑い.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 12"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"林業が重要です。","romaji":"Ringyō ga jūyō desu.","german":"Forestry is important.","furigana":"りんぎょう|じゅうよう"},{"japanese":"この村では昔から林業が盛んです。","romaji":"Kono mura de wa mukashi kara ringyou ga sakan desu.","german":"Forestry has been thriving in this village since long ago.","furigana":"むら|むかし|りんぎょう|さか"},{"japanese":"林業で働く若い人が減っています。","romaji":"Ringyou de hataraku wakai hito ga hette imasu.","german":"The number of young people working in forestry is decreasing.","furigana":"りんぎょう|はたら|わか|ひと|へ"}],"notes":"林業 is forestry, i.e. planting, tending and felling forests for timber. It belongs to the series 農業 \"agriculture\", 漁業 \"fishing\", 林業 and 鉱業 \"mining\". Typical: 林業が盛んだ (\"forestry is significant\"), 林業で働く, formally 林業に従事する. A matter-of-fact word, common in geography and the news.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この町は鉱業で発展しました。","romaji":"Kono machi wa kougyou de hatten shimashita.","german":"This town developed through mining.","furigana":"まち|こうぎょう|はってん"},{"japanese":"オーストラリアは鉱業が盛んな国です。","romaji":"Oosutoraria wa kougyou ga sakan na kuni desu.","german":"Australia is a country with a thriving mining industry.","furigana":"こうぎょう|さか|くに"},{"japanese":"鉱業の仕事は危険が多いと言われています。","romaji":"Kougyou no shigoto wa kiken ga ooi to iwarete imasu.","german":"Work in mining is said to be very dangerous.","furigana":"こうぎょう|しごと|きけん|おお|い"}],"notes":"鉱業 is mining as an industry: the extraction of ores, coal and other mineral resources (鉱物). Caution: it sounds exactly like 工業 \"industry\", so the meaning can only be recognized from context or the script. The mine itself is called 鉱山. Typical: 鉱業が盛んだ, 鉱業で発展する; rather matter-of-fact style (geography, economics).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"工業が発達しています。","romaji":"Kōgyō ga hattatsu shite imasu.","german":"Industry is well developed.","furigana":"こうぎょう|はったつ"},{"japanese":"この辺りは工業地帯なので、工場が多いです。","romaji":"Kono atari wa kougyou chitai na node, koujou ga ooi desu.","german":"This area is an industrial zone, so there are many factories.","furigana":"あた|こうぎょうちたい|こうじょう|おお"},{"japanese":"兄は工業高校で機械について勉強しています。","romaji":"Ani wa kougyou koukou de kikai ni tsuite benkyou shite imasu.","german":"My older brother is studying machinery at a technical high school.","furigana":"あに|こうぎょうこうこう|きかい|べんきょう"}],"notes":"工業 is manufacturing industry, that is, the production of goods in factories. Typical compounds are 工業地帯 \"industrial zone\", 工業高校 \"technical high school\" and 重工業 \"heavy industry\". 産業 is broader and includes all branches of the economy, 商業 is commerce and 農業 is agriculture. A factual word from geography and economics.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今年の収穫は良いです。","romaji":"Kotoshi no shūkaku wa yoi desu.","german":"This year's harvest is good.","furigana":"ことし|しゅうかく|よ"},{"japanese":"秋になると、田んぼで米を収穫します。","romaji":"Aki ni naru to, tanbo de kome o shuukaku shimasu.","german":"In autumn, rice is harvested in the rice paddies.","furigana":"あき|た|こめ|しゅうかく"},{"japanese":"今回の旅行では、いろいろな人と話せたのが一番の収穫でした。","romaji":"Konkai no ryokou de wa, iroiro na hito to hanaseta no ga ichiban no shuukaku deshita.","german":"The biggest gain from this trip was that I could talk with all kinds of people.","furigana":"こんかい|りょこう|ひと|はな|いちばん|しゅうかく"}],"notes":"収穫 is the harvest - both the process and the yield. As a する-verb it is transitive: 米を収穫する, 野菜を収穫する. Figuratively it means the \"gain, yield\" of an experience: 〜が一番の収穫だった. In everyday speech, for fruit and vegetables one also simply says 取る/採る, for rice 稲刈り; 収穫 sounds somewhat more matter-of-fact. Also typical: 収穫期 and 収穫量.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"種をまきます。","romaji":"Tane o makimasu.","german":"I sow seeds.","furigana":"たね"},{"japanese":"このぶどうは種がないので食べやすいです。","romaji":"Kono budou wa tane ga nai node tabeyasui desu.","german":"These grapes are seedless, so they are easy to eat.","furigana":"たね|た"},{"japanese":"息子の成績が、いつも心配の種です。","romaji":"Musuko no seiseki ga, itsumo shinpai no tane desu.","german":"My son's grades are always a cause of worry.","furigana":"むすこ|せいせき|しんぱい|たね"}],"notes":"種 (たね) is the seed of a plant and also the pit in fruit: 種をまく \"to sow\", 種のないぶどう. Figuratively it is the cause or source of something: 心配の種 \"cause for worry\", けんかの種, 話の種 \"topic of conversation\"; 手品の種 is the trick behind a magic act. In compounds the kanji is read しゅ (種類, 品種). まく is mostly written in kana.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"肥料を与えます。","romaji":"Hiryō o ataemasu.","german":"I fertilize the plants.","furigana":"ひりょう|あた"},{"japanese":"春になったら、畑に肥料をまきましょう。","romaji":"Haru ni nattara, hatake ni hiryou o makimashou.","german":"When spring comes, let's spread fertilizer on the field.","furigana":"はる|はたけ|ひりょう"},{"japanese":"この野菜は化学肥料を使わずに育てました。","romaji":"Kono yasai wa kagaku hiryou o tsukawazu ni sodatemashita.","german":"We grew these vegetables without chemical fertilizer.","furigana":"やさい|かがくひりょう|つか|そだ"}],"notes":"肥料 is fertilizer for plants and fields. In everyday speech one says 肥料をやる (garden, potted plants), more formally 肥料を与える, and 肥料をまく when spreading it on a field. Important compounds: 化学肥料 \"chemical fertilizer\" and 有機肥料 \"organic fertilizer\". The older word 肥やし mainly means manure and is also used figuratively (芸の肥やし \"nourishment for one's art\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"灌漑設備を整えます。","romaji":"Kangai setsubi o totonoemasu.","german":"We will set up the irrigation system.","furigana":"かんがいせつび|ととの"},{"japanese":"この地域は雨が少ないので、農業には灌漑が欠かせません。","romaji":"Kono chiiki wa ame ga sukunai node, nougyou ni wa kangai ga kakasemasen.","german":"Since it rains little in this region, irrigation is indispensable for agriculture.","furigana":"ちいき|あめ|すく|のうぎょう|かんがい|か"},{"japanese":"昔の人は川から水を引いて、田んぼを灌漑しました。","romaji":"Mukashi no hito wa kawa kara mizu o hiite, tanbo o kangai shimashita.","german":"In the old days, people drew water from the river to irrigate the rice fields.","furigana":"むかし|ひと|かわ|みず|ひ|た|かんがい"}],"notes":"灌漑 is the artificial watering of fields via canals or pipes. Neither kanji is among the standard kanji (常用漢字), so it is often written in kana as かんがい. Typical: 灌漑設備, 灌漑用水, 灌漑農業; as a する-verb it is transitive (畑を灌漑する), but rather literary. In everyday speech it is paraphrased with 水を引く \"to channel water in\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"牧場で牛を飼っています。","romaji":"Bokujō de ushi o katte imasu.","german":"They keep cows on the ranch.","furigana":"ぼくじょう|うし|か"},{"japanese":"夏休みに北海道の牧場へ行って、アイスクリームを食べました。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi ni Hokkaidou no bokujou e itte, aisukuriimu o tabemashita.","german":"During summer vacation I went to a ranch in Hokkaido and ate ice cream.","furigana":"なつやす|ほっかいどう|ぼくじょう|い|た"},{"japanese":"広い牧場で馬がのんびり草を食べています。","romaji":"Hiroi bokujou de uma ga nonbiri kusa o tabete imasu.","german":"Horses are leisurely grazing in the wide pasture.","furigana":"ひろ|ぼくじょう|うま|くさ|た"}],"notes":"牧場 is an operation or area where livestock such as cows, horses or sheep are kept, that is, a livestock farm or pasture. For crop farming you say 農場 or 畑, not 牧場. Typical: 牧場で牛を飼う, 観光牧場 (a tourist farm with animals, milk and ice cream). The reading まきば is poetic and mainly refers to the green pasture.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 18"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"倉庫に保管します。","romaji":"Sōko ni hokan shimasu.","german":"I store it in the warehouse.","furigana":"そうこ|ほかん"},{"japanese":"使わない机は倉庫にしまっておいてください。","romaji":"Tsukawanai tsukue wa souko ni shimatte oite kudasai.","german":"Please put away the desks we don't use in the storeroom.","furigana":"つか|つくえ|そうこ"},{"japanese":"港の近くには倉庫がたくさん並んでいます。","romaji":"Minato no chikaku ni wa souko ga takusan narande imasu.","german":"Many warehouses stand in a row near the harbor.","furigana":"みなと|ちか|そうこ|なら"}],"notes":"倉庫 is a storage room, warehouse or storage hall, especially of companies, ports and shops. Typical: 倉庫に保管する, 倉庫にしまう, 倉庫から出す. The small storage shed by a house is rather 物置, a traditional storehouse 蔵. Not to be confused with 在庫 \"stock, goods in inventory\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"研究所で実験をします。","romaji":"Kenkyūjo de jikken o shimasu.","german":"I conduct experiments at the research institute.","furigana":"けんきゅうじょ|じっけん"},{"japanese":"兄は大学を出てから、薬の研究所で働いています。","romaji":"Ani wa daigaku o dete kara, kusuri no kenkyuujo de hataraite imasu.","german":"After graduating from university, my older brother has been working at a pharmaceutical research institute.","furigana":"あに|だいがく|で|くすり|けんきゅうじょ|はたら"},{"japanese":"この研究所では、地震について調べています。","romaji":"Kono kenkyuujo de wa, jishin ni tsuite shirabete imasu.","german":"This institute researches earthquakes.","furigana":"けんきゅうじょ|じしん|しら"}],"notes":"研究所 is a research institute or research laboratory as an institution, for example of companies, the state or at universities. There are two readings, けんきゅうじょ and けんきゅうしょ; which applies is decided by each institution itself. The individual lab room or professor's chair at a university is called 研究室 instead. Typical: 研究所で働く, 研究所に勤める, 〜の研究所.","levelNote":"Compound: 研究 (N4) + 所 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大使館に行きます。","romaji":"Taishikan ni ikimasu.","german":"I'm going to the embassy.","furigana":"たいしかん|い"},{"japanese":"大使館の前で待っていてください。","romaji":"Taishikan no mae de matte ite kudasai.","german":"Please wait in front of the embassy.","furigana":"たいしかん|まえ|ま"}],"notes":"大使館 is an embassy as an institution or a building. 大使館に行く names it as the destination of a visit. 大使館の前 describes a place in front of it; 前で待つ names it as a meeting point. たいしかん contains the vowel sequence ai, not a long a. 館 is read かん here.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 25"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"領事館でビザを申請します。","romaji":"Ryōjikan de biza o shinsei shimasu.","german":"I apply for a visa at the consulate.","furigana":"りょうじかん|しんせい"},{"japanese":"パスポートをなくしたので、すぐに領事館に連絡しました。","romaji":"Pasupooto o nakushita node, sugu ni ryoujikan ni renraku shimashita.","german":"I had lost my passport, so I contacted the consulate right away.","furigana":"りょうじかん|れんらく"},{"japanese":"ミュンヘンには日本の総領事館があります。","romaji":"Myunhen ni wa Nihon no souryoujikan ga arimasu.","german":"There is a Japanese consulate general in Munich.","furigana":"にほん|そうりょうじかん"}],"notes":"領事館 is a consulate, the representation of a state in a city other than the capital; one turns to it for visas, passports or emergencies abroad. The embassy in the capital is called 大使館, a consulate general 総領事館, and the consul himself 領事. Typical: 領事館で申請する, 領事館に連絡する.","levelNote":"Compound: 領事 (N2) + 館 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"裁判所に出頭します。","romaji":"Saibansho ni shuttō shimasu.","german":"Ich erscheine vor Gericht.","furigana":"さいばんしょ|しゅっとう"},{"japanese":"裁判所の前に、たくさんの記者が集まっていました。","romaji":"Saibansho no mae ni, takusan no kisha ga atsumatte imashita.","german":"Vor dem Gerichtsgebäude hatten sich viele Reporter versammelt.","furigana":"さいばんしょ|まえ|きしゃ|あつ"},{"japanese":"話し合いで解決できなかったので、裁判所に訴えることにしました。","romaji":"Hanashiai de kaiketsu dekinakatta node, saibansho ni uttaeru koto ni shimashita.","german":"Weil wir es im Gespräch nicht klären konnten, haben wir beschlossen, vor Gericht zu gehen.","furigana":"はな|あ|かいけつ|さいばんしょ|うった"}],"notes":"裁判所 ist das Gericht als Institution und Gebäude – nicht zu verwechseln mit dem deutschen „Gericht“ als Speise. Der Prozess selbst heißt 裁判. Instanzen: 地方裁判所 (kurz 地裁), 高等裁判所, 最高裁判所 (最高裁); Familiensachen verhandelt das 家庭裁判所. Typisch: 裁判所に訴える „vor Gericht gehen“, formell 裁判所に出頭する „vor Gericht erscheinen“.","levelNote":"Compound: 裁判 (N3) + 所 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"刑務所に入りました。","romaji":"Keimusho ni hairimashita.","german":"He went to prison.","furigana":"けいむしょ|はい"},{"japanese":"刑務所から出た後、仕事を見つけるのは簡単ではありません。","romaji":"Keimusho kara deta ato, shigoto o mitsukeru no wa kantan de wa arimasen.","german":"After getting out of prison, it is not easy to find a job.","furigana":"けいむしょ|で|あと|しごと|み|かんたん"},{"japanese":"映画の撮影に、古い刑務所の建物が使われました。","romaji":"Eiga no satsuei ni, furui keimusho no tatemono ga tsukawaremashita.","german":"An old prison building was used for the filming of the movie.","furigana":"えいが|さつえい|ふる|けいむしょ|たてもの|つか"}],"notes":"刑務所 is the prison for convicted offenders. Typical: 刑務所に入る/入れられる, 刑務所に入っている (\"to be in prison\"), 刑務所から出る, formally 出所する. Suspects awaiting trial, by contrast, are held in a 拘置所, and arrested people first in the police cell 留置場. The old word 牢屋 sounds historical or colloquial.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"消防署に通報します。","romaji":"Shōbōsho ni tsūhō shimasu.","german":"I report it to the fire station.","furigana":"しょうぼうしょ|つうほう"},{"japanese":"子どもたちが消防署を見学して、消防車に乗せてもらいました。","romaji":"Kodomotachi ga shoubousho o kengaku shite, shoubousha ni nosete moraimashita.","german":"The children toured the fire station and were allowed to ride in a fire engine.","furigana":"こ|しょうぼうしょ|けんがく|しょうぼうしゃ|の"},{"japanese":"うちの近くに消防署があるので、夜もよくサイレンが聞こえます。","romaji":"Uchi no chikaku ni shoubousho ga aru node, yoru mo yoku sairen ga kikoemasu.","german":"There is a fire station near our house, so we often hear sirens at night too.","furigana":"ちか|しょうぼうしょ|よる|き"}],"notes":"消防署 is the fire station, that is, the office and building of the fire department. The fire service as an organization is called 消防, the firefighter 消防士, the fire engine 消防車. In Japan, ambulances (救急車) also come from the 消防署; the emergency number for both is 119. Typical: 消防署に通報する, 消防署を見学する.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"役所で手続きをします。","romaji":"Yakusho de tetsuzuki o shimasu.","german":"I take care of the procedures at the government office.","furigana":"やくしょ|てつづ"},{"japanese":"役所は土日が休みなので、平日に行かなければなりません。","romaji":"Yakusho wa donichi ga yasumi na node, heijitsu ni ikanakereba narimasen.","german":"The government office is closed on weekends, so I have to go on a weekday.","furigana":"やくしょ|どにち|やす|へいじつ|い"},{"japanese":"あの会社の対応は、まるでお役所仕事だ。","romaji":"Ano kaisha no taiou wa, marude oyakusho shigoto da.","german":"The way that company handles things is pure bureaucratic red tape.","furigana":"かいしゃ|たいおう|やくしょしごと"}],"notes":"役所 is in general a government office or authority, in everyday life usually the city hall, where one handles registrations and applications. More concretely one says 市役所, 区役所, and in small municipalities 町役場/村役場. With お it is often ironic: お役所仕事 \"red tape, bureaucratic sluggishness\". Typical: 役所で手続きをする, 役所に行く, 役所に勤める.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"区役所に届出を出します。","romaji":"Kuyakusho ni todokede o dashimasu.","german":"I submit the notification at the ward office.","furigana":"くやくしょ|とどけで|だ"},{"japanese":"引っ越したら、区役所で住所の変更をしなければなりません。","romaji":"Hikkoshitara, kuyakusho de juusho no henkou o shinakereba narimasen.","german":"After moving, you have to change your address at the ward office.","furigana":"ひ|こ|くやくしょ|じゅうしょ|へんこう"},{"japanese":"区役所は駅から歩いて十分ぐらいです。","romaji":"Kuyakusho wa eki kara aruite juppun gurai desu.","german":"The ward office is about ten minutes' walk from the station.","furigana":"くやくしょ|えき|ある|じゅっぷん"}],"notes":"区役所 is the ward office, the administration of a city ward (区). Wards exist in the 23 wards of Tokyo and in large cities such as Osaka or Yokohama; elsewhere one goes to the 市役所. There one takes care of registering and re-registering residence, obtains the 住民票 \"certificate of residence\" and submits forms. Typical: 区役所に届出を出す, 区役所で手続きをする.","levelNote":"Compound: 区 (N4) + 役所 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"学費が高いです。","romaji":"Gakuhi ga takai desu.","german":"The tuition is high.","furigana":"がくひ|たか"},{"japanese":"アルバイトをして、自分で学費を払っています。","romaji":"Arubaito o shite, jibun de gakuhi o haratte imasu.","german":"I work part-time and pay my tuition myself.","furigana":"じぶん|がくひ|はら"},{"japanese":"私立の学校は公立より学費がかかります。","romaji":"Shiritsu no gakkou wa kouritsu yori gakuhi ga kakarimasu.","german":"Private schools cost more in fees than public ones.","furigana":"しりつ|がっこう|こうりつ|がくひ"}],"notes":"学費 is the total cost of education at a school or university: tuition, school fees, often also materials. The actual teaching fee is, more precisely, 授業料. Typical: 学費が高い, 学費を払う, 学費がかかる, 学費を稼ぐ (\"to earn one's way through school\"); financial support for it is a 奨学金 \"scholarship, student loan\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"学位を取得しました。","romaji":"Gakui o shutoku shimashita.","german":"Ich habe einen akademischen Grad erworben.","furigana":"がくい|しゅとく"},{"japanese":"彼女はドイツの大学で博士の学位を取りました。","romaji":"Kanojo wa Doitsu no daigaku de hakase no gakui o torimashita.","german":"Sie hat an einer deutschen Universität promoviert.","furigana":"かのじょ|だいがく|はかせ|がくい|と"},{"japanese":"この仕事に応募するには、修士以上の学位が必要です。","romaji":"Kono shigoto ni oubo suru ni wa, shuushi ijou no gakui ga hitsuyou desu.","german":"Für diese Stelle braucht man mindestens einen Masterabschluss.","furigana":"しごと|おうぼ|しゅうしいじょう|がくい|ひつよう"}],"notes":"学位 ist ein akademischer Grad: 学士 „Bachelor“, 修士 „Master“, 博士 „Doktor“ (formell はくし, sonst meist はかせ). Typisch: 学位を取る, sachlicher 学位を取得する, passiv 学位を授与される. Nicht verwechseln mit 学歴 „Bildungsweg, Schulabschlüsse“, das den gesamten schulischen Werdegang meint.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"環境汚染が問題です。","romaji":"Kankyō osen ga mondai desu.","german":"Environmental pollution is a problem.","furigana":"かんきょうおせん|もんだい"},{"japanese":"工場から出る煙が、環境汚染の原因になっています。","romaji":"Koujou kara deru kemuri ga, kankyou osen no gen'in ni natte imasu.","german":"The smoke from factories is a cause of environmental pollution.","furigana":"こうじょう|で|けむり|かんきょうおせん|げんいん"},{"japanese":"環境汚染を防ぐために、私たちにできることを考えましょう。","romaji":"Kankyou osen o fusegu tame ni, watashitachi ni dekiru koto o kangaemashou.","german":"Let's think about what we can do to prevent environmental pollution.","furigana":"かんきょうおせん|ふせ|わたし|かんが"}],"notes":"環境汚染 is composed of 環境 \"environment\" and 汚染 \"pollution, contamination\" and is a matter-of-fact word from the news and school textbooks. More concrete are 大気汚染 \"air pollution\", 水質汚染 \"water pollution\" and 土壌汚染 \"soil contamination\". Typical: 環境汚染を防ぐ, 環境汚染の原因, 環境汚染が進む. The older 公害 means environmental damage caused by industry that the population suffers from.","levelNote":"Compound: 環境 (N4) + 汚染 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"再利用できる物を分別します。","romaji":"Sairiyō dekiru mono o bunbetsu shimasu.","german":"I sort out items that can be reused.","furigana":"さいりよう|もの|ぶんべつ"},{"japanese":"このペットボトルは洗って再利用しています。","romaji":"Kono petto botoru wa aratte sairiyou shite imasu.","german":"I wash this plastic bottle and reuse it.","furigana":"あら|さいりよう"},{"japanese":"古い着物の布を再利用して、バッグを作りました。","romaji":"Furui kimono no nuno o sairiyou shite, baggu o tsukurimashita.","german":"I made a bag by reusing the fabric of an old kimono.","furigana":"ふる|きもの|ぬの|さいりよう|つく"}],"notes":"再利用 is using something again instead of throwing it away; as a する-verb it is transitive: 〜を再利用する. It overlaps with リサイクル (more recycling as raw material) and リユース (reuse in the same form); 再利用 covers both. Typical: 再利用できる, 再利用可能 (as a label), 紙を再利用する. It is neutral and common in notes on waste separation.","levelNote":"Compound: 再 (N2) + 利用 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"関税がかかります。","romaji":"Kanzei ga kakarimasu.","german":"Customs duty will be charged.","furigana":"かんぜい"},{"japanese":"政府は輸入される牛肉の関税を引き下げました。","romaji":"Seifu wa yunyuu sareru gyuuniku no kanzei o hikisagemashita.","german":"The government lowered the tariff on imported beef.","furigana":"せいふ|ゆにゅう|ぎゅうにく|かんぜい|ひ|さ"},{"japanese":"関税が上がると、外国の製品の値段も高くなります。","romaji":"Kanzei ga agaru to, gaikoku no seihin no nedan mo takaku narimasu.","german":"When tariffs go up, foreign products also become more expensive.","furigana":"かんぜい|あ|がいこく|せいひん|ねだん|たか"}],"notes":"関税 is customs duty, a levy on imported goods. Note: with the kanji swapped, 税関 means the customs authority or the customs inspection at the airport. Typical: 関税がかかる (\"duty is charged\"), 関税をかける (\"to impose duty\"), 関税を引き上げる/引き下げる. Opposite: 免税 \"duty-free, tax-free\". A matter-of-fact word from economics and the news, but also used when shopping online from abroad.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"保証人が必要です。","romaji":"Hoshōnin ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"A guarantor is required.","furigana":"ほしょうにん|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"このパソコンは一年間の保証が付いています。","romaji":"Kono pasokon wa ichinenkan no hoshou ga tsuite imasu.","german":"This computer comes with a one-year warranty.","furigana":"いちねんかん|ほしょう|つ"},{"japanese":"このやり方で成功するという保証はありません。","romaji":"Kono yarikata de seikou suru to iu hoshou wa arimasen.","german":"There is no guarantee that you will succeed with this method.","furigana":"かた|せいこう|ほしょう"}],"notes":"保証 is a guarantee or surety: the assurance that something will certainly happen, or that someone vouches for something. As a する-verb it is transitive: 品質を保証する. Typical: 保証が付いている, 保証期間, 保証書, 保証人 \"guarantor\" (e.g. for a rental contract), 〜という保証はない. Pronounced the same but different: 保障 \"security, safeguarding\" (社会保障, 安全保障) and 補償 \"compensation\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"育児休暇を取ります。","romaji":"Ikuji kyūka o torimasu.","german":"I take childcare leave.","furigana":"いくじきゅうか|と"},{"japanese":"夫婦で協力して育児をしています。","romaji":"Fuufu de kyouryoku shite ikuji o shite imasu.","german":"We share the childcare as a couple.","furigana":"ふうふ|きょうりょく|いくじ"},{"japanese":"育児と仕事を両立するのは大変です。","romaji":"Ikuji to shigoto o ryouritsu suru no wa taihen desu.","german":"Balancing childcare and work is hard.","furigana":"いくじ|しごと|りょうりつ|たいへん"}],"notes":"育児 is the care and raising of small children, especially in the baby and toddler years. It sounds more formal than the everyday 子育て; 教育, by contrast, means education and schooling. Typical: 育児をする, 育児休暇 (short 育休) \"parental leave\", 育児と仕事の両立 \"balancing childcare and work\". Not to be confused with the identically sounding 意気地 in 意気地なし \"coward\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"共働きの家庭が増えています。","romaji":"Tomobataraki no katei ga fuete imasu.","german":"The number of dual-income households is increasing.","furigana":"ともばたら|かてい|ふ"},{"japanese":"うちは共働きなので、夕飯は夫が作ることも多いです。","romaji":"Uchi wa tomobataraki na node, yuuhan wa otto ga tsukuru koto mo ooi desu.","german":"Since both of us work, my husband often makes dinner.","furigana":"ともばたら|ゆうはん|おっと|つく|おお"},{"japanese":"結婚してからも、共働きを続けるつもりです。","romaji":"Kekkon shite kara mo, tomobataraki o tsuzukeru tsumori desu.","german":"I intend to keep working even after getting married.","furigana":"けっこん|ともばたら|つづ"}],"notes":"共働き means that both spouses are employed. It usually stands with の before nouns (共働きの家庭/夫婦/世帯) or with だ: うちは共働きだ; as a する-verb it is rarer. The counterpart is a household with a housewife or househusband (専業主婦/専業主夫). Neutral, common in conversations about family and childcare, and in statistics.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一人暮らしを始めました。","romaji":"Hitorigurashi o hajimemashita.","german":"I started living alone.","furigana":"ひとりぐ|はじ"},{"japanese":"一人暮らしの学生は、自分で料理や洗濯をしなければなりません。","romaji":"Hitorigurashi no gakusei wa, jibun de ryouri ya sentaku o shinakereba narimasen.","german":"Students who live alone have to cook and do laundry themselves.","furigana":"ひとりぐ|がくせい|じぶん|りょうり|せんたく"},{"japanese":"祖母は田舎で一人暮らしをしているので、時々電話をかけます。","romaji":"Sobo wa inaka de hitorigurashi o shite iru node, tokidoki denwa o kakemasu.","german":"My grandmother lives alone in the countryside, so I call her from time to time.","furigana":"そぼ|いなか|ひとりぐ|ときどきでんわ"}],"notes":"一人暮らし is living alone in one's own apartment, often by students, young professionals or elderly people. Through rendaku, 暮らし becomes ぐらし here. Typical: 一人暮らしをする/始める, 一人暮らしの学生, 一人暮らしのお年寄り; also written 独り暮らし. It is not the same as 独身 \"single (unmarried)\"; the opposite is, for example, 実家暮らし \"living with one's parents\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 22"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"両親と同居しています。","romaji":"Ryōshin to dōkyo shite imasu.","german":"I live with my parents.","furigana":"りょうしん|どうきょ"},{"japanese":"結婚後は、夫の両親と同居することになりました。","romaji":"Kekkongo wa, otto no ryoushin to doukyo suru koto ni narimashita.","german":"After the wedding, it was decided that we would live with my husband's parents.","furigana":"けっこんご|おっと|りょうしん|どうきょ"},{"japanese":"同居している家族は何人ですか。","romaji":"Doukyo shite iru kazoku wa nannin desu ka.","german":"How many family members live with you?","furigana":"どうきょ|かぞく|なんにん"}],"notes":"同居 is living together under one roof, especially within the family, for example several generations. As an intransitive する-verb, the partner takes と: 両親と同居する. The counterpart is 別居, which also means spouses living apart. For unmarried couples one says 同棲, for shared housing ルームシェア. Forms ask about 同居している家族 \"family members living in the household\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"部屋の模様替えをしました。","romaji":"Heya no moyōgae o shimashita.","german":"I rearranged the room.","furigana":"へや|もようが"},{"japanese":"春になったので、カーテンを明るい色に替えて模様替えしました。","romaji":"Haru ni natta node, kaaten o akarui iro ni kaete moyougae shimashita.","german":"Since it is spring, I redecorated by changing the curtains to a bright color.","furigana":"はる|あか|いろ|か|もようが"},{"japanese":"駅前の店が模様替えのため、一週間休むそうです。","romaji":"Ekimae no mise ga moyougae no tame, isshuukan yasumu sou desu.","german":"I hear the shop in front of the station will be closed for a week for a makeover.","furigana":"えきまえ|みせ|もようが|いっしゅうかんやす"}],"notes":"模様替え literally means \"pattern change\": rearranging furniture or redecorating a room, also remodeling a shop. Typical: 部屋の模様替えをする, 模様替えする (transitive: 部屋を模様替えする), 季節の模様替え. Larger construction work is more often called 改装 or リフォーム. It is neutral and everyday.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"築年数は十年です。","romaji":"Chikunensū wa jūnen desu.","german":"The building is ten years old.","furigana":"ちくねんすう|じゅうねん"},{"japanese":"築年数が古い部屋は、家賃が安いことが多いです。","romaji":"Chikunensuu ga furui heya wa, yachin ga yasui koto ga ooi desu.","german":"Rooms in older buildings are often cheaper in rent.","furigana":"ちくねんすう|ふる|へや|やちん|やす|おお"},{"japanese":"部屋を探すときは、駅からの距離と築年数を確認します。","romaji":"Heya o sagasu toki wa, eki kara no kyori to chikunensuu o kakunin shimasu.","german":"When looking for a room, I check the distance from the station and the age of the building.","furigana":"へや|さが|えき|きょり|ちくねんすう|かくにん"}],"notes":"築年数 is the number of years since a building was completed, a typical word from real estate and apartment listings. 築 means \"built\": 築十年 \"built ten years ago\", 新築 \"newly built\", 築浅 \"only a few years old\". Typical: 築年数が古い/新しい, also 築年数が浅い; when looking for an apartment 築年数を確認する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日当たりがいいです。","romaji":"Hiatari ga ii desu.","german":"It gets a lot of sunlight.","furigana":"ひあ"},{"japanese":"日当たりの悪い場所では、この花はうまく育ちません。","romaji":"Hiatari no warui basho de wa, kono hana wa umaku sodachimasen.","german":"This flower does not grow well in places with poor sunlight.","furigana":"ひあ|わる|ばしょ|はな|そだ"},{"japanese":"日当たりのいい窓際で、猫が昼寝をしています。","romaji":"Hiatari no ii madogiwa de, neko ga hirune o shite imasu.","german":"A cat is taking a nap by the sunny window.","furigana":"ひあ|まどぎわ|ねこ|ひるね"}],"notes":"日当たり describes how much direct sun an apartment, a room or a spot gets. Almost always with いい/悪い: 日当たりがいい, 日当たりの悪い部屋; in listings 日当たり良好. Related: 日差し \"sunbeams\", 日なた \"sunny spot\", 日陰 \"shade\". A south-facing aspect (南向き) with good 日当たり is considered especially valuable in Japan.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"騒音がひどいです。","romaji":"Souon ga hidoi desu.","german":"The noise is terrible.","furigana":"そうおん"},{"japanese":"線路の近くに住んでいるので、電車の騒音に悩んでいます。","romaji":"Senro no chikaku ni sunde iru node, densha no souon ni nayande imasu.","german":"I live near the tracks, so I suffer from the noise of the trains.","furigana":"せんろ|ちか|す|でんしゃ|そうおん|なや"},{"japanese":"夜十時以降は、騒音を出さないでください。","romaji":"Yoru juuji ikou wa, souon o dasanaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't make noise after 10 p.m.","furigana":"よるじゅうじいこう|そうおん|だ"}],"notes":"騒音 is disturbing, unpleasant noise, for example from traffic, construction sites or neighbors; the neutral word for a sound is 音. Typical: 騒音がひどい, 騒音に悩む, 騒音を出す, 騒音問題, 騒音対策. 雑音, by contrast, means background noise or static. 騒音がうるさい is redundant; better is 騒音がひどい or simply 音がうるさい.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 37"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"治安がいい地域です。","romaji":"Chian ga ii chiiki desu.","german":"It is an area with good public order.","furigana":"ちあん|ちいき"},{"japanese":"日本は治安がいいので、夜一人で歩いても比較的安全です。","romaji":"Nihon wa chian ga ii node, yoru hitori de aruite mo hikakuteki anzen desu.","german":"Japan is safe, so it is relatively safe to walk alone at night.","furigana":"にほん|ちあん|よるひとり|ある|ひかくてきあんぜん"},{"japanese":"最近、この辺りは治安が悪くなったそうです。","romaji":"Saikin, kono atari wa chian ga waruku natta sou desu.","german":"I hear this area has become less safe recently.","furigana":"さいきん|あた|ちあん|わる"}],"notes":"治安 is public security or the security situation in a country, city or neighborhood. One says 治安がいい/悪い (not 高い/低い), 治安が悪化する, 治安を守る. 安全, by contrast, generally means the safety of a thing or situation, and 防犯 means crime prevention. Common in apartment hunting and travel planning.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"健康的な生活を心掛けています。","romaji":"Kenkouteki na seikatsu o kokorogakete imasu.","german":"I try to live a healthy lifestyle.","furigana":"けんこうてき|せいかつ|こころが"},{"japanese":"毎日、早く寝るように心掛けています。","romaji":"Mainichi, hayaku neru you ni kokorogakete imasu.","german":"I try to go to bed early every day.","furigana":"まいにち|はや|ね|こころが"},{"japanese":"お客様には、いつも笑顔で話すように心掛けてください。","romaji":"Okyakusama ni wa, itsumo egao de hanasu you ni kokorogakete kudasai.","german":"Please always try to speak to customers with a smile.","furigana":"きゃくさま|えがお|はな|こころが"}],"notes":"心掛ける means to firmly resolve something and consciously pay attention to it in daily life. It is transitive: 〜を心掛ける (健康的な生活を心掛ける) or 〜ように心掛ける (早く寝るように心掛ける); there is no 〜に心掛ける; for that one says 健康に気をつける. Often written in kana: 心がける; the noun 心掛け means \"attitude, resolution\". 気をつける stresses caution, 心掛ける conscious habit.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大事な点を見落としました。","romaji":"Daiji na ten o miotoshimashita.","german":"I overlooked an important point.","furigana":"だいじ|てん|みお"},{"japanese":"メールを見落としていて、返事が遅れてしまいました。","romaji":"Meeru o miotoshite ite, henji ga okurete shimaimashita.","german":"I had overlooked the email, so my reply was late.","furigana":"みお|へんじ|おく"},{"japanese":"小さな字で書いてあったので、つい見落としてしまった。","romaji":"Chiisana ji de kaite atta node, tsui miotoshite shimatta.","german":"It was written in small letters, so I simply overlooked it.","furigana":"ちい|じ|か|みお"}],"notes":"見落とす means to fail to notice something through inattention although one could have seen it. It is transitive: 〜を見落とす. The noun is 見落とし (見落としがないか確認する \"to check that nothing was overlooked\"). 見逃す, by contrast, stresses missing a chance or a broadcast, or deliberately letting something pass; 見過ごす rather means \"to let something go by without acting\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"サービスが行き届いています。","romaji":"Sābisu ga ikitodoite imasu.","german":"The service is thoroughly attentive.","furigana":"い|とど"},{"japanese":"このホテルは掃除が行き届いていて、とても気持ちがいいです。","romaji":"Kono hoteru wa souji ga ikitodoite ite, totemo kimochi ga ii desu.","german":"This hotel is cleaned thoroughly, which is very pleasant.","furigana":"そうじ|い|とど|きも"},{"japanese":"社員が多すぎて、社長の目が行き届かないこともあります。","romaji":"Shain ga oosugite, shachou no me ga ikitodokanai koto mo arimasu.","german":"With so many employees, the president's eye cannot always reach everywhere.","furigana":"しゃいん|おお|しゃちょう|め|い|とど"}],"notes":"行き届く means that care or attention reaches into every corner. It is intransitive; the subject is the effort itself: サービスが行き届く, 掃除が行き届く, 目が行き届く, 気配りが行き届く. Mostly in the state form 行き届いている or attributively 行き届いたサービス. Negated, it serves as a polite apology formula: 行き届かない点もございますが. Also read ゆきとどく.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"思い切って行動しました。","romaji":"Omoikitte kōdō shimashita.","german":"I took the plunge and acted.","furigana":"おも|き|こうどう"},{"japanese":"思い切って、彼女に気持ちを伝えました。","romaji":"Omoikitte, kanojo ni kimochi o tsutaemashita.","german":"I summoned my courage and told her my feelings.","furigana":"おも|き|かのじょ|きも|つた"},{"japanese":"子どものころからの夢を、なかなか思い切れません。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro kara no yume o, nakanaka omoikiremasen.","german":"I just can't give up the dream I've had since childhood.","furigana":"こ|ゆめ|おも|き"}],"notes":"思い切る occurs almost always as 思い切って: \"to take heart, to dare the step\" - you overcome your hesitation and do something bold. Attributively: 思い切った決断 \"a bold decision\". The other meaning is transitive: 〜を思い切る \"to give something up, to put something out of one's mind\", often negated (夢を思い切れない). Not to be confused with 思い切り \"with full force, to one's heart's content\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"根回しをしておきます。","romaji":"Nemawashi o shite okimasu.","german":"I will do the groundwork in advance.","furigana":"ねまわ"},{"japanese":"会議の前に、部長に根回しをしておいたほうがいいですよ。","romaji":"Kaigi no mae ni, buchou ni nemawashi o shite oita hou ga ii desu yo.","german":"You should lay the groundwork with the department head before the meeting.","furigana":"かいぎ|まえ|ぶちょう|ねまわ"},{"japanese":"根回しが足りなかったので、新しい計画は反対されてしまいました。","romaji":"Nemawashi ga tarinakatta node, atarashii keikaku wa hantai sarete shimaimashita.","german":"Because there had not been enough groundwork, the new plan was opposed.","furigana":"ねまわ|た|あたら|けいかく|はんたい"}],"notes":"根回し literally means digging around the roots (根) of a tree before transplanting it. Figuratively, it is the informal coordination with everyone involved before a matter is officially discussed or decided, typical of Japanese companies and politics. Typical: 〜に根回しをする, 根回しをしておく, 根回しが足りない. Neutral to businesslike, sometimes with a slightly critical undertone (\"backroom dealing\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"気を遣わないでください。","romaji":"Ki o tsukawanaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't go to any trouble.","furigana":"き|つか"},{"japanese":"上司と食事をすると、気を遣って疲れます。","romaji":"Joushi to shokuji o suru to, ki o tsukatte tsukaremasu.","german":"Eating with my boss makes me tired because I have to be so attentive.","furigana":"じょうし|しょくじ|き|つか|つか"},{"japanese":"彼女はいつも周りの人に気を遣っています。","romaji":"Kanojo wa itsumo mawari no hito ni ki o tsukatte imasu.","german":"She is always considerate of the people around her.","furigana":"かのじょ|まわ|ひと|き|つか"}],"notes":"気を遣う means to pay attention to the feelings and needs of others, to be considerate, to go to trouble. The person is marked with に: 周りの人に気を遣う. 気を遣わないでください is said politely when someone offers a gift or hospitality: \"Please don't go to any trouble.\" Often also written 気を使う; noun: 気遣い. Not to be confused with 気をつける \"to be careful\" and 気にする \"to worry about\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"恩返しをしたいです。","romaji":"Ongaeshi o shitai desu.","german":"I want to repay their kindness.","furigana":"おんがえ"},{"japanese":"育ててくれた両親に恩返しするために、一生懸命働いています。","romaji":"Sodatete kureta ryoushin ni ongaeshi suru tame ni, isshoukenmei hataraite imasu.","german":"I am working hard to repay my parents, who raised me.","furigana":"そだ|りょうしん|おんがえ|いっしょうけんめいはたら"},{"japanese":"「鶴の恩返し」は、助けてもらった鶴がお礼をする昔話です。","romaji":"Tsuru no ongaeshi wa, tasukete moratta tsuru ga orei o suru mukashibanashi desu.","german":"\"Tsuru no Ongaeshi\" is an old folktale in which a crane that was helped shows its gratitude.","furigana":"つる|おんがえ|たす|つる|れい|むかしばなし"}],"notes":"恩返し means to repay a kindness (恩) received, that is, to show gratitude. As an intransitive する-verb the person is marked with に: 両親に恩返しする. Unlike a one-time お礼, 恩返し sounds like a deep, often long-term debt of gratitude. Well known is the folktale 鶴の恩返し; the opposite is described by the saying 恩を仇で返す \"to repay kindness with evil\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"義理チョコを渡します。","romaji":"Giri choko o watashimasu.","german":"I give obligation chocolate.","furigana":"ぎり|わた"},{"japanese":"義理で結婚式に出席しました。","romaji":"Giri de kekkonshiki ni shusseki shimashita.","german":"I attended the wedding out of obligation.","furigana":"ぎり|けっこんしき|しゅっせき"},{"japanese":"彼は私の義理の兄です。","romaji":"Kare wa watashi no giri no ani desu.","german":"He is my brother-in-law.","furigana":"かれ|わたし|ぎり|あに"}],"notes":"義理 is the social obligation one feels out of relationships, politeness or gratitude. Typical: 義理がある, 義理で〜する \"to do out of obligation\", 義理を欠く, 義理堅い \"dutiful\"; 義理チョコ is obligation chocolate for Valentine's Day. With の it denotes in-law relatives: 義理の母 \"mother-in-law\", 義理の兄 \"brother-in-law\". Often paired with 人情 (義理人情).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人情に厚い人です。","romaji":"Ninjō ni atsui hito desu.","german":"He/she is a warmhearted person.","furigana":"にんじょう|あつ|ひと"},{"japanese":"この町には、昔ながらの人情が残っています。","romaji":"Kono machi ni wa, mukashinagara no ninjou ga nokotte imasu.","german":"The warmth of the old days remains in this town.","furigana":"まち|むかし|にんじょう|のこ"},{"japanese":"困っている人を助けたくなるのは人情です。","romaji":"Komatte iru hito o tasuketaku naru no wa ninjou desu.","german":"It is only human to want to help people in trouble.","furigana":"こま|ひと|たす|にんじょう"}],"notes":"人情 is humanity and warmth toward others. Typical: 人情に厚い \"warmhearted\", 人情味がある, 下町の人情 \"the warmth of the old downtown districts\". The second meaning is \"human nature\": 〜のが人情だ \"it is only human to ...\". Together with 義理 it forms the classic pair 義理人情. It is more general than 思いやり, which means concrete consideration.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"思いやりのある人です。","romaji":"Omoiyari no aru hito desu.","german":"He/she is a considerate person.","furigana":"おも|ひと"},{"japanese":"相手の気持ちを考える思いやりが大切です。","romaji":"Aite no kimochi o kangaeru omoiyari ga taisetsu desu.","german":"Consideration, thinking about the other person's feelings, is important.","furigana":"あいて|きも|かんが|おも|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"電車でお年寄りに席を譲るのは、ちょっとした思いやりです。","romaji":"Densha de otoshiyori ni seki o yuzuru no wa, chotto shita omoiyari desu.","german":"Offering your seat to an elderly person on the train is a small act of consideration.","furigana":"でんしゃ|としよ|せき|ゆず|おも"}],"notes":"思いやり is the ability to put oneself in others' shoes and act considerately; it is the noun of 思いやる. Typical: 思いやりがある/ない, 思いやりのある人, 相手への思いやり. The second part is almost always written in kana. 気遣い puts more emphasis on concrete attentiveness in dealing with others, 優しさ on kindness in general.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"やり甲斐のある仕事です。","romaji":"Yarigai no aru shigoto desu.","german":"It is rewarding work.","furigana":"がい|しごと"},{"japanese":"一生懸命勉強した甲斐があって、試験に合格しました。","romaji":"Isshoukenmei benkyou shita kai ga atte, shiken ni goukaku shimashita.","german":"My hard studying paid off - I passed the exam.","furigana":"いっしょうけんめいべんきょう|かい|しけん|ごうかく"},{"japanese":"急いだ甲斐もなく、電車に乗り遅れてしまいました。","romaji":"Isoida kai mo naku, densha ni noriokurete shimaimashita.","german":"Despite hurrying, I missed the train anyway.","furigana":"いそ|かい|でんしゃ|の|おく"}],"notes":"甲斐 is the reward or point of an effort, that is, the result that makes an investment worthwhile. Typical: 〜た甲斐があった \"it was worth it\", 〜甲斐もなく \"in vain despite all the effort\". As a suffix it becomes がい: やり甲斐 \"fulfillment in a task\", 生き甲斐, 〜し甲斐がある. Often written in kana (かい/がい). As a proper name, 甲斐 is also an old province (today Yamanashi).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"生き甲斐を見つけました。","romaji":"Ikigai o mitsukemashita.","german":"I found my reason for living.","furigana":"い|がい|み"},{"japanese":"祖母にとって、孫の成長が生き甲斐です。","romaji":"Sobo ni totte, mago no seichou ga ikigai desu.","german":"For my grandmother, watching her grandchildren grow up is what makes life worth living.","furigana":"そぼ|まご|せいちょう|い|がい"},{"japanese":"定年後に生き甲斐をなくす人も少なくありません。","romaji":"Teinengo ni ikigai o nakusu hito mo sukunaku arimasen.","german":"Not a few people lose their purpose in life after retirement.","furigana":"ていねんご|い|がい|ひと|すく"}],"notes":"生き甲斐 is that for which one lives and which gives life meaning and joy. Typical: 〜が生き甲斐だ, 〜を生き甲斐にする, 生き甲斐を感じる/見つける/なくす. Often also written 生きがい. やり甲斐, by contrast, means the fulfillment in a particular job or task, and 目標 a concrete goal. The word is now also known in the West as a loanword.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"几帳面な人です。","romaji":"Kichōmen na hito desu.","german":"He/she is a methodical person.","furigana":"きちょうめん|ひと"},{"japanese":"父は几帳面で、毎日日記をつけています。","romaji":"Chichi wa kichoumen de, mainichi nikki o tsukete imasu.","german":"My father is very conscientious and keeps a diary every day.","furigana":"ちち|きちょうめん|まいにちにっき"},{"japanese":"彼女の部屋は、いつも几帳面に片付けられています。","romaji":"Kanojo no heya wa, itsumo kichoumen ni katazukerarete imasu.","german":"Her room is always tidied meticulously.","furigana":"かのじょ|へや|きちょうめん|かたづ"}],"notes":"几帳面 is a な-adjective for people who are careful, orderly and precise and reliably keep rules or deadlines. Typical: 几帳面な人/性格, 几帳面に片付ける, 几帳面に記録する. Opposite: 大雑把 or ずぼら. It is meant positively, unlike 神経質 \"overly sensitive, fussy\". The kanji 几 is rare; the word is also seen in kana (きちょうめん).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大雑把な性格です。","romaji":"Ōzappa na seikaku desu.","german":"He/she is a careless sort of person who doesn't bother with details.","furigana":"おおざっぱ|せいかく"},{"japanese":"彼は仕事が大雑把で、よくミスをします。","romaji":"Kare wa shigoto ga oozappa de, yoku misu o shimasu.","german":"He is sloppy in his work and often makes mistakes.","furigana":"かれ|しごと|おおざっぱ"},{"japanese":"大雑把に言うと、計画は三つの段階に分かれています。","romaji":"Oozappa ni iu to, keikaku wa mittsu no dankai ni wakarete imasu.","german":"Roughly speaking, the plan is divided into three stages.","furigana":"おおざっぱ|い|けいかく|みっ|だんかい|わ"}],"notes":"大雑把 is a な-adjective with two sides: \"rough, approximate\" for estimates and explanations (大雑把に言うと \"roughly speaking\", 大雑把な計算) and \"not fussy, sloppy\" for people and work (大雑把な性格). For people it sounds slightly critical, but it is often also used self-ironically. Opposite: 几帳面 or 細かい. Often in kana: おおざっぱ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"手先が器用です。","romaji":"Tesaki ga kiyō desu.","german":"He is good with his hands.","furigana":"てさき|きよう"},{"japanese":"彼女は器用で、自分で服を作ってしまいます。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kiyou de, jibun de fuku o tsukutte shimaimasu.","german":"She is so skillful that she makes her own clothes.","furigana":"かのじょ|きよう|じぶん|ふく|つく"},{"japanese":"兄は何でも器用にできますが、私は不器用です。","romaji":"Ani wa nan demo kiyou ni dekimasu ga, watashi wa bukiyou desu.","german":"My brother is good at doing anything, but I am clumsy.","furigana":"あに|なん|きよう|わたし|ぶきよう"}],"notes":"器用 is a な-adjective: skillful with the hands (手先が器用だ), but also skillful in handling situations (器用に生きる \"to get through life skillfully\"). Adverbially: 器用に使う, 器用にこなす. Opposite: 不器用, which can also mean \"socially awkward\". 器用貧乏 is someone who can do many things a little but nothing properly. For skill in a particular subject, one more often says 上手 or 得意.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不器用ですみません。","romaji":"Bukiyō de sumimasen.","german":"Sorry for being clumsy.","furigana":"ぶきよう"},{"japanese":"私は手先が不器用なので、細かい作業が苦手です。","romaji":"Watashi wa tesaki ga bukiyou na node, komakai sagyou ga nigate desu.","german":"I am clumsy with my hands, so I am bad at fine work.","furigana":"わたし|てさき|ぶきよう|こま|さぎょう|にがて"},{"japanese":"父は不器用だけど、家族のことを大切に思っている。","romaji":"Chichi wa bukiyou dakedo, kazoku no koto o taisetsu ni omotte iru.","german":"My father is awkward, but he cares deeply about his family.","furigana":"ちち|ぶきよう|かぞく|たいせつ|おも"}],"notes":"不器用 is a な-adjective (also a noun): 不器用な人, 手先が不器用だ \"clumsy with the hands\". It describes not only a lack of manual dexterity but also awkwardness in dealing with people or feelings: 生き方が不器用 \"clumsy at life\". The opposite is 器用; colloquially one also hears ぶきっちょ. Unlike 下手 \"bad at a particular thing\", 不器用 describes more a general trait of the person.","levelNote":"Compound: 不 (N3) + 器用 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"臆病な性格です。","romaji":"Okubyō na seikaku desu.","german":"He/she has a timid personality.","furigana":"おくびょう|せいかく"},{"japanese":"うちの猫は臆病で、お客さんが来るとすぐに隠れてしまう。","romaji":"Uchi no neko wa okubyou de, okyakusan ga kuru to sugu ni kakurete shimau.","german":"Our cat is timid and hides immediately when guests come.","furigana":"ねこ|おくびょう|きゃく|く|かく"},{"japanese":"一度失敗してから、新しいことに臆病になった。","romaji":"Ichido shippai shite kara, atarashii koto ni okubyou ni natta.","german":"Since failing once, I have become timid about new things.","furigana":"いちどしっぱい|あたら|おくびょう"}],"notes":"臆病 is a な-adjective: 臆病な人/性格, plus 臆病者 \"coward\". With 〜に臆病になる one expresses that one has become hesitant about something (恋愛に臆病になる). More colloquial and childlike are 怖がり \"scaredy-cat\" and 弱虫; antonyms are 勇敢 and 大胆. Despite the rare kanji 臆, the word is usually written in kanji.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"勇敢な行動です。","romaji":"Yūkan na kōdō desu.","german":"It is a brave act.","furigana":"ゆうかん|こうどう"},{"japanese":"消防士たちは勇敢に火の中へ入っていった。","romaji":"Shouboushitachi wa yuukan ni hi no naka e haitte itta.","german":"The firefighters bravely went into the flames.","furigana":"しょうぼうし|ゆうかん|ひ|なか|はい"},{"japanese":"昔話の主人公は、勇敢な若者であることが多い。","romaji":"Mukashibanashi no shujinkou wa, yuukan na wakamono de aru koto ga ooi.","german":"The heroes of old folktales are often brave young men.","furigana":"むかしばなし|しゅじんこう|ゆうかん|わかもの|おお"}],"notes":"勇敢 is a な-adjective with a praising, somewhat elevated tone: 勇敢な兵士/行動, adverbial 勇敢に戦う. It stresses bravery in the face of real danger and therefore suits news, stories and heroic deeds. In everyday speech one usually says 勇気がある \"to have courage\"; the opposite is 臆病.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"繊細な感性を持っています。","romaji":"Sensai na kansei o motte imasu.","german":"He has a delicate sensibility.","furigana":"せんさい|かんせい|も"},{"japanese":"この料理は、味がとても繊細だ。","romaji":"Kono ryouri wa, aji ga totemo sensai da.","german":"The flavor of this dish is very delicate.","furigana":"りょうり|あじ|せんさい"},{"japanese":"彼女は繊細なので、強い言い方をすると傷ついてしまう。","romaji":"Kanojo wa sensai na node, tsuyoi iikata o suru to kizutsuite shimau.","german":"She is sensitive, so she gets hurt if you speak harshly.","furigana":"かのじょ|せんさい|つよ|い|かた|きず"}],"notes":"繊細 has two sides: \"fine, delicate\" for things (繊細な指, 繊細な味, 繊細な模様) and \"sensitive, delicate-minded\" for people (繊細な人, 繊細な心). For people it is usually meant kindly, but it can also hint at \"easily hurt\"; the loanword デリケート is similar. 敏感, by contrast, means \"reacting sensitively to stimuli\" (肌が敏感).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"軽率な発言でした。","romaji":"Keisotsu na hatsugen deshita.","german":"It was a thoughtless remark.","furigana":"けいそつ|はつげん"},{"japanese":"よく調べずに、軽率に契約してはいけない。","romaji":"Yoku shirabezu ni, keisotsu ni keiyaku shite wa ikenai.","german":"You must not sign a contract rashly without checking carefully.","furigana":"しら|けいそつ|けいやく"},{"japanese":"SNSに軽率に写真を載せるのは危ない。","romaji":"SNS ni keisotsu ni shashin o noseru no wa abunai.","german":"It is dangerous to post photos on social media thoughtlessly.","furigana":"けいそつ|しゃしん|の|あぶ"}],"notes":"軽率 is a な-adjective: 軽率な発言/行動/判断, adverbial 軽率に決める. It criticizes that someone acted without thinking, and often appears in apologies (軽率でした \"that was thoughtless of me\") and in news about politicians' remarks. 不注意 stresses lack of attention, うっかり a one-time slip; 軽率 stresses lack of forethought. Opposite: 慎重.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"正直に言ってください。","romaji":"Shōjiki ni itte kudasai.","german":"Please say it honestly.","furigana":"しょうじき|い"},{"japanese":"彼は正直な人なので、嘘がつけない。","romaji":"Kare wa shoujiki na hito na node, uso ga tsukenai.","german":"He is so honest that he can't tell a lie.","furigana":"かれ|しょうじき|ひと|うそ"},{"japanese":"正直、このプレゼントはあまりうれしくなかった。","romaji":"Shoujiki, kono purezento wa amari ureshiku nakatta.","german":"Honestly, I wasn't very happy with this present.","furigana":"しょうじき"}],"notes":"正直 is a な-adjective: 正直な人, 正直に話す/言う. Very common is the sentence opener 正直に言うと or just 正直、… \"honestly\", which introduces a frank, often uncomfortable opinion; the short 正直、… is colloquial. 素直 means more \"unaffected, obedient, open-minded\", not \"truthful\". Opposite: 不正直 or simply 嘘つき \"liar\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ずるいことをしてはいけません。","romaji":"Zurui koto o shite wa ikemasen.","german":"You must not do anything sly."},{"japanese":"お兄ちゃんだけケーキが大きいなんて、ずるい！","romaji":"Oniichan dake keeki ga ookii nante, zurui!","german":"Only my big brother gets a bigger piece of cake? That's not fair!","furigana":"にい|おお"},{"japanese":"彼はずるくて、面倒な仕事をいつも他の人にやらせる。","romaji":"Kare wa zurukute, mendou na shigoto o itsumo hoka no hito ni yaraseru.","german":"He is sly and always makes other people do the tedious work.","furigana":"かれ|めんどう|しごと|ほか|ひと"}],"notes":"ずるい is an い-adjective and is almost always written in kana; the kanji 狡い is rare. It means \"unfair, cunning, sly\" and is very common in everyday life, especially as an exclamation ずるい！ \"That's not fair!\" when someone gets an undeserved advantage. With the noun ずる one says ずるをする \"to cheat\". 卑怯 is stronger and means \"cowardly and mean\", 不公平 soberly \"unfairly distributed\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"呑気な人です。","romaji":"Nonki na hito desu.","german":"He is a carefree person.","furigana":"のんき|ひと"},{"japanese":"明日は試験なのに、弟はのんきにゲームをしている。","romaji":"Ashita wa shiken na noni, otouto wa nonki ni geemu o shite iru.","german":"Even though the exam is tomorrow, my little brother is calmly playing video games.","furigana":"あした|しけん|おとうと"},{"japanese":"定年後は、田舎でのんきに暮らしたい。","romaji":"Teinen go wa, inaka de nonki ni kurashitai.","german":"After retirement, I want to live a carefree life in the countryside.","furigana":"ていねんご|いなか|く"}],"notes":"呑気 is mostly written in kana のんき. As a な-adjective (のんきな性格, のんきに暮らす) it can be positive, \"relaxed, carefree\", but often it is critical, \"too carefree\" - when someone doesn't worry although they should (この状況でよくそんなにのんきでいられるね). のんびり describes rather a leisurely pace or a relaxed situation, 気楽 the absence of burdens and obligations.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"プールに飛び込みました。","romaji":"Pūru ni tobikomimashita.","german":"I jumped into the pool.","furigana":"と|こ"},{"japanese":"子どもが泣きながら部屋に飛び込んできました。","romaji":"Kodomo ga nakinagara heya ni tobikonde kimashita.","german":"The child came rushing into the room, crying.","furigana":"こ|な|へや|と|こ"},{"japanese":"大学を出てすぐ、ITの世界に飛び込んだ。","romaji":"Daigaku o dete sugu, IT no sekai ni tobikonda.","german":"Right after graduating from university, I plunged into the IT world.","furigana":"だいがく|で|せかい|と|こ"}],"notes":"飛び込む is intransitive; the destination takes に: プールに飛び込む. Besides \"to jump in\" it also means \"to rush in\" (部屋に飛び込んでくる) and figuratively \"to plunge into something\" (新しい世界に飛び込む). The counterpart is 飛び出す (\"to jump out, to rush out\"). The noun 飛び込み denotes diving, but also spontaneous things such as 飛び込み営業 (\"cold calling\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"犬が庭を駆け回っています。","romaji":"Inu ga niwa o kakemawatte imasu.","german":"The dog is running around the garden.","furigana":"いぬ|にわ|か|まわ"},{"japanese":"引っ越しの準備で、一日中あちこち駆け回った。","romaji":"Hikkoshi no junbi de, ichinichijuu achikochi kakemawatta.","german":"Because of the moving preparations, I ran around here and there all day.","furigana":"ひ|こ|じゅんび|いちにちじゅう|か|まわ"},{"japanese":"記者は情報を集めるために町中を駆け回った。","romaji":"Kisha wa jouhou o atsumeru tame ni machijuu o kakemawatta.","german":"The reporter ran all over town to gather information.","furigana":"きしゃ|じょうほう|あつ|まちじゅう|か|まわ"}],"notes":"駆け回る is intransitive, but takes を for the area one runs through: 庭を駆け回る, 町中を駆け回る. Literally it means \"to run around\" (children, dogs), figuratively \"to rush about, to be on the go a lot\" when running errands (資金集めに駆け回る). In everyday speech 走り回る is the more common word for literal running around; 駆け回る sounds livelier and somewhat more literary.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"赤ちゃんが這っています。","romaji":"Akachan ga hatte imasu.","german":"The baby is crawling.","furigana":"あか|は"},{"japanese":"足の上を虫が這っていて、びっくりした。","romaji":"Ashi no ue o mushi ga hatte ite, bikkuri shita.","german":"An insect was crawling over my foot, and I was startled.","furigana":"あし|うえ|むし|は"},{"japanese":"狭いトンネルの中を、這って進んだ。","romaji":"Semai tonneru no naka o, hatte susunda.","german":"I crawled forward through the narrow tunnel.","furigana":"せま|なか|は|すす"}],"notes":"這う is intransitive; the path takes を (床を這う), the destination に or へ. It describes crawling on all fours or along the ground - babies, insects, snakes - and also plants that spread along walls (ツタが壁を這う). For crawling babies, children's talk also uses はいはい. The て-form is 這って (like 買う → 買って); do not confuse it with 入って from 入る.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"信念が揺らいでいます。","romaji":"Shinnen ga yuraide imasu.","german":"My conviction is wavering.","furigana":"しんねん|ゆ"},{"japanese":"先生の一言で、留学する決心が揺らいだ。","romaji":"Sensei no hitokoto de, ryuugaku suru kesshin ga yuraida.","german":"A single remark from my teacher shook my resolve to study abroad.","furigana":"せんせい|ひとこと|りゅうがく|けっしん|ゆ"},{"japanese":"ろうそくの火が風で揺らいでいる。","romaji":"Rousoku no hi ga kaze de yuraide iru.","german":"The candle flame is flickering in the wind.","furigana":"ひ|かぜ|ゆ"}],"notes":"揺らぐ is intransitive; the transitive counterpart is 揺るがす \"to shake\". Physically it means \"to sway, flicker\" (炎が揺らぐ), but far more often it is used figuratively: 決心/自信/信頼が揺らぐ \"to be shaken\". Unlike 揺れる, the everyday word for shaking and swaying (地震で家が揺れる), 揺らぐ sounds more elevated and stresses that something actually firm becomes unstable. Negated: 揺らがない決意 \"unshakable resolve\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"山が紅葉で赤く染まりました。","romaji":"Yama ga kouyou de akaku somarimashita.","german":"The mountains were dyed red by the autumn leaves.","furigana":"やま|こうよう|あか|そ"},{"japanese":"白いシャツがワインで赤く染まってしまった。","romaji":"Shiroi shatsu ga wain de akaku somatte shimatta.","german":"My white shirt got stained red by the wine.","furigana":"しろ|あか|そ"},{"japanese":"彼は東京に来てから、すっかり都会の生活に染まった。","romaji":"Kare wa Toukyou ni kite kara, sukkari tokai no seikatsu ni somatta.","german":"Since coming to Tokyo, he has completely taken on the city lifestyle.","furigana":"かれ|とうきょう|き|とかい|せいかつ|そ"}],"notes":"染まる is intransitive; the transitive counterpart is 染める \"to dye\" (髪を染める). The color and the cause take に or で (夕日に染まる, ワインで染まる), often also adverbial 赤く染まる. Figuratively it means \"to be imbued with, influenced by something\", often negatively: 悪に染まる \"to go astray\", 都会の色に染まる. For mere soiling one rather says 汚れる or シミになる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"雪が溶けて、草花が芽生え始めた。","romaji":"Yuki ga tokete, kusabana ga mebae hajimeta.","german":"The snow has melted, and the plants have started to sprout.","furigana":"ゆき|と|くさばな|めば|はじ"},{"japanese":"一緒に旅行するうちに、二人の間に友情が芽生えた。","romaji":"Issho ni ryokou suru uchi ni, futari no aida ni yuujou ga mebaeta.","german":"As the two traveled together, a friendship budded between them.","furigana":"いっしょ|りょこう|ふたり|あいだ|ゆうじょう|めば"},{"japanese":"試合に負けて、「もっと強くなりたい」という気持ちが芽生えた。","romaji":"Shiai ni makete, \"motto tsuyoku naritai\" to iu kimochi ga mebaeta.","german":"After losing the match, the feeling \"I want to get stronger\" awoke in me.","furigana":"しあい|ま|つよ|きも|めば"}],"notes":"芽生える is intransitive and literally means \"to put out shoots, to sprout\" (芽 \"bud\" + 生える \"to grow\"). That is why 芽が芽生える sounds redundant; for plants one says more simply 芽が出る. Much more common is the figurative use for feelings or attitudes that newly arise: 恋/友情/自覚/責任感が芽生える, often with 〜の間に or 〜の中に. The noun 芽生え \"budding\" is used in the same way.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"草が茂っています。","romaji":"Kusa ga shigette imasu.","german":"The grass is growing thick.","furigana":"くさ|しげ"},{"japanese":"夏になると、庭の木の葉がよく茂る。","romaji":"Natsu ni naru to, niwa no ki no ha ga yoku shigeru.","german":"In summer, the leaves of the trees in the garden grow really dense.","furigana":"なつ|にわ|き|は|しげ"},{"japanese":"山道の両側には木が茂っていて、昼でも暗い。","romaji":"Yamamichi no ryougawa ni wa ki ga shigette ite, hiru demo kurai.","german":"Trees grow thick on both sides of the mountain path, so it is dark even in the daytime.","furigana":"やまみち|りょうがわ|き|しげ|ひる|くら"}],"notes":"茂る is intransitive: 草/木/葉が茂る. It usually stands as a state, 茂っている \"to be densely overgrown\". Intensified, it is 生い茂る \"to grow luxuriantly\"; the noun 茂み means \"thicket, undergrowth\". Unlike 生える \"to grow, to sprout\" (also hair, teeth), 茂る emphasizes the density and abundance of plants and is not used for hair.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"水が濁っています。","romaji":"Mizu ga nigotte imasu.","german":"The water is cloudy.","furigana":"みず|にご"},{"japanese":"たばこの煙で、部屋の空気が濁っていた。","romaji":"Tabako no kemuri de, heya no kuuki ga nigotte ita.","german":"The air in the room was foul from the cigarette smoke.","furigana":"けむり|へや|くうき|にご"},{"japanese":"「ひと」と「ひと」が続くと、「ひとびと」のように音が濁る。","romaji":"\"Hito\" to \"hito\" ga tsuzuku to, \"hitobito\" no you ni oto ga nigoru.","german":"When \"hito\" and \"hito\" follow each other, the sound becomes voiced, as in \"hitobito\".","furigana":"つづ|おと|にご"}],"notes":"濁る is intransitive; the opposite is 澄む. It describes cloudy water or polluted air (川の水が濁る), figuratively also hoarse voices, dull colors or an impure heart. In linguistics, 濁る means \"to become voiced\" - hence 濁音 (が, ざ ...) and 濁点 for the mark ゛. The transitive 濁す is known mainly from 言葉を濁す \"to answer evasively\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"空気が澄んでいます。","romaji":"Kūki ga sunde imasu.","german":"The air is clear.","furigana":"くうき|す"},{"japanese":"この湖の水は、底が見えるほど澄んでいる。","romaji":"Kono mizuumi no mizu wa, soko ga mieru hodo sunde iru.","german":"The water of this lake is so clear that you can see the bottom.","furigana":"みずうみ|みず|そこ|み|す"},{"japanese":"彼女は澄んだ声で歌った。","romaji":"Kanojo wa sunda koe de utatta.","german":"She sang in a clear voice.","furigana":"かのじょ|す|こえ|うた"}],"notes":"澄む is intransitive and usually appears as the state 澄んでいる or attributively 澄んだ: 澄んだ空気/水/目/声. The opposite is 濁る. The transitive counterpart 澄ます is known from 耳を澄ます \"to listen closely\". Beware of the homophones: 住む \"to live\" and 済む \"to be finished\" are also read すむ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"胡麻で和えます。","romaji":"Goma de aemasu.","german":"I dress it with sesame.","furigana":"ごま|あ"},{"japanese":"野菜をドレッシングで和えて、食卓に出す。","romaji":"Yasai o doresshingu de aete, shokutaku ni dasu.","german":"I toss the vegetables with dressing and bring them to the table.","furigana":"やさい|あ|しょくたく|だ"},{"japanese":"ゆでたパスタをソースとよく和えてください。","romaji":"Yudeta pasuta o soosu to yoku aete kudasai.","german":"Please toss the boiled pasta well with the sauce.","furigana":"あ"}],"notes":"和える is transitive: AをBで和える \"to dress A with B\" or AをBと和える \"to toss A with B\". It is a cooking term for mixing ingredients with a sauce, dressing or paste - typical for Japanese side dishes such as ごま和え or 白和え; the related noun is 和え物. Unlike the general 混ぜる \"to mix\", the ingredients stay in pieces and are only coated. Not to be confused with the identically sounding 会える \"to be able to meet\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"生地をこねます。","romaji":"Kiji o konemasu.","german":"I knead the dough.","furigana":"きじ"},{"japanese":"粘土をこねて、小さなお皿を作った。","romaji":"Nendo o konete, chiisana osara o tsukutta.","german":"I kneaded clay and made a small plate.","furigana":"ねんど|ちい|さら|つく"},{"japanese":"娘が「まだ帰りたくない」と駄々をこねている。","romaji":"Musume ga \"mada kaeritakunai\" to dada o konete iru.","german":"My daughter is throwing a tantrum, saying she doesn't want to go home yet.","furigana":"むすめ|かえ|だだ"}],"notes":"こねる is transitive and is mostly written in kana (捏ねる). Literally it means \"to knead\": 生地/粘土をこねる; the milder 混ぜる means \"to mix\". Two set phrases are important: 駄々をこねる \"to throw a tantrum, to be stubborn\" (of children) and 理屈をこねる \"to split hairs, to make up excuses\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"蕎麦をすすります。","romaji":"Soba o susurimasu.","german":"I slurp my soba noodles.","furigana":"そば"},{"japanese":"寒くて、鼻をすすりながら歩いた。","romaji":"Samukute, hana o susurinagara aruita.","german":"It was so cold that I walked along sniffling.","furigana":"さむ|はな|ある"},{"japanese":"祖父は熱いお茶を少しずつすすっている。","romaji":"Sofu wa atsui ocha o sukoshi zutsu susutte iru.","german":"My grandfather is sipping his hot tea in small sips.","furigana":"そふ|あつ|ちゃ|すこ"}],"notes":"すする is transitive and is mostly written in kana (啜る). It means audible sucking in: 麺/そば/ラーメンをすする, お茶/スープをすする. In Japan, slurping noodles is not considered impolite but is normal. With 鼻をすする it means \"to sniffle\". More neutral is 飲む; 吸う means generally \"to suck, to inhale\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お客様をもてなします。","romaji":"Okyakusama o motenashimasu.","german":"I entertain the guests.","furigana":"きゃくさま"},{"japanese":"祖母は手作りの料理で私たちをもてなしてくれた。","romaji":"Sobo wa tezukuri no ryouri de watashitachi o motenashite kureta.","german":"My grandmother treated us to her homemade cooking.","furigana":"そぼ|てづく|りょうり|わたし"},{"japanese":"この旅館では、スタッフが心をこめて客をもてなす。","romaji":"Kono ryokan de wa, sutaffu ga kokoro o komete kyaku o motenasu.","german":"At this inn, the staff welcome guests with all their heart.","furigana":"りょかん|こころ|きゃく"}],"notes":"もてなす is transitive and usually written in kana (持て成す): 客をもてなす, with the means marked by で (料理でもてなす). The noun もてなし, or politely おもてなし, stands for Japanese hospitality and has almost become a slogan. 接待する is the business \"to entertain clients\"; 招く refers only to the inviting itself.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"パーティーを催します。","romaji":"Pātī o moyōshimasu.","german":"I will hold a party.","furigana":"もよお"},{"japanese":"市民ホールで、子ども向けの音楽会が催された。","romaji":"Shimin hooru de, kodomo muke no ongakukai ga moyoosareta.","german":"A concert for children was held at the city hall.","furigana":"しみん|こ|む|おんがくかい|もよお"},{"japanese":"薬を飲んだら、急に眠気を催した。","romaji":"Kusuri o nondara, kyuu ni nemuke o moyooshita.","german":"After taking the medicine, I suddenly felt drowsy.","furigana":"くすり|の|きゅう|ねむけ|もよお"}],"notes":"催す is transitive and elevated or literary. Meaning 1: to host an event (会/宴/展覧会を催す), often in the passive 〜が催される in announcements and news; in everyday speech one rather says 開く, 行う or 開催する. Meaning 2: to feel a physical sensation: 眠気を催す \"to feel drowsy\", 吐き気を催す \"to feel nauseous\". The noun 催し(物) means \"event\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"パーティーが盛り上がりました。","romaji":"Pātī ga moriagarimashita.","german":"The party livened up.","furigana":"も|あ"},{"japanese":"久しぶりに友達と会って、昔の話で盛り上がった。","romaji":"Hisashiburi ni tomodachi to atte, mukashi no hanashi de moriagatta.","german":"I met a friend after a long time, and we got caught up talking about old times.","furigana":"ひさ|ともだち|あ|むかし|はなし|も|あ"},{"japanese":"木の根で、歩道の一部が盛り上がっている。","romaji":"Ki no ne de, hodou no ichibu ga moriagatte iru.","german":"Tree roots have made part of the sidewalk bulge up.","furigana":"き|ね|ほどう|いちぶ|も|あ"}],"notes":"盛り上がる is intransitive; the transitive counterpart is 盛り上げる \"to liven up\" (場を盛り上げる). Most often it describes atmosphere: パーティー/試合/会話が盛り上がる, 〜の話で盛り上がる \"to have a lively conversation about ...\". Literally it means \"to bulge, swell\" (地面/筋肉が盛り上がる). The noun 盛り上がり \"excitement, climax\" is also common.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子猫と戯れています。","romaji":"Koneko to tawamurete imasu.","german":"I am playing with the kitten.","furigana":"こねこ|たわむ"},{"japanese":"子どもたちが浜辺で波と戯れている。","romaji":"Kodomotachi ga hamabe de nami to tawamurete iru.","german":"The children are frolicking with the waves on the beach.","furigana":"こ|はまべ|なみ|たわむ"},{"japanese":"花の周りで、二匹の蝶が戯れるように飛んでいる。","romaji":"Hana no mawari de, nihiki no chou ga tawamureru you ni tonde iru.","german":"Two butterflies are flying around the flowers as if playing.","furigana":"はな|まわ|にひき|ちょう|たわむ|と"}],"notes":"戯れる is intransitive and literary; the partner takes と (子犬と戯れる, 波と戯れる). In everyday speech one says 遊ぶ, or ふざける for fooling around. 戯れる sounds poetic and is gladly used for animals, children or nature scenes; it can also mean \"to flirt, to dally\". The noun 戯れ appears in phrases such as 戯れに \"for fun, on a whim\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"家で寛いでいます。","romaji":"Ie de kutsuroide imasu.","german":"I am relaxing at home.","furigana":"いえ|くつろ"},{"japanese":"どうぞ、ごゆっくりおくつろぎください。","romaji":"Douzo, goyukkuri okutsurogi kudasai.","german":"Please make yourself at home and take your time."},{"japanese":"週末はソファでくつろぎながら映画を見る。","romaji":"Shuumatsu wa sofa de kutsuroginagara eiga o miru.","german":"On weekends, I relax on the sofa and watch movies.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|えいが|み"}],"notes":"くつろぐ is intransitive and mostly written in kana; 寛ぐ is seen more in texts. It means \"to make oneself comfortable, to feel at home\"; the place takes で (家/ソファでくつろぐ). Hosts say the set phrase どうぞおくつろぎください. Compared with リラックスする, くつろぐ emphasizes the cozy, informal atmosphere; 休む means merely \"to rest, to take a break\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"温泉に浸ります。","romaji":"Onsen ni hitarimasu.","german":"I soak in the hot spring.","furigana":"おんせん|ひた"},{"japanese":"卒業アルバムを見ながら、思い出に浸った。","romaji":"Sotsugyou arubamu o minagara, omoide ni hitatta.","german":"Looking through the graduation album, I lost myself in memories.","furigana":"そつぎょう|み|おも|で|ひた"},{"japanese":"試合に勝って、選手たちはしばらく喜びに浸っていた。","romaji":"Shiai ni katte, senshutachi wa shibaraku yorokobi ni hitatte ita.","german":"After winning the game, the players basked in joy for a while.","furigana":"しあい|か|せんしゅ|よろこ|ひた"}],"notes":"浸る is intransitive; what one is immersed in is marked with に. Literally it means \"to be immersed in liquid\" (湯に浸る); for bathing, however, 浸かる (お風呂に浸かる) is more common in everyday speech. Much more common is the figurative sense: 思い出/喜び/感傷/幸せに浸る \"to indulge in something, to give oneself up to a mood\". The transitive counterpart is 浸す \"to immerse, to soak\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仕事に没頭しています。","romaji":"Shigoto ni bottō shite imasu.","german":"I am absorbed in my work.","furigana":"しごと|ぼっとう"},{"japanese":"彼は時間を忘れて読書に没頭した。","romaji":"Kare wa jikan o wasurete dokusho ni bottou shita.","german":"He forgot the time and became absorbed in reading.","furigana":"かれ|じかん|わす|どくしょ|ぼっとう"},{"japanese":"退職してから、父は趣味の写真に没頭している。","romaji":"Taishoku shite kara, chichi wa shumi no shashin ni bottou shite iru.","german":"Since retiring, my father has been devoting himself to his hobby, photography.","furigana":"たいしょく|ちち|しゅみ|しゃしん|ぼっとう"}],"notes":"没頭 is a noun and is mostly used as an intransitive する-verb with に: 仕事/研究/読書に没頭する \"to immerse oneself completely in something\". It stresses that one forgets everything around oneself, and sounds somewhat more elevated. 夢中になる is more everyday and emotional (ゲームに夢中), 熱中する stresses enthusiasm, 集中する concentration for a certain time.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"情熱を持って取り組みます。","romaji":"Jōnetsu o motte torikumimasu.","german":"I tackle it with passion.","furigana":"じょうねつ|も|と|く"},{"japanese":"彼は音楽に情熱を注いでいる。","romaji":"Kare wa ongaku ni jounetsu o sosoide iru.","german":"He pours his passion into music.","furigana":"かれ|おんがく|じょうねつ|そそ"},{"japanese":"年を取って、若い頃の情熱を失ってしまった気がする。","romaji":"Toshi o totte, wakai koro no jounetsu o ushinatte shimatta ki ga suru.","german":"I feel that as I have gotten older, I have lost the passion of my youth.","furigana":"とし|と|わか|ころ|じょうねつ|うしな|き"}],"notes":"情熱 is \"passion, enthusiasm\" for a thing or a person. Typical combinations: 情熱を持つ, 〜に情熱を注ぐ/燃やす, 情熱を失う; the な-adjective 情熱的 means \"passionate\" (情熱的な恋). 熱意 is more sober \"zeal, commitment\", やる気 colloquially \"motivation\". 情熱 sounds more emotional and somewhat more pathetic.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"活力に満ちています。","romaji":"Katsuryoku ni michite imasu.","german":"He is full of vitality.","furigana":"かつりょく|み"},{"japanese":"朝ごはんは一日の活力になります。","romaji":"Asagohan wa ichinichi no katsuryoku ni narimasu.","german":"Breakfast gives you energy for the day.","furigana":"あさ|いちにち|かつりょく"},{"japanese":"若い人が増えて、町に活力が戻ってきた。","romaji":"Wakai hito ga fuete, machi ni katsuryoku ga modotte kita.","german":"As more young people have moved in, vitality has returned to the town.","furigana":"わか|ひと|ふ|まち|かつりょく|もど"}],"notes":"活力 is a somewhat elevated noun for life force and vigor - for people as well as for towns, companies or the economy (経済の活力). Common are 活力に満ちる, 活力を与える, 〜の活力になる and 活力源 \"source of energy\". In everyday speech about people one rather says 元気; エネルギー is the loanword, which is also used in the physical sense.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"忍者の歴史を学びます。","romaji":"Ninja no rekishi o manabimasu.","german":"I study the history of ninja.","furigana":"にんじゃ|れきし|まな"},{"japanese":"子どもの頃、忍者になりたかった。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, ninja ni naritakatta.","german":"When I was a child, I wanted to become a ninja.","furigana":"こ|ころ|にんじゃ"},{"japanese":"弟は忍者のように静かに部屋に入ってきた。","romaji":"Otouto wa ninja no you ni shizuka ni heya ni haitte kita.","german":"My little brother came into the room as quietly as a ninja.","furigana":"おとうと|にんじゃ|しず|へや|はい"}],"notes":"忍者 refers to the spies and covert fighters of feudal Japan; the kanji 忍 means \"to endure, to hide\" (cf. 忍ぶ), and an older term is 忍び. Their techniques are called 忍術, and famous centers were 伊賀 and 甲賀. Today the word is encountered mainly in tourism, manga and comparisons like 忍者のように \"as silent as a ninja\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"侍の精神を学びます。","romaji":"Samurai no seishin o manabimasu.","german":"I study the spirit of the samurai.","furigana":"さむらい|せいしん|まな"},{"japanese":"昔の侍は、いつも刀を二本持っていた。","romaji":"Mukashi no samurai wa, itsumo katana o nihon motte ita.","german":"The samurai of old always carried two swords.","furigana":"むかし|さむらい|かたな|にほんも"},{"japanese":"彼は時代劇で侍の役を演じた。","romaji":"Kare wa jidaigeki de samurai no yaku o enjita.","german":"He played a samurai in a period drama.","furigana":"かれ|じだいげき|さむらい|やく|えん"}],"notes":"侍 refers to the warrior class of old Japan; originally it meant \"someone who serves (a lord)\", from the old verb さぶらう \"to serve, attend\". In historiography, 武士 is the more neutral technical term; 侍 sounds more popular and romantic. In katakana, サムライ often stands for courage and discipline today, for example as the nickname of Japanese national teams. A samurai's sword is called 刀, and his code of honor 武士道.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"武士道の精神です。","romaji":"Bushidō no seishin desu.","german":"It is the spirit of bushido.","furigana":"ぶしどう|せいしん"},{"japanese":"新渡戸稲造は、英語で『武士道』という本を書いた。","romaji":"Nitobe Inazou wa, eigo de \"Bushidou\" to iu hon o kaita.","german":"Inazō Nitobe wrote a book called \"Bushido\" in English.","furigana":"|えいご|ぶしどう|ほん|か"},{"japanese":"武士道では、名誉が何よりも大切だとされた。","romaji":"Bushidou de wa, meiyo ga nani yori mo taisetsu da to sareta.","german":"In bushido, honor was regarded as the most important thing of all.","furigana":"ぶしどう|めいよ|なに|たいせつ"}],"notes":"武士道 is composed of 武士 \"warrior, samurai\" and 道 \"way\" and denotes the samurai code of honor with values such as loyalty, honor, courage and self-control. The term became internationally known mainly through 新渡戸稲造's English book of 1900. As in 柔道 or 茶道, 道 stands for a spiritual path; figuratively, in sports one speaks of 武士道精神 for fairness and self-discipline.","levelNote":"Compound: 武士 (N2) + 道 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"将軍の城を見学しました。","romaji":"Shōgun no shiro o kengaku shimashita.","german":"I toured the shogun's castle.","furigana":"しょうぐん|しろ|けんがく"},{"japanese":"徳川家康は江戸幕府の最初の将軍だ。","romaji":"Tokugawa Ieyasu wa Edo bakufu no saisho no shougun da.","german":"Tokugawa Ieyasu was the first shogun of the Edo shogunate.","furigana":"|えどばくふ|さいしょ|しょうぐん"},{"japanese":"その将軍は多くの戦いに勝った。","romaji":"Sono shougun wa ooku no tatakai ni katta.","german":"That general won many battles.","furigana":"しょうぐん|おお|たたか|か"}],"notes":"将軍 generally means \"general, military commander\", but in the Japanese context it mostly means the shogun - short for 征夷大将軍, the military ruler who actually governed. His government is called 幕府 (江戸幕府), his family 将軍家. For officers of modern armies, rank titles like 大将 are used instead; 将軍 is heard today mainly in history, tourism and literature.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大名の屋敷がありました。","romaji":"Daimyō no yashiki ga arimashita.","german":"There was a daimyo's mansion.","furigana":"だいみょう|やしき"},{"japanese":"江戸時代、大名は一年おきに江戸に住まなければならなかった。","romaji":"Edo jidai, daimyou wa ichinen oki ni Edo ni sumanakereba naranakatta.","german":"In the Edo period, daimyo had to live in Edo every other year.","furigana":"えどじだい|だいみょう|いちねん|えど|す"},{"japanese":"このお祭りでは、大名行列のパレードが行われる。","romaji":"Kono omatsuri de wa, daimyou gyouretsu no pareedo ga okonawareru.","german":"At this festival, a parade is held that reenacts a daimyo procession.","furigana":"まつ|だいみょうぎょうれつ|おこな"}],"notes":"大名 were the feudal lords of Japan who governed their own territory (藩) under the 将軍. Well known are the 大名行列 \"daimyo procession\" and the 参勤交代 system, which forced them to reside in Edo regularly. Figuratively, 大名旅行 means \"luxury trip\", often ironic for expensive business trips. Not to be confused with 有名 \"famous\" or 題名 \"title\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大阪城を見ました。","romaji":"Oosakajou o mimashita.","german":"I saw Osaka Castle.","furigana":"おおさかじょう|み"},{"japanese":"城の一番上から、町全体がよく見える。","romaji":"Shiro no ichiban ue kara, machi zentai ga yoku mieru.","german":"From the top of the castle you can see the whole town well.","furigana":"しろ|いちばんうえ|まちぜんたい|み"},{"japanese":"子どもと砂浜で砂の城を作った。","romaji":"Kodomo to sunahama de suna no shiro o tsukutta.","german":"I built a sandcastle on the beach with my child.","furigana":"こ|すなはま|すな|しろ|つく"}],"notes":"城 alone is read しろ (often politely お城), but in names it is じょう: 大阪城, 姫路城. It refers to Japanese castles with a main keep (天守閣) as well as European castles and palaces; an unfortified palace is called 宮殿 instead. Common combinations are 城を建てる, 城を攻める and 城下町 \"castle town\". Figuratively, 城 can also mean one's own little realm (この部屋は私の城だ).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"甲冑が展示されています。","romaji":"Kacchū ga tenji sarete imasu.","german":"Suits of armor are on display.","furigana":"かっちゅう|てんじ"},{"japanese":"お祭りで、甲冑を着た人たちが町を歩いた。","romaji":"Omatsuri de, kacchuu o kita hitotachi ga machi o aruita.","german":"At the festival, people in armor walked through the town.","furigana":"まつ|かっちゅう|き|ひと|まち|ある"},{"japanese":"本物の甲冑は重くて、一人では着られない。","romaji":"Honmono no kacchuu wa omokute, hitori de wa kirarenai.","german":"A real suit of armor is so heavy that you can't put it on alone.","furigana":"ほんもの|かっちゅう|おも|ひとり|き"}],"notes":"甲冑 consists of 甲 \"armor plate\" and 冑 \"helmet\" and refers to the complete armor including the helmet, especially that of the samurai. The word is rather technical and is encountered in museums, exhibitions and history books; in everyday speech one mostly says 鎧 for the armor and 兜 for the helmet. For putting it on, one says 甲冑を着る or, in elevated style, 甲冑を身に着ける.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"刀を鍛えます。","romaji":"Katana o kitaemasu.","german":"I forge a sword.","furigana":"かたな|きた"},{"japanese":"侍は腰に刀を差していた。","romaji":"Samurai wa koshi ni katana o sashite ita.","german":"The samurai wore a sword at the waist.","furigana":"さむらい|こし|かたな|さ"},{"japanese":"劇の最後に、主人公が静かに刀を抜いた。","romaji":"Geki no saigo ni, shujinkou ga shizuka ni katana o nuita.","german":"At the end of the play, the hero quietly drew his sword.","furigana":"げき|さいご|しゅじんこう|しず|かたな|ぬ"}],"notes":"刀 is the single-edged Japanese sword; 日本刀 emphasizes \"Japanese sword\" as opposed to foreign weapons. 剣 is the more general, often double-edged sword and appears in 剣道. Fixed verbs are 刀を差す \"to wear (in the belt)\", 刀を抜く \"to draw\" and 刀を鍛える \"to forge\"; a swordsmith is a 刀鍛冶. In compounds it is mostly read とう: 日本刀, 木刀 (竹刀 has the special reading しない).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"草履を履いて歩きます。","romaji":"Zōri o haite arukimasu.","german":"I walk in zori sandals.","furigana":"ぞうり|は|ある"},{"japanese":"着物を着るときは、草履を履くのが普通です。","romaji":"Kimono o kiru toki wa, zouri o haku no ga futsuu desu.","german":"When wearing a kimono, it is usual to wear zori.","furigana":"きもの|き|ぞうり|は|ふつう"},{"japanese":"玄関で草履を脱いで、お寺の中に入った。","romaji":"Genkan de zouri o nuide, otera no naka ni haitta.","german":"I took off my zori at the entrance and went into the temple.","furigana":"げんかん|ぞうり|ぬ|てら|なか|はい"}],"notes":"草履 are flat, traditional thong sandals (originally made of straw, hence 草) that are worn with kimono or yukata, usually with 足袋 \"split-toe socks\". The verbs are 草履を履く and 草履を脱ぐ. To be distinguished from this is 下駄, the wooden sandal with ridges under the sole. Simple flip-flops are colloquially also called ゴム草履 or ビーチサンダル.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"暖簾をくぐります。","romaji":"Noren o kugurimasu.","german":"I walk through the noren.","furigana":"のれん"},{"japanese":"店の前にのれんが出ていれば、営業中です。","romaji":"Mise no mae ni noren ga dete ireba, eigyouchuu desu.","german":"If the noren is out in front of the shop, it is open for business.","furigana":"みせ|まえ|で|えいぎょうちゅう"},{"japanese":"何を言っても反応がないことを、「のれんに腕押し」と言う。","romaji":"Nani o itte mo hannou ga nai koto o, \"noren ni udeoshi\" to iu.","german":"When there is no reaction whatever you say, it is called \"pushing against a noren\".","furigana":"なに|い|はんのう|うでお|い"}],"notes":"暖簾 is mostly written in kana のれん and denotes the short, split cloth curtain at the entrance of shops, restaurants and bathhouses. Set expressions are のれんをくぐる \"to enter (an establishment)\" and のれんを出す/しまう \"to open/close\". Figuratively, のれん stands for the good reputation of a business (のれんを守る, のれん分け \"a branch shop under the same name\"). The proverb のれんに腕押し means \"wasted effort, no reaction\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"提灯が飾ってあります。","romaji":"Chōchin ga kazatte arimasu.","german":"Paper lanterns are hung as decoration.","furigana":"ちょうちん|かざ"},{"japanese":"夏祭りの夜、通りに赤い提灯がたくさん並んでいた。","romaji":"Natsumatsuri no yoru, toori ni akai chouchin ga takusan narande ita.","german":"On the night of the summer festival, many red lanterns were lined up along the street.","furigana":"なつまつ|よる|とお|あか|ちょうちん|なら"},{"japanese":"仕事の後、駅前の赤提灯で一杯飲んだ。","romaji":"Shigoto no ato, ekimae no akachouchin de ippai nonda.","german":"After work, I had a drink at a red-lantern pub in front of the station.","furigana":"しごと|あと|えきまえ|あかちょうちん|いっぱいの"}],"notes":"提灯 is the foldable lantern of paper or silk over a bamboo frame; the reading ちょう for 提 is unusual. They are seen at festivals, at temples and in front of restaurants; 赤提灯 stands for a simple, inexpensive pub. Typical verbs are 提灯を飾る/下げる/ともす. Pejoratively, 提灯記事 means a \"puff piece\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"床の間に花を飾ります。","romaji":"Tokonoma ni hana o kazarimasu.","german":"I decorate the alcove with flowers.","furigana":"とこ|ま|はな|かざ"},{"japanese":"旅館の部屋には、立派な床の間があった。","romaji":"Ryokan no heya ni wa, rippa na tokonoma ga atta.","german":"The room at the ryokan had a magnificent alcove.","furigana":"りょかん|へや|りっぱ|とこ|ま"},{"japanese":"床の間の前は、お客さんが座る一番いい席だ。","romaji":"Tokonoma no mae wa, okyakusan ga suwaru ichiban ii seki da.","german":"The seat in front of the alcove is the best seat, where guests sit.","furigana":"とこ|ま|まえ|きゃく|すわ|いちばん|せき"}],"notes":"床の間 is the raised decorative alcove in a Japanese tatami room, in which a hanging scroll (掛け軸) is hung and flowers or ceramics are displayed. 床 is read とこ here, not ゆか \"floor\". The place in front of the alcove is considered the seat of honor (上座) for guests. Typical is 床の間に飾る \"to display in the alcove\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"盆栽を育てています。","romaji":"Bonsai o sodatete imasu.","german":"I am growing bonsai.","furigana":"ぼんさい|そだ"},{"japanese":"祖父は毎朝、盆栽に水をやっている。","romaji":"Sofu wa maiasa, bonsai ni mizu o yatte iru.","german":"My grandfather waters his bonsai every morning.","furigana":"そふ|まいあさ|ぼんさい|みず"},{"japanese":"盆栽は今、海外でも人気がある。","romaji":"Bonsai wa ima, kaigai demo ninki ga aru.","german":"Bonsai are popular overseas now as well.","furigana":"ぼんさい|いま|かいがい|にんき"}],"notes":"盆栽 is composed of 盆 \"tray, shallow dish\" and 栽 \"to plant\" and refers to artfully kept miniature trees in dishes. Typical verbs are 盆栽を育てる/手入れする and 盆栽に水をやる. In Japan it is traditionally regarded as a hobby of older people, but it is also known internationally. An ordinary potted plant is a 鉢植え, a garden plant 植木.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"漆器のお椀です。","romaji":"Shikki no owan desu.","german":"This is a lacquered bowl.","furigana":"しっき|わん"},{"japanese":"輪島は漆器で有名な町です。","romaji":"Wajima wa shikki de yuumei na machi desu.","german":"Wajima is a town famous for its lacquerware.","furigana":"わじま|しっき|ゆうめい|まち"},{"japanese":"漆器は電子レンジで使わないでください。","romaji":"Shikki wa denshi renji de tsukawanaide kudasai.","german":"Please do not use lacquerware in the microwave.","furigana":"しっき|でんし|つか"}],"notes":"漆器 is tableware and craft made with lacquer from the sap of the lacquer tree (漆); in everyday speech also called 塗り物. Typical pieces are お椀, お盆 and 重箱, well-known production places are 輪島 and 会津. Unlike 陶器 \"earthenware\" and 磁器 \"porcelain\", 漆器 usually consists of lacquered wood - which is why it does not belong in the microwave or dishwasher.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この町は焼き物で有名です。","romaji":"Kono machi wa yakimono de yūmei desu.","german":"This town is famous for its pottery.","furigana":"まち|や|もの|ゆうめい"},{"japanese":"旅行のお土産に、焼き物のお皿を買いました。","romaji":"Ryokou no omiyage ni, yakimono no osara o kaimashita.","german":"As a travel souvenir, I bought a ceramic plate.","furigana":"りょこう|みやげ|や|もの|さら|か"},{"japanese":"和食のコースでは、刺身のあとに焼き物が出てきます。","romaji":"Washoku no koosu de wa, sashimi no ato ni yakimono ga dete kimasu.","german":"In a Japanese course meal, a grilled dish comes after the sashimi.","furigana":"わしょく|さしみ|や|もの|で"}],"notes":"焼き物 is above all \"ceramics, pottery\" as the umbrella term for 陶器 (earthenware) and 磁器 (porcelain). Well-known regional styles carry the suffix 〜焼, such as 有田焼 or 信楽焼. In Japanese cuisine, especially in a 懐石 menu, 焼き物 also denotes the course of grilled food, usually fish. On menus the spelling 焼物 is also seen.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"手拭いで汗を拭きます。","romaji":"Tenugui de ase o fukimasu.","german":"I wipe the sweat with a tenugui.","furigana":"てぬぐ|あせ|ふ"},{"japanese":"お祭りでは、頭に手拭いを巻いている人が多い。","romaji":"Omatsuri de wa, atama ni tenugui o maite iru hito ga ooi.","german":"At festivals, many people have a tenugui tied around their heads.","furigana":"まつ|あたま|てぬぐ|ま|ひと|おお"},{"japanese":"京都のお土産に、かわいい柄の手拭いを買った。","romaji":"Kyouto no omiyage ni, kawaii gara no tenugui o katta.","german":"As a souvenir from Kyoto, I bought a tenugui with a pretty pattern.","furigana":"きょうと|みやげ|がら|てぬぐ|か"}],"notes":"手拭い (often てぬぐい) is a thin, printed cotton cloth about 90 cm long that is used as a hand towel, headscarf, sweatband or wrapping. Unlike the fluffy タオル, it dries quickly and has open, unhemmed edges. Typical are 手拭いで拭く and 頭に手拭いを巻く. Today it is also popular as a fashionable souvenir.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"風呂敷で包みます。","romaji":"Furoshiki de tsutsumimasu.","german":"I wrap it in a furoshiki.","furigana":"ふろしき|つつ"},{"japanese":"最近は、エコバッグの代わりに風呂敷を使う人もいる。","romaji":"Saikin wa, eko baggu no kawari ni furoshiki o tsukau hito mo iru.","german":"Recently, some people use a furoshiki instead of a shopping bag.","furigana":"さいきん|か|ふろしき|つか|ひと"},{"japanese":"彼はすぐ大風呂敷を広げるので、話を半分に聞いている。","romaji":"Kare wa sugu oofuroshiki o hirogeru node, hanashi o hanbun ni kiite iru.","german":"He is always boasting, so I only believe half of what he says.","furigana":"かれ|おおふろしき|ひろ|はなし|はんぶん|き"}],"notes":"風呂敷 is a square cloth for wrapping and carrying things; the name comes from the fact that it was once spread out (敷く) in the bathhouse (風呂). Typical are 風呂敷で包む and 風呂敷を広げる. Today it is experiencing a comeback as an environmentally friendly alternative to plastic bags. The idiom 大風呂敷を広げる means \"to talk big, to boast\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"算盤を使って計算します。","romaji":"Soroban o tsukatte keisan shimasu.","german":"I calculate with an abacus.","furigana":"そろばん|つか|けいさん"},{"japanese":"子どもの頃、そろばん教室に通っていた。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, soroban kyoushitsu ni kayotte ita.","german":"As a child, I went to a soroban class.","furigana":"こ|ころ|きょうしつ|かよ"},{"japanese":"彼は何をするにも、まずそろばんをはじく。","romaji":"Kare wa nani o suru ni mo, mazu soroban o hajiku.","german":"Whatever he does, he first calculates what's in it for him.","furigana":"かれ|なに"}],"notes":"算盤 is mostly written in kana そろばん and denotes the Japanese abacus. Calculating with it is called そろばんをはじく; そろばん教室 are still popular classes for children today. Figuratively, そろばん stands for cost-benefit calculation: そろばんをはじく \"to calculate one's advantage\", そろばんが合わない \"to not add up, not to be worth it\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"正座がつらいです。","romaji":"Seiza ga tsurai desu.","german":"Sitting in seiza is painful.","furigana":"せいざ"},{"japanese":"長い間正座していたので、足がしびれてしまった。","romaji":"Nagai aida seiza shite ita node, ashi ga shibirete shimatta.","german":"I sat in seiza for so long that my legs fell asleep.","furigana":"なが|あいだせいざ|あし"},{"japanese":"茶道では、正座してお茶をいただく。","romaji":"Sadou de wa, seiza shite ocha o itadaku.","german":"In the tea ceremony, you drink the tea sitting in seiza.","furigana":"さどう|せいざ|ちゃ"}],"notes":"正座 is the formal kneeling posture on tatami (knees together, buttocks on the heels) and is used as an intransitive する-verb: 正座する. It is regarded as a polite posture at ceremonies, in martial arts or on visits; if you sit like this for a long time, your legs fall asleep (足がしびれる). Hosts often say どうぞ足を崩してください \"Please sit comfortably.\" Casual counterparts are あぐら \"cross-legged sitting\" and 横座り; not to be confused with 星座 \"constellation\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"食事の作法を学びます。","romaji":"Shokuji no sahō o manabimasu.","german":"I am learning table manners.","furigana":"しょくじ|さほう|まな"},{"japanese":"神社でお参りするときの作法を教えてもらった。","romaji":"Jinja de omairi suru toki no sahou o oshiete moratta.","german":"I was taught the proper etiquette for praying at a shrine.","furigana":"じんじゃ|まい|さほう|おし"},{"japanese":"食べながら話すのは無作法だと母に言われた。","romaji":"Tabenagara hanasu no wa busahou da to haha ni iwareta.","german":"My mother told me it is bad manners to talk while eating.","furigana":"た|はな|ぶさほう|はは|い"}],"notes":"作法 refers to fixed, often traditional rules for correct behavior in a situation: 食事の作法, 茶道の作法, 礼儀作法 \"rules of etiquette\". Typical are 作法を守る/学ぶ and 作法に従う; the opposite 無作法 means \"rude, impolite\". In everyday speech one often simply says マナー (電車のマナー); 作法 sounds more traditional and emphasizes the correct sequence of actions. The reading is さほう, not さくほう.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お歳暮を贈ります。","romaji":"Oseibo o okurimasu.","german":"I send a year-end gift.","furigana":"せいぼ|おく"},{"japanese":"毎年、お世話になった上司にお歳暮を贈っている。","romaji":"Maitoshi, osewa ni natta joushi ni oseibo o okutte iru.","german":"Every year I send a year-end gift to my superior who has looked after me.","furigana":"まいとし|せわ|じょうし|せいぼ|おく"},{"japanese":"十二月のデパートは、お歳暮を選ぶ客で込んでいる。","romaji":"Juunigatsu no depaato wa, oseibo o erabu kyaku de konde iru.","german":"In December, department stores are crowded with customers choosing year-end gifts.","furigana":"じゅうにがつ|せいぼ|えら|きゃく|こ"}],"notes":"歳暮 appears almost always with the polite prefix as お歳暮: a gift sent in December to people to whom one feels indebted (superiors, relatives, teachers), as thanks for the past year. Typical are お歳暮を贈る and お歳暮をいただく. The summer counterpart is お中元. The meaning \"end of the year\" is rare and literary.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お中元を贈ります。","romaji":"Ochūgen o okurimasu.","german":"I will send a midsummer gift.","furigana":"ちゅうげん|おく"},{"japanese":"取引先からお中元にビールが届いた。","romaji":"Torihikisaki kara ochuugen ni biiru ga todoita.","german":"A business partner sent us beer as a midsummer gift.","furigana":"とりひきさき|ちゅうげん|とど"},{"japanese":"最近は、お中元を贈らない家庭も増えている。","romaji":"Saikin wa, ochuugen o okuranai katei mo fuete iru.","german":"Recently, more and more households no longer send midsummer gifts.","furigana":"さいきん|ちゅうげん|おく|かてい|ふ"}],"notes":"中元 appears in everyday life almost only as お中元: a summer gift sent, roughly from July to mid-August, to people to whom one is indebted. Originally 中元 refers to the 15th day of the 7th lunar month, which is connected with the お盆 festival. Popular gifts are beer, juices, noodles or sweets; the winter counterpart is お歳暮.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"初詣に行きます。","romaji":"Hatsumōde ni ikimasu.","german":"I am going on the first shrine visit of the new year.","furigana":"はつもうで|い"},{"japanese":"初詣でおみくじを引いたら、大吉でした。","romaji":"Hatsumoude de omikuji o hiitara, daikichi deshita.","german":"When I drew a fortune slip on the first shrine visit of the year, it was \"great luck\"!","furigana":"はつもうで|ひ|だいきち"},{"japanese":"元日の明治神宮は初詣の人でとても混雑します。","romaji":"Ganjitsu no Meiji Jinguu wa hatsumoude no hito de totemo konzatsu shimasu.","german":"On New Year's Day, Meiji Shrine is very crowded with people making their first visit of the year.","furigana":"がんじつ|めいじじんぐう|はつもうで|ひと|こんざつ"}],"notes":"初詣 is the first visit to a Shinto shrine or Buddhist temple in the new year, usually within the first three days (三が日). The usual phrase is 初詣に行く; there one prays for the new year, draws a fortune slip (おみくじを引く) and buys charms (お守り). The spelling 初詣で also occurs. Famous shrines are extremely crowded at this time (初詣の人で混雑する).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"紅葉狩りに行きましょう。","romaji":"Momijigari ni ikimashō.","german":"Let's go leaf viewing.","furigana":"もみじが|い"},{"japanese":"週末に京都へ紅葉狩りに出かけました。","romaji":"Shuumatsu ni Kyouto e momijigari ni dekakemashita.","german":"On the weekend I went to Kyoto to see the autumn leaves.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|きょうと|もみじが|で"},{"japanese":"紅葉狩りの季節になると、山へ行く道が込みます。","romaji":"Momijigari no kisetsu ni naru to, yama e iku michi ga komimasu.","german":"When the leaf-viewing season comes, the roads into the mountains are crowded.","furigana":"もみじが|きせつ|やま|い|みち|こ"}],"notes":"紅葉狩り is the viewing of the colorful autumn leaves in mountains, parks or temple gardens; 狩り (\"hunt\") is only figurative here, nothing is picked or collected. Typical is 紅葉狩りに行く／出かける, the counterpart to cherry blossom viewing 花見 in spring. 紅葉 alone is read こうよう (\"autumn coloring\") or もみじ (\"maple, red leaves\"); in the compound 紅葉狩り it is always もみじ.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"札幌の雪祭りを見ました。","romaji":"Sapporo no yukimatsuri o mimashita.","german":"I saw the Sapporo Snow Festival.","furigana":"さっぽろ|ゆきまつ|み"},{"japanese":"雪祭りでは大きな雪の像がたくさん並びます。","romaji":"Yukimatsuri de wa ookina yuki no zou ga takusan narabimasu.","german":"At the snow festival, many large snow sculptures are lined up.","furigana":"ゆきまつ|おお|ゆき|ぞう|なら"},{"japanese":"来年の二月に雪祭りを見に北海道へ行くつもりです。","romaji":"Rainen no nigatsu ni yukimatsuri o mi ni Hokkaidou e iku tsumori desu.","german":"Next February I plan to go to Hokkaido to see the snow festival.","furigana":"らいねん|にがつ|ゆきまつ|み|ほっかいどう|い"}],"notes":"雪祭り is a winter festival with large sculptures of snow and ice; the best known is the Sapporo snow festival in February, officially written さっぽろ雪まつり. As with many festival names, the spelling 雪まつり is very common. You say 雪祭りに行く or 雪祭りを見に行く; 夏祭り and 秋祭り are formed similarly.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 16"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"盛大な祝賀会でした。","romaji":"Seidai na shukugakai deshita.","german":"It was a grand celebration.","furigana":"せいだい|しゅくがかい"},{"japanese":"二人の結婚式は盛大に行われました。","romaji":"Futari no kekkonshiki wa seidai ni okonawaremashita.","german":"Their wedding was held on a grand scale.","furigana":"ふたり|けっこんしき|せいだい|おこな"},{"japanese":"発表が終わると、盛大な拍手が起こりました。","romaji":"Happyou ga owaru to, seidai na hakushu ga okorimashita.","german":"When the presentation ended, there was thunderous applause.","furigana":"はっぴょう|お|せいだい|はくしゅ|お"}],"notes":"盛大 describes events that are celebrated big, lavishly and with many guests: 盛大な結婚式, 盛大なパーティー, adverbial 盛大に祝う／盛大に行われる. A fixed combination is 盛大な拍手 \"thunderous applause\". Colloquially and often jokingly, 盛大に also means \"badly, thoroughly\", e.g. 盛大に転ぶ. Not to be confused with 壮大, which stresses size and scale (landscape, plan).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"壮大な景色です。","romaji":"Sōdai na keshiki desu.","german":"It is a magnificent view.","furigana":"そうだい|けしき"},{"japanese":"彼は世界一周という壮大な計画を立てた。","romaji":"Kare wa sekai isshuu to iu soudai na keikaku o tateta.","german":"He made a grand plan to travel around the world.","furigana":"かれ|せかいいっしゅう|そうだい|けいかく|た"},{"japanese":"この映画は家族の百年を描いた壮大な物語です。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa kazoku no hyakunen o egaita soudai na monogatari desu.","german":"This film is a grand epic that depicts a hundred years of a family.","furigana":"えいが|かぞく|ひゃくねん|えが|そうだい|ものがたり"}],"notes":"壮大 means \"immense, grand\" in the sense of great scale and sublime effect. Typical partners are 景色, 自然, 計画, 夢 and 物語: 壮大な計画, 壮大なスケール. Unlike 盛大 (a lavishly celebrated event), it is not about celebrating, and unlike 荘厳 not about solemnity, but about size and breadth. The word is somewhat literary, but common in travel writing and film or book reviews.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"荘厳な雰囲気です。","romaji":"Sougon na fun'iki desu.","german":"It is a solemn atmosphere.","furigana":"そうごん|ふんいき"},{"japanese":"教会の中にオルガンの荘厳な音が響きました。","romaji":"Kyoukai no naka ni orugan no sougon na oto ga hibikimashita.","german":"The majestic sound of the organ resounded in the church.","furigana":"きょうかい|なか|そうごん|おと|ひび"},{"japanese":"式は神社で荘厳に行われました。","romaji":"Shiki wa jinja de sougon ni okonawaremashita.","german":"The ceremony was held solemnly at the shrine.","furigana":"しき|じんじゃ|そうごん|おこな"}],"notes":"荘厳 means \"solemn, majestic\" and describes a dignified, awe-inspiring mood, above all at temples, churches, ceremonies and sacred music: 荘厳な雰囲気, 荘厳な儀式, adverbial 荘厳に行われる. The word is written-language and elevated; in Buddhism the same spelling with the reading しょうごん is used for the adornment of temples. Distinction: 壮大 emphasizes grandeur, 盛大 splendor and many guests, 荘厳 dignity and reverence.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"優雅な踊りです。","romaji":"Yūga na odori desu.","german":"It is an elegant dance.","furigana":"ゆうが|おど"},{"japanese":"白鳥が湖を優雅に泳いでいます。","romaji":"Hakuchou ga mizuumi o yuuga ni oyoide imasu.","german":"A swan is gliding gracefully across the lake.","furigana":"はくちょう|みずうみ|ゆうが|およ"},{"japanese":"退職したら、海の近くで優雅に暮らしたいです。","romaji":"Taishoku shitara, umi no chikaku de yuuga ni kurashitai desu.","german":"When I retire, I want to live a leisurely life by the sea.","furigana":"たいしょく|うみ|ちか|ゆうが|く"}],"notes":"優雅 has two meanings: \"elegant, graceful\" for movements, bearing and clothing (優雅な踊り, 優雅に泳ぐ), and \"carefree, leisurely\" for a life without money worries (優雅な生活, 優雅に暮らす). In the second meaning it often sounds slightly envious or ironic, for example 平日に旅行なんて優雅だね. Related is 上品 (\"refined, cultivated\"), which describes more character, taste and manners.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鮮やかな色です。","romaji":"Azayaka na iro desu.","german":"It is a vivid color.","furigana":"あざ|いろ"},{"japanese":"秋になると、山が鮮やかな赤や黄色に染まります。","romaji":"Aki ni naru to, yama ga azayaka na aka ya kiiro ni somarimasu.","german":"In autumn, the mountains turn vivid red and yellow.","furigana":"あき|やま|あざ|あか|きいろ|そ"},{"japanese":"彼は鮮やかなシュートでゴールを決めた。","romaji":"Kare wa azayaka na shuuto de gooru o kimeta.","german":"He scored a goal with a brilliant shot.","furigana":"かれ|あざ|き"}],"notes":"鮮やか first describes bright, clear colors (鮮やかな赤, 色鮮やか) and also vivid memories (記憶に鮮やかに残る). Equally common is the meaning \"skillful, brilliant\" for a masterly performance: 鮮やかな手つき, 鮮やかなシュート, 鮮やかに解決する. For colors, 淡い is the opposite; 派手 (\"flashy, showy\") often sounds judgmental, whereas 鮮やか is positive.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"淡い色が好きです。","romaji":"Awai iro ga suki desu.","german":"I like pale colors.","furigana":"あわ|いろ|す"},{"japanese":"春らしい淡いピンクのシャツを買いました。","romaji":"Harurashii awai pinku no shatsu o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a spring-like pale pink shirt.","furigana":"はる|あわ|か"},{"japanese":"合格できるかもしれないという淡い期待を持っていた。","romaji":"Goukaku dekiru kamo shirenai to iu awai kitai o motte ita.","german":"I held a faint hope that I might pass.","furigana":"ごうかく|あわ|きたい|も"}],"notes":"淡い means \"pale, delicate, light\" for colors, light, taste and scent (淡い色, 淡いピンク, 淡い味) and sounds more elevated and poetic than the everyday word 薄い. Figuratively it describes weak, fleeting feelings: 淡い期待 \"faint hope\", 淡い恋 \"tender, shy love\". The opposite is 濃い; unlike 薄い, however, 淡い is never used for the thickness of objects (a thin book is 薄い本).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"濃い味が好きです。","romaji":"Koi aji ga suki desu.","german":"I like strong flavors.","furigana":"こ|あじ|す"},{"japanese":"今朝は濃い霧のせいで、電車が遅れました。","romaji":"Kesa wa koi kiri no sei de, densha ga okuremashita.","german":"This morning the trains were delayed because of dense fog.","furigana":"けさ|こ|きり|でんしゃ|おく"},{"japanese":"兄はひげが濃いので、毎朝そらないといけません。","romaji":"Ani wa hige ga koi node, maiasa soranai to ikemasen.","german":"My older brother has a heavy beard, so he has to shave every morning.","furigana":"あに|こ|まいあさ"}],"notes":"濃い is the opposite of 薄い and covers several areas: dark or strong colors (濃い青), strong taste or smell (味が濃い, 濃いコーヒー), dense consistency (濃い霧, 濃いスープ) and thick hair growth (眉が濃い, ひげが濃い). Figuratively it stands for high probability (可能性が濃い) and close relationships (濃い人間関係). 化粧が濃い means \"wearing heavy makeup\" and is meant rather critically.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 44"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"密接な関係があります。","romaji":"Missetsu na kankei ga arimasu.","german":"There is a close relationship.","furigana":"みっせつ|かんけい"},{"japanese":"食生活と健康は密接に関係しています。","romaji":"Shokuseikatsu to kenkou wa missetsu ni kankei shite imasu.","german":"Diet and health are closely related.","furigana":"しょくせいかつ|けんこう|みっせつ|かんけい"},{"japanese":"コロナの時は、人と密接する場面を避けるように言われました。","romaji":"Korona no toki wa, hito to missetsu suru bamen o sakeru you ni iwaremashita.","german":"During COVID, we were told to avoid situations of close contact with other people.","furigana":"とき|ひと|みっせつ|ばめん|さ|い"}],"notes":"密接 means \"close\" in the sense of a tight connection or interaction between things and almost always appears in 密接な関係 or 密接に関係する／関わる／結びつく. For personal, warm closeness between people one rather says 親密. As a する-verb, 密接する means \"to be close together\"; this became well known through the COVID rule 三密 (密閉・密集・密接). The word is slightly literary and common in news and factual texts.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"抜本的な改革が必要です。","romaji":"Bapponteki na kaikaku ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"Fundamental reform is necessary.","furigana":"ばっぽんてき|かいかく|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"政府は交通事故を減らすために、抜本的な対策を発表した。","romaji":"Seifu wa koutsuu jiko o herasu tame ni, bapponteki na taisaku o happyou shita.","german":"The government announced drastic measures to reduce traffic accidents.","furigana":"せいふ|こうつうじこ|へ|ばっぽんてき|たいさく|はっぴょう"},{"japanese":"赤字が続いたため、会社は経営を抜本的に見直した。","romaji":"Akaji ga tsuzuita tame, kaisha wa keiei o bapponteki ni minaoshita.","german":"Because losses continued, the company fundamentally reviewed its management.","furigana":"あかじ|つづ|かいしゃ|けいえい|ばっぽんてき|みなお"}],"notes":"抜本的 means \"fundamental, sweeping\" and refers to measures that tackle a problem at its root (抜本 = \"pulling out the root\"). It is used almost only with words for reforms and measures: 抜本的な改革, 抜本的な対策, 抜本的な見直し, adverbially 抜本的に見直す／改める. The word belongs to the language of politics, economics and the news; in everyday speech one says more 根本的 or 根本から変える.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"非合理的な行動です。","romaji":"Higōriteki na kōdō desu.","german":"It is irrational behavior.","furigana":"ひごうりてき|こうどう"},{"japanese":"毎日遅くまで会議をするのは非合理的だと思います。","romaji":"Mainichi osoku made kaigi o suru no wa higouriteki da to omoimasu.","german":"I think it is irrational to hold meetings until late every day.","furigana":"まいにちおそ|かいぎ|ひごうりてき|おも"},{"japanese":"人は不安になると、非合理的な判断をしがちです。","romaji":"Hito wa fuan ni naru to, higouriteki na handan o shigachi desu.","german":"When people become anxious, they tend to make irrational decisions.","furigana":"ひと|ふあん|ひごうりてき|はんだん"}],"notes":"非合理的 is the opposite of 合理的 (\"rational, reasonable, efficient\") and means \"irrational, unreasonable\" - both for thinking and behavior (非合理的な判断) and for inefficient rules and procedures (非合理的なルール). Synonymous and somewhat more common is 不合理; 非論理的, by contrast, stresses the lack of logical coherence. The prefix 非 negates, as in 非常識 or 非公式.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"道徳的に正しいです。","romaji":"Dōtokuteki ni tadashii desu.","german":"It is morally right.","furigana":"どうとくてき|ただ"},{"japanese":"法律に違反していなくても、道徳的に問題がある行為もある。","romaji":"Houritsu ni ihan shite inakute mo, doutokuteki ni mondai ga aru koui mo aru.","german":"Some conduct is morally questionable even if it violates no law.","furigana":"ほうりつ|いはん|どうとくてき|もんだい|こうい"},{"japanese":"子どもに道徳的な考え方を教えるのは親の役目です。","romaji":"Kodomo ni doutokuteki na kangaekata o oshieru no wa oya no yakume desu.","german":"It is the parents' role to teach children moral ways of thinking.","furigana":"こ|どうとくてき|かんが|かた|おし|おや|やくめ"}],"notes":"道徳的 means \"moral, ethical\" and judges actions by general social norms: 道徳的に正しい／問題がある, 道徳的な責任. 道徳 itself is also the name of the school subject \"moral education\". Closely related is 倫理的 (\"ethical\"), which is used more for professional ethics, medicine or philosophy; the opposite is 不道徳 (\"immoral\").","levelNote":"Compound: 道徳 (N3) + 的 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"堅固な建物です。","romaji":"Kengo na tatemono desu.","german":"It is a solid building.","furigana":"けんご|たてもの"},{"japanese":"この城は山の上にあり、守りが堅固だった。","romaji":"Kono shiro wa yama no ue ni ari, mamori ga kengo datta.","german":"This castle stood on a mountain and was well defended.","furigana":"しろ|やま|うえ|まも|けんご"},{"japanese":"彼女は意志が堅固で、一度決めたことは必ずやり通す。","romaji":"Kanojo wa ishi ga kengo de, ichido kimeta koto wa kanarazu yaritoosu.","german":"She has a firm will and always sees through what she has decided.","furigana":"かのじょ|いし|けんご|いちどき|かなら|とお"}],"notes":"堅固 means \"firm, solid, hard to capture\" and is a written-language word; typical are 堅固な城, 堅固な守り, 堅固な建物. Figuratively it describes an unshakable will: 意志が堅固, as a four-character word 意志堅固. In everyday speech, for sturdy objects one rather says 頑丈 or 丈夫; 堅固 sounds like fortress, defense and history.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"脆弱なシステムです。","romaji":"Zeijaku na shisutemu desu.","german":"It is a vulnerable system.","furigana":"ぜいじゃく"},{"japanese":"このソフトに脆弱性が見つかったので、すぐに更新してください。","romaji":"Kono sofuto ni zeijakusei ga mitsukatta node, sugu ni koushin shite kudasai.","german":"A vulnerability was found in this software, so please update it immediately.","furigana":"ぜいじゃくせい|み|こうしん"},{"japanese":"この地域は地盤が脆弱なので、地震の被害が大きくなりやすい。","romaji":"Kono chiiki wa jiban ga zeijaku na node, jishin no higai ga ookiku nariyasui.","german":"Because the ground in this area is weak, earthquake damage tends to be large.","furigana":"ちいき|じばん|ぜいじゃく|じしん|ひがい|おお"}],"notes":"脆弱 means \"weak, susceptible, vulnerable\" and is a written-language word for structures, systems and foundations: 脆弱な地盤, 脆弱な経済基盤, 体制が脆弱だ. In IT, 脆弱性 is the technical term for a \"security vulnerability\". The character 脆 is known from 脆い (\"brittle, fragile\"). For people and objects in everyday speech, one more often says 弱い, 壊れやすい or 脆い.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"推測に過ぎません。","romaji":"Suisoku ni sugimasen.","german":"It is nothing more than conjecture.","furigana":"すいそく|す"},{"japanese":"彼の表情から、試験の結果は悪かったと推測した。","romaji":"Kare no hyoujou kara, shiken no kekka wa warukatta to suisoku shita.","german":"From his expression, I inferred that the exam results had been bad.","furigana":"かれ|ひょうじょう|しけん|けっか|わる|すいそく"},{"japanese":"私の推測は当たっていて、彼は本当に会社を辞めるつもりだった。","romaji":"Watashi no suisoku wa atatte ite, kare wa hontou ni kaisha o yameru tsumori datta.","german":"My guess was right: he really was planning to quit the company.","furigana":"わたし|すいそく|あ|かれ|ほんとう|かいしゃ|や"}],"notes":"推測 is a conjecture derived from available clues; as a transitive する-verb: 〜を推測する, 〜と推測する, passive 〜と推測される (common in the news). Fixed combinations are 推測が当たる／外れる and 推測に過ぎない \"is only a conjecture\". 予想 refers more to future results, 推理 to logical deduction as in a detective story, and 想像 to free imagination.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"プロジェクトを推進します。","romaji":"Purojekuto o suishin shimasu.","german":"I will push the project forward.","furigana":"すいしん"},{"japanese":"政府は再生可能エネルギーの利用を推進している。","romaji":"Seifu wa saisei kanou enerugii no riyou o suishin shite iru.","german":"The government is promoting the use of renewable energy.","furigana":"せいふ|さいせいかのう|りよう|すいしん"},{"japanese":"このエンジンは船に強い推進力を与える。","romaji":"Kono enjin wa fune ni tsuyoi suishinryoku o ataeru.","german":"This engine gives the ship strong propulsion.","furigana":"ふね|つよ|すいしんりょく|あた"}],"notes":"推進 means \"to push forward, promote\" and is used mainly for politics, projects and movements: 〜を推進する, 改革を推進する, 推進派 \"proponents\"; the passive 〜が推進されている is also common. The basic technical meaning is \"propulsion\" (推進力 \"thrust\"). The word is literary and typical of official and corporate language; in conversation one rather says 進める.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"障害を排除します。","romaji":"Shōgai o haijo shimasu.","german":"I will eliminate obstacles.","furigana":"しょうがい|はいじょ"},{"japanese":"警察はデモ隊を道路から排除した。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa demotai o douro kara haijo shita.","german":"The police removed the demonstrators from the road.","furigana":"けいさつ|たい|どうろ|はいじょ"},{"japanese":"事故の可能性も排除できません。","romaji":"Jiko no kanousei mo haijo dekimasen.","german":"The possibility of an accident cannot be ruled out either.","furigana":"じこ|かのうせい|はいじょ"}],"notes":"排除 means \"to exclude, eliminate, remove\", often forcefully or with force: 障害を排除する, 反対派を排除する, 警察がデモ隊を排除する. Very common is 可能性を排除する／排除できない \"to rule out (be unable to rule out) a possibility\". More neutral are 除く／取り除く; 除外 means \"to take out of a list or evaluation\" and sounds less harsh. A transitive する-verb, literary.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しいシステムを導入します。","romaji":"Atarashii shisutemu o dōnyū shimasu.","german":"We will introduce a new system.","furigana":"あたら|どうにゅう"},{"japanese":"来年から新しい給料の制度が導入される予定です。","romaji":"Rainen kara atarashii kyuuryou no seido ga dounyuu sareru yotei desu.","german":"A new salary system is scheduled to be introduced from next year.","furigana":"らいねん|あたら|きゅうりょう|せいど|どうにゅう|よてい"},{"japanese":"この小説は導入部分が面白くて、一気に読んでしまった。","romaji":"Kono shousetsu wa dounyuu bubun ga omoshirokute, ikki ni yonde shimatta.","german":"The opening part of this novel was so interesting that I read it in one go.","furigana":"しょうせつ|どうにゅうぶぶん|おもしろ|いっき|よ"}],"notes":"導入 means \"to introduce\" systems, technology, rules or devices: 〜を導入する, 制度を導入する, passive 〜が導入される. For introducing people one says 紹介 instead, not 導入. The second meaning is the \"introduction\" of a text, lecture or piece of music (導入部, 導入部分). The word is matter-of-fact and very common in business and the news.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"市場から撤退しました。","romaji":"Shijō kara tettai shimashita.","german":"We withdrew from the market.","furigana":"しじょう|てったい"},{"japanese":"軍は前線から撤退した。","romaji":"Gun wa zensen kara tettai shita.","german":"The army withdrew from the front.","furigana":"ぐん|ぜんせん|てったい"},{"japanese":"雨が強くなったので、頂上をあきらめて撤退することにした。","romaji":"Ame ga tsuyoku natta node, choujou o akiramete tettai suru koto ni shita.","german":"The rain got heavier, so we gave up on the summit and decided to turn back.","furigana":"あめ|つよ|ちょうじょう|てったい"}],"notes":"撤退 is an intransitive する-verb: 〜から撤退する \"to withdraw from ...\". Originally military (軍が撤退する), it is now often used for companies that give up a market or a line of business (海外から撤退する, 事業撤退). In mountaineering, too, one speaks of 撤退 when turning back before the summit. It should be distinguished from 撤去 (\"to remove, to dismantle objects\"), which is transitive.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二つの部門を統合します。","romaji":"Futatsu no bumon o tōgō shimasu.","german":"We will merge the two departments.","furigana":"ふた|ぶもん|とうごう"},{"japanese":"複数のアプリを一つに統合して、使いやすくしました。","romaji":"Fukusuu no apuri o hitotsu ni tougou shite, tsukaiyasuku shimashita.","german":"We combined several apps into one to make them easier to use.","furigana":"ふくすう|ひと|とうごう|つか"},{"japanese":"子どもが減って、町の小学校が三つ統合された。","romaji":"Kodomo ga hette, machi no shougakkou ga mittsu tougou sareta.","german":"As the number of children declined, three of the town's elementary schools were merged.","furigana":"こ|へ|まち|しょうがっこう|みっ|とうごう"}],"notes":"統合 means \"to bring together, unify\" several parts into a whole: AとBを統合する, 〜を一つに統合する, passive 〜が統合される. Common are 経営統合 (\"merger of two companies\"), 学校の統合 and データの統合. 合併, by contrast, is the legal merger of companies or municipalities, and 統一 means \"to unify\" (rules, countries).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"政教分離の原則です。","romaji":"Seikyō bunri no gensoku desu.","german":"It is the principle of the separation of religion and state.","furigana":"せいきょうぶんり|げんそく"},{"japanese":"水と油は混ぜてもすぐに分離します。","romaji":"Mizu to abura wa mazete mo sugu ni bunri shimasu.","german":"Water and oil separate right away even if you mix them.","furigana":"みず|あぶら|ま|ぶんり"},{"japanese":"車が高速道路の中央分離帯にぶつかった。","romaji":"Kuruma ga kousoku douro no chuuou bunritai ni butsukatta.","german":"A car hit the median strip on the expressway.","furigana":"くるま|こうそくどうろ|ちゅうおうぶんりたい"}],"notes":"分離 means \"to separate, become separated\" and is both transitive (〜を分離する) and intransitive (〜が分離する), for example when water and oil separate. It is technical and literary: 政教分離 (\"separation of state and religion\"), 中央分離帯 (\"median strip\"). For sorting trash one says 分別, and for people separating 別れる or 別居.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"方針を転換しました。","romaji":"Hōshin o tenkan shimashita.","german":"We changed our policy.","furigana":"ほうしん|てんかん"},{"japanese":"散歩でもして気分転換しませんか。","romaji":"Sanpo demo shite kibun tenkan shimasen ka.","german":"Why don't we take a walk to change our mood?","furigana":"さんぽ|きぶんてんかん"},{"japanese":"発想を転換すれば、問題は簡単に解決できる。","romaji":"Hassou o tenkan sureba, mondai wa kantan ni kaiketsu dekiru.","german":"If you change your way of thinking, the problem can be solved easily.","furigana":"はっそう|てんかん|もんだい|かんたん|かいけつ"}],"notes":"転換 means a fundamental switch or turn in direction or kind: 方針を転換する, 方向転換, 発想の転換 (\"rethinking\"), 転換期 (\"turning point\"). Especially everyday is 気分転換 \"a change of pace to take your mind off things\". As a する-verb it can be transitive (〜を転換する) and intransitive (〜に転換する). Not to be confused with 変換, for example when converting kana to kanji on a computer.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"信頼関係を構築します。","romaji":"Shinrai kankei o kōchiku shimasu.","german":"We build a relationship of trust.","furigana":"しんらいかんけい|こうちく"},{"japanese":"会社は新しい顧客管理システムを構築した。","romaji":"Kaisha wa atarashii kokyaku kanri shisutemu o kouchiku shita.","german":"The company built a new customer management system.","furigana":"かいしゃ|あたら|こきゃくかんり|こうちく"},{"japanese":"研究者たちは新しい理論の構築に取り組んでいる。","romaji":"Kenkyuushatachi wa atarashii riron no kouchiku ni torikunde iru.","german":"The researchers are working on constructing a new theory.","furigana":"けんきゅうしゃ|あたら|りろん|こうちく|と|く"}],"notes":"構築 means \"to build up, to construct\", mostly in the abstract sense: systems, networks, theories and relationships are built (システムを構築する, 信頼関係を構築する). For actual buildings, 建築 or 建設 are used instead. The word is matter-of-fact and written-language and very common in IT and business; in conversation 作る or 築く are often enough.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"経済が崩壊しました。","romaji":"Keizai ga hōkai shimashita.","german":"The economy collapsed.","furigana":"けいざい|ほうかい"},{"japanese":"地震で古い橋が崩壊した。","romaji":"Jishin de furui hashi ga houkai shita.","german":"An old bridge collapsed in the earthquake.","furigana":"じしん|ふる|はし|ほうかい"},{"japanese":"1989年にベルリンの壁が崩壊しました。","romaji":"Sen kyuuhyaku hachijuu kyuu-nen ni Berurin no kabe ga houkai shimashita.","german":"The Berlin Wall fell in 1989.","furigana":"ねん|かべ|ほうかい"}],"notes":"崩壊 is an intransitive する-verb for the complete collapse of structures, systems and orders: 建物が崩壊する, 経済が崩壊する, バブル崩壊, 家庭崩壊, 学級崩壊 (\"the class getting out of control\"). In physics it denotes radioactive decay. The everyday word for things falling down is 崩れる; 崩壊 sounds more dramatic and final.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"安全を確保します。","romaji":"Anzen o kakuho shimasu.","german":"We will ensure safety.","furigana":"あんぜん|かくほ"},{"japanese":"早く行って、いい席を確保しておくね。","romaji":"Hayaku itte, ii seki o kakuho shite oku ne.","german":"I'll go early and save us a good seat.","furigana":"はや|い|せき|かくほ"},{"japanese":"毎日、勉強の時間を確保するのは難しいです。","romaji":"Mainichi, benkyou no jikan o kakuho suru no wa muzukashii desu.","german":"It is difficult to secure time for studying every day.","furigana":"まいにち|べんきょう|じかん|かくほ|むずか"}],"notes":"確保 means \"to secure something, make sure of it\": you make sure that something necessary is reliably available: 席を確保する, 時間を確保する, 人材を確保する, 安全を確保する, 食料や水の確保. A transitive する-verb with を. In police reports, 身柄を確保する means \"to arrest, take into custody\". For merely obtaining something one says 手に入れる; 確保 stresses securing it.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"能力を発揮します。","romaji":"Nōryoku o hakki shimasu.","german":"I make full use of my abilities.","furigana":"のうりょく|はっき"},{"japanese":"緊張して、試合で実力を発揮できなかった。","romaji":"Kinchou shite, shiai de jitsuryoku o hakki dekinakatta.","german":"I was so nervous that I couldn't show my true ability in the match.","furigana":"きんちょう|しあい|じつりょく|はっき"},{"japanese":"この薬は飲んでから三十分ほどで効果を発揮します。","romaji":"Kono kusuri wa nonde kara sanjuppun hodo de kouka o hakki shimasu.","german":"This medicine takes effect about thirty minutes after taking it.","furigana":"くすり|の|さんじゅっぷん|こうか|はっき"}],"notes":"発揮 means bringing an existing power or ability into play: 能力／実力／力を発揮する, リーダーシップを発揮する, 本領を発揮する \"to show what one is made of\". Things can also take effect: 薬が効果を発揮する. A transitive する-verb with を, often in the potential or negated: 実力を発揮できなかった. Not to be confused with 発表 (\"to announce, to present\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"資源を活用します。","romaji":"Shigen o katsuyō shimasu.","german":"I make use of the resources.","furigana":"しげん|かつよう"},{"japanese":"留学の経験を仕事に活用したいです。","romaji":"Ryuugaku no keiken o shigoto ni katsuyou shitai desu.","german":"I want to use my experience from studying abroad in my job.","furigana":"りゅうがく|けいけん|しごと|かつよう"},{"japanese":"日本語の動詞の活用はなかなか覚えられません。","romaji":"Nihongo no doushi no katsuyou wa nakanaka oboeraremasen.","german":"I just can't memorize the conjugation of Japanese verbs.","furigana":"にほんご|どうし|かつよう|おぼ"}],"notes":"活用 means \"to make good use of, to put to use\" - one gets something out of what is available: 経験を活用する, データを活用する, 〜を〜に活用する, 有効活用 \"effective use\". It sounds more active than 利用 (\"to use\") and is common in business and administration. In grammar, 活用 means \"conjugation, inflection\": 動詞の活用, 活用形; in this sense 活用する is intransitive (動詞が活用する).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい規則を適用します。","romaji":"Atarashii kisoku o tekiyō shimasu.","german":"We apply the new rules.","furigana":"あたら|きそく|てきよう"},{"japanese":"この治療には健康保険が適用されません。","romaji":"Kono chiryou ni wa kenkou hoken ga tekiyou saremasen.","german":"Health insurance does not cover this treatment.","furigana":"ちりょう|けんこうほけん|てきよう"},{"japanese":"割引は学生にのみ適用されます。","romaji":"Waribiki wa gakusei ni nomi tekiyou saremasu.","german":"The discount applies only to students.","furigana":"わりびき|がくせい|てきよう"}],"notes":"適用 means to apply a rule, a law, an insurance or a rate to a case: 規則を適用する, 法律が適用される. The passive is very common: 保険が適用される \"the insurance covers the costs\", 割引が適用される \"the discount applies\". Distinctions: 応用 is the creative transfer of knowledge or technology to something new, 活用 the sensible use of resources; 適用 is the formal application of a rule.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"理論を応用します。","romaji":"Riron o ōyō shimasu.","german":"I apply the theory.","furigana":"りろん|おうよう"},{"japanese":"基本がわかったら、次は応用問題に挑戦しましょう。","romaji":"Kihon ga wakattara, tsugi wa ouyou mondai ni chousen shimashou.","german":"Once you understand the basics, let's try the application problems next.","furigana":"きほん|つぎ|おうようもんだい|ちょうせん"},{"japanese":"この技術は医療にも応用されています。","romaji":"Kono gijutsu wa iryou ni mo ouyou sarete imasu.","german":"This technology is also applied in medicine.","furigana":"ぎじゅつ|いりょう|おうよう"}],"notes":"応用 means applying basic knowledge, theories or techniques practically to something new: 理論を応用する, 〜を〜に応用する, passive 〜に応用されている. In compounds it stands for \"applied\" or \"advanced\": 応用化学, 応用問題 (\"transfer problem\"), 応用力 (\"ability to transfer what has been learned\"). The counterpart is 基本／基礎; 適用, by contrast, is the formal application of rules and laws.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"影響が波及しています。","romaji":"Eikyō ga hakyū shite imasu.","german":"The effects are spreading.","furigana":"えいきょう|はきゅう"},{"japanese":"アメリカの不景気が世界中に波及した。","romaji":"Amerika no fukeiki ga sekaijuu ni hakyuu shita.","german":"The recession in America spread to the whole world.","furigana":"ふけいき|せかいじゅう|はきゅう"},{"japanese":"オリンピックは地域の経済に大きな波及効果をもたらす。","romaji":"Orinpikku wa chiiki no keizai ni ookina hakyuu kouka o motarasu.","german":"The Olympics bring a large ripple effect to the regional economy.","furigana":"ちいき|けいざい|おお|はきゅうこうか"}],"notes":"波及 describes how an effect spreads in waves (波 \"wave\" + 及 \"to reach\"): 影響が〜に波及する, 〜全体に波及する. A fixed combination is 波及効果 \"ripple effect, spillover effect\", mostly economic. It is an intransitive する-verb, and its subjects are effects, crises or unrest. It is written-language and typical of the news; in conversation one says more 広がる or 影響が出る.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"経済が停滞しています。","romaji":"Keizai ga teitai shite imasu.","german":"The economy is stagnating.","furigana":"けいざい|ていたい"},{"japanese":"梅雨前線が九州付近に停滞しています。","romaji":"Baiu zensen ga Kyuushuu fukin ni teitai shite imasu.","german":"The rainy-season front is stalled near Kyushu.","furigana":"ばいうぜんせん|きゅうしゅうふきん|ていたい"},{"japanese":"リーダーが辞めてから、プロジェクトが停滞している。","romaji":"Riidaa ga yamete kara, purojekuto ga teitai shite iru.","german":"Since the leader quit, the project has been stalled.","furigana":"や|ていたい"}],"notes":"停滞 means \"standstill, stagnation\": something that should move or develop does not progress: 経済が停滞する, 景気の停滞, 議論が停滞する, 作業が停滞する. In weather reports one hears 前線が停滞する and 停滞前線 (\"stationary front\"), for example in the rainy season. An intransitive する-verb. Not to be confused with 渋滞, which stands for traffic jams.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"リスクを回避します。","romaji":"Risuku o kaihi shimasu.","german":"I avoid risks.","furigana":"かいひ"},{"japanese":"運転手がとっさにハンドルを切って、事故を回避した。","romaji":"Untenshu ga tossa ni handoru o kitte, jiko o kaihi shita.","german":"The driver swerved instantly and avoided an accident.","furigana":"うんてんしゅ|き|じこ|かいひ"},{"japanese":"彼は責任を回避するような発言ばかりしている。","romaji":"Kare wa sekinin o kaihi suru you na hatsugen bakari shite iru.","german":"He keeps making statements that dodge responsibility.","furigana":"かれ|せきにん|かいひ|はつげん"}],"notes":"回避 means \"to avoid something undesirable, to avert it\". Although dictionaries list it as intransitive, in practice it almost always takes を: 危険／リスク／事故を回避する, 責任を回避する (\"to evade responsibility\"). It is more matter-of-fact and formal than 避ける; also common are 回避策 (\"workaround\") and 衝突回避. In law, it refers to a judge's recusal of himself.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"問題に対処します。","romaji":"Mondai ni taisho shimasu.","german":"I will deal with the problem.","furigana":"もんだい|たいしょ"},{"japanese":"地震のときにどう対処すればいいか、家族で話し合った。","romaji":"Jishin no toki ni dou taisho sureba ii ka, kazoku de hanashiatta.","german":"We discussed as a family what to do in an earthquake.","furigana":"じしん|たいしょ|かぞく|はな|あ"},{"japanese":"頭痛の対処法として、まず水を飲むことをすすめます。","romaji":"Zutsuu no taishohou to shite, mazu mizu o nomu koto o susumemasu.","german":"As a way to deal with a headache, I recommend first drinking water.","furigana":"ずつう|たいしょほう|みず|の"}],"notes":"対処 means to respond appropriately to a problem or situation and cope with it. It is intransitive and takes に: 問題に対処する, 事態に対処する, 迅速に対処する; plus the noun 対処法 (\"remedy, way of dealing with it\"). Very similar is 対応, which is broader and also covers customer service or replying (お客様の対応); 対処 stresses solving a difficulty.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新たな方策を考えます。","romaji":"Arata na hōsaku o kangaemasu.","german":"I will think of a new approach.","furigana":"あら|ほうさく|かんが"},{"japanese":"少子化を止めるための有効な方策が見つからない。","romaji":"Shoushika o tomeru tame no yuukou na housaku ga mitsukaranai.","german":"We cannot find an effective measure to stop the declining birthrate.","furigana":"しょうしか|と|ゆうこう|ほうさく|み"},{"japanese":"会社は売り上げを伸ばすための方策を立てた。","romaji":"Kaisha wa uriage o nobasu tame no housaku o tateta.","german":"The company drew up a plan to increase sales.","furigana":"かいしゃ|う|あ|の|ほうさく|た"}],"notes":"方策 is a plan or measure for reaching a goal or solving a problem: 方策を考える／立てる, 有効な方策, 〜ための方策. In formal texts it appears as 方策を講じる \"to take measures\". The word is literary; in everyday speech one rather says 方法 or やり方, and for countermeasures against a specific problem 対策 is far more common.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不適切な発言でした。","romaji":"Futekisetsu na hatsugen deshita.","german":"It was an inappropriate remark.","furigana":"ふてきせつ|はつげん"},{"japanese":"この動画には不適切な内容が含まれています。","romaji":"Kono douga ni wa futekisetsu na naiyou ga fukumarete imasu.","german":"This video contains inappropriate content.","furigana":"どうが|ふてきせつ|ないよう|ふく"},{"japanese":"薬を不適切に使うと、体に悪い影響が出ることがある。","romaji":"Kusuri o futekisetsu ni tsukau to, karada ni warui eikyou ga deru koto ga aru.","german":"Using medicine improperly can sometimes have bad effects on the body.","furigana":"くすり|ふてきせつ|つか|からだ|わる|えいきょう|で"}],"notes":"不適切 is the negation of 適切 (\"appropriate, suitable\") and means \"inappropriate, unsuitable\". Typical are 不適切な発言, 不適切な表現, 不適切な内容, 不適切な関係 and adverbially 不適切に使う. The word is matter-of-fact and formal and often appears in apologies, news and guidelines; in conversation one more often says よくない or ふさわしくない. The similar 不適当 stresses more \"not suited for a purpose\".","levelNote":"Compound: 不 (N3) + 適切 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"的確な指摘です。","romaji":"Tekikaku na shiteki desu.","german":"It is an apt remark.","furigana":"てきかく|してき"},{"japanese":"部長はいつも的確な指示を出してくれる。","romaji":"Buchou wa itsumo tekikaku na shiji o dashite kureru.","german":"The department head always gives clear, accurate instructions.","furigana":"ぶちょう|てきかく|しじ|だ"},{"japanese":"彼女は質問に短く的確に答えた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa shitsumon ni mijikaku tekikaku ni kotaeta.","german":"She answered the question briefly and precisely.","furigana":"かのじょ|しつもん|みじか|てきかく|こた"}],"notes":"的確 means \"accurate, exactly right\" - something hits the target like an arrow (的 \"target\"): 的確な判断, 的確な指示, 的確なアドバイス, 的確に答える. While 正確 emphasizes that something is error-free and exact (正確な時間), 的確 emphasizes that it fits the situation and hits the heart of the matter. Besides てきかく, the reading てっかく is also possible; the tone is matter-of-fact and mostly praising.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"以前ここに住んでいました。","romaji":"Izen koko ni sunde imashita.","german":"I used to live here.","furigana":"いぜん|す"},{"japanese":"以前より日本語が上手になりましたね。","romaji":"Izen yori Nihongo ga jouzu ni narimashita ne.","german":"Your Japanese has gotten better than before.","furigana":"いぜん|にほんご|じょうず"},{"japanese":"結婚する以前は、東京の会社で働いていた。","romaji":"Kekkon suru izen wa, Toukyou no kaisha de hataraite ita.","german":"Before I got married, I worked at a company in Tokyo.","furigana":"けっこん|いぜん|とうきょう|かいしゃ|はたら"}],"notes":"以前 as an adverb or noun means \"previously, before\": 以前は〜, 以前の家, 以前より上手になった; it is somewhat more formal than 前 and usually refers to a nearer past than 昔. As a suffix it means \"before, up to\": 明治以前, 結婚する以前. The phrase それ以前の問題 means \"an even more fundamental problem\"; not to be confused with the similar-sounding 依然 (\"still, as before\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"卒業以来会っていません。","romaji":"Sotsugyō irai atte imasen.","german":"I haven't seen him/her since graduation.","furigana":"そつぎょういらいあ"},{"japanese":"日本に来て以来、毎日日本語で日記を書いています。","romaji":"Nihon ni kite irai, mainichi Nihongo de nikki o kaite imasu.","german":"Ever since I came to Japan, I have written a diary in Japanese every day.","furigana":"にほん|き|いらい|まいにちにほんご|にっき|か"},{"japanese":"去年の夏に一度会ったが、それ以来、彼からは連絡がない。","romaji":"Kyonen no natsu ni ichido atta ga, sore irai, kare kara wa renraku ga nai.","german":"We met once last summer, but I haven't heard from him since then.","furigana":"きょねん|なつ|いちどあ|いらい|かれ|れんらく"}],"notes":"以来 means \"since, ever since\" and describes a state that continues from a point in the past up to now. It follows nouns (卒業以来, 去年以来), the て-form (日本に来て以来) or stands alone as それ以来. Unlike から or 以降, 以来 cannot refer to the future: \"from next week\" is 来週から or 来週以降. It is often followed by a negation or a continuing state (〜ていない, ずっと〜).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"直ちに対応します。","romaji":"Tadachi ni taiō shimasu.","german":"I will respond immediately.","furigana":"ただ|たいおう"},{"japanese":"津波警報が出ました。直ちに高い所へ避難してください。","romaji":"Tsunami keihou ga demashita. Tadachi ni takai tokoro e hinan shite kudasai.","german":"A tsunami warning has been issued. Please evacuate to high ground immediately.","furigana":"つなみけいほう|で|ただ|たか|ところ|ひなん"},{"japanese":"この量なら、直ちに健康に影響が出るわけではありません。","romaji":"Kono ryou nara, tadachi ni kenkou ni eikyou ga deru wake de wa arimasen.","german":"At this amount, there is no immediate effect on health.","furigana":"りょう|ただ|けんこう|えいきょう|で"}],"notes":"直ちに means \"immediately, without delay\" and is the formal counterpart of すぐに. It is heard in announcements, warnings, laws and business language: 直ちに避難してください, 直ちに対応いたします. With a negation it means \"not immediately, not automatically\": 直ちに健康に影響が出るわけではない. 速やかに is similar but allows a bit more leeway (\"promptly, without unnecessary delay\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"速やかに退去してください。","romaji":"Sumiyaka ni taikyo shite kudasai.","german":"Please vacate promptly.","furigana":"すみ|たいきょ"},{"japanese":"書類は速やかに提出してください。","romaji":"Shorui wa sumiyaka ni teishutsu shite kudasai.","german":"Please submit the documents promptly.","furigana":"しょるい|すみ|ていしゅつ"},{"japanese":"会社の速やかな対応のおかげで、問題はすぐに解決した。","romaji":"Kaisha no sumiyaka na taiou no okage de, mondai wa sugu ni kaiketsu shita.","german":"Thanks to the company's prompt response, the problem was solved quickly.","furigana":"かいしゃ|すみ|たいおう|もんだい|かいけつ"}],"notes":"速やかに is the adverbial form of the な-adjective 速やか and means \"swiftly, promptly\". It is formal and typically appears in requests and instructions from authorities, companies or schools: 速やかに提出してください, 速やかに退出する, as an adjective 速やかな対応. Compared with 直ちに (\"immediately, without any delay\"), it sounds somewhat less urgent; in conversation one simply says すぐ or 早めに.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あえて言いません。","romaji":"Aete iimasen.","german":"I deliberately won't say it.","furigana":"い"},{"japanese":"彼女はあえて難しい道を選んだ。","romaji":"Kanojo wa aete muzukashii michi o eranda.","german":"She deliberately chose the difficult path.","furigana":"かのじょ|むずか|みち|えら"},{"japanese":"あえて言うなら、もう少し短いほうがいいと思います。","romaji":"Aete iu nara, mou sukoshi mijikai hou ga ii to omoimasu.","german":"If I had to say something, I think it would be better a little shorter.","furigana":"い|すこ|みじか|おも"}],"notes":"あえて (rarely 敢えて) means \"deliberately, on purpose\": you do something although it is unnecessary, difficult or unexpected: あえて難しい道を選ぶ, あえて何も言わない. The phrase あえて言うなら／あえて言えば introduces an answer one gives reluctantly: \"if I had to say something\". With a negative it means \"not necessarily, not especially\" (あえて〜する必要はない). Unlike わざと (\"on purpose\", often with ill intent), あえて sounds considered and bold.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ところで明日は暇ですか。","romaji":"Tokoro de ashita wa hima desu ka.","german":"By the way, are you free tomorrow?","furigana":"あした|ひま"},{"japanese":"ところで、この前話していた旅行はどうなった？","romaji":"Tokoro de, kono mae hanashite ita ryokou wa dou natta?","german":"By the way, what happened with that trip you were talking about the other day?","furigana":"まえはな|りょこう"},{"japanese":"今から急いだところで、もう間に合わないよ。","romaji":"Ima kara isoida tokoro de, mou ma ni awanai yo.","german":"Even if we hurry now, we won't make it anymore.","furigana":"いま|いそ|ま|あ"}],"notes":"ところで introduces a change of topic at the beginning of a sentence: \"by the way, speaking of which\". It signals that the new topic is unrelated to what came before; そういえば (\"that reminds me\"), by contrast, picks up on something that was said, and さて (\"well then\") sounds more like a transition in speeches. After the た-form, ところで has a different meaning: 〜たところで \"even if ..., (it is no use)\", e.g. 今さら謝ったところで遅い.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 40"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"差し当たりこれで十分です。","romaji":"Sashiatari kore de jūbun desu.","german":"This is enough for now.","furigana":"さ|あ|じゅうぶん"},{"japanese":"さしあたり必要なものだけ買っておこう。","romaji":"Sashiatari hitsuyou na mono dake katte okou.","german":"Let's just buy what we need for now.","furigana":"ひつよう|か"},{"japanese":"新しい仕事が見つかるまで、さしあたって貯金で生活します。","romaji":"Atarashii shigoto ga mitsukaru made, sashiatatte chokin de seikatsu shimasu.","german":"Until I find a new job, I will live off my savings for the time being.","furigana":"あたら|しごと|み|ちょきん|せいかつ"}],"notes":"差し当たり (mostly written in kana as さしあたり) means \"for the time being, for now, to begin with\": one adopts a provisional solution until something final comes up. In spoken language the variant さしあたって is just as common. Close in meaning are とりあえず (more casual, very everyday) and 当面 (more formal, \"for the foreseeable future\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"精一杯頑張ります。","romaji":"Seiippai ganbarimasu.","german":"I will do my best.","furigana":"せいいっぱいがんば"},{"japanese":"毎月の生活で精一杯で、貯金なんてできません。","romaji":"Maitsuki no seikatsu de seiippai de, chokin nante dekimasen.","german":"I can barely make ends meet each month - saving is out of the question.","furigana":"まいつき|せいかつ|せいいっぱい|ちょきん"},{"japanese":"緊張して、「はい」と答えるのが精一杯だった。","romaji":"Kinchou shite, \"hai\" to kotaeru no ga seiippai datta.","german":"I was so nervous that all I could do was answer \"yes\".","furigana":"きんちょう|こた|せいいっぱい"}],"notes":"精一杯 means \"with all one's strength, as well as one can\". As an adverb it stands before verbs like 頑張る, 生きる or やる; 精一杯頑張ります is a typical formula for introductions and promises. As a noun, 〜のが精一杯／〜で精一杯 expresses that one can just manage that much and no more: 生活するだけで精一杯. It is often also written 精いっぱい.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一生懸命に日本語を勉強しています。","romaji":"Isshō kenmei ni nihongo o benkyō shite imasu.","german":"I am studying Japanese with all my might.","furigana":"いっしょうけんめい|にほんご|べんきょう"},{"japanese":"一生懸命に走ったが、間に合わなかった。","romaji":"Isshō kenmei ni hashitta ga, ma ni awanakatta.","german":"I ran as hard as I could, but I did not make it in time.","furigana":"いっしょうけんめい|はし|ま|あ"},{"japanese":"一生懸命働いたお金で車を買いました。","romaji":"Isshō kenmei hataraita okane de kuruma o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a car with money I earned by working hard.","furigana":"いっしょうけんめいはたら|かね|くるま|か"}],"notes":"Literally: 一生 \"one's whole life\" and 懸命 \"to risk one's life\"; originally it was 一所懸命, that is, defending one's own fief at the risk of one's life. Today it simply means, in an everyday way, \"with all one's might, to the best of one's ability\". It is used as an adverb before verbs (一生懸命(に)働く), as a な-adjective before nouns (一生懸命な人) or predicatively (一生懸命だ), and goes well with 頑張る. The register is neutral and it is very common.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 37"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お互い様ですから。","romaji":"Otagaisama desu kara.","german":"It is mutual.","furigana":"たが|さま"},{"japanese":"「手伝ってくれてありがとう。」「困った時はお互い様だよ。」","romaji":"\"Tetsudatte kurete arigatou.\" \"Komatta toki wa otagaisama da yo.\"","german":"\"Thank you for helping me.\" \"We help each other when in trouble.\"","furigana":"てつだ|こま|とき|たが|さま"},{"japanese":"忙しいのはお互い様ですから、気にしないでください。","romaji":"Isogashii no wa otagaisama desu kara, ki ni shinaide kudasai.","german":"We are both busy, so please don't worry about it.","furigana":"いそが|たが|さま|き"}],"notes":"お互い様 is said when both sides are in the same situation or do each other favors - it takes away the other person's feeling of owing something. Typical is 困った時はお互い様 \"when in need, we simply help each other\" as a reply to thanks or an apology, and 〜のはお互い様 \"that goes for both of us\". It is friendly and very common among neighbors and coworkers; お互いに alone, by contrast, is just the adverb \"mutually\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ご苦労様でした。","romaji":"Gokurōsama deshita.","german":"Thank you for your efforts.","furigana":"くろうさま"},{"japanese":"宅配便の人に「暑い中、ご苦労様です」と言った。","romaji":"Takuhaibin no hito ni \"Atsui naka, gokurousama desu\" to itta.","german":"I said to the delivery person, \"Thank you for your trouble in this heat.\"","furigana":"たくはいびん|ひと|あつ|なか|くろうさま|い"},{"japanese":"部長は残業した部下に「遅くまでご苦労様」と声をかけた。","romaji":"Buchou wa zangyou shita buka ni \"Osoku made gokurousama\" to koe o kaketa.","german":"The department head said to the staff who had worked overtime, \"Thank you for staying so late.\"","furigana":"ぶちょう|ざんぎょう|ぶか|おそ|くろうさま|こえ"}],"notes":"ご苦労様（です／でした） thanks someone for their work or effort. Traditionally it is said by a person of higher rank to subordinates, or to service providers such as delivery people; toward superiors it is considered impolite, where one says お疲れ様です instead. With でした it is used after finished work, with です during ongoing work or on arrival. Also written ご苦労さま.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大変申し訳ございません。","romaji":"Taihen mōshiwake gozaimasen.","german":"I am terribly sorry.","furigana":"たいへんもう|わけ"},{"japanese":"お待たせして申し訳ございません。","romaji":"Omatase shite moushiwake gozaimasen.","german":"I am sorry to have kept you waiting.","furigana":"ま|もう|わけ"},{"japanese":"申し訳ございませんが、本日の受付は終了いたしました。","romaji":"Moushiwake gozaimasen ga, honjitsu no uketsuke wa shuuryou itashimashita.","german":"We are sorry, but reception has already closed for today.","furigana":"もう|わけ|ほんじつ|うけつけ|しゅうりょう"}],"notes":"申し訳ございません is a very polite apology, more polite than 申し訳ありません and much more formal than すみません or ごめんなさい. It is heard mainly in customer service, in business e-mails and for serious mistakes, often intensified by 大変 or 誠に. With が it introduces a polite refusal (申し訳ございませんが、…); sometimes it also expresses thanks for the trouble one causes someone (お忙しいところ申し訳ございません).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"恐れ入りますが、お名前を教えていただけますか。","romaji":"Osore irimasu ga, onamae o oshiete itadakemasu ka.","german":"Excuse me, but could you tell me your name?","furigana":"おそ|い|なまえ|おし"},{"japanese":"「お荷物、お持ちしましょうか。」「恐れ入ります。」","romaji":"\"Onimotsu, omochi shimashou ka.\" \"Osore irimasu.\"","german":"\"Shall I carry your luggage?\" \"That is very kind of you, thank you.\"","furigana":"にもつ|も|おそ|い"},{"japanese":"恐れ入りますが、少々お待ちください。","romaji":"Osore irimasu ga, shoushou omachi kudasai.","german":"Excuse me, but please wait a moment.","furigana":"おそ|い|しょうしょう|ま"}],"notes":"恐れ入ります is a humble, polite formula with three functions: before a request or question as 恐れ入りますが \"excuse me, but ...\", as thanks for kindness (\"that is very kind of you\"), and as an apology for causing trouble. It is typical of business life, customer service and phone calls and sounds much more polite than すみませんが. After 恐れ入りますが there usually follows an equally polite request such as 〜ていただけますか.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"承知しました。すぐに対応します。","romaji":"Shōchi shimashita. Sugu ni taiō shimasu.","german":"Understood. I will deal with it right away.","furigana":"しょうち|たいおう"},{"japanese":"「この書類を明日までにまとめてください。」「承知しました。」","romaji":"Kono shorui o ashita made ni matomete kudasai. – Shouchi shimashita.","german":"\"Please put these documents together by tomorrow.\" \"Certainly, I will take care of it.\"","furigana":"しょるい|あした|しょうち"},{"japanese":"「では、三時に伺います。」「承知しました。お待ちしております。」","romaji":"Dewa, sanji ni ukagaimasu. – Shouchi shimashita. Omachi shite orimasu.","german":"\"Then I will come by at three o'clock.\" \"Certainly. We will be expecting you.\"","furigana":"さんじ|うかが|しょうち|ま"}],"notes":"承知しました is the polite past form of 承知する \"to take note of, to agree\" and the standard reply at work when one accepts an assignment or a piece of information. Toward superiors and customers it replaces the too casual わかりました; 了解しました is often considered inappropriate toward those of higher rank. Even humbler is 承知いたしました; in service and hospitality one mostly hears かしこまりました. Related: 承知しております \"I am aware of that\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"かしこまりました。少々お待ちください。","romaji":"Kashikomarimashita. Shōshō omachi kudasai.","german":"Certainly. Please wait a moment.","furigana":"しょうしょう|ま"},{"japanese":"「コーヒーを二つお願いします。」「かしこまりました。」","romaji":"\"Koohii o futatsu onegai shimasu.\" \"Kashikomarimashita.\"","german":"\"Two coffees, please.\" \"Certainly.\"","furigana":"ふた|ねが"},{"japanese":"部長、かしこまりました。明日までに準備します。","romaji":"Buchou, kashikomarimashita. Ashita made ni junbi shimasu.","german":"Understood, sir. I will have it ready by tomorrow.","furigana":"ぶちょう|あした|じゅんび"}],"notes":"かしこまりました is the very polite, humble confirmation \"Certainly, it will be done\", typical for staff toward customers and, at work, toward superiors. It is more polite than わかりました and expresses not only understanding but also readiness to comply with the request. Among friends it would be out of place; there one says わかった or 了解. The kanji spelling 畏まりました is rare.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 20"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ご遠慮なくどうぞ。","romaji":"Goenryo naku dōzo.","german":"Please, go ahead and help yourself.","furigana":"えんりょ"},{"japanese":"ご質問があれば、ご遠慮なくお聞きください。","romaji":"Goshitsumon ga areba, goenryo naku okiki kudasai.","german":"If you have any questions, please feel free to ask.","furigana":"しつもん|えんりょ|き"},{"japanese":"何か必要なものがあったら、ご遠慮なくお申し付けください。","romaji":"Nanika hitsuyou na mono ga attara, goenryo naku omoushitsuke kudasai.","german":"If you need anything, please don't hesitate to let us know.","furigana":"なに|ひつよう|えんりょ|もう|つ"}],"notes":"ご遠慮なく is the polite form of 遠慮なく \"without reserve, freely\" and tells the other person not to be shy: when offering food, when inviting questions or requests. Typical: ご遠慮なくどうぞ, ご遠慮なくお申し付けください, ご遠慮なくお召し上がりください. Not to be confused with ご遠慮ください, a polite prohibition (\"please refrain from\", e.g. 喫煙はご遠慮ください). About oneself one says it without ご: では、遠慮なくいただきます.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"気にしないでください。","romaji":"Ki ni shinaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't worry about it.","furigana":"き"},{"japanese":"「遅れてごめん。」「気にしないで。私も今来たところだから。」","romaji":"Okurete gomen. – Ki ni shinaide. Watashi mo ima kita tokoro dakara.","german":"\"Sorry I'm late.\" \"Don't worry about it. I just got here too.\"","furigana":"おく|き|わたし|いまき"},{"japanese":"失敗したことは気にしないで、次に頑張ろう。","romaji":"Shippai shita koto wa ki ni shinaide, tsugi ni ganbarou.","german":"Don't dwell on the mistake - let's do our best next time.","furigana":"しっぱい|き|つぎ|がんば"}],"notes":"気にしないで is the negative て-form of 気にする \"to take something to heart, to worry about\" and reassures someone who is apologizing or is upset about a mistake. Among friends 気にしないで(ね) is enough, politely 気にしないでください, very politely お気になさらないでください. 気にしないで usually refers to something that has already happened; 心配しないで comforts more with worries about the future. Similarly casual: 大丈夫だよ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"資料を拝見しました。","romaji":"Shiryō o haiken shimashita.","german":"I have looked at the materials.","furigana":"しりょう|はいけん"},{"japanese":"切符を拝見いたします。","romaji":"Kippu o haiken itashimasu.","german":"Tickets, please.","furigana":"きっぷ|はいけん"},{"japanese":"ちょっとそのお写真を拝見してもよろしいですか。","romaji":"Chotto sono oshashin o haiken shite mo yoroshii desu ka.","german":"Would you mind if I take a quick look at that photo?","furigana":"しゃしん|はいけん"}],"notes":"拝見する is the humble form (謙譲語) of 見る and is used when one looks at or reads something that belongs to a person of higher status: 資料を拝見する, お手紙を拝見しました. The conductor says 切符を拝見します during a ticket check. For the actions of the other person it is wrong; there the honorific ご覧になる (ご覧ください) is used. A common request is 拝見してもよろしいですか \"May I take a look?\"","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お名刺を頂戴できますか。","romaji":"Omeishi o chōdai dekimasu ka.","german":"May I have your business card?","furigana":"めいし|ちょうだい"},{"japanese":"皆様から多くのご意見を頂戴しました。","romaji":"Minasama kara ooku no goiken o choudai shimashita.","german":"We have received many opinions from all of you.","furigana":"みなさま|おお|いけん|ちょうだい"},{"japanese":"お母さん、そのお菓子ちょうだい。","romaji":"Okaasan, sono okashi choudai.","german":"Mom, give me that snack.","furigana":"かあ|かし"}],"notes":"As a する-verb, 頂戴する is the humble form of もらう as well as of 食べる/飲む: 名刺を頂戴する, ご意見を頂戴する, お電話を頂戴する. It sounds slightly more formal than いただく. There is also the familiar ちょうだい (mostly in kana) as a request \"give me / please\": それちょうだい, 見せてちょうだい - typical of children, parents and rather feminine speech, inappropriate toward superiors.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先生がおっしゃいました。","romaji":"Sensei ga osshaimashita.","german":"The teacher said so.","furigana":"せんせい"},{"japanese":"すみません、もう一度おっしゃってください。","romaji":"Sumimasen, mou ichido osshatte kudasai.","german":"Excuse me, could you please say that once more?","furigana":"いちど"},{"japanese":"おっしゃる通りです。","romaji":"Ossharu toori desu.","german":"You are quite right.","furigana":"とお"}],"notes":"仰る is the honorific verb (尊敬語) for \"to say\" and is used only for statements by other, higher-status people; for oneself one says humble 申す. It is a special Godan verb: the ます-form is おっしゃいます (not おっしゃります). Common are 何とおっしゃいましたか \"What did you say?\" and おっしゃる通りです \"You are quite right\". It is almost always written in kana, おっしゃる; the kanji forms 仰る and 仰っしゃる are rare.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先生が教えて下さいました。","romaji":"Sensei ga oshiete kudasaimashita.","german":"The teacher taught me.","furigana":"せんせい|おし|くだ"},{"japanese":"部長が誕生日に花をくださいました。","romaji":"Buchou ga tanjoubi ni hana o kudasaimashita.","german":"My department head gave me flowers for my birthday.","furigana":"ぶちょう|たんじょうび|はな"},{"japanese":"遠くから来てくださって、ありがとうございます。","romaji":"Tooku kara kite kudasatte, arigatou gozaimasu.","german":"Thank you for coming from so far away.","furigana":"とお|き"}],"notes":"くださる is the honorific form (尊敬語) of くれる: a person of higher status gives something to the speaker or their side. As an auxiliary verb, ～てくださる expresses gratitude for a favor: 先生が教えてくださった. The ます-form is irregular (くださいます), and the request ください is actually its imperative. It is mostly written in kana, especially as an auxiliary verb; the kanji spelling 下さる is rarer. The counterpart from the recipient's point of view is humble いただく.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 19"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お目にかかれて光栄です。","romaji":"Ome ni kakarete kōei desu.","german":"It is an honor to meet you.","furigana":"め|こうえい"},{"japanese":"来週、社長にお目にかかる予定です。","romaji":"Raishuu, shachou ni ome ni kakaru yotei desu.","german":"I am scheduled to meet the company president next week.","furigana":"らいしゅう|しゃちょう|め|よてい"},{"japanese":"初めてお目にかかります。山田と申します。","romaji":"Hajimete ome ni kakarimasu. Yamada to moushimasu.","german":"Pleased to meet you. My name is Yamada.","furigana":"はじ|め|やまだ|もう"}],"notes":"お目にかかる is the humble form (謙譲語) of 会う: one places oneself below the person one meets; that person is marked with に - 先生にお目にかかる. Fixed formulas: 初めてお目にかかります, お目にかかれてうれしいです/光栄です, またお目にかかれるのを楽しみにしております. Somewhat less formal is お会いする. For the other person's meeting, never use お目にかかる, but お会いになる. Not to be confused with お目にかける \"to show (something)\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お手数をおかけします。","romaji":"Otesū o okake shimasu.","german":"I am sorry to trouble you.","furigana":"てすう"},{"japanese":"お手数ですが、こちらの用紙にご記入ください。","romaji":"Otesuu desu ga, kochira no youshi ni gokinyuu kudasai.","german":"Sorry for the trouble, but please fill out this form.","furigana":"てすう|ようし|きにゅう"},{"japanese":"先日はお手数をおかけしました。","romaji":"Senjitsu wa otesuu o okake shimashita.","german":"Thank you for the trouble you took the other day.","furigana":"せんじつ|てすう"}],"notes":"お手数 \"trouble, bother\" always means the effort one causes OTHERS and appears almost only in fixed politeness formulas: お手数をおかけします (apology or thanks in advance), お手数ですが… (introducing a request), お手数をおかけしました (thanks afterwards). The standard reading is おてすう; おてかず also occurs. ご迷惑をおかけします sounds similar but stresses real inconvenience rather than just extra work.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ご迷惑をおかけしました。","romaji":"Gomeiwaku o okake shimashita.","german":"I apologize for the inconvenience.","furigana":"めいわく"},{"japanese":"他のお客様のご迷惑にならないよう、携帯電話はマナーモードにしてください。","romaji":"Hoka no okyakusama no gomeiwaku ni naranai you, keitai denwa wa manaa moodo ni shite kudasai.","german":"Please set your mobile phone to silent mode so as not to disturb other customers.","furigana":"ほか|きゃくさま|めいわく|けいたいでんわ"},{"japanese":"ご迷惑でなければ、駅まで送りましょうか。","romaji":"Gomeiwaku de nakereba, eki made okurimashou ka.","german":"If it's no trouble, shall I take you to the station?","furigana":"めいわく|えき|おく"}],"notes":"ご迷惑 is the polite 迷惑 \"nuisance, inconvenience\" and refers to the burden placed on others. Formulas: ご迷惑をおかけしました (apology), 〜のご迷惑になる \"to disturb someone\" (typical in announcements), ご迷惑でなければ \"if it is not inconvenient for you\". Without ご, 迷惑 is also a な-adjective: 迷惑な人 \"annoying person\", 迷惑メール \"spam\". お手数 is milder and means only extra effort.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ご指導よろしくお願いします。","romaji":"Goshidō yoroshiku onegai shimasu.","german":"I look forward to your guidance.","furigana":"しどう|ねが"},{"japanese":"先生のご指導のおかげで、試験に合格できました。","romaji":"Sensei no goshidou no okage de, shiken ni goukaku dekimashita.","german":"Thanks to my teacher's guidance, I was able to pass the exam.","furigana":"せんせい|しどう|しけん|ごうかく"},{"japanese":"部長から厳しいご指導をいただきました。","romaji":"Buchou kara kibishii goshidou o itadakimashita.","german":"I received strict guidance from the department manager.","furigana":"ぶちょう|きび|しどう"}],"notes":"ご指導 is the respectful form of 指導 \"guidance, supervision, coaching\" and refers to instruction by teachers, superiors or trainers. Fixed phrases: ご指導よろしくお願いします (when starting a job or at the beginning of a course), ご指導いただく, ご指導のおかげで. In speeches one hears the stiff formula ご指導ご鞭撻のほど…. Whoever gives the guidance says it without ご: 新人を指導する. Unlike 説明 or 案内, it is about longer-term instruction.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ご検討のほどよろしくお願いします。","romaji":"Gokentō no hodo yoroshiku onegai shimasu.","german":"We ask for your consideration.","furigana":"けんとう|ねが"},{"japanese":"前向きにご検討いただけないでしょうか。","romaji":"Maemuki ni gokentou itadakenai deshou ka.","german":"Could you please give it favorable consideration?","furigana":"まえむ|けんとう"},{"japanese":"料金プランについて、ご検討いただけましたか。","romaji":"Ryoukin puran ni tsuite, gokentou itadakemashita ka.","german":"Have you had a chance to consider the pricing plans?","furigana":"りょうきん|けんとう"}],"notes":"ご検討 is the polite 検討 \"examination, consideration\" and refers to the weighing up by customers or business partners. Typical in business e-mails: ご検討のほどよろしくお願いいたします, ご検討いただけますと幸いです, 前向きにご検討ください. One's own consideration is called without ご: 検討いたします. Caution: 検討します as a reply is often a polite refusal in Japanese.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ご了承ください。","romaji":"Goryōshō kudasai.","german":"Please bear with us.","furigana":"りょうしょう"},{"japanese":"予告なく変更する場合がありますので、あらかじめご了承ください。","romaji":"Yokoku naku henkou suru baai ga arimasu node, arakajime goryoushou kudasai.","german":"Please note in advance that changes may be made without notice.","furigana":"よこく|へんこう|ばあい|りょうしょう"},{"japanese":"お客様のご了承をいただいてから、作業を始めます。","romaji":"Okyakusama no goryoushou o itadaite kara, sagyou o hajimemasu.","german":"We will begin the work after we have received the customer's consent.","furigana":"きゃくさま|りょうしょう|さぎょう|はじ"}],"notes":"ご了承 means \"acknowledgment and acceptance\" and appears almost always in one-sided notices: one announces a restriction and asks that it be accepted - ご了承ください, あらかじめご了承ください (on websites, signs, in announcements). It is more a polite notice than a real request for permission. Softer is ご理解ください \"please understand\". As a verb: 了承する \"to consent\", ご了承いただく.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ご確認をお願いします。","romaji":"Gokakunin o onegai shimasu.","german":"Please check it.","furigana":"かくにん|ねが"},{"japanese":"添付ファイルをご確認ください。","romaji":"Tenpu fairu o gokakunin kudasai.","german":"Please check the attached file.","furigana":"てんぷ|かくにん"},{"japanese":"お降りの際は、お忘れ物のないようご確認ください。","romaji":"Ori no sai wa, owasuremono no nai you gokakunin kudasai.","german":"When getting off, please make sure you have not left anything behind."}],"notes":"ご確認 is the polite 確認 \"checking, verification\". Note: it mostly means \"to look over, to check\", not \"to confirm\" in the sense of giving a commitment. Typical: ご確認ください, ご確認をお願いします, ご確認のうえ… \"after you have checked\", ご確認いただけますか. For one's own checking there is no ご: 確認いたします, 確認させていただきます. Very common in e-mails (添付ファイルをご確認ください) and announcements.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ご連絡お待ちしております。","romaji":"Gorenraku omachi shite orimasu.","german":"I look forward to hearing from you.","furigana":"れんらく|ま"},{"japanese":"遅れる場合は、ご連絡ください。","romaji":"Okureru baai wa, gorenraku kudasai.","german":"If you are going to be late, please let us know.","furigana":"おく|ばあい|れんらく"},{"japanese":"後ほどこちらからご連絡いたします。","romaji":"Nochihodo kochira kara gorenraku itashimasu.","german":"We will contact you later.","furigana":"のち|れんらく"}],"notes":"ご連絡 is the polite 連絡 \"contact, notification\". A peculiarity: ご is used both for the other person's action (ご連絡ください, ご連絡お待ちしております) and in the humble pattern ご〜いたします for one's own: こちらからご連絡いたします. The person notified is marked with に: お客様にご連絡する. 連絡 includes calls, e-mails and messages; 連絡先 is the contact address.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ご報告いたします。","romaji":"Gohōkoku itashimasu.","german":"I will report to you.","furigana":"ほうこく"},{"japanese":"皆様にうれしいご報告があります。","romaji":"Minasama ni ureshii gohoukoku ga arimasu.","german":"We have some happy news for all of you.","furigana":"みなさま|ほうこく"},{"japanese":"会議の結果は、明日部長にご報告します。","romaji":"Kaigi no kekka wa, ashita buchou ni gohoukoku shimasu.","german":"I will report the result of the meeting to the department manager tomorrow.","furigana":"かいぎ|けっか|あしたぶちょう|ほうこく"}],"notes":"ご報告 is the polite 報告 \"report, notification\". Since one usually reports to a superior oneself, it typically appears in the humble pattern ご報告いたします/ご報告します, with the recipient marked by に. In speeches and on social media, ご報告があります often announces private news (marriage, a baby). A written report is more often called 報告書 or レポート.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ご案内いたします。","romaji":"Goannai itashimasu.","german":"I will guide you there.","furigana":"あんない"},{"japanese":"新商品のご案内をお送りします。","romaji":"Shinshouhin no goannai o ookuri shimasu.","german":"We will send you information about our new products.","furigana":"しんしょうひん|あんない|おく"},{"japanese":"結婚式のご案内が届きました。","romaji":"Kekkonshiki no goannai ga todokimashita.","german":"The invitation to the wedding has arrived.","furigana":"けっこんしき|あんない|とど"}],"notes":"ご案内 is the polite 案内 with two main meanings: (1) to guide someone, to show the way - お席へご案内いたします; (2) notice, information, invitation - 新商品のご案内, 説明会のご案内 (often as a subject line). In announcements, ご案内いたします introduces a piece of information. As with ご連絡, ご is used in the humble pattern ご案内いたします for one's own action too. 案内所 is an information desk.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ご注文はお決まりですか。","romaji":"Gochūmon wa okimari desu ka.","german":"Have you decided on your order?","furigana":"ちゅうもん|き"},{"japanese":"ご注文を繰り返します。","romaji":"Gochuumon o kurikaeshimasu.","german":"Let me repeat your order.","furigana":"ちゅうもん|く|かえ"},{"japanese":"ご注文の商品は明日発送いたします。","romaji":"Gochuumon no shouhin wa ashita hassou itashimasu.","german":"The item you ordered will be shipped tomorrow.","furigana":"ちゅうもん|しょうひん|あしたはっそう"}],"notes":"ご注文 is the polite 注文 \"order\" and is used by staff for the customer's order. In a restaurant: ご注文はお決まりですか, ご注文は以上でよろしいですか, ご注文を繰り返します; in online shopping: ご注文ありがとうございます. The customer himself says it without ご: 注文をお願いします, 注文してもいいですか. A reservation, by contrast, is 予約.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"公共施設を利用します。","romaji":"Kōkyō shisetsu o riyō shimasu.","german":"I use public facilities.","furigana":"こうきょうしせつ|りよう"},{"japanese":"このホテルにはプールやジムなどの施設があります。","romaji":"Kono hoteru ni wa puuru ya jimu nado no shisetsu ga arimasu.","german":"This hotel has facilities such as a pool and a gym.","furigana":"しせつ"},{"japanese":"祖母は去年から介護施設で暮らしています。","romaji":"Sobo wa kyonen kara kaigo shisetsu de kurashite imasu.","german":"My grandmother has lived in a care facility since last year.","furigana":"そぼ|きょねん|かいごしせつ|く"}],"notes":"施設 denotes a facility as a building or installation with a specific purpose: 公共施設, スポーツ施設, 医療施設, 研究施設. On its own, 施設 can also mean a home (children's home or care home): 施設で育つ \"to grow up in an institution\". Distinction: 設備 is the technical equipment inside a building (air conditioning, piping), 施設 the facility as a whole. As a する-verb it is hardly used.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"設備が整っています。","romaji":"Setsubi ga totonotte imasu.","german":"The facilities are complete.","furigana":"せつび|ととの"},{"japanese":"この工場は古い設備を新しくしました。","romaji":"Kono koujou wa furui setsubi o atarashiku shimashita.","german":"This factory has replaced its old equipment.","furigana":"こうじょう|ふる|せつび|あたら"},{"japanese":"設備の故障で、電車が止まっています。","romaji":"Setsubi no koshou de, densha ga tomatte imasu.","german":"Because of an equipment failure, the trains have stopped.","furigana":"せつび|こしょう|でんしゃ|と"}],"notes":"設備 is permanently installed technical equipment: heating, piping, machines, safety systems. Collocations: 設備が整う \"to be well equipped\", 設備投資 \"investment in equipment\", 設備の点検/故障. Distinctions: 施設 is the facility itself (building, complex), 設備 what is installed in it; 装置 is a single device, 備品 movable inventory such as furniture or office supplies.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 35"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"少し譲歩しましょう。","romaji":"Sukoshi jōho shimashō.","german":"Let's give a little.","furigana":"すこ|じょうほ"},{"japanese":"交渉で相手に大きく譲歩しました。","romaji":"Koushou de aite ni ookiku jouho shimashita.","german":"In the negotiations, we made major concessions to the other side.","furigana":"こうしょう|あいて|おお|じょうほ"},{"japanese":"これ以上の譲歩はできません。","romaji":"Kore ijou no jouho wa dekimasen.","german":"We cannot make any further concessions.","furigana":"いじょう|じょうほ"}],"notes":"譲歩 means \"concession, giving way\" in negotiations and conflicts; you give way to someone with に: 相手に譲歩する. Typical: お互いに譲歩する, 譲歩を引き出す \"to extract concessions\", 一歩も譲歩しない. In everyday speech one says 譲る (to give up a seat or way); 妥協 \"compromise\" often carries the flavor of settling for less. In grammar, 譲歩 also denotes concessive clauses (〜ても).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"貿易摩擦が起きています。","romaji":"Bōeki masatsu ga okiteimasu.","german":"Trade friction is occurring.","furigana":"ぼうえきまさつ|お"},{"japanese":"手をこすると、摩擦で温かくなります。","romaji":"Te o kosuru to, masatsu de atatakaku narimasu.","german":"When you rub your hands, they become warm through friction.","furigana":"て|まさつ|あたた"},{"japanese":"部署の間で摩擦があるようだ。","romaji":"Busho no aida de masatsu ga aru you da.","german":"There seems to be friction between the departments.","furigana":"ぶしょ|あいだ|まさつ"}],"notes":"摩擦 literally means physical \"friction\" (摩擦が大きい, 摩擦熱) and figuratively \"frictions, tensions\" between people, groups or states: 貿易摩擦, 〜との間に摩擦が起きる/生じる. As a する-verb (\"to rub\") it is rare; in everyday speech one says こする. 対立 \"confrontation\" is stronger; 摩擦 rather suggests smaller, ongoing tensions.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"和解に至りました。","romaji":"Wakai ni itarimashita.","german":"We reached a reconciliation.","furigana":"わかい|いた"},{"japanese":"長年仲が悪かった兄弟が、ついに和解した。","romaji":"Naganen naka ga warukatta kyoudai ga, tsui ni wakai shita.","german":"The brothers, who had been on bad terms for years, finally reconciled.","furigana":"ながねんなか|わる|きょうだい|わかい"},{"japanese":"裁判は和解で終わりました。","romaji":"Saiban wa wakai de owarimashita.","german":"The lawsuit ended in a settlement.","furigana":"さいばん|わかい|お"}],"notes":"和解 is reconciliation or an amicable agreement after a serious dispute; the partner is marked with と: 父と和解する. Legally it means \"settlement\": 和解が成立する, 和解金. For small everyday quarrels among friends, one prefers 仲直りする - 和解 sounds more formal and weighty. Fixed phrases: 和解に至る, 和解を図る.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"業務妨害になります。","romaji":"Gyōmu bōgai ni narimasu.","german":"That constitutes obstruction of business.","furigana":"ぎょうむぼうがい"},{"japanese":"隣の工事の音で睡眠を妨害されています。","romaji":"Tonari no kouji no oto de suimin o bougai sarete imasu.","german":"The noise from the construction next door is disturbing my sleep.","furigana":"となり|こうじ|おと|すいみん|ぼうがい"},{"japanese":"電波が妨害されて、テレビが映らない。","romaji":"Denpa ga bougai sarete, terebi ga utsuranai.","german":"The signal is being jammed, so the TV has no picture.","furigana":"でんぱ|ぼうがい|うつ"}],"notes":"妨害 is the (often deliberate) obstructing or disrupting of an activity: 〜を妨害する, 〜に妨害される. Common compounds: 業務妨害 (a criminal offense), 営業妨害, 睡眠妨害, 電波妨害. It sounds harsher and more formal than the everyday word 邪魔 (\"to disturb, be in the way\": 邪魔しないで); 妨害 often carries a sense of intent or illegality.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"権利の侵害です。","romaji":"Kenri no shingai desu.","german":"It is a violation of rights.","furigana":"けんり|しんがい"},{"japanese":"他人の写真を勝手に使うと、著作権の侵害になります。","romaji":"Tanin no shashin o katte ni tsukau to, chosakuken no shingai ni narimasu.","german":"Using other people's photos without permission is copyright infringement.","furigana":"たにん|しゃしん|かって|つか|ちょさくけん|しんがい"},{"japanese":"そのアプリはプライバシーを侵害するおそれがある。","romaji":"Sono apuri wa puraibashii o shingai suru osore ga aru.","german":"That app may violate privacy.","furigana":"しんがい"}],"notes":"侵害 is the violation of rights or protected spheres: 権利を侵害する, 人権侵害, 著作権侵害, プライバシーの侵害. The word is formal and typical of law, media and politics. Distinctions: 侵略 is a military invasion, 違反 the violation of a rule (交通違反) - 侵害 always concerns the rights or interests of a person or group.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"町の繁栄を願います。","romaji":"Machi no han'ei o negaimasu.","german":"I wish the town prosperity.","furigana":"まち|はんえい|ねが"},{"japanese":"この港町は江戸時代に貿易で繁栄した。","romaji":"Kono minatomachi wa Edo jidai ni boueki de han'ei shita.","german":"This port town prospered through trade in the Edo period.","furigana":"みなとまち|えどじだい|ぼうえき|はんえい"},{"japanese":"会社の繁栄は社員の努力のおかげです。","romaji":"Kaisha no han'ei wa shain no doryoku no okage desu.","german":"The company's prosperity is thanks to the employees' efforts.","furigana":"かいしゃ|はんえい|しゃいん|どりょく"}],"notes":"繁栄 \"prosperity, flourishing\" describes the thriving of cities, countries, companies or families over a longer period; as an intransitive する-verb: 〜で繁栄する. The opposite is 衰退 \"decline\". Unlike 繁盛 (a shop \"doing well\": 店が繁盛する), 繁栄 is thought of on a larger scale and is more formal. In speeches and greetings: ますますのご繁栄をお祈りします.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"産業が衰退しています。","romaji":"Sangyō ga suitai shiteimasu.","german":"The industry is in decline.","furigana":"さんぎょう|すいたい"},{"japanese":"若い人が減って、商店街が衰退してしまった。","romaji":"Wakai hito ga hette, shoutengai ga suitai shite shimatta.","german":"As young people decreased, the shopping street went into decline.","furigana":"わか|ひと|へ|しょうてんがい|すいたい"},{"japanese":"ローマ帝国の衰退について調べています。","romaji":"Rooma teikoku no suitai ni tsuite shirabete imasu.","german":"I am researching the decline of the Roman Empire.","furigana":"ていこく|すいたい|しら"}],"notes":"衰退 \"decline, decay\" is a formal, intransitive する-verb for the slow weakening of industries, towns, cultures or states: 産業が衰退する, 地方の衰退. Opposites are 繁栄 and 発展. For waning physical strength, one says 衰える (体力が衰える), not 衰退; 衰弱 is physical exhaustion.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"技術革新が進んでいます。","romaji":"Gijutsu kakushin ga susundeimasu.","german":"Technological innovation is advancing.","furigana":"ぎじゅつかくしん|すす"},{"japanese":"この会社は革新的な製品を次々に出している。","romaji":"Kono kaisha wa kakushinteki na seihin o tsugitsugi ni dashite iru.","german":"This company is releasing one innovative product after another.","furigana":"かいしゃ|かくしんてき|せいひん|つぎつぎ|だ"},{"japanese":"古い制度を革新する必要がある。","romaji":"Furui seido o kakushin suru hitsuyou ga aru.","german":"The old system needs to be fundamentally renewed.","furigana":"ふる|せいど|かくしん|ひつよう"}],"notes":"革新 means \"innovation, fundamental renewal\" and is formal: 技術革新, 革新的な \"innovative\", and as a transitive する-verb 制度を革新する. In politics, 革新 stands for \"progressive, left-wing\" as the counterpart to 保守 \"conservative\". 改革 \"reform\" stresses rebuilding existing systems, 革新 the completely new; in everyday speech one also hears イノベーション. Not to be confused with the identically read 確信 \"conviction\" and 核心 \"core\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"儀式に参加しました。","romaji":"Gishiki ni sanka shimashita.","german":"I took part in the ceremony.","furigana":"ぎしき|さんか"},{"japanese":"結婚式は神社で伝統的な儀式に従って行われた。","romaji":"Kekkonshiki wa jinja de dentouteki na gishiki ni shitagatte okonawareta.","german":"The wedding was held at the shrine according to a traditional ritual.","furigana":"けっこんしき|じんじゃ|でんとうてき|ぎしき|したが|おこな"},{"japanese":"毎朝コーヒーを飲むのが、私にとって一日の始まりの儀式です。","romaji":"Maiasa koohii o nomu no ga, watashi ni totte ichinichi no hajimari no gishiki desu.","german":"Drinking coffee every morning is the ritual that starts my day.","furigana":"まいあさ|の|わたし|いちにち|はじ|ぎしき"}],"notes":"儀式 is a formal ceremony or rite with a fixed procedure, often religious or traditional: 儀式を行う, 儀式に参加する/出席する. For concrete celebrations, 式 usually appears in compounds (結婚式, 卒業式, 入学式); 儀式 is the more general, somewhat more abstract term. Figuratively it can also denote a personal \"ritual\"; 儀式的 means \"purely formal, ceremonial\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"古い慣習を見直します。","romaji":"Furui kanshū o minaoshimasu.","german":"We will reexamine old customs.","furigana":"ふる|かんしゅう|みなお"},{"japanese":"その地域には、お正月に餅をつく慣習が残っている。","romaji":"Sono chiiki ni wa, oshougatsu ni mochi o tsuku kanshuu ga nokotte iru.","german":"The custom of pounding rice cakes at New Year remains in that region.","furigana":"ちいき|しょうがつ|もち|かんしゅう|のこ"},{"japanese":"この業界では、口約束だけで仕事を始める慣習がある。","romaji":"Kono gyoukai de wa, kuchiyakusoku dake de shigoto o hajimeru kanshuu ga aru.","german":"In this industry, it is customary to begin work on a verbal promise alone.","furigana":"ぎょうかい|くちやくそく|しごと|はじ|かんしゅう"}],"notes":"慣習 is a socially handed-down custom, a practice in a community, region or industry: 地域の慣習, 商慣習, 慣習に従う, 慣習を守る. Not to be confused with 習慣 (the same characters reversed), which mainly means personal habits (早起きの習慣). 風習 stresses more folk customs, 慣例 an established institutional practice. 慣習 is literary and formal.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"軽蔑の目で見られました。","romaji":"Keibetsu no me de miraremashita.","german":"I was looked at with contempt.","furigana":"けいべつ|め|み"},{"japanese":"嘘をつく人を軽蔑します。","romaji":"Uso o tsuku hito o keibetsu shimasu.","german":"I despise people who lie.","furigana":"うそ|ひと|けいべつ"},{"japanese":"彼は貧しい人を軽蔑するような態度をとった。","romaji":"Kare wa mazushii hito o keibetsu suru you na taido o totta.","german":"He behaved in a way that looked down on poor people.","furigana":"かれ|まず|ひと|けいべつ|たいど"}],"notes":"軽蔑 \"contempt\" is used as a transitive する-verb: 人を軽蔑する, passive 軽蔑される. Collocations: 軽蔑の目/まなざし, 軽蔑的な態度. Somewhat weaker and more everyday are 見下す \"to look down on someone\" and, colloquially, バカにする \"to make fun of\". Opposite: 尊敬 \"respect, esteem\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"焦燥感に駆られます。","romaji":"Shōsō kan ni kararemasu.","german":"I am driven by impatience.","furigana":"しょうそうかん|か"},{"japanese":"締め切りが近づくにつれて、焦燥感が強くなった。","romaji":"Shimekiri ga chikazuku ni tsurete, shousoukan ga tsuyoku natta.","german":"As the deadline approached, my restlessness grew stronger.","furigana":"し|き|ちか|しょうそうかん|つよ"},{"japanese":"結果が出ない日々が続き、彼の顔には焦燥の色が見えた。","romaji":"Kekka ga denai hibi ga tsuzuki, kare no kao ni wa shousou no iro ga mieta.","german":"After days without results, a look of agitation showed on his face.","furigana":"けっか|で|ひび|つづ|かれ|かお|しょうそう|いろ|み"}],"notes":"焦燥 is a written-language word for tormenting impatience and inner unrest when something is not progressing or time is pressing. It usually appears as 焦燥感 \"a feeling of restlessness\" - 焦燥感に駆られる, 焦燥感を覚える - or in 焦燥の色 \"signs of nervousness\". In everyday speech one says 焦る \"to get flustered, nervous\" or イライラする \"to be irritated\" instead. It practically does not occur as a する-verb.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"憂鬱な気分です。","romaji":"Yūutsu na kibun desu.","german":"I feel gloomy.","furigana":"ゆううつ|きぶん"},{"japanese":"月曜日の朝はいつも憂鬱だ。","romaji":"Getsuyoubi no asa wa itsumo yuuutsu da.","german":"Monday mornings are always depressing.","furigana":"げつようび|あさ|ゆううつ"},{"japanese":"雨が何日も続くと、憂鬱になります。","romaji":"Ame ga nannichi mo tsuzuku to, yuuutsu ni narimasu.","german":"When it rains for days, I get gloomy.","furigana":"あめ|なんにち|つづ|ゆううつ"}],"notes":"憂鬱 describes a dejected, listless mood, often because of something coming up: 試験が憂鬱だ, 月曜日の朝は憂鬱. It is mostly used as a な-adjective (憂鬱な気分, 憂鬱になる), less often as a noun. In everyday speech it means \"to feel down, not feel like doing anything\" rather than clinical depression; that is called うつ病. Because of the difficult kanji it is often written ゆううつ or 憂うつ; casually one also says ブルーになる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"孤独を感じます。","romaji":"Kodoku o kanjimasu.","german":"I feel lonely.","furigana":"こどく|かん"},{"japanese":"一人暮らしのお年寄りの孤独が社会問題になっている。","romaji":"Hitorigurashi no otoshiyori no kodoku ga shakai mondai ni natte iru.","german":"The loneliness of elderly people living alone has become a social problem.","furigana":"ひとりぐ|としよ|こどく|しゃかいもんだい"},{"japanese":"彼は孤独な少年時代を過ごした。","romaji":"Kare wa kodoku na shounen jidai o sugoshita.","german":"He spent a lonely childhood.","furigana":"かれ|こどく|しょうねんじだい|す"}],"notes":"孤独 \"loneliness, solitude\" describes the state of having no one close to you; as a noun (孤独を感じる, 孤独に耐える) and as a な-adjective (孤独な人, 孤独な生活). Compared with 寂しい (the subjective feeling of \"feeling lonely\"), 孤独 sounds more objective and serious. Well-known compounds: 孤独感, 孤独死 (\"lonely death, dying alone unnoticed\"). 一人 means only \"alone\", with no negative coloring.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"挫折を乗り越えました。","romaji":"Zasetsu o norikoemashita.","german":"I overcame the setback.","furigana":"ざせつ|の|こ"},{"japanese":"一度も挫折したことがない人はいないでしょう。","romaji":"Ichido mo zasetsu shita koto ga nai hito wa inai deshou.","german":"There is probably no one who has never experienced a setback.","furigana":"いちど|ざせつ|ひと"},{"japanese":"ギターに挑戦したけど、三か月で挫折してしまった。","romaji":"Gitaa ni chousen shita kedo, sankagetsu de zasetsu shite shimatta.","german":"I took up the guitar, but gave up after three months.","furigana":"ちょうせん|さん|げつ|ざせつ"}],"notes":"挫折 is a \"setback\" in which one loses courage, and as an intransitive する-verb \"to fail, to give up\". The thing one fails at is marked with に: 英語の勉強に挫折する; 計画が挫折する is also possible. Typical are 挫折を味わう, 挫折を乗り越える and 挫折感 (\"feeling of failure\"). Unlike 失敗 (a single failure), 挫折 stresses giving up and the discouragement afterwards.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"潔く負けを認めました。","romaji":"Isagiyoku make o mitomemashita.","german":"He gracefully admitted defeat.","furigana":"いさぎよ|ま|みと"},{"japanese":"言い訳をしない潔い態度に感心しました。","romaji":"Iiwake o shinai isagiyoi taido ni kanshin shimashita.","german":"I was impressed by his upright attitude of making no excuses.","furigana":"い|わけ|いさぎよ|たいど|かんしん"},{"japanese":"彼は責任を取って、潔く会社を辞めた。","romaji":"Kare wa sekinin o totte, isagiyoku kaisha o yameta.","german":"He took responsibility and resigned from the company without any fuss.","furigana":"かれ|せきにん|と|いさぎよ|かいしゃ|や"}],"notes":"潔い praises someone who accepts a defeat, a mistake or responsibility without excuses, hesitation or clinging. Most often adverbial: 潔く負けを認める, 潔く謝る, 潔く諦める, 潔く身を引く. As an attribute: 潔い態度, 潔い決断. Negated, 潔くない means \"unsporting, unable to let go\". Unlike 正直 \"honest\", it is about the dignified, clean manner of accepting something.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"粘り強く交渉します。","romaji":"Nebarizuyoku kōshō shimasu.","german":"I negotiate persistently.","furigana":"ねば|づよ|こうしょう"},{"japanese":"彼女は粘り強い性格で、最後まであきらめない。","romaji":"Kanojo wa nebarizuyoi seikaku de, saigo made akiramenai.","german":"She has a persevering nature and never gives up until the end.","furigana":"かのじょ|ねば|づよ|せいかく|さいご"},{"japanese":"粘り強く練習を続けた結果、優勝できた。","romaji":"Nebarizuyoku renshuu o tsuzuketa kekka, yuushou dekita.","german":"As a result of continuing to practice persistently, I was able to win.","furigana":"ねば|づよ|れんしゅう|つづ|けっか|ゆうしょう"}],"notes":"粘り強い is composed of 粘り \"tenacity\" and 強い and praises endurance: sticking with it even when it gets difficult. Typically adverbial: 粘り強く交渉する/説得する/取り組む; as an attribute 粘り強い性格. It is positive - in contrast to しつこい \"annoyingly persistent\" and 頑固 \"stubborn\". Related: 粘る \"to hold out, not to let up\" and 根気強い \"patiently persevering\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"生意気な態度はやめなさい。","romaji":"Namaiki na taido wa yamenasai.","german":"Stop that cheeky attitude.","furigana":"なまいき|たいど"},{"japanese":"子どものくせに生意気なことを言うな。","romaji":"Kodomo no kuse ni namaiki na koto o iu na.","german":"Don't talk back like that - you're only a child!","furigana":"こ|なまいき|い"},{"japanese":"新人なのに先輩に意見したら、生意気だと思われるかな。","romaji":"Shinjin na noni senpai ni iken shitara, namaiki da to omowareru kana.","german":"I wonder if people will think I'm cheeky if I voice my opinion to my seniors as a newcomer.","furigana":"しんじん|せんぱい|いけん|なまいき|おも"}],"notes":"生意気 criticizes someone who takes too much liberty for their age or rank - typically said of children, juniors or subordinates: 生意気な子, 生意気な態度, 生意気を言う, 生意気だ. So it presupposes a hierarchy gap; about people of equal rank one rather says 偉そう \"self-important\" or 失礼. Sometimes it also sounds affectionately teasing: 生意気な弟.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"卑怯なやり方です。","romaji":"Hikyō na yarikata desu.","german":"That is an underhanded method.","furigana":"ひきょう|かた"},{"japanese":"後ろから攻撃するなんて卑怯だ。","romaji":"Ushiro kara kougeki suru nante hikyou da.","german":"Attacking from behind is cowardly!","furigana":"うし|こうげき|ひきょう"},{"japanese":"自分の失敗を人のせいにするのは卑怯だよ。","romaji":"Jibun no shippai o hito no sei ni suru no wa hikyou da yo.","german":"It's unfair to blame others for your own mistakes.","furigana":"じぶん|しっぱい|ひと|ひきょう"}],"notes":"卑怯 condemns unfair, cowardly behavior: sneaky tricks, shifting responsibility, dodging duty. Often as an indignant exclamation: 卑怯だ!. ずるい \"unfair, mean\" is more everyday and weaker and can also be jocular; 卑怯 is morally sharper. Collocations: 卑怯な手を使う, 卑怯なやり方, 卑怯者 \"coward\". Mere timidity, by contrast, is 臆病.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"豪快に笑います。","romaji":"Gōkai ni waraimasu.","german":"He laughs heartily.","furigana":"ごうかい|わら"},{"japanese":"彼はビールを豪快に飲み干した。","romaji":"Kare wa biiru o goukai ni nomihoshita.","german":"He downed the beer in one hearty gulp.","furigana":"かれ|ごうかい|の|ほ"},{"japanese":"選手が豪快なホームランを打った。","romaji":"Senshu ga goukai na hoomuran o utta.","german":"The player hit a mighty home run.","furigana":"せんしゅ|ごうかい|う"}],"notes":"豪快 describes a powerful, generous, carefree manner on a grand scale - mostly admiringly: 豪快に笑う, 豪快な性格, 豪快なホームラン, 豪快に飲む. There is something rough and unconstrained about it, but in a positive way. Distinctions: 大胆 stresses daring, 派手 a showy appearance, 豪華 the luxury and splendor of things.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"陰湿ないじめは許せません。","romaji":"Inshitsu na ijime wa yurusemasen.","german":"Insidious bullying is unforgivable.","furigana":"いんしつ|ゆる"},{"japanese":"陰で悪口を言うなんて、陰湿なやり方だ。","romaji":"Kage de warukuchi o iu nante, inshitsu na yarikata da.","german":"Talking badly about someone behind their back is an insidious way to behave.","furigana":"かげ|わるくち|い|いんしつ|かた"},{"japanese":"ネットでの陰湿な嫌がらせが問題になっている。","romaji":"Netto de no inshitsu na iyagarase ga mondai ni natte iru.","german":"Insidious harassment on the internet has become a problem.","furigana":"いんしつ|いや|もんだい"}],"notes":"陰湿 literally means \"shady and damp\", but today it is used almost only figuratively for insidious, covert malice: 陰湿ないじめ, 陰湿な嫌がらせ, 陰湿な性格. It means meanness that is practiced in secret and persistently. Open, crude violence, by contrast, is 乱暴; 意地悪 is more general and weaker. For damp, dark rooms, people in everyday speech rather say じめじめした.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"見栄を張ります。","romaji":"Mie o harimasu.","german":"He puts on airs.","furigana":"みえ|は"},{"japanese":"見栄を張って高いブランド品を買ってしまった。","romaji":"Mie o hatte takai burandohin o katte shimatta.","german":"I put on a show and bought expensive brand-name goods.","furigana":"みえ|は|たか|ひん|か"},{"japanese":"彼は見栄っ張りで、いつも自分を大きく見せようとする。","romaji":"Kare wa mieppari de, itsumo jibun o ookiku miseyou to suru.","german":"He is a show-off and always tries to make himself look bigger.","furigana":"かれ|みえ|ぱ|じぶん|おお|み"}],"notes":"見栄 is the outward appearance one wants to keep up, and the vanity behind it. It appears almost always in 見栄を張る \"to show off, to live beyond one's means\"; also 見栄っ張り \"show-off\". 自慢する, by contrast, is open boasting in words, 見栄を張る the pretending through conduct or consumption. Not to be confused with 見栄え（みばえ）\"good looks, impression\" and 見得を切る (a kabuki pose, \"to strike a pose, to act grandly\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"見識のある人です。","romaji":"Kenshiki no aru hito desu.","german":"He is a person of discernment.","furigana":"けんしき|ひと"},{"japanese":"彼は国際問題について高い見識を持っている。","romaji":"Kare wa kokusai mondai ni tsuite takai kenshiki o motte iru.","german":"He has deep insight into international affairs.","furigana":"かれ|こくさいもんだい|たか|けんしき|も"},{"japanese":"専門家の見識を疑うような発言だった。","romaji":"Senmonka no kenshiki o utagau you na hatsugen datta.","german":"The remark made one doubt the expert's judgment.","furigana":"せんもんか|けんしき|うたが|はつげん"}],"notes":"見識 is judgment based on knowledge and experience, a well-founded outlook: 見識がある, 見識が高い, 見識を持つ, 見識を疑う. The word is formal and praising. Distinctions: 知識 is mere knowledge, 意見 an opinion; 見識 is the ability to assess things correctly. Not to be confused with 面識 \"personal acquaintance\" (面識がある).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"意地悪なことを言わないで。","romaji":"Ijiwaru na koto o iwanaide.","german":"Don't say mean things.","furigana":"いじわる|い"},{"japanese":"子どものころ、兄によく意地悪をされた。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, ani ni yoku ijiwaru o sareta.","german":"When I was a child, my older brother often teased me.","furigana":"こ|あに|いじわる"},{"japanese":"彼は意地悪な質問をして、相手を困らせた。","romaji":"Kare wa ijiwaru na shitsumon o shite, aite o komaraseta.","german":"He asked a nasty question to embarrass the other person.","furigana":"かれ|いじわる|しつもん|あいて|こま"}],"notes":"意地悪 means \"mean, nasty\" and describes small, deliberate acts of spite: 意地悪な人, 意地悪を言う, 意地悪をする/される. It is common among children and siblings and is often meant teasingly: 意地悪! Heavier are いじめ \"bullying\" and 陰湿 \"insidious\". 意地 alone means \"stubbornness, pride\" (意地を張る).","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 9"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼はお人好しです。","romaji":"Kare wa ohitoyoshi desu.","german":"He is a good-natured person.","furigana":"かれ|ひとよ"},{"japanese":"お人好しだから、頼まれると断れない。","romaji":"Ohitoyoshi dakara, tanomareru to kotowarenai.","german":"Since I'm so good-natured, I can't refuse when asked.","furigana":"ひとよ|たの|ことわ"},{"japanese":"そんな話を信じるなんて、お人好しにもほどがある。","romaji":"Sonna hanashi o shinjiru nante, ohitoyoshi ni mo hodo ga aru.","german":"Believing such a story - that is naive beyond measure.","furigana":"はなし|しん|ひとよ"}],"notes":"お人好し describes someone so good-natured that he is easily taken advantage of or deceived. It is half praise, half criticism: お人好しすぎる, お人好しにもほどがある, お人好しな性格. As a noun also \"good-natured person\": 彼はお人好しだ. Purely positive are 優しい or 親切; 人がいい is similar and can also hint at \"gullible\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"八方美人では本当の友達はできない。","romaji":"Happō bijin de wa hontō no tomodachi wa dekinai.","german":"If you try to be everyone's friend, you can't make real friends.","furigana":"はっぽうびじん|ほんとう|ともだち"},{"japanese":"彼は八方美人だから信用できない。","romaji":"Kare wa happō bijin da kara shin'yō dekinai.","german":"He tries to please everybody, so he can't be trusted.","furigana":"かれ|はっぽうびじん|しんよう"},{"japanese":"八方美人と言われないように、自分の意見もはっきり言おう。","romaji":"Happō bijin to iwarenai yō ni, jibun no iken mo hakkiri iō.","german":"So that people don't call you a people-pleaser, state your own opinion clearly too.","furigana":"はっぽうびじん|い|じぶん|いけん|い"}],"notes":"Literally \"a beauty from all eight directions\", originally a flawless beauty. Today it is used almost only pejoratively: someone who is friendly to everyone and wants to please everybody, but shows no stance of his own - a \"people-pleaser\". Frames: 八方美人だ, 八方美人な人, 八方美人と言われる. Not a compliment, so use with care.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"問題が拗れました。","romaji":"Mondai ga kojiremashita.","german":"The problem got complicated.","furigana":"もんだい|こじ"},{"japanese":"話し合いがこじれて、裁判になった。","romaji":"Hanashiai ga kojirete, saiban ni natta.","german":"The talks went sour and ended up in court.","furigana":"はな|あ|さいばん"},{"japanese":"風邪がこじれて、一週間も学校を休んだ。","romaji":"Kaze ga kojirete, isshuukan mo gakkou o yasunda.","german":"My cold dragged on, and I missed school for a whole week.","furigana":"かぜ|いっしゅうかん|がっこう|やす"}],"notes":"こじれる (usually in kana; the kanji 拗れる are rare) means that something gets \"stuck, worse\" and hard to resolve: relationships and negotiations (話がこじれる, 関係がこじれる) as well as lingering illnesses (風邪がこじれる). It is intransitive with が; the transitive counterpart is こじらせる \"to aggravate, let drag on\": 風邪をこじらせる. 複雑になる is more neutral; こじれる stresses that something has become deadlocked.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"指輪を嵌めます。","romaji":"Yubiwa o hamemasu.","german":"I put on the ring.","furigana":"ゆびわ|は"},{"japanese":"窓枠に新しいガラスをはめました。","romaji":"Madowaku ni atarashii garasu o hamemashita.","german":"I fitted a new pane of glass into the window frame.","furigana":"まどわく|あたら"},{"japanese":"私は彼にはめられたんだ。","romaji":"Watashi wa kare ni hamerareta n da.","german":"He set me up!","furigana":"わたし|かれ"}],"notes":"はめる (mostly in kana, the kanji 嵌める is rare) means \"to fit or slip something snugly into or onto something\": 指輪/手袋をはめる \"to put on\", 窓枠にガラスをはめる \"to fit in\", ボタンをはめる \"to button up\". Transitive with を, the target with に; the intransitive counterpart is はまる \"to fit in, to get stuck; to become hooked on something\". Colloquially, はめる also means \"to set up, to frame\" (人をはめる, passive はめられる). For clothing in general, 着る/履く are used; はめる only for tight-fitting things such as rings or gloves.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"参加を促します。","romaji":"Sanka o unagashimasu.","german":"I urge participation.","furigana":"さんか|うなが"},{"japanese":"先生は生徒に早く帰るよう促した。","romaji":"Sensei wa seito ni hayaku kaeru you unagashita.","german":"The teacher urged the students to go home soon.","furigana":"せんせい|せいと|はや|かえ|うなが"},{"japanese":"適度な運動は消化を促す。","romaji":"Tekido na undou wa shouka o unagasu.","german":"Moderate exercise promotes digestion.","furigana":"てきど|うんどう|しょうか|うなが"}],"notes":"促す is more formal than 勧める or 言う and has two uses: (1) to urge or prompt someone to do something: 人に〜するよう促す, 注意を促す \"to call for caution\", 参加を促す; (2) to promote or speed up a process: 成長/消化/発展を促す. Transitive with を, the person addressed with に. Common in news and official texts. 勧める is gentler (\"to recommend\"), 催促する \"to press\" (e.g. for payment).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"前進を阻みます。","romaji":"Zenshin o habamimasu.","german":"It blocks progress.","furigana":"ぜんしん|はば"},{"japanese":"大雪が私たちの行く手を阻んだ。","romaji":"Ooyuki ga watashitachi no yukute o habanda.","german":"Heavy snow blocked our way.","furigana":"おおゆき|わたし|ゆ|て|はば"},{"japanese":"相手チームの連勝を阻むことができた。","romaji":"Aite chiimu no renshou o habamu koto ga dekita.","german":"We managed to stop the opposing team's winning streak.","furigana":"あいて|れんしょう|はば"}],"notes":"阻む is a literary verb for \"to prevent, stop, stand in the way\"; transitive with を: 行く手を阻む (fixed phrase), 侵入を阻む, 連勝を阻む. The subject is often an obstacle (snow, a wall) or an opponent. In everyday speech one says 止める or 邪魔する. 妨げる is similarly formal but stresses disrupting a process, 阻む rather blocking progress.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"安全を脅かします。","romaji":"Anzen o obiyakashimasu.","german":"It endangers safety.","furigana":"あんぜん|おびや"},{"japanese":"地球温暖化が多くの動物の生活を脅かしている。","romaji":"Chikyuu ondanka ga ooku no doubutsu no seikatsu o obiyakashite iru.","german":"Global warming is threatening the lives of many animals.","furigana":"ちきゅうおんだんか|おお|どうぶつ|せいかつ|おびや"},{"japanese":"新しい会社が業界トップの地位を脅かしている。","romaji":"Atarashii kaisha ga gyoukai toppu no chii o obiyakashite iru.","german":"A new company is threatening the industry leader's position.","furigana":"あたら|かいしゃ|ぎょうかい|ちい|おびや"}],"notes":"脅かす（おびやかす）means \"to threaten, endanger\" and usually takes abstract objects: 平和/安全/生活/地位を脅かす. It is formal and common in the news; passive 脅かされる. Caution: the same kanji read おどかす mean \"to startle, scare\" (後ろから脅かす). 脅す（おどす）is \"to threaten, intimidate\" a person.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"痛みに堪えます。","romaji":"Itami ni taemasu.","german":"I endure the pain.","furigana":"いた|た"},{"japanese":"彼の悪口は聞くに堪えない。","romaji":"Kare no warukuchi wa kiku ni taenai.","german":"His slander is unbearable to listen to.","furigana":"かれ|わるくち|き|た"},{"japanese":"長い入院生活に堪えて、ついに退院できた。","romaji":"Nagai nyuuin seikatsu ni taete, tsui ni taiin dekita.","german":"I endured the long hospital stay and was finally able to be discharged.","furigana":"なが|にゅういんせいかつ|た|たいいん"}],"notes":"堪える（たえる）means \"to endure, bear\"; the thing endured takes に: 痛みに堪える, 寒さに堪える. In this sense the spelling 耐える is much more common today. 堪える remains common in fixed phrases such as 聞くに堪えない/見るに堪えない \"unbearable to hear/see\" and 〜に堪える \"to be worth ...\". Caution: read こらえる, the same kanji means \"to hold back\" (涙をこらえる), and read こたえる \"to take its toll\" (寒さが堪える). The everyday word is 我慢する.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"過去を悔やんでも仕方ない。","romaji":"Kako o kuyande mo shikata nai.","german":"There is no use regretting the past.","furigana":"かこ|く|しかた"},{"japanese":"あの時もっと勉強しておけばよかったと、今でも悔やんでいる。","romaji":"Ano toki motto benkyou shite okeba yokatta to, ima demo kuyande iru.","german":"I still regret not having studied more back then.","furigana":"とき|べんきょう|いま|く"},{"japanese":"心からお悔やみ申し上げます。","romaji":"Kokoro kara okuyami moushiagemasu.","german":"Please accept my sincere condolences.","furigana":"こころ|く|もう|あ"}],"notes":"悔やむ means \"to regret, to rue\" with を: 失敗を悔やむ, 〜たことを悔やむ. It is somewhat more written than the everyday word 後悔する. 悔しい, by contrast, is the vexation over a defeat or injustice. From the meaning \"to mourn\" comes お悔やみ \"condolences\": お悔やみ申し上げます. Set phrase: 悔やんでも悔やみきれない \"one cannot regret it enough\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"別れを惜しみます。","romaji":"Wakare o oshimimasu.","german":"We part only with reluctance.","furigana":"わか|お"},{"japanese":"彼は努力を惜しまない人だ。","romaji":"Kare wa doryoku o oshimanai hito da.","german":"He is someone who spares no effort.","furigana":"かれ|どりょく|お|ひと"},{"japanese":"多くのファンが彼の引退を惜しんだ。","romaji":"Ooku no fan ga kare no intai o oshinda.","german":"Many fans lamented his retirement.","furigana":"おお|かれ|いんたい|お"}],"notes":"惜しむ has two sides: (1) to \"be reluctant to give up, to be sparing with\" something, mostly negated as praise: 努力を惜しまない \"to spare no effort\", 協力を惜しまない; (2) \"to regret, mourn\": 別れを惜しむ \"to part with reluctance\", 死を惜しむ, 名残を惜しむ. Transitive with を. Related is the adjective 惜しい \"a pity, so close\". 悔やむ, by contrast, means regret over one's own actions.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"他人の成功を妬みます。","romaji":"Tanin no seikō o netamimasu.","german":"He envies other people's success.","furigana":"たにん|せいこう|ねた"},{"japanese":"同僚に昇進を妬まれて、悪口を言われた。","romaji":"Douryou ni shoushin o netamarete, warukuchi o iwareta.","german":"My colleagues were jealous of my promotion and spoke ill of me.","furigana":"どうりょう|しょうしん|ねた|わるくち|い"},{"japanese":"人を妬んでも、自分が幸せになるわけではない。","romaji":"Hito o netande mo, jibun ga shiawase ni naru wake de wa nai.","german":"Envying others won't make you happy yourself.","furigana":"ひと|ねた|じぶん|しあわ"}],"notes":"妬む is resentful envy: you begrudge others their happiness: 人の成功を妬む, 人を妬む, passive 妬まれる. More neutral are うらやむ/うらやましい \"to envy, want to have too\" without resentment. 嫉妬する also covers jealousy in love; colloquially one says やきもちを焼く. Noun: 妬み (妬みを買う \"to draw envy\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"勉強を怠ってはいけません。","romaji":"Benkyō o okotatte wa ikemasen.","german":"You must not neglect your studies.","furigana":"べんきょう|おこた"},{"japanese":"確認を怠ったせいで、大きなミスが起きた。","romaji":"Kakunin o okotatta sei de, ookina misu ga okita.","german":"Because the check was neglected, a major mistake occurred.","furigana":"かくにん|おこた|おお|お"},{"japanese":"運転中は周りへの注意を怠らないようにしましょう。","romaji":"Untenchuu wa mawari e no chuui o okotaranai you ni shimashou.","german":"While driving, let's make sure never to stop paying attention to our surroundings.","furigana":"うんてんちゅう|まわ|ちゅうい|おこた"}],"notes":"怠る is transitive and takes を: 努力を怠る, 注意を怠る, 確認を怠る, 準備を怠る - one \"fails to\" do a duty or care that would actually be necessary. It sounds written to formal (reports, admonitions, safety notices). Related is 怠ける (same kanji, reading なまける): that means \"to be lazy\" in general, while 怠る emphasizes omitting a specific task; colloquially one says サボる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"勉強に励みます。","romaji":"Benkyō ni hagemimasu.","german":"I work hard at my studies.","furigana":"べんきょう|はげ"},{"japanese":"選手たちは大会に向けて練習に励んでいる。","romaji":"Senshutachi wa taikai ni mukete renshuu ni hagende iru.","german":"The athletes are training hard for the tournament.","furigana":"せんしゅ|たいかい|む|れんしゅう|はげ"},{"japanese":"父は定年まで仕事に励みました。","romaji":"Chichi wa teinen made shigoto ni hagemimashita.","german":"My father worked diligently until retirement.","furigana":"ちち|ていねん|しごと|はげ"}],"notes":"励む is intransitive; what one strives at takes に: 勉強に励む, 練習に励む, 仕事に励む, 研究に励む. It sounds more elevated than the everyday 頑張る and likes to appear in speeches, letters and reports (日々練習に励んでおります). Not to be confused with 励ます (transitive, \"to encourage someone\"); the noun 励み means \"incentive\": 〜が励みになる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"現地に赴きます。","romaji":"Genchi ni omomukimasu.","german":"I will go to the site.","furigana":"げんち|おもむ"},{"japanese":"社長は自ら工場へ赴いて、説明を聞いた。","romaji":"Shachou wa mizukara koujou e omomuite, setsumei o kiita.","german":"The company president went to the factory himself and listened to the explanation.","furigana":"しゃちょう|みずか|こうじょう|おもむ|せつめい|き"},{"japanese":"休日は気の赴くままに町を歩きます。","romaji":"Kyuujitsu wa ki no omomuku mama ni machi o arukimasu.","german":"On days off, I wander around town as the mood takes me.","furigana":"きゅうじつ|き|おもむ|まち|ある"}],"notes":"赴く is an elevated, literary word for \"to go somewhere (on duty, with a task)\"; the destination takes に or へ: 現地に赴く, 現場へ赴く, 任地に赴く. In everyday speech one simply says 行く. Very common is the fixed phrase 気の赴くままに \"entirely as the mood takes one\", in which 赴く means \"to give oneself over to an inclination\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"肌が潤います。","romaji":"Hada ga uruoimasu.","german":"My skin gets moisturized.","furigana":"はだ|うるお"},{"japanese":"観光客が増えて、町が潤っている。","romaji":"Kankoukyaku ga fuete, machi ga uruotte iru.","german":"With more tourists, the town is prospering.","furigana":"かんこうきゃく|ふ|まち|うるお"},{"japanese":"久しぶりの雨で畑が潤った。","romaji":"Hisashiburi no ame de hatake ga uruotta.","german":"The first rain in a long time moistened the fields.","furigana":"ひさ|あめ|はたけ|うるお"}],"notes":"潤う is intransitive (〜が潤う); the transitive counterpart is 潤す: のどを潤す \"to moisten the throat\". Literally it means \"to become moist\" (肌が潤う, 雨で大地が潤う), typical of cosmetics ads. Figuratively it means \"to flourish, benefit\" (観光で町が潤う, 懐が潤う \"to have more money in one's pocket\") or \"to be spiritually refreshed\" (心が潤う). The noun 潤い appears in 潤いのある肌／生活.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"事実が歪んで伝えられました。","romaji":"Jijitsu ga yugande tsutaeraremashita.","german":"The facts were reported in a distorted way.","furigana":"じじつ|ゆが|つた"},{"japanese":"地震のあと、ドアの枠が少し歪んでしまった。","romaji":"Jishin no ato, doa no waku ga sukoshi yugande shimatta.","german":"After the earthquake, the door frame got slightly warped.","furigana":"じしん|わく|すこ|ゆが"},{"japanese":"痛みで彼の顔が歪んだ。","romaji":"Itami de kare no kao ga yuganda.","german":"His face twisted with pain.","furigana":"いた|かれ|かお|ゆが"}],"notes":"歪む is intransitive: something warps or bends out of its proper shape unintentionally (枠が歪む, 眼鏡が歪む, 痛みで顔が歪む). The transitive counterpart is the ichidan verb 歪める: 事実を歪める \"to twist the facts\". Figuratively, 歪んだ性格／考え means \"twisted, warped character／way of thinking\". Unlike 曲がる (\"to bend, to turn\", neutral), 歪む always stresses an undesirable deformation; the noun is 歪み.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"記憶が蘇りました。","romaji":"Kioku ga yomigaerimashita.","german":"The memory came back.","furigana":"きおく|よみがえ"},{"japanese":"古い商店街が、若い人たちの力で蘇った。","romaji":"Furui shoutengai ga, wakai hitotachi no chikara de yomigaetta.","german":"The old shopping street was revived by the efforts of young people.","furigana":"ふる|しょうてんがい|わか|ひと|ちから|よみがえ"},{"japanese":"水をやったら、元気のなかった花が蘇った。","romaji":"Mizu o yattara, genki no nakatta hana ga yomigaetta.","german":"When I watered them, the drooping flowers revived.","furigana":"みず|げんき|はな|よみがえ"}],"notes":"蘇る (also 甦る, often in kana よみがえる) is a godan verb although it ends in -eru: 蘇って, 蘇らない. It means \"to come alive again\" - of the dead, but also of towns, plants or traditions (町が蘇る). Very common is 記憶／思い出が蘇る: the memory comes back by itself, whereas with 思い出す one actively \"remembers\". More everyday is 生き返る, which also means \"to feel reborn\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"古い習慣が廃れました。","romaji":"Furui shūkan ga sutaremashita.","german":"Old customs died out.","furigana":"ふる|しゅうかん|すた"},{"japanese":"流行の服はすぐに廃れてしまう。","romaji":"Ryuukou no fuku wa sugu ni sutarete shimau.","german":"Trendy clothes quickly go out of fashion.","furigana":"りゅうこう|ふく|すた"},{"japanese":"大型スーパーができてから、駅前の商店街は廃れていった。","romaji":"Oogata suupaa ga dekite kara, ekimae no shoutengai wa sutarete itta.","german":"Since the big supermarket opened, the shopping street in front of the station has been declining.","furigana":"おおがた|えきまえ|しょうてんがい|すた"}],"notes":"廃れる is intransitive and describes how something gradually falls out of use or decays by itself: 習慣／流行／言葉が廃れる, 町が廃れる. The opposite is 流行る \"to be in fashion\". Unlike 廃止する (to abolish deliberately), 廃れる happens without any decision; 衰える, on the other hand, stresses the loss of strength. The word sounds somewhat literary, but is common in the news and in conversation about urban development.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人間関係を培います。","romaji":"Ningen kankei o tsuchikaimasu.","german":"I cultivate human relationships.","furigana":"にんげんかんけい|つちか"},{"japanese":"留学で培った語学力を仕事に生かしたい。","romaji":"Ryuugaku de tsuchikatta gogakuryoku o shigoto ni ikashitai.","german":"I want to put the language skills I developed while studying abroad to use at work.","furigana":"りゅうがく|つちか|ごがくりょく|しごと|い"},{"japanese":"この店は百年かけて培ってきた技術を守っている。","romaji":"Kono mise wa hyakunen kakete tsuchikatte kita gijutsu o mamotte iru.","german":"This shop preserves the craftsmanship it has built up over a hundred years.","furigana":"みせ|ひゃくねん|つちか|ぎじゅつ|まも"}],"notes":"培う is transitive and elevated; it means \"to build up, to develop over a long time\" and usually takes abstract objects: 経験／能力／技術／信頼関係を培う. Typical are relative clauses such as 〜で培った経験 or 長年培ってきた技術. Originally it means \"to hill up plants with soil\". More everyday are 育てる (\"to raise\") and 養う (\"to nourish, to foster\", also \"to support a family\").","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自分で費用を賄います。","romaji":"Jibun de hiyō o makanaimasu.","german":"I will cover the costs myself.","furigana":"じぶん|ひよう|まかな"},{"japanese":"学費はアルバイトで賄っています。","romaji":"Gakuhi wa arubaito de makanatte imasu.","german":"I pay my tuition with a part-time job.","furigana":"がくひ|まかな"},{"japanese":"この町の電気は、ほとんど太陽光発電で賄われている。","romaji":"Kono machi no denki wa, hotondo taiyoukou hatsuden de makanawarete iru.","german":"Almost all of this town's electricity is supplied by solar power.","furigana":"まち|でんき|たいようこうはつでん|まかな"}],"notes":"賄う is transitive: to \"cover, defray\" costs or needs, with the source marked by で: 生活費をアルバイトで賄う. In reports the passive is common: 税金で賄われている. A second sense is \"to provide meals\"; from it comes 賄い（まかない）, the staff meal in restaurants. Not to be confused with 補う (\"to make up for a shortage\") or 払う (\"to pay\"). According to the dictionary it is often in kana, but newspapers mostly use kanji.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不安を煽らないでください。","romaji":"Fuan o aoranaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't stir up anxiety.","furigana":"ふあん|あお"},{"japanese":"強い風に煽られて、火が一気に広がった。","romaji":"Tsuyoi kaze ni aorarete, hi ga ikki ni hirogatta.","german":"Fanned by the strong wind, the fire spread all at once.","furigana":"つよ|かぜ|あお|ひ|いっき|ひろ"},{"japanese":"高速道路で後ろの車に煽られて怖かった。","romaji":"Kousoku douro de ushiro no kuruma ni aorarete kowakatta.","german":"On the expressway, I was scared when the car behind me tailgated me.","furigana":"こうそくどうろ|うし|くるま|あお|こわ"}],"notes":"煽る is transitive and has a whole bundle of meanings. Literally: to fan a fire; often in the passive 強風に煽られる \"to be caught by the wind / buffeted\". Figuratively: to \"stir up\" feelings or conflicts (不安／危機感／対立を煽る) and to incite people. For some years now, 煽り運転 (\"aggressive tailgating in road traffic\") has been a major topic. It is often written in kana as あおる; for 酒をあおる \"to knock back (a drink)\" there is the kanji 呷る.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"悪い友達に唆されて、学校をさぼりました。","romaji":"Warui tomodachi ni sosonokasarete, gakkou o saborimashita.","german":"Egged on by bad friends, I skipped school.","furigana":"わる|ともだち|そそのか|がっこう"},{"japanese":"彼は弟を唆して、親のお金を盗ませた。","romaji":"Kare wa otouto o sosonokashite, oya no okane o nusumaseta.","german":"He incited his younger brother to steal money from their parents.","furigana":"かれ|おとうと|そそのか|おや|かね|ぬす"},{"japanese":"誰かに唆されたとしても、やったのはあなた自身です。","romaji":"Dareka ni sosonokasareta to shite mo, yatta no wa anata jishin desu.","german":"Even if someone tempted you into it, you did it yourself.","furigana":"だれ|そそのか|じしん"}],"notes":"唆す is transitive and means to \"incite, tempt\" someone to do something bad. Typical patterns are 人を唆して〜させる (with the causative) and, in the passive, 〜に唆されて \"incited by ...\". Unlike the neutral 誘う (\"to invite, to urge\"), 唆す is always negative; 煽る is aimed more at emotions or crowds. Mostly written in kana そそのかす.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人を欺いてはいけません。","romaji":"Hito o azamuite wa ikemasen.","german":"You must not deceive people.","furigana":"ひと|あざむ"},{"japanese":"犯人は警察の目を欺いて、海外へ逃げた。","romaji":"Hannin wa keisatsu no me o azamuite, kaigai e nigeta.","german":"The criminal deceived the police and fled abroad.","furigana":"はんにん|けいさつ|め|あざむ|かいがい|に"},{"japanese":"敵を欺くには、まず味方から。","romaji":"Teki o azamuku ni wa, mazu mikata kara.","german":"To deceive the enemy, start with your own side.","furigana":"てき|あざむ|みかた"}],"notes":"欺く is the elevated, literary word for \"to deceive, lead astray\": 人／敵／世間を欺く, 警察の目を欺く. In everyday speech one says 騙す; 裏切る, by contrast, means \"to betray, disappoint\". Well known is the proverb 敵を欺くにはまず味方から. In literary use, 〜を欺く can also mean \"to rival ...\": 昼を欺く明るさ \"bright as day\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自分の行動を省みます。","romaji":"Jibun no kōdō o kaerimimasu.","german":"I reflect on my own behavior.","furigana":"じぶん|こうどう|かえり"},{"japanese":"寝る前に、その日の自分を省みるようにしています。","romaji":"Neru mae ni, sono hi no jibun o kaerimiru you ni shite imasu.","german":"Before going to bed, I try to reflect on my day.","furigana":"ね|まえ|ひ|じぶん|かえり"},{"japanese":"失敗した時こそ、自分のやり方を省みることが大切だ。","romaji":"Shippai shita toki koso, jibun no yarikata o kaerimiru koto ga taisetsu da.","german":"Especially after a failure, it is important to reflect on your own way of doing things.","furigana":"しっぱい|とき|じぶん|かた|かえり|たいせつ"}],"notes":"省みる means \"to reflect on oneself, critically examine one's behavior\" and takes を, usually with 自分, 自ら or 我が身. The identically read 顧みる, by contrast, means \"to look back\" or \"to have regard for\" (家庭を顧みない, 危険を顧みず). Both are literary; in everyday speech one rather says 反省する or 振り返る.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"真実を見極めます。","romaji":"Shinjitsu o mikiwamemasu.","german":"I discern the truth.","furigana":"しんじつ|みきわ"},{"japanese":"本物か偽物かを見極めるのは難しい。","romaji":"Honmono ka nisemono ka o mikiwameru no wa muzukashii.","german":"It is hard to tell whether something is genuine or fake.","furigana":"ほんもの|にせもの|みきわ|むずか"},{"japanese":"もう少し状況を見極めてから決めましょう。","romaji":"Mou sukoshi joukyou o mikiwamete kara kimemashou.","german":"Let's observe the situation a little more closely before we decide.","furigana":"すこ|じょうきょう|みきわ|き"}],"notes":"見極める (見る + 極める) is transitive and means to \"ascertain, get to the bottom of\" something with certainty by looking closely: 真実／本質／原因を見極める, 状況を見極める. Often with an indirect question: 本物かどうかを見極める. The noun 見極め appears in 見極めが大切だ. Unlike 見分ける (\"to distinguish\") or 確かめる (\"to verify\"), 見極める stresses seeing through to the bottom, often before a decision.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"使い捨て容器を減らしましょう。","romaji":"Tsukaisute yōki o herashimashō.","german":"Let's reduce disposable containers.","furigana":"つか|す|ようき|へ"},{"japanese":"使い捨てのコンタクトレンズを使っています。","romaji":"Tsukaisute no kontakuto renzu o tsukatte imasu.","german":"I use disposable contact lenses.","furigana":"つか|す|つか"},{"japanese":"社員を使い捨てにするような会社では働きたくない。","romaji":"Shain o tsukaisute ni suru you na kaisha de wa hatarakitakunai.","german":"I don't want to work at a company that uses up its employees and throws them away.","furigana":"しゃいん|つか|す|かいしゃ|はたら"}],"notes":"使い捨て (使う + 捨てる) refers to things for single use. It stands directly before nouns (使い捨てカイロ, 使い捨てカメラ, 使い捨て容器) or with の (使い捨ての箸). Figuratively, 人を使い捨てにする means to exploit people and then drop them - often said about exploitative companies. The opposite is 繰り返し使える or 再利用.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"省エネを心がけます。","romaji":"Shō ene o kokorogakemasu.","german":"I make a point of saving energy.","furigana":"しょう|こころ"},{"japanese":"新しいエアコンは省エネ性能が高くて、電気代が安くなった。","romaji":"Atarashii eakon wa shou ene seinou ga takakute, denkidai ga yasuku natta.","german":"The new air conditioner has high energy-saving performance, and the electricity bill went down.","furigana":"あたら|しょう|せいのう|たか|でんきだい|やす"},{"japanese":"疲れているので、今日は省エネモードで働きます。","romaji":"Tsukarete iru node, kyou wa shou ene moodo de hatarakimasu.","german":"I'm tired, so today I'll work in energy-saving mode.","furigana":"つか|きょう|しょう|はたら"}],"notes":"省エネ is the usual short form of 省エネルギー and mixes kanji with katakana. Typical are compounds like 省エネ家電, 省エネ住宅, 省エネ対策 and 省エネ性能. Compared with 節電 (\"saving electricity\"), 省エネ is more general and often stresses the efficiency of appliances. Colloquially, it is jokingly called 省エネ when someone spends only the bare minimum of their own effort.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"天然資源を大切にします。","romaji":"Tennen shigen o taisetsu ni shimasu.","german":"We value natural resources.","furigana":"てんねんしげん|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"日本は天然資源が少ない国です。","romaji":"Nihon wa tennen shigen ga sukunai kuni desu.","german":"Japan is a country with few natural resources.","furigana":"にほん|てんねんしげん|すく|くに"},{"japanese":"その国は石油などの天然資源に恵まれている。","romaji":"Sono kuni wa sekiyu nado no tennen shigen ni megumarete iru.","german":"That country is blessed with natural resources such as oil.","furigana":"くに|せきゆ|てんねんしげん|めぐ"}],"notes":"天然資源 (天然 \"natural\" + 資源 \"resources\") are raw materials and mineral resources such as oil, natural gas, ores, water or forest. Typical phrases: 天然資源が豊富／乏しい, 天然資源に恵まれる, 限りある天然資源. The word belongs to the news, geography and economics; in conversation one often says just 資源 (資源ごみ, 資源を大切に).","levelNote":"Compound: 天然 (N3) + 資源 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"索引で調べます。","romaji":"Sakuin de shirabemasu.","german":"I look it up in the index.","furigana":"さくいん|しら"},{"japanese":"この本は最後に索引がついているので便利です。","romaji":"Kono hon wa saigo ni sakuin ga tsuite iru node benri desu.","german":"This book has an index at the back, which is convenient.","furigana":"ほん|さいご|さくいん|べんり"},{"japanese":"索引は五十音順に並んでいます。","romaji":"Sakuin wa gojuuonjun ni narande imasu.","german":"The index is arranged in gojūon order.","furigana":"さくいん|ごじゅうおんじゅん|なら"}],"notes":"索引 is the index or list of keywords at the end of a book; one looks things up in it with 索引で調べる or 索引を引く. Not to be confused with 目次 (\"table of contents\" at the beginning). In Japanese books, the index is usually arranged in 五十音順. In IT, for a database index one rather says インデックス.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地図の凡例を見てください。","romaji":"Chizu no hanrei o mite kudasai.","german":"Please look at the map legend.","furigana":"ちず|はんれい|み"},{"japanese":"辞書を使う前に、凡例を読んでおくといい。","romaji":"Jisho o tsukau mae ni, hanrei o yonde oku to ii.","german":"Before using a dictionary, it is a good idea to read the explanatory notes.","furigana":"じしょ|つか|まえ|はんれい|よ"},{"japanese":"グラフの凡例の色が分かりにくいです。","romaji":"Gurafu no hanrei no iro ga wakarinikui desu.","german":"The colors in the chart legend are hard to tell apart.","furigana":"はんれい|いろ|わ"}],"notes":"凡例 is read はんれい, not ぼんれい, although 凡 is otherwise mostly ぼん (平凡). It has two meanings: the \"user's guide\" at the beginning of a book or dictionary, which explains abbreviations and symbols, and the \"legend\" of a map or chart (地図／グラフの凡例). The word is technical and written, and is met mainly in dictionaries, atlases and presentations.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"脚注を読んでください。","romaji":"Kyakuchū o yonde kudasai.","german":"Please read the footnotes.","furigana":"きゃくちゅう|よ"},{"japanese":"引用した文献は脚注に書いてください。","romaji":"In'you shita bunken wa kyakuchuu ni kaite kudasai.","german":"Please list the cited literature in the footnotes.","furigana":"いんよう|ぶんけん|きゃくちゅう|か"},{"japanese":"専門用語には脚注で説明がついています。","romaji":"Senmon yougo ni wa kyakuchuu de setsumei ga tsuite imasu.","german":"Technical terms are explained in footnotes.","furigana":"せんもんようご|きゃくちゅう|せつめい"}],"notes":"脚注 (脚 \"foot\" + 注 \"note\") is the footnote at the bottom of the page; one says 脚注をつける／入れる and 脚注で説明する. More general are 注 or 注釈 (\"annotation\"); endnotes at the end of a chapter or book are called 後注 or 巻末注. The word belongs to academic and editorial vocabulary, for example in term papers.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"草稿を仕上げます。","romaji":"Sōkō o shiagemasu.","german":"I finish the draft.","furigana":"そうこう|しあ"},{"japanese":"大統領の演説の草稿が公開された。","romaji":"Daitouryou no enzetsu no soukou ga koukai sareta.","german":"The draft of the president's speech was made public.","furigana":"だいとうりょう|えんぜつ|そうこう|こうかい"},{"japanese":"作家の死後、未発表の草稿が見つかった。","romaji":"Sakka no shigo, mihappyou no soukou ga mitsukatta.","german":"After the writer's death, an unpublished manuscript was found.","furigana":"さっか|しご|みはっぴょう|そうこう|み"}],"notes":"草稿 is the first written draft of a text, such as a speech, a novel or a paper: 演説の草稿, 草稿を書く／仕上げる／練る. The word is elevated; in everyday speech one says 下書き (also for sketches and e-mail drafts). 原稿 is the (finished) manuscript for submission, 案 a draft plan or proposal.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"作文を添削してもらいました。","romaji":"Sakubun o tensaku shite moraimashita.","german":"I had my essay corrected.","furigana":"さくぶん|てんさく"},{"japanese":"先生は毎晩、学生のレポートを添削しています。","romaji":"Sensei wa maiban, gakusei no repooto o tensaku shite imasu.","german":"The teacher corrects the students' reports every evening.","furigana":"せんせい|まいばん|がくせい|てんさく"},{"japanese":"履歴書の添削サービスを利用しました。","romaji":"Rirekisho no tensaku saabisu o riyou shimashita.","german":"I used a service that reviews and revises résumés.","furigana":"りれきしょ|てんさく|りよう"}],"notes":"添削 (添 \"to add\" + 削 \"to strike out\") is the correcting and revising of a text by a more knowledgeable person, mostly a teacher: 作文／論文／答案を添削する, 添削してもらう. 通信添削 is a correspondence course with corrected assignments. To be distinguished are 訂正 (\"to correct an error\"), 修正 (\"to change, to touch up\") and 校正 (\"proofreading\" for typos).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"論文の要旨を書きます。","romaji":"Ronbun no yōshi o kakimasu.","german":"I write the abstract of the paper.","furigana":"ろんぶん|ようし|か"},{"japanese":"話の要旨をつかむ練習をしましょう。","romaji":"Hanashi no youshi o tsukamu renshuu o shimashou.","german":"Let's practice grasping the gist of a talk.","furigana":"はなし|ようし|れんしゅう"},{"japanese":"発表の要旨を来週までに提出してください。","romaji":"Happyou no youshi o raishuu made ni teishutsu shite kudasai.","german":"Please submit a summary of your presentation by next week.","furigana":"はっぴょう|ようし|らいしゅう|ていしゅつ"}],"notes":"要旨 is the core content of a text or talk, also the \"abstract\" of a scholarly paper: 論文の要旨, 発表要旨, 要旨をまとめる／つかむ. 要約, by contrast, is summarizing as an activity (要約する), 要点 are the individual key points, あらすじ the plot outline of a story. Beware of homophones: 用紙 \"form\", 容姿 \"appearance\", 養子 \"adopted child\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新刊の書評を読みました。","romaji":"Shinkan no shohyō o yomimashita.","german":"I read a review of a new book.","furigana":"しんかん|しょひょう|よ"},{"japanese":"新聞の書評欄で、面白そうな本を見つけた。","romaji":"Shinbun no shohyouran de, omoshirosou na hon o mitsuketa.","german":"In the newspaper's book review section, I found an interesting book.","furigana":"しんぶん|しょひょうらん|おもしろ|ほん|み"},{"japanese":"彼は雑誌に毎月書評を書いている。","romaji":"Kare wa zasshi ni maitsuki shohyou o kaite iru.","german":"He writes book reviews for a magazine every month.","furigana":"かれ|ざっし|まいつきしょひょう|か"}],"notes":"書評 is a (usually published) book review in a newspaper or magazine: 書評を書く, 新聞の書評欄 \"book review section\". 感想, by contrast, are personal impressions (読書感想文 \"book report\" at school), and 批評 is criticism in general. User reviews on the internet are usually called レビュー.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"イソップ寓話を読みます。","romaji":"Isoppu gūwa o yomimasu.","german":"I read Aesop's fables.","furigana":"ぐうわ|よ"},{"japanese":"この寓話には「うそをついてはいけない」という教訓がある。","romaji":"Kono guuwa ni wa 'uso o tsuite wa ikenai' to iu kyoukun ga aru.","german":"This fable has the lesson that you should not lie.","furigana":"ぐうわ|きょうくん"},{"japanese":"その映画は現代社会の寓話として見ることもできる。","romaji":"Sono eiga wa gendai shakai no guuwa to shite miru koto mo dekiru.","german":"That film can also be understood as a parable of modern society.","furigana":"えいが|げんだいしゃかい|ぐうわ|み"}],"notes":"寓話 is a fable or parable that conveys a lesson (教訓) in a story (often with animals); 寓 means \"to put something in, to hint\". Well known is イソップ寓話 \"Aesop's fables\"; the adjective 寓話的 means \"parable-like\". To be distinguished are 童話 (\"children's tale\"), 昔話 (\"folk tale\") and おとぎ話. The word is rather written.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ホメロスの叙事詩を読みました。","romaji":"Homerosu no jojishi o yomimashita.","german":"I read Homer's epic.","furigana":"じょじし|よ"},{"japanese":"ギルガメシュ叙事詩は、世界で最も古い物語の一つです。","romaji":"Girugameshu jojishi wa, sekai de mottomo furui monogatari no hitotsu desu.","german":"The Epic of Gilgamesh is one of the oldest stories in the world.","furigana":"じょじし|せかい|もっと|ふる|ものがたり|ひと"},{"japanese":"この映画は三世代の家族を描いた壮大な叙事詩だ。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa sansedai no kazoku o egaita soudai na jojishi da.","german":"This film is a grand epic depicting a family across three generations.","furigana":"えいが|さんせだい|かぞく|えが|そうだい|じょじし"}],"notes":"叙事詩 (叙事 \"to narrate events\" + 詩 \"poem\") is an epic that tells of the deeds of heroes or peoples, such as the works of Homer or the Epic of Gilgamesh. The counterpart is 叙情詩（抒情詩） \"lyric poetry\". Figuratively, big films or novels spanning generations are gladly called 壮大な叙事詩 \"monumental epic\". The word is literary and written.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"行間を読みます。","romaji":"Gyōkan o yomimasu.","german":"I read between the lines.","furigana":"ぎょうかん|よ"},{"japanese":"読みやすいように、行間を少し広げてください。","romaji":"Yomiyasui you ni, gyoukan o sukoshi hirogete kudasai.","german":"Please widen the line spacing a little so it is easier to read.","furigana":"よ|ぎょうかん|すこ|ひろ"},{"japanese":"日本語のメールは、行間を読む力が必要だと言われる。","romaji":"Nihongo no meeru wa, gyoukan o yomu chikara ga hitsuyou da to iwareru.","german":"It is said that with Japanese e-mails you need the ability to read between the lines.","furigana":"にほんご|ぎょうかん|よ|ちから|ひつよう|い"}],"notes":"行間 is literally the \"line spacing\" in a text: 行間を空ける／広げる／詰める, for example in Word documents. Much better known is the figurative phrase 行間を読む \"to read between the lines\", that is, to recognize unspoken intentions - an ability often expected in Japanese communication. The spacing between individual characters, by contrast, is called 字間.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"排水溝が詰まっています。","romaji":"Haisuikō ga tsumatteimasu.","german":"The drain is clogged.","furigana":"はいすいこう|つ"},{"japanese":"お風呂の排水溝を週に一度掃除しています。","romaji":"Ofuro no haisuikou o shuu ni ichido souji shite imasu.","german":"I clean the bathroom drain once a week.","furigana":"ふろ|はいすいこう|しゅう|いちどそうじ"},{"japanese":"大雨で道路の排水溝から水があふれた。","romaji":"Ooame de douro no haisuikou kara mizu ga afureta.","german":"Because of the heavy rain, water overflowed from the road drains.","furigana":"おおあめ|どうろ|はいすいこう|みず"}],"notes":"排水溝 (排水 \"drainage\" + 溝 \"ditch, channel\") is a drainage channel, both at the roadside and in the bathroom or kitchen: 排水溝が詰まる, 排水溝を掃除する. The identically read 排水口 strictly denotes the drain opening (e.g. in a sink); in everyday use the two are often mixed up. Colloquially, a dirty ditch is called どぶ, and the sewage system 下水.","levelNote":"Compound: 排水 (N1) + 溝 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"雨樋を掃除します。","romaji":"Amadoi o sōji shimasu.","german":"I clean the rain gutter.","furigana":"あまどい|そうじ"},{"japanese":"雨樋に落ち葉が詰まって、水があふれている。","romaji":"Amadoi ni ochiba ga tsumatte, mizu ga afurete iru.","german":"Fallen leaves are clogging the gutter, and the water is overflowing.","furigana":"あまどい|お|ば|つ|みず"},{"japanese":"台風で雨樋が壊れたので、修理を頼みました。","romaji":"Taifuu de amadoi ga kowareta node, shuuri o tanomimashita.","german":"The typhoon broke the rain gutter, so I asked for a repair.","furigana":"たいふう|あまどい|こわ|しゅうり|たの"}],"notes":"雨樋 (雨 reads あま in compounds + 樋 とい → どい) is the rain gutter including the downspout. It is often written 雨どい or simply called 樋（とい）. Typical problems are 落ち葉が詰まる and 雨樋が外れる／壊れる after a storm or snow; one has it repaired (修理を頼む).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"庇の下で雨宿りします。","romaji":"Hisashi no shita de amayadori shimasu.","german":"I shelter from the rain under the eaves.","furigana":"ひさし|した|あまやど"},{"japanese":"帽子のひさしを下げて、日差しを避けた。","romaji":"Boushi no hisashi o sagete, hizashi o saketa.","german":"I pulled down the brim of my cap to avoid the sunlight.","furigana":"ぼうし|さ|ひざ|さ"},{"japanese":"窓の上に庇があるので、雨が入りにくい。","romaji":"Mado no ue ni hisashi ga aru node, ame ga hairinikui.","german":"There is a small canopy above the window, so rain hardly gets in.","furigana":"まど|うえ|ひさし|あめ|はい"}],"notes":"庇 is mostly written in kana ひさし. It is a small canopy over a window or door that protects from rain and sun (庇の下で雨宿りする), and also the \"visor\" of a cap (帽子のひさし). The roof overhang of the whole house is rather called 軒 or 軒先. Well known is the proverb 庇を貸して母屋を取られる - literally \"to lend the canopy and lose the main house\", i.e. \"give them an inch and they take a mile\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの高級レストランは、私には敷居が高いです。","romaji":"Ano koukyuu resutoran wa, watashi ni wa shikii ga takai desu.","german":"That high-class restaurant is too intimidating for me.","furigana":"こうきゅう|わたし|しきい|たか"},{"japanese":"敷居を踏まないように部屋に入ってください。","romaji":"Shikii o fumanai you ni heya ni haitte kudasai.","german":"Please enter the room without stepping on the threshold.","furigana":"しきい|ふ|へや|はい"},{"japanese":"二度とこの家の敷居をまたぐな！","romaji":"Nido to kono ie no shikii o matagu na!","german":"Never set foot over the threshold of this house again!","furigana":"にど|いえ|しきい"}],"notes":"敷居 is the threshold with grooves for sliding doors (襖, 障子); the upper counterpart is called 鴨居. According to traditional etiquette, one does not step on the 敷居. Important are the idioms: 敷居をまたぐ \"to enter a house\" and 敷居が高い - originally \"to be ashamed to visit someone\" (because one owes them something), today mostly \"hard to approach, intimidating\", e.g. for expensive restaurants. Technically, しきい値 stands for \"threshold value\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鴨居に頭をぶつけました。","romaji":"Kamoi ni atama o butsukemashita.","german":"Ich habe meinen Kopf am Türrahmen gestoßen.","furigana":"かもい|あたま"},{"japanese":"鴨居にハンガーを掛けて、服を干しています。","romaji":"Kamoi ni hangaa o kakete, fuku o hoshite imasu.","german":"Ich hänge Kleiderbügel an den Türsturz und trockne so meine Wäsche.","furigana":"かもい|か|ふく|ほ"},{"japanese":"古い家は鴨居が低いので、気をつけてください。","romaji":"Furui ie wa kamoi ga hikui node, ki o tsukete kudasai.","german":"In alten Häusern sind die Türstürze niedrig, passen Sie also auf.","furigana":"ふる|いえ|かもい|ひく|き"}],"notes":"鴨居 ist der obere Querbalken mit Laufrillen für Schiebetüren, also das Gegenstück zum 敷居 am Boden. In alten japanischen Häusern ist er niedrig, sodass sich große Menschen oft den Kopf stoßen (鴨居に頭をぶつける). Praktisch dient er auch als Aufhängung: ハンガーを鴨居に掛ける. Im Deutschen entspricht er etwa dem „Türsturz“.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"欄干にもたれかかります。","romaji":"Rankan ni motarekakarimasu.","german":"I lean against the railing.","furigana":"らんかん"},{"japanese":"橋の欄干から川をのぞきました。","romaji":"Hashi no rankan kara kawa o nozokimashita.","german":"I peered down at the river from the bridge railing.","furigana":"はし|らんかん|かわ"},{"japanese":"危ないので、欄干に登らないでください。","romaji":"Abunai node, rankan ni noboranaide kudasai.","german":"Please do not climb on the railing; it is dangerous.","furigana":"あぶ|らんかん|のぼ"}],"notes":"欄干 is the railing on bridges, on the verandas of temples or on balconies of traditional buildings, often made of wood and with decorated posts. Typical are 橋の欄干, 欄干にもたれる and 欄干から川を見下ろす. For a modern handrail on stairs one says 手すり, for a fence 柵 or フェンス. The word sounds somewhat traditional and literary.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"軒先で雨宿りしました。","romaji":"Nokisaki de amayadori shimashita.","german":"I sheltered from the rain under the eaves.","furigana":"のきさき|あまやど"},{"japanese":"軒先に風鈴を吊るしました。","romaji":"Nokisaki ni fuurin o tsurushimashita.","german":"I hung a wind chime from the eaves.","furigana":"のきさき|ふうりん|つ"},{"japanese":"農家の軒先で、野菜が安く売られている。","romaji":"Nouka no nokisaki de, yasai ga yasuku urarete iru.","german":"Vegetables are being sold cheaply in front of the farmhouses.","furigana":"のうか|のきさき|やさい|やす|う"}],"notes":"軒先 (軒 \"eaves\" + 先 \"tip\") is the front edge of the roof overhang and the area directly in front of it, so also \"in front of the house\". Typical phrases: 軒先で雨宿りする, 軒先に吊るす／干す, 軒先を借りる (to take brief shelter at someone's place) and 軒先で売る (selling in front of the house). 庇, by contrast, is a small canopy over a window or door, 玄関先 the area in front of the entrance.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"瓦屋根の家です。","romaji":"Kawara yane no ie desu.","german":"It is a house with a tiled roof.","furigana":"かわらやね|いえ"},{"japanese":"台風で屋根の瓦が何枚か飛ばされた。","romaji":"Taifuu de yane no kawara ga nanmai ka tobasareta.","german":"The typhoon blew off several tiles from the roof.","furigana":"たいふう|やね|かわら|なんまい|と"},{"japanese":"空手の先生が瓦を十枚も割ってみせた。","romaji":"Karate no sensei ga kawara o juumai mo watte miseta.","german":"The karate teacher broke as many as ten roof tiles in front of us.","furigana":"からて|せんせい|かわら|じゅうまい|わ"}],"notes":"瓦 is the (usually fired) roof tile; it is counted with 枚. Common are 瓦屋根 \"tiled roof\", 瓦を葺く \"to lay a roof\", and damage after typhoons or earthquakes (瓦が落ちる／ずれる). 瓦割り is the breaking of tiles in karate. For wall and floor tiles, one does not say 瓦 but タイル.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"漆喰の壁が美しいです。","romaji":"Shikkui no kabe ga utsukushii desu.","german":"The plastered wall is beautiful.","furigana":"しっくい|かべ|うつく"},{"japanese":"姫路城の白い壁は漆喰で塗られている。","romaji":"Himejijou no shiroi kabe wa shikkui de nurarete iru.","german":"The white walls of Himeji Castle are coated with lime plaster.","furigana":"ひめじじょう|しろ|かべ|しっくい|ぬ"},{"japanese":"部屋の壁を自分で漆喰に塗り替えました。","romaji":"Heya no kabe o jibun de shikkui ni nurikaemashita.","german":"I re-coated the walls of my room with plaster myself.","furigana":"へや|かべ|じぶん|しっくい|ぬ|か"}],"notes":"漆喰 is traditional lime plaster, with which the walls of storehouses (土蔵) and castles are plastered white - Himeji Castle is famous for it. One says 漆喰を塗る, 漆喰の壁, 漆喰で仕上げる. Modern cement mortar is called モルタル, wallpaper 壁紙. Today, 漆喰 is also popular with DIYers because of its moisture-regulating effect.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"太い梁が見えます。","romaji":"Futoi hari ga miemasu.","german":"Thick beams are visible.","furigana":"ふと|はり|み"},{"japanese":"梁にロープを掛けてブランコを作った。","romaji":"Hari ni roopu o kakete buranko o tsukutta.","german":"We threw a rope over the beam and made a swing.","furigana":"はり|か|つく"},{"japanese":"古い梁を再利用して、テーブルを作りました。","romaji":"Furui hari o sairiyou shite, teeburu o tsukurimashita.","german":"We made a table from an old beam.","furigana":"ふる|はり|さいりよう|つく"}],"notes":"梁 is a horizontal supporting beam in the roof or under the ceiling; the vertical post is 柱. Typical are 天井の梁, 太い梁 and 梁がむき出しになっている (\"exposed beams\") in old houses or lofts. Beware of the homophone 針 \"needle\"; the context usually makes it clear.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"基礎をしっかり学びます。","romaji":"Kiso o shikkari manabimasu.","german":"I learn the fundamentals thoroughly.","furigana":"きそ|まな"},{"japanese":"家を建てる前に、まず基礎工事をします。","romaji":"Ie o tateru mae ni, mazu kiso kouji o shimasu.","german":"Before building a house, you first do the foundation work.","furigana":"いえ|た|まえ|きそこうじ"},{"japanese":"毎朝走って基礎体力をつけています。","romaji":"Maiasa hashitte kiso tairyoku o tsukete imasu.","german":"I run every morning to build my basic physical fitness.","furigana":"まいあさはし|きそたいりょく"}],"notes":"基礎 is both the structural foundation (建物の基礎, 基礎工事) and the basis of knowledge and skill (基礎知識, 基礎体力, 基礎から学ぶ, 基礎的な). Compared with 基本 (\"basic rule, standard\"), 基礎 emphasizes the base on which one builds; often both are interchangeable (基礎／基本を学ぶ). 土台 is more concrete and colloquial.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"瀬戸物を買いました。","romaji":"Setomono o kaimashita.","german":"I bought some ceramics.","furigana":"せともの|か"},{"japanese":"瀬戸物は割れやすいので、新聞紙で包んでください。","romaji":"Setomono wa wareyasui node, shinbunshi de tsutsunde kudasai.","german":"Ceramics break easily, so please wrap them in newspaper.","furigana":"せともの|わ|しんぶんし|つつ"},{"japanese":"祖母の家の台所には、古い瀬戸物の茶わんがたくさんある。","romaji":"Sobo no ie no daidokoro ni wa, furui setomono no chawan ga takusan aru.","german":"In my grandmother's kitchen there are many old ceramic bowls.","furigana":"そぼ|いえ|だいどころ|ふる|せともの|ちゃ"}],"notes":"瀬戸物 originally meant ceramics from Seto (Aichi Prefecture) and became, especially in eastern Japan, the collective term for tableware made of ceramic and porcelain (in western Japan, 唐津物 was traditionally said). Today it sounds somewhat old-fashioned; neutral are 焼き物, 陶器 (\"earthenware\") and 磁器 (\"porcelain\"). Typical: 瀬戸物屋, 瀬戸物の茶わん, 瀬戸物が割れる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"文楽を大阪で見ました。","romaji":"Bunraku o Ōsaka de mimashita.","german":"I saw bunraku in Osaka.","furigana":"ぶんらく|おおさか|み"},{"japanese":"文楽では、一体の人形を三人で動かします。","romaji":"Bunraku de wa, ittai no ningyou o sannin de ugokashimasu.","german":"In bunraku, one puppet is operated by three people.","furigana":"ぶんらく|いったい|にんぎょう|さんにん|うご"},{"japanese":"文楽と歌舞伎には、同じ話をもとにした作品が多い。","romaji":"Bunraku to kabuki ni wa, onaji hanashi o moto ni shita sakuhin ga ooi.","german":"Bunraku and kabuki have many plays based on the same stories.","furigana":"ぶんらく|かぶき|おな|はなし|さくひん|おお"}],"notes":"文楽 is the traditional puppet theater (人形浄瑠璃) from Osaka, today a UNESCO World Heritage item. It combines three arts: the narrator (太夫), the shamisen music and the puppeteers, three of whom operate one puppet together. Alongside 歌舞伎 and 能, it is one of the great classical stage arts; the main theater is the 国立文楽劇場 in Osaka. One says 文楽を見る／鑑賞する.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"浮世絵を美術館で見ました。","romaji":"Ukiyoe o bijutsukan de mimashita.","german":"I saw ukiyo-e at the museum.","furigana":"うきよえ|びじゅつかん|み"},{"japanese":"北斎の浮世絵は、ヨーロッパの画家にも影響を与えた。","romaji":"Hokusai no ukiyoe wa, Yooroppa no gaka ni mo eikyou o ataeta.","german":"Hokusai's ukiyo-e also influenced European painters.","furigana":"ほくさい|うきよえ|がか|えいきょう|あた"},{"japanese":"お土産に浮世絵のはがきを買いました。","romaji":"Omiyage ni ukiyoe no hagaki o kaimashita.","german":"I bought ukiyo-e postcards as a souvenir.","furigana":"みやげ|うきよえ|か"}],"notes":"浮世絵 (浮世 \"the fleeting, earthly world\" + 絵 \"picture\") are the color woodblock prints of the Edo period with motifs from theater, pleasure quarters and landscapes. Famous artists (浮世絵師) are 葛飾北斎 and 歌川広重; their works influenced the European Impressionists. One says 浮世絵を見る／集める, 浮世絵展.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"金の屏風が飾られています。","romaji":"Kin no byōbu ga kazarareteimasu.","german":"A golden folding screen is on display.","furigana":"きん|びょうぶ|かざ"},{"japanese":"結婚式では、新郎新婦が金屏風の前に座る。","romaji":"Kekkonshiki de wa, shinrou shinpu ga kinbyoubu no mae ni suwaru.","german":"At weddings, the bride and groom sit in front of a gold folding screen.","furigana":"けっこんしき|しんろうしんぷ|きんびょうぶ|まえ|すわ"},{"japanese":"屏風を立てて、部屋を二つに仕切りました。","romaji":"Byoubu o tatete, heya o futatsu ni shikirimashita.","german":"I set up a folding screen and divided the room into two.","furigana":"びょうぶ|た|へや|ふた|しき"}],"notes":"屏風 is a multi-panel folding screen, often artfully painted or covered with gold leaf; a pair is called 一双. One says 屏風を立てる／広げる. The golden 金屏風 still serves as a backdrop at weddings and press conferences. A simple one-panel room divider is called ついたて or パーティション.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"床の間に掛け軸を飾ります。","romaji":"Tokonoma ni kakejiku o kazarimasu.","german":"I hang a scroll in the tokonoma.","furigana":"とこ|ま|か|じく|かざ"},{"japanese":"季節に合わせて掛け軸を替えています。","romaji":"Kisetsu ni awasete kakejiku o kaete imasu.","german":"I change the hanging scroll to suit the season.","furigana":"きせつ|あ|か|じく|か"},{"japanese":"茶室の掛け軸には「和」という字が書かれていた。","romaji":"Chashitsu no kakejiku ni wa 'wa' to iu ji ga kakarete ita.","german":"The tea room's hanging scroll bore the character for \"harmony\".","furigana":"ちゃしつ|か|じく|わ|じ|か"}],"notes":"掛け軸 (掛ける + 軸 \"roll, shaft\", also written 掛軸) is a hanging scroll with calligraphy or a painting. It is hung in the alcove 床の間 (掛け軸を掛ける／飾る) and changed according to the season or occasion, especially in the tea ceremony. It is traditionally counted with 幅（ふく）.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"土間で靴を脱いで、部屋に上がります。","romaji":"Doma de kutsu o nuide, heya ni agarimasu.","german":"You take off your shoes on the earthen floor and then step up into the room.","furigana":"どま|くつ|ぬ|へや|あ"},{"japanese":"昔の農家では、土間で料理をしていた。","romaji":"Mukashi no nouka de wa, doma de ryouri o shite ita.","german":"In old farmhouses, people used to cook in the earthen-floored area.","furigana":"むかし|のうか|どま|りょうり"},{"japanese":"最近は、自転車を置ける広い土間のある家が人気だ。","romaji":"Saikin wa, jitensha o okeru hiroi doma no aru ie ga ninki da.","german":"Recently, houses with a spacious earthen-floored entrance area where you can park bicycles are popular.","furigana":"さいきん|じてんしゃ|お|ひろ|どま|いえ|にんき"}],"notes":"土間 is an area without a wooden floor at ground level, formerly made of pounded clay and now often of concrete. In traditional farmhouses it served as kitchen and work space; you take off your shoes there and step up (上がる) onto the raised floor. The small area in today's 玄関 is a kind of 土間; modern houses with a large 土間 for bicycles or hobbies are popular again. Do not confuse it with \"ground floor\" (一階).","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"古い蔵を改装しました。","romaji":"Furui kura o kaisō shimashita.","german":"We renovated an old storehouse.","furigana":"ふる|くら|かいそう"},{"japanese":"酒蔵を見学して、日本酒を試飲しました。","romaji":"Sakagura o kengaku shite, nihonshu o shiin shimashita.","german":"We toured a sake brewery and tasted sake.","furigana":"さかぐら|けんがく|にほんしゅ|しいん"},{"japanese":"祖父の家の蔵から、古い着物が出てきた。","romaji":"Sofu no ie no kura kara, furui kimono ga dete kita.","german":"Old kimonos turned up in the storehouse at my grandfather's house.","furigana":"そふ|いえ|くら|ふる|きもの|で"}],"notes":"蔵 is a traditional fireproof storehouse with thick clay and lime walls (土蔵), in which rice, valuables or sake were stored. Important compounds: 酒蔵（さかぐら） \"sake brewery\", 蔵元 \"brewery owner\", 蔵造りの町 \"town of storehouses\". お蔵入り means figuratively \"shelved, never released\". A modern warehouse is 倉庫.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"囲炉裏で暖まります。","romaji":"Irori de atatamarimasu.","german":"I warm myself at the hearth.","furigana":"いろり|あたた"},{"japanese":"囲炉裏で魚を焼いて食べました。","romaji":"Irori de sakana o yaite tabemashita.","german":"We grilled fish over the hearth and ate it.","furigana":"いろり|さかな|や|た"},{"japanese":"祖母の古い家には、今も囲炉裏があります。","romaji":"Sobo no furui ie ni wa, ima mo irori ga arimasu.","german":"My grandmother's old house still has an open hearth today.","furigana":"そぼ|ふる|いえ|いま|いろり"}],"notes":"囲炉裏 is the square hearth set into the floor of old farmhouses (古民家), used for heating and cooking; a kettle often hangs above it. Typical expressions are 囲炉裏を囲む (\"to sit around the hearth\") and 囲炉裏で魚を焼く. Today it is found mainly in old houses, country inns and restaurants. A Western fireplace is called 暖炉; 囲炉裏 is also often written in kana as いろり.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"手水舎で手を清めます。","romaji":"Chōzuya de te o kiyomemasu.","german":"I purify my hands at the temizuya.","furigana":"ちょうずや|て|きよ"},{"japanese":"お参りの前に、手水舎で手と口をすすぎます。","romaji":"Omairi no mae ni, chouzuya de te to kuchi o susugimasu.","german":"Before praying, you rinse your hands and mouth at the temizuya.","furigana":"まい|まえ|ちょうずや|て|くち"},{"japanese":"この神社の手水舎には、季節の花が浮かべてあります。","romaji":"Kono jinja no chouzuya ni wa, kisetsu no hana ga ukabete arimasu.","german":"In the temizuya of this shrine, seasonal flowers are floated.","furigana":"じんじゃ|ちょうずや|きせつ|はな|う"}],"notes":"手水舎 is the roofed fountain with a water basin and ladles at the entrance of shrines and temples. Before praying, you cleanse your left hand, right hand and mouth there, taking the water from your hand and not drinking directly from the ladle. It is usually read ちょうずや, but also てみずや or ちょうずしゃ; the water itself is called 手水. Recently, 花手水 with floating blossoms have become popular.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"赤い鳥居が見えます。","romaji":"Akai torii ga miemasu.","german":"I can see a red torii.","furigana":"あか|とりい|み"},{"japanese":"鳥居をくぐる前に、軽く一礼しましょう。","romaji":"Torii o kuguru mae ni, karuku ichirei shimashou.","german":"Let's bow lightly before passing through the torii.","furigana":"とりい|まえ|かる|いちれい"},{"japanese":"伏見稲荷大社には、たくさんの鳥居が並んでいる。","romaji":"Fushimi Inari Taisha ni wa, takusan no torii ga narande iru.","german":"Many torii stand in a row at Fushimi Inari Taisha.","furigana":"|とりい|なら"}],"notes":"鳥居 is the gate that marks the entrance to the sacred precinct of a Shinto shrine; Buddhist temples have a 門 or 山門 instead. One says 鳥居をくぐる \"to pass through the torii\" and bows briefly before it; the center of the path is regarded as the path of the gods. Famous are the 千本鳥居 at Fushimi Inari shrine. It is counted with 基.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"神社の入り口に狛犬があります。","romaji":"Jinja no iriguchi ni komainu ga arimasu.","german":"There are guardian lion-dogs at the shrine entrance.","furigana":"じんじゃ|い|ぐち|こまいぬ"},{"japanese":"狛犬は、片方が口を開け、もう片方が口を閉じています。","romaji":"Komainu wa, katahou ga kuchi o ake, mou katahou ga kuchi o tojite imasu.","german":"Of the komainu pair, one has its mouth open and the other has it closed.","furigana":"こまいぬ|かたほう|くち|あ|かたほう|くち|と"},{"japanese":"この神社の狛犬は、表情がかわいいと評判です。","romaji":"Kono jinja no komainu wa, hyoujou ga kawaii to hyouban desu.","german":"The komainu at this shrine are known for their cute expressions.","furigana":"じんじゃ|こまいぬ|ひょうじょう|ひょうばん"}],"notes":"狛犬 are the pairs of stone guardian figures (\"lion-dogs\") placed at the entrance of a Shinto shrine. Usually one figure has its mouth open and the other closed, after 阿吽 (\"a\" and \"un\", the beginning and the end). Although they are statues, besides 狛犬がある you also hear 狛犬がいる. At Inari shrines there are foxes (狐) instead of 狛犬, and in Okinawa the related シーサー.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"おみくじを引きました。","romaji":"Omikuji o hikimashita.","german":"I drew an omikuji.","furigana":"ひ"},{"japanese":"初詣でおみくじを引いたら、大吉だった。","romaji":"Hatsumoude de omikuji o hiitara, daikichi datta.","german":"When I drew a fortune slip on the first shrine visit of the year, it was \"great luck\".","furigana":"はつもうで|ひ|だいきち"},{"japanese":"悪い結果のおみくじは、神社の木に結んで帰ります。","romaji":"Warui kekka no omikuji wa, jinja no ki ni musunde kaerimasu.","german":"A fortune slip with a bad result is tied to a tree at the shrine and left there.","furigana":"わる|けっか|じんじゃ|き|むす|かえ"}],"notes":"御神籤 is almost always written in kana as おみくじ (rarely お神籤); it consists of the politeness prefix お, 神 \"deity\" and 籤 \"lot\". One says おみくじを引く. The results range from 大吉 (\"great luck\") through 吉, 中吉, 小吉, 末吉 to 凶 and 大凶. Unfavorable slips are often tied to a tree or rack at the shrine (結ぶ) instead of being taken home.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"賽銭を投げ入れます。","romaji":"Saisen o nageiremasu.","german":"I throw in an offering coin.","furigana":"さいせん|な|い"},{"japanese":"お賽銭に五円玉を入れるのは、「ご縁」があるようにという意味です。","romaji":"Osaisen ni goendama o ireru no wa, 'goen' ga aru you ni to iu imi desu.","german":"Putting a five-yen coin in as an offering is meant to bring \"good connections\" (ご縁).","furigana":"さいせん|ごえんだま|い|えん|いみ"},{"japanese":"賽銭箱の前で二回お辞儀をして、手を二回たたきます。","romaji":"Saisenbako no mae de nikai ojigi o shite, te o nikai tatakimasu.","german":"In front of the offering box, you bow twice and clap your hands twice.","furigana":"さいせんばこ|まえ|にかい|じぎ|て|にかい"}],"notes":"賽銭 is the money thrown into the offering box (賽銭箱) when praying at a shrine or temple; politely it is usually お賽銭. Typical expressions are お賽銭を入れる／投げる. The five-yen coin is popular because 五円 sounds like ご縁 (\"good connection, bond of fate\"). Donations to monks for ceremonies, by contrast, are called お布施, and general donations 寄付.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"御朱印帳を持っています。","romaji":"Goshuinchō o motteimasu.","german":"I have a goshuin book.","furigana":"ごしゅいんちょう|も"},{"japanese":"お参りのあとで、御朱印をいただきました。","romaji":"Omairi no ato de, goshuin o itadakimashita.","german":"After praying, I received a goshuin stamp.","furigana":"まい|ごしゅいん"},{"japanese":"最近、若い人の間で御朱印集めがはやっている。","romaji":"Saikin, wakai hito no aida de goshuin atsume ga hayatte iru.","german":"Recently, collecting goshuin has become popular among young people.","furigana":"さいきん|わか|ひと|あいだ|ごしゅいんあつ"}],"notes":"御朱印 (often written ご朱印) is the red seal stamp with brush calligraphy that you receive at shrines and temples as proof of your visit. Politely, you say 御朱印をいただく, not 買う, even though you pay a small amount. They are collected in a 御朱印帳; the hobby 御朱印集め has been very popular for some years.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"精進料理を食べました。","romaji":"Shōjin ryōri o tabemashita.","german":"I ate shojin ryori.","furigana":"しょうじんりょうり|た"},{"japanese":"精進料理には、肉や魚が一切使われていません。","romaji":"Shoujin ryouri ni wa, niku ya sakana ga issai tsukawarete imasen.","german":"No meat or fish is used at all in shojin ryori.","furigana":"しょうじんりょうり|にく|さかな|いっさいつか"},{"japanese":"精進料理は、ベジタリアンの外国人観光客にも人気がある。","romaji":"Shoujin ryouri wa, bejitarian no gaikokujin kankoukyaku ni mo ninki ga aru.","german":"Shojin ryori is also popular with vegetarian foreign tourists.","furigana":"しょうじんりょうり|がいこくじんかんこうきゃく|にんき"}],"notes":"精進料理 is Buddhist temple cuisine without meat and fish, traditionally also without strong-smelling ingredients such as leeks or garlic; typical are tofu, 湯葉 and ごま豆腐. 精進 originally means \"ascetic practice, devotion\" (精進する \"to strive diligently\"). It is eaten in temples or temple lodgings (宿坊); politely one says 精進料理をいただく.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"煎茶を入れましょう。","romaji":"Sencha o iremashō.","german":"Let's make some sencha.","furigana":"せんちゃ|い"},{"japanese":"煎茶は熱すぎないお湯でいれると、おいしくなります。","romaji":"Sencha wa atsusuginai oyu de ireru to, oishiku narimasu.","german":"Sencha tastes better when brewed with water that is not too hot.","furigana":"せんちゃ|あつ|ゆ"},{"japanese":"静岡は煎茶の産地として有名です。","romaji":"Shizuoka wa sencha no sanchi to shite yuumei desu.","german":"Shizuoka is famous as a sencha-growing region.","furigana":"しずおか|せんちゃ|さんち|ゆうめい"}],"notes":"煎茶 is ordinary Japanese leaf green tea made from steamed leaves, brewed with water that is not too hot (about 70-80 °C). In everyday speech people usually just say お茶 or 緑茶. It should be distinguished from 抹茶 (powdered tea), 玉露 (shade-grown top-grade tea), 番茶 (a plainer grade) and ほうじ茶 (roasted tea). \"To make tea\" is お茶を入れる, in formal writing with the kanji 淹れる.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"玄米茶の香りが好きです。","romaji":"Genmaicha no kaori ga suki desu.","german":"I like the aroma of genmaicha.","furigana":"げんまいちゃ|かお|す"},{"japanese":"寝る前は、カフェインが少ない玄米茶を飲むことが多いです。","romaji":"Neru mae wa, kafein ga sukunai genmaicha o nomu koto ga ooi desu.","german":"Before bed, I often drink genmaicha, which is low in caffeine.","furigana":"ね|まえ|すく|げんまいちゃ|の|おお"},{"japanese":"定食屋では、食事と一緒に温かい玄米茶が出てきた。","romaji":"Teishokuya de wa, shokuji to issho ni atatakai genmaicha ga dete kita.","german":"At the set-meal restaurant, warm genmaicha was served with the meal.","furigana":"ていしょくや|しょくじ|いっしょ|あたた|げんまいちゃ|で"}],"notes":"玄米茶 is composed of 玄米 \"brown rice\" and 茶 \"tea\": green tea (usually 番茶 or 煎茶) mixed with roasted rice grains, which gives it a nutty, toasty flavor. It is an inexpensive everyday tea with little caffeine and is often served free of charge in simple restaurants. Not to be confused with ほうじ茶 (\"roasted green tea\" without rice) or 抹茶 (\"powdered tea\"). One says 玄米茶を淹れる \"to brew genmaicha\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"醤油差しを取ってください。","romaji":"Shōyu sashi o totte kudasai.","german":"Please pass the soy sauce dispenser.","furigana":"しょうゆさ|と"},{"japanese":"醤油差しが空になったので、醤油を足しておきました。","romaji":"Shouyu sashi ga kara ni natta node, shouyu o tashite okimashita.","german":"The soy sauce dispenser was empty, so I refilled it.","furigana":"しょうゆさ|から|しょうゆ|た"},{"japanese":"倒してもこぼれない醤油差しを百円ショップで買った。","romaji":"Taoshite mo koborenai shouyu sashi o hyaku-en shoppu de katta.","german":"I bought a soy sauce dispenser at the 100-yen shop that doesn't spill even when knocked over.","furigana":"たお|しょうゆさ|ひゃくえん|か"}],"notes":"醤油差し is the small bottle or pitcher from which soy sauce is poured at the table; 差す here means \"to pour in, to refill\". It is found on every restaurant table and is also written しょうゆ差し or 醤油さし. Typical are 醤油差しを取る \"to pass the soy sauce dispenser\" and 醤油差しに醤油を入れる/足す \"to refill\". The sauce itself is just 醤油; the small dish for dipping is called 小皿.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"うどんに七味をかけます。","romaji":"Udon ni shichimi o kakemasu.","german":"I sprinkle shichimi on my udon.","furigana":"しちみ"},{"japanese":"牛丼に七味をたっぷりかけるのが好きだ。","romaji":"Gyuudon ni shichimi o tappuri kakeru no ga suki da.","german":"I like to sprinkle plenty of shichimi on my gyudon.","furigana":"ぎゅうどん|しちみ|す"},{"japanese":"辛いのが苦手なら、七味は少しだけにしてください。","romaji":"Karai no ga nigate nara, shichimi wa sukoshi dake ni shite kudasai.","german":"If you can't handle spicy food, please use only a little shichimi.","furigana":"から|にがて|しちみ|すこ"}],"notes":"七味 is the short form of 七味唐辛子, a spice blend of chili and six other ingredients such as sesame, sansho pepper and citrus peel. One sprinkles it with 七味をかける or 七味を振る over udon, soba, 牛丼 or yakitori. To be distinguished from it is 一味 (唐辛子), pure chili without other spices. The literal meaning \"seven flavors\" (sense 1) plays hardly any role in everyday life.","levelNote":"Compound: 味 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"昆布で出汁を取ります。","romaji":"Konbu de dashi o torimasu.","german":"I make stock from kelp.","furigana":"こんぶ|だし|と"},{"japanese":"おにぎりの具は、梅干しと昆布どちらがいいですか。","romaji":"Onigiri no gu wa, umeboshi to konbu dochira ga ii desu ka.","german":"Which onigiri filling would you like, pickled plum or kelp?","furigana":"ぐ|うめぼ|こんぶ"},{"japanese":"北海道のお土産に昆布を買ってきました。","romaji":"Hokkaidou no omiyage ni konbu o katte kimashita.","german":"I bought kelp as a souvenir from Hokkaido.","furigana":"ほっかいどう|みやげ|こんぶ|か"}],"notes":"昆布 is edible seaweed (kelp), which when dried is the basis of Japanese stock: 昆布で出汁を取る \"to make stock with kombu\", often together with 鰹節. It is also eaten as an onigiri filling or as 佃煮. In compounds there is the reading こぶ, e.g. 昆布茶. Other seaweeds have their own names: わかめ, のり, ひじき; the umbrella term is 海藻.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鰹節をかけます。","romaji":"Katsuobushi o kakemasu.","german":"I sprinkle bonito flakes on top.","furigana":"かつおぶし"},{"japanese":"熱いお好み焼きの上で、鰹節が踊っているように見えます。","romaji":"Atsui okonomiyaki no ue de, katsuobushi ga odotte iru you ni miemasu.","german":"On the hot okonomiyaki, the bonito flakes look like they are dancing.","furigana":"あつ|この|や|うえ|かつおぶし|おど|み"},{"japanese":"昆布と鰹節で出汁を取ると、味噌汁がおいしくなる。","romaji":"Konbu to katsuobushi de dashi o toru to, misoshiru ga oishiku naru.","german":"If you make the stock from kombu and bonito, the miso soup tastes better.","furigana":"こんぶ|かつおぶし|だし|と|みそしる"}],"notes":"鰹節 is boiled, smoked and rock-hard dried bonito; in everyday life it is almost always bought already shaved into flakes (削り節). Together with 昆布 it is the basis of 出汁: 鰹節で出汁を取る. It is also sprinkled over tofu, okonomiyaki or vegetables: 鰹節をかける/のせる. It is often written かつお節 or entirely in kana.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"糠漬けを作ります。","romaji":"Nukazuke o tsukurimasu.","german":"I make rice-bran pickles.","furigana":"ぬかづ|つく"},{"japanese":"祖母は毎日ぬか床をかき混ぜている。","romaji":"Sobo wa mainichi nukadoko o kakimazete iru.","german":"My grandmother stirs her pickling bran bed every day.","furigana":"そぼ|まいにち|どこ|ま"},{"japanese":"米ぬかは肌にいいと言われていて、昔から顔を洗うのに使われてきた。","romaji":"Komenuka wa hada ni ii to iwarete ite, mukashi kara kao o arau no ni tsukawarete kita.","german":"Rice bran is said to be good for the skin and has long been used for washing the face.","furigana":"こめ|はだ|い|むかし|かお|あら|つか"}],"notes":"糠 is the bran that comes off when rice is polished, usually called 米ぬか and almost always written in kana. It is best known through ぬか漬け, vegetables pickled in a fermenting bran bed (ぬか床), which must be stirred every day. Set phrases are ぬか喜び \"premature joy\" and ぬかに釘 \"futile, like a nail in bran\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"餡子のお菓子が好きです。","romaji":"Anko no okashi ga suki desu.","german":"I like sweets with bean paste.","furigana":"あんこ|かし|す"},{"japanese":"つぶあんとこしあん、どちらが好きですか。","romaji":"Tsubuan to koshian, dochira ga suki desu ka.","german":"Which do you like better, chunky or smooth bean paste?","furigana":"す"},{"japanese":"中華料理店で、あんかけ焼きそばを注文した。","romaji":"Chuuka ryouriten de, ankake yakisoba o chuumon shita.","german":"At the Chinese restaurant, I ordered fried noodles with thick sauce.","furigana":"ちゅうかりょうりてん|や|ちゅうもん"}],"notes":"餡 (mostly written あん) is above all the sweet paste made of red azuki beans; colloquially one says あんこ. A distinction is made between つぶあん \"with whole beans\" and こしあん \"strained, smooth\". More generally, あん also refers to the filling of dumplings (肉まんのあん) and a sauce thickened with starch, as in あんかけ. In kanji, the word is seen almost only on packaging.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お花見で団子を食べます。","romaji":"Ohanami de dango o tabemasu.","german":"I eat dango at the cherry blossom viewing.","furigana":"はなみ|だんご|た"},{"japanese":"駅前の和菓子屋でみたらし団子を三本買った。","romaji":"Ekimae no wagashiya de mitarashi dango o sanbon katta.","german":"I bought three skewers of mitarashi dango at the Japanese confectionery shop in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|わがしや|だんご|さんぼんか"},{"japanese":"子供たちはボールを追いかけて、団子になって走っている。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa booru o oikakete, dango ni natte hashitte iru.","german":"The children are running in a tight pack, chasing the ball.","furigana":"こども|お|だんご|はし"}],"notes":"団子 are small round dumplings made of rice flour, often three or four on a skewer, e.g. みたらし団子 with sweet soy sauce or 三色団子 for cherry blossom season. The proverb 花より団子 \"dumplings rather than blossoms\" means that practical things matter more to someone than beauty. Figuratively, 団子 stands for something round or a dense clump: 団子になる \"to bunch together\", お団子ヘア \"hair bun\". Skewers are counted with 本.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いちご大福が好きです。","romaji":"Ichigo daifuku ga suki desu.","german":"I like strawberry daifuku.","furigana":"だいふく|す"},{"japanese":"お茶の時間に、豆大福を半分ずつ食べた。","romaji":"Ocha no jikan ni, mamedaifuku o hanbun zutsu tabeta.","german":"At tea time, we shared a bean daifuku, half each.","furigana":"ちゃ|じかん|まめだいふく|はんぶん|た"},{"japanese":"大福は柔らかいので、お年寄りはのどに詰まらせないように気をつけてください。","romaji":"Daifuku wa yawarakai node, otoshiyori wa nodo ni tsumarasenai you ni ki o tsukete kudasai.","german":"Daifuku is soft, so elderly people should be careful not to choke on it.","furigana":"だいふく|やわ|としよ|つ|き"}],"notes":"大福 is the short form of 大福餅: soft mochi dough filled with sweet bean paste. Popular variants are いちご大福 with a whole strawberry and 豆大福 with beans in the dough. The literal meaning \"great fortune\" now occurs in everyday life practically only in names. Like all mochi sweets, 大福 is very chewy, which is why warnings about choking are issued, especially for older people.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"抹茶に羊羹が合います。","romaji":"Matcha ni yōkan ga aimasu.","german":"Yokan goes well with matcha.","furigana":"まっちゃ|ようかん|あ"},{"japanese":"夏は冷やした水羊羹がおいしい。","romaji":"Natsu wa hiyashita mizuyoukan ga oishii.","german":"In summer, chilled mizu-yokan tastes great.","furigana":"なつ|ひ|みずようかん"},{"japanese":"祖父の家に行くと、いつも厚く切った羊羹が出てくる。","romaji":"Sofu no ie ni iku to, itsumo atsuku kitta youkan ga dete kuru.","german":"When I visit my grandfather's house, thickly sliced yokan is always served.","furigana":"そふ|いえ|い|あつ|き|ようかん|で"}],"notes":"羊羹 is a firm, sliceable block of bean paste, agar and sugar, often also written ようかん. The classic is 練り羊羹; in summer one eats the softer, chilled 水羊羹. It is cut into slices (羊羹を切る) and served with green tea. Because it keeps for a long time, it is a popular gift and emergency food supply.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"温泉饅頭をお土産に買いました。","romaji":"Onsen manjū o omiyage ni kaimashita.","german":"I bought onsen manju as a souvenir.","furigana":"おんせんまんじゅう|みやげ|か"},{"japanese":"お客さんにお茶とお饅頭を出した。","romaji":"Okyakusan ni ocha to omanjuu o dashita.","german":"I served the guest tea and manju.","furigana":"きゃく|ちゃ|まんじゅう|だ"},{"japanese":"この饅頭は皮が薄くて、中のあんがたっぷり入っている。","romaji":"Kono manjuu wa kawa ga usukute, naka no an ga tappuri haitte iru.","german":"This manju has a thin skin and is generously filled with bean paste inside.","furigana":"まんじゅう|かわ|うす|なか|はい"}],"notes":"饅頭 is a small steamed or baked bun, in Japan mostly filled with sweet bean paste; politely one says お饅頭 and often writes まんじゅう. Almost every spa town sells 温泉饅頭 as a typical souvenir; there are also e.g. 栗饅頭. Savory Chinese variants are called 中華まん or 肉まん. The dough shell is called 皮, the filling あん.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"最中をお茶と一緒にいただきます。","romaji":"Monaka o ocha to issho ni itadakimasu.","german":"I enjoy monaka together with tea.","furigana":"もなか|ちゃ|いっしょ"},{"japanese":"この最中は皮がパリッとしていておいしい。","romaji":"Kono monaka wa kawa ga paritto shite ite oishii.","german":"The wafer shell of this monaka is nicely crisp and delicious.","furigana":"もなか|かわ"},{"japanese":"アイス最中は、皮が手につかないので食べやすい。","romaji":"Aisu monaka wa, kawa ga te ni tsukanai node tabeyasui.","german":"Ice cream monaka is easy to eat because the wafer doesn't stick to your hands.","furigana":"もなか|かわ|て|た"}],"notes":"With the reading もなか, 最中 is a sweet made of two crisp wafer shells of rice dough filled with bean paste. Note: the same kanji are much more often read さいちゅう and mean \"in the middle of\" (食事の最中に). That is why shops often write the sweet in kana as もなか. A modern variant is アイス最中 with ice cream instead of bean paste.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"金平糖は星のような形です。","romaji":"Konpeitō wa hoshi no yō na katachi desu.","german":"Konpeito has a star-like shape.","furigana":"こんぺいとう|ほし|かたち"},{"japanese":"瓶に入ったカラフルな金平糖をお土産にもらった。","romaji":"Bin ni haitta karafuru na konpeitou o omiyage ni moratta.","german":"I received a jar of colorful konpeito as a souvenir.","furigana":"びん|はい|こんぺいとう|みやげ"},{"japanese":"金平糖は作るのに二週間ぐらいかかるそうです。","romaji":"Konpeitou wa tsukuru no ni nishuukan gurai kakaru sou desu.","german":"Apparently it takes about two weeks to make konpeito.","furigana":"こんぺいとう|つく|にしゅうかん"}],"notes":"金平糖 are small, colorful sugar balls with spiky bumps that look like stars. The word comes from Portuguese \"confeito\"; the kanji were chosen only for their sound (ateji), so it is usually written こんぺいとう or コンペイトウ. Making it by hand takes several weeks because sugar syrup is applied layer by layer. It is a traditional souvenir and gift.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"夏には冷たい葛餅が美味しい。","romaji":"Natsu ni wa tsumetai kuzumochi ga oishii.","german":"Chilled kuzumochi is delicious in summer.","furigana":"なつ|つめ|くずもち|おい"},{"japanese":"葛餅にきな粉と黒蜜をかけて食べた。","romaji":"Kuzumochi ni kinako to kuromitsu o kakete tabeta.","german":"I ate the kuzumochi with soybean flour and dark syrup.","furigana":"くずもち|こ|くろみつ|た"},{"japanese":"川崎大師のお土産といえば、葛餅が有名です。","romaji":"Kawasaki Daishi no omiyage to ieba, kuzumochi ga yuumei desu.","german":"When it comes to souvenirs from Kawasaki Daishi Temple, kuzumochi is famous.","furigana":"かわさきだいし|みやげ|くずもち|ゆうめい"}],"notes":"葛餅 is a translucent, soft and springy sweet, traditionally made from kudzu starch (葛粉), eaten with きな粉 \"roasted soybean flour\" and 黒蜜 \"dark sugar syrup\". In the Tokyo area a similar but cloudy kind made from fermented wheat starch is also called くず餅, known for example from Kawasaki. It is often eaten chilled in summer. The word is also written くず餅.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"懐石料理を予約しました。","romaji":"Kaiseki ryōri o yoyaku shimashita.","german":"I reserved a kaiseki meal.","furigana":"かいせきりょうり|よやく"},{"japanese":"旅館の夕食は、季節の食材を使った懐石料理だった。","romaji":"Ryokan no yuushoku wa, kisetsu no shokuzai o tsukatta kaiseki ryouri datta.","german":"Dinner at the ryokan was a kaiseki meal using seasonal ingredients.","furigana":"りょかん|ゆうしょく|きせつ|しょくざい|つか|かいせきりょうり"},{"japanese":"懐石料理は一品ずつ順番に出てくるので、時間がかかる。","romaji":"Kaiseki ryouri wa ippin zutsu junban ni dete kuru node, jikan ga kakaru.","german":"A kaiseki meal takes a long time because the dishes come out one at a time in order.","furigana":"かいせきりょうり|いっぴん|じゅんばん|で|じかん"}],"notes":"懐石料理 was originally the simple meal before the tea ceremony (茶懐石); today it usually means an upscale menu with many small, seasonal courses served one after another. It is eaten at ryokan or 料亭. Pronounced the same but written differently is 会席料理, a banquet menu in which alcohol is in the foreground; the two are often confused.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"割烹料理店に行きました。","romaji":"Kappō ryōriten ni ikimashita.","german":"I went to a kappo restaurant.","furigana":"かっぽうりょうりてん|い"},{"japanese":"カウンターだけの小さな割烹で、板前さんと話しながら食べた。","romaji":"Kauntaa dake no chiisa na kappou de, itamae-san to hanashinagara tabeta.","german":"We ate at a small counter-only kappo place, chatting with the chef.","furigana":"ちい|かっぽう|いたまえ|はな|た"},{"japanese":"祖母はいつも白い割烹着を着て台所に立っていた。","romaji":"Sobo wa itsumo shiroi kappougi o kite daidokoro ni tatte ita.","german":"My grandmother always stood in the kitchen wearing her white apron.","furigana":"そぼ|しろ|かっぽうぎ|き|だいどころ|た"}],"notes":"割烹 literally means \"to cut and cook\" and stands for the Japanese culinary art and for the restaurants that practice it. Typical is the chef who prepares food in front of the guests behind a counter (カウンター割烹); this is somewhat more casual than an exclusive 料亭. As a standalone word it is mostly heard in 割烹料理店 or in restaurant names. Also well known is 割烹着, the white apron with sleeves.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"居酒屋で飲みましょう。","romaji":"Izakaya de nomimashō.","german":"Let's have a drink at an izakaya.","furigana":"いざかや|の"},{"japanese":"会社の飲み会は駅前の居酒屋で行われた。","romaji":"Kaisha no nomikai wa ekimae no izakaya de okonawareta.","german":"The company drinking party was held at an izakaya in front of the station.","furigana":"かいしゃ|の|かい|えきまえ|いざかや|おこな"},{"japanese":"居酒屋では、席に座るとお通しという小さい料理が出てくる。","romaji":"Izakaya de wa, seki ni suwaru to otooshi to iu chiisai ryouri ga dete kuru.","german":"At an izakaya, once you sit down, a small dish called otoshi is served.","furigana":"いざかや|せき|すわ|とお|ちい|りょうり|で"}],"notes":"居酒屋 is a Japanese pub where you order many small dishes with beer, sake or highballs; it is the typical place for a 飲み会 with coworkers or friends. There is often 飲み放題 \"all-you-can-drink\", and when you sit down you get a starter that is charged for (お通し). Not to be confused with 酒屋, the liquor store. Colloquially, 飲み屋 is also used as a general term.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"屋台でラーメンを食べました。","romaji":"Yatai de rāmen o tabemashita.","german":"I ate ramen at a food stall.","furigana":"やたい|た"},{"japanese":"夏祭りでは、神社の前にたくさんの屋台が並ぶ。","romaji":"Natsumatsuri de wa, jinja no mae ni takusan no yatai ga narabu.","german":"At the summer festival, many stalls line up in front of the shrine.","furigana":"なつまつ|じんじゃ|まえ|やたい|なら"},{"japanese":"文化祭でクラスの友達と焼きそばの屋台を出した。","romaji":"Bunkasai de kurasu no tomodachi to yakisoba no yatai o dashita.","german":"At the school festival, my classmates and I set up a yakisoba stall.","furigana":"ぶんかさい|ともだち|や|やたい|だ"}],"notes":"屋台 is a small, often mobile sales stand, mainly for food: at festivals (祭りの屋台) or as a ramen or oden stall, for example in Fukuoka. Typical combinations are 屋台が並ぶ \"stalls line up\" and 屋台を出す \"to set up a stall\". At festivals, 屋台 can also refer to a decorated festival float. Figuratively, 屋台骨 is the \"backbone\" of a family or company.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子供が風車で遊んでいます。","romaji":"Kodomo ga kazaguruma de asondeimasu.","german":"The children are playing with pinwheels.","furigana":"こども|かざぐるま|あそ"},{"japanese":"風が吹くと、色とりどりの風車がいっせいに回り始めた。","romaji":"Kaze ga fuku to, irotoridori no kazaguruma ga issei ni mawarihajimeta.","german":"When the wind blew, the colorful pinwheels all began to turn at once.","furigana":"かぜ|ふ|いろ|かざぐるま|まわ|はじ"},{"japanese":"折り紙で風車を作って、孫にあげた。","romaji":"Origami de kazaguruma o tsukutte, mago ni ageta.","german":"I made a pinwheel from origami and gave it to my grandchild.","furigana":"お|がみ|かざぐるま|つく|まご"}],"notes":"With the reading かざぐるま, 風車 is above all the colorful pinwheel, a children's toy that spins in the wind: 風車が回る. The real windmill or wind turbine, by contrast, is mostly read ふうしゃ, e.g. オランダの風車 or 風力発電の風車. Pinwheels are seen at festivals and in temples; they are also made from origami.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"正月に独楽を回します。","romaji":"Shōgatsu ni koma o mawashimasu.","german":"People spin tops at New Year.","furigana":"しょうがつ|こま|まわ"},{"japanese":"こまがなかなか止まらずに、ずっと回り続けている。","romaji":"Koma ga nakanaka tomarazu ni, zutto mawaritsuzukete iru.","german":"The top won't stop and just keeps spinning.","furigana":"と|まわ|つづ"},{"japanese":"祖父に独楽の回し方を教えてもらった。","romaji":"Sofu ni koma no mawashikata o oshiete moratta.","german":"My grandfather taught me how to spin a top.","furigana":"そふ|こま|まわ|かた|おし"}],"notes":"独楽 is a spinning top; the kanji spelling is rare, and こま or コマ are usual. One says こまを回す \"to spin a top\" and こまが回る \"the top spins\". こま回し is, together with 凧揚げ, one of the traditional New Year games. Homophones are 駒 \"game piece (shogi)\" and コマ \"class period, single frame\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"凧を揚げます。","romaji":"Tako o agemasu.","german":"I fly a kite.","furigana":"たこ|あ"},{"japanese":"お正月に、河原で子供たちと凧揚げをした。","romaji":"Oshougatsu ni, kawara de kodomotachi to takoage o shita.","german":"At New Year, I flew kites with the children by the riverbank.","furigana":"しょうがつ||こども|たこあ"},{"japanese":"風が強すぎて、凧の糸が切れてしまった。","romaji":"Kaze ga tsuyosugite, tako no ito ga kirete shimatta.","german":"The wind was so strong that the kite string broke.","furigana":"かぜ|つよ|たこ|いと|き"}],"notes":"凧 is the kite for flying. The fixed combination is 凧を揚げる (with 揚げる, not 上げる); the noun for it is 凧揚げ, a classic New Year game. It is often written たこ, but this can be confused with 蛸 \"octopus\". The string is called 凧の糸.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"デパートで福袋を買いました。","romaji":"Depāto de fukubukuro o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a lucky bag at the department store.","furigana":"ふくぶくろ|か"},{"japanese":"元日の朝から、人気の福袋を買うために長い列ができていた。","romaji":"Ganjitsu no asa kara, ninki no fukubukuro o kau tame ni nagai retsu ga dekite ita.","german":"A long line had formed already on the morning of New Year's Day to buy the popular lucky bags.","furigana":"がんじつ|あさ|にんき|ふくぶくろ|か|なが|れつ"},{"japanese":"今年の福袋は中身が当たりで、得した気分だ。","romaji":"Kotoshi no fukubukuro wa nakami ga atari de, toku shita kibun da.","german":"The contents of my lucky bag this year were a jackpot, and I feel like I got a good deal.","furigana":"ことし|ふくぶくろ|なかみ|あ|とく|きぶん"}],"notes":"福袋 literally means \"lucky bag\": a sealed bag that shops offer mainly at the New Year sale (初売り) and whose contents are usually worth more than the price. One speaks of 福袋の中身 \"contents\" and of whether it was a 当たり \"hit\" or a はずれ \"dud\". For popular brands, customers line up early on January 1 (福袋に並ぶ).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"年越しそばを食べます。","romaji":"Toshikoshi soba o tabemasu.","german":"I eat New Year's Eve soba.","furigana":"としこ|た"},{"japanese":"今年の年越しは、友達とカウントダウンイベントで過ごすつもりだ。","romaji":"Kotoshi no toshikoshi wa, tomodachi to kauntodaun ibento de sugosu tsumori da.","german":"This year I plan to spend the turn of the year with friends at a countdown event.","furigana":"ことし|としこ|ともだち|す"},{"japanese":"実家で家族と年越しをするのは三年ぶりです。","romaji":"Jikka de kazoku to toshikoshi o suru no wa sannen buri desu.","german":"It's the first time in three years that I'm spending New Year's Eve with my family at my parents' house.","furigana":"じっか|かぞく|としこ|さんねん"}],"notes":"年越し means \"to see in the new year\", that is, passing into the new year on the night of 大晦日. The best-known term is 年越しそば, soba noodles eaten on New Year's Eve for a long life. One can say 年越しをする or express it with the verb 年を越す. The date December 31 itself is called 大晦日, the New Year days お正月.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"年末年始は帰省します。","romaji":"Nenmatsu nenshi wa kisei shimasu.","german":"I go home over the New Year holidays.","furigana":"ねんまつねんし|きせい"},{"japanese":"年末年始の営業時間はホームページでご確認ください。","romaji":"Nenmatsu nenshi no eigyou jikan wa hoomupeeji de gokakunin kudasai.","german":"Please check our business hours over the year-end and New Year holidays on our website.","furigana":"ねんまつねんし|えいぎょうじかん|かくにん"},{"japanese":"年末年始に海外旅行をすると、飛行機代がとても高い。","romaji":"Nenmatsu nenshi ni kaigai ryokou o suru to, hikoukidai ga totemo takai.","german":"If you travel abroad over the year-end and New Year holidays, airfares are very high.","furigana":"ねんまつねんし|かいがいりょこう|ひこうきだい|たか"}],"notes":"年末年始 is the period around the turn of the year, roughly from December 29 to January 3, when government offices and many companies close. One often reads it on notices: 年末年始の営業時間 \"business hours over the holidays\", 年末年始休業 \"closure for the year-end and New Year holidays\". As a time expression it is used with は or に. It is more general than お正月, which means only the New Year days.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お盆に帰省します。","romaji":"Obon ni kisei shimasu.","german":"I go back to my hometown for Obon.","furigana":"ぼん|きせい"},{"japanese":"帰省ラッシュで、新幹線の自由席は満員だった。","romaji":"Kisei rasshu de, shinkansen no jiyuuseki wa man'in datta.","german":"Because of the holiday travel rush, the non-reserved seats on the Shinkansen were full.","furigana":"きせい|しんかんせん|じゆうせき|まんいん"},{"japanese":"今年の夏は仕事が忙しくて、実家に帰省できなかった。","romaji":"Kotoshi no natsu wa shigoto ga isogashikute, jikka ni kisei dekinakatta.","german":"This summer I was so busy with work that I couldn't go back to my parents' home.","furigana":"ことし|なつ|しごと|いそが|じっか|きせい"}],"notes":"帰省 is the trip home to one's hometown or parents' house (実家), typically at お盆 or 年末年始; as an intransitive する-verb: 実家に帰省する. The travel rush for it is called 帰省ラッシュ. To be distinguished are 帰宅 \"to come home\" (daily), 帰国 \"to return to one's home country\" and 里帰り, which especially stresses the visit to one's own parents after marriage.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"里帰り出産をしました。","romaji":"Satogaeri shussan o shimashita.","german":"I gave birth at my parents' home.","furigana":"さとがえ|しゅっさん"},{"japanese":"妻は子供を連れて、一週間ほど里帰りしている。","romaji":"Tsuma wa kodomo o tsurete, isshuukan hodo satogaeri shite iru.","german":"My wife has gone back to her parents' with the kids for about a week.","furigana":"つま|こども|つ|いっしゅうかん|さとがえ"},{"japanese":"海外に渡った絵が、百年ぶりに日本に里帰りした。","romaji":"Kaigai ni watatta e ga, hyakunen buri ni Nihon ni satogaeri shita.","german":"A painting that had gone abroad has come back to Japan for the first time in a hundred years.","furigana":"かいがい|わた|え|ひゃくねん|にほん|さとがえ"}],"notes":"里帰り is a return to one's parental home, originally mainly that of a married woman visiting her own parents (里 \"home village, parental home\"). Today it is best known as 里帰り出産: the woman moves in with her parents for a few weeks around the birth. As an intransitive する-verb: 実家に里帰りする. Figuratively it is also said of artworks that temporarily return to their country of origin. The more general, neutral word is 帰省.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"土産話を聞かせてください。","romaji":"Miyagebanashi o kikasete kudasai.","german":"Please tell me about your trip.","furigana":"みやげばなし|き"},{"japanese":"旅行から帰った姉は、夕食の間ずっと土産話をしていた。","romaji":"Ryokou kara kaetta ane wa, yuushoku no aida zutto miyagebanashi o shite ita.","german":"My sister, back from her trip, told stories about it throughout dinner.","furigana":"りょこう|かえ|あね|ゆうしょく|あいだ|みやげばなし"},{"japanese":"気をつけて行ってきてね。土産話、楽しみにしてるよ。","romaji":"Ki o tsukete itte kite ne. Miyagebanashi, tanoshimi ni shiteru yo.","german":"Have a safe trip. I'm looking forward to hearing your stories.","furigana":"き|い|みやげばなし|たの"}],"notes":"土産話 are the stories one \"brings back\" from a trip like a souvenir (土産). Typical are 土産話をする \"to tell about the trip\", 土産話を聞かせる \"to let someone hear (the stories)\" and 土産話を楽しみにしている \"to look forward to the travel stories\", often as a farewell to someone going on a trip. It sounds warm and personal, not like a formal 旅行記 \"travelogue\".","levelNote":"Compound: 土産 (N3) + 話 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この地域の名産品は何ですか。","romaji":"Kono chiiki no meisanhin wa nan desu ka.","german":"What is this region's specialty?","furigana":"ちいき|めいさんひん|なん"},{"japanese":"青森はりんごの名産地として知られている。","romaji":"Aomori wa ringo no meisanchi to shite shirarete iru.","german":"Aomori is known as a famous apple-producing area.","furigana":"あおもり|めいさんち|し"},{"japanese":"駅の売店には、地元の名産がたくさん並んでいた。","romaji":"Eki no baiten ni wa, jimoto no meisan ga takusan narande ita.","german":"The station kiosk displayed lots of local specialties.","furigana":"えき|ばいてん|じもと|めいさん|なら"}],"notes":"名産 is a product known for a region, usually food or crafts: 〜の名産, 名産品, 名産地 \"famous production area\". Similar is 特産 \"specialty produced mainly there\", which sounds more matter-of-fact. 名物 is broader and means anything a place is famous for, including dishes you eat on the spot, or even people.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"秘湯を巡る旅をしました。","romaji":"Hitō o meguru tabi o shimashita.","german":"I took a trip visiting secluded hot springs.","furigana":"ひとう|めぐ|たび"},{"japanese":"その秘湯へは、車を降りてから山道を一時間歩かなければならない。","romaji":"Sono hitou e wa, kuruma o orite kara yamamichi o ichijikan arukanakereba naranai.","german":"To get to that secluded hot spring, you have to walk a mountain path for an hour after getting out of the car.","furigana":"ひとう|くるま|お|やまみち|いちじかんある"},{"japanese":"テレビで紹介されてから、この秘湯にも観光客が増えてしまった。","romaji":"Terebi de shoukai sarete kara, kono hitou ni mo kankoukyaku ga fuete shimatta.","german":"Since it was featured on TV, more tourists have come to this secluded hot spring too.","furigana":"しょうかい|ひとう|かんこうきゃく|ふ"}],"notes":"秘湯, from 秘 \"secret\" and 湯 \"hot water\", is a remote, little-known hot spring, often deep in the mountains and hard to reach. Common are 秘湯巡り \"tour of secluded hot springs\", 山奥の秘湯 and 秘湯の宿. The general term is 温泉; touristy spa towns with many hotels are called 温泉街. The word has a romantic, advertising ring.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"宿坊に泊まりました。","romaji":"Shukubō ni tomarimashita.","german":"I stayed at a temple lodging.","furigana":"しゅくぼう|と"},{"japanese":"高野山の宿坊では、夕食に精進料理が出た。","romaji":"Kouyasan no shukubou de wa, yuushoku ni shoujin ryouri ga deta.","german":"At the temple lodging on Koyasan, vegetarian temple cuisine was served for dinner.","furigana":"こうやさん|しゅくぼう|ゆうしょく|しょうじんりょうり|で"},{"japanese":"宿坊では、朝早くお坊さんと一緒にお経を聞くことができる。","romaji":"Shukubou de wa, asa hayaku obousan to issho ni okyou o kiku koto ga dekiru.","german":"At a temple lodging, you can listen to sutra chanting with the monks early in the morning.","furigana":"しゅくぼう|あさはや|ぼう|いっしょ|きょう|き"}],"notes":"宿坊 is lodging in a Buddhist temple, originally for monks and pilgrims and now also for tourists. A stay usually includes 精進料理 \"vegetarian temple cuisine\" and taking part in the morning service (朝のお勤め). One says 宿坊に泊まる. The 宿坊 on 高野山 are particularly well known. An ordinary Japanese inn, by contrast, is a 旅館.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"城下町を散策しました。","romaji":"Jōkamachi o sansaku shimashita.","german":"I strolled around the castle town.","furigana":"じょうかまち|さんさく"},{"japanese":"金沢は昔、大きな城下町として栄えた。","romaji":"Kanazawa wa mukashi, ookina joukamachi to shite sakaeta.","german":"Kanazawa once flourished as a large castle town.","furigana":"かなざわ|むかし|おお|じょうかまち|さか"},{"japanese":"この城下町には、今も古い武家屋敷が残っている。","romaji":"Kono joukamachi ni wa, ima mo furui buke yashiki ga nokotte iru.","german":"Old samurai residences still remain in this castle town.","furigana":"じょうかまち|いま|ふる|ぶけやしき|のこ"}],"notes":"城下町 literally means \"the town below the castle\": a town that developed around a lord's castle in feudal times, e.g. 金沢, 松本 or 彦根. One speaks of 城下町の町並み \"the townscape\" and says 〜の城下町として栄えた \"flourished as the castle town of ...\". Similarly formed are 門前町 \"temple town\" and 宿場町 \"post town\".","levelNote":"Compound: 城下 (N1) + 町 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"門前町で買い物をしました。","romaji":"Monzenmachi de kaimono o shimashita.","german":"I went shopping in the temple town.","furigana":"もんぜんまち|か|もの"},{"japanese":"長野市は善光寺の門前町として発展した。","romaji":"Naganoshi wa Zenkouji no monzenmachi to shite hatten shita.","german":"The city of Nagano developed as a temple town around Zenko-ji.","furigana":"ながのし|ぜんこうじ|もんぜんまち|はってん"},{"japanese":"門前町の参道には、お土産屋や団子屋が並んでいる。","romaji":"Monzenmachi no sandou ni wa, omiyageya ya dangoya ga narande iru.","german":"Along the approach to the temple in the temple town, souvenir shops and dango shops line up.","furigana":"もんぜんまち|さんどう|みやげや|だんごや|なら"}],"notes":"門前町 is a town that arose in front of the gate (門前) of a large temple or shrine because pilgrims stayed overnight and shopped there; examples are 長野 at 善光寺 or 成田 at 新勝寺. The main street to the temple is called 参道 and is usually lined with souvenir shops and snack stands. 町 is read まち here. Comparable types of towns are 城下町 and 宿場町.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"昔の宿場町を見学しました。","romaji":"Mukashi no shukubamachi o kengaku shimashita.","german":"I toured an old post town.","furigana":"むかし|しゅくばまち|けんがく"},{"japanese":"妻籠には、江戸時代の宿場町の町並みがよく残っている。","romaji":"Tsumago ni wa, Edo jidai no shukubamachi no machinami ga yoku nokotte iru.","german":"In Tsumago, the townscape of an Edo-period post town is well preserved.","furigana":"つまご|えどじだい|しゅくばまち|まちな|のこ"},{"japanese":"宿場町には、旅人が泊まる宿がたくさんあった。","romaji":"Shukubamachi ni wa, tabibito ga tomaru yado ga takusan atta.","german":"In post towns, there were many inns for travelers.","furigana":"しゅくばまち|たびびと|と|やど"}],"notes":"宿場町 is a historical post town on the major highways (街道) of the Edo period, where travelers stayed overnight and horses were changed; the station itself is called 宿場. Well-preserved examples are 妻籠 and 馬籠 on the 中山道. One speaks of 宿場町の町並み. Related town types are 城下町 and 門前町.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"棚田の風景が美しいです。","romaji":"Tanada no fūkei ga utsukushii desu.","german":"The scenery of the terraced rice fields is beautiful.","furigana":"たなだ|ふうけい|うつく"},{"japanese":"山の斜面に、小さな棚田が何段も続いている。","romaji":"Yama no shamen ni, chiisa na tanada ga nandan mo tsuzuite iru.","german":"Countless small terraced fields continue in tiers up the mountain slope.","furigana":"やま|しゃめん|ちい|たなだ|なんだん|つづ"},{"japanese":"機械が入りにくいので、棚田の米作りは手間がかかる。","romaji":"Kikai ga hairinikui node, tanada no komezukuri wa tema ga kakaru.","german":"Because machines can hardly get in, growing rice on terraces takes a lot of work.","furigana":"きかい|はい|たなだ|こめづく|てま"}],"notes":"棚田, from 棚 \"shelf, step\" and 田 \"rice field\", are rice fields laid out in terraces on mountain slopes; a great many small terraces are also called 千枚田. They are regarded as a typical image of rural beauty, but are laborious to farm because machines can hardly get in. Common expressions are 棚田の風景, 棚田が続く and 棚田を守る.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"里山の自然を守ります。","romaji":"Satoyama no shizen o mamorimasu.","german":"We protect the nature of the satoyama.","furigana":"さとやま|しぜん|まも"},{"japanese":"週末は家族で近くの里山を歩いて、虫や鳥を観察した。","romaji":"Shuumatsu wa kazoku de chikaku no satoyama o aruite, mushi ya tori o kansatsu shita.","german":"On weekends, my family walked through the nearby satoyama and observed insects and birds.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|かぞく|ちか|さとやま|ある|むし|とり|かんさつ"},{"japanese":"人が手を入れなくなって、里山が荒れてきている。","romaji":"Hito ga te o irenaku natte, satoyama ga arete kite iru.","german":"Since people stopped looking after it, the satoyama has been becoming overgrown.","furigana":"ひと|て|い|さとやま|あ"}],"notes":"里山, from 里 \"village\" and 山 \"mountain\", is the landscape used by humans, consisting of groves, fields and ponds at the edge of villages, from which firewood and leaves were formerly gathered. Today the word is mainly a term from nature conservation and environmental education: 里山を守る, 里山の自然. The opposite term is 奥山 \"untouched, deep mountain land\". If the 里山 is no longer tended, one says 里山が荒れる \"it becomes overgrown\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"渓谷を歩きました。","romaji":"Keikoku o arukimashita.","german":"I walked through the gorge.","furigana":"けいこく|ある"},{"japanese":"秋になると、この渓谷は紅葉を見に来る人でにぎわう。","romaji":"Aki ni naru to, kono keikoku wa kouyou o mi ni kuru hito de nigiwau.","german":"In autumn, this gorge is bustling with people coming to see the autumn leaves.","furigana":"あき|けいこく|こうよう|み|く|ひと"},{"japanese":"渓谷にかかるつり橋は、少し揺れて怖かった。","romaji":"Keikoku ni kakaru tsuribashi wa, sukoshi yurete kowakatta.","german":"The suspension bridge over the gorge swayed a little, which was scary.","furigana":"けいこく|ばし|すこ|ゆ|こわ"}],"notes":"渓谷 is a valley with a river flowing through it, usually with steep slopes: a gorge. Popular are 紅葉の渓谷 in autumn, 渓谷沿いの遊歩道 \"walking trail along the gorge\" and the word 渓谷美 \"beauty of the gorge\". More general is 谷 \"valley\"; 峡谷 stresses a particularly narrow, deep gorge.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"峠を越えました。","romaji":"Tōge o koemashita.","german":"I crossed the mountain pass.","furigana":"とうげ|こ"},{"japanese":"熱は下がってきたので、病気の峠は越したようです。","romaji":"Netsu wa sagatte kita node, byouki no touge wa koshita you desu.","german":"My fever is going down, so the worst of the illness seems to be over.","furigana":"ねつ|さ|びょうき|とうげ|こ"},{"japanese":"峠の茶屋で一休みしてから、山を下りた。","romaji":"Touge no chaya de hitoyasumi shite kara, yama o orita.","german":"After a short rest at the teahouse on the pass, we went down the mountain.","furigana":"とうげ|ちゃや|ひとやす|やま|お"}],"notes":"峠 is the highest point of a road or path over a mountain, i.e. a pass; in names it appears as a suffix, e.g. 碓氷峠. One says 峠を越える \"to cross the pass\". Figuratively, 峠 means the critical peak: 峠を越す \"the worst is over\" (illness, heat, work), 今夜が峠だ \"tonight is the critical point\". The kanji is a Japanese creation made from 山, 上 and 下.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"岬からの眺めが素晴らしいです。","romaji":"Misaki kara no nagame ga subarashii desu.","german":"The view from the cape is wonderful.","furigana":"みさき|なが|すば"},{"japanese":"岬の先に白い灯台が立っている。","romaji":"Misaki no saki ni shiroi toudai ga tatte iru.","german":"A white lighthouse stands at the tip of the cape.","furigana":"みさき|さき|しろ|とうだい|た"},{"japanese":"足摺岬は四国の一番南にある。","romaji":"Ashizuri misaki wa Shikoku no ichiban minami ni aru.","german":"Cape Ashizuri is at the southernmost point of Shikoku.","furigana":"|しこく|いちばんみなみ"}],"notes":"岬 is a cape, a point of land jutting into the sea; in place names it appears as 〜岬, e.g. 足摺岬 or 宗谷岬. Typical expressions are 岬の先, 岬の灯台 and 岬からの眺め. A larger projection of land is a 半島 \"peninsula\". In many names 崎 alone is also used for a point of land.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"静かな入り江があります。","romaji":"Shizuka na irie ga arimasu.","german":"There is a quiet inlet.","furigana":"しず|い|え"},{"japanese":"漁船が入り江に静かに停まっている。","romaji":"Gyosen ga irie ni shizuka ni tomatte iru.","german":"Fishing boats are lying quietly in the inlet.","furigana":"ぎょせん|い|え|しず|と"},{"japanese":"入り江は波が穏やかなので、子供でも安心して泳げる。","romaji":"Irie wa nami ga odayaka na node, kodomo demo anshin shite oyogeru.","german":"The waves in the inlet are calm, so even children can swim safely.","furigana":"い|え|なみ|おだ|こども|あんしん|およ"}],"notes":"入り江, from 入る and 江 \"inlet\", is a small bay reaching into the land, usually with calm water; also written 入江. Typical expressions are 静かな入り江 and 入り江に船が停まる. Larger bays are called 湾, e.g. in names such as 東京湾. The word sounds rather descriptive and scenic.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"干潟で貝を拾います。","romaji":"Higata de kai o hiroimasu.","german":"Im Watt sammle ich Muscheln.","furigana":"ひがた|かい|ひろ"},{"japanese":"潮が引くと、海岸に広い干潟が現れる。","romaji":"Shio ga hiku to, kaigan ni hiroi higata ga arawareru.","german":"Bei Ebbe taucht an der Küste ein weites Watt auf.","furigana":"しお|ひ|かいがん|ひろ|ひがた|あらわ"},{"japanese":"干潟は渡り鳥にとって大切なえさ場だ。","romaji":"Higata wa wataridori ni totte taisetsu na esaba da.","german":"Das Watt ist für Zugvögel ein wichtiger Futterplatz.","furigana":"ひがた|わた|どり|たいせつ|ば"}],"notes":"干潟 ist eine Wattfläche aus Schlick oder Sand, die bei Ebbe trocken fällt (潮が引く) und bei Flut überspült wird. Dort sammelt man Muscheln (潮干狩り), und viele Zugvögel (渡り鳥) finden Nahrung, weshalb 干潟 oft im Naturschutz erwähnt wird. Bekannt ist z. B. das Watt im 有明海. Das deutsche „Wattenmeer“ als Name einer bestimmten Region ist enger als 干潟.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"湿原を散歩しました。","romaji":"Shitsugen o sanpo shimashita.","german":"I took a walk in the wetland.","furigana":"しつげん|さんぽ"},{"japanese":"釧路湿原では、タンチョウを見ることができる。","romaji":"Kushiro shitsugen de wa, tanchou o miru koto ga dekiru.","german":"At Kushiro Marsh you can see red-crowned cranes.","furigana":"|み"},{"japanese":"湿原の植物を傷つけないように、木の道の上を歩いてください。","romaji":"Shitsugen no shokubutsu o kizutsukenai you ni, ki no michi no ue o aruite kudasai.","german":"Please walk on the wooden boardwalk so as not to damage the wetland plants.","furigana":"しつげん|しょくぶつ|きず|き|みち|うえ|ある"}],"notes":"湿原 is a wet grassland, a marsh or wetland with its own plant life; famous ones are 釧路湿原 in Hokkaido and 尾瀬. Visitors walk on wooden boardwalks there to protect the plants. The more general, rather technical word is 湿地 \"wetland\"; 沼 is a swamp or pond.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"原生林を保護します。","romaji":"Genseirin o hogo shimasu.","german":"We protect the virgin forest.","furigana":"げんせいりん|ほご"},{"japanese":"白神山地には、ブナの原生林が広がっている。","romaji":"Shirakami sanchi ni wa, buna no genseirin ga hirogatte iru.","german":"A virgin beech forest spreads across the Shirakami Mountains.","furigana":"|げんせいりん|ひろ"},{"japanese":"原生林の中は昼でも暗く、道に迷いやすい。","romaji":"Genseirin no naka wa hiru demo kuraku, michi ni mayoiyasui.","german":"Inside the virgin forest it is dark even in the daytime, and it is easy to lose your way.","furigana":"げんせいりん|なか|ひる|くら|みち|まよ"}],"notes":"原生林 is a forest untouched by humans, a primeval forest; well-known examples in Japan are the beech forest of 白神山地 and the forests of 屋久島. Typical are ブナの原生林, 原生林が広がる and 原生林を守る. The opposite term is 人工林 \"planted forest\"; forest in general is 森 or 森林.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"富士山の樹海を歩きました。","romaji":"Fujisan no jukai o arukimashita.","german":"I walked through the sea of trees at Mt. Fuji.","furigana":"ふじさん|じゅかい|ある"},{"japanese":"山の上から見ると、緑の樹海がどこまでも続いていた。","romaji":"Yama no ue kara miru to, midori no jukai ga doko made mo tsuzuite ita.","german":"Seen from the mountaintop, a green sea of trees stretched as far as the eye could see.","furigana":"やま|うえ|み|みどり|じゅかい|つづ"},{"japanese":"樹海ではコンパスが使えないという話があるが、実際は使えるそうだ。","romaji":"Jukai de wa konpasu ga tsukaenai to iu hanashi ga aru ga, jissai wa tsukaeru sou da.","german":"There is a story that compasses don't work in the forest, but apparently they do work in reality.","furigana":"じゅかい|つか|はなし|じっさい|つか"}],"notes":"樹海 literally means \"sea of trees\": a huge, dense forest that looks like a green sea from above. In everyday life one almost always thinks of the 青木ヶ原樹海 at the foot of Mt. Fuji. In general it describes wide expanses of forest, e.g. 緑の樹海が広がる. It is a rather figurative word; neutrally one says 森 or 森林.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"川が氾濫しました。","romaji":"Kawa ga hanran shimashita.","german":"The river overflowed.","furigana":"かわ|はんらん"},{"japanese":"大雨で川が氾濫するおそれがあるので、早めに避難してください。","romaji":"Ooame de kawa ga hanran suru osore ga aru node, hayame ni hinan shite kudasai.","german":"The river may overflow because of the heavy rain, so please evacuate early.","furigana":"おおあめ|かわ|はんらん|はや|ひなん"},{"japanese":"インターネットには間違った情報が氾濫している。","romaji":"Intaanetto ni wa machigatta jouhou ga hanran shite iru.","german":"The internet is flooded with false information.","furigana":"まちが|じょうほう|はんらん"}],"notes":"氾濫 is a river overflowing its banks; as an intransitive する-verb: 川が氾濫する. In weather warnings one hears 氾濫のおそれ \"risk of overflow\". Figuratively, and mostly negatively, it stands for an oversupply: 情報が氾濫する \"a flood of information\". 洪水 rather means the flood as a disaster, 浸水 water entering buildings.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大雨で土砂崩れが起きました。","romaji":"Ōame de dosha kuzure ga okimashita.","german":"A landslide occurred because of heavy rain.","furigana":"おおあめ|どしゃくず|お"},{"japanese":"土砂崩れで道路が通行止めになっている。","romaji":"Dosha kuzure de douro ga tsuukoudome ni natte iru.","german":"The road is closed because of a landslide.","furigana":"どしゃくず|どうろ|つうこうど"},{"japanese":"地震の後は、土砂崩れに注意してください。","romaji":"Jishin no ato wa, dosha kuzure ni chuui shite kudasai.","german":"After an earthquake, please be careful of landslides.","furigana":"じしん|あと|どしゃくず|ちゅうい"}],"notes":"土砂崩れ, from 土砂 \"earth and rubble\" and 崩れる \"to collapse\", is a landslide, usually after heavy rain or earthquakes. One says 土砂崩れが起きる/起こる and 土砂崩れで道路が通行止めになる. Related are がけ崩れ \"collapse of a steep slope\", 山崩れ \"mountain collapse\" and 地すべり \"slow sliding of a slope\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"高潮の被害がありました。","romaji":"Takashio no higai ga arimashita.","german":"There was storm surge damage.","furigana":"たかしお|ひがい"},{"japanese":"台風が近づいているので、海の近くでは高潮に注意してください。","romaji":"Taifuu ga chikazuite iru node, umi no chikaku de wa takashio ni chuui shite kudasai.","german":"A typhoon is approaching, so please beware of storm surges near the coast.","furigana":"たいふう|ちか|うみ|ちか|たかしお|ちゅうい"},{"japanese":"高潮は地震ではなく、台風の強い風と低い気圧で起こる。","romaji":"Takashio wa jishin de wa naku, taifuu no tsuyoi kaze to hikui kiatsu de okoru.","german":"A storm surge is caused not by an earthquake but by a typhoon's strong winds and low air pressure.","furigana":"たかしお|じしん|たいふう|つよ|かぜ|ひく|きあつ|お"}],"notes":"高潮 is a storm surge: the sea level rises due to the low air pressure and strong wind of a typhoon. Not to be confused with 津波, which is caused by earthquakes, and 満潮 \"high tide\". Typical expressions are 高潮の被害 and 高潮に注意する. With the reading こうちょう, the same kanji mean \"climax\", mostly in 最高潮.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"竜巻が発生しました。","romaji":"Tatsumaki ga hassei shimashita.","german":"A tornado occurred.","furigana":"たつまき|はっせい"},{"japanese":"竜巻で屋根が飛ばされた家もあった。","romaji":"Tatsumaki de yane ga tobasareta ie mo atta.","german":"There were houses whose roofs were blown off by the tornado.","furigana":"たつまき|やね|と|いえ"},{"japanese":"竜巻注意情報が出たら、頑丈な建物の中に入ってください。","romaji":"Tatsumaki chuui jouhou ga detara, ganjou na tatemono no naka ni haitte kudasai.","german":"If a tornado advisory is issued, please go inside a sturdy building.","furigana":"たつまきちゅういじょうほう|で|がんじょう|たてもの|なか|はい"}],"notes":"竜巻, from 竜 \"dragon\" and 巻く \"to swirl\", is a tornado or whirlwind; the image is a dragon rising to the sky. In the news it is 竜巻が発生する, and the weather service issues a 竜巻注意情報. A small whirlwind is つむじ風; a typhoon (台風) is a completely different, large-scale weather system.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"雹が降ってきました。","romaji":"Hyō ga futte kimashita.","german":"It has started to hail.","furigana":"ひょう|ふ"},{"japanese":"大きなひょうで車に傷がついてしまった。","romaji":"Ookina hyou de kuruma ni kizu ga tsuite shimatta.","german":"Large hailstones dented my car.","furigana":"おお|くるま|きず"},{"japanese":"夏でも、急に雹が降ることがある。","romaji":"Natsu demo, kyuu ni hyou ga furu koto ga aru.","german":"Even in summer, it can suddenly hail.","furigana":"なつ|きゅう|ひょう|ふ"}],"notes":"雹 is hail made of ice pellets of at least 5 mm; the kanji is rare and ひょう is usually written in kana. As with rain, the verb is 降る: 雹が降る. Smaller pellets are called あられ \"graupel\", and sleet is みぞれ. In Japan hail occurs mainly during thunderstorms in early summer and can damage cars and crops.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"朝靄がかかっています。","romaji":"Asamoya ga kakatteimasu.","german":"There is morning haze.","furigana":"あさもや"},{"japanese":"湖の上に白いもやがかかっていて、とてもきれいだった。","romaji":"Mizuumi no ue ni shiroi moya ga kakatte ite, totemo kirei datta.","german":"White haze hung over the lake, and it was very beautiful.","furigana":"みずうみ|うえ|しろ"},{"japanese":"日が昇ると、もやはすぐに消えた。","romaji":"Hi ga noboru to, moya wa sugu ni kieta.","german":"When the sun rose, the haze disappeared quickly.","furigana":"ひ|のぼ|き"}],"notes":"靄 is light haze or mist through which one can still see fairly far; the kanji is rare and もや is usual. One says もやがかかる \"haze hangs\" and 朝もや \"morning haze\". Denser is 霧 \"fog\"; 霞 refers to poetic spring haze. Related is the onomatopoeic もやもや for an unclear, oppressive feeling.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今年は暖冬です。","romaji":"Kotoshi wa dantō desu.","german":"This year we have a mild winter.","furigana":"ことし|だんとう"},{"japanese":"暖冬で雪が少なく、スキー場はどこも困っている。","romaji":"Dantou de yuki ga sukunaku, sukiijou wa doko mo komatte iru.","german":"Because of the mild winter there is little snow, and all the ski resorts are in trouble.","furigana":"だんとう|ゆき|すく|じょう|こま"},{"japanese":"天気予報によると、この冬は暖冬になるそうだ。","romaji":"Tenki yohou ni yoru to, kono fuyu wa dantou ni naru sou da.","german":"According to the weather forecast, this winter will be mild.","furigana":"てんきよほう|ふゆ|だんとう"}],"notes":"暖冬 is an unusually mild winter and often appears in weather forecasts: 今年は暖冬になる \"this year the winter will be mild\". Typical consequences are 雪不足 \"lack of snow\" at ski resorts. The opposite is 厳冬 \"severe winter\"; for summer, the analogues are 冷夏 \"cool summer\" and 猛暑 \"scorching summer\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"冷夏で作物に影響が出ました。","romaji":"Reika de sakumotsu ni eikyō ga demashita.","german":"The cool summer affected the crops.","furigana":"れいか|さくもつ|えいきょう|で"},{"japanese":"今年は冷夏になるという予報が出ています。","romaji":"Kotoshi wa reika ni naru to iu yohou ga dete imasu.","german":"The forecast says this year will have a cool summer.","furigana":"ことし|れいか|よほう|で"},{"japanese":"冷夏のせいで、エアコンや扇風機があまり売れなかった。","romaji":"Reika no sei de, eakon ya senpuuki ga amari urenakatta.","german":"Because of the cool summer, air conditioners and fans did not sell well.","furigana":"れいか|せんぷうき|う"}],"notes":"冷夏 is a summer that turns out clearly cooler than normal; it is a factual term from weather reports and news, often in connection with poor harvests (冷夏で米が不作になる). Typical are 冷夏になる and 冷夏で/冷夏のせいで as a cause. Related weather terms are 暖冬 \"mild winter\" and 猛暑 \"intense summer heat\". In conversation people more often simply say 今年の夏は涼しい.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"五月雨が続いています。","romaji":"Samidare ga tsuzuite imasu.","german":"The early-summer rain continues.","furigana":"さみだれ|つづ"},{"japanese":"五月雨式に資料を送ってしまい、申し訳ありません。","romaji":"Samidare-shiki ni shiryou o okutte shimai, moushiwake arimasen.","german":"I'm sorry that I sent the materials in a piecemeal fashion.","furigana":"さみだれしき|しりょう|おく|もう|わけ"},{"japanese":"窓の外では静かに五月雨が降っていた。","romaji":"Mado no soto de wa shizuka ni samidare ga futte ita.","german":"Outside the window, the early-summer rain was falling quietly.","furigana":"まど|そと|しず|さみだれ|ふ"}],"notes":"五月雨 is the long-lasting rain of the early-summer rainy season; 五月 means the fifth month of the old lunar calendar, i.e. about June. The reading さみだれ is a special reading and cannot be derived from the individual kanji. The word itself is poetic (haiku, songs, literature); in everyday life one says 梅雨 or 梅雨の雨. Very common, however, is 五月雨式に \"piecemeal, little by little\", for example when sending documents in several installments in business emails.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"夕立に降られました。","romaji":"Yūdachi ni furaremashita.","german":"I got caught in an evening shower.","furigana":"ゆうだち|ふ"},{"japanese":"夕立が来そうだから、洗濯物を取り込もう。","romaji":"Yuudachi ga kisou da kara, sentakumono o torikomou.","german":"A shower is probably coming, so let's bring in the laundry.","furigana":"ゆうだち|き|せんたくもの|と|こ"},{"japanese":"夕立の後は少し涼しくなりますね。","romaji":"Yuudachi no ato wa sukoshi suzushiku narimasu ne.","german":"It gets a little cooler after an evening shower, doesn't it?","furigana":"ゆうだち|あと|すこ|すず"}],"notes":"夕立 is a sudden, heavy rain shower on summer afternoons or evenings, often with thunder, that stops after a short time. Typical are 夕立が降る/来る and the adversative passive 夕立に降られる \"to get caught in a shower\". Unlike にわか雨 \"shower\" in general, 夕立 is tied to summer and the time of day; extreme downpours are nowadays often called ゲリラ豪雨 by the media.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"木枯らしが吹いています。","romaji":"Kogarashi ga fuiteimasu.","german":"A cold wintry wind is blowing.","furigana":"こが|ふ"},{"japanese":"今日、東京で木枯らし1号が吹きました。","romaji":"Kyou, Toukyou de kogarashi ichigou ga fukimashita.","german":"Today the first kogarashi of the season blew in Tokyo.","furigana":"きょう|とうきょう|こが|ごう|ふ"},{"japanese":"木枯らしの中、コートの襟を立てて歩いた。","romaji":"Kogarashi no naka, kooto no eri o tatete aruita.","german":"I walked in the biting wind with my coat collar turned up.","furigana":"こが|なか|えり|た|ある"}],"notes":"木枯らし is the cold, dry north wind of late autumn and early winter that strips the trees bare (木 \"tree\" + 枯らす \"make wither\"). Typical is 木枯らしが吹く. The first such wind of the year is officially announced by the weather bureau in the Tokyo and Osaka areas as 木枯らし1号; it is regarded as a sign of the start of winter. Its spring counterpart is 春一番.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"春一番が吹きました。","romaji":"Haruichiban ga fukimashita.","german":"The first spring gale has blown.","furigana":"はるいちばん|ふ"},{"japanese":"関東地方で昨日、春一番が観測されました。","romaji":"Kantou chihou de kinou, haruichiban ga kansoku saremashita.","german":"The first spring gale was observed in the Kanto region yesterday.","furigana":"かんとうちほう|きのう|はるいちばん|かんそく"},{"japanese":"春一番で自転車が倒れてしまった。","romaji":"Haruichiban de jitensha ga taorete shimatta.","german":"The first spring gale knocked over my bicycle.","furigana":"はるいちばん|じてんしゃ|たお"}],"notes":"春一番 refers to the first strong, warm south wind of the year, which blows between the beginning of spring (立春, early February) and the spring equinox. The weather agency announces it officially (春一番が観測される); it is regarded as a harbinger of spring, but often brings violent gusts and dust, and afterwards it often turns cold again. Typical is 春一番が吹く; the autumn counterpart is 木枯らし1号.","levelNote":"Compound: 春 (N5) + 一番 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"朝焼けがきれいです。","romaji":"Asayake ga kirei desu.","german":"The sunrise glow is beautiful.","furigana":"あさや"},{"japanese":"早起きして、山の上から朝焼けを見た。","romaji":"Hayaoki shite, yama no ue kara asayake o mita.","german":"I got up early and watched the dawn glow from the mountaintop.","furigana":"はやお|やま|うえ|あさや|み"},{"japanese":"朝焼けの日は雨になると祖母がよく言っていました。","romaji":"Asayake no hi wa ame ni naru to sobo ga yoku itte imashita.","german":"My grandmother often said that a red dawn means rain.","furigana":"あさや|ひ|あめ|そぼ|い"}],"notes":"朝焼け is the red or orange coloring of the sky at sunrise (朝 \"morning\" + 焼ける \"glow\"). Typical are 朝焼けがきれい, 朝焼けの空 and 空が朝焼けに染まる. It differs from the sunrise itself (日の出) in that it is about the color of the sky. An old weather saying goes: 朝焼けは雨、夕焼けは晴れ - red sky in the morning brings rain, red sky in the evening brings fine weather.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"夕焼けを見ました。","romaji":"Yūyake o mimashita.","german":"I saw the sunset glow.","furigana":"ゆうや|み"},{"japanese":"西の空が夕焼けで真っ赤に染まっている。","romaji":"Nishi no sora ga yuuyake de makka ni somatte iru.","german":"The western sky is dyed deep red by the sunset glow.","furigana":"にし|そら|ゆうや|ま|か|そ"},{"japanese":"夕焼けがきれいだから、明日はきっと晴れるよ。","romaji":"Yuuyake ga kirei da kara, ashita wa kitto hareru yo.","german":"The sunset is beautiful, so it will surely be sunny tomorrow.","furigana":"ゆうや|あした|は"}],"notes":"夕焼け is the evening glow, the red coloring of the sky around sunset. Common combinations: 夕焼けがきれい, 真っ赤な夕焼け, 空が夕焼けに染まる and the children's song 夕焼け小焼け. The counterpart is 朝焼け \"morning glow\". It differs from 夕日 \"evening sun\" in that it means the colored sky, not the sun itself; according to the weather saying, an evening glow announces fine weather for the next day.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"道路に陽炎が立っています。","romaji":"Dōro ni kagerō ga tatteimasu.","german":"Heat haze is rising from the road.","furigana":"どうろ|かげろう|た"},{"japanese":"真夏のグラウンドに陽炎が揺れていた。","romaji":"Manatsu no guraundo ni kagerou ga yurete ita.","german":"Heat haze was shimmering over the field in midsummer.","furigana":"まなつ|かげろう|ゆ"},{"japanese":"陽炎の向こうに、遠くのビルがゆがんで見える。","romaji":"Kagerou no mukou ni, tooku no biru ga yugande mieru.","german":"Through the heat haze, distant buildings look distorted.","furigana":"かげろう|む|とお|み"}],"notes":"陽炎 is the shimmering of the air above hot ground, for example above asphalt in summer or on warm spring days. One says 陽炎が立つ or 陽炎が揺れる. The word sounds rather literary; in everyday speech the phenomenon is often described simply as 空気がゆらゆらしている. Not to be confused with the identically pronounced かげろう \"mayfly\" (蜉蝣), an image of transience.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"蜻蛉が飛んでいます。","romaji":"Tonbo ga tondeimasu.","german":"Dragonflies are flying around.","furigana":"とんぼ|と"},{"japanese":"秋になると、田んぼの上を赤とんぼが飛びます。","romaji":"Aki ni naru to, tanbo no ue o akatonbo ga tobimasu.","german":"In autumn, red dragonflies fly over the rice fields.","furigana":"あき|た|うえ|あか|と"},{"japanese":"子どものころ、網でとんぼを捕まえて遊んだ。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, ami de tonbo o tsukamaete asonda.","german":"As a child, I had fun catching dragonflies with a net.","furigana":"こ|あみ|つか|あそ"}],"notes":"蜻蛉 is the dragonfly; in everyday writing it is almost always in kana (とんぼ or トンボ), and the kanji are seen mostly in literature. Caution: the same kanji can also be read かげろう \"mayfly\". Dragonflies are a typical autumn symbol in Japan, especially the red 赤とんぼ from the well-known children's song. The expression とんぼ返り means \"an immediate return trip\", e.g. a business trip without an overnight stay.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鶴は長寿の象徴です。","romaji":"Tsuru wa chōju no shōchō desu.","german":"The crane is a symbol of longevity.","furigana":"つる|ちょうじゅ|しょうちょう"},{"japanese":"入院中の友達のために千羽鶴を折りました。","romaji":"Nyuuin-chuu no tomodachi no tame ni senbazuru o orimashita.","german":"We folded a thousand paper cranes for our friend in the hospital.","furigana":"にゅういんちゅう|ともだち|せんばづる|お"},{"japanese":"社長の鶴の一声で、会議の結論が決まった。","romaji":"Shachou no tsuru no hitokoe de, kaigi no ketsuron ga kimatta.","german":"The president's word of authority settled the conclusion of the meeting.","furigana":"しゃちょう|つる|ひとこえ|かいぎ|けつろん|き"}],"notes":"鶴 is the crane, in Japan above all the red-crowned crane (丹頂). Together with the turtle, it stands for long life and happiness: 鶴は千年、亀は万年. Well known are 折り鶴 \"folded paper crane\" and 千羽鶴, a thousand paper cranes strung together as a wish for recovery or peace. The phrase 鶴の一声 refers to the commanding word of an authority that ends a discussion immediately.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"亀はゆっくり歩きます。","romaji":"Kame wa yukkuri arukimasu.","german":"Die Schildkröte geht langsam.","furigana":"かめ|ある"},{"japanese":"息子が川で小さな亀を拾ってきました。","romaji":"Musuko ga kawa de chiisana kame o hirotte kimashita.","german":"Mein Sohn hat am Fluss eine kleine Schildkröte gefunden und mit nach Hause gebracht.","furigana":"むすこ|かわ|ちい|かめ|ひろ"},{"japanese":"うさぎと亀の話を知っていますか。","romaji":"Usagi to kame no hanashi o shitte imasu ka.","german":"Kennen Sie die Geschichte vom Hasen und der Schildkröte?","furigana":"かめ|はなし|し"}],"notes":"亀 umfasst Land- und Wasserschildkröten, entspricht also dem deutschen „Schildkröte“ allgemein. Das Tier steht für Langsamkeit und, zusammen mit dem Kranich, für langes Leben (鶴は千年、亀は万年). Bekannt sind die Fabel うさぎと亀 „Hase und Schildkröte“ und das Märchen von Urashima Taro, der eine Schildkröte rettet. Im Alltag wird das Wort oft in Kana (かめ/カメ) geschrieben.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"池の鯉に餌をやります。","romaji":"Ike no koi ni esa o yarimasu.","german":"I feed the carp in the pond.","furigana":"いけ|こい|えさ"},{"japanese":"五月になると、あちこちで鯉のぼりが泳いでいます。","romaji":"Gogatsu ni naru to, achikochi de koinobori ga oyoide imasu.","german":"In May, carp streamers are flying everywhere.","furigana":"ごがつ|こい|およ"},{"japanese":"この庭園の錦鯉はとても高いそうです。","romaji":"Kono teien no nishikigoi wa totemo takai sou desu.","german":"The ornamental carp in this garden are said to be very expensive.","furigana":"ていえん|にしきごい|たか"}],"notes":"鯉 is the carp, in Japan above all as a colorful ornamental carp (錦鯉) in the ponds of gardens, temples and castles. For the children's festival on May 5, 鯉のぼり are hung up, carp streamers as a wish for strong, successful children. \"To feed\" is 餌をやる, more kindly also 餌をあげる. The similar-sounding 恋 \"love\" is often used for wordplay.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鷹が空を飛んでいます。","romaji":"Taka ga sora o tondeimasu.","german":"Ein Falke fliegt am Himmel.","furigana":"たか|そら|と"},{"japanese":"能ある鷹は爪を隠すと言うように、彼は自分の実力を自慢しない。","romaji":"Nou aru taka wa tsume o kakusu to iu you ni, kare wa jibun no jitsuryoku o jiman shinai.","german":"Wie das Sprichwort sagt, versteckt der fähige Habicht seine Krallen – er prahlt nie mit seinem Können.","furigana":"のう|たか|つめ|かく|い|かれ|じぶん|じつりょく|じまん"},{"japanese":"公園で鷹狩りの実演を見ました。","romaji":"Kouen de takagari no jitsuen o mimashita.","german":"Im Park habe ich eine Falknerei-Vorführung gesehen.","furigana":"こうえん|たかが|じつえん|み"}],"notes":"鷹 bezeichnet mittelgroße Greifvögel wie den Habicht; im Deutschen passt je nach Art „Habicht“ oder „Falke“, genau genommen heißt der Falke aber 隼. Häufig wird das Wort in Kana (タカ) geschrieben. Bekannt sind 鷹狩り „Beizjagd, Falknerei“ und das Sprichwort 能ある鷹は爪を隠す „ein fähiger Habicht verbirgt seine Krallen“, also: Wer wirklich etwas kann, prahlt nicht damit.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"雀が屋根にいます。","romaji":"Suzume ga yane ni imasu.","german":"A sparrow is on the roof.","furigana":"すずめ|やね"},{"japanese":"ボーナスは雀の涙ほどしか出なかった。","romaji":"Boonasu wa suzume no namida hodo shika denakatta.","german":"The bonus was a mere pittance.","furigana":"すずめ|なみだ|で"},{"japanese":"朝、窓の外で雀がチュンチュン鳴いていた。","romaji":"Asa, mado no soto de suzume ga chunchun naite ita.","german":"In the morning, sparrows were chirping outside the window.","furigana":"あさ|まど|そと|すずめ|な"}],"notes":"雀 is the sparrow, more precisely the tree sparrow found everywhere in Japan. Sparrows chirp (チュンチュン鳴く) and sit on roofs and power lines. The phrase 雀の涙 \"a sparrow's tears\" means \"a tiny amount\", e.g. 雀の涙ほどのボーナス. The word is often written in kana (すずめ/スズメ); the kanji is also found in 麻雀 \"mahjong\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"烏がゴミを荒らしています。","romaji":"Karasu ga gomi o arashiteimasu.","german":"Crows are raiding the garbage.","furigana":"からす|あ"},{"japanese":"夕方になると、からすがカーカー鳴きながら山へ帰っていく。","romaji":"Yuugata ni naru to, karasu ga kaakaa nakinagara yama e kaette iku.","german":"When evening comes, the crows fly back to the mountains, cawing.","furigana":"ゆうがた|な|やま|かえ"},{"japanese":"彼はいつも烏の行水で、五分でお風呂から出てくる。","romaji":"Kare wa itsumo karasu no gyouzui de, gofun de ofuro kara dete kuru.","german":"He always takes a very quick bath and comes out of the tub in five minutes.","furigana":"かれ|からす|ぎょうずい|ごふん|ふろ|で"}],"notes":"烏 is the crow (also the raven); in everyday life it is almost always written カラス or からす. The kanji 烏 looks almost like 鳥 \"bird\" - it only lacks one stroke. In Japanese cities, crows are considered a pest that tear open garbage bags (ゴミを荒らす); their cawing is rendered as カーカー. The phrase 烏の行水 means \"a very short bath\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"森で梟の声を聞きました。","romaji":"Mori de fukurō no koe o kikimashita.","german":"I heard an owl in the forest.","furigana":"もり|ふくろう|こえ|き"},{"japanese":"ふくろうは夜行性なので、昼間はほとんど寝ています。","romaji":"Fukurou wa yakousei na node, hiruma wa hotondo nete imasu.","german":"Since owls are nocturnal, they sleep most of the day.","furigana":"やこうせい|ひるま|ね"},{"japanese":"ふくろうは「不苦労」に通じるので、縁起がいいと言われています。","romaji":"Fukurou wa 'fukurou' ni tsuujiru node, engi ga ii to iwarete imasu.","german":"The owl is said to be lucky because its name sounds like \"no hardship\" (不苦労).","furigana":"ふくろう|つう|えんぎ|い"}],"notes":"梟 is the owl, especially the Ural owl; in everyday writing it is usually フクロウ or ふくろう since the kanji is rare. Owls are nocturnal (夜行性) and are regarded as wise. Because ふくろう sounds like 不苦労 \"without hardship\", owl figures are popular good-luck charms in Japan. Their call is rendered as ホーホー.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"狸が庭に来ました。","romaji":"Tanuki ga niwa ni kimashita.","german":"A tanuki came into the garden.","furigana":"たぬき|にわ|き"},{"japanese":"電車で席を譲りたくなくて、たぬき寝入りをする人もいる。","romaji":"Densha de seki o yuzuritakunakute, tanuki neiri o suru hito mo iru.","german":"Some people pretend to be asleep on the train because they don't want to give up their seat.","furigana":"でんしゃ|せき|ゆず|ねい|ひと"},{"japanese":"あの部長はなかなかの狸だから、話を信じすぎないほうがいい。","romaji":"Ano buchou wa nakanaka no tanuki da kara, hanashi o shinjisuginai hou ga ii.","german":"That department manager is quite a sly fox, so you'd better not believe everything he says.","furigana":"ぶちょう|たぬき|はなし|しん"}],"notes":"狸 is the raccoon dog (tanuki), a wild animal native to Japan that appears in folklore as a shape-shifting trickster; clay figures with straw hats stand in front of many restaurants. Figuratively, 狸 refers to a cunning, crafty person, e.g. 古狸 or 狸親父. Well known are たぬき寝入り \"pretending to be asleep\" and たぬきうどん/たぬきそば with tempura crumbs. Often written in kana (たぬき/タヌキ).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"稲荷神社の狐の像です。","romaji":"Inari jinja no kitsune no zō desu.","german":"Das ist eine Fuchsstatue an einem Inari-Schrein.","furigana":"|きつね|ぞう"},{"japanese":"お昼はきつねうどんにしよう。","romaji":"Ohiru wa kitsune udon ni shiyou.","german":"Zu Mittag nehme ich Kitsune-Udon mit frittiertem Tofu.","furigana":"ひる"},{"japanese":"財布が急になくなって、狐につままれたような気分だ。","romaji":"Saifu ga kyuu ni nakunatte, kitsune ni tsumamareta you na kibun da.","german":"Mein Portemonnaie ist plötzlich verschwunden – ich komme mir vor wie verhext.","furigana":"さいふ|きゅう|きつね|きぶん"}],"notes":"狐 ist der Fuchs. In Japan gilt er als Bote der Reisgottheit Inari, deshalb stehen an Inari-Schreinen Fuchsstatuen. Wie im Deutschen steht er für List; in Märchen verwandelt sich der Fuchs gern und täuscht Menschen (狐に化かされる), daher 狐につままれたよう „wie verhext, völlig verdutzt“. Beim Essen bezeichnet きつね frittierten Tofu (油揚げ), etwa in きつねうどん. Oft in Kana geschrieben.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"猪が畑を荒らします。","romaji":"Inoshishi ga hatake o arashimasu.","german":"Wild boars are ravaging the fields.","furigana":"いのしし|はたけ|あ"},{"japanese":"夜道で猪に出会ったら、刺激しないで静かに離れてください。","romaji":"Yomichi de inoshishi ni deattara, shigeki shinaide shizuka ni hanarete kudasai.","german":"If you meet a wild boar on a night road, don't provoke it and quietly move away.","furigana":"よみち|いのしし|であ|しげき|しず|はな"},{"japanese":"私はいのしし年生まれです。","romaji":"Watashi wa inoshishi-doshi umare desu.","german":"I was born in the year of the boar.","furigana":"わたし|どしう"}],"notes":"猪 is the wild boar. In rural areas, wild boars cause great damage to fields (畑を荒らす), which is why fences and traps are set up. It is also one of the twelve zodiac animals (いのしし年), and 猪突猛進 means \"to charge blindly ahead\". In everyday life it is often written in kana (いのしし/イノシシ); as meat it is known from the stew ぼたん鍋.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"奈良公園に鹿がいます。","romaji":"Nara kōen ni shika ga imasu.","german":"There are deer in Nara Park.","furigana":"ならこうえん|しか"},{"japanese":"鹿せんべいを持っていると、鹿がたくさん寄ってきます。","romaji":"Shika senbei o motte iru to, shika ga takusan yotte kimasu.","german":"When you hold deer crackers, lots of deer come over.","furigana":"しか|も|しか|よ"},{"japanese":"夜の山道では、車の前に鹿が飛び出すことがある。","romaji":"Yoru no yamamichi de wa, kuruma no mae ni shika ga tobidasu koto ga aru.","german":"On mountain roads at night, deer sometimes jump out in front of the car.","furigana":"よる|やまみち|くるま|まえ|しか|と|だ"}],"notes":"鹿 covers both deer and roe; in everyday life Japanese does not distinguish here, and the Sika deer is usually meant. Famous are the freely roaming deer in Nara Park, which are fed with special crackers (鹿せんべい). The kanji is also found in 馬鹿 \"fool\". A fawn is called 子鹿 (こじか) - in compounds, しか often becomes じか.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"山で熊に注意してください。","romaji":"Yama de kuma ni chūi shite kudasai.","german":"Be careful of bears in the mountains.","furigana":"やま|くま|ちゅうい"},{"japanese":"この近くで熊が出たので、山道は通行止めです。","romaji":"Kono chikaku de kuma ga deta node, yamamichi wa tsuukoudome desu.","german":"A bear was sighted near here, so the mountain trail is closed.","furigana":"ちか|くま|で|やまみち|つうこうど"},{"japanese":"娘の誕生日に熊のぬいぐるみをあげました。","romaji":"Musume no tanjoubi ni kuma no nuigurumi o agemashita.","german":"I gave my daughter a teddy bear for her birthday.","furigana":"むすめ|たんじょうび|くま"}],"notes":"熊 is the bear. In Japan, Asian black bears (ツキノワグマ) live on 本州 and brown bears (ヒグマ) on 北海道; bear sightings in forests and villages are a frequent news topic (熊が出る/出没する). Hikers therefore carry bear bells (熊よけの鈴). In compounds, くま often becomes ぐま (白熊 \"polar bear\"); a teddy bear is called 熊のぬいぐるみ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"田んぼで蛙が鳴いています。","romaji":"Tanbo de kaeru ga naiteimasu.","german":"Frogs are croaking in the rice paddy.","furigana":"た|かえる|な"},{"japanese":"蛙の子は蛙で、彼の息子も医者になった。","romaji":"Kaeru no ko wa kaeru de, kare no musuko mo isha ni natta.","german":"Like father, like son: his son also became a doctor.","furigana":"かえる|こ|かえる|かれ|むすこ|いしゃ"},{"japanese":"雨の日に、庭で小さな緑のかえるを見つけた。","romaji":"Ame no hi ni, niwa de chiisana midori no kaeru o mitsuketa.","german":"On a rainy day, I found a small green frog in the garden.","furigana":"あめ|ひ|にわ|ちい|みどり|み"}],"notes":"蛙 is the frog (also the toad); it is usually written かえる or カエル. Frogs croak ケロケロ or ゲロゲロ, especially in rice paddies during the rainy season; the verb is 鳴く. Well-known expressions are 蛙の子は蛙 \"the apple doesn't fall far from the tree\" and 井の中の蛙 \"a frog in a well\" for someone with a narrow outlook. 帰る \"to return\" and 変える \"to change\" sound the same.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"蜘蛛の巣を見つけました。","romaji":"Kumo no su o mitsukemashita.","german":"I found a spider web.","furigana":"くも|す|み"},{"japanese":"部屋に大きな蜘蛛が出て、思わず叫んでしまった。","romaji":"Heya ni ookina kumo ga dete, omowazu sakende shimatta.","german":"A big spider appeared in my room and I screamed without thinking.","furigana":"へや|おお|くも|で|おも|さけ"},{"japanese":"先生が来たとたん、生徒たちは蜘蛛の子を散らすように逃げていった。","romaji":"Sensei ga kita totan, seitotachi wa kumo no ko o chirasu you ni nigete itta.","german":"As soon as the teacher came, the students scattered in all directions.","furigana":"せんせい|き|せいと|くも|こ|ち|に"}],"notes":"蜘蛛 is the spider; because of the difficult kanji it is usually written くも or クモ. The web is called 蜘蛛の巣, which also stands for cobwebs in old rooms; spinning a web is 巣を張る. Do not confuse it with the identically pronounced 雲 \"cloud\". The expression 蜘蛛の子を散らすように describes people suddenly running off in all directions.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"蟻が列を作っています。","romaji":"Ari ga retsu o tsukutteimasu.","german":"The ants are forming a line.","furigana":"あり|れつ|つく"},{"japanese":"台所に蟻が入ってきたので、砂糖をしまった。","romaji":"Daidokoro ni ari ga haitte kita node, satou o shimatta.","german":"Ants got into the kitchen, so I put the sugar away.","furigana":"だいどころ|あり|はい|さとう"},{"japanese":"会場の周りは警備が厳しく、蟻の這い出る隙もなかった。","romaji":"Kaijou no mawari wa keibi ga kibishiku, ari no haideru suki mo nakatta.","german":"Security around the venue was so tight that not even an ant could have slipped out.","furigana":"かいじょう|まわ|けいび|きび|あり|は|で|すき"}],"notes":"蟻 is the ant; it is usually written あり or アリ. Ants walk in columns (列を作る) and live in nests (蟻の巣). The fable アリとキリギリス \"The Ant and the Grasshopper\" about diligence and foresight is well known. The expression 蟻の這い出る隙もない describes a seamless cordon - not even an ant could get out.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"帳簿を付けます。","romaji":"Chōbo o tsukemasu.","german":"I keep the books.","furigana":"ちょうぼ|つ"},{"japanese":"税務署の調査で、過去五年分の帳簿を見せました。","romaji":"Zeimusho no chousa de, kako gonen-bun no choubo o misemashita.","german":"During the tax office audit, I showed the books for the past five years.","furigana":"ぜいむしょ|ちょうさ|かこごねんぶん|ちょうぼ|み"},{"japanese":"帳簿の数字が合わないので、もう一度計算します。","romaji":"Choubo no suuji ga awanai node, mou ichido keisan shimasu.","german":"The figures in the books don't add up, so I'll calculate again.","furigana":"ちょうぼ|すうじ|あ|いちどけいさん"}],"notes":"帳簿 is the business ledger in which income, expenses or stock of goods are recorded - today often digital as well. The typical collocation is 帳簿をつける \"to keep the books\"; also 帳簿を確認する and 帳簿が合わない \"the books don't add up\". It sounds businesslike and matter-of-fact; the private household account book is 家計簿, and accounting as an activity or department is 経理.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新商品を仕入れます。","romaji":"Shinshōhin o shiiremasu.","german":"I buy in new products.","furigana":"しんしょうひん|しい"},{"japanese":"毎朝、市場で魚の仕入れをしています。","romaji":"Maiasa, ichiba de sakana no shiire o shite imasu.","german":"Every morning I buy the fish for the restaurant at the market.","furigana":"まいあさ|いちば|さかな|しい"},{"japanese":"仕入れ値が上がったので、来月から値上げします。","romaji":"Shiirene ga agatta node, raigetsu kara neage shimasu.","german":"Because the purchase prices have risen, we will raise our prices from next month.","furigana":"しい|ね|あ|らいげつ|ねあ"}],"notes":"仕入れ is the purchase of goods or materials for resale or processing - that is, the business purchasing of a merchant, restaurant or company, not private shopping (買い物). The related transitive verb is 仕入れる (商品を仕入れる). Common compounds are 仕入れ先 \"supplier\" and 仕入れ値 \"purchase price\". Figuratively, one can also \"pick up\" information: 情報を仕入れる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"卸売り価格で買います。","romaji":"Oroshiuri kakaku de kaimasu.","german":"I buy at the wholesale price.","furigana":"おろしう|かかく|か"},{"japanese":"父は野菜の卸売りの仕事をしています。","romaji":"Chichi wa yasai no oroshiuri no shigoto o shite imasu.","german":"My father works in vegetable wholesale.","furigana":"ちち|やさい|おろしう|しごと"},{"japanese":"豊洲の卸売市場は、朝早くからとてもにぎやかです。","romaji":"Toyosu no oroshiuri shijou wa, asa hayaku kara totemo nigiyaka desu.","german":"The Toyosu wholesale market is very lively from early in the morning.","furigana":"|おろしうりしじょう|あさはや"}],"notes":"卸売り is wholesale, i.e. selling large quantities to retailers rather than to end customers; the opposite is 小売り \"retail\". In compounds the り is usually dropped: 卸売業, 卸売市場 \"wholesale market\", 卸売価格. The related verb is 卸す \"to supply to retailers\" (商品を店に卸す); in everyday speech you also hear the short form 卸値 \"wholesale price\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"小売店で働いています。","romaji":"Kouriten de hataraite imasu.","german":"I work at a retail store.","furigana":"こうりてん|はたら"},{"japanese":"メーカー希望小売価格は三千円です。","romaji":"Meekaa kibou kouri kakaku wa sanzen-en desu.","german":"The manufacturer's suggested retail price is 3,000 yen.","furigana":"きぼうこうりかかく|さんぜんえん"},{"japanese":"ネット通販が増えて、町の小売りは厳しい状況にある。","romaji":"Netto tsuuhan ga fuete, machi no kouri wa kibishii joukyou ni aru.","german":"With the growth of online shopping, local retail is in a tough situation.","furigana":"つうはん|ふ|まち|こう|きび|じょうきょう"}],"notes":"小売り is retail, the sale to end customers in small quantities; the counterpart is 卸売り \"wholesale\". In compounds it is usually written without り: 小売店 \"retail shop\", 小売業 \"retail industry\", 小売価格 \"retail price\". As a する-verb (小売りする) it means \"to offer for retail sale\", but it is rarer than the noun.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"競合他社を分析します。","romaji":"Kyōgō tasha o bunseki shimasu.","german":"I analyze competing companies.","furigana":"きょうごうたしゃ|ぶんせき"},{"japanese":"この商品は大手メーカーの商品と競合している。","romaji":"Kono shouhin wa oote meekaa no shouhin to kyougou shite iru.","german":"This product competes with products from a major manufacturer.","furigana":"しょうひん|おおて|しょうひん|きょうごう"},{"japanese":"二つのセキュリティソフトが競合して、パソコンが動かなくなった。","romaji":"Futatsu no sekyuriti sofuto ga kyougou shite, pasokon ga ugokanaku natta.","german":"Two security programs conflicted with each other, and the PC stopped working.","furigana":"ふた|きょうごう|うご"}],"notes":"競合 means that several parties compete for the same thing. In business language it appears mainly in 競合他社 \"competing company\" and 競合商品 \"competing product\"; short 競合 also simply means \"the competition\". As an intransitive する-verb one says 〜と競合する \"to compete with ...\", and technically it is also used for programs that interfere with each other. The more general, everyday word is 競争 \"competition\"; 競合 emphasizes the direct overlap of offers or interests.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"市場調査を行います。","romaji":"Shijō chōsa o okonaimasu.","german":"I am conducting market research.","furigana":"しじょうちょうさ|おこな"},{"japanese":"新しいカフェを開く前に、近所で市場調査をしました。","romaji":"Atarashii kafe o hiraku mae ni, kinjo de shijou chousa o shimashita.","german":"Before opening the new cafe, I did market research in the neighborhood.","furigana":"あたら|ひら|まえ|きんじょ|しじょうちょうさ"},{"japanese":"市場調査の結果、若い女性に人気があることが分かった。","romaji":"Shijou chousa no kekka, wakai josei ni ninki ga aru koto ga wakatta.","german":"The market research showed that the product is popular with young women.","furigana":"しじょうちょうさ|けっか|わか|じょせい|にんき|わ"}],"notes":"市場調査 is market research, that is, the study of demand, customer wishes and competition, for example before launching a product. Note the reading: 市場 is read しじょう here, \"market in the economic sense\", not いちば \"marketplace, market hall\". Typical are 市場調査を行う/実施する/する and 市場調査の結果; in practice one also often hears マーケティングリサーチ or simply リサーチ.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 42"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"広報部に所属しています。","romaji":"Kōhōbu ni shozoku shiteimasu.","german":"I belong to the PR department.","furigana":"こうほうぶ|しょぞく"},{"japanese":"イベントの情報は市の広報に載っています。","romaji":"Ibento no jouhou wa shi no kouhou ni notte imasu.","german":"The information about the event is in the city's bulletin.","furigana":"じょうほう|し|こうほう|の"},{"japanese":"記者からの問い合わせには広報担当者が対応します。","romaji":"Kisha kara no toiawase ni wa kouhou tantousha ga taiou shimasu.","german":"Our press office handles inquiries from reporters.","furigana":"きしゃ|と|あ|こうほうたんとうしゃ|たいおう"}],"notes":"広報 is public relations, that is, informing the public on the part of companies, public authorities or schools. Very common are 広報部/広報課 \"PR department\", 広報担当 \"press officer\" and 市の広報 \"city bulletin\". Compared with 宣伝 \"advertising\", 広報 is less about selling than about information and image; in company jargon one also says PR.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人事異動がありました。","romaji":"Jinji idō ga arimashita.","german":"There was a personnel reshuffle.","furigana":"じんじいどう"},{"japanese":"休みの取り方については人事に相談してください。","romaji":"Yasumi no torikata ni tsuite wa jinji ni soudan shite kudasai.","german":"Please consult HR about how to take time off.","furigana":"やす|と|かた|じんじ|そうだん"},{"japanese":"来週、人事評価の面談があります。","romaji":"Raishuu, jinji hyouka no mendan ga arimasu.","german":"Next week I have a performance review interview.","furigana":"らいしゅう|じんじひょうか|めんだん"}],"notes":"人事 in working life refers to personnel matters: hiring, transfers, promotions and evaluations. Common are 人事部 \"personnel department\", 人事異動 \"transfer, reshuffle\" and 人事評価 \"employee evaluation\"; one often simply says 人事 for the department (人事に相談する). Do not confuse it with 他人事 (ひとごと) \"someone else's affair\". The proverb 人事を尽くして天命を待つ uses the basic meaning \"human effort\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"総務部に連絡してください。","romaji":"Sōmubu ni renraku shite kudasai.","german":"Please contact the general affairs department.","furigana":"そうむぶ|れんらく"},{"japanese":"コピー用紙がなくなったら、総務に言ってください。","romaji":"Kopii youshi ga nakunattara, soumu ni itte kudasai.","german":"If we run out of copy paper, please tell general affairs.","furigana":"ようし|そうむ|い"},{"japanese":"小さい会社なので、総務と経理を一人でやっています。","romaji":"Chiisai kaisha na node, soumu to keiri o hitori de yatte imasu.","german":"The company is small, so I handle general affairs and accounting by myself.","furigana":"ちい|かいしゃ|そうむ|けいり|ひとり"}],"notes":"総務 is the general administration of a company or public office, which takes care of everything not assigned to a specialist department: office supplies, buildings, events, documents. Typical are 総務部/総務課 and, short, 総務 for the department (総務に聞く). In larger companies it is separate from 人事 \"personnel\" and 経理 \"accounting\"; in small businesses 総務 often handles everything together. At the national level there is the 総務省 \"Ministry of Internal Affairs and Communications\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"経理に提出します。","romaji":"Keiri ni teishutsu shimasu.","german":"I'll submit it to the accounting department.","furigana":"けいり|ていしゅつ"},{"japanese":"経理の経験がある人を募集しています。","romaji":"Keiri no keiken ga aru hito o boshuu shite imasu.","german":"We are looking for people with accounting experience.","furigana":"けいり|けいけん|ひと|ぼしゅう"},{"japanese":"彼女は会社で経理を担当しています。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kaisha de keiri o tantou shite imasu.","german":"She is in charge of accounting at the company.","furigana":"かのじょ|かいしゃ|けいり|たんとう"}],"notes":"経理 refers to the accounting or bookkeeping of a company: payments, receipts, settlements, salaries. It means both the activity (経理の仕事, 経理を担当する) and, short, the department (経理部, 経理に出す); receipts and expense reports typically go to 経理. Related are 会計 \"accounting; the till\" (also paying the bill at a restaurant) and 帳簿 \"business ledgers\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"検品作業をします。","romaji":"Kenpin sagyō o shimasu.","german":"I carry out product inspection work.","furigana":"けんぴんさぎょう"},{"japanese":"届いた商品を検品したら、箱が一つ足りなかった。","romaji":"Todoita shouhin o kenpin shitara, hako ga hitotsu tarinakatta.","german":"When I inspected the delivered goods, one box was missing.","furigana":"とど|しょうひん|けんぴん|はこ|ひと|た"},{"japanese":"すべての製品は出荷前に検品しています。","romaji":"Subete no seihin wa shukka mae ni kenpin shite imasu.","german":"All products are inspected before shipping.","furigana":"せいひん|しゅっかまえ|けんぴん"}],"notes":"検品 is the inspection of goods for quantity, damage or defects, for example on receipt in a warehouse or before shipping. As a する-verb it is transitive: 商品を検品する; also common are 検品作業 and 検品済み \"inspected\". Compared with 品質管理 \"quality management\", 検品 is the concrete checking step on an individual product; 点検, by contrast, is the inspection of machines or facilities.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不良品を返品します。","romaji":"Furyōhin o henpin shimasu.","german":"I'm returning the defective product.","furigana":"ふりょうひん|へんぴん"},{"japanese":"不良品の場合は、無料で新しいものと交換いたします。","romaji":"Furyouhin no baai wa, muryou de atarashii mono to koukan itashimasu.","german":"If the product is defective, we will exchange it for a new one free of charge.","furigana":"ふりょうひん|ばあい|むりょう|あたら|こうかん"},{"japanese":"この工場では不良品を減らすために機械を新しくした。","romaji":"Kono koujou de wa furyouhin o herasu tame ni kikai o atarashiku shita.","german":"This factory renewed its machines to reduce defective products.","furigana":"こうじょう|ふりょうひん|へ|きかい|あたら"}],"notes":"不良品 is faulty or defective merchandise that does not meet quality requirements. Typical combinations are 不良品が出る \"defective products turn up\" and 不良品を交換する/返品する. 不良 alone means \"bad, defective\" and also appears in 体調不良 \"feeling unwell\" or colloquially for \"delinquent\". A product with a fundamental design flaw is more likely called 欠陥品.","levelNote":"Compound: 不良 (N2) + 品 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"返品は可能ですか。","romaji":"Henpin wa kanō desu ka.","german":"Is a return possible?","furigana":"へんぴん|かのう"},{"japanese":"サイズが合わなかったので、ネットで買った靴を返品しました。","romaji":"Saizu ga awanakatta node, netto de katta kutsu o henpin shimashita.","german":"The size didn't fit, so I returned the shoes I bought online.","furigana":"あ|か|くつ|へんぴん"},{"japanese":"セール品は返品できませんので、ご注意ください。","romaji":"Seeruhin wa henpin dekimasen node, gochuui kudasai.","german":"Sale items cannot be returned, so please note this.","furigana":"ひん|へんぴん|ちゅうい"}],"notes":"返品 is the return of purchased goods to the shop or supplier, but also the returned goods themselves. As a する-verb: 商品を返品する. In shops and online stores one reads 返品・交換 \"returns and exchanges\", 返品不可 \"no returns\" and 返品期限 \"return deadline\". Unlike the general 返す \"to give back\", 返品 applies only to purchased or delivered goods.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"サイズ交換をお願いします。","romaji":"Saizu kōkan o onegai shimasu.","german":"I'd like to exchange it for another size.","furigana":"こうかん|ねが"},{"japanese":"初めて会った人と名刺を交換しました。","romaji":"Hajimete atta hito to meishi o koukan shimashita.","german":"I exchanged business cards with someone I met for the first time.","furigana":"はじ|あ|ひと|めいし|こうかん"},{"japanese":"リモコンが動かないので、電池を交換してみた。","romaji":"Rimokon ga ugokanai node, denchi o koukan shite mita.","german":"The remote control didn't work, so I tried replacing the batteries.","furigana":"うご|でんち|こうかん"}],"notes":"交換 means \"exchange\" in a broad sense: swapping things with each other (AとBを交換する), replacing something old with something new (電池を交換する) or exchanging in a shop (サイズ交換). Also common are 情報交換 \"exchange of information\", 名刺交換 \"exchange of business cards\" and 交換留学 \"exchange study\". When replacing parts one can also say 取り替える; 交換 sounds somewhat more formal and is the standard form on signs and in shops.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"建物を改修します。","romaji":"Tatemono o kaishū shimasu.","german":"We will renovate the building.","furigana":"たてもの|かいしゅう"},{"japanese":"駅は改修工事のため、北口が使えません。","romaji":"Eki wa kaishuu kouji no tame, kitaguchi ga tsukaemasen.","german":"The north exit is unavailable because of renovation work at the station.","furigana":"えき|かいしゅうこうじ|きたぐち|つか"},{"japanese":"市は古い橋を改修して、歩道を広くした。","romaji":"Shi wa furui hashi o kaishuu shite, hodou o hiroku shita.","german":"The city renovated the old bridge and widened the sidewalk.","furigana":"し|ふる|はし|かいしゅう|ほどう|ひろ"}],"notes":"改修 is the repair and improvement of structures and facilities such as buildings, roads, bridges or riverbanks; typical are 〜を改修する and 改修工事 \"renovation work\". It sounds rather official and appears in notices and the news. For renovating a home one rather says リフォーム in everyday speech, for a simple repair 修理, and for remodeling a shop 改装.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"家を増築しました。","romaji":"Ie o zōchiku shimashita.","german":"We added an extension to our house.","furigana":"いえ|ぞうちく"},{"japanese":"子どもが生まれるので、部屋を一つ増築するつもりです。","romaji":"Kodomo ga umareru node, heya o hitotsu zouchiku suru tsumori desu.","german":"We are expecting a child, so we plan to build on one more room.","furigana":"こ|う|へや|ひと|ぞうちく"},{"japanese":"この旅館は何度も増築されたので、中が迷路のようだ。","romaji":"Kono ryokan wa nando mo zouchiku sareta node, naka ga meiro no you da.","german":"This inn has been extended so many times that the inside is like a maze.","furigana":"りょかん|なんど|ぞうちく|なか|めいろ"}],"notes":"増築 is the extension of an existing building, for example by an additional room or floor: 家を増築する, 部屋を増築する. To be distinguished from it are 改築 \"remodeling (without enlarging)\", 新築 \"new construction\" and 建て替え \"demolition and rebuilding\". The compound 増改築 combines extension and remodeling.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"古い建物を解体します。","romaji":"Furui tatemono o kaitai shimasu.","german":"The old building will be demolished.","furigana":"ふる|たてもの|かいたい"},{"japanese":"スーパーでマグロの解体ショーをやっていた。","romaji":"Suupaa de maguro no kaitai shoo o yatte ita.","german":"The supermarket held a show where a whole tuna was cut up.","furigana":"かいたい"},{"japanese":"経営が苦しくなり、そのチームは解体されることになった。","romaji":"Keiei ga kurushiku nari, sono chiimu wa kaitai sareru koto ni natta.","german":"Finances became difficult, and it was decided to disband the team.","furigana":"けいえい|くる|かいたい"}],"notes":"解体 means taking a whole apart into its parts: demolishing buildings (ビルを解体する, 解体工事), taking machines apart or cutting up a large fish (マグロの解体ショー). Figuratively it stands for the dissolution of organizations (組織の解体). For small things like appliances, 分解 is more common in daily life; 解体 sounds larger and more official. It is mostly transitive (〜を解体する), but intransitive use is also possible (組織が解体する).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"来月着工します。","romaji":"Raigetsu chakkō shimasu.","german":"Construction will start next month.","furigana":"らいげつちゃっこう"},{"japanese":"新しい図書館は来年の春に着工する予定です。","romaji":"Atarashii toshokan wa rainen no haru ni chakkou suru yotei desu.","german":"Construction of the new library is scheduled to start next spring.","furigana":"あたら|としょかん|らいねん|はる|ちゃっこう|よてい"},{"japanese":"住民の反対で、工事の着工が遅れている。","romaji":"Juumin no hantai de, kouji no chakkou ga okurete iru.","german":"The start of construction is delayed because of residents' opposition.","furigana":"じゅうみん|はんたい|こうじ|ちゃっこう|おく"}],"notes":"着工 denotes the start of construction work, i.e. groundbreaking. It is a formal word from construction and the news: 来月着工する, 工事に着工する; in reports one also reads 新しいビルを着工する. The counterpart is 竣工 or 完工 \"completion\"; in everyday speech one simply says 工事が始まる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ビルが竣工しました。","romaji":"Biru ga shunkō shimashita.","german":"The building has been completed.","furigana":"しゅんこう"},{"japanese":"新社屋の竣工式に社員全員が出席した。","romaji":"Shinshaoku no shunkoushiki ni shain zen'in ga shusseki shita.","german":"All employees attended the completion ceremony of the new company building.","furigana":"しんしゃおく|しゅんこうしき|しゃいんぜんいん|しゅっせき"},{"japanese":"この橋は1964年に竣工しました。","romaji":"Kono hashi wa 1964-nen ni shunkou shimashita.","german":"This bridge was completed in 1964.","furigana":"はし|ねん|しゅんこう"}],"notes":"竣工 is the completion of a structure and a formal, written-language word from construction, press releases and news: ビルが竣工する, 竣工式 \"completion ceremony\". Its counterpart is 着工 \"start of construction\". 完工 and 落成 (落成式) mean the same; in daily life one simply says 完成する or ビルができる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"用途を教えてください。","romaji":"Yōto o oshiete kudasai.","german":"Please tell me the purpose of use.","furigana":"ようと|おし"},{"japanese":"このナイフは用途が広くて、キャンプでとても便利です。","romaji":"Kono naifu wa youto ga hirokute, kyanpu de totemo benri desu.","german":"This knife has a wide range of uses and is very handy for camping.","furigana":"ようと|ひろ|べんり"},{"japanese":"用途に合わせて、パソコンを選んだほうがいいですよ。","romaji":"Youto ni awasete, pasokon o eranda hou ga ii desu yo.","german":"You should choose a computer according to how you will use it.","furigana":"ようと|あ|えら"}],"notes":"用途 is the purpose of use - what something is or can be used for. Typical are 用途が広い \"widely usable\", 用途に合わせて/応じて選ぶ \"choose according to the purpose\" and 多用途 \"multi-purpose\". It sounds factual (product descriptions, forms); in conversation one more often says 使い道. 目的, by contrast, is the goal of an action, not the use of an object.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"防水加工がしてあります。","romaji":"Bōsui kakō ga shite arimasu.","german":"It has a waterproof finish.","furigana":"ぼうすいかこう"},{"japanese":"このスマホは防水なので、お風呂でも使えます。","romaji":"Kono sumaho wa bousui na node, ofuro de mo tsukaemasu.","german":"This smartphone is waterproof, so you can use it even in the bath.","furigana":"ぼうすい|ふろ|つか"},{"japanese":"雨の日に備えて、靴に防水スプレーをかけた。","romaji":"Ame no hi ni sonaete, kutsu ni bousui supuree o kaketa.","german":"In preparation for rainy days, I sprayed my shoes with waterproofing spray.","furigana":"あめ|ひ|そな|くつ|ぼうすい"}],"notes":"防水 means protection against water, or making something waterproof. It is mostly used attributively: 防水の時計 \"waterproof watch\", 防水スプレー \"waterproofing spray\", 防水加工 \"waterproof treatment\", or predicatively as in このスマホは防水だ. As a する-verb (屋根を防水する) it is technical. To distinguish: 防水 keeps water out completely, whereas 撥水 only makes it bead off (\"water-repellent\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"断熱材を入れました。","romaji":"Dannetsuzai o iremashita.","german":"We installed insulation material.","furigana":"だんねつざい|い"},{"japanese":"この家は断熱性が高いので、冬でも暖かいです。","romaji":"Kono ie wa dannetsusei ga takai node, fuyu demo atatakai desu.","german":"This house is well insulated, so it is warm even in winter.","furigana":"いえ|だんねつせい|たか|ふゆ|あたた"},{"japanese":"窓を断熱ガラスに替えたら、電気代が下がった。","romaji":"Mado o dannetsu garasu ni kaetara, denkidai ga sagatta.","german":"After we replaced the windows with insulating glass, the electricity bill went down.","furigana":"まど|だんねつ|か|でんきだい|さ"}],"notes":"断熱 is thermal insulation, preventing heat transfer through walls, windows or containers. It most often appears in compounds: 断熱材 \"insulating material\", 断熱性 \"insulating property\", 断熱効果, 断熱ガラス. Since older Japanese houses are often poorly insulated, 断熱性が高い is an important selling point. Related are 遮音 \"sound insulation\" and 保温 \"keeping warm\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"遮音性の高い壁です。","romaji":"Shaonsei no takai kabe desu.","german":"It's a wall with high sound insulation.","furigana":"しゃおんせい|たか|かべ"},{"japanese":"隣の部屋の音が気になるので、遮音カーテンを買いました。","romaji":"Tonari no heya no oto ga ki ni naru node, shaon kaaten o kaimashita.","german":"The noise from the next room bothers me, so I bought soundproof curtains.","furigana":"となり|へや|おと|き|しゃおん|か"},{"japanese":"このマンションは遮音性が低くて、上の階の足音がよく聞こえる。","romaji":"Kono manshon wa shaonsei ga hikukute, ue no kai no ashioto ga yoku kikoeru.","german":"This apartment has poor sound insulation, and you can clearly hear footsteps from upstairs.","furigana":"しゃおんせい|ひく|うえ|かい|あしおと|き"}],"notes":"遮音 is sound insulation, i.e. the shielding of noise (遮る \"to shield\" + 音 \"sound\"). It is a technical word from construction and housing, found mostly in 遮音性 \"sound insulation\", 遮音シート or 遮音カーテン. In everyday life 防音 \"soundproofing\" is better known (防音室); but when viewing apartments, 遮音性 is an important criterion.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"換気をしてください。","romaji":"Kanki o shite kudasai.","german":"Please ventilate the room.","furigana":"かんき"},{"japanese":"料理をするときは、換気扇を回してください。","romaji":"Ryouri o suru toki wa, kankisen o mawashite kudasai.","german":"When cooking, please turn on the ventilation fan.","furigana":"りょうり|かんきせん|まわ"},{"japanese":"この部屋は窓が小さくて換気が悪い。","romaji":"Kono heya wa mado ga chiisakute kanki ga warui.","german":"This room has small windows and poor ventilation.","furigana":"へや|まど|ちい|かんき|わる"}],"notes":"換気 is the ventilation of a room, i.e. exchanging stale air for fresh. Typical are 換気する/換気をする, colloquially also 部屋を換気する, as well as 換気扇 \"ventilation fan, extractor\" and 換気が悪い \"poorly ventilated\". Compared with 窓を開ける, 換気 names the purpose; in everyday speech one also says 空気を入れ替える.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"採光のよい部屋です。","romaji":"Saikō no yoi heya desu.","german":"It's a room with good natural light.","furigana":"さいこう|へや"},{"japanese":"北向きですが、大きな窓があるので採光は十分です。","romaji":"Kitamuki desu ga, ookina mado ga aru node saikou wa juubun desu.","german":"It faces north, but there is a large window, so the natural light is sufficient.","furigana":"きたむ|おお|まど|さいこう|じゅうぶん"},{"japanese":"天井に採光窓をつけて、廊下を明るくした。","romaji":"Tenjou ni saikoumado o tsukete, rouka o akaruku shita.","german":"We installed a skylight in the ceiling to brighten the hallway.","furigana":"てんじょう|さいこうまど|ろうか|あか"}],"notes":"採光 is the admission of daylight into an interior (採る \"to take in\" + 光 \"light\"). It is a technical word from architecture and real-estate ads: 採光がよい, 採光窓, 採光を確保する. In everyday speech one more often talks about 日当たり \"sunlight exposure\" (日当たりがいい); 採光 means daylight in general, even without direct sun, for example with north-facing windows.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"水はけのいい土地です。","romaji":"Mizuhake no ii tochi desu.","german":"It's land with good drainage.","furigana":"みず|とち"},{"japanese":"このグラウンドは水はけが悪くて、雨の後はすぐに使えない。","romaji":"Kono guraundo wa mizuhake ga warukute, ame no ato wa sugu ni tsukaenai.","german":"This sports field drains poorly, so it can't be used right after rain.","furigana":"みず|わる|あめ|あと|つか"},{"japanese":"ラベンダーは水はけのよい土で育ててください。","romaji":"Rabendaa wa mizuhake no yoi tsuchi de sodatete kudasai.","german":"Please grow lavender in well-drained soil.","furigana":"みず|つち|そだ"}],"notes":"水はけ describes how well water drains away or soaks in, for example with soil, building plots, sports fields or drains: 水はけがいい/よい or 水はけが悪い. The all-kanji spelling 水捌け is rarely seen; the mixed form is usual. It is an important criterion for gardeners and when buying a plot; 排水, by contrast, is the technical word for drainage through pipes and drains.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地盤が強いです。","romaji":"Jiban ga tsuyoi desu.","german":"The ground is solid.","furigana":"じばん|つよ"},{"japanese":"この辺りは地盤が弱いので、地震のときに揺れやすいです。","romaji":"Kono atari wa jiban ga yowai node, jishin no toki ni yureyasui desu.","german":"The ground around here is weak, so it shakes easily in an earthquake.","furigana":"あた|じばん|よわ|じしん|ゆ"},{"japanese":"家を建てる前に、地盤の調査をしました。","romaji":"Ie o tateru mae ni, jiban no chousa o shimashita.","german":"Before building the house, we had a ground survey done.","furigana":"いえ|た|まえ|じばん|ちょうさ"}],"notes":"地盤 is the natural ground on which one builds, i.e. the \"building ground\" or \"subsoil\". Its firmness is described with 地盤が固い／強い or 地盤が弱い／緩い; set phrases are 地盤沈下 \"ground subsidence\" and 地盤調査 \"ground survey\". Unlike 土台 (the \"foundation\" of concrete or stone that belongs to the building), 地盤 means the ground itself. Figuratively, it denotes a politician's power base in his constituency (選挙の地盤).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"更地にして売ります。","romaji":"Sarachi ni shite urimasu.","german":"I'll clear the land and sell it.","furigana":"さらち|う"},{"japanese":"古い家を壊して、更地になりました。","romaji":"Furui ie o kowashite, sarachi ni narimashita.","german":"The old house was torn down, and it became a vacant lot.","furigana":"ふる|いえ|こわ|さらち"},{"japanese":"駅の近くの更地に、新しいマンションが建つそうです。","romaji":"Eki no chikaku no sarachi ni, atarashii manshon ga tatsu sou desu.","german":"A new apartment building is said to be going up on the vacant lot near the station.","furigana":"えき|ちか|さらち|あたら|た"}],"notes":"更地 is a plot with nothing (any longer) standing on it - usually after an old house has been demolished, so ready for building and free of structures. Typical are 更地にする \"to clear a plot\", 更地になる and 更地で売る. The everyday word 空き地, by contrast, means a \"vacant, unused area\" in general, for example where children play; 更地 is more a real-estate and construction term. 更 here has the sense of \"new, fresh\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この地域では土地の区画整理が行われています。","romaji":"Kono chiiki de wa tochi no kukaku seiri ga okonawarete imasu.","german":"Land readjustment is being carried out in this area.","furigana":"ちいき|とち|くかくせいり|おこな"},{"japanese":"この住宅地は全部で三十の区画に分かれています。","romaji":"Kono juutakuchi wa zenbu de sanjuu no kukaku ni wakarete imasu.","german":"This residential area is divided into thirty lots in total.","furigana":"じゅうたくち|ぜんぶ|さんじゅう|くかく|わ"},{"japanese":"市民農園で一区画を借りて、野菜を育てています。","romaji":"Shimin nouen de hito kukaku o karite, yasai o sodatete imasu.","german":"I rent a plot in a community garden and grow vegetables.","furigana":"しみんのうえん|ひとくかく|か|やさい|そだ"}],"notes":"区画 is a demarcated area: \"lot\", \"building plot\", \"block\". It also serves as a counter for plots of land (一区画、二区画). Fixed combinations are 区画整理 \"land readjustment, reorganization of plots\" and 〜を区画に分ける. As a する-verb (区画する \"to demarcate, divide\") it is rather literary. For administrative zones one says 区域, for sections of a text 部分 or 段落, not 区画.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"用水路で田んぼに水を引きます。","romaji":"Yōsuiro de tanbo ni mizu o hikimasu.","german":"Water is drawn to the rice paddies through the irrigation canal.","furigana":"ようすいろ|た|みず|ひ"},{"japanese":"子供が用水路に落ちないように、柵が作られました。","romaji":"Kodomo ga yousuiro ni ochinai you ni, saku ga tsukuraremashita.","german":"A fence was built so that children don't fall into the irrigation canal.","furigana":"こども|ようすいろ|お|さく|つく"},{"japanese":"大雨で用水路の水があふれました。","romaji":"Ooame de yousuiro no mizu ga afuremashita.","german":"The water in the irrigation canal overflowed because of heavy rain.","furigana":"おおあめ|ようすいろ|みず"}],"notes":"用水路 is an artificially built canal or ditch that brings utility water (用水) for agriculture, industry or households - in Japan mostly narrow concrete ditches between rice fields. Water is led in with 水を引く. A shipping canal is a 運河, a drainage ditch a 排水路, and a natural river a 川. In the news the word often appears in connection with accidents and floods after heavy rain.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"堤防を歩きます。","romaji":"Teibō o arukimasu.","german":"I walk along the embankment.","furigana":"ていぼう|ある"},{"japanese":"大雨で川の堤防が切れて、町が水につかりました。","romaji":"Ooame de kawa no teibou ga kirete, machi ga mizu ni tsukarimashita.","german":"The river levee broke in the heavy rain, and the town was flooded.","furigana":"おおあめ|かわ|ていぼう|き|まち|みず"},{"japanese":"堤防の上で子供たちが凧を揚げています。","romaji":"Teibou no ue de kodomotachi ga tako o agete imasu.","german":"Children are flying kites on top of the embankment.","furigana":"ていぼう|うえ|こども|たこ|あ"}],"notes":"堤防 is an earthen wall or dike along a river or coast that protects against floods. Typical phrases: 堤防を築く \"to build an embankment\", 堤防が切れる／決壊する \"the embankment breaks\". The grassy bank on which one takes walks is often called 土手 in everyday speech. A dam that impounds a lake is ダム, and a breakwater in a harbor is 防波堤.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"貯水池の水位が下がりました。","romaji":"Chosuichi no suii ga sagarimashita.","german":"The water level of the reservoir has dropped.","furigana":"ちょすいち|すいい|さ"},{"japanese":"貯水池の水が少なくなって、水不足が心配されています。","romaji":"Chosuichi no mizu ga sukunaku natte, mizubusoku ga shinpai sarete imasu.","german":"The reservoir is running low, and there are worries about a water shortage.","furigana":"ちょすいち|みず|すく|みずぶそく|しんぱい"},{"japanese":"町の水道水は山の貯水池から来ています。","romaji":"Machi no suidousui wa yama no chosuichi kara kite imasu.","german":"The town's tap water comes from a reservoir in the mountains.","furigana":"まち|すいどうすい|やま|ちょすいち|き"}],"notes":"貯水池 is an artificially built water store (貯水 \"store water\" + 池 \"pond\") for drinking water, agriculture or power generation. The structure itself is called ダム, and the lake dammed up behind it ダム湖 or simply 貯水池. Common accompanying words are 水位 \"water level\" and 貯水率 \"fill level\", which is reported in the news in summer during dry spells. A natural lake is a 湖.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"水源を保護します。","romaji":"Suigen o hogo shimasu.","german":"We protect the water source.","furigana":"すいげん|ほご"},{"japanese":"この川の水源は、あの山の中にあります。","romaji":"Kono kawa no suigen wa, ano yama no naka ni arimasu.","german":"The source of this river is in those mountains.","furigana":"かわ|すいげん|やま|なか"},{"japanese":"この町は地下水を水源にしています。","romaji":"Kono machi wa chikasui o suigen ni shite imasu.","german":"This town uses groundwater as its water source.","furigana":"まち|ちかすい|すいげん"}],"notes":"水源 is the origin of a river or the source of a water supply (river, lake, groundwater). Typical combinations: 水源地 \"headwater area\", 水源を守る／保護する, 〜を水源にする \"to draw one's water from ...\". A single spring from which water gushes is called 泉 or 湧き水, and a hot spring 温泉. The word is rather matter-of-fact and often appears in environmental and administrative texts.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"遊歩道を散歩します。","romaji":"Yūhodō o sanpo shimasu.","german":"I take a walk on the promenade.","furigana":"ゆうほどう|さんぽ"},{"japanese":"川沿いに遊歩道が整備されています。","romaji":"Kawazoi ni yuuhodou ga seibi sarete imasu.","german":"A walking path has been built along the river.","furigana":"かわぞ|ゆうほどう|せいび"},{"japanese":"この遊歩道は自転車の通行が禁止です。","romaji":"Kono yuuhodou wa jitensha no tsuukou ga kinshi desu.","german":"Cycling is prohibited on this walking path.","furigana":"ゆうほどう|じてんしゃ|つうこう|きんし"}],"notes":"遊歩道 is a constructed footpath for strolling and enjoying the surroundings - along a river, in a park, on the coast or as a wooden walkway through a marsh. Cars are not allowed there, often not bicycles either. To be distinguished are 歩道 \"sidewalk\" beside a road and 登山道 or ハイキングコース for real mountain hiking trails. In daily life one also simply says 散歩道.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"展望台から景色を見ます。","romaji":"Tenbōdai kara keshiki o mimasu.","german":"I look at the view from the observation deck.","furigana":"てんぼうだい|けしき|み"},{"japanese":"タワーの展望台までエレベーターで一分です。","romaji":"Tawaa no tenboudai made erebeetaa de ippun desu.","german":"It takes one minute by elevator to the tower's observation deck.","furigana":"てんぼうだい|いっぷん"},{"japanese":"展望台は風が強いので、上着を持って行ってください。","romaji":"Tenboudai wa kaze ga tsuyoi node, uwagi o motte itte kudasai.","german":"It's windy on the observation deck, so please bring a jacket.","furigana":"てんぼうだい|かぜ|つよ|うわぎ|も|い"}],"notes":"展望台 is an observation platform on a mountain, tower or high-rise (展望 \"view\" + 台 \"platform\"). Typical: 展望台に上る／上がる and 展望台から〜を眺める／見下ろす. 展望 on its own is also used figuratively (\"prospects, outlook\", e.g. 将来の展望), whereas 展望台 is used only concretely. At airports one often speaks of 展望デッキ.","levelNote":"Compound: 展望 (N1) + 台 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"灯台が見えます。","romaji":"Tōdai ga miemasu.","german":"I can see the lighthouse.","furigana":"とうだい|み"},{"japanese":"岬の先に白い灯台が立っています。","romaji":"Misaki no saki ni shiroi toudai ga tatte imasu.","german":"A white lighthouse stands at the tip of the cape.","furigana":"みさき|さき|しろ|とうだい|た"},{"japanese":"「灯台下暗し」で、探していた眼鏡は机の上にありました。","romaji":"\"Toudai moto kurashi\" de, sagashite ita megane wa tsukue no ue ni arimashita.","german":"As the saying 「灯台下暗し」 goes, the glasses I was looking for were on the desk.","furigana":"とうだいもとくら|さが|めがね|つくえ|うえ"}],"notes":"灯台 is the \"lighthouse\"; related are 灯台の光 and 灯台守 \"lighthouse keeper\". The idiom 灯台下暗し \"it is dark under the lamp stand\" means that one overlooks what is close at hand - originally the old oil-lamp stand (meaning 2) is meant, not the lighthouse. Do not confuse it with 東大 (same reading, short form for the University of Tokyo).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"桟橋から船に乗ります。","romaji":"Sanbashi kara fune ni norimasu.","german":"I board the ship from the pier.","furigana":"さんばし|ふね|の"},{"japanese":"夕方、桟橋で釣りをしている人が多いです。","romaji":"Yuugata, sanbashi de tsuri o shite iru hito ga ooi desu.","german":"In the evening, many people fish on the pier.","furigana":"ゆうがた|さんばし|つ|ひと|おお"},{"japanese":"台風で桟橋が壊れて、船が出せません。","romaji":"Taifuu de sanbashi ga kowarete, fune ga dasemasen.","german":"The typhoon destroyed the pier, so the boats cannot go out.","furigana":"たいふう|さんばし|こわ|ふね|だ"}],"notes":"桟橋 is a pier or jetty that projects into the water and where ships and boats dock, from a large ferry pier to a small boat jetty on a lake. Typical: 船が桟橋に着く, 桟橋から船に乗る. Despite 橋, it does not mean a bridge across a river. Large reinforced harbor quays for cargo ships are rather called 埠頭 or 岸壁; the whole harbor is 港.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"埠頭でコンテナを降ろします。","romaji":"Futō de kontena o oroshimasu.","german":"Containers are unloaded at the wharf.","furigana":"ふとう|お"},{"japanese":"大きな客船が埠頭に止まっています。","romaji":"Ookina kyakusen ga futou ni tomatte imasu.","german":"A large passenger ship is moored at the pier.","furigana":"おお|きゃくせん|ふとう|と"},{"japanese":"夜の埠頭から見る港の景色はきれいです。","romaji":"Yoru no futou kara miru minato no keshiki wa kirei desu.","german":"The view of the harbor from the wharf at night is beautiful.","furigana":"よる|ふとう|み|みなと|けしき"}],"notes":"埠頭 is a paved harbor quay where large ships dock and are loaded or unloaded; it often appears in place names of port facilities (〜埠頭). The word is rather written and technical - in daily life one usually simply says 港. Unlike 桟橋 \"jetty, pier\" (which can also be small), 埠頭 is a large facility. Since 埠 is not a Jōyō kanji, government offices and newspapers often write ふ頭.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"造船所で船を造ります。","romaji":"Zōsenjo de fune o tsukurimasu.","german":"Ships are built at the shipyard.","furigana":"ぞうせんじょ|ふね|つく"},{"japanese":"祖父は長崎の造船所で働いていました。","romaji":"Sofu wa Nagasaki no zousenjo de hataraite imashita.","german":"My grandfather worked at a shipyard in Nagasaki.","furigana":"そふ|ながさき|ぞうせんじょ|はたら"},{"japanese":"造船所の見学ツアーに参加しました。","romaji":"Zousenjo no kengaku tsuaa ni sanka shimashita.","german":"I took part in a shipyard tour.","furigana":"ぞうせんじょ|けんがく|さんか"}],"notes":"造船所 is the \"shipyard\" (造船 \"shipbuilding\" + 所 \"place\"). 造船 alone denotes shipbuilding as an industry (造船業). For building ships and other large things, 造る is written instead of 作る: 船を造る. 所 is read じょ here, as in 研究所; other words ending in 所 have しょ (製材所, 事務所), which has to be learned individually. A dry dock or repair dock is called ドック.","levelNote":"Compound: 造船 (N2) + 所 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"精錬所で鉱石から銅を取り出します。","romaji":"Seirenjo de kouseki kara dou o toridashimasu.","german":"In der Hütte wird Kupfer aus dem Erz gewonnen.","furigana":"せいれんじょ|こうせき|どう|と|だ"},{"japanese":"この町は昔、銅の精錬所で栄えました。","romaji":"Kono machi wa mukashi, dou no seirenjo de sakaemashita.","german":"Diese Stadt ist früher durch ihre Kupferhütte reich geworden.","furigana":"まち|むかし|どう|せいれんじょ|さか"},{"japanese":"精錬所の煙で、周りの山の木が枯れてしまいました。","romaji":"Seirenjo no kemuri de, mawari no yama no ki ga karete shimaimashita.","german":"Durch den Rauch der Schmelzhütte sind die Bäume auf den umliegenden Bergen abgestorben.","furigana":"せいれんじょ|けむり|まわ|やま|き|か"}],"notes":"精錬所 ist ein Hüttenwerk, in dem aus Erz Metall gewonnen und gereinigt wird (精錬 „verhütten, raffinieren“). Das deutsche „Raffinerie“ im Sinn von Erdölraffinerie heißt dagegen 製油所, und Zucker oder Öl werden 精製 („raffiniert“), nicht 精錬. In der Fachsprache unterscheidet man manchmal 製錬 (Gewinnung aus Erz) und 精錬 (Reinigung), im allgemeinen Gebrauch werden beide gemischt. Historisch sind Kupferhütten wie in Ashio auch mit Umweltschäden verbunden.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ビール醸造所を見学しました。","romaji":"Bīru jōzōsho o kengaku shimashita.","german":"I toured a beer brewery.","furigana":"じょうぞうしょ|けんがく"},{"japanese":"この醸造所では、昔ながらの方法で醤油を作っています。","romaji":"Kono jouzousho de wa, mukashinagara no houhou de shouyu o tsukutte imasu.","german":"This brewery makes soy sauce using traditional methods.","furigana":"じょうぞうしょ|むかし|ほうほう|しょうゆ|つく"},{"japanese":"地元の醸造所で作られたワインを飲んでみました。","romaji":"Jimoto no jouzousho de tsukurareta wain o nonde mimashita.","german":"I tried wine made at a local winery.","furigana":"じもと|じょうぞうしょ|つく|の"}],"notes":"醸造所 is the general, neutral term for an establishment that produces by fermentation (醸造): beer, sake, wine, but also soy sauce, miso and vinegar. In daily life more specific words are more common: 酒蔵 or 蔵元 for sake breweries, ブルワリー for beer breweries, ワイナリー for wineries. To tour one is 醸造所を見学する. 所 is read しょ here.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"製材所で木材を加工します。","romaji":"Seizaisho de mokuzai o kakō shimasu.","german":"Timber is processed at the sawmill.","furigana":"せいざいしょ|もくざい|かこう"},{"japanese":"製材所から木の香りがしてきます。","romaji":"Seizaisho kara ki no kaori ga shite kimasu.","german":"The smell of wood is coming from the sawmill.","furigana":"せいざいしょ|き|かお"},{"japanese":"父は山の近くの製材所で働いています。","romaji":"Chichi wa yama no chikaku no seizaisho de hataraite imasu.","german":"My father works at a sawmill near the mountains.","furigana":"ちち|やま|ちか|せいざいしょ|はたら"}],"notes":"製材 means sawing logs (丸太) into boards and beams; 製材所 is the \"sawmill\" that makes 木材 \"lumber\" from them. 所 is read しょ here, unlike in 造船所 (じょ) - such readings must be learned word by word. The word is neutral and matter-of-fact; in company names 〜製材所 is common.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鋳物の鍋は丈夫です。","romaji":"Imono no nabe wa jōbu desu.","german":"Cast-iron pots are sturdy.","furigana":"いもの|なべ|じょうぶ"},{"japanese":"この町は昔から鋳物の産地として有名です。","romaji":"Kono machi wa mukashi kara imono no sanchi to shite yuumei desu.","german":"This town has long been famous as a production center for castings.","furigana":"まち|むかし|いもの|さんち|ゆうめい"},{"japanese":"南部鉄器は岩手県の伝統的な鋳物です。","romaji":"Nanbu tekki wa Iwate-ken no dentouteki na imono desu.","german":"Nanbu ironware is a traditional casting from Iwate Prefecture.","furigana":"なんぶてっき|いわてけん|でんとうてき|いもの"}],"notes":"鋳物 is a cast metal object (鋳る \"to cast\" + 物), i.e. castings or cast goods; the workshop is called 鋳物工場. The material cast iron is technically 鋳鉄, and a cast-iron pan is often simply called 鉄鍋 or 鉄のフライパン in everyday speech. The counterpart is 鍛造 \"forged\". Well-known cast goods are the iron kettles 南部鉄器 from Iwate.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"チタン合金を使います。","romaji":"Chitan gōkin o tsukaimasu.","german":"We use a titanium alloy.","furigana":"ごうきん|つか"},{"japanese":"真鍮は銅と亜鉛の合金です。","romaji":"Shinchuu wa dou to aen no goukin desu.","german":"Brass is an alloy of copper and zinc.","furigana":"しんちゅう|どう|あえん|ごうきん"},{"japanese":"この自転車のフレームはアルミ合金でできていて、とても軽いです。","romaji":"Kono jitensha no fureemu wa arumi goukin de dekite ite, totemo karui desu.","german":"The frame of this bicycle is made of an aluminum alloy and is very light.","furigana":"じてんしゃ|ごうきん|かる"}],"notes":"合金 means \"alloy\". It mostly stands as the second element after the main metal: アルミ合金, チタン合金, 形状記憶合金 \"shape-memory alloy\". The composition is described with 〜と〜の合金. The word is technical, but appears often in product descriptions. The reading is ごうきん, not がっきん.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"天然染料で染めます。","romaji":"Tennen senryō de somemasu.","german":"I dye with natural dyes.","furigana":"てんねんせんりょう|そ"},{"japanese":"昔は植物から染料を作っていました。","romaji":"Mukashi wa shokubutsu kara senryou o tsukutte imashita.","german":"In the past, dyes were made from plants.","furigana":"むかし|しょくぶつ|せんりょう|つく"},{"japanese":"化学染料は色がきれいですが、環境への影響も考える必要があります。","romaji":"Kagaku senryou wa iro ga kirei desu ga, kankyou e no eikyou mo kangaeru hitsuyou ga arimasu.","german":"Chemical dyes have beautiful colors, but you also need to consider their impact on the environment.","furigana":"かがくせんりょう|いろ|かんきょう|えいきょう|かんが|ひつよう"}],"notes":"染料 is a dye used for dyeing (染める) textiles, yarn or leather; a distinction is made between 天然染料 and 合成染料 or 化学染料. To be distinguished from it are 顔料 \"pigment\" in paints and lacquers, 絵の具 \"artist's paint\" and ペンキ \"house paint\". For food, one speaks of 着色料, not 染料.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"藍染め体験をしました。","romaji":"Aizome taiken o shimashita.","german":"I tried indigo dyeing.","furigana":"あいぞ|たいけん"},{"japanese":"藍染めのハンカチをお土産に買いました。","romaji":"Aizome no hankachi o omiyage ni kaimashita.","german":"I bought an indigo-dyed handkerchief as a souvenir.","furigana":"あいぞ|みやげ|か"},{"japanese":"徳島は昔から藍染めが盛んな所です。","romaji":"Tokushima wa mukashi kara aizome ga sakan na tokoro desu.","german":"Tokushima has long been a place where indigo dyeing thrives.","furigana":"とくしま|むかし|あいぞ|さか|ところ"}],"notes":"藍染め is dyeing with indigo (藍) and also the fabric dyed this way; the typical deep blue color is called 藍色 and is regarded as \"Japan blue\". One says 藍染めのハンカチ, 藍染め体験 (a workshop for tourists) or, as a verb, 藍で染める. The spelling 藍染 without め also occurs. A well-known center is Tokushima Prefecture.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"伝統的な織物を見ました。","romaji":"Dentōteki na orimono o mimashita.","german":"I saw traditional textiles.","furigana":"でんとうてき|おりもの|み"},{"japanese":"西陣織は京都の有名な織物です。","romaji":"Nishijin-ori wa Kyouto no yuumei na orimono desu.","german":"Nishijin-ori is a famous woven textile from Kyoto.","furigana":"にしじんおり|きょうと|ゆうめい|おりもの"},{"japanese":"祖母は若い頃、織物工場で働いていました。","romaji":"Sobo wa wakai koro, orimono koujou de hataraite imashita.","german":"In her youth, my grandmother worked in a textile mill.","furigana":"そぼ|わか|ころ|おりものこうじょう|はたら"}],"notes":"織物 is woven cloth (織る \"to weave\" + 物), i.e. \"woven fabric, textile\". Knitted goods, by contrast, are 編み物, and fabric in general 布 or 生地. For well-known weaving traditions, 〜織 is used as a suffix, e.g. 西陣織 (Kyoto) or 博多織. The weaving mill as a business is 織物工場.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"刺繍を趣味にしています。","romaji":"Shishū o shumi ni shiteimasu.","german":"Embroidery is my hobby.","furigana":"ししゅう|しゅみ"},{"japanese":"ハンカチに名前を刺繍しました。","romaji":"Hankachi ni namae o shishuu shimashita.","german":"I embroidered my name on the handkerchief.","furigana":"なまえ|ししゅう"},{"japanese":"このドレスには花の刺繍が入っています。","romaji":"Kono doresu ni wa hana no shishuu ga haitte imasu.","german":"This dress has flower embroidery.","furigana":"はな|ししゅう|はい"}],"notes":"刺繍 is \"embroidery\"; as a する-verb it is transitive: 〜に〜を刺繍する \"to embroider something on something\". If a garment is embroidered, one says 刺繍が入っている or 刺繍入りの〜. As a hobby: 刺繍をする. Since 繍 is not a Jōyō kanji, the word is often seen in kana (ししゅう). Distinguish it from 縫う \"to sew\" and 編む \"to knit\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"七宝焼きの作品を買いました。","romaji":"Shippō yaki no sakuhin o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a piece of cloisonne work.","furigana":"しっぽうや|さくひん|か"},{"japanese":"七宝焼きのブローチを母にプレゼントしました。","romaji":"Shippou yaki no buroochi o haha ni purezento shimashita.","german":"I gave my mother a cloisonne brooch as a present.","furigana":"しっぽうや|はは"},{"japanese":"体験教室で七宝焼きのペンダントを作りました。","romaji":"Taiken kyoushitsu de shippou yaki no pendanto o tsukurimashita.","german":"At the workshop I made a cloisonne pendant.","furigana":"たいけんきょうしつ|しっぽうや|つく"}],"notes":"七宝焼き is enamel art, especially cloisonné: colored glass powder is applied to metal and fired (焼き). 七宝 \"the seven treasures\" comes from Buddhism and stands for precious colors. Despite 焼き, it is not ceramics - pottery is called 焼き物. It is often seen in brooches, pendants and vases; in workshops you can make 七宝焼き yourself (七宝焼きを作る).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"蒔絵の箱が美しいです。","romaji":"Makie no hako ga utsukushii desu.","german":"The maki-e box is beautiful.","furigana":"まきえ|はこ|うつく"},{"japanese":"蒔絵は漆の上に金や銀の粉をまいて模様を描く技法です。","romaji":"Makie wa urushi no ue ni kin ya gin no kona o maite moyou o egaku gihou desu.","german":"Maki-e is a technique of sprinkling gold or silver powder on lacquer to draw patterns.","furigana":"まきえ|うるし|うえ|きん|ぎん|こな|もよう|えが|ぎほう"},{"japanese":"美術館で江戸時代の蒔絵の作品を見ました。","romaji":"Bijutsukan de Edo jidai no makie no sakuhin o mimashita.","german":"I saw maki-e works from the Edo period at the museum.","furigana":"びじゅつかん|えどじだい|まきえ|さくひん|み"}],"notes":"蒔絵 is a Japanese lacquer art: gold or silver powder is sprinkled onto still-wet urushi lacquer (漆) (蒔く \"sprinkle, sow\" + 絵 \"picture\"). It is found on small boxes, bowls, writing boxes and other lacquerware (漆器). Typical are 蒔絵の箱 or 蒔絵を施す (written language, \"to decorate with maki-e\"). The word is a technical term of the decorative crafts.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"切り絵の作品を飾ります。","romaji":"Kirie no sakuhin o kazarimasu.","german":"I display paper-cut artworks.","furigana":"き|え|さくひん|かざ"},{"japanese":"カッターナイフで細かい切り絵を作るのが好きです。","romaji":"Kattaa naifu de komakai kirie o tsukuru no ga suki desu.","german":"I like making fine paper cuttings with a utility knife.","furigana":"こま|き|え|つく|す"},{"japanese":"子供たちは色紙で切り絵をして遊びました。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa irogami de kirie o shite asobimashita.","german":"The children played making paper cuttings with colored paper.","furigana":"こども|いろがみ|き|え|あそ"}],"notes":"切り絵 is a picture made of cut paper (切る + 絵), made with scissors or a utility knife - that is, a \"paper cut\". One says 切り絵を作る or 切り絵をする. Related are 切り紙 (folded paper in which patterns are cut), 折り紙 \"paper folding\" and 貼り絵 \"collage\". The spelling 切絵 without り also occurs.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"水墨画を描いています。","romaji":"Suibokuga o kaiteimasu.","german":"I paint ink wash pictures.","furigana":"すいぼくが|か"},{"japanese":"水墨画は墨の濃さだけで山や川を表します。","romaji":"Suibokuga wa sumi no kosa dake de yama ya kawa o arawashimasu.","german":"Ink wash painting depicts mountains and rivers solely through the gradation of the ink.","furigana":"すいぼくが|すみ|こ|やま|かわ|あらわ"},{"japanese":"お寺のふすまに見事な水墨画が描かれています。","romaji":"Otera no fusuma ni migoto na suibokuga ga egakarete imasu.","german":"A magnificent ink wash painting is drawn on the sliding doors of the temple.","furigana":"てら|みごと|すいぼくが|えが"}],"notes":"水墨画 is ink painting using black ink (墨) and water (水), working only with gradations of light and dark. It came from China together with Zen Buddhism; the most famous Japanese master is 雪舟. A more everyday synonym is 墨絵. To paint is 水墨画を描く (かく or, more elevated, えがく).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本画展に行きました。","romaji":"Nihongaten ni ikimashita.","german":"I went to an exhibition of Japanese-style painting.","furigana":"にほんがてん|い"},{"japanese":"日本画は岩絵の具という鉱物の絵の具を使います。","romaji":"Nihonga wa iwaenogu to iu koubutsu no enogu o tsukaimasu.","german":"Nihonga uses mineral pigments called iwa-enogu.","furigana":"にほんが|いわえ|ぐ|こうぶつ|え|ぐ|つか"},{"japanese":"姉は美大で日本画を専攻しています。","romaji":"Ane wa bidai de nihonga o senkou shite imasu.","german":"My older sister is majoring in Japanese-style painting at art college.","furigana":"あね|びだい|にほんが|せんこう"}],"notes":"日本画 is not simply \"a picture from Japan\" but a genre: painting in the traditional Japanese style with mineral pigments (岩絵の具) and glue on silk or washi. The term arose in the Meiji period as a counterpart to 洋画 \"Western painting, oil painting\" - 洋画 also means \"foreign film\". Artists are called 日本画家, exhibitions 日本画展.","levelNote":"Compound: 日本 (N5) + 画 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"版画を刷りました。","romaji":"Hanga o surimashita.","german":"I printed a print.","furigana":"はんが|す"},{"japanese":"小学校の図工の時間に木版画を作りました。","romaji":"Shougakkou no zukou no jikan ni mokuhanga o tsukurimashita.","german":"In art class at elementary school, we made woodblock prints.","furigana":"しょうがっこう|ずこう|じかん|もくはんが|つく"},{"japanese":"浮世絵は江戸時代の版画です。","romaji":"Ukiyoe wa Edo jidai no hanga desu.","german":"Ukiyo-e are prints from the Edo period.","furigana":"うきよえ|えどじだい|はんが"}],"notes":"版画 is the general term for prints; more precisely one distinguishes 木版画 \"woodblock print\", 銅版画 \"copperplate engraving, etching\" and シルクスクリーン \"silk screen\". Typical verbs: 版を彫る \"to carve the printing block\" and 版画を刷る \"to print\" (刷る, not 擦る). The famous ukiyo-e are woodblock prints; in Japanese elementary schools, children often make their own woodblock prints in art class.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"古い仏像が安置されています。","romaji":"Furui butsuzō ga anchi sareteimasu.","german":"Old Buddhist statues are enshrined.","furigana":"ふる|ぶつぞう|あんち"},{"japanese":"奈良のお寺で大きな仏像を見ました。","romaji":"Nara no otera de ookina butsuzou o mimashita.","german":"I saw a large Buddha statue at a temple in Nara.","furigana":"なら|てら|おお|ぶつぞう|み"},{"japanese":"最近は仏像が好きな若い人も増えています。","romaji":"Saikin wa butsuzou ga suki na wakai hito mo fuete imasu.","german":"Recently, the number of young people who love Buddhist statues is also increasing.","furigana":"さいきん|ぶつぞう|す|わか|ひと|ふ"}],"notes":"仏像 denotes the statue or image of a Buddha and, generally, of Buddhist figures such as Kannon or temple guardians. Respectful phrases are 仏像を拝む \"to pray before a statue\" and 仏像が安置されている \"a statue is (reverently) enshrined\". A \"great Buddha\" is 大仏; statues in general are 像 (銅像 \"bronze statue\"). Buddha statues are counted with 体.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"庭に石灯籠があります。","romaji":"Niwa ni ishidōrō ga arimasu.","german":"There is a stone lantern in the garden.","furigana":"にわ|いしどうろう"},{"japanese":"石灯籠に火が入ると、庭の雰囲気が変わります。","romaji":"Ishidourou ni hi ga hairu to, niwa no fun'iki ga kawarimasu.","german":"When the stone lanterns are lit, the atmosphere of the garden changes.","furigana":"いしどうろう|ひ|はい|にわ|ふんいき|か"},{"japanese":"神社の参道に石灯籠が並んでいます。","romaji":"Jinja no sandou ni ishidourou ga narande imasu.","german":"Stone lanterns line the approach to the shrine.","furigana":"じんじゃ|さんどう|いしどうろう|なら"}],"notes":"石灯籠 is a stone lantern, as found at temples, shrines and in Japanese gardens. 灯籠 (とうろう) undergoes rendaku in the compound: いしどうろう. Originally they held votive lights; today they are mostly decoration. Do not confuse with 提灯 (portable paper lantern); 灯籠流し is the floating of lanterns on water at Obon.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"枯山水の庭を眺めます。","romaji":"Karesansui no niwa o nagamemasu.","german":"I gaze at the dry landscape garden.","furigana":"かれさんすい|にわ|なが"},{"japanese":"枯山水では、白い砂で水の流れを表します。","romaji":"Karesansui de wa, shiroi suna de mizu no nagare o arawashimasu.","german":"In a karesansui garden, the flow of water is represented by white sand.","furigana":"かれさんすい|しろ|すな|みず|なが|あらわ"},{"japanese":"龍安寺の石庭は枯山水の代表です。","romaji":"Ryouanji no sekitei wa karesansui no daihyou desu.","german":"The rock garden of Ryoanji is the representative karesansui.","furigana":"|せきてい|かれさんすい|だいひょう"}],"notes":"枯山水 is the Japanese dry landscape garden without real water: stones, gravel or sand and moss represent mountains, islands and watercourses. Literally a \"dried-up mountain-and-water landscape\" (山水 = landscape). One mostly says 枯山水の庭; it is typical of Zen temples. The counterpart is the pond garden 池泉庭園. Reading かれさんすい - 枯 is not こ here.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"坪庭に植物を植えました。","romaji":"Tsuboniwa ni shokubutsu o uemashita.","german":"I planted plants in the courtyard garden.","furigana":"つぼにわ|しょくぶつ|う"},{"japanese":"京都の町家には小さな坪庭があります。","romaji":"Kyouto no machiya ni wa chiisana tsuboniwa ga arimasu.","german":"The old townhouses in Kyoto have small courtyard gardens.","furigana":"きょうと|まちや|ちい|つぼにわ"},{"japanese":"坪庭から光と風が入って、家の中が明るいです。","romaji":"Tsuboniwa kara hikari to kaze ga haitte, ie no naka ga akarui desu.","german":"Light and air come in through the small courtyard garden, so the house is bright.","furigana":"つぼにわ|ひかり|かぜ|はい|いえ|なか|あか"}],"notes":"坪庭 is a tiny garden enclosed by parts of a building in the interior of a house - typical of the narrow townhouses (町家) in Kyoto. 坪 is a unit of area of about 3.3 m² and emphasizes smallness. The garden brings light and air into the house. 中庭, by contrast, is any \"inner courtyard\", including a large one, for example in schools or hotels.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"苔むした庭が美しい。","romaji":"Koke mushita niwa ga utsukushii.","german":"The moss-covered garden is beautiful.","furigana":"こけ|にわ|うつく"},{"japanese":"日の当たらない北側の壁に苔が生えています。","romaji":"Hi no ataranai kitagawa no kabe ni koke ga haete imasu.","german":"Moss is growing on the shady north wall.","furigana":"ひ|あ|きたがわ|かべ|こけ|は"},{"japanese":"池の周りの石は苔で滑りやすいので、気をつけてください。","romaji":"Ike no mawari no ishi wa koke de suberiyasui node, ki o tsukete kudasai.","german":"The stones around the pond are slippery with moss, so please be careful.","furigana":"いけ|まわ|いし|こけ|すべ|き"}],"notes":"苔 is \"moss\" and, in general, moss-like growth. According to the dictionary it is often written in kana (こけ, コケ), but in garden and cultural contexts 苔 is usual. Moss \"grows\": 苔が生える; 苔むす means \"to be moss-covered\" and suggests venerable age (also in the national anthem: 苔のむすまで). In Japan moss is aesthetically appreciated, for example at the 苔寺 in Kyoto.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"梅雨に紫陽花が咲きます。","romaji":"Tsuyu ni ajisai ga sakimasu.","german":"Hydrangeas bloom in the rainy season.","furigana":"つゆ|あじさい|さ"},{"japanese":"あじさいの花の色は、土によって青やピンクに変わります。","romaji":"Ajisai no hana no iro wa, tsuchi ni yotte ao ya pinku ni kawarimasu.","german":"The color of hydrangea flowers changes to blue or pink depending on the soil.","furigana":"はな|いろ|つち|あお|か"},{"japanese":"鎌倉の紫陽花を見に行きませんか。","romaji":"Kamakura no ajisai o mi ni ikimasen ka.","german":"Shall we go see the hydrangeas in Kamakura?","furigana":"かまくら|あじさい|み|い"}],"notes":"紫陽花 is the hydrangea. The kanji are a purely meaning-based spelling (熟字訓); in everyday life it is usually written あじさい or アジサイ. The hydrangea is the symbol of the June rainy season 梅雨, and many temples are famous hydrangea spots. Its flower color depends on the soil: rather blue in acidic soil, rather pink in alkaline soil.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"秋に彼岸花が咲きます。","romaji":"Aki ni higanbana ga sakimasu.","german":"Red spider lilies bloom in autumn.","furigana":"あき|ひがんばな|さ"},{"japanese":"彼岸花には毒があるので、触ったら手を洗いましょう。","romaji":"Higanbana ni wa doku ga aru node, sawattara te o araimashou.","german":"Red spider lilies are poisonous, so let's wash our hands if we touch them.","furigana":"ひがんばな|どく|さわ|て|あら"},{"japanese":"彼岸花は「曼珠沙華」とも呼ばれます。","romaji":"Higanbana wa \"manjushage\" to mo yobaremasu.","german":"The red spider lily is also called 「曼珠沙華」.","furigana":"ひがんばな||よ"}],"notes":"彼岸花 is the red spider lily, named after 彼岸, the week around the autumn equinox in which people visit graves - exactly when it blooms. Because it often grows at cemeteries and along the edges of rice fields and is poisonous, it is associated with death and is not suitable as a gift. A second, poetic name is 曼珠沙華.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"金木犀の香りが漂います。","romaji":"Kinmokusei no kaori ga tadayoimasu.","german":"The scent of osmanthus is drifting.","furigana":"きんもくせい|かお|ただよ"},{"japanese":"金木犀の花はオレンジ色で、とても小さいです。","romaji":"Kinmokusei no hana wa orenji-iro de, totemo chiisai desu.","german":"Osmanthus flowers are orange and very small.","furigana":"きんもくせい|はな|いろ|ちい"},{"japanese":"金木犀の香りのハンドクリームが人気です。","romaji":"Kinmokusei no kaori no hando kuriimu ga ninki desu.","german":"Osmanthus-scented hand cream is popular.","furigana":"きんもくせい|かお|にんき"}],"notes":"金木犀 is the fragrant orange osmanthus, a shrub with tiny orange flowers that give off an intense sweet scent from late September to October - for many Japanese the epitome of the start of autumn. Typical: 金木犀の香りがする／漂う. It is often written in katakana (キンモクセイ). The white-flowering relative is called 銀木犀.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"椿の花が咲いています。","romaji":"Tsubaki no hana ga saiteimasu.","german":"The camellias are in bloom.","furigana":"つばき|はな|さ"},{"japanese":"椿油は髪にいいと言われています。","romaji":"Tsubaki abura wa kami ni ii to iwarete imasu.","german":"Camellia oil is said to be good for the hair.","furigana":"つばきあぶら|かみ|い"},{"japanese":"椿は花ごと落ちるので、お見舞いには向かないと言われます。","romaji":"Tsubaki wa hana goto ochiru node, omimai ni wa mukanai to iwaremasu.","german":"Because camellia flowers fall off whole, they are said to be unsuitable for hospital visits.","furigana":"つばき|はな|お|みま|む|い"}],"notes":"椿 is the Japanese camellia, which blooms red or white from winter into spring. Its flowers fall off whole, which recalls a falling head - for this reason one doesn't bring them to hospital visits. 山茶花 (さざんか) looks similar, but blooms earlier and loses its petals one by one. From the seeds one obtains 椿油, a traditional hair oil.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"梅の花が綺麗です。","romaji":"Ume no hana ga kirei desu.","german":"Die Pflaumenblüten sind schön.","furigana":"うめ|はな|きれい"},{"japanese":"梅の実で梅酒を作りました。","romaji":"Ume no mi de umeshu o tsukurimashita.","german":"Aus den Ume-Früchten habe ich Pflaumenlikör angesetzt.","furigana":"うめ|み|うめしゅ|つく"},{"japanese":"松竹梅のコースで、一番安いのは梅です。","romaji":"Shouchikubai no koosu de, ichiban yasui no wa ume desu.","german":"Bei den Menüs „Kiefer, Bambus, Pflaume“ ist „Pflaume“ das günstigste.","furigana":"しょうちくばい|いちばんやす|うめ"}],"notes":"梅 ist botanisch die Japanische Aprikose (Prunus mume), im Deutschen meist „Pflaume“ genannt. Sie blüht im Februar und März, noch vor den Kirschen; Blütenbetrachtung heißt 梅見 oder 観梅. Die sauren Früchte isst man nicht roh, sondern als 梅干し oder 梅酒. In der Rangfolge 松竹梅 (Kiefer, Bambus, Pflaume) steht 梅 für die einfachste Stufe, etwa bei Menüs.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 45"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"藤棚が見事です。","romaji":"Fujidana ga migoto desu.","german":"The wisteria trellis is magnificent.","furigana":"ふじだな|みごと"},{"japanese":"五月になると、藤の花が紫色に咲きます。","romaji":"Gogatsu ni naru to, fuji no hana ga murasakiiro ni sakimasu.","german":"In May, the wisteria blooms in purple.","furigana":"ごがつ|ふじ|はな|むらさきいろ|さ"},{"japanese":"藤は名字にもよく使われる漢字で、佐藤や伊藤などがあります。","romaji":"Fuji wa myouji ni mo yoku tsukawareru kanji de, Satou ya Itou nado ga arimasu.","german":"The kanji for wisteria is also often used in family names, such as Satō and Itō.","furigana":"ふじ|みょうじ|つか|かんじ|さとう|いとう"}],"notes":"藤 is the wisteria, a climbing plant with long, hanging purple flower clusters that blooms from late April to May. It is grown on a pergola, the 藤棚; the light purple color is called 藤色. In family names 藤 is mostly read とう (佐藤, 伊藤), and at the start of a word often ふじ (藤田). Do not confuse with 富士 (same reading, Mount Fuji).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"菖蒲園に行きました。","romaji":"Shōbuen ni ikimashita.","german":"I went to an iris garden.","furigana":"しょうぶえん|い"},{"japanese":"五月五日には菖蒲湯に入る習慣があります。","romaji":"Gogatsu itsuka ni wa shoubuyu ni hairu shuukan ga arimasu.","german":"On May 5th there is a custom of taking a sweet flag bath.","furigana":"ごがついつか|しょうぶゆ|はい|しゅうかん"},{"japanese":"六月になると、池の周りに菖蒲が咲きます。","romaji":"Rokugatsu ni naru to, ike no mawari ni shoubu ga sakimasu.","german":"In June, irises bloom around the pond.","furigana":"ろくがつ|いけ|まわ|しょうぶ|さ"}],"notes":"菖蒲 (しょうぶ) actually refers to the sweet flag, whose fragrant leaves are put into the bath water on Children's Day (May 5) (菖蒲湯). Colloquially, however, it mostly means the Japanese water iris 花菖蒲, which blooms in June in 菖蒲園. The same kanji read あやめ refer to yet another kind of iris. Because しょうぶ sounds like 尚武 \"martial spirit\", the plant is regarded as a good-luck symbol for boys.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"月見団子を食べます。","romaji":"Tsukimi dango o tabemasu.","german":"I eat tsukimi dango, the rice dumplings for moon viewing.","furigana":"つきみだんご|た"},{"japanese":"十五夜にみんなでお月見をしました。","romaji":"Juugoya ni minna de otsukimi o shimashita.","german":"On the night of the harvest moon, we all viewed the moon together.","furigana":"じゅうごや|つきみ"},{"japanese":"月見そばを注文しました。","romaji":"Tsukimi soba o chuumon shimashita.","german":"I ordered tsukimi soba, soba with a raw egg as the \"full moon\".","furigana":"つきみ|ちゅうもん"}],"notes":"月見 is the viewing of the autumn full moon, especially on the night of 十五夜 (the 15th day of the 8th lunar month, mid-September to early October), traditionally with 月見団子 and pampas grass. Politely and warmly one says お月見 and お月見をする. Figuratively, dishes with an egg as the \"moon\" are called 月見〜 (月見そば, 月見バーガー). Comparable formations are 花見 and 雪見.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"露天風呂で雪見をします。","romaji":"Rotenburo de yukimi o shimasu.","german":"We enjoy snow viewing from an open-air bath.","furigana":"ろてんぶろ|ゆきみ"},{"japanese":"雪を見ながら飲むお酒を「雪見酒」と言います。","romaji":"Yuki o minagara nomu osake o \"yukimizake\" to iimasu.","german":"Sake drunk while looking at the snow is called 「雪見酒」.","furigana":"ゆき|み|の|さけ|ゆきみざけ|い"},{"japanese":"雪見障子のある和室から、庭の雪景色を楽しみました。","romaji":"Yukimi shouji no aru washitsu kara, niwa no yukigeshiki o tanoshimimashita.","german":"From a Japanese-style room with a yukimi shoji, I enjoyed the snowy scenery of the garden.","furigana":"ゆきみしょうじ|わしつ|にわ||たの"}],"notes":"雪見 is the enjoyable viewing of snow-covered landscapes, one of the classic seasonal pleasures alongside 花見 and 月見 (together 雪月花). One says 雪見をする and forms compounds like 雪見酒 \"sake while viewing snow\", 雪見風呂 or 雪見障子 (shoji with a glass window in the lower part). The word sounds traditional and poetic; in everyday speech one more often says 雪景色を楽しむ.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"潮干狩りに行きましょう。","romaji":"Shiohigari ni ikimashō.","german":"Let's go shellfish gathering.","furigana":"しおひが|い"},{"japanese":"潮干狩りでアサリをたくさん取りました。","romaji":"Shiohigari de asari o takusan torimashita.","german":"We gathered lots of clams while shellfish gathering.","furigana":"しおひが|と"},{"japanese":"潮干狩りのシーズンは春から初夏までです。","romaji":"Shiohigari no shiizun wa haru kara shoka made desu.","german":"The shellfish-gathering season runs from spring to early summer.","furigana":"しおひが|はる|しょか"}],"notes":"潮干狩り is gathering shellfish at low tide: when the water recedes (潮が引く), people dig in the tidal flats for shellfish such as アサリ. The parts: 潮 \"tide\" + 干る \"dry up\" + 狩り \"hunt\" - as in いちご狩り or 紅葉狩り, 狩り stands for an outing pastime. Typical: 潮干狩りに行く／をする. The season is spring to early summer.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子供と虫取りをします。","romaji":"Kodomo to mushitori o shimasu.","german":"I catch insects with my children.","furigana":"こども|むしと"},{"japanese":"夏休みに虫取り網を持って公園へ行きました。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi ni mushitori ami o motte kouen e ikimashita.","german":"In the summer vacation, I went to the park with a butterfly net.","furigana":"なつやす|むしと|あみ|も|こうえん|い"},{"japanese":"子供のころは虫取りに夢中で、毎日森に行っていました。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro wa mushitori ni muchuu de, mainichi mori ni itte imashita.","german":"As a child I was crazy about catching insects and went to the woods every day.","furigana":"こども|むしと|むちゅう|まいにちもり|い"}],"notes":"虫取り is catching insects such as beetles, cicadas or dragonflies - a classic summer pastime of Japanese children. The equipment is a 虫取り網 \"insect net\" and a 虫かご \"insect cage\". One says 虫取りをする or 虫取りに行く; the spelling 虫捕り also occurs. In Japanese, 虫 also includes animals that are not insects, such as spiders.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"春に山菜採りに行きます。","romaji":"Haru ni sansai tori ni ikimasu.","german":"In spring I go foraging for wild vegetables.","furigana":"はる|さんさいと|い"},{"japanese":"山菜採りで採ったワラビを天ぷらにしました。","romaji":"Sansai tori de totta warabi o tenpura ni shimashita.","german":"I made the bracken I had gathered into tempura.","furigana":"さんさいと|と|てん"},{"japanese":"毎年、山菜採りで山に入った人が道に迷う事故があります。","romaji":"Maitoshi, sansai tori de yama ni haitta hito ga michi ni mayou jiko ga arimasu.","german":"Every year there are accidents in which people who go into the mountains to gather wild plants get lost.","furigana":"まいとし|さんさいと|やま|はい|ひと|みち|まよ|じこ"}],"notes":"山菜 are edible wild plants from the mountains, such as fern shoots (ワラビ, ゼンマイ), butterbur buds (ふきのとう) or angelica-tree buds (タラの芽). 山菜採り is the gathering of these plants in spring. For picking plants one writes 採る instead of 取る. In the news one reads every year about gatherers who get lost or encounter bears.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"釣り堀で釣りをしました。","romaji":"Tsuribori de tsuri o shimashita.","german":"I went fishing at a fishing pond.","furigana":"つ|ぼり|つ"},{"japanese":"釣り堀なら、初心者でも魚が釣れます。","romaji":"Tsuribori nara, shoshinsha demo sakana ga tsuremasu.","german":"At a fishing pond, even beginners can catch fish.","furigana":"つ|ぼり|しょしんしゃ|さかな|つ"},{"japanese":"釣り堀で釣った魚をその場で焼いてもらいました。","romaji":"Tsuribori de tsutta sakana o sono ba de yaite moraimashita.","german":"I had the fish I caught at the fishing pond grilled on the spot.","furigana":"つ|ぼり|つ|さかな|ば|や"}],"notes":"釣り堀 is a commercial fishing pond or tank stocked with fish, usually paid for by the hour (堀 = \"ditch, pool\"). Even beginners and children are sure to catch something there, and some facilities grill the catch right away. A fishing spot in the wild, by contrast, is called 釣り場. Typical: 釣り堀で釣る, 釣り堀に行く.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"肝試しをしました。","romaji":"Kimodameshi o shimashita.","german":"We did a test of courage.","furigana":"きもだめ"},{"japanese":"夏の夜に、子供たちはお寺のお墓で肝試しをします。","romaji":"Natsu no yoru ni, kodomotachi wa otera no ohaka de kimodameshi o shimasu.","german":"On summer nights, the children hold a spooky test of courage in the temple graveyard.","furigana":"なつ|よる|こども|てら|はか|きもだめ"},{"japanese":"肝試しで一人で暗い道を歩いて、本当に怖かったです。","romaji":"Kimodameshi de hitori de kurai michi o aruite, hontou ni kowakatta desu.","german":"In the test of courage I had to walk a dark path alone, and it was really scary.","furigana":"きもだめ|ひとり|くら|みち|ある|ほんとう|こわ"}],"notes":"肝試し is a nighttime test of courage in an eerie place such as a graveyard or an abandoned building - a typical summer pastime, often combined with ghost stories (怪談). 肝 \"liver\" is regarded as the seat of courage (cf. 肝が据わる \"to be fearless\"), and 試す means \"to test\". One says 肝試しをする or 肝試しに行く. For a test of courage without a spooky element, 度胸試し fits better.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日は私のおごりです。","romaji":"Kyō wa watashi no ogori desu.","german":"Today it's my treat.","furigana":"きょう|わたし"},{"japanese":"先輩のおごりで焼肉を食べました。","romaji":"Senpai no ogori de yakiniku o tabemashita.","german":"A senior colleague treated me to yakiniku.","furigana":"せんぱい|やきにく|た"},{"japanese":"いつもおごってもらって悪いから、今日は割り勘にしよう。","romaji":"Itsumo ogotte moratte warui kara, kyou wa warikan ni shiyou.","german":"You always treat me and I feel bad, so let's split the bill today.","furigana":"わる|きょう|わ|かん"}],"notes":"おごり is a treat in which someone pays for others; the related verb is おごる (〜に〜をおごる \"to treat someone to something\"). Typical: 今日は私のおごり, 先輩のおごりで. The word is colloquial and suits friends or people younger than you; more polite are ごちそうする and ごちそうになる. It is mostly written in kana (kanji 奢り); do not confuse with 驕り \"arrogance, haughtiness\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"手土産を持って行きます。","romaji":"Temiyage o motte ikimasu.","german":"I'll bring a small gift.","furigana":"てみやげ|も|い"},{"japanese":"友達の家に呼ばれたので、手土産にケーキを買いました。","romaji":"Tomodachi no ie ni yobareta node, temiyage ni keeki o kaimashita.","german":"I was invited to a friend's house, so I bought a cake as a gift to bring.","furigana":"ともだち|いえ|よ|てみやげ|か"},{"japanese":"取引先への手土産は、何がいいでしょうか。","romaji":"Torihikisaki e no temiyage wa, nani ga ii deshou ka.","german":"What would be good as a gift to bring to a business partner?","furigana":"とりひきさき|てみやげ|なに"}],"notes":"手土産 is a small gift that one brings in person when visiting (手 \"hand\"), e.g. sweets or cake for hosts or business partners. お土産, by contrast, mainly means a souvenir from a trip. Typical: 手土産を持って行く, 手土産に〜を買う; more formally 手土産を持参する. When handing it over, one traditionally says modestly つまらないものですが.","levelNote":"Compound: 手 (N5) + 土産 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お歳暮を贈ります。","romaji":"Oseibo o okurimasu.","german":"I send a year-end gift.","furigana":"せいぼ|おく"},{"japanese":"デパートのお歳暮コーナーはいつも混んでいます。","romaji":"Depaato no oseibo koonaa wa itsumo konde imasu.","german":"The year-end gift section at the department store is always crowded.","furigana":"せいぼ|こ"},{"japanese":"叔父からお歳暮にハムが届きました。","romaji":"Oji kara oseibo ni hamu ga todokimashita.","german":"A ham arrived from my uncle as a year-end gift.","furigana":"おじ|せいぼ|とど"}],"notes":"お歳暮 is the gift given in December to people to whom one felt indebted during the year - superiors, teachers, relatives, business partners. The summer counterpart is お中元. One says お歳暮を贈る (formally \"to give\") or お歳暮を送る (\"to send\", when shipped). Department stores have their own sections for it. 歳暮 without お also means \"year-end\" in writing.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お中元を頂きました。","romaji":"Ochūgen o itadakimashita.","german":"I received a midsummer gift.","furigana":"ちゅうげん|いただ"},{"japanese":"取引先にお中元を贈りました。","romaji":"Torihikisaki ni ochuugen o okurimashita.","german":"We sent midsummer gifts to our business partners.","furigana":"とりひきさき|ちゅうげん|おく"},{"japanese":"デパートでお中元にビールのセットを選びました。","romaji":"Depaato de ochuugen ni biiru no setto o erabimashita.","german":"At the department store I picked out a beer set as a midsummer gift.","furigana":"ちゅうげん|えら"}],"notes":"お中元 is the thank-you gift sent in summer (in the Tokyo area early to mid-July, in western Japan often until mid-August) to superiors, business partners, teachers or relatives; the winter counterpart is お歳暮. It is almost always said with the polite お: お中元を贈る, お中元を頂く, お中元のお礼を言う. Without お, 中元 appears mainly in business language such as 中元セール.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"結婚式の祝儀を準備します。","romaji":"Kekkonshiki no shūgi o junbi shimasu.","german":"I prepare the money gift for the wedding.","furigana":"けっこんしき|しゅうぎ|じゅんび"},{"japanese":"受付でご祝儀を渡しました。","romaji":"Uketsuke de goshuugi o watashimashita.","german":"I handed over the congratulatory money at the reception desk.","furigana":"うけつけ|しゅうぎ|わた"},{"japanese":"ご祝儀には新しいお札を入れるのがマナーです。","romaji":"Goshuugi ni wa atarashii osatsu o ireru no ga manaa desu.","german":"Etiquette says to put new bills in the congratulatory money.","furigana":"しゅうぎ|あたら|さつ|い"}],"notes":"祝儀 refers above all to a gift of money for a joyful occasion, especially a wedding; in daily life one almost always says ご祝儀. The money is put in a ご祝儀袋, and new bills are used if possible. In addition, ご祝儀 can also be a tip or a reward for artists and craftsmen. The counterpart for sad occasions is 不祝儀 or 香典.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不祝儀袋を買いました。","romaji":"Bushūgi bukuro o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a condolence envelope.","furigana":"ぶしゅうぎぶくろ|か"},{"japanese":"不祝儀の場では、派手な服は避けましょう。","romaji":"Bushuugi no ba de wa, hade na fuku wa sakemashou.","german":"At sad occasions, let's avoid flashy clothes.","furigana":"ぶしゅうぎ|ば|はで|ふく|さ"},{"japanese":"祝儀と不祝儀では、お金の包み方が違います。","romaji":"Shuugi to bushuugi de wa, okane no tsutsumikata ga chigaimasu.","german":"The way of wrapping money differs between congratulatory and condolence occasions.","furigana":"しゅうぎ|ぶしゅうぎ|かね|つつ|かた|ちが"}],"notes":"不祝儀 is literally a \"non-celebration\", i.e. a sad occasion, above all a funeral or memorial service - the opposite of 祝儀. The word is most often encountered in 不祝儀袋, the black-and-white envelope in which condolence money (香典) is handed over. It is a somewhat formal word from the area of etiquette; in conversation one more often says お葬式 or お通夜.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"香典を持って行きます。","romaji":"Kōden o motte ikimasu.","german":"I'll bring condolence money.","furigana":"こうでん|も|い"},{"japanese":"お通夜で香典を受付に渡しました。","romaji":"Otsuya de kouden o uketsuke ni watashimashita.","german":"At the wake, I handed the condolence money to the reception desk.","furigana":"つや|こうでん|うけつけ|わた"},{"japanese":"香典返しにお茶が届きました。","romaji":"Kouden-gaeshi ni ocha ga todokimashita.","german":"A return gift of tea arrived in thanks for the condolence money.","furigana":"こうでんがえ|ちゃ|とど"}],"notes":"香典 is the money one brings to a wake (お通夜) or funeral; literally it was originally an offering for incense (香). It is put into a condolence envelope, and one says 香典を包む, 香典を渡す or 香典を供える; new banknotes are avoided here. The family later thanks the giver with a return gift, the 香典返し.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"数珠を持って参列します。","romaji":"Juzu o motte sanretsu shimasu.","german":"I attend the ceremony with prayer beads.","furigana":"じゅず|も|さんれつ"},{"japanese":"祖母はいつも数珠を手にお経を読んでいます。","romaji":"Sobo wa itsumo juzu o te ni okyou o yonde imasu.","german":"My grandmother always reads the sutras with prayer beads in her hand.","furigana":"そぼ|じゅず|て|きょう|よ"},{"japanese":"連休で車が数珠つなぎになっています。","romaji":"Renkyuu de kuruma ga juzu-tsunagi ni natte imasu.","german":"Because of the holidays, the cars are lined up bumper to bumper.","furigana":"れんきゅう|くるま|じゅず"}],"notes":"数珠 are the Buddhist prayer beads that one holds in the hand at funerals, memorial services and grave visits, or drapes over the folded hands when praying: 数珠を持つ, 数珠を手にかける. The reading じゅず is irregular and must be learned. Also very common is 数珠つなぎ \"strung together like beads\", for example for a long line of cars.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"戒名を頂きました。","romaji":"Kaimyō o itadakimashita.","german":"I received a posthumous Buddhist name.","furigana":"かいみょう|いただ"},{"japanese":"お坊さんに父の戒名をつけていただきました。","romaji":"Obousan ni chichi no kaimyou o tsukete itadakimashita.","german":"I had a monk give my father a posthumous Buddhist name.","furigana":"ぼう|ちち|かいみょう"},{"japanese":"お墓には祖父の戒名が刻まれています。","romaji":"Ohaka ni wa sofu no kaimyou ga kizamarete imasu.","german":"My grandfather's posthumous Buddhist name is carved on the grave.","furigana":"はか|そふ|かいみょう|きざ"}],"notes":"戒名 is the Buddhist name that a monk gives to a deceased person; it is written on the memorial tablet (位牌) and on the gravestone. One says 戒名をつける or, politely, 戒名をつけていただく, and the family 戒名をもらう. Originally it was the name received on taking the Buddhist precepts (戒); in the Jodo Shinshu school it is called 法名.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"盂蘭盆に先祖を祀ります。","romaji":"Urabon ni senzo o matsurimasu.","german":"I honor my ancestors at Urabon.","furigana":"うらぼん|せんぞ|まつ"},{"japanese":"お寺で盂蘭盆の行事が行われます。","romaji":"Otera de urabon no gyouji ga okonawaremasu.","german":"Urabon events are held at the temple.","furigana":"てら|うらぼん|ぎょうじ|おこな"},{"japanese":"盂蘭盆は、普通は「お盆」と呼ばれています。","romaji":"Urabon wa, futsuu wa obon to yobarete imasu.","german":"Urabon is usually called 「お盆」.","furigana":"うらぼん|ふつう|ぼん|よ"}],"notes":"盂蘭盆 is the full, Buddhist name of the festival of the dead that is simply called お盆 in everyday life; the word comes from Sanskrit (ullambana). It is encountered mainly in temple and written contexts, for example in 盂蘭盆会, the Obon memorial service. At Obon (mid-July or mid-August, depending on the region), the spirits of the ancestors are welcomed and sent off again; the difficult kanji are often also written as うら盆.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お盆に墓参りをします。","romaji":"Obon ni hakamairi o shimasu.","german":"I visit the graves at Obon.","furigana":"ぼん|はかまい"},{"japanese":"祖父の命日に家族で墓参りに行きました。","romaji":"Sofu no meinichi ni kazoku de hakamairi ni ikimashita.","german":"On my grandfather's death anniversary, our family went to visit his grave.","furigana":"そふ|めいにち|かぞく|はかまい|い"},{"japanese":"墓参りの前に、花と線香を買いました。","romaji":"Hakamairi no mae ni, hana to senkou o kaimashita.","german":"Before the grave visit, I bought flowers and incense sticks.","furigana":"はかまい|まえ|はな|せんこう|か"}],"notes":"墓参り is a visit to the family grave; politely and very commonly also お墓参り. One says 墓参りに行く or, as a する-verb, 墓参りする. Typical occasions are お盆, お彼岸, New Year and the death anniversary (命日); one cleans the grave, pours water over it, places flowers and lights incense sticks (線香をあげる).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"父の還暦を祝いました。","romaji":"Chichi no kanreki o iwaimashita.","german":"We celebrated my father's 60th birthday.","furigana":"ちち|かんれき|いわ"},{"japanese":"還暦のお祝いに、赤いちゃんちゃんこを贈るのが伝統です。","romaji":"Kanreki no oiwai ni, akai chanchanko o okuru no ga dentou desu.","german":"It is traditional to give a red vest as a gift for the 60th birthday.","furigana":"かんれき|いわ|あか|おく|でんとう"},{"japanese":"母は還暦を迎えても、毎日元気に働いています。","romaji":"Haha wa kanreki o mukaete mo, mainichi genki ni hataraite imasu.","german":"Even though my mother has turned sixty, she works energetically every day.","furigana":"はは|かんれき|むか|まいにちげんき|はたら"}],"notes":"還暦 is the 60th birthday: after 60 years, the cycle of combinations of the zodiac and celestial stems (干支) is complete and one \"returns to the starting calendar\". One says 還暦を迎える and 還暦を祝う; traditionally one gives something red, such as a red ちゃんちゃんこ, as a sign of a new beginning. Further milestones are 古希 (70), 喜寿 (77) and 米寿 (88).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今年は厄年です。","romaji":"Kotoshi wa yakudoshi desu.","german":"This year is an unlucky year for me.","furigana":"ことし|やくどし"},{"japanese":"厄年なので、神社で厄払いをしてもらいました。","romaji":"Yakudoshi na node, jinja de yakubarai o shite moraimashita.","german":"Because it's an unlucky year, I had a purification ritual done at the shrine.","furigana":"やくどし|じんじゃ|やくばら"},{"japanese":"事故に病気に、今年はまるで厄年のようだった。","romaji":"Jiko ni byouki ni, kotoshi wa marude yakudoshi no you datta.","german":"Between the accident and the illness, this year was like a real unlucky year.","furigana":"じこ|びょうき|ことし|やくどし"}],"notes":"厄年 are the years of life in which, according to old folk belief, one is especially prone to misfortune - by the traditional count (数え年), mainly 25 and 42 for men and 19 and 33 for women; the worst one is called 大厄, the year before 前厄, and the year after 後厄. Many people then have themselves purified at a shrine (厄払い, 厄除け). Loosely, 厄年 is also used for a year in which everything goes wrong.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"旧暦で祝います。","romaji":"Kyūreki de iwaimasu.","german":"We celebrate according to the old calendar.","furigana":"きゅうれき|いわ"},{"japanese":"暦の上ではもう春ですが、まだ寒い日が続いています。","romaji":"Koyomi no ue de wa mou haru desu ga, mada samui hi ga tsuzuite imasu.","german":"According to the calendar it is already spring, but cold days continue.","furigana":"こよみ|うえ|はる|さむ|ひ|つづ"},{"japanese":"昔の人は暦を見て、田植えの時期を決めました。","romaji":"Mukashi no hito wa koyomi o mite, taue no jiki o kimemashita.","german":"In the old days, people looked at the calendar to decide when to plant rice.","furigana":"むかし|ひと|こよみ|み|たう|じき|き"}],"notes":"暦 denotes the calendar as a system of timekeeping or almanac, for example with moon phases, lucky days and seasonal divisions; the everyday wall calendar is called カレンダー. Very typical is the phrase 暦の上では \"according to the calendar\", for example when it is already spring by the calendar but still cold. In compounds, 暦 is read れき: 旧暦, 西暦, 還暦.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"昔の人は、二十四節気に基づいて農作業をしていました。","romaji":"Mukashi no hito wa, nijūshi sekki ni motozuite nōsagyō o shite imashita.","german":"People in the past did their farm work according to the 24 solar terms.","furigana":"むかし|ひと|にじゅうしせっき|もと|のうさぎょう"},{"japanese":"立春や夏至は、二十四節気の一つです。","romaji":"Risshun ya geshi wa, nijuushi sekki no hitotsu desu.","german":"Risshun and the summer solstice are among the 24 solar terms.","furigana":"りっしゅん|げし|にじゅうしせっき|ひと"},{"japanese":"カレンダーに二十四節気が書いてあると、季節の変化を感じられます。","romaji":"Karendaa ni nijuushi sekki ga kaite aru to, kisetsu no henka o kanjiraremasu.","german":"If the 24 solar terms are written on the calendar, you can feel the change of seasons.","furigana":"にじゅうしせっき|か|きせつ|へんか|かん"}],"notes":"二十四節気 divide the solar year into 24 sections of about 15 days each according to the position of the sun. Well-known names are 立春 (\"start of spring\"), 春分, 夏至, 秋分, 冬至 and 大寒. They originally served agriculture; today one encounters them in weather reports, calendars and seasonal greeting formulas in letters. A single section is called 節気.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"土用の丑の日に鰻を食べます。","romaji":"Doyō no ushi no hi ni unagi o tabemasu.","german":"On the Day of the Ox in the doyo period, we eat eel.","furigana":"どよう|うし|ひ||た"},{"japanese":"土用に入ると、暑さが一段と厳しくなります。","romaji":"Doyou ni hairu to, atsusa ga ichidan to kibishiku narimasu.","german":"When doyo begins, the heat gets even harsher.","furigana":"どよう|はい|あつ|いちだん|きび"},{"japanese":"母は毎年、夏の土用に着物を干します。","romaji":"Haha wa maitoshi, natsu no doyou ni kimono o hoshimasu.","german":"Every year my mother airs out her kimono in the summer doyo.","furigana":"はは|まいとし|なつ|どよう|きもの|ほ"}],"notes":"土用 are, by the old calendar, the roughly 18 days before the start of each season; today it almost always means the summer 土用 from about late July to early August, the hottest days of the year. Well known is 土用の丑の日, on which eel (うなぎ) is traditionally eaten. Other compounds are 土用干し (airing out clothes and books in high summer) and 土用波 (high waves in late summer).","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お彼岸にお墓参りをします。","romaji":"Ohigan ni ohakamairi o shimasu.","german":"I visit graves during the equinox week.","furigana":"ひがん|はかまい"},{"japanese":"「暑さ寒さも彼岸まで」と言いますね。","romaji":"Atsusa samusa mo higan made to iimasu ne.","german":"They do say: the heat and the cold last only until the equinox.","furigana":"あつ|さむ|ひがん|い"},{"japanese":"秋のお彼岸に、祖母とおはぎを作りました。","romaji":"Aki no ohigan ni, sobo to ohagi o tsukurimashita.","german":"During the autumn Higan week, I made ohagi with my grandmother.","furigana":"あき|ひがん|そぼ|つく"}],"notes":"彼岸 in daily life means the week around the equinox in spring and autumn, when one visits graves and offers sweets such as おはぎ; one mostly says お彼岸. Literally it means \"the other shore\", in Buddhism the nirvana beyond this world. Well known is the proverb 暑さ寒さも彼岸まで: heat and cold last only until the equinox.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"結婚式は大安の日にします。","romaji":"Kekkonshiki wa taian no hi ni shimasu.","german":"We'll hold the wedding on a taian day.","furigana":"けっこんしき|たいあん|ひ"},{"japanese":"大安の土曜日は、式場の予約がすぐ埋まります。","romaji":"Taian no doyoubi wa, shikijou no yoyaku ga sugu umarimasu.","german":"On a taian Saturday, wedding venues get booked up quickly.","furigana":"たいあん|どようび|しきじょう|よやく|う"},{"japanese":"店のオープンは大安を選びました。","romaji":"Mise no oopun wa taian o erabimashita.","german":"I chose a taian day for the shop opening.","furigana":"みせ|たいあん|えら"}],"notes":"大安 is the luckiest of the six day types of the traditional calendar (六曜), which also include 仏滅 and 友引. On 大安 days people like to marry, open businesses or pick up a new car; the set phrase 大安吉日 stresses a particularly favorable day. One says 大安の日 or simply 大安に.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仏滅の日は避けます。","romaji":"Butsumetsu no hi wa sakemasu.","german":"I avoid butsumetsu days.","furigana":"ぶつめつ|ひ|さ"},{"japanese":"仏滅だと結婚式場が安くなるそうです。","romaji":"Butsumetsu da to kekkonshikijou ga yasuku naru sou desu.","german":"Apparently wedding halls get cheaper on butsumetsu.","furigana":"ぶつめつ|けっこんしきじょう|やす"},{"japanese":"祖母は仏滅に引っ越すのを嫌がりました。","romaji":"Sobo wa butsumetsu ni hikkosu no o iyagarimashita.","german":"My grandmother disliked moving on a butsumetsu day.","furigana":"そぼ|ぶつめつ|ひ|こ|いや"}],"notes":"仏滅 is the least favorable day in the six-part calendar cycle 六曜 and the opposite of 大安. People avoid it for weddings, moves or openings, which is why wedding halls often give discounts on 仏滅. Literally it means \"Buddha's death\", but the calendar custom actually has nothing to do with Buddhism.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"吉日に引っ越します。","romaji":"Kichijitsu ni hikkoshimasu.","german":"I'll move on an auspicious day.","furigana":"きちじつ|ひ|こ"},{"japanese":"思い立ったが吉日、今日から運動を始めよう。","romaji":"Omoitatta ga kichijitsu, kyou kara undou o hajimeyou.","german":"\"The day you make up your mind is the lucky day\" - let's start exercising from today.","furigana":"おも|た|きちじつ|きょう|うんどう|はじ"},{"japanese":"招待状の日付は「十月吉日」と書きます。","romaji":"Shoutaijou no hizuke wa juugatsu kichijitsu to kakimasu.","german":"On invitations, the date is written as 「十月吉日」.","furigana":"しょうたいじょう|ひづけ|じゅうがつきちじつ|か"}],"notes":"吉日 (also read きちにち) is a day that promises good luck, often strengthened to 大安吉日. Very well known is the proverb 思い立ったが吉日: the day you decide to do something is the right day to start. In letters and invitations, \"〇月吉日\" is often written instead of an exact date, to leave the day of writing open.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日柄のいい日を選びます。","romaji":"Higara no ii hi o erabimasu.","german":"I'll choose a day with good auspiciousness.","furigana":"ひがら|ひ|えら"},{"japanese":"本日はお日柄もよく、誠におめでとうございます。","romaji":"Honjitsu wa ohigara mo yoku, makoto ni omedetou gozaimasu.","german":"It is a beautiful, auspicious day today, and congratulations are truly in order.","furigana":"ほんじつ|ひがら|まこと"},{"japanese":"父は日柄を気にして、仏滅に契約するのを嫌がります。","romaji":"Chichi wa higara o ki ni shite, butsumetsu ni keiyaku suru no o iyagarimasu.","german":"My father cares about the auspiciousness of days and dislikes signing contracts on butsumetsu.","furigana":"ちち|ひがら|き|ぶつめつ|けいやく|いや"}],"notes":"日柄 is the quality of luck or misfortune of a day as determined by the traditional calendar, i.e. whether it is, for example, a 大安 or a 仏滅. One says 日柄がいい/悪い or, politely, お日柄; especially well known is the set phrase 本日はお日柄もよく at the start of wedding speeches. Do not confuse it with 日付 (date).","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人生の節目です。","romaji":"Jinsei no fushime desu.","german":"It is a turning point in life.","furigana":"じんせい|ふしめ"},{"japanese":"創立五十周年という節目を迎えました。","romaji":"Souritsu gojusshuunen to iu fushime o mukaemashita.","german":"We have reached the milestone of our 50th anniversary.","furigana":"そうりつごじゅっしゅうねん|ふしめ|むか"},{"japanese":"年度の節目に、目標を見直しましょう。","romaji":"Nendo no fushime ni, mokuhyou o minaoshimashou.","german":"Let's review our goals at the turn of the fiscal year.","furigana":"ねんど|ふしめ|もくひょう|みなお"}],"notes":"節目 is originally the knot in bamboo or wood; figuratively it is a break or milestone at which one stage ends and a new one begins. Typical are 人生の節目, 節目の年 and 〜周年という節目を迎える. Unlike 転機 (a turning point that changes something), 節目 emphasizes the marked section in a sequence, such as anniversaries, graduations or year changes.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人生の岐路に立っています。","romaji":"Jinsei no kiro ni tatteimasu.","german":"I'm standing at a crossroads in life.","furigana":"じんせい|きろ|た"},{"japanese":"会社は今、大きな岐路に立たされている。","romaji":"Kaisha wa ima, ookina kiro ni tatasarete iru.","german":"The company is now facing a major crossroads.","furigana":"かいしゃ|いま|おお|きろ|た"},{"japanese":"進学か就職か、その岐路で彼は迷った。","romaji":"Shingaku ka shuushoku ka, sono kiro de kare wa mayotta.","german":"He wavered at that crossroads between further education and employment.","furigana":"しんがく|しゅうしょく|きろ|かれ|まよ"}],"notes":"岐路 is literally a fork in the road, but it is used almost only figuratively: 岐路に立つ \"to stand at a crossroads\", 人生の岐路, 重大な岐路. The word sounds literary and elevated; in conversation one more often says 分かれ道. Unlike 瀬戸際 (on the verge of success or ruin), 岐路 is about the choice between different directions.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"病気は峠を越えました。","romaji":"Byōki wa tōge o koemashita.","german":"The illness has passed its peak.","furigana":"びょうき|とうげ|こ"},{"japanese":"暑さもようやく峠を越えたようです。","romaji":"Atsusa mo youyaku touge o koeta you desu.","german":"The worst of the heat seems to be over at last.","furigana":"あつ|とうげ|こ"},{"japanese":"忙しい時期はもう峠を越えたので、来週は休めそうです。","romaji":"Isogashii jiki wa mou touge o koeta node, raishuu wa yasumesou desu.","german":"The busy period is over the hump, so I should be able to take next week off.","furigana":"いそが|じき|とうげ|こ|らいしゅう|やす"}],"notes":"峠 is a mountain pass; 峠を越える literally means \"to cross the pass\", figuratively \"to have the worst behind one, to get past the peak\". It is typical for illnesses (病気が峠を越える), heat, work peaks or crises; 峠を越す means the same. With 峠 alone one also says 今が峠だ \"now is the most critical point\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"技術革新に拍車をかけます。","romaji":"Gijutsu kakushin ni hakusha o kakemasu.","german":"We will accelerate technological innovation.","furigana":"ぎじゅつかくしん|はくしゃ"},{"japanese":"円安が物価の上昇に拍車をかけています。","romaji":"En'yasu ga bukka no joushou ni hakusha o kakete imasu.","german":"The weak yen is further fueling the rise in prices.","furigana":"えんやす|ぶっか|じょうしょう|はくしゃ"},{"japanese":"新しいコーチが来て、チームの練習に拍車がかかった。","romaji":"Atarashii koochi ga kite, chiimu no renshuu ni hakusha ga kakatta.","german":"With the new coach, the team's training has really picked up speed.","furigana":"あたら|き|れんしゅう|はくしゃ"}],"notes":"拍車 is the rider's spur; X に拍車をかける means \"to spur X on, to accelerate or intensify X\". It is often about developments that are already moving anyway, frequently negative ones: 少子化に拍車をかける, 物価高に拍車をかける. The intransitive form is 拍車がかかる \"to gain momentum\". The idiom is typical of news and written language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"事業が軌道に乗りました。","romaji":"Jigyō ga kidō ni norimashita.","german":"The business has gotten on track.","furigana":"じぎょう|きどう|の"},{"japanese":"新しい生活もやっと軌道に乗ってきました。","romaji":"Atarashii seikatsu mo yatto kidou ni notte kimashita.","german":"My new life has finally settled into a routine too.","furigana":"あたら|せいかつ|きどう|の"},{"japanese":"まずは新しいプロジェクトを軌道に乗せることが大事です。","romaji":"Mazu wa atarashii purojekuto o kidou ni noseru koto ga daiji desu.","german":"First of all, it is important to get the new project on track.","furigana":"あたら|きどう|の|だいじ"}],"notes":"軌道 is an orbit or a track; 軌道に乗る means \"to get onto the track\", figuratively \"to get going, to run as planned\". Typical subjects with が are 事業, 仕事, 計画 or 新しい生活. The transitive counterpart is 軌道に乗せる \"to bring something on track\"; literally, the expression can also refer to a satellite reaching orbit.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"資金の算段をつけます。","romaji":"Shikin no sandan o tsukemasu.","german":"I'll arrange the funds.","furigana":"しきん|さんだん"},{"japanese":"家賃をどうやって払うか、算段しなければならない。","romaji":"Yachin o dou yatte harau ka, sandan shinakereba naranai.","german":"I have to figure out how to pay the rent.","furigana":"やちん|はら|さんだん"},{"japanese":"彼はうまく休みを取る算段をしている。","romaji":"Kare wa umaku yasumi o toru sandan o shite iru.","german":"He is working out a way to get time off cleverly.","furigana":"かれ|やす|と|さんだん"}],"notes":"算段 means thinking up ways and means to manage something, especially to raise money. Typical are 金の算段, 算段をつける \"to find a way\" and, as a する-verb, 〜か算段する \"to figure out how to ...\". The word sounds a bit old-fashioned; close synonyms are 工面 (raising money) and 段取り (planning the sequence of steps), while 計画 is \"plan\" in general.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"完成の目処が立ちました。","romaji":"Kansei no medo ga tachimashita.","german":"The prospect of completion has been established.","furigana":"かんせい|めど|た"},{"japanese":"来月末をめどに報告書をまとめます。","romaji":"Raigetsumatsu o medo ni houkokusho o matomemasu.","german":"We aim to put the report together by around the end of next month.","furigana":"らいげつまつ|ほうこくしょ"},{"japanese":"電車の運転再開のめどは立っていません。","romaji":"Densha no unten saikai no medo wa tatte imasen.","german":"There is no prospect yet of when train service will resume.","furigana":"でんしゃ|うんてんさいかい|た"}],"notes":"目処 is mostly written in kana めど (more rarely 目途) and denotes a foreseeable prospect or goal. The main patterns are めどが立つ/つく \"to become foreseeable\" (intransitive), めどを立てる/つける (transitive) and 〜をめどに \"aiming at about ...\", e.g. 月末をめどに. Unlike 見通し (forecast, outlook), めど emphasizes the tangible reference point as to when or how something will succeed.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"口実を見つけました。","romaji":"Kōjitsu o mitsukemashita.","german":"I found a pretext.","furigana":"こうじつ|み"},{"japanese":"彼は体調不良を口実に、飲み会を断った。","romaji":"Kare wa taichou furyou o koujitsu ni, nomikai o kotowatta.","german":"He declined the drinking party on the pretext of feeling unwell.","furigana":"かれ|たいちょうふりょう|こうじつ|の|かい|ことわ"},{"japanese":"彼女に会う口実を作るために、本を借りた。","romaji":"Kanojo ni au koujitsu o tsukuru tame ni, hon o karita.","german":"I borrowed a book in order to create an excuse to meet her.","furigana":"かのじょ|あ|こうじつ|つく|ほん|か"}],"notes":"口実 is a pretext that one puts forward in order to do or avoid something. Typical patterns are 〜を口実に(して) \"under the pretext of ...\" and 口実を作る/見つける. Unlike 言い訳 (an excuse, a justification after the fact), 口実 is usually used in advance as a reason that is not the real one.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"裏付けのある情報です。","romaji":"Urazuke no aru jouhou desu.","german":"It is corroborated information.","furigana":"うらづ|じょうほう"},{"japanese":"記者は情報の裏付けを取ってから記事を書きます。","romaji":"Kisha wa jouhou no urazuke o totte kara kiji o kakimasu.","german":"Reporters write an article only after obtaining corroboration of the information.","furigana":"きしゃ|じょうほう|うらづ|と|きじ|か"},{"japanese":"彼の話を裏付ける証拠は見つからなかった。","romaji":"Kare no hanashi o urazukeru shouko wa mitsukaranakatta.","german":"No evidence was found to support his story.","furigana":"かれ|はなし|うらづ|しょうこ|み"}],"notes":"裏付け is evidence that supports a statement or assumption, literally something applied from behind (裏). Common are 裏付けがある/ない, 裏付けを取る \"to check and substantiate something\" (press, police) and the verb 裏付ける \"to substantiate\". 証拠 is the proof itself, 根拠 the grounds; 裏付け emphasizes confirmation through facts or data.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"解決の糸口が見つかりました。","romaji":"Kaiketsu no itoguchi ga mitsukarimashita.","german":"A clue to the solution has been found.","furigana":"かいけつ|いとぐち|み"},{"japanese":"天気の話を糸口に、会話が弾みました。","romaji":"Tenki no hanashi o itoguchi ni, kaiwa ga hazumimashita.","german":"Starting with talk about the weather, the conversation took off.","furigana":"てんき|はなし|いとぐち|かいわ|はず"},{"japanese":"警察は事件の糸口をつかもうとしている。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa jiken no itoguchi o tsukamou to shite iru.","german":"The police are trying to get a lead on the case.","furigana":"けいさつ|じけん|いとぐち"}],"notes":"糸口 is literally the end of a thread, where one begins to wind up a ball of yarn; figuratively a first starting point or hint. Typical are 解決の糸口, 事件の糸口 and 話の糸口 (a conversation opener), plus 糸口をつかむ/見つける/探る. 手がかり is more a concrete clue in a search, きっかけ a trigger; 糸口 stresses the first step with which a matter can be unraveled.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"交渉の突破口を開きます。","romaji":"Kōshō no toppakō o hirakimasu.","german":"I will open a breakthrough in the negotiations.","furigana":"こうしょう|とっぱこう|ひら"},{"japanese":"不況から抜け出す突破口が見えない。","romaji":"Fukyou kara nukedasu toppakou ga mienai.","german":"I can't see a breakthrough that gets us out of the recession.","furigana":"ふきょう|ぬ|だ|とっぱこう|み"},{"japanese":"新人の一言が、議論の突破口になった。","romaji":"Shinjin no hitokoto ga, giron no toppakou ni natta.","german":"A remark by the newcomer became the breakthrough in the discussion.","furigana":"しんじん|ひとこと|ぎろん|とっぱこう"}],"notes":"突破口 is the opening or breach through which a breakthrough succeeds; originally military, today mostly figurative for negotiations, research or crises. Typical are 突破口を開く, 突破口を見つける and 〜の突破口になる. 突破 on its own is the breaking through itself (e.g. 予選突破), whereas 糸口 is rather a cautious first lead.","levelNote":"Compound: 突破 (N1) + 口 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"会社は倒産の瀬戸際に立っています。","romaji":"Kaisha wa tousan no setogiwa ni tatte imasu.","german":"The company is on the brink of bankruptcy.","furigana":"かいしゃ|とうさん|せとぎわ|た"},{"japanese":"勝つか負けるかの瀬戸際で、彼は落ち着いていた。","romaji":"Katsu ka makeru ka no setogiwa de, kare wa ochitsuite ita.","german":"At the critical moment between winning and losing, he stayed calm.","furigana":"か|ま|せとぎわ|かれ|お|つ"}],"notes":"瀬戸際 is literally the edge of a strait (瀬戸); figuratively the critical moment in which success or ruin is decided. Typical are 倒産の瀬戸際, 〜か〜かの瀬戸際 (生きるか死ぬかの瀬戸際) and 瀬戸際に立つ/立たされる. While 岐路 means a choice between paths, at 瀬戸際 one stands just before a fall.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"板挟みの状態です。","romaji":"Itabasami no jōtai desu.","german":"I'm caught in the middle.","furigana":"いたばさ|じょうたい"},{"japanese":"中間管理職は、上司と部下の板挟みになりやすい。","romaji":"Chuukan kanrishoku wa, joushi to buka no itabasami ni nariyasui.","german":"Middle managers easily get caught between their bosses and their subordinates.","furigana":"ちゅうかんかんりしょく|じょうし|ぶか|いたばさ"},{"japanese":"母と妻の板挟みにあって、困っています。","romaji":"Haha to tsuma no itabasami ni atte, komatte imasu.","german":"I'm in trouble, caught between my mother and my wife.","furigana":"はは|つま|いたばさ|こま"}],"notes":"板挟み is literally \"pinched between boards\": one stands between two sides with conflicting demands and cannot satisfy either. Typical is AとBの板挟みになる or 板挟みにあう, for example between boss and staff or between mother and wife. For a pure decision dilemma without people involved, one more often says ジレンマ.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"社内で四面楚歌の状態だ。","romaji":"Shanai de shimen soka no jōtai da.","german":"I am completely isolated within the company.","furigana":"しゃない|しめんそか|じょうたい"},{"japanese":"不祥事が発覚し、大臣は四面楚歌に陥った。","romaji":"Fushōji ga hakkaku shi, daijin wa shimen soka ni ochiitta.","german":"After the scandal came to light, the minister found himself isolated on all sides.","furigana":"ふしょうじ|はっかく|だいじん|しめんそか|おちい"},{"japanese":"クラスで一人だけ反対して、四面楚歌になってしまった。","romaji":"Kurasu de hitori dake hantai shite, shimen soka ni natte shimatta.","german":"Because I was the only one in the class who opposed it, I found myself totally isolated.","furigana":"ひとり|はんたい|しめんそか"}],"notes":"四面楚歌 goes back to a Chinese story: the besieged general Xiang Yu heard songs of his homeland Chu (楚歌) from all sides (四面) and thought his own people had gone over to the enemy. Today it means to be attacked from all sides and to have no allies. Typical are 四面楚歌の状態, 四面楚歌に陥る and 四面楚歌になる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この分野は彼の独壇場です。","romaji":"Kono bunya wa kare no dokudanjō desu.","german":"This field is his domain.","furigana":"ぶんや|かれ|どくだんじょう"},{"japanese":"カラオケになると、もう部長の独壇場だった。","romaji":"Karaoke ni naru to, mou buchou no dokudanjou datta.","german":"When it came to karaoke, it was once again the department head's show.","furigana":"ぶちょう|どくだんじょう"},{"japanese":"後半は、エースの独壇場となった。","romaji":"Kouhan wa, eesu no dokudanjou to natta.","german":"In the second half, it became the ace's show.","furigana":"こうはん|どくだんじょう"}],"notes":"独壇場 is an area or situation in which someone shines without competition: 〜の独壇場だ, 〜の独壇場となる. The word was originally 独擅場 (どくせんじょう); through confusion of the character 擅 with 壇 (\"stage\"), the present form became established. It does not mean an economic monopoly (that would be 独占), but a stage that someone dominates alone.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"臨機応変に対応できる人が求められている。","romaji":"Rinki ōhen ni taiō dekiru hito ga motomerarete iru.","german":"People who can respond flexibly as the situation demands are in demand.","furigana":"りんきおうへん|たいおう|ひと|もと"},{"japanese":"予定通りにいかない時は、臨機応変に考えましょう。","romaji":"Yotei dōri ni ikanai toki wa, rinki ōhen ni kangaemashō.","german":"When things don't go as planned, let's think flexibly.","furigana":"よていどお|とき|りんきおうへん|かんが"},{"japanese":"マニュアルだけでなく、臨機応変な判断も必要です。","romaji":"Manyuaru dake de naku, rinki ōhen na handan mo hitsuyō desu.","german":"Not only the manual but also flexible judgment is necessary.","furigana":"りんきおうへん|はんだん|ひつよう"}],"notes":"Literally: 臨機 \"to meet the moment\" and 応変 \"to adapt to change\". It means acting flexibly and appropriately according to the situation instead of rigidly following a plan or rules. Typical are 臨機応変に対応する／考える and 臨機応変な判断. Common in job ads, in working life and in advice; neutral to slightly formal.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"紆余曲折を経て、ようやく夢を実現した。","romaji":"Uyo kyokusetsu o hete, yōyaku yume o jitsugen shita.","german":"After many twists and turns, he finally realized his dream.","furigana":"うよきょくせつ|へ|ゆめ|じつげん"},{"japanese":"この計画は紆余曲折があったが、成功した。","romaji":"Kono keikaku wa uyo kyokusetsu ga atta ga, seikō shita.","german":"This plan had many twists and turns, but succeeded in the end.","furigana":"けいかく|うよきょくせつ|せいこう"},{"japanese":"労使交渉は紆余曲折の末、ようやく合意に達した。","romaji":"Rōshi kōshō wa uyo kyokusetsu no sue, yōyaku gōi ni tasshita.","german":"The labor-management negotiations finally reached agreement after many twists and turns.","furigana":"ろうしこうしょう|うよきょくせつ|すえ|ごうい|たっ"}],"notes":"紆余 means \"to wind, meander\" and 曲折 \"to bend\": 紆余曲折 describes a path full of turns, complications and setbacks before one reaches the goal. Fixed frames are 紆余曲折を経て, 紆余曲折の末（に） and 紆余曲折がある. The expression usually looks back at a difficult but ultimately completed process; for mere ups and downs of fortune, 浮き沈み fits better.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どんな方法にも一長一短がある。","romaji":"Donna hōhō ni mo icchō ittan ga aru.","german":"Every method has its pros and cons.","furigana":"ほうほう|いっちょういったん"},{"japanese":"この製品は一長一短で、完璧ではない。","romaji":"Kono seihin wa icchō ittan de, kanpeki de wa nai.","german":"This product has strengths and weaknesses and is not perfect.","furigana":"せいひん|いっちょういったん|かんぺき"},{"japanese":"都会の生活と田舎の生活は一長一短だと思う。","romaji":"Tokai no seikatsu to inaka no seikatsu wa icchō ittan da to omou.","german":"I think life in the city and life in the country each have their pros and cons.","furigana":"とかい|せいかつ|いなか|せいかつ|いっちょういったん|おも"}],"notes":"Literally \"one long, one short\", that is, one strength and one weakness. It means that something has both advantages and disadvantages and no option is clearly superior. Typical are 一長一短がある and 〜は一長一短だ, often when comparing methods, products or ways of living. It is neutral; it is not used for people in the sense of \"strengths of character\", but for things and options.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一石二鳥の方法です。","romaji":"Isseki nichō no hōhō desu.","german":"Das ist eine Methode, die zwei Fliegen mit einer Klappe schlägt.","furigana":"いっせきにちょう|ほうほう"},{"japanese":"自転車通勤は、運動にもなるし交通費も浮くので一石二鳥です。","romaji":"Jitensha tsuukin wa, undou ni mo naru shi koutsuuhi mo uku node isseki nichou desu.","german":"Mit dem Rad zur Arbeit zu fahren ist doppelt gut: Man bewegt sich und spart Fahrtkosten.","furigana":"じてんしゃつうきん|うんどう|こうつうひ|う|いっせきにちょう"},{"japanese":"料理をしながら英語のポッドキャストを聞けば、一石二鳥だ。","romaji":"Ryouri o shinagara eigo no poddokyasuto o kikeba, isseki nichou da.","german":"Wenn man beim Kochen einen englischen Podcast hört, schlägt man zwei Fliegen mit einer Klappe.","furigana":"りょうり|えいご|き|いっせきにちょう"}],"notes":"一石二鳥 heißt wörtlich „ein Stein, zwei Vögel“ und ist eine Lehnübersetzung des englischen Sprichworts; im Deutschen entspricht es „zwei Fliegen mit einer Klappe schlagen“. Man gebraucht es prädikativ (一石二鳥だ) oder attributiv (一石二鳥の方法, 一石二鳥のアイデア), oft nach einer Begründung mit 〜し〜し. Ein gehobeneres Synonym ist 一挙両得.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自業自得ですよ。","romaji":"Jigō jitoku desu yo.","german":"That's your own doing.","furigana":"じごうじとく"},{"japanese":"徹夜でゲームをして寝坊したのは自業自得だ。","romaji":"Tetsuya de geemu o shite nebou shita no wa jigou jitoku da.","german":"You overslept because you stayed up all night gaming - that's your own fault.","furigana":"てつや|ねぼう|じごうじとく"},{"japanese":"勉強しないで試験に落ちたのだから、自業自得としか言えない。","romaji":"Benkyou shinaide shiken ni ochita no da kara, jigou jitoku to shika ienai.","german":"He didn't study and failed the exam, so all you can say is: he brought it on himself.","furigana":"べんきょう|しけん|お|じごうじとく|い"}],"notes":"自業自得 is composed of 自業 \"one's own deeds\" and 自得 \"receiving oneself\" and comes from the Buddhist doctrine of karma: one reaps the consequences of one's own actions. Today it is used almost only negatively in the sense of \"serves you right\": 自業自得だ, 自業自得としか言えない. It sounds quite harsh and accusatory; a figurative synonym is 身から出た錆.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"以心伝心で分かります。","romaji":"Ishin denshin de wakarimasu.","german":"We understand each other without words.","furigana":"いしんでんしん|わ"},{"japanese":"長く一緒にいる夫婦は、以心伝心で気持ちが通じる。","romaji":"Nagaku issho ni iru fuufu wa, ishin denshin de kimochi ga tsuujiru.","german":"Couples who have been together for a long time understand each other's feelings without words.","furigana":"なが|いっしょ|ふうふ|いしんでんしん|きも|つう"},{"japanese":"仕事では以心伝心に頼らず、はっきり伝えたほうがいい。","romaji":"Shigoto de wa ishin denshin ni tayorazu, hakkiri tsutaeta hou ga ii.","german":"At work, it's better to say things clearly rather than rely on unspoken understanding.","furigana":"しごと|いしんでんしん|たよ|つた"}],"notes":"以心伝心 literally means \"to transmit heart to heart\" and comes from Zen, where teaching passes from master to disciple without words. In everyday use it describes tacit understanding between people who are close: 以心伝心で分かる/通じる, 以心伝心の仲. It is not about telepathy but about familiarity; similar is 阿吽の呼吸 (perfectly synchronized timing).","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一期一会を大切にします。","romaji":"Ichigo ichie o taisetsu ni shimasu.","german":"I cherish each unrepeatable encounter.","furigana":"いちごいちえ|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"旅先での出会いは一期一会だと思う。","romaji":"Tabisaki de no deai wa ichigo ichie da to omou.","german":"I think encounters while traveling are once-in-a-lifetime.","furigana":"たびさき|であ|いちごいちえ|おも"},{"japanese":"一期一会の気持ちで、お客様をおもてなししています。","romaji":"Ichigo ichie no kimochi de, okyakusama o omotenashi shite imasu.","german":"We treat our guests in the spirit of ichigo ichie.","furigana":"いちごいちえ|きも|きゃくさま"}],"notes":"一期一会 comes from the tea ceremony: 一期 (いちご) is \"a lifetime\", 一会 (いちえ) \"one meeting\" - every gathering is unique and should be conducted with full devotion. One says 一期一会を大切にする, 一期一会の出会い or 〜は一期一会だ. The readings いちご and え are unusual and must be learned; the expression is popular in hospitality, tourism and greetings.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本文化は花鳥風月を大切にする。","romaji":"Nihon bunka wa kachō fūgetsu o taisetsu ni suru.","german":"Japanese culture values the beauties of nature.","furigana":"にほんぶんか|かちょうふうげつ|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"昔の歌人は花鳥風月を和歌に詠んだ。","romaji":"Mukashi no kajin wa kachō fūgetsu o waka ni yonda.","german":"The poets of old composed waka about the beauties of nature.","furigana":"むかし|かじん|かちょうふうげつ|わか|よ"},{"japanese":"定年後は、花鳥風月を楽しむ穏やかな暮らしがしたい。","romaji":"Teinen-go wa, kachō fūgetsu o tanoshimu odayaka na kurashi ga shitai.","german":"After retirement, I want to live a peaceful life enjoying the beauties of nature.","furigana":"ていねんご|かちょうふうげつ|たの|おだ|く"}],"notes":"花鳥風月 lists flowers, birds, wind and moon and stands for the beauties of nature as classic motifs of Japanese aesthetics. Typical are 花鳥風月を愛でる/楽しむ/詠む. In a second sense, it also denotes the artful engagement with these themes in poetry, painting or the tea ceremony. The expression is elevated and literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"哲学は森羅万象を対象とする学問だ。","romaji":"Tetsugaku wa shinra banshō o taishō to suru gakumon da.","german":"Philosophy is a discipline that takes all things in the universe as its subject.","furigana":"てつがく|しんらばんしょう|たいしょう|がくもん"},{"japanese":"森羅万象に感謝の気持ちを持つ。","romaji":"Shinra banshō ni kansha no kimochi o motsu.","german":"I feel gratitude toward all of creation.","furigana":"しんらばんしょう|かんしゃ|きも|も"},{"japanese":"神道では森羅万象に神が宿ると考えられている。","romaji":"Shintō de wa shinra banshō ni kami ga yadoru to kangaerarete iru.","german":"In Shinto, it is believed that gods dwell in all things in nature.","furigana":"しんとう|しんらばんしょう|かみ|やど|かんが"}],"notes":"森羅 means \"densely lined up like trees in a forest\", 万象 \"all phenomena\": 森羅万象 encompasses everything there is in the universe. It usually stands as an object or theme: 森羅万象を対象とする, 森羅万象に宿る. The expression is solemn and appears in philosophy, religion and descriptions of nature; in everyday speech one simply says あらゆるもの or 世の中のすべて.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"喜怒哀楽を素直に表現する人だ。","romaji":"Kido airaku o sunao ni hyōgen suru hito da.","german":"He is a person who expresses his emotions honestly.","furigana":"きどあいらく|すなお|ひょうげん|ひと"},{"japanese":"喜怒哀楽の激しい性格だ。","romaji":"Kido airaku no hageshii seikaku da.","german":"He has a temperamental personality with strong emotions.","furigana":"きどあいらく|はげ|せいかく"},{"japanese":"父はあまり喜怒哀楽を顔に出さない。","romaji":"Chichi wa amari kido airaku o kao ni dasanai.","german":"My father hardly shows his emotions on his face.","furigana":"ちち|きどあいらく|かお|だ"}],"notes":"喜 \"joy\", 怒 \"anger\", 哀 \"sorrow\", 楽 \"pleasure\": the four basic emotions together stand for the whole range of human emotions. Typical frames: 喜怒哀楽を表す／顔に出す \"to show emotions\", 喜怒哀楽が激しい \"very emotional, moody\" and 喜怒哀楽がない. Neutral, often in descriptions of character or acting.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"七転び八起きの精神で頑張ろう。","romaji":"Nana korobi ya oki no seishin de ganbarō.","german":"Let's do our best in the spirit of \"fall seven times, get up eight\".","furigana":"ななころ|やお|せいしん|がんば"},{"japanese":"人生は七転び八起きだから、一度の失敗で落ち込むなよ。","romaji":"Jinsei wa nana korobi ya oki da kara, ichido no shippai de ochikomu na yo.","german":"Life is full of ups and downs, so don't get discouraged by one failure.","furigana":"じんせい|ななころ|やお|いちど|しっぱい|お|こ"},{"japanese":"祖父の人生は、まさに七転び八起きだった。","romaji":"Sofu no jinsei wa, masa ni nana korobi ya oki datta.","german":"My grandfather's life was truly one of falling seven times and getting up eight.","furigana":"そふ|じんせい|ななころ|やお"}],"notes":"七転び八起き literally means \"fall down seven times, get up eight times\": one does not let setbacks get one down and keeps getting back up. It also denotes the ups and downs of life: 七転び八起きの人生. Typical are 七転び八起きの精神 and 人生は七転び八起きだ. The proverb is associated with the Daruma figure, which rights itself again and again. Neutral, encouraging.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は石橋を叩いて渡るタイプだから、決断に時間がかかる。","romaji":"Kare wa ishibashi o tataite wataru taipu da kara, ketsudan ni jikan ga kakaru.","german":"He is the type to tap a stone bridge before crossing it, so decisions take him a long time.","furigana":"かれ|いしばし|たた|わた|けつだん|じかん"},{"japanese":"大きな買い物をする時は、石橋を叩いて渡るくらいでちょうどいい。","romaji":"Ōkina kaimono o suru toki wa, ishibashi o tataite wataru kurai de chōdo ii.","german":"When making a big purchase, it's just right to be extremely careful.","furigana":"おお|か|もの|とき|いしばし|たた|わた"},{"japanese":"あの銀行は石橋を叩いて渡るような経営で、不景気でも安定している。","romaji":"Ano ginkō wa ishibashi o tataite wataru yō na keiei de, fukeiki de mo antei shite iru.","german":"That bank is run with extreme caution, so it is stable even in a recession.","furigana":"ぎんこう|いしばし|たた|わた|けいえい|ふけいき|あんてい"}],"notes":"Literally \"to tap a stone bridge before crossing it\": one double-checks even what is safe. Depending on context, it is either praise (thorough, prudent) or mildly critical (overcautious, hesitant). Typical: 石橋を叩いて渡る性格／タイプ, 石橋を叩いて渡るような〜. A humorous intensification: 石橋を叩いても渡らない \"so cautious that one never acts at all\". Neutral; the verb is conjugated normally (渡って, 渡った).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"猫の手も借りたいほど忙しい。","romaji":"Neko no te mo karitai hodo isogashii.","german":"I'm so busy that I need any help I can get.","furigana":"ねこ|て|か|いそが"},{"japanese":"年末は猫の手も借りたいくらいの忙しさです。","romaji":"Nenmatsu wa neko no te mo karitai kurai no isogashisa desu.","german":"At the end of the year it's so busy that we'd welcome any helping hand.","furigana":"ねんまつ|ねこ|て|か|いそが"},{"japanese":"引っ越しの日は、猫の手も借りたかったよ。","romaji":"Hikkoshi no hi wa, neko no te mo karitakatta yo.","german":"On moving day, I really could have used any help.","furigana":"ひ|こ|ひ|ねこ|て|か"}],"notes":"Literally: \"I'd even like to borrow a cat's paws\" - so much to do that even a cat would be welcome as help. Almost always with a degree expression: 猫の手も借りたいほど/くらい忙しい or 猫の手も借りたいくらいの忙しさ. The expression is colloquial and humorous; 借りたい inflects like an い-adjective (借りたかった).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい同僚とは馬が合いそうだ。","romaji":"Atarashii dōryō to wa uma ga aisō da.","german":"I think I'll get along with the new coworker.","furigana":"あたら|どうりょう|うま|あ"},{"japanese":"二人は性格が正反対なのに、不思議と馬が合う。","romaji":"Futari wa seikaku ga seihantai na noni, fushigi to uma ga au.","german":"The two have opposite personalities, yet strangely they get along.","furigana":"ふたり|せいかく|せいはんたい|ふしぎ|うま|あ"},{"japanese":"悪い人ではないが、彼とはどうも馬が合わない。","romaji":"Warui hito de wa nai ga, kare to wa dōmo uma ga awanai.","german":"He's not a bad person, but somehow I don't click with him.","furigana":"わる|ひと|かれ|うま|あ"}],"notes":"馬が合う literally means \"the horse fits\" - originally, rider and horse were in harmony. Today it means that two people get along well right away, often for no explainable reason. The partner takes と: Xとは馬が合う. Very similar is 気が合う; the negative 馬が合わない is also common. Neutral, colloquial.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"目から鱗が落ちました。","romaji":"Me kara uroko ga ochimashita.","german":"Es war eine Offenbarung.","furigana":"め|うろこ|お"},{"japanese":"先生の説明を聞いて、まさに目から鱗だった。","romaji":"Sensei no setsumei o kiite, masa ni me kara uroko datta.","german":"Als ich die Erklärung des Lehrers hörte, fiel es mir wie Schuppen von den Augen.","furigana":"せんせい|せつめい|き|め|うろこ"},{"japanese":"この料理の本には、目から鱗のアイデアがたくさんある。","romaji":"Kono ryouri no hon ni wa, me kara uroko no aidea ga takusan aru.","german":"In diesem Kochbuch stehen viele Ideen, die einem die Augen öffnen.","furigana":"りょうり|ほん|め|うろこ"}],"notes":"目から鱗 ist die Kurzform von 目から鱗が落ちる „es fällt einem wie Schuppen von den Augen“ und geht auf das Neue Testament (Paulus) zurück – genau wie das deutsche Bild. Die Kurzform wird als Nomen benutzt: 目から鱗だった, 目から鱗の話/アイデア. Gemeint ist eine überraschende Erkenntnis, die das bisherige Verständnis plötzlich klärt; 鱗 wird oft auch うろこ geschrieben.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"腹を割って話しましょう。","romaji":"Hara o watte hanashimashō.","german":"Let's talk openly with each other.","furigana":"はら|わ|はな"},{"japanese":"一度、腹を割って話し合う必要がある。","romaji":"Ichido, hara o watte hanashiau hitsuyou ga aru.","german":"We need to have a frank talk once.","furigana":"いちど|はら|わ|はな|あ|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"部長とお酒を飲みながら、腹を割った話ができた。","romaji":"Buchou to osake o nominagara, hara o watta hanashi ga dekita.","german":"Over drinks with the department head, we were able to talk openly.","furigana":"ぶちょう|さけ|の|はら|わ|はなし"}],"notes":"In Japanese, 腹 (belly) stands for one's true intentions; 腹を割る literally means \"to cut open the belly\", that is, to lay one's real thoughts bare. It almost always appears as 腹を割って話す/話し合う or attributively as 腹を割った話. It emphasizes mutual openness in private; related are 本音で話す and the quite different 腹を括る (to prepare for the worst).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい技術を身につけます。","romaji":"Atarashii gijutsu o mi ni tsukemasu.","german":"I acquire new skills.","furigana":"あたら|ぎじゅつ|み"},{"japanese":"子どものうちに、正しいマナーを身につけさせたい。","romaji":"Kodomo no uchi ni, tadashii manaa o mi ni tsukesasetai.","german":"I want them to learn proper manners while they are still children.","furigana":"こ|ただ|み"},{"japanese":"彼女はいつも祖母の指輪を身につけている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa itsumo sobo no yubiwa o mi ni tsukete iru.","german":"She always wears her grandmother's ring.","furigana":"かのじょ|そぼ|ゆびわ|み"}],"notes":"身につける literally means \"to attach to the body\" and has two meanings: to internalize skills, knowledge or habits (技術・マナーを身につける) and to wear clothes or jewelry on the body (for the latter also written 身に着ける). The intransitive counterpart is 身につく \"to sink in, to stick\": 習慣が身につく. Unlike 覚える, 身につける emphasizes that something truly becomes one's own ability.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仕事で手を抜いてはいけない。","romaji":"Shigoto de te o nuite wa ikenai.","german":"You must not cut corners at work.","furigana":"しごと|て|ぬ"},{"japanese":"子供とテニスをするときも、父は絶対に手を抜かない。","romaji":"Kodomo to tenisu o suru toki mo, chichi wa zettai ni te o nukanai.","german":"Even when playing tennis with us kids, my father never goes easy.","furigana":"こども|ちち|ぜったい|て|ぬ"},{"japanese":"忙しい日は、少し料理の手を抜いてもいいと思う。","romaji":"Isogashii hi wa, sukoshi ryōri no te o nuite mo ii to omou.","german":"On busy days, I think it's fine to cut corners a bit when cooking.","furigana":"いそが|ひ|すこ|りょうり|て|ぬ|おも"}],"notes":"手を抜く literally means \"to pull out the hand\" and means \"to work carelessly, take shortcuts, spare oneself\". The area is marked with の or で: 掃除の手を抜く, 仕事で手を抜く. In competition it means \"not playing at full strength\": 子供相手でも手を抜かない. The related noun is 手抜き (手抜き工事 \"shoddy construction work\", 手抜き料理 \"a quick meal without effort\"). Depending on context it is critical or, with 少し手を抜く, quite relaxed.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は気が利くので、みんなに好かれている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa ki ga kiku node, minna ni sukarete iru.","german":"She is thoughtful, so everyone likes her.","furigana":"かのじょ|き|き|す"},{"japanese":"このホテルは、細かいところまで気が利いている。","romaji":"Kono hoteru wa, komakai tokoro made ki ga kiite iru.","german":"This hotel is attentive down to the smallest details.","furigana":"こま|き|き"},{"japanese":"スピーチで何か気の利いた一言が言えればよかったのに。","romaji":"Supīchi de nani ka ki no kiita hitokoto ga iereba yokatta noni.","german":"I wish I could have said something clever in my speech.","furigana":"なに|き|き|ひとこと|い"}],"notes":"気が利く means being attentive and noticing on one's own what others need - high praise in Japanese everyday life. The negative 気が利かない is a common reproach. Before nouns, 気の利いた \"clever, fitting, well done\" is often used: 気の利いたプレゼント, 気の利いた一言. Do not confuse it with 気をつける \"to watch out\" or 気を使う \"to be considerate, to worry about others\". Neutral.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は顔が広いから、何でも頼める。","romaji":"Kare wa kao ga hiroi kara, nan demo tanomeru.","german":"He knows a lot of people, so you can ask him for anything.","furigana":"かれ|かお|ひろ|なん|たの"},{"japanese":"顔が広い先輩のおかげで、いいアルバイトが見つかった。","romaji":"Kao ga hiroi senpai no okage de, ii arubaito ga mitsukatta.","german":"Thanks to my well-connected senior, I found a good part-time job.","furigana":"かお|ひろ|せんぱい|み"},{"japanese":"父は町で顔が広く、どこへ行っても声をかけられる。","romaji":"Chichi wa machi de kao ga hiroku, doko e itte mo koe o kakerareru.","german":"My father is widely known in town and gets greeted wherever he goes.","furigana":"ちち|まち|かお|ひろ|い|こえ"}],"notes":"顔が広い literally means \"the face is wide\" and means that someone knows many people and is known everywhere. Before a noun, が usually becomes の: 顔の広い人. Related but different: 顔が利く \"to have influence, to be able to pull strings\" and 顔を出す \"to drop by, to show up\". A neutral, everyday expression with a positive tone.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"部長は頭が固くて、新しい提案を受け入れない。","romaji":"Buchō wa atama ga katakute, atarashii teian o ukeirenai.","german":"The department head is inflexible and doesn't accept new proposals.","furigana":"ぶちょう|あたま|かた|あたら|ていあん|う|い"},{"japanese":"年を取ると頭が固くなると言うが、祖母は新しいことが大好きだ。","romaji":"Toshi o toru to atama ga kataku naru to iu ga, sobo wa atarashii koto ga daisuki da.","german":"They say people become set in their ways as they age, but my grandmother loves new things.","furigana":"とし|と|あたま|かた|い|そぼ|あたら|だいす"},{"japanese":"頭が固いと言われないように、いろいろな意見を聞くようにしている。","romaji":"Atama ga katai to iwarenai yō ni, iroiro na iken o kiku yō ni shite iru.","german":"So that no one calls me inflexible, I try to listen to various opinions.","furigana":"あたま|かた|い|いけん|き"}],"notes":"頭が固い literally means \"the head is hard\" and describes someone who stubbornly clings to their own ideas and does not accept anything new. The opposite is 頭が柔らかい \"to think flexibly\"; with なる one says 頭が固くなる \"to become set in one's ways\". A similar insult is 石頭. Besides 固い, the spelling 堅い is also seen, but not 硬い. Neutral to colloquial, judgmental.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は口が堅いから、秘密を話しても大丈夫だ。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kuchi ga katai kara, himitsu o hanashitemo daijōbu da.","german":"She can keep a secret, so it's fine to tell her secrets.","furigana":"かのじょ|くち|かた|ひみつ|はな|だいじょうぶ"},{"japanese":"この仕事は秘密が多いので、口の堅い人を探している。","romaji":"Kono shigoto wa himitsu ga ōi node, kuchi no katai hito o sagashite iru.","german":"This job involves many secrets, so we are looking for a discreet person.","furigana":"しごと|ひみつ|おお|くち|かた|ひと|さが"},{"japanese":"口が堅いと信じて相談したのに、みんなに話されてしまった。","romaji":"Kuchi ga katai to shinjite sōdan shita noni, minna ni hanasarete shimatta.","german":"I consulted him believing he could keep a secret, but he told everyone.","furigana":"くち|かた|しん|そうだん|はな"}],"notes":"口が堅い literally means \"the mouth is firm\" and describes someone who is discreet and keeps secrets to themselves - clear praise. The opposite is 口が軽い. Attributively one says 口の堅い人. The spelling with 堅い (firm, reliable) is usual; 固い also occurs. Neutral.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は腰が重くて、なかなか行動しない。","romaji":"Kare wa koshi ga omokute, nakanaka kōdō shinai.","german":"He is slow to act and hardly ever takes action.","furigana":"かれ|こし|おも|こうどう"},{"japanese":"掃除をしなきゃと思っても、休みの日はどうも腰が重い。","romaji":"Sōji o shinakya to omotte mo, yasumi no hi wa dōmo koshi ga omoi.","german":"I know I should clean, but on days off I just can't get myself going.","furigana":"そうじ|おも|やす|ひ|こし|おも"},{"japanese":"腰が重い夫を説得して、やっと病院に連れて行った。","romaji":"Koshi ga omoi otto o settoku shite, yatto byōin ni tsurete itta.","german":"I persuaded my reluctant husband and finally took him to the hospital.","furigana":"こし|おも|おっと|せっとく|びょういん|つ|い"}],"notes":"腰が重い literally means \"the hips are heavy\" and means \"to find it hard to get going, to get started only reluctantly\". It is not about back pain (that would be 腰が痛い) but about a lack of willingness to tackle something: 腰が重くてなかなか〜しない. The opposite is 腰が軽い \"quick to act, helpful\". Similar is なかなか腰が上がらない. The expression is neutral and behaves like an い-adjective.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"プロジェクトが終わって肩の荷が下りた。","romaji":"Purojekuto ga owatte kata no ni ga orita.","german":"When the project was over, a weight was lifted off my shoulders.","furigana":"お|かた|に|お"},{"japanese":"子供が全員独立して、やっと肩の荷が下りた。","romaji":"Kodomo ga zen'in dokuritsu shite, yatto kata no ni ga orita.","german":"Now that all the children have become independent, I'm finally free of that responsibility.","furigana":"こども|ぜんいんどくりつ|かた|に|お"},{"japanese":"大事な発表が無事に終わり、肩の荷が下りた気分だ。","romaji":"Daiji na happyō ga buji ni owari, kata no ni ga orita kibun da.","german":"The important presentation went off without a hitch, and I feel truly relieved.","furigana":"だいじ|はっぴょう|ぶじ|お|かた|に|お|きぶん"}],"notes":"肩の荷が下りる literally means \"the load on the shoulders is unloaded\" and corresponds to \"a weight is lifted off one's shoulders\". One says it when a responsibility or worry ends: 〜が終わって肩の荷が下りた, やっと肩の荷が下りた, often together with ほっとする. The transitive counterpart is 肩の荷を下ろす \"to free oneself of a burden\". Compare 荷が重い \"to be too heavy a burden\". The expression is neutral and mostly used in the past tense.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"楽しい雰囲気に水を差さないで。","romaji":"Tanoshii fun'iki ni mizu o sasanaide.","german":"Don't put a damper on the cheerful mood.","furigana":"たの|ふんいき|みず|さ"},{"japanese":"水を差すようで悪いけど、その計画は予算が足りないと思う。","romaji":"Mizu o sasu you de warui kedo, sono keikaku wa yosan ga tarinai to omou.","german":"I don't want to be a killjoy, but I think the budget isn't enough for that plan.","furigana":"みず|さ|わる|けいかく|よさん|た|おも"},{"japanese":"雨が祭りの盛り上がりに水を差した。","romaji":"Ame ga matsuri no moriagari ni mizu o sashita.","german":"The rain dampened the excitement of the festival.","furigana":"あめ|まつ|も|あ|みず|さ"}],"notes":"水を差す literally means \"to pour in water\" (for example into hot water or sake), figuratively \"to put a damper on, to disturb something\". The target is marked with に: 雰囲気に水を差す, 話に水を差す, 二人の仲に水を差す (to drive a wedge between two people). Very common is the apologetic introduction 水を差すようで悪いけど before one raises an objection.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"こんなところで油を売っている場合じゃない。","romaji":"Konna tokoro de abura o utte iru baai ja nai.","german":"This is no time to be wasting time in a place like this.","furigana":"あぶら|う|ばあい"},{"japanese":"どこで油を売っていたの？もう一時間も遅れているよ。","romaji":"Doko de abura o utte ita no? Mō ichi-jikan mo okurete iru yo.","german":"Where have you been dawdling? You're already an hour late.","furigana":"あぶら|う|いちじかん|おく"},{"japanese":"仕事中に油を売っていると、課長に叱られるよ。","romaji":"Shigoto-chū ni abura o utte iru to, kachō ni shikarareru yo.","german":"If you loiter during work, the section chief will scold you.","furigana":"しごとちゅう|あぶら|う|かちょう|しか"}],"notes":"油を売る literally means \"to sell oil\": in the Edo period, hair-oil sellers chatted with their customers for a long time while the viscous oil slowly dripped. So it means to dawdle during work or on the way and waste time chatting. Almost always in the progressive form: どこで油を売っていたの？ Colloquial, mostly reproachful.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"遅刻しないように釘を刺しておいた。","romaji":"Chikoku shinai yō ni kugi o sashite oita.","german":"I cautioned him in advance not to be late.","furigana":"ちこく|くぎ|さ"},{"japanese":"「このことは誰にも言うな」と釘を刺された。","romaji":"\"Kono koto wa dare ni mo iu na\" to kugi o sasareta.","german":"I was warned, \"Don't tell anyone about this.\"","furigana":"だれ|い|くぎ|さ"},{"japanese":"政府は企業に対し、値上げを控えるよう釘を刺した。","romaji":"Seifu wa kigyō ni taishi, neage o hikaeru yō kugi o sashita.","german":"The government warned companies firmly to refrain from raising prices.","furigana":"せいふ|きぎょう|たい|ねあ|ひか|くぎ|さ"}],"notes":"釘を刺す literally means \"to drive in a nail\" (to secure something). Figuratively: to firmly warn someone in advance so that they do not make a mistake or break a promise later. Typical frames are 〜ように釘を刺す, 「…」と釘を刺す and 〜に釘を刺す, often with 〜ておく; passive 釘を刺される. Unlike the neutral 念を押す (\"to confirm once more\"), 釘を刺す sounds sterner and more warning.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"料理の腕を磨きたい。","romaji":"Ryōri no ude o migakitai.","german":"I want to improve my cooking skills.","furigana":"りょうり|うで|みが"},{"japanese":"彼はフランスで三年間、パン作りの腕を磨いた。","romaji":"Kare wa Furansu de san-nenkan, pan-zukuri no ude o migaita.","german":"He honed his bread-making skills in France for three years.","furigana":"かれ|さんねんかん|づく|うで|みが"},{"japanese":"大会に向けて、毎日テニスの腕を磨いている。","romaji":"Taikai ni mukete, mainichi tenisu no ude o migaite iru.","german":"I'm honing my tennis skills every day in preparation for the tournament.","furigana":"たいかい|む|まいにち|うで|みが"}],"notes":"腕を磨く literally means \"to polish the arm\"; 腕 stands for skill and ability here. Typical is 〜の腕を磨く with a field: 料理の腕, テニスの腕. Related expressions are 腕を上げる / 腕が上がる \"to get better\" and 腕がいい \"to be skilled\". Neutral, often used for crafts, sports and cooking.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"チームの足を引っ張りたくない。","romaji":"Chīmu no ashi o hipparitakunai.","german":"I don't want to hold the team back.","furigana":"あし|ひ|ぱ"},{"japanese":"人の足を引っ張るより、自分が努力したほうがいい。","romaji":"Hito no ashi o hipparu yori, jibun ga doryoku shita hō ga ii.","german":"Rather than dragging others down, you should make an effort yourself.","furigana":"ひと|あし|ひ|ぱ|じぶん|どりょく"},{"japanese":"数学の点数が足を引っ張って、志望校に合格できなかった。","romaji":"Sūgaku no tensū ga ashi o hippatte, shibōkō ni gōkaku dekinakatta.","german":"My math score dragged me down, and I couldn't get into the school I wanted.","furigana":"すうがく|てんすう|あし|ひ|ぱ|しぼうこう|ごうかく"}],"notes":"足を引っ張る literally means \"to pull by the leg\". It means, on the one hand, to unintentionally set a group back (チームの足を引っ張る \"to hold the team back\"), and on the other, to deliberately harm someone out of envy (人の足を引っ張る). Things can also be the subject: 数学の点数が足を引っ張った \"the math score dragged everything down\". Neutral and very common.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"堂々と胸を張って歩きなさい。","romaji":"Dōdō to mune o hatte arukinasai.","german":"Walk with your chest out, confidently.","furigana":"どうどう|むね|は|ある"},{"japanese":"試合には負けたけど、胸を張って帰ろう。","romaji":"Shiai ni wa maketa kedo, mune o hatte kaerō.","german":"We lost the game, but let's go home with our heads held high.","furigana":"しあい|ま|むね|は|かえ"},{"japanese":"自分の仕事に胸を張れるようになりたい。","romaji":"Jibun no shigoto ni mune o hareru yō ni naritai.","german":"I want to become able to be proud of my work.","furigana":"じぶん|しごと|むね|は"}],"notes":"胸を張る literally means \"to stretch the chest\", i.e. to stand upright with the chest thrust out. Figuratively it means being confident and proud without having to feel ashamed: 胸を張って歩く, 胸を張れる仕事. Unlike 威張る \"to show off, to act important\", it is usually meant positively. Neutral, often used in encouragement.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"腹を括って挑みます。","romaji":"Hara o kukutte idomimasu.","german":"I take on the challenge with resolve.","furigana":"はら|くく|いど"},{"japanese":"もう逃げられないと腹をくくった。","romaji":"Mou nigerarenai to hara o kukutta.","german":"I realized I couldn't escape anymore and resigned myself to it.","furigana":"に|はら"},{"japanese":"失敗してもいいと腹を括ったら、気が楽になった。","romaji":"Shippai shite mo ii to hara o kukuttara, ki ga raku ni natta.","german":"When I resolved that it was fine to fail, I felt relieved.","furigana":"しっぱい|はら|くく|き|らく"}],"notes":"腹を括る literally means \"to tie up the belly\": one makes a firm decision and is prepared for the worst. It is mostly written 腹をくくる in kana; typical are 〜と腹をくくる and 腹をくくって〜する. Similar are 覚悟を決める and 腹を決める; do not confuse with 腹を割る (to speak openly).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの社長は社員を顎で使うことで有名だ。","romaji":"Ano shachō wa shain o ago de tsukau koto de yūmei da.","german":"That company president is famous for ordering his employees around.","furigana":"しゃちょう|しゃいん|あご|つか|ゆうめい"},{"japanese":"先輩だからといって、後輩を顎で使うのはよくない。","romaji":"Senpai da kara to itte, kōhai o ago de tsukau no wa yokunai.","german":"Just because you're a senior, it's not good to boss juniors around.","furigana":"せんぱい|こうはい|あご|つか"},{"japanese":"弟は親を顎で使って、自分では何もしない。","romaji":"Otōto wa oya o ago de tsukatte, jibun de wa nani mo shinai.","german":"My younger brother orders our parents around and does nothing himself.","furigana":"おとうと|おや|あご|つか|じぶん|なに"}],"notes":"顎で使う literally means \"to use with the chin\" - one gives orders merely with a nod of the head, without lifting a finger oneself. It means to order others around arrogantly: Xを顎で使う. The expression is clearly derogatory and is used for superiors, older colleagues or family members. 顎 is often written in hiragana (あごで使う). Colloquial.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"締め切りが近づいて尻に火がついた。","romaji":"Shimekiri ga chikazuite shiri ni hi ga tsuita.","german":"The deadline is near, and now I'm under pressure.","furigana":"し|き|ちか|しり|ひ"},{"japanese":"試験の一週間前になって、やっと尻に火がついた。","romaji":"Shiken no isshūkan mae ni natte, yatto shiri ni hi ga tsuita.","german":"It was only a week before the exam that I finally got a fire under me.","furigana":"しけん|いっしゅうかんまえ|しり|ひ"},{"japanese":"私は尻に火がつかないと、なかなか本気になれない。","romaji":"Watashi wa shiri ni hi ga tsukanai to, nakanaka honki ni narenai.","german":"If I'm not under pressure, I can't get serious.","furigana":"わたし|しり|ひ|ほんき"}],"notes":"尻に火がつく literally means \"the backside catches fire\" and describes a matter becoming so urgent that one finally has to act - typically shortly before a deadline. Often やっと尻に火がついた \"now it's finally burning\". More polite is お尻に火がつく. Also spelled 尻に火が付く. Colloquial, slightly crude.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"治験に参加します。","romaji":"Chiken ni sanka shimasu.","german":"I take part in a clinical trial.","furigana":"ちけん|さんか"},{"japanese":"新しい薬は、治験で安全性が確認されてから発売されます。","romaji":"Atarashii kusuri wa, chiken de anzensei ga kakunin sarete kara hatsubai saremasu.","german":"New drugs are released only after their safety has been confirmed in clinical trials.","furigana":"あたら|くすり|ちけん|あんぜんせい|かくにん|はつばい"},{"japanese":"学生のころ、治験のアルバイトをしたことがあります。","romaji":"Gakusei no koro, chiken no arubaito o shita koto ga arimasu.","german":"When I was a student, I once did a paid job as a clinical trial participant.","furigana":"がくせい|ちけん"}],"notes":"治験 is a clinical trial in which a new drug is tested on humans to obtain government approval. One says 治験に参加する, 治験を行う; the test drug is called 治験薬. 臨床試験 is the more general technical term for clinical research; in Japan one also knows the paid 治験のアルバイト for test subjects.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"点滴を受けています。","romaji":"Tenteki o uketeimasu.","german":"I am receiving an IV drip.","furigana":"てんてき|う"},{"japanese":"脱水症状がひどかったので、病院で点滴を打ってもらいました。","romaji":"Dassui shoujou ga hidokatta node, byouin de tenteki o utte moraimashita.","german":"I was severely dehydrated, so I got an IV drip at the hospital.","furigana":"だっすいしょうじょう|びょういん|てんてき|う"},{"japanese":"点滴が終わるまで、一時間ぐらいかかります。","romaji":"Tenteki ga owaru made, ichijikan gurai kakarimasu.","german":"It takes about an hour until the drip is finished.","furigana":"てんてき|お|いちじかん"}],"notes":"点滴 in daily life is the infusion (short for 点滴注射): 点滴を受ける, 点滴を打つ/打ってもらう, and as a する-verb also 点滴する. Typical for dehydration, exhaustion or in the hospital; the stand is called 点滴スタンド. The original meaning \"falling drops of water\" is still known from the proverb 点滴石を穿つ (constant dripping wears away the stone).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"午前中に診察を受けます。","romaji":"Gozenchū ni shinsatsu o ukemasu.","german":"I have an examination in the morning.","furigana":"ごぜんちゅう|しんさつ|う"},{"japanese":"先生は丁寧に患者を診察しました。","romaji":"Sensei wa teinei ni kanja o shinsatsu shimashita.","german":"The doctor examined the patient carefully.","furigana":"せんせい|ていねい|かんじゃ|しんさつ"},{"japanese":"受付で診察券を出してください。","romaji":"Uketsuke de shinsatsuken o dashite kudasai.","german":"Please hand in your patient card at the reception.","furigana":"うけつけ|しんさつけん|だ"}],"notes":"診察 is the medical examination or consultation: the patient says 診察を受ける, the doctor 患者を診察する. Related are 診察室 (examination room), 診察券 (patient card) and 診察時間 (consultation hours). 検査 are individual tests such as blood draws, 診断 is the resulting diagnosis, and 健康診断 the regular health checkup.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一日も早いご快復をお祈りしております。","romaji":"Ichinichi mo hayai gokaifuku o oinori shite orimasu.","german":"I sincerely wish you a speedy recovery.","furigana":"いちにち|はや|かいふく|いの"},{"japanese":"手術のあと、祖父は順調に快復しています。","romaji":"Shujutsu no ato, sofu wa junchou ni kaifuku shite imasu.","german":"After the operation, my grandfather is recovering well.","furigana":"しゅじゅつ|そふ|じゅんちょう|かいふく"},{"japanese":"彼女は病気から快復して、仕事に戻った。","romaji":"Kanojo wa byouki kara kaifuku shite, shigoto ni modotta.","german":"She recovered from the illness and returned to work.","furigana":"かのじょ|びょうき|かいふく|しごと|もど"}],"notes":"快復 mainly means recovery from an illness; one says 病気から快復する or, in get-well wishes, ご快復をお祈りします. The far more common spelling 回復 has the same reading and covers all areas, including 景気の回復 or 体力の回復; newspapers usually write 回復 even for illnesses. 快復 therefore seems somewhat more elevated and is especially suited to polite letters.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"インフルエンザの予防接種を受けました。","romaji":"Infuruenza no yobō sesshu o ukemashita.","german":"I got the flu shot.","furigana":"よぼうせっしゅ|う"},{"japanese":"子どもの予防接種のスケジュールを確認しました。","romaji":"Kodomo no yobou sesshu no sukejuuru o kakunin shimashita.","german":"I checked my child's vaccination schedule.","furigana":"こ|よぼうせっしゅ|かくにん"},{"japanese":"海外旅行の前に、必要な予防接種を受けてください。","romaji":"Kaigai ryokou no mae ni, hitsuyou na yobou sesshu o ukete kudasai.","german":"Please get the necessary vaccinations before traveling abroad.","furigana":"かいがいりょこう|まえ|ひつよう|よぼうせっしゅ|う"}],"notes":"予防接種 is composed of 予防 \"prevention\" and 接種 \"inoculation\" and is the official, neutral term for vaccination. One says 予防接種を受ける, and for a child 予防接種を受けさせる; colloquially also ワクチンを打つ. Typically the disease is given with の: インフルエンザの予防接種.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"生活習慣病を予防します。","romaji":"Seikatsu shūkan byō o yobō shimasu.","german":"I prevent lifestyle-related diseases.","furigana":"せいかつしゅうかんびょう|よぼう"},{"japanese":"運動不足は生活習慣病の原因になります。","romaji":"Undou busoku wa seikatsu shuukan byou no gen'in ni narimasu.","german":"Lack of exercise can cause lifestyle-related diseases.","furigana":"うんどうぶそく|せいかつしゅうかんびょう|げんいん"},{"japanese":"父は糖尿病などの生活習慣病に気をつけています。","romaji":"Chichi wa tounyoubyou nado no seikatsu shuukan byou ni ki o tsukete imasu.","german":"My father is careful about lifestyle-related diseases such as diabetes.","furigana":"ちち|とうにょうびょう|せいかつしゅうかんびょう|き"}],"notes":"生活習慣病 are diseases that depend strongly on diet, exercise, smoking and alcohol, such as diabetes, high blood pressure or lipid metabolism disorders. In 1996 the term replaced the older 成人病 \"adult diseases\" in order to emphasize the role of lifestyle. Typical are 生活習慣病を予防する, 生活習慣病になる and 生活習慣病のリスク; often in connection with メタボ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人間ドックの予約をしました。","romaji":"Ningen dokku no yoyaku o shimashita.","german":"I made an appointment for a comprehensive medical checkup.","furigana":"にんげん|よやく"},{"japanese":"四十歳になったので、初めて人間ドックを受けました。","romaji":"Yonjussai ni natta node, hajimete ningen dokku o ukemashita.","german":"Since I turned forty, I had a comprehensive checkup for the first time.","furigana":"よんじゅっさい|はじ|にんげん|う"},{"japanese":"人間ドックで、胃に小さなポリープが見つかった。","romaji":"Ningen dokku de, i ni chiisana poriipu ga mitsukatta.","german":"A small polyp was found in my stomach at the comprehensive checkup.","furigana":"にんげん|い|ちい|み"}],"notes":"人間ドック is a comprehensive health checkup lasting half a day to two days; the name alludes to the dry dock in which ships are thoroughly overhauled. One says 人間ドックを受ける or 人間ドックに入る. Unlike the annual, legally required 健康診断, it is voluntary, more extensive and usually partly paid for by oneself.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"漢方薬を飲んでいます。","romaji":"Kanpōyaku o nondeimasu.","german":"I'm taking herbal medicine.","furigana":"かんぽうやく|の"},{"japanese":"冷え性を治すために漢方薬を試してみました。","romaji":"Hieshou o naosu tame ni kanpouyaku o tameshite mimashita.","german":"I tried herbal medicine to cure my sensitivity to cold.","furigana":"ひ|しょう|なお|かんぽうやく|ため"},{"japanese":"漢方薬は苦いけれど、体に合っているようです。","romaji":"Kanpouyaku wa nigai keredo, karada ni atte iru you desu.","german":"Herbal medicine is bitter, but it seems to suit my body.","furigana":"かんぽうやく|にが|からだ|あ"}],"notes":"漢方薬 are medicines of traditional Chinese medicine (漢方) as developed further in Japan, usually mixtures of herbs and roots. Like all medicines, one \"drinks\" them: 漢方薬を飲む, not 食べる. Many kampo preparations are also prescribed by doctors in Japan; the counterpart is Western medicine (西洋医学の薬).","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鍼灸治療を受けています。","romaji":"Shinkyū chiryō o uketeimasu.","german":"I am receiving acupuncture and moxibustion treatment.","furigana":"しんきゅうちりょう|う"},{"japanese":"肩こりがひどいので、鍼灸院に通い始めました。","romaji":"Katakori ga hidoi node, shinkyuuin ni kayoihajimemashita.","german":"My shoulder stiffness is severe, so I started going to an acupuncture clinic.","furigana":"かた|しんきゅういん|かよ|はじ"},{"japanese":"兄は鍼灸師の資格を取りました。","romaji":"Ani wa shinkyuushi no shikaku o torimashita.","german":"My older brother obtained his license as an acupuncturist.","furigana":"あに|しんきゅうし|しかく|と"}],"notes":"鍼灸 combines 鍼 (acupuncture needle, also written 針) and 灸 (moxibustion, in daily life お灸). Typical words are 鍼灸院 (clinic), 鍼灸師 (state-certified acupuncturist) and 鍼灸治療; one goes there with 鍼灸院に通う. Also well known is the idiom お灸を据える \"to teach someone a lesson\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"整体に通っています。","romaji":"Seitai ni kayotteimasu.","german":"I go for chiropractic treatment.","furigana":"せいたい|かよ"},{"japanese":"腰が痛いので、整体で体のゆがみを直してもらいました。","romaji":"Koshi ga itai node, seitai de karada no yugami o naoshite moraimashita.","german":"My back hurt, so I had my body misalignment corrected at a seitai clinic.","furigana":"こし|いた|せいたい|からだ|なお"},{"japanese":"駅の近くに新しい整体院ができました。","romaji":"Eki no chikaku ni atarashii seitaiin ga dekimashita.","german":"A new body-therapy clinic has opened near the station.","furigana":"えき|ちか|あたら|せいたいいん"}],"notes":"整体 is a manual body therapy meant to \"put in order\" posture, spine and pelvis; one goes to a 整体院 to see a 整体師. Typical are 整体に行く/通う and 整体で体のゆがみを直す. Unlike 鍼灸 or 接骨院, 整体 is not a state-recognized medical profession in Japan but a private healing method.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"指圧マッサージを受けました。","romaji":"Shiatsu massāji o ukemashita.","german":"I had a shiatsu massage.","furigana":"しあつ|う"},{"japanese":"疲れたときは、首のツボを指圧すると楽になります。","romaji":"Tsukareta toki wa, kubi no tsubo o shiatsu suru to raku ni narimasu.","german":"When you're tired, pressing the pressure points on your neck brings relief.","furigana":"つか|くび|しあつ|らく"},{"japanese":"祖父は駅前で指圧の店をやっています。","romaji":"Sofu wa ekimae de shiatsu no mise o yatte imasu.","german":"My grandfather runs a shiatsu shop in front of the station.","furigana":"そふ|えきまえ|しあつ|みせ"}],"notes":"指圧 is the Japanese pressure massage in which specific points are pressed with the fingers and thumbs. Typical are 指圧を受ける \"to receive a shiatsu treatment\", 指圧師 \"shiatsu therapist\" and, as a transitive する-verb, ツボを指圧する \"to press pressure points\". Unlike マッサージ, which generally means kneading and rubbing, 指圧 emphasizes pressure on specific points; 指圧マッサージ is a common advertising term.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"禁煙席をお願いします。","romaji":"Kin'en seki o onegai shimasu.","german":"A non-smoking seat, please.","furigana":"きんえんせき|ねが"},{"japanese":"父は去年から禁煙しています。","romaji":"Chichi wa kyonen kara kin'en shite imasu.","german":"My father has quit smoking since last year.","furigana":"ちち|きょねん|きんえん"},{"japanese":"この店は店内禁煙です。","romaji":"Kono mise wa tennai kin'en desu.","german":"Smoking is prohibited inside this restaurant.","furigana":"みせ|てんないきんえん"}],"notes":"禁煙 has two uses: as a notice or attribute meaning \"smoking prohibited\" (店内は禁煙です, 禁煙席, 全席禁煙) and as an intransitive する-verb meaning \"to quit smoking\" (去年から禁煙している, 禁煙中). The counterpart is 喫煙 \"smoking\" (喫煙席, 喫煙所). In conversation, for quitting you can also simply say タバコをやめる; 禁煙 sounds somewhat firmer and more determined.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 17"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"健康のために断酒しています。","romaji":"Kenkō no tame ni danshu shiteimasu.","german":"I'm abstaining from alcohol for my health.","furigana":"けんこう|だんしゅ"},{"japanese":"断酒して三年になります。","romaji":"Danshu shite sannen ni narimasu.","german":"It has been three years since I gave up drinking.","furigana":"だんしゅ|さんねん"},{"japanese":"医者に断酒を勧められました。","romaji":"Isha ni danshu o susumeraremashita.","german":"The doctor advised me to give up drinking completely.","furigana":"いしゃ|だんしゅ|すす"}],"notes":"断酒 means giving up alcohol completely and permanently, and is often used in connection with alcohol dependence (断酒会 \"self-help group for alcoholics\"). As an intransitive する-verb: 断酒する, 断酒している, 断酒を続ける. The similar 禁酒 can also refer to temporary abstinence or a ban on alcohol; 断酒 emphasizes the final break. In daily life one usually simply says お酒をやめる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"偏食はよくないです。","romaji":"Henshoku wa yokunai desu.","german":"Picky eating is not good.","furigana":"へんしょく"},{"japanese":"息子は偏食がひどくて、野菜をほとんど食べません。","romaji":"Musuko wa henshoku ga hidokute, yasai o hotondo tabemasen.","german":"My son is a terribly picky eater and hardly eats any vegetables.","furigana":"むすこ|へんしょく|やさい|た"},{"japanese":"一人暮らしを始めてから偏食になりがちです。","romaji":"Hitorigurashi o hajimete kara henshoku ni narigachi desu.","german":"Since I started living alone, I tend to eat an unbalanced diet.","furigana":"ひとりぐ|はじ|へんしょく"}],"notes":"偏食 refers to picky, one-sided eating: one eats only what one likes and leaves the rest - said especially often of children. Typical are 偏食がひどい, 偏食の子ども, 偏食を直す; as an intransitive する-verb 偏食する. More colloquial is 好き嫌いが多い \"to have many food dislikes\"; for the unbalanced diet itself one also says 栄養が偏る.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"過労で倒れました。","romaji":"Karō de taoremashita.","german":"He collapsed from overwork.","furigana":"かろう|たお"},{"japanese":"過労死は日本の大きな社会問題です。","romaji":"Karoushi wa Nihon no ookina shakai mondai desu.","german":"Death from overwork is a major social problem in Japan.","furigana":"かろうし|にほん|おお|しゃかいもんだい"},{"japanese":"最近、過労気味なので少し休みを取ります。","romaji":"Saikin, karou gimi na node sukoshi yasumi o torimasu.","german":"I've been a bit overworked lately, so I'll take some time off.","furigana":"さいきん|かろうぎみ|すこ|やす|と"}],"notes":"過労 is exhaustion from too much work. Typical are 過労で倒れる \"to collapse from overwork\", 過労がたまる and 過労気味 \"somewhat overworked\"; well known is 過労死 \"death from overwork\". 過労 is not a する-verb; \"to overwork oneself\" is 働きすぎる or 無理をする. General tiredness is 疲労 or 疲れ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"燃え尽き症候群になりました。","romaji":"Moetsuki shōkōgun ni narimashita.","german":"I got burnout syndrome.","furigana":"も|つ|しょうこうぐん"},{"japanese":"大会が終わって、燃え尽きたような気分です。","romaji":"Taikai ga owatte, moetsukita you na kibun desu.","german":"Now that the tournament is over, I feel completely burned out.","furigana":"たいかい|お|も|つ|きぶん"},{"japanese":"受験の後に燃え尽きてしまう学生も多いです。","romaji":"Juken no ato ni moetsukite shimau gakusei mo ooi desu.","german":"Many students burn out after entrance exams.","furigana":"じゅけん|あと|も|つ|がくせい|おお"}],"notes":"燃え尽き is the noun form of 燃え尽きる \"to burn up completely; to burn out\" (intransitive). For burnout it is almost always in the compound 燃え尽き症候群; besides that one says verbally 燃え尽きてしまう or 燃え尽きたような気分. It is also used for the emptiness after a big goal, for example for athletes after a tournament or students after entrance exams. The loanword バーンアウト is also in use.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"花粉症がつらいです。","romaji":"Kafunshō ga tsurai desu.","german":"My hay fever is bad.","furigana":"かふんしょう"},{"japanese":"去年、急に花粉症になりました。","romaji":"Kyonen, kyuu ni kafunshou ni narimashita.","german":"I suddenly developed hay fever last year.","furigana":"きょねん|きゅう|かふんしょう"},{"japanese":"花粉症の季節は、外に出るときマスクをします。","romaji":"Kafunshou no kisetsu wa, soto ni deru toki masuku o shimasu.","german":"During hay fever season, I wear a mask when I go outside.","furigana":"かふんしょう|きせつ|そと|で"}],"notes":"花粉症 is pollen allergy, in Japan mainly to cedar pollen (スギ花粉) in spring. One says 花粉症だ / 花粉症がある \"to have hay fever\", 花粉症になる \"to develop hay fever\" and 花粉症がつらい / ひどい. Typical symptoms are くしゃみ, 鼻水 and 目がかゆい. 花粉 on its own is \"pollen\" (花粉が飛ぶ, 花粉情報). Not a する-verb.","levelNote":"Compound: 花粉 (N1) + 症 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"熱中症に注意してください。","romaji":"Necchūshō ni chūi shite kudasai.","german":"Please watch out for heatstroke.","furigana":"ねっちゅうしょう|ちゅうい"},{"japanese":"こまめに水を飲んで、熱中症を防ぎましょう。","romaji":"Komame ni mizu o nonde, necchuushou o fusegimashou.","german":"Let's drink water frequently to prevent heatstroke.","furigana":"みず|の|ねっちゅうしょう|ふせ"},{"japanese":"部活の練習中に、生徒が熱中症で倒れました。","romaji":"Bukatsu no renshuuchuu ni, seito ga necchuushou de taoremashita.","german":"During club practice, a student collapsed from heatstroke.","furigana":"ぶかつ|れんしゅうちゅう|せいと|ねっちゅうしょう|たお"}],"notes":"熱中症 is the umbrella term for heat-related illnesses, from heatstroke to heat exhaustion; in the Japanese summer one hears it daily in the news. Typical are 熱中症になる, 熱中症で倒れる / 運ばれる, 熱中症に注意 and 熱中症対策. The 中 here is in the sense of \"being hit\" (熱に中る) - the word has nothing to do with 熱中する \"to be enthusiastic about something\". The older 日射病 refers specifically to sunstroke.","levelNote":"Compound: 熱中 (N3) + 症 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"凍傷にならないように気をつけます。","romaji":"Tōshō ni naranai yō ni ki o tsukemasu.","german":"I'll be careful not to get frostbite.","furigana":"とうしょう|き"},{"japanese":"登山中に足の指が凍傷になりました。","romaji":"Tozanchuu ni ashi no yubi ga toushou ni narimashita.","german":"My toes got frostbitten while mountain climbing.","furigana":"とざんちゅう|あし|ゆび|とうしょう"},{"japanese":"軽い凍傷なら、ぬるいお湯でゆっくり温めます。","romaji":"Karui toushou nara, nurui oyu de yukkuri atatamemasu.","german":"For mild frostbite, warm the area slowly with lukewarm water.","furigana":"かる|とうしょう|ゆ|あたた"}],"notes":"凍傷 is frostbite, i.e. tissue damage from severe cold. One says 凍傷になる or 凍傷にかかる, often with a body part: 指が凍傷になる. Milder, everyday cold damage is called しもやけ \"chilblains\". The word is neutral to medical and is encountered mainly in mountaineering, winter sports and cold snaps.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"脱水症状に気をつけてください。","romaji":"Dassui shōjō ni ki o tsukete kudasai.","german":"Please watch out for dehydration.","furigana":"だっすいしょうじょう|き"},{"japanese":"下痢が続いて、脱水症状を起こしました。","romaji":"Geri ga tsuzuite, dassui shoujou o okoshimashita.","german":"I had persistent diarrhea and became dehydrated.","furigana":"げり|つづ|だっすいしょうじょう|お"},{"japanese":"お年寄りは、のどが渇かなくても脱水症状になることがあります。","romaji":"Otoshiyori wa, nodo ga kawakanakute mo dassui shoujou ni naru koto ga arimasu.","german":"Elderly people can become dehydrated even if they don't feel thirsty.","furigana":"としよ|かわ|だっすいしょうじょう"}],"notes":"脱水症状 denotes the condition or symptoms of dehydration (脱水 \"removal of water\" + 症状 \"symptom\"). Typical are 脱水症状を起こす, 脱水症状になる and 脱水症状が出る, often in connection with heat, fever or diarrhea. 脱水 alone is also used for the spin cycle of a washing machine (脱水する). Neutral to medical.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"著作権を侵害してはいけません。","romaji":"Chosakuken o shingai shite wa ikemasen.","german":"You must not infringe copyright.","furigana":"ちょさくけん|しんがい"},{"japanese":"この写真の著作権はカメラマンにあります。","romaji":"Kono shashin no chosakuken wa kameraman ni arimasu.","german":"The copyright of this photo belongs to the photographer.","furigana":"しゃしん|ちょさくけん"},{"japanese":"著作権が切れた古い小説は、無料で読めます。","romaji":"Chosakuken ga kireta furui shousetsu wa, muryou de yomemasu.","german":"Old novels whose copyright has expired can be read for free.","furigana":"ちょさくけん|き|ふる|しょうせつ|むりょう|よ"}],"notes":"著作権 is copyright (著作 \"authoring, work\" + 権 \"right\"). Typical collocations are 著作権を侵害する \"to infringe copyright\", 著作権侵害, 著作権がある / 切れる \"to expire\" and 著作権者 \"rights holder\"; on the internet one often reads 著作権フリー. Related legal terms are 特許 \"patent\" and 商標 \"trademark\". Legal, but also common in daily life.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"商標を登録しました。","romaji":"Shōhyō o tōroku shimashita.","german":"We registered the trademark.","furigana":"しょうひょう|とうろく"},{"japanese":"この名前はすでに他の会社の登録商標です。","romaji":"Kono namae wa sude ni hoka no kaisha no touroku shouhyou desu.","german":"This name is already a registered trademark of another company.","furigana":"なまえ|ほか|かいしゃ|とうろくしょうひょう"},{"japanese":"似たロゴを使って、商標権の侵害で訴えられました。","romaji":"Nita rogo o tsukatte, shouhyouken no shingai de uttaeraremashita.","german":"They used a similar logo and were sued for trademark infringement.","furigana":"に|つか|しょうひょうけん|しんがい|うった"}],"notes":"商標 is the trademark in the legal sense. Typical are 商標を登録する, 登録商標 \"registered trademark (®)\" and 商標権 \"trademark right\" (商標権を侵害する). For \"brand\" in the sense of image or label, one says ブランド in daily life, and ロゴ for the pictorial mark. The word belongs to business and legal language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"肖像権に注意してください。","romaji":"Shōzōken ni chūi shite kudasai.","german":"Please be mindful of portrait rights.","furigana":"しょうぞうけん|ちゅうい"},{"japanese":"知らない人の顔が写った写真をSNSに載せると、肖像権の侵害になることがあります。","romaji":"Shiranai hito no kao ga utta shashin o SNS ni noseru to, shouzouken no shingai ni naru koto ga arimasu.","german":"Posting photos that show strangers' faces on social media can violate their portrait rights.","furigana":"し|ひと|かお||しゃしん|の|しょうぞうけん|しんがい"},{"japanese":"芸能人の写真には肖像権があるので、勝手に使えません。","romaji":"Geinoujin no shashin ni wa shouzouken ga aru node, katte ni tsukaemasen.","german":"Photos of celebrities have portrait rights, so you can't just use them freely.","furigana":"げいのうじん|しゃしん|しょうぞうけん|かって|つか"}],"notes":"肖像権 is the right to one's own image (肖像 \"likeness, portrait\" + 権 \"right\"). Typical collocations are 肖像権を侵害する, 肖像権の侵害 and 肖像権に配慮する. The word comes up today mainly when it is about photos of other people on social networks. It is a legal term, but also used in daily life; related are 著作権 and プライバシー.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"個人情報を保護します。","romaji":"Kojin jōhō o hogo shimasu.","german":"We protect personal information.","furigana":"こじんじょうほう|ほご"},{"japanese":"会社から顧客の個人情報が漏れました。","romaji":"Kaisha kara kokyaku no kojin jouhou ga moremashita.","german":"Customers' personal information leaked from the company.","furigana":"かいしゃ|こきゃく|こじんじょうほう|も"},{"japanese":"怪しいサイトに個人情報を入力しないでください。","romaji":"Ayashii saito ni kojin jouhou o nyuuryoku shinaide kudasai.","german":"Don't enter your personal information on suspicious websites.","furigana":"あや|こじんじょうほう|にゅうりょく"}],"notes":"個人情報 is personal data such as name, address or phone number. Typical are 個人情報を守る / 保護する, 個人情報が漏れる \"data gets out\", 個人情報の取り扱い \"handling of personal data\" and the law 個人情報保護法. More general is プライバシー \"privacy\". The word is neutral and constantly encountered on forms and websites.","levelNote":"Compound: 個人 (N3) + 情報 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"守秘義務があります。","romaji":"Shuhi gimu ga arimasu.","german":"I have a duty of confidentiality.","furigana":"しゅひぎむ"},{"japanese":"入社のときに、守秘義務の契約書にサインしました。","romaji":"Nyuusha no toki ni, shuhi gimu no keiyakusho ni sain shimashita.","german":"When I joined the company, I signed a confidentiality agreement.","furigana":"にゅうしゃ|しゅひぎむ|けいやくしょ"},{"japanese":"弁護士は守秘義務を守らなければなりません。","romaji":"Bengoshi wa shuhi gimu o mamoranakereba narimasen.","german":"Lawyers must observe their duty of confidentiality.","furigana":"べんごし|しゅひぎむ|まも"}],"notes":"守秘義務 is the duty of confidentiality or secrecy (守秘 \"to keep secrets\" + 義務 \"duty\"), for example for doctors, lawyers, civil servants or employees. Typical are 守秘義務がある, 守秘義務を守る, 守秘義務に違反する and 守秘義務契約 \"non-disclosure agreement\". In conversation, 守秘義務があるので… serves as a polite reason why one may not say something. Formal, business and legal language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"内部告発で不正が発覚しました。","romaji":"Naibu kokuhatsu de fusei ga hakkaku shimashita.","german":"The misconduct came to light through whistleblowing.","furigana":"ないぶこくはつ|ふせい|はっかく"},{"japanese":"社員の内部告発を受けて、会社は調査を始めました。","romaji":"Shain no naibu kokuhatsu o ukete, kaisha wa chousa o hajimemashita.","german":"Following a whistleblower report from an employee, the company began an investigation.","furigana":"しゃいん|ないぶこくはつ|う|かいしゃ|ちょうさ|はじ"},{"japanese":"内部告発者を守るための法律があります。","romaji":"Naibu kokuhatsusha o mamoru tame no houritsu ga arimasu.","german":"There is a law to protect whistleblowers.","furigana":"ないぶこくはつしゃ|まも|ほうりつ"}],"notes":"内部告発 is the exposure of wrongdoing by insiders (内部 \"internal\" + 告発 \"accusation, exposure\"). Typical are 内部告発で発覚する \"to come to light through a whistleblower\", 内部告発を受けて調査する and 内部告発者 \"whistleblower\"; occasionally also as a する-verb (不正を内部告発する). 告発 alone means \"to file a criminal complaint\" or \"to denounce\". News and business language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"談合は違法です。","romaji":"Dangō wa ihō desu.","german":"Bid rigging is illegal.","furigana":"だんごう|いほう"},{"japanese":"建設会社が公共工事の入札で談合していました。","romaji":"Kensetsu gaisha ga koukyou kouji no nyuusatsu de dangou shite imashita.","german":"The construction companies had colluded on bids for public works.","furigana":"けんせつがいしゃ|こうきょうこうじ|にゅうさつ|だんごう"},{"japanese":"談合が発覚して、社長が謝罪しました。","romaji":"Dangou ga hakkaku shite, shachou ga shazai shimashita.","german":"After the bid rigging came to light, the company president apologized.","furigana":"だんごう|はっかく|しゃちょう|しゃざい"}],"notes":"談合 today is mainly the prohibited price-fixing, especially in public tenders (入札談合). Typical are 談合する (intransitive), 談合が発覚する, 談合に加わる and 官製談合 \"collusion orchestrated by government agencies\". The older meaning \"consultation, discussion\" is obsolete; for ordinary arrangements one says 打ち合わせ or 話し合い. News language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"政治家と企業の癒着が問題です。","romaji":"Seijika to kigyō no yuchaku ga mondai desu.","german":"The cozy ties between politicians and companies are a problem.","furigana":"せいじか|きぎょう|ゆちゃく|もんだい"},{"japanese":"警察官が暴力団と癒着していたことが分かりました。","romaji":"Keisatsukan ga bouryokudan to yuchaku shite ita koto ga wakarimashita.","german":"It came out that a police officer was in cahoots with the yakuza.","furigana":"けいさつかん|ぼうりょくだん|ゆちゃく|わ"},{"japanese":"役所と業者の癒着を防ぐため、担当者を定期的に変えています。","romaji":"Yakusho to gyousha no yuchaku o fusegu tame, tantousha o teikiteki ni kaete imasu.","german":"To prevent collusion between the government office and contractors, the people in charge are rotated regularly.","furigana":"やくしょ|ぎょうしゃ|ゆちゃく|ふせ|たんとうしゃ|ていきてき|か"}],"notes":"癒着 has two meanings: medically \"adhesion\" (e.g. after an operation) and figuratively an \"improper, entangled connection\" between politics, government offices and companies. In the second sense it is AとBの癒着 or, as an intransitive する-verb, Aと癒着する \"to be in cahoots with A\". The word is clearly negative and typical of the news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"天下りが批判されています。","romaji":"Amakudari ga hihan sareteimasu.","german":"Amakudari is being criticized.","furigana":"あまくだ|ひはん"},{"japanese":"退職した官僚が、業界団体に天下りしました。","romaji":"Taishoku shita kanryou ga, gyoukai dantai ni amakudari shimashita.","german":"A retired senior bureaucrat took a post at an industry association.","furigana":"たいしょく|かんりょう|ぎょうかいだんたい|あまくだ"},{"japanese":"政府は天下りを規制する新しいルールを作りました。","romaji":"Seifu wa amakudari o kisei suru atarashii ruuru o tsukurimashita.","german":"The government made new rules to restrict amakudari.","furigana":"せいふ|あまくだ|きせい|あたら|つく"}],"notes":"天下り literally means \"descending from heaven\" (天 + 下る), originally said of gods. Today it refers almost exclusively to the move of retired senior civil servants to well-paid posts in companies or associations within their former area of responsibility. Typical are 天下りする, 天下り先 \"destination post\" and 天下りを規制する; the word has a strongly critical tone. More rarely it denotes orders from above (天下り人事).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"裏金問題が発覚しました。","romaji":"Uragane mondai ga hakkaku shimashita.","german":"A slush fund scandal came to light.","furigana":"うらがねもんだい|はっかく"},{"japanese":"その会社は、税金を払わないために裏金を作っていました。","romaji":"Sono kaisha wa, zeikin o harawanai tame ni uragane o tsukutte imashita.","german":"That company was creating a slush fund in order not to pay taxes.","furigana":"かいしゃ|ぜいきん|はら|うらがね|つく"},{"japanese":"選手の獲得に裏金が使われたと報道されました。","romaji":"Senshu no kakutoku ni uragane ga tsukawareta to houdou saremashita.","german":"It was reported that under-the-table money was used to sign players.","furigana":"せんしゅ|かくとく|うらがね|つか|ほうどう"}],"notes":"裏金 is money that is managed covertly and outside the official books - a \"slush fund\" - and often also money for bribery (裏 \"back side, hidden\" + 金 \"money\"). Typical are 裏金を作る, 裏金を渡す and 裏金問題 / 裏金事件. Unlike 賄賂, the concrete bribe money given to a person, 裏金 emphasizes the hiding of the money. News language.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"賄賂を受け取りました。","romaji":"Wairo o uketorimashita.","german":"He accepted a bribe.","furigana":"わいろ|う|と"},{"japanese":"役人に賄賂を渡して、許可をもらおうとしました。","romaji":"Yakunin ni wairo o watashite, kyoka o moraou to shimashita.","german":"He tried to get the permit by handing a bribe to an official.","furigana":"やくにん|わいろ|わた|きょか"},{"japanese":"この国では、賄賂なしでは何も進まないと言われています。","romaji":"Kono kuni de wa, wairo nashi de wa nani mo susumanai to iwarete imasu.","german":"It is said that in this country nothing moves without bribes.","furigana":"くに|わいろ|なに|すす|い"}],"notes":"賄賂 is the bribe money or gift itself. Typical are 賄賂を贈る / 渡す \"to bribe\" and 賄賂を受け取る / もらう \"to accept a bribe\". The offenses are legally called 贈賄 \"active bribery\" and 収賄 \"passive bribery (accepting bribes)\". Because of the difficult kanji, the word is often written わいろ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"市長に陳情しました。","romaji":"Shichō ni chinjō shimashita.","german":"We petitioned the mayor.","furigana":"しちょう|ちんじょう"},{"japanese":"住民たちは、信号の設置を市に陳情しました。","romaji":"Juumintachi wa, shingou no secchi o shi ni chinjou shimashita.","german":"The residents petitioned the city to install a traffic light.","furigana":"じゅうみん|しんごう|せっち|し|ちんじょう"},{"japanese":"地元の団体が、陳情のために東京の省庁を訪れました。","romaji":"Jimoto no dantai ga, chinjou no tame ni Toukyou no shouchou o otozuremashita.","german":"A local organization visited ministries in Tokyo to present its petitions.","furigana":"じもと|だんたい|ちんじょう|とうきょう|しょうちょう|おとず"}],"notes":"陳情 means presenting a concern to a government office or politician and asking for a remedy, often in person (陳 \"to state\" + 情 \"circumstances\"). As a する-verb, the addressee takes に and the concern takes を: 市に信号の設置を陳情する; also 陳情書 and 陳情団. The more formal 請願 is a legally regulated, written petition procedure. Administrative and news language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"請願書を提出しました。","romaji":"Seigansho o teishutsu shimashita.","german":"I submitted a written petition.","furigana":"せいがんしょ|ていしゅつ"},{"japanese":"市民団体が、一万人の署名を集めて国会に請願しました。","romaji":"Shimin dantai ga, ichimannin no shomei o atsumete kokkai ni seigan shimashita.","german":"A citizens' group collected ten thousand signatures and petitioned the Diet.","furigana":"しみんだんたい|いちまんにん|しょめい|あつ|こっかい|せいがん"},{"japanese":"その請願は、議会で採択されました。","romaji":"Sono seigan wa, gikai de saitaku saremashita.","german":"The petition was adopted by the assembly.","furigana":"せいがん|ぎかい|さいたく"}],"notes":"請願 is a formal petition to parliament or authorities under fixed rules (請願権, 請願法), usually in writing and often with collected signatures (署名). Typical are 請願書を提出する, 〜に請願する and 請願を採択する \"to adopt a petition\". Compared with 陳情, the rather personal presenting of a concern, 請願 is the more official procedure. Very formal, written language.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"民意を反映します。","romaji":"Min'i o han'ei shimasu.","german":"We reflect the will of the people.","furigana":"みんい|はんえい"},{"japanese":"選挙の結果は、民意の表れだと言えます。","romaji":"Senkyo no kekka wa, min'i no araware da to iemasu.","german":"The election result can be seen as an expression of the will of the people.","furigana":"せんきょ|けっか|みんい|あらわ|い"},{"japanese":"首相は国会を解散して、民意を問うことにしました。","romaji":"Shushou wa kokkai o kaisan shite, min'i o tou koto ni shimashita.","german":"The prime minister decided to dissolve the Diet and ask the people for their verdict.","furigana":"しゅしょう|こっかい|かいさん|みんい|と"}],"notes":"民意 is the will of the people as a political quantity. Typical are 民意を反映する \"to reflect the will of the people\", 民意を問う \"to let the people decide\" (for example through new elections), 民意に従う / 背く and 民意が示される. Similar is 世論 \"public opinion\", which refers more to polls and moods; 民意 sounds more fundamental. Political and news language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"公聴会が開かれました。","romaji":"Kōchōkai ga hirakaremashita.","german":"A public hearing was held.","furigana":"こうちょうかい|ひら"},{"japanese":"新しい空港の計画について、住民が公聴会で意見を述べました。","romaji":"Atarashii kuukou no keikaku ni tsuite, juumin ga kouchoukai de iken o nobemashita.","german":"Residents voiced their opinions at the public hearing on the plans for the new airport.","furigana":"あたら|くうこう|けいかく|じゅうみん|こうちょうかい|いけん|の"},{"japanese":"予算について専門家の意見を聞くため、国会で公聴会を開く予定です。","romaji":"Yosan ni tsuite senmonka no iken o kiku tame, kokkai de kouchoukai o hiraku yotei desu.","german":"A public hearing is planned in the Diet to hear experts' opinions on the budget.","furigana":"よさん|せんもんか|いけん|き|こっかい|こうちょうかい|ひら|よてい"}],"notes":"公聴会 is a public hearing organized by parliament or government offices to hear the opinions of experts and citizens (公 \"public\" + 聴 \"to listen\" + 会 \"meeting\"). Typical are 公聴会を開く / 開催する, 公聴会が開かれる and 公聴会で意見を述べる. At a 説明会, by contrast, the authority itself explains its plans. Formal, administrative language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"首相の答弁を聞きました。","romaji":"Shushō no tōben o kikimashita.","german":"I listened to the prime minister's reply.","furigana":"しゅしょう|とうべん|き"},{"japanese":"大臣は野党の質問に答弁しました。","romaji":"Daijin wa yatou no shitsumon ni touben shimashita.","german":"The minister replied to the opposition's questions.","furigana":"だいじん|やとう|しつもん|とうべん"},{"japanese":"あいまいな答弁が続き、議員たちは怒りました。","romaji":"Aimai na touben ga tsuzuki, giintachi wa okorimashita.","german":"Vague answers continued, and the members got angry.","furigana":"とうべん|つづ|ぎいん|おこ"}],"notes":"答弁 is the answer to questions in an official session, above all the government's reply to interpellations by members of parliament (国会答弁); in court also the defense's rebuttal. As an intransitive する-verb, the question takes に: 質問に答弁する; also 答弁に立つ, 答弁を求める and 答弁書. For ordinary answers in daily life one says 答え, 返事 or 回答. Political and news language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"法案の採決が行われました。","romaji":"Hōan no saiketsu ga okonawaremashita.","german":"The vote on the bill was held.","furigana":"ほうあん|さいけつ|おこな"},{"japanese":"それでは、この案について採決をとります。","romaji":"Sore de wa, kono an ni tsuite saiketsu o torimasu.","german":"Then, let's take a vote on this proposal.","furigana":"あん|さいけつ"},{"japanese":"与党は野党の反対を押し切って、法案を強行採決しました。","romaji":"Yotou wa yatou no hantai o oshikitte, houan o kyoukou saiketsu shimashita.","german":"The ruling party forced the bill through a vote over the opposition's objections.","furigana":"よとう|やとう|はんたい|お|き|ほうあん|きょうこうさいけつ"}],"notes":"採決 is the formal vote on a motion or bill in a body. Typical are 採決を行う, 採決をとる, 採決に入る and 強行採決 \"forced vote, ramming something through\"; transitive also 法案を採決する. The result is 可決 \"passed\" or 否決 \"rejected\". For casting ballots in elections one says 投票, and for the principle of majority decision 多数決.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"法案が可決されました。","romaji":"Hōan ga kaketsu saremashita.","german":"The bill was passed.","furigana":"ほうあん|かけつ"},{"japanese":"予算案は賛成多数で可決されました。","romaji":"Yosan'an wa sansei tasuu de kaketsu saremashita.","german":"The budget proposal was passed by a majority vote.","furigana":"よさんあん|さんせいたすう|かけつ"},{"japanese":"クラス会で、文化祭の出し物が全員一致で可決されました。","romaji":"Kurasukai de, bunkasai no dashimono ga zen'in icchi de kaketsu saremashita.","german":"At the class meeting, the cultural festival act was approved unanimously.","furigana":"かい|ぶんかさい|だ|もの|ぜんいんいっち|かけつ"}],"notes":"可決 is the adoption of a motion or bill by vote. It mostly appears in the passive: 法案が可決される; in the active it is 議会が法案を可決する. Typical are 賛成多数で可決 \"adopted by a majority\" and 全会一致で可決 \"adopted unanimously\". The opposite is 否決; the vote itself is called 採決.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"提案が否決されました。","romaji":"Teian ga hiketsu saremashita.","german":"The proposal was rejected.","furigana":"ていあん|ひけつ"},{"japanese":"内閣不信任案は反対多数で否決されました。","romaji":"Naikaku fushinnin'an wa hantai tasuu de hiketsu saremashita.","german":"The no-confidence motion against the cabinet was rejected by a majority.","furigana":"ないかくふしんにんあん|はんたいたすう|ひけつ"},{"japanese":"住民投票で、ダム建設の計画が否決されました。","romaji":"Juumin touhyou de, damu kensetsu no keikaku ga hiketsu saremashita.","german":"In a referendum, the plan to build a dam was rejected.","furigana":"じゅうみんとうひょう|けんせつ|けいかく|ひけつ"}],"notes":"否決 is the rejection of a motion or bill by a vote, the opposite of 可決. Mostly in the passive: 提案が否決される; also 反対多数で否決 and 不信任案が否決される \"the no-confidence motion fails\". In the active: 議会が案を否決する. For personally declining a request one says 断る instead.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい法律が施行されました。","romaji":"Atarashii hōritsu ga shikō saremashita.","german":"The new law came into effect.","furigana":"あたら|ほうりつ|しこう"},{"japanese":"改正法は来年四月一日から施行されます。","romaji":"Kaiseihou wa rainen shigatsu tsuitachi kara shikou saremasu.","german":"The amended law will come into effect on April 1 next year.","furigana":"かいせいほう|らいねんしがつついたち|しこう"},{"japanese":"条例の施行に向けて、市は説明会を開きました。","romaji":"Jourei no shikou ni mukete, shi wa setsumeikai o hirakimashita.","german":"Ahead of the ordinance coming into effect, the city held briefing sessions.","furigana":"じょうれい|しこう|む|し|せつめいかい|ひら"}],"notes":"施行 is the putting into force of a law or ordinance. It mostly appears in the passive: 法律が施行される \"a law takes effect\"; also 施行日 and 〜から施行. Legal professionals often read it せこう to distinguish it from 試行 \"trial run\" (しこう); not to be confused with 施工 \"construction work\". Administrative and news language.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"法律が改正されました。","romaji":"Hōritsu ga kaisei saremashita.","german":"The law was revised.","furigana":"ほうりつ|かいせい"},{"japanese":"三月にダイヤ改正があって、電車の時間が変わりました。","romaji":"Sangatsu ni daiya kaisei ga atte, densha no jikan ga kawarimashita.","german":"There was a timetable revision in March, and the train times changed.","furigana":"さんがつ|かいせい|でんしゃ|じかん|か"},{"japanese":"憲法改正について、国民の意見は分かれています。","romaji":"Kenpou kaisei ni tsuite, kokumin no iken wa wakarete imasu.","german":"Public opinion is divided on constitutional revision.","furigana":"けんぽうかいせい|こくみん|いけん|わ"}],"notes":"改正 is the official change of laws, rules, timetables or fares. Typical are 法律を改正する, 憲法改正, ダイヤ改正 \"timetable change\" and 料金改正 \"fare revision\" (often a euphemistic increase). Similar is 改定 \"re-setting\" of prices or rules; 修正 is the correction of a draft, 改善 the improvement of a condition. Formal.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"規制を撤廃します。","romaji":"Kisei o teppai shimasu.","german":"We will abolish the regulation.","furigana":"きせい|てっぱい"},{"japanese":"多くの国が関税の撤廃に合意しました。","romaji":"Ooku no kuni ga kanzei no teppai ni goui shimashita.","german":"Many countries agreed to the abolition of tariffs.","furigana":"おお|くに|かんぜい|てっぱい|ごうい"},{"japanese":"この学校では、髪の色に関する校則が撤廃されました。","romaji":"Kono gakkou de wa, kami no iro ni kansuru kousoku ga teppai saremashita.","german":"At this school, the rule on hair color was abolished.","furigana":"がっこう|かみ|いろ|かん|こうそく|てっぱい"}],"notes":"撤廃 is the complete abolition of rules, restrictions or discrimination. Typical are 規制を撤廃する, 関税撤廃 \"abolition of tariffs\", 差別撤廃 and 制限を撤廃する. The more general 廃止 is also used for institutions, bus lines or laws; 撤回 is the withdrawal of a statement or plan. Formal, news language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"サービスを拡充します。","romaji":"Sābisu o kakujū shimasu.","german":"We will expand our services.","furigana":"かくじゅう"},{"japanese":"市は保育園を拡充する計画を発表しました。","romaji":"Shi wa hoikuen o kakujuu suru keikaku o happyou shimashita.","german":"The city announced a plan to expand daycare centers.","furigana":"し|ほいくえん|かくじゅう|けいかく|はっぴょう"},{"japanese":"子育て支援の拡充を求める声が高まっています。","romaji":"Kosodate shien no kakujuu o motomeru koe ga takamatte imasu.","german":"Calls for expanded child-rearing support are growing.","furigana":"こそだ|しえん|かくじゅう|もと|こえ|たか"}],"notes":"拡充 means \"to expand and improve\" (拡 \"enlarge\" + 充 \"fill up\") - mostly of services, facilities, support programs or offerings. Typical are 制度を拡充する, 支援の拡充 and サービスを拡充する. 拡大 only stresses the enlargement of scope, 充実 only the fullness of content; 拡充 combines both. Formal, typical of authorities and companies.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"東西文化の融合です。","romaji":"Tōzai bunka no yūgō desu.","german":"It is a fusion of Eastern and Western cultures.","furigana":"とうざいぶんか|ゆうごう"},{"japanese":"この料理は、和食とイタリア料理がうまく融合しています。","romaji":"Kono ryouri wa, washoku to Itaria ryouri ga umaku yuugou shite imasu.","german":"In this dish, Japanese and Italian cuisine blend well together.","furigana":"りょうり|わしょく|りょうり|ゆうごう"},{"japanese":"太陽のエネルギーは核融合によって生まれます。","romaji":"Taiyou no enerugii wa kakuyuugou ni yotte umaremasu.","german":"The sun's energy comes from nuclear fusion.","furigana":"たいよう|かくゆうごう|う"}],"notes":"融合 is the merging of different things into a unity. As an intransitive する-verb it is AとBが融合する, transitive AとBを融合させる; common are 文化の融合, 伝統と現代の融合 and 核融合 \"nuclear fusion\". Note: a company merger is not 融合 but 合併. Rather elevated, neutral style.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"環境を考慮します。","romaji":"Kankyō o kōryo shimasu.","german":"I take the environment into consideration.","furigana":"かんきょう|こうりょ"},{"japanese":"天気を考慮して、出発を一日遅らせました。","romaji":"Tenki o kouryo shite, shuppatsu o ichinichi okurasemashita.","german":"Taking the weather into account, we postponed our departure by a day.","furigana":"てんき|こうりょ|しゅっぱつ|いちにちおく"},{"japanese":"給料は経験や能力を考慮に入れて決めます。","romaji":"Kyuuryou wa keiken ya nouryoku o kouryo ni irete kimemasu.","german":"Salary is determined taking experience and ability into account.","furigana":"きゅうりょう|けいけん|のうりょく|こうりょ|い|き"}],"notes":"考慮 is taking circumstances into account when making a decision. Typical are 〜を考慮する, 〜を考慮に入れる \"to take into consideration\", 〜を考慮して \"taking ... into account\" and 考慮の上 \"after careful consideration\". 配慮, by contrast, is consideration for the feelings or needs of others, and 検討 is examining a matter. Formal, rather written language.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"予算の配分を決めます。","romaji":"Yosan no haibun o kimemasu.","german":"I will decide the budget allocation.","furigana":"よさん|はいぶん|き"},{"japanese":"試験では時間配分が大切です。","romaji":"Shiken de wa jikan haibun ga taisetsu desu.","german":"Time allocation is important in exams.","furigana":"しけん|じかんはいぶん|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"マラソンはペース配分を間違えると、最後まで走れません。","romaji":"Marason wa peesu haibun o machigaeru to, saigo made hashiremasen.","german":"In a marathon, if you pace yourself wrongly, you can't run to the end.","furigana":"はいぶん|まちが|さいご|はし"}],"notes":"配分 is the distribution of something by shares: budget, profit, points, or also time and energy. Typical are 予算を配分する, 利益の配分, 時間配分 \"time management\" (e.g. in exams) and ペース配分 \"pacing\". 分配 is similar, but stresses dividing up profit or assets; 割り当て is the concrete assignment to people, 配る the physical handing out.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仕事の割り当てをします。","romaji":"Shigoto no wariate o shimasu.","german":"I assign the work.","furigana":"しごと|わ|あ"},{"japanese":"一人あたりの割り当ては五千円です。","romaji":"Hitori atari no wariate wa gosen en desu.","german":"The allotment per person is 5,000 yen.","furigana":"ひとり|わ|あ|ごせんえん"},{"japanese":"新人に割り当てられた席は窓の近くでした。","romaji":"Shinjin ni wariaterareta seki wa mado no chikaku deshita.","german":"The seat assigned to the newcomer was near the window.","furigana":"しんじん|わ|あ|せき|まど|ちか"}],"notes":"割り当て is the allotment of tasks, shares or quantities, also in the sense of a quota (輸入割り当て \"import quota\"). It is the noun form of the transitive verb 割り当てる: 仕事を新人に割り当てる; passive 割り当てられた仕事. In official texts, 割当 often appears without okurigana. 分担, by contrast, stresses dividing something among each other.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"試行錯誤を重ねて、やっと成功した。","romaji":"Shikō sakugo o kasanete, yatto seikō shita.","german":"After repeated trial and error, I finally succeeded.","furigana":"しこうさくご|かさ|せいこう"},{"japanese":"プログラミングは試行錯誤の連続だ。","romaji":"Puroguramingu wa shikō sakugo no renzoku da.","german":"Programming is a series of trial and error.","furigana":"しこうさくご|れんぞく"},{"japanese":"母は試行錯誤しながら、新しいカレーの作り方を考えた。","romaji":"Haha wa shikō sakugo shinagara, atarashii karē no tsukurikata o kangaeta.","german":"My mother worked out a new way to make curry through trial and error.","furigana":"はは|しこうさくご|あたら|つく|かた|かんが"}],"notes":"試行 \"trial\" and 錯誤 \"error\" together make \"trial and error\": one tries things, makes mistakes and thus approaches the solution. Typical are 試行錯誤を重ねる \"to make many attempts\", 試行錯誤の末に \"after much trial and error\" and, as a する-verb, 試行錯誤する or 試行錯誤しながら. Neutral to slightly formal; frequent in reports on development, research and cooking.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"創意工夫を凝らします。","romaji":"Sōi kufū o korashimasu.","german":"I put in originality and ingenuity.","furigana":"そういくふう|こ"},{"japanese":"子どもたちは、創意工夫して段ボールでロボットを作りました。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa, soui kufuu shite danbooru de robotto o tsukurimashita.","german":"The children used a lot of ingenuity to build a robot out of cardboard boxes.","furigana":"こ|そういくふう|だん|つく"},{"japanese":"少ない予算でも、創意工夫次第でいいイベントができます。","romaji":"Sukunai yosan demo, soui kufuu shidai de ii ibento ga dekimasu.","german":"Even with a small budget, a good event is possible, depending on ingenuity.","furigana":"すく|よさん|そういくふうしだい"}],"notes":"創意工夫 is a yojijukugo from 創意 \"original idea\" and 工夫 \"tinkering, clever solution\": creative resourcefulness with which one makes something good out of little. Typical are 創意工夫を凝らす \"to put a lot of ingenuity into it\", 創意工夫する, 創意工夫が見られる and 創意工夫次第で \"depending on ingenuity\". Popular in schools, companies and competitions; neutral to slightly formal.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"即興で演奏します。","romaji":"Sokkyō de ensō shimasu.","german":"I play by improvisation.","furigana":"そっきょう|えんそう"},{"japanese":"彼は結婚式で、即興の詩を読みました。","romaji":"Kare wa kekkonshiki de, sokkyou no shi o yomimashita.","german":"At the wedding he recited an improvised poem.","furigana":"かれ|けっこんしき|そっきょう|し|よ"},{"japanese":"即興劇のクラスで、人前で話すことに慣れました。","romaji":"Sokkyougeki no kurasu de, hitomae de hanasu koto ni naremashita.","german":"In the improv theater class, I got used to speaking in front of people.","furigana":"そっきょうげき|ひとまえ|はな|な"}],"notes":"即興 is improvisation, that is, doing something off the cuff without preparation. Typical are 即興で演奏する / 歌う / 作る, 即興の詩, 即興演奏 and 即興劇 \"improv theater\". Colloquially, especially in acting, one also says アドリブ. Do not confuse with 即席 \"instant, quickly prepared\" (即席ラーメン).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"旅程を確認します。","romaji":"Ryotei o kakunin shimasu.","german":"I'll check the itinerary.","furigana":"りょてい|かくにん"},{"japanese":"台風のため、旅程を一日短くしました。","romaji":"Taifuu no tame, ryotei o ichinichi mijikaku shimashita.","german":"Because of the typhoon, we shortened the itinerary by one day.","furigana":"たいふう|りょてい|いちにちみじか"},{"japanese":"旅行会社から詳しい旅程表が届きました。","romaji":"Ryokou gaisha kara kuwashii ryoteihyou ga todokimashita.","german":"A detailed itinerary arrived from the travel agency.","furigana":"りょこうがいしゃ|くわ|りょていひょう|とど"}],"notes":"旅程 is the travel plan over several days with places and dates. Typical are 旅程を立てる / 組む, 旅程を変更する and 旅程表 \"itinerary (document)\". 行程 means more the route or stage to be covered, 日程 a schedule in general, also for appointments. 旅程 sounds somewhat written; in conversation one often says 旅行の予定 or スケジュール.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日の行程は長いです。","romaji":"Kyō no kōtei wa nagai desu.","german":"Today's journey is long.","furigana":"きょう|こうてい|なが"},{"japanese":"山頂までは片道四時間の行程です。","romaji":"Sanchou made wa katamichi yojikan no koutei desu.","german":"It is a four-hour trek one way to the summit.","furigana":"さんちょう|かたみちよじかん|こうてい"},{"japanese":"ツアーの全行程を無事に終えました。","romaji":"Tsuaa no zenkoutei o buji ni oemashita.","german":"We completed the entire tour safely.","furigana":"ぜんこうてい|ぶじ|お"}],"notes":"行程 is the distance or stage to be covered on a trip, hike or tour, often with a time or distance specified: 片道三時間の行程, 一日の行程, 全行程. 行程を終える and 行程を組む go with it. Do not confuse with the identically read 工程 \"work step, manufacturing process\". 旅程, by contrast, is the travel plan as a whole.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"入国審査に並びます。","romaji":"Nyūkoku shinsa ni narabimasu.","german":"I get in line for immigration.","furigana":"にゅうこくしんさ|なら"},{"japanese":"入国審査で滞在の目的を聞かれました。","romaji":"Nyuukoku shinsa de taizai no mokuteki o kikaremashita.","german":"At immigration, I was asked the purpose of my stay.","furigana":"にゅうこくしんさ|たいざい|もくてき|き"},{"japanese":"入国審査を通ったら、次は税関です。","romaji":"Nyuukoku shinsa o tottara, tsugi wa zeikan desu.","german":"After getting through immigration, customs is next.","furigana":"にゅうこくしんさ||つぎ|ぜいかん"}],"notes":"入国審査 is the entry or passport control at an airport or port. Typical are 入国審査を受ける, 入国審査を通る / 通過する, 入国審査に並ぶ and 入国審査官 \"immigration officer\". The counterpart on departure is 出国審査; customs is separate, 税関, and the health inspection is 検疫. Neutral, travel language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"免税店で買い物をします。","romaji":"Menzeiten de kaimono o shimasu.","german":"I shop at a duty-free store.","furigana":"めんぜいてん|か|もの"},{"japanese":"外国人観光客は、パスポートを見せると免税で買い物ができます。","romaji":"Gaikokujin kankoukyaku wa, pasupooto o miseru to menzei de kaimono ga dekimasu.","german":"Foreign tourists can shop tax-free by showing their passport.","furigana":"がいこくじんかんこうきゃく|み|めんぜい|か|もの"},{"japanese":"この商品は免税の対象外です。","romaji":"Kono shouhin wa menzei no taishougai desu.","german":"This product is not eligible for tax exemption.","furigana":"しょうひん|めんぜい|たいしょうがい"}],"notes":"免税 is exemption from taxes or customs duties. Typical are 免税店 \"duty-free shop\", 免税品, 免税手続き and 免税で買う; in Japanese shops, the \"Tax-free\" sign indicates the tax waiver for tourists. Do not confuse it with 減税 \"tax cut\" and 非課税 \"not taxable by law\". Neutral.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"海外に送金します。","romaji":"Kaigai ni sōkin shimasu.","german":"I will send money abroad.","furigana":"かいがい|そうきん"},{"japanese":"毎月、国の家族に送金しています。","romaji":"Maitsuki, kuni no kazoku ni soukin shite imasu.","german":"Every month I send money to my family back home.","furigana":"まいつき|くに|かぞく|そうきん"},{"japanese":"スマホのアプリで、友達にすぐ送金できます。","romaji":"Sumaho no apuri de, tomodachi ni sugu soukin dekimasu.","german":"With a smartphone app, you can send money to friends instantly.","furigana":"ともだち|そうきん"}],"notes":"送金 is sending money to a person or place, often abroad or to family. The recipient takes に: 家族に送金する, 海外に送金する; also 送金手数料 and 海外送金. 振込 is a transfer to a specific bank account, for example for bills; regular support for children who are studying is called 仕送り.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"振込で支払います。","romaji":"Furikomi de shiharaimasu.","german":"I pay by bank transfer.","furigana":"ふりこみ|しはら"},{"japanese":"給料の振込は毎月二十五日です。","romaji":"Kyuuryou no furikomi wa maitsuki nijuugonichi desu.","german":"Salary is transferred on the 25th of every month.","furigana":"きゅうりょう|ふりこみ|まいつきにじゅうごにち"},{"japanese":"振込手数料はお客様のご負担となります。","romaji":"Furikomi tesuuryou wa okyakusama no gofutan to narimasu.","german":"The transfer fee is borne by the customer.","furigana":"ふりこみてすうりょう|きゃくさま|ふたん"}],"notes":"振込 is a transfer to a bank account, for example for bills or salary. The verb is the transitive 振り込む: お金を口座に振り込む. It is written 振込 (as on bank forms) or 振り込み; typical are 振込手数料, 振込先 \"recipient account\" and, politely, お振込み. Also well known is 振り込め詐欺, a fraud involving tricked transfers.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"銀行引き落としにしています。","romaji":"Ginkō hikiotoshi ni shiteimasu.","german":"I pay by bank direct debit.","furigana":"ぎんこうひ|お"},{"japanese":"電気代は毎月二十七日に口座から引き落とされます。","romaji":"Denkidai wa maitsuki nijuushichinichi ni kouza kara hikiotosaremasu.","german":"The electricity bill is debited from my account on the 27th of every month.","furigana":"でんきだい|まいつきにじゅうしちにち|こうざ|ひ|お"},{"japanese":"残高が足りなくて、引き落としができませんでした。","romaji":"Zandaka ga tarinakute, hikiotoshi ga dekimasen deshita.","german":"There wasn't enough balance, so the debit could not be made.","furigana":"ざんだか|た|ひ|お"}],"notes":"引き落とし is the automatic debit from a bank account, for example for electricity, rent or a credit card. The verb is the transitive 引き落とす; one mostly hears the passive 口座から引き落とされる. Typical are 引き落とし日, 自動引き落とし and 〜を引き落としにする \"to pay by direct debit\". With 振込, by contrast, one actively transfers the money oneself.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"定期預金に入れました。","romaji":"Teiki yokin ni iremashita.","german":"I put it into a time deposit.","furigana":"ていきよきん|い"},{"japanese":"定期預金は普通預金より金利が少し高いです。","romaji":"Teiki yokin wa futsuu yokin yori kinri ga sukoshi takai desu.","german":"A time deposit pays slightly higher interest than a regular account.","furigana":"ていきよきん|ふつうよきん|きんり|すこ|たか"},{"japanese":"急にお金が必要になって、定期預金を解約しました。","romaji":"Kyuu ni okane ga hitsuyou ni natte, teiki yokin o kaiyaku shimashita.","german":"I suddenly needed money and closed my time deposit.","furigana":"きゅう|かね|ひつよう|ていきよきん|かいやく"}],"notes":"定期預金 is a time deposit: money placed for a fixed term, which earns somewhat more interest in return. Typical are 定期預金にする / 入れる / 預ける, 定期預金を解約する \"to close\" and 満期 \"maturity\"; often shortened to 定期. The account available at any time is called 普通預金. 貯金 means savings in general.","levelNote":"Compound: 定期 (N3) + 預金 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"住宅ローンを組みました。","romaji":"Jūtaku rōn o kumimashita.","german":"I took out a home loan.","furigana":"じゅうたく|く"},{"japanese":"住宅ローンがあと二十年残っています。","romaji":"Juutaku roon ga ato nijuunen nokotte imasu.","german":"We still have twenty years left on our home loan.","furigana":"じゅうたく|にじゅうねんのこ"},{"japanese":"金利が上がって、住宅ローンの返済が苦しくなりました。","romaji":"Kinri ga agatte, juutaku roon no hensai ga kurushiku narimashita.","german":"Interest rates rose, and repaying the mortgage has become hard.","furigana":"きんり|あ|じゅうたく|へんさい|くる"}],"notes":"住宅ローン is the loan for buying or building an apartment or house. Typical are 住宅ローンを組む \"to take out\", 住宅ローンを返す / 返済する \"to repay\" and 住宅ローンが残っている; in Japan 35-year loans (35年ローン) are common. In conversation one often says just ローン. 借金 means debt in general.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"確定申告をしました。","romaji":"Kakutei shinkoku o shimashita.","german":"I filed my tax return.","furigana":"かくていしんこく"},{"japanese":"フリーランスなので、毎年三月までに確定申告をしなければなりません。","romaji":"Furiiransu na node, maitoshi sangatsu made ni kakutei shinkoku o shinakereba narimasen.","german":"Since I'm a freelancer, I have to file a tax return by March every year.","furigana":"まいとしさんがつ|かくていしんこく"},{"japanese":"医療費が多かったので、確定申告で税金が少し戻ってきました。","romaji":"Iryouhi ga ookatta node, kakutei shinkoku de zeikin ga sukoshi modotte kimashita.","german":"Because I had high medical expenses, I got some tax back through my tax return.","furigana":"いりょうひ|おお|かくていしんこく|ぜいきん|すこ|もど"}],"notes":"確定申告 is the annual income tax return filed with the tax office (税務署), due roughly from February 16 to March 15. Employees usually do not need it because the employer handles the 年末調整 \"year-end tax adjustment\"; it is required for self-employed people, side income, or to get a refund, e.g. for high medical expenses. Typical: 確定申告をする, 確定申告の時期, 確定申告で税金が戻る.","levelNote":"Compound: 確定 (N1) + 申告 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"源泉徴収票をもらいました。","romaji":"Gensen chōshūhyō o moraimashita.","german":"I received my withholding tax slip.","furigana":"げんせんちょうしゅうひょう"},{"japanese":"給料から所得税が源泉徴収されています。","romaji":"Kyuuryou kara shotokuzei ga gensen choushuu sarete imasu.","german":"Income tax is withheld from my salary at the source.","furigana":"きゅうりょう|しょとくぜい|げんせんちょうしゅう"},{"japanese":"原稿料は、一割ほど源泉徴収されて振り込まれます。","romaji":"Genkouryou wa, ichiwari hodo gensen choushuu sarete furikomaremasu.","german":"About ten percent is withheld from the manuscript fee before it is transferred.","furigana":"げんこうりょう|いちわり|げんせんちょうしゅう|ふ|こ"}],"notes":"源泉徴収 is tax deduction at the source: the employer or client withholds the income tax directly from wages or fees and pays it to the government. Typical are 源泉徴収される and 源泉徴収票 \"withholding tax slip\", which one receives at the end of the year; colloquially also just 源泉. The topic is closely connected with 年末調整 and 確定申告. Administrative language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"扶養家族が三人います。","romaji":"Fuyō kazoku ga sannin imasu.","german":"I have three dependents.","furigana":"ふようかぞく|さんにん"},{"japanese":"妻は私の扶養に入っています。","romaji":"Tsuma wa watashi no fuyou ni haitte imasu.","german":"My wife is covered as my dependent.","furigana":"つま|わたし|ふよう|はい"},{"japanese":"年老いた両親を扶養しています。","romaji":"Toshioita ryoushin o fuyou shite imasu.","german":"I am supporting my elderly parents.","furigana":"としお|りょうしん|ふよう"}],"notes":"扶養 is the official and tax term for the financial support of relatives; as a transitive する-verb: 家族を扶養する \"to support one's family\". It is very common in fixed combinations: 扶養家族 \"dependent family members\", 扶養控除 \"dependent deduction\" and 扶養に入る \"to be covered as a dependent (of one's spouse) for insurance and tax purposes\". In everyday conversation one rather says 養う or 面倒を見る; 扶養 sounds like forms, taxes and health insurance.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"医療費控除を受けます。","romaji":"Iryōhi kōjo o ukemasu.","german":"I will claim the medical expense deduction.","furigana":"いりょうひこうじょ|う"},{"japanese":"社会保険料は給料から控除されます。","romaji":"Shakai hokenryou wa kyuuryou kara koujo saremasu.","german":"Social insurance premiums are deducted from your salary.","furigana":"しゃかいほけんりょう|きゅうりょう|こうじょ"},{"japanese":"この費用は税金の控除の対象になりますか。","romaji":"Kono hiyou wa zeikin no koujo no taishou ni narimasu ka.","german":"Is this expense eligible for a tax deduction?","furigana":"ひよう|ぜいきん|こうじょ|たいしょう"}],"notes":"控除 means the \"deduction\" of an amount, especially in tax and payroll matters. Typical are 控除を受ける \"to claim a deduction\" and the passive 給料から控除される \"to be deducted from one's salary\". Many fixed compounds: 基礎控除 \"basic deduction\", 扶養控除, 医療費控除. In everyday speech one rather says 引く or 差し引く for \"to subtract\"; 控除 is the technical term on pay slips and tax forms. Not to be confused with 控訴 \"appeal (in court)\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"還付金が振り込まれました。","romaji":"Kanpukin ga furikomaremashita.","german":"The refund was transferred to my account.","furigana":"かんぷきん|ふ|こ"},{"japanese":"確定申告をすれば、税金の一部が還付されます。","romaji":"Kakutei shinkoku o sureba, zeikin no ichibu ga kanpu saremasu.","german":"If you file a tax return, you will get part of your taxes back.","furigana":"かくていしんこく|ぜいきん|いちぶ|かんぷ"},{"japanese":"還付金詐欺の電話に気をつけてください。","romaji":"Kanpukin sagi no denwa ni ki o tsukete kudasai.","german":"Beware of scam calls about refunds.","furigana":"かんぷきんさぎ|でんわ|き"}],"notes":"還付 is the refund of money by a government office, above all of overpaid taxes: 税金が還付される, 還付を受ける, 還付金 \"refund amount\". For refunds in shops or for tickets one does not say 還付 but 返金 or 払い戻し. The word is formal and is encountered mainly with tax returns (確定申告) - and unfortunately also in scams (還付金詐欺) in which callers promise supposed refunds.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"共済に加入しています。","romaji":"Kyōsai ni kanyū shiteimasu.","german":"I am a member of a mutual aid association.","furigana":"きょうさい|かにゅう"},{"japanese":"火事で家が焼けて、共済金を受け取りました。","romaji":"Kaji de ie ga yakete, kyousaikin o uketorimashita.","german":"My house burned down in a fire, and I received the mutual-aid payout.","furigana":"かじ|いえ|や|きょうさいきん|う|と"},{"japanese":"共済は保険より掛け金が安いことが多いです。","romaji":"Kyousai wa hoken yori kakekin ga yasui koto ga ooi desu.","german":"Mutual-aid premiums are often cheaper than insurance.","furigana":"きょうさい|ほけん|か|きん|やす|おお"}],"notes":"共済 literally means \"helping together\" and denotes mutual-benefit organizations run by cooperatives, unions or professional groups (e.g. for civil servants or farmers). In daily life this mostly means their insurance-like products: 共済に加入する \"to join\", 掛け金 \"premium\", 共済金 \"benefit\". Unlike 保険 (commercial insurance), 共済 is not profit-oriented and often cheaper. Do not confuse with the identically read 共催 \"to co-host\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"毎月積立をしています。","romaji":"Maitsuki tsumitate o shiteimasu.","german":"I save every month.","furigana":"まいつきつみたて"},{"japanese":"老後のために積立投資をしています。","romaji":"Rougo no tame ni tsumitate toushi o shite imasu.","german":"I'm investing regularly in an accumulation plan for my old age.","furigana":"ろうご|つみたてとうし"},{"japanese":"クラスで積み立てたお金で卒業旅行に行きます。","romaji":"Kurasu de tsumitateta okane de sotsugyou ryokou ni ikimasu.","german":"We're going on a graduation trip with the money the class saved up.","furigana":"つ|た|かね|そつぎょうりょこう|い"}],"notes":"積立 (also written 積み立て) is the regular saving of money for a particular purpose; the related transitive verb is 積み立てる \"to save up, set aside\". Typical compounds: 積立預金 \"savings plan\", 積立金 \"reserve fund\", 積立投資 \"fund savings plan\". In compounds it is mostly written without okurigana (積立金), for the verb with it (積み立てる). 貯金 is saving in general or savings; 積立 emphasizes planned, repeated deposits.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"遺言書を作成しました。","romaji":"Yuigonsho o sakusei shimashita.","german":"I drew up a will.","furigana":"ゆいごんしょ|さくせい"},{"japanese":"祖父は遺言で家を孫に残しました。","romaji":"Sofu wa yuigon de ie o mago ni nokoshimashita.","german":"My grandfather left the house to his grandchildren in his will.","furigana":"そふ|ゆいごん|いえ|まご|のこ"},{"japanese":"「家族を頼む」というのが父の遺言でした。","romaji":"“Kazoku o tanomu” to iu no ga chichi no yuigon deshita.","german":"\"Take care of the family\" were my father's last words.","furigana":"かぞく|たの|ちち|ゆいごん"}],"notes":"遺言 is on the one hand a will, and on the other hand the \"last wish\" or last words of a dying person. Typical: 遺言を残す \"to leave a will\", 遺言書を書く/作成する, 遺言で〜を残す \"to bequeath by will\". The general reading is ゆいごん; lawyers say いごん. Do not confuse with 遺書 (a farewell letter, often before a suicide) and 遺産 \"inheritance, estate\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"贈与税がかかります。","romaji":"Zōyozei ga kakarimasu.","german":"Gift tax is levied.","furigana":"ぞうよぜい"},{"japanese":"父から土地の贈与を受けました。","romaji":"Chichi kara tochi no zouyo o ukemashita.","german":"I received a gift of land from my father.","furigana":"ちち|とち|ぞうよ|う"},{"japanese":"相続税対策として、生前に子どもへ財産を贈与する人もいます。","romaji":"Souzokuzei taisaku to shite, seizen ni kodomo e zaisan o zouyo suru hito mo imasu.","german":"To reduce inheritance tax, some people give their property to their children while still alive.","furigana":"そうぞくぜいたいさく|せいぜん|こ|ざいさん|ぞうよ|ひと"}],"notes":"贈与 is the legal and tax term for the gift of property (money, land, shares): 贈与する, 贈与を受ける, 贈与税 \"gift tax\", 生前贈与 \"gift during one's lifetime\". For an ordinary present one says プレゼント or 贈り物, and for donations to organizations 寄付. 贈与 is thus encountered almost only in connection with inheritance, taxes and contracts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"質屋で時計を売りました。","romaji":"Shichiya de tokei o urimashita.","german":"I sold my watch at the pawnshop.","furigana":"しちや|とけい|う"},{"japanese":"お金に困って、指輪を質屋に入れました。","romaji":"Okane ni komatte, yubiwa o shichiya ni iremashita.","german":"I was short of money, so I took my ring to the pawnshop.","furigana":"かね|こま|ゆびわ|しちや|い"},{"japanese":"期限までにお金を返さないと、品物は質屋のものになります。","romaji":"Kigen made ni okane o kaesanai to, shinamono wa shichiya no mono ni narimasu.","german":"If you don't repay the money by the deadline, the item becomes the pawnshop's.","furigana":"きげん|かね|かえ|しなもの|しちや"}],"notes":"質屋 is the pawnshop: 質 \"pledge\" (reading しち, not しつ) + 屋 \"shop\". One brings valuables and gets a loan for them: 〜を質に入れる or 質屋に入れる \"to pawn\". If one doesn't repay in time, the pledge is forfeited (質流れ). Many pawnshops today also buy items outright, so one can also \"sell\" something there. The word is somewhat old-fashioned; modern shops often advertise with 買取.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"骨董品の競売に参加しました。","romaji":"Kottōhin no kyōbai ni sanka shimashita.","german":"I took part in an antiques auction.","furigana":"こっとうひん|きょうばい|さんか"},{"japanese":"ローンが払えず、家が競売にかけられました。","romaji":"Roon ga haraezu, ie ga kyoubai ni kakeraremashita.","german":"He couldn't pay the loan, and his house was put up for auction.","furigana":"はら|いえ|きょうばい"},{"japanese":"有名な画家の絵が、競売で高値で落札されました。","romaji":"Yuumei na gaka no e ga, kyoubai de takane de rakusatsu saremashita.","german":"A painting by a famous artist was sold at auction for a high price.","furigana":"ゆうめい|がか|え|きょうばい|たかね|らくさつ"}],"notes":"競売 is an auction, especially a formal or court-ordered one: 競売にかける \"to put up for auction\", 競売にかけられる \"to go under the hammer\", 競売で落札する \"to win at auction\". In forced sales, lawyers also read the word けいばい. In daily life, especially online, one usually says オークション; at fish markets the auction is called せり. 競売 is rather written and news language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"価格変動が激しいです。","romaji":"Kakaku hendō ga hageshii desu.","german":"Price fluctuations are severe.","furigana":"かかくへんどう|はげ"},{"japanese":"野菜は天候によって価格変動が大きいです。","romaji":"Yasai wa tenkou ni yotte kakaku hendou ga ookii desu.","german":"Vegetable prices fluctuate greatly depending on the weather.","furigana":"やさい|てんこう|かかくへんどう|おお"},{"japanese":"仮想通貨は価格変動のリスクがあるので、注意が必要です。","romaji":"Kasou tsuuka wa kakaku hendou no risuku ga aru node, chuui ga hitsuyou desu.","german":"Cryptocurrencies carry the risk of price fluctuations, so caution is needed.","furigana":"かそうつうか|かかくへんどう|ちゅうい|ひつよう"}],"notes":"価格変動 is made up of 価格 \"price\" (formal) and 変動 \"fluctuation, change\", and is a term from economics and the news. Typical: 価格変動が大きい/激しい, 価格変動のリスク. For movement in one direction only, one says 値上がり (price rise) or 値下がり (price drop); the general price level is called 物価. In casual conversation one would rather say 値段がよく変わる.","levelNote":"Compound: 価格 (N3) + 変動 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"円安で輸入品が高くなりました。","romaji":"En'yasu de yunyuuhin ga takaku narimashita.","german":"Imports have become more expensive because of the weak yen.","furigana":"えんやす|ゆにゅうひん|たか"},{"japanese":"円安が進んで、海外からの観光客が増えています。","romaji":"En'yasu ga susunde, kaigai kara no kankoukyaku ga fuete imasu.","german":"As the yen weakens, the number of tourists from abroad is increasing.","furigana":"えんやす|すす|かいがい|かんこうきゃく|ふ"},{"japanese":"円安は輸出企業にとっては有利です。","romaji":"En'yasu wa yushutsu kigyou ni totte wa yuuri desu.","german":"A weak yen is advantageous for export companies.","furigana":"えんやす|ゆしゅつきぎょう|ゆうり"}],"notes":"円安 literally means \"yen cheap\" and describes a weak yen relative to other currencies. Typical: 円安になる, 円安が進む \"the yen weakens further\", 円安の影響. The opposite is 円高. A weak yen makes imports and foreign travel more expensive, but helps export firms and lures tourists to Japan. The word is equally familiar in the news and in everyday conversation.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"円高で海外旅行が安くなっています。","romaji":"Endaka de kaigai ryokou ga yasuku natte imasu.","german":"Because of the strong yen, overseas travel is getting cheaper.","furigana":"えんだか|かいがいりょこう|やす"},{"japanese":"円高が続き、輸出企業の利益が減りました。","romaji":"Endaka ga tsuzuki, yushutsu kigyou no rieki ga herimashita.","german":"The continued strong yen reduced the profits of export companies.","furigana":"えんだか|つづ|ゆしゅつきぎょう|りえき|へ"},{"japanese":"一ドル八十円という円高の時代もありました。","romaji":"Ichi doru hachijuu en to iu endaka no jidai mo arimashita.","german":"There was also an era of a strong yen, with one dollar at 80 yen.","furigana":"いち|はちじゅうえん|えんだか|じだい"}],"notes":"円高 literally means \"yen high\": the yen is strong, and one yen buys a lot of foreign currency. Typical: 円高になる, 円高が進む, 円高が続く. The opposite is 円安. A strong yen makes foreign travel and imports cheaper, but hurts export firms. The word is heard constantly in business news, but also in daily life, for example when traveling or shopping online abroad.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"株価が下がりました。","romaji":"Kabuka ga sagarimashita.","german":"Stock prices fell.","furigana":"かぶか|さ"},{"japanese":"新製品の発表で、その会社の株価が急に上がりました。","romaji":"Shinseihin no happyou de, sono kaisha no kabuka ga kyuu ni agarimashita.","german":"After the announcement of a new product, the company's stock price rose sharply.","furigana":"しんせいひん|はっぴょう|かいしゃ|かぶか|きゅう|あ"},{"japanese":"毎朝ニュースで株価をチェックしています。","romaji":"Maiasa nyuusu de kabuka o chekku shite imasu.","german":"I check stock prices in the news every morning.","furigana":"まいあさ|かぶか"}],"notes":"株価 is the stock price (株 \"share\" + 価 \"price\"). It rises or falls: 株価が上がる/下がる, in the news 株価が上昇する/下落する, in a crash 株価が暴落する. The best-known index is the 日経平均株価. Do not confuse with 物価 \"price level, cost of living\"; 株 alone denotes the share or shares as an investment (株を買う).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"配当金を受け取りました。","romaji":"Haitōkin o uketorimashita.","german":"I received dividends.","furigana":"はいとうきん|う|と"},{"japanese":"この会社は毎年、株主に配当を出しています。","romaji":"Kono kaisha wa maitoshi, kabunushi ni haitou o dashite imasu.","german":"This company pays a dividend to its shareholders every year.","furigana":"かいしゃ|まいとし|かぶぬし|はいとう|だ"},{"japanese":"競馬で当たったけど、配当は少なかった。","romaji":"Keiba de atatta kedo, haitou wa sukunakatta.","german":"I won at the horse race, but the payout was small.","furigana":"けいば|あ|はいとう|すく"}],"notes":"配当 first means \"allotment\", but in daily life mainly the dividend of a joint-stock company: 配当を出す \"to pay a dividend\", 配当を受け取る, 配当金, 高配当株 \"high-dividend stock\". At horse races and lotteries, 配当 is the payout per stake. For interest on savings one says 利息 or 利子, and for the distribution of resources in general 配分.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自己破産を申請しました。","romaji":"Jiko hasan o shinsei shimashita.","german":"I filed for personal bankruptcy.","furigana":"じこはさん|しんせい"},{"japanese":"ギャンブルで借金が増えて、破産してしまいました。","romaji":"Gyanburu de shakkin ga fuete, hasan shite shimaimashita.","german":"His debts grew from gambling, and he went bankrupt.","furigana":"しゃっきん|ふ|はさん"},{"japanese":"その会社は去年倒産し、破産の手続きに入りました。","romaji":"Sono kaisha wa kyonen tousan shi, hasan no tetsuzuki ni hairimashita.","german":"The company went under last year and entered bankruptcy proceedings.","furigana":"かいしゃ|きょねんとうさん|はさん|てつづ|はい"}],"notes":"破産 is bankruptcy or insolvency in the legal sense, for private individuals as well as firms; as an intransitive する-verb: 破産する \"to go bankrupt\". Important are 自己破産 \"personal bankruptcy (self-filed)\" and 破産手続き \"bankruptcy proceedings\". 倒産 is said only of companies and means economic failure in general; 破産 is the court procedure. Colloquially one also says つぶれる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"負債が多いです。","romaji":"Fusai ga ōi desu.","german":"The debts are large.","furigana":"ふさい|おお"},{"japanese":"その会社は約十億円の負債を抱えて倒産しました。","romaji":"Sono kaisha wa yaku juuoku en no fusai o kakaete tousan shimashita.","german":"The company went bankrupt with liabilities of about one billion yen.","furigana":"かいしゃ|やくじゅうおくえん|ふさい|かか|とうさん"},{"japanese":"住宅ローンも負債の一つです。","romaji":"Juutaku roon mo fusai no hitotsu desu.","german":"A home loan is also a kind of liability.","furigana":"じゅうたく|ふさい|ひと"}],"notes":"負債 is the formal expression for debts or liabilities, typical in balance sheets, business news and bankruptcies: 負債を抱える \"to carry debts\", 負債総額 \"total liabilities\". In everyday life one almost always says 借金; 負債 sounds like bookkeeping and companies. Its counterpart on the balance sheet is 資産 \"assets\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"債権を回収します。","romaji":"Saiken o kaishū shimasu.","german":"I collect the debt.","furigana":"さいけん|かいしゅう"},{"japanese":"債権者が返済を求めて会社に来ました。","romaji":"Saikensha ga hensai o motomete kaisha ni kimashita.","german":"The creditors came to the company and demanded repayment.","furigana":"さいけんしゃ|へんさい|もと|かいしゃ|き"},{"japanese":"銀行は貸したお金の債権を別の会社に売りました。","romaji":"Ginkou wa kashita okane no saiken o betsu no kaisha ni urimashita.","german":"The bank sold the claim on the loan it made to another company.","furigana":"ぎんこう|か|かね|さいけん|べつ|かいしゃ|う"}],"notes":"債権 is a claim, that is, the right to demand money or a service from someone: 債権を回収する \"to collect a claim\", 債権者 \"creditor\". The opposite is 債務 \"liability, debt\" or 債務者 \"debtor\". Note: the identically read 債券 means \"bond, security\" - that is a different word. Both are legal and financial technical terms.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不動産を担保に入れます。","romaji":"Fudōsan o tanpo ni iremasu.","german":"I put up real estate as collateral.","furigana":"ふどうさん|たんぽ|い"},{"japanese":"無担保で借りられるローンは金利が高いです。","romaji":"Mutanpo de karirareru roon wa kinri ga takai desu.","german":"Loans that can be borrowed without collateral have high interest rates.","furigana":"むたんぽ|か|きんり|たか"},{"japanese":"この制度で利用者の安全が担保されています。","romaji":"Kono seido de riyousha no anzen ga tanpo sarete imasu.","german":"This system guarantees the safety of users.","furigana":"せいど|りようしゃ|あんぜん|たんぽ"}],"notes":"担保 is the security for a loan: 〜を担保に入れる / 担保にする \"to put up as collateral\", 〜を担保に借りる, 担保を取る (the bank demands security), 無担保 \"without collateral\". In business and government jargon, 担保する additionally means \"to ensure, to guarantee\": 安全を担保する. For a personal guarantee one says 保証, and for a pledge at a pawnshop 質.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"家賃を滞納しています。","romaji":"Yachin o tainō shiteimasu.","german":"I am behind on my rent.","furigana":"やちん|たいのう"},{"japanese":"電気代を三か月滞納したら、電気を止められました。","romaji":"Denkidai o sankagetsu tainou shitara, denki o tomeraremashita.","german":"When I was three months behind on the electricity bill, my power was cut off.","furigana":"でんきだい|さん|げつたいのう|でんき|と"},{"japanese":"税金の滞納があると、延滞金がかかります。","romaji":"Zeikin no tainou ga aru to, entaikin ga kakarimasu.","german":"If you are behind on your taxes, you will be charged late fees.","furigana":"ぜいきん|たいのう|えんたいきん"}],"notes":"滞納 means being in arrears on a payment that is due: rent, taxes, fees, insurance premiums. As a transitive する-verb: 家賃を滞納する; also 税金の滞納, 滞納者 \"delinquent payer\". The tone is formal and official; in everyday speech one would rather say 払うのが遅れる or まだ払っていない. 未払い only stresses that something is unpaid, whereas 滞納 stresses that the deadline has already passed.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"督促状が届きました。","romaji":"Tokusokujō ga todokimashita.","german":"A reminder notice arrived.","furigana":"とくそくじょう|とど"},{"japanese":"カード会社から支払いの督促の電話がありました。","romaji":"Kaado gaisha kara shiharai no tokusoku no denwa ga arimashita.","german":"I got a phone call from the card company urging payment.","furigana":"がいしゃ|しはら|とくそく|でんわ"},{"japanese":"市役所は税金を払っていない人に支払いを督促しています。","romaji":"Shiyakusho wa zeikin o haratte inai hito ni shiharai o tokusoku shite imasu.","german":"The city hall is urging people who haven't paid their taxes to pay.","furigana":"しやくしょ|ぜいきん|はら|ひと|しはら|とくそく"}],"notes":"督促 is a formal demand to fulfill an obligation - almost always a payment reminder from authorities, banks or companies: 支払いを督促する, 督促を受ける, 督促状 \"reminder letter\". The similar 催促 is more everyday and also common among private individuals (返事を催促する \"to press for a reply\"). 督促 sounds official and strict.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"財産を差し押さえられました。","romaji":"Zaisan o sashiosaeraremashita.","german":"My property was seized.","furigana":"ざいさん|さ|お"},{"japanese":"税金を払わないと、給料の差し押さえを受けることがあります。","romaji":"Zeikin o harawanai to, kyuuryou no sashiosae o ukeru koto ga arimasu.","german":"If you don't pay your taxes, your wages may be garnished.","furigana":"ぜいきん|はら|きゅうりょう|さ|お|う"},{"japanese":"警察は事件の証拠として、パソコンを差し押さえました。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa jiken no shouko to shite, pasokon o sashiosaemashita.","german":"The police seized the computer as evidence in the case.","furigana":"けいさつ|じけん|しょうこ|さ|お"}],"notes":"差し押さえ is seizure or attachment by a court, tax office or police. The related transitive verb is 差し押さえる; those affected appear in the passive: 財産を差し押さえられる. Typical objects: 給料, 口座, 家, 証拠品. In official texts also written without okurigana as 差押え or 差押. A synonym from criminal matters is 押収 \"seizure of evidence\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"訴訟を起こしました。","romaji":"Soshō o okoshimashita.","german":"I filed a lawsuit.","furigana":"そしょう|お"},{"japanese":"その訴訟は三年も続いています。","romaji":"Sono soshou wa sannen mo tsuzuite imasu.","german":"The lawsuit has been going on for three years.","furigana":"そしょう|さんねん|つづ"},{"japanese":"訴訟になる前に、話し合いで解決したいです。","romaji":"Soshou ni naru mae ni, hanashiai de kaiketsu shitai desu.","german":"I would like to settle this by talking before it turns into a lawsuit.","furigana":"そしょう|まえ|はな|あ|かいけつ"}],"notes":"訴訟 is a lawsuit or legal action in the legal sense: 訴訟を起こす \"to bring a lawsuit\", 訴訟に勝つ/負ける, 民事訴訟 \"civil lawsuit\". In conversation one would rather say 裁判 \"trial\" or the verb 訴える \"to sue\"; 訴訟 sounds like the news and a law firm. As a する-verb it is intransitive, but it is rarely used that way.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"控訴しました。","romaji":"Kōso shimashita.","german":"I filed an appeal.","furigana":"こうそ"},{"japanese":"被告は判決を不服として控訴しました。","romaji":"Hikoku wa hanketsu o fufuku to shite kouso shimashita.","german":"The defendant, dissatisfied with the verdict, appealed.","furigana":"ひこく|はんけつ|ふふく|こうそ"},{"japanese":"控訴審では一審の判決が取り消されました。","romaji":"Kousoshin de wa isshin no hanketsu ga torikesaremashita.","german":"On appeal, the first-instance judgment was overturned.","furigana":"こうそしん|いっしん|はんけつ|と|け"}],"notes":"控訴 is the appeal against a first-instance judgment to a higher court; as an intransitive する-verb: 控訴する \"to appeal\". Typical: 判決を不服として控訴する, 控訴審 \"appeal trial\", 控訴を棄却する \"to dismiss the appeal\". The next stage to the Supreme Court is 上告; the umbrella term for both is 上訴. Do not confuse with 控除 \"deduction, allowance\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"時効が成立しました。","romaji":"Jikō ga seiritsu shimashita.","german":"The statute of limitations has run out.","furigana":"じこう|せいりつ"},{"japanese":"殺人事件の時効は廃止されました。","romaji":"Satsujin jiken no jikou wa haishi saremashita.","german":"The statute of limitations for murder has been abolished.","furigana":"さつじんじけん|じこう|はいし"},{"japanese":"もう時効だから言うけど、あの花瓶を割ったのは僕なんだ。","romaji":"Mou jikou dakara iu kedo, ano kabin o watta no wa boku nanda.","german":"It's long past the statute of limitations, so I can say it: I'm the one who broke that vase.","furigana":"じこう|い|かびん|わ|ぼく"}],"notes":"時効 is the statute of limitations: 時効が成立する, 時効になる, 時効を迎える \"to become time-barred\"; 時効が成立した事件 \"a time-barred case\". Colloquially it is used jokingly in a figurative sense: もう時効だから言うけど \"it's long past the statute of limitations, so I can say it\" - with it one admits old secrets or mishaps. Unlike 期限 \"deadline\", 時効 emphasizes that a claim or prosecution lapses through the passage of time.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"示談で解決しました。","romaji":"Jidan de kaiketsu shimashita.","german":"The matter was settled out of court.","furigana":"じだん|かいけつ"},{"japanese":"交通事故の相手と示談が成立しました。","romaji":"Koutsuu jiko no aite to jidan ga seiritsu shimashita.","german":"An out-of-court settlement was reached with the other party in the traffic accident.","furigana":"こうつうじこ|あいて|じだん|せいりつ"},{"japanese":"保険会社が示談交渉をしてくれました。","romaji":"Hoken gaisha ga jidan koushou o shite kuremashita.","german":"The insurance company conducted the settlement negotiations for me.","furigana":"ほけんがいしゃ|じだんこうしょう"}],"notes":"示談 is the out-of-court settlement between the parties, typically after traffic accidents or minor offenses: 示談にする, 示談が成立する, 示談で解決する/済ませる, 示談金 \"settlement money\", 示談交渉 (often conducted by the insurance company). As an intransitive する-verb: 相手と示談する. 和解 is the more general term for compromise/reconciliation and can also occur in court.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"慰謝料を請求しました。","romaji":"Isharyō o seikyū shimashita.","german":"Ich habe Schmerzensgeld verlangt.","furigana":"いしゃりょう|せいきゅう"},{"japanese":"浮気が原因で離婚し、慰謝料を払うことになりました。","romaji":"Uwaki ga gen'in de rikon shi, isharyou o harau koto ni narimashita.","german":"Nach der Scheidung wegen seiner Affäre muss er nun eine Entschädigung zahlen.","furigana":"うわき|げんいん|りこん|いしゃりょう|はら"},{"japanese":"事故の被害者は慰謝料として百万円を受け取りました。","romaji":"Jiko no higaisha wa isharyou to shite hyakuman en o uketorimashita.","german":"Das Unfallopfer erhielt eine Million Yen Schmerzensgeld.","furigana":"じこ|ひがいしゃ|いしゃりょう|ひゃくまんえん|う|と"}],"notes":"慰謝料 ist eine Entschädigung für seelisches Leid (immaterieller Schaden), etwa nach einem Unfall, bei Scheidung wegen Untreue oder bei Belästigung. Typisch: 慰謝料を請求する „verlangen“, 慰謝料を払う/もらう. Im Unterschied zu 損害賠償, das jeden Schaden einschließlich Sachschäden abdeckt, geht es bei 慰謝料 um Schmerz und Kränkung. Im Deutschen passt je nach Kontext „Schmerzensgeld“ oder „Entschädigung“; selten auch 慰藉料 geschrieben.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"損害賠償を求めます。","romaji":"Songai baishō o motomemasu.","german":"I am demanding damages.","furigana":"そんがいばいしょう|もと"},{"japanese":"工事の騒音で、住民が会社に損害賠償を請求しました。","romaji":"Kouji no souon de, juumin ga kaisha ni songai baishou o seikyuu shimashita.","german":"Because of the construction noise, the residents demanded damages from the company.","furigana":"こうじ|そうおん|じゅうみん|かいしゃ|そんがいばいしょう|せいきゅう"},{"japanese":"裁判所は会社に一億円の損害賠償を命じました。","romaji":"Saibansho wa kaisha ni ichioku en no songai baishou o meijimashita.","german":"The court ordered the company to pay 100 million yen in damages.","furigana":"さいばんしょ|かいしゃ|いちおくえん|そんがいばいしょう|めい"}],"notes":"損害賠償 is made up of 損害 \"damage\" and 賠償 \"compensation\" and is the legal term for damages: 損害賠償を請求する/求める, 損害賠償を支払う, 損害賠償責任 \"liability\". When you break something in everyday life and pay for it, you say 弁償する. 慰謝料 is the portion for emotional suffering, and 補償 is compensation without fault of one's own (e.g. from the state).","levelNote":"Compound: 損害 (N3) + 賠償 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"素粒子物理学を研究しています。","romaji":"Soryūshi butsurigaku o kenkyū shiteimasu.","german":"I am researching elementary particle physics.","furigana":"そりゅうしぶつりがく|けんきゅう"},{"japanese":"電子は素粒子の一つです。","romaji":"Denshi wa soryuushi no hitotsu desu.","german":"The electron is one of the elementary particles.","furigana":"でんし|そりゅうし|ひと"},{"japanese":"ニュートリノという素粒子の研究で、日本人がノーベル賞を受賞しました。","romaji":"Nyuutorino to iu soryuushi no kenkyuu de, nihonjin ga nooberu shou o jushou shimashita.","german":"A Japanese person won the Nobel Prize for research on a particle called the neutrino.","furigana":"そりゅうし|けんきゅう|にほんじん|しょう|じゅしょう"}],"notes":"素粒子 is a technical term of physics: 素 \"basic, element\" + 粒子 \"particle\". It refers to particles that cannot be broken down further, e.g. electron, quark or neutrino; the discipline is called 素粒子物理学. By comparison, 原子 \"atom\" and 分子 \"molecule\" are larger units, and 粒子 alone is any small particle (including dust). The word is encountered in science news and popular science texts.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"植物は光合成をします。","romaji":"Shokubutsu wa kōgōsei o shimasu.","german":"Plants carry out photosynthesis.","furigana":"しょくぶつ|こうごうせい"},{"japanese":"光合成には光と水と二酸化炭素が必要です。","romaji":"Kougousei ni wa hikari to mizu to nisanka tanso ga hitsuyou desu.","german":"Photosynthesis requires light, water and carbon dioxide.","furigana":"こうごうせい|ひかり|みず|にさんかたんそ|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"日が当たらない部屋では、この植物は光合成ができずに枯れてしまいます。","romaji":"Hi ga ataranai heya de wa, kono shokubutsu wa kougousei ga dekizu ni karete shimaimasu.","german":"In a room that gets no sunlight, this plant cannot photosynthesize and withers.","furigana":"ひ|あ|へや|しょくぶつ|こうごうせい|か"}],"notes":"光合成 (光 \"light\" + 合成 \"synthesis\") is photosynthesis, a school term from biology. One says 光合成をする/行う or 光合成によって〜を作る; in the process plants take in 二酸化炭素 and release 酸素. Its counterpart in class is 呼吸 \"respiration\". The word is neutral and part of the basic vocabulary of natural science and environmental topics.","levelNote":"Compound: 光 (N4) + 合成 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"恐竜の化石を発見しました。","romaji":"Kyōryū no kaseki o hakken shimashita.","german":"I discovered a dinosaur fossil.","furigana":"きょうりゅう|かせき|はっけん"},{"japanese":"石油や石炭などの化石燃料を減らす必要があります。","romaji":"Sekiyu ya sekitan nado no kaseki nenryou o herasu hitsuyou ga arimasu.","german":"We need to reduce fossil fuels such as oil and coal.","furigana":"せきゆ|せきたん|かせきねんりょう|へ|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"シーラカンスは「生きた化石」と呼ばれています。","romaji":"Shiirakansu wa “ikita kaseki” to yobarete imasu.","german":"The coelacanth is called a \"living fossil\".","furigana":"い|かせき|よ"}],"notes":"化石 is a fossil (literally \"something turned to stone\"): 化石が見つかる / 化石を発見する, 恐竜の化石. Important compounds are 化石燃料 \"fossil fuels\" and 生きた化石 \"living fossil\". In colloquial speech, completely outdated things or views are also jokingly called 化石. The meaning \"to fossilize\" as a する-verb is rare.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地層を調査します。","romaji":"Chisō o chōsa shimasu.","german":"We are surveying the strata.","furigana":"ちそう|ちょうさ"},{"japanese":"この崖では、何万年もかけてできた地層が見られます。","romaji":"Kono gake de wa, nanman nen mo kakete dekita chisou ga miraremasu.","german":"At this cliff you can see strata formed over tens of thousands of years.","furigana":"がけ|なんまんねん|ちそう|み"},{"japanese":"古い地層から貝の化石が見つかりました。","romaji":"Furui chisou kara kai no kaseki ga mitsukarimashita.","german":"Shell fossils were found in an old stratum.","furigana":"ふる|ちそう|かい|かせき|み"}],"notes":"地層 is a geological layer in the ground or rock, as seen on steep slopes or in excavation pits: 地層を観察する/調べる, 地層ができる, 地層が重なる. Fossils (化石) are often recovered from 地層. For any layer one says 層 in general, e.g. オゾン層; 断層, by contrast, is a fault, that is, a break between layers. A typical school and technical term.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鍾乳洞を見学しました。","romaji":"Shōnyūdō o kengaku shimashita.","german":"I toured the limestone cave.","furigana":"しょうにゅうどう|けんがく"},{"japanese":"鍾乳洞の中は夏でもひんやりしています。","romaji":"Shounyuudou no naka wa natsu demo hinyari shite imasu.","german":"Inside the limestone cave, it is cool even in summer."},{"japanese":"鍾乳洞は、雨水が長い時間をかけて岩を溶かしてできたものです。","romaji":"Shounyuudou wa, amamizu ga nagai jikan o kakete iwa o tokashite dekita mono desu.","german":"A limestone cave is formed by rainwater dissolving rock over a long time.","furigana":"しょうにゅうどう|あまみず|なが|じかん|いわ|と"}],"notes":"鍾乳洞 is a stalactite cave: 鍾乳 stands for the dripstones (鍾乳石 \"stalactite\"), 洞 for \"cave\". In Japan such caves are popular excursion destinations; one says 鍾乳洞を見学する/探検する, 鍾乳洞に入る. Since 鍾 is not an everyday character, signs often read しょうにゅう洞. A cave in general is called 洞窟 or 洞穴.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"溶岩が流れています。","romaji":"Yōgan ga nagareteimasu.","german":"Lava is flowing.","furigana":"ようがん|なが"},{"japanese":"地下のマグマが地上に出ると、溶岩と呼ばれます。","romaji":"Chika no maguma ga chijou ni deru to, yougan to yobaremasu.","german":"When underground magma comes to the surface, it is called lava.","furigana":"ちか|ちじょう|で|ようがん|よ"},{"japanese":"この島の黒い岩は、溶岩が冷えて固まったものです。","romaji":"Kono shima no kuroi iwa wa, yougan ga hiete katamatta mono desu.","german":"The black rocks on this island are lava that cooled and hardened.","furigana":"しま|くろ|いわ|ようがん|ひ|かた"}],"notes":"溶岩 (溶 \"melt\" + 岩 \"rock\") is lava, i.e. molten rock at the surface; underground it is called マグマ. Typical: 溶岩が流れる/流れ出す, 溶岩流 \"lava flow\", 溶岩が冷えて固まる. Solidified lava forms entire landscapes in Japan, e.g. at Fuji or Sakurajima. A neutral word from geography and news about volcanic eruptions.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"火山灰が降りました。","romaji":"Kazanbai ga furimashita.","german":"Volcanic ash fell.","furigana":"かざんばい|ふ"},{"japanese":"桜島の近くでは、洗濯物に火山灰がつくことがよくあります。","romaji":"Sakurajima no chikaku de wa, sentakumono ni kazanbai ga tsuku koto ga yoku arimasu.","german":"Near Sakurajima, volcanic ash often gets on the laundry.","furigana":"さくらじま|ちか|せんたくもの|かざんばい"},{"japanese":"火山灰の影響で、飛行機が欠航になりました。","romaji":"Kazanbai no eikyou de, hikouki ga kekkou ni narimashita.","german":"Flights were canceled because of the volcanic ash.","furigana":"かざんばい|えいきょう|ひこうき|けっこう"}],"notes":"火山灰 is volcanic ash; note the reading with voicing (rendaku): かざん + はい → かざんばい. Typical: 火山灰が降る \"ash rains down\", 火山灰が積もる, 火山灰の影響で欠航する. In the news, ashfall is also called 降灰. In places like Kagoshima, volcanic ash is part of daily life - on cars, laundry and streets.","levelNote":"Compound: 火山 (N2) + 灰 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"間欠泉が噴き出しました。","romaji":"Kanketsusen ga fukidashimashita.","german":"The geyser erupted.","furigana":"かんけつせん|ふ|だ"},{"japanese":"この間欠泉は約十分ごとにお湯を噴き上げます。","romaji":"Kono kanketsusen wa yaku juppun goto ni oyu o fukiagemasu.","german":"This geyser shoots up hot water about every ten minutes.","furigana":"かんけつせん|やくじゅっぷん|ゆ|ふ|あ"},{"japanese":"アイスランドで有名な間欠泉を見に行きました。","romaji":"Aisurando de yuumei na kanketsusen o mi ni ikimashita.","german":"I went to see the famous geysers in Iceland.","furigana":"ゆうめい|かんけつせん|み|い"}],"notes":"間欠泉 is a geyser: 間欠 \"at intervals, periodically\" + 泉 \"spring\". Typical verbs are 噴き出す and 噴き上げる; one says, for example, 〇分ごとにお湯を噴き上げる. Occasionally it is also written 間歇泉. An ordinary hot bath or thermal spring, by contrast, is an 温泉. The word is encountered mainly in travel and nature texts.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"活断層の上に家を建てないでください。","romaji":"Katsu dansō no ue ni ie o tatenaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't build your house on an active fault.","furigana":"かつだんそう|うえ|いえ|た"},{"japanese":"この地震は断層がずれたことで起きました。","romaji":"Kono jishin wa dansou ga zureta koto de okimashita.","german":"This earthquake was caused by a shift along a fault.","furigana":"じしん|だんそう|お"},{"japanese":"親と子の世代の間には、考え方の断層があります。","romaji":"Oya to ko no sedai no aida ni wa, kangaekata no dansou ga arimasu.","german":"There is a gap in thinking between the generation of the parents and that of the children.","furigana":"おや|こ|せだい|あいだ|かんが|かた|だんそう"}],"notes":"断層 in geology is a fault, that is, a break in the earth's crust along which rock layers shift against each other: 断層がずれる/動く. Particularly important in Japan is 活断層 \"active fault\", which can trigger earthquakes. In the figurative, written-language sense, 断層 means a \"gap\" between groups, such as 世代間の断層. Do not confuse with 地層 \"stratum\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"震源は海底でした。","romaji":"Shingen wa kaitei deshita.","german":"The focus of the earthquake was on the seabed.","furigana":"しんげん|かいてい"},{"japanese":"震源の深さは約十キロです。","romaji":"Shingen no fukasa wa yaku jukkiro desu.","german":"The focus of the earthquake is about ten kilometers deep.","furigana":"しんげん|ふか|やくじゅっ"},{"japanese":"震源に近い町では、多くの家が壊れました。","romaji":"Shingen ni chikai machi de wa, ooku no ie ga kowaremashita.","german":"In towns near the epicenter, many houses were destroyed.","furigana":"しんげん|ちか|まち|おお|いえ|こわ"}],"notes":"震源 is strictly speaking the earthquake's focus (hypocenter) inside the earth; the point directly above it on the surface is called 震央 \"epicenter\". In Japanese earthquake reports one constantly hears 震源は〇〇沖 \"the focus is off the coast of ...\" and 震源の深さは〇キロ. Figuratively, 震源 also denotes the origin of rumors or unrest (うわさの震源).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"震度五の地震がありました。","romaji":"Shindo go no jishin ga arimashita.","german":"There was an earthquake of intensity 5.","furigana":"しんどご|じしん"},{"japanese":"東京では震度4を観測しました。","romaji":"Toukyou de wa shindo yon o kansoku shimashita.","german":"Intensity 4 was recorded in Tokyo.","furigana":"とうきょう|しんど|かんそく"},{"japanese":"マグニチュードは一つでも、震度は場所によって違います。","romaji":"Magunichuudo wa hitotsu demo, shindo wa basho ni yotte chigaimasu.","german":"A quake has only one magnitude, but its intensity differs by location.","furigana":"ひと|しんど|ばしょ|ちが"}],"notes":"震度 is the shaking intensity on the Japanese scale (0 to 7, with 5弱/5強 and 6弱/6強) and indicates how strongly the ground shakes at a particular place. Typical: 震度〇を観測する, 最大震度, 震度〇の地震. The energy released by the quake itself, by contrast, is the マグニチュード: a quake has one magnitude, but at each place a different 震度. In the news, the numbers are mostly written in Arabic numerals.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"余震に注意してください。","romaji":"Yoshin ni chūi shite kudasai.","german":"Please watch out for aftershocks.","furigana":"よしん|ちゅうい"},{"japanese":"大きな地震のあと、一週間ぐらい余震が続きました。","romaji":"Ookina jishin no ato, isshuukan gurai yoshin ga tsuzukimashita.","german":"After the big earthquake, aftershocks continued for about a week.","furigana":"おお|じしん|いっしゅうかん|よしん|つづ"},{"japanese":"余震が怖くて、昨夜はよく眠れませんでした。","romaji":"Yoshin ga kowakute, sakuya wa yoku nemuremasen deshita.","german":"I was afraid of aftershocks and couldn't sleep well last night.","furigana":"よしん|こわ|さくや|ねむ"}],"notes":"余震 (余 \"remaining, after-\" + 震 \"quake\") are aftershocks following a larger earthquake. Typical: 余震が続く, 余震に注意する/警戒する, 大きな余震. The main shock is called 本震, a foreshock 前震. After strong quakes, authorities and media warn for days with 余震に注意してください.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地震で液状化が起きました。","romaji":"Jishin de ekijōka ga okimashita.","german":"Liquefaction occurred because of the earthquake.","furigana":"じしん|えきじょうか|お"},{"japanese":"埋め立て地では、地盤が液状化しやすいです。","romaji":"Umetatechi de wa, jiban ga ekijouka shiyasui desu.","german":"On reclaimed land, the ground liquefies easily.","furigana":"う|た|ち|じばん|えきじょうか"},{"japanese":"家を買う前に、液状化のリスクを調べました。","romaji":"Ie o kau mae ni, ekijouka no risuku o shirabemashita.","german":"Before buying a house, I checked the risk of liquefaction.","furigana":"いえ|か|まえ|えきじょうか|しら"}],"notes":"液状化 (液状 \"liquid state\" + 化 \"becoming\") refers mainly to soil liquefaction during earthquakes: water-saturated, sandy ground loses its firmness, houses sink, and sand and water well up. As an intransitive する-verb: 地盤が液状化する; also 液状化現象 and 液状化が起きる. Reclaimed land (埋め立て地) is especially at risk.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"耐震工事をしました。","romaji":"Taishin kōji o shimashita.","german":"We carried out seismic retrofitting.","furigana":"たいしんこうじ"},{"japanese":"このビルは古い耐震基準で建てられています。","romaji":"Kono biru wa furui taishin kijun de taterarete imasu.","german":"This building was built to old seismic standards.","furigana":"ふる|たいしんきじゅん|た"},{"japanese":"部屋を探すときは、耐震性も確認したほうがいいです。","romaji":"Heya o sagasu toki wa, taishinsei mo kakunin shita hou ga ii desu.","german":"When looking for a room, it's better to check the earthquake resistance too.","furigana":"へや|さが|たいしんせい|かくにん"}],"notes":"耐震 (耐 \"to withstand\" + 震 \"quake\") means \"earthquake-proof\" and almost always stands as a modifier before other nouns: 耐震構造 \"earthquake-resistant construction\", 耐震基準 \"seismic building code\", 耐震工事 \"seismic retrofitting\", 耐震性 \"earthquake resistance\". More rarely with の: 耐震の建物. Related are 免震 (base isolation, the building is decoupled from the ground) and 制震 (vibration damping). An important term when looking for housing and buying a house.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"避難所に行きました。","romaji":"Hinanjo ni ikimashita.","german":"I went to the evacuation shelter.","furigana":"ひなんじょ|い"},{"japanese":"近くの小学校が避難所になっています。","romaji":"Chikaku no shougakkou ga hinanjo ni natte imasu.","german":"A nearby elementary school serves as an evacuation shelter.","furigana":"ちか|しょうがっこう|ひなんじょ"},{"japanese":"地震のあと、避難所で一か月生活しました。","romaji":"Jishin no ato, hinanjo de ikkagetsu seikatsu shimashita.","german":"After the earthquake, I lived in an evacuation shelter for a month.","furigana":"じしん|ひなんじょ|いっ|げつせいかつ"}],"notes":"避難所 (避難 \"evacuation, flight\" + 所 \"place\", read here as じょ) is the emergency shelter where people live temporarily after a disaster, usually school gymnasiums or community centers. Typical: 避難所に避難する/行く, 避難所で生活する, 指定避難所 \"designated shelter\". To be distinguished from this is 避難場所, an open gathering place (park, square) where people only briefly get to safety.","levelNote":"Compound: 避難 (N1) + 所 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"食料を備蓄しています。","romaji":"Shokuryō o bichiku shiteimasu.","german":"I keep stockpiles of food.","furigana":"しょくりょう|びちく"},{"japanese":"家族の人数分、三日分の水を備蓄しておきましょう。","romaji":"Kazoku no ninzuubun, mikkabun no mizu o bichiku shite okimashou.","german":"Let's stock up on three days' worth of water for each family member.","furigana":"かぞく|にんずうぶん|みっかぶん|みず|びちく"},{"japanese":"政府は備蓄米を市場に出すことを決めました。","romaji":"Seifu wa bichikumai o shijou ni dasu koto o kimemashita.","german":"The government decided to release rice from its stockpile onto the market.","furigana":"せいふ|びちくまい|しじょう|だ|き"}],"notes":"備蓄 is the planned building up of supplies for emergencies; as a transitive する-verb: 食料/水を備蓄する. Also 備蓄品 \"emergency supplies\", 備蓄米 \"government rice reserve\", 石油の備蓄. In contrast to 買いだめ, the (often panicky) hoarding, 備蓄 sounds sensible and forward-looking. The word is slightly formal and typical of disaster prevention and politics.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"救援物資を送ります。","romaji":"Kyūen busshi o okurimasu.","german":"We will send relief supplies.","furigana":"きゅうえんぶっし|おく"},{"japanese":"自衛隊が被災地の救援に向かいました。","romaji":"Jieitai ga hisaichi no kyuuen ni mukaimashita.","german":"The Self-Defense Forces headed to the disaster area for relief work.","furigana":"じえいたい|ひさいち|きゅうえん|む"},{"japanese":"ピッチャーが打たれたので、救援投手が出てきました。","romaji":"Pitchaa ga utareta node, kyuuen toushu ga dete kimashita.","german":"Since the pitcher was hit hard, a relief pitcher came out.","furigana":"う|きゅうえんとうしゅ|で"}],"notes":"救援 is organized help for people in need, especially after disasters: 救援物資 \"relief supplies\", 救援活動 \"relief operation\", 救援隊 \"relief team\", 救援に向かう/駆けつける. 救助, on the other hand, means the direct rescue of people from mortal danger (人命救助), and 支援 means \"support\" in general. In baseball, 救援投手 is the reliever who steps in for the starter.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"義援金を寄付しました。","romaji":"Gienkin o kifu shimashita.","german":"I donated money for disaster victims.","furigana":"ぎえんきん|きふ"},{"japanese":"駅前で地震の義援金を集めています。","romaji":"Ekimae de jishin no gienkin o atsumete imasu.","german":"Money for earthquake victims is being collected in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|じしん|ぎえんきん|あつ"},{"japanese":"全国から集まった義援金が被災者に配られました。","romaji":"Zenkoku kara atsumatta gienkin ga hisaisha ni kubararemashita.","german":"The donations collected from all over the country were distributed to the victims.","furigana":"ぜんこく|あつ|ぎえんきん|ひさいしゃ|くば"}],"notes":"義援金 is donation money for victims of disasters or accidents, which is distributed directly to those affected, e.g. through the Red Cross: 義援金を送る/寄付する/集める, 義援金が集まる. The original spelling is 義捐金. 寄付金 is the general term for donations; 募金 refers to the collecting itself (fundraising drive, collection box). The word is neutral-formal and appears in the news after every major earthquake.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仮設住宅で暮らしています。","romaji":"Kasetsu jūtaku de kurashiteimasu.","german":"I'm living in temporary housing.","furigana":"かせつじゅうたく|く"},{"japanese":"避難所を出て、仮設住宅に移りました。","romaji":"Hinanjo o dete, kasetsu juutaku ni utsurimashita.","german":"We left the shelter and moved into temporary housing.","furigana":"ひなんじょ|で|かせつじゅうたく|うつ"},{"japanese":"仮設住宅は壁が薄くて、冬はとても寒いです。","romaji":"Kasetsu juutaku wa kabe ga usukute, fuyu wa totemo samui desu.","german":"The walls of temporary housing are thin, and it's very cold in winter.","furigana":"かせつじゅうたく|かべ|うす|ふゆ|さむ"}],"notes":"仮設住宅 (仮設 \"provisionally built\" + 住宅 \"housing\") is temporary housing built after disasters for people whose homes were destroyed. The usual sequence is 避難所 → 仮設住宅 → 復興住宅 (permanent housing). Typical: 仮設住宅に入居する/移る, 仮設住宅で暮らす; those affected often say just 仮設.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"瓦礫を撤去します。","romaji":"Gareki o tekkyo shimasu.","german":"We clear the rubble.","furigana":"がれき|てっきょ"},{"japanese":"地震で崩れた家のがれきの下から、犬が助け出されました。","romaji":"Jishin de kuzureta ie no gareki no shita kara, inu ga tasukedasaremashita.","german":"A dog was rescued from under the rubble of a house that collapsed in the earthquake.","furigana":"じしん|くず|いえ|した|いぬ|たす|だ"},{"japanese":"津波のあと、町はがれきの山になってしまいました。","romaji":"Tsunami no ato, machi wa gareki no yama ni natte shimaimashita.","german":"After the tsunami, the town was nothing but a heap of rubble.","furigana":"つなみ|まち|やま"}],"notes":"瓦礫 (瓦 \"roof tile\" + 礫 \"pebble\") is rubble and debris, for example after earthquakes, tsunamis or demolition. Since 礫 is not a Jōyō kanji, it is mostly written がれき. Typical: がれきを撤去する/片付ける, がれきの山, がれきの下敷きになる \"to be buried under rubble\". Figuratively it also means \"worthless junk\", but that is rare.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"行方不明者を捜索しています。","romaji":"Yukue fumeisha o sōsaku shiteimasu.","german":"We are searching for the missing persons.","furigana":"ゆくえふめいしゃ|そうさく"},{"japanese":"登山に行った友人が行方不明になりました。","romaji":"Tozan ni itta yuujin ga yukue fumei ni narimashita.","german":"A friend who went mountain climbing has gone missing.","furigana":"とざん|い|ゆうじん|ゆくえふめい"},{"japanese":"家の鍵が行方不明で、朝から探しています。","romaji":"Ie no kagi ga yukue fumei de, asa kara sagashite imasu.","german":"My house key is missing, and I've been looking for it since morning.","furigana":"いえ|かぎ|ゆくえふめい|あさ|さが"}],"notes":"行方不明 means \"missing, lost, whereabouts unknown\"; 行方 has the special reading ゆくえ \"whereabouts\". Typical: 行方不明になる, 行方不明者 \"missing persons\", 行方不明の子ども. A child who has lost their way, by contrast, is a 迷子. Jokingly it is also said of misplaced objects: 鍵が行方不明. In serious cases it is a word from police and disaster reports.","levelNote":"Compound: 行方 (N2) + 不明 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"安否確認をしています。","romaji":"Anpi kakunin o shiteimasu.","german":"We are checking whether everyone is safe.","furigana":"あんぴかくにん"},{"japanese":"地震のあと、会社から安否確認のメールが届きました。","romaji":"Jishin no ato, kaisha kara anpi kakunin no meeru ga todokimashita.","german":"After the earthquake, I received an email from the company checking whether I was safe.","furigana":"じしん|かいしゃ|あんぴかくにん|とど"},{"japanese":"電話がつながらず、家族の安否確認ができませんでした。","romaji":"Denwa ga tsunagarazu, kazoku no anpi kakunin ga dekimasen deshita.","german":"The phone lines were down, and I couldn't confirm whether my family was safe.","furigana":"でんわ|かぞく|あんぴかくにん"}],"notes":"安否確認 means \"checking whether someone is safe and well\": 安否 = \"whether someone is safe or not\" (安 \"safe\", 否 \"not\"), 確認 = \"confirmation\". After earthquakes, companies and schools inquire by email or app (安否確認メール, 安否確認システム); one says 家族の安否確認をする or 家族の安否を確認する. 安否が分からない means \"one doesn't know whether someone is alive\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"帰宅困難者が多数出ました。","romaji":"Kitaku konnansha ga tasū demashita.","german":"Many commuters were stranded.","furigana":"きたくこんなんしゃ|たすうで"},{"japanese":"駅の近くのホールが帰宅困難者のために開放されました。","romaji":"Eki no chikaku no hooru ga kitaku konnansha no tame ni kaihou saremashita.","german":"A hall near the station was opened for stranded commuters.","furigana":"えき|ちか|きたくこんなんしゃ|かいほう"},{"japanese":"帰宅困難者になったときのために、会社に水と食料を置いています。","romaji":"Kitaku konnansha ni natta toki no tame ni, kaisha ni mizu to shokuryou o oite imasu.","german":"In case we can't get home, we keep water and food at the company.","furigana":"きたくこんなんしゃ|かいしゃ|みず|しょくりょう|お"}],"notes":"帰宅困難者 (帰宅 \"return home\" + 困難 \"difficulty\" + 者 \"person\") are people who cannot get home after an earthquake or a breakdown of public transport. The word became well known through the millions of stranded commuters in Tokyo in March 2011. Typical: 帰宅困難者が出る/発生する, 帰宅困難者向けの施設. In the media one also reads the more colloquial 帰宅難民.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ライフラインが寸断されました。","romaji":"Raifurain ga sundan saremashita.","german":"Lifelines were cut off.","furigana":"すんだん"},{"japanese":"地震のあと、ライフラインが復旧するまで一週間かかりました。","romaji":"Jishin no ato, raifurain ga fukkyuu suru made isshuukan kakarimashita.","german":"After the earthquake, it took a week for utilities to be restored.","furigana":"じしん|ふっきゅう|いっしゅうかん"},{"japanese":"引っ越しの前に、ライフラインの手続きを済ませておきましょう。","romaji":"Hikkoshi no mae ni, raifurain no tetsuzuki o sumasete okimashou.","german":"Before moving, let's take care of the utility procedures.","furigana":"ひ|こ|まえ|てつづ|す"}],"notes":"In Japanese, ライフライン refers above all to vital basic services: electricity, gas and water, often also telephone, internet and transport. The disaster context is typical: ライフラインが止まる／寸断される \"are cut off, are disrupted\", ライフラインが復旧する \"are restored\". When moving, too, people talk about ライフラインの手続き (starting and stopping electricity, gas and water). The English meaning \"rescue line\" is rare; for that one says 命綱.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ロボット工学を研究しています。","romaji":"Robotto kōgaku o kenkyū shiteimasu.","german":"I'm researching robotics.","furigana":"こうがく|けんきゅう"},{"japanese":"大学でロボット工学を専攻したいです。","romaji":"Daigaku de robotto kougaku o senkou shitai desu.","german":"I want to major in robotics at university.","furigana":"だいがく|こうがく|せんこう"},{"japanese":"兄はロボット工学の知識を生かして、介護用のロボットを作っています。","romaji":"Ani wa robotto kougaku no chishiki o ikashite, kaigoyou no robotto o tsukutteimasu.","german":"My brother uses his knowledge of robotics to build care robots.","furigana":"あに|こうがく|ちしき|い|かいごよう|つく"}],"notes":"ロボット工学 is the technical term for \"robotics\" as a scientific and engineering discipline; 工学 means \"engineering\" (cf. 機械工学, 電子工学). Typical: ロボット工学を学ぶ／専攻する／研究する, ロボット工学の専門家. In specialist circles one also hears the loan form ロボティクス. The machines themselves are simply called ロボット; ロボット工学 always means the field of study.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自動運転の車が増えています。","romaji":"Jidō unten no kuruma ga fueteimasu.","german":"Self-driving cars are increasing.","furigana":"じどううんてん|くるま|ふ"},{"japanese":"この地下鉄は自動運転なので、運転士がいません。","romaji":"Kono chikatetsu wa jidou unten na node, untenshi ga imasen.","german":"This subway is run automatically, so there is no driver.","furigana":"ちかてつ|じどううんてん|うんてんし"},{"japanese":"自動運転の技術が進めば、お年寄りも安心して出かけられます。","romaji":"Jidou unten no gijutsu ga susumeba, otoshiyori mo anshin shite dekakeraremasu.","german":"If self-driving technology advances, even elderly people can go out with peace of mind.","furigana":"じどううんてん|ぎじゅつ|すす|としよ|あんしん|で"}],"notes":"自動運転 denotes automatic operation without human intervention, today above all \"autonomous driving\" of cars. Typical: 自動運転の車／自動運転車, 自動運転の技術, 自動運転で走る. 運転 means \"driving, operating\" here, so the word can also apply to trains and machines (自動運転の電車). The opposite is 手動運転; mere driver-assistance systems are called 運転支援.","levelNote":"Compound: 自動 (N3) + 運転 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仮想通貨に投資しています。","romaji":"Kasō tsūka ni tōshi shiteimasu.","german":"I'm investing in cryptocurrency.","furigana":"かそうつうか|とうし"},{"japanese":"仮想通貨の値段は一日で大きく変わることがあります。","romaji":"Kasou tsuuka no nedan wa ichinichi de ookiku kawaru koto ga arimasu.","german":"The price of cryptocurrencies can change greatly in a single day.","furigana":"かそうつうか|ねだん|いちにち|おお|か"},{"japanese":"仮想通貨の取引所がハッキングされたというニュースを見ました。","romaji":"Kasou tsuuka no torihikijo ga hakkingu sareta to iu nyuusu o mimashita.","german":"I saw in the news that a crypto exchange was hacked.","furigana":"かそうつうか|とりひきじょ|み"}],"notes":"仮想通貨 (\"virtual currency\") is the word long customary in Japan for cryptocurrencies such as ビットコイン. Laws and authorities have officially used 暗号資産 since 2020, but 仮想通貨 is still very widespread in the media and daily life. Typical: 仮想通貨に投資する, 仮想通貨を買う／売る, 仮想通貨の取引所 \"crypto exchange\". Do not confuse with 電子マネー - that is ordinary yen balance on a card or in a smartphone.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"電子決済が普及しています。","romaji":"Denshi kessai ga fukyū shiteimasu.","german":"Electronic payment is becoming widespread.","furigana":"でんしけっさい|ふきゅう"},{"japanese":"この店は電子決済に対応していないので、現金で払ってください。","romaji":"Kono mise wa denshi kessai ni taiou shiteinai node, genkin de haratte kudasai.","german":"This shop doesn't accept electronic payment, so please pay in cash.","furigana":"みせ|でんしけっさい|たいおう|げんきん|はら"},{"japanese":"スマホの電子決済なら、財布を出さなくても買い物ができます。","romaji":"Sumaho no denshi kessai nara, saifu o dasanakute mo kaimono ga dekimasu.","german":"With smartphone payment, you can shop without taking out your wallet.","furigana":"でんしけっさい|さいふ|だ|か|もの"}],"notes":"電子決済 is the umbrella term for cashless, electronic payment - by credit card, IC card such as Suica, or QR-code payment with a smartphone. 決済 is a rather formal word for \"settling a payment\"; in daily life one therefore often hears キャッシュレス決済 or simply キャッシュレス. Typical: 電子決済に対応している \"accepts electronic payment\", 電子決済で払う, 電子決済が普及する. The counterpart is 現金払い \"cash payment\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"遠隔操作でロボットを動かします。","romaji":"Enkaku sōsa de robotto o ugokashimasu.","german":"I move the robot by remote control.","furigana":"えんかくそうさ|うご"},{"japanese":"スマホでエアコンを遠隔操作できるので、帰る前に部屋を涼しくしておけます。","romaji":"Sumaho de eakon o enkaku sousa dekiru node, kaeru mae ni heya o suzushiku shite okemasu.","german":"I can control the air conditioner remotely with my phone, so I can cool the room before I get home.","furigana":"えんかくそうさ|かえ|まえ|へや|すず"},{"japanese":"パソコンがウイルスに感染して、誰かに遠隔操作されていました。","romaji":"Pasokon ga uirusu ni kansen shite, dareka ni enkaku sousa sareteimashita.","german":"My computer was infected with a virus and was being remotely controlled by someone.","furigana":"かんせん|だれ|えんかくそうさ"}],"notes":"遠隔操作 means \"remote control\" of machines, robots or computers from a distance; 遠隔 means \"distant\" (cf. 遠隔授業 \"distance learning\"). As a transitive する-verb: ドローンを遠隔操作する; often instrumental with で: 遠隔操作で動かす. The device for remote operation of the TV, by contrast, is called リモコン, and in IT, for remote access, one also says リモート操作. A neutral, rather technical word.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"在宅勤務をしています。","romaji":"Zaitaku kinmu o shiteimasu.","german":"I work from home.","furigana":"ざいたくきんむ"},{"japanese":"うちの会社では、週に二回在宅勤務ができます。","romaji":"Uchi no kaisha de wa, shuu ni nikai zaitaku kinmu ga dekimasu.","german":"At our company, you can work from home twice a week.","furigana":"かいしゃ|しゅう|にかいざいたくきんむ"},{"japanese":"在宅勤務になってから、通勤の時間がなくなって楽になりました。","romaji":"Zaitaku kinmu ni natte kara, tsuukin no jikan ga nakunatte raku ni narimashita.","german":"Since I started working from home, I no longer spend time commuting, and it's easier.","furigana":"ざいたくきんむ|つうきん|じかん|らく"}],"notes":"在宅勤務 literally means \"duty at home\" and is the neutral word for employees' home office. Typical: 在宅勤務をする, 在宅勤務になる, 週に二日在宅勤務する. Almost synonymous and very common in daily life are テレワーク and リモートワーク; 在宅勤務 sounds somewhat more official and explicitly means one's own home. Someone who works independently at home says rather 在宅で仕事をしている.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"時差出勤が認められています。","romaji":"Jisa shukkin ga mitomerareteimasu.","german":"Staggered commuting is permitted.","furigana":"じさしゅっきん|みと"},{"japanese":"満員電車を避けるために、時差出勤をしています。","romaji":"Man'in densha o sakeru tame ni, jisa shukkin o shiteimasu.","german":"I commute at off-peak hours to avoid crowded trains.","furigana":"まんいんでんしゃ|さ|じさしゅっきん"},{"japanese":"来月から時差出勤が始まって、十時に出社してもよくなります。","romaji":"Raigetsu kara jisa shukkin ga hajimatte, juuji ni shussha shite mo yoku narimasu.","german":"Starting next month, staggered commuting begins, and I will be allowed to arrive at the office at ten.","furigana":"らいげつ|じさしゅっきん|はじ|じゅうじ|しゅっしゃ"}],"notes":"時差出勤 refers to a staggered work start: you travel to work earlier or later than rush hour in order to avoid crowded trains. 時差 is otherwise known as the \"time difference\" between time zones; here it means \"offset in time\", and 出勤 means \"to go to work\". Typical: 時差出勤をする／時差出勤する, 時差出勤を導入する, 時差出勤が認められる. Unlike フレックスタイム \"flextime\", usually only the start time is shifted and the working hours stay the same.","levelNote":"Compound: 時差 (N1) + 出勤 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"副業を始めました。","romaji":"Fukugyō o hajimemashita.","german":"I started a side job.","furigana":"ふくぎょう|はじ"},{"japanese":"うちの会社は副業が禁止されています。","romaji":"Uchi no kaisha wa fukugyou ga kinshi sareteimasu.","german":"Side jobs are prohibited at our company.","furigana":"かいしゃ|ふくぎょう|きんし"},{"japanese":"週末に副業でウェブデザインの仕事をしています。","romaji":"Shuumatsu ni fukugyou de webu dezain no shigoto o shiteimasu.","german":"On weekends I do web design work on the side.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|ふくぎょう|しごと"}],"notes":"副業 is a secondary activity alongside one's main occupation (本業) - such as a side job, freelance assignments or a small online business. Typical: 副業をする／始める, 副業で稼ぐ, 副業が禁止されている／認められている; many Japanese companies long prohibited side jobs, and that is changing now. A student's part-time job, by contrast, is アルバイト, because 副業 presupposes a main occupation. Somewhat more casual and synonymous is サイドビジネス.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"脱サラして農業を始めました。","romaji":"Datsu sara shite nōgyō o hajimemashita.","german":"I quit my office job and started farming.","furigana":"だつ|のうぎょう|はじ"},{"japanese":"父は五十歳で脱サラして、ラーメン屋を開きました。","romaji":"Chichi wa gojussai de datsu sara shite, raamen'ya o hirakimashita.","german":"At fifty, my father quit his salaried job and opened a ramen shop.","furigana":"ちち|ごじゅっさい|だつ|や|ひら"},{"japanese":"脱サラしたいけど、収入がなくなるのが怖いです。","romaji":"Datsu sara shitai kedo, shuunyuu ga nakunaru no ga kowai desu.","german":"I want to leave the salaried life, but I'm afraid of losing my income.","furigana":"だつ|しゅうにゅう|こわ"}],"notes":"脱サラ is an abbreviation of 脱 \"to break away from\" and サラ(リーマン) \"salaried employee\": one quits a steady employee job to become self-employed, for example with a shop, a farm or one's own business. Almost always used as a する-verb: 脱サラして店を開く, 脱サラした人. The word is casual, but perfectly normal in conversation and the media. Merely switching to another company is 転職, not 脱サラ.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ワークライフバランスを大切にしています。","romaji":"Wāku raifu baransu o taisetsu ni shiteimasu.","german":"I value work-life balance.","furigana":"たいせつ"},{"japanese":"残業が多くて、ワークライフバランスが取れていません。","romaji":"Zangyou ga ookute, waaku raifu baransu ga toreteimasen.","german":"I work a lot of overtime and don't have a good work-life balance.","furigana":"ざんぎょう|おお|と"},{"japanese":"転職するとき、給料よりワークライフバランスを重視しました。","romaji":"Tenshoku suru toki, kyuuryou yori waaku raifu baransu o juushi shimashita.","german":"When changing jobs, I put work-life balance above salary.","furigana":"てんしょく|きゅうりょう|じゅうし"}],"notes":"ワークライフバランス is the English loanword for the balance between work and private life; it is also written ワーク・ライフ・バランス. Typical: ワークライフバランスを大切にする／重視する, ワークライフバランスが取れている \"is balanced\", ワークライフバランスを改善する. The word often comes up together with 残業 \"overtime\" and 働き方改革 \"work style reform\". The Japanese equivalent 仕事と生活の調和 is found mainly in government texts.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"育児休暇を取得しました。","romaji":"Ikuji kyūka o shutoku shimashita.","german":"I took childcare leave.","furigana":"いくじきゅうか|しゅとく"},{"japanese":"夫も三か月の育児休暇を取ることにしました。","romaji":"Otto mo sankagetsu no ikuji kyuuka o toru koto ni shimashita.","german":"My husband has also decided to take three months of childcare leave.","furigana":"おっと|さん|げつ|いくじきゅうか|と"},{"japanese":"育児休暇が終わったら、また同じ部署に戻る予定です。","romaji":"Ikuji kyuuka ga owattara, mata onaji busho ni modoru yotei desu.","german":"When my childcare leave ends, I plan to return to the same department.","furigana":"いくじきゅうか|お|おな|ぶしょ|もど|よてい"}],"notes":"育児休暇 is parental leave for caring for a small child; colloquially it is almost always shortened to 育休. Legally it is actually called 育児休業, and in everyday life the two words are interchangeable. Typical: 育児休暇を取る／取得する, 育休中, 育休から復帰する \"to return from parental leave\". Maternity leave before and after the birth, by contrast, is called 産休.","levelNote":"Compound: 育児 (N2) + 休暇 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"待機児童問題が深刻です。","romaji":"Taiki jidō mondai ga shinkoku desu.","german":"The problem of children on daycare waiting lists is serious.","furigana":"たいきじどうもんだい|しんこく"},{"japanese":"この市では、待機児童をゼロにするために保育園を増やしています。","romaji":"Kono shi de wa, taiki jidou o zero ni suru tame ni hoikuen o fuyashiteimasu.","german":"This city is building more daycare centers so that no child has to wait for a place.","furigana":"し|たいきじどう|ほいくえん|ふ"},{"japanese":"娘が待機児童になってしまい、仕事に戻れません。","romaji":"Musume ga taiki jidou ni natte shimai, shigoto ni modoremasen.","german":"My daughter ended up on the waiting list, so I can't go back to work.","furigana":"むすめ|たいきじどう|しごと|もど"}],"notes":"待機児童 are children who are registered for a daycare center (保育園) but, due to lack of space, get no place and have to \"wait\" (待機). The word is known mainly from politics and the news, mostly as 待機児童問題. Typical: 待機児童が多い／減る, 待機児童をゼロにする, 待機児童になる. About one's own child one also says 待機児童になる or simply 保育園に入れない.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"少子高齢化が進んでいます。","romaji":"Shōshi kōreika ga susundeimasu.","german":"The aging of society is progressing.","furigana":"しょうしこうれいか|すす"},{"japanese":"少子高齢化で、働く人が足りなくなっています。","romaji":"Shoushi koureika de, hataraku hito ga tarinaku natteimasu.","german":"Because of the low birthrate and aging population, there is a growing shortage of workers.","furigana":"しょうしこうれいか|はたら|ひと|た"},{"japanese":"少子高齢化について、レポートを書かなければなりません。","romaji":"Shoushi koureika ni tsuite, repooto o kakanakereba narimasen.","german":"I have to write a report on the declining birthrate and aging population.","furigana":"しょうしこうれいか|か"}],"notes":"少子高齢化 combines two processes: 少子化 \"falling birth numbers\" and 高齢化 \"aging of the population\"; together it describes Japan's demographic change. Typical: 少子高齢化が進む, 少子高齢化社会, 少子高齢化による人手不足. It is a word from the news, politics and essays, but also fits conversations about society. The individual words 少子化 and 高齢化 likewise go with 進む.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"都市の過密が問題です。","romaji":"Toshi no kamitsu ga mondai desu.","german":"The overcrowding of cities is a problem.","furigana":"とし|かみつ|もんだい"},{"japanese":"来週は会議と出張で過密なスケジュールです。","romaji":"Raishuu wa kaigi to shucchou de kamitsu na sukejuuru desu.","german":"Next week my schedule is packed with meetings and a business trip.","furigana":"らいしゅう|かいぎ|しゅっちょう|かみつ"},{"japanese":"東京の朝の電車は、ダイヤがとても過密です。","romaji":"Toukyou no asa no densha wa, daiya ga totemo kamitsu desu.","german":"The train timetable in Tokyo in the morning is extremely dense.","furigana":"とうきょう|あさ|でんしゃ|かみつ"}],"notes":"過密 means \"too dense\": too many people, buildings or appointments in too little space or too short a time. As a noun it often stands in contrast to 過疎 \"depopulation\" (都市の過密と地方の過疎); as a な-adjective mainly in 過密なスケジュール or 過密ダイヤ \"tightly packed timetable\". For a full train or shop, everyday speech uses 混んでいる or 混雑; 過密 is more matter-of-fact and sounds like planning, statistics and the news.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"限界集落を活性化します。","romaji":"Genkai shūraku o kasseika shimasu.","german":"We are revitalizing marginal villages.","furigana":"げんかいしゅうらく|かっせいか"},{"japanese":"祖母が住んでいる村は、若い人がいなくて限界集落になっています。","romaji":"Sobo ga sundeiru mura wa, wakai hito ga inakute genkai shuuraku ni natteimasu.","german":"The village where my grandmother lives has no young people and has become a marginal village.","furigana":"そぼ|す|むら|わか|ひと|げんかいしゅうらく"},{"japanese":"限界集落では、バスもなくなって買い物が大変です。","romaji":"Genkai shuuraku de wa, basu mo nakunatte kaimono ga taihen desu.","german":"In marginal villages, even the bus is gone and shopping is difficult.","furigana":"げんかいしゅうらく|か|もの|たいへん"}],"notes":"限界集落 is a sociological term for villages in which more than half of the inhabitants are 65 or older, so that community life can hardly be maintained any more. It belongs to the topic area of 過疎 \"depopulation\" and 少子高齢化. Typical: 限界集落になる, 限界集落を活性化する／支援する. 集落 itself is a rather formal word for \"settlement, hamlet\"; the whole sounds factual and news-like.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地域おこし協力隊に参加しました。","romaji":"Chiiki okoshi kyōryoku tai ni sanka shimashita.","german":"I took part in the Community Revitalization Cooperator program.","furigana":"ちいき|きょうりょくたい|さんか"},{"japanese":"地元の野菜を使った料理で、地域おこしをしています。","romaji":"Jimoto no yasai o tsukatta ryouri de, chiiki okoshi o shiteimasu.","german":"We are revitalizing the region with dishes made from local vegetables.","furigana":"じもと|やさい|つか|りょうり|ちいき"},{"japanese":"地域おこしのために、空き家を若い人に安く貸しています。","romaji":"Chiiki okoshi no tame ni, akiya o wakai hito ni yasuku kashiteimasu.","german":"To revitalize the region, vacant houses are rented cheaply to young people.","furigana":"ちいき|あ|や|わか|ひと|やす|か"}],"notes":"地域おこし is the revival of a region - through tourism, local products, events or the arrival of young people. おこし comes from 起こす \"to awaken, to set in motion\" and is usually written in hiragana. Well known is the government program 地域おこし協力隊, in which city dwellers move to the countryside for a few years. Almost synonymous are 町おこし (town) and 村おこし (village); more formal is 地域活性化.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"町おこしのイベントを開催します。","romaji":"Machi okoshi no ibento o kaisai shimasu.","german":"We are holding a town revitalization event.","furigana":"まち|かいさい"},{"japanese":"この町はアニメの舞台になったことで、町おこしに成功しました。","romaji":"Kono machi wa anime no butai ni natta koto de, machi okoshi ni seikou shimashita.","german":"This town succeeded in revitalizing itself by becoming the setting of an anime.","furigana":"まち|ぶたい|まち|せいこう"},{"japanese":"町おこしのために、商店街の人たちが新しい名物料理を考えました。","romaji":"Machi okoshi no tame ni, shoutengai no hitotachi ga atarashii meibutsu ryouri o kangaemashita.","german":"To revitalize the town, the shopkeepers of the shopping street came up with a new local specialty dish.","furigana":"まち|しょうてんがい|ひと|あたら|めいぶつりょうり|かんが"}],"notes":"町おこし means breathing new life into a town or community, e.g. with festivals, mascots (ゆるキャラ), regional specialties or anime settings. It is also written 町興し or まちおこし; おこし comes from 起こす. Typical: 町おこしをする, 町おこしのイベント, 町おこしに成功する. Compared with 地域おこし, the focus is smaller (one town instead of a whole region); for villages one says 村おこし.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"観光資源を活用します。","romaji":"Kankō shigen o katsuyō shimasu.","german":"We make use of tourism resources.","furigana":"かんこうしげん|かつよう"},{"japanese":"この県は温泉や海など、観光資源が豊富です。","romaji":"Kono ken wa onsen ya umi nado, kankou shigen ga houfu desu.","german":"This prefecture is rich in tourism resources such as hot springs and the sea.","furigana":"けん|おんせん|うみ|かんこうしげん|ほうふ"},{"japanese":"古い町並みも大切な観光資源の一つです。","romaji":"Furui machinami mo taisetsu na kankou shigen no hitotsu desu.","german":"The old streetscape is also one of the important tourism assets.","furigana":"ふる|まちな|たいせつ|かんこうしげん|ひと"}],"notes":"観光資源 are all the things that can attract tourists: scenery, hot springs, temples, festivals or regional cuisine. 資源 actually means \"resource, raw material\", so the word sounds planning-oriented and economic and appears mainly with government offices, in the tourism industry and in the news. Typical: 観光資源を活用する／生かす, 観光資源が豊富だ, 豊かな観光資源. For individual sights one says 観光地 or 名所 in daily life.","levelNote":"Compound: 観光 (N4) + 資源 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"世界遺産に登録されました。","romaji":"Sekai isan ni tōroku saremashita.","german":"It was registered as a World Heritage site.","furigana":"せかいいさん|とうろく"},{"japanese":"夏休みに、世界遺産の屋久島に行きたいです。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi ni, sekai isan no Yakushima ni ikitai desu.","german":"In the summer vacation, I want to go to Yakushima, a World Heritage site.","furigana":"なつやす|せかいいさん|やくしま|い"},{"japanese":"世界遺産になってから、観光客が急に増えました。","romaji":"Sekai isan ni natte kara, kankoukyaku ga kyuu ni fuemashita.","german":"Since it became a World Heritage site, the number of tourists has suddenly increased.","furigana":"せかいいさん|かんこうきゃく|きゅう|ふ"}],"notes":"世界遺産 is a UNESCO World Heritage site and includes both cultural heritage (文化遺産) such as 姫路城 and natural heritage (自然遺産) such as 屋久島. Typical: 世界遺産に登録される \"to be inscribed on the World Heritage List\", 世界遺産に選ばれる, 世界遺産を訪ねる／巡る. Intangible heritage such as 和食 is not called 世界遺産 but 無形文化遺産. A neutral, very common word from travel guides and the news.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 37"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"無形文化財に指定されました。","romaji":"Mukei bunkazai ni shitei saremashita.","german":"It was designated an intangible cultural property.","furigana":"むけいぶんかざい|してい"},{"japanese":"この村の伝統的な踊りは、県の無形文化財です。","romaji":"Kono mura no dentouteki na odori wa, ken no mukei bunkazai desu.","german":"The traditional dance of this village is an intangible cultural property of the prefecture.","furigana":"むら|でんとうてき|おど|けん|むけいぶんかざい"},{"japanese":"祖父は無形文化財の技術を若い職人に伝えています。","romaji":"Sofu wa mukei bunkazai no gijutsu o wakai shokunin ni tsutaeteimasu.","german":"My grandfather passes on the techniques of an intangible cultural property to young craftsmen.","furigana":"そふ|むけいぶんかざい|ぎじゅつ|わか|しょくにん|つた"}],"notes":"無形文化財 (\"formless cultural property\") is a term from Japanese cultural property protection for intangible cultural assets such as theater, music and craft techniques, e.g. 能, 歌舞伎 or lacquer art. A person recognized as a holder of an 重要無形文化財 is popularly called 人間国宝. Typical: 無形文化財に指定される, 県の無形文化財, 重要無形文化財. The UNESCO list, by contrast, is called 無形文化遺産; the counterpart for buildings and objects is 有形文化財.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人間国宝の作品を見ました。","romaji":"Ningen kokuhō no sakuhin o mimashita.","german":"I saw the work of a living national treasure.","furigana":"にんげんこくほう|さくひん|み"},{"japanese":"あの陶芸家は去年、人間国宝に認定されました。","romaji":"Ano tougeika wa kyonen, ningen kokuhou ni nintei saremashita.","german":"That ceramic artist was certified as a living national treasure last year.","furigana":"とうげいか|きょねん|にんげんこくほう|にんてい"},{"japanese":"人間国宝の先生から直接、着物の染め方を習いました。","romaji":"Ningen kokuhou no sensei kara chokusetsu, kimono no somekata o naraimashita.","german":"I learned how to dye kimono directly from a teacher who is a living national treasure.","furigana":"にんげんこくほう|せんせい|ちょくせつ|きもの|そ|かた|なら"}],"notes":"人間国宝 (\"living national treasure\") is the popular name for persons whom the state has recognized as holders of an 重要無形文化財 - masters of, for example, pottery, dyeing, 能 or 歌舞伎. The official title is 重要無形文化財保持者, but in the news and daily life one almost always says 人間国宝. Typical: 人間国宝に認定される, 人間国宝の作品, 人間国宝の職人. It always refers to a person; an object or building is a 国宝.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"名勝を訪ねました。","romaji":"Meishō o tazunemashita.","german":"I visited a scenic spot.","furigana":"めいしょう|たず"},{"japanese":"この庭園は国の特別名勝に指定されています。","romaji":"Kono teien wa kuni no tokubetsu meishou ni shitei sareteimasu.","german":"This garden is designated as a Special Place of Scenic Beauty of the nation.","furigana":"ていえん|くに|とくべつめいしょう|してい"},{"japanese":"京都には名勝が多く、一日では回りきれません。","romaji":"Kyouto ni wa meishou ga ooku, ichinichi de wa mawarikiremasen.","german":"Kyoto has many scenic spots, and you can't see them all in one day.","furigana":"きょうと|めいしょう|おお|いちにち|まわ"}],"notes":"名勝 is a place famous for its beautiful scenery; in cultural property protection it is also an official category (国の名勝, 特別名勝) for gardens, gorges or coastal landscapes. The word is rather literary and is encountered on signs, in guidebooks and in 名勝地. In conversation, one mostly says 名所 or 景色のいい所; 名所 is more general and also includes buildings or cherry-blossom spots (桜の名所), while 名勝 emphasizes the landscape.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"史跡を見学しました。","romaji":"Shiseki o kengaku shimashita.","german":"I visited a historic site.","furigana":"しせき|けんがく"},{"japanese":"週末は、自転車で町の史跡めぐりをしました。","romaji":"Shuumatsu wa, jitensha de machi no shiseki meguri o shimashita.","german":"On the weekend, I took a bike tour of the town's historic sites.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|じてんしゃ|まち|しせき"},{"japanese":"この城跡は国の史跡に指定されています。","romaji":"Kono shiroato wa kuni no shiseki ni shitei sareteimasu.","german":"This castle ruin is designated a national historic site.","furigana":"しろあと|くに|しせき|してい"}],"notes":"史跡 is a historic site: a place where something historical happened or where remains of it are preserved, such as castle ruins, ancient burial mounds or battlefields. It is also an official category in cultural property protection (国の史跡, 特別史跡). Typical: 史跡を見学する, 史跡めぐり \"tour of historic sites\", 史跡に指定される. Unlike 名勝 (scenic beauty), it is about history; a building that is still standing is rather called 歴史的建造物.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"天然記念物に指定されています。","romaji":"Tennen kinenbutsu ni shitei sareteimasu.","german":"Es ist zum Naturdenkmal erklärt.","furigana":"てんねんきねんぶつ|してい"},{"japanese":"この大きな杉の木は、国の天然記念物です。","romaji":"Kono ookina sugi no ki wa, kuni no tennen kinenbutsu desu.","german":"Diese große Zeder ist ein nationales Naturdenkmal.","furigana":"おお|すぎ|き|くに|てんねんきねんぶつ"},{"japanese":"カモシカは天然記念物なので、捕まえてはいけません。","romaji":"Kamoshika wa tennen kinenbutsu na node, tsukamaete wa ikemasen.","german":"Der Japanische Serau ist eine geschützte Tierart, deshalb darf man ihn nicht fangen.","furigana":"てんねんきねんぶつ|つか"}],"notes":"天然記念物 sind nach japanischem Recht geschützte Naturgüter: nicht nur Orte, Felsen und alte Bäume wie ein deutsches Naturdenkmal, sondern auch seltene Tier- und Pflanzenarten, etwa der Japanische Ibis (トキ) oder der Japanische Serau (カモシカ). Typisch: 天然記念物に指定される, 国の天然記念物, 特別天然記念物 als höchste Stufe. Ein geschütztes Tier darf man deshalb nicht fangen oder jagen. Formelles Wort aus Verwaltung, Schule und Nachrichten.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"社会貢献活動をしています。","romaji":"Shakai kōken katsudō o shiteimasu.","german":"I do community service activities.","furigana":"しゃかいこうけんかつどう"},{"japanese":"仕事を通して社会貢献がしたいと思っています。","romaji":"Shigoto o tooshite shakai kouken ga shitai to omotteimasu.","german":"I want to contribute to society through my work.","furigana":"しごと|とお|しゃかいこうけん|おも"},{"japanese":"この会社は売り上げの一部を寄付して、社会貢献をしています。","romaji":"Kono kaisha wa uriage no ichibu o kifu shite, shakai kouken o shiteimasu.","german":"This company donates part of its sales and thus contributes to society.","furigana":"かいしゃ|う|あ|いちぶ|きふ|しゃかいこうけん"}],"notes":"社会貢献 is a \"contribution to society\" - through volunteer work, donations or the social engagement of companies (企業の社会貢献, roughly corresponding to CSR). Typical: 社会貢献をする, 社会貢献活動, 社会貢献につながる. As a verb one uses 貢献する with に: 社会に貢献する. The word is neutral to formal and often appears in job interviews, applications and corporate mission statements.","levelNote":"Compound: 社会 (N4) + 貢献 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"奉仕活動に参加します。","romaji":"Hōshi katsudō ni sanka shimasu.","german":"I will take part in community service.","furigana":"ほうしかつどう|さんか"},{"japanese":"日曜日の朝、近所の公園で奉仕活動としてごみを拾いました。","romaji":"Nichiyoubi no asa, kinjo no kouen de houshi katsudou to shite gomi o hiroimashita.","german":"On Sunday morning, I picked up trash in the neighborhood park as community service.","furigana":"にちようび|あさ|きんじょ|こうえん|ほうしかつどう|ひろ"},{"japanese":"うちの高校では、一年に一回奉仕活動があります。","romaji":"Uchi no koukou de wa, ichinen ni ikkai houshi katsudou ga arimasu.","german":"At our high school, there is community service once a year.","furigana":"こうこう|いちねん|いっかいほうしかつどう"}],"notes":"奉仕活動 is unpaid work for the community, such as picking up trash, park maintenance or helping at a nursing home. 奉仕 means \"to serve\" and sounds somewhat formal and traditional; you often hear it in schools, where students do 奉仕活動 as part of class, as well as in clubs and religious communities. In general usage, ボランティア and ボランティア活動 are more common and more casual. Typical: 奉仕活動に参加する, 奉仕活動をする, 地域の奉仕活動.","levelNote":"Compound: 奉仕 (N1) + 活動 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"街頭で募金活動をしています。","romaji":"Gaitō de bokin katsudō o shiteimasu.","german":"We are doing a fundraising drive on the street.","furigana":"がいとう|ぼきんかつどう"},{"japanese":"地震で困っている人たちのために、駅前で募金を集めました。","romaji":"Jishin de komatteiru hitotachi no tame ni, ekimae de bokin o atsumemashita.","german":"We collected donations in front of the station for people in trouble because of the earthquake.","furigana":"じしん|こま|ひと|えきまえ|ぼきん|あつ"},{"japanese":"コンビニのレジの横の箱に、おつりを募金しました。","romaji":"Konbini no reji no yoko no hako ni, otsuri o bokin shimashita.","german":"I donated my change to the box next to the convenience store register.","furigana":"よこ|はこ|ぼきん"}],"notes":"募金 primarily means collecting donations (募 \"to call for, to collect\" + 金 \"money\"): 募金活動 \"fundraising drive\", 街頭募金 \"street fundraising\", 募金を集める, 募金箱 \"donation box\". Colloquially, however, 募金する is also used in the sense of \"to donate (into a collection box)\": おつりを募金する. Larger or formal donations to organizations are rather called 寄付 (寄付する). The word is heard very often especially after natural disasters.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"生活保護を申請しました。","romaji":"Seikatsu hogo o shinsei shimashita.","german":"I applied for public assistance.","furigana":"せいかつほご|しんせい"},{"japanese":"病気で働けなくなり、生活保護を受けています。","romaji":"Byouki de hatarakenaku nari, seikatsu hogo o uketeimasu.","german":"I became unable to work because of illness, and I am receiving public assistance.","furigana":"びょうき|はたら|せいかつほご|う"},{"japanese":"生活保護を受ける人の数が年々増えています。","romaji":"Seikatsu hogo o ukeru hito no kazu ga nennen fueteimasu.","german":"The number of people receiving public assistance is increasing year by year.","furigana":"せいかつほご|う|ひと|かず|ねんねんふ"}],"notes":"生活保護 is Japan's state social assistance, which secures a minimum standard of living for people without sufficient income. Typical: 生活保護を受ける \"to receive public assistance\", 生活保護を申請する, 生活保護費 \"welfare benefits\", 生活保護世帯. Unemployment benefit is something different (colloquially 失業保険, officially from 雇用保険), as is the pension (年金). The topic carries a certain stigma in Japan, so people tend to talk about it with restraint.","levelNote":"Compound: 生活 (N5) + 保護 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"障害者の権利を守ります。","romaji":"Shōgaisha no kenri o mamorimasu.","german":"We protect the rights of people with disabilities.","furigana":"しょうがいしゃ|けんり|まも"},{"japanese":"駅のエレベーターは、障害者やお年寄りにとって大切です。","romaji":"Eki no erebeetaa wa, shougaisha ya otoshiyori ni totte taisetsu desu.","german":"Elevators in stations are important for people with disabilities and the elderly.","furigana":"えき|しょうがいしゃ|としよ|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"この会社は障害者の雇用に力を入れています。","romaji":"Kono kaisha wa shougaisha no koyou ni chikara o ireteimasu.","german":"This company is focusing on employing people with disabilities.","furigana":"かいしゃ|しょうがいしゃ|こよう|ちから|い"}],"notes":"障害者 is the standard word for \"person with a disability\" in laws, government offices and the news: 障害者手帳 \"disability certificate\", 障害者用トイレ, 身体障害者. Because the character 害 \"harm\" has a negative effect, many government offices and media write 障がい者, more rarely 障碍者. In polite conversation about a particular person, one prefers to say 障害のある方 or 体の不自由な方. 障害 alone can also mean \"obstacle, disruption\" (通信障害 \"network outage\").","levelNote":"Compound: 障害 (N4) + 者 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"バリアフリーの施設が増えています。","romaji":"Baria furī no shisetsu ga fueteimasu.","german":"Barrier-free facilities are increasing.","furigana":"しせつ|ふ"},{"japanese":"両親のために、家をバリアフリーにリフォームしました。","romaji":"Ryoushin no tame ni, ie o baria furii ni rifoomu shimashita.","german":"For my parents, we renovated the house to be barrier-free.","furigana":"りょうしん|いえ"},{"japanese":"このホテルはバリアフリーなので、車いすでも安心して泊まれます。","romaji":"Kono hoteru wa baria furii na node, kurumaisu demo anshin shite tomaremasu.","german":"This hotel is barrier-free, so even wheelchair users can stay without worry.","furigana":"くるま|あんしん|と"}],"notes":"バリアフリー describes buildings, transportation or homes without obstacles for older people, wheelchair users or people with disabilities - that is, without steps, with ramps, elevators and wide doors. Grammatically it is a noun used adjectivally with の: バリアフリーの家／トイレ; also バリアフリー化 \"barrier-free conversion\" and バリアフリーにする. In daily life one also simply says 段差がない \"without steps\". A more broadly defined, related concept is ユニバーサルデザイン.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ユニバーサルデザインを取り入れます。","romaji":"Yunibāsaru dezain o toriiremasu.","german":"We incorporate universal design.","furigana":"と|い"},{"japanese":"この駅は、ユニバーサルデザインの考え方で作られています。","romaji":"Kono eki wa, yunibāsaru dezain no kangaekata de tsukurarete imasu.","german":"This station was built on the idea of universal design.","furigana":"えき|かんが|かた|つく"},{"japanese":"シャンプーのボトルのギザギザも、ユニバーサルデザインの一つです。","romaji":"Shanpū no botoru no gizagiza mo, yunibāsaru dezain no hitotsu desu.","german":"The ridges on shampoo bottles are also an example of universal design.","furigana":"ひと"}],"notes":"ユニバーサルデザイン means design that is usable by everyone from the start, regardless of age, disability or language. By contrast, バリアフリー means removing existing obstacles for particular groups after the fact. The word is encountered with stations, public buildings and everyday products, often abbreviated as UD. Typical are ユニバーサルデザインを取り入れる and ユニバーサルデザインの〜.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"手話を学んでいます。","romaji":"Shuwa o manande imasu.","german":"I'm learning sign language.","furigana":"しゅわ|まな"},{"japanese":"テレビのニュースに、手話の通訳がついています。","romaji":"Terebi no nyuusu ni, shuwa no tsuuyaku ga tsuite imasu.","german":"The TV news comes with sign language interpretation.","furigana":"しゅわ|つうやく"},{"japanese":"耳の聞こえない友達と、手話で話しました。","romaji":"Mimi no kikoenai tomodachi to, shuwa de hanashimashita.","german":"I talked in sign language with a deaf friend.","furigana":"みみ|き|ともだち|しゅわ|はな"}],"notes":"手話 is \"sign language\". Typical are 手話で話す, 手話を使う and 手話を習う; the person who interprets is a 手話通訳者. Japanese Sign Language (日本手話) is a language in its own right with its own grammar; spelling with the fingers is called 指文字. General gestures while speaking, by contrast, are called ジェスチャー or 身振り手振り.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"点字ブロックの上に立たないでください。","romaji":"Tenji burokku no ue ni tatanaide kudasai.","german":"Please do not stand on the tactile paving.","furigana":"てんじ|うえ|た"},{"japanese":"エレベーターのボタンには点字が付いています。","romaji":"Erebeetaa no botan ni wa tenji ga tsuiteimasu.","german":"The elevator buttons have braille on them.","furigana":"てんじ|つ"},{"japanese":"祖父は目が悪くなってから、点字を習い始めました。","romaji":"Sofu wa me ga waruku natte kara, tenji o naraihajimemashita.","german":"After his eyesight got worse, my grandfather started learning braille.","furigana":"そふ|め|わる|てんじ|なら|はじ"}],"notes":"点字 is braille, literally \"dot script\"; Japanese braille represents the kana, not the kanji. Typical: 点字を読む, 点字で書かれた本, 点字の案内. Very common is 点字ブロック: the yellow, ridged guide strips on sidewalks and station platforms, on which one should not stand or park bicycles. Of people, one politely says 目の不自由な方.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"多文化共生社会を目指します。","romaji":"Tabunka kyōsei shakai o mezashimasu.","german":"We aim for a multicultural society.","furigana":"たぶんかきょうせいしゃかい|めざ"},{"japanese":"この町では、多文化共生のために日本語教室を開いています。","romaji":"Kono machi de wa, tabunka kyousei no tame ni nihongo kyoushitsu o hiraiteimasu.","german":"This town runs Japanese classes to promote multicultural coexistence.","furigana":"まち|たぶんかきょうせい|にほんごきょうしつ|ひら"},{"japanese":"外国人の住民が増えて、多文化共生が大きなテーマになっています。","romaji":"Gaikokujin no juumin ga fuete, tabunka kyousei ga ookina teema ni natteimasu.","german":"As the number of foreign residents grows, multicultural coexistence has become a major issue.","furigana":"がいこくじん|じゅうみん|ふ|たぶんかきょうせい|おお"}],"notes":"多文化共生 means that people from different cultural and national backgrounds live together as equal members of one society. 共生 means \"living together, coexistence\" (in biology, \"symbiosis\"). The word is a buzzword in local politics and administration: 多文化共生社会, 多文化共生を進める／推進する, 多文化共生センター. In conversation it sounds official; casually, people tend to say 外国人と一緒に暮らす.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"異文化理解が大切です。","romaji":"Ibunka rikai ga taisetsu desu.","german":"Intercultural understanding is important.","furigana":"いぶんかりかい|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"留学は異文化理解を深めるいい機会です。","romaji":"Ryuugaku wa ibunka rikai o fukameru ii kikai desu.","german":"Studying abroad is a good opportunity to deepen your understanding of other cultures.","furigana":"りゅうがく|いぶんかりかい|ふか|きかい"},{"japanese":"新入社員向けに、異文化理解の研修が行われました。","romaji":"Shinnyuu shain muke ni, ibunka rikai no kenshuu ga okonawaremashita.","german":"A training course on intercultural understanding was held for the new employees.","furigana":"しんにゅうしゃいんむ|いぶんかりかい|けんしゅう|おこな"}],"notes":"異文化理解 is the understanding of other, unfamiliar cultures: 異文化 \"other culture\" + 理解 \"understanding\". The word is typical of education, exchange programs and corporate training: 異文化理解を深める, 異文化理解の授業, 異文化理解に役立つ. Related terms are 異文化コミュニケーション \"intercultural communication\" and 異文化交流 \"cultural exchange\". It is rather formal and matter-of-fact; in casual conversation people say 外国の文化を理解する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本に帰化しました。","romaji":"Nihon ni kika shimashita.","german":"I became a naturalized Japanese citizen.","furigana":"にほん|きか"},{"japanese":"帰化の手続きには一年ぐらいかかるそうです。","romaji":"Kika no tetsuzuki ni wa ichinen gurai kakaru sou desu.","german":"I hear the naturalization procedure takes about a year.","furigana":"きか|てつづ|いちねん"},{"japanese":"あの選手はブラジル出身ですが、帰化して日本代表になりました。","romaji":"Ano senshu wa Burajiru shusshin desu ga, kika shite Nihon daihyou ni narimashita.","german":"That athlete is originally from Brazil, but he was naturalized and now represents Japan.","furigana":"せんしゅ|しゅっしん|きか|にほんだいひょう"}],"notes":"帰化 is naturalization: a foreigner takes on the citizenship of a country. As an intransitive する-verb, the country is marked with に: 日本に帰化する; related are 帰化申請 \"naturalization application\" and 帰化を申請する. In principle, anyone who becomes naturalized in Japan has to give up their previous citizenship - this distinguishes it from 永住権, where one remains a foreign national. In biology, 帰化 also refers to naturalized plants and animals (帰化植物).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"在留資格を更新します。","romaji":"Zairyū shikaku o kōshin shimasu.","german":"I am renewing my residence status.","furigana":"ざいりゅうしかく|こうしん"},{"japanese":"留学生が卒業後に日本で働くには、在留資格を変更しなければなりません。","romaji":"Ryuugakusei ga sotsugyougo ni Nihon de hataraku ni wa, zairyuu shikaku o henkou shinakereba narimasen.","german":"To work in Japan after graduating, international students have to change their residence status.","furigana":"りゅうがくせい|そつぎょうご|にほん|はたら|ざいりゅうしかく|へんこう"},{"japanese":"アルバイトを始める前に、在留カードで在留資格を確認されました。","romaji":"Arubaito o hajimeru mae ni, zairyuu kaado de zairyuu shikaku o kakunin saremashita.","german":"Before I started my part-time job, my residence status was checked against my residence card.","furigana":"はじ|まえ|ざいりゅう|ざいりゅうしかく|かくにん"}],"notes":"在留資格 is a foreigner's residence status in Japan, that is, the legal category under which one may live in the country (e.g. 留学, 家族滞在, 永住者). In everyday speech people often just say ビザ, although strictly speaking a visa only concerns entry into the country. Typical: 在留資格を取得する／変更する, 在留資格の確認; officially one extends the 在留期間 \"period of stay\", but in daily life people also speak of 在留資格の更新. The status is shown on the 在留カード \"residence card\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"永住権を取得しました。","romaji":"Eijūken o shutoku shimashita.","german":"I obtained permanent residency.","furigana":"えいじゅうけん|しゅとく"},{"japanese":"日本に十年以上住んでいるので、永住権を申請しようと思っています。","romaji":"Nihon ni juunen ijou sundeiru node, eijuuken o shinsei shiyou to omotteimasu.","german":"Since I have lived in Japan for more than ten years, I am thinking of applying for permanent residency.","furigana":"にほん|じゅうねんいじょうす|えいじゅうけん|しんせい|おも"},{"japanese":"永住権があれば、ビザを更新しなくてもいいです。","romaji":"Eijuuken ga areba, biza o koushin shinakute mo ii desu.","german":"With permanent residency, you don't have to renew your visa.","furigana":"えいじゅうけん|こうしん"}],"notes":"永住権 is the right to reside permanently in a country without taking on its citizenship. In Japan, the official equivalent is the residence status 永住者; in everyday speech people say 永住権 or 永住ビザ. Typical: 永住権を取る／取得する, 永住権を申請する, 永住権が下りる \"is granted\". Unlike after a 帰化, you keep your own nationality, but you must still carry the 在留カード.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"政治亡命を求めました。","romaji":"Seiji bōmei o motomemashita.","german":"He asked for political asylum.","furigana":"せいじぼうめい|もと"},{"japanese":"その作家は戦争中にフランスへ亡命しました。","romaji":"Sono sakka wa sensouchuu ni Furansu e boumei shimashita.","german":"The writer went into exile in France during the war.","furigana":"さっか|せんそうちゅう|ぼうめい"},{"japanese":"大会に参加した選手が、そのまま外国に亡命したというニュースがありました。","romaji":"Taikai ni sanka shita senshu ga, sono mama gaikoku ni boumei shita to iu nyuusu ga arimashita.","german":"There was news that an athlete who had taken part in a tournament had defected abroad on the spot.","furigana":"たいかい|さんか|せんしゅ|がいこく|ぼうめい"}],"notes":"亡命 means fleeing one's own country for political or religious reasons and seeking protection abroad. As an intransitive する-verb, the destination country is marked with に or へ: アメリカに亡命する; also 亡命を求める \"to ask for asylum\", 亡命政府 \"government in exile\", 亡命者. The word comes from the news and from history and stresses the political flight of individuals. People who flee war or persecution in large numbers, on the other hand, are called 難民 \"refugees\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"国交を正常化しました。","romaji":"Kokkō o seijōka shimashita.","german":"They normalized diplomatic relations.","furigana":"こっこう|せいじょうか"},{"japanese":"日本と中国は1972年に国交を結びました。","romaji":"Nihon to Chuugoku wa 1972-nen ni kokkou o musubimashita.","german":"Japan and China established diplomatic relations in 1972.","furigana":"にほん|ちゅうごく|ねん|こっこう|むす"},{"japanese":"その二つの国は今も国交がありません。","romaji":"Sono futatsu no kuni wa ima mo kokkou ga arimasen.","german":"Those two countries still have no diplomatic relations today.","furigana":"ふた|くに|いま|こっこう"}],"notes":"国交 means the official diplomatic relations between two states; 国 \"state\" + 交 \"intercourse, exchange\". Fixed phrases: 国交を結ぶ \"to establish\", 国交を断絶する \"to sever\", 国交を回復する／正常化する \"to restore / normalize\", 国交がない. 国交 refers to the formal status; general relations between countries are called 関係 (日中関係), and cultural exchange 交流. A typical word from the news and history class.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"経済制裁を実施します。","romaji":"Keizai seisai o jisshi shimasu.","german":"We will impose economic sanctions.","furigana":"けいざいせいさい|じっし"},{"japanese":"国連はその国に新しい制裁を加えることを決めました。","romaji":"Kokuren wa sono kuni ni atarashii seisai o kuwaeru koto o kimemashita.","german":"The United Nations decided to impose new sanctions on that country.","furigana":"こくれん|くに|あたら|せいさい|くわ|き"},{"japanese":"ルールを破った選手には、チームから厳しい制裁がありました。","romaji":"Ruuru o yabutta senshu ni wa, chiimu kara kibishii seisai ga arimashita.","german":"The player who broke the rules received a severe penalty from the team.","furigana":"やぶ|せんしゅ|きび|せいさい"}],"notes":"制裁 is a punitive measure against someone who has broken rules. In the news it mostly concerns international politics: 経済制裁 \"economic sanctions\", 制裁を加える／科す \"to impose\", 制裁を解除する \"to lift\". It can also mean a punishment within a group, e.g. 社会的制裁 \"social ostracism\". For punishments by courts one says 刑罰, and for simple punishments in everyday life 罰. As a transitive する-verb (〜を制裁する) it is possible, but it mostly appears as a noun.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"停戦合意に達しました。","romaji":"Teisen gōi ni tasshimashita.","german":"A ceasefire agreement was reached.","furigana":"ていせんごうい|たっ"},{"japanese":"両国は三日間停戦することに合意しました。","romaji":"Ryoukoku wa mikkakan teisen suru koto ni goui shimashita.","german":"The two countries agreed to a three-day ceasefire.","furigana":"りょうこく|みっかかんていせん|ごうい"},{"japanese":"停戦が破られて、また戦闘が始まりました。","romaji":"Teisen ga yaburarete, mata sentou ga hajimarimashita.","german":"The ceasefire was broken, and fighting started again.","furigana":"ていせん|やぶ|せんとう|はじ"}],"notes":"停戦 is the cessation of hostilities, i.e. \"ceasefire, truce\". Typical: 停戦する, 停戦に合意する, 停戦合意, 停戦協定, 停戦を呼びかける, 停戦が破られる. A 停戦 is often provisional; the final end of a war is 終戦 (for Japan especially 1945) or a 平和条約 \"peace treaty\". The similar 休戦 sounds more like a pause for a limited time; in today's news 停戦 is more common.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"平和条約を締結しました。","romaji":"Heiwa jōyaku o teiketsu shimashita.","german":"They concluded a peace treaty.","furigana":"へいわじょうやく|ていけつ"},{"japanese":"日本とロシアの間には、まだ平和条約がありません。","romaji":"Nihon to Roshia no aida ni wa, mada heiwa jouyaku ga arimasen.","german":"Japan and Russia still do not have a peace treaty between them.","furigana":"にほん|あいだ|へいわじょうやく"},{"japanese":"歴史の授業で、戦争のあとに結ばれた平和条約について学びました。","romaji":"Rekishi no jugyou de, sensou no ato ni musubareta heiwa jouyaku ni tsuite manabimashita.","german":"In history class, I learned about the peace treaty concluded after the war.","furigana":"れきし|じゅぎょう|せんそう|むす|へいわじょうやく|まな"}],"notes":"平和条約 is a peace treaty that officially ends a war between states. 条約 means \"(international) treaty\", as opposed to 契約 for private contracts. Typical: 平和条約を結ぶ／締結する, 平和条約に署名する. Well known are the サンフランシスコ平和条約 of 1951 and the peace treaty between Japan and Russia, which is still missing today. A 停戦, by contrast, is only a provisional ceasefire.","levelNote":"Compound: 平和 (N4) + 条約 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"国際協力を推進します。","romaji":"Kokusai kyōryoku o suishin shimasu.","german":"We promote international cooperation.","furigana":"こくさいきょうりょく|すいしん"},{"japanese":"将来は国際協力の仕事をしたいです。","romaji":"Shourai wa kokusai kyouryoku no shigoto o shitai desu.","german":"In the future I want to work in international cooperation.","furigana":"しょうらい|こくさいきょうりょく|しごと"},{"japanese":"地球温暖化の問題は、国際協力がなければ解決できません。","romaji":"Chikyuu ondanka no mondai wa, kokusai kyouryoku ga nakereba kaiketsu dekimasen.","german":"The problem of global warming cannot be solved without international cooperation.","furigana":"ちきゅうおんだんか|もんだい|こくさいきょうりょく|かいけつ"}],"notes":"国際協力 is cooperation between countries, especially support for developing countries by the state, NGOs or volunteers. Typical: 国際協力を進める／推進する, 国際協力に関わる, 国際協力の仕事; the government agency JICA is called 国際協力機構. 協力 means \"cooperation, help\" and otherwise takes に: 調査に協力する. A neutral, somewhat formal word from politics, education and the working world.","levelNote":"Compound: 国際 (N5) + 協力 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"開発援助を行います。","romaji":"Kaihatsu enjo o okonaimasu.","german":"We will provide development aid.","furigana":"かいはつえんじょ|おこな"},{"japanese":"日本の開発援助で、この国に新しい橋が作られました。","romaji":"Nihon no kaihatsu enjo de, kono kuni ni atarashii hashi ga tsukuraremashita.","german":"Thanks to Japanese development aid, a new bridge was built in this country.","furigana":"にほん|かいはつえんじょ|くに|あたら|はし|つく"},{"japanese":"開発援助のお金が、本当に困っている人に届いているか調べる必要があります。","romaji":"Kaihatsu enjo no okane ga, hontou ni komatteiru hito ni todoiteiru ka shiraberu hitsuyou ga arimasu.","german":"We need to check whether the development aid money is really reaching the people who are truly in need.","furigana":"かいはつえんじょ|かね|ほんとう|こま|ひと|とど|しら|ひつよう"}],"notes":"開発援助 is \"development aid\": financial or technical support from richer countries for the economic and social development of poorer countries. Best known is 政府開発援助 (ODA), Japan's government development aid. Typical: 開発援助を行う, 開発援助を受ける, 開発援助の予算. 援助 alone means \"help, support\" more generally (経済援助, 食料援助), and 開発 here means the development of a country, not product development. A formal word from politics and the news.","levelNote":"Compound: 開発 (N1) + 援助 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"技術移転を進めています。","romaji":"Gijutsu iten o susumeteimasu.","german":"We are promoting technology transfer.","furigana":"ぎじゅついてん|すす"},{"japanese":"工場を海外に作るときは、技術移転が問題になることがあります。","romaji":"Koujou o kaigai ni tsukuru toki wa, gijutsu iten ga mondai ni naru koto ga arimasu.","german":"When building a factory overseas, technology transfer can become an issue.","furigana":"こうじょう|かいがい|つく|ぎじゅついてん|もんだい"},{"japanese":"大学の研究成果を企業に技術移転して、新しい製品が生まれました。","romaji":"Daigaku no kenkyuu seika o kigyou ni gijutsu iten shite, atarashii seihin ga umaremashita.","german":"Research results from the university were transferred to a company, and a new product was born.","furigana":"だいがく|けんきゅうせいか|きぎょう|ぎじゅついてん|あたら|せいひん|う"}],"notes":"技術移転 refers to the passing on of technology and know-how, for example from industrialized to developing countries, from a company to a partner company abroad, or from universities to businesses. 移転 means \"relocation, transfer\" and is otherwise also used for moving an office (事務所を移転する). Typical: 技術移転を進める／行う, 海外への技術移転. A technical word from business and politics; in conversation one says more simply 技術を伝える／教える.","levelNote":"Compound: 技術 (N4) + 移転 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"単位を取りました。","romaji":"Tan'i o torimashita.","german":"I earned the credits.","furigana":"たんい|と"},{"japanese":"この授業の単位を落とすと、卒業できません。","romaji":"Kono jugyou no tan'i o otosu to, sotsugyou dekimasen.","german":"If I fail this course, I won't be able to graduate.","furigana":"じゅぎょう|たんい|お|そつぎょう"},{"japanese":"日本では、長さの単位にメートルを使います。","romaji":"Nihon de wa, nagasa no tan'i ni meetoru o tsukaimasu.","german":"In Japan, the meter is used as the unit of length.","furigana":"にほん|なが|たんい|つか"}],"notes":"単位 has two main meanings: \"unit of measurement\" (長さの単位はメートル) and, at school or university, \"credit\". Typical for students: 単位を取る \"to pass a course, earn credits\", 単位を落とす \"to fail\", 単位が足りない \"to not have enough credits\". As a suffix, 〜単位 means \"in units of, per ...\": 千円単位, グループ単位で行動する. Not to be confused with 単語 \"word, vocabulary item\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"卒論のテーマを決めました。","romaji":"Sotsuron no tēma o kimemashita.","german":"I decided on the topic of my graduation thesis.","furigana":"そつろん|き"},{"japanese":"卒論の締め切りまで、あと一週間しかありません。","romaji":"Sotsuron no shimekiri made, ato isshuukan shika arimasen.","german":"There is only one week left until the thesis deadline.","furigana":"そつろん|し|き|いっしゅうかん"},{"japanese":"卒論で、日本の若者の言葉について調べました。","romaji":"Sotsuron de, Nihon no wakamono no kotoba ni tsuite shirabemashita.","german":"In my graduation thesis, I researched the language of young people in Japan.","furigana":"そつろん|にほん|わかもの|ことば|しら"}],"notes":"卒論 is the common abbreviation of 卒業論文, the thesis written at the end of a bachelor's degree (at Japanese universities usually in the fourth year). Typical: 卒論を書く, 卒論のテーマ, 卒論を提出する, 卒論発表 \"presentation of the thesis\". The short form is completely normal among students and instructors; official documents use 卒業論文. Master's theses are called 修士論文 (short: 修論), doctoral dissertations 博士論文.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"就活中です。","romaji":"Shūkatsuchū desu.","german":"I'm in the middle of job hunting.","furigana":"しゅうかつちゅう"},{"japanese":"就活のために、黒いスーツを買いました。","romaji":"Shuukatsu no tame ni, kuroi suutsu o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a black suit for job hunting.","furigana":"しゅうかつ|くろ|か"},{"japanese":"三年生の冬から就活を始める人が多いです。","romaji":"Sannensei no fuyu kara shuukatsu o hajimeru hito ga ooi desu.","german":"Many people start job hunting in the winter of their third year.","furigana":"さんねんせい|ふゆ|しゅうかつ|はじ|ひと|おお"}],"notes":"就活 is the short form of 就職活動, the very rigidly structured job hunt that students go through in Japan before graduating: company information sessions, application forms (エントリーシート), tests and several interviews. Typical: 就活をする, 就活中, 就活生 \"students job hunting\", 就活スーツ. The short form is colloquial but used everywhere. Working people who change jobs, by contrast, do 転職活動; following the same pattern there is 婚活 \"spouse hunting\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"内定をもらいました。","romaji":"Naitei o moraimashita.","german":"I received an informal job offer.","furigana":"ないてい"},{"japanese":"第一志望の会社から内定が出ました。","romaji":"Daiichi shibou no kaisha kara naitei ga demashita.","german":"I got an offer from my first-choice company.","furigana":"だいいちしぼう|かいしゃ|ないてい|で"},{"japanese":"景気が悪くなって、内定を取り消された学生もいます。","romaji":"Keiki ga waruku natte, naitei o torikesareta gakusei mo imasu.","german":"Because of the poor economy, some students had their offers withdrawn.","furigana":"けいき|わる|ないてい|と|け|がくせい"}],"notes":"内定 is a provisional, not yet official offer, above all a company's job offer to students who will start only after graduation, in April. Typical: 内定をもらう／取る, 内定が出る, 内定者, 内定式 \"ceremony for future employees\"; the opposite is 内定取り消し \"withdrawal of the offer\". The formal hiring is called 採用, and 内定 is the step before it. In principle it can be used for other provisional decisions as well (人事が内定する), but in daily life it is almost always in the 就活 context.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"営業部に配属されました。","romaji":"Eigyōbu ni haizoku saremashita.","german":"I was assigned to the sales department.","furigana":"えいぎょうぶ|はいぞく"},{"japanese":"研修が終わったら、配属先が発表されます。","romaji":"Kenshuu ga owattara, haizokusaki ga happyou saremasu.","german":"Once the training is over, the assignments will be announced.","furigana":"けんしゅう|お|はいぞくさき|はっぴょう"},{"japanese":"希望していた部署に配属されて、とてもうれしいです。","romaji":"Kibou shiteita busho ni haizoku sarete, totemo ureshii desu.","german":"I'm very happy to have been assigned to the department I wanted.","furigana":"きぼう|ぶしょ|はいぞく"}],"notes":"配属 is the assignment of a person to a department, post or unit, typically after being hired. Almost always in the passive: 営業部に配属される \"to be assigned to the sales department\"; in the active, the company says 新人を総務部に配属する. Other combinations: 配属先 \"assigned unit\", 配属が決まる. A later move to another department is called 異動, a transfer to another location 転勤.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子会社に出向しています。","romaji":"Kogaisha ni shukkō shiteimasu.","german":"I'm on secondment to a subsidiary.","furigana":"こがいしゃ|しゅっこう"},{"japanese":"部長は来月から取引先に二年間出向することになった。","romaji":"Buchou wa raigetsu kara torihikisaki ni ninenkan shukkou suru koto ni natta.","german":"The department head will be seconded to a business partner for two years starting next month.","furigana":"ぶちょう|らいげつ|とりひきさき|にねんかんしゅっこう"},{"japanese":"出向先の会社は雰囲気がとてもいいです。","romaji":"Shukkousaki no kaisha wa fun'iki ga totemo ii desu.","german":"The atmosphere at the company I'm seconded to is very good.","furigana":"しゅっこうさき|かいしゃ|ふんいき"}],"notes":"出向 is the temporary secondment of an employee to another company, usually a subsidiary or a business partner; the contract with the original employer remains in place. As an intransitive する-verb, the destination is marked with に: 子会社に出向する; the company one works for is called 出向先. To be distinguished from 転勤 (\"transfer to another location of the same company\"), 転職 (\"job change\") and 派遣 (\"dispatch, temp work\"). Not to be confused with the verb 出向く (でむく, \"to go to a place\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地方に左遷されました。","romaji":"Chihō ni sasen saremashita.","german":"I was transferred to a provincial branch as a demotion.","furigana":"ちほう|させん"},{"japanese":"社長に反対した彼は、小さな支店に左遷された。","romaji":"Shachou ni hantai shita kare wa, chiisana shiten ni sasen sareta.","german":"Because he opposed the president, he was sent down to a small branch office.","furigana":"しゃちょう|はんたい|かれ|ちい|してん|させん"},{"japanese":"これは昇進じゃなくて、左遷だよ。","romaji":"Kore wa shoushin ja nakute, sasen da yo.","german":"This isn't a promotion, it's a demotion.","furigana":"しょうしん|させん"}],"notes":"左遷 is a transfer to a worse position or an unattractive place, in effect a punitive transfer. It is usually used in the passive from the point of view of the person affected: 地方に左遷される, 支店に左遷された. The opposite is 栄転 (\"transfer with promotion\"); neutral is 転勤, and a formal demotion in rank is called 降格. The word always sounds negative and is often used with pity or irony.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"来年定年を迎えます。","romaji":"Rainen teinen o mukaemasu.","german":"I will reach retirement age next year.","furigana":"らいねんていねん|むか"},{"japanese":"父は定年後、毎日庭で野菜を育てています。","romaji":"Chichi wa teinengo, mainichi niwa de yasai o sodatete imasu.","german":"After retirement, my father grows vegetables in the garden every day.","furigana":"ちち|ていねんご|まいにちにわ|やさい|そだ"},{"japanese":"この会社の定年は六十五歳です。","romaji":"Kono kaisha no teinen wa rokujuugo-sai desu.","german":"The retirement age at this company is sixty-five.","furigana":"かいしゃ|ていねん|ろくじゅうごさい"}],"notes":"定年 is the fixed age limit at which one leaves the company, and also the leaving itself. Typical are 定年を迎える, 定年になる, 定年退職する (\"to retire\") as well as 定年後 (\"after retirement\") and 定年延長 (\"raising of the age limit\"). 退職 is generally \"to leave one's post\" (also early), 引退 \"to withdraw\" (e.g. athletes); the pension as money is called 年金. The older spelling 停年 is only rarely seen now.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これは私の天職です。","romaji":"Kore wa watashi no tenshoku desu.","german":"This is my calling.","furigana":"わたし|てんしょく"},{"japanese":"子どもと遊ぶのが好きな彼女にとって、保育士は天職だ。","romaji":"Kodomo to asobu no ga suki na kanojo ni totte, hoikushi wa tenshoku da.","german":"For her, who loves playing with children, being a nursery teacher is a true calling.","furigana":"こ|あそ|す|かのじょ|ほいくし|てんしょく"},{"japanese":"やっと自分の天職を見つけた気がします。","romaji":"Yatto jibun no tenshoku o mitsuketa ki ga shimasu.","german":"I feel I have finally found my calling.","furigana":"じぶん|てんしょく|み|き"}],"notes":"天職 is literally \"the occupation given by heaven\": work for which one is as if made. Typical are 〜は私の天職だ, 天職だと思う, 天職に出会う, 天職を見つける. Attention, homophone: 転職 (also てんしょく) means \"job change\" - in spoken language the context decides. Related is 生きがい (\"reason for living\"), which is not limited to a job.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仕事が私の生きがいです。","romaji":"Shigoto ga watashi no ikigai desu.","german":"Die Arbeit ist mein Lebenssinn.","furigana":"しごと|わたし|い"},{"japanese":"祖母は孫の成長を生きがいにしています。","romaji":"Sobo wa mago no seichou o ikigai ni shite imasu.","german":"Meine Großmutter lebt dafür, ihre Enkel aufwachsen zu sehen.","furigana":"そぼ|まご|せいちょう|い"},{"japanese":"退職してから、生きがいが見つからなくて困っている。","romaji":"Taishoku shite kara, ikigai ga mitsukaranakute komatte iru.","german":"Seit ich in Rente bin, finde ich nichts, wofür ich lebe, und weiß nicht weiter.","furigana":"たいしょく|い|み|こま"}],"notes":"生きがい (auch 生き甲斐) ist das, wofür man lebt und was dem Leben Sinn gibt – Arbeit, Familie, ein Hobby. Typisch sind 〜が生きがいだ, 〜を生きがいにする, 生きがいを感じる, 生きがいがない. Es ist stärker als 趣味 („Hobby“) oder 楽しみ („Freude“): Es geht um den Halt im Leben. Als „Ikigai“ ist das Wort auch im Deutschen bekannt, im Japanischen aber ein ganz alltägliches Wort.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"侘び寂びの美学を大切にします。","romaji":"Wabi sabi no bigaku o taisetsu ni shimasu.","german":"I cherish the aesthetics of wabi-sabi.","furigana":"わ|さ|びがく|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"古い茶室に入ると、侘び寂びを感じます。","romaji":"Furui chashitsu ni hairu to, wabi sabi o kanjimasu.","german":"When you enter an old tea room, you can feel wabi-sabi.","furigana":"ふる|ちゃしつ|はい|わ|さ|かん"},{"japanese":"外国人に侘び寂びを説明するのは難しい。","romaji":"Gaikokujin ni wabi sabi o setsumei suru no wa muzukashii.","german":"It is difficult to explain wabi-sabi to foreigners.","furigana":"がいこくじん|わ|さ|せつめい|むずか"}],"notes":"侘び寂び (often written in kana as わびさび) combines two aesthetic concepts: 侘び, plain, quiet simplicity and contentment with little, and 寂び, the beauty of age, patina and calm solitude. The word belongs to the world of the tea ceremony, gardens and haiku. Typical: 侘び寂びを感じる, 侘び寂びの世界, 侘び寂びがある. It is a rather abstract cultural concept that even Japanese people find hard to explain precisely.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"雅な文化に触れました。","romaji":"Miyabi na bunka ni furemashita.","german":"I experienced the elegant culture of the court.","furigana":"みやび|ぶんか|ふ"},{"japanese":"京都の祭りでは、平安時代の雅な衣装が見られます。","romaji":"Kyouto no matsuri de wa, Heian jidai no miyabi na ishou ga miraremasu.","german":"At festivals in Kyoto, you can see the elegant costumes of the Heian period.","furigana":"きょうと|まつ|へいあんじだい|みやび|いしょう|み"},{"japanese":"琴の雅な音色に心が落ち着いた。","romaji":"Koto no miyabi na neiro ni kokoro ga ochitsuita.","german":"The graceful sound of the koto calmed my heart.","furigana":"こと|みやび|ねいろ|こころ|お|つ"}],"notes":"雅 refers to the refined, courtly elegance of the old imperial court, especially of the Heian period and of Kyoto. It is mostly used as a な-adjective: 雅な衣装, 雅な音色; there is also 雅やか(な). More general words are 上品 (\"refined, cultured\") and 優雅 (\"elegant, graceful\", also for a luxurious lifestyle). The word sounds literary and is often used in advertising, names and descriptions of traditional culture.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"情緒のある風景です。","romaji":"Jōcho no aru fūkei desu.","german":"It is a scenic view full of charm.","furigana":"じょうちょ|ふうけい"},{"japanese":"夜の祇園は、昔ながらの情緒にあふれています。","romaji":"Yoru no Gion wa, mukashinagara no joucho ni afurete imasu.","german":"Gion at night is full of old-fashioned charm.","furigana":"よる||むかし|じょうちょ"},{"japanese":"最近、彼は情緒不安定で、すぐに怒る。","romaji":"Saikin, kare wa joucho fuantei de, sugu ni okoru.","german":"Lately he has been emotionally unstable and gets angry easily.","furigana":"さいきん|かれ|じょうちょふあんてい|おこ"}],"notes":"情緒 has two uses: first, the atmospheric charm or flair of a place, often nostalgic: 情緒がある, 情緒あふれる町, 下町情緒, 異国情緒. Second, in psychology, a person's emotional state: 情緒不安定 (\"emotionally unstable\"), 情緒教育. The original reading is じょうしょ; today じょうちょ is usual. 雰囲気 is neutral \"atmosphere\", whereas 情緒 sounds like poetic charm; for individual feelings one says 感情 or 気持ち.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"趣のある建物です。","romaji":"Omomuki no aru tatemono desu.","german":"It is a building with charm.","furigana":"おもむき|たてもの"},{"japanese":"雨の日の庭も、また違った趣があります。","romaji":"Ame no hi no niwa mo, mata chigatta omomuki ga arimasu.","german":"The garden on a rainy day has a different charm as well.","furigana":"あめ|ひ|にわ|ちが|おもむき"},{"japanese":"ご依頼の趣、承知いたしました。","romaji":"Goirai no omomuki, shouchi itashimashita.","german":"I have understood the content of your request.","furigana":"いらい|おもむき|しょうち"}],"notes":"趣 is mostly the quiet appeal or charm of something, often old and traditional: 趣がある, 趣のある建物, 違った趣. Very similar is 風情, while 情緒 rather means the flair of a place. In formal letters 趣 also means \"gist, content\" (ご依頼の趣, cf. 趣旨), and in literary language there is 趣を異にする (\"to differ\"). The kanji is known from 趣味 (\"hobby\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"奥ゆかしい女性です。","romaji":"Okuyukashii josei desu.","german":"She is a gracefully reserved woman.","furigana":"おく|じょせい"},{"japanese":"自分の成功を自慢しないところが、彼の奥ゆかしいところだ。","romaji":"Jibun no seikou o jiman shinai tokoro ga, kare no okuyukashii tokoro da.","german":"The fact that he doesn't boast about his success is part of his modest charm.","furigana":"じぶん|せいこう|じまん|かれ|おく"},{"japanese":"その旅館の奥ゆかしいおもてなしに感動しました。","romaji":"Sono ryokan no okuyukashii omotenashi ni kandou shimashita.","german":"I was moved by that inn's gracefully understated hospitality.","furigana":"りょかん|おく|かんどう"}],"notes":"奥ゆかしい praises a discreet, modest reserve behind which one senses depth and refinement - in people, their behavior or an atmosphere. It is always positive and sounds somewhat elevated. 控えめ is neutral \"restrained\", 上品 \"refined\", 遠慮がち \"hesitant, shy\". The word is almost always written in kana; 奥床しい is rare.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いとおしい存在です。","romaji":"Itōshii sonzai desu.","german":"She is a beloved presence.","furigana":"そんざい"},{"japanese":"眠っている赤ちゃんの顔を見ると、いとおしくてたまらない。","romaji":"Nemutte iru akachan no kao o miru to, itooshikute tamaranai.","german":"When I look at the sleeping baby's face, I'm overcome with tenderness.","furigana":"ねむ|あか|かお|み"},{"japanese":"家族と過ごす何気ない毎日が、いとおしく感じられる。","romaji":"Kazoku to sugosu nanigenai mainichi ga, itooshiku kanjirareru.","german":"Ordinary days spent with family feel precious.","furigana":"かぞく|す|なにげ|まいにち|かん"}],"notes":"いとおしい (also written 愛おしい) describes tender, protective affection for a loved person, a child or animal, but also for things and times (いとおしい日々). Typical is 〜がいとおしくてたまらない. It sounds emotional and somewhat literary; in everyday speech one more often says 可愛い or 大好き. Similar is 愛しい (いとしい); the older meaning \"pitiable\" is rare today.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"物悲しい秋の夕暮れです。","romaji":"Monoganashii aki no yūgure desu.","german":"It is a melancholy autumn dusk.","furigana":"ものがな|あき|ゆうぐ"},{"japanese":"どこからか物悲しいメロディーが聞こえてきた。","romaji":"Doko kara ka monoganashii merodii ga kikoete kita.","german":"A wistful melody could be heard from somewhere.","furigana":"ものがな|き"},{"japanese":"祭りが終わった後の町は、なんだか物悲しい。","romaji":"Matsuri ga owatta ato no machi wa, nandaka monoganashii.","german":"The town after the festival has ended feels somehow melancholy.","furigana":"まつ|お|あと|まち|ものがな"}],"notes":"The prefix 物- means \"somehow, vaguely\" here (cf. 物寂しい, 物足りない): 物悲しい is a diffuse wistfulness without a concrete reason. It describes above all moods, sounds, landscapes and seasons, classically autumn. 悲しい is concrete sorrow, 寂しい loneliness. The word sounds somewhat literary, but also occurs in conversation.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"もの寂しい風景です。","romaji":"Monosabishii fūkei desu.","german":"It is a desolate landscape.","furigana":"さび|ふうけい"},{"japanese":"秋の夕暮れは、なんだかもの寂しいですね。","romaji":"Aki no yuugure wa, nanda ka monosabishii desu ne.","german":"Autumn dusk feels somehow lonely, doesn't it?","furigana":"あき|ゆうぐ|さび"},{"japanese":"子どもたちが独立して、家の中がもの寂しくなった。","romaji":"Kodomotachi ga dokuritsu shite, ie no naka ga monosabishiku natta.","german":"After the children became independent, the house became lonely.","furigana":"こ|どくりつ|いえ|なか|さび"}],"notes":"もの寂しい is an い-adjective; the prefix もの〜 means \"somehow, vaguely\", as in もの悲しい or もの静か. It describes a wistful, desolate mood of landscapes, seasons, places or sounds, hardly the direct feeling of missing someone; for that one simply says 寂しい. It is also written 物寂しい or 物淋しい.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"空しい気持ちになりました。","romaji":"Munashii kimochi ni narimashita.","german":"I felt empty.","furigana":"むな|きも"},{"japanese":"必死に説得したが、努力は空しく終わった。","romaji":"Hisshi ni settoku shita ga, doryoku wa munashiku owatta.","german":"I tried desperately to persuade them, but my efforts ended in vain.","furigana":"ひっし|せっとく|どりょく|むな|お"},{"japanese":"一日中ゲームをしていたら、なんだか空しくなった。","romaji":"Ichinichijuu geemu o shite itara, nandaka munashiku natta.","german":"After playing games all day, I somehow felt empty.","furigana":"いちにちじゅう|むな"}],"notes":"空しい (today often written 虚しい for feelings, same reading) has two main meanings: the feeling of inner emptiness (空しい気持ち, 空しくなる) and futility (空しい努力, 努力が空しく終わる). Adverbially: 空しく時間が過ぎる (\"time passes uselessly\"). 寂しい, by contrast, is \"lonely\", and 無駄 is the matter-of-fact \"useless\". The kanji 空 is read むな- here, not そら or から.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"疎ましく感じます。","romaji":"Utomashiku kanjimasu.","german":"I find it off-putting.","furigana":"うと|かん"},{"japanese":"思春期には、親の注意が疎ましく思えるものだ。","romaji":"Shishunki ni wa, oya no chuui ga utomashiku omoeru mono da.","german":"In adolescence, one tends to find parents' warnings annoying.","furigana":"ししゅんき|おや|ちゅうい|うと|おも"},{"japanese":"何度も連絡してくる彼を、だんだん疎ましく思うようになった。","romaji":"Nando mo renraku shite kuru kare o, dandan utomashiku omou you ni natta.","german":"I gradually came to find him, who contacted me over and over, annoying.","furigana":"なんど|れんらく|かれ|うと|おも"}],"notes":"疎ましい expresses that someone or something is bothersome and one wants to keep one's distance; it is mostly about people and their behavior (親が疎ましい, 干渉が疎ましい). Typical are 疎ましく思う and 疎ましく感じる. Related are 疎む (\"to shun\") and 疎遠 (\"estranged\"). The word sounds rather written; in conversation one says うるさい, 面倒くさい or, casually, うざい.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"凛とした佇まいです。","romaji":"Rin to shita tatazumai desu.","german":"He/She has a dignified bearing.","furigana":"りん|たたず"},{"japanese":"彼女は凛とした声で「いいえ」と答えた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa rin to shita koe de \"iie\" to kotaeta.","german":"In a clear, firm voice, she answered, \"No.\"","furigana":"かのじょ|りん|こえ|こた"},{"japanese":"冬の朝は空気が凛としていて、気持ちがいい。","romaji":"Fuyu no asa wa kuuki ga rin to shite ite, kimochi ga ii.","german":"On winter mornings the air is crisp and clear, which feels good.","furigana":"ふゆ|あさ|くうき|りん|きも"}],"notes":"凛とした is the attributive form of the word 凛 (a と-adjective): 凛と + した. It describes an upright, dignified and clear presence - in posture, voice or appearance - and also cold, clear air (凛とした空気). In the predicate, one says 凛としている. Not to be confused with 凛々しい (りりしい, \"gallant, brave\"). The word is positive and somewhat elevated; 凜 is a variant of the kanji.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"したたかな交渉相手です。","romaji":"Shitataka na kōshō aite desu.","german":"He is a shrewd negotiating partner.","furigana":"こうしょうあいて"},{"japanese":"彼女は厳しい環境でもしたたかに生きてきた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kibishii kankyou demo shitataka ni ikite kita.","german":"She has lived tenaciously even in harsh circumstances.","furigana":"かのじょ|きび|かんきょう|い"},{"japanese":"転んで、膝をしたたか打ってしまった。","romaji":"Koronde, hiza o shitataka utte shimatta.","german":"I fell and hit my knee hard.","furigana":"ころ|ひざ|う"}],"notes":"したたか (mostly in kana) describes, as a な-adjective, someone who is tough, resilient and skillfully pursues their interests - depending on context admiring (\"tough, clever\") or pejorative (\"cunning\"). Typical: したたかな人, したたかに生きる. As an adverb it means \"hard, thoroughly\": 頭をしたたか打つ, したたかに酔う. ずるい is clearly negative \"unfair, sly\", 粘り強い positive \"persistent\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"概算で見積もります。","romaji":"Gaisan de mitsumorimasu.","german":"I'll give a rough estimate.","furigana":"がいさん|みつ"},{"japanese":"引っ越しの費用を概算すると、二十万円ぐらいです。","romaji":"Hikkoshi no hiyou o gaisan suru to, nijuuman-en gurai desu.","german":"Roughly calculated, the moving costs are about 200,000 yen.","furigana":"ひ|こ|ひよう|がいさん|にじゅうまんえん"},{"japanese":"まず概算を出して、後で正確な金額をお知らせします。","romaji":"Mazu gaisan o dashite, ato de seikaku na kingaku o oshirase shimasu.","german":"I'll first give a rough estimate and let you know the exact amount later.","furigana":"がいさん|だ|あと|せいかく|きんがく|し"}],"notes":"概算 is a rough calculation: 概算で \"roughly calculated\", 概算を出す, 概算見積もり; as a transitive する-verb: 費用を概算する. The counterpart is 精算 (\"exact settlement\"), e.g. with an advance payment and a later settlement. 見積もり is the cost estimate itself; in conversation people often simply say だいたい or ざっと. The word belongs to business and administrative language.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"交通費を精算します。","romaji":"Kōtsūhi o seisan shimasu.","german":"I'm settling my travel expenses.","furigana":"こうつうひ|せいさん"},{"japanese":"乗り越したので、改札の前で精算機を使った。","romaji":"Norikoshita node, kaisatsu no mae de seisanki o tsukatta.","german":"Since I had ridden past my stop, I used the fare adjustment machine in front of the ticket gate.","furigana":"の|こ|かいさつ|まえ|せいさんき|つか"},{"japanese":"出張の経費は、月末までに精算してください。","romaji":"Shucchou no keihi wa, getsumatsu made ni seisan shite kudasai.","german":"Please settle your business trip expenses by the end of the month.","furigana":"しゅっちょう|けいひ|げつまつ|せいさん"}],"notes":"精算 is the exact (after-the-fact) settling of accounts: 交通費を精算する (\"to settle travel expenses\"), 経費精算, at the station 乗り越し精算 (\"paying the fare difference\") at the 精算機, as well as paying at a parking garage. Beware of homophones: 清算 (\"to clear up debts or the past\", e.g. 過去を清算する) and 生産 (\"production\"). The counterpart is 概算 (\"rough estimate\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"端数を切り捨てます。","romaji":"Hasū o kirisutemasu.","german":"I'll round off the odd amount.","furigana":"はすう|き|す"},{"japanese":"1,030円ですが、端数はおまけして1,000円でいいですよ。","romaji":"Sen sanjuu en desu ga, hasuu wa omake shite sen en de ii desu yo.","german":"It's 1,030 yen, but I'll throw in the odd amount, so 1,000 yen is fine."},{"japanese":"割り勘の端数は、私が払います。","romaji":"Warikan no hasuu wa, watashi ga haraimasu.","german":"I'll pay the leftover odd amount of the split bill.","furigana":"わ|かん|はすう|わたし|はら"}],"notes":"端数 is the \"odd\" remainder below a round unit, such as the 30 yen in 1,030 yen. Typical are 端数を切り捨てる (\"to round down\"), 端数を切り上げる (\"to round up\") and 四捨五入する (\"to round off in the usual way\"). To be distinguished are お釣り (\"change\"), 残り (\"remainder\" in general) and 余り (\"remainder\" in division). A fraction in the mathematical sense, by contrast, is 分数.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"桁が一つ違います。","romaji":"Keta ga hitotsu chigaimasu.","german":"It's off by one order of magnitude.","furigana":"けた|ひと|ちが"},{"japanese":"暗証番号は四桁の数字です。","romaji":"Anshou bangou wa yonketa no suuji desu.","german":"The PIN is a four-digit number.","furigana":"あんしょうばんごう|よんけた|すうじ"},{"japanese":"値段の桁を間違えて、高すぎる値札を付けてしまった。","romaji":"Nedan no keta o machigaete, takasugiru nefuda o tsukete shimatta.","german":"I got the digits of the price wrong and put on a price tag that was far too high.","furigana":"ねだん|けた|まちが|たか|ねふだ|つ"}],"notes":"桁 is the place of a number: 三桁の数字 (\"three-digit number\"), 桁を間違える (\"to get the number of digits wrong\"). As a counter: 一桁 (ひとけた), 二桁 (ふたけた), 四桁 (よんけた). 桁が違う literally means \"to have a different number of digits\" and figuratively \"to be on a completely different level\"; related are 桁違い and 桁外れ (\"extraordinary\"). In construction, 桁 also means \"girder, beam\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"暗算が得意です。","romaji":"Anzan ga tokui desu.","german":"I am good at mental arithmetic.","furigana":"あんざん|とくい"},{"japanese":"電卓を使わずに暗算で答えを出した。","romaji":"Dentaku o tsukawazu ni anzan de kotae o dashita.","german":"I worked out the answer in my head without using a calculator.","furigana":"でんたく|つか|あんざん|こた|だ"},{"japanese":"買い物のとき、合計金額を暗算します。","romaji":"Kaimono no toki, goukei kingaku o anzan shimasu.","german":"When shopping, I calculate the total amount in my head.","furigana":"か|もの|ごうけいきんがく|あんざん"}],"notes":"暗算 is mental arithmetic; 暗 here stands for \"without aids, in the head\", as in 暗記 (\"memorization\"). Typical are 暗算が得意/苦手, 暗算で計算する, 暗算する. Counterparts are 筆算 (\"written calculation\") and 珠算 (\"calculation with an abacus\"). Beware of the homophone 安産 (\"easy childbirth\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"珠算を習っています。","romaji":"Shuzan o naratteimasu.","german":"I'm learning abacus calculation.","furigana":"しゅざん|なら"},{"japanese":"珠算の検定で二級に合格しました。","romaji":"Shuzan no kentei de nikyuu ni goukaku shimashita.","german":"I passed level 2 of the abacus certification exam.","furigana":"しゅざん|けんてい|にきゅう|ごうかく"},{"japanese":"小学校の授業で、少しだけ珠算を勉強した。","romaji":"Shougakkou no jugyou de, sukoshi dake shuzan o benkyou shita.","german":"In elementary school class, I studied abacus a little.","furigana":"しょうがっこう|じゅぎょう|すこ|しゅざん|べんきょう"}],"notes":"珠算 is the technical, somewhat formal term for calculating with an abacus; in everyday speech one usually simply says そろばん (そろばんを習う). 珠算 appears mainly in set expressions such as 珠算検定 (\"abacus exam\") or 珠算教室. Someone with good practice calculates in their head with an imagined abacus (珠算式暗算). To be distinguished from 暗算 (\"mental arithmetic\") and 筆算 (\"written calculation\").","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"横領で逮捕されました。","romaji":"Ōryō de taiho saremashita.","german":"He was arrested for embezzlement.","furigana":"おうりょう|たいほ"},{"japanese":"経理担当者が会社の金を三千万円横領していた。","romaji":"Keiri tantousha ga kaisha no kane o sanzenman-en ouryou shite ita.","german":"The accountant had embezzled 30 million yen of company money.","furigana":"けいりたんとうしゃ|かいしゃ|かね|さんぜんまんえんおうりょう"},{"japanese":"部長は横領の疑いを否定している。","romaji":"Buchou wa ouryou no utagai o hitei shite iru.","german":"The department head denies the suspicion of embezzlement.","furigana":"ぶちょう|おうりょう|うたが|ひてい"}],"notes":"横領 is the misappropriation of money or goods that have been entrusted to one: 会社の金を横領する, 公金横領, legally 業務上横領. In contrast to 盗む/窃盗 (\"theft\" of someone else's property), one already legitimately holds the goods. Almost synonymous is 着服 (\"pocketing\"). Typical of news and legal language: 横領で逮捕される, 横領の疑い.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"脱税は犯罪です。","romaji":"Datsuzei wa hanzai desu.","german":"Tax evasion is a crime.","furigana":"だつぜい|はんざい"},{"japanese":"その社長は売り上げを隠して脱税していた。","romaji":"Sono shachou wa uriage o kakushite datsuzei shite ita.","german":"The company president had hidden sales and evaded taxes.","furigana":"しゃちょう|う|あ|かく|だつぜい"},{"japanese":"脱税ではなく、合法的な節税の方法を教えてください。","romaji":"Datsuzei de wa naku, gouhouteki na setsuzei no houhou o oshiete kudasai.","german":"I don't want to evade taxes - please tell me legal ways to save on taxes.","furigana":"だつぜい|ごうほうてき|せつぜい|ほうほう|おし"}],"notes":"脱税 is tax evasion, i.e. the illegal non-payment of taxes: 脱税する, 脱税で逮捕される, 脱税の疑い. The counterpart is the legal 節税 (\"saving taxes\"); more colloquial and vaguer is 税金逃れ. Taxes that have been declared but not paid are 滞納 (\"payment arrears\"). Typical of news and legal language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"粉飾決算が発覚しました。","romaji":"Funshoku kessan ga hakkaku shimashita.","german":"The falsification of the accounts came to light.","furigana":"ふんしょくけっさん|はっかく"},{"japanese":"その会社は五年間にわたって粉飾決算をしていた。","romaji":"Sono kaisha wa gonenkan ni watatte funshoku kessan o shite ita.","german":"The company had falsified its financial statements for five years.","furigana":"かいしゃ|ごねんかん|ふんしょくけっさん"},{"japanese":"粉飾決算の責任を取って、社長が辞任した。","romaji":"Funshoku kessan no sekinin o totte, shachou ga jinin shita.","german":"The president resigned to take responsibility for the accounting fraud.","furigana":"ふんしょくけっさん|せきにん|と|しゃちょう|じにん"}],"notes":"粉飾決算 is made up of 粉飾 (\"to make up, to gloss over\") and 決算 (\"annual financial statement\"): the falsification or embellishment of the books. Typical: 粉飾決算をする, 粉飾決算が発覚する (\"to come to light\"), 粉飾決算の疑い; often just 粉飾 is said for short. 改竄 is the manipulation of documents or data in general, 脱税 is tax evasion. A technical term from business news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"党内で内紛が起きています。","romaji":"Tōnai de naifun ga okiteimasu.","german":"Internal strife is occurring within the party.","furigana":"とうない|ないふん|お"},{"japanese":"社長の後継者をめぐって、会社で内紛が起きた。","romaji":"Shachou no koukeisha o megutte, kaisha de naifun ga okita.","german":"Internal strife broke out in the company over the president's successor.","furigana":"しゃちょう|こうけいしゃ|かいしゃ|ないふん|お"},{"japanese":"そのバンドは内紛が原因で解散した。","romaji":"Sono bando wa naifun ga gen'in de kaisan shita.","german":"The band broke up because of internal conflict.","furigana":"ないふん|げんいん|かいさん"}],"notes":"内紛 is a dispute within an organization - party, company, club or country: 内紛が起きる, 内紛が続く, 〜をめぐる内紛, 内紛に揺れる. To be distinguished from 内戦 (\"civil war\", armed) and the colloquial 内輪もめ (\"squabbling among themselves\", also in families). The word belongs to news language.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"秘密が暴露されました。","romaji":"Himitsu ga bakuro saremashita.","german":"The secret was exposed.","furigana":"ひみつ|ばくろ"},{"japanese":"元社員が会社の不正をネットで暴露した。","romaji":"Moto shain ga kaisha no fusei o netto de bakuro shita.","german":"A former employee exposed the company's wrongdoing on the internet.","furigana":"もとしゃいん|かいしゃ|ふせい|ばくろ"},{"japanese":"その俳優の私生活を暴露する本が出版された。","romaji":"Sono haiyuu no shiseikatsu o bakuro suru hon ga shuppan sareta.","german":"A book exposing the actor's private life was published.","furigana":"はいゆう|しせいかつ|ばくろ|ほん|しゅっぱん"}],"notes":"暴露 is the exposing or revealing of something that was supposed to stay hidden, often scandals or private matters: 秘密を暴露する, 不正を暴露する, 暴露本 (\"tell-all book\"), 暴露記事 (\"exposé\"). The neutral \"to make public\" is 公開, the intransitive \"to come to light\" is 発覚; colloquially one says ばらす (\"to spill\"). In technical language 暴露 also means \"to be exposed\" (放射線に暴露される). The kanji 暴 is read ばく here.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"事実を隠蔽しました。","romaji":"Jijitsu o inpei shimashita.","german":"The facts were covered up.","furigana":"じじつ|いんぺい"},{"japanese":"会社は製品の欠陥を何年も隠蔽していた。","romaji":"Kaisha wa seihin no kekkan o nannen mo inpei shite ita.","german":"The company had covered up the product's defects for years.","furigana":"かいしゃ|せいひん|けっかん|なんねん|いんぺい"},{"japanese":"事故の隠蔽が明らかになり、国民の怒りが広がった。","romaji":"Jiko no inpei ga akiraka ni nari, kokumin no ikari ga hirogatta.","german":"When the cover-up of the accident came to light, public anger spread.","furigana":"じこ|いんぺい|あき|こくみん|いか|ひろ"}],"notes":"隠蔽 is the deliberate covering up of mistakes, accidents or scandals, mostly by organizations: 事実を隠蔽する, 隠蔽工作 (\"cover-up operation\"), 組織的な隠蔽, 隠蔽体質 (\"culture of covering things up\"). 隠す is the general word for \"to hide\"; the opposites are 公表 (\"to announce publicly\") and 暴露 (\"to expose\"). Because 蔽 is not a Jōyō kanji, newspapers often write 隠ぺい.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"データが捏造されました。","romaji":"Dēta ga netsuzō saremashita.","german":"The data was fabricated.","furigana":"ねつぞう"},{"japanese":"その記者は記事のためにインタビューを捏造していた。","romaji":"Sono kisha wa kiji no tame ni intabyuu o netsuzou shite ita.","german":"The reporter had made up interviews for his articles.","furigana":"きしゃ|きじ|ねつぞう"},{"japanese":"証拠を捏造したとして、警察官が逮捕された。","romaji":"Shouko o netsuzou shita to shite, keisatsukan ga taiho sareta.","german":"A police officer was arrested for allegedly fabricating evidence.","furigana":"しょうこ|ねつぞう|けいさつかん|たいほ"}],"notes":"捏造 means to invent something non-existent and pass it off as true - data, evidence, quotations or stories: データを捏造する, 証拠の捏造, 記事の捏造. In contrast, 改竄 alters something that already exists, and 偽造 is the counterfeiting of documents or money (偽造パスポート). Because 捏 is not a Jōyō kanji, the press often writes ねつ造.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"書類を改竄しました。","romaji":"Shorui o kaizan shimashita.","german":"I falsified the documents.","furigana":"しょるい|かいざん"},{"japanese":"検査データの改竄が発覚し、工場が止まった。","romaji":"Kensa deeta no kaizan ga hakkaku shi, koujou ga tomatta.","german":"After the falsification of inspection data came to light, the factory shut down.","furigana":"けんさ|かいざん|はっかく|こうじょう|と"},{"japanese":"ウェブサイトが改竄されて、偽の情報が表示された。","romaji":"Webusaito ga kaizan sarete, nise no jouhou ga hyouji sareta.","german":"The website was tampered with, and false information was displayed.","furigana":"かいざん|にせ|じょうほう|ひょうじ"}],"notes":"改竄 is the subsequent altering and falsifying of something that exists - documents, data, records, web pages: 書類を改竄する, データ改竄, 公文書改竄. 捏造, by contrast, invents something entirely new, and 偽造 imitates an original. Because 竄 is not a Jōyō kanji, in newspapers you almost always see 改ざん. It is typically combined with 発覚する (\"to come to light\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大統領が弾劾されました。","romaji":"Daitōryō ga dangai saremashita.","german":"The president was impeached.","furigana":"だいとうりょう|だんがい"},{"japanese":"市民団体は政府の責任を厳しく弾劾した。","romaji":"Shimin dantai wa seifu no sekinin o kibishiku dangai shita.","german":"The citizens' group sharply condemned the government's responsibility.","furigana":"しみんだんたい|せいふ|せきにん|きび|だんがい"},{"japanese":"日本では、裁判官を辞めさせるために弾劾裁判が行われることがある。","romaji":"Nihon de wa, saibankan o yamesaseru tame ni dangai saiban ga okonawareru koto ga aru.","german":"In Japan, impeachment trials are sometimes held to remove judges from office.","furigana":"にほん|さいばんかん|や|だんがいさいばん|おこな"}],"notes":"弾劾 is the public accusation of an official for misconduct; politically, it means impeachment proceedings: 大統領を弾劾する, 弾劾決議, 弾劾裁判 (in Japan against judges). 弾劾 is the proceeding or the accusation - the actual removal from office is called 罷免. In a broader sense it also means \"to denounce sharply\": 政府の責任を弾劾する; general criticism is 非難 or 批判. Very formal.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大臣が罷免されました。","romaji":"Daijin ga himen saremashita.","german":"The minister was dismissed.","furigana":"だいじん|ひめん"},{"japanese":"首相は問題発言をした大臣を罷免した。","romaji":"Shushou wa mondai hatsugen o shita daijin o himen shita.","german":"The prime minister dismissed the minister who had made problematic remarks.","furigana":"しゅしょう|もんだいはつげん|だいじん|ひめん"},{"japanese":"国民審査で罷免された裁判官は、まだ一人もいない。","romaji":"Kokumin shinsa de himen sareta saibankan wa, mada hitori mo inai.","german":"No judge has ever been dismissed through a national review.","furigana":"こくみんしんさ|ひめん|さいばんかん|ひとり"}],"notes":"罷免 is the removal of an office holder by a higher authority, for example when the prime minister dismisses a minister: 首相が大臣を罷免する, 大臣が罷免される. Someone who leaves on their own resigns (辞任); the dismissal of ordinary employees is 解雇, a replacement in a post often euphemistically 更迭. A very formal word from politics and law; also in the constitution (公務員を選定し、罷免する権利).","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"社長が辞任しました。","romaji":"Shachō ga jinin shimashita.","german":"The company president resigned.","furigana":"しゃちょう|じにん"},{"japanese":"大臣は責任を取って辞任した。","romaji":"Daijin wa sekinin o totte jinin shita.","german":"The minister took responsibility and resigned.","furigana":"だいじん|せきにん|と|じにん"},{"japanese":"彼女は健康上の理由でキャプテンを辞任した。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kenkoujou no riyuu de kyaputen o jinin shita.","german":"She stepped down as captain for health reasons.","furigana":"かのじょ|けんこうじょう|りゆう|じにん"}],"notes":"辞任 is the voluntary resignation from an office or post: 社長が辞任する; the office can take を (会長を辞任する). A set phrase is 責任を取って辞任する (\"to take responsibility and resign\"). To be distinguished from 辞職 (\"to give up one's position\", often for civil servants), 退職 (\"to leave employment\") and 罷免/解任 (\"to be dismissed\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"内閣が総辞職しました。","romaji":"Naikaku ga sōjishoku shimashita.","german":"The cabinet resigned en masse.","furigana":"ないかく|そうじしょく"},{"japanese":"事故の責任を取って、会社の役員が総辞職した。","romaji":"Jiko no sekinin o totte, kaisha no yakuin ga soujishoku shita.","german":"Taking responsibility for the accident, the company's executives all resigned.","furigana":"じこ|せきにん|と|かいしゃ|やくいん|そうじしょく"},{"japanese":"不信任案が可決されれば、内閣は総辞職か解散を選ばなければならない。","romaji":"Fushinnin'an ga kaketsu sarereba, naikaku wa soujishoku ka kaisan o erabanakereba naranai.","german":"If the no-confidence motion passes, the cabinet must choose between mass resignation and dissolution.","furigana":"ふしんにんあん|かけつ|ないかく|そうじしょく|かいさん|えら"}],"notes":"総辞職 is the joint resignation of all members of a body; 総 means \"entire\". Most common is 内閣総辞職 or 内閣が総辞職する, but a board of directors can also choose 総辞職. After a successful no-confidence motion, the Japanese cabinet must either choose 総辞職 or dissolve the lower house (解散). Individual persons, on the other hand, resign with 辞任.","levelNote":"Compound: 総 (N1) + 辞職 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"議会が解散しました。","romaji":"Gikai ga kaisan shimashita.","german":"The assembly was dissolved.","furigana":"ぎかい|かいさん"},{"japanese":"明日は駅に九時集合、五時に解散の予定です。","romaji":"Ashita wa eki ni kuji shuugou, goji ni kaisan no yotei desu.","german":"Tomorrow we meet at the station at nine and plan to disperse at five.","furigana":"あした|えき|くじしゅうごう|ごじ|かいさん|よてい"},{"japanese":"大好きだったバンドが、去年解散してしまった。","romaji":"Daisuki datta bando ga, kyonen kaisan shite shimatta.","german":"My favorite band broke up last year.","furigana":"だいす|きょねんかいさん"}],"notes":"解散 has three uses: a group disperses (at the end of an outing: ここで解散します, opposite 集合 \"meeting point, assembly\"), an organization or band disbands (バンドが解散する), and parliament is dissolved (衆議院を解散する, 衆議院が解散する). The word is both intransitive and transitive. For the end of a romantic relationship one does not say 解散, but 別れる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"総選挙が行われます。","romaji":"Sōsenkyo ga okonawaremasu.","german":"A general election will be held.","furigana":"そうせんきょ|おこな"},{"japanese":"衆議院の解散を受けて、各党は総選挙の準備を始めた。","romaji":"Shuugiin no kaisan o ukete, kakutou wa sousenkyo no junbi o hajimeta.","german":"Following the dissolution of the House of Representatives, each party began preparing for the general election.","furigana":"しゅうぎいん|かいさん|う|かくとう|そうせんきょ|じゅんび|はじ"},{"japanese":"与党は総選挙で過半数を取った。","romaji":"Yotou wa sousenkyo de kahansuu o totta.","german":"The ruling party won a majority in the general election.","furigana":"よとう|そうせんきょ|かはんすう|と"}],"notes":"総選挙 in Japan is the election of all members of the lower house (衆議院), either after its dissolution or at the end of the term; the upper house election, by contrast, is called 参議院選挙. Typical: 総選挙が行われる, 総選挙で勝つ, 総選挙に立候補する. Colloquially, the word also refers to fan votes on idols or characters (人気投票).","levelNote":"Compound: 総 (N1) + 選挙 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"公約を守ってください。","romaji":"Kōyaku o mamotte kudasai.","german":"Please keep your campaign pledges.","furigana":"こうやく|まも"},{"japanese":"彼は消費税を下げると公約して当選した。","romaji":"Kare wa shouhizei o sageru to kouyaku shite tousen shita.","german":"He pledged to lower the consumption tax and was elected.","furigana":"かれ|しょうひぜい|さ|こうやく|とうせん"},{"japanese":"新しい市長は、選挙の公約をほとんど果たしていない。","romaji":"Atarashii shichou wa, senkyo no kouyaku o hotondo hatashite inai.","german":"The new mayor has hardly fulfilled any of his campaign pledges.","furigana":"あたら|しちょう|せんきょ|こうやく|は"}],"notes":"公約 is a publicly given promise, above all a politician's campaign promise: 公約を掲げる (\"to put forward\"), 公約を守る/果たす (\"to keep\"), 公約を破る (\"to break\"), 選挙公約. As a する-verb: 減税を公約する. A private promise is 約束; the whole election platform is often called マニフェスト.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"投票率が低いです。","romaji":"Tōhyōritsu ga hikui desu.","german":"Voter turnout is low.","furigana":"とうひょうりつ|ひく"},{"japanese":"今回の選挙の投票率は五十パーセントを下回った。","romaji":"Konkai no senkyo no touhyouritsu wa gojuu paasento o shitamawatta.","german":"Turnout in this election fell below 50 percent.","furigana":"こんかい|せんきょ|とうひょうりつ|ごじゅう|したまわ"},{"japanese":"若者の投票率を上げるために、何ができるだろうか。","romaji":"Wakamono no touhyouritsu o ageru tame ni, nani ga dekiru darou ka.","german":"What can be done to raise turnout among young people?","furigana":"わかもの|とうひょうりつ|あ|なに"}],"notes":"投票率 is the voter turnout; 率 means \"rate, ratio\". Typical: 投票率が高い/低い, 投票率が上がる/下がる, 投票率は〜パーセントだった. Not to be confused with 得票率 (\"share of the vote\" of a candidate) and 支持率 (\"approval rating\" in polls).","levelNote":"Compound: 投票 (N3) + 率 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"浮動票の獲得を目指します。","romaji":"Fudōhyō no kakutoku o mezashimasu.","german":"We aim to win the swing votes.","furigana":"ふどうひょう|かくとく|めざ"},{"japanese":"都市部では浮動票が多く、選挙結果が読みにくい。","romaji":"Toshibu de wa fudouhyou ga ooku, senkyo kekka ga yominikui.","german":"There are many swing voters in urban areas, so election results are hard to predict.","furigana":"としぶ|ふどうひょう|おお|せんきょけっか|よ"},{"japanese":"浮動票が最後に動いて、野党の候補が勝った。","romaji":"Fudouhyou ga saigo ni ugoite, yatou no kouho ga katta.","german":"The swing voters made up their minds at the last minute, and the opposition candidate won.","furigana":"ふどうひょう|さいご|うご|やとう|こうほ|か"}],"notes":"浮動票 is made up of 浮動 (\"to fluctuate, to drift\") and 票 (\"vote\"): the votes of voters with no fixed party loyalty, who vote one way or another. Counterparts are 固定票 (\"votes of loyal supporters\") and 組織票 (\"votes of organized groups\"). Typical: 浮動票を集める/狙う/取り込む. The voter group itself is called 無党派層.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地方自治を推進します。","romaji":"Chihō jichi o suishin shimasu.","german":"We will promote local self-government.","furigana":"ちほうじち|すいしん"},{"japanese":"地方自治体は、住民の生活に身近なサービスを提供している。","romaji":"Chihou jichitai wa, juumin no seikatsu ni mijika na saabisu o teikyou shite iru.","german":"Local governments provide services close to residents' daily lives.","furigana":"ちほうじちたい|じゅうみん|せいかつ|みぢか|ていきょう"},{"japanese":"「地方自治は民主主義の学校」という言葉があります。","romaji":"\"Chihou jichi wa minshu shugi no gakkou\" to iu kotoba ga arimasu.","german":"There is a saying, \"Local self-government is a school of democracy.\"","furigana":"ちほうじち|みんしゅしゅぎ|がっこう|ことば"}],"notes":"地方自治 is the self-government of prefectures and municipalities vis-a-vis the central state (国). The bodies themselves are called 地方自治体 or, for short, 自治体 (\"municipality\"); the legal basis is the 地方自治法. Well known is the saying 地方自治は民主主義の学校. The word belongs to the language of politics, administration and school lessons (civics).","levelNote":"Compound: 地方 (N3) + 自治 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"住民投票を実施します。","romaji":"Jūmin tōhyō o jisshi shimasu.","german":"We will hold a local referendum.","furigana":"じゅうみんとうひょう|じっし"},{"japanese":"市町村の合併について住民投票が行われた。","romaji":"Shichouson no gappei ni tsuite juumin touhyou ga okonawareta.","german":"A referendum was held on the merger of municipalities.","furigana":"しちょうそん|がっぺい|じゅうみんとうひょう|おこな"},{"japanese":"住民投票の結果、空港の建設に反対する票が多数を占めた。","romaji":"Juumin touhyou no kekka, kuukou no kensetsu ni hantai suru hyou ga tasuu o shimeta.","german":"In the referendum, most votes were against building the airport.","furigana":"じゅうみんとうひょう|けっか|くうこう|けんせつ|はんたい|ひょう|たすう|し"}],"notes":"住民投票 is a vote of the residents (住民) of a municipality or prefecture on a concrete issue, such as a merger of municipalities, a power plant or a military base - that is, a local referendum. A nationwide referendum (e.g. on a constitutional amendment) is called 国民投票; in an 選挙, people are elected. Typical: 住民投票を行う/実施する, 住民投票の結果, 反対多数.","levelNote":"Compound: 住民 (N3) + 投票 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"情報公開を求めます。","romaji":"Jōhō kōkai o motomemasu.","german":"I request the disclosure of information.","furigana":"じょうほうこうかい|もと"},{"japanese":"市民団体が、市の支出について情報公開を請求した。","romaji":"Shimin dantai ga, shi no shishutsu ni tsuite jouhou koukai o seikyuu shita.","german":"A citizens' group requested information disclosure regarding the city's expenditures.","furigana":"しみんだんたい|し|ししゅつ|じょうほうこうかい|せいきゅう"},{"japanese":"企業には、もっと積極的な情報公開が求められている。","romaji":"Kigyou ni wa, motto sekkyokuteki na jouhou koukai ga motomerarete iru.","german":"Companies are being asked to disclose information more proactively.","furigana":"きぎょう|せっきょくてき|じょうほうこうかい|もと"}],"notes":"情報公開 refers, on the one hand, to citizens' right to inspect government records (情報公開法, 情報公開請求 \"request for access to records\"), and on the other, generally to the disclosure of information by companies or organizations. Related are 公表 (\"to announce\") and 開示 (\"to disclose\", e.g. 情報開示 in the financial world); 暴露, by contrast, is an exposure against the will of those concerned.","levelNote":"Compound: 情報 (N4) + 公開 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"報道の自由を守ります。","romaji":"Hōdō no jiyū o mamorimasu.","german":"We will protect freedom of the press.","furigana":"ほうどう|じゆう|まも"},{"japanese":"報道によると、事故で三人がけがをしたそうです。","romaji":"Houdou ni yoru to, jiko de sannin ga kega o shita sou desu.","german":"According to reports, three people were injured in the accident.","furigana":"ほうどう|じこ|さんにん"},{"japanese":"その事件は、テレビで大きく報道された。","romaji":"Sono jiken wa, terebi de ookiku houdou sareta.","german":"The incident was widely covered on TV.","furigana":"じけん|おお|ほうどう"}],"notes":"報道 is media coverage: 〜と報道する, 報道によると (\"according to media reports\"), 大きく報道される, 報道の自由 (\"freedom of the press\"), 報道陣 (\"members of the press\"), 報道番組 \"news program\". ニュース is the individual news item, 放送 the broadcast, 記事 the newspaper article. The word is neutral, but occurs mainly in news language.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"論説を読みました。","romaji":"Ronsetsu o yomimashita.","german":"I read the editorial.","furigana":"ろんせつ|よ"},{"japanese":"父は新聞の論説委員として働いています。","romaji":"Chichi wa shinbun no ronsetsu iin to shite hataraite imasu.","german":"My father works as an editorial writer for a newspaper.","furigana":"ちち|しんぶん|ろんせついいん|はたら"},{"japanese":"国語のテストで、論説文を読んで筆者の意見をまとめた。","romaji":"Kokugo no tesuto de, ronsetsubun o yonde hissha no iken o matometa.","german":"In the Japanese test, I read an argumentative essay and summarized the author's opinion.","furigana":"こくご|ろんせつぶん|よ|ひっしゃ|いけん"}],"notes":"論説 is an argumentative opinion piece or commentary, in newspapers also the editorial; the author is called 論説委員 (\"editorial writer\"). In school lessons, 論説文 is the argumentative text type (as opposed to 物語文 and 説明文). 社説 is specifically the editorial in the newspaper's name, 記事 an article in general, コラム a column. An elevated, written word.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"社説に目を通しました。","romaji":"Shasetsu ni me o tōshimashita.","german":"I skimmed the editorial.","furigana":"しゃせつ|め|とお"},{"japanese":"今日の社説は、政府の経済政策を厳しく批判している。","romaji":"Kyou no shasetsu wa, seifu no keizai seisaku o kibishiku hihan shite iru.","german":"Today's editorial sharply criticizes the government's economic policy.","furigana":"きょう|しゃせつ|せいふ|けいざいせいさく|きび|ひはん"},{"japanese":"新聞によって、社説の意見はかなり違う。","romaji":"Shinbun ni yotte, shasetsu no iken wa kanari chigau.","german":"The opinions in editorials differ quite a lot depending on the newspaper.","furigana":"しんぶん|しゃせつ|いけん|ちが"}],"notes":"社説 is the editorial that expresses the official opinion of the newspaper (社 = publishing company) and is usually not signed. Typical: 〜新聞の社説, 社説で〜を批判する, 社説は〜と主張している. 論説 is the broader term for commentaries, コラム a column with a personal touch. Anyone who wants to know a newspaper's standpoint reads its 社説.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"見出しだけ読みました。","romaji":"Midashi dake yomimashita.","german":"I only read the headlines.","furigana":"みだ|よ"},{"japanese":"新聞の一面に「首相辞任」という大きな見出しが出ていた。","romaji":"Shinbun no ichimen ni \"shushou jinin\" to iu ookina midashi ga dete ita.","german":"A big headline reading 「首相辞任」 was on the front page of the newspaper.","furigana":"しんぶん|いちめん|しゅしょうじにん|おお|みだ|で"},{"japanese":"レポートの各章に見出しを付けてください。","romaji":"Repooto no kakushou ni midashi o tsukete kudasai.","german":"Please add a heading to each chapter of the report.","furigana":"かくしょう|みだ|つ"}],"notes":"見出し is the heading of an article or chapter, or the headline: 大きな見出し, 見出しを付ける, 見出しだけ読む. In a dictionary, 見出し(語) is the headword. タイトル and 題名 rather refer to the title of a whole work. Do not confuse it with the verb 見出す, which is read みいだす and means \"to find, to discover\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"記者会見を開きます。","romaji":"Kisha kaiken o hirakimasu.","german":"We will hold a press conference.","furigana":"きしゃかいけん|ひら"},{"japanese":"社長は記者会見で深く頭を下げて謝罪した。","romaji":"Shachou wa kisha kaiken de fukaku atama o sagete shazai shita.","german":"At the press conference, the company president bowed deeply and apologized.","furigana":"しゃちょう|きしゃかいけん|ふか|あたま|さ|しゃざい"},{"japanese":"試合の後、監督が記者会見に応じた。","romaji":"Shiai no ato, kantoku ga kisha kaiken ni oujita.","german":"After the game, the manager agreed to a press conference.","furigana":"しあい|あと|かんとく|きしゃかいけん|おう"}],"notes":"記者会見 is a press conference: 記者会見を開く/行う, 記者会見で発表する/謝罪する, 緊急記者会見 \"emergency press conference\". It is often shortened to 会見 (会見に応じる \"to face the press's questions\"). A one-on-one interview is インタビュー. In Japan, the deep bow at an apology 会見 is a fixed part of the picture of scandals.","levelNote":"Compound: 記者 (N3) + 会見 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地震速報が出ました。","romaji":"Jishin sokuhō ga demashita.","german":"A breaking news alert about an earthquake was issued.","furigana":"じしんそくほう|で"},{"japanese":"ドラマの途中で、画面の上にニュース速報が流れた。","romaji":"Dorama no tochuu de, gamen no ue ni nyuusu sokuhou ga nagareta.","german":"In the middle of the drama, a news flash scrolled across the top of the screen.","furigana":"とちゅう|がめん|うえ|そくほう|なが"},{"japanese":"選挙の結果は、テレビで速報されます。","romaji":"Senkyo no kekka wa, terebi de sokuhou saremasu.","german":"The election results will be reported on TV right away.","furigana":"せんきょ|けっか|そくほう"}],"notes":"速報 is a quick first report or news flash: ニュース速報 (the ticker on TV), 選挙速報, 速報値 (\"preliminary value\"), 緊急地震速報 (\"earthquake early warning\"). As a する-verb: 結果を速報する. News coverage in general is 報道; a printed extra edition is 号外.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"政治的な論争が続いています。","romaji":"Seijiteki na ronsou ga tsuzuiteimasu.","german":"The political controversy continues.","furigana":"せいじてき|ろんそう|つづ"},{"japanese":"その映画は、表現の自由をめぐって大きな論争を巻き起こした。","romaji":"Sono eiga wa, hyougen no jiyuu o megutte ookina ronsou o makiokoshita.","german":"The film set off a major controversy over freedom of expression.","furigana":"えいが|ひょうげん|じゆう|おお|ろんそう|ま|お"},{"japanese":"二人の学者は、その説について何年も論争した。","romaji":"Futari no gakusha wa, sono setsu ni tsuite nannen mo ronsou shita.","german":"The two scholars argued about that theory for years.","furigana":"ふたり|がくしゃ|せつ|なんねん|ろんそう"}],"notes":"論争 is a dispute carried on with arguments, often a public controversy: 論争が起きる/続く, 〜をめぐる論争, 論争を巻き起こす (\"to spark\"); as an intransitive する-verb: 〜と論争する. 議論 is a neutral \"discussion\", 口論 a personal verbal quarrel, 喧嘩 a fight or quarrel in general. Rather written language or news language.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"世論操作に注意が必要です。","romaji":"Yoron sōsa ni chūi ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"We need to be careful about the manipulation of public opinion.","furigana":"よろんそうさ|ちゅうい|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"SNSを使った世論操作が問題になっている。","romaji":"SNS o tsukatta yoron sousa ga mondai ni natte iru.","german":"Manipulation of public opinion using social media has become a problem.","furigana":"つか|よろんそうさ|もんだい"},{"japanese":"政府が世論操作をしていたとして、批判を浴びた。","romaji":"Seifu ga yoron sousa o shite ita to shite, hihan o abita.","german":"The government was criticized for having manipulated public opinion.","furigana":"せいふ|よろんそうさ|ひはん|あ"}],"notes":"世論操作 is the deliberate influencing of public opinion, for example by governments, the media or bots on social networks: 世論操作をする, 〜による世論操作, 世論操作の疑い \"suspicion of opinion manipulation\". 世論 is read よろん or せろん, and 操作 means \"to steer, to manipulate\". Not to be confused with 世論調査 (\"opinion poll\"); similar are 情報操作 (\"information manipulation\") and プロパガンダ.","levelNote":"Compound: 世論 (N1) + 操作 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"偏向報道を批判します。","romaji":"Henkō hōdō o hihan shimasu.","german":"I criticize biased reporting.","furigana":"へんこうほうどう|ひはん"},{"japanese":"一部の視聴者は、その番組を偏向報道だと非難した。","romaji":"Ichibu no shichousha wa, sono bangumi o henkou houdou da to hinan shita.","german":"Some viewers condemned the program as biased reporting.","furigana":"いちぶ|しちょうしゃ|ばんぐみ|へんこうほうどう|ひなん"},{"japanese":"偏向報道にだまされないように、複数の新聞を読むようにしている。","romaji":"Henkou houdou ni damasarenai you ni, fukusuu no shinbun o yomu you ni shite iru.","german":"I make a point of reading several newspapers so that I'm not fooled by biased reporting.","furigana":"へんこうほうどう|ふくすう|しんぶん|よ"}],"notes":"偏向報道 is made up of 偏向 (\"one-sided orientation, bias\") and 報道 (\"news coverage\"). The word almost always appears as an accusation in political debates: 〜は偏向報道だ, 偏向報道を批判する. It means distorted, partisan presentation - not necessarily false facts as with フェイクニュース. The opposite concept is 公平/中立な報道. Note the homophone: 変更 (\"change\") is also read へんこう.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"風刺画を描きます。","romaji":"Fūshiga o kakimasu.","german":"I draw satirical cartoons.","furigana":"ふうしが|か"},{"japanese":"この小説は現代の社会を風刺しています。","romaji":"Kono shousetsu wa gendai no shakai o fuushi shite imasu.","german":"This novel satirizes contemporary society.","furigana":"しょうせつ|げんだい|しゃかい|ふうし"},{"japanese":"そのお笑い番組は政治への風刺が効いていて面白い。","romaji":"Sono owarai bangumi wa seiji e no fuushi ga kiite ite omoshiroi.","german":"That comedy show is funny because its political satire really hits the mark.","furigana":"わら|ばんぐみ|せいじ|ふうし|き|おもしろ"}],"notes":"風刺 is satire: society, politics or well-known people are criticized through exaggeration and mockery. As a transitive する-verb, the target takes を: 政治を風刺する. Common compounds are 風刺画 \"caricature\" and 風刺漫画; a pointed satire \"hits home\": 風刺が効いている. Unlike 皮肉 (\"irony, sarcastic remark\"), which one makes toward individuals in daily life, 風刺 is a form of criticism in art, literature and the media.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"比喩的な表現を使います。","romaji":"Hiyuteki na hyōgen o tsukaimasu.","german":"I use figurative expressions.","furigana":"ひゆてき|ひょうげん|つか"},{"japanese":"「人生は旅だ」というのは比喩です。","romaji":"Jinsei wa tabi da to iu no wa hiyu desu.","german":"\"Life is a journey\" is a metaphor.","furigana":"じんせい|たび|ひゆ"},{"japanese":"先生の説明は比喩が多くて分かりやすかった。","romaji":"Sensei no setsumei wa hiyu ga ookute wakariyasukatta.","german":"The teacher's explanation was easy to understand because of its many metaphors.","furigana":"せんせい|せつめい|ひゆ|おお|わ"}],"notes":"比喩 is the umbrella term for figurative expressions of all kinds - metaphor, simile, parable. Typical: 比喩を使う, 比喩的な表現 and 比喩的に言えば \"figuratively speaking\". Technically, one distinguishes 直喩 (a comparison with ような／みたいな) from 隠喩 or 暗喩 (a metaphor without a comparison word). The word sounds somewhat elevated; in daily life people tend to say たとえ or 例える (\"to compare, to use an analogy\"). Because of the rare kanji 喩, the spelling 比ゆ is also seen.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この詩人は隠喩を多用しています。","romaji":"Kono shijin wa in'yu o tayou shite imasu.","german":"This poet uses a lot of metaphors.","furigana":"しじん|いんゆ|たよう"},{"japanese":"「彼女は太陽だ」は隠喩で、「太陽のようだ」は直喩です。","romaji":"Kanojo wa taiyou da wa in'yu de, taiyou no you da wa chokuyu desu.","german":"\"She is the sun\" is a metaphor, and \"She is like the sun\" is a simile.","furigana":"かのじょ|たいよう|いんゆ|たいよう|ちょくゆ"},{"japanese":"歌詞の中の隠喩の意味を考えてみましょう。","romaji":"Kashi no naka no in'yu no imi o kangaete mimashou.","german":"Let's think about the meaning of the metaphor in the lyrics.","furigana":"かし|なか|いんゆ|いみ|かんが"}],"notes":"隠喩 is the metaphor in the narrower sense: an image without a comparison word like ような, for example 彼女は太陽だ. The counterpart is 直喩 (\"simile\", literally \"direct comparison\") with ような／みたいだ: 太陽のようだ. 隠喩 is a technical term from literature and language teaching and means the same as 暗喩; 隠 means \"hidden\". In everyday speech one talks more generally of 比喩.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この題名には暗喩が含まれています。","romaji":"Kono daimei ni wa an'yu ga fukumarete imasu.","german":"This title contains a metaphor.","furigana":"だいめい|あんゆ|ふく"},{"japanese":"暗喩は「ような」などを使わずに例える方法です。","romaji":"An'yu wa you na nado o tsukawazu ni tatoeru houhou desu.","german":"A metaphor is a way of comparing without using words like 「ような」.","furigana":"あんゆ|つか|たと|ほうほう"},{"japanese":"彼の絵には戦争の暗喩が隠されているそうだ。","romaji":"Kare no e ni wa sensou no an'yu ga kakusarete iru sou da.","german":"It is said that a metaphor for war is hidden in his paintings.","furigana":"かれ|え|せんそう|あんゆ|かく"}],"notes":"暗喩 means \"metaphor\" and is a synonym of 隠喩: both denote an image without a comparison word (人生は旅だ), in contrast to 直喩 or 明喩 with ような. 暗 (\"dark, concealed\") and 明 (\"bright, open\") form the pair of opposites. The word belongs to literary criticism and teaching; typical are 暗喩を使う and 〜の暗喩 \"a metaphor for ...\". In everyday speech, 比喩 or たとえ is enough.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"和歌には掛詞が多いです。","romaji":"Waka ni wa kakekotoba ga ōi desu.","german":"Waka poetry has many pivot words.","furigana":"わか|かけことば|おお"},{"japanese":"「まつ」は「松」と「待つ」をかけた掛詞です。","romaji":"Matsu wa matsu to matsu o kaketa kakekotoba desu.","german":"「まつ」 is a pivot word that plays on 「松」 and 「待つ」.","furigana":"まつ|ま|かけことば"},{"japanese":"古典の授業で、この歌の掛詞を見つけるのに苦労した。","romaji":"Koten no jugyou de, kono uta no kakekotoba o mitsukeru no ni kurou shita.","german":"In classics class, I struggled to find the pivot words in this poem.","furigana":"こてん|じゅぎょう|うた|かけことば|み|くろう"}],"notes":"掛詞 is a stylistic device of classical poetry (waka): one sound-word carries two meanings at once, e.g. まつ as 松 \"pine\" and 待つ \"to wait\". One says 掛詞を使う or 〜と〜をかけた掛詞; the related verb is かける (\"to combine two meanings\"). The term belongs to literature lessons; an everyday wordplay or pun is called ダジャレ. It is also written 掛け詞.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"万葉集には枕詞が多いです。","romaji":"Man'yōshū ni wa makura kotoba ga ōi desu.","german":"The Manyoshu has many pillow words.","furigana":"まんようしゅう|まくらことば|おお"},{"japanese":"「ひさかたの」は「光」や「空」にかかる枕詞です。","romaji":"\"Hisakata no\" wa \"hikari\" ya \"sora\" ni kakaru makura kotoba desu.","german":"「ひさかたの」 is a pillow word that attaches to 「光」 or 「空」.","furigana":"ひかり|そら|まくらことば"},{"japanese":"彼女の名前には、いつも「天才」という枕詞がついてくる。","romaji":"Kanojo no namae ni wa, itsumo \"tensai\" to iu makura kotoba ga tsuite kuru.","german":"The epithet \"genius\" always comes attached to her name.","furigana":"かのじょ|なまえ|てんさい|まくらことば"}],"notes":"枕詞 are fixed epithets of classical poetry, mostly of five morae, placed before a particular word, such as あしひきの before 山 or たらちねの before 母. One says that a 枕詞 \"attaches to\" a word: 〜にかかる. Their original meaning is often unclear, so they are mostly not translated. In modern Japanese, 枕詞 figuratively denotes a standing epithet or a fixed introductory phrase: 「天才」という枕詞がつく.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"季語を入れてください。","romaji":"Kigo o irete kudasai.","german":"Please include a seasonal word.","furigana":"きご|い"},{"japanese":"「桜」は春の季語です。","romaji":"Sakura wa haru no kigo desu.","german":"\"Cherry blossoms\" is a spring seasonal word.","furigana":"さくら|はる|きご"},{"japanese":"この俳句の季語はどれですか。","romaji":"Kono haiku no kigo wa dore desu ka.","german":"Which is the seasonal word in this haiku?","furigana":"はいく|きご"}],"notes":"季語 are words that indicate the season in a haiku, e.g. 桜 for spring or 紅葉 for autumn. One says 春の季語, 季語を入れる and 季語が入っている; they are collected in seasonal word almanacs (歳時記). The assignment follows the traditional calendar, so for example 月 counts as an autumn word. The humorous 川柳, unlike 俳句, does not need a 季語.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"サラリーマン川柳が面白いです。","romaji":"Sarariiman senryuu ga omoshiroi desu.","german":"The salaryman senryu are funny.","furigana":"せんりゅう|おもしろ"},{"japanese":"川柳は俳句と違って季語がいりません。","romaji":"Senryuu wa haiku to chigatte kigo ga irimasen.","german":"Unlike haiku, senryu don't need a seasonal word.","furigana":"せんりゅう|はいく|ちが|きご"},{"japanese":"祖父は毎週、新聞に川柳を投稿している。","romaji":"Sofu wa maishuu, shinbun ni senryuu o toukou shite iru.","german":"My grandfather submits senryu to the newspaper every week.","furigana":"そふ|まいしゅう|しんぶん|せんりゅう|とうこう"}],"notes":"川柳 is a humorous short poem in the 5-7-5 pattern like the 俳句, but without a 季語 and mostly in everyday language. It pokes fun at human weaknesses, work and family life; competitions such as the サラリーマン川柳 are well known. Typical expressions are 川柳を作る／詠む and 川柳を投稿する. The name goes back to the Edo-period poet 柄井川柳.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"百人一首で遊びます。","romaji":"Hyakunin isshu de asobimasu.","german":"I'm playing Hyakunin Isshu.","furigana":"ひゃくにんいっしゅ|あそ"},{"japanese":"中学生のとき、百人一首を全部覚えました。","romaji":"Chuugakusei no toki, hyakunin isshu o zenbu oboemashita.","german":"In junior high school, I memorized all of the Hyakunin Isshu.","furigana":"ちゅうがくせい|ひゃくにんいっしゅ|ぜんぶおぼ"},{"japanese":"百人一首には恋の歌がたくさんあります。","romaji":"Hyakunin isshu ni wa koi no uta ga takusan arimasu.","german":"The Hyakunin Isshu contains a lot of love poems.","furigana":"ひゃくにんいっしゅ|こい|うた"}],"notes":"百人一首 refers above all to the collection 小倉百人一首: one hundred waka by one hundred poets, compiled in the 13th century. It is also a card game (百人一首かるた), traditionally played at New Year: someone reads a poem aloud, and the players try to grab the card with the second half as fast as possible. One says 百人一首をする／で遊ぶ and 百人一首を覚える; as a competitive sport it is called 競技かるた.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"和紙で手紙を書きます。","romaji":"Washi de tegami o kakimasu.","german":"I write letters on Japanese paper.","furigana":"わし|てがみ|か"},{"japanese":"和紙は薄いのに丈夫で長持ちします。","romaji":"Washi wa usui no ni joubu de nagamochi shimasu.","german":"Washi is thin, yet strong and long-lasting.","furigana":"わし|うす|じょうぶ|ながも"},{"japanese":"和紙で作ったランプがとてもきれいでした。","romaji":"Washi de tsukutta ranpu ga totemo kirei deshita.","german":"The lamp made of washi paper was very beautiful.","furigana":"わし|つく"}],"notes":"和紙 is traditional Japanese paper made from plant fibers, such as those of the paper mulberry (楮). It is thin but tear-resistant and very durable, and is used for calligraphy, 障子 sliding doors, lanterns and handicrafts. The opposite is 洋紙 \"Western paper\"; the prefix 和 (\"Japanese\") is also familiar from 和食 and 和室. The handcrafted production (紙すき) is part of UNESCO's intangible cultural heritage.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"墨をすります。","romaji":"Sumi o surimasu.","german":"I'm grinding ink.","furigana":"すみ"},{"japanese":"書道の時間に、墨で服が汚れてしまった。","romaji":"Shodou no jikan ni, sumi de fuku ga yogorete shimatta.","german":"During calligraphy class, I got ink on my clothes.","furigana":"しょどう|じかん|すみ|ふく|よご"},{"japanese":"イカ墨のパスタを食べたら、歯が黒くなった。","romaji":"Ikasumi no pasuta o tabetara, ha ga kuroku natta.","german":"After eating squid ink pasta, my teeth turned black.","furigana":"すみ|た|は|くろ"}],"notes":"墨 is black ink, originally a solid ink stick that is ground with water on an 硯 (inkstone): 墨をする (also written 磨る or 擦る). Ready-made liquid ink is called 墨汁. 墨 also refers to the ink of squid and octopus, as in イカ墨. Well-known compounds are 水墨画 \"ink wash painting\" and 墨色 \"ink black\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"硯で墨をすります。","romaji":"Suzuri de sumi o surimasu.","german":"I grind ink on the inkstone.","furigana":"すずり|すみ"},{"japanese":"書道の後は、硯をきれいに洗いましょう。","romaji":"Shodou no ato wa, suzuri o kirei ni araimashou.","german":"After calligraphy, let's wash the inkstone clean.","furigana":"しょどう|あと|すずり|あら"},{"japanese":"祖父の硯は百年以上前のものだそうです。","romaji":"Sofu no suzuri wa hyakunen ijou mae no mono da sou desu.","german":"My grandfather's inkstone is said to be more than a hundred years old.","furigana":"そふ|すずり|ひゃくねんいじょうまえ"}],"notes":"硯 is the grinding stone on which one rubs an ink stick with a little water to make ink: 硯で墨をする. Together with 筆, 墨 and 紙, it is one of the \"four treasures of the study\" (文房四宝). Since 硯 is not a standard kanji (常用漢字), the word is often written すずり. A box for inkstone and writing utensils is called 硯箱.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"筆で文字を書きます。","romaji":"Fude de moji o kakimasu.","german":"I write characters with a brush.","furigana":"ふで|もじ|か"},{"japanese":"筆箱を家に忘れてしまいました。","romaji":"Fudebako o ie ni wasurete shimaimashita.","german":"I left my pencil case at home.","furigana":"ふでばこ|いえ|わす"},{"japanese":"彼女は筆まめで、よく手紙をくれる。","romaji":"Kanojo wa fudemame de, yoku tegami o kureru.","german":"She loves writing and often sends me letters.","furigana":"かのじょ|ふで|てがみ"}],"notes":"筆 is the brush for writing and painting; one says 筆で書く and counts with 本. Many derivatives relate to writing in general: 筆箱 \"pencil case\" (also for pencils), 筆者 \"author\", 筆まめ \"diligent at writing\", 筆が速い \"to write quickly\". Well known is the proverb 弘法も筆の誤り \"Even masters make mistakes\". A painting brush is more precisely called 絵筆.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"半紙に何枚も書いて練習します。","romaji":"Hanshi ni nanmai mo kaite renshuu shimasu.","german":"I practice by writing on many sheets of calligraphy paper.","furigana":"はんし|なんまい|か|れんしゅう"},{"japanese":"書道教室に行くので、半紙と墨を買いました。","romaji":"Shodou kyoushitsu ni iku node, hanshi to sumi o kaimashita.","german":"I'm going to a calligraphy class, so I bought calligraphy paper and ink.","furigana":"しょどうきょうしつ|い|はんし|すみ|か"},{"japanese":"半紙は薄いので、下に新聞紙を敷いてください。","romaji":"Hanshi wa usui node, shita ni shinbunshi o shiite kudasai.","german":"Calligraphy paper is thin, so please put newspaper underneath.","furigana":"はんし|うす|した|しんぶんし|し"}],"notes":"半紙 is the standardized calligraphy paper (about 24 x 33 cm) that students practice on in 書道 class. It is thin and absorbent, so one puts a pad or newspaper underneath to keep the ink from soaking through. It is counted with 枚: 半紙を一枚. In daily life, 半紙 is practically always connected with 書道; ordinary writing paper is called 便箋 or simply 紙.","levelNote":"Compound: 紙 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"書類に朱印を押します。","romaji":"Shorui ni shuin o oshimasu.","german":"I stamp the document with a red seal.","furigana":"しょるい|しゅいん|お"},{"japanese":"お寺で御朱印をいただきました。","romaji":"Otera de goshuin o itadakimashita.","german":"I received a goshuin at the temple.","furigana":"てら|ごしゅいん"},{"japanese":"旅行のたびに御朱印帳を持って神社を回ります。","romaji":"Ryokou no tabi ni goshuinchou o motte jinja o mawarimasu.","german":"Every time I travel, I take my goshuin book and visit shrines.","furigana":"りょこう|ごしゅいんちょう|も|じんじゃ|まわ"}],"notes":"朱印 is a seal impression stamped in red ink: 朱印を押す. Today it is mostly encountered as 御朱印: artistic stamps with brush writing that one receives for a donation at temples and shrines and collects in a 御朱印帳. For this one politely says 御朱印をいただく, not 買う. Historically there are the 朱印状, permits sealed in red by the shoguns.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"実印を市役所に登録しました。","romaji":"Jitsuin o shiyakusho ni touroku shimashita.","german":"I registered my registered seal at city hall.","furigana":"じついん|しやくしょ|とうろく"},{"japanese":"家を買うときは実印と印鑑証明が必要です。","romaji":"Ie o kau toki wa jitsuin to inkan shoumei ga hitsuyou desu.","german":"When buying a house, you need your registered seal and a seal certificate.","furigana":"いえ|か|じついん|いんかんしょうめい|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"実印は大切なので、金庫にしまっています。","romaji":"Jitsuin wa taisetsu na node, kinko ni shimatte imasu.","german":"My registered seal is important, so I keep it in a safe.","furigana":"じついん|たいせつ|きんこ"}],"notes":"実印 is the personal seal registered at the city hall (印鑑登録). It is used for important contracts such as buying a house or a car and presented together with the 印鑑証明. It is distinguished from the 銀行印 deposited with the bank and the simple 認印 for everyday use. A seal in general is called 印鑑, colloquially はんこ.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"認印で大丈夫です。","romaji":"Mitomein de daijōbu desu.","german":"An informal seal is fine.","furigana":"みとめいん|だいじょうぶ"},{"japanese":"荷物を受け取るときに認印を押しました。","romaji":"Nimotsu o uketoru toki ni mitomein o oshimashita.","german":"I stamped my informal seal when receiving the package.","furigana":"にもつ|う|と|みとめいん|お"},{"japanese":"認印なら百円ショップでも買えます。","romaji":"Mitomein nara hyakuen shoppu de mo kaemasu.","german":"You can buy an informal seal even at a 100-yen shop.","furigana":"みとめいん|ひゃくえん|か"}],"notes":"認印 is a simple, unregistered name stamp for everyday purposes, such as signing for parcels or for internal forms. One says 認印を押す; on forms 認印可 \"an ordinary stamp is acceptable\" often appears. Self-inking stamps (シャチハタ) are nevertheless often not accepted for this. The counterpart is the officially registered 実印.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"はんこは朱肉をつけて押します。","romaji":"Hanko wa shuniku o tsukete oshimasu.","german":"You stamp a seal after dipping it in the red ink pad.","furigana":"しゅにく|お"},{"japanese":"朱肉が乾いていて、きれいに押せなかった。","romaji":"Shuniku ga kawaite ite, kirei ni osenakatta.","german":"The red ink pad was dry, so I couldn't stamp cleanly.","furigana":"しゅにく|かわ|お"},{"japanese":"受付に朱肉を借りに行った。","romaji":"Uketsuke ni shuniku o kari ni itta.","german":"I went to the reception desk to borrow a red ink pad.","furigana":"うけつけ|しゅにく|か|い"}],"notes":"朱肉 is the red stamp ink, or the ink pad, with which one stamps a はんこ: 朱肉をつけて押す. Seals such as 実印 or 認印 are almost always stamped in red in Japan. For normal stamps with black or blue ink, one says スタンプ台 instead of 朱肉. A stamp with built-in ink (シャチハタ) does not need 朱肉.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"書留で送ります。","romaji":"Kakitome de okurimasu.","german":"I'll send it by registered mail.","furigana":"かきとめ|おく"},{"japanese":"お金を送るときは現金書留を使わなければなりません。","romaji":"Okane o okuru toki wa genkin kakitome o tsukawanakereba narimasen.","german":"When sending money, you must use cash registered mail.","furigana":"かね|おく|げんきんかきとめ|つか"},{"japanese":"書留が届いたので、サインをしました。","romaji":"Kakitome ga todoita node, sain o shimashita.","german":"A registered letter arrived, so I signed for it.","furigana":"かきとめ|とど"}],"notes":"書留 is mail sent as registered mail: 書留で送る. Japan Post offers, among others, the normal 一般書留, the cheaper 簡易書留, and 現金書留, the only permitted way to send cash. Receipt is confirmed by signature or seal. The word comes from 書き留める \"to note down, record\": the item is registered.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 11"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"宛先を確認してください。","romaji":"Atesaki o kakunin shite kudasai.","german":"Please check the address.","furigana":"あてさき|かくにん"},{"japanese":"メールの宛先を間違えて送ってしまった。","romaji":"Meeru no atesaki o machigaete okutte shimatta.","german":"I sent the email to the wrong recipient.","furigana":"あてさき|まちが|おく"},{"japanese":"宛先不明で手紙が戻ってきました。","romaji":"Atesaki fumei de tegami ga modotte kimashita.","german":"The letter came back as addressee unknown.","furigana":"あてさきふめい|てがみ|もど"}],"notes":"宛先 is the destination address or the recipient of a shipment - for letters as well as for emails (メールの宛先). Typical are 宛先を書く, 宛先を間違える and 宛先不明 \"addressee unknown\". The recipient's name alone is more likely called 宛名; the counterpart is 差出人 \"sender\". 宛 also serves as a suffix: 田中様宛て \"to Mr. Tanaka\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"差出人を書いてください。","romaji":"Sashidashinin o kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write the sender.","furigana":"さしだしにん|か"},{"japanese":"差出人の名前がない手紙が届いた。","romaji":"Sashidashinin no namae ga nai tegami ga todoita.","german":"A letter arrived with no sender's name.","furigana":"さしだしにん|なまえ|てがみ|とど"},{"japanese":"差出人不明のメールは開かないほうがいいです。","romaji":"Sashidashinin fumei no meeru wa hirakanai hou ga ii desu.","german":"It's better not to open emails from unknown senders.","furigana":"さしだしにんふめい|ひら"}],"notes":"差出人 is the sender of a letter or parcel, the counterpart of 宛先 or 受取人. The word is rather formal and appears on forms and envelopes; in conversation one also says 送り主, and for emails 送信者. Typical are 差出人の住所 and 差出人不明. The underlying verb is 差し出す \"to submit, to send off\", hence also the spelling 差し出し人.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"消印の日付を確認します。","romaji":"Keshiin no hizuke o kakunin shimasu.","german":"I'll check the date of the postmark.","furigana":"けしいん|ひづけ|かくにん"},{"japanese":"締め切りは三月三十一日消印有効です。","romaji":"Shimekiri wa sangatsu sanjuuichinichi keshiin yuukou desu.","german":"The deadline is March 31; the postmark date counts.","furigana":"し|き|さんがつさんじゅういちにちけしいんゆうこう"},{"japanese":"父は消印のある古い切手も大切に集めている。","romaji":"Chichi wa keshiin no aru furui kitte mo taisetsu ni atsumete iru.","german":"My father carefully collects old stamps that still carry a postmark too.","furigana":"ちち|けしいん|ふる|きって|たいせつ|あつ"}],"notes":"消印 is the postmark that cancels the stamp (消す \"to erase, to invalidate\"). The key formula is 〇月〇日消印有効: for mailed submissions, the date of the postmark counts, not the date of receipt. You say 消印を押す; it is the post office that stamps, not the sender. Do not confuse it with personal seals such as 認印 or 実印.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ポストに不在通知が入っていました。","romaji":"Posuto ni fuzai tsuuchi ga haitte imashita.","german":"There was a notice of absence in the mailbox.","furigana":"ふざいつうち|はい"},{"japanese":"不在通知を見て、再配達を頼みました。","romaji":"Fuzai tsuuchi o mite, saihaitatsu o tanomimashita.","german":"I saw the notice and asked for redelivery.","furigana":"ふざいつうち|み|さいはいたつ|たの"},{"japanese":"休暇中はメールの不在通知を設定しておきます。","romaji":"Kyuukachuu wa meeru no fuzai tsuuchi o settei shite okimasu.","german":"I will set up an out-of-office reply for my email while I am on vacation.","furigana":"きゅうかちゅう|ふざいつうち|せってい"}],"notes":"不在通知 in everyday life is mainly the notification card that the post office or a delivery service drops in the mailbox when nobody was home; its official name is ご不在連絡票, abbreviated 不在票. It states how to request 再配達 (\"redelivery\"). In the office, the word also refers to the automatic out-of-office reply by email. Typical are 不在通知が入っている and 不在通知を設定する.","levelNote":"Compound: 不在 (N1) + 通知 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"郵便を転送してもらいます。","romaji":"Yūbin o tensō shite moraimasu.","german":"I'm having my mail forwarded.","furigana":"ゆうびん|てんそう"},{"japanese":"このメールを部長に転送してください。","romaji":"Kono meeru o buchou ni tensou shite kudasai.","german":"Please forward this email to the department head.","furigana":"ぶちょう|てんそう"},{"japanese":"留守のときは、電話を携帯に転送しています。","romaji":"Rusu no toki wa, denwa o keitai ni tensou shite imasu.","german":"When I'm out, I forward calls to my cell phone.","furigana":"るす|でんわ|けいたい|てんそう"}],"notes":"転送 means \"forwarding\": emails, calls or mail are sent on to another address. As a transitive する-verb: メールを上司に転送する, 電話を携帯に転送する. After a move, a 転居届 filed with the post office ensures that letters are forwarded for one year. To be distinguished from 送信 (\"to send\") and 返信 (\"to reply\"); in IT, 転送 also means \"data transfer\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"配達時間を指定します。","romaji":"Haitatsu jikan o shitei shimasu.","german":"I'm specifying a delivery time.","furigana":"はいたつじかん|してい"},{"japanese":"学生のころ、新聞配達のアルバイトをしていました。","romaji":"Gakusei no koro, shinbun haitatsu no arubaito o shite imashita.","german":"When I was a student, I had a part-time job delivering newspapers.","furigana":"がくせい|しんぶんはいたつ"},{"japanese":"荷物は明日の午前中に配達されます。","romaji":"Nimotsu wa ashita no gozenchuu ni haitatsu saremasu.","german":"The package will be delivered tomorrow morning.","furigana":"にもつ|あした|ごぜんちゅう|はいたつ"}],"notes":"配達 is the delivery of mail, packages, newspapers or food right to the front door. As a transitive する-verb: 荷物を配達する, passive 配達される. Important compounds are 新聞配達, 配達員 \"delivery person\" and 再配達 \"redelivery\". 配送 stresses more the whole transport route from the company, 宅配 delivery to the home; ordered food is also called 出前 or デリバリー.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日時指定で送ります。","romaji":"Nichiji shitei de okurimasu.","german":"I'll send it with a specified date and time.","furigana":"にちじしてい|おく"},{"japanese":"平日は留守なので、土曜日の午前に日時指定しました。","romaji":"Heijitsu wa rusu na node, doyoubi no gozen ni nichiji shitei shimashita.","german":"I'm not home on weekdays, so I specified Saturday morning for delivery.","furigana":"へいじつ|るす|どようび|ごぜん|にちじしてい"},{"japanese":"このチケットは日時指定なので、ほかの日には入場できません。","romaji":"Kono chiketto wa nichiji shitei na node, hoka no hi ni wa nyuujou dekimasen.","german":"This ticket is for a specified date and time, so you can't enter on any other day.","furigana":"にちじしてい|ひ|にゅうじょう"}],"notes":"日時指定 is made up of 日時 \"date and time\" and 指定 \"to specify\" and mainly refers to choosing a delivery time when shipping something: 日時指定で送る, 日時指定をする. Admission tickets can also be 日時指定; they are then valid only for a certain day and time slot (日時指定券). Related patterns with 指定 are 時間指定 \"requested time of day\" and 指定席 \"reserved seat\".","levelNote":"Compound: 日時 (N2) + 指定 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"着払いで送ります。","romaji":"Chakubarai de okurimasu.","german":"Ich schicke es unfrei – die Versandkosten zahlt der Empfänger.","furigana":"ちゃくばら|おく"},{"japanese":"返品は着払いでお送りください。","romaji":"Henpin wa chakubarai de ookuri kudasai.","german":"Bitte schicken Sie Rücksendungen unfrei, das Porto übernehmen wir.","furigana":"へんぴん|ちゃくばら|おく"},{"japanese":"着払いの荷物が届いて、千円払いました。","romaji":"Chakubarai no nimotsu ga todoite, sen'en haraimashita.","german":"Ein unfreies Paket kam an, und ich musste 1.000 Yen Porto bezahlen.","furigana":"ちゃくばら|にもつ|とど|せんえんはら"}],"notes":"着払い bedeutet, dass der Empfänger die Versandkosten bei Ankunft (着) bezahlt – im Deutschen „unfrei“. Gegenstück ist 元払い (auch 発払い), bei dem der Absender zahlt. Wichtig: 着払い betrifft nur das Porto; wird der Warenpreis bei der Lieferung kassiert, heißt das 代金引換, kurz 代引き („Nachnahme“). Typisch sind 着払いで送る und 着払いの荷物.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"元払いで発送します。","romaji":"Motobarai de hassō shimasu.","german":"I'll ship it prepaid.","furigana":"もとばら|はっそう"},{"japanese":"お客様都合の返品は元払いでお願いします。","romaji":"Okyakusama tsugou no henpin wa motobarai de onegai shimasu.","german":"For returns for customer-side reasons, please pay the shipping yourself.","furigana":"きゃくさまつごう|へんぴん|もとばら|ねが"},{"japanese":"元払いか着払いか、伝票にチェックを入れてください。","romaji":"Motobarai ka chakubarai ka, denpyou ni chekku o irete kudasai.","german":"Please tick on the slip whether it is prepaid or cash on delivery.","furigana":"もとばら|ちゃくばら|でんぴょう|い"}],"notes":"元払い means that the sender pays the shipping costs when handing in the item - \"postage paid, delivered free of charge\". The opposite is 着払い (the recipient pays); with courier companies 発払い is also used. Typical uses are 元払いで発送する and 送料元払い. For returns, shops specify whether 元払い or 着払い applies.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"丁寧に梱包します。","romaji":"Teinei ni konpō shimasu.","german":"I pack it carefully.","furigana":"ていねい|こんぽう"},{"japanese":"引っ越しの前に、本を全部段ボールに梱包した。","romaji":"Hikkoshi no mae ni, hon o zenbu danbooru ni konpou shita.","german":"Before the move, I packed all my books into cardboard boxes.","furigana":"ひ|こ|まえ|ほん|ぜんぶだん|こんぽう"},{"japanese":"商品の梱包が雑で、箱がつぶれていた。","romaji":"Shouhin no konpou ga zatsu de, hako ga tsuburete ita.","german":"The product was sloppily packed, and the box was crushed.","furigana":"しょうひん|こんぽう|ざつ|はこ"}],"notes":"梱包 is the transport-safe packing of goods for shipping or moving, mostly in cardboard boxes with cushioning material. As a transitive する-verb: 商品を梱包する, 段ボールに梱包する. To be distinguished from 包装 (\"wrapping, gift wrapping\") and 荷造り (\"packing one's things for a trip or a move\"). Common compounds are 梱包材 \"packing material\" and 梱包作業.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"段ボールに詰めます。","romaji":"Dan bōru ni tsumemasu.","german":"I'm packing it into cardboard boxes.","furigana":"だん|つ"},{"japanese":"引っ越し用に段ボールを二十個もらってきました。","romaji":"Hikkoshiyou ni danbooru o nijukko moratte kimashita.","german":"I picked up twenty cardboard boxes for the move.","furigana":"ひ|こ|よう|だん|にじゅっこ"},{"japanese":"段ボールは資源ごみの日に出してください。","romaji":"Danbooru wa shigen gomi no hi ni dashite kudasai.","german":"Please put cardboard boxes out on recyclables collection day.","furigana":"だん|しげん|ひ|だ"}],"notes":"段ボール is corrugated cardboard and, above all, the box made from it (more precisely 段ボール箱). The word mixes kanji and katakana: 段 \"step\" for the corrugated layer and ボール from English \"board\"; it is very often written entirely in katakana as ダンボール. Typical are 段ボールに詰める, 段ボールを組み立てる and 段ボールをつぶす. It is counted with 箱 or 個.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"緩衝材で包みます。","romaji":"Kanshōzai de tsutsumimasu.","german":"I'm wrapping it in cushioning material.","furigana":"かんしょうざい|つつ"},{"japanese":"割れ物なので、緩衝材をたくさん入れてください。","romaji":"Waremono na node, kanshouzai o takusan irete kudasai.","german":"It's fragile, so please put in plenty of cushioning material.","furigana":"わ|もの|かんしょうざい|い"},{"japanese":"箱の中は緩衝材ばかりで、商品はとても小さかった。","romaji":"Hako no naka wa kanshouzai bakari de, shouhin wa totemo chiisakatta.","german":"The box was full of packing material, and the item itself was tiny.","furigana":"はこ|なか|かんしょうざい|しょうひん|ちい"}],"notes":"緩衝材 is cushioning or filling material that protects goods in a package from impact, e.g. bubble wrap, paper or Styrofoam (発泡スチロール). 緩衝 means \"to cushion, to buffer\"; the word sounds somewhat technical and often appears in shipping instructions. Typical are 緩衝材で包む and 緩衝材を入れる／詰める. Bubble wrap is usually called プチプチ in daily life.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"伝票を発行します。","romaji":"Denpyō o hakkō shimasu.","german":"I'll issue a slip.","furigana":"でんぴょう|はっこう"},{"japanese":"お会計のときは、この伝票をレジまでお持ちください。","romaji":"Okaikei no toki wa, kono denpyou o reji made omochi kudasai.","german":"Please bring this order slip to the register when paying.","furigana":"かいけい|でんぴょう|も"},{"japanese":"宅配便の伝票に住所と名前を書きました。","romaji":"Takuhaibin no denpyou ni juusho to namae o kakimashita.","german":"I wrote my address and name on the parcel delivery slip.","furigana":"たくはいびん|でんぴょう|じゅうしょ|なまえ|か"}],"notes":"伝票 is a slip or voucher that records a transaction: in a restaurant the order slip you hand in at the register, with a courier the waybill (宅配便の伝票), in accounting a journal voucher. Typical uses are 伝票を書く and 伝票を切る \"to issue a slip\"; it is counted with 枚. Do not confuse it with 領収書 (\"receipt\") or レシート (\"register receipt\").","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"送り状を作成します。","romaji":"Okurijō o sakusei shimasu.","german":"I'll prepare a waybill.","furigana":"おく|じょう|さくせい"},{"japanese":"送り状に品名と届け先を書いてください。","romaji":"Okurijou ni hinmei to todokesaki o kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write the item name and the delivery address on the waybill.","furigana":"おく|じょう|ひんめい|とど|さき|か"},{"japanese":"送り状の控えは、荷物が届くまで捨てないでください。","romaji":"Okurijou no hikae wa, nimotsu ga todoku made sutenaide kudasai.","german":"Don't throw away the copy of the waybill until the package has arrived.","furigana":"おく|じょう|ひか|にもつ|とど|す"}],"notes":"送り状 is the accompanying document of a shipment: the waybill or label with recipient and sender details, and in commerce also the delivery invoice. Courier companies often use 伝票 or 配送伝票 with the same meaning. Typical uses are 送り状を書く／作成する／貼る and 送り状の控え \"the sender's copy\". The word is rather businesslike.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"追跡番号を教えてください。","romaji":"Tsuiseki bangō o oshiete kudasai.","german":"Please tell me the tracking number.","furigana":"ついせきばんごう|おし"},{"japanese":"追跡番号で、荷物が今どこにあるか調べられます。","romaji":"Tsuiseki bangou de, nimotsu ga ima doko ni aru ka shiraberaremasu.","german":"With the tracking number, you can check where the package is now.","furigana":"ついせきばんごう|にもつ|いま|しら"},{"japanese":"発送後、追跡番号をメールでお知らせします。","romaji":"Hassougo, tsuiseki bangou o meeru de oshirase shimasu.","german":"After shipping, we will notify you of the tracking number by email.","furigana":"はっそうご|ついせきばんごう|し"}],"notes":"追跡番号 is the shipment number with which you can follow online where a package currently is (追跡 \"to track\"). Japanese delivery services often officially call it お問い合わせ番号 or 伝票番号. Typical are 追跡番号を教える／入力する and 追跡番号で調べる. The tracking itself is called 荷物を追跡する.","levelNote":"Compound: 追跡 (N1) + 番号 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人気商品が欠品中です。","romaji":"Ninki shōhin ga keppinchū desu.","german":"Popular products are currently out of stock.","furigana":"にんきしょうひん|けっぴんちゅう"},{"japanese":"欠品している商品は、来週入荷する予定です。","romaji":"Keppin shite iru shouhin wa, raishuu nyuuka suru yotei desu.","german":"The out-of-stock items are scheduled to arrive next week.","furigana":"けっぴん|しょうひん|らいしゅうにゅうか|よてい"},{"japanese":"家具を組み立てようとしたら、ねじが一本欠品していた。","romaji":"Kagu o kumitateyou to shitara, neji ga ippon keppin shite ita.","german":"When I tried to assemble the furniture, one screw was missing.","furigana":"かぐ|く|た|いっぽんけっぴん"}],"notes":"欠品 means that an item is missing from or not in stock at a store or warehouse; it is a term from retail and logistics. Typical are 欠品中, 欠品している and 欠品が出る. From the customer's point of view, one tends to say 売り切れ (\"sold out\") or 在庫切れ. A second meaning is a missing part in a delivery, for example with furniture kits.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新商品が入荷しました。","romaji":"Shinshōhin ga nyūka shimashita.","german":"New products have arrived in stock.","furigana":"しんしょうひん|にゅうか"},{"japanese":"この商品は現在入荷待ちです。","romaji":"Kono shouhin wa genzai nyuuka machi desu.","german":"This product is currently awaiting restock.","furigana":"しょうひん|げんざいにゅうかま"},{"japanese":"再入荷したら、メールでお知らせします。","romaji":"Sainyuuka shitara, meeru de oshirase shimasu.","german":"When it's back in stock, we'll let you know by email.","furigana":"さいにゅうか|し"}],"notes":"入荷 is the arrival of goods at a store or warehouse. Mostly intransitive: 商品が入荷する; from the store's point of view also transitive: 新商品を入荷する. On signs you often read 入荷待ち \"awaiting restock\", 再入荷 \"back in stock\" and 新入荷 \"newly arrived\". The counterpart is 出荷 \"shipment, dispatch\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"本日出荷します。","romaji":"Honjitsu shukka shimasu.","german":"We ship today.","furigana":"ほんじつしゅっか"},{"japanese":"台風の影響で、野菜の出荷が遅れています。","romaji":"Taifuu no eikyou de, yasai no shukka ga okurete imasu.","german":"Because of the typhoon, vegetable shipments are delayed.","furigana":"たいふう|えいきょう|やさい|しゅっか|おく"},{"japanese":"この工場では一日に一万個の製品を出荷している。","romaji":"Kono koujou de wa ichinichi ni ichimanko no seihin o shukka shite iru.","german":"This factory ships 10,000 products a day.","furigana":"こうじょう|いちにち|いちまんこ|せいひん|しゅっか"}],"notes":"出荷 is the shipping of goods from a manufacturer, farm or warehouse to retailers or customers. As a transitive する-verb: 商品を出荷する; in online shopping, 本日出荷 means \"ships today\". In contrast to 発送 (\"to send off\", also by private individuals), 出荷 sounds commercial and implies larger quantities. The counterpart is 入荷.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"月末に棚卸しをします。","romaji":"Getsumatsu ni tanaoroshi o shimasu.","german":"We do the stocktaking at the end of the month.","furigana":"げつまつ|たなおろ"},{"japanese":"棚卸しのため、明日は午後から営業します。","romaji":"Tanaoroshi no tame, ashita wa gogo kara eigyou shimasu.","german":"Because of stocktaking, we will open tomorrow only from the afternoon.","furigana":"たなおろ|あした|ごご|えいぎょう"},{"japanese":"年に一度、自分のスキルの棚卸しをしています。","romaji":"Nen ni ichido, jibun no sukiru no tanaoroshi o shite imasu.","german":"Once a year, I take stock of my own skills.","furigana":"ねん|いちど|じぶん|たなおろ"}],"notes":"棚卸し is stocktaking: the stock of goods is counted and valued (literally \"taking goods down from the shelf\"). Typical uses are 棚卸しをする, 棚卸しのため休業 and 年末の棚卸し; in business texts it is also written without し: 棚卸. Figuratively one says キャリアの棚卸し \"taking stock of one's career\". The older meaning \"picking at others' faults\" (人の棚卸しをする) is rare.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お取り寄せグルメが人気です。","romaji":"Otoriyose gurume ga ninki desu.","german":"Gourmet specialties ordered in from around the country are popular.","furigana":"と|よ|にんき"},{"japanese":"在庫がないので、お取り寄せになります。","romaji":"Zaiko ga nai node, otoriyose ni narimasu.","german":"We don't have it in stock, so it will have to be a special order.","furigana":"ざいこ|と|よ"},{"japanese":"取り寄せには一週間ほどかかります。","romaji":"Toriyose ni wa isshuukan hodo kakarimasu.","german":"A special order takes about a week.","furigana":"と|よ|いっしゅうかん"}],"notes":"取り寄せ is ordering an item that is not in stock or that comes from another region; the related verb is 取り寄せる (\"to have sent in, to order in\"). In a shop you hear お取り寄せになります \"We'll have to order that in\". With お, お取り寄せ is also a trend word for regional specialties ordered online (お取り寄せグルメ). More general is 注文 \"order\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"通販で買いました。","romaji":"Tsūhan de kaimashita.","german":"I bought it by mail order.","furigana":"つうはん|か"},{"japanese":"最近は服もネット通販で買うことが多いです。","romaji":"Saikin wa fuku mo netto tsuuhan de kau koto ga ooi desu.","german":"Recently I often buy even clothes through online shopping.","furigana":"さいきん|ふく|つうはん|か|おお"},{"japanese":"夜中にテレビ通販を見ていると、つい何か欲しくなる。","romaji":"Yonaka ni terebi tsuuhan o mite iru to, tsui nanika hoshiku naru.","german":"When I watch TV shopping late at night, I end up wanting something.","furigana":"よなか|つうはん|み|なに|ほ"}],"notes":"通販 is the short form of 通信販売 \"mail-order sales\" and today mainly means online shopping. Typical are 通販で買う, ネット通販, 通販サイト and テレビ通販 \"TV shopping\". The word is neutral and everyday; the long form 通信販売 sounds more formal. For shopping on the internet, people also say ネットショッピング.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"銀行の窓口に行きます。","romaji":"Ginkō no madoguchi ni ikimasu.","german":"I'm going to the bank counter.","furigana":"ぎんこう|まどぐち|い"},{"japanese":"三番の窓口でお待ちください。","romaji":"Sanban no madoguchi de omachi kudasai.","german":"Please wait at counter number 3.","furigana":"さんばん|まどぐち|ま"},{"japanese":"この件については、田中さんが窓口になります。","romaji":"Kono ken ni tsuite wa, Tanaka-san ga madoguchi ni narimasu.","german":"Mr. Tanaka will be the point of contact for this matter.","furigana":"けん|たなか|まどぐち"}],"notes":"窓口 is the counter at a bank, post office, station or government office where you are served: 窓口に行く, 〇番の窓口. Figuratively it means \"point of contact\": 相談窓口 \"consultation desk\", 〜の窓口になる \"to be in charge of ...\". In both meanings the word is very common and neutral.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"整理券を取ってください。","romaji":"Seiriken o totte kudasai.","german":"Please take a numbered ticket.","furigana":"せいりけん|と"},{"japanese":"整理券の番号と同じ欄の運賃を払ってください。","romaji":"Seiriken no bangou to onaji ran no unchin o haratte kudasai.","german":"Please pay the fare in the column matching the number on your ticket.","furigana":"せいりけん|ばんごう|おな|らん|うんちん|はら"},{"japanese":"人気のパン屋は朝九時から整理券を配っている。","romaji":"Ninki no pan'ya wa asa kuji kara seiriken o kubatte iru.","german":"The popular bakery hands out numbered tickets from 9 a.m.","furigana":"にんき|や|あさくじ|せいりけん|くば"}],"notes":"整理券 is a numbered card that regulates the order: when there is a big crowd in front of shops or events (整理券を配る), or at counters. On many local buses you take a 整理券 when boarding; its number shows the fare on the display board. Typical are 整理券を取る／もらう and 整理券番号. A reusable numbered tag made of plastic is more often called 番号札.","levelNote":"Compound: 整理 (N3) + 券 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"番号札の番号が呼ばれるまでお待ちください。","romaji":"Bangou fuda no bangou ga yobareru made omachi kudasai.","german":"Please wait until the number on your tag is called.","furigana":"ばんごうふだ|ばんごう|よ|ま"},{"japanese":"番号札を持って、席でお待ちください。","romaji":"Bangou fuda o motte, seki de omachi kudasai.","german":"Please take your number tag and wait at your seat.","furigana":"ばんごうふだ|も|せき|ま"},{"japanese":"コートを預けると、番号札を渡されました。","romaji":"Kooto o azukeru to, bangou fuda o watasaremashita.","german":"When I checked my coat, I was handed a number tag.","furigana":"あず|ばんごうふだ|わた"}],"notes":"番号札 is a small tag or card with a number, e.g. a cloakroom tag, a waiting number at a counter or a pickup number at a restaurant. Unlike the 整理券, which is mostly made of paper, a 番号札 is often made of plastic or wood and is returned afterwards. 札 is read ふだ here (\"sign, tablet\"), not さつ as in banknotes. Typical are 番号札を取る／渡す／持つ.","levelNote":"Compound: 番号 (N5) + 札 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"小銭がありません。","romaji":"Kozeni ga arimasen.","german":"I have no small change.","furigana":"こぜに"},{"japanese":"小銭入れを落としてしまいました。","romaji":"Kozeniire o otoshite shimaimashita.","german":"I dropped my coin purse.","furigana":"こぜにい|お"},{"japanese":"すみません、小銭がたくさんあるので、これで払ってもいいですか。","romaji":"Sumimasen, kozeni ga takusan aru node, kore de haratte mo ii desu ka.","german":"Excuse me, I have a lot of coins - may I pay with them?","furigana":"こぜに|はら"}],"notes":"小銭 is small change, especially coins: 小銭がない, 小銭を出す, 小銭入れ \"coin purse\". In conversation people also say 細かいお金 or simply 細かいの. A second meaning is a small amount of money, as in 小銭を稼ぐ \"to earn a little extra money\". The official word for coin is 硬貨.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"千円札でお願いします。","romaji":"Sen'en satsu de onegai shimasu.","german":"In 1,000-yen bills, please.","furigana":"せんえんさつ|ねが"},{"japanese":"財布にお札が一枚も入っていなかった。","romaji":"Saifu ni osatsu ga ichimai mo haitte inakatta.","german":"There wasn't a single bill in my wallet.","furigana":"さいふ|さつ|いちまい|はい"},{"japanese":"この自動販売機は一万円札が使えません。","romaji":"Kono jidou hanbaiki wa ichiman'en satsu ga tsukaemasen.","german":"This vending machine doesn't accept 10,000-yen bills.","furigana":"じどうはんばいき|いちまんえんさつ|つか"}],"notes":"札 (さつ) is a banknote. On its own it is mostly used politely as お札, otherwise as a suffix: 千円札, 一万円札, 札束 \"bundle of banknotes\"; it is counted with 枚. Careful: with the reading ふだ, the same kanji means \"sign, tablet\", and お札 (おふだ) is a protective amulet from a shrine. The formal word for banknote is 紙幣.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"硬貨を集めています。","romaji":"Kōka o atsumeteimasu.","german":"I collect coins.","furigana":"こうか|あつ"},{"japanese":"この機械は硬貨しか使えません。","romaji":"Kono kikai wa kouka shika tsukaemasen.","german":"This machine only accepts coins.","furigana":"きかい|こうか|つか"},{"japanese":"オリンピックの記念硬貨が発行されることになった。","romaji":"Orinpikku no kinen kouka ga hakkou sareru koto ni natta.","german":"It was decided to issue a commemorative coin for the Olympics.","furigana":"きねんこうか|はっこう"}],"notes":"硬貨 is the official, rather formal word for coin and the counterpart of 紙幣 \"banknote\". It appears on machines, in the news and among collectors: 記念硬貨 \"commemorative coin\", 硬貨投入口 \"coin slot\". In conversation people mostly say 小銭 or, specifically, 百円玉, 十円玉. Rarely, 硬貨 also means \"hard currency\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"貯金箱にお金を入れます。","romaji":"Chokinbako ni okane o iremasu.","german":"I put money into the piggy bank.","furigana":"ちょきんばこ|かね|い"},{"japanese":"五百円玉だけを貯金箱にためています。","romaji":"Gohyakuendama dake o chokinbako ni tamete imasu.","german":"I save only 500-yen coins in my piggy bank.","furigana":"ごひゃくえんだま|ちょきんばこ"},{"japanese":"貯金箱がいっぱいになったので、開けてみた。","romaji":"Chokinbako ga ippai ni natta node, akete mita.","german":"The piggy bank was full, so I opened it.","furigana":"ちょきんばこ|あ"}],"notes":"貯金箱 is a piggy bank or coin bank into which you put coins: 貯金箱に入れる, 貯金箱にためる. The classic piggy bank is called 豚の貯金箱. 貯金 is everyday saving; bank deposits in the narrower sense are 預金. It is popular to collect only one particular coin in the 貯金箱, for example as 五百円玉貯金.","levelNote":"Compound: 貯金 (N3) + 箱 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"通帳記入をします。","romaji":"Tsūchō kinyū o shimasu.","german":"I'll have my passbook updated.","furigana":"つうちょうきにゅう"},{"japanese":"口座を作ったときに通帳をもらいました。","romaji":"Kouza o tsukutta toki ni tsuuchou o moraimashita.","german":"I got a passbook when I opened the account.","furigana":"こうざ|つく|つうちょう"},{"japanese":"最近は紙の通帳がないネット銀行も多いです。","romaji":"Saikin wa kami no tsuuchou ga nai netto ginkou mo ooi desu.","german":"Recently, there are many online banks with no paper passbook.","furigana":"さいきん|かみ|つうちょう|ぎんこう|おお"}],"notes":"通帳 is the passbook of a bank or post office in which deposits and withdrawals are recorded (預金通帳); it exists for ordinary checking accounts as well, not only for savings accounts. At the ATM you have it updated: 通帳記入 or 記帳. For transactions at the counter you traditionally need 通帳と印鑑. Many banks now offer accounts without a paper book (通帳なし, ネット通帳).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"暗証番号を入力してください。","romaji":"Anshō bangō o nyūryoku shite kudasai.","german":"Please enter your PIN.","furigana":"あんしょうばんごう|にゅうりょく"},{"japanese":"暗証番号を三回間違えて、カードが使えなくなった。","romaji":"Anshou bangou o sankai machigaete, kaado ga tsukaenaku natta.","german":"I entered my PIN wrong three times and now the card is blocked.","furigana":"あんしょうばんごう|さんかいまちが|つか"},{"japanese":"暗証番号に誕生日を使うのは危ないですよ。","romaji":"Anshou bangou ni tanjoubi o tsukau no wa abunai desu yo.","german":"It's dangerous to use your birthday as your PIN.","furigana":"あんしょうばんごう|たんじょうび|つか|あぶ"}],"notes":"暗証番号 is the secret number (PIN) for a bank card, credit card, locker or government card. Typical are 暗証番号を入力する／押す and 暗証番号を忘れる／間違える. Unlike パスワード, a 暗証番号 usually consists only of digits, often four. 暗証 is composed of 暗 \"hidden\" and 証 \"proof\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 16"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"印鑑証明を取得しました。","romaji":"Inkan shōmei o shutoku shimashita.","german":"I obtained a seal registration certificate.","furigana":"いんかんしょうめい|しゅとく"},{"japanese":"印鑑証明は市役所やコンビニで取れます。","romaji":"Inkan shoumei wa shiyakusho ya konbini de toremasu.","german":"You can get a seal registration certificate at city hall or a convenience store.","furigana":"いんかんしょうめい|しやくしょ|と"},{"japanese":"車を買うとき、印鑑証明を二通出すように言われた。","romaji":"Kuruma o kau toki, inkan shoumei o nitsuu dasu you ni iwareta.","german":"When I bought a car, I was told to submit two seal registration certificates.","furigana":"くるま|か|いんかんしょうめい|につうだ|い"}],"notes":"印鑑証明 is the official certificate that a seal (実印) is registered with the municipality; the document is officially called 印鑑登録証明書. You need it for important contracts such as buying real estate or a car. Typical are 印鑑証明を取る／取得する and 印鑑証明が必要だ; it is counted with 通. You can have it issued at city hall or, with the マイナンバーカード, also at a コンビニ.","levelNote":"Compound: 印鑑 (N3) + 証明 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"戸籍謄本が必要です。","romaji":"Koseki tōhon ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"I need a certified copy of the family register.","furigana":"こせきとうほん|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"結婚すると、二人の新しい戸籍が作られます。","romaji":"Kekkon suru to, futari no atarashii koseki ga tsukuraremasu.","german":"When you get married, a new family register is created for the two of you.","furigana":"けっこん|ふたり|あたら|こせき|つく"},{"japanese":"日本人と結婚しても、外国人は戸籍を持つことができません。","romaji":"Nihonjin to kekkon shite mo, gaikokujin wa koseki o motsu koto ga dekimasen.","german":"Even if a foreigner marries a Japanese person, they cannot have a family register.","furigana":"にほんじん|けっこん|がいこくじん|こせき|も"}],"notes":"戸籍 is the Japanese family register, in which births, marriages, divorces and deaths of family members are recorded. For official procedures you need extracts: 戸籍謄本 (full copy) or 戸籍抄本 (partial extract). Typical are 戸籍に入る／入れる and 戸籍を移す. To be clearly distinguished from it is the 住民票 (\"certificate of residence\"); foreign nationals do not have their own 戸籍.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"住民票を取りに行きます。","romaji":"Jūminhyō o tori ni ikimasu.","german":"I'm going to pick up a certificate of residence.","furigana":"じゅうみんひょう|と|い"},{"japanese":"引っ越したら、住民票を移さなければなりません。","romaji":"Hikkoshitara, juuminhyou o utsusanakereba narimasen.","german":"After moving, you have to transfer your residence registration.","furigana":"ひ|こ|じゅうみんひょう|うつ"},{"japanese":"今はコンビニでも住民票が取れるようになりました。","romaji":"Ima wa konbini demo juuminhyou ga toreru you ni narimashita.","german":"Nowadays you can even get a certificate of residence at a convenience store.","furigana":"いま|じゅうみんひょう|と"}],"notes":"住民票 is the certificate of residence that you get at the 市役所 or 区役所; one says 住民票を取る or 住民票を取りに行く. Officially you receive a copy, the 住民票の写し, which serves as proof of residence for employers, banks or landlords. When you move to another municipality, you \"transfer\" your registration: 住民票を移す. Not to be confused with the 戸籍, the family register.","levelNote":"Compound: 住民 (N3) + 票 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"転出届を提出します。","romaji":"Tenshutsu todoke o teishutsu shimasu.","german":"I'm submitting a moving-out notification.","furigana":"てんしゅつとどけ|ていしゅつ"},{"japanese":"引っ越しの前に、市役所で転出届を出してください。","romaji":"Hikkoshi no mae ni, shiyakusho de tenshutsu todoke o dashite kudasai.","german":"Please file a moving-out notification at city hall before you move.","furigana":"ひ|こ|まえ|しやくしょ|てんしゅつとどけ|だ"},{"japanese":"転出届はオンラインでも出せるそうです。","romaji":"Tenshutsu todoke wa onrain demo daseru sou desu.","german":"I hear you can also file a moving-out notification online.","furigana":"てんしゅつとどけ|だ"}],"notes":"転出届 is the notification you file before moving to another municipality at your current 市役所 or 区役所: 転出届を出す or, more formally, 転出届を提出する. You receive the 転出証明書 for it, which you need at your new place of residence for the 転入届. For a move within the same municipality, a 転居届 is enough instead. The 届 (\"notification, report\") is read どけ here.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"転入届を出しました。","romaji":"Tennyū todoke o dashimashita.","german":"I filed a moving-in notification.","furigana":"てんにゅうとどけ|だ"},{"japanese":"引っ越してから14日以内に転入届を出さなければなりません。","romaji":"Hikkoshite kara juuyokka inai ni tennyuu todoke o dasanakereba narimasen.","german":"You must file a moving-in notification within 14 days of moving.","furigana":"ひ|こ|かいない|てんにゅうとどけ|だ"},{"japanese":"転入届を出すときは、転出証明書を持っていってください。","romaji":"Tennyuu todoke o dasu toki wa, tenshutsu shoumeisho o motte itte kudasai.","german":"When you file the moving-in notification, please bring your moving-out certificate.","furigana":"てんにゅうとどけ|だ|てんしゅつしょうめいしょ|も"}],"notes":"転入届 is the registration at your new place of residence when you move in from another municipality; it must be filed at the 市役所 or 区役所 within 14 days of moving in. Typical: 転入届を出す / 提出する. You must bring the 転出証明書 from your old place of residence, which you received when filing the 転出届. For a move within the same city, people say 転居届.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"婚姻届を提出しました。","romaji":"Kon'in todoke o teishutsu shimashita.","german":"We submitted our marriage registration.","furigana":"こんいんとどけ|ていしゅつ"},{"japanese":"婚姻届には証人二人のサインが必要です。","romaji":"Kon'in todoke ni wa shounin futari no sain ga hitsuyou desu.","german":"The marriage registration form needs the signatures of two witnesses.","furigana":"こんいんとどけ|しょうにんふたり|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"11月22日に婚姻届を出す人が多いです。","romaji":"Juuichigatsu nijuuni-nichi ni kon'in todoke o dasu hito ga ooi desu.","german":"Many couples file their marriage registration on November 22.","furigana":"がつ|にち|こんいんとどけ|だ|ひと|おお"}],"notes":"婚姻届 is not a marriage certificate but the form with which you register a marriage at the municipal office. In Japan you are legally married as soon as the 婚姻届 is accepted; therefore 婚姻届を出す practically means \"to get married officially\" (colloquially also 入籍する). The form needs the signatures of both partners and of two witnesses (証人). Many people choose a special date for it.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"出生届を出します。","romaji":"Shusshō todoke o dashimasu.","german":"I will submit the birth notification.","furigana":"しゅっしょうとどけ|だ"},{"japanese":"赤ちゃんの名前が決まったので、夫が区役所に出生届を出しに行った。","romaji":"Akachan no namae ga kimatta node, otto ga kuyakusho ni shusshou todoke o dashi ni itta.","german":"Since the baby's name was decided, my husband went to the ward office to submit the birth notification.","furigana":"あか|なまえ|き|おっと|くやくしょ|しゅっしょうとどけ|だ|い"},{"japanese":"出生届は生まれてから14日以内に出す必要があります。","romaji":"Shusshou todoke wa umarete kara juuyokka inai ni dasu hitsuyou ga arimasu.","german":"The birth notification must be submitted within 14 days of birth.","furigana":"しゅっしょうとどけ|う|かいない|だ|ひつよう"}],"notes":"出生届 is the official registration of a birth, not a certificate; it is submitted to the 市役所 or 区役所 within 14 days after the birth: 出生届を出す / 提出する. On the same form, the doctor or midwife confirms the birth (出生証明書), and the parents enter the child's name. The standard reading is しゅっしょう, but in everyday speech you also hear しゅっせい.","levelNote":"Compound: 出生 (N1) + 届 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"委任状を書いてください。","romaji":"Ininjō o kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write a letter of proxy.","furigana":"いにんじょう|か"},{"japanese":"代理の方が来る場合は、委任状が必要です。","romaji":"Dairi no kata ga kuru baai wa, ininjou ga hitsuyou desu.","german":"If a representative comes, a letter of proxy is required.","furigana":"だいり|かた|く|ばあい|いにんじょう|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"母の委任状を持って、銀行の手続きをしてきた。","romaji":"Haha no ininjou o motte, ginkou no tetsuzuki o shite kita.","german":"With my mother's letter of proxy, I took care of the procedures at the bank.","furigana":"はは|いにんじょう|も|ぎんこう|てつづ"}],"notes":"委任状 is a written authorization by which you commission someone to act in your place, for example at government offices or banks. Typical: 委任状を書く / 作成する, 委任状を持参する; the authorized person is called 代理人. The related verb is 委任する \"to entrust, to delegate\". Offices often require: 代理の場合は委任状が必要です.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"診断書が必要です。","romaji":"Shindansho ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"I need a medical certificate.","furigana":"しんだんしょ|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"会社を休んだので、医者に診断書を書いてもらいました。","romaji":"Kaisha o yasunda node, isha ni shindansho o kaite moraimashita.","german":"Since I missed work, I had a doctor write me a medical certificate.","furigana":"かいしゃ|やす|いしゃ|しんだんしょ|か"},{"japanese":"事故のけがについて、保険会社に診断書を送った。","romaji":"Jiko no kega ni tsuite, hoken-gaisha ni shindansho o okutta.","german":"I sent the insurance company a medical certificate about my accident injuries.","furigana":"じこ|ほけんがいしゃ|しんだんしょ|おく"}],"notes":"診断書 is a medical certificate or report on an illness or injury. You have the doctor write it (医者に診断書を書いてもらう) and submit it to a company, school or insurance company (診断書を提出する). In Japan it usually costs money. Not to be confused with 処方箋, the prescription for medication.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"始末書を提出しました。","romaji":"Shimatsusho o teishutsu shimashita.","german":"I submitted a written apology.","furigana":"しまつしょ|ていしゅつ"},{"japanese":"会社のパソコンをなくして、始末書を書かされた。","romaji":"Kaisha no pasokon o nakushite, shimatsusho o kakasareta.","german":"I lost my company laptop and was made to write a written apology.","furigana":"かいしゃ|しまつしょ|か"},{"japanese":"次に遅刻したら始末書だぞ、と部長に言われた。","romaji":"Tsugi ni chikoku shitara shimatsusho da zo, to buchou ni iwareta.","german":"The department head told me that if I'm late again, I'll have to write a written apology.","furigana":"つぎ|ちこく|しまつしょ|ぶちょう|い"}],"notes":"始末書 is a written statement with an apology that an employee has to hand to a superior after a mistake or misconduct (e.g. lateness, loss of company property). Typical: 始末書を書く, 始末書を提出する and often the causative-passive 始末書を書かされる \"to be made to write a written apology\". A neutral report without an admission of guilt is called 報告書; at school, people tend to write a 反省文.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"念書にサインしてください。","romaji":"Nensho ni sain shite kudasai.","german":"Please sign the written pledge.","furigana":"ねんしょ"},{"japanese":"お金を貸すときは、念書を書いてもらったほうがいい。","romaji":"Okane o kasu toki wa, nensho o kaite moratta hou ga ii.","german":"When you lend money, it's better to have them write a written pledge.","furigana":"かね|か|ねんしょ|か"},{"japanese":"もう二度としないという念書を書かされた。","romaji":"Mou nido to shinai to iu nensho o kakasareta.","german":"I was made to write a pledge that I would never do it again.","furigana":"にど|ねんしょ|か"}],"notes":"念書 is a signed written assurance, mostly between private individuals, for example that one will repay borrowed money or will not do something again. You say 念書を書く (to write one yourself), 念書を取る (to have someone give you one) and 念書にサインする. More formal, and often a prepared form, is the 誓約書; a full contract is called 契約書.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"誓約書に署名します。","romaji":"Seiyakusho ni shomei shimasu.","german":"I'm signing the written pledge.","furigana":"せいやくしょ|しょめい"},{"japanese":"入社のとき、秘密を守るという誓約書を出しました。","romaji":"Nyuusha no toki, himitsu o mamoru to iu seiyakusho o dashimashita.","german":"When I joined the company, I submitted a pledge to keep secrets.","furigana":"にゅうしゃ|ひみつ|まも|せいやくしょ|だ"},{"japanese":"ジムに入会するとき、けがをしても自分の責任だという誓約書にサインした。","romaji":"Jimu ni nyuukai suru toki, kega o shite mo jibun no sekinin da to iu seiyakusho ni sain shita.","german":"When I joined the gym, I signed a pledge stating that I'm responsible for any injury myself.","furigana":"にゅうかい|じぶん|せきにん|せいやくしょ"}],"notes":"誓約書 is a written declaration of commitment in which you promise (誓う \"to swear, to pledge\") to follow certain rules, such as confidentiality when joining a company or a liability waiver at a gym. It is not an oath in court; usually it is a prepared form: 誓約書に署名する / サインする, 誓約書を提出する. Less formal private assurances are more often called 念書.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"覚書を交わしました。","romaji":"Oboegaki o kawashimashita.","german":"We exchanged a memorandum.","furigana":"おぼえがき|か"},{"japanese":"正式な契約の前に、まず覚書を作りましょう。","romaji":"Seishiki na keiyaku no mae ni, mazu oboegaki o tsukurimashou.","german":"Before the formal contract, let's first draw up a memorandum.","furigana":"せいしき|けいやく|まえ|おぼえがき|つく"},{"japanese":"古い日記の中から、祖父の覚書が見つかった。","romaji":"Furui nikki no naka kara, sofu no oboegaki ga mitsukatta.","german":"My grandfather's notes were found in an old diary.","furigana":"ふる|にっき|なか|そふ|おぼえがき|み"}],"notes":"覚書 (also written 覚え書き) is, in business, a written agreement or declaration of intent that is less comprehensive than a contract (契約書); you say 覚書を交わす or 覚書を結ぶ. In diplomacy it means a memorandum. It can also mean personal notes, but in everyday life メモ is usually used for that.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"琴の音色が美しい。","romaji":"Koto no neiro ga utsukushii.","german":"The sound of the koto is beautiful.","furigana":"こと|ねいろ|うつく"},{"japanese":"祖母はお正月に琴を弾いてくれます。","romaji":"Sobo wa oshougatsu ni koto o hiite kuremasu.","german":"My grandmother plays the koto for us at New Year.","furigana":"そぼ|しょうがつ|こと|ひ"},{"japanese":"大学で琴のサークルに入りました。","romaji":"Daigaku de koto no saakuru ni hairimashita.","german":"I joined a koto club at university.","furigana":"だいがく|こと|はい"}],"notes":"琴 is the traditional Japanese zither with usually 13 strings, which is plucked with finger picks. One \"plays\" it with 弾く: 琴を弾く; its sound is called 琴の音色. Technically the instrument is written 箏, but in daily life it is almost always written 琴. A Western harp, on the other hand, is called ハープ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"三味線を習いたい。","romaji":"Shamisen o naraitai.","german":"I want to learn the shamisen.","furigana":"しゃみせん|なら"},{"japanese":"津軽三味線の演奏はとても力強い。","romaji":"Tsugaru jamisen no ensou wa totemo chikarazuyoi.","german":"Tsugaru shamisen playing is very powerful.","furigana":"|えんそう|ちからづよ"},{"japanese":"祭りで、三味線に合わせて踊った。","romaji":"Matsuri de, shamisen ni awasete odotta.","german":"At the festival, I danced to the shamisen.","furigana":"まつ|しゃみせん|あ|おど"}],"notes":"三味線 is the three-stringed Japanese long-necked lute, played with a large plectrum (撥); one says 三味線を弾く. It accompanies kabuki, folk songs and festivals; particularly powerful is the 津軽三味線 style, in which the reading changes to じゃみせん. Colloquially, 三味線を弾く also means \"to put on an act, to evade\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"尺八の音が響く。","romaji":"Shakuhachi no oto ga hibiku.","german":"The sound of the shakuhachi echoes.","furigana":"しゃくはち|おと|ひび"},{"japanese":"父は定年後、尺八を吹き始めた。","romaji":"Chichi wa teinen-go, shakuhachi o fukihajimeta.","german":"After retiring, my father began playing the shakuhachi.","furigana":"ちち|ていねんご|しゃくはち|ふ|はじ"},{"japanese":"尺八と琴の合奏を聞きに行きました。","romaji":"Shakuhachi to koto no gassou o kiki ni ikimashita.","german":"I went to hear a concert of shakuhachi and koto together.","furigana":"しゃくはち|こと|がっそう|き|い"}],"notes":"尺八 is the Japanese bamboo flute played held vertically; its name comes from its standard length 一尺八寸 (about 54 cm). It is played with 吹く: 尺八を吹く. It is typically played together with the 琴 and the 三味線. Note: in vulgar slang the word also has a sexual meaning, but in normal contexts everyone thinks of the instrument.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"太鼓の演奏を見ました。","romaji":"Taiko no ensō o mimashita.","german":"I saw a taiko performance.","furigana":"たいこ|えんそう|み"},{"japanese":"夏祭りで子どもたちが太鼓をたたいていた。","romaji":"Natsumatsuri de kodomotachi ga taiko o tataite ita.","german":"At the summer festival, the children were beating the drum.","furigana":"なつまつ|こ|たいこ"},{"japanese":"この店の味は私が太鼓判を押します。","romaji":"Kono mise no aji wa watashi ga taikoban o oshimasu.","german":"I'll personally vouch for the taste of this restaurant.","furigana":"みせ|あじ|わたし|たいこばん|お"}],"notes":"太鼓 is the (traditional Japanese) drum; you strike it with たたく: 太鼓をたたく. To set it clearly apart from Western instruments, 和太鼓 is used; a drum set is called ドラム. A well-known idiom is 太鼓判を押す \"to guarantee something, to vouch for something\" (literally \"to press a big seal\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"笛を吹く練習をします。","romaji":"Fue o fuku renshū o shimasu.","german":"I practice playing the flute.","furigana":"ふえ|ふ|れんしゅう"},{"japanese":"審判が笛を吹いて、試合が始まった。","romaji":"Shinpan ga fue o fuite, shiai ga hajimatta.","german":"The referee blew the whistle, and the game began.","furigana":"しんぱん|ふえ|ふ|しあい|はじ"},{"japanese":"遠くから祭りの笛の音が聞こえてくる。","romaji":"Tooku kara matsuri no fue no oto ga kikoete kuru.","german":"From far away I can hear the flutes of the festival.","furigana":"とお|まつ|ふえ|おと|き"}],"notes":"笛 is a general term for flutes and whistles: from the Japanese transverse flute at festivals to the referee's whistle. One blows it with 吹く: 笛を吹く; the sound is called 笛の音. The recorder at school is usually called リコーダー, and a whistle also ホイッスル. Whistling with the mouth is 口笛を吹く.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鈴の音が聞こえる。","romaji":"Suzu no oto ga kikoeru.","german":"I can hear the sound of a bell.","furigana":"すず|おと|き"},{"japanese":"猫の首に鈴をつけました。","romaji":"Neko no kubi ni suzu o tsukemashita.","german":"I put a bell on the cat's collar.","furigana":"ねこ|くび|すず"},{"japanese":"山に登るときは、熊よけの鈴を持っていく。","romaji":"Yama ni noboru toki wa, kumayoke no suzu o motte iku.","german":"When I climb in the mountains, I take a bear bell with me.","furigana":"やま|のぼ|くま|すず|も"}],"notes":"鈴 is a small, usually spherical bell with a ball inside, as seen at shrines, on cat collars or as a bear bell when hiking. Typical uses: 鈴が鳴る (intransitive), 鈴を鳴らす (transitive), 鈴をつける. A large temple bell is called 鐘, a doorbell ベル or チャイム. Onomatopoeically, a 鈴 sounds like チリンチリン.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"絹のスカーフを買いました。","romaji":"Kinu no sukāfu o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a silk scarf.","furigana":"きぬ|か"},{"japanese":"この着物は絹でできています。","romaji":"Kono kimono wa kinu de dekite imasu.","german":"This kimono is made of silk.","furigana":"きもの|きぬ"},{"japanese":"スーパーで絹ごし豆腐を買ってきてくれる？","romaji":"Suupaa de kinugoshi doufu o katte kite kureru?","german":"Can you buy silken tofu at the supermarket?","furigana":"きぬ|どうふ|か"}],"notes":"絹 is silk as a material; before items of clothing it takes の: 絹のスカーフ, 絹のブラウス. When shopping, the loanword シルク is at least as common. Also well known is 絹ごし豆腐 \"silken tofu\", the especially delicate tofu in contrast to the firmer 木綿豆腐. Other materials are 綿 (cotton) and ウール or 毛 (wool).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"麻の服は涼しい。","romaji":"Asa no fuku wa suzushii.","german":"Linen clothes are cool.","furigana":"あさ|ふく|すず"},{"japanese":"このテーブルクロスは麻でできています。","romaji":"Kono teeburukurosu wa asa de dekite imasu.","german":"This tablecloth is made of linen.","furigana":"あさ"},{"japanese":"昔は麻から縄や布を作っていた。","romaji":"Mukashi wa asa kara nawa ya nuno o tsukutte ita.","german":"In the old days, rope and cloth were made from hemp.","furigana":"むかし|あさ|なわ|ぬの|つく"}],"notes":"麻 refers both to the hemp plant and to fibers and fabrics such as linen or ramie; on clothing labels, 麻 stands for linen. Typical: 麻の服, 麻のシャツ, which are considered cool in summer. The drug cannabis, by contrast, is called 大麻; the character is also found in 麻薬 \"narcotics\". Linen as a fashion word is also リネン.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"布団の綿が古くなって、固くなった。","romaji":"Futon no wata ga furuku natte, kataku natta.","german":"The cotton batting in my futon has gotten old and hard.","furigana":"ふとん|わた|ふる|かた"},{"japanese":"ぬいぐるみから綿が出ている。","romaji":"Nuigurumi kara wata ga dete iru.","german":"Stuffing is coming out of the stuffed toy.","furigana":"わた|で"},{"japanese":"お祭りで綿あめを買ってもらった。","romaji":"Omatsuri de wataame o katte moratta.","german":"I got cotton candy at the festival.","furigana":"まつ|わた|か"}],"notes":"綿 with the reading わた means raw cotton, the cotton plant and above all stuffing, such as in a 布団 or stuffed toys; cotton candy is called 綿あめ or 綿菓子. For the fabric on clothing labels, the same character is read めん (綿100%), or people say 木綿 or コットン. A cotton shirt is therefore 綿 (めん) のシャツ, not わた.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"布を織る技術を学ぶ。","romaji":"Nuno o oru gijutsu o manabu.","german":"I'm learning the technique of weaving cloth.","furigana":"ぬの|お|ぎじゅつ|まな"},{"japanese":"祖母は昔、自分で着物の布を織っていた。","romaji":"Sobo wa mukashi, jibun de kimono no nuno o otte ita.","german":"My grandmother used to weave the cloth for her kimonos herself.","furigana":"そぼ|むかし|じぶん|きもの|ぬの|お"},{"japanese":"この地方では、今も手でじゅうたんを織っています。","romaji":"Kono chihou de wa, ima mo te de juutan o otte imasu.","german":"In this region, carpets are still woven by hand.","furigana":"ちほう|いま|て|お"}],"notes":"織る means \"to weave\" and is transitive: 布を織る, 着物の生地を織る; the result is an 織物 (\"woven fabric, textile\"), the loom is called 機 (はた), hence also 機を織る. There is no intransitive counterpart. Not to be confused with the similar-sounding 折る \"to fold, to break\" or with 降りる \"to get off\". Figuratively: 織り込む \"to weave in, to incorporate\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"道場で稽古します。","romaji":"Dōjō de keiko shimasu.","german":"I train at the dojo.","furigana":"どうじょう|けいこ"},{"japanese":"子どものころ、毎週空手の道場に通っていた。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, maishuu karate no doujou ni kayotte ita.","german":"When I was a child, I went to a karate dojo every week.","furigana":"こ|まいしゅうからて|どうじょう|かよ"},{"japanese":"道場に入るときは、必ず礼をします。","romaji":"Doujou ni hairu toki wa, kanarazu rei o shimasu.","german":"When entering the dojo, you always bow.","furigana":"どうじょう|はい|かなら|れい"}],"notes":"道場 is the training hall for martial arts such as 柔道, 剣道 or 空手; one says 道場に通う \"to go to the dojo regularly\" and 道場で稽古する \"to train\". One bows when entering and leaving (礼をする). An ordinary sports hall is called 体育館, a gym ジム. Originally, the word referred to a place of Buddhist practice.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"三段の試験に合格しました。","romaji":"Sandan no shiken ni goukaku shimashita.","german":"I passed the third-dan exam.","furigana":"さんだん|しけん|ごうかく"},{"japanese":"彼は柔道二段の腕前です。","romaji":"Kare wa juudou nidan no udemae desu.","german":"He has a second dan in judo.","furigana":"かれ|じゅうどうにだん|うでまえ"},{"japanese":"初段を取るまで、五年かかった。","romaji":"Shodan o toru made, gonen kakatta.","german":"It took me five years to get my first dan.","furigana":"しょだん|と|ごねん"}],"notes":"段 here means the master grade (dan) in martial arts, Go or Shogi. The first grade is called 初段, not 一段; after that come 二段, 三段 and so on, and the student grades below it are called 級. One says 段を取る or 三段に合格する; \"He has a 2nd dan in judo\" is 柔道二段だ. In daily life, 段 also means \"step, level\", for example for stairs (階段) or shelves.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"釜でご飯を炊きます。","romaji":"Kama de gohan o takimasu.","german":"I cook rice in an iron pot.","furigana":"かま|はん|た"},{"japanese":"炊飯器の釜を洗ってください。","romaji":"Suihanki no kama o aratte kudasai.","german":"Please wash the inner pot of the rice cooker.","furigana":"すいはんき|かま|あら"},{"japanese":"彼とは同じ釜の飯を食った仲だ。","romaji":"Kare to wa onaji kama no meshi o kutta naka da.","german":"He and I have shared the same pot of rice; we are very close.","furigana":"かれ|おな|かま|めし|く|なか"}],"notes":"釜 is a heavy (iron) pot, mainly for cooking rice, often politely お釜; the inner pot of a rice cooker is also called 釜 or 内釜, and the tea kettle in the tea ceremony 茶釜. A dish is 釜飯, rice cooked in a pot. For stews you use a 鍋, for water a やかん. Idiom: 同じ釜の飯を食う \"to have lived and worked closely together\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"桶に水を入れます。","romaji":"Oke ni mizu o iremasu.","german":"I put water in the bucket.","furigana":"おけ|みず|い"},{"japanese":"寿司桶にご飯を移して、酢を混ぜる。","romaji":"Sushioke ni gohan o utsushite, su o mazeru.","german":"I transfer the rice into the sushi tub and mix in vinegar.","furigana":"すしおけ|はん|うつ|す|ま"},{"japanese":"温泉の桶で体にお湯をかけた。","romaji":"Onsen no oke de karada ni oyu o kaketa.","german":"At the onsen, I poured hot water over my body with a wooden bucket.","furigana":"おんせん|おけ|からだ|ゆ"}],"notes":"桶 is a tub or bucket, traditionally made of wood: 桶に水を入れる / くむ. Well known are the 寿司桶 for sushi rice, the 手桶 as a ladle bucket and the bath bucket at an onsen; the coffin is also called 棺桶. A modern bucket made of plastic or metal is usually called バケツ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"杵で餅をつきます。","romaji":"Kine de mochi o tsukimasu.","german":"I pound mochi with the pestle.","furigana":"きね|もち"},{"japanese":"杵を振り上げて、力いっぱい下ろした。","romaji":"Kine o furiagete, chikara ippai oroshita.","german":"I raised the pestle and brought it down with all my strength.","furigana":"きね|ふ|あ|ちから|お"},{"japanese":"月ではうさぎが杵で餅をついていると言われている。","romaji":"Tsuki de wa usagi ga kine de mochi o tsuite iru to iwarete iru.","german":"They say a rabbit on the moon is pounding mochi with a pestle.","furigana":"つき|きね|もち|い"}],"notes":"杵 is the large wooden mallet or pestle used to pound steamed rice into mochi in a 臼; you say 杵で餅をつく. In the traditional 餅つき at New Year, one person strikes with the 杵 while another turns the dough by hand. In Japan it is said that you can see a rabbit in the moon pounding mochi with a 杵.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"臼と杵で餅つきをします。","romaji":"Usu to kine de mochitsuki o shimasu.","german":"We make mochi with a mortar and pestle.","furigana":"うす|きね|もち"},{"japanese":"石臼でそば粉をひいています。","romaji":"Ishiusu de sobako o hiite imasu.","german":"Buckwheat flour is being ground with a stone mill.","furigana":"いしうす|こ"},{"japanese":"餅つきの前に、臼をお湯で温めておく。","romaji":"Mochitsuki no mae ni, usu o oyu de atatamete oku.","german":"Before pounding mochi, the mortar is warmed with hot water.","furigana":"もち|まえ|うす|ゆ|あたた"}],"notes":"臼 is a mortar or millstone. For pounding mochi, a large 臼 made of wood or stone is used together with the pestle 杵: 臼と杵で餅をつく. A grindstone for flour or tea is called 石臼, and you grind (ひく) with it. The character is also found in 臼歯 (きゅうし) \"molar\", literally \"mortar tooth\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"湯呑みでお茶を飲みます。","romaji":"Yunomi de ocha o nomimasu.","german":"I drink tea from a teacup.","furigana":"ゆの|ちゃ|の"},{"japanese":"来客用の湯呑みを五つ買いました。","romaji":"Raikyakuyou no yunomi o itsutsu kaimashita.","german":"I bought five teacups for guests.","furigana":"らいきゃくよう|ゆの|いつ|か"},{"japanese":"寿司屋の大きな湯呑みには、魚の漢字が書いてある。","romaji":"Sushiya no ookina yunomi ni wa, sakana no kanji ga kaite aru.","german":"The large teacups at the sushi restaurant have fish kanji written on them.","furigana":"すしや|おお|ゆの|さかな|かんじ|か"}],"notes":"湯呑み is the handleless Japanese teacup for everyday green tea; it is the short form of 湯呑み茶碗 and is also written 湯飲み or 湯のみ. A cup with a handle is called カップ or マグカップ. Do not confuse it with 茶碗, which in everyday use mainly means the rice bowl (and in the tea ceremony also the matcha bowl).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"徳利にお酒を入れます。","romaji":"Tokkuri ni osake o iremasu.","german":"I pour sake into the flask.","furigana":"とっくり|さけ|い"},{"japanese":"熱燗を徳利で頼んだ。","romaji":"Atsukan o tokkuri de tanonda.","german":"I ordered hot sake in a flask.","furigana":"|とっくり|たの"},{"japanese":"父は昔、とっくりのセーターをよく着ていた。","romaji":"Chichi wa mukashi, tokkuri no seetaa o yoku kite ita.","german":"My father often wore turtleneck sweaters in the old days.","furigana":"ちち|むかし|き"}],"notes":"徳利 is the bulbous flask with a narrow neck in which sake is served (often warmed); you drink from it into small cups (お猪口). In restaurants one often orders with the synonym お銚子: お銚子を一本. Because of its shape, a turtleneck sweater is colloquially also called とっくり(のセーター), though today タートルネック is usual. The word is often written in kana.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"猪口でお酒を楽しみます。","romaji":"Choko de osake o tanoshimimasu.","german":"I enjoy sake from a small cup.","furigana":"ちょこ|さけ|たの"},{"japanese":"お猪口を二つ持ってきてください。","romaji":"Ochoko o futatsu motte kite kudasai.","german":"Please bring two sake cups.","furigana":"ちょこ|ふた|も"},{"japanese":"強い風で傘がおちょこになった。","romaji":"Tsuyoi kaze de kasa ga ochoko ni natta.","german":"The strong wind turned my umbrella inside out.","furigana":"つよ|かぜ|かさ"}],"notes":"猪口 is the small sake cup that you drink from together with the 徳利. It is almost always said politely as お猪口 and usually written in kana: おちょこ. A well-known expression is 傘がおちょこになる \"the umbrella turns inside out (in the wind)\", because it then looks like a small cup. For Western-style glasses, グラス is used.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"俎板の上で野菜を切ります。","romaji":"Manaita no ue de yasai o kirimasu.","german":"I cut vegetables on the cutting board.","furigana":"まないた|うえ|やさい|き"},{"japanese":"使ったまな板は熱湯で消毒します。","romaji":"Tsukatta manaita wa nettou de shoudoku shimasu.","german":"I disinfect the used cutting board with boiling water.","furigana":"つか|いた|ねっとう|しょうどく"},{"japanese":"手術の前は、まな板の上の鯉の気分だった。","romaji":"Shujutsu no mae wa, manaita no ue no koi no kibun datta.","german":"Before the operation, I felt completely at the mercy of fate.","furigana":"しゅじゅつ|まえ|いた|うえ|こい|きぶん"}],"notes":"俎板 is the cutting board in the kitchen; today it is almost always written まな板, and the spelling 俎板 is rare. You cut on it: まな板の上で切る. The idiom まな板の(上の)鯉 \"a carp on the cutting board\" describes someone who is at the mercy of fate and can do nothing more, for example before an operation or exam results.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"おろし金で大根をおろす。","romaji":"Oroshigane de daikon o orosu.","german":"I grate daikon radish with the grater.","furigana":"がね|だいこん"},{"japanese":"おろし金は使ったらすぐに洗わないと、汚れが落ちにくい。","romaji":"Oroshigane wa tsukattara sugu ni arawanai to, yogore ga ochinikui.","german":"If you don't wash the grater right after use, the dirt is hard to remove.","furigana":"がね|つか|あら|よご|お"},{"japanese":"100円ショップで小さいおろし金を買った。","romaji":"Hyaku-en shoppu de chiisai oroshigane o katta.","german":"I bought a small grater at the 100-yen shop.","furigana":"えん|ちい|がね|か"}],"notes":"おろし金 is the fine Japanese grater, used mainly to grate daikon radish, ginger or wasabi. The matching verb is おろす or すりおろす: 大根をおろす; the result is called 大根おろし. The 金 stands for the metal it is traditionally made of and is read がね in this word. A coarse grater for cheese or vegetables is more often called グレーター or スライサー.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この路線は便利です。","romaji":"Kono rosen wa benri desu.","german":"This route is convenient.","furigana":"ろせん|べんり"},{"japanese":"駅で路線図をもらいました。","romaji":"Eki de rosenzu o moraimashita.","german":"I got a route map at the station.","furigana":"えき|ろせんず"},{"japanese":"会社は経営の路線を大きく変えた。","romaji":"Kaisha wa keiei no rosen o ookiku kaeta.","german":"The company greatly changed its management course.","furigana":"かいしゃ|けいえい|ろせん|おお|か"}],"notes":"路線 is the transport line or route of a train, bus or aircraft: 路線バス \"local bus\", 路線図 \"route map\", 新しい路線が開通する. An individual named railway line is designated with 〜線 (山手線). Figuratively, 路線 also means the \"course\" of an organization or policy: 路線を変更する, 改革路線.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"架線が切れて電車が止まった。","romaji":"Kasen ga kirete densha ga tomatta.","german":"The overhead wire snapped and the train stopped.","furigana":"かせん|き|でんしゃ|と"},{"japanese":"架線に風船が引っかかったため、運転を見合わせています。","romaji":"Kasen ni fuusen ga hikkakatta tame, unten o miawasete imasu.","german":"Because a balloon got caught in the overhead wire, service is suspended.","furigana":"かせん|ふうせん|ひ|うんてん|みあ"},{"japanese":"強風で架線が揺れて、火花が出た。","romaji":"Kyoufuu de kasen ga yurete, hibana ga deta.","german":"The overhead wire swayed in the strong wind and sparks flew.","furigana":"きょうふう|かせん|ゆ|ひばな|で"}],"notes":"架線 is the overhead wire above railway tracks; you mostly hear the word in announcements and news about disruptions: 架線トラブル, 架線が切れる, 架線に物が引っかかる. Note that there are homophones: 河川 \"rivers\", 化繊 \"synthetic fiber\" and 下線 \"underline\"; in railway jargon it is therefore often said がせん. General power lines are usually called 電線.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"回数券を買いました。","romaji":"Kaisūken o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a coupon ticket.","furigana":"かいすうけん|か"},{"japanese":"回数券なら、11回分が10回分の値段で買えます。","romaji":"Kaisuuken nara, juuikkai-bun ga jukkai-bun no nedan de kaemasu.","german":"With a coupon booklet, you get 11 rides for the price of 10.","furigana":"かいすうけん|かいぶん|かいぶん|ねだん|か"},{"japanese":"この店のコーヒー回数券はお得です。","romaji":"Kono mise no koohii kaisuuken wa otoku desu.","german":"The coffee coupon booklet of this shop is a good deal.","furigana":"みせ|かいすうけん|とく"}],"notes":"回数券 is a booklet of tickets or multi-use card that usually gives a discount, typically eleven rides for the price of ten. It exists for buses and trains, but also for cafes, baths or classes. You say 回数券を買う / 使う; with IC cards such as Suica, paper 回数券 have become rarer. A monthly pass, by contrast, is called 定期券.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"車を車庫に入れます。","romaji":"Kuruma o shako ni iremasu.","german":"I put the car in the garage.","furigana":"くるま|しゃこ|い"},{"japanese":"このバスは車庫行きです。","romaji":"Kono basu wa shako-iki desu.","german":"This bus is going to the depot.","furigana":"しゃこい"},{"japanese":"車を買うには、車庫証明が必要です。","romaji":"Kuruma o kau ni wa, shako shoumei ga hitsuyou desu.","german":"To buy a car, you need a garage certificate.","furigana":"くるま|か|しゃこしょうめい|ひつよう"}],"notes":"車庫 is a garage for a car, but also the depot for buses and trains: このバスは車庫行きです \"goes to the depot\". Typical: 車を車庫に入れる; parking into the garage is called 車庫入れ. In Japan you need a 車庫証明 (proof of parking space) to register a car. A parking lot or parking garage, by contrast, is called 駐車場; ガレージ is a synonym for a private garage.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地下道を通ります。","romaji":"Chikadō o tōrimasu.","german":"I go through the underpass.","furigana":"ちかどう|とお"},{"japanese":"雨の日は、地下道を通って駅まで行けるので便利です。","romaji":"Ame no hi wa, chikadou o tootte eki made ikeru node benri desu.","german":"On rainy days it's convenient, because you can get to the station through the underground passage.","furigana":"あめ|ひ|ちかどう|とお|えき|い|べんり"},{"japanese":"夜の地下道は暗くて少し怖い。","romaji":"Yoru no chikadou wa kurakute sukoshi kowai.","german":"The underpass is dark and a little scary at night.","furigana":"よる|ちかどう|くら|すこ|こわ"}],"notes":"地下道 is an underground pedestrian passage or underpass, for example under a large road or between a station and buildings: 地下道を通る / 抜ける. Not to be confused with 地下街, the underground shopping arcade, and 地下鉄, the subway. Over the road, on the other hand, runs the pedestrian bridge 歩道橋.","levelNote":"Compound: 地下 (N5) + 道 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この道は袋小路です。","romaji":"Kono michi wa fukuro kōji desu.","german":"Diese Straße ist eine Sackgasse.","furigana":"みち|ふくろこうじ"},{"japanese":"道に迷って、袋小路に入ってしまった。","romaji":"Michi ni mayotte, fukuro kouji ni haitte shimatta.","german":"Ich habe mich verlaufen und bin in einer Sackgasse gelandet.","furigana":"みち|まよ|ふくろこうじ|はい"},{"japanese":"話し合いは袋小路に入り込んでしまった。","romaji":"Hanashiai wa fukuro kouji ni hairikonde shimatta.","german":"Die Gespräche sind in eine Sackgasse geraten.","furigana":"はな|あ|ふくろこうじ|はい|こ"}],"notes":"袋小路 ist wörtlich eine „Sack-Gasse“ (袋 „Beutel“ + 小路 „Gasse“), also eine Straße ohne Ausgang: 袋小路に入る / 迷い込む. Wie im Deutschen wird es auch übertragen gebraucht: 交渉が袋小路に入り込む „die Verhandlungen geraten in eine Sackgasse“. Auf Schildern und im Alltag sagt man für eine Sackgasse meist schlicht 行き止まり.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"データベースを更新します。","romaji":"Dētabēsu o kōshin shimasu.","german":"I'm updating the database.","furigana":"こうしん"},{"japanese":"お客様の情報はすべてデータベースで管理しています。","romaji":"Okyakusama no jouhou wa subete deetabeesu de kanri shite imasu.","german":"We manage all customer information in a database.","furigana":"きゃくさま|じょうほう|かんり"},{"japanese":"図書館のデータベースで論文を探した。","romaji":"Toshokan no deetabeesu de ronbun o sagashita.","german":"I looked for papers in the library's database.","furigana":"としょかん|ろんぶん|さが"}],"notes":"データベース is the database in IT, often abbreviated DB. Typical combinations: データベースを作る / 構築する, データベースを更新する, データベースで検索する, データベースに登録する. It is also used for collections of information in general, such as a library's literature database. The individual data are called データ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"サーバーがダウンしました。","romaji":"Sābā ga daun shimashita.","german":"The server went down."},{"japanese":"ファイルは共有サーバーに保存してください。","romaji":"Fairu wa kyouyuu saabaa ni hozon shite kudasai.","german":"Please save the files on the shared server.","furigana":"きょうゆう|ほぞん"},{"japanese":"オフィスにウォーターサーバーを置きました。","romaji":"Ofisu ni wootaa saabaa o okimashita.","german":"We put a water dispenser in the office."}],"notes":"サーバー is above all the server in IT; in technical texts it is often written サーバ without the long-vowel mark. Typical: サーバーがダウンする / 落ちる \"the server goes down\", サーバーに保存する / 接続する. In daily life you also encounter the word as ウォーターサーバー \"water dispenser\" and in sports for the server in tennis or volleyball.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ネットワークに接続します。","romaji":"Nettowāku ni setsuzoku shimasu.","german":"I'm connecting to the network.","furigana":"せつぞく"},{"japanese":"ネットワークの障害で、メールが送れません。","romaji":"Nettowaaku no shougai de, meeru ga okuremasen.","german":"Because of a network failure, I can't send email.","furigana":"しょうがい|おく"},{"japanese":"仕事のネットワークを広げるために、交流会に参加した。","romaji":"Shigoto no nettowaaku o hirogeru tame ni, kouryuukai ni sanka shita.","german":"To expand my professional network, I took part in a networking event.","furigana":"しごと|ひろ|こうりゅうかい|さんか"}],"notes":"ネットワーク is a network, first of all a computer network: ネットワークに接続する / つなぐ, ネットワークの障害 \"network failure\". It can also refer to a network of people, stores or broadcasters: ネットワークを広げる \"to expand one's network\". The short form ネット, on the other hand, mostly means the internet.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"暗号を解読する。","romaji":"Angō o kaidoku suru.","german":"I'm decoding the code.","furigana":"あんごう|かいどく"},{"japanese":"このサイトでは、データは暗号化されて送られます。","romaji":"Kono saito de wa, deeta wa angouka sarete okuraremasu.","german":"On this website, data is sent encrypted.","furigana":"あんごうか|おく"},{"japanese":"子どものころ、友達と暗号で手紙を書いて遊んだ。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, tomodachi to angou de tegami o kaite asonda.","german":"When we were children, my friends and I had fun writing letters in code.","furigana":"こ|ともだち|あんごう|てがみ|か|あそ"}],"notes":"暗号 is a secret code or cipher, that is, an encrypted message: 暗号を解く / 解読する \"to decipher\", 暗号で書く. The process of encryption is called 暗号化 (暗号化する \"to encrypt\"). A login password is not called 暗号 but パスワード, and a PIN is 暗証番号.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ファイルを添付します。","romaji":"Fairu o tenpu shimasu.","german":"I'm attaching the file.","furigana":"てんぷ"},{"japanese":"添付ファイルが開けないのですが。","romaji":"Tenpu fairu ga hirakenai no desu ga.","german":"I can't open the attachment, though.","furigana":"てんぷ|ひら"},{"japanese":"申込書には住民票を添付してください。","romaji":"Moushikomisho ni wa juuminhyou o tenpu shite kudasai.","german":"Please attach a certificate of residence to the application form.","furigana":"もうしこみしょ|じゅうみんひょう|てんぷ"}],"notes":"添付 means \"to attach, to append\" and is used as a transitive する-verb: メールにファイルを添付する; an attachment is called 添付ファイル. Documents are also attached to applications: 書類を添付する, formally 〜を添付の上、提出してください. If you put something in an envelope, people tend to say 同封する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"メールを受信しました。","romaji":"Mēru o jushin shimashita.","german":"I received an email.","furigana":"じゅしん"},{"japanese":"受信箱に未読のメールがたくさんある。","romaji":"Jushinbako ni midoku no meeru ga takusan aru.","german":"There are lots of unread emails in the inbox.","furigana":"じゅしんばこ|みどく"},{"japanese":"山の中なので、テレビの受信状態が悪い。","romaji":"Yama no naka na node, terebi no jushin joutai ga warui.","german":"Because we're in the mountains, TV reception is poor.","furigana":"やま|なか|じゅしんじょうたい|わる"}],"notes":"受信 is the reception of messages or signals and is used as a transitive する-verb: メールを受信する. Common compounds: 受信箱 \"inbox\", 受信料 \"broadcast reception fee\", 受信状態 \"reception quality\". The counterpart is 送信 \"sending\". For physical things such as packages, people say 受け取る instead.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"メールを送信します。","romaji":"Mēru o sōshin shimasu.","german":"I'm sending an email.","furigana":"そうしん"},{"japanese":"間違えて、書きかけのメールを送信してしまった。","romaji":"Machigaete, kakikake no meeru o soushin shite shimatta.","german":"I accidentally sent an unfinished email.","furigana":"まちが|か|そうしん"},{"japanese":"入力が終わったら、送信ボタンを押してください。","romaji":"Nyuuryoku ga owattara, soushin botan o oshite kudasai.","german":"When you have finished typing, please press the send button.","furigana":"にゅうりょく|お|そうしん|お"}],"notes":"送信 is the electronic sending of messages or data, as a transitive する-verb: メールを送信する; the button is called 送信ボタン. In conversation people usually just say 送る; 送信 is typical of on-screen text and technology. The counterpart is 受信; related are 返信 \"reply\" and 転送 \"forwarding\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"資料を閲覧します。","romaji":"Shiryō o etsuran shimasu.","german":"I will look through the documents.","furigana":"しりょう|えつらん"},{"japanese":"図書館の閲覧室では静かにしてください。","romaji":"Toshokan no etsuranshitsu de wa shizuka ni shite kudasai.","german":"Please be quiet in the library's reading room.","furigana":"としょかん|えつらんしつ|しず"},{"japanese":"ブラウザの閲覧履歴を消しました。","romaji":"Burauza no etsuran rireki o keshimashita.","german":"I cleared my browser's browsing history.","furigana":"えつらんりれき|け"}],"notes":"閲覧 means \"to look through, to inspect\" documents, books or web pages and is a formal transitive する-verb: 資料を閲覧する. Typical are 閲覧室 \"reading room\", 閲覧履歴 \"browsing history\" and 閲覧禁止 \"no viewing allowed\". In everyday speech one simply says 見る or 読む; 閲覧 sounds official or technical.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"パソコンを起動します。","romaji":"Pasokon o kidō shimasu.","german":"I'm starting up the computer.","furigana":"きどう"},{"japanese":"スマホが起動しなくなって、困っています。","romaji":"Sumaho ga kidou shinaku natte, komatte imasu.","german":"My smartphone won't start anymore, and I'm in trouble.","furigana":"きどう|こま"},{"japanese":"アプリを起動すると、すぐに広告が出る。","romaji":"Apuri o kidou suru to, sugu ni koukoku ga deru.","german":"As soon as I launch the app, ads appear.","furigana":"きどう|こうこく|で"}],"notes":"起動 is the starting of computers, programs or machines. As a する-verb it is both transitive and intransitive: パソコンを起動する \"to boot up the PC\", but also パソコンが起動しない \"the PC won't start\". Restarting is 再起動する. Colloquially people also say 立ち上げる / 立ち上がる; a vehicle, on the other hand, is started with エンジンをかける.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"防波堤で釣りをします。","romaji":"Bōhatei de tsuri o shimasu.","german":"I fish at the breakwater.","furigana":"ぼうはてい|つ"},{"japanese":"台風の日は、波が防波堤を越えることがある。","romaji":"Taifuu no hi wa, nami ga bouhatei o koeru koto ga aru.","german":"On typhoon days, waves sometimes go over the breakwater.","furigana":"たいふう|ひ|なみ|ぼうはてい|こ"},{"japanese":"課長はいつも私たちの防波堤になってくれる。","romaji":"Kachou wa itsumo watashitachi no bouhatei ni natte kureru.","german":"Our section chief always shields us.","furigana":"かちょう|わたし|ぼうはてい"}],"notes":"防波堤 is a breakwater or mole that protects a harbor from waves (防ぐ \"to ward off\" + 波 \"wave\" + 堤 \"embankment\"). 防波堤で釣りをする is popular. A levee along rivers, by contrast, is called 堤防. Figuratively, a person can be 〜の防波堤になる, that is, shield others from attacks or criticism.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"水路に沿って歩きます。","romaji":"Suiro ni sotte arukimasu.","german":"I walk along the canal.","furigana":"すいろ|そ|ある"},{"japanese":"田んぼの横の用水路に落ちないように気をつけて。","romaji":"Tanbo no yoko no yousuiro ni ochinai you ni ki o tsukete.","german":"Be careful not to fall into the irrigation ditch next to the rice field.","furigana":"た|よこ|ようすいろ|お|き"},{"japanese":"この町には昔から水路が多く、舟で荷物を運んでいた。","romaji":"Kono machi ni wa mukashi kara suiro ga ooku, fune de nimotsu o hakonde ita.","german":"This town has had many canals since long ago, and goods were carried by boat.","furigana":"まち|むかし|すいろ|おお|ふね|にもつ|はこ"}],"notes":"水路 is a waterway in the broad sense: canal, ditch or water channel. Very common is 用水路 \"irrigation ditch\" next to rice fields. A large navigable canal is more often called 運河, a natural river 川. In swimming, 水路 also refers to a swimmer's lane (第3水路).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"竹林を散歩しました。","romaji":"Chikurin o sanpo shimashita.","german":"I took a walk through the bamboo grove.","furigana":"ちくりん|さんぽ"},{"japanese":"京都の嵯峨野の竹林は、観光客にとても人気がある。","romaji":"Kyouto no Sagano no chikurin wa, kankoukyaku ni totemo ninki ga aru.","german":"The bamboo grove at Sagano in Kyoto is very popular with tourists.","furigana":"きょうと||ちくりん|かんこうきゃく|にんき"},{"japanese":"裏の竹林で、春になるとたけのこが採れる。","romaji":"Ura no chikurin de, haru ni naru to takenoko ga toreru.","german":"In the bamboo grove behind the house, you can harvest bamboo shoots in spring.","furigana":"うら|ちくりん|はる|と"}],"notes":"竹林 is a bamboo grove or small bamboo wood. The Sino-Japanese reading ちくりん sounds somewhat more elevated; there is also the native reading たけばやし. The bamboo forest in 嵯峨野 near Kyoto is famous. In spring, young bamboo shoots (たけのこ) are harvested in the 竹林.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"雑木林で虫を探す。","romaji":"Zōkibayashi de mushi o sagasu.","german":"I look for insects in the copse.","furigana":"ぞうきばやし|むし|さが"},{"japanese":"家の裏には小さな雑木林がある。","romaji":"Ie no ura ni wa chiisana zoukibayashi ga aru.","german":"There is a small copse behind our house.","furigana":"いえ|うら|ちい|ぞうきばやし"},{"japanese":"秋になると、雑木林の木々が赤や黄色に色づく。","romaji":"Aki ni naru to, zoukibayashi no kigi ga aka ya kiiro ni irozuku.","german":"When autumn comes, the trees in the copse turn red and yellow.","furigana":"あき|ぞうきばやし|きぎ|あか|きいろ|いろ"}],"notes":"雑木林 is a small grove of various, mostly deciduous trees such as oaks, typical of the landscape at the edge of a village (里山). So it is not a large forest; for that one says 森 or 森林. The reading is ぞうき (as in 雑巾), not ざつ. Children like to look there in summer for beetles such as カブトムシ.","levelNote":"Compound: 雑木 (N1) + 林 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"湿地に珍しい鳥がいます。","romaji":"Shicchi ni mezurashii tori ga imasu.","german":"Rare birds live in the wetland.","furigana":"しっち|めずら|とり"},{"japanese":"この辺りは昔、湿地だったそうです。","romaji":"Kono atari wa mukashi, shicchi datta sou desu.","german":"I hear this area used to be a wetland.","furigana":"あた|むかし|しっち"},{"japanese":"湿地を守るために、ボランティアがごみを拾っています。","romaji":"Shicchi o mamoru tame ni, borantia ga gomi o hirotte imasu.","german":"Volunteers pick up trash to protect the wetland.","furigana":"しっち|まも|ひろ"}],"notes":"湿地 is an area with permanently wet, marshy ground - \"wetland, marshland\". Typical are 湿地帯 (\"wetland, marsh area\") and 湿地に生息する鳥. A marsh or pond with standing water is more often called 沼, a larger moorland landscape 湿原 (e.g. 釧路湿原). The word is factual and appears mainly with nature and environmental topics; for damp ground in daily life, people tend to describe it with じめじめした土地.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"洞窟を探検します。","romaji":"Dōkutsu o tanken shimasu.","german":"I'm exploring the cave.","furigana":"どうくつ|たんけん"},{"japanese":"洞窟の中は夏でもひんやりしています。","romaji":"Doukutsu no naka wa natsu demo hinyari shite imasu.","german":"It's pleasantly cool inside the cave even in summer."},{"japanese":"大昔の人は洞窟に住んでいたそうです。","romaji":"Oomukashi no hito wa doukutsu ni sunde ita sou desu.","german":"People in ancient times are said to have lived in caves.","furigana":"おおむかし|ひと|どうくつ|す"}],"notes":"洞窟 is a natural cave in rock or a mountain. Typical combinations are 洞窟に入る, 洞窟を探検する and 洞窟の中は暗い／涼しい. The synonymous 洞穴（ほらあな） sounds somewhat more colloquial; a small hole or an animal's burrow is simply called 穴, a dug tunnel トンネル. The kanji 窟 is rare, but the word itself is common in travel guides and the news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"生垣を刈り込みます。","romaji":"Ikegaki o karikomimasu.","german":"I trim the hedge.","furigana":"いけがき|か|こ"},{"japanese":"家の周りは高い生垣に囲まれています。","romaji":"Ie no mawari wa takai ikegaki ni kakomarete imasu.","german":"The house is surrounded by a tall hedge.","furigana":"いえ|まわ|たか|いけがき|かこ"},{"japanese":"猫が生垣の隙間から出てきました。","romaji":"Neko ga ikegaki no sukima kara dete kimashita.","german":"A cat came out through a gap in the hedge.","furigana":"ねこ|いけがき|すきま|で"}],"notes":"生垣 (also written 生け垣) is a hedge of living shrubs that marks the boundary of a property - literally a \"living fence\". By contrast, 塀 is a wall or solid fence made of stone, concrete or wood, フェンス is a (metal) fence; 垣根 is the general term \"enclosure\". Typical uses are 生垣を刈り込む／手入れする (\"to trim/maintain the hedge\") and 生垣に囲まれた家.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"欄間の彫刻が見事です。","romaji":"Ranma no chōkoku ga migoto desu.","german":"The carving on the transom is magnificent.","furigana":"らんま|ちょうこく|みごと"},{"japanese":"欄間から隣の部屋の明かりが漏れています。","romaji":"Ranma kara tonari no heya no akari ga morete imasu.","german":"Light from the next room leaks through the transom.","furigana":"らんま|となり|へや|あ|も"},{"japanese":"古い家を直すとき、欄間だけは残しました。","romaji":"Furui ie o naosu toki, ranma dake wa nokoshimashita.","german":"When we repaired the old house, we kept only the transom.","furigana":"ふる|いえ|なお|らんま|のこ"}],"notes":"欄間 is, in traditional Japanese houses, the open, often elaborately carved or latticed wooden panel between the lintel of the sliding doors (鴨居) and the ceiling. It lets light and air pass between two rooms and also serves as decoration; it roughly corresponds to a \"transom window\". Typical uses are 欄間の彫刻 (\"carving on the ranma\") and 欄間から光が入る. It is an architectural technical term that you mostly hear in connection with old houses, temples and ryokan.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"台風の前に雨戸を閉めます。","romaji":"Taifū no mae ni amado o shimemasu.","german":"I close the storm shutters before the typhoon.","furigana":"たいふう|まえ|あまど|し"},{"japanese":"朝起きたら、まず雨戸を開けます。","romaji":"Asa okitara, mazu amado o akemasu.","german":"When I get up in the morning, the first thing I do is open the shutters.","furigana":"あさお|あまど|あ"},{"japanese":"夜中に風で雨戸がガタガタ鳴っていました。","romaji":"Yonaka ni kaze de amado ga gatagata natte imashita.","german":"In the middle of the night, the shutters were rattling in the wind.","furigana":"よなか|かぜ|あまど|な"}],"notes":"雨戸 are sliding shutters made of wood or metal that are pulled across the outside of windows and glass sliding doors - as protection against storms, rain and burglars. You say 雨戸を閉める／開ける, and in bad weather also 雨戸を閉め切る (\"to shut all the shutters tight\"). Modern roller shutters are usually called シャッター; European hinged shutters are more often called よろい戸. Closing the 雨戸 before a typhoon is a typical precaution in Japan.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"戸棚に食器を入れます。","romaji":"Todana ni shokki o iremasu.","german":"I put the dishes in the cupboard.","furigana":"とだな|しょっき|い"},{"japanese":"お菓子は台所の戸棚の上の段にあるよ。","romaji":"Okashi wa daidokoro no todana no ue no dan ni aru yo.","german":"The sweets are on the top shelf of the kitchen cupboard.","furigana":"かし|だいどころ|とだな|うえ|だん"},{"japanese":"戸棚の中を整理したら、古い缶詰が出てきた。","romaji":"Todana no naka o seiri shitara, furui kanzume ga dete kita.","german":"When I tidied up the cupboard, some old canned food turned up.","furigana":"とだな|なか|せいり|ふる|かんづめ|で"}],"notes":"戸棚 is a cupboard with doors and shelves, mainly for dishes, supplies or small items in the kitchen and living room; the dedicated dish cabinet is called 食器棚. For clothing, タンス or クローゼット is more usual, and for the built-in futon closet 押し入れ. Typical uses are 戸棚にしまう (\"to put away in the cupboard\"), 戸棚から出す and 戸棚の上の段.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"庭を掃きます。","romaji":"Niwa o hakimasu.","german":"I sweep the garden.","furigana":"にわ|は"},{"japanese":"玄関の前の落ち葉を掃いてくれる？","romaji":"Genkan no mae no ochiba o haite kureru?","german":"Could you sweep up the fallen leaves in front of the entrance?","furigana":"げんかん|まえ|お|ば|は"},{"japanese":"割れたガラスをほうきで掃いて集めました。","romaji":"Wareta garasu o houki de haite atsumemashita.","german":"I swept up the broken glass with a broom.","furigana":"わ|は|あつ"}],"notes":"掃く means \"to sweep with a broom (ほうき)\". The object with を is either the place (庭を掃く, 床を掃く) or the dirt (落ち葉を掃く, ごみを掃き集める). Not to be confused with the identically read 履く (\"to put on shoes/trousers\") and 吐く (\"to spit, to vomit\"). Vacuuming is 掃除機をかける, wiping with a cloth 拭く; sweeping as a household chore is called 掃き掃除.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"テーブルを拭きます。","romaji":"Tēburu o fukimasu.","german":"I wipe the table.","furigana":"ふ"},{"japanese":"このタオルで汗を拭いてください。","romaji":"Kono taoru de ase o fuite kudasai.","german":"Please wipe off your sweat with this towel.","furigana":"あせ|ふ"},{"japanese":"洗った皿をふきんで拭いて、棚に戻しました。","romaji":"Aratta sara o fukin de fuite, tana ni modoshimashita.","german":"I dried the washed dishes with a dishcloth and put them back on the shelf.","furigana":"あら|さら|ふ|たな|もど"}],"notes":"拭く means \"to wipe, to dry (with a cloth)\". The object is the surface or what is being removed: テーブルを拭く, 汗を拭く, 涙を拭く; the means takes で (タオルで拭く, ふきんで拭く). Not to be confused with 掃く (\"to sweep\") and the identically read 吹く (\"to blow\"). For drying hands, people say 手を拭く; wet wiping can be specified as 水拭き, dry wiping as 乾拭き.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"洗濯物を畳みます。","romaji":"Sentakumono o tatamimasu.","german":"I fold the laundry.","furigana":"せんたくもの|たた"},{"japanese":"電車に乗る前に傘を畳みました。","romaji":"Densha ni noru mae ni kasa o tatamimashita.","german":"I closed my umbrella before getting on the train.","furigana":"でんしゃ|の|まえ|かさ|たた"},{"japanese":"祖父は四十年続けた店を畳むことにしました。","romaji":"Sofu wa yonjuunen tsuzuketa mise o tatamu koto ni shimashita.","german":"My grandfather decided to close down the shop he had run for forty years.","furigana":"そふ|よんじゅうねんつづ|みせ|たた"}],"notes":"畳む means \"to fold up, to fold together, to close\" - above all for fabric and larger things: 洗濯物を畳む, 布団を畳む, 傘を畳む (\"to close an umbrella\"). Paper, on the other hand, is mostly folded with 折る. Figuratively, 店を畳む means \"to give up a business (for good)\". The word is often written in kana as たたむ; the same kanji is in 畳 (\"tatami\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"布団を干します。","romaji":"Futon o hoshimasu.","german":"I air out the futon.","furigana":"ふとん|ほ"},{"japanese":"雨が降りそうだから、洗濯物は部屋の中に干そう。","romaji":"Ame ga furisou dakara, sentakumono wa heya no naka ni hosou.","german":"It looks like rain, so let's hang the laundry inside.","furigana":"あめ|ふ|せんたくもの|へや|なか|ほ"},{"japanese":"彼はビールを一気に飲み干した。","romaji":"Kare wa biiru o ikki ni nomihoshita.","german":"He drained the beer in one go.","furigana":"かれ|いっき|の|ほ"}],"notes":"干す means \"to hang or spread out to dry (in the sun or air)\": 洗濯物を干す, 布団を干す; the place is marked with に (ベランダに干す). The intransitive \"to become dry\" is 乾く, and active drying, for example with a hair dryer, is 乾かす (髪を乾かす) - 干す emphasizes hanging or spreading out. Derived from it are 干物 (\"dried fish\") and 干し柿; as a verb suffix, 飲み干す means \"to drink up completely\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鉄が錆びた。","romaji":"Tetsu ga sabita.","german":"The iron rusted.","furigana":"てつ|さ"},{"japanese":"雨の中に置いておいたら、自転車が錆びてしまった。","romaji":"Ame no naka ni oite oitara, jitensha ga sabite shimatta.","german":"I left the bicycle out in the rain and it has rusted.","furigana":"あめ|なか|お|じてんしゃ|さ"},{"japanese":"錆びないように、使った後は包丁をよく拭いてください。","romaji":"Sabinai you ni, tsukatta ato wa houchou o yoku fuite kudasai.","german":"To keep the kitchen knife from rusting, wipe it well after use.","furigana":"さ|つか|あと|ほうちょう|ふ"}],"notes":"錆びる means \"to rust, to get rusty\" and is intransitive: 鉄が錆びる, 自転車が錆びてしまった. The attributive form 錆びた (\"rusty\") is very common: 錆びた釘, 錆びたナイフ. The noun is 錆（さび）, for example 錆が出る／つく (\"rust forms\"). Both the verb and the noun are often written in kana (さびる, サビ); letting an object rust is expressed causatively with 錆びさせる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"りんごの皮を剥きます。","romaji":"Ringo no kawa o mukimasu.","german":"I peel the apple.","furigana":"かわ|む"},{"japanese":"みかんを剥いて子どもにあげました。","romaji":"Mikan o muite kodomo ni agemashita.","german":"I peeled a mandarin and gave it to the child.","furigana":"む|こ"},{"japanese":"じゃがいもを剥くのを手伝ってくれる？","romaji":"Jagaimo o muku no o tetsudatte kureru?","german":"Will you help me peel the potatoes?","furigana":"む|てつだ"}],"notes":"剥く means \"to peel, to remove the skin or shell\". The object is either the fruit itself (みかんを剥く) or the peel (りんごの皮を剥く, ゆで卵の殻を剥く); it is mostly written in kana as むく. To be distinguished from 剥がす (\"to peel off, to detach\", e.g. a sticker) and the intransitive 剥ける (\"to peel, to come off\": 日焼けで皮が剥ける). Figuratively, 目を剥く means \"to open one's eyes wide\"; not to be confused with 向く (\"to turn toward\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"蛇口を捻ります。","romaji":"Jaguchi o hinerimasu.","german":"I turn the faucet.","furigana":"じゃぐち|ひね"},{"japanese":"階段で足首を捻ってしまいました。","romaji":"Kaidan de ashikubi o hinette shimaimashita.","german":"I twisted my ankle on the stairs.","furigana":"かいだん|あしくび|ひね"},{"japanese":"みんなで頭を捻ったが、いいアイデアが出なかった。","romaji":"Minna de atama o hinetta ga, ii aidea ga denakatta.","german":"We all racked our brains, but no good idea came up.","furigana":"あたま|ひね|で"}],"notes":"捻る means \"to turn, to twist (with the fingers)\": 蛇口を捻る (\"to turn on the faucet\"), 体を捻る (\"to twist the upper body\"). For injuries, 足首を捻る means \"to twist/sprain one's ankle\" (medically 捻挫する). Fixed expressions are 頭を捻る (\"to rack one's brains\") and ひねった問題 (\"a tricky, clever problem\"). It is mostly written in kana; similar is ねじる (\"to wring, to twist\"), whereas 回す rather means \"to turn around in a circle\" (key, knob).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"種を撒きます。","romaji":"Tane o makimasu.","german":"I scatter seeds.","furigana":"たね|ま"},{"japanese":"暑いので、庭に水を撒きました。","romaji":"Atsui node, niwa ni mizu o makimashita.","german":"It was hot, so I sprinkled water on the garden.","furigana":"あつ|にわ|みず|ま"},{"japanese":"節分の日には「鬼は外」と言いながら豆を撒きます。","romaji":"Setsubun no hi ni wa \"Oni wa soto\" to iinagara mame o makimasu.","german":"On Setsubun, people scatter beans while calling out \"Demons out!\"","furigana":"せつぶん|ひ|おに|そと|い|まめ|ま"}],"notes":"撒く means \"to scatter, to strew, to spray\": 水を撒く (\"to sprinkle water\"), 餌を撒く, 節分に豆を撒く. \"To sow\" in the narrower sense is mostly written 蒔く (種を蒔く), in kana simply 種をまく. Other meanings are ビラを撒く (\"to distribute flyers\") and 尾行を撒く (\"to shake off a pursuer\"). Mostly written in kana as まく; not to be confused with 巻く (\"to wind\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"芝生を刈ります。","romaji":"Shibafu o karimasu.","german":"I mow the lawn.","furigana":"しばふ|か"},{"japanese":"秋になると、農家の人が稲を刈ります。","romaji":"Aki ni naru to, nouka no hito ga ine o karimasu.","german":"When autumn comes, the farmers harvest the rice.","furigana":"あき|のうか|ひと|いね|か"},{"japanese":"夏休みに父に髪を短く刈ってもらった。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi ni chichi ni kami o mijikaku katte moratta.","german":"During the summer vacation, my father cut my hair short.","furigana":"なつやす|ちち|かみ|みじか|か"}],"notes":"刈る means \"to cut, to mow, to shear (grass, hair, grain)\": 芝生を刈る, 草を刈る, 稲を刈る (\"to harvest rice\"), 髪を短く刈る. For ordinary cutting with a knife or scissors, people say 切る; at the hairdresser it is 髪を切ってもらう, whereas 刈る is more \"to shear short\" (刈り上げ). Derived nouns are 草刈り and 稲刈り; not to be confused with the identically read 狩る (\"to hunt\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"畑を耕します。","romaji":"Hatake o tagayashimasu.","german":"I plow the field.","furigana":"はたけ|たがや"},{"japanese":"種をまく前に、土をよく耕しておきます。","romaji":"Tane o maku mae ni, tsuchi o yoku tagayashite okimasu.","german":"Before sowing, I till the soil thoroughly.","furigana":"たね|まえ|つち|たがや"},{"japanese":"昔は牛を使って田んぼを耕していました。","romaji":"Mukashi wa ushi o tsukatte tanbo o tagayashite imashita.","german":"In the old days, rice paddies were plowed with oxen.","furigana":"むかし|うし|つか|た|たがや"}],"notes":"耕す means \"to work the soil, to plow, to dig over\" - whether with a plow, tractor or spade: 畑を耕す, 田んぼを耕す, 土を耕す. Related words are 耕作 (\"farming\") and 耕運機 (\"power tiller\"). The verb belongs to the vocabulary of agriculture and gardening; harvesting, by contrast, is 収穫する or, for grain, 刈る.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"桜の蕾がふくらんでいる。","romaji":"Sakura no tsubomi ga fukurande iru.","german":"The cherry blossom buds are swelling.","furigana":"さくら|つぼみ"},{"japanese":"バラにたくさん蕾がついています。","romaji":"Bara ni takusan tsubomi ga tsuite imasu.","german":"The rose has many buds on it.","furigana":"つぼみ"},{"japanese":"朝はまだ蕾だったのに、夕方には花が開いていた。","romaji":"Asa wa mada tsubomi datta noni, yuugata ni wa hana ga hiraite ita.","german":"It was still a bud in the morning, but by evening the flower had opened.","furigana":"あさ|つぼみ|ゆうがた|はな|ひら"}],"notes":"蕾 is the flower bud; a leaf or shoot bud, by contrast, is called 芽. Typical combinations: 蕾がつく (\"to form buds\"), 蕾がふくらむ (\"the buds swell\"), 蕾が開く (\"to bloom\"), まだ蕾だ (\"has not bloomed yet\"). つぼみ is mostly written in kana. Figuratively it can refer to a promising young person, but that is rather literary.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"蔓が壁に這っている。","romaji":"Tsuru ga kabe ni hatte iru.","german":"The vine is creeping along the wall.","furigana":"つる|かべ|は"},{"japanese":"朝顔の蔓が窓のほうまで伸びてきた。","romaji":"Asagao no tsuru ga mado no hou made nobite kita.","german":"The morning glory vines have grown all the way to the window.","furigana":"あさがお|つる|まど|の"},{"japanese":"眼鏡の蔓が折れてしまった。","romaji":"Megane no tsuru ga orete shimatta.","german":"The temple of my glasses broke.","furigana":"めがね|つる|お"}],"notes":"蔓 is the vine or climbing shoot of climbing and creeping plants such as morning glory, cucumber or grapevine. Typical uses are 蔓が伸びる, 蔓が絡まる (\"to get tangled\") and 蔓が壁を這う. It is mostly written つる in kana; the ivy plant itself is called 蔦（つた）. つる also refers to the temple (arm) of a pair of glasses (眼鏡のつる); do not confuse it with the identically read 鶴 (\"crane\").","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"冬の日本海側では、霰が降ることがよくあります。","romaji":"Fuyu no Nihonkai-gawa de wa, arare ga furu koto ga yoku arimasu.","german":"On the Sea of Japan side in winter, graupel often falls.","furigana":"ふゆ|にほんかいがわ|あられ|ふ"},{"japanese":"霰が窓に当たって、パラパラと音を立てている。","romaji":"Arare ga mado ni atatte, parapara to oto o tatete iru.","german":"Graupel hits the window and makes a pattering sound.","furigana":"あられ|まど|あ|おと|た"},{"japanese":"お茶と一緒に、しょうゆ味のあられを出した。","romaji":"Ocha to issho ni, shouyu-aji no arare o dashita.","german":"I served soy sauce flavored arare with tea.","furigana":"ちゃ|いっしょ|あじ|だ"}],"notes":"あられ is \"graupel\": small, whitish ice pellets under about 5 mm in diameter. Larger hailstones are called 雹（ひょう）, sleet みぞれ; one says あられが降る. あられ is mostly written in kana; the kanji 霰 is rare. The same word denotes small toasted rice crackers made from mochi (e.g. ひなあられ for the Doll Festival) - in this meaning always in kana; in cooking, a fine dice cut is called あられ切り.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"雷鳴が響いた。","romaji":"Raimei ga hibiita.","german":"Thunder rang out.","furigana":"らいめい|ひび"},{"japanese":"遠くで雷鳴が聞こえたので、急いで山を下りました。","romaji":"Tooku de raimei ga kikoeta node, isoide yama o orimashita.","german":"We heard thunder in the distance, so we hurried down the mountain.","furigana":"とお|らいめい|き|いそ|やま|お"},{"japanese":"天気予報によると、今夜は雷鳴を伴う激しい雨になるそうです。","romaji":"Tenki yohou ni yoru to, kon'ya wa raimei o tomonau hageshii ame ni naru sou desu.","german":"According to the weather forecast, tonight there will be heavy rain with thunder.","furigana":"てんきよほう|こんや|らいめい|ともな|はげ|あめ"}],"notes":"雷鳴 is the rumble of thunder, the thunderclap as a sound. It sounds literary and appears in news, weather reports and narratives (雷鳴が響く／轟く, 雷鳴を伴う雨). In everyday speech, 雷が鳴る or the onomatopoeia ゴロゴロ is used instead. Lightning is called 稲妻 or 稲光, and a thunderstorm in general 雷.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"潮が満ちてきた。","romaji":"Shio ga michite kita.","german":"The tide has come in.","furigana":"しお|み"},{"japanese":"潮が引いたら、貝を探しに行こう。","romaji":"Shio ga hiitara, kai o sagashi ni ikou.","german":"When the tide goes out, let's go look for shellfish.","furigana":"しお|ひ|かい|さが|い"},{"japanese":"この辺りは潮の流れが速いので、泳がないでください。","romaji":"Kono atari wa shio no nagare ga hayai node, oyoganaide kudasai.","german":"The currents around here are fast, so please do not swim.","furigana":"あた|しお|なが|はや|およ"}],"notes":"潮 means \"tide, ocean current\" and also \"seawater\". Typical: 潮が満ちる (\"the tide comes in\"), 潮が引く (\"the water recedes, low tide\"), 満ち潮／引き潮, 潮の流れ (\"current\"). Well-known compounds are 潮風 (\"sea breeze\") and 潮干狩り (\"gathering shellfish at low tide\"). Not to be confused with the identically read 塩 (\"salt\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"波打ち際を歩きます。","romaji":"Namiuchigiwa o arukimasu.","german":"I walk along the water's edge.","furigana":"なみう|ぎわ|ある"},{"japanese":"子どもたちが波打ち際で水をかけ合って遊んでいる。","romaji":"Kodomotachi ga namiuchigiwa de mizu o kakeatte asonde iru.","german":"The children are playing at the water's edge, splashing each other.","furigana":"こ|なみう|ぎわ|みず|あ|あそ"},{"japanese":"嵐の次の日、波打ち際にたくさんの貝殻が落ちていた。","romaji":"Arashi no tsugi no hi, namiuchigiwa ni takusan no kaigara ga ochite ita.","german":"The day after the storm, there were many shells at the water's edge.","furigana":"あらし|つぎ|ひ|なみう|ぎわ|かいがら|お"}],"notes":"波打ち際 is the line on the beach where the waves run out - the \"water's edge, strandline\"; literally \"the edge (際) where waves beat (波打つ)\". Typical uses: 波打ち際を歩く, 波打ち際で遊ぶ. The beach in general is called 浜辺 or 砂浜, the coast 海岸; 波打ち際 refers precisely to the narrow wet strip.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"磯で魚を釣ります。","romaji":"Iso de sakana o tsurimasu.","german":"I fish on the rocky shore.","furigana":"いそ|さかな|つ"},{"japanese":"夏休みに家族で磯遊びをして、小さなカニを見つけた。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi ni kazoku de isoasobi o shite, chiisana kani o mitsuketa.","german":"In the summer vacation, our family played in the tide pools and found small crabs.","furigana":"なつやす|かぞく|いそあそ|ちい|み"},{"japanese":"窓を開けると、磯の香りがしました。","romaji":"Mado o akeru to, iso no kaori ga shimashita.","german":"When I opened the window, it smelled of the sea.","furigana":"まど|あ|いそ|かお"}],"notes":"磯 is a rocky coast or a rocky beach with stones and tide pools - in contrast to 砂浜 (\"sandy beach\"). Typical are 磯で釣る (\"to fish on the rocky shore\"), 磯遊び (\"to look for crabs and shellfish in tide pools\") and 磯の香り, the smell of the sea and seaweed. In the kitchen, it appears in names such as 磯辺焼き (mochi wrapped in nori).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"団扇であおぎます。","romaji":"Uchiwa de aogimasu.","german":"I fan myself with an uchiwa.","furigana":"うちわ"},{"japanese":"夏祭りに浴衣を着て、うちわを持って出かけた。","romaji":"Natsumatsuri ni yukata o kite, uchiwa o motte dekaketa.","german":"I went out to the summer festival wearing a yukata and carrying an uchiwa.","furigana":"なつまつ|ゆかた|き|も|で"},{"japanese":"駅前で店の宣伝のうちわを配っていました。","romaji":"Ekimae de mise no senden no uchiwa o kubatte imashita.","german":"They were handing out uchiwa advertising a shop in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|みせ|せんでん|くば"}],"notes":"団扇 is a flat, non-folding round fan, usually made of paper or plastic on a bamboo frame; the folding fan is called 扇子（せんす）. One says うちわであおぐ (\"to fan oneself\"). It is almost always written うちわ in kana. In summer, promotional fans are handed out, and at concerts fans hold up homemade うちわ; not to be confused with 内輪 (\"inner circle, internal\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"簾を下ろして日差しを遮る。","romaji":"Sudare o oroshite hizashi o saegiru.","german":"I lower the bamboo blind to block the sunlight.","furigana":"すだれ|お|ひざ|さえぎ"},{"japanese":"夏になると、祖母の家ではベランダにすだれを掛けます。","romaji":"Natsu ni naru to, sobo no ie de wa beranda ni sudare o kakemasu.","german":"When summer comes, they hang a bamboo blind on the veranda at my grandmother's house.","furigana":"なつ|そぼ|いえ|か"},{"japanese":"すだれ越しに涼しい風が入ってくる。","romaji":"Sudaregoshi ni suzushii kaze ga haitte kuru.","german":"A cool breeze comes in through the bamboo blind.","furigana":"ご|すず|かぜ|はい"}],"notes":"簾 is a blind made of thin bamboo or reed slats that is hung outside windows or the veranda in summer: it blocks the sun but lets air through. Typical uses are すだれを下ろす／上げる and すだれを掛ける; it is usually written すだれ in kana. A larger reed screen that is leaned against a wall is called よしず; the bamboo mat for rolling sushi is called 巻きす.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"蚊帳を吊ります。","romaji":"Kaya o tsurimasu.","german":"I hang up the mosquito net.","furigana":"かや|つ"},{"japanese":"子どものころ、夏は蚊帳の中で寝ていました。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, natsu wa kaya no naka de nete imashita.","german":"As a child, I slept under a mosquito net in summer.","furigana":"こ|なつ|かや|なか|ね"},{"japanese":"大事な話なのに、私だけ蚊帳の外だった。","romaji":"Daiji na hanashi na noni, watashi dake kaya no soto datta.","german":"Even though it was an important matter, only I was left out.","furigana":"だいじ|はなし|わたし|かや|そと"}],"notes":"蚊帳 is a large mosquito net that is traditionally hung from the corners of a room in summer so that you sleep under it on the futon. You say 蚊帳を吊る (\"to hang up\") and 蚊帳の中で寝る. The reading かや is a special reading. The idiom 蚊帳の外 (\"left out, not in the loop\") is very common: 私だけ蚊帳の外だった.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この番組は視聴率が高い。","romaji":"Kono bangumi wa shichōritsu ga takai.","german":"This program has high ratings.","furigana":"ばんぐみ|しちょうりつ|たか"},{"japanese":"最終回の視聴率は30パーセントを超えました。","romaji":"Saishuukai no shichouritsu wa sanjuu paasento o koemashita.","german":"The ratings of the final episode exceeded 30 percent.","furigana":"さいしゅうかい|しちょうりつ|こ"},{"japanese":"視聴率が取れない番組は、すぐに終わってしまう。","romaji":"Shichouritsu ga torenai bangumi wa, sugu ni owatte shimau.","german":"Programs that can't get ratings end quickly.","furigana":"しちょうりつ|と|ばんぐみ|お"}],"notes":"視聴率 is the viewership rating of TV programs, given in percent. Typical: 視聴率が高い／低い, 視聴率を取る (\"to get good ratings\"), 視聴率が20パーセントを超える. 視聴者 are the viewers; for internet videos, people speak of 再生回数 (\"view count\") instead. The word belongs to the language of media and news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"生放送で中継します。","romaji":"Nama hōsō de chūkei shimasu.","german":"We'll cover it live.","furigana":"なまほうそう|ちゅうけい"},{"japanese":"この番組は毎週日曜日の朝に生放送されています。","romaji":"Kono bangumi wa maishuu nichiyoubi no asa ni nama housou sarete imasu.","german":"This program is broadcast live every Sunday morning.","furigana":"ばんぐみ|まいしゅうにちようび|あさ|なまほうそう"},{"japanese":"生放送中に、ゲストが言葉を間違えてしまった。","romaji":"Nama housou-chuu ni, gesuto ga kotoba o machigaete shimatta.","german":"During the live broadcast, a guest made a slip of the tongue.","furigana":"なまほうそうちゅう|ことば|まちが"}],"notes":"生放送 is a live broadcast; 生 here means \"raw, unedited, live\", as in 生ビール. The opposite terms are 録画放送 and 収録 (\"recording\"). Typical: 生放送で, 生放送中, and as a する-verb 番組を生放送する. A live transmission from a location is called 生中継, a livestream on the internet 生配信.","levelNote":"Compound: 生 (N3) + 放送 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"番組を録画します。","romaji":"Bangumi o rokuga shimasu.","german":"I'm recording the program.","furigana":"ばんぐみ|ろくが"},{"japanese":"昨日のサッカーの試合、録画しておいたから一緒に見よう。","romaji":"Kinou no sakkaa no shiai, rokuga shite oita kara issho ni miyou.","german":"I recorded yesterday's soccer match, so let's watch it together.","furigana":"きのう|しあい|ろくが|いっしょ|み"},{"japanese":"防犯カメラの録画に犯人の顔が映っていた。","romaji":"Bouhan kamera no rokuga ni hannin no kao ga utsutte ita.","german":"The face of the culprit was captured on the security camera recording.","furigana":"ぼうはん|ろくが|はんにん|かお|うつ"}],"notes":"録画 is video recording, especially of TV programs or by cameras; 録画する = \"to record\". Pure sound recording is 録音. Typical: 番組を録画する, 録画予約 (\"to schedule a recording\"), 録画したドラマ. When you film yourself with a camera or phone, people tend to say 動画を撮る or 撮影する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"字幕付きの映画を見ます。","romaji":"Jimaku tsuki no eiga o mimasu.","german":"I'm watching a movie with subtitles.","furigana":"じまくつ|えいが|み"},{"japanese":"字幕なしで英語の映画が分かるようになりたい。","romaji":"Jimaku nashi de eigo no eiga ga wakaru you ni naritai.","german":"I want to be able to understand English movies without subtitles.","furigana":"じまく|えいご|えいが|わ"},{"japanese":"電車の中なので、音を消して字幕を出して見ています。","romaji":"Densha no naka na node, oto o keshite jimaku o dashite mite imasu.","german":"I'm on the train, so I'm watching with the sound off and subtitles on.","furigana":"でんしゃ|なか|おと|け|じまく|だ|み"}],"notes":"字幕 are subtitles in film and television. At the cinema you choose between the 字幕版 (\"subtitled version\") and the 吹き替え版 (\"dubbed version\"). Typical: 字幕付き, 日本語字幕, 字幕を出す／消す (\"to show/hide\"), 字幕なしで分かる. The word is neutral and everyday.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"吹き替え版を見ます。","romaji":"Fukikae-ban o mimasu.","german":"I'm watching the dubbed version.","furigana":"ふ|か|ばん|み"},{"japanese":"子どもと見るときは、字幕より吹き替えのほうが楽です。","romaji":"Kodomo to miru toki wa, jimaku yori fukikae no hou ga raku desu.","german":"When I watch with children, dubbing is easier than subtitles.","furigana":"こ|み|じまく|ふ|か|らく"},{"japanese":"あの俳優の吹き替えは、いつも同じ声優がしています。","romaji":"Ano haiyuu no fukikae wa, itsumo onaji seiyuu ga shite imasu.","german":"That actor is always dubbed by the same voice actor.","furigana":"はいゆう|ふ|か|おな|せいゆう"}],"notes":"吹き替え is the dubbing of a film or series into another language; the 吹き替え版 is the opposite of the 字幕版. The related verb is 吹き替える, and the dubbing actors are usually 声優. The spelling 吹替 also occurs. Less often, the word also refers to an actor's stunt double in dangerous scenes.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"脚本を書いています。","romaji":"Kyakuhon o kaite imasu.","german":"I am writing a screenplay.","furigana":"きゃくほん|か"},{"japanese":"このドラマは脚本がよくできている。","romaji":"Kono dorama wa kyakuhon ga yoku dekite iru.","german":"This drama has a well-crafted script.","furigana":"きゃくほん"},{"japanese":"有名な小説をもとに、新しい映画の脚本が作られた。","romaji":"Yuumei na shousetsu o moto ni, atarashii eiga no kyakuhon ga tsukurareta.","german":"A screenplay for a new movie was written based on a famous novel.","furigana":"ゆうめい|しょうせつ|あたら|えいが|きゃくほん|つく"}],"notes":"脚本 is the screenplay or stage text as a literary work; the author is called 脚本家. Typical: 脚本を書く, 脚本がいい／よくできている, in the credits 脚本：〇〇. 台本, by contrast, rather means the working copy from which actors and presenters learn their lines; シナリオ is a loanword with a similar meaning.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"台本を覚えます。","romaji":"Daihon o oboemasu.","german":"I will memorize the script.","furigana":"だいほん|おぼ"},{"japanese":"司会者は台本にないことを言って、会場を笑わせた。","romaji":"Shikaisha wa daihon ni nai koto o itte, kaijou o warawaseta.","german":"The host said something that was not in the script and made the venue laugh.","furigana":"しかいしゃ|だいほん|い|かいじょう|わら"},{"japanese":"リアリティ番組といっても、実は台本があるらしい。","romaji":"Riariti bangumi to itte mo, jitsu wa daihon ga aru rashii.","german":"Even though it is called reality TV, apparently there is actually a script.","furigana":"ばんぐみ|じつ|だいほん"}],"notes":"台本 is the text book or script that actors, presenters or announcers use at work - with dialogue and stage directions. Typical: 台本を覚える／読む, 台本通りに (\"as in the script\"), 台本にない (\"not in the script, improvised\"). In contrast to 脚本, which emphasizes the author's work, 台本 is the practical working material - also for shows and presentations.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"衣装を着替えます。","romaji":"Ishō o kigaemasu.","german":"I will change my costume.","furigana":"いしょう|きが"},{"japanese":"文化祭の劇のために、みんなで衣装を手作りした。","romaji":"Bunkasai no geki no tame ni, minna de ishou o tezukuri shita.","german":"Everyone made the costumes by hand for the play at the school festival.","furigana":"ぶんかさい|げき|いしょう|てづく"},{"japanese":"姉の花嫁衣装はとてもきれいだった。","romaji":"Ane no hanayome ishou wa totemo kirei datta.","german":"My older sister's wedding dress was very beautiful.","furigana":"あね|はなよめいしょう"}],"notes":"衣装 is special, often striking clothing for performances or occasions - \"costume, garb\": 舞台衣装, 花嫁衣装 (\"bridal gown\"), ハロウィンの衣装. Everyday clothing, by contrast, is called 服 or 洋服, and clothing in general (formal) 衣服. Typical are 衣装を着る, 衣装を着替える (\"to change costumes\") and 衣装を用意する; the spelling 衣裳 also occurs.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大道具を準備します。","romaji":"Oodougu o junbi shimasu.","german":"I'm preparing the stage sets.","furigana":"おおどうぐ|じゅんび"},{"japanese":"学園祭の劇で、私は大道具係を担当しました。","romaji":"Gakuensai no geki de, watashi wa oodougu-gakari o tantou shimashita.","german":"For the school festival play, I was in charge of the stage sets.","furigana":"がくえんさい|げき|わたし|おおどうぐがかり|たんとう"},{"japanese":"公演が終わると、すぐに大道具を片付けなければならない。","romaji":"Kouen ga owaru to, sugu ni oodougu o katazukenakereba naranai.","german":"As soon as the performance is over, the stage sets have to be cleared away right away.","furigana":"こうえん|お|おおどうぐ|かたづ"}],"notes":"大道具 are the large stage structures and scenery in the theater or TV studio - walls, houses, trees, stairs. The counterpart 小道具 are the small props that performers take in their hands. Typical: 大道具を組み立てる／運ぶ／片付ける, 大道具係 (\"stage-set crew\"). In short form, 大道具 can also refer to the stagehands themselves.","levelNote":"Compound: 大 (N3) + 道具 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"小道具を揃えます。","romaji":"Kodougu o soroemasu.","german":"I'm gathering the props.","furigana":"こどうぐ|そろ"},{"japanese":"この劇では、古い手紙が大切な小道具になっている。","romaji":"Kono geki de wa, furui tegami ga taisetsu na kodougu ni natte iru.","german":"In this play, an old letter is an important prop.","furigana":"げき|ふる|てがみ|たいせつ|こどうぐ"},{"japanese":"キャンプで使える便利な小道具を百円ショップで買った。","romaji":"Kyanpu de tsukaeru benri na kodougu o hyakuen shoppu de katta.","german":"I bought handy little camping gadgets at a 100-yen shop.","furigana":"つか|べんり|こどうぐ|ひゃくえん|か"}],"notes":"小道具 are the small props that performers use in theater, film or television - letters, cups, swords; in contrast to 大道具, the large sets. Typical: 小道具を揃える／用意する, 小道具係. In daily life, the word also refers to small practical aids or gadgets: 便利な小道具.","levelNote":"Compound: 小 (N3) + 道具 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"舞台裏を見学しました。","romaji":"Butai ura o kengaku shimashita.","german":"I toured backstage.","furigana":"ぶたいうら|けんがく"},{"japanese":"舞台裏では、スタッフが忙しく走り回っていた。","romaji":"Butai ura de wa, sutaffu ga isogashiku hashirimawatte ita.","german":"Backstage, the staff were running around busily.","furigana":"ぶたいうら|いそが|はし|まわ"},{"japanese":"この本は、オリンピックの舞台裏を詳しく書いている。","romaji":"Kono hon wa, Orinpikku no butai ura o kuwashiku kaite iru.","german":"This book describes in detail what went on behind the scenes at the Olympics.","furigana":"ほん|ぶたいうら|くわ|か"}],"notes":"舞台裏 is literally the area behind the stage (\"backstage\"). Equally common is the figurative meaning \"behind the scenes\", i.e. what stays hidden from the public: 政治の舞台裏, 舞台裏を明かす. The performers' dressing room, on the other hand, is called 楽屋; the loanword バックステージ is also in use.","levelNote":"Compound: 舞台 (N3) + 裏 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"楽屋で準備します。","romaji":"Gakuya de junbi shimasu.","german":"I'm getting ready in the dressing room.","furigana":"がくや|じゅんび"},{"japanese":"コンサートの後、楽屋を訪ねて花束を渡しました。","romaji":"Konsaato no ato, gakuya o tazunete hanataba o watashimashita.","german":"After the concert, I visited the dressing room and handed over a bouquet.","furigana":"あと|がくや|たず|はなたば|わた"},{"japanese":"それは楽屋ネタだから、お客さんには分からないよ。","romaji":"Sore wa gakuya neta dakara, okyakusan ni wa wakaranai yo.","german":"That's an inside joke, so the audience won't get it.","furigana":"がくや|きゃく|わ"}],"notes":"楽屋 is the lounge and changing room of performers behind the stage - the \"artists' dressing room\" (not the coat check for the audience, which is called クローク). Typical: 楽屋に入る, 楽屋を訪ねる, 楽屋見舞い (a gift for performers). 楽屋ネタ or 楽屋落ち is an inside joke that only insiders understand.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"拍手が鳴り響いた。","romaji":"Hakushu ga narihibiita.","german":"Applause rang out.","furigana":"はくしゅ|な|ひび"},{"japanese":"発表が終わると、みんなが拍手してくれた。","romaji":"Happyou ga owaru to, minna ga hakushu shite kureta.","german":"When the presentation was over, everyone applauded for me.","furigana":"はっぴょう|お|はくしゅ"},{"japanese":"優勝したチームに大きな拍手を送りましょう。","romaji":"Yuushou shita chiimu ni ookina hakushu o okurimashou.","german":"Let's give the winning team a big round of applause.","furigana":"ゆうしょう|おお|はくしゅ|おく"}],"notes":"拍手 is clapping, applause; as an intransitive する-verb: 拍手する. The one who is applauded takes に: 選手に拍手する, 〜に拍手を送る (\"to give applause to someone\"). Other expressions are 大きな拍手が起こる and 拍手喝采 (\"thunderous applause\"). The ritual clapping of hands at a shrine is usually called 柏手（かしわで）.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"第一幕が始まります。","romaji":"Dai ichi maku ga hajimarimasu.","german":"The first act is about to begin.","furigana":"だいいちまく|はじ"},{"japanese":"幕が上がると、会場が静かになりました。","romaji":"Maku ga agaru to, kaijou ga shizuka ni narimashita.","german":"When the curtain rose, the hall fell silent.","furigana":"まく|あ|かいじょう|しず"},{"japanese":"オリンピックが十七日間の幕を閉じました。","romaji":"Orinpikku ga juunananichikan no maku o tojimashita.","german":"The Olympics came to a close after seventeen days.","furigana":"じゅうななにちかん|まく|と"}],"notes":"幕 is first of all the \"curtain\" in the theater: 幕が上がる / 幕が開く (\"the curtain rises\"), 幕が下りる (\"falls\"). As a counter, it stands for acts: 第一幕, 三幕. Figuratively, 幕を開ける means \"to begin\" and 幕を閉じる \"to come to an end\", hence also 開幕 and 閉幕. In 君の出る幕じゃない, 幕 means \"role, place\": \"This is none of your business\"; it is also familiar from 字幕 (\"subtitles\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"興行が成功しました。","romaji":"Kōgyō ga seikō shimashita.","german":"The show was a success.","furigana":"こうぎょう|せいこう"},{"japanese":"この映画は興行収入が百億円を超えた。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa kougyou shuunyuu ga hyakuoku en o koeta.","german":"This movie grossed over 10 billion yen.","furigana":"えいが|こうぎょうしゅうにゅう|ひゃくおくえん|こ"},{"japanese":"プロレスの興行が、来月この町で行われる。","romaji":"Puroresu no kougyou ga, raigetsu kono machi de okonawareru.","german":"A pro wrestling event will be held in this town next month.","furigana":"こうぎょう|らいげつ|まち|おこな"}],"notes":"興行 is a commercial event for which admission is charged - theater, film, circus, sumo, boxing or wrestling - and also the event business itself. Typical are 興行収入 (\"box-office revenue\"), 興行成績 and 興行が成功する. The word sounds businesslike and appears mainly in the news; not to be confused with the identically read 工業 (\"industry\") and 鉱業 (\"mining\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"千秋楽を迎えました。","romaji":"Senshūraku o mukaemashita.","german":"The final day of the run has arrived.","furigana":"せんしゅうらく|むか"},{"japanese":"大相撲の優勝は千秋楽で決まった。","romaji":"Oozumou no yuushou wa senshuuraku de kimatta.","german":"The sumo tournament championship was decided only on the final day.","furigana":"|ゆうしょう|せんしゅうらく|き"},{"japanese":"千秋楽のチケットは、すぐに売り切れてしまった。","romaji":"Senshuuraku no chiketto wa, sugu ni urikirete shimatta.","german":"The tickets for the final performance sold out immediately.","furigana":"せんしゅうらく|う|き"}],"notes":"千秋楽 is the last day of a run of performances in the theater or of a sumo tournament (場所); the counterpart is 初日 (\"premiere, first day\"). Typical: 千秋楽を迎える (\"to reach the final day\"), 千秋楽まで, 千秋楽のチケット. Among theater people it is shortened to 楽日 or 楽. The word comes from the world of the stage and sumo and is also often used in sports news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"幼なじみに会いました。","romaji":"Osananajimi ni aimashita.","german":"I met my childhood friend.","furigana":"おさな|あ"},{"japanese":"彼とは幼稚園のころからの幼なじみです。","romaji":"Kare to wa youchien no koro kara no osananajimi desu.","german":"He and I have been friends since kindergarten.","furigana":"かれ|ようちえん|おさな"},{"japanese":"幼なじみと結婚するなんて、まるでドラマみたいだね。","romaji":"Osananajimi to kekkon suru nante, marude dorama mitai da ne.","german":"Marrying your childhood friend - it's just like a TV drama.","furigana":"おさな|けっこん"}],"notes":"幼なじみ (also written 幼馴染) is someone you have known since early childhood - often the neighborhood child you grew up with; the word is used for both boys and girls. Typical: 幼なじみの〇〇, 〜とは幼なじみだ (\"I grew up with ...\"). An old friend from a later period is more likely 昔の友達 or 旧友; 幼なじみ stresses the shared childhood.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仲人を頼みました。","romaji":"Nakōdo o tanomimashita.","german":"I asked someone to be our matchmaker.","furigana":"なこうど|たの"},{"japanese":"両親の結婚式では、父の上司が仲人を務めたそうだ。","romaji":"Ryoushin no kekkonshiki de wa, chichi no joushi ga nakoudo o tsutometa sou da.","german":"At my parents' wedding, my father's boss reportedly served as the matchmaker.","furigana":"りょうしん|けっこんしき|ちち|じょうし|なこうど|つと"},{"japanese":"最近は仲人を立てない結婚式がほとんどです。","romaji":"Saikin wa nakoudo o tatenai kekkonshiki ga hotondo desu.","german":"Recently, almost all weddings are held without a matchmaker.","furigana":"さいきん|なこうど|た|けっこんしき"}],"notes":"仲人 is the traditional marriage go-between - usually a respected married couple who arrange a 見合い and officially act as intermediaries at the wedding. Typical: 仲人を頼む, 仲人をする／務める, 仲人を立てる. The reading なこうど is irregular. Today, weddings with a 仲人 are rare; professional matchmaking is called 結婚相談所.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"管理人に鍵を預けます。","romaji":"Kanrinin ni kagi o azukemasu.","german":"I leave the key with the building manager.","furigana":"かんりにん|かぎ|あず"},{"japanese":"水道の調子が悪いので、管理人さんに連絡しました。","romaji":"Suidou no choushi ga warui node, kanrinin-san ni renraku shimashita.","german":"Something is wrong with the water supply, so I contacted the caretaker.","furigana":"すいどう|ちょうし|わる|かんりにん|れんらく"},{"japanese":"このサイトの管理人は、毎日記事を更新している。","romaji":"Kono saito no kanrinin wa, mainichi kiji o koushin shite iru.","german":"The administrator of this site updates the articles every day.","furigana":"かんりにん|まいにちきじ|こうしん"}],"notes":"管理人 is the person who looks after a building or facility – in apartment complexes (マンションの管理人) the \"caretaker/building manager\", who sits in the 管理人室; in conversation often politely 管理人さん. The word also applies to dormitories, campsites or websites (サイトの管理人 \"operator, admin\"). The owner or landlord, by contrast, is 大家（おおや）.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 28"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"風通しの良い家です。","romaji":"Kaze tōshi no yoi ie desu.","german":"It is a well-ventilated house.","furigana":"かぜとお|よ|いえ"},{"japanese":"この部屋は風通しが悪くて、湿気がこもりやすい。","romaji":"Kono heya wa kaze tooshi ga warukute, shikke ga komoriyasui.","german":"This room is poorly ventilated, so moisture easily builds up.","furigana":"へや|かぜとお|わる|しっけ"},{"japanese":"新しい社長は、風通しのいい会社を作りたいと言っている。","romaji":"Atarashii shachou wa, kaze tooshi no ii kaisha o tsukuritai to itte iru.","german":"The new president says he wants to create a company with open communication.","furigana":"あたら|しゃちょう|かぜとお|かいしゃ|つく|い"}],"notes":"風通し is the air circulation, the draft in a room or building; it is almost always said as 風通しがいい／悪い or attributively 風通しのいい部屋. Actively airing out is 換気（する）, by contrast. Figuratively, 風通しのいい職場 describes a work environment with open communication. Besides かぜとおし, there is also the reading かざとおし.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"窓に結露がつく。","romaji":"Mado ni ketsuro ga tsuku.","german":"Condensation forms on the window.","furigana":"まど|けつろ"},{"japanese":"冬の朝は、窓が結露してびしょびしょになる。","romaji":"Fuyu no asa wa, mado ga ketsuro shite bishobisho ni naru.","german":"On winter mornings, the windows get condensation and are soaking wet.","furigana":"ふゆ|あさ|まど|けつろ"},{"japanese":"結露を放っておくと、カビの原因になります。","romaji":"Ketsuro o houtte oku to, kabi no gen'in ni narimasu.","german":"If you leave condensation alone, it causes mold.","furigana":"けつろ|ほう|げんいん"}],"notes":"結露 is condensation, when moisture settles on cold surfaces - a typical winter problem in Japanese homes. Typical uses: 窓に結露がつく, 結露が発生する, as an intransitive する-verb 窓が結露する, and 結露対策. Literally \"bound dew\" (露 = dew); moisture in general is 湿気. Untreated 結露 often leads to カビ.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"浴室に黴が生えた。","romaji":"Yokushitsu ni kabi ga haeta.","german":"Mold grew in the bathroom.","furigana":"よくしつ|かび|は"},{"japanese":"梅雨の時期は、かびが生えやすいので注意してください。","romaji":"Tsuyu no jiki wa, kabi ga haeyasui node chuui shite kudasai.","german":"Mold grows easily during the rainy season, so be careful.","furigana":"つゆ|じき|は|ちゅうい"},{"japanese":"押し入れを開けたら、かびのにおいがした。","romaji":"Oshiire o aketara, kabi no nioi ga shita.","german":"When I opened the closet, it smelled of mold.","furigana":"お|い|あ"}],"notes":"黴 means \"mold\"; the kanji is very rare, and it is almost always written かび or カビ. Typical uses: カビが生える (\"mold forms\"), カビ臭い (\"musty\"), カビ取り (\"mold remover\"). In Japan mold appears especially in the rainy season (梅雨) and as a result of 結露; bacteria, by contrast, are called 細菌 or 菌.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"水漏れを修理します。","romaji":"Mizumore o shūri shimasu.","german":"I'm repairing the leak.","furigana":"みずも|しゅうり"},{"japanese":"台所の蛇口から水漏れしています。","romaji":"Daidokoro no jaguchi kara mizumore shite imasu.","german":"Water is leaking from the kitchen faucet.","furigana":"だいどころ|じゃぐち|みずも"},{"japanese":"上の階の水漏れで、うちの天井にしみができた。","romaji":"Ue no kai no mizumore de, uchi no tenjou ni shimi ga dekita.","german":"Because of a leak in the apartment above, a stain formed on our ceiling.","furigana":"うえ|かい|みずも|てんじょう"}],"notes":"水漏れ is a water leak: water comes out where it shouldn't - from pipes, faucets or through the ceiling. Typical: 水漏れする, 水漏れが起きる, 天井から水漏れする, 水漏れを修理する. When rain comes in through the roof, people say 雨漏り. The underlying verb is 漏れる (\"to leak, to seep through\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"粗利を計算します。","romaji":"Arari o keisan shimasu.","german":"I'm calculating the gross profit.","furigana":"あらり|けいさん"},{"japanese":"この商品は安いけれど、粗利が少ない。","romaji":"Kono shouhin wa yasui keredo, arari ga sukunai.","german":"This product is cheap, but the gross profit is small.","furigana":"しょうひん|やす|あらり|すく"},{"japanese":"今月は売上は増えたが、粗利率は下がった。","romaji":"Kongetsu wa uriage wa fueta ga, arariritsu wa sagatta.","german":"Sales rose this month, but the gross margin fell.","furigana":"こんげつ|うりあげ|ふ|あらりりつ|さ"}],"notes":"粗利 is the gross profit: sales minus cost of goods. It is the business-language short form of 粗利益（あらりえき）; in accounting it is called 売上総利益. Typical: 粗利が出る／少ない, 粗利率 (\"gross margin\"), 粗利を計算する. To be distinguished from 売上 (\"sales, revenue\") and 純利益 (\"net profit\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"商品を陳列します。","romaji":"Shōhin o chinretsu shimasu.","german":"I will display the goods.","furigana":"しょうひん|ちんれつ"},{"japanese":"新商品は入口近くの棚に陳列してください。","romaji":"Shinshouhin wa iriguchi chikaku no tana ni chinretsu shite kudasai.","german":"Please display the new products on the shelf near the entrance.","furigana":"しんしょうひん|いりぐちちか|たな|ちんれつ"},{"japanese":"博物館には昔の道具がたくさん陳列されていた。","romaji":"Hakubutsukan ni wa mukashi no dougu ga takusan chinretsu sarete ita.","german":"Many old tools were on display at the museum.","furigana":"はくぶつかん|むかし|どうぐ|ちんれつ"}],"notes":"陳列 is the exhibiting and orderly laying out of goods or exhibits: 商品を陳列する, 陳列棚 (\"display shelf\"), 陳列ケース (\"display case\"). It sounds businesslike or literary; in everyday speech one rather says 並べる or 飾る. 展示 emphasizes showing something to be looked at (museum, trade fair), while 陳列 mainly means arranging goods for sale, but is also used for museum pieces.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"試着してもいいですか。","romaji":"Shichaku shite mo ii desu ka.","german":"May I try it on?","furigana":"しちゃく"},{"japanese":"試着室はどちらですか。","romaji":"Shichakushitsu wa dochira desu ka.","german":"Where is the fitting room?","furigana":"しちゃくしつ"},{"japanese":"試着してみたら、サイズが少し大きかったです。","romaji":"Shichaku shite mitara, saizu ga sukoshi ookikatta desu.","german":"When I tried it on, the size was a little big.","furigana":"しちゃく|すこ|おお"}],"notes":"試着 is trying on clothes in a store, mostly as a する-verb: 服を試着する, asked politely 試着してもいいですか. The fitting room is called 試着室, and staff respectfully say ご試着. For shoes, people tend to say 履いてみる, and in private settings for clothing also simply 着てみる. A sign saying 試着はご遠慮ください means that trying on is not allowed.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"裾上げをお願いします。","romaji":"Susoage o onegai shimasu.","german":"Please shorten the hem.","furigana":"すそあ|ねが"},{"japanese":"このズボン、裾上げに何日かかりますか。","romaji":"Kono zubon, susoage ni nannichi kakarimasu ka.","german":"How many days does it take to hem these trousers?","furigana":"すそあ|なんにち"},{"japanese":"母に頼んで、スカートを裾上げしてもらいました。","romaji":"Haha ni tanonde, sukaato o susoage shite moraimashita.","german":"I asked my mother to hem my skirt.","furigana":"はは|たの|すそあ"}],"notes":"裾上げ is shortening trousers or skirts by sewing up the hem (裾 \"hem, lower edge\" + 上げ \"to raise\"). In clothing stores it is a common service: 裾上げをお願いします, 裾上げは無料です. As a する-verb: ズボンを裾上げする. The opposite, letting the hem out to lengthen, is called 裾出し; shortening sleeves or length in general is expressed with 丈を詰める.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"テーラーでスーツの仕立てを頼みました。","romaji":"Teeraa de suutsu no shitate o tanomimashita.","german":"I ordered a made-to-measure suit at a tailor's.","furigana":"した|たの"},{"japanese":"このコートは仕立てがいいですね。","romaji":"Kono kooto wa shitate ga ii desu ne.","german":"This coat is really well made, isn't it?","furigana":"した"},{"japanese":"着物の仕立てには一か月ほどかかります。","romaji":"Kimono no shitate ni wa ikkagetsu hodo kakarimasu.","german":"Tailoring a kimono takes about a month.","furigana":"きもの|した|いっ|げつ"}],"notes":"仕立て is the noun for 仕立てる \"to tailor, to make (clothing)\" and refers to the tailoring as well as the workmanship of a garment: 仕立てがいい \"well cut and well made\", 着物の仕立て. For custom-made clothing as opposed to ready-made goods (既製服), people today often say オーダーメイド. In a broader sense, 仕立て means \"preparation, style\", e.g. 和風仕立て \"prepared in Japanese style\" or 特別仕立ての列車 \"special train\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"寸法を測ります。","romaji":"Sunpō o hakarimasu.","german":"I take measurements.","furigana":"すんぽう|はか"},{"japanese":"家具を買う前に、部屋の寸法を確認してください。","romaji":"Kagu o kau mae ni, heya no sunpou o kakunin shite kudasai.","german":"Before buying furniture, please check the dimensions of the room.","furigana":"かぐ|か|まえ|へや|すんぽう|かくにん"},{"japanese":"寸法が合わなくて、棚が入りませんでした。","romaji":"Sunpou ga awanakute, tana ga hairimasen deshita.","german":"The dimensions didn't match, so the shelf didn't fit.","furigana":"すんぽう|あ|たな|はい"}],"notes":"寸法 are the exact dimensions of an object (length, width, height); the word goes back to the old unit of length 寸. Typical uses: 寸法を測る \"to take measurements\", 寸法が合わない \"the dimensions don't match\", 部屋の寸法, 寸法図. For clothing, サイズ is usually used in everyday speech; 寸法 sounds more exact and craft-technical. Colloquially, 寸法 can also mean \"plan, intention\" (〜という寸法だ \"that's the plan\"), but that is rarer.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"帯を結びます。","romaji":"Obi o musubimasu.","german":"I tie the obi.","furigana":"おび|むす"},{"japanese":"浴衣の帯がほどけてしまいました。","romaji":"Yukata no obi ga hodokete shimaimashita.","german":"The obi of my yukata came undone.","furigana":"ゆかた|おび"},{"japanese":"弟は空手で黒帯を取りました。","romaji":"Otouto wa karate de kuroobi o torimashita.","german":"My younger brother got his black belt in karate.","furigana":"おとうと|からて|くろおび|と"}],"notes":"帯 is the wide sash with which a kimono or yukata is tied: 帯を締める \"to put on the obi\", 帯を結ぶ for tying the knot. In addition, 帯 generally refers to bands and strips, such as the wrap-around band on books and CDs (本の帯) or the belt in martial arts: 黒帯 \"black belt\". A Western trouser belt, on the other hand, is called ベルト. As a suffix, it is read たい: 時間帯 \"time period\", 地帯 \"zone\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"足袋を履きます。","romaji":"Tabi o hakimasu.","german":"I put on tabi.","furigana":"たび|は"},{"japanese":"着物には白い足袋を合わせます。","romaji":"Kimono ni wa shiroi tabi o awasemasu.","german":"With a kimono, you wear white tabi.","furigana":"きもの|しろ|たび|あ"},{"japanese":"祖父は庭仕事のとき、地下足袋を履いています。","romaji":"Sofu wa niwa shigoto no toki, jikatabi o haite imasu.","german":"My grandfather wears rubber-soled tabi work shoes when gardening.","furigana":"そふ|にわしごと|じかたび|は"}],"notes":"足袋 are traditional socks with a separate big toe, worn with kimono and 草履 or 下駄; with formal clothing they are white. As with socks and shoes, one says 履く, not 着る: 足袋を履く, 足袋を脱ぐ. Work shoes in tabi form with rubber soles are called 地下足袋. Modern toe socks, on the other hand, are called 五本指ソックス.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"羽織を着ます。","romaji":"Haori o kimasu.","german":"I put on a haori.","furigana":"はおり|き"},{"japanese":"成人式で、彼は羽織袴を着ていました。","romaji":"Seijinshiki de, kare wa haori hakama o kite imashita.","german":"At the Coming of Age Day ceremony, he wore haori and hakama.","furigana":"せいじんしき|かれ|はおりはかま|き"},{"japanese":"寒いので、着物の上に羽織を羽織りました。","romaji":"Samui node, kimono no ue ni haori o haorimashita.","german":"It was cold, so I threw a haori over my kimono.","furigana":"さむ|きもの|うえ|はおり|はお"}],"notes":"羽織 is a short, front-open jacket worn over the kimono and fastened with cords (羽織紐). Together with the divided skirt, it forms the formal men's dress 羽織袴. You say 羽織を着る or 羽織を羽織る; the related verb 羽織る \"to throw something on over\" is also used for Western clothing: カーディガンを羽織る.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"昔は長屋に住んでいました。","romaji":"Mukashi wa nagaya ni sunde imashita.","german":"I used to live in a nagaya.","furigana":"むかし|ながや|す"},{"japanese":"江戸時代、多くの人は狭い長屋で暮らしていました。","romaji":"Edo jidai, ooku no hito wa semai nagaya de kurashite imashita.","german":"In the Edo period, many people lived in cramped nagaya.","furigana":"えどじだい|おお|ひと|せま|ながや|く"},{"japanese":"長屋では、隣の人の話し声がよく聞こえました。","romaji":"Nagaya de wa, tonari no hito no hanashigoe ga yoku kikoemashita.","german":"In the nagaya, you could hear the neighbors' voices well.","furigana":"ながや|となり|ひと|はな|ごえ|き"}],"notes":"長屋 is an elongated, mostly single-story wooden house divided into several narrow dwellings side by side - the typical rental housing of ordinary people in the Edo period. The word appears mainly in history, 落語 and samurai dramas (長屋暮らし, 長屋の住人). Modern row houses are more often called テラスハウス or タウンハウス, and rental apartments in general アパート.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"横丁に居酒屋があります。","romaji":"Yokochō ni izakaya ga arimasu.","german":"There is a pub in the alley.","furigana":"よこちょう|いざかや"},{"japanese":"駅前の横丁には小さな店がたくさん並んでいます。","romaji":"Ekimae no yokochou ni wa chiisana mise ga takusan narande imasu.","german":"Many small shops line the alley in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|よこちょう|ちい|みせ|なら"},{"japanese":"次の横丁を右に曲がってください。","romaji":"Tsugi no yokochou o migi ni magatte kudasai.","german":"Please turn right at the next side street.","furigana":"つぎ|よこちょう|みぎ|ま"}],"notes":"横丁 is a narrow side street branching off a main street (横 \"side\" + 丁 \"block of street\"); the spelling 横町 is equally common. Today the word often appears in the names of small pub and snack alleys such as 新宿の思い出横丁 and sounds nostalgic and folksy. Compared with 路地, a very narrow lane between houses, 横丁 stresses the branching off from the main street and the shops in it.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"路地を歩きます。","romaji":"Roji o arukimasu.","german":"I walk through the alley.","furigana":"ろじ|ある"},{"japanese":"細い路地に入ると、静かなカフェがありました。","romaji":"Hosoi roji ni hairu to, shizuka na kafe ga arimashita.","german":"When I went into a narrow alley, there was a quiet cafe.","furigana":"ほそ|ろじ|はい|しず"},{"japanese":"子どもたちが路地で遊んでいます。","romaji":"Kodomotachi ga roji de asonde imasu.","german":"The children are playing in the alley.","furigana":"こ|ろじ|あそ"}],"notes":"路地 is a narrow lane between houses, often so narrow that no car can pass: 路地に入る, 路地に迷い込む, 路地裏 \"back alley\". Compared with 横丁 (a side street with shops), 路地 sounds more like a residential area and something hidden. For an ordinary street, people say 道 or 通り. The garden in front of a tea house is read the same way but is mostly written 露地.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"飛び石を渡ります。","romaji":"Tobiishi o watarimasu.","german":"I cross the stepping stones.","furigana":"と|いし|わた"},{"japanese":"庭の飛び石は雨の日に滑りやすいです。","romaji":"Niwa no tobiishi wa ame no hi ni suberiyasui desu.","german":"The stepping stones in the garden are slippery on rainy days.","furigana":"にわ|と|いし|あめ|ひ|すべ"},{"japanese":"今年のゴールデンウィークは飛び石連休です。","romaji":"Kotoshi no Goorudenwiiku wa tobiishi renkyuu desu.","german":"This year's Golden Week is a broken-up holiday stretch.","furigana":"ことし|と|いしれんきゅう"}],"notes":"飛び石 are flat stepping stones placed with gaps in Japanese gardens or across streams, so that you walk from stone to stone: 飛び石を渡る, 飛び石の上を歩く. Figuratively the word describes things that are spread out with gaps; well known is 飛び石連休 \"holidays with working days in between\". Stones in general are called 石, a gravel path 砂利道.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"庭に灯籠があります。","romaji":"Niwa ni tōrō ga arimasu.","german":"There is a stone lantern in the garden.","furigana":"にわ|とうろう"},{"japanese":"お寺の参道に石の灯籠が並んでいます。","romaji":"Otera no sandou ni ishi no tourou ga narande imasu.","german":"Stone lanterns line the approach to the temple.","furigana":"てら|さんどう|いし|とうろう|なら"},{"japanese":"夏祭りの夜、川に灯籠を流しました。","romaji":"Natsumatsuri no yoru, kawa ni tourou o nagashimashita.","german":"On the night of the summer festival, we floated lanterns down the river.","furigana":"なつまつ|よる|かわ|とうろう|なが"}],"notes":"灯籠 is a traditional lantern in which a flame used to burn. In gardens and at shrines, it usually means stone lanterns, more precisely 石灯籠; there are also hanging metal lanterns (釣り灯籠) and paper lanterns that are floated on the water at 灯籠流し. The portable, collapsible paper lantern in front of shops and at festivals, on the other hand, is called 提灯. The word is often also written 灯ろう.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"手水鉢で手を清めます。","romaji":"Chōzubachi de te o kiyomemasu.","german":"I purify my hands at the stone water basin.","furigana":"ちょうずばち|て|きよ"},{"japanese":"茶室の前に小さな手水鉢があります。","romaji":"Chashitsu no mae ni chiisana chouzubachi ga arimasu.","german":"There is a small stone water basin in front of the tea room.","furigana":"ちゃしつ|まえ|ちい|ちょうずばち"},{"japanese":"手水鉢の水に花が浮かべてありました。","romaji":"Chouzubachi no mizu ni hana ga ukabete arimashita.","german":"Flowers were floating in the water of the stone basin.","furigana":"ちょうずばち|みず|はな|う"}],"notes":"手水鉢 is a water basin, usually of stone, for purifying the hands - in a tea garden (often set low) or at shrines and temples. The roofed washing place at a shrine entrance is called 手水舎; the 手水鉢 is the basin itself. 手水 is read ちょうず (contracted from てみず); typical verbs are 手を清める \"to purify the hands\" and 口をすすぐ \"to rinse the mouth\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鹿威しの音が響く。","romaji":"Shishi odoshi no oto ga hibiku.","german":"The sound of the shishi-odoshi echoes.","furigana":"ししおど|おと|ひび"},{"japanese":"旅館の庭で、ししおどしが静かに鳴っています。","romaji":"Ryokan no niwa de, shishiodoshi ga shizuka ni natte imasu.","german":"In the ryokan's garden, the shishi-odoshi quietly clacks.","furigana":"りょかん|にわ|しず|な"},{"japanese":"鹿威しは、もともと動物を追い払うための道具でした。","romaji":"Shishiodoshi wa, motomoto doubutsu o oiharau tame no dougu deshita.","german":"The shishi-odoshi was originally a device for scaring away animals.","furigana":"ししおど|どうぶつ|お|はら|どうぐ"}],"notes":"鹿威し is a bamboo tube that slowly fills with water, tips over, empties and, as it snaps back, strikes a stone with a bright clack. Originally it was meant to scare away wild animals such as deer (鹿) (威す \"to frighten\"); today it is a sound element in the Japanese garden. It is mostly written in kana: ししおどし. The sound is rendered onomatopoeically as カーン or コーン.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"着付けを習いました。","romaji":"Kitsuke o naraimashita.","german":"I learned how to put on a kimono.","furigana":"きつ|なら"},{"japanese":"成人式の朝、美容院で着付けをしてもらいました。","romaji":"Seijinshiki no asa, biyouin de kitsuke o shite moraimashita.","german":"On the morning of Coming of Age Day, I had my kimono put on at the beauty salon.","furigana":"せいじんしき|あさ|びよういん|きつ"},{"japanese":"浴衣なら、自分で着付けができます。","romaji":"Yukata nara, jibun de kitsuke ga dekimasu.","german":"If it's a yukata, I can put it on by myself.","furigana":"ゆかた|じぶん|きつ"}],"notes":"着付け is the proper putting on of a kimono or yukata, including tying the obi - a skill of its own that you learn in a 着付け教室. People often have it done professionally: 着付けをしてもらう, 美容院で着付けを頼む. For the ordinary putting on of Western clothing, 服を着る is used, not 着付け. The related verb is 着付ける.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"蝋燭に火をつけます。","romaji":"Rōsoku ni hi o tsukemasu.","german":"I light the candle.","furigana":"ろうそく|ひ"},{"japanese":"停電したので、ろうそくをつけました。","romaji":"Teiden shita node, rousoku o tsukemashita.","german":"The power went out, so I lit candles.","furigana":"ていでん"},{"japanese":"ケーキのろうそくを一気に吹き消しました。","romaji":"Keeki no rousoku o ikki ni fukikeshimashita.","german":"I blew out the candles on the cake in one go.","furigana":"いっき|ふ|け"}],"notes":"蝋燭 is the candle; because the kanji are difficult, it is almost always written ろうそく or ロウソク. Typical uses: ろうそくに火をつける \"to light\", ろうそくを消す or 吹き消す \"to blow out\". キャンドル sounds modern and decorative (scented candle, candlelight dinner), whereas ろうそく is the neutral word, for example in a power outage, at the household altar (仏壇) or at a grave.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"香炉でお香を焚きます。","romaji":"Kōro de okō o takimasu.","german":"I burn incense in the incense burner.","furigana":"こうろ|こう|た"},{"japanese":"お寺の大きな香炉の煙を浴びると、健康になると言われています。","romaji":"Otera no ookina kouro no kemuri o abiru to, kenkou ni naru to iwarete imasu.","german":"It is said that if you bathe in the smoke of the large incense burner at the temple, you will become healthy.","furigana":"てら|おお|こうろ|けむり|あ|けんこう|い"},{"japanese":"仏壇の香炉に線香を立てました。","romaji":"Butsudan no kouro ni senkou o tatemashita.","german":"I stood an incense stick in the incense burner on the household altar.","furigana":"ぶつだん|こうろ|せんこう|た"}],"notes":"香炉 is a vessel made of metal or ceramic in which incense is burned - at the household altar (仏壇), at graves or in front of temples, where large bronze 香炉 stand and visitors waft the smoke toward themselves. The incense stick itself is called 線香, incense in general お香. Typical uses: 香炉に線香を立てる, お香を焚く.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"御守りを買いました。","romaji":"Omamori o kaimashita.","german":"I bought an amulet.","furigana":"おまも|か"},{"japanese":"受験の前に、祖母が合格のお守りをくれました。","romaji":"Juken no mae ni, sobo ga goukaku no omamori o kuremashita.","german":"Before the entrance exam, my grandmother gave me an amulet for passing.","furigana":"じゅけん|まえ|そぼ|ごうかく|まも"},{"japanese":"車に交通安全のお守りを付けています。","romaji":"Kuruma ni koutsuu anzen no omamori o tsukete imasu.","german":"I have a traffic safety amulet on my car.","furigana":"くるま|こうつうあんぜん|まも|つ"}],"notes":"御守り is an amulet from a shrine or temple, usually a small cloth pouch that is meant to bring protection or luck in a particular area: 交通安全 (traffic), 合格 (exams), 安産 (childbirth). The spelling お守り is usual. One says お守りを買う, more reverently お守りを受ける, and carries them on a bag or key ring. Not to be confused with お守り read おもり \"babysitting\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"御籤を引きました。","romaji":"Omikuji o hikimashita.","german":"I drew an omikuji.","furigana":"おみくじ|ひ"},{"japanese":"初詣のおみくじで大吉が出ました！","romaji":"Hatsumoude no omikuji de daikichi ga demashita!","german":"I got \"great blessing\" in the omikuji at my first shrine visit of the year!","furigana":"はつもうで|だいきち|で"},{"japanese":"悪いおみくじは、神社の木に結んで帰ります。","romaji":"Warui omikuji wa, jinja no ki ni musunde kaerimasu.","german":"A bad omikuji is tied to a tree at the shrine before going home.","furigana":"わる|じんじゃ|き|むす|かえ"}],"notes":"おみくじ are fortune slips that you draw at shrines and temples for a small donation, especially on the New Year visit 初詣. You say おみくじを引く; the result ranges from 大吉 (\"great blessing\") through 吉, 中吉, 小吉 and 末吉 to 凶 (\"misfortune\"). Bad slips are often tied to a tree or a rack at the shrine to leave the bad luck there. The word is almost always written in kana; 御籤 or 御神籤 are rare.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"参道を歩きます。","romaji":"Sandō o arukimasu.","german":"I walk along the approach path.","furigana":"さんどう|ある"},{"japanese":"参道の両側にお土産屋さんが並んでいます。","romaji":"Sandou no ryougawa ni omiyageyasan ga narande imasu.","german":"Souvenir shops line both sides of the approach.","furigana":"さんどう|りょうがわ|みやげや|なら"},{"japanese":"参道の真ん中は神様の通り道だと言われています。","romaji":"Sandou no mannaka wa kamisama no toorimichi da to iwarete imasu.","german":"It is said that the middle of the approach is the path of the gods.","furigana":"さんどう|ま|なか|かみさま|とお|みち|い"}],"notes":"参道 is the path that leads to the sanctuary of a shrine or temple, often lined with 鳥居, stone lanterns and shops (参道の店); 表参道 is the main approach. The middle of the 参道 is considered the path of the deity, so it is polite to walk at the edge. The word is related to 参る \"to visit (a shrine)\", as in お参り.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"拝殿でお参りします。","romaji":"Haiden de omairi shimasu.","german":"I pray at the worship hall.","furigana":"はいでん|まい"},{"japanese":"拝殿の前で二回お辞儀をして、手を二回たたきます。","romaji":"Haiden no mae de nikai ojigi o shite, te o nikai tatakimasu.","german":"In front of the worship hall, you bow twice and clap your hands twice.","furigana":"はいでん|まえ|にかい|じぎ|て|にかい"},{"japanese":"拝殿の奥に本殿があります。","romaji":"Haiden no oku ni honden ga arimasu.","german":"The main hall is behind the worship hall.","furigana":"はいでん|おく|ほんでん"}],"notes":"拝殿 is the worship hall of a shrine (神社), in front of which visitors throw coins into the offertory box (賽銭箱), ring the bell and pray. Behind it lies the 本殿, in which the deity is enshrined and which is normally not entered. For Buddhist temples, 本堂 is used instead. Typical uses: 拝殿で手を合わせる, 拝殿の前でお参りする.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"本殿は一般公開されていません。","romaji":"Honden wa ippan kōkai sarete imasen.","german":"The main hall is not open to the public.","furigana":"ほんでん|いっぱんこうかい"},{"japanese":"この神社の本殿は国宝です。","romaji":"Kono jinja no honden wa kokuhou desu.","german":"The main hall of this shrine is a national treasure.","furigana":"じんじゃ|ほんでん|こくほう"},{"japanese":"本殿の屋根は、去年新しくなりました。","romaji":"Honden no yane wa, kyonen atarashiku narimashita.","german":"The roof of the main hall was renewed last year.","furigana":"ほんでん|やね|きょねんあたら"}],"notes":"本殿 is the main building of a shrine in which the deity or its symbol is enshrined. It usually lies behind the 拝殿 and is generally closed to visitors; prayers are made in front of the 拝殿. For the main hall of a Buddhist temple, 本堂 is used, not 本殿. It is common in descriptions such as 本殿は国宝に指定されている.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"五重塔が見えます。","romaji":"Gojū no tō ga miemasu.","german":"I can see the five-story pagoda.","furigana":"ごじゅうのとう|み"},{"japanese":"法隆寺の五重塔は世界で一番古い木の塔の一つです。","romaji":"Houryuuji no gojuu no tou wa sekai de ichiban furui ki no tou no hitotsu desu.","german":"The five-story pagoda of Horyuji is one of the oldest wooden towers in the world.","furigana":"ほうりゅうじ|ごじゅうのとう|せかい|いちばんふる|き|とう|ひと"},{"japanese":"五重塔は地震でもなかなか倒れないそうです。","romaji":"Gojuu no tou wa jishin demo nakanaka taorenai sou desu.","german":"Five-story pagodas are said to hardly collapse even in earthquakes.","furigana":"ごじゅうのとう|じしん|たお"}],"notes":"五重塔 is the five-story pagoda of a Buddhist temple; it is read ごじゅうのとう with an inserted の that is not written. Well-known examples stand in 京都 (東寺) and 奈良 (法隆寺). Correspondingly, a three-story pagoda is called 三重塔; for the floors of ordinary buildings, 階 is used instead (五階建て). 塔 alone means \"tower\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"梵鐘の音が響きます。","romaji":"Bonshō no oto ga hibikimasu.","german":"The sound of the temple bell echoes.","furigana":"ぼんしょう|おと|ひび"},{"japanese":"この寺の梵鐘は四百年前に作られました。","romaji":"Kono tera no bonshou wa yonhyakunen mae ni tsukuraremashita.","german":"The bell of this temple was cast 400 years ago.","furigana":"てら|ぼんしょう|よんひゃくねんまえ|つく"},{"japanese":"戦争中、多くの梵鐘が金属として集められました。","romaji":"Sensouchuu, ooku no bonshou ga kinzoku to shite atsumeraremashita.","german":"During the war, many temple bells were collected as metal.","furigana":"せんそうちゅう|おお|ぼんしょう|きんぞく|あつ"}],"notes":"梵鐘 is the large bronze bell in the bell tower (鐘楼) of a Buddhist temple; it has no clapper but is struck from the outside with a wooden beam: 鐘をつく. In everyday speech, people usually just say お寺の鐘 or 鐘; 梵鐘 is the technical, written-language term (museums, cultural properties). Well known is the ringing on New Year's Eve, 除夜の鐘.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"石段を登ります。","romaji":"Ishidan o noborimasu.","german":"I climb the stone steps.","furigana":"いしだん|のぼ"},{"japanese":"神社まで三百段の石段があります。","romaji":"Jinja made sanbyakudan no ishidan ga arimasu.","german":"There are 300 stone steps up to the shrine.","furigana":"じんじゃ|さんびゃくだん|いしだん"},{"japanese":"石段に座って、町の景色を眺めました。","romaji":"Ishidan ni suwatte, machi no keshiki o nagamemashita.","german":"We sat on the stone steps and looked at the view of the town.","furigana":"いしだん|すわ|まち|けしき|なが"}],"notes":"石段 is a staircase of stone steps outdoors, typical on slopes and as the approach to shrines and temples: 石段を上る/登る, 石段を下りる. Staircases inside buildings are called 階段; 石段 emphasizes the material and the outdoor location. The steps are counted with 段: 三百段の石段.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"苔寺を訪れました。","romaji":"Kokedera o otozuremashita.","german":"I visited the Moss Temple.","furigana":"こけでら|おとず"},{"japanese":"苔寺の庭は、雨の後が一番きれいだそうです。","romaji":"Kokedera no niwa wa, ame no ato ga ichiban kirei da sou desu.","german":"The garden of the Moss Temple is said to be most beautiful after rain.","furigana":"こけでら|にわ|あめ|あと|いちばん"},{"japanese":"苔寺を見学するには、事前に申し込みが必要です。","romaji":"Kokedera o kengaku suru ni wa, jizen ni moushikomi ga hitsuyou desu.","german":"To tour the Moss Temple, you need to apply in advance.","furigana":"こけでら|けんがく|じぜん|もう|こ|ひつよう"}],"notes":"苔寺 is the popular name of the temple 西芳寺 in 京都, whose garden is covered with over a hundred kinds of moss (苔 \"moss\" + 寺 \"temple\"). It is thus a proper name and not a general word; a moss garden in general is called 苔庭. In the compound, 寺 becomes voiced: てら → でら, as in 山寺. A visit is possible only with prior application.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"茅葺きの屋根が残っている。","romaji":"Kayabuki no yane ga nokotte iru.","german":"The thatched roof is still preserved.","furigana":"かやぶ|やね|のこ"},{"japanese":"白川郷には茅葺きの家がたくさんあります。","romaji":"Shirakawagou ni wa kayabuki no ie ga takusan arimasu.","german":"In Shirakawa-go there are many houses with thatched roofs.","furigana":"|かやぶ|いえ"},{"japanese":"茅葺きの屋根は、夏は涼しく、冬は暖かいそうです。","romaji":"Kayabuki no yane wa, natsu wa suzushiku, fuyu wa atatakai sou desu.","german":"Thatched roofs are said to be cool in summer and warm in winter.","furigana":"かやぶ|やね|なつ|すず|ふゆ|あたた"}],"notes":"茅葺き refers to a roof thatched with grasses such as Chinese silvergrass or reed (茅) (葺く \"to roof\"), as well as the thatching itself. It is mostly used attributively with の: 茅葺きの屋根, 茅葺きの家; the farmhouses of 白川郷 are famous. A roof made of rice straw is more precisely called 藁葺き, a tiled roof 瓦屋根. The kana spelling かやぶき is also common.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"轆轤を回して器を作ります。","romaji":"Rokuro o mawashite utsuwa o tsukurimasu.","german":"I turn the potter's wheel and make vessels.","furigana":"ろくろ|まわ|うつわ|つく"},{"japanese":"陶芸教室で、初めてろくろを使いました。","romaji":"Tougei kyoushitsu de, hajimete rokuro o tsukaimashita.","german":"In the pottery class, I used a potter's wheel for the first time.","furigana":"とうげいきょうしつ|はじ|つか"},{"japanese":"ろくろの上で、土の形がどんどん変わっていきます。","romaji":"Rokuro no ue de, tsuchi no katachi ga dondon kawatte ikimasu.","german":"On the potter's wheel, the shape of the clay keeps changing.","furigana":"うえ|つち|かたち|か"}],"notes":"轆轤 is mainly the potter's wheel; since the kanji are very difficult, it is mostly written ろくろ. Typical uses: ろくろを回す \"to turn the wheel\", ろくろを挽く \"to shape on the wheel\", 電動ろくろ \"electric potter's wheel\". The word also refers to a lathe, as well as a winch or pulley. Pottery as an art and hobby is called 陶芸.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"窯で焼き物を焼きます。","romaji":"Kama de yakimono o yakimasu.","german":"I fire pottery in the kiln.","furigana":"かま|や|もの|や"},{"japanese":"このピザは石窯で焼いています。","romaji":"Kono piza wa ishigama de yaite imasu.","german":"This pizza is baked in a stone oven.","furigana":"いしがま|や"},{"japanese":"窯から出したばかりの茶碗は、まだ熱いです。","romaji":"Kama kara dashita bakari no chawan wa, mada atsui desu.","german":"The tea bowl fresh out of the kiln is still hot.","furigana":"かま|だ|ちゃわん|あつ"}],"notes":"窯 is a kiln, above all for pottery but also for charcoal or bricks; today it often appears in 窯焼きピザ \"wood-fired oven pizza\". Typical: 窯で焼く, 窯に入れる, 窯から出す. Not to be confused with the identically read 釜 \"kettle, pot\" (e.g. the inner pot of a rice cooker). In compounds it is often がま: 石窯 \"stone oven\", 登り窯 \"climbing kiln\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"釉薬をかけます。","romaji":"Yūyaku o kakemasu.","german":"I apply glaze.","furigana":"ゆうやく"},{"japanese":"釉薬の色は、焼く前と後でかなり違います。","romaji":"Yuuyaku no iro wa, yaku mae to ato de kanari chigaimasu.","german":"The color of the glaze is quite different before and after firing.","furigana":"ゆうやく|いろ|や|まえ|あと|ちが"},{"japanese":"この皿には青い釉薬が使われています。","romaji":"Kono sara ni wa aoi yuuyaku ga tsukawarete imasu.","german":"A blue glaze was used for this plate.","furigana":"さら|あお|ゆうやく|つか"}],"notes":"釉薬 is the glaze that is applied to ceramics before the second firing and that melts into a glass-like layer during firing: 釉薬をかける (by dipping or pouring), 釉薬を塗る (with a brush). Synonymous, but older and without the difficult kanji, is うわぐすり. Sugar glaze on baked goods, by contrast, is called アイシング.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"藍を使った染色を習っています。","romaji":"Ai o tsukatta senshoku o naratte imasu.","german":"I'm learning dyeing with indigo.","furigana":"あい|つか|せんしょく|なら"},{"japanese":"京都には昔からの染色の工場が多いです。","romaji":"Kyouto ni wa mukashi kara no senshoku no koujou ga ooi desu.","german":"Kyoto has many traditional dyeing workshops.","furigana":"きょうと|むかし|せんしょく|こうじょう|おお"},{"japanese":"この布は天然の材料で染色されています。","romaji":"Kono nuno wa tennen no zairyou de senshoku sarete imasu.","german":"This cloth is dyed with natural materials.","furigana":"ぬの|てんねん|ざいりょう|せんしょく"}],"notes":"染色 is the dyeing of fabrics, yarn or hair, and in biology the staining of samples; it is the technical Sino-Japanese word for 染める. In daily life people mostly say 染める or 〜染め (藍染め, 草木染め); 染色 is found in crafts, industry and science: 染色工場, 染色体 \"chromosome\". As a する-verb: 布を染色する, often in the passive 染色される. Dyeing hair at the hairdresser, on the other hand, is called カラー or 髪を染める.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"藍で布を染めます。","romaji":"Ai de nuno o somemasu.","german":"I dye cloth with indigo.","furigana":"あい|ぬの|そ"},{"japanese":"藍染めのハンカチをお土産に買いました。","romaji":"Aizome no hankachi o omiyage ni kaimashita.","german":"I bought an indigo-dyed handkerchief as a souvenir.","furigana":"あいぞ|みやげ|か"},{"japanese":"藍色の浴衣がとても似合っていますね。","romaji":"Aiiro no yukata ga totemo niatte imasu ne.","german":"The indigo yukata suits you very well.","furigana":"あいいろ|ゆかた|にあ"}],"notes":"藍 is the dye plant (Japanese indigo), the indigo dye obtained from it and its hue (藍色 \"indigo blue\"). It is most often encountered in 藍染め \"indigo dyeing\", a traditional craft, for example from 徳島. As a color, 藍色 is darker than 青 but lighter than 紺 \"dark blue, navy\". Well known is the proverb 青は藍より出でて藍より青し: the student surpasses the master.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"紅を差す。","romaji":"Beni o sasu.","german":"I apply lip color.","furigana":"べに|さ"},{"japanese":"祖母は出かける前に、いつも口紅を差します。","romaji":"Sobo wa dekakeru mae ni, itsumo kuchibeni o sashimasu.","german":"My grandmother always puts on lipstick before going out.","furigana":"そぼ|で|まえ|くちべに|さ"},{"japanese":"庭に紅色の花が咲いています。","romaji":"Niwa ni beniiro no hana ga saite imasu.","german":"Crimson flowers are blooming in the garden.","furigana":"にわ|べにいろ|はな|さ"}],"notes":"紅（べに） is a strong crimson, originally the dye obtained from safflower (紅花), and hence also rouge or lip color: 紅を差す \"to apply red to the lips or cheeks\", 口紅 \"lipstick\", 頬紅 \"blush\". The color is called 紅色; poetically, 紅 is also read くれない. The Sino-Japanese reading こう is in 紅茶 and 紅葉. In daily life, people otherwise mostly use 赤 or 真っ赤 for the color.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"千代紙で鶴を折ります。","romaji":"Chiyogami de tsuru o orimasu.","german":"I fold a crane from patterned paper.","furigana":"ちよがみ|つる|お"},{"japanese":"千代紙を貼ると、普通の箱がかわいくなります。","romaji":"Chiyogami o haru to, futsuu no hako ga kawaiku narimasu.","german":"Covered with chiyogami, an ordinary box becomes really cute.","furigana":"ちよがみ|は|ふつう|はこ"},{"japanese":"お土産屋さんで、きれいな千代紙を買いました。","romaji":"Omiyageyasan de, kirei na chiyogami o kaimashita.","german":"I bought some beautiful chiyogami paper at the souvenir shop.","furigana":"みやげや|ちよがみ|か"}],"notes":"千代紙 is colorful paper with traditional patterns such as flowers, cranes or waves, used for folding, for paper dolls or for covering small boxes. 千代 \"a thousand generations\" alludes to the auspicious motifs. Plain-colored folding paper is simply called 折り紙; 和紙 is handmade Japanese paper in general.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"水引を結びます。","romaji":"Mizuhiki o musubimasu.","german":"I tie the mizuhiki.","furigana":"みずひき|むす"},{"japanese":"結婚のお祝いには、ほどけない結び方の水引を使います。","romaji":"Kekkon no oiwai ni wa, hodokenai musubikata no mizuhiki o tsukaimasu.","german":"For wedding gifts, you use a mizuhiki with a knot that cannot be untied.","furigana":"けっこん|いわ|むす|かた|みずひき|つか"},{"japanese":"お葬式の袋には、黒と白の水引がついています。","romaji":"Osoushiki no fukuro ni wa, kuro to shiro no mizuhiki ga tsuite imasu.","german":"A black-and-white mizuhiki is attached to the envelope for a funeral.","furigana":"そうしき|ふくろ|くろ|しろ|みずひき"}],"notes":"水引 are stiff decorative cords made of twisted, dyed paper that are tied around money envelopes and gifts. Color and knot carry meaning: red-white or gold-silver for joyful occasions, black-white for bereavements; the unbreakable knot 結び切り stands for occasions that should not be repeated (wedding, mourning), the bow 蝶結び for repeatable ones such as births. On many envelopes today the 水引 is only printed.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"熨斗をつけてください。","romaji":"Noshi o tsukete kudasai.","german":"Please add a noshi.","furigana":"のし"},{"japanese":"お中元に「御中元」と書いたのしをつけました。","romaji":"Ochuugen ni \"Ochuugen\" to kaita noshi o tsukemashita.","german":"I attached a noshi paper reading \"Ochugen\" to the summer gift.","furigana":"ちゅうげん|おちゅうげん|か"},{"japanese":"店員さんに「のしはどうなさいますか」と聞かれました。","romaji":"Ten'in-san ni \"Noshi wa dou nasaimasu ka\" to kikaremashita.","german":"The shop assistant asked me whether I wanted noshi on it.","furigana":"てんいん|き"}],"notes":"熨斗 was originally a strip of dried abalone in folded paper that was enclosed with gifts as a good-luck sign; today it is mostly just a small symbol at the top right of the wrapping paper. In shops you hear のしをお付けしますか; this usually means のし紙, a sheet with a printed 水引 and the occasion (御祝, 御中元). For bereavements, no のし is used. The word is almost always written in kana.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"祝儀袋にお金を入れます。","romaji":"Shūgi bukuro ni okane o iremasu.","german":"I put money in the celebration envelope.","furigana":"しゅうぎぶくろ|かね|い"},{"japanese":"友達の結婚式のために、コンビニで祝儀袋を買いました。","romaji":"Tomodachi no kekkonshiki no tame ni, konbini de shuugi bukuro o kaimashita.","german":"For my friend's wedding, I bought a celebration envelope at a convenience store.","furigana":"ともだち|けっこんしき|しゅうぎぶくろ|か"},{"japanese":"祝儀袋の表に自分の名前を書きます。","romaji":"Shuugi bukuro no omote ni jibun no namae o kakimasu.","german":"You write your own name on the front of the envelope.","furigana":"しゅうぎぶくろ|おもて|じぶん|なまえ|か"}],"notes":"祝儀袋 is the decorated envelope for gifts of money on joyful occasions, above all weddings; politely often ご祝儀袋. It bears red-and-white or gold-and-silver 水引, the occasion on the front (寿, 御祝) and below it the name of the giver. It should contain new, unfolded bills; the money itself is called ご祝儀. The counterpart for bereavements is the 不祝儀袋 or 香典袋.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不祝儀袋を持参します。","romaji":"Bushūgi bukuro o jisan shimasu.","german":"I'm bringing a condolence envelope.","furigana":"ぶしゅうぎぶくろ|じさん"},{"japanese":"急なお葬式だったので、コンビニで不祝儀袋を買いました。","romaji":"Kyuu na osoushiki datta node, konbini de bushuugi bukuro o kaimashita.","german":"The funeral was so sudden that I bought a condolence envelope at a convenience store.","furigana":"きゅう|そうしき|ぶしゅうぎぶくろ|か"},{"japanese":"不祝儀袋には、新しいお札を入れないほうがいいです。","romaji":"Bushuugi bukuro ni wa, atarashii osatsu o irenai hou ga ii desu.","german":"You should not put new bills in a condolence envelope.","furigana":"ぶしゅうぎぶくろ|あたら|さつ|い"}],"notes":"不祝儀袋 is the envelope for money on occasions of mourning, above all for condolence money (香典) at funerals; in daily life, people mostly say 香典袋. It has black-and-white or silver 水引 and inscriptions such as 御霊前 or 御香典. 不祝儀 means \"sad occasion\" and is the opposite of 祝儀. Traditionally, you do not put new bills in it, so that it does not look as if you had expected the death.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"暑中見舞いを出します。","romaji":"Shochū mimai o dashimasu.","german":"I'm sending a summer greeting.","furigana":"しょちゅうみま|だ"},{"japanese":"先生から暑中見舞いのはがきが届きました。","romaji":"Sensei kara shochuu mimai no hagaki ga todokimashita.","german":"A summer greeting card arrived from my teacher.","furigana":"せんせい|しょちゅうみま|とど"},{"japanese":"八月の後半なら、暑中見舞いではなく残暑見舞いを送ります。","romaji":"Hachigatsu no kouhan nara, shochuu mimai de wa naku zansho mimai o okurimasu.","german":"In the second half of August, you send a late-summer greeting instead of a summer greeting.","furigana":"はちがつ|こうはん|しょちゅうみま|ざんしょみま|おく"}],"notes":"暑中見舞い is a greeting card sent during the hottest time (roughly mid-July to early August) to friends, relatives and business partners to ask how they are doing. Typical: 暑中見舞いを出す/送る; the card begins with 暑中お見舞い申し上げます. After 立秋 (around August 8), people write 残暑見舞い instead. The counterpart at the turn of the year is the New Year's card 年賀状.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"喪中なので年賀状は出しません。","romaji":"Mochū nanode nengajō wa dashimasen.","german":"We are in mourning, so we won't send New Year's cards.","furigana":"もちゅう|ねんがじょう|だ"},{"japanese":"十一月に、友達から喪中のはがきが届きました。","romaji":"Juuichigatsu ni, tomodachi kara mochuu no hagaki ga todokimashita.","german":"In November, I received a card from a friend saying his family is in mourning.","furigana":"じゅういちがつ|ともだち|もちゅう|とど"},{"japanese":"喪中の人には、「おめでとう」と言わないようにしましょう。","romaji":"Mochuu no hito ni wa, \"omedetou\" to iwanai you ni shimashou.","german":"Let's avoid saying \"congratulations\" to people in mourning.","furigana":"もちゅう|ひと|い"}],"notes":"喪中 is the mourning period after the death of a close relative, usually about one year. During this time you refrain from celebrations and above all from New Year's greetings: instead of 年賀状, you send a 喪中はがき in November or early December announcing that you will not be sending New Year's cards. Greetings such as あけましておめでとう are also avoided toward those in mourning. In writing, it is 喪中につき \"due to mourning\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"除夜の鐘を聞きます。","romaji":"Joya no kane o kikimasu.","german":"I listen to the New Year's Eve bells.","furigana":"じょや|かね|き"},{"japanese":"近くのお寺で、除夜の鐘をつかせてもらいました。","romaji":"Chikaku no otera de, joya no kane o tsukasete moraimashita.","german":"At a nearby temple, I was allowed to ring the New Year's Eve bell.","furigana":"ちか|てら|じょや|かね"},{"japanese":"除夜の鐘が鳴り始めたら、もうすぐ新年です。","romaji":"Joya no kane ga narihajimetara, mou sugu shinnen desu.","german":"When the New Year's Eve bells begin to ring, the new year is almost here.","furigana":"じょや|かね|な|はじ|しんねん"}],"notes":"除夜の鐘 is the ringing of the temple bells on New Year's Eve night (除夜 \"the last night of the year\"): the bell is struck 108 times, corresponding to the 108 worldly desires (煩悩) in Buddhism. Typical uses: 除夜の鐘を聞く, 除夜の鐘が鳴る, 除夜の鐘をつく (to strike it yourself, possible for visitors at many temples). After that often follows the first shrine or temple visit of the year, 初詣.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鏡餅を飾ります。","romaji":"Kagami mochi o kazarimasu.","german":"I put up the kagami mochi.","furigana":"かがみもち|かざ"},{"japanese":"鏡開きの日に、鏡餅をおしるこにして食べました。","romaji":"Kagamibiraki no hi ni, kagami mochi o oshiruko ni shite tabemashita.","german":"On Kagami-biraki day, we turned the kagami mochi into oshiruko and ate it.","furigana":"かがみびら|ひ|かがみもち|た"},{"japanese":"最近は、プラスチックの鏡餅もよく売られています。","romaji":"Saikin wa, purasuchikku no kagami mochi mo yoku urarete imasu.","german":"Recently, plastic kagami mochi are also often sold.","furigana":"さいきん|かがみもち|う"}],"notes":"鏡餅 are two round rice cakes stacked on top of each other with a bitter orange (橙, today often a mandarin) on top, set up in the house at New Year as an offering for the New Year deity; the name comes from the round shape of old mirrors (鏡). You say 鏡餅を飾る or 鏡餅を供える. Around January 11, it is broken at the 鏡開き - not cut, since cutting is considered bad luck - and eaten, for example, in sweet bean soup (おしるこ).","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"門松を飾ります。","romaji":"Kadomatsu o kazarimasu.","german":"I put up the kadomatsu.","furigana":"かどまつ|かざ"},{"japanese":"デパートの入り口に大きな門松が立っています。","romaji":"Depaato no iriguchi ni ookina kadomatsu ga tatte imasu.","german":"A large kadomatsu stands at the entrance of the department store.","furigana":"い|ぐち|おお|かどまつ|た"},{"japanese":"一月七日を過ぎたら、門松を片付けます。","romaji":"Ichigatsu nanoka o sugitara, kadomatsu o katazukemasu.","german":"After January 7, we take down the kadomatsu.","furigana":"いちがつなのか|す|かどまつ|かたづ"}],"notes":"門松 is the New Year decoration made of pine branches and bamboo stalks cut at an angle, set up in pairs on the left and right of the house entrance or in front of shops. It is regarded as a guide and a seat for the New Year deity (年神). It is put up in the second half of December and taken down again after the New Year period (松の内, until January 7 or 15 depending on the region). Typical uses: 門松を飾る, 門松を立てる.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"注連縄を飾ります。","romaji":"Shimenawa o kazarimasu.","german":"I hang up the shimenawa.","furigana":"しめなわ|かざ"},{"japanese":"神社の大きな木に、しめ縄が巻いてあります。","romaji":"Jinja no ookina ki ni, shimenawa ga maite arimasu.","german":"A shimenawa is tied around the large tree at the shrine.","furigana":"じんじゃ|おお|き|なわ|ま"},{"japanese":"出雲大社のしめ縄は、とても大きいことで有名です。","romaji":"Izumo Taisha no shimenawa wa, totemo ookii koto de yuumei desu.","german":"The shimenawa of Izumo Taisha is famous for being enormous.","furigana":"|なわ|おお|ゆうめい"}],"notes":"注連縄 is a twisted rope of rice straw that marks off a sacred area - at shrines, around sacred trees or rocks - and is often hung with zigzag paper strips (紙垂). At New Year, a small decorated version, 注連飾り, is hung on the front door to ward off evil. Because of the difficult kanji, it is mostly written しめ縄 or しめなわ. Typical uses: しめ縄を張る, しめ縄を飾る.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鯉のぼりが風に泳いでいる。","romaji":"Koinobori ga kaze ni oyoide iru.","german":"The carp streamers are swimming in the wind.","furigana":"こい|かぜ|およ"},{"japanese":"息子が生まれたので、庭に鯉のぼりを上げました。","romaji":"Musuko ga umareta node, niwa ni koinobori o agemashita.","german":"Since our son was born, we raised carp streamers in the garden.","furigana":"むすこ|う|にわ|こい|あ"},{"japanese":"川の上にたくさんの鯉のぼりが飾られています。","romaji":"Kawa no ue ni takusan no koinobori ga kazararete imasu.","german":"Many carp streamers are displayed above the river.","furigana":"かわ|うえ|こい|かざ"}],"notes":"鯉のぼり are carp-shaped wind socks made of cloth that are raised on a pole in spring until Children's Day (こどもの日, May 5) - traditionally for the health and success of sons, because the carp swims against the current. Classically a black carp for the father hangs at the top, below it a red one and smaller colored ones for the mother and children. You say 鯉のぼりを上げる/立てる, and the streamers \"swim\" in the wind: 鯉のぼりが泳ぐ. Also often written in kana: こいのぼり.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"兜を飾ります。","romaji":"Kabuto o kazarimasu.","german":"I'm displaying the samurai helmet.","furigana":"かぶと|かざ"},{"japanese":"子どもの日に、新聞紙で兜を折りました。","romaji":"Kodomo no hi ni, shinbunshi de kabuto o orimashita.","german":"On Children's Day, I folded a helmet out of newspaper.","furigana":"こ|ひ|しんぶんし|かぶと|お"},{"japanese":"彼の知識には、さすがに兜を脱ぎました。","romaji":"Kare no chishiki ni wa, sasuga ni kabuto o nugimashita.","german":"I really had to admit defeat in the face of his knowledge.","furigana":"かれ|ちしき|かぶと|ぬ"}],"notes":"兜 is the helmet of a samurai or warrior. Today it is encountered mainly on Children's Day: an ornamental helmet or armor (五月人形) is put on display, and a helmet is folded for children out of newspaper (新聞紙で兜を折る). Modern protective helmets for construction or cycling are called ヘルメット. Idiom: 兜を脱ぐ \"to admit defeat\"; カブトムシ (兜虫) is the rhinoceros beetle.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"七夕に短冊を書きます。","romaji":"Tanabata ni tanzaku o kakimasu.","german":"On Tanabata, we write wish strips.","furigana":"たなばた|たんざく|か"},{"japanese":"仙台の七夕祭りは八月に行われます。","romaji":"Sendai no tanabata matsuri wa hachigatsu ni okonawaremasu.","german":"The Tanabata festival in Sendai is held in August.","furigana":"せんだい|たなばたまつ|はちがつ|おこな"},{"japanese":"七夕の夜は、晴れるといいですね。","romaji":"Tanabata no yoru wa, hareru to ii desu ne.","german":"I hope the sky is clear on the night of Tanabata.","furigana":"たなばた|よる|は"}],"notes":"七夕 is the star festival on July 7 (in some regions in August). It goes back to the legend of the weaver 織姫 (Vega) and the cowherd 彦星 (Altair), who are allowed to meet only on this night across the Milky Way (天の川). People write wishes on 短冊 and hang them on bamboo branches (笹). The reading たなばた is a special reading of the whole word and cannot be derived from the individual kanji.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"短冊に願い事を書きます。","romaji":"Tanzaku ni negaigoto o kakimasu.","german":"I write a wish on the tanzaku.","furigana":"たんざく|ねが|ごと|か"},{"japanese":"子どもたちが短冊を笹に飾りました。","romaji":"Kodomotachi ga tanzaku o sasa ni kazarimashita.","german":"The children hung their tanzaku on the bamboo.","furigana":"こ|たんざく|ささ|かざ"},{"japanese":"大根を短冊切りにしてください。","romaji":"Daikon o tanzakugiri ni shite kudasai.","german":"Please cut the daikon radish into thin rectangular strips.","furigana":"だいこん|たんざくぎ"}],"notes":"短冊 is a long, narrow strip of paper; today it is best known from the 七夕 festival, where wishes are written on it and it is hung on bamboo branches (短冊に願い事を書く, 短冊を笹に飾る). Traditionally, poems such as 俳句 or 短歌 are also written on sturdier 短冊. In the kitchen, 短冊切り means cutting vegetables into thin rectangular strips.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"精霊流しで灯籠を流します。","romaji":"Shōrō nagashi de tōrō o nagashimasu.","german":"We float lanterns at the shoro nagashi.","furigana":"しょうろうなが|とうろう|なが"},{"japanese":"長崎の精霊流しでは、大きな船を引いて町を歩きます。","romaji":"Nagasaki no shourou nagashi de wa, ookina fune o hiite machi o arukimasu.","german":"At the Shoro Nagashi in Nagasaki, people pull large boats through the town.","furigana":"ながさき|しょうろうなが|おお|ふね|ひ|まち|ある"},{"japanese":"祖父が亡くなった年、家族で精霊流しをしました。","romaji":"Sofu ga nakunatta toshi, kazoku de shourou nagashi o shimashita.","german":"The year my grandfather died, we held a shoro nagashi as a family.","furigana":"そふ|な|とし|かぞく|しょうろうなが"}],"notes":"精霊流し is a custom at the end of お盆: small boats or lanterns with offerings are set on a river or the sea to send off the returned souls of the deceased (精霊, read しょうろう here). Very similar is 灯籠流し, in which only paper lanterns are floated. In 長崎, by contrast, families pull large, decorated boats through the streets to the noise of fireworks during the 精霊流し. Typical uses: 精霊流しをする, 精霊流しが行われる.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"迎え火を焚きます。","romaji":"Mukaebi o takimasu.","german":"I light the welcoming fire.","furigana":"むか|び|た"},{"japanese":"お盆の初日の夕方、玄関の前で迎え火をしました。","romaji":"Obon no shonichi no yuugata, genkan no mae de mukaebi o shimashita.","german":"On the evening of the first day of Obon, we lit the welcoming fire in front of the entrance.","furigana":"ぼん|しょにち|ゆうがた|げんかん|まえ|むか|び"},{"japanese":"マンションなので、迎え火の代わりに電気の提灯を使っています。","romaji":"Manshon nanode, mukaebi no kawari ni denki no chouchin o tsukatte imasu.","german":"Since we live in an apartment, we use an electric lantern instead of a welcoming fire.","furigana":"むか|び|か|でんき|ちょうちん|つか"}],"notes":"迎え火 is the small fire lit in front of the house or at the grave on the first evening of お盆 (usually on July 13 or August 13) so that the souls of the ancestors can find their way home. Traditionally, hemp stalks (おがら) are burned in a clay dish for this; you say 迎え火を焚く. The counterpart on the last evening is 送り火. The word comes from 迎える \"to welcome\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"送り火で先祖を送ります。","romaji":"Okuribi de senzo o okurimasu.","german":"We send off our ancestors with the send-off fire.","furigana":"おく|び|せんぞ|おく"},{"japanese":"京都では、八月十六日に五山の送り火が行われます。","romaji":"Kyouto de wa, hachigatsu juurokunichi ni gozan no okuribi ga okonawaremasu.","german":"In Kyoto, the Gozan Okuribi is held on August 16.","furigana":"きょうと|はちがつじゅうろくにち|ござん|おく|び|おこな"},{"japanese":"送り火を焚いて、お盆が終わりました。","romaji":"Okuribi o taite, obon ga owarimashita.","german":"Once the send-off fire was lit, Obon was over.","furigana":"おく|び|た|ぼん|お"}],"notes":"送り火 is the fire on the last evening of お盆 with which the souls of the ancestors are sent back to the other world (送る \"to see off\"); it is the counterpart of 迎え火. Typical use: 送り火を焚く. The best known is the 五山送り火 in 京都 on August 16, in which huge characters such as 大 burn on the mountainsides (popularly also called 大文字焼き).","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"金魚すくいに挑戦します。","romaji":"Kingyo sukui ni chōsen shimasu.","german":"I'm going to try goldfish scooping.","furigana":"きんぎょ|ちょうせん"},{"japanese":"夏祭りで金魚すくいをして、三匹も取れました。","romaji":"Natsumatsuri de kingyo sukui o shite, sanbiki mo toremashita.","german":"At the summer festival, I played goldfish scooping and caught as many as three.","furigana":"なつまつ|きんぎょ||と"},{"japanese":"金魚すくいのポイがすぐに破れてしまった。","romaji":"Kingyo sukui no poi ga sugu ni yaburete shimatta.","german":"The paper scoop for goldfish scooping tore right away.","furigana":"きんぎょ|やぶ"}],"notes":"金魚すくい is the classic stall game at summer festivals (夏祭り, 縁日): you try to scoop goldfish out of a shallow tank into a small bowl with a thin paper scoop (ポイ). Typical expressions are 金魚すくいをする and 金魚すくいに挑戦する; you can usually take the fish you caught home in a plastic bag. Besides the kana spelling すくい, 金魚掬い is also seen, but in daily life 金魚すくい is usual.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"射的で景品を狙います。","romaji":"Shateki de keihin o neraimasu.","german":"I aim for the prizes at the shooting gallery.","furigana":"しゃてき|けいひん|ねら"},{"japanese":"子どもの頃、お祭りでよく射的をした。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, omatsuri de yoku shateki o shita.","german":"As a child, I often played the shooting gallery at festivals.","furigana":"こ|ころ|まつ|しゃてき"},{"japanese":"射的でお菓子の箱を落として、もらいました。","romaji":"Shateki de okashi no hako o otoshite, moraimashita.","german":"I knocked down a box of sweets at the shooting gallery and got to keep it.","furigana":"しゃてき|かし|はこ|お"}],"notes":"射的 now mainly refers to the shooting gallery at festivals (縁日, 夏祭り): you shoot with a cork gun (コルク銃) at small prizes (景品) and get to keep what falls down. You say 射的をする or 射的で景品を狙う/取る. The general meaning \"target shooting, shooting practice\" is rare; for shooting as a sport or in the military, 射撃 is used.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"綿飴を買いました。","romaji":"Wataame o kaimashita.","german":"I bought cotton candy.","furigana":"わたあめ|か"},{"japanese":"お祭りで子どもが大きな綿飴を持って歩いていた。","romaji":"Omatsuri de kodomo ga ookina wataame o motte aruite ita.","german":"At the festival, a child was walking around holding a huge cotton candy.","furigana":"まつ|こ|おお|わたあめ|も|ある"},{"japanese":"綿飴は口に入れると、すぐに溶けてしまう。","romaji":"Wataame wa kuchi ni ireru to, sugu ni tokete shimau.","german":"Cotton candy melts right away when you put it in your mouth.","furigana":"わたあめ|くち|い|と"}],"notes":"綿飴 is cotton candy, as bought at festival stalls (屋台). The word is mostly written in kana (わたあめ); synonymous is 綿菓子 (わたがし), and depending on region and person, one or the other is more common. The name is made up of 綿 \"cotton, fluff\" and 飴 \"candy, sweet\", because of its cotton-like shape.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"屋台の焼きそばが美味しい。","romaji":"Yatai no yakisoba ga oishii.","german":"The yakisoba from the food stall is delicious.","furigana":"やたい|や|おい"},{"japanese":"バーベキューの最後に、鉄板で焼きそばを作った。","romaji":"Baabekyuu no saigo ni, teppan de yakisoba o tsukutta.","german":"At the end of the barbecue, we made yakisoba on the iron griddle.","furigana":"さいご|てっぱん|や|つく"},{"japanese":"忙しい日の昼ご飯は、カップ焼きそばで済ませます。","romaji":"Isogashii hi no hirugohan wa, kappu yakisoba de sumasemasu.","german":"On busy days, I get by with instant yakisoba for lunch.","furigana":"いそが|ひ|ひる|はん|や|す"}],"notes":"焼きそば are fried wheat noodles with vegetables and meat, usually with a sweet-savory sauce (ソース焼きそば). Despite そば in the name, they have nothing to do with buckwheat noodles; そば here stands generally for Chinese noodles, as in 中華そば. They are typical at festival stalls (屋台) and barbecues; one says 焼きそばを作る or 焼く. There are also 塩焼きそば and the instant version カップ焼きそば.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"たこ焼きを焼きます。","romaji":"Takoyaki o yakimasu.","german":"I'm making takoyaki.","furigana":"や|や"},{"japanese":"大阪に行ったら、本場のたこ焼きを食べたい。","romaji":"Oosaka ni ittara, honba no takoyaki o tabetai.","german":"When I go to Osaka, I want to eat authentic takoyaki.","furigana":"おおさか|い|ほんば|や|た"},{"japanese":"週末、友達の家でたこ焼きパーティーをした。","romaji":"Shuumatsu, tomodachi no ie de takoyaki paatii o shita.","german":"On the weekend, we had a takoyaki party at a friend's house.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|ともだち|いえ|や"}],"notes":"たこ焼き are batter balls with pieces of octopus (たこ), baked in a special pan with half-round hollows (たこ焼き器) and served with sauce, mayonnaise, 青のり and かつお節. They are considered a specialty of Osaka. One says たこ焼きを焼く or たこ焼きを作る; a party where people make them together is colloquially called たこパ. たこ is almost always written in kana, rarely 蛸焼き.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"かき氷を食べます。","romaji":"Kakigōri o tabemasu.","german":"I'm eating shaved ice.","furigana":"ごおり|た"},{"japanese":"暑かったので、いちご味のかき氷を頼んだ。","romaji":"Atsukatta node, ichigo aji no kakigoori o tanonda.","german":"It was hot, so I ordered strawberry-flavored shaved ice.","furigana":"あつ|あじ|ごおり|たの"},{"japanese":"かき氷を急いで食べたら、頭がキーンとした。","romaji":"Kakigoori o isoide tabetara, atama ga kiin to shita.","german":"When I ate the shaved ice in a hurry, I got a brain freeze.","furigana":"ごおり|いそ|た|あたま"}],"notes":"かき氷 is finely shaved ice over which sweet syrup is poured, such as strawberry (いちご), melon, or matcha with sweet beans (宇治金時). It is a typical summer snack at festival stalls and in cafes; a little flag with the character 氷 shows that it is sold. The word comes from かく \"to scrape\" and 氷, where こおり becomes ごおり in the compound. If you eat too fast, you get a brain freeze: 頭がキーンとする.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"白玉を作ります。","romaji":"Shiratama o tsukurimasu.","german":"I make shiratama.","furigana":"しらたま|つく"},{"japanese":"白玉を丸めて、お湯に入れてください。","romaji":"Shiratama o marumete, oyu ni irete kudasai.","german":"Roll the dough into balls and put them in hot water.","furigana":"しらたま|まる|ゆ|い"},{"japanese":"抹茶アイスに白玉をのせると、とても美味しい。","romaji":"Matcha aisu ni shiratama o noseru to, totemo oishii.","german":"Shiratama on top of matcha ice cream tastes great.","furigana":"まっちゃ|しらたま|おい"}],"notes":"白玉 are small, soft dumplings made of glutinous rice flour (白玉粉), which is kneaded with water, rolled into balls and boiled. They are eaten in sweets such as あんみつ or ぜんざい, with sweet bean paste or on ice cream; you say 白玉を作る or 白玉を丸める. Unlike だんご, which are often sold on a skewer, 白玉 are usually loose. The basic meaning \"white gem, pearl\" is literary and hardly used in everyday speech.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"寒天でゼリーを作ります。","romaji":"Kanten de zerī o tsukurimasu.","german":"I make jelly with agar.","furigana":"かんてん|つく"},{"japanese":"寒天はカロリーが低いので、ダイエット中によく食べます。","romaji":"Kanten wa karorii ga hikui node, daietto chuu ni yoku tabemasu.","german":"Agar is low in calories, so I often eat it when I'm on a diet.","furigana":"かんてん|ひく|ちゅう|た"},{"japanese":"果物のジュースを寒天で固めて、デザートにした。","romaji":"Kudamono no juusu o kanten de katamete, dezaato ni shita.","german":"I set fruit juice with agar and made a dessert.","furigana":"くだもの|かんてん|かた"}],"notes":"寒天 is a gelling agent made from red algae (agar-agar), used in Japan for sweets such as ようかん, あんみつ or fruit jelly. Unlike ゼラチン, it is plant-based, sets firmer and does not melt at room temperature. Typical are 寒天で固める \"to set with agar\" and 粉寒天 \"agar powder\". The literal meaning \"cold winter weather\" is literary and rare.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"焙じ茶は香ばしい。","romaji":"Hōjicha wa kōbashii.","german":"Hojicha has a roasted aroma.","furigana":"ほう|ちゃ|こう"},{"japanese":"夜はカフェインが少ない焙じ茶を飲むようにしています。","romaji":"Yoru wa kafein ga sukunai houjicha o nomu you ni shite imasu.","german":"In the evening, I make a point of drinking hojicha because it is low in caffeine.","furigana":"よる|すく|ほう|ちゃ|の"},{"japanese":"このカフェのほうじ茶ラテはとても人気がある。","romaji":"Kono kafe no houjicha rate wa totemo ninki ga aru.","german":"The hojicha latte at this cafe is very popular.","furigana":"ちゃ|にんき"}],"notes":"焙じ茶 is green tea (usually 番茶 or 煎茶) that has been roasted at high heat. This makes it brown, gives it a nutty, roasted fragrance (香ばしい) and leaves it with little caffeine and bitterness, which is why it is gladly drunk in the evening or given to children. Instead of the difficult character 焙, it is mostly written ほうじ茶; the related verb is 焙じる \"to roast\". In cafes, ほうじ茶ラテ is popular.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"吊り橋を渡りました。","romaji":"Tsuribashi o watarimashita.","german":"I crossed the suspension bridge.","furigana":"つ|ばし|わた"},{"japanese":"この吊り橋は風が吹くと大きく揺れる。","romaji":"Kono tsuribashi wa kaze ga fuku to ookiku yureru.","german":"This suspension bridge sways a lot when the wind blows.","furigana":"つ|ばし|かぜ|ふ|おお|ゆ"},{"japanese":"高い所が苦手なので、吊り橋の上で足が震えた。","romaji":"Takai tokoro ga nigate na node, tsuribashi no ue de ashi ga furueta.","german":"I'm bad with heights, so my legs shook on the suspension bridge.","furigana":"たか|ところ|にがて|つ|ばし|うえ|あし|ふる"}],"notes":"吊り橋 is a suspension bridge, from narrow rope bridges over gorges to large structures across straits. You say 吊り橋を渡る; that it sways is expressed with 揺れる. Other spellings are 吊橋 and つり橋. Also well known is the 吊り橋効果: a pounding heart from fear is easily interpreted as being in love.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"断崖から海が見える。","romaji":"Dangai kara umi ga mieru.","german":"You can see the sea from the cliff.","furigana":"だんがい|うみ|み"},{"japanese":"海沿いには高い断崖が何キロも続いている。","romaji":"Umizoi ni wa takai dangai ga nan kiro mo tsuzuite iru.","german":"Along the coast, high cliffs continue for many kilometers.","furigana":"うみぞ|たか|だんがい|なん|つづ"},{"japanese":"断崖の近くは危ないので、柵の外に出ないでください。","romaji":"Dangai no chikaku wa abunai node, saku no soto ni denaide kudasai.","german":"It is dangerous near the cliff, so please do not go beyond the fence.","furigana":"だんがい|ちか|あぶ|さく|そと|で"}],"notes":"断崖 is a vertically dropping rock face, especially a sea cliff. The word is rather literary and appears in travel guides, news and crime novels; in everyday speech, simply 崖 (がけ) is usually used. A fixed combination is 断崖絶壁 \"sheer, vertical cliff face\". For a hopeless situation in the figurative sense, 崖っぷち is more often used.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鳥取砂丘は有名です。","romaji":"Tottori sakyū wa yūmei desu.","german":"The Tottori Sand Dunes are famous.","furigana":"|ゆうめい"},{"japanese":"砂丘の上まで登ると、日本海が見えた。","romaji":"Sakyuu no ue made noboru to, Nihonkai ga mieta.","german":"When I climbed to the top of the dune, I could see the Sea of Japan.","furigana":"さきゅう|うえ|のぼ|にほんかい|み"},{"japanese":"砂丘は風によって少しずつ形が変わる。","romaji":"Sakyuu wa kaze ni yotte sukoshi zutsu katachi ga kawaru.","german":"Dunes gradually change shape because of the wind.","furigana":"さきゅう|かぜ|すこ|かたち|か"}],"notes":"砂丘 is a sand dune piled up by the wind, on coasts as well as in deserts. In Japan, one immediately thinks of the 鳥取砂丘, where you can ride camels and go sandboarding. Unlike 砂漠 \"desert\", 砂丘 refers only to the dune or dune area; a sandy beach, by contrast, is 砂浜.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"硫黄の匂いがする。","romaji":"Iō no nioi ga suru.","german":"It smells of sulfur.","furigana":"いおう|にお"},{"japanese":"この温泉は硫黄を多く含んでいて、お湯が白く濁っている。","romaji":"Kono onsen wa iou o ooku fukunde ite, oyu ga shiroku nigotte iru.","german":"This hot spring contains a lot of sulfur, so the water is cloudy white.","furigana":"おんせん|いおう|おお|ふく|ゆ|しろ|にご"},{"japanese":"火山の近くでは、黄色い硫黄が地面に見える。","romaji":"Kazan no chikaku de wa, kiiroi iou ga jimen ni mieru.","german":"Near the volcano, you can see yellow sulfur on the ground.","furigana":"かざん|ちか|きいろ|いおう|じめん|み"}],"notes":"硫黄 is the chemical element sulfur. In daily life it is encountered mainly in volcanic and onsen areas: 硫黄の匂い（におい）がする \"it smells of sulfur\" (strictly speaking of hydrogen sulfide, 硫化水素), and 硫黄泉 is a sulfur spring. The reading いおう is an irregular reading of the whole word and cannot be derived from the individual characters.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"源泉掛け流しの温泉です。","romaji":"Gensen kakenagashi no onsen desu.","german":"This is an onsen with source water flowing freely through the bath.","furigana":"げんせんか|なが|おんせん"},{"japanese":"ここの源泉は九十度もあるので、水で冷ましている。","romaji":"Koko no gensen wa kyuujuudo mo aru node, mizu de samashite iru.","german":"The source water here is as hot as 90 degrees, so it is cooled with water.","furigana":"げんせん|きゅうじゅうど|みず|さ"},{"japanese":"給料から税金が源泉徴収されている。","romaji":"Kyuuryou kara zeikin ga gensen choushuu sarete iru.","german":"Taxes are withheld at source from my salary.","furigana":"きゅうりょう|ぜいきん|げんせんちょうしゅう"}],"notes":"源泉 is the origin of a spring, in daily life above all the point where a hot spring emerges. On onsen signs you read 源泉掛け流し: the source water flows fresh into the bath and runs off without being circulated or treated; 源泉の温度 is the temperature at the source. Figuratively, 源泉 stands for a source of power or knowledge, which is more literary. Very common in daily life is 源泉徴収 \"withholding at source (of income tax)\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"脱衣所で服を脱ぎます。","romaji":"Datsuijo de fuku o nugimasu.","german":"I take off my clothes in the changing room.","furigana":"だついじょ|ふく|ぬ"},{"japanese":"冬は脱衣所が寒いので、ヒーターを置いています。","romaji":"Fuyu wa datsuijo ga samui node, hiitaa o oite imasu.","german":"In winter the changing room is cold, so I keep a heater there.","furigana":"ふゆ|だついじょ|さむ|お"},{"japanese":"温泉の脱衣所には、鍵付きのロッカーがあります。","romaji":"Onsen no datsuijo ni wa, kagitsuki no rokkaa ga arimasu.","german":"The onsen changing room has lockers with keys.","furigana":"おんせん|だついじょ|かぎつ"}],"notes":"脱衣所 is the room for undressing and dressing in front of the bath: in a private house, the anteroom of the bathroom, often with a sink and washing machine; at a 銭湯, 温泉 or swimming pool, the changing room. One says 脱衣所で服を脱ぐ. The fitting room in a shop, on the other hand, is called 試着室, and the changing room in gyms or companies usually 更衣室. Synonymous is 脱衣場.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"番台でお金を払います。","romaji":"Bandai de okane o haraimasu.","german":"I pay at the bathhouse counter.","furigana":"ばんだい|かね|はら"},{"japanese":"番台に座っているおばあさんが、笑顔で迎えてくれた。","romaji":"Bandai ni suwatte iru obaasan ga, egao de mukaete kureta.","german":"The old woman sitting at the attendant's seat welcomed us with a smile.","furigana":"ばんだい|すわ|えがお|むか"},{"japanese":"最近の銭湯は、番台ではなくフロントで受付をする所が多い。","romaji":"Saikin no sentou wa, bandai de wa naku furonto de uketsuke o suru tokoro ga ooi.","german":"In today's bathhouses, many places have you check in at a front desk instead of an attendant's seat.","furigana":"さいきん|せんとう|ばんだい|うけつけ|ところ|おお"}],"notes":"番台 is the raised seat of the attendant in a traditional public bath (銭湯), usually at the entrance between the men's and women's changing rooms. From there, the attendant collects the admission fee and keeps an eye on the changing rooms; one says 番台に座る or 番台で料金を払う. Many 銭湯 today have a reception desk (フロント) in the entrance area instead, so 番台 sounds nostalgic.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"桜吹雪が舞っています。","romaji":"Sakura fubuki ga matte imasu.","german":"Cherry blossom petals are swirling.","furigana":"さくらふぶき|ま"},{"japanese":"桜吹雪の中、新入生たちが学校に入っていった。","romaji":"Sakura fubuki no naka, shinnyuusei-tachi ga gakkou ni haitte itta.","german":"Amid the falling cherry petals, the new students walked into the school.","furigana":"さくらふぶき|なか|しんにゅうせい|がっこう|はい"},{"japanese":"桜吹雪がきれいなうちに、写真を撮りに行こう。","romaji":"Sakura fubuki ga kirei na uchi ni, shashin o tori ni ikou.","german":"Let's go take photos while the falling cherry petals are still beautiful.","furigana":"さくらふぶき|しゃしん|と|い"}],"notes":"桜吹雪 describes cherry blossom petals swirling down in large numbers in the wind, as if it were snowing (吹雪 \"snow flurry\"). You say 桜吹雪が舞う or 桜吹雪の中を歩く; the image stands for the end of the cherry blossom season and is popular in songs, poems and advertising. 花吹雪 is almost synonymous, since 花 in such expressions usually means cherry blossoms.","levelNote":"Compound: 桜 (N5) + 吹雪 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"花吹雪が美しい。","romaji":"Hana fubuki ga utsukushii.","german":"The falling blossoms are beautiful.","furigana":"はなふぶき|うつく"},{"japanese":"公園を歩いていると、急に風が吹いて花吹雪になった。","romaji":"Kouen o aruite iru to, kyuu ni kaze ga fuite hana fubuki ni natta.","german":"While I was walking through the park, a sudden wind blew and the petals swirled like snow.","furigana":"こうえん|ある|きゅう|かぜ|ふ|はなふぶき"},{"japanese":"花吹雪の中で、二人は記念写真を撮った。","romaji":"Hana fubuki no naka de, futari wa kinen shashin o totta.","german":"The two took a commemorative photo amid the falling blossoms.","furigana":"はなふぶき|なか|ふたり|きねんしゃしん|と"}],"notes":"花吹雪 refers to flower petals swirling through the air like snowflakes; this almost always means cherry blossoms, so it is largely synonymous with 桜吹雪. Typical uses are 花吹雪が舞う and 花吹雪の中で. The word sounds poetic and is a spring word in haiku. Swirling paper confetti, by contrast, is called 紙吹雪.","levelNote":"Compound: 花 (N5) + 吹雪 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新緑が眩しい。","romaji":"Shinryoku ga mabushii.","german":"The fresh greenery is dazzling.","furigana":"しんりょく|まぶ"},{"japanese":"五月は新緑の季節で、山歩きにちょうどいい。","romaji":"Gogatsu wa shinryoku no kisetsu de, yamaaruki ni choudo ii.","german":"May is the season of fresh greenery and perfect for hiking.","furigana":"ごがつ|しんりょく|きせつ|やまある"},{"japanese":"新緑の中を自転車で走るのは気持ちがいい。","romaji":"Shinryoku no naka o jitensha de hashiru no wa kimochi ga ii.","german":"It feels great to cycle through the fresh greenery.","furigana":"しんりょく|なか|じてんしゃ|はし|きも"}],"notes":"新緑 is the fresh, light-green foliage of trees in early summer, roughly from the end of April through May. It is a typical seasonal word in letters, travel brochures and weather reports: 新緑の季節, 新緑が美しい, 新緑がまぶしい. In autumn, 紅葉 is spoken of instead. 新緑 sounds somewhat more elevated than 若葉 \"young leaves\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"落葉を掃きます。","romaji":"Ochiba o hakimasu.","german":"I sweep up the fallen leaves.","furigana":"おちば|は"},{"japanese":"公園の道に落葉がたくさん積もっている。","romaji":"Kouen no michi ni ochiba ga takusan tsumotte iru.","german":"Fallen leaves have piled up on the park path.","furigana":"こうえん|みち|おちば|つ"},{"japanese":"子どもたちは落葉を集めて遊んでいた。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa ochiba o atsumete asonde ita.","german":"The children were playing, gathering fallen leaves.","furigana":"こ|おちば|あつ|あそ"}],"notes":"落葉 (おちば) are leaves that have fallen, i.e. leaf litter. In everyday life it is mostly written 落ち葉; typical are 落ち葉を掃く, 落ち葉を拾う and 落ち葉が積もる. The spelling 落葉 is also read らくよう and then means \"leaf shedding\", for example in 落葉樹 \"deciduous tree\". The colorful autumn foliage on the trees, by contrast, is called 紅葉.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"入道雲が発達している。","romaji":"Nyūdō gumo ga hattatsu shite iru.","german":"Thunderheads are building up.","furigana":"にゅうどうぐも|はったつ"},{"japanese":"夏の青い空に、大きな入道雲が出ている。","romaji":"Natsu no aoi sora ni, ookina nyuudou gumo ga dete iru.","german":"A huge thunderhead has risen in the blue summer sky.","furigana":"なつ|あお|そら|おお|にゅうどうぐも|で"},{"japanese":"入道雲が見えるから、夕方は雷雨になるかもしれない。","romaji":"Nyuudou gumo ga mieru kara, yuugata wa raiu ni naru kamo shirenai.","german":"I can see a thunderhead, so there may be a thunderstorm in the evening.","furigana":"にゅうどうぐも|み|ゆうがた|らいう"}],"notes":"入道雲 is a towering thunderhead (cumulonimbus), such as forms on hot summer afternoons. The name compares the cloud to a bald-headed giant (入道). You say 入道雲が出る or 入道雲が湧く; it often heralds thunder (雷) and sudden downpours (夕立). The technical term is 積乱雲; 入道雲 is the everyday word and a typical summer image.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"氷柱が軒から垂れている。","romaji":"Tsurara ga noki kara tarete iru.","german":"Icicles hang from the eaves.","furigana":"つらら|のき|た"},{"japanese":"今朝はとても寒くて、屋根に長い氷柱ができていた。","romaji":"Kesa wa totemo samukute, yane ni nagai tsurara ga dekite ita.","german":"It was so cold this morning that long icicles had formed on the roof.","furigana":"けさ|さむ|やね|なが|つらら"},{"japanese":"氷柱が落ちてくると危ないので、下を歩かないでください。","romaji":"Tsurara ga ochite kuru to abunai node, shita o arukanaide kudasai.","german":"Falling icicles are dangerous, so please do not walk underneath.","furigana":"つらら|お|あぶ|した|ある"}],"notes":"氷柱 (つらら) is an icicle, such as hangs from the edge of the roof (軒) in winter. The word is mostly written in kana; typical combinations are つららが下がる, つららが垂れる and つららができる. The spelling 氷柱 can also be read ひょうちゅう and then refers to an ice column, for example a large block of ice for cooling in summer.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"霞がかかっている。","romaji":"Kasumi ga kakatte iru.","german":"There is haze in the air.","furigana":"かすみ"},{"japanese":"霞の向こうに、遠くの山がうっすらと見える。","romaji":"Kasumi no mukou ni, tooku no yama ga ussura to mieru.","german":"Beyond the haze, you can faintly see distant mountains.","furigana":"かすみ|む|とお|やま|み"},{"japanese":"窓から見える町は、霞に包まれていた。","romaji":"Mado kara mieru machi wa, kasumi ni tsutsumarete ita.","german":"The town seen from the window was wrapped in haze.","furigana":"まど|み|まち|かすみ|つつ"}],"notes":"霞 is the milky haze that lies over the landscape especially in spring and makes the distance blur; one says 霞がかかる or 霞に包まれる. It is a classic spring word, while dense fog, especially in autumn, is called 霧. Related is the verb 霞む \"to blur, to become hazy\", also for the eyes: 目が霞む. The word is often written in kana and is also a given name.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"朝露が光っている。","romaji":"Asatsuyu ga hikatte iru.","german":"The morning dew glitters.","furigana":"あさつゆ|ひか"},{"japanese":"夜の間に露が降りて、車の窓がぬれていた。","romaji":"Yoru no aida ni tsuyu ga orite, kuruma no mado ga nurete ita.","german":"Dew had settled during the night, and the car windows were wet.","furigana":"よる|あいだ|つゆ|お|くるま|まど"},{"japanese":"朝早く庭に出ると、葉っぱに露がついていた。","romaji":"Asa hayaku niwa ni deru to, happa ni tsuyu ga tsuite ita.","german":"When I went out into the garden early in the morning, there was dew on the leaves.","furigana":"あさはや|にわ|で|は|つゆ"}],"notes":"露 is dew, the water droplets that form at night on grass and leaves; one says 露が降りる \"dew falls\" and 葉に露がつく. Common is the compound 朝露 \"morning dew\". Not to be confused with the identically read 梅雨 (つゆ) \"rainy season\". Literarily, 露 also stands for tears or transience, and adverbially つゆ…ない means \"not in the least\", as in つゆ知らず \"without the faintest idea\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"山に木霊が響く。","romaji":"Yama ni kodama ga hibiku.","german":"The echo resounds in the mountains.","furigana":"やま|こだま|ひび"},{"japanese":"山頂で「ヤッホー」と叫ぶと、木霊が返ってきた。","romaji":"Sanchou de yahhoo to sakebu to, kodama ga kaette kita.","german":"When I shouted \"yahoo\" on the summit, an echo came back.","furigana":"さんちょう|さけ|こだま|かえ"},{"japanese":"子どもたちの笑い声が谷に木霊した。","romaji":"Kodomotachi no waraigoe ga tani ni kodama shita.","german":"The children's laughter echoed through the valley.","furigana":"こ|わら|ごえ|たに|こだま"}],"notes":"木霊 is an echo, especially in mountains and valleys; originally people believed a tree spirit (木の霊) was calling back. You say 木霊が返ってくる or, as a する-verb, 声が谷に木霊する \"the voice echoes in the valley\". It is mostly written こだま in kana; 山彦 (やまびこ) is synonymous, whereas エコー stands more for technical reverb. こだま is also the name of the Shinkansen that stops at every station.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鈴虫の音色が秋を感じさせる。","romaji":"Suzumushi no neiro ga aki o kanjisaseru.","german":"The sound of the bell crickets makes you feel autumn.","furigana":"すずむし|ねいろ|あき|かん"},{"japanese":"夜になると、庭で鈴虫が鳴き始めた。","romaji":"Yoru ni naru to, niwa de suzumushi ga nakihajimeta.","german":"When night came, bell crickets began to chirp in the garden.","furigana":"よる|にわ|すずむし|な|はじ"},{"japanese":"息子は夏休みに鈴虫を飼っている。","romaji":"Musuko wa natsuyasumi ni suzumushi o katte iru.","german":"My son keeps bell crickets during summer vacation.","furigana":"むすこ|なつやす|すずむし|か"}],"notes":"鈴虫 is a species of cricket whose chirping is reminiscent of a fine little bell (鈴) and is usually rendered as \"リーンリーン\". Its song is considered a typical sound of autumn; some people keep the insects at home in a container in summer. You say 鈴虫が鳴く, and the sound is called 鈴虫の声 or 鈴虫の音色. Other well-known autumn singers are コオロギ and マツムシ, which chirp differently.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"蟷螂を見つけました。","romaji":"Kamakiri o mitsukemashita.","german":"I found a praying mantis.","furigana":"かまきり|み"},{"japanese":"蟷螂は前足で小さな虫を捕まえて食べる。","romaji":"Kamakiri wa maeashi de chiisana mushi o tsukamaete taberu.","german":"The praying mantis catches small insects with its forelegs and eats them.","furigana":"かまきり|まえあし|ちい|むし|つか|た"},{"japanese":"弟は公園で捕まえたかまきりを虫かごに入れた。","romaji":"Otouto wa kouen de tsukamaeta kamakiri o mushikago ni ireta.","german":"My little brother put the praying mantis he caught in the park into a bug cage.","furigana":"おとうと|こうえん|つか|むし|い"}],"notes":"蟷螂 is the praying mantis. The difficult kanji are hardly seen in daily life; it is almost always written カマキリ or かまきり. The name is usually interpreted as 鎌切り \"sickle cutter\", because of the sickle-shaped catching legs (鎌). Typical uses are カマキリを捕まえる and カマキリが虫を食べる; it is a popular insect for children to catch.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"瓢箪を水筒にします。","romaji":"Hyōtan o suitō ni shimasu.","german":"I use a calabash as a water bottle.","furigana":"ひょうたん|すいとう"},{"japanese":"庭で育てた瓢箪が、面白い形になった。","romaji":"Niwa de sodateta hyoutan ga, omoshiroi katachi ni natta.","german":"The calabash we grew in the garden took on a funny shape.","furigana":"にわ|そだ|ひょうたん|おもしろ|かたち"},{"japanese":"冗談で言ったことが本当になるなんて、まさに瓢箪から駒だ。","romaji":"Joudan de itta koto ga hontou ni naru nante, masa ni hyoutan kara koma da.","german":"Something I said as a joke coming true - that's truly a horse out of a gourd.","furigana":"じょうだん|い|ほんとう|ひょうたん|こま"}],"notes":"瓢箪 is the bottle gourd with its typical pinched shape, and also the dried vessel made from it, which used to serve as a water or sake bottle. It is mostly written ひょうたん in kana. Well known is the idiom 瓢箪から駒（が出る） \"a horse comes out of the gourd\": something said in jest unexpectedly becomes reality. A pond in this shape is called 瓢箪池.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"朝顔を育てています。","romaji":"Asagao o sodatete imasu.","german":"I am growing morning glories.","furigana":"あさがお|そだ"},{"japanese":"朝早く起きたら、ベランダの朝顔が咲いていた。","romaji":"Asa hayaku okitara, beranda no asagao ga saite ita.","german":"When I got up early, the morning glories on the balcony were in bloom.","furigana":"あさはや|お|あさがお|さ"},{"japanese":"小学生のとき、夏休みに朝顔の観察日記を書いた。","romaji":"Shougakusei no toki, natsuyasumi ni asagao no kansatsu nikki o kaita.","german":"In elementary school, I kept an observation diary of morning glories during summer vacation.","furigana":"しょうがくせい|なつやす|あさがお|かんさつにっき|か"}],"notes":"朝顔 is the Japanese morning glory, a climbing plant whose funnel-shaped flowers open early in the morning and wilt by noon; hence the name \"morning face\". It is the classic summer plant: elementary school pupils often grow it as a school project and keep an observation diary (観察日記). Typical uses are 朝顔が咲く, 朝顔を育てる and 朝顔のつる \"vine\". Summer markets for morning glories (朝顔市) are also well known.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"向日葵が太陽に向いている。","romaji":"Himawari ga taiyō ni muite iru.","german":"The sunflower faces the sun.","furigana":"ひまわり|たいよう|む"},{"japanese":"夏休みに、一面のひまわり畑を見に行った。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi ni, ichimen no himawari batake o mi ni itta.","german":"During the summer vacation, we went to see a field full of sunflowers.","furigana":"なつやす|いちめん|ばたけ|み|い"},{"japanese":"彼女はひまわりのような明るい笑顔の人だ。","romaji":"Kanojo wa himawari no you na akarui egao no hito da.","german":"She is a person with a bright smile like a sunflower.","furigana":"かのじょ|あか|えがお|ひと"}],"notes":"向日葵 is the sunflower; the kanji literally mean \"mallow that turns toward the sun\", and the reading ひまわり applies only to the whole word. In daily life it is mostly written in kana as ひまわり. It is a symbol of summer and cheerfulness; a radiant smile is called ひまわりのような笑顔. Typical expressions are ひまわりが咲く, ひまわり畑 \"sunflower field\" and ひまわりの種 \"sunflower seeds\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"萩が秋風に揺れている。","romaji":"Hagi ga akikaze ni yurete iru.","german":"The bush clover sways in the autumn wind.","furigana":"はぎ|あきかぜ|ゆ"},{"japanese":"お寺の庭に萩の花がたくさん咲いていた。","romaji":"Otera no niwa ni hagi no hana ga takusan saite ita.","german":"Lots of bush clover flowers were blooming in the temple garden.","furigana":"てら|にわ|はぎ|はな|さ"},{"japanese":"萩は秋の七草の一つです。","romaji":"Hagi wa aki no nanakusa no hitotsu desu.","german":"Bush clover is one of the seven flowers of autumn.","furigana":"はぎ|あき|ななくさ|ひと"}],"notes":"萩 is bush clover, a shrub with small pink to purple pea-like flowers on long, arching branches that blooms in late summer and autumn. It is one of the seven plants of autumn (秋の七草) and appears often in classical poetry. The sweet おはぎ is named after it because the bean paste is reminiscent of its blossoms. 萩 is also the name of a city in Yamaguchi.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"芒の原が広がっている。","romaji":"Susuki no hara ga hirogatte iru.","german":"A field of silvergrass stretches out.","furigana":"すすき|はら|ひろ"},{"japanese":"お月見の日に、芒と団子を飾りました。","romaji":"Otsukimi no hi ni, susuki to dango o kazarimashita.","german":"On moon-viewing day, we displayed silvergrass and dumplings.","furigana":"つきみ|ひ|すすき|だんご|かざ"},{"japanese":"秋の夕日に、芒の穂が銀色に光っていた。","romaji":"Aki no yuuhi ni, susuki no ho ga gin'iro ni hikatte ita.","german":"In the autumn evening sun, the silvergrass plumes shone silver.","furigana":"あき|ゆうひ|すすき|ほ|ぎんいろ|ひか"}],"notes":"芒 is silvergrass (Miscanthus), a tall grass with silvery-white, feathery plumes that covers whole slopes and fields in autumn. It is one of the seven autumn flowers (秋の七草) and is displayed together with rice dumplings (月見団子) at the moon-viewing festival (お月見). It is almost always written すすき, more rarely 薄; typical uses are すすきが揺れる and すすきの穂. South American pampas grass, by contrast, is called パンパスグラス.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"松茸は高級食材です。","romaji":"Matsutake wa kōkyū shokuzai desu.","german":"Matsutake is a luxury ingredient.","furigana":"まつたけ|こうきゅうしょくざい"},{"japanese":"秋になると、母が松茸ご飯を作ってくれる。","romaji":"Aki ni naru to, haha ga matsutake gohan o tsukutte kureru.","german":"When autumn comes, my mother makes matsutake rice for me.","furigana":"あき|はは|まつたけ|はん|つく"},{"japanese":"国産の松茸は高すぎて、なかなか買えない。","romaji":"Kokusan no matsutake wa takasugite, nakanaka kaenai.","german":"Domestic matsutake is too expensive, and I can hardly ever buy it.","furigana":"こくさん|まつたけ|たか|か"}],"notes":"松茸 is a strongly fragrant edible mushroom that grows in pine forests (松) in autumn and can hardly be cultivated. For that reason it is regarded as an expensive delicacy and the epitome of the taste of autumn (秋の味覚). Typical dishes are 松茸ご飯 and 土瓶蒸し; people rave about the 松茸の香り. Domestic mushrooms are especially expensive, so imported ones are often bought.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"山葵を添えます。","romaji":"Wasabi o soemasu.","german":"I add wasabi on the side.","furigana":"わさび|そ"},{"japanese":"山葵をつけすぎて、鼻がツーンとした。","romaji":"Wasabi o tsukesugite, hana ga tsuun to shita.","german":"I used too much wasabi and it stung my nose.","furigana":"わさび|はな"},{"japanese":"子ども用のお寿司は、わさび抜きでお願いします。","romaji":"Kodomo you no osushi wa, wasabi nuki de onegai shimasu.","german":"Please make the sushi for the children without wasabi.","furigana":"こ|よう|すし|ぬ|ねが"}],"notes":"山葵 is wasabi, the hot Japanese horseradish, whose rhizome is served freshly grated (すりおろす) with sushi and sashimi. It is mostly written わさび or ワサビ; the kanji are rare. The paste from a tube often consists largely of ordinary horseradish. The sharp pungency is described with 鼻にツーンとくる; \"without wasabi\" is called わさび抜き or simply サビ抜き for sushi.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"柚子の香りがいい。","romaji":"Yuzu no kaori ga ii.","german":"Yuzu smells good.","furigana":"ゆず|かお"},{"japanese":"冬至の日には、柚子湯に入る習慣がある。","romaji":"Touji no hi ni wa, yuzuyu ni hairu shuukan ga aru.","german":"On the winter solstice, it is customary to take a yuzu bath.","furigana":"とうじ|ひ|ゆずゆ|はい|しゅうかん"},{"japanese":"鍋に柚子の皮を少し入れると、香りがよくなる。","romaji":"Nabe ni yuzu no kawa o sukoshi ireru to, kaori ga yoku naru.","german":"If you add a little yuzu peel to the hot pot, it smells better.","furigana":"なべ|ゆず|かわ|すこ|い|かお"}],"notes":"柚子 is an aromatic, very sour citrus fruit. It is hardly eaten on its own but is used mainly as a seasoning with its peel and juice, for example in ゆずポン酢 or as 柚子胡椒, a spicy paste of yuzu peel and chili. On the day of the winter solstice (冬至), people traditionally take a 柚子湯, a bath with floating yuzu. ゆず is usually written in kana.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"生姜をすりおろします。","romaji":"Shōga o surioroshimasu.","german":"I grate ginger.","furigana":"しょうが"},{"japanese":"今日の晩ご飯は豚の生姜焼きにしよう。","romaji":"Kyou no bangohan wa buta no shougayaki ni shiyou.","german":"Let's have ginger pork for dinner tonight.","furigana":"きょう|ばん|はん|ぶた|しょうがや"},{"japanese":"風邪をひいたときは、生姜を入れた温かい紅茶を飲みます。","romaji":"Kaze o hiita toki wa, shouga o ireta atatakai koucha o nomimasu.","german":"When I have a cold, I drink hot black tea with ginger.","furigana":"かぜ|しょうが|い|あたた|こうちゃ|の"}],"notes":"生姜 is ginger. In Japanese cooking it is mostly used freshly grated (すりおろす) or finely chopped, for example in the popular 豚の生姜焼き (pork in ginger-soy sauce). Pickled pink ginger served with sushi is called ガリ, and red ginger served with yakisoba 紅しょうが. In daily life it is often written in kana as しょうが; since ginger is considered warming, people like to drink 生姜湯 when they have a cold.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"蒟蒻はカロリーが低い。","romaji":"Konnyaku wa karorī ga hikui.","german":"Konnyaku is low in calories.","furigana":"こんにゃく|ひく"},{"japanese":"おでんの具では、こんにゃくが一番好きです。","romaji":"Oden no gu de wa, konnyaku ga ichiban suki desu.","german":"Of the oden ingredients, I like konnyaku best.","furigana":"ぐ|いちばんす"},{"japanese":"蒟蒻は味がしみにくいので、先に切れ目を入れておく。","romaji":"Konnyaku wa aji ga shiminikui node, saki ni kireme o irete oku.","german":"Konnyaku doesn't absorb flavor easily, so score it first.","furigana":"こんにゃく|あじ|さき|き|め|い"}],"notes":"蒟蒻 is a firm, gray or white jelly made from the tuber of the konjac plant (devil's tongue). It has hardly any flavor of its own and almost no calories, so it is regarded as a diet food; it is typical in おでん and simmered dishes. It is almost always written こんにゃく in kana. In noodle form it is called しらたき, and こんにゃくゼリー is a popular snack.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"夏に素麺を食べます。","romaji":"Natsu ni sōmen o tabemasu.","german":"I eat somen in summer.","furigana":"なつ|そうめん|た"},{"japanese":"素麺をゆでたら、すぐに冷たい水で洗ってください。","romaji":"Soumen o yudetara, sugu ni tsumetai mizu de aratte kudasai.","german":"When the somen is boiled, rinse it right away with cold water.","furigana":"そうめん|つめ|みず|あら"},{"japanese":"夏休みに、家族で流しそうめんをして楽しかった。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi ni, kazoku de nagashi soumen o shite tanoshikatta.","german":"During summer vacation, we had nagashi somen as a family, and it was fun.","furigana":"なつやす|かぞく|なが|たの"}],"notes":"素麺 are very thin wheat noodles that are eaten cold, mainly in summer: briefly boiled, rinsed with cold water and dipped in a cold sauce (めんつゆ). They are thinner than 冷や麦 and much thinner than うどん. A summer fun activity is 流しそうめん, in which the noodles glide with water through a bamboo channel and you catch them with chopsticks. It is mostly written そうめん in kana.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"餃子を焼きます。","romaji":"Gyōza o yakimasu.","german":"I'm pan-frying gyoza.","furigana":"ぎょうざ|や"},{"japanese":"週末は家族みんなで餃子を包みました。","romaji":"Shuumatsu wa kazoku minna de gyouza o tsutsumimashita.","german":"On the weekend, the whole family wrapped gyoza together.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|かぞく|ぎょうざ|つつ"},{"japanese":"ラーメンと餃子のセットを一つください。","romaji":"Raamen to gyouza no setto o hitotsu kudasai.","german":"One ramen and gyoza set, please.","furigana":"ぎょうざ|ひと"}],"notes":"餃子 are dumplings of Chinese origin, usually filled with ground meat, cabbage and garlic. In Japan they are eaten mainly pan-fried (焼き餃子), often as a side dish to ラーメン, and dipped in soy sauce with vinegar and chili oil (ラー油). There are also 水餃子 (boiled) and 揚げ餃子 (deep-fried). One says 餃子を包む for filling and folding and 餃子を焼く for frying; on menus it is often written ギョーザ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"佃煮をご飯にのせます。","romaji":"Tsukudani o gohan ni nosemasu.","german":"I put tsukudani on top of the rice.","furigana":"つくだに|はん"},{"japanese":"おにぎりの中身は昆布の佃煮にしました。","romaji":"Onigiri no nakami wa konbu no tsukudani ni shimashita.","german":"I used kombu tsukudani as the filling for the rice balls.","furigana":"なかみ|こんぶ|つくだに"},{"japanese":"佃煮は日持ちするので、お土産にちょうどいい。","romaji":"Tsukudani wa himochi suru node, omiyage ni choudo ii.","german":"Tsukudani keeps well, so it is just right as a souvenir.","furigana":"つくだに|ひも|みやげ"}],"notes":"佃煮 are small fish, seaweed, shellfish or vegetables that are simmered for a long time in soy sauce, sugar and mirin until they are salty-sweet and keep for a long time. They are eaten in small amounts with rice or as the filling of おにぎり; well-known kinds are 昆布の佃煮 and のりの佃煮. The name goes back to the island 佃島 in Tokyo, where fishermen made these preserves in the Edo period.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"茶碗蒸しを作ります。","romaji":"Chawan mushi o tsukurimasu.","german":"I make chawanmushi.","furigana":"ちゃわんむ|つく"},{"japanese":"回転寿司に行くと、いつも茶碗蒸しを頼みます。","romaji":"Kaiten zushi ni iku to, itsumo chawan mushi o tanomimasu.","german":"When I go to a conveyor-belt sushi restaurant, I always order chawanmushi.","furigana":"かいてんずし|い|ちゃわんむ|たの"},{"japanese":"茶碗蒸しは熱いので、気をつけて食べてね。","romaji":"Chawan mushi wa atsui node, ki o tsukete tabete ne.","german":"Chawanmushi is hot, so eat carefully.","furigana":"ちゃわんむ|あつ|き|た"}],"notes":"茶碗蒸し is a savory steamed egg custard served in a small cup with a lid. The egg mixture is thinned with dashi and contains pieces such as chicken, shrimp, shiitake or ginkgo nuts (銀杏). It is eaten warm with a spoon, for example as a side dish in a sushi restaurant; you say 茶碗蒸しを作る or 蒸す. Unlike a pudding, it is not sweet.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鍋の後に雑炊を作ります。","romaji":"Nabe no ato ni zōsui o tsukurimasu.","german":"I make zosui after the hot pot.","furigana":"なべ|あと|ぞうすい|つく"},{"japanese":"風邪をひいたとき、母が卵の雑炊を作ってくれた。","romaji":"Kaze o hiita toki, haha ga tamago no zousui o tsukutte kureta.","german":"When I had a cold, my mother made egg zosui for me.","furigana":"かぜ|はは|たまご|ぞうすい|つく"},{"japanese":"残ったご飯で、簡単な雑炊を作りました。","romaji":"Nokotta gohan de, kantan na zousui o tsukurimashita.","german":"I made a simple zosui with leftover rice.","furigana":"のこ|はん|かんたん|ぞうすい|つく"}],"notes":"雑炊 is a rice porridge made from already cooked rice that is briefly simmered in seasoned broth with egg, vegetables or fish. It is typical as the finish of a hot pot (鍋の締め): you add rice to the remaining broth and stir in an egg. お粥, by contrast, is cooked for a long time from raw rice and is plainer; おじや is largely synonymous with 雑炊. When you have a cold, you like to eat a light 雑炊.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"釣瓶で水を汲みます。","romaji":"Tsurube de mizu o kumimasu.","german":"I draw water with the well bucket.","furigana":"つるべ|みず|く"},{"japanese":"昔はどの家でも、井戸の釣瓶で水をくんでいた。","romaji":"Mukashi wa dono ie demo, ido no tsurube de mizu o kunde ita.","german":"In the old days, every house drew water with a bucket from the well.","furigana":"むかし|いえ|いど|つるべ|みず"},{"japanese":"「秋の日は釣瓶落とし」と言うように、もう外が暗い。","romaji":"Aki no hi wa tsurube otoshi to iu you ni, mou soto ga kurai.","german":"As the saying goes \"an autumn day drops like a well bucket\", it is already dark outside.","furigana":"あき|ひ|つるべお|い|そと|くら"}],"notes":"釣瓶 is the draw bucket of a draw well (井戸), which is lowered into the well on a rope and pulled back up when full; you say 釣瓶で水を汲む. Since such wells hardly exist any more, the word is known today mainly from the saying 秋の日は釣瓶落とし: the autumn sun sinks as fast as a falling well bucket, so it gets dark quickly. The word sounds old-fashioned.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"火鉢で暖を取ります。","romaji":"Hibachi de dan o torimasu.","german":"I warm myself at the brazier.","furigana":"ひばち|だん|と"},{"japanese":"祖父の家では、冬になると火鉢を出していた。","romaji":"Sofu no ie de wa, fuyu ni naru to hibachi o dashite ita.","german":"At my grandfather's house, the brazier was brought out every winter.","furigana":"そふ|いえ|ふゆ|ひばち|だ"},{"japanese":"旅館の部屋に古い火鉢が飾ってあった。","romaji":"Ryokan no heya ni furui hibachi ga kazatte atta.","german":"An old brazier was displayed in the ryokan room.","furigana":"りょかん|へや|ふる|ひばち|かざ"}],"notes":"火鉢 is a portable charcoal brazier made of ceramic, metal or wood in which charcoal (炭) glows in ash. Before modern heating, people warmed their hands at it (火鉢に手をかざす) or heated water; 暖を取る is the fixed phrase for \"to warm oneself\". Today it is mostly seen only in old houses, ryokan or as decoration. What English calls a \"hibachi\" (a small grill) corresponds in Japanese more to a 七輪.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"行灯の灯りが柔らかい。","romaji":"Andon no akari ga yawarakai.","german":"The light of the andon is soft.","furigana":"あんどん|あか|やわ"},{"japanese":"夜になると、旅館の廊下に行灯がともされる。","romaji":"Yoru ni naru to, ryokan no rouka ni andon ga tomosareru.","german":"At night, andon are lit in the corridor of the ryokan.","furigana":"よる|りょかん|ろうか|あんどん"},{"japanese":"和室に合う行灯風の照明を買いました。","romaji":"Washitsu ni au andon fuu no shoumei o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a lantern-style light that suits a Japanese-style room.","furigana":"わしつ|あ|あんどんふう|しょうめい|か"}],"notes":"行灯 is a traditional standing lantern: a wooden frame covered with paper in which an oil lamp used to burn. Today 行灯 serve as atmospheric lights in ryokan, restaurants or at festivals, usually electric. The reading あんどん is irregular. Hanging, collapsible paper lanterns, by contrast, are called 提灯 (ちょうちん); colloquially, the illuminated sign on taxi roofs is also called 行灯.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"掛軸を床の間に飾ります。","romaji":"Kakejiku o tokonoma ni kazarimasu.","german":"I hang a scroll in the tokonoma.","furigana":"かけじく|とこ|ま|かざ"},{"japanese":"お正月に合わせて、掛軸を新しいものに掛け替えた。","romaji":"Oshougatsu ni awasete, kakejiku o atarashii mono ni kakekaeta.","german":"For New Year, we replaced the scroll with a new one.","furigana":"しょうがつ|あ|かけじく|あたら|か|か"},{"japanese":"美術館で有名な画家の掛軸を見ました。","romaji":"Bijutsukan de yuumei na gaka no kakejiku o mimashita.","german":"At the museum, I saw a hanging scroll by a famous painter.","furigana":"びじゅつかん|ゆうめい|がか|かけじく|み"}],"notes":"掛軸 is a hanging scroll with a painting or calligraphy that is hung in the alcove (床の間) of a Japanese room and changed according to the season or occasion. You say 掛軸を掛ける or 掛軸を飾る. The spelling 掛け軸 is also common; for short, people often say just 軸.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"衝立で部屋を仕切ります。","romaji":"Tsuitate de heya o shikirimasu.","german":"I divide the room with a partition.","furigana":"ついたて|へや|しき"},{"japanese":"店内には、テーブルの間に透明な衝立が置いてある。","romaji":"Tennai ni wa, teeburu no aida ni toumei na tsuitate ga oite aru.","german":"In the shop, transparent partitions are placed between the tables.","furigana":"てんない|あいだ|とうめい|ついたて|お"},{"japanese":"着替えるときは、この衝立の後ろを使ってください。","romaji":"Kigaeru toki wa, kono tsuitate no ushiro o tsukatte kudasai.","german":"When changing clothes, please use the area behind this screen.","furigana":"きが|ついたて|うし|つか"}],"notes":"衝立 is a freestanding, single-panel screen used to divide a room or shield an area from view, for example in a restaurant, office or reception. You say 衝立で仕切る or 衝立を立てる. Unlike the foldable 屏風, a 衝立 consists of a single panel on feet; modern partitions are often called パーティション.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"猫車で土を運びます。","romaji":"Nekoguruma de tsuchi o hakobimasu.","german":"I carry soil with the wheelbarrow.","furigana":"ねこぐるま|つち|はこ"},{"japanese":"工事現場で、猫車にセメントを入れて運んだ。","romaji":"Kouji genba de, nekoguruma ni semento o irete hakonda.","german":"At the construction site, I put cement in a wheelbarrow and carried it.","furigana":"こうじげんば|ねこぐるま|い|はこ"},{"japanese":"庭の落ち葉を猫車に積んで、裏まで押していった。","romaji":"Niwa no ochiba o nekoguruma ni tsunde, ura made oshite itta.","german":"I loaded the fallen leaves from the garden into the wheelbarrow and pushed it around back.","furigana":"にわ|お|ば|ねこぐるま|つ|うら|お"}],"notes":"猫車 is a one-wheeled wheelbarrow, as used on construction sites and in the garden. You say 猫車で運ぶ or 猫車を押す. 一輪車 is heard more often for it, but that also means \"unicycle\"; on construction sites people often say just ネコ. There are several explanations for the origin of the name, for example that the barrow can go along narrow paths where otherwise only a cat has room.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"竹馬に乗って遊びます。","romaji":"Takeuma ni notte asobimasu.","german":"I play on stilts.","furigana":"たけうま|の|あそ"},{"japanese":"小学校で竹馬の練習をして、やっと乗れるようになった。","romaji":"Shougakkou de takeuma no renshuu o shite, yatto noreru you ni natta.","german":"I practiced stilts in elementary school and can finally walk on them.","furigana":"しょうがっこう|たけうま|れんしゅう|の"},{"japanese":"竹馬は高いほど、バランスを取るのが難しい。","romaji":"Takeuma wa takai hodo, baransu o toru no ga muzukashii.","german":"The higher the stilts, the harder it is to keep your balance.","furigana":"たけうま|たか|と|むずか"}],"notes":"竹馬 are stilts made of bamboo with footrests, a traditional children's toy that is often practiced in elementary schools. You say 竹馬に乗る \"to walk on stilts\" or 竹馬で歩く. Also well known is the expression 竹馬の友 \"friend from childhood\", in which the characters are read in the Sino-Japanese way as ちくば.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"正月に羽根つきをします。","romaji":"Shōgatsu ni hanetsuki o shimasu.","german":"We play hanetsuki at New Year.","furigana":"しょうがつ|はね"},{"japanese":"羽根つきで負けた人は、顔に墨を塗られる。","romaji":"Hanetsuki de maketa hito wa, kao ni sumi o nurareru.","german":"The person who loses at hanetsuki gets ink painted on their face.","furigana":"はね|ま|ひと|かお|すみ|ぬ"},{"japanese":"最近は、羽根つきをする子どもをあまり見かけない。","romaji":"Saikin wa, hanetsuki o suru kodomo o amari mikakenai.","german":"These days, you rarely see children playing hanetsuki.","furigana":"さいきん|はね|こ|み"}],"notes":"羽根つき is a traditional New Year's game, similar to badminton: you hit a feathered shuttlecock back and forth with a decorated wooden paddle (羽子板). Whoever lets the shuttlecock drop traditionally gets something painted on their face with ink (墨). One says 羽根つきをする; the spelling 羽根突き is also common. It is rarely played today, but splendid 羽子板 are still sold as good-luck charms.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"福笑いをして笑いました。","romaji":"Fukuwarai o shite waraimashita.","german":"We laughed while playing fukuwarai.","furigana":"ふくわら|わら"},{"japanese":"お正月に子どもたちと福笑いで遊びました。","romaji":"Oshougatsu ni kodomotachi to fukuwarai de asobimashita.","german":"At New Year, I played fukuwarai with the children.","furigana":"しょうがつ|こ|ふくわら|あそ"},{"japanese":"福笑いでは、目隠しをして顔の部品を置きます。","romaji":"Fukuwarai de wa, mekakushi o shite kao no buhin o okimasu.","german":"In fukuwarai, you place the parts of a face while blindfolded.","furigana":"ふくわら|めかく|かお|ぶひん|お"}],"notes":"福笑い is made up of 福 \"luck\" and 笑い \"laughter\" and is a classic New Year's game: blindfolded (目隠しをして), you place eyes, nose, mouth and eyebrows on the outline of a face, usually a plump-cheeked おかめ figure. The crooked result causes laughter. One says 福笑いをする or 福笑いで遊ぶ; like かるた and 凧揚げ, it is one of the typical お正月の遊び.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"家族で双六をします。","romaji":"Kazoku de sugoroku o shimasu.","german":"We play sugoroku with the family.","furigana":"かぞく|すごろく"},{"japanese":"サイコロを振って、すごろくのコマを三つ進めた。","romaji":"Saikoro o futte, sugoroku no koma o mittsu susumeta.","german":"I rolled the dice and moved my sugoroku piece forward three spaces.","furigana":"ふ|みっ|すす"},{"japanese":"「ふりだしに戻る」は双六から来た言葉です。","romaji":"\"Furidashi ni modoru\" wa sugoroku kara kita kotoba desu.","german":"\"Back to square one\" is an expression that comes from sugoroku.","furigana":"もど|すごろく|き|ことば"}],"notes":"双六 is a dice racing game similar to snakes and ladders: you roll the dice (サイコロを振る), move your piece forward (コマを進める) and want to be the first to get from the start (ふりだし) to the goal (あがり). It is mostly written in kana as すごろく and used to be a typical New Year game. The expression ふりだしに戻る \"back to square one, to start over\" comes from it. The old 盤双六, by contrast, was a backgammon-like board game.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"将棋を指します。","romaji":"Shōgi o sashimasu.","german":"I play shogi.","furigana":"しょうぎ|さ"},{"japanese":"祖父は毎晩、近所の人と将棋を指しています。","romaji":"Sofu wa maiban, kinjo no hito to shougi o sashite imasu.","german":"My grandfather plays shogi with a neighbor every evening.","furigana":"そふ|まいばん|きんじょ|ひと|しょうぎ|さ"},{"japanese":"将棋のプロになるのはとても難しい。","romaji":"Shougi no puro ni naru no wa totemo muzukashii.","german":"Becoming a professional shogi player is very hard.","furigana":"しょうぎ|むずか"}],"notes":"将棋 is Japanese chess with 駒 \"pieces\" that you may place back on the board yourself after capturing them. The typical verb is 指す: 将棋を指す; colloquially, 将棋をする also works. For Go, 碁を打つ is said instead. A professional player is called 棋士. Figuratively, 将棋倒し means that a crowd of people topples like dominoes.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"碁盤に石を置きます。","romaji":"Goban ni ishi o okimasu.","german":"I place a stone on the go board.","furigana":"ごばん|いし|お"},{"japanese":"京都の町は碁盤の目のように道が並んでいます。","romaji":"Kyouto no machi wa goban no me no you ni michi ga narande imasu.","german":"The streets of Kyoto are laid out like a go board grid.","furigana":"きょうと|まち|ごばん|め|みち|なら"},{"japanese":"祖父の家には古い木の碁盤があります。","romaji":"Sofu no ie ni wa furui ki no goban ga arimasu.","german":"There is an old wooden go board at my grandfather's house.","furigana":"そふ|いえ|ふる|き|ごばん"}],"notes":"碁盤 is the game board for Go (囲碁), on which 19 x 19 lines are drawn; the stones are placed on the intersections (碁盤に石を置く / 打つ). The board for shogi, by contrast, is called 将棋盤. Very common is the figurative expression 碁盤の目のよう \"like a checkerboard, grid-like\", for example for the street network of Kyoto or Sapporo.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"碁石には白と黒があります。","romaji":"Goishi ni wa shiro to kuro ga arimasu.","german":"Go stones come in white and black.","furigana":"ごいし|しろ|くろ"},{"japanese":"子どもが碁石を床に落としてしまった。","romaji":"Kodomo ga goishi o yuka ni otoshite shimatta.","german":"The child dropped the go stones on the floor.","furigana":"こ|ごいし|ゆか|お"},{"japanese":"先生は碁石を一つ盤の真ん中に打った。","romaji":"Sensei wa goishi o hitotsu ban no mannaka ni utta.","german":"The teacher placed a go stone in the center of the board.","furigana":"せんせい|ごいし|ひと|ばん|ま|なか|う"}],"notes":"碁石 are the round playing pieces of the game of Go, black (黒石) and white (白石). When playing you say 石を打つ or 石を置く; in conversation, simply 石 is often enough. For counting, 〜個 or 〜目 (points on the board) is mostly used. Do not confuse it with 碁盤, the playing board.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"麻雀で遊びます。","romaji":"Mājan de asobimasu.","german":"We play mahjong.","furigana":"|あそ"},{"japanese":"父は週末に友達と麻雀を打つのが好きです。","romaji":"Chichi wa shuumatsu ni tomodachi to maajan o utsu no ga suki desu.","german":"My father likes playing mahjong with his friends on weekends.","furigana":"ちち|しゅうまつ|ともだち||う|す"},{"japanese":"麻雀のルールは複雑で、覚えるのに時間がかかった。","romaji":"Maajan no ruuru wa fukuzatsu de, oboeru no ni jikan ga kakatta.","german":"The rules of mahjong are complicated, and it took me a long time to learn them.","furigana":"|ふくざつ|おぼ|じかん"}],"notes":"麻雀 is read with the Chinese-derived reading マージャン and is therefore often written in katakana as well. One says 麻雀をする; among players 麻雀を打つ is common. It is played by four people with 牌 \"tiles\", often in a 雀荘 \"mahjong parlor\". In Japan the game is regarded as a rather adult hobby, often associated with betting.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"花札は日本の伝統的なカードゲームです。","romaji":"Hanafuda wa nihon no dentōteki na kādo gēmu desu.","german":"Hanafuda is a traditional Japanese card game.","furigana":"はなふだ|にほん|でんとうてき"},{"japanese":"お正月に親戚と花札で遊びました。","romaji":"Oshougatsu ni shinseki to hanafuda de asobimashita.","german":"At New Year, I played hanafuda with relatives.","furigana":"しょうがつ|しんせき|はなふだ|あそ"},{"japanese":"花札の札には、それぞれ月の花が描かれている。","romaji":"Hanafuda no fuda ni wa, sorezore tsuki no hana ga egakarete iru.","german":"Each hanafuda card has the flower of a month painted on it.","furigana":"はなふだ|ふだ|つき|はな|えが"}],"notes":"花札 are Japanese flower cards: 48 cards in 12 suits, each standing for a month and showing its flower or plant (e.g., 桜 for March). A single card is called 札. You say 花札をする or 花札で遊ぶ; the best-known game played with them is こいこい. Incidentally, Nintendo started out as a maker of 花札.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"紙芝居を見せてもらいました。","romaji":"Kamishibai o misete moraimashita.","german":"I was shown a kamishibai.","furigana":"かみしばい|み"},{"japanese":"幼稚園の先生が子どもたちに紙芝居を読んであげた。","romaji":"Youchien no sensei ga kodomotachi ni kamishibai o yonde ageta.","german":"The kindergarten teacher read a kamishibai story to the children.","furigana":"ようちえん|せんせい|こ|かみしばい|よ"},{"japanese":"昔は紙芝居屋さんが自転車で公園に来ていたそうです。","romaji":"Mukashi wa kamishibaiya-san ga jitensha de kouen ni kite ita sou desu.","german":"In the old days, kamishibai storytellers reportedly came to the park by bicycle.","furigana":"むかし|かみしばいや|じてんしゃ|こうえん|き"}],"notes":"紙芝居 is a picture-story theater: the storyteller pulls large picture boards one after another out of a small wooden frame and reads the text on the back. In the past, 紙芝居屋さん came to squares by bicycle and sold sweets; today it is used mainly in kindergartens and libraries. Typical phrases are 紙芝居を読む, 紙芝居をする and 紙芝居を見せる.","levelNote":"Compound: 紙 (N5) + 芝居 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"噺家の話が面白い。","romaji":"Hanashika no hanashi ga omoshiroi.","german":"The rakugo performer's stories are funny.","furigana":"はなしか|はなし|おもしろ"},{"japanese":"祖父は若いころ、噺家になりたかったそうだ。","romaji":"Sofu wa wakai koro, hanashika ni naritakatta sou da.","german":"My grandfather reportedly wanted to become a rakugo storyteller when he was young.","furigana":"そふ|わか|はなしか"},{"japanese":"噺家は扇子と手ぬぐいだけで色々な場面を表現します。","romaji":"Hanashika wa sensu to tenugui dake de iroiro na bamen o hyougen shimasu.","german":"A rakugo performer portrays all sorts of scenes with just a fan and a hand towel.","furigana":"はなしか|せんす|て|いろいろ|ばめん|ひょうげん"}],"notes":"噺家 refers to a professional teller of comic stories, especially in 落語. The character 噺 \"tale\" is a kanji created in Japan (国字). In everyday speech and in the media, 落語家 is the more common term; 噺家 sounds more traditional and is often used by the performers themselves. A 噺家 appears in a 寄席 and works only with a fan (扇子) and a hand towel (手ぬぐい).","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"寄席に行きました。","romaji":"Yose ni ikimashita.","german":"I went to a yose.","furigana":"よせ|い"},{"japanese":"寄席で初めて生の落語を聞きました。","romaji":"Yose de hajimete nama no rakugo o kikimashita.","german":"At a yose, I heard live rakugo for the first time.","furigana":"よせ|はじ|なま|らくご|き"},{"japanese":"浅草には今も古い寄席が残っています。","romaji":"Asakusa ni wa ima mo furui yose ga nokotte imasu.","german":"Old yose theaters still remain in Asakusa.","furigana":"あさくさ|いま|ふる|よせ|のこ"}],"notes":"寄席 is a small traditional entertainment theater in which 落語, 漫才, magic and other variety acts perform one after another. You say 寄席に行く, 寄席に通う and 寄席で落語を聞く. The reading よせ is irregular and has to be learned. Classic 寄席 exist mainly in Tokyo, for example in Asakusa or Ueno.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"漫才を見て笑いました。","romaji":"Manzai o mite waraimashita.","german":"I laughed while watching manzai.","furigana":"まんざい|み|わら"},{"japanese":"二人は高校のときから漫才のコンビを組んでいる。","romaji":"Futari wa koukou no toki kara manzai no konbi o kunde iru.","german":"The two have been a manzai duo since high school.","furigana":"ふたり|こうこう|まんざい|く"},{"japanese":"文化祭で友達と漫才をすることになった。","romaji":"Bunkasai de tomodachi to manzai o suru koto ni natta.","german":"I am going to do a manzai routine with a friend at the school festival.","furigana":"ぶんかさい|ともだち|まんざい"}],"notes":"漫才 is a comic dialogue act for two: the ボケ says nonsense, and the ツッコミ corrects them with quick retorts. 漫才 thus means the performance, not the duo itself; the duo is called 漫才コンビ, and the performers 漫才師 or 芸人. One says 漫才をする and コンビを組む. The art form is especially associated with Osaka and the Kansai dialect.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"能面をつけて舞います。","romaji":"Nōmen o tsukete maimasu.","german":"He dances wearing a Noh mask.","furigana":"のうめん|ま"},{"japanese":"彼女は怒ると能面のような顔になる。","romaji":"Kanojo wa okoru to noumen no you na kao ni naru.","german":"When she is angry, her face becomes as stiff as a Noh mask.","furigana":"かのじょ|おこ|のうめん|かお"},{"japanese":"博物館で古い能面を見ました。","romaji":"Hakubutsukan de furui noumen o mimashita.","german":"I saw old Noh masks at the museum.","furigana":"はくぶつかん|ふる|のうめん|み"}],"notes":"能面 is the wooden mask of Noh theater (能). You say 能面をつける or 能面をかける. Although the mask looks rigid, its expression seems to change depending on the tilt of the head. Figuratively, 能面のような顔 is an \"expressionless, stony face\", often with suppressed anger.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仕舞を舞います。","romaji":"Shimai o maimasu.","german":"I perform a shimai dance.","furigana":"しまい|ま"},{"japanese":"仕舞は面や特別な衣装をつけずに舞う能です。","romaji":"Shimai wa men ya tokubetsu na ishou o tsukezu ni mau nou desu.","german":"Shimai is Noh danced without a mask or special costume.","furigana":"しまい|めん|とくべつ|いしょう|ま|のう"},{"japanese":"母は趣味で仕舞を習っています。","romaji":"Haha wa shumi de shimai o naratte imasu.","german":"My mother is learning shimai as a hobby.","furigana":"はは|しゅみ|しまい|なら"}],"notes":"仕舞 here is a short excerpt from a Nō play, danced without mask and costume, only in formal kimono with hakama, to the chanting of the chorus. The matching verb is 舞う: 仕舞を舞う. Note: the same spelling and reading also means \"end, finish\"; in that sense, however, it is nowadays usually written おしまい or 終い.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"浄瑠璃の語りが素晴らしい。","romaji":"Jōruri no katari ga subarashii.","german":"The joruri recitation is wonderful.","furigana":"じょうるり|かた|すば"},{"japanese":"人形浄瑠璃は文楽とも呼ばれています。","romaji":"Ningyou joururi wa bunraku to mo yobarete imasu.","german":"Puppet joruri is also called Bunraku.","furigana":"にんぎょうじょうるり|ぶんらく|よ"},{"japanese":"浄瑠璃は三味線に合わせて物語を語る芸能です。","romaji":"Joururi wa shamisen ni awasete monogatari o kataru geinou desu.","german":"Joruri is an art form in which a story is recited to shamisen accompaniment.","furigana":"じょうるり|しゃみせん|あ|ものがたり|かた|げいのう"}],"notes":"浄瑠璃 is a narrative art in which a reciter (太夫) performs a story in song, accompanied by the shamisen. It is best known as the accompaniment of puppet theater: 人形浄瑠璃, today mostly called 文楽. Typical expressions are 浄瑠璃を語る and 浄瑠璃の語り. The most important style is called 義太夫節.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"花道から役者が登場します。","romaji":"Hanamichi kara yakusha ga tōjō shimasu.","german":"The actor enters via the hanamichi.","furigana":"はなみち|やくしゃ|とうじょう"},{"japanese":"最後の試合で勝って、引退の花道を飾った。","romaji":"Saigo no shiai de katte, intai no hanamichi o kazatta.","german":"He won his last match and gave himself a glorious send-off into retirement.","furigana":"さいご|しあい|か|いんたい|はなみち|かざ"},{"japanese":"相撲では、力士が花道を通って土俵に向かう。","romaji":"Sumou de wa, rikishi ga hanamichi o tootte dohyou ni mukau.","german":"In sumo, the wrestlers walk to the ring along the hanamichi.","furigana":"すもう|りきし|はなみち|とお|どひょう|む"}],"notes":"花道 is, in Kabuki, the raised walkway that leads from the edge of the stage through the audience and is used for spectacular entrances and exits; in sumo, too, the wrestlers' path to the ring is called this. Very common is the figurative meaning \"glorious exit at the end of a career\", especially in 引退の花道を飾る. Not to be confused with 華道 (かどう, flower arranging).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"見得を切ります。","romaji":"Mie o kirimasu.","german":"He strikes a dramatic pose.","furigana":"みえ|き"},{"japanese":"歌舞伎の役者が見得を切ると、客席から声がかかった。","romaji":"Kabuki no yakusha ga mie o kiru to, kyakuseki kara koe ga kakatta.","german":"When the Kabuki actor struck his mie, people in the audience called out to him.","furigana":"かぶき|やくしゃ|みえ|き|きゃくせき|こえ"},{"japanese":"部長は「必ず成功させる」と大見得を切った。","romaji":"Buchou wa \"kanarazu seikou saseru\" to oomie o kitta.","german":"The department head boasted, \"I will make sure it succeeds.\"","furigana":"ぶちょう|かなら|せいこう|おおみえ|き"}],"notes":"見得 is the dramatic, briefly frozen pose of a Kabuki actor at a climax; the fixed verb for it is 切る: 見得を切る. Figuratively, 見得を切る or 大見得を切る means \"to boast, to put oneself on show with confidence\". Not to be confused with the identically read 見栄 \"vanity, outward show\", which is paired with 張る: 見栄を張る.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"早変わりに驚きました。","romaji":"Hayagawari ni odorokimashita.","german":"I was amazed by the quick change.","furigana":"はやが|おどろ"},{"japanese":"このソファは、広げるとベッドに早変わりします。","romaji":"Kono sofa wa, hirogeru to beddo ni hayagawari shimasu.","german":"This sofa turns into a bed in no time when you unfold it.","furigana":"ひろ|はやが"},{"japanese":"役者は一瞬で別の衣装に早変わりした。","romaji":"Yakusha wa isshun de betsu no ishou ni hayagawari shita.","german":"The actor changed into a different costume in an instant.","furigana":"やくしゃ|いっしゅん|べつ|いしょう|はやが"}],"notes":"早変わり is originally the lightning-fast costume and role change of a kabuki actor on an open stage. As an intransitive する-verb, it generally means \"to transform into something in no time\", with に: AがBに早変わりする. It is used, for example, to advertise convertible furniture or to describe a room that is suddenly used differently.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"三線の音色が心地良い。","romaji":"Sanshin no neiro ga kokochiyoi.","german":"The sound of the sanshin is pleasant.","furigana":"さんしん|ねいろ|ここちよ"},{"japanese":"沖縄旅行で三線を弾く体験をしました。","romaji":"Okinawa ryokou de sanshin o hiku taiken o shimashita.","german":"On my trip to Okinawa I tried playing the sanshin.","furigana":"おきなわりょこう|さんしん|ひ|たいけん"},{"japanese":"三線には蛇の皮が張ってあります。","romaji":"Sanshin ni wa hebi no kawa ga hatte arimasu.","german":"The sanshin is covered with snakeskin.","furigana":"さんしん|へび|かわ|は"}],"notes":"三線 is the three-stringed lute of Okinawa, whose body is traditionally covered with snakeskin; it is regarded as the predecessor of the shamisen (三味線). It is played by plucking: 三線を弾く. Its sound (音色) characterizes Okinawan folk music and is often associated with vacations and relaxation.","levelNote":"Compound: 線 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"篠笛を吹きます。","romaji":"Shinobue o fukimasu.","german":"I play the shinobue.","furigana":"しのぶえ|ふ"},{"japanese":"夏祭りでは、太鼓と篠笛の音が聞こえてくる。","romaji":"Natsumatsuri de wa, taiko to shinobue no oto ga kikoete kuru.","german":"At the summer festival, you can hear drums and bamboo flutes.","furigana":"なつまつ|たいこ|しのぶえ|おと|き"},{"japanese":"篠笛は横にして吹く竹の笛です。","romaji":"Shinobue wa yoko ni shite fuku take no fue desu.","german":"The shinobue is a bamboo flute that is held sideways.","furigana":"しのぶえ|よこ|ふ|たけ|ふえ"}],"notes":"篠笛 is a simple, bright-sounding transverse flute made of bamboo (篠竹). You say 篠笛を吹く. It is the typical melody instrument of festival music (祭囃子) and folk dance. Do not confuse it with the shakuhachi (尺八), which is held vertically; a flute in general is simply called 笛.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"琵琶の演奏を聞きます。","romaji":"Biwa no ensō o kikimasu.","german":"I listen to a biwa performance.","furigana":"びわ|えんそう|き"},{"japanese":"琵琶湖は形が琵琶に似ているので、その名前がついたそうです。","romaji":"Biwako wa katachi ga biwa ni nite iru node, sono namae ga tsuita sou desu.","german":"Lake Biwa is said to have gotten its name because its shape resembles a biwa.","furigana":"びわこ|かたち|びわ|に|なまえ"},{"japanese":"昔、琵琶法師は琵琶を弾きながら物語を語った。","romaji":"Mukashi, biwa houshi wa biwa o hikinagara monogatari o katatta.","german":"In the old days, biwa priests recited stories while playing the biwa.","furigana":"むかし|びわほうし|びわ|ひ|ものがたり|かた"}],"notes":"琵琶 is a pear-shaped lute with a short neck that is played with a large plectrum: 琵琶を弾く. In the Middle Ages, 琵琶法師, blind wandering monks, used it to recite the Tale of the Heike (平家物語). 琵琶湖 owes its name to the shape of the instrument. Read the same but written differently is 枇杷 \"loquat\" (fruit).","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"神楽を奉納します。","romaji":"Kagura o hōnō shimasu.","german":"We offer a kagura dance to the gods.","furigana":"かぐら|ほうのう"},{"japanese":"秋祭りの夜、神社で神楽を見ました。","romaji":"Akimatsuri no yoru, jinja de kagura o mimashita.","german":"On the evening of the autumn festival, I saw kagura at the shrine.","furigana":"あきまつ|よる|じんじゃ|かぐら|み"},{"japanese":"神楽の笛と太鼓の音が神社に響いていた。","romaji":"Kagura no fue to taiko no oto ga jinja ni hibiite ita.","german":"The sound of kagura flutes and drums echoed through the shrine.","furigana":"かぐら|ふえ|たいこ|おと|じんじゃ|ひび"}],"notes":"神楽 is Shinto music with dance that is offered to the gods, often on a dedicated stage in the shrine (神楽殿). The typical verb is 奉納する \"to dedicate as an offering\"; as a spectator one says 神楽を見る. The polite form is also お神楽. Besides the court kagura there are many regional folk forms (里神楽), which are performed at shrine festivals.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"雅楽の演奏を聴きました。","romaji":"Gagaku no ensō o kikimashita.","german":"I listened to court music.","furigana":"ががく|えんそう|き"},{"japanese":"神社の結婚式で、雅楽が流れていました。","romaji":"Jinja no kekkonshiki de, gagaku ga nagarete imashita.","german":"Gagaku was playing at the wedding at the shrine.","furigana":"じんじゃ|けっこんしき|ががく|なが"},{"japanese":"雅楽は千年以上前から宮中で演奏されてきた音楽です。","romaji":"Gagaku wa sennen ijou mae kara kyuuchuu de ensou sarete kita ongaku desu.","german":"Gagaku is music that has been played at the imperial court for over a thousand years.","furigana":"ががく|せんねんいじょうまえ|きゅうちゅう|えんそう|おんがく"}],"notes":"雅楽 is the ceremonial court music of Japan, which has been cultivated at the imperial court for over a thousand years; typical instruments are 笙, 篳篥 and 龍笛. You say 雅楽を演奏する or 雅楽を聴く. In daily life, you mainly encounter it at weddings at a shrine (神前式) and at New Year ceremonies. 雅 means \"elegant, courtly\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"獅子舞が来ました。","romaji":"Shishimai ga kimashita.","german":"The lion dancers have come.","furigana":"ししまい|き"},{"japanese":"お正月に獅子舞に頭をかんでもらうと、一年元気に過ごせると言われている。","romaji":"Oshougatsu ni shishimai ni atama o kande morau to, ichinen genki ni sugoseru to iwarete iru.","german":"It is said that if the lion bites your head at New Year, you will stay healthy all year.","furigana":"しょうがつ|ししまい|あたま|いちねんげんき|す|い"},{"japanese":"中華街のお祭りで獅子舞を見ました。","romaji":"Chuukagai no omatsuri de shishimai o mimashita.","german":"I saw a lion dance at the festival in Chinatown.","furigana":"ちゅうかがい|まつ|ししまい|み"}],"notes":"獅子舞 is the lion dance, in which one or two dancers dance under a cloth with a large wooden lion head. In Japan it is seen mainly at New Year and at festivals; it is said that if you let the lion bite your head (頭をかんでもらう), you will stay healthy for a year. 獅子 is the mythical lion; the real animal is called ライオン.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"御輿を担ぎます。","romaji":"Mikoshi o katsugimasu.","german":"I carry the portable shrine.","furigana":"みこし|かつ"},{"japanese":"祭りの日には、町の人たちが「わっしょい」と言いながら御輿を担いで歩く。","romaji":"Matsuri no hi ni wa, machi no hitotachi ga \"wasshoi\" to iinagara mikoshi o katsuide aruku.","german":"On festival day, the people of the neighborhood walk along carrying the mikoshi and shouting \"wasshoi\".","furigana":"まつ|ひ|まち|ひと|い|みこし|かつ|ある"},{"japanese":"彼は周りの人に御輿を担がれて、会長になった。","romaji":"Kare wa mawari no hito ni mikoshi o katsugarete, kaichou ni natta.","german":"With the people around him pushing him forward, he became chairman.","furigana":"かれ|まわ|ひと|みこし|かつ|かいちょう"}],"notes":"御輿 is the portable shrine in which the deity is carried through the streets at festivals; it is more often written 神輿 or simply みこし, politely お神輿. The fixed verb is 担ぐ \"to carry on the shoulders\", often with shouts like わっしょい. Figuratively, 御輿を担ぐ means \"to build someone up as a figurehead\"; a festival float with wheels, by contrast, is a 山車.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"山車が通りを練り歩く。","romaji":"Dashi ga tōri o neriaruku.","german":"The float parades through the street.","furigana":"だし|とお|ね|ある"},{"japanese":"子どもたちが綱を引いて、山車を動かしている。","romaji":"Kodomotachi ga tsuna o hiite, dashi o ugokashite iru.","german":"The children are pulling the ropes and moving the float.","furigana":"こ|つな|ひ|だし|うご"},{"japanese":"祭りの山車には、きれいな人形が飾られていた。","romaji":"Matsuri no dashi ni wa, kirei na ningyou ga kazararete ita.","german":"The festival float was decorated with beautiful dolls.","furigana":"まつ|だし|にんぎょう|かざ"}],"notes":"山車 is a decorated festival float that is pulled on ropes through the streets at shrine festivals: 山車を引く. The reading だし is irregular; regionally, such floats are also called 曳山 or 屋台. By contrast, a 御輿 is carried on the shoulders. Similar in sound but unrelated is 出汁 \"broth\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"法被を着てお祭りに参加します。","romaji":"Happi o kite omatsuri ni sanka shimasu.","german":"I take part in the festival wearing a happi coat.","furigana":"はっぴ|き|まつ|さんか"},{"japanese":"店員さんたちがおそろいの法被を着て、商品を売っていた。","romaji":"Ten'in-san-tachi ga osoroi no happi o kite, shouhin o utte ita.","german":"The shop assistants wore matching happi coats and sold their products.","furigana":"てんいん|はっぴ|き|しょうひん|う"},{"japanese":"法被は外国人観光客にお土産として人気です。","romaji":"Happi wa gaikokujin kankoukyaku ni omiyage to shite ninki desu.","german":"Happi coats are popular with foreign tourists as souvenirs.","furigana":"はっぴ|がいこくじんかんこうきゃく|みやげ|にんき"}],"notes":"法被 is a short, straight-cut cotton jacket, often printed with the name or crest of a neighborhood, club or shop. It is worn at festivals when carrying the shrine, but also by salespeople at promotions and by sports fans. The verb is 着る or 羽織る \"to throw on\". The reading はっぴ is irregular.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鉢巻きを締めます。","romaji":"Hachimaki o shimemasu.","german":"I tie on the headband.","furigana":"はちま|し"},{"japanese":"運動会では、赤と白の鉢巻きでチームを分けます。","romaji":"Undoukai de wa, aka to shiro no hachimaki de chiimu o wakemasu.","german":"At the sports festival, teams are distinguished by red and white headbands.","furigana":"うんどうかい|あか|しろ|はちま|わ"},{"japanese":"受験生が「必勝」と書いたはちまきをして勉強している。","romaji":"Jukensei ga \"hisshou\" to kaita hachimaki o shite benkyou shite iru.","german":"The exam candidate is studying wearing a headband that says \"certain victory\".","furigana":"じゅけんせい|ひっしょう|か|べんきょう"}],"notes":"鉢巻き is a cloth tied around the forehead, mostly written in kana as はちまき. You say 鉢巻きを締める or 鉢巻きをする. It stands for determination and effort: it is worn at the sports festival (運動会, red against white), at festivals, as a way of cheering someone on, or when studying for exams with an inscription such as 必勝 \"certain victory\". A cloth twisted into a rope is called ねじり鉢巻き.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"祭りで褌を締めます。","romaji":"Matsuri de fundoshi o shimemasu.","german":"I wear a loincloth at the festival.","furigana":"まつ|ふんどし|し"},{"japanese":"明日の試合は大事だから、褌を締めてかかろう。","romaji":"Ashita no shiai wa daiji dakara, fundoshi o shimete kakarou.","german":"The match tomorrow is important, so let's roll up our sleeves.","furigana":"あした|しあい|だいじ|ふんどし|し"},{"japanese":"人の褌で相撲を取るようなやり方は好きじゃない。","romaji":"Hito no fundoshi de sumou o toru you na yarikata wa suki ja nai.","german":"I don't like the kind of approach that takes credit for other people's work.","furigana":"ひと|ふんどし|すもう|と|かた|す"}],"notes":"褌 is the traditional Japanese loincloth for men made from a long strip of fabric, mostly written in kana as ふんどし. The verb is 締める. Today it is seen mainly at festivals such as the 裸祭り. Idioms are common: 褌を締めてかかる \"to get down to work in earnest\" and 人の褌で相撲を取る \"to seek one's own advantage with someone else's means\". The belt of sumo wrestlers, by contrast, is called まわし.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"手甲をはめます。","romaji":"Tekkō o hamemasu.","german":"I put on the hand guards.","furigana":"てっこう"},{"japanese":"昔の旅人は、手甲と脚絆をつけて歩いた。","romaji":"Mukashi no tabibito wa, tekkou to kyahan o tsukete aruita.","german":"Travelers in the old days walked wearing hand guards and leg wraps.","furigana":"むかし|たびびと|てっこう|きゃはん|ある"},{"japanese":"踊りの衣装に、白い手甲を合わせた。","romaji":"Odori no ishou ni, shiroi tekkou o awaseta.","german":"I paired white hand guards with the dance costume.","furigana":"おど|いしょう|しろ|てっこう|あ"}],"notes":"手甲 is a cloth protector for the back of the hand and the wrist that is held in place by a loop around the middle finger. It used to be worn by travelers and field workers; today it is seen in folk dances such as the 阿波踊り or in ninja costumes, often together with 脚絆. You say 手甲をはめる or つける. Do not confuse it with 手の甲 (てのこう) \"back of the hand\" or with the identically read 鉄鋼 \"steel\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"脚絆を巻きます。","romaji":"Kyahan o makimasu.","german":"I wrap on the leg wraps.","furigana":"きゃはん|ま"},{"japanese":"時代劇の旅人は、たいてい脚絆を巻いて草鞋を履いている。","romaji":"Jidaigeki no tabibito wa, taitei kyahan o maite waraji o haite iru.","german":"Travelers in period dramas usually wear leg wraps and straw sandals.","furigana":"じだいげき|たびびと|きゃはん|ま|わらじ|は"},{"japanese":"古い写真を見ると、兵隊はみんな足に脚絆を巻いている。","romaji":"Furui shashin o miru to, heitai wa minna ashi ni kyahan o maite iru.","german":"In old photos, all the soldiers have leg wraps wound around their legs.","furigana":"ふる|しゃしん|み|へいたい|あし|きゃはん|ま"}],"notes":"脚絆 are cloth gaiters that are wrapped around the lower leg to protect the legs when walking and to make them less tired. You say 脚絆を巻く or つける. They were part of the equipment of Edo-period travelers (often with 手甲 and 草鞋) and, as wrap puttees (巻脚絆), part of soldiers' uniforms. Today the word is mostly encountered in historical films and festival costumes.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"昔は草鞋を履いて旅をしました。","romaji":"Mukashi wa waraji o haite tabi o shimashita.","german":"In the old days, people traveled in straw sandals.","furigana":"むかし|わらじ|は|たび"},{"japanese":"彼は会社員と作家の二足のわらじを履いている。","romaji":"Kare wa kaishain to sakka no nisoku no waraji o haite iru.","german":"He holds two jobs at once, as an office worker and a writer.","furigana":"かれ|かいしゃいん|さっか|にそく|は"},{"japanese":"お寺の門に大きな草鞋が飾られていた。","romaji":"Otera no mon ni ookina waraji ga kazararete ita.","german":"A huge straw sandal was hung at the temple gate.","furigana":"てら|もん|おお|わらじ|かざ"}],"notes":"草鞋 are woven straw sandals that are tied around the ankle with cords; mostly written in kana as わらじ. The verb is 履く. The idiom 二足のわらじを履く \"to hold two jobs at the same time\" is important. Do not confuse it with 草履 (ぞうり), flat sandals without lacing, which are also worn with a kimono.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"万華鏡を覗きます。","romaji":"Mangekyō o nozokimasu.","german":"I look into the kaleidoscope.","furigana":"まんげきょう|のぞ"},{"japanese":"万華鏡を回すと、模様がどんどん変わる。","romaji":"Mangekyou o mawasu to, moyou ga dondon kawaru.","german":"When you turn the kaleidoscope, the pattern keeps changing.","furigana":"まんげきょう|まわ|もよう|か"},{"japanese":"夏休みに、子どもと紙で万華鏡を作った。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi ni, kodomo to kami de mangekyou o tsukutta.","german":"During summer vacation, I made a paper kaleidoscope with my child.","furigana":"なつやす|こ|かみ|まんげきょう|つく"}],"notes":"万華鏡 is a kaleidoscope, literally something like \"mirror of ten thousand flowers\". You say 万華鏡をのぞく (覗く is often written in kana) and 万華鏡を回す. Figuratively, 万華鏡のような describes something colorful and constantly changing, such as colors, landscapes or feelings.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"達磨に目を入れます。","romaji":"Daruma ni me o iremasu.","german":"I paint in the eye of the daruma.","furigana":"だるま|め|い"},{"japanese":"選挙に勝って、候補者がだるまにもう片方の目を書き入れた。","romaji":"Senkyo ni katte, kouhosha ga daruma ni mou katahou no me o kakiireta.","german":"After winning the election, the candidate painted in the daruma's other eye.","furigana":"せんきょ|か|こうほしゃ|かたほう|め|か|い"},{"japanese":"子どもたちが公園で雪だるまを作っている。","romaji":"Kodomotachi ga kouen de yukidaruma o tsukutte iru.","german":"The children are making a snowman in the park.","furigana":"こ|こうえん|ゆき|つく"}],"notes":"達磨 is the round, usually red good-luck doll modeled on the monk Bodhidharma, usually written in kana as だるま. When making a wish, you paint in one eye (目を入れる), and when it comes true, the second - typically done, for example, after election victories. Because it rights itself again and again, it stands for 七転び八起き. Well-known derivatives are 雪だるま \"snowman\" and 火だるま \"ablaze, engulfed in flames\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"電車が間もなく到着します。","romaji":"Densha ga mamonaku tōchaku shimasu.","german":"The train will arrive shortly.","furigana":"でんしゃ|ま|とうちゃく"},{"japanese":"間もなく会議が始まります。","romaji":"Mamonaku kaigi ga hajimarimasu.","german":"The meeting will start shortly.","furigana":"ま|かいぎ|はじ"},{"japanese":"日本に来て間もなく、友達ができた。","romaji":"Nihon ni kite mamonaku, tomodachi ga dekita.","german":"Soon after I came to Japan, I made friends.","furigana":"にほん|き|ま|ともだち"}],"notes":"間もなく means \"soon, shortly\" and is mostly written in kana as まもなく. It sounds more formal than もうすぐ and is typical of announcements: まもなく電車が参ります. After the て-form, 〜て間もなく means \"shortly after ...\"; 〜て間もない describes something that happened only a short time ago. Rarer is 〜する間もなく \"without having time to do something\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"何回矢を投げてもダーツボードに当たらなくて、結局全部外れだったよ。","romaji":"Nankai ya o nagete mo dātsu bōdo ni ataranakute, kekkyoku zenbu hazure datta yo.","german":"No matter how many times I threw the darts, they didn't hit the dartboard, and in the end they were all misses.","furigana":"なんかいや|な|あ|けっきょくぜんぶはず"},{"japanese":"くじを引いたけど、外れだった。","romaji":"Kuji o hiita kedo, hazure datta.","german":"I drew a lottery ticket, but it was a blank.","furigana":"ひ|はず"},{"japanese":"祖母の家は町の外れにあります。","romaji":"Sobo no ie wa machi no hazure ni arimasu.","german":"My grandmother's house is on the outskirts of town.","furigana":"そぼ|いえ|まち|はず"}],"notes":"外れ is the noun of 外れる \"to miss, to come off\". It means, on the one hand, \"blank, miss\" - the opposite of 当たり, for example with lottery tickets or predictions - and on the other hand \"edge, far end\", especially in 町の外れ or 町外れ \"outskirts of town\". As a suffix it is very productive: 期待外れ \"disappointing\", 季節外れ \"out of season\", 的外れ \"beside the point\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"世の中には、色々な人がいます。","romaji":"Yononaka ni wa, iroiro na hito ga imasu.","german":"There are all kinds of people in the world.","furigana":"よ|なか|いろいろ|ひと"},{"japanese":"世の中はそんなに甘くないよ。","romaji":"Yononaka wa sonna ni amaku nai yo.","german":"Life isn't that easy, you know.","furigana":"よ|なか|あま"},{"japanese":"世の中の役に立つ仕事がしたい。","romaji":"Yononaka no yaku ni tatsu shigoto ga shitai.","german":"I want to do work that is useful to society.","furigana":"よ|なか|やく|た|しごと"}],"notes":"世の中 means the world as the human society one lives in, with its rules and the spirit of the times - not the globe (世界). Typical phrases are 世の中は甘くない \"life is no picnic\", 世の中の役に立つ \"to be useful to society\" and 世の中が変わる. It sounds more everyday and emotional than the matter-of-fact 社会.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"感心なお子さんですね。","romaji":"Kanshin na okosan desu ne.","german":"What an admirable child you have.","furigana":"かんしん|こ"},{"japanese":"彼の日本語の上手さに感心した。","romaji":"Kare no nihongo no jouzusa ni kanshin shita.","german":"I was impressed by how good his Japanese is.","furigana":"かれ|にほんご|じょうず|かんしん"},{"japanese":"夜遅くまで騒ぐのは、あまり感心しないね。","romaji":"Yoru osoku made sawagu no wa, amari kanshin shinai ne.","german":"Making noise until late at night is not something I approve of much.","furigana":"よるおそ|さわ|かんしん"}],"notes":"感心 expresses appreciative impressedness: 〜に感心する \"to be impressed by something, to admire something\". As a な-adjective, 感心な means \"commendable\", often said of children or younger people; toward superiors it comes across as condescending, where 感動 or 感銘を受ける fit better. In the negative, あまり感心しない means \"not exactly approve of\". Not to be confused with the identically read 関心 \"interest\" (〜に関心がある).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの人、去年の冬に赤ちゃんを産んだばかりなのに今年の秋にもまた赤ちゃんを産むんですって。","romaji":"Ano hito, kyonen no fuyu ni akachan o unda bakari na noni kotoshi no aki ni mo mata akachan o umu n desu tte.","german":"I heard that she just had a baby last winter and is going to have another one this autumn.","furigana":"ひと|きょねん|ふゆ|あか|う|ことし|あき|あか|う"},{"japanese":"この鶏は毎朝卵を産みます。","romaji":"Kono niwatori wa maiasa tamago o umimasu.","german":"This hen lays an egg every morning.","furigana":"にわとり|まいあさたまご|う"},{"japanese":"姉は先月、元気な男の子を産みました。","romaji":"Ane wa sengetsu, genki na otoko no ko o umimashita.","german":"My sister gave birth to a healthy boy last month.","furigana":"あね|せんげつ|げんき|おとこ|こ|う"}],"notes":"産む is transitive: 子どもを産む \"to give birth to a child\", 卵を産む \"to lay eggs\". The intransitive counterpart is 生まれる \"to be born\". For the figurative \"to produce, to create\", 生む is usually written: 誤解を生む, 新記録を生む; 産む is reserved for actual birth. More formal and medical is 出産する.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その男の子は、うれしそうに口笛をふいていました。","romaji":"Sono otokonoko wa, ureshisō ni kuchibue o fuite imashita.","german":"The boy was whistling happily.","furigana":"おとこ|こ|くちぶえ"},{"japanese":"夜に口笛を吹くと蛇が来る、と祖母によく言われた。","romaji":"Yoru ni kuchibue o fuku to hebi ga kuru, to sobo ni yoku iwareta.","german":"My grandmother often told me that if you whistle at night, snakes will come.","furigana":"よる|くちぶえ|ふ|へび|く|そぼ|い"},{"japanese":"彼は口笛で犬を呼んだ。","romaji":"Kare wa kuchibue de inu o yonda.","german":"He called the dog with a whistle.","furigana":"かれ|くちぶえ|いぬ|よ"}],"notes":"口笛 is whistling with the lips; the verb is 吹く: 口笛を吹く, often written in kana as ふく. With で one can whistle something or call someone: 口笛で曲を吹く, 口笛で犬を呼ぶ. A whistle or a referee's whistle, on the other hand, is called 笛 or ホイッスル. A well-known superstition says that whistling at night attracts snakes.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"映画好きが集まるパーティーの最年長参加者は、なんと百歳だった。","romaji":"Eigazuki ga atsumaru pātī no sainenchō sankasha wa, nanto hyakusai datta.","german":"The oldest participant at the party for movie lovers was, believe it or not, a hundred years old.","furigana":"えいがず|あつ|さいねんちょうさんかしゃ|ひゃくさい"},{"japanese":"参加者は全員、名札をつけてください。","romaji":"Sankasha wa zen'in, nafuda o tsukete kudasai.","german":"All participants, please wear a name tag.","furigana":"さんかしゃ|ぜんいん|なふだ"},{"japanese":"今年のマラソン大会は、参加者が一万人を超えました。","romaji":"Kotoshi no marason taikai wa, sankasha ga ichiman-nin o koemashita.","german":"This year's marathon had more than ten thousand participants.","furigana":"ことし|たいかい|さんかしゃ|いちまんにん|こ"}],"notes":"参加者 are the \"participants\" in an event, course or competition, formed from 参加 + 者. Typical are 〜の参加者, 参加者を募集する and 参加者数. For people present at meetings or in class one rather says 出席者, for participants in competitions also 出場者, for visitors to an event 来場者. The word is neutral and sounds somewhat formal in announcements.","levelNote":"Compound: 参加 (N3) + 者 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"そういう発言がみんなの和を乱しているってこと、気づいてないの？","romaji":"Sou iu hatsugen ga minna no wa o midashite iru tte koto, kizuitenai no?","german":"Don't you realize that remarks like that are disturbing everyone's harmony?","furigana":"はつげん|わ|みだ|き"},{"japanese":"列を乱さないで、順番に並んでください。","romaji":"Retsu o midasanaide, junban ni narande kudasai.","german":"Please don't break the line; line up in order.","furigana":"れつ|みだ|じゅんばん|なら"},{"japanese":"強い風が彼女の髪を乱した。","romaji":"Tsuyoi kaze ga kanojo no kami o midashita.","german":"The strong wind tousled her hair.","furigana":"つよ|かぜ|かのじょ|かみ|みだ"}],"notes":"乱す is transitive and means \"to throw into disorder, to disturb\"; the intransitive counterpart is 乱れる \"to become disordered\". Typical objects are 列を乱す \"to break ranks\", 秩序を乱す \"to disturb order\", 和を乱す \"to disturb harmony\", 髪を乱す \"to tousle hair\" and 心を乱す \"to unsettle\". Unlike 邪魔する, it is less about obstruction than about destroying an order.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"宇宙人と友達になってはいけないと言われて、余計になりたくなった。","romaji":"Uchuujin to tomodachi ni natte wa ikenai to iwarete, yokei ni naritaku natta.","german":"When I was told not to become friends with an alien, I wanted to even more.","furigana":"うちゅうじん|ともだち|い|よけい"},{"japanese":"宇宙人は本当にいると思いますか。","romaji":"Uchuujin wa hontou ni iru to omoimasu ka.","german":"Do you think aliens really exist?","furigana":"うちゅうじん|ほんとう|おも"},{"japanese":"彼は考え方が独特で、みんなから宇宙人と呼ばれている。","romaji":"Kare wa kangaekata ga dokutoku de, minna kara uchuujin to yobarete iru.","german":"He thinks in such a unique way that everyone calls him an alien.","furigana":"かれ|かんが|かた|どくとく|うちゅうじん|よ"}],"notes":"宇宙人 refers to a being from another planet, \"extraterrestrial\", formed from 宇宙 \"outer space\" and 人. It is the normal everyday word; エイリアン sounds more like a science-fiction monster. Colloquially, a very eccentric person is also jokingly called 宇宙人: あの人は宇宙人みたいだ.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 20"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"敵の突然の攻撃で、宇宙船の中は大混乱になった。","romaji":"Teki no totsuzen no kougeki de, uchuusen no naka wa daikonran ni natta.","german":"Because of the enemy's sudden attack, the inside of the spaceship was thrown into great confusion.","furigana":"てき|とつぜん|こうげき|うちゅうせん|なか|だいこんらん"},{"japanese":"宇宙船の窓から地球が青く見えた。","romaji":"Uchuusen no mado kara chikyuu ga aoku mieta.","german":"Through the window of the spaceship, the Earth looked blue.","furigana":"うちゅうせん|まど|ちきゅう|あお|み"},{"japanese":"将来、宇宙船で月へ旅行できるかもしれない。","romaji":"Shourai, uchuusen de tsuki e ryokou dekiru kamo shirenai.","german":"In the future, we may be able to travel to the moon by spaceship.","furigana":"しょうらい|うちゅうせん|つき|りょこう"}],"notes":"宇宙船 is a \"spaceship\", especially a crewed vehicle for travel in space – in reality as in science fiction. One says 宇宙船に乗る and 宇宙船で〜へ行く. The launch rocket, by contrast, is called ロケット, a space station 宇宙ステーション, and a UFO (ユーフォー) is an unidentified flying object.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 26"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"アマゾンから、注文より二足余計に履き物が送られてきました。","romaji":"Amazon kara, chuumon yori nisoku yokei ni hakimono ga okurarete kimashita.","german":"Amazon sent me two more pairs of footwear than I ordered.","furigana":"ちゅうもん|にそくよけい|は|もの|おく"},{"japanese":"玄関で履き物を脱いでください。","romaji":"Genkan de hakimono o nuide kudasai.","german":"Please take off your footwear in the entrance.","furigana":"げんかん|は|もの|ぬ"},{"japanese":"下駄や草履などの日本の履き物を集めています。","romaji":"Geta ya zouri nado no nihon no hakimono o atsumete imasu.","german":"I collect Japanese footwear such as geta and zori.","furigana":"げた|ぞうり|にほん|は|もの|あつ"}],"notes":"履き物 (also 履物) is the umbrella term for everything worn on the feet: 靴, サンダル, 下駄, 草履 and so on. It sounds somewhat more formal than 靴 and often appears on signs: 履き物をお脱ぎください. Typical uses are 履き物を脱ぐ and 履き物をそろえる \"to line up the shoes you took off neatly\". The corresponding verb is 履く.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"急なルール変更に、多くの利用者が混乱した。","romaji":"Kyuu na ruuru henkou ni, ooku no riyousha ga konran shita.","german":"Many users were confused by the sudden rule change.","furigana":"きゅう|へんこう|おお|りようしゃ|こんらん"},{"japanese":"事故のせいで、駅は大混乱していた。","romaji":"Jiko no sei de, eki wa daikonran shite ita.","german":"The station was in total chaos because of the accident.","furigana":"じこ|えき|だいこんらん"},{"japanese":"一度にたくさん説明されて、頭が混乱してしまった。","romaji":"Ichido ni takusan setsumei sarete, atama ga konran shite shimatta.","german":"So much was explained to me at once that my head was in a muddle.","furigana":"いちど|せつめい|あたま|こんらん"}],"notes":"混乱する is intransitive and describes two things: a situation falls into disorder (駅が混乱する, 交通が混乱する), or a person is confused (頭が混乱する). The cause is marked with に or で: 急な変更に混乱する. Intensified, it is 大混乱. To confuse someone is 混乱させる. Not to be confused with 混雑 \"crowding, congestion\".","levelNote":"Derived from 混乱 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"しりとりに固有名詞は使えません。","romaji":"Shiritori ni koyūmeishi wa tsukaemasen.","german":"You can't use proper nouns in shiritori.","furigana":"こゆうめいし|つか"},{"japanese":"「東京」や「富士山」は固有名詞です。","romaji":"\"Toukyou\" ya \"Fujisan\" wa koyuumeishi desu.","german":"\"Tokyo\" and \"Mt. Fuji\" are proper nouns.","furigana":"とうきょう|ふじさん|こゆうめいし"},{"japanese":"日本語では固有名詞を大文字で書かないので、分かりにくいことがある。","romaji":"Nihongo de wa koyuumeishi o oomoji de kakanai node, wakarinikui koto ga aru.","german":"Japanese does not capitalize proper nouns, so they can sometimes be hard to recognize.","furigana":"にほんご|こゆうめいし|おおもじ|か|わ"}],"notes":"固有名詞 is the grammatical term for proper nouns such as names of people, places or companies; the counterpart is 普通名詞 \"common noun\". Since Japanese has no capitalization, proper nouns can only be recognized from context or from katakana and readings. In everyday life you hear the word, for example, in the rules of games like しりとり or when using a dictionary.","levelNote":"Compound: 固有 (N1) + 名詞 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"余計な事を言うようだけど、暴力団から足を洗った方がいいと思うよ。","romaji":"Yokei na koto o iu yō da kedo, bōryokudan kara ashi o aratta hō ga ii to omou yo.","german":"I know it's not my place to say, but I think you should leave the yakuza.","furigana":"よけい|こと|い|ぼうりょくだん|あし|あら|ほう|おも"},{"japanese":"警察は暴力団の事務所を調べた。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa bouryokudan no jimusho o shirabeta.","german":"The police searched the office of the crime syndicate.","furigana":"けいさつ|ぼうりょくだん|じむしょ|しら"},{"japanese":"このお店は暴力団関係者の入店をお断りしています。","romaji":"Kono omise wa bouryokudan kankeisha no nyuuten o okotowari shite imasu.","german":"People connected to organized crime are refused entry to this shop.","furigana":"みせ|ぼうりょくだんかんけいしゃ|にゅうてん|ことわ"}],"notes":"暴力団 is the official term used by the police, in laws and in the news for yakuza organizations. Colloquially, ヤクザ is said; in contracts and on signs you also read 反社会的勢力 (short: 反社). Members are called 暴力団員. Typical expressions are 暴力団に入る and, for leaving, 暴力団から足を洗う.","levelNote":"Compound: 暴力 (N2) + 団 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"暴走族は、アライグマをひいたことを認めた。","romaji":"Bōsōzoku wa, araiguma o hiita koto o mitometa.","german":"The motorcycle gang admitted to running over the raccoon.","furigana":"ぼうそうぞく|みと"},{"japanese":"夜中に暴走族のバイクの音がうるさくて眠れなかった。","romaji":"Yonaka ni bousouzoku no baiku no oto ga urusakute nemurenakatta.","german":"In the middle of the night I couldn't sleep because of the noise of a biker gang's motorcycles.","furigana":"よなか|ぼうそうぞく|おと|ねむ"},{"japanese":"彼は若いころ暴走族に入っていたが、今はまじめに働いている。","romaji":"Kare wa wakai koro bousouzoku ni haitte ita ga, ima wa majime ni hataraite iru.","german":"He was in a motorcycle gang when he was young, but now he works honestly.","furigana":"かれ|わか|ぼうそうぞく|はい|いま|はたら"}],"notes":"暴走族 are youth motorcycle or car gangs who drive noisily and recklessly through the streets at night; formed from 暴走 \"to race, to drive out of control\" and 族 \"tribe, group\". Typical phrases: 暴走族に入る, 暴走族のバイク. The phenomenon was especially widespread in the 1970s and 80s; today the word is known mainly from the news and films.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いい加減にしろ！最近判断ミスが多すぎるぞ。","romaji":"Ii kagen ni shiro! Saikin handan misu ga oosugiru zo.","german":"Knock it off! You've made too many errors of judgment lately.","furigana":"かげん|さいきんはんだん|おお"},{"japanese":"「いい加減にしろよ！」と兄が弟を怒鳴った。","romaji":"\"Ii kagen ni shiro yo!\" to ani ga otouto o donatta.","german":"\"Enough is enough!\" the older brother yelled at his younger brother.","furigana":"かげん|あに|おとうと|どな"},{"japanese":"何度同じことを言わせるんだ。いい加減にしろ。","romaji":"Nando onaji koto o iwaseru n da. Ii kagen ni shiro.","german":"How many times do I have to say the same thing? Knock it off.","furigana":"なんどおな|い|かげん"}],"notes":"いい加減にしろ is the rough imperative of いい加減にする \"to let it be enough\" and means \"That's enough!, Stop it!\". It sounds harsh and rather masculine; it is shouted in anger when someone keeps doing something over and over. Somewhat softer are いい加減にして (a request), いい加減にしなさい (parents to children) or いい加減にしてください. Not to be confused with the adjective いい加減な \"careless, sloppy\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"湿気はカメラの大敵です。","romaji":"Shikke wa kamera no taiteki desu.","german":"Humidity is a camera's worst enemy.","furigana":"しっけ|たいてき"},{"japanese":"リードしていても、油断大敵だよ。","romaji":"Riido shite ite mo, yudan taiteki da yo.","german":"Even if we're in the lead, carelessness is the great enemy.","furigana":"ゆだんたいてき"},{"japanese":"寝不足は美容の大敵だと言われている。","romaji":"Nebusoku wa biyou no taiteki da to iwarete iru.","german":"Lack of sleep is said to be the enemy of beauty.","furigana":"ねぶそく|びよう|たいてき|い"}],"notes":"大敵 is a \"great, dangerous enemy\". In everyday life it is almost always used figuratively in the pattern AはBの大敵: 湿気はカメラの大敵 \"humidity is the enemy of any camera\". Very well known is the proverb 油断大敵 \"carelessness is the greatest enemy\". For a personal rival, ライバル is used more often; an archenemy in the sense of a mortal enemy is 宿敵.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"しりとりで、コウイチは無敵だ。","romaji":"Shiritori de, Kouichi wa muteki da.","german":"Koichi is invincible at shiritori.","furigana":"むてき"},{"japanese":"そのチームは今年一度も負けていない、無敵のチームだ。","romaji":"Sono chiimu wa kotoshi ichido mo makete inai, muteki no chiimu da.","german":"That team hasn't lost once this year; it is an invincible team.","furigana":"ことしいちど|ま|むてき"},{"japanese":"ゲームでこのアイテムを取ると、しばらく無敵になる。","romaji":"Geemu de kono aitemu o toru to, shibaraku muteki ni naru.","german":"In the game, if you pick up this item, you become invincible for a while.","furigana":"と|むてき"}],"notes":"無敵 means \"invincible, without an equal opponent\" (literally \"no enemy\"). It is used predicatively with だ or attributively with の: 無敵のチーム; less often with な. In games, 無敵 also denotes the state of invulnerability: 無敵になる. Intensified there is 天下無敵 \"invincible in the whole world\". Merely undefeated so far, on the other hand, is 負けなし.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今やっと仕事が済んだところだよ。","romaji":"Ima yatto shigoto ga sunda tokoro da yo.","german":"I have just now finally finished my work.","furigana":"いま|しごと|す"},{"japanese":"シートベルトをしていたので、軽いけがで済みました。","romaji":"Shiitoberuto o shite ita node, karui kega de sumimashita.","german":"I was wearing a seat belt, so I got off with a minor injury.","furigana":"かる|す"},{"japanese":"友達が車で送ってくれたので、タクシーを使わずに済みました。","romaji":"Tomodachi ga kuruma de okutte kureta node, takushii o tsukawazu ni sumimashita.","german":"A friend drove me, so I did not have to take a taxi.","furigana":"ともだち|くるま|おく|つか|す"}],"notes":"済む means \"to be done, to be over\", with the matter as the subject: 仕事が済む, 用事が済む; in contrast to 終わる, it emphasizes that something is checked off. Also important is the meaning \"to get off lightly, to get by with something\": 軽いけがで済む, and ～ずに済む or ～なくて済む \"not to have to do something\". The transitive counterpart is 済ませる or 済ます \"to finish, to get done\"; the apology すみません is originally the negated form of this verb. Do not confuse it with the identically sounding 住む \"to live\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの委員は、すぐれた判断力を持っていますね。","romaji":"Ano iin wa, sugureta handanryoku o motte imasu ne.","german":"That committee member has excellent judgment, doesn't he?","furigana":"いいん|はんだんりょく|も"},{"japanese":"クラスで図書委員に選ばれました。","romaji":"Kurasu de tosho iin ni erabaremashita.","german":"I was elected library committee member in my class.","furigana":"としょいいん|えら"},{"japanese":"その計画には、委員の半分以上が反対しました。","romaji":"Sono keikaku ni wa, iin no hanbun ijou ga hantai shimashita.","german":"More than half of the committee members opposed the plan.","furigana":"けいかく|いいん|はんぶんいじょう|はんたい"}],"notes":"委員 is a member of a committee; the committee itself is called 委員会, and its chairperson 委員長. At school one encounters the word constantly: 学級委員 \"class representative\", 図書委員 \"library duty member\", 保健委員 \"health duty member\". One is 委員に選ばれる \"elected as a member\". Not to be confused with 会員 \"member of an association or club\" or the general メンバー.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先生がそう言うと、たくさん手が挙がった。","romaji":"Sensei ga sō iu to, takusan te ga agatta.","german":"When the teacher said that, many hands went up.","furigana":"せんせい|い|て|あ"},{"japanese":"次のリーダーの候補として、田中さんの名前が挙がっています。","romaji":"Tsugi no riidaa no kouho to shite, Tanaka-san no namae ga agatte imasu.","german":"Mr. Tanaka's name has come up as a candidate for the next leader.","furigana":"つぎ|こうほ|たなか|なまえ|あ"},{"japanese":"新しい証拠が挙がって、事件はやっと解決した。","romaji":"Atarashii shouko ga agatte, jiken wa yatto kaiketsu shita.","german":"New evidence turned up, and the case was finally solved.","furigana":"あたら|しょうこ|あ|じけん|かいけつ"}],"notes":"挙がる is the intransitive counterpart of 挙げる \"to raise, to name\": 手が挙がる \"hands go up\", 名前が挙がる \"a name is mentioned / is under discussion\", 例が挙がる \"an example is given\", 証拠が挙がる \"evidence comes to light\", 犯人が挙がる \"the culprit is caught\". For general upward movement (prices, stairs, temperature), 上がる is written; for raising hands, both spellings are seen, with 挙がる emphasizing the deliberate raising.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"高校の制服はセーラー服だった？それともブレザーだった？","romaji":"Kōkō no seifuku wa sērāfuku datta? Soretomo burezā datta?","german":"Was your high school uniform a sailor suit or a blazer?","furigana":"こうこう|せいふく|ふく"},{"japanese":"うちの会社では、受付の人は制服を着なければなりません。","romaji":"Uchi no kaisha de wa, uketsuke no hito wa seifuku o kinakereba narimasen.","german":"At our company, the receptionists have to wear a uniform.","furigana":"かいしゃ|うけつけ|ひと|せいふく|き"},{"japanese":"その警官は制服を着ていなかったので、気づきませんでした。","romaji":"Sono keikan wa seifuku o kite inakatta node, kizukimasen deshita.","german":"That police officer was not wearing a uniform, so I didn't notice.","furigana":"けいかん|せいふく|き|き"}],"notes":"制服 is a prescribed uniform, for example of schools, police, railway staff or companies: 制服を着る, 制服姿 \"in uniform\". The opposite is 私服 \"civilian or casual clothes\", e.g. 私服の警官 \"plainclothes police officer\". For sports jerseys one rather says ユニフォーム. Japanese school uniforms are typically セーラー服 \"sailor suit\" or ブレザー \"blazer\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"えー、でも、強制されて学んだ知識って、すぐ忘れちゃうじゃん？","romaji":"Ee, demo, kyousei sarete mananda chishiki tte, sugu wasurechau jan?","german":"Well, but knowledge you learned under compulsion is quickly forgotten, isn't it?","furigana":"きょうせい|まな|ちしき|わす"},{"japanese":"参加は強制ではありませんので、ご自由にどうぞ。","romaji":"Sanka wa kyousei de wa arimasen node, gojiyuu ni douzo.","german":"Participation is not mandatory, so feel free to join as you like.","furigana":"さんか|きょうせい|じゆう"},{"japanese":"アプリが動かなくなったので、強制終了しました。","romaji":"Apuri ga ugokanaku natta node, kyousei shuuryou shimashita.","german":"The app stopped responding, so I force-quit it.","furigana":"うご|きょうせいしゅうりょう"}],"notes":"強制 is \"compulsion\"; as a する-verb it means \"to force someone to do something\": 人に〜を強制する. Very common are the passive 強制される \"to be forced\" and the adverb 強制的に \"forcibly\". On a computer, 強制終了 means the forced termination of a program. Colloquially one rather says 無理やり〜させる; 強制 sounds matter-of-fact to official, often in 強制ではない \"not obligatory\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この事務所では、喫煙は認められていません。","romaji":"Kono jimusho de wa, kitsuen wa mitomerarete imasen.","german":"Smoking is not permitted in this office.","furigana":"じむしょ|きつえん|みと"},{"japanese":"兄は弁護士事務所で働いています。","romaji":"Ani wa bengoshi jimusho de hataraite imasu.","german":"My older brother works at a law office.","furigana":"あに|べんごしじむしょ|はたら"},{"japanese":"書類は明日事務所に届けます。","romaji":"Shorui wa ashita jimusho ni todokemasu.","german":"I'll bring the documents to the office tomorrow.","furigana":"しょるい|あしたじむしょ|とど"}],"notes":"事務所 is an office as a workplace, often the whole office of a small company or firm: 弁護士事務所, 設計事務所, 芸能事務所 (talent agency). For the office in a large company, you also say オフィス, and for the administration in schools or buildings, 事務室. 会社, by contrast, is the company as an organization, not the room.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"美味しい混ぜご飯を作ることが、あなたの任務です。","romaji":"Oishii maze gohan o tsukuru koto ga, anata no ninmu desu.","german":"Making delicious mixed rice is your mission.","furigana":"おい|ま|はん|つく|にんむ"},{"japanese":"彼は危険な任務を無事に果たしました。","romaji":"Kare wa kiken na ninmu o buji ni hatashimashita.","german":"He accomplished the dangerous mission safely.","furigana":"かれ|きけん|にんむ|ぶじ|は"},{"japanese":"宇宙飛行士たちは、次の任務のために訓練を受けている。","romaji":"Uchuu hikoushi-tachi wa, tsugi no ninmu no tame ni kunren o ukete iru.","german":"The astronauts are undergoing training for their next mission.","furigana":"うちゅうひこうし|つぎ|にんむ|くんれん|う"}],"notes":"任務 is an officially assigned task, a mission - typical in the military, police, space travel or in games and films. Fixed phrases: 任務を果たす \"to fulfill the mission\", 任務を与える \"to give an assignment\", 任務に就く \"to take up a post\". For ordinary work one says 仕事, for a role (even an everyday one) 役目; 義務 is a duty. In everyday life, 任務 therefore often sounds jokingly solemn.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このサイトは、総合すると良くできていると思います。","romaji":"Kono saito wa, sougou suru to yoku dekite iru to omoimasu.","german":"Taking everything together, I think this site is well made.","furigana":"そうごう|よ|おも"},{"japanese":"父は駅前の総合病院で働いています。","romaji":"Chichi wa ekimae no sougou byouin de hataraite imasu.","german":"My father works at the general hospital in front of the station.","furigana":"ちち|えきまえ|そうごうびょういん|はたら"},{"japanese":"彼女はテストの総合点でクラス一位になった。","romaji":"Kanojo wa tesuto no sougouten de kurasu ichii ni natta.","german":"She came first in the class on the overall test score.","furigana":"かのじょ|そうごうてん|いちい"}],"notes":"総合 means \"summary, totality\". As a verb, 総合する means \"to sum up, to put together\", especially in 総合すると \"all in all\" and 総合的に \"comprehensively, holistically\". It is very common as a prefix \"overall, general\": 総合病院 \"general hospital\", 総合大学 \"comprehensive university\", 総合点 \"total score\", 総合優勝 \"overall victory\". In contrast to 全体, 総合 emphasizes bringing different parts together.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この旅では色々な事件が起きましたが、総体的にはとても楽しかったです。","romaji":"Kono tabi de wa iroiro na jiken ga okimashita ga, soutaiteki ni wa totemo tanoshikatta desu.","german":"Various incidents happened on this trip, but overall it was very enjoyable.","furigana":"たび|いろいろ|じけん|お|そうたいてき|たの"},{"japanese":"今年の売り上げは、総体的に見て悪くなかった。","romaji":"Kotoshi no uriage wa, soutaiteki ni mite warukunakatta.","german":"Looking at it overall, this year's sales were not bad.","furigana":"ことし|う|あ|そうたいてき|み|わる"},{"japanese":"総体的な印象としては、明るくて親切な町でした。","romaji":"Soutaiteki na inshou to shite wa, akarukute shinsetsu na machi deshita.","german":"My overall impression was of a bright and friendly town.","furigana":"そうたいてき|いんしょう|あか|しんせつ|まち"}],"notes":"総体的 is a na-adjective meaning \"overall, on the whole\" and is mostly used adverbially: 総体的に(は), 総体的に見て. It sounds bookish; in everyday speech one rather says 全体的に or 全体として. Note: the identically pronounced 相対的 means \"relative\" - the opposite of 絶対的 - and is much more common. So pay close attention to the kanji when typing.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの会社がついに日本に支店を設けるといううわさを聞いたんだけど、本当かな？","romaji":"Ano kaisha ga tsui ni Nihon ni shiten o moukeru to iu uwasa o kiita n da kedo, hontou ka na?","german":"I heard a rumor that that company is finally opening a branch in Japan; is it true?","furigana":"かいしゃ|にほん|してん|もう|き|ほんとう"},{"japanese":"駅の中に、観光客のための案内所が設けられています。","romaji":"Eki no naka ni, kankoukyaku no tame no annaijo ga moukerarete imasu.","german":"An information center for tourists has been set up inside the station.","furigana":"えき|なか|かんこうきゃく|あんないじょ|もう"},{"japanese":"この学校では、スマホの使い方に厳しいルールを設けている。","romaji":"Kono gakkou de wa, sumaho no tsukaikata ni kibishii ruuru o moukete iru.","german":"This school has strict rules on how to use smartphones.","furigana":"がっこう|つか|かた|きび|もう"}],"notes":"設ける is a formal transitive verb for \"to set up, to create, to provide for\": 支店を設ける \"to open a branch\", 窓口を設ける \"to set up a contact point\", ルールを設ける \"to establish rules\", 機会を設ける \"to create an opportunity\", 席を設ける \"to arrange a meeting\". In the passive, 〜が設けられている describes existing facilities. In everyday speech it often corresponds to 作る. Not to be confused with the similar-sounding 儲ける \"to make money\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"来年度から、公設の委員会を設けることになりました。","romaji":"Rainendo kara, kōsetsu no iinkai o mōkeru koto ni narimashita.","german":"Starting next fiscal year, a public committee will be set up.","furigana":"らいねんど|こうせつ|いいんかい|もう"},{"japanese":"町の公設市場では、新鮮な魚が安く買えます。","romaji":"Machi no kousetsu ichiba de wa, shinsen na sakana ga yasuku kaemasu.","german":"At the town's public market, you can buy fresh fish cheaply.","furigana":"まち|こうせついちば|しんせん|さかな|やす|か"},{"japanese":"この体育館は市の公設の施設なので、誰でも利用できます。","romaji":"Kono taiikukan wa shi no kousetsu no shisetsu na node, dare demo riyou dekimasu.","german":"This gymnasium is a public facility of the city, so anyone can use it.","furigana":"たいいくかん|し|こうせつ|しせつ|だれ|りよう"}],"notes":"公設 means \"established by the public sector (state, city, municipality)\" and almost always appears with の or as a prefix: 公設市場 \"municipal market\", 公設の施設 \"public facility\", 公設秘書 \"publicly paid secretary of a legislator\". The opposite is 私設 \"privately established\". For public schools and hospitals, 公立 is used instead, and for \"public\" in the general sense 公共 (公共交通機関).","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい会社を作るには、ある程度の資本が必要です。","romaji":"Atarashii kaisha o tsukuru ni wa, aru teido no shihon ga hitsuyou desu.","german":"To start a new company, you need a certain amount of capital.","furigana":"あたら|かいしゃ|つく|ていど|しほん|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"この会社には外国の資本が入っています。","romaji":"Kono kaisha ni wa gaikoku no shihon ga haitte imasu.","german":"Foreign capital has come into this company.","furigana":"かいしゃ|がいこく|しほん|はい"},{"japanese":"この仕事は体が資本だから、よく寝るようにしている。","romaji":"Kono shigoto wa karada ga shihon da kara, yoku neru you ni shite iru.","german":"In this job your body is your capital, so I make sure to sleep well.","furigana":"しごと|からだ|しほん|ね"}],"notes":"資本 is \"capital\" in the economic sense. Common compounds are 資本金 (\"capital stock\"), 資本主義 (\"capitalism\") and 外国資本, shortened 外資 (\"foreign capital\"). For money for a specific purpose one rather says 資金, and colloquially for start-up money 元手. Very common is the expression 体が資本 (\"health is the most important capital\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の父は岡山県出身です。","romaji":"Watashi no chichi wa Okayamaken shusshin desu.","german":"My father is from Okayama Prefecture.","furigana":"わたし|ちち|おかやまけんしゅっしん"},{"japanese":"岡山県は桃やぶどうが有名です。","romaji":"Okayamaken wa momo ya budou ga yuumei desu.","german":"Okayama Prefecture is famous for peaches and grapes.","furigana":"おかやまけん|もも|ゆうめい"},{"japanese":"夏休みに、岡山県の倉敷へ旅行しました。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi ni, Okayamaken no Kurashiki e ryokou shimashita.","german":"In the summer vacation I traveled to Kurashiki in Okayama Prefecture.","furigana":"なつやす|おかやまけん||りょこう"}],"notes":"岡山県 is a prefecture in the Chūgoku region of western Japan, with the capital 岡山市. It is known for the legend of 桃太郎, for peaches and muscat grapes, the garden 後楽園 and the old town of 倉敷; because of its sunny weather it calls itself 晴れの国. Typical combinations: 岡山県出身 \"from Okayama\", 岡山県の〜. Without 県, 岡山 often means the city.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"フォローは任意です。","romaji":"Forō wa nin'i desu.","german":"Following is optional.","furigana":"にんい"},{"japanese":"任意の数を一つ選んでください。","romaji":"Nin'i no kazu o hitotsu erande kudasai.","german":"Please choose any number.","furigana":"にんい|かず|ひと|えら"},{"japanese":"車を買ったら、任意保険にも入ったほうがいいですよ。","romaji":"Kuruma o kattara, nin'i hoken ni mo haitta hou ga ii desu yo.","german":"If you buy a car, you should also take out voluntary insurance.","furigana":"くるま|か|にんいほけん|はい"}],"notes":"任意 means \"voluntary, optional, at one's own discretion\" and is used with です, の or で: 参加は任意です, 任意の項目, 任意で提出する. On forms, 任意 is the opposite of 必須 \"required field\". In mathematics and technology, 任意の means \"arbitrary\" (任意の数). Fixed terms are 任意保険 \"voluntary supplementary car insurance\" and 任意同行 \"voluntarily accompanying the police\". The tone is matter-of-fact and official.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ほら、あそこで警察が検問しているよ。","romaji":"Hora, asoko de keisatsu ga kenmon shite iru yo.","german":"Look, the police are checking cars over there.","furigana":"けいさつ|けんもん"},{"japanese":"夜、飲酒運転の検問で車を止められました。","romaji":"Yoru, inshu unten no kenmon de kuruma o tomeraremashita.","german":"At night I was stopped at a drunk-driving checkpoint.","furigana":"よる|いんしゅうんてん|けんもん|くるま|と"},{"japanese":"国境では、兵士が車を一台ずつ検問していた。","romaji":"Kokkyou de wa, heishi ga kuruma o ichidai zutsu kenmon shite ita.","german":"At the border, soldiers inspected the cars one by one.","furigana":"こっきょう|へいし|くるま|いちだい|けんもん"}],"notes":"検問 is a check at a roadblock, above all road or border control by police or military; the place is called 検問所 \"checkpoint\". As a verb: 警察が車を検問する, often also without an object (検問している). Typical is 飲酒検問 \"drunk-driving checkpoint\". For technical inspections one says 検査 or 点検, for everyday checking チェックする.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「自分でちゃんとチェックするべきだったよ。」「そんなに自分を責めないで。あなたのせいじゃないわよ。」","romaji":"Jibun de chanto chekku suru beki datta yo. Sonna ni jibun o semenaide. Anata no sei ja nai wa yo.","german":"\"I should have checked it properly myself.\" \"Don't blame yourself so much. It's not your fault.\"","furigana":"じぶん|じぶん|せ"},{"japanese":"彼は遅刻したことで、上司に責められた。","romaji":"Kare wa chikoku shita koto de, joushi ni semerareta.","german":"He was reproached by his boss for being late.","furigana":"かれ|ちこく|じょうし|せ"},{"japanese":"失敗した人を責めても、問題は解決しないよ。","romaji":"Shippai shita hito o semete mo, mondai wa kaiketsu shinai yo.","german":"Blaming the person who failed won't solve the problem.","furigana":"しっぱい|ひと|せ|もんだい|かいけつ"}],"notes":"責める means \"to reproach someone, to put the blame on someone\": 人を責める, 〜のことで責める, often reflexive 自分を責める \"to blame oneself\". It is about blame, not about upbringing - for scolding by parents or teachers one says 叱る; 非難する is public, formal criticism. Not to be confused with the identically pronounced 攻める \"to attack\" (sports, war).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"最近はとても忙しいので、夕食を作る手間を省いてテイクアウトばかりしています。","romaji":"Saikin wa totemo isogashii node, yūshoku o tsukuru tema o habuite teikuauto bakari shite imasu.","german":"I've been very busy lately, so I skip the trouble of cooking dinner and just get takeout.","furigana":"さいきん|いそが|ゆうしょく|つく|てま|はぶ"},{"japanese":"時間がないので、詳しい説明は省きます。","romaji":"Jikan ga nai node, kuwashii setsumei wa habukimasu.","german":"Since I'm short on time, I will omit the detailed explanation.","furigana":"じかん|くわ|せつめい|はぶ"},{"japanese":"無駄を省いて、費用を減らしましょう。","romaji":"Muda o habuite, hiyou o herashimashou.","german":"Let's cut out waste and reduce costs.","furigana":"むだ|はぶ|ひよう|へ"}],"notes":"省く is transitive and means \"to omit, to save\": 説明を省く \"to omit the explanation\", 手間を省く \"to save oneself trouble\", 無駄を省く \"to cut out waste\", politely 詳しいことは省きます. For abbreviating words one says 略す, for skipping a meal rather 抜く (朝ご飯を抜く), for excluding people 除く.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"近年の、一人暮らしの若者の急増について、先生はどうお考えですか。","romaji":"Kinnen no, hitorigurashi no wakamono no kyuuzou ni tsuite, sensei wa dou okangae desu ka.","german":"What are your thoughts, Professor, on the recent surge in young people living alone?","furigana":"きんねん|ひとりぐ|わかもの|きゅうぞう|せんせい|かんが"},{"japanese":"最近、外国人観光客が急増しています。","romaji":"Saikin, gaikokujin kankoukyaku ga kyuuzou shite imasu.","german":"The number of foreign tourists has been rising sharply lately.","furigana":"さいきん|がいこくじんかんこうきゃく|きゅうぞう"},{"japanese":"ネットでの買い物が急増したため、配達が遅れています。","romaji":"Netto de no kaimono ga kyuuzou shita tame, haitatsu ga okurete imasu.","german":"Because online shopping has surged, deliveries are delayed.","furigana":"か|もの|きゅうぞう|はいたつ|おく"}],"notes":"急増 means \"abrupt increase\"; as a verb it is mostly intransitive: 観光客が急増する, as a noun 〜の急増. The word belongs to the language of news and reports; in conversation one rather says 急に増える. The opposite is 急減 \"sharp decline\"; more neutral is 増加, even stronger 激増.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"増税するなんて、ゼッタイに認めません。","romaji":"Zōzei suru nante, zettai ni mitomemasen.","german":"I will never accept a tax increase.","furigana":"ぞうぜい|みと"},{"japanese":"政府は来年、消費税の増税を予定しています。","romaji":"Seifu wa rainen, shouhizei no zouzei o yotei shite imasu.","german":"The government plans to raise the consumption tax next year.","furigana":"せいふ|らいねん|しょうひぜい|ぞうぜい|よてい"},{"japanese":"増税の前に、冷蔵庫を買い替えておこう。","romaji":"Zouzei no mae ni, reizouko o kaikaete okou.","german":"Let's replace the refrigerator before the tax increase.","furigana":"ぞうぜい|まえ|れいぞうこ|か|か"}],"notes":"増税 is the \"tax increase\"; as a verb, the state raises taxes: 政府が増税する, often with the name of the tax: 消費税の増税 \"increase of the consumption tax\". The opposite is 減税 \"tax cut\". The word is typical of politics and news; in everyday speech one also says 税金が上がる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お酒が無税だったらいいのにな。","romaji":"O sake ga muzei dattara ii noni na.","german":"I wish alcohol were tax-free.","furigana":"さけ|むぜい"},{"japanese":"この国では、本は無税で輸入できます。","romaji":"Kono kuni de wa, hon wa muzei de yunyuu dekimasu.","german":"In this country, books can be imported duty-free.","furigana":"くに|ほん|むぜい|ゆにゅう"},{"japanese":"昔、この島の住民は無税だったそうです。","romaji":"Mukashi, kono shima no juumin wa muzei datta sou desu.","german":"The residents of this island are said to have paid no taxes in the past.","furigana":"むかし|しま|じゅうみん|むぜい"}],"notes":"無税 means \"tax-free, duty-free\" in the sense of \"no tax at all is levied here\"; it is used with の, で or だ: 無税の品, 無税で輸入する. More common in everyday life are 免税 \"tax exemption\" (免税店 \"duty-free shop\") and 非課税 \"non-taxable\" (非課税の収入). The opposite is 課税 \"taxation\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"実際、宇宙人が人ごみに混じっていても、だれも気が付かないだろう。","romaji":"Jissai, uchuujin ga hitogomi ni majitte ite mo, dare mo ki ga tsukanai darou.","german":"In fact, even if an alien were mingling with the crowd, nobody would notice.","furigana":"じっさい|うちゅうじん|ひと|ま|き|つ"},{"japanese":"写真ではきれいに見えたけど、実際に行ってみたら狭い部屋だった。","romaji":"Shashin de wa kirei ni mieta kedo, jissai ni itte mitara semai heya datta.","german":"It looked nice in the photos, but when I actually went there, it was a small room.","furigana":"しゃしん|み|じっさい|い|せま|へや"},{"japanese":"実際のところ、まだ何も決まっていません。","romaji":"Jissai no tokoro, mada nani mo kimatte imasen.","german":"In actual fact, nothing has been decided yet.","furigana":"じっさい|なに|き"}],"notes":"実際 is both a noun and an adverb. 実際に means \"actually, in real life\" (実際に見る \"to see with one's own eyes\"), 実際は / 実際には \"in reality (however)\" as opposed to expectation or theory, 実際の \"actual, real\". At the beginning of a sentence, 実際、… means \"indeed, really\" and confirms what was said. 本当に is more colloquial, 現実 means reality as the opposite of a dream.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"確認画面が出たらIDとパスワードを入力してログインをしてください。","romaji":"Kakunin gamen ga detara ID to pasuwādo o nyūryoku shite roguin o shite kudasai.","german":"When the confirmation screen appears, please enter your ID and password to log in.","furigana":"かくにんがめん|で|にゅうりょく"},{"japanese":"出かける前に、窓が閉まっているか確認してください。","romaji":"Dekakeru mae ni, mado ga shimatte iru ka kakunin shite kudasai.","german":"Please check that the windows are closed before you go out.","furigana":"で|まえ|まど|し|かくにん"},{"japanese":"念のため、明日の会議の時間を確認させてください。","romaji":"Nen no tame, ashita no kaigi no jikan o kakunin sasete kudasai.","german":"Just to be sure, please let me confirm the time of tomorrow's meeting.","furigana":"ねん|あした|かいぎ|じかん|かくにん"}],"notes":"確認 is \"confirmation, checking\"; mostly used as a transitive する-verb: 〜を確認する \"to check something, make sure\". It is very common in polite formulas like ご確認ください \"please check\" and 念のため確認させてください \"let me double-check to be safe\". Compounds: 確認画面, 本人確認 \"identity verification\", 再確認. The Japanese verb 確かめる is somewhat more personal, チェックする more casual.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 16"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"グチをこぼしても何も解決しないので、私はグチを言わない主義なんです。","romaji":"Guchi o koboshite mo nani mo kaiketsu shinai node, watashi wa guchi o iwanai shugi na n desu.","german":"Complaining doesn't solve anything, so I make it a principle not to complain.","furigana":"なに|かいけつ|わたし|い|しゅぎ"},{"japanese":"日本は民主主義の国です。","romaji":"Nihon wa minshu shugi no kuni desu.","german":"Japan is a democracy.","furigana":"にほん|みんしゅしゅぎ|くに"},{"japanese":"彼は完璧主義なので、仕事にとても時間がかかる。","romaji":"Kare wa kanpeki shugi na node, shigoto ni totemo jikan ga kakaru.","german":"He is a perfectionist, so his work takes a very long time.","furigana":"かれ|かんぺきしゅぎ|しごと|じかん"}],"notes":"主義 is a noun meaning \"principle, doctrine\" and serves above all as a suffix for \"-ism\": 民主主義 \"democracy\", 資本主義 \"capitalism\", 個人主義 \"individualism\", 完璧主義 \"perfectionism\". Used personally: 〜ない主義だ \"it is my principle not to ...\", 主義を守る / 曲げる. Related are 信念 \"conviction\" and 方針 \"course, guideline\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"兄は外務省で働いています。","romaji":"Ani wa Gaimushō de hataraite imasu.","german":"My older brother works at the Ministry of Foreign Affairs.","furigana":"あに|がいむしょう|はたら"},{"japanese":"文部科学省が、新しい教育方針を発表しました。","romaji":"Monbu Kagakushō ga, atarashii kyōiku hōshin o happyō shimashita.","german":"The Ministry of Education, Culture, Sports, Science and Technology announced a new education policy.","furigana":"もんぶかがくしょう|あたら|きょういくほうしん|はっぴょう"},{"japanese":"どの省がこの問題を担当していますか。","romaji":"Dono shō ga kono mondai o tantō shite imasu ka.","german":"Which ministry is in charge of this problem?","furigana":"しょう|もんだい|たんとう"}],"notes":"〜省 forms the names of the Japanese ministries: 外務省 (\"Ministry of Foreign Affairs\"), 財務省, 文部科学省, 厚生労働省. The minister is called 〜大臣 (外務大臣); ministries and agencies together are called 省庁, and subordinate offices end in 〜庁 (気象庁). In China, 省 also denotes a province (広東省). Not to be confused with the verb 省く (\"to omit\"), which uses the same kanji.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"病気で休んだ分を休日に働いて埋め合わせるつもりだ。","romaji":"Byouki de yasunda bun o kyuujitsu ni hataraite umeawaseru tsumori da.","german":"I plan to make up for the time I took off sick by working on my days off.","furigana":"びょうき|やす|ぶん|きゅうじつ|はたら|う|あ"},{"japanese":"赤字を埋め合わせるために、ボーナスがなくなった。","romaji":"Akaji o umeawaseru tame ni, boonasu ga nakunatta.","german":"To make up for the deficit, the bonus was cancelled.","furigana":"あかじ|う|あ"},{"japanese":"約束を守れなくてごめん。今度、何かで埋め合わせるよ。","romaji":"Yakusoku o mamorenakute gomen. Kondo, nanika de umeawaseru yo.","german":"Sorry I couldn't keep my promise. I'll make it up to you next time.","furigana":"やくそく|まも|こんど|なに|う|あ"}],"notes":"埋め合わせる is transitive and means \"to make up for, to compensate\": to fill in absences, losses or a deficit with something else (〜を…で埋め合わせる). The noun 埋め合わせ is very common, especially toward people: この埋め合わせは必ずするよ \"I'll definitely make it up to you\". Related are 補う \"to supplement\" and 取り戻す \"to take back, to make up for lost ground\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日のレッスンは形容動詞でした。","romaji":"Kyō no ressun wa keiyōdōshi deshita.","german":"Today's lesson was on na-adjectives.","furigana":"きょう|けいようどうし"},{"japanese":"「静か」は形容動詞なので、名詞の前では「静かな」になります。","romaji":"\"Shizuka\" wa keiyoudoushi na node, meishi no mae de wa \"shizuka na\" ni narimasu.","german":"\"Shizuka\" is a na-adjective, so it becomes \"shizuka na\" before a noun.","furigana":"しず|けいようどうし|めいし|まえ|しず"},{"japanese":"国語の授業で、形容詞と形容動詞の違いを習った。","romaji":"Kokugo no jugyou de, keiyoushi to keiyoudoushi no chigai o naratta.","german":"In Japanese class at school, we learned the difference between i-adjectives and na-adjectives.","furigana":"こくご|じゅぎょう|けいようし|けいようどうし|ちが|なら"}],"notes":"形容動詞 is the term of traditional Japanese school grammar for na-adjectives such as 静か or 便利; the i-adjectives are simply called 形容詞 there. In teaching Japanese as a foreign language, ナ形容詞 and イ形容詞 are mostly used. Literally it means \"adjective-verb\", because these words are conjugated like a predicate with だ/です.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ようやく親になる実感が持てるようになってきました。","romaji":"Yōyaku oya ni naru jikkan ga moteru yō ni natte kimashita.","german":"I'm finally starting to feel like a parent.","furigana":"おや|じっかん|も"},{"japanese":"優勝したなんて、まだ実感がわきません。","romaji":"Yuushou shita nante, mada jikkan ga wakimasen.","german":"I still can't believe that we won.","furigana":"ゆうしょう|じっかん"},{"japanese":"一人暮らしを始めて、親のありがたさを実感しました。","romaji":"Hitorigurashi o hajimete, oya no arigatasa o jikkan shimashita.","german":"Since I started living on my own, I have realized how much I appreciate my parents.","furigana":"ひとりぐ|はじ|おや|じっかん"}],"notes":"実感 is the \"real, personal feeling\" for something - that it is really so. Typical: 実感がわく \"something becomes tangible\", often negated: まだ実感がわかない \"I still can't believe it\"; 実感が持てる, 実感がこもる. As a transitive verb, 〜を実感する means \"to feel something firsthand, to really notice\". Simple 感じる is more general; 実感 emphasizes personal experience.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"レースを布地に縫い付けた。","romaji":"Reesu o nunoji ni nuitsuketa.","german":"I sewed lace onto the fabric.","furigana":"ぬのじ|ぬ|つ"},{"japanese":"このシャツは布地が薄いので、夏にぴったりです。","romaji":"Kono shatsu wa nunoji ga usui node, natsu ni pittari desu.","german":"This shirt is made of thin fabric, so it's perfect for summer.","furigana":"ぬのじ|うす|なつ"},{"japanese":"手芸店で、カーテン用の布地を買いました。","romaji":"Shugeiten de, kaaten-you no nunoji o kaimashita.","german":"At the craft store, I bought fabric for curtains.","furigana":"しゅげいてん|よう|ぬのじ|か"}],"notes":"布地 is \"fabric\" as a material, especially for clothing, curtains or covers: 布地を選ぶ, 厚い布地, 布地が薄い. In sewing shops and fashion, 生地 is also often heard with the same meaning. 布 alone is more general, \"cloth, piece of fabric\". Type of weave: 綿の布地 \"cotton fabric\", ウールの布地 \"wool fabric\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"こんな得体の知れないもの、食べられないよ。","romaji":"Konna etai no shirenai mono, taberarenai yo.","german":"I can't eat something this mysterious.","furigana":"えたい|し|た"},{"japanese":"夜中に得体の知れない音がして、怖くて眠れなかった。","romaji":"Yonaka ni etai no shirenai oto ga shite, kowakute nemurenakatta.","german":"In the middle of the night I heard a strange noise and was too scared to sleep.","furigana":"よなか|えたい|し|おと|こわ|ねむ"},{"japanese":"得体の知れない男から、何度も電話がかかってきた。","romaji":"Etai no shirenai otoko kara, nando mo denwa ga kakatte kita.","german":"A shady stranger called me over and over.","furigana":"えたい|し|おとこ|なんど|でんわ"}],"notes":"得体 \"true nature, identity\" occurs almost only in the fixed phrase 得体の知れない (also 得体が知れない): \"unidentifiable, shady, eerie\" - one doesn't know what or who it really is. It is used for things, noises and people: 得体の知れない男, 得体の知れない音. It is often also written in kana as えたい. On its own the word is hardly used.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コウイチは、世界が羨む男だ。","romaji":"Kouichi wa, sekai ga urayamu otoko da.","german":"Koichi is a man the whole world envies.","furigana":"せかい|うらや|おとこ"},{"japanese":"他人を羨んでばかりいないで、自分も努力しなさい。","romaji":"Tanin o urayande bakari inaide, jibun mo doryoku shinasai.","german":"Stop just envying other people and make an effort yourself.","furigana":"たにん|うらや|じぶん|どりょく"},{"japanese":"友達の合格を羨む気持ちもあったが、心から「おめでとう」と言った。","romaji":"Tomodachi no goukaku o urayamu kimochi mo atta ga, kokoro kara \"omedetou\" to itta.","german":"I felt a bit of envy at my friend's passing, but I said \"congratulations\" from the bottom of my heart.","furigana":"ともだち|ごうかく|うらや|きも|こころ|い"}],"notes":"羨む is transitive: 人を羨む \"to envy someone\", 人の成功を羨む \"to envy someone's success\". It sounds rather written; in conversation, the adjective 羨ましい is almost always used (\"I envy you\" = 羨ましいなあ). The expression 誰もが羨む〜 \"enviable\" is common. 妬む or 嫉妬する, by contrast, contain ill will. It is also often written in kana as うらやむ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの人、いつも苛々しているね。","romaji":"Ano hito, itsumo iraira shite iru ne.","german":"That person is always on edge, isn't he?","furigana":"ひと|いらいら"},{"japanese":"電車がなかなか来なくて、苛々した。","romaji":"Densha ga nakanaka konakute, iraira shita.","german":"The train just wouldn't come, and I got irritated.","furigana":"でんしゃ|こ|いらいら"},{"japanese":"お腹がすくと、すぐ苛々してしまう。","romaji":"Onaka ga suku to, sugu iraira shite shimau.","german":"When I'm hungry, I get irritated easily.","furigana":"なか|いらいら"}],"notes":"苛々する means \"to be irritated, impatient, to get annoyed\". It is written almost always in kana, often in katakana イライラ; the kanji 苛々 are rare. The trigger is marked with に: 〜にイライラする \"to get worked up about ...\". As a noun: イライラが溜まる \"frustration builds up\". 怒る means to get openly angry, whereas イライラ describes rather inner tension; むかつく is cruder.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"飲酒運転は犯罪です。","romaji":"Inshu unten wa hanzai desu.","german":"Drunk driving is a crime.","furigana":"いんしゅうんてん|はんざい"},{"japanese":"彼は飲酒運転で捕まって、免許を取り消された。","romaji":"Kare wa inshu unten de tsukamatte, menkyo o torikesareta.","german":"He was caught drunk driving and his license was revoked.","furigana":"かれ|いんしゅうんてん|つか|めんきょ|と|け"},{"japanese":"今夜は飲み会だから、電車で行くよ。飲酒運転になっちゃうからね。","romaji":"Kon'ya wa nomikai da kara, densha de iku yo. Inshu unten ni nacchau kara ne.","german":"There's a drinking party tonight, so I'm going by train. Otherwise it would be drunk driving.","furigana":"こんや|の|かい|でんしゃ|い|いんしゅうんてん"}],"notes":"飲酒運転 is composed of 飲酒 \"alcohol consumption\" and 運転 \"driving\": \"driving under the influence of alcohol\". Typical: 飲酒運転をする, 飲酒運転で捕まる \"to be caught drunk driving\". In Japan the limit is practically zero, and even passengers who allow it are punished. The slogan is 飲んだら乗るな \"If you drink, don't drive\"; legally one also speaks of 酒気帯び運転.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大人は仕事をしてお金を貰うんでしょ？","romaji":"Otona wa shigoto o shite okane o morau n desho?","german":"Adults work and get money for it, right?","furigana":"おとな|しごと|かね|もら"},{"japanese":"誕生日に姉から時計を貰いました。","romaji":"Tanjoubi ni ane kara tokei o moraimashita.","german":"I received a watch from my older sister for my birthday.","furigana":"たんじょうび|あね|とけい|もら"},{"japanese":"分からないところは、先輩に教えてもらうといいよ。","romaji":"Wakaranai tokoro wa, senpai ni oshiete morau to ii yo.","german":"For the parts you don't understand, it is good to have a senior colleague explain them to you.","furigana":"わ|せんぱい|おし"}],"notes":"貰う \"to receive\" is mostly written in kana, もらう. The giver takes に or から: 姉に/から時計をもらう. As an auxiliary verb, 〜てもらう means \"to have someone do something for oneself, to get something done\". The humble form is いただく. On direction: あげる \"I give\", くれる \"someone gives to me\", もらう \"I receive\" – the subject is always the recipient.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"何も言わずに立ち去るなんて、ひどいよ。","romaji":"Nani mo iwazu ni tachisaru nante, hidoi yo.","german":"How awful, leaving without saying anything.","furigana":"なに|い|た|さ"},{"japanese":"事故の現場から立ち去ってはいけません。","romaji":"Jiko no genba kara tachisatte wa ikemasen.","german":"You must not leave the scene of an accident.","furigana":"じこ|げんば|た|さ"},{"japanese":"彼女は静かにお辞儀をして、部屋を立ち去った。","romaji":"Kanojo wa shizuka ni ojigi o shite, heya o tachisatta.","german":"She bowed quietly and left the room.","furigana":"かのじょ|しず|じぎ|へや|た|さ"}],"notes":"立ち去る is intransitive and means \"to move away, to go off\" - usually for good and often without a word. The place left takes を: その場を立ち去る \"to leave the place\". The word is narrative and literary; in conversation one rather says 出ていく, 帰る or いなくなる. 去る alone is even more formal.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いつかこの願いが叶うといいな。","romaji":"Itsuka kono negai ga kanau to ii na.","german":"I hope this wish comes true someday.","furigana":"ねが|かな"},{"japanese":"子供のころからの夢が叶って、パイロットになりました。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro kara no yume ga kanatte, pairotto ni narimashita.","german":"My dream from childhood came true, and I became a pilot.","furigana":"こども|ゆめ|かな"},{"japanese":"残念ながら、その恋は叶わなかった。","romaji":"Zannen nagara, sono koi wa kanawanakatta.","german":"Unfortunately, that love went unrequited.","furigana":"ざんねん|こい|かな"}],"notes":"叶う is intransitive: 夢が叶う \"a dream comes true\", 願いが叶う, 恋が叶う \"love is requited\". The transitive counterpart is 叶える: 夢を叶える \"to fulfill a dream\". Not to be confused with 敵う \"to be a match for someone\" (彼には敵わない) and 適う \"to meet, to suit\" (目的に適う), which are pronounced the same.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"実りの秋っていいますもんね。","romaji":"Minori no aki tte iimasu mon ne.","german":"They do say that autumn is the season of harvest.","furigana":"みの|あき"},{"japanese":"今年は天気が良かったので、米の実りがいい。","romaji":"Kotoshi wa tenki ga yokatta node, kome no minori ga ii.","german":"The weather was good this year, so the rice harvest is good.","furigana":"ことし|てんき|よ|こめ|みの"},{"japanese":"留学は、とても実りの多い経験になりました。","romaji":"Ryuugaku wa, totemo minori no ooi keiken ni narimashita.","german":"Studying abroad was a very fruitful experience.","furigana":"りゅうがく|みの|おお|けいけん"}],"notes":"実り is the noun of 実る \"to ripen, to bear fruit\" and denotes the ripening crop or the yield: 稲の実り, 実りの秋 \"autumn as the harvest season\" (fixed phrase). Figuratively, 実りの多い / 実りある means \"productive, fruitful\": 実りある話し合い, 実りの多い一年. 収穫, on the other hand, means rather the bringing in of the harvest or the \"gain\" from an experience.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新築のマンション以外には住みたくありません。","romaji":"Shinchiku no manshon igai ni wa sumitaku arimasen.","german":"I don't want to live anywhere but a newly built condominium.","furigana":"しんちく|いがい|す"},{"japanese":"来年、家を新築する予定です。","romaji":"Rainen, ie o shinchiku suru yotei desu.","german":"I plan to build a new house next year.","furigana":"らいねん|いえ|しんちく|よてい"},{"japanese":"友達の新築祝いに、きれいな花を持っていきました。","romaji":"Tomodachi no shinchiku iwai ni, kirei na hana o motte ikimashita.","german":"I brought beautiful flowers as a housewarming gift for my friend's new home.","furigana":"ともだち|しんちくいわ|はな|も"}],"notes":"新築 means a new building; as an attributive, 新築の家 / 新築マンション \"newly built\". As a transitive verb, 家を新築する \"to build a new house\". The opposite in the housing market is 中古 \"used, existing property\"; 改築 is \"rebuilding\", リフォーム \"renovation\". 新築祝い \"housewarming gift\" is also common.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"マリリンの猫の尻尾は、とてもフワフワです。","romaji":"Maririn no neko no shippo wa, totemo fuwafuwa desu.","german":"Marilyn's cat has a very fluffy tail.","furigana":"ねこ|しっぽ"},{"japanese":"犬が嬉しそうに尻尾を振っている。","romaji":"Inu ga ureshisou ni shippo o futte iru.","german":"The dog is wagging its tail happily.","furigana":"いぬ|うれ|しっぽ|ふ"},{"japanese":"警察はついに犯人の尻尾をつかんだ。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa tsui ni hannin no shippo o tsukanda.","german":"The police have finally caught on to the culprit.","furigana":"けいさつ|はんにん|しっぽ"}],"notes":"尻尾 is the tail of an animal and is often written in kana as しっぽ; the written-language word is 尾. Expressions: 尻尾を振る \"to wag the tail\" (figuratively also \"to fawn\"), 尻尾を出す \"to give oneself away\", 尻尾をつかむ \"to catch on to someone\". The end of things can also be called 尻尾, for example of a fish or bread.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"時が経つのを忘れて熱中していました。","romaji":"Toki ga tatsu no o wasurete netchū shite imashita.","german":"I was so absorbed that I lost track of time.","furigana":"とき|た|わす|ねっちゅう"},{"japanese":"日本に来てから、もう五年が経ちました。","romaji":"Nihon ni kite kara, mou gonen ga tachimashita.","german":"Five years have already passed since I came to Japan.","furigana":"にほん|き|ごねん|た"},{"japanese":"時間が経てば、きっと痛みも消えるよ。","romaji":"Jikan ga tateba, kitto itami mo kieru yo.","german":"As time passes, the pain will surely fade too.","furigana":"じかん|た|いた|き"}],"notes":"経つ is intransitive and used only for time: 時間が経つ, 三年が経つ, 〜てから一年経つ \"a year has passed since ...\". 経てば \"as time passes\" is common. It is often also written in kana as たつ. Not to be confused with the homophones 立つ \"to stand\", 建つ \"to be built\" and 発つ \"to depart\". 過ぎる is similar but rather emphasizes passing a point in time.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"経験者から、価値あるアドバイスをいただくことができました。","romaji":"Keikensha kara, kachi aru adobaisu o itadaku koto ga dekimashita.","german":"We were able to receive valuable advice from someone experienced.","furigana":"けいけんしゃ|かち"},{"japanese":"「経験者優遇」と書いてありますが、未経験でも応募できますか。","romaji":"\"Keikensha yuuguu\" to kaite arimasu ga, mikeiken demo oubo dekimasu ka.","german":"It says \"experienced applicants preferred\" - can I apply even without experience?","furigana":"けいけんしゃゆうぐう|か|みけいけん|おうぼ"},{"japanese":"留学の経験者に、話を聞いてみたいです。","romaji":"Ryuugaku no keikensha ni, hanashi o kiite mitai desu.","german":"I would like to hear from someone who has studied abroad.","furigana":"りゅうがく|けいけんしゃ|はなし|き"}],"notes":"経験者 is a person with experience in a particular field or someone who has experienced something themselves: 留学経験者 \"someone who has studied abroad\", 〜の経験者. In job ads one reads 経験者優遇 \"experienced applicants preferred\" and, as the opposite, 未経験者歓迎 \"career changers welcome\". ベテラン, on the other hand, means someone with very long experience.","levelNote":"Compound: 経験 (N4) + 者 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その時間だと道路はかなり混んでいますよね？","romaji":"Sono jikan da to dōro wa kanari konde imasu yo ne?","german":"The roads are pretty congested at that time, aren't they?","furigana":"じかん|どうろ|こ"},{"japanese":"朝の電車はいつも混んでいて、座れません。","romaji":"Asa no densha wa itsumo konde ite, suwaremasen.","german":"The morning train is always crowded, and I can't get a seat.","furigana":"あさ|でんしゃ|こ|すわ"},{"japanese":"週末は混むから、平日に行こう。","romaji":"Shuumatsu wa komu kara, heijitsu ni ikou.","german":"It gets crowded on weekends, so let's go on a weekday.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|こ|へいじつ|い"}],"notes":"混む is intransitive: the place or vehicle takes が/は, and for the state one usually uses 混んでいる: 電車が混んでいる \"the train is crowded\". It is also written 込む. The opposite is すいている \"to be empty\". More formal is 混雑する (station announcements); for car traffic jams one also says 渋滞する.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この評論はよくまとまっているね。","romaji":"Kono hyōron wa yoku matomatte iru ne.","german":"This review is quite well put together.","furigana":"ひょうろん"},{"japanese":"父は新聞に映画の評論を書いています。","romaji":"Chichi wa shinbun ni eiga no hyouron o kaite imasu.","german":"My father writes film reviews for the newspaper.","furigana":"ちち|しんぶん|えいが|ひょうろん|か"},{"japanese":"テレビで有名な評論家が、選挙の結果を説明していた。","romaji":"Terebi de yuumei na hyouronka ga, senkyo no kekka o setsumei shite ita.","german":"A well-known commentator on TV explained the election result.","furigana":"ゆうめい|ひょうろんか|せんきょ|けっか|せつめい"}],"notes":"評論 is an expert review or critical essay on literature, films, art or politics; its author is a 評論家 \"critic, commentator\". As a verb: 〜を評論する. It is an analysis, not necessarily negative - \"criticism\" in the sense of blame is 批判. Similar is 批評; casually, レビュー is used.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ダリンは自然の美しさに圧倒された。","romaji":"Darin wa shizen no utsukushisa ni attou sareta.","german":"Darin was overwhelmed by the beauty of nature.","furigana":"しぜん|うつく|あっとう"},{"japanese":"決勝戦では、相手チームに圧倒されてしまった。","romaji":"Kesshousen de wa, aite chiimu ni attou sarete shimatta.","german":"In the final, we were overwhelmed by the opposing team.","furigana":"けっしょうせん|あいて|あっとう"},{"japanese":"彼女の歌は、ほかの出場者を圧倒していた。","romaji":"Kanojo no uta wa, hoka no shutsujousha o attou shite ita.","german":"Her singing overwhelmed the other contestants.","furigana":"かのじょ|うた|しゅつじょうしゃ|あっとう"}],"notes":"圧倒する is transitive: 相手を圧倒する \"to overpower the opponent, to be clearly superior\". Very common is the passive 〜に圧倒される \"to be overwhelmed by something\" (beauty, size, presence). The adjective 圧倒的 \"overwhelming\" is also common: 圧倒的な勝利, 圧倒的に多い \"by far the most\". The tone is neutral to slightly formal.","levelNote":"Derived from 圧倒 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"辞書アプリが存在しなかった時代は、漢字を調べるのが大変だった。","romaji":"Jisho apuri ga sonzai shinakatta jidai wa, kanji o shiraberu no ga taihen datta.","german":"In the days before dictionary apps existed, looking up kanji was hard.","furigana":"じしょ|そんざい|じだい|かんじ|しら|たいへん"},{"japanese":"宇宙人は本当に存在すると思いますか。","romaji":"Uchuujin wa hontou ni sonzai suru to omoimasu ka.","german":"Do you think aliens really exist?","furigana":"うちゅうじん|ほんとう|そんざい|おも"},{"japanese":"この問題には、正しい答えが存在しない。","romaji":"Kono mondai ni wa, tadashii kotae ga sonzai shinai.","german":"There is no correct answer to this problem.","furigana":"もんだい|ただ|こた|そんざい"}],"notes":"存在する is intransitive and the formal, abstract counterpart of ある/いる: 〜が存在する \"there is, to exist\". It suits questions of existence (宇宙人は存在するか), rules, problems or data. In everyday speech it sounds stiff - there one says ある/いる. The noun 存在 also means \"presence, person of importance\": 大きな存在, 存在感.","levelNote":"Derived from 存在 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"こんなクイズも解けないのか、と嫌味を言われた。","romaji":"Konna kuizu mo tokenai no ka, to iyami o iwareta.","german":"I was told sarcastically, \"You can't even solve a quiz like this?\"","furigana":"と|いやみ|い"},{"japanese":"彼女はいつも自慢ばかりして、ちょっと嫌味な人だ。","romaji":"Kanojo wa itsumo jiman bakari shite, chotto iyami na hito da.","german":"She is always bragging and is a bit of an unpleasant person.","furigana":"かのじょ|じまん|いやみ|ひと"},{"japanese":"遅刻した私に「今日は早いですね」なんて、ひどい嫌味だ。","romaji":"Chikoku shita watashi ni \"kyou wa hayai desu ne\" nante, hidoi iyami da.","german":"Saying \"You're early today\" to me when I'm late - what a nasty dig.","furigana":"ちこく|わたし|きょう|はや|いやみ"}],"notes":"嫌味 is a pointed, spiteful remark or an unpleasant, affected manner. Typical: 嫌味を言う \"to make snide remarks\", 嫌味な人 \"unpleasant, arrogant person\", 嫌味っぽい \"snide, condescending\". Often in kana いやみ or イヤミ. 皮肉 is \"irony\", rather indirect and sometimes witty; 悪口 is badmouthing, usually behind someone's back.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"防犯のために多くの提案がなされました。","romaji":"Bouhan no tame ni ooku no teian ga nasaremashita.","german":"Many proposals were made for crime prevention.","furigana":"ぼうはん|おお|ていあん"},{"japanese":"コンビニには防犯カメラが付いています。","romaji":"Konbini ni wa bouhan kamera ga tsuite imasu.","german":"Convenience stores have security cameras installed.","furigana":"ぼうはん|つ"},{"japanese":"子どもに防犯ブザーを持たせています。","romaji":"Kodomo ni bouhan buzaa o motasete imasu.","german":"I have my child carry a personal safety alarm.","furigana":"こ|ぼうはん|も"}],"notes":"防犯 means \"crime prevention\" and appears mainly as a prefix or with の: 防犯カメラ \"security camera\", 防犯ブザー \"personal safety alarm\", 防犯対策, 防犯のため. Similarly built are 防災 \"disaster prevention\" and 防火 \"fire prevention\". 警備 is guard duty, and 防止 is general prevention (事故防止).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ちょこっと昼寝してくるねー。","romaji":"Chokotto hirune shite kuru nē.","german":"I'm going to take a quick nap.","furigana":"ひるね"},{"japanese":"日曜日の午後は、ソファで昼寝をするのが好きです。","romaji":"Nichiyoubi no gogo wa, sofa de hirune o suru no ga suki desu.","german":"On Sunday afternoons, I like to take a nap on the sofa.","furigana":"にちようび|ごご|ひるね|す"},{"japanese":"猫が日なたで気持ちよさそうに昼寝している。","romaji":"Neko ga hinata de kimochiyosasou ni hirune shite iru.","german":"The cat is napping comfortably in the sun.","furigana":"ねこ|ひ|きも|ひるね"}],"notes":"昼寝 is the midday nap; as an intransitive verb 昼寝する or 昼寝をする, often downplayed: ちょっと昼寝する. 居眠り, by contrast, is dozing off unintentionally (in class, on the train), and 仮眠 is a short nap, for example during a night shift. Do not confuse it with 寝坊 \"oversleeping\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「今日も残業するつもりなの？」「えっと…ですね、今日は寝不足なのでどちらかというと早く上がりたいと思っています。」","romaji":"Kyou mo zangyou suru tsumori na no? Etto... desu ne, kyou wa nebusoku na node dochira ka to iu to hayaku agaritai to omotte imasu.","german":"\"Are you planning to work overtime today too?\" \"Um... well, I'm sleep-deprived today, so I'd rather leave early, if anything.\"","furigana":"きょう|ざんぎょう|きょう|ねぶそく|はや|あ|おも"},{"japanese":"昨日は遅くまでゲームをしていたので、寝不足です。","romaji":"Kinou wa osoku made geemu o shite ita node, nebusoku desu.","german":"I was playing games until late yesterday, so I'm short on sleep.","furigana":"きのう|おそ|ねぶそく"},{"japanese":"寝不足が続くと、仕事でミスが多くなる。","romaji":"Nebusoku ga tsuzuku to, shigoto de misu ga ooku naru.","german":"If the lack of sleep continues, you make more mistakes at work.","furigana":"ねぶそく|つづ|しごと|おお"}],"notes":"寝不足 is \"lack of sleep\" and is used as a na-adjective or noun: 寝不足だ \"to have slept too little\", 寝不足で頭が痛い, 寝不足が続く. Toned down: 寝不足気味 \"a bit sleep-deprived\". More formal and medical is 睡眠不足; 徹夜 is an all-nighter.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"夏が過ぎて、朝晩は涼しくなりました。","romaji":"Natsu ga sugite, asaban wa suzushiku narimashita.","german":"Summer has passed, and mornings and evenings have become cool.","furigana":"なつ|す|あさばん|すず"},{"japanese":"約束の時間はもう十分も過ぎています。","romaji":"Yakusoku no jikan wa mou juppun mo sugite imasu.","german":"It is already ten minutes past the appointed time.","furigana":"やくそく|じかん|じゅっぷん|す"},{"japanese":"電車は今、東京駅を過ぎたところです。","romaji":"Densha wa ima, Toukyou eki o sugita tokoro desu.","german":"The train has just passed Tokyo Station.","furigana":"でんしゃ|いま|とうきょうえき|す"}],"notes":"過ぎる means \"to pass, to go by\" – spatially past a place, where the place then takes を (駅を過ぎる), and in time (夏が過ぎる, 約束の時間が過ぎる). In addition there is \"to exceed\": 四十歳を過ぎる. As a suffix on verb stems and adjectives, ～すぎる means \"too much, too\" (食べすぎる, 高すぎる) and is then mostly written in kana. The noun 過ぎ follows time expressions: 五時過ぎ \"just after five\". The transitive counterpart is 過ごす \"to spend time\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"玉ねぎが目にしみたことは過去に一度もありません。","romaji":"Tamanegi ga me ni shimita koto wa kako ni ichido mo arimasen.","german":"I have never once had onions sting my eyes.","furigana":"たま|め|かこ|いちど"},{"japanese":"今年の夏は、過去最高の暑さになった。","romaji":"Kotoshi no natsu wa, kako saikou no atsusa ni natta.","german":"This summer was the hottest on record.","furigana":"ことし|なつ|かこさいこう|あつ"},{"japanese":"過去のことはもう忘れて、前を向こう。","romaji":"Kako no koto wa mou wasurete, mae o mukou.","german":"Forget the past and look ahead.","furigana":"かこ|わす|まえ|む"}],"notes":"過去 is \"the past\" and forms a series with 現在 and 未来. Typical: 過去に \"in the past, so far\", 過去の〜 \"past ...\", 過去最高 \"record high\", 過去形 \"past tense\". Also one's personal, often undisclosed past: 暗い過去. 昔 is more colloquial and rather means more distant times, 以前 \"before, previously\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ゲームに負けた人は、罰ゲームとして変顔で写真を撮ることになった。","romaji":"Gēmu ni maketa hito wa, batsu gēmu to shite hengao de shashin o toru koto ni natta.","german":"The person who lost the game had to take a photo making a silly face as a penalty game.","furigana":"ま|ひと|ばつ|へんがお|しゃしん|と"},{"japanese":"罰ゲームで、みんなの前で歌を歌わされた。","romaji":"Batsu gēmu de, minna no mae de uta o utawasareta.","german":"As a penalty game, I was made to sing a song in front of everyone.","furigana":"ばつ|まえ|うた|うた"},{"japanese":"こんな暑い日に外で働くなんて、罰ゲームみたいだ。","romaji":"Konna atsui hi ni soto de hataraku nante, batsu gēmu mitai da.","german":"Working outside on such a hot day is like a penalty game.","furigana":"あつ|ひ|そと|はたら|ばつ"}],"notes":"罰ゲーム is the \"penalty task\" that the loser has to carry out in party games or TV shows, such as doing something embarrassing or eating something disgusting. Typical are 罰ゲームをする, 罰ゲームを受ける and 罰ゲームとして〜. Colloquially, anything unpleasant is called that: 〜なんて罰ゲームだ. Here 罰 is read ばつ; ばち in 罰が当たる, on the other hand, is divine punishment.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この会社はどうやって誕生したんですか？","romaji":"Kono kaisha wa dou yatte tanjou shita n desu ka?","german":"How did this company come into being?","furigana":"かいしゃ|たんじょう"},{"japanese":"先月、姉のところに赤ちゃんが誕生しました。","romaji":"Sengetsu, ane no tokoro ni akachan ga tanjou shimashita.","german":"Last month a baby was born to my sister.","furigana":"せんげつ|あね|あか|たんじょう"},{"japanese":"選挙の結果、この町に初めての女性市長が誕生した。","romaji":"Senkyo no kekka, kono machi ni hajimete no josei shichou ga tanjou shita.","german":"As a result of the election, this town got its first female mayor.","furigana":"せんきょ|けっか|まち|はじ|じょせいしちょう|たんじょう"}],"notes":"誕生 is \"birth\" and, as an intransitive verb, 誕生する \"to be born, to come into being\". For people it sounds solemn; in everyday speech one says 生まれる. It is very common for the emergence of things: 新しい会社 / 政権 / スターが誕生する. Well-known compounds are 誕生日 \"birthday\" and 誕生会 \"birthday party\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あっ、ヤバい。一字脱字がある！","romaji":"A, yabai. Ichiji datsuji ga aru!","german":"Oh no, there's a missing character!"},{"japanese":"提出する前に、誤字脱字がないかよく確認してください。","romaji":"Teishutsu suru mae ni, goji datsuji ga nai ka yoku kakunin shite kudasai.","german":"Before submitting, please check carefully for typos and omitted characters.","furigana":"ていしゅつ|まえ|ごじだつじ|かくにん"},{"japanese":"この本は脱字が多くて、読みにくい。","romaji":"Kono hon wa datsuji ga ookute, yominikui.","german":"This book has many missing characters, so it is hard to read.","furigana":"ほん|だつじ|おお|よ"}],"notes":"脱字 is an omitted character in a text. It mostly appears in the pair 誤字脱字 \"typos and omitted characters\", for example in 誤字脱字がないか確認する \"to proofread for errors\". 誤字 is a wrong character, 誤植 a printing error. The word belongs to written and office language.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの坊さんには、アメリカ人の血も混じっているんですよ。","romaji":"Ano bōsan ni wa, Amerikajin no chi mo majitte iru n desu yo.","german":"That monk also has some American blood.","furigana":"ぼう|じん|ち|ま"},{"japanese":"お寺で、坊さんがお経を読んでいた。","romaji":"Otera de, bousan ga okyou o yonde ita.","german":"At the temple, a monk was reciting sutras.","furigana":"てら|ぼう|きょう|よ"},{"japanese":"祖父のお葬式に、お坊さんを呼びました。","romaji":"Sofu no osoushiki ni, obousan o yobimashita.","german":"We called a priest for my grandfather's funeral.","furigana":"そふ|そうしき|ぼう|よ"}],"notes":"坊さん is a familiar but respectful term for a Buddhist monk or priest; the most common form heard is お坊さん. 坊主 alone can sound derogatory, while 僧 and 僧侶 are formal. Typical situations: funerals and memorial services (お坊さんを呼ぶ \"to call a priest\"), お経を読む \"to recite sutras\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"赤ん坊がやっと寝てくれました。","romaji":"Akanbou ga yatto nete kuremashita.","german":"The baby finally went to sleep for me.","furigana":"あか|ぼう|ね"},{"japanese":"赤ん坊のころの写真を母に見せてもらいました。","romaji":"Akanbou no koro no shashin o haha ni misete moraimashita.","german":"My mother showed me photos from when I was a baby.","furigana":"あか|ぼう|しゃしん|はは|み"},{"japanese":"電車の中で赤ん坊がずっと泣いていて、お母さんは困っていました。","romaji":"Densha no naka de akanbou ga zutto naite ite, okaasan wa komatte imashita.","german":"A baby was crying the whole time on the train, and the mother was at a loss.","furigana":"でんしゃ|なか|あか|ぼう|な|かあ|こま"}],"notes":"赤ん坊 refers to a baby or infant. In everyday speech, 赤ちゃん is much more common and sounds more affectionate and polite, especially when talking about someone else's baby; 赤ん坊 is more matter-of-fact and often appears in narratives and descriptions. Typical are 赤ん坊が泣く, 赤ん坊が生まれる and 赤ん坊のころ \"as a baby\". The literary 赤子 is dated.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"坊主になる前に頭を坊主にした。","romaji":"Bōzu ni naru mae ni atama o bōzu ni shita.","german":"Before becoming a monk, I shaved my head.","furigana":"ぼうず|まえ|あたま|ぼうず"},{"japanese":"日記を始めたけど、三日坊主で終わってしまった。","romaji":"Nikki o hajimeta kedo, mikka bouzu de owatte shimatta.","german":"I started a diary, but it ended up lasting only three days.","furigana":"にっき|はじ|みっかぼうず|お"},{"japanese":"今日の釣りは坊主だった。一匹も釣れなかったよ。","romaji":"Kyou no tsuri wa bouzu datta. Ippiki mo tsurenakatta yo.","german":"Fishing today was a bust. I didn't catch a single fish.","furigana":"きょう|つ|ぼうず|いっぴき|つ"}],"notes":"坊主 means \"Buddhist monk\", but today often sounds disrespectful - politely one says お坊さん. Other meanings: shaved head (坊主頭, 坊主にする \"to shave one's head\"), familiarly \"boy\" (うちの坊主 \"my boy\") and, in fishing, \"caught nothing\". The expression 三日坊主 denotes someone who quickly gives up on their plans.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"各会場に、案内係が立っているはずです。","romaji":"Kaku kaijō ni, annaigakari ga tatte iru hazu desu.","german":"There should be a guide stationed at each venue.","furigana":"かくかいじょう|あんないがかり|た"},{"japanese":"各学校に同じ資料を送りました。","romaji":"Kaku gakkō ni onaji shiryō o okurimashita.","german":"I sent the same materials to each school.","furigana":"かくがっこう|おな|しりょう|おく"}],"notes":"各〜 stands before a noun and means \"each individual\" or \"all respectively\": 各会場 means every venue, 各学校 every school. It suits organizational announcements and the distribution of tasks or documents. No の stands before the noun. The sign 〜 marks the following element and is not pronounced; 各 is the spelling of the prefix.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このアプリのユーザーは世界各地にいます。","romaji":"Kono apuri no yuuzaa wa sekai kakuchi ni imasu.","german":"This app has users all over the world.","furigana":"せかいかくち"},{"japanese":"週末は、全国各地で雪が降るでしょう。","romaji":"Shuumatsu wa, zenkoku kakuchi de yuki ga furu deshou.","german":"This weekend, it will probably snow all around the country.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|ぜんこくかくち|ゆき|ふ"},{"japanese":"旅行で、日本各地の温泉を回りました。","romaji":"Ryokou de, Nihon kakuchi no onsen o mawarimashita.","german":"On my trip, I toured hot springs all over Japan.","furigana":"りょこう|にほんかくち|おんせん|まわ"}],"notes":"各地 means \"various places, everywhere (in an area)\" and is a noun. Typical: 全国各地 \"all over the country\", 世界各地 \"all over the world\", 日本各地, 各地で \"in many places\", 各地の天気 in the weather report. The tone is factual (news, reports); in conversation, あちこち or いろいろな所 is more usual.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"位置について、用意、ドン！","romaji":"Ichi ni tsuite, yōi, don!","german":"On your marks, get set, go!","furigana":"いち|ようい"},{"japanese":"机の位置を少し変えてみました。","romaji":"Tsukue no ichi o sukoshi kaete mimashita.","german":"I changed the position of the desk a little.","furigana":"つくえ|いち|すこ|か"},{"japanese":"長野県は本州のほぼ中央に位置しています。","romaji":"Nagano-ken wa Honshū no hobo chūō ni ichi shite imasu.","german":"Nagano Prefecture is located almost in the center of Honshū.","furigana":"ながのけん|ほんしゅう|ちゅうおう|いち"}],"notes":"位置 is the position or location of an object or place: 位置を変える (\"to rearrange\"), 位置を確認する (\"to check the position\"). As an intransitive する-verb, Xに位置する means \"to be located in X\" - typical of geography and rather written. For an arbitrary place, everyday speech uses 場所; social standing or standpoint is usually expressed with 地位 or 立場. Fixed: 位置について、用意、ドン！ as the starting command.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本では引っ越し費用って大体どれくらいなの？","romaji":"Nippon de wa hikkoshi hiyō tte daitai dore kurai na no?","german":"Roughly how much does moving cost in Japan?","furigana":"にっぽん|ひ|こ|ひよう|だいたい"},{"japanese":"留学にはかなりの費用がかかります。","romaji":"Ryūgaku ni wa kanari no hiyō ga kakarimasu.","german":"Studying abroad costs quite a lot.","furigana":"りゅうがく|ひよう"},{"japanese":"修理の費用は会社が負担してくれました。","romaji":"Shūri no hiyō wa kaisha ga futan shite kuremashita.","german":"The company covered the repair costs.","furigana":"しゅうり|ひよう|かいしゃ|ふたん"}],"notes":"費用 is the cost incurred for an undertaking: 費用がかかる (\"it costs\"), 費用を払う, 費用を負担する (\"to bear the cost\"). It sounds somewhat more businesslike than everyday speech and often appears in compounds such as 引っ越し費用 or 旅行費用. The price of a product is 値段, a fee for a service 料金, and for individual running costs one uses the suffix 〜代 (電気代, タクシー代).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は社費で日本語を勉強させてもらっています。","romaji":"Watashi wa shahi de nihongo o benkyou sasete moratte imasu.","german":"I am being allowed to study Japanese at my company's expense.","furigana":"わたし|しゃひ|にほんご|べんきょう"},{"japanese":"彼は社費でアメリカの大学に留学した。","romaji":"Kare wa shahi de Amerika no daigaku ni ryūgaku shita.","german":"He studied abroad at an American university at company expense.","furigana":"かれ|しゃひ|だいがく|りゅうがく"},{"japanese":"社費を無駄に使わないようにしましょう。","romaji":"Shahi o muda ni tsukawanai yō ni shimashō.","german":"Let's not waste company money.","furigana":"しゃひ|むだ|つか"}],"notes":"社費 is costs borne by the company and almost always appears in the form 社費で \"at company expense\": 社費で留学する, 社費で研修に行く. The counterpart is 自費 (\"at one's own expense\"). Related is 経費 (business expenses), which is heard more often in the office, for example 経費で落とす \"to charge to the company\". Register: business language.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"中国市場の暴落による地価の下落が止まりません。","romaji":"Chūgoku shijō no bōraku niyoru chika no geraku ga tomarimasen.","german":"The fall in land prices caused by the Chinese market crash won't stop.","furigana":"ちゅうごくしじょう|ぼうらく|ちか|げらく|と"},{"japanese":"東京の地価は世界的に見てもかなり高いです。","romaji":"Tōkyō no chika wa sekaiteki ni mite mo kanari takai desu.","german":"Land prices in Tokyo are quite high even by global standards.","furigana":"とうきょう|ちか|せかいてき|み|たか"},{"japanese":"駅ができてから、この辺りの地価が上がった。","romaji":"Eki ga dekite kara, kono atari no chika ga agatta.","german":"Since the station was built, land prices in this area have risen.","furigana":"えき|あた|ちか|あ"}],"notes":"地価 is the price of land, not of buildings. Typical: 地価が上がる／下がる, 地価が高い, 地価の下落 (\"fall in land prices\"); the word belongs mainly to the news and economic topics. Not to be confused with 物価 (general price level) or 家賃 (rent) - and with the homophone 地下 (\"underground, basement\"), which you can tell apart only by the writing.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの高校生のビデオへの批判の勢い、また強くなったね。","romaji":"Ano kōkōsei no bideo e no hihan no ikioi, mata tsuyoku natta ne.","german":"The momentum of criticism of that high school student's video has grown stronger again, hasn't it?","furigana":"こうこうせい|ひはん|いきお|つよ"},{"japanese":"彼はドアを勢いよく開けた。","romaji":"Kare wa doa o ikioi yoku aketa.","german":"He flung the door open with force.","furigana":"かれ|いきお|あ"},{"japanese":"酔った勢いで、彼女に告白してしまった。","romaji":"Yotta ikioi de, kanojo ni kokuhaku shite shimatta.","german":"In the heat of drunkenness, I ended up confessing to her.","furigana":"よ|いきお|かのじょ|こくはく"}],"notes":"勢い is momentum, force or drive - of movements, but also of people, companies or trends. Common: 勢いがある (\"energetic, on the rise\"), 勢いよく (\"vigorously, with force\"), 勢いを増す, 勢いがつく. With 〜た勢いで one describes something done in a moment of excess, e.g. 酔った勢いで \"in a drunken state\". 力, on the other hand, is simply \"strength\", 元気 the liveliness of a person.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"世界の二大勢力と言えば、どの国とどの国を思いうかべますか？","romaji":"Sekai no nidai seiryoku to ieba, dono kuni to dono kuni o omoiukabemasu ka?","german":"When you think of the world's two major powers, which countries come to mind?","furigana":"せかい|にだいせいりょく|い|くに|くに|おも"},{"japanese":"台風の勢力が少しずつ弱まってきました。","romaji":"Taifū no seiryoku ga sukoshi zutsu yowamatte kimashita.","german":"The strength of the typhoon has gradually weakened.","furigana":"たいふう|せいりょく|すこ|よわ"},{"japanese":"その政党は若者の間で勢力を伸ばしている。","romaji":"Sono seitō wa wakamono no aida de seiryoku o nobashite iru.","german":"That political party is expanding its influence among young people.","furigana":"せいとう|わかもの|あいだ|せいりょく|の"}],"notes":"勢力 is the power or influence of groups, parties or states: 勢力を伸ばす／拡大する (\"to gain influence\"), 勢力争い (\"power struggle\"), 二大勢力 (\"the two major powers\"). In weather reports it refers to the strength of a typhoon: 台風の勢力が強い／弱まる. 勢い, by contrast, is the momentum of a movement, 権力 the governing power of the state, and 影響 means \"influence\" in the sense of \"effect\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの店長はいつも低姿勢なので、好感が持てます。","romaji":"Ano tenchō wa itsumo teishisei na node, kōkan ga motemasu.","german":"That store manager always takes a humble stance, so I like him.","furigana":"てんちょう|ていしせい|こうかん|も"},{"japanese":"姿勢が悪いと、腰が痛くなりますよ。","romaji":"Shisei ga warui to, koshi ga itaku narimasu yo.","german":"If your posture is bad, your lower back will hurt.","furigana":"しせい|わる|こし|いた"},{"japanese":"その会社は環境問題に積極的な姿勢を見せている。","romaji":"Sono kaisha wa kankyō mondai ni sekkyokuteki na shisei o misete iru.","german":"That company is showing a proactive stance on environmental issues.","furigana":"かいしゃ|かんきょうもんだい|せっきょくてき|しせい|み"}],"notes":"姿勢 is first of all body posture: 姿勢がいい／悪い, 姿勢を正す (\"to sit/stand up straight\"). Figuratively it means the stance or attitude toward a matter: 前向きな姿勢, 〜に対する姿勢. 低姿勢 means \"a humble, reserved manner\". 態度 describes more the concrete behavior toward others, and ポーズ a pose, for example for a photo.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大勢の生徒の出席を取る時、たまに一人か二人、名前を飛ばしてしまうことがあります。","romaji":"Ōzei no seito no shusseki o toru toki, tama ni hitori ka futari, namae o tobashite shimau koto ga arimasu.","german":"When I take attendance for a large number of students, I sometimes skip one or two names.","furigana":"おおぜい|せいと|しゅっせき|と|とき|ひとり|ふたり|なまえ|と"},{"japanese":"今日は大勢で公園に行きます。","romaji":"Kyō wa ōzei de kōen ni ikimasu.","german":"Today we're going to the park in a big group.","furigana":"きょう|おおぜい|こうえん|い"}],"notes":"大勢 refers to a large number of people. As a noun it connects with の: 大勢の生徒 \"many students\". It is not a general quantity expression for things. 出席を取る means to take attendance. 大勢で can describe an action as a joint undertaking of many people.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 39"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の両親は、私営の老人ホームで働いています。","romaji":"Watashi no ryōshin wa, shiei no rōjin hōmu de hataraite imasu.","german":"My parents work at a privately run nursing home.","furigana":"わたし|りょうしん|しえい|ろうじん|はたら"},{"japanese":"日本では私営の鉄道を「私鉄」と呼びます。","romaji":"Nihon de wa shiei no tetsudō o \"shitetsu\" to yobimasu.","german":"In Japan, privately run railways are called \"shitetsu\".","furigana":"にほん|しえい|てつどう|してつ|よ"},{"japanese":"この駐車場は私営なので、料金が少し高いです。","romaji":"Kono chūshajō wa shiei na node, ryōkin ga sukoshi takai desu.","german":"This parking lot is privately run, so the fee is a bit high.","furigana":"ちゅうしゃじょう|しえい|りょうきん|すこ|たか"}],"notes":"私営 means \"privately operated\", that is, run by private individuals or companies, in contrast to 公営, 国営, 市営 or 官営. It appears as a prefix (私営バス) or with の before a noun (私営の鉄道). In daily life, 民営 (\"privately owned\", cf. 民営化 \"privatization\") is heard more often, or 私鉄 for private railways. Note: 市営 (\"municipal\") is read しえい in the same way.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今は市営住宅に住んでいます。","romaji":"Ima wa shiei jūtaku ni sunde imasu.","german":"I live in municipal housing now.","furigana":"いま|しえいじゅうたく|す"},{"japanese":"この町では、お年寄りは市営バスに無料で乗れます。","romaji":"Kono machi de wa, otoshiyori wa shiei basu ni muryō de noremasu.","german":"In this town, seniors can ride the city buses for free.","furigana":"まち|としよ|しえい|むりょう|の"},{"japanese":"夏休みは毎日、市営プールに通っていた。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi wa mainichi, shiei pūru ni kayotte ita.","german":"I went to the municipal pool every day during summer vacation.","furigana":"なつやす|まいにち|しえい|かよ"}],"notes":"市営 means \"run by the city\" and mostly appears as a prefix: 市営バス, 市営地下鉄, 市営住宅 (municipal public housing), 市営プール; or with の: 市営の施設. In the same series are 国営, 県営, 町営 and, as the opposite, 私営 - which is read just the same, しえい. For municipal schools, hospitals or libraries, on the other hand, one says 市立 (市立図書館).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"明治時代には、官営の工場がたくさん作られた。","romaji":"Meiji jidai ni wa, kan'ei no kōjō ga takusan tsukurareta.","german":"In the Meiji period, many government-run factories were built.","furigana":"めいじじだい|かんえい|こうじょう|つく"},{"japanese":"官営と民営の違いを説明してください。","romaji":"Kan'ei to min'ei no chigai o setsumei shite kudasai.","german":"Please explain the difference between government-run and privately run.","furigana":"かんえい|みんえい|ちが|せつめい"}],"notes":"官営 means \"operated by the government\" (官 = government office, 営 = to operate). Today it appears mainly in historical texts about the Meiji period, for example 官営工場 or 官営八幡製鉄所. For present-day state enterprises, 国営 is used, for public facilities in general 公営; the opposite is 民営 or 私営. Register: written, history and economics.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"県営の公園で、毎朝ジョギングしています。","romaji":"Ken'ei no kōen de, maiasa jogingu shite imasu.","german":"I jog every morning in a prefecture-run park.","furigana":"けんえい|こうえん|まいあさ"},{"japanese":"県営住宅の申し込みは来月から始まります。","romaji":"Ken'ei jūtaku no mōshikomi wa raigetsu kara hajimarimasu.","german":"Applications for prefectural housing start next month.","furigana":"けんえいじゅうたく|もう|こ|らいげつ|はじ"},{"japanese":"この野球場は県営なので、使用料が安い。","romaji":"Kono yakyūjō wa ken'ei na node, shiyōryō ga yasui.","german":"This baseball stadium is run by the prefecture, so the usage fee is cheap.","furigana":"やきゅうじょう|けんえい|しようりょう|やす"}],"notes":"県営 means \"operated by the prefecture\" and appears mostly as a prefix or with の: 県営住宅, 県営球場, 県営の公園. It belongs to the series 国営 (national), 県営, 市営 (municipal), 町営 and 私営 (private). For schools and similar prefectural institutions, 県立 is used instead (県立高校).","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大統領のスピーチはとても感動的でした。","romaji":"Daitōryō no supīchi wa totemo kandōteki deshita.","german":"The president's speech was very moving.","furigana":"だいとうりょう|かんどうてき"},{"japanese":"アメリカの大統領選挙は4年に一度行われます。","romaji":"Amerika no daitōryō senkyo wa yonen ni ichido okonawaremasu.","german":"The US presidential election is held once every four years.","furigana":"だいとうりょうせんきょ|ねん|いちどおこな"},{"japanese":"日本には大統領はいなくて、首相がいます。","romaji":"Nihon ni wa daitōryō wa inakute, shushō ga imasu.","german":"Japan has no president; it has a prime minister.","furigana":"にほん|だいとうりょう|しゅしょう"}],"notes":"大統領 is the president of a state, such as the USA or France; 大統領選挙 is the presidential election. Other \"presidents\" have their own words: company president 社長, chairman 会長, university president 学長. Japan itself has no 大統領, but a prime minister (首相, 総理大臣). The shout よっ、大統領！ to a performer is an old-fashioned, joking compliment.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 21"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"本日のプレゼンでは、主に3つの策を取り上げます。","romaji":"Honjitsu no purezen de wa, omo ni mittsu no saku o toriagemasu.","german":"In today's presentation, I will mainly take up three measures.","furigana":"ほんじつ|おも|さく|と|あ"},{"japanese":"何かいい策はありませんか？","romaji":"Nani ka ii saku wa arimasen ka?","german":"Do you have any good ideas?","furigana":"なに|さく"},{"japanese":"政府は少子化対策として新しい制度を作った。","romaji":"Seifu wa shōshika taisaku to shite atarashii seido o tsukutta.","german":"As a measure against the declining birthrate, the government created a new system.","furigana":"せいふ|しょうしかたいさく|あたら|せいど|つく"}],"notes":"策 is a plan, measure or stratagem for solving a problem. On its own it appears in rather written expressions like 策を練る (\"to devise a plan\"), 策を講じる (\"to take measures\") or いい策がない. It is much more common in compounds: 対策 (countermeasure), 政策 (policy), 解決策 (solution). In everyday speech one rather says 方法, 手 (打つ手がない) or 作戦.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"昨晩、私の本にぴったりのすごくいい副題を思いついたの。","romaji":"Sakuban, watashi no hon ni pittari no sugoku ii fukudai o omoitsuita no.","german":"Last night I came up with a really good subtitle that fits my book perfectly.","furigana":"さくばん|わたし|ほん|ふくだい|おも"},{"japanese":"この論文の副題は少し長すぎると思う。","romaji":"Kono ronbun no fukudai wa sukoshi nagasugiru to omou.","german":"I think the subtitle of this paper is a bit too long.","furigana":"ろんぶん|ふくだい|すこ|なが|おも"},{"japanese":"その映画には「最後の夏」という副題が付いています。","romaji":"Sono eiga ni wa \"Saigo no natsu\" to iu fukudai ga tsuite imasu.","german":"The film has the subtitle \"The Last Summer\".","furigana":"えいが|さいご|なつ|ふくだい|つ"}],"notes":"副題 is the subtitle of a book, film or article, that is, the addition to the main title (題名, タイトル); サブタイトル is synonymous. Typical: 副題を付ける, 副題が付いている, 「〜」という副題. Note: the subtitles shown on screen in films are not called 副題 but 字幕. 見出し is a headline in a newspaper or text.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その試合を観た時は、確か二十才を過ぎていたと思います。","romaji":"Sono shiai o mita toki wa, tashika nijussai o sugite ita to omoimasu.","german":"I think I was past twenty when I watched that match.","furigana":"しあい|み|とき|たし|にじゅっさい|す|おも"},{"japanese":"同じ映画を何度も観ると、初めは気づかなかった表情が見えてくる。","romaji":"Onaji eiga o nando mo miru to, hajime wa kizukanakatta hyōjō ga miete kuru.","german":"When you watch the same movie many times, you start to see expressions you didn't notice at first.","furigana":"おな|えいが|なんど|み|はじ|き|ひょうじょう|み"}],"notes":"観る（みる）is used for watching films, performances or sporting events. What is watched takes を. It is a transitive ichidan verb: 観ます, 観て, 観た. 見る is the more general spelling for \"to see\"; the special spelling 観る is used for spectating and viewing. Even in this spelling the reading is みる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このマンションの外観が好きです。","romaji":"Kono manshon no gaikan ga suki desu.","german":"I like the exterior of this apartment building.","furigana":"がいかん|す"},{"japanese":"外観は古いけど、中はとてもきれいなホテルだった。","romaji":"Gaikan wa furui kedo, naka wa totemo kirei na hoteru datta.","german":"It was a hotel that looked old on the outside but was very clean inside.","furigana":"がいかん|ふる|なか"},{"japanese":"京都では、建物の外観の色にもルールがあります。","romaji":"Kyōto de wa, tatemono no gaikan no iro ni mo rūru ga arimasu.","german":"In Kyoto, there are even rules for the exterior color of buildings.","furigana":"きょうと|たてもの|がいかん|いろ"}],"notes":"外観 is the outward appearance, especially of buildings and objects: 建物の外観, 外観がいい, 外観は古いが…. The counterpart is the interior (内装, 中). For people's looks, one says 外見 or, colloquially, 見た目. The word sounds somewhat matter-of-fact and written, e.g. in real estate or product descriptions.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この区域の治安を良くする方法について、何か提案はありませんか？","romaji":"Kono kuiki no chian o yoku suru hōhō nitsuite, nani ka teian wa arimasen ka?","german":"Do you have any suggestions on how to improve public safety in this area?","furigana":"くいき|ちあん|よ|ほうほう|なに|ていあん"},{"japanese":"ここは駐車禁止区域です。","romaji":"Koko wa chūsha kinshi kuiki desu.","german":"This is a no-parking zone.","furigana":"ちゅうしゃきんしくいき"},{"japanese":"地震の後、この区域は立ち入り禁止になった。","romaji":"Jishin no ato, kono kuiki wa tachiiri kinshi ni natta.","german":"After the earthquake, this area was closed off to entry.","furigana":"じしん|あと|くいき|た|い|きんし"}],"notes":"区域 is a delimited area or zone, often officially designated: 禁煙区域, 駐車禁止区域, 危険区域, 立ち入り禁止区域. 地域, by contrast, is a region or locality in the general sense, 地区 is a district or neighborhood, and 区 is the administrative ward of a large city (新宿区). Register: rather matter-of-fact; typical of signs and government offices.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これを最後に、きっぱりとタバコを吸うのを止めるつもりなんだ。","romaji":"Kore o saigo ni, kippari to tabako o sū no o yameru tsumori nan da.","german":"After this one, I'm going to quit smoking for good.","furigana":"さいご|す|や"},{"japanese":"外に出て、新鮮な空気を吸いました。","romaji":"Soto ni dete, shinsen na kūki o suimashita.","german":"I went outside and breathed in fresh air.","furigana":"そと|で|しんせん|くうき|す"}],"notes":"吸う is a transitive godan verb: 吸います, 吸って. It can mean breathing in air or smoking a cigarette; with を you name the air or tobacco. 吸うのをやめる ends the activity, and つもり names the intention. In sū the u is long; the negative form is 吸わない.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このお皿、ヒビが入ってるみたいに見えるけど、わざとそういうデザインなの！","romaji":"Kono o-sara, hibi ga haitteru mitai ni mieru kedo, wazato sou iu dezain na no!","german":"This plate looks like it has a crack, but it's designed that way on purpose!","furigana":"さら|はい|み"},{"japanese":"彼はわざと負けたんじゃないかな。","romaji":"Kare wa wazato maketa n ja nai ka na.","german":"I wonder if he lost on purpose.","furigana":"かれ|ま"},{"japanese":"わざとじゃないんです。本当にすみません。","romaji":"Wazato ja nai n desu. Hontō ni sumimasen.","german":"I didn't do it on purpose. I'm really sorry.","furigana":"ほんとう"}],"notes":"わざと means \"on purpose, deliberately\" and is almost always written in kana; the kanji 態と are rare. Very common is the apology わざとじゃない (\"it wasn't on purpose\"). Not to be confused with わざわざ (\"specially, going out of one's way\"), which stresses an effort made for someone. More formal are 故意に and 意図的に; わざとらしい means \"affected, put on\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"社長は大失態を犯し、それをごまかそうとしたが、それがかえってアダとなった。","romaji":"Shachou wa daishittai o okashi, sore o gomakasou to shita ga, sore ga kaette ada to natta.","german":"The company president committed a huge blunder and tried to cover it up, but that only backfired.","furigana":"しゃちょう|だいしったい|おか"},{"japanese":"大事な会議に遅刻するという失態を演じてしまった。","romaji":"Daiji na kaigi ni chikoku suru to iu shittai o enjite shimatta.","german":"I made the blunder of being late to an important meeting.","furigana":"だいじ|かいぎ|ちこく|しったい|えん"},{"japanese":"首相の失態に、国民の批判が集まった。","romaji":"Shushō no shittai ni, kokumin no hihan ga atsumatta.","german":"The prime minister's blunder drew criticism from the public.","furigana":"しゅしょう|しったい|こくみん|ひはん|あつ"}],"notes":"失態 is an embarrassing mistake or blunder, especially when someone in a position of responsibility damages their reputation. Fixed combinations: 失態を演じる, 失態を犯す, 大失態. More neutral and general is 失敗 (\"failure, mistake\"), ミス is a small mistake. The word sounds rather written and often appears in the news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"元日に友達から賀状が届いた。","romaji":"Ganjitsu ni tomodachi kara gajō ga todoita.","german":"A New Year's card arrived from a friend on New Year's Day.","furigana":"がんじつ|ともだち|がじょう|とど"},{"japanese":"喪中なので、今年は賀状を出しません。","romaji":"Mochū na node, kotoshi wa gajō o dashimasen.","german":"We are in mourning, so we are not sending New Year's cards this year.","furigana":"もちゅう|ことし|がじょう|だ"},{"japanese":"最近は年賀状を書く人が減っている。","romaji":"Saikin wa nengajō o kaku hito ga hette iru.","german":"Recently, fewer and fewer people write New Year's cards.","furigana":"さいきん|ねんがじょう|か|ひと|へ"}],"notes":"賀状 is a greeting card, almost always in the sense of a New Year's card; in everyday speech people usually say 年賀状 instead. They are sent at the end of the year so that they arrive on January 1: 賀状を出す, 賀状が届く. Anyone in a year of mourning (喪中) does not send them and instead sends a notice of mourning. For congratulations on other occasions, one says お祝いのカード or お祝いの手紙.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"無敵のスーパーマンに、不可能なことは何もなかった。","romaji":"Muteki no sūpāman ni, fukanō na koto wa nani mo nakatta.","german":"Nothing was impossible for the invincible Superman.","furigana":"むてき|ふかのう|なに"},{"japanese":"この仕事を明日までに終わらせるのは不可能です。","romaji":"Kono shigoto o ashita made ni owaraseru no wa fukanō desu.","german":"It is impossible to finish this work by tomorrow.","furigana":"しごと|あした|お|ふかのう"},{"japanese":"彼は不可能を可能にした男だ。","romaji":"Kare wa fukanō o kanō ni shita otoko da.","german":"He is the man who made the impossible possible.","furigana":"かれ|ふかのう|かのう|おとこ"}],"notes":"不可能 is a な-adjective and noun: 不可能なこと, 〜のは不可能だ, 不可能に近い (\"nearly impossible\"). The opposite is 可能. In conversation one often says the more casual 無理 or simply できない; 無理 can additionally mean \"excessive, unreasonable\". A well-known phrase: 不可能を可能にする (\"to make the impossible possible\").","levelNote":"Compound: 不可 (N3) + 能 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大嫌いな食べ物は何ですか。","romaji":"Daikirai na tabemono wa nani desu ka.","german":"What food do you really hate?","furigana":"だいきら|た|もの|なに"},{"japanese":"私は虫が大嫌いです。","romaji":"Watashi wa mushi ga daikirai desu.","german":"I can't stand bugs.","furigana":"わたし|むし|だいきら"},{"japanese":"「大嫌い！」と言って、妹は部屋を出て行った。","romaji":"\"Daikirai!\" to itte, imōto wa heya o dete itta.","german":"\"I hate you!\" my little sister said and left the room.","furigana":"だいきら|い|いもうと|へや|で|い"}],"notes":"大嫌い is a な-adjective (\"hated, can't stand at all\"), not a verb: the thing one hates is marked with が - 私は虫が大嫌いだ. It is the intensification of 嫌い and the opposite of 大好き. In conversation it sounds emotional, often childish or defiant (\"I hate you!\"). The real verb for deep hatred is 憎む, which weighs much heavier.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"僕の順番を飛ばさないでよ！","romaji":"Boku no junban o tobasanaide yo!","german":"Don't skip my turn!","furigana":"ぼく|じゅんばん|と"},{"japanese":"強い風で帽子を飛ばされた。","romaji":"Tsuyoi kaze de bōshi o tobasareta.","german":"My hat was blown off by the strong wind.","furigana":"つよ|かぜ|ぼうし|と"},{"japanese":"高速道路で車を飛ばしすぎて、スピード違反で捕まった。","romaji":"Kōsoku dōro de kuruma o tobashisugite, supīdo ihan de tsukamatta.","german":"I drove too fast on the highway and got caught for speeding.","furigana":"こうそくどうろ|くるま|と|いはん|つか"}],"notes":"飛ばす is the transitive counterpart of 飛ぶ (\"to fly\") and first means \"to make fly\": 紙飛行機を飛ばす, often passive 風に帽子を飛ばされる. Other common meanings: \"to skip, to leave out\" (ページを飛ばす, 順番を飛ばす), \"to speed\" (車を飛ばす) and colloquially \"to transfer (as a punishment)\" (地方に飛ばされる). Also found are 冗談を飛ばす (\"to crack jokes\") and, in cooking, アルコールを飛ばす (\"to cook off the alcohol\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ちゃんとドアにカギをかけたかどうか確かめた方がいいですよ。","romaji":"Chanto doa ni kagi o kaketa ka dō ka tashikameta hō ga iidesu yo.","german":"You should check whether the door is properly locked.","furigana":"たし|ほう"},{"japanese":"うわさが本当かどうか、自分の目で確かめたい。","romaji":"Uwasa ga hontō ka dō ka, jibun no me de tashikametai.","german":"I want to see with my own eyes whether the rumor is true.","furigana":"ほんとう|じぶん|め|たし"},{"japanese":"出かける前に、電車の時間を確かめておこう。","romaji":"Dekakeru mae ni, densha no jikan o tashikamete okō.","german":"Let's check the train times before we leave.","furigana":"で|まえ|でんしゃ|じかん|たし"}],"notes":"確かめる means \"to make sure, to verify\" - by looking, asking or trying: Xを確かめる or 〜かどうか確かめる. 確認する means the same but sounds more formal and is more usual in business. 調べる, on the other hand, means \"to find out, to look up\" when one doesn't yet know something. Fixed phrase: 自分の目で確かめる (\"to see with one's own eyes\"). Related is the adjective 確か (\"certain\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"空港で荷物を検査されました。","romaji":"Kūkō de nimotsu o kensa saremashita.","german":"My luggage was inspected at the airport.","furigana":"くうこう|にもつ|けんさ"},{"japanese":"この工場では、すべての製品を一つずつ検査しています。","romaji":"Kono kōjō de wa, subete no seihin o hitotsu zutsu kensa shite imasu.","german":"In this factory, every product is inspected one by one.","furigana":"こうじょう|せいひん|ひと|けんさ"},{"japanese":"来週、病院で胃の検査を受けることになった。","romaji":"Raishū, byōin de i no kensa o ukeru koto ni natta.","german":"Next week I'm going to have my stomach examined at the hospital.","furigana":"らいしゅう|びょういん|い|けんさ|う"}],"notes":"検査する means \"to check, examine, inspect by fixed criteria\": 荷物を検査する, 製品を検査する. In medicine the patient usually says 検査を受ける (血液検査, 胃の検査); the doctor's examination in the consulting room, on the other hand, is called 診察. 調べる is more general (\"to look into, to find out\"), 試験 is an exam in the sense of a test. Register: neutral to formal.","levelNote":"Derived from 検査 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"僕は、ずっとあのアイドルを応援しています。","romaji":"Boku wa, zutto ano aidoru o ōen shite imasu.","german":"I've been supporting that idol all along.","furigana":"ぼく|おうえん"},{"japanese":"明日の試合、みんなで応援に行こう！","romaji":"Ashita no shiai, minna de ōen ni ikō!","german":"Let's all go and cheer at tomorrow's match!","furigana":"あした|しあい|おうえん|い"},{"japanese":"新しい仕事、頑張ってね。応援してるよ。","romaji":"Atarashii shigoto, ganbatte ne. Ōen shiteru yo.","german":"Good luck with the new job. I'm rooting for you.","furigana":"あたら|しごと|がんば|おうえん"}],"notes":"応援する means \"to cheer on\" (in sports: チームを応援する, 応援に行く) and \"to support, to keep one's fingers crossed\" - for a star or a friend, for example: 応援してるよ. Another meaning is \"reinforcement, temporary help\": 応援を頼む, 他の店から応援に来る. For formal, often financial help one says 支援, for concrete helping out 手伝う.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"パスワードの変更をしてください。","romaji":"Pasuwādo no henkō o shite kudasai.","german":"Please change your password.","furigana":"へんこう"},{"japanese":"会議の予定が変更になりました。","romaji":"Kaigi no yotei ga henkō ni narimashita.","german":"The schedule of the meeting has changed.","furigana":"かいぎ|よてい|へんこう"},{"japanese":"雨のため、試合の会場を体育館に変更します。","romaji":"Ame no tame, shiai no kaijō o taiikukan ni henkō shimasu.","german":"Because of the rain, we are changing the venue of the match to the gymnasium.","furigana":"あめ|しあい|かいじょう|たいいくかん|へんこう"}],"notes":"変更 is a change of plans, data or settings; as a transitive する-verb: Xを変更する, AをBに変更する. Without an agent one likes to say 変更になる (\"will be changed\"), typical in announcements and notices. In everyday speech one rather says 変える (transitive) or 変わる (intransitive); 変更 sounds businesslike and matter-of-fact. Common: 予定の変更, パスワードの変更, 変更点.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"降車の際は、お忘れ物にご注意ください。","romaji":"Kōsha no sai wa, o wasuremono ni go chūi kudasai.","german":"When getting off, please be careful not to leave anything behind.","furigana":"こうしゃ|さい|わす|もの|ちゅうい"},{"japanese":"次で降りる方は、降車ボタンを押してください。","romaji":"Tsugi de oriru kata wa, kōsha botan o oshite kudasai.","german":"If you are getting off at the next stop, please press the stop button.","furigana":"つぎ|お|かた|こうしゃ|お"},{"japanese":"このバスでは、前のドアから降車してください。","romaji":"Kono basu de wa, mae no doa kara kōsha shite kudasai.","german":"On this bus, please get off through the front door.","furigana":"まえ|こうしゃ"}],"notes":"降車 is the official word for \"getting off\" a bus, train or taxi and is found mainly in announcements and on signs: 降車口, 降車ボタン, 降車の際は…. In everyday speech people simply say 降りる. The opposite is 乗車 (\"boarding\"). Intransitive: Xから降車する, Xで降車する. Homophones such as 校舎, 公社 or 後者 are told apart by their written form.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"脱衣をしてから入浴してください。","romaji":"Datsui o shite kara nyūyoku shite kudasai.","german":"Please undress before bathing.","furigana":"だつい|にゅうよく"},{"japanese":"温泉の脱衣所に、貴重品を入れるロッカーがあります。","romaji":"Onsen no datsuijo ni, kichōhin o ireru rokkā ga arimasu.","german":"In the changing room of the hot spring, there are lockers for valuables.","furigana":"おんせん|だついじょ|きちょうひん|い"},{"japanese":"脱いだ服は脱衣かごに入れてください。","romaji":"Nuida fuku wa datsui kago ni irete kudasai.","german":"Please put your removed clothes in the clothes basket.","furigana":"ぬ|ふく|だつい|い"}],"notes":"脱衣 is the formal word for \"to undress, to take off one's clothes\" and appears mainly on signs and in compounds: 脱衣所 or 脱衣場 (changing room at a bath, onsen or sento), 脱衣かご (clothes basket). In everyday speech one says 服を脱ぐ. The opposite is 着衣. As a する-verb it is intransitive; the clothing is not named as an object.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お手洗いは、真っすぐ行って左です。","romaji":"Otearai wa, massugu itte hidari desu.","german":"The restroom is straight ahead and then on the left.","furigana":"てあら|ま|い|ひだり"},{"japanese":"お手洗いを借りてもいいですか。","romaji":"Otearai o karite mo ii desu ka.","german":"May I use your restroom?","furigana":"てあら|か"}],"notes":"お手洗い is a polite word for the toilet. The お is part of the word here and is written with it: otearai. 真っすぐ行って左 gives directions: go straight and then left. 借りてもいいですか politely asks whether you may use the restroom. 御手洗い is an additional dictionary and search spelling of the same word おてあらい. Practice the usual spelling お手洗い first; the variant changes neither the reading nor the meaning.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"外国人が日本に在留する時に必要になるものは何ですか？","romaji":"Gaikokujin ga Nippon ni zairyū suru toki ni hitsuyō ni naru mono wa nani desu ka?","german":"What do foreigners need when they stay in Japan?","furigana":"がいこくじん|にっぽん|ざいりゅう|とき|ひつよう|なに"},{"japanese":"在留カードはいつも持っていなければなりません。","romaji":"Zairyū kādo wa itsumo motte inakereba narimasen.","german":"You must always carry your residence card.","furigana":"ざいりゅう|も"},{"japanese":"在留期間が切れる前に、更新の手続きをした。","romaji":"Zairyū kikan ga kireru mae ni, kōshin no tetsuzuki o shita.","german":"Before my period of stay ran out, I went through the renewal procedure.","furigana":"ざいりゅうきかん|き|まえ|こうしん|てつづ"}],"notes":"在留する is an official word for the longer stay of foreigners in a country: Xに在留する. It appears in many official terms: 在留カード (residence card), 在留資格 (status of residence), 在留期間 (period of stay). For a temporary stay, such as on a trip or in a hotel, one says 滞在する; in everyday speech simply 住む. Register: administration, written.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"すぐ不機嫌になる人は嫌だ。","romaji":"Sugu fukigen ni naru hito wa iya da.","german":"I don't like people who get into a bad mood easily.","furigana":"ふきげん|ひと|いや"},{"japanese":"父は朝から不機嫌そうな顔をしている。","romaji":"Chichi wa asa kara fukigen sō na kao o shite iru.","german":"My father has had a grumpy look on his face since the morning.","furigana":"ちち|あさ|ふきげん|かお"},{"japanese":"寝不足で、一日中不機嫌だった。","romaji":"Nebusoku de, ichinichijū fukigen datta.","german":"Because I was short on sleep, I was in a bad mood all day.","furigana":"ねぶそく|いちにちじゅうふきげん"}],"notes":"不機嫌 is a な-adjective and noun for \"in a bad mood, grumpy\": 不機嫌になる, 不機嫌な顔, 不機嫌そう. The opposite is 上機嫌 (\"in the best of moods\"); ご機嫌 denotes good mood or appears in the polite question ご機嫌いかがですか. 怒る, on the other hand, means \"to get angry\", イライラする \"to be irritated, on edge\". 不機嫌 describes rather a quiet, visible ill humor.","levelNote":"Compound: 不 (N3) + 機嫌 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"衣服は着たままでかまいませんよ。","romaji":"Ifuku wa kita mama de kamaimasen yo.","german":"You can keep your clothes on.","furigana":"いふく|き"},{"japanese":"着なくなった衣服は、リサイクルに出しましょう。","romaji":"Kinakunatta ifuku wa, risaikuru ni dashimashō.","german":"Let's recycle clothes that we no longer wear.","furigana":"き|いふく|だ"},{"japanese":"衣服に付いた汚れは、すぐに洗えば落ちます。","romaji":"Ifuku ni tsuita yogore wa, sugu ni araeba ochimasu.","german":"Stains on clothing will come out if you wash them right away.","furigana":"いふく|つ|よご|あら|お"}],"notes":"衣服 is a matter-of-fact, written word for \"clothing\" and appears in notices, at the doctor's, on labels or in texts. In everyday speech one says 服 or 洋服. 衣類 denotes clothing rather as a product category (衣類の売り場), and 衣食住 (\"clothing, food, shelter\") the basic needs. Counter: 着.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この商品の価格はいくらですか？","romaji":"Kono shōhin no kakaku wa ikura desu ka?","german":"How much does this product cost?","furigana":"しょうひん|かかく"},{"japanese":"天気が悪くて、野菜の価格が上がっています。","romaji":"Tenki ga warukute, yasai no kakaku ga agatte imasu.","german":"Because of the bad weather, vegetable prices are rising.","furigana":"てんき|わる|やさい|かかく|あ"},{"japanese":"ネットで価格を比べてから買うことにしている。","romaji":"Netto de kakaku o kurabete kara kau koto ni shite iru.","german":"I make a point of comparing prices online before buying.","furigana":"かかく|くら|か"}],"notes":"価格 is the matter-of-fact word for \"price\" and appears above all in news, business texts and on price tags: 価格が上がる／下がる, 価格を比べる, 販売価格, 価格競争. In everyday conversation one mostly says 値段. To be distinguished are 物価 (general price level), 料金 (fee for services) and 費用 (cost of an undertaking).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"知らない人から急に頭に手を置かれたら、そりゃあ嫌悪感を感じるでしょう。","romaji":"Shiranai hito kara kyuu ni atama ni te o okaretara, soryaa ken'okan o kanjiru deshou.","german":"If a stranger suddenly put a hand on your head, of course you would feel revulsion.","furigana":"し|ひと|きゅう|あたま|て|お|けんおかん|かん"},{"japanese":"暴力的なシーンには強い嫌悪感がある。","romaji":"Bōryokuteki na shīn ni wa tsuyoi ken'okan ga aru.","german":"I have a strong aversion to violent scenes.","furigana":"ぼうりょくてき|つよ|けんおかん"},{"japanese":"彼の差別的な発言に、多くの人が嫌悪感を抱いた。","romaji":"Kare no sabetsuteki na hatsugen ni, ōku no hito ga ken'okan o idaita.","german":"His discriminatory remarks caused disgust in many people.","furigana":"かれ|さべつてき|はつげん|おお|ひと|けんおかん|いだ"}],"notes":"嫌悪感 is a strong feeling of disgust or aversion toward a person, action or thing. Typical verbs: 嫌悪感を抱く, 嫌悪感を覚える, 嫌悪感がある; 嫌悪感を感じる is widespread but is considered stylistically awkward because of the doubling of 感. The word sounds rather written; in conversation one says 嫌だ, 気持ち悪い (\"gross\") or 大嫌い. 憎しみ, on the other hand, is \"hatred\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"家で待機するように言われてたんだけど、こっそり外出してマクドナルドでハンバーガーを買ってきちゃった。","romaji":"Ie de taiki suru you ni iwareteta n da kedo, kossori gaishutsu shite Makudonarudo de hanbaagaa o katte kichatta.","german":"I was told to stay home on standby, but I sneaked out and bought a hamburger at McDonald's.","furigana":"いえ|たいき|い|がいしゅつ|か"},{"japanese":"救急隊員は24時間待機しています。","romaji":"Kyūkyū taiin wa nijūyo jikan taiki shite imasu.","german":"Paramedics are on standby 24 hours a day.","furigana":"きゅうきゅうたいいん|じかんたいき"},{"japanese":"順番が来るまで、こちらの部屋で待機してください。","romaji":"Junban ga kuru made, kochira no heya de taiki shite kudasai.","german":"Please wait in this room until it's your turn.","furigana":"じゅんばん|く|へや|たいき"}],"notes":"待機 means \"to wait on standby, to be ready\" until an assignment or instruction comes: 待機する, 待機中 (also \"standby\" for devices). Common compounds are 自宅待機 (\"standby at home\", also quarantine) and 待機児童 (\"children on a daycare waiting list\"). The general \"to wait\" is 待つ; 待機 sounds official and formal. Intransitive: Xで待機する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どうして素直にありがとうって言うことができないの？","romaji":"Dōshite sunao ni arigatō tte iu koto ga dekinai no?","german":"Why can't you just say thank you honestly?","furigana":"すなお|い"},{"japanese":"手伝ってくれて、ありがとう。","romaji":"Tetsudatte kurete, arigatō.","german":"Thank you for helping me.","furigana":"てつだ"},{"japanese":"先生、一年間本当にありがとうございました。","romaji":"Sensei, ichinenkan hontō ni arigatō gozaimashita.","german":"Teacher, thank you so much for the whole year.","furigana":"せんせい|いちねんかんほんとう"}],"notes":"ありがとう is the casual \"thank you\" among friends and in the family; politely one says ありがとうございます, and with ありがとうございました one thanks for something already completed. It is almost always written in kana; the kanji 有り難う go back to 有り難い (\"rare, precious\"). Other ways to thank: どうも (short), すみません (thanks with a shade of apology) and 恐れ入ります (very polite).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"それは心に刺さる言葉だった。","romaji":"Sore wa kokoro ni sasaru kotoba datta.","german":"Those were words that struck me to the heart.","furigana":"こころ|さ|ことば"},{"japanese":"魚の骨がのどに刺さった。","romaji":"Sakana no hone ga nodo ni sasatta.","german":"A fish bone got stuck in my throat.","furigana":"さかな|ほね|さ"},{"japanese":"ダーツが的の真ん中に刺さった。","romaji":"Dātsu ga mato no mannaka ni sasatta.","german":"The dart stuck in the center of the target.","furigana":"まと|ま|なか|さ"}],"notes":"刺さる is intransitive: something pointed pricks in and stays stuck - XがYに刺さる (とげが指に刺さる, 骨がのどに刺さる). The transitive counterpart is 刺す (\"to stab, to sting\", also of insects). Figuratively, 心に刺さる means \"to cut deep, to hit hard\"; in youth slang, 刺さる alone also means \"hits exactly my taste\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"アルコールの作用を教えてください。","romaji":"Arukōru no sayō o oshiete kudasai.","german":"Please tell me about the effects of alcohol.","furigana":"さよう|おし"},{"japanese":"この薬は副作用が少ないそうです。","romaji":"Kono kusuri wa fukusayō ga sukunai sō desu.","german":"I hear this medicine has few side effects.","furigana":"くすり|ふくさよう|すく"},{"japanese":"笑うことには、ストレスを減らす作用があるそうだ。","romaji":"Warau koto ni wa, sutoresu o herasu sayō ga aru sō da.","german":"Apparently, laughing has the effect of reducing stress.","furigana":"わら|へ|さよう"}],"notes":"作用 is an effect or function, especially in natural science and medicine: 〜作用がある, Xに作用する (\"to act on X\"). Well-known compounds are 副作用 (\"side effect\") and 反作用 (\"reaction, counteraction\"). 効果, by contrast, is desired effectiveness, and 影響 is influence; in everyday speech one simply says 効く (\"to work, to take effect\"). Register: matter-of-fact, technical.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この公社の設立には、多くの人々の協力が必要でした。","romaji":"Kono kōsha no setsuritsu ni wa, ōku no hitobito no kyōryoku ga hitsuyō deshita.","german":"The establishment of this public corporation required the cooperation of many people.","furigana":"こうしゃ|せつりつ|おお|ひとびと|きょうりょく|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"昔、日本の電話は電電公社が運営していた。","romaji":"Mukashi, Nihon no denwa wa Denden Kōsha ga un'ei shite ita.","german":"In the old days, Japan's telephones were run by the Nippon Telegraph and Telephone Public Corporation.","furigana":"むかし|にほん|でんわ|でんでんこうしゃ|うんえい"},{"japanese":"この団地は住宅供給公社が管理しています。","romaji":"Kono danchi wa jūtaku kyōkyū kōsha ga kanri shite imasu.","german":"This housing complex is managed by the housing supply corporation.","furigana":"だんち|じゅうたくきょうきゅうこうしゃ|かんり"}],"notes":"公社 is a public-law corporation that runs a business on behalf of the state or a municipality. Historically well known are the three big 公社 (国鉄, 電電公社, 専売公社), which were privatized in the 1980s into JR, NTT and JT; today there still exist, for example, regional 住宅供給公社. 会社, on the other hand, is an ordinary company. Homophones such as 降車 or 校舎 are distinguished by the writing.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この地区には、新しい学校を建てる計画があります。","romaji":"Kono chiku ni wa, atarashii gakkō o tateru keikaku ga arimasu.","german":"There is a plan to build a new school in this district.","furigana":"ちく|あたら|がっこう|た|けいかく"},{"japanese":"祖父は三十年前にこの家を建てました。","romaji":"Sofu wa sanjuunen mae ni kono ie o tatemashita.","german":"My grandfather built this house thirty years ago.","furigana":"そふ|さんじゅうねんまえ|いえ|た"},{"japanese":"駅の前に高いビルが建てられました。","romaji":"Eki no mae ni takai biru ga tateraremashita.","german":"A tall building was built in front of the station.","furigana":"えき|まえ|たか|た"}],"notes":"建てる means \"to erect, to build a building\": 家を建てる, ビルを建てる, 学校を建てる. The intransitive counterpart is 建つ: 駅前にビルが建った \"a building has gone up in front of the station\". Do not confuse it with the identically sounding 立てる \"to set up, to stand up\", which is also used for plans (計画を立てる) – in the first example both occur side by side. For things that are not structures, 作る is used.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お父さん、あんたが建築家になったから、鼻が高いのよ。","romaji":"Otōsan, anta ga kenchikuka ni natta kara, hana ga takai no yo.","german":"Dad, I'm so proud now that you have become an architect.","furigana":"とう|けんちくか|はな|たか"},{"japanese":"将来は建築家になって、自分の家を設計したい。","romaji":"Shōrai wa kenchikuka ni natte, jibun no ie o sekkei shitai.","german":"In the future I want to become an architect and design my own house.","furigana":"しょうらい|けんちくか|じぶん|いえ|せっけい"},{"japanese":"この美術館は有名な建築家が設計しました。","romaji":"Kono bijutsukan wa yūmei na kenchikuka ga sekkei shimashita.","german":"A famous architect designed this museum.","furigana":"びじゅつかん|ゆうめい|けんちくか|せっけい"}],"notes":"建築家 is the architect who designs buildings (設計する); the suffix 〜家, as in 作家 or 画家, marks an expert or artist. The officially certified professional title is 建築士 (e.g. 一級建築士), whereas 建築家 means more the creative, often well-known architect. A carpenter or construction craftsman is 大工.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 37"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"現在、この町の人口は約5万人です。","romaji":"Genzai, kono machi no jinkō wa yaku goman-nin desu.","german":"The population of this town is currently about 50,000.","furigana":"げんざい|まち|じんこう|やく|まんにん"},{"japanese":"現在の時刻は午後3時15分です。","romaji":"Genzai no jikoku wa gogo sanji jūgofun desu.","german":"The current time is 3:15 p.m."},{"japanese":"4月1日現在、会員は200人です。","romaji":"Shigatsu tsuitachi genzai, kaiin wa nihyaku-nin desu.","german":"As of April 1, there are 200 members."}],"notes":"現在 means \"the present, now, currently\" and is the more formal equivalent of 今: adverbial at the beginning of a sentence (現在、…), attributive with の (現在の状況). After a date it means \"as of\": 4月1日現在 \"as of April 1\". 現代, on the other hand, means the present as an era (\"today's age, the modern era\"). Opposites: 過去 (\"past\"), 未来 (\"future\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あれ、あの政治家って、保守主義者だっけ？","romaji":"Are, ano seijika tte, hoshushugisha da kke?","german":"Hey, is that politician a conservative?","furigana":"せいじか|ほしゅしゅぎしゃ"},{"japanese":"保守主義とは、伝統や今の社会の仕組みを大切にする考え方です。","romaji":"Hoshushugi to wa, dentō ya ima no shakai no shikumi o taisetsu ni suru kangaekata desu.","german":"Conservatism is a way of thinking that values tradition and the current structure of society.","furigana":"ほしゅしゅぎ|でんとう|いま|しゃかい|しく|たいせつ|かんが|かた"},{"japanese":"授業で保守主義と自由主義の違いについて学んだ。","romaji":"Jugyō de hoshushugi to jiyūshugi no chigai ni tsuite mananda.","german":"In class, we learned about the difference between conservatism and liberalism.","furigana":"じゅぎょう|ほしゅしゅぎ|じゆうしゅぎ|ちが|まな"}],"notes":"保守主義 is conservatism as a political outlook that wants to preserve tradition and the existing order (保守 = \"to preserve\", 主義 = \"-ism\"). Opposing concepts are 革新 (\"reform, progressive\") and 自由主義 (\"liberalism\"). For persons one says 保守派 or 保守主義者, as a quality 保守的 (\"conservative\"). Note: 保守 alone also means \"maintenance\" in IT. Register: written, politics and school.","levelNote":"Compound: 保守 (N1) + 主義 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"話が脱線しました。","romaji":"Hanashi ga dassen shimashita.","german":"The conversation went off topic.","furigana":"はなし|だっせん"},{"japanese":"大雨の影響で、電車が脱線する事故があった。","romaji":"Ōame no eikyō de, densha ga dassen suru jiko ga atta.","german":"There was an accident in which a train derailed because of heavy rain.","furigana":"おおあめ|えいきょう|でんしゃ|だっせん|じこ"},{"japanese":"先生の話はよく脱線するけど、それが面白い。","romaji":"Sensei no hanashi wa yoku dassen suru kedo, sore ga omoshiroi.","german":"The teacher often goes off on tangents, but that is what makes it interesting.","furigana":"せんせい|はなし|だっせん|おもしろ"}],"notes":"脱線 literally means the \"derailment\" of a train (脱線する, 脱線事故) and, figuratively, straying from the topic: 話が脱線する. The verb is intransitive; \"to take a conversation off topic\" is 話を脱線させる. Similar is 話がそれる (\"the conversation drifts off topic\"). In the figurative sense it is an everyday word; in the literal sense it is typical of news reports.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どうやって脱走に成功したの？","romaji":"Dō yatte dassō ni seikō shita no?","german":"How did you manage to escape?","furigana":"だっそう|せいこう"},{"japanese":"動物園からライオンが脱走して、大騒ぎになった。","romaji":"Dōbutsuen kara raion ga dassō shite, ōsawagi ni natta.","german":"A lion escaped from the zoo, causing a great uproar.","furigana":"どうぶつえん|だっそう|おおさわ"},{"japanese":"うちの猫は、窓が開いているとすぐ脱走する。","romaji":"Uchi no neko wa, mado ga aite iru to sugu dassō suru.","german":"Our cat escapes as soon as a window is open.","furigana":"ねこ|まど|あ|だっそう"}],"notes":"脱走する means \"to break out, to escape\" from a place where one is held: Xから脱走する (刑務所, 軍隊, 動物園, 檻). It is also used jokingly for pets that slip away. 逃げる is the general \"to flee, to run away\", 逃亡 the flight from the law (more formal), 家出 running away from home. As a noun: 脱走に成功する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この各駅停車はちょうどトンネルを通り過ぎたところです。","romaji":"Kono kakueki teisha wa chōdo tonneru o tōrisugita tokoro desu.","german":"This local train has just passed through a tunnel.","furigana":"かくえきていしゃ|とお|す"},{"japanese":"急行は止まらないので、各駅停車に乗ってください。","romaji":"Kyūkō wa tomaranai node, kakueki teisha ni notte kudasai.","german":"The express doesn't stop there, so please take the local train.","furigana":"きゅうこう|と|かくえきていしゃ|の"},{"japanese":"各駅停車でのんびり旅をするのが好きだ。","romaji":"Kakueki teisha de nonbiri tabi o suru no ga suki da.","german":"I like to travel at a leisurely pace by local train.","furigana":"かくえきていしゃ|たび|す"}],"notes":"各駅停車 is a train that stops at every station (各駅 = \"every station\", 停車 = \"stop\"). On display boards it is often shortened to 各停 or 普通. The opposite terms are the faster train types 快速, 急行 and 特急. Those in a hurry avoid it - those traveling at leisure gladly take it.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"買い物はオンラインで済ますことが多いです。","romaji":"Kaimono wa onrain de sumasu koto ga ōidesu.","german":"I often get my shopping done online.","furigana":"か|もの|す|おお"},{"japanese":"宿題を済ましてから遊びに行きなさい。","romaji":"Shukudai o sumashite kara asobi ni ikinasai.","german":"Finish your homework before you go out to play.","furigana":"しゅくだい|す|あそ|い"},{"japanese":"忙しかったので、昼ご飯はパン一つで済ました。","romaji":"Isogashikatta node, hirugohan wa pan hitotsu de sumashita.","german":"I was busy, so I made do with a single piece of bread for lunch.","furigana":"いそが|ひる|はん|ひと|す"}],"notes":"済ます is the transitive counterpart of 済む (\"to be finished\"): Xを済ます \"to get something done, to get something over with\". With で it means \"to make do with something, to get by with something\": パン一つで済ます; 謝って済ます means \"to dismiss it with an apology\". The variant 済ませる (ichidan) means the same and is in fact more common in present-day usage. Not to be confused with 澄ます (耳を澄ます \"to listen closely\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この人数なら、タクシーで行く方が経済的じゃない？","romaji":"Kono ninzū nara, takushī de iku hō ga keizaiteki ja nai?","german":"With this many people, wouldn't it be more economical to take a taxi?","furigana":"にんずう|い|ほう|けいざいてき"},{"japanese":"経済的な理由で、大学をやめることになった。","romaji":"Keizaiteki na riyū de, daigaku o yameru koto ni natta.","german":"For financial reasons, I had to quit university.","furigana":"けいざいてき|りゆう|だいがく"},{"japanese":"両親は経済的に苦しい中、私を育ててくれた。","romaji":"Ryōshin wa keizaiteki ni kurushii naka, watashi o sodatete kureta.","german":"My parents raised me despite financial hardship.","furigana":"りょうしん|けいざいてき|くる|なか|わたし|そだ"}],"notes":"経済的 is a な-adjective with two meanings: \"economic, financial\" (経済的な理由, 経済的に苦しい, 経済的に自立する) and \"economical, cheap\" (〜方が経済的だ). Adverbially: 経済的に. For \"cheap\" in the simple sense one says 安い; \"to save\" is 節約する. Register: neutral, also common in conversations about money.","levelNote":"Compound: 経済 (N5) + 的 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このクラブでは、毎年会長を選挙で選んでいます。","romaji":"Kono kurabu de wa, maitoshi kaichō o senkyo de erande imasu.","german":"In this club, the president is chosen by election every year.","furigana":"まいとしかいちょう|せんきょ|えら"},{"japanese":"日曜日に選挙に行きました。","romaji":"Nichiyōbi ni senkyo ni ikimashita.","german":"I went to vote on Sunday.","furigana":"にちようび|せんきょ|い"},{"japanese":"来月、市長の選挙が行われます。","romaji":"Raigetsu, shichō no senkyo ga okonawaremasu.","german":"Next month the mayoral election will be held.","furigana":"らいげつ|しちょう|せんきょ|おこな"}],"notes":"選挙 is an election (political or in clubs); the verb form 選挙する is rare and sounds stiff. Common are 選挙がある／行われる, 選挙に行く (\"to go to vote\"), 選挙に出る (\"to run as a candidate\") and 選挙で選ぶ (\"to choose by election\"). The casting of the vote itself is 投票する, and \"to vote for someone\" in everyday speech is simply 選ぶ. Important words: 選挙権 (right to vote), 総選挙 (general election).","levelNote":"Derived from 選挙 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は責任感が強い人だ。","romaji":"Kanojo wa sekininkan ga tsuyoi hito da.","german":"She is a person with a strong sense of responsibility.","furigana":"かのじょ|せきにんかん|つよ|ひと"},{"japanese":"責任感のない人に、この仕事は任せられない。","romaji":"Sekininkan no nai hito ni, kono shigoto wa makaserarenai.","german":"I can't entrust this job to someone with no sense of responsibility.","furigana":"せきにんかん|ひと|しごと|まか"},{"japanese":"面接で、自分の長所は責任感が強いところだと答えた。","romaji":"Mensetsu de, jibun no chōsho wa sekininkan ga tsuyoi tokoro da to kotaeta.","german":"In the interview, I answered that my strength is my strong sense of responsibility.","furigana":"めんせつ|じぶん|ちょうしょ|せきにんかん|つよ|こた"}],"notes":"責任感 is the feeling of being responsible for something, that is, a sense of responsibility or duty. It almost always appears with 強い / ある / ない: 責任感が強い \"very conscientious\", 責任感がない \"irresponsible\". In job interviews and references, 責任感が強い is a typical compliment. Not to be confused with 責任 itself, responsibility as duty or liability (責任を取る).","levelNote":"Compound: 責任 (N3) + 感 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"部品が足りなくて、工場は生産を停止した。","romaji":"Buhin ga tarinakute, kōjō wa seisan o teishi shita.","german":"The factory halted production because it was short of parts.","furigana":"ぶひん|た|こうじょう|せいさん|ていし"},{"japanese":"地震のため、電車が一時停止しています。","romaji":"Jishin no tame, densha ga ichiji teishi shite imasu.","german":"Because of the earthquake, trains are temporarily stopped.","furigana":"じしん|でんしゃ|いちじていし"},{"japanese":"違反を繰り返したので、運転免許が停止された。","romaji":"Ihan o kurikaeshita node, unten menkyo ga teishi sareta.","german":"Because of repeated violations, his driver's license was suspended.","furigana":"いはん|く|かえ|うんてんめんきょ|ていし"}],"notes":"停止 means \"stopping, standstill, suspension\" and is both intransitive (Xが停止する \"comes to a stop\") and transitive (Xを停止する \"to suspend, halt\"). Common combinations: 一時停止 (stop sign; pause in videos), 生産停止, 営業停止, 運転免許の停止 (colloquially 免停). In everyday speech people say 止まる／止める; 中止, by contrast, means the cancellation of an event. Register: formal, used in technology and administration.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"川崎市は停電しなかったの？","romaji":"Kawasakishi wa teiden shinakatta no?","german":"Didn't Kawasaki have a power outage?","furigana":"かわさきし|ていでん"},{"japanese":"台風で一晩中停電していた。","romaji":"Taifū de hitobanjū teiden shite ita.","german":"The power was out all night because of the typhoon.","furigana":"たいふう|ひとばんじゅうていでん"},{"japanese":"停電に備えて、懐中電灯を用意しておこう。","romaji":"Teiden ni sonaete, kaichū dentō o yōi shite okō.","german":"Let's get a flashlight ready in case of a power outage.","furigana":"ていでん|そな|かいちゅうでんとう|ようい"}],"notes":"停電 is a power outage; as an intransitive する-verb: 停電する, likewise 停電になる. The place or building appears as the subject (町が停電する) or as a location with で. When the power comes back, one says 電気が戻る or, formally, 復旧する; 計画停電 is a planned power cut. For water, the counterpart is 断水.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"吸血鬼はにんにくと十字架が苦手だと言われている。","romaji":"Kyūketsuki wa ninniku to jūjika ga nigate da to iwarete iru.","german":"Vampires are said to dislike garlic and crosses.","furigana":"きゅうけつき|じゅうじか|にがて|い"},{"japanese":"子どものころ、吸血鬼の映画を見て眠れなくなった。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, kyūketsuki no eiga o mite nemurenaku natta.","german":"As a child, I couldn't sleep after watching a vampire movie.","furigana":"こ|きゅうけつき|えいが|み|ねむ"},{"japanese":"給料が安いのに残業ばかりさせるなんて、まるで吸血鬼みたいな会社だ。","romaji":"Kyūryō ga yasui noni zangyō bakari saseru nante, marude kyūketsuki mitai na kaisha da.","german":"Low pay and constant overtime - that's a real bloodsucker of a company.","furigana":"きゅうりょう|やす|ざんぎょう|きゅうけつき|かいしゃ"}],"notes":"吸血鬼 is the vampire (吸血 = \"to suck blood\", 鬼 = \"demon\"). In pop culture and games one often also reads バンパイア or ヴァンパイア. Figuratively it denotes a \"bloodsucker\", someone who exploits others. Related is 吸血 in words like 吸血昆虫 (\"blood-sucking insects\" such as mosquitoes).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"若者の知識の吸収スピードにはついていけないよ。","romaji":"Wakamono no chishiki no kyūshū supīdo ni wa tsuiteikenai yo.","german":"I can't keep up with how fast young people absorb knowledge.","furigana":"わかもの|ちしき|きゅうしゅう"},{"japanese":"このタオルは水をよく吸収する。","romaji":"Kono taoru wa mizu o yoku kyuushuu suru.","german":"This towel absorbs water well.","furigana":"みず|きゅうしゅう"},{"japanese":"ビタミンCは鉄分の吸収を助けます。","romaji":"Bitamin shii wa tetsubun no kyuushuu o tasukemasu.","german":"Vitamin C helps the absorption of iron.","furigana":"てつぶん|きゅうしゅう|たす"}],"notes":"吸収 means \"to take in, to soak up, to absorb\" and, as a する-verb, is transitive: 水分を吸収する, 衝撃を吸収する, 知識を吸収する (\"to absorb knowledge\"). The passive or nominal form is very common: 栄養が体に吸収される, 鉄分の吸収. In business jargon, 吸収合併 stands for the takeover of one company by another. The everyday verb for \"to suck, to inhale\", on the other hand, is 吸う; 吸収 sounds more matter-of-fact and scientific.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この工場では皮革製品を作っています。","romaji":"Kono koujou de wa hikaku seihin o tsukutte imasu.","german":"This factory makes leather goods.","furigana":"こうじょう|ひかくせいひん|つく"},{"japanese":"この財布は天然皮革と合成皮革のどちらですか。","romaji":"Kono saifu wa tennen hikaku to gousei hikaku no dochira desu ka.","german":"Is this wallet made of genuine leather or synthetic leather?","furigana":"さいふ|てんねんひかく|ごうせいひかく"},{"japanese":"皮革の手入れには専用のクリームを使ってください。","romaji":"Hikaku no teire ni wa sen'you no kuriimu o tsukatte kudasai.","german":"Please use a special cream to care for leather.","furigana":"ひかく|てい|せんよう|つか"}],"notes":"皮革 is the factual technical term for \"leather, animal hide\" and appears mainly in industry, trade and on labels: 皮革製品 (\"leather goods\"), 天然皮革 (\"genuine leather\"), 合成皮革 (\"faux leather\"). In everyday speech people almost always say 革: 革の靴, 革のかばん. 皮 is raw skin or peel (みかんの皮), 革 is tanned leather; 皮革 covers both.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"また無職になってしまった。","romaji":"Mata mushoku ni natte shimatta.","german":"I'm unemployed again.","furigana":"むしょく"},{"japanese":"父は定年退職して、今は無職です。","romaji":"Chichi wa teinen taishoku shite, ima wa mushoku desu.","german":"My father retired and is now without a job.","furigana":"ちち|ていねんたいしょく|いま|むしょく"},{"japanese":"無職の期間が長いと、就職が難しくなる。","romaji":"Mushoku no kikan ga nagai to, shuushoku ga muzukashiku naru.","german":"The longer you are without a job, the harder it becomes to find employment.","furigana":"むしょく|きかん|なが|しゅうしょく|むずか"}],"notes":"無職 denotes the status \"without an occupation, without employment\" and is a noun that is used with の or だ: 無職の人, 今は無職だ, 無職になる. It also includes retirees or people who deliberately do not work, and often appears in forms and news reports (無職の男). 失業, on the other hand, emphasizes the loss of a job (失業中, 失業者), 求職中 the active job search. The opposite is 有職.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この職場、どう思う？","romaji":"Kono shokuba, dō omou?","german":"Was hältst du von diesem Arbeitsplatz?","furigana":"しょくば|おも"},{"japanese":"職場の人間関係に悩んでいる。","romaji":"Shokuba no ningen kankei ni nayande iru.","german":"Mir machen die zwischenmenschlichen Beziehungen bei der Arbeit zu schaffen.","furigana":"しょくば|にんげんかんけい|なや"},{"japanese":"来月から元の職場に戻ります。","romaji":"Raigetsu kara moto no shokuba ni modorimasu.","german":"Ab nächstem Monat kehre ich an meine alte Arbeitsstelle zurück.","furigana":"らいげつ|もと|しょくば|もど"}],"notes":"職場 ist der Ort, an dem man arbeitet, einschließlich der Atmosphäre und der Kollegen: 職場の同僚, 職場の雰囲気, 職場の人間関係, 職場結婚. 会社 meint dagegen die Firma als Organisation, 勤め先 den Arbeitgeber. Achtung: Das deutsche „Arbeitsplatz“ im Sinne von „Stelle, Job“ heißt nicht 職場, sondern 仕事 oder 職 (仕事を失う).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大人になったら、どんな職業につきたい？","romaji":"Otona ni nattara, donna shokugyō ni tsukitai?","german":"What occupation do you want to have when you grow up?","furigana":"おとな|しょくぎょう"},{"japanese":"書類の職業欄に「会社員」と書きました。","romaji":"Shorui no shokugyouran ni kaishain to kakimashita.","german":"I wrote \"company employee\" in the occupation field of the document.","furigana":"しょるい|しょくぎょうらん|かいしゃいん|か"},{"japanese":"教師という職業は大変だと思う。","romaji":"Kyoushi to iu shokugyou wa taihen da to omou.","german":"I think the teaching profession is tough.","furigana":"きょうし|しょくぎょう|たいへん|おも"}],"notes":"職業 is the general, somewhat formal word for \"occupation\": in forms (職業欄), statistics and conversations about career choice. To take up a profession is 職業に就く (often in kana 職業につく). In everyday speech one rather asks お仕事は何ですか, not about the 職業. Useful derivations: 職業病 (\"occupational disease\") and 職業柄 (\"owing to one's occupation\"). Not to be confused with 職場 (\"workplace\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は教師ではありませんが、学校職員です。","romaji":"Watashi wa kyōshi de wa arimasen ga, gakkō shokuin desu.","german":"I am not a teacher, but I am a school staff member.","furigana":"わたし|きょうし|がっこうしょくいん"},{"japanese":"市役所の職員に書類の書き方を教えてもらった。","romaji":"Shiyakusho no shokuin ni shorui no kakikata o oshiete moratta.","german":"A city hall employee taught me how to fill out the documents.","furigana":"しやくしょ|しょくいん|しょるい|か|かた|おし"},{"japanese":"先生に用があって職員室に行った。","romaji":"Sensei ni you ga atte shokuinshitsu ni itta.","german":"I had business with my teacher, so I went to the staff room.","furigana":"せんせい|よう|しょくいんしつ|い"}],"notes":"職員 are the staff of an organization, above all of government offices, schools, hospitals and associations: 市役所の職員, 学校の職員, 職員室 (\"staff room\"), 職員会議 \"staff meeting\". For employees of a private company one rather says 社員, for workers in shops or factories 従業員, casually also スタッフ. 職員 can mean a single person or the personnel as a whole.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私がインターネットで出会った男性は、三十代で有職ですが、バツイチ子持ちです。","romaji":"Watashi ga intānetto de deatta dansei wa, san jūdai de yūshoku desu ga, batsu ichi komochi desu.","german":"The man I met on the internet is in his thirties and has a job, but he is divorced and has a child.","furigana":"わたし|であ|だんせい|さんじゅうだい|ゆうしょく|こも"},{"japanese":"有職の女性の割合は年々増えている。","romaji":"Yuushoku no josei no wariai wa nennen fuete iru.","german":"The proportion of employed women is increasing year by year.","furigana":"ゆうしょく|じょせい|わりあい|ねんねんふ"},{"japanese":"アンケートの「有職・無職」のどちらかに丸をつけてください。","romaji":"Ankeeto no yuushoku, mushoku no dochira ka ni maru o tsukete kudasai.","german":"Please circle either \"employed\" or \"unemployed\" on the questionnaire.","furigana":"ゆうしょく|むしょく|まる"}],"notes":"有職 means \"employed, gainfully employed\" and is a written-language word from statistics and forms: 有職者, 有職率, 有職の女性, often in the pair 有職・無職. In conversation people say 働いている or 仕事をしている instead. The old meanings \"learned\" or \"versed in court customs\" (usually read ゆうそく, as in 有職故実) are obsolete.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いい求職サイト、知ってる？","romaji":"Ii kyuushoku saito, shitteru?","german":"Do you know a good job-hunting site?","furigana":"きゅうしょく|し"},{"japanese":"彼は今、求職中です。","romaji":"Kare wa ima, kyuushokuchuu desu.","german":"He is job hunting at the moment.","furigana":"かれ|いま|きゅうしょくちゅう"},{"japanese":"ハローワークで求職の申し込みをしました。","romaji":"Haroowaaku de kyuushoku no moushikomi o shimashita.","german":"I registered for job seeking at Hello Work.","furigana":"きゅうしょく|もう|こ"}],"notes":"求職 is the search for work from the applicant's point of view: 求職中 (\"looking for a job\"), 求職者, 求職活動. The counterpart from the employer's point of view is 求人 (\"job offer\", 求人広告). As a する-verb, 求職する is rare; one rather says 求職活動をする or, quite everyday, 仕事を探す. For students, the job search is called 就活 (就職活動).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は法律学の教師をしています。","romaji":"Watashi wa hōritsugaku no kyōshi o shite imasu.","german":"I work as a law teacher.","furigana":"わたし|ほうりつがく|きょうし"},{"japanese":"姉は小学校の教師です。","romaji":"Ane wa shougakkou no kyoushi desu.","german":"My older sister is an elementary school teacher.","furigana":"あね|しょうがっこう|きょうし"},{"japanese":"日本語教師になるために勉強しています。","romaji":"Nihongo kyoushi ni naru tame ni benkyou shite imasu.","german":"I'm studying to become a Japanese teacher.","furigana":"にほんごきょうし|べんきょう"}],"notes":"教師 refers to a teacher as an occupation and is used mainly descriptively: 教師をしている, 小学校の教師, 日本語教師, 家庭教師 (\"private tutor\"). 先生, by contrast, is a form of address and title (also for doctors or lawyers); about yourself you humbly say 教師です, not 先生です. Officially, teaching staff is called 教員, and university lecturers often 講師.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"七割る二の答えって、三余り一であってる？","romaji":"Nana waru ni no kotae tte, san amari ichi de atteru?","german":"Is the answer to \"seven divided by two\" three remainder one?","furigana":"ななわ|に|こた|さんあま|いち"},{"japanese":"洗っている時に、お皿を割ってしまいました。","romaji":"Aratte iru toki ni, osara o watte shimaimashita.","german":"I accidentally broke a plate while washing up.","furigana":"あら|とき|さら|わ"},{"japanese":"ウイスキーを水で割って飲みます。","romaji":"Uisukii o mizu de watte nomimasu.","german":"I drink whisky diluted with water.","furigana":"みず|わ|の"}],"notes":"割る is transitive (X を割る); the intransitive partner is 割れる (お皿が割れた). It means \"to break\" with glass, dishes and eggs (卵を割る), \"to split\" (薪を割る), in mathematics \"to divide\" (十割る二は五) and with drinks \"to dilute\" (水で割る, 水割り). It also means \"to fall below\": 1万円を割る. Unlike 壊す (to break devices) or 折る (to break a stick or bone), 割る is about shattering or dividing.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"裁判ではできるだけ公平に裁く必要があります。","romaji":"Saiban de wa dekirudake kōhei ni sabaku hitsuyō ga arimasu.","german":"In a trial, it is necessary to judge as fairly as possible.","furigana":"さいばん|こうへい|さば|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"人が人を裁くのは難しいことだ。","romaji":"Hito ga hito o sabaku no wa muzukashii koto da.","german":"It is hard for people to judge people.","furigana":"ひと|ひと|さば|むずか"},{"japanese":"この事件は来月、裁判所で裁かれる。","romaji":"Kono jiken wa raigetsu, saibansho de sabakareru.","german":"This case will be tried in court next month.","furigana":"じけん|らいげつ|さいばんしょ|さば"}],"notes":"裁く means \"to judge, to pass judgment\" and is transitive: 人を裁く, 罪を裁く, 法で裁く. The passive 裁かれる (\"to be put on trial\") is very common. Related are 裁判 (\"trial\") and 裁判官 (\"judge\"). Not to be confused with the similar-sounding 捌く (\"to handle skillfully, to fillet a fish\") or 砂漠 (\"desert\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"犯人は逃げたが、川崎で捕まった。","romaji":"Hannin wa nigeta ga, Kawasaki de tsukamatta.","german":"The culprit fled but was caught in Kawasaki.","furigana":"はんにん|に|かわさき|つか"},{"japanese":"川崎は東京と横浜の間にある大きな町です。","romaji":"Kawasaki wa Toukyou to Yokohama no aida ni aru ookina machi desu.","german":"Kawasaki is a big city between Tokyo and Yokohama.","furigana":"かわさき|とうきょう|よこはま|あいだ|おお|まち"},{"japanese":"川崎駅で電車を乗り換えてください。","romaji":"Kawasaki eki de densha o norikaete kudasai.","german":"Please change trains at Kawasaki Station.","furigana":"かわさきえき|でんしゃ|の|か"}],"notes":"川崎 is a large industrial and residential city in Kanagawa Prefecture between Tokyo and Yokohama. The name is also a common family name (川崎さん) and appears in company names like 川崎重工. Literally: 川 \"river\" + 崎 \"cape\". 崎 is read さき or ざき depending on the name: 川崎 かわさき, but 宮崎 みやざき.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の名字は長崎なので、みんなにはナガちゃんって呼ばれています。","romaji":"Watashi no myouji wa Nagasaki na node, minna ni wa Naga-chan tte yobarete imasu.","german":"My surname is Nagasaki, so everyone calls me Naga-chan.","furigana":"わたし|みょうじ|ながさき|よ"},{"japanese":"長崎は港町として有名です。","romaji":"Nagasaki wa minatomachi to shite yuumei desu.","german":"Nagasaki is famous as a port city.","furigana":"ながさき|みなとまち|ゆうめい"},{"japanese":"修学旅行で長崎の平和公園に行きました。","romaji":"Shuugaku ryokou de Nagasaki no heiwa kouen ni ikimashita.","german":"On the school trip we visited the Peace Park in Nagasaki.","furigana":"しゅうがくりょこう|ながさき|へいわこうえん|い"}],"notes":"長崎 is a city and prefecture (長崎県) in the west of Kyushu. During Japan's seclusion, the port with the island 出島 was the only gateway for trade with the Netherlands; in 1945 the city was destroyed by an atomic bomb. Well known today are, among other things, ちゃんぽん and カステラ. 長崎 is also a family name.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 40"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"キャンバスをフレームに張る方法を教わりました。","romaji":"Kyanbasu o furēmu ni haru hōhō o osowarimashita.","german":"I was taught how to stretch a canvas over a frame.","furigana":"は|ほうほう|おそ"},{"japanese":"暗くなる前にテントを張ろう。","romaji":"Kuraku naru mae ni tento o harou.","german":"Let's pitch the tent before it gets dark.","furigana":"くら|まえ|は"},{"japanese":"寒い朝、池に氷が張っていた。","romaji":"Samui asa, ike ni koori ga hatte ita.","german":"On a cold morning, ice had formed on the pond.","furigana":"さむ|あさ|いけ|こおり|は"}],"notes":"The basic idea of 張る is \"to stretch or spread something taut\": ロープを張る, テントを張る (\"to pitch a tent\"), キャンバスを張る. Intransitively it describes a surface forming or something becoming taut or swollen: 池に氷が張る (\"the pond freezes over\"), 肩が張る (\"stiff shoulders\"), 値段が張る (\"to be expensive\"). Other phrases: 胸を張る (\"to be proud\"), 意地を張る (\"to be stubborn\"). For sticking paper on, you usually write 貼る (切手を貼る).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの男優、ファンに対する態度がひどいので有名だよ。","romaji":"Ano dan'yū, fan ni taisuru taido ga hidoi node yūmei da yo.","german":"That actor is famous for his terrible attitude toward fans.","furigana":"だんゆう|たい|たいど|ゆうめい"},{"japanese":"この映画で、彼は主演男優賞を取りました。","romaji":"Kono eiga de, kare wa shuen dan'yū shō o torimashita.","german":"He won the best actor award for this film.","furigana":"えいが|かれ|しゅえんだんゆうしょう|と"},{"japanese":"その舞台には、男優が三人と女優が二人出ています。","romaji":"Sono butai ni wa, dan'yū ga sannin to joyū ga futari dete imasu.","german":"Three actors and two actresses appear in that stage play.","furigana":"ぶたい|だんゆう|さんにん|じょゆう|ふたりで"}],"notes":"男優 is a male actor and is used above all in contrast to 女優 or in award names: 主演男優賞 (\"best actor award\"), 助演男優賞. In normal conversation, 俳優 is mostly used for actors, and for stage performers also 役者. Since 男優 alone is often associated with the adult film industry, 俳優 is the more neutral choice.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"若い人が優先席に堂々と座っているのはいただけない。","romaji":"Wakai hito ga yuusenseki ni doudou to suwatte iru no wa itadakenai.","german":"It's not acceptable for young people to sit boldly in the priority seats.","furigana":"わか|ひと|ゆうせんせき|どうどう|すわ"},{"japanese":"仕事より家族を優先したい。","romaji":"Shigoto yori kazoku o yuusen shitai.","german":"I want to put family before work.","furigana":"しごと|かぞく|ゆうせん"},{"japanese":"安全を最優先に考えてください。","romaji":"Anzen o saiyuusen ni kangaete kudasai.","german":"Please give safety top priority.","furigana":"あんぜん|さいゆうせん|かんが"}],"notes":"優先 means \"precedence, priority\". As a する-verb it means \"to prefer, to give priority\": 仕事より家族を優先する. Common combinations are 優先的に (\"as a priority\"), 最優先 (\"top priority\"), 優先順位 \"order of priority\", 歩行者優先 \"pedestrians first\" and 優先席 (seats on trains for the elderly, pregnant women, etc.). 先に, by contrast, only means \"first\" in terms of time.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日は山田宅におじゃましてます。","romaji":"Kyō wa Yamada taku ni ojama shitemasu.","german":"Today I'm visiting the Yamadas' house.","furigana":"きょう|やまだたく"},{"japanese":"荷物は田中様宅にお届けします。","romaji":"Nimotsu wa Tanaka-sama taku ni otodoke shimasu.","german":"The package will be delivered to the Tanaka residence.","furigana":"にもつ|たなかさまたく|とど"},{"japanese":"年末は妻の両親宅で過ごします。","romaji":"Nenmatsu wa tsuma no ryoushin taku de sugoshimasu.","german":"We'll spend the year-end at my wife's parents' house.","furigana":"ねんまつ|つま|りょうしんたく|す"}],"notes":"〜宅 follows names or terms for people and means \"home, house of ...\": 山田宅, 田中様宅, 友人宅, 知人宅, 両親宅. It sounds formal and written (delivery slips, news, polite conversation); in everyday speech one says 山田さんの家 or 山田さんのところ. Related are お宅 (\"your home\", also a polite \"you\"), 自宅 and 住宅.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自宅のトイレから、助けを呼ぶ声が聞こえてきたんです。","romaji":"Jitaku no toire kara, tasuke o yobu koe ga kikoete kita n desu.","german":"I heard a voice calling for help from the toilet at home.","furigana":"じたく|たす|よ|こえ|き"},{"japanese":"今日は自宅で仕事をしています。","romaji":"Kyou wa jitaku de shigoto o shite imasu.","german":"I'm working from home today.","furigana":"きょう|じたく|しごと"},{"japanese":"書類は自宅の住所に送ってください。","romaji":"Shorui wa jitaku no juusho ni okutte kudasai.","german":"Please send the documents to my home address.","furigana":"しょるい|じたく|じゅうしょ|おく"}],"notes":"自宅 is one's own home, usually in contrast to the workplace or being out and about: 自宅で仕事をする, 自宅待機, 自宅療養, 自宅の住所. It sounds more matter-of-fact than 家 or うち and is typical of forms, work contexts and news reports. For someone else's home, one politely says お宅. 住宅, by contrast, means residential buildings in general.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この地域の住宅は、あの大地震のときも大丈夫だったんです。","romaji":"Kono chiiki no jūtaku wa, ano dai jishin no toki mo daijōbu datta n desu.","german":"The houses in this area were fine even in that big earthquake.","furigana":"ちいき|じゅうたく|だいじしん|だいじょうぶ"},{"japanese":"駅の近くに新しい住宅がたくさん建っている。","romaji":"Eki no chikaku ni atarashii juutaku ga takusan tatte iru.","german":"Many new houses are going up near the station.","furigana":"えき|ちか|あたら|じゅうたく|た"},{"japanese":"住宅ローンを三十五年で組みました。","romaji":"Juutaku roon o sanjuugo-nen de kumimashita.","german":"I took out a mortgage with a term of thirty-five years.","furigana":"じゅうたく|さんじゅうごねん|く"}],"notes":"住宅 means residential buildings as a category, that is, \"housing, living space\", rather matter-of-fact: 住宅地, 住宅街 (\"residential area\"), 住宅ローン (\"mortgage\"), 公営住宅 (\"public housing\"), 住宅問題. For one's own home one does not say 住宅 but 家, うち or 自宅. Similarly formal is 住居, warmer-sounding is 住まい.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ここで待っていれば、きっとまたあのトラ猫が現れるよ。","romaji":"Koko de matte ireba, kitto mata ano tora neko ga arawareru yo.","german":"If you wait here, that tabby cat will surely show up again.","furigana":"ま|ねこ|あらわ"},{"japanese":"約束の時間を一時間過ぎて、やっと彼が現れた。","romaji":"Yakusoku no jikan o ichijikan sugite, yatto kare ga arawareta.","german":"An hour after the appointed time, he finally showed up.","furigana":"やくそく|じかん|いちじかんす|かれ|あらわ"},{"japanese":"薬を飲んで三十分ほどで効果が現れた。","romaji":"Kusuri o nonde sanjuppun hodo de kouka ga arawareta.","german":"The effect appeared about thirty minutes after taking the medicine.","furigana":"くすり|の|さんじゅっぷん|こうか|あらわ"}],"notes":"現れる means \"to appear, to turn up, to become visible\" and is intransitive: 犯人が現れる, 効果が現れる. The transitive counterpart is 現す (姿を現す \"to show oneself\"). For feelings or traits that show, one usually writes 表れる (気持ちが顔に表れる). Compared with 出る or 来る, 現れる stresses the sudden, often unexpected becoming visible.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ライブストリーミングで、現場の楽しい空気感が伝わればいいなと思っています。","romaji":"Raibusutoriimingu de, genba no tanoshii kuukikan ga tsutawareba ii na to omotte imasu.","german":"With the live stream, I hope the fun atmosphere on site comes across.","furigana":"げんば|たの|くうきかん|つた|おも"},{"japanese":"警察はすぐに事故の現場に向かった。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa sugu ni jiko no genba ni mukatta.","german":"The police headed to the scene of the accident right away.","furigana":"けいさつ|じこ|げんば|む"},{"japanese":"父は建設現場で働いています。","romaji":"Chichi wa kensetsu genba de hataraite imasu.","german":"My father works at a construction site.","furigana":"ちち|けんせつげんば|はたら"}],"notes":"現場 is the place where something actually happens or has happened: 事故現場 (\"accident scene\"), 犯行現場 (\"crime scene\"), 工事現場・建設現場 (\"construction site\"), 撮影現場 (\"film set\"). Figuratively it means practice on the ground as opposed to administration: 教育の現場, 現場の声 (\"voices from the field\"). 現地, on the other hand, denotes a region or country on site, for example when traveling (現地の人).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"マラソンで乳首がすれて血が出た。","romaji":"Marason de chikubi ga surete chi ga deta.","german":"My nipples chafed during the marathon and bled.","furigana":"ちくび|ち|で"},{"japanese":"哺乳瓶の乳首を新しいのに替えた。","romaji":"Honyuubin no chikubi o atarashii no ni kaeta.","german":"I replaced the nipple of the baby bottle with a new one.","furigana":"ほにゅうびん|ちくび|あたら|か"},{"japanese":"胸や乳首に異常を感じたら、早めに病院に行ってください。","romaji":"Mune ya chikubi ni ijou o kanjitara, hayame ni byouin ni itte kudasai.","german":"If you notice anything abnormal in your breast or nipples, please see a doctor early.","furigana":"むね|ちくび|いじょう|かん|はや|びょういん|い"}],"notes":"乳首 is the nipple in humans and animals (also \"teat\" in the latter) and also the nipple of a baby bottle (哺乳瓶の乳首). The reading ちくび is irregular; otherwise 乳 is read ちち or にゅう. The word is neutral-anatomical, but in everyday speech it is used rather discreetly; in medical texts 乳頭 is also used.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この経済学についての記事を、今月末までにあげないといけないんです。","romaji":"Kono keizaigaku nitsuite no kiji o, kongetsumatsu made ni agenai to ikenai n desu.","german":"I have to finish this article on economics by the end of this month.","furigana":"けいざいがく|きじ|こんげつまつ"},{"japanese":"大学で経済学を専攻しています。","romaji":"Daigaku de keizaigaku o senkou shite imasu.","german":"I'm majoring in economics at university.","furigana":"だいがく|けいざいがく|せんこう"},{"japanese":"経済学部の学生は数学も勉強しなければならない。","romaji":"Keizai gakubu no gakusei wa suugaku mo benkyou shinakereba naranai.","german":"Students in the faculty of economics also have to study mathematics.","furigana":"けいざいがくぶ|がくせい|すうがく|べんきょう"}],"notes":"経済学 is economics as a subject: 経済学を専攻する, 経済学部 (\"faculty of economics\"), 経済学者 (\"economist\"), マクロ経済学・ミクロ経済学. The ending 〜学 forms names of academic fields, as in 経営学 (\"business administration\") or 心理学. 経済 alone means the economy itself (日本の経済).","levelNote":"Compound: 経済 (N5) + 学 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"例を挙げるとキリがないですよ。","romaji":"Rei o ageru to kiri ga nai desu yo.","german":"If I started citing examples, there'd be no end to it.","furigana":"れい|あ"},{"japanese":"来年の春、結婚式を挙げる予定です。","romaji":"Rainen no haru, kekkonshiki o ageru yotei desu.","german":"We plan to hold our wedding ceremony next spring.","furigana":"らいねん|はる|けっこんしき|あ|よてい"},{"japanese":"質問がある人は手を挙げてください。","romaji":"Shitsumon ga aru hito wa te o agete kudasai.","german":"If you have a question, please raise your hand.","furigana":"しつもん|ひと|て|あ"}],"notes":"挙げる is the writing of あげる for \"to cite, to name\" (例を挙げる, 名前を挙げる), \"to raise one's hand\" (手を挙げる), \"to hold a ceremony\" (結婚式を挙げる) and \"to muster\" (全力を挙げる). For lifting in general, 上げる is used, for deep-frying or hoisting flags 揚げる. The verb is transitive; the intransitive partner is 挙がる (例や名前が挙がる). Colloquially, 犯人を挙げる also means \"to arrest the culprit\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これでもう逃げ道は断たれてしまった。","romaji":"Kore de mou nigemichi wa tatarete shimatta.","german":"With this, every escape route has now been cut off.","furigana":"に|みち|た"},{"japanese":"健康のために、酒を断つことにした。","romaji":"Kenkou no tame ni, sake o tatsu koto ni shita.","german":"For the sake of my health, I decided to give up alcohol.","furigana":"けんこう|さけ|た"},{"japanese":"彼は家族との連絡を断って、一人で暮らしている。","romaji":"Kare wa kazoku to no renraku o tatte, hitori de kurashite iru.","german":"He cut off contact with his family and lives alone.","furigana":"かれ|かぞく|れんらく|た|ひとり|く"}],"notes":"断つ is transitive and means \"to cut off, to break off\", mostly in the figurative sense: 関係を断つ, 連絡を断つ, 退路を断つ. It also means \"to renounce something\": 酒を断つ, たばこを断つ. The passive 断たれる is common. Verbs that sound the same: 絶つ (命を絶つ, 消息を絶つ), 裁つ (to cut fabric); for concrete cutting in everyday life, one says 切る.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その時は、突然やって来ました。","romaji":"Sono toki wa, totsuzen yattekimashita.","german":"The moment came suddenly.","furigana":"とき|とつぜん|き"},{"japanese":"突然、電気が消えた。","romaji":"Totsuzen, denki ga kieta.","german":"Suddenly, the lights went out.","furigana":"とつぜん|でんき|き"},{"japanese":"突然のお願いで申し訳ありません。","romaji":"Totsuzen no onegai de moushiwake arimasen.","german":"I apologize for the sudden request.","furigana":"とつぜん|ねが|もう|わけ"}],"notes":"突然 means \"suddenly, unexpectedly\". As an adverb it stands alone or with a comma: 突然泣き出す, 突然、電気が消えた. Before nouns, 突然の is used: 突然の雨, 突然の知らせ. Related are 急に (more colloquial, also for quick changes) and いきなり (abrupt, often with an unpleasant undertone); 突然 above all emphasizes the unexpected. Polite phrase: 突然のご連絡失礼します.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"更衣室はあそこです。","romaji":"Kōishitsu wa asoko desu.","german":"The changing room is over there.","furigana":"こういしつ"},{"japanese":"更衣室で制服に着替えてから仕事を始めます。","romaji":"Kouishitsu de seifuku ni kigaete kara shigoto o hajimemasu.","german":"I change into my uniform in the changing room and then start work.","furigana":"こういしつ|せいふく|きが|しごと|はじ"},{"japanese":"このプールの更衣室にはロッカーがあります。","romaji":"Kono puuru no kouishitsu ni wa rokkaa ga arimasu.","german":"The changing room of this pool has lockers.","furigana":"こういしつ"}],"notes":"更衣室 is the changing room in gyms, swimming pools, fitness studios or at the workplace, often separate as 男子更衣室・女子更衣室. 更衣 is a formal word for \"changing clothes\"; in everyday speech one says 着替える. Note: the fitting room in a clothing store is not called 更衣室 but 試着室.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あれ、本棚にない？","romaji":"Are, hondana ni nai?","german":"Huh, isn't it on the bookshelf?","furigana":"ほんだな"},{"japanese":"新しい本棚を買いました。","romaji":"Atarashii hondana o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a new bookshelf.","furigana":"あたら|ほんだな|か"}],"notes":"本棚 is a bookshelf. 本棚に indicates where a sought item might be. 本棚を買う names the shelf as the thing bought. あれ can introduce a surprised question; ない？ asks here whether the thing sought is not there. 棚 is read だな in the compound word.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 27"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女はいつも制服の上に紫色のベストを着ている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa itsumo seifuku no ue ni murasakiiro no besuto o kite iru.","german":"She always wears a purple vest over her uniform.","furigana":"かのじょ|せいふく|うえ|むらさきいろ|き"},{"japanese":"夕方の空が、きれいな紫色に染まりました。","romaji":"Yuugata no sora ga, kirei na murasakiiro ni somarimashita.","german":"The evening sky was dyed a beautiful purple.","furigana":"ゆうがた|そら|むらさきいろ|そ"},{"japanese":"寒さで唇が紫色になっていますよ。","romaji":"Samusa de kuchibiru ga murasakiiro ni natte imasu yo.","german":"Your lips have turned purple from the cold.","furigana":"さむ|くちびる|むらさきいろ"}],"notes":"紫色 means \"purple, violet\" and is a noun: before nouns 紫色の is used (紫色のベスト), as a result 紫色になる or 紫色に染まる. Unlike 赤い, 黄色い or 茶色い, there is no form 紫色い. 紫 alone means the same; lighter shades are called 薄紫. In fashion one also hears パープル; traditionally, purple was considered a noble color in Japan.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"表情で演技ができる役者を目指しています。","romaji":"Hyōjō de engi ga dekiru yakusha o mezashite imasu.","german":"I aim to be an actor who can act with facial expressions.","furigana":"ひょうじょう|えんぎ|やくしゃ|めざ"},{"japanese":"その選手はオリンピックで完璧な演技を見せた。","romaji":"Sono senshu wa orinpikku de kanpeki na engi o miseta.","german":"That athlete gave a perfect performance at the Olympics.","furigana":"せんしゅ|かんぺき|えんぎ|み"},{"japanese":"泣いているけど、あれはきっと演技だよ。","romaji":"Naite iru kedo, are wa kitto engi da yo.","german":"She's crying, but that's surely an act.","furigana":"な|えんぎ"}],"notes":"演技 is the performance of actors, but also of athletes in disciplines like figure skating or gymnastics: 演技が上手い, 演技力, 迫真の演技. As a する-verb it is intransitive (舞台で演技する). Figuratively it means \"play-acting, pretense\": あれは演技だ (\"that's just an act\"). For music, on the other hand, one says 演奏, for the play itself 芝居 or 演劇.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"主人公を演ずるのは、新人の俳優だ。","romaji":"Shujinkou o enzuru no wa, shinjin no haiyuu da.","german":"The protagonist is played by a newcomer actor.","furigana":"しゅじんこう|えん|しんじん|はいゆう"},{"japanese":"劇団は来月、シェークスピアの作品を演ずる予定です。","romaji":"Gekidan wa raigetsu, Sheekusupia no sakuhin o enzuru yotei desu.","german":"The theater company plans to perform a Shakespeare work next month.","furigana":"げきだん|らいげつ|さくひん|えん|よてい"},{"japanese":"彼はその交渉で、重要な役割を演ずることになった。","romaji":"Kare wa sono koushou de, juuyou na yakuwari o enzuru koto ni natta.","german":"He ended up playing an important role in those negotiations.","furigana":"かれ|こうしょう|じゅうよう|やくわり|えん"}],"notes":"演ずる is the older, literary variant of 演じる and means the same: to play a role (役を演ずる), to perform a play, and figuratively to play a part in events (重要な役割を演ずる). In the dictionary form and in written texts you still often encounter 演ずる; most of the other forms today sound as for 演じる (演じて, 演じない, 演じた). Classical forms like 演ぜず or 演ぜられる sound very elevated. Also 失態を演ずる (\"to make a fool of oneself\") is a fixed phrase.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"演芸会は来週ですよ！みなさん、がんばりましょう！","romaji":"Engeikai wa raishū desu yo! Minasan, ganbarimashō!","german":"The variety show is next week! Let's do our best, everyone!","furigana":"えんげいかい|らいしゅう"},{"japanese":"浅草の演芸場で落語を聞いた。","romaji":"Asakusa no engeijou de rakugo o kiita.","german":"I listened to rakugo at a variety theater in Asakusa.","furigana":"あさくさ|えんげいじょう|らくご|き"},{"japanese":"祖父はテレビの演芸番組が大好きです。","romaji":"Sofu wa terebi no engei bangumi ga daisuki desu.","german":"My grandfather loves variety programs on TV.","furigana":"そふ|えんげいばんぐみ|だいす"}],"notes":"演芸 is popular stage entertainment such as 落語, 漫才, 手品 or 講談, so it means something closer to \"variety acts, vaudeville\" than to \"performing arts\" in general. Typical words: 演芸場 (\"variety theater\", cf. 寄席), 演芸会 (a \"talent show\" at schools or companies), 演芸番組. Broader is 芸能 (the entertainment industry, 芸能人); for theater one says 演劇, for high art 芸術.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ライオンキングの上演時間を教えて下さい。","romaji":"Raion kingu no jōen jikan o oshiete kudasai.","german":"Please tell me the running time of The Lion King.","furigana":"じょうえんじかん|おし|くだ"},{"japanese":"この劇場では毎年シェイクスピアの作品が上演される。","romaji":"Kono gekijou de wa maitoshi Sheikusupia no sakuhin ga jouen sareru.","german":"Shakespeare's works are staged at this theater every year.","furigana":"げきじょう|まいとし|さくひん|じょうえん"},{"japanese":"上演中は携帯電話の電源をお切りください。","romaji":"Jouenchuu wa keitai denwa no dengen o okiri kudasai.","german":"Please turn off your mobile phone during the performance.","furigana":"じょうえんちゅう|けいたいでんわ|でんげん|き"}],"notes":"上演 is the performance of a stage play, an opera or a musical; as a する-verb it is transitive (劇を上演する), often in the passive (作品が上演される). Films, by contrast, are shown with 上映, and music is 演奏. 公演 refers to a performance as an event (tour, guest performance). Typical: 上演中, 上演時間.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"こんな長い台詞、よく覚えられるわね。","romaji":"Konna nagai serifu, yoku oboerareru wa ne.","german":"How can you memorize such long lines?","furigana":"なが|せりふ|おぼ"},{"japanese":"緊張して台詞を忘れてしまった。","romaji":"Kinchou shite serifu o wasurete shimatta.","german":"I was so nervous that I forgot my lines.","furigana":"きんちょう|せりふ|わす"},{"japanese":"「愛してる」なんて、ドラマみたいなセリフは言えないよ。","romaji":"Ai shiteru nante, dorama mitai na serifu wa ienai yo.","german":"I can't say a line like \"I love you\" as if it were from a TV drama.","furigana":"あい|い"}],"notes":"台詞 are the spoken lines in theater, film or manga: 台詞を覚える, 台詞を言う, 台詞を忘れる. The word is usually written in kana, せりふ or セリフ. Figuratively it also means a remark or a hackneyed phrase: それはこっちのせりふだ (\"That's my line!\"), ドラマみたいなせりふ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このお方をどなたと心得るか。","romaji":"Kono okata o donata to kokoroeru ka.","german":"Do you know who this gentleman is?","furigana":"かた|こころえ"},{"japanese":"彼は客の扱い方をよく心得ている。","romaji":"Kare wa kyaku no atsukaikata o yoku kokoroete iru.","german":"He knows very well how to handle customers.","furigana":"かれ|きゃく|あつか|かた|こころえ"},{"japanese":"山に登るときの注意点を心得ておくべきだ。","romaji":"Yama ni noboru toki no chuuiten o kokoroete oku beki da.","german":"You should know the precautions for climbing mountains.","furigana":"やま|のぼ|ちゅういてん|こころえ"}],"notes":"心得る means \"to know thoroughly, to understand what matters\" and is almost always used as 心得ている: 扱い方を心得ている. The form AをBと心得る (\"to regard A as B\") sounds archaic and is familiar from samurai dramas. The noun 心得 refers to basic rules or knowledge (心得がある). The word is literary to elevated in register; in everyday speech people say 分かる or 知っている.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大雪で車がすっかり雪に埋まってしまった。","romaji":"Ooyuki de kuruma ga sukkari yuki ni umatte shimatta.","german":"The car was completely buried in snow because of the heavy snowfall.","furigana":"おおゆき|くるま|ゆき|う"},{"japanese":"週末の予定はもう埋まっています。","romaji":"Shuumatsu no yotei wa mou umatte imasu.","german":"My weekend schedule is already full.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|よてい|う"},{"japanese":"会場は大勢のファンで埋まった。","romaji":"Kaijou wa oozei no fan de umatta.","german":"The venue was filled with a large crowd of fans.","furigana":"かいじょう|おおぜい|う"}],"notes":"埋まる is intransitive and first means \"to be buried, to be covered\": 土に埋まる, 雪に埋まる. Very common is the sense \"to fill up, to be taken\": 席が埋まる, 予定が埋まる, 会場がファンで埋まる, 穴が埋まる. The transitive counterpart is 埋める (穴を埋める, 時間を埋める). The reading うずまる is a literary variant.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"兄は人付き合いが苦手ですが、楽天主義者なので自分ではあまり気にしていません。","romaji":"Ani wa hitozukiai ga nigate desu ga, rakuten shugisha na node jibun de wa amari ki ni shite imasen.","german":"My older brother is not good at socializing, but being an optimist, he doesn't worry about it much himself.","furigana":"あに|ひとづ|あ|にがて|らくてんしゅぎしゃ|じぶん|き"},{"japanese":"彼の楽天主義には、ときどき助けられます。","romaji":"Kare no rakuten shugi ni wa, tokidoki tasukeraremasu.","german":"His optimism sometimes helps me.","furigana":"かれ|らくてんしゅぎ|たす"},{"japanese":"楽天主義と悲観主義、あなたはどちらに近いですか。","romaji":"Rakuten shugi to hikan shugi, anata wa dochira ni chikai desu ka.","german":"Optimism or pessimism - which are you closer to?","furigana":"らくてんしゅぎ|ひかんしゅぎ|ちか"}],"notes":"楽天主義 is \"optimism\" as an attitude to life or worldview and sounds rather formal; synonymous and more common is 楽観主義, the opposite is 悲観主義. An optimistic person is a 楽天家 or 楽天主義者, and 楽天的 serves as the adjective (楽天的な性格). In everyday speech one often says simply ポジティブ or 前向き.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今月は沢山の金を手に入れた。","romaji":"Kongetsu wa takusan no kane o te ni ireta.","german":"This month I got a lot of money.","furigana":"こんげつ|たくさん|かね|て|い"},{"japanese":"昨日は沢山歩きました。","romaji":"Kinō wa takusan arukimashita.","german":"Yesterday I walked a lot.","furigana":"きのう|たくさんある"}],"notes":"沢山 refers to a large amount or number. Before a noun, 沢山の is common: 沢山の本. It can also be used as an adverb with an action: 沢山歩く \"to walk a lot\". It is often written in hiragana as たくさん. 金 is read かね in the sense of \"money\"; 手に入れる means \"to get\" or \"to obtain\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 9"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ママ、割り算のテストで100点取ったよ！","romaji":"Mama, warizan no tesuto de 100ten totta yo!","german":"Mom, I got 100 on my division test!","furigana":"わ|ざん|てんと"},{"japanese":"小学生のころ、割り算が苦手だった。","romaji":"Shougakusei no koro, warizan ga nigate datta.","german":"When I was in elementary school, I was bad at division.","furigana":"しょうがくせい|わ|ざん|にがて"},{"japanese":"割り算の答えに余りが出ることもあります。","romaji":"Warizan no kotae ni amari ga deru koto mo arimasu.","german":"Division answers sometimes come out with a remainder.","furigana":"わ|ざん|こた|あま|で"}],"notes":"割り算 is division, one of the four basic arithmetic operations alongside 足し算, 引き算 and 掛け算. The actual calculating is expressed with the verb 割る: 10を2で割る (\"to divide 10 by 2\"); 割り算する is possible but less common. Related: 余り (\"remainder\") and 割り切れる (\"to be divisible without remainder\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一億円、そのスーツケースに収まった？","romaji":"Ichi okuen, sono sūtsukēsu ni osamatta?","german":"Did the hundred million yen fit in that suitcase?","furigana":"いちおくえん|おさ"},{"japanese":"旅行の費用は予算内に収まった。","romaji":"Ryokou no hiyou wa yosannai ni osamatta.","german":"The travel expenses stayed within the budget.","furigana":"りょこう|ひよう|よさんない|おさ"},{"japanese":"風が収まるまで、外に出ないほうがいい。","romaji":"Kaze ga osamaru made, soto ni denai hou ga ii.","german":"You'd better not go outside until the wind dies down.","furigana":"かぜ|おさ|そと|で"}],"notes":"収まる is intransitive and means \"to fit in, to find room\": 箱に収まる, 予算内に収まる, 写真に収まる (\"to get into the photo\"). It also means \"to subside, to calm down\": 騒ぎが収まる, 怒りが収まる; for pain and illness it is usually written 治まる (痛みが治まる). The transitive counterpart is 収める (箱に収める).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"もうすぐ新しいキャラクターが加わるよ！","romaji":"Mōsugu atarashii kyarakutā ga kuwawaru yo!","german":"A new character will be added soon!","furigana":"あたら|くわ"},{"japanese":"私も話に加わってもいいですか。","romaji":"Watashi mo hanashi ni kuwawatte mo ii desu ka.","german":"May I join the conversation too?","furigana":"わたし|はなし|くわ"},{"japanese":"料金に消費税が加わると、けっこう高くなる。","romaji":"Ryoukin ni shouhizei ga kuwawaru to, kekkou takaku naru.","german":"When consumption tax is added to the fee, it gets quite expensive.","furigana":"りょうきん|しょうひぜい|くわ|たか"}],"notes":"加わる is intransitive: \"to be added\" (AにBが加わる), \"to join, to take part\" (仲間に加わる, 話に加わる) as well as \"to increase, to be applied\" (スピードが加わる, 力が加わる). The transitive counterpart is 加える (塩を加える \"to add salt\"). 参加する is more formal and means taking part in an event.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鼻の穴が痒い。","romaji":"Hana no ana ga kayui.","german":"The inside of my nose is itchy.","furigana":"はな|あな|かゆ"},{"japanese":"蚊に刺されたところがすごくかゆい。","romaji":"Ka ni sasareta tokoro ga sugoku kayui.","german":"The spot where the mosquito bit me is incredibly itchy.","furigana":"か|さ"},{"japanese":"花粉の季節は目が痒くてたまらない。","romaji":"Kafun no kisetsu wa me ga kayukute tamaranai.","german":"During pollen season, my eyes itch unbearably.","furigana":"かふん|きせつ|め|かゆ"}],"notes":"痒い is an い-adjective and is usually written in kana (かゆい). The itchy spot is marked with が: 背中がかゆい. To scratch is 掻く (かゆいところを掻く), the itch itself かゆみ. Idiom: 痒い所に手が届く (\"to think of everything, to be very attentive\"), for example about good service.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"男は町外れにあるガソリンスタンドで働いていた。","romaji":"Otoko wa machihazure ni aru gasorinsutando de hataraite ita.","german":"The man worked at a gas station on the outskirts of town.","furigana":"おとこ|まちはず|はたら"},{"japanese":"町外れに古いお寺があります。","romaji":"Machihazure ni furui otera ga arimasu.","german":"There is an old temple on the edge of town.","furigana":"まちはず|ふる|てら"},{"japanese":"町外れまで歩くと、田んぼが広がっていた。","romaji":"Machihazure made aruku to, tanbo ga hirogatte ita.","german":"When I walked to the edge of town, rice fields stretched out ahead.","furigana":"まちはず|ある|た|ひろ"}],"notes":"町外れ is the edge of a town, where the built-up area ends: 町外れに住む, 町外れの工場. 外れ comes from 外れる (\"to deviate, to lie outside\"); likewise there is 村外れ. 郊外, by contrast, refers more to the ring of suburbs around a large city (郊外に住む). 町外れ sounds slightly narrative and is often used in stories.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ゼッタイにこの夢を叶えるんだ！","romaji":"Zettai ni kono yume o kanaeru n da!","german":"I will definitely make this dream come true!","furigana":"ゆめ|かな"},{"japanese":"両親の願いを叶えるために、医者になった。","romaji":"Ryoushin no negai o kanaeru tame ni, isha ni natta.","german":"I became a doctor to fulfill my parents' wish.","furigana":"りょうしん|ねが|かな|いしゃ"},{"japanese":"魔法使いは少女の願いを叶えてくれた。","romaji":"Mahoutsukai wa shoujo no negai o kanaete kureta.","german":"The wizard granted the girl's wish.","furigana":"まほうつか|しょうじょ|ねが|かな"}],"notes":"叶える is transitive and means \"to fulfill a wish or dream, to make it come true\": 夢を叶える, 願いを叶える; often also in kana かなえる. The intransitive counterpart is 叶う (夢が叶う \"the dream comes true\"). For duties or promises, by contrast, one says 果たす (約束を果たす), for plans 実現する, for conditions 満たす.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自ら学ぶ姿勢が大切なんです。","romaji":"Mizukara manabu shisei ga taisetsu na n desu.","german":"A willingness to learn on one's own is important.","furigana":"みずか|まな|しせい|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"社長が自らお客様の家に行って謝った。","romaji":"Shachou ga mizukara okyakusama no ie ni itte ayamatta.","german":"The company president went to the customer's house in person to apologize.","furigana":"しゃちょう|みずか|きゃくさま|いえ|い|あやま"},{"japanese":"自らの失敗を認めるのは難しい。","romaji":"Mizukara no shippai o mitomeru no wa muzukashii.","german":"It is hard to admit one's own mistakes.","furigana":"みずか|しっぱい|みと|むずか"}],"notes":"自ら means \"oneself, personally, on one's own initiative\" and sounds more literary and weighty than 自分で: 自ら学ぶ, 社長自ら (\"the boss in person\"). As a noun it takes の: 自らの手で, 自らの責任. Colloquially it corresponds roughly to 自分から. Not to be confused with 自ずから or 自ずと (\"by itself, quite naturally\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私はこの長靴を驚くほどの安値で買いましたが、防水加工がされてませんでした。","romaji":"Watashi wa kono nagagutsu o odoroku hodo no yasune de kaimashita ga, bousui kakou ga saretemasen deshita.","german":"I bought these rain boots at a surprisingly low price, but they weren't waterproofed.","furigana":"わたし|ながぐつ|おどろ|やすね|か|ぼうすいかこう"},{"japanese":"株価が今年の最安値を更新した。","romaji":"Kabuka ga kotoshi no saiyasune o koushin shita.","german":"The stock price hit a new low for the year.","furigana":"かぶか|ことし|さいやすね|こうしん"},{"japanese":"野菜が安値で取引されて、農家は困っている。","romaji":"Yasai ga yasune de torihiki sarete, nouka wa komatte iru.","german":"Vegetables are being traded at low prices, and farmers are in trouble.","furigana":"やさい|やすね|とりひき|のうか|こま"}],"notes":"安値 is a low price or quotation, above all in trading and on the stock exchange: 安値で売る・買う, 最安値 (\"lowest point, cheapest price\"), 年初来安値. The opposite is 高値. 値 is read ね here (\"price\"). In everyday speech, for \"cheap\" one simply says 安い or 格安.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いつも高値の時に株を買ってしまうんです。","romaji":"Itsumo takane no toki ni kabu o katte shimau n desu.","german":"I always end up buying stocks when they're at a high.","furigana":"たかね|とき|かぶ|か"},{"japanese":"その絵はオークションで高値で売れた。","romaji":"Sono e wa ookushon de takane de ureta.","german":"The painting sold for a high price at auction.","furigana":"え|たかね|う"},{"japanese":"雨が少なかったので、キャベツが高値になっている。","romaji":"Ame ga sukunakatta node, kyabetsu ga takane ni natte iru.","german":"Because there was little rain, cabbage is expensive right now.","furigana":"あめ|すく|たかね"}],"notes":"高値 is a high price or quotation, especially in trading and on the stock exchange: 高値で売れる, 高値がつく, 最高値 (\"record high, highest price\"). The opposite is 安値. For \"expensive\" in everyday speech, 高い is enough. Not to be confused with the identically read 高嶺 (高嶺の花 \"unattainable\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"もっと規律正しい生活をしなさい。","romaji":"Motto kiritsu tadashii seikatsu o shinasai.","german":"Lead a more orderly life.","furigana":"きりつただ|せいかつ"},{"japanese":"この学校は規律が厳しいことで有名です。","romaji":"Kono gakkou wa kiritsu ga kibishii koto de yuumei desu.","german":"This school is famous for its strict discipline.","furigana":"がっこう|きりつ|きび|ゆうめい"},{"japanese":"チームの規律を守らない選手は、試合に出られません。","romaji":"Chiimu no kiritsu o mamoranai senshu wa, shiai ni deraremasen.","german":"Players who do not follow the team's discipline cannot play in games.","furigana":"きりつ|まも|せんしゅ|しあい|で"}],"notes":"規律 is \"discipline\" or \"order\" within a group such as a school, army, company or sports team. Typical are 規律を守る, 規律を乱す, 規律が厳しい and 規律正しい (\"disciplined, regular\", e.g. of a lifestyle). Concrete individual rules are more often called 規則 or ルール. Teaching children good manners, by contrast, is called しつけ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この新製品とあの型落ちした商品の違いは何ですか？","romaji":"Kono shin seihin to ano kataochi shita shōhin no chigai wa nani desu ka?","german":"What is the difference between this new product and that older model?","furigana":"しんせいひん|かたお|しょうひん|ちが|なに"},{"japanese":"二つの言葉の違いがよく分かりません。","romaji":"Futatsu no kotoba no chigai ga yoku wakarimasen.","german":"I don't really understand the difference between the two words.","furigana":"ふた|ことば|ちが|わ"},{"japanese":"姉とは三つ違いです。","romaji":"Ane to wa mittsu chigai desu.","german":"My older sister and I are three years apart.","furigana":"あね|みっ|ちが"}],"notes":"違い is the noun of 違う and means \"difference\": AとBの違い, 違いがある・ない, 違いが分かる, 大違い. With ages: 三つ違いの兄弟 (\"siblings three years apart\"). The meaning \"mistake\" is usually expressed with 間違い. Important grammar: 〜に違いない (\"must surely be\"). 差 emphasizes more a measurable gap (点数の差), and 相違 is formal.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 17"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日は可燃ゴミの日だよね。","romaji":"Kyō wa kanen gomi no hi da yo ne.","german":"Today is burnable garbage day, isn't it?","furigana":"きょう|かねん|ひ"},{"japanese":"生ゴミは可燃ゴミとして出してください。","romaji":"Namagomi wa kanen gomi to shite dashite kudasai.","german":"Please put kitchen waste out as burnable garbage.","furigana":"なま|かねん|だ"},{"japanese":"プラスチックは可燃ゴミと不燃ゴミのどちらですか。","romaji":"Purasuchikku wa kanen gomi to funen gomi no dochira desu ka.","german":"Is plastic burnable or non-burnable garbage?","furigana":"かねん|ふねん"}],"notes":"可燃ゴミ is a category of Japanese garbage sorting: kitchen waste, paper scraps and the like. Colloquially it is called 燃えるゴミ, and in some cities officially 燃やすゴミ. The counterparts are 不燃ゴミ (\"non-burnable garbage\"), 資源ゴミ (\"recyclables\") and 粗大ゴミ (\"bulky waste\"). To put out the garbage is ゴミを出す; the fixed collection day is 可燃ゴミの日.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私たちのカフェは、今日から営業するんです。","romaji":"Watashitachi no kafe wa, kyō kara eigyō suru n desu.","german":"Our cafe opens for business from today.","furigana":"わたし|きょう|えいぎょう"},{"japanese":"この店は夜十時まで営業しています。","romaji":"Kono mise wa yoru juuji made eigyou shite imasu.","german":"This shop is open until 10 p.m.","furigana":"みせ|よるじゅうじ|えいぎょう"},{"japanese":"父は車の会社で営業をしています。","romaji":"Chichi wa kuruma no kaisha de eigyou o shite imasu.","german":"My father works in sales at a car company.","furigana":"ちち|くるま|かいしゃ|えいぎょう"}],"notes":"営業する means that a business is \"open, in operation\": 夜十時まで営業している, 営業中 (\"open\"), 営業時間, 本日の営業は終了しました. In addition, 営業 refers to sales: 営業部, 営業の仕事, 営業に回る. In conversation, people often simply say 開いている about shops; someone who runs a company as its owner 経営する it. It is mostly intransitive, with the business as the subject.","levelNote":"Derived from 営業 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本には伝統的な文化がたくさんあります。","romaji":"Nippon ni wa dentōteki na bunka ga takusan arimasu.","german":"Japan has a lot of traditional culture.","furigana":"にっぽん|でんとうてき|ぶんか"},{"japanese":"祖母は今でも伝統的な方法で味噌を作っている。","romaji":"Sobo wa ima demo dentouteki na houhou de miso o tsukutte iru.","german":"My grandmother still makes miso in the traditional way.","furigana":"そぼ|いま|でんとうてき|ほうほう|みそ|つく"},{"japanese":"この会社は伝統的に男性の社員が多い。","romaji":"Kono kaisha wa dentouteki ni dansei no shain ga ooi.","german":"This company traditionally has many male employees.","furigana":"かいしゃ|でんとうてき|だんせい|しゃいん|おお"}],"notes":"伝統的 is a な-adjective made from 伝統 (\"tradition\") and 〜的: 伝統的な料理, 伝統的な祭り; as an adverb 伝統的に (\"traditionally, from time immemorial\"). Warmer and more nostalgic is 昔ながらの (昔ながらの作り方). 古典的 means \"classical\", 保守的 \"conservative\" in the sense of an attitude.","levelNote":"Compound: 伝統 (N3) + 的 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"寝坊して、試合前に肩をちゃんと作れなかったんだ。","romaji":"Nebō shite, shiai mae ni kata o chanto tsukurenakatta n da.","german":"I overslept and couldn't properly warm up my shoulder before the game.","furigana":"ねぼう|しあいまえ|かた|つく"},{"japanese":"寝坊して、学校に遅刻した。","romaji":"Nebou shite, gakkou ni chikoku shita.","german":"I overslept and was late for school.","furigana":"ねぼう|がっこう|ちこく"},{"japanese":"休みの日はつい寝坊してしまう。","romaji":"Yasumi no hi wa tsui nebou shite shimau.","german":"On days off I tend to oversleep.","furigana":"やす|ひ|ねぼう"}],"notes":"寝坊する means \"to sleep too long (unintentionally), to oversleep\" and is intransitive. The classic apology: 寝坊して遅刻しました. 朝寝坊 emphasizes getting up late in the morning; a late sleeper is called 寝坊 or, affectionately, お寝坊さん. 寝過ごす means to miss something by sleeping (電車で寝過ごす \"to sleep through one's station\"), and 二度寝 \"to fall asleep again\".","levelNote":"Derived from 寝坊 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この形容詞を過去形に変えてください。","romaji":"Kono keiyoushi o kakokei ni kaete kudasai.","german":"Please change this adjective into the past tense.","furigana":"けいようし|かこけい|か"},{"japanese":"「食べる」の過去形は「食べた」です。","romaji":"Taberu no kakokei wa tabeta desu.","german":"The past tense of 「食べる」 is 「食べた」.","furigana":"た|かこけい|た"},{"japanese":"英語の不規則動詞の過去形を覚えるのは大変だ。","romaji":"Eigo no fukisoku doushi no kakokei o oboeru no wa taihen da.","german":"Learning the past tenses of English irregular verbs is hard.","furigana":"えいご|ふきそくどうし|かこけい|おぼ|たいへん"}],"notes":"過去形 is the grammatical term for \"past tense\": 〜の過去形, 過去形にする・に変える. Related terms are 現在形, 未来形, 否定形, 辞書形 and て形. In these grammar terms, 形 is read けい, not かたち or がた. In Japanese grammars the past tense is also called タ形.","levelNote":"Compound: 過去 (N3) + 形 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"食べ過ぎでおなかが痛い。","romaji":"Tabesugi de onaka ga itai.","german":"My stomach hurts from eating too much.","furigana":"た|す|いた"},{"japanese":"五時過ぎに駅に着きます。","romaji":"Goji sugi ni eki ni tsukimasu.","german":"I will arrive at the station a little after five.","furigana":"ごじす|えき|つ"},{"japanese":"父は六十過ぎだが、毎朝走っている。","romaji":"Chichi wa rokujuu sugi da ga, maiasa hashitte iru.","german":"My father is past sixty, but he runs every morning.","furigana":"ちち|ろくじゅうす|まいあさはし"}],"notes":"過ぎ is a suffix with two main meanings. After verb stems it means \"too much\": 食べ過ぎ, 飲み過ぎ, 働き過ぎ (often in kana すぎ, as a noun: 食べ過ぎに注意). After times of day or ages it means \"past, over\": 五時過ぎ, 昼過ぎ, 六十過ぎ. The related verb is 過ぎる (食べ過ぎる); colloquially a sentence often ends directly in すぎ: 高すぎ!","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"中学校の時は野球部の応援団の一員でした。","romaji":"Chūgakkō no toki wa yakyūbu no ōendan no ichiin deshita.","german":"In junior high, I was a member of the baseball club's cheering squad.","furigana":"ちゅうがっこう|とき|やきゅうぶ|おうえんだん|いちいん"},{"japanese":"応援団の大きな声がスタジアムに響いた。","romaji":"Ouendan no ookina koe ga sutajiamu ni hibiita.","german":"The loud voices of the cheering squad echoed through the stadium.","furigana":"おうえんだん|おお|こえ|ひび"},{"japanese":"君の夢なら、私が応援団長になるよ。","romaji":"Kimi no yume nara, watashi ga ouendanchou ni naru yo.","german":"If it's your dream, I'll be your cheerleader.","furigana":"きみ|ゆめ|わたし|おうえんだんちょう"}],"notes":"応援団 is an organized cheering squad, typical of Japanese schools and universities, for example at high school baseball (高校野球): groups in black uniforms with drums and chants, led by the 応援団長. This is something different from cheerleading (チアリーディング). Figuratively, supporters of a person are also called this. Verb: 応援する (\"to cheer on, to support\").","levelNote":"Compound: 応援 (N3) + 団 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私はこの会社の日本語教育を担当しています。","romaji":"Watashi wa kono kaisha no nihongo kyōiku o tantō shite imasu.","german":"I'm in charge of Japanese language education at this company.","furigana":"わたし|かいしゃ|にほんごきょういく|たんとう"},{"japanese":"担当の者に代わりますので、少々お待ちください。","romaji":"Tantou no mono ni kawarimasu node, shoushou omachi kudasai.","german":"I'll connect you to the person in charge, so please wait a moment.","furigana":"たんとう|もの|か|しょうしょう|ま"},{"japanese":"来週の会議の準備は誰が担当するの？","romaji":"Raishuu no kaigi no junbi wa dare ga tantou suru no?","german":"Who is in charge of preparing for next week's meeting?","furigana":"らいしゅう|かいぎ|じゅんび|だれ|たんとう"}],"notes":"担当 means \"to be in charge of an area of work\"; as a する-verb it is transitive: 日本語教育を担当する. Very common are 担当者 (\"contact person, person in charge\"), 担当の先生 and compounds like 営業担当. On the phone: 担当の者に代わります. 係 means a smaller, often temporary task (受付係), 責任 responsibility in the sense of liability.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"会社は多額の投資を行って新しい製品を開発したが、失敗に終わった。","romaji":"Kaisha wa tagaku no tōshi o okonatte atarashii seihin o kaihatsu shita ga, shippai ni owatta.","german":"The company made a large investment to develop a new product, but it ended in failure.","furigana":"かいしゃ|たがく|とうし|おこな|あたら|せいひん|かいはつ|しっぱい|お"},{"japanese":"祖父は多額の借金を残して亡くなった。","romaji":"Sofu wa tagaku no shakkin o nokoshite nakunatta.","german":"My grandfather died leaving a large debt.","furigana":"そふ|たがく|しゃっきん|のこ|な"},{"japanese":"彼女は病院に多額の寄付をした。","romaji":"Kanojo wa byouin ni tagaku no kifu o shita.","german":"She made a large donation to the hospital.","furigana":"かのじょ|びょういん|たがく|きふ"}],"notes":"多額 refers exclusively to money and almost always appears as 多額の before a noun: 多額の借金, 多額の費用, 多額の寄付. It sounds formal and is typical of news and reports. The opposite is 少額. For large quantities of other things one says 大量 or 多量, in everyday speech simply たくさんのお金 or 大金.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"月額よりも年額の方がお得ですよ。","romaji":"Getsugaku yori mo nengaku no hou ga otoku desu yo.","german":"The annual amount is a better deal than the monthly one.","furigana":"げつがく|ねんがく|ほう|とく"},{"japanese":"会費は年額五千円です。","romaji":"Kaihi wa nengaku gosen-en desu.","german":"The membership fee is 5,000 yen per year.","furigana":"かいひ|ねんがくごせんえん"},{"japanese":"この保険の保険料は年額でいくらになりますか。","romaji":"Kono hoken no hokenryou wa nengaku de ikura ni narimasu ka.","german":"How much is the premium for this insurance per year?","furigana":"ほけん|ほけんりょう|ねんがく"}],"notes":"年額 is the \"annual amount\" for fees, subscriptions, salaries or dues: 年額五千円, 年額で払う. The counterparts are 月額 (\"monthly amount\") and 日額; the total sum is 総額. In conversation people also often say 一年で or 年間. Related are 年収 (\"annual income\") and 年会費 (\"annual membership fee\").","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"それって月額いくらですか？","romaji":"Sore tte getsugaku ikura desu ka?","german":"How much is that per month?","furigana":"げつがく"},{"japanese":"このアプリは月額五百円で使い放題です。","romaji":"Kono apuri wa getsugaku gohyaku en de tsukaihoudai desu.","german":"You can use this app without limit for 500 yen a month.","furigana":"げつがくごひゃくえん|つか|ほうだい"},{"japanese":"家賃と管理費を合わせると、月額で八万円になります。","romaji":"Yachin to kanrihi o awaseru to, getsugaku de hachiman en ni narimasu.","german":"Rent and management fees together come to 80,000 yen a month.","furigana":"やちん|かんりひ|あ|げつがく|はちまんえん"}],"notes":"月額 is the monthly amount, especially for subscriptions, fees, rents and salaries: 月額料金 \"monthly fee\", 月額制 \"monthly subscription model\", 月額千円 \"1,000 yen per month\". With で it is often adverbial: 月額でいくら？ \"How much per month?\". Formed in parallel are 年額 \"annual amount\" and 日額 \"daily rate\". In conversation one also simply says 月に〜円 or 毎月〜円; 月額 sounds somewhat more matter-of-fact, as in contracts and price lists.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"定額のプランにしました。","romaji":"Teigaku no puran ni shimashita.","german":"I chose the flat-rate plan.","furigana":"ていがく"},{"japanese":"このタクシーは空港まで定額で行ってくれます。","romaji":"Kono takushii wa kuukou made teigaku de itte kuremasu.","german":"This taxi takes you to the airport for a flat fare.","furigana":"くうこう|ていがく|い"},{"japanese":"定額制の動画配信サービスを使っています。","romaji":"Teigakusei no douga haishin saabisu o tsukatte imasu.","german":"I use a flat-rate video streaming service.","furigana":"ていがくせい|どうがはいしん|つか"}],"notes":"定額 denotes a fixed, unchanging amount, no matter how much one uses: 定額制 \"flat-rate model\", 定額料金 \"flat fee\", 定額で〜 \"at a fixed price\". It is a noun and is used with の before nouns (定額のプラン). Not to be confused with 定価 \"list price, regular price\" and 月額 \"monthly amount\" - a subscription can be both 月額 and 定額. Typical of mobile plans, streaming and taxi rides to the airport.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"念のため、ICカードのチャージ残額を確認しておくよ。","romaji":"Nen no tame, IC kādo no chāji zangaku o kakunin shite oku yo.","german":"Just in case, I'll check the remaining balance on my IC card.","furigana":"ねん|ざんがく|かくにん"},{"japanese":"ローンの残額はあと五十万円です。","romaji":"Roon no zangaku wa ato gojuuman en desu.","german":"The remaining balance on the loan is 500,000 yen.","furigana":"ざんがく|ごじゅうまんえん"},{"japanese":"残額は来月お支払いください。","romaji":"Zangaku wa raigetsu oshiharai kudasai.","german":"Please pay the remaining balance next month.","furigana":"ざんがく|らいげつ|しはら"}],"notes":"残額 is the amount still remaining, for example with installments, bills or credit: 残額を支払う \"to pay the remaining balance\", ローンの残額 \"outstanding loan balance\". For an account balance and the credit on cards, 残高 is the usual word (預金残高, チャージ残高); 残額 puts more emphasis on what is left over or still open from a total sum. Colloquially one also says あと〜円残っている.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私たちは今、プロトタイプの製作に取りかかっています。","romaji":"Watashitachi wa ima, purototaipu no seisaku ni torikakatte imasu.","german":"We are now getting started on making the prototype.","furigana":"わたし|いま|せいさく|と"},{"japanese":"この家具は父が一人で製作したものです。","romaji":"Kono kagu wa chichi ga hitori de seisaku shita mono desu.","german":"My father made this furniture all by himself.","furigana":"かぐ|ちち|ひとり|せいさく"},{"japanese":"その映画の製作には三年かかった。","romaji":"Sono eiga no seisaku ni wa sannen kakatta.","german":"It took three years to produce that film.","furigana":"えいが|せいさく|さんねん"}],"notes":"製作 is the making of concrete things such as furniture, machines, models or prototypes; as a する-verb it is transitive: 〜を製作する. For films and broadcasts it means \"production\" (映画製作). To distinguish: 制作 (same reading) is preferred for artistic works and programs, 製造 for industrial mass production in factories. Typical are 〜の製作に取りかかる \"to begin making\" and 製作費 \"production costs\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このテーブルは木製に見えますが、実はプラスチック製です。","romaji":"Kono teeburu wa mokusei ni miemasu ga, jitsu wa purasuchikkusei desu.","german":"This table looks wooden, but it's actually made of plastic.","furigana":"もくせい|み|じつ|せい"},{"japanese":"木製のおもちゃは温かみがあって人気です。","romaji":"Mokusei no omocha wa atatakami ga atte ninki desu.","german":"Wooden toys are popular because they feel warm.","furigana":"もくせい|あたた|にんき"},{"japanese":"ベランダに木製のベンチを置きました。","romaji":"Beranda ni mokusei no benchi o okimashita.","german":"I put a wooden bench on the balcony.","furigana":"もくせい|お"}],"notes":"木製 means \"made of wood\" and is used as a noun with の before the object (木製の机) or predicatively with です. It belongs to the series of 〜製 words for material: 鉄製 \"made of iron\", プラスチック製 \"made of plastic\", ガラス製 \"made of glass\". For buildings, on the other hand, one says 木造 (木造の家). In everyday speech 木でできた〜 is also common; 木製 sounds somewhat more matter-of-fact, for example in product descriptions.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この肩かけは、日本製ですか？","romaji":"Kono katakake wa, Nihonsei desu ka?","german":"Is this shawl made in Japan?","furigana":"かた|にほんせい"},{"japanese":"日本製の家電は壊れにくいと言われています。","romaji":"Nihonsei no kaden wa kowarenikui to iwarete imasu.","german":"Japanese home appliances are said to be hard to break.","furigana":"にほんせい|かでん|こわ|い"},{"japanese":"お土産を買うなら、ちゃんと日本製かどうか確認したほうがいいよ。","romaji":"Omiyage o kau nara, chanto Nihonsei ka dou ka kakunin shita hou ga ii yo.","german":"If you're buying souvenirs, you should check properly whether they are made in Japan.","furigana":"みやげ|か|にほんせい|かくにん"}],"notes":"日本製 means \"made in Japan\" and refers to products, not people - for persons one says 日本出身 or 日本人. It is used with の before nouns (日本製の車) or predicatively (これは日本製です). It is mostly read にほんせい, less often にっぽんせい. Formed likewise are 中国製, アメリカ製, 外国製; labels often say \"Made in Japan\" or 日本製.","levelNote":"Compound: 日本 (N5) + 製 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"中国製だからというだけで、品質が悪いと決めつけないでください。","romaji":"Chuugokusei da kara to iu dake de, hinshitsu ga warui to kimetsukenaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't assume the quality is bad just because it's made in China.","furigana":"ちゅうごくせい|ひんしつ|わる|き"},{"japanese":"このスマホは中国製ですが、とても使いやすいです。","romaji":"Kono sumaho wa Chuugokusei desu ga, totemo tsukaiyasui desu.","german":"This smartphone is made in China, but it's very easy to use.","furigana":"ちゅうごくせい|つか"},{"japanese":"店にある服のほとんどが中国製だった。","romaji":"Mise ni aru fuku no hotondo ga Chuugokusei datta.","german":"Most of the clothes in the store were made in China.","furigana":"みせ|ふく|ちゅうごくせい"}],"notes":"中国製 means \"made in China\" and describes goods, not people (for people: 中国出身, 中国人). It is used with の before nouns (中国製の服) or as a predicate with です. The pattern country + 製 is productive: 日本製, アメリカ製, ドイツ製; the general opposite is 外国製, or 国産 for \"produced domestically\". One encounters the word very often in conversations about quality and price.","levelNote":"Compound: 中国 (N5) + 製 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"昨日、あのアメリカ製のスーツの肩をつめてもらいました。","romaji":"Kinō, ano Amerikasei no sūtsu no kata o tsumete moraimashita.","german":"Yesterday I had the shoulders of that American-made suit taken in.","furigana":"きのう|せい|かた"},{"japanese":"このバイクはアメリカ製で、エンジンの音が大きい。","romaji":"Kono baiku wa Amerikasei de, enjin no oto ga ookii.","german":"This motorcycle is made in America and has a loud engine.","furigana":"せい|おと|おお"},{"japanese":"アメリカ製の冷蔵庫は大きすぎて、うちの台所に入らない。","romaji":"Amerikasei no reizouko wa ookisugite, uchi no daidokoro ni hairanai.","german":"American-made refrigerators are too big to fit in our kitchen.","furigana":"せい|れいぞうこ|おお|だいどころ|はい"}],"notes":"アメリカ製 means \"made in the USA\" and is a compound of アメリカ and the suffix 〜製 \"made in / of\". It describes products and is used with の before nouns (アメリカ製の車) or predicatively with です. In newspapers and official texts one often reads 米国製 instead. For persons one does not use 〜製 but アメリカ出身 or アメリカ人.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"美味しい自家製のベーコンを使っています。","romaji":"Oishii jikasei no bēkon o tsukatte imasu.","german":"We use delicious homemade bacon.","furigana":"おい|じかせい|つか"},{"japanese":"母の自家製の梅干しは少ししょっぱい。","romaji":"Haha no jikasei no umeboshi wa sukoshi shoppai.","german":"My mother's homemade umeboshi are a little salty.","furigana":"はは|じかせい|うめぼ|すこ"},{"japanese":"この店のパンはすべて自家製です。","romaji":"Kono mise no pan wa subete jikasei desu.","german":"All the bread in this shop is made in-house.","furigana":"みせ|じかせい"}],"notes":"自家製 means \"made by oneself, homemade\" and is used above all for food: made at home (自家製の梅干し) or made in-house at a restaurant (自家製パン, 自家製麺). It is a noun and is used with の before the object or as a predicate. 手作り, on the other hand, emphasizes \"made by hand\" and also suits gifts or clothing; on menus, 自家製 acts like a promise of quality.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不燃ゴミは火曜日に出してください。","romaji":"Funen gomi wa kayōbi ni dashite kudasai.","german":"Please put out non-burnable garbage on Tuesday.","furigana":"ふねん|かようび|だ"},{"japanese":"割れたコップは不燃ゴミの日に出します。","romaji":"Wareta koppu wa funen gomi no hi ni dashimasu.","german":"I put out broken glasses on non-burnable garbage day.","furigana":"わ|ふねん|ひ|だ"},{"japanese":"スプレー缶を不燃ゴミに入れてもいいですか。","romaji":"Supureekan o funen gomi ni irete mo ii desu ka.","german":"May I put spray cans in the non-burnable garbage?","furigana":"かん|ふねん|い"}],"notes":"不燃ゴミ is the \"non-burnable garbage\" of Japanese garbage sorting: glass, ceramics, metal, small electrical appliances, etc. The counterpart is 可燃ゴミ or, colloquially, 燃えるゴミ; in everyday speech 不燃ゴミ is often also called 燃えないゴミ. Garbage is put out with 出す: 不燃ゴミを出す, 不燃ゴミの日. On municipal schedules 不燃ごみ is often written in hiragana; what exactly belongs to it is regulated by each municipality itself.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今度の祝日の予定、もう立てた？","romaji":"Kondo no shukujitsu no yotei, mō tateta?","german":"Have you already made plans for the next holiday?","furigana":"こんど|しゅくじつ|よてい|た"},{"japanese":"月曜日が祝日なので、三連休になります。","romaji":"Getsuyoubi ga shukujitsu na node, sanrenkyuu ni narimasu.","german":"Monday is a holiday, so it will be a three-day weekend.","furigana":"げつようび|しゅくじつ|さんれんきゅう"},{"japanese":"この美術館は祝日も開いています。","romaji":"Kono bijutsukan wa shukujitsu mo aite imasu.","german":"This museum is open on holidays too.","furigana":"びじゅつかん|しゅくじつ|あ"}],"notes":"祝日 is a statutory public holiday (国民の祝日), e.g. 文化の日 or 海の日. 休日 is the more general \"day off\" and includes weekends; the older 祭日 can still be heard in 祝祭日. Signs often say 土日祝 \"Sat, Sun and holidays\". When a holiday falls on a Sunday, there is a 振替休日 \"substitute holiday\". Typical: 祝日も営業, 月曜日が祝日で三連休.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は高級腕時計を買いたいとは全然思いません。","romaji":"Watashi wa kōkyū udedokei o kaitai to wa zenzen omoimasen.","german":"I have absolutely no desire to buy a luxury wristwatch.","furigana":"わたし|こうきゅううでどけい|か|ぜんぜんおも"},{"japanese":"腕時計を見ると、もう十時を過ぎていた。","romaji":"Udedokei o miru to, mou juuji o sugite ita.","german":"When I looked at my wristwatch, it was already past ten.","furigana":"うでどけい|み|じゅうじ|す"},{"japanese":"誕生日に父から腕時計をもらいました。","romaji":"Tanjoubi ni chichi kara udedokei o moraimashita.","german":"I received a wristwatch from my father for my birthday.","furigana":"たんじょうび|ちち|うでどけい"}],"notes":"腕時計 is the wristwatch; through rendaku, とけい becomes どけい. One wears it with する, はめる or つける (腕時計をしている), and takes it off with 外す. If it runs fast or slow, one says 進んでいる or 遅れている. Other clocks: 置き時計 \"table clock\", 掛け時計 \"wall clock\", 目覚まし時計 \"alarm clock\"; in general, simply 時計 is often enough.","levelNote":"Compound: 腕 (N4) + 時計 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このストールの感触、シルクみたい。","romaji":"Kono sutooru no kanshoku, shiruku mitai.","german":"The feel of this stole is like silk.","furigana":"かんしょく"},{"japanese":"面接の感触はよかったので、たぶん大丈夫だと思う。","romaji":"Mensetsu no kanshoku wa yokatta node, tabun daijoubu da to omou.","german":"The interview felt good, so I think it will probably be fine.","furigana":"めんせつ|かんしょく|だいじょうぶ|おも"},{"japanese":"砂浜を裸足で歩くと、砂の感触が気持ちいい。","romaji":"Sunahama o hadashi de aruku to, suna no kanshoku ga kimochi ii.","german":"When you walk barefoot on the beach, the feel of the sand is pleasant.","furigana":"すなはま|はだし|ある|すな|かんしょく|きも"}],"notes":"感触 is first of all the feeling on touching, the tactile quality of a material: 感触がいい, さらさらした感触, 砂の感触. Figuratively it means the \"impression\" of how something went: 面接の感触はよかった \"The interview felt good\"; 手ごたえ is similar here. Not to be confused with 触覚 \"sense of touch\" as a sensory faculty; 手触り is a more everyday word specifically for the surface feel when handling something.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その記事なら五月号所載ですよ。","romaji":"Sono kiji nara gogatsugō shosai desu yo.","german":"That article is in the May issue.","furigana":"きじ|ごがつごうしょさい"},{"japanese":"本書所載の写真は、すべて著者が撮影したものです。","romaji":"Honsho shosai no shashin wa, subete chosha ga satsuei shita mono desu.","german":"All the photos in this book were taken by the author.","furigana":"ほんしょしょさい|しゃしん|ちょしゃ|さつえい"},{"japanese":"この資料は、昔の新聞所載の記事をまとめたものです。","romaji":"Kono shiryou wa, mukashi no shinbun shosai no kiji o matometa mono desu.","german":"This material is a compilation of articles that appeared in old newspapers.","furigana":"しりょう|むかし|しんぶんしょさい|きじ"}],"notes":"所載 is a very literary, bibliographic word: \"printed in, contained in\" a magazine or book. It follows the source directly (五月号所載, 本書所載の写真) and stands before a noun with の. In ordinary usage people say 〜に掲載されている or 〜に載っている instead. 記載, by contrast, means \"an entry or statement in a document\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ここには、「ビエトは気の弱いヤクザだ」という記載があります。","romaji":"Koko ni wa, 'Bieto wa ki no yowai yakuza da' to iu kisai ga arimasu.","german":"It says here, \"Bieto is a timid yakuza.\"","furigana":"き|よわ|きさい"},{"japanese":"申込書に記載された住所に書類を送ります。","romaji":"Moushikomisho ni kisai sareta juusho ni shorui o okurimasu.","german":"We will send the documents to the address entered on the application form.","furigana":"もうしこみしょ|きさい|じゅうしょ|しょるい|おく"},{"japanese":"説明書に記載されている方法で使ってください。","romaji":"Setsumeisho ni kisai sarete iru houhou de tsukatte kudasai.","german":"Please use it in the way described in the instruction manual.","furigana":"せつめいしょ|きさい|ほうほう|つか"}],"notes":"記載 is a written statement or entry in a document (form, contract, manual). As a する-verb it is transitive (住所を記載する), but mostly in the passive: 〜に記載されている \"is written in ...\". Typical: 記載事項 \"entries, particulars\", 記載漏れ \"missing entry\", 〜という記載がある. 記述 is a more detailed description, 掲載 is publication in a newspaper or on the web.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"え、リンゴ八個もあるの？このトレイに載るかな？","romaji":"E, ringo hakko mo aru no? Kono torei ni noru ka na?","german":"What, there are eight apples? I wonder if they'll fit on this tray?","furigana":"はっこ|の"},{"japanese":"その言葉は辞書に載っていない。","romaji":"Sono kotoba wa jisho ni notte inai.","german":"That word isn't in the dictionary.","furigana":"ことば|じしょ|の"},{"japanese":"うちの店が雑誌に載りました。","romaji":"Uchi no mise ga zasshi ni norimashita.","german":"Our shop was featured in a magazine.","furigana":"みせ|ざっし|の"}],"notes":"載る is intransitive and has two main meanings: \"to lie on something, to have room\" (〜に載る, e.g. on a tray) and \"to be printed, to appear\" (新聞に載る, 辞書に載っていない). The transitive counterpart is 載せる \"to put on; to publish\". Not to be confused with 乗る \"to board, to ride\" - same reading, different kanji. In everyday writing 載る is often written in hiragana too.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このファッションは、若者層に人気です。","romaji":"Kono fasshon wa, wakamonosou ni ninki desu.","german":"This fashion is popular with young people.","furigana":"わかものそう|にんき"},{"japanese":"物価が上がると、低所得層の生活が特に苦しくなる。","romaji":"Bukka ga agaru to, teishotokusou no seikatsu ga toku ni kurushiku naru.","german":"When prices rise, life becomes especially hard for low-income households.","furigana":"ぶっか|あ|ていしょとくそう|せいかつ|とく|くる"},{"japanese":"このケーキは五層になっています。","romaji":"Kono keeki wa gosou ni natte imasu.","german":"This cake has five layers.","furigana":"ごそう"}],"notes":"〜層 is a suffix for a group or stratum of society: 若者層 \"young people as a target group\", 高齢者層, 富裕層 \"the wealthy\", 中間層, 支持層 \"supporter base\". It sounds matter-of-fact and is typical of marketing, media and statistics. Literally 層 means \"layer, tier\", hence also 五層のケーキ, 地層 \"rock stratum\" and オゾン層. On its own it is heard in 層が厚い \"broad-based\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"へー、これがワニカニ人形の原型なんですか。","romaji":"Hee, kore ga Wanikani ningyou no genkei na n desu ka.","german":"Oh, so this is the original model of the Wanikani doll?","furigana":"にんぎょう|げんけい"},{"japanese":"この話の原型は、古い民話だと言われている。","romaji":"Kono hanashi no genkei wa, furui minwa da to iwarete iru.","german":"The archetype of this story is said to be an old folktale.","furigana":"はなし|げんけい|ふる|みんわ|い"},{"japanese":"スカートの原型を紙で作ってから、布を切ります。","romaji":"Sukaato no genkei o kami de tsukutte kara, nuno o kirimasu.","german":"I make the basic pattern of the skirt out of paper first, and then cut the fabric.","furigana":"げんけい|かみ|つく|ぬの|き"}],"notes":"原型 is the original model or template from which something was developed: 人形の原型, 物語の原型 \"archetype of a story\". In tailoring it is the basic pattern. Not to be confused with 原形 \"original form\" (原形をとどめない, 動詞の原形 \"base form of a verb\"). For technical prototypes, everyday speech rather says 試作品 or プロトタイプ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"都庁の前には、１９９５年型のホンダ・シビックが停まっていた。","romaji":"Tochō no mae ni wa, sen kyūhyaku kyūjūgo-nengata no Honda Shibikku ga tomatte ita.","german":"A 1995 Honda Civic was parked in front of the Metropolitan Government Building.","furigana":"とちょう|まえ|ねんがた|と"},{"japanese":"都庁の展望室から富士山が見えました。","romaji":"Tochō no tenbōshitsu kara Fujisan ga miemashita.","german":"I could see Mt. Fuji from the observation deck of the Tokyo Metropolitan Government Building.","furigana":"とちょう|てんぼうしつ|ふじさん|み"},{"japanese":"姉は都庁に勤めています。","romaji":"Ane wa Tochō ni tsutomete imasu.","german":"My older sister works at the Tokyo Metropolitan Government.","furigana":"あね|とちょう|つと"}],"notes":"都庁 is the short form of 東京都庁, the administration of Tokyo Prefecture, and at the same time denotes the building in Shinjuku. At its head is the 都知事. Correspondingly, the administrations of other prefectures are called 県庁, 府庁 or 道庁, and that of a city 市役所. The building's free observation decks are a popular destination for tourists.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"男性はぼったくりバーで40万円をだまし取られ、警視庁に相談していた。","romaji":"Dansei wa bottakuri baa de yonjuuman en o damashitorare, Keishichou ni soudan shite ita.","german":"A man was swindled out of 400,000 yen at a rip-off bar and had consulted the Metropolitan Police Department.","furigana":"だんせい|まんえん|と|けいしちょう|そうだん"},{"japanese":"警視庁は、東京都を担当する警察の組織です。","romaji":"Keishichou wa, Toukyouto o tantou suru keisatsu no soshiki desu.","german":"The Metropolitan Police Department is the police organization in charge of Tokyo.","furigana":"けいしちょう|とうきょうと|たんとう|けいさつ|そしき"},{"japanese":"ドラマでは、警視庁の刑事が事件を解決する。","romaji":"Dorama de wa, Keishichou no keiji ga jiken o kaiketsu suru.","german":"In the drama, a detective of the Metropolitan Police Department solves the case.","furigana":"けいしちょう|けいじ|じけん|かいけつ"}],"notes":"警視庁 is the police of Tokyo Prefecture (Tokyo Metropolitan Police Department), headquartered in Kasumigaseki. All other prefectures have a 〜県警 or 道警/府警; 警視庁 exists only for Tokyo. Not to be confused with 警察庁, the national police agency, which coordinates the prefectural police but does not investigate itself. Typical in the news: 警視庁によると, 警視庁は〜を逮捕した.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どうして警察庁は犯人の肩を持つような発言をしているんだ？","romaji":"Dōshite keisatsuchō wa hannin no kata o motsu yō na hatsugen o shite iru n da?","german":"Why is the National Police Agency making statements that seem to take the culprit's side?","furigana":"けいさつちょう|はんにん|かた|も|はつげん"},{"japanese":"警察庁は全国の警察をまとめる国の機関です。","romaji":"Keisatsuchou wa zenkoku no keisatsu o matomeru kuni no kikan desu.","german":"The National Police Agency is the national organization that oversees the police across the country.","furigana":"けいさつちょう|ぜんこく|けいさつ|くに|きかん"},{"japanese":"警察庁の発表によると、去年の交通事故の死者は減ったそうだ。","romaji":"Keisatsuchou no happyou ni yoru to, kyonen no koutsuu jiko no shisha wa hetta sou da.","german":"According to an announcement by the National Police Agency, traffic deaths decreased last year.","furigana":"けいさつちょう|はっぴょう|きょねん|こうつうじこ|ししゃ|へ"}],"notes":"警察庁 is Japan's National Police Agency; it plans, coordinates and publishes statistics, while the actual police work lies with the prefectural police (〜県警, in Tokyo 警視庁). In the news often: 警察庁の発表によると, 警察庁長官. Mind the reading: けいさつちょう, not to be confused with 警視庁 (けいしちょう). \"The police\" in general is simply 警察.","levelNote":"Compound: 警察 (N4) + 庁 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"法律を無視するような事はできません。","romaji":"Hōritsu o mushi suru yō na koto wa dekimasen.","german":"We can't do anything that disregards the law.","furigana":"ほうりつ|むし|こと"},{"japanese":"メッセージを送ったのに、ずっと無視されている。","romaji":"Messeeji o okutta noni, zutto mushi sarete iru.","german":"I sent a message, but I keep being ignored.","furigana":"おく|むし"},{"japanese":"信号を無視して道を渡るのは危ないよ。","romaji":"Shingou o mushi shite michi o wataru no wa abunai yo.","german":"It's dangerous to cross the road ignoring the traffic light.","furigana":"しんごう|むし|みち|わた|あぶ"}],"notes":"無視する means \"to ignore, to disregard\" and is transitive: 人を無視する, 注意を無視する, 法律を無視する. Very common in the passive: 無視される \"to be ignored\". Fixed expressions are 信号無視 \"running a red light / crossing on red\" and 既読無視 \"read but not answered\" (in chat apps). Colloquially one also says スルーする; 見て見ぬふりをする means \"to look the other way\".","levelNote":"Derived from 無視 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"うーん…もっと消費者にアピールできるような、視覚的なデザインにしたいよね。","romaji":"Ūn... motto shōhisha ni apīru dekiru yō na, shikakuteki na dezain ni shitai yo ne.","german":"Hmm... I'd like a more visual design that appeals to consumers more.","furigana":"しょうひしゃ|しかくてき"},{"japanese":"人間は情報の多くを視覚から得ている。","romaji":"Ningen wa jouhou no ooku o shikaku kara ete iru.","german":"Humans get much of their information through sight.","furigana":"にんげん|じょうほう|おお|しかく|え"},{"japanese":"視覚に障害のある人のために、点字の案内があります。","romaji":"Shikaku ni shougai no aru hito no tame ni, tenji no annai ga arimasu.","german":"For people with visual impairments, there are Braille guides.","furigana":"しかく|しょうがい|ひと|てんじ|あんない"}],"notes":"視覚 is the sense of sight as one of the five senses (五感): 視覚, 聴覚, 嗅覚, 味覚, 触覚. Visual acuity, on the other hand, is 視力. Common is the derivation 視覚的(な) \"visual\" (視覚的に分かりやすい). Other phrases: 視覚障害 \"visual impairment\", 視覚に訴える \"to appeal to the eye\". Beware of homophones: 資格 \"qualification\", 四角 \"square\", 死角 \"blind spot\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"メガネをかけてるのはファッションじゃなくて、単に近視なだけですよ。","romaji":"Megane o kaketeru no wa fasshon ja nakute, tan ni kinshi na dake desu yo.","german":"I wear glasses not as a fashion statement but simply because I'm nearsighted.","furigana":"たん|きんし"},{"japanese":"スマホの見すぎで、子どもの近視が増えている。","romaji":"Sumaho no misugi de, kodomo no kinshi ga fuete iru.","german":"Nearsightedness is increasing among children because they look at smartphones too much.","furigana":"み|こ|きんし|ふ"},{"japanese":"私は強い近視なので、メガネがないと何も見えません。","romaji":"Watashi wa tsuyoi kinshi na node, megane ga nai to nani mo miemasen.","german":"I'm severely nearsighted, so I can't see anything without glasses.","furigana":"わたし|つよ|きんし|なに|み"}],"notes":"近視 is the medically neutral term for nearsightedness; colloquially one says 近眼 (きんがん, also ちかめ) or simply 目が悪い. One \"is\" nearsighted: 近視です, 強い近視 \"severely nearsighted\"; nearsightedness \"progresses\": 近視が進む. As an attribute it takes の: 近視の人, 近視用のメガネ. The opposite is 遠視 \"farsightedness\"; age-related farsightedness is 老眼.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"遠視なので、メガネが必要なんです。","romaji":"Enshi na node, megane ga hitsuyō na n desu.","german":"I'm farsighted, so I need glasses.","furigana":"えんし|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"子どもの遠視は、早く見つけることが大切です。","romaji":"Kodomo no enshi wa, hayaku mitsukeru koto ga taisetsu desu.","german":"It is important to detect farsightedness in children early.","furigana":"こ|えんし|はや|み|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"遠視の人は、近くの文字を読むときに目が疲れやすい。","romaji":"Enshi no hito wa, chikaku no moji o yomu toki ni me ga tsukareyasui.","german":"People who are farsighted tire their eyes easily when reading nearby text.","furigana":"えんし|ひと|ちか|もじ|よ|め|つか"}],"notes":"遠視 is farsightedness: near objects are seen blurred. It is a noun (遠視です, 遠視の子ども, 遠視用のメガネ). The opposite is 近視. It is important to distinguish it from 老眼 \"presbyopia\", which comes with age; 遠視, by contrast, is a characteristic of the eye that already occurs in children. In everyday speech people more often say 近くが見えにくい.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"暗い部屋に光が差しています。","romaji":"Kurai heya ni hikari ga sashite imasu.","german":"Light is shining into the dark room.","furigana":"くら|へや|ひかり|さ"},{"japanese":"日が差す場所に椅子を置きました。","romaji":"Hi ga sasu basho ni isu o okimashita.","german":"I put a chair in a place where the sun shines in.","furigana":"ひ|さ|ばしょ|いす|お"}],"notes":"差す here means that light or sunlight shines into an area. It is an intransitive godan verb: 差します, 差して. With が you name the light, with に the lit area. 日が差す場所 refers to a place the sun shines on. 光が差している describes light currently coming in. In this sense of light, 射す／さす is also attested. This describes incoming light and is not 射る／いる \"to shoot\". The identically sounding spelling 注す, for pouring a liquid, belongs to a transitive verb.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"管を取り付ける前に、部品を確認してください。","romaji":"Kuda o toritsukeru mae ni, buhin o kakunin shite kudasai.","german":"Please check the parts before attaching the pipe.","furigana":"くだ|と|つ|まえ|ぶひん|かくにん"},{"japanese":"細い管を通して、水が流れている。","romaji":"Hosoi kuda o tooshite, mizu ga nagarete iru.","german":"Water is flowing through a thin pipe.","furigana":"ほそ|くだ|とお|みず|なが"},{"japanese":"入院中は、腕に点滴の管がつながっていた。","romaji":"Nyuuinchuu wa, ude ni tenteki no kuda ga tsunagatte ita.","german":"During my hospital stay, an IV tube was connected to my arm.","furigana":"にゅういんちゅう|うで|てんてき|くだ"}],"notes":"管 (くだ) is a pipe or tube, especially a thin one, e.g. on an IV stand. On its own it is mostly read くだ; in compounds it is read かん: 水道管, ガス管, 血管, 試験管. In everyday speech パイプ and チューブ are also very common. The expression くだを巻く means \"to ramble on drunkenly\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"父は血管が切れそうなぐらい怒っていた。","romaji":"Chichi wa kekkan ga kiresōna gurai okotte ita.","german":"My father was so angry that it looked like a blood vessel might burst.","furigana":"ちち|けっかん|き|おこ"},{"japanese":"採血のとき、看護師さんがなかなか血管を見つけられなかった。","romaji":"Saiketsu no toki, kangoshi-san ga nakanaka kekkan o mitsukerarenakatta.","german":"When drawing blood, the nurse couldn't find the vein for a long time.","furigana":"さいけつ|かんごし|けっかん|み"},{"japanese":"運動不足は血管の病気の原因になる。","romaji":"Undou busoku wa kekkan no byouki no gen'in ni naru.","german":"Lack of exercise can cause diseases of the blood vessels.","furigana":"うんどうぶそく|けっかん|びょうき|げんいん"}],"notes":"血管 is the blood vessel in general; more precisely one distinguishes 動脈 \"artery\", 静脈 \"vein\" and 毛細血管 \"capillary\". Typical: 血管が浮き出る \"veins stand out\", 血管が細い, 血管が詰まる \"a vessel gets blocked\". Figuratively, 血管が切れそう stands for extreme anger. The kanji 管 is read かん here, as in 水道管 or 試験管.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"気象予報によると、明日は大雪になるそうです。","romaji":"Kishō yohō ni yoru to, ashita wa ōyuki ni naru sō desu.","german":"According to the weather forecast, it will snow heavily tomorrow.","furigana":"きしょうよほう|あした|おおゆき"},{"japanese":"気象庁は、明日大雨になるおそれがあると発表した。","romaji":"Kishouchou wa, ashita ooame ni naru osore ga aru to happyou shita.","german":"The Meteorological Agency announced that there is a risk of heavy rain tomorrow.","furigana":"きしょうちょう|あしたおおあめ|はっぴょう"},{"japanese":"地球温暖化で、異常気象が増えている。","romaji":"Chikyuu ondanka de, ijou kishou ga fuete iru.","german":"Due to global warming, abnormal weather is increasing.","furigana":"ちきゅうおんだんか|いじょうきしょう|ふ"}],"notes":"気象 is weather phenomena in the scientific or official sense and almost always occurs in compounds: 気象庁 \"Meteorological Agency\", 気象情報, 気象予報士 \"meteorologist\", 気象衛星, 異常気象 \"extreme weather\". In everyday speech one says 天気 (\"how is the weather?\") and 天気予報; 天候 means the weather over a period (天候不順). 気象 on its own in conversation sounds technical.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"単に気が変わったという理由で、前日に大量のドーナツの注文をキャンセルした。","romaji":"Tanni ki ga kawatta toiu riyū de, zenjitsu ni tairyō no dōnatsu no chūmon o kyanseru shita.","german":"Just because I changed my mind, I canceled the order for a large quantity of donuts the day before.","furigana":"たん|き|か|りゆう|ぜんじつ|たいりょう|ちゅうもん"},{"japanese":"この部品は工場で大量に作られています。","romaji":"Kono buhin wa koujou de tairyou ni tsukurarete imasu.","german":"These parts are made in large quantities at the factory.","furigana":"ぶひん|こうじょう|たいりょう|つく"},{"japanese":"大雨のあと、川に大量のゴミが流れてきた。","romaji":"Ooame no ato, kawa ni tairyou no gomi ga nagarete kita.","german":"After the heavy rain, a large amount of trash was washed down the river.","furigana":"おおあめ|かわ|たいりょう|なが"}],"notes":"大量 means \"large quantity\" and is used attributively with の (大量のゴミ) or adverbially with に (大量に買う). In compounds it stands for \"mass-\": 大量生産, 大量消費. The opposite is 少量. In conversation たくさん or いっぱい sounds more casual; 大量 stresses a strikingly, often excessively large amount. The written 多量 is rarer.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コウイチは一週間も生死の境をさまよった。","romaji":"Kouichi wa isshuukan mo seishi no sakai o samayotta.","german":"Koichi wandered between life and death for a whole week.","furigana":"いっしゅうかん|せいし|さかい"},{"japanese":"この川が二つの村の境になっている。","romaji":"Kono kawa ga futatsu no mura no sakai ni natte iru.","german":"This river forms the boundary between the two villages.","furigana":"かわ|ふた|むら|さかい"},{"japanese":"あの事件を境に、彼は変わってしまった。","romaji":"Ano jiken o sakai ni, kare wa kawatte shimatta.","german":"He has changed since that incident.","furigana":"じけん|さかい|かれ|か"}],"notes":"境 is the border or dividing line between two areas: 庭と道の境, 村の境. For national and prefectural borders one mostly uses 国境 (こっきょう) and 県境; 境界 is the more formal expression. Temporally, 〜を境に(して) means \"from ... on, since ... (turning point)\". Fixed phrase: 生死の境をさまよう \"to hover between life and death\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"カフェをやめて、新しく武器の輸出のビジネスを始めることにしました。","romaji":"Kafe o yamete, atarashiku buki no yushutsu no bijinesu o hajimeru koto ni shimashita.","german":"I've decided to quit the cafe and start a new weapons export business.","furigana":"あたら|ぶき|ゆしゅつ|はじ"},{"japanese":"警察は男から武器を取り上げた。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa otoko kara buki o toriageta.","german":"The police took the weapon away from the man.","furigana":"けいさつ|おとこ|ぶき|と|あ"},{"japanese":"彼の一番の武器は、明るい笑顔です。","romaji":"Kare no ichiban no buki wa, akarui egao desu.","german":"His greatest weapon is his bright smile.","furigana":"かれ|いちばん|ぶき|あか|えがお"}],"notes":"武器 is a \"weapon\": 武器を持つ, 武器を捨てる, 武器の輸出. For military weapon systems 兵器 is usual (核兵器), for a weapon used in a crime 凶器. Very common is the figurative meaning \"strength, trump card\": 笑顔が彼の武器だ, 英語力を武器にする (\"to score points with one's English skills\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"朝食には、良質なタンパク質を取るよう心がけています。","romaji":"Chōshoku ni wa, ryōshitsu na tanpakushitsu o toru yō kokorogakete imasu.","german":"I try to get high-quality protein at breakfast.","furigana":"ちょうしょく|りょうしつ|しつ|と|こころ"},{"japanese":"良質な睡眠をとるために、寝る前はスマホを見ない。","romaji":"Ryoushitsu na suimin o toru tame ni, neru mae wa sumaho o minai.","german":"To get good-quality sleep, I don't look at my phone before bed.","furigana":"りょうしつ|すいみん|ね|まえ|み"},{"japanese":"この地方は良質な米がとれることで有名です。","romaji":"Kono chihou wa ryoushitsu na kome ga toreru koto de yuumei desu.","german":"This region is famous for producing high-quality rice.","furigana":"ちほう|りょうしつ|こめ|ゆうめい"}],"notes":"良質 means \"of good quality\" and stands as a な- or の-adjective before nouns: 良質なタンパク質, 良質の米, 良質な睡眠. It sounds somewhat elevated and is typical of advertising, health and nutrition texts. In conversation one rather says 質のいい〜 or 品質がいい. Note: 悪質 is not simply the opposite, but means \"malicious, fraudulent\" (悪質な詐欺).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これは、日本文化の特質と言えるでしょう。","romaji":"Kore wa, Nihon bunka no tokushitsu to ieru deshou.","german":"This can be called a distinctive quality of Japanese culture.","furigana":"にほんぶんか|とくしつ|い"},{"japanese":"この素材の特質は、軽くて丈夫なことです。","romaji":"Kono sozai no tokushitsu wa, karukute joubu na koto desu.","german":"The characteristic of this material is that it is light and sturdy.","furigana":"そざい|とくしつ|かる|じょうぶ"},{"japanese":"子ども一人一人の特質を生かした教育が大切だ。","romaji":"Kodomo hitori hitori no tokushitsu o ikashita kyouiku ga taisetsu da.","german":"Education that makes use of each child's individual qualities is important.","furigana":"こ|ひとりひとり|とくしつ|い|きょういく|たいせつ"}],"notes":"特質 is a special, essential quality that sets something apart from other things; it sounds abstract and rather literary: 日本文化の特質, 素材の特質. The everyday word is 特徴 \"feature\", often for visible characteristics; 特色 emphasizes what is unmistakable (地域の特色), 性質 the inner nature. Typical pattern: 〜の特質を生かす \"to make use of the special quality of ...\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"うーん、それは正しい記述ではないですね。","romaji":"Uun, sore wa tadashii kijutsu de wa nai desu ne.","german":"Hmm, that is not an accurate description.","furigana":"ただ|きじゅつ"},{"japanese":"この問題は記述式なので、答えを文章で書いてください。","romaji":"Kono mondai wa kijutsushiki na node, kotae o bunshou de kaite kudasai.","german":"This question is a written-answer type, so please write your answer in sentences.","furigana":"もんだい|きじゅつしき|こた|ぶんしょう|か"},{"japanese":"教科書には、その戦争について詳しく記述されている。","romaji":"Kyoukasho ni wa, sono sensou ni tsuite kuwashiku kijutsu sarete iru.","german":"The textbook describes that war in detail.","furigana":"きょうかしょ|せんそう|くわ|きじゅつ"}],"notes":"記述 is a written description or account; as a する-verb it is transitive: 〜を記述する, often in the passive 〜に記述されている. It is well known from exams: 記述式 \"free-response questions\" as opposed to 選択式 \"multiple choice\". To distinguish: 記載 is a brief entry in forms, 説明 is an explanation, 描写 is a vivid depiction in literature.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"上述の理由から、私は今回のプロジェクトには参加しないことに決めました。","romaji":"Jōjutsu no riyū kara, watashi wa konkai no purojekuto ni wa sanka shinai koto ni kimemashita.","german":"For the reasons mentioned above, I have decided not to take part in this project.","furigana":"じょうじゅつ|りゆう|わたし|こんかい|さんか|き"},{"japanese":"上述したとおり、申し込みの締め切りは三月末です。","romaji":"Joujutsu shita toori, moushikomi no shimekiri wa sangatsumatsu desu.","german":"As stated above, the application deadline is the end of March.","furigana":"じょうじゅつ|もう|こ|し|き|さんがつまつ"},{"japanese":"上述の表を見ると、売り上げが年々増えていることがわかる。","romaji":"Joujutsu no hyou o miru to, uriage ga nennen fuete iru koto ga wakaru.","german":"Looking at the table above, you can see that sales are increasing year by year.","furigana":"じょうじゅつ|ひょう|み|う|あ|ねんねんふ"}],"notes":"上述 refers in texts to something already said further above: 上述の理由, 上述したように \"as mentioned above\". It is purely written language (reports, theses, official texts). Synonyms are 前述 and 先述; in forms and e-mails 上記 is more usual, and the counterpart is 下記 or 以下. In speech one says 先ほど言ったように instead.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"じゃあ、親展で送るね。","romaji":"Jā, shinten de okuru ne.","german":"Then I'll send it marked confidential.","furigana":"しんてん|おく"},{"japanese":"封筒に「親展」と書いてあるので、本人以外は開けないでください。","romaji":"Fuutou ni 'shinten' to kaite aru node, honnin igai wa akenaide kudasai.","german":"The envelope says \"personal\", so please don't open it unless you are the addressee.","furigana":"ふうとう|しんてん|か|ほんにんいがい|あ"},{"japanese":"銀行から親展の手紙が届いた。","romaji":"Ginkou kara shinten no tegami ga todoita.","german":"A confidential letter arrived from the bank.","furigana":"ぎんこう|しんてん|てがみ|とど"}],"notes":"親展 is a marking on envelopes: \"personal, confidential - to be opened only by the addressee\". It is written next to the address, or one sends something 親展で. Typical for letters from banks, government offices or with health data. For confidentiality in general (information, documents), people say 機密, 内密 or 秘密 instead.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日は日曜日なのに営業中なんですね。","romaji":"Kyō wa nichiyōbi na noni eigyōchū na n desu ne.","german":"It's Sunday, and yet it's open.","furigana":"きょう|にちようび|えいぎょうちゅう"},{"japanese":"ドアに「営業中」の札がかかっている。","romaji":"Doa ni 'eigyouchuu' no fuda ga kakatte iru.","german":"A sign saying \"Open\" is hanging on the door.","furigana":"えいぎょうちゅう|ふだ"},{"japanese":"スマホの地図で、近くの営業中の薬局を探した。","romaji":"Sumaho no chizu de, chikaku no eigyouchuu no yakkyoku o sagashita.","german":"On my phone's map, I searched for a pharmacy nearby that is open.","furigana":"ちず|ちか|えいぎょうちゅう|やっきょく|さが"}],"notes":"営業中 means \"open, in operation\" and typically appears on signs at shops and restaurants; the counterpart is 準備中 \"not yet open / being prepared\" or 本日休業. In a sentence it is used with です or の (営業中の店). For opening hours one says 営業時間, and for \"is open\" in conversation often 開いている. 営業 alone also means \"sales\" (営業部).","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 33"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"川崎駅の自転車置き場に自転車を停めています。","romaji":"Kawasaki-eki no jitensha okiba ni jitensha o tomete imasu.","german":"I park my bicycle at the bicycle parking lot at Kawasaki Station.","furigana":"かわさきえき|じてんしゃお|ば|じてんしゃ|と"},{"japanese":"荷物が多すぎて、置き場がない。","romaji":"Nimotsu ga oosugite, okiba ga nai.","german":"We have so much luggage that there is nowhere to put it.","furigana":"にもつ|おお|お|ば"},{"japanese":"ゴミ置き場は、マンションの入り口の横にあります。","romaji":"Gomi okiba wa, manshon no iriguchi no yoko ni arimasu.","german":"The garbage area is next to the entrance of the apartment building.","furigana":"お|ば|い|ぐち|よこ"}],"notes":"置き場 is a place where one puts or stores something, mostly in compounds: 自転車置き場, ゴミ置き場, 傘置き場, 資材置き場. Important expressions: 置き場に困る / 置き場がない \"not to know where to put it\". Figuratively, 身の置き場がない means \"to feel out of place\". The neutral general term for a storage room is 倉庫; 置き場 is more everyday and concrete.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 26"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あのがっしりした広い肩の男が、この国の副大統領です。","romaji":"Ano gasshiri shita hiroi kata no otoko ga, kono kuni no fuku daitōryō desu.","german":"That man with the broad, sturdy shoulders is this country's vice president.","furigana":"ひろ|かた|おとこ|くに|ふくだいとうりょう"},{"japanese":"大統領が病気のため、副大統領が代わりに会議に出席した。","romaji":"Daitouryou ga byouki no tame, fukudaitouryou ga kawari ni kaigi ni shusseki shita.","german":"Because the president was ill, the vice president attended the meeting in his place.","furigana":"だいとうりょう|びょうき|ふくだいとうりょう|か|かいぎ|しゅっせき"},{"japanese":"アメリカでは、大統領と副大統領は一緒に選挙で選ばれる。","romaji":"Amerika de wa, daitouryou to fukudaitouryou wa issho ni senkyo de erabareru.","german":"In the United States, the president and vice president are elected together.","furigana":"だいとうりょう|ふくだいとうりょう|いっしょ|せんきょ|えら"}],"notes":"副大統領 is the vice president of a state led by a 大統領 \"president\", such as the USA. The prefix 副〜 means \"deputy, vice-\": 副社長, 副会長, 副市長. For presidents of companies one says 社長, not 大統領. Typical: 副大統領に就任する \"to take office\", 副大統領候補.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この町には毎年多くの観光客が訪れます。","romaji":"Kono machi ni wa maitoshi ooku no kankoukyaku ga otozuremasu.","german":"Many tourists visit this town every year.","furigana":"まち|まいとしおお|かんこうきゃく|おとず"},{"japanese":"外国人観光客のために、駅に英語の案内を増やした。","romaji":"Gaikokujin kankoukyaku no tame ni, eki ni Eigo no annai o fuyashita.","german":"More English signs were added at the station for foreign tourists.","furigana":"がいこくじんかんこうきゃく|えき|えいご|あんない|ふ"},{"japanese":"観光客が多すぎて、ゆっくり写真が撮れなかった。","romaji":"Kankoukyaku ga oosugite, yukkuri shashin ga torenakatta.","german":"There were so many tourists that I couldn't take photos in peace.","furigana":"かんこうきゃく|おお|しゃしん|と"}],"notes":"観光客 are tourists, that is, visitors who come for sightseeing; 〜客 means \"guest, customer\" as in 乗客 \"passenger\". Typical: 観光客が訪れる, 観光客でにぎわう \"crowded with tourists\", 外国人観光客. 旅行者 is the more neutral \"traveler\" (also on business); 観光地 is the tourist destination. Too many tourists is called 観光公害 or オーバーツーリズム in the media.","levelNote":"Compound: 観光 (N4) + 客 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ヒジで突くの、やめてくれない？","romaji":"Hiji de tsuku no, yamete kurenai?","german":"Could you stop poking me with your elbow?","furigana":"つ"},{"japanese":"祖母は杖を突いてゆっくり歩いている。","romaji":"Sobo wa tsue o tsuite yukkuri aruite iru.","german":"My grandmother walks slowly with a cane.","furigana":"そぼ|つえ|つ|ある"},{"japanese":"彼の質問は、私の説明の弱いところを突いていた。","romaji":"Kare no shitsumon wa, watashi no setsumei no yowai tokoro o tsuite ita.","german":"His question hit the weak points of my explanation.","furigana":"かれ|しつもん|わたし|せつめい|よわ|つ"}],"notes":"突く is transitive and means \"to poke, to thrust, to prod\" with a body part or an object: ヒジで突く, 棒で突く. One also supports oneself with it: 杖を突く \"to walk with a cane\", 手を突く. Figuratively, 弱点を突く means \"to hit the weak point\". Distinctions: 刺す is \"to pierce with something pointed\", つつく \"to poke repeatedly\". つく is often written in kana; homophones are 付く, 着く, 点く.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"落ち葉を燃やして焼きイモを作ります。","romaji":"Ochiba o moyashite yakiimo o tsukurimasu.","german":"We burn fallen leaves and make roasted sweet potatoes.","furigana":"お|ば|も|や|つく"},{"japanese":"別れた恋人の手紙を全部燃やしてしまった。","romaji":"Wakareta koibito no tegami o zenbu moyashite shimatta.","german":"I burned all the letters from my ex-lover.","furigana":"わか|こいびと|てがみ|ぜんぶも"},{"japanese":"彼は夢に向かって情熱を燃やしている。","romaji":"Kare wa yume ni mukatte jounetsu o moyashite iru.","german":"He is burning with passion for his dream.","furigana":"かれ|ゆめ|む|じょうねつ|も"}],"notes":"燃やす is transitive \"to burn something\" (ゴミを燃やす, 手紙を燃やす); the intransitive counterpart is 燃える \"to burn\" (燃えるゴミ). Figuratively it means \"to kindle passion\": 情熱を燃やす, 闘志を燃やす. Distinction: 焼く means \"to fry, bake, grill\" or \"to burn down\" (家を焼く); 燃やす stresses the burning itself.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"えっ、この金額を全額現金で！？","romaji":"E, kono kingaku o zengaku genkin de!?","german":"What, this whole amount in cash!?"},{"japanese":"旅行がキャンセルになったので、代金を全額返してもらった。","romaji":"Ryokou ga kyanseru ni natta node, daikin o zengaku kaeshite moratta.","german":"Since the trip was canceled, I got the full price refunded.","furigana":"りょこう|だいきん|ぜんがくかえ"},{"japanese":"交通費は会社が全額出してくれます。","romaji":"Koutsuuhi wa kaisha ga zengaku dashite kuremasu.","german":"The company pays the transportation costs in full.","furigana":"こうつうひ|かいしゃ|ぜんがくだ"}],"notes":"全額 is the full amount. It is often used adverbially before the verb without a particle: 全額払う, 全額返す; likewise 全額返金 \"full refund\" and 全額負担 \"full coverage of costs\". Counterparts are 一部 \"a part\" and 半額 \"half price\". 総額, on the other hand, is the total of several items (総額で十万円).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コウイチの左腕には、トーフグのタトゥーがある。","romaji":"Kōichi no hidariude ni wa, Tōfugu no tatū ga aru.","german":"Koichi has a Tofugu tattoo on his left arm.","furigana":"ひだりうで"},{"japanese":"転んで左腕を骨折しました。","romaji":"Koronde hidariude o kossetsu shimashita.","german":"I fell and broke my left arm.","furigana":"ころ|ひだりうで|こっせつ"},{"japanese":"腕時計はいつも左腕につけています。","romaji":"Udedokei wa itsumo hidariude ni tsukete imasu.","german":"I always wear my wristwatch on my left arm.","furigana":"うでどけい|ひだりうで"}],"notes":"左腕 is read ひだりうで in everyday speech, \"left arm\"; the counterpart is 右腕. In baseball it is read さわん and denotes a left-handed pitcher (左腕投手). 右腕 also figuratively means \"right-hand man, closest confidant\": 社長の右腕. For watches or injuries one says quite normally 左腕に〜 or 左腕を〜.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本では、人を指差すのは失礼なんだよ。","romaji":"Nihon de wa, hito o yubisasu no wa shitsurei na n da yo.","german":"In Japan, it is rude to point at people.","furigana":"にほん|ひと|ゆびさ|しつれい"},{"japanese":"子どもが空を指差して「飛行機！」と叫んだ。","romaji":"Kodomo ga sora o yubisashite 'Hikouki!' to sakenda.","german":"A child pointed at the sky and shouted, \"Airplane!\"","furigana":"こ|そら|ゆびさ|ひこうき|さけ"},{"japanese":"駅員は時計を指差して、出発時刻を確認した。","romaji":"Ekiin wa tokei o yubisashite, shuppatsu jikoku o kakunin shita.","german":"The station attendant pointed at the clock and checked the departure time.","furigana":"えきいん|とけい|ゆびさ|しゅっぱつじこく|かくにん"}],"notes":"指差す means \"to point at something with a finger\" and is transitive: 〜を指差す. It is also written 指さす. More general is 指す \"to point, to indicate\" (also with clock hands: 時計の針が三時を指す). In Japan, it is considered impolite to point at people with a finger. Among railway staff, 指差し確認 \"point and call\" is well known as a safety check.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今朝、次世代燃料自動車に関するニュースを見ましたよ。","romaji":"Kesa, jisedai nenryou jidousha ni kansuru nyuusu o mimashita yo.","german":"This morning I saw news about next-generation fuel vehicles.","furigana":"けさ|じせだいねんりょうじどうしゃ|かん|み"},{"japanese":"燃料がもうすぐなくなりそうだから、次のガソリンスタンドに寄ろう。","romaji":"Nenryou ga mou sugu nakunarisou da kara, tsugi no gasorin sutando ni yorou.","german":"We're about to run out of fuel, so let's stop at the next gas station.","furigana":"ねんりょう|つぎ|よ"},{"japanese":"燃料代が上がって、飛行機のチケットも高くなった。","romaji":"Nenryoudai ga agatte, hikouki no chiketto mo takaku natta.","german":"Fuel costs went up, and airplane tickets became more expensive too.","furigana":"ねんりょうだい|あ|ひこうき|たか"}],"notes":"燃料 is generally \"fuel\" for vehicles, heating or power plants; ガソリン is specifically gasoline. Typical phrases: 燃料を補給する \"to refuel\", 燃料が切れる \"to run out of fuel\", 燃料費/燃料代 \"fuel costs\", 化石燃料 \"fossil fuels\". On flight tickets, 燃油サーチャージ \"fuel surcharge\" often appears.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"両腕にホクロがたくさんあります。","romaji":"Ryō ude ni hokuro ga takusan arimasu.","german":"I have a lot of moles on both arms.","furigana":"りょううで"},{"japanese":"彼女は両腕いっぱいに花束を抱えていた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa ryouude ippai ni hanataba o kakaete ita.","german":"She was carrying an armful of flowers in both arms.","furigana":"かのじょ|りょううで|はなたば|かか"},{"japanese":"両腕を上に伸ばして、深呼吸しましょう。","romaji":"Ryouude o ue ni nobashite, shinkokyuu shimashou.","german":"Stretch both arms upward and take a deep breath.","furigana":"りょううで|うえ|の|しんこきゅう"}],"notes":"両腕 means \"both arms\"; the prefix 両〜 \"both\" is known from 両手, 両足, 両親. Typical phrases: 両腕を広げる \"to spread one's arms\", 両腕で抱える \"to carry with both arms\", 両腕を組む \"to fold one's arms\". The counterpart is 片腕 \"one arm\", which figuratively also means \"right-hand man, closest associate\". 腕 is the whole arm from the shoulder to the wrist.","levelNote":"Compound: 腕 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ええっ、日本から、コンテナでウィスキーを輸送したんですか！？","romaji":"Ē, Nippon kara, kontena de wisukī o yusō shita n desu ka!?","german":"What, you shipped whiskey from Japan in a container?!"},{"japanese":"大雪のため、鉄道の輸送に大きな影響が出ています。","romaji":"Ooyuki no tame, tetsudou no yusou ni ookina eikyou ga dete imasu.","german":"Heavy snow is having a major impact on rail transport.","furigana":"おおゆき|てつどう|ゆそう|おお|えいきょう|で"},{"japanese":"この荷物は船で輸送するので、一か月ぐらいかかります。","romaji":"Kono nimotsu wa fune de yusou suru node, ikkagetsu gurai kakarimasu.","german":"This package is going by ship, so it will take about a month.","furigana":"にもつ|ふね|ゆそう|いっ|げつ"}],"notes":"輸送 is the \"transport\" of goods or people by vehicle, ship or aircraft, usually on a larger scale; as a transitive する-verb: X を輸送する. Typical are トラック輸送, 航空輸送, 輸送費 and 輸送に影響が出る. In everyday speech people simply say 運ぶ; 運送 emphasizes commercial carriage (運送会社 \"freight company\"). The word sounds matter-of-fact and is typical of news and logistics.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この品物には、値札シールがありません。","romaji":"Kono shinamono ni wa, nefuda shīru ga arimasen.","german":"This item has no price sticker.","furigana":"しなもの|ねふだ"},{"japanese":"プレゼントなので、値札を取ってもらえますか。","romaji":"Purezento na node, nefuda o totte moraemasu ka.","german":"It's a present, so could you take off the price tag?","furigana":"ねふだ|と"},{"japanese":"値札を見たら、思っていたより高くて驚いた。","romaji":"Nefuda o mitara, omotte ita yori takakute odoroita.","german":"When I looked at the price tag, I was surprised that it was more expensive than I had thought.","furigana":"ねふだ|み|おも|たか|おどろ"}],"notes":"値札 is the price tag on goods; 値 \"price, value\" + 札 \"tag, slip\" (read ふだ). Typical phrases: 値札が付いている, 値札を外す/取る \"to remove the price tag\" (e.g. for gifts), 値札を見る. The price itself is called 値段 or 価格; 値札シール is a price sticker.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いやいや、半袖はまだ早すぎるでしょ。寒くないの？","romaji":"Iya iya, hansode wa mada hayasugiru desho. Samukunai no?","german":"No, no, short sleeves are way too early. Aren't you cold?","furigana":"はんそで|はや|さむ"},{"japanese":"夏の制服は半袖のシャツです。","romaji":"Natsu no seifuku wa hansode no shatsu desu.","german":"The summer uniform is a short-sleeved shirt.","furigana":"なつ|せいふく|はんそで"},{"japanese":"暑くなってきたので、半袖に着替えた。","romaji":"Atsuku natte kita node, hansode ni kigaeta.","german":"It was getting hot, so I changed into short sleeves.","furigana":"あつ|はんそで|きが"}],"notes":"半袖 denotes short sleeves and is also short for a short-sleeved top: 半袖を着る, 半袖に着替える. Before nouns with の: 半袖のシャツ, 半袖のTシャツ. The counterpart is 長袖; sleeveless is ノースリーブ. Related: 袖 \"sleeve\", 七分袖 \"three-quarter sleeve\".","levelNote":"Compound: 袖 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日は、長袖着ていった方がいいかな？","romaji":"Kyō wa, nagasode kite itta hō ga ii ka na?","german":"Should I wear long sleeves today?","furigana":"きょう|ながそでき|ほう"},{"japanese":"山の上は寒いので、長袖を一枚持っていってください。","romaji":"Yama no ue wa samui node, nagasode o ichimai motte itte kudasai.","german":"It's cold at the top of the mountain, so please bring one long-sleeved top.","furigana":"やま|うえ|さむ|ながそで|いちまいも"},{"japanese":"日焼けしないように、夏でも長袖のシャツを着ています。","romaji":"Hiyake shinai you ni, natsu demo nagasode no shatsu o kite imasu.","german":"To avoid getting sunburned, I wear long-sleeved shirts even in summer.","furigana":"ひや|なつ|ながそで|き"}],"notes":"長袖 means long sleeves and often also stands for a long-sleeved top: 長袖を着る, 長袖一枚. Before nouns with の: 長袖のシャツ. The counterpart is 半袖. Typical contexts are weather and seasons (朝晩は長袖が必要) as well as sun or mosquito protection in summer.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今、公用でパリにきています。","romaji":"Ima, kōyō de Pari ni kite imasu.","german":"I am in Paris on official business right now.","furigana":"いま|こうよう"},{"japanese":"公用車を私用で使ってはいけない。","romaji":"Kouyousha o shiyou de tsukatte wa ikenai.","german":"You must not use an official car for private purposes.","furigana":"こうようしゃ|しよう|つか"},{"japanese":"この国の公用語は英語とフランス語です。","romaji":"Kono kuni no kouyougo wa Eigo to Furansugo desu.","german":"The official languages of this country are English and French.","furigana":"くに|こうようご|えいご|ご"}],"notes":"公用 means \"official or governmental business\" (公用で出張する) and \"public/official use\" in compounds: 公用車 \"official car\", 公用語 \"official language\". The opposite is 私用 \"private\" (私用で使う). For companies one rather says 社用 or 仕事で; 公用 sounds like a government office or an official function.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この町では、校区が三区分に分けられています。","romaji":"Kono machi de wa, kouku ga san kubun ni wakerarete imasu.","german":"In this town, the school district is divided into three sections.","furigana":"まち|こうく|さんくぶん|わ"},{"japanese":"年齢によって、料金の区分が違います。","romaji":"Nenrei ni yotte, ryoukin no kubun ga chigaimasu.","german":"Fee categories differ depending on age.","furigana":"ねんれい|りょうきん|くぶん|ちが"},{"japanese":"書類を内容ごとに区分して、ファイルにしまった。","romaji":"Shorui o naiyou goto ni kubun shite, fairu ni shimatta.","german":"I sorted the documents by content and put them in files.","furigana":"しょるい|ないよう|くぶん"}],"notes":"区分 is the division into sections or categories according to fixed criteria; as a する-verb it is transitive: 〜を区分する. Typical in administration and fee schedules: 料金区分, 年齢区分, 区分けする (more everyday). To distinguish: 区別 is telling two things apart (公私を区別する), 分類 is systematic classification, e.g. in biology.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ビエトがやばい組織と係わっているのは知っているけど、その犯罪に係わっていたとはやっぱり思えないんだよな。","romaji":"Bieto ga yabai soshiki to kakawatte iru no wa shitte iru kedo, sono hanzai ni kakawatte ita to wa yappari omoenai n da yo na.","german":"I know Viet is involved with a shady organization, but I still can't believe he was involved in that crime.","furigana":"そしき|かか|し|はんざい|かか|おも"},{"japanese":"命に係わる病気ではないので、安心してください。","romaji":"Inochi ni kakawaru byouki de wa nai node, anshin shite kudasai.","german":"It's not a life-threatening illness, so please don't worry.","furigana":"いのち|かか|びょうき|あんしん"},{"japanese":"あの人とはあまり係わらないほうがいいよ。","romaji":"Ano hito to wa amari kakawaranai hou ga ii yo.","german":"You'd better not get involved with that person.","furigana":"ひと|かか"}],"notes":"係わる is a variant spelling of 関わる (today the usual spelling) and is intransitive. 〜に係わる means \"to be involved in something\" (事件に係わる) or \"to affect something\" (命に係わる \"life-threatening\"); 〜と係わる means \"to have dealings with someone\". Often negatively tinged: あの人と係わらないほうがいい. There is no direct transitive counterpart; similar is 関係する.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あそこには新しいビルが建つ予定です。","romaji":"Asoko ni wa atarashii biru ga tatsu yotei desu.","german":"A new building is planned to be built there.","furigana":"あたら|た|よてい"},{"japanese":"駅前に大きなマンションが建った。","romaji":"Ekimae ni ookina manshon ga tatta.","german":"A large apartment building was built in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|おお|た"},{"japanese":"この家は建ってから五十年になります。","romaji":"Kono ie wa tatte kara gojuunen ni narimasu.","german":"It has been fifty years since this house was built.","furigana":"いえ|た|ごじゅうねん"}],"notes":"建つ is intransitive and describes a building being erected or standing: ビルが建つ, 家が建った. The transitive partner is 建てる (\"to build\"): 家を建てる. Not to be confused with the homophone 立つ (\"to stand, to stand up\"), which is used for people, things or smoke - 建つ applies only to structures such as houses, towers or monuments. With 〜てから it expresses how long a building has been standing.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"こんなステキなチームで試合ができて光栄です。","romaji":"Konna suteki na chīmu de shiai ga dekite kōei desu.","german":"It is an honor to play in such a wonderful team.","furigana":"しあい|こうえい"},{"japanese":"お会いできて光栄です。","romaji":"O-ai dekite kouei desu.","german":"It is an honor to meet you.","furigana":"あ|こうえい"},{"japanese":"このような賞をいただき、大変光栄に思います。","romaji":"Kono you na shou o itadaki, taihen kouei ni omoimasu.","german":"I feel greatly honored to receive such an award.","furigana":"しょう|たいへんこうえい|おも"}],"notes":"光栄 expresses that one feels honored and appears almost always in polite phrases: 〜て光栄です (\"it is an honor to ...\"), 光栄に思います (\"I feel honored\") or, formally, 身に余る光栄 (\"an honor I hardly deserve\"). It refers to one's own joy at an award or encounter, whereas 名誉 is honor in the sense of prestige and good reputation (名誉を守る). Grammatically it is a な-adjective or noun; typical in speeches, interviews and greetings.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私なりの防火の心得を文章にまとめました。","romaji":"Watashi nari no bōka no kokoroe o bunshō ni matomemashita.","german":"I put my own fire safety tips into writing.","furigana":"わたし|ぼうか|こころえ|ぶんしょう"},{"japanese":"この建物には防火扉が付いています。","romaji":"Kono tatemono ni wa boukatobira ga tsuite imasu.","german":"This building is equipped with fire doors.","furigana":"たてもの|ぼうかとびら|つ"},{"japanese":"防火のため、寝る前にストーブを必ず消しましょう。","romaji":"Bouka no tame, neru mae ni sutoobu o kanarazu keshimashou.","german":"For fire prevention, be sure to turn off the heater before going to bed.","furigana":"ぼうか|ね|まえ|かなら|け"}],"notes":"防火 means \"fire protection, fire prevention\" and appears mainly in compounds: 防火扉 (\"fire door\"), 防火壁 (\"firewall (wall)\"), 防火対策 (\"fire prevention measures\"), 防火訓練 (\"fire drill\"). It is about preventing fires, in contrast to 消火 (\"extinguishing a fire\"). The more general term for disaster prevention is 防災. The word sounds rather matter-of-fact and official.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"配達が完了したら、配達先から受領書を忘れずに受け取ってください。","romaji":"Haitatsu ga kanryō shitara, haitatsusaki kara juryōsho o wasurezu ni uketotte kudasai.","german":"When the delivery is complete, please remember to get a receipt from the recipient.","furigana":"はいたつ|かんりょう|はいたつさき|じゅりょうしょ|わす|う|と"},{"japanese":"受領書にサインをお願いします。","romaji":"Juryousho ni sain o onegai shimasu.","german":"Please sign the acknowledgment of receipt.","furigana":"じゅりょうしょ|ねが"},{"japanese":"書類を提出したら、受領書をもらっておいたほうがいいですよ。","romaji":"Shorui o teishutsu shitara, juryousho o moratte oita hou ga ii desu yo.","german":"When you submit documents, you should get an acknowledgment of receipt.","furigana":"しょるい|ていしゅつ|じゅりょうしょ"}],"notes":"受領書 is a business document confirming that goods or documents have arrived; 受領 is the formal word for \"receipt\". Typical are 受領書にサインする, 受領書を受け取る or 受領書を発行する. Not to be confused with 領収書, the receipt for a payment, or the till receipt レシート. In everyday speech one rather says 受け取り; 受領書 sounds written and businesslike.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"やっぱり、日本語の勉強はトーフグに限るよ。","romaji":"Yappari, nihongo no benkyou wa Toufugu ni kagiru yo.","german":"As expected, there's nothing like Tofugu for studying Japanese.","furigana":"にほんご|べんきょう|かぎ"},{"japanese":"参加者は二十人に限ります。","romaji":"Sankasha wa nijuunin ni kagirimasu.","german":"Participants are limited to twenty.","furigana":"さんかしゃ|にじゅうにん|かぎ"},{"japanese":"今日に限って、傘を忘れてしまった。","romaji":"Kyou ni kagitte, kasa o wasurete shimatta.","german":"Of all days, I forgot my umbrella today.","furigana":"きょう|かぎ|かさ|わす"}],"notes":"限る has three important uses: transitive \"to limit\" (人数を限る, often passive: 限られた時間), with に \"to be restricted to something\" (参加者は二十人に限る) and colloquially 〜に限る \"there's nothing like ...\" (夏はビールに限る). In addition there are fixed patterns: 〜に限って \"of all things\", 〜とは限らない \"not necessarily\" and 〜限り \"as long as, as far as\". Close in meaning is 制限する, which sounds more formal and technical.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"若者には、無限の可能性がある。","romaji":"Wakamono ni wa, mugen no kanōsei ga aru.","german":"Young people have unlimited potential.","furigana":"わかもの|むげん|かのうせい"},{"japanese":"宇宙は無限に広がっているのだろうか。","romaji":"Uchuu wa mugen ni hirogatte iru no darou ka.","german":"I wonder whether the universe expands infinitely.","furigana":"うちゅう|むげん|ひろ"},{"japanese":"時間は無限にあるわけではないから、計画的に使おう。","romaji":"Jikan wa mugen ni aru wake de wa nai kara, keikakuteki ni tsukaou.","german":"Time is not unlimited, so let's use it wisely.","furigana":"じかん|むげん|けいかくてき|つか"}],"notes":"無限 means \"infinite, boundless\" and is mostly used attributively with の (無限の可能性, 無限の宇宙) or adverbially as 無限に (無限に広がる). The form 無限な is rare. The opposite is 有限 (\"finite, limited\"). Similar, but more emotional and literary, is 限りない; 無制限, by contrast, means \"without restriction\" in the sense of rules (無制限に使える).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ガラス製のフグの置物が、テーブルから落ちて割れてしまった。","romaji":"Garasusei no fugu no okimono ga, tēburu kara ochite warete shimatta.","german":"The glass pufferfish figurine fell off the table and broke.","furigana":"せい|おきもの|お|わ"},{"japanese":"卵が割れないように、袋の一番上に入れてください。","romaji":"Tamago ga warenai you ni, fukuro no ichiban ue ni irete kudasai.","german":"Please put the eggs at the very top of the bag so that they don't break.","furigana":"たまご|わ|ふくろ|いちばんうえ|い"},{"japanese":"その問題について、クラスの意見が二つに割れました。","romaji":"Sono mondai ni tsuite, kurasu no iken ga futatsu ni waremashita.","german":"On that issue, the class's opinion split in two.","furigana":"もんだい|いけん|ふた|わ"}],"notes":"割れる is intransitive: something breaks or gets a crack, typically glass, plates, eggs or ice. The transitive counterpart is 割る \"to break, to crack (an egg)\". Figuratively, opinions or votes also split: 意見が割れる. To be distinguished are 壊れる (general \"to break down\", also for devices) and 折れる (for long things such as sticks or bones).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"明日は遠足だから、お弁当を準備しなくちゃ。","romaji":"Ashita wa ensoku da kara, obentou o junbi shinakucha.","german":"Tomorrow is a field trip, so I have to prepare a bento.","furigana":"あした|えんそく|べんとう|じゅんび"},{"japanese":"旅行の準備はもうできましたか。","romaji":"Ryokou no junbi wa mou dekimashita ka.","german":"Have you finished preparing for the trip yet?","furigana":"りょこう|じゅんび"},{"japanese":"会議の前に資料を準備しておきます。","romaji":"Kaigi no mae ni shiryou o junbi shite okimasu.","german":"I will prepare the materials before the meeting.","furigana":"かいぎ|まえ|しりょう|じゅんび"}],"notes":"準備する means \"to prepare, to get ready\" and is transitive: 資料を準備する. The form ～の準備をする is very common: 旅行の準備をする \"to prepare for the trip\". \"To be ready\" is 準備ができる: 準備ができました \"Everything is ready\". Closely related is 用意する, which emphasizes more the providing of concrete things, whereas 準備 also includes planning and organization.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 21"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ワサビは抜いてもらえますか？","romaji":"Wasabi wa nuite moraemasu ka?","german":"Could you leave out the wasabi?","furigana":"ぬ"},{"japanese":"歯医者で歯を一本抜いてもらいました。","romaji":"Haisha de ha o ippon nuite moraimashita.","german":"I had a tooth pulled at the dentist.","furigana":"はいしゃ|は|いっぽんぬ"},{"japanese":"前の車を抜くときは、十分に注意してください。","romaji":"Mae no kuruma o nuku toki wa, juubun ni chuui shite kudasai.","german":"Please be extremely careful when overtaking the car ahead.","furigana":"まえ|くるま|ぬ|じゅうぶん|ちゅうい"}],"notes":"The basic meaning of 抜く is \"to pull out\": 歯を抜く, 草を抜く, 栓を抜く. From this follow \"to leave out\" (朝ご飯を抜く, ワサビを抜く; 手を抜く \"to slack off\") and \"to overtake\" (前の車を抜く, intensified 追い抜く). After a verb stem it means \"to carry through to the end\": やり抜く, 走り抜く. The intransitive partner is 抜ける (\"to come out, to fall out\"): 歯が抜ける, 力が抜ける.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"うちの会社のアルバイトが人をなぐって捕まってしまったんだ。","romaji":"Uchi no kaisha no arubaito ga hito o nagutte tsukamatte shimatta n da.","german":"A part-timer at our company hit someone and got arrested.","furigana":"かいしゃ|ひと|つか"},{"japanese":"揺れるので、つり革にしっかりつかまってください。","romaji":"Yureru node, tsurikawa ni shikkari tsukamatte kudasai.","german":"It will shake, so please hold on firmly to the strap.","furigana":"ゆ|かわ"},{"japanese":"雨の日は、なかなかタクシーがつかまらない。","romaji":"Ame no hi wa, nakanaka takushii ga tsukamaranai.","german":"On rainy days, it's hard to get a taxi.","furigana":"あめ|ひ"}],"notes":"捕まる is intransitive: 犯人が警察に捕まる (\"the culprit is caught by the police\"), and the agent takes に. Its transitive partner is 捕まえる (\"to catch, to arrest\"); more formal is 逮捕される. In the meanings \"to hold on to\" (つり革につかまる) and \"to catch, to get hold of\" (タクシーがつかまる, 電話で人がつかまらない), it is mostly written in kana. Colloquially it also means \"to be held up by someone\": 先輩につかまって帰れなかった.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 21"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"おじいちゃんが、模型の飛行機を作ってくれたんだ。","romaji":"Ojiichan ga, mokei no hikōki o tsukutte kureta n da.","german":"My grandfather made me a model airplane.","furigana":"もけい|ひこうき|つく"},{"japanese":"博物館に昔の町の模型が展示されています。","romaji":"Hakubutsukan ni mukashi no machi no mokei ga tenji sarete imasu.","german":"A model of the old town is on display at the museum.","furigana":"はくぶつかん|むかし|まち|もけい|てんじ"},{"japanese":"兄は鉄道模型を集めるのが趣味です。","romaji":"Ani wa tetsudou mokei o atsumeru no ga shumi desu.","german":"My older brother's hobby is collecting model trains.","furigana":"あに|てつどうもけい|あつ|しゅみ"}],"notes":"模型 is a noun for a replica, usually scaled down: 模型飛行機 or 模型の飛行機, 鉄道模型 (\"model railway\"), 建築模型 (\"architectural model\"). Unlike the loanword モデル, which also means \"fashion model\", \"role model\" or \"product model\", 模型 always means a physical object. A plastic kit is colloquially called プラモデル.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"賞与金の話を聞いて、急に勉強する気になりました。","romaji":"Shōyokin no hanashi o kiite, kyū ni benkyō suru ki ni narimashita.","german":"After hearing about the bonus, I suddenly felt like studying.","furigana":"しょうよきん|はなし|き|きゅう|べんきょう|き"},{"japanese":"賞与金は年に二回、六月と十二月に支払われます。","romaji":"Shouyokin wa nen ni nikai, rokugatsu to juunigatsu ni shiharawaremasu.","german":"The bonus is paid twice a year, in June and December.","furigana":"しょうよきん|ねん|にかい|ろくがつ|じゅうにがつ|しはら"},{"japanese":"今年は会社の業績が悪く、賞与金が出ないそうだ。","romaji":"Kotoshi wa kaisha no gyouseki ga waruku, shouyokin ga denai sou da.","german":"The company is doing badly this year, so apparently there will be no bonus.","furigana":"ことし|かいしゃ|ぎょうせき|わる|しょうよきん|で"}],"notes":"賞与金 is the formal expression for the bonus payment, which in Japan is usually paid twice a year (summer and winter). In contracts and on pay slips it is usually shortened to 賞与; in everyday speech one almost always says ボーナス. Not to be confused with 賞金, the prize money in competitions. Typical are 賞与金が出る (\"there is a bonus\") and 賞与金を支給する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"言葉の使い方によって、政治的な含意が生じることがある。","romaji":"Kotoba no tsukaikata ni yotte, seijiteki na gan'i ga shoujiru koto ga aru.","german":"Depending on how words are used, political implications can arise.","furigana":"ことば|つか|かた|せいじてき|がんい|しょう"},{"japanese":"この文章の含意を読み取るのは難しい。","romaji":"Kono bunshou no gan'i o yomitoru no wa muzukashii.","german":"It is difficult to read the implications of this text.","furigana":"ぶんしょう|がんい|よ|と|むずか"},{"japanese":"「検討します」という言葉には、断りの含意があることも多い。","romaji":"\"Kentou shimasu\" to iu kotoba ni wa, kotowari no gan'i ga aru koto mo ooi.","german":"The phrase \"I'll consider it\" often carries the implication of a refusal.","furigana":"けんとう|ことば|ことわ|がんい|おお"}],"notes":"含意 is a literary, rather academic word for the underlying, unspoken meaning of a statement: 政治的な含意, 言葉の含意を読み取る. In everyday speech people say ニュアンス, 言外の意味 or simply 意味 instead. 暗示, by contrast, emphasizes deliberate hinting. As a する-verb (含意する \"to imply\") it occurs mainly in logic and linguistics.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今回の選挙でも投票するつもり？","romaji":"Konkai no senkyo de mo tōhyō suru tsumori?","german":"Hast du vor, auch bei dieser Wahl abzustimmen?","furigana":"こんかい|せんきょ|とうひょう"},{"japanese":"日本では十八歳から投票することができます。","romaji":"Nihon de wa juuhassai kara touhyou suru koto ga dekimasu.","german":"In Japan darf man ab achtzehn wählen.","furigana":"にほん|じゅうはっさい|とうひょう"},{"japanese":"クラスの代表を投票で決めましょう。","romaji":"Kurasu no daihyou o touhyou de kimemashou.","german":"Lasst uns den Klassensprecher per Abstimmung bestimmen.","furigana":"だいひょう|とうひょう|き"}],"notes":"投票する heißt „abstimmen, seine Stimme abgeben“. Wofür man stimmt, steht mit に: 候補者に投票する, 賛成に投票する; der Anlass mit で: 選挙で投票する. Zur Wahl gehen heißt 投票に行く, die Wahlbeteiligung 投票率. 選挙 ist die Wahl als Ereignis, 選ぶ das allgemeine „auswählen“ – deutsches „wählen“ im politischen Sinn entspricht meist 投票する.","levelNote":"Derived from 投票 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"毎日のように見ている光景なのに、それまで全く気づかなかったよ。","romaji":"Mainichi no yō ni mite iru kōkei na noni, sore made mattaku kizukanakatta yo.","german":"It's a sight I see almost every day, yet I never noticed it until then.","furigana":"まいにち|み|こうけい|まった|き"},{"japanese":"山の上から見た朝日の光景は、一生忘れられない。","romaji":"Yama no ue kara mita asahi no koukei wa, isshou wasurerarenai.","german":"I'll never forget the sight of the sunrise seen from the top of the mountain.","furigana":"やま|うえ|み|あさひ|こうけい|いっしょうわす"},{"japanese":"事故現場の光景を見て、言葉を失った。","romaji":"Jiko genba no koukei o mite, kotoba o ushinatta.","german":"Seeing the scene of the accident, I was at a loss for words.","furigana":"じこげんば|こうけい|み|ことば|うしな"}],"notes":"光景 is a scene that one sees with one's own eyes, often with people and events in it: 信じられない光景, ほほえましい光景, 事故の光景. 景色 and 風景, on the other hand, denote mainly landscapes and views. 光景 can be beautiful as well as frightening and is often used with 目にする or 目に浮かぶ (\"to come before one's eyes\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"全ての候補者がメガネを掛けていた。","romaji":"Subete no kōhosha ga megane o kakete ita.","german":"All the candidates were wearing glasses.","furigana":"すべ|こうほしゃ|か"},{"japanese":"選挙の前に、候補者の演説を聞きに行った。","romaji":"Senkyo no mae ni, kouhosha no enzetsu o kiki ni itta.","german":"Before the election, I went to hear a candidate's speech.","furigana":"せんきょ|まえ|こうほしゃ|えんぜつ|き|い"},{"japanese":"面接に来た候補者の中から、一人を選ばなければならない。","romaji":"Mensetsu ni kita kouhosha no naka kara, hitori o erabanakereba naranai.","german":"We have to choose one from among the candidates who came to the interview.","furigana":"めんせつ|き|こうほしゃ|なか|ひとり|えら"}],"notes":"候補者 is a person who is up for selection - above all in elections, but also on the shortlist for filling a position. 候補 alone can also mean things (候補地 \"candidate site\"). To run as a candidate is 立候補する. For applicants who apply for a job one mostly says 応募者; 候補者 sounds like a preselection.","levelNote":"Compound: 候補 (N3) + 者 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"時候のあいさつは月によって変わります。","romaji":"Jikou no aisatsu wa tsuki ni yotte kawarimasu.","german":"The seasonal greeting changes depending on the month.","furigana":"じこう|つき|か"},{"japanese":"手紙の初めに時候のあいさつを書くのが日本の習慣です。","romaji":"Tegami no hajime ni jikou no aisatsu o kaku no ga Nihon no shuukan desu.","german":"It is a Japanese custom to write a seasonal greeting at the start of a letter.","furigana":"てがみ|はじ|じこう|か|にほん|しゅうかん"},{"japanese":"ビジネスメールでは、時候のあいさつを省略することも多い。","romaji":"Bijinesu meeru de wa, jikou no aisatsu o shouryaku suru koto mo ooi.","german":"In business emails, the seasonal greeting is often omitted.","furigana":"じこう|しょうりゃく|おお"}],"notes":"時候 is an elevated word for \"season, seasonal weather\" and today occurs almost only in 時候のあいさつ: the seasonal greeting at the start of a formal letter, such as 初夏の候 (\"in the season of early summer\"). In everyday speech people say 季節. In business emails this greeting is often shortened or left out entirely.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"最近仕事が忙しくて、全然子供に構ってあげられてないんだよね。","romaji":"Saikin shigoto ga isogashikute, zenzen kodomo ni kamatte agerarete nai n da yo ne.","german":"Lately I'm so busy with work that I can't give my children any attention at all.","furigana":"さいきんしごと|いそが|ぜんぜんこども|かま"},{"japanese":"ここでたばこを吸ってもかまいませんか。","romaji":"Koko de tabako o sutte mo kamaimasen ka.","german":"Do you mind if I smoke here?","furigana":"す"},{"japanese":"私のことは構わないで、先に行ってください。","romaji":"Watashi no koto wa kamawanaide, saki ni itte kudasai.","german":"Don't worry about me, please go ahead.","furigana":"わたし|かま|さき|い"}],"notes":"構う means \"to care about something, to mind\" and is often written in kana. It is most often encountered in the negative: ～てもかまいません \"you may ..., it doesn't matter\" and simply かまいません \"no problem\". 構わないで(ください) means \"don't worry about me, don't go to any trouble\". In the meaning \"to pay attention to someone\", the person takes を or に: 子供に構ってあげる; 構ってほしい means \"to want attention\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の妻は、小さな花の模様が好きです。","romaji":"Watashi no tsuma wa, chiisana hana no moyō ga suki desu.","german":"My wife likes patterns with small flowers.","furigana":"わたし|つま|ちい|はな|もよう|す"},{"japanese":"天気予報によると、明日は雨模様だそうです。","romaji":"Tenki yohou ni yoru to, ashita wa amemoyou da sou desu.","german":"According to the weather forecast, it looks like rain tomorrow.","furigana":"てんきよほう|あした|あめもよう"},{"japanese":"事故の模様がニュースで伝えられた。","romaji":"Jiko no moyou ga nyuusu de tsutaerareta.","german":"The details of the accident were reported on the news.","furigana":"じこ|もよう|つた"}],"notes":"模様 first means a \"pattern\" on fabric or dishes: 花の模様, 水玉模様 (\"polka-dot pattern\"). It also refers to the \"state, course\" of an event (事故の模様) and, in news reports, to \"appearance, signs that something seems so\": 〜した模様です (\"it appears that ... has ...\"). In weather words it expresses a tendency: 雨模様 (\"it looks like rain\"). For the pattern of clothes and fabrics, 柄 is also used.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"なぜかスーパーの鮮魚コーナーに列ができていました。","romaji":"Naze ka suupaa no sengyo koonaa ni retsu ga dekite imashita.","german":"For some reason, a line had formed at the fresh fish counter of the supermarket.","furigana":"せんぎょ|れつ"},{"japanese":"この店では毎朝、港から届いた鮮魚を売っています。","romaji":"Kono mise de wa maiasa, minato kara todoita sengyo o utte imasu.","german":"This shop sells fresh fish brought in from the harbor every morning.","furigana":"みせ|まいあさ|みなと|とど|せんぎょ|う"},{"japanese":"鮮魚は買ったその日のうちに食べたほうがいい。","romaji":"Sengyo wa katta sono hi no uchi ni tabeta hou ga ii.","german":"Fresh fish is best eaten on the day you buy it.","furigana":"せんぎょ|か|ひ|た"}],"notes":"鮮魚 is a trade word for \"fresh fish\" and is found mainly on signs and in advertising: 鮮魚コーナー, 鮮魚店. In conversation, people more often say 新鮮な魚 or 生の魚. It refers to raw, unprocessed fish, not cooked or dried fish (干物).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"やっと薬が効いてきた。","romaji":"Yatto kusuri ga kiite kita.","german":"The medicine has finally started to work.","furigana":"くすり|き"},{"japanese":"この薬は頭痛によく効きます。","romaji":"Kono kusuri wa zutsuu ni yoku kikimasu.","german":"This medicine works well against headaches.","furigana":"くすり|ずつう|き"},{"japanese":"この部屋はエアコンがあまり効かない。","romaji":"Kono heya wa eakon ga amari kikanai.","german":"The air conditioner in this room doesn't work very well.","furigana":"へや|き"}],"notes":"効く means \"to be effective, to help\" and is intransitive: 薬が効く. What it works against is marked with に (頭痛に効く). It is also used for devices whose effect you can feel: エアコンが効く, 冷房が効きすぎる. The homophone 利く means \"to function\" in the sense of ability: 気が利く (\"to be attentive\"), ブレーキが利く; in everyday writing the two spellings are often mixed up. Do not confuse it with 聞く (\"to hear\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は芸能事務所には所属していません。","romaji":"Watashi wa geinō jimusho ni wa shozoku shite imasen.","german":"I do not belong to an entertainment agency.","furigana":"わたし|げいのうじむしょ|しょぞく"},{"japanese":"高校ではサッカー部に所属していました。","romaji":"Koukou de wa sakkaa-bu ni shozoku shite imashita.","german":"In high school, I belonged to the soccer club.","furigana":"こうこう|ぶ|しょぞく"},{"japanese":"名刺には名前と所属が書いてあります。","romaji":"Meishi ni wa namae to shozoku ga kaite arimasu.","german":"Your name and affiliation are written on the business card.","furigana":"めいし|なまえ|しょぞく|か"}],"notes":"所属 means \"affiliation\" with an organization and, as a する-verb, is mostly used in the progressive form: 〜に所属している (\"belongs to ..., is a member of ...\"). As a noun, it stands for the department or organization one belongs to: 所属部署, 名前と所属, politely ご所属. 属する is more general and more literary (哺乳類に属する), 入る emphasizes joining.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"モデルみたいに細い人より、豊満な体つきの女性の方がタイプです。","romaji":"Moderu mitai ni hosoi hito yori, hōman na karadatsuki no josei no hō ga taipu desu.","german":"I prefer women with a full-figured body over thin ones like models.","furigana":"ほそ|ひと|ほうまん|からだ|じょせい|ほう"},{"japanese":"絵の中の女性は、豊満な体つきで描かれている。","romaji":"E no naka no josei wa, houman na karadatsuki de egakarete iru.","german":"The woman in the painting is depicted with a voluptuous figure.","furigana":"え|なか|じょせい|ほうまん|からだ|えが"}],"notes":"豊満 describes a full, voluptuous body, almost always a woman's: 豊満な体つき, 豊満な胸. The word has a sensual overtone and is rather literary; in conversation it can sound suggestive. Neutral and casual is ぽっちゃり (\"plump\"); 太っている (\"fat\") is judgmental. Grammatically it is a な-adjective.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"満月の夜は、外が明るくて電気がなくても歩けます。","romaji":"Mangetsu no yoru wa, soto ga akarukute denki ga nakute mo arukemasu.","german":"On a full-moon night, it is bright outside and you can walk even without lights.","furigana":"まんげつ|よる|そと|あか|でんき|ある"},{"japanese":"今夜は満月だから、お月見をしよう。","romaji":"Kon'ya wa mangetsu da kara, otsukimi o shiyou.","german":"Tonight is a full moon, so let's do moon viewing.","furigana":"こんや|まんげつ|つきみ"},{"japanese":"満月まであと三日です。","romaji":"Mangetsu made ato mikka desu.","german":"Three more days until the full moon.","furigana":"まんげつ|みっか"}],"notes":"満月 is the full moon, a noun that takes の attributively: 満月の夜. The other moon phases are 新月 (\"new moon\"), 半月 (\"half moon\") and 三日月 (\"crescent moon\"). For the traditional moon viewing in autumn (お月見), one speaks of 十五夜 or 中秋の名月. At full moon the sky is bright, so fewer stars are visible then.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"できれば満員のバスには乗りたくない。","romaji":"Dekireba man'in no basu ni wa noritaku nai.","german":"If possible, I don't want to ride a packed bus.","furigana":"まんいん|の"},{"japanese":"朝の電車はいつも満員です。","romaji":"Asa no densha wa itsumo man'in desu.","german":"The morning train is always packed.","furigana":"あさ|でんしゃ|まんいん"},{"japanese":"コンサート会場は満員で、入れない人もいた。","romaji":"Konsaato kaijou wa man'in de, hairenai hito mo ita.","german":"The concert hall was full, and some people could not get in.","furigana":"かいじょう|まんいん|はい|ひと"}],"notes":"満員 means that a place or vehicle is filled with people to its capacity limit: 満員電車, 満員のバス, 会場は満員だ. 満員御礼 at sumo and in theaters means \"sold out\". For seats in a restaurant or aircraft, 満席 is more usual; for things and containers, いっぱい. 混む only means \"to be crowded, busy\", not necessarily full to the last place.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"満点をとったら、先生に花丸をもらえるの。","romaji":"Manten o tottara, sensei ni hana maru o moraeru no.","german":"If I get full marks, the teacher gives me a flower stamp.","furigana":"まんてん|せんせい|はなまる"},{"japanese":"このホテルはサービスも景色も満点だった。","romaji":"Kono hoteru wa saabisu mo keshiki mo manten datta.","german":"This hotel was perfect in both service and scenery.","furigana":"けしき|まんてん"},{"japanese":"栄養満点の朝ご飯を作りました。","romaji":"Eiyou manten no asagohan o tsukurimashita.","german":"I made a breakfast packed with nutrition.","furigana":"えいようまんてん|あさ|はん|つく"}],"notes":"満点 is the full score: 満点を取る, 百点満点 (\"100 out of 100 points\"); the opposite is 零点. Figuratively it means \"perfect, completely\" and is attached to nouns: 栄養満点 (\"extremely nutritious\"), 迫力満点 (\"full of power\"), サービス満点. 花丸 is the flower-shaped circle that Japanese teachers draw on especially good work.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"まずは自分の肥満度をチェックしてみましょう！","romaji":"Mazu wa jibun no himando o chekku shite mimashou!","german":"First, let's check your own degree of obesity!","furigana":"じぶん|ひまんど"},{"japanese":"肥満は多くの病気の原因になります。","romaji":"Himan wa ooku no byouki no gen'in ni narimasu.","german":"Obesity is the cause of many diseases.","furigana":"ひまん|おお|びょうき|げんいん"},{"japanese":"最近、子どもの肥満が増えているそうだ。","romaji":"Saikin, kodomo no himan ga fuete iru sou da.","german":"I hear that obesity among children has been increasing lately.","furigana":"さいきん|こ|ひまん|ふ"}],"notes":"肥満 is the medical, factual term for \"obesity, being overweight\": 肥満度 (\"degree of obesity\"), 肥満体, 子どもの肥満. In everyday speech people say 太っている or 太りすぎ; calling a person 肥満 directly sounds cold and impolite. The softened form is 肥満気味 (\"a bit too heavy\"). The form 肥満する is rare.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このマンガ、一巻しか持っていないんだよ。","romaji":"Kono manga, ikkan shika motte inai n da yo.","german":"I only have the first volume of this manga.","furigana":"いっかん|も"},{"japanese":"まずは一巻から読んでみて。","romaji":"Mazu wa ikkan kara yonde mite.","german":"Try reading it starting from the first volume.","furigana":"いっかん|よ"},{"japanese":"ここで転んだら一巻の終わりだ。","romaji":"Koko de korondara ikkan no owari da.","german":"If you fall here, it's all over.","furigana":"ころ|いっかん|お"}],"notes":"一巻 with the counter 巻 means \"volume 1\" of a book or manga series (more formally 第一巻) or \"one volume\". For works in two or three volumes, people say 上巻・中巻・下巻 instead. The set phrase 一巻の終わり means \"it's all over, the end\". Not to be confused with the homophone 一貫 (\"consistency, coherence\").","levelNote":"Compound: 巻 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この経済学の教科書は上巻と下巻があるんだよ。","romaji":"Kono keizaigaku no kyōkasho wa jōkan to gekan ga aru n da yo.","german":"This economics textbook has a first volume and a second volume.","furigana":"けいざいがく|きょうかしょ|じょうかん|げかん"},{"japanese":"上巻はおもしろかったので、すぐに下巻も買いました。","romaji":"Joukan wa omoshirokatta node, sugu ni gekan mo kaimashita.","german":"The first volume was interesting, so I bought the second one right away.","furigana":"じょうかん|げかん|か"},{"japanese":"図書館で下巻を予約したが、まだ順番が来ない。","romaji":"Toshokan de gekan o yoyaku shita ga, mada junban ga konai.","german":"I reserved the second volume at the library, but my turn hasn't come yet.","furigana":"としょかん|げかん|よやく|じゅんばん|こ"}],"notes":"下巻 is the last volume of a multi-volume work: the second for two volumes (上巻・下巻), the third for three volumes (上巻・中巻・下巻). For longer, consecutively numbered series, people speak of 第二巻, 第三巻 and so on instead. Typical: 下巻を買う, 下巻が出る, 上下巻セット.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"肩のこらない小説だったので、上巻は三十分で読めた。","romaji":"Kata no koranai shousetsu datta node, joukan wa sanjuppun de yometa.","german":"It was a light novel, so I could read the first volume in thirty minutes.","furigana":"かた|しょうせつ|じょうかん|さんじゅっぷん|よ"},{"japanese":"上巻を読み終わったら、下巻を貸してあげるね。","romaji":"Joukan o yomiowattara, gekan o kashite ageru ne.","german":"When you finish the first volume, I'll lend you the second.","furigana":"じょうかん|よ|お|げかん|か"},{"japanese":"この本は上巻と下巻に分かれています。","romaji":"Kono hon wa joukan to gekan ni wakarete imasu.","german":"This book is divided into a first volume and a second volume.","furigana":"ほん|じょうかん|げかん|わ"}],"notes":"上巻 is the first volume of a work in two or three parts; its counterparts are 中巻 and 下巻. For consecutively numbered series, people say 第一巻 or 一巻. Typical combinations: 上巻を読み終わる, 上巻と下巻に分かれている, 上下巻 (\"both volumes\").","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"楽しみにしていた小説の第二巻が発売されてうれしい。","romaji":"Tanoshimi ni shite ita shousetsu no dai nikan ga hatsubai sarete ureshii.","german":"I'm happy that the second volume of the novel I was looking forward to has been released.","furigana":"たの|しょうせつ|だいにかん|はつばい"},{"japanese":"このシリーズは二巻から急におもしろくなる。","romaji":"Kono shiriizu wa nikan kara kyuu ni omoshiroku naru.","german":"This series suddenly becomes interesting from the second volume.","furigana":"にかん|きゅう"},{"japanese":"全二巻の小説を一週間で読み終えた。","romaji":"Zen nikan no shousetsu o isshuukan de yomioeta.","german":"I finished reading the two-volume novel in a week.","furigana":"ぜんにかん|しょうせつ|いっしゅうかん|よ|お"}],"notes":"二巻 is made up of the number 二 and the counter 巻 for volumes. It means either \"volume 2\" (more formally 第二巻) or \"two volumes\" (全二巻 \"in two volumes\"). For works with exactly two volumes, people usually say 上巻・下巻 instead of 一巻・二巻. The counting goes いっかん, にかん, さんかん.","levelNote":"Compound: 巻 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"アルツハイマー病の祖母が昨日から家に帰ってないんです。一緒に捜してもらえませんか？","romaji":"Arutsuhaimaa-byou no sobo ga kinou kara ie ni kaettenai n desu. Issho ni sagashite moraemasen ka?","german":"My grandmother, who has Alzheimer's disease, hasn't come home since yesterday. Could you help me look for her?","furigana":"びょう|そぼ|きのう|いえ|かえ|いっしょ|さが"},{"japanese":"警察は行方不明の子どもを一晩中捜した。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa yukue fumei no kodomo o hitobanjuu sagashita.","german":"The police searched all night for the missing child.","furigana":"けいさつ|ゆくえふめい|こ|ひとばんじゅうさが"},{"japanese":"落とした財布を駅の周りで捜したが、見つからなかった。","romaji":"Otoshita saifu o eki no mawari de sagashita ga, mitsukaranakatta.","german":"I searched around the station for the wallet I dropped, but couldn't find it.","furigana":"お|さいふ|えき|まわ|さが|み"}],"notes":"捜す and 探す are both read さがす. By the traditional rule, 捜す is used for something you have lost or that has disappeared (a missing person, a lost wallet), while 探す is used for something you want to have (仕事を探す, 部屋を探す). In practice, 探す is increasingly used for both; 捜す is typical of police work and the news (cf. 捜査, 捜索). The result of finding is expressed with 見つかる or 見つける.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"男性は自宅で絞殺された状態で見つかった。","romaji":"Dansei wa jitaku de kousatsu sareta joutai de mitsukatta.","german":"The man was found strangled at his home.","furigana":"だんせい|じたく|こうさつ|じょうたい|み"},{"japanese":"警察は、被害者が絞殺されたとみて捜査している。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa, higaisha ga kousatsu sareta to mite sousa shite iru.","german":"The police believe the victim was strangled and are investigating.","furigana":"けいさつ|ひがいしゃ|こうさつ|そうさ"},{"japanese":"その小説では、最初の事件で女性が絞殺される。","romaji":"Sono shousetsu de wa, saisho no jiken de josei ga kousatsu sareru.","german":"In that novel, a woman is strangled in the first incident.","furigana":"しょうせつ|さいしょ|じけん|じょせい|こうさつ"}],"notes":"絞殺 is a technical term from police and news language for \"strangulation, to strangle\". As a する-verb it is almost always in the passive: 絞殺される, 絞殺された遺体. In conversation people say 首を絞めて殺す instead. Related words are 絞める (\"to choke\") and 殺害 (\"killing\").","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の父は隠居生活をエンジョイしていますよ。","romaji":"Watashi no chichi wa inkyo seikatsu o enjoi shite imasu yo.","german":"My father is enjoying his retired life.","furigana":"わたし|ちち|いんきょせいかつ"},{"japanese":"祖父は七十歳で店を息子に任せて隠居した。","romaji":"Sofu wa nanajussai de mise o musuko ni makasete inkyo shita.","german":"At seventy, my grandfather left the shop to his son and retired.","furigana":"そふ|ななじゅっさい|みせ|むすこ|まか|いんきょ"},{"japanese":"隠居するにはまだ早いと言って、父は今も畑で働いている。","romaji":"Inkyo suru ni wa mada hayai to itte, chichi wa ima mo hatake de hataraite iru.","german":"Saying it is still too early to retire, my father is still working in the field.","furigana":"いんきょ|はや|い|ちち|いま|はたけ|はたら"}],"notes":"隠居 means withdrawing from working and business life to live quietly, traditionally after handing over the business or the headship of the family to the next generation: 隠居する, 隠居生活. The word sounds somewhat old-fashioned; ご隠居 is an elderly gentleman in retirement. More neutral are 退職 (\"leaving a job\") and 定年 (\"retirement age\"); 引退 is said for athletes or politicians.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"トーフグのオフィスに、アヤがデザインしたカーテンが掛けられました。","romaji":"Tōfugu no ofisu ni, Aya ga dezain shita kāten ga kakeraremashita.","german":"The curtains designed by Aya were hung in the Tofugu office.","furigana":"か"},{"japanese":"壁に写真を掛けてください。","romaji":"Kabe ni shashin o kakete kudasai.","german":"Please hang the photo on the wall.","furigana":"かべ|しゃしん|か"}],"notes":"掛ける here means \"to hang\" and is a transitive ichidan verb: 掛けます, 掛けて. With を you name the object hung, with に the place. 掛けられました is passive in the curtain sentence: the curtains were hung. The intransitive 掛かる, by contrast, describes the hanging object.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"成田空港でドルを円に替えました。","romaji":"Narita kuukou de doru o en ni kaemashita.","german":"I exchanged dollars for yen at Narita Airport.","furigana":"なりたくうこう|えん|か"},{"japanese":"部屋の電球が切れたので、新しいのに替えた。","romaji":"Heya no denkyuu ga kireta node, atarashii no ni kaeta.","german":"The light bulb in the room burned out, so I replaced it with a new one.","furigana":"へや|でんきゅう|き|あたら|か"},{"japanese":"監督は後半に選手を二人替えた。","romaji":"Kantoku wa kouhan ni senshu o futari kaeta.","german":"The manager substituted two players in the second half.","furigana":"かんとく|こうはん|せんしゅ|ふたりか"}],"notes":"替える means \"to exchange for something else, to switch\": 電球を替える, ドルを円に替える (\"to exchange into ...\" with に). There are several spellings of the same reading: 変える (\"to change, alter\"), 換える (\"to convert, swap\"), 代える (\"to replace, to use in place of\"); 替える and 換える overlap strongly. The intransitive partner is 替わる. Common in compounds: 着替える, 取り替える, 両替.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"エルネスト・チェ・ゲバラはアルゼンチン出身の革命家です。","romaji":"Erunesuto Che Gebara wa Aruzenchin shusshin no kakumeika desu.","german":"Ernesto Che Guevara was a revolutionary from Argentina.","furigana":"しゅっしん|かくめいか"},{"japanese":"彼は若いころ、革命家になることを夢見ていた。","romaji":"Kare wa wakai koro, kakumeika ni naru koto o yumemite ita.","german":"In his youth, he dreamed of becoming a revolutionary.","furigana":"かれ|わか|かくめいか|ゆめみ"},{"japanese":"彼女は料理界の革命家と呼ばれている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa ryourikai no kakumeika to yobarete iru.","german":"She is called a revolutionary of the culinary world.","furigana":"かのじょ|りょうりかい|かくめいか|よ"}],"notes":"革命家 is composed of 革命 (\"revolution\") and the suffix 家 for people who pursue an activity (cf. 政治家, 芸術家). It refers to people who lead a political overthrow, but is also used figuratively for innovators in a field: 料理界の革命家. The adjective \"revolutionary\" is 革命的な.","levelNote":"Compound: 革命 (N1) + 家 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"古着屋さんでビンテージものの革ジャンを見つけた。","romaji":"Furugiya-san de binteeji mono no kawajan o mitsuketa.","german":"Ich habe eine Vintage-Lederjacke in einem Secondhand-Laden gefunden.","furigana":"ふるぎや|かわ|み"},{"japanese":"冬はいつも黒い革ジャンを着ています。","romaji":"Fuyu wa itsumo kuroi kawajan o kite imasu.","german":"Im Winter trage ich immer eine schwarze Lederjacke.","furigana":"ふゆ|くろ|かわ|き"},{"japanese":"雨にぬれると革ジャンが傷むから気をつけて。","romaji":"Ame ni nureru to kawajan ga itamu kara ki o tsukete.","german":"Pass auf, Lederjacken nehmen Schaden, wenn sie nass werden.","furigana":"あめ|かわ|いた|き"}],"notes":"革ジャン ist die umgangssprachliche Kurzform von 革ジャンパー, also eine Lederjacke. Achtung: ジャンパー bedeutet im Japanischen eine Jacke bzw. einen Blouson, keinen Pullover wie das deutsche „Jumper“. Gelegentlich wird es auch 皮ジャン geschrieben; im Modehandel sagt man eher レザージャケット.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いやー、世の中って狭いもんですね。まさか私の長崎県の友人がコウイチの友人の友人だったとは。","romaji":"Iyaa, yo no naka tte semai mon desu ne. Masaka watashi no Nagasaki-ken no yuujin ga Kouichi no yuujin no yuujin datta to wa.","german":"Wow, it's a small world. Who would have thought my friend from Nagasaki Prefecture was a friend of Koichi's friend.","furigana":"よ|なか|せま|わたし|ながさきけん|ゆうじん|ゆうじん|ゆうじん"},{"japanese":"長崎県は九州の西にあります。","romaji":"Nagasaki-ken wa Kyuushuu no nishi ni arimasu.","german":"Nagasaki Prefecture is in the west of Kyushu.","furigana":"ながさきけん|きゅうしゅう|にし"},{"japanese":"夏休みに長崎県の島を旅行した。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi ni Nagasaki-ken no shima o ryokou shita.","german":"I traveled to an island in Nagasaki Prefecture during the summer vacation.","furigana":"なつやす|ながさきけん|しま|りょこう"}],"notes":"長崎県 is the prefecture in the west of Kyushu, with 長崎市 as its capital. A large part of it consists of islands (e.g. 五島列島, 対馬), and it is known for its history as the only trading port during the period of national isolation (出島) and for the atomic bombing in 1945. In conversation people often just say 長崎; with 県 it is clear that the whole prefecture is meant and not only the city.","levelNote":"Compound: 長崎 (N4) + 県 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ワニカニは別名クラビゲーターとも呼ばれている。","romaji":"WaniKani wa betsumei Kurabigeetaa to mo yobarete iru.","german":"The alligator gar is also called the \"Clavigator\".","furigana":"べつめい|よ"},{"japanese":"名前を呼ばれたら、前に出てください。","romaji":"Namae o yobaretara, mae ni dete kudasai.","german":"When your name is called, please come to the front.","furigana":"なまえ|よ|まえ|で"},{"japanese":"友達の結婚式に呼ばれて、来月大阪へ行きます。","romaji":"Tomodachi no kekkonshiki ni yobarete, raigetsu Oosaka e ikimasu.","german":"I've been invited to a friend's wedding, so I'm going to Osaka next month.","furigana":"ともだち|けっこんしき|よ|らいげつおおさか|い"}],"notes":"呼ばれる is the passive of 呼ぶ and has several meanings: \"to be called (named)\" (〜と呼ばれる), \"to be called out\" (名前を呼ばれる), \"to be invited\" (結婚式に呼ばれる) and \"to be summoned\" (先生に呼ばれる). The agent is marked with に. For naming, 〜と呼ばれている is very common; in explanations 〜と言われる is also used.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ついにコウイチがコンサート会場に姿を現したよ！","romaji":"Tsui ni Kouichi ga konsaato kaijou ni sugata o arawashita yo!","german":"Koichi has finally shown up at the concert venue!","furigana":"かいじょう|すがた|あらわ"},{"japanese":"雲の間から富士山が姿を現した。","romaji":"Kumo no aida kara Fujisan ga sugata o arawashita.","german":"Mt. Fuji appeared from between the clouds.","furigana":"くも|あいだ|ふじさん|すがた|あらわ"},{"japanese":"犯人はなかなか正体を現さない。","romaji":"Hannin wa nakanaka shoutai o arawasanai.","german":"The culprit doesn't reveal his true identity easily.","furigana":"はんにん|しょうたい|あらわ"}],"notes":"現す means \"to make visible, to bring to light\" something that was previously hidden: 姿を現す (\"to appear, show up\"), 正体を現す (\"to show one's true colors\"). The intransitive partner is 現れる (\"to appear\"). Do not confuse it with 表す (\"to express, represent\": 気持ちを表す, 記号が表す) or 著す (\"to write a book\") - all are read あらわす.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お化けって本当に居ると思いますか？","romaji":"Obake tte hontō ni iru to omoimasu ka?","german":"Do you think ghosts really exist?","furigana":"ば|ほんとう|い|おも"},{"japanese":"庭に猫が居ます。","romaji":"Niwa ni neko ga imasu.","german":"There is a cat in the garden.","furigana":"にわ|ねこ|い"}],"notes":"居る is an intransitive ichidan verb for the presence of living things: 居ます, 居て. It is mostly written in hiragana as いる. With に you name the place, with が the person or animal. と思いますか asks for an opinion. For inanimate objects you normally use ある.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今更そんなことを言われても困るよ。","romaji":"Imasara sonna koto o iwarete mo komaru yo.","german":"It's a problem if you tell me that at this point.","furigana":"いまさら|い|こま"},{"japanese":"今更謝っても、もう遅い。","romaji":"Imasara ayamatte mo, mou osoi.","german":"Even if you apologize now, it's already too late.","furigana":"いまさらあやま|おそ"},{"japanese":"今更ですが、お名前を伺ってもいいですか。","romaji":"Imasara desu ga, o-namae o ukagatte mo ii desu ka.","german":"It's late to ask, but may I ask your name?","furigana":"いまさら|なまえ|うかが"}],"notes":"今更 expresses that something comes \"now\" although it is actually too late for it; mostly with reproach or resignation: 今更〜ても, 今更遅い, 今更言われても困る. Often written in kana (いまさら). Fixed phrases: 今更ながら (\"admittedly late, but ...\"), 今更聞けない (\"what one no longer dares to ask at this point\"). As a polite opener: 今更ですが (\"it may be a bit late, but ...\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"そろそろ衣がえの季節です。","romaji":"Sorosoro koromogae no kisetsu desu.","german":"It's almost time to change into the season's clothes.","furigana":"ころも|きせつ"},{"japanese":"えびに衣をつけて、油で揚げます。","romaji":"Ebi ni koromo o tsukete, abura de agemasu.","german":"Coat the shrimp in batter and deep-fry it.","furigana":"ころも|あぶら|あ"},{"japanese":"お坊さんが黒い衣を着て、お経を読んでいた。","romaji":"Obousan ga kuroi koromo o kite, okyou o yonde ita.","german":"The monk was wearing a black robe and reciting a sutra.","furigana":"ぼう|くろ|ころも|き|きょう|よ"}],"notes":"衣 (ころも) is an old, elevated word for \"clothing, garment\". Today it appears mainly as a monk's robe (お坊さんの衣) and in the kitchen as batter or breading: 天ぷらの衣, 衣をつける. For everyday clothing people say 服 or 衣服. You know it as a component of 衣替え (\"seasonal change of clothes\"); in Sino-Japanese words it is read い (衣類, 衣食住).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"辺りを見渡すと、たくさんの捜査員達に取り囲まれていた。","romaji":"Atari o miwatasu to, takusan no sousaintachi ni torikakomarete ita.","german":"When I looked around, I was surrounded by lots of investigators.","furigana":"あた|みわた|そうさいんたち|と|かこ"},{"japanese":"山の頂上から町全体を見渡すことができる。","romaji":"Yama no choujou kara machi zentai o miwatasu koto ga dekiru.","german":"From the top of the mountain, you can see the whole town.","furigana":"やま|ちょうじょう|まちぜんたい|みわた"},{"japanese":"見渡す限り、青い海が広がっていた。","romaji":"Miwatasu kagiri, aoi umi ga hirogatte ita.","german":"As far as the eye could see, the blue sea stretched out.","furigana":"みわた|かぎ|あお|うみ|ひろ"}],"notes":"見渡す means \"to survey, to let one's gaze sweep over something\" and takes を: 辺りを見渡す, 町を見渡す. The fixed phrase 見渡す限り means \"as far as the eye can see\". 見回す emphasizes turning the head to look around, 眺める calm viewing. The potential form 見渡せる (\"can be seen across\") is common: 海が見渡せる部屋.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"早寝早起きの規則正しい生活を送るよう、心がけてください。","romaji":"Hayane hayaoki no kisokutadashii seikatsu o okuru yō, kokorogakete kudasai.","german":"Please try to lead a regular life, going to bed and rising early.","furigana":"はやねはやお|きそくただ|せいかつ|おく|こころ"},{"japanese":"心臓は規則正しく動いています。","romaji":"Shinzou wa kisokutadashiku ugoite imasu.","german":"The heart beats regularly.","furigana":"しんぞう|きそくただ|うご"},{"japanese":"机の上に本が規則正しく並べられている。","romaji":"Tsukue no ue ni hon ga kisokutadashiku naraberarete iru.","german":"The books are lined up neatly on the desk.","furigana":"つくえ|うえ|ほん|きそくただ|なら"}],"notes":"規則正しい is an い-adjective for \"regular, orderly, following a fixed rhythm\". The most typical combination is 規則正しい生活 (\"a regular daily routine\"); adverbially it is 規則正しく (規則正しく並ぶ). The opposite is 不規則な. 定期的, by contrast, emphasizes fixed time intervals (定期的に検査する), 几帳面 a person's conscientiousness.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"家庭教師は、ホワイトボードをかべに立て掛けた。","romaji":"Katei kyoushi wa, howaito boodo o kabe ni tatekaketa.","german":"The tutor leaned the whiteboard against the wall.","furigana":"かていきょうし|た|か"},{"japanese":"大学生のとき、中学生の家庭教師のアルバイトをしていた。","romaji":"Daigakusei no toki, chuugakusei no katei kyoushi no arubaito o shite ita.","german":"When I was a university student, I had a part-time job as a tutor for a junior high school student.","furigana":"だいがくせい|ちゅうがくせい|かていきょうし"},{"japanese":"息子の成績が下がったので、家庭教師をつけることにした。","romaji":"Musuko no seiseki ga sagatta node, katei kyoushi o tsukeru koto ni shita.","german":"My son's grades dropped, so we decided to get him a tutor.","furigana":"むすこ|せいせき|さ|かていきょうし"}],"notes":"家庭教師 is a private teacher who comes to the students' home and usually teaches one-on-one. Typical: 家庭教師をつける (\"to get a tutor\"), 家庭教師をする (\"to work as a tutor\"), 家庭教師のアルバイト - a classic student job. By contrast, 塾 is a cram school where 講師 teach. Colloquially it is shortened to カテキョー.","levelNote":"Compound: 家庭 (N5) + 教師 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の仕事は時間が不規則です。","romaji":"Watashi no shigoto wa jikan ga fukisoku desu.","german":"My work hours are irregular.","furigana":"わたし|しごと|じかん|ふきそく"},{"japanese":"不規則な生活を続けると、体を壊しますよ。","romaji":"Fukisoku na seikatsu o tsuzukeru to, karada o kowashimasu yo.","german":"If you keep living an irregular lifestyle, you will ruin your health.","furigana":"ふきそく|せいかつ|つづ|からだ|こわ"},{"japanese":"「行く」は、て形が不規則な動詞です。","romaji":"Iku wa, te-kei ga fukisoku na doushi desu.","german":"\"行く\" is a verb with an irregular te-form.","furigana":"い|けい|ふきそく|どうし"}],"notes":"不規則 is a な-adjective for \"irregular, without a fixed rhythm\": 不規則な生活, 勤務時間が不規則だ, 不規則に並ぶ. In grammar, irregular verbs are called 不規則動詞. Opposites are 規則正しい and 規則的. 不定期, by contrast, refers to something that does not take place at fixed intervals (不定期便).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"荷物を降ろすのを手伝ってくれ。","romaji":"Nimotsu o orosu no o tetsudatte kure.","german":"Help me unload the luggage.","furigana":"にもつ|お|てつだ"},{"japanese":"次の角で降ろしてください。","romaji":"Tsugi no kado de oroshite kudasai.","german":"Please drop me off at the next corner.","furigana":"つぎ|かど|お"},{"japanese":"社長は彼をプロジェクトのリーダーから降ろした。","romaji":"Shachou wa kare o purojekuto no riidaa kara oroshita.","german":"The president removed him as leader of the project.","furigana":"しゃちょう|かれ|お"}],"notes":"降ろす is transitive and mainly means to bring people or cargo down from a vehicle or from above: 荷物を降ろす, 客を降ろす (\"to drop passengers off\"), as well as to remove someone from a post (リーダーから降ろす). The intransitive partner is 降りる. The homophone 下ろす means \"to lower\" (手を下ろす, シャッターを下ろす), to withdraw money (お金を下ろす) and to grate (大根を下ろす).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この記事への反響はとても大きかった。","romaji":"Kono kiji e no hankyō wa totemo ōkikatta.","german":"The response to this article was very large.","furigana":"きじ|はんきょう|おお"},{"japanese":"声がトンネルの中で反響した。","romaji":"Koe ga tonneru no naka de hankyou shita.","german":"The voice echoed inside the tunnel.","furigana":"こえ|なか|はんきょう"},{"japanese":"新しい商品は、発売直後から大きな反響を呼んだ。","romaji":"Atarashii shouhin wa, hatsubai chokugo kara ookina hankyou o yonda.","german":"The new product drew a big response right after its release.","furigana":"あたら|しょうひん|はつばいちょくご|おお|はんきょう|よ"}],"notes":"反響 has two meanings: literally \"echo, reverberation\", as an intransitive する-verb (声が反響する), and figuratively the \"response, reaction\" of the public to something (記事への反響). Typical are 反響がある, 大きな反響, 反響を呼ぶ (\"to find resonance\"). 反応 is the more general \"reaction\"; 響く means \"to resound, to ring\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"雨がふって川の水かさが増しました。","romaji":"Ame ga futte kawa no mizukasa ga mashimashita.","german":"It rained and the water level of the river rose.","furigana":"あめ|かわ|みず|ま"},{"japanese":"春になって、日差しが少しずつ強さを増してきた。","romaji":"Haru ni natte, hizashi ga sukoshi zutsu tsuyosa o mashite kita.","german":"With the arrival of spring, the sunlight has gradually grown stronger.","furigana":"はる|ひざ|すこ|つよ|ま"},{"japanese":"雪のため、高速道路では事故の危険が増しています。","romaji":"Yuki no tame, kousoku douro de wa jiko no kiken ga mashite imasu.","german":"Because of the snow, the danger of accidents is increasing on the expressway.","furigana":"ゆき|こうそくどうろ|じこ|きけん|ま"}],"notes":"増す means \"to increase, to rise\" and can be used both intransitively (水かさが増す, 危険が増す) and transitively (速度を増す, 強さを増す). It sounds more literary than the everyday words 増える (intransitive) and 増やす (transitive) and often appears with abstract quantities such as danger, difficulty or interest. Set phrase: いつにも増して (\"more than ever\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日は家庭教師に掛け算を教えてもらいました。","romaji":"Kyō wa katei kyōshi ni kakezan o oshiete moraimashita.","german":"Today my tutor taught me multiplication.","furigana":"きょう|かていきょうし|か|ざん|おし"},{"japanese":"小学二年生で掛け算の九九を覚えます。","romaji":"Shougaku ninensei de kakezan no kuku o oboemasu.","german":"In second grade of elementary school, children learn the multiplication table.","furigana":"しょうがくにねんせい|か|ざん|くく|おぼ"},{"japanese":"先に掛け算をしてから、足し算をしてください。","romaji":"Saki ni kakezan o shite kara, tashizan o shite kudasai.","german":"Please do the multiplication first, and then the addition.","furigana":"さき|か|ざん|た|ざん"}],"notes":"掛け算 is multiplication; the four basic arithmetic operations are 足し算, 引き算, 掛け算 and 割り算. The multiplication table is called 九九 and is memorized in Japan in second grade. \"To multiply\" is 掛ける: 三に四を掛ける. It is also often written かけ算; the mathematical technical term is 乗法.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本には世界に通用する優れた技術者がたくさんいます。","romaji":"Nippon ni wa sekai ni tsūyō suru sugureta gijutsusha ga takusan imasu.","german":"Japan has many excellent engineers who are recognized around the world.","furigana":"にっぽん|せかい|つうよう|すぐ|ぎじゅつしゃ"},{"japanese":"この新しいカメラは、暗い場所での性能が特に優れている。","romaji":"Kono atarashii kamera wa, kurai basho de no seinou ga toku ni sugurete iru.","german":"This new camera has particularly excellent performance in dark places.","furigana":"あたら|くら|ばしょ|せいのう|とく|すぐ"},{"japanese":"最近、母は体調が優れないようだ。","romaji":"Saikin, haha wa taichou ga sugurenai you da.","german":"My mother doesn't seem to be feeling well lately.","furigana":"さいきん|はは|たいちょう|すぐ"}],"notes":"優れる means \"to be outstanding, to be superior\" and is almost never used in its dictionary form: predicatively as 優れている (性能が優れている), attributively as 優れた + noun (優れた技術). Comparison is expressed with より, the area of excellence with に (記憶力に優れる). In the negative, 気分が優れない or 体調が優れない means \"not to feel well\". 上手, by contrast, describes a person's skill at an activity.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コップの半分まで入れられた水は、人によって「半分しかない」とも「半分もある」とも捉えられます。","romaji":"Koppu no hanbun made irerareta mizu wa, hito ni yotte \"hanbun shika nai\" to mo \"hanbun mo aru\" to mo toraeraremasu.","german":"Depending on the person, a glass filled halfway with water can be seen as either \"only half\" or \"half already\".","furigana":"はんぶん|い|みず|ひと|はんぶん|はんぶん|とら"},{"japanese":"彼の説明は問題の本質をよく捉えている。","romaji":"Kare no setsumei wa mondai no honshitsu o yoku toraete iru.","german":"His explanation captures the essence of the problem well.","furigana":"かれ|せつめい|もんだい|ほんしつ|とら"},{"japanese":"防犯カメラが犯人の姿を捉えていた。","romaji":"Bouhan kamera ga hannin no sugata o toraete ita.","german":"A security camera had captured the culprit's figure.","furigana":"ぼうはん|はんにん|すがた|とら"}],"notes":"捉える means \"to grasp, to capture\", above all in the figurative sense: 本質を捉える (\"to grasp the core\"), 〜を〜と捉える (\"to understand something as ...\"), 前向きに捉える (\"to see positively\"). Cameras or radar also \"capture\" things: カメラが犯人の姿を捉える. The physical catching of animals or criminals is more often written 捕らえる. Related is the passive 捉われる (\"to be caught up in something\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自分で自分の首を絞めるようなことはやめたほうがいい。","romaji":"Jibun de jibun no kubi o shimeru you na koto wa yameta hou ga ii.","german":"You'd better stop doing things that only hurt yourself.","furigana":"じぶん|じぶん|くび|し"},{"japanese":"男は相手の首を絞めようとして、警察に捕まった。","romaji":"Otoko wa aite no kubi o shimeyou to shite, keisatsu ni tsukamatta.","german":"The man was arrested after trying to strangle the other person.","furigana":"おとこ|あいて|くび|し|けいさつ|つか"},{"japanese":"祖母は昔、庭で飼っていた鶏を絞めて料理していたそうだ。","romaji":"Sobo wa mukashi, niwa de katte ita niwatori o shimete ryouri shite ita sou da.","german":"I hear that long ago my grandmother used to wring the necks of the chickens she kept in the garden and cook them.","furigana":"そぼ|むかし|にわ|か|にわとり|し|りょうり"}],"notes":"絞める means \"to constrict the neck, to choke\": 首を絞める. It is also used for slaughtering poultry (鶏を絞める). The idiom 自分で自分の首を絞める means \"to harm oneself, to shoot oneself in the foot\". Not to be confused with 締める (\"to tighten\": ネクタイ・ベルトを締める) and 閉める (\"to close\": ドアを閉める). The intransitive partner is 絞まる (首が絞まる).","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人狼を見たことがありますか。","romaji":"Jinrou o mita koto ga arimasu ka.","german":"Have you ever seen a werewolf?","furigana":"じんろう|み"},{"japanese":"友達の家で人狼ゲームをして盛り上がった。","romaji":"Tomodachi no ie de jinrou geemu o shite moriagatta.","german":"We played the Werewolf game at a friend's house and it got lively.","furigana":"ともだち|いえ|じんろう|も|あ"},{"japanese":"この映画では、満月の夜に人狼が村を襲う。","romaji":"Kono eiga de wa, mangetsu no yoru ni jinrou ga mura o osou.","german":"In this movie, a werewolf attacks the village on a full-moon night.","furigana":"えいが|まんげつ|よる|じんろう|むら|おそ"}],"notes":"人狼 is the \"werewolf\", a human who turns into a wolf. In everyday life the word is mostly found today in the party game 人狼ゲーム (\"Werewolf\", similar to \"Mafia\"), in which you have to find out who the 人狼 is. For the horror figure in films, 狼男 (\"wolf man\") is also very common; 人狼 sounds somewhat more literary or is the technical term in fantasy and games.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"岡山城のライトアップ、めっちゃきれいだったよ！","romaji":"Okayamajō no raitoappu, metcha kirei datta yo!","german":"The illumination of Okayama Castle was really beautiful!","furigana":"おかやまじょう"},{"japanese":"岡山城は黒い外観から「烏城」とも呼ばれています。","romaji":"Okayamajou wa kuroi gaikan kara \"Ujou\" to mo yobarete imasu.","german":"Okayama Castle is also called \"Crow Castle\" because of its black exterior.","furigana":"おかやまじょう|くろ|がいかん|うじょう|よ"},{"japanese":"後楽園を散歩してから、岡山城まで歩いた。","romaji":"Kourakuen o sanpo shite kara, Okayamajou made aruita.","german":"After strolling through Korakuen, I walked to Okayama Castle.","furigana":"こうらくえん|さんぽ|おかやまじょう|ある"}],"notes":"岡山城 is the castle in the city of Okayama. Because of its black outer walls it has the nickname 烏城 (\"Crow Castle\"), in contrast to the white castle of Himeji. It stands right next to the famous garden 後楽園, so the two are often visited together. In castle names 城 is read as the suffix じょう (大阪城, 名古屋城); on its own it is read しろ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この薬は6時間ごとに飲んでください。","romaji":"Kono kusuri wa roku jikan goto ni nonde kudasai.","german":"Please take this medicine every 6 hours.","furigana":"くすり|じかん|の"},{"japanese":"彼女は会うごとにきれいになっていく。","romaji":"Kanojo wa au goto ni kirei ni natte iku.","german":"She gets prettier every time I see her.","furigana":"かのじょ|あ"},{"japanese":"100リツイート毎に、プレゼントを1つ追加します！","romaji":"Hyaku ritsuiito goto ni, purezento o hitotsu tsuika shimasu!","german":"For every 100 retweets, we will add one more gift!","furigana":"ごと|ついか"}],"notes":"〜ごとに comes after nouns and counters and means \"every ..., per ...\": 3時間ごとに \"every three hours\", クラスごとに \"per class\". After verbs it means \"each time\" and often stresses a progressive change (会うごとに, 回を重ねるごとに); in everyday speech たびに is more common for this. Be careful with おきに: 1日おきに means \"every other day\", whereas 1日ごとに means \"every day\". It is usually written in hiragana; the spelling 毎に is rarer (毎 alone as a prefix is read まい, as in 毎日).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ただのまぐれだよ。","romaji":"Tada no magure da yo.","german":"It was just a fluke."},{"japanese":"テストはまぐれで満点が取れた。","romaji":"Tesuto wa magure de manten ga toreta.","german":"I got a perfect score on the test by sheer luck.","furigana":"まんてん|と"},{"japanese":"一回ならまぐれかもしれないけど、三回続けて勝つのは実力だよ。","romaji":"Ikkai nara magure kamo shirenai kedo, sankai tsuzukete katsu no wa jitsuryoku da yo.","german":"Once might be a fluke, but winning three times in a row is real ability.","furigana":"いっかい|さんかいつづ|か|じつりょく"}],"notes":"まぐれ is a \"lucky hit\" or \"fluke\" - a success that does not rest on skill. Typical are ただのまぐれ, まぐれで当たる/勝つ and the compound まぐれ当たり. It is often used modestly about one's own successes, but can also be said disparagingly about others. Unlike the neutral 偶然 (\"coincidence\"), まぐれ stresses that one had not really expected it; it is almost always written in hiragana and is rather colloquial.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お父さんは現役のプロレスラーです。","romaji":"Otōsan wa gen'eki no puroresurā desu.","german":"Her father is an active professional wrestler.","furigana":"とう|げんえき"},{"japanese":"祖母は80歳ですが、まだ現役で働いています。","romaji":"Sobo wa hachijussai desu ga, mada gen'eki de hataraite imasu.","german":"My grandmother is 80, but she is still working.","furigana":"そぼ|さい|げんえき|はたら"},{"japanese":"兄は現役で東京の大学に合格した。","romaji":"Ani wa gen'eki de Toukyou no daigaku ni goukaku shita.","german":"My older brother got into a university in Tokyo straight out of high school.","furigana":"あに|げんえき|とうきょう|だいがく|ごうかく"}],"notes":"現役 refers to someone who is \"still active in service/profession\": 現役の選手 \"active player\", 現役で働く \"to still be working\", 現役を引退する \"to retire from active service\". It is a noun that takes の before other nouns, not a true adjective. A second important meaning comes from the exam system: 現役で合格する means passing the university entrance exam directly after high school - the opposite is 浪人 (someone who repeats a year).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"やっぱりこの時間は、年配の人が多いですね。","romaji":"Yappari kono jikan wa, nenpai no hito ga ooi desu ne.","german":"As expected, there are a lot of elderly people at this time of day.","furigana":"じかん|ねんぱい|ひと|おお"},{"japanese":"電車でご年配の方に席を譲りました。","romaji":"Densha de gonenpai no kata ni seki o yuzurimashita.","german":"I gave up my seat on the train to an elderly person.","furigana":"でんしゃ|ねんぱい|かた|せき|ゆず"},{"japanese":"この店は年配のお客さんに人気がある。","romaji":"Kono mise wa nenpai no okyakusan ni ninki ga aru.","german":"This shop is popular with older customers.","furigana":"みせ|ねんぱい|きゃく|にんき"}],"notes":"年配 denotes \"advanced age\" and usually stands with の before people: 年配の人, politely 年配の方 or ご年配の方 \"elderly ladies and gentlemen\". It is a considerate, neutral expression; 老人 can sound blunt to impolite, and 高齢者 is the official term. There are also 同年配 \"about the same age\" and 50年配の男性 \"a man around 50\" (more written).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"昨日の台風がウソの様に晴れてるね。","romaji":"Kinou no taifuu ga uso no you ni hareteru ne.","german":"The sky is clear as if yesterday's typhoon had been a lie.","furigana":"きのう|たいふう|よう|は"},{"japanese":"彼は日本人のように日本語を話します。","romaji":"Kare wa nihonjin no you ni nihongo o hanashimasu.","german":"He speaks Japanese like a Japanese person.","furigana":"かれ|にほんじん|にほんご|はな"},{"japanese":"プレゼントをもらって、父は子供のように喜んだ。","romaji":"Purezento o moratte, chichi wa kodomo no you ni yorokonda.","german":"Receiving the present, my father was delighted like a child.","furigana":"ちち|こども|よろこ"}],"notes":"Noun + のように means \"like ...\" and describes how something happens: 子供のように喜ぶ \"to be delighted like a child\". Before a noun it becomes のような (夢のような話 \"a story like a dream\"). It can also introduce examples (\"such as\"). Colloquially people say みたいに. In this grammar 様 is usually written in hiragana; the kanji spelling の様に is rarer.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は、教育にお金をかければ国は栄えると思います。","romaji":"Watashi wa, kyouiku ni okane o kakereba kuni wa sakaeru to omoimasu.","german":"I think a country will prosper if it spends money on education.","furigana":"わたし|きょういく|かね|くに|さか|おも"},{"japanese":"この町は昔、港町として栄えていました。","romaji":"Kono machi wa mukashi, minatomachi to shite sakaete imashita.","german":"This town used to flourish as a port town.","furigana":"まち|むかし|みなとまち|さか"},{"japanese":"駅の周りは栄えているけど、少し離れると静かだ。","romaji":"Eki no mawari wa sakaete iru kedo, sukoshi hanareru to shizuka da.","german":"The area around the station is thriving, but a little further away it is quiet.","furigana":"えき|まわ|さか|すこ|はな|しず"}],"notes":"栄える is intransitive and means \"to bloom, to thrive, to flourish\" - mostly of countries, cities, industries or cultures: 町が栄える, 港町として栄えた. In the form 栄えている it also describes a lively place with many shops. The related 繁栄する is more formal, にぎわう rather emphasizes lively bustle. The opposite is 衰える (\"to decline, to grow weaker\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これはライフタイム会員限定のサービスなんです。","romaji":"Kore wa raifu taimu kaiin gentei no sābisu na n desu.","german":"This is a service exclusively for Lifetime members.","furigana":"かいいんげんてい"},{"japanese":"期間限定のメニューなので、早めに食べに行こう。","romaji":"Kikan gentei no menyuu na node, hayame ni tabe ni ikou.","german":"It's a limited-time menu, so let's go eat it early.","furigana":"きかんげんてい|はや|た|い"},{"japanese":"参加者は大学生に限定されています。","romaji":"Sankasha wa daigakusei ni gentei sarete imasu.","german":"Participants are limited to university students.","furigana":"さんかしゃ|だいがくせい|げんてい"}],"notes":"限定 means \"restriction to a certain group\" and very often comes after nouns: 会員限定 \"members only\", 期間限定 \"for a limited time only\", 数量限定 \"limited quantity\", 限定品 \"limited-edition item\". As a する-verb it is transitive: 対象を大学生に限定する; in the passive 〜に限定されている. Unlike 制限 (\"limit, restriction\" as in 速度制限), 限定 means narrowing down to something specific.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ドラえもんの声優が替わってから、観なくなってしまった。","romaji":"Doraemon no seiyū ga kawatte kara, minaku natte shimatta.","german":"I stopped watching Doraemon after the voice actor was replaced.","furigana":"せいゆう|か|み"},{"japanese":"4月から担任の先生が替わります。","romaji":"Shigatsu kara tannin no sensei ga kawarimasu.","german":"The homeroom teacher will change from April.","furigana":"がつ|たんにん|せんせい|か"},{"japanese":"すみません、席を替わっていただけませんか。","romaji":"Sumimasen, seki o kawatte itadakemasen ka.","german":"Excuse me, would you mind switching seats with me?","furigana":"せき|か"}],"notes":"替わる is intransitive and means that something or someone \"is exchanged, changes\": 担当者が替わる \"the person in charge changes\", 席を替わる \"to swap seats\". The transitive counterpart is 替える (\"to exchange\"). 変わる (\"to change, become different\") and 代わる (\"to take someone's place, substitute\") are read the same way; 替わる and 代わる often overlap, e.g. when handing over the phone (電話を代わる).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このトーフグ人形は、あのたなの上に載せておきますね。","romaji":"Kono Toufugu ningyou wa, ano tana no ue ni nosete okimasu ne.","german":"I'll leave this Tofugu doll up on that shelf.","furigana":"にんぎょう|うえ|の"},{"japanese":"店の情報をホームページに載せました。","romaji":"Mise no jouhou o hoomupeeji ni nosemashita.","german":"I posted the shop's information on the website.","furigana":"みせ|じょうほう|の"},{"japanese":"荷物を車の屋根に載せて運んだ。","romaji":"Nimotsu o kuruma no yane ni nosete hakonda.","german":"I carried the luggage on the roof of the car.","furigana":"にもつ|くるま|やね|の|はこ"}],"notes":"載せる is transitive: to \"put/place something on something\" (棚の上に載せる) or to \"load\" (荷物を車に載せる). Also very common is \"to publish, to print\": 記事を新聞に載せる, 写真をサイトに載せる. The intransitive counterpart is 載る (\"to be on top of; to be printed\"). For people you take along in a vehicle, write 乗せる (乗る/乗せる).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"気象庁の仕事の面接を受けてきました。","romaji":"Kishōchō no shigoto no mensetsu o ukete kimashita.","german":"I went for an interview for a job at the Meteorological Agency.","furigana":"きしょうちょう|しごと|めんせつ|う"},{"japanese":"気象庁によると、明日は大雨になるそうです。","romaji":"Kishouchou ni yoru to, ashita wa ooame ni naru sou desu.","german":"According to the Meteorological Agency, there will be heavy rain tomorrow.","furigana":"きしょうちょう|あした|おおあめ"},{"japanese":"気象庁は大雪警報を出しました。","romaji":"Kishouchou wa ooyuki keihou o dashimashita.","german":"The Meteorological Agency issued a heavy snow warning.","furigana":"きしょうちょう|おおゆきけいほう|だ"}],"notes":"気象庁 is the Japanese weather service (Japan Meteorological Agency), which issues weather forecasts, severe weather warnings and earthquake information. Typical in the news: 気象庁によると \"according to the weather service\", 気象庁が警報を出す/発表する. 気象 means \"weather (phenomena)\" in the technical sense, 庁 is a government \"agency\" as in 警察庁.","levelNote":"Compound: 気象 (N1) + 庁 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この件については再度検討した方がいいかもね。","romaji":"Kono ken ni tsuite wa saido kentou shita hou ga ii kamo ne.","german":"It might be better to reconsider this matter once more.","furigana":"けん|さいどけんとう|ほう"},{"japanese":"エラーが発生しました。しばらくしてから再度お試しください。","romaji":"Eraa ga hassei shimashita. Shibaraku shite kara saido otameshi kudasai.","german":"An error has occurred. Please try again after a while.","furigana":"はっせい|さいど|ため"},{"japanese":"書類に間違いがあったので、再度提出しました。","romaji":"Shorui ni machigai ga atta node, saido teishutsu shimashita.","german":"There was a mistake in the documents, so I submitted them again.","furigana":"しょるい|まちが|さいどていしゅつ"}],"notes":"再度 means \"again, once more\" and is considerably more formal than もう一度. It is found mainly in business language, announcements and error messages: 再度ご確認ください, 再度お試しください. It usually stands as an adverb directly before the verb; as a noun it also takes の (再度の連絡). In casual conversation, もう一度 or また sounds more natural.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"周りに若い人が多いので、私も刺激を受けています。","romaji":"Mawari ni wakai hito ga ōi node, watashi mo shigeki o ukete imasu.","german":"There are many young people around me, so I'm getting inspired too.","furigana":"まわ|わか|ひと|おお|わたし|しげき|う"},{"japanese":"このシャンプーは刺激が少ないので、子供にも使えます。","romaji":"Kono shanpuu wa shigeki ga sukunai node, kodomo ni mo tsukaemasu.","german":"This shampoo is mild, so it can be used on children too.","furigana":"しげき|すく|こども|つか"},{"japanese":"毎日同じ生活で、刺激が足りない。","romaji":"Mainichi onaji seikatsu de, shigeki ga tarinai.","german":"My life is the same every day, and I lack stimulation.","furigana":"まいにちおな|せいかつ|しげき|た"}],"notes":"刺激 is a \"stimulus\", both physical (肌への刺激, 刺激が強い \"irritating, strong\") and mental (\"inspiration, spur\"): 刺激を受ける \"to be inspired\", 刺激が足りない \"there is a lack of variety\". As a transitive する-verb: 食欲を刺激する \"to stimulate the appetite\". The na-adjective 刺激的 means \"exciting, stimulating\", often also \"provocative\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コウイチは、死ぬまでにトーフグ大学を創立するつもりだ。","romaji":"Kouichi wa, shinu made ni Toufugu daigaku o souritsu suru tsumori da.","german":"Koichi intends to found Tohugu University before he dies.","furigana":"し|だいがく|そうりつ"},{"japanese":"この学校は明治時代に創立されました。","romaji":"Kono gakkou wa Meiji jidai ni souritsu saremashita.","german":"This school was founded in the Meiji period.","furigana":"がっこう|めいじじだい|そうりつ"},{"japanese":"来月、会社の創立50周年のパーティーがあります。","romaji":"Raigetsu, kaisha no souritsu gojuu shuunen no paatii ga arimasu.","german":"Next month there is a party for the company's 50th anniversary.","furigana":"らいげつ|かいしゃ|そうりつ|しゅうねん"}],"notes":"創立 means \"to found, founding\" and is used above all for schools, universities, associations and long-established companies: 創立者, 創立記念日, 創立100周年. As a する-verb it is transitive, often in the passive: 〜年に創立された. 設立 is the sober, also legal term (会社を設立する), 創業 emphasizes the start of a business (創業100年の店).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コウイチ社長から、激励の言葉をいただきました。","romaji":"Kouichi shachou kara, gekirei no kotoba o itadakimashita.","german":"I received words of encouragement from President Koichi.","furigana":"しゃちょう|げきれい|ことば"},{"japanese":"監督は試合の前に選手たちを激励した。","romaji":"Kantoku wa shiai no mae ni senshutachi o gekirei shita.","german":"The coach encouraged the players before the game.","furigana":"かんとく|しあい|まえ|せんしゅ|げきれい"},{"japanese":"多くのファンから激励のメッセージが届いた。","romaji":"Ooku no fan kara gekirei no messeeji ga todoita.","german":"Many messages of encouragement arrived from fans.","furigana":"おお|げきれい|とど"}],"notes":"激励 is \"encouragement, cheering on\" and sounds more formal than 励ます or 応援する. Typical are 激励の言葉 \"encouraging words\", 激励会 \"send-off party (before a tournament)\" and the transitive する-verb: 選手を激励する. The fixed phrase 叱咤激励 means \"to drive on with rebuke and encouragement\". Grammatically it is a noun or する-verb, not an adjective.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"第一印象はあまりいいものではなかったらしいです。","romaji":"Daiichi inshou wa amari ii mono de wa nakatta rashii desu.","german":"The first impression apparently wasn't a very good one.","furigana":"だいいちいんしょう"},{"japanese":"面接では第一印象がとても大切です。","romaji":"Mensetsu de wa daiichi inshou ga totemo taisetsu desu.","german":"First impressions are very important in an interview.","furigana":"めんせつ|だいいちいんしょう|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"彼の第一印象は怖そうな人だったけど、話してみると優しかった。","romaji":"Kare no daiichi inshou wa kowasou na hito datta kedo, hanashite miru to yasashikatta.","german":"My first impression of him was that he looked scary, but when I talked to him he was kind.","furigana":"かれ|だいいちいんしょう|こわ|ひと|はな|やさ"}],"notes":"第一印象 is the \"first impression\" of a person or thing. Common combinations: 第一印象がいい/悪い, 第一印象で決まる \"to depend on the first impression\", 〜の第一印象は…だった. 第一 is the ordinal prefix \"first\" here; on its own 印象 means \"impression\" (印象に残る \"to stay in one's memory\").","levelNote":"Compound: 第一 (N2) + 印象 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どうして差別するんですか？","romaji":"Dōshite sabetsu suru n desu ka?","german":"Why do you discriminate?","furigana":"さべつ"},{"japanese":"国籍で人を差別してはいけません。","romaji":"Kokuseki de hito o sabetsu shite wa ikemasen.","german":"You must not discriminate against people because of their nationality.","furigana":"こくせき|ひと|さべつ"},{"japanese":"彼女は女性だという理由で、職場で差別されていた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa josei da to iu riyuu de, shokuba de sabetsu sarete ita.","german":"She was being discriminated against in the workplace because she was a woman.","furigana":"かのじょ|じょせい|りゆう|しょくば|さべつ"}],"notes":"差別する means \"to discriminate against, to treat unequally and unfairly\" and is transitive: 人を差別する, 国籍で/〜を理由に差別する. The passive 差別される and the noun 差別 in 人種差別 \"racism\" or 差別をなくす are very common. It must be clearly distinguished from the neutral 区別する (\"to distinguish\"): 区別 is factual, while 差別 always implies disadvantage.","levelNote":"Derived from 差別 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コウイチは、人差し指でメガネをクイッと上げるのがくせだ。","romaji":"Kouichi wa, hitosashiyubi de megane o kuitto ageru no ga kuse da.","german":"Koichi has a habit of pushing up his glasses with his index finger.","furigana":"ひとさ|ゆび|あ"},{"japanese":"彼女は人差し指を口に当てて「静かに」と言った。","romaji":"Kanojo wa hitosashiyubi o kuchi ni atete \"shizuka ni\" to itta.","german":"She put her index finger to her mouth and said \"Quiet.\"","furigana":"かのじょ|ひとさ|ゆび|くち|あ|しず|い"},{"japanese":"料理中に包丁で人差し指を切ってしまった。","romaji":"Ryourichuu ni houchou de hitosashiyubi o kitte shimatta.","german":"I cut my index finger with a kitchen knife while cooking.","furigana":"りょうりちゅう|ほうちょう|ひとさ|ゆび|き"}],"notes":"人差し指 is the \"index finger\", literally the finger with which one points at people (人を指す). The other fingers are called 親指, 中指, 薬指 and 小指. Typical: 人差し指で指す \"to point with the index finger\", 人差し指を口に当てる \"to put the finger to one's lips\". Pointing at people with a finger is considered impolite in Japan.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日は「て形」の復習をしましょう。","romaji":"Kyou wa “te-kei” no fukushuu o shimashou.","german":"Let's review the te-form today.","furigana":"きょう|けい|ふくしゅう"},{"japanese":"毎晩、その日に習った漢字を復習しています。","romaji":"Maiban, sono hi ni naratta kanji o fukushuu shite imasu.","german":"Every evening I review the kanji I learned that day.","furigana":"まいばん|ひ|なら|かんじ|ふくしゅう"},{"japanese":"テストの前にもう一度ノートを復習したほうがいいよ。","romaji":"Tesuto no mae ni mou ichido nooto o fukushuu shita hou ga ii yo.","german":"You should go over your notes once more before the test.","furigana":"まえ|いちど|ふくしゅう"}],"notes":"復習 is the review of material already learned; as a する-verb it is transitive: 漢字を復習する, or as a noun 復習をする. The counterpart is 予習 \"preparation\"; together one says 予習・復習. For repeating an action or statement in general, 繰り返す is used instead; 復習 applies only to studying. Do not confuse it with the identically sounding 復讐 \"revenge\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 22"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"授業の前に予習をします。","romaji":"Jugyō no mae ni yoshū o shimasu.","german":"I prepare before class.","furigana":"じゅぎょう|まえ|よしゅう"},{"japanese":"予習しておけば、授業がよく分かります。","romaji":"Yoshuu shite okeba, jugyou ga yoku wakarimasu.","german":"If you prepare in advance, you will understand the lesson much better.","furigana":"よしゅう|じゅぎょう|わ"},{"japanese":"先生は「次の課を予習してきてください」と言いました。","romaji":"Sensei wa “tsugi no ka o yoshuu shite kite kudasai” to iimashita.","german":"The teacher said, \"Please prepare the next lesson before coming.\"","furigana":"せんせい|つぎ|か|よしゅう|い"}],"notes":"予習 is preparing for the upcoming class, that is, studying new material in advance; as a する-verb: 次の課を予習する or 予習をする. It forms the pair 予習・復習 with 復習 \"review\". General preparation for a trip or event is 準備; 予習 applies only to study material. Typical of school and language courses, neutral in tone.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 22"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"とんだ怪事件だったね。","romaji":"Tonda kai jiken datta ne.","german":"What a bizarre mystery that was.","furigana":"かいじけん"},{"japanese":"町で起きた怪事件が、テレビで大きく報道された。","romaji":"Machi de okita kaijiken ga, terebi de ookiku houdou sareta.","german":"The mysterious incident that occurred in the town was widely reported on TV.","furigana":"まち|お|かいじけん|おお|ほうどう"},{"japanese":"この小説は、名探偵が怪事件を解決する話です。","romaji":"Kono shousetsu wa, meitantei ga kaijiken o kaiketsu suru hanashi desu.","german":"This novel is a story in which a famous detective solves a mysterious case.","furigana":"しょうせつ|めいたんてい|かいじけん|かいけつ|はなし"}],"notes":"怪事件 is a \"puzzling, eerie incident\"; the prefix 怪 means \"strange, mysterious\" (cf. 怪談, 怪獣). The word sounds like a newspaper headline or a detective story: 怪事件が起きる, 怪事件を解決する. In everyday speech people say 不思議な事件 or 謎の事件 instead.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"アメリカで有名な学校の怪談はありますか。","romaji":"Amerika de yūmei na gakkō no kaidan wa arimasu ka.","german":"Are there any famous school ghost stories in America?","furigana":"ゆうめい|がっこう|かいだん"},{"japanese":"夏になると、テレビで怪談の番組が増えます。","romaji":"Natsu ni naru to, terebi de kaidan no bangumi ga fuemasu.","german":"When summer comes, there are more ghost story programs on TV.","furigana":"なつ|かいだん|ばんぐみ|ふ"},{"japanese":"キャンプの夜、おじさんが怖い怪談を話してくれた。","romaji":"Kyanpu no yoru, ojisan ga kowai kaidan o hanashite kureta.","german":"On the camping night, my uncle told us a scary ghost story.","furigana":"よる|こわ|かいだん|はな"}],"notes":"怪談 are Japanese \"ghost and horror stories\". Traditionally they are told in summer, because the shivers are supposed to cool you down; well known are 学校の怪談 (\"school ghost stories\") and phrases like 怪談を話す/聞く or 怪談話. Careful when listening: 階段 (\"stairs\") is pronounced the same, which is often used for wordplay.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お店が開くと、みんな我も我もとセール品を目掛けて走り出した。","romaji":"Omise ga hiraku to, minna ware mo ware mo to sēruhin o megakete hashiridashita.","german":"When the shop opened, everyone ran toward the sale items, each one trying to get there first.","furigana":"みせ|ひら|われ|われ|ひん|めが|はし|だ"},{"japanese":"我を忘れて、夢中で絵を描いていた。","romaji":"Ware o wasurete, muchuu de e o kaite ita.","german":"Forgetting myself, I was painting absorbed in the picture.","furigana":"われ|わす|むちゅう|え|か"},{"japanese":"我に返ると、もう夜中の二時だった。","romaji":"Ware ni kaeru to, mou yonaka no niji datta.","german":"When I came to my senses, it was already two in the morning.","furigana":"われ|かえ|よなか|にじ"}],"notes":"我（われ） is an old, literary word for \"I\" or \"oneself\". Today it survives mainly in fixed phrases: 我を忘れる (\"to forget oneself\"), 我に返る (\"to come to one's senses\"), 我も我もと (\"everyone wants to be first\") and 我先に. The plural 我々 (\"we\") is common in speeches and texts. In the rough Kansai dialect, われ can also mean \"you\" in a derogatory way; 我が in 我が強い (\"self-willed\"), on the other hand, is read が.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あなたにも間接的な責任があると思います。","romaji":"Anata ni mo kansetsuteki na sekinin ga aru to omoimasu.","german":"I think you also bear indirect responsibility.","furigana":"かんせつてき|せきにん|おも"},{"japanese":"その話は友達から間接的に聞きました。","romaji":"Sono hanashi wa tomodachi kara kansetsuteki ni kikimashita.","german":"I heard that story indirectly from a friend.","furigana":"はなし|ともだち|かんせつてき|き"},{"japanese":"この部屋は間接照明なので、落ち着いた雰囲気です。","romaji":"Kono heya wa kansetsu shoumei na node, ochitsuita fun'iki desu.","german":"This room has indirect lighting, so it has a calm atmosphere.","furigana":"へや|かんせつしょうめい|お|つ|ふんいき"}],"notes":"間接 (\"indirectness\") is the counterpart of 直接. On its own it is rarely used; people almost always say 間接的な/間接的に (\"indirect, indirectly\") or use it as a prefix in compounds such as 間接照明 (\"indirect lighting\"), 間接税 (\"indirect tax\") or 間接キス. Not to be confused with the identically pronounced 関節 (\"joint\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"うちの母は、株式市場に関する知識はほとんどありません。","romaji":"Uchi no haha wa, kabushiki shijou ni kansuru chishiki wa hotondo arimasen.","german":"My mother has almost no knowledge about the stock market.","furigana":"はは|かぶしきしじょう|かん|ちしき"},{"japanese":"ニュースによると、今日は株式市場が大きく下がったそうです。","romaji":"Nyuusu ni yoru to, kyou wa kabushiki shijou ga ookiku sagatta sou desu.","german":"According to the news, the stock market fell sharply today.","furigana":"きょう|かぶしきしじょう|おお|さ"},{"japanese":"選挙の結果は株式市場にも影響を与える。","romaji":"Senkyo no kekka wa kabushiki shijou ni mo eikyou o ataeru.","german":"The results of the election also affect the stock market.","furigana":"せんきょ|けっか|かぶしきしじょう|えいきょう|あた"}],"notes":"株式市場 is the \"stock market\"; 株式 means \"shares\" (colloquially shortened to 株). The reading is important: in the abstract sense of \"market\", 市場 is read しじょう, whereas for a real market with stalls it is read いちば. Typical combinations: 株式市場が上がる/下がる, 株式市場に影響を与える, 株式市場が暴落する.","levelNote":"Compound: 株式 (N1) + 市場 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ゲッ、なんかこのシャツ、汗臭い！","romaji":"Ge, nanka kono shatsu, ase kusai!","german":"Ugh, this shirt somehow smells of sweat!"},{"japanese":"部活の後なので、ちょっと汗臭いかもしれません。","romaji":"Bukatsu no ato na node, chotto asekusai kamo shiremasen.","german":"It's right after club activities, so I might smell a bit sweaty.","furigana":"ぶかつ|あと|あせくさ"},{"japanese":"夏の満員電車は汗臭くて大変だ。","romaji":"Natsu no man'in densha wa asekusakute taihen da.","german":"A packed train in summer smells of sweat and is awful.","furigana":"なつ|まんいんでんしゃ|あせくさ|たいへん"}],"notes":"汗臭い is an i-adjective: \"smelling of sweat\". The suffix 〜臭い (\"smells of\") also appears in 焦げ臭い (\"smelling burnt\") and 酒臭い (\"smelling of alcohol\"). It sounds direct and can be hurtful when said about a person; about clothes or rooms it is unproblematic. Forms: 汗臭くなる, 汗臭くて.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"河の音が聞こえてきました。","romaji":"Kawa no oto ga kikoete kimashita.","german":"I began to hear the sound of the river.","furigana":"かわ|おと|き"},{"japanese":"大きな河がゆっくりと流れていた。","romaji":"Ookina kawa ga yukkuri to nagarete ita.","german":"A big river was flowing slowly.","furigana":"おお|かわ|なが"},{"japanese":"船で河を下って、海まで行きました。","romaji":"Fune de kawa o kudatte, umi made ikimashita.","german":"We went down the river by boat to the sea.","furigana":"ふね|かわ|くだ|うみ|い"}],"notes":"河 means \"river\" like 川, but tends to refer to large, wide rivers and feels written or literary. In daily life you almost always write 川; 河 appears mainly in compounds such as 運河 (\"canal\"), 河口 (\"river mouth\"), 銀河 (\"Milky Way\") or 大河 (\"great river\"). For the Yellow River, 黄河 is read こうが.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"パナマ運河を見てみたいです。","romaji":"Panama unga o mite mitai desu.","german":"I would like to see the Panama Canal.","furigana":"うんが|み"},{"japanese":"小樽では運河沿いを散歩しました。","romaji":"Otaru de wa unga-zoi o sanpo shimashita.","german":"In Otaru, we took a walk along the canal.","furigana":"|うんがぞ|さんぽ"},{"japanese":"昔、この運河を荷物を運ぶ船がたくさん通っていた。","romaji":"Mukashi, kono unga o nimotsu o hakobu fune ga takusan tootte ita.","german":"Long ago, many boats carrying cargo passed through this canal.","furigana":"むかし|うんが|にもつ|はこ|ふね|とお"}],"notes":"運河 is an artificially constructed, navigable \"canal\" - such as パナマ運河 or スエズ運河; in Japan the 小樽運河 is well known. Typical are 運河沿い (\"along the canal\") and 運河を通る (\"to pass through the canal\"). A natural river is called 川, a small ditch or irrigation channel more often 水路. A television channel, by contrast, is called チャンネル.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「豆腐と河豚のサンドイッチをお願いします。」 「同じく。」","romaji":"Tōfu to fugu no sandoitchi o onegai shimasu. Onajiku.","german":"\"A tofu and fugu sandwich, please.\" \"Same for me.\"","furigana":"とうふ|ふぐ|ねが|おな"},{"japanese":"下関は河豚料理で有名です。","romaji":"Shimonoseki wa fugu ryouri de yuumei desu.","german":"Shimonoseki is famous for its fugu dishes.","furigana":"|ふぐりょうり|ゆうめい"},{"japanese":"河豚には毒があるので、資格のある人しか料理できません。","romaji":"Fugu ni wa doku ga aru node, shikaku no aru hito shika ryouri dekimasen.","german":"Fugu is poisonous, so only qualified people can prepare it.","furigana":"ふぐ|どく|しかく|ひと|りょうり"}],"notes":"河豚 is the \"pufferfish\" (fugu), an expensive delicacy eaten mainly in winter as ふぐ刺し (thinly sliced sashimi) or in hot pot. Since parts of the fish are highly poisonous (ふぐの毒), only chefs with a special license may prepare it. The kanji spelling is rare; people almost always write ふぐ or フグ. The city of Shimonoseki is known for pufferfish.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これは燃えるゴミですか？燃えないゴミですか？","romaji":"Kore wa moeru gomi desu ka? Moenai gomi desu ka?","german":"Is this burnable trash or non-burnable trash?","furigana":"も|も"},{"japanese":"大変だ！隣の家が燃えている！","romaji":"Taihen da! Tonari no ie ga moete iru!","german":"Help! The house next door is on fire!","furigana":"たいへん|となり|いえ|も"},{"japanese":"決勝戦を前に、選手たちは燃えている。","romaji":"Kesshousen o mae ni, senshutachi wa moete iru.","german":"Ahead of the final match, the players are fired up.","furigana":"けっしょうせん|まえ|せんしゅ|も"}],"notes":"燃える is intransitive: \"to burn, to be in flames\" (家が燃える). The transitive counterpart is 燃やす (\"to burn, to set alight\"). In daily life it comes up constantly in garbage sorting: 燃えるゴミ (\"burnable trash\") and 燃えないゴミ. Figuratively it means \"to be fired up\": 試合に燃える, やる気に燃える. It differs from 焼ける (\"to be roasted, to burn down\") and 焦げる (\"to scorch\") in that real flames are involved.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"タバコの火がカーテンに燃え付いて、火事になりました。","romaji":"Tabako no hi ga kaaten ni moetsuite, kaji ni narimashita.","german":"A cigarette ignited the curtains and caused a fire.","furigana":"ひ|も|つ|かじ"},{"japanese":"乾いた木はすぐに燃え付く。","romaji":"Kawaita ki wa sugu ni moetsuku.","german":"Dry wood catches fire easily.","furigana":"かわ|き|も|つ"},{"japanese":"炭はなかなか燃え付かないので、新聞紙を使うといい。","romaji":"Sumi wa nakanaka moetsukanai node, shinbunshi o tsukau to ii.","german":"Charcoal doesn't catch fire easily, so it helps to use newspaper.","furigana":"すみ|も|つ|しんぶんし|つか"}],"notes":"燃え付く (also 燃えつく) means \"to catch fire, to ignite\" and is intransitive: 木が燃え付く, 火がカーテンに燃え付く. The word is rather rare; in everyday speech people usually say 火がつく, 燃え移る (\"to spread to something\") or, in technical language, 引火する. It describes the moment when a material starts to burn.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"全てを失って、独りぼっちになってしまった。","romaji":"Subete o ushinatte, hitoribotchi ni natte shimatta.","german":"I lost everything and ended up all alone.","furigana":"すべ|うしな|ひと"},{"japanese":"祖父は田舎で独りで暮らしています。","romaji":"Sofu wa inaka de hitori de kurashite imasu.","german":"My grandfather lives alone in the countryside.","furigana":"そふ|いなか|ひと|く"},{"japanese":"彼は電車の中で独り言を言っていた。","romaji":"Kare wa densha no naka de hitorigoto o itte ita.","german":"He was talking to himself on the train.","furigana":"かれ|でんしゃ|なか|ひと|ごと|い"}],"notes":"独り stresses \"being alone\" (without company, often also lonely or unmarried), while 一人 mainly means the number \"one person\". Typical are 独りで暮らす, 独り暮らし (also 一人暮らし), 独り言 (\"talking to oneself\") and 独りぼっち (\"all alone\"). In modern Japanese, plain 一人 is often written for \"alone\" too; 独り feels somewhat more emotional. Grammatically it is a noun or adverb, not an adjective.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"桜ちゃんの猫は、目が独特だよね。","romaji":"Sakurachan no neko wa, me ga dokutoku da yo ne.","german":"Sakura's cat has distinctive eyes, doesn't it.","furigana":"さくら|ねこ|め|どくとく"},{"japanese":"納豆には独特なにおいがあります。","romaji":"Nattou ni wa dokutoku na nioi ga arimasu.","german":"Natto has a distinctive smell.","furigana":"なっとう|どくとく"},{"japanese":"着物は日本独特の文化です。","romaji":"Kimono wa Nihon dokutoku no bunka desu.","german":"The kimono is a culture unique to Japan.","furigana":"きもの|にほんどくとく|ぶんか"}],"notes":"独特 means \"distinctive, peculiar, unmistakable\" and is used as a な-adjective or with の: 独特な味, 独特の雰囲気. It is not always praise - 独特なにおい can also mean that a smell takes some getting used to. 〜独特の means \"typical of\": 日本独特の文化. The related 特有 stresses more a feature that belongs only to one group; ユニーク is usually positive.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"スーパーマンは、怪獣に数分遅れて現れた。","romaji":"Sūpāman wa, kaijū ni sūfun okurete arawareta.","german":"Superman showed up a few minutes after the monster.","furigana":"かいじゅう|すうふんおく|あらわ"},{"japanese":"ゴジラは日本で一番有名な怪獣です。","romaji":"Gojira wa Nihon de ichiban yuumei na kaijuu desu.","german":"Godzilla is the most famous kaiju in Japan.","furigana":"にほん|いちばんゆうめい|かいじゅう"},{"japanese":"息子は怪獣のおもちゃで遊ぶのが大好きだ。","romaji":"Musuko wa kaijuu no omocha de asobu no ga daisuki da.","german":"My son loves playing with monster toys.","furigana":"むすこ|かいじゅう|あそ|だいす"}],"notes":"怪獣 are the giant monsters from Japanese films and series (kaiju) such as ゴジラ, often fought by heroes like ウルトラマン. Typical: 怪獣映画, 怪獣が現れる, 怪獣のおもちゃ. More general is 怪物 (\"monster, creature\"); 化け物 is rather a haunting creature or ghost. Children also use 怪獣 playfully for rough games (怪獣ごっこ).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"うちの子の夢は獣医になることです。","romaji":"Uchi no ko no yume wa jūi ni naru koto desu.","german":"My child's dream is to become a veterinarian.","furigana":"こ|ゆめ|じゅうい"},{"japanese":"犬の具合が悪いので、獣医に連れて行きました。","romaji":"Inu no guai ga warui node, juui ni tsurete ikimashita.","german":"My dog wasn't feeling well, so I took it to the vet.","furigana":"いぬ|ぐあい|わる|じゅうい|つ|い"},{"japanese":"動物病院の獣医さんは、とても優しかったです。","romaji":"Doubutsu byouin no juuisan wa, totemo yasashikatta desu.","german":"The vet at the animal hospital was very kind.","furigana":"どうぶつびょういん|じゅうい|やさ"}],"notes":"獣医 is the \"veterinarian\"; the official job title is 獣医師, the field 獣医学. The practice is called 動物病院, but colloquially people also say 獣医に連れて行く. When addressing the vet directly, you say 獣医さん or simply 先生. The kanji 獣 means \"animal, beast\" (けもの).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"狼に育てられた少女は、獣類のように四つ足で歩行をしていました。","romaji":"Ookami ni sodaterareta shoujo wa, juurui no you ni yotsuashi de hokou o shite imashita.","german":"The girl raised by wolves walked on all fours like a beast.","furigana":"おおかみ|そだ|しょうじょ|じゅうるい|よ|あし|ほこう"},{"japanese":"この森には、鳥類や獣類など多くの動物が住んでいる。","romaji":"Kono mori ni wa, chourui ya juurui nado ooku no doubutsu ga sunde iru.","german":"Many animals, including birds and beasts, live in this forest.","furigana":"もり|ちょうるい|じゅうるい|おお|どうぶつ|す"},{"japanese":"獣類の中には、夜に活動するものも多い。","romaji":"Juurui no naka ni wa, yoru ni katsudou suru mono mo ooi.","german":"Among the beasts, many are active at night.","furigana":"じゅうるい|なか|よる|かつどう|おお"}],"notes":"獣類 is a literary or technical collective term for \"beasts, mammals\", especially furry four-legged animals (獣, けもの). It appears in series such as 鳥類 (\"birds\") and 魚類 (\"fish\"). In modern biological usage people mostly say 哺乳類, in everyday speech simply 動物. Grammatically it is a noun, not an adjective.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その男は野獣のような目をしていた。","romaji":"Sono otoko wa yajū no yō na me o shite ita.","german":"That man had eyes like a wild beast.","furigana":"おとこ|やじゅう|め"},{"japanese":"『美女と野獣』は私の好きな映画です。","romaji":"\"Bijo to Yajuu\" wa watashi no suki na eiga desu.","german":"\"Beauty and the Beast\" is my favorite movie.","furigana":"びじょ|やじゅう|わたし|す|えいが"},{"japanese":"昔、この山には野獣が多くて、夜に出歩く人はいなかった。","romaji":"Mukashi, kono yama ni wa yajuu ga ookute, yoru ni dearuku hito wa inakatta.","german":"Long ago, this mountain had many wild beasts, and no one went out at night.","furigana":"むかし|やま|やじゅう|おお|よる|である|ひと"}],"notes":"野獣 is a \"wild animal, beast of prey, brute\" and sounds dramatic or literary. It is often applied to people figuratively: 野獣のような目 \"eyes like a beast of prey\", i.e. brutal or wild. Well known is the title 美女と野獣 (\"Beauty and the Beast\"). For wild animals in a neutral, factual sense, people say 野生動物. Grammatically it is a noun, not an adjective.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"弟はだれからもお誕生日おめでとうって言ってもらえなかったと言って、すねていましたよ。","romaji":"Otōto wa dare kara mo o tanjōbi omedetō tte itte moraenakatta to itte, sunete imashita yo.","german":"My younger brother was sulking, saying that nobody had wished him a happy birthday.","furigana":"おとうと|たんじょうび|い|い"},{"japanese":"お誕生日おめでとうございます！素敵な一年になりますように。","romaji":"Otanjoubi omedetou gozaimasu! Suteki na ichinen ni narimasu you ni.","german":"Happy birthday! I hope you have a wonderful year.","furigana":"たんじょうび|すてき|いちねん"},{"japanese":"ゆいちゃん、お誕生日おめでとう！これ、プレゼント。","romaji":"Yui-chan, otanjoubi omedetou! Kore, purezento.","german":"Yui, happy birthday! Here, this is a present.","furigana":"たんじょうび"}],"notes":"お誕生日おめでとう is the standard greeting \"Happy birthday!\". With friends and family people often say the shorter 誕生日おめでとう; toward superiors or in cards it becomes polite with ございます: お誕生日おめでとうございます. As a quotation it takes って/と: お誕生日おめでとうって言う. ハッピーバースデー is also common.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あ、あそこに自販機がある！","romaji":"A, asoko ni jihanki ga aru!","german":"Oh, there's a vending machine over there!","furigana":"じはんき"},{"japanese":"自販機で冷たいお茶を買った。","romaji":"Jihanki de tsumetai ocha o katta.","german":"I bought a cold tea from a vending machine.","furigana":"じはんき|つめ|ちゃ|か"},{"japanese":"この自販機は千円札が使えません。","romaji":"Kono jihanki wa sen'en satsu ga tsukaemasen.","german":"This vending machine doesn't accept 1,000-yen bills.","furigana":"じはんき|せんえんさつ|つか"}],"notes":"自販機 is the common short form of 自動販売機 (\"vending machine\"). In Japan they stand almost everywhere, mainly for drinks: 自販機で買う, 自販機のジュース. Ticket machines, by contrast, are called 券売機, and cash machines ATM. The short form is usual in conversation; official texts tend to use 自動販売機.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"僕は建造物を造るのに使われる重機が好きなんですよ。","romaji":"Boku wa kenzoubutsu o tsukuru no ni tsukawareru juuki ga suki na n desu yo.","german":"I like the heavy machinery used to build structures.","furigana":"ぼく|けんぞうぶつ|つく|つか|じゅうき|す"},{"japanese":"この橋は100年前に造られました。","romaji":"Kono hashi wa hyakunen mae ni tsukuraremashita.","german":"This bridge was built 100 years ago.","furigana":"はし|ねんまえ|つく"},{"japanese":"この酒蔵では、昔ながらの方法で日本酒を造っている。","romaji":"Kono sakagura de wa, mukashinagara no houhou de nihonshu o tsukutte iru.","german":"This sake brewery makes sake in the traditional way.","furigana":"さかぐら|むかし|ほうほう|にほんしゅ|つく"}],"notes":"造る is a spelling variant of つくる for large, elaborate production: buildings, bridges, ships (橋を造る, 船を造る) as well as brewing alcohol (酒を造る). For everyday things like food or plans, you write 作る, and for artistic creation sometimes 創る. The verb is transitive and often appears in the passive: 〜年前に造られた.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の友達は大学院で造園について学んでいます。","romaji":"Watashi no tomodachi wa daigakuin de zouen ni tsuite manande imasu.","german":"My friend is studying landscape gardening in graduate school.","furigana":"わたし|ともだち|だいがくいん|ぞうえん|まな"},{"japanese":"父は造園の会社で、庭を作る仕事をしています。","romaji":"Chichi wa zouen no kaisha de, niwa o tsukuru shigoto o shite imasu.","german":"My father works at a landscaping company, building gardens.","furigana":"ちち|ぞうえん|かいしゃ|にわ|つく|しごと"},{"japanese":"この公園は有名な造園家がデザインしたそうです。","romaji":"Kono kouen wa yuumei na zouenka ga dezain shita sou desu.","german":"I hear this park was designed by a famous landscape designer.","furigana":"こうえん|ゆうめい|ぞうえんか"}],"notes":"造園 is \"garden and landscape design\" as a profession or field. Typical are 造園業 (\"landscape gardening business\"), 造園会社 and 造園家 (\"landscape architect\"). Someone who tends gardens and prunes trees is called a 庭師; gardening as a hobby is called ガーデニング. The word belongs more to technical and professional language.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"すごいだろ？ガレージを改造して子供部屋にしたんだ。","romaji":"Sugoi daro? Gareeji o kaizou shite kodomobeya ni shita n da.","german":"Impressive, isn't it? I converted the garage into a kid's room.","furigana":"かいぞう|こどもべや"},{"japanese":"兄は車を改造するのが趣味だ。","romaji":"Ani wa kuruma o kaizou suru no ga shumi da.","german":"My older brother's hobby is modifying cars.","furigana":"あに|くるま|かいぞう|しゅみ"},{"japanese":"首相は来月、内閣を改造する予定だ。","romaji":"Shushou wa raigetsu, naikaku o kaizou suru yotei da.","german":"The prime minister plans to reshuffle the cabinet next month.","furigana":"しゅしょう|らいげつ|ないかく|かいぞう|よてい"}],"notes":"改造 means \"to remodel, to reconfigure, to modify\" and, as a する-verb, is transitive: XをYに改造する (\"to convert X into Y\"). Well-known compounds: 改造車 (\"tuned car\"), 内閣改造 (\"cabinet reshuffle\"); game modding is also called 改造. For renovating an apartment, people say リフォーム in everyday speech, for structural alterations 改築; 改良 means \"to improve\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この湖は自然の湖ではなく、人造湖です。","romaji":"Kono mizuumi wa shizen no mizuumi de wa naku, jinzouko desu.","german":"This lake is not a natural lake but a man-made one.","furigana":"みずうみ|しぜん|みずうみ|じんぞうこ"},{"japanese":"漫画に出てくる人造人間十八号は、とても強い。","romaji":"Manga ni dete kuru jinzou ningen juuhachigou wa, totemo tsuyoi.","german":"Android No. 18 who appears in the manga is very strong.","furigana":"まんが|で|じんぞうにんげんじゅうはちごう|つよ"},{"japanese":"このテーブルは人造大理石でできています。","romaji":"Kono teeburu wa jinzou dairiseki de dekite imasu.","german":"This table is made of artificial marble.","furigana":"じんぞうだいりせき"}],"notes":"人造 means \"artificial, made by humans\" and usually stands as a prefix or with の before nouns: 人造湖 (\"reservoir\"), 人造大理石 (\"artificial marble\"), 人造人間 (\"artificial human, android\"). In everyday modern usage 人工 is much more common (人工知能, 人工衛星); 人造 seems somewhat old-fashioned or like science fiction. 腎臓 (\"kidney\") is pronounced the same.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この町には酒造会社がたくさんあります。","romaji":"Kono machi ni wa shuzō kaisha ga takusan arimasu.","german":"There are many sake brewing companies in this town.","furigana":"まち|しゅぞうかいしゃ"},{"japanese":"祖父の家は昔から酒造の仕事をしています。","romaji":"Sofu no ie wa mukashi kara shuzou no shigoto o shite imasu.","german":"My grandfather's family has been in the sake brewing business for generations.","furigana":"そふ|いえ|むかし|しゅぞう|しごと"},{"japanese":"酒造には、きれいな水とおいしいお米が欠かせない。","romaji":"Shuzou ni wa, kirei na mizu to oishii okome ga kakasenai.","german":"Clean water and delicious rice are essential for sake brewing.","furigana":"しゅぞう|みず|こめ|か"}],"notes":"酒造 means \"brewing of sake\" (and in general the production of alcoholic drinks) and appears almost only in compounds: 酒造会社, 酒造業, or as part of company names (〇〇酒造). The brewery building itself is called 酒蔵; as a verb one says 酒を造る. For beer, people rather speak of a ビール工場.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"カナエは、たまに気違いじみたダンスをしながらオフィスに入ってくる。","romaji":"Kanae wa, tama ni kichigaijimita dansu o shinagara ofisu ni haitte kuru.","german":"Kanae sometimes comes into the office doing a frenzied, crazy-looking dance.","furigana":"きちが|はい"},{"japanese":"昔の小説には「気違い」という言葉がよく出てくるが、今は差別語とされている。","romaji":"Mukashi no shōsetsu ni wa \"kichigai\" to iu kotoba ga yoku dete kuru ga, ima wa sabetsugo to sarete iru.","german":"The word \"気違い\" often appears in old novels, but today it is considered discriminatory.","furigana":"むかし|しょうせつ|きちが|ことば|で|いま|さべつご"},{"japanese":"父は若い頃、釣り気違いと呼ばれるほど釣りに夢中だった。","romaji":"Chichi wa wakai koro, tsuri kichigai to yobareru hodo tsuri ni muchū datta.","german":"In his youth, my father was so absorbed in fishing that he was called a fishing fanatic.","furigana":"ちち|わか|ころ|つ|きちが|よ|つ|むちゅう"}],"notes":"Caution: 気違い is a derogatory, discriminatory expression (差別語) that is avoided in broadcasting and the press; one should only know it passively. It is often written in kana (きちがい, キチガイ). It means \"madman\" and, as a suffix, \"-fanatic, -maniac\" (釣り気違い, 野球気違い); today one neutrally says 〜マニア, 〜好き or 〜狂 instead. The derivation 気違いじみた means \"seeming crazy, insane\". For mental illnesses, 精神疾患 or 心の病 are appropriate.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人違いでした。すみません。","romaji":"Hitochigai deshita. Sumimasen.","german":"Sorry, I mistook you for someone else.","furigana":"ひとちが"},{"japanese":"「田中さん？」「いいえ、人違いですよ。」","romaji":"\"Tanaka-san?\" \"Iie, hitochigai desu yo.\"","german":"\"Are you Tanaka?\" \"No, you've got the wrong person.\"","furigana":"たなか|ひとちが"},{"japanese":"後ろ姿が友達に似ていたので、人違いをして声をかけてしまった。","romaji":"Ushirosugata ga tomodachi ni nite ita node, hitochigai o shite koe o kakete shimatta.","german":"From behind, he looked like my friend, so I mistook him for him and called out.","furigana":"うし|すがた|ともだち|に|ひとちが|こえ"}],"notes":"人違い is \"mistaken identity\": one takes someone for another person. Typical are 人違いをする and the apology 人違いでした, すみません; someone who is addressed wrongly answers 人違いですよ. To be distinguished from 勘違い (\"misunderstanding, wrong assumption\"), which is not limited to people.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日、超恥ずかしい読み違いをしてしまった。","romaji":"Kyō, chō hazukashii yomichigai o shite shimatta.","german":"Today I made a super embarrassing misreading.","furigana":"きょう|ちょうは|よ|ちが"},{"japanese":"「雰囲気」を「ふいんき」と読むのは、よくある読み違いだ。","romaji":"\"Fun'iki\" o \"fuinki\" to yomu no wa, yoku aru yomichigai da.","german":"Reading \"雰囲気\" as \"ふいんき\" is a common misreading.","furigana":"ふんいき|よ|よ|ちが"},{"japanese":"地図の読み違いで、道に迷ってしまった。","romaji":"Chizu no yomichigai de, michi ni mayotte shimatta.","german":"I misread the map and got lost.","furigana":"ちず|よ|ちが|みち|まよ"}],"notes":"読み違い is a \"reading error\", e.g. a wrong kanji reading or a misread value. One says 読み違いをする; the variant 読み間違い is more common in everyday use (verbs: 読み間違える or 読み違える). Figuratively it can also mean a \"misjudgment\" of a situation (状況の読み違い).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"やっぱり、聞くと見るでは大違いだね。","romaji":"Yappari, kiku to miru de wa ōchigai da ne.","german":"As expected, hearing about something and seeing it are worlds apart.","furigana":"き|み|おおちが"},{"japanese":"去年と比べると、今年の売り上げは大違いだ。","romaji":"Kyonen to kuraberu to, kotoshi no uriage wa ōchigai da.","german":"Compared to last year, this year's sales are completely different.","furigana":"きょねん|くら|ことし|う|あ|おおちが"},{"japanese":"彼を簡単な相手だと思ったら大違いだよ。","romaji":"Kare o kantan na aite da to omottara ōchigai da yo.","german":"If you think he's an easy opponent, you're greatly mistaken.","furigana":"かれ|かんたん|あいて|おも|おおちが"}],"notes":"大違い is a colloquial, emphatic form of 違い: AとBでは大違いだ (\"A and B are worlds apart\"). Very common is the pattern 〜と思ったら大違いだ (\"anyone who thinks that ... is gravely mistaken\"), which shows the second meaning \"big mistake\". In a neutral style people more often say 全然違う or 大きな違いがある.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の弟には、針医になるという夢があります。","romaji":"Watashi no otouto ni wa, harii ni naru to iu yume ga arimasu.","german":"My younger brother has a dream of becoming an acupuncturist.","furigana":"わたし|おとうと|はりい|ゆめ"},{"japanese":"祖母は腰が痛いとき、近所の針医に通っている。","romaji":"Sobo wa koshi ga itai toki, kinjo no harii ni kayotte iru.","german":"When her back hurts, my grandmother goes to an acupuncturist in the neighborhood.","furigana":"そぼ|こし|いた|きんじょ|はりい|かよ"},{"japanese":"昔の針医には、目の見えない人が多かったそうだ。","romaji":"Mukashi no harii ni wa, me no mienai hito ga ookatta sou da.","german":"I hear that many acupuncturists in the old days were blind.","furigana":"むかし|はりい|め|み|ひと|おお"}],"notes":"針医 is an \"acupuncturist\", someone who treats with needles (はり). The word is rather old-fashioned; today people usually say はり師 or 鍼灸師 (acupuncture and moxibustion). For the acupuncture needle, the kanji 鍼 is often used instead of 針. Colloquially people simply say はりに行く (\"to go for acupuncture\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ねむれなかったので、時計の秒針を見て夜を過ごしました。","romaji":"Nemurenakatta node, tokei no byōshin o mite yoru o sugoshimashita.","german":"I couldn't sleep, so I spent the night watching the second hand of the clock.","furigana":"とけい|びょうしん|み|よる|す"},{"japanese":"電池が切れたのか、秒針が止まっている。","romaji":"Denchi ga kireta no ka, byoushin ga tomatte iru.","german":"The second hand has stopped; maybe the battery has run out.","furigana":"でんち|き|びょうしん|と"},{"japanese":"試験中、秒針の音がとても大きく聞こえた。","romaji":"Shikenchuu, byoushin no oto ga totemo ookiku kikoeta.","german":"During the exam, the sound of the second hand seemed very loud.","furigana":"しけんちゅう|びょうしん|おと|おお|き"}],"notes":"秒針 is the \"second hand\" of a clock. The other hands are called 長針 (\"minute hand\", also 分針) and 短針 (\"hour hand\"); together they are 時計の針. Typical: 秒針が動く/止まる, 秒針の音 (\"the ticking\").","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"工事の人が、壁の中から古い鉛管を見つけた。","romaji":"Kouji no hito ga, kabe no naka kara furui enkan o mitsuketa.","german":"A worker found an old lead pipe inside the wall.","furigana":"こうじ|ひと|かべ|なか|ふる|えんかん|み"},{"japanese":"鉛管は体に悪いので、今はあまり使われていない。","romaji":"Enkan wa karada ni warui node, ima wa amari tsukawarete inai.","german":"Lead pipes are bad for the body, so they are rarely used now.","furigana":"えんかん|からだ|わる|いま|つか"},{"japanese":"古い水道の鉛管を新しい管に交換した。","romaji":"Furui suidou no enkan o atarashii kan ni koukan shita.","german":"We replaced the old lead water pipes with new pipes.","furigana":"ふる|すいどう|えんかん|あたら|かん|こうかん"}],"notes":"鉛管 is a \"lead pipe\". 鉛 is read えん in Sino-Japanese compounds (鉛筆, 鉛管, 鉛毒) but なまり (\"lead\") on its own. Lead pipes were formerly used for water supply and are now being replaced because of the health risk; the general term for water supply pipes is 水道管. The word is technical.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鉛筆には鉛は入っていないので、鉛毒はありません。","romaji":"Enpitsu ni wa namari wa haitte inai node, endoku wa arimasen.","german":"Pencils contain no lead, so there is no lead poisoning.","furigana":"えんぴつ|なまり|はい|えんどく"},{"japanese":"江戸時代のおしろいには鉛が入っていて、鉛毒に苦しむ人もいた。","romaji":"Edo jidai no oshiroi ni wa namari ga haitte ite, endoku ni kurushimu hito mo ita.","german":"Edo-period face powder contained lead, and some people suffered from lead poisoning.","furigana":"えどじだい|なまり|はい|えんどく|くる|ひと"},{"japanese":"古い水道管による鉛毒が心配されている。","romaji":"Furui suidoukan ni yoru endoku ga shinpai sarete iru.","german":"There are concerns about lead poisoning from old water pipes.","furigana":"ふる|すいどうかん|えんどく|しんぱい"}],"notes":"鉛毒 refers to the \"poison in lead\" or \"lead poisoning\". The word is literary and somewhat old-fashioned; for the illness, people mostly say 鉛中毒 today. 鉛 is read Sino-Japanese えん here. Historically well known are lead poisonings caused by lead-containing white makeup (おしろい) in the Edo period. Grammatically it is a noun, not an adjective.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ごめん。気に障ったのなら、あやまるよ。","romaji":"Gomen. Ki ni sawatta no nara, ayamaru yo.","german":"Sorry. If I offended you, I apologize.","furigana":"き|さわ"},{"japanese":"無理をすると体に障りますよ。","romaji":"Muri o suru to karada ni sawarimasu yo.","german":"If you overdo it, it will harm your health.","furigana":"むり|からだ|さわ"},{"japanese":"彼の偉そうな話し方が、どうも気に障る。","romaji":"Kare no erasou na hanashikata ga, doumo ki ni sawaru.","german":"His arrogant way of speaking somehow gets on my nerves.","furigana":"かれ|えら|はな|かた|き|さわ"}],"notes":"障る is intransitive and takes に: something \"harms\" or \"disturbs\" a person or thing. It appears almost always in fixed phrases: 気に障る (\"to offend, to annoy, to rub someone the wrong way\"), 体に障る (\"to harm one's health\") and 癪に障る (\"to irritate\"). Not to be confused with the identically read 触る (\"to touch\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本にはどんな社会保障がありますか。","romaji":"Nippon ni wa donna shakai hoshō ga arimasu ka.","german":"What kind of social security does Japan have?","furigana":"にっぽん|しゃかいほしょう"},{"japanese":"憲法は国民の自由を保障している。","romaji":"Kenpou wa kokumin no jiyuu o hoshou shite iru.","german":"The constitution guarantees the freedom of the people.","furigana":"けんぽう|こくみん|じゆう|ほしょう"},{"japanese":"この会社は社員の生活をしっかり保障してくれる。","romaji":"Kono kaisha wa shain no seikatsu o shikkari hoshou shite kureru.","german":"This company reliably secures its employees' livelihoods.","furigana":"かいしゃ|しゃいん|せいかつ|ほしょう"}],"notes":"保障 means \"to guarantee, to safeguard\" - to protect a condition, rights or safety: 社会保障 (\"social security\"), 安全保障 (\"security policy\"), 憲法で保障された権利, 生活を保障する. Three homophones are often confused: 保証 (\"guarantee, warranty, surety\", as in 保証書, 保証人), 保障 (\"safeguarding a condition\") and 補償 (\"compensation\"). As a する-verb it is transitive.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お正月は一人でゆっくりして過ごす予定です。","romaji":"Oshougatsu wa hitori de yukkuri shite sugosu yotei desu.","german":"I plan to spend the New Year holidays relaxing by myself.","furigana":"しょうがつ|ひとり|す|よてい"},{"japanese":"週末は家族と過ごすことが多いです。","romaji":"Shuumatsu wa kazoku to sugosu koto ga ooi desu.","german":"I often spend weekends with my family.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|かぞく|す|おお"},{"japanese":"寒い日が続きますが、いかがお過ごしですか。","romaji":"Samui hi ga tsuzukimasu ga, ikaga osugoshi desu ka.","german":"The cold days continue; how are you getting along?","furigana":"さむ|ひ|つづ|す"}],"notes":"過ごす is transitive and means \"to spend (time)\": 休みを過ごす, 家族と過ごす, 楽しく過ごす. It is very common in polite phrases such as いかがお過ごしですか (\"How have you been?\", in letters) or 良いお年をお過ごしください. The intransitive counterpart is 過ぎる (\"to pass\": 時間が過ぎる). As a verb suffix, 〜過ごす means \"to overlook, to miss\" (見過ごす, 寝過ごす).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"スマホが垂直に立っていてびっくりした。","romaji":"Sumaho ga suichoku ni tatte ite bikkuri shita.","german":"I was surprised that the smartphone was standing upright.","furigana":"すいちょく|た"},{"japanese":"この線に垂直な線を引きなさい。","romaji":"Kono sen ni suichoku na sen o hikinasai.","german":"Draw a line perpendicular to this line.","furigana":"せん|すいちょく|せん|ひ"},{"japanese":"崖がほぼ垂直で、登るのは無理だ。","romaji":"Gake ga hobo suichoku de, noboru no wa muri da.","german":"The cliff is almost vertical, so climbing it is impossible.","furigana":"がけ|すいちょく|のぼ|むり"}],"notes":"垂直 means \"perpendicular, vertical\" and is used as a な-adjective or with の: 垂直に立つ, 垂直な壁, 垂直の線. In geometry, 〜に垂直 means \"perpendicular to ...\". The opposite is 水平 \"horizontal\". In everyday speech, for the direction \"upright, lengthwise\", people more often say 縦; 垂直 sounds more precise and technical.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どこかでちょっと涼む？","romaji":"Doko ka de chotto suzumu?","german":"Shall we go somewhere to cool off a bit?","furigana":"すず"},{"japanese":"暑いから、木陰で少し涼もう。","romaji":"Atsui kara, kokage de sukoshi suzumou.","german":"It's hot, so let's cool off a little in the shade of a tree.","furigana":"あつ|こかげ|すこ|すず"},{"japanese":"夕方になると、祖父は縁側に座って涼んでいる。","romaji":"Yuugata ni naru to, sofu wa engawa ni suwatte suzunde iru.","german":"In the evening, my grandfather sits on the veranda and enjoys the cool air.","furigana":"ゆうがた|そふ|えんがわ|すわ|すず"}],"notes":"涼む means \"to cool off (in a cool place), to enjoy the cool air\". The place takes で: 木陰で涼む, 川辺で涼む, 縁側で涼む. It is intransitive and always describes people, not things: food or tea cools down with 冷める, and one cools it actively with 冷ます. Related is the adjective 涼しい \"pleasantly cool\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あいつは犬のように従順だ。","romaji":"Aitsu wa inu no yō ni jūjun da.","german":"That guy is as obedient as a dog.","furigana":"いぬ|じゅうじゅん"},{"japanese":"子どもの頃は、親に従順な性格でした。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro wa, oya ni juujun na seikaku deshita.","german":"As a child, I had a personality that was obedient to my parents.","furigana":"こ|ころ|おや|じゅうじゅん|せいかく"},{"japanese":"上司に従順なだけでは、いい仕事はできない。","romaji":"Joushi ni juujun na dake de wa, ii shigoto wa dekinai.","german":"Merely being obedient to your boss won't let you do good work.","furigana":"じょうし|じゅうじゅん|しごと"}],"notes":"従順 is a な-adjective meaning \"obedient, docile\"; whom one obeys is marked with に: 親に従順な子ども. For animals the word is positive, for people it can sound critical, \"submissive\". 素直, by contrast, means \"honest, open to advice\" and is almost always praise; 言いなり (\"at someone's beck and call\") is clearly negative. The word is slightly literary and related to the verb 従う.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"何故そんなヒドイことが言えるんだろう。","romaji":"Naze sonna hidoi koto ga ieru n darō.","german":"I wonder how anyone can say such terrible things.","furigana":"なぜ|い"},{"japanese":"何故今日は学校に行かないんですか。","romaji":"Naze kyō wa gakkō ni ikanai n desu ka.","german":"Why aren't you going to school today?","furigana":"なぜきょう|がっこう|い"}],"notes":"何故 is the interrogative adverb \"why\", read なぜ. It is often written in hiragana as なぜ. It asks for the reason of an action or state. なぜ…んだろう can express that you are wondering to yourself about a reason. In a direct question it is followed by, for example, ですか or the polite verb form with か.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"怪物には友達がほとんどいなかった。","romaji":"Kaibutsu ni wa tomodachi ga hotondo inakatta.","german":"The monster had almost no friends.","furigana":"かいぶつ|ともだち"},{"japanese":"この湖には怪物が住んでいるという伝説がある。","romaji":"Kono mizuumi ni wa kaibutsu ga sunde iru to iu densetsu ga aru.","german":"There is a legend that a monster lives in this lake.","furigana":"みずうみ|かいぶつ|す|でんせつ"},{"japanese":"あの選手は高校生なのに、160キロの球を投げる怪物だ。","romaji":"Ano senshu wa koukousei na noni, hyakurokujukkiro no tama o nageru kaibutsu da.","german":"Although that player is a high schooler, he's a monster who throws 160 km/h pitches.","furigana":"せんしゅ|こうこうせい|たま|な|かいぶつ"}],"notes":"怪物 is a \"monster, creature\" from legends, fairy tales and films: 怪物が現れる, 怪物の伝説. Figuratively it refers to people with almost uncanny strength or talent, especially in sports: 怪物投手 \"phenomenal pitcher\". モンスター is the loanword used in games and in phrases like モンスターペアレント; 化け物 sounds more ghostly and colloquial, and 妖怪 are Japanese folk spirits.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これって、ほぼ独裁政権みたいなものですよね。","romaji":"Kore tte, hobo dokusai seiken mitai na mono desu yo ne.","german":"This is pretty much like a dictatorial regime, isn't it?","furigana":"どくさいせいけん"},{"japanese":"その国では長い間、軍の独裁が続いた。","romaji":"Sono kuni de wa nagai aida, gun no dokusai ga tsuzuita.","german":"In that country, military dictatorship continued for a long time.","furigana":"くに|なが|あいだ|ぐん|どくさい|つづ"},{"japanese":"うちの部長は何でも一人で決める独裁者みたいな人だ。","romaji":"Uchi no buchou wa nan demo hitori de kimeru dokusaisha mitai na hito da.","german":"My department head is like a dictator who decides everything by himself.","furigana":"ぶちょう|なん|ひとり|き|どくさいしゃ|ひと"}],"notes":"独裁 is \"sole rule, dictatorship\". It mostly appears in compounds: 独裁者 \"dictator\", 独裁政権 \"dictatorial regime\", 独裁政治, 軍の独裁 \"military dictatorship\". Figuratively it is used to criticize bosses or leaders who decide everything alone: 社長の独裁. The opposite concept is 民主主義 \"democracy\"; the verb 独裁する is rare.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"万年筆を落とされましたよ。","romaji":"Mannenhitsu o otosaremashita yo.","german":"You dropped your fountain pen.","furigana":"まんねんひつ|お"},{"japanese":"この万年筆で手紙を書きます。","romaji":"Kono mannenhitsu de tegami o kakimasu.","german":"I'll write the letter with this fountain pen.","furigana":"まんねんひつ|てがみ|か"}],"notes":"万年筆 is a fountain pen. The reading on this card is まんねんひつ. With を you name it as the object of an action, with で as the writing tool. 落とされましたよ is a respectful form here for \"you dropped it\". The first n closes まん; then ねん begins with another n.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"もう少しここの幅を狭めることはできますか？","romaji":"Mou sukoshi koko no haba o sebameru koto wa dekimasu ka?","german":"Could you make the width here a little narrower?","furigana":"すこ|はば|せば"},{"japanese":"検索の範囲を狭めると、目的の情報が見つかりやすい。","romaji":"Kensaku no han'i o sebameru to, mokuteki no jouhou ga mitsukariyasui.","german":"Narrowing the search range makes it easier to find the information you want.","furigana":"けんさく|はんい|せば|もくてき|じょうほう|み"},{"japanese":"一つの考えにこだわると、自分の視野を狭めることになる。","romaji":"Hitotsu no kangae ni kodawaru to, jibun no shiya o sebameru koto ni naru.","german":"If you cling to a single idea, you end up narrowing your own outlook.","furigana":"ひと|かんが|じぶん|しや|せば"}],"notes":"狭める means \"to make narrower; to narrow down\" and is transitive: 幅を狭める, 間隔を狭める, 範囲を狭める \"to narrow the range\", 視野を狭める \"to narrow one's view\". The intransitive partner is 狭まる \"to become narrower\" (道幅が狭まる). Opposites are 広げる / 広がる. For reducing a number, one does not use 狭める but 減らす.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"右腕を骨折したため、しばらくの間仕事ができません。","romaji":"Migiude o kossetsu shita tame, shibaraku no aida shigoto ga dekimasen.","german":"I broke my right arm, so I can't work for a while.","furigana":"みぎうで|こっせつ|あいだしごと"},{"japanese":"テニスのやりすぎで右腕が痛くなった。","romaji":"Tenisu no yarisugi de migiude ga itaku natta.","german":"My right arm started to hurt from playing too much tennis.","furigana":"みぎうで|いた"},{"japanese":"田中さんは社長の右腕として会社を支えてきた。","romaji":"Tanaka-san wa shachou no migiude to shite kaisha o sasaete kita.","german":"Mr. Tanaka has supported the company as the president's right-hand man.","furigana":"たなか|しゃちょう|みぎうで|かいしゃ|ささ"}],"notes":"右腕 is literally the \"right arm\" (counterpart 左腕). Very common is the figurative meaning \"right-hand man, closest confidant\": 社長の右腕として働く. In baseball, 右腕 in 右腕投手 \"right-handed pitcher\" is read うわん. For arms in general 腕 suffices; 右腕 stresses the side.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大学に行くためのお金を捻出するためにアルバイトを頑張ってます。","romaji":"Daigaku ni iku tame no okane o nenshutsu suru tame ni arubaito o ganbattemasu.","german":"I'm working hard at my part-time job to raise the money to go to university.","furigana":"だいがく|い|かね|ねんしゅつ|がんば"},{"japanese":"忙しくても、毎日三十分は運動の時間を捻出している。","romaji":"Isogashikute mo, mainichi sanjuppun wa undou no jikan o nenshutsu shite iru.","german":"Even when I'm busy, I make time for thirty minutes of exercise every day.","furigana":"いそが|まいにちさんじゅっぷん|うんどう|じかん|ねんしゅつ"},{"japanese":"市は予算が足りず、橋の修理費用の捻出に苦労している。","romaji":"Shi wa yosan ga tarizu, hashi no shuuri hiyou no nenshutsu ni kurou shite iru.","german":"The city doesn't have enough budget and is struggling to raise the repair costs for the bridge.","furigana":"し|よさん|た|はし|しゅうりひよう|ねんしゅつ|くろう"}],"notes":"捻出する means to laboriously \"raise, set aside\" money or time although there is hardly anything left: 費用を捻出する, 時間を捻出する, 資金の捻出に苦労する. More rarely it also means \"to work out\" a plan or an idea. The word sounds rather written; in conversation people often say 時間を作る or お金を工面する. 稼ぐ, by contrast, is simply \"to earn\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"しばらく様子を見ましょう。","romaji":"Shibaraku yōsu o mimashō.","german":"Let's wait and see for a while.","furigana":"ようす|み"},{"japanese":"最近、弟の様子がおかしいので心配だ。","romaji":"Saikin, otouto no yousu ga okashii node shinpai da.","german":"My younger brother has been acting strange lately, so I am worried.","furigana":"さいきん|おとうと|ようす|しんぱい"},{"japanese":"彼はまだ帰りたくない様子だった。","romaji":"Kare wa mada kaeritakunai yousu datta.","german":"He didn't seem to want to go home yet.","furigana":"かれ|かえ|ようす"}],"notes":"様子 describes how something or someone appears from the outside: \"state, situation, appearance, behavior, sign\". Fixed expressions are 様子を見る \"to wait and see\", 様子がおかしい \"to behave strangely\" and 〜様子だ \"it seems that ...\" (more written). 状態 is the objective, often technical condition, and 状況 the overall situation; 様子 emphasizes the visible impression.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 40"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"夜中の二時にチャイムが鳴った。","romaji":"Yonaka no niji ni chaimu ga natta.","german":"The doorbell rang at two in the middle of the night.","furigana":"よなか|にじ|な"},{"japanese":"夜中に何度も目が覚めてしまった。","romaji":"Yonaka ni nando mo me ga samete shimatta.","german":"I woke up many times during the night.","furigana":"よなか|なんど|め|さ"},{"japanese":"夜中に大きな音で音楽を聞くのはやめてください。","romaji":"Yonaka ni ōkina oto de ongaku o kiku no wa yamete kudasai.","german":"Please stop listening to loud music in the middle of the night.","furigana":"よなか|おお|おと|おんがく|き"}],"notes":"夜中 refers to the time in the middle of the night, roughly from midnight until before dawn. It is usually used with に (夜中に目が覚める \"to wake up at night\") or attributively with の (夜中の二時). The general 夜 is \"evening/night\" without emphasis on the time of day; 真夜中 intensifies it to \"dead of night\", and 深夜 is the more factual, more written equivalent (深夜番組, 深夜料金).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"残品はこちらで引き取りますので、そちらのお店で販売させていただけませんか。","romaji":"Zanpin wa kochira de hikitorimasu node, sochira no omise de hanbai sasete itadakemasen ka.","german":"We'll take back the leftover stock, so could you sell it at your shop?","furigana":"ざんぴん|ひ|と|みせ|はんばい"},{"japanese":"閉店前に残品を半額で売ることにした。","romaji":"Heiten mae ni zanpin o hangaku de uru koto ni shita.","german":"We decided to sell the leftover goods at half price before closing the store.","furigana":"へいてんまえ|ざんぴん|はんがく|う"},{"japanese":"残品処分セールは今週末までです。","romaji":"Zanpin shobun sēru wa konshūmatsu made desu.","german":"The clearance sale on remaining stock runs until this weekend.","furigana":"ざんぴんしょぶん|こんしゅうまつ"}],"notes":"残品 is the leftover, unsold merchandise of a shop - a word from trade and business language. Typical are 残品処分 \"clearance of remaining stock\", 残品セール and 残品を引き取る \"to take back leftover goods\". In everyday speech people more often say 売れ残り \"unsold goods\"; 在庫, by contrast, is the normal stock in storage without any negative overtone.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今回、勝利の栄光を手にしたのは、赤チームです！","romaji":"Konkai, shouri no eikou o te ni shita no wa, aka chiimu desu!","german":"The one who has taken the glory of victory this time is the Red Team!","furigana":"こんかい|しょうり|えいこう|て|あか"},{"japanese":"いつまでも過去の栄光にしがみついていてはいけない。","romaji":"Itsu made mo kako no eikou ni shigamitsuite ite wa ikenai.","german":"You must not keep clinging to past glory forever.","furigana":"かこ|えいこう"},{"japanese":"この学校には百年の栄光ある歴史がある。","romaji":"Kono gakkou ni wa hyakunen no eikou aru rekishi ga aru.","german":"This school has a glorious history of a hundred years.","furigana":"がっこう|ひゃくねん|えいこう|れきし"}],"notes":"栄光 is \"glory, splendor\" in a solemn tone - typical of sports reports, speeches and school songs. Common combinations: 栄光をつかむ / 手にする, 栄光に輝く, 過去の栄光 \"past glory\", 栄光ある歴史. 名誉 is \"honor\", 名声 is \"reputation, fame\"; in daily life people speak more of 有名 or 人気 than of 栄光.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ちゃんと取り決めを守ってもらえるのか、不安です。","romaji":"Chanto torikime o mamotte moraeru no ka, fuan desu.","german":"I'm worried whether they will really keep to the agreement.","furigana":"と|き|まも|ふあん"},{"japanese":"家事の分担について、夫婦で取り決めをした。","romaji":"Kaji no buntan ni tsuite, fuufu de torikime o shita.","german":"My spouse and I made an arrangement about dividing the housework.","furigana":"かじ|ぶんたん|ふうふ|と|き"},{"japanese":"両国は漁業に関する取り決めを結んだ。","romaji":"Ryoukoku wa gyogyou ni kansuru torikime o musunda.","german":"The two countries concluded an agreement on fisheries.","furigana":"りょうこく|ぎょぎょう|かん|と|き|むす"}],"notes":"取り決め is an \"agreement, arrangement\" between two or more parties, often about rules or divisions: 取り決めをする / 結ぶ / 守る / 破る. The corresponding verb is 取り決める. 約束 is rather a personal promise, 契約 a legally binding contract, 決まり an existing rule. In official texts it is also written 取決め.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"アルコールの分解を進めてくれます。","romaji":"Arukōru no bunkai o susumete kuremasu.","german":"It helps to speed up the breakdown of alcohol.","furigana":"ぶんかい|すす"},{"japanese":"子どものころ、時計を分解して元に戻せなくなった。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, tokei o bunkai shite moto ni modosenaku natta.","german":"When I was a child, I took a clock apart and couldn't put it back together.","furigana":"こ|とけい|ぶんかい|もと|もど"},{"japanese":"プラスチックは自然の中ではなかなか分解されない。","romaji":"Purasuchikku wa shizen no naka de wa nakanaka bunkai sarenai.","german":"Plastic doesn't easily break down in nature.","furigana":"しぜん|なか|ぶんかい"}],"notes":"分解 means \"to take apart, to disassemble\" (machines, devices) and, in chemistry and biology, \"to break down, to decompose\". As a する-verb it is transitive (時計を分解する) and intransitive or passive (微生物によって分解される, アルコールが分解される). 解体 is used for buildings or large structures. Figuratively, 空中分解 \"to break apart in mid-air\" is also said of groups that fall apart.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この二つの線は平行です。","romaji":"Kono futatsu no sen wa heikō desu.","german":"These two lines are parallel.","furigana":"ふた|せん|へいこう"},{"japanese":"線路は川と平行に走っている。","romaji":"Senro wa kawa to heikou ni hashitte iru.","german":"The railroad runs parallel to the river.","furigana":"せんろ|かわ|へいこう|はし"},{"japanese":"話し合いは三時間続いたが、最後まで平行線のままだった。","romaji":"Hanashiai wa sanjikan tsuzuita ga, saigo made heikousen no mama datta.","german":"The discussion lasted three hours, but remained at an impasse to the end.","furigana":"はな|あ|さんじかんつづ|さいご|へいこうせん"}],"notes":"平行 means \"parallel\" for lines and directions: 二つの線は平行だ, 〜と平行に走る. The phrase 平行線をたどる / 平行線のままだ means that a discussion reaches no agreement. Beware of the homophone 並行 \"to proceed simultaneously\" (二つの仕事を並行して進める) - in everyday writing, 並行 is usually used for simultaneity.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ドイツには、景観を大切にしている町が多い。","romaji":"Doitsu ni wa, keikan o taisetsu ni shite iru machi ga ōi.","german":"In Deutschland gibt es viele Städte, die ihr Landschaftsbild pflegen.","furigana":"けいかん|たいせつ|まち|おお"},{"japanese":"新しいビルが町の景観を損なうと、住民が反対している。","romaji":"Atarashii biru ga machi no keikan o sokonau to, juumin ga hantai shite iru.","german":"Die Anwohner protestieren, weil das neue Hochhaus das Stadtbild verschandeln würde.","furigana":"あたら|まち|けいかん|そこ|じゅうみん|はんたい"},{"japanese":"京都では景観を守るため、建物の高さが制限されている。","romaji":"Kyouto de wa keikan o mamoru tame, tatemono no takasa ga seigen sarete iru.","german":"In Kyoto ist die Gebäudehöhe begrenzt, um das Stadtbild zu schützen.","furigana":"きょうと|けいかん|まも|たてもの|たか|せいげん"}],"notes":"景観 ist das „Landschafts- oder Stadtbild“ als Ganzes, vor allem in Stadtplanung, Tourismus und Verwaltung: 景観を守る, 景観を損なう „verschandeln“, 都市景観, 景観条例. 景色 ist das alltägliche Wort für das, was man sieht („Aussicht, Landschaft“), 風景 die Szenerie (auch in Kunst), 眺め der Ausblick von einem Ort.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"グループに属するのは私のガラじゃないんです。","romaji":"Gurūpu ni zokusuru no wa watashi no gara ja nai n desu.","german":"Being part of a group is not my style.","furigana":"ぞく|わたし"},{"japanese":"クジラは魚ではなく、哺乳類に属する。","romaji":"Kujira wa sakana de wa naku, honyuurui ni zokusuru.","german":"Whales are not fish; they belong to the mammals.","furigana":"さかな|ほにゅうるい|ぞく"},{"japanese":"この島は昔、別の国に属していた。","romaji":"Kono shima wa mukashi, betsu no kuni ni zoku shite ita.","german":"This island used to belong to another country.","furigana":"しま|むかし|べつ|くに|ぞく"}],"notes":"属する means \"to belong to\" and takes に: グループに属する, 哺乳類に属する. It sounds matter-of-fact to written in register and is often used for categories and assignments. For membership in a company, club or team, 所属する is more usual, and in conversation simply 〜に入っている. It is conjugated as a special form of する: 属さない, 属して.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このカフェは年輩の方に人気だそうです。","romaji":"Kono kafe wa nenpai no kata ni ninki da sō desu.","german":"I hear this cafe is popular with older people.","furigana":"ねんぱい|かた|にんき"},{"japanese":"電車でご年輩の方に席を譲りました。","romaji":"Densha de go-nenpai no kata ni seki o yuzurimashita.","german":"I gave up my seat on the train to an elderly person.","furigana":"でんしゃ|ねんぱい|かた|せき|ゆず"},{"japanese":"彼は私より少し年輩だが、とても話しやすい。","romaji":"Kare wa watashi yori sukoshi nenpai da ga, totemo hanashiyasui.","german":"He is a little older than me, but he is very easy to talk to.","furigana":"かれ|わたし|すこ|ねんぱい|はな"}],"notes":"年輩 (today more often written 年配) means \"of advanced age\" and is almost always used attributively with の: 年輩の方, 年輩の人 \"older people\". It is more polite and softer than 老人 or 年寄り and is well suited to speaking respectfully about seniors, often as ご年輩の方. With より it expresses \"older than\" (私より年輩), and 同年輩 means \"of the same age\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"再来月にいとこの式があるんです。","romaji":"Saraigetsu ni itoko no shiki ga aru n desu.","german":"My cousin's wedding is the month after next.","furigana":"さらいげつ|しき"},{"japanese":"引っ越しは再来月の予定です。","romaji":"Hikkoshi wa saraigetsu no yotei desu.","german":"The move is planned for the month after next.","furigana":"ひ|こ|さらいげつ|よてい"},{"japanese":"このチケットは再来月まで使えます。","romaji":"Kono chiketto wa saraigetsu made tsukaemasu.","german":"This ticket can be used until the month after next.","furigana":"さらいげつ|つか"}],"notes":"再来月 is \"the month after next\"; the prefix 再来 means \"after the next\" and also appears in 再来週 and 再来年. Like 来月, it can stand without a particle as a time expression (再来月引っ越します); a に after it is also heard in conversation. With の it connects to nouns (再来月の予定), and with まで it gives a deadline. In the opposite direction there are 先月 and 先々月 \"the month before last\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"再来年には景気もよくなっているでしょう。","romaji":"Sarainen ni wa keiki mo yoku natte iru deshō.","german":"The year after next, the economy will probably have improved, too.","furigana":"さらいねん|けいき"},{"japanese":"再来年、日本に留学したいです。","romaji":"Sarainen, Nihon ni ryūgaku shitai desu.","german":"The year after next I want to study abroad in Japan.","furigana":"さらいねん|にほん|りゅうがく"}],"notes":"再来年 is the year after next year. 再来年には can emphasize this point in time as the limit of an expected development; でしょう marks a supposition. 再来年…したいです states a personal wish for that year. さ来年 is a mixed spelling of the same reading, for example in simplified texts; the full kanji spelling is 再来年. さらいねん contains no long vowels.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"関西限定販売のアイスクリーム、やっと手に入れたんだ。","romaji":"Kansai gentei hanbai no aisukurīmu, yatto te ni ireta n da.","german":"I finally got my hands on the ice cream sold only in Kansai.","furigana":"かんさいげんていはんばい|て|い"},{"japanese":"このケーキは一日五十個の限定販売です。","romaji":"Kono keeki wa ichinichi gojikko no gentei hanbai desu.","german":"This cake is sold in a limited quantity of fifty a day.","furigana":"いちにちごじっこ|げんていはんばい"},{"japanese":"冬だけの限定販売なので、今のうちに買っておこう。","romaji":"Fuyu dake no gentei hanbai na node, ima no uchi ni katte okou.","german":"It is sold only in winter, so let's buy it while we can.","furigana":"ふゆ|げんていはんばい|いま|か"}],"notes":"限定販売 is a \"sale in limited quantity, time or region\" - not a special offer. You find it on advertising signs: 数量限定販売, 期間限定販売, 地域限定販売, 〜限定販売の商品; often only 限定 is said (期間限定, 関西限定). A cheap clearance sale, by contrast, is called 特売 or セール.","levelNote":"Compound: 限定 (N1) + 販売 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は腰抜けだから言い返したくても言い返せないの。","romaji":"Watashi wa koshinuke da kara iikaeshitakute mo iikaesenai no.","german":"I'm a coward, so even though I want to talk back, I can't.","furigana":"わたし|こしぬ|い|かえ|い|かえ"},{"japanese":"一人で謝りにも行けないなんて、腰抜けだな。","romaji":"Hitori de ayamari ni mo ikenai nante, koshinuke da na.","german":"You can't even go apologize on your own - what a coward.","furigana":"ひとり|あやま|い|こしぬ"},{"japanese":"映画の主人公は、最初はみんなに腰抜けと呼ばれていた。","romaji":"Eiga no shujinkou wa, saisho wa minna ni koshinuke to yobarete ita.","german":"At first, everyone called the movie's protagonist a coward.","furigana":"えいが|しゅじんこう|さいしょ|こしぬ|よ"}],"notes":"腰抜け comes from 腰が抜ける \"to go weak at the knees from fright, to be unable to stand up\" and is a derogatory insult for a \"coward, wimp\". It is colloquial and insulting if said to others. 臆病 describes the trait \"timid\" more neutrally, 弱虫 is the childish \"scaredy-cat\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この文では必要な助詞が抜けていますよ。","romaji":"Kono bun de wa hitsuyō na joshi ga nukete imasu yo.","german":"A necessary particle is missing in this sentence.","furigana":"ぶん|ひつよう|じょし|ぬ"},{"japanese":"最近、髪の毛がよく抜けて困っている。","romaji":"Saikin, kami no ke ga yoku nukete komatte iru.","german":"Lately my hair has been falling out a lot, and it's a problem.","furigana":"さいきん|かみ|け|ぬ|こま"},{"japanese":"トンネルを抜けると、目の前に海が広がった。","romaji":"Tonneru o nukeru to, me no mae ni umi ga hirogatta.","german":"When we came out of the tunnel, the sea spread out before our eyes.","furigana":"ぬ|め|まえ|うみ|ひろ"}],"notes":"抜ける is intransitive; the transitive partner is 抜く. Basic meaning: something comes loose or is missing - 髪の毛が抜ける, 歯が抜ける, 名前が抜けている \"is missing (from the list)\". Further: to leave a group (会議を途中で抜ける, チームを抜ける) and to pass through something, with the place passed through marked by を (トンネルを抜ける). Also: 力が抜ける \"to go limp\", 疲れが抜けない \"the fatigue won't go away\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"兵士たちは敵を生きたまま捕らえた。","romaji":"Heishitachi wa teki o ikita mama toraeta.","german":"The soldiers captured the enemy alive.","furigana":"へいし|てき|い|と"},{"japanese":"警察はついに犯人を捕らえた。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa tsui ni hannin o toraeta.","german":"The police finally caught the culprit.","furigana":"けいさつ|はんにん|と"},{"japanese":"猫が庭でネズミを捕らえた。","romaji":"Neko ga niwa de nezumi o toraeta.","german":"The cat caught a mouse in the garden.","furigana":"ねこ|にわ|と"}],"notes":"捕らえる is transitive (〜を捕らえる) and means \"to catch, to arrest, to take prisoner\": 犯人を捕らえる, 獲物を捕らえる. It sounds more literary than the everyday 捕まえる; the corresponding intransitive everyday verb is 捕まる \"to be caught\". In the figurative meanings \"to grasp, comprehend\" (要点をとらえる) and \"to captivate\" (心をとらえる), people mostly write 捉える or kana.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"時計をココに掛けたいんだけど、うまく掛かるかな。","romaji":"Tokei o koko ni kaketai n da kedo, umaku kakaru kana.","german":"I want to hang the clock here, but I wonder if it will hang properly.","furigana":"とけい|か|か"},{"japanese":"壁に絵が掛かっています。","romaji":"Kabe ni e ga kakatte imasu.","german":"A picture is hanging on the wall.","furigana":"かべ|え|か"},{"japanese":"家から学校まで歩いて二十分かかります。","romaji":"Uchi kara gakkō made aruite nijuppun kakarimasu.","german":"It takes twenty minutes on foot from home to school.","furigana":"うち|がっこう|ある|にじゅっぷん"},{"japanese":"この自転車の修理には三千円かかりました。","romaji":"Kono jitensha no shūri ni wa sanzen en kakarimashita.","german":"Repairing this bicycle cost three thousand yen.","furigana":"じてんしゃ|しゅうり|さんぜんえん"}],"notes":"掛かる is also often written かかる. Besides \"to hang\", it means that something takes time or money: 時間がかかる, お金がかかる. A duration or an amount can stand directly before かかる. It is an intransitive godan verb: 掛かります, 掛かって. When hanging, the object takes が; 掛ける, by contrast, describes actively hanging something. 掛かっている describes the hanging state.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"健忘症は不治の病なんでしょうか。","romaji":"Kenboushou wa fuji no yamai na n deshou ka.","german":"Is amnesia an incurable disease?","furigana":"けんぼうしょう|ふじ|やまい"},{"japanese":"最近、人の名前がすぐに出てこない。健忘症かもしれない。","romaji":"Saikin, hito no namae ga sugu ni dete konai. Kenboushou ka mo shirenai.","german":"Lately I can't recall people's names right away. Maybe I have amnesia.","furigana":"さいきん|ひと|なまえ|で|けんぼうしょう"},{"japanese":"事故の後、彼は一時的な健忘症になった。","romaji":"Jiko no ato, kare wa ichijiteki na kenboushou ni natta.","german":"After the accident, he suffered temporary amnesia.","furigana":"じこ|あと|かれ|いちじてき|けんぼうしょう"}],"notes":"健忘症 is the medical term for \"amnesia, memory disorder\". Colloquially it is often used jokingly for forgetfulness: 最近健忘症気味だ. For memory loss after an accident (as in films), people mostly say 記憶喪失, for everyday forgetfulness 物忘れ; dementia is 認知症.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"でも、あなたのチームは効率的だけど効果的ではないよね。","romaji":"Demo, anata no chīmu wa kōritsuteki da kedo kōkateki de wa nai yo ne.","german":"But your team is efficient, though not effective, isn't it.","furigana":"こうりつてき|こうかてき"},{"japanese":"単語を覚えるには、声に出して読むのが効果的だ。","romaji":"Tango o oboeru ni wa, koe ni dashite yomu no ga koukateki da.","german":"Reading aloud is effective for memorizing words.","furigana":"たんご|おぼ|こえ|だ|よ|こうかてき"},{"japanese":"限られた予算を効果的に使いたい。","romaji":"Kagirareta yosan o koukateki ni tsukaitai.","german":"I want to use my limited budget effectively.","furigana":"かぎ|よさん|こうかてき|つか"}],"notes":"効果的 is a な-adjective: \"effective, producing results\" - 効果的な方法, 効果的に使う, 〜には〜が効果的だ. The point is that the desired result is achieved. 効率的, by contrast, means \"efficient\" (much result for little effort). For medicines people rather say 効く or 効果がある, for valid documents 有効.","levelNote":"Compound: 効果 (N3) + 的 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"林さんの就業時間を教えてください。","romaji":"Hayashisan no shūgyō jikan o oshiete kudasai.","german":"Please tell me Mr. Hayashi's working hours.","furigana":"はやし|しゅうぎょうじかん|おし"},{"japanese":"就業中は私用の電話を控えてください。","romaji":"Shuugyouchuu wa shiyou no denwa o hikaete kudasai.","german":"Please refrain from private phone calls during working hours.","furigana":"しゅうぎょうちゅう|しよう|でんわ|ひか"},{"japanese":"この会社では、外国人も日本人と同じ条件で就業している。","romaji":"Kono kaisha de wa, gaikokujin mo Nihonjin to onaji jouken de shuugyou shite iru.","german":"At this company, foreigners work under the same conditions as Japanese people.","furigana":"かいしゃ|がいこくじん|にほんじん|おな|じょうけん|しゅうぎょう"}],"notes":"就業 is a formal word for \"work, employment\" or \"to take up work\". It appears mainly in compounds from the working world and administration: 就業時間 \"(contractual) working hours\", 就業規則 \"work rules\", 就業中 \"during work\", 就業率 \"employment rate\". 就職, by contrast, is \"to take up a job\", especially after studies. As a verb, 就業する is official; in conversation one says 働く.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今度の金曜日にコウイチが巻きずしパーティーをするんだって。","romaji":"Kondo no kin'yōbi ni Kōichi ga makizushi pātī o suru n datte.","german":"I hear Koichi is having a sushi roll party this Friday.","furigana":"こんど|きんようび|ま"},{"japanese":"節分の日に、家族で巻きずしを食べました。","romaji":"Setsubun no hi ni, kazoku de makizushi o tabemashita.","german":"On Setsubun, our family ate sushi rolls.","furigana":"せつぶん|ひ|かぞく|ま|た"},{"japanese":"巻きずしを作るときは、のりの上にご飯を薄く広げます。","romaji":"Makizushi o tsukuru toki wa, nori no ue ni gohan o usuku hirogemasu.","german":"When making sushi rolls, spread the rice thinly over the nori.","furigana":"ま|つく|うえ|はん|うす|ひろ"}],"notes":"巻きずし (also 巻き寿司) is sushi in which rice and filling are rolled in nori or a thin omelet; すし becomes voiced to ずし in the compound. A distinction is made between thin 細巻き (e.g. かっぱ巻き with cucumber, 鉄火巻き with tuna) and thick 太巻き; on 節分 people eat the uncut 恵方巻き. Counterparts are 握りずし (nigiri) and 手巻きずし, which one rolls into a cone oneself.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"作っても作っても春巻きをぬすまれるんです。","romaji":"Tsukutte mo tsukutte mo harumaki o nusumareru n desu.","german":"No matter how many spring rolls I make, they keep getting stolen.","furigana":"つく|つく|はるま"},{"japanese":"今夜は春巻きを揚げるから、手伝ってくれる？","romaji":"Kon'ya wa harumaki o ageru kara, tetsudatte kureru?","german":"I'm going to fry spring rolls tonight, so could you help me?","furigana":"こんや|はるま|あ|てつだ"},{"japanese":"ベトナム料理の店で生春巻きを注文した。","romaji":"Betonamu ryouri no mise de namaharumaki o chuumon shita.","german":"I ordered fresh spring rolls at a Vietnamese restaurant.","furigana":"りょうり|みせ|なまはるま|ちゅうもん"}],"notes":"春巻き is the \"spring roll\" of Chinese cuisine; in Japan it is usually fried and filled with glass noodles (春雨), pork and vegetables. It is also written 春巻. The thin dough sheets are called 春巻きの皮. 生春巻き is the Vietnamese, non-fried \"summer roll\". It is counted with 本.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"頑張れニッポン、負けるなニッポン！","romaji":"Ganbare Nippon, makeru na Nippon!","german":"Go, Japan! Don't lose, Japan!","furigana":"がんば|ま"},{"japanese":"あと少しだよ、頑張れ！","romaji":"Ato sukoshi da yo, ganbare!","german":"Just a little more, hang in there!","furigana":"すこ|がんば"},{"japanese":"試験の前の日、母が「頑張れ」とメールをくれた。","romaji":"Shiken no mae no hi, haha ga \"ganbare\" to meeru o kureta.","german":"The day before the exam, my mother sent me an email saying \"Do your best\".","furigana":"しけん|まえ|ひ|はは|がんば"}],"notes":"頑張れ is the imperative of 頑張る and a cheer: \"Go for it!, Hang in there!, Give it your all!\". It is shouted to athletes, friends or younger people, often written in kana as がんばれ. Toward superiors one says 頑張ってください, among friends the softer 頑張って. For people who are already exhausted or down, 頑張れ can be felt as pressure.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あんたは何の悩みもなさそうでいいよね。","romaji":"Anta wa nan no nayami mo nasasou de ii yo ne.","german":"You seem to have no worries at all, lucky you.","furigana":"なん|なや"},{"japanese":"何か悩みがあるなら、いつでも相談してね。","romaji":"Nani ka nayami ga aru nara, itsu demo soudan shite ne.","german":"If something is troubling you, talk to me anytime.","furigana":"なに|なや|そうだん"},{"japanese":"朝起きられないのが、私の一番の悩みです。","romaji":"Asa okirarenai no ga, watashi no ichiban no nayami desu.","german":"My biggest problem is that I can't get up in the morning.","furigana":"あさお|わたし|いちばん|なや"}],"notes":"悩み is the noun form of 悩む and refers to a \"worry, problem\" that preoccupies one inwardly: 悩みがある, 悩みを抱える, 悩みを相談する, 悩みの種 \"cause for worry\". 心配 is more concern about something in the future or about someone, and 問題 is a factual problem. 悩み sounds more personal and emotional.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 45"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あま〜い誘惑に負けて、ケーキを食べてしまった。","romaji":"Amāi yūwaku ni makete, kēki o tabete shimatta.","german":"I gave in to the sweet temptation and ate the cake.","furigana":"ゆうわく|ま|た"},{"japanese":"都会には誘惑が多いから、気をつけなさい。","romaji":"Tokai ni wa yūwaku ga ōi kara, ki o tsukenasai.","german":"There are many temptations in the city, so be careful.","furigana":"とかい|ゆうわく|おお|き"},{"japanese":"彼は甘い言葉で彼女を誘惑しようとした。","romaji":"Kare wa amai kotoba de kanojo o yūwaku shiyō to shita.","german":"He tried to seduce her with sweet words.","furigana":"かれ|あま|ことば|かのじょ|ゆうわく"}],"notes":"誘惑 is \"temptation, allurement\" and, as a transitive する-verb, \"to seduce\" (人を誘惑する). Fixed phrases are 誘惑に負ける \"to succumb to temptation\", 誘惑に勝つ \"to resist it\" and 誘惑が多い. Unlike the neutral 誘う \"to invite, to ask\", 誘惑 always carries the undertone of luring someone into something unreasonable or immoral.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"首藤さんは人の素質を見抜くのがうまいんですよ。","romaji":"Shudou-san wa hito no soshitsu o minuku no ga umai n desu yo.","german":"Mr. Shuto is good at discerning people's potential.","furigana":"しゅどう|ひと|そしつ|みぬ"},{"japanese":"子どもの嘘なんて、親はすぐに見抜くものだ。","romaji":"Kodomo no uso nante, oya wa sugu ni minuku mono da.","german":"Parents can see through a child's lies right away.","furigana":"こ|うそ|おや|みぬ"},{"japanese":"彼女は相手の本当の気持ちを見抜いていた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa aite no hontou no kimochi o minuite ita.","german":"She had seen through the other person's true feelings.","furigana":"かのじょ|あいて|ほんとう|きも|みぬ"}],"notes":"見抜く means \"to see through, to recognize what is hidden\": 嘘を見抜く, 本性を見抜く, 才能を見抜く, 相手の気持ちを見抜く. It is transitive and has no intransitive partner. 見破る stresses exposing a deception or disguise (変装を見破る), while 気づく is mere \"noticing\". 見抜く presupposes insight into people or sharp-mindedness.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"オレ、間抜けだから、気づかなかったよ。言ってくれてありがとう。","romaji":"Ore, manuke da kara, kizukanakatta yo. Itte kurete arigatō.","german":"I'm a fool, so I didn't notice. Thanks for telling me.","furigana":"まぬ|き|い"},{"japanese":"財布を家に忘れて買い物に行くなんて、間抜けな話だ。","romaji":"Saifu o ie ni wasurete kaimono ni iku nante, manuke na hanashi da.","german":"Going shopping after forgetting your wallet at home is a silly thing to do.","furigana":"さいふ|いえ|わす|か|もの|い|まぬ|はなし"},{"japanese":"寝起きの間抜けな顔を写真に撮られてしまった。","romaji":"Neoki no manuke na kao o shashin ni torarete shimatta.","german":"A photo was taken of my goofy face just after waking up.","furigana":"ねお|まぬ|かお|しゃしん|と"}],"notes":"間抜け comes from music and theater: someone who misses the right 間 (\"timing\") is clumsy. As a な-adjective it means \"foolish, silly\" (間抜けな顔, 間抜けな失敗), as a noun \"fool\" (この間抜け!). It is colloquial and insulting as a form of address. ばか is more general, あほ is typical of Kansai; it is also often written まぬけ or マヌケ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"エクセルで日付の月の部分だけを抜き出す関数ってありますか？","romaji":"Ekuseru de hizuke no tsuki no bubun dake o nukidasu kansū tte arimasu ka?","german":"Is there an Excel function that extracts only the month part of a date?","furigana":"ひづけ|つき|ぶぶん|ぬ|だ|かんすう"},{"japanese":"本文から大切な言葉を抜き出して、ノートに書きなさい。","romaji":"Honbun kara taisetsu na kotoba o nukidashite, nooto ni kakinasai.","german":"Pick out the important words from the text and write them in your notebook.","furigana":"ほんぶん|たいせつ|ことば|ぬ|だ|か"},{"japanese":"箱の中からカードを一枚抜き出してください。","romaji":"Hako no naka kara kaado o ichimai nukidashite kudasai.","german":"Please pull one card out of the box.","furigana":"はこ|なか|いちまいぬ|だ"}],"notes":"抜き出す means \"to pull out of a set, to pick out, to extract\": データを抜き出す, 必要な部分だけを抜き出す, 本文から言葉を抜き出す. It is transitive; the source takes から. 取り出す is the general \"to take out\" (from a pocket or refrigerator), 選ぶ is plain \"to choose\", 抽出する is the technical, formal variant \"to extract\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"母は、私の着替えを持ってくるのを忘れた事を謝りました。","romaji":"Haha wa, watashi no kigae o motte kuru no o wasureta koto o ayamarimashita.","german":"My mother apologized for forgetting to bring my change of clothes.","furigana":"はは|わたし|きが|も|わす|こと|あやま"},{"japanese":"旅行には三日分の着替えを持っていきます。","romaji":"Ryokou ni wa mikkabun no kigae o motte ikimasu.","german":"I'm taking three days' worth of changes of clothes on the trip.","furigana":"りょこう|みっかぶん|きが|も"},{"japanese":"今着替え中だから、ちょっと待って！","romaji":"Ima kigaechuu da kara, chotto matte!","german":"I'm changing right now, so wait a moment!","furigana":"いまきが|ちゅう|ま"}],"notes":"着替え is the noun form of 着替える and means both \"changing clothes\" and \"a change of clothes\": 着替えを持っていく, 着替えを用意する, 着替え中 \"in the middle of changing\". The verb is 服を着替える or simply 着替える (ichidan). For clothing in general, people say 服, for underwear 下着.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"死ぬまでに、究極のラーメンを極めるつもりです。","romaji":"Shinu made ni, kyūkyoku no rāmen o kiwameru tsumori desu.","german":"I intend to master the ultimate ramen before I die.","furigana":"し|きゅうきょく|きわ"},{"japanese":"一生、肉か魚のどちらかしか食べられないなんて、究極の選択だね。","romaji":"Isshou, niku ka sakana no dochira ka shika taberarenai nante, kyuukyoku no sentaku da ne.","german":"Being able to eat only meat or only fish for the rest of your life - that's an ultimate choice.","furigana":"いっしょう|にく|さかな|た|きゅうきょく|せんたく"},{"japanese":"この研究の究極の目的は、病気をなくすことだ。","romaji":"Kono kenkyuu no kyuukyoku no mokuteki wa, byouki o nakusu koto da.","german":"The ultimate goal of this research is to eliminate disease.","furigana":"けんきゅう|きゅうきょく|もくてき|びょうき"}],"notes":"究極 means \"ultimate, extreme, final\" and almost always stands with の before a noun: 究極の選択 \"a real dilemma\", 究極の目的 \"final goal\", 究極の味. As an adverb, 究極的に \"ultimately\" is used; 究極な is uncommon. 最高 means \"the best\", 最終 \"the last in a sequence\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「ごいっしょにポテトもいかがですか？」「いいえ、結構です。」","romaji":"Go issho ni poteto mo ikaga desu ka? Iie, kekkō desu.","german":"\"Would you like fries with that?\" \"No, thank you.\"","furigana":"けっこう"},{"japanese":"「カードで払ってもいいですか？」「はい、カードで結構です。」","romaji":"Kaado de haratte mo ii desu ka? Hai, kaado de kekkou desu.","german":"\"Can I pay by card?\" \"Yes, card is fine.\"","furigana":"はら|けっこう"},{"japanese":"お返事は明日で結構です。","romaji":"Ohenji wa ashita de kekkou desu.","german":"Replying tomorrow is fine.","furigana":"へんじ|あした|けっこう"}],"notes":"結構です works in two directions: as a polite refusal \"No thank you, not necessary\" (いいえ、結構です) and as approval \"that's fine, that will do\" (それで結構です, 明日で結構です). Without いいえ or context it can be ambiguous. As a refusal it sounds rather cool; 大丈夫です or いえ、けっこうです with thanks is friendlier. Do not confuse it with the adverb 結構 \"quite\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 6"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"すぐ帰りますので、お構いなく。","romaji":"Sugu kaerimasu node, okamainaku.","german":"I'll be leaving soon, so please don't trouble yourself.","furigana":"かえ|かま"},{"japanese":"「コーヒーでもいかがですか？」「どうぞお構いなく。」","romaji":"Koohii demo ikaga desu ka? Douzo okamainaku.","german":"\"Would you like some coffee?\" \"Please, don't go to any trouble.\"","furigana":"かま"},{"japanese":"私のことはお構いなく、先に食べ始めてください。","romaji":"Watashi no koto wa okamainaku, saki ni tabehajimete kudasai.","german":"Please don't worry about me; go ahead and start eating.","furigana":"わたし|かま|さき|た|はじ"}],"notes":"お構いなく is a polite phrase used by a guest when the host wants to offer tea or food: どうぞお構いなく \"please don't go to any trouble\". It comes from 構う \"to care for someone, to bother about\" plus negation. With 〜のことは you can say that you yourself need not be attended to: 私のことはお構いなく. At parting, the host modestly says 何のお構いもしませんで.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"皆さんは、もし皆さんが住む町で大規模な地震が起きたらどうしますか？","romaji":"Minasan wa, moshi minasan ga sumu machi de daikibo na jishin ga okitara dou shimasu ka?","german":"What would you do if a large-scale earthquake hit the town where you live?","furigana":"みな|みな|す|まち|だいきぼ|じしん|お"},{"japanese":"駅前で大規模な工事が行われている。","romaji":"Ekimae de daikibo na kouji ga okonawarete iru.","german":"Large-scale construction work is being carried out in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|だいきぼ|こうじ|おこな"},{"japanese":"昨夜、市内で大規模な停電が起きた。","romaji":"Sakuya, shinai de daikibo na teiden ga okita.","german":"A large-scale power outage occurred in the city last night.","furigana":"さくや|しない|だいきぼ|ていでん|お"}],"notes":"大規模 is a な-adjective: \"large-scale, on a large scale\" - 大規模な工事, 大規模な地震, 大規模に行う. In compounds the な drops: 大規模災害, 大規模停電. Opposites are 小規模 and 中規模. The word is typical of news and official texts; 大型, by contrast, describes the build or type (大型台風, 大型店).","levelNote":"Compound: 大 (N3) + 規模 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"うちの会社のプロジェクトチームは小回りが利くように、小規模化されているんです。","romaji":"Uchi no kaisha no purojekuto chīmu wa komawari ga kiku yō ni, shōkiboka sarete iru n desu.","german":"Our company's project team has been made smaller in scale so that it can be nimble.","furigana":"かいしゃ|こまわ|き|しょうきぼか"},{"japanese":"小規模な会社なので、社員全員の顔と名前が分かります。","romaji":"Shōkibo na kaisha na node, shain zen'in no kao to namae ga wakarimasu.","german":"It's a small company, so I know the face and name of every employee.","furigana":"しょうきぼ|かいしゃ|しゃいんぜんいん|かお|なまえ|わ"},{"japanese":"今年の夏祭りは小規模に行われる予定です。","romaji":"Kotoshi no natsumatsuri wa shōkibo ni okonawareru yotei desu.","german":"This year's summer festival is planned to be held on a small scale.","furigana":"ことし|なつまつ|しょうきぼ|おこな|よてい"}],"notes":"小規模 (\"small scale\") is a な-adjective and noun: 小規模な会社, 小規模の店, adverbially 小規模に行う \"to carry out on a small scale\". The opposite is 大規模; 規模 alone means \"scale, magnitude\". The word is typical of news, business and administration; in casual conversation one often simply says 小さい. 小規模化 means \"downsizing\".","levelNote":"Compound: 小 (N3) + 規模 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"では、第一段は森さんが読んでください。","romaji":"Dewa, dai ichidan wa Morisan ga yonde kudasai.","german":"Then, Ms. Mori, please read the first section.","furigana":"だいいちだん|もり|よ"},{"japanese":"この文章の第一段では、筆者の考えが述べられている。","romaji":"Kono bunshou no dai ichidan de wa, hissha no kangae ga noberarete iru.","german":"The first section of this text states the author's thoughts.","furigana":"ぶんしょう|だいいちだん|ひっしゃ|かんが|の"},{"japanese":"打ち上げから二分後、ロケットの第一段が切り離された。","romaji":"Uchiage kara nifungo, roketto no dai ichidan ga kirihanasareta.","german":"Two minutes after launch, the rocket's first stage was separated.","furigana":"う|あ|にふんご|だいいちだん|き|はな"}],"notes":"第一段 is made up of 第一 \"first\" and 段 \"section, stage\" and refers to the first section of a text or the first stage of, for example, a rocket. For paragraphs, 第一段落 is more common today; 第一段 is heard mostly in the classroom. A phase in a process is called 第一段階. Analogous: 第二段, 第三段.","levelNote":"Compound: 第一 (N2) + 段 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この、マンモグラフィ濃度って何ですか？","romaji":"Kono, manmogurafi noudo tte nani desu ka?","german":"What is this \"mammography density\"?","furigana":"のうど|なに"},{"japanese":"海水の塩分濃度は約3.5パーセントだ。","romaji":"Kaisui no enbun noudo wa yaku san ten go paasento da.","german":"The salt concentration of seawater is about 3.5 percent."},{"japanese":"部屋の二酸化炭素の濃度が高くなったら、窓を開けましょう。","romaji":"Heya no nisanka tanso no noudo ga takaku nattara, mado o akemashou.","german":"If the carbon dioxide concentration in the room gets high, let's open a window.","furigana":"へや|にさんかたんそ|のうど|たか|まど|あ"}],"notes":"濃度 is the \"concentration, density\" of a substance in a solution or in the air: 塩分濃度, アルコール濃度, 二酸化炭素の濃度. People say 濃度が高い / 低い; the adjectives 濃い and 薄い go with it. Watch out: \"concentration\" in the sense of attention is 集中. Physical density (mass per volume) and population density are called 密度.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"岸田さんのエッセーには、車いすのお母さんとダウン症の弟さんとの日々がつづられています。","romaji":"Kishida-san no essē ni wa, kurumaisu no okāsan to daunshō no otōto-san to no hibi ga tsuzurarete imasu.","german":"Kishida's essay recounts her days with her mother in a wheelchair and her younger brother with Down syndrome.","furigana":"きしだ|くるま|かあ|しょう|おとうと|ひび"},{"japanese":"毎年春になると、花粉症で目がかゆくなります。","romaji":"Maitoshi haru ni naru to, kafunshō de me ga kayuku narimasu.","german":"Every spring, my eyes get itchy from hay fever.","furigana":"まいとしはる|かふんしょう|め"},{"japanese":"夏は熱中症に気をつけてください。","romaji":"Natsu wa netchūshō ni ki o tsukete kudasai.","german":"Please watch out for heatstroke in summer.","furigana":"なつ|ねっちゅうしょう|き"}],"notes":"〜症 is a suffix for diseases, syndromes and disorders and occurs only in fixed compounds: 花粉症 \"hay fever\", 熱中症 \"heatstroke\", 不眠症 \"insomnia\", 認知症 \"dementia\", 後遺症 \"after-effects\". The general word for illness is 病気, for symptom 症状. Many other disease names, by contrast, end in 〜病 (糖尿病 \"diabetes\"), without the two being freely interchangeable.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"昨日の睡眠時間はたったの三時間でした。","romaji":"Kinō no suimin jikan wa tatta no sanjikan deshita.","german":"Yesterday I got only three hours of sleep.","furigana":"きのう|すいみんじかん|さんじかん"},{"japanese":"最近、仕事が忙しくて睡眠不足が続いている。","romaji":"Saikin, shigoto ga isogashikute suimin busoku ga tsuzuite iru.","german":"Work has been busy lately, and my lack of sleep continues.","furigana":"さいきん|しごと|いそが|すいみんぶそく|つづ"},{"japanese":"風邪を早く治すには、十分な睡眠をとることが大切です。","romaji":"Kaze o hayaku naosu ni wa, juubun na suimin o toru koto ga taisetsu desu.","german":"To get over a cold quickly, it is important to get enough sleep.","furigana":"かぜ|はや|なお|じゅうぶん|すいみん|たいせつ"}],"notes":"睡眠 is a noun for \"sleep\" and sounds factual to medical: 睡眠時間, 睡眠不足 \"lack of sleep\", 睡眠薬, 十分な睡眠をとる (with とる, not する). In everyday speech, sleeping is expressed with the verbs 寝る or 眠る; the noun 眠り is more general and also literary (深い眠り).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"そのデザイナーは、時代の先端をいくファッションを発表した。","romaji":"Sono dezainaa wa, jidai no sentan o iku fasshon o happyou shita.","german":"That designer presented fashion at the cutting edge of the times.","furigana":"じだい|せんたん|はっぴょう"},{"japanese":"針の先端が鋭いので、気をつけてください。","romaji":"Hari no sentan ga surudoi node, ki o tsukete kudasai.","german":"The tip of the needle is sharp, so please be careful.","furigana":"はり|せんたん|するど|き"},{"japanese":"この工場では最先端の技術が使われている。","romaji":"Kono koujou de wa saisentan no gijutsu ga tsukawarete iru.","german":"State-of-the-art technology is used in this factory.","furigana":"こうじょう|さいせんたん|ぎじゅつ|つか"}],"notes":"先端 is the \"tip, outermost end\" of an elongated object (針の先端, ペンの先端, 岬の先端) and, figuratively, the \"leading edge, forefront\" of a development: 時代の先端を行く, 最先端の技術 \"cutting-edge technology\" (very common). In everyday speech, for a fingertip or pen tip, people often simply say 先; the summit of a mountain is 頂上.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"中途半端にやるくらいなら、やらない方がいい。","romaji":"Chūto hanpa ni yaru kurai nara, yaranai hō ga ii.","german":"If you're going to do it half-heartedly, you're better off not doing it.","furigana":"ちゅうとはんぱ|ほう"},{"japanese":"仕事が中途半端なままだ。","romaji":"Shigoto ga chūto hanpa na mama da.","german":"My work is still half-done.","furigana":"しごと|ちゅうとはんぱ"},{"japanese":"中途半端な時間に起きてしまって、もう眠れない。","romaji":"Chūto hanpa na jikan ni okite shimatte, mō nemurenai.","german":"I woke up at an awkward time and now I can't sleep.","furigana":"ちゅうとはんぱ|じかん|お|ねむ"}],"notes":"中途 \"halfway\" and 半端 \"incomplete, odd\" together give \"half-finished, half-hearted\". As a na-adjective: 中途半端な気持ち, 中途半端にやる, 中途半端なままにする. It also describes things that are neither one thing nor the other, such as 中途半端な時間 \"an awkward time of day\" or 中途半端な長さ. Neutral, but often critical in conversation.","levelNote":"Compound: 中途 (N2) + 半端 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ちょっと嫌なことがあったからって会社を辞めるなんて、武井さんも極端ですねぇ！","romaji":"Chotto iya na koto ga atta kara tte kaisha o yameru nante, Takei-san mo kyokutan desu nē!","german":"Quitting the company just because something unpleasant happened - you're being extreme, Mr. Takei!","furigana":"いや|かいしゃ|や|たけい|きょくたん"},{"japanese":"極端なダイエットは体によくないですよ。","romaji":"Kyokutan na daietto wa karada ni yoku nai desu yo.","german":"Extreme dieting is not good for your body.","furigana":"きょくたん|からだ"},{"japanese":"この町は冬になると極端に人が少なくなる。","romaji":"Kono machi wa fuyu ni naru to kyokutan ni hito ga sukunaku naru.","german":"In winter, this town becomes extremely empty of people.","furigana":"まち|ふゆ|きょくたん|ひと|すく"}],"notes":"極端 is a な-adjective and noun for \"extreme, exaggerated, radical\": 極端な例, 極端な意見, adverbially 極端に寒い \"extremely cold\". It often sounds critical - one reproaches someone who exaggerates (極端だね). Unlike the neutral 非常に, 極端 judges the degree as unreasonable; 過激 rather means \"radical, militant\" in the political sense.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どうしてあの国が突然平和友好条約を締結したのか不思議に思うよ。","romaji":"Dōshite ano kuni ga totsuzen heiwa yūkō jōyaku o teiketsu shita no ka fushigi ni omou yo.","german":"I wonder why that country suddenly concluded a peace and friendship treaty.","furigana":"くに|とつぜんへいわゆうこうじょうやく|ていけつ|ふしぎ|おも"},{"japanese":"両社は来月、業務提携の契約を締結する予定だ。","romaji":"Ryousha wa raigetsu, gyoumu teikei no keiyaku o teiketsu suru yotei da.","german":"The two companies plan to conclude a business partnership agreement next month.","furigana":"りょうしゃ|らいげつ|ぎょうむていけい|けいやく|ていけつ|よてい"},{"japanese":"二つの国の間で貿易協定が締結された。","romaji":"Futatsu no kuni no aida de boueki kyoutei ga teiketsu sareta.","german":"A trade agreement was concluded between the two countries.","furigana":"ふた|くに|あいだ|ぼうえききょうてい|ていけつ"}],"notes":"締結する means \"to formally conclude (a treaty, agreement)\": 条約を締結する, 協定を締結する, 契約を締結する; often in the passive 締結される. The word belongs to the news, politics and law. In normal conversation people say 契約を結ぶ or 約束をする. Technically, 締結 can also refer to joining parts with screws.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ガスの元栓、ちゃんと締めた？","romaji":"Gasu no motosen, chanto shimeta?","german":"Did you properly turn off the gas valve?","furigana":"もとせん|し"},{"japanese":"このねじを締めてください。","romaji":"Kono neji o shimete kudasai.","german":"Please tighten this screw.","furigana":"し"}],"notes":"締める is a transitive ichidan verb: 締めます, 締めて. It means tightening or securely closing, for example a tap or a screw. With を you name the thing affected. For simply closing a door, 閉める usually fits. 締めた？ casually asks about the completed action.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"胃痛がひどくなって入院し、病院で一ヶ月過ごしました。","romaji":"Itsū ga hidoku natte nyūin shi, byōin de ikkagetsu sugoshimashita.","german":"My stomach pain got so bad that I was hospitalized and spent a month in the hospital.","furigana":"いつう|にゅういん|びょういん|いっかげつす"},{"japanese":"ストレスによる胃痛で、夜もよく眠れない。","romaji":"Sutoresu ni yoru itsuu de, yoru mo yoku nemurenai.","german":"Because of stress-induced stomach pain, I can't sleep well at night.","furigana":"いつう|よる|ねむ"},{"japanese":"この薬は胃痛や胸やけによく効きます。","romaji":"Kono kusuri wa itsuu ya muneyake ni yoku kikimasu.","german":"This medicine works well for stomach pain and heartburn.","furigana":"くすり|いつう|むね|き"}],"notes":"胃痛 is the factual, rather written word for \"stomachache\", for example at the doctor's or on package inserts: 胃痛がする, 胃痛に悩む, ストレスによる胃痛. In conversation people mostly say 胃が痛い, and for the belly in general お腹が痛い. Similarly formed are 頭痛 \"headache\" and 腹痛 \"abdominal pain\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"切腹とか考えるだけで痛いよね。","romaji":"Seppuku toka kangaeru dake de itai yo ne.","german":"Just thinking about seppuku is painful.","furigana":"せっぷく|かんが|いた"},{"japanese":"江戸時代、武士は責任を取って切腹することがあった。","romaji":"Edo jidai, bushi wa sekinin o totte seppuku suru koto ga atta.","german":"In the Edo period, samurai sometimes committed seppuku to take responsibility.","furigana":"えどじだい|ぶし|せきにん|と|せっぷく"},{"japanese":"社長の大事な書類をなくすなんて、切腹ものだよ。","romaji":"Shachou no daiji na shorui o nakusu nante, seppuku mono da yo.","german":"Losing the president's important documents is a seppuku-worthy offense.","furigana":"しゃちょう|だいじ|しょるい|せっぷく"}],"notes":"切腹 is the ritual suicide of samurai by cutting open the abdomen; the native Japanese paraphrase is 腹を切る. Abroad, \"harakiri\" is better known, while in Japan people say 切腹. Historically: 切腹を命じられる \"to be sentenced to seppuku\". Today it appears jokingly when a mistake seems unforgivable: 切腹ものだ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"家を訪ねる前に、電話してみた方がいいよ。","romaji":"Ie o tazuneru mae ni, denwa shite mita hō ga ii yo.","german":"You should call before you visit their house.","furigana":"いえ|たず|まえ|でんわ|ほう"},{"japanese":"京都に住んでいる友達を訪ねました。","romaji":"Kyouto ni sunde iru tomodachi o tazunemashita.","german":"I visited a friend who lives in Kyoto.","furigana":"きょうと|す|ともだち|たず"},{"japanese":"昨日、昔の先生が会社に訪ねてきました。","romaji":"Kinou, mukashi no sensei ga kaisha ni tazunete kimashita.","german":"Yesterday my former teacher came to see me at the company.","furigana":"きのう|むかし|せんせい|かいしゃ|たず"}],"notes":"訪ねる means \"to visit a person or place\"; the destination takes を: 友達を訪ねる, 実家を訪ねる. When someone comes to visit one, one says 訪ねてくる and marks the place with に. In conversation, 遊びに行く or 会いに行く are more casual, and 訪問する is more formal. Note: the identically read 尋ねる means \"to ask\"; only the kanji distinguish the two.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この間の訪問の際、何かお気に触るようなことをしてしまいましたか？","romaji":"Kono aida no houmon no sai, nani ka oki ni sawaru you na koto o shite shimaimashita ka?","german":"During my last visit, did I do something that offended you?","furigana":"あいだ|ほうもん|さい|なに|き|さわ"},{"japanese":"首相は来週、アメリカを訪問する予定だ。","romaji":"Shushou wa raishuu, Amerika o houmon suru yotei da.","german":"The prime minister plans to visit the United States next week.","furigana":"しゅしょう|らいしゅう|ほうもん|よてい"},{"japanese":"明日は先生が家庭訪問に来る。","romaji":"Ashita wa sensei ga katei houmon ni kuru.","german":"Tomorrow the teacher is coming for a home visit.","furigana":"あした|せんせい|かていほうもん|く"}],"notes":"訪問 is a formal \"visit\" to a person, company or country: 訪問する (transitive: 会社を訪問する, アメリカを訪問する), 家庭訪問 \"teacher's home visit\", 公式訪問, 訪問者. Visits to websites are also called 訪問. In private settings people rather say 遊びに行く or 訪ねる; a visit to a sick person is 見舞い.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"会社で飛行機を貸し切りにしてバミューダまで行きました。","romaji":"Kaisha de hikōki o kashikiri ni shite Bamyūda made ikimashita.","german":"Our company chartered a whole plane and went to Bermuda.","furigana":"かいしゃ|ひこうき|か|き|い"},{"japanese":"本日は貸し切りのため、一般のお客様はご利用いただけません。","romaji":"Honjitsu wa kashikiri no tame, ippan no okyakusama wa goriyou itadakemasen.","german":"Today is a private booking, so regular customers cannot use the facility.","furigana":"ほんじつ|か|き|いっぱん|きゃくさま|りよう"},{"japanese":"平日の朝早く行ったら、温泉がほとんど貸し切り状態だった。","romaji":"Heijitsu no asa hayaku ittara, onsen ga hotondo kashikiri joutai datta.","german":"I went early on a weekday morning, and I almost had the hot spring to myself.","furigana":"へいじつ|あさはや|い|おんせん|か|き|じょうたい"}],"notes":"貸し切り (from 貸す \"to lend\" and 切る \"completely\") means that a place or vehicle is reserved exclusively for one group: 貸し切りバス, 〜を貸し切りにする, the sign 本日貸し切り \"private party today\". Colloquially, 貸し切り状態 means that one has a place practically to oneself. The verb is 貸し切る; 予約 is an ordinary reservation.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼に金を返すよう迫ったが、逃げられてしまった。","romaji":"Kare ni kane o kaesu you sematta ga, nigerarete shimatta.","german":"I pressed him to pay the money back, but he got away.","furigana":"かれ|かね|かえ|せま|に"},{"japanese":"締め切りが迫っているので、今夜は寝られない。","romaji":"Shimekiri ga sematte iru node, kon'ya wa nerarenai.","german":"The deadline is approaching, so I can't sleep tonight.","furigana":"し|き|せま|こんや|ね"},{"japanese":"大型の台風が九州に迫っている。","romaji":"Oogata no taifuu ga Kyuushuu ni sematte iru.","german":"A large typhoon is approaching Kyushu.","furigana":"おおがた|たいふう|きゅうしゅう|せま"}],"notes":"迫る has two main meanings. Intransitive: \"to draw near, to be imminent\" - 締め切りが迫る, 台風が迫る, 試験が目前に迫る; the destination takes に. Transitive: \"to press, to force\" someone to do something - 人に返事を迫る, 〜よう迫る, in the passive 決断を迫られる. Related is 迫力 \"power, impact\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"何故か週末から胸が圧迫される感じがあるんです。","romaji":"Nazeka shuumatsu kara mune ga appaku sareru kanji ga aru n desu.","german":"For some reason, since the weekend I've had a feeling of pressure on my chest.","furigana":"なぜ|しゅうまつ|むね|あっぱく|かん"},{"japanese":"物価が上がって、家計を圧迫している。","romaji":"Bukka ga agatte, kakei o appaku shite iru.","german":"Rising prices are straining household budgets.","furigana":"ぶっか|あ|かけい|あっぱく"},{"japanese":"血が止まるまで、傷口を強く圧迫してください。","romaji":"Chi ga tomaru made, kizuguchi o tsuyoku appaku shite kudasai.","german":"Press firmly on the wound until the bleeding stops.","furigana":"ち|と|きずぐち|つよ|あっぱく"}],"notes":"圧迫 means \"to exert pressure, to compress\", physically (胸を圧迫する, 傷口を圧迫して止血する) and figuratively \"to burden, to constrict\": 家計を圧迫する \"to strain the household budget\". The passive 圧迫される is common, as is the noun 圧迫感 \"feeling of oppression\" (e.g. in cramped rooms). 圧力 is pressure as a force or as leverage, プレッシャー is psychological pressure to perform.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ヤツの状況はかなり切迫しているはずです。","romaji":"Yatsu no jōkyō wa kanari seppaku shite iru hazu desu.","german":"His situation must be quite desperate.","furigana":"じょうきょう|せっぱく"},{"japanese":"電話の向こうから切迫した声が聞こえた。","romaji":"Denwa no mukou kara seppaku shita koe ga kikoeta.","german":"I heard an urgent voice from the other end of the phone.","furigana":"でんわ|む|せっぱく|こえ|き"},{"japanese":"締め切りが切迫しているので、残業するしかない。","romaji":"Shimekiri ga seppaku shite iru node, zangyou suru shika nai.","german":"The deadline is pressing, so I have no choice but to work overtime.","furigana":"し|き|せっぱく|ざんぎょう"}],"notes":"切迫 describes a situation that is \"pressing, acute, close to coming to a head\". It mostly appears as 切迫している or attributively 切迫した: 切迫した状況, 切迫した声, 事態が切迫する; also the noun 切迫感. Medically: 切迫早産 \"threatened premature birth\". The verb 迫る is more neutral, \"to approach\"; 緊急 refers to an emergency.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ディズニーランドでは迷子の放送がないそうですよ。","romaji":"Dizunīrando de wa maigo no hōsō ga nai sō desu yo.","german":"I hear there are no lost-child announcements at Disneyland.","furigana":"まいご|ほうそう"},{"japanese":"デパートで子どもが迷子になって、本当に焦りました。","romaji":"Depāto de kodomo ga maigo ni natte, hontō ni aserimashita.","german":"My child got lost in the department store, and I was really panicked.","furigana":"こ|まいご|ほんとう|あせ"},{"japanese":"初めての町で迷子になってしまい、駅までの道を人に聞いた。","romaji":"Hajimete no machi de maigo ni natte shimai, eki made no michi o hito ni kiita.","german":"I got lost in a town I'd never been to and asked someone the way to the station.","furigana":"はじ|まち|まいご|えき|みち|ひと|き"}],"notes":"迷子 is a child or person who has gotten lost or lost their companions. The most important phrase is 迷子になる \"to get lost\", which adults also use jokingly about themselves. In a department store you hear 迷子のお知らせ \"announcement about a lost child\". The verb 道に迷う describes only losing one's way, whereas 迷子 describes the state as a person.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どこかに、迷路の地図が隠されています。","romaji":"Doko ka ni, meiro no chizu ga kakusarete imasu.","german":"Somewhere, a map of the maze is hidden.","furigana":"めいろ|ちず|かく"},{"japanese":"子どもたちは紙に書いた迷路で遊んでいる。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa kami ni kaita meiro de asonde iru.","german":"The children are playing with a maze drawn on paper.","furigana":"こ|かみ|か|めいろ|あそ"},{"japanese":"この町の古い通りは迷路のようで、すぐに道に迷ってしまう。","romaji":"Kono machi no furui toori wa meiro no you de, sugu ni michi ni mayotte shimau.","german":"The old streets of this town are like a maze, and you get lost right away.","furigana":"まち|ふる|とお|めいろ|みち|まよ"}],"notes":"迷路 (迷う \"to lose one's way\" + 路 \"road\") is a \"labyrinth, maze\" - as a puzzle on paper, in an amusement park or in imagery: 迷路のような道 \"winding alleys\", 迷路に迷い込む. 迷宮 is more literary; 迷宮入り means \"an unsolved criminal case\". In medicine 迷路 also refers to the inner ear.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"途中でスーパーによっておとうふを買って来てちょうだい。","romaji":"Tochū de sūpā ni yotte otōfu o katte kite chōdai.","german":"Could you stop by the supermarket on the way and buy some tofu?","furigana":"とちゅう|か|き"},{"japanese":"仕事の途中で電話がかかってきました。","romaji":"Shigoto no tochū de denwa ga kakatte kimashita.","german":"In the middle of work, I got a phone call.","furigana":"しごと|とちゅう|でんわ"}],"notes":"途中 refers to a point on a route or in a process that is not yet finished. 途中で in the first example means \"on the way\"; 仕事の途中で means \"in the middle of work\". The noun connects with の for the named process and with で for the point in time of an action. Here it does not mean that the trip or work is already finished.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どうやって逮捕するつもりですか？","romaji":"Dō yatte taiho suru tsumori desu ka?","german":"How do you intend to arrest him?","furigana":"たいほ"},{"japanese":"警察は昨日、泥棒を逮捕した。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa kinou, dorobou o taiho shita.","german":"The police arrested the thief yesterday.","furigana":"けいさつ|きのう|どろぼう|たいほ"},{"japanese":"男は酒を飲んで運転したため、その場で逮捕された。","romaji":"Otoko wa sake o nonde unten shita tame, sono ba de taiho sareta.","german":"The man was arrested on the spot for drunk driving.","furigana":"おとこ|さけ|の|うんてん|ば|たいほ"}],"notes":"逮捕する means \"to arrest, to detain\" and is the official term for the police: 犯人を逮捕する. Especially common in the news is the passive 逮捕される; also 逮捕状 \"arrest warrant\" and 現行犯逮捕 \"arrest in the act\". In everyday speech people also say 捕まえる \"to catch\" or, intransitively, 捕まる \"to be caught\".","levelNote":"Derived from 逮捕 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの野郎の物の言い方が気に入らねぇんだよ。","romaji":"Ano yarō no mono no iikata ga ki ni iranē n da yo.","german":"I don't like the way that guy talks.","furigana":"やろう|もの|い|かた|き|い"},{"japanese":"この野郎、待て！","romaji":"Kono yarō, mate!","german":"You bastard, wait!","furigana":"やろう|ま"},{"japanese":"今夜は野郎ばかりで飲みに行くんだ。","romaji":"Kon'ya wa yarō bakari de nomi ni iku n da.","german":"Tonight it's just us guys going out for a drink.","furigana":"こんや|やろう|の|い"}],"notes":"野郎 is rough, predominantly male colloquial speech. As a swear word it appears in この野郎! \"You bastard!\" or ばか野郎 \"idiot\"; in a more neutral but still casual sense it means \"guy, fellow\", e.g. 野郎ばかり \"only men\". You should never use it toward strangers, superiors or in polite situations; neutrally one says 男の人 or やつ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"靴下三足で五百円だって！めっちゃ安いっ。","romaji":"Kutsushita sansoku de gohyakuen datte! Metcha yasui.","german":"I hear three pairs of socks are 500 yen! That's so cheap!"},{"japanese":"毎晩、靴下を洗います。","romaji":"Maiban, kutsushita o araimasu.","german":"I wash my socks every night.","furigana":"まいばん|くつした|あら"}],"notes":"靴下 means socks. Pairs are counted with 足: 三足 is read さんそく. 三足で五百円 gives a total price for three pairs. だって reports a price heard from someone else here, and めっちゃ is a casual word for \"very\". 靴下を洗う names the socks as the thing being washed.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 41"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"高橋さんの家の前には有名な靴屋さんがあります。","romaji":"Takahashisan no ie no mae ni wa yūmei na kutsuyasan ga arimasu.","german":"There is a famous shoe store in front of Mr. Takahashi's house.","furigana":"たかはし|いえ|まえ|ゆうめい|くつや"},{"japanese":"駅前の靴屋で運動靴を買った。","romaji":"Ekimae no kutsuya de undougutsu o katta.","german":"I bought sneakers at the shoe store in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|くつや|うんどうぐつ|か"},{"japanese":"かかとが取れたので、靴屋さんに直してもらった。","romaji":"Kakato ga toreta node, kutsuyasan ni naoshite moratta.","german":"The heel came off, so I had the shoe store repair it.","furigana":"と|くつや|なお"}],"notes":"靴屋 is the \"shoe store\" or the \"shoemaker\"; 〜屋 forms shop names and occupational titles. With さん (靴屋さん) it sounds friendlier and can also mean the person. In department stores the section is called 靴売り場; modern shops often call themselves シューズショップ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コウイチはいつも雨の日はピカチュウの長靴をはいてくるよね。","romaji":"Kouichi wa itsumo ame no hi wa Pikachuu no nagagutsu o haite kuru yo ne.","german":"Koichi always comes in Pikachu rain boots on rainy days.","furigana":"あめ|ひ|ながぐつ"},{"japanese":"雪が深いので、長靴をはいて出かけた。","romaji":"Yuki ga fukai node, nagagutsu o haite dekaketa.","german":"The snow was deep, so I went out wearing boots.","furigana":"ゆき|ふか|ながぐつ|で"},{"japanese":"田植えのときは長靴が欠かせない。","romaji":"Taue no toki wa nagagutsu ga kakasenai.","german":"Rubber boots are indispensable when planting rice.","furigana":"たう|ながぐつ|か"}],"notes":"長靴 (with rendaku, ながぐつ) are tall boots, in daily life mainly \"rubber boots\" for rain, snow or field work. Shoes are put on with はく (履く): 長靴をはく. Fashionable leather boots are mostly called ブーツ; 靴 is the umbrella term for shoes.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの男がみんなに悪影響を与えたんだよ。","romaji":"Ano otoko ga minna ni akueikyō o ataeta n da yo.","german":"That man was a bad influence on everyone.","furigana":"おとこ|あくえいきょう|あた"},{"japanese":"スマホの使いすぎは目に悪影響を及ぼす。","romaji":"Sumaho no tsukaisugi wa me ni akueikyou o oyobosu.","german":"Using your smartphone too much has a negative effect on your eyes.","furigana":"つか|め|あくえいきょう|およ"},{"japanese":"天候不順で、野菜の収穫に悪影響が出ている。","romaji":"Tenkou fujun de, yasai no shuukaku ni akueikyou ga dete iru.","german":"Because of the unseasonable weather, the vegetable harvest is being adversely affected.","furigana":"てんこうふじゅん|やさい|しゅうかく|あくえいきょう|で"}],"notes":"悪影響 is a \"bad, negative influence\". Typical combinations: 悪影響を与える / 及ぼす \"to harm, to have a negative effect\", 悪影響を受ける, 〜に悪影響が出る. It sounds more compact and somewhat more formal than 悪い影響. The counterpart is いい影響 or, formally, 好影響.","levelNote":"Compound: 悪 (N2) + 影響 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"胃病のせいで、隠居することになりました。","romaji":"Ibyō no sei de, inkyo suru koto ni narimashita.","german":"Because of my stomach ailment, I had to retire.","furigana":"いびょう|いんきょ"},{"japanese":"父は会社を兄に任せて、田舎で隠居している。","romaji":"Chichi wa kaisha o ani ni makasete, inaka de inkyo shite iru.","german":"My father left the company to my older brother and lives in retirement in the countryside.","furigana":"ちち|かいしゃ|あに|まか|いなか|いんきょ"},{"japanese":"仕事をやめたら、海の近くで静かな隠居生活を送りたい。","romaji":"Shigoto o yametara, umi no chikaku de shizuka na inkyo seikatsu o okuritai.","german":"When I stop working, I want to lead a quiet retired life near the sea.","furigana":"しごと|うみ|ちか|しず|いんきょせいかつ|おく"}],"notes":"隠居する means \"to withdraw from working life (or as head of a family) and live quietly\". It sounds somewhat old-fashioned; common are 隠居生活 and 田舎で隠居する. ご隠居 is an old gentleman in retirement, known from rakugo stories. 引退 is withdrawal from sports or politics, 退職 leaving a company, 定年 the age limit.","levelNote":"Derived from 隠居 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「今、どこ？」「え、今、居間だよ」","romaji":"Ima, doko? E, ima, ima da yo","german":"\"Where are you now?\" \"Huh, I'm in the living room now.\"","furigana":"いま|いま|いま"},{"japanese":"夕食の後、家族は居間でテレビを見る。","romaji":"Yuushoku no ato, kazoku wa ima de terebi o miru.","german":"After dinner, the family watches TV in the living room.","furigana":"ゆうしょく|あと|かぞく|いま|み"},{"japanese":"お客さんを居間に通してください。","romaji":"Okyakusan o ima ni tooshite kudasai.","german":"Please show the guests into the living room.","furigana":"きゃく|いま|とお"}],"notes":"居間 is the \"living room\" where the family spends time. The word sounds somewhat traditional; today people often say リビング. 茶の間 is the old-fashioned family room with a low table. 居間 is a homophone of 今 \"now\", which is used for wordplay.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"そのタレントは引退を全く口にしない。","romaji":"Sono tarento wa intai o mattaku kuchi ni shinai.","german":"That entertainer doesn't mention retirement at all.","furigana":"いんたい|まった|くち"},{"japanese":"あの選手は今シーズンで引退するそうです。","romaji":"Ano senshu wa kon shiizun de intai suru sou desu.","german":"I hear that player will retire at the end of this season.","furigana":"せんしゅ|こん|いんたい"},{"japanese":"父は引退してから、毎日庭の手入れをしている。","romaji":"Chichi wa intai shite kara, mainichi niwa no teire o shite iru.","german":"Since retiring, my father tends the garden every day.","furigana":"ちち|いんたい|まいにちにわ|てい"}],"notes":"引退 refers to the final withdrawal from an active career, especially for athletes, artists and politicians; as an intransitive する-verb: 引退する, 現役を引退する, 〜から引退する. It is to be distinguished from 退職 \"leaving employment\" (e.g. 定年退職 \"retirement at the mandatory age\") and 辞任 \"resignation from an office\". Common combinations are 引退後 \"after retirement\" and 引退試合 \"farewell match\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"プレゼントを手渡す勇気はありません。","romaji":"Purezento o tewatasu yūki wa arimasen.","german":"I don't have the courage to hand over the present in person.","furigana":"てわた|ゆうき"},{"japanese":"先生は卒業生一人一人に卒業証書を手渡した。","romaji":"Sensei wa sotsugyousei hitori hitori ni sotsugyou shousho o tewatashita.","german":"The teacher handed each graduate their diploma personally.","furigana":"せんせい|そつぎょうせいひとりひとり|そつぎょうしょうしょ|てわた"},{"japanese":"受付でこの書類を係の人に手渡してください。","romaji":"Uketsuke de kono shorui o kakari no hito ni tewatashite kudasai.","german":"Please hand this document to the staff member at the reception desk.","furigana":"うけつけ|しょるい|かかり|ひと|てわた"}],"notes":"手渡す means \"to hand something over personally, from hand to hand\": 人に物を手渡す. In contrast to the general 渡す, it emphasizes that one hands it over directly oneself rather than sending it or passing it on through others. The noun 手渡し often stands adverbially: 手渡しでお願いします \"please hand it over in person\". A transitive godan verb with no intransitive partner.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"現実には理屈だけじゃ通用しないことは山ほどある。","romaji":"Genjitsu ni wa rikutsu dake ja tsūyō shinai koto wa yama hodo aru.","german":"In reality, there are loads of things that logic alone can't handle.","furigana":"げんじつ|りくつ|つうよう|やま"},{"japanese":"理屈では分かっていても、なかなか実行できない。","romaji":"Rikutsu de wa wakatte ite mo, nakanaka jikkou dekinai.","german":"Even though I understand it in theory, I can't put it into practice easily.","furigana":"りくつ|わ|じっこう"},{"japanese":"弟はすぐ理屈を言って、手伝おうとしない。","romaji":"Otouto wa sugu rikutsu o itte, tetsudaou to shinai.","german":"My younger brother immediately comes up with arguments and doesn't try to help.","furigana":"おとうと|りくつ|い|てつだ"}],"notes":"理屈 is the (often theoretical) reasoning or logic behind something: 理屈に合う \"to be logical\", 理屈では分かる \"to understand in theory\". It often sounds negative in the sense of \"hair-splitting, excuse\": 理屈を言う \"to make excuses, to argue\", 理屈っぽい \"argumentative\", 屁理屈 \"quibbling\". For logic as a subject or rigorous argumentation, one says 論理, for the mere reason 理由.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ヘアスタイルは、お任せでお願いできますか？","romaji":"Heasutairu wa, omakase de onegai dekimasu ka?","german":"Could I ask you to choose my hairstyle as you see fit?","furigana":"まか|ねが"},{"japanese":"細かいことは山田さんにお任せします。","romaji":"Komakai koto wa Yamada-san ni omakase shimasu.","german":"I'll leave the details to Mr. Yamada.","furigana":"こま|やまだ|まか"},{"japanese":"今夜はシェフのお任せコースを予約しました。","romaji":"Kon'ya wa shefu no omakase koosu o yoyaku shimashita.","german":"Tonight I reserved the chef's choice course.","furigana":"こんや|まか|よやく"}],"notes":"お任せ is the polite noun form of 任せる \"to leave to, to entrust\" (人に仕事を任せる). As a phrase, お任せします means \"I leave it to you\", often said at the hairdresser or to craftsmen. In a restaurant, お任せ means the chef decides the menu (お任せコース). It is mostly used as a noun with で or as the humble お任せする.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お客様の多種多様なニーズに応えるのは難しい。","romaji":"Okyakusama no tashu tayō na nīzu ni kotaeru no wa muzukashii.","german":"It is difficult to meet customers' diverse needs.","furigana":"きゃくさま|たしゅたよう|こた|むずか"},{"japanese":"両親の期待に応えたくて、毎日頑張っている。","romaji":"Ryoushin no kitai ni kotaetakute, mainichi ganbatte iru.","german":"I want to live up to my parents' expectations, so I work hard every day.","furigana":"りょうしん|きたい|こた|まいにちがんば"},{"japanese":"この寒さは年を取った体に応えるよ。","romaji":"Kono samusa wa toshi o totta karada ni kotaeru yo.","german":"This cold takes a toll on an aging body.","furigana":"さむ|とし|と|からだ|こた"}],"notes":"応える means \"to respond to, to live up to an expectation\" and takes に: 期待に応える, 要望に応える, 声援に応える. Do not confuse it with 答える \"to answer (a question)\" - same reading, but 応える responds to wishes, expectations or calls. A second meaning is \"to take a toll, to be hard on\" (寒さが体に応える), often written in kana こたえる here. It is an intransitive Ichidan verb.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"すいません、お手拭きをもらえますか？","romaji":"Suimasen, otefuki o moraemasu ka?","german":"Excuse me, could I have a wet wipe?","furigana":"てふ"},{"japanese":"コンビニでお弁当を買ったら、お手拭きが付いてきた。","romaji":"Konbini de obentou o kattara, otefuki ga tsuite kita.","german":"When I bought a bento at the convenience store, a wet wipe came with it.","furigana":"べんとう|か|てふ|つ"},{"japanese":"ピクニックにはお手拭きを持って行くと便利ですよ。","romaji":"Pikunikku ni wa otefuki o motte iku to benri desu yo.","german":"It's handy to bring wet wipes on a picnic.","furigana":"てふ|も|い|べんり"}],"notes":"お手拭き is a cloth for wiping the hands, today mostly a packaged wet wipe given in restaurants or with ready-made meals. The rolled warm or cold towel in a restaurant is usually called おしぼり; the two words overlap strongly in everyday use. The お is a fixed part of the word; without it (手拭き) it sounds unfamiliar. For packaged wipes people also say ウェットティッシュ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"秋も更けて、もうすぐキノコが美味しいシーズンですね。","romaji":"Aki mo fukete, mōsugu kinoko ga oishii shīzun desu ne.","german":"Autumn is deepening, and the mushroom season will soon be here.","furigana":"あき|ふ|おい"},{"japanese":"話に夢中になっているうちに、夜が更けてしまった。","romaji":"Hanashi ni muchuu ni natte iru uchi ni, yoru ga fukete shimatta.","german":"While we were absorbed in conversation, the night grew late.","furigana":"はなし|むちゅう|よる|ふ"},{"japanese":"夜が更けると、この辺りはとても静かになる。","romaji":"Yoru ga fukeru to, kono atari wa totemo shizuka ni naru.","german":"When the night grows late, this area becomes very quiet.","furigana":"よる|ふ|あた|しず"}],"notes":"更ける describes the night or a season advancing: 夜が更ける \"the night deepens, it gets late\" is by far the most common combination, along with 秋が更ける \"autumn wanes\". Derived from it are 夜更け \"late night\" and 夜更かし \"staying up late\". Not to be confused with the identically read 老ける \"to age\" and 耽る \"to indulge in\". An intransitive ichidan verb.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"未だ海外に行ったことがありません。","romaji":"Imada kaigai ni itta koto ga arimasen.","german":"I have never been abroad yet.","furigana":"いま|かいがい|い"},{"japanese":"事故の原因は未だ分かっていない。","romaji":"Jiko no gen'in wa imada wakatte inai.","german":"The cause of the accident is still not known.","furigana":"じこ|げんいん|いま|わ"},{"japanese":"兄は四十歳になっても、未だに独身だ。","romaji":"Ani wa yonjussai ni natte mo, imada ni dokushin da.","german":"Even though my older brother has turned forty, he is still single.","furigana":"あに|よんじゅっさい|いま|どくしん"}],"notes":"いまだ is the written, somewhat formal counterpart of まだ and mostly occurs with a negative: いまだ〜ない \"not yet, to this day\". The form いまだに \"still\" also occurs in affirmative sentences (いまだに独身だ). Since 未だ can also be read まだ, the word is often written in kana. In conversation, まだ sounds more natural; いまだ(に) emphasizes that something continues for a surprisingly long time.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"もう少し、目標に具体的な規準を設定してみてはいかがでしょうか。","romaji":"Mou sukoshi, mokuhyou ni gutaiteki na kijun o settei shite mite wa ikaga deshou ka.","german":"Why don't you try setting a somewhat more concrete standard for your goal?","furigana":"すこ|もくひょう|ぐたいてき|きじゅん|せってい"},{"japanese":"評価の規準は、学期の初めに先生が説明します。","romaji":"Hyouka no kijun wa, gakki no hajime ni sensei ga setsumei shimasu.","german":"The teacher explains the evaluation criteria at the start of the term.","furigana":"ひょうか|きじゅん|がっき|はじ|せんせい|せつめい"},{"japanese":"この学校では、制服の着方にも細かい規準がある。","romaji":"Kono gakkou de wa, seifuku no kikata ni mo komakai kijun ga aru.","german":"This school even has detailed standards for how to wear the uniform.","furigana":"がっこう|せいふく|きかた|こま|きじゅん"}],"notes":"規準 is a standard or norm by which one judges or acts: 規準を設ける, 評価の規準. The identically read and much more common 基準 means \"standard, criterion\"; 規準 stresses more a binding rule and appears mainly in education and administration (評価規準). When in doubt, people write 基準 in everyday life. The word is a pure noun and is connected with の (行動の規準).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ただし、一人当たり１００万円を限度とする。","romaji":"Tadashi, hitori atari hyakuman'en o gendo to suru.","german":"However, the limit is 1,000,000 yen per person.","furigana":"ひとりあ|まんえん|げんど"},{"japanese":"冗談にも限度があるよ。","romaji":"Jōdan ni mo gendo ga aru yo.","german":"Even jokes have their limits.","furigana":"じょうだん|げんど"},{"japanese":"カードの限度額を超えてしまって、支払いができなかった。","romaji":"Kādo no gendogaku o koete shimatte, shiharai ga dekinakatta.","german":"I went over my card limit and couldn't make the payment.","furigana":"げんどがく|こ|しはら"}],"notes":"限度 is a fixed or tolerable limit, a maximum: 限度額 \"credit limit, maximum amount\", 限度を超える \"to exceed the limit\", formal 〜を限度とする \"up to a maximum of ...\". Very common is ものには限度がある \"everything has its limits\". 限界, by contrast, means the limit of one's own strength or abilities (体力の限界).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ここから、町の全景が見わたせるんです。","romaji":"Koko kara, machi no zenkei ga miwataseru n desu.","german":"From here you can see the whole town.","furigana":"まち|ぜんけい|み"},{"japanese":"これは飛行機から撮った島の全景です。","romaji":"Kore wa hikouki kara totta shima no zenkei desu.","german":"This is a full view of the island taken from an airplane.","furigana":"ひこうき|と|しま|ぜんけい"},{"japanese":"ビルが多くて、駅前からは城の全景が見えない。","romaji":"Biru ga ookute, ekimae kara wa shiro no zenkei ga mienai.","german":"There are many buildings, so you can't see the whole castle from in front of the station.","furigana":"おお|えきまえ|しろ|ぜんけい|み"}],"notes":"全景 is the overall view of a place, usually from a height or distance: 町の全景, 全景を見渡す, 全景を撮る. In everyday speech, for a beautiful view people more often say 景色 or 眺め; 全景 stresses that one sees the whole at once. Not to be confused with the identically read 前景 \"foreground\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"前景をちょっとぼかしてみます。","romaji":"Zenkei o chotto bokashite mimasu.","german":"I'll try blurring the foreground a little.","furigana":"ぜんけい"},{"japanese":"この絵では、前景に花、遠くに山が描かれている。","romaji":"Kono e de wa, zenkei ni hana, tooku ni yama ga egakarete iru.","german":"In this painting, flowers are depicted in the foreground and mountains in the distance.","furigana":"え|ぜんけい|はな|とお|やま|えが"},{"japanese":"写真を撮るときは、前景に何か入れると奥行きが出ます。","romaji":"Shashin o toru toki wa, zenkei ni nanika ireru to okuyuki ga demasu.","german":"When taking a photo, putting something in the foreground gives it depth.","furigana":"しゃしん|と|ぜんけい|なに|い|おくゆ|で"}],"notes":"前景 is the foreground of a picture, photo or scene; antonyms are 背景 \"background\" as well as 中景 and 遠景. Typical are 前景に〜を置く/入れる and 前景をぼかす. The word belongs to the technical language of painting, photography and graphics; in everyday speech people often simply say 手前. Not to be confused with 全景 \"full view\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"男はよろめきながらも、再びゆっくりと歩き始めた。","romaji":"Otoko wa yoromeki nagara mo, futatabi yukkuri to aruki hajimeta.","german":"Although the man staggered, he slowly started walking again.","furigana":"おとこ|ふたた|ある|はじ"},{"japanese":"この町を再び訪れる日を楽しみにしています。","romaji":"Kono machi o futatabi otozureru hi o tanoshimi ni shite imasu.","german":"I look forward to the day I visit this town again.","furigana":"まち|ふたた|おとず|ひ|たの"},{"japanese":"試合は雨で中断したが、三十分後に再び始まった。","romaji":"Shiai wa ame de chuudan shita ga, sanjuppun go ni futatabi hajimatta.","german":"The game was interrupted by rain, but started again thirty minutes later.","furigana":"しあい|あめ|ちゅうだん|さんじゅっぷんご|ふたた|はじ"}],"notes":"再び is an adverb meaning \"again, a second time\"; it sounds more formal and more literary than また or もう一度. Typical in news, speeches and narratives: 再び訪れる, 再び始まる. In conversation people would mostly say また; 再び often stresses a return to an earlier state.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"運動会は今週じゃなくて再来週だよ。","romaji":"Undoukai wa konshuu ja nakute saraishuu da yo.","german":"The sports day is not this week but the week after next.","furigana":"うんどうかい|こんしゅう|さらいしゅう"},{"japanese":"再来週の金曜日に試験があります。","romaji":"Saraishuu no kin'youbi ni shiken ga arimasu.","german":"I have an exam on Friday the week after next.","furigana":"さらいしゅう|きんようび|しけん"},{"japanese":"再来週までにレポートを出してください。","romaji":"Saraishuu made ni repooto o dashite kudasai.","german":"Please submit the report by the week after next.","furigana":"さらいしゅう|だ"}],"notes":"再来週 is \"the week after next\", that is, in two weeks; formed with 再来 \"after the next\" like 再来月 and 再来年. As a time expression it usually stands without a particle, with の before weekdays (再来週の金曜日) and with までに for deadlines. The opposite direction is 先々週 \"the week before last\". The word is neutral and very common in everyday use.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"故に、この薬は人々に知られるようになったのです。","romaji":"Yueni, kono kusuri wa hitobito ni shirareru yō ni natta no desu.","german":"Therefore, this medicine came to be known to people.","furigana":"ゆえ|くすり|ひとびと|し"},{"japanese":"我思う、故に我あり。","romaji":"Ware omou, yueni ware ari.","german":"I think, therefore I am.","furigana":"われおも|ゆえ|われ"},{"japanese":"AはBより大きく、BはCより大きい。故に、AはCより大きい。","romaji":"A wa B yori ōkiku, B wa C yori ōkii. Yueni, A wa C yori ōkii.","german":"A is bigger than B, and B is bigger than C. Therefore, A is bigger than C.","furigana":"おお|おお|ゆえ|おお"}],"notes":"故に (mostly ゆえに in kana) is a literary conjunction \"consequently, therefore\" that introduces a logical conclusion - typical in proofs, treatises and quotations like 我思う、故に我あり. In conversation people say だから or それで, in factual texts often したがって. The noun 故 also appears after a phrase as \"because of\": 若さゆえの失敗 \"a mistake due to youthful recklessness\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"針金が床に落ちていたよ。","romaji":"Harigane ga yuka ni ochite ita yo.","german":"There was a wire lying on the floor.","furigana":"はりがね|ゆか|お"},{"japanese":"針金でフェンスの板を固定した。","romaji":"Harigane de fensu no ita o kotei shita.","german":"I fixed the fence boards with wire.","furigana":"はりがね|いた|こてい"},{"japanese":"このハンガーは針金でできている。","romaji":"Kono hangaa wa harigane de dekite iru.","german":"This hanger is made of wire.","furigana":"はりがね"}],"notes":"針金 is thin metal wire, as used for crafts, tying or fastening: 針金で留める, 針金を曲げる; counter 本. For electrical cable, people say 電線 or コード, for thick steel cables ワイヤー. The word is made up of 針 \"needle\" and 金 \"metal\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"虫にとっては、人間も巨人でしょ。","romaji":"Mushi ni totte wa, ningen mo kyojin desho.","german":"To insects, humans are giants too, aren't they.","furigana":"むし|にんげん|きょじん"},{"japanese":"昔話には、山のように大きい巨人がよく出てくる。","romaji":"Mukashibanashi ni wa, yama no you ni ookii kyojin ga yoku dete kuru.","german":"Folktales often feature giants as big as mountains.","furigana":"むかしばなし|やま|おお|きょじん|で"},{"japanese":"昨日の野球は、巨人が三対二で勝った。","romaji":"Kinou no yakyuu wa, Kyojin ga san tai ni de katta.","german":"In yesterday's baseball game, the Giants won 3 to 2.","furigana":"きのう|やきゅう|きょじん|さんたいに|か"}],"notes":"巨人 is a giant, literally a \"huge person\"; figuratively also an outstanding personality (文学界の巨人). In Japan, 巨人 very often stands for short for the baseball team the Yomiuri Giants. The adjective \"huge\" is 巨大な, not 巨人な.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今回のパーティーには、クライアントを百名招く予定です。","romaji":"Konkai no pātī ni wa, kuraianto o hyakumei maneku yotei desu.","german":"We plan to invite one hundred clients to this party.","furigana":"こんかい|ひゃくめいまね|よてい"},{"japanese":"彼の一言が大きな誤解を招いた。","romaji":"Kare no hitokoto ga ōkina gokai o maneita.","german":"One remark of his caused a big misunderstanding.","furigana":"かれ|ひとこと|おお|ごかい|まね"},{"japanese":"友人の結婚式に招かれて、スピーチをすることになった。","romaji":"Yūjin no kekkonshiki ni manekarete, supīchi o suru koto ni natta.","german":"I was invited to a friend's wedding and will be giving a speech.","furigana":"ゆうじん|けっこんしき|まね"}],"notes":"招く is transitive and first means \"to invite (formally)\": 人を〜に招く, passive 招かれる; it sounds more elevated than 呼ぶ or 誘う, and the related noun is 招待. Equally important is the meaning \"to bring about, to cause\", for mostly undesirable consequences: 誤解を招く \"to cause misunderstandings\", 事故を招く. 手招きする means \"to beckon\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コウイチは漢字の道を極めるために、日本に行って漢字道場に通いました。","romaji":"Kōichi wa kanji no michi o kiwameru tame ni, Nihon ni itte kanji dōjō ni kayoimashita.","german":"To master the way of kanji, Koichi went to Japan and attended a kanji dojo.","furigana":"かんじ|みち|きわ|にほん|い|かんじどうじょう|かよ"},{"japanese":"年末の空港は混雑を極めていた。","romaji":"Nenmatsu no kuukou wa konzatsu o kiwamete ita.","german":"The airport at the year's end was extremely crowded.","furigana":"ねんまつ|くうこう|こんざつ|きわ"},{"japanese":"彼女は長年の練習で、ピアノの技術を極めた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa naganen no renshuu de, piano no gijutsu o kiwameta.","german":"Through years of practice, she mastered piano technique.","furigana":"かのじょ|ながねん|れんしゅう|ぎじゅつ|きわ"}],"notes":"極める means \"to go to the utmost\": to fully master a path or an art (道を極める, 技術を極める) or to reach an extreme state (混雑を極める \"to be extremely crowded\", 多忙を極める \"to be extremely busy\"). For thorough research, 究める is also written. It is a transitive Ichidan verb with を; the intransitive partner is 極まる, and the adverb 極めて means \"extremely\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"現役を退くには、まだ早過ぎますよ。","romaji":"Gen'eki o shirizoku ni wa, mada hayasugimasu yo.","german":"It's still too early to retire from active duty.","furigana":"げんえき|しりぞ|はやす"},{"japanese":"一歩後ろに退いて、全体を見てみよう。","romaji":"Ippo ushiro ni shirizoite, zentai o mite miyou.","german":"Let's step back one pace and look at the whole picture.","furigana":"いっぽうし|しりぞ|ぜんたい|み"},{"japanese":"敵の軍は山の方へ退いた。","romaji":"Teki no gun wa yama no hou e shirizoita.","german":"The enemy army withdrew toward the mountains.","furigana":"てき|ぐん|やま|ほう|しりぞ"}],"notes":"退く means \"to draw back, to withdraw\", both spatially (一歩退く) and figuratively \"to leave an office or active service\" (現役を退く, 職を退く); the place left takes を. It sounds more formal than 下がる. The transitive partner is 退ける \"to repel, to reject\". Note: the same kanji read 退く (どく/のく) colloquially means \"to make way\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"矢印にそって進んでください。","romaji":"Yajirushi ni sotte susunde kudasai.","german":"Please proceed following the arrows.","furigana":"やじるし|すす"},{"japanese":"矢印の方向にふたを回してください。","romaji":"Yajirushi no houkou ni futa o mawashite kudasai.","german":"Please turn the lid in the direction of the arrow.","furigana":"やじるし|ほうこう|まわ"},{"japanese":"画面の右上の矢印をクリックすると、次のページに進みます。","romaji":"Gamen no migiue no yajirushi o kurikku suru to, tsugi no peeji ni susumimasu.","german":"If you click the arrow at the top right of the screen, you move on to the next page.","furigana":"がめん|みぎうえ|やじるし|つぎ|すす"}],"notes":"矢印 is the arrow symbol on signs, maps and screens, from 矢 \"arrow (weapon)\" and 印 \"mark\". Typical are 矢印に沿って進む \"to follow the arrows\", 矢印の方向 and 矢印キー \"arrow key\". The real arrow for shooting is called 矢. Colloquially, 矢印が向く also means that someone's romantic interest is directed at a person.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 34"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日は学校で、直列回路と並列回路について学びました。","romaji":"Kyou wa gakkou de, chokuretsu kairo to heiretsu kairo ni tsuite manabimashita.","german":"Today at school I learned about series circuits and parallel circuits.","furigana":"きょう|がっこう|ちょくれつかいろ|へいれつかいろ|まな"},{"japanese":"この文では、二つの名詞が「と」で並列されている。","romaji":"Kono bun de wa, futatsu no meishi ga 'to' de heiretsu sarete iru.","german":"In this sentence, two nouns are joined in parallel with 「と」.","furigana":"ぶん|ふた|めいし|へいれつ"},{"japanese":"新しいパソコンは、複数の作業を並列で処理できる。","romaji":"Atarashii pasokon wa, fukusuu no sagyou o heiretsu de shori dekiru.","german":"The new computer can process multiple tasks in parallel.","furigana":"あたら|ふくすう|さぎょう|へいれつ|しょり"}],"notes":"並列 means \"to arrange side by side, on an equal footing\" and is mainly a technical word: in electrical engineering 並列回路 \"parallel circuit\" (opposite 直列 \"series\"), in IT 並列処理 \"parallel processing\", in grammar the listing of coordinate elements with と or や. Not to be confused with 並行 \"to run side by side at the same time\" and 平行 \"geometrically parallel\". As a する-verb it is mostly passive: 並列される.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私、並行して何かをするのは苦手なんです。","romaji":"Watashi, heikō shite nani ka o suru no wa nigate na n desu.","german":"I'm not good at doing several things at once.","furigana":"わたし|へいこう|なに|にがて"},{"japanese":"線路と並行して、川が流れている。","romaji":"Senro to heikou shite, kawa ga nagarete iru.","german":"A river flows alongside the railway tracks.","furigana":"せんろ|へいこう|かわ|なが"},{"japanese":"仕事と並行して、夜は大学院で勉強している。","romaji":"Shigoto to heikou shite, yoru wa daigakuin de benkyou shite iru.","german":"Alongside my job, I study at graduate school in the evenings.","furigana":"しごと|へいこう|よる|だいがくいん|べんきょう"}],"notes":"並行 means \"to run side by side\" and, figuratively, \"to proceed simultaneously\"; most often in the form 〜と並行して \"parallel to, at the same time as\". It is an intransitive する-verb. For geometrically parallel lines, people write 平行 (平行線), for parallel circuits 並列. In conversation, for simultaneous things people also say 同時に.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"修辞学のレポートの締め切りはいつですか？","romaji":"Shuujigaku no repooto no shimekiri wa itsu desu ka?","german":"When is the deadline for the rhetoric report?","furigana":"しゅうじがく|し|き"},{"japanese":"修辞学は古代ギリシャで生まれた学問です。","romaji":"Shuujigaku wa kodai Girisha de umareta gakumon desu.","german":"Rhetoric is a field of study that was born in ancient Greece.","furigana":"しゅうじがく|こだい|う|がくもん"},{"japanese":"大学で修辞学を学んでから、スピーチが上手になった。","romaji":"Daigaku de shuujigaku o manande kara, supiichi ga jouzu ni natta.","german":"After studying rhetoric at university, I became better at giving speeches.","furigana":"だいがく|しゅうじがく|まな|じょうず"}],"notes":"修辞学 is rhetoric as a science, from 修辞 \"rhetorical expression, figure of speech\" and 学 \"study\". The word belongs to an academic context (university subject, literary studies). In daily life people more often hear レトリック, often disparagingly for clever but empty words; eloquence itself is called 話術 or 弁論.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"レストランの傘立てに傘を入れたまま忘れて出てきてしまいました。","romaji":"Resutoran no kasatate ni kasa o ireta mama wasurete dete kite shimaimashita.","german":"I left my umbrella in the restaurant's umbrella stand and walked out without it.","furigana":"かさた|かさ|い|わす|で"},{"japanese":"濡れた傘は、入口の傘立てに入れてください。","romaji":"Nureta kasa wa, iriguchi no kasatate ni irete kudasai.","german":"Please put wet umbrellas in the umbrella stand at the entrance.","furigana":"ぬ|かさ|いりぐち|かさた|い"},{"japanese":"百円ショップで、おしゃれな傘立てを見つけた。","romaji":"Hyaku-en shoppu de, oshare na kasatate o mitsuketa.","german":"I found a stylish umbrella stand at a 100-yen shop.","furigana":"ひゃくえん|かさた|み"}],"notes":"傘立て is an umbrella stand, usually in the entrance area of homes, shops and restaurants. It is formed like 本立て \"bookend\" or ペン立て \"pen holder\" from a noun and 立て (from 立てる \"to set upright\"). Typical: 傘立てに傘を入れる/立てる. In shops there is often 傘袋 \"umbrella bag\" as an alternative for wet umbrellas.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の姉は、児童相談所で働いています。","romaji":"Watashi no ane wa, jidō sōdansho de hataraite imasu.","german":"My older sister works at a child guidance center.","furigana":"わたし|あね|じどうそうだんしょ|はたら"},{"japanese":"この小学校には、約三百人の児童が通っている。","romaji":"Kono shougakkou ni wa, yaku sanbyaku-nin no jidou ga kayotte iru.","german":"About three hundred pupils attend this elementary school.","furigana":"しょうがっこう|やくさんびゃくにん|じどう|かよ"},{"japanese":"図書館の二階に児童書のコーナーがあります。","romaji":"Toshokan no nikai ni jidousho no koonaa ga arimasu.","german":"There is a children's book corner on the second floor of the library.","furigana":"としょかん|にかい|じどうしょ"}],"notes":"児童 is a formal, official word for children; in the school context it refers specifically to elementary school pupils, while junior and senior high school students are 生徒 and university students 学生. It appears mainly in compounds: 児童書 \"children's book\", 児童公園, 児童相談所 \"child guidance center\". In everyday speech people simply say 子ども.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"できるだけ冷静な口調で話すようにした。","romaji":"Dekirudake reisei na kuchō de hanasu yō ni shita.","german":"I tried to speak in as calm a tone as possible.","furigana":"れいせい|くちょう|はな"},{"japanese":"地震のときこそ、冷静に行動してください。","romaji":"Jishin no toki koso, reisei ni koudou shite kudasai.","german":"Especially during an earthquake, please act calmly.","furigana":"じしん|れいせい|こうどう"},{"japanese":"ちょっと冷静になって、もう一度考えてみよう。","romaji":"Chotto reisei ni natte, mou ichido kangaete miyou.","german":"Let's calm down a little and think about it once more.","furigana":"れいせい|いちどかんが"}],"notes":"冷静 is a な-adjective for \"calm, level-headed, with a cool head\", especially in difficult situations: 冷静な判断, 冷静に行動する, 冷静になる \"to calm down\", 冷静さを失う \"to lose one's composure\". Unlike 冷たい \"cold, unfriendly\", 冷静 is positive. For simply calming down, people also say 落ち着く in daily life.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"うちの冷凍庫はいつもいっぱいなんです。","romaji":"Uchi no reitouko wa itsumo ippai na n desu.","german":"Our freezer is always full.","furigana":"れいとうこ"},{"japanese":"アイスは冷凍庫に入れておいてね。","romaji":"Aisu wa reitouko ni irete oite ne.","german":"Please put the ice cream in the freezer.","furigana":"れいとうこ|い"},{"japanese":"冷凍庫で作った氷をジュースに入れた。","romaji":"Reitouko de tsukutta koori o juusu ni ireta.","german":"I put ice made in the freezer into the juice.","furigana":"れいとうこ|つく|こおり|い"}],"notes":"冷凍庫 is the freezer compartment or freezer, from 冷凍 \"deep-freezing\" and 庫 \"storehouse\". Not to be confused with 冷蔵庫 \"refrigerator\". In Japanese refrigerators the freezer is usually a drawer, also called 冷凍室. Typical: 冷凍庫に入れる, 冷凍庫で凍らせる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"犬小屋でネズミが凍死していました。","romaji":"Inugoya de nezumi ga toushi shite imashita.","german":"A mouse had frozen to death in the doghouse.","furigana":"いぬごや|とうし"},{"japanese":"冬の山で道に迷って、凍死しそうになった。","romaji":"Fuyu no yama de michi ni mayotte, toushi shisou ni natta.","german":"I got lost in the mountains in winter and almost froze to death.","furigana":"ふゆ|やま|みち|まよ|とうし"},{"japanese":"昔は寒さで凍死する人も少なくなかった。","romaji":"Mukashi wa samusa de toushi suru hito mo sukunaku nakatta.","german":"In the past, quite a few people froze to death in the cold.","furigana":"むかし|さむ|とうし|ひと|すく"}],"notes":"凍死 means dying of cold (凍 \"to freeze\" + 死 \"death\"); as an intransitive する-verb: 凍死する \"to freeze to death\". Typical in news: 凍死者 \"people who froze to death\", 凍死しそうになる. Not to be confused with 凍傷 \"frostbite (on hands, feet)\", in which one does not die. In conversation people jokingly say 凍死しそう for \"I'm freezing to death\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"アカウントが凍結されちゃいました。","romaji":"Akaunto ga touketsu sarechaimashita.","german":"My account has been frozen.","furigana":"とうけつ"},{"japanese":"冬の朝は道路が凍結するので、運転に気をつけてください。","romaji":"Fuyu no asa wa douro ga touketsu suru node, unten ni ki o tsukete kudasai.","german":"On winter mornings the roads freeze over, so please drive carefully.","furigana":"ふゆ|あさ|どうろ|とうけつ|うんてん|き"},{"japanese":"会社は来年度の採用を凍結することにした。","romaji":"Kaisha wa rainendo no saiyou o touketsu suru koto ni shita.","german":"The company decided to freeze hiring for next fiscal year.","furigana":"かいしゃ|らいねんど|さいよう|とうけつ"}],"notes":"凍結 has two uses: literally \"to freeze, to ice over\" as an intransitive する-verb (道路が凍結する, 水道管が凍結する) and figuratively \"to freeze, to put on ice\" as a transitive verb (資産を凍結する, 計画を凍結する), often passive: アカウントが凍結される \"the account is suspended\". In daily life, 凍る is more usual for water; food is frozen with 冷凍する, not with 凍結.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いらないものを全部処分しました。","romaji":"Iranai mono o zenbu shobun shimashita.","german":"I got rid of everything I don't need.","furigana":"ぜんぶしょぶん"},{"japanese":"引っ越しの前に、古い家具を安く処分した。","romaji":"Hikkoshi no mae ni, furui kagu o yasuku shobun shita.","german":"Before moving, I disposed of the old furniture cheaply.","furigana":"ひ|こ|まえ|ふる|かぐ|やす|しょぶん"},{"japanese":"彼は規則を破って、停学処分を受けた。","romaji":"Kare wa kisoku o yabutte, teigaku shobun o uketa.","german":"He broke the rules and received a suspension from school.","furigana":"かれ|きそく|やぶ|ていがくしょぶん|う"}],"notes":"処分 means \"to get rid of something unwanted\": to dispose of it or sell it cheaply (古い家具を処分する, 処分セール). It sounds more matter-of-fact than 捨てる and includes selling. The second important meaning is \"punishment, disciplinary action\": 処分を受ける, 停学処分, 厳しい処分. Transitive する-verb with を.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しいパスポートは、旅券番号も新しくなるはずだよ。","romaji":"Atarashii pasupōto wa, ryoken bangō mo atarashiku naru hazu da yo.","german":"With a new passport, the passport number should also be new.","furigana":"あたら|りょけんばんごう|あたら"},{"japanese":"旅券の有効期限を確認してください。","romaji":"Ryoken no yuukou kigen o kakunin shite kudasai.","german":"Please check the passport's expiration date.","furigana":"りょけん|ゆうこうきげん|かくにん"},{"japanese":"旅券の申請には、写真が一枚必要です。","romaji":"Ryoken no shinsei ni wa, shashin ga ichimai hitsuyou desu.","german":"One photo is required for a passport application.","furigana":"りょけん|しんせい|しゃしん|いちまいひつよう"}],"notes":"旅券 is the official term for the passport and is found mainly on forms, in laws and in official letters: 旅券番号, 旅券の申請, 一般旅券. In everyday speech one almost always says パスポート. The word is composed of 旅 \"travel\" and 券 \"ticket, certificate\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"前売り券はどこで買えるんですか？","romaji":"Maeuriken wa doko de kaeru n desu ka?","german":"Where can I buy advance tickets?","furigana":"まえう|けん|か"},{"japanese":"前売り券は当日券より三百円安い。","romaji":"Maeuriken wa toujitsuken yori sanbyaku-en yasui.","german":"Advance tickets are 300 yen cheaper than tickets on the day.","furigana":"まえう|けん|とうじつけん|さんびゃくえんやす"},{"japanese":"人気のライブなので、前売り券はすぐに売り切れた。","romaji":"Ninki no raibu na node, maeuriken wa sugu ni urikireta.","german":"It's a popular live show, so advance tickets sold out quickly.","furigana":"にんき|まえう|けん|う|き"}],"notes":"前売り券 is an admission ticket bought in advance, from 前売り \"advance sale\" and 券 \"ticket\"; the counterpart is 当日券 \"ticket sold on the day\". Typical: 前売り券を買う/購入する, 前売り券が売り切れる. It is often written without り (前売券); colloquially it is also shortened to 前売り.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あなたのような創造力のある人と仕事ができることを、とても楽しみにしています。","romaji":"Anata no yō na sōzōryoku no aru hito to shigoto ga dekiru koto o, totemo tanoshimi ni shite imasu.","german":"I'm really looking forward to working with a creative person like you.","furigana":"そうぞうりょく|ひと|しごと|たの"},{"japanese":"この仕事には、何より創造力が必要だ。","romaji":"Kono shigoto ni wa, nani yori souzouryoku ga hitsuyou da.","german":"This job requires creativity above all.","furigana":"しごと|なに|そうぞうりょく|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"絵を描くことは、子どもの創造力を育てるのに役立つ。","romaji":"E o kaku koto wa, kodomo no souzouryoku o sodateru no ni yakudatsu.","german":"Drawing pictures helps develop children's creativity.","furigana":"え|か|こ|そうぞうりょく|そだ|やくだ"}],"notes":"創造力 is the ability to create something new: 創造力がある, 創造力が豊かだ, 創造力を育てる/発揮する. Watch out: the identically read 想像力 means \"imagination, fantasy\" - 創造 means \"to create\", 想像 \"to imagine\". The corresponding adjective is 創造的な \"creative\"; casually people also say クリエイティブ.","levelNote":"Compound: 創造 (N2) + 力 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いつの時代も、創造的な仕事につきたいと思っている若者は多い。","romaji":"Itsu no jidai mo, sōzōteki na shigoto ni tsukitai to omotte iru wakamono wa ōi.","german":"In every era, there are many young people who want to take up creative work.","furigana":"じだい|そうぞうてき|しごと|おも|わかもの|おお"},{"japanese":"もっと創造的な解決方法を考えよう。","romaji":"Motto souzouteki na kaiketsu houhou o kangaeyou.","german":"Let's think of a more creative solution.","furigana":"そうぞうてき|かいけつほうほう|かんが"},{"japanese":"この会社では、社員が創造的に働ける環境を大切にしている。","romaji":"Kono kaisha de wa, shain ga souzouteki ni hatarakeru kankyou o taisetsu ni shite iru.","german":"This company values an environment where employees can work creatively.","furigana":"かいしゃ|しゃいん|そうぞうてき|はたら|かんきょう|たいせつ"}],"notes":"創造的 is a な-adjective for \"creative, inventive\": 創造的な仕事, 創造的な発想, adverbially 創造的に考える. It sounds somewhat more formal than the loanword クリエイティブ, which is common in everyday speech. The opposite would be something like 模倣的 \"imitative\". Not to be confused with 想像的, which is hardly used.","levelNote":"Compound: 創造 (N2) + 的 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"博打でお金を全部使い果たしてしまった。","romaji":"Bakuchi de okane o zenbu tsukaihatashite shimatta.","german":"I spent all my money on gambling.","furigana":"ばくち|かね|ぜんぶつか|は"},{"japanese":"新しい店を出すのは、一種の博打だ。","romaji":"Atarashii mise o dasu no wa, isshu no bakuchi da.","german":"Opening a new shop is a kind of gamble.","furigana":"あたら|みせ|だ|いっしゅ|ばくち"},{"japanese":"父は若い頃、博打で大きな借金を作った。","romaji":"Chichi wa wakai koro, bakuchi de ookina shakkin o tsukutta.","german":"When he was young, my father ran up a huge debt through gambling.","furigana":"ちち|わか|ころ|ばくち|おお|しゃっきん|つく"}],"notes":"博打 is gambling for money, often written ばくち in kana; it sounds somewhat old-fashioned and colloquial. Typical: 博打を打つ \"to gamble\", 博打で負ける. Figuratively it refers to a risky undertaking: それは一種の博打だ. In modern usage people mostly say ギャンブル, legally 賭博.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"毎日朝の番組で今日の星占いを見てから学校に行きます。","romaji":"Mainichi asa no bangumi de kyou no hoshiuranai o mite kara gakkou ni ikimasu.","german":"Every day I watch today's horoscope on the morning show and then go to school.","furigana":"まいにちあさ|ばんぐみ|きょう|ほしうらな|み|がっこう|い"},{"japanese":"星占いによると、今日は恋愛運がいいらしい。","romaji":"Hoshiuranai ni yoru to, kyou wa ren'aiun ga ii rashii.","german":"According to the horoscope, I apparently have good luck in love today.","furigana":"ほしうらな|きょう|れんあいうん"},{"japanese":"星占いなんて、全然信じていないよ。","romaji":"Hoshiuranai nante, zenzen shinjite inai yo.","german":"I don't believe in horoscopes at all.","furigana":"ほしうらな|ぜんぜんしん"}],"notes":"星占い is divination by star sign (星座), that is, astrology or a horoscope; 星座占い means the same. In Japan there are daily horoscopes on morning TV and in magazines: 今日の星占い, 星占いによると. Fortune-telling in general is 占い, and the fortune it predicts is 運勢.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 32"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ウナギは、昔から夏の暑さに負けないための妙薬として人気がありました。","romaji":"Unagi wa, mukashi kara natsu no atsusa ni makenai tame no myōyaku toshite ninki ga arimashita.","german":"Eel has long been popular as a wonder food for beating the summer heat.","furigana":"むかし|なつ|あつ|ま|みょうやく|にんき"},{"japanese":"笑いは何よりの妙薬だと言われる。","romaji":"Warai wa nani yori no myouyaku da to iwareru.","german":"Laughter is said to be the best medicine.","furigana":"わら|なに|みょうやく|い"},{"japanese":"失恋に効く妙薬なんてないよ。","romaji":"Shitsuren ni kiku myouyaku nante nai yo.","german":"There is no miracle cure for a broken heart.","furigana":"しつれん|き|みょうやく"}],"notes":"妙薬 is a \"miracle cure\", from 妙 \"wonderful, mysterious\" and 薬 \"medicine\". The word sounds literary and is mostly used figuratively: 〜の妙薬, 〜に効く妙薬, 何よりの妙薬 \"the best remedy\". A remedy for a specific illness is matter-of-factly called 特効薬.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"婦人服売り場はどこですか？","romaji":"Fujinfuku uriba wa doko desu ka?","german":"Where is the women's clothing section?","furigana":"ふじんふくう|ば"},{"japanese":"会場には、着物姿のご婦人が大勢いた。","romaji":"Kaijou ni wa, kimono sugata no gofujin ga oozei ita.","german":"There were many ladies in kimono at the venue.","furigana":"かいじょう|きものすがた|ふじん|おおぜい"},{"japanese":"駅前の婦人科で診てもらった。","romaji":"Ekimae no fujinka de mite moratta.","german":"I was examined at the gynecologist's in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|ふじんか|み"}],"notes":"婦人 is an older, formal word for \"lady, adult woman\"; as a standalone word it is considered dated today, and people mostly say 女性. It lives on in compounds: 婦人服 \"women's clothing\", 婦人科 \"gynecology\", 婦人用. Politely, one speaks of a lady one doesn't know as ご婦人. Do not confuse it with 夫人 \"wife\" (〜夫人 \"Mrs. 〜\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの子、まだ幼いのに、かわいそうだね。","romaji":"Ano ko, mada osanai noni, kawaisō da ne.","german":"That child is still so young, poor thing.","furigana":"こ|おさな"},{"japanese":"幼い頃、よく祖母の家に遊びに行った。","romaji":"Osanai koro, yoku sobo no ie ni asobi ni itta.","german":"When I was little, I often went to play at my grandmother's house.","furigana":"おさな|ころ|そぼ|いえ|あそ|い"},{"japanese":"考え方がまだ幼いと、上司に言われた。","romaji":"Kangaekata ga mada osanai to, joushi ni iwareta.","german":"My boss told me that my way of thinking is still immature.","furigana":"かんが|かた|おさな|じょうし|い"}],"notes":"幼い describes children at a very young age: 幼い子ども, 幼い頃 \"when I was little\". Figuratively it means \"childish, immature\" (考え方が幼い). For size, one says 小さい, for young adults 若い; 子どもっぽい is more colloquial and more derogatory than 幼い. i-adjective: 幼くて, 幼かった.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"『幼年時代』は、トルストイが自分の子どもの頃をもとに書いた小説です。","romaji":"Younen Jidai wa, Torusutoi ga jibun no kodomo no koro o moto ni kaita shousetsu desu.","german":"\"Childhood\" is a novel Tolstoy wrote based on his own childhood.","furigana":"ようねんじだい|じぶん|こ|ころ|か|しょうせつ"},{"japanese":"祖父は幼年時代を北海道の田舎で過ごした。","romaji":"Sofu wa younen jidai o Hokkaidou no inaka de sugoshita.","german":"My grandfather spent his childhood in the countryside of Hokkaido.","furigana":"そふ|ようねんじだい|ほっかいどう|いなか|す"},{"japanese":"幼年時代の思い出は、今でもはっきり覚えている。","romaji":"Younen jidai no omoide wa, ima demo hakkiri oboete iru.","german":"I still remember memories of my childhood clearly.","furigana":"ようねんじだい|おも|で|いま|おぼ"}],"notes":"幼年時代 refers to early childhood, roughly up to school age; it sounds literary and appears in biographies and memoirs. Typical: 幼年時代を過ごす, 幼年時代の思い出. In conversation people more often say 子どもの頃 or 子ども時代; the following stage of life is called 少年時代.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"家に金庫はありますか？","romaji":"Ie ni kinko wa arimasu ka?","german":"Do you have a safe at home?","furigana":"いえ|きんこ"},{"japanese":"大事な書類は金庫にしまってあります。","romaji":"Daiji na shorui wa kinko ni shimatte arimasu.","german":"Important documents are kept in the safe.","furigana":"だいじ|しょるい|きんこ"},{"japanese":"夜の間に、店の金庫からお金が盗まれた。","romaji":"Yoru no aida ni, mise no kinko kara okane ga nusumareta.","german":"During the night, money was stolen from the shop's safe.","furigana":"よる|あいだ|みせ|きんこ|かね|ぬす"}],"notes":"金庫 is a safe or strongbox: 金庫にしまう, 金庫を開ける, 金庫破り \"safecracker\". The second meaning \"treasury, financial institution\" is found in names like 信用金庫 \"credit association\". A safe-deposit box at a bank, by contrast, is called 貸金庫, and a small coin locker コインロッカー.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は、タイの憲政史上最も若い首相です。","romaji":"Kanojo wa, Tai no kensei shijō mottomo wakai shushō desu.","german":"She is the youngest prime minister in the history of Thailand's constitutional government.","furigana":"かのじょ|けんせいしじょうもっと|わか|しゅしょう"},{"japanese":"憲政とは、憲法に基づいて政治を行うことだ。","romaji":"Kensei to wa, kenpou ni motozuite seiji o okonau koto da.","german":"Constitutionalism means conducting politics on the basis of the constitution.","furigana":"けんせい|けんぽう|もと|せいじ|おこな"},{"japanese":"この国では、百年以上前から憲政が続いている。","romaji":"Kono kuni de wa, hyakunen ijou mae kara kensei ga tsuzuite iru.","german":"In this country, constitutional government has continued for more than a hundred years.","furigana":"くに|ひゃくねんいじょうまえ|けんせい|つづ"}],"notes":"憲政 is government based on a constitution, from 憲法 \"constitution\" and 政治 \"politics\". The word belongs to political, written language and appears mainly in set phrases such as 憲政史上 \"in the history of constitutional government\", 憲政の常道 \"the normal course of constitutional government\" or in the synonym 立憲政治. It hardly occurs in everyday speech.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"無我夢中でピアノを弾いた。","romaji":"Muga muchū de piano o hiita.","german":"I played the piano in a state of complete absorption.","furigana":"むがむちゅう|ひ"},{"japanese":"火事の時、無我夢中で子供を抱いて外へ逃げた。","romaji":"Kaji no toki, muga muchū de kodomo o daite soto e nigeta.","german":"When the fire broke out, I frantically picked up my child and fled outside.","furigana":"かじ|とき|むがむちゅう|こども|だ|そと|に"},{"japanese":"無我夢中で走っていたら、いつの間にか駅に着いていた。","romaji":"Muga muchū de hashitte itara, itsu no ma ni ka eki ni tsuite ita.","german":"I was running in a daze, and before I knew it I had arrived at the station.","furigana":"むがむちゅう|はし|ま|えき|つ"}],"notes":"Literally: 無我 \"without self, selfless\" and 夢中 \"as in a dream, completely absorbed\". It means being so wrapped up in something, or acting under pressure in such a way, that one forgets oneself and one's surroundings. Almost always adverbial as 無我夢中で + verb (走る, 逃げる, 練習する), also 無我夢中になる. Neutral; fits both enthusiasm and emergencies.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本の人たちと、身振り手振りで何とかコミュニケーションをとることができた。","romaji":"Nihon no hitotachi to, miburi teburi de nantoka komyunikēshon o toru koto ga dekita.","german":"I was somehow able to communicate with the Japanese people using gestures.","furigana":"にほん|ひと|みぶ|てぶ|なん"},{"japanese":"彼は話すとき、身振りが大きい。","romaji":"Kare wa hanasu toki, miburi ga ookii.","german":"He makes big gestures when he talks.","furigana":"かれ|はな|みぶ|おお"},{"japanese":"道に迷った観光客が、身振りで駅の場所を聞いてきた。","romaji":"Michi ni mayotta kankoukyaku ga, miburi de eki no basho o kiite kita.","german":"A tourist who had lost his way asked for the station's location using gestures.","furigana":"みち|まよ|かんこうきゃく|みぶ|えき|ばしょ|き"}],"notes":"身振り is a movement of the body with which one expresses something, that is, gesture or gesticulation. Very common is the fixed phrase 身振り手振りで \"with gestures, using hands and feet\", for example when communicating without a shared language. Also typical are 身振りが大きい and 身振りで伝える. Synonymous but more casual is the loanword ジェスチャー.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日は面接官として、受験生を十人面接しました。","romaji":"Kyou wa mensetsukan toshite, jukensei o juunin mensetsu shimashita.","german":"Today, as an examiner, I interviewed ten applicants.","furigana":"きょう|めんせつかん|じゅけんせい|じゅうにんめんせつ"},{"japanese":"部長が午後、アルバイトの応募者を面接する予定です。","romaji":"Buchou ga gogo, arubaito no oubosha o mensetsu suru yotei desu.","german":"The department head plans to interview applicants for the part-time job this afternoon.","furigana":"ぶちょう|ごご|おうぼしゃ|めんせつ|よてい"},{"japanese":"遠くに住んでいるので、オンラインで面接してもらえることになった。","romaji":"Tooku ni sunde iru node, onrain de mensetsu shite moraeru koto ni natta.","german":"Since I live far away, it was decided that I can have my interview online.","furigana":"とお|す|めんせつ"}],"notes":"面接する means \"to conduct an interview or selection interview\" from the point of view of the examining side: 応募者を面接する. The applicant, by contrast, says 面接を受ける \"to go to an interview\". Related are 面接官 \"interviewer\" and 面接試験 \"oral selection exam\". An interview with the media is called インタビュー, not 面接.","levelNote":"Derived from 面接 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"動くな。じっとしていないと、撃つぞ！","romaji":"Ugoku na. Jitto shite inai to, utsu zo!","german":"Don't move. If you don't stay still, I'll shoot!","furigana":"うご|う"},{"japanese":"警察官は犯人の足を撃った。","romaji":"Keisatsukan wa hannin no ashi o utta.","german":"The police officer shot the criminal in the leg.","furigana":"けいさつかん|はんにん|あし|う"},{"japanese":"運動会では、先生がピストルを撃ってスタートの合図をする。","romaji":"Undoukai de wa, sensei ga pisutoru o utte sutaato no aizu o suru.","german":"At the sports day, the teacher fires a pistol to signal the start.","furigana":"うんどうかい|せんせい|う|あいず"}],"notes":"撃つ means \"to shoot, to fire\" with a firearm: 銃を撃つ, 人や動物を撃つ. The homophones 打つ \"to hit, to type\" and 討つ \"to defeat, to avenge\" are read the same; the spelling shows the meaning. Transitive Godan verb; the passive 撃たれる \"to be shot\" is common.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"サーバーがサイバー攻撃を受けている。","romaji":"Sābā ga saibā kōgeki o ukete iru.","german":"The server is under a cyberattack.","furigana":"こうげき|う"},{"japanese":"サッカーでは、攻撃と守備のバランスが大切だ。","romaji":"Sakkaa de wa, kougeki to shubi no baransu ga taisetsu da.","german":"In soccer, the balance between attack and defense is important.","furigana":"こうげき|しゅび|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"彼は会議で部長の案を激しく攻撃した。","romaji":"Kare wa kaigi de buchou no an o hageshiku kougeki shita.","german":"He fiercely attacked the department head's proposal at the meeting.","furigana":"かれ|かいぎ|ぶちょう|あん|はげ|こうげき"}],"notes":"攻撃 is an attack - military, in sports or verbal: 攻撃する, 攻撃を受ける \"to be attacked\", サイバー攻撃. The opposite term is 守備 or 防御 \"defense\". Figuratively it stands for sharp criticism (人を攻撃する); the adjective 攻撃的 means \"aggressive\". The related verb 攻める is more everyday and especially common in sports.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"明日、敵の城を攻めるぞ。","romaji":"Ashita, teki no shiro o semeru zo.","german":"Tomorrow we attack the enemy's castle!","furigana":"あした|てき|しろ|せ"},{"japanese":"後半は、うちのチームがずっと攻めていた。","romaji":"Kouhan wa, uchi no chiimu ga zutto semete ita.","german":"In the second half, our team was attacking the whole time.","furigana":"こうはん|せ"},{"japanese":"今回のデザインは、かなり攻めているね。","romaji":"Konkai no dezain wa, kanari semete iru ne.","german":"This design is quite daring.","furigana":"こんかい|せ"}],"notes":"攻める means \"to attack, to go on the offensive\": 城を攻める, 相手のゴールを攻める; the opposite is 守る. Careful: the homophone 責める means \"to reproach, to blame\". Colloquially 攻めている also means \"to be bold, daring\" (攻めたデザイン). Transitive Ichidan verb; the noun 攻め means \"attack\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これと同時に、ソ連が反攻を開始しました。","romaji":"Kore to dōji ni, Soren ga hankō o kaishi shimashita.","german":"At the same time, the Soviet Union launched a counteroffensive.","furigana":"どうじ|れん|はんこう|かいし"},{"japanese":"敵が弱ったところで、味方は一気に反攻した。","romaji":"Teki ga yowatta tokoro de, mikata wa ikki ni hankou shita.","german":"When the enemy weakened, our side counterattacked all at once.","furigana":"てき|よわ|みかた|いっき|はんこう"},{"japanese":"歴史の授業で、連合軍の反攻作戦について習った。","romaji":"Rekishi no jugyou de, rengougun no hankou sakusen ni tsuite naratta.","german":"In history class, we learned about the Allied counteroffensive operations.","furigana":"れきし|じゅぎょう|れんごうぐん|はんこうさくせん|なら"}],"notes":"反攻 is a counterattack, especially the counteroffensive of a side that was previously inferior in war: 反攻を開始する, 反攻に出る, 反攻作戦. The word is military-historical and literary. For counterattacks in sports, quarrels or discussions, people almost always say 反撃. Not to be confused with the identically read 反抗 \"resistance, defiance\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"横浜まで乗せてもらえませんか？","romaji":"Yokohama made nosete moraemasen ka?","german":"Could you give me a ride to Yokohama?","furigana":"よこはま|の"},{"japanese":"横浜の中華街で昼ご飯を食べた。","romaji":"Yokohama no chuukagai de hirugohan o tabeta.","german":"I had lunch in Yokohama's Chinatown.","furigana":"よこはま|ちゅうかがい|ひる|はん|た"},{"japanese":"横浜は東京から電車で三十分ぐらいです。","romaji":"Yokohama wa Toukyou kara densha de sanjuppun gurai desu.","german":"Yokohama is about thirty minutes from Tokyo by train.","furigana":"よこはま|とうきょう|でんしゃ|さんじゅっぷん"}],"notes":"横浜 is the port city south of Tokyo in Kanagawa Prefecture and, by population, the second-largest city in Japan. The characters mean roughly \"sideways beach\". Well known are 横浜港, the 中華街 \"Chinatown\" and みなとみらい; residents are called 横浜市民, colloquially also ハマっ子.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"でも、心の清い人にしか見えないんだって。","romaji":"Demo, kokoro no kiyoi hito ni shika mienai n da tte.","german":"But I heard it can only be seen by people with a pure heart.","furigana":"こころ|きよ|ひと|み"},{"japanese":"この川の水は清くて、魚がたくさん泳いでいる。","romaji":"Kono kawa no mizu wa kiyokute, sakana ga takusan oyoide iru.","german":"The water in this river is clear, and many fish swim in it.","furigana":"かわ|みず|きよ|さかな|およ"},{"japanese":"選挙の前には「清き一票を」というポスターが貼られる。","romaji":"Senkyo no mae ni wa 'kiyoki ippyou o' to iu posutaa ga harareru.","german":"Before elections, posters appear saying \"Cast your honest vote.\"","furigana":"せんきょ|まえ|きよ|いっぴょう|は"}],"notes":"清い means \"clear, pure\" of water or air and, figuratively, \"pure, innocent\" of heart and character: 清い水, 心の清い人, 清い交際 \"platonic relationship\". It sounds elevated and literary; in everyday speech people say きれい or 澄んだ. The classical attributive form 清き lives on in phrases such as 清き一票 \"an honest vote (in elections)\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不潔な手で私をさわらないでください！","romaji":"Fuketsu na te de watashi o sawaranaide kudasai!","german":"Please don't touch me with those dirty hands!","furigana":"ふけつ|て|わたし"},{"japanese":"不潔なトイレは使いたくない。","romaji":"Fuketsu na toire wa tsukaitakunai.","german":"I don't want to use a dirty toilet.","furigana":"ふけつ|つか"},{"japanese":"手を洗わないで料理するなんて、不潔だよ。","romaji":"Te o arawanaide ryouri suru nante, fuketsu da yo.","german":"Cooking without washing your hands is unsanitary.","furigana":"て|あら|りょうり|ふけつ"}],"notes":"不潔 is a な-adjective for \"unhygienic, dirty\" and stresses a lack of cleanliness that causes disgust: 不潔な手, 不潔なトイレ. The opposite is 清潔. General \"dirty\" is 汚い; 不潔 is stronger and can also mean morally \"indecent, dirty\" (不潔な金).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"有益なアドバイス、ありがとうございます。","romaji":"Yūeki na adobaisu, arigatō gozaimasu.","german":"Thank you for the useful advice.","furigana":"ゆうえき"},{"japanese":"この本には、旅行に有益な情報がたくさん載っている。","romaji":"Kono hon ni wa, ryokou ni yuueki na jouhou ga takusan notte iru.","german":"This book contains a lot of information useful for travel.","furigana":"ほん|りょこう|ゆうえき|じょうほう|の"},{"japanese":"せっかくの休みを有益に使いたい。","romaji":"Sekkaku no yasumi o yuueki ni tsukaitai.","german":"I want to make good use of my precious time off.","furigana":"やす|ゆうえき|つか"}],"notes":"有益 is a な-adjective for \"useful, profitable, instructive\", often used for information, advice or time: 有益な情報, 有益なアドバイス, 時間を有益に使う. It sounds more formal than 役に立つ; 便利, by contrast, means \"handy, convenient\". Opposites are 無益 \"useless\" and 有害 \"harmful\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は公益法人で働いています。","romaji":"Watashi wa kōeki hōjin de hataraite imasu.","german":"I work at a public interest corporation.","furigana":"わたし|こうえきほうじん|はたら"},{"japanese":"この事業は、公益のために行われている。","romaji":"Kono jigyou wa, koueki no tame ni okonawarete iru.","german":"This project is carried out for the public good.","furigana":"じぎょう|こうえき|おこな"},{"japanese":"個人の利益より公益を優先するべきだ。","romaji":"Kojin no rieki yori koueki o yuusen suru beki da.","german":"The public interest should take priority over individual interests.","furigana":"こじん|りえき|こうえき|ゆうせん"}],"notes":"公益 is the public good, i.e. the benefit to the general public, from 公 \"public\" and 益 \"benefit\". The word is formal and often appears in compounds: 公益法人 \"public interest corporation\", 公益事業 \"public utility\". The opposite term is 私益 \"self-interest\"; in everyday speech people say 社会のため or 公共の利益.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"国益を守るのが外交官の仕事でしょ？","romaji":"Kokueki o mamoru no ga gaikōkan no shigoto desho?","german":"Protecting the national interest is a diplomat's job, isn't it?","furigana":"こくえき|まも|がいこうかん|しごと"},{"japanese":"首相は「国益を第一に考える」と述べた。","romaji":"Shushou wa \"kokueki o daiichi ni kangaeru\" to nobeta.","german":"The prime minister stated, \"We will put the national interest first.\"","furigana":"しゅしょう|こくえき|だいいち|かんが|の"},{"japanese":"この政策は国益に反するという声もある。","romaji":"Kono seisaku wa kokueki ni hansuru to iu koe mo aru.","german":"Some say this policy goes against the national interest.","furigana":"せいさく|こくえき|はん|こえ"}],"notes":"国益 is the \"national interest\" of a state and belongs to the language of politics, diplomacy and the news. Typical combinations are 国益を守る \"to safeguard the national interest\", 国益を損なう \"to harm the national interest\", 国益になる or 国益にかなう \"to be in the national interest\" and 国益に反する \"to run counter to the national interest\". The general 利益 means only \"profit, benefit\"; 国益 always refers to the state as a whole.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"銀座に事務所を移すことを検討しています。","romaji":"Ginza ni jimusho o utsusu koto o kentō shite imasu.","german":"We are considering moving the office to Ginza.","furigana":"ぎんざ|じむしょ|うつ|けんとう"},{"japanese":"来月から、いよいよ計画を実行に移します。","romaji":"Raigetsu kara, iyoiyo keikaku o jikkou ni utsushimasu.","german":"From next month, we will finally put the plan into action.","furigana":"らいげつ|けいかく|じっこう|うつ"},{"japanese":"子どもに風邪をうつされてしまった。","romaji":"Kodomo ni kaze o utsusarete shimatta.","german":"I caught a cold from my child.","furigana":"こ|かぜ"}],"notes":"移す is transitive: you bring something from A to B, often in the pattern AからBに〜を移す (事務所を銀座に移す). Figuratively, 計画を実行に移す means \"to put a plan into action\" and 視線を移す \"to shift one's gaze elsewhere\". For passing on an illness (風邪をうつす) it is mostly written in kana. The intransitive partner is 移る \"to move, to shift, to catch (an illness)\"; do not confuse it with the homophones 写す \"to copy, to photograph\" and 映す \"to reflect, to project\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今のところ、日本への移住の予定は無いです。","romaji":"Ima no tokoro, Nihon e no ijuu no yotei wa nai desu.","german":"For now, I have no plans to move to Japan.","furigana":"いま|にほん|いじゅう|よてい|な"},{"japanese":"定年後は沖縄に移住したいと考えています。","romaji":"Teinen-go wa Okinawa ni ijuu shitai to kangaete imasu.","german":"After retirement, I'm thinking of moving to Okinawa.","furigana":"ていねんご|おきなわ|いじゅう|かんが"},{"japanese":"昔、多くの日本人がブラジルに移住した。","romaji":"Mukashi, ooku no Nihonjin ga Burajiru ni ijuu shita.","german":"In the past, many Japanese people emigrated to Brazil.","furigana":"むかし|おお|にほんじん|いじゅう"}],"notes":"移住 means settling permanently in a different place, abroad or within the country (e.g. 地方への移住 \"moving to the countryside\"). As an intransitive する-verb the destination takes に: ブラジルに移住する; as a noun with への: 日本への移住. An ordinary change of residence is called 引っ越し; 移民 rather means \"migrant(s)\" or migration as a social phenomenon.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"うちの夫、本当に幼稚なんだよね。","romaji":"Uchi no otto, hontōni yōchi na n da yo ne.","german":"My husband is really childish.","furigana":"おっと|ほんとう|ようち"},{"japanese":"そんな幼稚なうそ、すぐばれるよ。","romaji":"Sonna youchi na uso, sugu bareru yo.","german":"A childish lie like that will be found out right away.","furigana":"ようち"},{"japanese":"この作文は、内容が少し幼稚ですね。","romaji":"Kono sakubun wa, naiyou ga sukoshi youchi desu ne.","german":"The content of this essay is a bit childish.","furigana":"さくぶん|ないよう|すこ|ようち"}],"notes":"幼稚 is a な-adjective and sounds derogatory: it describes the behavior, thinking or work of adults as \"childish, immature, primitive\" (幼稚な考え, 幼稚なうそ). Neutrally, the kanji means \"young child\" only in words such as 幼稚園 \"kindergarten\". Colloquially milder is 子どもっぽい; 未熟 means more \"not yet mature, inexperienced\" without mockery.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"恋人との関係を絶つ方法を教えてください。","romaji":"Koibito to no kankei o tatsu hōhō o oshiete kudasai.","german":"Please tell me how to end my relationship with my partner.","furigana":"こいびと|かんけい|た|ほうほう|おし"},{"japanese":"彼は会社を辞めてから、みんなとの連絡を絶った。","romaji":"Kare wa kaisha o yamete kara, minna to no renraku o tatta.","german":"After quitting the company, he cut off contact with everyone.","furigana":"かれ|かいしゃ|や|れんらく|た"},{"japanese":"この交差点では、事故が後を絶たない。","romaji":"Kono kousaten de wa, jiko ga ato o tatanai.","german":"Accidents keep happening at this intersection.","furigana":"こうさてん|じこ|あと|た"}],"notes":"絶つ is transitive and means to deliberately and definitively break something off: 関係を絶つ \"to sever a relationship\", 連絡を絶つ \"to cut off contact\", 消息を絶つ \"to go missing\". Very common is the fixed phrase 後を絶たない \"does not stop, never ends\". In the sense of \"to give up something\" (酒を断つ) and \"to cut off\", 断つ is usually written; the intransitive partner of 絶つ is 絶える \"to cease, to die out\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お母さん、心配しなくていいよ。あのお菓子屋さんなら僕、一人でも行けるよ。","romaji":"Okāsan, shinpai shinakute ii yo. Ano okashiya-san nara boku, hitori demo ikeru yo.","german":"Mom, you don't need to worry. I can go to that sweet shop by myself.","furigana":"かあ|しんぱい|かしや|ぼく|ひとり|い"},{"japanese":"駅前のお菓子屋さんで、お土産を買いました。","romaji":"Ekimae no okashiya-san de, omiyage o kaimashita.","german":"I bought souvenirs at the sweet shop in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|かしや|みやげ|か"},{"japanese":"子どものころは、お菓子屋になるのが夢でした。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro wa, okashiya ni naru no ga yume deshita.","german":"When I was a child, my dream was to become a confectioner.","furigana":"こ|かしや|ゆめ"}],"notes":"お菓子屋 is a shop for sweets and pastries; in conversation people almost always add さん (お菓子屋さん), as in パン屋さん or 本屋さん. With 屋, the person who runs the shop can also be meant. More specific are 和菓子屋 for Japanese sweets and 駄菓子屋 for the nostalgic shop with cheap children's sweets; in written, business style it is 菓子店.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"そのシャツ、前と後ろが逆じゃない？","romaji":"Sono shatsu, mae to ushiro ga gyaku ja nai?","german":"Isn't that shirt on backwards?","furigana":"まえ|うし|ぎゃく"},{"japanese":"薬を飲んだら、逆に具合が悪くなった。","romaji":"Kusuri o nondara, gyaku ni guai ga waruku natta.","german":"After I took the medicine, I actually felt worse.","furigana":"くすり|の|ぎゃく|ぐあい|わる"},{"japanese":"駅は逆の方向ですよ。","romaji":"Eki wa gyaku no houkou desu yo.","german":"The station is in the opposite direction.","furigana":"えき|ぎゃく|ほうこう"}],"notes":"逆 means \"reversed, opposite, the contrary\" and is used as a noun or な-adjective: 逆の方向 \"the opposite direction\", 順番が逆だ \"the order is reversed\". Very common is the adverb 逆に \"on the contrary, conversely\", in conversation often also as an introduction (逆に言えば). Unlike 反対, which also means \"objection, being against\", 逆 describes only the reversal; as a prefix it is found in 逆効果 or 逆転.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"それ、逆効果じゃない？","romaji":"Sore, gyaku kōka ja nai?","german":"Isn't that counterproductive?","furigana":"ぎゃくこうか"},{"japanese":"子どもを叱りすぎると、逆効果になることもある。","romaji":"Kodomo o shikarisugiru to, gyakukouka ni naru koto mo aru.","german":"Scolding a child too much can sometimes backfire.","furigana":"こ|しか|ぎゃくこうか"},{"japanese":"寝る前にお酒を飲むのは、睡眠には逆効果です。","romaji":"Neru mae ni osake o nomu no wa, suimin ni wa gyakukouka desu.","german":"Drinking alcohol before bed has the opposite effect for sleep.","furigana":"ね|まえ|さけ|の|すいみん|ぎゃくこうか"}],"notes":"逆効果 is an effect that runs counter to the actual goal: \"counterproductive, to backfire\". Grammatically it is a noun and is usually used with だ or になる: 逆効果だ, 逆効果になる, with reference to something as 〜には逆効果. Do not confuse it with 副作用 \"side effect\" or 効果がない \"ineffective\" - with 逆効果, the measure makes things worse.","levelNote":"Compound: 逆 (N3) + 効果 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの議員は、逆説的な言い方をよく使います。","romaji":"Ano giin wa, gyakusetsuteki na iikata o yoku tsukaimasu.","german":"That politician often uses paradoxical expressions.","furigana":"ぎいん|ぎゃくせつてき|い|かた|つか"},{"japanese":"「負けるが勝ち」は、よく知られた逆説です。","romaji":"\"Makeru ga kachi\" wa, yoku shirareta gyakusetsu desu.","german":"\"Losing is winning\" is a well-known paradox.","furigana":"ま|か|し|ぎゃくせつ"},{"japanese":"逆説的に言えば、失敗が多い人ほど成功に近い。","romaji":"Gyakusetsuteki ni ieba, shippai ga ooi hito hodo seikou ni chikai.","german":"Paradoxically speaking, the more failures a person has, the closer they are to success.","furigana":"ぎゃくせつてき|い|しっぱい|おお|ひと|せいこう|ちか"}],"notes":"逆説 is a \"paradox\": a statement that sounds contradictory but contains a truth, such as 負けるが勝ち \"losing is winning\". The derivation 逆説的な / 逆説的に \"paradoxical, paradoxically\" is common. Watch out: the identically read 逆接 is a grammatical term for adversative connections such as しかし or けれども. The word belongs more to written, elevated language.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ニコニコ笑っておけば何とかなるでしょって思ってる人は大間違いですよ。","romaji":"Nikoniko waratte okeba nantoka naru desho tte omotteru hito wa oomachigai desu yo.","german":"Anyone who thinks that things will work out somehow if they just keep smiling is greatly mistaken.","furigana":"わら|なん|おも|ひと|おおまちが"},{"japanese":"日本語なんて簡単だと思ったら大間違いだよ。","romaji":"Nihongo nante kantan da to omottara oomachigai da yo.","german":"If you think Japanese is easy, you're badly mistaken.","furigana":"にほんご|かんたん|おも|おおまちが"},{"japanese":"計算で大間違いをして、全部やり直すことになった。","romaji":"Keisan de oomachigai o shite, zenbu yarinaosu koto ni natta.","german":"I made a big mistake in the calculation and had to redo everything.","furigana":"けいさん|おおまちが|ぜんぶ|なお"}],"notes":"大間違い is a \"gross error\" or \"serious mistake\"; 大 is read おお here, not だい. Very typical is the warning frame 〜と思ったら大間違いだ \"anyone who thinks that ... is gravely mistaken\", often also 〜と思っている人は大間違い. As a concrete mistake it takes する: 計算で大間違いをする. The neutral base word is 間違い; colloquially, for small mistakes, people also say ミス.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"郵便番号は何番ですか？","romaji":"Yūbin bangō wa nanban desu ka?","german":"What is your postal code?","furigana":"ゆうびんばんごう|なんばん"},{"japanese":"住所の前に郵便番号を書いてください。","romaji":"Juusho no mae ni yuubin bangou o kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write the postal code before the address.","furigana":"じゅうしょ|まえ|ゆうびんばんごう|か"},{"japanese":"郵便番号を入力すると、住所が自動で出てきます。","romaji":"Yuubin bangou o nyuuryoku suru to, juusho ga jidou de dete kimasu.","german":"When you enter the postal code, the address appears automatically.","furigana":"ゆうびんばんごう|にゅうりょく|じゅうしょ|じどう|で"}],"notes":"郵便番号 is the \"postal code\"; in Japan it has seven digits in the format 123-4567 and is written before the address with the symbol 〒. Typical are 郵便番号を書く / 入力する / 調べる. On forms, often only 〒 appears as the field label; you ask for the number with 郵便番号は何番ですか or, more politely, 郵便番号を教えていただけますか.","levelNote":"Compound: 郵便 (N3) + 番号 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どうやって私の小さな郵便箱にこの本が入ったんだろう。","romaji":"Dō yatte watashi no chiisana yūbinbako ni kono hon ga haitta n darō.","german":"How did this book get into my little mailbox?","furigana":"わたし|ちい|ゆうびんばこ|ほん|はい"},{"japanese":"旅行中に、郵便箱がチラシでいっぱいになっていた。","romaji":"Ryokouchuu ni, yuubinbako ga chirashi de ippai ni natte ita.","german":"While I was traveling, the mailbox filled up with flyers.","furigana":"りょこうちゅう|ゆうびんばこ"},{"japanese":"郵便箱の鍵をなくしてしまいました。","romaji":"Yuubinbako no kagi o nakushite shimaimashita.","german":"I lost the key to my mailbox.","furigana":"ゆうびんばこ|かぎ"}],"notes":"郵便箱 is a \"mailbox\", especially one's own box for incoming mail. In everyday Japanese, however, people usually say 郵便受け or simply ポスト; the red public mailbox for outgoing mail is almost always called ポスト. Typical are 郵便箱に入れる, 郵便箱をのぞく and 郵便箱がいっぱいになる.","levelNote":"Compound: 郵便 (N3) + 箱 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コウイチのスピーチ、録音した？","romaji":"Kouichi no supiichi, rokuon shita?","german":"Did you record Koichi's speech?","furigana":"ろくおん"},{"japanese":"会議の内容は、スマホで録音しておきました。","romaji":"Kaigi no naiyou wa, sumaho de rokuon shite okimashita.","german":"I recorded the content of the meeting on my smartphone.","furigana":"かいぎ|ないよう|ろくおん"},{"japanese":"このラジオ番組は録音ではなく、生放送です。","romaji":"Kono rajio bangumi wa rokuon de wa naku, nama housou desu.","german":"This radio program is not recorded; it is live.","furigana":"ばんぐみ|ろくおん|なまほうそう"}],"notes":"録音 is a \"sound recording\"; as a transitive する-verb: 〜を録音する \"to record\". For picture and video recordings, people say 録画 instead. Typical combinations are 録音を聞く / 再生する \"to listen to / play back the recording\" and 留守番電話に録音する \"to leave a message on the answering machine\". As the opposite of a live broadcast, 録音 also stands for \"pre-recorded\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"軍隊ごっこをしているの？面白そう。私も入れてよ。","romaji":"Guntaigokko o shite iru no? Omoshirosō. Watashi mo irete yo.","german":"Are you playing army? Sounds fun. Let me join too.","furigana":"ぐんたい|おもしろ|わたし|い"},{"japanese":"祖父は若いころ軍隊にいたそうです。","romaji":"Sofu wa wakai koro guntai ni ita sō desu.","german":"I hear my grandfather was in the army when he was young.","furigana":"そふ|わか|ぐんたい"},{"japanese":"あの部活は軍隊みたいに厳しい。","romaji":"Ano bukatsu wa guntai mitai ni kibishii.","german":"That club is as strict as the military.","furigana":"ぶかつ|ぐんたい|きび"}],"notes":"軍隊 refers to the army or armed forces as an organization and to the troops: 軍隊に入る \"to join the military\", 軍隊生活. Shorter, and in compounds, 軍 is used (陸軍, 海軍); an individual soldier is 兵士. Japan's own armed forces are officially called 自衛隊, not 軍隊. Figuratively, 軍隊みたい describes extremely strict drill.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これは兵隊アリですね。","romaji":"Kore wa heitai ari desu ne.","german":"This is a soldier ant, isn't it?","furigana":"へいたい"},{"japanese":"祖父は若いころ、兵隊として戦争に行きました。","romaji":"Sofu wa wakai koro, heitai to shite sensou ni ikimashita.","german":"When my grandfather was young, he went to war as a soldier.","furigana":"そふ|わか|へいたい|せんそう|い"},{"japanese":"子どもたちがおもちゃの兵隊で遊んでいる。","romaji":"Kodomotachi ga omocha no heitai de asonde iru.","german":"The children are playing with toy soldiers.","furigana":"こ|へいたい|あそ"}],"notes":"兵隊 means \"soldiers, troops\", especially ordinary soldiers; it sounds somewhat old-fashioned and colloquial. In news and formal texts people prefer 兵士, and for military personnel in general (including officers) 軍人. Children say 兵隊さん, and it appears in comparisons such as 兵隊アリ \"soldier ant\" or おもちゃの兵隊 \"toy soldiers\". Colloquially it can also mean a company's \"lowly employee, foot soldier\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ついに音楽隊への入隊が決まりました。","romaji":"Tsuini ongakutai e no nyūtai ga kimarimashita.","german":"It's finally been decided that I'm joining the band.","furigana":"おんがくたい|にゅうたい|き"},{"japanese":"兄は十八歳で自衛隊に入隊しました。","romaji":"Ani wa juuhassai de Jieitai ni nyuutai shimashita.","german":"My older brother joined the Self-Defense Forces at eighteen.","furigana":"あに|じゅうはっさい|じえいたい|にゅうたい"},{"japanese":"入隊したばかりの新人は、毎朝早くから訓練を受ける。","romaji":"Nyuutai shita bakari no shinjin wa, maiasa hayaku kara kunren o ukeru.","german":"Newly enlisted recruits undergo training from early every morning.","furigana":"にゅうたい|しんじん|まいあさはや|くんれん|う"}],"notes":"入隊 is \"joining\" a unit called 隊: the army, the Self-Defense Forces (自衛隊), but also a music corps (音楽隊) or fire brigade (消防隊). As an intransitive する-verb, the destination takes に: 自衛隊に入隊する; as a noun it takes への. The opposite is 除隊 or 退隊. For a company, school or club, people say 入社, 入学 and 入会 instead.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"週末に買った靴が合わなくて、かかとが痛むんです。","romaji":"Shūmatsu ni katta kutsu ga awanakute, kakato ga itamu n desu.","german":"The shoes I bought at the weekend don't fit, and my heels hurt.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|か|くつ|あ|いた"},{"japanese":"雨の日は、昔けがをした膝が痛みます。","romaji":"Ame no hi wa, mukashi kega o shita hiza ga itamimasu.","german":"On rainy days, the knee I injured long ago aches.","furigana":"あめ|ひ|むかし|ひざ|いた"},{"japanese":"そのニュースを見て、胸が痛んだ。","romaji":"Sono nyuusu o mite, mune ga itanda.","german":"My heart ached when I saw that news.","furigana":"み|むね|いた"}],"notes":"痛む is intransitive: the body part takes が (頭が痛む, 傷が痛む). It sounds somewhat more descriptive and literary than the everyday adjective 痛い and emphasizes lasting pain (ずきずき痛む). Figuratively, 胸が痛む / 心が痛む means \"it pains me deeply\". The transitive partner is 痛める \"to injure oneself\" (腰を痛める); for \"to spoil, to be damaged\" it is usually written 傷む.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"睡眠薬が無いと眠れないんです。","romaji":"Suimin'yaku ga nai to nemurenai n desu.","german":"I can't sleep without sleeping pills.","furigana":"すいみんやく|な|ねむ"},{"japanese":"お医者さんに睡眠薬を出してもらいました。","romaji":"Oishasan ni suimin'yaku o dashite moraimashita.","german":"I had the doctor prescribe sleeping pills.","furigana":"いしゃ|すいみんやく|だ"},{"japanese":"睡眠薬を飲んだ後は、車を運転してはいけません。","romaji":"Suimin'yaku o nonda ato wa, kuruma o unten shite wa ikemasen.","german":"After taking sleeping pills, you must not drive a car.","furigana":"すいみんやく|の|あと|くるま|うんてん"}],"notes":"睡眠薬 are \"sleeping aids, sleeping pills\"; 薬 is read やく in this compound. Like all medicines, you \"take\" them with 飲む: 睡眠薬を飲む. Doctors prescribe them (睡眠薬を出す / 処方する). Somewhat more colloquial is 眠り薬; in advertising and among doctors you also hear 睡眠導入剤 for mild agents.","levelNote":"Compound: 睡眠 (N3) + 薬 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ダイエットしてるって知ってるのに松本さんはいつもケーキ屋さんに行こうと誘惑してくる。","romaji":"Daietto shiteru tte shitteru noni Matsumotosan wa itsumo keekiyasan ni ikou to yuuwaku shite kuru.","german":"Even though she knows I'm on a diet, Ms. Matsumoto always tempts me to go to the cake shop.","furigana":"し|まつもと|や|い|ゆうわく"},{"japanese":"甘い言葉で人を誘惑する詐欺に気をつけてください。","romaji":"Amai kotoba de hito o yuuwaku suru sagi ni ki o tsukete kudasai.","german":"Beware of scams that lure people with sweet words.","furigana":"あま|ことば|ひと|ゆうわく|さぎ|き"},{"japanese":"試験の前なのに、ゲームの誘惑に負けてしまった。","romaji":"Shiken no mae na noni, geemu no yuuwaku ni makete shimatta.","german":"Even though it was right before the exam, I gave in to the temptation of games.","furigana":"しけん|まえ|ゆうわく|ま"}],"notes":"誘惑する is transitive: one \"seduces\" or \"tempts\" someone (人を誘惑する), with bad intentions or also jokingly, as with sweets. The noun 誘惑 is very common in phrases such as 誘惑に負ける \"to succumb to temptation\", 誘惑に勝つ \"to resist temptation\" and 誘惑が多い. The neutral 誘う only means \"to invite, to ask\"; 誘惑 emphasizes the temptation to do something one should not actually do.","levelNote":"Derived from 誘惑 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"我々の力だけでは、どうにもならないんです。","romaji":"Wareware no chikara dake de wa, dō ni mo naranai n desu.","german":"We can't do anything about it with our strength alone.","furigana":"われわれ|ちから"},{"japanese":"我々は、この問題についてもう一度話し合う必要がある。","romaji":"Wareware wa, kono mondai ni tsuite mou ichido hanashiau hitsuyou ga aru.","german":"We need to discuss this problem once more.","furigana":"われわれ|もんだい|いちどはな|あ|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"我々人間は、もっと自然を大切にしなければならない。","romaji":"Wareware ningen wa, motto shizen o taisetsu ni shinakereba naranai.","german":"We humans must take better care of nature.","furigana":"われわれにんげん|しぜん|たいせつ"}],"notes":"我々 is a formal pronoun for \"we\". You hear it in speeches, lectures, company and newspaper texts, and in rather masculine, elevated language; in daily life people say 私たち, casually 僕ら or 俺たち. It often stands before a group designation: 我々人間 \"we humans\", 我々日本人. The mark 々 repeats the preceding kanji.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"健康に直接的な影響は無いと見られています。","romaji":"Kenkou ni chokusetsuteki na eikyou wa nai to mirarete imasu.","german":"It is thought that there is no direct effect on health.","furigana":"けんこう|ちょくせつてき|えいきょう|な|み"},{"japanese":"彼女はいつも直接的な言い方をするので、誤解されやすい。","romaji":"Kanojo wa itsumo chokusetsuteki na iikata o suru node, gokai sareyasui.","german":"She always speaks in a direct way, so she is easily misunderstood.","furigana":"かのじょ|ちょくせつてき|い|かた|ごかい"},{"japanese":"直接的には関係ないが、間接的には影響がある。","romaji":"Chokusetsuteki ni wa kankei nai ga, kansetsuteki ni wa eikyou ga aru.","german":"It is not directly related, but it has an indirect influence.","furigana":"ちょくせつてき|かんけい|かんせつてき|えいきょう"}],"notes":"直接的 is a な-adjective meaning \"direct, immediate\", mostly with abstract things: 直接的な原因, 直接的な影響, 直接的な言い方 \"a direct, blunt way of speaking\". The simple 直接 is a noun or adverb for concrete actions (直接会う, 直接話す). The opposite is 間接的 \"indirect\", often in contrast: 直接的には…、間接的には….","levelNote":"Compound: 直接 (N4) + 的 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"フタがちゃんと閉まっていなかったみたいで、横からソースが垂れている。","romaji":"Futa ga chanto shimatte inakatta mitai de, yoko kara sōsu ga tarete iru.","german":"The lid doesn't seem to be closed properly, and sauce is dripping from the side.","furigana":"し|よこ|た"},{"japanese":"この犬は耳が垂れていて、とてもかわいい。","romaji":"Kono inu wa mimi ga tarete ite, totemo kawaii.","german":"This dog has droopy ears and is very cute.","furigana":"いぬ|みみ|た"},{"japanese":"試合に負けた選手たちは、静かに頭を垂れた。","romaji":"Shiai ni maketa senshutachi wa, shizuka ni atama o tareta.","german":"The players who lost the game quietly bowed their heads.","furigana":"しあい|ま|せんしゅ|しず|あたま|た"}],"notes":"垂れる is mostly intransitive and has two basic meanings: \"to hang down\" (耳が垂れた犬, 枝が垂れる) and \"to drip, to run down\" (ソースが垂れる, 鼻水が垂れる). The transitive partner is 垂らす \"to let hang down, to let drip\" (ロープを垂らす). Elevated and transitive is 頭を垂れる \"to bow one's head\"; pejoratively one says 文句を垂れる \"to grumble\". The barbecue sauce タレ is derived from the same verb.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この沼地には、たくさんの野生動物が生息している。","romaji":"Kono numachi ni wa, takusan no yasei dōbutsu ga seisoku shite iru.","german":"Many wild animals live in this marshland.","furigana":"ぬまち|やせいどうぶつ|せいそく"},{"japanese":"沼地は足が沈むから、歩くときは気をつけて。","romaji":"Numachi wa ashi ga shizumu kara, aruku toki wa ki o tsukete.","german":"Your feet sink in marshland, so be careful when walking.","furigana":"ぬまち|あし|しず|ある|き"},{"japanese":"昔この辺りは沼地だったが、今は住宅地になっている。","romaji":"Mukashi kono atari wa numachi datta ga, ima wa juutakuchi ni natte iru.","german":"This area used to be a swamp, but now it's a residential district.","furigana":"むかし|あた|ぬまち|いま|じゅうたくち"}],"notes":"沼地 is a \"marshland\", that is, wet, muddy land with swamps (沼 \"swamp, pond\" + 地 \"land\"). The technical word for wetlands in environmental and nature conservation texts is 湿地; 池 is a small pond, 湖 a lake. Typical contexts are nature, animals and local history (昔は沼地だった).","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どうにかして敵の進撃をはばんでくれ！","romaji":"Dōnika shite teki no shingeki o habande kure!","german":"Somehow stop the enemy's advance!","furigana":"てき|しんげき"},{"japanese":"今年のチームは快進撃を続けている。","romaji":"Kotoshi no chiimu wa kaishingeki o tsuzukete iru.","german":"This year's team continues its triumphant run.","furigana":"ことし|かいしんげき|つづ"},{"japanese":"「進撃の巨人」は、世界中で人気のある漫画です。","romaji":"\"Shingeki no Kyojin\" wa, sekaijuu de ninki no aru manga desu.","german":"\"Attack on Titan\" is a manga popular all over the world.","furigana":"しんげき|きょじん|せかいじゅう|にんき|まんが"}],"notes":"進撃 is the military \"advance, thrust\" against the enemy; as an intransitive する-verb: 敵地へ進撃する. Typical are 敵の進撃を止める / 阻む. In sports and news, 快進撃 \"winning streak, triumphant run\" is very common. The more general 攻撃 means \"attack\", the neutral 前進 \"forward movement\"; the word is also known from the manga title 進撃の巨人.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今、浜辺でインスタライブしてまーす。","romaji":"Ima, hamabe de insutaraibu shite māsu.","german":"I'm doing an Instagram live on the beach right now.","furigana":"いま|はまべ"},{"japanese":"夕方、犬と一緒に浜辺を散歩しました。","romaji":"Yūgata, inu to issho ni hamabe o sanpo shimashita.","german":"In the evening, I took a walk on the beach with my dog.","furigana":"ゆうがた|いぬ|いっしょ|はまべ|さんぽ"},{"japanese":"浜辺にはきれいな貝殻がたくさん落ちていた。","romaji":"Hamabe ni wa kirei na kaigara ga takusan ochite ita.","german":"Many pretty shells were lying on the beach.","furigana":"はまべ|かいがら|お"}],"notes":"浜辺 is the flat, mostly sandy shore by the sea and sounds somewhat vivid and narrative: 浜辺を歩く, 浜辺で遊ぶ. ビーチ is rather used for swimming beaches and leisure, 砂浜 emphasizes the sand, 海岸 is the coast in general, also rocky. For river banks one does not say 浜辺 but 川辺 or 岸.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"何故なら、明日がカードの締め日だからです。","romaji":"Naze nara, ashita ga kādo no shimebi da kara desu.","german":"The reason is that tomorrow is the card's closing date.","furigana":"なぜ|あした|し|び"},{"japanese":"私は猫が好きです。なぜなら、自由に生きているように見えるからです。","romaji":"Watashi wa neko ga suki desu. Naze nara, jiyū ni ikite iru yō ni mieru kara desu.","german":"I like cats. That's because they seem to live freely.","furigana":"わたし|ねこ|す|じゆう|い|み"},{"japanese":"この計画には反対です。なぜなら、費用がかかりすぎるからです。","romaji":"Kono keikaku ni wa hantai desu. Naze nara, hiyō ga kakarisugiru kara desu.","german":"I'm against this plan. The reason is that it costs too much.","furigana":"けいかく|はんたい|ひよう"}],"notes":"なぜなら (mostly in kana) introduces, after a statement, a new sentence giving the reason and is almost always closed with 〜からだ / 〜からです: 〜。なぜなら、〜からです \"..., because ...\". It sounds somewhat formal and argumentative, suited to essays, speeches and discussions. In casual conversation people simply add 〜から or say なんでかというと.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ちょっと車を移動してくるよ。","romaji":"Chotto kuruma o idō shite kuru yo.","german":"I'll go move the car for a moment.","furigana":"くるま|いどう"},{"japanese":"東京から大阪までは、新幹線で移動します。","romaji":"Toukyou kara Oosaka made wa, shinkansen de idou shimasu.","german":"From Tokyo to Osaka, we travel by Shinkansen.","furigana":"とうきょう|おおさか|しんかんせん|いどう"},{"japanese":"このファイルを別のフォルダに移動してください。","romaji":"Kono fairu o betsu no foruda ni idou shite kudasai.","german":"Please move this file to a different folder.","furigana":"べつ|いどう"}],"notes":"移動 is the \"moving, relocating, traveling\" from one place to another and can be used transitively (車を移動する \"to move the car\") as well as intransitively (会場へ移動する \"to move to the venue\"). Common are 移動中 \"on the move\" and 移動時間 \"travel time\"; on a computer it means \"to move\". Do not confuse it with the homophone 異動 \"transfer (at work)\"; moving house is 引っ越し.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"全員が揃うの五年ぶりだね。","romaji":"Zen'in ga sorou no gonenburi da ne.","german":"It's the first time in five years that everyone is together.","furigana":"ぜんいん|そろ|ごねん"},{"japanese":"必要な書類が全部揃ったら、窓口に出してください。","romaji":"Hitsuyou na shorui ga zenbu sorottara, madoguchi ni dashite kudasai.","german":"Once all the necessary documents are ready, please submit them at the counter.","furigana":"ひつよう|しょるい|ぜんぶそろ|まどぐち|だ"},{"japanese":"このスーパーは、野菜の種類がよく揃っている。","romaji":"Kono suupaa wa, yasai no shurui ga yoku sorotte iru.","german":"This supermarket has a good selection of vegetables.","furigana":"やさい|しゅるい|そろ"}],"notes":"揃う is intransitive and means \"to be complete / all present\" (全員が揃う, 書類が揃う), \"to be well stocked\" (品物が揃っている) or \"to be even, in unison\" (足並みが揃う). The transitive partner is 揃える \"to complete, to align\" (靴を揃える). The て-form 揃って means \"all together\" (家族揃って). It is often written in kana そろう.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ハロウィンパーティーは夜更けまで続いた。","romaji":"Harowin pātī wa yofuke made tsuzuita.","german":"The Halloween party lasted until late at night.","furigana":"よふ|つづ"},{"japanese":"夜更けに電話が鳴って、びっくりした。","romaji":"Yofuke ni denwa ga natte, bikkuri shita.","german":"The phone rang late at night, and I was startled.","furigana":"よふ|でんわ|な"},{"japanese":"夜更けの町は静かで、人も車もほとんど通っていなかった。","romaji":"Yofuke no machi wa shizuka de, hito mo kuruma mo hotondo tōtte inakatta.","german":"The town late at night was quiet, with almost no people or cars passing.","furigana":"よふ|まち|しず|ひと|くるま|とお"}],"notes":"夜更け is \"late, advanced night\", for example after midnight, and sounds somewhat narrative and literary: 夜更けまで \"until deep into the night\", 夜更けに. It comes from 夜が更ける \"the night wears on\". Not to be confused with 夜更かし (する) \"to stay up late\". More matter-of-fact are 夜中 and 深夜.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その条約は明日で効力を失う。","romaji":"Sono jōyaku wa ashita de kōryoku o ushinau.","german":"That treaty will expire tomorrow.","furigana":"じょうやく|あした|こうりょく|うしな"},{"japanese":"この契約書は、サインがなければ効力がありません。","romaji":"Kono keiyakusho wa, sain ga nakereba kouryoku ga arimasen.","german":"This contract is not valid without a signature.","furigana":"けいやくしょ|こうりょく"},{"japanese":"この虫よけスプレーの効力は、約五時間続きます。","romaji":"Kono mushiyoke supuree no kouryoku wa, yaku go-jikan tsuzukimasu.","german":"The effect of this insect repellent spray lasts about five hours.","furigana":"むし|こうりょく|やくごじかんつづ"}],"notes":"効力 is \"effectiveness\" in the sense of legal validity (契約・法律の効力) or the potency of a remedy. Typical are 効力がある / を持つ \"to be valid\", 効力を失う \"to become invalid\" and 効力が切れる \"the effect wears off\". For the result of a measure, people more often say 効果; for medicines, 効き目 is more usual in everyday speech. The word sounds matter-of-fact and formal.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これは国家再建をかけた一大プロジェクトです。","romaji":"Kore wa kokka saiken o kaketa ichidai purojekuto desu.","german":"This is a major project aimed at rebuilding the nation.","furigana":"こっかさいけん|いちだい"},{"japanese":"地震で倒れたお寺が、十年かけて再建された。","romaji":"Jishin de taoreta otera ga, juunen kakete saiken sareta.","german":"The temple that collapsed in the earthquake was rebuilt over ten years.","furigana":"じしん|たお|てら|じゅうねん|さいけん"},{"japanese":"新しい社長は、会社の再建を任された。","romaji":"Atarashii shachou wa, kaisha no saiken o makasareta.","german":"The new president was entrusted with rebuilding the company.","furigana":"あたら|しゃちょう|かいしゃ|さいけん|まか"}],"notes":"再建 is the \"reconstruction\" of buildings (寺を再建する), but equally the \"restructuring\" of organizations and finances: 経営再建, 財政再建, 会社の再建. As a transitive する-verb: 〜を再建する, often in the passive 再建される. For the reconstruction of a whole region after a disaster, one rather says 復興; colloquially, \"to rebuild\" is also 建て直す. Note: the identically read 債権 means \"claim (creditor's right)\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どうして日当で給料を払う仕事に就きたいの？","romaji":"Doushite nittou de kyuuryou o harau shigoto ni tsukitai no?","german":"Why do you want a job that pays a daily wage?","furigana":"にっとう|きゅうりょう|はら|しごと|つ"},{"japanese":"四月から、田中さんが課長の職に就きます。","romaji":"Shigatsu kara, Tanakasan ga kachou no shoku ni tsukimasu.","german":"From April, Mr. Tanaka will take the post of section chief.","furigana":"しがつ|たなか|かちょう|しょく|つ"},{"japanese":"祖父は毎晩九時には床に就く。","romaji":"Sofu wa maiban kuji ni wa toko ni tsuku.","german":"My grandfather goes to bed at nine every night.","furigana":"そふ|まいばんくじ|とこ|つ"}],"notes":"就く is intransitive and takes に: you \"take up\" a job or position (仕事に就く, 職に就く, 社長の地位に就く). Set phrases are 床に就く \"to go to bed\" and 帰路に就く \"to set off for home\". With a company as the destination you don't say 会社に就く but 会社に入る or 就職する; the noun for taking office is 就任. It is also often simply written つく; do not confuse it with 付く \"to stick\" and 着く \"to arrive\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大丈夫。日はまた昇るさ。","romaji":"Daijōbu. Hi wa mata noboru sa.","german":"It's okay. The sun will rise again.","furigana":"だいじょうぶ|ひ|のぼ"},{"japanese":"たき火の煙が、空へゆっくり昇っていく。","romaji":"Takibi no kemuri ga, sora e yukkuri nobotte iku.","german":"The smoke of the bonfire slowly rises into the sky.","furigana":"び|けむり|そら|のぼ"},{"japanese":"彼は三十歳で部長の地位に昇った。","romaji":"Kare wa sanjussai de buchou no chii ni nobotta.","german":"He rose to the position of department head at thirty.","furigana":"かれ|さんじゅっさい|ぶちょう|ちい|のぼ"}],"notes":"昇る is intransitive and is used for things that rise on their own: 日が昇る \"the sun rises\", 煙が昇る, and for advancement in a career (地位に昇る). For stairs, slopes or numbers use 上る, and for climbing a mountain with effort 登る. The counterpart for the course of the sun is 日が沈む; figuratively 天にも昇る気持ち means \"to be overjoyed\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"本気で腹立つんだけど。練習のジャマだわ！","romaji":"Honki de haradatsu n da kedo. Renshū no jama da wa!","german":"I'm seriously furious. You're getting in the way of my practice!","furigana":"ほんき|はらだ|れんしゅう"},{"japanese":"あの店員の態度、ちょっと腹立たない？","romaji":"Ano ten'in no taido, chotto haradatanai?","german":"Doesn't that clerk's attitude annoy you a bit?","furigana":"てんいん|たいど|はらだ"},{"japanese":"腹立つことがあっても、すぐに怒らないようにしている。","romaji":"Haradatsu koto ga atte mo, sugu ni okoranai you ni shite iru.","german":"Even when something makes me angry, I try not to get angry right away.","furigana":"はらだ|おこ"}],"notes":"腹立つ is the colloquial short form of 腹が立つ \"to get angry, to become furious\"; in written or polite language the form with が is used. It describes inner anger, whereas 怒る expresses more the visible scolding. The cause is marked with に or stands as the subject: 彼の態度に腹が立つ, あの態度、腹立つ. The related adjective is 腹立たしい \"irritating\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大名はその下級武士に腹切りを命じた。","romaji":"Daimyō wa sono kakyū bushi ni harakiri o meijita.","german":"The daimyo ordered that low-ranking samurai to commit harakiri.","furigana":"だいみょう|かきゅうぶし|はらき|めい"},{"japanese":"時代劇の腹切りの場面は、少し怖かった。","romaji":"Jidaigeki no harakiri no bamen wa, sukoshi kowakatta.","german":"The harakiri scene in the period drama was a little scary.","furigana":"じだいげき|はらき|ばめん|すこ|こわ"},{"japanese":"外国では「腹切り」という言葉が有名だが、日本では「切腹」と言うほうが多い。","romaji":"Gaikoku de wa \"harakiri\" to iu kotoba ga yuumei da ga, Nihon de wa \"seppuku\" to iu hou ga ooi.","german":"The word \"harakiri\" is famous abroad, but in Japan people more often say \"seppuku\".","furigana":"がいこく|はらき|ことば|ゆうめい|にほん|せっぷく|い|おお"}],"notes":"腹切り is the ritual suicide of samurai by cutting open the belly (腹 \"belly\" + 切り \"cutting\"). Abroad it is known as \"harakiri\", but in Japanese 腹切り sounds rather colloquial; in history books and formal language people say 切腹. Typical are 腹切りを命じる and 腹切りをする.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その主婦は、株で三万円もうけた。","romaji":"Sono shufu wa, kabu de san man'en mouketa.","german":"The housewife made 30,000 yen on the stock market.","furigana":"しゅふ|かぶ|さんまんえん"},{"japanese":"母は結婚してから、ずっと専業主婦です。","romaji":"Haha wa kekkon shite kara, zutto sengyou shufu desu.","german":"My mother has been a full-time housewife ever since she got married.","furigana":"はは|けっこん|せんぎょうしゅふ"},{"japanese":"このスーパーは、昼間は主婦のお客さんが多い。","romaji":"Kono suupaa wa, hiruma wa shufu no okyakusan ga ooi.","german":"In this supermarket, many of the daytime customers are housewives.","furigana":"ひるま|しゅふ|きゃく|おお"}],"notes":"主婦 is the \"housewife\" who runs the household. A distinction is made between 専業主婦 \"full-time housewife\" and 兼業主婦 \"working housewife\"; a househusband is the identically pronounced 主夫. The word is neutral and often appears in advertising and the media as a target group (主婦向け). When talking about someone else's wife, however, you use 奥さん.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ついに、最終催告を送り付けられてしまいました。","romaji":"Tsuini, saishuu saikoku o okuritsukerarete shimaimashita.","german":"I finally received a final notice.","furigana":"さいしゅうさいこく|おく|つ"},{"japanese":"家賃を払わないでいたら、大家さんから支払いを催告された。","romaji":"Yachin o harawanaide itara, ooyasan kara shiharai o saikoku sareta.","german":"When I didn't pay my rent, the landlord formally demanded payment.","furigana":"やちん|はら|おおや|しはら|さいこく"},{"japanese":"期限までに返事がない場合は、書面で催告します。","romaji":"Kigen made ni henji ga nai baai wa, shomen de saikoku shimasu.","german":"If there is no reply by the deadline, we will send a formal demand in writing.","furigana":"きげん|へんじ|ばあい|しょめん|さいこく"}],"notes":"催告 is a legal technical term for a formal \"demand\" to fulfill an obligation, such as a payment (支払いの催告). As a transitive する-verb: 〜を催告する; the letter is called 催告書, the final demand 最終催告. In everyday speech, for payment reminders people more often say 督促 or simply 請求; 催告 is mainly encountered in contracts, official letters and legal texts.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"芸能人主催のゴルフ大会ってどんなのなんだろう？","romaji":"Geinōjin shusai no gorufu taikai tte donna no na n darō?","german":"I wonder what a golf tournament hosted by celebrities is like.","furigana":"げいのうじんしゅさい|たいかい"},{"japanese":"この花火大会は、毎年市が主催しています。","romaji":"Kono hanabi taikai wa, maitoshi shi ga shusai shite imasu.","german":"This fireworks festival is hosted by the city every year.","furigana":"はなびたいかい|まいとしし|しゅさい"},{"japanese":"最初に、主催者の方からごあいさつがあります。","romaji":"Saisho ni, shusaisha no kata kara goaisatsu ga arimasu.","german":"First, there will be a greeting from the organizer.","furigana":"さいしょ|しゅさいしゃ|かた"}],"notes":"主催 means \"hosting, organizing\" - the person or organization itself is called 主催者 \"organizer\". Typical is 〜主催の + event (市主催の祭り) as well as 〜が主催する \"to host, to organize\". Related are 共催 \"joint hosting\", 後援 \"patronage\" and 協賛 \"sponsorship\". Not to be confused with the identically read 主宰 \"to head (a group, magazine)\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"全壊したビルの中から何か出てきました。","romaji":"Zenkai shita biru no naka kara nani ka dete kimashita.","german":"Something came out of the completely collapsed building.","furigana":"ぜんかい|なか|なに|で"},{"japanese":"台風で、海の近くの家が全壊した。","romaji":"Taifuu de, umi no chikaku no ie ga zenkai shita.","german":"A house near the sea was completely destroyed by the typhoon.","furigana":"たいふう|うみ|ちか|いえ|ぜんかい"},{"japanese":"事故で車は全壊したが、運転手は無事だった。","romaji":"Jiko de kuruma wa zenkai shita ga, untenshu wa buji datta.","german":"The car was totaled in the accident, but the driver was unharmed.","furigana":"じこ|くるま|ぜんかい|うんてんしゅ|ぶじ"}],"notes":"全壊 means \"complete destruction\" of buildings or vehicles, for example by earthquake, typhoon or accident. As an intransitive する-verb: 家が全壊する (not with を). In damage reports it appears alongside 半壊 \"partially destroyed\" and 一部損壊 \"damaged\"; 倒壊 stresses collapsing. The identically read 全快 means \"complete recovery\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今、『テロでロンドンブリッジ倒壊』のテロップが流れてきたよ！本当かな？","romaji":"Ima, tero de Rondon burijji tōkai no teroppu ga nagarete kita yo! Hontō ka na?","german":"Just now a news ticker said \"London Bridge collapses in terror attack\"! Is that true?","furigana":"いま|とうかい|なが|ほんとう"},{"japanese":"大きな地震で、多くの古い家が倒壊した。","romaji":"Ookina jishin de, ooku no furui ie ga toukai shita.","german":"Many old houses collapsed in the big earthquake.","furigana":"おお|じしん|おお|ふる|いえ|とうかい"},{"japanese":"このビルは倒壊のおそれがあるので、近づかないでください。","romaji":"Kono biru wa toukai no osore ga aru node, chikazukanaide kudasai.","german":"This building is in danger of collapse, so please do not approach.","furigana":"とうかい|ちか"}],"notes":"倒壊 refers to the \"collapse\" of buildings, bridges or walls, mostly due to earthquakes or disasters. As an intransitive する-verb: 家が倒壊する; also typical is 倒壊のおそれがある \"danger of collapse\". In everyday speech people say 崩れる or 倒れる; 崩壊 is also used abstractly (経済の崩壊), while 全壊 stresses complete destruction. The word is typical of the news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"クララはゼーゼマン家の一人娘です。","romaji":"Kurara wa Zeezemanke no hitorimusume desu.","german":"Clara is the only daughter of the Sesemann family.","furigana":"け|ひとりむすめ"},{"japanese":"一人娘が結婚して、家が急に静かになった。","romaji":"Hitorimusume ga kekkon shite, ie ga kyuu ni shizuka ni natta.","german":"After my only daughter got married, the house suddenly became quiet.","furigana":"ひとりむすめ|けっこん|いえ|きゅう|しず"},{"japanese":"彼女は一人娘なので、両親にとても大切に育てられた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa hitorimusume na node, ryoushin ni totemo taisetsu ni sodaterareta.","german":"She is an only daughter, so she was raised very carefully by her parents.","furigana":"かのじょ|ひとりむすめ|りょうしん|たいせつ|そだ"}],"notes":"一人娘 is the \"only daughter\" and usually means that the couple has only this one child at all. Correspondingly, the only son is 一人息子; \"only child\" in general is 一人っ子. The word often carries the undertone that the daughter is especially sheltered (一人娘を大切に育てる).","levelNote":"Compound: 一人 (N5) + 娘 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"禅宗では、心を無にして今に集中することを教えています。","romaji":"Zenshū de wa, kokoro o mu ni shite ima ni shūchū suru koto o oshiete imasu.","german":"Zen Buddhism teaches emptying the mind and focusing on the present.","furigana":"ぜんしゅう|こころ|む|いま|しゅうちゅう|おし"},{"japanese":"京都には、禅宗のお寺がたくさんあります。","romaji":"Kyouto ni wa, zenshuu no otera ga takusan arimasu.","german":"There are many Zen temples in Kyoto.","furigana":"きょうと|ぜんしゅう|てら"},{"japanese":"禅宗は、鎌倉時代に中国から日本に伝わった。","romaji":"Zenshuu wa, Kamakura jidai ni Chuugoku kara Nihon ni tsutawatta.","german":"Zen Buddhism was transmitted from China to Japan in the Kamakura period.","furigana":"ぜんしゅう|かまくらじだい|ちゅうごく|にほん|つた"}],"notes":"禅宗 refers to Zen Buddhism as a school (宗 \"school, sect\", cf. 浄土宗). In Japan the most important branches are 臨済宗 and 曹洞宗, which came from China in the Kamakura period. In everyday speech people often say just 禅, for example in 座禅 \"Zen meditation\" or 禅寺 \"Zen temple\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今度の宴会の幹事をすることになりました。","romaji":"Kondo no enkai no kanji o suru koto ni narimashita.","german":"I've been put in charge of organizing the next banquet.","furigana":"こんど|えんかい|かんじ"},{"japanese":"会社の宴会で、部長がカラオケを歌った。","romaji":"Kaisha no enkai de, buchou ga karaoke o utatta.","german":"At the company banquet, the department head sang karaoke.","furigana":"かいしゃ|えんかい|ぶちょう|うた"},{"japanese":"この旅館には、百人入れる宴会場があります。","romaji":"Kono ryokan ni wa, hyakunin haireru enkaijou ga arimasu.","german":"This inn has a banquet hall that holds a hundred people.","furigana":"りょかん|ひゃくにんはい|えんかいじょう"}],"notes":"宴会 is a convivial meal with a lot of drinking in a group, typically at a company, club or ryokan; 忘年会, 新年会 and 歓迎会 are types of 宴会. Typical are 宴会を開く, 宴会に出る and 宴会の幹事 \"organizer of the party\"; the hall is called 宴会場. More casual is 飲み会, Western-influenced and often standing is パーティー.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不健康なやり方でダイエットをするのはよした方がいい。","romaji":"Fukenkou na yarikata de daietto o suru no wa yoshita hou ga ii.","german":"You'd better stop dieting in an unhealthy way.","furigana":"ふけんこう|かた|ほう"},{"japanese":"毎日コンビニ弁当ばかりじゃ、不健康だよ。","romaji":"Mainichi konbini bentou bakari ja, fukenkou da yo.","german":"Eating only convenience store bento every day is unhealthy.","furigana":"まいにち|べんとう|ふけんこう"},{"japanese":"夜遅くまで起きている不健康な生活を続けている。","romaji":"Yoru osoku made okite iru fukenkou na seikatsu o tsuzukete iru.","german":"I keep up an unhealthy lifestyle of staying up until late at night.","furigana":"よるおそ|お|ふけんこう|せいかつ|つづ"}],"notes":"不健康 is a な-adjective or noun meaning \"unhealthy\": 不健康な生活, 不健康な食事. In everyday speech people often simply say 体に悪い. Do not confuse it with 不健全 \"morally questionable, unsound\" (不健全な関係). The prefix 不 negates, as in 不便 or 不安.","levelNote":"Compound: 不 (N3) + 健康 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の怒りは三分以上続かない。","romaji":"Watashi no ikari wa sanpun ijou tsuzukanai.","german":"My anger doesn't last more than three minutes.","furigana":"わたし|いか|さんぷんいじょうつづ"},{"japanese":"大臣の無責任な発言に、国民の怒りが爆発した。","romaji":"Daijin no musekinin na hatsugen ni, kokumin no ikari ga bakuhatsu shita.","german":"The minister's irresponsible remark made the public's anger explode.","furigana":"だいじん|むせきにん|はつげん|こくみん|いか|ばくはつ"},{"japanese":"怒りを抑えて、落ち着いて話そうとした。","romaji":"Ikari o osaete, ochitsuite hanasou to shita.","german":"I held back my anger and tried to speak calmly.","furigana":"いか|おさ|お|つ|はな"}],"notes":"怒り is the noun for 怒る and means \"rage, anger, indignation\". Typical combinations are 怒りを感じる, 怒りを抑える \"to suppress anger\", 怒りが爆発する \"anger erupts\" and 怒りを買う \"to incur someone's wrath\". It is read いかり, although the verb is usually おこる. It is somewhat more factual than 腹立ち, and often used in the news for public outrage (国民の怒り).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私も父も高所恐怖症です。","romaji":"Watashi mo chichi mo kōsho kyōfushō desu.","german":"Both my father and I are afraid of heights.","furigana":"わたし|ちち|こうしょきょうふしょう"},{"japanese":"閉所恐怖症なので、狭いエレベーターが苦手です。","romaji":"Heisho kyoufushou na node, semai erebeetaa ga nigate desu.","german":"I have claustrophobia, so I'm not good with small elevators.","furigana":"へいしょきょうふしょう|せま|にがて"},{"japanese":"彼は対人恐怖症で、人と会うのがとても怖いそうだ。","romaji":"Kare wa taijin kyoufushou de, hito to au no ga totemo kowai sou da.","german":"He has social phobia and is said to be very afraid of meeting people.","furigana":"かれ|たいじんきょうふしょう|ひと|あ|こわ"}],"notes":"恐怖症 is a \"phobia\" (恐怖 \"fear\" + 症 \"condition\") and mostly appears as a suffix: 高所恐怖症 \"fear of heights\", 閉所恐怖症 \"claustrophobia\", 対人恐怖症 \"social phobia\". Colloquially it is also used jokingly (月曜日恐怖症). For a mere dislike people rather say 〜が苦手; 恐怖 alone is general \"fear, dread\".","levelNote":"Compound: 恐怖 (N3) + 症 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このレストランの看板は大きくてよく目立ちます。","romaji":"Kono resutoran no kanban wa ōkikute yoku medachimasu.","german":"This restaurant's sign is big and stands out well.","furigana":"かんばん|おお|めだ"},{"japanese":"このラーメンが、うちの店の看板メニューです。","romaji":"Kono raamen ga, uchi no mise no kanban menyuu desu.","german":"This ramen is our shop's signature dish.","furigana":"みせ|かんばん"},{"japanese":"すみません、今日はもう看板なんです。","romaji":"Sumimasen, kyou wa mou kanban nan desu.","german":"Sorry, we're closed for today.","furigana":"きょう|かんばん"}],"notes":"看板 is first of all a \"sign, shop sign, billboard\" (看板を出す / 掲げる). Figuratively it means the \"showpiece\" of a business: 看板メニュー \"house specialty\", 看板娘 \"the shop's poster girl\", and reputation (看板に傷がつく). In restaurants and bars, もう看板です means \"we're closing now\". A traffic sign, by contrast, is called 標識.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本の食べ物は欧米でも人気があります。","romaji":"Nippon no tabemono wa Ōbei de mo ninki ga arimasu.","german":"Japanese food is popular in the West too.","furigana":"にっぽん|た|もの|おうべい|にんき"},{"japanese":"欧米では、家の中でも靴をはいたまま過ごす人が多い。","romaji":"Oubei de wa, ie no naka de mo kutsu o haita mama sugosu hito ga ooi.","german":"In the West, many people keep their shoes on even inside the house.","furigana":"おうべい|いえ|なか|くつ|す|ひと|おお"},{"japanese":"戦後、日本人の食生活は急速に欧米化した。","romaji":"Sengo, Nihonjin no shokuseikatsu wa kyuusoku ni oubeika shita.","german":"After the war, the Japanese diet rapidly became Westernized.","furigana":"せんご|にほんじん|しょくせいかつ|きゅうそく|おうべいか"}],"notes":"欧米 stands for \"Europe and America\" and thus for \"the West\" (欧 = Europe, 米 = America). Common are 欧米人 \"Westerners\", 欧米諸国 \"Western countries\" and 欧米化 \"Westernization\". The older, culturally colored 西洋 \"the Occident\" is often contrasted with 東洋 \"the Orient\"; 欧米 is the usual word in news and everyday speech.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"中欧の国だったら、チェコに行ってみたいです。","romaji":"Chū Ō no kuni dattara, Cheko ni itte mitai desu.","german":"Von den mitteleuropäischen Ländern würde ich gerne Tschechien besuchen.","furigana":"ちゅうおう|くに|い"},{"japanese":"プラハやウィーンは、中欧を代表する町だ。","romaji":"Puraha ya Wiin wa, Chuuou o daihyou suru machi da.","german":"Prag und Wien sind typische Städte Mitteleuropas.","furigana":"ちゅうおう|だいひょう|まち"},{"japanese":"今回の旅行では、中欧の三つの国を回ります。","romaji":"Konkai no ryokou de wa, Chuuou no mittsu no kuni o mawarimasu.","german":"Auf dieser Reise besuchen wir drei Länder in Mitteleuropa.","furigana":"こんかい|りょこう|ちゅうおう|みっ|くに|まわ"}],"notes":"中欧 bedeutet „Mitteleuropa“ (Länder wie Deutschland, Österreich, Polen, Tschechien). Es gehört zur Reihe 西欧 „Westeuropa“, 東欧 „Osteuropa“, 北欧 „Nordeuropa, Skandinavien“ und 南欧 „Südeuropa“. Achtung: Das gleich gelesene 中央 bedeutet „Mitte, zentral“; der Kontext entscheidet.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"水野さんは仕事で日欧を行き来することが多い。","romaji":"Mizunosan wa shigoto de Nichi Ō o ikiki suru koto ga ōi.","german":"Herr Mizuno pendelt beruflich oft zwischen Japan und Europa.","furigana":"みずの|しごと|にちおう|い|き|おお"},{"japanese":"日欧の経済関係は、ますます深まっている。","romaji":"Nichiou no keizai kankei wa, masumasu fukamatte iru.","german":"Die wirtschaftlichen Beziehungen zwischen Japan und Europa werden immer enger.","furigana":"にちおう|けいざいかんけい|ふか"},{"japanese":"来月、東京で日欧の学生の交流会が開かれる。","romaji":"Raigetsu, Toukyou de Nichiou no gakusei no kouryuukai ga hirakareru.","german":"Nächsten Monat findet in Tokio ein Austauschtreffen japanischer und europäischer Studierender statt.","furigana":"らいげつ|とうきょう|にちおう|がくせい|こうりゅうかい|ひら"}],"notes":"日欧 bedeutet „Japan und Europa“ bzw. „japanisch-europäisch“. Es folgt dem Muster, bei dem 日 für Japan steht: 日米 „Japan–USA“, 日中, 日韓, 日独 „Japan–Deutschland“. Meist steht es vor einem Nomen oder mit の: 日欧関係, 日欧の交流; typisch ist die Zeitungs- und Politiksprache.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"江戸時代は約260年間続きました。","romaji":"Edo jidai wa yaku 260nenkan tsuzukimashita.","german":"The Edo period lasted about 260 years.","furigana":"えどじだい|やく|ねんかんつづ"},{"japanese":"江戸は、今の東京の昔の名前です。","romaji":"Edo wa, ima no Toukyou no mukashi no namae desu.","german":"Edo is the old name of present-day Tokyo.","furigana":"えど|いま|とうきょう|むかし|なまえ"},{"japanese":"祖父は自分のことを「江戸っ子」だと言っている。","romaji":"Sofu wa jibun no koto o \"Edokko\" da to itte iru.","german":"My grandfather says he himself is a true \"Edokko\".","furigana":"そふ|じぶん|えど|こ|い"}],"notes":"江戸 is the old name of Tokyo, until the city was renamed 東京 in 1868. It is most often encountered in 江戸時代 \"Edo period\" (1603-1868) and 江戸幕府 \"Tokugawa shogunate\". A true Tokyoite whose family has lived there for generations proudly calls himself 江戸っ子; words like 江戸前 designate the traditional Tokyo style, for example with sushi.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"アイスクリームにミントの葉っぱを添えると、少しオシャレな感じになりますよ。","romaji":"Aisukurīmu ni minto no happa o soeru to, sukoshi oshare na kanji ni narimasu yo.","german":"If you garnish the ice cream with mint leaves, it looks a bit stylish.","furigana":"は|そ|すこ|かん"},{"japanese":"誕生日プレゼントに、短い手紙を添えて送った。","romaji":"Tanjoubi purezento ni, mijikai tegami o soete okutta.","german":"I sent a short letter along with the birthday present.","furigana":"たんじょうび|みじか|てがみ|そ|おく"},{"japanese":"彼女の歌が、パーティーに花を添えた。","romaji":"Kanojo no uta ga, paatii ni hana o soeta.","german":"Her singing added a touch of splendor to the party.","furigana":"かのじょ|うた|はな|そ"}],"notes":"添える is transitive: AにBを添える means to put B with A as an accompaniment or supplement - a garnish to food, a letter with a gift, a helping hand (手を添える). Figuratively, 花を添える means \"to add extra splendor\". Unlike 加える or 足す (\"to add, to mix in\"), with 添える the added thing remains something separate standing alongside. The intransitive partner is 添う \"to accompany, to snuggle up to\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"初めて会った時の宮崎さんの印象は、とても強烈でした。","romaji":"Hajimete atta toki no Miyazakisan no inshō wa, totemo kyōretsu deshita.","german":"My impression of Mr. Miyazaki when we first met was very strong.","furigana":"はじ|あ|とき|みやざき|いんしょう|きょうれつ"},{"japanese":"夏の強烈な日差しで、肌が真っ赤になった。","romaji":"Natsu no kyouretsu na hizashi de, hada ga makka ni natta.","german":"The intense summer sunlight turned my skin bright red.","furigana":"なつ|きょうれつ|ひざ|はだ|ま|か"},{"japanese":"このチーズは、強烈なにおいがする。","romaji":"Kono chiizu wa, kyouretsu na nioi ga suru.","german":"This cheese has a pungent smell.","furigana":"きょうれつ"}],"notes":"強烈 is a な-adjective for a very strong, penetrating effect on the senses or the impression: 強烈な日差し, 強烈なにおい, 強烈な印象, 強烈なキャラ \"quirky, striking personality\". The neutral 強い is weaker; 激しい emphasizes violent movement or emotions, 猛烈 rather wild force and speed. Possible both positively and negatively.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は猛烈なスピードで仕事を終わらせました。","romaji":"Kare wa mouretsu na supiido de shigoto o owarasemashita.","german":"He finished the work at a furious speed.","furigana":"かれ|もうれつ|しごと|お"},{"japanese":"猛烈な台風が、九州に近づいています。","romaji":"Mouretsu na taifuu ga, Kyuushuu ni chikazuite imasu.","german":"A violent typhoon is approaching Kyushu.","furigana":"もうれつ|たいふう|きゅうしゅう|ちか"},{"japanese":"試験の一週間前から、猛烈に勉強した。","romaji":"Shiken no isshuukan mae kara, mouretsu ni benkyou shita.","german":"From a week before the exam, I studied like mad.","furigana":"しけん|いっしゅうかんまえ|もうれつ|べんきょう"}],"notes":"猛烈 is a な-adjective for wild, impetuous strength or speed: 猛烈な台風, 猛烈な暑さ, 猛烈なスピード. Adverbially, 猛烈に means \"like crazy, with all one's might\" (猛烈に反対する, 猛烈に勉強する). 強烈 refers more to sensory impressions and the impression made, 激しい is the more general \"violent\". In weather reports, 猛烈な is even an official level for typhoons and rain.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"そのバンドの熱烈なファンが会場をいっぱいにしていた。","romaji":"Sono bando no netsuretsu na fan ga kaijō o ippai ni shite ita.","german":"Passionate fans of the band filled the venue.","furigana":"ねつれつ|かいじょう"},{"japanese":"選手たちは空港でファンから熱烈な歓迎を受けた。","romaji":"Senshutachi wa kūkō de fan kara netsuretsu na kangei o uketa.","german":"The players received an enthusiastic welcome from fans at the airport.","furigana":"せんしゅ|くうこう|ねつれつ|かんげい|う"},{"japanese":"彼は彼女に熱烈なラブレターを書いた。","romaji":"Kare wa kanojo ni netsuretsu na raburetā o kaita.","german":"He wrote her a passionate love letter.","furigana":"かれ|かのじょ|ねつれつ|か"}],"notes":"熱烈 is a な-adjective for glowing, passionate feelings: 熱烈なファン \"ardent fan\", 熱烈な歓迎 \"enthusiastic welcome\", 熱烈な恋, adverbially 熱烈に応援する. 熱心, by contrast, means \"eager, dedicated\" (熱心に勉強する) and describes diligence, not passion. 熱烈歓迎 is often seen as a set greeting on signs.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は猛勉強して、大学に合格しました。","romaji":"Kare wa moubenkyou shite, daigaku ni goukaku shimashita.","german":"He studied like mad and passed the university entrance exam.","furigana":"かれ|もうべんきょう|だいがく|ごうかく"},{"japanese":"今年の夏は、猛暑の日が続いている。","romaji":"Kotoshi no natsu wa, mousho no hi ga tsuzuite iru.","german":"This summer, days of intense heat continue.","furigana":"ことし|なつ|もうしょ|ひ|つづ"},{"japanese":"車が猛スピードで、目の前を走っていった。","romaji":"Kuruma ga mou supiido de, me no mae o hashitte itta.","german":"A car sped past right in front of me at breakneck speed.","furigana":"くるま|もう|め|まえ|はし"}],"notes":"猛〜 is a prefix meaning \"fierce, extreme, wild\" and intensifies the following noun: 猛勉強 \"cramming\", 猛練習 \"hard training\", 猛反対 \"fierce opposition\", 猛スピード \"breakneck speed\". Some formations are fixed words, such as 猛暑 \"extreme heat\" (in the weather report, 猛暑日 is a day of 35 °C or more). Many of them are used with する (猛勉強する, 猛反対する).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本の若者はすぐ単語やフレーズを略したがる。","romaji":"Nihon no wakamono wa sugu tango ya furēzu o ryakushitagaru.","german":"Young people in Japan are quick to shorten words and phrases.","furigana":"にほん|わかもの|たんご|りゃく"},{"japanese":"「国際連合」は略して「国連」と言います。","romaji":"Kokusai Rengō wa ryakushite Kokuren to iimasu.","german":"\"国際連合\" is abbreviated to \"国連\".","furigana":"こくさいれんごう|りゃく|こくれん|い"},{"japanese":"時間がないので、細かい説明は略します。","romaji":"Jikan ga nai node, komakai setsumei wa ryakushimasu.","german":"We're short on time, so I'll skip the detailed explanation.","furigana":"じかん|こま|せつめい|りゃく"}],"notes":"略す means \"to abbreviate\" and \"to omit\" and is transitive: 〜を略す. Very typical is the pattern AをBと略す (\"to abbreviate A as B\") and the form 略して \"in short, abbreviated\": 国際連合、略して国連. For leaving out parts (e.g. an explanation), 省略する or 省く is more common in everyday speech; 略す sounds somewhat more concise and literary. The related noun is 略 (\"abbreviation\"), cf. 略語.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本語の会話では、必要なとき以外は文法上の主語を省略するのがデフォルトです。","romaji":"Nihongo no kaiwa de wa, hitsuyō na toki igai wa bunpōjō no shugo o shōryaku suru no ga deforuto desu.","german":"In Japanese conversation, the default is to omit the grammatical subject unless it is needed.","furigana":"にほんご|かいわ|ひつよう|いがい|ぶんぽうじょう|しゅご|しょうりゃく"},{"japanese":"「パーソナルコンピューター」は省略して「パソコン」と言います。","romaji":"Pāsonaru konpyūtā wa shōryaku shite pasokon to iimasu.","german":"\"パーソナルコンピューター\" is abbreviated and called \"パソコン\".","furigana":"しょうりゃく|い"},{"japanese":"時間がないので、詳しい説明は省略します。","romaji":"Jikan ga nai node, kuwashii setsumei wa shōryaku shimasu.","german":"I don't have time, so I'll omit the detailed explanation.","furigana":"じかん|くわ|せつめい|しょうりゃく"}],"notes":"省略 is \"omission, abbreviation\" and, as a transitive する-verb, \"to leave out, to abbreviate\": 説明を省略する, 主語を省略する. Typical are 省略形 \"short form\" and 以下省略 \"rest omitted\". More colloquial is the verb 略す (to abbreviate words), and 省く means \"to leave out, to save\" (手間を省く).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"駅から私の家までの略図をかいて、メールでお送りします。","romaji":"Eki kara watashi no ie made no ryakuzu o kaite, mēru de o-okuri shimasu.","german":"I'll draw a rough map from the station to my house and send it by email.","furigana":"えき|わたし|いえ|りゃくず|おく"},{"japanese":"会場までの略図は、案内状の裏にのっています。","romaji":"Kaijō made no ryakuzu wa, annaijō no ura ni notte imasu.","german":"A rough map to the venue is on the back of the invitation.","furigana":"かいじょう|りゃくず|あんないじょう|うら"},{"japanese":"事故のときの状況を、略図で説明してください。","romaji":"Jiko no toki no jōkyō o, ryakuzu de setsumei shite kudasai.","german":"Please explain the situation at the time of the accident with a sketch.","furigana":"じこ|じょうきょう|りゃくず|せつめい"}],"notes":"略図 is a simplified, rough drawing, usually a route sketch or location map that one makes quickly by hand. Typical are 略図をかく (描く/書く) and 〜までの略図 \"a sketch to ...\". A proper, scale city map is 地図; an artistic sketch is more often called スケッチ or 下絵, a floor plan 見取り図. On invitations one often finds 会場略図 \"sketch map of the venue\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"会議の大略だけでも教えていただけませんか。","romaji":"Kaigi no tairyaku dake demo oshiete itadakemasen ka?","german":"Could you at least tell me the outline of the meeting?","furigana":"かいぎ|たいりゃく|おし"},{"japanese":"それでは、新しい計画の大略をご説明いたします。","romaji":"Sore de wa, atarashii keikaku no tairyaku o go-setsumei itashimasu.","german":"Now, I will explain the outline of the new plan.","furigana":"あたら|けいかく|たいりゃく|せつめい"},{"japanese":"事件の経過は、大略次のとおりです。","romaji":"Jiken no keika wa, tairyaku tsugi no tōri desu.","german":"The course of the incident is, in outline, as follows.","furigana":"じけん|けいか|たいりゃくつぎ"}],"notes":"大略 is a formal, literary word for \"outline, summary\": 計画の大略, 大略を説明する. As an adverb it means \"by and large, in essence\", for example in reports: 大略次のとおりである. In everyday speech people instead say 概要, あらまし or simply だいたい or ざっと; 大略 therefore fits better in meetings, reports and polite requests.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"特に痛みはありません。","romaji":"Toku ni itami wa arimasen.","german":"I don't have any particular pain.","furigana":"とく|いた"},{"japanese":"この薬を飲むと、痛みがすぐに取れますよ。","romaji":"Kono kusuri o nomu to, itami ga sugu ni toremasu yo.","german":"If you take this medicine, the pain will go away right away.","furigana":"くすり|の|いた|と"},{"japanese":"友だちを失った心の痛みは、なかなか消えなかった。","romaji":"Tomodachi o ushinatta kokoro no itami wa, nakanaka kienakatta.","german":"The pain in my heart from losing my friend didn't disappear easily.","furigana":"とも|うしな|こころ|いた|き"}],"notes":"痛み is the noun for 痛い and denotes physical as well as emotional \"pain\". Typical combinations: 痛みがある/ない, 痛みを感じる, 痛みが取れる (\"the pain goes away\"), 痛みに強い/弱い and 心の痛み. In daily life people usually just say 頭が痛い; 痛み sounds more factual and is common at the doctor's, for example. Note: the meaning \"damage, wear\" is usually written 傷み (cf. 傷む).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"頭痛がひどいので、痛み止めを飲みました。","romaji":"Zutsū ga hidoi node, itamidome o nomimashita.","german":"My headache is bad, so I took a painkiller.","furigana":"ずつう|いた|ど|の"},{"japanese":"歯医者で痛み止めをもらいました。","romaji":"Haisha de itamidome o moraimashita.","german":"I got a painkiller from the dentist.","furigana":"はいしゃ|いた|ど"},{"japanese":"痛み止めが効いてきて、少し楽になった。","romaji":"Itamidome ga kiite kite, sukoshi raku ni natta.","german":"The painkiller has started to work, and I feel a little better.","furigana":"いた|ど|き|すこ|らく"}],"notes":"痛み止め (from 痛む \"to ache\" and 止める \"to stop\") is the everyday word for a painkiller; in technical language and on package inserts it is 鎮痛剤. Tablets are \"taken\" in Japanese with 飲む: 痛み止めを飲む. Also common are 痛み止めが効く \"the painkiller works\" and 痛み止めの注射 \"painkilling injection\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"最近ストレスでよく眠れないんです。","romaji":"Saikin sutoresu de yoku nemurenai n desu.","german":"Lately I can't sleep well because of stress.","furigana":"さいきん|ねむ"},{"japanese":"赤ちゃんがぐっすり眠っています。","romaji":"Akachan ga gussuri nemutte imasu.","german":"The baby is sound asleep.","furigana":"あか|ねむ"},{"japanese":"祖父はこのお寺に眠っています。","romaji":"Sofu wa kono otera ni nemutte imasu.","german":"My grandfather rests at this temple.","furigana":"そふ|てら|ねむ"}],"notes":"眠る means \"to sleep\" in the sense of \"actually being asleep\". 寝る is broader and also means \"to go to bed, to lie down\" – one can be 寝ている and still not be able to 眠れる. Very common is the potential form 眠れない \"cannot sleep\"; \"tired, sleepy\", by contrast, is 眠い. In an elevated register 眠る also stands for the dead (\"to rest\") and for unused things (眠っている才能 \"dormant talent\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"童話には、眠り薬を使って人を眠らせる話がよく出てきます。","romaji":"Dōwa ni wa, nemurigusuri o tsukatte hito o nemuraseru hanashi ga yoku dete kimasu.","german":"Fairy tales often have stories in which a sleeping potion is used to put someone to sleep.","furigana":"どうわ|ねむ|ぐすり|つか|ひと|ねむ|はなし|で"},{"japanese":"寝る前に眠り薬を飲んだので、朝まで一度も起きなかった。","romaji":"Neru mae ni nemurigusuri o nonda node, asa made ichido mo okinakatta.","german":"I took a sleeping pill before bed, so I didn't wake up once until morning.","furigana":"ね|まえ|ねむ|ぐすり|の|あさ|いちど|お"},{"japanese":"お酒と眠り薬をいっしょに飲むのは危険です。","romaji":"O-sake to nemurigusuri o issho ni nomu no wa kiken desu.","german":"It is dangerous to take alcohol and sleeping pills together.","furigana":"さけ|ねむ|ぐすり|の|きけん"}],"notes":"眠り薬 is made up of 眠り (\"sleep\") and 薬, with くすり becoming ぐすり. It is the everyday, somewhat old-fashioned word for \"sleeping drug\"; in medicine and on package inserts it is called 睡眠薬 or 睡眠導入剤. One says 眠り薬を飲む; in fairy tales and crime stories, 眠り薬を使う or 〜に眠り薬を飲ませる (\"to give someone a sleeping drug\") also occurs.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この立方体の体積を求めなさい。","romaji":"Kono rippōtai no taiseki o motomenasai.","german":"Find the volume of this cube.","furigana":"りっぽうたい|たいせき|もと"},{"japanese":"水は凍ると体積が増える。","romaji":"Mizu wa kōru to taiseki ga fueru.","german":"When water freezes, its volume increases.","furigana":"みず|こお|たいせき|ふ"},{"japanese":"この箱は体積が大きいわりに、とても軽い。","romaji":"Kono hako wa taiseki ga ōkii wari ni, totemo karui.","german":"This box is very light for its volume.","furigana":"はこ|たいせき|おお|かる"}],"notes":"体積 is the spatial \"volume\" of a body in mathematics and physics: 体積を求める/計算する, 体積が増える/減る. To be distinguished from 容積 (capacity, the internal volume of a container), 容量 (capacity, also amount of data) and 面積 (\"area\"). \"Loudness\" is not 体積 but 音量 or ボリューム.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"父は緊急手術を受けることになりました。","romaji":"Chichi wa kinkyū shujutsu o ukeru koto ni narimashita.","german":"My father is going to have emergency surgery.","furigana":"ちち|きんきゅうしゅじゅつ|う"},{"japanese":"緊急の場合は、この番号に電話してください。","romaji":"Kinkyū no baai wa, kono bangō ni denwa shite kudasai.","german":"In case of emergency, please call this number.","furigana":"きんきゅう|ばあい|ばんごう|でんわ"},{"japanese":"緊急な用事ができたので、今日は早く帰ります。","romaji":"Kinkyū na yōji ga dekita node, kyō wa hayaku kaerimasu.","german":"Something urgent has come up, so I'll go home early today.","furigana":"きんきゅう|ようじ|きょう|はや|かえ"}],"notes":"緊急 means \"urgent, emergency\" and mostly appears as the first element of compounds: 緊急手術, 緊急事態, 緊急停止, 緊急地震速報. Attributively both 緊急の (緊急の場合 \"in an emergency\") and 緊急な (緊急な用事) are possible; also 緊急時. Related are 急 (\"sudden, hurried\": 急な用事), 至急 (\"immediately\", typical in office emails: 至急ご連絡ください) and 非常 (\"emergency\": 非常口 \"emergency exit\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このシートベルトは自動的に締まります。","romaji":"Kono shītoberuto wa jidōteki ni shimarimasu.","german":"This seat belt tightens automatically.","furigana":"じどうてき|し"},{"japanese":"ネクタイがきつく締まっていて、苦しい。","romaji":"Nekutai ga kitsuku shimatte ite, kurushii.","german":"The tie is tight and I can hardly breathe.","furigana":"し|くる"},{"japanese":"毎日運動を続けたら、体が締まってきた。","romaji":"Mainichi undō o tsuzuketara, karada ga shimatte kita.","german":"Since I started exercising every day, my body has become firmer.","furigana":"まいにちうんどう|つづ|からだ|し"}],"notes":"締まる is intransitive: something \"becomes tight, tightens up\", such as a ねじ (screw), belt or ネクタイ (tie); the transitive counterpart is 締める (ベルトを締める). Figuratively it means \"to become taut, toned\" (体が締まる) or \"to become tense, focused\", often as 引き締まる (気が引き締まる). Do not confuse it with 閉まる \"to close\" (ドアが閉まる), which is read the same.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どこで空中航法を学んだんですか。","romaji":"Doko de kūchū kōhō o mananda n desu ka.","german":"Where did you learn aerial navigation?","furigana":"くうちゅうこうほう|まな"},{"japanese":"昔の船乗りは、星の位置を見て進む天文航法を使っていた。","romaji":"Mukashi no funanori wa, hoshi no ichi o mite susumu tenmon kōhō o tsukatte ita.","german":"Sailors of old used celestial navigation, steering by the positions of the stars.","furigana":"むかし|ふなの|ほし|いち|み|すす|てんもんこうほう|つか"},{"japanese":"今の飛行機は、GPSを使った航法で飛んでいる。","romaji":"Ima no hikōki wa, GPS o tsukatta kōhō de tonde iru.","german":"Today's airplanes fly using GPS-based navigation.","furigana":"いま|ひこうき|つか|こうほう|と"}],"notes":"航法 is a technical term for the \"navigation\" of ships and aircraft, that is, the technique of determining position and course: 天文航法 (\"celestial navigation\"), 推測航法 (\"dead reckoning\"), 衛星航法. In everyday speech, for example when driving a car, people say ナビ or カーナビ, not 航法. Watch out for similar-sounding words: 広報 (\"public relations\"), 後方 (\"rear\").","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この船は神戸港に直航します。","romaji":"Kono fune wa Kōbe-kō ni chokkō shimasu.","german":"This ship sails directly to Kobe Port.","furigana":"ふね||ちょっこう"},{"japanese":"台風のため、船は途中の港に寄らずに目的地へ直航した。","romaji":"Taifū no tame, fune wa tochū no minato ni yorazu ni mokutekichi e chokkō shita.","german":"Because of the typhoon, the ship went straight to its destination without calling at ports along the way.","furigana":"たいふう|ふね|とちゅう|みなと|よ|もくてきち|ちょっこう"},{"japanese":"以前は東京と那覇を結ぶ直航便が毎日出ていた。","romaji":"Izen wa Tōkyō to Naha o musubu chokkōbin ga mainichi dete ita.","german":"There used to be daily direct flights connecting Tokyo and Naha.","furigana":"いぜん|とうきょう|なは|むす|ちょっこうびん|まいにちで"}],"notes":"直航 refers to a direct ship or flight connection without a stopover; as a する-verb it is intransitive: 〜に/へ直航する. Common is the compound 直航便 (\"direct service, direct flight\"). For flights, people mostly write 直行便 today; 直行 generally means \"to go directly\" (会社に直行する), whereas 直航 is limited to ship and air traffic and is more literary.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この街道をずっとまっすぐ行くと、そのうち京都に着きますよ。","romaji":"Kono kaidō o zutto massugu iku to, sonouchi Kyōto ni tsukimasu yo.","german":"If you keep going straight along this highway, you'll eventually reach Kyoto.","furigana":"かいどう|い|きょうと|つ"},{"japanese":"昔の旅人は、東海道などの街道を歩いて江戸へ向かった。","romaji":"Mukashi no tabibito wa, Tōkaidō nado no kaidō o aruite Edo e mukatta.","german":"In the old days, travelers walked along highways such as the Tokaido toward Edo.","furigana":"むかし|たびびと|とうかいどう|かいどう|ある|えど|む"},{"japanese":"彼はデビュー以来、出世街道を走り続けている。","romaji":"Kare wa debyū irai, shusse kaidō o hashiri tsuzukete iru.","german":"Since his debut, he has kept running on the fast track to success.","furigana":"かれ|いらい|しゅっせかいどう|はし|つづ"}],"notes":"街道 are above all the historic long-distance roads of the Edo period, such as the 五街道 with 東海道 and 中山道; many roads today still bear names like 甲州街道 or 日光街道. For ordinary city streets one says 道, 通り or 道路, for national highways 国道. Figuratively, 〜街道 stands for a \"path\" in life, especially 出世街道 (\"career ladder, fast track to success\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"医者に診てもらったら、ただのカゼだと言われました。","romaji":"Isha ni mite morattara, tada no kaze da to iwaremashita.","german":"When I had the doctor examine me, I was told it was just a cold.","furigana":"いしゃ|み|い"},{"japanese":"先生、ちょっとこの傷を診ていただけますか。","romaji":"Sensei, chotto kono kizu o mite itadakemasu ka?","german":"Doctor, could you take a look at this wound, please?","furigana":"せんせい|きず|み"},{"japanese":"この病院は、日曜日でも患者を診てくれます。","romaji":"Kono byōin wa, nichiyōbi demo kanja o mite kuremasu.","german":"This hospital sees patients even on Sundays.","furigana":"びょういん|にちようび|かんじゃ|み"}],"notes":"診る is used only for medical examination: the subject is the doctor, the object the patient or a body part (患者を診る, 傷を診る, 脈を診る). From the patient's point of view, 医者に診てもらう is the standard form. Ordinary \"to see\" is 見る, \"to nurse, to look after\" is 看る (親を看る). More formal is 診察する; the kanji 診 is also in 診断 (\"diagnosis\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この案件を請けるのはリスクが高いですが、チャレンジしてみたいです。","romaji":"Kono anken o ukeru no wa risuku ga takai desu ga, charenji shite mitai desu.","german":"Taking on this project is risky, but I'd like to take up the challenge.","furigana":"あんけん|う|たか"},{"japanese":"フリーランスで、翻訳の仕事を請けています。","romaji":"Furīransu de, hon'yaku no shigoto o ukete imasu.","german":"I work freelance and take on translation jobs.","furigana":"ほんやく|しごと|う"},{"japanese":"この工事を請ける会社がなかなか見つからない。","romaji":"Kono kōji o ukeru kaisha ga nakanaka mitsukaranai.","german":"We just can't find a company that will take on this construction job.","furigana":"こうじ|う|かいしゃ|み"}],"notes":"請ける is a special spelling of うける for accepting jobs and contracts: 仕事を請ける, 注文を請ける, 工事を請ける. In all other meanings (\"to receive\", \"to take an exam\") one writes 受ける, and many write 受ける even for jobs. More common than the simple verb are the derivatives 請け負う (\"to take on a contract\"), 下請け (\"subcontractor\") and 元請け (\"main contractor\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これは私のじゃなくて、貸し自転車なんです。","romaji":"Kore wa watashi no ja nakute, kashi jitensha na n desu.","german":"This isn't mine; it's a rental bicycle.","furigana":"わたし|か|じてんしゃ"},{"japanese":"湖で貸しボートに乗って遊びました。","romaji":"Mizuumi de kashi bōto ni notte asobimashita.","german":"We played on the lake riding a rental boat.","furigana":"みずうみ|か|の|あそ"},{"japanese":"駅前の貸し会議室で打ち合わせをしましょう。","romaji":"Ekimae no kashi kaigishitsu de uchiawase o shimashō.","german":"Let's have the meeting in the rental meeting room in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|か|かいぎしつ|う|あ"}],"notes":"貸し〜 is a prefix from 貸す and denotes something lent or rented out for a fee - from the provider's point of view: 貸し自転車, 貸しボート, 貸し衣装, 貸し会議室. The okurigana し is often dropped on signs (貸自転車, 貸会議室). Today it competes with レンタル〜 (レンタサイクル). On its own, 貸し means \"a favor someone owes you\": 彼には貸しがある. Do not confuse it with 貸し切り (\"exclusively rented\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は方向音痴で、よく道に迷います。","romaji":"Watashi wa hōkō onchi de, yoku michi ni mayoimasu.","german":"I have no sense of direction and often get lost.","furigana":"わたし|ほうこうおんち|みち|まよ"},{"japanese":"道に迷ってしまって、約束の時間に遅れました。","romaji":"Michi ni mayotte shimatte, yakusoku no jikan ni okuremashita.","german":"I got lost and was late for the appointment.","furigana":"みち|まよ|やくそく|じかん|おく"},{"japanese":"山で道に迷ったときは、あまり動かないほうがいい。","romaji":"Yama de michi ni mayotta toki wa, amari ugokanai hō ga ii.","german":"When you get lost in the mountains, it is better not to move around much.","furigana":"やま|みち|まよ|うご"}],"notes":"道に迷う is the fixed expression for \"to get lost, to lose one's way\"; the particle に is fixed. On its own, 迷う means \"to hesitate, to be undecided\" (どちらにするか迷う). A lost child is 迷子 (迷子になる); colloquially adults also say 迷子になった. Someone who generally has no sense of direction is 方向音痴. Figuratively there is 人生の道に迷う (\"to lose one's bearings in life\").","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 16"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"去年、韓国に行きました。","romaji":"Kyonen, Kankoku ni ikimashita.","german":"Last year I went to South Korea.","furigana":"きょねん|かんこく|い"},{"japanese":"韓国の料理が好きです。","romaji":"Kankoku no ryōri ga suki desu.","german":"I like Korean food.","furigana":"かんこく|りょうり|す"}],"notes":"韓国 here means South Korea. With に you name the country as a travel destination; 韓国の料理 links country and cuisine with の. The word is read かんこく and has no long vowels. 去年 names the past year; 好き marks a personal preference.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この風習は日韓両国でよく見られます。","romaji":"Kono fūshū wa Nikkan ryōkoku de yoku miraremasu.","german":"This custom is often seen in both Japan and South Korea.","furigana":"ふうしゅう|にっかんりょうこく|み"},{"japanese":"サッカーの日韓戦は、いつもとても盛り上がる。","romaji":"Sakkā no Nikkan-sen wa, itsumo totemo moriagaru.","german":"Soccer matches between Japan and Korea are always very exciting.","furigana":"にっかんせん|も|あ"},{"japanese":"日韓辞典で、韓国語の単語を調べた。","romaji":"Nikkan jiten de, Kankokugo no tango o shirabeta.","german":"I looked up a Korean word in a Japanese-Korean dictionary.","furigana":"にっかんじてん|かんこくご|たんご|しら"}],"notes":"日韓 is the short form for \"Japan and (South) Korea\" and almost always stands before a noun: 日韓関係, 日韓戦, 日韓両国, 日韓辞典 (\"Japanese-Korean dictionary\"). From the Japanese perspective, 日 comes first; in Korea, people say it the other way round, 韓日. 韓 refers to South Korea (韓国); for Japan-North Korea one says 日朝. Formed on the same pattern are 日米, 日中 and 日独.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"奇数を3才の子どもに説明するのは難しいです。","romaji":"Kisū o sansai no kodomo ni setsumei suru no wa muzukashii desu.","german":"It is difficult to explain odd numbers to a three-year-old child.","furigana":"きすう|さい|こ|せつめい|むずか"},{"japanese":"奇数は、2で割ると1が余る数です。","romaji":"Kisū wa, ni de waru to ichi ga amaru kazu desu.","german":"An odd number is a number that leaves a remainder of 1 when divided by 2.","furigana":"きすう|わ|あま|かず"},{"japanese":"偶数の席の人は右へ、奇数の席の人は左へ進んでください。","romaji":"Gūsū no seki no hito wa migi e, kisū no seki no hito wa hidari e susunde kudasai.","german":"People in even-numbered seats, please move to the right; people in odd-numbered seats, please move to the left.","furigana":"ぐうすう|せき|ひと|みぎ|きすう|せき|ひと|ひだり|すす"}],"notes":"奇数 is the \"odd number\", the opposite is 偶数 (\"even number\"). Attributively it takes の: 奇数の日, 奇数の席, 奇数の月. The kanji 奇 also means \"strange\" (cf. 奇妙). Culturally, odd amounts are considered appropriate for wedding gifts in Japan because they cannot be \"divided\" - hence, for example, 3万円.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"『世にも奇妙な物語』っていう番組、知ってる？","romaji":"Yo ni mo Kimyō na Monogatari tte iu bangumi, shitteru?","german":"Do you know the TV program \"Yo ni mo Kimyo na Monogatari\"?","furigana":"よ|きみょう|ものがたり|ばんぐみ|し"},{"japanese":"夜中に奇妙な音が聞こえて、目が覚めた。","romaji":"Yonaka ni kimyō na oto ga kikoete, me ga sameta.","german":"I woke up when I heard a strange sound in the middle of the night.","furigana":"よなか|きみょう|おと|き|め|さ"},{"japanese":"二人が同じ夢を見たなんて、奇妙な話だね。","romaji":"Futari ga onaji yume o mita nante, kimyō na hanashi da ne.","german":"It's a curious story that the two of them had the same dream.","furigana":"ふたり|おな|ゆめ|み|きみょう|はなし"}],"notes":"奇妙 is a な-adjective for \"strange, odd\" in the sense of hard to explain or slightly eerie: 奇妙な音, 奇妙な話, 奇妙な形. It sounds somewhat more literary than 変な or おかしな; 不思議な stresses more the wondrous and is neutral to positive. A common phrase: 奇妙なことに (\"strangely enough\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「よくそんな冷たいことが言えるよな！」と、コウイチは怒気をおびた声で言った。","romaji":"\"Yoku sonna tsumetai koto ga ieru yo na!\" to, Kōichi wa doki o obita koe de itta.","german":"\"How can you say something so cold!\" said Koichi in an angry voice.","furigana":"つめ|い|どき|こえ|い"},{"japanese":"部長の言葉には、怒気が含まれていた。","romaji":"Buchō no kotoba ni wa, doki ga fukumarete ita.","german":"The department head's words carried a note of anger.","furigana":"ぶちょう|ことば|どき|ふく"},{"japanese":"彼は怒気を抑えて、静かに話し始めた。","romaji":"Kare wa doki o osaete, shizuka ni hanashihajimeta.","german":"He suppressed his anger and began to speak quietly.","furigana":"かれ|どき|おさ|しず|はな|はじ"}],"notes":"怒気 is a literary word for the \"anger\" that can be felt in a voice, words or expression. Typical are 怒気を帯びる (\"to be filled with anger\"), 怒気を含んだ声 and 怒気を抑える. In spoken language people say 怒り or 腹が立つ; 激怒, by contrast, refers to a violent outburst of rage.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"腹が減ったなぁ。何か作ってよ。","romaji":"Hara ga hetta nā. Nani ka tsukutte yo.","german":"I'm hungry. Make me something.","furigana":"はら|へ|なに|つく"},{"japanese":"部活の後はいつも腹が減って死にそうだ。","romaji":"Bukatsu no ato wa itsumo hara ga hette shini sō da.","german":"After club activities I'm always starving.","furigana":"ぶかつ|あと|はら|へ|し"},{"japanese":"あー、腹が減った。ラーメンでも食べに行こうぜ。","romaji":"Ā, hara ga hetta. Rāmen demo tabe ni ikō ze.","german":"Ah, I'm hungry. Let's go get some ramen or something.","furigana":"はら|へ|た|い"}],"notes":"腹が減った is the rough, mainly male way of saying \"I'm hungry\": 腹 \"belly\" is coarser than お腹, 減る means \"to decrease\", and the past tense describes the present state. Even more casual is 腹減った without が. Neutrally one says お腹が空いた, politely お腹が空きました; 腹が減った is inappropriate in polite situations.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ここを訪れるのは三年ぶりです。","romaji":"Koko o otozureru no wa sannenburi desu.","german":"It's been three years since I last visited here.","furigana":"おとず|さんねん"},{"japanese":"毎年、多くの観光客が京都を訪れる。","romaji":"Maitoshi, ōku no kankōkyaku ga Kyōto o otozureru.","german":"Every year, many tourists visit Kyoto.","furigana":"まいとし|おお|かんこうきゃく|きょうと|おとず"},{"japanese":"長い冬が終わり、ようやく春が訪れた。","romaji":"Nagai fuyu ga owari, yōyaku haru ga otozureta.","german":"The long winter ended, and spring finally arrived.","furigana":"なが|ふゆ|お|はる|おとず"}],"notes":"訪れる means \"to visit, to call on\" and sounds more elevated than 行く or 訪ねる; it is used mainly with places: 京都を訪れる, 現地を訪れる. For visits to people, 訪ねる is more usual. Intransitively it is used for times and events that \"come, set in\": 春が訪れる, チャンスが訪れる. The noun is 訪れ (春の訪れ).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は警察に監視されているというウワサがあります。","romaji":"Kare wa keisatsu ni kanshi sarete iru to iu uwasa ga arimasu.","german":"There are rumors that he is being watched by the police.","furigana":"かれ|けいさつ|かんし"},{"japanese":"この建物は、24時間カメラで監視されている。","romaji":"Kono tatemono wa, nijūyo-jikan kamera de kanshi sarete iru.","german":"This building is monitored by cameras 24 hours a day.","furigana":"たてもの|じかん|かんし"},{"japanese":"試験中、先生は教室を歩きながら学生を監視していた。","romaji":"Shiken-chū, sensei wa kyōshitsu o arukinagara gakusei o kanshi shite ita.","german":"During the exam, the teacher monitored the students while walking around the classroom.","furigana":"しけんちゅう|せんせい|きょうしつ|ある|がくせい|かんし"}],"notes":"監視 is \"surveillance, supervision\"; as a transitive する-verb: 〜を監視する, passive 監視される. Common compounds are 監視カメラ, 監視員 (e.g. lifeguard) and 監視下. 見張る is a simpler word for \"to keep watch\", 観察 means \"to observe\" in the neutral or scientific sense, and 見守る is a benevolent \"to watch over someone\". 監視 often carries an undertone of control and distrust.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"勧誘の電話は迷惑だと思う。","romaji":"Kan'yū no denwa wa meiwaku da to omou.","german":"I think solicitation phone calls are a nuisance.","furigana":"かんゆう|でんわ|めいわく|おも"},{"japanese":"大学に入学すると、いろいろなサークルから勧誘される。","romaji":"Daigaku ni nyūgaku suru to, iroiro na sākuru kara kan'yū sareru.","german":"When you enter university, you get recruited by various clubs.","furigana":"だいがく|にゅうがく|かんゆう"},{"japanese":"しつこい保険の勧誘を断るのは大変だ。","romaji":"Shitsukoi hoken no kan'yū o kotowaru no wa taihen da.","german":"It is hard to turn down persistent insurance sales pitches.","furigana":"ほけん|かんゆう|ことわ|たいへん"}],"notes":"勧誘 is recruiting people to join, buy or sign a contract, often with an intrusive undertone: 新聞の勧誘, 保険の勧誘, 宗教の勧誘, サークルの勧誘. Construction: 人を〜に勧誘する, passive 勧誘される. Advertising of products in the media, by contrast, is 広告 or 宣伝. The simple everyday word for \"to invite, to ask\" is 誘う.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は今、工場で研修生として働いています。","romaji":"Watashi wa ima, kōjō de kenshūsei toshite hataraite imasu.","german":"I'm currently working at a factory as a trainee.","furigana":"わたし|いま|こうじょう|けんしゅうせい|はたら"},{"japanese":"来月から、新しい研修生が三人入ってきます。","romaji":"Raigetsu kara, atarashii kenshūsei ga sannin haitte kimasu.","german":"Three new trainees will join us starting next month.","furigana":"らいげつ|あたら|けんしゅうせい|さんにんはい"},{"japanese":"研修生の間は、先輩について仕事を覚えます。","romaji":"Kenshūsei no aida wa, senpai ni tsuite shigoto o oboemasu.","german":"While you are a trainee, you learn the work by following your seniors.","furigana":"けんしゅうせい|あいだ|せんぱい|しごと|おぼ"}],"notes":"研修生 is someone who is going through a 研修 - training, orientation or further education - such as new employees at a company. In Japan it often also refers to foreign workers in training programs (外国人研修生, today mostly 技能実習生). An internship during university is more often called インターン; 新入社員 are simply \"new employees\". Typical: 研修生として働く, 研修生を受け入れる.","levelNote":"Compound: 研修 (N2) + 生 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"迷惑メールが次から次へと送られてくる。","romaji":"Meiwaku mēru ga tsugi kara tsugi e to okurarete kuru.","german":"Spam emails keep coming one after another.","furigana":"めいわく|つぎ|つぎ|おく"},{"japanese":"大事なメールが迷惑メールフォルダに入っていた。","romaji":"Daiji na mēru ga meiwaku mēru foruda ni haitte ita.","german":"An important email was in the spam folder.","furigana":"だいじ|めいわく|はい"},{"japanese":"知らない人からの迷惑メールは開かずに削除してください。","romaji":"Shiranai hito kara no meiwaku mēru wa hirakazu ni sakujo shite kudasai.","german":"Please delete spam emails from people you don't know without opening them.","furigana":"し|ひと|めいわく|ひら|さくじょ"}],"notes":"迷惑メール (迷惑 \"nuisance\" + メール) is the usual everyday word for spam; there is also スパムメール. Typical phrases are 迷惑メールフォルダ \"spam folder\", 迷惑メールに振り分けられる \"to end up in spam\" and 迷惑メール対策 \"spam protection\". On the same pattern, 迷惑電話 is formed for nuisance calls.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"分別のある家政婦さんで良かったよ。","romaji":"Funbetsu no aru kaseifu-san de yokatta yo.","german":"I'm glad the housekeeper is sensible.","furigana":"ふんべつ|かせいふ|よ"},{"japanese":"両親が共働きなので、週に二回家政婦さんに来てもらっている。","romaji":"Ryōshin ga tomobataraki na node, shū ni nikai kaseifu-san ni kite moratte iru.","german":"Both my parents work, so a housekeeper comes twice a week.","furigana":"りょうしん|ともばたら|しゅう|にかいかせいふ|き"},{"japanese":"家政婦として、お年寄りの家で料理や掃除をしています。","romaji":"Kaseifu to shite, otoshiyori no ie de ryōri ya sōji o shite imasu.","german":"I work as a housekeeper, cooking and cleaning at the homes of elderly people.","furigana":"かせいふ|としよ|いえ|りょうり|そうじ"}],"notes":"家政婦 is a paid housekeeper or domestic helper who cleans, cooks and does laundry; people usually say 家政婦さん politely. Today 家事代行 (\"housework service\") and ハウスキーパー are also common, and お手伝いさん is somewhat old-fashioned. Do not confuse it with 主婦 (\"housewife\"), who runs her own household. Typical: 家政婦さんに来てもらう, 家政婦として働く.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"父さんを怒らせると後が面倒よ。","romaji":"Tōsan o okoraseru to ato ga mendō yo.","german":"If you make Dad angry, it's a hassle afterwards.","furigana":"とう|おこ|あと|めんどう"},{"japanese":"冗談のつもりだったのに、彼女を怒らせてしまった。","romaji":"Jōdan no tsumori datta noni, kanojo o okorasete shimatta.","german":"I meant it as a joke, but I ended up making her angry.","furigana":"じょうだん|かのじょ|おこ"},{"japanese":"お客様を怒らせないように、言葉に気をつけてください。","romaji":"Okyakusama o okorasenai yō ni, kotoba ni ki o tsukete kudasai.","german":"Please be careful with your words so you don't anger the customers.","furigana":"きゃくさま|おこ|ことば|き"}],"notes":"怒らせる is the causative form of 怒る \"to get angry\" and means \"to anger, to make someone furious\": 人を怒らせる. Very common is 怒らせてしまう for accidentally angering someone. There is also the equivalent, somewhat more casual form 怒らす. Do not confuse it with 怒る in the sense of \"to scold\" (子どもを怒る) or the stricter 叱る.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"玄米は体に良い。","romaji":"Genmai wa karada ni yoi.","german":"Brown rice is good for the body.","furigana":"げんまい|からだ|よ"},{"japanese":"玄米は白米より、炊くのに時間がかかる。","romaji":"Genmai wa hakumai yori, taku no ni jikan ga kakaru.","german":"Brown rice takes longer to cook than white rice.","furigana":"げんまい|はくまい|た|じかん"},{"japanese":"最近、玄米を混ぜたご飯を食べるようにしています。","romaji":"Saikin, genmai o mazeta gohan o taberu yō ni shite imasu.","german":"Lately I've been trying to eat rice mixed with brown rice.","furigana":"さいきん|げんまい|ま|はん|た"}],"notes":"玄米 is unhulled \"natural\" or \"whole-grain rice\" from which only the husk has been removed; the opposite is 白米 or 精米 (\"polished rice\"). One says 玄米を炊く, and it is considered healthy but takes longer to cook. Also well known is 玄米茶, green tea with roasted rice. Cooked rice in general is ご飯, raw rice 米.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"学校の帰りに、図書館に寄るつもりです。","romaji":"Gakkō no kaeri ni, toshokan ni yoru tsumori desu.","german":"I plan to drop by the library on my way home from school.","furigana":"がっこう|かえ|としょかん|よ"},{"japanese":"写真を撮るので、もう少し真ん中に寄ってください。","romaji":"Shashin o toru node, mou sukoshi mannaka ni yotte kudasai.","german":"I'm taking a photo, so please move a little closer to the middle.","furigana":"しゃしん|と|すこ|ま|なか|よ"},{"japanese":"近くに来たら、ぜひうちにも寄ってください。","romaji":"Chikaku ni kitara, zehi uchi ni mo yotte kudasai.","german":"If you are ever nearby, please do drop by our place, too.","furigana":"ちか|き|よ"}],"notes":"寄る has two core meanings. First, \"to drop by briefly, to stop on the way\", with the destination marked by に: 帰りにスーパーに寄る; similar is 立ち寄る. Second, \"to approach, to move to the side\": もう少し右に寄ってください; 近寄る belongs here. The transitive counterpart is 寄せる \"to move closer, to bring near\". Do not confuse it with よる in ～によると \"according to\", which is written in kana.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 19"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"迷いがあるなら、もう少し考えたほうがいいよ。","romaji":"Mayoi ga arunara, mōsukoshi kangaeta hō ga ii yo.","german":"If you have doubts, you should think about it a little longer.","furigana":"まよ|すこ|かんが"},{"japanese":"彼の言葉を聞いて、迷いが消えた。","romaji":"Kare no kotoba o kiite, mayoi ga kieta.","german":"Hearing his words, my hesitation disappeared.","furigana":"かれ|ことば|き|まよ|き"},{"japanese":"彼女は迷いのない顔で「やります」と答えた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa mayoi no nai kao de \"yarimasu\" to kotaeta.","german":"She answered \"I'll do it\" with a face free of hesitation.","furigana":"かのじょ|まよ|かお|こた"}],"notes":"迷い is the noun for 迷う and denotes \"doubt, indecision, hesitation\" before a decision. Typical are 迷いがある/ない, 迷いが消える, 迷いを捨てる and attributively 迷いのない表情 (\"without any hesitation\"). ためらい stresses more the hesitation before an action, 疑い is \"suspicion\". In the Buddhist sense, 迷い also stands for \"delusion\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"夜になると捜索が難しくなるため、いったんストップして早朝から再開する予定です。","romaji":"Yoru ni naru to sōsaku ga muzukashiku naru tame, ittan sutoppu shite sōchō kara saikai suru yotei desu.","german":"Searching becomes difficult at night, so we plan to stop for now and resume early in the morning.","furigana":"よる|そうさく|むずか|そうちょう|さいかい|よてい"},{"japanese":"警察は、行方不明の子どもを捜索している。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa, yukue fumei no kodomo o sōsaku shite iru.","german":"The police are searching for the missing child.","furigana":"けいさつ|ゆくえふめい|こ|そうさく"},{"japanese":"警察が男の家を捜索し、証拠を見つけた。","romaji":"Keisatsu ga otoko no ie o sōsaku shi, shōko o mitsuketa.","german":"The police searched the man's house and found evidence.","furigana":"けいさつ|おとこ|いえ|そうさく|しょうこ|み"}],"notes":"捜索 is an organized \"search\" for missing persons or things (捜索隊, 捜索願を出す \"to file a missing person report\") and the police \"search\" of premises (家宅捜索). As a transitive する-verb: 人/場所を捜索する. In everyday speech one simply says 探す/捜す. Not to be confused with 検索 (\"search\" on the internet or in databases) and 捜査 (\"police investigation\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この店のことは全て君の判断に任すよ。","romaji":"Kono mise no koto wa subete kimi no handan ni makasu yo.","german":"I leave everything about this shop to your judgment.","furigana":"みせ|すべ|きみ|はんだん|まか"},{"japanese":"細かいことは君に任すから、好きにやっていいよ。","romaji":"Komakai koto wa kimi ni makasu kara, suki ni yatte ii yo.","german":"I'll leave the details to you, so feel free to do as you like.","furigana":"こま|きみ|まか|す"},{"japanese":"やるだけやったから、あとは運に任すしかない。","romaji":"Yaru dake yatta kara, ato wa un ni makasu shika nai.","german":"I've done all I can, so all that's left is to leave the rest to luck.","furigana":"うん|まか"}],"notes":"任す is a variant of the much more common 任せる (ichidan) with the same meaning \"to leave, to entrust\": AをBに任す. It sounds somewhat more colloquial or older; it is familiar mainly in forms such as 任しておけ (\"Leave that to me!\"). In neutral and polite contexts, it is better to use 任せる (お任せします, 任せてください).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この辺は、豚カツ屋が少ない。","romaji":"Kono hen wa, tonkatsuya ga sukunai.","german":"There are few tonkatsu restaurants around here.","furigana":"へん|とん|や|すく"},{"japanese":"試験の前の日に、「勝つ」にかけて豚カツを食べた。","romaji":"Shiken no mae no hi ni, katsu ni kakete tonkatsu o tabeta.","german":"The day before the exam, I ate tonkatsu as a pun on \"win\" (katsu).","furigana":"しけん|まえ|ひ|か|とん|た"},{"japanese":"豚カツには、ソースとキャベツがよく合います。","romaji":"Tonkatsu ni wa, sōsu to kyabetsu ga yoku aimasu.","german":"Sauce and cabbage go well with tonkatsu.","furigana":"とん|あ"}],"notes":"豚カツ is a breaded, deep-fried pork cutlet; it is mostly written とんかつ or トンカツ, and the mixed spelling 豚カツ is also common. カツ comes from \"cutlet\" and is found in カツ丼, カツカレー, ヒレカツ and ロースカツ. Before exams or competitions, people like to eat it as a pun on 勝つ (\"to win\"). A tonkatsu restaurant is called 豚カツ屋.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"それを見た母は激怒しました。","romaji":"Sore o mita haha wa gekido shimashita.","german":"When my mother saw that, she flew into a rage.","furigana":"み|はは|げきど"},{"japanese":"社長は、部下の大きなミスに激怒した。","romaji":"Shachō wa, buka no ōkina misu ni gekido shita.","german":"The company president was furious at his subordinate's big mistake.","furigana":"しゃちょう|ぶか|おお|げきど"},{"japanese":"選手の失礼な発言が、ファンを激怒させた。","romaji":"Senshu no shitsurei na hatsugen ga, fan o gekido saseta.","german":"The player's rude remark enraged the fans.","furigana":"せんしゅ|しつれい|はつげん|げきど"}],"notes":"激怒 is \"raging anger\" and, as an intransitive する-verb, \"to be beside oneself with rage\"; the cause takes に: 〜に激怒する. Causative: 〜を激怒させる (\"to send someone into a rage\"). It is clearly stronger than 怒る and typical of the news and reports; colloquially one more often says かんかんに怒る or ブチ切れる. The literary 怒気, by contrast, denotes anger that can be felt in the voice or expression.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あのYouTuberは、相次ぐ批判の声にも負けず、コンテンツを作り続けた。","romaji":"Ano YouTuber wa, aitsugu hihan no koe ni mo makezu, kontentsu o tsukuri tsuzuketa.","german":"That YouTuber kept making content, undeterred by the string of critical voices.","furigana":"あいつ|ひはん|こえ|ま|つく|つづ"},{"japanese":"今年は大きな地震が相次いだ。","romaji":"Kotoshi wa ōkina jishin ga aitsuida.","german":"This year, big earthquakes have occurred one after another.","furigana":"ことし|おお|じしん|あいつ"},{"japanese":"新しい店には、予約が相次いでいる。","romaji":"Atarashii mise ni wa, yoyaku ga aitsuide iru.","german":"Reservations are coming in one after another for the new shop.","furigana":"あたら|みせ|よやく|あいつ"}],"notes":"相次ぐ is intransitive and means \"to follow one after another, to pile up\"; the subject takes が: 事故が相次ぐ. Very common are the attributive form (相次ぐ批判, 相次ぐトラブル) and the te-form as the adverb 相次いで (\"one after another\"). The word is typical of the news; in daily life people more often say 次々に or 続けて. 続く is more general and stresses only continuation.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"元総理も同じ考えをもっていました。","romaji":"Moto sōri mo onaji kangae o motte imashita.","german":"The former prime minister held the same view.","furigana":"もとそうり|おな|かんが"},{"japanese":"総理は記者会見で、新しい政策について説明した。","romaji":"Sōri wa kisha kaiken de, atarashii seisaku ni tsuite setsumei shita.","german":"The prime minister explained the new policy at a press conference.","furigana":"そうり|きしゃかいけん|あたら|せいさく|せつめい"},{"japanese":"次の総理には、誰がなると思う？","romaji":"Tsugi no sōri ni wa, dare ga naru to omou?","german":"Who do you think will be the next prime minister?","furigana":"つぎ|そうり|だれ|おも"}],"notes":"総理 is the short form of 内閣総理大臣, the Japanese \"Prime Minister\". Newspapers mostly use 首相, while on TV and in spoken language 総理 or 総理大臣 is often heard; the prime minister is also addressed directly as 総理. 元総理 is a \"former prime minister\". A head of state, by contrast, is called 大統領.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"そのサムライは、とても美しい中段の構えをとることで有名です。","romaji":"Sono samurai wa, totemo utsukushii chūdan no kamae o toru koto de yūmei desu.","german":"That samurai is famous for taking a very beautiful middle-level stance.","furigana":"うつく|ちゅうだん|かま|ゆうめい"},{"japanese":"会社は、裁判で争う構えを見せている。","romaji":"Kaisha wa, saiban de arasou kamae o misete iru.","german":"The company is showing a posture of readiness to fight it out in court.","furigana":"かいしゃ|さいばん|あらそ|かま|み"},{"japanese":"駅前に、立派な構えの和菓子屋がある。","romaji":"Ekimae ni, rippa na kamae no wagashiya ga aru.","german":"There is an impressive-looking Japanese confectionery shop in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|りっぱ|かま|わがしや"}],"notes":"構え is the noun of 構える. In martial arts and sports it is the \"posture, basic stance\": 構えをとる, 中段の構え, バットの構え. Figuratively, 〜構え means \"readiness, determination\" to do something, for example in the news: 争う構えを見せる. For buildings it means the \"outward appearance, the facade\" (立派な構えの店). The inner attitude is called 心構え.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"食の博覧会、略して「食博」に行ってきました！","romaji":"Shoku no hakurankai, ryakushite shokuhaku ni itte kimashita!","german":"I went to the Food Expo, \"Shokuhaku\" for short!","furigana":"しょく|はくらんかい|りゃく|しょくはく|い"},{"japanese":"2025年に、大阪で万国博覧会が開かれた。","romaji":"Nisen nijūgo-nen ni, Ōsaka de bankoku hakurankai ga hirakareta.","german":"In 2025, a World Expo was held in Osaka.","furigana":"ねん|おおさか|ばんこくはくらんかい|ひら"},{"japanese":"博覧会の会場は、平日でも人でいっぱいだった。","romaji":"Hakurankai no kaijō wa, heijitsu demo hito de ippai datta.","german":"The expo grounds were full of people even on weekdays.","furigana":"はくらんかい|かいじょう|へいじつ|ひと"}],"notes":"博覧会 is a large public \"exhibition, expo\" on a theme, often with pavilions and many visitors; 万国博覧会 (short 万博) is the \"World Expo\". It is often abbreviated with 〜博 (食博, 花博). An art exhibition is called 展覧会, a product or trade fair 展示会 or 見本市. Typical: 博覧会が開かれる, 博覧会に行く.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"西海岸ぞいの町にはアジアからの移住者が多い。","romaji":"Nishikaiganzoi no machi ni wa Ajia kara no ijūsha ga ōi.","german":"There are many immigrants from Asia in the towns along the West Coast.","furigana":"にしかいがん|まち|いじゅうしゃ|おお"},{"japanese":"最近、都会から田舎への移住者が増えている。","romaji":"Saikin, tokai kara inaka e no ijūsha ga fuete iru.","german":"Recently, the number of people moving from cities to the countryside is increasing.","furigana":"さいきん|とかい|いなか|いじゅうしゃ|ふ"},{"japanese":"この村は、移住者に古い家を安く貸している。","romaji":"Kono mura wa, ijūsha ni furui ie o yasuku kashite iru.","german":"This village rents old houses cheaply to newcomers.","furigana":"むら|いじゅうしゃ|ふる|いえ|やす|か"}],"notes":"移住者 is someone who settles permanently in a new place - both abroad (\"emigrant/immigrant\") and within Japan, for example from the city to the countryside (地方移住, Iターン). The verb is 移住する. 移民 is narrower and more political and means migrants across state borders. Typical: 〜からの移住者, 移住者が増える, 移住者を受け入れる.","levelNote":"Compound: 移住 (N1) + 者 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この町は緑が乏しい。","romaji":"Kono machi wa midori ga toboshii.","german":"This town lacks greenery.","furigana":"まち|みどり|とぼ"},{"japanese":"彼は経験が乏しいが、やる気は十分ある。","romaji":"Kare wa keiken ga toboshii ga, yaruki wa jūbun aru.","german":"He has little experience, but he has plenty of motivation.","furigana":"かれ|けいけん|とぼ|き|じゅうぶん"},{"japanese":"資源に乏しい日本は、石油のほとんどを輸入している。","romaji":"Shigen ni toboshii Nihon wa, sekiyu no hotondo o yunyū shite iru.","german":"Japan, which is poor in resources, imports most of its oil.","furigana":"しげん|とぼ|にほん|せきゆ|ゆにゅう"}],"notes":"乏しい is an い-adjective for \"scarce, poor in, meager\". It takes が (経験が乏しい) or に in the sense of \"poor in\" (資源に乏しい, 変化に乏しい). Typical related words are abstract: 経験, 知識, 資源, 想像力, 表情. It sounds more literary than 少ない or 足りない; opposites are 豊か and 豊富.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ジュースを冷やすのを忘れたので、氷を入れますね。","romaji":"Jūsu o hiyasu no o wasureta node, kōri o iremasu ne.","german":"I forgot to chill the juice, so I'll put in some ice.","furigana":"ひ|わす|こおり|い"},{"japanese":"熱があるときは、おでこを冷やすといいですよ。","romaji":"Netsu ga aru toki wa, odeko o hiyasu to ii desu yo.","german":"When you have a fever, it's good to cool your forehead.","furigana":"ねつ|ひ"},{"japanese":"少し頭を冷やしてから、もう一度話し合おう。","romaji":"Sukoshi atama o hiyashite kara, mō ichido hanashiaō.","german":"Let's cool our heads a bit and then talk it over again.","furigana":"すこ|あたま|ひ|いちどはな|あ"}],"notes":"冷やす is transitive: to actively \"cool, make cold\" something (ビールを冷やす, 冷蔵庫で冷やす); the intransitive counterpart is 冷える (\"to get cold\"). 冷ます, by contrast, means letting something hot cool down to a normal temperature (お茶を冷ます). Idiomatically: 頭を冷やす (\"to get a cool head\") and 肝を冷やす (\"to get a fright\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"結婚についての考え方は、人によってさまざまです。","romaji":"Kekkon ni tsuite no kangaekata wa, hito ni yotte samazama desu.","german":"Views on marriage vary from person to person.","furigana":"けっこん|かんが|かた|ひと"},{"japanese":"姉は去年、友達と結婚しました。","romaji":"Ane wa kyonen, tomodachi to kekkon shimashita.","german":"My older sister married a friend last year.","furigana":"あね|きょねん|ともだち|けっこん"}],"notes":"結婚 is marriage or wedlock. 結婚する forms an intransitive する-verb. 人と結婚する names the partner with と. 結婚について means \"about marriage\"; 人によってさまざま describes differing personal views. The small っ in けっこん doubles the k.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"近いうちに求婚しようと思うんだ。","romaji":"Chikai uchi ni kyūkon shiyō to omou n da.","german":"I'm thinking of proposing soon.","furigana":"ちか|きゅうこん|おも"},{"japanese":"彼は彼女に何度も求婚したが、そのたびに断られた。","romaji":"Kare wa kanojo ni nando mo kyūkon shita ga, sono tabi ni kotowarareta.","german":"He proposed to her many times, but was turned down each time.","furigana":"かれ|かのじょ|なんど|きゅうこん|ことわ"},{"japanese":"物語の最後で、王子は姫に求婚する。","romaji":"Monogatari no saigo de, ōji wa hime ni kyūkon suru.","german":"At the end of the story, the prince proposes to the princess.","furigana":"ものがたり|さいご|おうじ|ひめ|きゅうこん"}],"notes":"求婚 is the \"marriage proposal\"; as an intransitive する-verb the person takes に: 〜に求婚する. The word is formal or literary and appears mainly in novels, fairy tales and reports. In daily life people almost always say プロポーズする or, neutrally, 結婚を申し込む. Someone who asks for a hand in marriage is a 求婚者.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新婚旅行はいつ行くんですか？","romaji":"Shinkon ryokō wa itsu iku n desu ka?","german":"When are you going on your honeymoon?","furigana":"しんこんりょこう|い"},{"japanese":"新婚旅行でハワイに行きました。","romaji":"Shinkon ryokō de Hawai ni ikimashita.","german":"We went to Hawaii for our honeymoon.","furigana":"しんこんりょこう|い"},{"japanese":"仕事が忙しくて、新婚旅行は結婚の一年後になった。","romaji":"Shigoto ga isogashikute, shinkon ryokō wa kekkon no ichinen-go ni natta.","german":"Work was busy, so our honeymoon took place one year after the wedding.","furigana":"しごと|いそが|しんこんりょこう|けっこん|いちねんご"}],"notes":"新婚旅行 is composed of 新婚 (\"newly married\") and 旅行 and means \"honeymoon trip\". One says 新婚旅行に行く or 新婚旅行で〜へ行く. The loanword ハネムーン is also common, especially in advertising and travel agencies. Newlyweds are colloquially called 新婚さん.","levelNote":"Compound: 新婚 (N1) + 旅行 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"帰国の予定を一週間延ばすことにしました。","romaji":"Kikoku no yotei o isshūkan nobasu koto ni shimashita.","german":"I decided to extend my return trip by one week.","furigana":"きこく|よてい|いっしゅうかんの"},{"japanese":"締め切りを三日延ばしてもらえませんか。","romaji":"Shimekiri o mikka nobashite moraemasen ka?","german":"Could you extend the deadline by three days?","furigana":"し|き|みっかの"},{"japanese":"地下鉄を空港まで延ばす計画がある。","romaji":"Chikatetsu o kūkō made nobasu keikaku ga aru.","german":"There are plans to extend the subway to the airport.","furigana":"ちかてつ|くうこう|の|けいかく"}],"notes":"延ばす is transitive and, with time and distances, means \"to extend\" or \"to postpone\": 予定を延ばす, 締め切りを延ばす, 寿命を延ばす, 路線を延ばす. For growth and stretching out the body, it is written 伸ばす (髪を伸ばす, 手を伸ばす, 才能を伸ばす). The intransitive counterpart is 延びる; the more formal word for \"to postpone\" is 延期する.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"もうカラオケ店に入ってから何回延長しているんだろうね？","romaji":"Mō karaoketen ni haitte kara nankai enchō shite iru n darō ne?","german":"I wonder how many times we've extended since we entered the karaoke place.","furigana":"てん|はい|なんかいえんちょう"},{"japanese":"試合は同点のまま延長に入った。","romaji":"Shiai wa dōten no mama enchō ni haitta.","german":"The game went into overtime with the score tied.","furigana":"しあい|どうてん|えんちょう|はい"},{"japanese":"ビザの期限を延長する手続きをしました。","romaji":"Biza no kigen o enchō suru tetsuzuki o shimashita.","german":"I completed the procedure to extend my visa's expiration date.","furigana":"きげん|えんちょう|てつづ"}],"notes":"延長 is the \"extension\" of time or distance; as a する-verb it is mostly transitive: 期限を延長する, passive 延長される. In sports, 延長 (延長戦) is the \"overtime\" of a game. Other compounds: 延長コード (\"extension cord\"), 延長料金 (e.g. at karaoke). The opposite is 短縮; the native verb with a similar meaning is 延ばす.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女の声は小川のせせらぎのように耳にとても快い。","romaji":"Kanojo no koe wa ogawa no seseragi no yō ni mimi ni totemo kokoroyoi.","german":"Her voice is very pleasing to the ear, like the murmur of a brook.","furigana":"かのじょ|こえ|おがわ|みみ|こころよ"},{"japanese":"頼みごとをしたら、彼は快く引き受けてくれた。","romaji":"Tanomigoto o shitara, kare wa kokoroyoku hikiukete kureta.","german":"When I asked him for a favor, he gladly accepted.","furigana":"たの|かれ|こころよ|ひ|う"},{"japanese":"朝の快い風に吹かれながら、海辺を散歩した。","romaji":"Asa no kokoroyoi kaze ni fukarenagara, umibe o sanpo shita.","german":"I took a walk by the sea, with the pleasant morning breeze blowing.","furigana":"あさ|こころよ|かぜ|ふ|うみべ|さんぽ"}],"notes":"快い is an elevated い-adjective for \"pleasant, soothing\": 快い風, 快い眠り, 耳に快い. In everyday speech one rather says 気持ちいい or 心地よい. Very common is the adverb 快く (\"willingly, gladly\"): 快く引き受ける, 快く承知する. The kanji 快 is also found in 快適, 快感 and 不快.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"毛抜きで腕の毛を抜くことが快感なんだよね。","romaji":"Kenuki de ude no ke o nuku koto ga kaikan na n da yo ne.","german":"Plucking the hair on my arm with tweezers feels satisfying.","furigana":"けぬ|うで|け|ぬ|かいかん"},{"japanese":"難しい問題が解けたときの快感は、忘れられない。","romaji":"Muzukashii mondai ga toketa toki no kaikan wa, wasurerarenai.","german":"I can't forget the pleasure of solving a difficult problem.","furigana":"むずか|もんだい|と|かいかん|わす"},{"japanese":"汗を流したあとのシャワーは、本当に快感だ。","romaji":"Ase o nagashita ato no shawā wa, hontō ni kaikan da.","german":"A shower after working up a sweat is truly delightful.","furigana":"あせ|なが|ほんとう|かいかん"}],"notes":"快感 is an intense \"pleasant feeling, thrill\", physical or as satisfaction after an achievement: 快感を覚える, 快感を味わう, 〜が快感になる. Depending on context, it can also denote sensual pleasure, so be careful with its use. 快適, by contrast, means \"comfortable\" (快適な部屋), and 喜び is general \"joy\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"快速電車に乗れば、目的地まで30分で着きます。","romaji":"Kaisoku densha ni noreba, mokutekichi made sanjuppun de tsukimasu.","german":"If you take the rapid train, you'll reach your destination in 30 minutes.","furigana":"かいそくでんしゃ|の|もくてきち|ぷん|つ"},{"japanese":"次の快速は、この駅には止まりません。","romaji":"Tsugi no kaisoku wa, kono eki ni wa tomarimasen.","german":"The next rapid train does not stop at this station.","furigana":"つぎ|かいそく|えき|と"},{"japanese":"その選手は自慢の快速を生かして、一気にゴールまで走った。","romaji":"Sono senshu wa jiman no kaisoku o ikashite, ikki ni gōru made hashitta.","german":"The player made use of his prized speed and ran straight to the goal.","furigana":"せんしゅ|じまん|かいそく|い|いっき|はし"}],"notes":"快速 is, in rail transport, a \"rapid service, rapid train\" that skips smaller stations but usually costs no surcharge. Rough order: 普通/各駅停車 < 快速 < 急行 < 特急 (varying by railway company). In general, 快速 also means \"high speed, swiftness\", as in 快速船 or 快速を生かす. One says 快速に乗る, 快速電車.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この服は生地がザラザラしていて不快に感じます。","romaji":"Kono fuku wa kiji ga zarazara shite ite fukai ni kanjimasu.","german":"The fabric of these clothes is rough and feels unpleasant.","furigana":"ふく|きじ|ふかい|かん"},{"japanese":"彼の失礼な態度に、みんな不快な思いをした。","romaji":"Kare no shitsurei na taido ni, minna fukai na omoi o shita.","german":"Everyone felt uncomfortable at his rude attitude.","furigana":"かれ|しつれい|たいど|ふかい|おも"},{"japanese":"梅雨の時期は湿気が多くて不快だ。","romaji":"Tsuyu no jiki wa shikke ga ōkute fukai da.","german":"The rainy season is humid and unpleasant.","furigana":"つゆ|じき|しっけ|おお|ふかい"}],"notes":"不快 is a な-adjective/noun meaning \"unpleasant, discomfort, displeasure\": 不快な音, 不快に感じる, 不快な思いをする. It is very common in polite apologies: ご不快な思いをおかけして申し訳ございません. It is more formal than 嫌な or 気持ち悪い; the opposites are 快適 and 愉快. Note: 深い (\"deep\") is read the same way.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その女優は映画の撮影で半年もホテルぐらしをしている。","romaji":"Sono joyū wa eiga no satsuei de hantoshi mo hoterugurashi o shite iru.","german":"That actress has been living in a hotel for half a year because of a film shoot.","furigana":"じょゆう|えいが|さつえい|はんとし"},{"japanese":"館内での写真撮影は、ご遠慮ください。","romaji":"Kannai de no shashin satsuei wa, go-enryo kudasai.","german":"Please refrain from taking photographs inside the building.","furigana":"かんない|しゃしんさつえい|えんりょ"},{"japanese":"この写真は、旅行中にスマホで撮影したものです。","romaji":"Kono shashin wa, ryokōchū ni sumaho de satsuei shita mono desu.","german":"This photo was taken with a smartphone during a trip.","furigana":"しゃしん|りょこうちゅう|さつえい"}],"notes":"撮影 covers both photographing and filming, so \"shooting, filming\"; as a transitive する-verb: 写真/映画を撮影する. It is more formal than 撮る and often appears on signs: 撮影禁止 (\"no photography\"), 撮影中. Other compounds are 撮影所 (\"film studio\") and ロケ撮影 (\"location shooting\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの駅、盗撮が多いから気をつけたほうがいいよ。","romaji":"Ano eki, tōsatsu ga ōi kara ki o tsuketa hō ga ii yo.","german":"There's a lot of secret filming at that station, so you'd better be careful.","furigana":"えき|とうさつ|おお|き"},{"japanese":"男が駅の階段で盗撮して逮捕された。","romaji":"Otoko ga eki no kaidan de tōsatsu shite taiho sareta.","german":"A man was arrested for secretly filming on the station stairs.","furigana":"おとこ|えき|かいだん|とうさつ|たいほ"},{"japanese":"ホテルの部屋に盗撮用のカメラが仕掛けられていた。","romaji":"Hoteru no heya ni tōsatsu-yō no kamera ga shikakerarete ita.","german":"A camera for secret filming had been hidden in the hotel room.","furigana":"へや|とうさつよう|しか"}],"notes":"盗撮 (盗 \"steal\" + 撮 \"photograph\") is secretly photographing or filming without consent, especially in a voyeuristic way on trains, stairs or in restrooms - a criminal offense in Japan (盗撮で逮捕される). As a transitive する-verb: 〜を盗撮する; also 盗撮カメラ. More neutral is 隠し撮り \"secret shooting\" (e.g. by paparazzi); the normal word for photographing is 撮影.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自分で症状を診断するのは危険だよ。","romaji":"Jibun de shōjō o shindan suru no wa kiken da yo.","german":"It's dangerous to diagnose your own symptoms.","furigana":"じぶん|しょうじょう|しんだん|きけん"},{"japanese":"病院でインフルエンザと診断されました。","romaji":"Byōin de infuruenza to shindan saremashita.","german":"I was diagnosed with influenza at the hospital.","furigana":"びょういん|しんだん"},{"japanese":"医者は検査の結果を見てから診断するそうです。","romaji":"Isha wa kensa no kekka o mite kara shindan suru sō desu.","german":"I hear the doctor makes a diagnosis after looking at the test results.","furigana":"いしゃ|けんさ|けっか|み|しんだん"}],"notes":"診断する means \"to diagnose\": 医者が病気を診断する. Very common is the passive 〜と診断される \"it is diagnosed as ...\" (インフルエンザと診断された). 診察 is the medical examination, 診断 the judgment resulting from it; related are 診断書 \"medical certificate\" and 健康診断 \"health checkup\". Figuratively there are also 性格診断 \"personality test\" and the diagnosis of systems.","levelNote":"Derived from 診断 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"旗で合図を送る方法を学んでみたいです。","romaji":"Hata de aizu o okuru hōhō o manande mitai desu.","german":"I'd like to try learning how to send signals with flags.","furigana":"はた|あいず|おく|ほうほう|まな"},{"japanese":"運動会で、子どもたちが小さな旗を振って応援した。","romaji":"Undōkai de, kodomotachi ga chiisana hata o futte ōen shita.","german":"At the sports day, the children cheered by waving little flags.","furigana":"うんどうかい|こ|ちい|はた|ふ|おうえん"},{"japanese":"山の頂上に着いて、記念に旗を立てた。","romaji":"Yama no chōjō ni tsuite, kinen ni hata o tateta.","german":"When we reached the summit, we planted a flag as a memento.","furigana":"やま|ちょうじょう|つ|きねん|はた|た"}],"notes":"旗 is the general word for \"flag, banner\"; typical verbs are 旗を振る (\"to wave\"), 旗を立てる (\"to put up\") and 旗を揚げる (\"to hoist\"). Compounds: 国旗 (\"national flag\"), 優勝旗 (\"championship flag\"), 白旗 (\"white flag\", 白旗を上げる \"to surrender\"). The loanword フラグ is used almost only figuratively (games, programming).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本の国旗には白地に日の丸がかかれています。","romaji":"Nippon no kokki ni wa shiroji ni hinomaru ga kakarete imasu.","german":"Auf der japanischen Nationalflagge ist eine rote Sonne auf weißem Grund abgebildet.","furigana":"にっぽん|こっき|しろじ|ひ|まる"},{"japanese":"表彰式で、優勝した国の国旗が上がった。","romaji":"Hyōshōshiki de, yūshō shita kuni no kokki ga agatta.","german":"Bei der Siegerehrung wurde die Flagge des Siegerlandes gehisst.","furigana":"ひょうしょうしき|ゆうしょう|くに|こっき|あ"},{"japanese":"ドイツの国旗は、黒、赤、金の三色です。","romaji":"Doitsu no kokki wa, kuro, aka, kin no sanshoku desu.","german":"Die deutsche Nationalflagge hat drei Farben: Schwarz, Rot und Gold.","furigana":"こっき|くろ|あか|きん|さんしょく"}],"notes":"国旗 ist die „Nationalflagge“ eines Staates; typische Verbindungen sind 国旗を揚げる/掲げる („hissen“) und 国旗掲揚 („Flaggenhissung“). Japans Nationalflagge wird 日の丸 genannt, offiziell 日章旗. Zusammen mit der Flagge wird oft die Nationalhymne, 国歌, erwähnt. Für Fahnen allgemein sagt man 旗.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"スイカもカボチャも水に浮く。","romaji":"Suika mo kabocha mo mizu ni uku.","german":"Both watermelons and pumpkins float on water.","furigana":"みず|う"},{"japanese":"新しいクラスで、自分だけ浮いている気がする。","romaji":"Atarashii kurasu de, jibun dake uite iru ki ga suru.","german":"In my new class, I feel like I'm the only one who sticks out.","furigana":"あたら|じぶん|う|き"},{"japanese":"自転車で通勤すれば、交通費が浮く。","romaji":"Jitensha de tsūkin sureba, kōtsūhi ga uku.","german":"If I commute by bicycle, I save on transportation costs.","furigana":"じてんしゃ|つうきん|こうつうひ|う"}],"notes":"浮く is intransitive and means \"to float on the surface, to drift, to hover\" (水に浮く, 空中に浮く) - in contrast to 泳ぐ, active swimming; the opposite is 沈む. The transitive counterpart is 浮かべる (\"to set afloat\"); related is 浮かぶ (\"to surface, to come to mind\"). Colloquially, 浮いている means \"to be out of place, not to fit in\", and お金が浮く means \"money is saved, is left over\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"喉が渇いたから、ジュース買ってきて！","romaji":"Nodo ga kawaita kara, jūsu katte kite!","german":"I'm thirsty, so go buy me some juice!","furigana":"のど|かわ|か"},{"japanese":"暑い日は、喉が渇く前に水を飲むようにしましょう。","romaji":"Atsui hi wa, nodo ga kawaku mae ni mizu o nomu yō ni shimashō.","german":"On hot days, try to drink water before you get thirsty.","furigana":"あつ|ひ|のど|かわ|まえ|みず|の"},{"japanese":"子どもの頃から、彼はずっと親の愛に渇いていた。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro kara, kare wa zutto oya no ai ni kawaite ita.","german":"Since childhood, he has always thirsted for his parents' love.","furigana":"こ|ころ|かれ|おや|あい|かわ"}],"notes":"渇く is used almost exclusively in 喉が渇く (\"to be thirsty, to get thirsty\"); \"I'm thirsty\" is expressed with 喉が渇いた (past tense). Do not confuse it with the homophone 乾く (\"to dry\": 洗濯物が乾く). Literarily, 〜に渇く also means \"to thirst for, to long for something\" (愛に渇く). The related noun is 渇き.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"漏水の原因は古くなったパイプでした。","romaji":"Rōsui no gen'in wa furuku natta paipu deshita.","german":"The cause of the water leak was an old pipe.","furigana":"ろうすい|げんいん|ふる"},{"japanese":"天井から漏水しているので、管理会社に連絡しました。","romaji":"Tenjou kara rousui shite iru node, kanri gaisha ni renraku shimashita.","german":"Water is leaking from the ceiling, so I contacted the management company.","furigana":"てんじょう|ろうすい|かんりがいしゃ|れんらく"},{"japanese":"漏水調査のため、明日の午前中は水道が使えません。","romaji":"Rousui chousa no tame, ashita no gozenchuu wa suidou ga tsukaemasen.","german":"The water supply will be unavailable tomorrow morning due to a leak survey.","furigana":"ろうすいちょうさ|あした|ごぜんちゅう|すいどう|つか"}],"notes":"漏水 is the leaking of water from pipes, roofs or tanks - a rather technical term from building management, construction and waterworks. As an intransitive する-verb: 天井から漏水している. Typical compounds are 漏水事故 and 漏水調査. In everyday speech people usually simply say 水漏れ or 水が漏れる.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"全壊したビルからガスが漏出して危険だから近寄らないように。","romaji":"Zenkai shita biru kara gasu ga rōshutsu shite kiken da kara chikayoranai yō ni.","german":"Gas is leaking from the collapsed building and it's dangerous, so stay away.","furigana":"ぜんかい|ろうしゅつ|きけん|ちかよ"},{"japanese":"工場の事故で、有害な物質が川に漏出した。","romaji":"Koujou no jiko de, yuugai na busshitsu ga kawa ni roushutsu shita.","german":"Because of an accident at the factory, harmful substances leaked into the river.","furigana":"こうじょう|じこ|ゆうがい|ぶっしつ|かわ|ろうしゅつ"},{"japanese":"水道管から水が漏出していないか、定期的に点検しています。","romaji":"Suidoukan kara mizu ga roushutsu shite inai ka, teikiteki ni tenken shite imasu.","german":"We regularly inspect the water pipes to check whether water is leaking.","furigana":"すいどうかん|みず|ろうしゅつ|ていきてき|てんけん"}],"notes":"漏出 is a technical, literary word for the escape of gas, liquids or radiation: ガスが漏出する. In everyday speech people say 漏れる (intransitive, ガスが漏れる) or 漏らす (transitive) instead; the leak itself is 漏れ. For the leaking of information, 漏えい (漏洩) is more usual (情報漏えい).","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"えっ、有り難う。照れるなぁ。","romaji":"E, arigatō. Tereru nā.","german":"Oh, thank you. You're making me blush."},{"japanese":"彼は褒められると、すぐ照れて顔が赤くなる。","romaji":"Kare wa homerareru to, sugu terete kao ga akaku naru.","german":"When he's praised, he gets embarrassed right away and his face turns red.","furigana":"かれ|ほ|て|かお|あか"},{"japanese":"照れないで、みんなの前で歌ってみてよ。","romaji":"Terenaide, minna no mae de utatte mite yo.","german":"Don't be shy; try singing in front of everyone.","furigana":"て|まえ|うた"}],"notes":"照れる is intransitive and describes a pleasantly embarrassed shyness, typically when one is praised or noticed: 褒められて照れる; as a reaction one says 照れるなあ oneself. Related are 照れくさい \"embarrassing\", 照れ屋 \"shy person\" and 照れ笑い \"embarrassed laugh\". 恥ずかしい is broader and can also express genuine shame over mistakes; 照れる cannot.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"版権は出版社にあるの？","romaji":"Hanken wa shuppansha ni aru no?","german":"Does the publisher hold the publishing rights?","furigana":"はんけん|しゅっぱんしゃ"},{"japanese":"映画会社がこの小説の版権を買ったそうだ。","romaji":"Eiga gaisha ga kono shousetsu no hanken o katta sou da.","german":"I hear a film company bought the rights to this novel.","furigana":"えいががいしゃ|しょうせつ|はんけん|か"},{"japanese":"版権の切れた古い作品は、ネットで無料で読めます。","romaji":"Hanken no kireta furui sakuhin wa, netto de muryou de yomemasu.","german":"Old works whose copyright has expired can be read online for free.","furigana":"はんけん|き|ふる|さくひん|むりょう|よ"}],"notes":"版権 refers to the right to publish and reproduce a work, i.e. mainly publishing and licensing rights: 版権を買う, 版権を持つ, 版権が切れる. The general legal term for \"copyright\" is today 著作権; 版権 is heard more in the publishing, film and anime world (版権イラスト = picture with licensed characters).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの店、強盗が入ったらしいよ！でも、幸い誰もケガはなかったって。","romaji":"Ano mise, goutou ga haitta rashii yo! Demo, saiwai dare mo kega wa nakatta tte.","german":"I heard that shop was robbed! But luckily nobody was hurt.","furigana":"みせ|ごうとう|はい|さいわ|だれ"},{"japanese":"銀行強盗の犯人が、昨日逮捕されました。","romaji":"Ginkou goutou no hannin ga, kinou taiho saremashita.","german":"The bank robber was arrested yesterday.","furigana":"ぎんこうごうとう|はんにん|きのうたいほ"},{"japanese":"夜道で強盗に襲われないように、気をつけてね。","romaji":"Yomichi de goutou ni osowarenai you ni, ki o tsukete ne.","german":"Be careful not to be attacked by a robber on a night road.","furigana":"よみち|ごうとう|おそ|き"}],"notes":"強盗 denotes both the act (robbery by force or threat) and the perpetrator (robber). Typical: 強盗が入る (to break into a shop/house), 強盗に襲われる, 銀行強盗, 強盗事件. In contrast to 泥棒 (\"thief\", who steals secretly) and すり (\"pickpocket\"), 強盗 stresses the use of violence.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの作家の新作、なんか盗作っぽくない？","romaji":"Ano sakka no shinsaku, nanka tōsaku ppoku nai?","german":"Doesn't that author's new work seem kind of plagiarized?","furigana":"さっか|しんさく|とうさく"},{"japanese":"他人の論文を盗作したことがばれて、大学をやめることになった。","romaji":"Tanin no ronbun o tousaku shita koto ga barete, daigaku o yameru koto ni natta.","german":"He was found to have plagiarized someone else's paper and ended up leaving the university.","furigana":"たにん|ろんぶん|とうさく|だいがく"},{"japanese":"その歌は盗作ではないかと、ネットで話題になっている。","romaji":"Sono uta wa tousaku de wa nai ka to, netto de wadai ni natte iru.","german":"People online are discussing whether that song is plagiarized.","furigana":"うた|とうさく|わだい"}],"notes":"盗作 is plagiarism, that is, taking over someone else's works as one's own; as a transitive する-verb: 論文を盗作する. Common are 盗作の疑い and 〜は盗作だ. Colloquially people say パクる or パクリ; the literary synonym is 剽窃. For theft of objects, 盗む is used instead.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"アイツらは、盗品の転売をしてかせいでいるんだ。","romaji":"Aitsura wa, tōhin no tenbai o shite kaseide iru n da.","german":"Those guys make money by reselling stolen goods.","furigana":"とうひん|てんばい"},{"japanese":"警察は倉庫から大量の盗品を見つけました。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa souko kara tairyou no touhin o mitsukemashita.","german":"The police found a large quantity of stolen goods in the warehouse.","furigana":"けいさつ|そうこ|たいりょう|とうひん|み"},{"japanese":"知らずに盗品を買ってしまった場合は、どうなりますか。","romaji":"Shirazu ni touhin o katte shimatta baai wa, dou narimasu ka.","german":"What happens if I unknowingly buy stolen goods?","furigana":"し|とうひん|か|ばあい"}],"notes":"盗品 are stolen objects, that is, stolen goods - a matter-of-fact term from police and news language. Typical: 盗品を売る, 盗品を買う, 盗品が見つかる. In everyday speech people often simply say 盗まれた物. Not to be confused with 盗作 (\"plagiarism\"), in which intellectual property is stolen.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この子には、今は精神的なケアが必要だと思うんです。","romaji":"Kono ko ni wa, ima wa seishinteki na kea ga hitsuyō da to omou n desu.","german":"I think this child needs psychological care right now.","furigana":"こ|いま|せいしんてき|ひつよう|おも"},{"japanese":"仕事が忙しくて、体より精神的に疲れています。","romaji":"Shigoto ga isogashikute, karada yori seishinteki ni tsukarete imasu.","german":"Work is busy, and I'm more mentally tired than physically.","furigana":"しごと|いそが|からだ|せいしんてき|つか"},{"japanese":"その事故は、彼女にとって大きな精神的ショックでした。","romaji":"Sono jiko wa, kanojo ni totte ookina seishinteki shokku deshita.","german":"The accident was a great psychological shock for her.","furigana":"じこ|かのじょ|おお|せいしんてき"}],"notes":"精神的 is a な-adjective: 精神的な疲れ, 精神的なショック; adverbially 精神的に疲れる, 精神的に強い. It refers to the psyche and mind and is the counterpart of 肉体的 or 身体的 (\"physical\"). In conversation you also hear 気持ち的に or メンタル（が強い）; 精神的 sounds somewhat more factual.","levelNote":"Compound: 精神 (N3) + 的 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"父は精神病をわずらって入院しています。","romaji":"Chichi wa seishinbyō o wazuratte nyūin shite imasu.","german":"My father is hospitalized with a mental illness.","furigana":"ちち|せいしんびょう|にゅういん"},{"japanese":"昔は、精神病に対する偏見がとても強かった。","romaji":"Mukashi wa, seishinbyou ni taisuru henken ga totemo tsuyokatta.","german":"In the past, prejudice against mental illness was very strong.","furigana":"むかし|せいしんびょう|たい|へんけん|つよ"},{"japanese":"最近は「精神病」より「精神疾患」という言葉がよく使われます。","romaji":"Saikin wa \"seishinbyou\" yori \"seishin shikkan\" to iu kotoba ga yoku tsukawaremasu.","german":"Recently, the word \"精神疾患\" is used more often than \"精神病\".","furigana":"さいきん|せいしんびょう|せいしんしっかん|ことば|つか"}],"notes":"精神病 is an older collective term for mental illnesses, especially severe ones such as psychoses. Today the word is considered stigmatizing; in medicine and the media people rather say 精神疾患 or 心の病気, and 精神病院 has mostly become 精神科病院. Typical: 精神病を患う, 精神病と診断される.","levelNote":"Compound: 精神 (N3) + 病 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の町には韓国系アメリカ人がたくさん住んでいます。","romaji":"Watashi no machi ni wa Kankoku-kei Amerika-jin ga takusan sunde imasu.","german":"Many Korean Americans live in my town.","furigana":"わたし|まち|かんこくけい|じん|す"},{"japanese":"彼は癒し系の俳優として知られている。","romaji":"Kare wa iyashi-kei no haiyū to shite shirarete iru.","german":"He is known as a soothing-type actor.","furigana":"かれ|いや|けい|はいゆう|し"}],"notes":"〜系 is attached to an element and roughly denotes origin or affiliation: 韓国系 means \"of Korean descent\". Colloquially it also describes a type or aura, as in 癒し系 for people who have a calming effect. The sign 〜 is a placeholder and is not pronounced; 系 is the spelling of the attached element. This card does not teach an independent mathematical term.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ロサンゼルスには多くの日系アメリカ人が住んでいます。","romaji":"Rosanzerusu ni wa ōku no nikkei Amerikajin ga sunde imasu.","german":"Many Japanese Americans live in Los Angeles.","furigana":"おお|にっけい|じん|す"},{"japanese":"ブラジルには、日系人が約200万人いると言われています。","romaji":"Burajiru ni wa, nikkeijin ga yaku nihyakuman-nin iru to iwarete imasu.","german":"It is said that there are about two million people of Japanese descent in Brazil.","furigana":"にっけいじん|やく|まんにん|い"},{"japanese":"姉はタイの日系企業で働いています。","romaji":"Ane wa Tai no nikkei kigyou de hataraite imasu.","german":"My older sister works at a Japanese company in Thailand.","furigana":"あね|にっけいきぎょう|はたら"}],"notes":"日系 usually stands as a prefix before a noun: 日系アメリカ人, 日系ブラジル人 (\"of Japanese descent\"). 日系人 are people of Japanese ancestry living abroad. In addition, 日系企業 or 日系の会社 means a Japanese company or branch abroad, as opposed to 外資系 (\"foreign-affiliated company\" in Japan).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"太陽系は銀河系のどの辺りにある？","romaji":"Taiyōkei wa gingakei no dono atari ni aru?","german":"Where in the Milky Way is the solar system located?","furigana":"たいようけい|ぎんがけい|あた"},{"japanese":"太陽系で一番大きい惑星は木星です。","romaji":"Taiyoukei de ichiban ookii wakusei wa mokusei desu.","german":"The largest planet in the solar system is Jupiter.","furigana":"たいようけい|いちばんおお|わくせい|もくせい"},{"japanese":"冥王星は、今は太陽系の惑星に数えられていません。","romaji":"Meiousei wa, ima wa taiyoukei no wakusei ni kazoerarete imasen.","german":"Pluto is no longer counted as a planet of the solar system.","furigana":"めいおうせい|いま|たいようけい|わくせい|かぞ"}],"notes":"太陽系 is the solar system; 系 here means \"system, group\" as in 銀河系 (\"Milky Way\") or 生態系 (\"ecosystem\"). Typical combinations: 太陽系の惑星, 太陽系の外 or 太陽系外惑星 (\"exoplanet\"). The word belongs to school and scientific vocabulary, but is also well known in daily life.","levelNote":"Compound: 太陽 (N4) + 系 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"経済学の理論について、体系的にまとめた本を買いたいと思っています。","romaji":"Keizaigaku no riron ni tsuite, taikeiteki ni matometa hon o kaitai to omotte imasu.","german":"I'd like to buy a book that summarizes economic theory systematically.","furigana":"けいざいがく|りろん|たいけいてき|ほん|か|おも"},{"japanese":"文法を体系的に勉強したいなら、この教科書がおすすめです。","romaji":"Bunpou o taikeiteki ni benkyou shitai nara, kono kyoukasho ga osusume desu.","german":"If you want to study grammar systematically, I recommend this textbook.","furigana":"ぶんぽう|たいけいてき|べんきょう|きょうかしょ"},{"japanese":"彼の知識は広いけれど、あまり体系的ではない。","romaji":"Kare no chishiki wa hiroi keredo, amari taikeiteki de wa nai.","german":"His knowledge is wide, but it isn't very systematic.","furigana":"かれ|ちしき|ひろ|たいけいてき"}],"notes":"体系的 is a な-adjective for 体系 (\"system, ordered whole\"): 体系的な知識, 体系的に学ぶ, 体系的にまとめる. It stresses that knowledge or content is logically ordered and coherent. Similar is 系統的; 組織的, by contrast, refers more to organized action by groups (組織的な犯罪).","levelNote":"Compound: 体系 (N2) + 的 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"電気や水が絶えることなく供給されているのはとてもありがたいことです。","romaji":"Denki ya mizu ga taeru koto naku kyoukyuu sarete iru no wa totemo arigatai koto desu.","german":"It is very much appreciated that electricity and water are supplied without interruption.","furigana":"でんき|みず|た|きょうきゅう"},{"japanese":"彼女の家は、いつも笑いが絶えません。","romaji":"Kanojo no ie wa, itsumo warai ga taemasen.","german":"Laughter never stops in her house.","furigana":"かのじょ|いえ|わら|た"},{"japanese":"この地方では、この言葉を話す人が絶えてしまった。","romaji":"Kono chihou de wa, kono kotoba o hanasu hito ga taete shimatta.","german":"In this region, the people who speak this language have died out.","furigana":"ちほう|ことば|はな|ひと|た"}],"notes":"絶える is intransitive and means \"to die out, to become extinct\" (家系が絶える, 息が絶える = to die) as well as \"to cease, to break off\" (連絡が絶える). The negative is very common: 笑いが絶えない (\"there is constant laughter\"), 〜が絶えることなく, and the adverb 絶えず (\"constantly\"). The transitive counterpart is 絶やす (火を絶やさない). Not to be confused with 耐える (\"to endure\"), which is read the same.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"スポーツ大会の翌日は、体中が痛くて大変でした。","romaji":"Supootsu taikai no yokujitsu wa, karadajuu ga itakute taihen deshita.","german":"The day after the sports festival, my whole body ached terribly.","furigana":"たいかい|よくじつ|からだじゅう|いた|たいへん"},{"japanese":"ご注文の商品は、翌日にお届けします。","romaji":"Gochuumon no shouhin wa, yokujitsu ni otodoke shimasu.","german":"The goods you ordered will be delivered the next day.","furigana":"ちゅうもん|しょうひん|よくじつ|とど"},{"japanese":"彼は日本に着いた翌日から、もう仕事を始めた。","romaji":"Kare wa Nihon ni tsuita yokujitsu kara, mou shigoto o hajimeta.","german":"He started working the day after he arrived in Japan.","furigana":"かれ|にほん|つ|よくじつ|しごと|はじ"}],"notes":"翌日 means \"the following day\" and refers to a reference point in a narrative or plan: 〜の翌日, 〜た翌日. For \"tomorrow\" counted from today, you say 明日; 翌日 sounds more matter-of-fact and is common in reports, schedules and delivery terms (翌日配送). 翌朝, 翌週, 翌月 and 翌年 belong to the same series.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"4月に入社し、翌月に研修が始まった。","romaji":"Shigatsu ni nyuusha shi, yokugetsu ni kenshuu ga hajimatta.","german":"I joined the company in April, and training began the following month.","furigana":"がつ|にゅうしゃ|よくげつ|けんしゅう|はじ"},{"japanese":"カードで使った分は、翌月にまとめて支払います。","romaji":"Kaado de tsukatta bun wa, yokugetsu ni matomete shiharaimasu.","german":"What I charge to my card, I pay in one lump sum the following month.","furigana":"つか|ぶん|よくげつ|しはら"},{"japanese":"彼女は結婚した翌月に、海外へ転勤した。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kekkon shita yokugetsu ni, kaigai e tenkin shita.","german":"The month after she got married, she was transferred overseas.","furigana":"かのじょ|けっこん|よくげつ|かいがい|てんきん"}],"notes":"翌月 is \"the month after\" a stated point in time: 〜の翌月, 〜た翌月, 翌月に. From today's standpoint, by contrast, you say 来月 (\"next month\"). The word is typical in contracts and billing: 翌月払い, 翌月末. It is rarely also read よくつき.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"翌朝目が覚めると、二日酔いで頭がガンガンしていた。","romaji":"Yokuasa me ga sameru to, futsukayoi de atama ga gangan shite ita.","german":"When I woke up the next morning, I had a pounding hangover headache.","furigana":"よくあさめ|さ|ふつかよ|あたま"},{"japanese":"夜行バスで出発して、翌朝早く大阪に着いた。","romaji":"Yakou basu de shuppatsu shite, yokuasa hayaku Oosaka ni tsuita.","german":"We left on an overnight bus and arrived in Osaka early the next morning.","furigana":"やこう|しゅっぱつ|よくあさはや|おおさか|つ"},{"japanese":"お荷物は翌朝、お部屋までお届けします。","romaji":"Onimotsu wa yokuasa, oheya made otodoke shimasu.","german":"Your luggage will be delivered to your room the next morning.","furigana":"にもつ|よくあさ|へや|とど"}],"notes":"翌朝 is \"the next morning\" after an event mentioned in the narrative, and it often stands without a particle as a time adverb: 翌朝早く, 翌朝出発する. From today's standpoint you say 明日の朝 or, formally, 明朝. The reading よくちょう also occurs in formal language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日野さんはアメリカから日本に帰国した翌年に起業しました。","romaji":"Hino-san wa Amerika kara Nihon ni kikoku shita yokunen ni kigyou shimashita.","german":"Mr. Hino started a business the year after he returned to Japan from America.","furigana":"ひの|にほん|きこく|よくねん|きぎょう"},{"japanese":"一度試験に落ちたが、翌年もう一度受けて合格した。","romaji":"Ichido shiken ni ochita ga, yokunen mou ichido ukete goukaku shita.","german":"I failed the exam once, but took it again the following year and passed.","furigana":"いちどしけん|お|よくねん|いちどう|ごうかく"},{"japanese":"二人は2020年に結婚し、翌年に長男が生まれた。","romaji":"Futari wa nisen-nijuu-nen ni kekkon shi, yokunen ni chounan ga umareta.","german":"The two married in 2020, and their eldest son was born the following year.","furigana":"ふたり|ねん|けっこん|よくねん|ちょうなん|う"}],"notes":"翌年 is \"the year after\" a stated event: 〜の翌年, 〜た翌年に. From the current year, \"next year\" is 来年 instead. The word is typical in résumés, history and reports; the reading よくとし also occurs.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"覚えたい日本語の単語の一覧を作りました。","romaji":"Oboetai nihongo no tango no ichiran o tsukurimashita.","german":"I made a list of the Japanese words I want to learn.","furigana":"おぼ|にほんご|たんご|いちらん|つく"},{"japanese":"料金の一覧は、ホームページに載っています。","romaji":"Ryoukin no ichiran wa, hoomupeeji ni notte imasu.","german":"A list of the prices is on the website.","furigana":"りょうきん|いちらん|の"},{"japanese":"参加者の名前を一覧にして、メールで送ります。","romaji":"Sankasha no namae o ichiran ni shite, meeru de okurimasu.","german":"I'll put the participants' names into a list and send it by email.","furigana":"さんかしゃ|なまえ|いちらん|おく"}],"notes":"一覧 is an overview or list on which you can see everything at a glance: 一覧を作る, 一覧にする, 一覧表 (\"overview table\"). It often follows a noun: 料金一覧, 商品一覧, 参加者一覧. リスト is synonymous and a bit more casual. The basic meaning \"to take a quick look\" (一覧する) is rare and formal.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"それではショーを楽しんでご覧ください。","romaji":"Sore de wa shoo o tanoshinde goran kudasai.","german":"Please enjoy the show.","furigana":"たの|らん"},{"japanese":"社長はもう資料をご覧になりましたか。","romaji":"Shachou wa mou shiryou o goran ni narimashita ka.","german":"Has the president already looked at the materials?","furigana":"しゃちょう|しりょう|らん"},{"japanese":"ちょっと食べてごらん。おいしいよ。","romaji":"Chotto tabete goran. Oishii yo.","german":"Try tasting it. It's delicious.","furigana":"た"}],"notes":"ご覧 is the respectful form for others' seeing: ご覧になる is the 尊敬語 of 見る, ご覧ください means \"please look\", ご覧のとおり \"as you can see\". With the て-form, 〜てごらん (\"try ...\") is a friendly request to children or younger people; here it is usually written in kana. For one's own seeing, one never uses ご覧 but 拝見する.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"観覧席はもう満席になりました。","romaji":"Kanranseki wa mō manseki ni narimashita.","german":"The spectator seats are already full.","furigana":"かんらんせき|まんせき"},{"japanese":"この美術館の観覧料は、大人千円です。","romaji":"Kono bijutsukan no kanranryou wa, otona sen'en desu.","german":"The admission fee for this museum is 1,000 yen for adults.","furigana":"びじゅつかん|かんらんりょう|おとなせんえん"},{"japanese":"練習試合は、どなたでも無料で観覧できます。","romaji":"Renshuu jiai wa, donata demo muryou de kanran dekimasu.","german":"Anyone can watch the practice game for free.","furigana":"れんしゅうじあい|むりょう|かんらん"}],"notes":"観覧 is watching events, sports, exhibitions or broadcasts as a spectator; as a transitive する-verb it is rather formal: 試合を観覧する. Frequent in compounds: 観覧席 (\"stand, spectator seats\"), 観覧料 (\"admission fee\"), 観覧無料, 観覧車 (\"Ferris wheel\"). 鑑賞, by contrast, emphasizes the enjoyment of art, 見学 a visit for learning.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大人になったら、貧しい人々を助けるために活動をしたいと思っています。","romaji":"Otona ni nattara, mazushii hitobito o tasukeru tame ni katsudō o shitai to omotte imasu.","german":"When I grow up, I want to work to help poor people.","furigana":"おとな|まず|ひとびと|たす|かつどう|おも"},{"japanese":"彼は貧しい家に生まれたが、一生懸命勉強して医者になった。","romaji":"Kare wa mazushii ie ni umareta ga, isshoukenmei benkyou shite isha ni natta.","german":"He was born into a poor family, but studied hard and became a doctor.","furigana":"かれ|まず|いえ|う|いっしょうけんめいべんきょう|いしゃ"},{"japanese":"お金はあっても、心の貧しい人にはなりたくない。","romaji":"Okane wa atte mo, kokoro no mazushii hito ni wa naritakunai.","german":"Even with money, I don't want to become a person who is poor in heart.","furigana":"かね|こころ|まず|ひと"}],"notes":"貧しい is an い-adjective meaning \"poor, needy\": 貧しい家, 貧しい国, 貧しい人々. It sounds more neutral and serious than 貧乏, which is colloquial and often self-deprecating. Figuratively, 貧しい also means \"meager, lacking in something\": 心が貧しい, 想像力が貧しい. The opposite is 豊か.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"学生時代はずっと貧乏で、毎日カップラーメンばっかりだった。","romaji":"Gakusei jidai wa zutto binbō de, mainichi kappurāmen bakkari datta.","german":"When I was a student I was poor all the time and ate nothing but cup ramen every day.","furigana":"がくせいじだい|びんぼう|まいにち"},{"japanese":"貧乏な画家が、死んだ後で有名になることもある。","romaji":"Binbou na gaka ga, shinda ato de yuumei ni naru koto mo aru.","german":"Sometimes a poor painter only becomes famous after he dies.","furigana":"びんぼう|がか|し|あと|ゆうめい"},{"japanese":"貧乏暇なしで、休みの日も働いています。","romaji":"Binbou hima nashi de, yasumi no hi mo hataraite imasu.","german":"Being poor leaves no time to rest – I work even on days off.","furigana":"びんぼうひま|やす|ひ|はたら"}],"notes":"貧乏 is a noun and a な-adjective: 貧乏な学生, 貧乏で…, 貧乏生活. It is more colloquial than 貧しい and is often said about one's own situation, often self-mockingly (貧乏学生). Fixed expressions are 貧乏暇なし (\"the poor have no time to rest\") and 貧乏ゆすり (\"nervous leg jiggling\").","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 22"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しいソフトウェアの購入を検討しています。","romaji":"Atarashii sofutowea no kōnyū o kentō shite imasu.","german":"I am considering purchasing new software.","furigana":"あたら|こうにゅう|けんとう"},{"japanese":"チケットはこちらのサイトから購入できます。","romaji":"Chiketto wa kochira no saito kara kōnyū dekimasu.","german":"You can purchase tickets from this site.","furigana":"こうにゅう"},{"japanese":"家を購入する前に、よく考えたほうがいい。","romaji":"Ie o kōnyū suru mae ni, yoku kangaeta hō ga ii.","german":"You should think carefully before buying a house.","furigana":"いえ|こうにゅう|まえ|かんが"}],"notes":"購入 is the formal word for \"purchase, to buy\" and is commonly used as a transitive する-verb: 商品を購入する. You find it on websites, in shops and in contracts (購入手続き, ご購入ありがとうございます, 購入者); in conversation one usually just says 買う. 購買, by contrast, means buying as an economic activity (購買力) and 購読 subscribing to newspapers.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この購買データをもとに、新しいマーケティング戦略を立てましょう。","romaji":"Kono kōbai dēta o moto ni, atarashii māketingu senryaku o tatemashō.","german":"Let's develop a new marketing strategy based on this purchasing data.","furigana":"こうばい|あたら|せんりゃく|た"},{"japanese":"昼休みに購買でパンを買ってくるね。","romaji":"Hiruyasumi ni kōbai de pan o katte kuru ne.","german":"I'll go buy some bread at the school store during lunch break.","furigana":"ひるやす|こうばい|か"},{"japanese":"円安で、消費者の購買意欲が下がっている。","romaji":"En'yasu de, shōhisha no kōbai iyoku ga sagatte iru.","german":"Because of the weak yen, consumers' willingness to buy is falling.","furigana":"えんやす|しょうひしゃ|こうばいいよく|さ"}],"notes":"購買 refers to buying as an economic activity and appears mainly in compounds: 購買力 \"purchasing power\", 購買意欲 \"willingness to buy\", 購買データ, 購買部 \"purchasing department\". For the actual purchase of a specific item, use 購入. In schools and universities, the small shop selling bread, drinks and stationery is called 購買 for short (\"school store\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このオンラインニュースサイトの購読者数は年々増加しています。","romaji":"Kono onrain nyuusu saito no koudokushasuu wa nennen zouka shite imasu.","german":"The number of subscribers to this online news site is increasing year by year.","furigana":"こうどくしゃすう|ねんねんぞうか"},{"japanese":"父は新聞を二つ購読しています。","romaji":"Chichi wa shinbun o futatsu koudoku shite imasu.","german":"My father subscribes to two newspapers.","furigana":"ちち|しんぶん|ふた|こうどく"},{"japanese":"読む時間がないので、雑誌の定期購読をやめることにした。","romaji":"Yomu jikan ga nai node, zasshi no teiki koudoku o yameru koto ni shita.","german":"I don't have time to read, so I decided to cancel my magazine subscription.","furigana":"よ|じかん|ざっし|ていきこうどく"}],"notes":"購読 means regularly receiving newspapers, magazines or newsletters; as a transitive する-verb: 新聞を購読する. Typical compounds are 定期購読 (\"subscription\"), 購読者 (\"subscriber\") and 購読料. For subscriptions in general (streaming, etc.) people tend to say サブスク, and for YouTube channels チャンネル登録.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どうしてチョウチョを逆さまに描いたの？","romaji":"Dōshite chōcho o sakasama ni egaita no?","german":"Why did you draw the butterfly upside down?","furigana":"さか|えが"},{"japanese":"あの絵、逆さまに掛かっているよ。","romaji":"Ano e, sakasama ni kakatte iru yo.","german":"That picture is hanging upside down.","furigana":"え|さか|か"},{"japanese":"彼は足をすべらせて、階段から真っ逆さまに落ちた。","romaji":"Kare wa ashi o suberasete, kaidan kara massakasama ni ochita.","german":"He slipped and fell headfirst down the stairs.","furigana":"かれ|あし|かいだん|ま|さか|お"}],"notes":"逆さま means \"upside down, inverted\" and is a な-adjective or noun: 逆さまにする, 逆さまになる, 逆さまに置く. Intensified it is 真っ逆さま (真っ逆さまに落ちる = \"to fall headfirst\"). 逆 means \"the opposite, reverse\" in general, 裏返し \"inside out\" or \"turned over (back side up)\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この遊園地はデートスポットとして人気があります。","romaji":"Kono yuuenchi wa deeto supotto toshite ninki ga arimasu.","german":"This amusement park is popular as a date spot.","furigana":"ゆうえんち|にんき"},{"japanese":"子どもの頃、毎年夏休みに家族で遊園地に行った。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, maitoshi natsuyasumi ni kazoku de yuuenchi ni itta.","german":"When I was a child, my family went to the amusement park every summer vacation.","furigana":"こ|ころ|まいとしなつやす|かぞく|ゆうえんち|い"},{"japanese":"遊園地のジェットコースターは、怖くて乗れません。","romaji":"Yuuenchi no jetto koosutaa wa, kowakute noremasen.","german":"I'm too scared to ride the roller coaster at the amusement park.","furigana":"ゆうえんち|こわ|の"}],"notes":"遊園地 is an amusement park with rides such as ジェットコースター (\"roller coaster\") and 観覧車 (\"Ferris wheel\"). Large themed parks such as Disneyland are usually called テーマパーク; a simple 公園, by contrast, is a park without attractions. Typical: 遊園地に行く, 遊園地で遊ぶ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"適当な答えを選んでください。","romaji":"Tekitō na kotae o erande kudasai.","german":"Please choose the appropriate answer.","furigana":"てきとう|こた|えら"},{"japanese":"彼はいつも適当なことばかり言います。","romaji":"Kare wa itsumo tekitou na koto bakari iimasu.","german":"He always just says whatever.","furigana":"かれ|てきとう|い"},{"japanese":"どうぞ、適当に座ってください。","romaji":"Douzo, tekitou ni suwatte kudasai.","german":"Please, sit down wherever you like.","furigana":"てきとう|すわ"}],"notes":"適当 has two very different meanings. In a factual sense, 適当な means \"suitable, appropriate\", for example in exam questions: 適当な答えを選びなさい. In everyday speech, however, the meaning \"any old way, as one likes, carelessly\" is much more common: 適当に座って \"sit anywhere\", 適当なことを言う \"to say whatever comes to mind\", 適当な人 \"an unreliable person\". Anyone who clearly means \"appropriate\" should rather choose 適切.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人には適不適がある。","romaji":"Hito ni wa teki futeki ga aru.","german":"People have aptitudes and non-aptitudes.","furigana":"ひと|てきふてき"},{"japanese":"この土地は、米作りには不適です。","romaji":"Kono tochi wa, komezukuri ni wa futeki desu.","german":"This land is unsuitable for growing rice.","furigana":"とち|こめづく|ふてき"},{"japanese":"彼はこの仕事には不適だと判断された。","romaji":"Kare wa kono shigoto ni wa futeki da to handan sareta.","german":"He was judged unsuitable for this job.","furigana":"かれ|しごと|ふてき|はんだん"}],"notes":"不適 means \"unsuitable, inappropriate\" and is a な-adjective or noun, mostly with に: 〜に不適だ, 〜には不適な. It sounds formal and literary. In everyday speech people more often say 向いていない or 不向き, and for inappropriate behavior 不適切. The compound 適不適 means \"suitability or unsuitability\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は服飾専門学校を卒業しました。","romaji":"Watashi wa fukushoku senmon gakkō o sotsugyō shimashita.","german":"I graduated from a fashion vocational school.","furigana":"わたし|ふくしょくせんもんがっこう|そつぎょう"},{"japanese":"彼女は服飾デザイナーとして、パリで働いています。","romaji":"Kanojo wa fukushoku dezainaa toshite, Pari de hataraite imasu.","german":"She works in Paris as a fashion designer.","furigana":"かのじょ|ふくしょく|はたら"},{"japanese":"この博物館では、江戸時代の服飾の歴史を学べます。","romaji":"Kono hakubutsukan de wa, Edo jidai no fukushoku no rekishi o manabemasu.","german":"At this museum you can learn about the history of clothing and accessories in the Edo period.","furigana":"はくぶつかん|えどじだい|ふくしょく|れきし|まな"}],"notes":"服飾 covers clothing together with accessories and appears mainly in compounds from fashion and training: 服飾デザイナー, 服飾専門学校, 服飾雑貨 (\"fashion accessories\"), 服飾史. In everyday speech, for clothes you say 服, 洋服 or ファッション; 服飾 sounds technical and written.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あのトラックの電飾、すごくキラキラしててきれいだったよ！","romaji":"Ano torakku no denshoku, sugoku kirakira shitete kirei datta yo!","german":"The lights on that truck were so sparkly and pretty!","furigana":"でんしょく"},{"japanese":"12月になると、駅前の木に電飾が付けられる。","romaji":"Jūnigatsu ni naru to, ekimae no ki ni denshoku ga tsukerareru.","german":"When December comes, lights are put up on the trees in front of the station.","furigana":"がつ|えきまえ|き|でんしょく|つ"},{"japanese":"夜になると、店の電飾看板が明るく光る。","romaji":"Yoru ni naru to, mise no denshoku kanban ga akaruku hikaru.","german":"At night, the shop's illuminated signboard glows brightly.","furigana":"よる|みせ|でんしょくかんばん|あか|ひか"}],"notes":"電飾 is decorative electric lighting, for example on buildings, trees or the gaudily decorated デコトラ trucks. Typical are 電飾看板 \"illuminated sign\" and 電飾を付ける. For seasonal light displays at Christmas or in winter, イルミネーション is mostly used today; 電飾 sounds a bit more technical.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"敵の基地を攻撃する計画が進行中だ。","romaji":"Teki no kichi o kōgeki suru keikaku ga shinkōchū da.","german":"A plan to attack the enemy base is under way.","furigana":"てき|きち|こうげき|けいかく|しんこうちゅう"},{"japanese":"ネットで彼の発言を攻撃する人が多い。","romaji":"Netto de kare no hatsugen o kougeki suru hito ga ooi.","german":"Many people attack his statements online.","furigana":"かれ|はつげん|こうげき|ひと|おお"},{"japanese":"後半は、相手チームが激しく攻撃してきた。","romaji":"Kouhan wa, aite chiimu ga hageshiku kougeki shite kita.","german":"In the second half, the opposing team attacked fiercely.","furigana":"こうはん|あいて|はげ|こうげき"}],"notes":"攻撃する is transitive: 敵を攻撃する, 基地を攻撃する. Besides military attack, it is often used figuratively for sharp criticism (相手の意見を攻撃する) and, in sports, for offense (攻撃と守備). The opposite is 守る or 防御/守備. 襲う, by contrast, stresses a sudden assault on people or animals.","levelNote":"Derived from 攻撃 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あれ、先程コンビニでもお会いしましたよね？","romaji":"Are, sakihodo konbini de mo o-ai shimashita yo ne?","german":"Oh, didn't we also meet earlier at the convenience store?","furigana":"さきほど|あ"},{"japanese":"先程お電話した田中ですが、山本さんはいらっしゃいますか。","romaji":"Sakihodo o-denwa shita Tanaka desu ga, Yamamoto-san wa irasshaimasu ka.","german":"This is Tanaka, who called you earlier. Is Mr. Yamamoto in?","furigana":"さきほど|でんわ|たなか|やまもと"},{"japanese":"先程の件について、もう少し詳しく説明させてください。","romaji":"Sakihodo no ken ni tsuite, mou sukoshi kuwashiku setsumei sasete kudasai.","german":"Please let me explain the earlier matter in a bit more detail.","furigana":"さきほど|けん|すこ|くわ|せつめい"}],"notes":"先程 is the polite equivalent of さっき (\"a while ago, just now\") and is often written in kana: さきほど. It stands adverbially (先程お電話しました) or with の before nouns (先程の件). A typical polite formula: 先程は失礼しました. The counterpart for \"later\" is 後程 (のちほど).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の官僚的な話し方に、少しイラっとしました。","romaji":"Kare no kanryouteki na hanashikata ni, sukoshi iratto shimashita.","german":"His bureaucratic way of speaking irritated me a little.","furigana":"かれ|かんりょうてき|はな|かた|すこ"},{"japanese":"役所の対応があまりに官僚的で、手続きに一か月もかかった。","romaji":"Yakusho no taiou ga amari ni kanryouteki de, tetsuzuki ni ikkagetsu mo kakatta.","german":"The government office's handling was so bureaucratic that the procedure took a whole month.","furigana":"やくしょ|たいおう|かんりょうてき|てつづ|いっ|げつ"},{"japanese":"この会社は、大きくなるにつれてだんだん官僚的になってきた。","romaji":"Kono kaisha wa, ookiku naru ni tsurete dandan kanryouteki ni natte kita.","german":"As this company has grown, it has gradually become bureaucratic.","furigana":"かいしゃ|おお|かんりょうてき"}],"notes":"官僚的 is a な-adjective and almost always derogatory: rigid, formalistic, oriented toward regulations rather than people. Typical: 官僚的な対応, 官僚的な組織, 官僚的になる. It refers not only to government offices but also to large companies. The base word 官僚 refers to the (high-ranking) officials of the ministries.","levelNote":"Compound: 官僚 (N2) + 的 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ぼくのおじいちゃんは昔、総理大臣だったんだって。","romaji":"Boku no ojiichan wa mukashi, sōri daijin datta n da tte.","german":"I hear my grandfather was a prime minister long ago.","furigana":"むかし|そうりだいじん"},{"japanese":"新しい総理大臣が国会で選ばれました。","romaji":"Atarashii souri daijin ga kokkai de erabaremashita.","german":"A new prime minister was elected in the Diet.","furigana":"あたら|そうりだいじん|こっかい|えら"},{"japanese":"子どもの頃の夢は、総理大臣になることでした。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro no yume wa, souri daijin ni naru koto deshita.","german":"My childhood dream was to become prime minister.","furigana":"こ|ころ|ゆめ|そうりだいじん"}],"notes":"総理大臣 is the Japanese prime minister; the official title is 内閣総理大臣. In everyday speech and in forms of address, people often say just 総理 (山田総理), and in news and newspapers 首相 is very common. Typical: 総理大臣になる, 総理大臣に選ばれる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"来月から新しい部署に移ることになりました。","romaji":"Raigetsu kara atarashii busho ni utsuru koto ni narimashita.","german":"From next month I will be moving to a new department.","furigana":"らいげつ|あたら|ぶしょ|うつ"},{"japanese":"風邪がうつるといけないので、マスクをしています。","romaji":"Kaze ga utsuru to ikenai node, masuku o shite imasu.","german":"I am wearing a mask so that I don't pass on my cold.","furigana":"かぜ"},{"japanese":"窓側の席に移ってもいいですか。","romaji":"Madogawa no seki ni utsutte mo ii desu ka.","german":"May I move to the seat by the window?","furigana":"まどがわ|せき|うつ"}],"notes":"移る is intransitive and means \"to change to another place or state\", with the destination marked by に: 新しい部署に移る, 窓側の席に移る. The transitive counterpart is 移す \"to relocate, to move\". Illnesses and smells also \"pass on\": 風邪がうつる; in this meaning it is mostly written in kana. For moving house, 引っ越す is more usual in everyday speech.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"最後に、こんな感じで、オリーブオイルを少し垂らすんです。","romaji":"Saigo ni, konna kanji de, orību oiru o sukoshi tarasu n desu.","german":"Finally, you drizzle on a little olive oil, like this.","furigana":"さいご|かん|すこ|た"},{"japanese":"犬がよだれを垂らして、ご飯を待っている。","romaji":"Inu ga yodare o tarashite, gohan o matte iru.","german":"The dog is drooling as it waits for its food.","furigana":"いぬ|た|はん|ま"},{"japanese":"橋の上から釣り糸を垂らして、魚を待った。","romaji":"Hashi no ue kara tsuriito o tarashite, sakana o matta.","german":"I dangled a fishing line from the bridge and waited for fish.","furigana":"はし|うえ|つ|いと|た|さかな|ま"}],"notes":"垂らす is transitive and means, on the one hand, \"to let drip, to drizzle\" (油を垂らす, よだれを垂らす, 汗を垂らす), and on the other, \"to let hang down\" (釣り糸を垂らす, 髪を垂らす). The intransitive counterpart is 垂れる (水が垂れる, 枝が垂れる). For pouring larger amounts, people instead say 注ぐ or かける.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"うちの犬はカミナリを怖がるんだ。","romaji":"Uchi no inu wa kaminari o kowagaru n da.","german":"Our dog is afraid of thunder.","furigana":"いぬ|こわ"},{"japanese":"娘は暗い所を怖がって、一人で寝られない。","romaji":"Musume wa kurai tokoro o kowagatte, hitori de nerarenai.","german":"My daughter is scared of dark places and can't sleep alone.","furigana":"むすめ|くら|ところ|こわ|ひとり|ね"},{"japanese":"そんなに怖がらなくても大丈夫だよ。この犬はかまないから。","romaji":"Sonna ni kowagaranakute mo daijoubu da yo. Kono inu wa kamanai kara.","german":"You don't need to be that scared. This dog doesn't bite.","furigana":"こわ|だいじょうぶ|いぬ"}],"notes":"怖がる describes the visible fear of another person or an animal: 〜を怖がる. For one's own fear, people mostly say 怖い (私は犬が怖い), because 〜がる expresses an observation from the outside. Common are 怖がらないで and そんなに怖がらなくても大丈夫. Similar is 恐れる, which sounds more literary.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"昨夜、雪が30cmも積もり、車が埋もれてしまいました。","romaji":"Sakuya, yuki ga sanjussenchi mo tsumori, kuruma ga umorete shimaimashita.","german":"Last night 30 cm of snow piled up, and my car got buried."},{"japanese":"机の上が書類に埋もれていて、パソコンが見えない。","romaji":"Tsukue no ue ga shorui ni umorete ite, pasokon ga mienai.","german":"My desk is buried in papers, and I can't see the computer.","furigana":"つくえ|うえ|しょるい|う|み"},{"japanese":"彼のような才能が埋もれたままなのは、もったいない。","romaji":"Kare no you na sainou ga umoreta mama na no wa, mottainai.","german":"It's a waste that talent like his remains undiscovered.","furigana":"かれ|さいのう|う"}],"notes":"埋もれる is intransitive and means \"to be buried, covered\": 雪に埋もれる, 書類に埋もれる. Figuratively it means \"to remain undiscovered\": 埋もれた才能, 歴史に埋もれる. The transitive counterpart is 埋める (\"to bury, to fill\"); close in meaning is 埋まる. The variant うずもれる is more literary.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ホラー映画で、背後に何かいるパターンが一番怖い！","romaji":"Horā eiga de, haigo ni nani ka iru patān ga ichiban kowai!","german":"In horror movies, the pattern where something is standing behind you is the scariest!","furigana":"えいが|はいご|なに|いちばんこわ"},{"japanese":"背後から急に名前を呼ばれて、びっくりした。","romaji":"Haigo kara kyuu ni namae o yobarete, bikkuri shita.","german":"I was startled when someone suddenly called my name from behind.","furigana":"はいご|きゅう|なまえ|よ"},{"japanese":"この事件の背後には、大きな組織がいるらしい。","romaji":"Kono jiken no haigo ni wa, ookina soshiki ga iru rashii.","german":"Apparently there is a large organization behind this incident.","furigana":"じけん|はいご|おお|そしき"}],"notes":"背後 is a noun meaning \"the space behind someone, the back\": 背後に立つ, 背後から近づく. Figuratively it refers to what stands hidden behind something: 事件の背後, 背後関係, 背後にいる人物 (\"mastermind\"). It is more literary than 後ろ and often sounds tense or ominous. For the \"background\" of a picture, on the other hand, use 背景.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"20代のころに一人ぐらしを始めて、ジャンクフードを食べる快楽に目覚めてしまったんです。","romaji":"20dai no koro ni hitorigurashi o hajimete, janku fūdo o taberu kairaku ni mezamete shimatta n desu.","german":"When I started living alone in my twenties, I awakened to the pleasure of eating junk food.","furigana":"だい|ひとり|はじ|た|かいらく|めざ"},{"japanese":"一時の快楽のためにお金を使いすぎてはいけない。","romaji":"Ichiji no kairaku no tame ni okane o tsukaisugite wa ikenai.","german":"You shouldn't spend too much money for a moment's pleasure.","furigana":"いちじ|かいらく|かね|つか"},{"japanese":"彼は快楽ばかり求めて、仕事をまじめにしない。","romaji":"Kare wa kairaku bakari motomete, shigoto o majime ni shinai.","german":"He only seeks pleasure and doesn't take his work seriously.","furigana":"かれ|かいらく|もと|しごと"}],"notes":"快楽 is sensual pleasure, lust or enjoyment, and often carries an overtone of hedonism or excess: 快楽を求める, 快楽に溺れる \"to give oneself up to pleasure\", 快楽主義 \"hedonism\". It does not fit everyday joy or fun; for those use 楽しみ or 喜び. The word sounds rather written and serious.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"エアコンのある生活は快適だ。","romaji":"Eakon no aru seikatsu wa kaiteki da.","german":"Life with air conditioning is comfortable.","furigana":"せいかつ|かいてき"},{"japanese":"新幹線のグリーン車は、とても快適でした。","romaji":"Shinkansen no guriinsha wa, totemo kaiteki deshita.","german":"The Shinkansen Green Car was very comfortable.","furigana":"しんかんせん|しゃ|かいてき"},{"japanese":"皆様が快適に過ごせるよう、館内の温度を調整しております。","romaji":"Minasama ga kaiteki ni sugoseru you, kannai no ondo o chousei shite orimasu.","german":"We are adjusting the temperature inside the building so that everyone can be comfortable.","furigana":"みなさま|かいてき|す|かんない|おんど|ちょうせい"}],"notes":"快適 is a な-adjective meaning \"pleasant, comfortable\" with regard to surroundings and living conditions: 快適な部屋, 快適な旅, 快適に過ごす. It sounds somewhat more refined than 気持ちいい or 楽 and is typical of advertising and announcements. 便利 (\"convenient\"), by contrast, refers to usefulness, not to well-being.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"成程、そういう理由だったんですね。","romaji":"Naruhodo, sou iu riyuu datta n desu ne.","german":"I see, so that was the reason.","furigana":"なるほど|りゆう"},{"japanese":"なるほど、このボタンを押せばいいのか。","romaji":"Naruhodo, kono botan o oseba ii no ka.","german":"I see, so I just have to press this button.","furigana":"お"},{"japanese":"評判どおり、なるほどおいしい店だ。","romaji":"Hyouban doori, naruhodo oishii mise da.","german":"Just as everyone says, it really is a good restaurant.","furigana":"ひょうばん|みせ"}],"notes":"成程 is almost always written in kana: なるほど. As an interjection it shows that one has understood something and finds it convincing (\"I see, I understand\"). As an adverb it means \"indeed, truly\" (なるほどおいしい). Toward superiors, a bare なるほど can come across as somewhat condescending; more polite are そうなんですね or おっしゃるとおりです.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"俺は押しに弱いんだよなあ。","romaji":"Ore wa oshi ni yowai n da yo nā.","german":"I'm weak against pushiness.","furigana":"おれ|お|よわ"},{"japanese":"彼女は押しが強いから、営業の仕事に向いている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa oshi ga tsuyoi kara, eigyou no shigoto ni muite iru.","german":"She is very pushy, so she's suited to a sales job.","furigana":"かのじょ|お|つよ|えいぎょう|しごと|む"},{"japanese":"あと一押しで、部長も賛成してくれそうだ。","romaji":"Ato hitooshi de, buchou mo sansei shite kuresou da.","german":"With just one more push, the department head seems likely to agree too.","furigana":"ひとお|ぶちょう|さんせい"}],"notes":"押し is the noun of 押す and means \"push, thrust\", but above all, figuratively, \"assertiveness, forcefulness\". Fixed expressions: 押しが強い (\"pushy, assertive\"), 押しに弱い (\"has trouble saying no\"), あと一押し (\"just one more little push\"). Do not confuse it with 推し (\"favorite idol\" in fan language), which is read the same.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ひき肉を程よくこねます。","romaji":"Hikiniku o hodoyoku konemasu.","german":"Knead the ground meat just enough.","furigana":"にく|ほど"},{"japanese":"程よく冷えたビールが一番おいしい。","romaji":"Hodoyoku hieta biiru ga ichiban oishii.","german":"Beer that is chilled just right tastes best.","furigana":"ほど|ひ|いちばん"},{"japanese":"この部屋は程よく日が当たって、気持ちがいい。","romaji":"Kono heya wa hodoyoku hi ga atatte, kimochi ga ii.","german":"This room gets just the right amount of sunlight, and it feels pleasant.","furigana":"へや|ほど|ひ|あ|きも"}],"notes":"程よく is the adverbial form of 程よい and means \"just right, in the proper measure\" - neither too much nor too little. It is often written in kana (ほどよく) and likes to appear in recipes and descriptions: 程よく冷えた, 程よく焼けた, 程よい甘さ. Similar but more general is ちょうどよく; 適当に, by contrast, can also mean \"sloppily\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この香水は、全く匂わない。","romaji":"Kono kōsui wa, mattaku niowanai.","german":"This perfume has no scent at all.","furigana":"こうすい|まった|にお"},{"japanese":"玄関を開けると、カレーが匂ってきた。","romaji":"Genkan o akeru to, karee ga niotte kita.","german":"When I opened the front door, I could smell curry.","furigana":"げんかん|あ|にお"},{"japanese":"このシャツ、汗でちょっと匂うから洗っておいて。","romaji":"Kono shatsu, ase de chotto niou kara aratte oite.","german":"This shirt smells a bit of sweat, so please wash it.","furigana":"あせ|にお|あら"}],"notes":"匂う is intransitive: the thing that smells takes が (花が匂う, 部屋が匂う). Someone who deliberately smells something uses the transitive 嗅ぐ (匂いを嗅ぐ). For pleasant smells, people write 匂う, for unpleasant ones often 臭う (足が臭う). Figuratively, 何か匂う means \"something smells fishy\". More common than the verb in everyday speech is 〜の匂いがする.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"髪をよく濡らしてから、シャンプーをするといいですよ。","romaji":"Kami o yoku nurashite kara, shanpuu o suru to ii desu yo.","german":"It's good to wet your hair well before shampooing.","furigana":"かみ|ぬ"},{"japanese":"雨の中を歩いて、靴を濡らしてしまった。","romaji":"Ame no naka o aruite, kutsu o nurashite shimatta.","german":"I walked in the rain and got my shoes wet.","furigana":"あめ|なか|ある|くつ|ぬ"},{"japanese":"タオルを水で濡らして、額に当てた。","romaji":"Taoru o mizu de nurashite, hitai ni ateta.","german":"I wet a towel with water and held it to my forehead.","furigana":"みず|ぬ|ひたい|あ"}],"notes":"濡らす is transitive: to make something wet, either on purpose or by accident (髪を濡らす, タオルを濡らす, 靴を濡らしてしまう). The intransitive counterpart is 濡れる (雨で服が濡れる). For \"to moisten\" slightly, you can also say 湿らせる; the opposite is 乾かす (\"to dry\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"オモチャをカベに寄せた。","romaji":"Omocha o kabe ni yoseta.","german":"I moved the toy up against the wall.","furigana":"よ"},{"japanese":"車を道の左側に寄せて止めてください。","romaji":"Kuruma o michi no hidarigawa ni yosete tomete kudasai.","german":"Please pull your car over to the left side of the road and stop.","furigana":"くるま|みち|ひだりがわ|よ|と"},{"japanese":"番組には、視聴者から多くの意見が寄せられた。","romaji":"Bangumi ni wa, shichousha kara ooku no iken ga yoserareta.","german":"The program received many opinions from viewers.","furigana":"ばんぐみ|しちょうしゃ|おお|いけん|よ"}],"notes":"寄せる is transitive and in its basic sense means \"to move up close to something, to push to the side\": 〜を〜に寄せる (机を壁に寄せる, 車を左に寄せる). Figuratively it means \"to send in, to submit\" (意見を寄せる, 多くの声が寄せられる) and \"to direct feelings toward\" (関心を寄せる, 好意を寄せる). The intransitive counterpart is 寄る; 波が寄せる is a fixed intransitive use.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このお守りには、災いを退ける力がある。","romaji":"Kono omamori ni wa, wazawai o shirizokeru chikara ga aru.","german":"This amulet has the power to ward off misfortune.","furigana":"まも|わざわ|しりぞ|ちから"},{"japanese":"会社は、組合の要求を退けた。","romaji":"Kaisha wa, kumiai no youkyuu o shirizoketa.","german":"The company rejected the union's demands.","furigana":"かいしゃ|くみあい|ようきゅう|しりぞ"},{"japanese":"決勝で去年の優勝チームを退けて、初めて優勝した。","romaji":"Kesshou de kyonen no yuushou chiimu o shirizokete, hajimete yuushou shita.","german":"In the final they beat last year's champions and won the title for the first time.","furigana":"けっしょう|きょねん|ゆうしょう|しりぞ|はじ|ゆうしょう"}],"notes":"退ける (しりぞける) is transitive and formal: \"to reject, turn down\" (要求を退ける, 訴えを退ける) and \"to repel, defeat\" (敵を退ける, 相手チームを退ける). The intransitive counterpart is 退く (しりぞく, \"to withdraw\"). Note: the same kanji is also read どける/のける, and then means \"to move aside\" in colloquial speech.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ゲームのやりすぎは目を痛める。","romaji":"Gēmu no yarisugi wa me o itameru.","german":"Playing too many video games is bad for your eyes.","furigana":"め|いた"},{"japanese":"重い荷物を持ち上げて、腰を痛めてしまった。","romaji":"Omoi nimotsu o mochiagete, koshi o itamete shimatta.","german":"I hurt my lower back lifting a heavy package.","furigana":"おも|にもつ|も|あ|こし|いた"},{"japanese":"母は兄のことで、ずっと心を痛めている。","romaji":"Haha wa ani no koto de, zutto kokoro o itamete iru.","german":"My mother has been very worried about my brother for a long time.","furigana":"はは|あに|こころ|いた"}],"notes":"痛める is transitive: to injure a body part or harm it (腰を痛める, 喉を痛める, 目を痛める); 心を痛める means \"to be deeply worried, distressed\". The intransitive counterpart is 痛む (\"to hurt\"). Read the same are 傷める (\"to damage\", e.g. 髪を傷める) and 炒める (\"to stir-fry\") - mind the spelling.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"そこに鉄柵をめぐらすのはどうでしょう。","romaji":"Soko ni tessaku o megurasu no wa dō deshō.","german":"How about putting up an iron fence there?","furigana":"てっさく"},{"japanese":"公園の池の周りには鉄柵があります。","romaji":"Kouen no ike no mawari ni wa tessaku ga arimasu.","german":"There is an iron fence around the pond in the park.","furigana":"こうえん|いけ|まわ|てっさく"},{"japanese":"鉄柵を乗り越えて線路に入るのは、とても危険です。","romaji":"Tessaku o norikoete senro ni hairu no wa, totemo kiken desu.","german":"Climbing over the iron fence and going onto the tracks is very dangerous.","furigana":"てっさく|の|こ|せんろ|はい|きけん"}],"notes":"鉄柵 is an iron grille or iron fence, for example around parks, railway tracks or buildings. 柵 on its own is a general fence or barrier; in everyday speech people often say フェンス. Typical verbs: 鉄柵を設ける, 鉄柵で囲む, 鉄柵を乗り越える. The word is rather literary and technical.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"電気柵を張りめぐらせる畑が増えている。","romaji":"Denkisaku o harimeguraseru hatake ga fuete iru.","german":"More and more fields are being surrounded with electric fences.","furigana":"でんきさく|は|はたけ|ふ"},{"japanese":"イノシシの被害を防ぐため、畑に電気柵を設置しました。","romaji":"Inoshishi no higai o fusegu tame, hatake ni denkisaku o secchi shimashita.","german":"To prevent damage from wild boars, we installed an electric fence around the field.","furigana":"ひがい|ふせ|はたけ|でんきさく|せっち"},{"japanese":"電気柵に触ると危ないので、注意してください。","romaji":"Denkisaku ni sawaru to abunai node, chuui shite kudasai.","german":"It's dangerous to touch the electric fence, so please be careful.","furigana":"でんきさく|さわ|あぶ|ちゅうい"}],"notes":"電気柵 is an electric fence, as used in Japan mainly in rural areas against wild boars, deer and monkeys. Typical: 電気柵を設置する, 電気柵を張る, 電気柵で囲む. Here 柵 is read さく; the reading しがらみ applies only to the standalone word (\"ties, obligations\").","levelNote":"Compound: 電気 (N5) + 柵 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"我が家の今日のディナーはエビフライです。","romaji":"Wagaya no kyō no dinā wa ebi furai desu.","german":"Tonight's dinner at our house is fried shrimp.","furigana":"わ|や|きょう"},{"japanese":"我が社の新製品をご紹介します。","romaji":"Waga sha no shinseihin o go-shōkai shimasu.","german":"Let me introduce our company's new product.","furigana":"わ|しゃ|しんせいひん|しょうかい"},{"japanese":"我が子の成長を見るのは本当にうれしい。","romaji":"Waga ko no seichō o miru no wa hontō ni ureshii.","german":"It's truly delightful to watch my child grow.","furigana":"わ|こ|せいちょう|み|ほんとう"}],"notes":"我が is a prenominal word meaning \"my, our\" that stands only directly before a noun. In everyday life it survives mainly in set phrases: 我が家 \"our home\", 我が子 \"one's own child\"; formally in speeches 我が社 \"our company\" and in politics and media 我が国 \"our country\". Freely formed, it sounds elevated or jokingly solemn; normally you say 私の or うちの.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ここは豚骨ラーメンの店です。","romaji":"Koko wa tonkotsu rāmen no mise desu.","german":"This is a tonkotsu ramen shop.","furigana":"とんこつ|みせ"},{"japanese":"博多に行ったら、本場の豚骨ラーメンを食べてみたい。","romaji":"Hakata ni ittara, honba no tonkotsu rāmen o tabete mitai.","german":"If I go to Hakata, I want to try authentic tonkotsu ramen.","furigana":"|い|ほんば|とんこつ|た"},{"japanese":"豚骨ラーメンはスープが濃いので、全部飲むとのどが渇く。","romaji":"Tonkotsu rāmen wa sūpu ga koi node, zenbu nomu to nodo ga kawaku.","german":"Tonkotsu ramen has a rich soup, so if you drink all of it you get thirsty.","furigana":"とんこつ|こ|ぜんぶの|かわ"}],"notes":"豚骨ラーメン (often also written とんこつラーメン) is ramen with a cloudy, whitish broth made from long-simmered pork bones (豚骨). It comes from 九州, especially 博多 and 久留米, and is usually served with thin, firm noodles; extra noodles for a refill are called 替え玉. Other basic types are 醤油ラーメン, 味噌ラーメン and 塩ラーメン.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"なんか体の節々がズキズキする。","romaji":"Nanka karada no fushibushi ga zukizuki suru.","german":"Somehow my joints are throbbing all over.","furigana":"からだ|ふしぶし"},{"japanese":"熱があって、体の節々が痛い。インフルエンザかもしれない。","romaji":"Netsu ga atte, karada no fushibushi ga itai. Infuruenza kamo shirenai.","german":"I have a fever and all my joints hurt. It might be the flu.","furigana":"ねつ|からだ|ふしぶし|いた"},{"japanese":"彼の言葉の節々に、家族への感謝が感じられた。","romaji":"Kare no kotoba no fushibushi ni, kazoku e no kansha ga kanjirareta.","german":"In his every word, I could feel his gratitude toward his family.","furigana":"かれ|ことば|ふしぶし|かぞく|かんしゃ|かん"}],"notes":"節々 are the joints of the whole body; almost always in 体の節々が痛い or 節々が痛む, typical of flu or sore muscles (\"aching limbs\"). Figuratively, 言葉の節々に means \"in many individual points (of a speech)\". Individual joints, by contrast, are called 関節.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このホテル、朝食バイキング付きで五千円だって。","romaji":"Kono hoteru, chōshoku baikingutsuki de go sen'en datte.","german":"I hear this hotel is 5,000 yen with a breakfast buffet included.","furigana":"ちょうしょく|つ|ごせんえん"},{"japanese":"ランチバイキングは、90分食べ放題です。","romaji":"Ranchi baikingu wa, kyuujuppun tabehoudai desu.","german":"The lunch buffet is all-you-can-eat for 90 minutes.","furigana":"ぷんた|ほうだい"},{"japanese":"バイキングだからって、取りすぎないようにね。","romaji":"Baikingu da kara tte, torisuginai you ni ne.","german":"Just because it's a buffet, don't take too much.","furigana":"と"}],"notes":"バイキング in Japan means above all \"self-service buffet\" (朝食バイキング, ランチバイキング) - a Japanese coinage based on a hotel restaurant. The original meaning \"Viking\" exists as well. Synonymous but more upscale is ビュッフェ; 食べ放題 stresses \"eat as much as you like\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"しまった、会議の書類を揃えるのを忘れてた。","romaji":"Shimatta, kaigi no shorui o soroeru no o wasureteta.","german":"Oh no, I forgot to collect the documents for the meeting.","furigana":"かいぎ|しょるい|そろ|わす"},{"japanese":"玄関で脱いだ靴は、きちんと揃えなさい。","romaji":"Genkan de nuida kutsu wa, kichinto soroenasai.","german":"Line up the shoes you took off in the entryway properly.","furigana":"げんかん|ぬ|くつ|そろ"},{"japanese":"みんなで声を揃えて「おめでとう！」と言った。","romaji":"Minna de koe o soroete \"omedetou!\" to itta.","german":"Everyone said \"Congratulations!\" in unison.","furigana":"こえ|そろ|い"}],"notes":"揃える is transitive and has three core meanings: \"to gather, to make complete\" (書類を揃える, 道具を揃える), \"to line up neatly\" (靴を揃える) and \"to make uniform, to match\" (声を揃える, 長さを揃える, 足並みを揃える). The intransitive counterpart is 揃う (全員が揃う).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"業務用の鉄板を一枚注文した。","romaji":"Gyōmuyō no teppan o ichimai chūmon shita.","german":"I ordered one commercial-grade griddle.","furigana":"ぎょうむよう|てっぱん|いちまいちゅうもん"},{"japanese":"鉄板の上で、お好み焼きを焼きました。","romaji":"Teppan no ue de, okonomiyaki o yakimashita.","german":"I cooked okonomiyaki on the griddle.","furigana":"てっぱん|うえ|この|や|や"},{"japanese":"この話は、絶対に笑いが取れる鉄板ネタだ。","romaji":"Kono hanashi wa, zettai ni warai ga toreru teppan neta da.","german":"This story is a sure-fire joke that will definitely get laughs.","furigana":"はなし|ぜったい|わら|と|てっぱん"}],"notes":"鉄板 is an iron or steel plate, in everyday life above all the hot griddle for お好み焼き or 鉄板焼き. Colloquially it means \"a sure thing, guaranteed to succeed\": 鉄板ネタ (\"a joke that always works\"), この組み合わせは鉄板だ. The phrase 鉄板の上で is typical in cooking.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"僕にとって、あなたは黒い海を照らしてくれる月のような存在です。","romaji":"Boku ni totte, anata wa kuroi umi o terashite kureru tsuki no you na sonzai desu.","german":"To me, you are like the moon that lights up the black sea.","furigana":"ぼく|くろ|うみ|て|つき|そんざい"},{"japanese":"懐中電灯で足元を照らしながら歩いた。","romaji":"Kaichuu dentou de ashimoto o terashinagara aruita.","german":"I walked while lighting the ground at my feet with a flashlight.","furigana":"かいちゅうでんとう|あしもと|て|ある"},{"japanese":"規則に照らして考えると、この行為は問題がある。","romaji":"Kisoku ni terashite kangaeru to, kono koui wa mondai ga aru.","german":"Considered in light of the rules, this act is problematic.","furigana":"きそく|て|かんが|こうい|もんだい"}],"notes":"照らす is transitive: to cast light on something (月が海を照らす, ライトで足元を照らす). The intransitive counterpart is 照る (日が照る). In the set form 〜に照らして it means \"in the light of, measured against\": 法律に照らして, 経験に照らして - a literary expression.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"航空券を予約しました。","romaji":"Kōkūken o yoyaku shimashita.","german":"I booked the plane ticket.","furigana":"こうくうけん|よやく"},{"japanese":"年末は、航空券の値段がかなり高くなる。","romaji":"Nenmatsu wa, koukuuken no nedan ga kanari takaku naru.","german":"Around the end of the year, plane ticket prices get quite high.","furigana":"ねんまつ|こうくうけん|ねだん|たか"},{"japanese":"安い航空券を探して、ネットで何時間も比べた。","romaji":"Yasui koukuuken o sagashite, netto de nanjikan mo kurabeta.","german":"I searched for a cheap plane ticket and compared options online for hours.","furigana":"やす|こうくうけん|さが|なんじかん|くら"}],"notes":"航空券 is the plane ticket that you buy or book: 航空券を予約する, 航空券を取る, 格安航空券, 往復航空券. In conversation people often just say チケット. The boarding pass you receive at check-in, by contrast, is 搭乗券. For train tickets use 切符 or 乗車券.","levelNote":"Compound: 航空 (N3) + 券 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鼻詰まりがひどくて眠れません。","romaji":"Hanazumari ga hidokute nemuremasen.","german":"My stuffy nose is so bad that I can't sleep.","furigana":"はなづ|ねむ"},{"japanese":"風邪で鼻詰まりがひどく、においが分からない。","romaji":"Kaze de hanazumari ga hidoku, nioi ga wakaranai.","german":"I have a terrible stuffy nose from a cold and can't smell anything.","furigana":"かぜ|はなづ|わ"},{"japanese":"鼻詰まりに効く薬はありますか。","romaji":"Hanazumari ni kiku kusuri wa arimasu ka.","german":"Is there any medicine that works for a stuffy nose?","furigana":"はなづ|き|くすり"}],"notes":"鼻詰まり (鼻 \"nose\" + 詰まる \"to be blocked\") is a stuffy nose as a complaint: 鼻詰まりがひどい, 鼻詰まりに効く薬. In conversation the condition is often described with the verb: 鼻が詰まっている. A runny nose, by contrast, is 鼻水が出る.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"失敗のことを一人で思い詰めて、ますます不安になってしまったんです。","romaji":"Shippai no koto o hitori de omoitsumete, masumasu fuan ni natte shimatta n desu.","german":"I brooded over my failure alone, and it only made me more anxious.","furigana":"しっぱい|ひとり|おも|つ|ふあん"},{"japanese":"そんなに思い詰めないで、誰かに相談してみたら？","romaji":"Sonna ni omoitsumenaide, dareka ni soudan shite mitara?","german":"Don't dwell on it so much; why not talk to someone about it?","furigana":"おも|つ|だれ|そうだん"},{"japanese":"彼は思い詰めた顔で「話がある」と言った。","romaji":"Kare wa omoitsumeta kao de hanashi ga aru to itta.","german":"He said \"I have something to tell you\" with a troubled look.","furigana":"かれ|おも|つ|かお|はなし|い"}],"notes":"思い詰める means to brood so long and one-sidedly over a matter that one sees no way out - stronger than 考える \"to think\" or 悩む \"to worry\". Typical are 一人で思い詰める, consolingly そんなに思い詰めないで and 思い詰めた顔 \"a grimly troubled expression\". 思い詰めて〜する often means taking a drastic step out of desperation. An object with を is possible, but the verb usually stands without one.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"好きな人をこっそり見詰めるのって、青春っぽいよね。","romaji":"Suki na hito o kossori mitsumeru no tte, seishun ppoi yo ne.","german":"Secretly gazing at the person you like feels so youthful, doesn't it?","furigana":"す|ひと|みつ|せいしゅん"},{"japanese":"赤ちゃんがじっと私の顔を見詰めている。","romaji":"Akachan ga jitto watashi no kao o mitsumete iru.","german":"The baby is staring fixedly at my face.","furigana":"あか|わたし|かお|みつ"},{"japanese":"自分の弱さをしっかり見詰めることも大切だ。","romaji":"Jibun no yowasa o shikkari mitsumeru koto mo taisetsu da.","german":"It is also important to look squarely at your own weaknesses.","furigana":"じぶん|よわ|みつ|たいせつ"}],"notes":"見詰める means to direct one's gaze at something long and intently: 〜をじっと見詰める. Unlike 見る \"to see\" or 眺める \"to look at (leisurely)\", it stresses intensity, but is not necessarily impolite - disapproving staring is more like じろじろ見る. Figuratively: 現実を見詰める \"to face reality\". In practice the word is usually written 見つめる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その雄犬は子犬のころから元気いっぱいだった。","romaji":"Sono osuinu wa koinu no koro kara genki ippai datta.","german":"That male dog was full of energy even as a puppy.","furigana":"おすいぬ|こいぬ|げんき"},{"japanese":"うちの犬は雄犬で、名前はタロウです。","romaji":"Uchi no inu wa osuinu de, namae wa Tarō desu.","german":"Our dog is a male, and his name is Taro.","furigana":"いぬ|おすいぬ|なまえ"},{"japanese":"雄犬は散歩中によく電柱にマーキングする。","romaji":"Osuinu wa sanpochū ni yoku denchū ni mākingu suru.","german":"Male dogs often mark utility poles during walks.","furigana":"おすいぬ|さんぽちゅう|でんちゅう"}],"notes":"雄犬 is a male dog; 雄 (オス) marks the male sex in animals, while for humans you say 男. The counterpart is 雌犬 \"female dog\", which, however, also occurs as an insult for women. In conversation you more often hear オスの犬 or simply うちの犬はオスです.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女はいつも笑顔を絶やすことがなかった。","romaji":"Kanojo wa itsumo egao o tayasu koto ga nakatta.","german":"She never let her smile fade.","furigana":"かのじょ|えがお|た"},{"japanese":"キャンプでは一晩中火を絶やさないようにした。","romaji":"Kyanpu de wa hitobanjuu hi o tayasanai you ni shita.","german":"While camping, we made sure not to let the fire go out all night.","furigana":"ひとばんじゅうひ|た"},{"japanese":"ゴキブリを完全に絶やすのは難しい。","romaji":"Gokiburi o kanzen ni tayasu no wa muzukashii.","german":"It is difficult to exterminate cockroaches completely.","furigana":"かんぜん|た|むずか"}],"notes":"絶やす is the transitive counterpart of 絶える \"to cease, to die out\". Very often used in the negative: 笑顔を絶やさない \"to always keep a smile\", 火を絶やさない \"to not let the fire go out\", 在庫を絶やさない \"to never run out of stock\". In the affirmative it means \"to eradicate, to put an end to\" (害虫を絶やす), intensified 根絶やしにする. Not to be confused with 耐える \"to endure\" (the same reading たえる as 絶える).","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"試合前に緊張するのはよくあることです。","romaji":"Shiai mae ni kinchō suru no wa yoku aru koto desu.","german":"It's common to be nervous before a match.","furigana":"しあいまえ|きんちょう"},{"japanese":"面接でとても緊張して、うまく話せなかった。","romaji":"Mensetsu de totemo kinchou shite, umaku hanasenakatta.","german":"I was so nervous at the interview that I couldn't speak well.","furigana":"めんせつ|きんちょう|はな"},{"japanese":"両国の関係が緊張している。","romaji":"Ryoukoku no kankei ga kinchou shite iru.","german":"Relations between the two countries are strained.","furigana":"りょうこく|かんけい|きんちょう"}],"notes":"緊張する describes inner tension before exams, speeches or job interviews: 面接で緊張する, 緊張して手が震える. It is intransitive; the situation takes で or comes before as a time expression, not with を. The opposite is リラックスする or 緊張がほぐれる \"the tension eases\"; colloquially, あがる is also used for stage fright. Relations between countries can also be \"tense\": 関係が緊張する.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 12"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ペットとしばらく離れるのがつらい。","romaji":"Petto to shibaraku hanareru no ga tsurai.","german":"It is hard to be apart from my pet for a while.","furigana":"はな"},{"japanese":"大学に入って、親元を離れて一人暮らしを始めた。","romaji":"Daigaku ni haitte, oyamoto o hanarete hitorigurashi o hajimeta.","german":"When I started university, I left my parents' home and began living alone.","furigana":"だいがく|はい|おやもと|はな|ひとりぐ|はじ"},{"japanese":"私のアパートは駅から少し離れています。","romaji":"Watashi no apaato wa eki kara sukoshi hanarete imasu.","german":"My apartment is a little way from the station.","furigana":"わたし|えき|すこ|はな"}],"notes":"離れる is intransitive and emphasizes distance or separation: the starting point takes から or を (駅から離れる, 親元を離れる \"to leave one's parents' home\"), the person one is separated from takes と (家族と離れて暮らす). As an attributive, 離れた describes a distant location: 駅から少し離れた所. The transitive counterpart is 離す (手を離す \"to let go\"). In contrast to 出る \"to go out\", the focus is on the distance that arises.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 23"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これ、壊れ物だから、気をつけてね！","romaji":"Kore, kowaremono da kara, ki o tsukete ne!","german":"This is fragile, so be careful!","furigana":"こわ|もの|き"},{"japanese":"荷物に「壊れ物注意」のシールを貼った。","romaji":"Nimotsu ni kowaremono chuui no shiiru o hatta.","german":"I put a \"Fragile\" sticker on the package.","furigana":"にもつ|こわ|ものちゅうい|は"},{"japanese":"郵便局で「中に壊れ物はありますか」と聞かれた。","romaji":"Yuubinkyoku de naka ni kowaremono wa arimasu ka to kikareta.","german":"At the post office, I was asked, \"Is there anything fragile inside?\"","furigana":"ゆうびんきょく|なか|こわ|もの|き"}],"notes":"壊れ物 refers to fragile objects such as glass, tableware or electronics, especially in shipping: 壊れ物注意 \"Caution, fragile\" is on parcels, and at the post office counter you are asked whether there is any 壊れ物 inside. Formed from 壊れる \"to break\" + 物 \"thing\". It can also mean an already broken thing, but that is rare. As an adjective, \"fragile\" is 壊れやすい.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"既に支払いは完了しています。","romaji":"Sudeni shiharai wa kanryō shite imasu.","german":"Payment has already been completed.","furigana":"すで|しはら|かんりょう"},{"japanese":"駅に着いたときには、電車は既に出ていた。","romaji":"Eki ni tsuita toki ni wa, densha wa sudeni dete ita.","german":"By the time I reached the station, the train had already left.","furigana":"えき|つ|でんしゃ|すで|で"},{"japanese":"既にご存じの方も多いと思いますが、来月から料金が変わります。","romaji":"Sudeni gozonji no kata mo ooi to omoimasu ga, raigetsu kara ryoukin ga kawarimasu.","german":"Many of you probably already know, but the fees will change from next month.","furigana":"すで|ぞん|かた|おお|おも|らいげつ|りょうきん|か"}],"notes":"既に corresponds to もう \"already\", but is more written and formal: typical of news, business emails and announcements, while in casual speech you say もう. Unlike もう, it cannot mean \"another\" (もう一つ) or \"soon\" (もうすぐ). It is often used with 〜ている/〜ていた for a state that has already come about, and in the formula 既にご存じのとおり \"as you already know\". According to dictionaries, it is often written in kana as すでに.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どれでもいいから、既存のファイルを開いてみてください。","romaji":"Dore demo ii kara, kison no fairu o hiraite mite kudasai.","german":"It doesn't matter which, please try opening an existing file.","furigana":"きそん|ひら"},{"japanese":"新しいアプリでも既存のデータをそのまま使えます。","romaji":"Atarashii apuri demo kison no dēta o sono mama tsukaemasu.","german":"Even with the new app, you can use your existing data as is.","furigana":"あたら|きそん|つか"},{"japanese":"新規のお客様だけでなく、既存のお客様も大切にしましょう。","romaji":"Shinki no okyakusama dake de naku, kison no okyakusama mo taisetsu ni shimashō.","german":"Let's value not only new customers but also existing customers.","furigana":"しんき|きゃくさま|きそん|きゃくさま|たいせつ"}],"notes":"既存 means \"already existing, present\" and almost always stands attributively with の: 既存のファイル, 既存のシステム, 既存の店舗. It is typical of business, technology and administration; the opposite is 新規 \"new\" (新規のお客様 vs. 既存のお客様 \"existing customers\"). Besides きそん, the reading きぞん is also common; in conversation people often simply say 今ある.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この問題は既決です。","romaji":"Kono mondai wa kiketsu desu.","german":"This matter has already been decided.","furigana":"もんだい|きけつ"},{"japanese":"既決の事項について、もう一度話し合う必要はない。","romaji":"Kiketsu no jikou ni tsuite, mou ichido hanashiau hitsuyou wa nai.","german":"There is no need to discuss matters that have already been decided again.","furigana":"きけつ|じこう|いちどはな|あ|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"書類を既決と未決の箱に分けた。","romaji":"Shorui o kiketsu to miketsu no hako ni waketa.","german":"I sorted the documents into the \"decided\" box and the \"pending\" box.","furigana":"しょるい|きけつ|みけつ|はこ|わ"}],"notes":"既決 is a formal official and legal word for \"already decided, settled\". It is a noun and takes の before other nouns (既決の事項 \"matters already decided\"), and it forms a pair with 未決 \"undecided, pending\" - for example on office filing trays. In criminal law it means \"convicted with final effect\" (既決囚). In everyday speech you say もう決まっている instead.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"普段、週末はどんな風に過ごしているの？","romaji":"Fudan, shūmatsu wa donna kaze ni sugoshite iru no?","german":"What do you usually do on weekends?","furigana":"ふだん|しゅうまつ|かぜ|す"},{"japanese":"普段はバスで通勤していますが、今日は歩いてきました。","romaji":"Fudan wa basu de tsuukin shite imasu ga, kyou wa aruite kimashita.","german":"I usually commute by bus, but today I walked.","furigana":"ふだん|つうきん|きょう|ある"},{"japanese":"パーティーは普段着のままで大丈夫ですよ。","romaji":"Paatii wa fudangi no mama de daijoubu desu yo.","german":"The party is fine with everyday clothes.","furigana":"ふだんぎ|だいじょうぶ"}],"notes":"普段 refers to normal everyday life as opposed to special occasions. As an adverb it is mostly 普段は \"usually\", often with a contrast: 普段は…が、今日は…. Attributively it takes の (普段の生活 \"everyday life\"), and it also appears in 普段着 \"everyday clothes\" and 普段通り \"as usual\". Compared with いつも \"always\", 普段 stresses the normal case, which has exceptions. Occasionally it is written in kana as ふだん.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この本の編者は誰ですか？","romaji":"Kono hon no hensha wa dare desu ka?","german":"Who is the editor of this book?","furigana":"ほん|へんしゃ|だれ"},{"japanese":"この辞書の編者は、二十年かけて言葉を集めた。","romaji":"Kono jisho no hensha wa, nijuunen kakete kotoba o atsumeta.","german":"The editor of this dictionary spent twenty years collecting words.","furigana":"じしょ|へんしゃ|にじゅうねん|ことば|あつ"},{"japanese":"表紙には、著者ではなく編者の名前が書いてある。","romaji":"Hyoushi ni wa, chosha de wa naku hensha no namae ga kaite aru.","german":"The cover shows the name of the editor, not the author.","furigana":"ひょうし|ちょしゃ|へんしゃ|なまえ|か"}],"notes":"編者 is the person who compiles texts by others into a book - for example dictionaries, anthologies or collected volumes; the title page then often has 〜編 \"edited by\". Distinguish it from 著者 \"author\" and from 編集者 \"editor\" as a job title at a publishing house. The word is rather literary; less often it is also read へんじゃ.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"昨日の外務大臣の国連でのスピーチ、見た？","romaji":"Kinō no gaimu daijin no kokuren de no supīchi, mita?","german":"Did you see the foreign minister's speech at the UN yesterday?","furigana":"きのう|がいむだいじん|こくれん|み"},{"japanese":"外務大臣は来週アメリカを訪問する予定です。","romaji":"Gaimu daijin wa raishuu Amerika o houmon suru yotei desu.","german":"The foreign minister plans to visit the United States next week.","furigana":"がいむだいじん|らいしゅう|ほうもん|よてい"},{"japanese":"両国の外務大臣が会談を行った。","romaji":"Ryoukoku no gaimu daijin ga kaidan o okonatta.","german":"The foreign ministers of the two countries held talks.","furigana":"りょうこく|がいむだいじん|かいだん|おこな"}],"notes":"外務大臣 is the head of the Ministry of Foreign Affairs 外務省. In headlines and news it is often shortened to 外相; with a name it is, for example, 山田外務大臣. Other ministerial posts follow the same pattern: 財務大臣 \"finance minister\", 総理大臣 \"prime minister\". The word belongs to the neutral to formal language of politics and news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"雨だし、地下街行かない？","romaji":"Ame da shi, chikagai ikanai?","german":"It's raining, so shall we go to the underground mall?","furigana":"あめ|ちかがいい"},{"japanese":"大阪駅の地下街は広くて、よく道に迷う。","romaji":"Oosaka eki no chikagai wa hirokute, yoku michi ni mayou.","german":"The underground mall at Osaka Station is huge, and I often get lost.","furigana":"おおさかえき|ちかがい|ひろ|みち|まよ"},{"japanese":"地下街のレストランで待ち合わせしましょう。","romaji":"Chikagai no resutoran de machiawase shimashou.","german":"Let's meet at a restaurant in the underground mall.","furigana":"ちかがい|ま|あ"}],"notes":"地下街 (地下 \"underground\" + 街 \"street, district\") is an underground shopping street with shops, restaurants and connecting passages, typically beneath large train stations in Osaka, Nagoya, Tokyo or Sapporo. You say 地下街に行く or 地下街を通る. A pure pedestrian tunnel, by contrast, is called 地下道, and the food section in the basement of a department store デパ地下.","levelNote":"Compound: 地下 (N5) + 街 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼を除く全員が会議に参加した。","romaji":"Kare o nozoku zen'in ga kaigi ni sanka shita.","german":"Everyone except him took part in the meeting.","furigana":"かれ|のぞ|ぜんいん|かいぎ|さんか"},{"japanese":"この店は日曜日を除いて、毎日開いています。","romaji":"Kono mise wa nichiyoubi o nozoite, mainichi aite imasu.","german":"This shop is open every day except Sundays.","furigana":"みせ|にちようび|のぞ|まいにちあ"},{"japanese":"種を除いてから、細かく切ってください。","romaji":"Tane o nozoite kara, komakaku kitte kudasai.","german":"Please remove the seeds and then cut it into small pieces.","furigana":"たね|のぞ|こま|き"}],"notes":"除く has two main meanings: \"to exclude, to except\" - very common as 〜を除いて/〜を除き \"except, with the exception of\" in opening hours and rules - and \"to remove, to get rid of\" (種を除く), for which 取り除く is often used in everyday speech. 〜を除いて is more formal than 〜以外. Do not confuse it with the identically read 覗く \"to peek in\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"除外理由を教えてもらえませんか？","romaji":"Jogai riyū o oshiete moraemasen ka?","german":"Could you tell me the reason for the exclusion?","furigana":"じょがいりゆう|おし"},{"japanese":"条件に合わないデータは、分析から除外しました。","romaji":"Jouken ni awanai deeta wa, bunseki kara jogai shimashita.","german":"We excluded data that did not meet the conditions from the analysis.","furigana":"じょうけん|あ|ぶんせき|じょがい"},{"japanese":"けがのため、彼は代表メンバーから除外された。","romaji":"Kega no tame, kare wa daihyou menbaa kara jogai sareta.","german":"Because of an injury, he was dropped from the national team.","furigana":"かれ|だいひょう|じょがい"}],"notes":"除外 is the formal Sino-Japanese noun for 除く \"to exclude\": 〜を…から除外する \"to exclude X from Y\", passive 除外される. Typical in rules, statistics and contracts: 対象から除外する, 除外理由, 除外対象. In everyday speech 〜を除く or 外す is usually enough. The opposite is 含める \"to include\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これは彼に直接尋ねるのが一番早いと思います。","romaji":"Kore wa kare ni chokusetsu tazuneru no ga ichiban hayai to omoimasu.","german":"I think the quickest way is to ask him directly.","furigana":"かれ|ちょくせつたず|いちばんはや|おも"},{"japanese":"駅で知らない人に道を尋ねられました。","romaji":"Eki de shiranai hito ni michi o tazuneraremashita.","german":"At the station a stranger asked me the way.","furigana":"えき|し|ひと|みち|たず"},{"japanese":"ちょっとお尋ねしますが、この近くに郵便局はありますか。","romaji":"Chotto otazune shimasu ga, kono chikaku ni yuubinkyoku wa arimasu ka.","german":"Excuse me, may I ask whether there is a post office near here?","furigana":"たず|ちか|ゆうびんきょく"}],"notes":"尋ねる means \"to ask, to inquire\" and is somewhat more formal than 聞く. The person asked takes に, the thing asked about takes を: 駅員に道を尋ねる. お尋ねします very politely introduces a question. The secondary meaning \"to search\" survives in 尋ね人 \"missing person being sought\". Read the same but different is 訪ねる \"to visit\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"警察は関係者全員を尋問しました。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa kankeisha zen'in o jinmon shimashita.","german":"The police interrogated everyone involved.","furigana":"けいさつ|かんけいしゃぜんいん|じんもん"},{"japanese":"証人への尋問は午後から行われる。","romaji":"Shounin e no jinmon wa gogo kara okonawareru.","german":"The questioning of the witness will take place from the afternoon.","furigana":"しょうにん|じんもん|ごご|おこな"},{"japanese":"彼は何時間も厳しい尋問を受けた。","romaji":"Kare wa nanjikan mo kibishii jinmon o uketa.","german":"He underwent harsh interrogation for hours.","furigana":"かれ|なんじかん|きび|じんもん|う"}],"notes":"尋問 is the formal word for questioning by the police, a court or the military: 容疑者を尋問する, 尋問を受ける \"to be interrogated\"; in court one speaks of 証人尋問 \"examination of witnesses\". Questioning after an arrest is usually called 取り調べ, and a stop-and-question check on the street 職務質問. Do not use it for ordinary questions in daily life - use 質問する or 聞く for that. Older spelling: 訊問.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これはお幾らですか？","romaji":"Kore wa oikura desu ka?","german":"How much is this?","furigana":"いく"},{"japanese":"いくら説明しても、彼は納得しません。","romaji":"Ikura setsumei shite mo, kare wa nattoku shimasen.","german":"No matter how much I explain, he won't be convinced.","furigana":"せつめい|かれ|なっとく"}],"notes":"いくら asks for an amount, especially the price; おいくらですか is a polite question. In いくら～ても it means \"no matter how much / even if ...\". It is usually written in hiragana. The identically pronounced イクラ means salmon roe and is a separate word.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この箱にりんごが幾つ入っていますか。","romaji":"Kono hako ni ringo ga ikutsu haitte imasu ka.","german":"How many apples are in this box?","furigana":"はこ|いく|はい"},{"japanese":"お子さんはお幾つですか。","romaji":"Okosan wa oikutsu desu ka.","german":"How old is your child?","furigana":"こ|いく"}],"notes":"いくつ asks for the number of things counted with the general counter ～つ. It also asks for age: おいくつですか is more polite than 何歳ですか. For people or special counters, you use, for example, 何人 or 何枚. The kana spelling is usual; the kanji spelling 幾つ is read the same way.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"幾何学のテストでAプラス取ったの？すごいじゃん、よくやったね！","romaji":"Kikagaku no tesuto de A purasu totta no? Sugoi jan, yoku yatta ne!","german":"You got an A+ on the geometry test? That's great, well done!","furigana":"きかがく|と"},{"japanese":"このスカーフは幾何学模様がおしゃれだ。","romaji":"Kono sukāfu wa kikagaku moyō ga oshare da.","german":"The geometric pattern on this scarf is stylish.","furigana":"きかがくもよう"},{"japanese":"古代ギリシャで幾何学が大きく発展した。","romaji":"Kodai Girisha de kikagaku ga ōkiku hatten shita.","german":"Geometry developed greatly in ancient Greece.","furigana":"こだい|きかがく|おお|はってん"}],"notes":"幾何学 is geometry as a branch of mathematics, alongside 代数 \"algebra\"; in school lessons at lower levels, 図形 is used more often. In everyday life the word appears mainly in 幾何学模様 \"geometric pattern\" and 幾何学的 \"geometric\", for example in fashion, architecture or design. 幾何 alone can also mean \"geometry\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は自分の画廊を開いた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa jibun no garō o hiraita.","german":"She opened her own gallery.","furigana":"かのじょ|じぶん|がろう|ひら"},{"japanese":"銀座には小さな画廊がたくさんある。","romaji":"Ginza ni wa chiisana garou ga takusan aru.","german":"There are many small galleries in Ginza.","furigana":"ぎんざ|ちい|がろう"},{"japanese":"画廊で気に入った絵を一枚買った。","romaji":"Garou de ki ni itta e o ichimai katta.","german":"I bought a painting I liked at the gallery.","furigana":"がろう|き|い|え|いちまいか"}],"notes":"画廊 is an art gallery, usually commercial, where pictures are exhibited and sold. Today ギャラリー is also often used; a large public art museum, by contrast, is called 美術館. Typical are 画廊を開く \"to open a gallery\" and 画廊で個展を開く \"to hold a solo exhibition in a gallery\". The word sounds a bit more refined than ギャラリー.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その川の水はすっかり泥水に変わってしまった。","romaji":"Sono kawa no mizu wa sukkari doromizu ni kawatte shimatta.","german":"The water in that river has turned completely into muddy water.","furigana":"かわ|みず|どろみず|か"},{"japanese":"車に泥水をかけられて、服が汚れた。","romaji":"Kuruma ni doromizu o kakerarete, fuku ga yogoreta.","german":"A car splashed me with muddy water and my clothes got dirty.","furigana":"くるま|どろみず|ふく|よご"},{"japanese":"子どもたちは泥水の中で楽しそうに遊んでいた。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa doromizu no naka de tanoshisou ni asonde ita.","german":"The children were playing happily in the muddy puddles.","furigana":"こ|どろみず|なか|たの|あそ"}],"notes":"泥水 is cloudy, muddy water, for example after rain, in floods or in puddles. Typical combinations are 泥水をかけられる \"to be splashed with muddy water\" and 川が泥水になる. Figuratively, 泥水をすする \"to slurp muddy water\" stands for a life of poverty and hardship. The dictionary meaning \"pleasure district\" is obsolete and unimportant for learners.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の演技には、皆が驚嘆した。","romaji":"Kare no engi ni wa, mina ga kyōtan shita.","german":"Everyone marveled at his performance.","furigana":"かれ|えんぎ|みな|きょうたん"},{"japanese":"その職人の技術には驚嘆するばかりだ。","romaji":"Sono shokunin no gijutsu ni wa kyoutan suru bakari da.","german":"I can only marvel at that craftsman's skill.","furigana":"しょくにん|ぎじゅつ|きょうたん"},{"japanese":"彼の記憶力は驚嘆に値する。","romaji":"Kare no kiokuryoku wa kyoutan ni atai suru.","german":"His memory is worthy of admiration.","furigana":"かれ|きおくりょく|きょうたん|あたい"}],"notes":"驚嘆 combines 驚く \"to be surprised\" with 嘆 \"to sigh\" and describes admiring amazement at something great - never fright. Construction: 〜に驚嘆する \"to marvel at something\"; set phrases are 驚嘆に値する \"admirable\" and 驚嘆の声を上げる. The word is literary; in conversation people say びっくりする or すごいと思う instead. Related is 感嘆 \"admiration\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"結果を見て、彼は深く嘆息した。","romaji":"Kekka o mite, kare wa fukaku tansoku shita.","german":"He sighed deeply when he saw the result.","furigana":"けっか|み|かれ|ふか|たんそく"},{"japanese":"政治の現状に嘆息する人は少なくない。","romaji":"Seiji no genjou ni tansoku suru hito wa sukunaku nai.","german":"Not a few people sigh over the current state of politics.","furigana":"せいじ|げんじょう|たんそく|ひと|すく"},{"japanese":"「もう間に合わない」と、部長は嘆息まじりに言った。","romaji":"Mou maniawanai to, buchou wa tansoku majiri ni itta.","german":"\"We can't make it in time anymore,\" the department head said with a sigh.","furigana":"ま|あ|ぶちょう|たんそく|い"}],"notes":"嘆息 is a literary word for a sigh of grief, disappointment or resignation and can also mean \"to lament\". As a verb it is 嘆息する; set phrases are 嘆息を漏らす and 嘆息まじりに \"with a sigh\". In everyday speech you say ため息をつく - 嘆息をつく is not a common combination. Related is 嘆く \"to grieve, to lament\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"感嘆符は強い感情を表すときに使います。","romaji":"Kantanfu wa tsuyoi kanjou o arawasu toki ni tsukaimasu.","german":"The exclamation mark is used to express strong emotion.","furigana":"かんたんふ|つよ|かんじょう|あらわ|つか"},{"japanese":"ビジネスメールでは、感嘆符はあまり使わないほうがいい。","romaji":"Bijinesu meeru de wa, kantanfu wa amari tsukawanai hou ga ii.","german":"In business emails, it's better not to use exclamation marks much.","furigana":"かんたんふ|つか"},{"japanese":"彼女のメッセージは感嘆符だらけだった。","romaji":"Kanojo no messeeji wa kantanfu darake datta.","german":"Her message was full of exclamation marks.","furigana":"かのじょ|かんたんふ"}],"notes":"感嘆符 (感嘆 \"exclamation, admiration\" + 符 \"mark\") is the technical term for the exclamation mark ！. In daily life people mostly say エクスクラメーションマーク or, colloquially, ビックリマーク. The question mark is accordingly called 疑問符, colloquially はてなマーク. In formal Japanese texts, both are traditionally used sparingly.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"倉で見つけた地図を広げてみた。","romaji":"Kura de mitsuketa chizu o hirogete mita.","german":"I spread out the map I had found in the storehouse.","furigana":"くら|み|ちず|ひろ"},{"japanese":"祖父の家には古い倉があって、昔の道具がしまってある。","romaji":"Sofu no ie ni wa furui kura ga atte, mukashi no dougu ga shimatte aru.","german":"There is an old storehouse at my grandfather's house where old tools are kept.","furigana":"そふ|いえ|ふる|くら|むかし|どうぐ"},{"japanese":"秋に収穫した米を倉に入れておく。","romaji":"Aki ni shuukaku shita kome o kura ni irete oku.","german":"The rice harvested in autumn is stored in the storehouse.","furigana":"あき|しゅうかく|こめ|くら|い"}],"notes":"倉 is a traditional storehouse, especially for grain and goods. 蔵, which has the same reading くら, tends to mean a solid storehouse with thick clay walls and appears in 酒蔵 \"sake brewery\"; in everyday use the two are often used similarly. Modern warehouses are called 倉庫. Typical: 倉にしまう \"to keep in the storehouse\", 米を倉に入れる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は宮家の子孫です。","romaji":"Kare wa miyake no shison desu.","german":"He is a descendant of an imperial branch family.","furigana":"かれ|みやけ|しそん"},{"japanese":"未来の子孫のために、森を守りたい。","romaji":"Mirai no shison no tame ni, mori o mamoritai.","german":"I want to protect the forest for our future descendants.","furigana":"みらい|しそん|もり|まも"},{"japanese":"生き物は子孫を残すために、さまざまな工夫をしている。","romaji":"Ikimono wa shison o nokosu tame ni, samazama na kufuu o shite iru.","german":"Living things employ various strategies in order to leave descendants.","furigana":"い|もの|しそん|のこ|くふう"}],"notes":"子孫 covers all descendants across generations - children, grandchildren and beyond - and is therefore broader than 子供. Typical are 〜の子孫 \"descendant of\", 子孫を残す \"to leave descendants\" (also in biology) and 子孫に伝える \"to pass on to posterity\". The opposite is 先祖 or 祖先 \"ancestors\". The word is neutral, rather literary.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ついに子供達の巣立ちの日がやってきてしまい、ちょっとさびしいです。","romaji":"Tsuini kodomotachi no sudachi no hi ga yatte kite shimai, chotto sabishii desu.","german":"The day has finally come for the children to leave the nest, and I feel a little sad.","furigana":"こどもたち|すだ|ひ"},{"japanese":"初夏には、ツバメのひなが巣立ちの時期を迎える。","romaji":"Shoka ni wa, tsubame no hina ga sudachi no jiki o mukaeru.","german":"In early summer, the swallow chicks reach the time to fledge.","furigana":"しょか|すだ|じき|むか"},{"japanese":"卒業は社会への巣立ちだ。","romaji":"Sotsugyou wa shakai e no sudachi da.","german":"Graduation is the step out into society.","furigana":"そつぎょう|しゃかい|すだ"}],"notes":"巣立ち is the noun of 巣立つ: literally young birds leaving the nest (ひなの巣立ち), figuratively the step into independence - children move out, students graduate. Common in graduation speeches, greeting cards and parents' reports: 子供の巣立ち, 社会への巣立ち, 巣立ちの日. It sounds warm and somewhat literary; it means \"fledging\", not the biological \"precocial\" behavior of leaving the nest right after birth.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ハチの巣を見つけても、危ないので近づかないでください。","romaji":"Hachi no su o mitsukete mo, abunai node chikazukanaide kudasai.","german":"Even if you find a bee's nest, it's dangerous, so please don't go near it.","furigana":"す|み|あぶ|ちか"},{"japanese":"軒下に大きなハチの巣ができていて、業者に取ってもらった。","romaji":"Nokishita ni ookina hachi no su ga dekite ite, gyousha ni totte moratta.","german":"A big wasp nest had formed under the eaves, and we had a pest control company remove it.","furigana":"のきした|おお|す|ぎょうしゃ|と"},{"japanese":"発表のあと、会場はハチの巣をつついたような騒ぎになった。","romaji":"Happyou no ato, kaijou wa hachi no su o tsutsuita you na sawagi ni natta.","german":"After the announcement, the venue erupted in an uproar as if someone had poked a hornet's nest.","furigana":"はっぴょう|かいじょう|す|さわ"}],"notes":"ハチ (蜂) covers bees and wasps, so ハチの巣 is, depending on context, a beehive or honeycomb or - very often in everyday life - a wasp or hornet nest (スズメバチの巣). It is usually written with katakana ハチ, also 蜂の巣. Idioms: ハチの巣をつついたような騒ぎ \"an uproar like a stirred-up wasp nest\" and ハチの巣にする \"to riddle with holes\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あ、クモの巣ができてる！","romaji":"A, kumo no su ga dekiteru!","german":"Oh, a spider web has formed!","furigana":"す"},{"japanese":"長い間使っていない部屋は、クモの巣だらけだった。","romaji":"Nagai aida tsukatte inai heya wa, kumo no su darake datta.","german":"The room that hadn't been used for a long time was full of cobwebs.","furigana":"なが|あいだつか|へや|す"},{"japanese":"朝露にぬれたクモの巣がきらきら光っていた。","romaji":"Asatsuyu ni nureta kumo no su ga kirakira hikatte ita.","german":"A spider web wet with morning dew was glittering.","furigana":"あさつゆ|す|ひか"}],"notes":"クモの巣 covers both the elaborate spider web in a garden and cobwebs in a dusty corner: クモの巣が張る/できる \"a web forms\", クモの巣を払う \"to brush away cobwebs\", クモの巣だらけ \"full of cobwebs\", クモの巣にかかる \"to get caught in the web\". Usually written with katakana クモ, also 蜘蛛の巣. Figuratively, クモの巣のように describes a dense network, for example of roads.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"立入禁止の危険地帯には、絶対に入らないでください。","romaji":"Tachiiri kinshi no kiken chitai ni wa, zettai ni hairanaide kudasai.","german":"Never enter the off-limits danger zone.","furigana":"たちいりきんし|きけんちたい|ぜったい|はい"},{"japanese":"日本は地震の多い地帯にある。","romaji":"Nihon wa jishin no ooi chitai ni aru.","german":"Japan lies in an earthquake-prone zone.","furigana":"にほん|じしん|おお|ちたい"},{"japanese":"この辺りは工業地帯なので、夜でもトラックが多い。","romaji":"Kono atari wa kougyou chitai na node, yoru demo torakku ga ooi.","german":"This area is an industrial zone, so there are many trucks even at night.","furigana":"あた|こうぎょうちたい|よる|おお"}],"notes":"地帯 denotes a larger area marked by a common characteristic and usually stands after another noun: 工業地帯 \"industrial zone\", 危険地帯 \"danger zone\", 安全地帯 \"safety zone, traffic island\", 山岳地帯 \"mountain region\". With a modifier it can also stand alone: 地震の多い地帯. Unlike 地域 \"area, region\" (general, also administrative) or 地区 \"district\", 地帯 emphasizes the extent as a zone or belt.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この円の直径は10センチです。","romaji":"Kono en no chokkei wa juu senchi desu.","german":"The diameter of this circle is 10 centimeters.","furigana":"えん|ちょっけい"},{"japanese":"道路に直径3メートルほどの穴が開いていた。","romaji":"Douro ni chokkei san meetoru hodo no ana ga aite ita.","german":"There was a hole about 3 meters in diameter in the road.","furigana":"どうろ|ちょっけい|あな|あ"},{"japanese":"このピザは直径30センチもある。","romaji":"Kono piza wa chokkei sanjussenchi mo aru.","german":"This pizza is as much as 30 centimeters across.","furigana":"ちょっけい"}],"notes":"直径 is the diameter; half of it is 半径 \"radius\", and the circumference is 円周. Statements follow the pattern 〜の直径は〇センチです or 直径〇センチの〜 (直径5ミリのねじ). The word is used in mathematics as well as in everyday life - for pizzas, plates, tires or holes.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この円の半径は10センチです。","romaji":"Kono en no hankei wa juu senchi desu.","german":"The radius of this circle is 10 centimeters.","furigana":"えん|はんけい"},{"japanese":"家から半径1キロ以内にコンビニが五軒もある。","romaji":"Ie kara hankei ichi kiro inai ni konbini ga goken mo aru.","german":"There are as many as five convenience stores within a 1 km radius of my home.","furigana":"いえ|はんけい|いない|ごけん"},{"japanese":"子どもが生まれてから、行動半径がずいぶん狭くなった。","romaji":"Kodomo ga umarete kara, koudou hankei ga zuibun semaku natta.","german":"Since my child was born, my range of movement has become much narrower.","furigana":"こ|う|こうどうはんけい|せま"}],"notes":"半径 is the radius, i.e. half of 直径 \"diameter\". Besides geometry (円の半径) it is very common in location statements: 半径〇キロ以内 \"within a radius of ... km\", for example for delivery services or restricted zones. Figuratively there is 行動半径 \"range of action, range of movement\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼らは人々を救うために、毎日働いています。","romaji":"Karera wa hitobito o sukuu tame ni, mainichi hataraite imasu.","german":"They work every day to save people.","furigana":"かれ|ひとびと|すく|まいにちはたら"},{"japanese":"医者は、事故にあった子どもの命を救った。","romaji":"Isha wa, jiko ni atta kodomo no inochi o sukutta.","german":"The doctor saved the life of the child who had been in an accident.","furigana":"いしゃ|じこ|こ|いのち|すく"},{"japanese":"落ち込んでいたとき、友達の一言に救われた。","romaji":"Ochikonde ita toki, tomodachi no hitokoto ni sukuwareta.","german":"When I was feeling down, a few words from a friend saved me.","furigana":"お|こ|ともだち|ひとこと|すく"}],"notes":"救う means \"to rescue, to free from distress\": 命を救う, 人を危険から救う, 被災者を救う. The passive 〜に救われる \"to be saved by ..., to find comfort through ...\" is very common. Compared with 助ける \"to help, to rescue\", which is more everyday and broader, 救う sounds more serious - it concerns great danger, hardship or emotional suffering. Do not confuse it with the identically read 掬う \"to scoop\" (water, goldfish).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これは麦粉をこねて作ったものです。","romaji":"Kore wa mugiko o konete tsukutta mono desu.","german":"This was made by kneading wheat flour.","furigana":"むぎこ|つく"},{"japanese":"この地方には、麦粉を使った料理が多い。","romaji":"Kono chihou ni wa, mugiko o tsukatta ryouri ga ooi.","german":"There are many dishes using wheat flour in this region.","furigana":"ちほう|むぎこ|つか|りょうり|おお"},{"japanese":"麦粉が服に付いて、真っ白になった。","romaji":"Mugiko ga fuku ni tsuite, masshiro ni natta.","german":"Wheat flour got on my clothes and I turned all white.","furigana":"むぎこ|ふく|つ|ま|しろ"}],"notes":"麦粉 is flour made from 麦 \"grain (wheat, barley)\", usually wheat flour. In present-day everyday life, on packaging and in recipes, people almost always say 小麦粉; 麦粉 sounds somewhat old-fashioned or regional. \"Flour, powder\" in general is 粉; barley flour is, more precisely, 大麦粉.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これはクレヨンを粉状にしたものです。","romaji":"Kore wa kureyon o funjou ni shita mono desu.","german":"This is crayon turned into powder.","furigana":"ふんじょう"},{"japanese":"この薬は粉状なので、水と一緒に飲んでください。","romaji":"Kono kusuri wa funjou na node, mizu to issho ni nonde kudasai.","german":"This medicine is a powder, so please take it with water.","furigana":"くすり|ふんじょう|みず|いっしょ|の"},{"japanese":"粉状の洗剤より、液体のほうが使いやすい。","romaji":"Funjou no senzai yori, ekitai no hou ga tsukaiyasui.","german":"Liquid is easier to use than powdered detergent.","furigana":"ふんじょう|せんざい|えきたい|つか"}],"notes":"粉状 means \"in powder form, powdery\" and is read ふんじょう or こなじょう. It stands as a noun with の (粉状の薬, 粉状の洗剤) or with に (粉状にする \"to pulverize\"). The word is rather technical and written (product descriptions, chemistry, medicine); in conversation people mostly say 粉の〜 or 粉末の〜. The kanji 状 forms many such shape words: 液状 \"liquid\", 球状 \"spherical\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は菜食主義者です。","romaji":"Kanojo wa saishoku shugisha desu.","german":"She is a vegetarian.","furigana":"かのじょ|さいしょくしゅぎしゃ"},{"japanese":"健康のために、週に二日は菜食にしています。","romaji":"Kenkō no tame ni, shū ni futsuka wa saishoku ni shite imasu.","german":"For my health, I eat vegetarian two days a week.","furigana":"けんこう|しゅう|ふつか|さいしょく"},{"japanese":"このお寺では、菜食の精進料理を食べることができる。","romaji":"Kono otera de wa, saishoku no shōjin ryōri o taberu koto ga dekiru.","german":"At this temple you can eat vegetarian shojin cuisine.","furigana":"てら|さいしょく|しょうじんりょうり|た"}],"notes":"菜食 is a plant-based diet, a diet without meat. It is most common in 菜食主義 \"vegetarianism\" and 菜食主義者 \"vegetarian\", also 菜食にする \"to eat vegetarian\" or 菜食中心の食事. As a する-verb it is rare. In everyday life today ベジタリアン and ヴィーガン are more common; the opposite is 肉食.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"貨物はトラックで運ばれた。","romaji":"Kamotsu wa torakku de hakobareta.","german":"The cargo was carried by truck.","furigana":"かもつ|はこ"},{"japanese":"夜中に長い貨物列車が通った。","romaji":"Yonaka ni nagai kamotsu ressha ga tootta.","german":"A long freight train passed in the middle of the night.","furigana":"よなか|なが|かもつれっしゃ|とお"},{"japanese":"この船は人ではなく貨物を運んでいます。","romaji":"Kono fune wa hito de wa naku kamotsu o hakonde imasu.","german":"This ship carries cargo, not people.","furigana":"ふね|ひと|かもつ|はこ"}],"notes":"貨物 is freight or cargo transported by rail, ship, truck or plane, often in compounds: 貨物列車 \"freight train\", 貨物船 \"cargo ship\", 航空貨物 \"air freight\". The opposite is 旅客 \"passengers\". For private luggage or parcels you say 荷物; 貨物 is the business and technical term for goods in transport.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その陸軍の記録に、なぜか私の名前が記されていたんです。","romaji":"Sono rikugun no kiroku ni, naze ka watashi no namae ga shirusarete ita n desu.","german":"For some reason, my name was recorded in that army's records.","furigana":"りくぐん|きろく|わたし|なまえ|しる"},{"japanese":"祖父は戦争中、陸軍の兵士だった。","romaji":"Sofu wa sensouchuu, rikugun no heishi datta.","german":"My grandfather was an army soldier during the war.","furigana":"そふ|せんそうちゅう|りくぐん|へいし"},{"japanese":"陸軍、海軍、空軍をまとめて三軍と言う。","romaji":"Rikugun, kaigun, kuugun o matomete sangun to iu.","german":"The army, navy and air force together are called the three services.","furigana":"りくぐん|かいぐん|くうぐん|さんぐん|い"}],"notes":"陸軍 is the army, i.e. the land forces, as opposed to 海軍 \"navy\" and 空軍 \"air force\"; the armed forces as a whole are called 軍隊 or 軍. For Japan it is used mainly historically (旧日本陸軍 until 1945); the present-day equivalent is called 陸上自衛隊. For other countries one says, for example, アメリカ陸軍.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"そのレストランの食事の平均価格は1500円です。","romaji":"Sono resutoran no shokuji no heikin kakaku wa sen gohyaku en desu.","german":"The average price of a meal at that restaurant is 1,500 yen.","furigana":"しょくじ|へいきんかかく|えん"},{"japanese":"テストの点数が平均より少し高かった。","romaji":"Tesuto no tensuu ga heikin yori sukoshi takakatta.","german":"My test score was slightly higher than average.","furigana":"てんすう|へいきん|すこ|たか"},{"japanese":"平均すると、一日六時間ぐらい寝ています。","romaji":"Heikin suru to, ichinichi rokujikan gurai nete imasu.","german":"On average, I sleep about six hours a day.","furigana":"へいきん|いちにちろくじかん|ね"}],"notes":"平均 is the average: 平均を出す \"to calculate the average\", 平均より上/下, 平均点 \"average score\", 平均気温, 平均価格. As a する-verb it is very common in 平均すると or 平均して \"on average\". The second meaning \"balance\" survives mainly in 平均台 \"balance beam\". Do not confuse it with 普通 \"normal, ordinary\", which is not meant statistically.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大きさがなるべく均等になるように切ってください。","romaji":"Ōkisa ga narubeku kintō ni naru yō ni kitte kudasai.","german":"Please cut it so that the sizes are as even as possible.","furigana":"おお|きんとう|き"},{"japanese":"利益はメンバー全員で均等に分けた。","romaji":"Rieki wa menbaa zen'in de kintou ni waketa.","german":"The profits were divided equally among all the members.","furigana":"りえき|ぜんいん|きんとう|わ"},{"japanese":"男女の均等な待遇が求められている。","romaji":"Danjo no kintou na taiguu ga motomerarete iru.","german":"Equal treatment of men and women is being demanded.","furigana":"だんじょ|きんとう|たいぐう|もと"}],"notes":"均等 means \"evenly distributed, equal\" - every part gets the same amount. As a な-adjective: 均等に分ける \"to divide into equal parts\", 均等な機会 \"equal opportunity\"; known from the law 男女雇用機会均等法. Unlike 平等 \"equality (of rights, treatment)\", 均等 stresses quantity and distribution; 平均, by contrast, is the statistical average.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女はとても均整のとれた体形をしている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa totemo kinsei no toreta taikei o shite iru.","german":"She has a very well-proportioned figure.","furigana":"かのじょ|きんせい|たいけい"},{"japanese":"この建物は左右の均整がとても美しい。","romaji":"Kono tatemono wa sayuu no kinsei ga totemo utsukushii.","german":"The left-right symmetry of this building is very beautiful.","furigana":"たてもの|さゆう|きんせい|うつく"},{"japanese":"均整のとれた字を書くのは難しい。","romaji":"Kinsei no toreta ji o kaku no wa muzukashii.","german":"It is difficult to write well-balanced characters.","furigana":"きんせい|じ|か|むずか"}],"notes":"均整 denotes a harmonious relationship of the parts to one another, i.e. symmetry and balance. It almost always appears in the phrase 均整のとれた〜 \"well-proportioned\" (body, figure, building, face) or 均整がとれている. Do not confuse it with 均等 \"evenly distributed\"; in everyday speech similar things are often expressed with バランスがいい. The word is descriptive and somewhat elevated.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"墓地に入るとき、なんとなく息を止めてしまうんです。","romaji":"Bochi ni hairu toki, nantonaku iki o tomete shimau n desu.","german":"When I enter a cemetery, I somehow end up holding my breath.","furigana":"ぼち|はい|いき|と"},{"japanese":"お盆には家族で墓地へお墓参りに行きます。","romaji":"Obon ni wa kazoku de bochi e ohakamairi ni ikimasu.","german":"At Obon, our family goes to the cemetery to visit the graves.","furigana":"ぼん|かぞく|ぼち|はかまい|い"},{"japanese":"町の外れに古い墓地がある。","romaji":"Machi no hazure ni furui bochi ga aru.","german":"There is an old cemetery on the edge of town.","furigana":"まち|はず|ふる|ぼち"}],"notes":"墓地 is a cemetery as a place with many graves; a single grave is (お)墓, and a grave visit is お墓参り. Large, park-like grounds are often called 霊園, and many cemeteries lie on temple grounds. In conversation people often simply say お墓 instead of 墓地; 墓地 sounds more matter-of-fact.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本一高い山は富士山です。","romaji":"Nippon'ichi takai yama wa Fujisan desu.","german":"The tallest mountain in Japan is Mt. Fuji.","furigana":"にっぽんいちたか|やま|ふじさん"},{"japanese":"晴れた日には、ここから富士山が見えます。","romaji":"Hareta hi ni wa, koko kara Fujisan ga miemasu.","german":"On clear days you can see Mt. Fuji from here.","furigana":"は|ひ|ふじさん|み"},{"japanese":"夏休みに友達と富士山に登りました。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi ni tomodachi to Fujisan ni noborimashita.","german":"During summer vacation I climbed Mt. Fuji with friends.","furigana":"なつやす|ともだち|ふじさん|のぼ"}],"notes":"富士山 is Japan's highest mountain at 3,776 m and a symbol of the country, a World Heritage Site since 2013. The さん here is the on-reading of 山 \"mountain\", not a polite suffix; \"Fujiyama\" is not used in Japanese. Typical are 富士山に登る \"to climb Mt. Fuji\" (season roughly July to September) and 富士山が見える.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 8"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このサイトの魅力は、バラエティに富むコンテンツだ。","romaji":"Kono saito no miryoku wa, baraeti ni tomu kontentsu da.","german":"The appeal of this website is its richly varied content.","furigana":"みりょく|と"},{"japanese":"この地方は天然資源に富んでいる。","romaji":"Kono chihou wa tennen shigen ni tonde iru.","german":"This region is rich in natural resources.","furigana":"ちほう|てんねんしげん|と"},{"japanese":"彼の話はいつもユーモアに富んでいて面白い。","romaji":"Kare no hanashi wa itsumo yuumoa ni tonde ite omoshiroi.","german":"His stories are always full of humor and entertaining.","furigana":"かれ|はなし|と|おもしろ"}],"notes":"富む means \"to be rich in\" and is almost always used with に: 〜に富む, 〜に富んでいる. Typical partners are abstract qualities: 変化に富む \"full of variety\", 経験に富む \"experienced\", 栄養に富む \"nutritious\", ユーモアに富む. Attributively often 〜に富んだ (変化に富んだ人生). The word is rather written; in conversation people say 〜が多い or 〜が豊富だ, and \"wealthy\" is usually expressed with お金持ち.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"モルディブは富裕層の間で人気の高いリゾート地です。","romaji":"Morudibu wa fuyūsō no aida de ninki no takai rizōtochi desu.","german":"The Maldives is a resort popular among the wealthy.","furigana":"ふゆうそう|あいだ|にんき|たか|ち"},{"japanese":"彼は富裕な家庭に生まれ、何不自由なく育った。","romaji":"Kare wa fuyū na katei ni umare, nani fujiyū naku sodatta.","german":"He was born into a wealthy family and grew up wanting for nothing.","furigana":"かれ|ふゆう|かてい|う|なにふじゆう|そだ"},{"japanese":"政府は富裕層への税金を上げることを決めた。","romaji":"Seifu wa fuyūsō e no zeikin o ageru koto o kimeta.","german":"The government decided to raise taxes on the wealthy.","furigana":"せいふ|ふゆうそう|ぜいきん|あ|き"}],"notes":"富裕 is a written な-adjective and noun meaning \"wealthy, rich\". It is most often seen in 富裕層 \"the wealthy, upper class\", and also in 富裕な家庭 and 富裕国 \"rich countries\". In conversation people say 金持ち or お金持ち, somewhat more refined 裕福; the opposite is 貧困 or 貧しい.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"徳川家康って誰？","romaji":"Tokugawa Ieyasu tte dare?","german":"Who is Tokugawa Ieyasu?","furigana":"|だれ"},{"japanese":"徳川幕府は約260年続いた。","romaji":"Tokugawa bakufu wa yaku nihyaku rokujuu nen tsuzuita.","german":"The Tokugawa shogunate lasted about 260 years.","furigana":"とくがわばくふ|やく|ねんつづ"},{"japanese":"日光東照宮には徳川家康がまつられている。","romaji":"Nikkou Toushouguu ni wa Tokugawa Ieyasu ga matsurarete iru.","german":"Tokugawa Ieyasu is enshrined at Nikko Toshogu.","furigana":"にっこうとうしょうぐう|"}],"notes":"徳川 is the family name of the shogun dynasty that ruled Japan from 1603 to 1867. It appears almost always in compounds: 徳川家康 (founder of the shogunate), 徳川幕府 \"Tokugawa shogunate\", 徳川時代 \"Tokugawa period\" (= 江戸時代) and 徳川家 \"the House of Tokugawa\". The name is ubiquitous in history lessons, museums and historical dramas.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"徳島県にはまだ行ったことがないけど、いつか行ってみたいんだよね。","romaji":"Tokushimaken ni wa mada itta koto ga nai kedo, itsuka itte mitai n da yo ne.","german":"I've never been to Tokushima Prefecture, but I'd like to go someday.","furigana":"とくしまけん|い|い"},{"japanese":"徳島県は阿波おどりで有名です。","romaji":"Tokushimaken wa Awa odori de yuumei desu.","german":"Tokushima Prefecture is famous for the Awa Odori.","furigana":"とくしまけん||ゆうめい"},{"japanese":"徳島県は四国の東側にあります。","romaji":"Tokushimaken wa Shikoku no higashigawa ni arimasu.","german":"Tokushima Prefecture is on the eastern side of Shikoku.","furigana":"とくしまけん|しこく|ひがしがわ"}],"notes":"徳島県 is a prefecture in the east of the island 四国 Shikoku, with 徳島市 as its capital. It is known for the Obon dance 阿波おどり (阿波 is the old province name) and the sea whirlpools of 鳴門. In conversation 県 is often dropped: 徳島に行く, 徳島出身.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"探究心を持ち続けることが、成長のカギです。","romaji":"Tankyuushin o mochitsuzukeru koto ga, seichou no kagi desu.","german":"Keeping a spirit of inquiry is the key to growth.","furigana":"たんきゅうしん|も|つづ|せいちょう"},{"japanese":"彼は一生をかけて宇宙の謎を探究した。","romaji":"Kare wa isshou o kakete uchuu no nazo o tankyuu shita.","german":"He spent his whole life investigating the mysteries of the universe.","furigana":"かれ|いっしょう|うちゅう|なぞ|たんきゅう"},{"japanese":"授業では、生徒が自分でテーマを決めて探究する。","romaji":"Jugyou de wa, seito ga jibun de teema o kimete tankyuu suru.","german":"In class, students choose their own topics and investigate them.","furigana":"じゅぎょう|せいと|じぶん|き|たんきゅう"}],"notes":"探究 means to get to the bottom of something in depth - truth, essence, causes: 真理を探究する, 〜の探究. 探究心 \"spirit of inquiry, thirst for knowledge\" is very common. Do not confuse it with the identically read 探求 \"to search for something, to strive for\" (幸福を探求する) or with 研究 \"scientific research\" as an activity. The word is elevated and literary and often appears in education and science.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"探偵ドラマの見すぎじゃない？","romaji":"Tantei dorama no misugi ja nai?","german":"Haven't you watched too many detective dramas?","furigana":"たんてい|み"},{"japanese":"彼女は夫の浮気を調べるために探偵を雇った。","romaji":"Kanojo wa otto no uwaki o shiraberu tame ni tantei o yatotta.","german":"She hired a detective to investigate her husband's affair.","furigana":"かのじょ|おっと|うわき|しら|たんてい|やと"},{"japanese":"子どものころは探偵になるのが夢だった。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro wa tantei ni naru no ga yume datta.","german":"As a child, my dream was to become a detective.","furigana":"こ|たんてい|ゆめ"}],"notes":"探偵 is a detective, usually a private detective; the agency is called 探偵事務所, and a master detective in novels 名探偵. Police investigators, by contrast, are called 刑事. Typical are 探偵を雇う \"to hire a detective\" and 探偵小説 \"detective novel\". As a する-verb (\"to investigate secretly\") it is rare; people prefer 調べる or 調査する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"警察はどんな時に内偵捜査をするのですか。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa donna toki ni naitei sōsa o suru no desu ka.","german":"When does the police conduct covert investigations?","furigana":"けいさつ|とき|ないていそうさ"},{"japanese":"警察は半年前からその会社を内偵していた。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa hantoshi mae kara sono kaisha o naitei shite ita.","german":"The police had been secretly investigating that company since six months ago.","furigana":"けいさつ|はんとしまえ|かいしゃ|ないてい"},{"japanese":"内偵の結果、不正が明らかになった。","romaji":"Naitei no kekka, fusei ga akiraka ni natta.","german":"The secret investigation revealed the wrongdoing.","furigana":"ないてい|けっか|ふせい|あき"}],"notes":"内偵 is covert preliminary investigation carried out before those concerned know about it - typical of the police, public prosecutors or tax authorities: 〜を内偵する, 内偵を進める, 内偵捜査. It is a news-language word and hardly occurs in everyday life. Do not confuse it with the identically read 内定 \"informal job offer\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ごめん、迷わすつもりじゃなかったんだけど…。","romaji":"Gomen, mayowasu tsumori ja nakatta n da kedo...","german":"Sorry, I didn't mean to confuse you...","furigana":"まよ"},{"japanese":"甘い言葉で人を迷わすような広告には気をつけて。","romaji":"Amai kotoba de hito o mayowasu you na koukoku ni wa ki o tsukete.","german":"Be careful of ads that mislead people with sweet words.","furigana":"あま|ことば|ひと|まよ|こうこく|き"},{"japanese":"メニューが多すぎると、かえってお客さんを迷わすことになる。","romaji":"Menyuu ga oosugiru to, kaette okyakusan o mayowasu koto ni naru.","german":"If there are too many items on the menu, you end up confusing the customers.","furigana":"おお|きゃく|まよ"}],"notes":"迷わす is the causative form of 迷う \"to get lost, to be undecided\": to confuse, unsettle or tempt someone. There is also the regular causative form 迷わせる, which is more common in present-day Japanese; 迷わす sounds more concise and somewhat more literary. Typical are 人を迷わす and 心を迷わす. For deliberate deception, people more often say だます.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このイベントは市が主催するものです。","romaji":"Kono ibento wa shi ga shusai suru mono desu.","german":"This event is organized by the city.","furigana":"し|しゅさい"},{"japanese":"来月、学生会が国際交流パーティーを主催します。","romaji":"Raigetsu, gakuseikai ga kokusai kouryuu paatii o shusai shimasu.","german":"Next month the student council is hosting an international exchange party.","furigana":"らいげつ|がくせいかい|こくさいこうりゅう|しゅさい"},{"japanese":"この大会は新聞社の主催で行われた。","romaji":"Kono taikai wa shinbunsha no shusai de okonawareta.","german":"This tournament was held under the sponsorship of a newspaper company.","furigana":"たいかい|しんぶんしゃ|しゅさい|おこな"}],"notes":"主催する means to put on an event as the main party responsible: 〜を主催する; as a noun phrase 〜主催の〜 (市主催のイベント) or 〜の主催で. On posters, 主催 \"organizer\", 共催 \"co-organizer\" and 後援 \"patronage, support\" are distinguished. Do not confuse it with the identically read 主宰 \"to head, preside over\" (e.g. a theater group). Neutral to formal.","levelNote":"Derived from 主催 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"バナナを催促するゴリラの顔が忘れられない。","romaji":"Banana o saisoku suru gorira no kao ga wasurerarenai.","german":"I can't forget the face of the gorilla begging for a banana.","furigana":"さいそく|かお|わす"},{"japanese":"大家さんに家賃を催促された。","romaji":"Ooyasan ni yachin o saisoku sareta.","german":"The landlord pressed me for the rent.","furigana":"おおや|やちん|さいそく"},{"japanese":"返事が来ないので、もう一度メールで催促した。","romaji":"Henji ga konai node, mou ichido meeru de saisoku shita.","german":"I got no reply, so I sent another email to press for one.","furigana":"へんじ|こ|いちど|さいそく"}],"notes":"催促する means to urge someone to do or deliver something that is due or expected - money, a reply, work: 返事を催促する, 支払いを催促する; passive 催促される \"to be pressed\". A written reminder is, more formally, 督促状. Because pressing directly seems impolite, it is often paraphrased in emails with お返事をお待ちしております. Compared with せかす \"to hurry, to urge on\", 催促 concerns something that one is owed.","levelNote":"Derived from 催促 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は、幾度失敗しても、諦めなかった。","romaji":"Kare wa, ikudo shippai shite mo, akiramenakatta.","german":"No matter how many times he failed, he never gave up.","furigana":"かれ|いくどしっぱい|あきら"},{"japanese":"幾度となく同じ夢を見た。","romaji":"Ikudo to naku onaji yume o mita.","german":"I had the same dream countless times.","furigana":"いくど|おな|ゆめ|み"},{"japanese":"その寺は幾度も火事で焼けたが、そのたびに建て直された。","romaji":"Sono tera wa ikudo mo kaji de yaketa ga, sono tabi ni tatenaosareta.","german":"That temple burned down many times, but each time it was rebuilt.","furigana":"てら|いくど|かじ|や|た|なお"}],"notes":"幾度 is the literary variant of 何度 \"how often, how many times\" and is also read いくたび. It mostly appears in fixed patterns: 幾度も \"many times, again and again\", 幾度となく \"countless times\", 幾度〜ても \"no matter how often\". As a genuine question (\"How many times?\") it sounds old-fashioned; in daily life people say 何度 or 何回.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"それは恐らく本当のことだと思います。","romaji":"Sore wa osoraku hontō no koto da to omoimasu.","german":"I think that is probably true.","furigana":"おそ|ほんとう|おも"},{"japanese":"恐らく明日は雨でしょう。","romaji":"Osoraku ashita wa ame deshou.","german":"It will probably rain tomorrow.","furigana":"おそ|あした|あめ"},{"japanese":"この記録は、恐らく二度と破られないだろう。","romaji":"Kono kiroku wa, osoraku nido to yaburarenai darou.","german":"This record will probably never be broken.","furigana":"きろく|おそ|にど|やぶ"}],"notes":"恐らく expresses a fairly confident conjecture and is almost always combined with だろう/でしょう or と思う. It is more formal and written than たぶん and is very common in news, reports and talks. Originally it carried a note of apprehension (\"I fear\"), but today it is mostly used neutrally. According to dictionaries, it is usually written in kana as おそらく.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は人前で話すことに対して強い恐れを感じています。","romaji":"Kanojo wa hitomae de hanasu koto ni taishite tsuyoi osore o kanjite imasu.","german":"She feels a strong fear of speaking in front of people.","furigana":"かのじょ|ひとまえ|はな|たい|つよ|おそ|かん"},{"japanese":"台風が上陸するおそれがあります。","romaji":"Taifuu ga jouriku suru osore ga arimasu.","german":"There is a risk that the typhoon will make landfall.","furigana":"たいふう|じょうりく"},{"japanese":"若いころの彼は恐れを知らなかった。","romaji":"Wakai koro no kare wa osore o shiranakatta.","german":"In his youth he knew no fear.","furigana":"わか|かれ|おそ|し"}],"notes":"恐れ has two uses: as a feeling, \"fear, dread\" (恐れを感じる, 恐れを抱く, 恐れを知らない \"fearless\"), which is rather literary - in everyday speech people say 怖さ or 不安. Very common is the pattern 〜おそれがある \"there is a risk that ...\" in news, weather reports and on warning signs, usually written in kana. It refers to undesirable possibilities; for neutral ones, say 可能性がある.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の用心棒をしてくれない？","romaji":"Watashi no yōjinbō o shite kurenai?","german":"Won't you be my bodyguard?","furigana":"わたし|ようじんぼう"},{"japanese":"そのバーの入口には、夜になると体の大きい用心棒が立っている。","romaji":"Sono baa no iriguchi ni wa, yoru ni naru to karada no ookii youjinbou ga tatte iru.","german":"A big bouncer stands at the entrance of that bar at night.","furigana":"いりぐち|よる|からだ|おお|ようじんぼう|た"},{"japanese":"一人暮らしなので、用心棒代わりに大きな犬を飼っています。","romaji":"Hitorigurashi na node, youjinbou gawari ni ookina inu o katte imasu.","german":"I live alone, so I keep a big dog as a sort of bodyguard.","furigana":"ひとりぐ|ようじんぼうが|おお|いぬ|か"}],"notes":"用心棒 is someone hired for protection: a \"bodyguard\" or \"bouncer\", often with a slightly old-fashioned ring (known from samurai films such as Kurosawa's \"Yojimbo\"). Typical are 用心棒を雇う and 〜の用心棒をする. For personal protection officers of politicians or stars, ボディーガード is more usual today, and 警備員 for security staff in buildings. Also used jokingly: 用心棒代わりに犬を飼う \"to keep a dog as a protector\".","levelNote":"Compound: 用心 (N3) + 棒 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"華美なドレスはひかえた方がいいんじゃないですか。","romaji":"Kabi na doresu wa hikaeta hō ga ii n ja naidesu ka.","german":"Wouldn't it be better to hold back on a showy dress?","furigana":"かび|ほう"},{"japanese":"面接には、華美な服装やアクセサリーは避けましょう。","romaji":"Mensetsu ni wa, kabi na fukusou ya akusesarii wa sakemashou.","german":"For an interview, avoid flashy clothes and accessories.","furigana":"めんせつ|かび|ふくそう|さ"},{"japanese":"その寺は外から見ると地味だが、中は華美に飾られている。","romaji":"Sono tera wa soto kara miru to jimi da ga, naka wa kabi ni kazararete iru.","german":"The temple looks plain from the outside, but inside it is lavishly decorated.","furigana":"てら|そと|み|じみ|なか|かび|かざ"}],"notes":"華美 is a literary な-adjective meaning \"gorgeous, splendid\" - often with an undertone of \"excessive, ostentatious\". Typical in dress codes and rules: 華美な服装は避けること \"avoid flashy clothing\". Adverbially: 華美に飾る. More positive for \"luxurious\" is 豪華, and colloquially for \"loud, flashy\" people say 派手. In daily life 華美 is rarely heard, more in notices from schools and companies or at funerals.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"工事のせいでいつものランニングのコースが延びた。","romaji":"Kōji no sei de itsumo no ranningu no kōsu ga nobita.","german":"Because of construction, my usual running course got longer.","furigana":"こうじ|の"},{"japanese":"会議が一時間も延びて、電車に乗り遅れた。","romaji":"Kaigi ga ichijikan mo nobite, densha ni noriokureta.","german":"The meeting ran an hour over, and I missed my train.","furigana":"かいぎ|いちじかん|の|でんしゃ|の|おく"},{"japanese":"台風のため、出発が明日に延びました。","romaji":"Taifuu no tame, shuppatsu ga ashita ni nobimashita.","german":"Because of the typhoon, the departure was postponed to tomorrow.","furigana":"たいふう|しゅっぱつ|あした|の"}],"notes":"延びる is intransitive: time, deadlines or distances \"get extended\", or something \"gets postponed\": 会議が延びる, 締め切りが延びる, 出発が明日に延びる. The transitive counterpart is 延ばす (締め切りを延ばす). Do not confuse it with the identically read 伸びる, which is for physical growth and stretching (背が伸びる, 髪が伸びる, ゴムが伸びる). For an official postponement, the more formal 延期になる is used.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"我が国の政治家の多くは男性だ。","romaji":"Wagakuni no seijika no ōku wa dansei da.","german":"Many politicians in our country are men.","furigana":"わ|くに|せいじか|おお|だんせい"},{"japanese":"首相は「我が国の経済は回復している」と述べた。","romaji":"Shushou wa \"wagakuni no keizai wa kaifuku shite iru\" to nobeta.","german":"The prime minister stated, \"Our country's economy is recovering.\"","furigana":"しゅしょう|わ|くに|けいざい|かいふく|の"},{"japanese":"我が国は四方を海に囲まれている。","romaji":"Wagakuni wa shihou o umi ni kakomarete iru.","german":"Our country is surrounded by sea on all sides.","furigana":"わ|くに|しほう|うみ|かこ"}],"notes":"我が国 (often also written わが国) means \"our country\" and belongs to the formal language of politicians, authorities, news and textbooks; 我が is an old possessive \"our, my\" as in 我が家. In conversation people simply say 日本 or 自分の国 or うちの国 instead. Typical: 我が国の経済, 我が国にとって. Foreigners, too, use it in speeches for their own country.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい社員には豊富な経験があります。","romaji":"Atarashii shain ni wa hōfu na keiken ga arimasu.","german":"The new employee has plenty of experience.","furigana":"あたら|しゃいん|ほうふ|けいけん"},{"japanese":"この店はメニューが豊富で、何度来ても飽きない。","romaji":"Kono mise wa menyuu ga houfu de, nando kite mo akinai.","german":"This shop has an extensive menu, so I never get bored no matter how often I come.","furigana":"みせ|ほうふ|なんどき|あ"},{"japanese":"ほうれん草は鉄分が豊富だと言われています。","romaji":"Hourensou wa tetsubun ga houfu da to iwarete imasu.","german":"Spinach is said to be rich in iron.","furigana":"そう|てつぶん|ほうふ|い"}],"notes":"豊富 is a な-adjective meaning \"abundant, plentiful\" in quantity or choice: 豊富な経験, 豊富な知識, 種類が豊富だ, ビタミンが豊富だ (\"rich in vitamins\"). It sounds more matter-of-fact than たくさん or 多い. Distinction: 豊か describes prosperity or fullness in a broader sense (豊かな生活, 自然が豊かだ), while 豊富 describes a concrete, countable abundance of something. The thing something is rich in takes が.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このお金は、未来の自分のために貯めるの。","romaji":"Kono okane wa, mirai no jibun no tame ni tameru no.","german":"I'm saving this money for my future self.","furigana":"かね|みらい|じぶん|た"},{"japanese":"旅行のために、毎月三万円ずつ貯めています。","romaji":"Ryokou no tame ni, maitsuki sanman en zutsu tamete imasu.","german":"I save 30,000 yen every month for a trip.","furigana":"りょこう|まいつきさんまんえん|た"},{"japanese":"このカードで買い物をすると、ポイントを貯められます。","romaji":"Kono kaado de kaimono o suru to, pointo o tameraremasu.","german":"If you shop with this card, you can accumulate points.","furigana":"か|もの|た"}],"notes":"貯める is transitive and means \"to save, to accumulate (money)\": お金を貯める, ポイントを貯める. The intransitive counterpart is 貯まる (お金が貯まる \"the money builds up\"). The identically read 溜める is used for accumulating things in general (水を溜める, ストレスを溜める); in everyday writing, ためる is often simply written in kana. 貯金する stresses depositing money into an account, 節約する saving by spending less.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私がベーコンを食べなくなって久しい。","romaji":"Watashi ga bēkon o tabenaku natte hisashii.","german":"It has been a long time since I stopped eating bacon.","furigana":"わたし|た|ひさ"},{"japanese":"久しく会っていない友人から、突然手紙が届いた。","romaji":"Hisashiku atte inai yuujin kara, totsuzen tegami ga todoita.","german":"A letter suddenly arrived from a friend I hadn't seen in a long time.","furigana":"ひさ|あ|ゆうじん|とつぜんてがみ|とど"},{"japanese":"少子化が問題だと言われて久しい。","romaji":"Shoushika ga mondai da to iwarete hisashii.","german":"It has long been said that the declining birthrate is a problem.","furigana":"しょうしか|もんだい|い|ひさ"}],"notes":"久しい is a literary い-adjective: \"long (ago)\". It appears almost always in the pattern 〜て久しい \"it has been a long time since ...\" (〜と言われて久しい \"it has long been said that ...\") or adverbially as 久しく \"for a long time\" (久しく会っていない). In conversation people say ずいぶん前から or 長い間 instead. Related is the everyday 久しぶり \"after a long time\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"警察は事故の原因を究明している。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa jiko no gen'in o kyuumei shite iru.","german":"The police are investigating the cause of the accident.","furigana":"けいさつ|じこ|げんいん|きゅうめい"},{"japanese":"被害者の家族は、真相の究明を求めています。","romaji":"Higaisha no kazoku wa, shinsou no kyuumei o motomete imasu.","german":"The victims' families are demanding that the truth be established.","furigana":"ひがいしゃ|かぞく|しんそう|きゅうめい|もと"},{"japanese":"科学者たちは、この病気の仕組みを究明しようとしている。","romaji":"Kagakushatachi wa, kono byouki no shikumi o kyuumei shiyou to shite iru.","german":"Scientists are trying to clarify the mechanism of this disease.","furigana":"かがくしゃ|びょうき|しく|きゅうめい"}],"notes":"究明 means \"to investigate thoroughly and clarify\", especially causes, truth or scientific relationships: 原因を究明する, 真相究明, 原因究明を急ぐ. It is formal and typical of news and specialist texts. Distinctions: 調査 is investigation or survey in general, 研究 is research, 解明 is unraveling a mystery. For personal matters such as one's own weaknesses, 究明 does not fit.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"試験勉強が難しすぎて、白旗を上げそうだよ。","romaji":"Shiken benkyou ga muzukashisugite, shirohata o agesou da yo.","german":"The exam material is so hard that I'm about to give up.","furigana":"しけんべんきょう|むずか|しろはた|あ"},{"japanese":"兵士たちは白旗を振りながら、ゆっくりと近づいてきた。","romaji":"Heishitachi wa shirohata o furinagara, yukkuri to chikazuite kita.","german":"The soldiers approached slowly, waving a white flag.","furigana":"へいし|しろはた|ふ|ちか"},{"japanese":"社長もついに白旗を上げて、計画を中止した。","romaji":"Shachou mo tsui ni shirohata o agete, keikaku o chuushi shita.","german":"The president finally gave up too and called off the plan.","furigana":"しゃちょう|しろはた|あ|けいかく|ちゅうし"}],"notes":"白旗 is the \"white flag\" as a sign of surrender. Most common is the phrase 白旗を上げる (also 揚げる) \"to raise the white flag\" - literally in war, figuratively in everyday life for \"to give up, to admit defeat\", for example with tasks that are too hard. Besides しろはた there is the reading はっき. Synonymous but more direct are 降参する and 諦める.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"まずはローカルの人脈を広げるとこから始めるつもりです。","romaji":"Mazu wa rookaru no jinmyaku o hirogeru toko kara hajimeru tsumori desu.","german":"First, I plan to start by expanding my local network of contacts.","furigana":"じんみゃく|ひろ|はじ"},{"japanese":"彼女は人脈が広いので、困ったときに頼りになる。","romaji":"Kanojo wa jinmyaku ga hiroi node, komatta toki ni tayori ni naru.","german":"She has a wide network of contacts, so she's reliable when you're in trouble.","furigana":"かのじょ|じんみゃく|ひろ|こま|たよ"},{"japanese":"前の会社で作った人脈を生かして、独立した。","romaji":"Mae no kaisha de tsukutta jinmyaku o ikashite, dokuritsu shita.","german":"He went independent, making use of the connections he built at his previous company.","furigana":"まえ|かいしゃ|つく|じんみゃく|い|どくりつ"}],"notes":"人脈 is a personal \"network\" of contacts, above all in working life: 人脈を広げる, 人脈を作る/築く, 人脈が広い, 人脈を生かす. The word is neutral to positive. コネ, by contrast, means \"connections\" that one exploits, often with an air of nepotism (コネで入社する). More general and softer is つながり \"connection\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"押しボタン式のドアでも、自動ドアって呼ぶのかな。","romaji":"Oshi botanshiki no doa de mo, jidō doa tte yobu no ka na.","german":"Is a door with a push button also called an automatic door?","furigana":"お|しき|じどう|よ"},{"japanese":"この信号は押しボタン式なので、ボタンを押して待ってください。","romaji":"Kono shingou wa oshibotan-shiki na node, botan o oshite matte kudasai.","german":"This traffic light is the push-button type, so press the button and wait.","furigana":"しんごう|お|しき|お|ま"},{"japanese":"非常の際は、この押しボタンで駅員を呼んでください。","romaji":"Hijou no sai wa, kono oshibotan de ekiin o yonde kudasai.","german":"In an emergency, please use this push button to call a station attendant.","furigana":"ひじょう|さい|お|えきいん|よ"}],"notes":"押しボタン is a \"push button\", especially on devices or installations that are operated by pressing a button. 押しボタン式 \"push-button type\" is very common: 押しボタン式信号 is a pedestrian-activated traffic light, and there are also emergency call buttons in stations or stop-request buttons on buses. In daily life people mostly say just ボタン; 押しボタン appears more on signs and notices.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は有名な小説家だが、時々自費出版することもあるんだよ。","romaji":"Kare wa yūmei na shōsetsuka da ga, tokidoki jihi shuppan suru koto mo aru n da yo.","german":"He is a famous novelist, but sometimes he also self-publishes.","furigana":"かれ|ゆうめい|しょうせつか|ときどきじひしゅっぱん"},{"japanese":"彼女は去年、初めての小説を出版した。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kyonen, hajimete no shousetsu o shuppan shita.","german":"She published her first novel last year.","furigana":"かのじょ|きょねん|はじ|しょうせつ|しゅっぱん"},{"japanese":"この本は五十年前に出版されたのに、今でもよく売れている。","romaji":"Kono hon wa gojuunen mae ni shuppan sareta noni, ima demo yoku urete iru.","german":"This book was published fifty years ago, yet it still sells well today.","furigana":"ほん|ごじゅうねんまえ|しゅっぱん|いま|う"}],"notes":"出版する is transitive: to \"publish\" a book - 本を出版する, and in the passive 出版される \"to come out\". Important compounds are 自費出版 \"self-publishing\" and 出版社 \"publishing house\". For newspapers and magazines people tend to say 発行する, and for research results or music 発表する. In conversation you also hear simply 本を出す.","levelNote":"Derived from 出版 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その出版社では、入館の際に身分証明書を提示しなければなりません。","romaji":"Sono shuppansha de wa, nyuukan no sai ni mibun shoumeisho o teiji shinakereba narimasen.","german":"At that publisher, you have to show ID when entering the building.","furigana":"しゅっぱんしゃ|にゅうかん|さい|みぶんしょうめいしょ|ていじ"},{"japanese":"兄は出版社で雑誌の編集をしています。","romaji":"Ani wa shuppansha de zasshi no henshuu o shite imasu.","german":"My older brother edits a magazine at a publishing company.","furigana":"あに|しゅっぱんしゃ|ざっし|へんしゅう"},{"japanese":"原稿をいくつかの出版社に送ったが、どこからも返事がない。","romaji":"Genkou o ikutsuka no shuppansha ni okutta ga, doko kara mo henji ga nai.","german":"I sent my manuscript to several publishers, but I haven't heard back from any of them.","furigana":"げんこう|しゅっぱんしゃ|おく|へんじ"}],"notes":"出版社 is a \"publisher\" of books and magazines: 出版社に勤める, 出版社から本を出す, 大手出版社 \"major publisher\". Do not confuse it with 書店 or 本屋 \"bookstore\", where books are sold. A newspaper publisher is called 新聞社. The staff who work with the texts are 編集者 \"editors\".","levelNote":"Compound: 出版 (N3) + 社 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お前は自分のことを素晴らしいと思っているみたいだけどな、ただの貧乏家族の貧乏人だってことを、よ〜く肝に銘じておくんだな。","romaji":"Omae wa jibun no koto o subarashii to omotte iru mitai da kedo na, tada no binbō kazoku no binbōnin datte koto o, yōku kimo ni meijite oku n da na.","german":"You seem to think you're wonderful, but keep firmly in mind that you're just a poor person from a poor family.","furigana":"まえ|じぶん|すば|おも|びんぼうかぞく|びんぼうにん|きも|めい"},{"japanese":"僕みたいな貧乏人には、こんな高いレストランは無理だよ。","romaji":"Boku mitai na binbōnin ni wa, konna takai resutoran wa muri da yo.","german":"A poor person like me can't afford such an expensive restaurant.","furigana":"ぼく|びんぼうにん|たか|むり"},{"japanese":"昔の物語には、貧乏人とお金持ちがよく出てくる。","romaji":"Mukashi no monogatari ni wa, binbōnin to okanemochi ga yoku dete kuru.","german":"Old tales often feature poor people and rich people.","furigana":"むかし|ものがたり|びんぼうにん|かねも|で"}],"notes":"貧乏人 is a \"poor person, poor wretch\" and sounds blunt to derogatory. It is often used self-deprecatingly (僕みたいな貧乏人) or as an insult; neutrally one says 貧しい人, 生活が苦しい人 or 低所得者. The opposite is 金持ち; a well-known proverb is 貧乏人の子だくさん \"the poor have many children\".","levelNote":"Compound: 貧乏 (N4) + 人 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女のお父さんに、彼女と結婚してもいいか聞いて、許しをもらった。","romaji":"Kanojo no otousan ni, kanojo to kekkon shite mo ii ka kiite, yurushi o moratta.","german":"I asked her father whether I could marry her and got his permission.","furigana":"かのじょ|とう|かのじょ|けっこん|き|ゆる"},{"japanese":"姉は去年、大学時代の友達と結婚しました。","romaji":"Ane wa kyonen, daigaku jidai no tomodachi to kekkon shimashita.","german":"My older sister married a friend from her university days last year.","furigana":"あね|きょねん|だいがくじだい|ともだち|けっこん"},{"japanese":"「結婚していますか。」「いいえ、まだ独身です。」","romaji":"Kekkon shite imasu ka? Iie, mada dokushin desu.","german":"\"Are you married?\" \"No, I'm still single.\"","furigana":"けっこん|どくしん"}],"notes":"結婚する means \"to marry\"; the person takes と, not を: 彼女と結婚する. 結婚している describes the state \"to be married\", 結婚した the event \"got married\". Related words: 結婚式 \"wedding ceremony\", 婚約 \"engagement\", 離婚 \"divorce\", 独身 \"single\". To congratulate, you say ご結婚おめでとうございます.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 7"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この幼稚園では、子どもたちの発表会は撮影禁止です。","romaji":"Kono yōchien de wa, kodomotachi no happyōkai wa satsuei kinshi desu.","german":"At this kindergarten, photography is prohibited at the children's performances.","furigana":"ようちえん|こ|はっぴょうかい|さつえいきんし"},{"japanese":"美術館の中は撮影禁止なので、カメラをしまってください。","romaji":"Bijutsukan no naka wa satsuei kinshi na node, kamera o shimatte kudasai.","german":"Photography is prohibited inside the museum, so please put your camera away.","furigana":"びじゅつかん|なか|さつえいきんし"},{"japanese":"入口に「撮影禁止」と書いてあるのに、写真を撮っている人がいた。","romaji":"Iriguchi ni satsuei kinshi to kaite aru noni, shashin o totte iru hito ga ita.","german":"The entrance says \"No photography,\" but someone was taking pictures.","furigana":"いりぐち|さつえいきんし|か|しゃしん|と|ひと"}],"notes":"撮影禁止 means \"photographing (and filming) prohibited\"; 撮影 covers photos as well as video. You see it mainly on signs in museums, temples, concerts or shops, and in a sentence mostly as 〜は撮影禁止です or 撮影禁止の場所. Variants are フラッシュ撮影禁止 and 撮影禁止区域. In speech, and more politely, you say 写真を撮らないでください.","levelNote":"Compound: 撮影 (N2) + 禁止 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「コウイチ、忘れ物は無い？」「無いよ。」「あ、ちょっと待って。あんたのお弁当に照り焼きサーモン入れるの忘れてたわ。」「ええっ！母さん、早くしてよ。」","romaji":"Kouichi, wasuremono wa nai? Nai yo. A, chotto matte. Anta no obentou ni teriyaki saamon ireru no wasureteta wa. Ee! Kaasan, hayaku shite yo.","german":"\"Koichi, did you forget anything?\" \"No.\" \"Oh, wait a moment. I forgot to put the teriyaki salmon in your lunchbox.\" \"What?! Mom, hurry up.\""},{"japanese":"今夜は鶏肉を照り焼きにしよう。","romaji":"Kon'ya wa toriniku o teriyaki ni shiyou.","german":"Let's make chicken teriyaki tonight.","furigana":"こんや|とりにく|て|や"},{"japanese":"この店では、照り焼きバーガーが一番人気です。","romaji":"Kono mise de wa, teriyaki baagaa ga ichiban ninki desu.","german":"At this shop, the teriyaki burger is the most popular.","furigana":"みせ|て|や|いちばんにんき"}],"notes":"照り焼き is a cooking method: meat or fish is glazed with a sweet sauce of soy sauce, mirin and sugar and fried or grilled until it shines (照り \"gloss\"). Typical are 鶏の照り焼き, ぶりの照り焼き and 照り焼きバーガー; \"to prepare something as teriyaki\" is 照り焼きにする. On menus you also see てりやき in kana.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その雑誌は購読する価値がありますよ。","romaji":"Sono zasshi wa kōdoku suru kachi ga arimasu yo.","german":"That magazine is worth subscribing to.","furigana":"ざっし|こうどく|かち"},{"japanese":"うちでは新聞を二つ購読しています。","romaji":"Uchi de wa shinbun o futatsu koudoku shite imasu.","german":"We subscribe to two newspapers at home.","furigana":"しんぶん|ふた|こうどく"},{"japanese":"料金が上がったので、雑誌の定期購読をやめることにした。","romaji":"Ryoukin ga agatta node, zasshi no teiki koudoku o yameru koto ni shita.","german":"The price went up, so I decided to cancel my magazine subscription.","furigana":"りょうきん|あ|ざっし|ていきこうどく"}],"notes":"購読する means \"to take, subscribe to (a newspaper or magazine)\": 新聞を購読する, 定期購読 \"subscription\", 購読料 \"subscription fee\". The word is rather formal and typical for print media and newsletters. For YouTube channels people say チャンネル登録, and for online services in general 登録する. To cancel a subscription, say 購読をやめる or, more formally, 解約する.","levelNote":"Derived from 購読 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どこかの国で、ゴリラが逃げ出して大騒ぎになっていたことがあったよね。","romaji":"Doko ka no kuni de, gorira ga nigedashite ōsawagi ni natte ita koto ga atta yo ne.","german":"I remember that in some country a gorilla escaped and caused an uproar.","furigana":"くに|に|だ|おおさわ"},{"japanese":"子どもたちは初めて雪を見て大騒ぎしている。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa hajimete yuki o mite oosawagi shite iru.","german":"The children are making a big fuss about seeing snow for the first time.","furigana":"こ|はじ|ゆき|み|おおさわ"},{"japanese":"財布が見つからなくて大騒ぎしたけど、かばんの中にあった。","romaji":"Saifu ga mitsukaranakute oosawagi shita kedo, kaban no naka ni atta.","german":"I made a big fuss because I couldn't find my wallet, but it was in my bag.","furigana":"さいふ|み|おおさわ|なか"}],"notes":"大騒ぎ is a \"great uproar, a big fuss\": 大騒ぎになる \"to cause an uproar\", 大騒ぎする \"to make a big fuss, to celebrate boisterously\" (intransitive). Depending on context it can mean real excitement, exaggerated fuss or loud partying. Weaker is 騒ぎ; more serious and formal is 騒動 (for example for riots or scandals).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ヤフオクで入札した後の手続きの順序についてブログでまとめてみました。","romaji":"Yafuoku de nyuusatsu shita ato no tetsuzuki no junjo ni tsuite burogu de matomete mimashita.","german":"I summarized on my blog the order of the procedures after bidding on Yahoo Auctions.","furigana":"にゅうさつ|あと|てつづ|じゅんじょ"},{"japanese":"説明の順序が逆だったので、分かりにくかった。","romaji":"Setsumei no junjo ga gyaku datta node, wakarinikukatta.","german":"The explanation was hard to follow because the order was reversed.","furigana":"せつめい|じゅんじょ|ぎゃく|わ"},{"japanese":"何があったのか、順序立てて話してください。","romaji":"Nani ga atta no ka, junjo datete hanashite kudasai.","german":"Please tell me in order what happened.","furigana":"なに|じゅんじょだ|はな"}],"notes":"順序 is the \"order\" or \"sequence\" of steps: 順序が逆だ, 順序を守る, 順序よく並べる, 順序立てて説明する \"to explain in order\". It sounds somewhat more factual than 順番, which is used above all for \"whose turn it is\" (順番を待つ). For concrete work steps one also says 手順.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 44"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自分が意志の弱い人間だってことは自覚してますよ。","romaji":"Jibun ga ishi no yowai ningen datte koto wa jikaku shitemasu yo.","german":"I'm well aware that I'm a weak-willed person.","furigana":"じぶん|いし|よわ|にんげん|じかく"},{"japanese":"彼は誰にも言われずに、自分の意志で会社を辞めた。","romaji":"Kare wa dare ni mo iwarezu ni, jibun no ishi de kaisha o yameta.","german":"Without anyone telling him to, he quit the company of his own will.","furigana":"かれ|だれ|い|じぶん|いし|かいしゃ|や"},{"japanese":"父は意志が強く、一度決めたことは最後までやり通す。","romaji":"Chichi wa ishi ga tsuyoku, ichido kimeta koto wa saigo made yaritoosu.","german":"My father has a strong will and sees through whatever he has decided to the end.","furigana":"ちち|いし|つよ|いちどき|さいご|とお"}],"notes":"意志 is the \"will\" in the sense of willpower and determination: 意志が強い/弱い, 強い意志, 自分の意志で \"of one's own free will\". Do not confuse it with the identically read 意思, which rather means \"intention, wish\" and appears in legal contexts (意思表示, 本人の意思を確認する). As a rule of thumb: 意志 = strength of will, 意思 = an opinion that one expresses.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この仕事を志望する動機は何ですか？","romaji":"Kono shigoto o shibō suru dōki wa nani desu ka?","german":"What is your motivation for applying for this job?","furigana":"しごと|しぼう|どうき|なに"},{"japanese":"第一志望の大学に合格しました。","romaji":"Daiichi shibou no daigaku ni goukaku shimashita.","german":"I got into my first-choice university.","furigana":"だいいちしぼう|だいがく|ごうかく"},{"japanese":"弟は医学部を志望していて、毎晩遅くまで勉強している。","romaji":"Otouto wa igakubu o shibou shite ite, maiban osoku made benkyou shite iru.","german":"My younger brother is aiming for medical school and studies late every night.","furigana":"おとうと|いがくぶ|しぼう|まいばんおそ|べんきょう"}],"notes":"志望 denotes the wish to aim for a particular school, university or profession: 〜を志望する, 第一志望 \"first choice\", 志望校 \"preferred school\", 志望動機 \"motivation for applying\". As a suffix, 〜志望 means \"aspiring\" (作家志望の若者). More general is 希望 \"wish, hope\"; actually submitting an application is 応募する.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コウイチは私の恩人です。私がベーコンに溺れていた時、私を救ってくれたんです。","romaji":"Kouichi wa watashi no onjin desu. Watashi ga beekon ni oborete ita toki, watashi o sukutte kureta n desu.","german":"Koichi is my benefactor. When I was drowning in bacon, he saved me.","furigana":"わたし|おんじん|わたし|おぼ|とき|わたし|すく"},{"japanese":"あの時助けてくれたあなたは、私の命の恩人です。","romaji":"Ano toki tasukete kureta anata wa, watashi no inochi no onjin desu.","german":"You, who helped me back then, are the savior of my life.","furigana":"ときたす|わたし|いのち|おんじん"},{"japanese":"社長は、仕事のなかった私を雇ってくれた恩人だ。","romaji":"Shachou wa, shigoto no nakatta watashi o yatotte kureta onjin da.","german":"The company president is my benefactor who hired me when I had no work.","furigana":"しゃちょう|しごと|わたし|やと|おんじん"}],"notes":"恩人 is a person to whom one owes a great deal: a \"benefactor\", someone to whom one is indebted. 命の恩人 \"life-saver\" is very common. Typical: 〜は私の恩人だ, 恩人に恩返しする \"to repay one's benefactor\". Related are 恩 \"debt of gratitude, favor\" and 恩師 \"revered former teacher\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あのベーコン泥棒を引っ捕えた者には恩賞を与えよう。","romaji":"Ano bēkon dorobō o hittoraeta mono ni wa onshō o ataeyō.","german":"I shall grant a reward to whoever captures that bacon thief.","furigana":"どろぼう|ひ|とら|もの|おんしょう|あた"},{"japanese":"戦いで活躍した武士は、恩賞として土地をもらった。","romaji":"Tatakai de katsuyaku shita bushi wa, onshou toshite tochi o moratta.","german":"The samurai who distinguished himself in battle received land as a reward.","furigana":"たたか|かつやく|ぶし|おんしょう|とち"},{"japanese":"時代劇には、恩賞が少ないと文句を言う家来がよく出てくる。","romaji":"Jidaigeki ni wa, onshou ga sukunai to monku o iu kerai ga yoku dete kuru.","german":"Period dramas often feature retainers complaining that the reward is small.","furigana":"じだいげき|おんしょう|すく|もんく|い|けらい|で"}],"notes":"恩賞 is a \"reward\" that a ruler or lord grants for meritorious service, for example land or money for achievements in war. The word is historical and today appears mainly in history books and samurai dramas: 恩賞を与える, 恩賞を受ける. For a reward in modern everyday life you say ご褒美, and for an offered cash prize 賞金 or 謝礼.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"僕のお父さんはカブト虫を採る名人です。","romaji":"Boku no otōsan wa kabutomushi o toru meijin desu.","german":"My father is a master at catching rhinoceros beetles.","furigana":"ぼく|とう|むし|と|めいじん"},{"japanese":"週末に山でキノコを採ってきた。","romaji":"Shuumatsu ni yama de kinoko o totte kita.","german":"I went and gathered mushrooms in the mountains at the weekend.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|やま|と"},{"japanese":"うちの会社は今年、新人を十人採る予定だ。","romaji":"Uchi no kaisha wa kotoshi, shinjin o juunin toru yotei da.","german":"Our company plans to hire ten new employees this year.","furigana":"かいしゃ|ことし|しんじん|じゅうにんと|よてい"}],"notes":"採る is a special spelling of とる for \"to gather, to pick\" (キノコを採る, 虫を採る), \"to hire (personnel)\" (新人を採る) and \"to choose (a measure, a method)\" (この案を採る, 決を採る). In everyday writing 取る is often used instead. Other spellings: 捕る \"to catch (animals)\", 撮る \"to photograph\". Related nouns are 採用 \"hiring, adoption\" and 採集 \"collecting\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この桜んぼ、やばいくらい美味しいんだけど。","romaji":"Kono sakuranbo, yabai kurai oishii n da kedo.","german":"These cherries are insanely delicious.","furigana":"さくら|おい"},{"japanese":"山形県はさくらんぼの産地として有名です。","romaji":"Yamagata-ken wa sakuranbo no sanchi toshite yuumei desu.","german":"Yamagata Prefecture is famous as a cherry-growing region.","furigana":"やまがたけん|さんち|ゆうめい"},{"japanese":"ケーキの上にさくらんぼを一つのせた。","romaji":"Keeki no ue ni sakuranbo o hitotsu noseta.","german":"I put one cherry on top of the cake.","furigana":"うえ|ひと"}],"notes":"さくらんぼ is the \"cherry\" as a fruit and is almost always written in kana; the spelling 桜んぼ is rare, and on packaging you often see サクランボ. Distinguish 桜, the cherry tree or cherry blossom. A well-known growing region is Yamagata Prefecture. In sweets and on menus you also see チェリー.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お前も一緒に桜肉パーティーに来いよ。イカしたパーティーになるぜ。","romaji":"Omae mo issho ni sakuraniku pātī ni koi yo. Ikashita pātī ni naru ze.","german":"You come to the horse meat party with me, too. It's going to be a cool party.","furigana":"まえ|いっしょ|さくらにく|こ"},{"japanese":"熊本では桜肉の刺身が名物です。","romaji":"Kumamoto de wa sakuraniku no sashimi ga meibutsu desu.","german":"In Kumamoto, horse meat sashimi is a local specialty.","furigana":"くまもと|さくらにく|さしみ|めいぶつ"},{"japanese":"冬になると、祖父は桜肉で鍋を作ってくれた。","romaji":"Fuyu ni naru to, sofu wa sakuraniku de nabe o tsukutte kureta.","german":"When winter came, my grandfather would make hot pot with horse meat.","furigana":"ふゆ|そふ|さくらにく|なべ|つく"}],"notes":"桜肉 is a traditional expression for horse meat (plainly 馬肉); the name is usually explained by the pink color of the meat. Well-known dishes are 馬刺し \"raw horse meat\" and 桜鍋 \"horse meat stew\", specialties for example in 熊本 or 長野. In a similarly euphemistic way, wild boar meat is called ぼたん and deer meat もみじ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自分が二歳か三歳の頃に、一丁前に桜色のタキシードを着てる写真を見ました。","romaji":"Jibun ga nisai ka sansai no koro ni, itchōmae ni sakurairo no takishīdo o kiteru shashin o mimashita.","german":"I saw a photo of myself at two or three years old, looking all grown up in a pale pink tuxedo.","furigana":"じぶん|にさい|さんさい|ころ|いっちょうまえ|さくらいろ|き|しゃしん|み"},{"japanese":"寒さで彼女のほおが桜色に染まっていた。","romaji":"Samusa de kanojo no hoo ga sakurairo ni somatte ita.","german":"Her cheeks were tinged pink from the cold.","furigana":"さむ|かのじょ|さくらいろ|そ"},{"japanese":"春らしい桜色のセーターを買いました。","romaji":"Harurashii sakurairo no sētā o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a spring-like cherry-pink sweater.","furigana":"はる|さくらいろ|か"}],"notes":"桜色 is the very delicate, pale pink of cherry blossoms. It stands attributively with の (桜色のセーター) or with に for changes (桜色に染まる \"to turn pale pink\") and sounds more poetic than ピンク. 桃色 is a stronger peach pink. 桜色 is also often used to describe rosy cheeks or lips.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"キッチンタイマーが鳴るのを待っていると、永遠に続くような気がするよね。","romaji":"Kitchin taimaa ga naru no o matte iru to, eien ni tsuzuku you na ki ga suru yo ne.","german":"When you're waiting for the kitchen timer to ring, it feels like it goes on forever, doesn't it?","furigana":"な|ま|えいえん|つづ|き"},{"japanese":"二人は教会で永遠の愛を誓った。","romaji":"Futari wa kyoukai de eien no ai o chikatta.","german":"The two vowed eternal love in the church.","furigana":"ふたり|きょうかい|えいえん|あい|ちか"},{"japanese":"この写真があれば、あの日の思い出は永遠に残る。","romaji":"Kono shashin ga areba, ano hi no omoide wa eien ni nokoru.","german":"With this photo, the memories of that day will remain forever.","furigana":"しゃしん|ひ|おも|で|えいえん|のこ"}],"notes":"永遠 means \"eternity\" and is used mainly in two forms: adverbially 永遠に \"forever, eternally\" (永遠に続く, 永遠に忘れない) and attributively with の (永遠の愛, 永遠のテーマ). The form 永遠な is unusual. The word sounds solemn or romantic; in conversation people prefer ずっと or いつまでも. 永久 is more matter-of-fact and technical (永久歯 \"permanent teeth\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"機内に持ち込み可能な液体の量はどれぐらいですか。","romaji":"Kinai ni mochikomi kanō na ekitai no ryō wa dore gurai desu ka.","german":"How much liquid can I bring on board the plane?","furigana":"きない|も|こ|かのう|えきたい|りょう"},{"japanese":"水は温度によって、固体、液体、気体に変わる。","romaji":"Mizu wa ondo ni yotte, kotai, ekitai, kitai ni kawaru.","german":"Water changes into solid, liquid and gas depending on temperature.","furigana":"みず|おんど|こたい|えきたい|きたい|か"},{"japanese":"洗濯には、粉ではなく液体の洗剤を使っています。","romaji":"Sentaku ni wa, kona de wa naku ekitai no senzai o tsukatte imasu.","german":"For laundry I use liquid detergent rather than powder.","furigana":"せんたく|こな|えきたい|せんざい|つか"}],"notes":"液体 is the technical term for \"liquid\" and, together with 固体 \"solid\" and 気体 \"gas\", forms the three states of matter. In everyday life it appears at airport security checks (液体の持ち込み) and in product names such as 液体洗剤 \"liquid detergent\" or 液体のり \"liquid glue\". As a suffix, often just 〜液 is used (消毒液, 洗浄液).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コンピュータを使う仕事は眼を酷使しやすい。","romaji":"Konpyūta o tsukau shigoto wa me o kokushi shi yasui.","german":"Work that involves using a computer easily strains the eyes.","furigana":"つか|しごと|め|こくし"},{"japanese":"彼は人を見る眼があるから、彼が選んだ人なら安心だ。","romaji":"Kare wa hito o miru me ga aru kara, kare ga eranda hito nara anshin da.","german":"He has an eye for people, so you can feel safe with anyone he chooses.","furigana":"かれ|ひと|み|め|かれ|えら|ひと|あんしん"},{"japanese":"病院で眼の検査をしてもらった。","romaji":"Byouin de me no kensa o shite moratta.","german":"I had my eyes examined at the hospital.","furigana":"びょういん|め|けんさ"}],"notes":"眼 is a rarer spelling of め \"eye\"; in daily life you almost always write 目. 眼 seems technical or literary and often appears in medical contexts or when a \"trained eye, judgment\" is meant: 人を見る眼がある, 鋭い眼. In compounds the character is mostly read がん: 眼科 \"ophthalmology\", 眼球 \"eyeball\"; 眼鏡 is read めがね.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は自分の左の眼球をあちこち捜したが、なかなか見つけられなかった。","romaji":"Watashi wa jibun no hidari no gankyuu o achikochi sagashita ga, nakanaka mitsukerarenakatta.","german":"I searched everywhere for my left eyeball but could not find it.","furigana":"わたし|じぶん|ひだり|がんきゅう|さが|み"},{"japanese":"眼球の表面が乾くと、目が疲れやすくなる。","romaji":"Gankyuu no hyoumen ga kawaku to, me ga tsukareyasuku naru.","german":"When the surface of the eyeball dries out, the eyes tire easily.","furigana":"がんきゅう|ひょうめん|かわ|め|つか"},{"japanese":"医者はライトを使って、患者の眼球の動きを調べた。","romaji":"Isha wa raito o tsukatte, kanja no gankyuu no ugoki o shirabeta.","german":"The doctor used a light to examine the patient's eye movements.","furigana":"いしゃ|つか|かんじゃ|がんきゅう|うご|しら"}],"notes":"眼球 is the medical-anatomical term for the \"eyeball\" (眼球運動 \"eye movement\", 眼球が乾く). In everyday speech people usually say 目玉, which is also found in 目玉焼き \"fried egg\" and 目玉商品 \"loss leader\". With の, 眼球 forms technical expressions such as 眼球の表面 or 眼球の動き.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"老眼は大体いつ頃から始まるんですか。","romaji":"Rōgan wa daitai itsu koro kara hajimaru n desu ka.","german":"Around when does presbyopia usually start?","furigana":"ろうがん|だいたい|ころ|はじ"},{"japanese":"最近老眼が進んで、小さい字が読みにくくなった。","romaji":"Saikin rougan ga susunde, chiisai ji ga yominikuku natta.","german":"My presbyopia has progressed lately, and small print has become hard to read.","furigana":"さいきんろうがん|すす|ちい|じ|よ"},{"japanese":"父は新聞を読むとき、老眼鏡をかけている。","romaji":"Chichi wa shinbun o yomu toki, rougankyou o kakete iru.","german":"My father wears reading glasses when he reads the newspaper.","furigana":"ちち|しんぶん|よ|ろうがんきょう"}],"notes":"老眼 is \"age-related farsightedness\" (presbyopia): with age, one sees worse up close. Typical: 老眼になる, 老眼が始まる/進む, and 老眼鏡 \"reading glasses\". Distinguish it from 近視 or 近眼 \"nearsightedness\" and 遠視 \"farsightedness\" (not age-related). Since the word alludes to age, people politely paraphrase it with 手元が見えにくい.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"うちには、先祖代々受け継がれてきた古い刀があります。","romaji":"Uchi ni wa, senzo daidai uketsugarete kita furui katana ga arimasu.","german":"My family has an old sword that has been passed down through generations from our ancestors.","furigana":"せんぞだいだいう|つ|ふる|かたな"},{"japanese":"お盆には、家族でご先祖様のお墓参りに行きます。","romaji":"Obon ni wa, kazoku de gosenzosama no ohakamairi ni ikimasu.","german":"At Obon, my family goes to visit our ancestors' graves.","furigana":"ぼん|かぞく|せんぞさま|はかまい|い"},{"japanese":"私の先祖は江戸時代、この村で米を作っていたらしい。","romaji":"Watashi no senzo wa Edo jidai, kono mura de kome o tsukutte ita rashii.","german":"My ancestors apparently grew rice in this village in the Edo period.","furigana":"わたし|せんぞ|えどじだい|むら|こめ|つく"}],"notes":"先祖 are the \"ancestors\" of a family. Respectfully you say ご先祖様, especially in connection with お盆 and grave visits (お墓参り). Common is 先祖代々 \"for generations\" (先祖代々の土地). The synonym 祖先 sounds more objective and is also used biologically (人類の祖先 \"ancestors of humankind\"); for a single family 先祖 is more usual.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自衛のための殺人は合法なのでしょうか。","romaji":"Jiei no tame no satsujin wa gōhō na no deshō ka.","german":"Is killing in self-defense legal?","furigana":"じえい|さつじん|ごうほう"},{"japanese":"詐欺の被害にあわないよう、一人一人が自衛することが大切だ。","romaji":"Sagi no higai ni awanai you, hitorihitori ga jiei suru koto ga taisetsu da.","german":"To avoid becoming a victim of fraud, it is important for each person to protect themselves.","furigana":"さぎ|ひがい|ひとりひとり|じえい|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"兄は高校を卒業して、自衛隊に入った。","romaji":"Ani wa koukou o sotsugyou shite, jieitai ni haitta.","german":"My older brother graduated from high school and joined the Self-Defense Forces.","furigana":"あに|こうこう|そつぎょう|じえいたい|はい"}],"notes":"自衛 means \"self-defense, self-protection\": 自衛のため, 自衛手段, 自衛する \"to protect oneself\" (also against fraud or infection). Well known is 自衛隊, Japan's \"Self-Defense Forces\". In criminal law, by contrast, self-defense is 正当防衛; national defense is 防衛 (防衛省).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私には複数のニックネームとアカウントがあります。","romaji":"Watashi ni wa fukusū no nikkunēmu to akaunto ga arimasu.","german":"I have multiple nicknames and accounts.","furigana":"わたし|ふくすう"},{"japanese":"この問題には、正しい答えが複数あります。","romaji":"Kono mondai ni wa, tadashii kotae ga fukusuu arimasu.","german":"This question has multiple correct answers.","furigana":"もんだい|ただ|こた|ふくすう"},{"japanese":"英語では、名詞の複数形にsをつけることが多い。","romaji":"Eigo de wa, meishi no fukusuukei ni s o tsukeru koto ga ooi.","german":"In English, an s is often added to make a noun plural.","furigana":"えいご|めいし|ふくすうけい|おお"}],"notes":"複数 means \"several, multiple\" and usually stands before the noun with の: 複数の人, 複数のアカウント; adverbially 複数回 \"several times\". It sounds more objective than いくつかの or たくさん and is typical of forms, instructions and news (複数回答可 \"multiple answers allowed\"). In grammar, 複数形 is the \"plural\", the opposite is 単数 \"singular\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この音声ファイルの雑音を消したいんです。","romaji":"Kono onsei fairu no zatsuon o keshitai n desu.","german":"I want to remove the noise from this audio file.","furigana":"おんせい|ざつおん|け"},{"japanese":"電話に雑音が入って、相手の声がよく聞こえない。","romaji":"Denwa ni zatsuon ga haitte, aite no koe ga yoku kikoenai.","german":"Static came onto the phone line, and I can't hear the other person's voice well.","furigana":"でんわ|ざつおん|はい|あいて|こえ|き"},{"japanese":"周りの雑音は気にしないで、自分のやり方を続けなさい。","romaji":"Mawari no zatsuon wa ki ni shinaide, jibun no yarikata o tsuzukenasai.","german":"Ignore the noise around you and keep going your own way.","furigana":"まわ|ざつおん|き|じぶん|かた|つづ"}],"notes":"雑音 is disturbing background noise: static on the radio or phone (雑音が入る), noise in recordings (雑音を消す). Figuratively it also means \"chatter, unqualified criticism\" from outside (周りの雑音). Distinction: 騒音 is loud, bothersome noise (traffic, construction), 物音 a single sound that one hears.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"祖父の一日は、銭湯から始まります。","romaji":"Sofu no ichinichi wa, sentō kara hajimarimasu.","german":"My grandfather's day begins at the public bathhouse.","furigana":"そふ|いちにち|せんとう|はじ"},{"japanese":"家のお風呂が壊れたので、近くの銭湯に行った。","romaji":"Ie no ofuro ga kowareta node, chikaku no sentou ni itta.","german":"My bath at home broke, so I went to the nearby public bathhouse.","furigana":"いえ|ふろ|こわ|ちか|せんとう|い"},{"japanese":"銭湯では、タオルを湯船に入れてはいけません。","romaji":"Sentou de wa, taoru o yubune ni irete wa ikemasen.","german":"At a public bathhouse, you must not put your towel in the bathtub.","furigana":"せんとう|ゆぶね|い"}],"notes":"銭湯 is a public bathhouse in a residential neighborhood that you go to for an entrance fee (銭 \"coin\", 湯 \"hot water\"): 銭湯に行く, 銭湯に入る. Unlike 温泉, it uses ordinary heated water, not a natural hot spring. Larger modern facilities with saunas are called スーパー銭湯. The rules include washing before bathing and not dipping the towel into the bath.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼に振られて落ち込んでいるので、パリへ傷心旅行に行くんです。","romaji":"Kare ni furarete ochikonde iru node, Pari e shoushin ryokou ni iku n desu.","german":"He dumped me and I'm down, so I'm going to Paris on a trip to heal my broken heart.","furigana":"かれ|ふ|お|こ|しょうしんりょこう|い"},{"japanese":"傷心の彼女を、友達がやさしく慰めた。","romaji":"Shoushin no kanojo o, tomodachi ga yasashiku nagusameta.","german":"Her friends gently comforted the heartbroken girl.","furigana":"しょうしん|かのじょ|ともだち|なぐさ"},{"japanese":"試合に負けた選手たちは、傷心のまま帰国した。","romaji":"Shiai ni maketa senshutachi wa, shoushin no mama kikoku shita.","german":"The players who lost the match returned home heartbroken.","furigana":"しあい|ま|せんしゅ|しょうしん|きこく"}],"notes":"傷心 is an elevated word for \"heartbreak, deep sorrow\", usually after a breakup or defeat. Typical are 傷心の〜 (傷心の彼女), 傷心のまま and 傷心旅行 \"a trip to get over heartbreak\". In conversation people more often say 落ち込む or 失恋してつらい. Grammatically 傷心 is a noun that modifies nouns with の.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その事故による死者は三名、傷者は二百人以上にのぼるという。","romaji":"Sono jiko niyoru shisha wa sanmei, shōsha wa ni hyakunin ijō ni noboru to iu.","german":"It is said that the accident left three dead and more than two hundred injured.","furigana":"じこ|ししゃ|さんめい|しょうしゃ|にひゃくにんいじょう"},{"japanese":"傷者は全員、近くの病院に運ばれた。","romaji":"Shousha wa zen'in, chikaku no byouin ni hakobareta.","german":"All the injured were taken to a nearby hospital.","furigana":"しょうしゃ|ぜんいん|ちか|びょういん|はこ"},{"japanese":"地震のあと、医者たちが集まって傷者の手当てをした。","romaji":"Jishin no ato, ishatachi ga atsumatte shousha no teate o shita.","german":"After the earthquake, doctors gathered and treated the injured.","furigana":"じしん|いしゃ|あつ|しょうしゃ|てあ"}],"notes":"傷者 means \"injured person\" and belongs to the language of news and reports. On its own it is rather rare; much more common are 負傷者 \"injured person\" and the compound 死傷者 \"dead and injured\". In everyday speech you say けが人. Typical are statements of numbers: 傷者は〜人にのぼる \"the number of injured amounts to ...\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"我らが自民党が今年は熱いな。もう誰も我々のことを「愚民党」なんて呼ぶことはないだろう。","romaji":"Warera ga Jimintō ga kotoshi wa atsui na. Mō dare mo wareware no koto o \"Gumintō\" nante yobu koto wa nai darō.","german":"Our LDP is on fire this year. No one will call us the \"Fool's Party\" anymore.","furigana":"われ|じみんとう|ことし|あつ|だれ|われわれ|ぐみんとう|よ"},{"japanese":"自民党は1955年に結成された。","romaji":"Jimintō wa 1955-nen ni kessei sareta.","german":"The Liberal Democratic Party was formed in 1955.","furigana":"じみんとう|ねん|けっせい"},{"japanese":"今回の選挙で、自民党は議席を減らした。","romaji":"Konkai no senkyo de, Jimintō wa giseki o herashita.","german":"In this election, the Liberal Democratic Party lost seats.","furigana":"こんかい|せんきょ|じみんとう|ぎせき|へ"}],"notes":"自民党 is the short form of 自由民主党, the \"Liberal Democratic Party\" (LDP), which has governed Japan almost continuously since its founding in 1955. Important related terms are 与党 \"ruling party\", 野党 \"opposition\" and 総裁 \"party president\". All party names are formed on the pattern 〜党.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"最初に気づいたのは、彼が共産党員だということでした。","romaji":"Saisho ni kizuita no wa, kare ga kyousantouin da toiu koto deshita.","german":"The first thing I noticed was that he is a Communist Party member.","furigana":"さいしょ|き|かれ|きょうさんとういん"},{"japanese":"中国では、共産党が長い間政治の中心にある。","romaji":"Chuugoku de wa, kyousantou ga nagai aida seiji no chuushin ni aru.","german":"In China, the Communist Party has long been at the center of politics.","furigana":"ちゅうごく|きょうさんとう|なが|あいだせいじ|ちゅうしん"},{"japanese":"日本にも共産党があり、国会に議員がいる。","romaji":"Nihon ni mo kyousantou ga ari, kokkai ni giin ga iru.","german":"Japan also has a Communist Party, and it has members in the Diet.","furigana":"にほん|きょうさんとう|こっかい|ぎいん"}],"notes":"共産党 is the \"Communist Party\", e.g. 日本共産党 or 中国共産党. A member is 共産党員, the ideology is 共産主義 \"communism\". In conversation party names are often shortened; 共産党 itself is already the usual short form of 日本共産党. Related terms: 政党 \"political party\", 与党 \"ruling party\", 野党 \"opposition party\".","levelNote":"Compound: 共産 (N2) + 党 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ある共和党の党員の演説にとても感動しました。","romaji":"Aru kyōwatō no tōin no enzetsu ni totemo kandō shimashita.","german":"I was deeply moved by the speech of a certain Republican Party member.","furigana":"きょうわとう|とういん|えんぜつ|かんどう"},{"japanese":"その党の党員数は、年々減っている。","romaji":"Sono tou no touinsuu wa, nennen hette iru.","german":"The number of members of that party is decreasing year by year.","furigana":"とう|とういんすう|ねんねんへ"},{"japanese":"党員になれば、党首を選ぶ選挙で投票できる。","romaji":"Touin ni nareba, toushu o erabu senkyo de touhyou dekiru.","german":"If you become a party member, you can vote in the election for the party leader.","furigana":"とういん|とうしゅ|えら|せんきょ|とうひょう"}],"notes":"党員 is a \"party member\"; usually the party name comes before it with の or directly as a compound (共産党員, 自民党員). Typical: 党員になる, 党員数 \"number of members\", 党員集会. Distinctions: 議員 is an elected member of a legislature, 党首 is the party leader. Not every 党員 is a politician - it can be any ordinary member.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"社会党の主張は現実的ではないような気がする。","romaji":"Shakaitō no shuchō wa genjitsuteki de wa nai yō na ki ga suru.","german":"I feel that the Socialist Party's claims are not realistic.","furigana":"しゃかいとう|しゅちょう|げんじつてき|き"},{"japanese":"祖父は若いころ、社会党を支持していた。","romaji":"Sofu wa wakai koro, shakaitou o shiji shite ita.","german":"When he was young, my grandfather supported the Socialist Party.","furigana":"そふ|わか|しゃかいとう|しじ"},{"japanese":"日本社会党は、一九九六年に社会民主党と名前を変えた。","romaji":"Nihon Shakaitou wa, sen kyuuhyaku kyuujuu roku nen ni Shakai Minshutou to namae o kaeta.","german":"The Japan Socialist Party changed its name to the Social Democratic Party in 1996.","furigana":"にほんしゃかいとう||しゃかいみんしゅとう|なまえ|か"}],"notes":"社会党 is a \"Socialist Party\". In Japan this usually means the former 日本社会党, for a long time the largest opposition party, which renamed itself 社会民主党 (short 社民党) in 1996. Foreign parties are also called this, for example the French 社会党. Related: 社会主義 \"socialism\", 野党 \"opposition\".","levelNote":"Compound: 社会 (N4) + 党 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「生卵アレルギーはありますか？」「私の知る限りではありません。」","romaji":"Nama tamago arerugī wa arimasu ka? Watashi no shiru kagiri de wa arimasen.","german":"\"Do you have an allergy to raw eggs?\" \"Not as far as I know.\"","furigana":"なまたまご|わたし|し|かぎ"},{"japanese":"日本では、ご飯に生卵をかけて食べる人が多い。","romaji":"Nihon de wa, gohan ni namatamago o kakete taberu hito ga ooi.","german":"In Japan, many people eat raw egg over rice.","furigana":"にほん|はん|なまたまご|た|ひと|おお"},{"japanese":"生卵は冷蔵庫に入れて保存してください。","romaji":"Namatamago wa reizouko ni irete hozon shite kudasai.","german":"Please store raw eggs in the refrigerator.","furigana":"なまたまご|れいぞうこ|い|ほぞん"}],"notes":"生卵 is a \"raw egg\" (生 \"raw\"). In Japan eggs are often eaten raw, for example as 卵かけご飯 (raw egg over rice) or as a dip for すき焼き. Counterparts are ゆで卵 \"boiled egg\", 半熟卵 \"soft-boiled egg\" and 温泉卵 \"egg cooked at low temperature\". Reading: なまたまご.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"僕が君のゆで卵を盗んだと思っているのなら、とんだお門違いだよ。","romaji":"Boku ga kimi no yude tamago o nusunda to omotte iru no nara, tonda okadochigai da yo.","german":"If you think I stole your boiled egg, you've got the wrong guy.","furigana":"ぼく|きみ|たまご|ぬす|おも|かどちが"},{"japanese":"朝ごはんに、ゆで卵を二つ食べた。","romaji":"Asagohan ni, yudetamago o futatsu tabeta.","german":"I ate two boiled eggs for breakfast.","furigana":"あさ|たまご|ふた|た"},{"japanese":"ゆで卵の殻がうまくむけない。","romaji":"Yudetamago no kara ga umaku mukenai.","german":"The shell of the boiled egg won't peel off properly.","furigana":"たまご|から"}],"notes":"ゆで卵 is an egg \"boiled\" in water, from ゆでる \"to boil in water\" (also written 茹で卵). Depending on cooking time, one distinguishes 固ゆで \"hard-boiled\" and 半熟 \"soft-boiled, medium-boiled\". Typical phrases: ゆで卵を作る, ゆで卵の殻をむく \"to peel the boiled egg\". A fried egg, by contrast, is called 目玉焼き.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"従業員同士の私語は厳禁です。","romaji":"Jūgyōin dōshi no shigo wa genkin desu.","german":"Private chatter among employees is strictly prohibited.","furigana":"じゅうぎょういんどうし|しご|げんきん"},{"japanese":"ガソリンスタンドでは火気厳禁です。","romaji":"Gasorin sutando de wa kaki genkin desu.","german":"Open flames are strictly prohibited at gas stations.","furigana":"かきげんきん"},{"japanese":"この部屋は土足厳禁なので、靴を脱いでください。","romaji":"Kono heya wa dosoku genkin na node, kutsu o nuide kudasai.","german":"Street shoes are strictly prohibited in this room, so please take off your shoes.","furigana":"へや|どそくげんきん|くつ|ぬ"}],"notes":"厳禁 is a \"strict prohibition\" and clearly stronger than 禁止. It appears mainly on signs and in regulations, often directly after the prohibited thing: 火気厳禁 \"open flames strictly prohibited\", 土足厳禁 \"no street shoes\", 〜は厳禁です. As a verb, 厳禁する is rare; you mostly hear 〜は厳禁だ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私達は密会によくこの部屋を利用しています。","romaji":"Watashitachi wa mikkai ni yoku kono heya o riyō shite imasu.","german":"We often use this room for our secret meetings.","furigana":"わたしたち|みっかい|へや|りよう"},{"japanese":"週刊誌に、人気俳優と歌手の密会が報じられた。","romaji":"Shuukanshi ni, ninki haiyuu to kashu no mikkai ga houjirareta.","german":"A weekly magazine reported a secret meeting between a popular actor and a singer.","furigana":"しゅうかんし|にんきはいゆう|かしゅ|みっかい|ほう"},{"japanese":"スパイは夜の公園で、外国の役人と密会していた。","romaji":"Supai wa yoru no kouen de, gaikoku no yakunin to mikkai shite ita.","german":"The spy was secretly meeting a foreign official in the park at night.","furigana":"よる|こうえん|がいこく|やくにん|みっかい"}],"notes":"密会 is a \"secret meeting\", often with the flavor of an affair or scandal: 〜と密会する, 密会が報じられる. It is typical of tabloids, crime stories and spy stories. As a する-verb it is intransitive; the person takes と. A neutral meeting is simply 会う or 会合, and a date is デート.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いつの時代でも、密告をすることで上司に取り入って、出世をねらう人間がいます。","romaji":"Itsu no jidai de mo, mikkoku o suru koto de jōshi ni toriitte, shusse o nerau ningen ga imasu.","german":"In every age, there are people who curry favor with their boss by informing on others, aiming for promotion.","furigana":"じだい|みっこく|じょうし|と|い|しゅっせ|にんげん"},{"japanese":"誰かが警察に密告したらしく、犯人はすぐに捕まった。","romaji":"Dareka ga keisatsu ni mikkoku shita rashiku, hannin wa sugu ni tsukamatta.","german":"Someone seems to have tipped off the police, and the culprit was caught immediately.","furigana":"だれ|けいさつ|みっこく|はんにん|つか"},{"japanese":"戦争中は、近所の人に密告されるのが怖くて、誰も本音を言わなかった。","romaji":"Sensouchuu wa, kinjo no hito ni mikkoku sareru no ga kowakute, daremo honne o iwanakatta.","german":"During the war, everyone was afraid of being reported by their neighbors, so no one said what they really thought.","furigana":"せんそうちゅう|きんじょ|ひと|みっこく|こわ|だれ|ほんね|い"}],"notes":"密告 is secretly reporting someone's misconduct to the authorities or superiors: \"denunciation, informing on someone\". Typical: 警察に密告する, 〜を密告する, 密告される. The word mostly carries a negative flavor of betrayal. Neutral is 通報する \"to report\" (for example an accident), and childish and colloquial is 告げ口する \"to tattle\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"俺は一度麻薬の密輸をする話を持ちかけられたことがあるよ。もちろん断ったけどね。","romaji":"Ore wa ichido mayaku no mitsuyu o suru hanashi o mochikakerareta koto ga aru yo. Mochiron kotowatta kedo ne.","german":"I was once offered a deal to smuggle drugs. Of course, I turned it down.","furigana":"おれ|いちどまやく|みつゆ|はなし|も|ことわ"},{"japanese":"空港で、珍しい動物を密輸しようとした男が逮捕された。","romaji":"Kuukou de, mezurashii doubutsu o mitsuyu shiyou to shita otoko ga taiho sareta.","german":"At the airport, a man who tried to smuggle in a rare animal was arrested.","furigana":"くうこう|めずら|どうぶつ|みつゆ|おとこ|たいほ"},{"japanese":"昔この港では、密輸がさかんに行われていたそうだ。","romaji":"Mukashi kono minato de wa, mitsuyu ga sakan ni okonawarete ita sou da.","german":"I hear smuggling used to be rampant at this port in the past.","furigana":"むかし|みなと|みつゆ|おこな"}],"notes":"密輸 means \"smuggling\": 〜を密輸する, 麻薬の密輸, 密輸品 \"contraband\", 密輸業者 \"smuggler\". More precisely, one distinguishes 密輸入 \"smuggling in\" and 密輸出 \"smuggling out\". The word is typical of news and crime stories. As a する-verb it is transitive: what is smuggled takes を.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"世界で一番危険な伝染病は何ですか。","romaji":"Sekai de ichiban kiken na densenbyou wa nan desu ka.","german":"What is the most dangerous infectious disease in the world?","furigana":"せかい|いちばんきけん|でんせんびょう|なん"},{"japanese":"昔は、伝染病で多くの人が亡くなった。","romaji":"Mukashi wa, densenbyou de ooku no hito ga nakunatta.","german":"In the past, many people died of infectious diseases.","furigana":"むかし|でんせんびょう|おお|ひと|な"},{"japanese":"その村では伝染病が流行して、学校が一か月休みになった。","romaji":"Sono mura de wa densenbyou ga ryuukou shite, gakkou ga ikkagetsu yasumi ni natta.","german":"An infectious disease broke out in that village, and school was closed for a month.","furigana":"むら|でんせんびょう|りゅうこう|がっこう|いっ|げつやす"}],"notes":"伝染病 is a \"contagious disease, epidemic\": 伝染病が流行する/広がる, 伝染病にかかる. The word sounds somewhat old-fashioned today; in medicine and official language people now mostly say 感染症 \"infectious disease\". The related verb is 伝染する \"to be transmitted\"; in everyday speech, for \"to infect\" people simply say うつる/うつす.","levelNote":"Compound: 伝染 (N2) + 病 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの事件は、彼の経歴に大きな汚点を残した。","romaji":"Ano jiken wa, kare no keireki ni ookina oten o nokoshita.","german":"That incident left a big blemish on his career.","furigana":"じけん|かれ|けいれき|おお|おてん|のこ"},{"japanese":"この負けは、チームの歴史で最大の汚点だ。","romaji":"Kono make wa, chiimu no rekishi de saidai no oten da.","german":"This loss is the biggest stain in the team's history.","furigana":"ま|れきし|さいだい|おてん"},{"japanese":"会社の汚点にならないよう、不正は絶対に許さない。","romaji":"Kaisha no oten ni naranai you, fusei wa zettai ni yurusanai.","german":"We will never tolerate wrongdoing, so that it does not become a blemish on the company.","furigana":"かいしゃ|おてん|ふせい|ぜったい|ゆる"}],"notes":"汚点 is a \"blemish, stain on one's reputation\" and is used almost only figuratively: 経歴の汚点, 歴史に汚点を残す, 〜の汚点となる. For a real stain on clothing, people say シミ or 汚れ in everyday speech. It is not synonymous with the internet slang 黒歴史, which means more an \"embarrassing past\" that one laughs about; 汚点 is more serious and sounds literary.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"カップラーメンでは、私のラーメン欲求をみたすことは出来ません。","romaji":"Kappurāmen de wa, watashi no rāmen yokkyū o mitasu koto wa dekimasen.","german":"A cup ramen can't satisfy my craving for ramen.","furigana":"わたし|よっきゅう|でき"},{"japanese":"人には、誰かに認められたいという欲求がある。","romaji":"Hito ni wa, dareka ni mitomeraretai to iu yokkyuu ga aru.","german":"People have a desire to be recognized by someone.","furigana":"ひと|だれ|みと|よっきゅう"},{"japanese":"毎日残業ばかりで、欲求不満がたまっている。","romaji":"Mainichi zangyou bakari de, yokkyuu fuman ga tamatte iru.","german":"I work overtime every day, and my frustration is building up.","furigana":"まいにちざんぎょう|よっきゅうふまん"}],"notes":"欲求 is a \"need, desire\", especially in the psychological sense: 欲求を満たす \"to satisfy a need\", 〜したいという欲求, 承認欲求 \"need for recognition\", 欲求不満 \"frustration\". It is used almost only as a noun; 欲求する is rare. Distinctions: 欲望 is stronger and rather negative, \"lust, greed\"; 欲 is colloquial \"desire, greed\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どうしてキリスト教徒と仏教徒の中には、肉欲を罪深いものだとして否定する人がいるのですか。","romaji":"Dōshite kirisutokyōto to bukkyōto no naka ni wa, nikuyoku o tsumibukai mono da toshite hitei suru hito ga iru no desu ka.","german":"Why do some Christians and Buddhists deny carnal desire as sinful?","furigana":"きょうと|ぶっきょうと|なか|にくよく|つみぶか|ひてい|ひと"},{"japanese":"修行中の僧は、肉欲を断たなければならない。","romaji":"Shugyouchuu no sou wa, nikuyoku o tatanakereba naranai.","german":"A monk in training must renounce carnal desire.","furigana":"しゅぎょうちゅう|そう|にくよく|た"},{"japanese":"その小説は、肉欲に負けて全てを失う男の話だ。","romaji":"Sono shousetsu wa, nikuyoku ni makete subete o ushinau otoko no hanashi da.","german":"That novel is about a man who gives in to carnal desire and loses everything.","furigana":"しょうせつ|にくよく|ま|すべ|うしな|おとこ|はなし"}],"notes":"肉欲 is \"carnal desire, sensual lust\" and comes from religious and literary language: 肉欲に溺れる \"to indulge in carnal desire\", 肉欲を断つ \"to renounce it\". More neutral and medical is 性欲 \"sexual desire\". The component 欲 \"desire\" also appears in 食欲 \"appetite\" and 物欲 \"greed for possessions\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今年の冬は例年より寒くて雪もたくさん降ったけど、本当に地球は温暖化しているのかな。","romaji":"Kotoshi no fuyu wa reinen yori samukute yuki mo takusan futta kedo, hontou ni chikyuu wa ondanka shite iru no ka na.","german":"This winter was colder than usual and there was a lot of snow, but is the Earth really warming?","furigana":"ことし|ふゆ|れいねん|さむ|ゆき|ふ|ほんとう|ちきゅう|おんだんか"},{"japanese":"この地方は一年中温暖な気候に恵まれている。","romaji":"Kono chihou wa ichinenjuu ondan na kikou ni megumarete iru.","german":"This region is blessed with a mild climate all year round.","furigana":"ちほう|いちねんじゅうおんだん|きこう|めぐ"},{"japanese":"定年後は温暖な所でのんびり暮らしたい。","romaji":"Teinengo wa ondan na tokoro de nonbiri kurashitai.","german":"After retirement, I want to live a relaxed life in a warm place.","furigana":"ていねんご|おんだん|ところ|く"}],"notes":"温暖 describes almost only climate and regions: 温暖な気候, 温暖な地域. For a warm room, water or clothing, you say 暖かい or 温かい, not 温暖. The derivation 温暖化 \"warming\" is very common, especially 地球温暖化 \"global warming\", also as a する-verb 温暖化する. The opposite is 寒冷 \"cold (climate)\"; overall it belongs more to written and news language.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は内に秘めるタイプなので、心配しています。","romaji":"Kare wa uchi ni himeru taipu na node, shinpai shite imasu.","german":"He is the type who keeps things inside, so I'm worried.","furigana":"かれ|うち|ひ|しんぱい"},{"japanese":"彼女は胸に秘めた思いを、ついに手紙に書いた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa mune ni himeta omoi o, tsui ni tegami ni kaita.","german":"She finally wrote in a letter the feelings she had kept in her heart.","furigana":"かのじょ|むね|ひ|おも|てがみ|か"},{"japanese":"この子は大きな可能性を秘めている。","romaji":"Kono ko wa ookina kanousei o himete iru.","german":"This child holds great potential.","furigana":"こ|おお|かのうせい|ひ"}],"notes":"秘める means \"to keep something hidden within oneself, to keep to oneself\" - usually feelings, intentions or abilities, not concrete objects. Typical are 胸に秘める / 心に秘める \"to carry in one's heart\" and 可能性を秘める \"to hold potential\", mostly as 秘めた + noun or 秘めている. To physically hide something is 隠す. The word sounds elevated to literary; related is 秘密 \"secret\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この単語を訳すのは難しい。","romaji":"Kono tango o yakusu no wa muzukashii.","german":"This word is hard to translate.","furigana":"たんご|やく|むずか"},{"japanese":"この手紙を日本語に訳してもらえませんか。","romaji":"Kono tegami o nihongo ni yakushite moraemasen ka.","german":"Could you translate this letter into Japanese for me?","furigana":"てがみ|にほんご|やく"},{"japanese":"好きな英語の歌の歌詞を自分で訳してみた。","romaji":"Suki na eigo no uta no kashi o jibun de yakushite mita.","german":"I tried translating the lyrics of my favorite English song myself.","furigana":"す|えいご|うた|かし|じぶん|やく"}],"notes":"訳す is the everyday verb for \"to translate\": X を Y に訳す \"to translate X into Y\", the source language takes から: 英語から日本語に訳す. 翻訳する means the same but sounds more formal and is preferred for professional translations; oral interpreting is 通訳する. The noun 訳 (やく) appears in 和訳 and 英訳; 訳 read わけ \"reason\" is a different word.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 16"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この本の訳者の名前、何て読むか知ってる？","romaji":"Kono hon no yakusha no namae, nan te yomu ka shitteru?","german":"Do you know how to read the name of this book's translator?","furigana":"ほん|やくしゃ|なまえ|なん|よ|し"},{"japanese":"訳者のあとがきを読むと、作品がもっと分かる。","romaji":"Yakusha no atogaki o yomu to, sakuhin ga motto wakaru.","german":"Reading the translator's afterword helps you understand the work better.","furigana":"やくしゃ|よ|さくひん|わ"},{"japanese":"同じ小説でも、訳者によって印象がかなり変わる。","romaji":"Onaji shousetsu demo, yakusha ni yotte inshou ga kanari kawaru.","german":"Even for the same novel, the impression changes quite a lot depending on the translator.","furigana":"おな|しょうせつ|やくしゃ|いんしょう|か"}],"notes":"訳者 is the translator of a particular work and appears mainly in books and reviews: 訳者あとがき \"translator's afterword\", 著者と訳者 \"author and translator\". In general, \"someone who translates\" is 翻訳者, as a profession 翻訳家; an interpreter is 通訳. In everyday conversation 訳者 sounds rather literary.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本語の言葉の中には、ぴったりの英語の訳語がないものもある。","romaji":"Nihongo no kotoba no naka ni wa, pittari no eigo no yakugo ga nai mono mo aru.","german":"Among Japanese words, some have no perfect English equivalent.","furigana":"にほんご|ことば|なか|えいご|やくご"},{"japanese":"辞書を引いて、この文に合う訳語を探した。","romaji":"Jisho o hiite, kono bun ni au yakugo o sagashita.","german":"I looked in the dictionary for a translation word that fits this sentence.","furigana":"じしょ|ひ|ぶん|あ|やくご|さが"},{"japanese":"この新しい専門用語には、まだ決まった訳語がない。","romaji":"Kono atarashii senmon yougo ni wa, mada kimatta yakugo ga nai.","german":"This new technical term doesn't have a fixed translation yet.","furigana":"あたら|せんもんようご|き|やくご"}],"notes":"訳語 is the individual word that corresponds to a foreign-language word in a translation - that is, the equivalent as in a dictionary, not the translation of a whole text (that is 訳 or 翻訳). Typical are 訳語を探す, 訳語を当てる \"to choose an equivalent\" and 適切な訳語 \"a fitting equivalent\". The word belongs more to technical and written language.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本語から英訳することしかできません。","romaji":"Nihongo kara eiyaku suru koto shika dekimasen.","german":"I can only translate from Japanese into English.","furigana":"にほんご|えいやく"},{"japanese":"このメールを英訳してもらえますか。","romaji":"Kono meeru o eiyaku shite moraemasu ka.","german":"Could you translate this email into English?","furigana":"えいやく"},{"japanese":"あの小説の英訳が来年出版されるそうだ。","romaji":"Ano shousetsu no eiyaku ga rainen shuppan sareru sou da.","german":"I hear an English translation of that novel will be published next year.","furigana":"しょうせつ|えいやく|らいねんしゅっぱん"}],"notes":"英訳 is always translation into English, not from English - that would be 和訳. As a する-verb: X を英訳する; the source language takes から. As a noun it also denotes the result: 英訳版 \"English edition\". From school, one knows the exercise types 和文英訳 \"Japanese -> English\" and 英文和訳 \"English -> Japanese\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いくつか小さな間違いはあるものの、うまく和訳できていると思いますよ。","romaji":"Ikutsu ka chiisana machigai wa aru mono no, umaku wayaku dekite iru to omoimasu yo.","german":"Although there are a few small mistakes, I think you translated it into Japanese well.","furigana":"ちい|まちが|わやく|おも"},{"japanese":"次の英文を和訳しなさい。","romaji":"Tsugi no eibun o wayaku shinasai.","german":"Translate the following English sentences into Japanese.","furigana":"つぎ|えいぶん|わやく"},{"japanese":"この本はまだ和訳が出ていないので、英語で読むしかない。","romaji":"Kono hon wa mada wayaku ga dete inai node, eigo de yomu shika nai.","german":"This book doesn't have a Japanese translation yet, so I have no choice but to read it in English.","furigana":"ほん|わやく|で|えいご|よ"}],"notes":"和訳 is translation into Japanese, usually from English; 和 stands for \"Japan\" here as in 和食. As a する-verb: X を和訳する. The counterpart is 英訳. For published translations of foreign books, 邦訳 is also used; 和訳 is known mainly from school and exams (英文和訳).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大人でも、おしっこを漏らすことはありますよ。","romaji":"Otona de mo, oshikko o morasu koto wa arimasu yo.","german":"Even adults wet themselves sometimes.","furigana":"おとな|も"},{"japanese":"この話は誰にも漏らさないでください。","romaji":"Kono hanashi wa dare ni mo morasanaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't tell anyone about this.","furigana":"はなし|だれ|も"},{"japanese":"部長は会議のあとで、新しい計画への不満を漏らした。","romaji":"Buchou wa kaigi no ato de, atarashii keikaku e no fuman o morashita.","german":"After the meeting, the department head voiced his dissatisfaction with the new plan.","furigana":"ぶちょう|かいぎ|あたら|けいかく|ふまん|も"}],"notes":"漏らす is transitive; the intransitive counterpart is 漏れる (水が漏れる \"water leaks\"). Besides \"to let leak\", its most important use is \"to leak, blab\": 秘密を漏らす, 情報を漏らす. It also means \"to voice, let out\": 不満を漏らす, ため息を漏らす; colloquially also \"to wet oneself\" (おしっこを漏らす). As a suffix, 〜漏らす means \"to miss by oversight\": 聞き漏らす.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"山を越すと、海が見えてきた。","romaji":"Yama o kosu to, umi ga miete kita.","german":"When we crossed the mountain, the sea came into view.","furigana":"やま|こ|うみ|み"},{"japanese":"庭の木は無事に冬を越して、春に花を咲かせた。","romaji":"Niwa no ki wa buji ni fuyu o koshite, haru ni hana o sakaseta.","german":"The tree in the garden got through the winter safely and bloomed in spring.","furigana":"にわ|き|ぶじ|ふゆ|こ|はる|はな|さ"},{"japanese":"来月、駅の近くのアパートに越すことになりました。","romaji":"Raigetsu, eki no chikaku no apāto ni kosu koto ni narimashita.","german":"Next month, I'm going to move to an apartment near the station.","furigana":"らいげつ|えき|ちか|こ"}],"notes":"越す is transitive (〜を越す) and has several meanings: \"to cross\" (山を越す), \"to get through\" (冬を越す), \"to exceed\" (with numbers mostly written 超す: 30度を超す) and \"to move house\" (新しい家に越す, more often 引っ越す). The related 越える is used similarly with を and is somewhat more common in the sense of \"to cross\". As a respectful form one knows お越しください \"please come\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"たくさんの人がオリンピックに浮かれすぎている。","romaji":"Takusan no hito ga orinpikku ni ukare sugite iru.","german":"Too many people are getting carried away over the Olympics.","furigana":"ひと|う"},{"japanese":"合格して浮かれていたら、電車に財布を忘れてしまった。","romaji":"Goukaku shite ukarete itara, densha ni saifu o wasurete shimatta.","german":"I was elated about passing, and then I left my wallet on the train.","furigana":"ごうかく|う|でんしゃ|さいふ|わす"},{"japanese":"お祭りの雰囲気に浮かれて、ついお金を使いすぎた。","romaji":"Omatsuri no fun'iki ni ukarete, tsui okane o tsukaisugita.","german":"Carried away by the festival atmosphere, I ended up spending too much money.","furigana":"まつ|ふんいき|う|かね|つか"}],"notes":"浮かれる is intransitive and describes an exuberant, buoyant mood, often with the undertone that one becomes careless in the process. The trigger takes に: お祭りに浮かれる, 勝利に浮かれる; mostly as 浮かれている or 浮かれて. 浮かれ気分 is \"high spirits\". Unlike はしゃぐ (romping around noisily, often of children), it concerns more the inner mood; simply being happy is 喜ぶ.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コウイチにはコンタクトよりもメガネが似合います。","romaji":"Kouichi ni wa kontakuto yori mo megane ga niaimasu.","german":"Glasses suit Koichi better than contact lenses.","furigana":"にあ"},{"japanese":"その帽子、よく似合ってるね。","romaji":"Sono boushi, yoku niatteru ne.","german":"That hat really suits you.","furigana":"ぼうし|にあ"},{"japanese":"派手な色の服は私には似合わない。","romaji":"Hade na iro no fuku wa watashi ni wa niawanai.","german":"Clothes in flashy colors don't suit me.","furigana":"はで|いろ|ふく|わたし|にあ"}],"notes":"似合う is intransitive: Person に clothing/hairstyle が似合う \"something suits someone\". For the current impression one usually says 似合っている / 似合ってる. Do not confuse it with 合う, which concerns fit (サイズが合う). The noun お似合い describes a well-matched couple (お似合いのカップル); 似合わない can also mean that a behavior does not suit someone.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 14"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"背丈の低い男が千鳥足でバーから出てきて、私の目の前でゲロを吐いたんです。","romaji":"Setake no hikui otoko ga chidoriashi de bā kara dete kite, watashi no me no mae de gero o haita n desu.","german":"A short man came stumbling out of the bar and threw up right in front of me.","furigana":"せたけ|ひく|おとこ|ちどりあし|で|わたし|め|まえ|は"},{"japanese":"子どもの背丈に合わせて、机の高さを変えた。","romaji":"Kodomo no setake ni awasete, tsukue no takasa o kaeta.","german":"I changed the height of the desk to match the child's height.","furigana":"こ|せたけ|あ|つくえ|たか|か"},{"japanese":"庭の草が背丈ほどまで伸びてしまった。","romaji":"Niwa no kusa ga setake hodo made nobite shimatta.","german":"The grass in the garden has grown as tall as a person.","furigana":"にわ|くさ|せたけ|の"}],"notes":"背丈 denotes a person's height or stature, more as a visible impression. The measured value in centimeters is 身長 (身長は170センチ); in everyday speech people usually just say 背 (背が高い). 背丈 often appears in comparisons: 背丈が同じくらい, 背丈ほどの草 \"grass as tall as a person\". It sounds somewhat more descriptive and literary than 背.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"金メダル獲得に意欲をみせた。","romaji":"Kinmedaru kakutoku ni iyoku o miseta.","german":"He showed his eagerness to win the gold medal.","furigana":"きん|かくとく|いよく"},{"japanese":"最近、仕事への意欲がなくなってきた。","romaji":"Saikin, shigoto e no iyoku ga nakunatte kita.","german":"Lately I've been losing my motivation for work.","furigana":"さいきん|しごと|いよく"},{"japanese":"当社では意欲のある若い人を募集しています。","romaji":"Tousha de wa iyoku no aru wakai hito o boshuu shite imasu.","german":"Our company is recruiting motivated young people.","furigana":"とうしゃ|いよく|わか|ひと|ぼしゅう"}],"notes":"意欲 is the active will to do something: 〜への意欲, 〜に意欲を見せる / 燃やす. Common are 意欲がある / ない, 意欲的な \"enthusiastic\" and compounds like 学習意欲 \"motivation to learn\" or 購買意欲 \"willingness to buy\". Compared with the colloquial やる気, 意欲 sounds more formal and is often used in news, job ads and reports.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"こんなに背が高いホビットは見たことがないよ。","romaji":"Konnani se ga takai hobitto wa mita koto ga nai yo.","german":"I've never seen such a tall hobbit.","furigana":"せ|たか|み"},{"japanese":"兄は家族の中でいちばん背が高い。","romaji":"Ani wa kazoku no naka de ichiban se ga takai.","german":"My older brother is the tallest in the family.","furigana":"あに|かぞく|なか|せ|たか"},{"japanese":"背が高いので、満員電車でも周りがよく見える。","romaji":"Se ga takai node, man'in densha demo mawari ga yoku mieru.","german":"Because I'm tall, I can see around me well even on a crowded train.","furigana":"せ|たか|まんいんでんしゃ|まわ|み"}],"notes":"背が高い means \"tall (in stature)\" and is used for people and animals; buildings or mountains are simply 高い. Before a noun, が usually becomes の: 背の高い人. The opposite is 背が低い, and \"to grow taller\" is 背が伸びる. Colloquially you also hear 大きい, but 背が高い refers unambiguously to body height.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 16"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"そろそろ正月飾りの準備をしておかなくちゃ。","romaji":"Sorosoro shōgatsukazari no junbi o shite okanakucha.","german":"I need to start getting the New Year decorations ready soon.","furigana":"しょうがつかざ|じゅんび"},{"japanese":"子どもたちとクリスマスツリーに飾りをつけた。","romaji":"Kodomotachi to kurisumasu tsurii ni kazari o tsuketa.","german":"I put ornaments on the Christmas tree with the kids.","furigana":"こ|かざ"},{"japanese":"部長といっても名前だけの飾りで、何も決められない。","romaji":"Buchou to itte mo namae dake no kazari de, nani mo kimerarenai.","german":"He's called a department head, but it's only a nominal title, and he can't decide anything.","furigana":"ぶちょう|なまえ|かざ|なに|き"}],"notes":"飾り is the noun of 飾る \"to decorate\" and means \"ornament, decoration\". It is common in compounds: 正月飾り, 髪飾り (かみかざり) \"hair ornament\", クリスマスの飾り; typical are 飾りをつける and 飾りを外す. Figuratively, (お)飾り means \"mere façade\": a position without real power is ただの飾り.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あいつは本当に飾り気のない女だ。","romaji":"Aitsu wa hontōni kazarike no nai onna da.","german":"That woman is really unpretentious.","furigana":"ほんとう|かざ|け|おんな"},{"japanese":"飾り気のない部屋だが、清潔で落ち着く。","romaji":"Kazarike no nai heya da ga, seiketsu de ochitsuku.","german":"It's a plain room, but clean and calming.","furigana":"かざ|け|へや|せいけつ|お|つ"},{"japanese":"社長の飾り気のない話し方は社員に人気がある。","romaji":"Shachou no kazarike no nai hanashikata wa shain ni ninki ga aru.","german":"The president's unpretentious way of speaking is popular with the employees.","furigana":"しゃちょう|かざ|け|はな|かた|しゃいん|にんき"}],"notes":"飾り気 is the \"inclination to adorn, affectation\" and appears almost only negated: 飾り気のない / 飾り気がない \"plain, unaffected, natural\". This is mostly praise for people, their way of speaking or a simple style. The affirmative 飾り気がある is rare; someone who puts on airs is more likely 気取っている.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"最近学校で流行っている遊びを教えて下さい。","romaji":"Saikin gakkō de hayatte iru asobi o oshiete kudasai.","german":"Please tell me about the games that are popular at schools these days.","furigana":"さいきんがっこう|はや|あそ|おし|くだ"},{"japanese":"週末、友達の家に遊びに行きました。","romaji":"Shuumatsu, tomodachi no ie ni asobi ni ikimashita.","german":"On the weekend I went to a friend's house to hang out.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|ともだち|いえ|あそ|い"},{"japanese":"これは遊びじゃなくて、仕事です。","romaji":"Kore wa asobi ja nakute, shigoto desu.","german":"This is not play, it's work.","furigana":"あそ|しごと"}],"notes":"遊び is the noun of 遊ぶ and means \"play, amusement, leisure activity\": 子どもの遊び, 新しい遊び. Especially common is 遊びに行く/来る, which usually does not mean \"to go to play\" but \"to visit someone for fun, to go out\". As a contrast to work or seriousness it appears in これは遊びじゃない. Technically it also refers to the play (the free travel) of, for example, a steering wheel.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"高熱で体が火照っています。","romaji":"Kōnetsu de karada ga hotette imasu.","german":"My body is flushed with a high fever.","furigana":"こうねつ|からだ|ほて"},{"japanese":"褒められて恥ずかしくなり、顔が火照った。","romaji":"Homerarete hazukashiku nari, kao ga hotetta.","german":"Being praised made me embarrassed, and my face flushed.","furigana":"ほ|は|かお|ほて"},{"japanese":"お風呂から上がっても、まだ体が火照っている。","romaji":"Ofuro kara agatte mo, mada karada ga hotette iru.","german":"Even after getting out of the bath, my body is still glowing with heat.","furigana":"ふろ|あ|からだ|ほて"}],"notes":"火照る describes the face or body becoming hot and glowing - from fever, shame, alcohol or a hot bath: 顔が火照る, 体が火照る. It is intransitive and, despite the -eru ending, a godan verb (火照って, 火照らない). It is often written in kana (ほてる); the noun is 火照り, e.g. 顔の火照り. Plain 熱い only says \"hot\", without the feeling of flushing up.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"背広のボタンをなくした。","romaji":"Sebiro no botan o nakushita.","german":"I lost a button from my suit.","furigana":"せびろ"},{"japanese":"明日は背広を着て会社に行きます。","romaji":"Ashita wa sebiro o kite kaisha ni ikimasu.","german":"Tomorrow I'm going to work in a suit.","furigana":"あした|せびろ|き|かいしゃ|い"}],"notes":"背広 is a business suit. 背広のボタン assigns the button to the suit; 背広を着る refers to putting on or wearing it. 着て行く means going to a place wearing this suit. The reading is せびろ, with a voiced b and short vowels.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今年の年次大会は無事終了いたしました。","romaji":"Kotoshi no nenji taikai wa buji shūryō itashimashita.","german":"This year's annual convention ended without incident.","furigana":"ことし|ねんじたいかい|ぶじしゅうりょう"},{"japanese":"年次有給休暇がまだ十日も残っている。","romaji":"Nenji yuukyuu kyuuka ga mada tooka mo nokotte iru.","german":"I still have ten days of annual paid leave left.","furigana":"ねんじゆうきゅうきゅうか|とおか|のこ"},{"japanese":"彼と私は入社年次が同じだ。","romaji":"Kare to watashi wa nyuusha nenji ga onaji da.","german":"He and I joined the company in the same year.","furigana":"かれ|わたし|にゅうしゃねんじ|おな"}],"notes":"年次 stands before nouns and means \"annual, yearly\": 年次大会, 年次報告書, 年次有給休暇 (short 年休). Placed after a noun, it denotes the year of joining or graduation: 入社年次, 卒業年次 - important for hierarchy in Japanese companies. It is official and business language; in daily life people say 毎年 or 年に一度.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"四歳になる娘が、見るもの全てを真似するので困っています。","romaji":"Yonsai ni naru musume ga, miru mono subete o mane suru node komatte imasu.","german":"My four-year-old daughter imitates everything she sees, which is a problem.","furigana":"よんさい|むすめ|み|すべ|まね|こま"},{"japanese":"人の真似ばかりしないで、自分で考えなさい。","romaji":"Hito no mane bakari shinaide, jibun de kangaenasai.","german":"Don't just imitate others; think for yourself.","furigana":"ひと|まね|じぶん|かんが"},{"japanese":"そんな危ない真似はもうやめてくれ。","romaji":"Sonna abunai mane wa mou yamete kure.","german":"Please stop such dangerous behavior.","furigana":"あぶ|まね"}],"notes":"真似 is \"imitation\"; you say X の真似をする or X を真似する \"to copy X\", and the plain verb for it is 真似る (まねる). ものまね is \"impression\" as a show act. With evaluative adjectives, 真似 means \"behavior, act\", almost always negative: 馬鹿な真似, 危ない真似をするな. It is often written in kana as まね.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"とりあえず、簡易テントを張っておいたよ。","romaji":"Toriaezu, kan'i tento o hatte oita yo.","german":"For now, I've put up a simple tent.","furigana":"かんい|は"},{"japanese":"この申し込みは簡易な手続きで、すぐに終わります。","romaji":"Kono moushikomi wa kan'i na tetsuzuki de, sugu ni owarimasu.","german":"This application uses a simplified procedure and is finished quickly.","furigana":"もう|こ|かんい|てつづ|お"},{"japanese":"大事な書類なので、簡易書留で送った。","romaji":"Daiji na shorui na node, kan'i kakitome de okutta.","german":"It was an important document, so I sent it by simple registered mail.","furigana":"だいじ|しょるい|かんいかきとめ|おく"}],"notes":"簡易 means \"simplified, makeshift\" - a simpler version than the full-fledged one. It usually stands directly before nouns: 簡易テント, 簡易書留 \"simple registered mail\", 簡易検査; as an adjective 簡易な手続き or 簡易的な. Unlike 簡単 \"easy, uncomplicated\", 簡易 emphasizes the reduced equipment or scope. Rather written and official language.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"何時くらいに散歩する予定なんですか？","romaji":"Nanji kurai ni sanpo suru yotei nan desu ka?","german":"About what time are you planning to take a walk?","furigana":"なんじ|さんぽ|よてい"},{"japanese":"犬を連れて河原を散歩しました。","romaji":"Inu o tsurete kawara o sanpo shimashita.","german":"I took a walk along the riverbank with my dog.","furigana":"いぬ|つ||さんぽ"}],"notes":"散歩する means \"to take a walk\". 散歩 alone is the noun \"walk\"; with する it becomes the verb, whose forms are, for example, 散歩します and 散歩した. A place can be given with で; for a route traversed, を is also used, as in 河原を散歩する. 犬を連れて means \"with the dog\" or \"taking the dog along\". The first question asks about the approximate time of a planned walk.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 9"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は厄介な仕事をうまくやってのけた…あることを除いて、だが。","romaji":"Kanojo wa yakkai na shigoto o umaku yatte noketa... aru koto o nozoite, daga.","german":"She pulled off the troublesome job well... except for one thing, that is.","furigana":"かのじょ|やっかい|しごと|のぞ"},{"japanese":"この店は日曜日を除いて、毎日開いています。","romaji":"Kono mise wa nichiyoubi o nozoite, mainichi aite imasu.","german":"This shop is open every day except Sundays.","furigana":"みせ|にちようび|のぞ|まいにちあ"},{"japanese":"田中さんを除いて、全員が賛成した。","romaji":"Tanaka-san o nozoite, zen'in ga sansei shita.","german":"Everyone except Mr. Tanaka agreed.","furigana":"たなか|のぞ|ぜんいん|さんせい"}],"notes":"除いて is the て-form of 除く \"to exclude\" and forms the fixed phrase N を除いて \"except, with the exception of\". It sounds somewhat more formal than 以外: 日曜日以外 and 日曜日を除いて mean the same. In writing and on notices, 〜を除き is common; 〜を除けば means \"if you exclude ...\". The excluded part always takes を.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"墓場の隣に住んだら、お化けがドアをノックすることもあるかもしれない。","romaji":"Hakaba no tonari ni sundara, obake ga doa o nokku suru koto mo aru kamo shirenai.","german":"If you lived next to a graveyard, a ghost might even knock on your door.","furigana":"はかば|となり|す|ば"},{"japanese":"夜の墓場は怖くて、一人では歩けない。","romaji":"Yoru no hakaba wa kowakute, hitori de wa arukenai.","german":"The graveyard at night is scary, and I can't walk there alone.","furigana":"よる|はかば|こわ|ひとり|ある"},{"japanese":"この秘密は墓場まで持っていくつもりだ。","romaji":"Kono himitsu wa hakaba made motte iku tsumori da.","german":"I intend to take this secret to my grave.","furigana":"ひみつ|はかば|も"}],"notes":"墓場 is a cemetery or burial ground, often with an eerie or figurative coloring, for example in ghost stories. The neutral, official words for cemetery are 墓地 or 霊園, and a single grave is (お)墓. Fixed phrases are 墓場まで持っていく \"to take to the grave\" and 揺りかごから墓場まで \"from the cradle to the grave\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その女優はダイエット中であることを否定した。","romaji":"Sono joyū wa daiettochū dearu koto o hitei shita.","german":"The actress denied that she was on a diet.","furigana":"じょゆう|ちゅう|ひてい"},{"japanese":"努力が大切だということは、誰も否定できない。","romaji":"Doryoku ga taisetsu da to iu koto wa, dare mo hitei dekinai.","german":"No one can deny that effort is important.","furigana":"どりょく|たいせつ|だれ|ひてい"},{"japanese":"日本語の否定の形は「〜ない」で作ります。","romaji":"Nihongo no hitei no katachi wa -nai de tsukurimasu.","german":"The negative form in Japanese is made with 「〜ない」.","furigana":"にほんご|ひてい|かたち|つく"}],"notes":"否定 means \"negation, denial\"; as a する-verb: X を否定する \"to deny, to dispute\". 否定できない \"undeniable\" and 否定的な \"negative, dismissive\" are common. The opposite is 肯定 \"affirmation\". In grammar, 否定形 is the negative form. Declining a request, by contrast, is 断る, not 否定する.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ジョギングをしてる時に足の筋を痛めちゃった。","romaji":"Jogingu o shiteru toki ni ashi no suji o itamechatta.","german":"I injured a tendon in my leg while jogging.","furigana":"とき|あし|すじ|いた"},{"japanese":"この映画は筋が複雑で、ちょっと分かりにくい。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa suji ga fukuzatsu de, chotto wakarinikui.","german":"This movie has a complicated plot and is a bit hard to follow.","furigana":"えいが|すじ|ふくざつ|わ"},{"japanese":"君の言っていることは筋が通っている。","romaji":"Kimi no itte iru koto wa suji ga tootte iru.","german":"What you're saying makes sense.","furigana":"きみ|い|すじ|とお"}],"notes":"筋 has many meanings, all going back to \"line, strand\". On the body it is a tendon or muscle strand (足の筋を痛める); in stories it is the plot (筋, あらすじ); in thinking it is logic: 筋が通る \"to be coherent\", 筋が通らない \"illogical\". 筋がいい means \"talented\", and in news 関係筋 denotes \"informed sources\". In the word 筋肉 \"muscle\", the kanji is read きん.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ポツダム宣言っていつだっけ？","romaji":"Potsudamu sengen tte itsu da kke?","german":"When was the Potsdam Declaration again?","furigana":"せんげん"},{"japanese":"政府は緊急事態宣言を出した。","romaji":"Seifu wa kinkyuu jitai sengen o dashita.","german":"The government declared a state of emergency.","furigana":"せいふ|きんきゅうじたいせんげん|だ"},{"japanese":"兄は家族の前で、今年こそたばこをやめると宣言した。","romaji":"Ani wa kazoku no mae de, kotoshi koso tabako o yameru to sengen shita.","german":"My brother declared in front of the family that this year he would really quit smoking.","furigana":"あに|かぞく|まえ|ことし|せんげん"}],"notes":"宣言 is a public, solemn declaration; as a する-verb: X を宣言する or 〜と宣言する. Typical in politics and history: 独立宣言, 緊急事態宣言, ポツダム宣言. In everyday life it is used half-jokingly for firm resolutions: 禁煙宣言, 〜すると宣言する. A mere \"announcement\" of results is 発表, and an official statement is 声明.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私達の子どもはあなたの子と違って、忠実に言うことを聞くんです。","romaji":"Watashitachi no kodomo wa anata no ko to chigatte, chūjitsu ni iu koto o kiku n desu.","german":"Unlike yours, our children obediently do what they're told.","furigana":"わたしたち|こ|こ|ちが|ちゅうじつ|い|き"},{"japanese":"この映画は原作に忠実に作られている。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa gensaku ni chuujitsu ni tsukurarete iru.","german":"This film was made faithfully to the original work.","furigana":"えいが|げんさく|ちゅうじつ|つく"},{"japanese":"犬は飼い主に忠実な動物だと言われる。","romaji":"Inu wa kainushi ni chuujitsu na doubutsu da to iwareru.","german":"Dogs are said to be animals loyal to their owners.","furigana":"いぬ|か|ぬし|ちゅうじつ|どうぶつ|い"}],"notes":"忠実 is a な-adjective: 〜に忠実な / 〜に忠実に. It means on the one hand \"loyal, devoted\" toward people (飼い主に忠実な犬), and on the other \"faithful, exact\" toward originals and rules: 原作に忠実, 基本に忠実, 忠実に再現する. 誠実, by contrast, is \"sincere, honest\" as a character trait. For faithfulness in a relationship, people tend to say 一途 or 浮気しない.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の忠告を甘く見るな。命を落とすかもしれないぞ。","romaji":"Kare no chuukoku o amaku miru na. Inochi o otosu kamo shirenai zo.","german":"Don't take his warning lightly. It could cost you your life.","furigana":"かれ|ちゅうこく|あま|み|いのち|お"},{"japanese":"医者の忠告を聞いて、お酒をやめた。","romaji":"Isha no chuukoku o kiite, osake o yameta.","german":"On the doctor's advice, I stopped drinking.","furigana":"いしゃ|ちゅうこく|き|さけ"},{"japanese":"友達として忠告しておくけど、あの人とは付き合わないほうがいい。","romaji":"Tomodachi to shite chuukoku shite oku kedo, ano hito to wa tsukiawanai hou ga ii.","german":"I'm telling you this as a friend: you'd better not get involved with that person.","furigana":"ともだち|ちゅうこく|ひと|つ|あ"}],"notes":"忠告 is well-meant advice intended to warn someone about a mistake or danger - \"advice, warning\". As a する-verb: Person に忠告する, often with 〜ように or 〜ほうがいい. Typical are 忠告を聞く / 守る / 無視する. More neutral is アドバイス, more formal 助言; 注意 refers more to a concrete misbehavior in the moment.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"薄暗い部屋の床に、血が静かに拡がっていた。","romaji":"Usugurai heya no yuka ni, chi ga shizuka ni hirogatte ita.","german":"Blood was spreading quietly across the floor of the dimly lit room.","furigana":"うすぐら|へや|ゆか|ち|しず|ひろ"},{"japanese":"火事の被害は隣の町まで拡がった。","romaji":"Kaji no higai wa tonari no machi made hirogatta.","german":"The damage from the fire spread to the neighboring town.","furigana":"かじ|ひがい|となり|まち|ひろ"},{"japanese":"うわさはあっという間に学校中に拡がった。","romaji":"Uwasa wa atto iu ma ni gakkouchuu ni hirogatta.","german":"The rumor spread throughout the school in no time.","furigana":"ま|がっこうちゅう|ひろ"}],"notes":"拡がる is an alternative spelling of 広がる; the standard spelling in newspapers and textbooks is 広がる. 拡 stresses expansion of extent or scale (被害が拡がる, 感染が拡がる). The verb is intransitive: X が Y に/まで拡がる. The transitive counterpart is 拡げる or 広げる \"to spread out, to expand\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どうやってそんなにピアスの穴を拡張したの？","romaji":"Dō yatte sonnani piasu no ana o kakuchō shita no?","german":"How did you stretch your piercing holes that much?","furigana":"あな|かくちょう"},{"japanese":"駅前の道路を拡張する工事が始まった。","romaji":"Ekimae no douro o kakuchou suru kouji ga hajimatta.","german":"Construction to widen the road in front of the station has begun.","furigana":"えきまえ|どうろ|かくちょう|こうじ|はじ"},{"japanese":"このブラウザは拡張機能を入れるともっと便利になる。","romaji":"Kono burauza wa kakuchou kinou o ireru to motto benri ni naru.","german":"This browser becomes even more convenient if you install extensions.","furigana":"かくちょうきのう|い|べんり"}],"notes":"拡張 means \"expansion, extension\" of scope, area or functions; as a する-verb it is transitive: 道路を拡張する, 事業を拡張する. It is common in IT: 拡張機能 \"extension, add-on\", 拡張子 \"file extension\". Unlike 拡大 \"to enlarge\" (写真を拡大する), 拡張 is about widening a framework or capacity. Rather written, administrative and technical language.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"クマが裏でみんなを操っている黒幕だっていう噂があるんだ。","romaji":"Kuma ga ura de minna o ayatsutte iru kuromaku da tte iu uwasa ga aru n da.","german":"There's a rumor that Kuma is the mastermind manipulating everyone behind the scenes.","furigana":"うら|あやつ|くろまく|うわさ"},{"japanese":"彼女は五か国語を自由に操る。","romaji":"Kanojo wa gokakokugo o jiyuu ni ayatsuru.","german":"She speaks five languages freely.","furigana":"かのじょ|ご|こくご|じゆう|あやつ"},{"japanese":"人形劇では、糸で人形を操っている。","romaji":"Ningyougeki de wa, ito de ningyou o ayatsutte iru.","german":"In puppet theater, the puppets are operated with strings.","furigana":"にんぎょうげき|いと|にんぎょう|あやつ"}],"notes":"操る is transitive and means \"to steer skillfully\": marionettes (人形を操る), vehicles (船を操る) or languages (英語を自由に操る \"to have fluent command of\"). Figuratively it means \"to manipulate, pull the strings behind the scenes\": 人を操る, 世論を操る. For the technical operation of machines, 操作する is more usual.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の兄は女性は結婚するまで操を守るべきだと言う。","romaji":"Watashi no ani wa josei wa kekkon suru made misao o mamoru beki da to iu.","german":"My older brother says that women should preserve their chastity until marriage.","furigana":"わたし|あに|じょせい|けっこん|みさお|まも|い"},{"japanese":"彼女は亡くなった夫に操を立てて、二度と結婚しなかった。","romaji":"Kanojo wa nakunatta otto ni misao o tatete, nido to kekkon shinakatta.","german":"She remained faithful to her late husband and never married again.","furigana":"かのじょ|な|おっと|みさお|た|にど|けっこん"},{"japanese":"彼はどんなに貧しくても、自分の操を守り通した。","romaji":"Kare wa donna ni mazushikute mo, jibun no misao o mamoritooshita.","german":"No matter how poor he was, he kept to his principles to the end.","furigana":"かれ|まず|じぶん|みさお|まも|とお"}],"notes":"操 is an old-fashioned, literary word for steadfastness and loyalty to principles or to a partner, traditionally especially a woman's chastity. Fixed phrases are 操を守る \"to preserve one's fidelity/chastity\" and 操を立てる \"to remain faithful to someone\". In modern daily life it sounds dated. The kanji otherwise occurs with the reading そう (体操, 操作); as a given name it is also read みさお.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は今は幸せに暮らしているが、ああ見えて苦難を経験してるんだぜ。","romaji":"Kare wa ima wa shiawase ni kurashite iru ga, aa miete kunan o keiken shiteru n da ze.","german":"He lives happily now, but, believe it or not, he has been through hardship.","furigana":"かれ|いま|しあわ|く|み|くなん|けいけん"},{"japanese":"大学に入ってから、東京で一人で暮らしています。","romaji":"Daigaku ni haitte kara, Toukyou de hitori de kurashite imasu.","german":"Since entering university, I have been living alone in Tokyo.","furigana":"だいがく|はい|とうきょう|ひとり|く"},{"japanese":"年金だけで暮らすのは大変だ。","romaji":"Nenkin dake de kurasu no wa taihen da.","german":"It's hard to live on a pension alone.","furigana":"ねんきん|く|たいへん"}],"notes":"暮らす is intransitive and means \"to lead one's life, to spend one's daily life\". The difference from 住む: 住む names the place of residence (東京に住む), 暮らす the way of living (幸せに暮らす, 一人で暮らす, 年金で暮らす); the place with 暮らす usually takes で. The noun is 暮らし \"life, livelihood\", e.g. 一人暮らし. As a suffix, 〜暮らす means \"to spend the whole day doing ...\" (泣き暮らす).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この灰色のお守りは、悪い気を追い払ってくれます。","romaji":"Kono haiiro no omamori wa, warui ki o oiharatte kuremasu.","german":"This gray charm drives away bad luck.","furigana":"はいいろ|まも|わる|き|お|はら"},{"japanese":"朝から灰色の雲が空を覆っている。","romaji":"Asa kara haiiro no kumo ga sora o ootte iru.","german":"Gray clouds have covered the sky since morning.","furigana":"あさ|はいいろ|くも|そら|おお"},{"japanese":"仕事ばかりで、毎日が灰色に見える。","romaji":"Shigoto bakari de, mainichi ga haiiro ni mieru.","german":"All I do is work, and every day looks gray.","furigana":"しごと|まいにち|はいいろ|み"}],"notes":"灰色 is \"ash gray, gray\" and is used as a noun with の: 灰色の雲, 灰色のスーツ. For clothing and fashion, グレー is at least as common; ねずみ色 is a more traditional word for gray. Figuratively, 灰色 stands for bleakness (灰色の毎日) and for unclarity or suspicion - a case that is neither black nor white.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"念のため灰皿を買っておきました。","romaji":"Nen no tame haizara o katte okimashita.","german":"I bought an ashtray just in case.","furigana":"ねん|はいざら|か"},{"japanese":"灰皿に灰が残っています。","romaji":"Haizara ni hai ga nokotte imasu.","german":"There are ashes left in the ashtray.","furigana":"はいざら|はい|のこ"}],"notes":"灰皿 is an ashtray. 灰皿を買う names it as the thing bought. 買っておきました describes the purchase as preparation; 念のため means \"just in case\". 灰皿に灰がある says what is in it. In はいざら, 皿 is read with voicing as ざら.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 49"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"そろそろ桃が熟れる頃だ。","romaji":"Sorosoro momo ga ureru koro da.","german":"It is about time for the peaches to ripen.","furigana":"もも|う|ころ"},{"japanese":"このトマトはよく熟れていて、とても甘い。","romaji":"Kono tomato wa yoku urete ite, totemo amai.","german":"These tomatoes are well ripened and very sweet.","furigana":"う|あま"},{"japanese":"熟れすぎたバナナでケーキを作った。","romaji":"Uresugita banana de keeki o tsukutta.","german":"I made a cake with overripe bananas.","furigana":"う|つく"}],"notes":"熟れる is intransitive and means \"to ripen\" of fruit, vegetables or grain: 柿が熟れる, 熟れたトマト. Synonymous and more common is 熟す (じゅくす), which is also used figuratively (機が熟す \"the time is ripe\"). Do not confuse it with the identically pronounced 売れる \"to sell\". With the reading なれる, 熟れる denotes fermented foods maturing - that is a different word.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"昨日その熟語を学んだところです。","romaji":"Kinō sono jukugo o mananda tokoro desu.","german":"I just learned that compound yesterday.","furigana":"きのう|じゅくご|まな"},{"japanese":"「学校」は「学」と「校」の二つの漢字からできた熟語だ。","romaji":"Gakkou wa gaku to kou no futatsu no kanji kara dekita jukugo da.","german":"\"学校\" is a compound made of the two kanji \"学\" and \"校\".","furigana":"がっこう|がく|こう|ふた|かんじ|じゅくご"},{"japanese":"英語の熟語は、単語の意味から想像できないものが多い。","romaji":"Eigo no jukugo wa, tango no imi kara souzou dekinai mono ga ooi.","german":"Many English idioms cannot be guessed from the meanings of the individual words.","furigana":"えいご|じゅくご|たんご|いみ|そうぞう|おお"}],"notes":"熟語 mainly denotes a word composed of several kanji: 二字熟語 like 学校, 四字熟語 like 一石二鳥. In English classes in Japan, 熟語 also means \"set phrase, idiom\" (e.g. look after). For Japanese idioms people more often say 慣用句; a single word is 単語.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"まだまだ未熟で分からないことばかりですが、がんばります。","romaji":"Madamada mijuku de wakaranai koto bakari desu ga, ganbarimasu.","german":"I'm still inexperienced and there's a lot I don't understand, but I'll do my best.","furigana":"みじゅく|わ"},{"japanese":"私の未熟なミスで、皆さんにご迷惑をおかけしました。","romaji":"Watashi no mijuku na misu de, minasan ni go-meiwaku o okake shimashita.","german":"My amateurish mistake caused trouble for all of you.","furigana":"わたし|みじゅく|みな|めいわく"},{"japanese":"このトマトはまだ未熟で、緑色をしている。","romaji":"Kono tomato wa mada mijuku de, midori-iro o shite iru.","german":"This tomato is still unripe and green.","furigana":"みじゅく|みどりいろ"}],"notes":"未熟 is a な-adjective and noun: literally \"unripe\" for fruit, but much more often \"inexperienced, not yet mature\" for people and skills (未熟な運転, 技術が未熟だ). As a humble self-description people say 未熟者ですが、よろしくお願いします. 未熟児 is a premature baby. 幼い, by contrast, describes childlike behavior or young age.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"成熟した女性の脳と成熟した男性の脳の相違点について、論文を書いています。","romaji":"Seijuku shita josei no nō to seijuku shita dansei no nō no sōiten nitsuite, ronbun o kaite imasu.","german":"I'm writing a paper on the differences between the mature female brain and the mature male brain.","furigana":"せいじゅく|じょせい|のう|せいじゅく|だんせい|のう|そういてん|ろんぶん|か"},{"japanese":"もう大人なんだから、成熟した対応をしてほしい。","romaji":"Mou otona na n da kara, seijuku shita taiou o shite hoshii.","german":"You're an adult now, so I want you to respond maturely.","furigana":"おとな|せいじゅく|たいおう"},{"japanese":"日本の携帯電話の市場は、すでに成熟している。","romaji":"Nihon no keitai denwa no shijou wa, sude ni seijuku shite iru.","german":"Japan's mobile phone market is already mature.","furigana":"にほん|けいたいでんわ|しじょう|せいじゅく"}],"notes":"成熟 means \"maturity\" and as a する-verb is intransitive: 成熟する \"to mature\", mostly as 成熟した + noun or 成熟している. It is used for body and personality (成熟した大人), for fruit, and for society or markets (成熟した市場). In everyday speech, people say 熟す / 熟れる for fruit and 大人になる for people; the opposite is 未熟 \"immature\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子宮がん検診の結果が「異状あり」だったのでびびっています。","romaji":"Shikyū gan kenshin no kekka ga ijō ari datta node bibitte imasu.","german":"I'm terrified because my cervical cancer screening result was \"abnormality found\".","furigana":"しきゅう|けんしん|けっか|いじょう"},{"japanese":"警備員が見回ったが、建物に異状はなかった。","romaji":"Keibiin ga mimawatta ga, tatemono ni ijou wa nakatta.","german":"The guard made his rounds, but there was nothing unusual about the building.","furigana":"けいびいん|みまわ|たてもの|いじょう"},{"japanese":"点検でエンジンに異状が見つかった。","romaji":"Tenken de enjin ni ijou ga mitsukatta.","german":"The inspection found an abnormality in the engine.","furigana":"てんけん|いじょう|み"}],"notes":"異状 is a noun for a specific unusual condition, an irregularity or malfunction: 異状がある / ない, 異状なし \"everything is fine\". Do not confuse it with the identically read 異常, which as a な-adjective means \"abnormal, extraordinary\" more generally (異常な暑さ). 異状 typically appears in medical findings, inspection and police reports; in conversation people more often say どこかおかしい.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"シェアハウスについて初めて知った時、知らない異性同士が暮らすなんてありえないと思いました。","romaji":"Shea hausu nitsuite hajimete shitta toki, shiranai isei dōshi ga kurasu nante arienai to omoimashita.","german":"When I first heard about shared houses, I thought it was impossible for strangers of the opposite sex to live together.","furigana":"はじ|し|とき|し|いせいどうし|く|おも"},{"japanese":"異性の友達と二人で映画を見に行った。","romaji":"Isei no tomodachi to futari de eiga o mi ni itta.","german":"I went to the movies, just the two of us, with a friend of the opposite sex.","furigana":"いせい|ともだち|ふたり|えいが|み|い"},{"japanese":"中学生になると、異性を意識し始める子が多い。","romaji":"Chuugakusei ni naru to, isei o ishiki shihajimeru ko ga ooi.","german":"Once they reach junior high school, many kids start becoming aware of the opposite sex.","furigana":"ちゅうがくせい|いせい|いしき|はじ|こ|おお"}],"notes":"異性 is \"the opposite sex\" from the viewpoint of the person in question; the counterpart is 同性 \"the same sex\". Typical are 異性の友達, 異性にもてる \"to be popular with the opposite sex\" and 異性を意識する. 異性同士 emphasizes mutuality. The word is neutral and used in conversation as well as in surveys.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この単語には同音異義語がありますか？","romaji":"Kono tango ni wa douon igigo ga arimasu ka?","german":"Does this word have a homophone?","furigana":"たんご|どうおんいぎご"},{"japanese":"「科学」と「化学」は同音異義語なので、話すときに区別しにくい。","romaji":"Kagaku to kagaku wa douon igigo na node, hanasu toki ni kubetsu shinikui.","german":"\"科学\" and \"化学\" are homophones, so they are hard to tell apart when speaking.","furigana":"かがく|かがく|どうおんいぎご|はな|くべつ"},{"japanese":"「異義」と「異議」は読み方が同じだが、意味が違う。","romaji":"Igi to igi wa yomikata ga onaji da ga, imi ga chigau.","german":"\"異義\" and \"異議\" are read the same but have different meanings.","furigana":"いぎ|いぎ|よ|かた|おな|いみ|ちが"}],"notes":"異義 means \"a different, divergent meaning\" and appears almost only in 同音異義語 \"homophone\" (a word that sounds the same but has a different meaning), e.g. 橋 and 箸. On its own it is hardly used. Beware of the identically read 異議 \"objection, protest\" (異議あり！, 異議を唱える) - \"a dissenting opinion\" is also normally written this way today. Rather technical and written language.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"天皇誕生日は国民の祝日です。","romaji":"Tennou tanjoubi wa kokumin no shukujitsu desu.","german":"The Emperor's Birthday is a national holiday.","furigana":"てんのうたんじょうび|こくみん|しゅくじつ"},{"japanese":"天皇陛下が地震の被災地を訪問された。","romaji":"Tennou heika ga jishin no hisaichi o houmon sareta.","german":"His Majesty the Emperor visited the earthquake-stricken area.","furigana":"てんのうへいか|じしん|ひさいち|ほうもん"},{"japanese":"日本では天皇が代わると元号も変わる。","romaji":"Nihon de wa tennou ga kawaru to gengou mo kawaru.","german":"In Japan, when the emperor changes, the era name changes too.","furigana":"にほん|てんのう|か|げんごう|か"}],"notes":"天皇 refers exclusively to the Japanese emperor; foreign emperors are called 皇帝 (ナポレオン皇帝). In respectful speech you say 天皇陛下 and use honorific forms such as 訪問される. Related words are 天皇誕生日 (national holiday), 皇室 \"imperial house\" and 上皇 \"emperor emeritus\". With each new emperor a new era (元号) such as 令和 begins.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"皇太子殿下に「うまくいくといいですね」と言った。","romaji":"Koutaishi denka ni umaku iku to ii desu ne to itta.","german":"I told His Imperial Highness the Crown Prince, \"I hope it goes well.\"","furigana":"こうたいしでんか|い"},{"japanese":"皇太子がヨーロッパを公式に訪問した。","romaji":"Koutaishi ga Yooroppa o koushiki ni houmon shita.","german":"The crown prince made an official visit to Europe.","furigana":"こうたいし|こうしき|ほうもん"},{"japanese":"皇太子とは、次に天皇になる人のことです。","romaji":"Koutaishi to wa, tsugi ni tennou ni naru hito no koto desu.","german":"The crown prince is the person who will become emperor next.","furigana":"こうたいし|つぎ|てんのう|ひと"}],"notes":"皇太子 is the crown prince, the designated heir to the Japanese emperor; in honorific language 皇太子殿下, and his wife is 皇太子妃. The word is also used for heirs of foreign monarchies (イギリスの皇太子). A prince who is not heir is called 王子 or, in the imperial house, 親王; the current heir bears the title 皇嗣. It belongs to news and formal language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"時々電車は、皇族専用の駅に特別に止まることがあります。","romaji":"Tokidoki densha wa, kōzoku sen'yō no eki ni tokubetsu ni tomaru koto ga arimasu.","german":"Trains sometimes make special stops at a station reserved for the imperial family.","furigana":"ときどきでんしゃ|こうぞくせんよう|えき|とくべつ|と"},{"japanese":"皇族の結婚は、いつも大きなニュースになる。","romaji":"Kouzoku no kekkon wa, itsumo ookina nyuusu ni naru.","german":"The marriage of an imperial family member always becomes big news.","furigana":"こうぞく|けっこん|おお"},{"japanese":"彼女は一般の男性と結婚して、皇族の身分を離れた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa ippan no dansei to kekkon shite, kouzoku no mibun o hanareta.","german":"She married a commoner and left the status of imperial family member.","furigana":"かのじょ|いっぱん|だんせい|けっこん|こうぞく|みぶん|はな"}],"notes":"皇族 are the individual members of the Japanese imperial family (legally excluding the emperor himself). 皇室, by contrast, denotes the imperial house as an institution. For the royal houses of other countries, 王族 or 王室 is used. Typical are 皇族の方々, 皇族の身分 and 皇族専用 - mostly in news and formal language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"皇室はここ最近、かなり大変な状況に陥っています。","romaji":"Kōshitsu wa koko saikin, kanari taihen na jōkyō ni ochiitte imasu.","german":"The Imperial Household has recently fallen into a rather difficult situation.","furigana":"こうしつ|さいきん|たいへん|じょうきょう|おちい"},{"japanese":"母は皇室のニュースをいつも楽しみにしている。","romaji":"Haha wa koushitsu no nyuusu o itsumo tanoshimi ni shite iru.","german":"My mother always looks forward to news about the Imperial Household.","furigana":"はは|こうしつ|たの"},{"japanese":"日本の皇室とイギリスの王室には長い交流がある。","romaji":"Nihon no koushitsu to Igirisu no oushitsu ni wa nagai kouryuu ga aru.","german":"The Japanese Imperial Household and the British royal family have a long history of exchange.","furigana":"にほん|こうしつ|おうしつ|なが|こうりゅう"}],"notes":"皇室 is the Japanese imperial house as a whole, i.e. institution and family: 皇室の行事, 皇室典範 \"Imperial Household Law\". The individual members are 皇族. For foreign royal houses, 王室 is used (イギリス王室). The word belongs to formal news language, and reports about it use honorifics.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ブドウ糖果糖液糖は体に悪いって言うじゃない？","romaji":"Budōtō katōekitō wa karada ni warui tte iu ja nai?","german":"They say high-fructose glucose syrup is bad for you, don't they?","furigana":"とうかとうえきとう|からだ|わる|い"},{"japanese":"果物には果糖がたくさん含まれている。","romaji":"Kudamono ni wa katou ga takusan fukumarete iru.","german":"Fruit contains a lot of fructose.","furigana":"くだもの|かとう|ふく"},{"japanese":"果糖は砂糖よりも甘く感じられる。","romaji":"Katou wa satou yori mo amaku kanjirareru.","german":"Fructose tastes sweeter than sugar.","furigana":"かとう|さとう|あま|かん"}],"notes":"果糖 is fruit sugar (fructose), a technical word from nutrition and chemistry. On food labels you often read 果糖ぶどう糖液糖 or ぶどう糖果糖液糖 - glucose-fructose syrup, named according to which sugar predominates. ぶどう糖 is glucose, 砂糖 is table sugar and 糖分 in general is \"sugar content\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"さっきも言ったけど、コーヒーは無糖派なの。","romaji":"Sakki mo itta kedo, koohii wa mutou-ha na no.","german":"As I said earlier, I'm an unsweetened-coffee person.","furigana":"い|むとうは"},{"japanese":"コンビニで無糖の紅茶を買ってきてくれる？","romaji":"Konbini de mutou no koucha o katte kite kureru?","german":"Could you buy me some unsweetened tea at the convenience store?","furigana":"むとう|こうちゃ|か"},{"japanese":"このヨーグルトは無糖なので、はちみつをかけて食べる。","romaji":"Kono yooguruto wa mutou na node, hachimitsu o kakete taberu.","german":"This yogurt is unsweetened, so I eat it with honey on top.","furigana":"むとう|た"}],"notes":"無糖 means \"without added sugar, unsweetened\" and appears mainly on drinks and foods: 無糖コーヒー, 無糖の紅茶, 無糖ヨーグルト. On labels there is the gradation 無糖 - 微糖 \"slightly sweetened\" - 加糖 \"sweetened\". It is a noun that takes の or, as a predicate, だ; in conversation people also say 砂糖なし, and for coffee ブラック.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の部屋にはあまり収納スペースがないので、ちょっとした工夫が必要です。","romaji":"Watashi no heya ni wa amari shūnō supēsu ga nai node, chottoshita kufū ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"There isn't much storage space in my room, so I need a bit of ingenuity.","furigana":"わたし|へや|しゅうのう|くふう|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"使わない冬服は押し入れに収納している。","romaji":"Tsukawanai fuyufuku wa oshiire ni shuunou shite iru.","german":"I keep winter clothes I don't use stored in the closet.","furigana":"つか|ふゆふく|お|い|しゅうのう"},{"japanese":"このベッドは下に物を収納できるので便利だ。","romaji":"Kono beddo wa shita ni mono o shuunou dekiru node benri da.","german":"This bed lets you store things underneath, so it's convenient.","furigana":"した|もの|しゅうのう|べんり"}],"notes":"収納 is the orderly stowing of objects: X を Y に収納する. As a noun it is common in apartment listings and furniture ads: 収納スペース, 収納ケース, 収納が多い部屋. In daily life, for \"to put away\", people rather say しまう or 片付ける. There is also the formal meaning \"receipt of money\" (収納代行 for payments).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"君の辛さはすごく分かるよ。僕も肺がんなんだ。","romaji":"Kimi no tsurasa wa sugoku wakaru yo. Boku mo haigan na n da.","german":"I understand how hard it is for you. I have lung cancer too.","furigana":"きみ|つら|わ|ぼく|はい"},{"japanese":"たばこを吸う人は肺がんになるリスクが高い。","romaji":"Tabako o suu hito wa haigan ni naru risuku ga takai.","german":"People who smoke have a higher risk of getting lung cancer.","furigana":"す|ひと|はい|たか"},{"japanese":"父は検診で早期の肺がんが見つかり、手術を受けた。","romaji":"Chichi wa kenshin de souki no haigan ga mitsukari, shujutsu o uketa.","german":"My father had early-stage lung cancer found at a checkup and underwent surgery.","furigana":"ちち|けんしん|そうき|はい|み|しゅじゅつ|う"}],"notes":"肺がん is lung cancer. がん is usually written in hiragana because the kanji 癌 is difficult; likewise 胃がん, 乳がん. Typical are 肺がんになる \"to get lung cancer\", 肺がんと診断される and 肺がんが見つかる. In technical texts 肺癌 is also used.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"肺病の治療のために、三日後に入院するんです。","romaji":"Haibyō no chiryō no tame ni, sannichigo ni nyūin suru n desu.","german":"I'm being admitted to the hospital in three days for treatment of a lung disease.","furigana":"はいびょう|ちりょう|さんにちご|にゅういん"},{"japanese":"昔は肺病で若くして亡くなる人が多かった。","romaji":"Mukashi wa haibyou de wakaku shite nakunaru hito ga ookatta.","german":"In the past, many people died young of lung disease.","furigana":"むかし|はいびょう|わか|な|ひと|おお"},{"japanese":"祖父は若いころ肺病にかかって、一年間入院していたそうだ。","romaji":"Sofu wa wakai koro haibyou ni kakatte, ichinenkan nyuuin shite ita sou da.","german":"My grandfather apparently caught a lung disease in his youth and was hospitalized for a year.","furigana":"そふ|わか|はいびょう|いちねんかんにゅういん"}],"notes":"肺病 is generally \"lung disease\", but in the past it was mainly a euphemism for tuberculosis (today 結核). The word therefore sounds somewhat old-fashioned; in modern texts and at the doctor's, people more often say 肺の病気 or, specifically, 肺炎, 肺がん. Typical are 肺病にかかる and 肺病の治療.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"なんと私の姉もその本の著者でした。","romaji":"Nanto watashi no ane mo sono hon no chosha deshita.","german":"As it happens, my older sister was also an author of that book.","furigana":"わたし|あね|ほん|ちょしゃ"},{"japanese":"本屋で著者のサイン会があった。","romaji":"Hon'ya de chosha no sainkai ga atta.","german":"There was an autograph session by the author at the bookstore.","furigana":"ほんや|ちょしゃ|かい"},{"japanese":"論文を引用するときは、著者名と出版年を書く。","romaji":"Ronbun o in'you suru toki wa, chosha mei to shuppan nen o kaku.","german":"When citing a paper, write the author's name and the year of publication.","furigana":"ろんぶん|いんよう|ちょしゃめい|しゅっぱんねん|か"}],"notes":"著者 is the writer of a particular book or document: この本の著者, 著者名, 著者のサイン会. 作家, by contrast, is the profession \"writer\", 作者 is the creator of a (often literary or artistic) work, and 筆者 is the writer of the text at hand - typical in reading-comprehension exercises. 著者 sounds matter-of-fact and written.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は少なくとも五十は著作があります。","romaji":"Kanojo wa sukunakutomo gojuu wa chosaku ga arimasu.","german":"She has at least fifty works to her name.","furigana":"かのじょ|すく|ごじゅう|ちょさく"},{"japanese":"インターネットの写真を勝手に使うと、著作権の問題になる。","romaji":"Intaanetto no shashin o katte ni tsukau to, chosakuken no mondai ni naru.","german":"If you use photos from the internet without permission, it becomes a copyright issue.","furigana":"しゃしん|かって|つか|ちょさくけん|もんだい"},{"japanese":"先生は引退してからも著作活動を続けている。","romaji":"Sensei wa intai shite kara mo chosaku katsudou o tsuzukete iru.","german":"Even after retiring, the teacher continues his writing activities.","furigana":"せんせい|いんたい|ちょさくかつどう|つづ"}],"notes":"著作 denotes the writing of books and the result, i.e. \"(literary) work\": 著作がある, 主な著作. As a する-verb it is rare; people prefer 本を書く or 執筆する. Compounds are very common: 著作権 \"copyright\", 著作物 \"copyrighted work\", 著作活動. A single book of one's own is usually called 著書.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この靴、可愛いんだけど、足が蒸れて臭うんだよね。","romaji":"Kono kutsu, kawaii n da kedo, ashi ga murete niou n da yo ne.","german":"These shoes are cute, but my feet get sweaty and smell.","furigana":"くつ|かわい|あし|む|にお"},{"japanese":"ご飯が蒸れるまで、ふたを開けないでください。","romaji":"Gohan ga mureru made, futa o akenaide kudasai.","german":"Don't open the lid until the rice has finished steaming.","furigana":"はん|む|あ"},{"japanese":"ヘルメットをかぶっていると、夏は頭が蒸れる。","romaji":"Herumetto o kabutte iru to, natsu wa atama ga mureru.","german":"When I wear a helmet in summer, my head gets stuffy and sweaty.","furigana":"なつ|あたま|む"}],"notes":"蒸れる is intransitive and has two uses. In cooking it is the finishing steaming: ご飯が蒸れる (transitive: ご飯を蒸らす \"to let it steam\"). In daily life it describes damp heat under clothing or in closed rooms: 足が蒸れる, 頭が蒸れる. 蒸し暑い, by contrast, is \"muggy\" for weather, and \"to sweat\" proper is 汗をかく.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コーヒーメーカーの蒸気でやけどしちゃった。","romaji":"Kōhī mēkā no jōki de yakedo shichatta.","german":"I got burned by the steam from the coffee maker.","furigana":"じょうき"},{"japanese":"昔はこの線を蒸気機関車が走っていました。","romaji":"Mukashi wa kono sen o jōki kikansha ga hashitte imashita.","german":"In the past, steam locomotives ran on this line.","furigana":"むかし|せん|じょうききかんしゃ|はし"},{"japanese":"このアイロンは蒸気でしわを伸ばします。","romaji":"Kono airon wa jōki de shiwa o nobashimasu.","german":"This iron smooths out wrinkles with steam.","furigana":"じょうき|の"}],"notes":"蒸気 is \"steam\" in the physical-technical sense: water vapor coming out of machines, boilers or irons, and steam as motive power, e.g. in 蒸気機関車 (\"steam locomotive\", abbreviated SL). For the visible steam over hot food, tea or the bath, people usually say 湯気 in everyday speech; invisible water vapor in the air is 水蒸気. Typical combinations: 蒸気が出る, 蒸気でやけどする.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"村の入り口にお地蔵さんが立っています。","romaji":"Mura no iriguchi ni o-jizō-san ga tatte imasu.","german":"A Jizo statue stands at the entrance of the village.","furigana":"むら|い|ぐち|じぞう|た"},{"japanese":"おばあちゃんは毎朝お地蔵さんに手を合わせる。","romaji":"Obāchan wa maiasa o-jizō-san ni te o awaseru.","german":"Grandma puts her hands together before the Jizo statue every morning.","furigana":"まいあさ|じぞう|て|あ"},{"japanese":"この地蔵は旅人の安全を守ると言われています。","romaji":"Kono jizō wa tabibito no anzen o mamoru to iwarete imasu.","german":"This Jizo is said to protect travelers' safety.","furigana":"じぞう|たびびと|あんぜん|まも|い"}],"notes":"地蔵 is the bodhisattva Jizō, who is venerated in Japan as a protector of children and travelers, and above all the small stone statues by the roadside or at temples. In daily life people lovingly say お地蔵さん or お地蔵さま; the bare 地蔵 sounds matter-of-fact and often appears in compounds like 地蔵菩薩. It is typical that Jizō statues wear red bibs and that people put their hands together (手を合わせる) before them.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"もうすっかり春の装いだね。","romaji":"Mō sukkari haru no yosooi da ne.","german":"It's already completely spring attire, isn't it?","furigana":"はる|よそお"},{"japanese":"駅前のカフェが装いも新たにオープンしました。","romaji":"Ekimae no kafe ga yosooi mo arata ni ōpun shimashita.","german":"The cafe in front of the station has opened with a brand-new look.","furigana":"えきまえ|よそお|あら"},{"japanese":"パーティーには、落ち着いた装いでお越しください。","romaji":"Pātī ni wa, ochitsuita yosooi de okoshi kudasai.","german":"Please come to the party in subdued attire.","furigana":"お|つ|よそお|こ"}],"notes":"装い is the elevated, somewhat literary word for outfit or appearance - from clothing to a place or shop. It is the noun of 装う (\"to dress; to feign\"). Typical are 春の装い (\"spring wardrobe, spring attire\") and the phrase 装いも新たに (\"in a new guise\"), for example at a reopening. In normal conversation about clothes, people rather say 服 or 服装.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"犯人は警察官に変装して銀行に入った。","romaji":"Hannin wa keisatsukan ni hensō shite ginkō ni haitta.","german":"The criminal disguised himself as a police officer and entered the bank.","furigana":"はんにん|けいさつかん|へんそう|ぎんこう|はい"},{"japanese":"その有名人は帽子とサングラスで変装していたが、すぐに気づかれた。","romaji":"Sono yūmeijin wa bōshi to sangurasu de hensō shite ita ga, sugu ni kizukareta.","german":"That celebrity was disguised with a hat and sunglasses, but was recognized immediately.","furigana":"ゆうめいじん|ぼうし|へんそう|き"},{"japanese":"変装がうまくて、母親も彼だと分からなかった。","romaji":"Hensō ga umakute, hahaoya mo kare da to wakaranakatta.","german":"He is so good at disguises that even his mother didn't recognize him.","furigana":"へんそう|ははおや|かれ|わ"}],"notes":"変装 means \"to disguise oneself, to go incognito\" so as not to be recognized; as an intransitive する-verb, the role takes に: 警察官に変装する (\"to disguise oneself as a police officer\"). The means takes で: 帽子とサングラスで変装する. To be distinguished from this is 仮装, a costume for fun (Halloween, carnival, 仮装パーティー). Celebrities or detectives 変装する; children at a festival 仮装する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼のことは好きなんだけど、服装があまり好きじゃないのよね。","romaji":"Kare no koto wa suki na n da kedo, fukusō ga amari suki ja nai no yo ne.","german":"I like him, but I don't really like his clothes.","furigana":"かれ|す|ふくそう|す"},{"japanese":"面接にはどんな服装で行けばいいですか。","romaji":"Mensetsu ni wa donna fukusō de ikeba ii desu ka.","german":"What should I wear to the interview?","furigana":"めんせつ|ふくそう|い"},{"japanese":"この会社は服装が自由です。","romaji":"Kono kaisha wa fukusō ga jiyū desu.","german":"This company has no dress code.","furigana":"かいしゃ|ふくそう|じゆう"}],"notes":"服装 denotes all the clothing or outfit that someone wears, and is the usual word in rules and notices: 服装自由 (\"no dress code\"), 動きやすい服装 (\"comfortable clothing\"), 服装に気をつける. 服 rather means the individual garment (\"I'm going to buy a 服\"); 格好 is more colloquial and stresses overall appearance. You ask about the dress code with どんな服装で….","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"配達の方は裏口から入ってください。","romaji":"Haitatsu no kata wa uraguchi kara haitte kudasai.","german":"Delivery people, please come in through the back door.","furigana":"はいたつ|かた|うらぐち|はい"},{"japanese":"その歌手は記者を避けて、ホテルの裏口から出ていった。","romaji":"Sono kashu wa kisha o sakete, hoteru no uraguchi kara dete itta.","german":"The singer avoided reporters and left through the hotel's back door.","furigana":"かしゅ|きしゃ|さ|うらぐち|で"},{"japanese":"裏口入学がばれて、大きなニュースになった。","romaji":"Uraguchi nyūgaku ga barete, ōki na nyūsu ni natta.","german":"The backdoor admission was exposed and became big news.","furigana":"うらぐちにゅうがく|おお"}],"notes":"裏口 is the rear entrance or back door of a building, the counterpart of 表口 or 正面玄関. You go 裏口から入る/出る, for example as a supplier or in order not to be seen. Figuratively, 裏口 means an improper route \"through the back door\", especially in 裏口入学 (\"admission to a university through bribery or connections\"). The front door itself is 玄関, and any door is ドア or 戸.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女の裏切りを知ったとき、目の前が真っ暗になった。","romaji":"Kanojo no uragiri o shitta toki, me no mae ga makkura ni natta.","german":"When I learned of her betrayal, everything went dark before my eyes.","furigana":"かのじょ|うらぎ|し|め|まえ|ま|くら"},{"japanese":"仲間の裏切りで、計画は失敗した。","romaji":"Nakama no uragiri de, keikaku wa shippai shita.","german":"The plan failed because of a comrade's betrayal.","furigana":"なかま|うらぎ|けいかく|しっぱい"},{"japanese":"この映画は最後に意外な裏切りがあって面白い。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa saigo ni igai na uragiri ga atte omoshiroi.","german":"This movie is interesting because of an unexpected betrayal at the end.","furigana":"えいが|さいご|いがい|うらぎ|おもしろ"}],"notes":"裏切り is the noun of 裏切る (\"to betray; to disappoint expectations\") and means \"betrayal, breach of trust\". Typical: 仲間の裏切り, 裏切りにあう (\"to be betrayed\"), 裏切りを許さない. A person who betrays is 裏切り者 (\"traitor\"). The verb is also used in a weaker sense: 期待を裏切る (\"to disappoint expectations\"), 予想を裏切る (\"to turn out differently than expected\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"バイクは、裏通りを縫うようにして走っていった。","romaji":"Baiku wa, uradōri o nuu yō ni shite hashitte itta.","german":"The motorcycle ran on, weaving through the back streets.","furigana":"うらどお|ぬ|はし"},{"japanese":"大通りより裏通りのほうが、安くておいしい店が多い。","romaji":"Ōdōri yori uradōri no hō ga, yasukute oishii mise ga ōi.","german":"There are more cheap, tasty restaurants on back streets than on main streets.","furigana":"おおどお|うらどお|やす|みせ|おお"},{"japanese":"夜は裏通りを一人で歩かないほうがいいですよ。","romaji":"Yoru wa uradōri o hitori de arukanai hō ga ii desu yo.","german":"It's better not to walk alone on back streets at night.","furigana":"よる|うらどお|ひとり|ある"}],"notes":"裏通り is a side street, usually running parallel behind a main street (大通り, 表通り). It often carries the connotation that it is quieter and has hidden little shops, but is also less safe at night. You walk 裏通りを歩く/通る; a particularly narrow passage is more likely 路地. The opposite 表通り is the busy street frontage.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"名前は紙の裏に書いてください。","romaji":"Namae wa kami no ura ni kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write your name on the back of the paper.","furigana":"なまえ|かみ|うら|か"},{"japanese":"自転車は家の裏に止めてあります。","romaji":"Jitensha wa ie no ura ni tomete arimasu.","german":"The bicycle is parked behind the house.","furigana":"じてんしゃ|いえ|うら|と"},{"japanese":"あの人の親切には何か裏がありそうです。","romaji":"Ano hito no shinsetsu ni wa nanika ura ga arisou desu.","german":"There seems to be something behind that person's kindness.","furigana":"ひと|しんせつ|なに|うら"}],"notes":"裏 is the back or hidden side of something; the opposite is 表 \"front\": 紙の裏, 写真の裏. For buildings it means the rear area: 家の裏, 裏口 \"back entrance\". Unlike 後ろ, which is simply \"behind\" from the viewer's perspective, 裏 emphasizes the side facing away, often invisible. Figuratively it stands for ulterior motives: 裏がある \"there is something behind it\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 40"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"諸君、トーフグのオフィスで独身の 10 人がみんな集まるのってこれがたぶん最後だってこと、気がついていたかい？","romaji":"Shokun, Tōfugu no ofisu de dokushin no 10nin ga minna atsumaru no tte kore ga tabun saigo datte koto, ki ga tsuite ita kai?","german":"Everyone, did you realize this is probably the last time all ten singles at the Tofugu office will be together?","furigana":"しょくん|どくしん|にん|あつ|さいご|き"},{"japanese":"学生諸君、試験まであと一週間だ。しっかり勉強しなさい。","romaji":"Gakusei shokun, shiken made ato isshūkan da. Shikkari benkyō shinasai.","german":"Students, there is one week left until the exam. Study hard.","furigana":"がくせいしょくん|しけん|いっしゅうかん|べんきょう"},{"japanese":"諸君の協力に心から感謝する。","romaji":"Shokun no kyōryoku ni kokoro kara kansha suru.","german":"I sincerely thank you all for your cooperation.","furigana":"しょくん|きょうりょく|こころ|かんしゃ"}],"notes":"諸君 is a form of address to a group, used by a person of higher status toward equals or juniors - such as teachers, commanders or speakers: 学生諸君, 諸君！ It sounds old-fashioned, authoritative and rather masculine. It is inappropriate toward superiors or customers; neutrally and politely, say 皆さん.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"諸事情により、本日は休業いたします。","romaji":"Shojijō ni yori, honjitsu wa kyūgyō itashimasu.","german":"Due to various circumstances, we will be closed today.","furigana":"しょじじょう|ほんじつ|きゅうぎょう"},{"japanese":"日本は諸外国と協力して、この問題に取り組んでいる。","romaji":"Nihon wa shogaikoku to kyōryoku shite, kono mondai ni torikunde iru.","german":"Japan is tackling this problem in cooperation with other countries.","furigana":"にほん|しょがいこく|きょうりょく|もんだい|と|く"},{"japanese":"引っ越しには、家賃のほかに諸費用がかかります。","romaji":"Hikkoshi ni wa, yachin no hoka ni shohiyō ga kakarimasu.","german":"When moving, various expenses arise in addition to the rent.","furigana":"ひ|こ|やちん|しょひよう"}],"notes":"諸 is a Sino-Japanese prefix meaning \"various, diverse, all\" and stands before Kango nouns: 諸外国 (\"foreign countries, other countries\"), 諸問題, 諸事情 (\"various circumstances\"), 諸費用 (\"miscellaneous expenses\"), 諸君 (\"gentlemen, everyone\"). It feels written and formal and is common in notices, news and contracts. In conversation people say いろいろな or 様々な instead; 各, by contrast, stresses \"each individual\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"友人が賃貸のアパートを探してるんですが、どこかいい物件を知りませんかね。","romaji":"Yuujin ga chintai no apaato o sagashiteru n desu ga, doko ka ii bukken o shirimasen ka ne.","german":"A friend of mine is looking for a rental apartment. Do you know of any good properties?","furigana":"ゆうじん|ちんたい|さが|ぶっけん|し"},{"japanese":"賃貸と持ち家、どちらが得だと思いますか。","romaji":"Chintai to mochiie, dochira ga toku da to omoimasu ka.","german":"Which do you think is better, renting or owning a home?","furigana":"ちんたい|も|いえ|とく|おも"},{"japanese":"父は古い家を賃貸に出すことにした。","romaji":"Chichi wa furui ie o chintai ni dasu koto ni shita.","german":"My father decided to put the old house up for rent.","furigana":"ちち|ふる|いえ|ちんたい|だ"}],"notes":"賃貸 means \"leasing\" or \"for rent\" and is almost always used attributively: 賃貸マンション, 賃貸のアパート, 賃貸物件 (\"rental property\"), 賃貸契約 (\"lease contract\"). The opposite terms are 持ち家 (\"owned home\") and 分譲 (\"for sale\"). The monthly rent itself is not called 賃貸 but 家賃. To rent something out is 賃貸に出す or simply 貸す.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の貨物船の中で騒ぐんじゃない！","romaji":"Watashi no kamotsusen no naka de sawagu n ja nai!","german":"Don't make a racket on my cargo ship!","furigana":"わたし|かもつせん|なか|さわ"},{"japanese":"港には大きな貨物船が何隻も止まっている。","romaji":"Minato ni wa ōkina kamotsusen ga nanseki mo tomatte iru.","german":"Several large cargo ships are anchored in the port.","furigana":"みなと|おお|かもつせん|なんせき|と"},{"japanese":"この車は貨物船でアジアから運ばれてきました。","romaji":"Kono kuruma wa kamotsusen de Ajia kara hakobarete kimashita.","german":"This car was shipped from Asia on a cargo ship.","furigana":"くるま|かもつせん|はこ"}],"notes":"貨物船 is a cargo ship, made up of 貨物 (\"freight, goods\") and 船. Counterparts are 客船 (\"passenger ship\") and 漁船 (\"fishing boat\"); more specific types are コンテナ船 and タンカー. Ships are counted with 隻 (何隻). Goods are 貨物船で運ぶ; a freight train is accordingly 貨物列車.","levelNote":"Compound: 貨物 (N2) + 船 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"23歳の新入社員は82歳のCEOと結婚して、一緒に仕事をする人全員を驚かした。","romaji":"23sai no shinnyū shain wa 82sai no CEO to kekkon shite, issho ni shigoto o suru hito zen'in o odorokashita.","german":"A 23-year-old new employee married an 82-year-old CEO, surprising everyone who worked with them.","furigana":"さい|しんにゅうしゃいん|さい|けっこん|いっしょ|しごと|ひとぜんいん|おどろ"},{"japanese":"後ろから急に声をかけて、友だちを驚かした。","romaji":"Ushiro kara kyū ni koe o kakete, tomodachi o odorokashita.","german":"I called out suddenly from behind and startled my friend.","furigana":"うし|きゅう|こえ|とも|おどろ"},{"japanese":"驚かさないでよ！心臓が止まるかと思った。","romaji":"Odorokasanaide yo! Shinzō ga tomaru ka to omotta.","german":"Don't scare me! I thought my heart would stop.","furigana":"おどろ|しんぞう|と|おも"}],"notes":"驚かす is the transitive counterpart of 驚く \"to be surprised, to be startled\" and means \"to surprise, to startle\": 人を驚かす, 世間を驚かす \"to cause a stir\". Synonymous and somewhat more common in everyday speech is the causative form 驚かせる; more casually people say びっくりさせる. A typical exclamation is 驚かさないでよ！ \"Don't startle me like that!\"","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「今朝は4時から起きてるよ。」「嘘でしょ？いったい何してるの？」「えっと…家を大掃除してるの。」","romaji":"\"Kesa wa yoji kara okiteru yo.\" \"Uso desho? Ittai nani shiteru no?\" \"Etto... ie o ōsōji shiteru no.\"","german":"\"I've been up since four this morning.\" \"No way! What on earth are you doing?\" \"Um... I'm giving the whole house a deep clean.\"","furigana":"けさ|じ|お|うそ|なに|いえ|おおそうじ"},{"japanese":"荷物を片付けてから、店の床を掃除しました。","romaji":"Nimotsu o katazukete kara, mise no yuka o sōji shimashita.","german":"After putting away the luggage, I cleaned the floor of the shop.","furigana":"にもつ|かたづ|みせ|ゆか|そうじ"}],"notes":"掃除する means \"to clean\" or \"to tidy up\". The place cleaned takes を as its object: 家を掃除する, 床を掃除する. 大掃除する refers to a thorough, large-scale cleaning. In conversation, 掃除している and 起きている are shortened to 掃除してる and 起きてる; this shows the casual tone of the first example. In the second example, 〜てから orders the actions: first put things away, then clean the floor. Without する, 掃除 is a noun.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 8"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"水泳中は、子供から目を離すと危ないよ！","romaji":"Suieichū wa, kodomo kara me o hanasu to abunai yo!","german":"It's dangerous to take your eyes off a child while swimming!","furigana":"すいえいちゅう|こども|め|はな|あぶ"},{"japanese":"電車が来るので、子どもの手を離さないでください。","romaji":"Densha ga kuru node, kodomo no te o hanasanaide kudasai.","german":"A train is coming, so please don't let go of your child's hand.","furigana":"でんしゃ|く|こ|て|はな"},{"japanese":"テストなので、机と机を少し離して並べてください。","romaji":"Tesuto na node, tsukue to tsukue o sukoshi hanashite narabete kudasai.","german":"Since it's a test, please line up the desks a little apart from each other.","furigana":"つくえ|つくえ|すこ|はな|なら"}],"notes":"離す is transitive and means \"to separate, to move apart, to leave a distance\" as well as \"to let go\": 机を離して並べる, 手を離す. The phrase 目を離す (\"to take one's eyes off something, to not pay attention\") is very common, mostly negated: 目を離さないで. The intransitive counterpart is 離れる (\"to move away\"). Do not confuse it with 話す (\"to speak\") and 放す (\"to set free\", e.g. 魚を川に放す), which are pronounced the same.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この訴訟は泥沼化しています。","romaji":"Kono soshō wa doronumaka shite imasu.","german":"This lawsuit has become a quagmire.","furigana":"そしょう|どろぬまか"},{"japanese":"雨のあと、畑の道が泥沼のようになった。","romaji":"Ame no ato, hatake no michi ga doronuma no yō ni natta.","german":"After the rain, the path through the field turned into a mudhole.","furigana":"あめ|はたけ|みち|どろぬま"},{"japanese":"彼は借金の泥沼から抜け出せずにいる。","romaji":"Kare wa shakkin no doronuma kara nukedasezu ni iru.","german":"He can't get out of the quagmire of debt.","furigana":"かれ|しゃっきん|どろぬま|ぬ|だ"}],"notes":"泥沼 is literally a muddy swamp, but it is mostly used figuratively for a stuck, hopeless situation from which one can no longer escape. Typical are 泥沼化する (\"to get bogged down, to turn into a mudslinging fight\"), 泥沼の争い/離婚 and 〜の泥沼から抜け出す (\"to get out of the quagmire of ...\"). For a real wetland in nature, the more neutral 沼 or 湿地 is used.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は出版社で雑誌の編集者として働いている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa shuppansha de zasshi no henshūsha to shite hataraite iru.","german":"She works as a magazine editor at a publishing company.","furigana":"かのじょ|しゅっぱんしゃ|ざっし|へんしゅうしゃ|はたら"},{"japanese":"原稿を編集者に送ったら、たくさん直された。","romaji":"Genkō o henshūsha ni okuttara, takusan naosareta.","german":"When I sent the manuscript to the editor, it came back with lots of corrections.","furigana":"げんこう|へんしゅうしゃ|おく|なお"},{"japanese":"動画の編集者を募集しています。","romaji":"Dōga no henshūsha o boshū shite imasu.","german":"We are recruiting video editors.","furigana":"どうが|へんしゅうしゃ|ぼしゅう"}],"notes":"編集者 is the person who edits books, magazines, newspapers or even videos - at a publishing house, an \"editor\". It consists of 編集 (\"editing\", 編集する) and 者. The head is 編集長 (\"editor-in-chief\"); among authors people often say 担当編集 or simply 編集さん. Whoever compiles a work is listed in the imprint as 編者.","levelNote":"Compound: 編集 (N2) + 者 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"スーパーのちらし、ここに置いてなかった？","romaji":"Sūpā no chirashi, koko ni oitenakatta?","german":"Wasn't the supermarket flyer left here?","furigana":"お"},{"japanese":"駅前でイベントのちらしを配るアルバイトをした。","romaji":"Ekimae de ibento no chirashi o kubaru arubaito o shita.","german":"I had a part-time job handing out event flyers in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|くば"},{"japanese":"ひな祭りには、母がちらし寿司を作ってくれる。","romaji":"Hinamatsuri ni wa, haha ga chirashizushi o tsukutte kureru.","german":"On Girls' Day, my mother makes chirashi sushi for me.","furigana":"まつ|はは|ずし|つく"}],"notes":"ちらし is an advertising flyer or leaflet, the kind that lands in your mailbox, is inserted in the newspaper or is handed out on the street. The word is almost never written with kanji, but as チラシ or ちらし; typical verbs are 配る (\"to distribute\") and 入る (\"to be inserted\"). It comes from 散らす (\"to scatter\"). As a short form of ちらし寿司, it also denotes sushi rice with colorful toppings scattered over it.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どうやら、武装勢力に襲われたようだ。","romaji":"Douyara, busou seiryoku ni osowareta you da.","german":"It seems they were attacked by an armed group.","furigana":"ぶそうせいりょく|おそ"},{"japanese":"この銀行の警備員は全員武装している。","romaji":"Kono ginkō no keibiin wa zen'in busō shite iru.","german":"All the security guards at this bank are armed.","furigana":"ぎんこう|けいびいん|ぜんいんぶそう"},{"japanese":"話し合いの結果、その国は武装解除に同意した。","romaji":"Hanashiai no kekka, sono kuni wa busō kaijo ni dōi shita.","german":"After the talks, the country agreed to disarmament.","furigana":"はな|あ|けっか|くに|ぶそうかいじょ|どうい"}],"notes":"武装 is \"armament\" and, as an intransitive する-verb, \"to arm oneself\"; the weapon takes で: 銃で武装する. Very common are the participle 武装した (\"armed\") and compounds like 武装勢力 (\"armed group, militia\") and 武装解除 (\"disarmament\"). The weapon itself is 武器. Figuratively people also say 理論で武装する (\"to arm oneself with arguments\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"異常気象が続いています。","romaji":"Ijō kishō ga tsuzuite imasu.","german":"Abnormal weather continues.","furigana":"いじょうきしょう|つづ"},{"japanese":"検査の結果、特に異常はありませんでした。","romaji":"Kensa no kekka, toku ni ijō wa arimasen deshita.","german":"The test results showed nothing in particular wrong.","furigana":"けんさ|けっか|とく|いじょう"},{"japanese":"彼は異常なほどきれい好きだ。","romaji":"Kare wa ijō na hodo kireizuki da.","german":"He is abnormally fond of cleanliness.","furigana":"かれ|いじょう|ず"}],"notes":"異常 is a noun and a な-adjective: \"abnormal, unusual\"; as a noun also \"malfunction, abnormality\". Typical are 異常がある/ない (for example in medical examinations or machines), 異常な + noun, the adverb 異常に (\"unusually, excessively\") and compounds like 異常気象 (\"extreme weather\"). The opposite is 正常. Do not confuse it with the identically pronounced 以上 (\"more than; that's all\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"股間がすごく痒い。","romaji":"Kokan ga sugoku kayui.","german":"My crotch itches terribly.","furigana":"こかん|かゆ"},{"japanese":"サッカーでボールが股間に当たって、彼はしばらく動けなかった。","romaji":"Sakkā de bōru ga kokan ni atatte, kare wa shibaraku ugokenakatta.","german":"In soccer, the ball hit him in the groin, and he couldn't move for a while.","furigana":"こかん|あ|かれ|うご"},{"japanese":"このズボンは股間の部分が少しきつい。","romaji":"Kono zubon wa kokan no bubun ga sukoshi kitsui.","german":"These pants are a little tight in the crotch.","furigana":"こかん|ぶぶん|すこ"}],"notes":"股間 is the crotch or groin area between the thighs. The word is matter-of-fact and anatomical, but direct; you hear it, for example, in sports (股間にボールが当たる) or when discussing complaints. For the crotch of trousers, people rather say 股 (また) or 股下 (\"inseam length\"); medically more precise is 鼠径部 (\"groin\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"次は股関節のストレッチです。","romaji":"Tsugi wa kokansetsu no sutoretchi desu.","german":"Als Nächstes kommt eine Dehnung für die Hüftgelenke.","furigana":"つぎ|こかんせつ"},{"japanese":"祖母は転んで股関節を骨折しました。","romaji":"Sobo wa koronde kokansetsu o kossetsu shimashita.","german":"Meine Großmutter ist gestürzt und hat sich das Hüftgelenk gebrochen.","furigana":"そぼ|ころ|こかんせつ|こっせつ"},{"japanese":"股関節が硬いと、腰を痛めやすいそうです。","romaji":"Kokansetsu ga katai to, koshi o itameyasui sō desu.","german":"Wer steife Hüftgelenke hat, bekommt angeblich leicht Rückenschmerzen.","furigana":"こかんせつ|かた|こし|いた"}],"notes":"股関節 ist das Hüftgelenk, gebildet aus 股 („Schritt, Oberschenkel“) und 関節 („Gelenk“); vgl. 膝関節 („Kniegelenk“) und 肩関節 („Schultergelenk“). Man hört es beim Sport und Dehnen (股関節が硬い/柔らかい) sowie in der Medizin (股関節を骨折する). Das deutsche „Hüfte“ entspricht sonst eher 腰 oder 腰回り, das aber auch den unteren Rücken meint.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"屁の話をしているんだと思うんですが、合ってますかね。","romaji":"He no hanashi o shite iru n da to omou n desu ga, attemasu ka ne.","german":"I think you're talking about farts, am I right?","furigana":"へ|はなし|おも|あ"},{"japanese":"こんな問題、屁でもないよ。","romaji":"Konna mondai, he demo nai yo.","german":"A problem like this is nothing to me.","furigana":"もんだい|へ"},{"japanese":"屁理屈ばかり言わないで、早く片付けなさい。","romaji":"Herikutsu bakari iwanaide, hayaku katazukenasai.","german":"Stop making hair-splitting excuses and clean up quickly.","furigana":"へりくつ|い|はや|かたづ"}],"notes":"屁 is the vulgar word for \"fart\"; the neutral and child-friendly word is おなら. To fart is vulgarly 屁をこく or 屁をひる, neutrally おならをする. Figuratively, 屁 stands for something worthless: 屁でもない \"a piece of cake, no problem at all\", and 屁理屈 are \"quibbles, far-fetched excuses\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"それは屁理屈ではなく事実である。","romaji":"Sore wa herikutsu de wa naku jijitsu dearu.","german":"That is not a quibble, it is a fact.","furigana":"へりくつ|じじつ"},{"japanese":"屁理屈を言わないで、早く宿題をしなさい。","romaji":"Herikutsu o iwanaide, hayaku shukudai o shinasai.","german":"Stop making excuses with your hair-splitting and do your homework quickly.","furigana":"へりくつ|い|はや|しゅくだい"},{"japanese":"彼は何を言っても屁理屈をこねて、謝ろうとしない。","romaji":"Kare wa nani o itte mo herikutsu o konete, ayamarō to shinai.","german":"No matter what you say, he makes quibbling arguments and won't apologize.","furigana":"かれ|なに|い|へりくつ|あやま"}],"notes":"屁理屈 is composed of 屁 (\"fart\") and 理屈 (\"logic, reasoning\") and denotes quibbles or far-fetched arguments with which one tries to talk one's way out. Typical are 屁理屈を言う and 屁理屈をこねる (\"to split hairs\"); 屁理屈ばかり言う is a common reproach from parents to children. The word is colloquial and derogatory. A mere apology or excuse, by contrast, is 言い訳.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その居酒屋の和風里芋サラダは美味しいよ。","romaji":"Sono izakaya no wafū satoimo sarada wa oishii yo.","german":"The Japanese-style taro salad at that izakaya is delicious.","furigana":"いざかや|わふうさといも|おい"},{"japanese":"里芋の煮物は祖母の得意料理です。","romaji":"Satoimo no nimono wa sobo no tokui ryōri desu.","german":"Simmered taro is my grandmother's specialty.","furigana":"さといも|にもの|そぼ|とくいりょうり"},{"japanese":"里芋の皮をむくと、手がかゆくなることがある。","romaji":"Satoimo no kawa o muku to, te ga kayuku naru koto ga aru.","german":"When you peel taro, your hands can get itchy.","furigana":"さといも|かわ|て"}],"notes":"里芋 is the taro tuber, a typical autumn vegetable of Japanese cuisine with a sticky, soft (ねっとり) texture. It is eaten mainly braised as 煮物 or in soups such as 豚汁. It is often written in kana (さといも). When peeling, it can make the skin itch. Other \"potatoes\": じゃがいも (\"potato\"), さつまいも (\"sweet potato\"), 長芋 (\"Chinese yam\").","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"居候をするなら、せめて料理くらいは覚えた方がいい。","romaji":"Isourou o suru nara, semete ryouri kurai wa oboeta hou ga ii.","german":"If you're going to freeload, you should at least learn to cook.","furigana":"いそうろう|りょうり|おぼ|ほう"},{"japanese":"大学を卒業してから、ずっとおじの家に居候している。","romaji":"Daigaku o sotsugyō shite kara, zutto oji no ie ni isōrō shite iru.","german":"Since graduating from university, I've been living off my uncle at his house.","furigana":"だいがく|そつぎょう|いえ|いそうろう"},{"japanese":"居候の身なので、あまり文句は言えない。","romaji":"Isōrō no mi na node, amari monku wa ienai.","german":"As I'm just a freeloader, I can't complain much.","furigana":"いそうろう|み|もんく|い"}],"notes":"居候 is someone who lives and eats at others' places without paying for it, and, as an intransitive する-verb, living in that way: 友達の家に居候する. The word sounds rather self-deprecating or slightly disparaging, but not as harsh as \"parasite\". Typical is 居候の身 (\"as someone who is only tolerated here\"). Someone who pays regular rent is instead a 下宿人 or ルームメイト.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"うちの子、まだ口をそでで拭うクセがあるんです。","romaji":"Uchi no ko, mada kuchi o sode de nuguu kuse ga aru n desu.","german":"My child still has the habit of wiping his mouth with his sleeve.","furigana":"こ|くち|ぬぐ"},{"japanese":"彼はハンカチで額の汗を拭った。","romaji":"Kare wa hankachi de hitai no ase o nugutta.","german":"He wiped the sweat from his forehead with a handkerchief.","furigana":"かれ|ひたい|あせ|ぬぐ"},{"japanese":"説明を聞いても、不安が拭えなかった。","romaji":"Setsumei o kiite mo, fuan ga nuguenakatta.","german":"Even after hearing the explanation, I couldn't shake my anxiety.","furigana":"せつめい|き|ふあん|ぬぐ"}],"notes":"拭う means \"to wipe away\" and is somewhat more elevated than the everyday 拭く, which is written with the same kanji. Typical are 汗を拭う, 涙を拭う, 口を拭う; the means takes で (ハンカチで, 袖で). Figuratively it means \"to get rid of a feeling or a stain\", mostly negated: 不安を拭えない (\"cannot shake the worry\"), 汚名を拭う (\"to clear one's name\"). A table or a window, by contrast, is wiped with 拭く.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"出張がたくさんありすぎて、マイルが本当にすぐ貯まるよ！","romaji":"Shutchou ga takusan arisugite, mairu ga hontou ni sugu tamaru yo!","german":"I have so many business trips that my miles really pile up quickly!","furigana":"しゅっちょう|ほんとう|た"},{"japanese":"毎月少しずつ貯金して、やっと十万円貯まった。","romaji":"Maitsuki sukoshi zutsu chokin shite, yatto jūman'en tamatta.","german":"I saved a little every month and finally reached 100,000 yen.","furigana":"まいつきすこ|ちょきん|じゅうまんえんた"},{"japanese":"このカードで買い物をすると、ポイントが貯まります。","romaji":"Kono kādo de kaimono o suru to, pointo ga tamarimasu.","german":"If you shop with this card, you'll accumulate points.","furigana":"か|もの|た"}],"notes":"貯まる is intransitive and means that money, savings or points accumulate: お金が貯まる, ポイントが貯まる, マイルが貯まる. The transitive counterpart is 貯める (\"to save, to build up savings\"). The identically pronounced 溜まる stands for things that pile up (often undesirably): dust, water, stress, work. It is also often simply written in kana as たまる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"僅差でライバルに負けて、すごくくやしかった。","romaji":"Kinsa de raibaru ni makete, sugoku kuyashikatta.","german":"I lost to my rival by a narrow margin and was really frustrated.","furigana":"きんさ|ま"},{"japanese":"選挙は最後まで分からず、僅差で野党が勝った。","romaji":"Senkyo wa saigo made wakarazu, kinsa de yatō ga katta.","german":"The election was undecided until the end, and the opposition won by a narrow margin.","furigana":"せんきょ|さいご|わ|きんさ|やとう|か"},{"japanese":"一位と二位の点数は、ほんの僅差だった。","romaji":"Ichi-i to ni-i no tensū wa, honno kinsa datta.","german":"The scores of first and second place were separated by only a hair.","furigana":"いちい|にい|てんすう|きんさ"}],"notes":"僅差 is a narrow margin or slight difference, especially in competitions, elections and votes: 僅差で勝つ/負ける, 僅差の勝利. The first kanji is also in 僅か (わずか, \"slight\"). The word sounds more like news and sports reports; in conversation people also say ぎりぎりで勝った or ちょっとの差で負けた. The opposite is 大差 (\"big margin\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「いつも何してるの？」「毎日一日中物真似の練習をしてるよ。」","romaji":"Itsumo nani shiteru no? Mainichi ichinichijuu monomane no renshuu o shiteru yo.","german":"\"What do you do all the time?\" \"I practice impressions all day every day.\"","furigana":"なに|まいにちいちにちじゅうものまね|れんしゅう"},{"japanese":"彼は先生のものまねが上手で、クラスのみんなを笑わせる。","romaji":"Kare wa sensei no monomane ga jōzu de, kurasu no minna o warawaseru.","german":"He is good at impressions of the teacher and makes everyone in the class laugh.","furigana":"かれ|せんせい|じょうず|わら"},{"japanese":"飲み会で部長の物真似をしたら、本人に見られてしまった。","romaji":"Nomikai de buchō no monomane o shitara, honnin ni mirarete shimatta.","german":"I did an impression of the department head at a drinking party, and he saw it himself.","furigana":"の|かい|ぶちょう|ものまね|ほんにん|み"}],"notes":"物真似 is imitating the voice, way of speaking or gestures of a certain person or animal, mostly for entertainment: 先生の物真似をする, 物真似が上手だ. It is mostly written in kana (ものまね, モノマネ); professionals are called ものまね芸人. The more general 真似 (真似をする, 真似る) means any kind of copying or imitating, including in learning or imitating a style.","levelNote":"Compound: 物 (N5) + 真似 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「sheep」の複数形は「sheep」です。","romaji":"Shiipu no fukusūkei wa shiipu desu.","german":"Der Plural von „sheep“ ist „sheep“.","furigana":"ふくすうけい"},{"japanese":"ドイツ語の名詞は、複数形が不規則なものが多い。","romaji":"Doitsugo no meishi wa, fukusūkei ga fukisoku na mono ga ōi.","german":"Viele deutsche Substantive haben eine unregelmäßige Pluralform.","furigana":"ご|めいし|ふくすうけい|ふきそく|おお"},{"japanese":"日本語の名詞には、ふつう複数形がありません。","romaji":"Nihongo no meishi ni wa, futsū fukusūkei ga arimasen.","german":"Japanische Substantive haben normalerweise keine Pluralform.","furigana":"にほんご|めいし|ふくすうけい"}],"notes":"複数形 ist die grammatische Pluralform, das Gegenstück ist 単数形 („Singularform“). Man sagt 〜の複数形 („der Plural von …“); 形 wird hier けい gelesen, nicht かたち. Im Japanischen selbst haben Nomen normalerweise keine Pluralform; Mehrzahl wird bei Bedarf mit Suffixen wie 〜たち oder 〜ら oder mit Wiederholung (人々) ausgedrückt. Das Wort begegnet vor allem im Fremdsprachenunterricht.","levelNote":"Compound: 複数 (N2) + 形 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"肺臓は胸の左右に一つずつあります。","romaji":"Haizō wa mune no sayū ni hitotsu zutsu arimasu.","german":"There is one lung on each side of the chest.","furigana":"はいぞう|むね|さゆう|ひと"},{"japanese":"たばこの煙は肺臓に大きなダメージを与える。","romaji":"Tabako no kemuri wa haizō ni ōkina damēji o ataeru.","german":"Cigarette smoke does great damage to the lungs.","furigana":"けむり|はいぞう|おお|あた"},{"japanese":"理科の授業で、心臓や肺臓のはたらきを習った。","romaji":"Rika no jugyō de, shinzō ya haizō no hataraki o naratta.","german":"In science class, we learned how the heart and lungs work.","furigana":"りか|じゅぎょう|しんぞう|はいぞう|なら"}],"notes":"肺臓 is the anatomical-medical term for the lung, comparable to 心臓 (\"heart\") or 腎臓 (\"kidney\"). In everyday life and in almost all compounds people simply say 肺: 肺炎 (\"pneumonia\"), 肺がん (\"lung cancer\"), 肺活量. 肺臓 appears mainly in textbooks, technical texts or lists of organs (内臓).","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"夏休みは田舎の祖父母の家で過ごします。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi wa inaka no sofubo no ie de sugoshimasu.","german":"I spend summer vacation at my grandparents' house in the countryside.","furigana":"なつやす|いなか|そふぼ|いえ|す"},{"japanese":"私は小さいころ、祖父母に育てられました。","romaji":"Watashi wa chiisai koro, sofubo ni sodateraremashita.","german":"I was raised by my grandparents when I was little.","furigana":"わたし|ちい|そふぼ|そだ"},{"japanese":"敬老の日に、祖父母に手紙を書いた。","romaji":"Keirō no hi ni, sofubo ni tegami o kaita.","german":"On Respect for the Aged Day, I wrote a letter to my grandparents.","furigana":"けいろう|ひ|そふぼ|てがみ|か"}],"notes":"祖父母 is the collective word for \"grandparents\", from 祖父 (\"grandfather\") and 祖母 (\"grandmother\"). It is matter-of-fact and is used for one's own grandparents toward outsiders, in forms and in texts. In family conversation people say おじいちゃんとおばあちゃん; when speaking about someone else's grandparents one politely says おじいさまとおばあさま. A pair of great-grandparents is 曾祖父母.","levelNote":"Compound: 祖父 (N4) + 母 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"散歩中の犬のふんは、飼い主が片づけてください。","romaji":"Sanpochū no inu no fun wa, kainushi ga katazukete kudasai.","german":"Please clean up your dog's droppings during walks.","furigana":"さんぽちゅう|いぬ|か|ぬし|かた"},{"japanese":"迷子の猫を見つけたので、飼い主を探しています。","romaji":"Maigo no neko o mitsuketa node, kainushi o sagashite imasu.","german":"I found a lost cat, so I'm looking for its owner.","furigana":"まいご|ねこ|み|か|ぬし|さが"},{"japanese":"ペットは飼い主に似るとよく言われる。","romaji":"Petto wa kainushi ni niru to yoku iwareru.","german":"It's often said that pets resemble their owners.","furigana":"か|ぬし|に|い"}],"notes":"飼い主 is the keeper or owner of an animal, formed from 飼う (\"to keep an animal\") and 主 (\"master, owner\"). It is used only for animals; the owner of an object is 持ち主, formally 所有者. Typical are 犬の飼い主, 飼い主を探す (for strays) and notices addressed to 飼い主. The kept animal itself is called 飼い犬 or 飼い猫.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コンタクトレンズを傷つけちゃったかも。","romaji":"Kontakutorenzu o kizutsukechatta kamo.","german":"I may have scratched my contact lens.","furigana":"きず"},{"japanese":"彼の一言は私を深く傷つけた。","romaji":"Kare no hitokoto wa watashi o fukaku kizutsuketa.","german":"His remark hurt me deeply.","furigana":"かれ|ひとこと|わたし|ふか|きず"},{"japanese":"ナイフで人を傷つけた男が逮捕された。","romaji":"Naifu de hito o kizutsuketa otoko ga taiho sareta.","german":"A man who injured someone with a knife was arrested.","furigana":"ひと|きず|おとこ|たいほ"}],"notes":"傷つける is transitive and has three meanings: to injure someone physically, to hurt someone emotionally (人を傷つける, プライドを傷つける), and to scratch or damage objects. The intransitive counterpart is 傷つく (\"to get hurt, be offended; to get scratched\"). In everyday speech it is most often used for feelings; for a physical injury people also say colloquially けがをさせる. Also written 傷付ける.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今朝、六時に目覚ましが鳴った時に、その時計を投げ捨ててしまいました。","romaji":"Kesa, rokuji ni mezamashi ga natta toki ni, sono tokei o nagesutete shimaimashita.","german":"When the alarm rang at six this morning, I ended up flinging the clock away.","furigana":"けさ|ろくじ|めざ|な|とき|とけい|な|す"},{"japanese":"車の窓からごみを投げ捨てるのはやめてください。","romaji":"Kuruma no mado kara gomi o nagesuteru no wa yamete kudasai.","german":"Please stop throwing trash out of the car window.","furigana":"くるま|まど|な|す"},{"japanese":"彼は歌手になる夢を投げ捨てて、親の店を継いだ。","romaji":"Kare wa kashu ni naru yume o nagesutete, oya no mise o tsuida.","german":"He threw away his dream of becoming a singer and took over his parents' shop.","furigana":"かれ|かしゅ|ゆめ|な|す|おや|みせ|つ"}],"notes":"投げ捨てる combines 投げる (\"to throw\") and 捨てる (\"to throw away\") and means to fling something away carelessly or violently - for example trash out of a window or an object in anger. For the wild dumping of garbage, people also say ポイ捨て colloquially. Figuratively, one can also 投げ捨てる plans, dreams or pride (\"to throw overboard\"). For normal disposal, 捨てる is enough.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ショットガンに弾を込める時間がなかったんだ。","romaji":"Shottogan ni tama o komeru jikan ga nakatta n da.","german":"I didn't have time to load the shotgun.","furigana":"たま|こ|じかん"},{"japanese":"母の誕生日に、心を込めて手紙を書きました。","romaji":"Haha no tanjōbi ni, kokoro o komete tegami o kakimashita.","german":"For my mother's birthday, I wrote her a letter with all my heart.","furigana":"はは|たんじょうび|こころ|こ|てがみ|か"},{"japanese":"力を込めてドアを押したが、開かなかった。","romaji":"Chikara o komete doa o oshita ga, akanakatta.","german":"I pushed the door with all my strength, but it wouldn't open.","furigana":"ちから|こ|お|あ"}],"notes":"込める means \"to put in, pack in\" and is used mainly figuratively: putting feelings, effort or strength into something. Very common are 心を込めて (\"with all one's heart\"), 気持ちを込める, 願いを込める and 力を込める (\"to put strength into\"). Concretely it means \"to load\": 銃に弾を込める. Related are 〜込み (\"included\", e.g. 税込み) and the intransitive 込む.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"駅で飛び込み自殺があって、電車が一時間止まった。","romaji":"Eki de tobikomi jisatsu ga atte, densha ga ichijikan tomatta.","german":"There was a suicide by jumping onto the tracks at the station, and the trains stopped for an hour.","furigana":"えき|と|こ|じさつ|でんしゃ|いちじかんと"},{"japanese":"飛び込み自殺を防ぐため、ホームドアをつける駅が増えている。","romaji":"Tobikomi jisatsu o fusegu tame, hōmu doa o tsukeru eki ga fuete iru.","german":"More and more stations are installing platform doors to prevent suicides by jumping in front of trains.","furigana":"と|こ|じさつ|ふせ|えき|ふ"},{"japanese":"ニュースによると、飛び込み自殺の件数は去年より減ったそうだ。","romaji":"Nyūsu ni yoru to, tobikomi jisatsu no kensū wa kyonen yori hetta sō da.","german":"According to the news, the number of train-jumping suicides decreased from last year.","furigana":"と|こ|じさつ|けんすう|きょねん|へ"}],"notes":"飛び込み自殺 denotes suicide by jumping in front of an approaching train, formed from 飛び込み (\"jumping in\") and 自殺. The word is matter-of-fact and appears mainly in news and statistics. Train announcements use the euphemism 人身事故 (\"accident involving a person\") instead. Jumping from a building is 飛び降り自殺. It is a sensitive topic - in conversation it should be used carefully.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"急いで申込書に書き込んだ方がいいよ。あと十分で事務所は閉まっちゃうんだから。","romaji":"Isoide moushikomisho ni kakikonda hou ga ii yo. Ato juppun de jimusho wa shimachau n da kara.","german":"You'd better fill in the application form quickly. The office closes in ten minutes."},{"japanese":"SNSに人の悪口を書き込むのはやめよう。","romaji":"Esu-enu-esu ni hito no waruguchi o kakikomu no wa yameyō.","german":"Let's stop posting bad things about people on social media.","furigana":"ひと|わるぐち|か|こ"},{"japanese":"大事なところを教科書に書き込んでおいた。","romaji":"Daiji na tokoro o kyōkasho ni kakikonde oita.","german":"I wrote the important parts into my textbook.","furigana":"だいじ|きょうかしょ|か|こ"}],"notes":"書き込む means \"to write in, to enter\": into a form, in the margin of a book or in a calendar; the place takes に: 申込書に名前を書き込む. Today the meaning \"to post online\" (掲示板やSNSに書き込む) is very common; the noun 書き込み is then the post or comment. For formally filling in forms, 記入する is also used. In IT it additionally means \"to write data\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"怖くて人込みの中に行けません。","romaji":"Kowakute hitogomi no naka ni ikemasen.","german":"I'm too scared to go into crowds.","furigana":"こわ|ひとご|なか|い"},{"japanese":"お祭りの人込みの中で、子どもとはぐれてしまった。","romaji":"O-matsuri no hitogomi no naka de, kodomo to hagurete shimatta.","german":"I got separated from my child in the crowd at the festival.","furigana":"まつ|ひとご|なか|こ"},{"japanese":"週末の原宿はすごい人込みだった。","romaji":"Shūmatsu no Harajuku wa sugoi hitogomi datta.","german":"Harajuku was incredibly crowded on the weekend.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|はらじゅく|ひとご"}],"notes":"人込み is a crowd or throng in a place, such as at a festival, in a station or in a shopping street. Today the spelling 人混み is actually more common. Typical are 人込みの中, 人込みを避ける, 人込みが苦手 (\"to dislike crowds\") and すごい人込みだ. That a place is full is expressed with 混む or 混雑する; 群衆 is a formal, rather written word for a crowd.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この染みなんとか取れないかなあ。","romaji":"Kono shimi nantoka torenai ka nā.","german":"I wonder if I can get this stain out somehow.","furigana":"し|と"},{"japanese":"白いシャツにコーヒーのしみが付いてしまった。","romaji":"Shiroi shatsu ni kōhī no shimi ga tsuite shimatta.","german":"A coffee stain got on my white shirt.","furigana":"しろ|つ"},{"japanese":"年を取って、顔のしみが増えてきた。","romaji":"Toshi o totte, kao no shimi ga fuete kita.","german":"As I've gotten older, the spots on my face have increased.","furigana":"とし|と|かお|ふ"}],"notes":"染み is a stain that has soaked into fabric or paper, and is mostly written in kana (しみ, シミ). Typical are しみが付く/できる, しみを取る/抜く (しみ抜き = \"stain removal\"). It also denotes pigment spots or age spots on the skin. General dirt is 汚れ. The word comes from 染みる (\"to soak in, to seep\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"なんとか洋服を全部スーツケースに詰め込めました。","romaji":"Nantoka yōfuku o zenbu sūtsukēsu ni tsumekomemashita.","german":"Somehow I managed to cram all my clothes into the suitcase.","furigana":"ようふく|ぜんぶ|つ|こ"},{"japanese":"試験の前の晩に、単語を一気に頭に詰め込んだ。","romaji":"Shiken no mae no ban ni, tango o ikki ni atama ni tsumekonda.","german":"The night before the exam, I crammed vocabulary into my head all at once.","furigana":"しけん|まえ|ばん|たんご|いっき|あたま|つ|こ"},{"japanese":"朝の電車には、人がぎゅうぎゅうに詰め込まれている。","romaji":"Asa no densha ni wa, hito ga gyūgyū ni tsumekomarete iru.","german":"The morning train is packed tight with people.","furigana":"あさ|でんしゃ|ひと|つ|こ"}],"notes":"詰め込む means \"to stuff in, to pack full\": clothes into a suitcase, people into a train, appointments into a calendar. The container takes に: スーツケースに服を詰め込む. Figuratively it means \"to cram, to drum knowledge in\" (頭に詰め込む); 詰め込み教育 criticizes pure rote learning. The simple 詰める means \"to fill, to pack\" without the connotation of \"too much\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"カウンターの上に「チップ大歓迎」と書かれた箱がありましたよ。","romaji":"Kauntaa no ue ni chippu dai kangei to kakareta hako ga arimashita yo.","german":"There was a box on the counter with \"Tips greatly welcome\" written on it.","furigana":"うえ|だいかんげい|か|はこ"},{"japanese":"新入社員を歓迎するパーティーが開かれた。","romaji":"Shinnyū shain o kangei suru pātī ga hirakareta.","german":"A party was held to welcome the new employees.","furigana":"しんにゅうしゃいん|かんげい|ひら"},{"japanese":"ホームステイ先の家族に温かく歓迎されました。","romaji":"Hōmusutei-saki no kazoku ni atatakaku kangei saremashita.","german":"I was warmly welcomed by my homestay family.","furigana":"さき|かぞく|あたた|かんげい"}],"notes":"歓迎 means \"welcome, reception\" and, as a transitive する-verb, \"to welcome someone\": 新入生を歓迎する; in the passive 温かく歓迎される. Common are 歓迎会 (\"welcome party\") and 大歓迎 (\"heartily welcome, very much appreciated\"), also for things: 初心者大歓迎. As a greeting formula at the door, one says ようこそ or いらっしゃいませ, not 歓迎.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"俺は顧客と個人的に仲良くなることには興味がないんだ。","romaji":"Ore wa kokyaku to kojinteki ni nakayoku naru koto ni wa kyōmi ga nai n da.","german":"I'm not interested in becoming personally close with customers.","furigana":"おれ|こきゃく|こじんてき|なかよ|きょうみ"},{"japanese":"スポーツにはあまり興味がない。","romaji":"Supōtsu ni wa amari kyōmi ga nai.","german":"I'm not very interested in sports.","furigana":"きょうみ"},{"japanese":"興味がないふりをしていたけど、本当は気になっていた。","romaji":"Kyōmi ga nai furi o shite ita kedo, hontō wa ki ni natte ita.","german":"I pretended not to be interested, but actually I was curious.","furigana":"きょうみ|ほんとう|き"}],"notes":"興味がない is the negative of 興味がある (\"to be interested\") and means \"to have no interest\"; the topic takes に: スポーツに興味がない. It is strengthened or softened with 全然 or あまり. Said directly to someone, it sounds rather blunt; more polite is 興味がありません or the evasive あまり詳しくなくて. For social topics, 関心がない sounds more matter-of-fact.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"とりあえず今は誤字脱字を見つけてくれればそれでいいから。","romaji":"Toriaezu ima wa goji datsuji o mitsukete kurereba sore de ii kara.","german":"For now, it's fine if you just find the typos and omitted characters.","furigana":"いま|ごじだつじ|み"},{"japanese":"この書類に誤字があるので、直してください。","romaji":"Kono shorui ni goji ga aru node, naoshite kudasai.","german":"There is a typo in this document, so please correct it.","furigana":"しょるい|ごじ|なお"},{"japanese":"テストで漢字の誤字が多くて、点を引かれた。","romaji":"Tesuto de kanji no goji ga ōkute, ten o hikareta.","german":"I had many wrong kanji on the test and lost points.","furigana":"かんじ|ごじ|おお|てん|ひ"}],"notes":"誤字 is a wrongly written character, i.e. a spelling or typing error; with kanji, for example a missing stroke or a similar but wrong character. The pair formula 誤字脱字 (\"typos and omissions\") is very common, e.g. in proofreading. Typical: 誤字がある, 誤字を直す. For errors in kanji conversion on the computer, 誤変換 is also used, and for pure typing mistakes, colloquially タイプミス.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"薬の誤用は命に関わることがあります。","romaji":"Kusuri no goyō wa inochi ni kakawaru koto ga arimasu.","german":"Misuse of medicine can be life-threatening.","furigana":"くすり|ごよう|いのち|かか"},{"japanese":"この言葉は、よく誤用されています。","romaji":"Kono kotoba wa, yoku goyō sarete imasu.","german":"This word is often misused.","furigana":"ことば|ごよう"},{"japanese":"敬語の誤用は、日本人にもよくあることです。","romaji":"Keigo no goyō wa, Nihonjin ni mo yoku aru koto desu.","german":"Misuse of honorific language is common even among Japanese people.","furigana":"けいご|ごよう|にほんじん"}],"notes":"誤用 means \"wrong usage, misuse\" and emphasizes the mistake, not the intent; as a transitive する-verb: 言葉を誤用する. It is particularly common with language (言葉の誤用, 敬語の誤用) and with medicines or devices. Deliberate abuse, e.g. of drugs or power, is by contrast 乱用 (濫用) or 悪用.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は英語がペラペラだと思ったが、とんだ誤算だった。","romaji":"Kanojo wa eigo ga perapera da to omotta ga, tonda gosan datta.","german":"I thought she was fluent in English, but that was a huge miscalculation.","furigana":"かのじょ|えいご|おも|ごさん"},{"japanese":"雨で客がほとんど来なかったのは、大きな誤算だった。","romaji":"Ame de kyaku ga hotondo konakatta no wa, ōkina gosan datta.","german":"It was a big miscalculation that hardly any customers came because of the rain.","furigana":"あめ|きゃく|こ|おお|ごさん"},{"japanese":"こんなに大勢来てくれるとは、うれしい誤算です。","romaji":"Konna ni ōzei kite kureru to wa, ureshii gosan desu.","german":"I didn't expect so many people to come - it's a happy miscalculation.","furigana":"おおぜいき|ごさん"}],"notes":"誤算 is literally an arithmetic error, but is almost always used in the sense of \"miscalculation, misjudgment\": one expected something, and it turned out differently. Typical are 大きな誤算, とんだ誤算 (\"a real blunder\") and the positive うれしい誤算 (\"a pleasant surprise\"). For a real arithmetic error, people more often say 計算ミス or 計算違い in everyday speech.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お前の兄貴はイケてるけど、俺の兄貴はダサイからな。","romaji":"Omae no aniki wa iketeru kedo, ore no aniki wa dasai kara na.","german":"Your older brother is cool, but mine is lame.","furigana":"まえ|あにき|おれ|あにき"},{"japanese":"兄貴は去年結婚して、今は大阪に住んでいる。","romaji":"Aniki wa kyonen kekkon shite, ima wa Ōsaka ni sunde iru.","german":"My older brother got married last year and now lives in Osaka.","furigana":"あにき|きょねんけっこん|いま|おおさか|す"},{"japanese":"田中先輩は頼りになる、みんなの兄貴のような人だ。","romaji":"Tanaka senpai wa tayori ni naru, minna no aniki no yō na hito da.","german":"Senior Tanaka is dependable, like an older brother to everyone.","furigana":"たなかせんぱい|たよ|あにき|ひと"}],"notes":"兄貴 is a familiar, rather male and colloquial word for one's older brother, both when talking about him and in direct address. Neutral are 兄 (one's own) and お兄さん; women use 兄貴 less often. It is also used for an admired older comrade or mentor - in clubs, among workers or even in the yakuza. The female counterpart is 姉貴.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私はまだ降参していません。","romaji":"Watashi wa mada kōsan shite imasen.","german":"I haven't given up yet.","furigana":"わたし|こうさん"},{"japanese":"敵は白い旗を上げて降参した。","romaji":"Teki wa shiroi hata o agete kōsan shita.","german":"The enemy raised a white flag and surrendered.","furigana":"てき|しろ|はた|あ|こうさん"},{"japanese":"このなぞなぞ、全然分からない。降参！","romaji":"Kono nazonazo, zenzen wakaranai. Kōsan!","german":"I have no idea about this riddle. I give up!","furigana":"ぜんぜんわ|こうさん"}],"notes":"降参 means \"to surrender, to yield\" and in everyday use mainly \"to give up, to admit defeat\"; it is intransitive, the opponent takes に: 敵に降参する. As an exclamation, 降参！ means \"I give up!\", for example at a riddle or game. For official military capitulation 降伏 is more usual; the neutral \"to give up on something\" is あきらめる.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「この牛丼は、私のおごりね。」「わあ、ありがとう。じゃあ、お言葉に甘えます。」","romaji":"\"Kono gyūdon wa, watashi no ogori ne.\" \"Wā, arigatō. Jā, okotoba ni amaemasu.\"","german":"\"This gyudon is my treat.\" \"Wow, thanks. Then I'll take you up on your offer.\"","furigana":"ぎゅうどん|わたし|ことば|あま"},{"japanese":"昼ご飯は駅前の牛丼屋で簡単に済ませた。","romaji":"Hiru gohan wa ekimae no gyūdon'ya de kantan ni sumaseta.","german":"I got lunch done quickly at the gyudon shop in front of the station.","furigana":"ひる|はん|えきまえ|ぎゅうどんや|かんたん|す"},{"japanese":"牛丼の大盛りに卵をつけてください。","romaji":"Gyūdon no ōmori ni tamago o tsukete kudasai.","german":"Please make it a large gyudon with an egg.","furigana":"ぎゅうどん|おおも|たまご"}],"notes":"牛丼 is a bowl of rice topped with thinly sliced beef and onions simmered in a sweetish soy sauce - the classic quick and cheap meal from chain restaurants called 牛丼屋. You order by size: 並盛り (regular) or 大盛り (large); popular extras are a raw egg (生卵) and pickled red ginger. The 丼 \"bowl\" is also in other rice dishes like 親子丼 or 天丼.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「遅れてごめんなさい。朝、少し気分が悪くて吐いてしまって。」「大丈夫だよ。遅れても来ないよりはましだよ。」","romaji":"Okurete gomennasai. Asa, sukoshi kibun ga warukute haite shimatte. Daijōbu da yo. Okurete mo konai yori wa mashi da yo.","german":"\"Sorry I'm late. I felt a bit sick this morning and threw up.\" \"It's okay. Better late than never.\"","furigana":"おく|あさ|すこ|きぶん|わる|は|だいじょうぶ|おく|こ"},{"japanese":"大きく息を吸って、ゆっくり吐いてください。","romaji":"Ōkiku iki o sutte, yukkuri haite kudasai.","german":"Please take a deep breath and exhale slowly.","furigana":"おお|いき|す|は"},{"japanese":"どんなにつらくても、彼は一度も弱音を吐かなかった。","romaji":"Donna ni tsurakute mo, kare wa ichido mo yowane o hakanakatta.","german":"No matter how hard it was, he never once complained.","furigana":"かれ|いちど|よわね|は"}],"notes":"吐く mainly means \"to vomit, to throw up\"; colloquially also もどす. It also means \"to breathe out, to emit\" (息を吐く, 煙を吐く) and \"to utter\", mostly negative: 弱音を吐く (\"to whine, to give in\"). Note: in ため息をつく or うそをつく, the same kanji is read つく. Related is 吐き気 (\"nausea\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二人の距離が少しずつ縮まってきた。","romaji":"Futari no kyori ga sukoshi zutsu chijimatte kita.","german":"The distance between the two of them has gradually been narrowing.","furigana":"ふたり|きょり|すこ|ちぢ"},{"japanese":"後半に入って、点差が一気に縮まった。","romaji":"Kōhan ni haitte, tensa ga ikki ni chijimatta.","german":"In the second half, the point gap narrowed all at once.","furigana":"こうはん|はい|てんさ|いっき|ちぢ"},{"japanese":"新しい道路ができて、通勤時間が十分縮まった。","romaji":"Atarashii dōro ga dekite, tsūkin jikan ga juppun chijimatta.","german":"With the new road, the commute got ten minutes shorter.","furigana":"あたら|どうろ|つうきんじかん|じゅっぷんちぢ"}],"notes":"縮まる is intransitive and means that a distance, difference or span of time gets smaller: 距離が縮まる, 差が縮まる, 時間が縮まる. Figuratively, 二人の距離が縮まる means \"to become closer\". The transitive counterpart is 縮める (\"to shorten\"). For fabric that shrinks in the wash, people say 縮む; to cower in fear is 縮こまる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は不純な動機から日本語を勉強し始めました。","romaji":"Watashi wa fujun na dōki kara nihongo o benkyō shi hajimemashita.","german":"I started studying Japanese out of impure motives.","furigana":"わたし|ふじゅん|どうき|にほんご|べんきょう|はじ"},{"japanese":"この水には不純物が多く含まれている。","romaji":"Kono mizu ni wa fujunbutsu ga ōku fukumarete iru.","german":"This water contains many impurities.","furigana":"みず|ふじゅんぶつ|おお|ふく"},{"japanese":"不純な目的で近づいてくる人には注意しよう。","romaji":"Fujun na mokuteki de chikazuite kuru hito ni wa chūi shiyō.","german":"Be careful of people who approach you with impure intentions.","furigana":"ふじゅん|もくてき|ちか|ひと|ちゅうい"}],"notes":"不純 is a な-adjective with two uses: materially \"impure, contaminated\" (especially in 不純物, \"impurity\") and morally \"dishonest, insincere\", typically in 不純な動機 or 不純な目的. The opposite is 純粋 (\"pure, sincere\"). Regarding motives it often sounds half self-deprecating: 動機が不純だ (\"my motives are not quite noble\").","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい純毛のセーター、チクチクして痒いんだよね。","romaji":"Atarashii junmō no sētā, chikuchiku shite kayui n da yo ne.","german":"My new pure wool sweater is scratchy and itchy.","furigana":"あたら|じゅんもう|かゆ"},{"japanese":"このコートは純毛なので、家で洗わないでください。","romaji":"Kono kōto wa junmō na node, ie de arawanaide kudasai.","german":"This coat is pure wool, so please don't wash it at home.","furigana":"じゅんもう|いえ|あら"},{"japanese":"純毛の毛布は暖かいけれど、値段が高い。","romaji":"Junmō no mōfu wa atatakai keredo, nedan ga takai.","german":"A pure wool blanket is warm, but it's expensive.","furigana":"じゅんもう|もうふ|あたた|ねだん|たか"}],"notes":"純毛 means \"pure wool\" (純 \"pure\" + 毛 \"hair, wool\") and appears mainly on labels and in product descriptions, mostly attributively: 純毛のセーター. In modern retail you more often read ウール100％. Sheep's wool as a material is 羊毛, blended fabric is 混紡. In conversation people often simply say ウールのセーター.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"文章を縦書きにしたいんです。","romaji":"Bunshō o tategaki ni shitai n desu.","german":"I want to make the text vertical.","furigana":"ぶんしょう|たてが"},{"japanese":"日本の新聞や小説は、ほとんど縦書きです。","romaji":"Nihon no shinbun ya shōsetsu wa, hotondo tategaki desu.","german":"Japanese newspapers and novels are mostly written vertically.","furigana":"にほん|しんぶん|しょうせつ|たてが"},{"japanese":"お礼の手紙は縦書きで書くことにした。","romaji":"Orei no tegami wa tategaki de kaku koto ni shita.","german":"I decided to write the thank-you letter vertically.","furigana":"れい|てがみ|たてが|か"}],"notes":"縦書き is the traditional Japanese writing direction: characters from top to bottom, columns from right to left - this is how novels, newspapers and many manga are set. The counterpart is 横書き (horizontal, from left to right), which is usual in math books, emails and on the computer. Typical are 縦書きにする, 縦書きで書く and 縦書きの手紙; in 縦書き, numbers are mostly written with kanji numerals.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"壁の縦横の長さを測りたいんだけど、ちょっと手伝ってくれない？","romaji":"Kabe no tateyoko no nagasa o hakaritai n da kedo, chotto tetsudatte kurenai?","german":"I want to measure the length and width of the wall - could you help me a bit?","furigana":"かべ|たてよこ|なが|はか|てつだ"},{"japanese":"この箱は縦横どちらも30センチです。","romaji":"Kono hako wa tateyoko dochira mo sanjussenchi desu.","german":"This box is 30 centimeters both lengthwise and widthwise.","furigana":"はこ|たてよこ"},{"japanese":"表の縦横を入れ替えると、見やすくなりますよ。","romaji":"Hyō no tateyoko o irekaeru to, miyasuku narimasu yo.","german":"If you swap the rows and columns of the table, it becomes easier to read.","furigana":"ひょう|たてよこ|い|か|み"}],"notes":"縦横 in the reading たてよこ very concretely denotes the two dimensions \"vertical and horizontal\", i.e. height or length and width: 縦横の長さ, 縦横30センチ. In tables it means rows and columns. The Sino-Japanese reading じゅうおう is a different word meaning \"in all directions, freely\" (縦横に走る道路, 縦横無尽). Individually: 縦 (\"length, vertical\"), 横 (\"width, horizontal\").","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日はユダヤ教の聖日にあたります。","romaji":"Kyō wa Yudaya kyō no seijitsu ni atarimasu.","german":"Today falls on a Jewish holy day.","furigana":"きょう|きょう|せいじつ"},{"japanese":"キリスト教では、日曜日を聖日として教会で礼拝をします。","romaji":"Kirisuto kyou de wa, nichiyoubi o seijitsu to shite kyoukai de reihai o shimasu.","german":"In Christianity, Sunday is treated as a holy day and worship is held at church.","furigana":"きょう|にちようび|せいじつ|きょうかい|れいはい"},{"japanese":"聖日には仕事を休む信者も少なくありません。","romaji":"Seijitsu ni wa shigoto o yasumu shinja mo sukunaku arimasen.","german":"Quite a few believers take time off work on holy days.","furigana":"せいじつ|しごと|やす|しんじゃ|すく"}],"notes":"聖日 denotes a religious \"holy day\", such as the Sabbath in Judaism or Sunday in Christianity (there also 主日). The word is literary and rather rare. Legal holidays, by contrast, are 祝日, and days off work in general are 休日. Typical are 〜の聖日 and 〜を聖日とする.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この道は聖地に続いています。","romaji":"Kono michi wa seichi ni tsuzuite imasu.","german":"This road leads to the holy land.","furigana":"みち|せいち|つづ"},{"japanese":"甲子園は高校野球の聖地と呼ばれています。","romaji":"Koushien wa koukou yakyuu no seichi to yobarete imasu.","german":"Koshien is called the sacred ground of high school baseball.","furigana":"こうしえん|こうこうやきゅう|せいち|よ"},{"japanese":"映画に出てきた町を「聖地巡礼」で訪れるファンが増えている。","romaji":"Eiga ni dete kita machi o 'seichi junrei' de otozureru fan ga fuete iru.","german":"More and more fans are visiting towns featured in films on \"pilgrimages\".","furigana":"えいが|で|まち|せいちじゅんれい|おとず|ふ"}],"notes":"聖地 is a holy place of a religion, e.g. エルサレム as the 聖地 of three religions; in this sense also \"the Holy Land\". Figuratively, the most important place of a sport or scene is called 〜の聖地 (\"Mecca\"), for example 甲子園 for school baseball. Colloquially, 聖地 also means the real-life settings of anime, films or novels; visiting them is called 聖地巡礼.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"磁気カードと磁石を一緒にしない方がいいよ。","romaji":"Jiki kādo to jishaku o issho ni shinai hō ga ii yo.","german":"You shouldn't keep magnetic cards and magnets together.","furigana":"じき|じしゃく|いっしょ|ほう"},{"japanese":"このはさみは磁気を帯びていて、クリップがくっつく。","romaji":"Kono hasami wa jiki o obite ite, kurippu ga kuttsuku.","german":"These scissors are magnetized, so paper clips stick to them.","furigana":"じき|お"},{"japanese":"定期券の磁気が弱くなって、改札を通れなかった。","romaji":"Teikiken no jiki ga yowaku natte, kaisatsu o toorenakatta.","german":"The magnetism of my commuter pass weakened, and I couldn't get through the ticket gate.","furigana":"ていきけん|じき|よわ|かいさつ|とお"}],"notes":"磁気 is magnetism as a physical property or force, not the magnet itself - that is 磁石. It is very often the first element of compounds: 磁気カード \"magnetic card\", 磁気テープ, 磁気嵐. To become magnetized is 磁気を帯びる. The field around a magnet is 磁場 or 磁界.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"方位磁石が北を指すのは、地球に磁場があるからです。","romaji":"Houi jishaku ga kita o sasu no wa, chikyuu ni jiba ga aru kara desu.","german":"A compass points north because the Earth has a magnetic field.","furigana":"ほういじしゃく|きた|さ|ちきゅう|じば"},{"japanese":"強い磁場の近くでは、機械が正しく動かないことがある。","romaji":"Tsuyoi jiba no chikaku de wa, kikai ga tadashiku ugokanai koto ga aru.","german":"Near a strong magnetic field, machines sometimes don't work properly.","furigana":"つよ|じば|ちか|きかい|ただ|うご"},{"japanese":"この町には、芸術家を引きつける不思議な磁場がある。","romaji":"Kono machi ni wa, geijutsuka o hikitsukeru fushigi na jiba ga aru.","german":"This town has a mysterious aura that attracts artists.","furigana":"まち|げいじゅつか|ひ|ふしぎ|じば"}],"notes":"磁場 is the magnetic field; in physics 磁界 is used synonymously. Typical are 地球の磁場, 強い磁場, 磁場が発生する. Figuratively, 磁場 denotes the aura or attractive force of a place or person. Do not confuse it with 磁気 \"magnetism\" and 磁石 \"magnet\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お紅茶はどのようにおいれ致しましょうか。","romaji":"Okōcha wa dono yō ni oire itashimashō ka.","german":"How would you like your black tea prepared?","furigana":"こうちゃ|いた"},{"japanese":"紅茶を一杯ください。","romaji":"Kōcha o ippai kudasai.","german":"One black tea, please.","furigana":"こうちゃ|いっぱい"}],"notes":"紅茶 is black tea. In especially polite language an お can precede it: お紅茶. おいれ致しましょうか offers to prepare the tea; it is a humble form for your own action. 紅茶をください orders the tea directly and politely. こうちゃ is written kōcha with a long o and a jointly pronounced ちゃ.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 6"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"雪野原は、巨大な鏡のように、冬の太陽の光をキラキラと反射します。","romaji":"Yukinohara wa, kyodai na kagami no you ni, fuyu no taiyou no hikari o kirakira to hansha shimasu.","german":"Das Schneefeld reflektiert das winterliche Sonnenlicht hell wie ein riesiger Spiegel.","furigana":"ゆきのはら|きょだい|かがみ|ふゆ|たいよう|ひかり|はんしゃ"},{"japanese":"窓ガラスに日光が反射して、画面がよく見えない。","romaji":"Mado garasu ni nikkou ga hansha shite, gamen ga yoku mienai.","german":"Das Sonnenlicht spiegelt sich in der Fensterscheibe, deshalb sehe ich den Bildschirm kaum.","furigana":"まど|にっこう|はんしゃ|がめん|み"},{"japanese":"ボールが飛んできて、反射的に目を閉じた。","romaji":"Booru ga tonde kite, hanshateki ni me o tojita.","german":"Ein Ball flog auf mich zu, und ich schloss reflexartig die Augen.","furigana":"と|はんしゃてき|め|と"}],"notes":"反射 ist transitiv (eine Fläche が 光を反射する „reflektiert das Licht“) und intransitiv (光が 〜に反射する „das Licht wird reflektiert“). Als Nomen bedeutet es außerdem „Reflex“: 反射的に „reflexartig“, 反射神経がいい „gute Reflexe haben“. Das deutsche „reflektieren“ im Sinne von „nachdenken“ ist dagegen 反省する oder 振り返る. Für ein Spiegelbild im Wasser sagt man eher 映る.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ミサイルは予定通り発射された。","romaji":"Misairu wa yotei doori hassha sareta.","german":"The missile was launched as scheduled.","furigana":"よていどお|はっしゃ"},{"japanese":"ロケットは午前十時に種子島から発射されました。","romaji":"Roketto wa gozen juuji ni Tanegashima kara hassha saremashita.","german":"The rocket was launched from Tanegashima at 10 a.m.","furigana":"ごぜんじゅうじ||はっしゃ"},{"japanese":"警官は空に向けて銃を一発発射した。","romaji":"Keikan wa sora ni mukete juu o ippatsu hassha shita.","german":"The police officer fired one shot into the sky.","furigana":"けいかん|そら|む|じゅう|いっぱつはっしゃ"}],"notes":"発射する is transitive: 弾・ミサイル・ロケットを発射する \"to fire, to shoot off, to launch\"; in the news often in the passive 〜が発射された. For space rockets, 打ち上げる is also common, and for firearms in daily life simply 撃つ. Beware of the homophone 発車 \"departure\" of a train or bus: 電車が発車します.","levelNote":"Derived from 発射 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"放射冷却のせいで今朝起きた時はものすごい冷え込みだったよ。","romaji":"Hōsha reikyaku no sei de kesa okita toki wa monosugoi hiekomi datta yo.","german":"Es war heute Morgen beim Aufstehen sehr kalt wegen der Strahlungskühlung.","furigana":"ほうしゃれいきゃく|けさお|とき|ひ|こ"},{"japanese":"駅前から道路が放射状に広がっている。","romaji":"Ekimae kara douro ga houshajou ni hirogatte iru.","german":"Vom Bahnhofsplatz aus gehen die Straßen strahlenförmig in alle Richtungen.","furigana":"えきまえ|どうろ|ほうしゃじょう|ひろ"},{"japanese":"太陽は宇宙に光と熱を放射している。","romaji":"Taiyou wa uchuu ni hikari to netsu o housha shite iru.","german":"Die Sonne strahlt Licht und Wärme ins All ab.","furigana":"たいよう|うちゅう|ひかり|ねつ|ほうしゃ"}],"notes":"放射 ist das Aus- bzw. Abstrahlen von Licht, Wärme oder Energie; als する-Verb transitiv: 熱を放射する. Im Alltag begegnet es vor allem in Komposita: 放射線 „(radioaktive) Strahlung“, 放射能 „Radioaktivität“, 放射冷却 „Ausstrahlungskühlung“ und 放射状 „strahlenförmig“. Meint man im Deutschen „Strahlung“ im Sinne von Radioaktivität, ist meist 放射線 das passende Wort.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"結局、あんたが黒幕だったんだな。どうりであの女を殺した時手慣れていたと思ったよ。","romaji":"Kekkyoku, anta ga kuromaku datta n da na. Dōri de ano onna o koroshita toki tenarete ita to omotta yo.","german":"So you were the mastermind after all. No wonder you seemed so practiced when you killed that woman.","furigana":"けっきょく|くろまく|おんな|ころ|ときてな|おも"},{"japanese":"この事件には、裏で糸を引く黒幕がいるらしい。","romaji":"Kono jiken ni wa, ura de ito o hiku kuromaku ga iru rashii.","german":"There seems to be a mastermind pulling strings behind the scenes in this case.","furigana":"じけん|うら|いと|ひ|くろまく"},{"japanese":"その政治家は、長い間政界の黒幕と呼ばれていた。","romaji":"Sono seijika wa, nagai aida seikai no kuromaku to yobarete ita.","german":"That politician was long called the power broker of the political world.","furigana":"せいじか|なが|あいだせいかい|くろまく|よ"}],"notes":"黒幕 literally means \"black curtain\" (from kabuki theater), but today it is almost always used figuratively: the person who pulls the strings in the background without appearing themselves. Typical are 事件の黒幕, 黒幕がいる and 〜が黒幕だった - a favorite word in crime stories, political reports and conspiracy stories. It always has a negative coloring; a neutral organizer is called 中心人物 or まとめ役.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"社長の推定年収は五千万円です。","romaji":"Shachō no suitei nenshū wa go sen man'en desu.","german":"The president's estimated annual income is 50 million yen.","furigana":"しゃちょう|すいていねんしゅう|ごせんまんえん"},{"japanese":"この寺は約千年前に建てられたと推定されている。","romaji":"Kono tera wa yaku sennen mae ni taterareta to suitei sarete iru.","german":"This temple is presumed to have been built about a thousand years ago.","furigana":"てら|やくせんねんまえ|た|すいてい"},{"japanese":"事故の原因は、今のところ推定にすぎません。","romaji":"Jiko no gen'in wa, ima no tokoro suitei ni sugimasen.","german":"The cause of the accident is, for now, nothing more than a presumption.","furigana":"じこ|げんいん|いま|すいてい"}],"notes":"推定 is an estimate or assumption supported by data or indications. Very typical is the passive form 〜と推定される in news and reports, as well as its use as a first element: 推定年収, 推定年齢. Mere guessing is more 推測, a cost estimate is 見積もり, a forecast is 予想. In legal language 推定 stands for \"presumption\", e.g. 推定無罪 \"presumption of innocence\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日付の書き方から、その脅迫状はアメリカ人によって書かれたものだと推理しました。","romaji":"Hizuke no kakikata kara, sono kyouhakujou wa Amerikajin ni yotte kakareta mono da to suiri shimashita.","german":"From the way the date was written, I deduced that the threatening letter was written by an American.","furigana":"ひづけ|か|かた|きょうはくじょう|じん|か|すいり"},{"japanese":"寝る前に推理小説を読むのが好きです。","romaji":"Neru mae ni suiri shousetsu o yomu no ga suki desu.","german":"I like reading mystery novels before going to sleep.","furigana":"ね|まえ|すいりしょうせつ|よ|す"},{"japanese":"犯人が誰なのか、みんなで推理してみよう。","romaji":"Hannin ga dare na no ka, minna de suiri shite miyou.","german":"Let's all try to deduce who the culprit is.","furigana":"はんにん|だれ|すいり"}],"notes":"推理 is logical deduction from clues, especially in the manner of a detective: 〜と推理する, 推理が当たる/外れる. As a noun it also stands for the mystery genre: 推理小説, 推理ドラマ (loosely also ミステリー). Compared with 推測 \"guess\", 推理 emphasizes the logical train of thought; 推定 is a factual estimate based on data.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今回のモデルへの応募について、自薦・他薦は問いません。","romaji":"Konkai no moderu e no oubo ni tsuite, jisen tasen wa toimasen.","german":"For this model application, both self-nominations and nominations by others are accepted.","furigana":"こんかい|おうぼ|じせん|たせん|と"},{"japanese":"彼は自薦でプロジェクトのリーダーになった。","romaji":"Kare wa jisen de purojekuto no riidaa ni natta.","german":"He became project leader by nominating himself.","furigana":"かれ|じせん"},{"japanese":"応募用紙には、自薦の理由を書いてください。","romaji":"Oubo youshi ni wa, jisen no riyuu o kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write your reason for self-nomination on the application form.","furigana":"おうぼようし|じせん|りゆう|か"}],"notes":"自薦 means \"to recommend or propose oneself\", for example for a post, an election or a competition. The counterpart is 他薦 \"nomination by others\"; the set phrase 自薦・他薦は問いません \"self-nominations and nominations by others both welcome\" often appears in calls for applications. With で: 自薦で立候補する. The word is formal; casually people say 自分から手を挙げる.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この番組を彼に薦められたんだけど、アニメを観るのはあまり好きじゃないんだよね。","romaji":"Kono bangumi o kare ni susumerareta n da kedo, anime o miru no wa amari suki ja nai n da yo ne.","german":"He recommended this show to me, but I don't really like watching anime.","furigana":"ばんぐみ|かれ|すす|み|す"},{"japanese":"おすすめを聞かれたので、駅前の店を薦めました。","romaji":"Osusume o kikareta node, ekimae no mise o susumemashita.","german":"I was asked for a recommendation, so I recommended the shop in front of the station.","furigana":"き|えきまえ|みせ|すす"},{"japanese":"部長は彼女を次のリーダーに薦めた。","romaji":"Buchou wa kanojo o tsugi no riidaa ni susumeta.","german":"The department head recommended her as the next leader.","furigana":"ぶちょう|かのじょ|つぎ|すす"}],"notes":"薦める means \"to recommend (a person or thing as suitable)\": AにBを薦める or BをCに薦める \"to propose B for C (a position)\". There are three すすめる spellings: 勧める \"to advise, to offer\" (お茶を勧める), 薦める \"to recommend\" and 進める \"to advance\". In practice, 勧める or kana is often used for recommendations too; the noun is おすすめ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"問題解決にリーダーシップを一番発揮していたのは誰ですか。","romaji":"Mondai kaiketsu ni rīdāshippu o ichiban hakki shite ita no wa dare desu ka.","german":"Who showed the most leadership in solving the problem?","furigana":"もんだいかいけつ|いちばんはっき|だれ"},{"japanese":"緊張して、試験で実力を発揮できなかった。","romaji":"Kinchou shite, shiken de jitsuryoku o hakki dekinakatta.","german":"I was nervous and couldn't show my true ability on the exam.","furigana":"きんちょう|しけん|じつりょく|はっき"},{"japanese":"この薬は飲んでから三十分ほどで効果を発揮します。","romaji":"Kono kusuri wa nonde kara sanjuppun hodo de kouka o hakki shimasu.","german":"This medicine takes effect about thirty minutes after you take it.","furigana":"くすり|の|さんじゅっぷん|こうか|はっき"}],"notes":"発揮する means to bring an existing ability or effect fully into play. Typical objects: 実力/能力/才能/リーダーシップを発揮する, 効果を発揮する \"to take effect\". For merely showing or demonstrating, people use 見せる or 示す. It often appears negated in the potential: 実力を発揮できなかった \"couldn't show what I'm capable of\".","levelNote":"Derived from 発揮 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今度の日曜日にヨットで沿岸を一緒に帆走しないかって彼に誘われたんだ。","romaji":"Kondo no nichiyoubi ni yotto de engan o issho ni hansou shinai ka tte kare ni sasowareta n da.","german":"He invited me to go sailing along the coast with him on a yacht this Sunday.","furigana":"こんど|にちようび|えんがん|いっしょ|はんそう|かれ|さそ"},{"japanese":"台風の影響で、沿岸の地域では高波に注意が必要です。","romaji":"Taifuu no eikyou de, engan no chiiki de wa takanami ni chuui ga hitsuyou desu.","german":"Because of the typhoon, people in coastal areas need to watch out for high waves.","furigana":"たいふう|えいきょう|えんがん|ちいき|たかなみ|ちゅうい|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"この町の人々は、昔から沿岸漁業で生活してきた。","romaji":"Kono machi no hitobito wa, mukashi kara engan gyogyou de seikatsu shite kita.","german":"The people of this town have long made their living from coastal fishing.","furigana":"まち|ひとびと|むかし|えんがんぎょぎょう|せいかつ"}],"notes":"沿岸 refers to the coast as the area along the sea, a lake or a river, including the near-shore waters: 沿岸部, 沿岸地域, 太平洋沿岸, 沿岸漁業, 沿岸警備隊. It is matter-of-fact and typical of news and weather reports. The beach or shore where you take a walk is more often called 海岸 or 海辺. Grammatically 沿岸 is a noun and takes の: 沿岸の町.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"沿海部での生活について、月一でコラムを書いてみませんか。","romaji":"Enkaibu de no seikatsu ni tsuite, tsuki ichi de koramu o kaite mimasen ka.","german":"Why not try writing a monthly column about life in coastal areas?","furigana":"えんかいぶ|せいかつ|つきいち|か"},{"japanese":"中国の沿海部は、内陸部より経済が発展している。","romaji":"Chuugoku no enkaibu wa, nairikubu yori keizai ga hatten shite iru.","german":"China's coastal regions have more developed economies than its inland regions.","furigana":"ちゅうごく|えんかいぶ|ないりくぶ|けいざい|はってん"},{"japanese":"この魚は、日本の沿海で一年中とれます。","romaji":"Kono sakana wa, Nihon no enkai de ichinenjuu toremasu.","german":"This fish can be caught in Japan's coastal waters all year round.","furigana":"さかな|にほん|えんかい|いちねんじゅう"}],"notes":"沿海 means the waters near the coast or the coastal region. The word is literary and much rarer than 沿岸; it appears most often in 沿海部 \"coastal region\" (often about China: 中国の沿海部) and 沿海州 \"Primorye region\". In everyday speech people mostly say 海の近く or 沿岸. 沿海 is a noun and is linked with の.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の弟は、京王沿線に住んでいます。","romaji":"Watashi no otouto wa, Keiou ensen ni sunde imasu.","german":"My younger brother lives along the Keio line.","furigana":"わたし|おとうと|けいおうえんせん|す"},{"japanese":"この沿線には大学が多いので、学生向けのアパートがたくさんあります。","romaji":"Kono ensen ni wa daigaku ga ooi node, gakusei muke no apaato ga takusan arimasu.","german":"There are many universities along this line, so there are lots of apartments for students.","furigana":"えんせん|だいがく|おお|がくせいむ"},{"japanese":"鉄道会社は、工事について沿線の住民に説明した。","romaji":"Tetsudou gaisha wa, kouji ni tsuite ensen no juumin ni setsumei shita.","german":"The railway company explained the construction work to residents along the line.","furigana":"てつどうがいしゃ|こうじ|えんせん|じゅうみん|せつめい"}],"notes":"沿線 is not the railway track itself (that is 路線 or 線路), but the area along a train or bus line. Typical: 〜線沿線に住む \"to live along the ... line\", 沿線の住民, 沿線の観光地; the word is very common in apartment hunting and advertising. Along a road, by contrast, is 沿道, e.g. for spectators at a marathon.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"川の両岸に沿って、ずうっと、美しい桜並木があるんです。","romaji":"Kawa no ryōgan ni sotte, zūtto, utsukushii sakura namiki ga aru n desu.","german":"Along both banks of the river there is a beautiful row of cherry trees all the way.","furigana":"かわ|りょうがん|そ|うつく|さくらなみき"},{"japanese":"会議は、事前に配った資料に沿って進めます。","romaji":"Kaigi wa, jizen ni kubatta shiryō ni sotte susumemasu.","german":"We will proceed with the meeting following the materials distributed in advance.","furigana":"かいぎ|じぜん|くば|しりょう|そ|すす"},{"japanese":"ご希望に沿えず、申し訳ございません。","romaji":"Gokibō ni soezu, mōshiwake gozaimasen.","german":"I'm sorry that we could not meet your wishes.","furigana":"きぼう|そ|もう|わけ"}],"notes":"沿う always takes に: 川に沿う \"to run along the river\". Most common is the て-form 〜に沿って \"along ...\" (道に沿って歩く) as well as figuratively \"in accordance with, in line with\": 方針に沿って, マニュアルに沿って. Very polite is ご期待に沿えるよう \"in order to live up to your expectations\", often negated as ご希望に沿えず申し訳ございません. Do not confuse it with the identically read 添う \"to accompany, to stand by\" (寄り添う).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"源氏と平家の戦いは、日本の歴史でよく知られている。","romaji":"Genji to Heike no tatakai wa, Nihon no rekishi de yoku shirarete iru.","german":"The battle between the Genji and the Heike is well known in Japanese history.","furigana":"げんじ|へいけ|たたか|にほん|れきし|し"},{"japanese":"『源氏物語』は、千年ほど前に紫式部が書いた物語です。","romaji":"'Genji monogatari' wa, sennen hodo mae ni Murasaki Shikibu ga kaita monogatari desu.","german":"The Tale of Genji is a story written about a thousand years ago by Murasaki Shikibu.","furigana":"げんじものがたり|せんねん|まえ|むらさきしきぶ|か|ものがたり"},{"japanese":"ホステスがお店で使う名前を「源氏名」と言います。","romaji":"Hosutesu ga omise de tsukau namae o 'genjina' to iimasu.","german":"The name a hostess uses at the club is called a \"genji-na\".","furigana":"みせ|つか|なまえ|げんじな|い"}],"notes":"源氏 has two meanings: historically the warrior nobility of the Minamoto, whose struggle against the Taira (平家/平氏) in the 源平合戦 led to the Kamakura shogunate; and in literature Prince Hikaru Genji (光源氏), the main character of the 源氏物語. Derived from this is 源氏名, originally the names of court ladies taken from chapters of the novel, today the stage name of hostesses. In modern daily life, 源氏 occurs almost only in these contexts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"出かける時は、完全に電源を切るために全部のコンセントを抜きます。","romaji":"Dekakeru toki wa, kanzen ni dengen o kiru tame ni zenbu no konsento o nukimasu.","german":"When I go out, I unplug all the outlets to cut the power completely.","furigana":"で|とき|かんぜん|でんげん|き|ぜんぶ|ぬ"},{"japanese":"電源を入れても、パソコンが動かない。","romaji":"Dengen o irete mo, pasokon ga ugokanai.","german":"Even when I turn it on, the computer doesn't run.","furigana":"でんげん|い|うご"},{"japanese":"カフェで電源が使える席を探した。","romaji":"Kafe de dengen ga tsukaeru seki o sagashita.","german":"At the cafe I looked for a seat where I could use a power outlet.","furigana":"でんげん|つか|せき|さが"}],"notes":"電源 is the power source or power supply of a device and in everyday use mainly its on/off state: 電源を入れる \"to switch on\", 電源を切る \"to switch off\", 電源が入らない \"does not turn on\", 電源ボタン. In cafes and trains, 電源 also refers to a usable power outlet; the outlet as a component is called コンセント. In announcements one often hears 携帯電話の電源をお切りください.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 38"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"震源地に近いところに住んでる友達が心配だよ。","romaji":"Shingenchi ni chikai tokoro ni sunderu tomodachi ga shinpai da yo.","german":"I'm worried about my friend who lives near the epicenter.","furigana":"しんげんち|ちか|す|ともだち|しんぱい"},{"japanese":"震源地は静岡県の沖で、マグニチュードは5.8でした。","romaji":"Shingenchi wa Shizuoka-ken no oki de, magunichuudo wa go ten hachi deshita.","german":"The epicenter was off the coast of Shizuoka Prefecture, and the magnitude was 5.8."},{"japanese":"そのうわさの震源地は、どうやら隣のクラスらしい。","romaji":"Sono uwasa no shingenchi wa, douyara tonari no kurasu rashii.","german":"The epicenter of that rumor seems to be the next-door class.","furigana":"しんげんち|となり"}],"notes":"震源地 is the place or area from which an earthquake originates. Technically, 震源 is the earthquake focus at depth; the news often says 震源は〜 and 震源の深さ \"focal depth\". Figuratively, 震源地 denotes the starting point of a rumor, scandal or trend: うわさの震源地. On strength: マグニチュード is the magnitude, 震度 the locally measured intensity.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子どもがもうちょっと大きくなるまでは、常勤でのお仕事はちょっと無理かな。","romaji":"Kodomo ga mō chotto ōkiku naru made wa, jōkin de no o shigoto wa chotto muri ka na.","german":"Until my child gets a bit older, full-time work seems a bit impossible.","furigana":"こ|おお|じょうきん|しごと|むり"},{"japanese":"この病院には、常勤の医師が五人います。","romaji":"Kono byouin ni wa, joukin no ishi ga gonin imasu.","german":"This hospital has five full-time doctors.","furigana":"びょういん|じょうきん|いし|ごにん"},{"japanese":"来月から、パートではなく常勤の職員として働くことになった。","romaji":"Raigetsu kara, paato de wa naku joukin no shokuin to shite hataraku koto ni natta.","german":"From next month, I will work as a full-time employee instead of part-time.","furigana":"らいげつ|じょうきん|しょくいん|はたら"}],"notes":"常勤 means working regularly full time at one post; it usually takes の (常勤の医師, 常勤職員) or で (常勤で働く). The opposite is 非常勤 \"part-time, on a non-regular basis\", e.g. 非常勤講師 \"part-time lecturer\". It is more formal than フルタイム and typical of job ads, schools and hospitals; 正社員, by contrast, emphasizes the permanent employment contract.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"無理を承知でお願いしています。","romaji":"Muri o shōchi de onegai shite imasu.","german":"I am asking, knowing that it is unreasonable.","furigana":"むり|しょうち|ねが"},{"japanese":"「明日までに資料を送ってください。」「承知しました。」","romaji":"'Ashita made ni shiryou o okutte kudasai.' 'Shouchi shimashita.'","german":"\"Please send me the materials by tomorrow.\" \"Understood.\"","furigana":"あした|しりょう|おく|しょうち"},{"japanese":"ご承知のとおり、来月から料金が上がります。","romaji":"Goshouchi no toori, raigetsu kara ryoukin ga agarimasu.","german":"As you know, the fees go up from next month.","furigana":"しょうち|らいげつ|りょうきん|あ"}],"notes":"承知 means \"knowledge\" and \"consent\". In working life, 承知しました (even more politely 承知いたしました) is the standard answer \"Understood, will do\"; toward customers also かしこまりました. ご承知のとおり means \"as you know\", and 〜を承知で \"although one knows ...\". In the negative as a threat: 今度やったら承知しないぞ \"I won't let you get away with it next time\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"黒田はそう言い残すと、歓楽街へと消えていった。","romaji":"Kuroda wa sō iinokosu to, kanrakugai e to kiete itta.","german":"Having said that, Kuroda disappeared into the entertainment district.","furigana":"くろだ|い|のこ|かんらくがい|き"},{"japanese":"新宿の歌舞伎町は、日本で一番有名な歓楽街だ。","romaji":"Shinjuku no Kabukichou wa, Nihon de ichiban yuumei na kanrakugai da.","german":"Shinjuku's Kabukicho is the most famous entertainment district in Japan.","furigana":"しんじゅく|かぶきちょう|にほん|いちばんゆうめい|かんらくがい"},{"japanese":"夜の歓楽街を一人で歩くのは、少し危ないかもしれない。","romaji":"Yoru no kanrakugai o hitori de aruku no wa, sukoshi abunai kamo shirenai.","german":"Walking alone in the entertainment district at night may be a little dangerous.","furigana":"よる|かんらくがい|ひとり|ある|すこ|あぶ"}],"notes":"歓楽街 is an entertainment district with bars, clubs, hostess venues and often also red-light businesses; the word therefore has a slightly shady connotation. A lively downtown area with shops and restaurants is neutrally called 繁華街, a street full of pubs 飲み屋街. Typical are 〜の歓楽街 and 夜の歓楽街.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大儲けするつもりでパチンコに行ったが、結局大損してしまった。","romaji":"Oomouke suru tsumori de pachinko ni itta ga, kekkyoku oozon shite shimatta.","german":"I went to play pachinko intending to win big, but I ended up losing a lot.","furigana":"おおもう|い|けっきょくおおぞん"},{"japanese":"株で大損した友達は、もう投資をやめたそうだ。","romaji":"Kabu de oozon shita tomodachi wa, mou toushi o yameta sou da.","german":"My friend who lost a lot on stocks reportedly quit investing.","furigana":"かぶ|おおぞん|ともだち|とうし"},{"japanese":"このセールを逃したら大損だよ。","romaji":"Kono seeru o nogashitara oozon da yo.","german":"If you miss this sale, you'll lose out big.","furigana":"のが|おおぞん"}],"notes":"大損 is a heavy loss, usually of money; as a verb 大損する or 大損をする, the cause takes で: 株で大損する. The opposite is 大儲け \"big profit\". Colloquially, 大損だ is also used exaggeratedly for missed opportunities (\"you're missing out a lot\"). More neutral or formal are 大きな損失 and 損害.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"車が全損になった場合、修理代は保険で全額支払われないことが多いです。","romaji":"Kuruma ga zenson ni natta baai, shuuridai wa hoken de zengaku shiharawarenai koto ga ooi desu.","german":"If a car is a total loss, the repair cost is often not paid in full by insurance.","furigana":"くるま|ぜんそん|ばあい|しゅうりだい|ほけん|ぜんがくしはら|おお"},{"japanese":"事故のあと、車は全損と判断された。","romaji":"Jiko no ato, kuruma wa zenson to handan sareta.","german":"After the accident, the car was judged a total loss.","furigana":"じこ|くるま|ぜんそん|はんだん"},{"japanese":"火事で、倉庫の商品が全損になった。","romaji":"Kaji de, souko no shouhin ga zenson ni natta.","german":"The goods in the warehouse were a total loss in the fire.","furigana":"かじ|そうこ|しょうひん|ぜんそん"}],"notes":"全損 is a term from insurance and business: the complete loss or total damage of a vehicle, building or goods. Typical are 全損になる, 全損と判断される and 全損事故; the counterpart is 分損 \"partial loss\". In daily life, for cars people often say 廃車になる \"has to be scrapped\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"節分の日には、家族で豆まきをします。","romaji":"Setsubun no hi ni wa, kazoku de mamemaki o shimasu.","german":"On Setsubun, we do the bean-throwing ritual with the family.","furigana":"せつぶん|ひ|かぞく|まめ"},{"japanese":"このコーヒー豆は、ブラジルから輸入したものです。","romaji":"Kono kōhī mame wa, Burajiru kara yunyū shita mono desu.","german":"These coffee beans were imported from Brazil.","furigana":"まめ|ゆにゅう"},{"japanese":"豆は体にいいので、毎日少しずつ食べるようにしている。","romaji":"Mame wa karada ni ii node, mainichi sukoshi zutsu taberu yō ni shite iru.","german":"Beans are good for the body, so I try to eat a little every day.","furigana":"まめ|からだ|まいにちすこ|た"}],"notes":"豆 is the general term for legumes and their seeds - beans, peas and above all the soybean, from which 豆腐, 納豆 and 味噌 are made. In daily life you often hear the friendly-polite form お豆; coffee beans are コーヒー豆. Well known is the bean-throwing 豆まき at 節分 (\"鬼は外、福は内\"). As a prefix, 豆〜 means \"mini-\": 豆知識 \"a little piece of trivia\", 豆電球 \"miniature light bulb\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あんこは小豆から作られているが、けっこう糖分が高い。","romaji":"Anko wa azuki kara tsukurarete iru ga, kekkou toubun ga takai.","german":"Anko is made from azuki beans, but it has quite a lot of sugar.","furigana":"あずき|つく|とうぶん|たか"},{"japanese":"お祝いの日には、もち米と小豆でお赤飯を作ります。","romaji":"Oiwai no hi ni wa, mochigome to azuki de osekihan o tsukurimasu.","german":"On celebration days, we make sekihan with glutinous rice and azuki beans.","furigana":"いわ|ひ|ごめ|あずき|せきはん|つく"},{"japanese":"小豆色のセーターを買いました。","romaji":"Azukiiro no seetaa o kaimashita.","german":"I bought an azuki-colored sweater.","furigana":"あずきいろ|か"}],"notes":"小豆 is irregularly read あずき; compare 大豆 (だいず) \"soybean\". It refers to the small red bean from which sweet bean paste (あんこ) for Japanese sweets, お赤飯 and ぜんざい are made. The word is also often written in kana as あずき; 小豆色 is a dark reddish brown.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"誤解しないでね。日本料理は大好きなんだけど、納豆だけはどうしても食べられないの。","romaji":"Gokai shinaide ne. Nihon ryouri wa daisuki na n da kedo, nattou dake wa doushitemo taberarenai no.","german":"Don't get me wrong. I love Japanese food, but I just can't eat natto.","furigana":"ごかい|にほんりょうり|だいす|なっとう|た"},{"japanese":"納豆はよく混ぜてから、ご飯にかけて食べます。","romaji":"Nattou wa yoku mazete kara, gohan ni kakete tabemasu.","german":"Mix natto well and then eat it over rice.","furigana":"なっとう|ま|はん|た"},{"japanese":"納豆は体にいいと言われているが、においが苦手な人も多い。","romaji":"Nattou wa karada ni ii to iwarete iru ga, nioi ga nigate na hito mo ooi.","german":"Natto is said to be good for you, but many people can't stand the smell.","furigana":"なっとう|からだ|い|にがて|ひと|おお"}],"notes":"納豆 is fermented soybeans with sticky strings (糸を引く) and a strong smell. You stir it well (よく混ぜる) and mostly eat it with mustard and soy sauce on rice (納豆ご飯), especially for breakfast. 納豆 is sold in small packs, counted with 〜パック. In eastern Japan (関東) it is traditionally more popular than in western Japan (関西).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"犬の爪を爪切りで切ってあげようとしてたんですけど、つい携帯に気を取られちゃいました。","romaji":"Inu no tsume o tsumekiri de kitte ageyou to shite tan desu kedo, tsui keitai ni ki o torarechaimashita.","german":"I was trying to cut my dog's nails with the nail clippers, but I got distracted by my phone.","furigana":"いぬ|つめ|つめき|き|けいたい|き|と"},{"japanese":"爪切りってどこにある？","romaji":"Tsumekiri tte doko ni aru?","german":"Where are the nail clippers?","furigana":"つめき"},{"japanese":"夜に爪切りをすると縁起が悪い、と言う人もいる。","romaji":"Yoru ni tsumekiri o suru to engi ga warui, to iu hito mo iru.","german":"Some people say it's bad luck to cut your nails at night.","furigana":"よる|つめき|えんぎ|わる|い|ひと"}],"notes":"爪切り denotes both the tool (nail clipper, nail scissors) and nail cutting itself: 爪切りをする. The corresponding verb is 爪を切る; 爪 includes fingernails and toenails as well as the claws of animals. The き stays unchanged, so it is not つめぎり.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"幕府が俺の家族を皆殺しにした。","romaji":"Bakufu ga ore no kazoku o minagoroshi ni shita.","german":"The shogunate killed my entire family.","furigana":"ばくふ|おれ|かぞく|みなごろ"},{"japanese":"1603年、徳川家康は江戸に幕府を開いた。","romaji":"1603-nen, Tokugawa Ieyasu wa Edo ni bakufu o hiraita.","german":"In 1603, Tokugawa Ieyasu established the shogunate in Edo.","furigana":"ねん||えど|ばくふ|ひら"},{"japanese":"江戸幕府は、長い間外国との交流を制限していた。","romaji":"Edo bakufu wa, nagai aida gaikoku to no kouryuu o seigen shite ita.","german":"The Edo shogunate restricted contact with foreign countries for a long time.","furigana":"えどばくふ|なが|あいだがいこく|こうりゅう|せいげん"}],"notes":"幕府 is the military government of a shogun (将軍) in premodern Japan. One distinguishes 鎌倉幕府, 室町幕府 and 江戸幕府 (also 徳川幕府). Typical phrases are 幕府を開く \"to found a shogunate\" and 幕府が倒れる \"the shogunate falls\"; the final phase of the Edo shogunate is called 幕末. A purely historical word from school lessons and historical dramas.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"駅まで迎えをよこすよ。","romaji":"Eki made mukae o yokosu yo.","german":"I'll send someone to pick you up at the station.","furigana":"えき|むか"},{"japanese":"六時に保育園へ子どもを迎えに行きます。","romaji":"Rokuji ni hoikuen e kodomo o mukae ni ikimasu.","german":"I go to pick up my child at nursery school at six.","furigana":"ろくじ|ほいくえん|こ|むか|い"},{"japanese":"ホテルの前にお迎えの車が来ています。","romaji":"Hoteru no mae ni omukae no kuruma ga kite imasu.","german":"A car to pick you up has arrived in front of the hotel.","furigana":"まえ|むか|くるま|き"}],"notes":"迎え is the nominal form of 迎える and means picking up or receiving a person. Most common: 〜を迎えに行く/来る \"to go/come to pick up\", 駅まで迎えに来て. Polite is お迎え (お迎えの車). The counterpart is 見送り \"seeing off, escorting out\"; 出迎え stresses going to meet someone upon arrival. お迎えが来る can also euphemistically mean \"death is approaching\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このアボカド、昨日買ったばかりなのにもう傷んでるよ。","romaji":"Kono abokado, kinou katta bakari na noni mou itanderu yo.","german":"This avocado, I only bought it yesterday and it's already gone bad.","furigana":"きのうか|いた"},{"japanese":"夏は食べ物が傷みやすいので、早めに冷蔵庫に入れてください。","romaji":"Natsu wa tabemono ga itamiyasui node, hayame ni reizouko ni irete kudasai.","german":"Food spoils easily in summer, so please put it in the fridge early.","furigana":"なつ|た|もの|いた|はや|れいぞうこ|い"},{"japanese":"ドライヤーの使いすぎで、髪がかなり傷んでしまった。","romaji":"Doraiyaa no tsukaisugi de, kami ga kanari itande shimatta.","german":"From overusing the hair dryer, my hair got quite damaged.","furigana":"つか|かみ|いた"}],"notes":"傷む is intransitive and means \"to get damaged, to spoil\": with food (野菜が傷む), hair (髪が傷む), houses or furniture (家が傷む). The transitive counterpart is 傷める (髪を傷める). Do not confuse it with the identically read 痛む \"to hurt\" (頭が痛む). For completely rotten food 腐る is stronger; 傷む means more \"going off, no longer fresh\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"そのエステは痩身を専門にしているんだって。","romaji":"Sono esute wa sōshin o senmon ni shite iru n da tte.","german":"I hear that salon specializes in slimming.","furigana":"そうしん|せんもん"},{"japanese":"駅前で痩身エステの広告をよく見かける。","romaji":"Ekimae de soushin esute no koukoku o yoku mikakeru.","german":"I often see ads for slimming salons in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|そうしん|こうこく|み"},{"japanese":"彼は長身痩身の青年だった。","romaji":"Kare wa choushin soushin no seinen datta.","german":"He was a tall, slender young man.","furigana":"かれ|ちょうしんそうしん|せいねん"}],"notes":"痩身 has two meanings: elevated and literary \"slender body\" (e.g. 長身痩身 \"tall and slim\") and \"slimming, weight loss\", especially in advertising and cosmetics: 痩身エステ, 痩身効果. In everyday speech people say やせる, ダイエット or スリムな体 instead. 痩身 is a noun; as a description it takes の, not な.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コウイチの股には星の形のホクロがある。","romaji":"Kouichi no mata ni wa hoshi no katachi no hokuro ga aru.","german":"Koichi has a star-shaped mole on his crotch.","furigana":"また|ほし|かたち"},{"japanese":"ズボンの股のところが破れてしまった。","romaji":"Zubon no mata no tokoro ga yaburete shimatta.","german":"The crotch of my pants tore.","furigana":"また|やぶ"},{"japanese":"この先で道が二股に分かれています。","romaji":"Kono saki de michi ga futamata ni wakarete imasu.","german":"Up ahead, the road splits into two.","furigana":"さき|みち|ふたまた|わ"}],"notes":"股 is the crotch, i.e. the area between the legs up to the inner side of the thighs; the thigh itself is 太もも. Important compounds are 股下 \"inseam length (trousers)\", 内股 \"inner thigh\" and 股関節 \"hip joint\". 二股 is a fork (道が二股に分かれる), and colloquially also \"two-timing\" in relationships: 二股をかける.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このプールでは飛び込みは禁止されています。","romaji":"Kono puuru de wa tobikomi wa kinshi sarete imasu.","german":"Diving is prohibited in this pool.","furigana":"と|こ|きんし"},{"japanese":"飛び込みの選手が、十メートルの台からきれいに水に入った。","romaji":"Tobikomi no senshu ga, juu meetoru no dai kara kirei ni mizu ni haitta.","german":"The diver entered the water cleanly from the ten-meter platform.","furigana":"と|こ|せんしゅ|じゅう|だい|みず|はい"},{"japanese":"予約なしの飛び込みのお客さんにも、丁寧に対応した。","romaji":"Yoyaku nashi no tobikomi no okyakusan ni mo, teinei ni taiou shita.","german":"We also politely handled walk-in customers without reservations.","furigana":"よやく|と|こ|きゃく|ていねい|たいおう"}],"notes":"飛び込み is the nominal form of 飛び込む and first denotes jumping into water; as a sport it is diving (高飛び込み \"platform diving\"). Figuratively it means showing up unannounced: 飛び込みの客 \"walk-in customer\", 飛び込み営業 \"door-to-door cold calling\", 飛び込みで参加する. At swimming pools, 飛び込み禁止 is often posted.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"アイツは金儲けがうまい。","romaji":"Aitsu wa kanemōke ga umai.","german":"That guy is good at making money.","furigana":"かねもう"},{"japanese":"お金は大事だけど、金儲けのためだけに働いているわけじゃない。","romaji":"Okane wa daiji da kedo, kanemouke no tame dake ni hataraite iru wake ja nai.","german":"Money is important, but I don't work only to make money.","furigana":"かね|だいじ|かねもう|はたら"},{"japanese":"彼は株で金儲けしようとして、逆に損をした。","romaji":"Kare wa kabu de kanemouke shiyou to shite, gyaku ni son o shita.","german":"He tried to make money on stocks and lost instead.","furigana":"かれ|かぶ|かねもう|ぎゃく|そん"}],"notes":"金儲け means \"making money, turning a profit\" and is colloquial, often with a slightly negative ring (\"money-grubbing\"); 金儲け主義 criticizes pure profit-seeking. Typical are 金儲けがうまい, 金儲けのために and 金儲けをする/金儲けする. Neutral for \"earning money\" through work is お金を稼ぐ; the verb 儲ける stresses the profit.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"保険料は、年齢と収入をもとに算定されます。","romaji":"Hokenryou wa, nenrei to shuunyuu o moto ni santei saremasu.","german":"Insurance premiums are calculated based on age and income.","furigana":"ほけんりょう|ねんれい|しゅうにゅう|さんてい"},{"japanese":"事故による損害額を算定するには、時間がかかります。","romaji":"Jiko ni yoru songaigaku o santei suru ni wa, jikan ga kakarimasu.","german":"It takes time to assess the amount of damage caused by the accident.","furigana":"じこ|そんがいがく|さんてい|じかん"},{"japanese":"来月から電気料金の算定方法が変わります。","romaji":"Raigetsu kara denki ryoukin no santei houhou ga kawarimasu.","german":"From next month, the method for calculating electricity charges will change.","furigana":"らいげつ|でんきりょうきん|さんていほうほう|か"}],"notes":"算定する means to calculate or determine an amount or value according to fixed rules. It is formal and typical of administration, insurance and business: 保険料/税額/損害額を算定する, 算定方法, 算定基準; often in the passive 〜をもとに算定される. For simple arithmetic, 計算する is used, and for counting people 数える or 集計する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"総理府は2001年に廃止され、その仕事は内閣府に引き継がれた。","romaji":"Sourifu wa 2001-nen ni haishi sare, sono shigoto wa naikakufu ni hikitsugareta.","german":"Das Amt des Premierministers wurde 2001 aufgelöst, seine Aufgaben übernahm das Kabinettsbüro.","furigana":"そうりふ|ねん|はいし|しごと|ないかくふ|ひ|つ"},{"japanese":"この統計は、当時の総理府が行った調査の結果です。","romaji":"Kono toukei wa, touji no sourifu ga okonatta chousa no kekka desu.","german":"Diese Statistik ist das Ergebnis einer Umfrage des damaligen Amts des Premierministers.","furigana":"とうけい|とうじ|そうりふ|おこな|ちょうさ|けっか"},{"japanese":"祖父は若いころ、総理府で働いていたそうです。","romaji":"Sofu wa wakai koro, sourifu de hataraite ita sou desu.","german":"Mein Großvater soll in jungen Jahren im Amt des Premierministers gearbeitet haben.","furigana":"そふ|わか|そうりふ|はたら"}],"notes":"総理府 war bis 2001 eine Behörde, die direkt dem Premierminister (総理大臣) unterstand und u. a. Statistiken und Meinungsumfragen durchführte. Bei der Verwaltungsreform 2001 ging sie im 内閣府 „Kabinettsbüro“ auf; das Wort ist daher heute historisch und erscheint vor allem in älteren Texten und Statistiken (総理府の調査). Ein deutsches „Kanzleramt“ im Sinne des Amtssitzes entspricht eher 首相官邸.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"おばちゃんって呼ばれる年齢かなあ。傷つくなあ。","romaji":"Obachan tte yobareru nenrei ka naa. Kizutsuku naa.","german":"Am I at the age where people call me \"auntie\"? That hurts.","furigana":"よ|ねんれい|きず"},{"japanese":"友達の一言に、とても傷ついた。","romaji":"Tomodachi no hitokoto ni, totemo kizutsuita.","german":"A friend's remark hurt me very much.","furigana":"ともだち|ひとこと|きず"},{"japanese":"引っ越しのとき、新しいテーブルが傷ついてしまった。","romaji":"Hikkoshi no toki, atarashii teeburu ga kizutsuite shimatta.","german":"When we moved, the new table got scratched.","furigana":"ひ|こ|あたら|きず"}],"notes":"傷つく is intransitive: someone or something gets hurt or damaged. Most often it concerns feelings (心が傷つく, 〜に傷つく \"to be hurt by ...\", 傷つきやすい \"sensitive\"), and also objects that get scratched. The transitive counterpart is 傷つける (人を傷つける). For physical injuries, people usually say けがをする in daily life. Also written 傷付く.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"うちの長男は野球一筋なのよ。女っ気も無くてさ。","romaji":"Uchi no chōnan wa yakyū hitosuji na no yo. Onnakke mo nakute sa.","german":"Our eldest son is all about baseball. He has no interest in girls, either.","furigana":"ちょうなん|やきゅうひとすじ|おんな|け|な"},{"japanese":"父はパン作り一筋で四十年、この店を続けてきた。","romaji":"Chichi wa panzukuri hitosuji de yonjūnen, kono mise o tsuzukete kita.","german":"My father has devoted himself to bread-making for forty years and kept this shop going.","furigana":"ちち|づく|ひとすじ|よんじゅうねん|みせ|つづ"},{"japanese":"暗い部屋に、窓から一筋の光が差し込んでいた。","romaji":"Kurai heya ni, mado kara hitosuji no hikari ga sashikonde ita.","german":"A single ray of light came in through the window into the dark room.","furigana":"くら|へや|まど|ひとすじ|ひかり|さ|こ"}],"notes":"一筋 has two uses. Literally it denotes a single narrow streak: 一筋の光 \"a ray of light\", 一筋の涙 \"a single tear\", figuratively also 一筋の希望 \"a glimmer of hope\". After a noun, 〜一筋 means \"wholly and exclusively devoted to ...\": 野球一筋, 仕事一筋, この道一筋三十年 - usually appreciative, sometimes with a slight mockery of one-sidedness. Similar is 一途 \"single-minded, devoted\", which describes the person more (一途な人).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの時、日本政府は非常事態を宣言するべきだったと思わないかい。","romaji":"Ano toki, Nihon seifu wa hijou jitai o sengen suru beki datta to omowanai kai.","german":"Denkst du nicht, dass die japanische Regierung damals den Notstand hätte ausrufen sollen?","furigana":"とき|にほんせいふ|ひじょうじたい|せんげん|おも"},{"japanese":"父は家族の前で禁煙を宣言した。","romaji":"Chichi wa kazoku no mae de kin'en o sengen shita.","german":"Mein Vater hat vor der Familie verkündet, mit dem Rauchen aufzuhören.","furigana":"ちち|かぞく|まえ|きんえん|せんげん"},{"japanese":"議長が会議の開会を宣言した。","romaji":"Gichou ga kaigi no kaikai o sengen shita.","german":"Der Vorsitzende erklärte die Sitzung für eröffnet.","furigana":"ぎちょう|かいぎ|かいかい|せんげん"}],"notes":"宣言する heißt „offiziell erklären, verkünden, ausrufen“: 〜を宣言する (独立/非常事態/開会を宣言する) oder 〜と宣言する. Als Nomen steht es in festen Ausdrücken wie 緊急事態宣言 „Ausrufung des Notstands“ und 独立宣言. Scherzhaft auch für persönliche Vorsätze: 禁煙宣言, ダイエット宣言. Das deutsche „erklären“ im Sinne von „erläutern“ ist dagegen 説明する.","levelNote":"Derived from 宣言 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"俺の友達はメチャクチャ面白くて、パーティーを盛り上げるのが得意なんだよ。","romaji":"Ore no tomodachi wa mechakucha omoshirokute, pātī o moriageru no ga tokui na n da yo.","german":"My friend is hilarious and good at livening up parties.","furigana":"おれ|ともだち|おもしろ|も|あ|とくい"},{"japanese":"みんなで応援して、試合を盛り上げましょう！","romaji":"Minna de ōen shite, shiai o moriagemashō!","german":"Let's all cheer together and get the game going!","furigana":"おうえん|しあい|も|あ"},{"japanese":"司会者が上手に話を盛り上げてくれたので、会は楽しかった。","romaji":"Shikaisha ga jōzu ni hanashi o moriagete kureta node, kai wa tanoshikatta.","german":"The host skillfully livened up the conversation, so the gathering was enjoyable.","furigana":"しかいしゃ|じょうず|はなし|も|あ|かい|たの"}],"notes":"盛り上げる is transitive (〜を盛り上げる) and mostly means \"to create atmosphere, to get something going\": パーティーを盛り上げる, 会場を盛り上げる, 話を盛り上げる. The intransitive counterpart is 盛り上がる: パーティーが盛り上がった \"the party really got going\". The literal meaning \"to heap up\" (土を盛り上げる) is rare in daily life. Someone who is good at livening up a group is colloquially the 盛り上げ役.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は裏切り者なんかじゃないよ。ただ、生まれつきちょっととぼけているだけさ。","romaji":"Kare wa uragirimono nanka ja nai yo. Tada, umaretsuki chotto tobokete iru dake sa.","german":"He's not a traitor or anything. He's just a bit scatterbrained by nature.","furigana":"かれ|うらぎ|もの|う"},{"japanese":"このチームの中に裏切り者がいるに違いない。","romaji":"Kono chiimu no naka ni uragirimono ga iru ni chigainai.","german":"There must be a traitor on this team.","furigana":"なか|うらぎ|もの|ちが"},{"japanese":"「裏切り者！」と叫んで、彼女は部屋を出ていった。","romaji":"'Uragirimono!' to sakende, kanojo wa heya o dete itta.","german":"Shouting \"Traitor!\", she left the room.","furigana":"うらぎ|もの|さけ|かのじょ|へや|で"}],"notes":"裏切り者 is someone who breaks trust: traitor, turncoat. It is composed of 裏切り (the noun of 裏切る \"to betray, to deceive\") and 者 \"person\". The word is strong and emotional and often comes up as an accusation or exclamation: 裏切り者！. It is used for friends, partners, groups or states; a spy, by contrast, is スパイ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"諸々の理由がありまして、しばらく一時的に店を閉めることにしました。","romaji":"Moromoro no riyū ga arimashite, shibaraku ichijiteki ni mise o shimeru koto ni shimashita.","german":"For various reasons, we have decided to temporarily close the shop for a while.","furigana":"もろもろ|りゆう|いちじてき|みせ|し"},{"japanese":"では、明日の打ち合わせの件、諸々よろしくお願いします。","romaji":"Dewa, ashita no uchiawase no ken, moromoro yoroshiku onegai shimasu.","german":"Well then, regarding tomorrow's meeting, I'm counting on you for all the various things.","furigana":"あした|う|あ|けん|もろもろ|ねが"},{"japanese":"引っ越しの諸々の手続きが、やっと終わった。","romaji":"Hikkoshi no moromoro no tetsuzuki ga, yatto owatta.","german":"The various procedures for the move are finally finished.","furigana":"ひ|こ|もろもろ|てつづ|お"}],"notes":"諸々 means \"all sorts of, all the various things\" and mostly stands with の before a noun: 諸々の事情 \"various circumstances\" (often as a polite reason without details), 諸々の手続き. It sounds somewhat more refined than 色々 or いろんな, but in the office it is also used casually as an adverb: 諸々よろしくお願いします. Often also written in kana as もろもろ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"おいおい、頼むよ。明らかにお前たちの相性全然よくなかっただろ！絶対にあの三つ編みの女に電話なんてしない方がいいって！","romaji":"Oi oi, tanomu yo. Akiraka ni omaetachi no aishō zenzen yokunakatta daro! Zettai ni ano mitsuami no onna ni denwa nante shinai hō ga ii tte!","german":"Come on, please. It was obvious that you two were not compatible at all! You really shouldn't call that girl with the braid!","furigana":"たの|あき|まえ|あいしょうぜんぜん|ぜったい|み|あ|おんな|でんわ|ほう"},{"japanese":"娘は毎朝、妻に髪を三つ編みにしてもらっている。","romaji":"Musume wa maiasa, tsuma ni kami o mitsuami ni shite moratte iru.","german":"My daughter has her hair braided by my wife every morning.","furigana":"むすめ|まいあさ|つま|かみ|み|あ"},{"japanese":"長い髪を三つ編みにすると、運動するときに邪魔にならない。","romaji":"Nagai kami o mitsuami ni suru to, undō suru toki ni jama ni naranai.","german":"If you braid long hair, it doesn't get in the way when you exercise.","furigana":"なが|かみ|み|あ|うんどう|じゃま"}],"notes":"三つ編み literally means \"three-strand weave\": a braid plaited from three strands. Typical are 髪を三つ編みにする \"to braid one's hair\" and 三つ編みの女の子 \"a girl with a braid\". An unbraided ponytail, by contrast, is ポニーテール; two braids on the left and right are also called おさげ. The word is likewise used for braided cords or braided bread loaves and is often written in kana as みつあみ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"金を払うだけで、彼らが納まるとは思えないけどな。","romaji":"Kane o harau dake de, karera ga osamaru to wa omoenai kedo na.","german":"I don't think they'll be satisfied just by being paid money.","furigana":"かね|はら|かれ|おさ|おも"},{"japanese":"荷物が全部この箱に納まった。","romaji":"Nimotsu ga zenbu kono hako ni osamatta.","german":"All the luggage fit into this box.","furigana":"にもつ|ぜんぶ|はこ|おさ"},{"japanese":"彼は去年から社長のいすに納まっている。","romaji":"Kare wa kyonen kara shachou no isu ni osamatte iru.","german":"Since last year he has been installed in the president's seat.","furigana":"かれ|きょねん|しゃちょう|おさ"}],"notes":"納まる is intransitive and means \"to fit in, come to its place\" (箱に納まる, 予算内に納まる), \"to take up a position\" (社長のいすに納まる), \"to be paid\" (税金が納まる) and \"to be satisfied\" (それでは彼が納まらない). For \"to fit in\", 収まる is usually written in everyday text; pain or unrest that subsides is 治まる. Transitive counterpart: 納める \"to pay in, to store\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の意見には納得だね。","romaji":"Kare no iken ni wa nattoku da ne.","german":"I'm convinced by his opinion.","furigana":"かれ|いけん|なっとく"},{"japanese":"先生の説明を聞いて、やっと納得した。","romaji":"Sensei no setsumei o kiite, yatto nattoku shita.","german":"After listening to the teacher's explanation, I finally understood and accepted it.","furigana":"せんせい|せつめい|き|なっとく"},{"japanese":"この結果には、どうしても納得できません。","romaji":"Kono kekka ni wa, doushitemo nattoku dekimasen.","german":"I simply cannot accept this result.","furigana":"けっか|なっとく"}],"notes":"納得 means to inwardly understand and accept something after an explanation. Typical patterns are 〜に納得する, 納得できる/できない, 納得がいく/いかない \"to be convinced/not convinced\", and 納得させる \"to convince\". It is also used casually as an exclamation: なるほど、納得！ or 〜には納得だ. Compared with 理解 \"to understand\", 納得 stresses that you also agree.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この文章を否定形にすることができますか。","romaji":"Kono bunshou o hiteikei ni suru koto ga dekimasu ka.","german":"Can you turn this sentence into a negative one?","furigana":"ぶんしょう|ひていけい"},{"japanese":"「食べる」の否定形は「食べない」です。","romaji":"'Taberu' no hiteikei wa 'tabenai' desu.","german":"The negative form of 「食べる」 is 「食べない」.","furigana":"た|ひていけい|た"},{"japanese":"丁寧な否定形は「〜ません」の形になります。","romaji":"Teinei na hiteikei wa '-masen' no katachi ni narimasu.","german":"The polite negative form takes the form 「〜ません」.","furigana":"ていねい|ひていけい|かたち"}],"notes":"否定形 is the grammatical term for the negative form of a verb or adjective, e.g. 行かない, 高くない, 静かじゃない. In Japanese classes for foreigners the plain form is often called ない形; the opposite is 肯定形 \"affirmative form\". A whole negative sentence is a 否定文. Typical: 〜を否定形にする \"to put into the negative\".","levelNote":"Compound: 否定 (N3) + 形 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"テクノロジーは人々の暮らしを大きく変えました。","romaji":"Tekunorojī wa hitobito no kurashi o ōkiku kaemashita.","german":"Technology has greatly changed people's lives.","furigana":"ひとびと|く|おお|か"},{"japanese":"田舎での暮らしにも、もうすっかり慣れました。","romaji":"Inaka de no kurashi ni mo, mou sukkari naremashita.","german":"I have already gotten completely used to life in the countryside.","furigana":"いなか|く|な"},{"japanese":"一人暮らしを始めてから、自分で料理をするようになった。","romaji":"Hitorigurashi o hajimete kara, jibun de ryouri o suru you ni natta.","german":"Since I started living alone, I have started cooking for myself.","furigana":"ひとりぐ|はじ|じぶん|りょうり"}],"notes":"暮らし is the noun form of 暮らす and refers to everyday life, lifestyle, and also one's livelihood. Typical are 暮らしが楽/苦しい \"to get by comfortably/barely\", 田舎暮らし and 一人暮らし \"living alone\". Compared with 生活, 暮らし sounds softer and closer to daily life; 生活 is more matter-of-fact (生活費, 生活習慣). Life in the sense of a lifetime is 人生, and life as the opposite of death is 命.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"またその話？もう終わったことを蒸し返さないでよ。","romaji":"Mata sono hanashi? Mou owatta koto o mushikaesanaide yo.","german":"That again? Don't rehash something that's already over.","furigana":"はなし|お|む|かえ"},{"japanese":"一度決まったことを、会議で蒸し返すのはやめてください。","romaji":"Ichido kimatta koto o, kaigi de mushikaesu no wa yamete kudasai.","german":"Please stop bringing up in meetings things that have already been decided.","furigana":"いちどき|かいぎ|む|かえ"},{"japanese":"冷めたまんじゅうを蒸し返して食べた。","romaji":"Sameta manjuu o mushikaeshite tabeta.","german":"I steamed a cooled manju again and ate it.","furigana":"さ|む|かえ|た"}],"notes":"蒸し返す literally means \"to steam again, to reheat\" (蒸す + 返す), but is mostly used figuratively: to bring up a closed topic or an old quarrel again: 話/問題/昔のことを蒸し返す. It sounds reproachful and often appears in requests or negation: 蒸し返さないで. More neutral is 話を戻す \"to return to the topic\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"予想していた昇進が無かったが、彼は平気を装って昇進した社員たちにおめでとうと言った。","romaji":"Yosō shite ita shōshin ga nakatta ga, kare wa heiki o yosōtte shōshin shita shaintachi ni omedetō to itta.","german":"He had expected a promotion that didn't come, but he pretended to be unbothered and congratulated the employees who were promoted.","furigana":"よそう|しょうしん|な|かれ|へいき|よそお|しょうしん|しゃいん|い"},{"japanese":"犯人は客を装って店に入った。","romaji":"Hannin wa kyaku o yosootte mise ni haitta.","german":"The culprit entered the shop posing as a customer.","furigana":"はんにん|きゃく|よそお|みせ|はい"},{"japanese":"結婚式には、美しく装った人たちが集まっていた。","romaji":"Kekkonshiki ni wa, utsukushiku yosootta hitotachi ga atsumatte ita.","german":"People dressed up beautifully had gathered at the wedding.","furigana":"けっこんしき|うつく|よそお|ひと|あつ"}],"notes":"装う has two meanings: in a literary sense \"to dress, to adorn oneself\" (美しく装う), and, more often, \"to feign, to put on an appearance\": 平気を装う \"to act calm\", 〜を装って \"disguised as ...\" (客/警察官を装って, typical in news about fraud). In everyday speech, \"to pretend\" is more commonly 〜ふりをする. The same kanji is used to write よそう \"to dish up food\" (ご飯をよそう), which is usually written in kana.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"事故で電車が三十分遅れました。","romaji":"Jiko de densha ga sanjuppun okuremashita.","german":"The train was thirty minutes late because of an accident.","furigana":"じこ|でんしゃ|さんじゅっぷんおく"},{"japanese":"約束の時間に遅れて、すみません。","romaji":"Yakusoku no jikan ni okurete, sumimasen.","german":"I'm sorry for being late for our appointment.","furigana":"やくそく|じかん|おく"},{"japanese":"一か月休んだので、勉強がみんなより遅れています。","romaji":"Ikkagetsu yasunda node, benkyou ga minna yori okurete imasu.","german":"Because I was absent for a month, I'm behind everyone else in my studies.","furigana":"いっ|げつやす|べんきょう|おく"}],"notes":"遅れる means \"to be late, not to be on time\"; the appointment or occasion takes に: 授業に遅れる, 約束の時間に遅れる. 遅刻する is narrower and means above all arriving late for school or work. Means of transport also run late (電車が遅れる), and clocks run slow (時計が遅れている). It also means \"to fall behind\": 勉強が遅れる, 流行に遅れる. The related adjective is 遅い, and the transitive 遅らせる means \"to delay, to postpone\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 19"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"兎小屋は火事によって破壊されました。","romaji":"Usagi koya wa kaji ni yotte hakai saremashita.","german":"The rabbit hutch was destroyed by fire.","furigana":"うさぎこや|かじ|はかい"},{"japanese":"人間の活動による環境破壊が、大きな問題になっている。","romaji":"Ningen no katsudou ni yoru kankyou hakai ga, ookina mondai ni natte iru.","german":"Environmental destruction caused by human activity has become a major problem.","furigana":"にんげん|かつどう|かんきょうはかい|おお|もんだい"},{"japanese":"犯人は窓ガラスを破壊して、家の中に入った。","romaji":"Hannin wa mado garasu o hakai shite, ie no naka ni haitta.","german":"The culprit smashed the window and entered the house.","furigana":"はんにん|まど|はかい|いえ|なか|はい"}],"notes":"破壊 is violent, often complete destruction; as a する-verb it is mostly transitive (建物を破壊する) and very often used in the passive 〜によって破壊される. Typical compounds are 環境破壊, 自然破壊 and 破壊的 \"destructive\". Compared with the everyday word 壊す, 破壊する sounds heavier and more formal; opposites are 建設 \"construction\" and 創造 \"creation\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"江戸時代の有名な刀を載せた船は、嵐によって破船してしまいました。","romaji":"Edo jidai no yuumei na katana o noseta fune wa, arashi ni yotte hasen shite shimaimashita.","german":"The ship carrying a famous Edo-period sword was wrecked in a storm.","furigana":"えどじだい|ゆうめい|かたな|の|ふね|あらし|はせん"},{"japanese":"嵐の翌朝、破船の木材が海岸に流れ着いていた。","romaji":"Arashi no yokuasa, hasen no mokuzai ga kaigan ni nagaretsuite ita.","german":"The morning after the storm, timber from the wrecked ship had washed up on the shore.","furigana":"あらし|よくあさ|はせん|もくざい|かいがん|なが|つ"},{"japanese":"昔の物語には、破船して無人島にたどり着く話が多い。","romaji":"Mukashi no monogatari ni wa, hasen shite mujintou ni tadoritsuku hanashi ga ooi.","german":"Many old tales are about being shipwrecked and reaching a deserted island.","furigana":"むかし|ものがたり|はせん|むじんとう|つ|はなし|おお"}],"notes":"破船 is a literary, rather archaic word for a shipwreck or the destroyed ship itself (wreck). It can be used as a する-verb: 嵐で破船する. Today people mostly say 難破 (難破する, 難破船) or speak of sinking: 沈没する. It is found mainly in novels, historical texts and stories.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その兵舎には異臭が漂っており、息をするのもやっとだった。","romaji":"Sono heisha ni wa ishū ga tadayotte ori, iki o suru no mo yatto datta.","german":"A foul smell hung in the barracks, and it was hard even to breathe.","furigana":"へいしゃ|いしゅう|ただよ|いき"},{"japanese":"兵士たちは、古い兵舎で寝泊まりしていた。","romaji":"Heishitachi wa, furui heisha de netomari shite ita.","german":"The soldiers slept and lived in an old barracks.","furigana":"へいし|ふる|へいしゃ|ねと"},{"japanese":"戦後、その兵舎は学校の校舎として使われた。","romaji":"Sengo, sono heisha wa gakkou no kousha to shite tsukawareta.","german":"After the war, the barracks was used as a school building.","furigana":"せんご|へいしゃ|がっこう|こうしゃ|つか"}],"notes":"兵舎 is the building where soldiers are housed: barracks. The word is military and historical; for the Japanese Self-Defense Forces people rather speak of 隊舎, and the whole grounds are called 駐屯地 or 基地. 舎 means \"building\" as in 校舎 \"school building\", 庁舎 \"government office building\" and 宿舎 \"lodging\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"庁舎に雷が落ちたみたいだよ。","romaji":"Chōsha ni kaminari ga ochita mitai da yo.","german":"It seems lightning struck the government building.","furigana":"ちょうしゃ|かみなり|お"},{"japanese":"市役所の新しい庁舎が、来年完成する予定です。","romaji":"Shiyakusho no atarashii chousha ga, rainen kansei suru yotei desu.","german":"The city hall's new building is scheduled to be completed next year.","furigana":"しやくしょ|あたら|ちょうしゃ|らいねんかんせい|よてい"},{"japanese":"住民票は、庁舎の一階の窓口で受け取れます。","romaji":"Juuminhyou wa, chousha no ikkai no madoguchi de uketoremasu.","german":"You can pick up your residence certificate at the counter on the first floor of the government building.","furigana":"じゅうみんひょう|ちょうしゃ|いっかい|まどぐち|う|と"}],"notes":"庁舎 is the building of a government office: 市庁舎 \"city hall building\", 県庁舎, 合同庁舎. It is a formal word for the structure itself and often appears on signs, maps and in the news (新庁舎の建設). In everyday speech people usually say just 市役所, 区役所 or 県庁, meaning the office together with its building.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その講師、講演会場にリムジンで登場したんだよ。","romaji":"Sono kōshi, kōen kaijō ni rimujin de tōjō shita n da yo.","german":"That lecturer showed up at the lecture hall in a limousine.","furigana":"こうし|こうえんかいじょう|とうじょう"},{"japanese":"有名な作家が、来週大学で講演をすることになった。","romaji":"Yuumei na sakka ga, raishuu daigaku de kouen o suru koto ni natta.","german":"A famous author is going to give a lecture at the university next week.","furigana":"ゆうめい|さっか|らいしゅうだいがく|こうえん"},{"japanese":"昨日の講演はとても興味深くて、時間があっという間に過ぎた。","romaji":"Kinou no kouen wa totemo kyoumibukakute, jikan ga atto iu ma ni sugita.","german":"Yesterday's lecture was so interesting that the time passed in no time.","furigana":"きのう|こうえん|きょうみぶか|じかん|ま|す"}],"notes":"講演 is a public talk before an audience, usually by experts or well-known figures: 講演をする/行う, 講演会, 講演者, 記念講演. A regular university lecture course is 講義, a political speech is 演説, and a speech in general is スピーチ. Beware of homophones: 公園 \"park\", 公演 \"performance\", and 後援 \"support, sponsorship\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その動画を遅滞なく削除せよ。","romaji":"Sono dōga o chitai naku sakujo seyo.","german":"Delete that video without delay.","furigana":"どうが|ちたい|さくじょ"},{"japanese":"支払いが遅滞した場合、追加料金がかかります。","romaji":"Shiharai ga chitai shita baai, tsuika ryoukin ga kakarimasu.","german":"If a payment is delayed, an additional fee will be charged.","furigana":"しはら|ちたい|ばあい|ついかりょうきん"},{"japanese":"大雪の影響で、配送に遅滞が生じています。","romaji":"Ooyuki no eikyou de, haisou ni chitai ga shoujite imasu.","german":"Due to heavy snow, delays are occurring in deliveries.","furigana":"おおゆき|えいきょう|はいそう|ちたい|しょう"}],"notes":"遅滞 denotes a delay or default, especially with obligations, payments and processes; it is a word of official, legal and business language. Very common is the set phrase 遅滞なく \"without delay, promptly\", typical in instructions and contracts. As an intransitive する-verb it takes が: 支払いが遅滞する \"the payment falls into default\". In everyday speech people say 遅れ or 遅れる instead.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"警察の介入により、事態は一層複雑化した。","romaji":"Keisatsu no kainyuu ni yori, jitai wa issou fukuzatsuka shita.","german":"With the police's intervention, the situation became even more complicated.","furigana":"けいさつ|かいにゅう|じたい|いっそうふくざつか"},{"japanese":"政府は円安を止めるために市場に介入した。","romaji":"Seifu wa en'yasu o tomeru tame ni shijou ni kainyuu shita.","german":"The government intervened in the market to stop the yen's decline.","furigana":"せいふ|えんやす|と|しじょう|かいにゅう"},{"japanese":"他人の家庭の問題に介入するべきではない。","romaji":"Tanin no katei no mondai ni kainyuu suru beki de wa nai.","german":"You shouldn't interfere in other people's family problems.","furigana":"たにん|かてい|もんだい|かいにゅう"}],"notes":"介入 is intervention from outside in a matter (often unwelcome, or official). As an intransitive する-verb, the target takes に: 市場に介入する \"to intervene in the market\". Typical combinations are 軍事介入, 政府の介入 or 警察の介入. Close in meaning is 干渉 \"interference\", which sounds more negative; colloquially people say 口を出す \"to butt in\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"俺は、みんな死ぬ前に己の墓は己で建てるべきだと思うけどね。","romaji":"Ore wa, minna shinu mae ni onore no haka wa onore de taterubeki da to omou kedo ne.","german":"I think everyone should build their own grave themselves before they die.","furigana":"おれ|し|まえ|おのれ|はか|おのれ|た|おも"},{"japanese":"他人を知る前に、まず己を知ることが大切だ。","romaji":"Tanin o shiru mae ni, mazu onore o shiru koto ga taisetsu da.","german":"Before getting to know others, it is important to first know yourself.","furigana":"たにん|し|まえ|おのれ|し|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"彼は己の力を信じて、最後まで戦った。","romaji":"Kare wa onore no chikara o shinjite, saigo made tatakatta.","german":"He believed in his own strength and fought to the end.","furigana":"かれ|おのれ|ちから|しん|さいご|たたか"}],"notes":"己 is a literary, archaic word for \"oneself, one's own self\"; in normal daily life people say 自分. It survives mainly in set phrases such as 己を知る \"to know oneself\", 己に勝つ \"to overcome oneself\" or 己の力 \"one's own strength\". As a form of address, おのれ is derogatory (\"you there!\"), and as an exclamation おのれ！ expresses anger, typical of samurai dramas and anime.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は、「俺様が一番好きな歌手は俺様自身だよ」と自己満足の笑みを浮かべながら豪語していた。","romaji":"Kare wa, oresama ga ichiban suki na kashu wa oresama jishin da yo to jiko manzoku no emi o ukabe nagara gōgo shite ita.","german":"\"The singer I like best is myself,\" he boasted with a self-satisfied smile.","furigana":"かれ|おれさま|いちばんす|かしゅ|おれさまじしん|じこまんぞく|え|う|ごうご"},{"japanese":"面接では、まず簡単に自己紹介をしてください。","romaji":"Mensetsu de wa, mazu kantan ni jiko shoukai o shite kudasai.","german":"In the interview, please start with a brief self-introduction.","furigana":"めんせつ|かんたん|じこしょうかい"},{"japanese":"一人で冬山に登るなら、自己責任でお願いします。","romaji":"Hitori de fuyuyama ni noboru nara, jiko sekinin de onegai shimasu.","german":"If you climb a winter mountain alone, you do so at your own risk.","furigana":"ひとり|ふゆやま|のぼ|じこせきにん|ねが"}],"notes":"自己 means \"the self\" and almost never stands alone but appears in fixed compounds: 自己紹介 \"self-introduction\", 自己満足 \"self-satisfaction\", 自己責任 \"personal responsibility\", 自己中心的 \"self-centered\", 自己管理 \"self-management\". In daily speech, \"I myself / oneself\" is 自分, and after a person 自身 is used (彼自身 \"he himself\"). 自己 sounds objective and abstract, as in psychology and on forms.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ビエットの顔に落書きなんてしたら、後々厄介なことになるよ。","romaji":"Bietto no kao ni rakugaki nante shitara, atoato yakkai na koto ni naru yo.","german":"If you scribble on Biet's face, it will cause trouble later.","furigana":"かお|らくが|あとあとやっかい"},{"japanese":"部長から厄介な仕事を頼まれてしまった。","romaji":"Buchou kara yakkai na shigoto o tanomarete shimatta.","german":"My department head asked me to do a troublesome job.","furigana":"ぶちょう|やっかい|しごと|たの"},{"japanese":"大学時代、私はおじの家に厄介になっていました。","romaji":"Daigaku jidai, watashi wa oji no ie ni yakkai ni natte imashita.","german":"In my university days, I was staying at my uncle's house, relying on his care.","furigana":"だいがくじだい|わたし|いえ|やっかい"}],"notes":"厄介 is a な-adjective for something \"troublesome, tricky\" that causes effort and annoyance: 厄介な問題, 厄介な客. Common are 厄介なことになる \"to get into trouble\" and 厄介者 \"a person who is a burden\". A second meaning is \"to depend on someone's care\": 〜の家に厄介になる \"to stay at someone's house\". Similar is 面倒 \"bothersome\" (more everyday); 迷惑, by contrast, stresses the nuisance caused to others.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その種の海亀は、絶滅の危機にあります。","romaji":"Sono shu no umigame wa, zetsumetsu no kiki ni arimasu.","german":"That species of sea turtle is in danger of extinction.","furigana":"しゅ|うみがめ|ぜつめつ|きき"},{"japanese":"夏の夜になると、海亀が浜辺に卵を産みに来ます。","romaji":"Natsu no yoru ni naru to, umigame ga hamabe ni tamago o umi ni kimasu.","german":"On summer nights, sea turtles come to the beach to lay eggs.","furigana":"なつ|よる|うみがめ|はまべ|たまご|う|き"},{"japanese":"沖縄の海で海亀と一緒に泳ぎました。","romaji":"Okinawa no umi de umigame to issho ni oyogimashita.","german":"I swam with sea turtles in the sea off Okinawa.","furigana":"おきなわ|うみ|うみがめ|いっしょ|およ"}],"notes":"海亀 is the sea turtle; in biology, zoos and the news it is usually written in katakana as ウミガメ. 亀 alone is the general term for turtles and tortoises, including land tortoises. Common combinations are 海亀の産卵 \"egg-laying of sea turtles\" and 海亀が卵を産む - a well-known summer event on beaches in Okinawa and Yakushima.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの二人は、「どうぞお座りください」とお互いに席の譲り合いをしたんだ。","romaji":"Ano futari wa, \"Dōzo osuwari kudasai\" to otagai ni seki no yuzuriai o shita n da.","german":"The two of them each offered the other their seat, saying \"Please, sit down.\"","furigana":"ふたり|すわ|たが|せき|ゆず|あ"},{"japanese":"困ったときは、お互い様ですよ。","romaji":"Komatta toki wa, otagaisama desu yo.","german":"When you're in trouble, it's the same for all of us.","furigana":"こま|たが|さま"},{"japanese":"チームのメンバーが互いに協力して、プロジェクトを成功させた。","romaji":"Chīmu no menbā ga tagai ni kyōryoku shite, purojekuto o seikō saseta.","german":"The team members cooperated with each other and made the project a success.","furigana":"たが|きょうりょく|せいこう"}],"notes":"互い is mostly used with the polite prefix as お互い; without お it sounds more literary. As an adverb, お互いに / 互いに means \"each other, mutually\": 互いに助け合う; with の it forms attributive phrases like お互いの意見 \"the opinions of both sides\". The set phrase お互い様 means \"it's the same for all of us, no need to thank me\" and is a typical reply to thanks or an apology among equals.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ページを交互に読んでいきましょう。","romaji":"Pēji o kōgo ni yonde ikimashō.","german":"Let's read the pages alternately.","furigana":"こうご|よ"},{"japanese":"長い旅行だったので、運転は兄と交互にしました。","romaji":"Nagai ryokou datta node, unten wa ani to kougo ni shimashita.","german":"It was a long trip, so my brother and I took turns driving.","furigana":"なが|りょこう|うんてん|あに|こうご"},{"japanese":"道の両側に赤と白の旗が交互に並んでいる。","romaji":"Michi no ryougawa ni aka to shiro no hata ga kougo ni narande iru.","german":"Red and white flags are lined up alternately on both sides of the road.","furigana":"みち|りょうがわ|あか|しろ|はた|こうご|なら"}],"notes":"交互 is almost always used adverbially as 交互に \"alternately, in turns\": AとBを交互に使う \"to use A and B alternately\"; attributively it stands as 交互の. It describes a regular alternation between two things or people. Similar are 代わる代わる \"in turn (people one after another)\", 交代で \"in shifts, taking turns\" and 順番に \"in order\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"携帯の電池の寿命が短くなってきてるんだけど。","romaji":"Keitai no denchi no jumyou ga mijikaku natte kiteru n da kedo.","german":"The battery life of my phone has been getting shorter.","furigana":"けいたい|でんち|じゅみょう|みじか"},{"japanese":"日本人の平均寿命は世界でもトップクラスです。","romaji":"Nihonjin no heikin jumyou wa sekai demo toppu kurasu desu.","german":"The average life expectancy of Japanese people is among the highest in the world.","furigana":"にほんじん|へいきんじゅみょう|せかい"},{"japanese":"急に車が飛び出してきて、寿命が縮んだよ。","romaji":"Kyuu ni kuruma ga tobidashite kite, jumyou ga chijinda yo.","german":"A car suddenly darted out, and it took years off my life.","furigana":"きゅう|くるま|と|だ|じゅみょう|ちぢ"}],"notes":"寿命 is the lifespan of living things and - very commonly - also of devices and objects: 電池の寿命 \"battery life over the years\", 電球の寿命. Typical are 寿命が長い／短い, 寿命が延びる and 平均寿命 \"average life expectancy\". 寿命が来る means a device is \"at the end of its life\"; the idiom 寿命が縮む (\"one's lifespan shrinks\") expresses a great shock.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"婆ちゃんにいつも、「バナナを食べることは長寿に繋がる」と言われるんだよね。","romaji":"Baachan ni itsumo, banana o taberu koto wa chouju ni tsunagaru to iwareru n da yo ne.","german":"My grandma always tells me, \"Eating bananas leads to a long life.\"","furigana":"ばあ|た|ちょうじゅ|つな|い"},{"japanese":"沖縄は長寿の地域として知られています。","romaji":"Okinawa wa chouju no chiiki to shite shirarete imasu.","german":"Okinawa is known as a region of longevity.","furigana":"おきなわ|ちょうじゅ|ちいき|し"},{"japanese":"この番組は三十年も続いている長寿番組だ。","romaji":"Kono bangumi wa sanjuunen mo tsuzuite iru chouju bangumi da.","german":"This program is a long-running show that has continued for thirty years.","furigana":"ばんぐみ|さんじゅうねん|つづ|ちょうじゅばんぐみ"}],"notes":"長寿 means \"long life, longevity\" and sounds somewhat more elevated than the everyday 長生き. Typical are 長寿の秘訣 \"the secret of a long life\", 長寿を祝う and 長寿国. Before nouns it also means \"long-running\": 長寿番組 is a TV program that has been on for decades. As a verb one does not say 長寿する but 長生きする.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"バイトのみんなで、店長の喜寿をコンビニでお祝いする予定なんだよ。","romaji":"Baito no minna de, tenchō no kiju o konbini de oiwai suru yotei na n da yo.","german":"All of us part-timers are planning to celebrate the manager's 77th birthday at a convenience store.","furigana":"てんちょう|きじゅ|いわ|よてい"},{"japanese":"祖父の喜寿のお祝いに、家族全員が集まりました。","romaji":"Sofu no kiju no oiwai ni, kazoku zenin ga atsumarimashita.","german":"The whole family gathered to celebrate my grandfather's 77th birthday.","furigana":"そふ|きじゅ|いわ|かぞくぜんいん|あつ"},{"japanese":"母は来年、喜寿を迎えます。","romaji":"Haha wa rainen, kiju o mukaemasu.","german":"My mother turns 77 next year.","furigana":"はは|らいねん|きじゅ|むか"}],"notes":"喜寿 is the 77th birthday, one of the traditional congratulatory birthdays for older people. The name comes from the cursive form of 喜 looking like 七十七. The series is 還暦 (60), 古希 (70), 喜寿 (77), 傘寿 (80), 米寿 (88) and 白寿 (99). Typical are 喜寿を迎える \"to turn 77\" and 喜寿のお祝い; the lucky color is purple.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お二人の末永い福寿をお祈り申し上げます。","romaji":"Ofutari no suenagai fukuju o oinori moushiagemasu.","german":"I pray for the two of you to enjoy happiness and long life for years to come.","furigana":"ふたり|すえなが|ふくじゅ|いの|もう|あ"},{"japanese":"お正月に、祖父母の福寿を願って神社にお参りしました。","romaji":"Oshougatsu ni, sofubo no fukuju o negatte jinja ni omairi shimashita.","german":"At New Year, we visited the shrine, wishing for our grandparents' happiness and longevity.","furigana":"しょうがつ|そふぼ|ふくじゅ|ねが|じんじゃ|まい"},{"japanese":"玄関に福寿草の鉢を飾りました。","romaji":"Genkan ni fukujusou no hachi o kazarimashita.","german":"I decorated the entrance with a pot of pheasant's eye.","furigana":"げんかん|ふくじゅそう|はち|かざ"}],"notes":"福寿 means \"happiness and long life\" and is an elevated, auspicious word for good wishes, New Year cards and inscriptions. It is not a celebration in itself, but something one wishes for someone: 福寿をお祈りします. It is best known in 福寿草 \"pheasant's eye (Adonis)\", a yellow New Year flower, and in the four-character word 福寿円満 \"happiness, long life and harmony\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"俺が果汁百パーセントのオレンジジュースをストローですすっていると、誰かが俺の肩をトントンと叩いた。","romaji":"Ore ga kajuu hyakupaasento no orenji juusu o sutoroo de susutte iru to, dare ka ga ore no kata o tonton to tataita.","german":"While I was sipping 100% orange juice through a straw, someone tapped me on the shoulder.","furigana":"おれ|かじゅうひゃく|だれ|おれ|かた|たた"},{"japanese":"このジュースは果汁が10パーセントしか入っていない。","romaji":"Kono juusu wa kajuu ga juppaasento shika haitte inai.","german":"This juice contains only 10 percent fruit juice.","furigana":"かじゅう|はい"},{"japanese":"レモンを絞って、果汁を少し魚にかけます。","romaji":"Remon o shibotte, kajuu o sukoshi sakana ni kakemasu.","german":"Squeeze the lemon and sprinkle a little of the juice over the fish.","furigana":"しぼ|かじゅう|すこ|さかな"}],"notes":"果汁 is the juice from fruit. Because ジュース in Japanese can refer to any sweet soft drink, 果汁 on labels indicates the actual fruit content: 果汁100％ \"100% juice\". Typical combinations are 果汁を絞る \"to squeeze out juice\" and 果汁たっぷり \"extra juicy\". 汁 alone means more \"broth, soup, liquid\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"夜勤の為に寝ておきたかったが、電車の音がうるさすぎた為に眠れなかった。","romaji":"Yakin no tame ni nete okitakatta ga, densha no oto ga urusa sugita tame ni nemurenakatta.","german":"I wanted to sleep before my night shift, but I couldn't because the train noise was too loud.","furigana":"やきん|ため|ね|でんしゃ|おと|ため|ねむ"},{"japanese":"家を買うために、毎月貯金しています。","romaji":"Ie o kau tame ni, maitsuki chokin shite imasu.","german":"I save money every month in order to buy a house.","furigana":"いえ|か|まいつきちょきん"},{"japanese":"大雪のために、電車が止まってしまった。","romaji":"Ooyuki no tame ni, densha ga tomatte shimatta.","german":"The trains stopped because of the heavy snow.","furigana":"おおゆき|でんしゃ|と"}],"notes":"ために (usually written in kana, rarely 為に) has two main meanings. Purpose: after nouns with の or after the dictionary form of volitional verbs, \"for, in order to\": 家族のために, 家を買うために. Reason: after nouns with の or after past forms, \"because of, as a result of\": 大雪のために - this sounds written and formal; in conversation people say から or ので. With non-volitional verbs or potential forms, ように is usually used instead of ために.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"それは裏切り行為ですよ。","romaji":"Sore wa uragiri kōi desu yo.","german":"That is an act of betrayal.","furigana":"うらぎ|こうい"},{"japanese":"電車の中での迷惑行為はやめましょう。","romaji":"Densha no naka de no meiwaku koui wa yamemashou.","german":"Let's stop disruptive behavior on the train.","furigana":"でんしゃ|なか|めいわくこうい"},{"japanese":"見知らぬ人の親切な行為に、心から感動した。","romaji":"Mishiranu hito no shinsetsu na koui ni, kokoro kara kandou shita.","german":"I was deeply moved by the kind act of a stranger.","furigana":"みし|ひと|しんせつ|こうい|こころ|かんどう"}],"notes":"行為 is a single \"act, deed\", usually with a legal or moral evaluation: 違法行為 \"illegal act\", 迷惑行為 \"nuisance behavior\", 犯罪行為, 親切な行為. It sounds formal and is not used with する. 行動 is more general \"behavior, action\" (行動する), and 行い refers more to a person's moral conduct.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"夜行バスは、熊本県で発生した事故のため一時運休になった。","romaji":"Yakou basu wa, Kumamoto-ken de hassei shita jiko no tame ichiji unkyuu ni natta.","german":"The night bus was temporarily suspended because of an accident that occurred in Kumamoto Prefecture.","furigana":"やこう|くまもとけん|はっせい|じこ|いちじうんきゅう"},{"japanese":"熊本県は九州の真ん中にあり、阿蘇山で有名です。","romaji":"Kumamoto-ken wa Kyuushuu no mannaka ni ari, Asosan de yuumei desu.","german":"Kumamoto Prefecture is in the middle of Kyushu and is famous for Mount Aso.","furigana":"くまもとけん|きゅうしゅう|ま|なか||ゆうめい"},{"japanese":"くまモンは熊本県のキャラクターです。","romaji":"Kumamon wa Kumamoto-ken no kyarakutaa desu.","german":"Kumamon is the mascot of Kumamoto Prefecture.","furigana":"くまもとけん"}],"notes":"熊本県 is a prefecture in the middle of the island of Kyushu; its capital is 熊本市. Well known are Kumamoto Castle (熊本城), the volcano 阿蘇山 and the mascot くまモン. In conversation people often say just 熊本 when it is clear whether the city or the prefecture is meant; 県 is added to name the prefecture explicitly, for example in the news and in addresses.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は獄内で小説を書き続けた。","romaji":"Kare wa gokunai de shousetsu o kakitsuzuketa.","german":"He kept writing novels inside the prison.","furigana":"かれ|ごくない|しょうせつ|か|つづ"},{"japanese":"獄内の生活は厳しく、自由はほとんどなかった。","romaji":"Gokunai no seikatsu wa kibishiku, jiyuu wa hotondo nakatta.","german":"Life inside the prison was harsh, with almost no freedom.","furigana":"ごくない|せいかつ|きび|じゆう"},{"japanese":"獄内での暴力が大きな問題になっている。","romaji":"Gokunai de no bouryoku ga ookina mondai ni natte iru.","german":"Violence inside the prison has become a major problem.","furigana":"ごくない|ぼうりょく|おお|もんだい"}],"notes":"獄内 means \"in prison, inside the prison\" and is a rare, literary word, found mainly in historical texts and reports. It usually appears as 獄内で or attributively as 獄内の. The synonymous 獄中 is more common (獄中で書いた手紙); in modern everyday speech people simply say 刑務所の中 or 刑務所内.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"初めて入獄した時、何故か安心感を得たんですよね。","romaji":"Hajimete nyūgoku shita toki, nazeka anshinkan o eta n desu yo ne.","german":"The first time I went to prison, for some reason I felt a sense of relief.","furigana":"はじ|にゅうごく|とき|なぜ|あんしんかん|え"},{"japanese":"彼は詐欺の罪で三年間入獄した。","romaji":"Kare wa sagi no tsumi de sannenkan nyuugoku shita.","german":"He was imprisoned for three years for fraud.","furigana":"かれ|さぎ|つみ|さんねんかんにゅうごく"},{"japanese":"若い頃に入獄した経験が、彼の人生を大きく変えた。","romaji":"Wakai koro ni nyuugoku shita keiken ga, kare no jinsei o ookiku kaeta.","german":"The experience of having been in prison in his youth changed his life greatly.","furigana":"わか|ころ|にゅうごく|けいけん|かれ|じんせい|おお|か"}],"notes":"入獄 means \"to go to prison, to begin serving a prison sentence\"; it is an intransitive する-verb from formal, somewhat old-fashioned written language. The opposite is 出獄 \"to be released from prison\". If someone is locked up, the transitive 投獄 is used in the passive: 投獄される. In everyday speech people more often say 刑務所に入る, in news 服役する \"to serve a sentence\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"出獄した日に万引きしてまた警察に逮捕されました。","romaji":"Shutsugoku shita hi ni manbiki shite mata keisatsu ni taiho saremashita.","german":"On the day I was released from prison, I shoplifted and was arrested by the police again.","furigana":"しゅつごく|ひ|まんび|けいさつ|たいほ"},{"japanese":"十年ぶりに出獄した彼を、母親が門の前で待っていた。","romaji":"Juunen buri ni shutsugoku shita kare o, hahaoya ga mon no mae de matte ita.","german":"His mother was waiting in front of the gate for him, released after ten years.","furigana":"じゅうねん|しゅつごく|かれ|ははおや|もん|まえ|ま"},{"japanese":"出獄後、彼は小さな店を開いて真面目に働いた。","romaji":"Shutsugokugo, kare wa chiisana mise o hiraite majime ni hataraita.","german":"After his release, he opened a small shop and worked diligently.","furigana":"しゅつごくご|かれ|ちい|みせ|ひら|まじめ|はたら"}],"notes":"出獄 means \"to be released from prison\" and is an intransitive する-verb of written language; the opposite is 入獄. In today's news you mostly hear 出所 (出所する) or 釈放 (釈放される \"to be set free\"). Typical are time expressions such as 十年ぶりに出獄する or 出獄後 \"after release\".","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の母親は彼に脱獄を強要した。","romaji":"Kare no hahaoya wa kare ni datsugoku o kyōyō shita.","german":"His mother forced him to break out of prison.","furigana":"かれ|ははおや|かれ|だつごく|きょうよう"},{"japanese":"夜中に囚人が二人、刑務所から脱獄した。","romaji":"Yonaka ni shuujin ga futari, keimusho kara datsugoku shita.","german":"Two prisoners escaped from the prison in the middle of the night.","furigana":"よなか|しゅうじん|ふたり|けいむしょ|だつごく"},{"japanese":"スマホを脱獄すると、メーカーの保証が受けられなくなります。","romaji":"Sumaho o datsugoku suru to, meekaa no hoshou ga ukerarenaku narimasu.","german":"If you jailbreak your smartphone, you will no longer be covered by the manufacturer's warranty.","furigana":"だつごく|ほしょう|う"}],"notes":"脱獄 is a \"prison break\"; as an intransitive する-verb: 刑務所から脱獄する. The escapee is called 脱獄囚. More general is 脱走 \"escape, desertion\" (also from barracks or zoos). In IT language 脱獄 also means \"jailbreak\" of a smartphone or game console, and it is then used transitively: スマホを脱獄する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"みんな船に酔ってしまい、そこら中に吐き散らかしていました。","romaji":"Minna fune ni yotte shimai, sokorajuu ni hakichirakashite imashita.","german":"Everyone got seasick and was throwing up all over the place.","furigana":"ふね|よ|じゅう|は|ち"},{"japanese":"昨日はビールを飲みすぎて、すっかり酔ってしまった。","romaji":"Kinou wa biiru o nomisugite, sukkari yotte shimatta.","german":"I drank too much beer yesterday and got completely drunk.","furigana":"きのう|の|よ"},{"japanese":"観客はみんな、彼女の歌声に酔っていた。","romaji":"Kankyaku wa minna, kanojo no utagoe ni yotte ita.","german":"The whole audience was enchanted by her singing voice.","furigana":"かんきゃく|かのじょ|うたごえ|よ"}],"notes":"酔う is an intransitive godan verb with three meanings: \"to get drunk\" (酒に酔う), \"to feel sick in vehicles\" (車に酔う, 船に酔う; also 車酔い, 船酔い), and figuratively \"to be intoxicated, carried away\" (音楽に酔う). The state \"to be drunk\" is expressed by 酔っている; colloquial and stronger is 酔っ払う. The causative 酔わせる means \"to make drunk\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"完璧に泥酔していて、方向感覚を失っていました。","romaji":"Kanpeki ni deisui shite ite, hōkō kankaku o ushinatte imashita.","german":"I was completely plastered and had lost my sense of direction.","furigana":"かんぺき|でいすい|ほうこうかんかく|うしな"},{"japanese":"泥酔した男性が駅のホームで寝ていた。","romaji":"Deisui shita dansei ga eki no hoomu de nete ita.","german":"A drunken man was sleeping on the station platform.","furigana":"でいすい|だんせい|えき|ね"},{"japanese":"泥酔状態での運転は、とても危険です。","romaji":"Deisui joutai de no unten wa, totemo kiken desu.","german":"Driving while dead drunk is very dangerous.","furigana":"でいすいじょうたい|うんてん|きけん"}],"notes":"泥酔 is a state of total drunkenness in which one can hardly stand or remember anything - much stronger than 酔う or 酔っ払う. It sounds matter-of-fact to formal and often appears in news and police reports: 泥酔状態 \"state of being dead drunk\", 泥酔客, 泥酔した男性. As an intransitive する-verb it usually appears in the forms 泥酔している／泥酔した.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大人になったら酢の物が好きになりました。","romaji":"Otona ni nattara sunomono ga suki ni narimashita.","german":"I came to like vinegared dishes once I became an adult.","furigana":"おとな|す|もの|す"},{"japanese":"きゅうりとわかめで酢の物を作りました。","romaji":"Kyuuri to wakame de sunomono o tsukurimashita.","german":"I made a vinegared dish with cucumber and wakame.","furigana":"す|もの|つく"},{"japanese":"定食に、小さな酢の物が付いてきた。","romaji":"Teishoku ni, chiisana sunomono ga tsuite kita.","german":"A small vinegared dish came with the set meal.","furigana":"ていしょく|ちい|す|もの|つ"}],"notes":"酢の物 is a small Japanese dish whose ingredients are dressed in a vinegar mixture - typically cucumber, wakame seaweed or octopus. It is served as a side dish in a small bowl (小鉢), for example with a set menu. It is not the same as 漬物, which is pickled with salt, rice bran or miso. To prepare it is 酢の物を作る.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"安物のカメラで撮ったにしては、山中湖の写真すごく綺麗に撮れてるじゃん。","romaji":"Yasumono no kamera de totta ni shite wa, Yamanakako no shashin sugoku kirei ni toreteru jan.","german":"Considering they were taken with a cheap camera, your photos of Lake Yamanaka came out really beautiful.","furigana":"やすもの|と|やまなかこ|しゃしん|きれい|と"},{"japanese":"山中湖は富士五湖の中で一番大きい湖です。","romaji":"Yamanakako wa Fuji goko no naka de ichiban ookii mizuumi desu.","german":"Lake Yamanaka is the largest of the Fuji Five Lakes.","furigana":"やまなかこ|ふじごこ|なか|いちばんおお|みずうみ"},{"japanese":"夏休みに、山中湖の近くで友達とキャンプをしました。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi ni, Yamanakako no chikaku de tomodachi to kyanpu o shimashita.","german":"During summer vacation, I went camping near Lake Yamanaka with friends.","furigana":"なつやす|やまなかこ|ちか|ともだち"}],"notes":"山中湖 is the largest of the five Fuji lakes (富士五湖) in Yamanashi Prefecture, right at the foot of Mt. Fuji. It is a popular excursion spot with a view of the mountain, for camping and for training camps of school and university clubs. In lake names 湖 is read こ (山中湖, 琵琶湖), whereas the word on its own is みずうみ.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"青銅でコウイチの像を鋳造するっていうのはどうよ。","romaji":"Seidou de Kouichi no zou o chuuzou suru tte iu no wa dou yo.","german":"How about casting a statue of Koichi in bronze?","furigana":"せいどう|ぞう|ちゅうぞう"},{"japanese":"青銅は、銅とすずを混ぜて作られます。","romaji":"Seidou wa, dou to suzu o mazete tsukuraremasu.","german":"Bronze is made by mixing copper and tin.","furigana":"せいどう|どう|ま|つく"},{"japanese":"博物館で古代中国の青銅器を見ました。","romaji":"Hakubutsukan de kodai Chuugoku no seidouki o mimashita.","german":"I saw ancient Chinese bronze ware at the museum.","furigana":"はくぶつかん|こだいちゅうごく|せいどうき|み"}],"notes":"青銅 is bronze, an alloy of copper and tin; the name (\"blue copper\") comes from the greenish patina. The word sounds technical and historical: 青銅器 \"bronze vessel\", 青銅器時代 \"Bronze Age\". In everyday speech people often say ブロンズ. Note: the bronze medal is 銅メダル, not 青銅メダル, and a bronze statue is usually simply 銅像.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本では、銅山は次々に閉山していっています。","romaji":"Nippon de wa, dōzan wa tsugitsugi ni heizan shite itte imasu.","german":"In Japan, copper mines are closing one after another.","furigana":"にっぽん|どうざん|つぎつぎ|へいざん"},{"japanese":"足尾銅山は、日本の公害問題の始まりとして知られている。","romaji":"Ashio douzan wa, Nihon no kougai mondai no hajimari to shite shirarete iru.","german":"The Ashio copper mine is known as the beginning of Japan's pollution problems.","furigana":"あしおどうざん|にほん|こうがいもんだい|はじ|し"},{"japanese":"祖父は若い頃、銅山で働いていました。","romaji":"Sofu wa wakai koro, douzan de hataraite imashita.","german":"My grandfather worked in a copper mine when he was young.","furigana":"そふ|わか|ころ|どうざん|はたら"}],"notes":"銅山 is a copper mine. In words for mines, 山 is read ざん or さん: 金山 (きんざん) \"gold mine\", 銀山 (ぎんざん) \"silver mine\", generally 鉱山 (こうざん) \"mine\"; a coal mine, by contrast, is 炭鉱. Closing a mine is 閉山 (閉山する). Historically well known is the 足尾銅山, the scene of Japan's first major environmental scandal.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"誰かが銅像を粉々に破壊しました。","romaji":"Dare ka ga dōzō o konagona ni hakai shimashita.","german":"Someone smashed the bronze statue to pieces.","furigana":"だれ|どうぞう|こなごな|はかい"},{"japanese":"渋谷駅の前には、ハチ公の銅像があります。","romaji":"Shibuya eki no mae ni wa, Hachikou no douzou ga arimasu.","german":"In front of Shibuya Station there is a bronze statue of Hachiko.","furigana":"しぶやえき|まえ|こう|どうぞう"},{"japanese":"町の公園に、有名な作家の銅像が建てられた。","romaji":"Machi no kouen ni, yuumei na sakka no douzou ga taterareta.","german":"A bronze statue of a famous author was erected in the town park.","furigana":"まち|こうえん|ゆうめい|さっか|どうぞう|た"}],"notes":"銅像 is a statue (usually cast in bronze), typically of a person or an animal on a pedestal. One says 銅像を建てる \"to erect a statue\" or, in the passive, 銅像が建てられる. The general term is 像 \"statue, image\"; one made of stone is a 石像. Famous is the ハチ公の銅像 in Shibuya, a popular meeting spot (ハチ公前で待ち合わせ).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"残念ですが、どの油田も私のものではありません。","romaji":"Zannen desu ga, dono yuden mo watashi no mono de wa arimasen.","german":"I'm sorry, but none of the oil fields belong to me.","furigana":"ざんねん|ゆでん|わたし"},{"japanese":"中東には大きな油田がたくさんある。","romaji":"Chuutou ni wa ookina yuden ga takusan aru.","german":"There are many large oil fields in the Middle East.","furigana":"ちゅうとう|おお|ゆでん"},{"japanese":"海の底で新しい油田が発見された。","romaji":"Umi no soko de atarashii yuden ga hakken sareta.","german":"A new oil field was discovered on the seabed.","furigana":"うみ|そこ|あたら|ゆでん|はっけん"}],"notes":"油田 is an oil field, an area where petroleum is produced. The word is built like 炭田 \"coalfield\" and ガス田 \"gas field\": 田 here means \"field, deposit\". Typical combinations are 油田を発見する／開発する and 海底油田 \"offshore oil field\". The extracted oil is 原油 \"crude oil\", petroleum in general is 石油.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"原油の値段は上がり続けている。","romaji":"Gen'yu no nedan wa agari tsuzukete iru.","german":"The price of crude oil keeps rising.","furigana":"げんゆ|ねだん|あ|つづ"},{"japanese":"日本は原油のほとんどを輸入に頼っている。","romaji":"Nihon wa gen'yu no hotondo o yunyuu ni tayotte iru.","german":"Japan relies on imports for almost all of its crude oil.","furigana":"にほん|げんゆ|ゆにゅう|たよ"},{"japanese":"原油価格が下がって、ガソリンが少し安くなった。","romaji":"Gen'yu kakaku ga sagatte, gasorin ga sukoshi yasuku natta.","german":"Crude oil prices fell, and gasoline became a little cheaper.","furigana":"げんゆかかく|さ|すこ|やす"}],"notes":"原油 is unprocessed crude oil as it comes out of the ground; 原 here means \"raw, original\" as in 原料. Products such as ガソリン \"gasoline\" and 灯油 \"kerosene, heating oil\" are obtained from it; 石油 is the umbrella term \"petroleum\". Common in business news: 原油価格 \"crude oil price\", 原油高 \"high oil price\", 原油を輸入する.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"給油ホースが車まで届きません。","romaji":"Kyuuyu hoosu ga kuruma made todokimasen.","german":"The refueling hose doesn't reach the car.","furigana":"きゅうゆ|くるま|とど"},{"japanese":"高速道路に乗る前に、ガソリンスタンドで給油しよう。","romaji":"Kousoku douro ni noru mae ni, gasorin sutando de kyuuyu shiyou.","german":"Let's refuel at a gas station before getting on the expressway.","furigana":"こうそくどうろ|の|まえ|きゅうゆ"},{"japanese":"飛行機は給油のため、途中の空港に着陸しました。","romaji":"Hikouki wa kyuuyu no tame, tochuu no kuukou ni chakuriku shimashita.","german":"The plane landed at an airport along the way to refuel.","furigana":"ひこうき|きゅうゆ|とちゅう|くうこう|ちゃくりく"}],"notes":"給油 is the \"refueling, filling up\" of cars, planes or ships and can be used as a する-verb: 給油する. The word sounds somewhat formal; in conversation people often say ガソリンを入れる. Typical are セルフ給油 \"self-service refueling\", 給油口 \"fuel filler opening\" and 給油所 (= ガソリンスタンド). A second meaning is oiling machines.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"明日は４時起床の予定です。","romaji":"Ashita wa yonji kishō no yotei desu.","german":"Tomorrow I'm scheduled to get up at 4.","furigana":"あした|じきしょう|よてい"},{"japanese":"毎朝六時に起床して、ジョギングをしています。","romaji":"Maiasa rokuji ni kishou shite, jogingu o shite imasu.","german":"I get up at six every morning and go jogging.","furigana":"まいあさろくじ|きしょう"},{"japanese":"合宿では、起床時間と就寝時間が決まっている。","romaji":"Gasshuku de wa, kishou jikan to shuushin jikan ga kimatte iru.","german":"At the training camp, wake-up time and bedtime are fixed.","furigana":"がっしゅく|きしょうじかん|しゅうしんじかん|き"}],"notes":"起床 is the formal word for \"getting up (out of bed)\"; in everyday speech people say 起きる. It appears in schedules, dormitories, hospitals and in the military: 起床時間 \"wake-up time\", 6時起床 \"getting up at 6\". The counterpart is 就寝 \"going to bed\". 起床 means getting up, whereas 目覚める or 目が覚める means merely waking up.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子どもたちは喜び勇んで海へ走っていった。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa yorokobi isande umi e hashitte itta.","german":"The children ran off to the sea, joyful and full of spirit.","furigana":"こ|よろこ|いさ|うみ|はし"},{"japanese":"彼は勇んで試合に出かけたが、一回戦で負けてしまった。","romaji":"Kare wa isande shiai ni dekaketa ga, ikkaisen de makete shimatta.","german":"He set off for the match full of eagerness, but lost in the first round.","furigana":"かれ|いさ|しあい|で|いっかいせん|ま"},{"japanese":"まだ決まっていないのに発表するなんて、ちょっと勇み足だったね。","romaji":"Mada kimatte inai noni happyou suru nante, chotto isamiashi datta ne.","german":"Announcing it when it hasn't been decided yet was a bit of a rash move.","furigana":"き|はっぴょう|いさ|あし"}],"notes":"勇む means \"to be full of spirit, to be in high spirits\" and is used almost only adverbially in the て-form: 勇んで出かける \"to set off full of zeal\", especially in the set phrase 喜び勇んで \"joyfully and full of vigor\". Other forms such as 勇む必要はない are hardly used. Related is the noun 勇み足 \"overeagerness, rash step\" (originally a sumo foul).","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"早くしないと、日が暮れるわよ。","romaji":"Hayaku shinai to, hi ga kureru wa yo.","german":"If you don't hurry, it will get dark.","furigana":"はや|ひ|く"},{"japanese":"冬は五時ごろにはもう日が暮れます。","romaji":"Fuyu wa goji goro ni wa mou hi ga kuremasu.","german":"In winter it is already dark by around five o'clock.","furigana":"ふゆ|ごじ|ひ|く"},{"japanese":"道に迷って、途方に暮れました。","romaji":"Michi ni mayotte, tohou ni kuremashita.","german":"I had lost my way and was at a loss.","furigana":"みち|まよ|とほう|く"}],"notes":"暮れる means \"to get dark\" and almost always has 日 as its subject: 日が暮れる. The opposite is 夜が明ける \"dawn breaks\". Figuratively a year can also come to an end (年が暮れる); hence 暮れ \"year-end\" and 夕暮れ \"dusk\". The fixed expression 途方に暮れる means \"to be at a loss\". Do not confuse it with the verb くれる \"to give\", which is written in kana.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どうしてここに白熊がいるんだ？","romaji":"Dōshite koko ni shirokuma ga iru n da?","german":"Why is there a polar bear here?","furigana":"しろくま"},{"japanese":"北極の氷が減って、白熊の生活が危なくなっている。","romaji":"Hokkyoku no koori ga hette, shirokuma no seikatsu ga abunaku natte iru.","german":"As Arctic ice shrinks, the lives of polar bears are becoming endangered.","furigana":"ほっきょく|こおり|へ|しろくま|せいかつ|あぶ"},{"japanese":"鹿児島に行ったら、かき氷の「白熊」をぜひ食べてみて。","romaji":"Kagoshima ni ittara, kakigoori no \"Shirokuma\" o zehi tabete mite.","german":"If you go to Kagoshima, be sure to try the shaved ice \"Shirokuma\".","furigana":"かごしま|い|ごおり|しろくま|た"}],"notes":"白熊 is the everyday name for the polar bear and is often written in kana (シロクマ, しろくま); the zoological name is ホッキョクグマ \"North Pole bear\". In Kagoshima, 白熊 is also a famous shaved-ice specialty with condensed milk and fruit. 熊 on its own is the bear in general.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あとでちょっと喋ろうよ。","romaji":"Ato de chotto shaberō yo.","german":"Let's chat for a bit later.","furigana":"しゃべ"},{"japanese":"授業中に隣の人と喋らないでください。","romaji":"Jugyouchuu ni tonari no hito to shaberanaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't chat with the person next to you during class.","furigana":"じゅぎょうちゅう|となり|ひと|しゃべ"},{"japanese":"この秘密は誰にも喋らないでね。","romaji":"Kono himitsu wa dare ni mo shaberanaide ne.","german":"Please don't tell this secret to anyone.","furigana":"ひみつ|だれ|しゃべ"}],"notes":"喋る is colloquial for \"to talk, to chat, to blab\" and is usually written in kana as しゃべる. Unlike the neutral 話す, it stresses casual, often voluminous talking; with を it can also mean \"to blab out\": 秘密を喋る. Related are お喋り \"chat; chatterbox\" and お喋りな人. In formal situations 話す is used.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"そのジーンズって股下何センチ？","romaji":"Sono jīnzu tte matashita nansenchi?","german":"What is the inseam on those jeans?","furigana":"またしたなん"},{"japanese":"裾上げのために、店員さんに股下を測ってもらいました。","romaji":"Susoage no tame ni, ten'in-san ni matashita o hakatte moraimashita.","german":"To have my pants hemmed, I had the clerk measure my inseam.","furigana":"すそあ|てんいん|またした|はか"},{"japanese":"彼は背が高くて股下も長いので、合うズボンがなかなかない。","romaji":"Kare wa se ga takakute matashita mo nagai node, au zubon ga nakanaka nai.","german":"He is tall and has a long inseam, so it's hard to find pants that fit.","furigana":"かれ|せ|たか|またした|なが|あ"}],"notes":"股下 is the inseam or inside leg length, measured from the crotch to the hem; the word appears mainly with trouser sizes and alterations. Typical are 股下を測る \"to measure the inseam\" and figures like 股下75センチ. The counterpart 股上 denotes the rise of trousers. Shortening trousers is called 裾上げ.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"スマホで自撮りした。","romaji":"Sumaho de jidori shita.","german":"I took a selfie with my smartphone.","furigana":"じど"},{"japanese":"観光地では、自撮り棒を使っている人が多い。","romaji":"Kankouchi de wa, jidoribou o tsukatte iru hito ga ooi.","german":"At tourist spots, many people use selfie sticks.","furigana":"かんこうち|じど|ぼう|つか|ひと|おお"},{"japanese":"自撮りに夢中になって、駅の階段で転びそうになった。","romaji":"Jidori ni muchuu ni natte, eki no kaidan de korobisou ni natta.","german":"I got so absorbed in taking selfies that I almost fell on the station stairs.","furigana":"じど|むちゅう|えき|かいだん|ころ"}],"notes":"自撮り (from 自分 + 撮る, with voiced ど) is the colloquial word for \"selfie\" and can be used as a する-verb: 自撮りする. Well known is 自撮り棒 \"selfie stick\". The loanword セルフィー also exists but is rarer. When you have others take your picture, you say 写真を撮ってもらう instead.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"雨天の場合、花火大会は中止です。","romaji":"Uten no baai, hanabi taikai wa chūshi desu.","german":"In case of rain, the fireworks festival is canceled.","furigana":"うてん|ばあい|はなびたいかい|ちゅうし"},{"japanese":"マラソン大会は雨天決行なので、雨が降っても行われます。","romaji":"Marason taikai wa uten kekkou na node, ame ga futte mo okonawaremasu.","german":"The marathon is held rain or shine, so it will take place even if it rains.","furigana":"たいかい|うてんけっこう|あめ|ふ|おこな"},{"japanese":"雨天でも使える屋根付きのテニスコートを予約した。","romaji":"Uten demo tsukaeru yane tsuki no tenisu kooto o yoyaku shita.","german":"I reserved a tennis court with a roof that can be used even in rainy weather.","furigana":"うてん|つか|やねつ|よやく"}],"notes":"雨天 is the formal word for \"rainy weather\" and appears mainly in announcements and notices: 雨天中止 \"canceled in case of rain\", 雨天決行 \"held even in the rain\", 雨天順延 \"postponed to the next day in case of rain\", 雨天の場合は… The opposite is 晴天. In conversation people simply say 雨の日 or 雨だったら.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"やっぱりプロと素人は違うね！","romaji":"Yappari puro to shirōto wa chigau ne!","german":"Professionals really are different from amateurs!","furigana":"しろうと|ちが"},{"japanese":"素人の私が見ても、この絵が上手なのはわかる。","romaji":"Shirouto no watashi ga mite mo, kono e ga jouzu na no wa wakaru.","german":"Even as an amateur, I can tell this painting is good.","furigana":"しろうと|わたし|み|え|じょうず"},{"japanese":"素人が電気の工事をするのは危ないですよ。","romaji":"Shirouto ga denki no kouji o suru no wa abunai desu yo.","german":"It's dangerous for amateurs to do electrical work.","furigana":"しろうと|でんき|こうじ|あぶ"}],"notes":"素人 (\"layperson, amateur, non-specialist\") has an irregular reading: しろうと. The opposite is 玄人 (くろうと) \"expert, pro\"; today people often say プロ instead. Typical are 素人の私でも \"even I as a layperson\", 素人目には \"to the eye of a layperson\" and 素人っぽい \"amateurish\". Colloquially, ド素人 \"total beginner\" is an intensified (derogatory) form.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの会社は最近すごく儲かってるらしい。","romaji":"Ano kaisha wa saikin sugoku moukatteru rashii.","german":"That company seems to be making a lot of profit lately.","furigana":"かいしゃ|さいきん|もう"},{"japanese":"この商売は、思ったほど儲からない。","romaji":"Kono shoubai wa, omotta hodo moukaranai.","german":"This business isn't as profitable as I thought.","furigana":"しょうばい|おも|もう"},{"japanese":"株で十万円儲かった。","romaji":"Kabu de juuman'en moukatta.","german":"I made 100,000 yen on stocks.","furigana":"かぶ|じゅうまんえんもう"}],"notes":"儲かる is intransitive: a business or a person \"yields profit, makes a profit\" - 店が儲かる, 株で儲かる. The transitive counterpart is 儲ける \"to earn (money), to make a profit\": お金を儲ける. 稼ぐ, by contrast, emphasizes earning through one's own work. Often written in kana もうかる; well known is the Osaka greeting 儲かりまっか \"How's business?\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"父は心理学の学者です。","romaji":"Chichi wa shinrigaku no gakusha desu.","german":"My father is a scholar of psychology.","furigana":"ちち|しんりがく|がくしゃ"},{"japanese":"あの人は有名な学者です。","romaji":"Ano hito wa yūmei na gakusha desu.","german":"That person is a famous scholar.","furigana":"ひと|ゆうめい|がくしゃ"},{"japanese":"この問題については、学者の間でも意見が分かれている。","romaji":"Kono mondai ni tsuite wa, gakusha no aida demo iken ga wakarete iru.","german":"Scholars are divided in their opinions on this issue.","furigana":"もんだい|がくしゃ|あいだ|いけん|わ"}],"notes":"学者 refers to a scholar or scientist, especially one doing research at a university, in both the humanities and the natural sciences. Fields are formed with 〜学者: 経済学者, 言語学者, 心理学者. 研究者 \"researcher\" is more neutral and modern, while 科学者 means specifically natural scientists. 学者肌 describes someone with a scholarly, theoretical temperament.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"正しい角度にするのに時間がかかりました。","romaji":"Tadashii kakudo ni suru no ni jikan ga kakarimashita.","german":"It took time to get it to the correct angle.","furigana":"ただ|かくど|じかん"},{"japanese":"この角度から写真を撮りましょう。","romaji":"Kono kakudo kara shashin o torimashō.","german":"Let's take a photo from this angle.","furigana":"かくど|しゃしん|と"},{"japanese":"角度を変えて考えれば、別の答えが見つかるかもしれない。","romaji":"Kakudo o kaete kangaereba, betsu no kotae ga mitsukaru kamoshirenai.","german":"If you think about it from a different angle, you might find another answer.","furigana":"かくど|か|かんが|べつ|こた|み"}],"notes":"角度 is the \"angle\" in the geometric sense - 角度を測る \"to measure an angle\", 30度の角度 - and also the viewing angle of a camera or of consideration. Figuratively, 別の角度から見る／考える means \"to look at or think about from a different angle\". Distinguish it from 角 (かど) \"corner\"; 視点 and 観点 refer more to a \"standpoint\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"飛行機は高度一万メートルで飛んでいます。","romaji":"Hikōki wa kōdo ichiman mētoru de tonde imasu.","german":"The plane is flying at an altitude of ten thousand meters.","furigana":"ひこうき|こうどいちまん|と"},{"japanese":"山に登ると、高度が上がるにつれて気温が下がっていく。","romaji":"Yama ni noboru to, kōdo ga agaru ni tsurete kion ga sagatte iku.","german":"As you climb a mountain, the temperature drops as the altitude rises.","furigana":"やま|のぼ|こうど|あ|きおん|さ"},{"japanese":"この仕事には、高度な専門知識が必要です。","romaji":"Kono shigoto ni wa, kōdo na senmon chishiki ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"This job requires advanced specialized knowledge.","furigana":"しごと|こうど|せんもんちしき|ひつよう"}],"notes":"高度 has two meanings. As a technical word it is the height above sea level or flight altitude: 高度一万メートル, 高度を上げる / 下げる; for the height of a building or a person's height, by contrast, people say 高さ or 身長. As a な-adjective, 高度 means \"high-level, sophisticated\": 高度な技術, 高度な知識. Also well known is 高度経済成長, Japan's rapid economic growth after the war.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"出発の直前に雨がふりだした。","romaji":"Shuppatsu no chokuzen ni ame ga furidashita.","german":"It began to rain just before departure.","furigana":"しゅっぱつ|ちょくぜん|あめ"},{"japanese":"試験の直前になって、急に不安になった。","romaji":"Shiken no chokuzen ni natte, kyuu ni fuan ni natta.","german":"Right before the exam, I suddenly became anxious.","furigana":"しけん|ちょくぜん|きゅう|ふあん"},{"japanese":"車の直前に、子どもが飛び出してきた。","romaji":"Kuruma no chokuzen ni, kodomo ga tobidashite kita.","german":"A child ran out right in front of the car.","furigana":"くるま|ちょくぜん|こ|と|だ"}],"notes":"直前 means \"immediately before\" in time and \"directly in front of\" in space. It follows a noun with の or the dictionary form: 出発の直前に, 寝る直前に. 直前になって emphasizes \"only at the last minute\", 直前のキャンセル is a last-minute cancellation. The opposite is 直後 \"immediately after\"; 寸前 \"on the verge of\" usually stands before something negative (倒れる寸前).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"こんな高級なプレゼントもらえません。","romaji":"Konna kōkyū na purezento moraemasen.","german":"I can't accept such an expensive present.","furigana":"こうきゅう"},{"japanese":"高級な料理を食べました。","romaji":"Kōkyū na ryōri o tabemashita.","german":"I ate a high-class meal.","furigana":"こうきゅう|りょうり|た"},{"japanese":"駅前に新しい高級ホテルができた。","romaji":"Ekimae ni atarashii koukyuu hoteru ga dekita.","german":"A new luxury hotel opened in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|あたら|こうきゅう"}],"notes":"高級 means \"high-quality, luxurious, upscale\" and is used as a な-adjective (高級な料理) or directly before nouns in set combinations: 高級車, 高級ホテル, 高級品, 高級住宅街. 高級感がある means \"looks classy\". Distinguish it from 高価 \"expensive, of high price\": 高級 emphasizes class and quality. Less often it means \"high-ranking\" as in 高級官僚.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あなたの国の高等教育について教えてください。","romaji":"Anata no kuni no koutou kyouiku ni tsuite oshiete kudasai.","german":"Please tell me about higher education in your country.","furigana":"くに|こうとうきょういく|おし"},{"japanese":"「高校」は「高等学校」を短くした言葉です。","romaji":"\"Koukou\" wa \"koutou gakkou\" o mijikaku shita kotoba desu.","german":"\"高校\" is a shortened form of \"高等学校\".","furigana":"こうこう|こうとうがっこう|みじか|ことば"},{"japanese":"それはかなり高等なテクニックだね。","romaji":"Sore wa kanari koutou na tekunikku da ne.","german":"That's quite an advanced technique.","furigana":"こうとう"}],"notes":"高等 means \"higher, of a higher level\" and occurs mainly in set compounds: 高等学校 (short 高校) \"high school\", 高等教育 \"higher education\", 高等裁判所 \"high court\", 高等動物 \"higher animals\". As a な-adjective it is rarer and means \"sophisticated, advanced\", e.g. 高等なテクニック. Do not confuse it with 高級 \"luxurious\" and 高度 \"highly developed\" (高度な技術).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"国語教育の在り方について議論が交わされた。","romaji":"Kokugo kyōiku no arikata ni tsuite giron ga kawasareta.","german":"There was a debate about the state of Japanese language education.","furigana":"こくごきょういく|あ|かた|ぎろん|か"},{"japanese":"小学生の時、国語の授業で漢字をたくさん覚えた。","romaji":"Shougakusei no toki, kokugo no jugyou de kanji o takusan oboeta.","german":"In elementary school, I memorized a lot of kanji in Japanese class.","furigana":"しょうがくせい|とき|こくご|じゅぎょう|かんじ|おぼ"},{"japanese":"分からない言葉を国語辞典で調べた。","romaji":"Wakaranai kotoba o kokugo jiten de shirabeta.","german":"I looked up the words I didn't know in a Japanese dictionary.","furigana":"わ|ことば|こくごじてん|しら"}],"notes":"国語 is generally the \"national language\", but in Japan it mainly means the school subject \"Japanese\" for native speakers: 国語の授業, 国語の先生. For Japanese as a foreign language, 日本語 is used instead (日本語教育 vs. 国語教育). 国語辞典 is a monolingual Japanese dictionary. For one's own mother tongue there are 母語 and 母国語.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"本人 is \"the person in question himself/herself\", as opposed to relatives, representatives or third parties: 本人に聞く \"to ask the person directly\", 本人以外 \"other than the person himself/herself\". Very common is 本人確認 \"identity verification\"; in shops and on the phone the polite ご本人様 is used. Unlike 自分 (\"I myself / oneself\"), 本人 always refers to a specific person meant in the context.","examples":[{"japanese":"詳しいことは本人に聞いてください。","romaji":"Kuwashii koto wa honnin ni kiite kudasai.","german":"Please ask the person directly for the details.","furigana":"くわ|ほんにん|き"},{"japanese":"申し込みには本人の署名が必要です。","romaji":"Mōshikomi ni wa honnin no shomei ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"The applicant's own signature is required for the application.","furigana":"もう|こ|ほんにん|しょめい|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"本人確認のため、身分証明書を見せてください。","romaji":"Honnin kakunin no tame, mibun shoumeisho o misete kudasai.","german":"Please show your ID card so we can verify your identity.","furigana":"ほんにんかくにん|みぶんしょうめいしょ|み"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"一人一人 (also written 一人ひとり) stresses \"each individual\" of a group as an individual: 一人一人の意見, 一人一人に配る. It often sounds appreciative, for example in speeches: 一人一人が気をつける. それぞれ \"respectively\" can also refer to things, 各自 is the formal \"each for himself/herself\", and 一人ずつ stresses the order \"one by one\".","examples":[{"japanese":"先生は一人一人に質問しました。","romaji":"Sensei wa hitorihitori ni shitsumon shimashita.","german":"The teacher asked each person a question.","furigana":"せんせい|ひとりひとり|しつもん"},{"japanese":"一人一人の意見を大切にします。","romaji":"Hitorihitori no iken o taisetsu ni shimasu.","german":"We value each individual's opinion.","furigana":"ひとりひとり|いけん|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"一人一人が気をつければ、事故は減るはずです。","romaji":"Hitorihitori ga ki o tsukereba, jiko wa heru hazu desu.","german":"If each person is careful, accidents should decrease.","furigana":"ひとりひとり|き|じこ|へ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"一言 means \"a word, a few words, a brief remark\". Common phrases: 一言で言うと／言えば \"in a word, in short\", 一言も話さない \"not to say a single word\", 一言お願いします \"please say a few words\" (a request for a short speech) and 一言多い \"always to say one word too many\". The reading is ひとこと; いちごん appears only in fixed expressions such as 一言一句.","examples":[{"japanese":"最後に一言お願いします。","romaji":"Saigo ni hitokoto onegai shimasu.","german":"Finally, please say a few words.","furigana":"さいご|ひとこと|ねが"},{"japanese":"彼女は何も言わず、一言だけ書きました。","romaji":"Kanojo wa nani mo iwazu, hitokoto dake kakimashita.","german":"She said nothing and wrote just one word.","furigana":"かのじょ|なに|い|ひとこと|か"},{"japanese":"一言で言えば、この映画は最高です。","romaji":"Hitokoto de ieba, kono eiga wa saikou desu.","german":"In a word, this movie is the best.","furigana":"ひとこと|い|えいが|さいこう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"出会い is the noun of 出会う \"to meet (by chance), to get to know\" and often means a meaningful encounter: 〜との出会い, 運命の出会い \"fateful encounter\", 出会いを大切にする. 出会いがない colloquially means \"I'm not meeting anyone (a partner)\". In a romantic context it is sometimes written 出逢い. 春は出会いと別れの季節 is a set saying.","examples":[{"japanese":"旅では新しい出会いがあります。","romaji":"Tabi de wa atarashii deai ga arimasu.","german":"On a trip, you have new encounters.","furigana":"たび|あたら|であ"},{"japanese":"先生との出会いが人生を変えました。","romaji":"Sensei to no deai ga jinsei o kaemashita.","german":"Meeting my teacher changed my life.","furigana":"せんせい|であ|じんせい|か"},{"japanese":"春は出会いと別れの季節です。","romaji":"Haru wa deai to wakare no kisetsu desu.","german":"Spring is the season of meetings and partings.","furigana":"はる|であ|わか|きせつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"記入 is the entering of information into forms, lists or diaries; as a transitive する-verb: 住所を用紙に記入する. Typical are ご記入ください, 記入例 \"filling-in example\", 記入漏れ \"missing entry\" and 記入欄 \"field to fill in\". 書く is generally \"to write\", 入力 refers to digital input, and 記載 to something being listed (formally) in documents.","examples":[{"japanese":"ここに住所を記入してください。","romaji":"Koko ni jūsho o kinyū shite kudasai.","german":"Please write your address here.","furigana":"じゅうしょ|きにゅう"},{"japanese":"記入した用紙を受付に出しました。","romaji":"Kinyū shita yōshi o uketsuke ni dashimashita.","german":"I handed the completed form in at the reception desk.","furigana":"きにゅう|ようし|うけつけ|だ"},{"japanese":"名前は必ずボールペンで記入してください。","romaji":"Namae wa kanarazu boorupen de kinyuu shite kudasai.","german":"Please be sure to write your name with a ballpoint pen.","furigana":"なまえ|かなら|きにゅう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"学習 is more matter-of-fact and formal than 勉強 and is often used in school, pedagogy and technology: 学習時間, 学習者 \"learner\", 学習塾 \"cram school\", 生涯学習 \"lifelong learning\". It can also refer to the learning of animals or machines: 機械学習 \"machine learning\". 勉強 stresses laborious cramming, and 習う learning from a teacher.","examples":[{"japanese":"毎日の学習時間を決めています。","romaji":"Mainichi no gakushū jikan o kimete imasu.","german":"I set a fixed study time for each day.","furigana":"まいにち|がくしゅうじかん|き"},{"japanese":"新しい単語を例文と一緒に学習します。","romaji":"Atarashii tango o reibun to issho ni gakushū shimasu.","german":"I study new words together with example sentences.","furigana":"あたら|たんご|れいぶん|いっしょ|がくしゅう"},{"japanese":"このロボットは、自分で学習してどんどん賢くなる。","romaji":"Kono robotto wa, jibun de gakushuu shite dondon kashikoku naru.","german":"This robot learns by itself and gets smarter and smarter.","furigana":"じぶん|がくしゅう|かしこ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"読書 is reading books as an activity or hobby; as an intransitive する-verb it is 読書する or 読書をする. It does not take an object with を: one says 本を読む, not ×本を読書する. Typical are 趣味は読書です, 読書の秋 (\"autumn as the reading season\") and 読書家 (\"avid reader\"). For reading newspapers, e-mails or signs, only 読む is used.","examples":[{"japanese":"趣味は読書です。","romaji":"Shumi wa dokusho desu.","german":"My hobby is reading.","furigana":"しゅみ|どくしょ"},{"japanese":"寝る前に三十分ほど読書します。","romaji":"Neru mae ni sanjuppun hodo dokusho shimasu.","german":"I read for about thirty minutes before going to bed.","furigana":"ね|まえ|さんじゅっぷん|どくしょ"},{"japanese":"電車の中で読書をする人が減りました。","romaji":"Densha no naka de dokusho o suru hito ga herimashita.","german":"The number of people who read books on the train has decreased.","furigana":"でんしゃ|なか|どくしょ|ひと|へ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"語学 refers to learning or studying (foreign) languages as a subject or skill, not the language itself - that is 言語 or 〜語. Common are 語学が得意だ (\"to be good at languages\"), 語学力 (\"language skills\"), 語学留学 (\"language study abroad\") and 語学学校 (\"language school\"). The scientific study of language is nowadays usually called 言語学.","examples":[{"japanese":"語学を学ぶために留学しました。","romaji":"Gogaku o manabu tame ni ryūgaku shimashita.","german":"I studied abroad in order to learn languages.","furigana":"ごがく|まな|りゅうがく"},{"japanese":"彼女は語学が得意です。","romaji":"Kanojo wa gogaku ga tokui desu.","german":"She is good at languages.","furigana":"かのじょ|ごがく|とくい"},{"japanese":"この仕事では高い語学力が必要です。","romaji":"Kono shigoto de wa takai gogakuryoku ga hitsuyou desu.","german":"This job requires strong language skills.","furigana":"しごと|たか|ごがくりょく|ひつよう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"外出 is leaving one's home or office to go somewhere; as an intransitive する-verb: 外出する. It sounds more matter-of-fact than 出かける and is typical in notices, phone calls and office life: 外出中 (\"currently out\"), 外出先 (\"where one is while out\"), 外出を控える (\"to refrain from unnecessary outings\"). In conversation among friends, 出かける is more common.","examples":[{"japanese":"外出する時は窓を閉めてください。","romaji":"Gaishutsu suru toki wa mado o shimete kudasai.","german":"When you go out, please close the windows.","furigana":"がいしゅつ|とき|まど|し"},{"japanese":"担当者はただ今、外出中です。","romaji":"Tantōsha wa tadaima, gaishutsuchū desu.","german":"The person in charge is out at the moment.","furigana":"たんとうしゃ|いま|がいしゅつちゅう"},{"japanese":"台風の日は外出を控えてください。","romaji":"Taifuu no hi wa gaishutsu o hikaete kudasai.","german":"On typhoon days, please refrain from going out.","furigana":"たいふう|ひ|がいしゅつ|ひか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"帰宅 is the return to one's own home; as an intransitive する-verb: 帰宅する. It is more matter-of-fact than 帰る and often appears in the news, at school and at work: 帰宅時間, 帰宅途中 (\"on the way home\"), 帰宅が遅くなる. It means only returning home - for returning to one's home country 帰国 is used, and for traveling to one's hometown 帰省.","examples":[{"japanese":"帰宅してから夕飯を作ります。","romaji":"Kitaku shite kara yūhan o tsukurimasu.","german":"I'll cook dinner after I get home.","furigana":"きたく|ゆうはん|つく"},{"japanese":"今日は帰宅が遅くなりそうです。","romaji":"Kyō wa kitaku ga osoku narisō desu.","german":"It looks like I'll be getting home late today.","furigana":"きょう|きたく|おそ"},{"japanese":"帰宅途中にスーパーに寄りました。","romaji":"Kitaku tochuu ni suupaa ni yorimashita.","german":"I stopped by the supermarket on the way home.","furigana":"きたくとちゅう|よ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"会合 is an arranged meeting of several people for an exchange or consultation, for example of neighbors, clubs or representatives. It sounds more formal than 集まり; 会議, by contrast, is a meeting with a fixed agenda, especially in companies. Typical are 会合を開く (\"to hold a meeting\"), 会合に出席する/参加する and 会合で話し合う.","examples":[{"japanese":"来週、地域の会合があります。","romaji":"Raishū, chiiki no kaigō ga arimasu.","german":"There is a community meeting next week.","furigana":"らいしゅう|ちいき|かいごう"},{"japanese":"会合で新しい計画について話しました。","romaji":"Kaigō de atarashii keikaku ni tsuite hanashimashita.","german":"We talked about the new plan at the meeting.","furigana":"かいごう|あたら|けいかく|はな"},{"japanese":"体調が悪くて、昨日の会合に出席できませんでした。","romaji":"Taichou ga warukute, kinou no kaigou ni shusseki dekimasen deshita.","german":"I felt unwell and couldn't attend yesterday's meeting.","furigana":"たいちょう|わる|きのう|かいごう|しゅっせき"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"昼食 is lunch in a matter-of-fact, written style - typical of schedules, menus and hotels. In daily life people say 昼ご飯, and casually also お昼 or ランチ. 昼食をとる goes especially well with 昼食; there are also 昼食会 and 昼食代. The parallel words are 朝食 and 夕食.","examples":[{"japanese":"昼食は十二時からです。","romaji":"Chūshoku wa jūniji kara desu.","german":"Lunch starts at twelve.","furigana":"ちゅうしょく|じゅうにじ"},{"japanese":"同僚と昼食を取りながら話しました。","romaji":"Dōryō to chūshoku o torinagara hanashimashita.","german":"I talked with a colleague while having lunch.","furigana":"どうりょう|ちゅうしょく|と|はな"},{"japanese":"研修中の昼食代は会社が払います。","romaji":"Kenshuuchuu no chuushokudai wa kaisha ga haraimasu.","german":"The company pays for lunch during the training.","furigana":"けんしゅうちゅう|ちゅうしょくだい|かいしゃ|はら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"正午 refers to exactly 12:00 noon, the boundary between 午前 and 午後. 昼, by contrast, means roughly the midday period or the whole daytime. The word is rather formal and appears in announcements, deadlines and the news: 正午に, 正午まで, 正午過ぎ, 正午のニュース.","examples":[{"japanese":"正午に駅の前で会いましょう。","romaji":"Shōgo ni eki no mae de aimashō.","german":"Let's meet in front of the station at noon.","furigana":"しょうご|えき|まえ|あ"},{"japanese":"受付は正午までです。","romaji":"Uketsuke wa shōgo made desu.","german":"Reception is open until noon.","furigana":"うけつけ|しょうご"},{"japanese":"正午を過ぎると、気温がさらに上がりました。","romaji":"Shougo o sugiru to, kion ga sara ni agarimashita.","german":"After noon, the temperature rose even more.","furigana":"しょうご|す|きおん|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"長期 means \"a long period\" and is mostly used attributively: 長期の計画, or directly in compounds such as 長期休暇, 長期入院, 長期滞在. Its counterpart is 短期, with 中期 in between. As an adjective, 長期的な/長期的に (\"long-term\") is used; the duration itself is 長期間 (\"over a long time\").","examples":[{"japanese":"長期の旅行を計画しています。","romaji":"Chōki no ryokō o keikaku shite imasu.","german":"I am planning a long trip.","furigana":"ちょうき|りょこう|けいかく"},{"japanese":"この問題は長期的に考える必要があります。","romaji":"Kono mondai wa chōkiteki ni kangaeru hitsuyō ga arimasu.","german":"This problem needs to be considered from a long-term perspective.","furigana":"もんだい|ちょうきてき|かんが|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"夏の長期休暇に家族で北海道へ行きます。","romaji":"Natsu no chouki kyuuka ni kazoku de Hokkaidou e ikimasu.","german":"In the long summer vacation, I'm going to Hokkaido with my family.","furigana":"なつ|ちょうききゅうか|かぞく|ほっかいどう|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"中学 is the usual short form of 中学校, the three-year Japanese junior high school (roughly grades 7-9). In conversation people almost always say 中学: 中学に入る, 中学の時, 中学の友達; the students are 中学生, and the grades are 中学一年 (short: 中一). It comes after 小学校 and before 高校.","examples":[{"japanese":"中学では英語を勉強しました。","romaji":"Chūgaku de wa eigo o benkyō shimashita.","german":"I studied English in junior high school.","furigana":"ちゅうがく|えいご|べんきょう"},{"japanese":"中学の友達と久しぶりに会いました。","romaji":"Chūgaku no tomodachi to hisashiburi ni aimashita.","german":"I met a friend from junior high school for the first time in a long while.","furigana":"ちゅうがく|ともだち|ひさ|あ"},{"japanese":"息子は今年の四月に中学に入りました。","romaji":"Musuko wa kotoshi no shigatsu ni chuugaku ni hairimashita.","german":"My son entered junior high school this April.","furigana":"むすこ|ことし|しがつ|ちゅうがく|はい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"大部分 refers to by far the largest part of a whole, mostly in the patterns 〜の大部分は/が or 大部分の + noun. It is somewhat more matter-of-fact than ほとんど, which is more common in everyday speech and also works as an adverb before verbs (ほとんど終わった). 大半 is close in meaning but tends to stress \"more than half\".","examples":[{"japanese":"仕事の大部分は終わりました。","romaji":"Shigoto no daibubun wa owarimashita.","german":"Most of the work is finished.","furigana":"しごと|だいぶぶん|お"},{"japanese":"参加者の大部分は学生でした。","romaji":"Sankasha no daibubun wa gakusei deshita.","german":"Most of the participants were students.","furigana":"さんかしゃ|だいぶぶん|がくせい"},{"japanese":"この辺りでは、大部分の店が日曜日も開いています。","romaji":"Kono atari de wa, daibubun no mise ga nichiyoubi mo aite imasu.","german":"In this area, most shops are open on Sundays too.","furigana":"あた|だいぶぶん|みせ|にちようび|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"大半 literally means \"big half\", i.e. more than half up to almost all: 〜の大半, 大半が/は, 大半を占める \"to make up the majority\". It is somewhat more written in style than ほとんど and is close to 大部分, which tends to stress \"almost all\". As an adverb (\"for the most part\"), 大半 is rarer and rather written.","examples":[{"japanese":"休日の大半を読書に使いました。","romaji":"Kyūjitsu no taihan o dokusho ni tsukaimashita.","german":"I spent most of my day off reading.","furigana":"きゅうじつ|たいはん|どくしょ|つか"},{"japanese":"会場の席は大半が埋まっています。","romaji":"Kaijō no seki wa taihan ga umatte imasu.","german":"Most of the seats in the hall are already filled.","furigana":"かいじょう|せき|たいはん|う"},{"japanese":"給料の大半が家賃に消えてしまいます。","romaji":"Kyuuryou no taihan ga yachin ni kiete shimaimasu.","german":"Most of my salary disappears into rent.","furigana":"きゅうりょう|たいはん|やちん|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"同一 means \"one and the same, identical\" and is the formal, written counterpart of 同じ. It usually appears as 同一の before a noun or in fixed combinations such as 同一人物 \"the same person\" and 同一視する \"to equate\". It is typical in forms, rules, science and the news; in conversation people say 同じ.","examples":[{"japanese":"二つの書類には同一の番号が付いています。","romaji":"Futatsu no shorui ni wa dōitsu no bangō ga tsuite imasu.","german":"The two documents bear the same number.","furigana":"ふた|しょるい|どういつ|ばんごう|つ"},{"japanese":"同一人物だとは気付きませんでした。","romaji":"Dōitsu jinbutsu da to wa kizukimasen deshita.","german":"I didn't realize it was the same person.","furigana":"どういつじんぶつ|きづ"},{"japanese":"同一の条件で実験をもう一度行いました。","romaji":"Douitsu no jouken de jikken o mou ichido okonaimashita.","german":"I conducted the experiment once more under the same conditions.","furigana":"どういつ|じょうけん|じっけん|いちどおこな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"不利 is a な-adjective (also a noun) for an unfavorable, disadvantageous position in a competition or a decision; the opposite is 有利. Typical are 〜に不利だ \"disadvantageous for someone\", 不利になる, 不利な条件/立場. Not to be confused with 不便 \"inconvenient\": 不利 concerns chances and prospects of success.","examples":[{"japanese":"雨はこのチームに不利です。","romaji":"Ame wa kono chīmu ni furi desu.","german":"Rain is unfavorable for this team.","furigana":"あめ|ふり"},{"japanese":"不利な条件でも諦めませんでした。","romaji":"Furi na jōken de mo akiramemasen deshita.","german":"He didn't give up even under unfavorable conditions.","furigana":"ふり|じょうけん|あきら"},{"japanese":"経験がないと、就職に不利になりますか。","romaji":"Keiken ga nai to, shuushoku ni furi ni narimasu ka.","german":"Is it a disadvantage in job hunting if you have no experience?","furigana":"けいけん|しゅうしょく|ふり"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"地平線 is the horizon line where land and sky seem to touch. Over the sea, 水平線 is used. Typical are 地平線の向こう, 地平線まで続く \"to stretch to the horizon\" and 地平線から日が昇る/地平線に沈む. The word sounds somewhat descriptive and literary and suits wide plains, deserts and steppes.","examples":[{"japanese":"地平線の向こうに日が沈みました。","romaji":"Chiheisen no mukō ni hi ga shizumimashita.","german":"The sun set beyond the horizon.","furigana":"ちへいせん|む|ひ|しず"},{"japanese":"広い平野では地平線まで見渡せます。","romaji":"Hiroi heiya de wa chiheisen made miwatasemasu.","german":"On a wide plain, you can see all the way to the horizon.","furigana":"ひろ|へいや|ちへいせん|みわた"},{"japanese":"砂漠の朝、地平線から太陽が昇ってきました。","romaji":"Sabaku no asa, chiheisen kara taiyou ga nobotte kimashita.","german":"On a desert morning, the sun rose from the horizon.","furigana":"さばく|あさ|ちへいせん|たいよう|のぼ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"国家 is the state as a political organization with a government and laws; in daily life, \"country\" is simply 国. It often appears in matter-of-fact compounds: 国家試験 \"state examination\", 国家公務員 \"national civil servant\", 国家予算 \"national budget\". For the population of a country, 国民 is used.","examples":[{"japanese":"教育は国家の大切な役割です。","romaji":"Kyōiku wa kokka no taisetsu na yakuwari desu.","german":"Education is an important role of the state.","furigana":"きょういく|こっか|たいせつ|やくわり"},{"japanese":"国家と個人の関係について学びました。","romaji":"Kokka to kojin no kankei ni tsuite manabimashita.","german":"I learned about the relationship between the state and the individual.","furigana":"こっか|こじん|かんけい|まな"},{"japanese":"姉は国家試験に合格して看護師になりました。","romaji":"Ane wa kokka shiken ni goukaku shite kangoshi ni narimashita.","german":"My older sister passed the national exam and became a nurse.","furigana":"あね|こっかしけん|ごうかく|かんごし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"あらゆる is a prenominal word (連体詞): it always stands directly before a noun, never with の and never as a predicate. It stresses \"every conceivable kind of\" and sounds more emphatic than すべての: あらゆる方法, あらゆる手段, あらゆる角度から. It is almost always written in kana.","examples":[{"japanese":"あらゆる方法を試しました。","romaji":"Arayuru hōhō o tameshimashita.","german":"I tried every possible method.","furigana":"ほうほう|ため"},{"japanese":"あらゆる年齢の人が参加できます。","romaji":"Arayuru nenrei no hito ga sanka dekimasu.","german":"People of all ages can take part.","furigana":"ねんれい|ひと|さんか"},{"japanese":"この店ではあらゆる種類のお茶が買えます。","romaji":"Kono mise de wa arayuru shurui no ocha ga kaemasu.","german":"This shop sells every kind of tea.","furigana":"みせ|しゅるい|ちゃ|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"単なる is a prenominal word (連体詞) and stands directly before a noun: 単なる偶然, 単なるうわさ, 単なる間違い. It is often followed by 〜にすぎない \"is nothing more than\", or negated (単なる〜ではない). In daily speech the more casual ただの is used instead; the corresponding adverb is 単に \"merely\".","examples":[{"japanese":"これは単なる間違いではありません。","romaji":"Kore wa tannaru machigai de wa arimasen.","german":"This is not a mere mistake.","furigana":"たん|まちが"},{"japanese":"最初は単なる趣味でした。","romaji":"Saisho wa tannaru shumi deshita.","german":"At first it was just a hobby.","furigana":"さいしょ|たん|しゅみ"},{"japanese":"それは単なるうわさにすぎないから、気にしないで。","romaji":"Sore wa tannaru uwasa ni suginai kara, ki ni shinaide.","german":"That is nothing more than a rumor, so don't worry about it.","furigana":"たん|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"一種 has two typical patterns: 〜の一種 assigns something to a group (\"a kind of, a type of\"), while 一種の + noun serves as a cautious paraphrase (\"a sort of ..., in a way a ...\"). There is also the set phrase 一種独特の \"quite peculiar\". For \"kind, sort\" as a counter, 種類 is used instead (三種類).","examples":[{"japanese":"これはハーブの一種です。","romaji":"Kore wa hābu no isshu desu.","german":"This is a kind of herb.","furigana":"いっしゅ"},{"japanese":"読書も一種の旅だと思います。","romaji":"Dokusho mo isshu no tabi da to omoimasu.","german":"I think reading is also a kind of journey.","furigana":"どくしょ|いっしゅ|たび|おも"},{"japanese":"このお茶には一種独特の香りがあります。","romaji":"Kono ocha ni wa isshu dokutoku no kaori ga arimasu.","german":"This tea has a certain unique fragrance.","furigana":"ちゃ|いっしゅどくとく|かお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"一家 is the whole family as a unit, or the household; it sounds somewhat more formal than 家族. Typical are 一家で \"as a whole family\", 一家そろって \"the whole family together\", 一家の主 \"head of the family\" and surname + 一家 (田中さん一家). With the names of groups, 〜一家 can also refer to a yakuza family.","examples":[{"japanese":"一家で田舎に引っ越しました。","romaji":"Ikka de inaka ni hikkoshimashita.","german":"The whole family moved to the countryside.","furigana":"いっか|いなか|ひ|こ"},{"japanese":"その店は一家で経営しています。","romaji":"Sono mise wa ikka de keiei shite imasu.","german":"The whole family runs that shop.","furigana":"みせ|いっか|けいえい"},{"japanese":"お正月は一家そろって祖父の家に集まります。","romaji":"Oshougatsu wa ikka sorotte sofu no ie ni atsumarimasu.","german":"At New Year, the whole family gathers at my grandfather's house.","furigana":"しょうがつ|いっか|そふ|いえ|あつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"今にも describes that something appears to be imminent, and it almost always occurs with 〜そうだ or 〜ばかりに: 今にも雨が降りそうだ, 今にも泣き出しそうな顔. It does not express intention – for \"I'm going soon\" one says すぐ行く or もうすぐ, not ×今にも行く. It is often about something threatening or emotionally charged.","examples":[{"japanese":"今にも雨が降りそうです。","romaji":"Ima ni mo ame ga furisō desu.","german":"It looks like it could rain at any moment.","furigana":"いま|あめ|ふ"},{"japanese":"子供は今にも泣き出しそうでした。","romaji":"Kodomo wa ima ni mo nakidashisō deshita.","german":"The child looked about to burst into tears.","furigana":"こども|いま|な|だ"},{"japanese":"今にも壊れそうな古い橋を渡りました。","romaji":"Ima ni mo kowaresou na furui hashi o watarimashita.","german":"We crossed an old bridge that looked as if it might collapse at any moment.","furigana":"いま|こわ|ふる|はし|わた"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 43"},
{"notes":"少々 means \"a little\" or \"a moment\" and is the polite, formal equivalent of 少し or the casual ちょっと. The fixed phrase 少々お待ちください is very common in customer service and on the phone. In recipes, 塩少々 stands for \"a pinch of salt\"; 少々お時間をいただけますか is also typical of polite requests.","examples":[{"japanese":"少々お待ちください。","romaji":"Shōshō omachi kudasai.","german":"Please wait a moment.","furigana":"しょうしょう|ま"},{"japanese":"塩を少々加えます。","romaji":"Shio o shōshō kuwaemasu.","german":"I add a little salt.","furigana":"しお|しょうしょうくわ"},{"japanese":"すみません、少々お時間をいただけますか。","romaji":"Sumimasen, shoushou ojikan o itadakemasu ka.","german":"Excuse me, could I take a moment of your time?","furigana":"しょうしょう|じかん"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 20"},
{"notes":"少しも is always used with a negative and means \"not a bit, not at all\": 少しも〜ない. It is neutral to slightly formal; colloquially people say ちっとも, and quite generally also 全然 or 全く. Without a negative it is ungrammatical - for \"a little\" only 少し is used.","examples":[{"japanese":"昨日は少しも眠れませんでした。","romaji":"Kinō wa sukoshi mo nemuremasen deshita.","german":"I couldn't sleep at all last night.","furigana":"きのう|すこ|ねむ"},{"japanese":"説明を聞いても少しも分かりません。","romaji":"Setsumei o kiite mo sukoshi mo wakarimasen.","german":"Even after hearing the explanation, I don't understand at all.","furigana":"せつめい|き|すこ|わ"},{"japanese":"長い旅の後でも、彼は少しも疲れた様子を見せませんでした。","romaji":"Nagai tabi no ato de mo, kare wa sukoshi mo tsukareta yousu o misemasen deshita.","german":"Even after the long journey, he showed no sign of being tired.","furigana":"なが|たび|あと|かれ|すこ|つか|ようす|み"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"食品 is the factual collective term for food as products – typical in retail, on labels and in the news. In everyday speech one says 食べ物; 食料, by contrast, emphasizes food as a supply. Common compounds are 冷凍食品 (\"frozen food\"), 食品売り場 (\"food department\"), 健康食品 and 食品ロス (\"food waste\").","examples":[{"japanese":"食品は涼しい所に置いてください。","romaji":"Shokuhin wa suzushii tokoro ni oite kudasai.","german":"Please store food in a cool place.","furigana":"しょくひん|すず|ところ|お"},{"japanese":"この店では外国の食品も売っています。","romaji":"Kono mise de wa gaikoku no shokuhin mo utte imasu.","german":"This store also sells foreign foods.","furigana":"みせ|がいこく|しょくひん|う"},{"japanese":"忙しい日は冷凍食品をよく使います。","romaji":"Isogashii hi wa reitou shokuhin o yoku tsukaimasu.","german":"On busy days I often use frozen food.","furigana":"いそが|ひ|れいとうしょくひん|つか"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 42"},
{"notes":"書物 is an elevated, rather literary word for \"book\", especially for old, valuable or scholarly works. In daily life people say 本; 書籍 is the matter-of-fact term from publishing and the book trade. Typical are 古い書物, 書物を読む, 書物を通して \"through books\".","examples":[{"japanese":"図書館には古い書物が残っています。","romaji":"Toshokan ni wa furui shomotsu ga nokotte imasu.","german":"Old books remain in the library.","furigana":"としょかん|ふる|しょもつ|のこ"},{"japanese":"書物を通して昔の生活を知ることができます。","romaji":"Shomotsu o tōshite mukashi no seikatsu o shiru koto ga dekimasu.","german":"Through books, we can learn about life in the past.","furigana":"しょもつ|とお|むかし|せいかつ|し"},{"japanese":"祖父の部屋には難しい書物がたくさん並んでいます。","romaji":"Sofu no heya ni wa muzukashii shomotsu ga takusan narande imasu.","german":"Lots of difficult books are lined up in my grandfather's room.","furigana":"そふ|へや|むずか|しょもつ|なら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"急激 is a な-adjective for a change that is both sudden and strong: 急激に増える/減る/下がる, 急激な変化. It is used especially with numbers and developments such as temperature, prices or population. 急 is more general (\"sudden\"), 急速 stresses high speed, and 突然 only the unexpectedness of an event.","examples":[{"japanese":"気温が急激に下がりました。","romaji":"Kion ga kyūgeki ni sagarimashita.","german":"The temperature dropped sharply.","furigana":"きおん|きゅうげき|さ"},{"japanese":"急激な変化に対応するのは難しいです。","romaji":"Kyūgeki na henka ni taiō suru no wa muzukashii desu.","german":"It is difficult to cope with abrupt changes.","furigana":"きゅうげき|へんか|たいおう|むずか"},{"japanese":"最近、この町の人口が急激に増えています。","romaji":"Saikin, kono machi no jinkou ga kyuugeki ni fuete imasu.","german":"Recently, the population of this town has been increasing rapidly.","furigana":"さいきん|まち|じんこう|きゅうげき|ふ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"現状 is the present state of a thing or situation, often in work, politics and the news. Typical are 現状を把握する \"to grasp the situation\", 現状に満足する, 現状維持 \"maintaining the status quo\" and 現状では \"under the present circumstances\". 状況 stresses more a developing situation, and 現在 only the point in time \"now\".","examples":[{"japanese":"まず現状を確認しましょう。","romaji":"Mazu genjō o kakunin shimashō.","german":"First, let's check the current situation.","furigana":"げんじょう|かくにん"},{"japanese":"現状では計画を変える必要があります。","romaji":"Genjō de wa keikaku o kaeru hitsuyō ga arimasu.","german":"Under the present circumstances, we need to change the plan.","furigana":"げんじょう|けいかく|か|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"彼は今の給料に満足せず、現状を変えたいと思っています。","romaji":"Kare wa ima no kyuuryou ni manzoku sezu, genjou o kaetai to omotte imasu.","german":"He is not satisfied with his current salary and wants to change his situation.","furigana":"かれ|いま|きゅうりょう|まんぞく|げんじょう|か|おも"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"見解 is a considered, reasoned view, especially of experts, authorities or organizations. It is more formal than 意見 \"opinion\" and even more so than 考え. Typical are 見解を述べる/示す/発表する, 見解が分かれる \"opinions are divided\", 見解が一致する and 公式の見解 \"official statement\".","examples":[{"japanese":"この問題について専門家の見解を聞きました。","romaji":"Kono mondai ni tsuite senmonka no kenkai o kikimashita.","german":"I heard the experts' views on this issue.","furigana":"もんだい|せんもんか|けんかい|き"},{"japanese":"二人の見解は大きく異なります。","romaji":"Futari no kenkai wa ōkiku kotonarimasu.","german":"The two of them hold very different views.","furigana":"ふたり|けんかい|おお|こと"},{"japanese":"会社は事故について公式の見解を発表しました。","romaji":"Kaisha wa jiko ni tsuite koushiki no kenkai o happyou shimashita.","german":"The company announced its official position on the accident.","furigana":"かいしゃ|じこ|こうしき|けんかい|はっぴょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"候補 is a person or thing that comes into question for an election or selection: 〜の候補, 候補に挙がる \"to come into consideration\", 候補に入れる. In elections 候補者 is mostly used, and \"to run as a candidate\" is 立候補する. In sports, 優勝候補 is the title favorite; travel destinations, dates or names can also be 候補.","examples":[{"japanese":"旅行先の候補を三つ選びました。","romaji":"Ryokōsaki no kōho o mittsu erabimashita.","german":"I picked three candidates for the travel destination.","furigana":"りょこうさき|こうほ|みっ|えら"},{"japanese":"彼は次の部長の候補です。","romaji":"Kare wa tsugi no buchō no kōho desu.","german":"He is a candidate for the next department head.","furigana":"かれ|つぎ|ぶちょう|こうほ"},{"japanese":"このチームは今年の優勝候補です。","romaji":"Kono chiimu wa kotoshi no yuushou kouho desu.","german":"This team is a favorite to win the championship this year.","furigana":"ことし|ゆうしょうこうほ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"参考 is something you consult for orientation or as a point of reference. The main patterns are 〜を参考にする \"to take something as a guide\" and 〜が参考になる \"to be helpful, informative\"; politely and casually one says 参考までに \"just for your information\". Compounds are 参考書 \"study guide, reference book\" and 参考資料.","examples":[{"japanese":"先輩の意見を参考にしました。","romaji":"Senpai no iken o sankō ni shimashita.","german":"I used my senior's opinion as a reference.","furigana":"せんぱい|いけん|さんこう"},{"japanese":"この資料は参考になります。","romaji":"Kono shiryō wa sankō ni narimasu.","german":"This material is helpful.","furigana":"しりょう|さんこう"},{"japanese":"参考までに、去年の発表の資料もお送りします。","romaji":"Sankou made ni, kyonen no happyou no shiryou mo ookuri shimasu.","german":"For your reference, I will also send you the materials from last year's presentation.","furigana":"さんこう|きょねん|はっぴょう|しりょう|おく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"事態 is a (mostly serious or escalating) state of affairs, especially in the news, politics and business: 事態が悪化する \"the situation worsens\", 最悪の事態, 緊急事態 \"state of emergency\", 事態を見守る. 状況 is more neutral and general; 状態 describes the condition of a person or thing (体の状態).","examples":[{"japanese":"事態は予想より深刻でした。","romaji":"Jitai wa yosō yori shinkoku deshita.","german":"The situation was more serious than expected.","furigana":"じたい|よそう|しんこく"},{"japanese":"事態が変われば連絡します。","romaji":"Jitai ga kawareba renraku shimasu.","german":"I will contact you if the situation changes.","furigana":"じたい|か|れんらく"},{"japanese":"最悪の事態にならないよう、早めに準備しておきましょう。","romaji":"Saiaku no jitai ni naranai you, hayame ni junbi shite okimashou.","german":"Let's prepare early so that the worst doesn't happen.","furigana":"さいあく|じたい|はや|じゅんび"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"実行 means to actually put a plan or resolution into practice; as a transitive する-verb: 計画を実行する, passive 実行される. Typical are 実行に移す \"to put into action\" and 実行力 \"ability to act\". 実施 sounds more official (carrying out exams, measures), and 実現 means realizing a goal or dream. In IT, 実行 means \"to run\" (a program).","examples":[{"japanese":"計画を実行する時が来ました。","romaji":"Keikaku o jikkō suru toki ga kimashita.","german":"The time has come to carry out the plan.","furigana":"けいかく|じっこう|とき|き"},{"japanese":"言うだけでなく実行することが大切です。","romaji":"Iu dake de naku jikkō suru koto ga taisetsu desu.","german":"It is important not only to talk but to act.","furigana":"い|じっこう|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"このボタンを押すと、プログラムが実行されます。","romaji":"Kono botan o osu to, puroguramu ga jikkou saremasu.","german":"When you press this button, the program is executed.","furigana":"お|じっこう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"実は introduces information that the other person doesn't know yet: a confession, a surprise or a request (\"I have to tell you something...\"). Very typical is 実は、お願いがあるんですが… as a preparation for a request. 本当は, by contrast, stresses the contrast with reality (\"actually I wanted to...\"), and 実際 means \"in practice, in fact\".","examples":[{"japanese":"実は、来月引っ越すことになりました。","romaji":"Jitsu wa, raigetsu hikkosu koto ni narimashita.","german":"Actually, I'm going to be moving next month.","furigana":"じつ|らいげつひ|こ"},{"japanese":"実は、その映画はもう見ました。","romaji":"Jitsu wa, sono eiga wa mō mimashita.","german":"To be honest, I've already seen that movie.","furigana":"じつ|えいが|み"},{"japanese":"実は、ちょっとお願いがあるんですが…。","romaji":"Jitsu wa, chotto onegai ga arun desu ga...","german":"Actually, I have a small favor to ask...","furigana":"じつ|ねが"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 19"},
{"notes":"重大 is a な-adjective for things with serious consequences: 重大な問題, 重大なミス, 重大な責任, 重大な決定. It stresses the weight of the consequences and often sounds serious or threatening. 重要, by contrast, is neutral \"important\" (重要な会議), while 深刻 describes a serious, worrying situation.","examples":[{"japanese":"重大な間違いに気付きました。","romaji":"Jūdai na machigai ni kizukimashita.","german":"I noticed a serious mistake.","furigana":"じゅうだい|まちが|きづ"},{"japanese":"これは将来に関わる重大な決定です。","romaji":"Kore wa shōrai ni kakawaru jūdai na kettei desu.","german":"This is an important decision that concerns the future.","furigana":"しょうらい|かか|じゅうだい|けってい"},{"japanese":"社長から全員に重大な発表があるそうです。","romaji":"Shachou kara zen'in ni juudai na happyou ga aru sou desu.","german":"I hear the president is going to make a major announcement to everyone.","furigana":"しゃちょう|ぜんいん|じゅうだい|はっぴょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"順調 is a な-adjective for a problem-free course that goes as planned: 順調に進む/いく, 準備は順調だ, 順調な回復. It suits work, projects, recovery and business. Similar is スムーズ, which stresses the smoothness of a process; 予定通り means only \"as planned\".","examples":[{"japanese":"仕事は順調に進んでいます。","romaji":"Shigoto wa junchō ni susunde imasu.","german":"The work is progressing smoothly.","furigana":"しごと|じゅんちょう|すす"},{"japanese":"今のところ、準備は順調です。","romaji":"Ima no tokoro, junbi wa junchō desu.","german":"So far, the preparations are going well.","furigana":"いま|じゅんび|じゅんちょう"},{"japanese":"手術の後、父の回復は順調です。","romaji":"Shujutsu no ato, chichi no kaifuku wa junchou desu.","german":"After the surgery, my father's recovery is going well.","furigana":"しゅじゅつ|あと|ちち|かいふく|じゅんちょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"正式 means \"official, formally correct\", i.e. in accordance with the prescribed procedure: 正式な返事, 正式に決まる/発表する, 正式名称 \"full, official name\". The opposite is 非公式 or 略式 \"simplified\". 公式 is close in meaning but tends to refer to what has been made public (公式サイト, 公式発表).","examples":[{"japanese":"正式な返事は来週届きます。","romaji":"Seishiki na henji wa raishū todokimasu.","german":"The official reply will arrive next week.","furigana":"せいしき|へんじ|らいしゅうとど"},{"japanese":"新しい名前が正式に決まりました。","romaji":"Atarashii namae ga seishiki ni kimarimashita.","german":"The new name has been officially decided.","furigana":"あたら|なまえ|せいしき|き"},{"japanese":"書類には会社の正式な名前を書いてください。","romaji":"Shorui ni wa kaisha no seishiki na namae o kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write the company's official name on the document.","furigana":"しょるい|かいしゃ|せいしき|なまえ|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"代表 is a representative or a representation; 〜を代表して means \"on behalf of\" (used as a transitive する-verb). The second important meaning: 〜を代表する + noun or 代表的な \"typical, representative of\", for example 日本を代表する料理. Other forms are 代表者, 代表選手 and 日本代表 \"the national team\".","examples":[{"japanese":"クラスの代表を選びました。","romaji":"Kurasu no daihyō o erabimashita.","german":"We chose the class representative.","furigana":"だいひょう|えら"},{"japanese":"会社を代表して挨拶しました。","romaji":"Kaisha o daihyō shite aisatsu shimashita.","german":"I gave a greeting on behalf of the company.","furigana":"かいしゃ|だいひょう|あいさつ"},{"japanese":"寿司は日本を代表する料理の一つです。","romaji":"Sushi wa Nihon o daihyou suru ryouri no hitotsu desu.","german":"Sushi is one of the dishes that represent Japan.","furigana":"すし|にほん|だいひょう|りょうり|ひと"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"中心 is the central point in both the spatial and the figurative sense: 町の中心, 円の中心, 話題の中心. Very common is 〜を中心に(して) \"centering on, around\", plus 中心となる and the suffix 〜中心 (家族中心の生活). 真ん中 is the everyday word for the physical middle, while 中央 sounds more official (中央駅).","examples":[{"japanese":"町の中心に大きな公園があります。","romaji":"Machi no chūshin ni ōkina kōen ga arimasu.","german":"There is a big park in the center of town.","furigana":"まち|ちゅうしん|おお|こうえん"},{"japanese":"会話を中心に日本語を学んでいます。","romaji":"Kaiwa o chūshin ni nihongo o manande imasu.","german":"I am studying Japanese with a focus on conversation.","furigana":"かいわ|ちゅうしん|にほんご|まな"},{"japanese":"彼女はいつもクラスの話題の中心にいます。","romaji":"Kanojo wa itsumo kurasu no wadai no chuushin ni imasu.","german":"She is always at the center of the class's conversation.","furigana":"かのじょ|わだい|ちゅうしん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"出来事 is something that has happened – neutral as to whether it is big or small, good or bad; often also written in kana, できごと. Typical are 今日の出来事, 不思議な出来事, 一番うれしかった出来事. 事件, by contrast, means an incident or crime, 事故 an accident, and 行事 and イベント a planned event.","examples":[{"japanese":"今日の出来事を日記に書きました。","romaji":"Kyō no dekigoto o nikki ni kakimashita.","german":"I wrote the events of the day in my diary.","furigana":"きょう|できごと|にっき|か"},{"japanese":"その出来事は今でも覚えています。","romaji":"Sono dekigoto wa ima de mo oboete imasu.","german":"I still remember that event today.","furigana":"できごと|いま|おぼ"},{"japanese":"去年一番うれしかった出来事は何ですか。","romaji":"Kyonen ichiban ureshikatta dekigoto wa nan desu ka.","german":"What was the happiest event of last year for you?","furigana":"きょねんいちばん|できごと|なん"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 46"},
{"notes":"問い is a question in a written or reflective sense: exam questions (次の問いに答えなさい) or fundamental questions (〜という問い). For a question you ask a person, everyday speech uses 質問; 問題 is a problem to solve or an issue. The related verb is 問う \"to ask, to question\".","examples":[{"japanese":"次の問いに答えてください。","romaji":"Tsugi no toi ni kotaete kudasai.","german":"Please answer the following questions.","furigana":"つぎ|と|こた"},{"japanese":"その問いには簡単に答えられません。","romaji":"Sono toi ni wa kantan ni kotaeraremasen.","german":"I can't answer that question easily.","furigana":"と|かんたん|こた"},{"japanese":"授業で「幸せとは何か」という問いについて考えました。","romaji":"Jugyou de \"shiawase to wa nani ka\" to iu toi ni tsuite kangaemashita.","german":"In class, we thought about the question \"What is happiness?\"","furigana":"じゅぎょう|しあわ|なに|と|かんが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"年間 has two uses: Alone before numbers or nouns it means \"yearly, per year\" (年間百冊, 年間計画, 年間を通して \"all year round\"). After a number, 〜年間 gives a duration: 三年間 \"for three years\", as opposed to 三年 as a mere year count. With eras, it stands for \"during the period\" (明治年間).","examples":[{"japanese":"年間で百冊ほど本を読みます。","romaji":"Nenkan de hyakusatsu hodo hon o yomimasu.","german":"I read about a hundred books a year.","furigana":"ねんかん|ひゃくさつ|ほん|よ"},{"japanese":"三年間、日本に住んでいました。","romaji":"Sannenkan, Nihon ni sunde imashita.","german":"I lived in Japan for three years.","furigana":"さんねんかん|にほん|す"},{"japanese":"この町には年間を通して多くの観光客が来ます。","romaji":"Kono machi ni wa nenkan o tooshite ooku no kankoukyaku ga kimasu.","german":"Many tourists come to this town throughout the year.","furigana":"まち|ねんかん|とお|おお|かんこうきゃく|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"年代 refers to a period of time. With a year number it stands for a decade: 1990年代 \"the 1990s\", 八十年代の音楽. For people, it means the age group: 同じ年代の人, 幅広い年代 - similar to 世代 \"generation\", which however stresses a shared formative background more. For historical eras such as the Edo period, 時代 is used. Set phrases are 年代順 \"in chronological order\" and 年代物 \"old, valuable item\".","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"この建物は1980年代に建てられました。","romaji":"Kono tatemono wa sen kyūhyaku hachijū nendai ni tateraremashita.","german":"This building was built in the 1980s.","furigana":"たてもの|ねんだい|た"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"幅広い年代の人が集まりました。","romaji":"Habahiroi nendai no hito ga atsumarimashita.","german":"People of a wide range of ages gathered.","furigana":"はばひろ|ねんだい|ひと|あつ"},{"japanese":"写真を年代順に並べて、アルバムを作った。","romaji":"Shashin o nendaijun ni narabete, arubamu o tsukutta.","german":"I arranged the photos in chronological order and made an album.","furigana":"しゃしん|ねんだいじゅん|なら|つく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"日付 is the calendar date, for example on documents, letters or receipts: 日付を書く/入れる, 日付順 \"sorted by date\". 日付が変わる means \"to pass midnight\". For the number of days or an appointed day, 日にち or 日数 is more common; it is sometimes written 日付け.","examples":[{"japanese":"書類に今日の日付を書いてください。","romaji":"Shorui ni kyō no hizuke o kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write today's date on the document.","furigana":"しょるい|きょう|ひづけ|か"},{"japanese":"日付を一日間違えていました。","romaji":"Hizuke o ichinichi machigaete imashita.","german":"I got the date wrong by one day.","furigana":"ひづけ|いちにちまちが"},{"japanese":"仕事が長引いて、日付が変わる頃に家に着きました。","romaji":"Shigoto ga nagabiite, hizuke ga kawaru koro ni ie ni tsukimashita.","german":"The work dragged on, and I got home around the time the date changed.","furigana":"しごと|ながび|ひづけ|か|ころ|いえ|つ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"表面 is the surface or outer side of an object (机の表面, 月の表面) and, figuratively, the outward appearance. Common are 表面上 \"outwardly\", 表面的な \"superficial\" and 表面化する \"to come to the surface\". The opposite is 内面, or 裏面 for paper; 表 refers more to the front side.","examples":[{"japanese":"机の表面をきれいに拭きました。","romaji":"Tsukue no hyōmen o kirei ni fukimashita.","german":"I wiped the surface of the desk clean.","furigana":"つくえ|ひょうめん|ふ"},{"japanese":"表面だけでは本当の問題は分かりません。","romaji":"Hyōmen dake de wa hontō no mondai wa wakarimasen.","german":"You can't tell the real problem from the surface alone.","furigana":"ひょうめん|ほんとう|もんだい|わ"},{"japanese":"あの二人は表面上は仲が良さそうに見えます。","romaji":"Ano futari wa hyoumenjou wa naka ga yosasou ni miemasu.","german":"Those two seem to get along well on the surface.","furigana":"ふたり|ひょうめんじょう|なか|よ|み"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"人物 is a person seen in terms of their role, importance or character; it sounds more matter-of-fact than 人. Typical are 登場人物 \"characters in a story\", 重要人物, 危険人物 and 写真の人物. As an evaluation, 〜な人物だ means \"to be a ... kind of person\"; 人間, by contrast, stresses the human being as a creature.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は信頼できる人物です。","romaji":"Kare wa shinrai dekiru jinbutsu desu.","german":"He is a trustworthy person.","furigana":"かれ|しんらい|じんぶつ"},{"japanese":"物語に出てくる人物を紹介します。","romaji":"Monogatari ni dete kuru jinbutsu o shōkai shimasu.","german":"I will introduce the characters who appear in the story.","furigana":"ものがたり|で|じんぶつ|しょうかい"},{"japanese":"この写真の真ん中の人物は誰ですか。","romaji":"Kono shashin no mannaka no jinbutsu wa dare desu ka.","german":"Who is the person in the middle of this photo?","furigana":"しゃしん|ま|なか|じんぶつ|だれ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"姿 is the visible figure or appearance, especially of people: 姿を見せる/現す (\"to appear\"), 姿を消す (\"to disappear\"), 姿が見えない, 後ろ姿 (\"view from behind\"). As a suffix, 〜姿 describes clothing: 着物姿, 制服姿. 形, by contrast, is the shape of objects, and 格好 is more one's get-up and clothing.","examples":[{"japanese":"駅で友達の姿を見つけました。","romaji":"Eki de tomodachi no sugata o mitsukemashita.","german":"I spotted my friend at the station.","furigana":"えき|ともだち|すがた|み"},{"japanese":"子供が一生懸命学ぶ姿に感動しました。","romaji":"Kodomo ga isshōkenmei manabu sugata ni kandō shimashita.","german":"I was moved by the sight of the child studying so hard.","furigana":"こども|いっしょうけんめいまな|すがた|かんどう"},{"japanese":"お祭りには着物姿の人がたくさんいました。","romaji":"Omatsuri ni wa kimono sugata no hito ga takusan imashita.","german":"There were many people in kimono at the festival.","furigana":"まつ|きものすがた|ひと"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 48"},
{"notes":"命 ist das Leben als Lebenskraft eines Lebewesens: 命を守る/救う, 命を落とす („ums Leben kommen“), 命の恩人 („Lebensretter“), 命がけで („unter Einsatz des Lebens“). Übertragen heißt 〜が命だ „… ist das Allerwichtigste“. Das deutsche „Leben“ als Lebenslauf heißt dagegen 人生, als Alltag 生活; 生命 ist das förmlich-wissenschaftliche Wort.","examples":[{"japanese":"命より大切な物はありません。","romaji":"Inochi yori taisetsu na mono wa arimasen.","german":"Nichts ist wichtiger als das Leben.","furigana":"いのち|たいせつ|もの"},{"japanese":"医者が子供の命を救いました。","romaji":"Isha ga kodomo no inochi o sukuimashita.","german":"Die Ärztin hat das Leben des Kindes gerettet.","furigana":"いしゃ|こども|いのち|すく"},{"japanese":"このラーメン屋はスープが命です。","romaji":"Kono raamen'ya wa suupu ga inochi desu.","german":"Bei diesem Ramen-Laden ist die Brühe das A und O.","furigana":"や|いのち"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"差 is a measurable or clearly recognizable difference, often in numbers: 差がある, 差が出る/つく \"to make a difference\", 差が縮まる/開く \"the gap narrows/widens\", 〜点差で \"by a margin of ... points\". 違い is more general and refers to any kind of difference. Compounds are 時差 \"time difference\" and 温度差.","examples":[{"japanese":"二つの商品には値段の差があります。","romaji":"Futatsu no shōhin ni wa nedan no sa ga arimasu.","german":"There is a price difference between the two products.","furigana":"ふた|しょうひん|ねだん|さ"},{"japanese":"練習を続けると差が出ます。","romaji":"Renshū o tsuzukeru to sa ga demasu.","german":"If you keep practicing, it will make a difference.","furigana":"れんしゅう|つづ|さ|で"},{"japanese":"昨日の試合は一点差で負けました。","romaji":"Kinou no shiai wa itten sa de makemashita.","german":"Yesterday's match was lost by one point.","furigana":"きのう|しあい|いってんさ|ま"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"金額 is a concrete sum of money, such as on bills, receipts or contracts: 金額を確認する, 金額が大きい, 合計金額 (\"total amount\"). 値段 is the price of a product, 料金 a fee for services, and 代金 the purchase price to be paid. お金 is the general word for money.","examples":[{"japanese":"支払う金額を確認してください。","romaji":"Shiharau kingaku o kakunin shite kudasai.","german":"Please check the amount to be paid.","furigana":"しはら|きんがく|かくにん"},{"japanese":"予想より大きな金額になりました。","romaji":"Yosō yori ōkina kingaku ni narimashita.","german":"The amount turned out larger than expected.","furigana":"よそう|おお|きんがく"},{"japanese":"レシートの金額が間違っていたので、店員に言いました。","romaji":"Reshiito no kingaku ga machigatte ita node, ten'in ni iimashita.","german":"The amount on the receipt was wrong, so I told the clerk.","furigana":"きんがく|まちが|てんいん|い"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 16"},
{"notes":"好み is the noun of 好む and refers to preferences and taste: 〜の好み, 好みに合う \"to suit someone's taste\", 好みのタイプ \"my type\", 好みがうるさい \"to be picky\". Polite is お好みで \"as you like\", also known from お好み焼き. To state that you like something, 好きだ is otherwise used.","examples":[{"japanese":"味の好みは人によって違います。","romaji":"Aji no konomi wa hito ni yotte chigaimasu.","german":"Taste preferences differ from person to person.","furigana":"あじ|この|ひと|ちが"},{"japanese":"自分の好みに合う服を選びました。","romaji":"Jibun no konomi ni au fuku o erabimashita.","german":"I chose clothes that suit my taste.","furigana":"じぶん|この|あ|ふく|えら"},{"japanese":"お好みでレモンをかけてお召し上がりください。","romaji":"Okonomi de remon o kakete omeshiagari kudasai.","german":"Please add lemon to your liking before eating.","furigana":"この|め|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"決まり is the noun of 決まる and refers to rules or fixed habits in daily life: 決まりを守る/破る, 〜する決まりだ \"it is the rule that ...\". It sounds more everyday than 規則; ルール is similarly casual. お決まりの means \"usual, as always\"; the phrase 決まりが悪い means \"to feel embarrassed\".","examples":[{"japanese":"学校の決まりを守ってください。","romaji":"Gakkō no kimari o mamotte kudasai.","german":"Please follow the school rules.","furigana":"がっこう|き|まも"},{"japanese":"ここでは靴を脱ぐ決まりです。","romaji":"Koko de wa kutsu o nugu kimari desu.","german":"The rule here is to take off your shoes.","furigana":"くつ|ぬ|き"},{"japanese":"日曜日の朝は家族で散歩するのが我が家の決まりです。","romaji":"Nichiyoubi no asa wa kazoku de sanpo suru no ga wagaya no kimari desu.","german":"Taking a family walk on Sunday morning is our family's custom.","furigana":"にちようび|あさ|かぞく|さんぽ|わ|や|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"癖 is a mostly unconscious and often unwanted habit or quirk: 〜する癖がある, 癖を直す, 癖がつく, 口癖 \"favorite phrase\", 寝癖 \"hair sticking up from sleep\". 習慣, by contrast, is a conscious, neutral to positive habit. 癖になる means \"to become addictive, to be unable to quit\"; the word is often written in kana as くせ.","examples":[{"japanese":"緊張すると髪を触る癖があります。","romaji":"Kinchō suru to kami o sawaru kuse ga arimasu.","german":"I have a habit of touching my hair when I get nervous.","furigana":"きんちょう|かみ|さわ|くせ"},{"japanese":"早口になる癖を直したいです。","romaji":"Hayakuchi ni naru kuse o naoshitai desu.","german":"I want to fix my habit of speaking fast.","furigana":"はやくち|くせ|なお"},{"japanese":"このお菓子は一度食べると癖になります。","romaji":"Kono okashi wa ichido taberu to kuse ni narimasu.","german":"Once you eat this snack, you get hooked.","furigana":"かし|いちどた|くせ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"お互い refers to \"each other\" or \"both sides\". As an adverb it usually appears as お互いに before a verb, often together with 〜合う: お互いに助け合う, お互いに話し合う. Before nouns it is お互いの: お互いの気持ち, お互いの意見. The set phrase お互い様 means \"we're in the same boat, it's only natural\". The form without お, 互いに, sounds more literary.","examples":[{"japanese":"お互いに助け合いましょう。","romaji":"Otagai ni tasukeaimashō.","german":"Let's help each other.","furigana":"たが|たす|あ"},{"japanese":"お互いの意見を尊重することが大切です。","romaji":"Otagai no iken o sonchō suru koto ga taisetsu desu.","german":"It is important to respect each other's opinions.","furigana":"たが|いけん|そんちょう|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"困った時はお互い様ですから、気にしないでください。","romaji":"Komatta toki wa otagaisama desu kara, ki ni shinaide kudasai.","german":"It's the same for both of us when we're in trouble, so please don't worry about it.","furigana":"こま|とき|たが|さま|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"気の毒 is a な-adjective expressing sympathy for the misfortune of others: 気の毒な人, 気の毒に思う \"to feel sorry for someone\". To those affected, one politely says お気の毒に or それはお気の毒でしたね. Unlike かわいそう, which is more emotional and often used for children or animals, 気の毒 sounds more adult and distanced. 気の毒なことをした also means \"I caused him trouble, and I'm sorry\".","examples":[{"japanese":"彼の話を聞いて気の毒に思いました。","romaji":"Kare no hanashi o kiite kinodoku ni omoimashita.","german":"I felt sorry after hearing his story.","furigana":"かれ|はなし|き|き|どく|おも"},{"japanese":"せっかく来てくださったのに会えなくて、お気の毒でしたね。","romaji":"Sekkaku kite kudasatta no ni aenakute, okinodoku deshita ne.","german":"You came all this way and couldn't meet him - that was unfortunate for you.","furigana":"き|あ|き|どく"},{"japanese":"火事で家を失った気の毒な家族のために、寄付をしました。","romaji":"Kaji de ie o ushinatta kinodoku na kazoku no tame ni, kifu o shimashita.","german":"I made a donation for the poor family who lost their home in a fire.","furigana":"かじ|いえ|うしな|き|どく|かぞく|きふ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"気味 (きみ) means \"feeling, sensation\" and appears almost only in set phrases. Most common is 気味が悪い \"eerie, creepy\" (intensified: 不気味). Not to be confused with 気持ちが悪い, which tends to mean \"disgusting\" or \"I feel sick\". いい気味だ means, gloatingly, \"serves him right\". As a suffix, 〜気味 is read ぎみ and expresses a touch of something: 風邪気味 \"slightly under the weather with a cold\", 疲れ気味.","examples":[{"japanese":"暗い道は気味が悪かったです。","romaji":"Kurai michi wa kimi ga warukatta desu.","german":"The dark road felt creepy.","furigana":"くら|みち|きみ|わる"},{"japanese":"誰もいない家から音がして、気味が悪くなりました。","romaji":"Dare mo inai ie kara oto ga shite, kimi ga waruku narimashita.","german":"A sound came from the empty house, and I got creeped out.","furigana":"だれ|いえ|おと|きみ|わる"},{"japanese":"いつも人を笑っていた彼が失敗して、いい気味だと思いました。","romaji":"Itsumo hito o waratte ita kare ga shippai shite, ii kimi da to omoimashita.","german":"I thought it served him right when he, who had always laughed at others, failed.","furigana":"ひと|わら|かれ|しっぱい|きみ|おも"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"知らせ is the noun of 知らせる \"to notify\" and means a piece of news that is passed on to someone: 知らせが届く, 知らせが入る, 知らせを受ける. It often comes with an evaluation: うれしい知らせ, 悲しい知らせ. The polite form お知らせ is used for official notices and announcements. Unlike ニュース (media reports), it concerns personal news. 虫の知らせ means \"a premonition\".","examples":[{"japanese":"友達からうれしい知らせが届きました。","romaji":"Tomodachi kara ureshii shirase ga todokimashita.","german":"I received good news from a friend.","furigana":"ともだち|し|とど"},{"japanese":"詳しい知らせを待っています。","romaji":"Kuwashii shirase o matte imasu.","german":"I am waiting for detailed information.","furigana":"くわ|し|ま"},{"japanese":"虫の知らせか、急に祖母に電話したくなりました。","romaji":"Mushi no shirase ka, kyuu ni sobo ni denwa shitaku narimashita.","german":"Maybe it was a premonition, but I suddenly wanted to call my grandmother.","furigana":"むし|し|きゅう|そぼ|でんわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"願い is a wish or a request, the noun of 願う. Typical are 願いがかなう \"a wish comes true\", 願いをかなえる \"to grant a wish\" and 願いを込める \"to put a wish into something\". As a request to others, the polite form お願い is almost always used: お願いがあります. Compared with 希望 (more matter-of-fact, also \"preference\") and 望み (also \"prospect, chance\"), 願い sounds more personal and heartfelt.","examples":[{"japanese":"長年の願いがかないました。","romaji":"Naganen no negai ga kanaimashita.","german":"My long-held wish came true.","furigana":"ながねん|ねが"},{"japanese":"皆の願いは家族の健康です。","romaji":"Mina no negai wa kazoku no kenkō desu.","german":"Everyone's wish is for their family's health.","furigana":"みな|ねが|かぞく|けんこう"},{"japanese":"実は、一つお願いがあるんですが…。","romaji":"Jitsu wa, hitotsu onegai ga aru n desu ga...","german":"Actually, I have a favor to ask...","furigana":"じつ|ひと|ねが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"適度 is a な-adjective for \"the right measure, neither too much nor too little\": 適度な運動, 適度な湿度, adverbially 適度に休む. It sounds matter-of-fact and often appears in health tips and instructions. The opposite is 過度 \"excessive\". Not to be confused with 適当, which also means \"suitable\" but colloquially often means \"careless, half-hearted\". In daily speech, ちょうどいい is also simply used.","examples":[{"japanese":"健康のために適度な運動を続けます。","romaji":"Kenkō no tame ni tekido na undō o tsuzukemasu.","german":"I keep up moderate exercise for my health.","furigana":"けんこう|てきど|うんどう|つづ"},{"japanese":"作業中は適度に休憩を取ってください。","romaji":"Sagyouchuu wa tekido ni kyuukei o totte kudasai.","german":"Please take breaks at appropriate intervals during work.","furigana":"さぎょうちゅう|てきど|きゅうけい|と"},{"japanese":"お酒は適度に楽しみましょう。","romaji":"Osake wa tekido ni tanoshimimashou.","german":"Let's enjoy alcohol in moderation.","furigana":"さけ|てきど|たの"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"手間 is the amount of time and effort something takes. Typical: 手間がかかる \"to be laborious\", 手間が省ける / 手間を省く \"to save oneself work\". 手間をかける means \"to invest effort\", but also \"to cause someone trouble\"; politely one apologizes with お手間を取らせてすみません. The verb 手間取る \"to take longer than expected\" is derived from it. 面倒 stresses more the annoying feeling, while 手間 is the objective effort.","examples":[{"japanese":"この料理は手間がかかります。","romaji":"Kono ryōri wa tema ga kakarimasu.","german":"This dish takes a lot of effort.","furigana":"りょうり|てま"},{"japanese":"道具を使えば手間が省けます。","romaji":"Dōgu o tsukaeba tema ga habukemasu.","german":"Using a tool saves you the trouble.","furigana":"どうぐ|つか|てま|はぶ"},{"japanese":"お忙しいところ、お手間を取らせてすみません。","romaji":"Oisogashii tokoro, otema o torasete sumimasen.","german":"I'm sorry to trouble you when you're so busy.","furigana":"いそが|てま|と"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"流れ is the noun of 流れる and first means the current of water or air: 流れが速い, 川の流れ. Figuratively it stands for the course of a process (仕事の流れ, 話の流れ \"flow of conversation\") and for developments and trends (時代の流れ). 流れに乗る means \"to go with the flow, to ride the momentum\". For fashion trends in the narrower sense, 流行 is used instead.","examples":[{"japanese":"この川は流れが速いです。","romaji":"Kono kawa wa nagare ga hayai desu.","german":"The flow of this river is fast.","furigana":"かわ|なが|はや"},{"japanese":"仕事の流れを説明します。","romaji":"Shigoto no nagare o setsumei shimasu.","german":"I will explain the flow of the work.","furigana":"しごと|なが|せつめい"},{"japanese":"時代の流れに合わせて、店のやり方も変わりました。","romaji":"Jidai no nagare ni awasete, mise no yarikata mo kawarimashita.","german":"In line with the trends of the times, the way the shop does things has also changed.","furigana":"じだい|なが|あ|みせ|かた|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"幅 is first the measurable width: 道の幅, 横幅, 幅が広い / 狭い. Figuratively it refers to range or leeway: 知識の幅を広げる, 値段の幅 \"price range\", 選択の幅 \"range of choices\". With 大 it forms 大幅 \"drastic, considerable\": 大幅に値上がりする. 広さ, by contrast, is an area, while 幅 is only the extent from side to side.","examples":[{"japanese":"この道の幅は三メートルです。","romaji":"Kono michi no haba wa san mētoru desu.","german":"The width of this road is three meters.","furigana":"みち|はば|さん"},{"japanese":"読書を通して知識の幅を広げたいです。","romaji":"Dokusho o tōshite chishiki no haba o hirogetai desu.","german":"I want to broaden the range of my knowledge through reading.","furigana":"どくしょ|とお|ちしき|はば|ひろ"},{"japanese":"この店のワインは、値段の幅が広いです。","romaji":"Kono mise no wain wa, nedan no haba ga hiroi desu.","german":"This shop's wines come in a wide range of prices.","furigana":"みせ|ねだん|はば|ひろ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"水準 is a matter-of-fact, rather written word for \"level, standard\": 生活水準, 教育水準, 高い水準. Typical combinations are 水準が高い / 低い, 水準に達する \"to reach the level\" and 一定の水準に保つ. In conversation people mostly say レベル. Not to be confused with 基準, which refers to a yardstick or criterion by which something is measured.","examples":[{"japanese":"この地域の生活水準は高いです。","romaji":"Kono chiiki no seikatsu suijun wa takai desu.","german":"The standard of living in this region is high.","furigana":"ちいき|せいかつすいじゅん|たか"},{"japanese":"製品の品質を一定の水準に保ちます。","romaji":"Seihin no hinshitsu o ittei no suijun ni tamochimasu.","german":"We keep the quality of our products at a certain standard.","furigana":"せいひん|ひんしつ|いってい|すいじゅん|たも"},{"japanese":"この大学の研究は、世界でも高い水準にあります。","romaji":"Kono daigaku no kenkyuu wa, sekai demo takai suijun ni arimasu.","german":"The research at this university is at a high level even by world standards.","furigana":"だいがく|けんきゅう|せかい|たか|すいじゅん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"前者 means \"the former\" and refers to the first of exactly two previously mentioned things or persons; its counterpart is 後者 \"the latter\". Both often appear in one sentence: 前者は…、後者は…. The word is matter-of-fact to written and typical of comparisons, discussions and reports. With more than two possibilities, 最初の is used instead.","examples":[{"japanese":"電車とバスなら、前者を選びます。","romaji":"Densha to basu nara, zensha o erabimasu.","german":"Between the train and the bus, I'd choose the former.","furigana":"でんしゃ|ぜんしゃ|えら"},{"japanese":"二つの案のうち、前者の方が安く済みます。","romaji":"Futatsu no an no uchi, zensha no hō ga yasuku sumimasu.","german":"Of the two proposals, the former costs less.","furigana":"ふた|あん|ぜんしゃ|ほう|やす|す"},{"japanese":"会議では値上げと値下げの意見が出ましたが、私は前者に賛成です。","romaji":"Kaigi de wa neage to nesage no iken ga demashita ga, watashi wa zensha ni sansei desu.","german":"In the meeting, opinions for a price increase and a price decrease came up, but I agree with the former.","furigana":"かいぎ|ねあ|ねさ|いけん|で|わたし|ぜんしゃ|さんせい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"話し合う is composed of 話す and 〜合う \"mutually\": you discuss something together in order to reach agreement. The partner is marked by と, the topic by を or について: 家族と将来について話し合う. The noun 話し合い means \"discussion, talk\". Unlike 相談する (asking someone for advice) and the more formal 議論する (to debate), 話し合う stresses an exchange between equals.","examples":[{"japanese":"家族と将来について話し合いました。","romaji":"Kazoku to shōrai ni tsuite hanashiaimashita.","german":"I talked with my family about the future.","furigana":"かぞく|しょうらい|はな|あ"},{"japanese":"問題を皆で話し合いましょう。","romaji":"Mondai o mina de hanashiaimashō.","german":"Let's all discuss the problem together.","furigana":"もんだい|みな|はな|あ"},{"japanese":"けんかをしないで、ちゃんと話し合ったほうがいいよ。","romaji":"Kenka o shinaide, chanto hanashiatta hou ga ii yo.","german":"Instead of fighting, you'd better talk it over properly.","furigana":"はな|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"済ませる is transitive: you bring something to an end or get it done (宿題を済ませる, 支払いを済ませる, 用事を済ませる). The intransitive counterpart is 済む \"to be done\"; there is also the equivalent godan variant 済ます. With 簡単に, 安く or 〜で, it also means \"to make do, to get by with little\": 昼食をパンで済ませる. Compared with 終える, it stresses that an obligation has been ticked off.","examples":[{"japanese":"出かける前に宿題を済ませました。","romaji":"Dekakeru mae ni shukudai o sumasemashita.","german":"I finished my homework before going out.","furigana":"で|まえ|しゅくだい|す"},{"japanese":"昼食は簡単に済ませました。","romaji":"Chūshoku wa kantan ni sumasemashita.","german":"I kept lunch simple.","furigana":"ちゅうしょく|かんたん|す"},{"japanese":"レジで支払いを済ませてから、二階へどうぞ。","romaji":"Reji de shiharai o sumasete kara, nikai e douzo.","german":"Please go upstairs after you have paid at the register.","furigana":"しはら|す|にかい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"取り上げる has several meanings, which the context clarifies: to take up a topic (授業で問題を取り上げる), to accept a proposal (意見を取り上げる), to report on something in the media (新聞に取り上げられる, often in the passive) and to take something away from someone (子供からはさみを取り上げる). The person something is taken from is marked with から. Compared with 奪う \"to rob\", the taking away here suggests authority, for example by parents or teachers.","examples":[{"japanese":"授業で環境問題を取り上げました。","romaji":"Jugyō de kankyō mondai o toriagemashita.","german":"We took up environmental issues in class.","furigana":"じゅぎょう|かんきょうもんだい|と|あ"},{"japanese":"危ないので、子供からはさみを取り上げました。","romaji":"Abunai node, kodomo kara hasami o toriagemashita.","german":"It was dangerous, so I took the scissors away from the child.","furigana":"あぶ|こども|と|あ"},{"japanese":"その事件は新聞でも大きく取り上げられました。","romaji":"Sono jiken wa shinbun demo ookiku toriageraremashita.","german":"That incident was also covered prominently in the newspapers.","furigana":"じけん|しんぶん|おお|と|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"微笑む is intransitive and means \"to smile softly\", without audible laughter. 笑う is more general and also includes laughing out loud. The person one smiles at is marked with に: 〜に微笑みかける. The noun is 微笑み \"smile\". The word sounds somewhat elevated and narrative; in daily life people often say にっこりする or にこにこする. In newspapers you also see ほほ笑む.","examples":[{"japanese":"母は何も言わずに微笑みました。","romaji":"Haha wa nani mo iwazu ni hohoemimashita.","german":"My mother smiled without saying anything.","furigana":"はは|なに|い|ほほえ"},{"japanese":"写真の中で二人が微笑んでいます。","romaji":"Shashin no naka de futari ga hohoende imasu.","german":"The two of them are smiling in the photo.","furigana":"しゃしん|なか|ふたり|ほほえ"},{"japanese":"目が合うと、赤ちゃんがにっこり微笑みました。","romaji":"Me ga au to, akachan ga nikkori hohoemimashita.","german":"When our eyes met, the baby gave a big smile.","furigana":"め|あ|あか|ほほえ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"一度に means \"at once, in one go\": several things happen together instead of one after another. Not to be confused with 一度 \"once\" (一度行ったことがある). 一気に, by contrast, stresses momentum and speed (\"in one breath\"), and 同時に exact simultaneity. 一度に often appears with quantities: 一度に十人まで, 一度にたくさん.","examples":[{"japanese":"一度に全部覚える必要はありません。","romaji":"Ichido ni zenbu oboeru hitsuyō wa arimasen.","german":"You don't need to memorize everything at once.","furigana":"いちど|ぜんぶおぼ|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"注文した料理が一度に運ばれてきました。","romaji":"Chūmon shita ryōri ga ichido ni hakobarete kimashita.","german":"The dishes we ordered were all brought out at once.","furigana":"ちゅうもん|りょうり|いちど|はこ"},{"japanese":"このエレベーターには一度に十人まで乗れます。","romaji":"Kono erebeetaa ni wa ichido ni juunin made noremasu.","german":"This elevator can carry up to ten people at a time.","furigana":"いちど|じゅうにん|の"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"共に is the more formal equivalent of 一緒に: 〜と共に \"together with\". In the form AとBは共に it means \"both\". After nouns or verbs, 〜と共に also expresses a parallel development: 時代と共に \"with the times\". In everyday speech it sounds stiff; in speeches, articles and grammar it is often written in kana as ともに.","examples":[{"japanese":"友人と共に会社を作りました。","romaji":"Yūjin to tomo ni kaisha o tsukurimashita.","german":"I started a company together with a friend.","furigana":"ゆうじん|とも|かいしゃ|つく"},{"japanese":"二つの方法は共に役立ちます。","romaji":"Futatsu no hōhō wa tomo ni yakudachimasu.","german":"Both of the methods are useful.","furigana":"ふた|ほうほう|とも|やくだ"},{"japanese":"時代と共に、言葉も変わっていきます。","romaji":"Jidai to tomo ni, kotoba mo kawatte ikimasu.","german":"Along with the times, words also change.","furigana":"じだい|とも|ことば|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"たっぷり means \"plenty, more than enough\" and is used for quantities, time and liquids: たっぷり食べる, 時間がたっぷりある, before nouns たっぷりの + noun. As a suffix it intensifies qualities: 自信たっぷり, 愛情たっぷり. Before numbers it means \"a good, a full\" (たっぷり一時間), and たっぷりした服 is loose-fitting clothing. Compared with 十分, it sounds more generous; compared with いっぱい, it is somewhat less colloquial.","examples":[{"japanese":"野菜をたっぷり食べてください。","romaji":"Yasai o tappuri tabete kudasai.","german":"Please eat plenty of vegetables.","furigana":"やさい|た"},{"japanese":"出発まで時間がたっぷりあります。","romaji":"Shuppatsu made jikan ga tappuri arimasu.","german":"We have plenty of time before departure.","furigana":"しゅっぱつ|じかん"},{"japanese":"駅からたっぷり一時間は歩きました。","romaji":"Eki kara tappuri ichijikan wa arukimashita.","german":"I walked a full hour from the station.","furigana":"えき|いちじかん|ある"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"何か means \"something, anything\" and stands for an unspecified thing; the particles が and を often drop after it: 何か飲みますか. Do not confuse it with 何が \"what?\" as a real question word. Questions with 何か are first answered with yes or no. 何か can stand directly before a noun (何か質問, 何か飲み物). The negative is 何も…ない \"nothing\"; colloquially 何か becomes なんか.","examples":[{"japanese":"何か飲みますか。","romaji":"Nanika nomimasu ka.","german":"Would you like something to drink?","furigana":"なに|の"},{"japanese":"何か問題があれば教えてください。","romaji":"Nanika mondai ga areba oshiete kudasai.","german":"If there are any problems, please let me know.","furigana":"なに|もんだい|おし"},{"japanese":"誕生日に何か欲しいものはある？","romaji":"Tanjoubi ni nanika hoshii mono wa aru?","german":"Is there anything you want for your birthday?","furigana":"たんじょうび|なに|ほ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 13"},
{"notes":"何でも means \"everything, no matter what\" and takes an affirmative verb: 何でも食べる. The counterpart 何も takes a negative (\"nothing\"). 何でもない means \"it's nothing, no big deal\". At the beginning of a sentence, 何でも also means \"apparently, I hear\" and is combined with 〜そうだ or 〜らしい. It is often written in kana, なんでも.","examples":[{"japanese":"分からないことは何でも聞いてください。","romaji":"Wakaranai koto wa nandemo kiite kudasai.","german":"Ask about anything you don't understand.","furigana":"わ|なん|き"},{"japanese":"弟は何でも食べます。","romaji":"Otōto wa nandemo tabemasu.","german":"My younger brother eats anything.","furigana":"おとうと|なん|た"},{"japanese":"何でも、あの店は来月閉まるそうです。","romaji":"Nandemo, ano mise wa raigetsu shimaru sou desu.","german":"Apparently that shop is going to close next month.","furigana":"なん|みせ|らいげつし"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 26"},
{"notes":"分 (ぶん) is the share or portion that falls to someone or serves a purpose: 自分の分, 明日の分. As a suffix after quantities it means \"enough for ...\": 三人分, 三日分の薬. Other expressions are その分 \"to that extent, correspondingly\" and この分では \"if things go on like this\". Do not confuse it with 分 (ふん/ぷん) \"minute\" and with 部分 \"part of a whole\".","examples":[{"japanese":"自分の分は自分で払います。","romaji":"Jibun no bun wa jibun de haraimasu.","german":"I pay for my own share.","furigana":"じぶん|ぶん|じぶん|はら"},{"japanese":"明日の分も作っておきました。","romaji":"Ashita no bun mo tsukutte okimashita.","german":"I also made some for tomorrow.","furigana":"あした|ぶん|つく"},{"japanese":"病院で三日分の薬をもらいました。","romaji":"Byouin de mikka bun no kusuri o moraimashita.","german":"At the hospital I got three days' worth of medicine.","furigana":"びょういん|みっかぶん|くすり"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 34"},
{"notes":"図 (ず) is an explanatory representation: figure, sketch, diagram. In textbooks and reports one reads 図1, 次の図, 図のように. Typical verbs are 図を見る, 図にかく and 図で示す. 絵, by contrast, is a picture of an artistic nature, and グラフ specifically a chart with data. 図 is also in 地図, 図形 and 図書館.","examples":[{"japanese":"次の図を見てください。","romaji":"Tsugi no zu o mite kudasai.","german":"Please look at the following figure.","furigana":"つぎ|ず|み"},{"japanese":"図を使って説明すると分かりやすいです。","romaji":"Zu o tsukatte setsumei suru to wakariyasui desu.","german":"It is easy to understand if you explain using a diagram.","furigana":"ず|つか|せつめい|わ"},{"japanese":"駅までの道を図にかいてもらえますか。","romaji":"Eki made no michi o zu ni kaite moraemasu ka.","german":"Could you draw me a map of the way to the station?","furigana":"えき|みち|ず"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 34"},
{"notes":"文 (ぶん) is a single sentence in the grammatical sense: 文を作る, 文を書く, この文の意味. A coherent text of several sentences is a 文章, and an essay is a 作文. Many compounds contain 文: 例文 \"example sentence\", 文法 \"grammar\". In 文句 and 注文 the character is read もん. 言葉, by contrast, means \"word\" or language in general.","examples":[{"japanese":"短い文を三つ作ってください。","romaji":"Mijikai bun o mittsu tsukutte kudasai.","german":"Please make three short sentences.","furigana":"みじか|ぶん|みっ|つく"},{"japanese":"この文の意味が分かりません。","romaji":"Kono bun no imi ga wakarimasen.","german":"I don't understand the meaning of this sentence.","furigana":"ぶん|いみ|わ"},{"japanese":"メールの最後の文が少し失礼に聞こえます。","romaji":"Meeru no saigo no bun ga sukoshi shitsurei ni kikoemasu.","german":"The last sentence of the e-mail sounds a bit rude.","furigana":"さいご|ぶん|すこ|しつれい|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"印 (しるし) is a sign or mark: 印を付ける \"to mark, to tick\", 目印 \"landmark\", 矢印 \"arrow\". Figuratively it is a token of affection or thanks: 感謝の印, お礼の印; in this meaning it is often written in kana as しるし. Read as いん, 印 means \"stamp, seal\" (印鑑).","examples":[{"japanese":"正しい答えに印を付けてください。","romaji":"Tadashii kotae ni shirushi o tsukete kudasai.","german":"Please mark the correct answer.","furigana":"ただ|こた|しるし|つ"},{"japanese":"赤い印が入口の場所を示しています。","romaji":"Akai shirushi ga iriguchi no basho o shimeshite imasu.","german":"The red mark shows the location of the entrance.","furigana":"あか|しるし|いりぐち|ばしょ|しめ"},{"japanese":"これはほんの感謝の印です。","romaji":"Kore wa honno kansha no shirushi desu.","german":"This is just a token of my gratitude.","furigana":"かんしゃ|しるし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"生 (なま) means \"raw, uncooked\" and in general \"unprocessed, direct\". Adverbially it appears as 生で (生で食べる, 生で聞く \"to hear live\"), before nouns as 生の or as a prefix: 生卵, 生ビール \"draft beer\", 生放送 \"live broadcast\", 生クリーム. 新鮮, by contrast, means \"fresh\" in the sense of not old. Other readings have other meanings, for example せい in 生活 or う in 生まれる.","examples":[{"japanese":"この魚は生で食べられます。","romaji":"Kono sakana wa nama de taberaremasu.","german":"This fish can be eaten raw.","furigana":"さかな|なま|た"},{"japanese":"生の野菜をよく洗ってください。","romaji":"Nama no yasai o yoku aratte kudasai.","german":"Please wash the raw vegetables well.","furigana":"なま|やさい|あら"},{"japanese":"好きな歌手の歌を初めて生で聞きました。","romaji":"Suki na kashu no uta o hajimete nama de kikimashita.","german":"I heard my favorite singer's song live for the first time.","furigana":"す|かしゅ|うた|はじ|なま|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"跡 (あと) is a visible trace that something has left behind: 足の跡 or 足跡 \"footprint\", 傷跡 \"scar\", コップの跡. It also refers to the site or remains of something past: 城跡 \"castle ruins\". Typical verbs are 跡が残る and 跡を残す. Not to be confused with the identically read 後 \"after, later\".","examples":[{"japanese":"雪の上に足の跡があります。","romaji":"Yuki no ue ni ashi no ato ga arimasu.","german":"There are footprints in the snow.","furigana":"ゆき|うえ|あし|あと"},{"japanese":"机にコップの跡が残りました。","romaji":"Tsukue ni koppu no ato ga nokorimashita.","german":"A ring from the glass remained on the desk.","furigana":"つくえ|あと|のこ"},{"japanese":"ここは昔のお城の跡です。","romaji":"Koko wa mukashi no oshiro no ato desu.","german":"This is the site of an old castle.","furigana":"むかし|しろ|あと"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"穴 is a hole or pit. Note the verb pair: 穴が開く \"a hole appears\" (intransitive) and 穴を開ける \"to make a hole\" (transitive); in the ground it is 穴を掘る. Figuratively 穴 refers to a gap, for example due to lack of staff or money: 穴を埋める \"to fill the gap\". 穴場 is a \"hidden gem\" that hardly anyone knows.","examples":[{"japanese":"靴下に穴が開いています。","romaji":"Kutsushita ni ana ga aite imasu.","german":"There is a hole in my sock.","furigana":"くつした|あな|あ"},{"japanese":"木を植えるために穴を掘りました。","romaji":"Ki o ueru tame ni ana o horimashita.","german":"I dug a hole to plant a tree.","furigana":"き|う|あな|ほ"},{"japanese":"田中さんが辞めた穴を、みんなで埋めています。","romaji":"Tanaka-san ga yameta ana o, minna de umete imasu.","german":"Everyone is filling the gap left by Mr. Tanaka's resignation.","furigana":"たなか|や|あな|う"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"波 is a wave on water: 波が高い / 荒い / 静か, 波の音. Figuratively it stands for fluctuations and movements: 気分に波がある \"to be moody\", 人の波 \"stream of people\", 時代の波. 波に乗る means \"to ride the wave\", that is, to take advantage of a trend. As a component it appears in 寒波 \"cold wave\" and 電波 \"radio waves, reception\".","examples":[{"japanese":"今日は波が高いです。","romaji":"Kyō wa nami ga takai desu.","german":"The waves are high today.","furigana":"きょう|なみ|たか"},{"japanese":"波の音を聞きながら眠りました。","romaji":"Nami no oto o kikinagara nemurimashita.","german":"I fell asleep listening to the sound of the waves.","furigana":"なみ|おと|き|ねむ"},{"japanese":"彼は気分に波があるので、話しかけるタイミングが難しいです。","romaji":"Kare wa kibun ni nami ga aru node, hanashikakeru taimingu ga muzukashii desu.","german":"His mood fluctuates, so it is hard to find the right moment to talk to him.","furigana":"かれ|きぶん|なみ|はな|むずか"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 27"},
{"notes":"肌 is skin as a surface, especially in connection with care, appearance and sensation: 肌が乾燥する, 肌が荒れる, 肌に優しい. 皮膚 is the matter-of-fact, medical term, and 皮 the skin of animals or the peel of fruit. Figuratively 肌で感じる means \"to experience firsthand\", and 肌が合う \"to get along well with someone\". Related is 肌寒い \"chilly, cool\".","examples":[{"japanese":"冬は肌が乾燥しやすいです。","romaji":"Fuyu wa hada ga kansō shiyasui desu.","german":"Skin dries out easily in winter.","furigana":"ふゆ|はだ|かんそう"},{"japanese":"この服は肌に優しいです。","romaji":"Kono fuku wa hada ni yasashii desu.","german":"These clothes are gentle on the skin.","furigana":"ふく|はだ|やさ"},{"japanese":"現地に行って、文化の違いを肌で感じました。","romaji":"Genchi ni itte, bunka no chigai o hada de kanjimashita.","german":"I went there and felt the cultural differences firsthand.","furigana":"げんち|い|ぶんか|ちが|はだ|かん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"布 is fabric as a material or a piece of cloth: 布で拭く, 布で作る, 布をかける. Something \"made of fabric\" is 布製. For yard goods used for sewing, 生地 is also used; specific cloths have their own words such as タオル, ハンカチ or 雑巾 \"cleaning rag\". In contrast to 紙 or ビニール, 布 stresses woven textile.","examples":[{"japanese":"柔らかい布で画面を拭いてください。","romaji":"Yawarakai nuno de gamen o fuite kudasai.","german":"Please wipe the screen with a soft cloth.","furigana":"やわ|ぬの|がめん|ふ"},{"japanese":"この布でかばんを作りました。","romaji":"Kono nuno de kaban o tsukurimashita.","german":"I made a bag out of this cloth.","furigana":"ぬの|つく"},{"japanese":"机の上に白い布がかけてあります。","romaji":"Tsukue no ue ni shiroi nuno ga kakete arimasu.","german":"A white cloth is laid over the desk.","furigana":"つくえ|うえ|しろ|ぬの"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"瓶 is a container made of glass: a bottle (ビールの瓶, 瓶ビール) and also a screw-top jar (ジャムの瓶). Plastic bottles, by contrast, are called ペットボトル, and cans 缶; in waste separation one often reads びん・缶. Empty bottles are 空き瓶. The character is difficult and is often written in kana, びん. A vase is 花瓶 (かびん).","examples":[{"japanese":"空の瓶を集めています。","romaji":"Kara no bin o atsumete imasu.","german":"I collect empty bottles.","furigana":"から|びん|あつ"},{"japanese":"ジャムを瓶に入れて保存します。","romaji":"Jamu o bin ni irete hozon shimasu.","german":"I put jam into a jar to preserve it.","furigana":"びん|い|ほぞん"},{"japanese":"ビールの瓶を開けてもらえますか。","romaji":"Biiru no bin o akete moraemasu ka.","german":"Could you open the beer bottle for me?","furigana":"びん|あ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 26"},
{"notes":"土 (つち) is earth as a material: 土を入れる, 土が柔らかい, 土が付く. 地面, by contrast, refers to the surface of the ground, 土地 to a plot of land, and 泥 to wet mud. 土に返る means \"to return to earth\". As the abbreviation for Saturday (土曜日), the character is read ど, while in 土地 it is read と.","examples":[{"japanese":"雨で土が柔らかくなりました。","romaji":"Ame de tsuchi ga yawarakaku narimashita.","german":"The soil became soft from the rain.","furigana":"あめ|つち|やわ"},{"japanese":"鉢に土を入れて花を植えます。","romaji":"Hachi ni tsuchi o irete hana o uemasu.","german":"I put soil in the pot and plant flowers.","furigana":"はち|つち|い|はな|う"},{"japanese":"靴に土が付いているから、玄関で落としてね。","romaji":"Kutsu ni tsuchi ga tsuite iru kara, genkan de otoshite ne.","german":"There is dirt on your shoes, so shake it off in the entrance.","furigana":"くつ|つち|つ|げんかん|お"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"岩 is a rock or large boulder that cannot be lifted. 石 is a stone of handy size, 小石 a pebble, and 砂 sand. Common compounds are 岩場 \"rocky terrain\" and 岩山 \"rocky mountain\". One sits on a rock (岩に座る) or climbs it (岩に登る).","examples":[{"japanese":"海の近くに大きな岩があります。","romaji":"Umi no chikaku ni ōkina iwa ga arimasu.","german":"There is a big rock near the sea.","furigana":"うみ|ちか|おお|いわ"},{"japanese":"岩に座って休みました。","romaji":"Iwa ni suwatte yasumimashita.","german":"I sat on a rock and rested.","furigana":"いわ|すわ|やす"},{"japanese":"この山は岩が多くて、登るのが大変です。","romaji":"Kono yama wa iwa ga ookute, noboru no ga taihen desu.","german":"This mountain has many rocks, so it is hard to climb.","furigana":"やま|いわ|おお|のぼ|たいへん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"牛 (うし) is the general term for cattle, whether cow, bull or ox. The meat is 牛肉 and milk 牛乳; in these words and in 牛丼 the character is read ぎゅう. Large animals are counted with 頭 (とう): 牛が三頭. Keeping or raising cattle is 牛を飼う or 牛を育てる.","examples":[{"japanese":"牧場で牛を見ました。","romaji":"Bokujō de ushi o mimashita.","german":"I saw cows at the ranch.","furigana":"ぼくじょう|うし|み"},{"japanese":"牛が草を食べています。","romaji":"Ushi ga kusa o tabete imasu.","german":"The cows are eating grass.","furigana":"うし|くさ|た"},{"japanese":"この辺りには、牛を育てている農家が多いです。","romaji":"Kono atari ni wa, ushi o sodatete iru nouka ga ooi desu.","german":"There are many farms around here that raise cattle.","furigana":"あた|うし|そだ|のうか|おお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"馬 is the horse; riding is 馬に乗る, with に for the mount. Horses are counted like other large animals with 頭 (とう). In 競馬 \"horse racing\" the character is read ば. The idiom 馬が合う means \"to get along well, to be on the same wavelength\".","examples":[{"japanese":"子供の時、馬に乗ったことがあります。","romaji":"Kodomo no toki, uma ni notta koto ga arimasu.","german":"When I was a child, I rode a horse once.","furigana":"こども|とき|うま|の"},{"japanese":"馬が広い草原を走っています。","romaji":"Uma ga hiroi sōgen o hashitte imasu.","german":"A horse is running across the wide grassland.","furigana":"うま|ひろ|そうげん|はし"},{"japanese":"彼女とは最初から馬が合いました。","romaji":"Kanojo to wa saisho kara uma ga aimashita.","german":"I got along with her from the very start.","furigana":"かのじょ|さいしょ|うま|あ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 18"},
{"notes":"兎 (うさぎ) does not distinguish between rabbit and hare; the wild hare is more precisely called 野うさぎ. The kanji is rare; usually it is written うさぎ or, especially in biology and at the zoo, ウサギ. Counting is nowadays mostly done with 匹, traditionally also with 羽. In the Japanese imagination, a rabbit pounds rice cakes on the moon.","examples":[{"japanese":"白い兎を飼っています。","romaji":"Shiroi usagi o katte imasu.","german":"I keep a white rabbit.","furigana":"しろ|うさぎ|か"},{"japanese":"兎が耳を動かしました。","romaji":"Usagi ga mimi o ugokashimashita.","german":"The rabbit moved its ears.","furigana":"うさぎ|みみ|うご"},{"japanese":"日本では、月で兎が餅をついていると言われています。","romaji":"Nihon de wa, tsuki de usagi ga mochi o tsuite iru to iwarete imasu.","german":"In Japan, it is said that a rabbit pounds rice cakes on the moon.","furigana":"にほん|つき|うさぎ|もち|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"嵐 is a violent storm, usually with rain: 嵐が来る, 嵐がやむ, 嵐が去る. 台風 is specifically a typhoon, and 暴風雨 is the formal term for a storm with rain. Figuratively 嵐 stands for a violent outburst: 拍手の嵐 \"thunderous applause\", 批判の嵐 \"a storm of criticism\". The saying 嵐の前の静けさ corresponds to \"the calm before the storm\".","examples":[{"japanese":"嵐が来る前に家に戻りました。","romaji":"Arashi ga kuru mae ni ie ni modorimashita.","german":"I got back home before the storm came.","furigana":"あらし|く|まえ|いえ|もど"},{"japanese":"嵐で多くの木が倒れました。","romaji":"Arashi de ōku no ki ga taoremashita.","german":"Many trees fell in the storm.","furigana":"あらし|おお|き|たお"},{"japanese":"演奏が終わると、会場は拍手の嵐に包まれました。","romaji":"Ensou ga owaru to, kaijou wa hakushu no arashi ni tsutsumaremashita.","german":"When the performance ended, the hall was engulfed in a storm of applause.","furigana":"えんそう|お|かいじょう|はくしゅ|あらし|つつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"泡 covers foam and individual bubbles in liquids: 石鹸の泡, ビールの泡, 泡が立つ \"it foams\". Related verbs are 泡立つ \"to foam\" and 泡立てる \"to whip\", for example cream or egg whites. The idiom 水の泡になる means \"to vanish into thin air, to have been in vain\". It is also often written in kana as あわ.","examples":[{"japanese":"石鹸の泡で手を洗います。","romaji":"Sekken no awa de te o araimasu.","german":"I wash my hands with soap lather.","furigana":"せっけん|あわ|て|あら"},{"japanese":"水の中から泡が上がってきました。","romaji":"Mizu no naka kara awa ga agatte kimashita.","german":"Bubbles rose from the water.","furigana":"みず|なか|あわ|あ"},{"japanese":"雨で試合が中止になり、練習が水の泡になりました。","romaji":"Ame de shiai ga chuushi ni nari, renshuu ga mizu no awa ni narimashita.","german":"The match was canceled because of rain, so all our practice came to nothing.","furigana":"あめ|しあい|ちゅうし|れんしゅう|みず|あわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"粉 (こな) is any powdery substance, in a kitchen context often flour. More precisely, wheat flour is 小麦粉 (こむぎこ); in compounds the character is often read こ. Other words: 粉薬 \"powdered medicine\", 粉雪 \"powder snow\", 粉々 \"to pieces\". 粉末 is the more technical term, for example on packaging.","examples":[{"japanese":"粉を少しずつ水に混ぜます。","romaji":"Kona o sukoshizutsu mizu ni mazemasu.","german":"Mix the powder into the water little by little.","furigana":"こな|すこ|みず|ま"},{"japanese":"机の上に白い粉が落ちています。","romaji":"Tsukue no ue ni shiroi kona ga ochite imasu.","german":"White powder has fallen on the desk.","furigana":"つくえ|うえ|しろ|こな|お"},{"japanese":"パンを作るので、小麦粉を買ってきてくれる？","romaji":"Pan o tsukuru node, komugiko o katte kite kureru?","german":"I'm making bread, so could you buy some flour?","furigana":"つく|こむぎこ|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"呼吸 is breathing and, as a する-verb, \"to breathe\"; it sounds more matter-of-fact and medical than 息 or 息をする. Typical are 呼吸を整える \"to calm one's breathing\", 呼吸が苦しい \"to have difficulty breathing\" and 深呼吸 \"to take a deep breath\". Figuratively 呼吸が合う means that people are perfectly in tune with each other.","examples":[{"japanese":"ゆっくり呼吸してください。","romaji":"Yukkuri kokyū shite kudasai.","german":"Please breathe slowly.","furigana":"こきゅう"},{"japanese":"走った後で呼吸を整えました。","romaji":"Hashitta ato de kokyū o totonoemashita.","german":"After running, I caught my breath.","furigana":"はし|あと|こきゅう|ととの"},{"japanese":"二人は呼吸がぴったり合っていました。","romaji":"Futari wa kokyuu ga pittari atte imashita.","german":"The two of them were perfectly in sync.","furigana":"ふたり|こきゅう|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"苦痛 refers to physical or mental pain and distress. It sounds more formal than 痛み (concrete pain) or 苦しみ (suffering). It is often used with 〜にとって苦痛だ, 苦痛を感じる, 苦痛を和らげる. It also often describes things one finds unbearably annoying, such as long waits or crowded trains: 〜が苦痛だ.","examples":[{"japanese":"長く待つことは彼にとって苦痛でした。","romaji":"Nagaku matsu koto wa kare ni totte kutsū deshita.","german":"Waiting a long time was torture for him.","furigana":"なが|ま|かれ|くつう"},{"japanese":"痛み止めで苦痛が和らぎました。","romaji":"Itamidome de kutsū ga yawaragimashita.","german":"The painkiller eased the pain.","furigana":"いた|ど|くつう|やわ"},{"japanese":"毎朝の満員電車が苦痛でたまりません。","romaji":"Maiasa no man'in densha ga kutsuu de tamarimasen.","german":"The packed train every morning is unbearably painful for me.","furigana":"まいあさ|まんいんでんしゃ|くつう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"アイスクリーム is ice cream; in daily life people almost always say just アイス, and アイス alone can also mean an ice pop. Soft-serve is ソフトクリーム, shaved ice with syrup かき氷. Flavors are named with 〜味: バニラ味のアイスクリーム. アイスクリームが溶ける means \"the ice cream melts\".","examples":[{"japanese":"暑いのでアイスクリームを買いました。","romaji":"Atsui node aisukurīmu o kaimashita.","german":"It's hot, so I bought ice cream.","furigana":"あつ|か"},{"japanese":"アイスクリームが溶ける前に食べてください。","romaji":"Aisukurīmu ga tokeru mae ni tabete kudasai.","german":"Please eat the ice cream before it melts.","furigana":"と|まえ|た"},{"japanese":"コンビニでアイスを買って帰ろう。","romaji":"Konbini de aisu o katte kaerou.","german":"Let's buy some ice cream at the convenience store and head home.","furigana":"か|かえ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 11"},
{"notes":"チーズ is cheese. Typical combinations are チーズを載せる \"to put cheese on top\", チーズを溶かす, 粉チーズ \"grated Parmesan\" and チーズケーキ. When taking a photo, people say はい、チーズ so that everyone smiles. In Japanese supermarkets, sliced processed cheese (スライスチーズ) is especially common.","examples":[{"japanese":"パンにチーズを載せて焼きます。","romaji":"Pan ni chīzu o nosete yakimasu.","german":"I put cheese on the bread and bake it.","furigana":"の|や"},{"japanese":"このチーズは塩の味が強いです。","romaji":"Kono chīzu wa shio no aji ga tsuyoi desu.","german":"This cheese has a strong salty taste.","furigana":"しお|あじ|つよ"},{"japanese":"写真を撮る時は「はい、チーズ」と言います。","romaji":"Shashin o toru toki wa \"hai, chiizu\" to iimasu.","german":"When taking a photo, people say \"はい、チーズ\".","furigana":"しゃしん|と|とき|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"スープ refers mainly to Western soups such as コーンスープ or 野菜スープ, and also to the broth of ramen (ラーメンのスープ). Japanese soups like 味噌汁, by contrast, are called 汁 or お吸い物. In Japanese, soup is not eaten but drunk: スープを飲む. To cook it is スープを作る.","examples":[{"japanese":"野菜を使ってスープを作りました。","romaji":"Yasai o tsukatte sūpu o tsukurimashita.","german":"I made soup using vegetables.","furigana":"やさい|つか|つく"},{"japanese":"スープが熱いので気を付けてください。","romaji":"Sūpu ga atsui node ki o tsukete kudasai.","german":"The soup is hot, so please be careful.","furigana":"あつ|き|つ"},{"japanese":"寒い日は温かいスープを飲むと体が温まります。","romaji":"Samui hi wa atatakai suupu o nomu to karada ga atatamarimasu.","german":"On cold days, drinking warm soup warms the body.","furigana":"さむ|ひ|あたた|の|からだ|あたた"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"カード is a card in the broadest sense. カードで払う usually means by credit card (クレジットカード); also ポイントカード, 図書館のカード and 誕生日カード. For playing cards, people in daily life tend to say トランプ. Figuratively, カード also stands for a trump card in negotiations: 最後のカード.","examples":[{"japanese":"カードで払ってもいいですか。","romaji":"Kādo de haratte mo ii desu ka.","german":"Can I pay by card?","furigana":"はら"},{"japanese":"図書館のカードを作りました。","romaji":"Toshokan no kādo o tsukurimashita.","german":"I made a library card.","furigana":"としょかん|つく"},{"japanese":"誕生日カードを書いて、友達に送りました。","romaji":"Tanjoubi kaado o kaite, tomodachi ni okurimashita.","german":"I wrote a birthday card and sent it to a friend.","furigana":"たんじょうび|か|ともだち|おく"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 2"},
{"notes":"オフィス means office space or the office workplace and sounds modern; オフィス街 is a district of office towers. 事務所 is the classic word for an office, also for law firms or agencies. The daily commute is usually expressed with 会社に行く, not with オフィス. 職場 means \"workplace\" in general.","examples":[{"japanese":"新しいオフィスは駅に近いです。","romaji":"Atarashii ofisu wa eki ni chikai desu.","german":"The new office is close to the station.","furigana":"あたら|えき|ちか"},{"japanese":"今日はオフィスではなく家で働きます。","romaji":"Kyō wa ofisu de wa naku ie de hatarakimasu.","german":"Today I'm working at home, not at the office.","furigana":"きょう|いえ|はたら"},{"japanese":"昼になると、オフィス街のレストランは込みます。","romaji":"Hiru ni naru to, ofisugai no resutoran wa komimasu.","german":"At noon, the restaurants in the office district get crowded.","furigana":"ひる|がい|こ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"グループ is a group of people or things that belong together: グループで, グループに分かれる \"to split into groups\", グループを作る. Corporate groups (〜グループ) and chat groups are also called this. 団体 is more formal and refers to organized groups, as in 団体料金 \"group rate\". チーム stresses a shared goal, 仲間 personal bonds.","examples":[{"japanese":"四人のグループで話し合ってください。","romaji":"Yonin no gurūpu de hanashiatte kudasai.","german":"Please discuss it in groups of four.","furigana":"よにん|はな|あ"},{"japanese":"同じグループの人に自己紹介しました。","romaji":"Onaji gurūpu no hito ni jikoshōkai shimashita.","german":"I introduced myself to the people in my group.","furigana":"おな|ひと|じこしょうかい"},{"japanese":"グループで旅行すると、料金が安くなります。","romaji":"Guruupu de ryokou suru to, ryoukin ga yasuku narimasu.","german":"Traveling as a group makes it cheaper.","furigana":"りょこう|りょうきん|やす"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 25"},
{"notes":"ケース has two main meanings: a container or cover (眼鏡ケース, スマホのケース, ビール一ケース \"a case of beer\") and a case in the sense of a situation (よくあるケース, このようなケース). ケースバイケース means \"it depends on the individual case\". In the sense of \"case\", 場合 is more neutral and grammatically versatile (〜場合は); ケース sounds businesslike.","examples":[{"japanese":"このケースでは予約が必要です。","romaji":"Kono kēsu de wa yoyaku ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"In this case, a reservation is required.","furigana":"よやく|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"眼鏡をケースに入れました。","romaji":"Megane o kēsu ni iremashita.","german":"I put my glasses in the case.","furigana":"めがね|い"},{"japanese":"スマホのケースを新しくしました。","romaji":"Sumaho no keesu o atarashiku shimashita.","german":"I got a new case for my smartphone.","furigana":"あたら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"プラン is a plan or project (旅行のプラン, プランを立てる) and often a selectable package or rate: 宿泊プラン, 料金プラン. 計画 is the more general, more serious word for planning, while 予定 is a fixed appointment or schedule. プラン sounds casual and is common in advertising and booking sites.","examples":[{"japanese":"旅行のプランを立てました。","romaji":"Ryokō no puran o tatemashita.","german":"I made a travel plan.","furigana":"りょこう|た"},{"japanese":"朝食付きのプランを選びました。","romaji":"Chōshokutsuki no puran o erabimashita.","german":"I chose the plan that includes breakfast.","furigana":"ちょうしょくつ|えら"},{"japanese":"携帯の料金プランを安いものに変えました。","romaji":"Keitai no ryoukin puran o yasui mono ni kaemashita.","german":"I switched my mobile phone rate plan to a cheaper one.","furigana":"けいたい|りょうきん|やす|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"マイク is the usual short form of マイクロホン \"microphone\". Typical are マイクを使う, マイクを通して話す and マイクを持つ. When a microphone is switched off, one says マイクが入っていない. At karaoke and events the word is everywhere, and the long form is hardly ever heard.","examples":[{"japanese":"マイクを使って話してください。","romaji":"Maiku o tsukatte hanashite kudasai.","german":"Please use the microphone when you speak.","furigana":"つか|はな"},{"japanese":"発表の前にマイクを確認しました。","romaji":"Happyō no mae ni maiku o kakunin shimashita.","german":"I checked the microphone before the presentation.","furigana":"はっぴょう|まえ|かくにん"},{"japanese":"カラオケでマイクを持つと、彼はなかなか離しません。","romaji":"Karaoke de maiku o motsu to, kare wa nakanaka hanashimasen.","german":"Once he picks up the microphone at karaoke, he hardly lets go.","furigana":"も|かれ|はな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"チャンス is a favorable opportunity, often one you can seize or miss. Typical combinations: チャンスをつかむ \"to seize a chance\", チャンスを生かす \"to make use of an opportunity\", チャンスを逃す \"to miss a chance\" and 今がチャンスだ \"now is the right moment\". The Sino-Japanese 機会 is more neutral and formal and stands more for a plain \"opportunity\" without the overtone of luck or success.","examples":[{"japanese":"海外で働くチャンスを得ました。","romaji":"Kaigai de hataraku chansu o emashita.","german":"I got the chance to work overseas.","furigana":"かいがい|はたら|え"},{"japanese":"失敗しても次のチャンスがあります。","romaji":"Shippai shite mo tsugi no chansu ga arimasu.","german":"Even if you fail, there's another chance.","furigana":"しっぱい|つぎ"},{"japanese":"セールは今日までだから、買うなら今がチャンスだよ。","romaji":"Sēru wa kyō made da kara, kau nara ima ga chansu da yo.","german":"The sale ends today, so if you're going to buy, now's your chance.","furigana":"きょう|か|いま"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 25"},
{"notes":"ブレーキ is the brake on a car, bicycle or train. \"To brake\" is ブレーキをかける or, with a view to the pedal, ブレーキを踏む; an emergency stop is 急ブレーキ. Intransitively one says ブレーキがかかる \"the brake engages\". Figuratively it means slowing down a development: 景気にブレーキがかかる \"the economy is slowed down\".","examples":[{"japanese":"危ないのでブレーキをかけました。","romaji":"Abunai node burēki o kakemashita.","german":"It was dangerous, so I hit the brakes.","furigana":"あぶ"},{"japanese":"自転車のブレーキを修理しました。","romaji":"Jitensha no burēki o shūri shimashita.","german":"I repaired my bicycle's brakes.","furigana":"じてんしゃ|しゅうり"},{"japanese":"円高で、輸出の伸びにブレーキがかかった。","romaji":"Endaka de, yushutsu no nobi ni burēki ga kakatta.","german":"The strong yen put the brakes on export growth.","furigana":"えんだか|ゆしゅつ|の"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"ベンチ is a bench, for example in a park, on a platform or at a bus stop - not a bank for money (that would be 銀行). One says ベンチに座る \"to sit on the bench\", but ベンチで休む \"to rest on the bench\". In sports, ベンチ is also the substitutes' or coaches' bench; ベンチ入り means \"to make the game-day roster\".","examples":[{"japanese":"公園のベンチで休みました。","romaji":"Kōen no benchi de yasumimashita.","german":"I rested on a park bench.","furigana":"こうえん|やす"},{"japanese":"ベンチの上に忘れ物がありました。","romaji":"Benchi no ue ni wasuremono ga arimashita.","german":"There was a lost item on the bench.","furigana":"うえ|わす|もの"},{"japanese":"駅のベンチに座って、次の電車を待ちました。","romaji":"Eki no benchi ni suwatte, tsugi no densha o machimashita.","german":"I sat on a bench at the station and waited for the next train.","furigana":"えき|すわ|つぎ|でんしゃ|ま"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"ボール is a ball for sports and play; typical are ボールを投げる \"to throw\", ボールを蹴る \"to kick\" and ボールを打つ \"to hit\". Compounds such as サッカーボール or テニスボール are very common. The bowl (dish) is mostly written ボウル, although both are pronounced the same; in baseball, ボール also means a pitch outside the strike zone.","examples":[{"japanese":"子供がボールを投げています。","romaji":"Kodomo ga bōru o nagete imasu.","german":"The child is throwing a ball.","furigana":"こども|な"},{"japanese":"ボールが道路に転がりました。","romaji":"Bōru ga dōro ni korogarimashita.","german":"The ball rolled onto the road.","furigana":"どうろ|ころ"},{"japanese":"誕生日に新しいサッカーボールを買ってもらいました。","romaji":"Tanjōbi ni atarashii sakkā bōru o katte moraimashita.","german":"For my birthday I got a new soccer ball.","furigana":"たんじょうび|あたら|か"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 33"},
{"notes":"スタイル means, on the one hand, \"style, manner\" (生活スタイル, 学習スタイル, ヘアスタイル). On the other hand, スタイルがいい does not mean \"stylish\" but \"to have a good figure\", i.e. to be slim and well proportioned. For \"fashionable, chic\", おしゃれ is used instead.","examples":[{"japanese":"自分に合う学習スタイルを見つけました。","romaji":"Jibun ni au gakushū sutairu o mitsukemashita.","german":"I found a learning style that suits me.","furigana":"じぶん|あ|がくしゅう|み"},{"japanese":"この店は昔のスタイルを大切にしています。","romaji":"Kono mise wa mukashi no sutairu o taisetsu ni shite imasu.","german":"This shop values the old style.","furigana":"みせ|むかし|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"姉は背が高くて、スタイルがいいです。","romaji":"Ane wa se ga takakute, sutairu ga ii desu.","german":"My older sister is tall and has a good figure.","furigana":"あね|せ|たか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"スピーチ is usually a short, prepared speech before an audience, such as at weddings, parties or in school; you say スピーチをする. 演説 is the formal, often political speech, and 発表 is more a talk or presentation of results. Also common are スピーチコンテスト and 結婚式のスピーチ.","examples":[{"japanese":"結婚式で短いスピーチをしました。","romaji":"Kekkonshiki de mijikai supīchi o shimashita.","german":"I gave a short speech at the wedding.","furigana":"けっこんしき|みじか"},{"japanese":"スピーチの内容を何度も練習しました。","romaji":"Supīchi no naiyō o nando mo renshū shimashita.","german":"I practiced the content of my speech many times.","furigana":"ないよう|なんど|れんしゅう"},{"japanese":"日本語のスピーチコンテストで二位になりました。","romaji":"Nihongo no supīchi kontesuto de ni-i ni narimashita.","german":"I took second place in the Japanese speech contest.","furigana":"にほんご|にい"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 21"},
{"notes":"ノック is knocking at a door; as a する-verb it takes を: ドアをノックする. The sound is ノックの音, and the knocking itself can be described onomatopoeically as トントン. In baseball, ノック also refers to hitting balls for fielding practice.","examples":[{"japanese":"部屋に入る前にノックしてください。","romaji":"Heya ni hairu mae ni nokku shite kudasai.","german":"Please knock before entering the room.","furigana":"へや|はい|まえ"},{"japanese":"ドアをノックしても返事がありませんでした。","romaji":"Doa o nokku shite mo henji ga arimasen deshita.","german":"I knocked on the door, but there was no answer.","furigana":"へんじ"},{"japanese":"夜中にノックの音がして、目が覚めました。","romaji":"Yonaka ni nokku no oto ga shite, me ga samemashita.","german":"I heard a knocking sound in the middle of the night and woke up.","furigana":"よなか|おと|め|さ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"板 is a flat board of wood, but also a plate of other material, such as ガラスの板 or 鉄の板. It is counted with 枚; typical verbs are 板を切る and 板を張る. A cutting board is called まな板 in daily life. The idiom 板につく means \"to become accustomed to something\", e.g. 制服姿が板についてきた.","examples":[{"japanese":"棚を作るために、板を三枚買いました。","romaji":"Tana o tsukuru tame ni, ita o sanmai kaimashita.","german":"I bought three boards to build a shelf.","furigana":"たな|つく|いた|さんまいか"},{"japanese":"割れたガラスの板には触らないでください。","romaji":"Wareta garasu no ita ni wa sawaranaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't touch the broken sheet of glass.","furigana":"わ|いた|さわ"},{"japanese":"古い家なので、床の板が一枚割れてしまいました。","romaji":"Furui ie na node, yuka no ita ga ichimai warete shimaimashita.","german":"It's an old house, so one of the floorboards has cracked.","furigana":"ふる|いえ|ゆか|いた|いちまいわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"丘 is a gentle, rather low elevation; a real mountain is 山. Typical are 丘の上 \"on top of the hill\", 丘に登る \"to climb a hill\" and 丘を下りる. In place names and housing developments, 〜が丘 is very common. For a small rise in the terrain, 小高い丘 is also used.","examples":[{"japanese":"丘の上から海が見えます。","romaji":"Oka no ue kara umi ga miemasu.","german":"You can see the sea from the top of the hill.","furigana":"おか|うえ|うみ|み"},{"japanese":"子供たちは草の生えた丘を駆け下りた。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa kusa no haeta oka o kakeorita.","german":"The children ran down the grassy hill.","furigana":"こども|くさ|は|おか|か|お"},{"japanese":"週末は家族で近くの丘までハイキングに行きました。","romaji":"Shūmatsu wa kazoku de chikaku no oka made haikingu ni ikimashita.","german":"On the weekend, my family went hiking to a nearby hill.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|かぞく|ちか|おか|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"画家 is a fine-art painter, i.e. someone who paints pictures as art; someone who paints walls is a ペンキ屋 or 塗装工. Typical are 画家になる and 画家の作品. 芸術家 is the broader term \"artist\"; colloquially 絵描き is also used. For comic artists, 漫画家 is used, and for illustrators イラストレーター.","examples":[{"japanese":"姉は画家になるために美術大学で学んでいます。","romaji":"Ane wa gaka ni naru tame ni bijutsu daigaku de manande imasu.","german":"My older sister is studying at an art university to become a painter.","furigana":"あね|がか|びじゅつだいがく|まな"},{"japanese":"この美術館では地元の画家の作品を展示しています。","romaji":"Kono bijutsukan de wa jimoto no gaka no sakuhin o tenji shite imasu.","german":"This museum exhibits works by local painters.","furigana":"びじゅつかん|じもと|がか|さくひん|てんじ"},{"japanese":"ゴッホは生きている間、あまり有名な画家ではなかった。","romaji":"Gohho wa ikite iru aida, amari yūmei na gaka de wa nakatta.","german":"During his lifetime, Van Gogh was not a very famous painter.","furigana":"い|あいだ|ゆうめい|がか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Transitive verb: 〜を嫌う. In everyday speech, the な-adjective 嫌い（〜が嫌いだ）is much more common; 嫌う sounds more written and often describes a general aversion. The passive 嫌われる means \"to be disliked\".","examples":[{"japanese":"猫は水を嫌うと言われています。","romaji":"Neko wa mizu o kirau to iwarete imasu.","german":"Cats are said to dislike water.","furigana":"ねこ|みず|きら|い"},{"japanese":"そんなことを言うと、みんなに嫌われますよ。","romaji":"Sonna koto o iu to, minna ni kirawaremasu yo.","german":"If you say things like that, everyone will dislike you.","furigana":"い|きら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"General term for everything alive, especially animals and insects. More everyday than 生物（せいぶつ）, which is used more in science and school.","examples":[{"japanese":"この池にはどんな生き物がいますか。","romaji":"Kono ike ni wa donna ikimono ga imasu ka.","german":"What kind of creatures live in this pond?","furigana":"いけ|い|もの"},{"japanese":"生き物を飼うなら、最後まで責任を持ちましょう。","romaji":"Ikimono o kau nara, saigo made sekinin o mochimashō.","german":"If you keep a living creature, take responsibility for it to the end.","furigana":"い|もの|か|さいご|せきにん|も"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Today mostly a video recording; formerly also a videocassette or VCR. For clips on the Internet, 動画（どうが）is often used.","examples":[{"japanese":"結婚式のビデオを家族みんなで見ました。","romaji":"Kekkonshiki no bideo o kazoku minna de mimashita.","german":"We watched the wedding video with the whole family.","furigana":"けっこんしき|かぞく|み"},{"japanese":"子どもの運動会をビデオに撮りました。","romaji":"Kodomo no undōkai o bideo ni torimashita.","german":"I filmed my child's sports day on video.","furigana":"こ|うんどうかい|と"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 6"},
{"notes":"Cow's milk for drinking is usually called 牛乳 in daily life; ミルク is used especially for milk in coffee or tea and for infant formula.","examples":[{"japanese":"コーヒーにミルクを入れますか。","romaji":"Kōhī ni miruku o iremasu ka.","german":"Would you like milk in your coffee?","furigana":"い"},{"japanese":"赤ちゃんにミルクをあげる時間です。","romaji":"Akachan ni miruku o ageru jikan desu.","german":"It's time to give the baby his milk.","furigana":"あか|じかん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Pants made of denim; also ジーパン. Pants are put on with はく: ジーンズをはく.","examples":[{"japanese":"週末はいつもジーンズをはいています。","romaji":"Shūmatsu wa itsumo jīnzu o haite imasu.","german":"I always wear jeans on weekends.","furigana":"しゅうまつ"},{"japanese":"この店のジーンズは丈夫で安いです。","romaji":"Kono mise no jīnzu wa jōbu de yasui desu.","german":"The jeans at this store are durable and cheap.","furigana":"みせ|じょうぶ|やす"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 2"},
{"notes":"Refers to the sport and to the skis themselves. \"To go skiing\": スキーに行く; \"to ski\": スキーをする.","examples":[{"japanese":"冬休みに長野へスキーに行きます。","romaji":"Fuyuyasumi ni Nagano e sukī ni ikimasu.","german":"During winter vacation I'm going skiing in Nagano.","furigana":"ふゆやす|ながの|い"},{"japanese":"スキーを借りるのに、いくらかかりますか。","romaji":"Sukī o kariru no ni, ikura kakarimasu ka.","german":"How much does it cost to rent skis?","furigana":"か"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 13"},
{"notes":"Mostly Western or modern dance; as a verb, ダンスする. Traditional Japanese dance is more often called 踊り（おどり）.","examples":[{"japanese":"妹は毎週ダンスを習っています。","romaji":"Imōto wa maishū dansu o naratte imasu.","german":"My younger sister takes dance lessons every week.","furigana":"いもうと|まいしゅう|なら"},{"japanese":"パーティーでみんなとダンスをしました。","romaji":"Pātī de minna to dansu o shimashita.","german":"At the party I danced with everyone."}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 9"},
{"notes":"Comes after the number: 50パーセント; often written with %. \"... percent off\" is …パーセント引き.","examples":[{"japanese":"この店では全品二十パーセント引きです。","romaji":"Kono mise de wa zenpin nijū pāsento biki desu.","german":"All items in this store are 20 percent off.","furigana":"みせ|ぜんぴんにじゅう|び"},{"japanese":"クラスの八十パーセントの学生が試験に合格しました。","romaji":"Kurasu no hachijū pāsento no gakusei ga shiken ni gōkaku shimashita.","german":"Eighty percent of the students in the class passed the exam.","furigana":"はちじゅう|がくせい|しけん|ごうかく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Noun from 余る (to be left over). The identically pronounced adverb あまり (\"not very\", used with negation) is a separate entry and is usually written in hiragana. In mathematics: 割り算の余り \"remainder of a division\".","examples":[{"japanese":"布の余りで小さなかばんを作りました。","romaji":"Nuno no amari de chiisana kaban o tsukurimashita.","german":"I made a small bag from the leftover fabric.","furigana":"ぬの|あま|ちい|つく"},{"japanese":"十を三で割ると、余りは一です。","romaji":"Jū o san de waru to, amari wa ichi desu.","german":"If you divide ten by three, the remainder is one.","furigana":"じゅう|さん|わ|あま|いち"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"notes":"兆 ist die Zahlstufe nach 億: 一兆（いっちょう） = 10^12, auf Deutsch eine „Billion“, nicht „Milliarde“ (das englische „trillion“ entspricht der deutschen Billion). Man sagt 一兆円, 千兆円. Die zweite Bedeutung „Anzeichen, Vorzeichen“ ist gehoben und erscheint fast nur in Komposita wie 前兆 „Vorzeichen“ und 兆候 „Anzeichen, Symptom“; als eigenständiges Wort sagt man dafür 兆し（きざし）.","examples":[{"japanese":"国の借金は千兆円を超えています。","romaji":"Kuni no shakkin wa senchō en o koete imasu.","german":"Die Staatsschulden übersteigen 1.000 Billionen Yen.","furigana":"くに|しゃっきん|せんちょうえん|こ"},{"japanese":"この会社の売り上げは一兆円に達しました。","romaji":"Kono kaisha no uriage wa itchō en ni tasshimashita.","german":"Der Umsatz dieses Unternehmens hat eine Billion Yen erreicht.","furigana":"かいしゃ|う|あ|いっちょうえん|たっ"},{"japanese":"動物のおかしな行動を、地震の前兆だと言う人もいる。","romaji":"Doubutsu no okashina koudou o, jishin no zenchou da to iu hito mo iru.","german":"Manche halten seltsames Verhalten von Tieren für ein Vorzeichen eines Erdbebens.","furigana":"どうぶつ|こうどう|じしん|ぜんちょう|い|ひと"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Grape wine; rice wine is called 日本酒. Red wine is 赤ワイン, white wine 白ワイン.","examples":[{"japanese":"夕食に赤ワインを一杯飲みました。","romaji":"Yūshoku ni aka wain o ippai nomimashita.","german":"I had a glass of red wine with dinner.","furigana":"ゆうしょく|あか|いっぱいの"},{"japanese":"このワインはフランスから輸入されたものです。","romaji":"Kono wain wa Furansu kara yunyū sareta mono desu.","german":"This wine was imported from France.","furigana":"ゆにゅう"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 3"},
{"notes":"Mostly a television series（テレビドラマ）. Figuratively also a dramatic event: ドラマのような話.","examples":[{"japanese":"毎週水曜日に好きなドラマを見ています。","romaji":"Maishū suiyōbi ni suki na dorama o mite imasu.","german":"Every Wednesday I watch my favorite drama.","furigana":"まいしゅうすいようび|す|み"},{"japanese":"二人の出会いは、まるでドラマのようでした。","romaji":"Futari no deai wa, marude dorama no yō deshita.","german":"The way the two met was just like a drama.","furigana":"ふたり|であ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 28"},
{"notes":"A romantic meeting between two people; as a verb デートする. A calendar date is 日付, not デート.","examples":[{"japanese":"日曜日に彼女とデートします。","romaji":"Nichiyōbi ni kanojo to dēto shimasu.","german":"I have a date with my girlfriend on Sunday.","furigana":"にちようび|かのじょ"},{"japanese":"初めてのデートで映画を見に行きました。","romaji":"Hajimete no dēto de eiga o mi ni ikimashita.","german":"On our first date we went to see a movie.","furigana":"はじ|えいが|み|い"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 3"},
{"notes":"Not a public holiday in Japan. Christmas Eve (クリスマスイブ) is seen more as an evening for couples; many people eat chicken and クリスマスケーキ.","examples":[{"japanese":"クリスマスに家族とケーキを食べました。","romaji":"Kurisumasu ni kazoku to kēki o tabemashita.","german":"I ate cake with my family at Christmas.","furigana":"かぞく|た"},{"japanese":"十二月になると、町はクリスマスの飾りでいっぱいになります。","romaji":"Jūnigatsu ni naru to, machi wa kurisumasu no kazari de ippai ni narimasu.","german":"When December comes, the town is full of Christmas decorations.","furigana":"じゅうにがつ|まち|かざ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 7"},
{"notes":"Country name in katakana. Known for watches (スイスの時計) and the Alps (アルプス).","examples":[{"japanese":"スイスの時計は高いけれど丈夫です。","romaji":"Suisu no tokei wa takai keredo jōbu desu.","german":"Swiss watches are expensive but durable.","furigana":"とけい|たか|じょうぶ"},{"japanese":"スイスでは四つの言葉が話されています。","romaji":"Suisu de wa yottsu no kotoba ga hanasarete imasu.","german":"Four languages are spoken in Switzerland.","furigana":"よっ|ことば|はな"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 3"},
{"notes":"Country name in katakana. Easily confused with オーストラリア (Australia); Japanese people joke about this themselves.","examples":[{"japanese":"ウィーンはオーストリアの首都です。","romaji":"Wīn wa Ōsutoria no shuto desu.","german":"Vienna is the capital of Austria.","furigana":"しゅと"},{"japanese":"オーストリアとオーストラリアをよく間違えます。","romaji":"Ōsutoria to Ōsutoraria o yoku machigaemasu.","german":"I often mix up Austria and Australia.","furigana":"まちが"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Country name (from Portuguese \"Holanda\"). Historically important: in the Edo period, the Dutch (on Dejima in Nagasaki) were the only Europeans allowed to trade with Japan; hence 蘭学（らんがく）\"Dutch learning\" for Western sciences.","examples":[{"japanese":"オランダは花と風車で有名です。","romaji":"Oranda wa hana to fūsha de yūmei desu.","german":"The Netherlands is famous for flowers and windmills.","furigana":"はな|ふうしゃ|ゆうめい"},{"japanese":"江戸時代、日本はオランダと貿易をしていました。","romaji":"Edo jidai, Nihon wa Oranda to bōeki o shite imashita.","german":"In the Edo period, Japan traded with the Netherlands.","furigana":"えどじだい|にほん|ぼうえき"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Country name in katakana. No plural: フィリピン stands for the whole island nation.","examples":[{"japanese":"フィリピンには島がたくさんあります。","romaji":"Firipin ni wa shima ga takusan arimasu.","german":"The Philippines has many islands.","furigana":"しま"},{"japanese":"フィリピンで英語の授業を受けました。","romaji":"Firipin de eigo no jugyō o ukemashita.","german":"I took English classes in the Philippines.","furigana":"えいご|じゅぎょう|う"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Country name in katakana. Do not confuse it with インド (India).","examples":[{"japanese":"バリ島はインドネシアにあります。","romaji":"Baritō wa Indoneshia ni arimasu.","german":"Bali is in Indonesia.","furigana":"とう"},{"japanese":"インドネシアから来た同僚と一緒に働いています。","romaji":"Indoneshia kara kita dōryō to issho ni hataraite imasu.","german":"I work with a colleague from Indonesia.","furigana":"き|どうりょう|いっしょ|はたら"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 1"},
{"notes":"Country name in katakana. Mexican food: メキシコ料理.","examples":[{"japanese":"メキシコの料理は辛いものが多いです。","romaji":"Mekishiko no ryōri wa karai mono ga ōi desu.","german":"Mexican cuisine has many spicy dishes.","furigana":"りょうり|から|おお"},{"japanese":"メキシコはアメリカの南にあります。","romaji":"Mekishiko wa Amerika no minami ni arimasu.","german":"Mexico is south of the United States.","furigana":"みなみ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 6"},
{"notes":"Country name in katakana. Pyramids: ピラミッド.","examples":[{"japanese":"いつかエジプトでピラミッドを見たいです。","romaji":"Itsuka Ejiputo de piramiddo o mitai desu.","german":"Someday I want to see the pyramids in Egypt.","furigana":"み"},{"japanese":"エジプトは雨があまり降りません。","romaji":"Ejiputo wa ame ga amari furimasen.","german":"It doesn't rain much in Egypt.","furigana":"あめ|ふ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Country name in katakana (since 2022 also officially テュルキエ, but in daily life mostly トルコ).","examples":[{"japanese":"トルコはヨーロッパとアジアの間にあります。","romaji":"Toruko wa Yōroppa to Ajia no aida ni arimasu.","german":"Turkey lies between Europe and Asia.","furigana":"あいだ"},{"japanese":"トルコのアイスクリームはよく伸びます。","romaji":"Toruko no aisukurīmu wa yoku nobimasu.","german":"Turkish ice cream is very stretchy.","furigana":"の"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Wörtlich „Sprache des Mutterlandes“; im Alltag häufiger als das sprachwissenschaftliche 母語（ぼご）, das keine Staatszugehörigkeit voraussetzt.","examples":[{"japanese":"私の母国語はドイツ語です。","romaji":"Watashi no bokokugo wa doitsugo desu.","german":"Meine Muttersprache ist Deutsch.","furigana":"わたし|ぼこくご|ご"},{"japanese":"疲れると、つい母国語で話してしまいます。","romaji":"Tsukareru to, tsui bokokugo de hanashite shimaimasu.","german":"Wenn ich müde bin, spreche ich unwillkürlich in meiner Muttersprache.","furigana":"つか|ぼこくご|はな"}],"levelNote":"Compound: 母国 (N1) + 語 (N5)"},
{"notes":"As a verb with する: 写真をアップロードする. Colloquially often shortened to アップする.","examples":[{"japanese":"旅行の写真をネットにアップロードしました。","romaji":"Ryokō no shashin o netto ni appurōdo shimashita.","german":"I uploaded my travel photos to the internet.","furigana":"りょこう|しゃしん"},{"japanese":"ファイルが大きすぎて、アップロードできません。","romaji":"Fairu ga ōkisugite, appurōdo dekimasen.","german":"The file is too large, so I can't upload it.","furigana":"おお"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"As a verb with する. On computers that aren't yours: 使ったあとは必ずログアウトする.","examples":[{"japanese":"共用のパソコンでは必ずログアウトしてください。","romaji":"Kyōyō no pasokon de wa kanarazu roguauto shite kudasai.","german":"On a shared computer, be sure to log out.","furigana":"きょうよう|かなら"},{"japanese":"しばらく操作しないと、自動的にログアウトされます。","romaji":"Shibaraku sōsa shinai to, jidōteki ni roguauto saremasu.","german":"If you don't do anything for a while, you will be logged out automatically.","furigana":"そうさ|じどうてき"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Literally \"moving picture\"; the usual word for online videos (動画を見る／撮る). ビデオ sounds more like a recording or a cassette.","examples":[{"japanese":"寝る前にスマホで動画を見ます。","romaji":"Neru mae ni sumaho de dōga o mimasu.","german":"I watch videos on my smartphone before going to bed.","furigana":"ね|まえ|どうが|み"},{"japanese":"子どもの誕生日を動画で撮りました。","romaji":"Kodomo no tanjōbi o dōga de torimashita.","german":"I filmed my child's birthday on video.","furigana":"こ|たんじょうび|どうが|と"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Full form; in conversation usually shortened to サイト or (increasingly outdated) ホームページ.","examples":[{"japanese":"市のウェブサイトにごみの出し方が載っています。","romaji":"Shi no webusaito ni gomi no dashikata ga notte imasu.","german":"The city's website has information on how to dispose of garbage.","furigana":"し|だ|かた|の"},{"japanese":"新しいウェブサイトを作る仕事をしています。","romaji":"Atarashii webusaito o tsukuru shigoto o shite imasu.","german":"I work on building new websites.","furigana":"あたら|つく|しごと"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Written in Latin letters (SNS, also full-width ＳＮＳ) and read エスエヌエス. In Japanese, the usual word for \"social media\" (ソーシャルメディア is less common).","examples":[{"japanese":"SNSで友達の近況を知りました。","romaji":"Esuenuesu de tomodachi no kinkyō o shirimashita.","german":"I found out how my friends are doing through social media.","furigana":"ともだち|きんきょう|し"},{"japanese":"個人情報をSNSに書かないほうがいいです。","romaji":"Kojin jōhō o esuenuesu ni kakanai hō ga ii desu.","german":"It's better not to post personal information on social media.","furigana":"こじんじょうほう|か"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Indicator in messenger apps such as LINE. 既読スルー／既読無視: to read a message but not reply. Opposite: 未読（みどく）.","examples":[{"japanese":"メッセージに既読がついたのに、返事が来ません。","romaji":"Messēji ni kidoku ga tsuita noni, henji ga kimasen.","german":"My message shows as read, but I haven't gotten a reply.","furigana":"きどく|へんじ|き"},{"japanese":"既読スルーされると、少し寂しいです。","romaji":"Kidoku surū sareru to, sukoshi sabishii desu.","german":"When someone leaves my message on read, I feel a little lonely.","furigana":"きどく|すこ|さび"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"As a verb with する. On social media: アカウントをフォローする. At work it also means \"to support someone, to cover for a mistake\" (部下をフォローする).","examples":[{"japanese":"好きな歌手をSNSでフォローしています。","romaji":"Suki na kashu o esuenuesu de forō shite imasu.","german":"I follow my favorite singer on social media.","furigana":"す|かしゅ"},{"japanese":"新人のミスを先輩がフォローしました。","romaji":"Shinjin no misu o senpai ga forō shimashita.","german":"A senior colleague covered for the newcomer's mistake.","furigana":"しんじん|せんぱい"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"\"To write a blog\": ブログを書く. A post is called 記事（きじ）.","examples":[{"japanese":"毎日の料理をブログに書いています。","romaji":"Mainichi no ryōri o burogu ni kaite imasu.","german":"I write about my daily cooking on my blog.","furigana":"まいにち|りょうり|か"},{"japanese":"そのブログで日本の生活について読みました。","romaji":"Sono burogu de Nihon no seikatsu ni tsuite yomimashita.","german":"I read about life in Japan on that blog.","furigana":"にほん|せいかつ|よ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"A user of a product or service. Username: ユーザー名. Also written ユーザ.","examples":[{"japanese":"このアプリのユーザーは若い人が多いです。","romaji":"Kono apuri no yūzā wa wakai hito ga ōi desu.","german":"Many of this app's users are young people.","furigana":"わか|ひと|おお"},{"japanese":"ユーザー名とパスワードを入力してください。","romaji":"Yūzāmei to pasuwādo o nyūryoku shite kudasai.","german":"Please enter your username and password.","furigana":"めい|にゅうりょく"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"充電 (charging) + 器 (device). Portable battery pack: モバイルバッテリー.","examples":[{"japanese":"充電器を家に忘れてしまいました。","romaji":"Jūdenki o ie ni wasurete shimaimashita.","german":"I left my charger at home.","furigana":"じゅうでんき|いえ|わす"},{"japanese":"すみません、充電器を貸してもらえませんか。","romaji":"Sumimasen, jūdenki o kashite moraemasen ka.","german":"Excuse me, could I borrow a charger?","furigana":"じゅうでんき|か"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Two meanings: a stamp (e.g. スタンプラリー at train stations, a stamp card) and the large stickers in messenger apps such as LINE.","examples":[{"japanese":"返事の代わりにかわいいスタンプを送りました。","romaji":"Henji no kawari ni kawaii sutanpu o okurimashita.","german":"Instead of a reply, I sent a cute sticker.","furigana":"へんじ|か|おく"},{"japanese":"駅でスタンプを押して集めました。","romaji":"Eki de sutanpu o oshite atsumemashita.","german":"I collected stamps at the stations.","furigana":"えき|お|あつ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Display on a mobile phone: 着信あり (call received). Ringtone: 着信音. Opposite: 発信 (outgoing call, sending).","examples":[{"japanese":"会議中に母から着信がありました。","romaji":"Kaigichū ni haha kara chakushin ga arimashita.","german":"I got a call from my mother during the meeting.","furigana":"かいぎちゅう|はは|ちゃくしん"},{"japanese":"着信音を小さくしてください。","romaji":"Chakushin'on o chiisaku shite kudasai.","german":"Please turn down the ringtone.","furigana":"ちゃくしんおん|ちい"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"As a verb with する. Opposite: アンインストール. For apps, people often simply say ダウンロード.","examples":[{"japanese":"新しいソフトをインストールしました。","romaji":"Atarashii sofuto o insutōru shimashita.","german":"I installed new software.","furigana":"あたら"},{"japanese":"インストールが終わるまで電源を切らないでください。","romaji":"Insutōru ga owaru made dengen o kiranaide kudasai.","german":"Please do not turn off the power until the installation is finished.","furigana":"お|でんげん|き"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Also written フォルダー. A paper folder is more often called ファイル.","examples":[{"japanese":"写真を新しいフォルダに入れました。","romaji":"Shashin o atarashii foruda ni iremashita.","german":"I put the photos in a new folder.","furigana":"しゃしん|あたら|い"},{"japanese":"そのファイルはどのフォルダにありますか。","romaji":"Sono fairu wa dono foruda ni arimasu ka.","german":"Which folder is that file in?"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Also a zodiac sign (未年). Wool is 羊毛（ようもう）; lamb meat is ラム, mutton マトン.","examples":[{"japanese":"眠れないときは羊を数えます。","romaji":"Nemurenai toki wa hitsuji o kazoemasu.","german":"When I can't sleep, I count sheep.","furigana":"ねむ|ひつじ|かぞ"},{"japanese":"北海道の牧場で羊にえさをあげました。","romaji":"Hokkaidō no bokujō de hitsuji ni esa o agemashita.","german":"I fed the sheep at a ranch in Hokkaido.","furigana":"ほっかいどう|ぼくじょう|ひつじ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Often written in kana (へび／ヘビ). Zodiac sign: 巳年（みどし）.","examples":[{"japanese":"山道で大きな蛇を見て、びっくりしました。","romaji":"Yamamichi de ōki na hebi o mite, bikkuri shimashita.","german":"I was startled to see a big snake on a mountain path.","furigana":"やまみち|おお|へび|み"},{"japanese":"私は蛇がどうしても苦手です。","romaji":"Watashi wa hebi ga dōshitemo nigate desu.","german":"I just can't stand snakes.","furigana":"わたし|へび|にがて"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Often written in kana (くじら／クジラ). Despite the 魚 radical, it is a mammal. Whale watching: ホエールウォッチング.","examples":[{"japanese":"船から鯨が見えました。","romaji":"Fune kara kujira ga miemashita.","german":"I could see a whale from the boat.","furigana":"ふね|くじら|み"},{"japanese":"鯨は魚ではなく、哺乳類です。","romaji":"Kujira wa sakana de wa naku, honyūrui desu.","german":"A whale is not a fish but a mammal.","furigana":"くじら|さかな|ほにゅうるい"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Often written in kana (やぎ／ヤギ); 山羊 is a jukujikun spelling: the kanji (\"mountain sheep\") convey the meaning, not the reading. Goat's milk: ヤギのミルク.","examples":[{"japanese":"牧場で山羊にえさをあげました。","romaji":"Bokujō de yagi ni esa o agemashita.","german":"I fed the goats at the ranch.","furigana":"ぼくじょう|やぎ"},{"japanese":"山羊は紙を食べると言われますが、本当ですか。","romaji":"Yagi wa kami o taberu to iwaremasu ga, hontō desu ka.","german":"Goats are said to eat paper - is that true?","furigana":"やぎ|かみ|た|い|ほんとう"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"The living animal; the meat is called 鶏肉（とりにく）or simply とり. Zodiac sign: 酉年.","examples":[{"japanese":"祖母の家では鶏を飼っています。","romaji":"Sobo no ie de wa niwatori o katte imasu.","german":"My grandmother keeps chickens at her house.","furigana":"そぼ|いえ|にわとり|か"},{"japanese":"朝早く鶏の鳴き声で目が覚めました。","romaji":"Asa hayaku niwatori no nakigoe de me ga samemashita.","german":"I woke up early in the morning to the crowing of a rooster.","furigana":"あさはや|にわとり|な|ごえ|め|さ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Common in parks and at temples; a symbol of peace (平和の象徴). Often in kana (ハト).","examples":[{"japanese":"公園で鳩にパンをあげないでください。","romaji":"Kōen de hato ni pan o agenaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't feed bread to the pigeons in the park.","furigana":"こうえん|はと"},{"japanese":"鳩は平和のシンボルです。","romaji":"Hato wa heiwa no shinboru desu.","german":"The dove is a symbol of peace.","furigana":"はと|へいわ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"A zoo animal, written in katakana.","examples":[{"japanese":"ゴリラは力がとても強いです。","romaji":"Gorira wa chikara ga totemo tsuyoi desu.","german":"Gorillas are very strong.","furigana":"ちから|つよ"},{"japanese":"動物園のゴリラがこちらをじっと見ていました。","romaji":"Dōbutsuen no gorira ga kochira o jitto mite imashita.","german":"The gorilla at the zoo was staring straight at us.","furigana":"どうぶつえん|み"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"A zoo animal, written in katakana; lives in Australia (オーストラリア).","examples":[{"japanese":"コアラは一日のほとんどを寝て過ごします。","romaji":"Koara wa ichinichi no hotondo o nete sugoshimasu.","german":"Koalas spend almost the whole day sleeping.","furigana":"いちにち|ね|す"},{"japanese":"オーストラリアでコアラを抱っこしました。","romaji":"Ōsutoraria de koara o dakko shimashita.","german":"I held a koala in Australia.","furigana":"だ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Zoo animal in katakana; the pouch is called 袋（ふくろ）.","examples":[{"japanese":"カンガルーはおなかの袋で子どもを育てます。","romaji":"Kangarū wa onaka no fukuro de kodomo o sodatemasu.","german":"Kangaroos raise their young in the pouch on their belly.","furigana":"ふくろ|こ|そだ"},{"japanese":"道路にカンガルーが飛び出してきました。","romaji":"Dōro ni kangarū ga tobidashite kimashita.","german":"A kangaroo jumped out onto the road.","furigana":"どうろ|と|だ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 21"},
{"notes":"Usually written in katakana; kanji: 鮫. Shark fin soup: ふかひれスープ.","examples":[{"japanese":"この海岸にはサメが出ることがあります。","romaji":"Kono kaigan ni wa same ga deru koto ga arimasu.","german":"Sharks sometimes show up on this coast.","furigana":"かいがん|で"},{"japanese":"水族館で大きなサメを見ました。","romaji":"Suizokukan de ōki na same o mimashita.","german":"I saw a big shark at the aquarium.","furigana":"すいぞくかん|おお|み"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"A popular pet (ペット), written in katakana.","examples":[{"japanese":"娘はハムスターを飼いたがっています。","romaji":"Musume wa hamusutā o kaitagatte imasu.","german":"My daughter really wants to keep a hamster.","furigana":"むすめ|か"},{"japanese":"ハムスターは夜になると元気に動き回ります。","romaji":"Hamusutā wa yoru ni naru to genki ni ugokimawarimasu.","german":"At night the hamster becomes lively and runs around.","furigana":"よる|げんき|うご|まわ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Usually written in katakana; often referred to euphemistically as G（ジー）. A common nuisance in the Japanese summer.","examples":[{"japanese":"台所にゴキブリが出て、大騒ぎになりました。","romaji":"Daidokoro ni gokiburi ga dete, ōsawagi ni narimashita.","german":"A cockroach appeared in the kitchen and caused a big commotion.","furigana":"だいどころ|で|おおさわ"},{"japanese":"夏はゴキブリが増えるので、ごみをすぐに捨てます。","romaji":"Natsu wa gokiburi ga fueru node, gomi o sugu ni sutemasu.","german":"There are more cockroaches in summer, so I take the trash out right away.","furigana":"なつ|ふ|す"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"At the cash register you are almost always asked ポイントカードはお持ちですか (Do you have a points card?). If you don't, answer: 持っていません／大丈夫です.","examples":[{"japanese":"ポイントカードはお持ちですか。","romaji":"Pointokādo wa omochi desu ka.","german":"Do you have a points card?","furigana":"も"},{"japanese":"財布がポイントカードでいっぱいです。","romaji":"Saifu ga pointokādo de ippai desu.","german":"My wallet is full of points cards.","furigana":"さいふ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Very common since plastic bags became chargeable in Japan (2020). Counterpart: レジ袋.","examples":[{"japanese":"買い物にはいつもエコバッグを持って行きます。","romaji":"Kaimono ni wa itsumo ekobaggu o motte ikimasu.","german":"I always take a reusable bag when I go shopping.","furigana":"か|もの|も|い"},{"japanese":"このエコバッグは小さくたためて便利です。","romaji":"Kono ekobaggu wa chiisaku tatamete benri desu.","german":"This eco bag folds up small and is handy.","furigana":"ちい|べんり"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"レジ (cash register) + 袋. At the register: レジ袋はご利用ですか (Do you need a bag?). Costs extra since 2020.","examples":[{"japanese":"レジ袋はご利用ですか。","romaji":"Rejibukuro wa goriyō desu ka.","german":"Would you like a bag?","furigana":"ぶくろ|りよう"},{"japanese":"レジ袋が有料になって、使う人が減りました。","romaji":"Rejibukuro ga yūryō ni natte, tsukau hito ga herimashita.","german":"Since bags started costing money, fewer people use them.","furigana":"ぶくろ|ゆうりょう|つか|ひと|へ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Parcel delivery to the front door, for example by Yamato (宅急便 is their brand name). If nobody is home: 不在票 in the mailbox.","examples":[{"japanese":"実家から宅配便で野菜が届きました。","romaji":"Jikka kara takuhaibin de yasai ga todokimashita.","german":"Vegetables arrived from my parents' home by parcel delivery.","furigana":"じっか|たくはいびん|やさい|とど"},{"japanese":"この荷物を宅配便で送りたいんですが。","romaji":"Kono nimotsu o takuhaibin de okuritai n desu ga.","german":"I would like to send this package by parcel delivery.","furigana":"にもつ|たくはいびん|おく"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 46"},
{"notes":"Coin-operated laundry; many have large dryers (乾燥機) suitable for futons.","examples":[{"japanese":"雨の日はコインランドリーで乾かします。","romaji":"Ame no hi wa koinrandorī de kawakashimasu.","german":"On rainy days I dry my clothes at the laundromat.","furigana":"あめ|ひ|かわ"},{"japanese":"アパートの近くにコインランドリーがあります。","romaji":"Apāto no chikaku ni koinrandorī ga arimasu.","german":"There is a laundromat near the apartment.","furigana":"ちか"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"満員 (full to capacity) + 電車. Typical of the rush hour (ラッシュアワー) in Tokyo.","examples":[{"japanese":"毎朝、満員電車で会社に行きます。","romaji":"Maiasa, man'in densha de kaisha ni ikimasu.","german":"I go to work on a packed train every morning.","furigana":"まいあさ|まんいんでんしゃ|かいしゃ|い"},{"japanese":"満員電車の中では、かばんを前に持ちましょう。","romaji":"Man'in densha no naka de wa, kaban o mae ni mochimashō.","german":"On a packed train, let's hold our bags in front of us.","furigana":"まんいんでんしゃ|なか|まえ|も"}],"levelNote":"Compound: 満員 (N2) + 電車 (N5)"},
{"notes":"Working from home or on the go; similar: リモートワーク, 在宅勤務 (N3).","examples":[{"japanese":"週に二日はテレワークです。","romaji":"Shū ni futsuka wa terewāku desu.","german":"I telework two days a week.","furigana":"しゅう|ふつか"},{"japanese":"テレワークで通勤の時間がなくなりました。","romaji":"Terewāku de tsūkin no jikan ga nakunarimashita.","german":"Teleworking has eliminated my commuting time.","furigana":"つうきん|じかん"}],"levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"notes":"Garbage category; also written 燃えるゴミ, officially 可燃ごみ. Opposite: 燃えないごみ. The collection days are in the municipality's garbage calendar.","examples":[{"japanese":"燃えるごみは月曜日と木曜日に出してください。","romaji":"Moeru gomi wa getsuyōbi to mokuyōbi ni dashite kudasai.","german":"Please put out burnable garbage on Mondays and Thursdays.","furigana":"も|げつようび|もくようび|だ"},{"japanese":"紙くずは燃えるごみに入れます。","romaji":"Kamikuzu wa moeru gomi ni iremasu.","german":"Paper scraps go into the burnable garbage.","furigana":"かみ|も|い"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 26"},
{"notes":"Mostly the Japanese macaque (ニホンザル). Proverb: 猿も木から落ちる (Even monkeys fall from trees). Sounding the same: 去る (to leave).","examples":[{"japanese":"日光の山で猿を見ました。","romaji":"Nikkō no yama de saru o mimashita.","german":"I saw monkeys in the mountains of Nikko.","furigana":"にっこう|やま|さる|み"},{"japanese":"猿が観光客の食べ物を取っていきました。","romaji":"Saru ga kankōkyaku no tabemono o totte ikimashita.","german":"A monkey snatched a tourist's food.","furigana":"さる|かんこうきゃく|た|もの|と"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 41"},
{"notes":"Usually written in kana (ねずみ／ネズミ); in everyday Japanese there is no distinction between mouse and rat. Zodiac sign: 子年（ねどし）. Also the color 鼠色 (mouse gray).","examples":[{"japanese":"古い家の天井に鼠がいるようです。","romaji":"Furui ie no tenjō ni nezumi ga iru yō desu.","german":"There seems to be a mouse in the ceiling of the old house.","furigana":"ふる|いえ|てんじょう|ねずみ"},{"japanese":"猫が鼠を追いかけています。","romaji":"Neko ga nezumi o oikakete imasu.","german":"The cat is chasing a mouse.","furigana":"ねこ|ねずみ|お"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Mostly the face mask that people in Japan wear for colds or hay fever: マスクをする／つける. Also a theatrical or facial mask.","examples":[{"japanese":"風邪をひいたので、マスクをしています。","romaji":"Kaze o hiita node, masuku o shite imasu.","german":"I caught a cold, so I'm wearing a mask.","furigana":"かぜ"},{"japanese":"花粉の季節はマスクが手放せません。","romaji":"Kafun no kisetsu wa masuku ga tebanasemasen.","german":"I can't do without a mask during pollen season.","furigana":"かふん|きせつ|てばな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Used with に: 甘いものに目がない (\"to be crazy about sweets\"). Less often also \"to have no eye for something\": 人を見る目がない (to be a poor judge of character).","examples":[{"japanese":"私はチョコレートに目がない。","romaji":"Watashi wa chokorēto ni me ga nai.","german":"I can't resist chocolate.","furigana":"わたし|め"},{"japanese":"彼は人を見る目がないから、よくだまされる。","romaji":"Kare wa hito o miru me ga nai kara, yoku damasareru.","german":"He has no eye for people, so he often gets fooled.","furigana":"かれ|ひと|み|め"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Literally \"bones break\", but it means great effort (骨が折れる仕事 = laborious work). Related: 骨を折る (to make an effort, to go out of one's way for someone).","examples":[{"japanese":"百人分の料理を作るのは骨が折れる。","romaji":"Hyakuninbun no ryōri o tsukuru no wa hone ga oreru.","german":"Cooking for a hundred people is hard work.","furigana":"ひゃくにんぶん|りょうり|つく|ほね|お"},{"japanese":"この資料を全部翻訳するのは骨が折れる仕事だ。","romaji":"Kono shiryō o zenbu hon'yaku suru no wa hone ga oreru shigoto da.","german":"Translating all of these materials is laborious work.","furigana":"しりょう|ぜんぶほんやく|ほね|お|しごと"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"The inner need is satisfied. Common: 気が済むまで (\"until one is satisfied\") and, reproachfully, 何度謝れば気が済むの？","examples":[{"japanese":"気が済むまで泣いていいよ。","romaji":"Ki ga sumu made naite ii yo.","german":"You may cry until you feel better.","furigana":"き|す|な"},{"japanese":"全部自分で確認しないと気が済まない。","romaji":"Zenbu jibun de kakunin shinai to ki ga sumanai.","german":"I can't rest until I have checked everything myself.","furigana":"ぜんぶじぶん|かくにん|き|す"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Several meanings: exaggerated (オーバーな話), excess (予算オーバー = over budget) and overcoat (short for オーバーコート).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼はいつも話がオーバーだ。","romaji":"Kare wa itsumo hanashi ga ōbā da.","german":"He always exaggerates when he tells a story.","furigana":"かれ|はなし"},{"japanese":"今月は予算オーバーになってしまった。","romaji":"Kongetsu wa yosan ōbā ni natte shimatta.","german":"This month I went over budget.","furigana":"こんげつ|よさん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Capital, imperial residence (literary, often historical: Kyoto was the 都 for over 1000 years). Also \"city as a center\": 花の都パリ.","examples":[{"japanese":"京都は長い間、日本の都だった。","romaji":"Kyōto wa nagai aida, Nihon no miyako datta.","german":"Kyoto was the capital of Japan for a long time.","furigana":"きょうと|なが|あいだ|にほん|みやこ"},{"japanese":"パリは「花の都」と呼ばれている。","romaji":"Pari wa \"hana no miyako\" to yobarete iru.","german":"Paris is called the \"city of flowers\".","furigana":"はな|みやこ|よ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Child language for \"mama\"; also the proprietress of a bar (ママさん). Formal: 母; term of address: お母さん.","examples":[{"japanese":"ママ、おなかすいた。","romaji":"Mama, onaka suita.","german":"Mom, I'm hungry."},{"japanese":"ママ友とランチに行きました。","romaji":"Mama tomo to ranchi ni ikimashita.","german":"I went to lunch with the other mothers I'm friends with.","furigana":"とも|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Meeting, gathering, party (歓迎会 = welcome party, 飲み会 = drinking party) and society, club (会に入る). Very productive as a suffix.","examples":[{"japanese":"来週、新入生の歓迎会があります。","romaji":"Raishū, shinnyūsei no kangeikai ga arimasu.","german":"Next week there is a welcome party for the new students.","furigana":"らいしゅう|しんにゅうせい|かんげいかい"},{"japanese":"町の歴史を調べる会に入りました。","romaji":"Machi no rekishi o shiraberu kai ni hairimashita.","german":"I joined a society that researches the town's history.","furigana":"まち|れきし|しら|かい|はい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Ceremony, celebration (式を挙げる = to hold a wedding; 卒業式, 入学式), mathematical formula (式を立てる) and style (日本式 = in the Japanese way).","examples":[{"japanese":"二人は来年の春に式を挙げる予定だ。","romaji":"Futari wa rainen no haru ni shiki o ageru yotei da.","german":"The two plan to hold their wedding next spring.","furigana":"ふたり|らいねん|はる|しき|あ|よてい"},{"japanese":"この問題を解く式を書きなさい。","romaji":"Kono mondai o toku shiki o kakinasai.","german":"Write the equation for solving this problem.","furigana":"もんだい|と|しき|か"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 31"},
{"notes":"Mental capacity, often thought of as measurable: 知能指数 (IQ), 人工知能 (artificial intelligence). For everyday \"smart\", 頭がいい is more usual.","examples":[{"japanese":"イルカは知能が高い動物だと言われている。","romaji":"Iruka wa chinō ga takai dōbutsu da to iwarete iru.","german":"Dolphins are said to be highly intelligent animals.","furigana":"ちのう|たか|どうぶつ|い"},{"japanese":"このテストで子どもの知能を測ります。","romaji":"Kono tesuto de kodomo no chinō o hakarimasu.","german":"This test measures children's intelligence.","furigana":"こ|ちのう|はか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Anatomically the nerve; figuratively the nerves or attention: 神経を使う (to have to concentrate hard), 神経質 (nervous, overly sensitive), 無神経 (insensitive, tactless).","examples":[{"japanese":"歯の神経を取る治療を受けた。","romaji":"Ha no shinkei o toru chiryō o uketa.","german":"I had a treatment in which the nerve of my tooth was removed.","furigana":"は|しんけい|と|ちりょう|う"},{"japanese":"細かい作業なので、とても神経を使う。","romaji":"Komakai sagyō na node, totemo shinkei o tsukau.","german":"It is delicate work, so it takes a lot of concentration.","furigana":"こま|さぎょう|しんけい|つか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"空き refers to a free space, a vacant room or free time. 空きがある reports an available opening; at a hotel it is used to ask about a vacant room. 今日の午後は空きがある means one has free time in the afternoon. The dictionary variant 明き has the same reading あき; 秋 \"autumn\" and 飽き \"weariness\" are different words.","examples":[{"japanese":"今日の午後は少し空きがあります。","romaji":"Kyō no gogo wa sukoshi aki ga arimasu.","german":"I have a little free time this afternoon.","furigana":"きょう|ごご|すこ|あ"},{"japanese":"ホテルに空きがあるか電話で聞いた。","romaji":"Hoteru ni aki ga aru ka denwa de kiita.","german":"I called to ask whether the hotel had any vacancies.","furigana":"あ|でんわ|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Tax in general, often as a suffix: 消費税 (consumption tax), 所得税 (income tax). In everyday speech usually 税金. Price indications: 税込み (tax included), 税抜き (tax excluded).","examples":[{"japanese":"商品の値段に十パーセントの税がかかります。","romaji":"Shōhin no nedan ni juppāsento no zei ga kakarimasu.","german":"A ten percent tax is added to the price of goods.","furigana":"しょうひん|ねだん|じゅっ|ぜい"},{"japanese":"国は国民から税を集めている。","romaji":"Kuni wa kokumin kara zei o atsumete iru.","german":"The state collects taxes from its citizens.","furigana":"くに|こくみん|ぜい|あつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"The plant in the field; harvested, hulled rice is called 米, cooked rice ご飯. Rice harvest: 稲刈り（いねかり）.","examples":[{"japanese":"秋になると、田んぼの稲が黄色くなる。","romaji":"Aki ni naru to, tanbo no ine ga kiiroku naru.","german":"In autumn, the rice plants in the paddies turn yellow.","furigana":"あき|た|いね|きいろ"},{"japanese":"農家の人たちが稲を刈っている。","romaji":"Nōka no hitotachi ga ine o katte iru.","german":"The farmers are harvesting the rice.","furigana":"のうか|ひと|いね|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"A chapter of a book or text: 第一章 (Chapter 1). Also a counter for chapters. Related: 文章 (text, writing).","examples":[{"japanese":"この本は全部で十の章に分かれている。","romaji":"Kono hon wa zenbu de jū no shō ni wakarete iru.","german":"This book is divided into ten chapters in all.","furigana":"ほん|ぜんぶ|じゅう|しょう|わ"},{"japanese":"次の章では、日本の歴史について説明します。","romaji":"Tsugi no shō de wa, Nihon no rekishi ni tsuite setsumei shimasu.","german":"The next chapter explains the history of Japan.","furigana":"つぎ|しょう|にほん|れきし|せつめい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"The outer end or edge: 道の端 (edge of the road), 端から端まで (from one end to the other). Similar sounding: 橋 (bridge) and 箸 (chopsticks). Colloquially also 端っこ.","examples":[{"japanese":"道の端を歩いてください。","romaji":"Michi no hashi o aruite kudasai.","german":"Please walk along the edge of the road.","furigana":"みち|はし|ある"},{"japanese":"部屋の端から端まで掃除した。","romaji":"Heya no hashi kara hashi made sōji shita.","german":"I cleaned the room from one end to the other.","furigana":"へや|はし|はし|そうじ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Several meanings: a knot in wood or bamboo, a joint (指の節), the melody of a song, and \"point, spot\" (思い当たる節がある = something comes to mind). The reading せつ means section or season (季節).","examples":[{"japanese":"竹には節がたくさんある。","romaji":"Take ni wa fushi ga takusan aru.","german":"Bamboo has many nodes.","furigana":"たけ|ふし"},{"japanese":"この歌の節はどこかで聞いたことがある。","romaji":"Kono uta no fushi wa dokoka de kiita koto ga aru.","german":"I have heard the melody of this song somewhere before.","furigana":"うた|ふし|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Basket (買い物かご = shopping basket) or cage (鳥かご). Usually written in hiragana かご.","examples":[{"japanese":"籠いっぱいにみかんを摘んだ。","romaji":"Kago ippai ni mikan o tsunda.","german":"I picked a whole basket full of mandarin oranges.","furigana":"かご|つ"},{"japanese":"鳥を籠から出してやった。","romaji":"Tori o kago kara dashite yatta.","german":"I let the bird out of the cage.","furigana":"とり|かご|だ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"な-adjective: simple, of low quality (粗末な食事). Important: 〜を粗末にする = to treat something carelessly, to waste (食べ物を粗末にする). Modest phrase when giving a gift: 粗末なものですが.","examples":[{"japanese":"食べ物を粗末にしてはいけない。","romaji":"Tabemono o somatsu ni shite wa ikenai.","german":"You must not waste food.","furigana":"た|もの|そまつ"},{"japanese":"彼は粗末な小屋に一人で住んでいた。","romaji":"Kare wa somatsu na koya ni hitori de sunde ita.","german":"He lived alone in a shabby hut.","furigana":"かれ|そまつ|こや|ひとり|す"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"String, cord, shoelace (靴の紐). Colloquially also a derogatory term for a man who lives off his partner.","examples":[{"japanese":"靴の紐がほどけているよ。","romaji":"Kutsu no himo ga hodokete iru yo.","german":"Your shoelace is untied.","furigana":"くつ|ひも"},{"japanese":"古新聞を紐で縛って出した。","romaji":"Furushinbun o himo de shibatte dashita.","german":"I tied up the old newspapers with a string and put them out.","furigana":"ふるしんぶん|ひも|しば|だ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Rank or level, e.g. in exams (漢字検定3級) or weight classes in sports. Compounds: 高級 (high-class), 上級 (advanced), 同級生 (classmate). Similar sounding: 九.","examples":[{"japanese":"漢字検定の三級に合格した。","romaji":"Kanji kentei no sankyū ni gōkaku shita.","german":"I passed level 3 of the Kanji Test.","furigana":"かんじけんてい|さんきゅう|ごうかく"},{"japanese":"彼とは級が違うので、試合では当たらない。","romaji":"Kare to wa kyū ga chigau node, shiai de wa ataranai.","german":"He is in a different class from me, so we won't meet in the competition.","furigana":"かれ|きゅう|ちが|しあい|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"School class (三年二組 = class 2 of the 3rd year), group or team, and a set of things that belong together. As a counter for pairs and groups: 一組.","examples":[{"japanese":"私は三年二組です。","romaji":"Watashi wa sannen nikumi desu.","german":"I am in class 3-2.","furigana":"わたし|さんねんにくみ"},{"japanese":"クラスを四つの組に分けて、ゲームをした。","romaji":"Kurasu o yottsu no kumi ni wakete, gēmu o shita.","german":"We divided the class into four groups and played a game.","furigana":"よっ|くみ|わ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Godan verb, mostly transitive: 腕を組む (to fold one's arms), 足を組む (to cross one's legs), チームを組む (to form a team), 予定を組む (to draw up a plan); intransitive 〜と組む (to team up with someone). Related: 取り組む (to tackle, to work on something).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は腕を組んで考えていた。","romaji":"Kare wa ude o kunde kangaete ita.","german":"He was thinking with his arms crossed.","furigana":"かれ|うで|く|かんが"},{"japanese":"友達とチームを組んで大会に出た。","romaji":"Tomodachi to chīmu o kunde taikai ni deta.","german":"I formed a team with friends and entered the tournament.","furigana":"ともだち|く|たいかい|で"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"A union of people with shared interests: 労働組合 (labor union), 協同組合 (cooperative).","examples":[{"japanese":"父は会社の組合に入っている。","romaji":"Chichi wa kaisha no kumiai ni haitte iru.","german":"My father belongs to his company's union.","furigana":"ちち|かいしゃ|くみあい|はい"},{"japanese":"農家が集まって組合を作った。","romaji":"Nōka ga atsumatte kumiai o tsukutta.","german":"The farmers got together and formed a cooperative.","furigana":"のうか|あつ|くみあい|つく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Continuation of a story, series or activity: 話の続き. As a suffix, \"continuous\": 雨続き. Not to be confused with 手続き (procedures, formalities).","examples":[{"japanese":"このドラマの続きが早く見たい。","romaji":"Kono dorama no tsuzuki ga hayaku mitai.","german":"I want to see the next part of this drama soon.","furigana":"つづ|はや|み"},{"japanese":"話の続きはまた明日しましょう。","romaji":"Hanashi no tsuzuki wa mata ashita shimashō.","german":"Let's continue the conversation tomorrow.","furigana":"はなし|つづ|あした"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"The rim or edge of an object: コップの縁 (rim of a glass), 眼鏡の縁 (rim of glasses). In the reading えん, 縁 means \"fate, bond, relationship\" (ご縁).","examples":[{"japanese":"コップの縁が欠けている。","romaji":"Koppu no fuchi ga kakete iru.","german":"The rim of the cup is chipped.","furigana":"ふち|か"},{"japanese":"黒い縁の眼鏡をかけた男性が来た。","romaji":"Kuroi fuchi no megane o kaketa dansei ga kita.","german":"A man wearing glasses with black frames came.","furigana":"くろ|ふち|めがね|だんせい|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Rope made of straw or hemp. Jump rope: 縄跳び（なわとび）. Related: 縄張り (territory, turf).","examples":[{"japanese":"荷物を縄でしっかり縛った。","romaji":"Nimotsu o nawa de shikkari shibatta.","german":"I tied the luggage up firmly with a rope.","furigana":"にもつ|なわ|しば"},{"japanese":"古い井戸に縄をたらして、深さを測った。","romaji":"Furui ido ni nawa o tarashite, fukasa o hakatta.","german":"I lowered a rope into the old well and measured its depth.","furigana":"ふる|いど|なわ|ふか|はか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Wings of birds and airplanes; poetically also a symbol of freedom and dreams.","examples":[{"japanese":"鳥が大きな翼を広げて飛んでいった。","romaji":"Tori ga ōki na tsubasa o hirogete tonde itta.","german":"The bird spread its large wings and flew away.","furigana":"とり|おお|つばさ|ひろ|と"},{"japanese":"飛行機の翼に雪が積もっている。","romaji":"Hikōki no tsubasa ni yuki ga tsumotte iru.","german":"Snow is piled on the wings of the airplane.","furigana":"ひこうき|つばさ|ゆき|つ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Noun from 老いる (to grow old): aging and its effects. Rather literary; in conversation one says 年を取る. Similar sounding: 甥 (nephew).","examples":[{"japanese":"最近、体の老いを感じる。","romaji":"Saikin, karada no oi o kanjiru.","german":"Lately I feel my body aging.","furigana":"さいきん|からだ|お|かん"},{"japanese":"老いは誰にでも訪れる。","romaji":"Oi wa dare ni demo otozureru.","german":"Old age comes to everyone.","furigana":"お|だれ|おとず"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Person, almost always with a modifier before it: 若い者 (young people), 担当の者 (the person in charge). Humble, about one's own side: 係の者が参ります. As a suffix, read しゃ in 医者 or 学者. Sounding the same: 物 (thing).","examples":[{"japanese":"担当の者が参りますので、少々お待ちください。","romaji":"Tantō no mono ga mairimasu node, shōshō omachi kudasai.","german":"The person in charge will come shortly, so please wait a moment.","furigana":"たんとう|もの|まい|しょうしょう|ま"},{"japanese":"この町の若い者はみんな都会に出てしまった。","romaji":"Kono machi no wakai mono wa minna tokai ni dete shimatta.","german":"The young people of this town have all moved to the city.","furigana":"まち|わか|もの|とかい|で"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 48"},
{"notes":"Occupation or place of work: 職を探す (to look for work), 職を失う (to lose one's job). Idiom: 手に職をつける (to learn a trade). Compounds: 職業, 職場, 就職.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は去年、職を失った。","romaji":"Kare wa kyonen, shoku o ushinatta.","german":"He lost his job last year.","furigana":"かれ|きょねん|しょく|うしな"},{"japanese":"手に職をつけたいと思っている。","romaji":"Te ni shoku o tsuketai to omotte iru.","german":"I want to learn a trade.","furigana":"て|しょく|おも"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Emphasizes a noun or pronoun: 彼自身 (he himself), 自分自身 (oneself). Similar sounding: 地震 (earthquake), 自信 (self-confidence).","examples":[{"japanese":"それは彼自身が決めたことだ。","romaji":"Sore wa kare jishin ga kimeta koto da.","german":"He decided that himself.","furigana":"かれじしん|き"},{"japanese":"先生自身も若いころは勉強が苦手だったそうだ。","romaji":"Sensei jishin mo wakai koro wa benkyō ga nigate datta sō da.","german":"They say that the teacher himself was also bad at studying when he was young.","furigana":"せんせいじしん|わか|べんきょう|にがて"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Tongue: 舌を出す (to stick out one's tongue), 舌をかむ (to bite one's tongue). Similar sounding: 下 (below).","examples":[{"japanese":"熱いスープで舌をやけどした。","romaji":"Atsui sūpu de shita o yakedo shita.","german":"I burned my tongue on the hot soup.","furigana":"あつ|した"},{"japanese":"子どもが舌を出して笑った。","romaji":"Kodomo ga shita o dashite waratta.","german":"The child stuck out his tongue and laughed.","furigana":"こ|した|だ|わら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"A play or performance: 芝居を見る (to go to the theater), 芝居に出る (to act in a play). Colloquially also \"act, show\" in the sense of pretense. 紙芝居 is paper theater.","examples":[{"japanese":"週末に友達と芝居を見に行った。","romaji":"Shūmatsu ni tomodachi to shibai o mi ni itta.","german":"I went to see a play with friends on the weekend.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|ともだち|しばい|み|い"},{"japanese":"子どものころ、学芸会で芝居に出た。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, gakugeikai de shibai ni deta.","german":"When I was a child, I acted in a play at the school festival.","furigana":"こ|がくげいかい|しばい|で"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Sleeve: 半袖 (short-sleeved), 長袖 (long-sleeved), 袖をまくる (to roll up one's sleeves).","examples":[{"japanese":"暑いので、シャツの袖をまくった。","romaji":"Atsui node, shatsu no sode o makutta.","german":"Because it was hot, I rolled up my shirt sleeves.","furigana":"あつ|そで"},{"japanese":"このセーターは袖が少し長い。","romaji":"Kono sētā wa sode ga sukoshi nagai.","german":"The sleeves of this sweater are a little long.","furigana":"そで|すこ|なが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"A rough estimate: 見当がつく (to be able to guess), 見当もつかない (to have no idea), 見当違い (misjudgment). Similar sounding: 検討 (examination, consideration).","examples":[{"japanese":"犯人が誰なのか、全く見当がつかない。","romaji":"Hannin ga dare na no ka, mattaku kentō ga tsukanai.","german":"I have no idea who the culprit is.","furigana":"はんにん|だれ|まった|けんとう"},{"japanese":"駅はだいたいこの辺だと見当をつけて歩いた。","romaji":"Eki wa daitai kono hen da to kentō o tsukete aruita.","german":"I walked on the assumption that the station was roughly around here.","furigana":"えき|へん|けんとう|ある"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Accompanying someone to say goodbye (空港まで見送りに行く); figuratively also shelving a plan (計画は見送りになった). Verb: 見送る.","examples":[{"japanese":"空港まで友達の見送りに行った。","romaji":"Kūkō made tomodachi no miokuri ni itta.","german":"I went to the airport to see a friend off.","furigana":"くうこう|ともだち|みおく|い"},{"japanese":"予算が足りず、計画は見送りになった。","romaji":"Yosan ga tarizu, keikaku wa miokuri ni natta.","german":"The plan was put off for lack of budget.","furigana":"よさん|た|けいかく|みおく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Transitive godan verb: 目を覚ます (to wake up), 眠気を覚ます (to dispel sleepiness), 酔いを覚ます (to sober up). Intransitive: 覚める. Similar sounding: 冷ます (to let cool).","examples":[{"japanese":"大きな音に目を覚ました。","romaji":"Ōki na oto ni me o samashita.","german":"I woke up to a loud noise.","furigana":"おお|おと|め|さ"},{"japanese":"冷たい水で顔を洗って眠気を覚ました。","romaji":"Tsumetai mizu de kao o aratte nemuke o samashita.","german":"I washed my face with cold water to chase away my sleepiness.","furigana":"つめ|みず|かお|あら|ねむけ|さ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Intransitive ichidan verb: 目が覚める (to wake up), 酔いが覚める (to sober up). Transitive: 覚ます. Similar sounding: 冷める (to cool down).","examples":[{"japanese":"今朝は五時に目が覚めた。","romaji":"Kesa wa goji ni me ga sameta.","german":"I woke up at five this morning.","furigana":"けさ|ごじ|め|さ"},{"japanese":"外の風に当たって、酔いが覚めた。","romaji":"Soto no kaze ni atatte, yoi ga sameta.","german":"In the fresh air outside, I sobered up.","furigana":"そと|かぜ|あ|よ|さ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Mostly in 試しに (as a trial): 試しに使ってみる. 〜た試しがない = it has never happened that ... Verb: 試す.","examples":[{"japanese":"試しに新しいレストランに行ってみた。","romaji":"Tameshi ni atarashii resutoran ni itte mita.","german":"I tried out a new restaurant just to see.","furigana":"ため|あたら|い"},{"japanese":"彼が約束を守った試しがない。","romaji":"Kare ga yakusoku o mamotta tameshi ga nai.","german":"He has never once kept a promise.","furigana":"かれ|やくそく|まも|ため"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"A theory or opinion, often alongside others: いろいろな説がある (there are various theories). Compounds: 学説, 仮説, 小説.","examples":[{"japanese":"恐竜が絶滅した理由には、いくつかの説がある。","romaji":"Kyōryū ga zetsumetsu shita riyū ni wa, ikutsuka no setsu ga aru.","german":"There are several theories about why the dinosaurs went extinct.","furigana":"きょうりゅう|ぜつめつ|りゆう|せつ"},{"japanese":"その説は今ではあまり信じられていない。","romaji":"Sono setsu wa ima de wa amari shinjirarete inai.","german":"Hardly anyone believes that theory today.","furigana":"せつ|いま|しん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"The reading of a kanji (音読み, 訓読み), reading itself, and the assessment of a situation (読みが当たる = to predict correctly). Verb: 読む.","examples":[{"japanese":"この漢字の読みを教えてください。","romaji":"Kono kanji no yomi o oshiete kudasai.","german":"Please tell me the reading of this kanji.","furigana":"かんじ|よ|おし"},{"japanese":"監督の読みが当たって、試合に勝った。","romaji":"Kantoku no yomi ga atatte, shiai ni katta.","german":"The coach's judgment was right, and we won the game.","furigana":"かんとく|よ|あ|しあい|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"A lesson in a textbook (第三課 = Lesson 3) and a section in a company or government office (課長 = section chief). Similar sounding: 蚊 (mosquito).","examples":[{"japanese":"今日は第五課を勉強します。","romaji":"Kyō wa daigoka o benkyō shimasu.","german":"Today we will study Lesson 5.","furigana":"きょう|だいごか|べんきょう"},{"japanese":"同じ課の先輩に仕事を教えてもらった。","romaji":"Onaji ka no senpai ni shigoto o oshiete moratta.","german":"A senior colleague from my section taught me the work.","furigana":"おな|か|せんぱい|しごと|おし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Literary ichidan verb: to discuss a topic systematically (問題を論じる). Also 論ずる. In conversation, 話し合う or 議論する is more usual.","examples":[{"japanese":"この本は日本の教育問題を論じている。","romaji":"Kono hon wa Nihon no kyōiku mondai o ronjite iru.","german":"This book discusses Japan's education problems.","furigana":"ほん|にほん|きょういくもんだい|ろん"},{"japanese":"会議で二つの案の長所と短所を論じた。","romaji":"Kaigi de futatsu no an no chōsho to tansho o ronjita.","german":"At the meeting we discussed the advantages and disadvantages of the two proposals.","furigana":"かいぎ|ふた|あん|ちょうしょ|たんしょ|ろん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Noun from 負ける: 勝ち負け (victory and defeat), 負けを認める (to admit defeat). Opposite: 勝ち.","examples":[{"japanese":"今日の試合は三対一でうちの負けだった。","romaji":"Kyō no shiai wa san tai ichi de uchi no make datta.","german":"Today's game was a 3-1 loss for us.","furigana":"きょう|しあい|さんたいいち|ま"},{"japanese":"素直に負けを認めることも大切だ。","romaji":"Sunao ni make o mitomeru koto mo taisetsu da.","german":"It is also important to admit defeat honestly.","furigana":"すなお|ま|みと|たいせつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Assets and property (money, land); figuratively also a valuable asset: 友達は一生の財産だ.","examples":[{"japanese":"祖父は財産を全部寄付した。","romaji":"Sofu wa zaisan o zenbu kifu shita.","german":"My grandfather donated all of his assets.","furigana":"そふ|ざいさん|ぜんぶきふ"},{"japanese":"この経験は私の財産になった。","romaji":"Kono keiken wa watashi no zaisan ni natta.","german":"This experience has become a treasure to me.","furigana":"けいけん|わたし|ざいさん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"What has been lent, or what someone owes you: 〜に貸しがある = someone owes me something (also a favor). Opposite: 借り. As a prefix in 貸し切り, 貸し出し.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼には一万円の貸しがある。","romaji":"Kare ni wa ichiman en no kashi ga aru.","german":"He owes me ten thousand yen.","furigana":"かれ|いちまんえん|か"},{"japanese":"手伝ったんだから、これで君に一つ貸しができたね。","romaji":"Tetsudatta n dakara, kore de kimi ni hitotsu kashi ga dekita ne.","german":"I helped you, so now you owe me one.","furigana":"てつだ|きみ|ひと|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Quality, often in contrast to 量 (quantity): 量より質 (quality over quantity). Compounds: 品質, 性質, 体質. The reading しち means \"pawn\" (質屋).","examples":[{"japanese":"量より質が大切だ。","romaji":"Ryō yori shitsu ga taisetsu da.","german":"Quality is more important than quantity.","furigana":"りょう|しつ|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"この店のサービスは質が高い。","romaji":"Kono mise no sābisu wa shitsu ga takai.","german":"The service at this shop is of high quality.","furigana":"みせ|しつ|たか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"The body and one's own person, especially in set phrases: 身につける (to acquire; to wear), 身を守る (to protect oneself), 相手の身になる (to put oneself in someone's shoes).","examples":[{"japanese":"外国語を身につけるには時間がかかる。","romaji":"Gaikokugo o mi ni tsukeru ni wa jikan ga kakaru.","german":"Learning a foreign language takes time.","furigana":"がいこくご|み|じかん"},{"japanese":"自分の身は自分で守らなければならない。","romaji":"Jibun no mi wa jibun de mamoranakereba naranai.","german":"You have to protect yourself.","furigana":"じぶん|み|じぶん|まも"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"A person's height: 身長が高い／低い, 身長を測る. Similar sounding: 慎重 (cautious).","examples":[{"japanese":"私の身長は百七十センチです。","romaji":"Watashi no shinchō wa hyaku nanajussenchi desu.","german":"I am 170 centimeters tall.","furigana":"わたし|しんちょう|ひゃくななじゅっ"},{"japanese":"健康診断で身長と体重を測った。","romaji":"Kenkō shindan de shinchō to taijū o hakatta.","german":"Height and weight were measured at the health checkup.","furigana":"けんこうしんだん|しんちょう|たいじゅう|はか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Military, army: 陸軍 (army), 海軍 (navy), 空軍 (air force). Japan's armed forces are officially called 自衛隊 (Self-Defense Forces). Similar sounding: 郡 (district).","examples":[{"japanese":"祖父は若いころ軍にいた。","romaji":"Sofu wa wakai koro gun ni ita.","german":"My grandfather was in the military when he was young.","furigana":"そふ|わか|ぐん"},{"japanese":"両国の軍が国境に集まっている。","romaji":"Ryōkoku no gun ga kokkyō ni atsumatte iru.","german":"The armies of both countries are gathering at the border.","furigana":"りょうこく|ぐん|こっきょう|あつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"The part of the roof that projects beyond the wall of the house: 軒下 (under the eaves). The reading けん is the counter for houses (一軒).","examples":[{"japanese":"雨が降ってきたので、軒下で雨宿りをした。","romaji":"Ame ga futte kita node, nokishita de amayadori o shita.","german":"Since it started to rain, I took shelter from the rain under the eaves.","furigana":"あめ|ふ|のきした|あまやど"},{"japanese":"軒につららが下がっている。","romaji":"Noki ni tsurara ga sagatte iru.","german":"Icicles are hanging from the eaves.","furigana":"のき|さ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Ring or circle: 輪になる (to form a circle), 輪ゴム (rubber band). Figuratively: 友達の輪 (circle of friends). The reading りん is a counter for flowers (一輪).","examples":[{"japanese":"みんなで手をつないで輪になった。","romaji":"Minna de te o tsunaide wa ni natta.","german":"Everyone held hands and formed a circle.","furigana":"て|わ"},{"japanese":"大学に入って、友達の輪が広がった。","romaji":"Daigaku ni haitte, tomodachi no wa ga hirogatta.","german":"Since I started university, my circle of friends has grown.","furigana":"だいがく|はい|ともだち|わ|ひろ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Mostly as ～た途端(に): immediately after something happened, often surprisingly. Practiced in grammar as ～たとたん; often also written in hiragana.","examples":[{"japanese":"家を出た途端に、雨が降り出した。","romaji":"Ie o deta totan ni, ame ga furidashita.","german":"The moment I left the house, it started to rain.","furigana":"いえ|で|とたん|あめ|ふ|だ"},{"japanese":"彼は私の顔を見た途端、笑い出した。","romaji":"Kare wa watashi no kao o mita totan, waraidashita.","german":"The moment he saw my face, he burst out laughing.","furigana":"かれ|わたし|かお|み|とたん|わら|だ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Ichidan verb from 通る and 過ぎる; the place takes を: 駅を通り過ぎる (to go past a station without getting off).","examples":[{"japanese":"話に夢中になって、降りる駅を通り過ぎてしまった。","romaji":"Hanashi ni muchū ni natte, oriru eki o tōrisugite shimatta.","german":"I was so absorbed in the conversation that I rode past my station.","furigana":"はなし|むちゅう|お|えき|とお|す"},{"japanese":"台風が通り過ぎて、空が明るくなった。","romaji":"Taifū ga tōrisugite, sora ga akaruku natta.","german":"The typhoon passed and the sky became bright.","furigana":"たいふう|とお|す|そら|あか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Passing through without stopping (この電車は次の駅を通過します) and, figuratively, passing an exam or a bill (法案が通過する). As a verb 通過する. Similar sounding: 通貨 (currency).","examples":[{"japanese":"この電車は次の駅には止まらずに通過します。","romaji":"Kono densha wa tsugi no eki ni wa tomarazu ni tsūka shimasu.","german":"This train passes through the next station without stopping.","furigana":"でんしゃ|つぎ|えき|と|つうか"},{"japanese":"一次試験は無事に通過した。","romaji":"Ichiji shiken wa buji ni tsūka shita.","german":"I passed the first round of the exam without any problem.","furigana":"いちじしけん|ぶじ|つうか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"A person one is traveling or going out with: お連れ様 (your companion, polite), 子ども連れ (with a child). Verb: 連れる.","examples":[{"japanese":"お連れの方はもういらっしゃいましたか。","romaji":"Otsure no kata wa mō irasshaimashita ka.","german":"Has your companion arrived yet?","furigana":"つ|かた"},{"japanese":"連れが遅れているので、もう少し待ちます。","romaji":"Tsure ga okurete iru node, mō sukoshi machimasu.","german":"My companion is running late, so I will wait a little longer.","furigana":"つ|おく|すこ|ま"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Something evokes another image; as a verb: AからBを連想する (to think of B from A), Bを連想させる (to remind one of B).","examples":[{"japanese":"富士山と聞くと、日本を連想する人が多い。","romaji":"Fujisan to kiku to, Nihon o rensō suru hito ga ōi.","german":"Many people think of Japan when they hear Mt. Fuji.","furigana":"ふじさん|き|にほん|れんそう|ひと|おお"},{"japanese":"この曲は夏の海を連想させる。","romaji":"Kono kyoku wa natsu no umi o rensō saseru.","german":"This song reminds me of the sea in summer.","furigana":"きょく|なつ|うみ|れんそう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"After a long time or many steps: 遂に完成した. Usually written in hiragana ついに. With a negative: not until the very end (遂に来なかった). Similar: とうとう, やっと.","examples":[{"japanese":"十年かけて、遂に夢がかなった。","romaji":"Jūnen kakete, tsui ni yume ga kanatta.","german":"After ten years, my dream finally came true.","furigana":"じゅうねん|つい|ゆめ"},{"japanese":"待っていたが、彼は遂に現れなかった。","romaji":"Matte ita ga, kare wa tsui ni arawarenakatta.","german":"I waited, but he never showed up in the end.","furigana":"ま|かれ|つい|あらわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"After a sentence, mostly as ～に違いない: the speaker's strong conviction. More literary than きっと～だろう. Polite: 違いありません.","examples":[{"japanese":"電気が消えているから、もう寝ているに違いない。","romaji":"Denki ga kiete iru kara, mō nete iru ni chigai nai.","german":"The light is off, so he must already be asleep.","furigana":"でんき|き|ね|ちが"},{"japanese":"あれだけ練習したのだから、合格するに違いない。","romaji":"Are dake renshū shita no dakara, gōkaku suru ni chigai nai.","german":"After practicing that much, he is sure to pass.","furigana":"れんしゅう|ごうかく|ちが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Mostly 〜に適する (to be suitable for something), often attributive: 子どもに適した本. The negative is usually 適さない. More formal than 向いている.","examples":[{"japanese":"この土地は米作りに適している。","romaji":"Kono tochi wa komezukuri ni tekishite iru.","german":"This land is suitable for growing rice.","furigana":"とち|こめづく|てき"},{"japanese":"自分に適した仕事を見つけるのは難しい。","romaji":"Jibun ni tekishita shigoto o mitsukeru no wa muzukashii.","german":"It is hard to find a job that suits you.","furigana":"じぶん|てき|しごと|み|むずか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"To regard something as important, as a verb 重視する: 経験を重視する (to value experience). As a suffix: 品質重視 (quality first). Opposite: 軽視 (to belittle).","examples":[{"japanese":"この会社は経験より人柄を重視している。","romaji":"Kono kaisha wa keiken yori hitogara o jūshi shite iru.","german":"This company values character more than experience.","furigana":"かいしゃ|けいけん|ひとがら|じゅうし"},{"japanese":"私たちは品質重視の商品づくりを続けている。","romaji":"Watashitachi wa hinshitsu jūshi no shōhinzukuri o tsuzukete iru.","german":"We continue to make products with an emphasis on quality.","furigana":"わたし|ひんしつじゅうし|しょうひん|つづ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Open land, meadow (rather literary): 野の花 (wildflowers); in everyday speech 野原 is used. Common in names (上野, 野村).","examples":[{"japanese":"野の花を摘んで、花瓶に飾った。","romaji":"No no hana o tsunde, kabin ni kazatta.","german":"I picked wildflowers and arranged them in a vase.","furigana":"の|はな|つ|かびん|かざ"},{"japanese":"子どもたちは野や山を走り回って遊んだ。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa no ya yama o hashirimawatte asonda.","german":"The children played, running around the fields and mountains.","furigana":"こ|の|やま|はし|まわ|あそ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Formal word for money: 金銭トラブル (money trouble), 金銭感覚 (sense of money). In everyday speech, お金 is used.","examples":[{"japanese":"友達と金銭の貸し借りはしないほうがいい。","romaji":"Tomodachi to kinsen no kashikari wa shinai hō ga ii.","german":"It is better not to lend or borrow money with friends.","furigana":"ともだち|きんせん|か|か"},{"japanese":"彼は金銭感覚がしっかりしている。","romaji":"Kare wa kinsen kankaku ga shikkari shite iru.","german":"He has a sound sense of money.","furigana":"かれ|きんせんかんかく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Sewing needle (針と糸), hand of a clock (時計の針) and sting. Sounding the same: 梁 (roof beam).","examples":[{"japanese":"ボタンが取れたので、針と糸で付けた。","romaji":"Botan ga toreta node, hari to ito de tsuketa.","german":"The button had come off, so I sewed it on with a needle and thread.","furigana":"と|はり|いと|つ"},{"japanese":"時計の針が十二時を指している。","romaji":"Tokei no hari ga jūniji o sashite iru.","german":"The clock hands point to twelve.","furigana":"とけい|はり|じゅうにじ|さ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 29"},
{"notes":"Silver as a metal and a color: 銀のスプーン, 銀色. Silver medal: 銀メダル; gold: 金（きん）.","examples":[{"japanese":"オリンピックで銀メダルを取った。","romaji":"Orinpikku de ginmedaru o totta.","german":"She won a silver medal at the Olympics.","furigana":"ぎん|と"},{"japanese":"母は銀の指輪を大切にしている。","romaji":"Haha wa gin no yubiwa o taisetsu ni shite iru.","german":"My mother treasures her silver ring.","furigana":"はは|ぎん|ゆびわ|たいせつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Firearm in general; handgun: 拳銃. In Japan, private gun ownership is strictly restricted. Similar sounding: 十.","examples":[{"japanese":"日本では銃を持つことが厳しく制限されている。","romaji":"Nihon de wa jū o motsu koto ga kibishiku seigen sarete iru.","german":"In Japan, owning a gun is strictly restricted.","furigana":"にほん|じゅう|も|きび|せいげん"},{"japanese":"警察官が銃を構えた。","romaji":"Keisatsukan ga jū o kamaeta.","german":"The police officer aimed his gun.","furigana":"けいさつかん|じゅう|かま"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"A chain made of metal links: 犬を鎖でつなぐ. Compound: 連鎖 (chain reaction, linkage).","examples":[{"japanese":"犬が鎖でつながれている。","romaji":"Inu ga kusari de tsunagarete iru.","german":"The dog is tied up with a chain.","furigana":"いぬ|くさり"},{"japanese":"自転車を鎖で柱につないだ。","romaji":"Jitensha o kusari de hashira ni tsunaida.","german":"I chained the bicycle to a post.","furigana":"じてんしゃ|くさり|はしら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"A place without direct light (木の陰 = in the shade of a tree) or something hidden: 陰で (behind someone's back). The silhouette of a person or thing is more often 影.","examples":[{"japanese":"暑いので、木の陰で休もう。","romaji":"Atsui node, ki no kage de yasumō.","german":"It's hot, so let's rest in the shade of the tree.","furigana":"あつ|き|かげ|やす"},{"japanese":"人の悪口を陰で言うのはやめなさい。","romaji":"Hito no warukuchi o kage de iu no wa yamenasai.","german":"Stop badmouthing people behind their backs.","furigana":"ひと|わるくち|かげ|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Land as opposed to the sea: 陸に上がる (to go ashore). Compounds: 大陸 (continent), 着陸 (landing), 陸上 (track and field).","examples":[{"japanese":"長い航海の後、ようやく陸が見えてきた。","romaji":"Nagai kōkai no ato, yōyaku riku ga miete kita.","german":"After the long voyage, land finally came into view.","furigana":"なが|こうかい|あと|りく|み"},{"japanese":"カエルは水の中でも陸でも生活できる。","romaji":"Kaeru wa mizu no naka demo riku demo seikatsu dekiru.","german":"Frogs can live both in the water and on land.","furigana":"みず|なか|りく|せいかつ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 41"},
{"notes":"Formal \"when, on the occasion of\": 〜の際(に), 〜する際. この際 = on this occasion. Common in announcements and written documents.","examples":[{"japanese":"お降りの際は、お忘れ物にご注意ください。","romaji":"Oori no sai wa, owasuremono ni gochūi kudasai.","german":"When getting off, please be careful not to leave anything behind.","furigana":"お|さい|わす|もの|ちゅうい"},{"japanese":"この際、はっきり言っておきたい。","romaji":"Kono sai, hakkiri itte okitai.","german":"I want to say it clearly on this occasion.","furigana":"さい|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"The summit of a mountain; figuratively also the highest level (頂上会談 = summit meeting). Similar: 山頂.","examples":[{"japanese":"三時間登って、やっと頂上に着いた。","romaji":"Sanjikan nobotte, yatto chōjō ni tsuita.","german":"After climbing for three hours, we finally reached the summit.","furigana":"さんじかんのぼ|ちょうじょう|つ"},{"japanese":"頂上からの景色はすばらしかった。","romaji":"Chōjō kara no keshiki wa subarashikatta.","german":"The view from the summit was wonderful.","furigana":"ちょうじょう|けしき"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Title or subject: 作文の題 (essay topic), 題をつける (to give a title). Compounds: 問題, 話題, 宿題, 題名.","examples":[{"japanese":"作文の題は「私の夢」です。","romaji":"Sakubun no dai wa \"watashi no yume\" desu.","german":"The title of the essay is \"My Dream\".","furigana":"さくぶん|だい|わたし|ゆめ"},{"japanese":"この写真に題をつけるとしたら、何がいいかな。","romaji":"Kono shashin ni dai o tsukeru to shitara, nani ga ii ka na.","german":"If you were to give this photo a title, what would be good?","furigana":"しゃしん|だい|なに"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Flying (formal): 飛行時間 (flight time), 夜間飛行 (night flight). As a verb 飛行する. Airplane: 飛行機.","examples":[{"japanese":"東京から大阪までの飛行時間は約一時間だ。","romaji":"Tōkyō kara Ōsaka made no hikō jikan wa yaku ichijikan da.","german":"The flight time from Tokyo to Osaka is about one hour.","furigana":"とうきょう|おおさか|ひこうじかん|やくいちじかん"},{"japanese":"ドローンの飛行には許可が必要な場所がある。","romaji":"Dorōn no hikō ni wa kyoka ga hitsuyō na basho ga aru.","german":"There are places where a permit is required to fly a drone.","furigana":"ひこう|きょか|ひつよう|ばしょ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Food in general: 食料品 (groceries in a store). For staple foods such as grain, e.g. in politics, 食糧 is often written.","examples":[{"japanese":"台風に備えて、水と食料を買っておいた。","romaji":"Taifū ni sonaete, mizu to shokuryō o katte oita.","german":"In preparation for the typhoon, I bought water and food.","furigana":"たいふう|そな|みず|しょくりょう|か"},{"japanese":"キャンプに三日分の食料を持っていく。","romaji":"Kyanpu ni mikkabun no shokuryō o motte iku.","german":"I'm taking three days' worth of food on the camping trip.","furigana":"みっかぶん|しょくりょう|も"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Formal word for food, especially in science and health: 食物アレルギー, 食物繊維 (dietary fiber). In everyday speech: 食べ物.","examples":[{"japanese":"私は食物アレルギーがあるので、卵が食べられない。","romaji":"Watashi wa shokumotsu arerugī ga aru node, tamago ga taberarenai.","german":"I have a food allergy, so I can't eat eggs.","furigana":"わたし|しょくもつ|たまご|た"},{"japanese":"動物は食物を求めて移動する。","romaji":"Dōbutsu wa shokumotsu o motomete idō suru.","german":"Animals migrate in search of food.","furigana":"どうぶつ|しょくもつ|もと|いどう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Leaving a vehicle standing, as a verb 駐車する: 駐車禁止 (no parking), 駐車場 (parking lot). Similar sounding: 注射 (injection).","examples":[{"japanese":"ここは駐車禁止です。","romaji":"Koko wa chūsha kinshi desu.","german":"Parking is prohibited here.","furigana":"ちゅうしゃきんし"},{"japanese":"店の前に車を駐車した。","romaji":"Mise no mae ni kuruma o chūsha shita.","german":"I parked the car in front of the shop.","furigana":"みせ|まえ|くるま|ちゅうしゃ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Noise, unrest or a big stir: 大騒ぎ, 騒ぎになる (to cause a stir). Verb: 騒ぐ.","examples":[{"japanese":"駅の前で何か騒ぎが起きているようだ。","romaji":"Eki no mae de nanika sawagi ga okite iru yō da.","german":"There seems to be some kind of commotion in front of the station.","furigana":"えき|まえ|なに|さわ|お"},{"japanese":"その写真はネットで大きな騒ぎになった。","romaji":"Sono shashin wa netto de ōki na sawagi ni natta.","german":"That photo caused a big uproar online.","furigana":"しゃしん|おお|さわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"な-adjective and noun, more formal than 高い: 高価な宝石. Similar sounding: 効果 (effect), 硬貨 (coin).","examples":[{"japanese":"高価な時計を落としてしまった。","romaji":"Kōka na tokei o otoshite shimatta.","german":"I dropped an expensive watch.","furigana":"こうか|とけい|お"},{"japanese":"この絵は非常に高価なものだ。","romaji":"Kono e wa hijō ni kōka na mono da.","german":"This painting is extremely valuable.","furigana":"え|ひじょう|こうか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"High speed (高速で走る, 高速インターネット) and, in conversation, short for 高速道路 (expressway): 高速に乗る. Similar sounding: 拘束 (restraint).","examples":[{"japanese":"週末は高速が混んでいた。","romaji":"Shūmatsu wa kōsoku ga konde ita.","german":"The expressway was crowded on the weekend.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|こうそく|こ"},{"japanese":"この列車は高速で走る。","romaji":"Kono ressha wa kōsoku de hashiru.","german":"This train runs at high speed.","furigana":"れっしゃ|こうそく|はし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Tanned animal hide: 革の靴, 革製品. Raw hide is 皮. The reading かく appears in 革命 (revolution). Similar sounding: 川 (river).","examples":[{"japanese":"誕生日に革の財布をもらった。","romaji":"Tanjōbi ni kawa no saifu o moratta.","german":"I got a leather wallet for my birthday.","furigana":"たんじょうび|かわ|さいふ"},{"japanese":"この革のかばんは使うほど味が出る。","romaji":"Kono kawa no kaban wa tsukau hodo aji ga deru.","german":"This leather bag gets better the more you use it.","furigana":"かわ|つか|あじ|で"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"A visible expression of an inner attitude, mostly 〜の現れ (a sign of ...). Verb: 現れる.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼の厳しい言葉は、期待の現れだ。","romaji":"Kare no kibishii kotoba wa, kitai no araware da.","german":"His harsh words are a sign of his expectations.","furigana":"かれ|きび|ことば|きたい|あらわ"},{"japanese":"この行動は不安の現れかもしれない。","romaji":"Kono kōdō wa fuan no araware kamo shirenai.","german":"This behavior may be an expression of anxiety.","furigana":"こうどう|ふあん|あらわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Side or aspect (費用の面で = in terms of cost), mask (能の面) and surface (水面). The face itself is 顔.","examples":[{"japanese":"この計画は費用の面で問題がある。","romaji":"Kono keikaku wa hiyō no men de mondai ga aru.","german":"This plan has a problem in terms of cost.","furigana":"けいかく|ひよう|めん|もんだい"},{"japanese":"祭りで鬼の面をかぶった。","romaji":"Matsuri de oni no men o kabutta.","german":"At the festival I wore a demon mask.","furigana":"まつ|おに|めん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"The Japanese reading of a kanji, e.g. 山 = やま; counterpart: 音 (Sino-Japanese reading, 山 = さん). Mostly as 訓読み. Similar sounding: 〜君.","examples":[{"japanese":"この漢字の訓と音を覚えてください。","romaji":"Kono kanji no kun to on o oboete kudasai.","german":"Please memorize the kun and on readings of this kanji.","furigana":"かんじ|くん|おん|おぼ"},{"japanese":"この辞書では、訓はひらがなで書かれている。","romaji":"Kono jisho de wa, kun wa hiragana de kakarete iru.","german":"In this dictionary, the kun readings are written in hiragana.","furigana":"じしょ|くん|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"An easy walk in nature, usually as a day trip; strenuous mountain climbing is called 登山. As a verb: ハイキングする.","examples":[{"japanese":"週末に友達と山へハイキングに行きました。","romaji":"Shūmatsu ni tomodachi to yama e haikingu ni ikimashita.","german":"On the weekend I went hiking in the mountains with friends.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|ともだち|やま|い"},{"japanese":"秋はハイキングにちょうどいい季節です。","romaji":"Aki wa haikingu ni chōdo ii kisetsu desu.","german":"Autumn is just the right season for hiking.","furigana":"あき|きせつ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 35"},
{"notes":"Usually written in hiragana; kanji 鋏. To cut with scissors: はさみで切る.","examples":[{"japanese":"このはさみはよく切れます。","romaji":"Kono hasami wa yoku kiremasu.","german":"These scissors cut well.","furigana":"き"},{"japanese":"はさみで紙を半分に切ってください。","romaji":"Hasami de kami o hanbun ni kitte kudasai.","german":"Please cut the paper in half with scissors.","furigana":"かみ|はんぶん|き"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 7"},
{"notes":"な-adjective, used almost only for men: ハンサムな人. For women, きれい or 美人 is more usual.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女のお兄さんはとてもハンサムだ。","romaji":"Kanojo no oniisan wa totemo hansamu da.","german":"Her older brother is very handsome.","furigana":"かのじょ|にい"},{"japanese":"ハンサムな俳優が主役を演じている。","romaji":"Hansamu na haiyū ga shuyaku o enjite iru.","german":"A handsome actor plays the lead role.","furigana":"はいゆう|しゅやく|えん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Also written ヴァイオリン. \"To play the violin\": バイオリンを弾く（ひく）.","examples":[{"japanese":"娘は五歳からバイオリンを習っています。","romaji":"Musume wa gosai kara baiorin o naratte imasu.","german":"My daughter has been learning the violin since she was five.","furigana":"むすめ|ごさい|なら"},{"japanese":"彼はバイオリンを弾くのが上手だ。","romaji":"Kare wa baiorin o hiku no ga jōzu da.","german":"He is good at playing the violin.","furigana":"かれ|ひ|じょうず"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 13"},
{"notes":"General term for handbags, shoulder bags or shopping bags; compounds: ハンドバッグ, エコバッグ. A backpack is called リュック.","examples":[{"japanese":"新しいバッグを買いました。","romaji":"Atarashii baggu o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a new bag.","furigana":"あたら|か"},{"japanese":"バッグの中に財布を入れた。","romaji":"Baggu no naka ni saifu o ireta.","german":"I put my wallet in the bag.","furigana":"なか|さいふ|い"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 41"},
{"notes":"Common: ばったり会う (to run into by chance). Also for suddenly falling over (ばったり倒れる) or stopping abruptly (客がばったり来なくなった).","examples":[{"japanese":"駅で昔の友達にばったり会った。","romaji":"Eki de mukashi no tomodachi ni battari atta.","german":"I ran into an old friend at the station by chance.","furigana":"えき|むかし|ともだち|あ"},{"japanese":"疲れて、ベッドにばったり倒れた。","romaji":"Tsukarete, beddo ni battari taoreta.","german":"Exhausted, I flopped onto the bed.","furigana":"つか|たお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Pipe or conduit (水道のパイプ), tobacco pipe, and figuratively a connection between people or organizations (政界とのパイプ).","examples":[{"japanese":"台所のパイプから水が漏れている。","romaji":"Daidokoro no paipu kara mizu ga morete iru.","german":"Water is leaking from the pipe in the kitchen.","furigana":"だいどころ|みず|も"},{"japanese":"彼は政界に太いパイプを持っている。","romaji":"Kare wa seikai ni futoi paipu o motte iru.","german":"He has strong connections in the political world.","furigana":"かれ|せいかい|ふと|も"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Airplane pilot; more formal: 操縦士. As a prefix also \"pilot\": パイロット版 (pilot version).","examples":[{"japanese":"子どものころ、パイロットになりたかった。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, pairotto ni naritakatta.","german":"When I was a child, I wanted to become a pilot.","furigana":"こ"},{"japanese":"パイロットが機内でアナウンスをした。","romaji":"Pairotto ga kinai de anaunsu o shita.","german":"The pilot made an announcement on board.","furigana":"きない"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Many meanings: 試験にパスする (to pass an exam), a travel or free pass, a pass in ball sports (パスを出す) and \"to pass, to decline\" (今回はパスします). The file path on a computer is a different word with the same spelling.","examples":[{"japanese":"難しい試験に一回でパスした。","romaji":"Muzukashii shiken ni ikkai de pasu shita.","german":"I passed the difficult exam on the first try.","furigana":"むずか|しけん|いっかい"},{"japanese":"飲み会は今回はパスします。","romaji":"Nomikai wa konkai wa pasu shimasu.","german":"I'll skip the drinking party this time.","furigana":"の|かい|こんかい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"An outing with food outdoors: ピクニックに行く, ピクニックをする.","examples":[{"japanese":"公園でピクニックをしました。","romaji":"Kōen de pikunikku o shimashita.","german":"We had a picnic in the park.","furigana":"こうえん"},{"japanese":"日曜日は家族でピクニックに行く予定だ。","romaji":"Nichiyōbi wa kazoku de pikunikku ni iku yotei da.","german":"On Sunday we plan to go on an outing with the family.","furigana":"にちようび|かぞく|い|よてい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"A pin for fastening: 安全ピン (safety pin), ヘアピン (hairpin); a thumbtack, on the other hand, is called 画びょう. Also the pin in bowling.","examples":[{"japanese":"地図をピンで壁に留めた。","romaji":"Chizu o pin de kabe ni tometa.","german":"I pinned the map to the wall.","furigana":"ちず|かべ|と"},{"japanese":"前髪をピンで留めている。","romaji":"Maegami o pin de tomete iru.","german":"She keeps her bangs pinned with a hairpin.","furigana":"まえがみ|と"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Something happens without intention or cause: ふと思い出す (to suddenly remember), ふと見ると (when one happens to look). The kanji 不図 is very rare.","examples":[{"japanese":"ふと昔のことを思い出した。","romaji":"Futo mukashi no koto o omoidashita.","german":"I suddenly remembered the old days.","furigana":"むかし|おも|だ"},{"japanese":"ふと空を見上げると、虹が出ていた。","romaji":"Futo sora o miageru to, niji ga dete ita.","german":"When I happened to look up at the sky, there was a rainbow.","furigana":"そら|みあ|にじ|で"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"The arithmetic sign \"plus\" (三プラス四), but also \"advantage\": プラスになる (to be of benefit). Opposite: マイナス.","examples":[{"japanese":"三プラス四は七です。","romaji":"San purasu yon wa nana desu.","german":"Three plus four is seven.","furigana":"さん|よん|なな"},{"japanese":"留学の経験は仕事にもプラスになった。","romaji":"Ryūgaku no keiken wa shigoto ni mo purasu ni natta.","german":"The experience of studying abroad was also an asset for my work.","furigana":"りゅうがく|けいけん|しごと"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Short for プロフェッショナル; opposite: アマ(チュア). Often a prefix: プロ野球 (professional baseball), プロ選手.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼はプロのカメラマンです。","romaji":"Kare wa puro no kameraman desu.","german":"He is a professional photographer.","furigana":"かれ"},{"japanese":"さすがプロだ。仕事が早くてきれいだ。","romaji":"Sasuga puro da. Shigoto ga hayakute kirei da.","german":"Typical pro: fast and clean work.","furigana":"しごと|はや"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"A belt on clothing (ベルトを締める), also a strap or conveyor belt in machines. Seat belt: シートベルト.","examples":[{"japanese":"ズボンが大きいので、ベルトをしている。","romaji":"Zubon ga ōkii node, beruto o shite iru.","german":"The pants are too big, so I wear a belt.","furigana":"おお"},{"japanese":"工場のベルトが急に止まった。","romaji":"Kōjō no beruto ga kyū ni tomatta.","german":"The belt in the factory suddenly stopped.","furigana":"こうじょう|きゅう|と"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Loanword from Dutch (pek). \"Wet paint\" sign: ペンキ塗りたて. Paints for pictures are called 絵の具.","examples":[{"japanese":"壁にペンキを塗った。","romaji":"Kabe ni penki o nutta.","german":"I painted the wall.","furigana":"かべ|ぬ"},{"japanese":"「ペンキ塗りたて」と書いてあるベンチに座ってしまった。","romaji":"\"Penki nuritate\" to kaite aru benchi ni suwatte shimatta.","german":"I accidentally sat on a bench with a \"Wet Paint\" sign.","furigana":"ぬ|か|すわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Today mainly waiter or hotel bellboy; \"boy\" is mostly found only in compounds (ボーイフレンド, カウボーイ).","examples":[{"japanese":"ボーイに水を頼んだ。","romaji":"Bōi ni mizu o tanonda.","german":"I asked the waiter for water.","furigana":"みず|たの"},{"japanese":"ホテルのボーイが荷物を部屋まで運んでくれた。","romaji":"Hoteru no bōi ga nimotsu o heya made hakonde kureta.","german":"The hotel bellboy carried my luggage to the room.","furigana":"にもつ|へや|はこ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"A small boat, especially a rowboat: ボートをこぐ (to row). Large ships are called 船.","examples":[{"japanese":"池でボートに乗った。","romaji":"Ike de bōto ni notta.","german":"We went boating on the pond.","furigana":"いけ|の"},{"japanese":"二人でボートをこいで島まで行った。","romaji":"Futari de bōto o koide shima made itta.","german":"The two of us rowed a boat to the island.","furigana":"ふたり|しま|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"A versatile filler word: soothing (まあ、落ち着いて), qualifying \"fairly, so-so\" (まあまあ), hedging \"well\" and as an exclamation of surprise (まあ、きれい！).","examples":[{"japanese":"まあ、そんなに怒らないで。","romaji":"Mā, sonna ni okoranaide.","german":"Come on, don't be so angry.","furigana":"おこ"},{"japanese":"まあ、なんてきれいな花でしょう。","romaji":"Mā, nante kirei na hana deshō.","german":"Oh, what beautiful flowers!","furigana":"はな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"The owner of a small cafe or bar, also as a form of address: マスター. As a verb マスターする = to master a skill.","examples":[{"japanese":"喫茶店のマスターがコーヒーをいれてくれた。","romaji":"Kissaten no masutā ga kōhī o irete kureta.","german":"The owner of the coffee shop made me a coffee.","furigana":"きっさてん"},{"japanese":"一年で運転をマスターした。","romaji":"Ichinen de unten o masutā shita.","german":"I mastered driving in one year.","furigana":"いちねん|うんてん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Market as a place (フリーマーケット = flea market) and as a sales market (海外マーケット). A traditional market is also called 市場（いちば）.","examples":[{"japanese":"駅前のマーケットで野菜を買った。","romaji":"Ekimae no māketto de yasai o katta.","german":"I bought vegetables at the market in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|やさい|か"},{"japanese":"この商品はアジアのマーケットで人気がある。","romaji":"Kono shōhin wa Ajia no māketto de ninki ga aru.","german":"This product is popular in the Asian market.","furigana":"しょうひん|にんき"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Mistake, blunder; as a verb ミスする, colloquially ミスる. Homophone: ミス \"Miss\" (ミス日本).","examples":[{"japanese":"計算にミスがあった。","romaji":"Keisan ni misu ga atta.","german":"There was a mistake in the calculation.","furigana":"けいさん"},{"japanese":"同じミスを二度としないように気をつけます。","romaji":"Onaji misu o nido to shinai yō ni ki o tsukemasu.","german":"I'll be careful not to make the same mistake twice.","furigana":"おな|にど|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"A short note; as a verb メモする (to jot down), colloquially メモる. Notepad: メモ帳.","examples":[{"japanese":"大事なことはメモしておいてください。","romaji":"Daiji na koto wa memo shite oite kudasai.","german":"Please make a note of the important things.","furigana":"だいじ"},{"japanese":"机の上にメモが置いてあった。","romaji":"Tsukue no ue ni memo ga oite atta.","german":"There was a note lying on the desk.","furigana":"つくえ|うえ|お"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"A member of a group, team or band. For registered members of a club, 会員 is also used.","examples":[{"japanese":"チームのメンバーは全部で十一人です。","romaji":"Chīmu no menbā wa zenbu de jūichinin desu.","german":"The team has eleven members in all.","furigana":"ぜんぶ|じゅういちにん"},{"japanese":"新しいメンバーを紹介します。","romaji":"Atarashii menbā o shōkai shimasu.","german":"Let me introduce a new member.","furigana":"あたら|しょうかい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Emphatic もし, stressing that something is unlikely. もしものとき／もしもの場合 = in an emergency. The kanji 若しも is rare.","examples":[{"japanese":"もしも雨が降ったら、試合は中止です。","romaji":"Moshimo ame ga futtara, shiai wa chūshi desu.","german":"If it rains, the game is canceled.","furigana":"あめ|ふ|しあい|ちゅうし"},{"japanese":"もしものときのために、水を買っておこう。","romaji":"Moshimo no toki no tame ni, mizu o katte okō.","german":"Let's buy water just in case.","furigana":"みず|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"To have a sense of humor: ユーモアがある, ユーモアのセンス. A single joke is more often 冗談 or ジョーク.","examples":[{"japanese":"父はユーモアのある人です。","romaji":"Chichi wa yūmoa no aru hito desu.","german":"My father has a sense of humor.","furigana":"ちち|ひと"},{"japanese":"彼のスピーチはユーモアにあふれていた。","romaji":"Kare no supīchi wa yūmoa ni afurete ita.","german":"His speech was full of humor.","furigana":"かれ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 21"},
{"notes":"In Japanese, usually a sailboat, not a motorized luxury yacht.","examples":[{"japanese":"夏は海でヨットに乗ります。","romaji":"Natsu wa umi de yotto ni norimasu.","german":"In summer I go sailing on the sea.","furigana":"なつ|うみ|の"},{"japanese":"港にたくさんのヨットが並んでいる。","romaji":"Minato ni takusan no yotto ga narande iru.","german":"Many sailboats are lined up in the harbor.","furigana":"みなと|なら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Lighter. Homophone: ライター \"writer\" (フリーライター).","examples":[{"japanese":"ライターを貸してもらえますか。","romaji":"Raitā o kashite moraemasu ka.","german":"Could you lend me your lighter?","furigana":"か"},{"japanese":"ライターの火でろうそくをつけた。","romaji":"Raitā no hi de rōsoku o tsuketa.","german":"I lit the candle with the lighter's flame.","furigana":"ひ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"A racket with strings or a rubber surface. A golf club is called クラブ, a baseball bat バット.","examples":[{"japanese":"新しいテニスのラケットを買った。","romaji":"Atarashii tenisu no raketto o katta.","german":"I bought a new tennis racket.","furigana":"あたら|か"},{"japanese":"卓球のラケットを忘れてしまった。","romaji":"Takkyū no raketto o wasurete shimatta.","german":"I forgot my table tennis racket.","furigana":"たっきゅう|わす"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Rocket; launch: ロケットを打ち上げる. Homophone: ロケット \"locket\" (a pendant that opens).","examples":[{"japanese":"ロケットが宇宙へ打ち上げられた。","romaji":"Roketto ga uchū e uchiagerareta.","german":"The rocket was launched into space.","furigana":"うちゅう|う|あ"},{"japanese":"子どもたちはペットボトルでロケットを作った。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa pettobotoru de roketto o tsukutta.","german":"The children made rockets out of plastic bottles.","furigana":"こ|つく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Three meanings: one o'clock (午後一時), once or for a while (一時は人気があった) and temporary (一時停止, 一時的).","examples":[{"japanese":"会議は午後一時に始まります。","romaji":"Kaigi wa gogo ichiji ni hajimarimasu.","german":"The meeting starts at one o'clock in the afternoon.","furigana":"かいぎ|ごごいちじ|はじ"},{"japanese":"雨が強くなったので、試合は一時中止になった。","romaji":"Ame ga tsuyoku natta node, shiai wa ichiji chūshi ni natta.","german":"As the rain got heavier, the game was temporarily suspended.","furigana":"あめ|つよ|しあい|いちじちゅうし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"For very unlikely, usually undesirable cases: 万一の場合 (in an emergency), 万一遅れたら (if I should be late).","examples":[{"japanese":"万一のために、保険に入っている。","romaji":"Man'ichi no tame ni, hoken ni haitte iru.","german":"I have insurance just in case.","furigana":"まんいち|ほけん|はい"},{"japanese":"万一遅れる場合は、電話してください。","romaji":"Man'ichi okureru baai wa, denwa shite kudasai.","german":"If by any chance you are late, please call.","furigana":"まんいちおく|ばあい|でんわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Formerly \"to go to the capital\", today almost always \"to go or move to Tokyo\", especially from the provinces. As a verb 上京する. Homophone: 状況 (situation).","examples":[{"japanese":"十八歳のとき、大学に入るために上京した。","romaji":"Jūhassai no toki, daigaku ni hairu tame ni jōkyō shita.","german":"At eighteen, I moved to Tokyo to enter university.","furigana":"じゅうはっさい|だいがく|はい|じょうきょう"},{"japanese":"母が来週上京するので、東京を案内するつもりだ。","romaji":"Haha ga raishū jōkyō suru node, Tōkyō o annai suru tsumori da.","german":"My mother is coming to Tokyo next week, so I plan to show her around.","furigana":"はは|らいしゅうじょうきょう|とうきょう|あんない"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Of good quality: 上等な肉, 上等な服. Sounds a little old-fashioned; today 高級 is often used.","examples":[{"japanese":"今日は上等な肉を買ってきた。","romaji":"Kyō wa jōtō na niku o katte kita.","german":"Today I bought good meat.","furigana":"きょう|じょうとう|にく|か"},{"japanese":"このコートは古いが、生地は上等だ。","romaji":"Kono kōto wa furui ga, kiji wa jōtō da.","german":"This coat is old, but the fabric is first-rate.","furigana":"ふる|きじ|じょうとう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Direction away from Tokyo or from the starting point of a line: 下りの電車. Opposite: 上り（のぼり）. Also a downward slope: 下り坂.","examples":[{"japanese":"下りの電車は五分遅れています。","romaji":"Kudari no densha wa gofun okurete imasu.","german":"The outbound train is five minutes late.","furigana":"くだ|でんしゃ|ごふんおく"},{"japanese":"ここから先は長い下りの道が続く。","romaji":"Koko kara saki wa nagai kudari no michi ga tsuzuku.","german":"From here on, a long downhill road continues.","furigana":"さき|なが|くだ|みち|つづ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Dissatisfaction that one voices: 不平を言う (to complain). Often as a pair: 不平不満.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼はいつも仕事の不平を言っている。","romaji":"Kare wa itsumo shigoto no fuhei o itte iru.","german":"He is always complaining about his work.","furigana":"かれ|しごと|ふへい|い"},{"japanese":"社員の間に不平が広がっている。","romaji":"Shain no aida ni fuhei ga hirogatte iru.","german":"Discontent is spreading among the employees.","furigana":"しゃいん|あいだ|ふへい|ひろ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Written form for \"not allowed, not possible\": 持ち込み不可 (no outside items), 使用不可. As a university grade, \"fail\" (scale 優・良・可・不可).","examples":[{"japanese":"この会場は飲食不可です。","romaji":"Kono kaijō wa inshoku fuka desu.","german":"Eating and drinking are not allowed in this hall.","furigana":"かいじょう|いんしょくふか"},{"japanese":"試験の成績は「不可」だった。","romaji":"Shiken no seiseki wa \"fuka\" datta.","german":"My exam grade was \"fail\".","furigana":"しけん|せいせき|ふか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"な-adjective, mostly before nouns: 主要な都市 (the major cities), 主要国. Homophone: 腫瘍 (tumor).","examples":[{"japanese":"日本の主要な都市を旅行した。","romaji":"Nihon no shuyō na toshi o ryokō shita.","german":"I traveled to the major cities of Japan.","furigana":"にほん|しゅよう|とし|りょこう"},{"japanese":"会議の主要なテーマは環境問題だった。","romaji":"Kaigi no shuyō na tēma wa kankyō mondai datta.","german":"The main theme of the conference was environmental issues.","furigana":"かいぎ|しゅよう|かんきょうもんだい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Mostly negated: 予期しない出来事 (an unexpected event), in writing 予期せぬ. As a verb 予期する.","examples":[{"japanese":"予期しない問題が起きた。","romaji":"Yoki shinai mondai ga okita.","german":"An unexpected problem occurred.","furigana":"よき|もんだい|お"},{"japanese":"結果は予期していたとおりだった。","romaji":"Kekka wa yoki shite ita tōri datta.","german":"The result was as expected.","furigana":"けっか|よき"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Desk and administrative work: 事務の仕事, 事務員 (office worker), 事務所 (office).","examples":[{"japanese":"私は銀行で事務の仕事をしています。","romaji":"Watashi wa ginkō de jimu no shigoto o shite imasu.","german":"I do clerical work at a bank.","furigana":"わたし|ぎんこう|じむ|しごと"},{"japanese":"事務の手続きに時間がかかった。","romaji":"Jimu no tetsuzuki ni jikan ga kakatta.","german":"The administrative procedures took a long time.","furigana":"じむ|てつづ|じかん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Formal term for social contact (交際が広い = to have many connections) and for a romantic relationship: 交際している (to be going out).","examples":[{"japanese":"二人は三年前から交際している。","romaji":"Futari wa sannen mae kara kōsai shite iru.","german":"The two have been together for three years.","furigana":"ふたり|さんねんまえ|こうさい"},{"japanese":"彼は交際が広くて、知り合いが多い。","romaji":"Kare wa kōsai ga hirokute, shiriai ga ōi.","german":"He has a wide circle of acquaintances and knows many people.","furigana":"かれ|こうさい|ひろ|し|あ|おお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Announces something that will soon happen, often as a warning: 今に分かる (you'll see soon enough). Not to be confused with 今にも (any moment now).","examples":[{"japanese":"今に見ていろ。必ず勝ってみせる。","romaji":"Ima ni mite iro. Kanarazu katte miseru.","german":"Just you wait. I will definitely win.","furigana":"いま|み|かなら|か"},{"japanese":"そんなに食べると、今におなかを壊すよ。","romaji":"Sonna ni taberu to, ima ni onaka o kowasu yo.","german":"If you eat that much, you'll upset your stomach before long.","furigana":"た|いま|こわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Dealings with others: 付き合いが長い (to have known each other for a long time), 近所付き合い (relations with neighbors); also obligatory events such as drinking parties with colleagues (付き合いで飲む).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼とは十年以上の付き合いです。","romaji":"Kare to wa jūnen ijō no tsukiai desu.","german":"I have known him for over ten years.","furigana":"かれ|じゅうねんいじょう|つ|あ"},{"japanese":"付き合いで飲み会に参加した。","romaji":"Tsukiai de nomikai ni sanka shita.","german":"I attended the drinking party out of social obligation.","furigana":"つ|あ|の|かい|さんか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Someone who acts in place of another: 部長の代理 (deputy for the department head), 代理で出席する. Agency: 代理店.","examples":[{"japanese":"部長の代理で会議に出た。","romaji":"Buchō no dairi de kaigi ni deta.","german":"I attended the meeting in place of the department head.","furigana":"ぶちょう|だいり|かいぎ|で"},{"japanese":"本人が来られない場合は、代理の人でもかまいません。","romaji":"Honnin ga korarenai baai wa, dairi no hito demo kamaimasen.","german":"If you cannot come yourself, a representative is also fine.","furigana":"ほんにん|こ|ばあい|だいり|ひと"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Rank in a hierarchy or level of quality (位が高い) and a digit position (十の位 = tens place). The homophone くらい \"about\" is a particle and is usually written in hiragana.","examples":[{"japanese":"昔は位の高い人しか入れなかった。","romaji":"Mukashi wa kurai no takai hito shika hairenakatta.","german":"In the past, only people of high rank could enter.","furigana":"むかし|くらい|たか|ひと|はい"},{"japanese":"百の位の数字を答えなさい。","romaji":"Hyaku no kurai no sūji o kotaenasai.","german":"Give the digit in the hundreds place.","furigana":"ひゃく|くらい|すうじ|こた"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Colloquial for どうして／なぜ, often with a reproachful tone. The same spelling can also be read なにで \"with what\" (何で行きますか).","examples":[{"japanese":"何で昨日来なかったの？","romaji":"Nande kinō konakatta no?","german":"Why didn't you come yesterday?","furigana":"なん|きのうこ"},{"japanese":"何でそんなことを言うんですか。","romaji":"Nande sonna koto o iu n desu ka.","german":"Why would you say such a thing?","furigana":"なん|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"More than needed: 余分なお金 (surplus money), 余分に買う (to buy extra).","examples":[{"japanese":"念のため、紙を余分に持ってきた。","romaji":"Nen no tame, kami o yobun ni motte kita.","german":"Just in case, I brought extra paper.","furigana":"ねん|かみ|よぶん|も"},{"japanese":"余分な物は買わないようにしている。","romaji":"Yobun na mono wa kawanai yō ni shite iru.","german":"I try not to buy unnecessary things.","furigana":"よぶん|もの|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Cultivated plants such as rice, grain or vegetables: 作物を育てる. The reading さくぶつ, on the other hand, means \"(artistic) work\".","examples":[{"japanese":"今年は雨が少なくて、作物がよく育たなかった。","romaji":"Kotoshi wa ame ga sukunakute, sakumotsu ga yoku sodatanakatta.","german":"This year there was little rain, and the crops did not grow well.","furigana":"ことし|あめ|すく|さくもつ|そだ"},{"japanese":"この地方では主に米などの作物を作っている。","romaji":"Kono chihō de wa omo ni kome nado no sakumotsu o tsukutte iru.","german":"This region mainly produces crops such as rice.","furigana":"ちほう|おも|こめ|さくもつ|つく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"A transport connection, especially a flight: 午後の便, 次の便, 直行便 (direct flight). Also a shipping method: 船便 (sea mail), 航空便 (air mail). Homophone: 瓶 (bottle).","examples":[{"japanese":"午後三時の便で大阪へ行きます。","romaji":"Gogo sanji no bin de Ōsaka e ikimasu.","german":"I'm taking the 3 p.m. flight to Osaka.","furigana":"ごごさんじ|びん|おおさか|い"},{"japanese":"資料は明日の便でお送りします。","romaji":"Shiryō wa ashita no bin de ookuri shimasu.","german":"I will send you the documents by tomorrow's mail.","furigana":"しりょう|あした|びん|おく"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 10"},
{"notes":"News from someone, especially a letter: 便りがない (to not hear from someone). Proverb: 便りのないのは良い便り (No news is good news).","examples":[{"japanese":"故郷の母から便りが届いた。","romaji":"Kokyō no haha kara tayori ga todoita.","german":"A letter arrived from my mother back home.","furigana":"こきょう|はは|たよ|とど"},{"japanese":"留学した友達からしばらく便りがない。","romaji":"Ryūgaku shita tomodachi kara shibaraku tayori ga nai.","german":"I haven't heard from my friend studying abroad for a while.","furigana":"りゅうがく|ともだち|たよ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Person or office responsible for something: 係の人, 受付係 (reception). In school classes, tasks are assigned as 係 (掃除係).","examples":[{"japanese":"分からないことは係の人に聞いてください。","romaji":"Wakaranai koto wa kakari no hito ni kiite kudasai.","german":"If you have questions, please ask the person in charge.","furigana":"わ|かかり|ひと|き"},{"japanese":"クラスで花の水やりの係になった。","romaji":"Kurasu de hana no mizuyari no kakari ni natta.","german":"In class, I became the one in charge of watering the flowers.","furigana":"はな|みず|かかり"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 45"},
{"notes":"A bus or tram stop; in everyday speech usually shortened to バス停. Train stations are called 駅.","examples":[{"japanese":"次の停留所で降ります。","romaji":"Tsugi no teiryūjo de orimasu.","german":"I'll get off at the next stop.","furigana":"つぎ|ていりゅうじょ|お"},{"japanese":"停留所でバスを二十分待った。","romaji":"Teiryūjo de basu o nijuppun matta.","german":"I waited twenty minutes for the bus at the stop.","furigana":"ていりゅうじょ|にじゅっぷんま"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Work performed (いい働きをする) and the function of something: 心臓の働き (heart function), 薬の働き (effect of a medicine).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼の働きのおかげで、仕事が早く終わった。","romaji":"Kare no hataraki no okage de, shigoto ga hayaku owatta.","german":"Thanks to his work, we finished early.","furigana":"かれ|はたら|しごと|はや|お"},{"japanese":"この薬は胃の働きをよくする。","romaji":"Kono kusuri wa i no hataraki o yoku suru.","german":"This medicine improves stomach function.","furigana":"くすり|い|はたら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"もと means \"origin, cause\" (事故の元, 風邪は万病の元) and \"basis\"; in the meaning \"basis\" it is usually written 基: 〜を基にする \"to be based on something\". For base materials and ingredients, 素 is used: スープの素 \"soup base\". As a prefix, 元 means \"former, ex-\" (元社長, 元彼), and 元の〜 or 元に戻る means \"original, former\" or \"to return to the original state\" (元の場所に戻す).","examples":[{"japanese":"風邪は万病の元と言われる。","romaji":"Kaze wa manbyō no moto to iwareru.","german":"A cold is said to be the source of all illnesses.","furigana":"かぜ|まんびょう|もと|い"},{"japanese":"このドラマは実話を基に作られた。","romaji":"Kono dorama wa jitsuwa o moto ni tsukurareta.","german":"This drama is based on a true story.","furigana":"じつわ|もと|つく"},{"japanese":"使った物は元の場所に戻してください。","romaji":"Tsukatta mono wa moto no basho ni modoshite kudasai.","german":"Please put used items back in their original place.","furigana":"つか|もの|もと|ばしょ|もど"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Political party, often as a suffix: 自民党, 野党 (opposition), 与党 (ruling party). Homophone: 問う (to ask).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は新しい党を作った。","romaji":"Kare wa atarashii tō o tsukutta.","german":"He founded a new party.","furigana":"かれ|あたら|とう|つく"},{"japanese":"どの党に投票するか、まだ決めていない。","romaji":"Dono tō ni tōhyō suru ka, mada kimete inai.","german":"I haven't decided yet which party to vote for.","furigana":"とう|とうひょう|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"A cut-off piece (パン一切れ = a slice of bread; also a counter), a piece of cloth (布切れ) and the sharpness of a blade (切れがいい).","examples":[{"japanese":"ケーキを一切れもらえますか。","romaji":"Kēki o hitokire moraemasu ka.","german":"May I have a piece of cake?","furigana":"ひとき"},{"japanese":"この包丁は切れがいい。","romaji":"Kono hōchō wa kire ga ii.","german":"This kitchen knife cuts very well.","furigana":"ほうちょう|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Parting (別れのあいさつ) and the breakup of a couple (別れ話). Verb: 別れる.","examples":[{"japanese":"駅で友達との別れを惜しんだ。","romaji":"Eki de tomodachi to no wakare o oshinda.","german":"At the station, I was reluctant to say goodbye to my friend.","furigana":"えき|ともだち|わか|お"},{"japanese":"つらい別れを経験して、彼女は強くなった。","romaji":"Tsurai wakare o keiken shite, kanojo wa tsuyoku natta.","german":"Having gone through a painful breakup, she became stronger.","furigana":"わか|けいけん|かのじょ|つよ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"な-adjective: clever (利口な犬) and, of children, \"well-behaved\", often as お利口さん. Slightly more colloquial than 賢い.","examples":[{"japanese":"この犬はとても利口で、言葉が分かるみたいだ。","romaji":"Kono inu wa totemo rikō de, kotoba ga wakaru mitai da.","german":"This dog is very smart and seems to understand words.","furigana":"いぬ|りこう|ことば|わ"},{"japanese":"お利口にしていたら、あとでアイスを買ってあげる。","romaji":"Orikō ni shite itara, ato de aisu o katte ageru.","german":"If you are good, I'll buy you ice cream later.","furigana":"りこう|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Transitive godan verb: ひげを剃る (to shave one's beard), 頭を剃る (to shave one's head bald).","examples":[{"japanese":"毎朝ひげを剃ります。","romaji":"Maiasa hige o sorimasu.","german":"I shave every morning.","furigana":"まいあさ|そ"},{"japanese":"夏になると、足の毛を剃る人も多い。","romaji":"Natsu ni naru to, ashi no ke o soru hito mo ōi.","german":"When summer comes, many people also shave their leg hair.","furigana":"なつ|あし|け|そ|ひと|おお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The right degree: 火加減 (heat level), 塩加減 (amount of salt), 手加減 (going easy). Also one's condition: お加減はいかがですか (How are you feeling?). Verb: 加減する (to adjust, to regulate).","examples":[{"japanese":"力の加減が難しい。","romaji":"Chikara no kagen ga muzukashii.","german":"It is difficult to judge how much force to use.","furigana":"ちから|かげん|むずか"},{"japanese":"お加減はいかがですか。","romaji":"Okagen wa ikaga desu ka.","german":"How are you feeling?","furigana":"かげん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Often in compounds: 乗車券 (train ticket), 入場券 (admission ticket), 食券 (meal ticket), 割引券 (discount coupon).","examples":[{"japanese":"入り口で券を見せてください。","romaji":"Iriguchi de ken o misete kudasai.","german":"Please show your ticket at the entrance.","furigana":"い|ぐち|けん|み"},{"japanese":"この店では先に券を買ってから注文します。","romaji":"Kono mise de wa saki ni ken o katte kara chūmon shimasu.","german":"In this shop, you buy a ticket first and then order.","furigana":"みせ|さき|けん|か|ちゅうもん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"The middle of a period (四月の半ば = mid-April), the half and, adverbially, \"half, partly\" (半ばあきらめている = I have half given up).","examples":[{"japanese":"五月の半ばに引っ越します。","romaji":"Gogatsu no nakaba ni hikkoshimasu.","german":"I'm moving in the middle of May.","furigana":"ごがつ|なか|ひ|こ"},{"japanese":"彼は半ば冗談で、半ば本気で言った。","romaji":"Kare wa nakaba jōdan de, nakaba honki de itta.","german":"He said it half in jest, half seriously.","furigana":"かれ|なか|じょうだん|なか|ほんき|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"A wide, undeveloped area, mostly in compounds: 野原 (meadow), 草原, 焼け野原. Also a common family name. Homophone: 腹 (belly).","examples":[{"japanese":"子どもたちは広い原で遊んでいる。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa hiroi hara de asonde iru.","german":"The children are playing in the wide field.","furigana":"こ|ひろ|はら|あそ"},{"japanese":"昔、この辺りは一面の原だった。","romaji":"Mukashi, kono atari wa ichimen no hara datta.","german":"Long ago, this area was all open field.","furigana":"むかし|あた|いちめん|はら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"More elevated, often more poetic than 友達: 生涯の友 (friend for life). Also \"constant companion\": 本を友とする.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は私の一生の友です。","romaji":"Kare wa watashi no isshō no tomo desu.","german":"He is my lifelong friend.","furigana":"かれ|わたし|いっしょう|とも"},{"japanese":"一人旅では、いつも本を友にしている。","romaji":"Hitoritabi de wa, itsumo hon o tomo ni shite iru.","german":"When traveling alone, I always have a book as my companion.","furigana":"ひとりたび|ほん|とも"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"A linguistic segment (語句 = words and phrases), a verse of five or seven morae, and especially a haiku (句を詠む). Homophones: 九, 区.","examples":[{"japanese":"次の句の意味を説明しなさい。","romaji":"Tsugi no ku no imi o setsumei shinasai.","german":"Explain the meaning of the following phrase.","furigana":"つぎ|く|いみ|せつめい"},{"japanese":"祖父は毎朝一つ句を作っている。","romaji":"Sofu wa maiasa hitotsu ku o tsukutte iru.","german":"My grandfather composes a haiku every morning.","furigana":"そふ|まいあさひと|く|つく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"な-adjective: 同様の問題 (a similar problem), 同様に (likewise). More formal than 同じ. Homophones: 動揺 (agitation), 童謡 (children's song).","examples":[{"japanese":"去年と同様に、今年も祭りが行われる。","romaji":"Kyonen to dōyō ni, kotoshi mo matsuri ga okonawareru.","german":"As in last year, the festival will be held this year too.","furigana":"きょねん|どうよう|ことし|まつ|おこな"},{"japanese":"同様の事故が前にもあった。","romaji":"Dōyō no jiko ga mae ni mo atta.","german":"There was a similar accident before.","furigana":"どうよう|じこ|まえ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Elevated for 名前: 名を名乗る (to give one's name), 名もない (nameless). Also reputation, fame: 名が知られる (to be well known). In compounds usually めい (有名).","examples":[{"japanese":"その花の名を知っていますか。","romaji":"Sono hana no na o shitte imasu ka.","german":"Do you know the name of that flower?","furigana":"はな|な|し"},{"japanese":"彼の名は世界中に知られている。","romaji":"Kare no na wa sekaijū ni shirarete iru.","german":"His name is known all over the world.","furigana":"かれ|な|せかいじゅう|し"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"The opposite side: 駅の向かい (across from the station), 向かいの家. Verb: 向かう.","examples":[{"japanese":"郵便局は駅の向かいにあります。","romaji":"Yūbinkyoku wa eki no mukai ni arimasu.","german":"The post office is across from the station.","furigana":"ゆうびんきょく|えき|む"},{"japanese":"向かいの家に新しい家族が引っ越してきた。","romaji":"Mukai no ie ni atarashii kazoku ga hikkoshite kita.","german":"A new family has moved into the house across the street.","furigana":"む|いえ|あたら|かぞく|ひ|こ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Transitive ichidan verb, formal; the person takes に: 部下に調査を命じる. Passive: 〜を命じられる (to be appointed to something). Written variant: 命ずる.","examples":[{"japanese":"社長は部下に調査を命じた。","romaji":"Shachō wa buka ni chōsa o meijita.","german":"The president ordered his subordinates to conduct an investigation.","furigana":"しゃちょう|ぶか|ちょうさ|めい"},{"japanese":"彼は大阪支店の支店長を命じられた。","romaji":"Kare wa Ōsaka shiten no shitenchō o meijirareta.","german":"He was appointed manager of the Osaka branch.","furigana":"かれ|おおさかしてん|してんちょう|めい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Goods in a shop, or an item: 品物, 品数 (range of goods), 品切れ (sold out), お祝いの品 (congratulatory gift). Read ひん, 品 means \"elegance, refinement\".","examples":[{"japanese":"この店はいい品をそろえている。","romaji":"Kono mise wa ii shina o soroete iru.","german":"This shop stocks good merchandise.","furigana":"みせ|しな"},{"japanese":"お祝いの品を送りました。","romaji":"Oiwai no shina o okurimashita.","german":"I sent a congratulatory gift.","furigana":"いわ|しな|おく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"A refined manner or good taste: 品がある (to be elegant), 品のない言葉 (vulgar words). Adjectives: 上品 (refined), 下品 (vulgar).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女の話し方には品がある。","romaji":"Kanojo no hanashikata ni wa hin ga aru.","german":"There is a refinement in the way she speaks.","furigana":"かのじょ|はな|かた|ひん"},{"japanese":"そんな品のない言葉を使うのはやめなさい。","romaji":"Sonna hin no nai kotoba o tsukau no wa yamenasai.","german":"Stop using such vulgar language.","furigana":"ひん|ことば|つか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Written-language collective term for books, mainly in compounds: 図書館 (library), 図書室 (school library), 図書券 (book voucher). In everyday speech, individual books are called 本.","examples":[{"japanese":"この図書は来週までに返してください。","romaji":"Kono tosho wa raishū made ni kaeshite kudasai.","german":"Please return these books by next week.","furigana":"としょ|らいしゅう|かえ"},{"japanese":"市は子ども向けの図書を増やしている。","romaji":"Shi wa kodomo muke no tosho o fuyashite iru.","german":"The city is expanding its collection of children's books.","furigana":"し|こ|む|としょ|ふ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Literary word for \"earth, ground\" and \"place\": 天と地 (heaven and earth), 約束の地 (the promised land). Very common in compounds: 土地, 地下, 地元, 目的地.","examples":[{"japanese":"祖父はこの地で生まれ育った。","romaji":"Sofu wa kono chi de umaresodatta.","german":"My grandfather was born and raised in this land.","furigana":"そふ|ち|う|そだ"},{"japanese":"枯れ葉が風に舞って、静かに地に落ちた。","romaji":"Kareha ga kaze ni matte, shizuka ni chi ni ochita.","german":"Dead leaves danced in the wind and quietly fell to the ground.","furigana":"か|は|かぜ|ま|しず|ち|お"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Usually written in kana (ほこり). \"To dust\": ほこりを払う／拭く. Similar sound: 誇り (pride).","examples":[{"japanese":"本棚にほこりがたまっている。","romaji":"Hondana ni hokori ga tamatte iru.","german":"Dust has collected on the bookshelf.","furigana":"ほんだな"},{"japanese":"窓を開けて、部屋のほこりを払った。","romaji":"Mado o akete, heya no hokori o haratta.","german":"I opened the window and dusted the room.","furigana":"まど|あ|へや|はら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"A water-filled moat around a castle (お堀) or an artificial canal. Common in place names and surnames (堀川, 堀田).","examples":[{"japanese":"城の周りには深い堀がある。","romaji":"Shiro no mawari ni wa fukai hori ga aru.","german":"There is a deep moat around the castle.","furigana":"しろ|まわ|ふか|ほり"},{"japanese":"春になると、お堀の周りに桜が咲く。","romaji":"Haru ni naru to, ohori no mawari ni sakura ga saku.","german":"In spring, cherry trees bloom around the moat.","furigana":"はる|ほり|まわ|さくら|さ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"On its own, mostly in set phrases: その場で (on the spot), 場を和ませる (to lighten the mood), 場に合う (to suit the occasion). In compounds as 〜場 (駐車場, 会場).","examples":[{"japanese":"その場ですぐに返事をした。","romaji":"Sono ba de sugu ni henji o shita.","german":"I replied on the spot.","furigana":"ば|へんじ"},{"japanese":"彼の冗談で、場の雰囲気が明るくなった。","romaji":"Kare no jōdan de, ba no fun'iki ga akaruku natta.","german":"His joke lightened the mood.","furigana":"かれ|じょうだん|ば|ふんいき|あか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"A tall, slender structure: 東京タワー is called タワー, but a temple's pagoda is a 塔 (五重塔 = five-story pagoda). Similar sound: 問う.","examples":[{"japanese":"お寺の五重の塔は七百年前に建てられた。","romaji":"Otera no gojū no tō wa nanahyakunen mae ni taterareta.","german":"The five-story pagoda of the temple was built 700 years ago.","furigana":"てら|ごじゅう|とう|ななひゃくねんまえ|た"},{"japanese":"丘の上に白い塔が立っていて、遠くからでもよく見える。","romaji":"Oka no ue ni shiroi tō ga tatte ite, tōku kara demo yoku mieru.","german":"A white tower stands on the hill and is clearly visible even from afar.","furigana":"おか|うえ|しろ|とう|た|とお|み"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Respectful term for the wife of a (usually well-known) person: 首相夫人 (the prime minister's wife), 田中夫人. Not to be confused with 婦人 (woman in general, 婦人服).","examples":[{"japanese":"大統領夫人が学校を訪問した。","romaji":"Daitōryō fujin ga gakkō o hōmon shita.","german":"The president's wife visited the school.","furigana":"だいとうりょうふじん|がっこう|ほうもん"},{"japanese":"パーティーには社長夫人も出席した。","romaji":"Pātī ni wa shachō fujin mo shusseki shita.","german":"The company president's wife also attended the party.","furigana":"しゃちょうふじん|しゅっせき"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"The back or inner part of a room or place: 引き出しの奥, 店の奥. Also figurative: 心の奥 (deep in the heart). Related: 奥さん (someone else's wife).","examples":[{"japanese":"探していた鍵は引き出しの奥にあった。","romaji":"Sagashite ita kagi wa hikidashi no oku ni atta.","german":"The key I had been looking for was at the back of the drawer.","furigana":"さが|かぎ|ひ|だ|おく"},{"japanese":"彼の言葉は心の奥に残っている。","romaji":"Kare no kotoba wa kokoro no oku ni nokotte iru.","german":"His words remain deep in my heart.","furigana":"かれ|ことば|こころ|おく|のこ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 10"},
{"notes":"Noun form of 始まる (to begin). Common: 新しい生活の始まり, 事件の始まり. Opposite: 終わり.","examples":[{"japanese":"春は新しい生活の始まりだ。","romaji":"Haru wa atarashii seikatsu no hajimari da.","german":"Spring is the beginning of a new life.","furigana":"はる|あたら|せいかつ|はじ"},{"japanese":"この祭りの始まりは江戸時代だと言われている。","romaji":"Kono matsuri no hajimari wa Edo jidai da to iwarete iru.","german":"The origin of this festival is said to date back to the Edo period.","furigana":"まつ|はじ|えどじだい|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"On its own, somewhat literary: 学がある (to be educated). As a suffix meaning \"-ology, -science\": 経済学, 心理学, 言語学.","examples":[{"japanese":"祖父は学はないが、とても賢い人だった。","romaji":"Sofu wa gaku wa nai ga, totemo kashikoi hito datta.","german":"My grandfather had no education, but he was a very wise man.","furigana":"そふ|がく|かしこ|ひと"},{"japanese":"大学で心理学を勉強しています。","romaji":"Daigaku de shinrigaku o benkyō shite imasu.","german":"I am studying psychology at university.","furigana":"だいがく|しんりがく|べんきょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"A valuable possession, also figuratively: 子どもは国の宝 (children are the treasure of the nation). Related: 宝物（たからもの）, 宝くじ (lottery).","examples":[{"japanese":"この写真は私の一番の宝です。","romaji":"Kono shashin wa watashi no ichiban no takara desu.","german":"This photo is my greatest treasure.","furigana":"しゃしん|わたし|いちばん|たから"},{"japanese":"子どもたちは島で宝を探す遊びをした。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa shima de takara o sagasu asobi o shita.","german":"The children played a treasure-hunting game on the island.","furigana":"こ|しま|たから|さが|あそ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"\"To cause harm\": 害を与える; \"harmful\": 害がある. In compounds: 被害, 公害, 害虫.","examples":[{"japanese":"たばこは体に害がある。","romaji":"Tabako wa karada ni gai ga aru.","german":"Smoking is harmful to the body.","furigana":"からだ|がい"},{"japanese":"この虫は人に害を与えません。","romaji":"Kono mushi wa hito ni gai o ataemasen.","german":"This insect does no harm to people.","furigana":"むし|ひと|がい|あた"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"A neutral word for containers of all kinds (plastic, glass), often seen on packaging and in waste-disposal notices: プラスチック容器. Similar sound: 陽気 (cheerful).","examples":[{"japanese":"残ったカレーを容器に入れて冷凍した。","romaji":"Nokotta karē o yōki ni irete reitō shita.","german":"I put the leftover curry in a container and froze it.","furigana":"のこ|ようき|い|れいとう"},{"japanese":"この容器は電子レンジで使えません。","romaji":"Kono yōki wa denshi renji de tsukaemasen.","german":"This container cannot be used in a microwave.","furigana":"ようき|でんし|つか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Lodging when traveling, especially traditional inns; \"to book lodging\": 宿を取る. More formal: 宿泊, 旅館, ホテル.","examples":[{"japanese":"駅の近くに安い宿を見つけた。","romaji":"Eki no chikaku ni yasui yado o mitsuketa.","german":"I found a cheap place to stay near the station.","furigana":"えき|ちか|やす|やど|み"},{"japanese":"連休なので、早めに宿を取ったほうがいい。","romaji":"Renkyū na node, hayame ni yado o totta hō ga ii.","german":"Since it is a long holiday weekend, you should book lodging early.","furigana":"れんきゅう|はや|やど|と"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"A small, simple building: 山小屋 (mountain hut), 物置小屋 (tool shed), 犬小屋 (doghouse).","examples":[{"japanese":"山の上の小屋で一晩泊まった。","romaji":"Yama no ue no koya de hitoban tomatta.","german":"We spent a night in a hut at the top of the mountain.","furigana":"やま|うえ|こや|ひとばんと"},{"japanese":"庭の小屋に古い自転車をしまってある。","romaji":"Niwa no koya ni furui jitensha o shimatte aru.","german":"An old bicycle is stored in the shed in the garden.","furigana":"にわ|こや|ふる|じてんしゃ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"The plant or grain; flour is called 小麦粉. Allergy notice on packaging: 小麦を含む (contains wheat).","examples":[{"japanese":"パンやうどんは小麦から作られる。","romaji":"Pan ya udon wa komugi kara tsukurareru.","german":"Bread and udon are made from wheat.","furigana":"こむぎ|つく"},{"japanese":"この商品には小麦が含まれています。","romaji":"Kono shōhin ni wa komugi ga fukumarete imasu.","german":"This product contains wheat.","furigana":"しょうひん|こむぎ|ふく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"An administrative department (〜局 in ministries) and short for a broadcasting station or post office: テレビ局 (TV station), 郵便局. In conversation, 局 alone means \"the station\".","examples":[{"japanese":"市役所に観光を担当する新しい局ができた。","romaji":"Shiyakusho ni kankō o tantō suru atarashii kyoku ga dekita.","german":"A new bureau in charge of tourism was set up at city hall.","furigana":"しやくしょ|かんこう|たんとう|あたら|きょく"},{"japanese":"その番組は局の方針で中止になった。","romaji":"Sono bangumi wa kyoku no hōshin de chūshi ni natta.","german":"The program was cancelled due to the station's policy.","furigana":"ばんぐみ|きょく|ほうしん|ちゅうし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"The edge of a body of water: 川岸 (riverbank), 向こう岸 (the opposite shore), 海岸 (seashore).","examples":[{"japanese":"ボートを岸につないだ。","romaji":"Bōto o kishi ni tsunaida.","german":"I tied the boat to the shore.","furigana":"きし"},{"japanese":"川の向こうの岸まで泳いで渡った。","romaji":"Kawa no mukō no kishi made oyoide watatta.","german":"I swam across to the opposite bank of the river.","furigana":"かわ|む|きし|およ|わた"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Verwaltungseinheit in Bundesstaaten: アメリカの州, ドイツの州 (Bundesland), カリフォルニア州. Die Lesung す bedeutet „Sandbank“.","examples":[{"japanese":"ドイツには十六の州がある。","romaji":"Doitsu ni wa jūroku no shū ga aru.","german":"Deutschland hat sechzehn Bundesländer.","furigana":"じゅうろく|しゅう"},{"japanese":"彼はアメリカのテキサス州出身です。","romaji":"Kare wa Amerika no Tekisasu shū shusshin desu.","german":"Er stammt aus dem US-Bundesstaat Texas.","furigana":"かれ|しゅうしゅっしん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Colloquial; usually the polite form お年寄り. Rude as a direct form of address; neutral: 高齢者. In sumo, also an elder of the association.","examples":[{"japanese":"電車ではお年寄りに席を譲りましょう。","romaji":"Densha de wa otoshiyori ni seki o yuzurimashō.","german":"On the train, let us offer our seats to elderly people.","furigana":"でんしゃ|としよ|せき|ゆず"},{"japanese":"この村には年寄りばかりが残っている。","romaji":"Kono mura ni wa toshiyori bakari ga nokotte iru.","german":"Only old people are left in this village.","furigana":"むら|としよ|のこ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Evocative word for a long span of time that has passed: 長い年月がたつ. Also read ねんげつ (more literary). Not to be confused with 年月日 (date).","examples":[{"japanese":"長い年月をかけて、この橋は完成した。","romaji":"Nagai toshitsuki o kakete, kono hashi wa kansei shita.","german":"This bridge was completed over many years.","furigana":"なが|としつき|はし|かんせい"},{"japanese":"あれから三十年の年月が流れた。","romaji":"Are kara sanjūnen no toshitsuki ga nagareta.","german":"Thirty years have passed since then.","furigana":"さんじゅうねん|としつき|なが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Mostly as この度 (this time, formal) or 〜たびに (every time): 会うたびに. Often written in kana; 度 is otherwise also read ど (degree, time).","examples":[{"japanese":"この度はお世話になりました。","romaji":"Kono tabi wa osewa ni narimashita.","german":"Thank you for your help on this occasion.","furigana":"たび|せわ"},{"japanese":"この歌を聞く度に、昔を思い出す。","romaji":"Kono uta o kiku tabi ni, mukashi o omoidasu.","german":"Every time I hear this song, I remember the old days.","furigana":"うた|き|たび|むかし|おも|だ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"A bullet from a firearm; same reading as 玉 (ball, sphere), but written 弾 when ammunition is meant. Also 弾丸（だんがん）.","examples":[{"japanese":"兵士は弾が当たって、けがをした。","romaji":"Heishi wa tama ga atatte, kega o shita.","german":"The soldier was hit by a bullet and injured.","furigana":"へいし|たま|あ"},{"japanese":"この銃にはもう弾が入っていない。","romaji":"Kono jū ni wa mō tama ga haitte inai.","german":"There are no more bullets in this gun.","furigana":"じゅう|たま|はい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Noun/adverb for a point in the past: 当時の日本 (Japan at that time), 大学生の当時. Not used for the present.","examples":[{"japanese":"当時はまだ携帯電話がなかった。","romaji":"Tōji wa mada keitai denwa ga nakatta.","german":"Back then there were no mobile phones yet.","furigana":"とうじ|けいたいでんわ"},{"japanese":"事故の当時、私は外国にいた。","romaji":"Jiko no tōji, watashi wa gaikoku ni ita.","german":"At the time of the accident, I was abroad.","furigana":"じこ|とうじ|わたし|がいこく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"The outline that a body casts (人の影), or a reflection (水に映る影). In contrast, a shady place is 陰（かげ）.","examples":[{"japanese":"夕方になると影が長くなる。","romaji":"Yūgata ni naru to kage ga nagaku naru.","german":"Toward evening the shadows grow long.","furigana":"ゆうがた|かげ|なが"},{"japanese":"湖に山の影が映っている。","romaji":"Mizuumi ni yama no kage ga utsutte iru.","german":"The mountain is reflected in the lake.","furigana":"みずうみ|やま|かげ|うつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Refers to the second of two things mentioned; counterpart: 前者（ぜんしゃ, the former). Typical in comparisons and written texts.","examples":[{"japanese":"紅茶とコーヒーなら、私は後者が好きだ。","romaji":"Kōcha to kōhī nara, watashi wa kōsha ga suki da.","german":"Between tea and coffee, I like the latter.","furigana":"こうちゃ|わたし|こうしゃ|す"},{"japanese":"二つの案のうち、会議では後者が選ばれた。","romaji":"Futatsu no an no uchi, kaigi de wa kōsha ga erabareta.","german":"Of the two proposals, the latter was chosen at the meeting.","furigana":"ふた|あん|かいぎ|こうしゃ|えら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Romantic, yearning love: 恋をする (to fall in love), 初恋 (first love). 愛 is broader and deeper (including parental love and love of one's neighbor). Similar sound: 鯉 (carp).","examples":[{"japanese":"高校生の時、初めて恋をした。","romaji":"Kōkōsei no toki, hajimete koi o shita.","german":"I fell in love for the first time in high school.","furigana":"こうこうせい|とき|はじ|こい"},{"japanese":"この映画は二人の切ない恋の物語だ。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa futari no setsunai koi no monogatari da.","german":"This film is a story of the painful love between two people.","furigana":"えいが|ふたり|せつ|こい|ものがたり"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"A kindness received that one feels obliged to repay - central to Japanese thinking: 恩を返す (to repay a favor), 恩を忘れる (to be ungrateful), 恩人 (benefactor).","examples":[{"japanese":"先生の恩は一生忘れません。","romaji":"Sensei no on wa isshō wasuremasen.","german":"I will never forget my teacher's kindness as long as I live.","furigana":"せんせい|おん|いっしょうわす"},{"japanese":"いつか両親に恩を返したい。","romaji":"Itsuka ryōshin ni on o kaeshitai.","german":"Someday I want to repay my parents for all they have done.","furigana":"りょうしん|おん|かえ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"What one wants to do or communicate: 意思を伝える, 本人の意思. 意志 is written more for strong will and willpower (意志が強い).","examples":[{"japanese":"自分の意思をはっきり伝えることが大切だ。","romaji":"Jibun no ishi o hakkiri tsutaeru koto ga taisetsu da.","german":"It is important to state your intentions clearly.","furigana":"じぶん|いし|つた|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"本人に辞める意思はないようだ。","romaji":"Honnin ni yameru ishi wa nai yō da.","german":"The person himself does not seem to intend to quit.","furigana":"ほんにん|や|いし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Prenominal word (連体詞), usually in kana: ある日 (one day), ある人 (someone). A typical opening of fairy tales and stories. Not to be confused with the verb ある (there is).","examples":[{"japanese":"ある日、森で不思議な鳥を見つけた。","romaji":"Aru hi, mori de fushigi na tori o mitsuketa.","german":"One day, I found a strange bird in the forest.","furigana":"ひ|もり|ふしぎ|とり|み"},{"japanese":"ある人から、この本を勧められた。","romaji":"Aru hito kara, kono hon o susumerareta.","german":"Someone recommended this book to me.","furigana":"ひと|ほん|すす"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"A sleight-of-hand trick for entertainment; \"to perform a trick\": 手品をする. Magician: 手品師 or マジシャン.","examples":[{"japanese":"パーティーで手品をして、みんなを驚かせた。","romaji":"Pātī de tejina o shite, minna o odorokaseta.","german":"I performed magic tricks at the party and amazed everyone.","furigana":"てじな|おどろ"},{"japanese":"この手品の種がどうしても分からない。","romaji":"Kono tejina no tane ga dōshitemo wakaranai.","german":"I just can't figure out how this trick works.","furigana":"てじな|たね|わ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Transitive ichidan verb: to raise something (荷物を持ち上げる). Figuratively: to praise someone excessively to put them in a good mood.","examples":[{"japanese":"この箱は重くて、一人では持ち上げられない。","romaji":"Kono hako wa omokute, hitori de wa mochiagerarenai.","german":"This box is so heavy that I can't lift it by myself.","furigana":"はこ|おも|ひとり|も|あ"},{"japanese":"みんなに持ち上げられて、彼はすっかり調子に乗った。","romaji":"Minna ni mochiagerarete, kare wa sukkari chōshi ni notta.","german":"Flattered by everyone, he got completely carried away.","furigana":"も|あ|かれ|ちょうし|の"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Transitive godan verb; more formal than 払う, typical for bills, fees and wages. Noun: 支払い.","examples":[{"japanese":"料金はカードで支払うことができます。","romaji":"Ryōkin wa kādo de shiharau koto ga dekimasu.","german":"The fee can be paid by card.","furigana":"りょうきん|しはら"},{"japanese":"会社は社員に毎月給料を支払う。","romaji":"Kaisha wa shain ni maitsuki kyūryō o shiharau.","german":"The company pays its employees a salary every month.","furigana":"かいしゃ|しゃいん|まいつききゅうりょう|しはら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Official, businesslike: money, goods or equipment is handed out - 交通費を支給する (to reimburse travel expenses), 制服が支給される. As a verb 支給する.","examples":[{"japanese":"この会社では交通費が全額支給される。","romaji":"Kono kaisha de wa kōtsūhi ga zengaku shikyū sareru.","german":"In this company, transportation costs are fully reimbursed.","furigana":"かいしゃ|こうつうひ|ぜんがくしきゅう"},{"japanese":"新入社員には制服が支給されます。","romaji":"Shinnyū shain ni wa seifuku ga shikyū saremasu.","german":"New employees are issued a uniform.","furigana":"しんにゅうしゃいん|せいふく|しきゅう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Formal: 敬意を表す／払う (to express one's respect). Similar sound: 経緯 (course of events).","examples":[{"japanese":"選手たちの努力に敬意を表します。","romaji":"Senshutachi no doryoku ni keii o hyōshimasu.","german":"I pay my respects to the athletes' efforts.","furigana":"せんしゅ|どりょく|けいい|ひょう"},{"japanese":"年上の人には敬意を払って話すべきだ。","romaji":"Toshiue no hito ni wa keii o haratte hanasu beki da.","german":"You should speak to older people with respect.","furigana":"としうえ|ひと|けいい|はら|はな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Respectful plural of 方（かた）: 関係者の方々 (all those involved), ご来場の方々. The same spelling read ほうぼう means \"everywhere\".","examples":[{"japanese":"協力してくださった方々に感謝いたします。","romaji":"Kyōryoku shite kudasatta katagata ni kansha itashimasu.","german":"I thank all the people who cooperated with us.","furigana":"きょうりょく|かたがた|かんしゃ"},{"japanese":"会場には地元の方々が大勢集まった。","romaji":"Kaijō ni wa jimoto no katagata ga ōzei atsumatta.","german":"Many local people gathered at the venue.","furigana":"かいじょう|じもと|かたがた|おおぜいあつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"In many places: 方々探す (to search everywhere), 方々に知り合いがいる. Often written in kana too (ほうぼう). The same spelling read かたがた means \"ladies and gentlemen\".","examples":[{"japanese":"なくした財布を方々探したが、見つからなかった。","romaji":"Nakushita saifu o hōbō sagashita ga, mitsukaranakatta.","german":"I searched everywhere for my lost wallet but could not find it.","furigana":"さいふ|ほうぼうさが|み"},{"japanese":"彼は仕事で日本の方々を回っている。","romaji":"Kare wa shigoto de Nihon no hōbō o mawatte iru.","german":"He travels all over Japan for work.","furigana":"かれ|しごと|にほん|ほうぼう|まわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"A little more evocative than 旅行: 一人旅 (solo trip), 旅に出る (to set out on a journey). Proverb: かわいい子には旅をさせよ.","examples":[{"japanese":"来月、一人で北海道へ旅に出ます。","romaji":"Raigetsu, hitori de Hokkaidō e tabi ni demasu.","german":"Next month I am going on a trip to Hokkaido by myself.","furigana":"らいげつ|ひとり|ほっかいどう|たび|で"},{"japanese":"旅の思い出を日記に書いた。","romaji":"Tabi no omoide o nikki ni kaita.","german":"I wrote my travel memories in my diary.","furigana":"たび|おも|で|にっき|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"The daylight hours: 日中は暑い. In the meaning \"Japan-China\" (日中関係) it is also read にっちゅう.","examples":[{"japanese":"日中は暑いが、夜は涼しい。","romaji":"Nitchū wa atsui ga, yoru wa suzushii.","german":"It is hot during the day, but cool at night.","furigana":"にっちゅう|あつ|よる|すず"},{"japanese":"日中は仕事で電話に出られません。","romaji":"Nitchū wa shigoto de denwa ni deraremasen.","german":"I can't answer the phone during the day because of work.","furigana":"にっちゅう|しごと|でんわ|で"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"A specific point in time, especially for timetables and appointments: 出発時刻, 時刻表 (timetable). 時間, by contrast, often means a length of time.","examples":[{"japanese":"電車の出発時刻を確認してください。","romaji":"Densha no shuppatsu jikoku o kakunin shite kudasai.","german":"Please check the departure time of the train.","furigana":"でんしゃ|しゅっぱつじこく|かくにん"},{"japanese":"約束の時刻になっても、彼は来なかった。","romaji":"Yakusoku no jikoku ni natte mo, kare wa konakatta.","german":"He did not come even at the agreed time.","furigana":"やくそく|じこく|かれ|こ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Especially 年の暮れ／暮れ (year-end, also the お歳暮 season) and 日の暮れ (nightfall). Verb: 暮れる.","examples":[{"japanese":"暮れは忙しくて、ゆっくり休めない。","romaji":"Kure wa isogashikute, yukkuri yasumenai.","german":"The end of the year is so busy that I can't rest properly.","furigana":"く|いそが|やす"},{"japanese":"秋は日の暮れが早い。","romaji":"Aki wa hi no kure ga hayai.","german":"In autumn, dusk comes early.","furigana":"あき|ひ|く|はや"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"な-adjective: 有利な条件 (favorable conditions), 就職に有利だ. Opposite: 不利.","examples":[{"japanese":"英語ができると就職に有利だ。","romaji":"Eigo ga dekiru to shūshoku ni yūri da.","german":"Being able to speak English is an advantage in finding a job.","furigana":"えいご|しゅうしょく|ゆうり"},{"japanese":"ホームで試合をするチームのほうが有利だ。","romaji":"Hōmu de shiai o suru chīmu no hō ga yūri da.","german":"The team playing at home has the advantage.","furigana":"しあい|ゆうり"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"The end of a period: 今月の末 (the end of this month). After a verb in the た-form: 考えた末に (after long deliberation). Also 末っ子 (youngest child).","examples":[{"japanese":"今月の末までにレポートを出してください。","romaji":"Kongetsu no sue made ni repōto o dashite kudasai.","german":"Please submit the report by the end of this month.","furigana":"こんげつ|すえ|だ"},{"japanese":"よく考えた末に、会社を辞めることにした。","romaji":"Yoku kangaeta sue ni, kaisha o yameru koto ni shita.","german":"After careful consideration, I decided to quit the company.","furigana":"かんが|すえ|かいしゃ|や"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Things tied together: 花束（はなたば, bouquet), 札束 (wad of banknotes). Also a counter: ほうれん草一束.","examples":[{"japanese":"古新聞を束にして出した。","romaji":"Furushinbun o taba ni shite dashita.","german":"I bundled up the old newspapers and put them out.","furigana":"ふるしんぶん|たば|だ"},{"japanese":"古い手紙の束が引き出しから出てきた。","romaji":"Furui tegami no taba ga hikidashi kara dete kita.","german":"A bundle of old letters turned up in the drawer.","furigana":"ふる|てがみ|たば|ひ|だ|で"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"An evergreen tree and a symbol of good luck (long life); 門松 are set up at New Year. In the 松竹梅 ranking, 松 is the highest. Similar sound: 待つ.","examples":[{"japanese":"海岸に沿って松の木が並んでいる。","romaji":"Kaigan ni sotte matsu no ki ga narande iru.","german":"Pine trees line the coast.","furigana":"かいがん|そ|まつ|き|なら"},{"japanese":"庭の松の手入れを植木屋さんに頼んだ。","romaji":"Niwa no matsu no teire o uekiya-san ni tanonda.","german":"I asked a gardener to take care of the pine in the garden.","furigana":"にわ|まつ|てい|うえきや|たの"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"The handle of a tool or device: 傘の柄, ほうきの柄, 包丁の柄. The same kanji read がら means \"pattern\".","examples":[{"japanese":"傘の柄が壊れてしまった。","romaji":"Kasa no e ga kowarete shimatta.","german":"The handle of my umbrella broke.","furigana":"かさ|え|こわ"},{"japanese":"ほうきの柄を短く切った。","romaji":"Hōki no e o mijikaku kitta.","german":"I cut the broom handle shorter.","furigana":"え|みじか|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Pattern on fabric (花柄 = floral pattern, 柄のシャツ); also nature or physique: 柄が悪い (rough, uncouth), 大柄 (large-framed). The same kanji read え means \"handle\".","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は柄が悪く見えるが、根は優しい。","romaji":"Kare wa gara ga waruku mieru ga, ne wa yasashii.","german":"He looks rough, but he is kind at heart.","furigana":"かれ|がら|わる|み|ね|やさ"},{"japanese":"このシャツは柄が派手すぎる。","romaji":"Kono shatsu wa gara ga hadesugiru.","german":"The pattern of this shirt is too loud.","furigana":"がら|はで"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Pole, stick: 木の棒, 鉄の棒. Also a straight line (棒を引く). Idiom: 足が棒になる (legs become stiff from walking).","examples":[{"japanese":"犬が木の棒をくわえて走ってきた。","romaji":"Inu ga ki no bō o kuwaete hashitte kita.","german":"The dog came running with a wooden stick in its mouth.","furigana":"いぬ|き|ぼう|はし"},{"japanese":"一日中歩いて、足が棒になった。","romaji":"Ichinichijū aruite, ashi ga bō ni natta.","german":"After walking all day, my legs were stiff as sticks.","furigana":"いちにちじゅうある|あし|ぼう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Intensifier before adjectives and nouns: ごく普通 (quite ordinary), ごく一部 (only a very small part), ごくわずか. Usually in kana; kanji 極く／極. Not to be confused with すごく.","examples":[{"japanese":"これはごく普通の家庭料理です。","romaji":"Kore wa goku futsū no katei ryōri desu.","german":"This is quite ordinary home cooking.","furigana":"ふつう|かていりょうり"},{"japanese":"反対したのは、ごく一部の人だけだった。","romaji":"Hantai shita no wa, goku ichibu no hito dake datta.","german":"Only a very small number of people opposed it.","furigana":"はんたい|いちぶ|ひと"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"な-adjective and noun: 楽な仕事 (an easy job), 楽になる (to feel relieved), 気が楽だ. The same kanji is in 楽しい (たのしい, \"enjoyable, fun\").","examples":[{"japanese":"薬を飲んだら、ずいぶん楽になった。","romaji":"Kusuri o nondara, zuibun raku ni natta.","german":"After I took the medicine, I felt much better.","furigana":"くすり|の|らく"},{"japanese":"この仕事は楽そうに見えて、実は大変だ。","romaji":"Kono shigoto wa rakusō ni miete, jitsu wa taihen da.","german":"This job looks easy but is actually hard.","furigana":"しごと|らく|み|じつ|たいへん"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 22"},
{"notes":"Transitive godan verb (not ichidan despite the る ending): 道を横切る, 目の前を猫が横切った. Similar: 渡る (to cross over).","examples":[{"japanese":"急に子どもが道を横切ったので、車を止めた。","romaji":"Kyū ni kodomo ga michi o yokogitta node, kuruma o tometa.","german":"A child suddenly crossed the road, so I stopped the car.","furigana":"きゅう|こ|みち|よこぎ|くるま|と"},{"japanese":"黒い猫が目の前を横切った。","romaji":"Kuroi neko ga me no mae o yokogitta.","german":"A black cat ran across in front of me.","furigana":"くろ|ねこ|め|まえ|よこぎ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Colloquial for やめる; usually in kana: よしなさい (Stop that!), 行くのはよそう (Let's not go after all). Kanji 止す.","examples":[{"japanese":"危ないから、そんなことはよしなさい。","romaji":"Abunai kara, sonna koto wa yoshinasai.","german":"That's dangerous, so stop that!","furigana":"あぶ"},{"japanese":"雨が強くなってきたし、出かけるのはよそう。","romaji":"Ame ga tsuyoku natte kita shi, dekakeru no wa yosō.","german":"It's raining harder, so let's not go out after all.","furigana":"あめ|つよ|で"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Poisonous substance (毒キノコ, 毒蛇) and, figuratively, something harmful: 体に毒だ (bad for the body), 目の毒 (a tempting sight one shouldn't indulge in).","examples":[{"japanese":"このキノコには毒があるので、食べてはいけない。","romaji":"Kono kinoko ni wa doku ga aru node, tabete wa ikenai.","german":"This mushroom is poisonous, so you must not eat it.","furigana":"どく|た"},{"japanese":"夜更かしは体に毒だよ。","romaji":"Yofukashi wa karada ni doku da yo.","german":"Staying up late is bad for your health.","furigana":"よふ|からだ|どく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"The sea some distance from the shore: 沖に出る (to head out to the open sea), 東京湾沖 (off Tokyo Bay). Also in earthquake reports: 〜沖で地震.","examples":[{"japanese":"漁船が朝早く沖に出ていった。","romaji":"Gyosen ga asa hayaku oki ni dete itta.","german":"The fishing boats went out to sea early in the morning.","furigana":"ぎょせん|あさはや|おき|で"},{"japanese":"千葉県沖で地震がありました。","romaji":"Chiba-ken oki de jishin ga arimashita.","german":"There was an earthquake off the coast of Chiba Prefecture.","furigana":"ちばけんおき|じしん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Law in general: 法を守る (to obey the law), 法に触れる (to violate the law). As a suffix also \"method\": 学習法, 方法.","examples":[{"japanese":"法を守るのは市民の義務だ。","romaji":"Hō o mamoru no wa shimin no gimu da.","german":"Obeying the law is a citizen's duty.","furigana":"ほう|まも|しみん|ぎむ"},{"japanese":"この本では効果的な学習法を紹介している。","romaji":"Kono hon de wa kōkateki na gakushūhō o shōkai shite iru.","german":"This book introduces effective study methods.","furigana":"ほん|こうかてき|がくしゅうほう|しょうかい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Wet earth: 泥だらけ (covered in mud), 泥んこ. Idiom: 顔に泥を塗る (to bring shame on someone). 泥棒 = thief.","examples":[{"japanese":"雨の中でサッカーをして、服が泥だらけになった。","romaji":"Ame no naka de sakkā o shite, fuku ga doro darake ni natta.","german":"I played soccer in the rain and my clothes got covered in mud.","furigana":"あめ|なか|ふく|どろ"},{"japanese":"車のタイヤが泥にはまって動かない。","romaji":"Kuruma no taiya ga doro ni hamatte ugokanai.","german":"The car's tires are stuck in the mud and won't move.","furigana":"くるま|どろ|うご"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"An explanatory note in books and texts: 注を付ける, ※注. More detailed: 注釈, 脚注 (footnote).","examples":[{"japanese":"難しい言葉には注が付いている。","romaji":"Muzukashii kotoba ni wa chū ga tsuite iru.","german":"Difficult words come with a note.","furigana":"むずか|ことば|ちゅう|つ"},{"japanese":"詳しくはページの下の注を見てください。","romaji":"Kuwashiku wa pēji no shita no chū o mite kudasai.","german":"For details, see the note at the bottom of the page.","furigana":"くわ|した|ちゅう|み"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Intransitive ichidan verb, rather literary: 自信に満ちる (to be full of confidence), 喜びに満ちた顔. For tides: 潮が満ちる (the tide comes in). Transitive: 満たす.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女の声は自信に満ちていた。","romaji":"Kanojo no koe wa jishin ni michite ita.","german":"Her voice was full of confidence.","furigana":"かのじょ|こえ|じしん|み"},{"japanese":"潮が満ちると、この道は海に沈む。","romaji":"Shio ga michiru to, kono michi wa umi ni shizumu.","german":"When the tide comes in, this road is submerged in the sea.","furigana":"しお|み|みち|うみ|しず"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Residue left after burning: たばこの灰, 火山灰 (volcanic ash). Ash gray: 灰色.","examples":[{"japanese":"たばこの灰を床に落とさないでください。","romaji":"Tabako no hai o yuka ni otosanaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't drop cigarette ash on the floor.","furigana":"はい|ゆか|お"},{"japanese":"火山の灰が町に降ってきた。","romaji":"Kazan no hai ga machi ni futte kita.","german":"Volcanic ash fell on the town.","furigana":"かざん|はい|まち|ふ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"The visible flame of a fire; also figurative for strong emotions: 怒りの炎, 情熱の炎. Fire in general is 火.","examples":[{"japanese":"ろうそくの炎が風で揺れている。","romaji":"Rōsoku no honō ga kaze de yurete iru.","german":"The candle flame is flickering in the wind.","furigana":"ほのお|かぜ|ゆ"},{"japanese":"家は一瞬で炎に包まれた。","romaji":"Ie wa isshun de honō ni tsutsumareta.","german":"The house was engulfed in flames in an instant.","furigana":"いえ|いっしゅん|ほのお|つつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"As a verb 熱中する: to immerse oneself in something (ゲームに熱中する). Not to be confused with 熱中症 (heatstroke).","examples":[{"japanese":"息子はサッカーに熱中している。","romaji":"Musuko wa sakkā ni netchū shite iru.","german":"My son is totally into soccer.","furigana":"むすこ|ねっちゅう"},{"japanese":"本に熱中して、電車を降りるのを忘れた。","romaji":"Hon ni netchū shite, densha o oriru no o wasureta.","german":"I was so absorbed in my book that I forgot to get off the train.","furigana":"ほん|ねっちゅう|でんしゃ|お|わす"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Things of life in general: 物事をよく考える, 物事には順序がある (everything has its order). Somewhat more abstract than こと.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は物事を前向きに考える人だ。","romaji":"Kare wa monogoto o maemuki ni kangaeru hito da.","german":"He is a person who looks at things positively.","furigana":"かれ|ものごと|まえむ|かんが|ひと"},{"japanese":"物事には順序があるから、焦らないで。","romaji":"Monogoto ni wa junjo ga aru kara, aseranaide.","german":"Everything has its order, so don't rush.","furigana":"ものごと|じゅんじょ|あせ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"A sound whose source one cannot see: 変な物音がする, 物音一つしない (not a sound can be heard).","examples":[{"japanese":"夜中に台所で変な物音がした。","romaji":"Yonaka ni daidokoro de hen na monooto ga shita.","german":"In the middle of the night, I heard a strange noise in the kitchen.","furigana":"よなか|だいどころ|へん|ものおと"},{"japanese":"家の中は物音一つしなかった。","romaji":"Ie no naka wa monooto hitotsu shinakatta.","german":"There was not a single sound in the house.","furigana":"いえ|なか|ものおとひと"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"On its own somewhat technical (率が高い); mostly as a suffix: 合格率 (pass rate), 失業率, 確率. Note: in 引率 (to lead a group), the same kanji is read そつ.","examples":[{"japanese":"この試験は合格の率がとても低い。","romaji":"Kono shiken wa gōkaku no ritsu ga totemo hikui.","german":"The pass rate for this exam is very low.","furigana":"しけん|ごうかく|りつ|ひく"},{"japanese":"若い人ほど投票する率が低い。","romaji":"Wakai hito hodo tōhyō suru ritsu ga hikui.","german":"The younger the people, the lower the voting rate.","furigana":"わか|ひと|とうひょう|りつ|ひく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Geometric sphere (球の体積); in compounds e.g. 野球 (baseball), 地球 (the Earth), 電球 (light bulb). In baseball also a counter for pitches: 一球.","examples":[{"japanese":"球の体積を求める問題が出た。","romaji":"Kyū no taiseki o motomeru mondai ga deta.","german":"A problem asking to find the volume of a sphere came up.","furigana":"きゅう|たいせき|もと|もんだい|で"},{"japanese":"ピッチャーは最後の一球に全力を込めた。","romaji":"Pitchā wa saigo no ikkyū ni zenryoku o kometa.","german":"The pitcher put all his strength into the last pitch.","furigana":"さいご|いっきゅう|ぜんりょく|こ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Rare on its own, mostly in 田んぼ (rice field), 田植え (rice planting) and in many names (田中, 山田). Dry field: 畑.","examples":[{"japanese":"春になると、田に水が入る。","romaji":"Haru ni naru to, ta ni mizu ga hairu.","german":"In spring, the paddies are flooded with water.","furigana":"はる|た|みず|はい"},{"japanese":"田には稲が青々と育っている。","romaji":"Ta ni wa ine ga aoao to sodatte iru.","german":"Rice is growing lush and green in the paddy.","furigana":"た|いね|あおあお|そだ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Mainly in school and sports: 男子生徒, 男子トイレ, 男子サッカー. Counterpart: 女子.","examples":[{"japanese":"このクラスは男子が十五人、女子が十五人です。","romaji":"Kono kurasu wa danshi ga jūgonin, joshi ga jūgonin desu.","german":"This class has fifteen boys and fifteen girls.","furigana":"だんし|じゅうごにん|じょし|じゅうごにん"},{"japanese":"男子百メートルの決勝が始まった。","romaji":"Danshi hyaku mētoru no kesshō ga hajimatta.","german":"The men's 100 meters final has begun.","furigana":"だんしひゃく|けっしょう|はじ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"The outer covering of living things and fruit: りんごの皮をむく (to peel an apple), 木の皮. Tanned leather is written 革.","examples":[{"japanese":"りんごの皮をむいてください。","romaji":"Ringo no kawa o muite kudasai.","german":"Please peel the apple.","furigana":"かわ"},{"japanese":"日焼けして、背中の皮がむけた。","romaji":"Hiyake shite, senaka no kawa ga muketa.","german":"I got sunburned and the skin on my back peeled.","furigana":"ひや|せなか|かわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"The peak of a season or condition: 夏の盛り, 桜が盛りだ, 働き盛り (prime working age). The reading もり means \"portion\".","examples":[{"japanese":"今、桜がちょうど盛りです。","romaji":"Ima, sakura ga chōdo sakari desu.","german":"The cherry blossoms are at their peak right now.","furigana":"いま|さくら|さか"},{"japanese":"若い盛りを勉強だけで過ごすのはもったいない。","romaji":"Wakai sakari o benkyō dake de sugosu no wa mottainai.","german":"It would be a waste to spend one's youthful prime only studying.","furigana":"わか|さか|べんきょう|す"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Colloquial for すぐ／まもなく, usually in kana: もうじき (very soon), じきに分かる (will be clear soon). Kanji 直.","examples":[{"japanese":"もうじき春ですね。","romaji":"Mō jiki haru desu ne.","german":"It will soon be spring, won't it?","furigana":"はる"},{"japanese":"彼ならじきに来るから、少し待とう。","romaji":"Kare nara jiki ni kuru kara, sukoshi matō.","german":"He will come soon, so let's wait a little.","furigana":"かれ|く|すこ|ま"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Without a detour or intermediate layer: 床に直に座る, 本人に直に聞く. Often in kana じかに. Not to be confused with じきに (soon), which can also be written 直に.","examples":[{"japanese":"大事なことは本人に直に聞いたほうがいい。","romaji":"Daiji na koto wa honnin ni jika ni kiita hō ga ii.","german":"It is better to ask the person concerned directly about important matters.","furigana":"だいじ|ほんにん|じか|き"},{"japanese":"この薬は肌に直に塗らないでください。","romaji":"Kono kusuri wa hada ni jika ni nuranaide kudasai.","german":"Please do not apply this medicine directly to the skin.","furigana":"くすり|はだ|じか|ぬ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"な-adjective: 真っ赤なりんご, 顔が真っ赤になる (from shame or anger). Also 真っ赤なうそ (outright lie). Similarly formed: 真っ白, 真っ青.","examples":[{"japanese":"恥ずかしくて、顔が真っ赤になった。","romaji":"Hazukashikute, kao ga makka ni natta.","german":"I was so embarrassed that my face turned bright red.","furigana":"は|かお|ま|か"},{"japanese":"それは真っ赤なうそだ。","romaji":"Sore wa makka na uso da.","german":"That is a downright lie.","furigana":"ま|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Polite expression of regret when something is inconvenient: あいにく雨だった. As a reply: あいにく… (Sorry, but ...). Kanji 生憎 is rare.","examples":[{"japanese":"あいにく父は留守にしております。","romaji":"Ainiku chichi wa rusu ni shite orimasu.","german":"Unfortunately, my father is out right now.","furigana":"ちち|るす"},{"japanese":"旅行の日はあいにくの雨だった。","romaji":"Ryokō no hi wa ainiku no ame datta.","german":"Unfortunately, it rained on the day of the trip.","furigana":"りょこう|ひ|あめ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"In sports \"out\" (ball out of bounds, baseball out). Colloquially also \"not okay, too late\": それはアウトだ. Opposite: セーフ.","examples":[{"japanese":"ボールがラインの外に出て、アウトになった。","romaji":"Bōru ga rain no soto ni dete, auto ni natta.","german":"The ball went outside the line and was out.","furigana":"そと|で"},{"japanese":"会議に一時間も遅れたら、さすがにアウトだよ。","romaji":"Kaigi ni ichijikan mo okuretara, sasuga ni auto da yo.","german":"Being a whole hour late to the meeting is really unacceptable.","furigana":"かいぎ|いちじかん|おく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Various places: あちこちに (in many places), あちこち探す (to search everywhere). More formal: あちらこちら.","examples":[{"japanese":"鍵をなくして、家の中をあちこち探した。","romaji":"Kagi o nakushite, ie no naka o achikochi sagashita.","german":"I lost my key and searched all over the house.","furigana":"かぎ|いえ|なか|さが"},{"japanese":"春になると、あちこちで桜が咲く。","romaji":"Haru ni naru to, achikochi de sakura ga saku.","german":"In spring, cherry blossoms bloom everywhere.","furigana":"はる|さくら|さ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"A short exclamation of surprise or of suddenly remembering something; the small っ represents the abrupt cutoff. Longer and calmer: ああ.","examples":[{"japanese":"あっ、財布を忘れた！","romaji":"A, saifu o wasureta!","german":"Oh, I forgot my wallet!"},{"japanese":"あっ、田中さんだ。こんにちは。","romaji":"A, Tanaka-san da. Konnichiwa.","german":"Ah, it's Mr. Tanaka. Hello!"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Written-language \"or\" (everyday: または, か). As an adverb also \"perhaps\": あるいは本当かもしれない. Kanji 或いは is rare.","examples":[{"japanese":"申し込みはメール、あるいは電話でお願いします。","romaji":"Mōshikomi wa mēru, aruiwa denwa de onegai shimasu.","german":"Please apply by email or by phone.","furigana":"もう|こ|でんわ|ねが"},{"japanese":"あるいは彼の言うことが正しいのかもしれない。","romaji":"Aruiwa kare no iu koto ga tadashii no kamo shirenai.","german":"Perhaps what he says is right.","furigana":"かれ|い|ただ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Photo album (卒業アルバム = the school's graduation album) and music album.","examples":[{"japanese":"古いアルバムを見ると、子どもの頃を思い出す。","romaji":"Furui arubamu o miru to, kodomo no koro o omoidasu.","german":"When I look at old photo albums, I remember my childhood.","furigana":"ふる|み|こ|ころ|おも|だ"},{"japanese":"好きな歌手の新しいアルバムが出た。","romaji":"Suki na kashu no atarashii arubamu ga deta.","german":"My favorite singer's new album has come out.","furigana":"す|かしゅ|あたら|で"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 50"},
{"notes":"Series こんなに・そんなに・あんなに・どんなに. あんなに refers to something both people know or that lies in the past: あんなに練習したのに.","examples":[{"japanese":"あんなに練習したのに、試合に負けてしまった。","romaji":"Anna ni renshū shita noni, shiai ni makete shimatta.","german":"Even though we practiced so much, we lost the match.","furigana":"れんしゅう|しあい|ま"},{"japanese":"あんなに楽しい旅行は初めてだった。","romaji":"Anna ni tanoshii ryokō wa hajimete datta.","german":"I had never had such a fun trip before.","furigana":"たの|りょこう|はじ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 32"},
{"notes":"Colloquial form of あまり. With a negative: あんまり好きじゃない (not very much). Without a negative: \"too much, excessively\": あんまり食べると太るよ.","examples":[{"japanese":"納豆はあんまり好きじゃない。","romaji":"Nattō wa anmari suki ja nai.","german":"I don't like natto very much.","furigana":"なっとう|す"},{"japanese":"あんまり無理しないでね。","romaji":"Anmari muri shinaide ne.","german":"Don't push yourself too hard.","furigana":"むり"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"\"Whenever\": いつでも来てください. \"Never\" is not expressed with いつでも or いつも, but with 一度も～ない or 決して～ない. The kanji 何時でも is rare.","examples":[{"japanese":"困ったときは、いつでも連絡してください。","romaji":"Komatta toki wa, itsudemo renraku shite kudasai.","german":"If you're in trouble, contact me anytime.","furigana":"こま|れんらく"},{"japanese":"このコンビニはいつでも開いている。","romaji":"Kono konbini wa itsudemo aite iru.","german":"This convenience store is always open.","furigana":"あ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 26"},
{"notes":"\"For all eternity\" (いつまでも元気で) or, reproachfully, \"for ages\" (いつまでも寝ている). Kanji 何時までも is rare.","examples":[{"japanese":"いつまでもお元気でいてください。","romaji":"Itsumademo ogenki de ite kudasai.","german":"Please stay healthy forever.","furigana":"げんき"},{"japanese":"いつまでも寝ていないで、早く起きなさい。","romaji":"Itsumademo nete inaide, hayaku okinasai.","german":"Don't sleep forever, get up already.","furigana":"ね|はや|お"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Casual greeting for guests (at home, in a small shop); in stores the formal form is いらっしゃいませ. Also a polite request: こっちへいらっしゃい (come here).","examples":[{"japanese":"いらっしゃい。どうぞ上がって。","romaji":"Irasshai. Dōzo agatte.","german":"Welcome! Please come in.","furigana":"あ"},{"japanese":"またいつでも遊びにいらっしゃい。","romaji":"Mata itsudemo asobi ni irasshai.","german":"Come visit us again anytime.","furigana":"あそ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Ink for fountain pens and printers (プリンターのインク). Also written インキ.","examples":[{"japanese":"プリンターのインクがなくなった。","romaji":"Purintā no inku ga nakunatta.","german":"The printer is out of ink."},{"japanese":"このペンは青いインクを使っている。","romaji":"Kono pen wa aoi inku o tsukatte iru.","german":"This pen uses blue ink.","furigana":"あお|つか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Also written ウィスキー. Japanese whisky is internationally known; mixed with soda it is called ハイボール.","examples":[{"japanese":"父は寝る前にウイスキーを少し飲む。","romaji":"Chichi wa neru mae ni uisukī o sukoshi nomu.","german":"My father drinks a little whisky before bed.","furigana":"ちち|ね|まえ|すこ|の"},{"japanese":"日本のウイスキーは海外でも人気がある。","romaji":"Nihon no uisukī wa kaigai demo ninki ga aru.","german":"Japanese whisky is popular overseas too.","furigana":"にほん|かいがい|にんき"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"As a verb うがいする. In Japan, 手洗い・うがい (hand washing and gargling) after coming home is considered a basic rule against colds. Kanji 嗽／含嗽 are rare.","examples":[{"japanese":"家に帰ったら、手を洗ってうがいをしましょう。","romaji":"Ie ni kaettara, te o aratte ugai o shimashō.","german":"When you get home, wash your hands and gargle.","furigana":"いえ|かえ|て|あら"},{"japanese":"のどが痛いので、塩水でうがいした。","romaji":"Nodo ga itai node, shiomizu de ugai shita.","german":"My throat hurt, so I gargled with salt water.","furigana":"いた|しおみず"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Noun (楽しいおしゃべり), verb おしゃべりする, and な-adjective for people who talk a lot or too much: おしゃべりな人. Kanji お喋り.","examples":[{"japanese":"友達とカフェで二時間もおしゃべりした。","romaji":"Tomodachi to kafe de nijikan mo oshaberi shita.","german":"I chatted with friends at a cafe for a full two hours.","furigana":"ともだち|にじかん"},{"japanese":"彼はおしゃべりだから、秘密は話さないほうがいい。","romaji":"Kare wa oshaberi dakara, himitsu wa hanasanai hō ga ii.","german":"He is a chatterbox, so you'd better not tell him secrets.","furigana":"かれ|ひみつ|はな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Polite, very common form of 昼: お昼にしよう (Let's have lunch). Lunch is also お昼ご飯.","examples":[{"japanese":"そろそろお昼にしましょう。","romaji":"Sorosoro ohiru ni shimashō.","german":"Let's have lunch soon.","furigana":"ひる"},{"japanese":"お昼ごろに駅で会いましょう。","romaji":"Ohiru goro ni eki de aimashō.","german":"Let's meet at the station around noon.","furigana":"ひる|えき|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"い-adjective, somewhat more refined than かわいい; emphasizes an endearing impression. Kanji 可愛らしい.","examples":[{"japanese":"庭にかわいらしい花が咲いている。","romaji":"Niwa ni kawairashii hana ga saite iru.","german":"Lovely flowers are blooming in the garden.","furigana":"にわ|はな|さ"},{"japanese":"その子はかわいらしい声で歌った。","romaji":"Sono ko wa kawairashii koe de utatta.","german":"The child sang in a sweet voice.","furigana":"こ|こえ|うた"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Modern kitchen (in apartment listings: 1LDK = one room plus living, dining and kitchen area). Traditional word: 台所.","examples":[{"japanese":"新しいアパートのキッチンは広い。","romaji":"Atarashii apāto no kitchin wa hiroi.","german":"The kitchen in the new apartment is spacious.","furigana":"あたら|ひろ"},{"japanese":"キッチンで母が料理をしている。","romaji":"Kitchin de haha ga ryōri o shite iru.","german":"My mother is cooking in the kitchen.","furigana":"はは|りょうり"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 27"},
{"notes":"Mainly a team captain in sports; in a school club also 部長. A ship's captain is usually 船長.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼はサッカー部のキャプテンだ。","romaji":"Kare wa sakkābu no kyaputen da.","german":"He is the captain of the soccer team.","furigana":"かれ|ぶ"},{"japanese":"キャプテンがチームのみんなを励ました。","romaji":"Kyaputen ga chīmu no minna o hagemashita.","german":"The captain encouraged everyone on the team.","furigana":"はげ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"On its own it usually means \"classical music\" (クラシックを聴く); also \"classic, timeless\" (クラシックなデザイン).","examples":[{"japanese":"寝る前にクラシックを聴くと落ち着く。","romaji":"Neru mae ni kurashikku o kiku to ochitsuku.","german":"Listening to classical music before bed calms me down.","furigana":"ね|まえ|き|お|つ"},{"japanese":"この店はクラシックな雰囲気がある。","romaji":"Kono mise wa kurashikku na fun'iki ga aru.","german":"This restaurant has a classic atmosphere.","furigana":"みせ|ふんいき"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 9"},
{"notes":"Cream for cakes (生クリーム = whipped cream) and skin cream (ハンドクリーム). Ice cream: アイスクリーム.","examples":[{"japanese":"ケーキにクリームをたっぷりのせた。","romaji":"Kēki ni kurīmu o tappuri noseta.","german":"I put plenty of cream on the cake."},{"japanese":"冬は手が乾燥するので、クリームを塗る。","romaji":"Fuyu wa te ga kansō suru node, kurīmu o nuru.","german":"My hands get dry in winter, so I apply cream.","furigana":"ふゆ|て|かんそう|ぬ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"A drinking glass, especially for wine and beverages (ワイングラス); a water glass is often コップ. In compounds also eyeglasses: サングラス (sunglasses). Homophone: グラス (grass).","examples":[{"japanese":"グラスにワインを注いだ。","romaji":"Gurasu ni wain o tsuida.","german":"I poured wine into the glass.","furigana":"つ"},{"japanese":"グラスを落として割ってしまった。","romaji":"Gurasu o otoshite watte shimatta.","german":"I dropped a glass and it broke.","furigana":"お|わ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Noun and な-adjective: けちな人 (a stingy person), けちをつける (to find fault). Often in katakana, ケチ.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼はけちで、コーヒー一杯もおごってくれない。","romaji":"Kare wa kechi de, kōhī ippai mo ogotte kurenai.","german":"He is so stingy that he won't even treat you to a cup of coffee.","furigana":"かれ|いっぱい"},{"japanese":"そんなけちなことを言わないで。","romaji":"Sonna kechi na koto o iwanaide.","german":"Don't be so petty.","furigana":"い"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 14"},
{"notes":"Plural of これ, mainly in texts and presentations. In conversation, mostly これ or こういうもの. Also written これ等.","examples":[{"japanese":"これらの資料を明日までに読んでください。","romaji":"Korera no shiryō o ashita made ni yonde kudasai.","german":"Please read these materials by tomorrow.","furigana":"しりょう|あした|よ"},{"japanese":"これらはすべて手作りです。","romaji":"Korera wa subete tezukuri desu.","german":"All of these are handmade.","furigana":"てづく"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 44"},
{"notes":"Refers to something close to the speaker or in the current situation: こんなに寒いとは思わなかった. Series こんなに・そんなに・あんなに・どんなに.","examples":[{"japanese":"こんなに寒いとは思わなかった。","romaji":"Konna ni samui to wa omowanakatta.","german":"I never thought it would be this cold.","furigana":"さむ|おも"},{"japanese":"こんなにたくさん食べられないよ。","romaji":"Konna ni takusan taberarenai yo.","german":"I can't eat this much.","furigana":"た"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 32"},
{"notes":"The coach of a team; as a verb コーチする (to coach, to instruct). The head coach is often called 監督.","examples":[{"japanese":"テニスのコーチに教えてもらっている。","romaji":"Tenisu no kōchi ni oshiete moratte iru.","german":"I take lessons from a tennis coach.","furigana":"おし"},{"japanese":"コーチの指示で練習メニューが変わった。","romaji":"Kōchi no shiji de renshū menyū ga kawatta.","german":"The training schedule was changed on the coach's instructions.","furigana":"しじ|れんしゅう|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Code in the sense of an identifier or symbol (QRコード, バーコード), program code, and rules (ドレスコード). Homophones: コード (cord) and コード (chord).","examples":[{"japanese":"このコードを入力すると、送料が無料になります。","romaji":"Kono kōdo o nyūryoku suru to, sōryō ga muryō ni narimasu.","german":"If you enter this code, shipping is free.","furigana":"にゅうりょく|そうりょう|むりょう"},{"japanese":"兄は毎日パソコンでコードを書いている。","romaji":"Ani wa mainichi pasokon de kōdo o kaite iru.","german":"My older brother writes code on his computer every day.","furigana":"あに|まいにち|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"A goal in soccer (ゴールを決める = to score a goal), the finish line in running, and figuratively a goal in life or a project. As a verb ゴールする (to reach the finish line).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は試合の最後にゴールを決めた。","romaji":"Kare wa shiai no saigo ni gōru o kimeta.","german":"He scored a goal at the end of the match.","furigana":"かれ|しあい|さいご|き"},{"japanese":"マラソンで五時間かけてゴールした。","romaji":"Marason de gojikan kakete gōru shita.","german":"I crossed the finish line of the marathon after five hours.","furigana":"ごじかん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"A signature on documents (ここにサインしてください), a star's autograph, and a sign or signal (OKのサイン). In Japan, a seal (印鑑) is often used instead of a signature.","examples":[{"japanese":"書類の一番下にサインしてください。","romaji":"Shorui no ichiban shita ni sain shite kudasai.","german":"Please sign at the bottom of the document.","furigana":"しょるい|いちばんした"},{"japanese":"好きな選手にサインをもらった。","romaji":"Suki na senshu ni sain o moratta.","german":"I got an autograph from my favorite player.","furigana":"す|せんしゅ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Introduces a new topic or the next step, typical in speeches and letters: さて、次の話題です. Kanji 扠／偖 are rare.","examples":[{"japanese":"さて、次の議題に移りましょう。","romaji":"Sate, tsugi no gidai ni utsurimashō.","german":"Well then, let's move on to the next agenda item.","furigana":"つぎ|ぎだい|うつ"},{"japanese":"さて、そろそろ出かけようか。","romaji":"Sate, sorosoro dekakeyō ka.","german":"Well, shall we get going soon?","furigana":"で"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"An approximate amount (ざっと百人 = about a hundred people) or a quick skim (ざっと目を通す).","examples":[{"japanese":"会場にはざっと五百人が集まった。","romaji":"Kaijō ni wa zatto gohyakunin ga atsumatta.","german":"Roughly five hundred people gathered at the venue.","furigana":"かいじょう|ごひゃくにん|あつ"},{"japanese":"会議の前に資料にざっと目を通した。","romaji":"Kaigi no mae ni shiryō ni zatto me o tōshita.","german":"Before the meeting, I skimmed through the materials.","furigana":"かいぎ|まえ|しりょう|め|とお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"More literary than よく or たびたび. Kanji 屡々 are rare.","examples":[{"japanese":"この地域では冬にしばしば雪が降る。","romaji":"Kono chiiki de wa fuyu ni shibashiba yuki ga furu.","german":"In this region it often snows in winter.","furigana":"ちいき|ふゆ|ゆき|ふ"},{"japanese":"彼はしばしば会議に遅れてくる。","romaji":"Kare wa shibashiba kaigi ni okurete kuru.","german":"He often arrives late to meetings.","furigana":"かれ|かいぎ|おく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Exclamation when one notices a mistake (literally the past tense of しまう). Very common in everyday life, usually said to oneself.","examples":[{"japanese":"しまった！傘を電車に忘れた。","romaji":"Shimatta! Kasa o densha ni wasureta.","german":"Oops! I left my umbrella on the train.","furigana":"かさ|でんしゃ|わす"},{"japanese":"しまった、もうこんな時間だ。","romaji":"Shimatta, mō konna jikan da.","german":"Damn, it's already this late.","furigana":"じかん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"ジェット (jet) + 機 (machine, aircraft). Today people usually just say 飛行機.","examples":[{"japanese":"空を大きなジェット機が飛んでいく。","romaji":"Sora o ōki na jettoki ga tonde iku.","german":"A big jet is flying across the sky.","furigana":"そら|おお|き|と"},{"japanese":"社長は自家用ジェット機を持っているらしい。","romaji":"Shachō wa jikayō jettoki o motte iru rashii.","german":"The company president apparently owns a private jet.","furigana":"しゃちょう|じかよう|き|も"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Mainly ice skating (スケートをする); skating rink: スケートリンク. Skateboard is スケボー.","examples":[{"japanese":"冬になると湖でスケートをする。","romaji":"Fuyu ni naru to mizuumi de sukēto o suru.","german":"In winter we skate on the lake.","furigana":"ふゆ|みずうみ"},{"japanese":"スケートは初めてなので、まだうまく滑れない。","romaji":"Sukēto wa hajimete na node, mada umaku suberenai.","german":"It's my first time skating, so I can't glide well yet.","furigana":"はじ|すべ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Several meanings: spectator stand in a stadium, vending stand, desk or floor lamp (電気スタンド), and short for gas station (ガソリンスタンド).","examples":[{"japanese":"スタンドは応援する人でいっぱいだった。","romaji":"Sutando wa ōen suru hito de ippai datta.","german":"The stands were full of fans.","furigana":"おうえん|ひと"},{"japanese":"スタンドの明かりをつけて本を読んだ。","romaji":"Sutando no akari o tsukete hon o yonda.","german":"I turned on the desk lamp and read a book.","furigana":"あ|ほん|よ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"A famous person (映画スター) and the star symbol. A star in the sky is 星.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は一晩でスターになった。","romaji":"Kanojo wa hitoban de sutā ni natta.","german":"She became a star overnight.","furigana":"かのじょ|ひとばん"},{"japanese":"大事なメールにはスターを付けている。","romaji":"Daiji na mēru ni wa sutā o tsukete iru.","german":"I mark important emails with a star.","furigana":"だいじ|つ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"A set of things that belong together (食器のセット), a menu combination (ランチセット), a set in tennis/volleyball, and as the verb セットする (to set, to prepare: 目覚ましをセットする).","examples":[{"japanese":"ハンバーガーとポテトのセットを注文した。","romaji":"Hanbāgā to poteto no setto o chūmon shita.","german":"I ordered a set of a hamburger and fries.","furigana":"ちゅうもん"},{"japanese":"目覚まし時計を六時にセットした。","romaji":"Mezamashi dokei o rokuji ni setto shita.","german":"I set the alarm clock for six o'clock.","furigana":"めざ|どけい|ろくじ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 42"},
{"notes":"A facility (ショッピングセンター, 保健センター) or a central position (center stage; in baseball, center field).","examples":[{"japanese":"集合写真では、社長がセンターに立った。","romaji":"Shūgō shashin de wa, shachō ga sentā ni tatta.","german":"In the group photo, the company president stood in the center.","furigana":"しゅうごうしゃしん|しゃちょう|た"},{"japanese":"詳しいことは、センターの窓口でお問い合わせください。","romaji":"Kuwashii koto wa, sentā no madoguchi de otoiawase kudasai.","german":"For details, please inquire at the center's counter.","furigana":"くわ|まどぐち|と|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Conjunction: introduces what one does in response to the situation (そこで、私は…). Not to be confused with そこで = \"there\" (そこ + で).","examples":[{"japanese":"道が混んでいた。そこで、電車で行くことにした。","romaji":"Michi ga konde ita. Sokode, densha de iku koto ni shita.","german":"The roads were crowded. So I decided to go by train.","furigana":"みち|こ|でんしゃ|い"},{"japanese":"予算が足りない。そこで、スポンサーを探すことになった。","romaji":"Yosan ga tarinai. Sokode, suponsā o sagasu koto ni natta.","german":"The budget is insufficient. So we decided to look for sponsors.","furigana":"よさん|た|さが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"\"Sometime soon\" (そのうち会おう). With numbers also \"of those\": 十人来て、そのうち三人は学生だった. Kanji その内.","examples":[{"japanese":"そのうち一緒に飲みに行きましょう。","romaji":"Sonouchi issho ni nomi ni ikimashō.","german":"Let's go for a drink together sometime soon.","furigana":"いっしょ|の|い"},{"japanese":"十人が応募して、そのうち三人が合格した。","romaji":"Jūnin ga ōbo shite, sonouchi sannin ga gōkaku shita.","german":"Ten people applied, and three of them passed.","furigana":"じゅうにん|おうぼ|さんにん|ごうかく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Without changing anything (そのままでいい) or without interruption (そのまま寝てしまった). Kanji 其の儘 are very rare.","examples":[{"japanese":"靴はそのままで大丈夫ですよ。","romaji":"Kutsu wa sonomama de daijōbu desu yo.","german":"You can keep your shoes on.","furigana":"くつ|だいじょうぶ"},{"japanese":"疲れていたので、家に帰ってそのまま寝てしまった。","romaji":"Tsukarete ita node, ie ni kaette sonomama nete shimatta.","german":"I was tired, so I fell asleep as soon as I got home.","furigana":"つか|いえ|かえ|ね"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Also written ソファ.","examples":[{"japanese":"ソファーに座ってテレビを見た。","romaji":"Sofā ni suwatte terebi o mita.","german":"I sat on the sofa and watched TV.","furigana":"すわ|み"},{"japanese":"新しいソファーを部屋に運んだ。","romaji":"Atarashii sofā o heya ni hakonda.","german":"We carried the new sofa into the room.","furigana":"あたら|へや|はこ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Conjunction: what follows holds even though the preceding statement argues against it. The kanji spelling 其れでも is rare.","examples":[{"japanese":"雨が降っていた。それでも、試合は続けられた。","romaji":"Ame ga futte ita. Soredemo, shiai wa tsuzukerareta.","german":"It was raining. Even so, the game went on.","furigana":"あめ|ふ|しあい|つづ"},{"japanese":"何度失敗しても、それでも彼はあきらめなかった。","romaji":"Nando shippai shite mo, soredemo kare wa akiramenakatta.","german":"No matter how many times he failed, he still did not give up.","furigana":"なんどしっぱい|かれ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 45"},
{"notes":"Several uses: \"only\" (ただ見ているだけ), \"free\" (ただで = free of charge), \"ordinary\" (ただの風邪) and, at the start of a sentence, \"however\". The kanji 只／唯 are rare.","examples":[{"japanese":"このイベントは入場がただです。","romaji":"Kono ibento wa nyūjō ga tada desu.","german":"Admission to this event is free.","furigana":"にゅうじょう"},{"japanese":"ただの風邪だから、心配しないで。","romaji":"Tada no kaze dakara, shinpai shinaide.","german":"It's just an ordinary cold, so don't worry.","furigana":"かぜ|しんぱい"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 23"},
{"notes":"Something happens repeatedly (often slightly annoying): たびたびすみません (sorry for the repeated disturbance). Kanji 度々.","examples":[{"japanese":"たびたびご連絡してすみません。","romaji":"Tabitabi gorenraku shite sumimasen.","german":"Sorry to contact you again and again.","furigana":"れんらく"},{"japanese":"この道ではたびたび事故が起きている。","romaji":"Kono michi de wa tabitabi jiko ga okite iru.","german":"Accidents happen frequently on this road.","furigana":"みち|じこ|お"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"A very strong feeling: unbearable (暑くてたまらない), irresistibly good (この味がたまらない) or \"wanting badly\" (会いたくてたまらない). Kanji 堪らない.","examples":[{"japanese":"今日は暑くてたまらない。","romaji":"Kyō wa atsukute tamaranai.","german":"Today is unbearably hot.","furigana":"きょう|あつ"},{"japanese":"家族に会いたくてたまらない。","romaji":"Kazoku ni aitakute tamaranai.","german":"I miss my family terribly.","furigana":"かぞく|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"ダイヤ is short for ダイヤグラム and refers to the operating schedule of a train or bus, i.e. when which train runs - not the printed booklet, which is called 時刻表. Typical are ダイヤが乱れる \"train service is disrupted\" and ダイヤ改正 \"timetable revision\". The same sound ダイヤ is also short for ダイヤモンド \"diamond\" (ダイヤの指輪); the playing-card suit \"diamonds\" is also called ダイヤ. Which word is meant depends on the context.","examples":[{"japanese":"大雪で電車のダイヤが乱れている。","romaji":"Ōyuki de densha no daiya ga midarete iru.","german":"Train schedules are disrupted because of heavy snow.","furigana":"おおゆき|でんしゃ|みだ"},{"japanese":"来月から新しいダイヤになる。","romaji":"Raigetsu kara atarashii daiya ni naru.","german":"A new timetable takes effect next month.","furigana":"らいげつ|あたら"},{"japanese":"彼女は婚約指輪のダイヤを見せてくれた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kon'yaku yubiwa no daiya o misete kureta.","german":"She showed me the diamond on her engagement ring.","furigana":"かのじょ|こんやくゆびわ|み"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"A written-language, somewhat harsh conjunction (newspapers, novels, male speech). Polite: ですが; everyday: でも, けど.","examples":[{"japanese":"計画は完璧だった。だが、うまくいかなかった。","romaji":"Keikaku wa kanpeki datta. Daga, umaku ikanakatta.","german":"The plan was perfect. But it did not work out.","furigana":"けいかく|かんぺき"},{"japanese":"彼はまだ若い。だが、経験は豊富だ。","romaji":"Kare wa mada wakai. Daga, keiken wa hōfu da.","german":"He is still young. But he has plenty of experience.","furigana":"かれ|わか|けいけん|ほうふ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Casual sentence-initial conjunction, meaning the same as でも; often shortened to けど. More polite: ですが, けれども.","examples":[{"japanese":"行きたかった。だけど、仕事があった。","romaji":"Ikitakatta. Dakedo, shigoto ga atta.","german":"I wanted to go. But I had work.","furigana":"い|しごと"},{"japanese":"高いね。だけど、すごくおいしい。","romaji":"Takai ne. Dakedo, sugoku oishii.","german":"It's expensive, isn't it? But it's really delicious.","furigana":"たか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Colloquial, at the start of a sentence as a justification (\"but ... because ...\"): だって、知らなかったんだもん. After a noun \"also, even\": 子どもだって分かる.","examples":[{"japanese":"「どうして遅れたの？」「だって、電車が止まったんだもん。」","romaji":"\"Dōshite okureta no?\" \"Datte, densha ga tomatta n da mon.\"","german":"\"Why are you late?\" \"Well, because the train stopped.\"","furigana":"おく|でんしゃ|と"},{"japanese":"そんなこと、子どもだって分かるよ。","romaji":"Sonna koto, kodomo datte wakaru yo.","german":"Even a child understands something like that.","furigana":"こ|わ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"To pitch a tent: テントを張る. Very common when camping (キャンプ).","examples":[{"japanese":"川の近くにテントを張った。","romaji":"Kawa no chikaku ni tento o hatta.","german":"We pitched the tent near the river.","furigana":"かわ|ちか|は"},{"japanese":"雨でテントの中まで濡れてしまった。","romaji":"Ame de tento no naka made nurete shimatta.","german":"The rain soaked even the inside of the tent.","furigana":"あめ|なか|ぬ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Polite form of だから at the beginning of a sentence. It can sound impatient when you are repeating something: ですから、先ほど申し上げたように….","examples":[{"japanese":"明日は祝日です。ですから、店は休みです。","romaji":"Ashita wa shukujitsu desu. Desukara, mise wa yasumi desu.","german":"Tomorrow is a holiday. Therefore, the shop is closed.","furigana":"あした|しゅくじつ|みせ|やす"},{"japanese":"道が混んでいます。ですから、早めに出発しましょう。","romaji":"Michi ga konde imasu. Desukara, hayame ni shuppatsu shimashō.","german":"The roads are crowded. So let's leave early.","furigana":"みち|こ|はや|しゅっぱつ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 17"},
{"notes":"Introduces an unexpected result: one expected something, but it turned out differently. Kanji 所が are rare.","examples":[{"japanese":"晴れると思っていた。ところが、急に雨が降り出した。","romaji":"Hareru to omotte ita. Tokoroga, kyū ni ame ga furidashita.","german":"I thought it would be sunny. But suddenly it started to rain.","furigana":"は|おも|きゅう|あめ|ふ|だ"},{"japanese":"簡単な試験だと聞いていた。ところが、全然できなかった。","romaji":"Kantan na shiken da to kiite ita. Tokoroga, zenzen dekinakatta.","german":"I had heard it was an easy exam. But I couldn't do any of it.","furigana":"かんたん|しけん|き|ぜんぜん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"First place (クラスでトップ), the head of an organization (会社のトップ), and the upper side. Also the home page of a website (トップページ).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は試験でトップの成績を取った。","romaji":"Kanojo wa shiken de toppu no seiseki o totta.","german":"She got the top score on the exam.","furigana":"かのじょ|しけん|せいせき|と"},{"japanese":"会社のトップが記者会見で謝罪した。","romaji":"Kaisha no toppu ga kisha kaiken de shazai shita.","german":"The company's top management apologized at a press conference.","furigana":"かいしゃ|きしゃかいけん|しゃざい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"A unit of weight, usually the metric ton. The kanji 屯 is a rare ateji spelling. Homophone: とん (sound of a knock).","examples":[{"japanese":"このトラックは十トンの荷物を運べる。","romaji":"Kono torakku wa jū ton no nimotsu o hakoberu.","german":"This truck can carry ten tons of cargo.","furigana":"じゅう|にもつ|はこ"},{"japanese":"毎年、何百万トンもの食べ物が捨てられている。","romaji":"Maitoshi, nanbyakuman ton mo no tabemono ga suterarete iru.","german":"Every year, millions of tons of food are thrown away.","furigana":"まいとし|なんびゃくまん|た|もの|す"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"い-adjective: outrageous, preposterous (とんでもない値段). As a modest reply to thanks or praise, \"Not at all, don't mention it\": とんでもないです.","examples":[{"japanese":"駅前でとんでもない事故が起きた。","romaji":"Ekimae de tondemonai jiko ga okita.","german":"An unbelievable accident happened in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|じこ|お"},{"japanese":"「ありがとうございました。」「いえ、とんでもないです。」","romaji":"\"Arigatō gozaimashita.\" \"Ie, tondemonai desu.\"","german":"\"Thank you very much.\" \"Not at all, don't mention it.\""}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"With a wish: \"by all means\" (どうしても行きたい). With a negative: \"try as I might, can't\" (どうしても思い出せない). Kanji 如何しても are rare.","examples":[{"japanese":"どうしてもこの映画が見たい。","romaji":"Dōshitemo kono eiga ga mitai.","german":"I really want to see this movie.","furigana":"えいが|み"},{"japanese":"彼の名前がどうしても思い出せない。","romaji":"Kare no namae ga dōshitemo omoidasenai.","german":"I just can't remember his name.","furigana":"かれ|なまえ|おも|だ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Indefinite place: どこかへ行きたい. Adverbially also \"somehow, in a certain way\": どこか寂しそうだ. The kanji 何処か is rare.","examples":[{"japanese":"週末はどこかへ出かけたい。","romaji":"Shūmatsu wa dokoka e dekaketai.","german":"I want to go out somewhere on the weekend.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|で"},{"japanese":"彼の笑顔はどこか寂しそうだった。","romaji":"Kare no egao wa dokoka sabishisō datta.","german":"His smile looked somehow sad.","furigana":"かれ|えがお|さび"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 13"},
{"notes":"An outing by car: ドライブに行く／ドライブする. It does not mean \"driving a car\" in general (that is 運転する).","examples":[{"japanese":"日曜日に海までドライブした。","romaji":"Nichiyōbi ni umi made doraibu shita.","german":"On Sunday we drove to the sea.","furigana":"にちようび|うみ"},{"japanese":"紅葉を見にドライブに行こう。","romaji":"Kōyō o mi ni doraibu ni ikō.","german":"Let's go for a drive to see the autumn leaves.","furigana":"こうよう|み|い"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 11"},
{"notes":"Usually an elegant dress (ウエディングドレス = wedding dress). An everyday dress is more often ワンピース.","examples":[{"japanese":"結婚式のために新しいドレスを買った。","romaji":"Kekkonshiki no tame ni atarashii doresu o katta.","german":"I bought a new dress for the wedding.","furigana":"けっこんしき|あたら|か"},{"japanese":"花嫁の白いドレスがとてもきれいだった。","romaji":"Hanayome no shiroi doresu ga totemo kirei datta.","german":"The bride's white dress was very beautiful.","furigana":"はなよめ|しろ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"A question or exclamation (どんなにうれしかったか = how happy I was) and, with ても, \"no matter how much\": どんなに頑張っても.","examples":[{"japanese":"どんなに頑張っても、彼には勝てなかった。","romaji":"Donna ni ganbatte mo, kare ni wa katenakatta.","german":"No matter how hard I tried, I couldn't beat him.","furigana":"がんば|かれ|か"},{"japanese":"合格したとき、どんなにうれしかったことか。","romaji":"Gōkaku shita toki, donna ni ureshikatta koto ka.","german":"How happy I was when I passed!","furigana":"ごうかく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"With a negative, \"nothing\" (なにも食べていない). Also \"there's no need to (go so far as to)\": なにも泣くことはない (There's no need to cry). Often also written 何も.","examples":[{"japanese":"朝からなにも食べていない。","romaji":"Asa kara nanimo tabete inai.","german":"I haven't eaten anything since this morning.","furigana":"あさ|た"},{"japanese":"なにもそんなに怒らなくてもいいじゃない。","romaji":"Nanimo sonna ni okoranakute mo ii ja nai.","german":"There's no need to get so angry about it.","furigana":"おこ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Colloquial, after nouns: gives an example (お茶なんかどう？) or plays something down (私なんか…). Cf. the grammar entry ～なんて／～なんか. Identical in sound: なんか = 何か (somehow, something).","examples":[{"japanese":"お土産にお菓子なんかどうですか。","romaji":"Omiyage ni okashi nanka dō desu ka.","german":"How about some sweets as a souvenir?","furigana":"みやげ|かし"},{"japanese":"私なんか、まだまだ下手です。","romaji":"Watashi nanka, madamada heta desu.","german":"Someone like me is still far from good.","furigana":"わたし|へた"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A brief, warm smile: にっこり笑う／にっこりする. A lasting smile: にこにこ.","examples":[{"japanese":"赤ちゃんが私を見てにっこり笑った。","romaji":"Akachan ga watashi o mite nikkori waratta.","german":"The baby looked at me and smiled.","furigana":"あか|わたし|み|わら"},{"japanese":"店員さんがにっこりして「ありがとうございます」と言った。","romaji":"Ten'in-san ga nikkori shite \"arigatō gozaimasu\" to itta.","german":"The shop assistant smiled and said, \"Thank you very much.\"","furigana":"てんいん|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"A clear \"no\" (ノーと言う) and, as a prefix, \"without\": ノーメイク, ノー残業デー (no-overtime day).","examples":[{"japanese":"いやなときは、はっきりノーと言ったほうがいい。","romaji":"Iya na toki wa, hakkiri nō to itta hō ga ii.","german":"When you don't want something, you should say no clearly.","furigana":"い"},{"japanese":"水曜日はノー残業デーです。","romaji":"Suiyōbi wa nō zangyō dē desu.","german":"Wednesday is a no-overtime day.","furigana":"すいようび|ざんぎょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Hesitant agreement (はあ、そうですか); with rising intonation a disbelieving \"Huh?\"; and as a sigh. With superiors, はあ? can come across as impolite.","examples":[{"japanese":"「明日も残業だって。」「はあ、そうですか……。」","romaji":"\"Ashita mo zangyō datte.\" \"Hā, sō desu ka...\"","german":"\"We have overtime again tomorrow.\" \"Oh, I see...\"","furigana":"あした|ざんぎょう"},{"japanese":"はあ、今日も疲れたなあ。","romaji":"Hā, kyō mo tsukareta nā.","german":"Haa, I was tired again today.","furigana":"きょう|つか"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 41"},
{"notes":"Short form: オートマ (automatic transmission). Opposite: マニュアル.","examples":[{"japanese":"私の免許はオートマチック車限定です。","romaji":"Watashi no menkyo wa ōtomachikku sha gentei desu.","german":"My license is limited to automatic cars.","furigana":"わたし|めんきょ|しゃげんてい"},{"japanese":"このカメラはオートマチックでピントを合わせてくれる。","romaji":"Kono kamera wa ōtomachikku de pinto o awasete kureru.","german":"This camera focuses automatically.","furigana":"あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"As a verb オープンする (to open): 新しい店がオープンした. As a な-adjective \"open, open-minded\": オープンな性格.","examples":[{"japanese":"駅前に新しいカフェがオープンした。","romaji":"Ekimae ni atarashii kafe ga ōpun shita.","german":"A new cafe opened in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|あたら"},{"japanese":"彼女はオープンな性格で、誰とでも話せる。","romaji":"Kanojo wa ōpun na seikaku de, dare to demo hanaseru.","german":"She has an open personality and can talk to anyone.","furigana":"かのじょ|せいかく|だれ|はな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mostly written in hiragana (the kanji 襁褓 is very rare). Disposable diaper: 紙おむつ. おむつを替える (to change a diaper).","examples":[{"japanese":"赤ちゃんのおむつを替えた。","romaji":"Akachan no omutsu o kaeta.","german":"I changed the baby's diaper.","furigana":"あか|か"},{"japanese":"旅行のために、おむつを多めに買っておいた。","romaji":"Ryokō no tame ni, omutsu o ōme ni katte oita.","german":"I bought extra diapers for the trip.","furigana":"りょこう|おお|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"E.g. for new students or employees: 新入生オリエンテーション.","examples":[{"japanese":"入学式のあと、オリエンテーションがあります。","romaji":"Nyūgakushiki no ato, orientēshon ga arimasu.","german":"There will be an orientation after the entrance ceremony.","furigana":"にゅうがくしき"},{"japanese":"オリエンテーションで授業の選び方を教わった。","romaji":"Orientēshon de jugyō no erabikata o osowatta.","german":"At the orientation, I was taught how to choose classes.","furigana":"じゅぎょう|えら|かた|おそ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"As a verb おんぶする (to carry a child on one's back). Figuratively: 親におんぶする (to live off one's parents). To carry in one's arms is だっこ.","examples":[{"japanese":"父は疲れた娘をおんぶして家まで歩いた。","romaji":"Chichi wa tsukareta musume o onbu shite ie made aruita.","german":"My father carried his tired daughter piggyback and walked home.","furigana":"ちち|つか|むすめ|いえ|ある"},{"japanese":"いつまでも親におんぶしているわけにはいかない。","romaji":"Itsu made mo oya ni onbu shite iru wake ni wa ikanai.","german":"I can't keep depending on my parents forever.","furigana":"おや"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The guidebook as a book; the person who guides is called ガイド.","examples":[{"japanese":"ガイドブックを見ながら、京都の寺を回った。","romaji":"Gaidobukku o minagara, Kyōto no tera o mawatta.","german":"I went around the temples of Kyoto with a guidebook in hand.","furigana":"み|きょうと|てら|まわ"},{"japanese":"ガイドブックに載っていない店を探した。","romaji":"Gaidobukku ni notte inai mise o sagashita.","german":"I looked for shops that are not in the guidebook.","furigana":"の|みせ|さが"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 30"},
{"notes":"A mixed alcoholic drink: カクテルを作る (to mix a cocktail).","examples":[{"japanese":"バーでカクテルを注文した。","romaji":"Bā de kakuteru o chūmon shita.","german":"I ordered a cocktail at the bar.","furigana":"ちゅうもん"},{"japanese":"彼女はお酒に弱いので、甘いカクテルを一杯だけ飲んだ。","romaji":"Kanojo wa osake ni yowai node, amai kakuteru o ippai dake nonda.","german":"She can't hold her liquor, so she had just one sweet cocktail.","furigana":"かのじょ|さけ|よわ|あま|いっぱい|の"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A children's word; at the school sports day: 運動会の駆けっこ.","examples":[{"japanese":"公園で子どもたちが駆けっこをしている。","romaji":"Kōen de kodomotachi ga kakekko o shite iru.","german":"Children are racing each other in the park.","furigana":"こうえん|こ|か"},{"japanese":"運動会の駆けっこで一等になった。","romaji":"Undōkai no kakekko de ittō ni natta.","german":"I came in first in the footrace at the sports day.","furigana":"うんどうかい|か|いっとう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Noun of 片付ける; 後片付け (tidying up afterward, e.g. after a meal).","examples":[{"japanese":"年末は部屋の片付けで忙しい。","romaji":"Nenmatsu wa heya no katazuke de isogashii.","german":"At the end of the year I'm busy tidying my room.","furigana":"ねんまつ|へや|かたづ|いそが"},{"japanese":"パーティーの片付けをみんなで手伝った。","romaji":"Pātī no katazuke o minna de tetsudatta.","german":"Everyone helped with the cleanup after the party.","furigana":"かたづ|てつだ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"As the verb カットする: 髪をカットする, 予算をカットする (to cut back), シーンをカットする (to cut out).","examples":[{"japanese":"美容院で髪をカットしてもらった。","romaji":"Biyōin de kami o katto shite moratta.","german":"I had my hair cut at the hair salon.","furigana":"びよういん|かみ"},{"japanese":"予算の関係で、そのシーンはカットされた。","romaji":"Yosan no kankei de, sono shīn wa katto sareta.","german":"That scene was cut because of the budget.","furigana":"よさん|かんけい"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 44"},
{"notes":"Also じゅうたん. カーペットを敷く (to lay a carpet).","examples":[{"japanese":"リビングに新しいカーペットを敷いた。","romaji":"Ribingu ni atarashii kāpetto o shiita.","german":"I laid a new carpet in the living room.","furigana":"あたら|し"},{"japanese":"カーペットにコーヒーをこぼしてしまった。","romaji":"Kāpetto ni kōhī o koboshite shimatta.","german":"I spilled coffee on the carpet."}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Occupation: photographer (press, weddings) or cameraman in film and television.","examples":[{"japanese":"結婚式にプロのカメラマンを呼んだ。","romaji":"Kekkonshiki ni puro no kameraman o yonda.","german":"We called a professional photographer to the wedding.","furigana":"けっこんしき|よ"},{"japanese":"テレビ局のカメラマンが事故現場を撮影していた。","romaji":"Terebikyoku no kameraman ga jiko genba o satsuei shite ita.","german":"A cameraman from the TV station was filming the scene of the accident.","furigana":"きょく|じこげんば|さつえい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"At or in a house; a parking lot is 駐車場.","examples":[{"japanese":"車をガレージに入れた。","romaji":"Kuruma o garēji ni ireta.","german":"I put the car in the garage.","furigana":"くるま|い"},{"japanese":"ガレージを片付けて、自転車を置く場所を作った。","romaji":"Garēji o katazukete, jitensha o oku basho o tsukutta.","german":"I cleaned out the garage to make space for the bicycles.","furigana":"かたづ|じてんしゃ|お|ばしょ|つく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"This card covers a geographical meaning of 州 read しゅう, which the 精選版日本国語大辞典 records with an old attestation. Classifying it as older dictionary usage is an editorial judgment. For present-day geographical descriptions you will more often encounter 州・す, 中州・なかす or 砂州・さす. The examples show looking up and explaining this meaning. The common meaning \"state, province\" belongs to the other card 州・しゅう.","examples":[{"japanese":"辞書で「州」を調べると、土砂が積もってできた陸地という意味も見つかった。","romaji":"Jisho de \"shū\" o shiraberu to, dosha ga tsumotte dekita rikuchi to iu imi mo mitsukatta.","german":"When I looked up \"州\" in the dictionary, I also found the meaning of land formed by accumulated earth and sand.","furigana":"じしょ|しゅう|しら|どしゃ|つ|りくち|いみ|み"},{"japanese":"先生は、「州」のこの意味は、アメリカの州とは違うと説明した。","romaji":"Sensei wa, \"shū\" no kono imi wa, Amerika no shū to wa chigau to setsumei shita.","german":"The teacher explained that this meaning of \"州\" is different from the \"state\" of the American states.","furigana":"せんせい|しゅう|いみ|しゅう|ちが|せつめい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"運 is a noun for luck, fate or the role of chance. 運がいい describes good luck, 運が悪い accordingly bad luck. The word should be distinguished from the identically sounding casual reply うん. 運 itself is not inflected; in 運が悪く, the following i-adjective 悪い takes its connective form.","examples":[{"japanese":"財布が戻ってきたのは、運がよかったからです。","romaji":"Saifu ga modotte kita no wa, un ga yokatta kara desu.","german":"I got my wallet back because I was lucky.","furigana":"さいふ|もど|うん"},{"japanese":"今日は運が悪く、乗ろうとしたバスが故障しました。","romaji":"Kyō wa un ga waruku, norō to shita basu ga koshō shimashita.","german":"I had bad luck today: the bus I was about to take broke down.","furigana":"きょう|うん|わる|の|こしょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Here 計 means a plan or scheme and appears especially in literary compounds such as 一年の計. In ordinary conversation, 計画 is the usual, more explicit word. の links the plan with its time frame or its owner. In other compounds the same character also has meanings such as measuring instrument or total; this reading means the scheme.","examples":[{"japanese":"正月に、今年一年の計を家族と話し合いました。","romaji":"Shōgatsu ni, kotoshi ichinen no kei o kazoku to hanashiaimashita.","german":"At New Year, I discussed this year's plan with my family.","furigana":"しょうがつ|ことしいちねん|けい|かぞく|はな|あ"},{"japanese":"町の百年の計として、森を守る方針が決まりました。","romaji":"Machi no hyakunen no kei to shite, mori o mamoru hōshin ga kimarimashita.","german":"The policy of protecting the forest was decided as the town's hundred-year plan.","furigana":"まち|ひゃくねん|けい|もり|まも|ほうしん|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"昨 is a time prefix. It stands before a time expression, e.g. 昨年（さくねん）for last year and 昨夜（さくや）for last night. It is read as part of a compound and is not a standalone word for yesterday. 昨日 has the formal reading さくじつ and the everyday reading きのう; the reading of the whole word must therefore be learned too.","examples":[{"japanese":"昨年は、この町で働いていました。","romaji":"Sakunen wa, kono machi de hataraite imashita.","german":"Last year I worked in this town.","furigana":"さくねん|まち|はたら"},{"japanese":"昨夜の雨で、庭の土がぬれています。","romaji":"Sakuya no ame de, niwa no tsuchi ga nurete imasu.","german":"The ground in the garden is wet from last night's rain.","furigana":"さくや|あめ|にわ|つち"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"次第 can denote, as a noun, the course or sequence of events of something, e.g. 式の次第 or 事の次第. After a noun it also expresses dependence: 天気次第 means depending on the weather. After the stem of the ます-form it means \"as soon as\", as in でき次第. This attachment form distinguishes the temporal use from the dependence use.","examples":[{"japanese":"式の次第を確認してから、会場に入りました。","romaji":"Shiki no shidai o kakunin shite kara, kaijō ni hairimashita.","german":"I checked the program of the ceremony before entering the venue.","furigana":"しき|しだい|かくにん|かいじょう|はい"},{"japanese":"事情を知らない人にも、事の次第を説明しました。","romaji":"Jijō o shiranai hito ni mo, koto no shidai o setsumei shimashita.","german":"I explained how things had come about even to people who did not know the circumstances.","furigana":"じじょう|し|ひと|こと|しだい|せつめい"},{"japanese":"参加するかどうかは、明日の天気次第です。","romaji":"Sanka suru ka dō ka wa, ashita no tenki shidai desu.","german":"Whether I take part depends on tomorrow's weather.","furigana":"さんか|あした|てんきしだい"},{"japanese":"準備ができ次第、出発します。","romaji":"Junbi ga deki shidai, shuppatsu shimasu.","german":"We will set off as soon as preparations are ready.","furigana":"じゅんび|しだい|しゅっぱつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"辛い is read つらい here and describes emotional pain or a situation that is hard to bear. It is often written in hiragana as つらい. The reading からい of the same kanji denotes a spicy taste and belongs to a different word. As an i-adjective, つらい becomes つらかった in the past and つらくて before て.","examples":[{"japanese":"家族と別れるのは、つらいです。","romaji":"Kazoku to wakareru no wa, tsurai desu.","german":"It is painful to part from my family.","furigana":"かぞく|わか"},{"japanese":"一人で全部の仕事をするのは辛かったので、同僚に手伝ってもらいました。","romaji":"Hitori de zenbu no shigoto o suru no wa tsurakatta node, dōryō ni tetsudatte moraimashita.","german":"It was hard doing all the work alone, so I had a colleague help me.","furigana":"ひとり|ぜんぶ|しごと|つら|どうりょう|てつだ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"間 is read ま here and denotes an interval in time, a pause or a gap. 間を置く means to pause or leave an interval. 間がある describes free space between things. The reading あいだ of the same kanji belongs to a different usage and is not replaced by this entry.","examples":[{"japanese":"少し間を置いてから、質問に答えました。","romaji":"Sukoshi ma o oite kara, shitsumon ni kotaemashita.","german":"After a brief pause, I answered the question.","furigana":"すこ|ま|お|しつもん|こた"},{"japanese":"椅子は、間を空けて並べてください。","romaji":"Isu wa, ma o akete narabete kudasai.","german":"Please arrange the chairs with gaps between them.","furigana":"いす|ま|あ|なら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"役 denotes an assigned duty or function and also the role of a character in theater or film. 役を引き受ける means to take on the duty; 父親の役 names a father figure that is played. The exact function is clear from the context. This noun is read やく and should be distinguished from similar-sounding words such as 焼く or 約.","examples":[{"japanese":"私は、会議の司会の役を引き受けました。","romaji":"Watashi wa, kaigi no shikai no yaku o hikiukemashita.","german":"I took on the role of moderating the meeting.","furigana":"わたし|かいぎ|しかい|やく|ひ|う"},{"japanese":"この映画で、彼は父親の役を演じています。","romaji":"Kono eiga de, kare wa chichioya no yaku o enjite imasu.","german":"In this film, he plays the role of a father.","furigana":"えいが|かれ|ちちおや|やく|えん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"訳 wird hier やく gelesen und bezeichnet eine Übersetzung oder die übersetzte Fassung eines Textes. 訳を読む heißt die Übersetzung lesen; 訳が違う kann unterschiedliche Formulierungen in Übersetzungen bezeichnen. Mit Deutsch und anderen Sprachbezeichnungen entstehen Verbindungen wie ドイツ語訳. Die Lesung わけ bedeutet unter anderem Grund und gehört zu einem anderen Eintrag.","examples":[{"japanese":"原文が難しかったので、ドイツ語の訳を読みました。","romaji":"Genbun ga muzukashikatta node, Doitsugo no yaku o yomimashita.","german":"Da der Originaltext schwierig war, las ich die deutsche Übersetzung.","furigana":"げんぶん|むずか|ご|やく|よ"},{"japanese":"同じ小説でも、二つの訳では言葉の選び方が違います。","romaji":"Onaji shōsetsu de mo, futatsu no yaku de wa kotoba no erabikata ga chigaimasu.","german":"Auch beim selben Roman unterscheidet sich die Wortwahl zwischen zwei Übersetzungen.","furigana":"おな|しょうせつ|ふた|やく|ことば|えら|かた|ちが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"轢く means to hit or run over someone or something with a vehicle. The affected living being comes before を; in a passive sentence, 車に names the vehicle responsible. The godan verb becomes 轢きます, 轢いて and 轢かれる. It is also written ひく in hiragana; the identically pronounced word 引く means \"to pull\" depending on context and is a different entry.","examples":[{"japanese":"車が道を渡っていた人を轢きました。","romaji":"Kuruma ga michi o watatte ita hito o hikimashita.","german":"A car ran over a person who was crossing the road.","furigana":"くるま|みち|わた|ひと|ひ"},{"japanese":"あの犬は、車に轢かれたそうです。","romaji":"Ano inu wa, kuruma ni hikareta sō desu.","german":"I heard that dog was run over by a car.","furigana":"いぬ|くるま|ひ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"前進 is a noun for forward movement and for progress on a task. 大きな前進です evaluates a result as great progress. With する it becomes the intransitive verb \"to move forward\" or \"to make progress\"; 前進できる expresses that this is possible. The identically read 全身 means \"the whole body\".","examples":[{"japanese":"話し合いで意見がまとまったことは、大きな前進です。","romaji":"Hanashiai de iken ga matomatta koto wa, ōkina zenshin desu.","german":"Reaching agreement in the discussion is a big step forward.","furigana":"はな|あ|いけん|おお|ぜんしん"},{"japanese":"車は前進できず、後ろに戻りました。","romaji":"Kuruma wa zenshin dekizu, ushiro ni modorimashita.","german":"The car could not move forward and went back.","furigana":"くるま|ぜんしん|うし|もど"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"無視 is a noun for deliberately ignoring or disregarding. 規則の無視 names the disregard of rules as a matter being discussed. With する it forms the transitive verb 無視する; 無視される is its passive form. The one who ignores stands before に in the passive. The similar-sounding 虫, by contrast, means \"insect\".","examples":[{"japanese":"規則の無視は認められません。","romaji":"Kisoku no mushi wa mitomeraremasen.","german":"Disregard of the rules is not permitted.","furigana":"きそく|むし|みと"},{"japanese":"彼に無視されて、悲しくなりました。","romaji":"Kare ni mushi sarete, kanashiku narimashita.","german":"Being ignored by him made me sad.","furigana":"かれ|むし|かな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"描く is read かく here and denotes drawing, painting or sketching. The picture or depicted thing comes before を; the surface can be marked with に. The て-form is 描いて（かいて）. The same kanji also has the reading えがく; for \"to describe\" or \"to depict\", えがく is normally used. This entry practices concrete drawing with かく.","examples":[{"japanese":"妹は紙に猫の絵を描きました。","romaji":"Imōto wa kami ni neko no e o kakimashita.","german":"My younger sister drew a picture of a cat on paper.","furigana":"いもうと|かみ|ねこ|え|か"},{"japanese":"ここに簡単な地図を描いてください。","romaji":"Koko ni kantan na chizu o kaite kudasai.","german":"Please draw a simple map here.","furigana":"かんたん|ちず|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"魚 is read うお on this card; the reading さかな has the same meaning. Pronounce u and o one after the other: うお has two morae and does not become a long vowel. In 魚河岸（うおがし, fish market district）this reading is preserved. The examples describe fish as animals, not a prepared fish dish.","examples":[{"japanese":"川の底を泳ぐ魚の影が見えた。","romaji":"Kawa no soko o oyogu uo no kage ga mieta.","german":"I could see the shadows of fish swimming along the river bottom.","furigana":"かわ|そこ|およ|うお|かげ|み"},{"japanese":"網を引くと、銀色の魚が跳ねた。","romaji":"Ami o hiku to, gin’iro no uo ga haneta.","german":"When the net was pulled in, a silver fish leapt.","furigana":"あみ|ひ|ぎんいろ|うお|は"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"にっぽん and にほん are two readings of the country name 日本. In にっぽん, the small っ creates a brief closure before p; romanization therefore has pp. 日本の links the country name with e.g. 選手, \"athletes from Japan\". Both readings denote the same country; にっぽん is not a different territory.","examples":[{"japanese":"日本は島の多い国です。","romaji":"Nippon wa shima no ōi kuni desu.","german":"Japan is a country with many islands.","furigana":"にっぽん|しま|おお|くに"},{"japanese":"日本の選手を応援しています。","romaji":"Nippon no senshu o ōen shite imasu.","german":"I support the athletes from Japan.","furigana":"にっぽん|せんしゅ|おうえん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"みょうごにち denotes the day after tomorrow just like あさって, but sounds more formal and suits e.g. a business notice. 明後日までに sets a deadline: by the day after tomorrow at the latest. 明後日は makes the day the topic of an announcement. The ょ forms a unit with み; う then lengthens the o sound.","examples":[{"japanese":"資料は明後日までにお送りいたします。","romaji":"Shiryō wa myōgonichi made ni ookuri itashimasu.","german":"I will send you the materials by the day after tomorrow.","furigana":"しりょう|みょうごにち|おく"},{"japanese":"明後日は研修のため、窓口を休ませていただきます。","romaji":"Myōgonichi wa kenshū no tame, madoguchi o yasumasete itadakimasu.","german":"The day after tomorrow, our counter will be closed for training.","furigana":"みょうごにち|けんしゅう|まどぐち|やす"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"こんにち can mean \"these days\" or, in a formal text, \"today\". 今日では contrasts the present time with earlier conditions; 今日より can mean \"from today\" in a notice. For the single day of today, きょう is also used. Do not confuse the word with the greeting こんにちは: its final part is pronounced wa.","examples":[{"japanese":"今日では、海外の人とも簡単に連絡が取れます。","romaji":"Konnichi de wa, kaigai no hito to mo kantan ni renraku ga toremasu.","german":"These days it is easy to contact people overseas.","furigana":"こんにち|かいがい|ひと|かんたん|れんらく|と"},{"japanese":"今日より、受付の場所を変更いたします。","romaji":"Konnichi yori, uketsuke no basho o henkō itashimasu.","german":"As of today, we are changing the location of the reception.","furigana":"こんにち|うけつけ|ばしょ|へんこう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"ばいう is, besides つゆ, a reading for the East Asian rainy season. It appears e.g. in weather descriptions and in 梅雨前線（ばいうぜんせん, rainy-season front）. 梅雨の時期 denotes this period; 梅雨が長引く describes a rainy season lasting longer than expected. The word can also denote the rain during this season, e.g. in a description of a landscape. The vowels a, i and u remain three morae.","examples":[{"japanese":"梅雨の時期は、洗濯物が乾きにくい。","romaji":"Baiu no jiki wa, sentakumono ga kawaki nikui.","german":"During the rainy season, laundry is slow to dry.","furigana":"ばいう|じき|せんたくもの|かわ"},{"japanese":"梅雨が長引いたため、畑の作物の生育が遅れた。","romaji":"Baiu ga nagabiita tame, hatake no sakumotsu no seiiku ga okureta.","german":"Because the rainy season dragged on, the growth of the crops in the fields was delayed.","furigana":"ばいう|ながび|はたけ|さくもつ|せいいく|おく"},{"japanese":"梅雨が窓を静かに濡らしていた。","romaji":"Baiu ga mado o shizuka ni nurashite ita.","german":"The rain of the rainy season quietly wet the window.","furigana":"ばいう|まど|しず|ぬ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"もんじ and もじ can both mean \"written character\". The reading もんじ keeps the ん between も and じ; monji therefore has three morae. This reading also occurs in older literary texts. The examples focus on the visible writing of an inscription and a letter; \"letter\" as a whole message is 手紙.","examples":[{"japanese":"古い石碑の文字は、雨で読めなくなっていた。","romaji":"Furui sekihi no monji wa, ame de yomenaku natte ita.","german":"The characters on the old stone monument had become illegible because of the rain.","furigana":"ふる|せきひ|もんじ|あめ|よ"},{"japanese":"手紙の文字から、祖父の思いが伝わってきた。","romaji":"Tegami no monji kara, sofu no omoi ga tsutawatte kita.","german":"I could feel my grandfather's feelings from the handwriting in the letter.","furigana":"てがみ|もんじ|そふ|おも|つた"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"よ means \"night\" just like よる. This shorter reading appears e.g. in 夜が明ける, \"day breaks\", and 夜が更ける, \"the night wears on\". Here が marks the night as the subject. In 夜が明ける前に, the action takes place before dawn. Pronounce よ short; it is neither よう nor や.","examples":[{"japanese":"夜が明ける前に、港を出発しました。","romaji":"Yo ga akeru mae ni, minato o shuppatsu shimashita.","german":"We left the harbor before dawn.","furigana":"よ|あ|まえ|みなと|しゅっぱつ"},{"japanese":"話しているうちに、すっかり夜が更けた。","romaji":"Hanashite iru uchi ni, sukkari yo ga fuketa.","german":"While we were talking, it had become deep night.","furigana":"はな|よ|ふ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"うわ stands as a word component before another word: 上唇（うわくちびる）is the upper lip, 上着（うわぎ）a garment for the upper body or the outer layer of clothing. In 上着, き becomes ぎ. For the independent location word \"above/on\", 上（うえ）is normally used; うわ cannot simply be substituted there.","examples":[{"japanese":"上唇に少し傷がある。","romaji":"Uwakuchibiru ni sukoshi kizu ga aru.","german":"There is a small injury on the upper lip.","furigana":"うわくちびる|すこ|きず"},{"japanese":"風が冷たいので、上着をもう一枚着ます。","romaji":"Kaze ga tsumetai no de, uwagi o mō ichimai kimasu.","german":"The wind is cold, so I'll put on one more jacket.","furigana":"かぜ|つめ|うわぎ|いちまいき"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"音（おん）denotes here a musical tone, a speech sound, or the reading of a kanji borrowed from Chinese. In the last sense, 音 contrasts with 訓（くん）, the Japanese reading. For an everyday noise, 音（おと）is the usual reading; おん is especially useful in explanations about music and language.","examples":[{"japanese":"ピアノで同じ音を繰り返し弾きました。","romaji":"Piano de onaji on o kurikaeshi hikimashita.","german":"I played the same note on the piano over and over.","furigana":"おな|おん|く|かえ|ひ"},{"japanese":"この二つの音を聞き分けるのは難しい。","romaji":"Kono futatsu no on o kikiwakeru no wa muzukashii.","german":"It is difficult to tell these two sounds apart.","furigana":"ふた|おん|き|わ|むずか"},{"japanese":"この字の音は「サン」、訓は「やま」です。","romaji":"Kono ji no on wa “san”, kun wa “yama” desu.","german":"The on reading of this character is \"san\", the kun reading is \"yama\".","furigana":"じ|おん|くん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"ほんと is the shortened reading of ほんとう. In conversation, 本当？ alone can ask whether something is really true. 本当は introduces the actual state of affairs, often in contrast to a supposition or what has been said so far. The final う of ほんとう is dropped here; the form is therefore shorter.","examples":[{"japanese":"それ、本当？明日ここに来るの？","romaji":"Sore, honto? Ashita koko ni kuru no?","german":"Really? Are you coming here tomorrow?","furigana":"ほんと|あした|く"},{"japanese":"本当は、まだ誰にも話していない。","romaji":"Honto wa, mada dare ni mo hanashite inai.","german":"Actually, I haven't told anyone yet.","furigana":"ほんと|だれ|はな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"行き（いき）is the outbound trip or the way there. 行き and 帰り（かえり）are often contrasted: one means of transport out, another back. 行きに means \"on the way there\". The card covers the noun; the identically written form in destination expressions and the reading ゆき must be distinguished according to their respective usage.","examples":[{"japanese":"行きは電車で、帰りはバスでした。","romaji":"Iki wa densha de, kaeri wa basu deshita.","german":"I went by train on the way there and by bus on the way back.","furigana":"い|でんしゃ|かえ"},{"japanese":"行きに寄った店で、お土産を買いました。","romaji":"Iki ni yotta mise de, omiyage o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a souvenir at a shop I stopped by on the way there.","furigana":"い|よ|みせ|みやげ|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"角 is read つの here and refers to a horn or an antler. In 鹿の角, の names the animal the antler belongs to. 角で describes that a horn can cause, for example, an injury. This card is about the body part; the readings かど for corner and かく for angle belong to other entries.","examples":[{"japanese":"あの鹿は角が長いです。","romaji":"Ano shika wa tsuno ga nagai desu.","german":"That deer has long antlers.","furigana":"しか|つの|なが"},{"japanese":"角でけがをしないように、この牛には近づかないでください。","romaji":"Tsuno de kega o shinai yō ni, kono ushi ni wa chikazukanaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't go near this cow so you don't get hurt by its horns.","furigana":"つの|うし|ちか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"ちゅう is used here as a suffix. 十人中 means \"out of ten people\", 夏休み中 \"during the summer vacation\" and 工事中 \"under construction / while construction work is going on\". The suffix attaches directly to the noun. It can indicate an ongoing process; 一日中（いちにちじゅう）, on the other hand, emphasizes the whole day.","examples":[{"japanese":"十人中、三人が欠席した。","romaji":"Jūnin chū, sannin ga kesseki shita.","german":"Three out of ten people were absent.","furigana":"じゅうにんちゅう|さんにん|けっせき"},{"japanese":"夏休み中は、毎日図書館へ通った。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi chū wa, mainichi toshokan e kayotta.","german":"During the summer vacation, I went to the library every day.","furigana":"なつやす|ちゅう|まいにちとしょかん|かよ"},{"japanese":"この道は工事中なので、通れません。","romaji":"Kono michi wa kōji chū na no de, tōremasen.","german":"This road is closed because of ongoing construction.","furigana":"みち|こうじちゅう|とお"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 33"},
{"notes":"巻 (かん) denotes a volume of a multivolume book or a reel of a film. この巻 means the volume currently being looked at; 映画の最後の巻 the last film reel. After numbers, 巻 can also be used to count these units, but this card practices the noun without a preceding number. The reading まき and the verb 巻く (まく) are thereby not practiced.","examples":[{"japanese":"この巻には、主人公の子どものころが描かれています。","romaji":"Kono kan ni wa, shujinkō no kodomo no koro ga egakarete imasu.","german":"This volume depicts the protagonist's childhood.","furigana":"かん|しゅじんこう|こ|えが"},{"japanese":"古い映画の最後の巻が、倉庫で見つかりました。","romaji":"Furui eiga no saigo no kan ga, sōko de mitsukarimashita.","german":"The last reel of the old film was found in a warehouse.","furigana":"ふる|えいが|さいご|かん|そうこ|み"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"湖（こ）stands at the end of lake names. 琵琶湖 is read びわこ, 諏訪湖 すわこ. The suffix marks the lake as part of the name; the free word for lake is 湖（みずうみ）. Not every name has to be read by the same pattern, so one learns the full place name.","examples":[{"japanese":"琵琶湖の近くに泊まり、朝は湖岸を歩きました。","romaji":"Biwako no chikaku ni tomari, asa wa kogan o arukimashita.","german":"I stayed near Lake Biwa and walked along the lakeshore in the morning.","furigana":"びわこ|ちか|と|あさ|こがん|ある"},{"japanese":"図書館で、諏訪湖の古い写真を探しています。","romaji":"Toshokan de, Suwako no furui shashin o sagashite imasu.","german":"I'm looking for old photos of Lake Suwa in the library.","furigana":"としょかん||ふる|しゃしん|さが"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"日 after a number stands for a calendar day or a number of days. The basic reading にち appears e.g. in 二十一日（にじゅういちにち）. Many low numbers form special forms: 三日（みっか）, 十日（とおか）. For a duration, 間 can follow: 十日間, for ten days. 日曜日, by contrast, denotes a day of the week.","examples":[{"japanese":"二十一日に東京へ行きます。","romaji":"Nijūichi-nichi ni Tōkyō e ikimasu.","german":"I'm going to Tokyo on the 21st.","furigana":"にじゅういちにち|とうきょう|い"},{"japanese":"旅行は十日間です。","romaji":"Ryokō wa tōkakan desu.","german":"The trip lasts ten days.","furigana":"りょこう|とおかかん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"館 can refer independently to an institution already mentioned, such as a library or a museum. 館の運営 means the operation of this house; この館では introduces a statement about its rules. This usage is more common in reports and descriptions. The identically written word 館 (やかた) for a stately residence has a different reading. The ending in building names is practiced separately.","examples":[{"japanese":"図書館の職員が、館の運営について話し合いました。","romaji":"Toshokan no shokuin ga, kan no un'ei ni tsuite hanashiaimashita.","german":"Staff of the library discussed the operation of the facility.","furigana":"としょかん|しょくいん|かん|うんえい|はな|あ"},{"japanese":"この館では、展示室での撮影を禁止しています。","romaji":"Kono kan de wa, tenjishitsu de no satsuei o kinshi shite imasu.","german":"This facility prohibits photography in its exhibition rooms.","furigana":"かん|てんじしつ|さつえい|きんし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The suffix 館 belongs to names of larger buildings and institutions: 公民館 (こうみんかん) is a community center, 旅館 (りょかん) a Japanese inn. The whole expression determines the kind of house. In older descriptions 館 can also denote a lodging or a large house; it does not follow that every apartment or hotel is simply called 館 today. For an ordinary hotel, ホテル is usual.","examples":[{"japanese":"公民館では、毎週料理教室が開かれます。","romaji":"Kōminkan de wa, maishū ryōri kyōshitsu ga hirakaremasu.","german":"Cooking classes are held at the community center every week.","furigana":"こうみんかん|まいしゅうりょうりきょうしつ|ひら"},{"japanese":"旅館の部屋から川が見えました。","romaji":"Ryokan no heya kara kawa ga miemashita.","german":"I could see the river from my room at the ryokan.","furigana":"りょかん|へや|かわ|み"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"感 is a rather literary noun for a feeling or an impression. 感を抱く means to gain an impression or harbor a feeling; 奇異の感に打たれる describes feeling something strange. The content can be explained by a statement with という. In everyday conversation, 感じ is often more common. Here you practice 感 as a noun in its own right; compound feeling words such as 安心感 are further expressions.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼の説明を聞いて、準備が十分ではないという感を抱きました。","romaji":"Kare no setsumei o kiite, junbi ga jūbun de wa nai to iu kan o idakimashita.","german":"Hearing his explanation, I got the impression that the preparations were not sufficient.","furigana":"かれ|せつめい|き|じゅんび|じゅうぶん|かん|いだ"},{"japanese":"初めてその建物を見たとき、奇異の感に打たれました。","romaji":"Hajimete sono tatemono o mita toki, kii no kan ni utaremashita.","german":"When I first saw that building, I was struck by a sense of strangeness.","furigana":"はじ|たてもの|み|きい|かん|う"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"数（すう） denotes in mathematical texts a number as a concept or arithmetic value. 正の数 are positive numbers; 二つの数を掛ける means to multiply two numbers. In general everyday use, the independent word 数 is often read かず. This entry specifically practices the technical noun reading すう. The identically read prefix before quantities, e.g. 数人, means \"several\" and is practiced separately.","examples":[{"japanese":"正の数に負の数を足すと、結果はどうなりますか。","romaji":"Sei no sū ni fu no sū o tasu to, kekka wa dō narimasu ka.","german":"What happens to the result when you add a negative number to a positive number?","furigana":"せい|すう|ふ|すう|た|けっか"},{"japanese":"二つの数を掛ける前に、それぞれの符号を確認してください。","romaji":"Futatsu no sū o kakeru mae ni, sorezore no fugō o kakunin shite kudasai.","german":"Before multiplying two numbers, please check the sign of each.","furigana":"ふた|すう|か|まえ|ふごう|かくにん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"数 stands before a quantity and leaves the exact number open: 数人（すうにん） are several people, 数日（すうじつ） several days. It can also stand before larger units, e.g. 数百, several hundred. The meaning depends on the following unit; 数 alone gives no fixed numerical value. This prefix use is to be distinguished from 数（すう） as a mathematical noun and 数（かず） as a count.","examples":[{"japanese":"数人の学生が、片付けを手伝ってくれました。","romaji":"Sūnin no gakusei ga, katazuke o tetsudatte kuremashita.","german":"Several students helped me tidy up.","furigana":"すうにん|がくせい|かたづ|てつだ"},{"japanese":"修理には数日かかるそうです。","romaji":"Shūri ni wa sūjitsu kakaru sō desu.","german":"The repair is said to take a few days.","furigana":"しゅうり|すうじつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"As an independent noun, 氏（し） refers to a family group with common descent. This card practices the meaning in a historical context. The name suffix 〜氏 has a different function. For a present-day surname one usually uses 姓 or 名字; in other contexts 氏 is also read うじ.","examples":[{"japanese":"氏は、同じ血筋をもつ一族を指す。","romaji":"Shi wa, onaji chisuji o motsu ichizoku o sasu.","german":"氏 refers to a family group with common descent.","furigana":"し|おな|ちすじ|いちぞく|さ"},{"japanese":"歴史の授業で、氏の成り立ちを学んだ。","romaji":"Rekishi no jugyō de, shi no naritachi o mananda.","german":"In history class, I learned how such clans came to be.","furigana":"れきし|じゅぎょう|し|な|た|まな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"At the end of a sentence and before a noun, 言う is often pronounced like ゆう. In ordinary kana spelling, however, it is still written いう. ～と言う reports what someone says; ～という人 names a person with a particular name. This pronunciation belongs to the same verb as 言う（いう）. In the polite form the stem stays いい: 言います is iimasu. The usual forms 言わない（いわない）and 言った（いった）remain the standard for learning; no polite form ゆいます is formed from ゆう.","examples":[{"japanese":"母は「急がなくていい」と言う。","romaji":"Haha wa “isoganakute ii” to yū.","german":"My mother says, \"You don't need to hurry.\"","furigana":"はは|いそ|ゆ"},{"japanese":"田中という人から電話がありました。","romaji":"Tanaka to yū hito kara denwa ga arimashita.","german":"There was a phone call from a person called Tanaka."}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"In technical descriptions, 管（かん） refers to a hollow pipe or tube. 管を通して水を送る describes a conduit; 管に亀裂が入る describes damage to the component. The other reading くだ can also refer to a tube; かん is found especially in technical descriptions and compounds.","examples":[{"japanese":"細い管を通して、水を別の容器に送ります。","romaji":"Hosoi kan o tōshite, mizu o betsu no yōki ni okurimasu.","german":"We send water into another container through a thin pipe.","furigana":"ほそ|かん|とお|みず|べつ|ようき|おく"},{"japanese":"古い管に亀裂が入ったため、交換が必要になりました。","romaji":"Furui kan ni kiretsu ga haitta tame, kōkan ga hitsuyō ni narimashita.","german":"Because the old pipe developed a crack, it had to be replaced.","furigana":"ふる|かん|きれつ|はい|こうかん|ひつよう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"いえ is a short form of いいえ. With this reply you contradict or correct an assumption; an explanation can follow. いえ、結構です politely declines an offer. Pronounce two morae, い・え; いいえ is longer. The identically read 家 means \"house\" and is a different word. This card practices the negative reply, not older exclamations of surprise or forms of address.","examples":[{"japanese":"いえ、予約したのは明日です。","romaji":"Ie, yoyaku shita no wa ashita desu.","german":"No, I booked for tomorrow.","furigana":"よやく|あした"},{"japanese":"いえ、結構です。タクシーは必要ありません。","romaji":"Ie, kekkō desu. Takushī wa hitsuyō arimasen.","german":"No, thank you. I don't need a taxi.","furigana":"けっこう|ひつよう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"病気にかかる means to fall ill with a disease. The illness takes に, the affected person takes が or は. The spelling 罹る marks this illness sense; in everyday writing, kana かかる is usual. As an intransitive godan verb it forms かかります, かかって, かかった. The identically read uses of 掛かる for time spent or hanging belong to a different card.","examples":[{"japanese":"去年、インフルエンザに罹って一週間学校を休みました。","romaji":"Kyonen, infuruenza ni kakatte isshūkan gakkō o yasumimashita.","german":"Last year I caught the flu and stayed away from school for a week.","furigana":"きょねん|かか|いっしゅうかんがっこう|やす"},{"japanese":"旅行の前に、現地でかかりやすい病気について調べた。","romaji":"Ryokō no mae ni, genchi de kakariyasui byōki ni tsuite shirabeta.","german":"Before the trip, I looked up illnesses that are easy to catch locally.","furigana":"りょこう|まえ|げんち|びょうき|しら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"In formal language, 得る／うる can mean \"to obtain\", e.g. 承認をうる. After the stem before ます it expresses possibility: 起こりうる \"can happen\", 予想しうる \"can be foreseen\". These forms say nothing about whether something actually happens. The reading うる is used mainly at the end of a sentence or before a noun; in today's negative and past, えない and えた are used, not うない or うた. The ordinary reading える is learned separately. The accent shown here applies to 得る／うる on its own; compound words have their own accents.","examples":[{"japanese":"住民の承認をうることが、計画を進める条件である。","romaji":"Jūmin no shōnin o uru koto ga, keikaku o susumeru jōken de aru.","german":"Obtaining the residents' approval is a condition for moving the plan forward.","furigana":"じゅうみん|しょうにん|けいかく|すす|じょうけん"},{"japanese":"小さな間違いでも、大きな事故につながりうる。","romaji":"Chīsana machigai demo, ōkina jiko ni tsunagariuru.","german":"Even a small mistake can lead to a big accident.","furigana":"ちい|まちが|おお|じこ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"空／から refers to a state without contents. As a noun it can take の: 空の箱 \"an empty box\"; at the end of a sentence it is 箱は空だ. For a wallet, 空 means there is no money in it. Other uses as part of a word are learned with the respective compound. 空／そら means \"sky\" and is a different reading.","examples":[{"japanese":"届いた箱を開けたら、中は空だった。","romaji":"Todoita hako o aketara, naka wa kara datta.","german":"When I opened the box that had arrived, it was empty inside.","furigana":"とど|はこ|あ|なか|から"},{"japanese":"財布が空なので、今日は歩いて帰ります。","romaji":"Saifu ga kara na node, kyō wa aruite kaerimasu.","german":"My wallet is empty, so I'll walk home today.","furigana":"さいふ|から|きょう|ある|かえ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"かみ refers to the beginning or upper side of a connected whole: for a river, the direction toward the source; for a text, the front part. Its counterpart is しも. 上の巻 can be read かみのまき in older texts; for the first volume, じょう is usual today. Rating something as especially good quality also belongs to older uses. In historical narratives, 上 can refer to the authorities or their offices; with お this meaning appears in お上. The reading うえ is used for ordinary location expressions such as \"on the table\".","examples":[{"japanese":"船は川の上へゆっくり進んだ。","romaji":"Fune wa kawa no kami e yukkuri susunda.","german":"The boat slowly moved upstream along the river.","furigana":"ふね|かわ|かみ|すす"},{"japanese":"この物語は、上の巻から順に読んでください。","romaji":"Kono monogatari wa, kami no maki kara jun ni yonde kudasai.","german":"Please read this story in order, starting with the first volume.","furigana":"ものがたり|かみ|まき|じゅん|よ"},{"japanese":"この物語の商人は、質のよい反物を「上」と呼んでいる。","romaji":"Kono monogatari no shōnin wa, shitsu no yoi tanmono o \"kami\" to yonde iru.","german":"The merchant in this story calls fine-quality bolts of cloth \"top grade\".","furigana":"ものがたり|しょうにん|しつ|たんもの|かみ|よ"},{"japanese":"村人たちは、上から届いたお触れに従った。","romaji":"Murabitotachi wa, kami kara todoita ofure ni shitagatta.","german":"The villagers obeyed the notice handed down from the authorities.","furigana":"むらびと|かみ|とど|ふ|したが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"As a noun, じょう refers to a higher quality grade or to the first volume of a two- or three-volume edition. In a ranking it often appears together with 中 and 下, e.g. 中の上 \"upper middle\". On menus, 上 can mark a better version. When reading a book series, 上 is the counterpart of 下, or of 中 and 下. The identically written suffix in 安全上 \"for safety reasons\" has its own use.","examples":[{"japanese":"成績は中の上だった。","romaji":"Seiseki wa chū no jō datta.","german":"My grades were in the upper middle.","furigana":"せいせき|ちゅう|じょう"},{"japanese":"この小説は三冊に分かれています。まず上を買いました。","romaji":"Kono shōsetsu wa sansatsu ni wakarete imasu. Mazu jō o kaimashita.","german":"This novel is split into three volumes. I bought the first one first.","furigana":"しょうせつ|さんさつ|わ|じょう|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Here 上 is attached to a noun and names the viewpoint or area to which a statement relates. 安全上の理由 are reasons concerning safety; 法律上 means \"legally speaking\". Often の and another noun follow, or は as a topic particle. Unlike うえ \"above\", this じょう is firmly attached to the preceding word. It does not rate quality and does not denote a book volume.","examples":[{"japanese":"安全上の理由で、この扉は開けられません。","romaji":"Anzenjō no riyū de, kono tobira wa akeraremasen.","german":"For safety reasons, this door cannot be opened.","furigana":"あんぜんじょう|りゆう|とびら|あ"},{"japanese":"法律上は問題がなくても、近所の人に説明したほうがいい。","romaji":"Hōritsujō wa mondai ga nakute mo, kinjo no hito ni setsumei shita hō ga ii.","german":"Even if there is no legal problem, you should explain it to the neighbors.","furigana":"ほうりつじょう|もんだい|きんじょ|ひと|せつめい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"As a noun, げ refers to the lower level of a classification, e.g. 下 in 上・中・下. It can therefore sound derogatory when ranking people or performances. For a two- or three-volume work, 下 stands for the last volume. The reading した, by contrast, is used in ordinary spatial expressions such as \"under the table\"; for a stretch of a river, しも is found.","examples":[{"japanese":"品物の質は、上・中・下の三段階で評価された。","romaji":"Shinamono no shitsu wa, jō, chū, ge no sandankai de hyōka sareta.","german":"The quality of the goods was rated on three levels: high, medium and low.","furigana":"しなもの|しつ|じょう|ちゅう|げ|さんだんかい|ひょうか"},{"japanese":"上と中は図書館にありましたが、下は貸し出し中でした。","romaji":"Jō to chū wa toshokan ni arimashita ga, ge wa kashidashichū deshita.","german":"The first and middle volumes were at the library, but the last one was on loan.","furigana":"じょう|ちゅう|としょかん|げ|か|だ|ちゅう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"しも names the lower or final part of a connected whole. For a river, 下 is closer to the mouth; its counterpart is 上 かみ. In older or technical descriptions it can also refer to a lower-lying section. For everyday location expressions, した is usually used. The division into book volumes or quality grades is usually read げ.","examples":[{"japanese":"雨のあと、川の下では水が濁っていた。","romaji":"Ame no ato, kawa no shimo de wa mizu ga nigotte ita.","german":"After the rain, the water in the lower reaches of the river was muddy.","furigana":"あめ|かわ|しも|みず|にご"},{"japanese":"上の田で余った水を、下の田へ流した。","romaji":"Kami no ta de amatta mizu o, shimo no ta e nagashita.","german":"The surplus water from the upper rice field was channeled to the lower rice field.","furigana":"かみ|た|あま|みず|しも|た|なが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"園 (その) refers to a delimited, cultivated piece of land with flowers, fruit trees or other plants. The reading often sounds literary and appears in, for example, 梅の園 \"plum garden\". For a public park, 公園 is more common in daily life. The attested spellings 苑 and the rarer 薗 apply here to the same reading and meaning. The identically read その \"that\" is a demonstrative word. The reading えん, as in 公園, belongs to other word forms.","examples":[{"japanese":"春になると、梅の園に白い花が咲く。","romaji":"Haru ni naru to, ume no sono ni shiroi hana ga saku.","german":"In spring, white flowers bloom in the plum garden.","furigana":"はる|うめ|その|しろ|はな|さ"},{"japanese":"旅人は、園の木陰で静かに休んだ。","romaji":"Tabibito wa, sono no kokage de shizuka ni yasunda.","german":"The traveler rested quietly in the shade of the trees in the garden.","furigana":"たびびと|その|こかげ|しず|やす"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"周 (しゅう) is here the name of the ancient Chinese Zhou dynasty. It appears in history texts and in the terms 西周 \"Western Zhou\" and 東周 \"Eastern Zhou\". \"Zhou\" and the older romanization \"Chou\" render the Chinese name; when reading a Japanese text, the reading is しゅう. The identically written word for laps or the circumference of a figure has a different meaning. 週 \"week\" and the family name 周 are likewise not studied here.","examples":[{"japanese":"歴史の授業で、周の政治制度を学んだ。","romaji":"Rekishi no jugyō de, Shū no seiji seido o mananda.","german":"In history class, I learned about the political system of the Zhou.","furigana":"れきし|じゅぎょう|しゅう|せいじせいど|まな"},{"japanese":"博物館には、周の時代に作られた青銅器が展示されている。","romaji":"Hakubutsukan ni wa, Shū no jidai ni tsukurareta seidōki ga tenji sarete iru.","german":"Bronzeware made in the Zhou period is on display at the museum.","furigana":"はくぶつかん|しゅう|じだい|つく|せいどうき|てんじ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Here と follows the plain form of a verb or i-adjective and links a condition or event to a following result. It is especially suited to regular or automatic results, e.g. ボタンを押すと \"when you press the button\". It can also introduce the event that immediately follows in a narrative. Requests and freely chosen intentions as the result usually fit たら or なら better. The functions \"and\", \"with\" and quoting have their own uses; the identically read shogi piece is a noun.","examples":[{"japanese":"このボタンを押すと、ドアが開きます。","romaji":"Kono botan o osu to, doa ga akimasu.","german":"When you press this button, the door opens.","furigana":"お|あ"},{"japanese":"駅を出ると、友達が手を振っていた。","romaji":"Eki o deru to, tomodachi ga te o futte ita.","german":"When I came out of the station, a friend was waving.","furigana":"えき|で|ともだち|て|ふ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"In the iroha order イ・ロ・ハ・ニ・ホ・ヘ・ト, ト comes seventh and can thus mark the seventh position of an ordered list. As a Japanese note name, ト denotes the note G, e.g. in ト長調 \"G major\". These are different systems of reference: counting from ハ（C）, G is the fifth note; \"seventh iroha position\" does not mean \"seventh degree of the C major scale\". The particle と and the shogi piece と have other functions. Katakana is usually used for the note name.","examples":[{"japanese":"申込書では、イからトまでの七項目に答えてください。","romaji":"Mōshikomisho de wa, i kara to made no nana kōmoku ni kotaete kudasai.","german":"In the application form, please answer the seven items from イ to ト.","furigana":"もうしこみしょ|ななこうもく|こた"},{"japanese":"一覧表のトの欄には、七番目の候補が載っている。","romaji":"Ichiranhyō no to no ran ni wa, nanabanme no kōho ga notte iru.","german":"The column ト of the table lists the seventh candidate.","furigana":"いちらんひょう|らん|ななばんめ|こうほ|の"},{"japanese":"先生は、トの音をピアノで弾いて聞かせた。","romaji":"Sensei wa, to no oto o piano de hiite kikaseta.","german":"The teacher played the note G on the piano for us to hear.","furigana":"せんせい|おと|ひ|き"},{"japanese":"この曲はト長調で書かれている。","romaji":"Kono kyoku wa tochōchō de kakarete iru.","german":"This piece is written in G major.","furigana":"きょく|ちょうちょう|か"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"As an independent noun, 対（たい）here means a counterpart or an opposite, especially in a conceptual contrast. The word sounds objective or formal. Its use between sports teams or numbers belongs to other meanings; an ordinary pair of objects is usually called 対（つい）.","examples":[{"japanese":"「動」の対は「静」だ。","romaji":"“Dō” no tai wa “sei” da.","german":"The counterpart of 「動」 is 「静」.","furigana":"どう|たい|せい"},{"japanese":"この絵では、光と闇を対として描いている。","romaji":"Kono e de wa, hikari to yami o tai to shite egaite iru.","german":"This painting depicts light and darkness as opposites.","furigana":"え|ひかり|やみ|たい|えが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"In older or literary language, the reading たい can refer to a matching pair or its fitting counterpart, e.g. two verses that relate to each other. This rare meaning is explicitly attested as a noun. In today's everyday language, 対 for an ordinary pair is usually read つい; 一対 is also read いっつい. The examples deliberately use the literary sense with the reading たい.","examples":[{"japanese":"この二つの句は、意味の上で対をなしている。","romaji":"Kono futatsu no ku wa, imi no ue de tai o nashite iru.","german":"These two phrases form a matching pair in terms of meaning.","furigana":"ふた|く|いみ|うえ|たい"},{"japanese":"詩人は、先の句にふさわしい対を考えた。","romaji":"Shijin wa, saki no ku ni fusawashii tai o kangaeta.","german":"The poet thought of a fitting counterpart to the first line.","furigana":"しじん|さき|く|たい|かんが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"愛 is deep, often selfless love (母の愛, 家族への愛) and sounds rather solemn or literary in everyday life; in conversation one usually says 好き. 恋, by contrast, is romantic love. The verb \"to love\" is 愛する (its own card); あい written with the kanji 藍 means \"indigo\".","examples":[{"japanese":"母の愛はとても深い。","romaji":"Haha no ai wa totemo fukai.","german":"A mother's love is very deep.","furigana":"はは|あい|ふか"},{"japanese":"この歌は家族への愛について歌っています。","romaji":"Kono uta wa kazoku e no ai ni tsuite utatte imasu.","german":"This song is about love for one's family.","furigana":"うた|かぞく|あい|うた"},{"japanese":"子どもたちのために、愛を込めて料理を作りました。","romaji":"Kodomotachi no tame ni, ai o komete ryōri o tsukurimashita.","german":"I cooked for the children with a lot of love.","furigana":"こ|あい|こ|りょうり|つく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Short form of 火曜日 \"Tuesday\", very common in timetables, schedules, opening hours and casual conversation (火曜の午後, 毎週火曜). Meaning and use are the same, just without 日; in tables it is often shortened further to 火. Do not confuse with the homophones 通う \"to go regularly\" and 歌謡 \"song\".","examples":[{"japanese":"火曜の午後は会議があります。","romaji":"Kayou no gogo wa kaigi ga arimasu.","german":"I have a meeting on Tuesday afternoon.","furigana":"かよう|ごご|かいぎ"},{"japanese":"燃えるゴミは毎週火曜と金曜です。","romaji":"Moeru gomi wa maishuu kayou to kin'you desu.","german":"Burnable trash is collected every Tuesday and Friday.","furigana":"も|まいしゅうかよう|きんよう"},{"japanese":"来週は火曜から木曜まで出張です。","romaji":"Raishuu wa kayou kara mokuyou made shucchou desu.","german":"Next week I'll be away on a business trip from Tuesday to Thursday.","furigana":"らいしゅう|かよう|もくよう|しゅっちょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Same spelling as 工場（こうじょう） \"factory\", but with the mixed reading こうば (Sino-Japanese こう + native ば). こうば sounds more colloquial and refers more to a small workshop or craft business, especially in 町工場（まちこうば） \"small family-run factory\". For large, modern industrial plants (自動車工場) one says こうじょう; in written text without context, こうじょう is usually meant.","examples":[{"japanese":"父は町の小さな工場で働いています。","romaji":"Chichi wa machi no chiisana kouba de hataraite imasu.","german":"My father works in a small workshop in town.","furigana":"ちち|まち|ちい|こうば|はたら"},{"japanese":"この町工場では、飛行機の部品を作っている。","romaji":"Kono machikouba de wa, hikouki no buhin o tsukutte iru.","german":"This small neighborhood factory makes aircraft parts.","furigana":"まちこうば|ひこうき|ぶひん|つく"},{"japanese":"家の裏に古い工場があって、毎日機械の音が聞こえる。","romaji":"Ie no ura ni furui kouba ga atte, mainichi kikai no oto ga kikoeru.","german":"There is an old workshop behind the house, and I hear the machines every day.","furigana":"いえ|うら|ふる|こうば|まいにちきかい|おと|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"こきょう is the Sino-Japanese reading of 故郷 and sounds more matter-of-fact than ふるさと (its own card), which sounds emotional and poetic. Typical are 故郷に帰る \"to return to one's hometown\", 故郷を離れる \"to leave one's hometown\" and 第二の故郷 \"second home\". In songs and poems, 故郷 is usually read ふるさと; in reports, news and forms, rather こきょう.","examples":[{"japanese":"祖父は六十年ぶりに故郷に帰りました。","romaji":"Sofu wa rokujuunen buri ni kokyou ni kaerimashita.","german":"My grandfather returned to his hometown for the first time in sixty years.","furigana":"そふ|ろくじゅうねん|こきょう|かえ"},{"japanese":"仕事のために故郷を離れて東京に来ました。","romaji":"Shigoto no tame ni kokyou o hanarete Toukyou ni kimashita.","german":"I left my hometown for work and came to Tokyo.","furigana":"しごと|こきょう|はな|とうきょう|き"},{"japanese":"日本は私の第二の故郷です。","romaji":"Nihon wa watashi no daini no kokyou desu.","german":"Japan is my second home.","furigana":"にほん|わたし|だいに|こきょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"A negating prefix in the reading ぶ, only in a few fixed words: 不器用 \"clumsy\", 不気味 \"eerie\", 不用心 \"careless, insecure\", 不細工 \"clumsy, ugly\". Most words with 不, by contrast, are read ふ (不便, 不安, 不足); which reading applies must be learned word by word. The same syllable ぶ is also written 無 (無事, 無礼); see also 非〜（ひ） \"non-\".","examples":[{"japanese":"弟は不器用で、ボタンを付けるのも苦手だ。","romaji":"Otouto wa bukiyou de, botan o tsukeru no mo nigate da.","german":"My little brother is clumsy and has trouble even sewing on buttons.","furigana":"おとうと|ぶきよう|つ|にがて"},{"japanese":"夜の古い病院は、なんだか不気味だ。","romaji":"Yoru no furui byouin wa, nandaka bukimi da.","german":"An old hospital at night is somehow eerie.","furigana":"よる|ふる|びょういん|ぶきみ"},{"japanese":"鍵をかけないで出かけるのは不用心ですよ。","romaji":"Kagi o kakenaide dekakeru no wa buyoujin desu yo.","german":"Going out without locking up is careless.","furigana":"かぎ|で|ぶようじん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"A rarer reading of the negating prefix 無, only in fixed words such as 無事 \"safe and sound\", 無礼 \"rude\", 無愛想 \"unfriendly\", 無精 \"lazy\", 無難 \"safe, unobjectionable\" and ご無沙汰 \"long silence (not keeping in touch)\". The normal, productive reading is む (無理, 無料, 無意味); which applies must be learned word by word. Comparable to the prefixes 非〜 and 不〜, where 不 is also read ぶ in some words (不気味, 不用心).","examples":[{"japanese":"台風でしたが、みんな無事に家に着きました。","romaji":"Taifuu deshita ga, minna buji ni ie ni tsukimashita.","german":"Despite the typhoon, everyone arrived home safely.","furigana":"たいふう|ぶじ|いえ|つ"},{"japanese":"長い間ご無沙汰しておりますが、お元気ですか。","romaji":"Nagai aida go-busata shite orimasu ga, ogenki desu ka.","german":"It has been a long time since I last contacted you; how are you?","furigana":"なが|あいだ|ぶさた|げんき"},{"japanese":"あの店の店員はちょっと無愛想だ。","romaji":"Ano mise no ten'in wa chotto buaisou da.","german":"The clerks in that shop are a bit unfriendly.","furigana":"みせ|てんいん|ぶあいそう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"〜分（ぶ）denotes one tenth of the next higher unit: after 割 (10%) it is one percent, e.g. 打率三割二分 \"batting average .320\"; after 度 it is a tenth of a degree for a fever (三十七度五分). Fixed expressions are 五分五分 \"fifty-fifty\" and 九分九厘 \"almost certain\". Do not confuse with 〜分/ふん \"minute\" and 分/ぶん \"share\" (their own cards).","examples":[{"japanese":"熱を測ったら、三十七度五分ありました。","romaji":"Netsu o hakattara, sanjuunanado gobu arimashita.","german":"When I took my temperature, it was 37.5 degrees.","furigana":"ねつ|はか|さんじゅうななどごぶ"},{"japanese":"あの選手の打率は三割二分です。","romaji":"Ano senshu no daritsu wa sanwari nibu desu.","german":"That player's batting average is .320.","furigana":"せんしゅ|だりつ|さんわりにぶ"},{"japanese":"勝つか負けるかは五分五分だと思います。","romaji":"Katsu ka makeru ka wa gobugobu da to omoimasu.","german":"I think the chances of winning or losing are fifty-fifty.","furigana":"か|ま|ごぶごぶ|おも"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Colloquial \"to hit (someone)\", typical in the speech of children and parents (ぶたないで!). Mostly written in kana, because 打つ is normally read うつ (its own card, much broader in meaning: to hit, type, inject, etc.). The more neutral word for \"to hit, to knock\" is たたく. Ironically, 演説をぶつ means \"to hold forth with a big speech\".","examples":[{"japanese":"弟が急に私の頭をぶった。","romaji":"Otōto ga kyū ni watashi no atama o butta.","german":"My little brother suddenly hit me on the head.","furigana":"おとうと|きゅう|わたし|あたま"},{"japanese":"どんなに怒っても、子どもをぶってはいけません。","romaji":"Donna ni okotte mo, kodomo o butte wa ikemasen.","german":"No matter how angry you are, you must not hit a child.","furigana":"おこ|こ"},{"japanese":"部長は会議で一時間も演説をぶった。","romaji":"Buchō wa kaigi de ichijikan mo enzetsu o butta.","german":"The department head gave a speech for a whole hour at the meeting.","furigana":"ぶちょう|かいぎ|いちじかん|えんぜつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"礼 without お appears mainly in fixed expressions: 礼を言う \"to thank\", 礼をする \"to bow\" and the school command 起立、礼！. For \"thanks\" in everyday speech one usually says the polite お礼 (its own card, おれい). Related are 礼儀 \"courtesy, manners\" and 失礼 \"rude; excuse me\". Do not confuse with the similar-sounding 例 \"example\".","examples":[{"japanese":"先生に礼を言ってから、教室を出ました。","romaji":"Sensei ni rei o itte kara, kyoushitsu o demashita.","german":"I thanked the teacher and then left the classroom.","furigana":"せんせい|れい|い|きょうしつ|で"},{"japanese":"授業の始めに、みんなで先生に礼をします。","romaji":"Jugyou no hajime ni, minna de sensei ni rei o shimasu.","german":"At the start of class, everyone bows to the teacher.","furigana":"じゅぎょう|はじ|せんせい|れい"},{"japanese":"彼は本当に礼を知らない人ですね。","romaji":"Kare wa hontou ni rei o shiranai hito desu ne.","german":"He really has no manners, does he?","furigana":"かれ|ほんとう|れい|し|ひと"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Same spelling as 紅葉 (こうよう): こうよう is the Sino-Japanese word for the changing of leaf color as a natural event (紅葉が見頃 \"the autumn leaves are at their best\", in news and weather reports), while もみじ is the native word for the maple and its red leaves. The reading もみじ is fixed in 紅葉狩り \"trip to see the autumn leaves\" and もみじのような手 \"tiny child's hands\"; the word is often written in kana to avoid confusion.","examples":[{"japanese":"秋になると、庭のもみじが真っ赤になります。","romaji":"Aki ni naru to, niwa no momiji ga makka ni narimasu.","german":"In autumn, the maple in the garden turns bright red.","furigana":"あき|にわ|ま|か"},{"japanese":"週末に家族で京都へ紅葉狩りに行きました。","romaji":"Shuumatsu ni kazoku de Kyouto e momijigari ni ikimashita.","german":"On the weekend we went to Kyoto with the family to see the autumn leaves.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|かぞく|きょうと|もみじが|い"},{"japanese":"赤ちゃんはもみじのような小さな手をしている。","romaji":"Akachan wa momiji no you na chiisana te o shite iru.","german":"The baby has tiny hands like maple leaves.","furigana":"あか|ちい|て"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 12"},
{"notes":"くぐる means \"to pass under something\" or \"to go through an opening\"; what is passed through takes を (門をくぐる, 鳥居をくぐる). Figuratively it means \"to get around, slip through\" (法の網をくぐる, 警察の目をくぐる). It is almost always written in kana, because 潜る is normally read もぐる \"to dive, go underground\" (separate card).","examples":[{"japanese":"神社の鳥居をくぐって、中に入りました。","romaji":"Jinja no torii o kugutte, naka ni hairimashita.","german":"I passed through the shrine's torii gate and went inside.","furigana":"じんじゃ|とりい|なか|はい"},{"japanese":"この道は線路の下をくぐって、駅の反対側に出ます。","romaji":"Kono michi wa senro no shita o kugutte, eki no hantaigawa ni demasu.","german":"This road goes under the railway tracks and comes out on the opposite side of the station.","furigana":"みち|せんろ|した|えき|はんたいがわ|で"},{"japanese":"犯人は警察の目をくぐって逃げた。","romaji":"Hannin wa keisatsu no me o kugutte nigeta.","german":"The culprit slipped past the police and escaped.","furigana":"はんにん|けいさつ|め|に"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"コツ is the practical knack that makes something easier to accomplish: 料理のコツ, 〜するコツ. Typical is コツをつかむ \"to get the hang of it\". In this meaning it is almost always written in katakana; the kanji 骨 is normally read ほね \"bone\" (separate card).","examples":[{"japanese":"おいしいご飯を炊くコツを母に教えてもらいました。","romaji":"Oishii gohan o taku kotsu o haha ni oshiete moraimashita.","german":"My mother taught me the trick to cooking delicious rice.","furigana":"はん|た|はは|おし"},{"japanese":"自転車に乗るコツがやっとつかめた。","romaji":"Jitensha ni noru kotsu ga yatto tsukameta.","german":"I finally got the knack of riding a bicycle.","furigana":"じてんしゃ|の"},{"japanese":"日本語の発音には、ちょっとしたコツがあります。","romaji":"Nihongo no hatsuon ni wa, chotto shita kotsu ga arimasu.","german":"There is a little knack to Japanese pronunciation.","furigana":"にほんご|はつおん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"例え is a figurative comparison or an illustrative example; it belongs to the verb 例える \"to compare\", typical are 例え話 \"parable\" and 例えを使う. 例 れい, by contrast, is a concrete example, and 例えば means \"for example\". Do not confuse with the adverb たとえ \"even if\" (たとえ〜ても), which is usually written in kana and has its own card.","examples":[{"japanese":"先生はわかりやすい例えを使って説明してくれました。","romaji":"Sensei wa wakariyasui tatoe o tsukatte setsumei shite kuremashita.","german":"The teacher explained it using an easy-to-understand analogy.","furigana":"せんせい|たと|つか|せつめい"},{"japanese":"彼の話は例えが多くて、とても面白い。","romaji":"Kare no hanashi wa tatoe ga ōkute, totemo omoshiroi.","german":"His talks contain many analogies and are very entertaining.","furigana":"かれ|はなし|たと|おお|おもしろ"},{"japanese":"それは本当の話じゃなくて、ただの例え話だよ。","romaji":"Sore wa hontō no hanashi ja nakute, tada no tatoebanashi da yo.","german":"That's not a true story, just a parable.","furigana":"ほんとう|はなし|たと|ばなし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"A colloquial noun from the verb 流行る (はやる) \"to be in fashion\"; typically attributive with の: 流行りの服, 流行りの店, 今の流行り. In meaning it corresponds to 流行 (りゅうこう), which is more formal and literary (流行語, インフルエンザの流行). The spelling 流行 without り can also be read はやり, but is ambiguous; therefore people mostly write 流行り or はやり.","examples":[{"japanese":"今年の流行りの色は緑だそうです。","romaji":"Kotoshi no hayari no iro wa midori da sou desu.","german":"I hear this year's trendy color is green.","furigana":"ことし|はや|いろ|みどり"},{"japanese":"駅前の流行りのカフェは、いつも行列ができている。","romaji":"Ekimae no hayari no kafe wa, itsumo gyouretsu ga dekite iru.","german":"The popular cafe in front of the station always has a line.","furigana":"えきまえ|はや|ぎょうれつ"},{"japanese":"流行りの服を買っても、すぐ着なくなってしまう。","romaji":"Hayari no fuku o katte mo, sugu kinaku natte shimau.","german":"Even if I buy trendy clothes, I soon stop wearing them.","furigana":"はや|ふく|か|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Refers to publications that come out once a week; mostly in compounds or attributively with の: 週刊誌 \"weekly magazine\", 週刊の新聞. Formed in parallel are 日刊（にっかん） \"published daily\" and 月刊（げっかん） \"published monthly\". Do not confuse with the homophones 週間 \"week (as a period of time)\" and 習慣 \"habit\" - the kanji 刊 means \"to publish\".","examples":[{"japanese":"父は毎週、電車の中で週刊誌を読んでいます。","romaji":"Chichi wa maishuu, densha no naka de shuukanshi o yonde imasu.","german":"Every week my father reads a weekly magazine on the train.","furigana":"ちち|まいしゅう|でんしゃ|なか|しゅうかんし|よ"},{"japanese":"この漫画は週刊の雑誌に連載されている。","romaji":"Kono manga wa shuukan no zasshi ni rensai sarete iru.","german":"This manga is serialized in a weekly magazine.","furigana":"まんが|しゅうかん|ざっし|れんさい"},{"japanese":"その新聞は週刊で、毎週金曜日に出る。","romaji":"Sono shinbun wa shuukan de, maishuu kin'youbi ni deru.","german":"That newspaper is a weekly and comes out every Friday.","furigana":"しんぶん|しゅうかん|まいしゅうきんようび|で"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"A suffix for a (usually undesirable) tendency: attached to a verb stem (忘れがち, 遅れがち, なりがち) or to a noun (病気がち, 曇りがち). It behaves like a na-adjective: 忘れがちだ, 忘れがちな人. 〜やすい expresses more the easy possibility, 〜ぎみ a slight touch of something. Mostly written in kana; 勝ち/かち \"victory\" is a different word with its own card.","examples":[{"japanese":"冬は風邪をひきがちです。","romaji":"Fuyu wa kaze o hikigachi desu.","german":"In winter I tend to catch colds.","furigana":"ふゆ|かぜ"},{"japanese":"忙しいと、運動不足になりがちだ。","romaji":"Isogashii to, undō busoku ni narigachi da.","german":"When busy, you tend to get too little exercise.","furigana":"いそが|うんどうぶそく"},{"japanese":"祖母は病気がちで、あまり外に出られない。","romaji":"Sobo wa byōkigachi de, amari soto ni derarenai.","german":"My grandmother is often ill and can't go out much.","furigana":"そぼ|びょうき|そと|で"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The intransitive counterpart of ほどく \"to untie\" (separate card): 靴ひもがほどける \"the shoelace comes undone\", but 靴ひもをほどく \"to undo the shoelace\". Figuratively tension also loosens: 緊張がほどける. Mostly written in kana, because 解ける would otherwise be read とける \"to be solved\" (separate card).","examples":[{"japanese":"あ、靴ひもがほどけていますよ。","romaji":"A, kutsuhimo ga hodokete imasu yo.","german":"Oh, your shoelace is untied.","furigana":"くつ"},{"japanese":"プレゼントのリボンがすぐにほどけてしまった。","romaji":"Purezento no ribon ga sugu ni hodokete shimatta.","german":"The ribbon on the present came undone right away."},{"japanese":"先生の笑顔を見て、緊張がほどけた。","romaji":"Sensei no egao o mite, kinchō ga hodoketa.","german":"Seeing the teacher's smile, my nervousness melted away.","furigana":"せんせい|えがお|み|きんちょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Usually written in kana (あら, for fish often アラ); 粗 is the kanji spelling. Typical are 粗を探す and 粗探し \"to nitpick, to look for faults\" and 魚のアラ \"head and bones after filleting\", which are used for soup or braised dishes. Do not confuse with the interjection あら \"oh!\" (its own card, a different word) and the adjective 粗い \"coarse\".","examples":[{"japanese":"彼はいつも人の粗探しばかりしている。","romaji":"Kare wa itsumo hito no arasagashi bakari shite iru.","german":"He is always just nitpicking at other people's faults.","furigana":"かれ|ひと|あらさが"},{"japanese":"鯛のアラで味噌汁を作った。","romaji":"Tai no ara de misoshiru o tsukutta.","german":"I made miso soup from the sea bream scraps.","furigana":"たい|みそしる|つく"},{"japanese":"この計画は粗が多くて、すぐには実行できない。","romaji":"Kono keikaku wa ara ga ōkute, sugu ni wa jikkō dekinai.","german":"This plan has too many weak points to be carried out right away.","furigana":"けいかく|あら|おお|じっこう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Same spelling as 抱く/だく \"to embrace\" (its own card), but いだく is formal and is used mainly for feelings and thoughts: 夢を抱く \"to harbor a dream\", 疑問を抱く \"to harbor doubts\", 不安を抱く \"to feel anxious\". Physically embracing a person is だく in everyday speech; いだく sounds literary in this sense. Since the kanji form can have both readings, the collocation decides.","examples":[{"japanese":"子どものころからパイロットになる夢を抱いていた。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro kara pairotto ni naru yume o idaite ita.","german":"Since childhood I had harbored the dream of becoming a pilot.","furigana":"こ|ゆめ|いだ"},{"japanese":"多くの市民がその計画に疑問を抱いている。","romaji":"Ōku no shimin ga sono keikaku ni gimon o idaite iru.","german":"Many citizens harbor doubts about this plan.","furigana":"おお|しみん|けいかく|ぎもん|いだ"},{"japanese":"母親は生まれたばかりの子を胸に抱いた。","romaji":"Hahaoya wa umareta bakari no ko o mune ni idaita.","german":"The mother held the newborn to her chest.","furigana":"ははおや|う|こ|むね|いだ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"いちば is the concrete market with stalls where goods are sold: 魚市場 (うおいちば) \"fish market\", 青果市場 \"fruit and vegetable market\". The identically written 市場/しじょう (its own card), by contrast, refers to the abstract economic market: 株式市場, 市場経済, 市場調査. Rule of thumb: if you can go there and shop, いちば is usually meant.","examples":[{"japanese":"毎朝、港の近くの魚市場に新鮮な魚が並ぶ。","romaji":"Maiasa, minato no chikaku no uoichiba ni shinsen na sakana ga narabu.","german":"Every morning, fresh fish is laid out at the fish market near the harbor.","furigana":"まいあさ|みなと|ちか|うおいちば|しんせん|さかな|なら"},{"japanese":"週末は市場で野菜と果物を買う。","romaji":"Shūmatsu wa ichiba de yasai to kudamono o kau.","german":"On weekends I buy vegetables and fruit at the market.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|いちば|やさい|くだもの|か"},{"japanese":"旅行先では、地元の市場を歩くのが好きだ。","romaji":"Ryokōsaki de wa, jimoto no ichiba o aruku no ga suki da.","german":"When traveling, I like to walk around the local markets.","furigana":"りょこうさき|じもと|いちば|ある|す"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"A casual \"no\" in conversation; the more polite form is いいえ (its own card). In the middle of a sentence, いや corrects one's own statement: 百人、いや二百人 \"a hundred, no, rather two hundred\". Almost always written in kana; 否 is a rare spelling. A word that sounds the same but is a different word is 嫌/いや \"unpleasant, to dislike\".","examples":[{"japanese":"「疲れた？」「いや、まだ大丈夫。」","romaji":"'Tsukareta?' 'Iya, mada daijōbu.'","german":"\"Are you tired?\" \"No, I'm still fine.\"","furigana":"つか|だいじょうぶ"},{"japanese":"会場には百人、いや二百人はいたと思う。","romaji":"Kaijō ni wa hyakunin, iya nihyakunin wa ita to omou.","german":"I think there were a hundred people in the hall, no, two hundred.","furigana":"かいじょう|ひゃくにん|にひゃくにん|おも"},{"japanese":"いや、それは違うと思いますよ。","romaji":"Iya, sore wa chigau to omoimasu yo.","german":"No, I don't think that's right.","furigana":"ちが|おも"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 23"},
{"notes":"The plain form of お祝い (its own card), which is more polite and more common in conversation. Without お, 祝い appears mainly in compounds such as 誕生祝い, 入学祝い, 結婚祝い \"gift for ...\" and in fixed expressions such as 祝いの席 \"celebration, festive occasion\". Related verb: 祝う \"to celebrate, to congratulate\".","examples":[{"japanese":"祖父から入学祝いに腕時計をもらった。","romaji":"Sofu kara nyūgaku iwai ni udedokei o moratta.","german":"I received a wristwatch from my grandfather as a gift for starting school.","furigana":"そふ|にゅうがくいわ|うでどけい"},{"japanese":"優勝の祝いに、チームで焼き肉を食べに行った。","romaji":"Yūshō no iwai ni, chīmu de yakiniku o tabe ni itta.","german":"To celebrate the victory, the team went out for yakiniku together.","furigana":"ゆうしょう|いわ|や|にく|た|い"},{"japanese":"祝いの席で乾杯の挨拶をした。","romaji":"Iwai no seki de kanpai no aisatsu o shita.","german":"At the celebration, I gave the toast.","furigana":"いわ|せき|かんぱい|あいさつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Noun for 横断する \"to cross\" (its own card). Very common in fixed compounds: 横断歩道 \"crosswalk\", 横断禁止 \"no crossing\", 大陸横断 \"crossing of a continent\". The counterpart is 縦断 \"traversing lengthwise\" (日本列島を縦断する).","examples":[{"japanese":"横断歩道を渡るときは、左右をよく確認しよう。","romaji":"Ōdan hodō o wataru toki wa, sayū o yoku kakunin shiyō.","german":"When you cross at the crosswalk, check carefully left and right.","furigana":"おうだんほどう|わた|さゆう|かくにん"},{"japanese":"彼はヨットで太平洋横断に挑戦した。","romaji":"Kare wa yotto de Taiheiyō ōdan ni chōsen shita.","german":"He attempted to cross the Pacific in a sailing yacht.","furigana":"かれ|たいへいようおうだん|ちょうせん"},{"japanese":"この道路は横断禁止なので、歩道橋を使ってください。","romaji":"Kono dōro wa ōdan kinshi na node, hodōkyō o tsukatte kudasai.","german":"Crossing this road is prohibited, so please use the pedestrian bridge.","furigana":"どうろ|おうだんきんし|ほどうきょう|つか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Means \"allowed, permissible\" and appears mainly in brief form on signs, in ads and on forms: ペット可, 喫煙可, 未経験者可. The opposite is 不可 \"not allowed\"; 可もなく不可もなく means \"neither good nor bad, mediocre\". In university grades, 可 is the lowest passing level (優・良・可). Do not confuse with the question particle か.","examples":[{"japanese":"このアパートはペット可なので、犬を飼える。","romaji":"Kono apāto wa petto ka na node, inu o kaeru.","german":"Pets are allowed in this apartment, so I can keep a dog.","furigana":"か|いぬ|か"},{"japanese":"求人広告に「未経験者可」と書いてあった。","romaji":"Kyūjin kōkoku ni 'mikeikensha ka' to kaite atta.","german":"The job ad said \"inexperienced applicants welcome\".","furigana":"きゅうじんこうこく|みけいけんしゃか|か"},{"japanese":"前期の数学の成績は可だった。","romaji":"Zenki no sūgaku no seiseki wa ka datta.","german":"In math, I only got a \"pass\" in the first semester.","furigana":"ぜんき|すうがく|せいせき|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Rare on its own; mainly a component of loanwords and compounds: レンタカー \"rental car\", マイカー \"one's own car\", パトカー \"patrol car\", カーナビ \"car navigation\", カーシェア \"car sharing\". The normal everyday word for \"car\" is 車 (くるま), more formally 自動車.","examples":[{"japanese":"空港でレンタカーを借りて、北海道を一周した。","romaji":"Kūkō de rentakā o karite, Hokkaidō o isshū shita.","german":"I rented a car at the airport and drove all the way around Hokkaido.","furigana":"くうこう|か|ほっかいどう|いっしゅう"},{"japanese":"都会ではマイカーを持たない人も多い。","romaji":"Tokai de wa maikā o motanai hito mo ōi.","german":"In big cities, many people don't own a car.","furigana":"とかい|も|ひと|おお"},{"japanese":"サイレンを鳴らしたパトカーが通り過ぎた。","romaji":"Sairen o narashita patokā ga tōrisugita.","german":"A police car with its siren on drove past.","furigana":"な|とお|す"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"With the reading がく, 額 means on the one hand \"picture frame, framed picture\" (額に入れる \"to frame\", 額縁), and on the other \"amount, sum\" (かなりの額, 多額, 金額, 総額). The same kanji read ひたい means \"forehead\" (its own card); the context shows which reading is meant.","examples":[{"japanese":"卒業証書を額に入れて部屋に飾った。","romaji":"Sotsugyō shōsho o gaku ni irete heya ni kazatta.","german":"I framed my graduation certificate and hung it in my room.","furigana":"そつぎょうしょうしょ|がく|い|へや|かざ"},{"japanese":"修理の費用は、思ったよりかなりの額になった。","romaji":"Shūri no hiyō wa, omotta yori kanari no gaku ni natta.","german":"The repair cost turned out to be a considerably larger sum than I expected.","furigana":"しゅうり|ひよう|おも|がく"},{"japanese":"寄付の額は人によって違う。","romaji":"Kifu no gaku wa hito ni yotte chigau.","german":"The amount of the donation differs from person to person.","furigana":"きふ|がく|ひと|ちが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Means the same as お金 (its own card), but sounds more direct without お: rather casual or rough in conversation (金がない \"to have no money\"), matter-of-fact in writing. Common in compounds and sayings such as 金持ち \"rich person\" and 時は金なり \"time is money\". The same kanji read きん means \"gold\"; 鐘/かね \"bell\" is a different word.","examples":[{"japanese":"今月は金がないから、外食はやめておく。","romaji":"Kongetsu wa kane ga nai kara, gaishoku wa yamete oku.","german":"I have no money this month, so I'll skip eating out.","furigana":"こんげつ|かね|がいしょく"},{"japanese":"時は金なりと言うから、時間を無駄にしないようにしよう。","romaji":"Toki wa kane nari to iu kara, jikan o muda ni shinai yō ni shiyō.","german":"They say time is money, so let's not waste time.","furigana":"とき|かね|い|じかん|むだ"},{"japanese":"彼は金のために働いているわけではない。","romaji":"Kare wa kane no tame ni hataraite iru wake de wa nai.","german":"He isn't working for the money.","furigana":"かれ|かね|はたら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"旧〜 is a Sino-Japanese prefix for \"former, old, previous\" and stands directly before nouns: 旧校舎 \"old school building\", 旧ソ連 \"former Soviet Union\". Common compounds such as 旧姓, 旧暦 and 旧市街 have their own cards. The counterpart is 新〜 \"new\" (新校舎); 古い, by contrast, is the general adjective \"old\" and not a prefix.","examples":[{"japanese":"この旧校舎は来年取り壊されるそうです。","romaji":"Kono kyūkōsha wa rainen torikowasareru sō desu.","german":"I hear this old school building will be demolished next year.","furigana":"きゅうこうしゃ|らいねんと|こわ"},{"japanese":"結婚した後も、仕事では旧姓を使っています。","romaji":"Kekkon shita ato mo, shigoto de wa kyūsei o tsukatte imasu.","german":"Even after getting married, I use my maiden name at work.","furigana":"けっこん|あと|しごと|きゅうせい|つか"},{"japanese":"旧ソ連の国々を旅行したことがある。","romaji":"Kyū-Soren no kuniguni o ryokō shita koto ga aru.","german":"I have traveled through the countries of the former Soviet Union.","furigana":"きゅう|れん|くにぐに|りょこう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"記念 means \"commemoration, remembrance of an event\" and is found in many compounds such as 記念写真 \"commemorative photo\"; 記念日 and 記念品 have their own cards. Typical patterns are 〜の記念に \"in memory of ...\" and 〜を記念して \"on the occasion of, in honor of ...\". Do not confuse with the homophone 祈念 \"prayer\" (its own card).","examples":[{"japanese":"入学式の後で、校門の前で記念写真を撮った。","romaji":"Nyūgakushiki no ato de, kōmon no mae de kinen shashin o totta.","german":"After the entrance ceremony, we took a commemorative photo in front of the school gate.","furigana":"にゅうがくしき|あと|こうもん|まえ|きねんしゃしん|と"},{"japanese":"結婚十周年を記念して、家族で旅行に行った。","romaji":"Kekkon jusshūnen o kinen shite, kazoku de ryokō ni itta.","german":"To mark our tenth wedding anniversary, we went on a family trip.","furigana":"けっこんじゅっしゅうねん|きねん|かぞく|りょこう|い"},{"japanese":"旅行の記念に、この小さな人形を買いました。","romaji":"Ryokō no kinen ni, kono chiisana ningyō o kaimashita.","german":"I bought this little doll as a souvenir of the trip.","furigana":"りょこう|きねん|ちい|にんぎょう|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"金 with the reading きん means the metal \"gold\", the color (金色) and first place (金メダル); on calendars it is also an abbreviation for 金曜日 \"Friday\". The same kanji with the reading かね means \"money\" and in everyday use is almost always used politely as お金 (its own card). In sayings, 金 stands for something very valuable, such as 沈黙は金.","examples":[{"japanese":"その選手はオリンピックで金メダルを取った。","romaji":"Sono senshu wa Orinpikku de kin medaru o totta.","german":"That athlete won a gold medal at the Olympics.","furigana":"せんしゅ|きん|と"},{"japanese":"この指輪は本物の金でできている。","romaji":"Kono yubiwa wa honmono no kin de dekite iru.","german":"This ring is made of real gold.","furigana":"ゆびわ|ほんもの|きん"},{"japanese":"「沈黙は金」ということわざを知っていますか。","romaji":"\"Chinmoku wa kin\" to iu kotowaza o shitte imasu ka.","german":"Do you know the proverb \"Silence is golden\"?","furigana":"ちんもく|きん|し"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 37"},
{"notes":"金曜 is the short form of 金曜日 \"Friday\" (its own card). It is common in spoken language as well as in timetables, schedules and calendars, e.g. 金曜の夜 or 月曜から金曜まで. In formal texts and announcements the full form 金曜日 is preferred; calendars often show just 金.","examples":[{"japanese":"金曜の夜は、たいてい友だちと飲みに行く。","romaji":"Kin'yō no yoru wa, taitei tomodachi to nomi ni iku.","german":"On Friday nights, I usually go out for drinks with friends.","furigana":"きんよう|よる|とも|の|い"},{"japanese":"レポートの締め切りは来週の金曜です。","romaji":"Repōto no shimekiri wa raishū no kin'yō desu.","german":"The deadline for the report is next Friday.","furigana":"し|き|らいしゅう|きんよう"},{"japanese":"この店は月曜から金曜まで営業しています。","romaji":"Kono mise wa getsuyō kara kin'yō made eigyō shite imasu.","german":"This shop is open from Monday to Friday.","furigana":"みせ|げつよう|きんよう|えいぎょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"グランド is a common spelling of グラウンド \"sports field, playing field\"; both are the same word, and グラウンド is regarded as a more accurate rendering of English \"ground\". One trains or plays グランドで; a school's sports field is typical. 校庭 (its own card) is the more general \"schoolyard\". Do not confuse with グランド \"grand\" as in グランドピアノ.","examples":[{"japanese":"放課後、グランドでサッカーの練習をした。","romaji":"Hōkago, gurando de sakkā no renshū o shita.","german":"After school, we practiced soccer on the field.","furigana":"ほうかご|れんしゅう"},{"japanese":"雨でグランドがぬかるんでいるので、試合は中止です。","romaji":"Ame de gurando ga nukarunde iru node, shiai wa chūshi desu.","german":"The field is muddy from the rain, so the game is canceled.","furigana":"あめ|しあい|ちゅうし"},{"japanese":"朝礼のとき、全校生徒がグラウンドに集まった。","romaji":"Chōrei no toki, zenkō seito ga guraundo ni atsumatta.","german":"At the morning assembly, all the students gathered on the field.","furigana":"ちょうれい|ぜんこうせいと|あつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"月曜 is the short form of 月曜日 \"Monday\" (its own card). It is common in spoken language as well as in timetables, garbage collection calendars and schedules, e.g. 月曜の朝 or 月曜と木曜. In formal texts and announcements the full form 月曜日 is preferred; calendars often show just 月.","examples":[{"japanese":"月曜の朝はいつも電車が混んでいる。","romaji":"Getsuyō no asa wa itsumo densha ga konde iru.","german":"On Monday mornings, the trains are always crowded.","furigana":"げつよう|あさ|でんしゃ|こ"},{"japanese":"祝日なので、今週の月曜は授業がありません。","romaji":"Shukujitsu na node, konshū no getsuyō wa jugyō ga arimasen.","german":"It's a holiday, so there's no class this Monday.","furigana":"しゅくじつ|こんしゅう|げつよう|じゅぎょう"},{"japanese":"燃えるゴミは月曜と木曜に出してください。","romaji":"Moeru gomi wa getsuyō to mokuyō ni dashite kudasai.","german":"Please put out burnable trash on Mondays and Thursdays.","furigana":"も|げつよう|もくよう|だ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"〜後 (ご) is the Sino-Japanese reading of 後 and stands directly after time spans and events: 三日後 \"three days later\", 卒業後 \"after graduation\", 食後 \"after a meal\"; その後 means \"after that, since then\". The independent noun 後 (あと, its own card), by contrast, is connected with の or the past form (食事の後で, 食べた後で) and sounds more everyday. 〜後 is more concise and rather literary; see also 以後 \"from now on\" (its own card).","examples":[{"japanese":"手術の一週間後に退院できました。","romaji":"Shujutsu no isshūkan go ni taiin dekimashita.","german":"I was able to leave the hospital a week after the surgery.","furigana":"しゅじゅつ|いっしゅうかんご|たいいん"},{"japanese":"この薬は食後に飲んでください。","romaji":"Kono kusuri wa shokugo ni nonde kudasai.","german":"Please take this medicine after meals.","furigana":"くすり|しょくご|の"},{"japanese":"彼とは大学で会ったが、その後一度も連絡していない。","romaji":"Kare to wa daigaku de atta ga, sono go ichido mo renraku shite inai.","german":"I met him at university, but I haven't contacted him once since then.","furigana":"かれ|だいがく|あ|ごいちど|れんらく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"最中 (さいちゅう) means \"in the middle of, right while\" and follows 〜の or 〜ている: 食事の最中に, 話している最中に. It stresses that something happens exactly during an activity, usually as an interruption; 途中 (its own card) means more \"on the way, not yet finished\". With the same spelling, もなか denotes a wafer confection with bean paste (its own card); the reading さなか is formal.","examples":[{"japanese":"食事の最中に電話が鳴った。","romaji":"Shokuji no saichū ni denwa ga natta.","german":"The phone rang in the middle of the meal.","furigana":"しょくじ|さいちゅう|でんわ|な"},{"japanese":"今、会議の最中なので、後でかけ直します。","romaji":"Ima, kaigi no saichū na node, ato de kakenaoshimasu.","german":"I'm in the middle of a meeting right now, so I'll call back later.","furigana":"いま|かいぎ|さいちゅう|あと|なお"},{"japanese":"試合をしている最中に、雨が降り出した。","romaji":"Shiai o shite iru saichū ni, ame ga furidashita.","german":"It started raining in the middle of the game.","furigana":"しあい|さいちゅう|あめ|ふ|だ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"酒 (さけ) generally means \"alcohol, alcoholic drink\" and specifically \"sake\"; in polite everyday speech one usually says お酒 (its own card). The plain form is used in matter-of-fact speech, rules and expressions such as 酒に強い/弱い \"to have a high/low tolerance\". In compounds the reading changes: 日本酒 (しゅ), 酒屋 (さか). Do not confuse with the homophone 鮭 \"salmon\".","examples":[{"japanese":"父は毎晩、夕食のときに酒を少し飲む。","romaji":"Chichi wa maiban, yūshoku no toki ni sake o sukoshi nomu.","german":"My father drinks a little alcohol with dinner every evening.","furigana":"ちち|まいばん|ゆうしょく|さけ|すこ|の"},{"japanese":"私は酒に弱いので、ビール一杯で顔が赤くなる。","romaji":"Watashi wa sake ni yowai node, bīru ippai de kao ga akaku naru.","german":"I can't hold my alcohol, so my face turns red after a single beer.","furigana":"わたし|さけ|よわ|いっぱい|かお|あか"},{"japanese":"酒を飲んだら、絶対に車を運転してはいけない。","romaji":"Sake o nondara, zettai ni kuruma o unten shite wa ikenai.","german":"If you've been drinking, you must never drive a car.","furigana":"さけ|の|ぜったい|くるま|うんてん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"支配 is the noun \"rule, control\"; the verb is 支配する \"to rule\" (its own card). Typical combinations are 〜の支配を受ける \"to be under someone's rule\", 〜の支配下にある \"to be under the control of ...\" and 支配者 \"ruler\". The word is rather literary and is used for states and markets, and figuratively for emotions.","examples":[{"japanese":"その国は長い間、外国の支配を受けていた。","romaji":"Sono kuni wa nagai aida, gaikoku no shihai o ukete ita.","german":"The country was under foreign rule for a long time.","furigana":"くに|なが|あいだ|がいこく|しはい|う"},{"japanese":"王の支配は国の隅々まで及んでいた。","romaji":"Ō no shihai wa kuni no sumizumi made oyonde ita.","german":"The king's rule extended to every corner of the country.","furigana":"おう|しはい|くに|すみずみ|およ"},{"japanese":"この市場は、わずか三社の支配下にある。","romaji":"Kono shijō wa, wazuka sansha no shihaika ni aru.","german":"This market is controlled by just three companies.","furigana":"しじょう|さんしゃ|しはいか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"修正 is the noun \"correction, revision\"; the verb 修正する has its own card. Common are 修正を加える \"to make changes\", 修正が必要だ and 修正案 \"amendment proposal\"; for photos, 修正 also means \"retouching\". 訂正 (its own card) corrects a clear mistake, whereas 修正 adjusts or improves something even if it was not wrong.","examples":[{"japanese":"先生に言われて、作文に少し修正を加えた。","romaji":"Sensei ni iwarete, sakubun ni sukoshi shūsei o kuwaeta.","german":"At the teacher's suggestion, I made a few revisions to my composition.","furigana":"せんせい|い|さくぶん|すこ|しゅうせい|くわ"},{"japanese":"この計画にはまだ修正が必要だ。","romaji":"Kono keikaku ni wa mada shūsei ga hitsuyō da.","german":"This plan still needs revision.","furigana":"けいかく|しゅうせい|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"国会で予算の修正案が話し合われた。","romaji":"Kokkai de yosan no shūseian ga hanashiawareta.","german":"A revised budget proposal was discussed in the Diet.","furigana":"こっかい|よさん|しゅうせいあん|はな|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"順 means \"order, sequence\" and appears mainly in fixed patterns: 順に \"in order\", 〜の順に \"in the order of ...\" and as a suffix as in 五十音順 \"in gojuon (kana) order\" or 背の順 \"in order of height\". On its own, e.g. for \"to be one's turn\", one usually says 順番 (its own card): 順番が来る.","examples":[{"japanese":"名前を呼ばれた順に部屋に入ってください。","romaji":"Namae o yobareta jun ni heya ni haitte kudasai.","german":"Please enter the room in the order your names are called.","furigana":"なまえ|よ|じゅん|へや|はい"},{"japanese":"子どもたちは背の順に並んだ。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa se no jun ni naranda.","german":"The children lined up in order of height.","furigana":"こ|せ|じゅん|なら"},{"japanese":"この辞書の単語は五十音順に並んでいる。","romaji":"Kono jisho no tango wa gojūonjun ni narande iru.","german":"The words in this dictionary are arranged in gojuon order.","furigana":"じしょ|たんご|ごじゅうおんじゅん|なら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"小 (しょう) denotes the small size in the series 大・中・小 and is the counterpart of 中 (ちゅう) \"medium size\" (its own card) and 大 (だい). As a prefix it means \"small-\", e.g. 小規模 \"small scale\" or 小学校 (its own card). On its own it appears almost only in size indications and in sayings such as 大は小を兼ねる; the normal adjective \"small\" is 小さい, and in native words 小 is read こ (小鳥).","examples":[{"japanese":"コーヒーのサイズは大・中・小から選べます。","romaji":"Kōhī no saizu wa dai, chū, shō kara erabemasu.","german":"You can choose the coffee size from large, medium or small.","furigana":"だい|ちゅう|しょう|えら"},{"japanese":"この会社は小規模だが、技術力が高い。","romaji":"Kono kaisha wa shōkibo da ga, gijutsuryoku ga takai.","german":"This company is small-scale, but its technical ability is high.","furigana":"かいしゃ|しょうきぼ|ぎじゅつりょく|たか"},{"japanese":"「大は小を兼ねる」と言って、母は大きい鍋を買った。","romaji":"\"Dai wa shō o kaneru\" to itte, haha wa ōkii nabe o katta.","german":"Saying \"large can serve as small\", my mother bought a big pot.","furigana":"だい|しょう|か|い|はは|おお|なべ|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"尻 is the plain word for \"buttocks, bottom\"; in everyday speech and with children, the more polite お尻 (its own card) is usually used. The bare form sounds a bit coarse and appears mainly in idioms: 尻に敷く \"to have under one's thumb\", 尻が重い \"to be slow to act\". Figuratively it also means \"end, last place\"; 尻尾 (しっぽ, its own card), by contrast, is an animal's \"tail\".","examples":[{"japanese":"長い時間座っていたので、尻が痛くなった。","romaji":"Nagai jikan suwatte ita node, shiri ga itaku natta.","german":"I sat for so long that my bottom started to hurt.","furigana":"なが|じかんすわ|しり|いた"},{"japanese":"彼は奥さんの尻に敷かれている。","romaji":"Kare wa okusan no shiri ni shikarete iru.","german":"He is under his wife's thumb.","furigana":"かれ|おく|しり|し"},{"japanese":"弟は尻が重くて、なかなか宿題を始めない。","romaji":"Otōto wa shiri ga omokute, nakanaka shukudai o hajimenai.","german":"My little brother is lazy and just won't start his homework.","furigana":"おとうと|しり|おも|しゅくだい|はじ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Noun for 進歩する (vocab-n3:27), mostly with の before it: 技術の進歩 \"technological progress\", 医学の進歩 \"progress in medicine\". Typical combinations are 進歩が速い \"to advance quickly\" and 進歩が見られる \"progress can be seen\". Unlike 発展 \"development, growth\", 進歩 stresses improvement toward a higher level.","examples":[{"japanese":"科学技術の進歩はとても速い。","romaji":"Kagaku gijutsu no shinpo wa totemo hayai.","german":"The progress of science and technology is very fast.","furigana":"かがくぎじゅつ|しんぽ|はや"},{"japanese":"彼の日本語には大きな進歩が見られる。","romaji":"Kare no nihongo ni wa ookina shinpo ga mirareru.","german":"Great progress can be seen in his Japanese.","furigana":"かれ|にほんご|おお|しんぽ|み"},{"japanese":"医学の進歩のおかげで、多くの病気が治るようになった。","romaji":"Igaku no shinpo no okage de, ooku no byouki ga naoru you ni natta.","german":"Thanks to progress in medicine, many diseases have become curable.","furigana":"いがく|しんぽ|おお|びょうき|なお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"On its own, 性 means mainly \"sex, gender\" (性の違い, 性別) and \"sexuality\" (性教育), more rarely \"nature, character of a person\". Very common is the suffix -性, which forms qualities like English \"-ity/-ness\": 可能性 \"possibility\", 安全性 \"safety\", 重要性 \"importance\". On forms, 性別 is usually used instead of 性. Homophones: 背 \"height\", 姓 \"family name\".","examples":[{"japanese":"この調査では性による違いはほとんどなかった。","romaji":"Kono chousa de wa sei ni yoru chigai wa hotondo nakatta.","german":"In this survey, there were hardly any differences by gender.","furigana":"ちょうさ|せい|ちが"},{"japanese":"学校で性について正しく教えることが大切だ。","romaji":"Gakkou de sei ni tsuite tadashiku oshieru koto ga taisetsu da.","german":"It is important to teach sex education properly at school.","furigana":"がっこう|せい|ただ|おし|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"明日は雨が降る可能性が高い。","romaji":"Ashita wa ame ga furu kanousei ga takai.","german":"There is a high probability of rain tomorrow.","furigana":"あした|あめ|ふ|かのうせい|たか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Almost always written in kana; the kanji form 所為 is rare. It follows nouns with の or follows a clause: 〜のせいで \"because of\", 〜のせいだ \"is to blame\", 〜せいか \"maybe because\". In contrast to 〜のおかげで \"thanks to\", せい denotes a cause with a negative result and often shifts the blame. Homophones: 背 \"height\", 姓 \"family name\".","examples":[{"japanese":"雨のせいで、試合が中止になった。","romaji":"Ame no sei de, shiai ga chuushi ni natta.","german":"Because of the rain, the game was canceled.","furigana":"あめ|しあい|ちゅうし"},{"japanese":"失敗したのは私のせいです。","romaji":"Shippai shita no wa watashi no sei desu.","german":"It's my fault that it failed.","furigana":"しっぱい|わたし"},{"japanese":"寝不足のせいか、今日は頭が痛い。","romaji":"Nebusoku no sei ka, kyou wa atama ga itai.","german":"Maybe because I didn't sleep enough, I have a headache today.","furigana":"ねぶそく|きょう|あたま|いた"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"An abstract, rather literary word for the morally good, usually in contrast to 悪 \"evil\": 善と悪, 善悪 \"good and evil\". The proverb 善は急げ \"don't put off a good deed\" is well known. For everyday \"good\" one says 良い/いい; 善 on its own is used only in the ethical sense. Do not confuse with the homophones 禅 \"Zen\", 膳 \"tray\" and 前 \"former\".","examples":[{"japanese":"小さい子どもはまだ善と悪の区別がつかない。","romaji":"Chiisai kodomo wa mada zen to aku no kubetsu ga tsukanai.","german":"Small children cannot yet tell good from evil.","furigana":"ちい|こ|ぜん|あく|くべつ"},{"japanese":"善は急げだ。今すぐ電話しよう。","romaji":"Zen wa isoge da. Ima sugu denwa shiyou.","german":"Don't put off a good deed. I'll call right now.","furigana":"ぜん|いそ|いま|でんわ"},{"japanese":"人間の本性は善だと考える人もいる。","romaji":"Ningen no honshou wa zen da to kangaeru hito mo iru.","german":"Some people think that human nature is good.","furigana":"にんげん|ほんしょう|ぜん|かんが|ひと"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"A productive Sino-Japanese prefix before nouns: 全世界 \"the whole world\", 全社員 \"all employees\", 全国民 \"the whole nation\". Before numbers with a counter it means \"in total\": 全10巻 \"ten volumes in total\". Fixed words such as 全部, 全国, 全員 are learned as vocabulary of their own; similar is 総〜, which stresses \"total, general\" (総人口). Homophones: 前 \"former\", 善 \"good\", 禅 \"Zen\".","examples":[{"japanese":"そのニュースは全世界に伝えられた。","romaji":"Sono nyuusu wa zensekai ni tsutaerareta.","german":"The news was reported to the whole world.","furigana":"ぜんせかい|つた"},{"japanese":"朝九時に全社員が会議室に集まった。","romaji":"Asa kuji ni zenshain ga kaigishitsu ni atsumatta.","german":"At nine in the morning, all employees gathered in the meeting room.","furigana":"あさくじ|ぜんしゃいん|かいぎしつ|あつ"},{"japanese":"この漫画は全20巻で完結している。","romaji":"Kono manga wa zen nijukkan de kanketsu shite iru.","german":"This manga is complete in 20 volumes in total.","furigana":"まんが|ぜん|かん|かんけつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"The opposite of 得 \"gain, advantage\"; typical are 損をする \"to lose money, to get the short end of the stick\" and 損得 \"gain and loss\". As a na-adjective it means \"disadvantageous\": 損な役 \"the thankless role\"; 〜と損だ means \"it would be a disadvantage if ...\". For large losses in business texts, 損失 or 損害 are more usual.","examples":[{"japanese":"安いと思って買ったら、かえって損をした。","romaji":"Yasui to omotte kattara, kaette son o shita.","german":"I bought it thinking it was cheap, and ended up losing out instead.","furigana":"やす|おも|か|そん"},{"japanese":"遠慮ばかりしていると損だよ。","romaji":"Enryo bakari shite iru to son da yo.","german":"If you're always holding back, you lose out.","furigana":"えんりょ|そん"},{"japanese":"兄は株で百万円の損を出した。","romaji":"Ani wa kabu de hyakuman-en no son o dashita.","german":"My older brother took a loss of one million yen on stocks.","furigana":"あに|かぶ|ひゃくまんえん|そん|だ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Noun for 尊重する (vocab-n3:18): 人権の尊重 \"respect for human rights\", 個性の尊重 \"respect for individuality\". It sounds formal and often appears in legal, school and newspaper language. Unlike 尊敬 \"admiration (of a person)\", 尊重 refers to opinions, rights and decisions that one takes seriously.","examples":[{"japanese":"憲法は基本的人権の尊重を定めている。","romaji":"Kenpou wa kihonteki jinken no sonchou o sadamete iru.","german":"The constitution stipulates respect for fundamental human rights.","furigana":"けんぽう|きほんてきじんけん|そんちょう|さだ"},{"japanese":"この学校は生徒の個性の尊重を大切にしている。","romaji":"Kono gakkou wa seito no kosei no sonchou o taisetsu ni shite iru.","german":"This school places great importance on respect for each student's individuality.","furigana":"がっこう|せいと|こせい|そんちょう|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"相手の意見への尊重がなければ、話し合いは進まない。","romaji":"Aite no iken e no sonchou ga nakereba, hanashiai wa susumanai.","german":"Without respect for the other person's opinion, a discussion won't make progress.","furigana":"あいて|いけん|そんちょう|はな|あ|すす"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Sino-Japanese reading of 他, fixed mainly in その他 \"others, and other things\" and 自他 \"oneself and others\". With 他の \"other\", both readings are possible: in conversation the native reading ほか (vocab-n5:1433) predominates, while the reading た sounds more literary and matter-of-fact. ほかに \"besides\", on the other hand, is never read た.","examples":[{"japanese":"名前、住所、その他の情報をここに書いてください。","romaji":"Namae, juusho, sono ta no jouhou o koko ni kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write your name, address and other information here.","furigana":"なまえ|じゅうしょ|た|じょうほう|か"},{"japanese":"果物はりんご、みかん、その他いろいろあります。","romaji":"Kudamono wa ringo, mikan, sono ta iroiro arimasu.","german":"As for fruit, there are apples, mandarins and many other kinds.","furigana":"くだもの|た"},{"japanese":"他の方法がないか、もう一度考えてみよう。","romaji":"Ta no houhou ga nai ka, mou ichido kangaete miyou.","german":"Let's think again whether there isn't another method.","furigana":"た|ほうほう|いちどかんが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Counterpart of 中 \"medium\" (vocab-n5:correction:chu-medium) and 小 \"small\": sizes are given as 大・中・小. As a prefix, だい- intensifies mainly Sino-Japanese nouns: 大問題 \"big problem\", 大成功 \"great success\", 大事件 \"major incident\". Before native Japanese words it is usually おお- (大雨, 大きい). Homophones: 台 \"stand\", 〜代 \"fee\", 題 \"title\".","examples":[{"japanese":"コーヒーのサイズは大と小、どちらにしますか。","romaji":"Koohii no saizu wa dai to shou, dochira ni shimasu ka.","german":"Which coffee size would you like, large or small?","furigana":"だい|しょう"},{"japanese":"昨日のパーティーは大成功だった。","romaji":"Kinou no paatii wa daiseikou datta.","german":"Yesterday's party was a great success.","furigana":"きのう|だいせいこう"},{"japanese":"大は小を兼ねるから、大きい箱を買おう。","romaji":"Dai wa shou o kaneru kara, ookii hako o kaou.","german":"Large can serve as small, so let's buy the big box.","furigana":"だい|しょう|か|おお|はこ|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Refers to a large, usually worldwide war and appears almost always in 第一次世界大戦 / 第二次世界大戦 \"First/Second World War\", or in short as 大戦中 \"during the war\" and 大戦後 \"after the war\". The general word for war is 戦争. Do not confuse with the homophone 対戦 \"match, competition\" (vocab-n2:400).","examples":[{"japanese":"祖父は第二次世界大戦を経験した。","romaji":"Sofu wa dai niji sekai taisen o keiken shita.","german":"My grandfather lived through the Second World War.","furigana":"そふ|だいにじせかいたいせん|けいけん"},{"japanese":"この建物は大戦中に一度焼けてしまった。","romaji":"Kono tatemono wa taisenchuu ni ichido yakete shimatta.","german":"This building burned down once during the war.","furigana":"たてもの|たいせんちゅう|いちどや"},{"japanese":"大戦後、町は少しずつ復興した。","romaji":"Taisengo, machi wa sukoshi zutsu fukkou shita.","german":"After the war, the town was gradually rebuilt.","furigana":"たいせんご|まち|すこ|ふっこう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Bare form of お茶 (vocab-n5:119). On its own, 茶 sounds written or terse; in conversation one says お茶. Common in compounds and fixed expressions: 茶色 \"brown\", 紅茶 \"black tea\", 茶の湯 \"tea ceremony\", 茶畑 \"tea plantation\". As a short form, 茶 also stands for the color brown, e.g. 茶のコート \"brown coat\".","examples":[{"japanese":"静岡は茶の産地として有名だ。","romaji":"Shizuoka wa cha no sanchi to shite yuumei da.","german":"Shizuoka is famous as a tea-growing region.","furigana":"しずおか|ちゃ|さんち|ゆうめい"},{"japanese":"駅の前で茶のコートを着た人が待っていた。","romaji":"Eki no mae de cha no kooto o kita hito ga matte ita.","german":"A person in a brown coat was waiting in front of the station.","furigana":"えき|まえ|ちゃ|き|ひと|ま"},{"japanese":"京都で初めて茶の湯を体験した。","romaji":"Kyouto de hajimete cha no yu o taiken shita.","german":"In Kyoto, I experienced a tea ceremony for the first time.","furigana":"きょうと|はじ|ちゃ|ゆ|たいけん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Noun for 注目する (vocab-n3:279). Typical combinations: 注目を集める \"to attract attention\", 注目を浴びる \"to be in the spotlight\", 注目の的 \"center of attention\" and, attributively, 注目の新人 \"closely watched newcomer\". Unlike 注意 \"caution, heed (of danger)\", 注目 means looking with interest.","examples":[{"japanese":"新しいスマホが世界中の注目を集めている。","romaji":"Atarashii sumaho ga sekaijuu no chuumoku o atsumete iru.","german":"The new smartphone is drawing attention from around the world.","furigana":"あたら|せかいじゅう|ちゅうもく|あつ"},{"japanese":"彼女は今年いちばん注目の新人歌手だ。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kotoshi ichiban chuumoku no shinjin kashu da.","german":"She is the most closely watched newcomer singer this year.","furigana":"かのじょ|ことし|ちゅうもく|しんじんかしゅ"},{"japanese":"皆さん、ご注目ください。","romaji":"Minasan, go-chuumoku kudasai.","german":"Everyone, may I have your attention, please!","furigana":"みな|ちゅうもく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"注ぐ in the reading つぐ means \"to pour into a vessel\" and is mostly written in kana: お茶をつぐ, ご飯をつぐ \"to fill a bowl with rice\". The reading そそぐ (its own card) is more general and more formal; rivers also flow into the sea, or one \"pours\" energy into something (力を注ぐ). For eating and drinking in daily life one says つぐ; do not confuse with 継ぐ \"to inherit\" and 次ぐ \"to follow\".","examples":[{"japanese":"お客さんにお茶を注いでください。","romaji":"Okyakusan ni ocha o tsuide kudasai.","german":"Please pour tea for the guest.","furigana":"きゃく|ちゃ|つ"},{"japanese":"ご飯をもう少し注ぎましょうか。","romaji":"Gohan o mō sukoshi tsugimashō ka.","german":"Shall I serve you a bit more rice?","furigana":"はん|すこ|つ"},{"japanese":"飲み会で部長のグラスにビールを注いだ。","romaji":"Nomikai de buchō no gurasu ni bīru o tsuida.","german":"At the drinking party, I poured beer into the department head's glass.","furigana":"の|かい|ぶちょう|つ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"どうか is almost always written in kana. At the start of a sentence it strengthens a request: どうかお願いします \"please, I beg you\" - more earnest and formal than どうぞ, which rather offers something. It also forms 〜かどうか \"whether (or not)\" and どうかしている \"to not be quite oneself, to be strange\".","examples":[{"japanese":"どうかよろしくお願いいたします。","romaji":"Dōka yoroshiku onegai itashimasu.","german":"I sincerely ask for your kind cooperation.","furigana":"ねが"},{"japanese":"明日晴れるかどうか分からない。","romaji":"Ashita hareru ka dōka wakaranai.","german":"I don't know whether it will be sunny tomorrow.","furigana":"あしたは|わ"},{"japanese":"今日の彼はどうかしている。","romaji":"Kyō no kare wa dōka shite iru.","german":"Something is wrong with him today.","furigana":"きょう|かれ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"値 (ね) is the price as a market quantity and is found in 値段 (its own card), 値上げ \"price increase\" and 値下げ \"price reduction\". On its own it appears mainly in fixed expressions: 値が上がる/下がる, 値がつく \"to fetch a price\", 値が張る \"to be expensive\". For the price of a specific item one usually says 値段; the reading あたい means \"value\", also in the mathematical sense.","examples":[{"japanese":"天候不順で野菜の値が上がっている。","romaji":"Tenkō fujun de yasai no ne ga agatte iru.","german":"Vegetable prices are rising because of the unseasonable weather.","furigana":"てんこうふじゅん|やさい|ね|あ"},{"japanese":"その古い絵には思ったより高い値がついた。","romaji":"Sono furui e ni wa omotta yori takai ne ga tsuita.","german":"That old painting fetched a higher price than expected.","furigana":"ふる|え|おも|たか|ね"},{"japanese":"この時計は少し値が張るが、一生使える。","romaji":"Kono tokei wa sukoshi ne ga haru ga, isshō tsukaeru.","german":"This watch is a bit pricey, but you can use it for a lifetime.","furigana":"とけい|すこ|ね|は|いっしょうつか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"年月 has two readings with the same meaning \"years and months, a long time\". ねんげつ is the Sino-Japanese, more matter-of-fact reading and is more common in news and factual texts (長い年月をかけて, 十年の年月); としつき (its own card) sounds softer and more literary, often emotional. In writing, the reading cannot be told; 年月日 \"date\", by contrast, is read ねんがっぴ.","examples":[{"japanese":"この橋は長い年月をかけて完成した。","romaji":"Kono hashi wa nagai nengetsu o kakete kansei shita.","german":"This bridge was completed after many years of work.","furigana":"はし|なが|ねんげつ|かんせい"},{"japanese":"十年の年月を経て、二人は再会した。","romaji":"Jūnen no nengetsu o hete, futari wa saikai shita.","german":"After ten years, the two met again.","furigana":"じゅうねん|ねんげつ|へ|ふたり|さいかい"},{"japanese":"研究には膨大な年月と費用がかかった。","romaji":"Kenkyū ni wa bōdai na nengetsu to hiyō ga kakatta.","german":"The research took an enormous amount of time and money.","furigana":"けんきゅう|ぼうだい|ねんげつ|ひよう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"後 in the reading のち means \"later, afterward\" and is more formal than あと (its own card); it is often written in kana. Typical are 後に \"later (on)\", 後ほど \"later\" (polite) and the weather report: 晴れ後曇り \"sunny, later cloudy\". In everyday speech one says あとで rather; 後ろ (うしろ), by contrast, means \"behind\" in a spatial sense.","examples":[{"japanese":"明日は晴れ後曇りでしょう。","romaji":"Ashita wa hare nochi kumori deshō.","german":"Tomorrow will be sunny, later cloudy.","furigana":"あした|は|のちくも"},{"japanese":"詳しいことは後ほどご連絡します。","romaji":"Kuwashii koto wa nochihodo go-renraku shimasu.","german":"I will contact you later with the details.","furigana":"くわ|のち|れんらく"},{"japanese":"彼は後に有名な画家になった。","romaji":"Kare wa nochi ni yūmei na gaka ni natta.","german":"He later became a famous painter.","furigana":"かれ|のち|ゆうめい|がか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"発車 denotes the departure of a vehicle (train, bus, car) and is used as a verb with する: 電車が発車する. Typical at a station are 発車時刻 \"departure time\" and the 発車ベル \"departure bell\". 出発 (its own card) is more general and also applies to people; the homophone 発射 means \"to fire\" (rocket, shot).","examples":[{"japanese":"まもなく電車が発車します。","romaji":"Mamonaku densha ga hassha shimasu.","german":"The train will depart shortly.","furigana":"でんしゃ|はっしゃ"},{"japanese":"バスの発車時刻まであと五分ある。","romaji":"Basu no hassha jikoku made ato gofun aru.","german":"There are five more minutes until the bus departs.","furigana":"はっしゃじこく|ごふん"},{"japanese":"発車間際の駆け込み乗車は危険です。","romaji":"Hassha magiwa no kakekomi jōsha wa kiken desu.","german":"Rushing to board just before departure is dangerous.","furigana":"はっしゃまぎわ|か|こ|じょうしゃ|きけん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"発達 is development as growth from within: physical and mental (子どもの発達, 筋肉が発達する), technical (交通の発達) and in weather (台風が発達する \"to intensify\"). As a verb with する it is intransitive. 発展 (its own card), by contrast, stresses expansion and upswing, e.g. of cities and the economy (経済の発展).","examples":[{"japanese":"科学技術の発達で、生活が便利になった。","romaji":"Kagaku gijutsu no hattatsu de, seikatsu ga benri ni natta.","german":"With advances in technology, life has become more convenient.","furigana":"かがくぎじゅつ|はったつ|せいかつ|べんり"},{"japanese":"台風が発達しながら北上している。","romaji":"Taifū ga hattatsu shinagara hokujō shite iru.","german":"The typhoon is moving north while intensifying.","furigana":"たいふう|はったつ|ほくじょう"},{"japanese":"子どもの心と体の発達には睡眠が大切だ。","romaji":"Kodomo no kokoro to karada no hattatsu ni wa suimin ga taisetsu da.","german":"Sleep is important for the mental and physical development of children.","furigana":"こ|こころ|からだ|はったつ|すいみん|たいせつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"バン is the loanword \"van\": a box van or small truck for goods and tools, often used for business (配達用のバン, 営業用のバン). For large trucks one says トラック (its own card), for station wagons and minivans rather ワゴン. Do not confuse with 晩 \"evening\" and 番 \"number\"; バン as the sound of a bang is a different word.","examples":[{"japanese":"引っ越しの荷物をバンで運んだ。","romaji":"Hikkoshi no nimotsu o ban de hakonda.","german":"We transported our moving belongings in a van.","furigana":"ひ|こ|にもつ|はこ"},{"japanese":"店の前に配達用のバンが止まっている。","romaji":"Mise no mae ni haitatsuyō no ban ga tomatte iru.","german":"A delivery van is parked in front of the shop.","furigana":"みせ|まえ|はいたつよう|と"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"密 みつ is a na-adjective or noun for \"dense, close\", both spatially (人口が密だ) and for relationships and contact (密な関係, 連絡を密にする \"to stay in close contact\"). Since the coronavirus period it has been widely known through 密を避ける and 三密 \"the three Cs (closed spaces, crowded places, close contact)\". In compounds it is more common than on its own: 密接, 秘密, 密度. Do not confuse with the homophone 蜜/みつ \"honey, nectar\".","examples":[{"japanese":"仕事では、上司と連絡を密にすることが大切だ。","romaji":"Shigoto de wa, joushi to renraku o mitsu ni suru koto ga taisetsu da.","german":"At work, it is important to keep in close contact with your boss.","furigana":"しごと|じょうし|れんらく|みつ|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"二つの会社は密な関係にある。","romaji":"Futatsu no kaisha wa mitsu na kankei ni aru.","german":"The two companies have a close relationship.","furigana":"ふた|かいしゃ|みつ|かんけい"},{"japanese":"都市部は人口が密で、空き地が少ない。","romaji":"Toshibu wa jinkou ga mitsu de, akichi ga sukunai.","german":"In urban areas the population is dense and there is little vacant land.","furigana":"としぶ|じんこう|みつ|あ|ち|すく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"反抗 is the noun \"resistance, defiance\", especially against parents, teachers or superiors: 親への反抗, 反抗期 \"rebellious phase, adolescence\", 反抗的な態度 \"defiant attitude\". The verb form 反抗する (with に) has its own card. 抵抗 is the more neutral \"resistance\" (also in physics); the homophone 反攻 means \"counterattack\".","examples":[{"japanese":"息子は今、反抗期で口をきいてくれない。","romaji":"Musuko wa ima, hankōki de kuchi o kiite kurenai.","german":"My son is in a defiant phase right now and won't talk to me.","furigana":"むすこ|いま|はんこうき|くち"},{"japanese":"彼の反抗的な態度に先生は困っている。","romaji":"Kare no hankōteki na taido ni sensei wa komatte iru.","german":"The teacher is struggling with his rebellious attitude.","furigana":"かれ|はんこうてき|たいど|せんせい|こま"},{"japanese":"親への反抗は成長の一部だ。","romaji":"Oya e no hankō wa seichō no ichibu da.","german":"Rebelling against one's parents is part of growing up.","furigana":"おや|はんこう|せいちょう|いちぶ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"表 in the reading ひょう means \"table, list, chart\" and very often stands at the end of compounds such as 時刻表 \"timetable\", 一覧表 \"overview list\" or 成績表 \"report card\". Typical are 表にする／表にまとめる \"to put in tabular form\" and 表1 \"Table 1\" in texts. Do not confuse with the same spelling 表/おもて \"front side, outside\" (表に出る) and the homophone 雹/ひょう \"hail\".","examples":[{"japanese":"調査の結果を表にまとめました。","romaji":"Chousa no kekka o hyou ni matomemashita.","german":"I summarized the survey results in a table.","furigana":"ちょうさ|けっか|ひょう"},{"japanese":"駅で電車の時刻表を確認した。","romaji":"Eki de densha no jikokuhyou o kakunin shita.","german":"At the station, I checked the train timetable.","furigana":"えき|でんしゃ|じこくひょう|かくにん"},{"japanese":"詳しい数字は表2を見てください。","romaji":"Kuwashii suuji wa hyou ni o mite kudasai.","german":"For the detailed figures, please see Table 2.","furigana":"くわ|すうじ|ひょう|み"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"The negating prefix 不 \"un-, non-\" in its standard reading ふ: 不便 \"inconvenient\", 不安 \"anxiety, worry\", 不足 \"shortage\", 不可能 \"impossible\", 不正確 \"inaccurate\". It combines mainly with Sino-Japanese words and usually forms nouns or na-adjectives. The reading ぶ (its own card 不〜/ぶ) applies only in a few fixed words such as 不器用 or 不気味; when in doubt, ふ is right. Close in meaning is 無〜, which rather expresses the absence of something (\"without, -less\").","examples":[{"japanese":"この町は駅から遠くて不便だ。","romaji":"Kono machi wa eki kara tookute fuben da.","german":"This town is far from the station and inconvenient.","furigana":"まち|えき|とお|ふべん"},{"japanese":"試験の結果が不安で眠れなかった。","romaji":"Shiken no kekka ga fuan de nemurenakatta.","german":"I was so anxious about the exam results that I couldn't sleep.","furigana":"しけん|けっか|ふあん|ねむ"},{"japanese":"一日でこの仕事を終わらせるのは不可能です。","romaji":"Ichinichi de kono shigoto o owaraseru no wa fukanou desu.","german":"Finishing this job in one day is impossible.","furigana":"いちにち|しごと|お|ふかのう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"丸 まる is the \"circle\" and, in everyday Japanese, also the mark for \"correct, yes\" (丸をつける, opposite バツ). As a prefix it means \"whole, full\": 丸一日 \"a whole day\", 丸ごと \"as a whole\", 丸三年 \"three full years\". As a suffix it appears in ship names (〜丸). The adjective 丸い \"round\" is its own card; the spelling 円 for まる is rare and not to be confused with 円/えん \"yen\".","examples":[{"japanese":"正しい答えに丸をつけてください。","romaji":"Tadashii kotae ni maru o tsukete kudasai.","german":"Please circle the correct answer.","furigana":"ただ|こた|まる"},{"japanese":"昨日は丸一日、部屋の掃除をしていた。","romaji":"Kinou wa maru ichinichi, heya no souji o shite ita.","german":"Yesterday I spent a whole day cleaning my room.","furigana":"きのう|まるいちにち|へや|そうじ"},{"japanese":"港に「日本丸」という大きな船が停まっている。","romaji":"Minato ni \"Nippon Maru\" to iu ookina fune ga tomatte iru.","german":"A large ship called \"Nippon Maru\" is moored in the harbor.","furigana":"みなと|にっぽんまる|おお|ふね|と"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 35"},
{"notes":"実 み is the native word for \"fruit, nut, berry\" on plants: 実がなる \"to bear fruit\", 木の実 \"nuts, tree fruit\". Figuratively it stands for \"substance, content\" (実のある話) and in 実を結ぶ \"to bear fruit, to lead to success\". Edible fruit in a shop is called 果物; do not confuse with the homophones 身/み \"body\" and 味/み.","examples":[{"japanese":"秋になると、この木に赤い実がなる。","romaji":"Aki ni naru to, kono ki ni akai mi ga naru.","german":"In autumn, this tree bears red fruit.","furigana":"あき|き|あか|み"},{"japanese":"子供たちは森で木の実を拾った。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa mori de ki no mi o hirotta.","german":"The children gathered nuts in the forest.","furigana":"こども|もり|き|み|ひろ"},{"japanese":"長い会議だったが、実のある話はほとんどなかった。","romaji":"Nagai kaigi datta ga, mi no aru hanashi wa hotondo nakatta.","german":"It was a long meeting, but hardly anything substantial came of it.","furigana":"なが|かいぎ|み|はなし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"見舞い みまい is a visit or greeting to someone who is ill or has suffered a misfortune, also the accompanying gift or letter. On its own, in conversation the polite form お見舞い (its own card, お見舞いに行く) is almost always used; the bare form appears mainly in compounds such as 見舞い客 \"visitor to a patient\", 見舞い金 \"sympathy money\" and 暑中見舞い \"midsummer greeting\". The related verb is 見舞う \"to visit; to strike (of misfortune)\".","examples":[{"japanese":"病院のロビーは見舞い客でにぎわっていた。","romaji":"Byouin no robii wa mimaikyaku de nigiwatte ita.","german":"The hospital lobby was bustling with visitors coming to see patients.","furigana":"びょういん|みま|きゃく"},{"japanese":"夏には友達に暑中見舞いのはがきを出す。","romaji":"Natsu ni wa tomodachi ni shochuu mimai no hagaki o dasu.","german":"In summer, I send midsummer greeting cards to friends.","furigana":"なつ|ともだち|しょちゅうみま|だ"},{"japanese":"会社から被災した社員に見舞い金が送られた。","romaji":"Kaisha kara hisai shita shain ni mimaikin ga okurareta.","german":"The company sent sympathy money to employees affected by the disaster.","furigana":"かいしゃ|ひさい|しゃいん|みま|きん|おく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"妙 みょう is a na-adjective meaning \"strange, odd\": 妙な音 \"a strange noise\", 妙な話 \"an odd story\"; adverbially, 妙に means \"remarkably, somehow\" (妙に静かだ). It sounds a bit more elevated than 変 (変な音) and stresses that one cannot quite explain the strangeness. The older meaning \"excellent, exquisite\" lives on in 絶妙 \"exquisite\" and 巧妙 \"clever\".","examples":[{"japanese":"夜中に台所から妙な音が聞こえた。","romaji":"Yonaka ni daidokoro kara myou na oto ga kikoeta.","german":"In the middle of the night, I heard a strange noise from the kitchen.","furigana":"よなか|だいどころ|みょう|おと|き"},{"japanese":"今日の教室は妙に静かだね。","romaji":"Kyou no kyoushitsu wa myou ni shizuka da ne.","german":"The classroom is oddly quiet today, isn't it?","furigana":"きょう|きょうしつ|みょう|しず"},{"japanese":"彼の話し方には妙な説得力があった。","romaji":"Kare no hanashikata ni wa myou na settokuryoku ga atta.","german":"His way of speaking had a peculiar persuasiveness.","furigana":"かれ|はな|かた|みょう|せっとくりょく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"As a noun, 無 む means \"nothing, zero\" (無になる \"to come to nothing\", 無から \"from nothing\") and is above all the main reading of the prefix \"without, -less, un-\": 無料 \"free of charge\", 無理 \"impossible\", 無意味 \"meaningless\", 無責任 \"irresponsible\". The reading ぶ (its own card 無〜/ぶ) applies only in a few words such as 無事 or 無難. Unlike 不〜 \"non-\", 無〜 stresses the absence of something.","examples":[{"japanese":"失敗したら、今までの努力が無になってしまう。","romaji":"Shippai shitara, ima made no doryoku ga mu ni natte shimau.","german":"If I fail, all my efforts so far will come to nothing.","furigana":"しっぱい|いま|どりょく|む"},{"japanese":"彼は無から会社を立ち上げた。","romaji":"Kare wa mu kara kaisha o tachiageta.","german":"He built the company from nothing.","furigana":"かれ|む|かいしゃ|た|あ"},{"japanese":"そんな無責任な発言は許されない。","romaji":"Sonna musekinin na hatsugen wa yurusarenai.","german":"Such an irresponsible remark is unacceptable.","furigana":"むせきにん|はつげん|ゆる"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"飯 めし is the colloquial, rather masculine and rough word for \"food, meal\" and \"cooked rice\"; typical is 飯を食う instead of the polite ご飯を食べる (its own card ご飯). It is very common in compounds: 昼飯 \"lunch\", 朝飯 \"breakfast\", 晩飯 \"dinner\" and the idiom 朝飯前 \"a piece of cake\". With superiors or in polite speech, the standalone 飯 is avoided.","examples":[{"japanese":"腹が減ったな。飯でも食いに行こうぜ。","romaji":"Hara ga hetta na. Meshi demo kui ni ikou ze.","german":"I'm hungry. Let's go get something to eat.","furigana":"はら|へ|めし|く|い"},{"japanese":"忙しくて昼飯を食べる時間もなかった。","romaji":"Isogashikute hirumeshi o taberu jikan mo nakatta.","german":"I was so busy that I didn't even have time for lunch.","furigana":"いそが|ひるめし|た|じかん"},{"japanese":"こんな問題、朝飯前だよ。","romaji":"Konna mondai, asameshimae da yo.","german":"A problem like this is a piece of cake.","furigana":"もんだい|あさめしまえ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"綿 めん is the Sino-Japanese reading for \"cotton\" as a material, as used on labels and when buying clothes: 綿100%, 綿のシャツ, 綿製品. The card 綿/わた, by contrast, means the cotton plant or \"cotton wool, stuffing\" (布団の綿, 綿あめ). Rule of thumb: fabric and clothing → めん, fluffy filling → わた; for cotton cloth, 木綿 (もめん) is also said in daily life.","examples":[{"japanese":"このシャツは綿100%なので、肌に優しい。","romaji":"Kono shatsu wa men hyaku paasento na node, hada ni yasashii.","german":"This shirt is 100% cotton, so it is gentle on the skin.","furigana":"めん|はだ|やさ"},{"japanese":"夏は綿のTシャツがいちばん涼しい。","romaji":"Natsu wa men no T-shatsu ga ichiban suzushii.","german":"In summer, cotton T-shirts are the coolest.","furigana":"なつ|めん|すず"},{"japanese":"洗う前に、綿かポリエステルかを確認してください。","romaji":"Arau mae ni, men ka poriesuteru ka o kakunin shite kudasai.","german":"Before washing, please check whether it is cotton or polyester.","furigana":"あら|まえ|めん|かくにん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"申し訳 もうしわけ is the noun \"apology, excuse\" and appears almost only in fixed expressions: 申し訳ありません／申し訳ございません \"I am very sorry\" (much more polite than すみません), 申し訳が立たない \"to be unable to justify oneself\" and 申し訳程度 \"just for show, a token amount\". The adjective 申し訳ない \"sorry, inexcusable\" has its own card.","examples":[{"japanese":"お待たせして申し訳ありません。","romaji":"Omatase shite moushiwake arimasen.","german":"I am sorry to have kept you waiting.","furigana":"ま|もう|わけ"},{"japanese":"こんな失敗をしては、チームに申し訳が立たない。","romaji":"Konna shippai o shite wa, chiimu ni moushiwake ga tatanai.","german":"After making such a mistake, I can't face the team.","furigana":"しっぱい|もう|わけ|た"},{"japanese":"料理には申し訳程度に野菜が添えてあった。","romaji":"Ryouri ni wa moushiwake teido ni yasai ga soete atta.","german":"The dish had a token amount of vegetables on the side.","furigana":"りょうり|もう|わけていど|やさい|そ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"木曜 もくよう is the short form of 木曜日 \"Thursday\" and is often used in conversation, in schedules and on signs (木曜の夜, 毎週木曜). Meaning and use correspond to 木曜日 (its own card); the full form sounds a little more careful, the short form more casual and terse. Parallel to it is 火曜 \"Tuesday\"; in calendars it is often just (木).","examples":[{"japanese":"木曜の午後は会議が入っている。","romaji":"Mokuyou no gogo wa kaigi ga haitte iru.","german":"I have a meeting on Thursday afternoon.","furigana":"もくよう|ごご|かいぎ|はい"},{"japanese":"この店は毎週木曜が定休日です。","romaji":"Kono mise wa maishuu mokuyou ga teikyuubi desu.","german":"This shop's regular closing day is every Thursday.","furigana":"みせ|まいしゅうもくよう|ていきゅうび"},{"japanese":"次の木曜までにレポートを出してください。","romaji":"Tsugi no mokuyou made ni repooto o dashite kudasai.","german":"Please submit the report by next Thursday.","furigana":"つぎ|もくよう|だ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"用心 ようじん \"caution, precaution\" is used as a noun and as a suru-verb: 用心する \"to be on guard\", 用心のため \"as a precaution\", 火の用心 \"beware of fire\" (the classic call of the night watch). Compared with 注意 \"attention, warning\", 用心 stresses preventive vigilance against danger, thieves or fire. Derivatives: 用心深い \"cautious\", 不用心 (ぶようじん) \"careless, insecure\".","examples":[{"japanese":"冬は空気が乾燥しているので、火の用心をしましょう。","romaji":"Fuyu wa kuuki ga kansou shite iru node, hi no youjin o shimashou.","german":"The air is dry in winter, so let's be careful with fire.","furigana":"ふゆ|くうき|かんそう|ひ|ようじん"},{"japanese":"夜道を一人で歩くときは用心してください。","romaji":"Yorumichi o hitori de aruku toki wa youjin shite kudasai.","german":"Please be careful when walking alone on a night road.","furigana":"よるみち|ひとり|ある|ようじん"},{"japanese":"用心のために、玄関に鍵を二つ付けた。","romaji":"Youjin no tame ni, genkan ni kagi o futatsu tsuketa.","german":"As a precaution, I put two locks on the front door.","furigana":"ようじん|げんかん|かぎ|ふた|つ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"来 らい is a time affix. As a prefix it means \"next, coming\" and combines with time periods: 来学期, 来年度, 来シーズン (compare the fixed words 来年, 来月, 来週). As a suffix after time expressions it means \"since, for ... now\": 十年来の友人, 昨年来の問題. Do not confuse with the verb 来る (くる) with the same kanji; on its own, 来 is never used as らい.","examples":[{"japanese":"来学期から授業料が上がるそうだ。","romaji":"Raigakki kara jugyouryou ga agaru sou da.","german":"I hear tuition fees will rise from next semester.","furigana":"らいがっき|じゅぎょうりょう|あ"},{"japanese":"来年度の予算はまだ決まっていない。","romaji":"Rainendo no yosan wa mada kimatte inai.","german":"The budget for the next fiscal year has not been decided yet.","furigana":"らいねんど|よさん|き"},{"japanese":"彼とは十年来の付き合いだ。","romaji":"Kare to wa juunenrai no tsukiai da.","german":"I've known him for ten years.","furigana":"かれ|じゅうねんらい|つ|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Basic word for a row or waiting line: 列に並ぶ \"to line up\", 列を作る \"to form a line\", 列に割り込む \"to cut in line\". As a counter it counts rows (一列, 三列目); in tables it denotes the column (行 = row). The compound 行列 (its own card) stresses more a long waiting line or a procession; 列 is the neutral basic word.","examples":[{"japanese":"人気のラーメン屋の前に長い列ができていた。","romaji":"Ninki no rāmen-ya no mae ni nagai retsu ga dekite ita.","german":"A long line had formed in front of the popular ramen shop.","furigana":"にんき|や|まえ|なが|れつ"},{"japanese":"順番に列に並んでお待ちください。","romaji":"Junban ni retsu ni narande omachi kudasai.","german":"Please line up in order and wait.","furigana":"じゅんばん|れつ|なら|ま"},{"japanese":"映画館では前から三列目に座った。","romaji":"Eigakan de wa mae kara sanretsume ni suwatta.","german":"At the cinema, I sat in the third row from the front.","furigana":"えいがかん|まえ|さんれつめ|すわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Noun for the verb card 交流する: 文化交流 \"cultural exchange\", 国際交流 \"international exchange\", 交流会 \"get-together\", 〜との交流 \"exchange with ...\". Typical are 交流が盛んだ and 交流を深める. In electrical engineering, 交流 means \"alternating current\" (opposite 直流 \"direct current\"). Not to be confused with the homophones 拘留/勾留 \"detention\".","examples":[{"japanese":"両国の間では文化交流が盛んに行われている。","romaji":"Ryōkoku no aida de wa bunka kōryū ga sakan ni okonawarete iru.","german":"Cultural exchange is actively carried out between the two countries.","furigana":"りょうこく|あいだ|ぶんかこうりゅう|さか|おこな"},{"japanese":"週末、留学生との交流会に参加した。","romaji":"Shūmatsu, ryūgakusei to no kōryūkai ni sanka shita.","german":"At the weekend, I took part in a social event with international students.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|りゅうがくせい|こうりゅうかい|さんか"},{"japanese":"家庭のコンセントには交流の電気が流れている。","romaji":"Katei no konsento ni wa kōryū no denki ga nagarete iru.","german":"Alternating current flows through household outlets.","furigana":"かてい|こうりゅう|でんき|なが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A neutral, factual noun for \"measurement\", the basis of the verb card 測定する. Common in compounds: 体力測定 \"fitness test\", 測定器 \"measuring device\", 測定結果 \"measurement result\", 測定値 \"measured value\". In everyday life one measures with the verb 測る (はかる); 測定 sounds more technical and is used in school, reports and science.","examples":[{"japanese":"来週、学校で体力測定がある。","romaji":"Raishū, gakkō de tairyoku sokutei ga aru.","german":"Next week there is a physical fitness test at school.","furigana":"らいしゅう|がっこう|たいりょくそくてい"},{"japanese":"測定の結果、水道水は安全だと分かった。","romaji":"Sokutei no kekka, suidōsui wa anzen da to wakatta.","german":"The measurement showed that the tap water is safe.","furigana":"そくてい|けっか|すいどうすい|あんぜん|わ"},{"japanese":"この測定器は温度と湿度を同時に表示する。","romaji":"Kono sokuteiki wa ondo to shitsudo o dōji ni hyōji suru.","german":"This measuring instrument displays temperature and humidity at the same time.","furigana":"そくていき|おんど|しつど|どうじ|ひょうじ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Transitive counterpart of 溜まる: one lets something accumulate, e.g. ストレスを溜める, 風呂にお湯を溜める, 目に涙を溜める. Also used for things left undone: 仕事を溜める, 家賃を溜める \"to fall behind on the rent\". For money or points that one saves deliberately, 貯める (its own card) is written; in kana, ためる, both are very common.","examples":[{"japanese":"ストレスを溜めないように、週末は運動している。","romaji":"Sutoresu o tamenai yō ni, shūmatsu wa undō shite iru.","german":"To avoid bottling up stress, I exercise on weekends.","furigana":"た|しゅうまつ|うんどう"},{"japanese":"お風呂にお湯を溜めておいてくれる？","romaji":"Ofuro ni oyu o tamete oite kureru?","german":"Could you run some water into the bath for me?","furigana":"ふろ|ゆ|た"},{"japanese":"家賃を三か月も溜めてしまい、大家さんに怒られた。","romaji":"Yachin o sankagetsu mo tamete shimai, ōya-san ni okorareta.","german":"I let three months of rent pile up and got scolded by my landlord.","furigana":"やちん|さん|げつ|た|おおや|おこ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Used mainly attributively with の: 特定の人 \"specific people\", 特定の目的 \"a specific purpose\", 不特定多数 \"an unspecified large number\". As a noun it means \"identification, determination\" (犯人の特定, 原因の特定); the verb form is the card 特定する. Unlike 特別 \"special, extraordinary\", 特定 only says that something particular has been selected, not that it stands out.","examples":[{"japanese":"この部屋には特定の社員しか入れない。","romaji":"Kono heya ni wa tokutei no shain shika hairenai.","german":"Only certain employees can enter this room.","furigana":"へや|とくてい|しゃいん|はい"},{"japanese":"特定の食べ物にアレルギーがある人は、事前に知らせてください。","romaji":"Tokutei no tabemono ni arerugī ga aru hito wa, jizen ni shirasete kudasai.","german":"People with an allergy to specific foods, please let us know in advance.","furigana":"とくてい|た|もの|ひと|じぜん|し"},{"japanese":"防犯カメラの映像が犯人の特定につながった。","romaji":"Bōhan kamera no eizō ga hannin no tokutei ni tsunagatta.","german":"The security camera footage led to the identification of the culprit.","furigana":"ぼうはん|えいぞう|はんにん|とくてい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Noun for the verb card 発射する: the firing or launch of rockets, projectiles and the like. Typical: ミサイル発射, ロケットの発射, 発射台 \"launch pad\", 発射ボタン. For the launch of a spacecraft, 打ち上げ is also common; 発射 stresses the firing itself and belongs more to news and technical language.","examples":[{"japanese":"ロケットの発射は明日の朝六時に予定されている。","romaji":"Roketto no hassha wa ashita no asa rokuji ni yotei sarete iru.","german":"The rocket launch is scheduled for six o'clock tomorrow morning.","furigana":"はっしゃ|あした|あさろくじ|よてい"},{"japanese":"ミサイル発射のニュースを聞いて、みんな不安になった。","romaji":"Misairu hassha no nyūsu o kiite, minna fuan ni natta.","german":"Everyone became anxious when they heard the news of the missile launch.","furigana":"はっしゃ|き|ふあん"},{"japanese":"子どもたちは発射台に立つロケットを見に行った。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa hasshadai ni tatsu roketto o mi ni itta.","german":"The children went to see the rocket standing on the launch pad.","furigana":"こ|はっしゃだい|た|み|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Noun for the verb card 反映する: \"reflection\" (社会の反映, 世論の反映) and, in IT and office life, \"incorporation, taking effect of changes\" (変更の反映, 設定の反映). Common in patterns like 〜の反映だ \"is a mirror image of ...\" or 〜に反映される \"is taken into account in ...\". Not to be confused with the homophone 繁栄 \"prosperity, flourishing\".","examples":[{"japanese":"この小説は当時の社会の反映だと言われている。","romaji":"Kono shōsetsu wa tōji no shakai no han'ei da to iwarete iru.","german":"This novel is said to be a reflection of the society of its time.","furigana":"しょうせつ|とうじ|しゃかい|はんえい|い"},{"japanese":"設定変更の反映には数分かかることがあります。","romaji":"Settei henkō no han'ei ni wa sūfun kakaru koto ga arimasu.","german":"It may take a few minutes for the settings change to take effect.","furigana":"せっていへんこう|はんえい|すうふん"},{"japanese":"市民の意見は新しい計画にきちんと反映された。","romaji":"Shimin no iken wa atarashii keikaku ni kichinto han'ei sareta.","german":"The citizens' opinions were properly reflected in the new plan.","furigana":"しみん|いけん|あたら|けいかく|はんえい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A literary word for \"value\" or \"price\"; in everyday speech people say 値段 (ねだん) for price and 価値 (かち) for value. Especially common in mathematics and science: xの値を求めなさい \"find the value of x\". In compounds 値 is usually read ね or ち (値上げ, 数値); related is the verb 値する \"to be worth\" (一読に値する).","examples":[{"japanese":"次の式で、xの値を求めなさい。","romaji":"Tsugi no shiki de, ekkusu no atai o motomenasai.","german":"In the following equation, find the value of x.","furigana":"つぎ|しき|あたい|もと"},{"japanese":"この古い絵には百万円以上の値があるそうだ。","romaji":"Kono furui e ni wa hyakuman-en ijō no atai ga aru sō da.","german":"This old painting is said to be worth over a million yen.","furigana":"ふる|え|ひゃくまんえんいじょう|あたい"},{"japanese":"実験で得られた値をノートに記録した。","romaji":"Jikken de erareta atai o nōto ni kiroku shita.","german":"I recorded the values obtained in the experiment in my notebook.","furigana":"じっけん|え|あたい|きろく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"市 in the reading いち means \"market, fair\", a place where goods are sold regularly. On its own it appears mainly in 市が立つ \"a market is held\"; it is more often found in words such as 朝市 \"morning market\", 蚤の市 \"flea market\" and 市場/いちば \"market (hall)\". The reading し (its own card 市/し), by contrast, means \"city\" as an administrative unit (横浜市). Do not confuse with 位置/いち \"position\".","examples":[{"japanese":"日曜日の朝、港で市が立ちます。","romaji":"Nichiyōbi no asa, minato de ichi ga tachimasu.","german":"On Sunday mornings, a market is held at the harbor.","furigana":"にちようび|あさ|みなと|いち|た"},{"japanese":"旅行先の朝市で新鮮な魚を買った。","romaji":"Ryokōsaki no asaichi de shinsen na sakana o katta.","german":"I bought fresh fish at the morning market at my travel destination.","furigana":"りょこうさき|あさいち|しんせん|さかな|か"},{"japanese":"公園の蚤の市で古いカメラを見つけました。","romaji":"Kōen no nomi no ichi de furui kamera o mitsukemashita.","german":"I found an old camera at the flea market in the park.","furigana":"こうえん||いち|ふる|み"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"確立 is the noun for \"to firmly establish something\": a system, method or position is set up permanently. Typical are 制度の確立 \"establishment of a system\", 地位の確立 \"consolidation of one's position\" and 確立を目指す. The verb 確立する \"to establish, settle\" has its own card. Do not confuse it with the similar-sounding 確率 \"probability\".","examples":[{"japanese":"新しい治療法の確立には時間がかかる。","romaji":"Atarashii chiryōhō no kakuritsu ni wa jikan ga kakaru.","german":"Establishing a new treatment takes time.","furigana":"あたら|ちりょうほう|かくりつ|じかん"},{"japanese":"政府は安全な制度の確立を目指している。","romaji":"Seifu wa anzen na seido no kakuritsu o mezashite iru.","german":"The government is aiming to establish a safe system.","furigana":"せいふ|あんぜん|せいど|かくりつ|めざ"},{"japanese":"このブランドは地位の確立までに十年かかった。","romaji":"Kono burando wa chii no kakuritsu made ni jūnen kakatta.","german":"This brand took ten years to establish its position.","furigana":"ちい|かくりつ|じゅうねん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"共存 \"coexistence, peaceful living side by side\" has two readings: きょうそん is the original, traditional one, and きょうぞん (separate card) is more common today in daily life and the media. Both are considered correct and mean the same; in the set phrase 共存共栄 \"to exist and prosper together\" one often hears きょうそん. Typical combinations are 自然と共存する and 共存を図る.","examples":[{"japanese":"人間と野生動物の共存は簡単ではない。","romaji":"Ningen to yasei dōbutsu no kyōson wa kantan de wa nai.","german":"Coexistence between humans and wild animals is not easy.","furigana":"にんげん|やせいどうぶつ|きょうそん|かんたん"},{"japanese":"地元の商店と大型スーパーの共存共栄を目指す。","romaji":"Jimoto no shōten to ōgata sūpā no kyōson kyōei o mezasu.","german":"We aim for the mutual prosperity of local shops and the large supermarket.","furigana":"じもと|しょうてん|おおがた|きょうそんきょうえい|めざ"},{"japanese":"この町では、いろいろな文化が共存している。","romaji":"Kono machi de wa, iroiro na bunka ga kyōson shite iru.","german":"In this town, various cultures coexist.","furigana":"まち|ぶんか|きょうそん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"設立 is the founding of an organization such as a company, association, foundation or school: 会社の設立, 設立者 \"founder\", 設立記念日. The verb form \"to found\" has its own card, 設立する. 創立 emphasizes more the historical founding event (創立百周年), whereas 建設 is the construction of a building, not the founding of an institution.","examples":[{"japanese":"会社の設立には、たくさんの書類が必要だ。","romaji":"Kaisha no setsuritsu ni wa, takusan no shorui ga hitsuyō da.","german":"Establishing a company requires many documents.","furigana":"かいしゃ|せつりつ|しょるい|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"このボランティア団体は、設立からまだ三年だ。","romaji":"Kono borantia dantai wa, setsuritsu kara mada sannen da.","german":"This volunteer organization has existed for only three years since its founding.","furigana":"だんたい|せつりつ|さんねん"},{"japanese":"新しい研究所の設立が、今日発表された。","romaji":"Atarashii kenkyūjo no setsuritsu ga, kyō happyō sareta.","german":"The establishment of a new research institute was announced today.","furigana":"あたら|けんきゅうじょ|せつりつ|きょうはっぴょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"蓄積 is the gradual accumulation of something over a longer period, often with の: 疲労の蓄積 \"accumulated exhaustion\", 知識の蓄積, データの蓄積. The verb form \"to accumulate\" is on the card 蓄積する. For money, people say 貯金 or 貯蓄 instead; 蓄積 sounds matter-of-fact and suits science, technology and economics.","examples":[{"japanese":"疲労の蓄積が、事故の原因になった。","romaji":"Hirō no chikuseki ga, jiko no gen'in ni natta.","german":"The accumulation of fatigue caused the accident.","furigana":"ひろう|ちくせき|じこ|げんいん"},{"japanese":"長年の経験の蓄積が、彼の一番の強みだ。","romaji":"Naganen no keiken no chikuseki ga, kare no ichiban no tsuyomi da.","german":"His years of accumulated experience are his greatest strength.","furigana":"ながねん|けいけん|ちくせき|かれ|いちばん|つよ"},{"japanese":"このシステムは、データの蓄積と分析を自動で行う。","romaji":"Kono shisutemu wa, dēta no chikuseki to bunseki o jidō de okonau.","german":"This system automatically accumulates and analyzes data.","furigana":"ちくせき|ぶんせき|じどう|おこな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"着目 means to direct one's attention deliberately to a particular point, often as the starting point of an analysis: 着目点 \"point of focus\", 〜への着目. The verb form is on the card 着目する (〜に着目する). Unlike 注目, which also refers to the attention of many people (注目を集める), 着目 is active and analytical and appears mainly in reports and specialist texts.","examples":[{"japanese":"この研究の着目点は、子どもの睡眠時間だ。","romaji":"Kono kenkyū no chakumokuten wa, kodomo no suimin jikan da.","german":"The focus of this research is children's sleep time.","furigana":"けんきゅう|ちゃくもくてん|こ|すいみんじかん"},{"japanese":"若い客層への着目が、店の成功につながった。","romaji":"Wakai kyakusō e no chakumoku ga, mise no seikō ni tsunagatta.","german":"Focusing on young customers led to the shop's success.","furigana":"わか|きゃくそう|ちゃくもく|みせ|せいこう"},{"japanese":"彼の着目点はいつもほかの人と違って面白い。","romaji":"Kare no chakumokuten wa itsumo hoka no hito to chigatte omoshiroi.","german":"His points of focus are always different from other people's and interesting.","furigana":"かれ|ちゃくもくてん|ひと|ちが|おもしろ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"庁 mostly appears as a suffix in names of government agencies: 気象庁 \"Meteorological Agency\", 警察庁, 都庁 and 県庁 \"prefectural government\" (all with their own cards), and also 文化庁. A 庁 is often a subordinate agency, whereas 省 is a ministry; for municipal administration people say 役所 or 役場. On its own, 庁 appears almost only in newspaper language (同庁 \"the said agency\", 庁内). Homophones such as 蝶 \"butterfly\" and 兆 \"trillion\" are different words.","examples":[{"japanese":"気象庁によると、明日は大雨になるそうだ。","romaji":"Kishōchō ni yoru to, ashita wa ōame ni naru sō da.","german":"According to the Meteorological Agency, there will be heavy rain tomorrow.","furigana":"きしょうちょう|あした|おおあめ"},{"japanese":"父は県庁で働いている。","romaji":"Chichi wa kenchō de hataraite iru.","german":"My father works at the prefectural office.","furigana":"ちち|けんちょう|はたら"},{"japanese":"文化庁は、古い建物の保存に力を入れている。","romaji":"Bunkachō wa, furui tatemono no hozon ni chikara o irete iru.","german":"The Agency for Cultural Affairs focuses on preserving old buildings.","furigana":"ぶんかちょう|ふる|たてもの|ほぞん|ちから|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"つつく is almost always written in kana; without context, the kanji 突く is usually read つく (separate card \"to thrust, stab\"). つつく means a light, repeated tapping: 肩をつつく \"to tap on the shoulder\", a bird つつく \"pecks\". Figuratively, 人のミスをつつく means \"to nitpick at mistakes\" and 箸でご飯をつつく \"to poke listlessly at one's food\". Colloquial intensified form: 突っつく.","examples":[{"japanese":"肩をつつかれて振り向くと、友だちが立っていた。","romaji":"Kata o tsutsukarete furimuku to, tomodachi ga tatte ita.","german":"When my shoulder was tapped and I turned around, a friend was standing there.","furigana":"かた|ふ|む|とも|た"},{"japanese":"公園でハトがパンくずをつついている。","romaji":"Kōen de hato ga pankuzu o tsutsuite iru.","german":"Pigeons are pecking at breadcrumbs in the park.","furigana":"こうえん"},{"japanese":"人の小さなミスばかりつつくのはやめなさい。","romaji":"Hito no chiisa na misu bakari tsutsuku no wa yamenasai.","german":"Stop picking at other people's small mistakes.","furigana":"ひと|ちい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"ばち is the punishment from gods or Buddhas for bad behavior and appears almost always in the phrase 罰が当たる \"to receive one's just punishment\", often also written バチが当たる. 罰当たり means \"sacrilegious, impious\". The card 罰/ばつ, by contrast, means a punishment imposed by humans (罰を受ける, 罰ゲーム); in sentences with 当たる the kanji is always read ばち.","examples":[{"japanese":"食べ物を粗末にすると、罰が当たるよ。","romaji":"Tabemono o somatsu ni suru to, bachi ga ataru yo.","german":"If you treat food carelessly, you'll be punished by the gods.","furigana":"た|もの|そまつ|ばち|あ"},{"japanese":"嘘ばかりついていたから、バチが当たったんだ。","romaji":"Uso bakari tsuite ita kara, bachi ga atatta n da.","german":"I told nothing but lies, so I got what I deserved.","furigana":"うそ|あ"},{"japanese":"こんなに幸せだと、罰が当たりそうで怖い。","romaji":"Konna ni shiawase da to, bachi ga atarisō de kowai.","german":"Being this happy, I'm afraid I'll be punished.","furigana":"しあわ|ばち|あ|こわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"匹敵 is practically only used with する: 〜に匹敵する \"to be equal to, to rival\"; the thing compared takes に; there is a separate verb card 匹敵する. Typical are 〜に匹敵するほど \"to such a degree that it equals ...\" and, negated, 〜に匹敵するものはない \"nothing equals ...\". The expression is rather literary; in conversation people often say 〜と同じくらい or 〜に負けない.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼の料理の腕は、プロに匹敵する。","romaji":"Kare no ryōri no ude wa, puro ni hitteki suru.","german":"His cooking skills rival those of professionals.","furigana":"かれ|りょうり|うで|ひってき"},{"japanese":"東京の人口は、小さな国一つに匹敵するほどだ。","romaji":"Tōkyō no jinkō wa, chiisa na kuni hitotsu ni hitteki suru hodo da.","german":"Tokyo's population is comparable to that of a small country.","furigana":"とうきょう|じんこう|ちい|くにひと|ひってき"},{"japanese":"数学で彼女に匹敵する生徒は、このクラスにはいない。","romaji":"Sūgaku de kanojo ni hitteki suru seito wa, kono kurasu ni wa inai.","german":"No student in this class can match her in math.","furigana":"すうがく|かのじょ|ひってき|せいと"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"要する is a literary verb: what is needed takes を (時間を要する, 注意を要する), the purpose often takes に (この作業には〜を要する). In daily life people say 必要とする, いる or, for time and money, かかる instead. The adverb 要するに \"in short\" (separate card) derives from it; 擁する \"to possess, comprise\" sounds the same but is a different word.","examples":[{"japanese":"この作業には、高度な技術を要する。","romaji":"Kono sagyō ni wa, kōdo na gijutsu o yōsuru.","german":"This task requires advanced skills.","furigana":"さぎょう|こうど|ぎじゅつ|よう"},{"japanese":"駅から会場までは、徒歩で約二十分を要します。","romaji":"Eki kara kaijō made wa, toho de yaku nijuppun o yōshimasu.","german":"It takes about twenty minutes on foot from the station to the venue.","furigana":"えき|かいじょう|とほ|やくにじゅっぷん|よう"},{"japanese":"至急を要する案件なので、すぐに対応してください。","romaji":"Shikyū o yōsuru anken na node, sugu ni taiō shite kudasai.","german":"This is a matter requiring urgent attention, so please respond right away.","furigana":"しきゅう|よう|あんけん|たいおう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A native Japanese word for a technique or craftsmanship acquired through practice, especially in martial arts (柔道の技 \"judo technique\", 技をかける \"to apply a technique\") and crafts (職人の技). Set phrase: 技を磨く \"to hone one's skill\". Unlike 技術/ぎじゅつ (\"technique, technology\", more factual-technical), 技 emphasizes personal ability; 特技 is one's own special skill.","examples":[{"japanese":"柔道の試合で、彼はきれいな技を決めた。","romaji":"Juudou no shiai de, kare wa kirei na waza o kimeta.","german":"In the judo match, he executed a beautiful technique.","furigana":"じゅうどう|しあい|かれ|わざ|き"},{"japanese":"職人は何十年もかけて技を磨いてきた。","romaji":"Shokunin wa nanjuunen mo kakete waza o migaite kita.","german":"The craftsman has polished his skill over decades.","furigana":"しょくにん|なんじゅうねん|わざ|みが"},{"japanese":"このスケーターは難しい技を次々に見せた。","romaji":"Kono sukeetaa wa muzukashii waza o tsugitsugi ni miseta.","german":"This skater showed off one difficult trick after another.","furigana":"むずか|わざ|つぎつぎ|み"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A native Japanese prefix before nouns: \"small\" (小鳥 \"little bird\", 小雨 \"drizzle\", 小声 \"low voice\"), \"just under, about\" before quantities (小一時間 \"nearly an hour\") and derogatory \"insignificant, petty\" (小役人 \"petty official\"). Before adjectives it intensifies slightly (小ぎれい \"neat and pretty\"). The on reading しょう is used in Sino-Japanese words such as 小学校; the independent adjective is 小さい.","examples":[{"japanese":"朝から小雨が降り続いている。","romaji":"Asa kara kosame ga furitsuzuite iru.","german":"A light rain has kept falling since morning.","furigana":"あさ||ふ|つづ"},{"japanese":"図書館では小声で話してください。","romaji":"Toshokan de wa kogoe de hanashite kudasai.","german":"Please speak in a low voice in the library.","furigana":"としょかん|こごえ|はな"},{"japanese":"駅で小一時間待たされた。","romaji":"Eki de ko-ichijikan matasareta.","german":"I was made to wait nearly an hour at the station.","furigana":"えき|こいちじかんま"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"The standard counter for birds, traditionally also for rabbits: 一羽（いちわ）, 二羽（にわ）, but 三羽（さんば）, 六羽（ろっぱ）, 十羽（じゅっぱ） with sound changes. Do not confuse with the card 羽/はね \"feather, wing\" - same spelling, different reading and meaning. For other animals one uses 〜匹 or 〜頭.","examples":[{"japanese":"庭にすずめが二羽来ています。","romaji":"Niwa ni suzume ga niwa kite imasu.","german":"Two sparrows have come to the garden.","furigana":"にわ|にわき"},{"japanese":"うちではうさぎを一羽飼っています。","romaji":"Uchi de wa usagi o ichiwa katte imasu.","german":"We keep one rabbit at our house.","furigana":"いちわか"},{"japanese":"池にあひるが六羽泳いでいた。","romaji":"Ike ni ahiru ga roppa oyoide ita.","german":"Six ducks were swimming in the pond.","furigana":"いけ|ろっぱおよ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"The noun form of the card 圧倒する; the noun alone is rare and mostly appears as 圧倒される \"to be overwhelmed\" (by impressions, strength) or as the adjective 圧倒的な/圧倒的に \"overwhelming, by a landslide\". Typical: 迫力に圧倒される, 圧倒的な勝利, 圧倒的多数.","examples":[{"japanese":"相手チームの迫力に圧倒されて、うまくプレーできなかった。","romaji":"Aite chīmu no hakuryoku ni attō sarete, umaku purē dekinakatta.","german":"I was overwhelmed by the opposing team's intensity and couldn't play well.","furigana":"あいて|はくりょく|あっとう"},{"japanese":"選挙は与党の圧倒的な勝利に終わった。","romaji":"Senkyo wa yotō no attōteki na shōri ni owatta.","german":"The election ended in an overwhelming victory for the ruling party.","furigana":"せんきょ|よとう|あっとうてき|しょうり|お"},{"japanese":"初めて間近で見た富士山の大きさに圧倒された。","romaji":"Hajimete majika de mita Fujisan no ōkisa ni attō sareta.","german":"I was overwhelmed by the size of Mt. Fuji, seen up close for the first time.","furigana":"はじ|まじか|み|ふじさん|おお|あっとう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A prefix placed before a number that turns it into an ordinal: 第一回, 第二次世界大戦, 第三章. Rather literary and formal (chapters, meetings, wars, articles of law); in daily life people often use the suffixed 〜番目 or 〜回目 instead. The card 第一 (だいいち) teaches the independent word \"first of all, above all\"; 台, 代, 題 and 大 are homophones, but different words.","examples":[{"japanese":"第二次世界大戦は千九百四十五年に終わった。","romaji":"Dai-niji sekai taisen wa sen kyūhyaku yonjūgo-nen ni owatta.","german":"World War II ended in 1945.","furigana":"だいにじせかいたいせん|せんきゅうひゃくよんじゅうごねん|お"},{"japanese":"授業の前に、教科書の第三章を読んでおいてください。","romaji":"Jugyō no mae ni, kyōkasho no dai-sanshō o yonde oite kudasai.","german":"Please read Chapter 3 of the textbook before class.","furigana":"じゅぎょう|まえ|きょうかしょ|だいさんしょう|よ"},{"japanese":"第十回の大会は大阪で開かれる。","romaji":"Dai-jukkai no taikai wa Ōsaka de hirakareru.","german":"The 10th tournament will be held in Osaka.","furigana":"だいじゅっかい|たいかい|おおさか|ひら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"〜ぶり has two main meanings: after verb stems and nouns \"manner, way\" (話しぶり, 仕事ぶり, 暮らしぶり), and after time expressions \"for the first time in ...\" (三年ぶり, 久しぶり - its own card). With に it becomes adverbial: 五年ぶりに会った. It is almost always written in kana; the kanji form 振り is rare and easily confused with ふり \"pretending\".","examples":[{"japanese":"十年ぶりに故郷に帰った。","romaji":"Jūnen-buri ni kokyō ni kaetta.","german":"I returned to my hometown for the first time in ten years.","furigana":"じゅうねん|こきょう|かえ"},{"japanese":"彼の落ち着いた話しぶりに、皆が安心した。","romaji":"Kare no ochitsuita hanashiburi ni, mina ga anshin shita.","german":"His calm way of speaking reassured everyone.","furigana":"かれ|お|つ|はな|みな|あんしん"},{"japanese":"部長は新入社員の仕事ぶりを褒めていた。","romaji":"Buchō wa shinnyū shain no shigotoburi o homete ita.","german":"The department head was praising the new employee's work style.","furigana":"ぶちょう|しんにゅうしゃいん|しごと|ほ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Polite form of the verbal noun 待ち from 待つ. Most often in fixed patterns: お待ちください (request), お待ちしております (humble), お待ちになる (honorific), plus お待ちの間 \"while you wait\". To be distinguished from the thank-you formula お待ちどおさま (its own card). Almost always written in kana with お; 御待ち is rare.","examples":[{"japanese":"担当者が参りますので、少々お待ちください。","romaji":"Tantōsha ga mairimasu node, shōshō omachi kudasai.","german":"The person in charge will come shortly, so please wait a moment.","furigana":"たんとうしゃ|まい|しょうしょう|ま"},{"japanese":"お待ちの間、こちらのパンフレットをご覧ください。","romaji":"Omachi no aida, kochira no panfuretto o goran kudasai.","german":"While you wait, please take a look at this brochure.","furigana":"ま|あいだ|らん"},{"japanese":"皆様のご来店を心よりお待ちしております。","romaji":"Minasama no goraiten o kokoro yori omachi shite orimasu.","german":"We sincerely look forward to your visit.","furigana":"みなさま|らいてん|こころ|ま"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"A plural suffix for people, almost always written in kana (彼ら, 僕ら, 子供ら); 等 as a kanji looks old-fashioned. Compared with 〜たち, 〜ら sounds more familiar and can seem condescending toward others (お前ら), or modest for one's own group (私ら). Do not confuse with the same spelling 〜等 in the reading とう \"etc.; rank\" (its own card).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼らは毎朝一緒に駅まで歩いている。","romaji":"Karera wa maiasa issho ni eki made aruite iru.","german":"They walk to the station together every morning.","furigana":"かれ|まいあさいっしょ|えき|ある"},{"japanese":"僕らが子供の頃は、スマホなんてなかった。","romaji":"Bokura ga kodomo no koro wa, sumaho nante nakatta.","german":"When we were kids, there was no such thing as smartphones.","furigana":"ぼく|こども|ころ"},{"japanese":"公園では近所の子供らが元気に遊んでいた。","romaji":"Kōen de wa kinjo no kodomora ga genki ni asonde ita.","german":"In the park, the neighborhood children were playing energetically.","furigana":"こうえん|きんじょ|こども|げんき|あそ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Native Japanese reading of 日 in dates and day counts with native numerals: ふつか, みっか, よっか up to とおか as well as じゅうよっか, はつか (20) and にじゅうよっか. All other numbers take 〜日/にち (its own card), e.g. じゅういちにち; the first is ついたち, \"one day\" is いちにち. The same form denotes the day of the month and the duration in days.","examples":[{"japanese":"詳しいことは三日後にまた連絡します。","romaji":"Kuwashii koto wa mikka-go ni mata renraku shimasu.","german":"I'll contact you again in three days regarding the details.","furigana":"くわ|みっかご|れんらく"},{"japanese":"五月五日はこどもの日で、学校が休みになる。","romaji":"Gogatsu itsuka wa kodomo no hi de, gakkō ga yasumi ni naru.","german":"May 5 is Children's Day, and schools are closed.","furigana":"ごがついつか|ひ|がっこう|やす"},{"japanese":"この作業なら十日もあれば終わるだろう。","romaji":"Kono sagyō nara tōka mo areba owaru darō.","german":"This work should be finished in ten days.","furigana":"さぎょう|とおか|お"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"A colloquial particle that singles something out while disparaging or downplaying it (勉強なんて嫌い), expresses surprise or indignation after sentences (…なんて信じられない) and can mean \"someone named\" (田中なんて人). Compared with なんか (separate card), なんて is a bit more emphatic and also follows whole sentences. Do not confuse it with the exclamation 何て \"how ...!\" (なんてきれいなんだ).","examples":[{"japanese":"数学なんて、もう二度と勉強したくない。","romaji":"Sūgaku nante, mō nido to benkyō shitaku nai.","german":"I never want to study math again.","furigana":"すうがく|にど|べんきょう"},{"japanese":"こんな所で君に会うなんて、思ってもみなかった。","romaji":"Konna tokoro de kimi ni au nante, omotte mo minakatta.","german":"I never imagined I'd run into you in a place like this.","furigana":"ところ|きみ|あ|おも"},{"japanese":"田中なんて人は、うちの部署にはいませんよ。","romaji":"Tanaka nante hito wa, uchi no busho ni wa imasen yo.","german":"There's no one called Tanaka in our department.","furigana":"たなか|ひと|ぶしょ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Polite form of 世話 \"care, looking after\" (its own card), friendlier in tone and typical when it comes to children, animals or guests: 猫のお世話をする. It is the core of fixed expressions such as お世話になります / お世話になりました (its own card お世話になる) and 余計なお世話 or 大きなお世話 \"mind your own business\" (its own card). The usual spelling is お世話; 御世話 is rare.","examples":[{"japanese":"旅行の間、猫のお世話をお願いできますか。","romaji":"Ryokō no aida, neko no osewa o onegai dekimasu ka.","german":"Could you take care of the cat while I'm traveling?","furigana":"りょこう|あいだ|ねこ|せわ|ねが"},{"japanese":"皆さまには大変お世話になりました。","romaji":"Minasama ni wa taihen osewa ni narimashita.","german":"Thank you all very much for your help.","furigana":"みな|たいへん|せわ"},{"japanese":"「早く結婚したら？」「余計なお世話だよ。」","romaji":"\"Hayaku kekkon shitara?\" \"Yokei na osewa da yo.\"","german":"\"Why don't you get married soon?\" \"Mind your own business.\"","furigana":"はや|けっこん|よけい|せわ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"A very productive suffix attached to Sino-Japanese words to denote persons: 研究者 \"researcher\", 参加者 \"participant\", 責任者 \"person in charge\"; it is also found in basic words such as 医者 and 学者. Read on its own, 者 is もの (its own card 者/もの, e.g. 田中という者です). For nationality or occupation, 〜人 or 〜家 (日本人, 作家) is often used instead of 〜者.","examples":[{"japanese":"会議の参加者は全部で二十人です。","romaji":"Kaigi no sankasha wa zenbu de nijūnin desu.","german":"There are twenty participants in the meeting in total.","furigana":"かいぎ|さんかしゃ|ぜんぶ|にじゅうにん"},{"japanese":"責任者と話をさせてください。","romaji":"Sekininsha to hanashi o sasete kudasai.","german":"Please let me speak with the person in charge.","furigana":"せきにんしゃ|はなし"},{"japanese":"姉は大学で研究者として働いている。","romaji":"Ane wa daigaku de kenkyūsha to shite hataraite iru.","german":"My older sister works as a researcher at a university.","furigana":"あね|だいがく|けんきゅうしゃ|はたら"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Mostly in kana, especially in 〜ふりをする \"to pretend to\" after a verb form or noun + の: 寝たふり, 知らないふり, 学生のふり; also 見て見ぬふり \"to deliberately look away\". In kanji, 振り also stands for \"swing, swinging\" (バットの振り) and dance movements. Do not confuse with the suffix 〜ぶり \"manner; after a long time\" and with 不利/ふり \"disadvantageous\" (their own cards).","examples":[{"japanese":"弟は母が来ると寝たふりをした。","romaji":"Otōto wa haha ga kuru to neta furi o shita.","german":"When Mother came, my little brother pretended to be asleep.","furigana":"おとうと|はは|く|ね"},{"japanese":"彼女は何も知らないふりをしている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa nani mo shiranai furi o shite iru.","german":"She is pretending not to know anything.","furigana":"かのじょ|なに|し"},{"japanese":"あのバッターはバットの振りが鋭い。","romaji":"Ano battā wa batto no furi ga surudoi.","german":"That batter has a sharp swing of the bat.","furigana":"ふ|するど"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"An exclamation with which one spurs oneself or others on (\"let's go!\"), ticks something off as done, or gives someone approval (\"good!\"). Stretched as よーし or ようし it sounds more determined. Casual; not really used toward superiors. Unlike さあ, which urges others to begin, よし mainly expresses one's own resolve. Do not confuse with the classical adjective 良し (良しとする \"to approve\").","examples":[{"japanese":"よし、これで準備はできた。","romaji":"Yoshi, kore de junbi wa dekita.","german":"Good, that's everything prepared.","furigana":"じゅんび"},{"japanese":"「できました！」「よし、よくやった。」","romaji":"\"Dekimashita!\" \"Yoshi, yoku yatta.\"","german":"\"I'm done!\" \"Good, well done.\""},{"japanese":"ようし、今日こそ十キロ走るぞ。","romaji":"Yōshi, kyō koso jukkiro hashiru zo.","german":"All right, today I'm finally going to run ten kilometers!","furigana":"きょう|じゅっ|はし"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい政権が発足した。","romaji":"Atarashii seiken ga hossoku shita.","german":"A new government has taken office.","furigana":"あたら|せいけん|ほっそく"},{"japanese":"選挙の結果、十年ぶりに政権交代が起きた。","romaji":"Senkyo no kekka, jūnen buri ni seiken kōtai ga okita.","german":"As a result of the election, there was a change of government for the first time in ten years.","furigana":"せんきょ|けっか|じゅうねん|せいけんこうたい|お"},{"japanese":"その党は長い間政権を握っていた。","romaji":"Sono tō wa nagai aida seiken o nigitte ita.","german":"That party held power for a long time.","furigana":"とう|なが|あいだせいけん|にぎ"}],"notes":"政権 is governmental power or the sitting government as a political entity, often named after the head of government: 〇〇政権 (\"the 〇〇 administration\"). Typical collocations: 政権を握る (\"to come to power\"), 政権交代 (\"change of government\"), 長期政権, 新政権が発足する. 政府, by contrast, refers to the government as a state institution; people say 政府の発表, but 政権が倒れる. Typical news vocabulary.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は新しい政党を結成した。","romaji":"Kare wa atarashii seitō o kessei shita.","german":"He formed a new political party.","furigana":"かれ|あたら|せいとう|けっせい"},{"japanese":"あなたが支持している政党はどこですか。","romaji":"Anata ga shiji shite iru seitō wa doko desu ka.","german":"Which political party do you support?","furigana":"しじ|せいとう"},{"japanese":"彼女はどの政党にも所属していない。","romaji":"Kanojo wa dono seitō ni mo shozoku shite inai.","german":"She doesn't belong to any political party.","furigana":"かのじょ|せいとう|しょぞく"}],"notes":"政党 is the general term for a political party. Specific party names usually end in 党 (自民党, 共産党), and in the news often just 党 is said. Typical: 政党を結成する (\"to found\"), 政党に所属する (\"to belong to\"), 支持する政党 (\"the party one supports\"), 政党政治. Do not confuse it with 正当 (\"justified\") or 正統, which sound the same.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"野党は法案に反対した。","romaji":"Yatō wa hōan ni hantai shita.","german":"The opposition parties opposed the bill.","furigana":"やとう|ほうあん|はんたい"},{"japanese":"野党は首相の発言を厳しく追及した。","romaji":"Yatō wa shushō no hatsugen o kibishiku tsuikyū shita.","german":"The opposition parties sharply questioned the prime minister's remarks.","furigana":"やとう|しゅしょう|はつげん|きび|ついきゅう"},{"japanese":"野党が協力しなければ、政権交代は難しいだろう。","romaji":"Yatō ga kyōryoku shinakereba, seiken kōtai wa muzukashii darō.","german":"Unless the opposition parties cooperate, a change of government will be difficult.","furigana":"やとう|きょうりょく|せいけんこうたい|むずか"}],"notes":"野党 are the parties that do not take part in the government, i.e. the opposition; the counterpart is 与党. The 野 here stands for \"outside of office\", as in 下野する (\"to lose power\"). Typical contexts: 野党が政府を追及する, 野党第一党 (\"largest opposition party\"), 野党が反対する. 野党 can mean either a single party or the opposition as a whole.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"与党が選挙で勝利した。","romaji":"Yotō ga senkyo de shōri shita.","german":"The ruling party won the election.","furigana":"よとう|せんきょ|しょうり"},{"japanese":"与党は国会で過半数の議席を持っている。","romaji":"Yotō wa kokkai de kahansū no giseki o motte iru.","german":"The ruling party holds a majority of seats in the Diet.","furigana":"よとう|こっかい|かはんすう|ぎせき|も"},{"japanese":"この問題では与党の中でも意見が分かれている。","romaji":"Kono mondai de wa yotō no naka de mo iken ga wakarete iru.","german":"On this issue, opinions are divided even within the ruling party.","furigana":"もんだい|よとう|なか|いけん|わ"}],"notes":"与党 is the ruling party or the parties of a governing coalition; the opposite is 野党. In the news both often appear as a pair: 与野党 (\"government and opposition\"). Typical: 与党が過半数を占める, 与党が議席を減らす, 連立与党 (\"coalition parties\"). 与 here means \"to take part in\" as in 関与.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"投票率が低かった。","romaji":"Tōhyōritsu ga hikukatta.","german":"The voter turnout was low.","furigana":"とうひょうりつ|ひく"},{"japanese":"日曜日は雨だったけど、ちゃんと投票に行ったよ。","romaji":"Nichiyōbi wa ame datta kedo, chanto tōhyō ni itta yo.","german":"It rained on Sunday, but I went to vote anyway.","furigana":"にちようび|あめ|とうひょう|い"},{"japanese":"文化祭のテーマはクラス全員の投票で決めた。","romaji":"Bunkasai no tēma wa kurasu zen'in no tōhyō de kimeta.","german":"We decided the theme for the school festival by a vote of the whole class.","furigana":"ぶんかさい|ぜんいん|とうひょう|き"}],"notes":"投票 is the casting of a vote, i.e. the concrete act of voting with a ballot or a show of hands; 選挙, by contrast, is the election as a whole event. As an intransitive する-verb, the candidate or party takes に: 〇〇に投票する (\"to vote for 〇〇\"). Common: 投票に行く, 投票率 (\"voter turnout\"), 投票用紙, 期日前投票 (\"early voting\"). Also used outside politics, e.g. for votes in clubs or online polls.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"議会で新しい法案が審議された。","romaji":"Gikai de atarashii hōan ga shingi sareta.","german":"A new bill was deliberated in the assembly.","furigana":"ぎかい|あたら|ほうあん|しんぎ"},{"japanese":"市議会で駅前の再開発計画が承認された。","romaji":"Shigikai de ekimae no saikaihatsu keikaku ga shōnin sareta.","german":"The city council approved the redevelopment plan for the area in front of the station.","furigana":"しぎかい|えきまえ|さいかいはつけいかく|しょうにん"},{"japanese":"アメリカの議会は二つの院から成っている。","romaji":"Amerika no gikai wa futatsu no in kara natte iru.","german":"The US Congress consists of two chambers.","furigana":"ぎかい|ふた|いん|な"}],"notes":"議会 is the general word for an elected legislative assembly, from a city council to a country's parliament: 市議会, 県議会, イギリス議会. Japan's national parliament, however, is almost always called 国会. Typical collocations: 議会で審議する, 議会を解散する, 議会制民主主義. Its members are called 議員.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"国会で予算案が可決された。","romaji":"Kokkai de yosan'an ga kaketsu sareta.","german":"The budget proposal was passed in the Diet.","furigana":"こっかい|よさんあん|かけつ"},{"japanese":"社会科見学で国会議事堂を見に行った。","romaji":"Shakaika kengaku de kokkai gijidō o mi ni itta.","german":"On a social studies field trip, we went to see the National Diet Building.","furigana":"しゃかいかけんがく|こっかいぎじどう|み|い"},{"japanese":"首相は国会で野党の質問に答えた。","romaji":"Shushō wa kokkai de yatō no shitsumon ni kotaeta.","german":"The prime minister answered the opposition's questions in the Diet.","furigana":"しゅしょう|こっかい|やとう|しつもん|こた"}],"notes":"国会 is above all the name of the Japanese parliament with the chambers 衆議院 and 参議院. Typical: 国会で審議する, 国会を召集する, 国会議員, 国会議事堂. For the parliaments of other countries one usually says 議会, though 国会 is also possible. The component 国 makes clear that it is the national level, in contrast to 市議会 or 県議会.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その法案は国会で否決された。","romaji":"Sono hōan wa kokkai de hiketsu sareta.","german":"The bill was voted down in the Diet.","furigana":"ほうあん|こっかい|ひけつ"},{"japanese":"政府は来月、新しい法案を提出する予定だ。","romaji":"Seifu wa raigetsu, atarashii hōan o teishutsu suru yotei da.","german":"The government plans to submit a new bill next month.","furigana":"せいふ|らいげつ|あたら|ほうあん|ていしゅつ|よてい"},{"japanese":"反対の声が多く、法案の成立は難しそうだ。","romaji":"Hantai no koe ga ōku, hōan no seiritsu wa muzukashisō da.","german":"With so many voices against it, passage of the bill looks difficult.","furigana":"はんたい|こえ|おお|ほうあん|せいりつ|むずか"}],"notes":"法案 is a bill that has not yet been passed; after adoption it becomes a 法律. The path of a bill can be described with set verbs: 法案を提出する (\"to introduce\"), 審議する (\"to deliberate\"), 可決する (\"to pass\") or 否決する (\"to reject\"), and finally 法案が成立する (\"to be enacted\"). For the budget, people say 予算案 in the same way.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"政府は新しい経済政策を発表した。","romaji":"Seifu wa atarashii keizai seisaku o happyō shita.","german":"Die Regierung kündigte eine neue Wirtschaftspolitik an.","furigana":"せいふ|あたら|けいざいせいさく|はっぴょう"},{"japanese":"選挙の前に、各党の政策をよく比べてみよう。","romaji":"Senkyo no mae ni, kakutō no seisaku o yoku kurabete miyō.","german":"Vor der Wahl sollten wir die Programme der einzelnen Parteien genau vergleichen.","furigana":"せんきょ|まえ|かくとう|せいさく|くら"},{"japanese":"その政策は若い世代にはあまり効果がなかった。","romaji":"Sono seisaku wa wakai sedai ni wa amari kōka ga nakatta.","german":"Diese Maßnahme hat bei der jüngeren Generation kaum Wirkung gezeigt.","furigana":"せいさく|わか|せだい|こうか"}],"notes":"政策 sind konkrete politische Maßnahmen oder Programme, im Deutschen meist „-politik“: 経済政策 („Wirtschaftspolitik“), 外交政策, 少子化対策. Typische Verben: 政策を打ち出す („vorlegen“), 実施する, 見直す. Nicht verwechseln mit 政治, das „Politik“ als Tätigkeit oder Bereich allgemein meint (政治に興味がある), und mit dem gleich klingenden 制作 („Produktion“).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい外務大臣が任命された。","romaji":"Atarashii gaimu daijin ga ninmei sareta.","german":"A new foreign minister was appointed.","furigana":"あたら|がいむだいじん|にんめい"},{"japanese":"失言の責任をとって、大臣が辞任した。","romaji":"Shitsugen no sekinin o totte, daijin ga jinin shita.","german":"The minister resigned, taking responsibility for his gaffe.","furigana":"しつげん|せきにん|だいじん|じにん"},{"japanese":"財務大臣は来年度の予算について説明した。","romaji":"Zaimu daijin wa rainendo no yosan ni tsuite setsumei shita.","german":"The finance minister explained the budget for the next fiscal year.","furigana":"ざいむだいじん|らいねんど|よさん|せつめい"}],"notes":"大臣 is a minister of cabinet rank; the portfolio comes before it: 外務大臣, 財務大臣, 文部科学大臣. The prime minister is officially called 内閣総理大臣. In headlines the short form with 相 is often used: 外相, 財務相. Typical: 大臣に就任する (\"to take up the ministerial post\"), 大臣を辞任する. As a form of address one says 〇〇大臣.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"首相が記者会見を開いた。","romaji":"Shushō ga kisha kaiken o hiraita.","german":"Der Premierminister hielt eine Pressekonferenz ab.","furigana":"しゅしょう|きしゃかいけん|ひら"},{"japanese":"ドイツの首相が初めて日本を訪問した。","romaji":"Doitsu no shushō ga hajimete Nihon o hōmon shita.","german":"Der deutsche Bundeskanzler besuchte zum ersten Mal Japan.","furigana":"しゅしょう|はじ|にほん|ほうもん"},{"japanese":"彼は四十代の若さで首相に就任した。","romaji":"Kare wa yonjūdai no wakasa de shushō ni shūnin shita.","german":"Er wurde schon mit Anfang vierzig Premierminister.","furigana":"かれ|よんじゅうだい|わか|しゅしょう|しゅうにん"}],"notes":"首相 ist der Regierungschef; in Japan entspricht es dem offiziellen Titel 内閣総理大臣, im Gespräch sagt man oft 総理. Nachrichten verwenden 首相 auch für ausländische Regierungschefs, etwa ドイツの首相 („Bundeskanzler“) oder イギリスの首相. Typisch: 首相に就任する, 首相官邸 („Amtssitz“), 首相が辞任する. Den Staatspräsidenten nennt man dagegen 大統領.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 21 (source: N5; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は国会議員に当選した。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kokkai giin ni tōsen shita.","german":"She was elected as a member of the Diet.","furigana":"かのじょ|こっかいぎいん|とうせん"},{"japanese":"地元の市議会議員に道路の問題を相談した。","romaji":"Jimoto no shigikai giin ni dōro no mondai o sōdan shita.","german":"I consulted our local city council member about the road problem.","furigana":"じもと|しぎかいぎいん|どうろ|もんだい|そうだん"},{"japanese":"その議員は不祥事の責任をとって辞職した。","romaji":"Sono giin wa fushōji no sekinin o totte jishoku shita.","german":"The legislator resigned, taking responsibility for the scandal.","furigana":"ぎいん|ふしょうじ|せきにん|じしょく"}],"notes":"議員 is an elected member of a representative body, at all levels: 国会議員, 衆議院議員, 市議会議員. Typical: 議員に当選する (\"to be elected\"), 議員を辞職する, 議員バッジ. As a form of address or title it follows the name: 山田議員. For members of a club or committee, 会員 or 委員 is used instead.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"閣僚会議が開かれた。","romaji":"Kakuryō kaigi ga hirakareta.","german":"A cabinet meeting was held.","furigana":"かくりょうかいぎ|ひら"},{"japanese":"新しい内閣では、女性の閣僚が五人に増えた。","romaji":"Atarashii naikaku de wa, josei no kakuryō ga gonin ni fueta.","german":"In the new cabinet, the number of female ministers has increased to five.","furigana":"あたら|ないかく|じょせい|かくりょう|ごにん|ふ"},{"japanese":"首相は若手議員を閣僚に起用した。","romaji":"Shushō wa wakate giin o kakuryō ni kiyō shita.","german":"The prime minister appointed a young lawmaker as a cabinet minister.","furigana":"しゅしょう|わかてぎいん|かくりょう|きよう"}],"notes":"閣僚 denotes the members of a cabinet (内閣), i.e. the ministers including the head of government, often as a group. It is a formal news word; for a single minister people more often say 大臣 in everyday speech. Typical: 閣僚に起用する (\"to appoint to the cabinet\"), 閣僚会議, 閣僚の顔ぶれ (\"composition of the cabinet\"), 女性閣僚.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"内閣が総辞職した。","romaji":"Naikaku ga sōjishoku shita.","german":"The cabinet resigned en masse.","furigana":"ないかく|そうじしょく"},{"japanese":"最新の調査で、内閣の支持率が三十パーセントを下回った。","romaji":"Saishin no chōsa de, naikaku no shijiritsu ga sanjuppāsento o shitamawatta.","german":"According to the latest survey, the cabinet's approval rating fell below 30 percent.","furigana":"さいしん|ちょうさ|ないかく|しじりつ|さんじゅっ|したまわ"},{"japanese":"首相は秋に内閣改造を行うと発表した。","romaji":"Shushō wa aki ni naikaku kaizō o okonau to happyō shita.","german":"The prime minister announced a cabinet reshuffle in the fall.","furigana":"しゅしょう|あき|ないかくかいぞう|おこな|はっぴょう"}],"notes":"内閣 is the cabinet, i.e. the prime minister with his ministers as a government body; often named after the head of government (〇〇内閣). Typical: 内閣を組織する (= 組閣する), 内閣改造 (\"cabinet reshuffle\"), 内閣支持率 (\"approval rating\"), 内閣が総辞職する. Unlike 政権 (\"governmental power\"), 内閣 refers to the concrete body.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"行政の効率化が求められている。","romaji":"Gyōsei no kōritsuka ga motomerarete iru.","german":"Greater efficiency in administration is being called for.","furigana":"ぎょうせい|こうりつか|もと"},{"japanese":"災害のとき、行政の対応が遅いと批判された。","romaji":"Saigai no toki, gyōsei no taiō ga osoi to hihan sareta.","german":"The administration was criticized for responding slowly during the disaster.","furigana":"さいがい|ぎょうせい|たいおう|おそ|ひはん"},{"japanese":"国の権力は立法、行政、司法の三つに分かれている。","romaji":"Kuni no kenryoku wa rippō, gyōsei, shihō no mittsu ni wakarete iru.","german":"The nation's power is divided into three: legislative, executive and judicial.","furigana":"くに|けんりょく|りっぽう|ぎょうせい|しほう|みっ|わ"}],"notes":"行政 is the executive power of the state and public administration as a whole. In the separation of powers (三権分立) it stands beside 立法 (\"legislation\") and 司法 (\"judiciary\"). In daily use it often means \"the authorities\": 行政の対応, 行政サービス, 地方行政, 行政機関. For the office of an individual company, people do not say 行政 but 管理 or 事務.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"官僚が政策の立案に関わっている。","romaji":"Kanryō ga seisaku no ritsuan ni kakawatte iru.","german":"Bureaucrats are involved in drafting policy.","furigana":"かんりょう|せいさく|りつあん|かか"},{"japanese":"彼は大学を出てから二十年間、外務省の官僚として働いた。","romaji":"Kare wa daigaku o dete kara nijūnenkan, gaimushō no kanryō to shite hataraita.","german":"For twenty years after graduating from university, he worked as an official at the Ministry of Foreign Affairs.","furigana":"かれ|だいがく|で|にじゅうねんかん|がいむしょう|かんりょう|はたら"},{"japanese":"あの役所の対応はいかにも官僚的だ。","romaji":"Ano yakusho no taiō wa ikanimo kanryōteki da.","german":"That government office's handling is thoroughly bureaucratic.","furigana":"やくしょ|たいおう|かんりょうてき"}],"notes":"官僚 are the senior ministry officials who prepare and implement policy in the ministries, not every civil servant. The general term for state employees is 公務員. The word often has a critical undertone, especially in 官僚的 (\"bureaucratic, stiff\") and 官僚主義. Typical: 財務省の官僚, エリート官僚, 官僚出身の政治家.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"政治家は国民の声を聞くべきだ。","romaji":"Seijika wa kokumin no koe o kiku beki da.","german":"Politicians should listen to the voice of the people.","furigana":"せいじか|こくみん|こえ|き"},{"japanese":"子どもの頃から政治家になるのが夢だった。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro kara seijika ni naru no ga yume datta.","german":"Becoming a politician has been my dream since I was a child.","furigana":"こ|ころ|せいじか|ゆめ"},{"japanese":"またある政治家の失言がニュースになっている。","romaji":"Mata aru seijika no shitsugen ga nyūsu ni natte iru.","german":"Another politician's gaffe is in the news.","furigana":"せいじか|しつげん"}],"notes":"政治家 is the general word for a politician, whether an elected member of parliament or a minister. The component 家 denotes, as in 作家 or 画家, a person with an occupation or role. Typical: 政治家になる, 政治家を目指す, 若手政治家, 政治家の失言. A specific elected member of a parliament is, more precisely, 議員; derogatory is 政治屋.","levelNote":"Compound: 政治 (N5) + 家 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"国民の支持を得ることが重要だ。","romaji":"Kokumin no shiji o eru koto ga jūyō da.","german":"It is important to gain the people's support.","furigana":"こくみん|しじ|え|じゅうよう"},{"japanese":"私はその意見を支持します。","romaji":"Watashi wa sono iken o shiji shimasu.","german":"I support that opinion.","furigana":"わたし|いけん|しじ"},{"japanese":"新しい市長の支持率は六割を超えている。","romaji":"Atarashii shichō no shijiritsu wa rokuwari o koete iru.","german":"The new mayor's approval rating exceeds 60 percent.","furigana":"あたら|しちょう|しじりつ|ろくわり|こ"}],"notes":"支持 mainly means to support or back a person, party or opinion; as a transitive する-verb with を: 〇〇党を支持する. Common are 支持を得る, 支持を集める, 支持率 (\"approval rating\") and 支持者 (\"supporter\"). In technical texts 支持 also means \"to prop up, bear\"; in daily life people say 支える for that. Do not confuse it with the similar-sounding 指示 (\"instruction\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"世論調査の結果が発表された。","romaji":"Seron chōsa no kekka ga happyō sareta.","german":"The results of the opinion poll were announced.","furigana":"せろんちょうさ|けっか|はっぴょう"},{"japanese":"世論の強い反対を受けて、政府は計画を見直した。","romaji":"Seron no tsuyoi hantai o ukete, seifu wa keikaku o minaoshita.","german":"In response to strong public opposition, the government reviewed the plan.","furigana":"せろん|つよ|はんたい|う|せいふ|けいかく|みなお"},{"japanese":"一人の若者のSNSへの投稿が世論を動かした。","romaji":"Hitori no wakamono no esu-enu-esu e no tōkō ga seron o ugokashita.","german":"One young person's social media post moved public opinion.","furigana":"ひとり|わかもの|とうこう|せろん|うご"}],"notes":"世論 is public opinion, i.e. what the population as a whole thinks. There are two readings: せろん and よろん; on television and in daily life よろん is in fact more common today. Originally よろん was written 輿論 (\"well-founded public view\") and せろん 世論 (\"popular sentiment\"), but the distinction has disappeared. Typical: 世論調査 (\"opinion poll\"), 世論の反発, 世論を動かす.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"国民主権は民主主義の基本だ。","romaji":"Kokumin shuken wa minshushugi no kihon da.","german":"Popular sovereignty is the foundation of democracy.","furigana":"こくみんしゅけん|みんしゅしゅぎ|きほん"},{"japanese":"政府は、他国の行動を主権の侵害だと強く抗議した。","romaji":"Seifu wa, takoku no kōdō o shuken no shingai da to tsuyoku kōgi shita.","german":"The government strongly protested that the other country's actions were a violation of sovereignty.","furigana":"せいふ|たこく|こうどう|しゅけん|しんがい|つよ|こうぎ"},{"japanese":"日本は1952年に主権を回復した。","romaji":"Nihon wa sen kyūhyaku gojūni-nen ni shuken o kaifuku shita.","german":"Japan regained its sovereignty in 1952.","furigana":"にほん|ねん|しゅけん|かいふく"}],"notes":"主権 is supreme state authority, i.e. the sovereignty of a state. Important combinations: 国民主権 (\"popular sovereignty\", a basic principle of the Japanese constitution), 主権国家 (\"sovereign state\"), 主権を侵害する (\"to violate sovereignty\"), 主権を回復する. The word belongs to political and legal written language. Do not confuse it with 主導権 (\"leadership role, initiative\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"民主主義を守ることが大切だ。","romaji":"Minshushugi o mamoru koto ga taisetsu da.","german":"Es ist wichtig, die Demokratie zu schützen.","furigana":"みんしゅしゅぎ|まも|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"選挙で代表を選ぶことは、民主主義の基本です。","romaji":"Senkyo de daihyō o erabu koto wa, minshushugi no kihon desu.","german":"Vertreter in Wahlen zu bestimmen, ist ein Grundprinzip der Demokratie.","furigana":"せんきょ|だいひょう|えら|みんしゅしゅぎ|きほん"},{"japanese":"その国は長い独裁の後、民主主義の国に変わった。","romaji":"Sono kuni wa nagai dokusai no ato, minshushugi no kuni ni kawatta.","german":"Nach langer Diktatur wurde das Land zu einer Demokratie.","furigana":"くに|なが|どくさい|あと|みんしゅしゅぎ|くに|か"}],"notes":"民主主義 ist die Demokratie als Prinzip oder Staatsform; die Endung 主義 entspricht dem deutschen „-ismus“. Das Adjektiv ist 民主的な („demokratisch“), z. B. 民主的な方法で決める. Typisch: 民主主義国家, 民主主義を守る, 議会制民主主義. Parteinamen wie 民主党 verwenden nur 民主. Gegenbegriffe sind etwa 独裁 („Diktatur“) oder 全体主義.","levelNote":"Compound: 民主 (N2) + 主義 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"共産主義は二十世紀に大きな影響を与えた。","romaji":"Kyōsanshugi wa nijisseiki ni ōkina eikyō o ataeta.","german":"Communism had a major influence in the twentieth century.","furigana":"きょうさんしゅぎ|にじっせいき|おお|えいきょう|あた"},{"japanese":"冷戦は資本主義と共産主義の対立だった。","romaji":"Reisen wa shihonshugi to kyōsanshugi no tairitsu datta.","german":"The Cold War was a confrontation between capitalism and communism.","furigana":"れいせん|しほんしゅぎ|きょうさんしゅぎ|たいりつ"},{"japanese":"祖父は若い頃、共産主義の思想に影響を受けたそうだ。","romaji":"Sofu wa wakai koro, kyōsanshugi no shisō ni eikyō o uketa sō da.","german":"My grandfather was reportedly influenced by communist ideas when he was young.","furigana":"そふ|わか|ころ|きょうさんしゅぎ|しそう|えいきょう|う"}],"notes":"共産主義 is communism as an ideology and economic order, literally \"doctrine of common (共) property (産)\". Related words: 共産党 (\"Communist Party\"), 共産主義者 (\"communist\"), 共産圏 (\"Eastern Bloc\"). 社会主義 (\"socialism\") is often mentioned as a preliminary stage or related current; the opposite is 資本主義. The word belongs to the domain of history, politics and news.","levelNote":"Compound: 共産 (N2) + 主義 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"資本主義の経済体制が世界に広まった。","romaji":"Shihonshugi no keizai taisei ga sekai ni hiromatta.","german":"The capitalist economic system spread across the world.","furigana":"しほんしゅぎ|けいざいたいせい|せかい|ひろ"},{"japanese":"資本主義社会では、貧富の差が広がりやすいと言われる。","romaji":"Shihonshugi shakai de wa, hinpu no sa ga hirogari yasui to iwareru.","german":"In capitalist societies, the gap between rich and poor is said to widen easily.","furigana":"しほんしゅぎしゃかい|ひんぷ|さ|ひろ|い"},{"japanese":"授業で資本主義と社会主義の違いについて議論した。","romaji":"Jugyō de shihonshugi to shakaishugi no chigai ni tsuite giron shita.","german":"In class we debated the differences between capitalism and socialism.","furigana":"じゅぎょう|しほんしゅぎ|しゃかいしゅぎ|ちが|ぎろん"}],"notes":"資本主義 is capitalism, i.e. an economic order based on private ownership of capital (資本) and market competition. Typical: 資本主義社会, 資本主義経済, 資本主義国. Related are 資本家 (\"capitalist\") and 資本金 (\"capital stock\"). Opposites are 共産主義 and 社会主義. The word belongs to the language of economics, history and news.","levelNote":"Compound: 資本 (N3) + 主義 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"多くの国がかつて植民地だった。","romaji":"Ōku no kuni ga katsute shokuminchi datta.","german":"Many countries used to be colonies.","furigana":"おお|くに|しょくみんち"},{"japanese":"この町には植民地時代の建物が今も残っている。","romaji":"Kono machi ni wa shokuminchi jidai no tatemono ga ima mo nokotte iru.","german":"Buildings from the colonial era still remain in this town.","furigana":"まち|しょくみんちじだい|たてもの|いま|のこ"},{"japanese":"その国は約百年間、ヨーロッパの国の植民地支配を受けた。","romaji":"Sono kuni wa yaku hyakunenkan, Yōroppa no kuni no shokuminchi shihai o uketa.","german":"That country was under the colonial rule of a European country for about a hundred years.","furigana":"くに|やくひゃくねんかん|くに|しょくみんちしはい|う"}],"notes":"植民地 is a colony, a territory ruled by a foreign power; literally \"land (地) where people (民) are settled (植)\". Typical: 植民地支配 (\"colonial rule\"), 植民地にする, 旧植民地, 植民地時代. The related verb is 植民地化する (\"to colonize\"). The rare meaning \"Japanese settlement in Brazil\" plays no role in normal usage.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"領土問題は外交上の課題だ。","romaji":"Ryōdo mondai wa gaikōjō no kadai da.","german":"Territorial disputes are a diplomatic issue.","furigana":"りょうどもんだい|がいこうじょう|かだい"},{"japanese":"両国はその島を自国の領土だと主張している。","romaji":"Ryōkoku wa sono shima o jikoku no ryōdo da to shuchō shite iru.","german":"Both countries claim that island as their own territory.","furigana":"りょうこく|しま|じこく|りょうど|しゅちょう"},{"japanese":"昔、多くの国が戦争で領土を広げようとした。","romaji":"Mukashi, ōku no kuni ga sensō de ryōdo o hirogeyō to shita.","german":"In the past, many countries tried to expand their territory through war.","furigana":"むかし|おお|くに|せんそう|りょうど|ひろ"}],"notes":"領土 is the territory of a state, especially the land area over which a state has sovereignty. It is accompanied by 領海 (\"territorial waters\") and 領空 (\"airspace\"), together 領域. Typical: 領土問題 (\"territorial dispute\"), 領土を拡大する, 領土を主張する, 北方領土. For an area in the general sense, people say 地域 or 土地 instead.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"国境を越えるにはパスポートが必要だ。","romaji":"Kokkyō o koeru ni wa pasupōto ga hitsuyō da.","german":"You need a passport to cross the border.","furigana":"こっきょう|こ|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"ヨーロッパでは電車で簡単に国境を越えられる。","romaji":"Yōroppa de wa densha de kantan ni kokkyō o koerareru.","german":"In Europe, you can easily cross borders by train.","furigana":"でんしゃ|かんたん|こっきょう|こ"},{"japanese":"国境付近で両国の軍が向かい合っている。","romaji":"Kokkyō fukin de ryōkoku no gun ga mukaiatte iru.","german":"Near the border, the armies of the two countries face each other.","furigana":"こっきょうふきん|りょうこく|ぐん|む|あ"}],"notes":"国境 is the border between two states, normally read こっきょう. Typical: 国境を越える (\"to cross the border\"), 国境線, 国境付近, 国境なき医師団 (\"Doctors Without Borders\"). For borders within a country, e.g. between prefectures, one says 県境（けんざかい）; the old reading くにざかい referred to the historical provinces. In general, \"border, limit\" is 境 or 限界.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"消費税が引き上げられた。","romaji":"Shōhizei ga hikiagerareta.","german":"Die Mehrwertsteuer wurde erhöht.","furigana":"しょうひぜい|ひ|あ"},{"japanese":"この値段は消費税込みですか。","romaji":"Kono nedan wa shōhizei komi desu ka.","german":"Ist in diesem Preis die Mehrwertsteuer enthalten?","furigana":"ねだん|しょうひぜいこ"},{"japanese":"店内で食べると、持ち帰りより消費税が高くなります。","romaji":"Tennai de taberu to, mochikaeri yori shōhizei ga takaku narimasu.","german":"Wenn Sie hier im Laden essen, ist die Mehrwertsteuer höher als beim Mitnehmen.","furigana":"てんない|た|も|かえ|しょうひぜい|たか"}],"notes":"消費税 ist die japanische Verbrauchssteuer, die beim Einkauf auf den Preis aufgeschlagen wird und der deutschen Mehrwertsteuer entspricht. Typisch: 消費税率 („Steuersatz“), 消費税が上がる/を引き上げる, 消費税込み. Preise werden als 税込 („inklusive Steuer“) oder 税抜き („ohne Steuer“) angegeben. Für Lebensmittel gilt in Japan ein ermäßigter Satz (軽減税率).","levelNote":"Compound: 消費 (N3) + 税 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"所得税を申告する必要がある。","romaji":"Shotokuzei o shinkoku suru hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"You need to file an income tax return.","furigana":"しょとくぜい|しんこく|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"所得が低い家庭には、国から支援がある。","romaji":"Shotoku ga hikui katei ni wa, kuni kara shien ga aru.","german":"Households with low incomes receive support from the state.","furigana":"しょとく|ひく|かてい|くに|しえん"},{"japanese":"都市と地方の所得の差が問題になっている。","romaji":"Toshi to chihō no shotoku no sa ga mondai ni natte iru.","german":"The income gap between the city and the countryside has become a problem.","furigana":"とし|ちほう|しょとく|さ|もんだい"}],"notes":"所得 is income in the economic and tax sense, strictly speaking revenue minus expenses or allowances. In daily life, for \"what one earns\" people usually say 収入 or 給料; 所得 sounds official. Typical: 所得税 (\"income tax\"), 所得が低い/高い, 低所得者, 所得格差 (\"income disparity\"), 国民所得.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"昨夜、駅前で事件が起きた。","romaji":"Sakuya, ekimae de jiken ga okita.","german":"Last night an incident occurred in front of the station.","furigana":"さくや|えきまえ|じけん|お"},{"japanese":"警察は一週間でその事件を解決した。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa isshūkan de sono jiken o kaiketsu shita.","german":"The police solved the case within a week.","furigana":"けいさつ|いっしゅうかん|じけん|かいけつ"},{"japanese":"旅行中に事件に巻き込まれないよう、気をつけてください。","romaji":"Ryokōchū ni jiken ni makikomarenai yō, ki o tsukete kudasai.","german":"Please be careful not to get caught up in any criminal incident while traveling.","furigana":"りょこうちゅう|じけん|ま|こ|き"}],"notes":"事件 is a conspicuous, usually negative event, especially a crime, a scandal or a criminal case: 殺人事件, 事件が起きる, 事件に巻き込まれる, 事件を解決する. 事故, by contrast, is an unintentional accident (交通事故), and 出来事 a neutral event. Historical incidents are also called 〇〇事件. In legal language, 事件 also means a legal case.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 40"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"加害者は逮捕された。","romaji":"Kagaisha wa taiho sareta.","german":"The perpetrator was arrested.","furigana":"かがいしゃ|たいほ"},{"japanese":"交通事故の加害者は、被害者に謝罪した。","romaji":"Kōtsū jiko no kagaisha wa, higaisha ni shazai shita.","german":"The person responsible for the traffic accident apologized to the victim.","furigana":"こうつうじこ|かがいしゃ|ひがいしゃ|しゃざい"},{"japanese":"いじめの加害者は、自分のしたことの重さに気づいていないことが多い。","romaji":"Ijime no kagaisha wa, jibun no shita koto no omosa ni kizuite inai koto ga ōi.","german":"Perpetrators of bullying often don't realize the gravity of what they've done.","furigana":"かがいしゃ|じぶん|おも|き|おお"}],"notes":"加害者 is the person who inflicts (加) harm (害) on another, i.e. a perpetrator or offender; the opposite is 被害者 (\"victim, injured party\"). It is used for crimes, bullying (いじめの加害者) and also for the person who caused a traffic accident, so it emphasizes the harming role, not necessarily a crime. 犯人, by contrast, specifically means the perpetrator of a crime. A factual, formal word from news and legal language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"証人が法廷で証言した。","romaji":"Shōnin ga hōtei de shōgen shita.","german":"The witness testified in court.","furigana":"しょうにん|ほうてい|しょうげん"},{"japanese":"その場にいた私が証人になります。","romaji":"Sono ba ni ita watashi ga shōnin ni narimasu.","german":"I was there, so I will be a witness.","furigana":"ば|わたし|しょうにん"},{"japanese":"元社長が証人として国会に呼ばれた。","romaji":"Moto shachō ga shōnin to shite kokkai ni yobareta.","german":"The former company president was summoned to the Diet as a witness.","furigana":"もとしゃちょう|しょうにん|こっかい|よ"}],"notes":"証人 is a witness who attests to something or gives testimony in court; the testimony itself is called 証言. Typical: 証人として出廷する, 証人になる, 証人喚問 (summoning of witnesses in the Diet). An eyewitness who has seen something is more precisely 目撃者. Do not confuse it with 保証人 (\"guarantor\"), who is needed for a rental contract, for example, or with the similar-sounding 商人 (\"merchant\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"犯罪に対して厳しい刑罰が科された。","romaji":"Hanzai ni taishite kibishii keibatsu ga kasareta.","german":"Für das Verbrechen wurde eine strenge Strafe verhängt.","furigana":"はんざい|たい|きび|けいばつ|か"},{"japanese":"刑罰を重くするだけでは、犯罪は減らないという意見もある。","romaji":"Keibatsu o omoku suru dake de wa, hanzai wa heranai to iu iken mo aru.","german":"Manche meinen, dass härtere Strafen allein die Kriminalität nicht senken.","furigana":"けいばつ|おも|はんざい|へ|いけん"},{"japanese":"法律には、どんな行為にどんな刑罰が与えられるかが書かれている。","romaji":"Hōritsu ni wa, donna kōi ni donna keibatsu ga ataerareru ka ga kakarete iru.","german":"Im Gesetz steht, welche Handlung mit welcher Strafe geahndet wird.","furigana":"ほうりつ|こうい|けいばつ|あた|か"}],"notes":"刑罰 ist eine staatliche Strafe für eine Straftat, etwa Freiheitsstrafe oder Geldstrafe, und gehört zur Rechts- und Nachrichtensprache. Typisch: 刑罰を科す („eine Strafe verhängen“; 科す, nicht 課す), 刑罰が重い/軽い, 刑罰を受ける. Im Alltag sagt man für Strafen allgemein 罰, z. B. 遅刻した罰 in der Schule; 罰金 ist eine Geldstrafe. 刑罰 ist also enger und förmlicher als das deutsche „Strafe“.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"死刑制度の廃止を求める声がある。","romaji":"Shikei seido no haishi o motomeru koe ga aru.","german":"There are voices calling for the abolition of the death penalty system.","furigana":"しけいせいど|はいし|もと|こえ"},{"japanese":"裁判所は被告に死刑を言い渡した。","romaji":"Saibansho wa hikoku ni shikei o iiwatashita.","german":"The court sentenced the defendant to death.","furigana":"さいばんしょ|ひこく|しけい|い|わた"},{"japanese":"ヨーロッパの多くの国では、死刑はもう行われていない。","romaji":"Yōroppa no ōku no kuni de wa, shikei wa mō okonawarete inai.","german":"In many European countries, the death penalty is no longer carried out.","furigana":"おお|くに|しけい|おこな"}],"notes":"死刑 is the death penalty, which still exists in Japan. Typical: 死刑判決 (\"death sentence\"), 死刑を言い渡す (\"to sentence to death\"), 死刑が執行される (\"to be carried out\"), 死刑制度, 死刑廃止 (\"abolition of the death penalty\"). A person sentenced to death is a 死刑囚. The word belongs to news and legal language.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"犯人が現場で逮捕された。","romaji":"Hannin ga genba de taiho sareta.","german":"The culprit was arrested at the scene.","furigana":"はんにん|げんば|たいほ"},{"japanese":"警察は昨夜、四十代の男を逮捕した。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa sakuya, yonjūdai no otoko o taiho shita.","german":"The police arrested a man in his forties last night.","furigana":"けいさつ|さくや|よんじゅうだい|おとこ|たいほ"},{"japanese":"万引きをして、店の前で現行犯逮捕された。","romaji":"Manbiki o shite, mise no mae de genkōhan taiho sareta.","german":"He shoplifted and was arrested in the act in front of the store.","furigana":"まんび|みせ|まえ|げんこうはんたいほ"}],"notes":"逮捕 is arrest by the police; as a transitive する-verb: 警察が容疑者を逮捕する, often in the passive 逮捕される. Typical: 現行犯逮捕 (\"arrest in the act\"), 逮捕状 (\"arrest warrant\"). In the news, the arrested person is called 容疑者, not 犯人, as long as guilt has not been proven. In everyday speech one also says 捕まる/捕まえる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"犯人はまだ見つかっていない。","romaji":"Hannin wa mada mitsukatte inai.","german":"The culprit has not been found yet.","furigana":"はんにん|み"},{"japanese":"このミステリー小説は、最後まで犯人が分からない。","romaji":"Kono misuterī shōsetsu wa, saigo made hannin ga wakaranai.","german":"In this mystery novel, you don't know who the culprit is until the end.","furigana":"しょうせつ|さいご|はんにん|わ"},{"japanese":"冷蔵庫のプリンを食べた犯人は誰？","romaji":"Reizōko no purin o tabeta hannin wa dare?","german":"Who is the culprit who ate the pudding in the fridge?","furigana":"れいぞうこ|た|はんにん|だれ"}],"notes":"犯人 is the perpetrator of a crime, often in the context of detective stories and investigations: 犯人を捕まえる, 犯人を探す, 犯人は誰だ. In news reports, a person who has been arrested but not yet convicted is called 容疑者; 犯人 presupposes that the crime is proven. 加害者, by contrast, emphasizes the role of harming a victim. Also used jokingly in everyday life: who ate the cake - 犯人は誰？","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 40"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"消防車がすぐに到着した。","romaji":"Shōbōsha ga sugu ni tōchaku shita.","german":"The fire truck arrived immediately.","furigana":"しょうぼうしゃ|とうちゃく"},{"japanese":"息子は将来、消防士になりたいと言っている。","romaji":"Musuko wa shōrai, shōbōshi ni naritai to itte iru.","german":"My son says he wants to become a firefighter in the future.","furigana":"むすこ|しょうらい|しょうぼうし|い"},{"japanese":"地域の消防団が火事の予防を呼びかけている。","romaji":"Chiiki no shōbōdan ga kaji no yobō o yobikakete iru.","german":"The local volunteer fire corps is calling for fire prevention.","furigana":"ちいき|しょうぼうだん|かじ|よぼう|よ"}],"notes":"消防 denotes fire fighting and, in short, also the fire department as an institution. Mostly it appears in compounds: 消防車 (\"fire truck\"), 消防署 (\"fire station\"), 消防士 (\"firefighter\"), 消防団 (\"volunteer fire corps\"). The emergency number 119 connects to the fire department and ambulance (消防・救急). Extinguishing itself is called 消火する.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"救急車を呼んでください。","romaji":"Kyūkyūsha o yonde kudasai.","german":"Please call an ambulance.","furigana":"きゅうきゅうしゃ|よ"},{"japanese":"夜中に熱が出て、救急病院に行った。","romaji":"Yonaka ni netsu ga dete, kyūkyū byōin ni itta.","german":"I ran a fever in the middle of the night and went to an emergency hospital.","furigana":"よなか|ねつ|で|きゅうきゅうびょういん|い"},{"japanese":"家に救急箱を置いておくと安心です。","romaji":"Ie ni kyūkyūbako o oite oku to anshin desu.","german":"Keeping a first-aid kit at home gives peace of mind.","furigana":"いえ|きゅうきゅうばこ|お|あんしん"}],"notes":"救急 means first aid or emergency care and almost always appears in compounds: 救急車 (\"ambulance\"), 救急病院 (\"emergency hospital\"), 救急隊員 (\"paramedic\"), 救急箱 (\"first-aid kit\"), 救急外来 (\"emergency room\"). On its own it is rare. 緊急, by contrast, is generally \"urgent, emergency\" (緊急事態); 救急 refers specifically to medical help.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"慈善事業に力を入れている。","romaji":"Jizen jigyō ni chikara o irete iru.","german":"He puts effort into charitable work.","furigana":"じぜんじぎょう|ちから|い"},{"japanese":"コンサートの収益は慈善団体に寄付される。","romaji":"Konsāto no shūeki wa jizen dantai ni kifu sareru.","german":"The proceeds of the concert will be donated to a charity.","furigana":"しゅうえき|じぜんだんたい|きふ"},{"japanese":"彼女は引退後、子どもを支援する慈善活動を続けている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa intaigo, kodomo o shien suru jizen katsudō o tsuzukete iru.","german":"After retiring, she continues charitable activities supporting children.","furigana":"かのじょ|いんたいご|こ|しえん|じぜんかつどう|つづ"}],"notes":"慈善 is charity, i.e. help for the needy out of compassion. It almost always stands as a modifier: 慈善事業 (\"charitable work\"), 慈善団体 (\"charity organization\"), 慈善活動, 慈善コンサート. The word is rather formal; in everyday life the loanword チャリティー is often heard. For a single donation, people say 寄付.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"団体旅行に申し込んだ。","romaji":"Dantai ryokō ni mōshikonda.","german":"I signed up for a group tour.","furigana":"だんたいりょこう|もう|こ"},{"japanese":"二十人以上の団体は、入場料が割引になります。","romaji":"Nijūnin ijō no dantai wa, nyūjōryō ga waribiki ni narimasu.","german":"Groups of twenty or more people get a discount on the admission fee.","furigana":"にじゅうにんいじょう|だんたい|にゅうじょうりょう|わりびき"},{"japanese":"この市民団体は、川をきれいにする活動をしている。","romaji":"Kono shimin dantai wa, kawa o kirei ni suru katsudō o shite iru.","german":"This citizens' group works on cleaning up the river.","furigana":"しみんだんたい|かわ|かつどう"}],"notes":"団体 is on the one hand a group of people who do something together (団体旅行, 団体割引, 団体行動), and on the other an organization or association (市民団体, 宗教団体, 政治団体). In sports, 団体戦 (\"team competition\") is contrasted with 個人戦. グループ is more casual and everyday; 組織 stresses the internal structure of an organization.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼はスポーツ協会の会長だ。","romaji":"Kare wa supōtsu kyōkai no kaichō da.","german":"He is the chairman of the sports association.","furigana":"かれ|きょうかい|かいちょう"},{"japanese":"観光協会でおすすめの店を教えてもらった。","romaji":"Kankō kyōkai de osusume no mise o oshiete moratta.","german":"I asked the tourist association to recommend some shops.","furigana":"かんこうきょうかい|みせ|おし"},{"japanese":"日本サッカー協会が新しい監督を発表した。","romaji":"Nihon Sakkā Kyōkai ga atarashii kantoku o happyō shita.","german":"The Japan Football Association announced a new manager.","furigana":"にほん|きょうかい|あたら|かんとく|はっぴょう"}],"notes":"協会 refers to an association or federation that represents a field or a particular purpose, often with companies or persons as members: 日本サッカー協会, 観光協会. It is more formal and larger than a leisure \"club\" (クラブ, 同好会) and mostly appears in proper names; even the broadcaster NHK is officially called 日本放送協会. Do not confuse it with the identically read 教会 \"church\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"財団から奨学金をもらっている。","romaji":"Zaidan kara shōgakukin o moratte iru.","german":"I receive a scholarship from a foundation.","furigana":"ざいだん|しょうがくきん"},{"japanese":"祖父の遺産で財団が設立された。","romaji":"Sofu no isan de zaidan ga setsuritsu sareta.","german":"A foundation was established with my grandfather's estate.","furigana":"そふ|いさん|ざいだん|せつりつ"},{"japanese":"この美術館は民間の財団によって運営されている。","romaji":"Kono bijutsukan wa minkan no zaidan ni yotte un'ei sarete iru.","german":"This museum is run by a private foundation.","furigana":"びじゅつかん|みんかん|ざいだん|うんえい"}],"notes":"財団 is a foundation, i.e. an organization based on endowed assets that supports, for example, research, culture or scholarships. The legal form is called 財団法人 (e.g. 公益財団法人), as opposed to the membership-based association 社団法人. Typical phrases: 財団を設立する \"to establish a foundation\", 財団から援助を受ける. Not to be confused with 財閥, the large family conglomerates of the prewar era.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"政府機関が調査を行った。","romaji":"Seifu kikan ga chōsa o okonatta.","german":"A government agency conducted an investigation.","furigana":"せいふきかん|ちょうさ|おこな"},{"japanese":"会場へは公共交通機関をご利用ください。","romaji":"Kaijō e wa kōkyō kōtsū kikan o goriyō kudasai.","german":"Please use public transportation to get to the venue.","furigana":"かいじょう|こうきょうこうつうきかん|りよう"},{"japanese":"ローンの相談なら、まず金融機関に行ったほうがいい。","romaji":"Rōn no sōdan nara, mazu kin'yū kikan ni itta hō ga ii.","german":"If it's about a loan, it's best to go to a financial institution first.","furigana":"そうだん|きんゆうきかん|い"}],"notes":"機関 denotes an institution or organization that fulfills a particular function in the state and society, and appears almost always in compounds: 政府機関 \"government agency\", 金融機関 \"financial institution\", 報道機関 \"news organization\", 教育機関, 公共交通機関 \"public transportation\". The older meaning \"engine, machine\" lives on in 機関車 \"locomotive\". Unlike 組織 (organization as a structure or association), 機関 stresses the role as an executing organ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"憲法は国の最高法規だ。","romaji":"Kenpō wa kuni no saikō hōki da.","german":"The constitution is the supreme law of the country.","furigana":"けんぽう|くに|さいこうほうき"},{"japanese":"その法律は憲法に違反しているという意見がある。","romaji":"Sono hōritsu wa kenpō ni ihan shite iru to iu iken ga aru.","german":"Some say that this law violates the constitution.","furigana":"ほうりつ|けんぽう|いはん|いけん"},{"japanese":"五月三日は憲法記念日で、学校は休みだ。","romaji":"Gogatsu mikka wa Kenpō Kinenbi de, gakkō wa yasumi da.","german":"May 3 is Constitution Memorial Day, and schools are closed.","furigana":"ごがつみっか|けんぽうきねんび|がっこう|やす"}],"notes":"憲法 is the constitution of a state; in Japan usually short for 日本国憲法. Typical combinations: 憲法で保障されている \"guaranteed by the constitution\", 憲法に違反する \"to violate the constitution\" (憲法違反), 憲法を改正する \"to amend the constitution\" (political slogan 憲法改正). Ordinary laws are called 法律, municipal regulations 条例. May 3 is the Japanese holiday 憲法記念日.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"法廷で証拠が提出された。","romaji":"Hōtei de shōko ga teishutsu sareta.","german":"Evidence was submitted in court.","furigana":"ほうてい|しょうこ|ていしゅつ"},{"japanese":"両社は特許をめぐって法廷で争うことになった。","romaji":"Ryōsha wa tokkyo o megutte hōtei de arasou koto ni natta.","german":"The two companies will fight over the patent in court.","furigana":"りょうしゃ|とっきょ|ほうてい|あらそ"},{"japanese":"彼は証人として法廷に立った。","romaji":"Kare wa shōnin toshite hōtei ni tatta.","german":"He took the stand in court as a witness.","furigana":"かれ|しょうにん|ほうてい|た"}],"notes":"法廷 is the courtroom, or the court as the place of a hearing. Typical: 法廷で争う \"to fight in court\", 法廷に立つ \"to stand in court or to testify\", 法廷に持ち込む \"to bring to court\". The court as an institution or building is 裁判所, and the proceedings themselves 裁判. 法廷 sounds formal and appears mainly in news, crime stories and legal dramas.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"検察が容疑者を起訴した。","romaji":"Kensatsu ga yōgisha o kiso shita.","german":"The prosecution indicted the suspect.","furigana":"けんさつ|ようぎしゃ|きそ"},{"japanese":"検察側と弁護側の主張は大きく異なっていた。","romaji":"Kensatsu-gawa to bengo-gawa no shuchō wa ōkiku kotonatte ita.","german":"The claims of the prosecution and the defense were very different.","furigana":"けんさつがわ|べんごがわ|しゅちょう|おお|こと"},{"japanese":"警察は事件の資料を検察に送った。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa jiken no shiryō o kensatsu ni okutta.","german":"The police sent the case materials to the prosecutors.","furigana":"けいさつ|じけん|しりょう|けんさつ|おく"}],"notes":"検察 is the public prosecutor's office as an authority (short for 検察庁), which investigates crimes and brings charges (起訴する). The individual person is a 検察官 or, colloquially, 検事. In trial reports, 検察側 \"the prosecution (side)\" is contrasted with 弁護側 \"the defense\". The police (警察) investigate first and then hand the case over to the 検察.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"原告は損害賠償を請求した。","romaji":"Genkoku wa songai baishō o seikyū shita.","german":"The plaintiff demanded compensation for damages.","furigana":"げんこく|そんがいばいしょう|せいきゅう"},{"japanese":"裁判所は原告の訴えを認めた。","romaji":"Saibansho wa genkoku no uttae o mitometa.","german":"The court accepted the plaintiff's claim.","furigana":"さいばんしょ|げんこく|うった|みと"},{"japanese":"公害の被害者たちが原告となって国を訴えた。","romaji":"Kōgai no higaishatachi ga genkoku to natte kuni o uttaeta.","german":"The victims of pollution became plaintiffs and sued the state.","furigana":"こうがい|ひがいしゃ|げんこく|くに|うった"}],"notes":"原告 is the suing party in a civil or administrative lawsuit, the \"plaintiff\"; the opposing party is the 被告. Typical: 原告側 \"the plaintiff's side\", 原告の訴えを認める \"to uphold the claim\", 原告が勝訴する／敗訴する \"to win/lose the case\". For class actions people speak of the 原告団. In a criminal trial there is no 原告 - there the 検察 brings the charges.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"被告は無罪を主張した。","romaji":"Hikoku wa muzai o shuchō shita.","german":"The defendant pleaded not guilty.","furigana":"ひこく|むざい|しゅちょう"},{"japanese":"裁判所は被告に三年の刑を言い渡した。","romaji":"Saibansho wa hikoku ni sannen no kei o iiwatashita.","german":"The court sentenced the defendant to three years.","furigana":"さいばんしょ|ひこく|さんねん|けい|い|わた"},{"japanese":"原告と被告の間で和解が成立した。","romaji":"Genkoku to hikoku no aida de wakai ga seiritsu shita.","german":"A settlement was reached between the plaintiff and the defendant.","furigana":"げんこく|ひこく|あいだ|わかい|せいりつ"}],"notes":"被告 is the sued or accused party. In a civil lawsuit it is the \"defendant\" (opposing party of the 原告); in a criminal trial the accused is legally called 被告人, which the media shorten to 被告 and attach to names like a title: 山田被告 \"the defendant Yamada\". Before the indictment, people speak of a 容疑者 \"suspect\". Typical: 被告側, 被告に判決を言い渡す.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この判例は今後の裁判に影響を与える。","romaji":"Kono hanrei wa kongo no saiban ni eikyō o ataeru.","german":"This precedent will influence future trials.","furigana":"はんれい|こんご|さいばん|えいきょう|あた"},{"japanese":"弁護士は似たような判例を調べた。","romaji":"Bengoshi wa nita yō na hanrei o shirabeta.","german":"The lawyer researched similar precedents.","furigana":"べんごし|に|はんれい|しら"},{"japanese":"このような事件には、まだ判例がない。","romaji":"Kono yō na jiken ni wa, mada hanrei ga nai.","german":"There is no precedent yet for a case like this.","furigana":"じけん|はんれい"}],"notes":"判例 are earlier court decisions that serve as a standard for later cases - \"precedent\" or \"case law\". Typical: 判例に従う \"to follow case law\", 判例を調べる, 最高裁の判例. A single verdict is a 判決; 判例 is a verdict in its function as a model. Precedents outside the judiciary (\"this has never happened before\") are called 前例.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"市が新しい条例を制定した。","romaji":"Shi ga atarashii jōrei o seitei shita.","german":"The city enacted a new ordinance.","furigana":"し|あたら|じょうれい|せいてい"},{"japanese":"この区では、条例で歩きたばこが禁止されている。","romaji":"Kono ku de wa, jōrei de arukitabako ga kinshi sarete iru.","german":"In this ward, walking while smoking is prohibited by ordinance.","furigana":"く|じょうれい|ある|きんし"},{"japanese":"条例に違反すると、罰金を払わなければならない。","romaji":"Jōrei ni ihan suru to, bakkin o harawanakereba naranai.","german":"If you violate the ordinance, you have to pay a fine.","furigana":"じょうれい|いはん|ばっきん|はら"}],"notes":"条例 is a bylaw or ordinance that a prefecture or municipality enacts itself - as opposed to the national law (法律). Typical verbs: 条例を制定する \"to enact\", 条例で禁止する, 条例に違反する. Well-known examples are 路上喫煙禁止条例 or 迷惑防止条例. The word is found mainly in news and on notice signs.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その行為は合法だと認められた。","romaji":"Sono kōi wa gōhō da to mitomerareta.","german":"That act was recognized as legal.","furigana":"こうい|ごうほう|みと"},{"japanese":"国によっては、その薬は合法だ。","romaji":"Kuni ni yotte wa, sono kusuri wa gōhō da.","german":"In some countries, that drug is legal.","furigana":"くに|くすり|ごうほう"},{"japanese":"税金を減らすための合法的な方法を税理士に相談した。","romaji":"Zeikin o herasu tame no gōhōteki na hōhō o zeirishi ni sōdan shita.","german":"I consulted a tax accountant about legal ways to reduce taxes.","furigana":"ぜいきん|へ|ごうほうてき|ほうほう|ぜいりし|そうだん"}],"notes":"合法 means \"lawful, legal\" and is used predicatively (合法だ／合法である) or as a na-adjective (合法な手段); 合法的に \"in a legal way\" is also very common. Opposites are 違法 and the stronger 非合法 \"illegal, underground\". In everyday speech people often say more simply 法律で認められている. The register is factual to formal.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"違法な取引が摘発された。","romaji":"Ihō na torihiki ga tekihatsu sareta.","german":"The illegal transaction was exposed.","furigana":"いほう|とりひき|てきはつ"},{"japanese":"駅前の違法駐車が問題になっている。","romaji":"Ekimae no ihō chūsha ga mondai ni natte iru.","german":"Illegal parking in front of the station has become a problem.","furigana":"えきまえ|いほうちゅうしゃ|もんだい"},{"japanese":"映画を違法にダウンロードするのはやめよう。","romaji":"Eiga o ihō ni daunrōdo suru no wa yameyō.","german":"Let's stop downloading movies illegally.","furigana":"えいが|いほう"}],"notes":"違法 means \"against the law, illegal\" and is used mostly as a na-adjective or in compounds: 違法な取引, 違法駐車 \"illegal parking\", 違法ダウンロード. The opposite is 合法. The similar 不法 (\"unlawful\") is tied to set combinations such as 不法侵入 or 不法滞在. To violate a specific rule is 〜に違反する, the noun for it is 違反.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は会社を訴えることにした。","romaji":"Kare wa kaisha o uttaeru koto ni shita.","german":"He decided to sue the company.","furigana":"かれ|かいしゃ|うった"},{"japanese":"子供が腹痛を訴えたので、保健室へ連れていった。","romaji":"Kodomo ga fukutsū o uttaeta node, hokenshitsu e tsurete itta.","german":"Because the child complained of a stomachache, I took him to the school infirmary.","furigana":"こども|ふくつう|うった|ほけんしつ|つ"},{"japanese":"環境団体は森を守る必要性を訴えている。","romaji":"Kankyō dantai wa mori o mamoru hitsuyōsei o uttaete iru.","german":"Environmental groups are appealing for the need to protect the forest.","furigana":"かんきょうだんたい|もり|まも|ひつようせい|うった"}],"notes":"訴える has three main meanings: to sue someone (会社を訴える, 裁判所に訴える), to voice complaints or complain of something (腹痛を訴える, 不満を訴える), and to appeal to something or convey something urgently (平和を訴える, 感情に訴える). In a medical context, 〜を訴える is the usual wording for \"to complain of ...\". The noun is 訴え \"lawsuit, appeal\"; there is no intransitive counterpart, \"to be sued\" is the passive 訴えられる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"被害者が犯人を告訴した。","romaji":"Higaisha ga hannin o kokuso shita.","german":"The victim filed a criminal complaint against the culprit.","furigana":"ひがいしゃ|はんにん|こくそ"},{"japanese":"被害者は後で告訴を取り下げた。","romaji":"Higaisha wa ato de kokuso o torisageta.","german":"The victim later withdrew the complaint.","furigana":"ひがいしゃ|あと|こくそ|と|さ"},{"japanese":"告訴するかどうか、まだ迷っている。","romaji":"Kokuso suru ka dō ka, mada mayotte iru.","german":"I'm still undecided about whether to file a complaint.","furigana":"こくそ|まよ"}],"notes":"告訴 is the criminal complaint or petition that the victim (or their representative) files with the police or public prosecutor: 犯人を告訴する, 告訴を取り下げる \"to withdraw the complaint\". When an uninvolved third party reports an offense, it is called 告発. 起訴, by contrast, is the bringing of charges by the prosecutor, and a civil suit is usually expressed with 訴える or 提訴.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"容疑者が正式に起訴された。","romaji":"Yōgisha ga seishiki ni kiso sareta.","german":"The suspect was formally indicted.","furigana":"ようぎしゃ|せいしき|きそ"},{"japanese":"男は詐欺の罪で起訴された。","romaji":"Otoko wa sagi no tsumi de kiso sareta.","german":"The man was indicted on a charge of fraud.","furigana":"おとこ|さぎ|つみ|きそ"},{"japanese":"証拠が足りず、彼は不起訴になった。","romaji":"Shōko ga tarizu, kare wa fukiso ni natta.","german":"Due to insufficient evidence, he was not prosecuted.","furigana":"しょうこ|た|かれ|ふきそ"}],"notes":"起訴 is the bringing of charges by the public prosecutor's office (検察) before a criminal court. It usually appears in the passive: 〜の罪で起訴される \"to be indicted for ...\"; the opposite is 不起訴 \"no indictment, dropping of the case\". Do not confuse it with 告訴 (complaint by the victim) and the general 訴える \"to sue\". A word of news and judicial language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"証人の証言が裁判の鍵となった。","romaji":"Shōnin no shōgen ga saiban no kagi to natta.","german":"The witness's testimony became the key to the trial.","furigana":"しょうにん|しょうげん|さいばん|かぎ"},{"japanese":"目撃者は、犯人は背の高い男だったと証言した。","romaji":"Mokugekisha wa, hannin wa se no takai otoko datta to shōgen shita.","german":"The eyewitness testified that the culprit was a tall man.","furigana":"もくげきしゃ|はんにん|せ|たか|おとこ|しょうげん"},{"japanese":"戦争を体験した人々の証言を記録に残す。","romaji":"Sensō o taiken shita hitobito no shōgen o kiroku ni nokosu.","german":"We are preserving the testimonies of people who experienced the war.","furigana":"せんそう|たいけん|ひとびと|しょうげん|きろく|のこ"}],"notes":"証言 is a witness statement, i.e. what someone attests about something experienced or seen firsthand - in court (法廷で証言する) or in a wider sense, such as accounts of eyewitnesses to history (被爆者の証言). As a する-verb: 〜と証言する \"to testify that ...\". The person testifying is the 証人. Statements by suspects during interrogation, on the other hand, are called 供述.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"容疑者の供述に矛盾があった。","romaji":"Yōgisha no kyōjutsu ni mujun ga atta.","german":"There was a contradiction in the suspect's statement.","furigana":"ようぎしゃ|きょうじゅつ|むじゅん"},{"japanese":"容疑者は「お金に困っていた」と供述している。","romaji":"Yōgisha wa \"okane ni komatte ita\" to kyōjutsu shite iru.","german":"The suspect is stating that he \"was in financial trouble.\"","furigana":"ようぎしゃ|かね|こま|きょうじゅつ"},{"japanese":"彼は取り調べの途中で供述を変えた。","romaji":"Kare wa torishirabe no tochū de kyōjutsu o kaeta.","german":"He changed his statement partway through the interrogation.","furigana":"かれ|と|しら|とちゅう|きょうじゅつ|か"}],"notes":"供述 is the statement that a suspect, defendant or also a witness gives on record to the police, public prosecutor or court. Typical in the news: 容疑者は「…」と供述している, 供述を変える, 供述調書 \"interrogation record\". A confession in the narrower sense is 自白 - a 供述 can also be a denial. 証言, by contrast, is a witness's statement about what was observed.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"被告には無罪判決が言い渡された。","romaji":"Hikoku ni wa muzai hanketsu ga iiwatasareta.","german":"A not-guilty verdict was handed down to the defendant.","furigana":"ひこく|むざいはんけつ|い|わた"},{"japanese":"二十年後、新しい証拠によって彼の無罪が証明された。","romaji":"Nijūnen go, atarashii shōko ni yotte kare no muzai ga shōmei sareta.","german":"Twenty years later, new evidence proved his innocence.","furigana":"にじゅうねんご|あたら|しょうこ|かれ|むざい|しょうめい"},{"japanese":"裁判で無罪になって、彼女はようやく家族のもとへ帰った。","romaji":"Saiban de muzai ni natte, kanojo wa yōyaku kazoku no moto e kaetta.","german":"Having been found not guilty at trial, she finally returned to her family.","furigana":"さいばん|むざい|かのじょ|かぞく|かえ"}],"notes":"無罪 is the opposite of 有罪: \"not guilty\" or \"acquittal\". Typical phrases: 無罪を主張する \"to plead not guilty, to assert one's innocence\", 無罪判決 \"acquittal\", 無罪になる \"to be acquitted\", 無罪が証明される. For innocence in a general, moral sense, people more often say 潔白 or 罪のない (罪のない人々 \"innocent people\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"陪審員は有罪の評決を下した。","romaji":"Baishin'in wa yūzai no hyōketsu o kudashita.","german":"The jury returned a guilty verdict.","furigana":"ばいしんいん|ゆうざい|ひょうけつ|くだ"},{"japanese":"元社長の有罪が確定した。","romaji":"Moto shachō no yūzai ga kakutei shita.","german":"The former president's conviction was finalized.","furigana":"もとしゃちょう|ゆうざい|かくてい"},{"japanese":"有罪になれば、彼は医師の資格を失う。","romaji":"Yūzai ni nareba, kare wa ishi no shikaku o ushinau.","german":"If he is found guilty, he will lose his medical license.","furigana":"ゆうざい|かれ|いし|しかく|うしな"}],"notes":"有罪 means \"guilty\" in the legal sense, or the court's finding of guilt. Typical: 有罪判決 \"guilty verdict, conviction\", 有罪になる \"to be convicted\", 有罪が確定する \"to be convicted with final effect\". The opposite is 無罪. For guilt in an everyday or moral sense (\"That's my fault\"), you do not use 有罪 but 責任 or 〜のせい.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その王朝は数百年にわたって栄えた。","romaji":"Sono ōchō wa sūhyaku nen ni watatte sakaeta.","german":"That dynasty flourished for several hundred years.","furigana":"おうちょう|すうひゃくねん|さか"},{"japanese":"反乱によって王朝は滅びた。","romaji":"Hanran ni yotte ōchō wa horobita.","german":"The dynasty fell due to a rebellion.","furigana":"はんらん|おうちょう|ほろ"},{"japanese":"源氏物語は王朝文学の代表作だ。","romaji":"Genji monogatari wa ōchō bungaku no daihyōsaku da.","german":"The Tale of Genji is a representative work of court literature.","furigana":"げんじものがたり|おうちょうぶんがく|だいひょうさく"}],"notes":"王朝 is a dynasty, i.e. a ruling family and the period of its rule: 清王朝, ブルボン王朝, 王朝が滅びる \"a dynasty perishes\", 王朝を開く \"to found a dynasty\". In Japan, 王朝 also stands for the court culture of the Heian period, e.g. 王朝文学 \"court literature\". The ruling family itself, without reference to an era, is 王家 or 王室. In sports, 〜王朝 is used figuratively for a long era of victories by a team.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ローマ帝国は広大な領土を支配した。","romaji":"Rōma teikoku wa kōdai na ryōdo o shihai shita.","german":"The Roman Empire ruled vast territories.","furigana":"ていこく|こうだい|りょうど|しはい"},{"japanese":"第一次世界大戦の後、多くの帝国が崩壊した。","romaji":"Daiichiji sekai taisen no ato, ōku no teikoku ga hōkai shita.","german":"After World War I, many empires collapsed.","furigana":"だいいちじせかいたいせん|あと|おお|ていこく|ほうかい"},{"japanese":"彼は一代で巨大なIT帝国を築いた。","romaji":"Kare wa ichidai de kyodai na IT teikoku o kizuita.","german":"He built a huge IT empire in a single generation.","furigana":"かれ|いちだい|きょだい|ていこく|きず"}],"notes":"帝国 is an empire, i.e. a large state ruled by an emperor (皇帝) or dominating many peoples: ローマ帝国, 大英帝国, 帝国が崩壊する \"an empire falls apart\". Historically it also stands for the Japanese empire before 1945 (大日本帝国); derived from it is 帝国主義 \"imperialism\". Figuratively it denotes a powerful corporate empire: IT帝国.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"中世ヨーロッパでは城が多く建てられた。","romaji":"Chūsei Yōroppa de wa shiro ga ōku taterareta.","german":"Many castles were built in medieval Europe.","furigana":"ちゅうせい|しろ|おお|た"},{"japanese":"この町には中世の街並みが残っている。","romaji":"Kono machi ni wa chūsei no machinami ga nokotte iru.","german":"Medieval streetscapes remain in this town.","furigana":"まち|ちゅうせい|まちな|のこ"},{"japanese":"大学で日本の中世史を研究している。","romaji":"Daigaku de Nihon no chūseishi o kenkyū shite iru.","german":"I study medieval Japanese history at university.","furigana":"だいがく|にほん|ちゅうせいし|けんきゅう"}],"notes":"中世 is the Middle Ages: roughly the 5th to 15th century for Europe, and for Japan mainly the Kamakura and Muromachi periods. As an attribute it takes の or stands directly before the noun: 中世の城, 中世ヨーロッパ, 中世史. The usual periodization is 古代 - 中世 - 近世 - 近代 - 現代. \"Medieval\" in the derogatory sense (\"backward\") is expressed rather by 時代遅れ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"近代化が急速に進んだ。","romaji":"Kindaika ga kyūsoku ni susunda.","german":"Modernization progressed rapidly.","furigana":"きんだいか|きゅうそく|すす"},{"japanese":"明治時代に日本は近代国家への道を歩み始めた。","romaji":"Meiji jidai ni Nihon wa kindai kokka e no michi o ayumihajimeta.","german":"In the Meiji period, Japan began to walk the path toward a modern state.","furigana":"めいじじだい|にほん|きんだいこっか|みち|あゆ|はじ"},{"japanese":"近代文学の授業で夏目漱石の小説を読んだ。","romaji":"Kindai bungaku no jugyō de Natsume Sōseki no shōsetsu o yonda.","german":"In modern literature class, we read a novel by Natsume Soseki.","furigana":"きんだいぶんがく|じゅぎょう|なつめそうせき|しょうせつ|よ"}],"notes":"近代 denotes the modern era as a period; in Japan usually the time from the Meiji Restoration to 1945, for Europe roughly since industrialization. Common in compounds: 近代化 \"modernization\", 近代的 \"modern\", 近代文学, 近代国家. Unlike 現代 (the present, \"today\"), 近代 is a historical period; before it comes 近世 (in Japan the Edo period).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"戦争の悲惨さを忘れてはならない。","romaji":"Sensō no hisansa o wasurete wa naranai.","german":"We must not forget the horrors of war.","furigana":"せんそう|ひさん|わす"},{"japanese":"祖父は子どものころ、戦争を経験しました。","romaji":"Sofu wa kodomo no koro, sensō o keiken shimashita.","german":"My grandfather experienced the war as a child.","furigana":"そふ|こ|せんそう|けいけん"},{"japanese":"戦争が早く終わって、平和になってほしいです。","romaji":"Sensō ga hayaku owatte, heiwa ni natte hoshii desu.","german":"I hope the war ends soon and peace comes.","furigana":"せんそう|はや|お|へいわ"}],"notes":"戦争 is \"war\" between states; typical are 戦争が始まる/終わる/起こる and 戦争をする. In Japan, 戦前 and 戦後 without further qualification refer to the Second World War. Figuratively it means fierce competition: 受験戦争 \"exam war\", 価格戦争 \"price war\". A single fight or battle, on the other hand, is 戦い; the opposite concept is 平和.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 42"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"激しい戦闘が続いた。","romaji":"Hageshii sentō ga tsuzuita.","german":"Fierce fighting continued.","furigana":"はげ|せんとう|つづ"},{"japanese":"両国の間で停戦が成立し、戦闘は終わった。","romaji":"Ryōkoku no aida de teisen ga seiritsu shi, sentō wa owatta.","german":"A ceasefire was reached between the two countries and the fighting ended.","furigana":"りょうこく|あいだ|ていせん|せいりつ|せんとう|お"},{"japanese":"このゲームは戦闘シーンの迫力がすごい。","romaji":"Kono gēmu wa sentō shīn no hakuryoku ga sugoi.","german":"The combat scenes in this game are incredibly intense.","furigana":"せんとう|はくりょく"}],"notes":"戦闘 refers to a concrete military engagement or combat between armed units: 戦闘が始まる／続く, 戦闘に参加する, 戦闘機 \"fighter aircraft\". 戦争, by contrast, is war as a whole, and 戦い generally \"fight\" (also in sports or figuratively). In video games, 戦闘 is the usual term for fights (戦闘シーン). As a する-verb it is intransitive, but is rarely used that way.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"他国への侵略は国際法で禁じられている。","romaji":"Takoku e no shinryaku wa kokusaihō de kinjirarete iru.","german":"Invading other countries is prohibited under international law.","furigana":"たこく|しんりゃく|こくさいほう|きん"},{"japanese":"その国は隣国に侵略された歴史がある。","romaji":"Sono kuni wa ringoku ni shinryaku sareta rekishi ga aru.","german":"That country has a history of being invaded by a neighboring country.","furigana":"くに|りんごく|しんりゃく|れきし"},{"japanese":"映画では宇宙人が地球を侵略しに来る。","romaji":"Eiga de wa uchūjin ga chikyū o shinryaku shi ni kuru.","german":"In the movie, aliens come to invade Earth.","furigana":"えいが|うちゅうじん|ちきゅう|しんりゃく|く"}],"notes":"侵略 is a military invasion or aggression in which a state attacks and appropriates foreign territory: 他国を侵略する, 侵略戦争 \"war of aggression\", 侵略者. The word is strongly judgmental; more neutral is 侵攻, which only describes the military advance. In biology there is the technical term 侵略的外来種 \"invasive alien species\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"アレクサンダー大王は広大な地域を征服した。","romaji":"Arekusandā Daiō wa kōdai na chiiki o seifuku shita.","german":"Alexander the Great conquered a vast region.","furigana":"だいおう|こうだい|ちいき|せいふく"},{"japanese":"エベレストが初めて征服されたのは1953年だ。","romaji":"Eberesuto ga hajimete seifuku sareta no wa 1953-nen da.","german":"Everest was first conquered in 1953.","furigana":"はじ|せいふく|ねん"},{"japanese":"アニメの悪役はよく世界征服を目指している。","romaji":"Anime no akuyaku wa yoku sekai seifuku o mezashite iru.","german":"Anime villains are often aiming to conquer the world.","furigana":"あくやく|せかいせいふく|めざ"}],"notes":"征服 is the conquest and subjugation of a country or people: 〜を征服する, 世界征服 \"world domination\" (often jokingly for anime villains). Figuratively it also means \"to conquer, master\": 山を征服する, 病気を征服する. Unlike 侵略 (judgmental: aggression, invasion), 征服 emphasizes the successful subjugation. Do not confuse it with the homophone 制服 \"uniform\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"奴隷制度は十九世紀に廃止された。","romaji":"Dorei seido wa jūkyūseiki ni haishi sareta.","german":"The slave system was abolished in the nineteenth century.","furigana":"どれいせいど|じゅうきゅうせいき|はいし"},{"japanese":"リンカーンは奴隷解放で知られている。","romaji":"Rinkān wa dorei kaihō de shirarete iru.","german":"Lincoln is known for emancipating the slaves.","furigana":"どれいかいほう|し"},{"japanese":"毎日残業ばかりで、会社の奴隷みたいだ。","romaji":"Mainichi zangyō bakari de, kaisha no dorei mitai da.","german":"I do nothing but overtime every day - I feel like a slave to the company.","furigana":"まいにちざんぎょう|かいしゃ|どれい"}],"notes":"奴隷 denotes a slave or slavery as an institution, often in compounds such as 奴隷制度 \"slavery\", 奴隷制, 奴隷解放 \"emancipation of slaves\". Figuratively it describes someone completely dominated by something: 会社の奴隷, お金の奴隷, 〜の奴隷になる. The word is strong and negative; for servants or attendants people say 召使い.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ドイツは1990年に統一された。","romaji":"Doitsu wa sen kyūhyaku kyūjū nen ni tōitsu sareta.","german":"Deutschland wurde 1990 vereinigt.","furigana":"ねん|とういつ"},{"japanese":"資料の書式を統一してください。","romaji":"Shiryō no shoshiki o tōitsu shite kudasai.","german":"Bitte vereinheitlichen Sie das Format der Unterlagen.","furigana":"しりょう|しょしき|とういつ"},{"japanese":"部屋の家具を白で統一したら、広く見えるようになった。","romaji":"Heya no kagu o shiro de tōitsu shitara, hiroku mieru yō ni natta.","german":"Seit die Möbel im Zimmer einheitlich weiß sind, wirkt es größer.","furigana":"へや|かぐ|しろ|とういつ|ひろ|み"}],"notes":"統一 hat zwei Hauptbedeutungen: die politische „Vereinigung“ (国を統一する, 南北統一, oft passiv 統一される) und im Alltag die „Vereinheitlichung“, also etwas auf einen gemeinsamen Standard bringen: 書式を統一する, 色を白で統一する (mit で für das gemeinsame Merkmal). Als transitives する-Verb steht das Objekt mit を. Häufig sind 統一感 „einheitlicher Eindruck“ und 統一性. Abzugrenzen sind 合併 „Fusion“ (Firmen, Gemeinden) und 団結 „Zusammenhalt“ von Menschen.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"建国記念日を祝った。","romaji":"Kenkoku kinenbi o iwatta.","german":"We celebrated National Foundation Day.","furigana":"けんこくきねんび|いわ"},{"japanese":"ワシントンはアメリカ建国の父の一人とされている。","romaji":"Washinton wa Amerika kenkoku no chichi no hitori to sarete iru.","german":"Washington is regarded as one of the founding fathers of America.","furigana":"けんこく|ちち|ひとり"},{"japanese":"建国以来、この国は一度も戦争をしていない。","romaji":"Kenkoku irai, kono kuni wa ichido mo sensō o shite inai.","german":"Since the founding of the nation, this country has never fought a war.","furigana":"けんこくいらい|くに|いちど|せんそう"}],"notes":"建国 is the founding of a state: 建国する, 建国以来 \"since the founding of the nation\", 建国の父 \"founding father\". In Japan, February 11 is the holiday 建国記念の日 (colloquially also 建国記念日). 建国 applies only to states; companies or schools are 設立 or 創立.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"遺跡の発掘調査が行われた。","romaji":"Iseki no hakkutsu chōsa ga okonawareta.","german":"An excavation survey of the ruins was carried out.","furigana":"いせき|はっくつちょうさ|おこな"},{"japanese":"この地域で恐竜の化石が発掘された。","romaji":"Kono chiiki de kyōryū no kaseki ga hakkutsu sareta.","german":"Dinosaur fossils were excavated in this area.","furigana":"ちいき|きょうりゅう|かせき|はっくつ"},{"japanese":"彼女は若い才能を発掘するのが得意なプロデューサーだ。","romaji":"Kanojo wa wakai sainō o hakkutsu suru no ga tokui na purodyūsā da.","german":"She is a producer who is good at discovering young talent."}],"notes":"発掘 is the digging up of something hidden, especially archaeologically: 遺跡を発掘する, 発掘調査 \"excavation\", 化石が発掘される. Figuratively it means \"to discover, unearth\", especially of talent: 新人を発掘する \"to discover new talent\", 人材発掘. For ordinary digging, such as in a garden, people simply say 掘る.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"考古学の研究で新しい発見があった。","romaji":"Kōkogaku no kenkyū de atarashii hakken ga atta.","german":"There was a new discovery in archaeological research.","furigana":"こうこがく|けんきゅう|あたら|はっけん"},{"japanese":"子どものころから考古学者になるのが夢だった。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro kara kōkogakusha ni naru no ga yume datta.","german":"Since childhood it has been my dream to become an archaeologist.","furigana":"こ|こうこがくしゃ|ゆめ"},{"japanese":"その土器は考古学的に非常に価値がある。","romaji":"Sono doki wa kōkogakuteki ni hijō ni kachi ga aru.","german":"That earthenware is of great value archaeologically.","furigana":"どき|こうこがくてき|ひじょう|かち"}],"notes":"考古学 is archaeology, the study of the past through finds and excavations (発掘); literally something like \"the study of investigating the old\". The archaeologist is called 考古学者; typical combinations: 考古学を専攻する, 考古学の研究, 考古学的な発見. To be distinguished from 歴史学, the science of history based on written sources.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は国の英雄として称えられている。","romaji":"Kare wa kuni no eiyū toshite tataerarete iru.","german":"He is praised as a national hero.","furigana":"かれ|くに|えいゆう|たた"},{"japanese":"金メダルを取った選手は、町に帰ると英雄扱いされた。","romaji":"Kinmedaru o totta senshu wa, machi ni kaeru to eiyū atsukai sareta.","german":"The athlete who won the gold medal was treated as a hero on returning to town.","furigana":"きん|と|せんしゅ|まち|かえ|えいゆうあつか"},{"japanese":"ナポレオンはフランスの英雄だと考える人も多い。","romaji":"Naporeon wa Furansu no eiyū da to kangaeru hito mo ōi.","german":"Many people consider Napoleon a hero of France.","furigana":"えいゆう|かんが|ひと|おお"}],"notes":"英雄 is a hero in the grand, often historical or national sense: 国民的英雄, 戦争の英雄, 英雄扱いされる \"to be treated like a hero\". In films, comics and daily life people tend to say ヒーロー; 英雄 sounds more solemn and literary. The word applies to men and women alike. Also well known is Beethoven's symphony 英雄 (\"Eroica\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ローマ皇帝は絶大な権力を持っていた。","romaji":"Rōma kōtei wa zetsudai na kenryoku o motte ita.","german":"The Roman emperor held enormous power.","furigana":"こうてい|ぜつだい|けんりょく|も"},{"japanese":"清の最後の皇帝は、まだ子どもだった。","romaji":"Shin no saigo no kōtei wa, mada kodomo datta.","german":"The last emperor of the Qing was still a child.","furigana":"|さいご|こうてい|こ"},{"japanese":"その映画は、ある皇帝の一生を描いている。","romaji":"Sono eiga wa, aru kōtei no isshō o egaite iru.","german":"That film depicts the life of a certain emperor.","furigana":"えいが|こうてい|いっしょう|えが"}],"notes":"皇帝 is an emperor, especially as the title of foreign rulers: ローマ皇帝, 中国の皇帝, 皇帝に即位する \"to ascend the imperial throne\". The Japanese emperor, by contrast, is called 天皇 (respectfully 天皇陛下), never 皇帝. The realm ruled by an emperor is a 帝国; a king is 王 or 国王.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"王が国を治めていた。","romaji":"Ō ga kuni o osamete ita.","german":"The king ruled the country.","furigana":"おう|くに|おさ"},{"japanese":"ライオンは百獣の王と呼ばれている。","romaji":"Raion wa hyakujū no ō to yobarete iru.","german":"The lion is called the king of beasts.","furigana":"ひゃくじゅう|おう|よ"},{"japanese":"エジソンは発明王として知られている。","romaji":"Ejison wa hatsumei-ō toshite shirarete iru.","german":"Edison is known as the \"king of inventors\".","furigana":"はつめいおう|し"}],"notes":"王 is the king or ruler. On its own it appears more in history books, fairy tales and news; in conversation one usually says 王様, and for real heads of state 国王. Queen is 女王, prince 王子. As a suffix, 王 denotes the best in a field: 発明王 \"king of inventors\", ホームラン王; 百獣の王 \"king of beasts\" is the lion.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"貴族は豪華な生活を送っていた。","romaji":"Kizoku wa gōka na seikatsu o okutte ita.","german":"The aristocrats led a luxurious life.","furigana":"きぞく|ごうか|せいかつ|おく"},{"japanese":"平安時代の貴族は、和歌を作って楽しんだ。","romaji":"Heian jidai no kizoku wa, waka o tsukutte tanoshinda.","german":"Aristocrats in the Heian period enjoyed composing waka poems.","furigana":"へいあんじだい|きぞく|わか|つく|たの"},{"japanese":"兄は独身貴族で、給料を全部自分の趣味に使っている。","romaji":"Ani wa dokushin kizoku de, kyūryō o zenbu jibun no shumi ni tsukatte iru.","german":"My older brother is a carefree bachelor and spends his entire salary on his hobbies.","furigana":"あに|どくしんきぞく|きゅうりょう|ぜんぶじぶん|しゅみ|つか"}],"notes":"貴族 is the nobility or a noble, i.e. a privileged upper class by birth: ヨーロッパの貴族, 平安時代の貴族, 貴族社会. For the Japanese court nobility, 公家 is also common, and for the warrior class 武士. Figuratively, 〜貴族 describes people with a carefree, luxurious lifestyle, e.g. 独身貴族 \"well-paid single who lives only for himself\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"武士は刀を持って戦った。","romaji":"Bushi wa katana o motte tatakatta.","german":"Samurai fought with swords.","furigana":"ぶし|かたな|も|たたか"},{"japanese":"江戸時代、武士は人口の一割にも満たなかった。","romaji":"Edo jidai, bushi wa jinkō no ichiwari ni mo mitanakatta.","german":"In the Edo period, samurai made up less than one tenth of the population.","furigana":"えどじだい|ぶし|じんこう|いちわり|み"},{"japanese":"「武士に二言はない」と言って、父は約束を守った。","romaji":"\"Bushi ni nigon wa nai\" to itte, chichi wa yakusoku o mamotta.","german":"\"A samurai does not go back on his word,\" said my father, and he kept his promise.","furigana":"ぶし|にごん|い|ちち|やくそく|まも"}],"notes":"武士 denotes the Japanese warrior or samurai as a class, especially from the Heian to the Edo period. 武士 is the neutral, historical word; 侍 (often written サムライ) sounds more popular and is readily used for national teams (侍ジャパン). Typical: 武士道 \"the way of the warrior\", 武士の家に生まれる, and the saying 武士に二言はない \"a samurai stands by his word\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"信仰の自由は憲法で保障されている。","romaji":"Shinkō no jiyū wa kenpō de hoshō sarete iru.","german":"Freedom of religion is guaranteed by the constitution.","furigana":"しんこう|じゆう|けんぽう|ほしょう"},{"japanese":"祖母は信仰が厚く、毎朝お寺にお参りしている。","romaji":"Sobo wa shinkō ga atsuku, maiasa otera ni omairi shite iru.","german":"My grandmother is deeply devout and visits the temple every morning.","furigana":"そぼ|しんこう|あつ|まいあさ|てら|まい"},{"japanese":"富士山は古くから信仰の対象とされてきた。","romaji":"Fujisan wa furuku kara shinkō no taishō to sarete kita.","german":"Mt. Fuji has been an object of worship since ancient times.","furigana":"ふじさん|ふる|しんこう|たいしょう"}],"notes":"信仰 is religious faith or the worship of gods or the sacred: 信仰を持つ, 信仰が厚い \"to be deeply religious\", 山岳信仰 \"mountain worship\"; as a する-verb it is transitive: 仏教を信仰する. Unlike 信じる or 信用 (to trust, to believe true), 信仰 is always religious or quasi-religious. Religion as an institution is 宗教.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本では多くの神が信仰されている。","romaji":"Nihon de wa ōku no kami ga shinkō sarete iru.","german":"Many gods are worshipped in Japan.","furigana":"にほん|おお|かみ|しんこう"},{"japanese":"試験の前に神社で神様に合格をお願いした。","romaji":"Shiken no mae ni jinja de kamisama ni gōkaku o onegai shita.","german":"Before the exam, I prayed to the gods at the shrine for success.","furigana":"しけん|まえ|じんじゃ|かみさま|ごうかく|ねが"},{"japanese":"あの店員さんの対応は神だった。","romaji":"Ano ten'in-san no taiō wa kami datta.","german":"The way that clerk served me was simply amazing.","furigana":"てんいん|たいおう|かみ"}],"notes":"神 is a god or deity; in Shinto there are countless 神 (八百万の神), in Christianity it is the one God. In daily life one respectfully says 神様: 神様にお願いする. In youth slang, 神 also means \"awesome, divine\": 神対応 \"superb service\", この漫画は神. Do not confuse with the homophones 紙 \"paper\" and 髪 \"hair\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仏に手を合わせてお祈りした。","romaji":"Hotoke ni te o awasete oinori shita.","german":"I put my hands together before the Buddha and prayed.","furigana":"ほとけ|て|あ|いの"},{"japanese":"知らぬが仏で、彼はまだ何も気づいていない。","romaji":"Shiranu ga hotoke de, kare wa mada nani mo kizuite inai.","german":"Ignorance is bliss - he still hasn't noticed anything.","furigana":"し|ほとけ|かれ|なに|き"},{"japanese":"亡くなった祖父も、仏様になって私たちを見守ってくれているはずだ。","romaji":"Nakunatta sofu mo, hotokesama ni natte watashitachi o mimamotte kurete iru hazu da.","german":"Our late grandfather, too, must be watching over us as a buddha.","furigana":"な|そふ|ほとけさま|わたし|みまも"}],"notes":"仏 denotes the Buddha or a Buddha statue; in daily life one respectfully says 仏様. Euphemistically, 仏 also stands for a deceased person (仏になる \"to die\"; in crime stories, police say 仏さん about the corpse). Idioms: 知らぬが仏 \"ignorance is bliss\", 仏の顔も三度まで \"even Buddha's patience has limits\". In compounds such as 仏教 or 仏像, 仏 is read ぶつ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"毎週日曜日に教会に通っている。","romaji":"Maishū nichiyōbi ni kyōkai ni kayotte iru.","german":"I go to church every Sunday.","furigana":"まいしゅうにちようび|きょうかい|かよ"},{"japanese":"この町には古い教会があります。","romaji":"Kono machi ni wa furui kyōkai ga arimasu.","german":"There is an old church in this town.","furigana":"まち|ふる|きょうかい"},{"japanese":"姉は教会で結婚式を挙げました。","romaji":"Ane wa kyōkai de kekkonshiki o agemashita.","german":"My older sister had her wedding in a church.","furigana":"あね|きょうかい|けっこんしき|あ"}],"notes":"教会 is a Christian church, both the building and the congregation. To go regularly is 教会に通う, to get married in a church is 教会で結婚式を挙げる. For Buddhist temples one says 寺, for Shinto shrines 神社. The identically read 協会 means \"association, society\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 31"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"聖書は世界で最も読まれている本だ。","romaji":"Seisho wa sekai de mottomo yomarete iru hon da.","german":"The Bible is the most widely read book in the world.","furigana":"せいしょ|せかい|もっと|よ|ほん"},{"japanese":"聖書には「隣人を愛せよ」と書かれている。","romaji":"Seisho ni wa \"rinjin o aiseyo\" to kakarete iru.","german":"The Bible says, \"Love your neighbor.\"","furigana":"せいしょ|りんじん|あい|か"},{"japanese":"ホテルの部屋の引き出しに聖書が入っていた。","romaji":"Hoteru no heya no hikidashi ni seisho ga haitte ita.","german":"There was a Bible in the drawer of the hotel room.","furigana":"へや|ひ|だ|せいしょ|はい"}],"notes":"聖書 is the Bible; 旧約聖書 \"Old Testament\", 新約聖書 \"New Testament\". Typical: 聖書を読む, 聖書の言葉, 聖書に書かれている. An authoritative standard work of a field (\"the cooks' bible\"), on the other hand, is called バイブル. Holy scriptures of other religions are called 経典 or are referred to by name, such as コーラン.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"平和への祈りを捧げた。","romaji":"Heiwa e no inori o sasageta.","german":"I offered a prayer for peace.","furigana":"へいわ|いの|ささ"},{"japanese":"教会では毎朝祈りの時間がある。","romaji":"Kyōkai de wa maiasa inori no jikan ga aru.","german":"At the church there is prayer time every morning.","furigana":"きょうかい|まいあさいの|じかん"},{"japanese":"千羽鶴には、早く元気になってほしいという祈りが込められている。","romaji":"Senbazuru ni wa, hayaku genki ni natte hoshii to iu inori ga komerarete iru.","german":"The thousand paper cranes carry the prayer that the person will get well soon.","furigana":"せんばづる|はや|げんき|いの|こ"}],"notes":"祈り is a prayer or a heartfelt wish, the noun of 祈る \"to pray, wish\". Typical: 祈りを捧げる \"to offer a prayer\", 祈りを込める \"to put a heartfelt wish into something\", 平和への祈り. Unlike お願い (a simple request), 祈り has a solemn, religious or emotional ring. The polite お祈り also appears in rejection letters (ご活躍をお祈りいたします), hence the jargon お祈りメール for rejection emails in job hunting.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お盆に先祖の墓参りをする。","romaji":"Obon ni senzo no hakamairi o suru.","german":"During Obon, people visit their ancestors' graves.","furigana":"ぼん|せんぞ|はかまい"},{"japanese":"この秘密はお墓まで持っていくつもりだ。","romaji":"Kono himitsu wa ohaka made motte iku tsumori da.","german":"I intend to take this secret to my grave.","furigana":"ひみつ|はか|も"},{"japanese":"祖父の墓は海の見える丘の上にある。","romaji":"Sofu no haka wa umi no mieru oka no ue ni aru.","german":"My grandfather's grave is on a hill with a view of the sea.","furigana":"そふ|はか|うみ|み|おか|うえ"}],"notes":"墓 is the grave or burial place; politely お墓. A grave visit is called (お)墓参り, typically at お盆 and お彼岸. In Japan, graves are mostly family graves (〜家の墓), in which the urns are interred. Other words: 墓地 \"cemetery\", 墓石 \"gravestone\"; the saying 墓まで持っていく means \"to take a secret to the grave\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"武士の魂は刀に宿ると言われている。","romaji":"Bushi no tamashii wa katana ni yadoru to iwarete iru.","german":"It is said that a samurai's soul resides in his sword.","furigana":"ぶし|たましい|かたな|やど|い"},{"japanese":"職人は一つ一つの作品に魂を込めて作っている。","romaji":"Shokunin wa hitotsu hitotsu no sakuhin ni tamashii o komete tsukutte iru.","german":"The craftsman puts his soul into every single piece.","furigana":"しょくにん|ひと|ひと|さくひん|たましい|こ|つく"},{"japanese":"彼女はショックで魂が抜けたような顔をしていた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa shokku de tamashii ga nuketa yō na kao o shite ita.","german":"She had a face as if her soul had left her from the shock.","furigana":"かのじょ|たましい|ぬ|かお"}],"notes":"魂 is the soul, which in traditional belief outlives death, and also spirit, vital force or devotion: 魂を込める \"to put one's whole soul into it\", 魂が抜けたよう \"as if lifeless, completely absent-minded\". As a suffix it denotes a typical mindset: 大和魂 \"Japanese spirit\", 職人魂 \"craftsman's ethos\". Compared with 心 (heart, feeling), 魂 sounds deeper and more solemn.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は天国で安らかに眠っているだろう。","romaji":"Kanojo wa tengoku de yasuraka ni nemutte iru darō.","german":"She is probably resting peacefully in heaven.","furigana":"かのじょ|てんごく|やす|ねむ"},{"japanese":"週末になると、この通りは歩行者天国になる。","romaji":"Shūmatsu ni naru to, kono tōri wa hokōsha tengoku ni naru.","german":"On weekends, this street becomes a pedestrian paradise.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|とお|ほこうしゃてんごく"},{"japanese":"仕事の後の温泉は、まさに天国だ。","romaji":"Shigoto no ato no onsen wa, masa ni tengoku da.","german":"A hot spring after work is pure heaven.","furigana":"しごと|あと|おんせん|てんごく"}],"notes":"天国 is heaven or paradise in the religious sense, especially in Christianity: 天国へ行く, 天国の母. The physical sky, by contrast, is 空, and the Buddhist paradise is more 極楽. Figuratively, 天国 denotes a wonderful place or state (ここは天国だ) and, as a suffix, a place full of ...: 歩行者天国 \"street temporarily closed to cars\". Antonym: 地獄.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仏教では悪いことをすると地獄に落ちると言われている。","romaji":"Bukkyō de wa warui koto o suru to jigoku ni ochiru to iwarete iru.","german":"In Buddhism, it is said that those who do bad things fall into hell.","furigana":"ぶっきょう|わる|じごく|お|い"},{"japanese":"毎朝の満員電車は地獄のようだ。","romaji":"Maiasa no man'in densha wa jigoku no yō da.","german":"The crowded train every morning is like hell.","furigana":"まいあさ|まんいんでんしゃ|じごく"},{"japanese":"別府では温泉の「地獄めぐり」が人気だ。","romaji":"Beppu de wa onsen no \"jigoku meguri\" ga ninki da.","german":"In Beppu, the \"hell tour\" of hot springs is popular.","furigana":"べっぷ|おんせん|じごく|にんき"}],"notes":"地獄 is hell in the religious sense: 地獄に落ちる \"to go to hell\", 天国と地獄. Very common is the figurative use for an agonizing situation: 受験地獄 \"exam hell\", 通勤地獄, 地獄のような毎日. In spa towns such as Beppu, steaming hot springs are also called 地獄 (地獄めぐり). Proverb: 地獄で仏 \"a savior in the hour of greatest need\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"罪を犯した者は罰を受ける。","romaji":"Tsumi o okashita mono wa batsu o ukeru.","german":"Those who commit a crime will be punished.","furigana":"つみ|おか|もの|ばつ|う"},{"japanese":"彼は盗みの罪で逮捕された。","romaji":"Kare wa nusumi no tsumi de taiho sareta.","german":"He was arrested for the crime of theft.","furigana":"かれ|ぬす|つみ|たいほ"},{"japanese":"罪のない子どもたちが戦争の犠牲になった。","romaji":"Tsumi no nai kodomotachi ga sensō no gisei ni natta.","german":"Innocent children became victims of the war.","furigana":"つみ|こ|せんそう|ぎせい"}],"notes":"罪 covers \"crime, offense\" (罪を犯す \"to commit a crime\", 〜の罪で逮捕される \"to be arrested for ...\"), \"sin\" in the religious sense, and \"guilt\" (罪を認める \"to admit guilt\", 罪のない \"innocent\"). As a na-adjective, 罪な means \"cruel, thoughtless\", often jokingly: 罪な人 \"heartbreaker\". 犯罪 is the factual term for crime, 罰 the punishment for it.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大学で哲学を専攻している。","romaji":"Daigaku de tetsugaku o senkō shite iru.","german":"I am majoring in philosophy at university.","furigana":"だいがく|てつがく|せんこう"},{"japanese":"社長には独自の経営哲学がある。","romaji":"Shachō ni wa dokuji no keiei tetsugaku ga aru.","german":"The company president has his own management philosophy.","furigana":"しゃちょう|どくじ|けいえいてつがく"},{"japanese":"「人はなぜ生きるのか」なんて、ずいぶん哲学的な質問だね。","romaji":"\"Hito wa naze ikiru no ka\" nante, zuibun tetsugakuteki na shitsumon da ne.","german":"\"Why do people live?\" - that's quite a philosophical question.","furigana":"ひと|い|てつがくてき|しつもん"}],"notes":"哲学 is philosophy as an academic discipline (哲学を専攻する, 西洋哲学, 哲学者) and, figuratively, a personal philosophy of life or work: 人生哲学, 経営哲学, 彼なりの哲学がある. In daily life, 哲学的 also stands for \"profound, hard to answer\": 哲学的な質問. The word was coined in the Meiji period as a translation of \"philosophy\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"医療倫理について議論されている。","romaji":"Iryō rinri ni tsuite giron sarete iru.","german":"Medical ethics is being debated.","furigana":"いりょうりんり|ぎろん"},{"japanese":"その実験は倫理的に問題があると批判された。","romaji":"Sono jikken wa rinriteki ni mondai ga aru to hihan sareta.","german":"That experiment was criticized as ethically problematic.","furigana":"じっけん|りんりてき|もんだい|ひはん"},{"japanese":"政治家には高い倫理観が求められる。","romaji":"Seijika ni wa takai rinrikan ga motomerareru.","german":"Politicians are expected to have a high sense of ethics.","furigana":"せいじか|たか|りんりかん|もと"}],"notes":"倫理 is ethics or morality as a system of rules for right action, often professional or social: 医療倫理, 企業倫理, 倫理に反する \"to be unethical\", 倫理的な問題, 倫理観 \"moral values\". In high school, 倫理 is also a school subject. Compared with 道徳 (morality as a personal virtue, also the school subject \"moral education\"), 倫理 sounds more abstract and is preferred for professional and scientific questions.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"良心に従って行動するべきだ。","romaji":"Ryōshin ni shitagatte kōdō suru beki da.","german":"You should act according to your conscience.","furigana":"りょうしん|したが|こうどう"},{"japanese":"嘘をついたことに良心が痛んだ。","romaji":"Uso o tsuita koto ni ryōshin ga itanda.","german":"My conscience hurt because I had lied.","furigana":"うそ|りょうしん|いた"},{"japanese":"この店は量が多くて、値段も良心的だ。","romaji":"Kono mise wa ryō ga ōkute, nedan mo ryōshinteki da.","german":"This shop gives large portions and its prices are fair, too.","furigana":"みせ|りょう|おお|ねだん|りょうしんてき"}],"notes":"良心 is the conscience, the inner voice for good and evil. Typical: 良心が痛む \"to have a guilty conscience\", 良心に従う \"to follow one's conscience\", formally 良心の呵責 \"pangs of conscience\". 良心的 means \"conscientious, fair\" and in everyday speech is often used for fair prices: 良心的な値段. Do not confuse it with the identically read 両親 \"parents\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"正義のために戦う。","romaji":"Seigi no tame ni tatakau.","german":"I fight for justice.","furigana":"せいぎ|たたか"},{"japanese":"彼は正義感が強く、いじめを見ると黙っていられない。","romaji":"Kare wa seigikan ga tsuyoku, ijime o miru to damatte irarenai.","german":"He has a strong sense of justice and can't stay silent when he sees bullying.","furigana":"かれ|せいぎかん|つよ|み|だま"},{"japanese":"子どもの頃、正義の味方に憧れていた。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, seigi no mikata ni akogarete ita.","german":"As a child, I admired defenders of justice.","furigana":"こ|ころ|せいぎ|みかた|あこが"}],"notes":"正義 is justice in the moral sense, what is right: 正義のために戦う, 正義感が強い \"to have a strong sense of justice\", 正義の味方 \"defender of justice\" (typical for heroes in anime and children's series). Justice in the sense of fair distribution or treatment is more 公平 or 公正. The word sounds serious and is sometimes used ironically when someone thinks they are infallible.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"善と悪の区別は時に難しい。","romaji":"Zen to aku no kubetsu wa toki ni muzukashii.","german":"The distinction between good and evil is sometimes difficult.","furigana":"ぜん|あく|くべつ|とき|むずか"},{"japanese":"暴力は絶対的な悪だと考える人が多い。","romaji":"Bōryoku wa zettaiteki na aku da to kangaeru hito ga ōi.","german":"Many people think violence is an absolute evil.","furigana":"ぼうりょく|ぜったいてき|あく|かんが|ひと|おお"},{"japanese":"この映画では、主人公が悪の組織と戦う。","romaji":"Kono eiga de wa, shujinkō ga aku no soshiki to tatakau.","german":"In this film, the protagonist fights an evil organization.","furigana":"えいが|しゅじんこう|あく|そしき|たたか"}],"notes":"悪 is evil as an abstract principle, mostly in contrast to 善: 善と悪, 悪と戦う, 社会の悪 \"social evil\". In everyday speech, bad things are described with the adjective 悪い; 悪 on its own sounds abstract, philosophical or dramatic, as in films: 悪の組織 \"villain organization\". As a prefix it forms words such as 悪影響 \"bad influence\", 悪循環 \"vicious circle\", 悪人 \"villain\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"哲学者は真理を追究する。","romaji":"Tetsugakusha wa shinri o tsuikyū suru.","german":"Philosophers pursue the truth.","furigana":"てつがくしゃ|しんり|ついきゅう"},{"japanese":"彼の意見にも一面の真理がある。","romaji":"Kare no iken ni mo ichimen no shinri ga aru.","german":"There is some truth in his opinion too.","furigana":"かれ|いけん|いちめん|しんり"},{"japanese":"「急がば回れ」は人生の真理だと思う。","romaji":"\"Isogaba maware\" wa jinsei no shinri da to omou.","german":"I think \"Haste makes waste\" is a truth of life.","furigana":"いそ|まわ|じんせい|しんり|おも"}],"notes":"真理 is universally valid, often philosophical or scientific \"truth\": 真理を追究する／探究する, 永遠の真理, 普遍的な真理. For the truth about a concrete matter (what really happened) one says 真実, and in everyday speech simply 本当. The phrase 一面の真理 means \"a grain of truth\". The word sounds elevated and appears mainly in texts, speeches and discussions.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"感情ではなく理性で判断するべきだ。","romaji":"Kanjō de wa naku risei de handan suru beki da.","german":"You should judge by reason, not emotion.","furigana":"かんじょう|りせい|はんだん"},{"japanese":"怒りのあまり理性を失って、大声を出してしまった。","romaji":"Ikari no amari risei o ushinatte, ōgoe o dashite shimatta.","german":"I lost my composure in anger and shouted.","furigana":"いか|りせい|うしな|おおごえ|だ"},{"japanese":"人間は理性を持つ動物だと言われる。","romaji":"Ningen wa risei o motsu dōbutsu da to iwareru.","german":"Humans are said to be animals possessing reason.","furigana":"にんげん|りせい|も|どうぶつ|い"}],"notes":"理性 is \"reason\" as the ability to judge objectively and thoughtfully, usually in contrast to 感情 (\"emotion\") or 本能 (\"instinct\"). Typical are 理性を失う (\"to lose one's composure/reason\"), 理性を保つ, 理性で判断する; the adjective is 理性的な. Do not confuse it with 理由 (\"reason, cause\") or 知性 (\"intellect\"). Rather literary, but also understood in conversation.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人間の存在の意味を考える。","romaji":"Ningen no sonzai no imi o kangaeru.","german":"I think about the meaning of human existence.","furigana":"にんげん|そんざい|いみ|かんが"},{"japanese":"宇宙人は本当に存在すると思いますか。","romaji":"Uchūjin wa hontō ni sonzai suru to omoimasu ka.","german":"Do you think aliens really exist?","furigana":"うちゅうじん|ほんとう|そんざい|おも"},{"japanese":"彼はチームにとって欠かせない存在だ。","romaji":"Kare wa chīmu ni totte kakasenai sonzai da.","german":"He is an indispensable presence for the team.","furigana":"かれ|か|そんざい"}],"notes":"存在 means \"existence, being\" and, as an intransitive する-verb, \"to exist\": 〜が存在する is the formal equivalent of ある／いる. Also common is the meaning \"a person with a certain role\": 欠かせない存在, 大きな存在, 母のような存在. Other combinations: 存在を知る, 存在感 (\"presence\"), 存在意義 (\"reason for being\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人によって価値観は異なる。","romaji":"Hito ni yotte kachikan wa kotonaru.","german":"Values differ from person to person.","furigana":"ひと|かちかん|こと"},{"japanese":"価値観の違いで彼と別れた。","romaji":"Kachikan no chigai de kare to wakareta.","german":"I broke up with him because of differing values.","furigana":"かちかん|ちが|かれ|わか"},{"japanese":"自分の価値観を他人に押し付けないほうがいい。","romaji":"Jibun no kachikan o tanin ni oshitsukenai hō ga ii.","german":"You shouldn't impose your own values on others.","furigana":"じぶん|かちかん|たにん|お|つ"}],"notes":"価値観 are one's personal \"values\", i.e. what a person considers important in life. Very common in relationships: 価値観が合う／合わない, 価値観の違い (a classic reason for breaking up), 価値観を押し付ける (\"to impose one's values\"). In contrast to 価値 (the \"value\" of a thing), the focus here is a person's outlook. Neutral and very common in everyday speech.","levelNote":"Compound: 価値 (N3) + 観 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"恋人と映画を見に行った。","romaji":"Koibito to eiga o mi ni itta.","german":"I went to see a movie with my boyfriend/girlfriend.","furigana":"こいびと|えいが|み|い"},{"japanese":"日本では、クリスマスを恋人と過ごす人が多い。","romaji":"Nihon de wa, kurisumasu o koibito to sugosu hito ga ōi.","german":"In Japan, many people spend Christmas with their partner.","furigana":"にほん|こいびと|す|ひと|おお"},{"japanese":"恋人ができたら、一緒に旅行に行きたい。","romaji":"Koibito ga dekitara, issho ni ryokō ni ikitai.","german":"When I have a girlfriend/boyfriend, I want to go on a trip together.","furigana":"こいびと|いっしょ|りょこう|い"}],"notes":"恋人 is the neutral umbrella term for \"partner in a romantic relationship\" and applies to men and women alike. In conversation one usually says 彼氏 or 彼女; 恋人 sounds somewhat more written or romantic, but is often used in surveys and songs, for example. Typical: 恋人ができる, 恋人がいる, 恋人同士. It is not used for spouses.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 32"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"配偶者の同意が必要だ。","romaji":"Haigūsha no dōi ga hitsuyō da.","german":"Die Zustimmung des Ehepartners ist erforderlich.","furigana":"はいぐうしゃ|どうい|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"配偶者のいる方は、こちらの欄にチェックを入れてください。","romaji":"Haigūsha no iru kata wa, kochira no ran ni chekku o irete kudasai.","german":"Wer verheiratet ist, kreuzt bitte dieses Feld an.","furigana":"はいぐうしゃ|かた|らん|い"},{"japanese":"彼女は日本人の配偶者としてビザを取った。","romaji":"Kanojo wa Nihonjin no haigūsha to shite biza o totta.","german":"Sie hat ein Visum als Ehefrau eines Japaners bekommen.","furigana":"かのじょ|にほんじん|はいぐうしゃ|と"}],"notes":"配偶者 ist der amtliche, juristische Begriff für „Ehepartner“ und kommt vor allem in Formularen, Gesetzen und Behördendeutsch vor: 配偶者控除 (Steuerfreibetrag), 配偶者ビザ, 配偶者の有無. Im Gespräch sagt man stattdessen 夫／妻, höflich ご主人／奥さん, oder neutral パートナー. Über sich selbst sagt man kaum „mein 配偶者“.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新郎がスピーチをした。","romaji":"Shinrō ga supīchi o shita.","german":"The groom gave a speech.","furigana":"しんろう"},{"japanese":"それでは、新郎新婦の入場です。","romaji":"Sore de wa, shinrō shinpu no nyūjō desu.","german":"And now, the bride and groom enter.","furigana":"しんろうしんぷ|にゅうじょう"},{"japanese":"私は新郎の大学時代の友人です。","romaji":"Watashi wa shinrō no daigaku jidai no yūjin desu.","german":"I am a friend of the groom from his university days.","furigana":"わたし|しんろう|だいがくじだい|ゆうじん"}],"notes":"新郎 is the \"bridegroom\" in the context of the wedding celebration, very often in the pair 新郎新婦 (\"bride and groom\"). It is heard mainly in wedding announcements and speeches: 新郎側の親族, 新郎の友人. Outside a wedding one simply says 夫 or 彼. The more everyday, somewhat more poetic word is 花婿.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新婦は美しいドレスを着ていた。","romaji":"Shinpu wa utsukushii doresu o kite ita.","german":"The bride wore a beautiful dress.","furigana":"しんぷ|うつく|き"},{"japanese":"新婦の父親が、式の間ずっと泣いていた。","romaji":"Shinpu no chichioya ga, shiki no aida zutto naite ita.","german":"The bride's father cried throughout the ceremony.","furigana":"しんぷ|ちちおや|しき|あいだ|な"},{"japanese":"新婦の友人代表としてスピーチを頼まれた。","romaji":"Shinpu no yūjin daihyō to shite supīchi o tanomareta.","german":"I was asked to give a speech on behalf of the bride's friends.","furigana":"しんぷ|ゆうじんだいひょう|たの"}],"notes":"新婦 is the \"bride\" during the wedding celebration, usually in the pair 新郎新婦. Typical in announcements and speeches: 新婦の父, 新婦側の席, 新婦の友人代表. The more everyday word is 花嫁 (also 花嫁衣装). Beware of the homophone: 神父 (しんぷ) is a Catholic \"priest\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二人は先月婚約した。","romaji":"Futari wa sengetsu kon'yaku shita.","german":"The two got engaged last month.","furigana":"ふたり|せんげつこんやく"},{"japanese":"誕生日に彼から婚約指輪をもらった。","romaji":"Tanjōbi ni kare kara kon'yaku yubiwa o moratta.","german":"I received an engagement ring from him on my birthday.","furigana":"たんじょうび|かれ|こんやくゆびわ"},{"japanese":"話し合った結果、二人は婚約を解消することにした。","romaji":"Hanashiatta kekka, futari wa kon'yaku o kaishō suru koto ni shita.","german":"After talking it over, the two decided to break off their engagement.","furigana":"はな|あ|けっか|ふたり|こんやく|かいしょう"}],"notes":"婚約 is \"engagement\"; as an intransitive する-verb, 〜と婚約する means \"to get engaged to someone\". Common compounds: 婚約者 (\"fiance(e)\"), 婚約指輪 (\"engagement ring\"), 婚約発表. Breaking off an engagement is 婚約を解消する (by mutual agreement) or 婚約を破棄する (unilaterally). Neutral to slightly formal.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"離婚率が年々上がっている。","romaji":"Rikonritsu ga nennen agatte iru.","german":"Die Scheidungsrate steigt von Jahr zu Jahr.","furigana":"りこんりつ|ねんねんあ"},{"japanese":"両親は私が十歳のときに離婚した。","romaji":"Ryōshin wa watashi ga jussai no toki ni rikon shita.","german":"Meine Eltern haben sich scheiden lassen, als ich zehn war.","furigana":"りょうしん|わたし|じゅっさい|りこん"},{"japanese":"二人は話し合いの末、離婚届を役所に出した。","romaji":"Futari wa hanashiai no sue, rikon todoke o yakusho ni dashita.","german":"Nach langen Gesprächen reichten die beiden die Scheidungspapiere beim Amt ein.","furigana":"ふたり|はな|あ|すえ|りこんとどけ|やくしょ|だ"}],"notes":"離婚 ist die „Scheidung“; als intransitives する-Verb: 〜と離婚する („sich von jemandem scheiden lassen“), ohne Passiv wie im Deutschen. Typisch: 離婚届 (Scheidungsformular beim Amt), 離婚が成立する, 離婚率, 熟年離婚 (Scheidung im Alter). Die bloße Trennung eines unverheirateten Paares heißt dagegen 別れる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は再婚して幸せに暮らしている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa saikon shite shiawase ni kurashite iru.","german":"She remarried and is living happily.","furigana":"かのじょ|さいこん|しあわ|く"},{"japanese":"父は母が亡くなって五年後に再婚した。","romaji":"Chichi wa haha ga nakunatte gonen go ni saikon shita.","german":"My father remarried five years after my mother died.","furigana":"ちち|はは|な|ごねんご|さいこん"},{"japanese":"再婚相手には小学生の子どもが二人いる。","romaji":"Saikon aite ni wa shōgakusei no kodomo ga futari iru.","german":"My new spouse has two children in elementary school.","furigana":"さいこんあいて|しょうがくせい|こ|ふたり"}],"notes":"再婚 is \"remarriage\"; as an intransitive する-verb: 〜と再婚する. The new partner is called 再婚相手. The counterpart is 初婚 (\"first marriage\"), e.g. in statistics. The word is neutral and is used both after divorce and after being widowed.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"既婚者向けのイベントが開催された。","romaji":"Kikonsha muke no ibento ga kaisai sareta.","german":"An event for married people was held.","furigana":"きこんしゃむ|かいさい"},{"japanese":"アンケートの「既婚・未婚」の欄に丸をつけた。","romaji":"Ankēto no \"kikon / mikon\" no ran ni maru o tsuketa.","german":"I circled \"married\" in the \"married/unmarried\" box of the questionnaire.","furigana":"きこん|みこん|らん|まる"},{"japanese":"この調査は既婚の男性社員を対象に行われた。","romaji":"Kono chōsa wa kikon no dansei shain o taishō ni okonawareta.","german":"This survey was conducted on married male employees.","furigana":"ちょうさ|きこん|だんせいしゃいん|たいしょう|おこな"}],"notes":"既婚 (\"married\") almost never stands alone but in compounds or with の: 既婚者, 既婚の女性, 既婚・未婚. It is a formal, often statistical or official expression. In conversation, people say 結婚している. Antonyms are 未婚 (\"never married\") and 独身 (\"unattached\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"未婚率が上昇している。","romaji":"Mikonritsu ga jōshō shite iru.","german":"The rate of unmarried people is rising.","furigana":"みこんりつ|じょうしょう"},{"japanese":"彼女は未婚の母として一人で子どもを育てた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa mikon no haha to shite hitori de kodomo o sodateta.","german":"She raised her child alone as an unmarried mother.","furigana":"かのじょ|みこん|はは|ひとり|こ|そだ"},{"japanese":"四十代で未婚の人も今では珍しくない。","romaji":"Yonjūdai de mikon no hito mo ima de wa mezurashiku nai.","german":"It is no longer unusual for people in their forties to be unmarried.","furigana":"よんじゅうだい|みこん|ひと|いま|めずら"}],"notes":"未婚 means \"(still) never married\" and is used with の or in compounds: 未婚の母, 未婚率, 未婚化, 未婚者. Unlike 独身 (\"unattached, single\"), which also includes divorced people, 未婚 refers only to people who have never been married. Rather formal and common in statistics or news.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"恋愛小説を読むのが好きだ。","romaji":"Ren'ai shōsetsu o yomu no ga suki da.","german":"I like reading romance novels.","furigana":"れんあいしょうせつ|よ|す"},{"japanese":"両親はお見合いではなく、恋愛結婚だった。","romaji":"Ryōshin wa omiai de wa naku, ren'ai kekkon datta.","german":"My parents did not have an arranged marriage but a love marriage.","furigana":"りょうしん|みあ|れんあいけっこん"},{"japanese":"今は仕事が忙しくて、恋愛どころではない。","romaji":"Ima wa shigoto ga isogashikute, ren'ai dokoro de wa nai.","german":"I'm so busy with work right now that romance is out of the question.","furigana":"いま|しごと|いそが|れんあい"}],"notes":"恋愛 refers to romantic love as a relationship or topic, in a rather matter-of-fact way: 恋愛小説, 恋愛結婚 (love marriage, as opposed to お見合い結婚), 恋愛感情, 恋愛中. As an intransitive する-verb (恋愛する) it is less common than 付き合う. The feeling of being in love itself is more 恋 (恋をする), all-embracing love 愛.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 32"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"高校時代に片思いをしていた。","romaji":"Kōkō jidai ni kataomoi o shite ita.","german":"I had an unrequited love in high school.","furigana":"こうこうじだい|かたおも"},{"japanese":"ずっと片思いしていた先輩に、やっと告白した。","romaji":"Zutto kataomoi shite ita senpai ni, yatto kokuhaku shita.","german":"I finally confessed to the senior I'd had a crush on for so long.","furigana":"かたおも|せんぱい|こくはく"},{"japanese":"結局、その恋は片思いのまま終わった。","romaji":"Kekkyoku, sono koi wa kataomoi no mama owatta.","german":"In the end, that love ended as unrequited.","furigana":"けっきょく|こい|かたおも|お"}],"notes":"片思い (literally \"one-sided thinking of someone\") is \"unrequited love\", when the other person does not know or share the feelings. Typical: 〜に片思いする／している, 片思いの相手, 片思いのまま終わる. The opposite is 両思い (\"mutual love\"). Neutral to colloquial, very common in daily life.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"失恋して落ち込んでいる。","romaji":"Shitsuren shite ochikonde iru.","german":"I'm down because I got my heart broken.","furigana":"しつれん|お|こ"},{"japanese":"失恋した友達を一晩中慰めた。","romaji":"Shitsuren shita tomodachi o hitobanjū nagusameta.","german":"I comforted my friend, who had just been dumped, all night.","furigana":"しつれん|ともだち|ひとばんじゅうなぐさ"},{"japanese":"失恋の痛みは、時間が解決してくれるはずだ。","romaji":"Shitsuren no itami wa, jikan ga kaiketsu shite kureru hazu da.","german":"The pain of lost love will surely be healed by time.","furigana":"しつれん|いた|じかん|かいけつ"}],"notes":"失恋 refers to the failure of a love, being rejected or left, and as an intransitive する-verb (失恋する) means \"to be turned down, to be heartbroken\". Typical: 失恋の痛み, 失恋から立ち直る, 失恋した友達を慰める. Unlike 片思い (love that is not yet returned), 失恋 stands for the painful end. Neutral, common in everyday speech.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"浮気が原因で離婚した。","romaji":"Uwaki ga gen'in de rikon shita.","german":"We divorced because of infidelity.","furigana":"うわき|げんいん|りこん"},{"japanese":"彼氏の浮気がばれて、大げんかになった。","romaji":"Kareshi no uwaki ga barete, ōgenka ni natta.","german":"My boyfriend's cheating came out and we had a huge fight.","furigana":"かれし|うわき|おお"},{"japanese":"いつもはこの店のパンだけど、今日はちょっと浮気して隣の店で買った。","romaji":"Itsumo wa kono mise no pan da kedo, kyō wa chotto uwaki shite tonari no mise de katta.","german":"I usually buy bread from this shop, but today I cheated a little and bought it from the shop next door.","furigana":"みせ|きょう|うわき|となり|みせ|か"}],"notes":"浮気 is \"cheating, infidelity\" in a relationship, whether married or not: 浮気する, 浮気がばれる, 浮気相手. 不倫 is narrower and refers to an affair in which at least one party is married. As a な-adjective or in 浮気性 it means \"fickle, unfaithful\". Colloquially also jokingly for \"trying something different\" instead of one's usual brand.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子供に愛情を注ぐことが大切だ。","romaji":"Kodomo ni aijō o sosogu koto ga taisetsu da.","german":"It is important to give children affection.","furigana":"こども|あいじょう|そそ|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"母は毎朝、愛情を込めてお弁当を作ってくれた。","romaji":"Haha wa maiasa, aijō o komete obentō o tsukutte kureta.","german":"My mother made me a packed lunch with lots of love every morning.","furigana":"はは|まいあさ|あいじょう|こ|べんとう|つく"},{"japanese":"父は家族への愛情を言葉で表すのが苦手だ。","romaji":"Chichi wa kazoku e no aijō o kotoba de arawasu no ga nigate da.","german":"My father has trouble expressing his love for the family in words.","furigana":"ちち|かぞく|あいじょう|ことば|あらわ|にがて"}],"notes":"愛情 is \"affection, love\" as a warm feeling toward family, children, animals or partners - broader than romantic 恋愛. Typical combinations: 愛情を注ぐ (\"to give love\"), 愛情を込めて (\"with love\"), 愛情たっぷり, 愛情表現. It is a noun; as a verb one uses 愛する or かわいがる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二人の友情は長年続いている。","romaji":"Futari no yūjō wa naganen tsuzuite iru.","german":"Their friendship has lasted for many years.","furigana":"ふたり|ゆうじょう|ながねんつづ"},{"japanese":"同じ部活で練習するうちに、二人の間に友情が芽生えた。","romaji":"Onaji bukatsu de renshū suru uchi ni, futari no aida ni yūjō ga mebaeta.","german":"While practicing in the same club, a friendship grew between the two.","furigana":"おな|ぶかつ|れんしゅう|ふたり|あいだ|ゆうじょう|めば"},{"japanese":"お金の貸し借りで友情が壊れることもある。","romaji":"Okane no kashikari de yūjō ga kowareru koto mo aru.","german":"Sometimes a friendship is ruined over lending and borrowing money.","furigana":"かね|か|か|ゆうじょう|こわ"}],"notes":"友情 is \"friendship\" as a feeling or relationship, not the person (that would be 友達／友人). Typical: 友情が芽生える (\"a friendship develops\"), 友情を深める, 友情が壊れる, 友情と恋愛. Neutral, often in stories, speeches and films; in conversation one also says 仲がいい.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"家族の絆を大切にしている。","romaji":"Kazoku no kizuna o taisetsu ni shite iru.","german":"I cherish family bonds.","furigana":"かぞく|きずな|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"震災をきっかけに、地域の絆が強まった。","romaji":"Shinsai o kikkake ni, chiiki no kizuna ga tsuyomatta.","german":"After the earthquake, the bonds within the community grew stronger.","furigana":"しんさい|ちいき|きずな|つよ"},{"japanese":"一緒に苦しい練習を乗り越えて、チームの絆が深まった。","romaji":"Issho ni kurushii renshū o norikoete, chīmu no kizuna ga fukamatta.","german":"Having gotten through the hard training together, the team's bond has deepened.","furigana":"いっしょ|くる|れんしゅう|の|こ|きずな|ふか"}],"notes":"絆 is the emotional \"bond\" between people, e.g. in a family, team or neighborhood: 家族の絆, 絆が深まる／強まる, 絆を深める. After the 2011 earthquake it became a key word for solidarity (Kanji of the Year 2011). The original meaning \"fetter, leash\" is rare today. Positive and somewhat sentimental; occasionally also written in kana as きずな.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"知人の紹介で仕事を見つけた。","romaji":"Chijin no shōkai de shigoto o mitsuketa.","german":"I found a job through an acquaintance's introduction.","furigana":"ちじん|しょうかい|しごと|み"},{"japanese":"知人から聞いた話ですが、あの店は来月閉店するそうです。","romaji":"Chijin kara kiita hanashi desu ga, ano mise wa raigetsu heiten suru sō desu.","german":"This is something I heard from an acquaintance, but that store is reportedly closing next month.","furigana":"ちじん|き|はなし|みせ|らいげつへいてん"},{"japanese":"彼は友人というより、仕事上の知人です。","romaji":"Kare wa yūjin to iu yori, shigotojō no chijin desu.","german":"He is less a friend than a professional acquaintance.","furigana":"かれ|ゆうじん|しごとじょう|ちじん"}],"notes":"知人 is an \"acquaintance\", someone you know without being close friends. It is the more formal counterpart of 知り合い and sounds natural in news, business contexts and polite speech: 知人の紹介, 知人から聞いた話. 友人 (\"friend\", formal) and 親友 (\"close friend\") express more closeness.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"親友に悩みを相談した。","romaji":"Shin'yū ni nayami o sōdan shita.","german":"I talked to my best friend about my worries.","furigana":"しんゆう|なや|そうだん"},{"japanese":"彼女とは小学校のころからの親友だ。","romaji":"Kanojo to wa shōgakkō no koro kara no shin'yū da.","german":"She has been my best friend since elementary school.","furigana":"かのじょ|しょうがっこう|しんゆう"},{"japanese":"親友だと思っていた人に裏切られた。","romaji":"Shin'yū da to omotte ita hito ni uragirareta.","german":"I was betrayed by someone I thought was my best friend.","furigana":"しんゆう|おも|ひと|うらぎ"}],"notes":"親友 is a \"close, trusted friend\" or \"best friend\" - clearly more than 友達 or 友人. One usually has only a few: 一番の親友, 小学校からの親友, 親友に打ち明ける. Do not confuse with 親戚 (\"relatives\"). Neutral and very common in everyday speech.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"結婚相手を探している。","romaji":"Kekkon aite o sagashite iru.","german":"I'm looking for a marriage partner.","furigana":"けっこんあいて|さが"},{"japanese":"話すときは、相手の気持ちを考えなさい。","romaji":"Hanasu toki wa, aite no kimochi o kangaenasai.","german":"When you talk, think about the other person's feelings.","furigana":"はな|あいて|きも|かんが"},{"japanese":"決勝の相手は去年の優勝チームだ。","romaji":"Kesshō no aite wa kyonen no yūshō chīmu da.","german":"Our opponent in the final is last year's champion team.","furigana":"けっしょう|あいて|きょねん|ゆうしょう"}],"notes":"相手 is \"the other party\", that is, the other person in a relationship or action - depending on context partner, conversation partner or opponent: 結婚相手, 話し相手, 相談相手, 対戦相手, 相手チーム. Important expressions: 〜の相手をする (\"to look after someone, to play with someone\"), 相手にしない (\"not to take seriously, to ignore\"), 相手の気持ちを考える. Neutral and very common.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 33"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女はいつも私の味方だ。","romaji":"Kanojo wa itsumo watashi no mikata da.","german":"She is always on my side.","furigana":"かのじょ|わたし|みかた"},{"japanese":"兄弟げんかになると、母はいつも弟の味方をする。","romaji":"Kyōdai genka ni naru to, haha wa itsumo otōto no mikata o suru.","german":"When we siblings fight, Mom always takes my little brother's side.","furigana":"きょうだい|はは|おとうと|みかた"},{"japanese":"当日は天気も味方して、運動会は大成功だった。","romaji":"Tōjitsu wa tenki mo mikata shite, undōkai wa daiseikō datta.","german":"On the day itself, even the weather was on our side, and the sports day was a great success.","furigana":"とうじつ|てんき|みかた|うんどうかい|だいせいこう"}],"notes":"味方 is an \"ally\" or someone who is on one's own side; the opposite is 敵. Very common: 〜の味方だ, 〜の味方をする (\"to take someone's side\"), 味方につける (\"to win over to one's side\"), 正義の味方. As an intransitive する-verb (〜に味方する) also figuratively: 天気が味方する (\"the weather is on one's side\"). Do not confuse with the homophone 見方 (\"way of looking\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"敵を許すことも時には必要だ。","romaji":"Teki o yurusu koto mo toki ni wa hitsuyō da.","german":"Sometimes it is necessary to forgive an enemy.","furigana":"てき|ゆる|とき|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"あの人を敵に回すと、面倒なことになるよ。","romaji":"Ano hito o teki ni mawasu to, mendō na koto ni naru yo.","german":"If you make an enemy of that person, you'll be in trouble.","furigana":"ひと|てき|まわ|めんどう"},{"japanese":"夜遅くに食べる甘いものはダイエットの敵だ。","romaji":"Yoru osoku ni taberu amai mono wa daietto no teki da.","german":"Sweets late at night are the enemy of any diet.","furigana":"よるおそ|た|あま|てき"}],"notes":"敵 is the \"enemy, opponent\"; the opposite is 味方. Typical: 敵に回す (\"to make an enemy of someone\"), 敵を作る, 敵チーム. Figuratively, 〜の敵 means \"enemy of something, bad for\": ダイエットの敵, 女性の敵. More neutral than 敵 is 相手 or ライバル for sports opponents. In expressions such as 目の敵, the kanji is read かたき.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"他人に迷惑をかけてはいけない。","romaji":"Tanin ni meiwaku o kakete wa ikenai.","german":"You must not cause trouble for others.","furigana":"たにん|めいわく"},{"japanese":"他人の目を気にしすぎないほうがいいよ。","romaji":"Tanin no me o ki ni shisuginai hō ga ii yo.","german":"You shouldn't worry too much about what other people think.","furigana":"たにん|め|き"},{"japanese":"よく似ていると言われるけど、私たちは赤の他人です。","romaji":"Yoku nite iru to iwareru kedo, watashitachi wa aka no tanin desu.","german":"People often say we look alike, but we are complete strangers.","furigana":"に|い|わたし|あか|たにん"}],"notes":"他人 are \"other people\" in contrast to oneself, often with the undertone \"not belonging to the family, strangers\": 他人に迷惑をかける, 他人の目を気にする. 赤の他人 means \"a total stranger\", 他人事 (ひとごと／たにんごと) \"someone else's business, which does not concern one\". More neutral in daily life is 他の人 or simply 人 (人の物を取る). For a \"stranger\" on the street, one rather says 知らない人.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"青年が社会貢献活動に参加した。","romaji":"Seinen ga shakai kōken katsudō ni sanka shita.","german":"The young man took part in community service activities.","furigana":"せいねん|しゃかいこうけんかつどう|さんか"},{"japanese":"彼は礼儀正しくて、さわやかな好青年だ。","romaji":"Kare wa reigi tadashikute, sawayaka na kōseinen da.","german":"He is a polite, refreshing young man.","furigana":"かれ|れいぎただ|こうせいねん"},{"japanese":"祖父は青年時代をブラジルで過ごした。","romaji":"Sofu wa seinen jidai o Burajiru de sugoshita.","german":"My grandfather spent his youth in Brazil.","furigana":"そふ|せいねんじだい|す"}],"notes":"青年 is a \"young man\" or \"young person\" roughly between the end of school and the late twenties; it usually refers to men. The word sounds somewhat literary or elevated: 好青年 (\"likable young man\"), 青年時代, 青年海外協力隊. More neutral and gender-independent is 若者; younger boys are called 少年.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"中年になって健康に気をつけるようになった。","romaji":"Chūnen ni natte kenkō ni ki o tsukeru yō ni natta.","german":"After reaching middle age, I started paying attention to my health.","furigana":"ちゅうねん|けんこう|き"},{"japanese":"中年太りが気になって、毎朝走ることにした。","romaji":"Chūnen butori ga ki ni natte, maiasa hashiru koto ni shita.","german":"I'm bothered by my middle-age spread, so I decided to run every morning.","furigana":"ちゅうねんぶと|き|まいあさはし"},{"japanese":"犯人は中年の男性だったそうだ。","romaji":"Hannin wa chūnen no dansei datta sō da.","german":"The culprit was reportedly a middle-aged man.","furigana":"はんにん|ちゅうねん|だんせい"}],"notes":"中年 is the \"middle period of life\", roughly from the early forties to the mid-fifties, and also refers to people of this age: 中年の男性, 中年になる. Well-known expressions are 中年太り (\"middle-age spread\") and 中高年 (\"middle-aged and older people\"). The word is neutral but can sound slightly unflattering; something like 四十代の方 is more polite.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"幼児向けの教育番組がある。","romaji":"Yōji muke no kyōiku bangumi ga aru.","german":"There are educational programs for small children.","furigana":"ようじむ|きょういくばんぐみ"},{"japanese":"薬は幼児の手の届かない所に保管してください。","romaji":"Kusuri wa yōji no te no todokanai tokoro ni hokan shite kudasai.","german":"Please keep medicine out of the reach of small children.","furigana":"くすり|ようじ|て|とど|ところ|ほかん"},{"japanese":"最近は幼児のころから英語を習わせる親も多い。","romaji":"Saikin wa yōji no koro kara eigo o narawaseru oya mo ōi.","german":"Recently, many parents have their children learn English from early childhood.","furigana":"さいきん|ようじ|えいご|なら|おや|おお"}],"notes":"幼児 is a \"small child\" of roughly one to six years, i.e. before starting school. Distinctions: 乳児 (\"baby\", under one year), 児童 (\"schoolchild\"). The word is factual and appears in notices, medicine and education: 幼児教育, 幼児向け, 乳幼児. In conversation one tends to say 小さい子 or 子ども.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"少年がサッカーをしている。","romaji":"Shōnen ga sakkā o shite iru.","german":"The boy is playing soccer.","furigana":"しょうねん"},{"japanese":"父は少年時代を田舎で過ごした。","romaji":"Chichi wa shōnen jidai o inaka de sugoshita.","german":"My father spent his boyhood in the countryside.","furigana":"ちち|しょうねんじだい|いなか|す"},{"japanese":"少年犯罪のニュースを見るたびに心が痛む。","romaji":"Shōnen hanzai no nyūsu o miru tabi ni kokoro ga itamu.","german":"Every time I see news about juvenile crime, it pains my heart.","furigana":"しょうねんはんざい|み|こころ|いた"}],"notes":"少年 is a \"boy\" roughly from school age to adolescence and sounds somewhat literary or narrative; in daily life one says 男の子. Typical: 少年時代, 少年野球, 少年漫画. In law (少年法, 少年犯罪), 少年 refers to all minors regardless of gender. The female counterpart is 少女.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"少女が公園で遊んでいる。","romaji":"Shōjo ga kōen de asonde iru.","german":"The girl is playing in the park.","furigana":"しょうじょ|こうえん|あそ"},{"japanese":"姉は昔から少女漫画が大好きだ。","romaji":"Ane wa mukashi kara shōjo manga ga daisuki da.","german":"My older sister has loved shojo manga since long ago.","furigana":"あね|むかし|しょうじょまんが|だいす"},{"japanese":"この小説の主人公は十二歳の少女だ。","romaji":"Kono shōsetsu no shujinkō wa jūnisai no shōjo da.","german":"The protagonist of this novel is a twelve-year-old girl.","furigana":"しょうせつ|しゅじんこう|じゅうにさい|しょうじょ"}],"notes":"少女 is a \"girl\" roughly from school age to the teens and sounds somewhat literary, written or factual; in daily life one says 女の子. Typical are 少女漫画 (\"manga for girls\"), 少女時代 and news language such as 十歳の少女. The male counterpart is 少年.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本では十八歳で成人になる。","romaji":"Nihon de wa jūhassai de seijin ni naru.","german":"In Japan, you become an adult at eighteen.","furigana":"にほん|じゅうはっさい|せいじん"},{"japanese":"成人の日に、振袖を着て家族と写真を撮った。","romaji":"Seijin no hi ni, furisode o kite kazoku to shashin o totta.","german":"On Coming of Age Day, I wore a furisode kimono and took photos with my family.","furigana":"せいじん|ひ|ふりそで|き|かぞく|しゃしん|と"},{"japanese":"子どもたちは皆成人して、家を出ていった。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa mina seijin shite, ie o dete itta.","german":"The children have all grown up and left home.","furigana":"こ|みなせいじん|いえ|で"}],"notes":"成人 is the \"adult\" in the legal or formal sense and, as an intransitive する-verb, \"to come of age, to grow up\" (子どもが成人する). In daily life one usually says 大人. Since 2022, the age of majority in Japan is 18; drinking and smoking, however, remain allowed only from 20. Well known are 成人式 and the holiday 成人の日 in January.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"未成年の飲酒は法律で禁止されている。","romaji":"Miseinen no inshu wa hōritsu de kinshi sarete iru.","german":"Drinking by minors is prohibited by law.","furigana":"みせいねん|いんしゅ|ほうりつ|きんし"},{"japanese":"未成年の方がアルバイトをする場合は、保護者の同意が必要です。","romaji":"Miseinen no kata ga arubaito o suru baai wa, hogosha no dōi ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"If a minor wants to take a part-time job, parental consent is required.","furigana":"みせいねん|かた|ばあい|ほごしゃ|どうい|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"事件の容疑者が未成年だったため、名前は公表されなかった。","romaji":"Jiken no yōgisha ga miseinen datta tame, namae wa kōhyō sarenakatta.","german":"The suspect in the case was a minor, so the name was not made public.","furigana":"じけん|ようぎしゃ|みせいねん|なまえ|こうひょう"}],"notes":"未成年 means \"underage\" or \"minor\" (also 未成年者) and is the legal, formal term; it is combined with の: 未成年の子ども, 未成年の飲酒. Since 2022, people under 18 in Japan count as 未成年; alcohol and cigarettes are nevertheless allowed only from 20 (二十歳未満). The opposite is 成人.","levelNote":"Compound: 未 (N1) + 成年 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子供の個性を尊重するべきだ。","romaji":"Kodomo no kosei o sonchō suru beki da.","german":"Children's individuality should be respected.","furigana":"こども|こせい|そんちょう"},{"japanese":"彼女はいつも個性的な服を着ている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa itsumo koseiteki na fuku o kite iru.","german":"She always wears distinctive clothes.","furigana":"かのじょ|こせいてき|ふく|き"},{"japanese":"社員一人一人の個性を生かせる職場を作りたい。","romaji":"Shain hitori hitori no kosei o ikaseru shokuba o tsukuritai.","german":"I want to create a workplace where each employee's individuality can be put to good use.","furigana":"しゃいんひとりひとり|こせい|い|しょくば|つく"}],"notes":"個性 is \"individuality\", i.e. what distinguishes a person or thing from others. Typical: 個性を尊重する, 個性を生かす／伸ばす, 個性が強い; the adjective 個性的な means \"original, distinctive\". 性格, by contrast, is \"character\" in the sense of temperament (性格がいい／明るい). Positive to neutral, very common in education and advertising.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この街には多くの魅力がある。","romaji":"Kono machi ni wa ōku no miryoku ga aru.","german":"This city has a lot of charm.","furigana":"まち|おお|みりょく"},{"japanese":"この仕事の一番の魅力は、いろいろな国の人に会えることだ。","romaji":"Kono shigoto no ichiban no miryoku wa, iroiro na kuni no hito ni aeru koto da.","german":"The best appeal of this job is that you get to meet people from many countries.","furigana":"しごと|いちばん|みりょく|くに|ひと|あ"},{"japanese":"彼の優しい笑顔に魅力を感じた。","romaji":"Kare no yasashii egao ni miryoku o kanjita.","german":"I was drawn to his kind smile.","furigana":"かれ|やさ|えがお|みりょく|かん"}],"notes":"魅力 is \"charm, appeal, attraction\" of people, places or things: 〜に魅力を感じる, 〜の魅力, 魅力がある. The adjective 魅力的な (\"attractive, appealing\") is very common. Unlike かっこいい／かわいい, 魅力 refers not only to appearance but to the overall impression. Neutral, equally common in advertising and conversation.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一人世帯が増えている。","romaji":"Hitori setai ga fuete iru.","german":"Single-person households are increasing.","furigana":"ひとりせたい|ふ"},{"japanese":"住民票には世帯主の名前が書かれている。","romaji":"Jūminhyō ni wa setainushi no namae ga kakarete iru.","german":"The head of household's name is written on the residence certificate.","furigana":"じゅうみんひょう|せたいぬし|なまえ|か"},{"japanese":"一世帯あたりの電気代は、去年より上がった。","romaji":"Hitosetai atari no denkidai wa, kyonen yori agatta.","german":"The electricity cost per household went up compared to last year.","furigana":"ひとせたい|でんきだい|きょねん|あ"}],"notes":"世帯 is the \"household\" as a statistical or administrative unit (people who live and manage finances together): 世帯主 (\"head of household\"), 一人世帯, 共働き世帯, 一世帯あたり. For family life at home people say 家庭, for the people 家族. Do not confuse it with 所帯 (しょたい, \"one's own household\", more colloquial).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"公共の場でのマナーを守ろう。","romaji":"Kōkyō no ba de no manā o mamorō.","german":"Let's observe good manners in public places.","furigana":"こうきょう|ば|まも"},{"japanese":"当日は公共交通機関をご利用ください。","romaji":"Tōjitsu wa kōkyō kōtsū kikan o goriyō kudasai.","german":"On the day, please use public transportation.","furigana":"とうじつ|こうきょうこうつうきかん|りよう"},{"japanese":"電気やガスなどの公共料金はカードで払っている。","romaji":"Denki ya gasu nado no kōkyō ryōkin wa kādo de haratte iru.","german":"I pay public utility bills such as electricity and gas by card.","furigana":"でんき|こうきょうりょうきん|はら"}],"notes":"公共 means \"public, serving the general public\" and almost always appears with の or as the first part of a compound: 公共の場, 公共施設, 公共交通機関, 公共料金 (fees for electricity, gas, water). 公共の福祉 is the \"public welfare\". Similar is 公衆 in 公衆トイレ／公衆電話; the noun \"the public\" is rather 公 (おおやけ) or 世間.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"都市部に人口が集中している。","romaji":"Toshibu ni jinkō ga shūchū shite iru.","german":"Die Bevölkerung konzentriert sich in den Stadtgebieten.","furigana":"としぶ|じんこう|しゅうちゅう"},{"japanese":"京都は毎年たくさんの観光客が訪れる歴史ある都市だ。","romaji":"Kyōto wa maitoshi takusan no kankōkyaku ga otozureru rekishi aru toshi da.","german":"Kyoto ist eine geschichtsträchtige Stadt, die jedes Jahr viele Touristen besuchen.","furigana":"きょうと|まいとし|かんこうきゃく|おとず|れきし|とし"},{"japanese":"私の町はドイツの町と姉妹都市になっている。","romaji":"Watashi no machi wa Doitsu no machi to shimai toshi ni natte iru.","german":"Meine Stadt hat eine Städtepartnerschaft mit einer deutschen Stadt.","furigana":"わたし|まち|まち|しまいとし"}],"notes":"都市 ist die „Stadt“ als sachlicher, geografischer oder planerischer Begriff: 大都市, 都市部, 都市計画, 姉妹都市. 都会 meint dagegen die Großstadt als Lebensumfeld im Gegensatz zum Land (都会の生活, 都会に住む). Für eine kleinere Stadt im Alltag sagt man 町／街, für die Verwaltungseinheit 市.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地方の活性化が課題だ。","romaji":"Chihō no kasseika ga kadai da.","german":"Revitalizing the regions is a challenge.","furigana":"ちほう|かっせいか|かだい"},{"japanese":"明日は関東地方で雪が降るでしょう。","romaji":"Ashita wa Kantō chihō de yuki ga furu deshō.","german":"Tomorrow it will probably snow in the Kanto region.","furigana":"あした|かんとうちほう|ゆき|ふ"},{"japanese":"地方から東京に出てきて、もう十年になる。","romaji":"Chihō kara Tōkyō ni dete kite, mō jūnen ni naru.","german":"It has already been ten years since I came to Tokyo from the provinces.","furigana":"ちほう|とうきょう|で|じゅうねん"}],"notes":"地方 has two main meanings: 1. a larger \"region\" of a country (関東地方, 東北地方, especially in weather reports); 2. \"the provinces\" in contrast to the capital or the big cities (地方出身, 地方都市, 地方から上京する). 田舎 is more emotional and means rather \"rural, in the countryside\". The reading じかた is a different word.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地域の人々と交流を深めた。","romaji":"Chiiki no hitobito to kōryū o fukameta.","german":"I deepened my interaction with the people of the community.","furigana":"ちいき|ひとびと|こうりゅう|ふか"},{"japanese":"台風の影響で、一部の地域では停電が続いている。","romaji":"Taifū no eikyō de, ichibu no chiiki de wa teiden ga tsuzuite iru.","german":"Because of the typhoon, power outages continue in some areas.","furigana":"たいふう|えいきょう|いちぶ|ちいき|ていでん|つづ"},{"japanese":"同じ料理でも、地域によって味付けが違う。","romaji":"Onaji ryōri de mo, chiiki ni yotte ajitsuke ga chigau.","german":"Even the same dish is seasoned differently depending on the region.","furigana":"おな|りょうり|ちいき|あじつ|ちが"}],"notes":"地域 is an \"area, district\" with shared characteristics, often in the sense of a local community: 地域社会, 地域の住民, 地域活動, 地域によって異なる. Unlike 地方 (large region or provinces), 地域 is flexible in size and can even mean a residential neighborhood. 地元 stresses \"one's own home area\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自治体がごみの分別を呼びかけている。","romaji":"Jichitai ga gomi no bunbetsu o yobikakete iru.","german":"The local government is calling for waste separation.","furigana":"じちたい|ぶんべつ|よ"},{"japanese":"子育て支援の内容は自治体によって異なる。","romaji":"Kosodate shien no naiyō wa jichitai ni yotte kotonaru.","german":"Child-raising support differs depending on the municipality.","furigana":"こそだ|しえん|ないよう|じちたい|こと"},{"japanese":"引っ越したら、新しい自治体で住所変更の手続きをしなければならない。","romaji":"Hikkoshitara, atarashii jichitai de jūsho henkō no tetsuzuki o shinakereba naranai.","german":"After moving, you have to complete the change-of-address procedure with the new municipality.","furigana":"ひ|こ|あたら|じちたい|じゅうしょへんこう|てつづ"}],"notes":"自治体 is a \"municipality\" or \"local authority\" - cities, towns and prefectures as administrative bodies (in full 地方自治体). Typical: 各自治体, 自治体によって異なる, 自治体の支援. The term is factual and appears mainly in news and official documents; in everyday speech one speaks more concretely of 市役所 or 区役所.","levelNote":"Compound: 自治 (N2) + 体 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"学校の行事に参加した。","romaji":"Gakkō no gyōji ni sanka shita.","german":"I took part in a school event.","furigana":"がっこう|ぎょうじ|さんか"},{"japanese":"お正月は日本で最も大切な年中行事の一つだ。","romaji":"Oshōgatsu wa Nihon de mottomo taisetsu na nenjū gyōji no hitotsu da.","german":"New Year is one of the most important annual events in Japan.","furigana":"しょうがつ|にほん|もっと|たいせつ|ねんじゅうぎょうじ|ひと"},{"japanese":"十二月は会社の行事が多くて、毎週末忙しい。","romaji":"Jūnigatsu wa kaisha no gyōji ga ōkute, maishūmatsu isogashii.","german":"December has many company events, so I'm busy every weekend.","furigana":"じゅうにがつ|かいしゃ|ぎょうじ|おお|まいしゅうまついそが"}],"notes":"行事 is an \"event\" (usually regular, traditional or organized): 学校行事 (sports day, school trip), 年中行事 (\"annual events and customs\"), 行事に参加する, 行事を行う. イベント sounds more casual and commercial, 祭り is a \"festival\" with processions and stalls. Neutral; very common in school and community calendars.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"食事のマナーを守ることが大切だ。","romaji":"Shokuji no manā o mamoru koto ga taisetsu da.","german":"It is important to observe table manners.","furigana":"しょくじ|まも|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"電車の中では、携帯電話をマナーモードにしてください。","romaji":"Densha no naka de wa, keitai denwa o manā mōdo ni shite kudasai.","german":"On the train, please set your mobile phone to silent mode.","furigana":"でんしゃ|なか|けいたいでんわ"},{"japanese":"道にごみを捨てるのはマナー違反だ。","romaji":"Michi ni gomi o suteru no wa manā ihan da.","german":"Throwing trash on the street is bad manners.","furigana":"みち|す|いはん"}],"notes":"マナー means \"manners, etiquette\" in public: マナーを守る, マナーが悪い, マナー違反, テーブルマナー. マナーモード is the silent/vibrate mode of a mobile phone. Related: 礼儀 (\"politeness, decorum\", more of an attitude), 行儀 (behavior, especially of children: 行儀が悪い). The English word \"manner\" in the sense of \"way\" does not correspond to マナー.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ルールを守って安全に遊ぼう。","romaji":"Rūru o mamotte anzen ni asobō.","german":"Let's follow the rules and play safely.","furigana":"まも|あんぜん|あそ"},{"japanese":"うちでは、夜十時以降はゲームをしないというルールを決めた。","romaji":"Uchi de wa, yoru jūji ikō wa gēmu o shinai to iu rūru o kimeta.","german":"At our house, we made a rule not to play video games after ten at night.","furigana":"よるじゅうじいこう|き"},{"japanese":"息子にサッカーのルールを説明した。","romaji":"Musuko ni sakkā no rūru o setsumei shita.","german":"I explained the rules of soccer to my son.","furigana":"むすこ|せつめい"}],"notes":"ルール is a \"rule\" in games, sports or daily life: ルールを守る／破る, ルールを決める, ルール違反. It sounds more casual than 規則 (a formal regulation, e.g. 学校の規則) and is similar to 決まり. For laws one says 法律, for social norms rather 規範 or 常識.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"社会の規範に従うべきだ。","romaji":"Shakai no kihan ni shitagau beki da.","german":"We should follow the norms of society.","furigana":"しゃかい|きはん|したが"},{"japanese":"その会社は社員の行動規範を新しく定めた。","romaji":"Sono kaisha wa shain no kōdō kihan o atarashiku sadameta.","german":"The company established a new code of conduct for its employees.","furigana":"かいしゃ|しゃいん|こうどうきはん|あたら|さだ"},{"japanese":"若者の規範意識が低下していると言われることがある。","romaji":"Wakamono no kihan ishiki ga teika shite iru to iwareru koto ga aru.","german":"It is sometimes said that young people's awareness of norms is declining.","furigana":"わかもの|きはんいしき|ていか|い"}],"notes":"規範 is a \"norm, standard\" for correct behavior, mostly in abstract or official contexts: 社会規範, 行動規範 (\"code of conduct\"), 規範意識 (\"awareness of norms\"), 規範に従う. It is considerably more formal than ルール or 決まり and appears mainly in texts on society, law and corporate governance. 基準 is more a measurable \"standard\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"社会の階層間の格差が問題だ。","romaji":"Shakai no kaisōkan no kakusa ga mondai da.","german":"Disparities between the strata of society are a problem.","furigana":"しゃかい|かいそうかん|かくさ|もんだい"},{"japanese":"フォルダの階層が深すぎて、ファイルが見つからない。","romaji":"Foruda no kaisō ga fukasugite, fairu ga mitsukaranai.","german":"The folder hierarchy is so deep that I can't find the file.","furigana":"かいそう|ふか|み"},{"japanese":"この調査では、所得の階層ごとに生活の満足度を比べた。","romaji":"Kono chōsa de wa, shotoku no kaisō goto ni seikatsu no manzokudo o kurabeta.","german":"In this survey, we compared life satisfaction by income bracket.","furigana":"ちょうさ|しょとく|かいそう|せいかつ|まんぞくど|くら"}],"notes":"階層 means \"layer, level\" in a hierarchy: social (社会階層, 所得階層, 階層間の格差) or structural, for example in organizations and computer folders (フォルダの階層, 組織の階層). For individual population groups, people often use just the suffix 〜層 (富裕層, 若年層). Matter-of-fact, more in texts and technical language.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"身分証明書を提示してください。","romaji":"Mibun shōmeisho o teiji shite kudasai.","german":"Please show your ID.","furigana":"みぶんしょうめいしょ|ていじ"},{"japanese":"江戸時代には厳しい身分制度があった。","romaji":"Edo jidai ni wa kibishii mibun seido ga atta.","german":"In the Edo period there was a strict class system.","furigana":"えどじだい|きび|みぶんせいど"},{"japanese":"平日の昼間から遊んでいるなんて、いいご身分だね。","romaji":"Heijitsu no hiruma kara asonde iru nante, ii gomibun da ne.","german":"Lounging around on a weekday afternoon - must be nice!","furigana":"へいじつ|ひるま|あそ|みぶん"}],"notes":"身分 is a person's \"standing, status\" - historically a fixed social rank (身分制度, 身分の違い), today mainly \"identity, status\": 身分証明書 (\"ID\"), 身分を隠す, 学生という身分. Ironically one says いいご身分だね (\"Must be nice for you\"). In contrast to 地位 (a position one has worked for), 身分 emphasizes the ascribed or legal status.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"社会的地位の高い人が集まった。","romaji":"Shakaiteki chii no takai hito ga atsumatta.","german":"People of high social standing gathered.","furigana":"しゃかいてきちい|たか|ひと|あつ"},{"japanese":"彼は長年の努力で、業界で確かな地位を築いた。","romaji":"Kare wa naganen no doryoku de, gyōkai de tashika na chii o kizuita.","german":"Through years of effort, he built a firm position in the industry.","furigana":"かれ|ながねん|どりょく|ぎょうかい|たし|ちい|きず"},{"japanese":"女性の地位向上を目指す運動が広がっている。","romaji":"Josei no chii kōjō o mezasu undō ga hirogatte iru.","german":"Movements aiming to improve the status of women are spreading.","furigana":"じょせい|ちいこうじょう|めざ|うんどう|ひろ"}],"notes":"地位 is the \"standing, position\" of a person in society or an organization: 社会的地位, 地位が高い, 地位を築く, 地位を失う, 女性の地位向上. Unlike 身分 (ascribed, legal status), 地位 is usually acquired. 立場, by contrast, means the \"standpoint\" or the situation from which one acts. Neutral to formal.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この件について決定する権限がない。","romaji":"Kono ken ni tsuite kettei suru kengen ga nai.","german":"I don't have the authority to decide this matter.","furigana":"けん|けってい|けんげん"},{"japanese":"新しい部長に予算を決める権限が与えられた。","romaji":"Atarashii buchō ni yosan o kimeru kengen ga ataerareta.","german":"The new department head was given the authority to decide the budget.","furigana":"あたら|ぶちょう|よさん|き|けんげん|あた"},{"japanese":"このファイルを編集する権限がありません。","romaji":"Kono fairu o henshū suru kengen ga arimasen.","german":"You don't have permission to edit this file.","furigana":"へんしゅう|けんげん"}],"notes":"権限 is the \"authority\" a person has by virtue of office, role or system: 権限がある／ない, 権限を与える, 権限を持つ, 管理者権限 (administrator rights on a computer). Do not confuse it with 権利 (a person's \"right, entitlement\") and 権力 (\"power\" over others). Matter-of-fact, common in work and administration.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼はその分野の権威だ。","romaji":"Kare wa sono bun'ya no ken'i da.","german":"He is an authority in that field.","furigana":"かれ|ぶんや|けんい"},{"japanese":"これは文学の世界で最も権威のある賞だ。","romaji":"Kore wa bungaku no sekai de mottomo ken'i no aru shō da.","german":"This is the most prestigious prize in the literary world.","furigana":"ぶんがく|せかい|もっと|けんい|しょう"},{"japanese":"その事件で、政府の権威は大きく傷ついた。","romaji":"Sono jiken de, seifu no ken'i wa ōkiku kizutsuita.","german":"The incident greatly damaged the government's authority.","furigana":"じけん|せいふ|けんい|おお|きず"}],"notes":"権威 has two meanings: 1. the \"authority, prestige\" of an institution or thing (権威がある, 権威を失う, 権威のある賞); 2. a person as an \"authority, expert\" in a field (〜の権威). 権威主義 means \"authoritarianism\". To be distinguished from 権限 (\"authority to act\") and 権力 (\"power\"). Rather formal.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女はメディアで大きな影響力を持っている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa media de ōkina eikyōryoku o motte iru.","german":"She has great influence in the media.","furigana":"かのじょ|おお|えいきょうりょく|も"},{"japanese":"その国は国際社会での影響力を失いつつある。","romaji":"Sono kuni wa kokusai shakai de no eikyōryoku o ushinaitsutsu aru.","german":"That country is gradually losing its influence in the international community.","furigana":"くに|こくさいしゃかい|えいきょうりょく|うしな"},{"japanese":"先生の言葉は子どもたちに対して強い影響力がある。","romaji":"Sensei no kotoba wa kodomotachi ni taishite tsuyoi eikyōryoku ga aru.","german":"The teacher's words have a strong influence on the children.","furigana":"せんせい|ことば|こ|たい|つよ|えいきょうりょく"}],"notes":"影響力 is the ability to affect others - \"influence\" in the sense of power or weight of a person, company or country. Typical are 影響力がある／大きい／強い, 影響力を持つ, 影響力を失う and 〜に対する影響力. In contrast to 影響, which denotes the concrete effect itself (天気の影響で遅れる), 影響力 means the potential to exert influence. Factual-neutral, common in media, politics and business.","levelNote":"Compound: 影響 (N3) + 力 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"多数決で決めることにした。","romaji":"Tasūketsu de kimeru koto ni shita.","german":"We decided to settle it by majority vote.","furigana":"たすうけつ|き"},{"japanese":"この事故で多数の負傷者が出た。","romaji":"Kono jiko de tasū no fushōsha ga deta.","german":"The accident left a large number of injured.","furigana":"じこ|たすう|ふしょうしゃ|で"},{"japanese":"賛成が多数を占め、計画は承認された。","romaji":"Sansei ga tasū o shime, keikaku wa shōnin sareta.","german":"Supporters were in the majority, and the plan was approved.","furigana":"さんせい|たすう|し|けいかく|しょうにん"}],"notes":"多数 means \"a large number, many\" and, as the opposite of 少数, \"the majority\". Attributively it takes の (多数の人々, 多数の応募), but it can also be used adverbially: 応募が多数寄せられた. Fixed combinations are 多数決 (\"majority vote\") and 多数派 (\"majority faction\"). It sounds factual and literary; in everyday speech one mostly says たくさん or 多くの, and for \"more than half\" more precisely 過半数.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"少数意見も尊重するべきだ。","romaji":"Shōsū iken mo sonchō suru beki da.","german":"Minority opinions should also be respected.","furigana":"しょうすういけん|そんちょう"},{"japanese":"この村には少数民族が暮らしている。","romaji":"Kono mura ni wa shōsū minzoku ga kurashite iru.","german":"An ethnic minority lives in this village.","furigana":"むら|しょうすうみんぞく|く"},{"japanese":"反対したのはごく少数の人だけだった。","romaji":"Hantai shita no wa goku shōsū no hito dake datta.","german":"Only a very small number of people opposed it.","furigana":"はんたい|しょうすう|ひと"}],"notes":"少数 means \"a small number, few\" and is the counterpart of 多数. Common combinations are 少数意見 (\"minority opinion\"), 少数派 (\"minority, minority faction\") and 少数民族 (\"ethnic minority\"); attributively it takes の: ごく少数の人. Note: 小数 with 小 is the \"decimal number\" in mathematics - same reading, different meaning.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"過半数の賛成で議案が通った。","romaji":"Kahansū no sansei de gian ga tōtta.","german":"The bill passed with a majority vote.","furigana":"かはんすう|さんせい|ぎあん|とお"},{"japanese":"その政党は選挙で過半数を獲得した。","romaji":"Sono seitō wa senkyo de kahansū o kakutoku shita.","german":"That party won a majority in the election.","furigana":"せいとう|せんきょ|かはんすう|かくとく"},{"japanese":"回答者の過半数が今の生活に満足していると答えた。","romaji":"Kaitōsha no kahansū ga ima no seikatsu ni manzoku shite iru to kotaeta.","german":"More than half of the respondents said they were satisfied with their current lives.","furigana":"かいとうしゃ|かはんすう|いま|せいかつ|まんぞく|こた"}],"notes":"過半数 is \"more than half\", i.e. an absolute majority - in contrast to 多数, which only means \"the larger number\". Typical expressions are 過半数を占める (\"to hold the majority\"), 過半数を超える, 過半数に達する／届かない and 過半数の賛成. The word is factual and appears mainly in elections, votes and surveys.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"問題解決には対話が欠かせない。","romaji":"Mondai kaiketsu ni wa taiwa ga kakasenai.","german":"Dialogue is indispensable for solving problems.","furigana":"もんだいかいけつ|たいわ|か"},{"japanese":"市長は住民と直接対話する機会を設けた。","romaji":"Shichō wa jūmin to chokusetsu taiwa suru kikai o mōketa.","german":"The mayor set up an opportunity for direct dialogue with residents.","furigana":"しちょう|じゅうみん|ちょくせつたいわ|きかい|もう"},{"japanese":"親子の対話が減っていると言われている。","romaji":"Oyako no taiwa ga hette iru to iwarete iru.","german":"It is said that conversation between parents and children is decreasing.","furigana":"おやこ|たいわ|へ|い"}],"notes":"対話 is a conversation with genuine exchange, especially a \"dialogue\" between parties with different viewpoints. Typical are 〜と対話する, 対話を重ねる, 対話を通じて and 対話の場を設ける. Unlike 会話, which means any everyday conversation, 対話 sounds more serious and aims at mutual understanding; 会談, by contrast, is an official meeting of high-ranking representatives. Common in politics, education and diplomacy.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不景気のせいで多くの店が閉まった。","romaji":"Fukeiki no sei de ōku no mise ga shimatta.","german":"Many shops closed because of the recession.","furigana":"ふけいき|おお|みせ|し"},{"japanese":"不景気が続いて、なかなか給料が上がらない。","romaji":"Fukeiki ga tsuzuite, nakanaka kyūryō ga agaranai.","german":"The recession continues and salaries hardly go up.","furigana":"ふけいき|つづ|きゅうりょう|あ"},{"japanese":"朝から不景気な顔をして、どうしたの？","romaji":"Asa kara fukeiki na kao o shite, dō shita no?","german":"Why the gloomy face since the morning? What's wrong?","furigana":"あさ|ふけいき|かお"}],"notes":"不景気 refers to a poor economic situation, a \"slump\" or \"recession\"; the opposite is 好景気. It is used as a noun (不景気が続く, 不景気のせいで) and as a な-adjective (不景気な世の中). In conversation people also simply say 景気が悪い; the more technical word for a deep crisis is 不況. Figuratively, 不景気な顔 means a \"gloomy face\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"好景気が続き、失業率が下がった。","romaji":"Kōkeiki ga tsuzuki, shitsugyōritsu ga sagatta.","german":"The boom continued and the unemployment rate fell.","furigana":"こうけいき|つづ|しつぎょうりつ|さ"},{"japanese":"好景気のおかげで、ボーナスが去年より増えた。","romaji":"Kōkeiki no okage de, bōnasu ga kyonen yori fueta.","german":"Thanks to the good economy, my bonus increased from last year.","furigana":"こうけいき|きょねん|ふ"},{"japanese":"当時の日本は好景気に沸いていた。","romaji":"Tōji no Nihon wa kōkeiki ni waite ita.","german":"Japan was in the midst of a boom at that time.","furigana":"とうじ|にほん|こうけいき|わ"}],"notes":"好景気 is a good economic situation, a \"boom\" or \"prosperity\", the opposite of 不景気. Typical are 好景気が続く, 好景気に沸く (\"to be swept up in a boom\") and 好景気の波に乗る. In conversation one usually simply says 景気がいい; 好景気 sounds more factual and often appears in news and business texts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この国の経済成長は著しい。","romaji":"Kono kuni no keizai seichō wa ichijirushii.","german":"This country's economic growth is remarkable.","furigana":"くに|けいざいせいちょう|いちじる"},{"japanese":"しばらく会わないうちに、ずいぶん成長したね。","romaji":"Shibaraku awanai uchi ni, zuibun seichō shita ne.","german":"You've really grown since I last saw you.","furigana":"あ|せいちょう"},{"japanese":"この仕事を通じて、人として大きく成長できた。","romaji":"Kono shigoto o tsūjite, hito to shite ōkiku seichō dekita.","german":"Through this work, I was able to grow greatly as a person.","furigana":"しごと|つう|ひと|おお|せいちょう"}],"notes":"成長 is the growing and maturing of living things - children, animals, but also personal development - as well as the growth of companies and economies. As an intransitive する-verb: 子どもが成長する, 会社が成長する; typical compounds are 経済成長 and 成長率. Unlike 発展 (\"development, upswing\"), 成長 stresses getting bigger and more mature; for plants, 生長 is also written in technical language.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"アジアの経済発展は目覚ましい。","romaji":"Ajia no keizai hatten wa mezamashii.","german":"Asia's economic development is remarkable.","furigana":"けいざいはってん|めざ"},{"japanese":"この町は鉄道が開通してから大きく発展した。","romaji":"Kono machi wa tetsudō ga kaitsū shite kara ōkiku hatten shita.","german":"This town developed greatly after the railway opened.","furigana":"まち|てつどう|かいつう|おお|はってん"},{"japanese":"小さな口げんかが大きなトラブルに発展した。","romaji":"Chiisana kuchigenka ga ōkina toraburu ni hatten shita.","german":"A small quarrel developed into big trouble.","furigana":"ちい|くち|おお|はってん"}],"notes":"発展 means \"development, upswing\" toward a larger, higher or more prosperous state: 経済が発展する, 町が発展する, 発展途上国 (\"developing country\"). It also describes how a situation develops further: 〜に発展する (\"to grow into ...\"). It is an intransitive する-verb; \"to develop something\" (product, technology) is 開発する. Next to 成長 (getting bigger and more mature), 発展 stresses expansion and progress.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"毎月の収支を記録している。","romaji":"Maitsuki no shūshi o kiroku shite iru.","german":"I keep a record of my monthly income and expenses.","furigana":"まいつき|しゅうし|きろく"},{"japanese":"今月は収支が赤字になってしまった。","romaji":"Kongetsu wa shūshi ga akaji ni natte shimatta.","german":"This month my budget ended up in the red.","furigana":"こんげつ|しゅうし|あかじ"},{"japanese":"日本の貿易収支は三か月ぶりに黒字となった。","romaji":"Nihon no bōeki shūshi wa sankagetsu buri ni kuroji to natta.","german":"Japan's trade balance turned to a surplus for the first time in three months.","furigana":"にほん|ぼうえきしゅうし|さん|げつ|くろじ"}],"notes":"収支 is composed of 収 (\"income\") and 支 (\"expenditure\") and means the comparison of both, i.e. the balance or financial position. Typical are 収支が合う (\"to balance out\"), 収支のバランス, 収支が赤字／黒字になる and 貿易収支 (\"trade balance\"). It is matter-of-fact and used for private households as well as companies and states; in daily life people also simply say 収入と支出.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"決算の結果、利益が出た。","romaji":"Kessan no kekka, rieki ga deta.","german":"According to the financial results, a profit was made.","furigana":"けっさん|けっか|りえき|で"},{"japanese":"三月は決算期なので、経理部はとても忙しい。","romaji":"Sangatsu wa kessanki na node, keiribu wa totemo isogashii.","german":"March is the closing period, so the accounting department is very busy.","furigana":"さんがつ|けっさんき|けいりぶ|いそが"},{"japanese":"その会社は明日、四半期決算を発表する。","romaji":"Sono kaisha wa ashita, shihanki kessan o happyō suru.","german":"That company will announce its quarterly results tomorrow.","furigana":"かいしゃ|あした|しはんきけっさん|はっぴょう"}],"notes":"決算 is the closing of the books at the end of an accounting period and the resulting business result. Common are 決算期 (\"closing period\"), 決算発表 (\"announcement of financial results\"), 四半期決算 (\"quarterly results\") and 決算書 (\"annual financial statements\"). As a する-verb it is rare; it mostly stands as a noun. Compared with the more general 会計 (\"accounting, bill\"), 決算 specifically denotes the period-end closing.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の資産は数億円にのぼる。","romaji":"Kare no shisan wa sūokuen ni noboru.","german":"His assets amount to several hundred million yen.","furigana":"かれ|しさん|すうおくえん"},{"japanese":"老後のために資産を運用している。","romaji":"Rōgo no tame ni shisan o un'yō shite iru.","german":"I am managing my assets for retirement.","furigana":"ろうご|しさん|うんよう"},{"japanese":"社員こそが会社の一番の資産だ。","romaji":"Shain koso ga kaisha no ichiban no shisan da.","german":"Employees are the company's greatest asset.","furigana":"しゃいん|かいしゃ|いちばん|しさん"}],"notes":"資産 refers to wealth and assets, i.e. everything with monetary value such as cash, real estate or stocks. Typical are 資産を持つ／築く／運用する (\"to invest\"), 資産家 (\"wealthy person\") and 資産価値; the counterpart on the balance sheet is 負債 (\"liabilities\"). Unlike 財産, which tends to mean personal possessions and inheritance, 資産 sounds business-economic. Figuratively also: 社員は会社の資産だ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"事業を始めるための資金が必要だ。","romaji":"Jigyō o hajimeru tame no shikin ga hitsuyō da.","german":"Funds are needed to start a business.","furigana":"じぎょう|はじ|しきん|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"クラウドファンディングで映画の制作資金を集めた。","romaji":"Kuraudo fandingu de eiga no seisaku shikin o atsumeta.","german":"We raised funds for the film's production through crowdfunding.","furigana":"えいが|せいさくしきん|あつ"},{"japanese":"資金不足のため、計画は中止になった。","romaji":"Shikin busoku no tame, keikaku wa chūshi ni natta.","german":"Due to a lack of funds, the plan was canceled.","furigana":"しきんぶそく|けいかく|ちゅうし"}],"notes":"資金 are funds that are available or needed for a specific purpose - for a company, a project or privately. Typical are 資金を集める, 資金を調達する (\"to procure\"), 資金が足りない, 資金不足 and compounds such as 結婚資金 or 開業資金. Unlike 資産 (existing wealth), 資金 denotes money that is to be deployed; 資本 is capital in the economic sense.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"低金利の貸付を利用した。","romaji":"Teikinri no kashitsuke o riyō shita.","german":"I used a low-interest loan.","furigana":"ていきんり|かしつけ|りよう"},{"japanese":"銀行は中小企業への貸付を増やしている。","romaji":"Ginkō wa chūshō kigyō e no kashitsuke o fuyashite iru.","german":"Banks are increasing lending to small and medium-sized businesses.","furigana":"ぎんこう|ちゅうしょうきぎょう|かしつけ|ふ"},{"japanese":"学生向けの貸付制度について市役所に問い合わせた。","romaji":"Gakusei muke no kashitsuke seido ni tsuite shiyakusho ni toiawaseta.","german":"I asked city hall about the loan program for students.","furigana":"がくせいむ|かしつけせいど|しやくしょ|と|あ"}],"notes":"貸付 is the lending of money from the creditor's point of view, i.e. \"loan, extension of credit\"; the counterpart from the debtor's point of view is 借入. It is a technical word of banking and administrative language: 貸付金, 貸付制度, 貸付を受ける (\"to receive a loan\"), as a verb 〜に貸し付ける. In everyday speech one says rather ローン or お金を貸す. The spelling varies between 貸付, 貸付け and 貸し付け.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"借入の返済期限が迫っている。","romaji":"Kariire no hensai kigen ga sematte iru.","german":"The deadline for repaying the loan is approaching.","furigana":"かりいれ|へんさいきげん|せま"},{"japanese":"設備投資のため、銀行から五千万円を借り入れた。","romaji":"Setsubi tōshi no tame, ginkō kara gosenman'en o kariireta.","german":"For capital investment, we borrowed 50 million yen from the bank.","furigana":"せつびとうし|ぎんこう|ごせんまんえん|か|い"},{"japanese":"会社の借入金は年々減っている。","romaji":"Kaisha no kariirekin wa nennen hette iru.","german":"The company's borrowings are decreasing year by year.","furigana":"かいしゃ|かりいれきん|ねんねんへ"}],"notes":"借入 refers to taking up money, i.e. \"borrowing\" from the debtor's point of view - the counterpart of 貸付. Typical are 借入金 (\"borrowed funds, loans payable\"), 借入額, 借入を返済する and as a verb 銀行から借り入れる. It belongs to business and banking language; in everyday speech one says お金を借りる or ローンを組む. The spelling varies: 借入, 借入れ, 借り入れ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"金利が上がると住宅ローンの負担が増える。","romaji":"Kinri ga agaru to jūtaku rōn no futan ga fueru.","german":"When interest rates rise, the burden of mortgages increases.","furigana":"きんり|あ|じゅうたく|ふたん|ふ"},{"japanese":"中央銀行は金利を引き下げると発表した。","romaji":"Chūō ginkō wa kinri o hikisageru to happyō shita.","german":"The central bank announced it would lower interest rates.","furigana":"ちゅうおうぎんこう|きんり|ひ|さ|はっぴょう"},{"japanese":"どの銀行が一番金利が低いか比べてみた。","romaji":"Dono ginkō ga ichiban kinri ga hikui ka kurabete mita.","german":"I compared which bank has the lowest interest rate.","furigana":"ぎんこう|いちばんきんり|ひく|くら"}],"notes":"金利 is the interest rate or, in general, the level of interest, i.e. how high the interest is in percent. Typical are 金利が上がる／下がる, 金利を引き上げる／引き下げる, 低金利, 高金利 and 住宅ローンの金利. In contrast to 利子 and 利息, which mean the concrete amount of interest paid or received, 金利 refers more to the rate. Common in economic news and conversations at the bank.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"預金の利子はとても低い。","romaji":"Yokin no rishi wa totemo hikui.","german":"The interest on savings deposits is very low.","furigana":"よきん|りし|ひく"},{"japanese":"この奨学金は無利子で借りられる。","romaji":"Kono shōgakukin wa murishi de karirareru.","german":"This scholarship can be borrowed interest-free.","furigana":"しょうがくきん|むりし|か"},{"japanese":"借金には毎月利子がつくので、早く返したほうがいい。","romaji":"Shakkin ni wa maitsuki rishi ga tsuku node, hayaku kaeshita hō ga ii.","german":"Debt accrues interest every month, so it's better to pay it back early.","furigana":"しゃっきん|まいつきりし|はや|かえ"}],"notes":"利子 is the \"interest\" you receive on savings or pay on borrowed money - i.e. the concrete amount. Typical are 利子がつく, 利子を払う, 利子が高い／低い and 無利子 (\"interest-free\"). Nearly identical in meaning is 利息, which appears more often in contracts and at banks; 金利, by contrast, means the interest rate. Figuratively people also say 利子をつけて返す \"to pay back with interest\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"インフレが進むと生活が苦しくなる。","romaji":"Infure ga susumu to seikatsu ga kurushiku naru.","german":"When inflation progresses, life becomes harder.","furigana":"すす|せいかつ|くる"},{"japanese":"今年のインフレ率は三パーセントを超えた。","romaji":"Kotoshi no infureritsu wa san pāsento o koeta.","german":"This year's inflation rate exceeded three percent.","furigana":"ことし|りつ|さん|こ"},{"japanese":"政府はインフレ対策として電気代の補助を始めた。","romaji":"Seifu wa infure taisaku to shite denkidai no hojo o hajimeta.","german":"The government began subsidizing electricity costs as an anti-inflation measure.","furigana":"せいふ|たいさく|でんきだい|ほじょ|はじ"}],"notes":"インフレ is the short form of インフレーション, \"inflation\"; the opposite is デフレ. Typical are インフレが進む, インフレが起こる, インフレ率 and インフレ対策. It describes the general rise in the price level; for individual price increases, people say 値上がり or 物価の上昇. Neutral, common in news and daily life.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"デフレから脱却するのは難しい。","romaji":"Defure kara dakkyaku suru no wa muzukashii.","german":"It is difficult to escape deflation.","furigana":"だっきゃく|むずか"},{"japanese":"日本は長い間デフレに苦しんできた。","romaji":"Nihon wa nagai aida defure ni kurushinde kita.","german":"Japan has suffered from deflation for a long time.","furigana":"にほん|なが|あいだ|くる"},{"japanese":"デフレが続くと、企業は給料を上げにくくなる。","romaji":"Defure ga tsuzuku to, kigyō wa kyūryō o agenikuku naru.","german":"If deflation continues, it becomes hard for companies to raise salaries.","furigana":"つづ|きぎょう|きゅうりょう|あ"}],"notes":"デフレ is the short form of デフレーション, \"deflation\", i.e. a sustained decline in the general price level - the opposite of インフレ. Typical are デフレが続く, デフレから脱却する (\"to overcome deflation\") and デフレ経済. In Japan the word is closely associated with the decades after the bursting of the bubble economy; a downward spiral is called デフレスパイラル.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"法人税が引き下げられた。","romaji":"Hōjinzei ga hikisagerareta.","german":"The corporate tax was lowered.","furigana":"ほうじんぜい|ひ|さ"},{"japanese":"このサービスは個人ではなく法人向けです。","romaji":"Kono sābisu wa kojin de wa naku hōjin muke desu.","german":"This service is for corporations, not individuals.","furigana":"こじん|ほうじんむ"},{"japanese":"事業が大きくなったので、個人事業から法人にした。","romaji":"Jigyō ga ōkiku natta node, kojin jigyō kara hōjin ni shita.","german":"As the business had grown, I turned it from a sole proprietorship into a corporation.","furigana":"じぎょう|おお|こじんじぎょう|ほうじん"}],"notes":"法人 is a \"legal entity\" - an organization such as a company, association or foundation that can act legally like a person; the opposite term is 個人. Common in compounds: 法人税 (\"corporate tax\"), 法人化 (\"incorporation\"), 法人向け (\"for business customers\"), NPO法人 and 学校法人. Technical and administrative language; in everyday speech one speaks rather of 会社 or 企業.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"株主総会が来月開かれる。","romaji":"Kabunushi sōkai ga raigetsu hirakareru.","german":"The shareholders' meeting will be held next month.","furigana":"かぶぬしそうかい|らいげつひら"},{"japanese":"株主優待で食事券がもらえる。","romaji":"Kabunushi yūtai de shokujiken ga moraeru.","german":"With the shareholder benefit, you get meal vouchers.","furigana":"かぶぬしゆうたい|しょくじけん"},{"japanese":"会社は株主への配当を増やすことにした。","romaji":"Kaisha wa kabunushi e no haitō o fuyasu koto ni shita.","german":"The company decided to increase dividends to shareholders.","furigana":"かいしゃ|かぶぬし|はいとう|ふ"}],"notes":"株主 is a \"shareholder\", i.e. someone who owns shares (株) in a stock company. Common combinations are 株主総会 (\"general meeting\"), 大株主 (\"major shareholder\"), 株主優待 (\"shareholder benefits\" - vouchers or gifts that are popular in Japan) and 株主への配当 (\"dividend\"). Factual vocabulary from business and finance; the shares themselves are called 株 or 株式.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"証券会社に口座を開いた。","romaji":"Shōken gaisha ni kōza o hiraita.","german":"I opened an account at a securities firm.","furigana":"しょうけんがいしゃ|こうざ|ひら"},{"japanese":"東京証券取引所は日本最大の取引所だ。","romaji":"Tōkyō shōken torihikijo wa Nihon saidai no torihikijo da.","german":"The Tokyo Stock Exchange is the largest exchange in Japan.","furigana":"とうきょうしょうけんとりひきじょ|にほんさいだい|とりひきじょ"},{"japanese":"保険証券は大切に保管しておいてください。","romaji":"Hoken shōken wa taisetsu ni hokan shite oite kudasai.","german":"Please keep your insurance policy safe.","furigana":"ほけんしょうけん|たいせつ|ほかん"}],"notes":"証券 refers to \"securities\" such as stocks and bonds as well as, in general, certificates of rights, e.g. 保険証券 (\"insurance policy\"). It is most often encountered in 証券会社 (\"securities firm, broker\"), 証券取引所 (\"stock exchange\") and 証券口座 (\"brokerage account\"). It is technical vocabulary of the financial world; for concrete instruments one mostly says 株 or 債券.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"国債は安全な債券とされている。","romaji":"Kokusai wa anzen na saiken to sarete iru.","german":"Government bonds are considered safe bonds.","furigana":"こくさい|あんぜん|さいけん"},{"japanese":"金利が上がると、債券の価格は下がる。","romaji":"Kinri ga agaru to, saiken no kakaku wa sagaru.","german":"When interest rates rise, bond prices fall.","furigana":"きんり|あ|さいけん|かかく|さ"},{"japanese":"その会社は資金を集めるために債券を発行した。","romaji":"Sono kaisha wa shikin o atsumeru tame ni saiken o hakkō shita.","german":"The company issued bonds to raise funds.","furigana":"かいしゃ|しきん|あつ|さいけん|はっこう"}],"notes":"債券 is a \"bond\" or \"debenture\": a security with which the state or companies borrow money and pay interest for it. Subtypes are 国債 (\"government bond\") and 社債 (\"corporate bond\"); typical verbs are 債券を発行する and 債券を買う. Not to be confused with the identically read 債権 (\"claim, receivable\"), the counterpart of 債務 (\"debt\"). Technical vocabulary of the financial world.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しいデザインの紙幣が発行された。","romaji":"Atarashii dezain no shihei ga hakkō sareta.","german":"Banknotes with a new design were issued.","furigana":"あたら|しへい|はっこう"},{"japanese":"この自動販売機は一万円紙幣が使えない。","romaji":"Kono jidō hanbaiki wa ichiman'en shihei ga tsukaenai.","german":"This vending machine does not accept 10,000-yen bills.","furigana":"じどうはんばいき|いちまんえんしへい|つか"},{"japanese":"空港で紙幣を現地のお金に両替した。","romaji":"Kūkō de shihei o genchi no okane ni ryōgae shita.","german":"At the airport I exchanged my banknotes for local currency.","furigana":"くうこう|しへい|げんち|かね|りょうがえ"}],"notes":"紙幣 is the official, rather written word for \"banknote, bill\"; in everyday speech one mostly says お札. The counterpart is 硬貨 (\"coins\"). Typical are 紙幣を発行する, 新紙幣, 一万円紙幣 and 紙幣を両替する. On machines, in announcements and in the news one reads 紙幣, while in conversation one says rather 千円札 or 一万円札.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい銀行口座を開設した。","romaji":"Atarashii ginkō kōza o kaisetsu shita.","german":"I opened a new bank account.","furigana":"あたら|ぎんこうこうざ|かいせつ"},{"japanese":"家賃は毎月二十七日に口座から引き落とされます。","romaji":"Yachin wa maitsuki nijūshichinichi ni kōza kara hikiotosaremasu.","german":"The rent is debited from my account on the 27th of every month.","furigana":"やちん|まいつきにじゅうしちにち|こうざ|ひ|お"},{"japanese":"給料の振込先の口座番号を教えてください。","romaji":"Kyūryō no furikomisaki no kōza bangō o oshiete kudasai.","german":"Please tell me the account number for the salary transfer.","furigana":"きゅうりょう|ふりこみさき|こうざばんごう|おし"}],"notes":"口座 is an \"account\", above all 銀行口座; also 証券口座 (\"brokerage account\"). Typical verbs are 口座を開く／開設する／作る, 口座に振り込む (\"to transfer to an account\"), 口座から引き落とす (\"to debit\") and 口座を解約する (\"to close\"). The account number is 口座番号, the account holder 口座名義. Not to be confused with the identically read 講座 (\"course, lecture series\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"振込の手数料は三百円です。","romaji":"Furikomi no tesūryō wa sanbyakuen desu.","german":"The transfer fee is 300 yen.","furigana":"ふりこみ|てすうりょう|さんびゃくえん"},{"japanese":"前日のキャンセルには手数料がかかります。","romaji":"Zenjitsu no kyanseru ni wa tesūryō ga kakarimasu.","german":"A fee applies to cancellations made the day before.","furigana":"ぜんじつ|てすうりょう"},{"japanese":"不動産会社に家賃一か月分の手数料を払った。","romaji":"Fudōsan gaisha ni yachin ikkagetsubun no tesūryō o haratta.","german":"I paid the real estate company a fee equal to one month's rent.","furigana":"ふどうさんがいしゃ|やちんいっ|げつぶん|てすうりょう|はら"}],"notes":"手数料 is a \"fee\" for a service or processing, for example for bank transfers and cancellations, and in brokerage also a \"commission\". Typical are 手数料がかかる, 手数料を払う／取る, 手数料無料, 振込手数料 and キャンセル手数料. Unlike 料金 (generally \"price, rate\") and 費用 (\"costs\"), 手数料 specifically means the charge for processing effort. Neutral and very common in daily life.","levelNote":"Compound: 手数 (N1) + 料 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"長年の取引で信用を築いた。","romaji":"Naganen no torihiki de shin'yō o kizuita.","german":"Trust was built through many years of business dealings.","furigana":"ながねん|とりひき|しんよう|きず"},{"japanese":"一度失った信用を取り戻すのは難しい。","romaji":"Ichido ushinatta shin'yō o torimodosu no wa muzukashii.","german":"Once trust is lost, it is hard to win back.","furigana":"いちどうしな|しんよう|と|もど|むずか"},{"japanese":"あの人の言うことは、あまり信用しないほうがいい。","romaji":"Ano hito no iu koto wa, amari shin'yō shinai hō ga ii.","german":"You shouldn't trust what that person says too much.","furigana":"ひと|い|しんよう"}],"notes":"信用 is trust in the reliability of a person or company and the good reputation one earns through it; in finance also \"credit(worthiness)\", e.g. in 信用取引 or 信用金庫. As a transitive する-verb: 人を信用する, 彼の話は信用できない; also typical are 信用を得る／失う／築く. Unlike 信頼, which expresses a deeper reliance, 信用 stresses credibility based on past experience.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"午後から大事な商談がある。","romaji":"Gogo kara daiji na shōdan ga aru.","german":"I have an important business meeting this afternoon.","furigana":"ごご|だいじ|しょうだん"},{"japanese":"何度も話し合った結果、ついに商談がまとまった。","romaji":"Nando mo hanashiatta kekka, tsui ni shōdan ga matomatta.","german":"After talking it over many times, the deal was finally settled.","furigana":"なんど|はな|あ|けっか|しょうだん"},{"japanese":"商談のため、来週大阪に出張します。","romaji":"Shōdan no tame, raishū Ōsaka ni shutchō shimasu.","german":"I'm going on a business trip to Osaka next week for a business meeting.","furigana":"しょうだん|らいしゅうおおさか|しゅっちょう"}],"notes":"商談 is a \"business talk\" or negotiation about buying, selling or cooperation. Typical are 商談がある, 商談をする, 商談に行く, 商談がまとまる (\"to come to a conclusion\") and 商談が成立する. In contrast to 交渉 (any kind of negotiation) and 会議 (\"meeting\"), 商談 always refers to a concrete deal with a customer or partner. Neutral business language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"納税は国民の義務だ。","romaji":"Nōzei wa kokumin no gimu da.","german":"Paying taxes is a duty of citizens.","furigana":"のうぜい|こくみん|ぎむ"},{"japanese":"納税の期限は今月末までです。","romaji":"Nōzei no kigen wa kongetsumatsu made desu.","german":"The deadline for paying taxes is the end of this month.","furigana":"のうぜい|きげん|こんげつまつ"},{"japanese":"ふるさと納税で、地方の特産品をもらった。","romaji":"Furusato nōzei de, chihō no tokusanhin o moratta.","german":"Through hometown tax donations, I received local specialties.","furigana":"のうぜい|ちほう|とくさんひん"}],"notes":"納税 is the payment of taxes to the state or municipality, i.e. \"tax payment\"; as a する-verb 納税する. Typical are 納税者 (\"taxpayer\"), 納税の義務, 納税期限 and ふるさと納税 - a Japanese system in which one donates to municipalities and can deduct this from taxes. It is official and factual; in everyday speech one mostly says 税金を払う.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"合法的な節税対策を行っている。","romaji":"Gōhōteki na setsuzei taisaku o okonatte iru.","german":"We are taking legal tax-saving measures.","furigana":"ごうほうてき|せつぜいたいさく|おこな"},{"japanese":"この保険に入ると節税になるそうだ。","romaji":"Kono hoken ni hairu to setsuzei ni naru sō da.","german":"Apparently you can save on taxes if you take out this insurance.","furigana":"ほけん|はい|せつぜい"},{"japanese":"税理士に節税の方法を相談した。","romaji":"Zeirishi ni setsuzei no hōhō o sōdan shita.","german":"I consulted a tax accountant about ways to save on taxes.","furigana":"ぜいりし|せつぜい|ほうほう|そうだん"}],"notes":"節税 means reducing the tax burden by legal means - \"saving on taxes\". It is meant neutrally to positively, in contrast to 脱税 (\"tax evasion\"), which is illegal. Typical combinations are 節税する, 節税対策, 節税効果 and 節税になる. The word follows the pattern of 節約 and 節電 (\"saving electricity\"), in which 節 means \"to allocate sparingly\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"インターネットで求人情報を探した。","romaji":"Intānetto de kyūjin jōhō o sagashita.","german":"I looked for job listings on the internet.","furigana":"きゅうじんじょうほう|さが"},{"japanese":"駅前のコンビニがアルバイトの求人を出している。","romaji":"Ekimae no konbini ga arubaito no kyūjin o dashite iru.","german":"The convenience store in front of the station is advertising for part-timers.","furigana":"えきまえ|きゅうじん|だ"},{"japanese":"人手不足で、求人の数が増えている。","romaji":"Hitode busoku de, kyūjin no kazu ga fuete iru.","german":"Because of labor shortages, the number of job openings is increasing.","furigana":"ひとでぶそく|きゅうじん|かず|ふ"}],"notes":"求人 is an employer's search for workers, i.e. a \"job opening, job advertisement\". Typical are 求人を出す, 求人広告, 求人情報, 求人サイト and 有効求人倍率 (\"ratio of job openings to applicants\"). The counterpart from the job seekers' point of view is 求職 (\"job hunting\"). Neutral, very common in advertisements and economic news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"毎年一回昇給がある。","romaji":"Maitoshi ikkai shōkyū ga aru.","german":"There is a pay raise once a year.","furigana":"まいとしいっかいしょうきゅう"},{"japanese":"今年は業績がよかったので、全員昇給した。","romaji":"Kotoshi wa gyōseki ga yokatta node, zen'in shōkyū shita.","german":"Business results were good this year, so everyone got a raise.","furigana":"ことし|ぎょうせき|ぜんいんしょうきゅう"},{"japanese":"昇給を求めて、上司と話し合うことにした。","romaji":"Shōkyū o motomete, jōshi to hanashiau koto ni shita.","german":"I decided to talk with my boss to ask for a raise.","furigana":"しょうきゅう|もと|じょうし|はな|あ"}],"notes":"昇給 is a \"pay raise\" for an employee, usually as part of regular adjustments; the opposite is 減給 (\"pay cut\"). Typical are 昇給がある, 昇給する, 定期昇給 and 昇給率. In contrast to 昇進 (\"promotion\"), it is only about money, not position; the general wage increase in collective bargaining is called 賃上げ or ベースアップ. Neutral language of the working world.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"失敗の責任を問われて降格された。","romaji":"Shippai no sekinin o towarete kōkaku sareta.","german":"He was held responsible for the failure and demoted.","furigana":"しっぱい|せきにん|と|こうかく"},{"japanese":"そのチームは成績が悪く、二部リーグに降格した。","romaji":"Sono chīmu wa seiseki ga waruku, nibu rīgu ni kōkaku shita.","german":"That team performed poorly and was relegated to the second division.","furigana":"せいせき|わる|にぶ|こうかく"},{"japanese":"降格人事に納得できず、彼は会社を辞めた。","romaji":"Kōkaku jinji ni nattoku dekizu, kare wa kaisha o yameta.","german":"He could not accept the demotion and quit the company.","furigana":"こうかくじんじ|なっとく|かれ|かいしゃ|や"}],"notes":"降格 is a lowering to a lower level: at work a \"demotion\", in sports \"relegation\" to a lower league. It is used both transitively and intransitively: 部長を降格させる／降格する, 課長に降格される, チームが二部に降格する. The opposite is 昇格 (\"promotion to a higher rank\"); related is 昇進 (\"promotion\"). Factual, common in company and sports news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"最低賃金が引き上げられた。","romaji":"Saitei chingin ga hikiagerareta.","german":"The minimum wage was raised.","furigana":"さいていちんぎん|ひ|あ"},{"japanese":"男女の賃金格差はまだ大きい。","romaji":"Danjo no chingin kakusa wa mada ōkii.","german":"The gender wage gap is still large.","furigana":"だんじょ|ちんぎんかくさ|おお"},{"japanese":"労働組合は会社に賃金の引き上げを求めた。","romaji":"Rōdō kumiai wa kaisha ni chingin no hikiage o motometa.","german":"The labor union demanded a wage increase from the company.","furigana":"ろうどうくみあい|かいしゃ|ちんぎん|ひ|あ|もと"}],"notes":"賃金 is pay for work in the general, factual sense, \"wages\". Typical are 最低賃金 (\"minimum wage\"), 賃金を引き上げる, 賃金格差 (\"wage gap\") and the short form 賃上げ (\"wage increase\"). In daily life, people talk about their own income more as 給料 or 給与; 賃金 sounds like statistics, labor law and unions. For hourly or monthly pay there are 時給 and 月給.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このアルバイトの時給は千円だ。","romaji":"Kono arubaito no jikyū wa sen'en da.","german":"The hourly wage for this part-time job is 1,000 yen.","furigana":"じきゅう|せんえん"},{"japanese":"深夜は時給が二十五パーセント上がる。","romaji":"Shin'ya wa jikyū ga nijūgo pāsento agaru.","german":"At night, the hourly wage goes up by 25 percent.","furigana":"しんや|じきゅう|にじゅうご|あ"},{"japanese":"時給は安いけど、仕事が楽だから続けている。","romaji":"Jikyū wa yasui kedo, shigoto ga raku da kara tsuzukete iru.","german":"The hourly wage is low, but the work is easy, so I keep at it.","furigana":"じきゅう|やす|しごと|らく|つづ"}],"notes":"時給 is the wage per hour, \"hourly wage\", typical for アルバイト and パート. Common expressions are 時給千円, 時給がいい／高い, 時給が上がる and 時給で働く. Besides this there are 日給 (\"daily wage\"), 月給 (\"monthly salary\") and 年収 (\"annual income\"). Everyday word, omnipresent in job ads.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"有給を使って旅行に行った。","romaji":"Yūkyū o tsukatte ryokō ni itta.","german":"I used my paid leave to go on a trip.","furigana":"ゆうきゅう|つか|りょこう|い"},{"japanese":"有給休暇は年に二十日ある。","romaji":"Yūkyū kyūka wa nen ni hatsuka aru.","german":"I have twenty days of paid leave a year.","furigana":"ゆうきゅうきゅうか|ねん|はつか"},{"japanese":"退職する前に、残りの有給を全部消化した。","romaji":"Taishoku suru mae ni, nokori no yūkyū o zenbu shōka shita.","german":"Before resigning, I used up all my remaining paid leave.","furigana":"たいしょく|まえ|のこ|ゆうきゅう|ぜんぶしょうか"}],"notes":"有給 first means \"paid, with continued pay\" (有給の仕事, 有給休暇); in everyday speech it is the common short form of 有給休暇 (\"paid leave\"). Typical are 有給を取る, 有給を使う and 有給を消化する (\"to use up the remaining leave\"). The opposite is 無給 (\"unpaid\"). One also reads 有休 - same reading, different character - with the same meaning \"paid leave\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"夏季休暇は八月に取る予定だ。","romaji":"Kaki kyūka wa hachigatsu ni toru yotei da.","german":"I plan to take my summer vacation in August.","furigana":"かききゅうか|はちがつ|と|よてい"},{"japanese":"妻の出産に合わせて一週間の休暇を取った。","romaji":"Tsuma no shussan ni awasete isshūkan no kyūka o totta.","german":"I took a week off to coincide with my wife's delivery.","furigana":"つま|しゅっさん|あ|いっしゅうかん|きゅうか|と"},{"japanese":"長い休暇明けで、なかなか仕事に集中できない。","romaji":"Nagai kyūka ake de, nakanaka shigoto ni shūchū dekinai.","german":"After the long vacation, I can hardly concentrate on work.","furigana":"なが|きゅうかあ|しごと|しゅうちゅう"}],"notes":"休暇 is time off from work or duty that is officially taken - \"vacation, leave\". Typical are 休暇を取る, 休暇をもらう, 夏季休暇, 育児休暇 (\"parental leave\") and 有給休暇. Unlike 休み (general word for days off, breaks, holidays) and 休日 (\"day off\"), 休暇 sounds more formal and refers mainly to leave taken from an employer. School vacations are called 夏休み and so on.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"勤務時間は朝九時から夕方五時までだ。","romaji":"Kinmu jikan wa asa kuji kara yūgata goji made da.","german":"Working hours are from nine in the morning to five in the evening.","furigana":"きんむじかん|あさくじ|ゆうがたごじ"},{"japanese":"兄は東京の商社に勤務している。","romaji":"Ani wa Tōkyō no shōsha ni kinmu shite iru.","german":"My older brother works at a trading company in Tokyo.","furigana":"あに|とうきょう|しょうしゃ|きんむ"},{"japanese":"週に二日は在宅勤務をしている。","romaji":"Shū ni futsuka wa zaitaku kinmu o shite iru.","german":"I work from home two days a week.","furigana":"しゅう|ふつか|ざいたくきんむ"}],"notes":"勤務 refers to regular work at a company or government office - \"duty, work\". As an intransitive する-verb: 〜に勤務する (\"to be employed at ...\"), e.g. 銀行に勤務している. Typical compounds are 勤務時間, 勤務先 (\"place of work\"), 夜間勤務 and 在宅勤務 (\"working from home\"). It sounds more formal than 働く or 勤める and often appears in resumes, forms and employment contracts.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"営業部に配属された。","romaji":"Eigyōbu ni haizoku sareta.","german":"I was assigned to the sales department.","furigana":"えいぎょうぶ|はいぞく"},{"japanese":"このスーパーは二十四時間営業している。","romaji":"Kono sūpā wa nijūyo jikan eigyō shite iru.","german":"This supermarket is open 24 hours a day.","furigana":"にじゅうよじかんえいぎょう"},{"japanese":"年末年始の営業時間をホームページで確認した。","romaji":"Nenmatsu nenshi no eigyō jikan o hōmupēji de kakunin shita.","german":"I checked the business hours over New Year on the website.","furigana":"ねんまつねんし|えいぎょうじかん|かくにん"}],"notes":"営業 has two main meanings: the business operation of a shop or company (営業中 \"open\", 営業時間 \"business hours\", 24時間営業) and \"sales\" or customer acquisition (営業部, 営業の仕事, 営業に回る). As a する-verb it usually takes no object: 店が営業している. Unlike 販売 (the \"sale\" of a product), 営業 covers the whole of customer care and acquisition. Neutral, very common in the workplace.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 48"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この製品の販売が好調だ。","romaji":"Kono seihin no hanbai ga kōchō da.","german":"Sales of this product are going well.","furigana":"せいひん|はんばい|こうちょう"},{"japanese":"チケットは来週の月曜日から販売されます。","romaji":"Chiketto wa raishū no getsuyōbi kara hanbai saremasu.","german":"Tickets will go on sale from next Monday.","furigana":"らいしゅう|げつようび|はんばい"},{"japanese":"この店では地元の野菜を販売している。","romaji":"Kono mise de wa jimoto no yasai o hanbai shite iru.","german":"This shop sells locally grown vegetables.","furigana":"みせ|じもと|やさい|はんばい"}],"notes":"販売 is the sale of goods, mostly from the seller's point of view and in a business context. As a transitive する-verb: 商品を販売する; typical are 販売店, 自動販売機, 通信販売 (\"mail order\", short 通販) and 販売中. Do not confuse with 発売 (\"market launch, start of sales\"): 新製品を発売する, but 店で販売する. In daily life one rather says 売る; 販売 is more matter-of-fact and appears on signs and in the news.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"部品の発注を急いでください。","romaji":"Buhin no hacchū o isoide kudasai.","german":"Please hurry with the order for the parts.","furigana":"ぶひん|はっちゅう|いそ"},{"japanese":"ウェブサイトの制作を外部の会社に発注した。","romaji":"Webusaito no seisaku o gaibu no kaisha ni hacchū shita.","german":"We outsourced the creation of the website to an external company.","furigana":"せいさく|がいぶ|かいしゃ|はっちゅう"},{"japanese":"発注ミスで、商品が百個も多く届いてしまった。","romaji":"Hacchū misu de, shōhin ga hyakko mo ōku todoite shimatta.","german":"Because of an ordering mistake, a hundred extra items arrived.","furigana":"はっちゅう|しょうひん|ひゃっこ|おお|とど"}],"notes":"発注 means placing an order or commission with a supplier or service provider. As a transitive する-verb: 部品を発注する, 〜に…を発注する. The counterpart from the supplier's point of view is 受注 (\"receipt of orders\"); compounds are 発注書 (\"purchase order\") and 発注ミス. As a private customer one says 注文する; 発注 is business language for purchasing materials or services.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大型の受注を獲得した。","romaji":"Ōgata no juchū o kakutoku shita.","german":"We won a large order.","furigana":"おおがた|じゅちゅう|かくとく"},{"japanese":"この家具は受注生産なので、届くまで二か月かかります。","romaji":"Kono kagu wa juchū seisan na node, todoku made nikagetsu kakarimasu.","german":"This furniture is made to order, so it takes two months to arrive.","furigana":"かぐ|じゅちゅうせいさん|とど|に|げつ"},{"japanese":"その建設会社は新しい空港の工事を受注した。","romaji":"Sono kensetsu gaisha wa atarashii kūkō no kōji o juchū shita.","german":"The construction company won the contract for the new airport.","furigana":"けんせつがいしゃ|あたら|くうこう|こうじ|じゅちゅう"}],"notes":"受注 is the acceptance or receipt of an order from the point of view of the supplier or manufacturer - the counterpart of 発注. As a transitive する-verb: 工事を受注する; typical compounds are 受注生産 (\"production to order\"), 受注額 and 大型受注 (\"large order\"). Business language, common in economic reports and in the B2B field.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ご注文の商品は三日以内に配送いたします。","romaji":"Gochūmon no shōhin wa mikka inai ni haisō itashimasu.","german":"Your ordered goods will be delivered within three days.","furigana":"ちゅうもん|しょうひん|みっかいない|はいそう"},{"japanese":"配送先の住所を間違えて入力してしまった。","romaji":"Haisōsaki no jūsho o machigaete nyūryoku shite shimatta.","german":"I entered the delivery address incorrectly.","furigana":"はいそうさき|じゅうしょ|まちが|にゅうりょく"},{"japanese":"五千円以上お買い上げで配送料が無料になります。","romaji":"Gosen'en ijō okaiage de haisōryō ga muryō ni narimasu.","german":"For purchases of 5,000 yen or more, shipping is free.","furigana":"ごせんえんいじょう|か|あ|はいそうりょう|むりょう"}],"notes":"配送 means delivering goods to the respective recipients - \"delivery, shipping\". As a transitive する-verb: 商品を配送する; typical are 配送料 (\"shipping fee\"), 配送先 (\"delivery address\"), 配送センター and 配送無料. Compared to 配達 (delivery by a courier to the door, e.g. 新聞配達), 配送 sounds like logistics; 発送, on the other hand, is the sending off. Very common in online retail.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"原材料の価格が高騰している。","romaji":"Genzairyō no kakaku ga kōtō shite iru.","german":"Raw material prices are soaring.","furigana":"げんざいりょう|かかく|こうとう"},{"japanese":"アレルギーがあるので、原材料をよく確認してから買う。","romaji":"Arerugī ga aru node, genzairyō o yoku kakunin shite kara kau.","german":"I have an allergy, so I check the ingredients carefully before buying.","furigana":"げんざいりょう|かくにん|か"},{"japanese":"この工場では、原材料を海外から輸入している。","romaji":"Kono kōjō de wa, genzairyō o kaigai kara yunyū shite iru.","german":"This factory imports its raw materials from abroad.","furigana":"こうじょう|げんざいりょう|かいがい|ゆにゅう"}],"notes":"原材料 covers the starting materials from which a product is made: raw materials and ingredients. On food packaging 原材料名 stands for the \"list of ingredients\". Typical are 原材料費, 原材料の価格 and 原材料を輸入する. Related are 原料 (the basic material, e.g. 石油を原料とする) and 材料 (\"material, ingredient\" in a broader sense); 原材料 combines both and is factual-business.","levelNote":"Compound: 原 (N3) + 材料 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自動車の部品を海外から仕入れている。","romaji":"Jidōsha no buhin o kaigai kara shiirete iru.","german":"We source automobile parts from abroad.","furigana":"じどうしゃ|ぶひん|かいがい|しい"},{"japanese":"壊れた部品を交換したら、また動くようになった。","romaji":"Kowareta buhin o kōkan shitara, mata ugoku yō ni natta.","german":"After replacing the broken part, it worked again.","furigana":"こわ|ぶひん|こうかん|うご"},{"japanese":"古い型なので、部品がもう手に入らないそうです。","romaji":"Furui kata na node, buhin ga mō te ni hairanai sō desu.","german":"Since it's an old model, apparently you can't get the parts anymore.","furigana":"ふる|かた|ぶひん|て|はい"}],"notes":"部品 are the individual parts from which a machine, device or vehicle is assembled - \"component, spare part\". Typical are 部品を交換する, 部品を組み立てる, 自動車部品, 交換部品 and 部品メーカー. In contrast to 部分 (\"part of a whole\" in general), 部品 always means a physical component. Neutral, from technology and industry, but also in everyday life for repairs.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"完成品を倉庫に保管する。","romaji":"Kanseihin o sōko ni hokan suru.","german":"The finished products are stored in the warehouse.","furigana":"かんせいひん|そうこ|ほかん"},{"japanese":"出荷する前に、完成品を一つずつ検査します。","romaji":"Shukka suru mae ni, kanseihin o hitotsuzutsu kensa shimasu.","german":"Before shipping, we inspect the finished products one by one.","furigana":"しゅっか|まえ|かんせいひん|ひと|けんさ"},{"japanese":"組み立てが苦手なので、完成品の棚を買った。","romaji":"Kumitate ga nigate na node, kanseihin no tana o katta.","german":"I'm bad at assembling things, so I bought an already assembled shelf.","furigana":"く|た|にがて|かんせいひん|たな|か"}],"notes":"完成品 is a completely manufactured product, a \"finished product\", as opposed to 部品, 半製品 (\"semi-finished product\") or 原材料. Typical are 完成品を出荷する, 完成品を検査する and 完成品の在庫. For furniture or models it also contrasts with 組み立て式 or キット and then means \"already assembled\". Factual, from production and trade.","levelNote":"Compound: 完成 (N3) + 品 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お客様からクレームが来た。","romaji":"Okyakusama kara kurēmu ga kita.","german":"We received a complaint from a customer.","furigana":"きゃくさま|き"},{"japanese":"隣の客が料理が冷たいとクレームをつけていた。","romaji":"Tonari no kyaku ga ryōri ga tsumetai to kurēmu o tsukete ita.","german":"The customer at the next table complained that the food was cold.","furigana":"となり|きゃく|りょうり|つめ"},{"japanese":"新入社員はクレーム対応の研修を受けた。","romaji":"Shinnyū shain wa kurēmu taiō no kenshū o uketa.","german":"The new employees received training in handling complaints.","furigana":"しんにゅうしゃいん|たいおう|けんしゅう|う"}],"notes":"The Japanese クレーム comes from the English \"claim\", but mostly means \"complaint\", especially from customers. Typical are クレームをつける (\"to complain, to make a claim\"), クレームが来る／入る, クレーム対応 (\"complaint handling\") and クレーマー for habitual complainers. More factual is 苦情; toward the customer themselves one politely says ご指摘 or ご意見. A loanword from French with the same spelling means \"cream\" (in sweets).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい仕入先を探している。","romaji":"Atarashii shiiresaki o sagashite iru.","german":"We are looking for a new supplier.","furigana":"あたら|しいれさき|さが"},{"japanese":"仕入先から値上げの連絡があった。","romaji":"Shiiresaki kara neage no renraku ga atta.","german":"We were notified of a price increase by our supplier.","furigana":"しいれさき|ねあ|れんらく"},{"japanese":"仕入先を変えたら、コストが一割下がった。","romaji":"Shiiresaki o kaetara, kosuto ga ichiwari sagatta.","german":"After we changed suppliers, costs fell by ten percent.","furigana":"しいれさき|か|いちわりさ"}],"notes":"仕入先 is the \"supplier\" from which a business buys goods or materials for resale or processing - from 仕入れる (\"to purchase, to source\") and 先 (\"counterpart, address\"). Related is 取引先 (\"business partner\" in general); the counterpart is 販売先 (\"buyer\"). Typical are 仕入先を探す／変える and 仕入先との交渉. The spelling varies between 仕入先 and 仕入れ先; business language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"卸売価格で商品を仕入れた。","romaji":"Oroshiuri kakaku de shōhin o shiireta.","german":"We purchased goods at the wholesale price.","furigana":"おろしうりかかく|しょうひん|しい"},{"japanese":"毎朝、卸売市場で新鮮な魚を仕入れている。","romaji":"Maiasa, oroshiuri shijō de shinsen na sakana o shiirete iru.","german":"Every morning we buy fresh fish at the wholesale market.","furigana":"まいあさ|おろしうりしじょう|しんせん|さかな|しい"},{"japanese":"父は食品の卸売の会社を経営している。","romaji":"Chichi wa shokuhin no oroshiuri no kaisha o keiei shite iru.","german":"My father runs a wholesale company for foodstuffs.","furigana":"ちち|しょくひん|おろしうり|かいしゃ|けいえい"}],"notes":"卸売 is the sale of goods in large quantities to dealers instead of to end customers - \"wholesale\"; the counterpart is 小売 (\"retail\"). Typical are 卸売業者 (\"wholesaler\"), 卸売市場 (\"wholesale market\") and 卸売価格. The related verb is 卸す (\"to sell to dealers\"); for short one also says 卸, e.g. in 卸値. Spellings: 卸売 and 卸売り.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"小売店の数が減っている。","romaji":"Kouriten no kazu ga hette iru.","german":"The number of retail stores is decreasing.","furigana":"こうりてん|かず|へ"},{"japanese":"メーカー希望小売価格より二割安く売られている。","romaji":"Mēkā kibō kouri kakaku yori niwari yasuku urarete iru.","german":"It is being sold at 20 percent below the manufacturer's suggested retail price.","furigana":"きぼうこうりかかく|にわりやす|う"},{"japanese":"彼は大学を出てから小売業で働いている。","romaji":"Kare wa daigaku o dete kara kourigyō de hataraite iru.","german":"He has worked in the retail industry since graduating from university.","furigana":"かれ|だいがく|で|こうりぎょう|はたら"}],"notes":"小売 is selling in small quantities directly to end consumers - \"retail\", the counterpart of 卸売. Typical are 小売店 (\"retail store\"), 小売業, 小売価格 and メーカー希望小売価格 (\"manufacturer's suggested retail price\"). The reading is こうり from こ＋うり, so no long o; also written 小売り. Matter-of-fact business language.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"問屋から直接仕入れると安くなる。","romaji":"Ton'ya kara chokusetsu shiireru to yasuku naru.","german":"Buying directly from a wholesaler makes it cheaper.","furigana":"とんや|ちょくせつしい|やす"},{"japanese":"浅草橋には人形やおもちゃの問屋が多く集まっている。","romaji":"Asakusabashi ni wa ningyō ya omocha no ton'ya ga ōku atsumatte iru.","german":"Many wholesalers of dolls and toys are gathered in Asakusabashi.","furigana":"あさくさばし|にんぎょう|とんや|おお|あつ"},{"japanese":"一日で全部覚えられると思ったが、そうは問屋が卸さなかった。","romaji":"Ichinichi de zenbu oboerareru to omotta ga, sō wa ton'ya ga orosanakatta.","german":"I thought I could memorize it all in one day, but it wasn't that easy.","furigana":"いちにち|ぜんぶおぼ|おも|とんや|おろ"}],"notes":"問屋 means a \"wholesaler\" or wholesale business that buys goods from the manufacturer and resells them to retailers; more neutral and modern are 卸売業者 or simply 卸. Typical combinations are 問屋から仕入れる \"to buy from a wholesaler\" and 問屋街 \"wholesale district\". Very well known is the idiom そうは問屋が卸さない \"it won't be that easy\" - literally \"the wholesaler won't supply on those terms\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"海外の代理店と契約を結んだ。","romaji":"Kaigai no dairiten to keiyaku o musunda.","german":"We signed a contract with an overseas agent.","furigana":"かいがい|だいりてん|けいやく|むす"},{"japanese":"旅行代理店で北海道へのツアーを申し込んだ。","romaji":"Ryokō dairiten de Hokkaidō e no tsuā o mōshikonda.","german":"I booked a tour to Hokkaido at a travel agency.","furigana":"りょこうだいりてん|ほっかいどう|もう|こ"},{"japanese":"兄は広告代理店で働いている。","romaji":"Ani wa kōkoku dairiten de hataraite iru.","german":"My older brother works at an advertising agency.","furigana":"あに|こうこくだいりてん|はたら"}],"notes":"代理店 is a company that sells products or arranges services on behalf of another firm: \"agency, agent, authorized dealer\". It is most often encountered in compounds such as 旅行代理店 \"travel agency\", 広告代理店 \"advertising agency\" and 保険代理店 \"insurance agency\"; a 正規代理店 is an \"authorized dealer\". 代理店 stresses that one is not the manufacturer or provider oneself; in everyday speech one often simply says 旅行会社 for the travel agency.","levelNote":"Compound: 代理 (N3) + 店 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"来年、大阪に新しい支店を開く。","romaji":"Rainen, Ōsaka ni atarashii shiten o hiraku.","german":"Next year a new branch will be opened in Osaka.","furigana":"らいねん|おおさか|あたら|してん|ひら"},{"japanese":"父は来月から福岡支店に転勤することになった。","romaji":"Chichi wa raigetsu kara Fukuoka shiten ni tenkin suru koto ni natta.","german":"My father is going to be transferred to the Fukuoka branch next month.","furigana":"ちち|らいげつ|ふくおかしてん|てんきん"},{"japanese":"口座を作ったのは駅前の支店です。","romaji":"Kōza o tsukutta no wa ekimae no shiten desu.","german":"The place where I opened my account was the branch in front of the station.","furigana":"こうざ|つく|えきまえ|してん"}],"notes":"支店 is the \"branch, branch office\" of a company, especially at banks, department stores and trading firms; the counterpart is 本店 \"main store\" or 本社 \"headquarters\". Typical are 支店を開く/出す \"to open a branch\", 支店長 \"branch manager\" and 〜支店に転勤する \"to be transferred to the ... branch\". 支社, by contrast, is more of a regional office with administrative functions, and 店舗 simply a single retail store.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 31"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい事業を立ち上げた。","romaji":"Atarashii jigyō o tachiageta.","german":"I launched a new business.","furigana":"あたら|じぎょう|た|あ"},{"japanese":"不況のため、会社は海外事業を縮小した。","romaji":"Fukyō no tame, kaisha wa kaigai jigyō o shukushō shita.","german":"Due to the recession, the company scaled back its overseas operations.","furigana":"ふきょう|かいしゃ|かいがいじぎょう|しゅくしょう"},{"japanese":"市は子育て支援の事業に予算を増やした。","romaji":"Shi wa kosodate shien no jigyō ni yosan o fuyashita.","german":"The city increased its budget for child-rearing support programs.","furigana":"し|こそだ|しえん|じぎょう|よさん|ふ"}],"notes":"事業 refers to a larger-scale business or social undertaking: \"business, line of business, project\". Typical are 事業を始める/立ち上げる/拡大する, 事業に失敗する and compounds such as 事業部 \"business division\", 公共事業 \"public works\" and 慈善事業 \"charity work\". Unlike 仕事 \"work, job\", 事業 does not mean an individual's activity but a whole undertaking of a company, agency or organization; 企業, by contrast, is the enterprise as a firm.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"さまざまな業種の企業が参加した。","romaji":"Samazama na gyōshu no kigyō ga sanka shita.","german":"Companies from various industries took part.","furigana":"ぎょうしゅ|きぎょう|さんか"},{"japanese":"就職活動では、業種よりも職種を重視して会社を選んだ。","romaji":"Shūshoku katsudō de wa, gyōshu yori mo shokushu o jūshi shite kaisha o eranda.","german":"In job hunting, I chose companies by job type rather than by industry.","furigana":"しゅうしょくかつどう|ぎょうしゅ|しょくしゅ|じゅうし|かいしゃ|えら"},{"japanese":"平均の給料は業種によって大きく異なる。","romaji":"Heikin no kyūryō wa gyōshu ni yotte ōkiku kotonaru.","german":"Average salaries differ greatly by industry.","furigana":"へいきん|きゅうりょう|ぎょうしゅ|おお|こと"}],"notes":"業種 is the \"industry\" in the sense of a category of types of business, such as retail, construction or restaurants. Typical are 業種別 \"by industry\", 異業種 \"different industry\" (異業種交流 \"cross-industry exchange\") and the question about the 業種 in job applications. 業界, in contrast, means the industry as the world of the companies active in it (IT業界), while 職種 denotes the type of work of the individual (\"occupational group\", e.g. sales or engineering).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"IT業界は急速に成長している。","romaji":"Aitī gyōkai wa kyūsoku ni seichō shite iru.","german":"The IT industry is growing rapidly.","furigana":"ぎょうかい|きゅうそく|せいちょう"},{"japanese":"彼は出版業界で二十年働いてきた。","romaji":"Kare wa shuppan gyōkai de nijūnen hataraite kita.","german":"He has worked in the publishing industry for twenty years.","furigana":"かれ|しゅっぱんぎょうかい|にじゅうねんはたら"},{"japanese":"その言葉は業界用語なので、一般の人には通じない。","romaji":"Sono kotoba wa gyōkai yōgo na node, ippan no hito ni wa tsūjinai.","german":"That word is industry jargon, so ordinary people won't understand it.","furigana":"ことば|ぎょうかいようご|いっぱん|ひと|つう"}],"notes":"業界 refers to an industry as the community of the companies and people active in it: \"the industry, the scene\". It usually follows the name of the industry: IT業界, 出版業界 \"publishing\", 芸能業界 \"show business\"; 業界トップ is the \"industry leader\". Also common are 業界用語 \"industry jargon\" and 業界人 \"insider\". In contrast to 業種, a factual classification category, 業界 sounds like a world of its own with rules and relationships.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"同業他社との競争が激しい。","romaji":"Dōgyō tasha to no kyōsō ga hageshii.","german":"Competition with other companies in the same industry is fierce.","furigana":"どうぎょうたしゃ|きょうそう|はげ"},{"japanese":"同業者の集まりで、業界の最新情報を交換した。","romaji":"Dōgyōsha no atsumari de, gyōkai no saishin jōhō o kōkan shita.","german":"At a gathering of people in the same line of business, we exchanged the latest industry information.","furigana":"どうぎょうしゃ|あつ|ぎょうかい|さいしんじょうほう|こうかん"},{"japanese":"妻とは同業なので、仕事の悩みもよく分かり合える。","romaji":"Tsuma to wa dōgyō na node, shigoto no nayami mo yoku wakariaeru.","german":"My wife and I are in the same line of work, so we understand each other's work worries well.","furigana":"つま|どうぎょう|しごと|なや|わ|あ"}],"notes":"同業 means \"same industry, same occupation\" and is used almost only in compounds or with の/だ: 同業他社 \"other companies in the same industry, competitors\", 同業者 \"colleague in the same industry or trade\". It stresses the sameness of the trade, not cooperation; colleagues within the same company are 同僚 instead. The tone is factual and business-like.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二つの銀行が合併した。","romaji":"Futatsu no ginkō ga gappei shita.","german":"The two banks merged.","furigana":"ふた|ぎんこう|がっぺい"},{"japanese":"隣の町と合併して、新しい市が誕生した。","romaji":"Tonari no machi to gappei shite, atarashii shi ga tanjō shita.","german":"It merged with the neighboring town and a new city was born.","furigana":"となり|まち|がっぺい|あたら|し|たんじょう"},{"japanese":"子どもの数が減ったため、二つの小学校を合併することになった。","romaji":"Kodomo no kazu ga hetta tame, futatsu no shōgakkō o gappei suru koto ni natta.","german":"Because the number of children decreased, the two elementary schools will be merged.","furigana":"こ|かず|へ|ふた|しょうがっこう|がっぺい"}],"notes":"合併 is the \"merger, consolidation\" of companies, municipalities, schools and the like. As a する-verb it is used both intransitively (AとBが合併する, AがBと合併する) and transitively (AとBを合併する). Common are 吸収合併 \"merger by absorption\", 市町村合併 \"municipal merger\" and, in medicine, 合併症 \"complication\". 買収, by contrast, is a takeover by purchase; both together correspond to \"M&A\" (合併・買収).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大手企業がスタートアップを買収した。","romaji":"Ōte kigyō ga sutātoappu o baishū shita.","german":"A major company acquired the startup.","furigana":"おおてきぎょう|ばいしゅう"},{"japanese":"新しい道路のために、市は周辺の土地を買収した。","romaji":"Atarashii dōro no tame ni, shi wa shūhen no tochi o baishū shita.","german":"For the new road, the city bought up the surrounding land.","furigana":"あたら|どうろ|し|しゅうへん|とち|ばいしゅう"},{"japanese":"選挙で有権者を買収した疑いで、議員が逮捕された。","romaji":"Senkyo de yūkensha o baishū shita utagai de, giin ga taiho sareta.","german":"A lawmaker was arrested on suspicion of bribing voters in the election.","furigana":"せんきょ|ゆうけんしゃ|ばいしゅう|うたが|ぎいん|たいほ"}],"notes":"買収 has two meanings: economically the \"takeover, buyout\" of a company or land (企業買収, 土地を買収する), and negatively the \"bribery\" of people, e.g. in elections (有権者を買収する \"to buy voters\"). It is a transitive する-verb with を; in the passive it is 買収される \"to be taken over / bribed\". 合併, in contrast, is the merger into one company; the bribe money itself is called 賄賂.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"海外に子会社を設立した。","romaji":"Kaigai ni kogaisha o setsuritsu shita.","german":"We established a subsidiary overseas.","furigana":"かいがい|こがいしゃ|せつりつ"},{"japanese":"買収されたあと、その会社は大手メーカーの子会社になった。","romaji":"Baishū sareta ato, sono kaisha wa ōte mēkā no kogaisha ni natta.","german":"After being acquired, the company became a subsidiary of a major manufacturer.","furigana":"ばいしゅう|かいしゃ|おおて|こがいしゃ"},{"japanese":"子会社に出向して、営業部長を任された。","romaji":"Kogaisha ni shukkō shite, eigyō buchō o makasareta.","german":"I was seconded to a subsidiary and appointed sales manager.","furigana":"こがいしゃ|しゅっこう|えいぎょうぶちょう|まか"}],"notes":"子会社 is a \"subsidiary\" that is majority-owned by another company, the 親会社. Typical are 子会社を設立する \"to establish a subsidiary\", 〜の子会社になる and 完全子会社 \"wholly owned subsidiary\". In the compound, 会社 becomes がいしゃ (voiced). In general, for companies of a group one also speaks of グループ会社 or 関連会社.","levelNote":"Compound: 子 (N4) + 会社 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"親会社の方針に従わなければならない。","romaji":"Oyagaisha no hōshin ni shitagawanakereba naranai.","german":"We have to follow the parent company's policy.","furigana":"おやがいしゃ|ほうしん|したが"},{"japanese":"親会社から新しい社長が送られてきた。","romaji":"Oyagaisha kara atarashii shachō ga okurarete kita.","german":"A new president was sent from the parent company.","furigana":"おやがいしゃ|あたら|しゃちょう|おく"},{"japanese":"その球団の親会社は鉄道会社だ。","romaji":"Sono kyūdan no oyagaisha wa tetsudō gaisha da.","german":"The parent company of that baseball team is a railway company.","furigana":"きゅうだん|おやがいしゃ|てつどうがいしゃ"}],"notes":"親会社 is the \"parent company\" that controls a subsidiary (子会社); the image of \"parent and child\" is literally present in the Japanese. Typical are 親会社の方針/意向 \"the parent company's directives\" and 親会社から出向する \"to be seconded from the parent company\". As with 子会社, 会社 is read がいしゃ here; for pure holding structures one speaks of 持株会社.","levelNote":"Compound: 親 (N4) + 会社 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"科学の進歩は目覚ましい。","romaji":"Kagaku no shinpo wa mezamashii.","german":"The progress of science is remarkable.","furigana":"かがく|しんぽ|めざ"},{"japanese":"兄は将来、科学者になりたいと言っています。","romaji":"Ani wa shōrai, kagakusha ni naritai to itte imasu.","german":"My older brother says he wants to become a scientist in the future.","furigana":"あに|しょうらい|かがくしゃ|い"},{"japanese":"子どもと一緒に科学博物館へ行きました。","romaji":"Kodomo to issho ni kagaku hakubutsukan e ikimashita.","german":"I went to the science museum with my child.","furigana":"こ|いっしょ|かがくはくぶつかん|い"}],"notes":"科学 is \"science\", especially the natural sciences and the scientific method; it includes 科学者, 科学的 and 科学技術. The school subject, by contrast, is 理科. Note: 化学 \"chemistry\" is read the same way, かがく; to avoid misunderstandings, people often say ばけがく for chemistry in conversation. Neutral, common in school and the news.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"動物の行動を観察している。","romaji":"Dōbutsu no kōdō o kansatsu shite iru.","german":"We are observing the behavior of animals.","furigana":"どうぶつ|こうどう|かんさつ"},{"japanese":"夏休みにアサガオの観察日記をつけた。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi ni asagao no kansatsu nikki o tsuketa.","german":"During summer vacation, I kept an observation diary of a morning glory.","furigana":"なつやす|かんさつにっき"},{"japanese":"彼は観察力が鋭いので、小さな変化にもすぐ気づく。","romaji":"Kare wa kansatsuryoku ga surudoi node, chiisa na henka ni mo sugu kizuku.","german":"He has sharp powers of observation and notices even small changes right away.","furigana":"かれ|かんさつりょく|するど|ちい|へんか|き"}],"notes":"観察 is the careful, systematic \"observing\" of processes, e.g. in nature, in the lab or of people; as a する-verb it is transitive with を. Typical are 観察日記 \"observation diary\" (a classic school assignment), 観察力 \"powers of observation\" and, at the doctor's, 経過観察 \"follow-up monitoring\". 観測, by contrast, is used for measurements with instruments (weather, stars), 監視 for \"surveillance\" for control.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"数学的な証明が必要だ。","romaji":"Sūgakuteki na shōmei ga hitsuyō da.","german":"A mathematical proof is required.","furigana":"すうがくてき|しょうめい|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"本人であることを証明するために、パスポートを見せた。","romaji":"Honnin de aru koto o shōmei suru tame ni, pasupōto o miseta.","german":"To prove who I was, I showed my passport.","furigana":"ほんにん|しょうめい|み"},{"japanese":"彼女は自分の無実を証明しようとしている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa jibun no mujitsu o shōmei shiyō to shite iru.","german":"She is trying to prove her innocence.","furigana":"かのじょ|じぶん|むじつ|しょうめい"}],"notes":"証明 means \"proof, verification\" - to show something to be true or correct, whether mathematically, scientifically or officially. As a transitive する-verb: 無実を証明する \"to prove one's innocence\", 本人であることを証明する \"to prove one's identity\". Very common are 証明書 \"certificate\" and 身分証明書 \"ID card\". 証拠, by contrast, is the \"piece of evidence\" itself with which one 証明する.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"国際学会で研究成果を発表した。","romaji":"Kokusai gakkai de kenkyū seika o happyō shita.","german":"I presented the research results at an international conference.","furigana":"こくさいがっかい|けんきゅうせいか|はっぴょう"},{"japanese":"来週は学会に出るので、研究室を留守にします。","romaji":"Raishū wa gakkai ni deru node, kenkyūshitsu o rusu ni shimasu.","german":"I'll be at a conference next week, so I'll be away from the lab.","furigana":"らいしゅう|がっかい|で|けんきゅうしつ|るす"},{"japanese":"彼は日本心理学会の会員だ。","romaji":"Kare wa Nihon Shinri Gakkai no kaiin da.","german":"He is a member of the Japanese Society of Psychology.","furigana":"かれ|にほんしんりがっかい|かいいん"}],"notes":"学会 has two closely connected meanings: the scientific \"academic society\" (e.g. 日本心理学会) and its \"conference\". Typical are 学会で発表する \"to present at a conference\", 学会に参加する/出る and 学会誌 \"the society's journal\". Not to be confused with the identically read 学界 \"academic world\"; 大会 is used more for sports or large events.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"試験管に液体を入れて加熱した。","romaji":"Shikenkan ni ekitai o irete kanetsu shita.","german":"I put liquid in the test tube and heated it.","furigana":"しけんかん|えきたい|い|かねつ"},{"japanese":"試験管を軽く振って、二つの液体を混ぜた。","romaji":"Shikenkan o karuku futte, futatsu no ekitai o mazeta.","german":"I shook the test tube gently and mixed the two liquids.","furigana":"しけんかん|かる|ふ|ふた|えきたい|ま"},{"japanese":"試験管が割れないように、ゆっくり温めてください。","romaji":"Shikenkan ga warenai yō ni, yukkuri atatamete kudasai.","german":"Please heat it slowly so the test tube doesn't crack.","furigana":"しけんかん|わ|あたた"}],"notes":"試験管 is the \"test tube\" in the laboratory or chemistry class, composed of 試験 \"test, experiment\" and 管 \"tube\". Typical verbs are 試験管に入れる/注ぐ, 試験管を振る \"to shake\" and 試験管を加熱する \"to heat\". 試験管ベビー is an older term for a child conceived by artificial insemination; today one says 体外受精. Not to be confused with the identically read 試験官 \"examiner\".","levelNote":"Compound: 試験 (N5) + 管 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"DNA鑑定で犯人が特定された。","romaji":"Dīenuē kantei de hannin ga tokutei sareta.","german":"Durch eine DNS-Analyse wurde der Täter identifiziert.","furigana":"かんてい|はんにん|とくてい"},{"japanese":"子どもは両親からDNAを半分ずつ受け継ぐ。","romaji":"Kodomo wa ryōshin kara dīenuē o hanbun zutsu uketsugu.","german":"Ein Kind erbt je zur Hälfte die DNA seiner Eltern.","furigana":"こ|りょうしん|はんぶん|う|つ"},{"japanese":"挑戦を恐れないのが、この会社のDNAだ。","romaji":"Chōsen o osorenai no ga, kono kaisha no dīenuē da.","german":"Keine Angst vor Herausforderungen zu haben, liegt dieser Firma in der DNA.","furigana":"ちょうせん|おそ|かいしゃ"}],"notes":"Wird im Japanischen mit lateinischen Buchstaben geschrieben (DNA, auch ＤＮＡ) und ディーエヌエー gelesen; fachsprachlich heißt es デオキシリボ核酸. Häufige Verbindungen sind DNA鑑定 „DNA-Analyse“ und DNAを受け継ぐ „DNA erben“; das einzelne Gen heißt 遺伝子. Wie im Deutschen wird 〜のDNA auch übertragen für eine ererbte Grundhaltung gebraucht, etwa bei Firmen oder Sportvereinen.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"周期表には百十八の元素が載っている。","romaji":"Shūkihyō ni wa hyakujūhachi no genso ga notte iru.","german":"The periodic table lists 118 elements.","furigana":"しゅうきひょう|ひゃくじゅうはち|げんそ|の"},{"japanese":"酸素の元素記号はOだ。","romaji":"Sanso no genso kigō wa O da.","german":"The element symbol for oxygen is O.","furigana":"さんそ|げんそきごう"},{"japanese":"日本の研究チームが新しい元素を発見した。","romaji":"Nihon no kenkyū chīmu ga atarashii genso o hakken shita.","german":"A Japanese research team discovered a new element.","furigana":"にほん|けんきゅう|あたら|げんそ|はっけん"}],"notes":"元素 is the chemical \"element\", i.e. a basic substance such as 水素 \"hydrogen\" or 炭素 \"carbon\"; related are 元素記号 \"element symbol\" and 周期表 \"periodic table\". For \"element\" in the general sense of component or factor, one uses 要素 instead - a common mistake. The four elements of antiquity (earth, water, air, fire) are also called 四元素.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"水は水素と酸素の化合物だ。","romaji":"Mizu wa suiso to sanso no kagōbutsu da.","german":"Water is a compound of hydrogen and oxygen.","furigana":"みず|すいそ|さんそ|かごうぶつ"},{"japanese":"塩は混合物ではなく、ナトリウムと塩素の化合物だ。","romaji":"Shio wa kongōbutsu de wa naku, natoriumu to enso no kagōbutsu da.","german":"Salt is not a mixture but a compound of sodium and chlorine.","furigana":"しお|こんごうぶつ|えんそ|かごうぶつ"},{"japanese":"ある植物から新しい化合物が見つかった。","romaji":"Aru shokubutsu kara atarashii kagōbutsu ga mitsukatta.","german":"A new compound was found in a certain plant.","furigana":"しょくぶつ|あたら|かごうぶつ|み"}],"notes":"化合物 is a chemical \"compound\" of two or more elements chemically bonded to one another (化合する \"to combine chemically\"). Antonyms are 単体 \"pure substance of a single element\" and 混合物 \"mixture\", whose components are merely mixed. Common are 有機化合物 \"organic compound\" and 炭素化合物. For \"connection\" in the sense of contact or link, one of course says 連絡 or 接続, not 化合物.","levelNote":"Compound: 化合 (N1) + 物 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"化学の実験で新しい物質を合成した。","romaji":"Kagaku no jikken de atarashii busshitsu o gōsei shita.","german":"In a chemistry experiment, we synthesized a new substance.","furigana":"かがく|じっけん|あたら|ぶっしつ|ごうせい"},{"japanese":"高校では物理より化学のほうが得意だった。","romaji":"Kōkō de wa butsuri yori kagaku no hō ga tokui datta.","german":"In high school, I was better at chemistry than physics.","furigana":"こうこう|ぶつり|かがく|とくい"},{"japanese":"工場から化学物質が川に流れ出た。","romaji":"Kōjō kara kagaku busshitsu ga kawa ni nagaredeta.","german":"Chemicals flowed from the factory into the river.","furigana":"こうじょう|かがくぶっしつ|かわ|なが|で"}],"notes":"化学 is \"chemistry\" as a science and school subject; important compounds are 化学反応 \"chemical reaction\", 化学物質 \"chemical substance\" and 化学者 \"chemist\". Because it sounds exactly like 科学 \"science\", people in conversation often say ばけがく (from 化ける) to tell them apart. \"Chemical\" is usually rendered with 化学的 or 化学の.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"物理の法則は宇宙のどこでも同じだ。","romaji":"Butsuri no hōsoku wa uchū no doko de mo onaji da.","german":"The laws of physics are the same everywhere in the universe.","furigana":"ぶつり|ほうそく|うちゅう|おな"},{"japanese":"物理のテストで公式を忘れてしまった。","romaji":"Butsuri no tesuto de kōshiki o wasurete shimatta.","german":"I forgot the formula in the physics test.","furigana":"ぶつり|こうしき|わす"},{"japanese":"今から三十分で空港に着くのは物理的に無理だ。","romaji":"Ima kara sanjuppun de kūkō ni tsuku no wa butsuriteki ni muri da.","german":"Getting to the airport in thirty minutes from now is physically impossible.","furigana":"いま|さんじゅっぷん|くうこう|つ|ぶつりてき|むり"}],"notes":"物理 mainly denotes the subject \"physics\" (short for 物理学) and appears in compounds such as 物理法則 \"physical law\" and 物理学者 \"physicist\". 物理的 means \"physical, material\" and is often used figuratively: 物理的に無理 \"simply impossible in practice\" (e.g. in terms of time or space). For school subjects one simply says 物理, in science rather 物理学.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"数学は科学の基礎だ。","romaji":"Sūgaku wa kagaku no kiso da.","german":"Mathematik ist die Grundlage der Wissenschaft.","furigana":"すうがく|かがく|きそ"},{"japanese":"私は数学が苦手です。","romaji":"Watashi wa sūgaku ga nigate desu.","german":"Mathe liegt mir nicht.","furigana":"わたし|すうがく|にがて"},{"japanese":"明日は数学のテストがあります。","romaji":"Ashita wa sūgaku no tesuto ga arimasu.","german":"Morgen schreiben wir einen Mathetest.","furigana":"あした|すうがく"}],"notes":"数学 ist die Mathematik als Fach an Mittel- und Oberschule sowie Universität; in der Grundschule heißt das Fach 算数 „Rechnen“. Ob man gut darin ist, sagt man mit 数学が得意 oder 数学が苦手. Typisch sind 数学の先生, 数学のテスト und 数学者 „Mathematiker“. Eine Kurzform wie das deutsche „Mathe“ gibt es nicht; man sagt einfach 数学.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"測定した数値を記録した。","romaji":"Sokutei shita sūchi o kiroku shita.","german":"I recorded the measured values.","furigana":"そくてい|すうち|きろく"},{"japanese":"健康診断で血圧の数値が少し高かった。","romaji":"Kenkō shindan de ketsuatsu no sūchi ga sukoshi takakatta.","german":"At the health checkup, my blood pressure reading was a little high.","furigana":"けんこうしんだん|けつあつ|すうち|すこ|たか"},{"japanese":"会社は売上の数値目標を発表した。","romaji":"Kaisha wa uriage no sūchi mokuhyō o happyō shita.","german":"The company announced numerical sales targets.","furigana":"かいしゃ|うりあげ|すうちもくひょう|はっぴょう"}],"notes":"数値 is a concrete \"numerical value, measured value\", e.g. in measurements, examinations or statistics. Typical are 数値が高い/低い, 数値を測る/記録する, 数値目標 \"quantitative target\" and 数値化する \"to express in numbers\"; at a health checkup 検査の数値 means the \"lab values\". 数字, in contrast, is the digit or number in general, also in everyday use (数字に強い \"good with numbers\").","levelNote":"Compound: 値 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この方程式には三つの変数がある。","romaji":"Kono hōteishiki ni wa mittsu no hensū ga aru.","german":"This equation has three variables.","furigana":"ほうていしき|みっ|へんすう"},{"japanese":"まず変数xに5を代入してください。","romaji":"Mazu hensū x ni go o dainyū shite kudasai.","german":"First, substitute 5 for the variable x.","furigana":"へんすう|だいにゅう"},{"japanese":"プログラムの変数の名前を分かりやすく変えた。","romaji":"Puroguramu no hensū no namae o wakariyasuku kaeta.","german":"I changed the variable names in the program to make them easier to understand.","furigana":"へんすう|なまえ|わ|か"}],"notes":"変数 is the \"variable\" in mathematics, statistics and programming; the counterpart is 定数 \"constant\". Typical expressions are 変数に値を代入する \"to assign / substitute a value to a variable\" and, in programming, 変数を定義する/宣言する. In everyday speech, an unknown quantity is more often referred to as 未知数 (\"still uncertain\"); 変数 remains technical.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この関数のグラフを描いてください。","romaji":"Kono kansū no gurafu o kaite kudasai.","german":"Please draw the graph of this function.","furigana":"かんすう|か"},{"japanese":"二次関数は中学三年生で習う。","romaji":"Niji kansū wa chūgaku sannensei de narau.","german":"Quadratic functions are learned in the ninth grade.","furigana":"にじかんすう|ちゅうがくさんねんせい|なら"},{"japanese":"エクセルの関数を使えば、合計がすぐに出せる。","romaji":"Ekuseru no kansū o tsukaeba, gōkei ga sugu ni daseru.","german":"If you use an Excel function, you can get the total right away.","furigana":"かんすう|つか|ごうけい|だ"}],"notes":"関数 is the mathematical \"function\" and, in programming or spreadsheets, a \"function\" as a calculation rule; formerly it was also written 函数. Typical are 一次関数 \"linear function\", 二次関数 \"quadratic function\" and 関数を呼び出す \"to call a function\". For \"function\" in the sense of a task or feature of a device, however, one says 機能 - a common mistake.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二次方程式の解き方を学んだ。","romaji":"Niji hōteishiki no tokikata o mananda.","german":"I learned how to solve quadratic equations.","furigana":"にじほうていしき|と|かた|まな"},{"japanese":"文章題を読んで、まず方程式を立ててみよう。","romaji":"Bunshōdai o yonde, mazu hōteishiki o tatete miyō.","german":"Read the word problem and first try setting up an equation.","furigana":"ぶんしょうだい|よ|ほうていしき|た"},{"japanese":"早めに点を取って守り切るのが、このチームの勝利の方程式だ。","romaji":"Hayame ni ten o totte mamorikiru no ga, kono chīmu no shōri no hōteishiki da.","german":"Scoring early and holding on to the lead is this team's winning formula.","furigana":"はや|てん|と|まも|き|しょうり|ほうていしき"}],"notes":"方程式 is the mathematical \"equation\" with unknowns; typical verbs are 方程式を解く \"to solve\" and 方程式を立てる \"to set up\". Well-known types are 一次方程式 \"linear equation\", 二次方程式 \"quadratic equation\" and 連立方程式 \"system of equations\". Figuratively it means a proven \"recipe for success\", especially in sports: 勝利の方程式 \"winning formula\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ピタゴラスの定理は有名だ。","romaji":"Pitagorasu no teiri wa yūmei da.","german":"Der Satz des Pythagoras ist berühmt.","furigana":"ていり|ゆうめい"},{"japanese":"三平方の定理を使って、この辺の長さを求めなさい。","romaji":"Sanheihō no teiri o tsukatte, kono hen no nagasa o motomenasai.","german":"Berechne mit dem Satz des Pythagoras die Länge dieser Seite.","furigana":"さんへいほう|ていり|つか|へん|なが|もと"},{"japanese":"その定理は三百年以上もの間、誰にも証明されなかった。","romaji":"Sono teiri wa sanbyakunen ijō mo no aida, dare ni mo shōmei sarenakatta.","german":"Über dreihundert Jahre lang konnte niemand dieses Theorem beweisen.","furigana":"ていり|さんびゃくねんいじょう|あいだ|だれ|しょうめい"}],"notes":"定理 ist ein bewiesener mathematischer „Lehrsatz, Theorem“; im Deutschen meist „Satz“ (ピタゴラスの定理 „Satz des Pythagoras“, in japanischen Schulen meist 三平方の定理 genannt). Typische Verben sind 定理を証明する und 定理を使う/応用する. Davon unterscheidet man 公式 „Formel“, die als Rechenregel angewendet wird, und 公理 „Axiom“, das ohne Beweis vorausgesetzt wird.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この公式を使えば簡単に計算できる。","romaji":"Kono kōshiki o tsukaeba kantan ni keisan dekiru.","german":"If you use this formula, you can calculate easily.","furigana":"こうしき|つか|かんたん|けいさん"},{"japanese":"詳しい情報は公式サイトで確認してください。","romaji":"Kuwashii jōhō wa kōshiki saito de kakunin shite kudasai.","german":"Please check the official website for detailed information.","furigana":"くわ|じょうほう|こうしき|かくにん"},{"japanese":"政府はまだ公式に発表していない。","romaji":"Seifu wa mada kōshiki ni happyō shite inai.","german":"The government has not yet announced it officially.","furigana":"せいふ|こうしき|はっぴょう"}],"notes":"公式 has two important meanings: in mathematics and natural science the \"formula\" (公式を覚える/使う/当てはめる), and in everyday life \"official\" as a の-adjective, adverb with に or in compounds: 公式サイト \"official website\", 公式発表 \"official announcement\", 公式に認める. Today 公式 alone often also means a company's official social media account. Close in meaning is 正式 \"formal, proper\" (正式な契約); 公式 stresses the public, official side.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"磁石の磁力で鉄を引き寄せる。","romaji":"Jishaku no jiryoku de tetsu o hikiyoseru.","german":"The magnet's magnetic force attracts iron.","furigana":"じしゃく|じりょく|てつ|ひ|よ"},{"japanese":"この磁石は磁力が強いので、指を挟まないように注意してください。","romaji":"Kono jishaku wa jiryoku ga tsuyoi node, yubi o hasamanai yō ni chūi shite kudasai.","german":"This magnet has strong magnetic force, so be careful not to pinch your fingers.","furigana":"じしゃく|じりょく|つよ|ゆび|はさ|ちゅうい"},{"japanese":"リニアモーターカーは磁力を利用して浮いて走る。","romaji":"Rinia mōtākā wa jiryoku o riyō shite uite hashiru.","german":"The maglev train uses magnetic force to float and run.","furigana":"じりょく|りよう|う|はし"}],"notes":"磁力 is the \"magnetic force\" with which a 磁石 \"magnet\" attracts iron or repels other magnets. Typical are 磁力が強い/弱い, 磁力で引き寄せる/くっつく and 磁力線 \"magnetic field lines\". The magnetic field as a space is called 磁場 or 磁界, magnetism as a property 磁気 (磁気カード \"magnetic card\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"再生可能エネルギーの利用が増えている。","romaji":"Saiseikanō enerugī no riyō ga fuete iru.","german":"Die Nutzung erneuerbarer Energien nimmt zu.","furigana":"さいせいかのう|りよう|ふ"},{"japanese":"子どもたちはエネルギーにあふれていて、一日中走り回っている。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa enerugī ni afurete ite, ichinichijū hashirimawatte iru.","german":"Die Kinder strotzen vor Energie und rennen den ganzen Tag herum.","furigana":"こ|いちにちじゅうはし|まわ"},{"japanese":"このお菓子は一つで二百キロカロリーのエネルギーがある。","romaji":"Kono okashi wa hitotsu de nihyaku kirokarorī no enerugī ga aru.","german":"Ein Stück dieser Süßigkeit hat 200 Kilokalorien.","furigana":"かし|ひと|にひゃく"}],"notes":"エネルギー stammt aus dem deutschen „Energie“ (daher ギー statt ジー) und bedeutet physikalische Energie, Energiequellen wie Strom oder Öl, aber auch Lebenskraft und Tatendrang. Typische Komposita sind 再生可能エネルギー „erneuerbare Energien“, エネルギー問題 und 省エネ (kurz für 省エネルギー) „Energiesparen“. Bei Menschen sagt man エネルギーにあふれる „vor Energie strotzen“; bei Lebensmitteln bedeutet エネルギー „Kalorien, Brennwert“.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この工場は大量の電力を消費する。","romaji":"Kono kōjō wa tairyō no denryoku o shōhi suru.","german":"This factory consumes a large amount of electric power.","furigana":"こうじょう|たいりょう|でんりょく|しょうひ"},{"japanese":"夏は冷房の使用で電力不足が心配される。","romaji":"Natsu wa reibō no shiyō de denryoku busoku ga shinpai sareru.","german":"In summer, power shortages are feared because of air-conditioner use.","furigana":"なつ|れいぼう|しよう|でんりょくぶそく|しんぱい"},{"japanese":"この冷蔵庫は消費電力が少ない。","romaji":"Kono reizōko wa shōhi denryoku ga sukunai.","german":"This refrigerator consumes little power.","furigana":"れいぞうこ|しょうひでんりょく|すく"}],"notes":"電力 is \"electric energy, electricity\" in the sense of supply and consumption, and in physics also the \"electric power\" in watts. Typical are 電力を消費する/供給する, 電力不足 \"power shortage\", 電力会社 \"power company\" and 消費電力 \"power consumption\" of a device. In everyday speech one mostly says simply 電気 for electricity (電気代 \"electricity bill\", 電気をつける); 電力 sounds more technical and belongs in news and business.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本の家庭の電圧は百ボルトだ。","romaji":"Nihon no katei no den'atsu wa hyaku boruto da.","german":"The household voltage in Japan is 100 volts.","furigana":"にほん|かてい|でんあつ|ひゃく"},{"japanese":"海外で使うときは、電圧が合っているか確認してください。","romaji":"Kaigai de tsukau toki wa, den'atsu ga atte iru ka kakunin shite kudasai.","german":"When using it abroad, please check that the voltage matches.","furigana":"かいがい|つか|でんあつ|あ|かくにん"},{"japanese":"電圧が急に下がって、パソコンの電源が落ちた。","romaji":"Den'atsu ga kyū ni sagatte, pasokon no dengen ga ochita.","german":"The voltage suddenly dropped and the computer shut off.","furigana":"でんあつ|きゅう|さ|でんげん|お"}],"notes":"電圧 is the electric \"voltage\", measured in ボルト \"volts\". Typical are 電圧が高い/低い, 電圧を上げる/下げる, 高電圧 \"high voltage\" and 電圧計 \"voltmeter\". For comparison: 電流 is the \"current\" (amperes), 電力 the \"power\" (watts). For \"tension\" in the sense of nervousness or suspense, however, one says 緊張 or ハラハラ; 電圧 is purely technical.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"電波の周波数を調整した。","romaji":"Denpa no shūhasū o chōsei shita.","german":"I adjusted the frequency of the radio waves.","furigana":"でんぱ|しゅうはすう|ちょうせい"},{"japanese":"東日本と西日本では電気の周波数が違う。","romaji":"Higashi Nihon to Nishi Nihon de wa denki no shūhasū ga chigau.","german":"The electrical frequency differs between eastern and western Japan.","furigana":"ひがしにほん|にしにほん|でんき|しゅうはすう|ちが"},{"japanese":"ラジオの周波数を合わせて、好きな番組を聞いた。","romaji":"Rajio no shūhasū o awasete, suki na bangumi o kiita.","german":"I tuned the radio to the frequency and listened to my favorite program.","furigana":"しゅうはすう|あ|す|ばんぐみ|き"}],"notes":"周波数 is the \"frequency\" of an oscillation or wave, measured in ヘルツ \"hertz\". Typical are 周波数を合わせる \"to tune (a radio) to a frequency\", 周波数が高い/低い and 周波数帯 \"frequency band\". A Japanese peculiarity: the mains frequency is 50 Hz in eastern Japan and 60 Hz in western Japan. For \"frequency\" in the general sense of how often, however, one says 頻度 or 回数.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"光の色は波長によって決まる。","romaji":"Hikari no iro wa hachō ni yotte kimaru.","german":"The color of light is determined by its wavelength.","furigana":"ひかり|いろ|はちょう|き"},{"japanese":"赤い光は青い光より波長が長い。","romaji":"Akai hikari wa aoi hikari yori hachō ga nagai.","german":"Red light has a longer wavelength than blue light.","furigana":"あか|ひかり|あお|ひかり|はちょう|なが"},{"japanese":"彼とは初めて会ったときから波長が合った。","romaji":"Kare to wa hajimete atta toki kara hachō ga atta.","german":"From the first time I met him, we were on the same wavelength.","furigana":"かれ|はじ|あ|はちょう|あ"}],"notes":"波長 is the \"wavelength\" in physics, e.g. of light, sound or radio waves (波長が長い/短い). Very common is the figurative expression 波長が合う \"to be on the same wavelength\", i.e. to get along well; negated 波長が合わない. In this meaning it is colloquial and similar to 気が合う.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"室内の温度を二十五度に設定した。","romaji":"Shitsunai no ondo o nijūgo do ni settei shita.","german":"The room temperature was set to 25 degrees.","furigana":"しつない|おんど|にじゅうごど|せってい"},{"japanese":"天ぷらを揚げるときは、油の温度に気をつけてください。","romaji":"Tenpura o ageru toki wa, abura no ondo ni ki o tsukete kudasai.","german":"When deep-frying tempura, pay attention to the temperature of the oil.","furigana":"てん|あ|あぶら|おんど|き"},{"japanese":"部屋と外の温度差で、窓ガラスが曇っている。","romaji":"Heya to soto no ondosa de, mado garasu ga kumotte iru.","german":"The window glass is fogged up because of the temperature difference between the room and outside.","furigana":"へや|そと|おんどさ|まど|くも"}],"notes":"温度 is \"temperature\" in general - of rooms, water, substances or devices; typical collocations are 温度が上がる/下がる, 温度を測る, 温度を設定する and 温度計 \"thermometer\". For the air temperature in the weather report one says 気温, for body temperature 体温 and for water 水温. 温度差 means \"temperature difference\", and figuratively also differing levels of enthusiasm among those involved.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 41"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"水深が深くなるほど、水の圧力は高くなる。","romaji":"Suishin ga fukaku naru hodo, mizu no atsuryoku wa takaku naru.","german":"The deeper the water, the higher the water pressure.","furigana":"すいしん|ふか|みず|あつりょく|たか"},{"japanese":"圧力鍋を使えば、肉が短時間で柔らかくなる。","romaji":"Atsuryoku nabe o tsukaeba, niku ga tanjikan de yawarakaku naru.","german":"With a pressure cooker, meat becomes tender in a short time.","furigana":"あつりょくなべ|つか|にく|たんじかん|やわ"},{"japanese":"上からの圧力で、その計画は中止された。","romaji":"Ue kara no atsuryoku de, sono keikaku wa chūshi sareta.","german":"Because of pressure from above, the plan was canceled.","furigana":"うえ|あつりょく|けいかく|ちゅうし"}],"notes":"圧力 is, physically, \"pressure\" (force per area), as in 圧力鍋 \"pressure cooker\"; more specific terms are 気圧 \"air pressure\" and 水圧 \"water pressure\". Very common is the figurative meaning \"political or social pressure\": 圧力をかける \"to exert pressure\", 圧力を受ける \"to be put under pressure\", 政治的な圧力. For personal performance pressure or nervousness, people rather say プレッシャー (プレッシャーを感じる).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"食品の加工技術が進歩した。","romaji":"Shokuhin no kakō gijutsu ga shinpo shita.","german":"Food processing technology has advanced.","furigana":"しょくひん|かこうぎじゅつ|しんぽ"},{"japanese":"加工食品ばかり食べるのは体によくない。","romaji":"Kakō shokuhin bakari taberu no wa karada ni yoku nai.","german":"Eating only processed foods is not good for your health.","furigana":"かこうしょくひん|た|からだ"},{"japanese":"この写真は加工しすぎて、本人だと分からない。","romaji":"Kono shashin wa kakō shisugite, honnin da to wakaranai.","german":"This photo is so heavily edited that I can't tell it's the person.","furigana":"しゃしん|かこう|ほんにん|わ"}],"notes":"加工 is the \"processing\" of a raw material into a product; as a transitive する-verb: 木材を加工する \"to process wood\". Typical are 加工食品 \"processed foods\", 加工品, 金属加工 and, in daily life, 写真を加工する \"to edit a photo\" (加工アプリ). 処理 is more general \"treatment, handling\" (data, waste, tasks), while 加工 changes the form of a material or image.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"部品の組立は手作業で行われる。","romaji":"Buhin no kumitate wa tesagyō de okonawareru.","german":"The assembly of the parts is done by hand.","furigana":"ぶひん|くみたて|てさぎょう|おこな"},{"japanese":"兄は自動車の組立工場で働いている。","romaji":"Ani wa jidōsha no kumitate kōjō de hataraite iru.","german":"My older brother works in an automobile assembly plant.","furigana":"あに|じどうしゃ|くみたてこうじょう|はたら"},{"japanese":"この本棚は組み立てに一時間かかった。","romaji":"Kono hondana wa kumitate ni ichijikan kakatta.","german":"Putting this bookshelf together took an hour.","furigana":"ほんだな|く|た|いちじかん"}],"notes":"組立 is the noun for 組み立てる \"to assemble\" and means \"assembly, putting together\", figuratively also \"structure, composition\" (文章の組み立て). The spelling without okurigana 組立 is seen mainly in technical terms and on signs such as 組立工場 \"assembly plant\" and 組立ライン; in running text and instructions one usually finds 組み立て (組み立て式の家具 \"furniture you assemble yourself\"). The related verb is transitive: 棚を組み立てる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい橋の建設が始まった。","romaji":"Atarashii hashi no kensetsu ga hajimatta.","german":"Construction of a new bridge has begun.","furigana":"あたら|はし|けんせつ|はじ"},{"japanese":"駅前に高層マンションが建設中だ。","romaji":"Ekimae ni kōsō manshon ga kensetsuchū da.","german":"A high-rise apartment building is under construction in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|こうそう|けんせつちゅう"},{"japanese":"文句を言うだけでなく、建設的な意見を出してください。","romaji":"Monku o iu dake de naku, kensetsuteki na iken o dashite kudasai.","german":"Don't just complain; please offer constructive opinions.","furigana":"もんく|い|けんせつてき|いけん|だ"}],"notes":"建設 is the \"construction\" of large structures and facilities such as roads, bridges, dams or airports; as a transitive する-verb: ダムを建設する. Typical are 建設中 \"under construction\", 建設会社 \"construction company\" and 建設現場 \"construction site\". For individual buildings such as houses, one rather says 建築 or simply 建てる. Figuratively, 建設 also stands for \"building up\" something abstract, and 建設的 means \"constructive\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鉄は多くの建築物に使われている。","romaji":"Tetsu wa ōku no kenchikubutsu ni tsukawarete iru.","german":"Iron is used in many buildings.","furigana":"てつ|おお|けんちくぶつ|つか"},{"japanese":"鉄は熱いうちに打てと言うから、若いうちにいろいろ挑戦しなさい。","romaji":"Tetsu wa atsui uchi ni ute to iu kara, wakai uchi ni iroiro chōsen shinasai.","german":"They say to strike while the iron is hot, so try many challenges while you are young.","furigana":"てつ|あつ|う|い|わか|ちょうせん"},{"japanese":"レバーには鉄分がたくさん含まれている。","romaji":"Rebā ni wa tetsubun ga takusan fukumarete iru.","german":"Liver contains a lot of iron.","furigana":"てつぶん|ふく"}],"notes":"鉄 is the metal \"iron\", in daily life often also meaning \"steel\" (鉄の扉, 鉄骨 \"steel frame\"). Common compounds are 鉄道 \"railway\", 地下鉄 \"subway\", 鉄分 \"iron content (in food)\" and 鉄棒 \"horizontal bar\". Well known is the proverb 鉄は熱いうちに打て \"strike while the iron is hot\". Colloquially, 〜鉄 also denotes railway fans, e.g. 撮り鉄 \"train photographer\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鋼は鉄より硬くて丈夫だ。","romaji":"Hagane wa tetsu yori katakute jōbu da.","german":"Steel is harder and more durable than iron.","furigana":"はがね|てつ|かた|じょうぶ"},{"japanese":"この包丁は良い鋼で作られているので、よく切れる。","romaji":"Kono hōchō wa yoi hagane de tsukurarete iru node, yoku kireru.","german":"This kitchen knife is made of good steel, so it cuts well.","furigana":"ほうちょう|よ|はがね|つく|き"},{"japanese":"彼は鋼のような強い意志を持っている。","romaji":"Kare wa hagane no yō na tsuyoi ishi o motte iru.","german":"He has a will of steel.","furigana":"かれ|はがね|つよ|いし|も"}],"notes":"鋼 (はがね) is \"steel\", i.e. iron hardened with carbon; the word sounds somewhat craftsmanlike or literary and appears, for example, with knives and swords. In industry and technology one mostly says 鋼鉄, 鉄鋼 (鉄鋼業 \"steel industry\") or スチール; in compounds 鋼 is read こう (鋼材 \"steel material\"). Figuratively it stands for hardness and strength: 鋼の意志 \"iron will\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"銅は電気をよく通す金属だ。","romaji":"Dō wa denki o yoku tōsu kinzoku da.","german":"Copper is a metal that conducts electricity well.","furigana":"どう|でんき|とお|きんぞく"},{"japanese":"彼はオリンピックで銅メダルを取った。","romaji":"Kare wa Orinpikku de dō medaru o totta.","german":"He won a bronze medal at the Olympics.","furigana":"かれ|どう|と"},{"japanese":"公園の入り口に有名な作家の銅像が立っている。","romaji":"Kōen no iriguchi ni yūmei na sakka no dōzō ga tatte iru.","german":"A statue of a famous writer stands at the entrance of the park.","furigana":"こうえん|い|ぐち|ゆうめい|さっか|どうぞう|た"}],"notes":"銅 is the metal \"copper\" (Cu), which conducts electricity and heat well. As a short form it stands for \"bronze\" in medals: 金・銀・銅, 銅メダル \"bronze medal\". Other compounds are 銅像 \"bronze statue\", 青銅 \"bronze\" and 銅線 \"copper wire\". In everyday life the word occurs mainly in these fixed combinations; on its own it is used more in technology and chemistry.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"アルミは軽くてリサイクルしやすい。","romaji":"Arumi wa karukute risaikuru shiyasui.","german":"Aluminum is light and easy to recycle.","furigana":"かる"},{"japanese":"アルミ缶とスチール缶は分けて捨ててください。","romaji":"Arumi kan to sutīru kan wa wakete sutete kudasai.","german":"Please dispose of aluminum cans and steel cans separately."},{"japanese":"魚をアルミホイルに包んでオーブンで焼いた。","romaji":"Sakana o arumi hoiru ni tsutsunde ōbun de yaita.","german":"I wrapped the fish in aluminum foil and baked it in the oven.","furigana":"さかな|つつ|や"}],"notes":"アルミ is the usual short form of アルミニウム \"aluminum\"; in everyday speech one hears almost only the short form. Typical compounds are アルミ缶 \"aluminum can\", アルミホイル \"aluminum foil\" and アルミサッシ \"aluminum window frame\". In waste sorting, people distinguish アルミ缶 and スチール缶 \"steel can\". In scientific texts the full form アルミニウム is more common.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"建物の基礎はコンクリートで作られる。","romaji":"Tatemono no kiso wa konkurīto de tsukurareru.","german":"The foundations of buildings are made of concrete.","furigana":"たてもの|きそ|つく"},{"japanese":"このマンションは鉄筋コンクリートで、地震に強い。","romaji":"Kono manshon wa tekkin konkurīto de, jishin ni tsuyoi.","german":"This apartment building is reinforced concrete and resistant to earthquakes.","furigana":"てっきん|じしん|つよ"},{"japanese":"夏はコンクリートの道路が熱くなるので、犬の散歩は夕方にしている。","romaji":"Natsu wa konkurīto no dōro ga atsuku naru node, inu no sanpo wa yūgata ni shite iru.","german":"In summer, concrete roads get hot, so I walk the dog in the evening.","furigana":"なつ|どうろ|あつ|いぬ|さんぽ|ゆうがた"}],"notes":"コンクリート is \"concrete\"; typical combinations are コンクリートの建物/壁, 鉄筋コンクリート \"reinforced concrete\" and コンクリートを流す \"to pour concrete\". Colloquially it is shortened to コンクリ, and コンクリートジャングル figuratively stands for a big city dominated by concrete. Note: the adjective \"concrete\" (as opposed to abstract) is not コンクリート but 具体的.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"プラスチックの使用を減らすべきだ。","romaji":"Purasuchikku no shiyō o herasu beki da.","german":"We should reduce the use of plastic.","furigana":"しよう|へ"},{"japanese":"プラスチックの容器の中には、電子レンジで使えないものもある。","romaji":"Purasuchikku no yōki no naka ni wa, denshi renji de tsukaenai mono mo aru.","german":"Some plastic containers can't be used in the microwave.","furigana":"ようき|なか|でんし|つか"},{"japanese":"海に流れ出たプラスチックごみが問題になっている。","romaji":"Umi ni nagaredeta purasuchikku gomi ga mondai ni natte iru.","german":"Plastic waste that flows into the sea has become a problem.","furigana":"うみ|なが|で|もんだい"}],"notes":"プラスチック is \"plastic\"; as an attribute it takes の: プラスチックの容器 \"plastic container\". Common are プラスチックごみ \"plastic waste\", 海洋プラスチック \"marine plastic\" and, in waste sorting, the short form プラ (プラごみ). A plastic bag, however, is not called プラスチック袋 but ビニール袋 or レジ袋.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"窓にはガラスがはめられている。","romaji":"Mado ni wa garasu ga hamerarete iru.","german":"Glass is fitted in the windows.","furigana":"まど"},{"japanese":"子どもがボールで窓ガラスを割ってしまいました。","romaji":"Kodomo ga bōru de mado garasu o watte shimaimashita.","german":"A child broke the window glass with a ball.","furigana":"こ|まど|わ"},{"japanese":"このコップはガラスでできているので、気をつけてください。","romaji":"Kono koppu wa garasu de dekite iru node, ki o tsukete kudasai.","german":"This cup is made of glass, so please be careful.","furigana":"き"}],"notes":"ガラス is glass as a material and is written in katakana (borrowed from Dutch). Typical are 窓ガラス \"window pane\", ガラス製 \"made of glass\" and ガラスが割れる. A drinking glass, on the other hand, is called コップ or, for wine and the like, グラス - ガラス refers only to the material. Neutral in tone.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 29"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このエンジンは燃費が良い。","romaji":"Kono enjin wa nenpi ga yoi.","german":"This engine has good fuel economy.","furigana":"ねんぴ|よ"},{"japanese":"寒い朝はなかなかエンジンがかからない。","romaji":"Samui asa wa nakanaka enjin ga kakaranai.","german":"On cold mornings the engine hardly starts.","furigana":"さむ|あさ"},{"japanese":"午後になって、やっとエンジンがかかってきた。","romaji":"Gogo ni natte, yatto enjin ga kakatte kita.","german":"It was only in the afternoon that I finally got going.","furigana":"ごご"}],"notes":"エンジン is the \"engine\" of cars, aircraft or ships, usually a combustion engine; electric motors are called モーター. Typical are エンジンをかける \"to start the engine\", エンジンを切る/止める \"to turn off\" and エンジンがかかる \"to start\". Figuratively エンジンがかかる means \"to get going\"; in IT there is also 検索エンジン \"search engine\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 32"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"電気自動車はモーターで走る。","romaji":"Denki jidōsha wa mōtā de hashiru.","german":"Electric cars run on a motor.","furigana":"でんきじどうしゃ|はし"},{"japanese":"洗濯機のモーターが壊れて、変な音がする。","romaji":"Sentakuki no mōtā ga kowarete, hen na oto ga suru.","german":"The washing machine's motor is broken and makes a strange noise.","furigana":"せんたくき|こわ|へん|おと"},{"japanese":"子どものころ、モーターで動くおもちゃの車を作った。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, mōtā de ugoku omocha no kuruma o tsukutta.","german":"As a child, I built a toy car powered by a motor.","furigana":"こ|うご|くるま|つく"}],"notes":"モーター is above all the \"electric motor\", which converts electrical energy into motion - in fans, washing machines, toys or electric cars. Combustion engines are mostly called エンジン in Japanese. In compounds such as モーターショー \"motor show\" and モータースポーツ \"motorsport\", モーター stands generally for motor vehicles; a power generator, on the other hand, is called 発電機.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"工場でロボットが作業をしている。","romaji":"Kōjō de robotto ga sagyō o shite iru.","german":"Robots are working in the factory.","furigana":"こうじょう|さぎょう"},{"japanese":"ロボット掃除機を買ってから、掃除がずいぶん楽になった。","romaji":"Robotto sōjiki o katte kara, sōji ga zuibun raku ni natta.","german":"Since I bought a robot vacuum cleaner, cleaning has become much easier.","furigana":"そうじき|か|そうじ|らく"},{"japanese":"彼は上司の言うことを聞くだけのロボットのようだ。","romaji":"Kare wa jōshi no iu koto o kiku dake no robotto no yō da.","german":"He is like a robot that only does what his boss says.","furigana":"かれ|じょうし|い|き"}],"notes":"ロボット means a \"robot\" - industrial robots (産業用ロボット), household appliances such as ロボット掃除機 \"robot vacuum cleaner\" or characters from anime and science fiction. Typical verbs are ロボットを作る/動かす/操作する. Figuratively and pejoratively, a person who only carries out orders is called a ロボット or 操り人形 \"puppet\". To be distinguished from this is AI or 人工知能, that is, software without a physical body.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 22 (source: N5; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この機器は精度が非常に高い。","romaji":"Kono kiki wa seido ga hijō ni takai.","german":"This instrument is extremely precise.","furigana":"きき|せいど|ひじょう|たか"},{"japanese":"天気予報の精度は年々上がっている。","romaji":"Tenki yohō no seido wa nennen agatte iru.","german":"The accuracy of weather forecasts is improving every year.","furigana":"てんきよほう|せいど|ねんねんあ"},{"japanese":"機械翻訳の精度を高めるために、大量のデータが使われている。","romaji":"Kikai hon'yaku no seido o takameru tame ni, tairyō no dēta ga tsukawarete iru.","german":"A large amount of data is used to improve the accuracy of machine translation.","furigana":"きかいほんやく|せいど|たか|たいりょう|つか"}],"notes":"精度 is the \"precision, accuracy\" of measurements, devices, machines or forecasts. Typical are 精度が高い/低い, 精度を上げる/高める and 高精度 \"high-precision\". Unlike 正確さ \"correctness\", which also applies to people and statements, 精度 is a technical term for measurable quality, e.g. 翻訳の精度 or 天気予報の精度. Not to be confused with the identically read 制度 \"system, institution\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しいシステムを導入した。","romaji":"Atarashii shisutemu o dōnyū shita.","german":"A new system was introduced.","furigana":"あたら|どうにゅう"},{"japanese":"システム障害のため、銀行のATMが使えなくなった。","romaji":"Shisutemu shōgai no tame, ginkō no ATM ga tsukaenaku natta.","german":"Because of a system failure, the bank's ATMs could not be used.","furigana":"しょうがい|ぎんこう|つか"},{"japanese":"この店は初めてなので、注文のシステムがよく分からない。","romaji":"Kono mise wa hajimete na node, chūmon no shisutemu ga yoku wakaranai.","german":"It's my first time at this restaurant, so I don't quite understand how the ordering system works.","furigana":"みせ|はじ|ちゅうもん|わ"}],"notes":"システム means \"system\" - technical systems (IT, control) as well as organizational procedures. Typical are システムを導入する \"to introduce\", システムが止まる \"to go down\", システム障害 \"system failure\" and システムエンジニア. For social or legal systems (tax system, education system) one uses 制度 more often. In everyday life, システム is often used to explain how something works: この店のシステム \"how things work in this shop\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 46"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ソフトウェアの更新が必要だ。","romaji":"Sofutowea no kōshin ga hitsuyō da.","german":"A software update is necessary.","furigana":"こうしん|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"姉はソフトウェア開発の会社で働いている。","romaji":"Ane wa sofutowea kaihatsu no kaisha de hataraite iru.","german":"My older sister works at a software development company.","furigana":"あね|かいはつ|かいしゃ|はたら"},{"japanese":"このソフトウェアは無料でダウンロードできる。","romaji":"Kono sofutowea wa muryō de daunrōdo dekiru.","german":"This software can be downloaded for free.","furigana":"むりょう"}],"notes":"ソフトウェア is \"software\"; in everyday speech it is almost always shortened to ソフト (ゲームソフト, 会計ソフト, ソフトを入れる \"to install software\"). Typical are ソフトウェアを更新する/アップデートする, ソフトウェア開発 and ソフトウェアエンジニア. The spelling ソフトウエア with a large エ also occurs; the counterpart is ハードウェア.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ハードウェアの故障で動かなくなった。","romaji":"Hādowea no koshō de ugokanaku natta.","german":"It stopped working due to a hardware failure.","furigana":"こしょう|うご"},{"japanese":"このゲームは古いハードウェアでは動かない。","romaji":"Kono gēmu wa furui hādowea de wa ugokanai.","german":"This game doesn't run on old hardware.","furigana":"ふる|うご"},{"japanese":"彼女はソフトだけでなくハードウェアの設計もできる。","romaji":"Kanojo wa sofuto dake de naku hādowea no sekkei mo dekiru.","german":"She can design not only software but also hardware.","furigana":"かのじょ|せっけい"}],"notes":"ハードウェア is \"hardware\", i.e. the physical components of a computer or device; in everyday speech often shortened to ハード (ハードとソフト). Typical are ハードウェアの故障/性能 and ハードウェアを交換する. Note: ハード alone can also mean \"hard, strenuous\" (ハードな仕事 \"a hard job\"). A hardware store, on the other hand, is called ホームセンター.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しいプログラムを書いた。","romaji":"Atarashii puroguramu o kaita.","german":"I wrote a new program.","furigana":"あたら|か"},{"japanese":"入口でコンサートのプログラムを受け取った。","romaji":"Iriguchi de konsāto no puroguramu o uketotta.","german":"I received the concert program at the entrance.","furigana":"いりぐち|う|と"},{"japanese":"新入社員は三か月の研修プログラムに参加する。","romaji":"Shinnyū shain wa sankagetsu no kenshū puroguramu ni sanka suru.","german":"New employees take part in a three-month training program.","furigana":"しんにゅうしゃいん|さん|げつ|けんしゅう|さんか"}],"notes":"プログラム has several meanings: a \"program\" as the schedule of an event (コンサートのプログラム), the printed \"program booklet\" and the computer program. Typical are プログラムを書く/組む \"to program\", プログラムを実行する \"to run\" as well as 研修プログラム \"training program\". Programming as an activity is mostly called プログラミング; a television program, on the other hand, is 番組.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"便利なアプリをダウンロードした。","romaji":"Benri na apuri o daunrōdo shita.","german":"I downloaded a handy app.","furigana":"べんり"},{"japanese":"このアプリを使えば、電車の乗り換えがすぐ分かります。","romaji":"Kono apuri o tsukaeba, densha no norikae ga sugu wakarimasu.","german":"With this app, you can instantly see where to transfer trains.","furigana":"つか|でんしゃ|の|か|わ"},{"japanese":"容量が足りないので、使わないアプリを削除した。","romaji":"Yōryō ga tarinai node, tsukawanai apuri o sakujo shita.","german":"I ran out of storage, so I deleted apps I don't use.","furigana":"ようりょう|た|つか|さくじょ"}],"notes":"アプリ is the common short form of アプリケーション; in everyday speech one almost only says アプリ, especially for smartphone apps (スマホアプリ). Typical combinations: アプリをダウンロードする／インストールする (colloquially アプリを入れる), アプリを開く, アプリを更新する, アプリを削除する or, casually, アプリを消す. The long form アプリケーション sounds more technical and often refers to computer programs.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"データをクラウドに保存した。","romaji":"Dēta o kuraudo ni hozon shita.","german":"I saved the data to the cloud.","furigana":"ほぞん"},{"japanese":"写真はクラウド上で家族と共有しています。","romaji":"Shashin wa kuraudo-jō de kazoku to kyōyū shite imasu.","german":"I share photos with my family on the cloud.","furigana":"しゃしん|じょう|かぞく|きょうゆう"},{"japanese":"会社は来年からクラウドサービスに切り替える予定だ。","romaji":"Kaisha wa rainen kara kuraudo sābisu ni kirikaeru yotei da.","german":"The company plans to switch to a cloud service from next year.","furigana":"かいしゃ|らいねん|き|か|よてい"}],"notes":"クラウド refers in IT to the \"cloud\", i.e. storage and services on the internet: クラウドに保存する, クラウドにアップロードする, クラウド上のデータ, クラウドサービス. Note: the same katakana spelling also stands for English \"crowd\", e.g. in クラウドファンディング \"crowdfunding\". The cloud in the sky, in contrast, is always 雲.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"セキュリティ対策を強化した。","romaji":"Sekyuriti taisaku o kyōka shita.","german":"We strengthened security measures.","furigana":"たいさく|きょうか"},{"japanese":"このビルはセキュリティが厳しくて、社員証がないと入れない。","romaji":"Kono biru wa sekyuriti ga kibishikute, shainshō ga nai to hairenai.","german":"Security in this building is strict, and you can't get in without an employee ID.","furigana":"きび|しゃいんしょう|はい"},{"japanese":"パスワードが簡単すぎると、セキュリティ上問題がある。","romaji":"Pasuwādo ga kantan sugiru to, sekyuriti-jō mondai ga aru.","german":"If a password is too simple, there is a security problem.","furigana":"かんたん|じょうもんだい"}],"notes":"セキュリティ means security in the sense of protection against attacks, theft or unauthorized access - for computers as well as for buildings. Typical: セキュリティ対策, セキュリティを強化する, セキュリティが厳しい／甘い, セキュリティ上の問題. For \"safety\" as harmlessness (traffic, food) one uses 安全, for national security 安全保障. The spelling セキュリティー with a long vowel is also common.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コンピューターがウイルスに感染した。","romaji":"Konpyūtā ga uirusu ni kansen shita.","german":"The computer was infected with a virus.","furigana":"かんせん"},{"japanese":"インフルエンザはウイルスによって引き起こされる病気です。","romaji":"Infuruenza wa uirusu ni yotte hikiokosareru byōki desu.","german":"Influenza is a disease caused by a virus.","furigana":"ひ|お|びょうき"},{"japanese":"手を洗って、ウイルスが体に入るのを防ぎましょう。","romaji":"Te o aratte, uirusu ga karada ni hairu no o fusegimashō.","german":"Let's wash our hands to prevent viruses from entering the body.","furigana":"て|あら|からだ|はい|ふせ"}],"notes":"ウイルス stands both for disease-causing agents and for computer viruses. Typical: ウイルスに感染する \"to get infected\", ウイルスを除去する／駆除する \"to remove a virus\", ウイルス対策ソフト. The spelling ウィルス with a small ィ is also common in everyday use, but ウイルス is the official and medical form. Bacteria, in contrast, are called 細菌 or simply 菌.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"帯域が不足して動画が止まる。","romaji":"Taiiki ga fusoku shite dōga ga tomaru.","german":"The video stalls because of insufficient bandwidth.","furigana":"たいいき|ふそく|どうが|と"},{"japanese":"会議中は帯域を確保するため、大きなファイルのダウンロードは控えてください。","romaji":"Kaigichū wa taiiki o kakuho suru tame, ōki na fairu no daunrōdo wa hikaete kudasai.","german":"During the meeting, please refrain from downloading large files so that bandwidth is kept available.","furigana":"かいぎちゅう|たいいき|かくほ|おお|ひか"},{"japanese":"この周波数帯域は携帯電話に使われています。","romaji":"Kono shūhasū taiiki wa keitai denwa ni tsukawarete imasu.","german":"This frequency band is used for mobile phones.","furigana":"しゅうはすうたいいき|けいたいでんわ|つか"}],"notes":"帯域 is a technical word: originally a \"frequency band, range\" (周波数帯域), in IT the short form of 帯域幅 \"bandwidth\". Typical: 帯域が不足する, 帯域が狭い／広い, 帯域を確保する, 帯域制限 \"throttling\". In everyday speech one rather says 回線速度 or 通信速度; 帯域 sounds technical.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"山の中では電波が届かない。","romaji":"Yama no naka de wa denpa ga todokanai.","german":"In the mountains, there is no reception.","furigana":"やま|なか|でんぱ|とど"},{"japanese":"地下だと電波が弱くて、電話が切れてしまった。","romaji":"Chika da to denpa ga yowakute, denwa ga kirete shimatta.","german":"In the basement the signal was so weak that the call dropped.","furigana":"ちか|でんぱ|よわ|でんわ|き"},{"japanese":"スマホは電波を使って基地局と通信している。","romaji":"Sumaho wa denpa o tsukatte kichikyoku to tsūshin shite iru.","german":"Smartphones communicate with the base station using radio waves.","furigana":"でんぱ|つか|きちきょく|つうしん"}],"notes":"電波 technically means \"radio waves\", but in everyday life above all the mobile or Wi-Fi \"reception\": 電波が届く／届かない, 電波が弱い／悪い, 電波が入らない \"no signal\"; with no signal at all one is 圏外. Technically: 電波を発する, 電波を受信する. Colloquially and pejoratively, someone who talks confused nonsense is called 電波系 - one should know this meaning but use it carefully.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仮想現実の技術がゲーム業界で注目されている。","romaji":"Kasō genjitsu no gijutsu ga gēmu gyōkai de chūmoku sarete iru.","german":"Virtual reality technology is attracting attention in the gaming industry.","furigana":"かそうげんじつ|ぎじゅつ|ぎょうかい|ちゅうもく"},{"japanese":"仮想現実を使えば、家にいながら世界中を旅行できる。","romaji":"Kasō genjitsu o tsukaeba, ie ni inagara sekaijū o ryokō dekiru.","german":"With virtual reality, you can travel all over the world while staying at home.","furigana":"かそうげんじつ|つか|いえ|せかいじゅう|りょこう"},{"japanese":"医学部では仮想現実で手術の練習をしている。","romaji":"Igakubu de wa kasō genjitsu de shujutsu no renshū o shite iru.","german":"At the medical school, they practice surgery in virtual reality.","furigana":"いがくぶ|かそうげんじつ|しゅじゅつ|れんしゅう"}],"notes":"仮想現実 is the Japanese translation of \"virtual reality\" and sounds technical or journalistic; in everyday speech one mostly says VR. 仮想 means \"virtual, hypothetical\" and is also found in 仮想通貨 \"cryptocurrency\". Typical: 仮想現実の世界, 仮想現実を体験する, 仮想現実の技術. To be distinguished from 拡張現実 \"augmented reality\", which only supplements the real environment.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"拡張現実を使ったアプリが人気だ。","romaji":"Kakuchō genjitsu o tsukatta apuri ga ninki da.","german":"Apps using augmented reality are popular.","furigana":"かくちょうげんじつ|つか|にんき"},{"japanese":"拡張現実の技術で、スマホを向けると、家具を部屋に置いたイメージが見られる。","romaji":"Kakuchō genjitsu no gijutsu de, sumaho o mukeru to, kagu o heya ni oita imēji ga mirareru.","german":"With augmented reality technology, if you point your smartphone, you can see how furniture would look placed in your room.","furigana":"かくちょうげんじつ|ぎじゅつ|む|かぐ|へや|お|み"},{"japanese":"博物館では拡張現実を使って、昔の町の様子を再現している。","romaji":"Hakubutsukan de wa kakuchō genjitsu o tsukatte, mukashi no machi no yōsu o saigen shite iru.","german":"The museum uses augmented reality to recreate what the town looked like in the past.","furigana":"はくぶつかん|かくちょうげんじつ|つか|むかし|まち|ようす|さいげん"}],"notes":"拡張現実 is \"augmented reality\": 拡張 means \"expansion\", so digital information is laid over the real environment. In contrast, 仮想現実 replaces reality completely. The word is technical-formal; in everyday speech one mostly says AR. Typical: 拡張現実を使ったアプリ, 拡張現実の技術, 拡張現実で再現する.","levelNote":"Compound: 拡張 (N2) + 現実 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"半導体の需要が世界中で高まっている。","romaji":"Handōtai no juyō ga sekaijū de takamatte iru.","german":"Demand for semiconductors is rising worldwide.","furigana":"はんどうたい|じゅよう|せかいじゅう|たか"},{"japanese":"半導体不足のせいで、新車が届くのが半年も遅れた。","romaji":"Handōtai busoku no sei de, shinsha ga todoku no ga hantoshi mo okureta.","german":"Because of the semiconductor shortage, my new car arrived a full six months late.","furigana":"はんどうたいぶそく|しんしゃ|とど|はんとし|おく"},{"japanese":"シリコンは代表的な半導体の材料だ。","romaji":"Shirikon wa daihyōteki na handōtai no zairyō da.","german":"Silicon is a typical semiconductor material.","furigana":"だいひょうてき|はんどうたい|ざいりょう"}],"notes":"半導体 is the \"semiconductor\", literally \"half-conductor\", between 導体 \"conductor\" and 絶縁体 \"insulator\". In business news it often stands for computer chips in general: 半導体メーカー, 半導体産業, 半導体不足 \"chip shortage\". Colloquially, one also says チップ for the individual component.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"電子回路の設計は複雑だ。","romaji":"Denshi kairo no sekkei wa fukuzatsu da.","german":"Designing electronic circuits is complicated.","furigana":"でんしかいろ|せっけい|ふくざつ"},{"japanese":"回路のどこかが切れているらしく、ライトがつかない。","romaji":"Kairo no dokoka ga kirete iru rashiku, raito ga tsukanai.","german":"The light won't turn on; the circuit seems to be broken somewhere.","furigana":"かいろ|き"},{"japanese":"あの人の思考回路はまったく理解できない。","romaji":"Ano hito no shikō kairo wa mattaku rikai dekinai.","german":"I can't understand that person's way of thinking at all.","furigana":"ひと|しこうかいろ|りかい"}],"notes":"回路 is a circuit: 電気回路, 電子回路, 集積回路 \"integrated circuit\"; 回路が切れる \"to be interrupted\". Figuratively, people speak of 思考回路 \"thought pattern, way of thinking\", often slightly mockingly. Do not confuse it with 回線, which means a line or connection (電話回線, インターネット回線).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"基板に部品をはんだ付けする。","romaji":"Kiban ni buhin o handazuke suru.","german":"I solder the components onto the circuit board.","furigana":"きばん|ぶひん|づ"},{"japanese":"パソコンが壊れたので修理に出したら、基板ごと交換された。","romaji":"Pasokon ga kowareta node shūri ni dashitara, kiban goto kōkan sareta.","german":"My computer was broken, and when I took it in for repair, the whole board was replaced.","furigana":"こわ|しゅうり|だ|きばん|こうかん"},{"japanese":"この工場ではスマホ用の基板を製造している。","romaji":"Kono kōjō de wa sumaho-yō no kiban o seizō shite iru.","german":"This factory manufactures circuit boards for smartphones.","furigana":"こうじょう|よう|きばん|せいぞう"}],"notes":"基板 is the circuit board in electronic devices (プリント基板), in manufacturing also a \"carrier material, substrate\". Typical: 基板に部品をはんだ付けする, 基板を交換する. Beware of the identically read 基盤 \"foundation, basis\" (生活の基盤, 経営基盤) - a common writing mistake, even among native speakers.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"電子機器の小型化が進んでいる。","romaji":"Denshi kiki no kogataka ga susunde iru.","german":"The miniaturization of electronic devices is progressing.","furigana":"でんしきき|こがたか|すす"},{"japanese":"最近は紙の本より電子書籍で読むことが多い。","romaji":"Saikin wa kami no hon yori denshi shoseki de yomu koto ga ōi.","german":"Recently I read e-books more often than paper books.","furigana":"さいきん|かみ|ほん|でんししょせき|よ|おお"},{"japanese":"原子は原子核と電子からできている。","romaji":"Genshi wa genshikaku to denshi kara dekite iru.","german":"An atom consists of a nucleus and electrons.","furigana":"げんし|げんしかく|でんし"}],"notes":"電子 is the \"electron\" in physics, but is used far more often as a modifier meaning \"electronic, digital\": 電子機器, 電子レンジ \"microwave\", 電子メール, 電子書籍 \"e-book\", 電子マネー. To be distinguished is 電気 \"electricity, power\", e.g. 電気製品 \"electrical appliance\". On its own, 電子 appears almost only in a scientific context.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 36"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しいデバイスが発売された。","romaji":"Atarashii debaisu ga hatsubai sareta.","german":"A new device went on sale.","furigana":"あたら|はつばい"},{"japanese":"このサービスは一つのアカウントで最大五台のデバイスに対応しています。","romaji":"Kono sābisu wa hitotsu no akaunto de saidai godai no debaisu ni taiō shite imasu.","german":"This service supports up to five devices with one account.","furigana":"ひと|さいだいごだい|たいおう"},{"japanese":"Bluetoothでデバイスを接続できない場合は、再起動してください。","romaji":"Bluetooth de debaisu o setsuzoku dekinai baai wa, saikidō shite kudasai.","german":"If you can't connect the device via Bluetooth, please restart it.","furigana":"せつぞく|ばあい|さいきどう"}],"notes":"デバイス is IT jargon for a (terminal) device such as a smartphone, tablet or PC, also for accessories and components. Typical: デバイスを接続する, 対応デバイス, 複数のデバイスで使う. In normal speech one rather says 機器, 端末 or simply 機械; デバイス sounds technical and appears mainly in manuals and advertising.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"温度センサーが異常を検知した。","romaji":"Ondo sensā ga ijō o kenchi shita.","german":"The temperature sensor detected an abnormality.","furigana":"おんど|いじょう|けんち"},{"japanese":"人が近づくと、センサーが反応して電気がつく。","romaji":"Hito ga chikazuku to, sensā ga hannō shite denki ga tsuku.","german":"When someone approaches, the sensor reacts and the light turns on.","furigana":"ひと|ちか|はんのう|でんき"},{"japanese":"この腕時計にはセンサーが付いていて、心拍数を測れる。","romaji":"Kono udedokei ni wa sensā ga tsuite ite, shinpakusū o hakareru.","german":"This wristwatch has a sensor and can measure heart rate.","furigana":"うでどけい|つ|しんぱくすう|はか"}],"notes":"センサー is a \"sensor\". Typical: センサーが反応する, センサーが異常を検知する／感知する, センサー付きの照明, 人感センサー \"motion detector\", 温度センサー. In technical texts one also finds the spelling センサ without the long vowel.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"スマホのバッテリーがすぐ切れる。","romaji":"Sumaho no batterī ga sugu kireru.","german":"My smartphone's battery runs out quickly.","furigana":"き"},{"japanese":"ライトをつけっぱなしにしていたら、車のバッテリーが上がってしまった。","romaji":"Raito o tsukeppanashi ni shite itara, kuruma no batterī ga agatte shimatta.","german":"I left the lights on and the car battery died.","furigana":"くるま|あ"},{"japanese":"この機種はバッテリーの持ちがいいので、一日充電しなくても大丈夫だ。","romaji":"Kono kishu wa batterī no mochi ga ii node, ichinichi jūden shinakute mo daijōbu da.","german":"This model has good battery life, so it's fine even if you don't charge it for a day.","furigana":"きしゅ|も|いちにちじゅうでん|だいじょうぶ"}],"notes":"バッテリー is mainly the rechargeable battery in a phone, laptop or car; ordinary disposable batteries are called 電池 or 乾電池. Typical: バッテリーが切れる／なくなる \"to be dead\", バッテリーが減る, バッテリーの持ちがいい \"lasts long\". For a car, people say バッテリーが上がる for \"the battery is dead\". In baseball, バッテリー refers to the duo of pitcher and catcher.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"寝る前にスマホを充電する。","romaji":"Neru mae ni sumaho o jūden suru.","german":"I charge my smartphone before going to bed.","furigana":"ね|まえ|じゅうでん"},{"japanese":"充電器を忘れたので、コンビニで買いました。","romaji":"Jūdenki o wasureta node, konbini de kaimashita.","german":"I forgot my charger, so I bought one at a convenience store.","furigana":"じゅうでんき|わす|か"},{"japanese":"仕事が忙しかったので、週末は家でゆっくり充電するつもりだ。","romaji":"Shigoto ga isogashikatta node, shūmatsu wa ie de yukkuri jūden suru tsumori da.","german":"Work was busy, so I plan to relax and recharge at home over the weekend.","furigana":"しごと|いそが|しゅうまつ|いえ|じゅうでん"}],"notes":"充電 is electrical \"charging\"; as a する-verb it is transitive: スマホを充電する. Noun combinations: 充電が切れる \"the battery is dead\", 充電が少ない, 充電中, 充電器 \"charger\". Colloquially, 充電する also means \"to recharge one's batteries\", e.g. after stressful weeks. The opposite is 放電 \"discharge\"; not to be confused with 発電 \"power generation\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"風力で発電している地域が増えた。","romaji":"Fūryoku de hatsuden shite iru chiiki ga fueta.","german":"The number of areas generating electricity with wind power has increased.","furigana":"ふうりょく|はつでん|ちいき|ふ"},{"japanese":"近くに新しい発電所が建設される予定だ。","romaji":"Chikaku ni atarashii hatsudensho ga kensetsu sareru yotei da.","german":"A new power plant is scheduled to be built nearby.","furigana":"ちか|あたら|はつでんしょ|けんせつ|よてい"},{"japanese":"災害で停電したときのために、家庭用の発電機を買った。","romaji":"Saigai de teiden shita toki no tame ni, kateiyō no hatsudenki o katta.","german":"I bought a home generator in case of a power outage during a disaster.","furigana":"さいがい|ていでん|かていよう|はつでんき|か"}],"notes":"発電 is \"power generation\"; as a する-verb the means takes で: 風力で発電する. Very common in compounds: 発電所 \"power plant\", 発電機 \"generator\", 火力発電, 水力発電, 原子力発電, 太陽光発電. Not to be confused with 充電 \"charging\" and 停電 \"power outage\"; 電力 denotes electricity as power or resource.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"太陽光パネルを屋根に設置した。","romaji":"Taiyōkō paneru o yane ni setchi shita.","german":"I installed solar panels on the roof.","furigana":"たいようこう|やね|せっち"},{"japanese":"太陽光発電のおかげで、電気代がかなり安くなった。","romaji":"Taiyōkō hatsuden no okage de, denkidai ga kanari yasuku natta.","german":"Thanks to solar power generation, the electricity bill has become much cheaper.","furigana":"たいようこうはつでん|でんきだい|やす"},{"japanese":"植物は太陽光を利用して栄養を作り出す。","romaji":"Shokubutsu wa taiyōkō o riyō shite eiyō o tsukuridasu.","german":"Plants use sunlight to produce nutrients.","furigana":"しょくぶつ|たいようこう|りよう|えいよう|つく|だ"}],"notes":"太陽光 is \"sunlight\" above all in the technical and energy-policy sense: 太陽光発電 \"photovoltaics\", 太陽光パネル \"solar module\", 太陽光エネルギー. For sunlight in everyday life, one rather says 日光 (直射日光を避ける), 日差し or speaks of the 日当たり of an apartment. In biology 太陽光 is usual in connection with photosynthesis.","levelNote":"Compound: 太陽 (N4) + 光 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"風力発電は環境に優しい。","romaji":"Fūryoku hatsuden wa kankyō ni yasashii.","german":"Wind power is environmentally friendly.","furigana":"ふうりょくはつでん|かんきょう|やさ"},{"japanese":"海の上に風力発電の風車がたくさん並んでいる。","romaji":"Umi no ue ni fūryoku hatsuden no fūsha ga takusan narande iru.","german":"Many wind turbines for wind power are lined up out at sea.","furigana":"うみ|うえ|ふうりょくはつでん|ふうしゃ|なら"},{"japanese":"風力と水力は、どちらも再生可能エネルギーだ。","romaji":"Fūryoku to suiryoku wa, dochira mo saisei kanō enerugī da.","german":"Wind power and hydropower are both renewable energy.","furigana":"ふうりょく|すいりょく|さいせいかのう"}],"notes":"風力 is \"wind power\" and appears mostly in compounds such as 風力発電 \"wind power generation\" or 風力発電所 \"wind farm\"; 水力, 火力 and 原子力 are formed analogously. In weather reports for shipping, 風力 also denotes the \"wind force\" on the Beaufort scale (風力3). Wind speed in meters per second, on the other hand, is called 風速.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"原子力発電の安全性が議論されている。","romaji":"Genshiryoku hatsuden no anzensei ga giron sarete iru.","german":"Die Sicherheit der Atomkraftwerke wird diskutiert.","furigana":"げんしりょくはつでん|あんぜんせい|ぎろん"},{"japanese":"事故の後、原子力に頼らないエネルギー政策を求める声が高まった。","romaji":"Jiko no ato, genshiryoku ni tayoranai enerugī seisaku o motomeru koe ga takamatta.","german":"Nach dem Unfall wurden die Rufe nach einer Energiepolitik ohne Atomkraft lauter.","furigana":"じこ|あと|げんしりょく|たよ|せいさく|もと|こえ|たか"},{"japanese":"ドイツは原子力から再生可能エネルギーへの転換を進めてきた。","romaji":"Doitsu wa genshiryoku kara saisei kanō enerugī e no tenkan o susumete kita.","german":"Deutschland hat den Umstieg von der Atomkraft auf erneuerbare Energien vorangetrieben.","furigana":"げんしりょく|さいせいかのう|てんかん|すす"}],"notes":"原子力 ist „Atomkraft, Kernenergie“, meist im Sinne der zivilen Nutzung: 原子力発電, 原子力発電所 – in Nachrichten fast immer kurz 原発 –, 原子力政策. Für Waffen verwendet man dagegen 核 „Kern“: 核兵器, 核実験. Typisch: 原子力に頼る, 原子力から脱却する.","levelNote":"Compound: 原子 (N1) + 力 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"産業廃棄物の処理が問題になっている。","romaji":"Sangyō haikibutsu no shori ga mondai ni natte iru.","german":"The disposal of industrial waste has become a problem.","furigana":"さんぎょうはいきぶつ|しょり|もんだい"},{"japanese":"放射性廃棄物をどこで保管するかは、大きな課題だ。","romaji":"Hōshasei haikibutsu o doko de hokan suru ka wa, ōki na kadai da.","german":"Where to store radioactive waste is a major challenge.","furigana":"ほうしゃせいはいきぶつ|ほかん|おお|かだい"},{"japanese":"工場は廃棄物を減らすため、製造工程を見直した。","romaji":"Kōjō wa haikibutsu o herasu tame, seizō kōtei o minaoshita.","german":"To reduce waste, the factory reviewed its manufacturing process.","furigana":"こうじょう|はいきぶつ|へ|せいぞうこうてい|みなお"}],"notes":"廃棄物 is the formal, official word for \"waste, waste materials\", especially from industry and technology: 産業廃棄物 (short 産廃), 放射性廃棄物, 廃棄物処理, 廃棄物を減らす. In daily life people say ごみ; the related verb is 廃棄する \"to dispose of, discard\".","levelNote":"Compound: 廃棄 (N1) + 物 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ペットボトルのリサイクル率が上がった。","romaji":"Pettobotoru no risaikuru ritsu ga agatta.","german":"The recycling rate of PET bottles has risen.","furigana":"りつ|あ"},{"japanese":"使わなくなった家具をリサイクルショップに売った。","romaji":"Tsukawanaku natta kagu o risaikuru shoppu ni utta.","german":"I sold furniture I no longer used to a secondhand shop.","furigana":"つか|かぐ|う"},{"japanese":"この会社は、回収したスマホから金属をリサイクルしている。","romaji":"Kono kaisha wa, kaishū shita sumaho kara kinzoku o risaikuru shite iru.","german":"This company recycles metals from collected smartphones.","furigana":"かいしゃ|かいしゅう|きんぞく"}],"notes":"リサイクル is \"recycling\"; as a する-verb it is transitive: 空き缶をリサイクルする. Typical: リサイクル率, リサイクルに出す. Note: a リサイクルショップ is not a recycling center but a secondhand store for used furniture, appliances and clothing. Related are 再利用 \"reuse\" and 分別 \"waste sorting\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本の製造業は世界的に競争力がある。","romaji":"Nihon no seizōgyō wa sekaiteki ni kyōsōryoku ga aru.","german":"Japan's manufacturing industry is globally competitive.","furigana":"にほん|せいぞうぎょう|せかいてき|きょうそうりょく"},{"japanese":"父は四十年間、製造業の現場で働いてきた。","romaji":"Chichi wa yonjū-nenkan, seizōgyō no genba de hataraite kita.","german":"My father worked on the manufacturing floor for forty years.","furigana":"ちち|よんじゅうねんかん|せいぞうぎょう|げんば|はたら"},{"japanese":"円安は輸出の多い製造業にとって追い風になる。","romaji":"En'yasu wa yushutsu no ōi seizōgyō ni totte oikaze ni naru.","german":"A weak yen is a tailwind for the export-heavy manufacturing industry.","furigana":"えんやす|ゆしゅつ|おお|せいぞうぎょう|お|かぜ"}],"notes":"製造業 is composed of 製造 \"manufacture\" and 業 \"industry\" and denotes the \"manufacturing industry\" as an economic sector - a word from business news and statistics. An individual manufacturing company is called メーカー; 工業 is \"industry\" in the technical sense, 産業 the most general term. Typical: 製造業で働く, 製造業の現場.","levelNote":"Compound: 製造 (N3) + 業 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"サービス業の需要が増えている。","romaji":"Sābisugyō no juyō ga fuete iru.","german":"Demand in the service industry is increasing.","furigana":"ぎょう|じゅよう|ふ"},{"japanese":"サービス業は土日に休めないことが多い。","romaji":"Sābisugyō wa donichi ni yasumenai koto ga ōi.","german":"In the service industry, you often can't take weekends off.","furigana":"ぎょう|どにち|やす|おお"},{"japanese":"大学を卒業してから、ずっとホテルなどのサービス業で働いている。","romaji":"Daigaku o sotsugyō shite kara, zutto hoteru nado no sābisugyō de hataraite iru.","german":"Since graduating from university, I've worked in the service industry, such as hotels, the whole time.","furigana":"だいがく|そつぎょう|ぎょう|はたら"}],"notes":"サービス業 is the \"service industry\": restaurants, hotels, retail, hairdressers, etc. In formal statistical terms it roughly corresponds to 第三次産業 \"tertiary sector\". Typical: サービス業で働く／に勤める, サービス業の人手不足. Caution: サービス alone often means \"free, on the house\" in Japanese (これはサービスです).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"運輸業界は人手不足に悩んでいる。","romaji":"Un'yu gyōkai wa hitodebusoku ni nayande iru.","german":"The transportation industry is suffering from a labor shortage.","furigana":"うんゆぎょうかい|ひとでぶそく|なや"},{"japanese":"兄は運輸会社に勤めていて、トラックの配送ルートを決めている。","romaji":"Ani wa un'yu gaisha ni tsutomete ite, torakku no haisō rūto o kimete iru.","german":"My older brother works at a transport company and decides truck delivery routes.","furigana":"あに|うんゆがいしゃ|つと|はいそう|き"},{"japanese":"政府は運輸の安全対策を強化すると発表した。","romaji":"Seifu wa un'yu no anzen taisaku o kyōka suru to happyō shita.","german":"The government announced it would strengthen transportation safety measures.","furigana":"せいふ|うんゆ|あんぜんたいさく|きょうか|はっぴょう"}],"notes":"運輸 refers to the \"transport sector\", i.e. the conveyance of people and goods as a branch of the economy; the word is formal and mostly appears in compounds: 運輸業, 運輸業界, 運輸会社. It is hardly used as a する-verb. For the concrete act of transporting, people use 輸送する or 運ぶ, for freight forwarders 運送 (運送会社); 交通 means traffic itself.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"物流の効率化が求められている。","romaji":"Butsuryū no kōritsuka ga motomerarete iru.","german":"Greater logistics efficiency is being demanded.","furigana":"ぶつりゅう|こうりつか|もと"},{"japanese":"台風で物流が止まり、スーパーの棚から商品が消えた。","romaji":"Taifū de butsuryū ga tomari, sūpā no tana kara shōhin ga kieta.","german":"The typhoon halted distribution, and goods disappeared from supermarket shelves.","furigana":"たいふう|ぶつりゅう|と|たな|しょうひん|き"},{"japanese":"ネット通販の拡大で、物流センターの数が増えている。","romaji":"Netto tsūhan no kakudai de, butsuryū sentā no kazu ga fuete iru.","german":"With the growth of online shopping, the number of distribution centers is increasing.","furigana":"つうはん|かくだい|ぶつりゅう|かず|ふ"}],"notes":"物流 is \"logistics\", i.e. the physical flow of goods from production to the customer: 物流センター, 物流業界, 物流コスト, 物流が止まる／滞る. To be distinguished from 流通 \"distribution\", which also includes wholesale and retail, as well as 配送 \"delivery\" and 運送 \"freight transport\". Typical for business news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"航空業界はコロナ後に回復した。","romaji":"Kōkū gyōkai wa korona go ni kaifuku shita.","german":"The aviation industry has recovered after COVID.","furigana":"こうくうぎょうかい|ご|かいふく"},{"japanese":"航空券は早めに予約したほうが安い。","romaji":"Kōkūken wa hayame ni yoyaku shita hō ga yasui.","german":"Plane tickets are cheaper if you book early.","furigana":"こうくうけん|はや|よやく|やす"},{"japanese":"この荷物は航空便で送ると三日で届きます。","romaji":"Kono nimotsu wa kōkūbin de okuru to mikka de todokimasu.","german":"If you send this package by air mail, it arrives in three days.","furigana":"にもつ|こうくうびん|おく|みっか|とど"}],"notes":"航空 means \"aviation\" and appears almost only in compounds: 航空会社 \"airline\", 航空券 \"plane ticket\", 航空便 \"air mail; flight\", 航空機 (formal for \"aircraft\"), 航空業界. The airplane in daily life is 飛行機, \"to fly\" is 飛ぶ. On its own, 航空 is rare.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本の鉄道は時間に正確だ。","romaji":"Nihon no tetsudō wa jikan ni seikaku da.","german":"Japan's railways are punctual.","furigana":"にほん|てつどう|じかん|せいかく"},{"japanese":"この町は鉄道が通ってから急に発展した。","romaji":"Kono machi wa tetsudō ga tōtte kara kyū ni hatten shita.","german":"This town developed rapidly after the railway came through.","furigana":"まち|てつどう|とお|きゅう|はってん"},{"japanese":"弟は鉄道が大好きで、休みの日は電車の写真を撮りに行く。","romaji":"Otōto wa tetsudō ga daisuki de, yasumi no hi wa densha no shashin o tori ni iku.","german":"My little brother loves trains and goes out to photograph trains on his days off.","furigana":"おとうと|てつどう|だいす|やす|ひ|でんしゃ|しゃしん|と|い"}],"notes":"鉄道 is the \"railway\" as a transport system: 鉄道会社, 鉄道網, 鉄道が通る \"a railway line runs through\", 私鉄 \"private railway\". The individual train is called 電車 (electric) or 列車 in daily life, the track 線路. Railway fans are called 鉄道ファン, jokingly 鉄ちゃん.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大型船舶で貨物を輸送する。","romaji":"Ōgata senpaku de kamotsu o yusō suru.","german":"Cargo is transported by large vessels.","furigana":"おおがたせんぱく|かもつ|ゆそう"},{"japanese":"台風が近づいているため、船舶の航行に注意が必要です。","romaji":"Taifū ga chikazuite iru tame, senpaku no kōkō ni chūi ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"A typhoon is approaching, so caution is needed when navigating ships.","furigana":"たいふう|ちか|せんぱく|こうこう|ちゅうい|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"この港には毎日多くの船舶が出入りしている。","romaji":"Kono minato ni wa mainichi ōku no senpaku ga deiri shite iru.","german":"Many ships enter and leave this port every day.","furigana":"みなと|まいにちおお|せんぱく|でい"}],"notes":"船舶 is the formal collective term for \"ships, watercraft\" and belongs in laws, news and technical texts: 船舶の航行, 船舶事故, 大型船舶. In daily life people simply say 船. The counter for larger ships is 隻, for small boats 艘.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"道路の補修工事が行われている。","romaji":"Dōro no hoshū kōji ga okonawarete iru.","german":"Road repair work is being carried out.","furigana":"どうろ|ほしゅうこうじ|おこな"},{"japanese":"雪で道路が凍っているので、運転に気をつけてください。","romaji":"Yuki de dōro ga kōtte iru node, unten ni ki o tsukete kudasai.","german":"The roads are frozen from the snow, so please drive carefully.","furigana":"ゆき|どうろ|こお|うんてん|き"},{"japanese":"急に道路に飛び出すと危ないよ。","romaji":"Kyū ni dōro ni tobidasu to abunai yo.","german":"It's dangerous to suddenly run out onto the road.","furigana":"きゅう|どうろ|と|だ|あぶ"}],"notes":"道路 is the \"road\" as a traffic route, rather matter-of-fact and official: 道路工事, 道路標識, 高速道路, 道路が混む／渋滞する, 道路を渡る. 道 is more general (\"path\", also figuratively), 通り is a (busy) street, often with a name. Roadway and sidewalk are called 車道 and 歩道.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"山を貫くトンネルが完成した。","romaji":"Yama o tsuranuku tonneru ga kansei shita.","german":"The tunnel through the mountain has been completed.","furigana":"やま|つらぬ|かんせい"},{"japanese":"トンネルに入ると、携帯の電波が届かなくなる。","romaji":"Tonneru ni hairu to, keitai no denpa ga todokanaku naru.","german":"When you enter the tunnel, your phone loses reception.","furigana":"はい|けいたい|でんぱ|とど"},{"japanese":"長い不況のトンネルを、ようやく抜け出した。","romaji":"Nagai fukyō no tonneru o, yōyaku nukedashita.","german":"We have finally emerged from the long tunnel of the recession.","furigana":"なが|ふきょう|ぬ|だ"}],"notes":"トンネル is the \"tunnel\": トンネルに入る, トンネルを抜ける \"to come out through the tunnel\", トンネルを掘る. Famous is the first sentence of Kawabata's \"Snow Country\": 国境の長いトンネルを抜けると雪国であった. Figuratively, 長いトンネルを抜ける stands for getting through a long, hard period. In baseball, トンネル(する) means letting a grounder go through one's legs.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この植物は日陰でもよく育ちます。","romaji":"Kono shokubutsu wa hikage demo yoku sodachimasu.","german":"This plant grows well even in the shade.","furigana":"しょくぶつ|ひかげ|そだ"},{"japanese":"週末に植物園へ行って、珍しい花を見た。","romaji":"Shūmatsu ni shokubutsuen e itte, mezurashii hana o mita.","german":"On the weekend, I went to the botanical garden and saw rare flowers.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|しょくぶつえん|い|めずら|はな|み"},{"japanese":"部屋に観葉植物を置くと、気分が落ち着く。","romaji":"Heya ni kan'yō shokubutsu o oku to, kibun ga ochitsuku.","german":"Having a houseplant in the room calms the mind.","furigana":"へや|かんようしょくぶつ|お|きぶん|お|つ"}],"notes":"植物 is \"plant\" as a general, rather factual-scientific term, the counterpart of 動物. Typical: 植物を育てる, 植物園 \"botanical garden\", 観葉植物 \"houseplant, foliage plant\", 植物性 \"plant-based\". In daily life one speaks more concretely of 花, 草 or 木; 草花 are flowering plants.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子供たちは動物園で動物を見るのが好きです。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa dōbutsuen de dōbutsu o miru no ga suki desu.","german":"The children like looking at the animals at the zoo.","furigana":"こども|どうぶつえん|どうぶつ|み|す"},{"japanese":"毎朝、動物の世話をしています。","romaji":"Maiasa, dōbutsu no sewa o shite imasu.","german":"I take care of the animals every morning.","furigana":"まいあさ|どうぶつ|せわ"}],"notes":"動物 is an animal; the number is clear from the context. 動物を見る names animals as the thing being looked at. 動物の世話をする means to take care of animals. 動物園 is the zoo, as a compound word. どうぶつ contains a long o.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 18"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"夏になると昆虫がたくさん現れます。","romaji":"Natsu ni naru to konchū ga takusan arawaremasu.","german":"Many insects appear when summer comes.","furigana":"なつ|こんちゅう|あらわ"},{"japanese":"子どものころ、夏休みは毎日昆虫採集をしていた。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, natsuyasumi wa mainichi konchū saishū o shite ita.","german":"As a child, I went insect collecting every day of the summer vacation.","furigana":"こ|なつやす|まいにちこんちゅうさいしゅう"},{"japanese":"クモは足が八本あるので、昆虫ではない。","romaji":"Kumo wa ashi ga happon aru node, konchū de wa nai.","german":"A spider has eight legs, so it is not an insect.","furigana":"あし|はっぽん|こんちゅう"}],"notes":"昆虫 is an \"insect\" in the biological sense (six legs). In daily life people mostly say 虫, which also includes spiders, worms and other creepy-crawlies. Typical: 昆虫採集 \"insect collecting\" (a classic summer vacation hobby), 昆虫図鑑 \"insect encyclopedia\", 昆虫食 \"insects as food\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"微生物は肉眼では見えません。","romaji":"Biseibutsu wa nikugan de wa miemasen.","german":"Microorganisms are not visible to the naked eye.","furigana":"びせいぶつ|にくがん|み"},{"japanese":"納豆やヨーグルトは、微生物の働きによって作られる。","romaji":"Nattō ya yōguruto wa, biseibutsu no hataraki ni yotte tsukurareru.","german":"Natto and yogurt are made through the action of microorganisms.","furigana":"なっとう|びせいぶつ|はたら|つく"},{"japanese":"土の中の微生物が落ち葉を分解する。","romaji":"Tsuchi no naka no biseibutsu ga ochiba o bunkai suru.","german":"Microorganisms in the soil break down fallen leaves.","furigana":"つち|なか|びせいぶつ|お|ば|ぶんかい"}],"notes":"微生物 is composed of 微 \"tiny\" and 生物 \"living thing\" and is the umbrella term for \"microorganisms\". More concrete are 細菌 \"bacteria\", 菌 \"germ, fungus\" and カビ \"mold\". Typical: 微生物の働き, 微生物が分解する, 微生物を利用する - especially in fermentation and environmental topics.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"熱帯雨林には多くの種が生息しています。","romaji":"Nettai urin ni wa ōku no shu ga seisoku shite imasu.","german":"Many species live in the tropical rainforest.","furigana":"ねったいうりん|おお|しゅ|せいそく"},{"japanese":"毎年、広い面積の熱帯雨林が失われている。","romaji":"Maitoshi, hiroi menseki no nettai urin ga ushinawarete iru.","german":"Every year, large areas of tropical rainforest are lost.","furigana":"まいとし|ひろ|めんせき|ねったいうりん|うしな"},{"japanese":"熱帯雨林は一年中暑く、雨がたくさん降る。","romaji":"Nettai urin wa ichinenjū atsuku, ame ga takusan furu.","german":"The tropical rainforest is hot all year round, and it rains a lot.","furigana":"ねったいうりん|いちねんじゅうあつ|あめ|ふ"}],"notes":"熱帯雨林 consists of 熱帯 \"tropics\" and 雨林 \"rainforest\". The word is factual and appears in news, geography and environmental topics: アマゾンの熱帯雨林, 熱帯雨林の破壊, 熱帯雨林が失われる. ジャングル is the more casual, more adventurous word; 森林 is the general term for forest.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"広い草原を馬が走っています。","romaji":"Hiroi sōgen o uma ga hashitte imasu.","german":"Horses are running across the wide grassland.","furigana":"ひろ|そうげん|うま|はし"},{"japanese":"モンゴルの草原で一晩過ごし、星空を眺めた。","romaji":"Mongoru no sōgen de hitoban sugoshi, hoshizora o nagameta.","german":"I spent a night on the Mongolian grassland and gazed at the starry sky.","furigana":"そうげん|ひとばんす|ほしぞら|なが"},{"japanese":"草原に寝転んで、青い空を見上げた。","romaji":"Sōgen ni nekoronde, aoi sora o miageta.","german":"I lay down on the grassy plain and looked up at the blue sky.","furigana":"そうげん|ねころ|あお|そら|みあ"}],"notes":"草原 is a wide area covered with grass - depending on context \"meadow, grassland\" or \"steppe, savanna\" (モンゴルの草原, アフリカの草原). 野原 is a simple, often smaller meadow or field in everyday life, 牧場 a pasture with livestock, 芝生 a lawn. Typical: 草原が広がる, 草原を走る.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"海洋汚染は深刻な問題です。","romaji":"Kaiyō osen wa shinkoku na mondai desu.","german":"Ocean pollution is a serious problem.","furigana":"かいようおせん|しんこく|もんだい"},{"japanese":"海洋生物の研究をするために、大学院に進んだ。","romaji":"Kaiyō seibutsu no kenkyū o suru tame ni, daigakuin ni susunda.","german":"I went on to graduate school to study marine life.","furigana":"かいようせいぶつ|けんきゅう|だいがくいん|すす"},{"japanese":"海洋資源を守るため、国際的なルールが必要だ。","romaji":"Kaiyō shigen o mamoru tame, kokusaiteki na rūru ga hitsuyō da.","german":"International rules are needed to protect marine resources.","furigana":"かいようしげん|まも|こくさいてき|ひつよう"}],"notes":"海洋 is the formal, scientific word for \"sea, ocean\" and appears almost always in compounds: 海洋汚染 \"ocean pollution\", 海洋生物, 海洋資源, 海洋性気候. In everyday speech one says 海; the great world oceans are also called 大洋 or 〜洋 (太平洋, 大西洋).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地球には七つの大陸があります。","romaji":"Chikyū ni wa nanatsu no tairiku ga arimasu.","german":"There are seven continents on Earth.","furigana":"ちきゅう|なな|たいりく"},{"japanese":"アメリカ大陸を車で横断するのが夢だ。","romaji":"Amerika tairiku o kuruma de ōdan suru no ga yume da.","german":"My dream is to cross the American continent by car.","furigana":"たいりく|くるま|おうだん|ゆめ"},{"japanese":"大昔、すべての大陸は一つにつながっていたと言われている。","romaji":"Ōmukashi, subete no tairiku wa hitotsu ni tsunagatte ita to iwarete iru.","german":"It is said that, in ancient times, all the continents were connected as one.","furigana":"おおむかし|たいりく|ひと|い"}],"notes":"大陸 is the \"continent\": アジア大陸, アメリカ大陸, 大陸を横断する. From a Japanese point of view, 大陸 on its own can also mean the Chinese mainland, from a British point of view continental Europe. The adjective 大陸的 describes something vast or a generous, easygoing manner. The opposite term is 島 \"island\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"イタリアは地中海に突き出た半島です。","romaji":"Itaria wa Chichūkai ni tsukideta hantō desu.","german":"Italy is a peninsula that juts into the Mediterranean.","furigana":"ちちゅうかい|つ|で|はんとう"},{"japanese":"週末に伊豆半島をドライブした。","romaji":"Shūmatsu ni Izu hantō o doraibu shita.","german":"On the weekend, I took a drive around the Izu Peninsula.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|いずはんとう"},{"japanese":"この半島は三方を海に囲まれていて、魚がおいしい。","romaji":"Kono hantō wa sanpō o umi ni kakomarete ite, sakana ga oishii.","german":"This peninsula is surrounded by sea on three sides, and the fish is delicious.","furigana":"はんとう|さんぽう|うみ|かこ|さかな"}],"notes":"半島 is the \"peninsula\", literally \"half island\"; as a suffix with proper names: 伊豆半島, 能登半島, 朝鮮半島. Typical: 海に突き出た半島, 三方を海に囲まれた半島. 半島 alone as a designation for Korea is colloquial and can come across as derogatory - better avoided. A headland is called 岬.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"アルプス山脈はヨーロッパを横断しています。","romaji":"Arupusu sanmyaku wa Yōroppa o ōdan shite imasu.","german":"The Alps mountain range crosses Europe.","furigana":"さんみゃく|おうだん"},{"japanese":"飛行機の窓から、雪をかぶった山脈が見えた。","romaji":"Hikōki no mado kara, yuki o kabutta sanmyaku ga mieta.","german":"From the airplane window, I could see a snow-capped mountain range.","furigana":"ひこうき|まど|ゆき|さんみゃく|み"},{"japanese":"日本列島の中央には、高い山脈が南北に走っている。","romaji":"Nihon rettō no chūō ni wa, takai sanmyaku ga nanboku ni hashitte iru.","german":"In the middle of the Japanese archipelago, high mountain ranges run north to south.","furigana":"にほんれっとう|ちゅうおう|たか|さんみゃく|なんぼく|はし"}],"notes":"山脈 is a \"mountain range\"; in names it serves as a suffix: ヒマラヤ山脈, アルプス山脈, 飛騨山脈. Typical: 山脈が連なる, 山脈が南北に走る \"runs north to south\". In contrast to 山々 \"the mountains\" or 山地 \"mountainous area\", 山脈 emphasizes the continuous chain. The Japanese high mountains are also called 日本アルプス.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"関東平野は日本最大の平野です。","romaji":"Kantō heiya wa Nihon saidai no heiya desu.","german":"The Kanto Plain is the largest plain in Japan.","furigana":"かんとうへいや|にほんさいだい|へいや"},{"japanese":"平野部では米作りが盛んだ。","romaji":"Heiyabu de wa komezukuri ga sakan da.","german":"Rice farming is thriving in the plains.","furigana":"へいやぶ|こめづく|さか"},{"japanese":"この平野は川が運んだ土でできている。","romaji":"Kono heiya wa kawa ga hakonda tsuchi de dekite iru.","german":"This plain was formed from soil carried by rivers.","furigana":"へいや|かわ|はこ|つち"}],"notes":"平野 is a \"plain, lowland\", usually shaped by rivers; as a suffix with names: 関東平野, 濃尾平野. 平野部 denotes the flat parts of a country, as opposed to 山間部. To be distinguished are 盆地 \"basin\" (enclosed by mountains), 高原 \"plateau\" and 平地 \"flat land\" in general.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"京都は盆地にあるため夏は蒸し暑いです。","romaji":"Kyōto wa bonchi ni aru tame natsu wa mushiatsui desu.","german":"Because Kyoto lies in a basin, summers are hot and humid.","furigana":"きょうと|ぼんち|なつ|む|あつ"},{"japanese":"甲府盆地はぶどうや桃の産地として有名だ。","romaji":"Kōfu bonchi wa budō ya momo no sanchi to shite yūmei da.","german":"The Kofu Basin is famous as a production area for grapes and peaches.","furigana":"こうふぼんち|もも|さんち|ゆうめい"},{"japanese":"盆地なので、冬の朝はよく霧が出る。","romaji":"Bonchi na node, fuyu no asa wa yoku kiri ga deru.","german":"Since it's a basin, fog often forms on winter mornings.","furigana":"ぼんち|ふゆ|あさ|きり|で"}],"notes":"盆地 is a \"basin\" enclosed by mountains - 盆 means \"tray\", so the image is a flat bowl. Well-known examples: 京都盆地, 奈良盆地, 甲府盆地. Typical is the 盆地特有の気候: hot, humid summers, cold winters and frequent fog. In contrast, a 平野 is an open plain, usually toward the sea.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"河川の水質を定期的に検査しています。","romaji":"Kasen no suishitsu o teikiteki ni kensa shite imasu.","german":"We test the water quality of rivers regularly.","furigana":"かせん|すいしつ|ていきてき|けんさ"},{"japanese":"大雨で河川が氾濫するおそれがあります。","romaji":"Ōame de kasen ga hanran suru osore ga arimasu.","german":"There is a risk that rivers will overflow due to heavy rain.","furigana":"おおあめ|かせん|はんらん"},{"japanese":"週末は河川敷でバーベキューをする人が多い。","romaji":"Shūmatsu wa kasenjiki de bābekyū o suru hito ga ōi.","german":"On weekends, many people have barbecues on the riverbank.","furigana":"しゅうまつ||ひと|おお"}],"notes":"河川 is the formal, official collective term for \"rivers, running waters\" and appears in official language, news and warnings: 河川の氾濫, 河川の水位, 一級河川. In daily life people simply say 川. A well-known compound is 河川敷, the riverside grounds between the river and the levee, where people exercise or barbecue.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"山の中に冷たい泉が湧いています。","romaji":"Yama no naka ni tsumetai izumi ga waite imasu.","german":"A cold spring wells up in the mountains.","furigana":"やま|なか|つめ|いずみ|わ"},{"japanese":"旅人は森の泉で水を飲み、少し休んだ。","romaji":"Tabibito wa mori no izumi de mizu o nomi, sukoshi yasunda.","german":"The traveler drank at a spring in the forest and rested a little.","furigana":"たびびと|もり|いずみ|みず|の|すこ|やす"},{"japanese":"祖母の話は、まるで知恵の泉のようだ。","romaji":"Sobo no hanashi wa, marude chie no izumi no yō da.","german":"My grandmother's stories are like a fountain of wisdom.","furigana":"そぼ|はなし|ちえ|いずみ"}],"notes":"泉 is a natural \"spring\" from which water emerges: 泉が湧く \"a spring wells up\". The character is also found in 温泉 \"hot spring\". Figuratively, 泉 stands for an inexhaustible source: 知恵の泉, アイデアの泉. An artificial fountain is called 噴水, a draw well 井戸; 泉 is also a common name.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"温暖化の影響で氷河が溶けています。","romaji":"Ondanka no eikyō de hyōga ga tokete imasu.","german":"Glaciers are melting because of global warming.","furigana":"おんだんか|えいきょう|ひょうが|と"},{"japanese":"スイスで氷河を見るツアーに参加した。","romaji":"Suisu de hyōga o miru tsuā ni sanka shita.","german":"In Switzerland, I took part in a tour to see a glacier.","furigana":"ひょうが|み|さんか"},{"japanese":"この谷は、氷河期に氷河が削ってできたものだ。","romaji":"Kono tani wa, hyōgaki ni hyōga ga kezutte dekita mono da.","german":"This valley was carved by a glacier during the ice age.","furigana":"たに|ひょうがき|ひょうが|けず"}],"notes":"氷河 is the \"glacier\": 氷河が溶ける, 氷河が後退する \"retreats\", 氷河期 \"ice age\", 氷河湖 \"glacial lake\". Not to be confused with 氷山 \"iceberg\" (氷山の一角 \"the tip of the iceberg\") and 流氷 \"drift ice\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本の気候は四季がはっきりしています。","romaji":"Nihon no kikō wa shiki ga hakkiri shite imasu.","german":"Japan's climate has four distinct seasons.","furigana":"にほん|きこう|しき"},{"japanese":"気候変動の影響で、各地で大雨が増えている。","romaji":"Kikō hendō no eikyō de, kakuchi de ōame ga fuete iru.","german":"Because of climate change, heavy rain is becoming more frequent in many places.","furigana":"きこうへんどう|えいきょう|かくち|おおあめ|ふ"},{"japanese":"沖縄の暖かい気候が体に合っているようで、引っ越してから元気になった。","romaji":"Okinawa no atatakai kikō ga karada ni atte iru yō de, hikkoshite kara genki ni natta.","german":"The warm climate of Okinawa seems to suit me; I've felt better since I moved.","furigana":"おきなわ|あたた|きこう|からだ|あ|ひ|こ|げんき"}],"notes":"気候 is \"climate,\" i.e. the long-term weather conditions of a region; the current weather is 天気, or more formally 天候. Typical: 温暖な気候, 気候変動 \"climate change,\" 気候が穏やか. 気候が合う／合わない means that the climate agrees or disagrees with you.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本は高度成長期に深刻な公害を経験しました。","romaji":"Nihon wa kōdo seichōki ni shinkoku na kōgai o keiken shimashita.","german":"Japan experienced severe pollution during its period of high growth.","furigana":"にほん|こうどせいちょうき|しんこく|こうがい|けいけん"},{"japanese":"水俣病は、工場の排水が原因で起きた公害病だ。","romaji":"Minamata-byō wa, kōjō no haisui ga gen'in de okita kōgaibyō da.","german":"Minamata disease is a pollution-caused disease that arose from factory wastewater.","furigana":"|こうじょう|はいすい|げんいん|お|こうがいびょう"},{"japanese":"空港の近くに住む人々は、騒音公害に悩まされている。","romaji":"Kūkō no chikaku ni sumu hitobito wa, sōon kōgai ni nayamasarete iru.","german":"People living near the airport suffer from noise pollution.","furigana":"くうこう|ちか|す|ひとびと|そうおんこうがい|なや"}],"notes":"公害 denotes environmental damage caused by industry or traffic that harms the health of the population - air and water pollution, but also noise (騒音公害). Historically formative are the 四大公害病 of the growth years, such as 水俣病. The neutral, broader term for pollution is 汚染 or 環境汚染; private littering is not called 公害.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自動車の排気ガスが大気汚染の原因です。","romaji":"Jidōsha no haiki gasu ga taiki osen no gen'in desu.","german":"Car exhaust fumes are the cause of air pollution.","furigana":"じどうしゃ|はいき|たいきおせん|げんいん"},{"japanese":"この車は排気量が大きいので、燃費が悪い。","romaji":"Kono kuruma wa haikiryō ga ōkii node, nenpi ga warui.","german":"This car has a large engine displacement, so its fuel economy is poor.","furigana":"くるま|はいきりょう|おお|ねんぴ|わる"},{"japanese":"パソコンの排気口をふさぐと、熱がこもって故障の原因になる。","romaji":"Pasokon no haikikō o fusagu to, netsu ga komotte koshō no gen'in ni naru.","german":"If you block a computer's exhaust vent, heat builds up and can cause a malfunction.","furigana":"はいきこう|ねつ|こしょう|げんいん"}],"notes":"排気 is the \"discharge\" of gas or air; the exhaust gas itself is called 排気ガス, abbreviated 排ガス. Other compounds: 排気口 \"exhaust/ventilation opening\" and 排気量 \"engine displacement\". To be distinguished are 換気 \"ventilating\" (letting in fresh air) and 排出 \"discharge\" in general, e.g. CO2の排出. As a する-verb, 排気する is rare.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この化粧品は天然の成分でできています。","romaji":"Kono keshōhin wa tennen no seibun de dekite imasu.","german":"This cosmetic product is made from natural ingredients.","furigana":"けしょうひん|てんねん|せいぶん"},{"japanese":"このマグロは養殖ではなく天然です。","romaji":"Kono maguro wa yōshoku de wa naku tennen desu.","german":"This tuna is wild, not farmed.","furigana":"ようしょく|てんねん"},{"japanese":"彼女はちょっと天然で、いつも面白いことを言う。","romaji":"Kanojo wa chotto tennen de, itsumo omoshiroi koto o iu.","german":"She's a bit of an airhead and is always saying funny things.","furigana":"かのじょ|てんねん|おもしろ|い"}],"notes":"天然 means \"natural, formed by nature,\" as opposed to 人工 \"artificial\" or 養殖 \"farmed.\" It is used with の or as a prefix: 天然の成分, 天然ガス, 天然資源, 天然水, 天然記念物. 自然, by contrast, means nature as scenery, or \"natural\" in the sense of unforced. In colloquial speech, a lovably naive person who is unintentionally funny is called 天然 or 天然ボケ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"石油は限りある資源です。","romaji":"Sekiyu wa kagiri aru shigen desu.","german":"Petroleum is a limited resource.","furigana":"せきゆ|かぎ|しげん"},{"japanese":"冬は石油ストーブで部屋を暖めている。","romaji":"Fuyu wa sekiyu sutōbu de heya o atatamete iru.","german":"In winter I heat the room with a kerosene heater.","furigana":"ふゆ|せきゆ|へや|あたた"},{"japanese":"日本は石油のほとんどを中東から輸入している。","romaji":"Nihon wa sekiyu no hotondo o Chūtō kara yunyū shite iru.","german":"Japan imports most of its petroleum from the Middle East.","furigana":"にほん|せきゆ|ちゅうとう|ゆにゅう"}],"notes":"石油 is \"petroleum\" as a raw material: 石油製品, 石油会社, 石油危機 \"oil crisis\" (also オイルショック). In everyday life 石油 also means the heating kerosene for the 石油ストーブ, correctly 灯油. To be distinguished are 油 \"oil\" in general (also cooking oil), オイル and ガソリン \"gasoline\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 37"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"石炭の使用を減らすべきだと言われています。","romaji":"Sekitan no shiyō o herasu beki da to iwarete imasu.","german":"It is said that the use of coal should be reduced.","furigana":"せきたん|しよう|へ|い"},{"japanese":"昔はこの町で石炭がたくさん掘られていました。","romaji":"Mukashi wa kono machi de sekitan ga takusan horarete imashita.","german":"In the past, a lot of coal was mined in this town.","furigana":"むかし|まち|せきたん|ほ"},{"japanese":"冬になると、祖父は石炭ストーブで部屋を暖めていた。","romaji":"Fuyu ni naru to, sofu wa sekitan sutōbu de heya o atatamete ita.","german":"When winter came, my grandfather would heat the room with a coal stove.","furigana":"ふゆ|そふ|せきたん|へや|あたた"}],"notes":"石炭 is coal as a mineral resource and fuel. Typical combinations: 石炭を掘る / 採掘する (\"to mine coal\"), 石炭を燃やす, 石炭火力発電 (\"coal-fired power generation\"); together with 石油 and 天然ガス it is one of the 化石燃料 (\"fossil fuels\"). Charcoal for grilling, on the other hand, is 炭 or 木炭, not 石炭.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"天然ガスは石油より環境への影響が少ないです。","romaji":"Tennen gasu wa sekiyu yori kankyō e no eikyō ga sukunai desu.","german":"Natural gas has less impact on the environment than oil.","furigana":"てんねん|せきゆ|かんきょう|えいきょう|すく"},{"japanese":"この国は天然ガスの多くを輸入に頼っている。","romaji":"Kono kuni wa tennen gasu no ōku o yunyū ni tayotte iru.","german":"This country relies on imports for most of its natural gas.","furigana":"くに|てんねん|おお|ゆにゅう|たよ"},{"japanese":"この発電所は天然ガスを燃料にしています。","romaji":"Kono hatsudensho wa tennen gasu o nenryō ni shite imasu.","german":"This power plant uses natural gas as fuel.","furigana":"はつでんしょ|てんねん|ねんりょう"}],"notes":"天然ガス is \"natural gas\" (天然 \"natural\" + ガス) and is encountered mainly in news and texts about energy: 天然ガスを輸入する, 液化天然ガス (\"liquefied natural gas, LNG\"). In everyday speech, for the gas from the pipe one simply says ガス (ガス代, ガスコンロ); 都市ガス is city gas from the network, プロパンガス is bottled gas.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"エコバッグを持ち歩くようにしています。","romaji":"Eko baggu o mochiaruku yō ni shite imasu.","german":"I make a point of carrying a reusable bag with me.","furigana":"も|ある"},{"japanese":"エコのために、エアコンの温度を少し高めに設定しています。","romaji":"Eko no tame ni, eakon no ondo o sukoshi takame ni settei shite imasu.","german":"For the sake of the environment, I set the air conditioner's temperature a little higher.","furigana":"おんど|すこ|たか|せってい"},{"japanese":"この車はエコだけど、値段が高すぎる。","romaji":"Kono kuruma wa eko da kedo, nedan ga takasugiru.","german":"This car is eco-friendly, but the price is too high.","furigana":"くるま|ねだん|たか"}],"notes":"エコ is the short form of エコロジー and mostly appears as a prefix in compounds: エコバッグ (\"reusable shopping bag\"), エコカー, エコ活動. On its own it is used like a な-adjective: エコな生活, この車はエコだ. Often \"economical\" also resonates (エコモード). The word is casual and advertising language; in formal texts one rather says 環境に優しい or 環境に配慮した.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自然環境の保護が求められています。","romaji":"Shizen kankyō no hogo ga motomerarete imasu.","german":"Protection of the natural environment is called for.","furigana":"しぜんかんきょう|ほご|もと"},{"japanese":"迷子の子供が警察に保護されました。","romaji":"Maigo no kodomo ga keisatsu ni hogo saremashita.","german":"The lost child was taken into protective custody by the police.","furigana":"まいご|こども|けいさつ|ほご"},{"japanese":"画面を保護するフィルムを貼りました。","romaji":"Gamen o hogo suru firumu o harimashita.","german":"I put a film on to protect the screen.","furigana":"がめん|ほご|は"}],"notes":"保護 means \"protection, preservation\" and is frequent as a transitive する-verb: 〜を保護する. Typical are 自然保護, 環境保護, 個人情報の保護 and, in the passive, 警察に保護される (\"to be taken into care\"). Compared with the everyday 守る, 保護 is more formal and stresses care for something weak or endangered; 防ぐ, in contrast, means \"to prevent, ward off\". 生活保護 is welfare.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この地域には希少な種が生息しています。","romaji":"Kono chiiki ni wa kishō na shu ga seisoku shite imasu.","german":"Rare species live in this region.","furigana":"ちいき|きしょう|しゅ|せいそく"},{"japanese":"この種の問題は、以前にも起きたことがある。","romaji":"Kono shu no mondai wa, izen ni mo okita koto ga aru.","german":"This kind of problem has happened before.","furigana":"しゅ|もんだい|いぜん|お"},{"japanese":"人間も動物の一つの種にすぎない。","romaji":"Ningen mo dōbutsu no hitotsu no shu ni suginai.","german":"Humans, too, are merely one species of animal.","furigana":"にんげん|どうぶつ|ひと|しゅ"}],"notes":"種 read しゅ is the biological \"species\" and, in a more formal register, generally \"kind, sort\": この種の問題 \"problems of this kind\". As a suffix it is found in 絶滅危惧種, 外来種 or 品種. Note: the same kanji read たね means \"seed, pit\" or \"cause\" (悩みの種). In daily life, for \"sort\" people more often say 種類 or タイプ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この種の個体数は年々減少しています。","romaji":"Kono shu no kotaisū wa nennen genshō shite imasu.","german":"The number of individuals of this species is declining year by year.","furigana":"しゅ|こたいすう|ねんねんげんしょう"},{"japanese":"同じ種でも、個体によって大きさがかなり違う。","romaji":"Onaji shu demo, kotai ni yotte ōkisa ga kanari chigau.","german":"Even within the same species, size differs considerably from individual to individual.","furigana":"おな|しゅ|こたい|おお|ちが"},{"japanese":"研究者は捕まえた個体に番号をつけて放した。","romaji":"Kenkyūsha wa tsukamaeta kotai ni bangō o tsukete hanashita.","german":"The researchers numbered the captured individuals and released them.","furigana":"けんきゅうしゃ|つか|こたい|ばんごう|はな"}],"notes":"個体 is a technical word from biology for a single living thing (\"individual, specimen\"), mostly for animals and plants. Common are 個体数 (\"population size\") and 個体差 (\"individual differences\", also for pets or products). For humans in everyday speech one says 個人. Not to be confused with the identically sounding 固体 (\"solid body\", cf. 固体・液体・気体).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"羊の群れが草原を移動しています。","romaji":"Hitsuji no mure ga sōgen o idō shite imasu.","german":"Eine Herde Schafe bewegt sich über das Grasland.","furigana":"ひつじ|む|そうげん|いどう"},{"japanese":"空を鳥の群れが飛んでいった。","romaji":"Sora o tori no mure ga tonde itta.","german":"Ein Vogelschwarm flog über den Himmel.","furigana":"そら|とり|む|と"},{"japanese":"オオカミは群れで狩りをする。","romaji":"Ōkami wa mure de kari o suru.","german":"Wölfe jagen im Rudel.","furigana":"む|か"}],"notes":"群れ ist das Substantiv zu 群れる („sich scharen“) und deckt alle deutschen Gruppenwörter für Tiere ab: 羊の群れ („Herde“), 鳥の群れ („Schwarm“), オオカミの群れ („Rudel“), 魚の群れ. 群れで〜する heißt „im Rudel / in Gruppen …“. Auf Menschen bezogen klingt 群れ eher abwertend oder literarisch (人の群れ „Menschenmenge“); neutral sagt man 集団 oder グループ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"魚の鱗がキラキラ光っています。","romaji":"Sakana no uroko ga kirakira hikatte imasu.","german":"Die Schuppen des Fisches glitzern.","furigana":"さかな|うろこ|ひか"},{"japanese":"魚を焼く前に、包丁でうろこを取ります。","romaji":"Sakana o yaku mae ni, hōchō de uroko o torimasu.","german":"Bevor ich den Fisch brate, entferne ich mit dem Messer die Schuppen.","furigana":"さかな|や|まえ|ほうちょう|と"},{"japanese":"先生の説明を聞いて、目から鱗が落ちる思いだった。","romaji":"Sensei no setsumei o kiite, me kara uroko ga ochiru omoi datta.","german":"Als ich die Erklärung des Lehrers hörte, fiel es mir wie Schuppen von den Augen.","furigana":"せんせい|せつめい|き|め|うろこ|お|おも"}],"notes":"鱗 ist die Schuppe von Fischen, Schlangen und Reptilien; da das Kanji schwer ist, schreibt man meist うろこ. Beim Kochen heißt es うろこを取る / 落とす („entschuppen“); うろこ雲 sind „Schäfchenwolken“. Die Redewendung 目から鱗が落ちる entspricht genau dem deutschen „es fällt einem wie Schuppen von den Augen“. Kopfschuppen sind dagegen ふけ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"卵の殻を割って中身を出しました。","romaji":"Tamago no kara o watte nakami o dashimashita.","german":"I cracked the eggshell and took out the contents.","furigana":"たまご|から|わ|なかみ|だ"},{"japanese":"海岸で貝殻を拾いました。","romaji":"Kaigan de kaigara o hiroimashita.","german":"I picked up seashells on the beach.","furigana":"かいがん|かいがら|ひろ"},{"japanese":"彼はなかなか自分の殻を破れずにいる。","romaji":"Kare wa nakanaka jibun no kara o yaburezu ni iru.","german":"He just can't seem to break out of his shell.","furigana":"かれ|じぶん|から|やぶ"}],"notes":"殻 is a hard outer covering: 卵の殻, ピーナッツの殻, カニの殻; in 貝殻 (\"seashell\") it is read voiced as がら. Soft peels of fruit or skin, on the other hand, are called 皮 (みかんの皮). Figuratively: 自分の殻に閉じこもる (\"to retreat into one's shell\"), 殻を破る (\"to come out of one's shell, break one's limits\"), もぬけの殻 (\"empty, deserted\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"動物の毛皮を使った製品に反対する人が増えています。","romaji":"Dōbutsu no kegawa o tsukatta seihin ni hantai suru hito ga fuete imasu.","german":"More and more people oppose products made with animal fur.","furigana":"どうぶつ|けがわ|つか|せいひん|はんたい|ひと|ふ"},{"japanese":"祖母は古い毛皮のコートを大切にしている。","romaji":"Sobo wa furui kegawa no kōto o taisetsu ni shite iru.","german":"My grandmother treasures her old fur coat.","furigana":"そぼ|ふる|けがわ|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"ホッキョクグマは厚い毛皮で寒さから身を守っている。","romaji":"Hokkyokuguma wa atsui kegawa de samusa kara mi o mamotte iru.","german":"Polar bears protect themselves from the cold with their thick fur.","furigana":"あつ|けがわ|さむ|み|まも"}],"notes":"毛皮 is fur or pelt with the hair, both as a material (毛皮のコート, 毛皮製品) and for the living animal in factual and documentary texts. To be distinguished: 革 (\"leather\", dehaired and tanned: 革の靴), 皮 (\"skin\" in general) and 毛 (\"hair\"). For the coat of pets, in everyday speech one rather says 毛 or 毛並み.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"健康のために毎日運動しています。","romaji":"Kenkō no tame ni mainichi undō shite imasu.","german":"I exercise every day for my health.","furigana":"けんこう|まいにちうんどう"},{"japanese":"祖父は九十歳ですが、とても健康です。","romaji":"Sofu wa kyūjussai desu ga, totemo kenkō desu.","german":"My grandfather is ninety, but he is very healthy.","furigana":"そふ|きゅうじゅっさい|けんこう"},{"japanese":"会社で毎年健康診断があります。","romaji":"Kaisha de maitoshi kenkō shindan ga arimasu.","german":"There is a health checkup at the company every year.","furigana":"かいしゃ|まいとしけんこうしんだん"}],"notes":"健康 is a noun and a な-adjective: 健康な人, 祖父は健康だ. Common combinations: 健康に気をつける, 健康を害する (\"to harm one's health\"), 健康に良い / 悪い, 健康診断 (\"health checkup\"). For \"healthy\" (food, lifestyle) one uses 健康的な. 元気 means more \"lively, fit, in good spirits\", 丈夫 \"sturdy, robust\"; 健康 describes the physical condition.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 32"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"医者に薬を処方してもらいました。","romaji":"Isha ni kusuri o shohō shite moraimashita.","german":"The doctor prescribed me medicine.","furigana":"いしゃ|くすり|しょほう"},{"japanese":"妹は医者になりたいと言っています。","romaji":"Imōto wa isha ni naritai to itte imasu.","german":"My younger sister says she wants to become a doctor.","furigana":"いもうと|いしゃ|い"}],"notes":"医者 means a doctor, male or female. Politely, people often say お医者さん. 医者に…してもらう names the person from whom one receives an action. 医者になりたい expresses a career wish. In いしゃ, しゃ forms a single mora; there is no long vowel.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"医師の指示に従って薬を飲んでください。","romaji":"Ishi no shiji ni shitagatte kusuri o nonde kudasai.","german":"Please take the medicine according to your doctor's instructions.","furigana":"いし|しじ|したが|くすり|の"},{"japanese":"彼は医師として地方の病院で働いている。","romaji":"Kare wa ishi to shite chihō no byōin de hataraite iru.","german":"He works as a doctor at a regional hospital.","furigana":"かれ|いし|ちほう|びょういん|はたら"},{"japanese":"医師の診断書を会社に提出しました。","romaji":"Ishi no shindansho o kaisha ni teishutsu shimashita.","german":"I submitted the doctor's medical certificate to my company.","furigana":"いし|しんだんしょ|かいしゃ|ていしゅつ"}],"notes":"医師 is the official, formal word for \"doctor\": used in job descriptions, documents and the news (医師免許, 医師の診断書). In everyday speech people say 医者, politely お医者さん, and a doctor is addressed as 先生 - 医師 is never a form of address. Homophones such as 意思 / 意志 (\"will, intention\") are distinguished only by context.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"薬剤師に薬の飲み方を聞きました。","romaji":"Yakuzaishi ni kusuri no nomikata o kikimashita.","german":"I asked the pharmacist how to take the medicine.","furigana":"やくざいし|くすり|の|かた|き"},{"japanese":"娘は薬剤師になるために薬学部で勉強しています。","romaji":"Musume wa yakuzaishi ni naru tame ni yakugakubu de benkyō shite imasu.","german":"My daughter is studying at the pharmacy faculty to become a pharmacist.","furigana":"むすめ|やくざいし|やくがくぶ|べんきょう"},{"japanese":"薬局の薬剤師さんに、この薬は眠くなると言われた。","romaji":"Yakkyoku no yakuzaishi-san ni, kono kusuri wa nemuku naru to iwareta.","german":"The pharmacist at the pharmacy told me this medicine would make me sleepy.","furigana":"やっきょく|やくざいし|くすり|ねむ|い"}],"notes":"薬剤師 is the state-licensed \"pharmacist\", composed of 薬剤 (\"medicine\") and 師 (\"professional\") as in 医師 or 看護師. One meets them at a 薬局 (\"pharmacy\", where you fill a 処方箋) or at a ドラッグストア; so the place is called 薬局, the person 薬剤師. In conversation one politely says 薬剤師さん.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"年に一度、がん検診を受けるべきです。","romaji":"Nen ni ichido, gan kenshin o ukeru beki desu.","german":"You should get a cancer screening once a year.","furigana":"ねん|いちど|けんしん|う"},{"japanese":"歯医者で定期検診を受けました。","romaji":"Haisha de teiki kenshin o ukemashita.","german":"I had a regular checkup at the dentist.","furigana":"はいしゃ|ていきけんしん|う"},{"japanese":"妊娠中は定期的に検診を受ける必要がある。","romaji":"Ninshinchū wa teikiteki ni kenshin o ukeru hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"During pregnancy, you need to have regular checkups.","furigana":"にんしんちゅう|ていきてき|けんしん|う|ひつよう"}],"notes":"検診 is an examination for a specific disease, a screening: がん検診, 歯科検診, 定期検診; the verb is 検診を受ける. To be distinguished are the identically read 健診 (short form of 健康診断, a general health checkup), 診察 (examination at a doctor's visit because of complaints) and 検査 (a test, e.g. a blood test).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しいワクチンが承認されました。","romaji":"Atarashii wakuchin ga shōnin saremashita.","german":"Ein neuer Impfstoff wurde zugelassen.","furigana":"あたら|しょうにん"},{"japanese":"子供にインフルエンザのワクチンを打ってもらった。","romaji":"Kodomo ni infuruenza no wakuchin o utte moratta.","german":"Ich habe mein Kind gegen Grippe impfen lassen.","furigana":"こども|う"},{"japanese":"ワクチンの接種は駅前のクリニックで受けられます。","romaji":"Wakuchin no sesshu wa ekimae no kurinikku de ukeraremasu.","german":"Die Impfung kann man in der Praxis am Bahnhof bekommen.","furigana":"せっしゅ|えきまえ|う"}],"notes":"ワクチン („Impfstoff“, aus dem Deutschen „Vakzine“) steht meist mit 打つ oder 接種する: ワクチンを打つ (alltäglich), ワクチンを接種する / ワクチン接種 (formell); sich impfen lassen heißt ワクチンを打ってもらう. Der allgemeine Begriff „Impfung“ ist 予防接種, Impfreaktionen heißen 副反応. Selten bedeutet ワクチン auch Antivirensoftware.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この病気は人から人へ伝染します。","romaji":"Kono byōki wa hito kara hito e densen shimasu.","german":"This disease spreads from person to person.","furigana":"びょうき|ひと|ひと|でんせん"},{"japanese":"インフルエンザは伝染しやすいので、マスクをしましょう。","romaji":"Infuruenza wa densen shiyasui node, masuku o shimashō.","german":"Influenza spreads easily, so let's wear masks.","furigana":"でんせん"},{"japanese":"あくびは伝染するとよく言われる。","romaji":"Akubi wa densen suru to yoku iwareru.","german":"It is often said that yawning is contagious.","furigana":"でんせん|い"}],"notes":"伝染 is transmission from person to person, as an intransitive する-verb: 病気が伝染する; 伝染病 is a \"contagious disease\". 感染, in contrast, denotes the infection of the individual organism (ウイルスに感染する) and is more common in modern medical language. Figuratively: あくびが伝染する. Colloquially one says うつる: 風邪がうつる, transitively 風邪をうつす.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"細菌の繁殖を防ぐために手を洗ってください。","romaji":"Saikin no hanshoku o fusegu tame ni te o aratte kudasai.","german":"Please wash your hands to prevent the growth of bacteria.","furigana":"さいきん|はんしょく|ふせ|て|あら"},{"japanese":"細菌とウイルスは大きさも性質も違う。","romaji":"Saikin to uirusu wa ōkisa mo seishitsu mo chigau.","german":"Bacteria and viruses differ in both size and nature.","furigana":"さいきん|おお|せいしつ|ちが"},{"japanese":"傷口から細菌が入らないように消毒した。","romaji":"Kizuguchi kara saikin ga hairanai yō ni shōdoku shita.","german":"I disinfected the wound so that bacteria wouldn't get in.","furigana":"きずぐち|さいきん|はい|しょうどく"}],"notes":"細菌 means \"bacterium, bacteria\" (same form for singular and plural) and is the factual, scientific word. In daily life one hears 菌 (大腸菌, 乳酸菌) or, childishly colloquial, ばい菌 (\"germs\"). Important is the difference from ウイルス: antibiotics (抗生物質) work against 細菌, not against viruses. Typical: 細菌が繁殖する / 増える, 細菌に感染する.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ストレスが原因で頭痛がひどいです。","romaji":"Sutoresu ga gen'in de zutsū ga hidoi desu.","german":"I have a terrible headache because of stress.","furigana":"げんいん|ずつう"},{"japanese":"朝から頭痛がするので、薬を飲んだ。","romaji":"Asa kara zutsū ga suru node, kusuri o nonda.","german":"I've had a headache since this morning, so I took some medicine.","furigana":"あさ|ずつう|くすり|の"},{"japanese":"息子の進路が最近の頭痛の種だ。","romaji":"Musuko no shinro ga saikin no zutsū no tane da.","german":"My son's career path is my latest headache.","furigana":"むすこ|しんろ|さいきん|ずつう|たね"}],"notes":"頭痛 (ずつう) is a headache; you say 頭痛がする (not ある), 頭痛がひどい, plus 頭痛薬 (\"headache pill\") and 頭痛持ち (\"someone who often has headaches\"). In conversation 頭が痛い is more common, and it also means \"to be a headache for (someone)\" figuratively. 頭痛の種 (\"a constant source of worry\") is also figurative.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"冷たいものを食べすぎて腹痛になりました。","romaji":"Tsumetai mono o tabesugite fukutsū ni narimashita.","german":"I got a stomachache from eating too many cold things.","furigana":"つめ|た|ふくつう"},{"japanese":"腹痛のため、本日は欠席させていただきます。","romaji":"Fukutsū no tame, honjitsu wa kesseki sasete itadakimasu.","german":"Because of a stomachache, I will be absent today.","furigana":"ふくつう|ほんじつ|けっせき"},{"japanese":"激しい腹痛を訴えて、救急車で運ばれた。","romaji":"Hageshii fukutsū o uttaete, kyūkyūsha de hakobareta.","german":"He complained of severe abdominal pain and was taken away in an ambulance.","furigana":"はげ|ふくつう|うった|きゅうきゅうしゃ|はこ"}],"notes":"腹痛 (ふくつう) is the Sino-Japanese, rather formal word for \"stomachache\": at the doctor's, in excuses, in the news. In everyday speech one says お腹が痛い; colloquially 腹痛 is also read はらいた. Typical: 腹痛がする, 腹痛を起こす, 腹痛を訴える (\"to complain of stomach pain\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"出血がひどかったので救急車を呼びました。","romaji":"Shukketsu ga hidokatta node kyūkyūsha o yobimashita.","german":"Because the bleeding was heavy, I called an ambulance.","furigana":"しゅっけつ|きゅうきゅうしゃ|よ"},{"japanese":"転んでひざから少し出血した。","romaji":"Koronde hiza kara sukoshi shukketsu shita.","german":"I fell and bled a little from my knee.","furigana":"ころ|すこ|しゅっけつ"},{"japanese":"閉店セールで、出血大サービスをしています。","romaji":"Heiten sēru de, shukketsu dai sābisu o shite imasu.","german":"The store is holding a closing-down sale with prices slashed below cost.","furigana":"へいてん|しゅっけつだい"}],"notes":"出血 means \"bleeding\"; as an intransitive する-verb: 出血する. Typical: 出血が止まる / 止まらない, 大量出血, 内出血 (\"internal bleeding, bruise\"). In everyday speech people usually just say 血が出る. In retail, 出血 figuratively means \"selling at a loss\": 出血大サービス (\"prices below cost\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"喉に炎症が起きて声が出にくいです。","romaji":"Nodo ni enshō ga okite koe ga denikui desu.","german":"My throat is inflamed and it's hard to speak.","furigana":"のど|えんしょう|お|こえ|で"},{"japanese":"医者から炎症を抑える薬をもらった。","romaji":"Isha kara enshō o osaeru kusuri o moratta.","german":"I got medicine from the doctor to reduce the inflammation.","furigana":"いしゃ|えんしょう|おさ|くすり"},{"japanese":"日焼けで肌が炎症を起こしている。","romaji":"Hiyake de hada ga enshō o okoshite iru.","german":"My skin is inflamed from sunburn.","furigana":"ひや|はだ|えんしょう|お"}],"notes":"炎症 is the neutral medical word for \"inflammation\": 炎症が起きる (intransitive) or 炎症を起こす, 炎症を抑える (\"to reduce inflammation\"), 炎症がひどい. Specific inflammatory diseases are formed with the suffix 〜炎: 胃炎, 肺炎, 鼻炎, 皮膚炎. The visible swelling that goes with it is described with 腫れる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"花粉アレルギーで春はつらいです。","romaji":"Kafun arerugī de haru wa tsurai desu.","german":"Wegen einer Pollenallergie ist der Frühling schwer für mich.","furigana":"かふん|はる"},{"japanese":"卵アレルギーがあるので、ケーキは食べられません。","romaji":"Tamago arerugī ga aru node, kēki wa taberaremasen.","german":"Ich habe eine Eierallergie und kann deshalb keinen Kuchen essen.","furigana":"たまご|た"},{"japanese":"学生時代から数学アレルギーで、数字を見るだけで嫌になる。","romaji":"Gakusei jidai kara sūgaku arerugī de, sūji o miru dake de iya ni naru.","german":"Seit meiner Schulzeit bin ich allergisch gegen Mathe – schon Zahlen verderben mir die Laune.","furigana":"がくせいじだい|すうがく|すうじ|み|いや"}],"notes":"アレルギー stammt aus dem Deutschen. „Ich habe eine Allergie“ heißt 〜アレルギーがある oder 〜にアレルギーがある: 卵アレルギー, 小麦アレルギー, アレルギー反応, アレルギー体質. Für Heuschnupfen ist 花粉症 viel geläufiger als 花粉アレルギー. Bildlich steht 〜アレルギー für eine starke Abneigung: 数学アレルギー.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子供の頃から喘息に悩まされています。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro kara zensoku ni nayamasarete imasu.","german":"I've suffered from asthma since childhood.","furigana":"こども|ころ|ぜんそく|なや"},{"japanese":"夜中に喘息の発作が起きて、息が苦しくなった。","romaji":"Yonaka ni zensoku no hossa ga okite, iki ga kurushiku natta.","german":"In the middle of the night I had an asthma attack and found it hard to breathe.","furigana":"よなか|ぜんそく|ほっさ|お|いき|くる"},{"japanese":"喘息の子供のために、家ではたばこを吸わないようにしている。","romaji":"Zensoku no kodomo no tame ni, ie de wa tabako o suwanai yō ni shite iru.","german":"For the sake of our child with asthma, we don't smoke at home.","furigana":"ぜんそく|こども|いえ|す"}],"notes":"喘息 is \"asthma\"; because 喘 is a difficult kanji, it is often written ぜんそく or ぜん息. Typical are 喘息の発作 (\"asthma attack\"), 喘息持ち (\"asthmatic\"), 小児喘息 and 喘息に悩まされる. The symptoms are described with 咳, 息苦しい or the onomatopoeic ゼーゼーする.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"食事管理で糖尿病をコントロールしています。","romaji":"Shokuji kanri de tōnyōbyō o kontorōru shite imasu.","german":"I manage my diabetes through diet control.","furigana":"しょくじかんり|とうにょうびょう"},{"japanese":"父は糖尿病なので、甘いものを控えている。","romaji":"Chichi wa tōnyōbyō na node, amai mono o hikaete iru.","german":"My father has diabetes, so he cuts back on sweets.","furigana":"ちち|とうにょうびょう|あま|ひか"},{"japanese":"健康診断で糖尿病の疑いがあると言われた。","romaji":"Kenkō shindan de tōnyōbyō no utagai ga aru to iwareta.","german":"At my health checkup, I was told that diabetes is suspected.","furigana":"けんこうしんだん|とうにょうびょう|うたが|い"}],"notes":"糖尿病 is \"diabetes\", literally \"sugar-urine disease\". Typical: 糖尿病になる, 糖尿病を患う (formal), 糖尿病の疑い, 糖尿病予備軍 (\"at-risk group, prediabetics\"); blood sugar is called 血糖値. It is one of the 生活習慣病 (\"lifestyle diseases\"); colloquially it is also shortened to 糖尿.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"塩分の取りすぎは高血圧の原因になります。","romaji":"Enbun no torisugi wa kōketsuatsu no gen'in ni narimasu.","german":"Too much salt can cause high blood pressure.","furigana":"えんぶん|と|こうけつあつ|げんいん"},{"japanese":"祖母は高血圧の薬を毎日飲んでいる。","romaji":"Sobo wa kōketsuatsu no kusuri o mainichi nonde iru.","german":"My grandmother takes medicine for high blood pressure every day.","furigana":"そぼ|こうけつあつ|くすり|まいにちの"},{"japanese":"健康診断で、高血圧気味だと言われた。","romaji":"Kenkō shindan de, kōketsuatsu gimi da to iwareta.","german":"At the health checkup, I was told my blood pressure was slightly high.","furigana":"けんこうしんだん|こうけつあつぎみ|い"}],"notes":"高血圧 is \"high blood pressure\" (高 + 血圧 \"blood pressure\"); the opposite is 低血圧, with which people in Japan often also explain being a poor morning person (低血圧で朝が弱い). Typical: 高血圧になる, 高血圧の薬, 高血圧気味 (\"slightly elevated blood pressure\"). In conversation one often simply says 血圧が高い.","levelNote":"Compound: 高 (N2) + 血圧 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"心臓病の予防には適度な運動が大切です。","romaji":"Shinzōbyō no yobō ni wa tekido na undō ga taisetsu desu.","german":"Moderate exercise is important for preventing heart disease.","furigana":"しんぞうびょう|よぼう|てきど|うんどう|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"彼は心臓病を抱えながらも、仕事を続けている。","romaji":"Kare wa shinzōbyō o kakaenagara mo, shigoto o tsuzukete iru.","german":"He continues to work despite having heart disease.","furigana":"かれ|しんぞうびょう|かか|しごと|つづ"},{"japanese":"祖父は心臓病で手術を受けたことがある。","romaji":"Sofu wa shinzōbyō de shujutsu o uketa koto ga aru.","german":"My grandfather once had surgery for heart disease.","furigana":"そふ|しんぞうびょう|しゅじゅつ|う"}],"notes":"心臓病 is the umbrella term \"heart disease\" (心臓 \"heart\" + 病 \"disease\"). Specific diagnoses are, for example, 心筋梗塞 (\"heart attack\") or 狭心症 (\"angina pectoris\"); technical texts also say 心疾患. Typical: 心臓病を抱える, 心臓病で亡くなる, 心臓病の予防. In everyday speech it is often simply 心臓が悪い.","levelNote":"Compound: 心臓 (N3) + 病 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"早期発見ができればがんは治療できます。","romaji":"Sōki hakken ga dekireba gan wa chiryō dekimasu.","german":"If cancer is detected early, it can be treated.","furigana":"そうきはっけん|ちりょう"},{"japanese":"叔父は肺がんで亡くなりました。","romaji":"Oji wa haigan de nakunarimashita.","german":"My uncle died of lung cancer.","furigana":"おじ|はい|な"},{"japanese":"タバコはがんのリスクを高めると言われている。","romaji":"Tabako wa gan no risuku o takameru to iwarete iru.","german":"Smoking is said to increase the risk of cancer.","furigana":"たか|い"}],"notes":"がん (\"cancer\") is mostly written in hiragana or as ガン; the kanji 癌 is rare. As a suffix it forms types of cancer: 胃がん, 肺がん, 乳がん, plus がん検診 and 末期がん (\"end-stage cancer\"). Typical: がんになる, がんが見つかる, がんと診断される, がんで亡くなる. Figuratively, 〜のがん is the \"cancer\" or root problem of an organization.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"検査で腫瘍が見つかりましたが良性でした。","romaji":"Kensa de shuyō ga mitsukarimashita ga ryōsei deshita.","german":"A tumor was found in the examination, but it was benign.","furigana":"けんさ|しゅよう|み|りょうせい"},{"japanese":"腫瘍を取り除く手術は無事に終わった。","romaji":"Shuyō o torinozoku shujutsu wa buji ni owatta.","german":"The surgery to remove the tumor ended successfully.","furigana":"しゅよう|と|のぞ|しゅじゅつ|ぶじ|お"},{"japanese":"悪性の腫瘍かどうかは、詳しい検査をしないとわからない。","romaji":"Akusei no shuyō ka dō ka wa, kuwashii kensa o shinai to wakaranai.","german":"Whether it is a malignant tumor can't be known without a detailed examination.","furigana":"あくせい|しゅよう|くわ|けんさ"}],"notes":"腫瘍 is the medical term for \"tumor, growth\" (腫 as in 腫れる \"to swell\"). A distinction is made between 良性腫瘍 (\"benign\") and 悪性腫瘍 (\"malignant\", i.e. がん). Typical: 腫瘍が見つかる / できる, 腫瘍を摘出する / 取り除く, 脳腫瘍. A harmless lump or pimple is called できもの in everyday speech.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"骨折が完治するまで三ヶ月かかりました。","romaji":"Kossetsu ga kanchi suru made sankagetsu kakarimashita.","german":"It took three months for the fracture to heal completely.","furigana":"こっせつ|かんち|さんかげつ"},{"japanese":"この病気は完治が難しいと言われている。","romaji":"Kono byōki wa kanchi ga muzukashii to iwarete iru.","german":"This disease is said to be difficult to cure completely.","furigana":"びょうき|かんち|むずか|い"},{"japanese":"けがが完治したので、来週から練習に復帰します。","romaji":"Kega ga kanchi shita node, raishū kara renshū ni fukki shimasu.","german":"The injury has healed completely, so I will return to practice next week.","furigana":"かんち|らいしゅう|れんしゅう|ふっき"}],"notes":"完治 (\"complete cure\") is an intransitive する-verb with the illness or injury as subject: 病気 / けがが完治する; transitively one says 完治させる. Compared with the everyday 治る it is more formal and stresses that nothing remains. 全快 is said more of the person (\"fully healthy again\"), 回復 is recovery in general, 治療 is treatment. Less often it is also read かんじ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"慢性的な腰痛に悩んでいます。","romaji":"Manseiteki na yōtsū ni nayande imasu.","german":"I suffer from chronic back pain.","furigana":"まんせいてき|ようつう|なや"},{"japanese":"慢性胃炎と診断されました。","romaji":"Mansei ien to shindan saremashita.","german":"I was diagnosed with chronic gastritis.","furigana":"まんせいいえん|しんだん"},{"japanese":"その会社では、慢性的な赤字が続いている。","romaji":"Sono kaisha de wa, manseiteki na akaji ga tsuzuite iru.","german":"That company keeps running chronic deficits.","furigana":"かいしゃ|まんせいてき|あかじ|つづ"}],"notes":"慢性 (\"chronic\") stands before disease names like a prefix (慢性胃炎, 慢性疲労) or with の: 慢性の病気. For problems outside medicine one uses the な-adjective 慢性的な: 慢性的な人手不足, 慢性的な渋滞. 慢性化する means \"to become chronic\"; the opposite is 急性 (\"acute\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"急性の胃炎で入院しました。","romaji":"Kyūsei no ien de nyūin shimashita.","german":"I was hospitalized for acute gastritis.","furigana":"きゅうせい|いえん|にゅういん"},{"japanese":"飲み会で一気飲みをして、急性アルコール中毒になった学生がいた。","romaji":"Nomikai de ikkinomi o shite, kyūsei arukōru chūdoku ni natta gakusei ga ita.","german":"At a drinking party, a student got acute alcohol poisoning from chugging.","furigana":"の|かい|いっきの|きゅうせい|ちゅうどく|がくせい"},{"japanese":"急性の症状が治まれば、退院できるでしょう。","romaji":"Kyūsei no shōjō ga osamareba, taiin dekiru deshō.","german":"Once the acute symptoms subside, you should be able to be discharged.","furigana":"きゅうせい|しょうじょう|おさ|たいいん"}],"notes":"急性 (\"acute\") describes an illness that occurs suddenly and severely and usually stands before the disease name: 急性胃腸炎, 急性アルコール中毒, 急性心不全, or with の: 急性の症状. The opposite is 慢性. For \"acute\" in the sense of \"urgent\", 急性 does not fit - for that one says 緊急 or 差し迫った.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"重症の患者は集中治療室に運ばれました。","romaji":"Jūshō no kanja wa shūchū chiryōshitsu ni hakobaremashita.","german":"The seriously ill patient was taken to the intensive care unit.","furigana":"じゅうしょう|かんじゃ|しゅうちゅうちりょうしつ|はこ"},{"japanese":"コロナに感染したが、重症にはならなかった。","romaji":"Korona ni kansen shita ga, jūshō ni wa naranakatta.","german":"I was infected with COVID, but it didn't become severe.","furigana":"かんせん|じゅうしょう"},{"japanese":"うちの夫のゲーム好きはもう重症だ。","romaji":"Uchi no otto no gēmuzuki wa mō jūshō da.","german":"My husband's love of games is already a severe case.","furigana":"おっと|ず|じゅうしょう"}],"notes":"重症 denotes a severe course of illness: 重症患者, 重症になる, 重症化する (\"to worsen\"). Beware of the identically read 重傷 (\"serious injury\"), which is used for accidents: 重傷を負う. The opposite is 軽症. Jokingly, about a passion one says 〜はもう重症だ (\"a hopeless case\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"幸い軽症で済んだので入院の必要はありません。","romaji":"Saiwai keishō de sunda node nyūin no hitsuyō wa arimasen.","german":"Fortunately it was only a mild case, so hospitalization is not necessary.","furigana":"さいわ|けいしょう|す|にゅういん|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"感染者の多くは軽症で、自宅で休んでいる。","romaji":"Kansensha no ōku wa keishō de, jitaku de yasunde iru.","german":"Most of the infected have mild symptoms and are resting at home.","furigana":"かんせんしゃ|おお|けいしょう|じたく|やす"},{"japanese":"軽症の患者は、まず近くのクリニックを受診してください。","romaji":"Keishō no kanja wa, mazu chikaku no kurinikku o jushin shite kudasai.","german":"Patients with mild symptoms, please first see a nearby clinic.","furigana":"けいしょう|かんじゃ|ちか|じゅしん"}],"notes":"軽症 is a mild course of illness: 軽症で済む (\"to get off lightly\"), 軽症者, 軽症患者. The identically read 軽傷, in contrast, means \"minor injury\" (in accidents: 軽傷を負う). The opposite term is 重症; in between, hospitals speak of 中等症 (\"moderate\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"学校で身体測定があって、身長が五センチ伸びていた。","romaji":"Gakkō de shintai sokutei ga atte, shinchō ga go-senchi nobite ita.","german":"We had a physical examination at school, and I had grown five centimeters.","furigana":"がっこう|しんたいそくてい|しんちょう|ご|の"},{"japanese":"この仕事は身体的な負担が大きい。","romaji":"Kono shigoto wa shintaiteki na futan ga ōkii.","german":"This job is physically very demanding.","furigana":"しごと|しんたいてき|ふたん|おお"},{"japanese":"彼は身体能力が高く、どんなスポーツもすぐ上手になる。","romaji":"Kare wa shintai nōryoku ga takaku, donna supōtsu mo sugu jōzu ni naru.","german":"He has great physical ability and quickly becomes good at any sport.","furigana":"かれ|しんたいのうりょく|たか|じょうず"}],"notes":"身体 read しんたい is the formal word for \"body\" and appears mainly in compounds: 身体検査, 身体測定, 身体能力, 身体障害, 身体的な. In everyday speech people say 体 (からだ); when 身体 stands alone in a text, it is often read からだ too (usually with furigana). 肉体 stresses the body as opposed to the mind, 人体 the human body in an anatomical sense.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人間の脳は非常に複雑な器官です。","romaji":"Ningen no nō wa hijō ni fukuzatsu na kikan desu.","german":"The human brain is a very complex organ.","furigana":"にんげん|のう|ひじょう|ふくざつ|きかん"},{"japanese":"祖父は脳の病気で倒れ、入院している。","romaji":"Sofu wa nō no byōki de taore, nyūin shite iru.","german":"My grandfather collapsed due to a brain disease and is in the hospital.","furigana":"そふ|のう|びょうき|たお|にゅういん"},{"japanese":"毎日パズルを解いて、脳を鍛えています。","romaji":"Mainichi pazuru o toite, nō o kitaete imasu.","german":"I solve puzzles every day to train my brain.","furigana":"まいにち|と|のう|きた"}],"notes":"脳 is the \"brain\" in the anatomical and scientific sense: 脳の病気, 脳卒中 (\"stroke\"), 脳死, 脳を鍛える, 脳トレ. For intelligence in everyday life one says 頭 (頭がいい), not 脳; 頭脳 (\"brains, clever mind\") is more elevated. Colloquially the brain is also called 脳みそ.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 47"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"熱があるか確認するために額に手を当てました。","romaji":"Netsu ga aru ka kakunin suru tame ni hitai ni te o atemashita.","german":"I put my hand on his forehead to check whether he had a fever.","furigana":"ねつ|かくにん|ひたい|て|あ"},{"japanese":"暑さで額から汗が流れてきた。","romaji":"Atsusa de hitai kara ase ga nagarete kita.","german":"Sweat ran down my forehead from the heat.","furigana":"あつ|ひたい|あせ|なが"},{"japanese":"猫の額ほどの庭ですが、野菜を育てています。","romaji":"Neko no hitai hodo no niwa desu ga, yasai o sodatete imasu.","german":"It's a garden the size of a cat's forehead, but I grow vegetables in it.","furigana":"ねこ|ひたい|にわ|やさい|そだ"}],"notes":"額 read ひたい is the \"forehead\"; the same kanji read がく means \"picture frame\" (額縁) or \"amount\" (金額) - context decides. Typical: 額に手を当てる, 額に汗をかく, 額に汗して働く (\"to work by the sweat of one's brow\"); 猫の額 denotes a tiny plot of land. In everyday speech, especially with children, people usually say おでこ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は驚いて眉を上げました。","romaji":"Kanojo wa odoroite mayu o agemashita.","german":"She raised her eyebrows in surprise.","furigana":"かのじょ|おどろ|まゆ|あ"},{"japanese":"彼は話を聞いて、不快そうに眉をひそめた。","romaji":"Kare wa hanashi o kiite, fukai sō ni mayu o hisometa.","german":"When he heard the story, he frowned in displeasure.","furigana":"かれ|はなし|き|ふかい|まゆ"},{"japanese":"毎朝鏡の前で眉を描いています。","romaji":"Maiasa kagami no mae de mayu o kaite imasu.","german":"Every morning I draw on my eyebrows in front of the mirror.","furigana":"まいあさかがみ|まえ|まゆ|か"}],"notes":"眉 is the \"eyebrow\"; in everyday speech people mostly say 眉毛 (まゆげ), while 眉 alone appears mainly in set phrases and in cosmetics: 眉をひそめる (\"to frown in disapproval\"), 眉を寄せる, 眉を描く / 整える. The spot between the eyebrows is 眉間 (みけん); 眉唾 means \"dubious, hard to believe\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"疲れてまぶたが重くなってきました。","romaji":"Tsukarete mabuta ga omoku natte kimashita.","german":"I'm tired and my eyelids are getting heavy.","furigana":"つか|おも"},{"japanese":"朝起きたら、まぶたが腫れていた。","romaji":"Asa okitara, mabuta ga harete ita.","german":"When I woke up in the morning, my eyelids were swollen.","furigana":"あさお|は"},{"japanese":"目を閉じると、故郷の景色がまぶたに浮かぶ。","romaji":"Me o tojiru to, kokyō no keshiki ga mabuta ni ukabu.","german":"When I close my eyes, the scenery of my hometown appears before my eyes.","furigana":"め|と|こきょう|けしき|う"}],"notes":"まぶた (\"eyelid\") is almost always written in kana (the kanji 瞼 / 目蓋 are rare). Typical: まぶたが重い (\"to be sleepy\"), まぶたが腫れる, 二重まぶた / 一重まぶた (\"double / single eyelid\", a common beauty topic). Figuratively: まぶたに浮かぶ (\"to appear before one's mind's eye\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"走った後、胸がドキドキしています。","romaji":"Hashitta ato, mune ga dokidoki shite imasu.","german":"My heart is pounding after running.","furigana":"はし|あと|むね"},{"japanese":"彼女の優しい言葉に胸が熱くなった。","romaji":"Kanojo no yasashii kotoba ni mune ga atsuku natta.","german":"Her kind words moved me deeply.","furigana":"かのじょ|やさ|ことば|むね|あつ"},{"japanese":"彼は胸を張って自分の作品を発表した。","romaji":"Kare wa mune o hatte jibun no sakuhin o happyō shita.","german":"He stuck out his chest and presented his work proudly.","furigana":"かれ|むね|は|じぶん|さくひん|はっぴょう"}],"notes":"胸 is the \"chest\" (rib cage, also bosom) and, figuratively, the feeling \"heart.\" Many expressions: 胸が痛む (\"to feel sympathy, grief\"), 胸がいっぱい (\"overwhelmed\"), 胸が熱くなる (\"to be moved\"), 胸がドキドキする, 胸を張る (\"to carry oneself proudly\"). The organ itself is 心臓, the abstract heart or mind 心.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"腹が減ったので何か食べたいです。","romaji":"Hara ga hetta node nanika tabetai desu.","german":"I'm hungry, so I want to eat something.","furigana":"はら|へ|なに|た"},{"japanese":"彼の失礼な態度に腹が立った。","romaji":"Kare no shitsurei na taido ni hara ga tatta.","german":"His rude attitude made me angry.","furigana":"かれ|しつれい|たいど|はら|た"},{"japanese":"食べすぎて腹が出てきたので、ダイエットを始めた。","romaji":"Tabesugite hara ga dete kita node, daietto o hajimeta.","german":"I ate too much and my belly started to stick out, so I started a diet.","furigana":"た|はら|で|はじ"}],"notes":"腹 is \"belly,\" but it sounds more direct and rather masculine-casual compared with the neutral お腹 (おなか): 腹が減った vs. お腹が空いた. In many idioms only 腹 is possible: 腹が立つ (\"to get angry\"), 腹を立てる (\"to lose one's temper\"), 腹黒い (\"devious\"), 腹を割って話す (\"to talk frankly\"). Medically one says 腹部.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"テーブルに肘をつくのは行儀が悪いです。","romaji":"Tēburu ni hiji o tsuku no wa gyōgi ga warui desu.","german":"It is bad manners to rest your elbows on the table.","furigana":"ひじ|ぎょうぎ|わる"},{"japanese":"転んだときに、肘を強く打ってしまった。","romaji":"Koronda toki ni, hiji o tsuyoku utte shimatta.","german":"When I fell, I hit my elbow hard.","furigana":"ころ|ひじ|つよ|う"},{"japanese":"このいすは肘掛けがあって楽だ。","romaji":"Kono isu wa hijikake ga atte raku da.","german":"This chair has armrests and is comfortable.","furigana":"ひじか|らく"}],"notes":"肘 (\"elbow\") is also often written in kana as ひじ. Typical: 肘をつく (\"to rest one's elbows\", a table manners topic), 肘を曲げる / 伸ばす, 肘を打つ, テニス肘. 肘掛け is the \"armrest\". Not to be confused with the similar-sounding 膝 (ひざ, \"knee\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"テニスのしすぎで手首が痛くなりました。","romaji":"Tenisu no shisugi de tekubi ga itaku narimashita.","german":"My wrist started to hurt from playing too much tennis.","furigana":"てくび|いた"},{"japanese":"腕時計を右の手首につけています。","romaji":"Udedokei o migi no tekubi ni tsukete imasu.","german":"I wear my watch on my right wrist.","furigana":"うでどけい|みぎ|てくび"},{"japanese":"転んで手をついたら、手首をひねってしまった。","romaji":"Koronde te o tsuitara, tekubi o hinette shimatta.","german":"When I fell and put my hand down, I twisted my wrist.","furigana":"ころ|て|てくび"}],"notes":"手首 is the \"wrist\" (手 \"hand\" + 首 \"neck\"), analogous to 足首 (\"ankle\"). Typical: 手首が痛い, 手首をひねる / くじく (\"to sprain one's wrist\"), 手首を回す, 手首をつかむ, 手首に時計をつける. 腕 is the whole arm, 手 the hand.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"マラソンの後、脚が筋肉痛です。","romaji":"Marason no ato, ashi ga kinnikutsū desu.","german":"After the marathon, my legs are sore.","furigana":"あと|あし|きんにくつう"},{"japanese":"このテーブルは脚が一本ぐらぐらしている。","romaji":"Kono tēburu wa ashi ga ippon guragura shite iru.","german":"One leg of this table is wobbly.","furigana":"あし|いっぽん"},{"japanese":"モデルの彼女は脚が長くて細い。","romaji":"Moderu no kanojo wa ashi ga nagakute hosoi.","german":"She is a model and has long, slender legs.","furigana":"かのじょ|あし|なが|ほそ"}],"notes":"脚 and 足 are both read あし: 足 is the foot and, in general, also the leg; 脚 refers more precisely to the leg from the hip to the ankle as well as the legs of furniture (机の脚). Hence 脚が長い, but 足が速い (\"to run fast\") or 足のサイズ. In everyday writing, 足 is often used for both. As a counter, 〜脚 (きゃく) is used to count chairs.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5 (source: N5; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい靴を履いたら踵が痛くなりました。","romaji":"Atarashii kutsu o haitara kakato ga itaku narimashita.","german":"When I put on the new shoes, my heel started to hurt.","furigana":"あたら|くつ|は|かかと|いた"},{"japanese":"かかとの高い靴を履くと、足が疲れる。","romaji":"Kakato no takai kutsu o haku to, ashi ga tsukareru.","german":"Wearing high-heeled shoes makes my feet tired.","furigana":"たか|くつ|は|あし|つか"},{"japanese":"冬はかかとがかさかさになりやすい。","romaji":"Fuyu wa kakato ga kasakasa ni nariyasui.","german":"In winter, heels easily become rough and dry.","furigana":"ふゆ"}],"notes":"踵 means \"heel\" and also the \"heel\" of a shoe or the heel of a sock; it is almost always written in kana as かかと. Typical: かかとが痛い, かかとの高い靴 (\"high-heeled shoes\"), 靴のかかとを踏む (\"to step on the back of one's shoe\"). In the elevated expression 踵を返す (\"to turn on one's heel\") the kanji is read きびす.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"内臓の健康を保つためにバランスの良い食事が大切です。","romaji":"Naizō no kenkō o tamotsu tame ni baransu no yoi shokuji ga taisetsu desu.","german":"A balanced diet is important for keeping your internal organs healthy.","furigana":"ないぞう|けんこう|たも|よ|しょくじ|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"魚の内臓を取ってから焼きます。","romaji":"Sakana no naizō o totte kara yakimasu.","german":"I remove the guts of the fish before grilling it.","furigana":"さかな|ないぞう|と|や"},{"japanese":"事故で内臓を傷つけ、緊急手術を受けた。","romaji":"Jiko de naizō o kizutsuke, kinkyū shujutsu o uketa.","german":"He injured his internal organs in the accident and underwent emergency surgery.","furigana":"じこ|ないぞう|きず|きんきゅうしゅじゅつ|う"}],"notes":"内臓 refers to the internal organs as a whole (\"viscera, entrails\"), in medicine, biology and cooking: 内臓脂肪 (\"visceral fat\"), 内臓を取る (\"to gut a fish\"). A single organ is called 臓器 (臓器移植). A classic mix-up: the identically read 内蔵 means \"built-in\" (カメラ内蔵のスマホ).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お酒の飲みすぎは肝臓に悪いです。","romaji":"Osake no nomisugi wa kanzō ni warui desu.","german":"Drinking too much alcohol is bad for the liver.","furigana":"さけ|の|かんぞう|わる"},{"japanese":"健康診断で肝臓の数値が高いと言われた。","romaji":"Kenkō shindan de kanzō no sūchi ga takai to iwareta.","german":"At my health checkup, I was told my liver values were high.","furigana":"けんこうしんだん|かんぞう|すうち|たか|い"},{"japanese":"レバーは牛や鶏の肝臓のことです。","romaji":"Rebā wa ushi ya niwatori no kanzō no koto desu.","german":"\"Rebā\" means the liver of cattle or chicken.","furigana":"うし|にわとり|かんぞう"}],"notes":"肝臓 is the \"liver\" as an organ. Typical: 肝臓を悪くする, 肝臓に負担をかける, 肝臓の数値 (\"liver values\"), 肝臓がん; because damage goes unnoticed for a long time, it is called 沈黙の臓器 (\"silent organ\"). As a food one says レバー. The older 肝 (きも) is found in expressions such as 肝に銘じる (\"to take to heart\") and 肝試し (\"test of courage\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"腎臓は血液をろ過する大切な臓器です。","romaji":"Jinzō wa ketsueki o roka suru taisetsu na zōki desu.","german":"The kidney is an important organ that filters the blood.","furigana":"じんぞう|けつえき|か|たいせつ|ぞうき"},{"japanese":"父は腎臓が悪く、週に三回透析を受けている。","romaji":"Chichi wa jinzō ga waruku, shū ni sankai tōseki o ukete iru.","german":"My father has a kidney problem and receives dialysis three times a week.","furigana":"ちち|じんぞう|わる|しゅう|さんかいとうせき|う"},{"japanese":"母に腎臓を一つ提供することに決めた。","romaji":"Haha ni jinzō o hitotsu teikyō suru koto ni kimeta.","german":"I decided to donate one kidney to my mother.","furigana":"はは|じんぞう|ひと|ていきょう|き"}],"notes":"腎臓 is the \"kidney\"; in technical terms often only 腎 appears: 腎不全 (\"kidney failure\"), 副腎 (\"adrenal gland\"). Typical: 腎臓が悪い, 腎臓病, 腎臓移植. Interesting: 肝心 (\"crucial, essential\") used to be written 肝腎 as well - liver and kidney as vital organs.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大学院で言語学を研究しています。","romaji":"Daigakuin de gengogaku o kenkyū shite imasu.","german":"I am doing research in linguistics at graduate school.","furigana":"だいがくいん|げんごがく|けんきゅう"},{"japanese":"大学を卒業したら、大学院に進むつもりです。","romaji":"Daigaku o sotsugyō shitara, daigakuin ni susumu tsumori desu.","german":"After I graduate from university, I intend to go on to graduate school.","furigana":"だいがく|そつぎょう|だいがくいん|すす"},{"japanese":"大学院の入試に向けて、毎日英語を勉強している。","romaji":"Daigakuin no nyūshi ni mukete, mainichi eigo o benkyō shite iru.","german":"I study English every day for the graduate school entrance exam.","furigana":"だいがくいん|にゅうし|む|まいにちえいご|べんきょう"}],"notes":"大学院 is the level after the bachelor's degree in the 学部: 修士課程 (\"master's\") and 博士課程 (\"doctorate\"). Typical: 大学院に進む / 進学する, 大学院に入る, 大学院を修了する (formal \"to complete\" instead of 卒業). A student there is a 大学院生, or 院生 for short; the entrance exam is called 院試.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 31"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は法学部に所属しています。","romaji":"Kare wa hōgakubu ni shozoku shite imasu.","german":"He belongs to the Faculty of Law.","furigana":"かれ|ほうがくぶ|しょぞく"},{"japanese":"経済学部の三年生です。","romaji":"Keizai gakubu no sannensei desu.","german":"I'm a third-year student in the Faculty of Economics.","furigana":"けいざいがくぶ|さんねんせい"},{"japanese":"学部を卒業してすぐ、大学院に進学した。","romaji":"Gakubu o sotsugyō shite sugu, daigakuin ni shingaku shita.","german":"Right after graduating from undergraduate studies, I went on to graduate school.","furigana":"がくぶ|そつぎょう|だいがくいん|しんがく"}],"notes":"学部 is the \"faculty\" of a university and mostly stands as a suffix: 法学部, 経済学部, 医学部, 文学部. Below it is the 学科 (\"department, program\"). In contrast to 大学院, 学部 also denotes the bachelor's level: 学部生 (\"undergraduate\"), 学部時代. Typical: 〜学部に入る / 所属する, 〜学部の学生.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は建築学科で学んでいます。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kenchiku gakka de manande imasu.","german":"She is studying in the architecture department.","furigana":"かのじょ|けんちくがっか|まな"},{"japanese":"文学部の英文学科に所属しています。","romaji":"Bungakubu no eibun gakka ni shozoku shite imasu.","german":"I belong to the English literature department of the Faculty of Letters.","furigana":"ぶんがくぶ|えいぶんがっか|しょぞく"},{"japanese":"運転免許の学科試験に一回で合格した。","romaji":"Unten menkyo no gakka shiken ni ikkai de gōkaku shita.","german":"I passed the written test for my driver's license on the first try.","furigana":"うんてんめんきょ|がっかしけん|いっかい|ごうかく"}],"notes":"学科 denotes at university the department or course of study below the faculty (学部): 文学部英文学科 \"Faculty of Letters, Department of English Literature\". In general it can also mean a subject of study or examination; well known is 学科試験 \"written/theory exam\" (e.g. for a driver's license) as opposed to 実技試験 \"practical exam\". Not to be confused with 専攻 \"major, specialization\" and 科目 \"an individual subject of instruction or examination\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の専攻は国際関係学です。","romaji":"Watashi no senkō wa kokusai kankeigaku desu.","german":"My major is international relations.","furigana":"わたし|せんこう|こくさいかんけいがく"},{"japanese":"大学では何を専攻していましたか。","romaji":"Daigaku de wa nani o senkō shite imashita ka.","german":"What did you major in at university?","furigana":"だいがく|なに|せんこう"},{"japanese":"経済学を専攻したが、今は全く違う仕事をしている。","romaji":"Keizaigaku o senkō shita ga, ima wa mattaku chigau shigoto o shite iru.","german":"I majored in economics, but now I do a completely different job.","furigana":"けいざいがく|せんこう|いま|まった|ちが|しごと"}],"notes":"専攻 is one's major or field of specialization in university studies. As a transitive する-verb it takes を: 経済学を専攻する \"to major in economics\"; the typical question is 専攻は何ですか. In contrast, 専門 is broader and also covers a professional field of expertise, while 学科 is the organizational department of a university. A minor is called 副専攻.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"オンラインの講座に申し込みました。","romaji":"Onrain no kōza ni mōshikomimashita.","german":"I signed up for an online course.","furigana":"こうざ|もう|こ"},{"japanese":"市の文化センターで料理講座が開かれています。","romaji":"Shi no bunka sentā de ryōri kōza ga hirakarete imasu.","german":"A cooking course is being held at the city cultural center.","furigana":"し|ぶんか|りょうりこうざ|ひら"},{"japanese":"来月から大学で公開講座を担当することになった。","romaji":"Raigetsu kara daigaku de kōkai kōza o tantō suru koto ni natta.","german":"From next month, I will be in charge of a public lecture series at the university.","furigana":"らいげつ|だいがく|こうかいこうざ|たんとう"}],"notes":"講座 is a course or lecture series, often outside regular instruction: 市民講座 \"community education course\", 通信講座 \"correspondence course\", 英会話講座, 公開講座 \"public lecture series\". One signs up with に (講座に申し込む) and a course is held (講座が開かれる). Regular school instruction, on the other hand, is 授業, a single lecture 講義. At universities, 講座 also denotes a chair.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"教育実習で二週間学校に行きました。","romaji":"Kyōiku jisshū de nishūkan gakkō ni ikimashita.","german":"I went to a school for two weeks for my teaching practicum.","furigana":"きょういくじっしゅう|にしゅうかんがっこう|い"},{"japanese":"家庭科の調理実習でカレーを作った。","romaji":"Kateika no chōri jisshū de karē o tsukutta.","german":"In home economics class, we made curry in cooking practice.","furigana":"かていか|ちょうりじっしゅう|つく"},{"japanese":"看護学生は病院で実習を受けなければならない。","romaji":"Kango gakusei wa byōin de jisshū o ukenakereba naranai.","german":"Nursing students must complete clinical training at a hospital.","furigana":"かんごがくせい|びょういん|じっしゅう|う"}],"notes":"実習 is practical exercise or a practicum as part of training, in which one applies something oneself: 教育実習 \"teaching practicum\", 調理実習 \"cooking practice in class\", 病院実習, 実習生 \"trainee, intern\". One says 実習を受ける or 実習する. An internship at a company for career orientation is rather called インターンシップ; mere practicing of a skill is 練習.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"プログラムを修了するには二年かかります。","romaji":"Puroguramu o shūryō suru ni wa ninen kakarimasu.","german":"It takes two years to complete the program.","furigana":"しゅうりょう|にねん"},{"japanese":"研修を修了した人には修了証が渡されます。","romaji":"Kenshū o shūryō shita hito ni wa shūryōshō ga watasaremasu.","german":"Those who have completed the training receive a certificate of completion.","furigana":"けんしゅう|しゅうりょう|ひと|しゅうりょうしょう|わた"},{"japanese":"彼は大学院の修士課程を修了して、メーカーに就職した。","romaji":"Kare wa daigakuin no shūshi katei o shūryō shite, mēkā ni shūshoku shita.","german":"After completing his master's program at graduate school, he got a job at a manufacturer.","furigana":"かれ|だいがくいん|しゅうしかてい|しゅうりょう|しゅうしょく"}],"notes":"修了 is the successful completion of a course, training program or stage of study: 課程を修了する, 研修を修了する, 修了証 \"certificate of completion\". For a master's degree one says 大学院を修了する, whereas 卒業 is used for school and bachelor's studies. Do not confuse it with the identically read 終了 \"end, termination\", which is used very generally for events, broadcasts or programs.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"修士課程で環境科学を専攻しています。","romaji":"Shūshi katei de kankyō kagaku o senkō shite imasu.","german":"I'm majoring in environmental science in a master's program.","furigana":"しゅうしかてい|かんきょうかがく|せんこう"},{"japanese":"修士号を取るために、イギリスに留学します。","romaji":"Shūshigō o toru tame ni, Igirisu ni ryūgaku shimasu.","german":"I'm going to study in England to get a master's degree.","furigana":"しゅうしごう|と|りゅうがく"},{"japanese":"今、修士論文の締め切りに追われている。","romaji":"Ima, shūshi ronbun no shimekiri ni owarete iru.","german":"Right now I'm under pressure from the deadline for my master's thesis.","furigana":"いま|しゅうしろんぶん|し|き|お"}],"notes":"修士 is the academic degree \"master's\" and appears almost only in compounds: 修士課程 \"master's program\", 修士号 \"master's degree\", 修士論文 \"master's thesis\" (colloquially 修論). The levels are 学士 \"bachelor's\", 修士 \"master's\", 博士 \"doctorate\". 修士 is not used as a form of address or title for a person; one says things like 修士の学生 or 修士課程の学生.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"博士論文のテーマを決めなければなりません。","romaji":"Hakase ronbun no tēma o kimenakereba narimasen.","german":"I have to decide on the topic of my doctoral dissertation.","furigana":"はかせろんぶん|き"},{"japanese":"弟は虫のことなら何でも知っていて、まるで虫博士だ。","romaji":"Otōto wa mushi no koto nara nan demo shitte ite, marude mushi hakase da.","german":"My younger brother knows everything about insects - a real insect expert.","furigana":"おとうと|むし|なん|し|むしはかせ"},{"japanese":"山中博士の研究は多くの患者に希望を与えた。","romaji":"Yamanaka hakase no kenkyū wa ōku no kanja ni kibō o ataeta.","german":"Dr. Yamanaka's research gave hope to many patients.","furigana":"やまなかはかせ|けんきゅう|おお|かんじゃ|きぼう|あた"}],"notes":"博士 has two readings: in official usage the doctorate is usually called はくし (博士号, 博士課程), while in everyday speech and as a title after names it is often はかせ: 山中博士. With はかせ one also jokingly refers to an expert who knows a lot about something: 虫博士, お天気博士. A doctor as a profession, on the other hand, is called 医者; so \"doctor\" in the sense of physician is not 博士.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 47"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"田中教授の講義はとても分かりやすいです。","romaji":"Tanaka kyōju no kōgi wa totemo wakariyasui desu.","german":"Professor Tanaka's lectures are very easy to understand.","furigana":"たなかきょうじゅ|こうぎ|わ"},{"japanese":"父は大学で経済学の教授をしています。","romaji":"Chichi wa daigaku de keizaigaku no kyōju o shite imasu.","german":"My father is a professor of economics at a university.","furigana":"ちち|だいがく|けいざいがく|きょうじゅ"},{"japanese":"教授に論文の内容についてアドバイスをいただいた。","romaji":"Kyōju ni ronbun no naiyō ni tsuite adobaisu o itadaita.","german":"I received advice from my professor about the content of my paper.","furigana":"きょうじゅ|ろんぶん|ないよう"}],"notes":"教授 is a professor; after a name it serves as a title (田中教授), but in direct address you say 先生. Other ranks are 准教授 and 講師, and a professor emeritus is 名誉教授. As a する-verb, 教授 also means \"to teach, instruct\" in writing (書道を教授する), which is rare in everyday speech; people say 教える instead.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は最近、准教授に昇進しました。","romaji":"Kanojo wa saikin, junkyōju ni shōshin shimashita.","german":"She was recently promoted to associate professor.","furigana":"かのじょ|さいきん|じゅんきょうじゅ|しょうしん"},{"japanese":"この研究室は佐藤准教授が指導している。","romaji":"Kono kenkyūshitsu wa Satō junkyōju ga shidō shite iru.","german":"This lab is supervised by Associate Professor Sato.","furigana":"けんきゅうしつ|さとうじゅんきょうじゅ|しどう"},{"japanese":"准教授から教授になるまで十年かかった。","romaji":"Junkyōju kara kyōju ni naru made jūnen kakatta.","german":"It took ten years to go from associate professor to professor.","furigana":"じゅんきょうじゅ|きょうじゅ|じゅうねん"}],"notes":"准教授 has been the official title since 2007 for the rank directly below 教授 and corresponds to \"associate professor\"; the earlier title was 助教授. Like 教授, it is used as a title after names (佐藤准教授), but in direct address one simply says 先生. Typical combinations are 准教授に昇進する and 准教授を務める.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい講師が英語のクラスを担当します。","romaji":"Atarashii kōshi ga eigo no kurasu o tantō shimasu.","german":"A new lecturer will take over the English class.","furigana":"あたら|こうし|えいご|たんとう"},{"japanese":"彼は大学で非常勤講師として働いている。","romaji":"Kare wa daigaku de hijōkin kōshi toshite hataraite iru.","german":"He works as a part-time lecturer at a university.","furigana":"かれ|だいがく|ひじょうきんこうし|はたら"},{"japanese":"講演会の講師として、有名な作家が招かれた。","romaji":"Kōenkai no kōshi toshite, yūmei na sakka ga manekareta.","german":"A famous writer was invited as the speaker for the lecture event.","furigana":"こうえんかい|こうし|ゆうめい|さっか|まね"}],"notes":"講師 is someone who gives courses or lectures: at a university a lecturer ranking below the 准教授, as a 非常勤講師 a part-time lecturer, as well as teachers at cram schools (塾の講師) and speakers at lecture events (講演会の講師). Unlike 教師, the general word for teacher, 講師 emphasizes giving courses. A 講師 is addressed as 先生.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 49"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"研究室の助手が実験の準備を手伝ってくれます。","romaji":"Kenkyūshitsu no joshu ga jikken no junbi o tetsudatte kuremasu.","german":"The lab assistant helps with preparing the experiments.","furigana":"けんきゅうしつ|じょしゅ|じっけん|じゅんび|てつだ"},{"japanese":"助手席に座って地図を見てくれる?","romaji":"Joshuseki ni suwatte chizu o mite kureru?","german":"Could you sit in the passenger seat and look at the map?","furigana":"じょしゅせき|すわ|ちず|み"},{"japanese":"マジシャンは観客の中から助手を一人選んだ。","romaji":"Majishan wa kankyaku no naka kara joshu o hitori eranda.","german":"The magician picked one assistant from the audience.","furigana":"かんきゃく|なか|じょしゅ|ひとりえら"}],"notes":"助手 is an assistant or helper, e.g. of a professor, doctor or magician. The compound 助手席 \"passenger seat\" is very common. At universities, the rank 助手 has now largely been replaced by 助教. In the office people often say アシスタント too; 助手 sounds somewhat more classical and stresses the helping role beside a main person.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"研究室で毎日遅くまで実験をしています。","romaji":"Kenkyūshitsu de mainichi osoku made jikken o shite imasu.","german":"I do experiments in the lab late every day.","furigana":"けんきゅうしつ|まいにちおそ|じっけん"},{"japanese":"午後、先生の研究室に質問に行きます。","romaji":"Gogo, sensei no kenkyūshitsu ni shitsumon ni ikimasu.","german":"In the afternoon I go to my professor's office with questions.","furigana":"ごご|せんせい|けんきゅうしつ|しつもん|い"},{"japanese":"四年生になったら、どの研究室に入るか決めなければなりません。","romaji":"Yonensei ni nattara, dono kenkyūshitsu ni hairu ka kimenakereba narimasen.","german":"When you become a fourth-year student, you have to decide which research lab to join.","furigana":"よねんせい|けんきゅうしつ|はい|き"}],"notes":"At Japanese universities, 研究室 is not only a laboratory but also a professor's office and the whole research group around them - including in the humanities. Students join a 研究室 in a later year of study (研究室に入る) and write their graduation thesis there. With questions, one goes 先生の研究室に. A pure experiment room is more likely called 実験室 or ラボ.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 38"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"来週までにレポートを提出しなければなりません。","romaji":"Raishū made ni repōto o teishutsu shinakereba narimasen.","german":"I have to hand in a report by next week.","furigana":"らいしゅう|ていしゅつ"},{"japanese":"週末はずっとレポートを書いていました。","romaji":"Shūmatsu wa zutto repōto o kaite imashita.","german":"I was writing a report all weekend.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|か"},{"japanese":"出張のレポートを部長に出しました。","romaji":"Shutchō no repōto o buchō ni dashimashita.","german":"I submitted the business trip report to the department manager.","furigana":"しゅっちょう|ぶちょう|だ"}],"notes":"At university, レポート is above all the written paper you hand in for a course: レポートを書く, レポートを出す or, more formally, レポートを提出する, レポートの締め切り (report deadline). At work it can also be a (shorter) report, e.g. 出張のレポート; very formal business reports are more often 報告書. A scholarly thesis, on the other hand, is 論文, and on television レポート means an on-site report.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 6"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大学入試に向けて毎日勉強しています。","romaji":"Daigaku nyūshi ni mukete mainichi benkyō shite imasu.","german":"I study every day for the university entrance exam.","furigana":"だいがくにゅうし|む|まいにちべんきょう"},{"japanese":"高校入試の結果は来週発表されます。","romaji":"Kōkō nyūshi no kekka wa raishū happyō saremasu.","german":"The results of the high school entrance exam will be announced next week.","furigana":"こうこうにゅうし|けっか|らいしゅうはっぴょう"},{"japanese":"今年の入試問題は去年より難しかったそうだ。","romaji":"Kotoshi no nyūshi mondai wa kyonen yori muzukashikatta sō da.","german":"This year's entrance exam questions were reportedly harder than last year's.","furigana":"ことし|にゅうしもんだい|きょねん|むずか"}],"notes":"入試 is the common short form of 入学試験 \"entrance examination\". Typical are 大学入試, 高校入試, 推薦入試 \"admission by recommendation\" and 入試問題. One passes with 入試に合格する or colloquially 受かる; to fail is 入試に落ちる. Preparing for the exam is expressed with 入試に向けて勉強する or 受験勉強.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"芸術は人々の心を豊かにします。","romaji":"Geijutsu wa hitobito no kokoro o yutaka ni shimasu.","german":"Art enriches people's hearts.","furigana":"げいじゅつ|ひとびと|こころ|ゆた"},{"japanese":"この料理はまるで芸術作品のようだ。","romaji":"Kono ryōri wa marude geijutsu sakuhin no yō da.","german":"This dish is like a work of art.","furigana":"りょうり|げいじゅつさくひん"},{"japanese":"彼は芸術家として生きる道を選んだ。","romaji":"Kare wa geijutsuka toshite ikiru michi o eranda.","german":"He chose to live his life as an artist.","furigana":"かれ|げいじゅつか|い|みち|えら"}],"notes":"芸術 is \"art\" in the broad sense and includes not only painting but also music, theater, literature and film; visual art in the narrower sense is 美術. Common derivatives are 芸術家 \"artist,\" 芸術作品 \"work of art\" and the adjective 芸術的な \"artistic.\" In autumn people like to speak of 芸術の秋. Manual craftsmanship, on the other hand, is more often 技術 or 技.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本文学の授業で夏目漱石の作品を読みました。","romaji":"Nihon bungaku no jugyō de Natsume Sōseki no sakuhin o yomimashita.","german":"In the Japanese literature class we read works by Natsume Soseki.","furigana":"にほんぶんがく|じゅぎょう|なつめそうせき|さくひん|よ"},{"japanese":"大学で英文学を勉強しています。","romaji":"Daigaku de eibungaku o benkyō shite imasu.","german":"I study English literature at university.","furigana":"だいがく|えいぶんがく|べんきょう"},{"japanese":"父は文学が好きで、家に本がたくさんあります。","romaji":"Chichi wa bungaku ga suki de, ie ni hon ga takusan arimasu.","german":"My father loves literature, so we have lots of books at home.","furigana":"ちち|ぶんがく|す|いえ|ほん"}],"notes":"文学 is literature as an art form and as a field of study: 日本文学, 英文学, 外国文学, 文学作品 \"literary work\", 文学部 \"faculty of letters\". A single novel is 小説, a single book 本; 文学 refers to the whole field. Do not confuse it with 文化 \"culture\" or 文法 \"grammar\", which share the same first kanji.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は美しい詩を書くことで知られています。","romaji":"Kanojo wa utsukushii shi o kaku koto de shirarete imasu.","german":"She is known for writing beautiful poems.","furigana":"かのじょ|うつく|し|か|し"},{"japanese":"卒業式で、生徒が自分で書いた詩を読み上げた。","romaji":"Sotsugyōshiki de, seito ga jibun de kaita shi o yomiageta.","german":"At the graduation ceremony, a student read aloud a poem he had written himself.","furigana":"そつぎょうしき|せいと|じぶん|か|し|よ|あ"},{"japanese":"この詩集には春の詩がたくさん入っている。","romaji":"Kono shishū ni wa haru no shi ga takusan haitte iru.","german":"This collection of poems contains many spring poems.","furigana":"ししゅう|はる|し|はい"}],"notes":"詩 is a poem or poetry in general, especially modern or Western verse; traditional Japanese forms have their own names (俳句, 短歌), and Chinese poems are called 漢詩. You say 詩を書く or 詩を作る; the poet is 詩人, a volume of poems 詩集. Song lyrics are not called 詩 but 歌詞.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"学校の文化祭で劇を上演しました。","romaji":"Gakkō no bunkasai de geki o jōen shimashita.","german":"We staged a play at the school festival.","furigana":"がっこう|ぶんかさい|げき|じょうえん"},{"japanese":"子どもたちは人形劇を見て大喜びだった。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa ningyōgeki o mite ōyorokobi datta.","german":"The children were delighted watching the puppet show.","furigana":"こ|にんぎょうげき|み|おおよろこ"},{"japanese":"祖父は毎晩テレビで時代劇を見ている。","romaji":"Sofu wa maiban terebi de jidaigeki o mite iru.","german":"My grandfather watches period dramas on TV every night.","furigana":"そふ|まいばん|じだいげき|み"}],"notes":"劇 is a play or drama, often as a suffix in compounds: 人形劇 \"puppet theater,\" 時代劇 \"samurai drama,\" 劇団 \"theater company.\" You say 劇を上演する or 劇を演じる. 演劇 refers more to theater as an art form; 芝居 is the more colloquial word for a play or stage performance and can also mean \"pretense.\"","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大学で演劇を専攻しています。","romaji":"Daigaku de engeki o senkō shite imasu.","german":"I'm majoring in theater at university.","furigana":"だいがく|えんげき|せんこう"},{"japanese":"高校時代は演劇部に入っていました。","romaji":"Kōkō jidai wa engekibu ni haitte imashita.","german":"In high school, I was in the drama club.","furigana":"こうこうじだい|えんげきぶ|はい"},{"japanese":"週末に友達と演劇を見に行く予定だ。","romaji":"Shūmatsu ni tomodachi to engeki o mi ni iku yotei da.","german":"On the weekend I plan to go see a play with friends.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|ともだち|えんげき|み|い|よてい"}],"notes":"演劇 is theater as an art form and activity: 演劇を見る \"to go to the theater\", 演劇部 \"drama club\", 演劇を専攻する. A single play is rather called 劇 or 芝居, the building is a 劇場. In conversation 演劇 sounds somewhat factual; colloquially one also says 芝居を見に行く.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"俳優たちが舞台の上で熱演しました。","romaji":"Haiyūtachi ga butai no ue de netsuen shimashita.","german":"The actors gave a passionate performance on the stage.","furigana":"はいゆう|ぶたい|うえ|ねつえん"},{"japanese":"この小説の舞台は明治時代の東京です。","romaji":"Kono shōsetsu no butai wa Meiji jidai no Tōkyō desu.","german":"The setting of this novel is Tokyo in the Meiji era.","furigana":"しょうせつ|ぶたい|めいじじだい|とうきょう"},{"japanese":"日本の選手が世界の舞台で活躍している。","romaji":"Nihon no senshu ga sekai no butai de katsuyaku shite iru.","german":"Japanese athletes are doing well on the world stage.","furigana":"にほん|せんしゅ|せかい|ぶたい|かつやく"}],"notes":"舞台 is first of all the stage: 舞台に立つ \"to stand on the stage,\" 舞台裏 \"behind the scenes.\" Figuratively it denotes the setting of a story (物語の舞台は京都だ \"the story is set in Kyōto\") and an area of activity such as 世界の舞台 \"the world stage.\" 舞台 can also mean the performance itself: 舞台を見に行く.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は日本で最も人気のある俳優の一人です。","romaji":"Kare wa Nihon de mottomo ninki no aru haiyū no hitori desu.","german":"He is one of the most popular actors in Japan.","furigana":"かれ|にほん|もっと|にんき|はいゆう|ひとり"},{"japanese":"彼は俳優を目指して東京に出てきた。","romaji":"Kare wa haiyū o mezashite Tōkyō ni dete kita.","german":"He came to Tokyo aiming to become an actor.","furigana":"かれ|はいゆう|めざ|とうきょう|で"},{"japanese":"この映画は俳優の演技がすばらしかった。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa haiyū no engi ga subarashikatta.","german":"The actors' performances in this film were wonderful.","furigana":"えいが|はいゆう|えんぎ"}],"notes":"俳優 is the neutral word for an actor in film, television and theater; you become an actor with 俳優になる or work 俳優として. It is often associated with male actors and contrasted with 女優, but it can include women. 役者 sounds more traditional (e.g. Kabuki) and stresses the craft; a voice actor is 声優.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は若い頃から女優として活躍しています。","romaji":"Kanojo wa wakai koro kara joyū toshite katsuyaku shite imasu.","german":"She has been active as an actress since she was young.","furigana":"かのじょ|わか|ころ|じょゆう|かつやく"},{"japanese":"彼女はこの映画で主演女優賞を取った。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kono eiga de shuen joyūshō o totta.","german":"She won the Best Actress award for this film.","furigana":"かのじょ|えいが|しゅえんじょゆうしょう|と"},{"japanese":"娘は将来女優になりたいと言っている。","romaji":"Musume wa shōrai joyū ni naritai to itte iru.","german":"My daughter says she wants to become an actress in the future.","furigana":"むすめ|しょうらいじょゆう|い"}],"notes":"女優 is an actress; typical combinations are 女優になる, 女優として活躍する and 主演女優 \"leading actress\" (e.g. 主演女優賞). The male counterpart is 男優, but it is rarer because for men one usually just says 俳優. In newer texts the neutral 俳優 is increasingly used for women as well.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女はこのドラマで主役を演じています。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kono dorama de shuyaku o enjite imasu.","german":"She plays the lead role in this drama.","furigana":"かのじょ|しゅやく|えん"},{"japanese":"学芸会で主役に選ばれて、娘はとても喜んでいる。","romaji":"Gakugeikai de shuyaku ni erabarete, musume wa totemo yorokonde iru.","german":"My daughter is delighted to have been chosen for the lead in the school play.","furigana":"がくげいかい|しゅやく|えら|むすめ|よろこ"},{"japanese":"今日の主役は誕生日の田中さんです。","romaji":"Kyō no shuyaku wa tanjōbi no Tanaka-san desu.","german":"Today's guest of honor is Mr. Tanaka, whose birthday it is.","furigana":"きょう|しゅやく|たんじょうび|たなか"}],"notes":"主役 is the leading role or lead actor: 主役を演じる, 主役を務める, 主役に選ばれる; the opposite is 脇役 \"supporting role\". Figuratively it denotes the main person of an occasion: 今日の主役 is, for example, the birthday person. 主人公, in contrast, means the main character within a story, not the actor's role.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"美術館には多くのすばらしい作品が展示されています。","romaji":"Bijutsukan ni wa ōku no subarashii sakuhin ga tenji sarete imasu.","german":"Many wonderful works are on display at the art museum.","furigana":"びじゅつかん|おお|さくひん|てんじ"},{"japanese":"この作家の作品はほとんど読みました。","romaji":"Kono sakka no sakuhin wa hotondo yomimashita.","german":"I have read almost all of this author's works.","furigana":"さっか|さくひん|よ"},{"japanese":"夏休みの宿題で、自由に作品を一つ作ることになった。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi no shukudai de, jiyū ni sakuhin o hitotsu tsukuru koto ni natta.","german":"For our summer vacation homework, we are to freely create one work of our own.","furigana":"なつやす|しゅくだい|じゆう|さくひん|ひと|つく"}],"notes":"作品 is a created work of art, literature, film or music, also by children or amateurs: 作品を発表する \"to publish a work\", 作品を展示する. An artist's best-known work is called 代表作. Industrially manufactured products, by contrast, are called 製品, and the similarly written 作物 (さくもつ) means \"crops\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 49"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この映画は二十世紀の傑作と言われています。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa nijisseiki no kessaku to iwarete imasu.","german":"This film is said to be a masterpiece of the twentieth century.","furigana":"えいが|にじっせいき|けっさく|い"},{"japanese":"これは彼の最高傑作だと思う。","romaji":"Kore wa kare no saikō kessaku da to omou.","german":"I think this is his greatest work.","furigana":"かれ|さいこうけっさく|おも"},{"japanese":"財布を冷蔵庫に入れたなんて、それは傑作だね。","romaji":"Saifu o reizōko ni ireta nante, sore wa kessaku da ne.","german":"You put your wallet in the refrigerator? That's a riot!","furigana":"さいふ|れいぞうこ|い|けっさく"}],"notes":"傑作 is a masterpiece, an outstandingly successful work; 最高傑作 is an artist's best work. 名作, in contrast, means a recognized, famous work, often a classic. Colloquially and jokingly, 傑作 also describes something unintentionally funny: それは傑作だ \"that's priceless\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自由な発想で創作活動に取り組んでいます。","romaji":"Jiyū na hassō de sōsaku katsudō ni torikunde imasu.","german":"I engage in creative activity with free imagination.","furigana":"じゆう|はっそう|そうさくかつどう|と|く"},{"japanese":"駅前に新しい創作料理の店ができた。","romaji":"Ekimae ni atarashii sōsaku ryōri no mise ga dekita.","german":"A new restaurant with creative cuisine opened in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|あたら|そうさくりょうり|みせ"},{"japanese":"その話は事実ではなく、彼の創作だった。","romaji":"Sono hanashi wa jijitsu de wa naku, kare no sōsaku datta.","german":"The story was not true; it was his invention.","furigana":"はなし|じじつ|かれ|そうさく"}],"notes":"創作 is the artistic creation of an original work of one's own (novel, film, music, etc.): 創作活動, 創作する, 創作料理 \"creative (fusion) cuisine\". It can also mean \"invention, fiction\", for instance when a story is not true: それは彼の創作だ. 創造 is more abstract (\"creation, creativity\"), 制作 stresses the practical production of a work.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本人は自然の中に美を見出します。","romaji":"Nihonjin wa shizen no naka ni bi o miidashimasu.","german":"Japanese people find beauty in nature.","furigana":"にほんじん|しぜん|なか|び|"},{"japanese":"この庭は日本的な美を感じさせる。","romaji":"Kono niwa wa Nihonteki na bi o kanjisaseru.","german":"This garden evokes a sense of typically Japanese beauty.","furigana":"にわ|にほんてき|び|かん"},{"japanese":"美の基準は時代によって変わる。","romaji":"Bi no kijun wa jidai ni yotte kawaru.","german":"Standards of beauty change with the times.","furigana":"び|きじゅん|じだい|か"}],"notes":"美 is \"beauty\" as an abstract concept and sounds literary: 美を見出す, 美の基準, 美意識 \"sense of beauty\", 自然美 \"natural beauty\". In everyday speech people tend to say 美しさ or きれいさ instead. As a prefix, 美 forms words such as 美人, 美少年 or 美術.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"来週、現代美術の展覧会が開かれます。","romaji":"Raishū, gendai bijutsu no tenrankai ga hirakaremasu.","german":"An exhibition of contemporary art will be held next week.","furigana":"らいしゅう|げんだいびじゅつ|てんらんかい|ひら"},{"japanese":"友達と美術館の展覧会を見に行きました。","romaji":"Tomodachi to bijutsukan no tenrankai o mi ni ikimashita.","german":"I went to an exhibition at the art museum with a friend.","furigana":"ともだち|びじゅつかん|てんらんかい|み|い"},{"japanese":"娘の絵が学校の展覧会に選ばれました。","romaji":"Musume no e ga gakkō no tenrankai ni erabaremashita.","german":"My daughter's picture was selected for the school exhibition.","furigana":"むすめ|え|がっこう|てんらんかい|えら"}],"notes":"展覧会 is an exhibition of works of art, photographs, pictures and the like: 展覧会が開かれる \"an exhibition takes place\", 展覧会を開く \"to hold an exhibition\", 展覧会を見に行く, 展覧会に出す \"to exhibit a work\". For product or trade fairs one more often says 展示会, and the museum itself is 美術館 or 博物館. On posters it is often abbreviated to 〜展, e.g. ピカソ展.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 31"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"博物館で恐竜の化石を見ることができます。","romaji":"Hakubutsukan de kyōryū no kaseki o miru koto ga dekimasu.","german":"At the museum you can see dinosaur fossils.","furigana":"はくぶつかん|きょうりゅう|かせき|み"},{"japanese":"国立博物館で古い刀の特別展をやっている。","romaji":"Kokuritsu hakubutsukan de furui katana no tokubetsuten o yatte iru.","german":"The national museum is holding a special exhibition of old swords.","furigana":"こくりつはくぶつかん|ふる|かたな|とくべつてん"},{"japanese":"月曜日は博物館の休館日なので気をつけてください。","romaji":"Getsuyōbi wa hakubutsukan no kyūkanbi na node ki o tsukete kudasai.","german":"The museum is closed on Mondays, so please be careful.","furigana":"げつようび|はくぶつかん|きゅうかんび|き"}],"notes":"博物館 is a museum of history, natural history, technology or ethnology: 国立博物館, 科学博物館. An art museum, on the other hand, is 美術館, and smaller collections are also called 資料館 or 記念館. You go to a museum with 博物館に行く and see exhibitions there (展示, 特別展); the closing day is 休館日.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい劇場が駅の近くにオープンしました。","romaji":"Atarashii gekijō ga eki no chikaku ni ōpun shimashita.","german":"A new theater has opened near the station.","furigana":"あたら|げきじょう|えき|ちか"},{"japanese":"このアニメの劇場版が来月公開される。","romaji":"Kono anime no gekijōban ga raigetsu kōkai sareru.","german":"The theatrical version of this anime will be released next month.","furigana":"げきじょうばん|らいげつこうかい"},{"japanese":"劇場の中では携帯電話の電源をお切りください。","romaji":"Gekijō no naka de wa keitai denwa no dengen o o-kiri kudasai.","german":"Please turn off your mobile phones inside the theater.","furigana":"げきじょう|なか|けいたいでんわ|でんげん|き"}],"notes":"劇場 is the theater building or venue for plays, opera or musicals: 劇場で芝居を見る. A cinema, on the other hand, is 映画館, and theater as an art form is 演劇. Very common is 劇場版, the movie version of an anime or TV series. In announcements you hear 劇場内では… for rules of conduct in the hall.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先週、クラシックのコンサートに行きました。","romaji":"Senshū, kurashikku no konsāto ni ikimashita.","german":"Last week I went to a classical concert.","furigana":"せんしゅう|い"},{"japanese":"好きな歌手のコンサートのチケットが取れました。","romaji":"Suki na kashu no konsāto no chiketto ga toremashita.","german":"I managed to get tickets for my favorite singer's concert.","furigana":"す|かしゅ|と"},{"japanese":"学校のコンサートで娘がピアノを弾きました。","romaji":"Gakkō no konsāto de musume ga piano o hikimashita.","german":"My daughter played the piano at the school concert.","furigana":"がっこう|むすめ|ひ"}],"notes":"コンサート is a concert of any kind: コンサートに行く, コンサートがある, コンサートを開く, コンサートのチケット. For pop and rock performances, young people often say ライブ, while 演奏会 sounds more formal and is used mostly for classical music or school performances. The venue is marked with で: ホールでコンサートがある.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 9"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"バレエ団の公演を見に行きませんか。","romaji":"Barēdan no kōen o mi ni ikimasen ka.","german":"Shall we go see a ballet company's performance?","furigana":"だん|こうえん|み|い"},{"japanese":"そのミュージカルは全国二十都市で公演される。","romaji":"Sono myūjikaru wa zenkoku nijū toshi de kōen sareru.","german":"That musical will be performed in twenty cities nationwide.","furigana":"ぜんこくにじゅうとし|こうえん"},{"japanese":"台風のため、今夜の公演は中止になりました。","romaji":"Taifū no tame, kon'ya no kōen wa chūshi ni narimashita.","german":"Because of the typhoon, tonight's performance was canceled.","furigana":"たいふう|こんや|こうえん|ちゅうし"}],"notes":"公演 is a public performance of theater, concert, ballet or musical: 公演を見に行く, 東京公演, 公演中止. As a transitive する-verb it often appears in the passive: 全国で公演される. Not to be confused with the identically read 講演 \"lecture\" and 公園 \"park\". 上演 stresses the staging of a particular play, 公演 the event in front of an audience.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"観客は感動してスタンディングオベーションを送りました。","romaji":"Kankyaku wa kandō shite sutandingu obēshon o okurimashita.","german":"The audience was moved and gave a standing ovation.","furigana":"かんきゃく|かんどう|おく"},{"japanese":"決勝戦には五万人の観客が集まった。","romaji":"Kesshōsen ni wa goman-nin no kankyaku ga atsumatta.","german":"Fifty thousand spectators gathered for the final.","furigana":"けっしょうせん|ごまんにん|かんきゃく|あつ"},{"japanese":"その映画は観客の数が少なくて、すぐに上映が終わってしまった。","romaji":"Sono eiga wa kankyaku no kazu ga sukunakute, sugu ni jōei ga owatte shimatta.","german":"That film had so few viewers that it stopped showing soon after.","furigana":"えいが|かんきゃく|かず|すく|じょうえい|お"}],"notes":"観客 are spectators who watch a performance, film or sports event on site: 観客席 \"spectator seats,\" 観客数, 観客が集まる. TV viewers, on the other hand, are 視聴者, and listeners at lectures or concerts are often 聴衆; 観衆 is a large crowd of spectators, rather literary. Mere onlookers are 見物人 or 野次馬.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この映画は批評家から高い評価を受けました。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa hihyōka kara takai hyōka o ukemashita.","german":"Dieser Film wurde von Kritikern hoch bewertet.","furigana":"えいが|ひひょうか|たか|ひょうか|う"},{"japanese":"友達に自分の小説を読んでもらい、批評してもらった。","romaji":"Tomodachi ni jibun no shōsetsu o yonde morai, hihyō shite moratta.","german":"Ich habe einen Freund meinen Roman lesen und beurteilen lassen.","furigana":"ともだち|じぶん|しょうせつ|よ|ひひょう"},{"japanese":"新聞にその映画の批評が載っていた。","romaji":"Shinbun ni sono eiga no hihyō ga notte ita.","german":"In der Zeitung stand eine Besprechung des Films.","furigana":"しんぶん|えいが|ひひょう|の"}],"notes":"批評 ist eine sachliche Beurteilung oder Besprechung, z. B. eines Films, Buchs oder Kunstwerks, und kann positiv wie negativ ausfallen: 作品を批評する, 映画の批評, 批評家 „Kritiker“. Im Unterschied dazu betont 批判 das Bemängeln und Kritisieren von Fehlern; 評価 ist die (oft numerische) Bewertung. Deshalb entspricht 批評 eher „Rezension, Besprechung“ als dem negativen deutschen „Kritik“.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女の小説は文学賞を受賞しました。","romaji":"Kanojo no shōsetsu wa bungakushō o jushō shimashita.","german":"Her novel won a literary prize.","furigana":"かのじょ|しょうせつ|ぶんがくしょう|じゅしょう"},{"japanese":"作文コンクールで賞をもらった。","romaji":"Sakubun konkūru de shō o moratta.","german":"I got a prize in the essay contest.","furigana":"さくぶん|しょう"},{"japanese":"この映画は三つの部門で賞にノミネートされた。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa mittsu no bumon de shō ni nominēto sareta.","german":"This film was nominated for an award in three categories.","furigana":"えいが|みっ|ぶもん|しょう"}],"notes":"賞 is a prize or award; it often appears as a suffix: ノーベル賞, 文学賞, 一等賞, 新人賞. You receive a prize with 賞を取る, 賞をもらう or, formally, 賞を受ける or 受賞する. The material prize (the object) is 賞品, the prize money 賞金.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ノーベル賞の授賞式で、受賞者がスピーチをしました。","romaji":"Nōberu shō no jushōshiki de, jushōsha ga supīchi o shimashita.","german":"At the Nobel Prize ceremony, the laureates gave speeches.","furigana":"しょう|じゅしょうしき|じゅしょうしゃ"},{"japanese":"彼女の小説が芥川賞を受賞した。","romaji":"Kanojo no shōsetsu ga Akutagawa-shō o jushō shita.","german":"Her novel won the Akutagawa Prize.","furigana":"かのじょ|しょうせつ||じゅしょう"},{"japanese":"受賞の知らせを聞いて、信じられない気持ちでした。","romaji":"Jushō no shirase o kiite, shinjirarenai kimochi deshita.","german":"When I heard the news of the award, I couldn't believe it.","furigana":"じゅしょう|し|き|しん|きも"}],"notes":"受賞 means to receive a prize or be honored: 〜賞を受賞する, 受賞者 \"prize winner\", 受賞作 \"award-winning work\". The award ceremony itself is called 授賞式 with the identically read 授賞 (授ける \"to confer\") - a common writing mistake. So 受賞 is the recipient's perspective, 授賞 that of the one conferring.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子供の頃に何か楽器を習っていましたか。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro ni nani ka gakki o naratte imashita ka.","german":"Did you learn any instrument when you were a child?","furigana":"こども|ころ|なに|がっき|なら"},{"japanese":"彼は楽器なら何でも演奏できる。","romaji":"Kare wa gakki nara nan demo ensō dekiru.","german":"He can play any instrument.","furigana":"かれ|がっき|なん|えんそう"},{"japanese":"駅前の楽器店でギターの弦を買った。","romaji":"Ekimae no gakkiten de gitā no gen o katta.","german":"I bought guitar strings at the music store in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|がっきてん|げん|か"}],"notes":"楽器 is the musical instrument. The matching verb depends on the instrument: 弾く for string and keyboard instruments, 吹く for wind instruments, 叩く for drums; neutral and suitable for all is 演奏する. To learn an instrument is 楽器を習う, a music store 楽器店 or 楽器屋.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 13"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は日本で人気のある歌手です。","romaji":"Kanojo wa Nihon de ninki no aru kashu desu.","german":"She is a popular singer in Japan.","furigana":"かのじょ|にほん|にんき|かしゅ"},{"japanese":"子供の頃の夢は歌手になることでした。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro no yume wa kashu ni naru koto deshita.","german":"My childhood dream was to become a singer.","furigana":"こども|ころ|ゆめ|かしゅ"},{"japanese":"祖母は昔の演歌歌手の曲をよく聞いている。","romaji":"Sobo wa mukashi no enka kashu no kyoku o yoku kiite iru.","german":"My grandmother often listens to songs by old enka singers.","furigana":"そぼ|むかし|えんかかしゅ|きょく|き"}],"notes":"歌手 is a singer, usually professional: 歌手になる, 人気歌手, 演歌歌手, オペラ歌手. The singer of a band is more likely called ボーカル. The word is gender-neutral; someone who simply likes to sing is not a 歌手 but 歌が上手な人 or 歌うのが好きな人.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 11 (source: N5; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"楽譜を見ながらピアノを弾いています。","romaji":"Gakufu o minagara piano o hiite imasu.","german":"I play the piano while looking at the sheet music.","furigana":"がくふ|み|ひ"},{"japanese":"私は楽譜が読めないので、耳で覚えて弾いています。","romaji":"Watashi wa gakufu ga yomenai node, mimi de oboete hiite imasu.","german":"I can't read music, so I learn pieces by ear and play them.","furigana":"わたし|がくふ|よ|みみ|おぼ|ひ"},{"japanese":"この曲の楽譜はインターネットで無料でダウンロードできる。","romaji":"Kono kyoku no gakufu wa intānetto de muryō de daunrōdo dekiru.","german":"The sheet music for this piece can be downloaded for free on the internet.","furigana":"きょく|がくふ|むりょう"}],"notes":"楽譜 is sheet music or a musical score, including the full score of an orchestral piece. Typical are 楽譜を読む \"to read music\", 楽譜を見て弾く and 楽譜なしで弾く \"to play from memory / by ear\". A synonym, especially for the opened sheet itself, is 譜面; a single musical note is 音符.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"リズムに合わせて手を叩きましょう。","romaji":"Rizumu ni awasete te o tatakimashō.","german":"Let's clap in rhythm.","furigana":"あ|て|たた"},{"japanese":"彼はリズム感がいいので、ダンスが上手だ。","romaji":"Kare wa rizumukan ga ii node, dansu ga jōzu da.","german":"He has a good sense of rhythm, so he dances well.","furigana":"かれ|かん|じょうず"},{"japanese":"夜勤が続いて、生活のリズムが崩れてしまった。","romaji":"Yakin ga tsuzuite, seikatsu no rizumu ga kuzurete shimatta.","german":"After many night shifts, my daily rhythm has been thrown off.","furigana":"やきん|つづ|せいかつ|くず"}],"notes":"リズム is rhythm in music, but also in life: リズムに合わせる \"to follow the rhythm\", リズムを取る \"to keep the beat\", リズム感 \"sense of rhythm\", 生活のリズム \"daily rhythm\". When everyday life gets disrupted, one says リズムが崩れる. The tempo of a piece, in contrast, is called テンポ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼はオーケストラでバイオリンを担当しています。","romaji":"Kare wa ōkesutora de baiorin o tantō shite imasu.","german":"He plays the violin in an orchestra.","furigana":"かれ|たんとう"},{"japanese":"市民オーケストラのメンバーを募集しています。","romaji":"Shimin ōkesutora no menbā o boshū shite imasu.","german":"The community orchestra is recruiting members.","furigana":"しみん|ぼしゅう"},{"japanese":"オーケストラの生演奏で映画音楽を聞くコンサートに行った。","romaji":"Ōkesutora no nama ensō de eiga ongaku o kiku konsāto ni itta.","german":"I went to a concert where film music was played live by an orchestra.","furigana":"なまえんそう|えいがおんがく|き|い"}],"notes":"オーケストラ is the orchestra; the formal Sino-Japanese word for it is 管弦楽団, in proper names often 〜交響楽団. One plays in an orchestra with で: オーケストラでバイオリンを弾く. Also typical are 市民オーケストラ \"community orchestra\" and オーケストラの生演奏 \"live music by the orchestra\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"有名な指揮者がコンサートを指揮しました。","romaji":"Yūmei na shikisha ga konsāto o shiki shimashita.","german":"A famous conductor conducted the concert.","furigana":"ゆうめい|しきしゃ|しき"},{"japanese":"指揮者が棒を振ると、演奏が始まった。","romaji":"Shikisha ga bō o furu to, ensō ga hajimatta.","german":"When the conductor raised the baton, the performance began.","furigana":"しきしゃ|ぼう|ふ|えんそう|はじ"},{"japanese":"合唱コンクールで、クラスの指揮者に選ばれた。","romaji":"Gasshō konkūru de, kurasu no shikisha ni erabareta.","german":"For the choral competition, I was chosen as our class's conductor.","furigana":"がっしょう|しきしゃ|えら"}],"notes":"指揮者 is above all the conductor of an orchestra or choir; the related verb is 指揮する (オーケストラを指揮する), and taking charge is 指揮を執る. In Japanese schools, each class chooses a 指揮者 for the choir competition. In a broader sense, 指揮者 can also denote a leader of an operation or troops; militarily, however, 指揮官 is more usual.","levelNote":"Compound: 指揮 (N3) + 者 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"学校の合唱コンクールで金賞を取りました。","romaji":"Gakkō no gasshō konkūru de kinshō o torimashita.","german":"We won the gold prize at the school choral competition.","furigana":"がっこう|がっしょう|きんしょう|と"},{"japanese":"卒業式で全員で校歌を合唱した。","romaji":"Sotsugyōshiki de zen'in de kōka o gasshō shita.","german":"At the graduation ceremony, everyone sang the school song together.","furigana":"そつぎょうしき|ぜんいん|こうか|がっしょう"},{"japanese":"母は地域の合唱団に入っている。","romaji":"Haha wa chiiki no gasshōdan ni haitte iru.","german":"My mother belongs to a local choir.","furigana":"はは|ちいき|がっしょうだん|はい"}],"notes":"合唱 is singing together, i.e. choral singing; as a する-verb: 校歌を合唱する. The group itself is 合唱団 \"choir\", at schools 合唱部 \"choir club\"; the 合唱コンクール is very well known. When someone sings alone, it is called 独唱. Figuratively, 大合唱 refers to a loud chorus of voices, e.g. of frogs or protesters.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ピアニストがバッハの曲を独奏しました。","romaji":"Pianisuto ga Bahha no kyoku o dokusō shimashita.","german":"The pianist played a Bach piece solo.","furigana":"きょく|どくそう"},{"japanese":"コンサートの最後にバイオリンの独奏がありました。","romaji":"Konsāto no saigo ni baiorin no dokusō ga arimashita.","german":"At the end of the concert there was a violin solo.","furigana":"さいご|どくそう"},{"japanese":"来月、初めてのピアノ独奏会を開くことになった。","romaji":"Raigetsu, hajimete no piano dokusōkai o hiraku koto ni natta.","german":"Next month I will give my first piano recital.","furigana":"らいげつ|はじ|どくそうかい|ひら"}],"notes":"独奏 is an instrumental solo; as a transitive する-verb: 曲を独奏する. A solo concert or recital is called 独奏会, e.g. ピアノ独奏会. A vocal solo, in contrast, is 独唱, playing together of several instruments 合奏. In everyday speech one often hears simply ソロ instead of 独奏.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"友達とバンドを組んでライブをしています。","romaji":"Tomodachi to bando o kunde raibu o shite imasu.","german":"I formed a band with friends and we play live.","furigana":"ともだち|く"},{"japanese":"学園祭でバンドの演奏を聞いた。","romaji":"Gakuensai de bando no ensō o kiita.","german":"I listened to a band's performance at the school festival.","furigana":"がくえんさい|えんそう|き"},{"japanese":"腕時計のバンドが切れてしまった。","romaji":"Udedokei no bando ga kirete shimatta.","german":"The band of my wristwatch broke.","furigana":"うでどけい|き"}],"notes":"バンド is above all a music group: バンドを組む \"to form a band\", バンドで演奏する. It also means a band or strap, e.g. 時計のバンド \"watch strap\", ヘアバンド or ゴムバンド \"rubber band\". A trouser belt is nowadays mostly called ベルト, and idol groups without instruments are rather called グループ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"来月、好きなバンドのライブに行きます。","romaji":"Raigetsu, suki na bando no raibu ni ikimasu.","german":"Next month I'm going to a live show by my favorite band.","furigana":"らいげつ|す|い"},{"japanese":"今夜、好きな歌手がライブ配信をするらしい。","romaji":"Kon'ya, suki na kashu ga raibu haishin o suru rashii.","german":"I hear my favorite singer is doing a livestream tonight.","furigana":"こんや|す|かしゅ|はいしん"},{"japanese":"小さなライブハウスで、知らないバンドの演奏を聞いた。","romaji":"Chiisana raibu hausu de, shiranai bando no ensō o kiita.","german":"At a small live music club, I heard a band I didn't know play.","furigana":"ちい|し|えんそう|き"}],"notes":"In modern Japanese, ライブ mainly means a live concert: ライブに行く \"go to a live show\", ライブをする \"give a live show\", ライブハウス \"small live music club\". As a modifier it means \"live\": ライブ配信 \"livestream\", ライブ中継 \"live broadcast\". For large, formal concerts (classical), コンサート or 演奏会 is more usual.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"準優勝でしたがすばらしい試合でした。","romaji":"Jun'yūshō deshita ga subarashii shiai deshita.","german":"It was second place, but it was a wonderful match.","furigana":"じゅんゆうしょう|しあい"},{"japanese":"日本チームは惜しくも準優勝に終わった。","romaji":"Nihon chīmu wa oshiku mo jun'yūshō ni owatta.","german":"The Japanese team narrowly ended up in second place.","furigana":"にほん|お|じゅんゆうしょう|お"},{"japanese":"弟はピアノコンクールで準優勝した。","romaji":"Otōto wa piano konkūru de jun'yūshō shita.","german":"My younger brother took second place in a piano competition.","furigana":"おとうと|じゅんゆうしょう"}],"notes":"準優勝 is second place in a tournament or competition, i.e. \"runner-up\": 準優勝する, 準優勝に終わる \"end up only second\" (often with regret, e.g. with 惜しくも). Do not confuse it with 準決勝 \"semifinal\". The prefix 準 means \"vice-, near-\", and 優勝 is overall victory.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"決勝戦は来週の日曜日に行われます。","romaji":"Kesshōsen wa raishū no nichiyōbi ni okonawaremasu.","german":"The final match will be held next Sunday.","furigana":"けっしょうせん|らいしゅう|にちようび|おこな"},{"japanese":"日本は三大会ぶりに決勝に進んだ。","romaji":"Nihon wa san taikai buri ni kesshō ni susunda.","german":"Japan reached the final for the first time in three tournaments.","furigana":"にほん|さんたいかい|けっしょう|すす"},{"japanese":"後半の終わりに決勝点を決めたのは新人選手だった。","romaji":"Kōhan no owari ni kesshōten o kimeta no wa shinjin senshu datta.","german":"It was a rookie who scored the deciding goal at the end of the second half.","furigana":"こうはん|お|けっしょうてん|き|しんじんせんしゅ"}],"notes":"決勝 is the deciding round, i.e. the final of a competition; most often you hear 決勝戦 \"final match\" and 決勝に進む / 決勝に進出する \"to advance to the final\". Before it come 予選 \"preliminary round\" and 準決勝 \"semifinal\". 決勝点 is the deciding score, 決勝トーナメント the knockout round after the group stage.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"まず予選を突破しなければなりません。","romaji":"Mazu yosen o toppa shinakereba narimasen.","german":"First we have to get through the preliminary round.","furigana":"よせん|とっぱ"},{"japanese":"残念ながら、予選で落ちてしまった。","romaji":"Zannen nagara, yosen de ochite shimatta.","german":"Unfortunately, I was eliminated in the preliminaries.","furigana":"ざんねん|よせん|お"},{"japanese":"書類による予選を通過した十人が、本選に出場できる。","romaji":"Shorui ni yoru yosen o tsūka shita jūnin ga, honsen ni shutsujō dekiru.","german":"The ten people who passed the document-based preliminary selection can enter the main competition.","furigana":"しょるい|よせん|つうか|じゅうにん|ほんせん|しゅつじょう"}],"notes":"予選 is the preliminary round or qualifying stage of a contest: 予選を通過する / 突破する \"get through the preliminaries\", 予選で敗退する or, colloquially, 予選落ちする \"be eliminated in the preliminaries\". After it come 本選 or 決勝. In a wider sense 予選 also means a preliminary selection, e.g. for applications or competitions (書類予選).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"毎朝ジムでトレーニングをしています。","romaji":"Maiasa jimu de torēningu o shite imasu.","german":"I work out at the gym every morning.","furigana":"まいあさ"},{"japanese":"新入社員はまず一か月のトレーニングを受ける。","romaji":"Shinnyū shain wa mazu ikkagetsu no torēningu o ukeru.","german":"New employees first undergo a month of training.","furigana":"しんにゅうしゃいん|いっ|げつ|う"},{"japanese":"マラソンに向けて、毎日トレーニングしている。","romaji":"Marason ni mukete, mainichi torēningu shite iru.","german":"I train every day for the marathon.","furigana":"む|まいにち"}],"notes":"トレーニング is training, above all physical (筋肉トレーニング, short 筋トレ), but also job training; as an intransitive する-verb: ジムでトレーニングする. トレーニングを受ける is used for a training program. 練習, by contrast, is practicing a specific skill (piano, serve), and 研修 is in-house professional training.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"オリンピックには多くの競技があります。","romaji":"Orinpikku ni wa ōku no kyōgi ga arimasu.","german":"There are many events at the Olympics.","furigana":"おお|きょうぎ"},{"japanese":"彼女は陸上競技の選手として大学に入った。","romaji":"Kanojo wa rikujō kyōgi no senshu toshite daigaku ni haitta.","german":"She entered university as a track and field athlete.","furigana":"かのじょ|りくじょうきょうぎ|せんしゅ|だいがく|はい"},{"japanese":"新しい競技場は駅から歩いて十分のところにある。","romaji":"Atarashii kyōgijō wa eki kara aruite juppun no tokoro ni aru.","german":"The new stadium is ten minutes' walk from the station.","furigana":"あたら|きょうぎじょう|えき|ある|じゅっぷん"}],"notes":"競技 is a sports competition or a sport in which people compete against each other: 陸上競技 \"track and field,\" 競技場 \"stadium, competition venue,\" 競技に出る / 参加する. The individual event within a competition is more precisely 種目, a single match 試合. As a する-verb, 競技する is rare.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"チーム全員で力を合わせて戦いました。","romaji":"Chīmu zen'in de chikara o awasete tatakaimashita.","german":"The whole team fought together.","furigana":"ぜんいん|ちから|あ|たたか"},{"japanese":"新しいプロジェクトのためにチームを作った。","romaji":"Atarashii purojekuto no tame ni chīmu o tsukutta.","german":"We formed a team for the new project.","furigana":"あたら|つく"},{"japanese":"どのチームを応援していますか。","romaji":"Dono chīmu o ōen shite imasu ka.","german":"Which team do you support?","furigana":"おうえん"}],"notes":"チーム is both the sports team and the team at work: チームを組む / 作る, チームに入る, チームワーク. For groups at school or in government offices one often says 班 or 組, for school clubs 部. To cheer on is チームを応援する.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 32"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"来週、強豪チームとの対戦があります。","romaji":"Raishū, kyōgō chīmu to no taisen ga arimasu.","german":"Next week we have a match against a strong team.","furigana":"らいしゅう|きょうごう|たいせん"},{"japanese":"一回戦の対戦相手が決まった。","romaji":"Ikkaisen no taisen aite ga kimatta.","german":"The opponent for the first round has been decided.","furigana":"いっかいせん|たいせんあいて|き"},{"japanese":"友達とオンラインでゲームの対戦をした。","romaji":"Tomodachi to onrain de gēmu no taisen o shita.","german":"I played a head-to-head game online with friends.","furigana":"ともだち|たいせん"}],"notes":"対戦 means facing each other in a game or contest: 〜と対戦する \"play against ...\", 対戦相手 \"opponent\", 対戦成績 \"head-to-head record\". 対戦 is also common for video games (オンライン対戦). 試合 is the game as an event, and 対決 is an especially tense showdown.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"チームは接戦の末に勝利を収めました。","romaji":"Chīmu wa sessen no sue ni shōri o osamemashita.","german":"The team won after a close game.","furigana":"せっせん|すえ|しょうり|おさ"},{"japanese":"彼女の最後のシュートがチームを勝利に導いた。","romaji":"Kanojo no saigo no shūto ga chīmu o shōri ni michibiita.","german":"Her last shot led the team to victory.","furigana":"かのじょ|さいご|しょうり|みちび"},{"japanese":"選挙で勝利した候補者が、支持者にお礼を述べた。","romaji":"Senkyo de shōri shita kōhosha ga, shijisha ni orei o nobeta.","german":"The candidate who won the election thanked his supporters.","furigana":"せんきょ|しょうり|こうほしゃ|しじしゃ|れい|の"}],"notes":"勝利 is victory and sounds more formal than 勝ち; typical of news and speeches: 勝利を収める \"to achieve victory\", 勝利する, 〜を勝利に導く \"to lead to victory\", 逆転勝利 \"comeback victory\". In everyday speech one simply says 勝つ or 勝った. The opposite is 敗北.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"敗北から学ぶことも大切です。","romaji":"Haiboku kara manabu koto mo taisetsu desu.","german":"It is also important to learn from defeat.","furigana":"はいぼく|まな|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"彼は記者会見で敗北を認めた。","romaji":"Kare wa kisha kaiken de haiboku o mitometa.","german":"He admitted defeat at the press conference.","furigana":"かれ|きしゃかいけん|はいぼく|みと"},{"japanese":"三対〇の敗北に、選手たちは言葉もなかった。","romaji":"San tai zero no haiboku ni, senshutachi wa kotoba mo nakatta.","german":"After the 3-0 defeat, the players were speechless.","furigana":"|はいぼく|せんしゅ|ことば"}],"notes":"敗北 is defeat and sounds more formal than 負け: 敗北する, 敗北を認める \"to admit defeat\", in written language 敗北を喫する \"to suffer a defeat\", 敗北感 \"feeling of defeat\". In everyday speech people mostly say 負ける or 負けた. The opposite is 勝利.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"反則を取られてペナルティーキックになりました。","romaji":"Hansoku o torarete penarutī kikku ni narimashita.","german":"A foul was called and it became a penalty kick.","furigana":"はんそく|と"},{"japanese":"ボクシングの試合で、相手は反則負けになった。","romaji":"Bokushingu no shiai de, aite wa hansokumake ni natta.","german":"In the boxing match, the opponent lost by disqualification.","furigana":"しあい|あいて|はんそくま"},{"japanese":"スピード違反で反則金を払わなければならなかった。","romaji":"Supīdo ihan de hansokukin o harawanakereba naranakatta.","german":"I had to pay a fine for speeding.","furigana":"いはん|はんそくきん|はら"}],"notes":"反則 is a violation of the rules, especially in sports: 反則を取られる \"have a foul called against you\", 反則負け \"loss by disqualification\". As an intransitive する-verb: 反則する. Outside sports it also refers to minor violations of regulations, e.g. in road traffic (反則金 \"fine\"). Colloquially, people exclaim 反則だ when something is \"unfairly\" good: その笑顔は反則だ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"メディアは社会に大きな影響を与えます。","romaji":"Media wa shakai ni ōkina eikyō o ataemasu.","german":"The media have a great influence on society.","furigana":"しゃかい|おお|えいきょう|あた"},{"japanese":"その事件は多くのメディアで取り上げられた。","romaji":"Sono jiken wa ōku no media de toriagerareta.","german":"The incident was covered by many media outlets.","furigana":"じけん|おお|と|あ"},{"japanese":"写真はこの記録メディアに保存してください。","romaji":"Shashin wa kono kiroku media ni hozon shite kudasai.","german":"Please save the photos on this storage medium.","furigana":"しゃしん|きろく|ほぞん"}],"notes":"メディア refers mainly to the mass media (television, newspapers, the internet): メディアで取り上げられる \"to be covered in the media\", メディアに出る \"to appear in the media\", ソーシャルメディア \"social media\". In everyday speech the press is often also called マスコミ, which puts more emphasis on journalists and broadcasters as actors (マスコミに追われる). Technically, メディア also stands for storage media such as SD cards or DVDs (記録メディア).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"そのニュースは昨夜の放送で報じられました。","romaji":"Sono nyūsu wa sakuya no hōsō de hōjiraremashita.","german":"That news was reported in last night's broadcast.","furigana":"さくや|ほうそう|ほう"},{"japanese":"駅の放送がよく聞こえませんでした。","romaji":"Eki no hōsō ga yoku kikoemasen deshita.","german":"I couldn't hear the announcement at the station well.","furigana":"えき|ほうそう|き"},{"japanese":"サッカーの試合はテレビで生放送されます。","romaji":"Sakkā no shiai wa terebi de namahōsō saremasu.","german":"The soccer match will be broadcast live on TV.","furigana":"しあい|なまほうそう"}],"notes":"放送 is broadcasting or transmission via television and radio, but also any loudspeaker announcement: 駅の放送, 校内放送 (school announcement), 館内放送 (announcement inside a building). As a する-verb it is transitive: 試合を放送する, often in the passive 放送される; 生放送 means \"live broadcast\". A single broadcast as a program item (\"last night's show\") is usually 番組.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 21"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"通勤中にラジオのニュースを聞いています。","romaji":"Tsūkinchū ni rajio no nyūsu o kiite imasu.","german":"I listen to the news on the radio during my commute.","furigana":"つうきんちゅう|き"},{"japanese":"ラジオをつけてください。","romaji":"Rajio o tsukete kudasai.","german":"Please turn on the radio."}],"notes":"ラジオ means the radio, or the medium. ラジオのニュース assigns the news to this medium. ラジオをつける means to turn on the radio. 通勤中に gives the time span during the commute. ラジオ has three short moras and is romanized rajio.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"記者が事件の現場で取材を行いました。","romaji":"Kisha ga jiken no genba de shuzai o okonaimashita.","german":"The reporter gathered information at the scene of the incident.","furigana":"きしゃ|じけん|げんば|しゅざい|おこな"},{"japanese":"テレビ局から取材を受けて、店が有名になった。","romaji":"Terebikyoku kara shuzai o ukete, mise ga yūmei ni natta.","german":"After a TV station covered us, the shop became famous.","furigana":"きょく|しゅざい|う|みせ|ゆうめい"},{"japanese":"作家は小説を書くために、小さな港町を取材した。","romaji":"Sakka wa shōsetsu o kaku tame ni, chiisana minatomachi o shuzai shita.","german":"To write her novel, the author researched a small port town.","furigana":"さっか|しょうせつ|か|ちい|みなとまち|しゅざい"}],"notes":"取材 means collecting information on the spot for an article, a program or a book: \"to research, report, conduct interviews\". Typical: 〜を取材する (an event), 〜に取材する (to interview a person), 取材を受ける \"to be interviewed\", 取材に応じる \"to agree to be interviewed\". Unlike 調査 (\"investigation, survey\"), 取材 is always tied to reporting or writing work.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"記者会見で大臣が質問に答えました。","romaji":"Kisha kaiken de daijin ga shitsumon ni kotaemashita.","german":"The minister answered questions at the press conference.","furigana":"きしゃかいけん|だいじん|しつもん|こた"},{"japanese":"父は新聞記者として三十年働いた。","romaji":"Chichi wa shinbun kisha toshite sanjūnen hataraita.","german":"My father worked as a newspaper reporter for thirty years.","furigana":"ちち|しんぶんきしゃ|さんじゅうねんはたら"},{"japanese":"事故現場には大勢の記者が集まっていた。","romaji":"Jiko genba ni wa ōzei no kisha ga atsumatte ita.","german":"A large number of reporters had gathered at the scene of the accident.","furigana":"じこげんば|おおぜい|きしゃ|あつ"}],"notes":"記者 is a journalist or reporter who researches and writes news, especially for newspapers and news agencies: 新聞記者, 記者会見 \"press conference,\" 記者クラブ \"press club.\" For a TV reporter in front of the camera people often say レポーター, for freelance writers ジャーナリスト. As a job description: 新聞社で記者をしている.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"雑誌の編集者として十年働いています。","romaji":"Zasshi no henshūsha toshite jūnen hataraite imasu.","german":"I have worked for ten years as a magazine editor.","furigana":"ざっし|へんしゅうしゃ|じゅうねんはたら"},{"japanese":"旅行の動画を編集してネットにアップした。","romaji":"Ryokō no dōga o henshū shite netto ni appu shita.","german":"I edited my travel video and uploaded it online.","furigana":"りょこう|どうが|へんしゅう"},{"japanese":"このファイルは読み取り専用なので編集できません。","romaji":"Kono fairu wa yomitori sen'yō na node henshū dekimasen.","german":"This file is read-only, so it can't be edited.","furigana":"よ|と|せんよう|へんしゅう"}],"notes":"編集 is the editing and compiling of texts, books and magazines, today also of videos and files: 原稿を編集する, 動画を編集する \"edit a video\". Related words are 編集者 \"editor\", 編集部 \"editorial department\" and 編集長 \"editor-in-chief\". Unlike 修正 (\"to correct\"), 編集 also covers the selection, arrangement and structure of the material. As a する-verb it is transitive.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい本の出版が来月に予定されています。","romaji":"Atarashii hon no shuppan ga raigetsu ni yotei sarete imasu.","german":"The publication of the new book is scheduled for next month.","furigana":"あたら|ほん|しゅっぱん|らいげつ|よてい"},{"japanese":"彼女は大学を出てから出版社に勤めている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa daigaku o dete kara shuppansha ni tsutomete iru.","german":"She has worked at a publishing company since graduating from university.","furigana":"かのじょ|だいがく|で|しゅっぱんしゃ|つと"},{"japanese":"祖父は自分の体験をまとめた本を自費で出版した。","romaji":"Sofu wa jibun no taiken o matometa hon o jihi de shuppan shita.","german":"My grandfather published a book summarizing his experiences at his own expense.","furigana":"そふ|じぶん|たいけん|ほん|じひ|しゅっぱん"}],"notes":"出版 is the publication of a book or magazine: 本を出版する, 〜から出版される \"to be published by ...,\" 出版社 \"publisher.\" In everyday speech, \"to bring out a book\" is often simply 本を出す. 発表, by contrast, is generally \"to announce, present\" (research results, plans), and 発行 stresses issuing (newspapers, ID cards, receipts).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの俳優が出ているCMが話題になっています。","romaji":"Ano haiyū ga dete iru shīemu ga wadai ni natte imasu.","german":"The commercial featuring that actor is the talk of the town.","furigana":"はいゆう|で|わだい"},{"japanese":"試合の結果はCMの後で発表されます。","romaji":"Shiai no kekka wa shīemu no ato de happyō saremasu.","german":"The result of the match will be announced after the commercial.","furigana":"しあい|けっか|あと|はっぴょう"},{"japanese":"この会社は新しい商品のCMに有名な歌手を使った。","romaji":"Kono kaisha wa atarashii shōhin no shīemu ni yūmei na kashu o tsukatta.","german":"The company used a famous singer in the commercial for its new product.","furigana":"かいしゃ|あたら|しょうひん|ゆうめい|かしゅ|つか"}],"notes":"CM (pronounced シーエム) is the usual word for a TV or radio commercial, abbreviated from \"commercial message\". Typical uses: CMに出る \"appear in a commercial\", CMが流れる \"a commercial is aired\", CMの後で \"after the commercial break\". The spelled-out form is コマーシャル; for advertising in general (posters, print ads) one says 広告. In English, \"CM\" is not used in this sense; it is a typically Japanese abbreviation.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"毎朝テレビでニュースを見ています。","romaji":"Maiasa terebi de nyūsu o mite imasu.","german":"I watch the news on TV every morning.","furigana":"まいあさ|み"},{"japanese":"このニュースを知っていますか。","romaji":"Kono nyūsu o shitte imasu ka.","german":"Do you know this news?","furigana":"し"}],"notes":"ニュース is a noun for news. テレビでニュースを見る names television as the medium. ニュースを知っていますか asks whether the content is already known. The small ュ in ニュー combines with ニ into one unit; the dash makes the u long: nyūsu.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 21"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"政府の対応に対する批判が高まっています。","romaji":"Seifu no taiō ni tai suru hihan ga takamatte imasu.","german":"Criticism of the government's response is growing.","furigana":"せいふ|たいおう|たい|ひはん|たか"},{"japanese":"社長の発言はSNSで厳しく批判された。","romaji":"Shachō no hatsugen wa esu-enu-esu de kibishiku hihan sareta.","german":"The president's remarks were harshly criticized on social media.","furigana":"しゃちょう|はつげん|きび|ひはん"},{"japanese":"他人を批判する前に、自分の行動を見直したほうがいい。","romaji":"Tanin o hihan suru mae ni, jibun no kōdō o minaoshita hō ga ii.","german":"Before criticizing others, you should reconsider your own behavior.","furigana":"たにん|ひはん|まえ|じぶん|こうどう|みなお"}],"notes":"批判 is critical judgment, mostly in the sense of criticism of politics, people or decisions: 〜を批判する, 〜に対する批判, 批判を受ける / 批判を浴びる \"to take criticism,\" 批判的な \"critical.\" 非難 (\"reproach, blame\") is stronger and morally condemning; 批評, by contrast, is the expert review of art, films or literature. Colloquial grumbling is more often 文句を言う.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"植物は根から水分を吸収する。","romaji":"Shokubutsu wa ne kara suibun wo kyūshū suru.","german":"Plants absorb water through their roots.","furigana":"しょくぶつ|ね|すいぶん|きゅうしゅう"},{"japanese":"子どもは新しい知識をどんどん吸収する。","romaji":"Kodomo wa atarashii chishiki o dondon kyuushuu suru.","german":"Children absorb new knowledge rapidly.","furigana":"こ|あたら|ちしき|きゅうしゅう"},{"japanese":"その小さな会社は大手企業に吸収された。","romaji":"Sono chiisana kaisha wa oote kigyou ni kyuushuu sareta.","german":"That small company was absorbed by a large corporation.","furigana":"ちい|かいしゃ|おおてきぎょう|きゅうしゅう"}],"notes":"吸収する means \"to take in, absorb\" and is transitive: 水分を吸収する, 衝撃を吸収する \"to absorb shocks.\" Figuratively one takes in knowledge or culture (知識を吸収する), and in business a company is taken over by another (〜に吸収される, 吸収合併). Compared with 吸う (\"to inhale, suck\"), 吸収 is factual and rather literary.","levelNote":"Derived from 吸収 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"工場は大量の二酸化炭素を排出している。","romaji":"Kōjō wa tairyō no nisankatanso wo haishutsu shite iru.","german":"The factory emits large amounts of carbon dioxide.","furigana":"こうじょう|たいりょう|にさんかたんそ|はいしゅつ"},{"japanese":"体には不要になった物を外に排出する仕組みがある。","romaji":"Karada ni wa fuyou ni natta mono o soto ni haishutsu suru shikumi ga aru.","german":"The body has a mechanism for expelling what it no longer needs.","furigana":"からだ|ふよう|もの|そと|はいしゅつ|しく"},{"japanese":"この市では家庭から排出されるごみの量が減っている。","romaji":"Kono shi de wa katei kara haishutsu sareru gomi no ryou ga hette iru.","german":"In this city, the amount of garbage produced by households is decreasing.","furigana":"し|かてい|はいしゅつ|りょう|へ"}],"notes":"排出する means \"to emit, discharge, excrete\" - of exhaust gases, pollutants and waste (二酸化炭素を排出する, 排出量 \"amount of emissions\") as well as of metabolic products in the body (体外に排出する). It is transitive but very often appears in the passive (排出される) and belongs to the factual vocabulary of environment and medicine. Do not confuse it with the identically read 輩出, which means \"to produce (capable people)\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"毎日十分なビタミンを摂取することが大切だ。","romaji":"Mainichi jūbun na bitamin wo sesshu suru koto ga taisetsu da.","german":"It is important to take in enough vitamins every day.","furigana":"まいにちじゅうぶん|せっしゅ|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"医者に塩分の摂取を控えるように言われた。","romaji":"Isha ni enbun no sesshu o hikaeru you ni iwareta.","german":"The doctor told me to cut down on my salt intake.","furigana":"いしゃ|えんぶん|せっしゅ|ひか|い"},{"japanese":"カフェインを摂取しすぎると、夜眠れなくなる。","romaji":"Kafein o sesshu shisugiru to, yoru nemurenaku naru.","german":"If you consume too much caffeine, you won't be able to sleep at night.","furigana":"せっしゅ|よるねむ"}],"notes":"摂取する means \"to take in (nutrients), to consume\" and sounds medical and matter-of-fact; typical with nutrients and amounts: ビタミンを摂取する, 塩分の摂取量 \"salt intake\", 過剰摂取 \"overdose, excessive consumption\". In daily life one simply says 食べる, 飲む or 栄養をとる. Figuratively it can also mean \"to absorb a foreign culture or knowledge\" (外国の文化を摂取する), but this is literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"血液は体内を循環している。","romaji":"Ketsueki wa tainai wo junkan shite iru.","german":"Blood circulates through the body.","furigana":"けつえき|たいない|じゅんかん"},{"japanese":"お金が社会の中でうまく循環すれば、経済は良くなる。","romaji":"Okane ga shakai no naka de umaku junkan sureba, keizai wa yoku naru.","german":"If money circulates well within society, the economy improves.","furigana":"かね|しゃかい|なか|じゅんかん|けいざい|よ"},{"japanese":"窓を開けて部屋の空気を循環させましょう。","romaji":"Mado o akete heya no kuuki o junkan sasemashou.","german":"Let's open the window and let the air in the room circulate.","furigana":"まど|あ|へや|くうき|じゅんかん"}],"notes":"循環する means \"to circulate, to go around in a cycle\" and is intransitive: 血液が循環する; for \"to circulate (something)\" use 循環させる. It is common in compounds: 血液循環 \"blood circulation\", 悪循環 \"vicious circle\", 循環バス \"loop bus line\". 回転, by contrast, is rotation around an axis; 循環 means going around through a system and returning to the starting point.","levelNote":"Derived from 循環 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この地域に電力を供給する計画が進んでいる。","romaji":"Kono chiiki ni denryoku wo kyōkyū suru keikaku ga susunde iru.","german":"Plans to supply electricity to this region are moving forward.","furigana":"ちいき|でんりょく|きょうきゅう|けいかく|すす"},{"japanese":"台風の影響で、一部の地域では水の供給が止まっている。","romaji":"Taifuu no eikyou de, ichibu no chiiki de wa mizu no kyoukyuu ga tomatte iru.","german":"Because of the typhoon, the water supply has stopped in some areas.","furigana":"たいふう|えいきょう|いちぶ|ちいき|みず|きょうきゅう|と"},{"japanese":"この工場は自動車メーカーに部品を供給している。","romaji":"Kono koujou wa jidousha meekaa ni buhin o kyoukyuu shite iru.","german":"This factory supplies parts to an automobile manufacturer.","furigana":"こうじょう|じどうしゃ|ぶひん|きょうきゅう"}],"notes":"供給する means \"to supply, provide, make available\" and is transitive, often in the pattern AにBを供給する: 地域に電力を供給する. The opposite is 需要 (\"demand\"), hence 需要と供給 \"supply and demand.\" It is used for energy, water, raw materials or goods on a large scale; the delivery of a single package is 届ける or 配達する.","levelNote":"Derived from 供給 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本は大量のエネルギーを消費している。","romaji":"Nihon wa tairyō no enerugī wo shōhi shite iru.","german":"Japan consumes a large amount of energy.","furigana":"にほん|たいりょう|しょうひ"},{"japanese":"三十分走ると、約三百キロカロリーを消費する。","romaji":"Sanjuppun hashiru to, yaku sanbyaku kirokarorii o shouhi suru.","german":"If you run for thirty minutes, you burn about three hundred kilocalories.","furigana":"さんじゅっぷんはし|やくさんびゃく|しょうひ"},{"japanese":"この牛乳は消費期限が今日までだ。","romaji":"Kono gyuunyuu wa shouhi kigen ga kyou made da.","german":"This milk has a use-by date of today.","furigana":"ぎゅうにゅう|しょうひきげん|きょう"}],"notes":"消費する means \"to use up, consume\" - energy, time, goods or calories: 電力を消費する, カロリーを消費する. Often in compounds: 消費者 \"consumer,\" 消費税 \"consumption tax,\" 消費期限 \"use-by date\" (as opposed to 賞味期限 \"best-before date\"). The word is value-neutral, while 浪費 stresses pointless wasting; the counterpart is 生産 (\"production\").","levelNote":"Derived from 消費 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"時間を浪費してはいけない。","romaji":"Jikan wo rōhi shite wa ikenai.","german":"You must not waste time.","furigana":"じかん|ろうひ"},{"japanese":"彼はギャンブルで給料を浪費してしまう。","romaji":"Kare wa gyanburu de kyuuryou o rouhi shite shimau.","german":"He always squanders his salary on gambling.","furigana":"かれ|きゅうりょう|ろうひ"},{"japanese":"こんな会議は時間の浪費だと思う。","romaji":"Konna kaigi wa jikan no rouhi da to omou.","german":"I think a meeting like this is a waste of time.","furigana":"かいぎ|じかん|ろうひ|おも"}],"notes":"浪費する means \"to waste, squander\" and criticizes money, time or energy being spent pointlessly: お金を浪費する, 時間の浪費 \"waste of time\", 浪費家 \"spendthrift\". 無駄遣い is close in meaning but more everyday (especially for money: 無駄遣いする); 消費, by contrast, is the neutral \"to consume\". Opposite: 節約 (\"saving, economizing\").","levelNote":"Derived from 浪費 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"将来のためにお金を蓄える。","romaji":"Shōrai no tame ni okane wo takuwaeru.","german":"I save money for the future.","furigana":"しょうらい|かね|たくわ"},{"japanese":"冬に備えて、リスは木の実を蓄える。","romaji":"Fuyu ni sonaete, risu wa ki no mi o takuwaeru.","german":"Squirrels store nuts in preparation for winter.","furigana":"ふゆ|そな|き|み|たくわ"},{"japanese":"祖父は白いひげを蓄えている。","romaji":"Sofu wa shiroi hige o takuwaete iru.","german":"My grandfather wears a white beard.","furigana":"そふ|しろ|たくわ"}],"notes":"蓄える means \"to accumulate and keep for later\": お金を蓄える \"put money aside\", 食料を蓄える \"lay in provisions\", 力を蓄える \"gather strength\". One can also 蓄える knowledge and experience (知識を蓄える), and ひげを蓄える means \"to grow a beard, to wear a beard\". For money, 貯める is more common in daily life; 蓄える sounds somewhat more elevated. The spelling 貯える is also in use; the noun 蓄え means \"savings, stock\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この会社は電子部品を製造している。","romaji":"Kono kaisha wa denshi buhin wo seizō shite iru.","german":"This company manufactures electronic components.","furigana":"かいしゃ|でんしぶひん|せいぞう"},{"japanese":"この薬は国内の工場で製造されています。","romaji":"Kono kusuri wa kokunai no koujou de seizou sarete imasu.","german":"This medicine is manufactured in a domestic factory.","furigana":"くすり|こくない|こうじょう|せいぞう"},{"japanese":"パッケージに製造年月日が書いてある。","romaji":"Pakkeeji ni seizou nengappi ga kaite aru.","german":"The date of manufacture is written on the package.","furigana":"せいぞうねんがっぴ|か"}],"notes":"製造する means \"to manufacture, make\" and refers to industrial production: 部品を製造する, 製造業 \"manufacturing industry,\" 製造元 \"manufacturer,\" 製造年月日 \"date of manufacture.\" 生産 stresses production quantities (also in agriculture), and 作る is the everyday word. It is transitive; in the passive 〜で製造される means \"is manufactured in ...\".","levelNote":"Derived from 製造 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"原材料を加工して製品を作る。","romaji":"Genzairyō wo kakō shite seihin wo tsukuru.","german":"Raw materials are processed to make products.","furigana":"げんざいりょう|かこう|せいひん|つく"},{"japanese":"アプリで写真を加工してからSNSに載せた。","romaji":"Apuri de shashin o kakou shite kara esu-enu-esu ni noseta.","german":"I edited the photo with an app and then posted it on social media.","furigana":"しゃしん|かこう|の"},{"japanese":"ソーセージやハムは肉を加工して作られる。","romaji":"Sooseeji ya hamu wa niku o kakou shite tsukurareru.","german":"Sausages and ham are made by processing meat.","furigana":"にく|かこう|つく"}],"notes":"加工する means \"to process, work on\": raw materials or foods are given a new form through processing: 木材を加工する, 加工食品 \"processed food\", 加工品. Today it is also very common for image editing: 写真を加工する \"to edit, retouch a photo\". Transitive. Do not confuse it with the identically read 下降 (\"descent, decline\").","levelNote":"Derived from 加工 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい技術を使って製品を改良した。","romaji":"Atarashii gijutsu wo tsukatte seihin wo kairyō shita.","german":"We improved the product using new technology.","furigana":"あたら|ぎじゅつ|つか|せいひん|かいりょう"},{"japanese":"このイチゴは品種改良によって甘くなった。","romaji":"Kono ichigo wa hinshu kairyou ni yotte amaku natta.","german":"These strawberries have become sweeter through selective breeding.","furigana":"ひんしゅかいりょう|あま"},{"japanese":"エンジンを改良して、燃費が二割よくなった。","romaji":"Enjin o kairyou shite, nenpi ga niwari yoku natta.","german":"Thanks to the improved engine, fuel efficiency is 20 percent better.","furigana":"かいりょう|ねんぴ|にわり"}],"notes":"改良する means \"to improve, refine\" - above all products, technology, varieties or methods whose quality is raised by modifying them: 製品を改良する, 品種改良 \"breeding of new varieties\", 改良型 \"improved version\". 改善, by contrast, remedies bad conditions or problems (working conditions, health); with 改良 something concrete that already works is made even better. Transitive.","levelNote":"Derived from 改良 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"労働環境を改善する必要がある。","romaji":"Rōdō kankyō wo kaizen suru hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"The working environment needs to be improved.","furigana":"ろうどうかんきょう|かいぜん|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"薬を飲み始めてから、症状がだいぶ改善した。","romaji":"Kusuri o nomihajimete kara, shoujou ga daibu kaizen shita.","german":"My symptoms have improved considerably since I started taking the medicine.","furigana":"くすり|の|はじ|しょうじょう|かいぜん"},{"japanese":"お客様の意見をもとに、サービスを改善していきます。","romaji":"Okyakusama no iken o moto ni, saabisu o kaizen shite ikimasu.","german":"We will improve our service based on our customers' opinions.","furigana":"きゃくさま|いけん|かいぜん"}],"notes":"改善する means \"to improve, remedy problems\": 環境を改善する, 生活習慣を改善する. It is used transitively (〜を改善する) and also intransitively (症状が改善する \"the symptoms improve\"). Compared with 改良 (developing products or technology further), 改善 targets bad conditions, processes or relationships; 改善点 are \"points that should be improved.\" As \"kaizen\" it is also known as a philosophy of continuous improvement.","levelNote":"Derived from 改善 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"セキュリティ対策を強化する。","romaji":"Sekyuriti taisaku o kyōka suru.","german":"We will strengthen security measures.","furigana":"たいさく|きょうか"},{"japanese":"大会に向けて、チームの守備を強化する必要がある。","romaji":"Taikai ni mukete, chīmu no shubi o kyōka suru hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"Ahead of the tournament, we need to strengthen the team's defense.","furigana":"たいかい|む|しゅび|きょうか|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"年末は警察が飲酒運転の取り締まりを強化している。","romaji":"Nenmatsu wa keisatsu ga inshu unten no torishimari o kyōka shite iru.","german":"At the end of the year, the police step up their crackdown on drunk driving.","furigana":"ねんまつ|けいさつ|いんしゅうんてん|と|し|きょうか"}],"notes":"強化する means \"to strengthen, to reinforce, to tighten\" and is transitive: 対策を強化する, 守備を強化する, 規制を強化する \"tighten regulations\" (opposite: 規制を緩和する). It sounds factual to formal and is typical of news, companies and sports; in daily life one says 強くする or 強める instead. The passive form 強化される describes something being strengthened. Common compounds: 強化合宿 \"training camp\", 強化ガラス \"tempered glass\".","levelNote":"Derived from 強化 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"高齢者の人口が増加している。","romaji":"Kōreisha no jinkō ga zōka shite iru.","german":"The elderly population is increasing.","furigana":"こうれいしゃ|じんこう|ぞうか"},{"japanese":"外国人観光客の数が前年より三割増加した。","romaji":"Gaikokujin kankoukyaku no kazu ga zennen yori sanwari zouka shita.","german":"The number of foreign tourists increased by thirty percent over the previous year.","furigana":"がいこくじんかんこうきゃく|かず|ぜんねん|さんわりぞうか"},{"japanese":"ストレスによる病気の増加が問題になっている。","romaji":"Sutoresu ni yoru byouki no zouka ga mondai ni natte iru.","german":"The increase in stress-related illnesses has become a problem.","furigana":"びょうき|ぞうか|もんだい"}],"notes":"増加する means \"to increase, rise\" (number, quantity) and is intransitive: 人口が増加する, 〜の増加 \"increase in ...\". It is the formal, statistical counterpart of 増える and often appears in news and reports; for \"to raise\" you need 増やす or 増加させる. Opposite: 減少. For prices and temperatures, 上昇 is more usual.","levelNote":"Derived from 増加 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"出生率が年々減少している。","romaji":"Shusshōritsu ga nennen genshō shite iru.","german":"The birth rate is declining year by year.","furigana":"しゅっしょうりつ|ねんねんげんしょう"},{"japanese":"この町の人口は減少傾向にある。","romaji":"Kono machi no jinkou wa genshou keikou ni aru.","german":"The population of this town is on a downward trend.","furigana":"まち|じんこう|げんしょうけいこう"},{"japanese":"ネットで買い物をする人が増え、店の売り上げが減少した。","romaji":"Netto de kaimono o suru hito ga fue, mise no uriage ga genshou shita.","german":"More people shop online, and the shop's sales have decreased.","furigana":"か|もの|ひと|ふ|みせ|う|あ|げんしょう"}],"notes":"減少する means \"to decrease, decline\" and is intransitive: 人口が減少する, 売り上げが減少する. It is the formal equivalent of 減る and typical of statistics and news (〜の減少, 減少傾向にある \"to be on the decline\"). Transitive \"to reduce\" is 減らす or 減少させる; the opposite is 増加.","levelNote":"Derived from 減少 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"気温が急激に上昇した。","romaji":"Kion ga kyūgeki ni jōshō shita.","german":"The temperature rose sharply.","furigana":"きおん|きゅうげき|じょうしょう"},{"japanese":"物価の上昇で、生活が苦しくなっている。","romaji":"Bukka no joushou de, seikatsu ga kurushiku natte iru.","german":"Rising prices are making life hard.","furigana":"ぶっか|じょうしょう|せいかつ|くる"},{"japanese":"そのバンドの新曲はランキングで一気に三位まで上昇した。","romaji":"Sono bando no shinkyoku wa rankingu de ikki ni san'i made joushou shita.","german":"The band's new song shot up to number three on the charts in one go.","furigana":"しんきょく|いっき|さんい|じょうしょう"}],"notes":"上昇する means \"to rise, go up\" and is intransitive: 気温が上昇する, 物価が上昇する, 飛行機が上昇する. It describes an upward movement or value (temperature, prices, stock prices, rankings) and is more formal than 上がる. 増加, by contrast, is used for numbers and quantities. Opposite: 下降, for prices also 下落, for levels 低下.","levelNote":"Derived from 上昇 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"飛行機が徐々に下降し始めた。","romaji":"Hikōki ga jojo ni kakō shihajimeta.","german":"The plane began to descend gradually.","furigana":"ひこうき|じょじょ|かこう|はじ"},{"japanese":"景気は去年の秋から下降している。","romaji":"Keiki wa kyonen no aki kara kakou shite iru.","german":"The economy has been in decline since last autumn.","furigana":"けいき|きょねん|あき|かこう"},{"japanese":"チームの成績は後半に入って下降線をたどった。","romaji":"Chiimu no seiseki wa kouhan ni haitte kakousen o tadotta.","german":"In the second half of the season, the team's results went downhill.","furigana":"せいせき|こうはん|はい|かこうせん"}],"notes":"下降する means \"to descend, to go down\" and is intransitive - for aircraft, but also for curves, values and performance: 飛行機が下降する, 景気が下降する, 下降線をたどる \"be on a downward trend\". Opposite: 上昇. For temperatures and abilities 低下 is more usual, for prices 下落. It is read the same as 加工 (\"to process\").","levelNote":"Derived from 下降 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"為替レートが大きく変動している。","romaji":"Kawase rēto ga ōkiku hendō shite iru.","german":"The exchange rate is fluctuating greatly.","furigana":"かわせ|おお|へんどう"},{"japanese":"野菜の値段は天候によって大きく変動する。","romaji":"Yasai no nedan wa tenkou ni yotte ookiku hendou suru.","german":"Vegetable prices fluctuate greatly depending on the weather.","furigana":"やさい|ねだん|てんこう|おお|へんどう"},{"japanese":"気候変動の影響で、各地で大雨が増えている。","romaji":"Kikou hendou no eikyou de, kakuchi de ooame ga fuete iru.","german":"Because of the effects of climate change, heavy rain is increasing in many places.","furigana":"きこうへんどう|えいきょう|かくち|おおあめ|ふ"}],"notes":"変動する means \"to fluctuate, change (greatly)\" and is intransitive; typical of values that go up and down: 為替が変動する, 株価の変動, 価格変動. Unlike 変化 (\"change\" in general), 変動 stresses fluctuation and instability, mostly for prices, exchange rates and figures. Important compounds: 気候変動 \"climate change\", 変動相場制 \"floating exchange rate system\".","levelNote":"Derived from 変動 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"患者の容態が安定した。","romaji":"Kanja no yōtai ga antei shita.","german":"The patient's condition has stabilized.","furigana":"かんじゃ|ようたい|あんてい"},{"japanese":"公務員は収入が安定しているので人気がある。","romaji":"Koumuin wa shuunyuu ga antei shite iru node ninki ga aru.","german":"Civil servants are popular because their income is stable.","furigana":"こうむいん|しゅうにゅう|あんてい|にんき"},{"japanese":"週末は天気が安定しないでしょう。","romaji":"Shuumatsu wa tenki ga antei shinai deshou.","german":"The weather probably won't be settled over the weekend.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|てんき|あんてい"}],"notes":"安定する means \"to become/be stable, to stabilize\" and is intransitive: 容態が安定する, 収入が安定している, 天気が安定する. Attributively 安定した is usual: 安定した生活 \"a secure life\"; the opposite is 不安定 \"unstable, unsteady.\" Transitive \"to stabilize something\" is 安定させる.","levelNote":"Derived from 安定 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"経済成長が停滞している。","romaji":"Keizai seichō ga teitai shite iru.","german":"Economic growth is stagnating.","furigana":"けいざいせいちょう|ていたい"},{"japanese":"リーダーが不在で、プロジェクトが停滞している。","romaji":"Riidaa ga fuzai de, purojekuto ga teitai shite iru.","german":"With no leader, the project is stalled.","furigana":"ふざい|ていたい"},{"japanese":"梅雨前線が本州付近に停滞し、雨が続いている。","romaji":"Tsuyu zensen ga Honshuu fukin ni teitai shi, ame ga tsuzuite iru.","german":"The seasonal rain front is stalling near Honshū, and the rain continues.","furigana":"つゆぜんせん|ほんしゅうふきん|ていたい|あめ|つづ"}],"notes":"停滞する means \"to stagnate, stall, make no progress\" and is intransitive: 経済が停滞する, 議論が停滞する, in weather reports 前線が停滞する \"a front stalls\". It stresses that something that should be moving is stuck in place. For a traffic jam, one says 渋滞 instead; 低迷 describes rather a longer period at a low level (sales, stock prices).","levelNote":"Derived from 停滞 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地球は二十四時間で一回転する。","romaji":"Chikyū wa nijūyo jikan de ikkaiten suru.","german":"The earth makes one rotation in twenty-four hours.","furigana":"ちきゅう|にじゅうよじかん|いっかいてん"},{"japanese":"あの人は頭の回転が速くて、すぐに答えを出す。","romaji":"Ano hito wa atama no kaiten ga hayakute, sugu ni kotae o dasu.","german":"That person's mind works fast and comes up with an answer right away.","furigana":"ひと|あたま|かいてん|はや|こた|だ"},{"japanese":"扇風機の羽根が回転しなくなった。","romaji":"Senpuuki no hane ga kaiten shinaku natta.","german":"The blades of the electric fan stopped spinning.","furigana":"せんぷうき|はね|かいてん"}],"notes":"回転する means \"to turn, rotate\" and is intransitive: 車輪が回転する, 地球が回転する; transitive is 回転させる or, in everyday speech, 回す (intransitive 回る). Figuratively: 頭の回転が速い \"to think fast,\" 客の回転がいい \"high customer turnover,\" 回転ずし \"conveyor-belt sushi.\" Compared with 回る, 回転 sounds more technical and precise.","levelNote":"Derived from 回転 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地震で建物が激しく振動した。","romaji":"Jishin de tatemono ga hageshiku shindō shita.","german":"The building shook violently in the earthquake.","furigana":"じしん|たてもの|はげ|しんどう"},{"japanese":"会議中にポケットの中でスマホが振動した。","romaji":"Kaigichuu ni poketto no naka de sumaho ga shindou shita.","german":"During the meeting, my smartphone vibrated in my pocket.","furigana":"かいぎちゅう|なか|しんどう"},{"japanese":"洗濯機の振動がうるさくて眠れない。","romaji":"Sentakuki no shindou ga urusakute nemurenai.","german":"The washing machine vibrates so loudly that I can't sleep.","furigana":"せんたくき|しんどう|ねむ"}],"notes":"振動する means \"to vibrate, oscillate\" and is intransitive: 携帯が振動する, 振り子が振動する; as a noun 振動を感じる \"to feel a tremor\", 振動数 \"frequency\". 揺れる (\"to sway, shake\") is more everyday; 振動 sounds technical and refers to quick, repeated movements. For earthquakes the spelling 震動 is also found.","levelNote":"Derived from 振動 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"金属は熱を加えると膨張する。","romaji":"Kinzoku wa netsu wo kuwaeru to bōchō suru.","german":"Metal expands when heat is applied.","furigana":"きんぞく|ねつ|くわ|ぼうちょう"},{"japanese":"宇宙は今も膨張し続けていると考えられている。","romaji":"Uchuu wa ima mo bouchou shitsuzukete iru to kangaerarete iru.","german":"The universe is thought to be still expanding.","furigana":"うちゅう|いま|ぼうちょう|つづ|かんが"},{"japanese":"国の予算が年々膨張している。","romaji":"Kuni no yosan ga nennen bouchou shite iru.","german":"The national budget is swelling year by year.","furigana":"くに|よさん|ねんねんぼうちょう"}],"notes":"膨張する means \"to expand, swell\" and is intransitive: 金属が熱で膨張する, 宇宙が膨張する. Figuratively it is used for budgets, cities or costs that keep growing: 予算が膨張する, 都市の膨張. Opposite: 収縮. In everyday speech, for a balloon or for hopes, people tend to say 膨らむ; 膨張 is technical and literary.","levelNote":"Derived from 膨張 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"筋肉が収縮して力が生まれる。","romaji":"Kinniku ga shūshuku shite chikara ga umareru.","german":"Force is produced when muscles contract.","furigana":"きんにく|しゅうしゅく|ちから|う"},{"japanese":"寒いと血管が収縮して、手足が冷たくなる。","romaji":"Samui to kekkan ga shuushuku shite, teashi ga tsumetaku naru.","german":"When it is cold, blood vessels contract and hands and feet get cold.","furigana":"さむ|けっかん|しゅうしゅく|てあし|つめ"},{"japanese":"景気が悪くなり、国内市場が収縮している。","romaji":"Keiki ga waruku nari, kokunai shijou ga shuushuku shite iru.","german":"With the economy doing badly, the domestic market is shrinking.","furigana":"けいき|わる|こくないしじょう|しゅうしゅく"}],"notes":"収縮する means \"to contract, to shrink\" and is intransitive: 筋肉が収縮する, 血管が収縮する. It is the opposite of 膨張 and is also used in economics (市場が収縮する \"the market shrinks\"). For clothes that shrink in the wash, daily speech uses 縮む; 収縮 sounds technical (medicine, physics, economics).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"細胞が分裂して増えていく。","romaji":"Saibō ga bunretsu shite fuete iku.","german":"Cells multiply by dividing.","furigana":"さいぼう|ぶんれつ|ふ"},{"japanese":"意見の対立から、党が二つに分裂した。","romaji":"Iken no tairitsu kara, tou ga futatsu ni bunretsu shita.","german":"Because of conflicting opinions, the party split into two.","furigana":"いけん|たいりつ|とう|ふた|ぶんれつ"},{"japanese":"原子力発電は核分裂のエネルギーを利用している。","romaji":"Genshiryoku hatsuden wa kakubunretsu no enerugii o riyou shite iru.","german":"Nuclear power generation uses the energy of nuclear fission.","furigana":"げんしりょくはつでん|かくぶんれつ|りよう"}],"notes":"分裂する means \"to split, divide\" and is intransitive: 細胞が分裂する (細胞分裂 \"cell division\"), 党が二つに分裂する, 意見が分裂する. It stresses the breaking apart of something that was a whole - often groups, parties or countries. For neutral \"to separate\" one uses 分ける or 分離; in physics: 核分裂 \"nuclear fission\".","levelNote":"Derived from 分裂 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二つの文化が融合して新しい文化が生まれた。","romaji":"Futatsu no bunka ga yūgō shite atarashii bunka ga umareta.","german":"Two cultures fused, and a new culture was born.","furigana":"ふた|ぶんか|ゆうごう|あたら|ぶんか|う"},{"japanese":"この店では和食とフランス料理が見事に融合している。","romaji":"Kono mise de wa washoku to Furansu ryouri ga migoto ni yuugou shite iru.","german":"In this restaurant, Japanese and French cuisine are wonderfully blended.","furigana":"みせ|わしょく|りょうり|みごと|ゆうごう"},{"japanese":"太陽は水素の核融合によって光を出している。","romaji":"Taiyou wa suiso no kakuyuugou ni yotte hikari o dashite iru.","german":"The sun produces light through the nuclear fusion of hydrogen.","furigana":"たいよう|すいそ|かくゆうごう|ひかり|だ"}],"notes":"融合する means \"to fuse, to merge\" and is intransitive: AとBが融合する, 伝統と現代が融合する; the transitive form is 融合させる. In technical use: 核融合 \"nuclear fusion\", 細胞融合 \"cell fusion\". For the merger of companies one does not say 融合 but 合併. 融合 stresses that different elements fuse into a new unity.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"研究室で新しい化合物を合成した。","romaji":"Kenkyūshitsu de atarashii kagōbutsu wo gōsei shita.","german":"A new compound was synthesized in the laboratory.","furigana":"けんきゅうしつ|あたら|かごうぶつ|ごうせい"},{"japanese":"この写真は二枚の画像を合成して作ったものだ。","romaji":"Kono shashin wa nimai no gazou o gousei shite tsukutta mono da.","german":"This photo was made by compositing two images.","furigana":"しゃしん|にまい|がぞう|ごうせい|つく"},{"japanese":"駅のアナウンスには合成音声が使われている。","romaji":"Eki no anaunsu ni wa gousei onsei ga tsukawarete iru.","german":"Synthetic voice is used for the announcements at the station.","furigana":"えき|ごうせいおんせい|つか"}],"notes":"合成する means \"to put together, synthesize\" and is transitive: chemically 化合物を合成する, 合成繊維 \"synthetic fiber\", 合成洗剤 \"synthetic detergent\"; for image and sound 写真を合成する \"to make a photo composite\", 合成音声 \"synthetic voice\". 合成 stresses artificial production from several parts; 加工, by contrast, is the processing of an existing material.","levelNote":"Derived from 合成 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"機械を分解して原因を調べた。","romaji":"Kikai wo bunkai shite gen'in wo shirabeta.","german":"I took the machine apart and investigated the cause.","furigana":"きかい|ぶんかい|げんいん|しら"},{"japanese":"プラスチックは自然の中ではなかなか分解されない。","romaji":"Purasuchikku wa shizen no naka de wa nakanaka bunkai sarenai.","german":"Plastic hardly breaks down in nature.","furigana":"しぜん|なか|ぶんかい"},{"japanese":"子どもの頃、おもちゃを分解して父に怒られた。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, omocha o bunkai shite chichi ni okorareta.","german":"As a child, I took toys apart and got scolded by my father.","furigana":"こ|ころ|ぶんかい|ちち|おこ"}],"notes":"分解する has two sides: transitive \"to take apart, to disassemble\" (時計を分解する) and intransitive or passive \"to decompose, to be broken down\" (プラスチックは分解されにくい, 微生物が落ち葉を分解する). The opposite of taking apart is 組み立てる (\"to assemble\"). In mathematics, 因数分解 means \"factorization\".","levelNote":"Derived from 分解 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"砂糖が水に溶解する。","romaji":"Satō ga mizu ni yōkai suru.","german":"Sugar dissolves in water.","furigana":"さとう|みず|ようかい"},{"japanese":"この薬は水に溶解しやすい。","romaji":"Kono kusuri wa mizu ni youkai shiyasui.","german":"This medicine dissolves easily in water.","furigana":"くすり|みず|ようかい"},{"japanese":"工場では古い鉄を高温で溶解して再利用している。","romaji":"Koujou de wa furui tetsu o kouon de youkai shite sairiyou shite iru.","german":"At the plant, old iron is melted at high temperature and reused.","furigana":"こうじょう|ふる|てつ|こうおん|ようかい|さいりよう"}],"notes":"溶解する means \"to dissolve (in a liquid)\" and also \"to melt\" (metals by heat). It is mostly intransitive: 砂糖が水に溶解する; in technology and industry it is also transitive: 鉄を溶解する \"melt iron\". The word is technical - in daily life one says 溶ける or 溶かす. Typical are 溶解度 \"solubility\" and 溶解炉 \"melting furnace\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"太陽の熱で水が蒸発する。","romaji":"Taiyō no netsu de mizu ga jōhatsu suru.","german":"Water evaporates from the heat of the sun.","furigana":"たいよう|ねつ|みず|じょうはつ"},{"japanese":"鍋のスープが蒸発して、半分に減ってしまった。","romaji":"Nabe no suupu ga jouhatsu shite, hanbun ni hette shimatta.","german":"The soup in the pot boiled away until only half was left.","furigana":"なべ|じょうはつ|はんぶん|へ"},{"japanese":"借金を抱えた男が、ある日突然蒸発した。","romaji":"Shakkin o kakaeta otoko ga, aru hi totsuzen jouhatsu shita.","german":"A man in debt suddenly disappeared one day.","furigana":"しゃっきん|かか|おとこ|ひとつぜんじょうはつ"}],"notes":"蒸発する means \"to evaporate, to vaporize\" and is intransitive: 水が蒸発する; the transitive form is 蒸発させる. Colloquially it also means \"to disappear without a trace, to go into hiding\" of people (夫が蒸発した). Unlike 沸騰 (\"to boil\"), 蒸発 can also happen at the surface of a liquid without boiling.","levelNote":"Derived from 蒸発 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"溶岩が冷えて凝固した。","romaji":"Yōgan ga hiete gyōko shita.","german":"The lava cooled and solidified.","furigana":"ようがん|ひ|ぎょうこ"},{"japanese":"傷口の血液が凝固して、出血が止まった。","romaji":"Kizuguchi no ketsueki ga gyouko shite, shukketsu ga tomatta.","german":"The blood in the wound clotted, and the bleeding stopped.","furigana":"きずぐち|けつえき|ぎょうこ|しゅっけつ|と"},{"japanese":"水は零度で凝固して氷になる。","romaji":"Mizu wa reido de gyouko shite koori ni naru.","german":"Water freezes at zero degrees and becomes ice.","furigana":"みず|れいど|ぎょうこ|こおり"}],"notes":"凝固する means \"to solidify, to become solid, to coagulate\" and is intransitive; it is technical and used for liquids: 溶岩が凝固する, 血液が凝固する \"blood clots\", 凝固点 \"freezing point\". In daily life one says 固まる. The opposite (melting) is technically 融解.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鉄は空気中で酸化して錆びる。","romaji":"Tetsu wa kūkichū de sanka shite sabiru.","german":"Iron oxidizes in air and rusts.","furigana":"てつ|くうきちゅう|さんか|さ"},{"japanese":"古い油は酸化しているので、使わないほうがいい。","romaji":"Furui abura wa sanka shite iru node, tsukawanai hou ga ii.","german":"Old oil has oxidized, so it is better not to use it.","furigana":"ふる|あぶら|さんか|つか"},{"japanese":"リンゴは切ってしばらく置くと、酸化して茶色くなる。","romaji":"Ringo wa kitte shibaraku oku to, sanka shite chairoku naru.","german":"If you leave a cut apple for a while, it oxidizes and turns brown.","furigana":"き|お|さんか|ちゃいろ"}],"notes":"酸化する means \"to oxidize\" and is intransitive: 鉄が酸化する, 油が酸化する (\"to go rancid\"); transitive 酸化させる, as a noun 酸化を防ぐ \"to prevent oxidation\". The chemical opposite is 還元 (\"reduction\"). For metals one says 錆びる (\"to rust\") in everyday speech; for foods such as cut apples, 酸化 is likewise used.","levelNote":"Derived from 酸化 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"酸化した金属を還元する実験を行った。","romaji":"Sanka shita kinzoku wo kangen suru jikken wo okonatta.","german":"We carried out an experiment to reduce oxidized metal.","furigana":"さんか|きんぞく|かんげん|じっけん|おこな"},{"japanese":"店は利益の一部をポイントとして客に還元する。","romaji":"Mise wa rieki no ichibu o pointo to shite kyaku ni kangen suru.","german":"The store returns part of its profits to customers as points.","furigana":"みせ|りえき|いちぶ|きゃく|かんげん"},{"japanese":"研究の成果を社会に還元したい。","romaji":"Kenkyuu no seika o shakai ni kangen shitai.","german":"I want to give the results of my research back to society.","furigana":"けんきゅう|せいか|しゃかい|かんげん"}],"notes":"還元する has two areas of use: in chemistry it means \"to reduce\" (to remove oxygen from a substance), the opposite of 酸化. In everyday life and business it means \"to give back (profits, benefits), to let benefit\", in the pattern AをBに還元する: 利益を社会に還元する, ポイント還元 \"rebate in points\". For \"to reduce\" in the sense of \"to decrease\", one does not say 還元 but 減らす or 削減する.","levelNote":"Derived from 還元 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"違法に設置された看板を撤去する。","romaji":"Ihō ni secchi sareta kanban wo tekkyo suru.","german":"We will remove signs that were put up illegally.","furigana":"いほう|せっち|かんばん|てっきょ"},{"japanese":"駅前に置かれた自転車は、市によって撤去されます。","romaji":"Ekimae ni okareta jitensha wa, shi ni yotte tekkyo saremasu.","german":"Bicycles left in front of the station are removed by the city.","furigana":"えきまえ|お|じてんしゃ|し|てっきょ"},{"japanese":"イベントが終わると、すぐにステージが撤去された。","romaji":"Ibento ga owaru to, sugu ni suteeji ga tekkyo sareta.","german":"Right after the event ended, the stage was dismantled.","furigana":"お|てっきょ"}],"notes":"撤去する means \"to remove, to dismantle, to clear away\": something that was set up or built somewhere is taken away - 看板を撤去する, 放置自転車を撤去する, 古い建物を撤去する. It is transitive and in notices and news often appears in the passive (撤去される). 取り除く is more general; withdrawal from a market or of troops is usually expressed with 撤退.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"本社を東京から大阪に移転する。","romaji":"Honsha wo Tōkyō kara Ōsaka ni iten suru.","german":"The head office will be relocated from Tokyo to Osaka.","furigana":"ほんしゃ|とうきょう|おおさか|いてん"},{"japanese":"いつも行っていたパン屋が駅前に移転した。","romaji":"Itsumo itte ita pan'ya ga ekimae ni iten shita.","german":"The bakery I always used to go to has moved to the front of the station.","furigana":"い|や|えきまえ|いてん"},{"japanese":"この技術は海外の工場に移転される予定だ。","romaji":"Kono gijutsu wa kaigai no koujou ni iten sareru yotei da.","german":"This technology is scheduled to be transferred to an overseas factory.","furigana":"ぎじゅつ|かいがい|こうじょう|いてん|よてい"}],"notes":"移転する means \"to relocate, to move\" - of companies, shops, government offices or institutions, not of private individuals (for that, 引っ越す). It can be transitive (本社を大阪に移転する) and intransitive (店が駅前に移転した); on notices you read 移転のお知らせ \"notice of relocation\". It also means \"transfer\", e.g. 技術移転 \"technology transfer\".","levelNote":"Derived from 移転 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"空港のターミナルを拡張する工事が始まった。","romaji":"Kūkō no tāminaru wo kakuchō suru kōji ga hajimatta.","german":"Construction to expand the airport terminal has begun.","furigana":"くうこう|かくちょう|こうじ|はじ"},{"japanese":"駅前の道路を拡張して、渋滞を減らす計画だ。","romaji":"Ekimae no douro o kakuchou shite, juutai o herasu keikaku da.","german":"There is a plan to widen the road in front of the station to reduce traffic jams.","furigana":"えきまえ|どうろ|かくちょう|じゅうたい|へ|けいかく"},{"japanese":"ブラウザに翻訳の拡張機能を入れた。","romaji":"Burauza ni hon'yaku no kakuchou kinou o ireta.","german":"I installed a translation extension in my browser.","furigana":"ほんやく|かくちょうきのう|い"}],"notes":"拡張する means \"to expand, to extend, to enlarge\" and is transitive: 道路を拡張する, 事業を拡張する, 機能を拡張する. In IT: 拡張子 \"file extension\", 拡張機能 \"extension, add-on\". Similar is 拡大 (\"to enlarge, to expand\"), which can also be intransitive (被害が拡大する); 拡張 is used more for facilities, areas and functions.","levelNote":"Derived from 拡張 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"契約期間を一年延長した。","romaji":"Keiyaku kikan wo ichinen enchō shita.","german":"The contract period was extended by one year.","furigana":"けいやくきかん|いちねんえんちょう"},{"japanese":"試合は同点のまま延長戦に入った。","romaji":"Shiai wa douten no mama enchousen ni haitta.","german":"The game ended in a tie and went into extra time.","furigana":"しあい|どうてん|えんちょうせん|はい"},{"japanese":"図書館で借りた本の貸出期間を延長してもらった。","romaji":"Toshokan de karita hon no kashidashi kikan o enchou shite moratta.","german":"I had the loan period for the book from the library extended.","furigana":"としょかん|か|ほん|かしだしきかん|えんちょう"}],"notes":"延長する means \"to extend, to lengthen\" - in time (期間を延長する, 営業時間を延長する) and in space (線路を延長する). It is transitive; as nouns you know 延長戦 \"extra time (sports)\" and 延長コード \"extension cord\". Do not confuse it with 延期 (\"to postpone\"): 延長 makes something longer, 延期 moves an appointment to a later date. Opposite: 短縮.","levelNote":"Derived from 延長 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"労働時間を短縮する方針が決まった。","romaji":"Rōdō jikan wo tanshuku suru hōshin ga kimatta.","german":"A policy of shortening working hours was decided.","furigana":"ろうどうじかん|たんしゅく|ほうしん|き"},{"japanese":"新しい道路ができて、通勤時間が二十分短縮された。","romaji":"Atarashii douro ga dekite, tsuukin jikan ga nijuppun tanshuku sareta.","german":"Thanks to the new road, the commute has been shortened by twenty minutes.","furigana":"あたら|どうろ|つうきんじかん|にじゅっぷんたんしゅく"},{"japanese":"この店は今月から営業時間を短縮している。","romaji":"Kono mise wa kongetsu kara eigyou jikan o tanshuku shite iru.","german":"This shop has had shortened business hours since this month.","furigana":"みせ|こんげつ|えいぎょうじかん|たんしゅく"}],"notes":"短縮する means \"to shorten, cut short\" and is transitive: 時間を短縮する, 距離を短縮する, 営業時間を短縮する \"to shorten business hours\". The abbreviation 時短 (from 時間短縮) is very common for saving time or shorter working hours. Opposite: 延長. 省略, by contrast, means \"to omit, leave out\".","levelNote":"Derived from 短縮 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"油と水は自然に分離する。","romaji":"Abura to mizu wa shizen ni bunri suru.","german":"Oil and water separate naturally.","furigana":"あぶら|みず|しぜん|ぶんり"},{"japanese":"打ち上げの数分後、ブースターがロケットから分離した。","romaji":"Uchiage no suufungo, buusutaa ga roketto kara bunri shita.","german":"A few minutes after launch, the booster separated from the rocket.","furigana":"う|あ|すうふんご|ぶんり"},{"japanese":"この装置は水から汚れを分離する。","romaji":"Kono souchi wa mizu kara yogore o bunri suru.","german":"This device separates dirt from water.","furigana":"そうち|みず|よご|ぶんり"}],"notes":"分離する means \"to separate, split off\" and is used both transitively (AからBを分離する) and intransitively (油と水が分離する). It sounds factual and technical; well-known compounds are 政教分離 \"separation of church and state\" and 中央分離帯 \"median strip\". In everyday speech one says 分ける / 分かれる; 分裂, by contrast, stresses the breaking apart of a whole.","levelNote":"Derived from 分離 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は二十歳で親から独立した。","romaji":"Kanojo wa hatachi de oya kara dokuritsu shita.","german":"She became independent from her parents at twenty.","furigana":"かのじょ|はたち|おや|どくりつ"},{"japanese":"彼は十年会社に勤めた後、独立して自分の店を開いた。","romaji":"Kare wa juunen kaisha ni tsutometa ato, dokuritsu shite jibun no mise o hiraita.","german":"After working at a company for ten years, he went independent and opened his own shop.","furigana":"かれ|じゅうねんかいしゃ|つと|あと|どくりつ|じぶん|みせ|ひら"},{"japanese":"その国は二十世紀の半ばに独立した。","romaji":"Sono kuni wa nijusseiki no nakaba ni dokuritsu shita.","german":"That country became independent in the middle of the twentieth century.","furigana":"くに|にじゅっせいき|なか|どくりつ"}],"notes":"独立する means \"to become independent, to become self-employed\" and is intransitive, mostly with から: 親から独立する \"to stand on one's own feet,\" 会社を辞めて独立する \"to quit one's company and go independent,\" 国が独立する. As a noun it appears in 独立記念日 \"Independence Day\" or 独立心 \"spirit of independence.\" Close in meaning is 自立 (\"to live self-reliantly\"), which stresses personal maturity more.","levelNote":"Derived from 独立 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"他人に依存しすぎてはいけない。","romaji":"Tanin ni izon shisugite wa ikenai.","german":"Man sollte nicht zu sehr von anderen abhängig sein.","furigana":"たにん|いぞん"},{"japanese":"日本はエネルギーの多くを輸入に依存している。","romaji":"Nihon wa enerugii no ooku o yunyuu ni izon shite iru.","german":"Japan ist bei seiner Energie größtenteils auf Importe angewiesen.","furigana":"にほん|おお|ゆにゅう|いぞん"},{"japanese":"彼はアルコール依存症の治療を受けている。","romaji":"Kare wa arukooru izonshou no chiryou o ukete iru.","german":"Er ist wegen Alkoholabhängigkeit in Behandlung.","furigana":"かれ|いぞんしょう|ちりょう|う"}],"notes":"依存する heißt „abhängig sein (von), sich stützen auf“ und ist intransitiv mit に: 輸入に依存する, 親に依存する. Oft negativ gefärbt, besonders in Komposita wie スマホ依存 „Handysucht“ oder アルコール依存症 „Alkoholabhängigkeit“. Das deutsche „es hängt davon ab“ heißt dagegen 〜による / 〜次第だ, nicht 依存. Alltäglicher für „sich verlassen auf“ ist 頼る; die Lesung いそん ist ebenfalls korrekt.","levelNote":"Derived from 依存 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その王は広大な領土を支配していた。","romaji":"Sono ō wa kōdai na ryōdo wo shihai shite ita.","german":"The king ruled a vast territory.","furigana":"おう|こうだい|りょうど|しはい"},{"japanese":"彼は恐怖に支配されて、何も言えなかった。","romaji":"Kare wa kyoufu ni shihai sarete, nani mo ienakatta.","german":"He was gripped by fear and could not say anything.","furigana":"かれ|きょうふ|しはい|なに|い"},{"japanese":"その会社は国内市場をほぼ支配している。","romaji":"Sono kaisha wa kokunai shijou o hobo shihai shite iru.","german":"That company dominates almost the whole domestic market.","furigana":"かいしゃ|こくないしじょう|しはい"}],"notes":"支配する means \"to rule, govern, control\" and is transitive: 国を支配する, 市場を支配する. Figuratively also for feelings or moods that determine someone: 恐怖に支配される \"to be gripped by fear.\" 支配人 is the \"manager\" of a hotel or cinema. Compared with 管理 (\"to manage\"), 支配 always has an aspect of power and rule.","levelNote":"Derived from 支配 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"データを安全に管理する必要がある。","romaji":"Dēta wo anzen ni kanri suru hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"Data needs to be managed securely.","furigana":"あんぜん|かんり|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"マンションの管理人に鍵を預けておいた。","romaji":"Manshon no kanrinin ni kagi o azukete oita.","german":"I left the key with the apartment building's manager.","furigana":"かんりにん|かぎ|あず"},{"japanese":"試験前は、体調をしっかり管理することが大切だ。","romaji":"Shiken mae wa, taichou o shikkari kanri suru koto ga taisetsu da.","german":"Before exams, it is important to take good care of your health.","furigana":"しけんまえ|たいちょう|かんり|たいせつ"}],"notes":"管理する means \"to manage, supervise, take care of\" and is transitive: データを管理する, 建物を管理する, 健康を管理する. Very common are 管理人 \"caretaker, building manager,\" 管理職 \"managerial position\" and 体調管理 \"looking after one's health.\" 経営, by contrast, means \"to run a company,\" and 支配 has an aspect of power; 管理 stresses order, control and upkeep.","levelNote":"Derived from 管理 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼はNPO法人を運営している。","romaji":"Kare wa NPO hōjin o un'ei shite iru.","german":"He runs a nonprofit organization.","furigana":"かれ|ほうじん|うんえい"},{"japanese":"このサイトは個人で運営されています。","romaji":"Kono saito wa kojin de un'ei sarete imasu.","german":"This site is run by an individual.","furigana":"こじん|うんえい"},{"japanese":"文化祭の運営を任されて、毎日準備で忙しい。","romaji":"Bunkasai no un'ei o makasarete, mainichi junbi de isogashii.","german":"I've been put in charge of running the school festival and am busy with preparations every day.","furigana":"ぶんかさい|うんえい|まか|まいにちじゅんび|いそが"}],"notes":"運営する means \"to run, organize, manage\" - for institutions, events, websites or clubs: 学校を運営する, イベントの運営. It should be distinguished from 経営する, which stresses profit-oriented business management (会社・店を経営する), whereas 運営 refers to the practical operation and administration. Common are 運営側 \"organizers, the operator side\", 運営会社 and 運営費. Transitive with を, passive 運営される.","levelNote":"Derived from 運営 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"父はレストランを経営している。","romaji":"Chichi wa resutoran o keiei shite iru.","german":"My father runs a restaurant.","furigana":"ちち|けいえい"},{"japanese":"祖父は駅前で小さな本屋を経営していた。","romaji":"Sofu wa ekimae de chiisana hon'ya o keiei shite ita.","german":"My grandfather used to run a small bookstore in front of the station.","furigana":"そふ|えきまえ|ちい|ほんや|けいえい"},{"japanese":"ホテルを経営するのは想像以上に大変だ。","romaji":"Hoteru o keiei suru no wa souzou ijou ni taihen da.","german":"Running a hotel is harder than you would imagine.","furigana":"けいえい|そうぞういじょう|たいへん"}],"notes":"経営する means to run and operate a company or business economically; the object takes を: 会社・店・ホテルを経営する. It mostly appears in the progressive form 経営している because it describes an ongoing state. For clubs, events or websites where profit is not the point, people more often say 運営する \"to operate, organize,\" and for pure administration 管理する. Common derivatives are 経営者 \"entrepreneur, manager\" and 経営が苦しい \"to be in financial difficulty.\"","levelNote":"Derived from 経営 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先輩が新入社員を指導する。","romaji":"Senpai ga shinnyuushain o shidou suru.","german":"A senior employee coaches the new employees.","furigana":"せんぱい|しんにゅうしゃいん|しどう"},{"japanese":"田中先生は卒業論文の書き方を丁寧に指導してくださった。","romaji":"Tanaka-sensei wa sotsugyou ronbun no kakikata o teinei ni shidou shite kudasatta.","german":"Professor Tanaka carefully guided me on how to write my graduation thesis.","furigana":"たなかせんせい|そつぎょうろんぶん|か|かた|ていねい|しどう"},{"japanese":"コーチに指導されて、フォームがかなり良くなった。","romaji":"Koochi ni shidou sarete, foomu ga kanari yoku natta.","german":"Since I was coached by my trainer, my form has improved a lot.","furigana":"しどう|よ"}],"notes":"指導する means to guide or supervise someone in a professional or academic way, typical for teachers, coaches, professors and experienced colleagues. The person being guided takes を (生徒を指導する), the content is often 〜の書き方を指導する or 〜について指導する. Compared with the everyday 教える \"to teach,\" 指導 sounds more official and stresses longer-term supervision. Common words: 指導者 \"instructor, leader,\" 指導教員 \"thesis supervisor,\" 行政指導 \"administrative guidance.\"","levelNote":"Derived from 指導 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は建設現場を監督している。","romaji":"Kare wa kensetsu genba o kantoku shite iru.","german":"He supervises the construction site.","furigana":"かれ|けんせつげんば|かんとく"},{"japanese":"花火は必ず大人の監督のもとで楽しんでください。","romaji":"Hanabi wa kanarazu otona no kantoku no moto de tanoshinde kudasai.","german":"Please enjoy fireworks only under adult supervision.","furigana":"はなび|かなら|おとな|かんとく|たの"},{"japanese":"黒澤明が監督した映画を全部見た。","romaji":"Kurosawa Akira ga kantoku shita eiga o zenbu mita.","german":"I have watched all the films directed by Akira Kurosawa.","furigana":"|かんとく|えいが|ぜんぶみ"}],"notes":"監督する means \"to supervise, oversee\": work, people or processes are checked so that everything goes correctly (現場を監督する, 試験を監督する). The noun often appears in the phrase 〜の監督のもとで \"under the supervision of.\" As a noun, 監督 also means \"film director\" or \"manager/coach of a team,\" and 映画を監督する accordingly means \"to direct a film.\" Compared with 指導する \"to guide,\" the emphasis is on control, not on teaching.","levelNote":"Derived from 監督 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女はオーケストラを指揮している。","romaji":"Kanojo wa ookesutora o shiki shite iru.","german":"She conducts the orchestra.","furigana":"かのじょ|しき"},{"japanese":"現場の消防隊長が救助活動を指揮した。","romaji":"Genba no shouboutaichou ga kyuujo katsudou o shiki shita.","german":"The fire chief on site directed the rescue operation.","furigana":"げんば|しょうぼうたいちょう|きゅうじょかつどう|しき"},{"japanese":"部長が自らプロジェクトの指揮をとっている。","romaji":"Buchou ga mizukara purojekuto no shiki o totte iru.","german":"The department head is personally leading the project.","furigana":"ぶちょう|みずか|しき"}],"notes":"指揮する means to lead a group and give it instructions: to conduct an orchestra, command troops or lead an operation (オーケストラを指揮する, 救助活動を指揮する). Very common is the set phrase 指揮を執る（とる） \"to take charge.\" Derived from it are 指揮者 \"conductor\" and 指揮官 \"commander.\" Unlike 指導する \"to guide, teach,\" it is not about teaching but about command in a concrete situation.","levelNote":"Derived from 指揮 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"上官が兵士に撤退を命令した。","romaji":"Joukan ga heishi ni tettai o meirei shita.","german":"The commanding officer ordered the soldiers to withdraw.","furigana":"じょうかん|へいし|てったい|めいれい"},{"japanese":"父は命令するような口調で「早く寝なさい」と言った。","romaji":"Chichi wa meirei suru you na kuchou de \"Hayaku nenasai\" to itta.","german":"My father said \"Go to bed early\" in a commanding tone.","furigana":"ちち|めいれい|くちょう|はや|ね|い"},{"japanese":"人に命令されるのが嫌いで、会社を辞めて独立した。","romaji":"Hito ni meirei sareru no ga kirai de, kaisha o yamete dokuritsu shita.","german":"I hate being given orders by people, so I quit my company and went independent.","furigana":"ひと|めいれい|きら|かいしゃ|や|どくりつ"}],"notes":"命令する means \"to order\": the person who receives the order takes に, the content takes を or appears as 〜ように／〜と (部下に待機するように命令する). The word is very direct and hierarchical; in the office people mostly say 指示する \"to instruct,\" among equals 頼む. In the passive, 命令される often carries a note of resentment at being bossed around. In writing there is the synonym 命じる.","levelNote":"Derived from 命令 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"兵士は命令に服従しなければならない。","romaji":"Heishi wa meirei ni fukujū shinakereba naranai.","german":"Soldiers must obey orders.","furigana":"へいし|めいれい|ふくじゅう"},{"japanese":"彼は上司の言うことに何でも服従するタイプではない。","romaji":"Kare wa joushi no iu koto ni nan demo fukujuu suru taipu de wa nai.","german":"He is not the type to submit to whatever his boss says.","furigana":"かれ|じょうし|い|なん|ふくじゅう"},{"japanese":"犬は群れのリーダーに服従する性質がある。","romaji":"Inu wa mure no riidaa ni fukujuu suru seishitsu ga aru.","german":"Dogs have a tendency to submit to the leader of the pack.","furigana":"いぬ|む|ふくじゅう|せいしつ"}],"notes":"服従する means to submit unconditionally to an authority or an order; the thing obeyed takes に (命令に服従する). The word is rather literary and often carries a negative connotation of subjugation, e.g. in 絶対服従 \"absolute obedience\". For neutrally following rules or advice, daily speech uses 従う. Opposites are 反抗する \"to rebel\" and 抵抗する \"to resist\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"市民は不当な法律に抵抗した。","romaji":"Shimin wa futō na hōritsu ni teikō shita.","german":"The citizens resisted the unjust law.","furigana":"しみん|ふとう|ほうりつ|ていこう"},{"japanese":"犯人は逮捕されるときに激しく抵抗した。","romaji":"Hannin wa taiho sareru toki ni hageshiku teikou shita.","german":"The culprit resisted fiercely when he was arrested.","furigana":"はんにん|たいほ|はげ|ていこう"},{"japanese":"新しいやり方に抵抗を感じる社員も多い。","romaji":"Atarashii yarikata ni teikou o kanjiru shain mo ooi.","german":"Many employees feel resistance to the new way of doing things.","furigana":"あたら|かた|ていこう|かん|しゃいん|おお"}],"notes":"抵抗する means \"to fight back, resist\" violence, pressure or something imposed; the target takes に (権力に抵抗する). As a noun, 抵抗 also describes an inner reluctance: 〜に抵抗がある／抵抗を感じる \"to have reservations about something.\" In physics, 抵抗 is electrical resistance. Compared with 反抗する, which is directed against authority figures such as parents, 抵抗 is more general and often physical.","levelNote":"Derived from 抵抗 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"思春期の子供は親に反抗することがある。","romaji":"Shishunki no kodomo wa oya ni hankō suru koto ga aru.","german":"Children in puberty sometimes rebel against their parents.","furigana":"ししゅんき|こども|おや|はんこう"},{"japanese":"中学生のころ、先生に反抗してばかりいた。","romaji":"Chuugakusei no koro, sensei ni hankou shite bakari ita.","german":"When I was in junior high school, I did nothing but defy my teachers.","furigana":"ちゅうがくせい|せんせい|はんこう"},{"japanese":"うちの息子は今、反抗期で何を言っても返事をしない。","romaji":"Uchi no musuko wa ima, hankouki de nani o itte mo henji o shinai.","german":"My son is in his rebellious phase now and doesn't answer whatever I say.","furigana":"むすこ|いま|はんこうき|なに|い|へんじ"}],"notes":"反抗する means to rebel against an authority, typically children against parents, pupils against teachers or subordinates against superiors; the target takes に (親に反抗する). The word stresses defiant, often emotional behavior, as in 反抗的な態度 \"rebellious attitude\" and 反抗期 \"defiant phase, rebellious phase.\" 抵抗する, by contrast, is the more general \"to resist,\" and 反発する is more \"to react with rejection.\"","levelNote":"Derived from 反抗 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"労働組合が会社と賃金について交渉する。","romaji":"Rōdō kumiai ga kaisha to chingin ni tsuite kōshō suru.","german":"The labor union negotiates with the company about wages.","furigana":"ろうどうくみあい|かいしゃ|ちんぎん|こうしょう"},{"japanese":"退去日を一週間延ばせないか、大家と交渉した。","romaji":"Taikyobi o isshūkan nobasenai ka, ōya to kōshō shita.","german":"I negotiated with the landlord to see if the move-out date could be pushed back a week.","furigana":"たいきょび|いっしゅうかんの|おおや|こうしょう"},{"japanese":"フリーマーケットで値段を交渉したら、千円安くしてもらえた。","romaji":"Furii maaketto de nedan o koushou shitara, sen'en yasuku shite moraeta.","german":"When I negotiated the price at the flea market, I got it for a thousand yen less.","furigana":"ねだん|こうしょう|せんえんやす"}],"notes":"交渉する means \"to negotiate\": the negotiating partner takes と, the topic takes について or を (値段を交渉する). Success and failure are expressed with 交渉がまとまる \"the negotiation reaches an agreement\" and 交渉が決裂する \"the negotiations break down\". In everyday life you often meet 値段交渉 \"price negotiation\". Someone who does not negotiate personally but stands between two parties 仲介する \"mediates\".","levelNote":"Derived from 交渉 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不動産会社が売買を仲介する。","romaji":"Fudousan gaisha ga baibai o chuukai suru.","german":"A real estate company brokers the sale.","furigana":"ふどうさんがいしゃ|ばいばい|ちゅうかい"},{"japanese":"先輩が仲介してくれて、研究者に話を聞けた。","romaji":"Senpai ga chūkai shite kurete, kenkyūsha ni hanashi o kiketa.","german":"A senior colleague acted as go-between, and I was able to talk to a researcher.","furigana":"せんぱい|ちゅうかい|けんきゅうしゃ|はなし|き"},{"japanese":"国連が仲介して、両国の話し合いが始まった。","romaji":"Kokuren ga chuukai shite, ryoukoku no hanashiai ga hajimatta.","german":"With the United Nations mediating, talks between the two countries began.","furigana":"こくれん|ちゅうかい|りょうこく|はな|あ|はじ"}],"notes":"仲介する means to stand as an intermediary between two parties and bring them together, especially in business: 売買を仲介する, 不動産の仲介, 仲介手数料 \"brokerage fee\", 仲介業者 \"broker\". It can also mean arranging contacts or talks. When it is about the official settlement of a dispute, one says 調停する \"to arbitrate\"; 紹介する only means \"to introduce\", without an active role in what follows.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"裁判所が両者の紛争を調停する。","romaji":"Saibansho ga ryousha no funsou o choutei suru.","german":"The court mediates the dispute between the two parties.","furigana":"さいばんしょ|りょうしゃ|ふんそう|ちょうてい"},{"japanese":"労使の対立を政府が調停した。","romaji":"Roushi no tairitsu o seifu ga choutei shita.","german":"The government mediated the conflict between labor and management.","furigana":"ろうし|たいりつ|せいふ|ちょうてい"},{"japanese":"離婚の話し合いがまとまらず、家庭裁判所に調停を申し立てた。","romaji":"Rikon no hanashiai ga matomarazu, katei saibansho ni choutei o moushitateta.","german":"The divorce talks failed to reach an agreement, so they applied to the family court for mediation.","furigana":"りこん|はな|あ|かていさいばんしょ|ちょうてい|もう|た"}],"notes":"調停する means \"to mediate\": a neutral body such as a court, an authority or a state helps disputing parties agree on a compromise (紛争を調停する). The word is formal and often comes from law and politics, e.g. 離婚調停 \"divorce mediation\" or 調停が成立する \"the mediation succeeds\". In 仲裁, an arbitrator makes a binding decision; 仲介 is mediating in general, e.g. in business deals.","levelNote":"Derived from 調停 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"長年の対立の末、両国は和解した。","romaji":"Naganen no tairitsu no sue, ryōkoku wa wakai shita.","german":"After years of conflict, the two countries reconciled.","furigana":"ながねん|たいりつ|すえ|りょうこく|わかい"},{"japanese":"謝って話し合い、けんかした友人と和解した。","romaji":"Ayamatte hanashiai, kenka shita yūjin to wakai shita.","german":"After apologizing and talking it over, I made up with the friend I had quarreled with.","furigana":"あやま|はな|あ|ゆうじん|わかい"},{"japanese":"裁判は双方が和解して終わった。","romaji":"Saiban wa souhou ga wakai shite owatta.","german":"The trial ended with both sides reaching a settlement.","furigana":"さいばん|そうほう|わかい|お"}],"notes":"和解する means \"to reconcile\" or, legally, \"to settle\"; the partner takes と (友人と和解する). Among friends or children, 仲直りする sounds more natural in daily life; 和解 is more formal and is used for states, for families after a long dispute, or for court proceedings. Typical legal expressions are 和解が成立する \"a settlement is reached\" and 和解金 \"settlement money\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"プロジェクトの成功のために全員が協力した。","romaji":"Purojekuto no seikō no tame ni zen'in ga kyōryoku shita.","german":"Everyone cooperated for the success of the project.","furigana":"せいこう|ぜんいん|きょうりょく"},{"japanese":"兄弟で協力して、母の誕生日パーティーを準備した。","romaji":"Kyoudai de kyouryoku shite, haha no tanjoubi paatii o junbi shita.","german":"The siblings worked together to prepare our mother's birthday party.","furigana":"きょうだい|きょうりょく|はは|たんじょうび|じゅんび"},{"japanese":"アンケートへのご協力をお願いします。","romaji":"Ankeeto e no go-kyouryoku o onegai shimasu.","german":"We ask for your cooperation with the questionnaire.","furigana":"きょうりょく|ねが"}],"notes":"協力する means \"to work together, to lend a hand.\" With と you work together with someone (地域と協力する), with に you contribute to something (調査に協力する \"to take part in a survey\"). Very common is the polite request ご協力をお願いします \"We ask for your cooperation.\" Compared with 手伝う \"to help,\" 協力 stresses the shared goal; 連携する emphasizes coordination between organizations more strongly.","levelNote":"Derived from 協力 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"各部署が連携して問題に対処する。","romaji":"Kaku busho ga renkei shite mondai ni taisho suru.","german":"The departments coordinate to deal with the problem.","furigana":"かくぶしょ|れんけい|もんだい|たいしょ"},{"japanese":"病院と地域の診療所が連携して、患者を支えている。","romaji":"Byouin to chiiki no shinryoujo ga renkei shite, kanja o sasaete iru.","german":"The hospital and local clinics work hand in hand to support patients.","furigana":"びょういん|ちいき|しんりょうじょ|れんけい|かんじゃ|ささ"},{"japanese":"このアプリはスマホのカレンダーと連携できる。","romaji":"Kono apuri wa sumaho no karendaa to renkei dekiru.","german":"This app can be linked with the calendar on your smartphone.","furigana":"れんけい"}],"notes":"連携する means that several parties coordinate their actions and work hand in hand; the partner takes と (警察と連携する), often as 連携して〜する. It is typical of government offices, departments, clinics or sports teams (連携プレー \"teamwork play\"). In IT it also means \"to link, to sync\": アプリと連携する. Compared with 協力する, 連携 stresses coordination between independent partners.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"当社は海外企業と提携した。","romaji":"Tōsha wa kaigai kigyō to teikei shita.","german":"Our company formed a partnership with a foreign company.","furigana":"とうしゃ|かいがいきぎょう|ていけい"},{"japanese":"この大学は海外の三つの大学と提携している。","romaji":"Kono daigaku wa kaigai no mittsu no daigaku to teikei shite iru.","german":"This university has partnerships with three universities abroad.","furigana":"だいがく|かいがい|みっ|だいがく|ていけい"},{"japanese":"提携しているホテルなら、会員は割引料金で泊まれる。","romaji":"Teikei shite iru hoteru nara, kaiin wa waribiki ryoukin de tomareru.","german":"At partner hotels, members can stay at discounted rates.","furigana":"ていけい|かいいん|わりびきりょうきん|と"}],"notes":"提携する means that companies or institutions enter a formal partnership; the partner takes と (海外企業と提携する). Typical words are 業務提携 \"business alliance\", 提携先 \"partner company\" and 提携ホテル \"partner hotel\". Unlike 協力する \"to cooperate\", 提携 presupposes a contractual agreement; for a real merger, one says 合併.","levelNote":"Derived from 提携 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"世界市場で他社と競争している。","romaji":"Sekai shijō de tasha to kyōsō shite iru.","german":"We are competing with other companies in the global market.","furigana":"せかいしじょう|たしゃ|きょうそう"},{"japanese":"兄とどちらが速く食べられるか競争した。","romaji":"Ani to dochira ga hayaku taberareru ka kyousou shita.","german":"I competed with my older brother to see who could eat faster.","furigana":"あに|はや|た|きょうそう"},{"japanese":"子どもの頃から周りと競争させられるのは、本当に良いことなのだろうか。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro kara mawari to kyousou saserareru no wa, hontou ni yoi koto na no darou ka.","german":"Is it really a good thing to be made to compete with everyone around you from childhood?","furigana":"こ|ころ|まわ|きょうそう|ほんとう|よ"}],"notes":"競争する means \"to compete, to vie\"; the opponent takes と (他社と競争する). It ranges from harmless rivalry among children to market competition; common are 競争が激しい \"the competition is fierce\", 競争相手 \"competitor\" and 競争率 \"competition ratio\". Not to be confused with the identically read 競走 \"footrace\". The verb 競う is similar but sounds somewhat more elevated.","levelNote":"Derived from 競争 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"商店街は大型スーパーに対抗して、毎週安売りを始めた。","romaji":"Shoutengai wa oogata suupaa ni taikou shite, maishuu yasuuri o hajimeta.","german":"The shopping street started weekly sales to compete with the big supermarket.","furigana":"しょうてんがい|おおがた|たいこう|まいしゅうやすう|はじ"},{"japanese":"体育祭ではクラス対抗リレーが一番盛り上がる。","romaji":"Taiikusai de wa kurasu taikou riree ga ichiban moriagaru.","german":"At the sports festival, the inter-class relay is always the highlight.","furigana":"たいいくさい|たいこう|いちばんも|あ"},{"japanese":"力では彼に対抗できないので、頭を使うことにした。","romaji":"Chikara de wa kare ni taikou dekinai node, atama o tsukau koto ni shita.","german":"I can't match him in strength, so I decided to use my head.","furigana":"ちから|かれ|たいこう|あたま|つか"}],"notes":"対抗する means to set oneself against an opponent or competitor and keep up with them; the counterpart takes に (大手に対抗する). It is less about mere rejection than about answering like with like, e.g. with countermeasures or a competition. Common are 対抗できる \"to be able to compete with\", 対抗策 \"countermeasure\" and 〜対抗 \"competition between ...\" as in クラス対抗. For rejecting a plan, one rather says 反対する.","levelNote":"Derived from 対抗 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"登山家はエベレストを征服した。","romaji":"Tozanka wa Eberesuto o seifuku shita.","german":"The mountaineer conquered Mount Everest.","furigana":"とざんか|せいふく"},{"japanese":"古代ローマは地中海の周りの国々を次々と征服した。","romaji":"Kodai Rooma wa Chichuukai no mawari no kuniguni o tsugitsugi to seifuku shita.","german":"Ancient Rome conquered the countries around the Mediterranean one after another.","furigana":"こだい|ちちゅうかい|まわ|くにぐに|つぎつぎ|せいふく"},{"japanese":"苦手だった数学をついに征服した気分だ。","romaji":"Nigate datta suugaku o tsui ni seifuku shita kibun da.","german":"I feel as if I have finally conquered math, which I always struggled with.","furigana":"にがて|すうがく|せいふく|きぶん"}],"notes":"征服する means \"to conquer, subjugate\" (国を征服する) and is used figuratively for conquering a mountain or a great challenge (エベレストを征服する). For difficulties or illnesses, 克服する \"to overcome\" is usually more natural; 征服 sounds more dramatic and is often jocular in everyday use. Do not confuse it with the identically read 制服 \"uniform\".","levelNote":"Derived from 征服 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"被災地を支援するために寄付を集めた。","romaji":"Hisaichi o shien suru tame ni kifu o atsumeta.","german":"We collected donations to support the disaster-stricken area.","furigana":"ひさいち|しえん|きふ|あつ"},{"japanese":"政府は子育て中の家庭を支援する制度を作った。","romaji":"Seifu wa kosodate-chuu no katei o shien suru seido o tsukutta.","german":"The government created a program to support families raising children.","furigana":"せいふ|こそだ|ちゅう|かてい|しえん|せいど|つく"},{"japanese":"留学生の生活を支援するボランティアをしています。","romaji":"Ryuugakusei no seikatsu o shien suru borantia o shite imasu.","german":"I do volunteer work supporting the daily life of international students.","furigana":"りゅうがくせい|せいかつ|しえん"}],"notes":"支援する means \"to support\", usually in an organized way and with money, materials or personnel: 被災地を支援する, 子育てを支援する. Typical words are 支援者 \"supporter\" and 支援物資 \"relief supplies\". 援助する stresses material or financial help from a stronger side, 応援する is \"to cheer on\" or personal support, and 手伝う is concrete help with a task.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先進国が発展途上国を経済的に援助する。","romaji":"Senshinkoku ga hatten tojoukoku o keizaiteki ni enjo suru.","german":"Developed countries give economic aid to developing countries.","furigana":"せんしんこく|はってんとじょうこく|けいざいてき|えんじょ"},{"japanese":"大学に通う間、叔父が学費を援助してくれた。","romaji":"Daigaku ni kayou aida, oji ga gakuhi o enjo shite kureta.","german":"While I attended university, my uncle supported my tuition.","furigana":"だいがく|かよ|あいだ|おじ|がくひ|えんじょ"},{"japanese":"この研究は市から資金の援助を受けている。","romaji":"Kono kenkyuu wa shi kara shikin no enjo o ukete iru.","german":"This research receives financial support from the city.","furigana":"けんきゅう|し|しきん|えんじょ|う"}],"notes":"援助する means \"to give aid,\" above all material or financial, often from a stronger side to a weaker one: 途上国を援助する, 学費を援助する. The noun often appears in 援助を受ける \"to receive aid\" and 経済援助 \"economic aid.\" 支援する is broader and can also mean organizational support; for spontaneous helping in everyday life people say 手伝う or 助ける.","levelNote":"Derived from 援助 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"消防隊が火事から住民を救助した。","romaji":"Shouboutai ga kaji kara juumin o kyuujo shita.","german":"The fire brigade rescued the residents from the fire.","furigana":"しょうぼうたい|かじ|じゅうみん|きゅうじょ"},{"japanese":"海でおぼれていた子どもがライフセーバーに救助された。","romaji":"Umi de oborete ita kodomo ga raifuseebaa ni kyuujo sareta.","german":"A child who was drowning in the sea was rescued by a lifeguard.","furigana":"うみ|こ|きゅうじょ"},{"japanese":"雪山で道に迷った登山者が、二日後にヘリで救助された。","romaji":"Yukiyama de michi ni mayotta tozansha ga, futsukago ni heri de kyuujo sareta.","german":"A hiker who got lost on a snowy mountain was rescued by helicopter two days later.","furigana":"ゆきやま|みち|まよ|とざんしゃ|ふつかご|きゅうじょ"}],"notes":"救助する means to rescue people from acute danger to life, for example in fires, accidents or in the mountains; often in the passive 救助される. Typical words are 救助隊 \"rescue team\" and 人命救助 \"life saving.\" 助ける is the general everyday word, 救う sounds more elevated and can also be figurative (心を救う), and 救出する stresses getting someone out of a trap or captivity.","levelNote":"Derived from 救助 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"絶滅危惧種を保護する法律がある。","romaji":"Zetsumetsu kigushu o hogo suru houritsu ga aru.","german":"There is a law that protects endangered species.","furigana":"ぜつめつきぐしゅ|ほご|ほうりつ"},{"japanese":"迷子の子どもが警察に保護された。","romaji":"Maigo no kodomo ga keisatsu ni hogo sareta.","german":"A lost child was taken into protective care by the police.","furigana":"まいご|こ|けいさつ|ほご"},{"japanese":"個人情報を保護するため、パスワードは定期的に変えてください。","romaji":"Kojin jouhou o hogo suru tame, pasuwaado wa teikiteki ni kaete kudasai.","german":"To protect your personal information, please change your password regularly.","furigana":"こじんじょうほう|ほご|ていきてき|か"}],"notes":"保護する means \"to protect\" in the sense of preserving long-term or taking into care: 自然を保護する, 個人情報を保護する. The police 保護する lost children or drunk people, i.e. they take them into care. Important derivatives are 保護者 \"guardian\" and 自然保護 \"nature conservation\". 守る is the general everyday word (also \"to keep a promise\"), and 防ぐ means \"to prevent, to ward off\".","levelNote":"Derived from 保護 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"台風が近づいたので高台に避難した。","romaji":"Taifū ga chikazuita node takadai ni hinan shita.","german":"As a typhoon was approaching, we took shelter on high ground.","furigana":"たいふう|ちか|たかだい|ひなん"},{"japanese":"火災報知器が鳴ったら、階段を使って外に避難してください。","romaji":"Kasai houchiki ga nattara, kaidan o tsukatte soto ni hinan shite kudasai.","german":"When the fire alarm sounds, please use the stairs to get outside.","furigana":"かさいほうちき|な|かいだん|つか|そと|ひなん"},{"japanese":"洪水で家に住めなくなり、今も体育館に避難している人がいる。","romaji":"Kouzui de ie ni sumenaku nari, ima mo taiikukan ni hinan shite iru hito ga iru.","german":"Because floods made their homes uninhabitable, some people are still staying in the gymnasium.","furigana":"こうずい|いえ|す|いま|たいいくかん|ひなん|ひと"}],"notes":"避難する is intransitive and means \"to move to safety, flee (from danger)\": the place of refuge takes に／へ, the danger takes から (津波から高台へ避難する). To evacuate others, use the causative 住民を避難させる. Common words are 避難所 \"evacuation shelter\", 避難訓練 \"evacuation drill\" and 避難指示 \"evacuation order\". Do not confuse it with the identically read 非難 \"criticism, reproach\".","levelNote":"Derived from 避難 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"囚人が刑務所から脱出した。","romaji":"Shūjin ga keimusho kara dasshutsu shita.","german":"The prisoner escaped from the prison.","furigana":"しゅうじん|けいむしょ|だっしゅつ"},{"japanese":"火事に気づいて、急いで窓から脱出した。","romaji":"Kaji ni kizuite, isoide mado kara dasshutsu shita.","german":"I noticed the fire and hurriedly escaped through the window.","furigana":"かじ|き|いそ|まど|だっしゅつ"},{"japanese":"新商品のヒットで、会社はようやく赤字から脱出した。","romaji":"Shinshouhin no hitto de, kaisha wa youyaku akaji kara dasshutsu shita.","german":"Thanks to the hit new product, the company finally got out of the red.","furigana":"しんしょうひん|かいしゃ|あかじ|だっしゅつ"}],"notes":"脱出する means \"to escape, free oneself from a bad situation\"; the place one escapes from takes から (建物から脱出する). It is also used figuratively: 赤字から脱出する \"to get out of the red\", 不況から脱出する. 逃げる is the general \"to flee, run away\"; for prison breaks there is the specific 脱獄する. 脱出ゲーム \"escape room game\" is also well known.","levelNote":"Derived from 脱出 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"泥棒が深夜に家に侵入した。","romaji":"Dorobō ga shinya ni ie ni shinnyū shita.","german":"A burglar broke into the house in the middle of the night.","furigana":"どろぼう|しんや|いえ|しんにゅう"},{"japanese":"何者かが会社のサーバーに侵入し、顧客データを盗んだ。","romaji":"Nanimono ka ga kaisha no saabaa ni shinnyuu shi, kokyaku deeta o nusunda.","german":"Someone broke into the company's server and stole customer data.","furigana":"なにもの|かいしゃ|しんにゅう|こきゃく|ぬす"},{"japanese":"隙間から虫が部屋に侵入してくる。","romaji":"Sukima kara mushi ga heya ni shinnyuu shite kuru.","german":"Insects come into the room through the gaps.","furigana":"すきま|むし|へや|しんにゅう"}],"notes":"侵入する means to enter a place or system without authorization or by force; the target takes に (家に侵入する, サーバーに侵入する). Typical words are 不法侵入 \"trespassing\" and 侵入者 \"intruder\". Do not confuse it with the identically read 進入する, which neutrally describes vehicles driving into an area; water seeping in is written 浸入. For the military invasion of whole countries, 侵略 is more usual.","levelNote":"Derived from 侵入 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"警察が建物に突入した。","romaji":"Keisatsu ga tatemono ni totsunyū shita.","german":"The police stormed into the building.","furigana":"けいさつ|たてもの|とつにゅう"},{"japanese":"両国はついに戦争に突入した。","romaji":"Ryoukoku wa tsui ni sensou ni totsunyuu shita.","german":"The two countries finally plunged into war.","furigana":"りょうこく|せんそう|とつにゅう"},{"japanese":"試合は同点のまま延長戦に突入した。","romaji":"Shiai wa douten no mama enchousen ni totsunyuu shita.","german":"The game went into extra time with the score tied.","furigana":"しあい|どうてん|えんちょうせん|とつにゅう"}],"notes":"突入する means to rush into something with force: police or troops 建物に突入する. Figuratively it means \"to enter a new phase\", often abruptly or unstoppably: 戦争に突入する, 延長戦に突入する, 夏休みに突入する. The destination always takes に. Compared with 入る \"to go in\", 突入 stresses speed and determination; 侵入する, by contrast, is stealthy, unauthorized intrusion.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"電車がホームに進入してきた。","romaji":"Densha ga hōmu ni shinnyū shite kita.","german":"The train pulled into the platform.","furigana":"でんしゃ|しんにゅう"},{"japanese":"この先は車両進入禁止です。","romaji":"Kono saki wa sharyou shinnyuu kinshi desu.","german":"Vehicles are prohibited from entering beyond this point.","furigana":"さき|しゃりょうしんにゅうきんし"},{"japanese":"この交差点では、右から進入する車に注意してください。","romaji":"Kono kousaten de wa, migi kara shinnyuu suru kuruma ni chuui shite kudasai.","german":"At this intersection, watch out for cars entering from the right.","furigana":"こうさてん|みぎ|しんにゅう|くるま|ちゅうい"}],"notes":"進入する is a neutral, rather technical word for vehicles, trains or aircraft entering an area; the destination takes に (交差点に進入する). You see it mainly on signs and in announcements, e.g. 進入禁止 \"no entry\" or 電車が進入します. Do not confuse it with the identically read 侵入する \"to intrude without authorization\". In daily life one simply says 入る.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"軍隊は前線から撤退した。","romaji":"Guntai wa zensen kara tettai shita.","german":"The army withdrew from the front line.","furigana":"ぐんたい|ぜんせん|てったい"},{"japanese":"売上が伸びず、その会社は日本市場から撤退した。","romaji":"Uriage ga nobizu, sono kaisha wa Nihon shijou kara tettai shita.","german":"Because sales did not grow, the company pulled out of the Japanese market.","furigana":"うりあげ|の|かいしゃ|にほんしじょう|てったい"},{"japanese":"天候が悪化したので、登山隊は山頂を目前に撤退した。","romaji":"Tenkou ga akka shita node, tozantai wa sanchou o mokuzen ni tettai shita.","german":"As the weather worsened, the climbing party turned back just short of the summit.","furigana":"てんこう|あっか|とざんたい|さんちょう|もくぜん|てったい"}],"notes":"撤退する means \"to withdraw (in a planned and complete way)\"; the place given up takes から (前線から撤退する). Besides the military, it is very common in business: 市場から撤退する \"withdraw from the market\", 事業から撤退する. In mountaineering or projects it also means \"to abort and turn back\". 後退する, by contrast, describes a gradual falling back, not a final giving up.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"敵の攻撃を受けて後退した。","romaji":"Teki no kougeki o ukete koutai shita.","german":"They fell back under enemy attack.","furigana":"てき|こうげき|う|こうたい"},{"japanese":"輸出が減って、景気が後退し始めている。","romaji":"Yushutsu ga hette, keiki ga koutai shihajimete iru.","german":"Exports are declining, and the economy is starting to slow down.","furigana":"ゆしゅつ|へ|けいき|こうたい|はじ"},{"japanese":"父の髪の生え際が年々後退している。","romaji":"Chichi no kami no haegiwa ga nennen koutai shite iru.","german":"My father's hairline is receding more every year.","furigana":"ちち|かみ|は|ぎわ|ねんねんこうたい"}],"notes":"後退する means \"to retreat, move backward\" and is the opposite of 前進する. Figuratively it describes a decline, especially in economics: 景気が後退する \"the economy slows down\", 景気後退 \"recession\". It is intransitive; to set something back, one says 後退させる. Unlike 撤退する \"to withdraw completely\", 後退 refers to going back a certain distance. For cars, people mostly say バックする in everyday speech.","levelNote":"Derived from 後退 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"困難を乗り越えて前進する。","romaji":"Konnan o norikoete zenshin suru.","german":"We overcome difficulties and move forward.","furigana":"こんなん|の|こ|ぜんしん"},{"japanese":"兵士たちは雪の中をゆっくり前進した。","romaji":"Heishi-tachi wa yuki no naka o yukkuri zenshin shita.","german":"The soldiers advanced slowly through the snow.","furigana":"へいし|ゆき|なか|ぜんしん"},{"japanese":"長い話し合いの末、交渉はようやく一歩前進した。","romaji":"Nagai hanashiai no sue, koushou wa youyaku ippo zenshin shita.","german":"After long discussions, the negotiations finally moved one step forward.","furigana":"なが|はな|あ|すえ|こうしょう|いっぽぜんしん"}],"notes":"前進する means \"to advance, move forward,\" literally as of troops or vehicles, and very often figuratively for progress: 交渉が一歩前進する \"the negotiations move one step forward.\" The opposite is 後退する. In everyday speech, for physically walking forward people more often say 前に進む; 前進 sounds more formal or motivational, as in speeches. Related is 進歩する \"to make progress (technology, skills).\"","levelNote":"Derived from 前進 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"売上が百億円を突破した。","romaji":"Uriage ga hyakuoku en o toppa shita.","german":"Sales broke through the 10 billion yen mark.","furigana":"うりあげ|ひゃくおくえん|とっぱ"},{"japanese":"一次試験を突破して、面接に進んだ。","romaji":"Ichiji shiken o toppa shite, mensetsu ni susunda.","german":"I got through the first-round exam and moved on to the interview.","furigana":"いちじしけん|とっぱ|めんせつ|すす"},{"japanese":"デモ隊が警察の列を突破した。","romaji":"Demotai ga keisatsu no retsu o toppa shita.","german":"The demonstrators broke through the police line.","furigana":"たい|けいさつ|れつ|とっぱ"}],"notes":"突破する means \"to break through\": literally a barrier or defense (警察の列を突破する), figuratively to pass a limit or mark (売上が百億円を突破する, 入場者が百万人を突破する) or to clear a difficult hurdle (難関を突破する, 予選を突破する). The object takes を. For a new approach to a solution, one says 突破口 \"breakthrough, starting point\". With numbers, 超える is more neutral; 突破 sounds like a success announcement.","levelNote":"Derived from 突破 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"登山隊が山頂に到達した。","romaji":"Tozantai ga sanchō ni tōtatsu shita.","german":"The climbing party reached the summit.","furigana":"とざんたい|さんちょう|とうたつ"},{"japanese":"長い議論の末、ようやく結論に到達した。","romaji":"Nagai giron no sue, youyaku ketsuron ni toutatsu shita.","german":"After a long discussion, we finally arrived at a conclusion.","furigana":"なが|ぎろん|すえ|けつろん|とうたつ"},{"japanese":"探査機が火星に到達するまで、約七か月かかる。","romaji":"Tansaki ga kasei ni toutatsu suru made, yaku nana kagetsu kakaru.","german":"It takes about seven months for the probe to reach Mars.","furigana":"たんさき|かせい|とうたつ|やくなな|げつ"}],"notes":"到達する means to reach a goal after a long way; the goal takes に (山頂に到達する, 結論に到達する). It is more formal than 着く and is also used abstractly for goals, levels or results. 達する is similar but typical for numbers and limits (気温が四十度に達する); 到着する is the arrival of people or vehicles.","levelNote":"Derived from 到達 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"台風が日本列島に接近している。","romaji":"Taifū ga Nihon rettō ni sekkin shite iru.","german":"A typhoon is approaching the Japanese archipelago.","furigana":"たいふう|にほんれっとう|せっきん"},{"japanese":"二台の車が接近しすぎて、あやうくぶつかるところだった。","romaji":"Nidai no kuruma ga sekkin shisugite, ayauku butsukaru tokoro datta.","german":"The two cars came too close and nearly collided.","furigana":"にだい|くるま|せっきん"},{"japanese":"知らない人がしつこく接近してきたので、警察に相談した。","romaji":"Shiranai hito ga shitsukoku sekkin shite kita node, keisatsu ni soudan shita.","german":"A stranger kept approaching me insistently, so I consulted the police.","furigana":"し|ひと|せっきん|けいさつ|そうだん"}],"notes":"接近する means \"to approach\" and is the formal counterpart of 近づく; the target takes に (台風が日本に接近する). It is often heard in news about typhoons, ships or aircraft, but also when someone approaches a person with intent. Figuratively it can mean that abilities or values are nearly equal (実力が接近している). From it comes 接近戦 \"close combat; tight race\".","levelNote":"Derived from 接近 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その国は国際条約から離脱した。","romaji":"Sono kuni wa kokusai jōyaku kara ridatsu shita.","german":"That country withdrew from the international treaty.","furigana":"くに|こくさいじょうやく|りだつ"},{"japanese":"意見の対立から、数人の議員が党を離脱した。","romaji":"Iken no tairitsu kara, suunin no giin ga tou o ridatsu shita.","german":"Because of a conflict of opinion, several legislators left the party.","furigana":"いけん|たいりつ|すうにん|ぎいん|とう|りだつ"},{"japanese":"けがをした選手が、しばらくチームを離脱することになった。","romaji":"Kega o shita senshu ga, shibaraku chiimu o ridatsu suru koto ni natta.","german":"The injured player will be away from the team for a while.","furigana":"せんしゅ|りだつ"}],"notes":"離脱する means to detach oneself from an organization, an agreement or a group; the thing left takes から or を (条約から離脱する, 党を離脱する). In sports it also means that a player drops out due to injury (チームを離脱する). It is more formal than 抜ける or やめる; for clubs and companies one rather says 退会する or 退職する. A well-known example from the news is EU離脱 \"withdrawal from the EU\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二つの川がここで合流する。","romaji":"Futatsu no kawa ga koko de gōryū suru.","german":"Two rivers join here.","furigana":"ふた|かわ|ごうりゅう"},{"japanese":"仕事が終わったら、みんなと駅前で合流するよ。","romaji":"Shigoto ga owattara, minna to ekimae de gouryuu suru yo.","german":"After work, I'll meet up with everyone in front of the station.","furigana":"しごと|お|えきまえ|ごうりゅう"},{"japanese":"高速道路に合流するときは、スピードを上げてください。","romaji":"Kousoku douro ni gouryuu suru toki wa, supiido o agete kudasai.","german":"When merging onto the expressway, please speed up.","furigana":"こうそくどうろ|ごうりゅう|あ"}],"notes":"合流する first means \"to flow together\" of rivers, but it is very often used for people: joining a group later (友達と駅で合流する). In traffic it means \"to merge\" (高速道路に合流する), and signs read 合流注意. The partner takes と, the place one merges into takes に. The opposite is 分岐する \"to branch off\"; 集まる, by contrast, stresses many coming together.","levelNote":"Derived from 合流 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"道がここで二つに分岐する。","romaji":"Michi ga koko de futatsu ni bunki suru.","german":"The road forks into two here.","furigana":"みち|ふた|ぶんき"},{"japanese":"この路線は次の駅で二つに分岐するので、乗り間違えに注意してください。","romaji":"Kono rosen wa tsugi no eki de futatsu ni bunki suru node, norimachigae ni chuui shite kudasai.","german":"This line splits in two at the next station, so be careful not to take the wrong train.","furigana":"ろせん|つぎ|えき|ふた|ぶんき|の|まちが|ちゅうい"},{"japanese":"あの出会いが人生の分岐点になった。","romaji":"Ano deai ga jinsei no bunkiten ni natta.","german":"That encounter became a turning point in my life.","furigana":"であ|じんせい|ぶんきてん"}],"notes":"分岐する means \"to branch, to fork\" of roads, railway lines or cables; the number of branches takes に (二つに分岐する). In daily life one usually just says 分かれる; 分岐 sounds more technical and appears in timetables, road maps and programming (条件分岐 \"conditional branch\"). Very common is 分岐点, literally \"fork\", figuratively a \"turning point\" in life. The opposite is 合流する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"工事中のため迂回しなければならない。","romaji":"Kōjichū no tame ukai shinakereba naranai.","german":"Because of construction, we have to take a detour.","furigana":"こうじちゅう|うかい"},{"japanese":"事故で高速道路が通行止めになり、一般道を迂回した。","romaji":"Jiko de kousoku douro ga tsuukoudome ni nari, ippandou o ukai shita.","german":"The expressway was closed due to an accident, so we took a detour via the regular roads.","furigana":"じこ|こうそくどうろ|つうこうど|いっぱんどう|うかい"},{"japanese":"大雨のため、バスは迂回して運行しています。","romaji":"Ooame no tame, basu wa ukai shite unkou shite imasu.","german":"Because of heavy rain, the buses are running on a detour.","furigana":"おおあめ|うかい|うんこう"}],"notes":"迂回する means \"to make a detour, to go around\", usually because of construction, accidents or closures; the obstacle or route takes を (現場を迂回する). On signs you read 迂回路 \"detour\"; because the kanji is difficult, it is often written う回. In daily life, 遠回りする or 回り道する are more usual, and they are also used without external compulsion. Figuratively, 迂回 can also mean getting around rules or problems.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"歩行者は横断歩道で道路を横断する。","romaji":"Hokousha wa oudan hodou de douro o oudan suru.","german":"Pedestrians cross the road at the crosswalk.","furigana":"ほこうしゃ|おうだんほどう|どうろ|おうだん"},{"japanese":"太平洋を船で横断するのが夢だ。","romaji":"Taiheiyou o fune de oudan suru no ga yume da.","german":"My dream is to cross the Pacific Ocean by ship.","furigana":"たいへいよう|ふね|おうだん|ゆめ"},{"japanese":"子どもが急に道路を横断してきて、慌ててブレーキを踏んだ。","romaji":"Kodomo ga kyuu ni douro o oudan shite kite, awatete bureeki o funda.","german":"A child suddenly crossed the road, and I hurriedly hit the brakes.","furigana":"こ|きゅう|どうろ|おうだん|あわ|ふ"}],"notes":"横断する means \"to cross over\"; the object takes を (道路を横断する, 太平洋を横断する). In everyday life it is known mainly from 横断歩道 \"crosswalk\"; for simply crossing a street people otherwise usually say 渡る. With continents or oceans, 横断 denotes the east-west direction or the crossing; 縦断する, by contrast, is traveling through the length of something.","levelNote":"Derived from 横断 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は自転車で日本を縦断した。","romaji":"Kare wa jitensha de Nihon o juudan shita.","german":"He traveled the length of Japan by bicycle.","furigana":"かれ|じてんしゃ|にほん|じゅうだん"},{"japanese":"大型の台風が日本列島を縦断した。","romaji":"Oogata no taifuu ga Nihon rettou o juudan shita.","german":"A large typhoon passed across the whole length of the Japanese archipelago.","furigana":"おおがた|たいふう|にほんれっとう|じゅうだん"},{"japanese":"アメリカ大陸を南北に縦断する旅に出た。","romaji":"Amerika tairiku o nanboku ni juudan suru tabi ni deta.","german":"I set out on a journey crossing the American continent from north to south.","furigana":"たいりく|なんぼく|じゅうだん|たび|で"}],"notes":"縦断する means to cross an area lengthwise, typically from north to south; the object takes を (日本を縦断する). It is used for trips, expeditions and especially for typhoons that sweep across the whole country (台風が列島を縦断する). The counterpart is 横断する \"to cross sideways\". The word is rather literary and often appears in news and travel reports.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鷹が空を旋回している。","romaji":"Taka ga sora o senkai shite iru.","german":"A hawk is circling in the sky.","furigana":"たか|そら|せんかい"},{"japanese":"飛行機は着陸の前に空港の上空を何度か旋回した。","romaji":"Hikouki wa chakuriku no mae ni kuukou no joukuu o nando ka senkai shita.","german":"Before landing, the plane circled several times above the airport.","furigana":"ひこうき|ちゃくりく|まえ|くうこう|じょうくう|なんど|せんかい"},{"japanese":"船は大きく右に旋回して港に向かった。","romaji":"Fune wa ookiku migi ni senkai shite minato ni mukatta.","german":"The ship made a wide turn to the right and headed for the port.","furigana":"ふね|おお|みぎ|せんかい|みなと|む"}],"notes":"旋回する means \"to circle, to turn in a curve\" and is used mainly for aircraft, birds, helicopters and ships. The area circled over takes を (上空を旋回する), the direction takes に (右に旋回する). The word is technical and literary; in daily life one says 回る or 曲がる. 回転する, by contrast, is turning around one's own axis.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"飛行機が無事に着陸した。","romaji":"Hikōki ga buji ni chakuriku shita.","german":"The plane landed safely.","furigana":"ひこうき|ぶじ|ちゃくりく"},{"japanese":"悪天候のため、飛行機は別の空港に着陸した。","romaji":"Akutenkou no tame, hikouki wa betsu no kuukou ni chakuriku shita.","german":"Because of bad weather, the plane landed at a different airport.","furigana":"あくてんこう|ひこうき|べつ|くうこう|ちゃくりく"},{"japanese":"人類が初めて月に着陸したのは1969年だ。","romaji":"Jinrui ga hajimete tsuki ni chakuriku shita no wa 1969-nen da.","german":"The first time humans landed on the moon was in 1969.","furigana":"じんるい|はじ|つき|ちゃくりく|ねん"}],"notes":"着陸する means \"to land\" (aircraft, spacecraft); the landing place takes に (空港に着陸する, 月に着陸する). The opposite is 離陸する \"to take off\". Landing on water is 着水, ships docking is 着岸. For simply arriving at a destination, one says 到着する; 着陸 refers only to the actual moment of touching down.","levelNote":"Derived from 着陸 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"飛行機が定刻通りに離陸した。","romaji":"Hikōki ga teikoku dōri ni ririku shita.","german":"The plane took off on schedule.","furigana":"ひこうき|ていこくどお|りりく"},{"japanese":"飛行機が離陸するとき、耳が痛くなることがある。","romaji":"Hikouki ga ririku suru toki, mimi ga itaku naru koto ga aru.","german":"When a plane takes off, my ears sometimes hurt.","furigana":"ひこうき|りりく|みみ|いた"},{"japanese":"霧のため、離陸が一時間遅れた。","romaji":"Kiri no tame, ririku ga ichijikan okureta.","german":"Because of fog, takeoff was delayed by an hour.","furigana":"きり|りりく|いちじかんおく"}],"notes":"離陸する means \"to take off\" (aircraft); the departure airport takes から (成田から離陸する). The opposite is 着陸する \"to land\". For departure in the sense of the journey, one says 出発する; 離陸 refers only to the moment the aircraft leaves the ground. The native verb 飛び立つ is similar but sounds more narrative and also suits birds.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"風で帽子が飛ばないように手で押さえた。","romaji":"Kaze de bōshi ga tobanai yō ni te de osaeta.","german":"I held my hat down with my hand so the wind wouldn't blow it away.","furigana":"かぜ|ぼうし|と|て|お"},{"japanese":"来週の会議のために、会議室を押さえておきました。","romaji":"Raishuu no kaigi no tame ni, kaigishitsu o osaete okimashita.","german":"I've already reserved a room for next week's meeting.","furigana":"らいしゅう|かいぎ|かいぎしつ|お"},{"japanese":"試験の前に、大事なポイントだけ押さえておこう。","romaji":"Shiken no mae ni, daiji na pointo dake osaete okou.","german":"Before the exam, let's at least make sure we have the key points down.","furigana":"しけん|まえ|だいじ|お"}],"notes":"押さえる first means \"to hold down or press with the hand\" (帽子を押さえる, 傷口を押さえる). Figuratively it means \"to secure something\", such as a room or a date (会議室を押さえる), and \"to grasp the essentials\" (ポイントを押さえる). For suppressing feelings or keeping costs down, 抑える is usually written (怒りを抑える, 費用を抑える). There is no direct intransitive counterpart.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ドアを叩いて中の人を呼んだ。","romaji":"Doa o tataite naka no hito o yonda.","german":"I knocked on the door and called the person inside.","furigana":"たた|なか|ひと|よ"},{"japanese":"後ろから誰かに肩を叩かれた。","romaji":"Ushiro kara dareka ni kata o tatakareta.","german":"Someone tapped me on the shoulder from behind.","furigana":"うし|だれ|かた|たた"},{"japanese":"その歌手は軽い一言のせいで、ネットでひどく叩かれた。","romaji":"Sono kashu wa karui hitokoto no sei de, netto de hidoku tatakareta.","german":"The singer was heavily criticized online because of a casual remark.","furigana":"かしゅ|かる|ひとこと|たた"}],"notes":"叩く means \"to hit, knock\" with the flat of the hand or an object: ドアを叩く, 肩を叩く, 手を叩く \"to clap,\" 太鼓を叩く \"to drum.\" For punches with the fist or violence against people, 殴る is more usual. Figuratively, 叩く means \"to criticize sharply,\" especially online: ネットで叩かれる. The word is also often written in kana as たたく.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"バッターがボールを遠くに打った。","romaji":"Battā ga bōru o tōku ni utta.","german":"The batter hit the ball far.","furigana":"とお|う"},{"japanese":"スマホでメールを打つのが速いですね。","romaji":"Sumaho de mēru o utsu no ga hayai desu ne.","german":"You type messages on your smartphone fast, don't you!","furigana":"う|はや"},{"japanese":"転んで頭を打ったので、病院に行きました。","romaji":"Koronde atama o utta node, byōin ni ikimashita.","german":"I fell and hit my head, so I went to the hospital.","furigana":"ころ|あたま|う|びょういん|い"}],"notes":"打つ means \"to hit, to strike\" with the hand or a tool: ボールを打つ, 釘を打つ; 頭を打つ is \"to bump one's head\". Also very common is \"to type\": メールを打つ, キーボードを打つ, and 注射を打つ \"to give an injection\". Repeated knocking or hitting is more 叩く, hitting with a fist 殴る; \"to shoot\" is read the same but written 撃つ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"選手がボールを力強く蹴った。","romaji":"Senshu ga booru o chikarazuyoku ketta.","german":"The player kicked the ball hard.","furigana":"せんしゅ|ちからづよ|け"},{"japanese":"弟は寝ている間に布団を蹴ってしまう。","romaji":"Otouto wa nete iru aida ni futon o kette shimau.","german":"My younger brother kicks off the futon in his sleep.","furigana":"おとうと|ね|あいだ|ふとん|け"},{"japanese":"会社は組合の要求をあっさり蹴った。","romaji":"Kaisha wa kumiai no youkyuu o assari ketta.","german":"The company flatly rejected the union's demands.","furigana":"かいしゃ|くみあい|ようきゅう|け"}],"notes":"蹴る means \"to kick\" (ボールを蹴る). Note: although it ends in -eru, it is a godan verb (蹴って, 蹴らない, not 蹴て). Figuratively it means \"to flatly reject\", e.g. an offer or a demand (要求を蹴る), which sounds rather blunt. Intensified forms are 蹴飛ばす \"to kick away\" and 蹴り \"a kick\" as a noun.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"蚊に腕を刺された。","romaji":"Ka ni ude o sasareta.","german":"I was bitten on the arm by a mosquito.","furigana":"か|うで|さ"},{"japanese":"犯人は男性をナイフで刺して逃げた。","romaji":"Hannin wa dansei o naifu de sashite nigeta.","german":"The criminal stabbed the man with a knife and fled.","furigana":"はんにん|だんせい|さ|に"},{"japanese":"焼き鳥は肉を串に刺して焼く。","romaji":"Yakitori wa niku o kushi ni sashite yaku.","german":"For yakitori, you put the meat on skewers and grill it.","furigana":"や|とり|にく|くし|さ|や"}],"notes":"刺す means \"to sting, to pierce\" with something pointed (ナイフで刺す, 串に刺す) and is also used for insect stings, mostly in the passive: 蚊に刺される. The intransitive counterpart is 刺さる \"to get stuck in\" (とげが刺さる). Not to be confused with the identically read 指す \"to point\", 差す \"to put up (an umbrella)\" and 挿す \"to insert (flowers)\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 21"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"布を手で二つに裂いた。","romaji":"Nuno o te de futatsu ni saita.","german":"I tore the cloth in two with my hands.","furigana":"ぬの|て|ふた|さ"},{"japanese":"魚の腹を包丁で裂いて、内臓を取り出した。","romaji":"Sakana no hara o houchou de saite, naizou o toridashita.","german":"I slit open the fish's belly with a kitchen knife and took out the innards.","furigana":"さかな|はら|ほうちょう|さ|ないぞう|と|だ"},{"japanese":"二人の仲を裂こうとする人がいた。","romaji":"Futari no naka o sakou to suru hito ga ita.","german":"There was someone who tried to drive the two of them apart.","furigana":"ふたり|なか|さ|ひと"}],"notes":"裂く means \"to tear (open), to slit\", often along a fiber or line: 布を裂く, 魚の腹を裂く. Figuratively 仲を裂く means \"to drive a couple or friends apart\". The intransitive counterpart is 裂ける (服が裂ける). 破る is more general, \"to tear up, to destroy\" (paper, letters). Do not confuse it with the identically read 割く \"to spare (time)\": 時間を割く.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は怒って手紙を破った。","romaji":"Kare wa okotte tegami o yabutta.","german":"He got angry and tore up the letter.","furigana":"かれ|おこ|てがみ|やぶ"},{"japanese":"友達は約束を破って、結局来なかった。","romaji":"Tomodachi wa yakusoku o yabutte, kekkyoku konakatta.","german":"My friend broke the promise and in the end didn't come.","furigana":"ともだち|やくそく|やぶ|けっきょくこ"},{"japanese":"決勝で前回の優勝チームを破り、初優勝を果たした。","romaji":"Kesshou de zenkai no yuushou chiimu o yaburi, hatsu yuushou o hatashita.","german":"In the final, they beat the previous champions and won their first title.","furigana":"けっしょう|ぜんかい|ゆうしょう|やぶ|はつゆうしょう|は"}],"notes":"破る literally means \"to tear, break\" (紙を破る), but is very often figurative: 約束・ルールを破る \"to break a promise / a rule,\" 記録を破る \"to break a record,\" 相手チームを破る \"to defeat the opposing team,\" 沈黙を破る \"to break the silence.\" The intransitive counterpart is 破れる (紙が破れる, 夢が破れる). For tearing apart along a line, 裂く fits better.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"紙を折って鶴を作った。","romaji":"Kami o otte tsuru o tsukutta.","german":"I folded paper and made a crane.","furigana":"かみ|お|つる|つく"},{"japanese":"スキーで転んで、足の骨を折りました。","romaji":"Sukī de koronde, ashi no hone o orimashita.","german":"I fell while skiing and broke my leg.","furigana":"ころ|あし|ほね|お"},{"japanese":"公園の木の枝を折ってはいけません。","romaji":"Kōen no ki no eda o otte wa ikemasen.","german":"You must not break branches off the trees in the park.","furigana":"こうえん|き|えだ|お"}],"notes":"折る has two basic meanings: \"to fold, to bend\" (紙を折る, 折り紙) and \"to break, to snap off\" (枝を折る, 足の骨を折る \"to break one's leg\"). The intransitive partner is 折れる: 枝が折れる \"the branch breaks\"; 骨が折れる also means \"to be laborious\" figuratively. Glass or plates, by contrast, are broken with 割る, and folding clothes or an umbrella is 畳む.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"膝を曲げてしゃがんだ。","romaji":"Hiza o magete shaganda.","german":"I bent my knees and crouched down.","furigana":"ひざ|ま"},{"japanese":"針金を曲げてハンガーを作った。","romaji":"Harigane o magete hangaa o tsukutta.","german":"I bent a wire to make a hanger.","furigana":"はりがね|ま|つく"},{"japanese":"彼女は誰に何を言われても、自分の考えを曲げなかった。","romaji":"Kanojo wa dare ni nani o iwarete mo, jibun no kangae o magenakatta.","german":"No matter what anyone said to her, she did not change her mind.","furigana":"かのじょ|だれ|なに|い|じぶん|かんが|ま"}],"notes":"曲げる means \"to bend, to flex\" (針金を曲げる, 膝を曲げる); the intransitive counterpart is 曲がる (道が曲がる). Figuratively it means \"to distort\" (事実を曲げる) and, often negated, \"to abandon one's convictions\" (自分の考えを曲げない \"stick to one's opinion\"). The idiom へそを曲げる means \"to sulk, to get in a huff\". 折る, by contrast, means \"to snap, to fold, to break\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"糸が絡んでほどけなくなった。","romaji":"Ito ga karande hodokenaku natta.","german":"The thread got tangled and could not be undone.","furigana":"いと|から"},{"japanese":"この事件には大きなお金が絡んでいるらしい。","romaji":"Kono jiken ni wa ookina okane ga karande iru rashii.","german":"Apparently a lot of money is involved in this case.","furigana":"じけん|おお|かね|から"},{"japanese":"酔った客が店員に絡んでいた。","romaji":"Yotta kyaku ga ten'in ni karande ita.","german":"A drunk customer was picking a fight with the clerk.","furigana":"よ|きゃく|てんいん|から"}],"notes":"絡む is intransitive and means \"to get tangled, entangled\" (糸が絡む). Figuratively, 〜が絡む means \"to be involved, to play a role\" (お金が絡む問題) and 人に絡む means \"to pick on someone, look for a fight\", typical of drunk people. The transitive counterpart is 絡める \"to entwine, coat\" (ソースを絡める); 絡まる is a close synonym for physical tangling.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"髪をゴムで束ねた。","romaji":"Kami wo gomu de tabaneta.","german":"I tied my hair back with a hair elastic.","furigana":"かみ|たば"},{"japanese":"古新聞をひもで束ねて、資源ごみの日に出した。","romaji":"Furushinbun o himo de tabanete, shigen gomi no hi ni dashita.","german":"I bundled the old newspapers with string and put them out on recycling day.","furigana":"ふるしんぶん|たば|しげん|ひ|だ"},{"japanese":"彼は五十人の部員を束ねるキャプテンだ。","romaji":"Kare wa gojuunin no buin o tabaneru kyaputen da.","german":"He is the captain who leads the fifty club members.","furigana":"かれ|ごじゅうにん|ぶいん|たば"}],"notes":"束ねる is transitive: to gather something with を into a bundle, with the binding material marked by で (ひもで束ねる, ゴムで束ねる). Typical for hair, newspapers, letters, banknotes or flowers. Figuratively it means \"to lead a group, hold it together\": チームを束ねる, 組織を束ねる. Related is the noun 束 (たば) \"bundle\"; 結ぶ \"to tie, knot\", by contrast, stresses the knot itself.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"皿を重ねて棚にしまった。","romaji":"Sara wo kasanete tana ni shimatta.","german":"I stacked the plates and put them away on the shelf.","furigana":"さら|かさ|たな"},{"japanese":"何度も練習を重ねて、ようやく本番で成功した。","romaji":"Nando mo renshuu o kasanete, youyaku honban de seikou shita.","german":"After practicing over and over, I finally succeeded in the actual performance.","furigana":"なんど|れんしゅう|かさ|ほんばん|せいこう"},{"japanese":"祖母は年を重ねても、毎朝散歩を続けている。","romaji":"Sobo wa toshi o kasanete mo, maiasa sanpo o tsuzukete iru.","german":"Even in her old age, my grandmother keeps going for a walk every morning.","furigana":"そぼ|とし|かさ|まいあささんぽ|つづ"}],"notes":"重ねる is transitive: to lay or stack things on top of each other with を (皿を重ねる, 手を重ねる). The intransitive counterpart is 重なる \"to lie on top of each other; to coincide\" (予定が重なる). The figurative meaning \"to repeat, to accumulate\" is very common: 努力を重ねる, 失敗を重ねる, 年を重ねる (\"to grow older\", a gentle paraphrase). For piling things into a heap or loading, 積む is more usual.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"本を棚にきれいに並べた。","romaji":"Hon o tana ni kirei ni narabeta.","german":"I arranged the books neatly on the shelf.","furigana":"ほん|たな|なら"},{"japanese":"テーブルに皿を並べてください。","romaji":"Tēburu ni sara o narabete kudasai.","german":"Please set the plates side by side on the table.","furigana":"さら|なら"}],"notes":"並べる is a transitive ichidan verb: 並べます, 並べて. It means to arrange things in order or side by side. を marks the things, に the place. きれいに describes the neat arrangement. The intransitive 並ぶ, by contrast, means to stand in a line.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"川が山から海へ流れている。","romaji":"Kawa ga yama kara umi e nagarete iru.","german":"The river flows from the mountains to the sea.","furigana":"かわ|やま|うみ|なが"},{"japanese":"店内には静かなジャズが流れていた。","romaji":"Tennai ni wa shizuka na jazu ga nagarete ita.","german":"Quiet jazz was playing in the shop.","furigana":"てんない|しず|なが"},{"japanese":"雨のせいで、楽しみにしていた試合が流れてしまった。","romaji":"Ame no sei de, tanoshimi ni shite ita shiai ga nagarete shimatta.","german":"Because of the rain, the game I had been looking forward to was called off.","furigana":"あめ|たの|しあい|なが"}],"notes":"流れる is intransitive: water, tears, time or music \"flow, run\"; the transitive counterpart is 流す (涙を流す, 音楽を流す). Typical are 川が流れる, 汗が流れる, 時間が流れる and 店内に音楽が流れる \"music is playing in the shop.\" The stretch flowed through takes を (町の中を川が流れる). Colloquially, 計画・試合が流れる also means \"to be cancelled, to fall through.\"","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子供が川で溺れそうになった。","romaji":"Kodomo ga kawa de obore sō ni natta.","german":"A child nearly drowned in the river.","furigana":"こども|かわ|おぼ"},{"japanese":"彼は仕事を失ってから、酒に溺れるようになった。","romaji":"Kare wa shigoto o ushinatte kara, sake ni oboreru you ni natta.","german":"After losing his job, he began to drown himself in drink.","furigana":"かれ|しごと|うしな|さけ|おぼ"},{"japanese":"海で溺れている人を見たら、すぐにライフセーバーに知らせてください。","romaji":"Umi de oborete iru hito o mitara, sugu ni raifuseebaa ni shirasete kudasai.","german":"If you see someone drowning in the sea, please notify a lifeguard right away.","furigana":"うみ|おぼ|ひと|み|し"}],"notes":"溺れる is intransitive and describes being in danger of drowning or drowning in water; the place takes で (海で溺れる). You often hear 溺れそうになる \"to nearly drown\"; actual death is usually expressed with 溺れて死ぬ or 溺死する. Figuratively, 〜に溺れる means \"to be addicted to something, to give oneself up to it\": 酒に溺れる, 恋に溺れる - always with a negative connotation. Well-known proverb: 溺れる者は藁をもつかむ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"強風で木が倒れた。","romaji":"Kyōfū de ki ga taoreta.","german":"The tree fell over in the strong wind.","furigana":"きょうふう|き|たお"},{"japanese":"父が仕事中に倒れて、病院に運ばれました。","romaji":"Chichi ga shigotochū ni taorete, byōin ni hakobaremashita.","german":"My father collapsed at work and was taken to the hospital.","furigana":"ちち|しごとちゅう|たお|びょういん|はこ"},{"japanese":"地震のとき、本棚が倒れないか心配です。","romaji":"Jishin no toki, hondana ga taorenai ka shinpai desu.","german":"I'm worried that the bookshelf will fall over during an earthquake.","furigana":"じしん|ほんだな|たお|しんぱい"}],"notes":"倒れる is intransitive: something standing falls or tips over (木が倒れる, 本棚が倒れる), or a person collapses or becomes seriously ill (過労で倒れる). The transitive partner is 倒す \"to knock over, to topple\". Stumbling and falling while walking is more 転ぶ, and falling from above 落ちる; the cause takes で: 強風で倒れる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"岩が崖から突き出している。","romaji":"Iwa ga gake kara tsukidashite iru.","german":"A rock juts out from the cliff.","furigana":"いわ|がけ|つ|だ"},{"japanese":"電車の窓から顔を突き出すのは危ないよ。","romaji":"Densha no mado kara kao o tsukidasu no wa abunai yo.","german":"It is dangerous to stick your head out of a train window.","furigana":"でんしゃ|まど|かお|つ|だ|あぶ"},{"japanese":"店員は万引きした男を警察に突き出した。","romaji":"Ten'in wa manbiki shita otoko o keisatsu ni tsukidashita.","german":"The shop clerk handed the shoplifter over to the police.","furigana":"てんいん|まんび|おとこ|けいさつ|つ|だ"}],"notes":"突き出す is transitive: to \"stick out\" a body part or object forward (窓から顔を突き出す, 口を突き出す \"pout\"). The intransitive counterpart is 突き出る \"to protrude\", but 〜が突き出している is also used for protruding things. A second fixed meaning is \"to hand someone over (to the police)\": 犯人を警察に突き出す. Do not confuse it with 差し出す \"to hold out, to hand over\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"猫を部屋から追い出した。","romaji":"Neko wo heya kara oidashita.","german":"I chased the cat out of the room.","furigana":"ねこ|へや|お|だ"},{"japanese":"家賃を払えなくて、アパートを追い出された。","romaji":"Yachin o haraenakute, apaato o oidasareta.","german":"Because I couldn't pay the rent, I was kicked out of the apartment.","furigana":"やちん|はら|お|だ"},{"japanese":"嫌な考えを頭から追い出そうとしたが、なかなか眠れなかった。","romaji":"Iya na kangae o atama kara oidasou to shita ga, nakanaka nemurenakatta.","german":"I tried to push the unpleasant thoughts out of my head, but I couldn't fall asleep for a long time.","furigana":"いや|かんが|あたま|お|だ|ねむ"}],"notes":"追い出す is transitive: to drive or throw a person or animal (を) out of a place; the place takes から or を (部屋から追い出す, 家を追い出す). Very common in the passive: アパートを追い出される \"to be thrown out of one's apartment\", チームから追い出される. The word stresses compulsion and unwillingness; its official, formal counterpart is 追放する \"to banish, expel\". Figuratively one can also drive thoughts out: 頭から追い出す.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"走って友達に追いついた。","romaji":"Hashitte tomodachi ni oitsuita.","german":"I ran and caught up with my friend.","furigana":"はし|ともだち|お"},{"japanese":"注文が多すぎて、生産が追いつかない。","romaji":"Chuumon ga oosugite, seisan ga oitsukanai.","german":"There are too many orders, and production can't keep up.","furigana":"ちゅうもん|おお|せいさん|お"},{"japanese":"入院で授業を休んだので、早くみんなに追いつきたい。","romaji":"Nyuuin de jugyou o yasunda node, hayaku minna ni oitsukitai.","german":"I missed classes because I was in the hospital, so I want to catch up with everyone quickly.","furigana":"にゅういん|じゅぎょう|やす|はや|お"}],"notes":"追いつく is intransitive: whom or what you catch up with takes に (前の車に追いつく); also written 追い付く. Figuratively it means \"to catch up, keep pace\" in level, performance or demand: 先進国に追いつく, 生産が需要に追いつかない - especially often negated. The counterpart 追い越す \"to overtake\" is transitive and takes を. 間に合う, on the other hand, means \"to be in time.\"","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"犯人は現場から逃げた。","romaji":"Hannin wa genba kara nigeta.","german":"The criminal fled from the scene.","furigana":"はんにん|げんば|に"},{"japanese":"火事のときは、すぐに外へ逃げてください。","romaji":"Kaji no toki wa, sugu ni soto e nigete kudasai.","german":"In case of fire, please escape outside immediately.","furigana":"かじ|そと|に"},{"japanese":"嫌なことから逃げてばかりいてはだめだよ。","romaji":"Iya na koto kara nigete bakari ite wa dame da yo.","german":"You can't just keep running away from unpleasant things.","furigana":"いや|に"}],"notes":"逃げる is intransitive \"to flee, to run away\"; the starting point takes から (現場から逃げる), the destination へ/に (外へ逃げる). Animals also \"run away\": 犬が逃げた. Figuratively it means \"to shirk, to evade\": 問題から逃げる, 責任から逃げる. The transitive partner is 逃がす \"to let escape, to set free\", and 逃げ出す emphasizes suddenly running away.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大事なチャンスを逃してしまった。","romaji":"Daiji na chansu wo nogashite shimatta.","german":"I missed an important chance.","furigana":"だいじ|のが"},{"japanese":"飲み会が長引いて、終電を逃した。","romaji":"Nomikai ga nagabiite, shuuden o nogashita.","german":"The party dragged on, and I missed the last train.","furigana":"の|かい|ながび|しゅうでん|のが"},{"japanese":"先生の説明を聞き逃さないように、メモを取った。","romaji":"Sensei no setsumei o kikinogasanai you ni, memo o totta.","german":"I took notes so as not to miss anything of the teacher's explanation.","furigana":"せんせい|せつめい|き|のが|と"}],"notes":"逃す is transitive and means above all \"to miss an opportunity, let it slip\": チャンスを逃す, 終電を逃す, 優勝を逃す. Attached to a verb stem, it means \"to fail to ...\": 聞き逃す \"to fail to hear\", 見逃す \"to overlook\". Distinguish it from 逃がす \"to set free, let escape\" (魚を逃がす). For means of transport 乗り遅れる is also common; 逃す stresses the loss.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子供が木の後ろに隠れた。","romaji":"Kodomo ga ki no ushiro ni kakureta.","german":"The child hid behind a tree.","furigana":"こども|き|うし|かく"},{"japanese":"月が雲に隠れて、辺りが急に暗くなった。","romaji":"Tsuki ga kumo ni kakurete, atari ga kyuu ni kuraku natta.","german":"The moon went behind the clouds, and the surroundings suddenly became dark.","furigana":"つき|くも|かく|あた|きゅう|くら"},{"japanese":"この路地には隠れた名店が多い。","romaji":"Kono roji ni wa kakureta meiten ga ooi.","german":"There are many hidden gem restaurants in this alley.","furigana":"ろじ|かく|めいてん|おお"}],"notes":"隠れる is intransitive: to hide or be hidden; the hiding place and the thing covering take に (木の後ろに隠れる, 雲に隠れる). The transitive counterpart is 隠す \"to hide, conceal\" (秘密を隠す). Attributively 隠れた means \"hidden, unknown\": 隠れた名店, 隠れた才能. The children's game of hide-and-seek is かくれんぼ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"星が夜空に輝いている。","romaji":"Hoshi ga yozora ni kagayaite iru.","german":"The stars are shining in the night sky.","furigana":"ほし|よぞら|かがや"},{"japanese":"子供たちはプレゼントを見て、目を輝かせた。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa purezento o mite, me o kagayakaseta.","german":"The children's eyes lit up when they saw the presents.","furigana":"こども|み|め|かがや"},{"japanese":"彼女はオリンピックで金メダルに輝いた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa orinpikku de kin medaru ni kagayaita.","german":"She was crowned with a gold medal at the Olympics.","furigana":"かのじょ|きん|かがや"}],"notes":"輝く is intransitive: the sun, stars or gems \"shine, sparkle\" (星が夜空に輝く). Figuratively, eyes or faces shine with joy: 目が輝く, 喜びに輝く; the causative form 輝かせる appears in 目を輝かせる. A set phrase is 〜に輝く \"to win a prize or title\": 金メダルに輝く, 優勝に輝く. Compared with 光る (\"to glow, flash\"), 輝く sounds more positive and elevated.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"湖に山が映っている。","romaji":"Mizuumi ni yama ga utsutte iru.","german":"The mountain is reflected in the lake.","furigana":"みずうみ|やま|うつ"},{"japanese":"昨日のニュースに、うちの近所の商店街が映っていた。","romaji":"Kinō no nyūsu ni, uchi no kinjo no shōtengai ga utsutte ita.","german":"The shopping street in our neighborhood appeared in yesterday's news.","furigana":"きのう|きんじょ|しょうてんがい|うつ"},{"japanese":"彼の態度は、周りの人には冷たく映ったようだ。","romaji":"Kare no taido wa, mawari no hito ni wa tsumetaku utsutta yō da.","german":"His attitude seems to have come across as cold to the people around him.","furigana":"かれ|たいど|まわ|ひと|つめ|うつ"}],"notes":"映る is intransitive: something is reflected or appears on a surface or screen, and the place takes に: 鏡に顔が映る, テレビに映る, 画面が映らない \"no picture\". The transitive counterpart is 映す (鏡に姿を映す). Do not confuse it with 写る \"to appear in a photo\" (写真に写る) and 移る \"to move, to shift\". Figuratively, 〜の目に…と映る or 〜く映る means \"to appear ... to someone\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"黒板の内容をノートに写した。","romaji":"Kokuban no naiyō o nōto ni utsushita.","german":"I copied the content of the blackboard into my notebook.","furigana":"こくばん|ないよう|うつ"},{"japanese":"友達の宿題を写してはいけません。","romaji":"Tomodachi no shukudai o utsushite wa ikemasen.","german":"You must not copy a friend's homework.","furigana":"ともだち|しゅくだい|うつ"},{"japanese":"旅行中に、きれいな景色をたくさんカメラで写しました。","romaji":"Ryokōchū ni, kirei na keshiki o takusan kamera de utsushimashita.","german":"On my trip I took many pictures of beautiful scenery with my camera.","furigana":"りょこうちゅう|けしき|うつ"}],"notes":"写す means \"to copy, to transcribe\": 黒板をノートに写す, 友達の宿題を写す; also \"to photograph, to capture\" (景色を写す), although in everyday speech 写真を撮る is said more often. The intransitive partner is 写る \"to appear in, to be in a photo\": 写真に写る. Not to be confused with the identically read 映す \"to reflect, to project\" (鏡に映す) and 移す \"to move, to transfer\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鏡が光を反射する。","romaji":"Kagami ga hikari wo hansha suru.","german":"The mirror reflects light.","furigana":"かがみ|ひかり|はんしゃ"},{"japanese":"雪に反射した日差しがまぶしくて、目を開けていられなかった。","romaji":"Yuki ni hansha shita hizashi ga mabushikute, me o akete irarenakatta.","german":"The sunlight reflected off the snow was so dazzling that I couldn't keep my eyes open.","furigana":"ゆき|はんしゃ|ひざ|め|あ"},{"japanese":"ボールが飛んできたので、反射的に目を閉じた。","romaji":"Booru ga tonde kita node, hanshateki ni me o tojita.","german":"When the ball came flying, I shut my eyes reflexively.","furigana":"と|はんしゃてき|め|と"}],"notes":"反射する means \"to reflect, throw back\" and is used mainly for light, sound and heat. Transitive: 鏡が光を反射する; intransitive: 光が水面に反射する \"the light is reflected on the water surface\". In biology 反射 is a \"reflex\"; 反射的に means \"reflexively, involuntarily\". For the mirror image itself, one says 映る/映す, not 反射する.","levelNote":"Derived from 反射 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女はピアノで美しい曲を奏でた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa piano de utsukushii kyoku wo kanadeta.","german":"She played a beautiful piece on the piano.","furigana":"かのじょ|うつく|きょく|かな"},{"japanese":"森の中では、鳥たちが美しいハーモニーを奏でていた。","romaji":"Mori no naka de wa, toritachi ga utsukushii haamonii o kanadete ita.","german":"In the forest, the birds were making beautiful harmonies.","furigana":"もり|なか|とり|うつく|かな"},{"japanese":"古いバイオリンが奏でる音色に、観客は聞き入った。","romaji":"Furui baiorin ga kanaderu neiro ni, kankyaku wa kikiitta.","german":"The audience listened intently to the tone of the old violin.","furigana":"ふる|かな|ねいろ|かんきゃく|き|い"}],"notes":"奏でる is an elevated, literary verb for \"to play an instrument, to let music sound\"; the piece takes を, the instrument takes で (ギターで曲を奏でる). In daily life one simply says 弾く (piano, guitar), 吹く (flute) or 演奏する. 奏でる stresses the beautiful sound and is often used figuratively, e.g. when birds or the wind \"play\" melodies.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"花の匂いを嗅いだ。","romaji":"Hana no nioi wo kaida.","german":"I smelled the flowers.","furigana":"はな|にお|か"},{"japanese":"犬が地面のにおいをくんくん嗅いでいる。","romaji":"Inu ga jimen no nioi o kunkun kaide iru.","german":"The dog is sniffing at the ground.","furigana":"いぬ|じめん|か"},{"japanese":"牛乳が古くなっていないか、においを嗅いで確かめた。","romaji":"Gyuunyuu ga furuku natte inai ka, nioi o kaide tashikameta.","german":"I smelled the milk to check whether it had gone bad.","furigana":"ぎゅうにゅう|ふる|か|たし"}],"notes":"嗅ぐ is transitive: actively \"to smell, to sniff at something\", mostly with 匂い/におい as object: 匂いを嗅ぐ. This differs from におう (匂う/臭う), where the thing itself \"smells, is fragrant or stinks\" (このシャツはにおう). Figuratively 嗅ぎつける means \"to get wind of something\". Since the kanji is rare, the verb is often written in kana as かぐ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"重いリュックを背負って山を登った。","romaji":"Omoi ryukku wo seotte yama wo nobotta.","german":"I climbed the mountain carrying a heavy rucksack on my back.","furigana":"おも|せお|やま|のぼ"},{"japanese":"父は事業に失敗して、多額の借金を背負った。","romaji":"Chichi wa jigyou ni shippai shite, tagaku no shakkin o seotta.","german":"My father failed in his business and took on a large debt.","furigana":"ちち|じぎょう|しっぱい|たがく|しゃっきん|せお"},{"japanese":"彼は日本中の期待を背負って試合に出た。","romaji":"Kare wa Nihonjuu no kitai o seotte shiai ni deta.","german":"He entered the match carrying the hopes of all Japan.","furigana":"かれ|にほんじゅう|きたい|せお|しあい|で"}],"notes":"背負う is transitive: to carry something or someone on the back with を (リュックを背負う, 子供を背負う); colloquially there is also the reading しょう (しょって). Figuratively it means \"to take a burden or responsibility upon oneself\": 借金を背負う, 責任を背負う, 期待を背負う. Carrying on the shoulder, by contrast, is 担ぐ; giving a child a piggyback ride is also おんぶする.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"祭りで神輿を担いだ。","romaji":"Matsuri de mikoshi wo katsuida.","german":"I carried the portable shrine at the festival.","furigana":"まつ|みこし|かつ"},{"japanese":"彼は重い米袋を肩に担いで運んだ。","romaji":"Kare wa omoi komebukuro o kata ni katsuide hakonda.","german":"He carried a heavy bag of rice on his shoulder.","furigana":"かれ|おも|こめぶくろ|かた|かつ|はこ"},{"japanese":"受験の前日、縁起を担いでカツ丼を食べた。","romaji":"Juken no zenjitsu, engi o katsuide katsudon o tabeta.","german":"The day before the entrance exam, I ate katsudon for good luck.","furigana":"じゅけん|ぜんじつ|えんぎ|かつ|どん|た"}],"notes":"担ぐ is transitive: to carry something on the shoulder with を (荷物を肩に担ぐ, 神輿を担ぐ). There are also fixed figurative meanings: 縁起を担ぐ \"to be superstitious, to pay attention to good omens\", 人を担ぐ \"to pull someone's leg\" and \"to put someone up as a candidate\". Unlike 背負う (on the back), with 担ぐ the load rests on the shoulder. The kanji 担 is also known from 担当.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"疲れて壁に寄りかかった。","romaji":"Tsukarete kabe ni yorikakatta.","german":"I was tired and leaned against the wall.","furigana":"つか|かべ|よ"},{"japanese":"電車のドアに寄りかからないでください。","romaji":"Densha no doa ni yorikakaranaide kudasai.","german":"Please do not lean against the train doors.","furigana":"でんしゃ|よ"},{"japanese":"いつまでも親に寄りかかっていないで、自分で働きなさい。","romaji":"Itsu made mo oya ni yorikakatte inaide, jibun de hatarakinasai.","german":"Stop leaning on your parents forever and work for yourself.","furigana":"おや|よ|じぶん|はたら"}],"notes":"寄りかかる is intransitive: what one leans on takes に (壁に寄りかかる, ドアに寄りかかる); also written 寄り掛かる. Figuratively it means \"to rely on someone, depend on someone\", often critically: 親に寄りかかって生活する. Similar is もたれる, which also means \"to lean against\" but in addition \"to sit heavily on the stomach\" (胃がもたれる).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は彼女の前で跪いてプロポーズした。","romaji":"Kare wa kanojo no mae de hizamazuite puropōzu shita.","german":"He knelt before her and proposed.","furigana":"かれ|かのじょ|まえ|ひざまず"},{"japanese":"女性は教会でひざまずいて、静かに祈った。","romaji":"Josei wa kyoukai de hizamazuite, shizuka ni inotta.","german":"The woman knelt in the church and prayed quietly.","furigana":"じょせい|きょうかい|しず|いの"},{"japanese":"騎士は王の前にひざまずいた。","romaji":"Kishi wa ou no mae ni hizamazuita.","german":"The knight knelt before the king.","furigana":"きし|おう|まえ"}],"notes":"跪く means \"to kneel down\", mostly as a sign of reverence, submission, or when proposing marriage. It is intransitive; the person before whom one kneels takes の前で or の前に. The kanji is rare, so it is mostly written in kana as ひざまずく. For mere kneeling in daily life (e.g. while cleaning), ひざをつく is more usual; formal sitting on the knees is 正座する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"秋に米を収穫する。","romaji":"Aki ni kome wo shūkaku suru.","german":"We harvest rice in autumn.","furigana":"あき|こめ|しゅうかく"},{"japanese":"週末は家族で畑のトマトを収穫した。","romaji":"Shuumatsu wa kazoku de hatake no tomato o shuukaku shita.","german":"On the weekend, the family harvested the tomatoes in the field.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|かぞく|はたけ|しゅうかく"},{"japanese":"今回のセミナーでは多くのことを学べて、大きな収穫があった。","romaji":"Konkai no seminaa de wa ooku no koto o manabete, ookina shuukaku ga atta.","german":"I was able to learn a lot at this seminar, so it was a great gain.","furigana":"こんかい|おお|まな|おお|しゅうかく"}],"notes":"収穫する means \"to harvest\" (米を収穫する, 野菜を収穫する); in everyday speech, for fruit and vegetables people also say 採る, for rice 刈り取る. The noun 収穫 also denotes the harvest itself (今年の収穫, 収穫量). Very common is the figurative use \"yield, gain\" from an experience: 今回の旅行は大きな収穫があった. In this sense 収穫 is mostly used as a noun with がある.","levelNote":"Derived from 収穫 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"テーラーがスーツを仕立てた。","romaji":"Tērā ga sūtsu wo shitateta.","german":"The tailor made a suit.","furigana":"した"},{"japanese":"母は私の成人式のために着物を仕立ててくれた。","romaji":"Haha wa watashi no seijinshiki no tame ni kimono o shitatete kureta.","german":"My mother had a kimono made for me for my Coming-of-Age ceremony.","furigana":"はは|わたし|せいじんしき|きもの|した"},{"japanese":"その事件は後に映画に仕立てられた。","romaji":"Sono jiken wa nochi ni eiga ni shitaterareta.","german":"The incident was later made into a movie.","furigana":"じけん|のち|えいが|した"}],"notes":"仕立てる is transitive and means above all \"to tailor, make (clothing)\": スーツを仕立てる; when having it made, one says 仕立ててもらう. The noun 仕立て is found in 仕立て屋 \"tailor's shop\". Figuratively it means \"to turn into something\" (小説をドラマに仕立てる) or, pejoratively, \"to cast someone as something\" (犯人に仕立てる, strengthened as 仕立て上げる). Simple sewing is 縫う.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"帰宅してすぐに部屋着に着替えた。","romaji":"Kitaku shite sugu ni heyagi ni kigaeta.","german":"As soon as I got home, I changed into my loungewear.","furigana":"きたく|へやぎ|きが"},{"japanese":"汗をかいたので、シャツを着替えた。","romaji":"Ase o kaita node, shatsu o kigaeta.","german":"I had sweated, so I changed my shirt.","furigana":"あせ|きが"},{"japanese":"選手たちはロッカールームでユニフォームに着替えている。","romaji":"Senshutachi wa rokkaaruumu de yunifoomu ni kigaete iru.","german":"The players are changing into their uniforms in the locker room.","furigana":"せんしゅ|きが"}],"notes":"着替える means \"to change clothes\"; the new clothing takes に (パジャマに着替える), and the garment being changed can take を (シャツを着替える). Besides きがえる, the reading きかえる is also common. The noun 着替え means \"changing clothes\" and also \"a change of clothes\" (着替えを持っていく). Not to be confused with 着る \"to put on\" and 脱ぐ \"to take off\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 21"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は美しいドレスを纏っていた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa utsukushii doresu wo matotte ita.","german":"She was wearing a beautiful dress.","furigana":"かのじょ|うつく|まと"},{"japanese":"あの俳優は、どこか寂しげな雰囲気をまとっている。","romaji":"Ano haiyuu wa, dokoka sabishige na fun'iki o matotte iru.","german":"That actor has a somewhat lonely air about him.","furigana":"はいゆう|さび|ふんいき"},{"japanese":"山は白い雪をまとい、冬の姿に変わった。","romaji":"Yama wa shiroi yuki o matoi, fuyu no sugata ni kawatta.","german":"The mountain donned white snow and changed into its winter appearance.","furigana":"やま|しろ|ゆき|ふゆ|すがた|か"}],"notes":"纏う is an elevated, rather literary verb for \"to wear, to wrap oneself in\": ドレスを纏う, 鎧を纏う; it is mostly written in kana as まとう. The figurative use for an aura or mood is common: 不思議な雰囲気をまとう. For clothing in daily life one simply says 着る; 纏う sounds poetic and is found in novels or advertising copy. Related is まとわりつく \"to cling to someone, to stick to\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"クリスマスツリーを飾った。","romaji":"Kurisumasu tsurī o kazatta.","german":"I decorated the Christmas tree.","furigana":"かざ"},{"japanese":"部屋に家族の写真を飾っています。","romaji":"Heya ni kazoku no shashin o kazatte imasu.","german":"I have family photos displayed in my room.","furigana":"へや|かぞく|しゃしん|かざ"},{"japanese":"彼は飾らない人で、誰とでもすぐ仲良くなります。","romaji":"Kare wa kazaranai hito de, dare to demo sugu nakayoku narimasu.","german":"He is unpretentious and quickly becomes friends with anyone.","furigana":"かれ|かざ|ひと|だれ|なかよ"}],"notes":"飾る means \"to decorate, to adorn\" (ツリーを飾る, 部屋を花で飾る) and likewise \"to put up or hang as decoration\": 写真を飾る, 絵を壁に飾る, 玄関に花を飾る. Merely putting something somewhere without a decorative purpose is 置く. The noun 飾り is \"decoration\", and 飾らない人 figuratively describes a natural, unpretentious person.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"木に美しい模様を彫った。","romaji":"Ki ni utsukushii moyō wo hotta.","german":"I carved a beautiful pattern into the wood.","furigana":"き|うつく|もよう|ほ"},{"japanese":"結婚指輪の内側に二人の名前を彫ってもらった。","romaji":"Kekkon yubiwa no uchigawa ni futari no namae o hotte moratta.","german":"I had both our names engraved on the inside of our wedding rings.","furigana":"けっこんゆびわ|うちがわ|ふたり|なまえ|ほ"},{"japanese":"この仏像は一本の木から彫られたそうだ。","romaji":"Kono butsuzou wa ippon no ki kara horareta sou da.","german":"This Buddhist statue is said to have been carved from a single piece of wood.","furigana":"ぶつぞう|いっぽん|き|ほ"}],"notes":"彫る is transitive: \"to carve, to engrave, to chisel\" in wood, stone or metal. The material takes に (木に模様を彫る), the result takes を (仏像を彫る, 名前を彫る). Colloquially, getting a tattoo is also 彫る (タトゥーを彫る). Do not confuse it with the identically read 掘る \"to dig\" (穴を掘る). Related nouns are 彫刻 \"sculpture\" and 木彫り \"wood carving\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鉛筆をナイフで削った。","romaji":"Enpitsu wo naifu de kezutta.","german":"I sharpened the pencil with a knife.","furigana":"えんぴつ|けず"},{"japanese":"不景気のため、会社は広告費を大幅に削った。","romaji":"Fukeiki no tame, kaisha wa koukokuhi o oohaba ni kezutta.","german":"Because of the recession, the company slashed its advertising budget.","furigana":"ふけいき|かいしゃ|こうこくひ|おおはば|けず"},{"japanese":"試験前は睡眠時間を削って勉強した。","romaji":"Shiken mae wa suimin jikan o kezutte benkyou shita.","german":"Before the exam, I cut into my sleeping time to study.","furigana":"しけんまえ|すいみんじかん|けず|べんきょう"}],"notes":"削る is transitive: to remove material in thin layers - \"to plane, to scrape, to sharpen\": 鉛筆を削る, 木を削る, かつお節を削る. The figurative meaning \"to cut back, to economize\" is very common: 予算を削る, 人件費を削る, 睡眠時間を削る. It also means \"to strike out, to remove\" in texts: 不要な部分を削る. The related 削除する \"to delete\" is more formal and is used above all for data and text.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"包丁を砥石で研いだ。","romaji":"Hōchō wo toishi de toida.","german":"I sharpened the kitchen knife on a whetstone.","furigana":"ほうちょう||と"},{"japanese":"炊く前に、お米を三回ほど研いでください。","romaji":"Taku mae ni, okome o sankai hodo toide kudasai.","german":"Please wash the rice about three times before cooking it.","furigana":"た|まえ|こめ|さんかい|と"},{"japanese":"猫が柱で爪を研いでいる。","romaji":"Neko ga hashira de tsume o toide iru.","german":"The cat is sharpening its claws on a pillar.","furigana":"ねこ|はしら|つめ|と"}],"notes":"研ぐ is transitive: to \"grind, sharpen\" blades on a whetstone (包丁を研ぐ, 刀を研ぐ). In the kitchen, 米を研ぐ means \"to wash rice\" by rubbing it in water - a very common use. Figuratively it appears in 爪を研ぐ \"to sharpen one's claws\", i.e. to lie in wait for a chance. For polishing surfaces or brushing teeth, one says 磨く, not 研ぐ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"毎日ジムで体を鍛えている。","romaji":"Mainichi jimu de karada wo kitaete iru.","german":"I work out my body at the gym every day.","furigana":"まいにち|からだ|きた"},{"japanese":"新人時代、厳しい先輩に鍛えられた。","romaji":"Shinjin jidai, kibishii senpai ni kitaerareta.","german":"When I was a newcomer, I was trained hard by a strict senior.","furigana":"しんじんじだい|きび|せんぱい|きた"},{"japanese":"職人が熱した鉄をたたいて刀を鍛える。","romaji":"Shokunin ga nesshita tetsu o tataite katana o kitaeru.","german":"The craftsman forges a sword by hammering heated iron.","furigana":"しょくにん|ねっ|てつ|かたな|きた"}],"notes":"鍛える is transitive: originally \"to forge, harden\" metal (刀を鍛える), today mostly \"to train, toughen\": 体を鍛える, 筋肉を鍛える, 精神を鍛える. People can also be 鍛える - \"to train hard, drill\": 新人を鍛える, passive 厳しく鍛えられる. Unlike 練習する or トレーニングする, 鍛える stresses becoming stronger in the long term through effort.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"職人が金属を鋳て仏像を作った。","romaji":"Shokunin ga kinzoku wo ite butsuzō wo tsukutta.","german":"The craftsman cast metal and made a Buddhist statue.","furigana":"しょくにん|きんぞく|い|ぶつぞう|つく"},{"japanese":"この寺の鐘は、江戸時代にこの町で鋳られたものだ。","romaji":"Kono tera no kane wa, Edo jidai ni kono machi de irareta mono da.","german":"The bell of this temple was cast here in town during the Edo period.","furigana":"てら|かね|えどじだい|まち|い"},{"japanese":"古代の人々は溶かした銅を型に流して貨幣を鋳た。","romaji":"Kodai no hitobito wa tokashita dou o kata ni nagashite kahei o ita.","german":"People in ancient times poured molten copper into molds and cast coins.","furigana":"こだい|ひとびと|と|どう|かた|なが|かへい|い"}],"notes":"鋳る is transitive and means \"to cast (metal)\", i.e. to pour molten metal into a mold: 鐘を鋳る, 貨幣を鋳る. The verb is rare today and sounds archaic; modern speech uses 鋳造する or paraphrases with 型に流し込む. Compounds are more common, such as 鋳型 \"casting mold\" and 鋳物 \"cast goods\". Do not confuse it with the similar-sounding いる \"to be/to need\" or 射る \"to shoot (an arrow)\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鉄板を溶接して組み合わせた。","romaji":"Teppan wo yōsetsu shite kumiawaseta.","german":"Steel plates were welded together.","furigana":"てっぱん|ようせつ|く|あ"},{"japanese":"兄は工場で車の部品を溶接する仕事をしている。","romaji":"Ani wa koujou de kuruma no buhin o yousetsu suru shigoto o shite iru.","german":"My older brother works in a factory welding car parts.","furigana":"あに|こうじょう|くるま|ぶひん|ようせつ|しごと"},{"japanese":"溶接をするときは、必ず保護メガネをかけてください。","romaji":"Yousetsu o suru toki wa, kanarazu hogo megane o kakete kudasai.","german":"When welding, be sure to wear protective goggles.","furigana":"ようせつ|かなら|ほご"}],"notes":"溶接する means \"to weld\", i.e. to join metal parts by heat; the workpiece takes を, the counterpart takes に or と (パイプを板に溶接する). The noun 溶接 appears in 溶接工 \"welder\" and 溶接機 \"welding machine\". To be distinguished are はんだ付け \"soldering\" and 接着する \"to glue\". It is a technical term from industry and trades and rather rare in daily life.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"棚をネジで壁に固定した。","romaji":"Tana wo neji de kabe ni kotei shita.","german":"I fixed the shelf to the wall with screws.","furigana":"たな|かべ|こてい"},{"japanese":"夜景を撮るときは、カメラを三脚に固定したほうがいい。","romaji":"Yakei o toru toki wa, kamera o sankyaku ni kotei shita hou ga ii.","german":"When shooting night views, it is better to fix the camera on a tripod.","furigana":"やけい|と|さんきゃく|こてい"},{"japanese":"骨折した腕をギプスで固定された。","romaji":"Kossetsu shita ude o gipusu de kotei sareta.","german":"My broken arm was immobilized with a cast.","furigana":"こっせつ|うで|こてい"}],"notes":"固定する is transitive: to \"fasten, fix\" something with を at a place; the place takes に, the means takes で (棚をネジで壁に固定する). The resulting state is expressed with 〜が固定されている. Figuratively 固定 means \"fixed, unchanging\": 固定給 \"fixed salary\", 固定電話 \"landline phone\", 固定観念 \"preconceived notion\". In everyday speech, for simple fastening, 留める is also used.","levelNote":"Derived from 固定 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"部屋の温度を測った。","romaji":"Heya no ondo o hakatta.","german":"I measured the temperature of the room.","furigana":"へや|おんど|はか"},{"japanese":"熱がありそうなので、体温を測ってみた。","romaji":"Netsu ga arisou na node, taion o hakatte mita.","german":"It seemed like I had a fever, so I took my temperature.","furigana":"ねつ|たいおん|はか"},{"japanese":"百メートル走のタイムをストップウォッチで計った。","romaji":"Hyaku meetoru sou no taimu o sutoppuwotchi de hakatta.","german":"I timed the 100-meter dash with a stopwatch."}],"notes":"はかる \"to measure\" has different kanji depending on what is measured: 測る for length, height, depth, temperature or blood pressure (身長を測る, 体温を測る), 計る for time and countable things (時間を計る, タイムを計る), 量る for weight and volume (体重を量る). In everyday writing 測る and 計る are often mixed up; when in doubt, write はかる in kana. The verb is transitive with を and has no intransitive counterpart. Not to be confused with 図る \"to aim at, plan.\"","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"料理のために砂糖を量った。","romaji":"Ryōri no tame ni satō wo hakatta.","german":"I measured out the sugar for cooking.","furigana":"りょうり|さとう|はか"},{"japanese":"郵便局で荷物の重さを量ってもらった。","romaji":"Yuubinkyoku de nimotsu no omosa o hakatte moratta.","german":"I had my package weighed at the post office.","furigana":"ゆうびんきょく|にもつ|おも|はか"},{"japanese":"計量カップで水を200ミリリットル量ってください。","romaji":"Keiryou kappu de mizu o nihyaku miririttoru hakatte kudasai.","german":"Please measure 200 milliliters of water with the measuring cup.","furigana":"けいりょう|みず|はか"}],"notes":"量る is transitive: weight or quantity \"to weigh, measure out\" (砂糖を量る, 体重を量る). The identically read はかる verbs divide up like this: 量る for weight and volume, 測る for length, height, depth and temperature, 計る for time and numbers, 図る for \"to plan, aim at.\" For body weight, both 量る and 測る are common. In the sense of \"to estimate, gauge,\" the compound 推し量る is usually used.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"電卓で合計金額を計算した。","romaji":"Dentaku de gōkei kingaku wo keisan shita.","german":"I calculated the total amount with a calculator.","furigana":"でんたく|ごうけいきんがく|けいさん"},{"japanese":"渋滞の時間も計算に入れて、早めに家を出た。","romaji":"Juutai no jikan mo keisan ni irete, hayame ni ie o deta.","german":"I left home early, factoring in the time for traffic jams.","furigana":"じゅうたい|じかん|けいさん|い|はや|いえ|で"},{"japanese":"彼女の涙は全部計算された演技だった。","romaji":"Kanojo no namida wa zenbu keisan sareta engi datta.","german":"Her tears were all a calculated act.","furigana":"かのじょ|なみだ|ぜんぶけいさん|えんぎ"}],"notes":"計算する is transitive: to \"calculate, work out\" something with を (合計を計算する, 費用を計算する). Set phrases are 計算に入れる \"to factor in, take into account\" and 計算が合う \"the numbers add up.\" Said of people, 計算された and 計算高い mean \"calculating, scheming\" (negative). In math class, 計算 is calculating in general, e.g. 計算問題; mental arithmetic is 暗算.","levelNote":"Derived from 計算 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"年間の売上高を算出した。","romaji":"Nenkan no uriagedaka o sanshutsu shita.","german":"The annual sales were calculated.","furigana":"ねんかん|うりあげだか|さんしゅつ"},{"japanese":"引っ越しにかかる費用をざっと算出してみた。","romaji":"Hikkoshi ni kakaru hiyō o zatto sanshutsu shite mita.","german":"I roughly worked out how much the move would cost.","furigana":"ひ|こ|ひよう|さんしゅつ"},{"japanese":"この数値は、どのような方法で算出されたのですか。","romaji":"Kono sūchi wa, dono yō na hōhō de sanshutsu sareta no desu ka?","german":"By what method was this figure calculated?","furigana":"すうち|ほうほう|さんしゅつ"}],"notes":"算出する means \"to calculate, to work out\", i.e. to determine a value or amount by calculation; transitive with を: 費用を算出する, 平均値を算出する. It sounds factual and formal (reports, statistics, business); in daily life 計算する is enough. 計算 stresses the process of calculating, 算出 the result worked out. Note: the identically read 産出 \"production, output\" (of raw materials) is a different word.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"修理費用を見積もってもらった。","romaji":"Shūri hiyō wo mitsumotte moratta.","german":"I had the repair costs estimated.","furigana":"しゅうりひよう|みつ"},{"japanese":"少なく見積もっても、完成まで三か月はかかるだろう。","romaji":"Sukunaku mitsumotte mo, kansei made sankagetsu wa kakaru darou.","german":"Even at a conservative estimate, it will take at least three months to finish.","furigana":"すく|みつ|かんせい|さん|げつ"},{"japanese":"引っ越しの前に、三つの業者に見積もりを頼んだ。","romaji":"Hikkoshi no mae ni, mittsu no gyousha ni mitsumori o tanonda.","german":"Before the move, I asked three companies for quotes.","furigana":"ひ|こ|まえ|みっ|ぎょうしゃ|みつ|たの"}],"notes":"見積もる is transitive: to \"estimate, to assess\" costs, time or quantities with を (費用を見積もる). Common is the noun 見積もり (also 見積り) \"cost estimate\": 見積もりを取る, 見積書. The phrase 少なく見積もっても means \"at a conservative estimate, at least\". Unlike 推測する (to guess), it concerns concrete figures; 評価する means \"to evaluate\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"状況から原因を推測した。","romaji":"Jōkyō kara gen'in wo suisoku shita.","german":"I inferred the cause from the situation.","furigana":"じょうきょう|げんいん|すいそく"},{"japanese":"彼の表情から、試験の結果は推測できた。","romaji":"Kare no hyoujou kara, shiken no kekka wa suisoku dekita.","german":"I could tell the exam result from his expression.","furigana":"かれ|ひょうじょう|しけん|けっか|すいそく"},{"japanese":"推測で物を言うのはやめてください。","romaji":"Suisoku de mono o iu no wa yamete kudasai.","german":"Please don't speak based on mere guesses.","furigana":"すいそく|もの|い"}],"notes":"推測する is transitive: to \"guess, infer\" something from available clues; the basis takes から (表情から気持ちを推測する). Often with a quotation: 〜と推測する, in a factual-formal style 〜と推測される. 推測で物を言う means \"to talk off the cuff\". Similar are 推定する (more technical, e.g. 推定年齢), 予想する (about the future) and 想像する (free imagination).","levelNote":"Derived from 推測 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"条件を仮定して計算する。","romaji":"Jōken wo katei shite keisan suru.","german":"I calculate by assuming certain conditions.","furigana":"じょうけん|かてい|けいさん"},{"japanese":"もし宝くじで一億円当たったと仮定してみよう。","romaji":"Moshi takarakuji de ichioku-en atatta to katei shite miyou.","german":"Let's suppose we won a hundred million yen in the lottery.","furigana":"たから|いちおくえんあ|かてい"},{"japanese":"仮定の話には、お答えできません。","romaji":"Katei no hanashi ni wa, okotae dekimasen.","german":"I can't answer hypothetical questions.","furigana":"かてい|はなし|こた"}],"notes":"仮定する means \"to assume, to presuppose\" in the sense of a hypothetical assumption, typically with a quotation: 〜と仮定する \"assuming that ...\". The noun 仮定 means \"assumption, hypothesis\": 仮定の話 \"a purely hypothetical question\". In school grammar, 仮定形 is the conditional form (〜ば). To be distinguished are 想定する \"to anticipate, to assume a scenario\" and 推測する \"to guess\".","levelNote":"Derived from 仮定 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"実験の結果を検証する。","romaji":"Jikken no kekka wo kenshō suru.","german":"We will verify the results of the experiment.","furigana":"じっけん|けっか|けんしょう"},{"japanese":"専門家が事故の原因を詳しく検証している。","romaji":"Senmonka ga jiko no gen'in o kuwashiku kenshou shite iru.","german":"Experts are examining the cause of the accident in detail.","furigana":"せんもんか|じこ|げんいん|くわ|けんしょう"},{"japanese":"その噂が本当かどうか、番組で検証してみた。","romaji":"Sono uwasa ga hontou ka dou ka, bangumi de kenshou shite mita.","german":"On the program, we tested whether that rumor is true.","furigana":"うわさ|ほんとう|ばんぐみ|けんしょう"}],"notes":"検証する is transitive: to systematically \"verify, to check\" a claim, result or cause with を (仮説を検証する, データを検証する). It sounds factual and formal and is typical of science, media and government offices; 現場検証 is a police \"examination of the scene\". For simple checks in daily life one rather says 確認する or チェックする; 点検する refers to technical inspections.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"データベースと照合して本人確認をする。","romaji":"Dētabēsu to shōgō shite honnin kakunin wo suru.","german":"We verify a person's identity by checking against the database.","furigana":"しょうごう|ほんにんかくにん"},{"japanese":"経理の担当者が領収書と帳簿を照合した。","romaji":"Keiri no tantousha ga ryoushuusho to choubo o shougou shita.","german":"The accounting clerk cross-checked the receipts against the books.","furigana":"けいり|たんとうしゃ|りょうしゅうしょ|ちょうぼ|しょうごう"},{"japanese":"現場に残された指紋を、警察が容疑者のものと照合した。","romaji":"Genba ni nokosareta shimon o, keisatsu ga yougisha no mono to shougou shita.","german":"The police compared the fingerprints left at the scene with the suspect's.","furigana":"げんば|のこ|しもん|けいさつ|ようぎしゃ|しょうごう"}],"notes":"照合する is transitive: to compare two things in order to establish a match; the object of comparison takes と (領収書と帳簿を照合する, データベースと照合する). Typical for administration, the police and bookkeeping: 指紋を照合する, 本人照合. In everyday speech people rather say 確かめる or 比べる; 照合 sounds official and technical.","levelNote":"Derived from 照合 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"車を定期的に点検する。","romaji":"Kuruma wo teikiteki ni tenken suru.","german":"The car is inspected regularly.","furigana":"くるま|ていきてき|てんけん"},{"japanese":"登山の前に、装備をもう一度点検した。","romaji":"Tozan no mae ni, soubi o mou ichido tenken shita.","german":"Before the mountain climb, I checked my equipment once more.","furigana":"とざん|まえ|そうび|いちどてんけん"},{"japanese":"エレベーターは現在点検中のため、ご利用いただけません。","romaji":"Erebeetaa wa genzai tenkenchuu no tame, goriyou itadakemasen.","german":"The elevator is currently under inspection and cannot be used.","furigana":"げんざいてんけんちゅう|りよう"}],"notes":"点検する is transitive: to \"check, inspect\" equipment, vehicles or facilities with を point by point (車を点検する, ガス設備を点検する). Common are 定期点検 \"regular inspection\" and 点検中 \"out of service for maintenance\". Unlike 検査 (examination, test, also medical) and 確認 (checking in general), 点検 is about function and safety.","levelNote":"Derived from 点検 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"報告書の誤りを修正した。","romaji":"Hōkokusho no ayamari wo shūsei shita.","german":"I corrected the errors in the report.","furigana":"ほうこくしょ|あやま|しゅうせい"},{"japanese":"状況の変化に合わせて、計画を少し修正しましょう。","romaji":"Joukyou no henka ni awasete, keikaku o sukoshi shuusei shimashou.","german":"Let's revise the plan a little to fit the change in the situation.","furigana":"じょうきょう|へんか|あ|けいかく|すこ|しゅうせい"},{"japanese":"アプリの不具合は次のアップデートで修正される予定です。","romaji":"Apuri no fuguai wa tsugi no appudeeto de shuusei sareru yotei desu.","german":"The app's bug is scheduled to be fixed in the next update.","furigana":"ふぐあい|つぎ|しゅうせい|よてい"}],"notes":"修正する is transitive: to \"correct, improve, adjust\" errors or defects with を (誤りを修正する, 計画を修正する). Unlike 訂正する (correcting false statements), 修正 also covers adjustments and revisions without an actual error, such as 予算を修正する or photo editing (写真を修正する). In everyday speech people often simply say 直す.","levelNote":"Derived from 修正 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"記事の間違いを訂正した。","romaji":"Kiji no machigai wo teisei shita.","german":"I corrected the mistakes in the article.","furigana":"きじ|まちが|ていせい"},{"japanese":"先ほどの発言に誤りがありましたので、訂正させていただきます。","romaji":"Sakihodo no hatsugen ni ayamari ga arimashita node, teisei sasete itadakimasu.","german":"There was an error in my previous statement, so I would like to correct it.","furigana":"さき|はつげん|あやま|ていせい"},{"japanese":"書類の数字を間違えたので、二重線を引いて訂正した。","romaji":"Shorui no suuji o machigaeta node, nijuusen o hiite teisei shita.","german":"I wrote a wrong number in the document, so I crossed it out with a double line and corrected it.","furigana":"しょるい|すうじ|まちが|にじゅうせん|ひ|ていせい"}],"notes":"訂正する is transitive: to \"correct, set right\" wrong statements in speech or writing (誤りを訂正する, 発言を訂正する). Typical for newspapers (訂正記事), forms (訂正印 \"correction stamp\") and public statements. Unlike 修正する, it is always about something wrong being replaced with the right thing. Politely one says: 訂正させていただきます.","levelNote":"Derived from 訂正 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先生が学生の作文を添削した。","romaji":"Sensei ga gakusei no sakubun wo tensaku shita.","german":"The teacher corrected the student's composition.","furigana":"せんせい|がくせい|さくぶん|てんさく"},{"japanese":"就職活動のエントリーシートを先輩に添削してもらった。","romaji":"Shuushoku katsudou no entorii shiito o senpai ni tensaku shite moratta.","german":"I had a senior student go over and improve my job-hunting entry sheet.","furigana":"しゅうしょくかつどう|せんぱい|てんさく"},{"japanese":"日本語で日記を書いて、オンラインで添削してもらっている。","romaji":"Nihongo de nikki o kaite, onrain de tensaku shite moratte iru.","german":"I write a diary in Japanese and have it corrected online.","furigana":"にほんご|にっき|か|てんさく"}],"notes":"添削する means to \"correct, improve\" other people's texts by adding (添) and deleting (削), typically done by teachers: 作文を添削する, 履歴書を添削してもらう. Unlike 校正 (proofreading for typos and sentence errors), it concerns content, expression and style, often with comments. Well known is 通信添削 \"correspondence course with corrections sent back\". One corrects one's own mistakes rather with 直す or 修正する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"印刷前に原稿を校正する。","romaji":"Insatsu mae ni genkō wo kōsei suru.","german":"The manuscript is proofread before printing.","furigana":"いんさつまえ|げんこう|こうせい"},{"japanese":"出版社で雑誌の記事を校正するアルバイトをしている。","romaji":"Shuppansha de zasshi no kiji o kousei suru arubaito o shite iru.","german":"I have a part-time job at a publisher proofreading magazine articles.","furigana":"しゅっぱんしゃ|ざっし|きじ|こうせい"},{"japanese":"正確に測るために、機械を定期的に校正する必要がある。","romaji":"Seikaku ni hakaru tame ni, kikai o teikiteki ni kousei suru hitsuyou ga aru.","german":"To measure accurately, the machine must be calibrated regularly.","furigana":"せいかく|はか|きかい|ていきてき|こうせい|ひつよう"}],"notes":"校正する means \"to proofread\": to compare printer's proofs with the manuscript and correct typos or sentence errors (原稿を校正する, ゲラを校正する). It belongs to publishing and printing; a proofreader is a 校正者. In technology 校正 also means \"to calibrate\" (計器を校正する). Unlike 添削, it is not about style or content but about formal errors.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"動画を編集してから公開した。","romaji":"Dōga wo henshū shite kara kōkai shita.","german":"I edited the video before publishing it.","furigana":"どうが|へんしゅう|こうかい"},{"japanese":"彼女は女性向けの雑誌を編集している。","romaji":"Kanojo wa josei-muke no zasshi o henshuu shite iru.","german":"She edits a women's magazine.","furigana":"かのじょ|じょせいむ|ざっし|へんしゅう"},{"japanese":"このファイルは読み取り専用なので、編集できません。","romaji":"Kono fairu wa yomitori sen'you na node, henshuu dekimasen.","german":"This file is read-only, so it can't be edited.","furigana":"よ|と|せんよう|へんしゅう"}],"notes":"編集する is transitive: to \"edit, to revise, to compile\" texts, books, films or data with を (雑誌を編集する, 動画を編集する). Job titles are 編集者 \"editor\", 編集長 \"editor-in-chief\" and 編集部 \"editorial department\". On a computer, 編集 appears as the menu item \"Edit\". Unlike 修正 (fixing errors), it is about shaping and putting together content.","levelNote":"Derived from 編集 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不要なファイルを削除した。","romaji":"Fuyō na fairu wo sakujo shita.","german":"I deleted the unnecessary files.","furigana":"ふよう|さくじょ"},{"japanese":"退会したメンバーの名前を名簿から削除した。","romaji":"Taikai shita menbaa no namae o meibo kara sakujo shita.","german":"I removed the names of members who had left from the roster.","furigana":"たいかい|なまえ|めいぼ|さくじょ"},{"japanese":"不適切なコメントは管理者によって削除されました。","romaji":"Futekisetsu na komento wa kanrisha ni yotte sakujo saremashita.","german":"Inappropriate comments were deleted by the administrator.","furigana":"ふてきせつ|かんりしゃ|さくじょ"}],"notes":"削除する is transitive: to \"delete, to strike out\" data, passages of text or names with を (ファイルを削除する, 名簿から名前を削除する). It is more formal than 消す and typical of computers, contracts and legal texts. Related is 削る, which besides \"to plane\" also means \"to cut back, to strike out\". The opposite is 追加する \"to add\".","levelNote":"Derived from 削除 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"文書に画像を挿入した。","romaji":"Bunsho ni gazō wo sōnyū shita.","german":"I inserted an image into the document.","furigana":"ぶんしょ|がぞう|そうにゅう"},{"japanese":"ATMにカードを挿入して、暗証番号を入力してください。","romaji":"ATM ni kaado o sounyuu shite, anshou bangou o nyuuryoku shite kudasai.","german":"Please insert your card into the ATM and enter your PIN.","furigana":"そうにゅう|あんしょうばんごう|にゅうりょく"},{"japanese":"この映画の挿入歌がとても人気になった。","romaji":"Kono eiga no sounyuuka ga totemo ninki ni natta.","german":"The insert song of this movie became very popular.","furigana":"えいが|そうにゅうか|にんき"}],"notes":"挿入する is transitive: to \"insert, to put in\" something with を into something else (に) (文書に画像を挿入する, カードを挿入する). It sounds formal to technical and appears with machines, software or medical instruments. In daily life one mostly says 入れる or 差し込む. A well-known derived word is 挿入歌 \"a song used within a film or series\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"データから必要な情報を抽出する。","romaji":"Dēta kara hitsuyō na jōhō wo chūshutsu suru.","german":"Necessary information is extracted from the data.","furigana":"ひつよう|じょうほう|ちゅうしゅつ"},{"japanese":"この機械は豆からコーヒーをゆっくり抽出する。","romaji":"Kono kikai wa mame kara koohii o yukkuri chuushutsu suru.","german":"This machine slowly extracts coffee from the beans.","furigana":"きかい|まめ|ちゅうしゅつ"},{"japanese":"全国から無作為に抽出した千人にアンケートを行った。","romaji":"Zenkoku kara musakui ni chuushutsu shita sennin ni ankeeto o okonatta.","german":"We conducted a survey of a thousand people randomly selected from across the country.","furigana":"ぜんこく|むさくい|ちゅうしゅつ|せんにん|おこな"}],"notes":"抽出する is transitive: to \"draw out, to extract\" something from a set with を; the source takes から (データから情報を抽出する). In chemistry and cooking it refers to extracting substances (コーヒーを抽出する). In statistics, 無作為に抽出する means \"to select at random\" (sampling). It is a formal technical word; in daily life one rather says 取り出す or 選ぶ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"品質の良いものを選別する。","romaji":"Hinshitsu no yoi mono wo senbetsu suru.","german":"Items of good quality are selected.","furigana":"ひんしつ|よ|せんべつ"},{"japanese":"収穫したみかんを大きさごとに選別して箱に詰める。","romaji":"Shuukaku shita mikan o ookisa goto ni senbetsu shite hako ni tsumeru.","german":"The harvested mandarins are sorted by size and packed in boxes.","furigana":"しゅうかく|おお|せんべつ|はこ|つ"},{"japanese":"工場では機械が不良品を自動で選別している。","romaji":"Koujou de wa kikai ga furyouhin o jidou de senbetsu shite iru.","german":"In the factory, a machine automatically sorts out defective products.","furigana":"こうじょう|きかい|ふりょうひん|じどう|せんべつ"}],"notes":"選別する is transitive: to \"sort, to screen\" a set according to certain criteria (大きさで選別する, 不良品を選別する). It is about classifying by quality, size or suitability - both picking out the good pieces and sorting out the bad ones. Garbage, by contrast, is separated with 分別する (ぶんべつ). 選ぶ is the general \"to choose\"; 選別 sounds factual and, applied to people, slightly derogatory (応募者を選別する \"to weed out applicants\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"重要な書類を金庫に保管する。","romaji":"Jūyō na shorui wo kinko ni hokan suru.","german":"Important documents are kept in the safe.","furigana":"じゅうよう|しょるい|きんこ|ほかん"},{"japanese":"落とし物は駅の事務所で一週間保管されます。","romaji":"Otoshimono wa eki no jimusho de isshuukan hokan saremasu.","german":"Lost items are kept at the station office for a week.","furigana":"お|もの|えき|じむしょ|いっしゅうかんほかん"},{"japanese":"この薬は直射日光を避けて、涼しい場所で保管してください。","romaji":"Kono kusuri wa chokusha nikkou o sakete, suzushii basho de hokan shite kudasai.","german":"Please store this medicine in a cool place away from direct sunlight.","furigana":"くすり|ちょくしゃにっこう|さ|すず|ばしょ|ほかん"}],"notes":"保管する is transitive: to keep objects (を) safely; the place takes に or で (金庫に保管する, 涼しい場所で保管する). It stresses care and responsibility, e.g. for lost property, documents or other people's belongings. Unlike 保存する (to preserve, conserve, save), it is about safe custody in one place; in everyday speech people often say しまう or 取っておく.","levelNote":"Derived from 保管 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ファイルをパソコンに保存した。","romaji":"Fairu wo pasokon ni hozon shita.","german":"I saved the file on my computer.","furigana":"ほぞん"},{"japanese":"カレーは冷凍すれば一か月ほど保存できる。","romaji":"Karee wa reitou sureba ikkagetsu hodo hozon dekiru.","german":"If you freeze curry, you can keep it for about a month.","furigana":"れいとう|いっ|げつ|ほぞん"},{"japanese":"この町では江戸時代の古い町並みが大切に保存されている。","romaji":"Kono machi de wa Edo jidai no furui machinami ga taisetsu ni hozon sarete iru.","german":"In this town, the old townscape of the Edo period is carefully preserved.","furigana":"まち|えどじだい|ふる|まちな|たいせつ|ほぞん"}],"notes":"保存する is transitive: to \"preserve, conserve\" something with を in good condition over a long time - food (冷凍して保存する), buildings and cultural assets (町並みを保存する). On the computer it means \"to save\" (ファイルを保存する, 上書き保存 \"overwrite and save\"). Related are 保存食 \"preserved food\" and 保存料 \"preservative.\" 保管する, by contrast, stresses safe keeping in one place.","levelNote":"Derived from 保存 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"会議の内容を記録した。","romaji":"Kaigi no naiyō wo kiroku shita.","german":"I recorded the contents of the meeting.","furigana":"かいぎ|ないよう|きろく"},{"japanese":"夏休みの宿題で、毎日の天気と気温を記録した。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi no shukudai de, mainichi no tenki to kion o kiroku shita.","german":"For my summer vacation homework, I recorded the weather and temperature every day.","furigana":"なつやす|しゅくだい|まいにち|てんき|きおん|きろく"},{"japanese":"今日の東京は38度を記録し、今年一番の暑さとなった。","romaji":"Kyou no Toukyou wa sanjuuhachi-do o kiroku shi, kotoshi ichiban no atsusa to natta.","german":"Tokyo recorded 38 degrees today, the hottest of the year.","furigana":"きょう|とうきょう|ど|きろく|ことしいちばん|あつ"}],"notes":"記録する is transitive: to \"record, document, log\" events or data with を in writing or by technical means (会議の内容を記録する, 毎日の気温を記録する). In sports and news it means \"to reach, register a value\": 過去最高を記録する. The noun 記録 also means \"record\": 記録を破る, 記録を更新する. For sound and video recordings, people more often say 録音する and 録画する respectively.","levelNote":"Derived from 記録 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ウェブサイトに会員登録した。","romaji":"Webusaito ni kaiin tōroku shita.","german":"I registered as a member on the website.","furigana":"かいいんとうろく"},{"japanese":"新しい友達の電話番号をスマホに登録した。","romaji":"Atarashii tomodachi no denwa bangou o sumaho ni touroku shita.","german":"I saved my new friend's phone number in my smartphone.","furigana":"あたら|ともだち|でんわばんごう|とうろく"},{"japanese":"引っ越したら、二週間以内に市役所で住民登録をしなければならない。","romaji":"Hikkoshitara, nishuukan inai ni shiyakusho de juumin touroku o shinakereba naranai.","german":"After moving, you have to register your residence at city hall within two weeks.","furigana":"ひ|こ|にしゅうかんいない|しやくしょ|じゅうみんとうろく"}],"notes":"登録する is transitive: to officially \"sign up, register, enter\" oneself or something; the list or place takes に (サイトに登録する, スマホに番号を登録する). Compounds such as 会員登録, ユーザー登録, 住民登録 and チャンネル登録 (\"to subscribe\" on video platforms) are very common. Unlike 申し込む (\"to apply, to sign up\"), 登録 emphasizes the permanent entry in a registry.","levelNote":"Derived from 登録 (N5) (source: N5; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新聞に広告を掲載した。","romaji":"Shinbun ni kōkoku wo keisai shita.","german":"An advertisement was placed in the newspaper.","furigana":"しんぶん|こうこく|けいさい"},{"japanese":"彼の論文が有名な科学雑誌に掲載された。","romaji":"Kare no ronbun ga yuumei na kagaku zasshi ni keisai sareta.","german":"His paper was published in a well-known science journal.","furigana":"かれ|ろんぶん|ゆうめい|かがくざっし|けいさい"},{"japanese":"最新の情報は、ホームページに掲載しております。","romaji":"Saishin no jouhou wa, hoomupeeji ni keisai shite orimasu.","german":"The latest information is posted on our homepage.","furigana":"さいしん|じょうほう|けいさい"}],"notes":"掲載する is transitive: to \"print, publish, run\" articles, advertisements or photos with を in a medium (に): 新聞に広告を掲載する, サイトに情報を掲載する. Very common in the passive: 論文が雑誌に掲載される. Unlike 発表する (to announce) and 公開する (to make publicly accessible), 掲載 always refers to appearing in a newspaper, magazine or on a website. A common notice: 無断掲載禁止 \"reproduction without permission prohibited\".","levelNote":"Derived from 掲載 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"研究の成果を公開した。","romaji":"Kenkyū no seika wo kōkai shita.","german":"The research results were made public.","furigana":"けんきゅう|せいか|こうかい"},{"japanese":"この映画は来月全国で公開される。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa raigetsu zenkoku de koukai sareru.","german":"This movie will be released nationwide next month.","furigana":"えいが|らいげつぜんこく|こうかい"},{"japanese":"普段は入れないお城の庭が、今週末だけ一般に公開されます。","romaji":"Fudan wa hairenai oshiro no niwa ga, konshuumatsu dake ippan ni koukai saremasu.","german":"The castle garden, normally off limits, will be open to the public only this weekend.","furigana":"ふだん|はい|しろ|にわ|こんしゅうまつ|いっぱん|こうかい"}],"notes":"公開する means \"to make accessible to the public\": data, information, research results or a website are 公開される. For films, 公開 means the theatrical release (映画が公開される), for buildings or gardens the opening to visitors (一般公開). The opposite is 非公開 \"not open to the public\". 発表する, by contrast, stresses announcing or presenting, 公表する the official announcement of facts. Note: 後悔する \"to regret\" sounds the same.","levelNote":"Derived from 公開 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"上司に進捗状況を報告した。","romaji":"Jōshi ni shinchoku jōkyō wo hōkoku shita.","german":"I reported the progress to my boss.","furigana":"じょうし|しんちょくじょうきょう|ほうこく"},{"japanese":"調査の結果は来週の会議で報告します。","romaji":"Chousa no kekka wa raishuu no kaigi de houkoku shimasu.","german":"We will report the results of the survey at next week's meeting.","furigana":"ちょうさ|けっか|らいしゅう|かいぎ|ほうこく"},{"japanese":"合格したことを両親に報告した。","romaji":"Goukaku shita koto o ryoushin ni houkoku shita.","german":"I reported to my parents that I had passed.","furigana":"ごうかく|りょうしん|ほうこく"}],"notes":"報告する means \"to report on results or the state of a matter,\" typically to superiors, an organization or people who should know: 上司に進捗を報告する, 結果について報告する. In everyday work it belongs to the mnemonic 報連相 (報告・連絡・相談). 連絡する is merely contacting someone or passing on information, and 知らせる is more everyday. You can also 報告する personal news in a formal-happy way, e.g. an exam result or a marriage to one's parents. The written document is 報告書.","levelNote":"Derived from 報告 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"合格者にメールで通知した。","romaji":"Gōkakusha ni mēru de tsūchi shita.","german":"We notified the successful candidates by e-mail.","furigana":"ごうかくしゃ|つうち"},{"japanese":"パスワードが変更されると、利用者に通知する。","romaji":"Pasuwādo ga henkō sareru to, riyōsha ni tsūchi suru.","german":"When the password is changed, the user is notified.","furigana":"へんこう|りようしゃ|つうち"},{"japanese":"会社は従業員に工場の閉鎖を通知した。","romaji":"Kaisha wa juugyouin ni koujou no heisa o tsuuchi shita.","german":"The company notified the employees of the closure of the factory.","furigana":"かいしゃ|じゅうぎょういん|こうじょう|へいさ|つうち"}],"notes":"通知する means \"to notify officially\", mostly in writing or electronically: 人に結果を通知する. It sounds factual and official; 連絡する is more general and personal, 知らせる is colloquial. As a noun it often appears: 合格通知 \"notice of acceptance\", 通知表 \"school report card\", スマホの通知 \"push notification\". The counterpart from the recipient's side is 通知を受ける.","levelNote":"Derived from 通知 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"会議の資料を参加者に配布した。","romaji":"Kaigi no shiryō wo sankasha ni haifu shita.","german":"The materials for the meeting were distributed to the participants.","furigana":"かいぎ|しりょう|さんかしゃ|はいふ"},{"japanese":"駅前で新商品のサンプルを無料で配布している。","romaji":"Ekimae de shinshouhin no sanpuru o muryou de haifu shite iru.","german":"Samples of a new product are being handed out free of charge in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|しんしょうひん|むりょう|はいふ"},{"japanese":"マスクは受付で一人一枚ずつ配布します。","romaji":"Masuku wa uketsuke de hitori ichimai zutsu haifu shimasu.","german":"Masks are handed out at the reception, one per person.","furigana":"うけつけ|ひとりいちまい|はいふ"}],"notes":"配布する is the formal word for \"to distribute, hand out (to many people)\": documents, flyers, samples or relief supplies are 配布される. Pattern: X を人に配布する. In everyday speech people mostly just say 配る; 配布 sounds organizational or official. Typical are 配布資料 \"handout\" and 無料配布 \"free distribution\". Do not confuse it with 配達 \"to deliver\".","levelNote":"Derived from 配布 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"アンケートでデータを収集した。","romaji":"Ankēto de dēta wo shūshū shita.","german":"We collected data through a questionnaire.","furigana":"しゅうしゅう"},{"japanese":"父は古い切手を収集するのが趣味だ。","romaji":"Chichi wa furui kitte o shuushuu suru no ga shumi da.","german":"My father's hobby is collecting old stamps.","furigana":"ちち|ふる|きって|しゅうしゅう|しゅみ"},{"japanese":"この地区では燃えるごみは月曜と木曜に収集されます。","romaji":"Kono chiku de wa moeru gomi wa getsuyou to mokuyou ni shuushuu saremasu.","german":"In this district, burnable garbage is collected on Mondays and Thursdays.","furigana":"ちく|も|げつよう|もくよう|しゅうしゅう"}],"notes":"収集する means \"to collect systematically\": data or information (情報を収集する), collectibles such as stamps or art, and in everyday life garbage collection (ごみの収集, 収集日). It is more formal than 集める, which fits any kind of collecting. 採集 is used for collecting insects or plants in nature. Often in the passive: ごみが収集される.","levelNote":"Derived from 収集 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"詳しくはマニュアルを参照してください。","romaji":"Kuwashiku wa manyuaru wo sanshō shite kudasai.","german":"For details, please refer to the manual.","furigana":"くわ|さんしょう"},{"japanese":"この報告書は政府の統計を参照して作成した。","romaji":"Kono houkokusho wa seifu no toukei o sanshou shite sakusei shita.","german":"This report was prepared with reference to government statistics.","furigana":"ほうこくしょ|せいふ|とうけい|さんしょう|さくせい"},{"japanese":"辞書を参照しながら、古い手紙を読んだ。","romaji":"Jisho o sanshou shinagara, furui tegami o yonda.","german":"I read the old letter while consulting a dictionary.","furigana":"じしょ|さんしょう|ふる|てがみ|よ"}],"notes":"参照する means \"to consult (a source), look up, refer to\": マニュアルを参照する, データを参照して作る. In texts, the noun alone often serves as a cross-reference: 10ページ参照 \"see page 10\". 参考にする, by contrast, means \"to take guidance from, use as a hint\", and 調べる is the general \"to look up, research\". 参照 is factual and typical of technical texts, instructions and computing.","levelNote":"Derived from 参照 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"論文の中で先行研究を引用した。","romaji":"Ronbun no naka de senkō kenkyū wo in'yō shita.","german":"I cited previous research in my paper.","furigana":"ろんぶん|なか|せんこうけんきゅう|いんよう"},{"japanese":"社長はスピーチで有名な作家の言葉を引用した。","romaji":"Shachou wa supiichi de yuumei na sakka no kotoba o in'you shita.","german":"The president quoted the words of a famous writer in his speech.","furigana":"しゃちょう|ゆうめい|さっか|ことば|いんよう"},{"japanese":"他人の文章を引用するときは、必ず引用元を書いてください。","romaji":"Tanin no bunshou o in'you suru toki wa, kanarazu in'youmoto o kaite kudasai.","german":"When you quote other people's writing, be sure to give the source.","furigana":"たにん|ぶんしょう|いんよう|かなら|いんようもと|か"}],"notes":"引用する means \"to quote verbatim, to cite as evidence\": 論文を引用する, 作家の言葉を引用する, 〜から引用する. The quotation itself is 引用文, the source 引用元. In contrast to 参照する (consulting a source), with 引用 you take over the wording. Typical of academic texts, speeches and articles.","levelNote":"Derived from 引用 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"長い報告書を一ページに要約した。","romaji":"Nagai hōkokusho wo ichi pēji ni yōyaku shita.","german":"I summarized the long report on one page.","furigana":"なが|ほうこくしょ|いち|ようやく"},{"japanese":"この記事の内容を200字以内で要約しなさい。","romaji":"Kono kiji no naiyou o nihyaku ji inai de youyaku shinasai.","german":"Summarize the content of this article in 200 characters or less.","furigana":"きじ|ないよう|じいない|ようやく"},{"japanese":"彼の話を要約すると、計画には反対だということだ。","romaji":"Kare no hanashi o youyaku suru to, keikaku ni wa hantai da to iu koto da.","german":"To sum up what he said, he is against the plan.","furigana":"かれ|はなし|ようやく|けいかく|はんたい"}],"notes":"要約する means \"to summarize the core of a text or speech concisely\": 記事を要約する, 〜字以内に要約する. The fixed phrase 要約すると introduces a conclusion (\"in short\"). まとめる is the everyday, broader word, which also means \"to put in order, to bundle\". Note the homophone: the adverb ようやく \"finally\" is written differently and has nothing to do with it.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"事故の原因を調査している。","romaji":"Jiko no gen'in wo chōsa shite iru.","german":"The cause of the accident is being investigated.","furigana":"じこ|げんいん|ちょうさ"},{"japanese":"市は高齢者の生活状況を調査した。","romaji":"Shi wa koureisha no seikatsu joukyou o chousa shita.","german":"The city surveyed the living conditions of elderly people.","furigana":"し|こうれいしゃ|せいかつじょうきょう|ちょうさ"},{"japanese":"この川の水質を定期的に調査しています。","romaji":"Kono kawa no suishitsu o teikiteki ni chousa shite imasu.","german":"We regularly test the water quality of this river.","furigana":"かわ|すいしつ|ていきてき|ちょうさ"}],"notes":"調査する denotes a systematic, often official investigation or survey: 原因を調査する, 住民の意識を調査する; as a noun 調査を行う, 世論調査 \"opinion poll\". 調べる is the everyday \"to look up, to examine\", 検査 the inspection of bodies or devices, 捜査 the police investigation. 調査 sounds like government, academia or business.","levelNote":"Derived from 調査 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大学で環境問題を研究している。","romaji":"Daigaku de kankyō mondai wo kenkyū shite iru.","german":"I am studying environmental problems at university.","furigana":"だいがく|かんきょうもんだい|けんきゅう"},{"japanese":"祖父は長年、日本の古い建築を研究してきた。","romaji":"Sofu wa naganen, Nihon no furui kenchiku o kenkyuu shite kita.","german":"My grandfather has researched old Japanese architecture for many years.","furigana":"そふ|ながねん|にほん|ふる|けんちく|けんきゅう"},{"japanese":"相手チームの試合のビデオを見て、作戦をよく研究した。","romaji":"Aite chiimu no shiai no bideo o mite, sakusen o yoku kenkyuu shita.","german":"I watched videos of the opposing team's games and studied their tactics carefully.","furigana":"あいて|しあい|み|さくせん|けんきゅう"}],"notes":"研究する means \"to research, to investigate scientifically\": 環境問題を研究する or 〜について研究する. In a broader sense also \"to study something thoroughly\", such as an opponent's tactics or a cooking recipe. 勉強する, by contrast, is \"to study\" (students, exams), 調査する a concrete investigation. Related: 研究者 \"researcher\", 研究室 \"lab, chair\".","levelNote":"Derived from 研究 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"祖母は昔の思い出を語ってくれた。","romaji":"Sobo wa mukashi no omoide wo katatte kureta.","german":"My grandmother told me about her memories of the past.","furigana":"そぼ|むかし|おも|で|かた"},{"japanese":"古い写真が、この町の歴史を語っている。","romaji":"Furui shashin ga, kono machi no rekishi o katatte iru.","german":"Old photographs tell the history of this town.","furigana":"ふる|しゃしん|まち|れきし|かた"},{"japanese":"社長は記者会見で今後の計画について語った。","romaji":"Shachou wa kisha kaiken de kongo no keikaku ni tsuite katatta.","german":"At the press conference, the president spoke about future plans.","furigana":"しゃちょう|きしゃかいけん|こんご|けいかく|かた"}],"notes":"語る means \"to tell at length, recount\" and stresses the content: experiences, memories, plans or feelings (思い出を語る, 夢を語る, 〜について語る). It sounds more elevated and narrative than 話す, which also fits any conversation or chat. Figuratively, things can \"speak\" too: 写真が歴史を語る, 数字が語る. Related are 物語 \"tale, story\" and 語り合う \"to talk with each other.\"","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"医者は患者に検査の結果を告げた。","romaji":"Isha wa kanja ni kensa no kekka wo tsugeta.","german":"The doctor told the patient the results of the examination.","furigana":"いしゃ|かんじゃ|けんさ|けっか|つ"},{"japanese":"庭の花が春の訪れを告げている。","romaji":"Niwa no hana ga haru no otozure o tsugete iru.","german":"The flowers in the garden announce the arrival of spring.","furigana":"にわ|はな|はる|おとず|つ"},{"japanese":"彼は誰にも行き先を告げずに家を出た。","romaji":"Kare wa dare ni mo ikisaki o tsugezu ni ie o deta.","german":"He left the house without telling anyone where he was going.","furigana":"かれ|だれ|い|さき|つ|いえ|で"}],"notes":"告げる means \"to tell, announce (something important)\": 人に結果を告げる, 名前を告げる, 行き先を告げずに. Figuratively \"to herald, signal\": 春の訪れを告げる, 時計が正午を告げる. A set phrase is 別れを告げる \"to say goodbye\". It sounds more literary and weighty than 言う or 伝える; in everyday speech one rather says 言う or 知らせる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"後で連絡しますので少々お待ちください。","romaji":"Ato de renraku shimasu node shōshō omachi kudasai.","german":"I will contact you later, so please wait a moment.","furigana":"あと|れんらく|しょうしょう|ま"},{"japanese":"駅に着いたら連絡してね。","romaji":"Eki ni tsuitara renraku shite ne.","german":"Let me know when you get to the station.","furigana":"えき|つ|れんらく"},{"japanese":"急に熱が出たので、会社に休むと連絡した。","romaji":"Kyuu ni netsu ga deta node, kaisha ni yasumu to renraku shita.","german":"I suddenly ran a fever, so I called the company to say I would be absent.","furigana":"きゅう|ねつ|で|かいしゃ|やす|れんらく"}],"notes":"連絡する means \"to get in touch, to let someone know, to make contact\" - by phone, message or e-mail: 人に連絡する, 人と連絡を取る. Important expressions: 連絡がつく/つかない \"to be able/unable to reach someone\", 連絡先 \"contact information\". 報告する, by contrast, is a report on results, 通知する a formal notification. With 〜と連絡する you give the content: 休むと連絡する.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 21"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"進路について先生に相談した。","romaji":"Shinro ni tsuite sensei ni sōdan shita.","german":"I asked my teacher for advice about my future path.","furigana":"しんろ|せんせい|そうだん"},{"japanese":"引っ越しの費用について、家族と相談しました。","romaji":"Hikkoshi no hiyō ni tsuite, kazoku to sōdan shimashita.","german":"I discussed the moving costs with my family.","furigana":"ひ|こ|ひよう|かぞく|そうだん"}],"notes":"相談する means to ask someone for advice or to discuss something together. The person from whom one wants advice often takes に: 先生に相談する. と emphasizes the conversation partner in a joint discussion: 家族と相談する. The topic can be named with について. In the first example, 進路 means the further educational or career path. The forms are, for example, 相談します, 相談した and 相談して; 相談 alone is the noun.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 14"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"環境問題について議論した。","romaji":"Kankyō mondai ni tsuite giron shita.","german":"We discussed environmental problems.","furigana":"かんきょうもんだい|ぎろん"},{"japanese":"結論を急がず、方法の長所と短所を議論した。","romaji":"Ketsuron o isogazu, hōhō no chōsho to tansho o giron shita.","german":"Without rushing to a conclusion, we discussed the strengths and weaknesses of the method.","furigana":"けつろん|いそ|ほうほう|ちょうしょ|たんしょ|ぎろん"},{"japanese":"その問題については、国会でも長い間議論されてきた。","romaji":"Sono mondai ni tsuite wa, kokkai demo nagai aida giron sarete kita.","german":"That issue has been debated in the Diet for a long time as well.","furigana":"もんだい|こっかい|なが|あいだぎろん"}],"notes":"議論する means \"to discuss something objectively, exchange arguments\": 問題について議論する or 問題を議論する. Phrases: 議論を交わす, 議論になる \"to end up in a discussion,\" 議論の余地がない \"beyond dispute.\" An 議論 need not be a quarrel; a heated verbal argument is 口論. 討論 is a formal debate with opposing positions, and 話し合う is talking something over together with the aim of reaching an agreement.","levelNote":"Derived from 議論 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"学生たちは政治について討論した。","romaji":"Gakuseitachi wa seiji ni tsuite tōron shita.","german":"The students debated about politics.","furigana":"がくせい|せいじ|とうろん"},{"japanese":"選挙の前に、候補者たちがテレビで討論した。","romaji":"Senkyo no mae ni, kouhoshatachi ga terebi de touron shita.","german":"Before the election, the candidates debated on television.","furigana":"せんきょ|まえ|こうほしゃ|とうろん"},{"japanese":"授業で、制服が必要かどうかについてグループに分かれて討論した。","romaji":"Jugyou de, seifuku ga hitsuyou ka dou ka ni tsuite guruupu ni wakarete touron shita.","german":"In class, we split into groups and debated whether school uniforms are necessary.","furigana":"じゅぎょう|せいふく|ひつよう|わ|とうろん"}],"notes":"討論する means \"to debate\": several people represent different positions and exchange arguments, often in a fixed setting such as a 討論会, a テレビ討論 or in class. Pattern: 〜について討論する. 議論する is broader and can also be an informal discussion; ディベート refers to the strictly regulated competitive form. 討論 sounds formal.","levelNote":"Derived from 討論 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は弁論大会で優勝した。","romaji":"Kanojo wa benron taikai de yūshō shita.","german":"She won the speech contest.","furigana":"かのじょ|べんろんたいかい|ゆうしょう"},{"japanese":"裁判では、まず原告側が弁論した。","romaji":"Saiban de wa, mazu genkokugawa ga benron shita.","german":"At the trial, the plaintiff's side argued first.","furigana":"さいばん|げんこくがわ|べんろん"},{"japanese":"弁護側は最終弁論で、被告人は無罪だと訴えた。","romaji":"Bengogawa wa saishuu benron de, hikokunin wa muzai da to uttaeta.","german":"In its closing argument, the defense claimed that the defendant was innocent.","furigana":"べんごがわ|さいしゅうべんろん|ひこくにん|むざい|うった"}],"notes":"弁論 refers to presenting arguments in public, for example as a speech or in court. It appears most often as a noun: 弁論大会 \"speech contest\", 最終弁論 \"closing argument\", 口頭弁論 \"oral proceedings\". The verb 弁論する is rare and mainly legal: a party presents its arguments in a trial. In everyday speech one says 主張する \"to assert, advocate\" or スピーチをする.","levelNote":"Derived from 弁論 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"両親を説得して留学の許可をもらった。","romaji":"Ryōshin wo settoku shite ryūgaku no kyoka wo moratta.","german":"Ich habe meine Eltern überzeugt und die Erlaubnis zum Auslandsstudium bekommen.","furigana":"りょうしん|せっとく|りゅうがく|きょか"},{"japanese":"安全性を説明して、家族を説得した。","romaji":"Anzensei o setsumei shite, kazoku o settoku shita.","german":"Mit einer Erklärung zur Sicherheit überzeugte ich meine Familie.","furigana":"あんぜんせい|せつめい|かぞく|せっとく"},{"japanese":"何度説得しても、彼は考えを変えなかった。","romaji":"Nando settoku shite mo, kare wa kangae o kaenakatta.","german":"So oft ich auch auf ihn einredete, er änderte seine Meinung nicht.","furigana":"なんどせっとく|かれ|かんが|か"}],"notes":"説得する heißt „jemanden zu überzeugen versuchen, überreden“: 人を説得する, 説得して〜させる. Anders als das deutsche „überzeugen“ beschreibt es vor allem den Versuch – 説得したが、だめだった ist möglich. Den Erfolg drückt man mit 説得に応じる oder 納得させる aus. 納得する ist das eigene Einsehen. Wichtig ist auch 説得力 „Überzeugungskraft“: 説得力のある説明.","levelNote":"Derived from 説得 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は一晩中彼女を口説いていた。","romaji":"Kare wa hitoban jū kanojo wo kudoite ita.","german":"He spent the whole night trying to win her over.","furigana":"かれ|ひとばんじゅうかのじょ|くど"},{"japanese":"社長を口説いて、新しいプロジェクトの予算を出してもらった。","romaji":"Shachou o kudoite, atarashii purojekuto no yosan o dashite moratta.","german":"I talked the company president into providing the budget for the new project.","furigana":"しゃちょう|くど|あたら|よさん|だ"},{"japanese":"バーで知らない男性に口説かれて困った。","romaji":"Baa de shiranai dansei ni kudokarete komatta.","german":"I was uncomfortable when an unknown man hit on me in a bar.","furigana":"し|だんせい|くど|こま"}],"notes":"口説く has two meanings: \"to work on someone persistently, to talk someone round\" (社長を口説いて予算を出してもらう) and \"to hit on, to woo\" (女性を口説く). The second is the more common today and sounds colloquial. Often in the passive: 口説かれる \"to be hit on\". 説得する is the neutral, factual word for persuading; a pickup line is 口説き文句.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この本を友人に勧めた。","romaji":"Kono hon wo yūjin ni susumeta.","german":"I recommended this book to a friend.","furigana":"ほん|ゆうじん|すす"},{"japanese":"医者に、もっと運動するように勧められた。","romaji":"Isha ni, motto undou suru you ni susumerareta.","german":"My doctor advised me to exercise more.","furigana":"いしゃ|うんどう|すす"},{"japanese":"電車でお年寄りに席を勧めた。","romaji":"Densha de otoshiyori ni seki o susumeta.","german":"I offered my seat to an elderly person on the train.","furigana":"でんしゃ|としよ|せき|すす"}],"notes":"勧める has three uses: to recommend something (本を友人に勧める), to advise someone to do something (〜するように勧める) and to offer something (席を勧める, お茶を勧める). The noun おすすめ \"recommendation\" comes from it. Not to be confused with the identically sounding 進める \"to move forward\"; 薦める is often written when recommending a person for a post.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"友人に無駄遣いをやめるよう忠告した。","romaji":"Yūjin ni mudazukai wo yameru yō chūkoku shita.","german":"I advised my friend to stop wasting money.","furigana":"ゆうじん|むだづか|ちゅうこく"},{"japanese":"先輩は、契約書をよく読むように忠告してくれた。","romaji":"Senpai wa, keiyakusho o yoku yomu yō ni chūkoku shite kureta.","german":"My senior colleague urged me to read the contract carefully.","furigana":"せんぱい|けいやくしょ|よ|ちゅうこく"},{"japanese":"医者の忠告を聞かずに、彼はまたお酒を飲み始めた。","romaji":"Isha no chuukoku o kikazu ni, kare wa mata osake o nomihajimeta.","german":"Ignoring the doctor's advice, he started drinking again.","furigana":"いしゃ|ちゅうこく|き|かれ|さけ|の|はじ"}],"notes":"忠告する means \"to advise earnestly, warn in admonition\", usually so that someone refrains from something or is careful: 人に〜ように忠告する. As a noun: 忠告を聞く/無視する. It usually comes from more experienced people; toward superiors it seems presumptuous. アドバイス or 助言 are neutral \"advice\", 注意する is everyday admonishing, 警告 an explicit warning of danger.","levelNote":"Derived from 忠告 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"台風が接近していると住民に警告した。","romaji":"Taifū ga sekkin shite iru to jūmin ni keikoku shita.","german":"He warned the residents that a typhoon was approaching.","furigana":"たいふう|せっきん|じゅうみん|けいこく"},{"japanese":"医者は、このままたばこを吸い続けると危ないと警告した。","romaji":"Isha wa, kono mama tabako o suitsuzukeru to abunai to keikoku shita.","german":"The doctor warned that it would be dangerous if I kept smoking like this.","furigana":"いしゃ|す|つづ|あぶ|けいこく"},{"japanese":"パソコンの画面に、ウイルスが見つかったという警告が出た。","romaji":"Pasokon no gamen ni, uirusu ga mitsukatta to iu keikoku ga deta.","german":"A warning appeared on the computer screen saying that a virus had been found.","furigana":"がめん|み|けいこく|で"}],"notes":"警告する means \"to expressly warn of a danger\": 人に〜と警告する, 〜ないよう警告する. As a noun: 警告を受ける/無視する, 警告音, and on screens 警告 \"warning\"; in sports also the yellow card. 注意する is milder, and 忠告する is personal advice. Official severe-weather warnings, on the other hand, are 警報 (大雨警報).","levelNote":"Derived from 警告 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"犯人は店員をナイフで脅した。","romaji":"Hannin wa ten'in wo naifu de odoshita.","german":"The criminal threatened the clerk with a knife.","furigana":"はんにん|てんいん|おど"},{"japanese":"「金を出さないと、秘密をばらすぞ」と脅された。","romaji":"Kane o dasanai to, himitsu o barasu zo to odosareta.","german":"I was threatened: \"If you don't hand over the money, I'll spill your secret.\"","furigana":"かね|だ|ひみつ|おど"},{"japanese":"子供を脅して言うことを聞かせるのはよくない。","romaji":"Kodomo o odoshite iu koto o kikaseru no wa yokunai.","german":"It is not good to make children obey by threatening them.","furigana":"こども|おど|い|き"}],"notes":"脅す means \"to threaten, intimidate\": 人をナイフで脅す, 〜と言って脅す, 脅して〜させる \"to force someone to do something through threats\". Often in the passive: 脅される. The formal, legal word is 脅迫する. Be careful with 脅かす: read おどかす it means \"to scare\", read おびやかす \"to endanger\" (平和を脅かす).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"詐欺師にお金を騙し取られた。","romaji":"Sagishi ni okane wo damashitorareta.","german":"I was cheated out of my money by a fraudster.","furigana":"さぎし|かね|だま|と"},{"japanese":"「絶対にもうかる」という話に騙されて、大金を失った。","romaji":"Zettai ni moukaru to iu hanashi ni damasarete, taikin o ushinatta.","german":"I fell for the claim that \"you'll definitely make a profit\" and lost a lot of money.","furigana":"ぜったい|はなし|だま|たいきん|うしな"},{"japanese":"古いパソコンを騙し騙し使っている。","romaji":"Furui pasokon o damashi damashi tsukatte iru.","german":"I keep using my old computer somehow, nursing it along.","furigana":"ふる|だま|だま|つか"}],"notes":"騙す means \"to deceive, to trick, to cheat\": 人を騙す, especially common in the passive 騙される \"to fall for it\" (〜に騙される). 騙し取る means \"to swindle out of\". It is often written in kana: だます. Unlike 嘘をつく \"to lie\", 騙す aims at deceiving as a whole. 騙し騙し使う means \"to keep using (something old) somehow\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の発言を誤解してしまった。","romaji":"Kare no hatsugen wo gokai shite shimatta.","german":"I misunderstood his remark.","furigana":"かれ|はつげん|ごかい"},{"japanese":"短い返事を怒っている証拠だと誤解した。","romaji":"Mijikai henji o okotte iru shōko da to gokai shita.","german":"I mistook the short reply for a sign that he was angry.","furigana":"みじか|へんじ|おこ|しょうこ|ごかい"},{"japanese":"誤解されやすい言い方は避けたほうがいい。","romaji":"Gokai sareyasui iikata wa saketa hou ga ii.","german":"It's better to avoid ways of speaking that are easily misunderstood.","furigana":"ごかい|い|かた|さ"}],"notes":"誤解する means \"to understand wrongly,\" mostly words, intentions or people: 発言を誤解する, AをBだと誤解する \"to mistake A for B.\" Important phrases: 誤解を招く \"to cause misunderstandings,\" 誤解を解く \"to clear up a misunderstanding,\" 誤解しないでください. 勘違いする is one's own mistake out of carelessness (mixing up a date or place), while 誤解 concerns more the interpretation of something said.","levelNote":"Derived from 誤解 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の提案に全員が同意した。","romaji":"Kare no teian ni zen'in ga dōi shita.","german":"Everyone agreed to his proposal.","furigana":"かれ|ていあん|ぜんいん|どうい"},{"japanese":"利用規約に同意してから、登録を完了した。","romaji":"Riyō kiyaku ni dōi shite kara, tōroku o kanryō shita.","german":"After agreeing to the terms of use, I completed the registration.","furigana":"りようきやく|どうい|とうろく|かんりょう"},{"japanese":"その意見には同意できません。","romaji":"Sono iken ni wa doui dekimasen.","german":"I cannot agree with that opinion.","furigana":"いけん|どうい"}],"notes":"同意する means \"to agree, consent\" and takes に: 意見に同意する, 規約に同意する - not 意見を同意する. As a noun: 同意を得る/求める, 同意書 \"consent form\". 賛成する is \"to be in favor\" (of proposals, votes), 合意 a mutual agreement between parties, 承認 an official approval.","levelNote":"Derived from 同意 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"予算案が委員会で承認された。","romaji":"Yosan'an ga iinkai de shōnin sareta.","german":"The draft budget was approved by the committee.","furigana":"よさんあん|いいんかい|しょうにん"},{"japanese":"休暇の申請が承認されたので、切符を買った。","romaji":"Kyūka no shinsei ga shōnin sareta node, kippu o katta.","german":"My vacation request was approved, so I bought a ticket.","furigana":"きゅうか|しんせい|しょうにん|きっぷ|か"},{"japanese":"友達申請を承認すると、相手のページが見られるようになる。","romaji":"Tomodachi shinsei o shounin suru to, aite no peeji ga mirareru you ni naru.","german":"When you approve a friend request, the other person can see your page.","furigana":"ともだちしんせい|しょうにん|あいて|み"}],"notes":"承認する means \"to officially approve, sanction\": 申請・計画・予算を承認する; very common in the passive (承認される) and in 上司の承認が必要 \"approval by the supervisor is required.\" Also \"to recognize\" in the political sense (国家を承認する) and \"to confirm\" digitally (友達申請を承認する). 許可する permits an action, 確認する only means to check or confirm, and 同意する to agree personally.","levelNote":"Derived from 承認 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"校長が校外学習を許可した。","romaji":"Kōchō ga kōgai gakushū wo kyoka shita.","german":"The principal approved the field trip.","furigana":"こうちょう|こうがいがくしゅう|きょか"},{"japanese":"この建物の中での撮影は許可されていません。","romaji":"Kono tatemono no naka de no satsuei wa kyoka sarete imasen.","german":"Photography is not permitted inside this building.","furigana":"たてもの|なか|さつえい|きょか"},{"japanese":"会社は社員が自宅で働くことを許可した。","romaji":"Kaisha wa shain ga jitaku de hataraku koto o kyoka shita.","german":"The company allowed employees to work from home.","furigana":"かいしゃ|しゃいん|じたく|はたら|きょか"}],"notes":"許可する means \"to allow, to approve\" (officially): X を許可する, 〜ことを許可する. As a noun it is very common: 許可を得る/もらう/取る, 許可なく \"without permission\", 許可証 \"permit\". It sounds official or institutional; in private settings one more often says 許す or いいと言う. 承認する refers to plans and applications, 許可 to a concrete action.","levelNote":"Derived from 許可 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は取材を拒否した。","romaji":"Kare wa shuzai wo kyohi shita.","german":"He refused the interview.","furigana":"かれ|しゅざい|きょひ"},{"japanese":"端末が古いパスワードでの接続を拒否した。","romaji":"Tanmatsu ga furui pasuwādo de no setsuzoku o kyohi shita.","german":"The device rejected the connection with the old password.","furigana":"たんまつ|ふる|せつぞく|きょひ"},{"japanese":"彼女は検査を受けることを拒否した。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kensa o ukeru koto o kyohi shita.","german":"She refused to undergo the examination.","furigana":"かのじょ|けんさ|う|きょひ"}],"notes":"拒否する means \"to refuse firmly, reject\": 要求・取材・接続を拒否する, 〜ことを拒否する \"to refuse to do something\". It is more explicit and formal than 断る, which can also be a polite decline. Related: 拒否権 \"right of veto\", 登校拒否 \"school refusal\". Devices and systems can also 拒否する (アクセス拒否).","levelNote":"Derived from 拒否 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の意見に対して反論した。","romaji":"Kare no iken ni taishite hanron shita.","german":"I argued against his opinion.","furigana":"かれ|いけん|たい|はんろん"},{"japanese":"その数字は古いと、彼女は資料を示して反論した。","romaji":"Sono sūji wa furui to, kanojo wa shiryō o shimeshite hanron shita.","german":"She rebutted him by showing documents, saying that the figures were out of date.","furigana":"すうじ|ふる|かのじょ|しりょう|しめ|はんろん"},{"japanese":"先生に注意されても、彼は一言も反論しなかった。","romaji":"Sensei ni chuui sarete mo, kare wa hitokoto mo hanron shinakatta.","german":"Even when the teacher admonished him, he did not say a word in rebuttal.","furigana":"せんせい|ちゅうい|かれ|ひとこと|はんろん"}],"notes":"反論する means \"to contradict with counterarguments\": 意見に反論する, 〜に対して反論する. Fixed expressions: 反論の余地がない \"irrefutable\", 反論を述べる \"to state a rebuttal\". 反対する can be mere rejection without reasons, whereas 反論 brings arguments. The colloquial \"to talk back\" is 言い返す.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"国際社会はその行為を非難した。","romaji":"Kokusai shakai wa sono kōi wo hinan shita.","german":"The international community condemned the act.","furigana":"こくさいしゃかい|こうい|ひなん"},{"japanese":"彼は約束を破った友人を激しく非難した。","romaji":"Kare wa yakusoku o yabutta yuujin o hageshiku hinan shita.","german":"He harshly condemned the friend who had broken his promise.","furigana":"かれ|やくそく|やぶ|ゆうじん|はげ|ひなん"},{"japanese":"その政治家の発言は多くの人から非難された。","romaji":"Sono seijika no hatsugen wa ooku no hito kara hinan sareta.","german":"The politician's remarks were criticized by many people.","furigana":"せいじか|はつげん|おお|ひと|ひなん"}],"notes":"非難する means \"to criticize sharply, to condemn, to denounce\", usually misconduct: 行為を非難する, 人を〜だと非難する; in the passive 非難される, and the fixed 非難を浴びる \"to be subjected to fierce criticism\". 批判する is more objective and can also be constructive, 責める is a personal reproach. Note the homophone: 避難する means \"to get to safety, to evacuate\".","levelNote":"Derived from 非難 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"弁護士が被告人を弁護した。","romaji":"Bengoshi ga hikokunin wo bengo shita.","german":"The lawyer defended the defendant.","furigana":"べんごし|ひこくにん|べんご"},{"japanese":"みんなが彼を責めたが、私だけは彼を弁護した。","romaji":"Minna ga kare o semeta ga, watashi dake wa kare o bengo shita.","german":"Everyone blamed him, but only I stood up for him.","furigana":"かれ|せ|わたし|かれ|べんご"},{"japanese":"自分のことばかり弁護していないで、まず謝るべきだ。","romaji":"Jibun no koto bakari bengo shite inaide, mazu ayamaru beki da.","german":"Instead of only defending yourself, you should apologize first.","furigana":"じぶん|べんご|あやま"}],"notes":"弁護する means \"to defend\", mainly in a legal sense: 弁護士が被告人を弁護する. In everyday life also \"to stand up for someone\" when others criticize them; colloquially one often says かばう for this. 自己弁護 is justifying oneself. 擁護する defends rather rights, principles or positions, and 守る is the general \"to protect\".","levelNote":"Derived from 弁護 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は人権を擁護する活動をしている。","romaji":"Kare wa jinken wo yōgo suru katsudō wo shite iru.","german":"He is involved in activities to protect human rights.","furigana":"かれ|じんけん|ようご|かつどう"},{"japanese":"批判が集まる中、監督は選手たちを擁護した。","romaji":"Hihan ga atsumaru naka, kantoku wa senshutachi o yougo shita.","german":"As criticism mounted, the manager defended his players.","furigana":"ひはん|あつ|なか|かんとく|せんしゅ|ようご"},{"japanese":"その新聞は、言論の自由を擁護する立場を取っている。","romaji":"Sono shinbun wa, genron no jiyuu o yougo suru tachiba o totte iru.","german":"That newspaper takes the position of defending freedom of speech.","furigana":"しんぶん|げんろん|じゆう|ようご|たちば|と"}],"notes":"擁護する means \"to stand up for (publicly), to defend\": 人権・憲法・言論の自由を擁護する, also to take a person or their position under protection against criticism. It is literary and typical of politics and media. 弁護する is above all the legal defense of a person, 保護する is the protection of the weak, animals or nature.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女の勇気ある行動が称賛された。","romaji":"Kanojo no yūki aru kōdō ga shōsan sareta.","german":"Her courageous action was praised.","furigana":"かのじょ|ゆうき|こうどう|しょうさん"},{"japanese":"審査員たちは彼の演奏を高く称賛した。","romaji":"Shinsaintachi wa kare no ensou o takaku shousan shita.","german":"The judges praised his performance highly.","furigana":"しんさいん|かれ|えんそう|たか|しょうさん"},{"japanese":"彼女の努力は称賛に値する。","romaji":"Kanojo no doryoku wa shousan ni atai suru.","german":"Her efforts deserve praise.","furigana":"かのじょ|どりょく|しょうさん|あたい"}],"notes":"称賛する means \"to praise, to extol (publicly)\" and is literary; it is common in the passive (称賛される) and in expressions such as 称賛を浴びる \"to receive great praise\", 称賛に値する \"to be praiseworthy\". The spelling 賞賛 also occurs. In daily life one says 褒める, which often points from top to bottom (parents praise children). 称賛 expresses admiring recognition.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人の作品をけなすのはよくない。","romaji":"Hito no sakuhin wo kenasu no wa yokunai.","german":"It is not good to run down other people's work.","furigana":"ひと|さくひん"},{"japanese":"彼はいつも私の料理をけなすので、作る気がなくなる。","romaji":"Kare wa itsumo watashi no ryouri o kenasu node, tsukuru ki ga nakunaru.","german":"He always criticizes my cooking, so I lose the desire to make it.","furigana":"かれ|わたし|りょうり|つく|き"},{"japanese":"その映画は批評家にけなされたが、観客には人気があった。","romaji":"Sono eiga wa hihyouka ni kenasareta ga, kankyaku ni wa ninki ga atta.","german":"The movie was panned by critics but was popular with audiences.","furigana":"えいが|ひひょうか|かんきゃく|にんき"}],"notes":"けなす means \"to put down, disparage\", i.e. to speak disparagingly of someone or their work: 作品をけなす, 人をけなす. It is almost always written in kana. The opposite is ほめる. 批判する is objective criticism, 悪口を言う is rather bad-mouthing behind someone's back, ばかにする is making fun of someone. In the passive: 批評家にけなされる \"to be panned by critics\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼を公の場で侮辱するのは許されない。","romaji":"Kare wo ōyake no ba de bujoku suru no wa yurusarenai.","german":"Insulting him in public is not acceptable.","furigana":"かれ|おおやけ|ば|ぶじょく|ゆる"},{"japanese":"そんな言い方は、私の家族を侮辱しているのと同じだ。","romaji":"Sonna iikata wa, watashi no kazoku o bujoku shite iru no to onaji da.","german":"Saying something like that is the same as insulting my family.","furigana":"い|かた|わたし|かぞく|ぶじょく|おな"},{"japanese":"客に侮辱されても、店員は冷静に対応した。","romaji":"Kyaku ni bujoku sarete mo, ten'in wa reisei ni taiou shita.","german":"Even when a customer insulted him, the clerk responded calmly.","furigana":"きゃく|ぶじょく|てんいん|れいせい|たいおう"}],"notes":"侮辱する means \"to insult, hold in contempt\" and is a strong, rather formal word: 人を侮辱する, 侮辱を受ける; in law there is 侮辱罪 \"insult\" as a criminal offense. ばかにする is the everyday \"to make fun of, look down on\", けなす a disparaging running down, 悪口 bad-mouthing. 侮辱 violates the dignity or honor of a person or group.","levelNote":"Derived from 侮辱 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は彼の過ちを許した。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kare no ayamachi wo yurushita.","german":"She forgave his mistake.","furigana":"かのじょ|かれ|あやま|ゆる"},{"japanese":"時間が許せば、帰りに美術館にも寄りたい。","romaji":"Jikan ga yuruseba, kaeri ni bijutsukan ni mo yoritai.","german":"If time permits, I'd like to stop by the art museum on the way home.","furigana":"じかん|ゆる|かえ|びじゅつかん|よ"},{"japanese":"父は私が一人暮らしをすることを許してくれなかった。","romaji":"Chichi wa watashi ga hitorigurashi o suru koto o yurushite kurenakatta.","german":"My father wouldn't allow me to live on my own.","furigana":"ちち|わたし|ひとりぐ|ゆる"}],"notes":"許す has two main meanings: \"to forgive\" (人の過ちを許す, 絶対に許せない \"unforgivable\") and \"to allow, permit\" (〜ことを許す, 時間が許せば \"if time permits\"). Formally you ask for forgiveness with お許しください. 許可する is official permission, 許す is more personal. Other phrases: 気を許す \"to trust someone,\" 点を許す \"to concede a point\" in sports.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"失敗を恐れていては何も始められない。","romaji":"Shippai wo osorete ite wa nani mo hajimerarenai.","german":"If you fear failure, you can't start anything.","furigana":"しっぱい|おそ|なに|はじ"},{"japanese":"多くの住民が、川があふれることを恐れて避難した。","romaji":"Ooku no juumin ga, kawa ga afureru koto o osorete hinan shita.","german":"Many residents evacuated, fearing that the river would overflow.","furigana":"おお|じゅうみん|かわ|おそ|ひなん"},{"japanese":"彼は上司を恐れて、本当のことを言えなかった。","romaji":"Kare wa joushi o osorete, hontou no koto o ienakatta.","german":"He feared his boss and could not tell the truth.","furigana":"かれ|じょうし|おそ|ほんとう|い"}],"notes":"恐れる means \"to fear, be afraid of\" and takes を: 失敗を恐れる, 〜ことを恐れる. It sounds somewhat more elevated than 怖がる, which describes visible fearful behavior and is often used for other people; in everyday speech people usually say 〜が怖い. Very important is the noun in 〜恐れがある \"there is a risk that.\" For reverence it is also written 畏れる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先生の温かい言葉に感激した。","romaji":"Sensei no atatakai kotoba ni kangeki shita.","german":"I was deeply moved by the teacher's warm words.","furigana":"せんせい|あたた|ことば|かんげき"},{"japanese":"大好きな歌手に会えて、感激のあまり泣いてしまった。","romaji":"Daisuki na kashu ni aete, kangeki no amari naite shimatta.","german":"I got to meet my favorite singer and cried from sheer emotion.","furigana":"だいす|かしゅ|あ|かんげき|な"},{"japanese":"初めて自分の本が書店に並んでいるのを見て、感激した。","romaji":"Hajimete jibun no hon ga shoten ni narande iru no o mite, kangeki shita.","german":"When I saw my own book on display in a bookstore for the first time, I was overwhelmed.","furigana":"はじ|じぶん|ほん|しょてん|なら|み|かんげき"}],"notes":"感激する means \"to be deeply moved, overwhelmed\", usually out of joy or gratitude about something personal: 〜に感激する, 感激のあまり泣く. 感動する is rather used for being moved by art, nature or stories; 感激 is more emotional and immediate, e.g. when meeting someone or experiencing kindness. 興奮する is mere excitement.","levelNote":"Derived from 感激 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"サッカーの試合を見て興奮した。","romaji":"Sakkā no shiai wo mite kōfun shita.","german":"I got excited watching the soccer match.","furigana":"しあい|み|こうふん"},{"japanese":"明日の遠足が楽しみで、子供たちは興奮してなかなか寝なかった。","romaji":"Ashita no ensoku ga tanoshimi de, kodomotachi wa koufun shite nakanaka nenakatta.","german":"The children were so looking forward to tomorrow's outing that they were too excited to fall asleep for a long time.","furigana":"あした|えんそく|たの|こども|こうふん|ね"},{"japanese":"興奮しないで、落ち着いて話してください。","romaji":"Koufun shinaide, ochitsuite hanashite kudasai.","german":"Please don't get worked up; speak calmly.","furigana":"こうふん|お|つ|はな"}],"notes":"興奮する means \"to be excited, aroused\" - from joy, tension or also anger: 試合に興奮する, 興奮して眠れない, 興奮しないで \"don't get worked up\". As a noun: 興奮を抑える, 興奮状態. わくわくする is joyful anticipation, 感激する is being touched. Caution: depending on context, 興奮 can also mean sexual arousal.","levelNote":"Derived from 興奮 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地震が起きて慌てて外に飛び出した。","romaji":"Jishin ga okite awatete soto ni tobidashita.","german":"When the earthquake struck, I ran outside in a panic.","furigana":"じしん|お|あわ|そと|と|だ"},{"japanese":"寝坊して、慌てて家を出たので財布を忘れた。","romaji":"Nebou shite, awatete ie o deta node saifu o wasureta.","german":"I overslept and rushed out of the house so hurriedly that I forgot my wallet.","furigana":"ねぼう|あわ|いえ|で|さいふ|わす"},{"japanese":"急に質問されても、慌てずに答えればいい。","romaji":"Kyuu ni shitsumon sarete mo, awatezu ni kotaereba ii.","german":"Even if you are asked a question suddenly, just answer calmly.","furigana":"きゅう|しつもん|あわ|こた"}],"notes":"慌てる means \"to get into a rush or panic, to act headlessly\". The て-form is very common as an adverb: 慌てて家を出る \"rush out of the house\"; 慌てずに / 慌てないで \"without panicking\". 焦る is inner impatience under time pressure, 急ぐ is the neutral \"to hurry\". A scatterbrain is called 慌て者. Intransitive, with no object.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"突然の質問に戸惑った。","romaji":"Totsuzen no shitsumon ni tomadotta.","german":"I was bewildered by the sudden question.","furigana":"とつぜん|しつもん|とまど"},{"japanese":"日本に来たばかりの頃は、ごみの分け方の細かさに戸惑った。","romaji":"Nihon ni kita bakari no koro wa, gomi no wakekata no komakasa ni tomadotta.","german":"When I had just come to Japan, I was bewildered by how detailed the garbage sorting was.","furigana":"にほん|き|ころ|わ|かた|こま|とまど"},{"japanese":"新しいシステムに戸惑う社員も多い。","romaji":"Atarashii shisutemu ni tomadou shain mo ooi.","german":"Many employees are bewildered by the new system.","furigana":"あたら|とまど|しゃいん|おお"}],"notes":"戸惑う means \"to be unsure, at a loss\" because something is unexpected or unfamiliar: 突然の質問に戸惑う, 新しい環境に戸惑う. The noun appears in 戸惑いを感じる / 戸惑いを隠せない. 迷う is indecision between options, 困る is generally \"to be in trouble\", 慌てる is hectic panic. Intransitive; the trigger takes に.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"将来のことで悩んでいる。","romaji":"Shōrai no koto de nayande iru.","german":"I'm worried about the future.","furigana":"しょうらい|なや"},{"japanese":"どちらの大学に行くか、まだ悩んでいる。","romaji":"Dochira no daigaku ni iku ka, mada nayande iru.","german":"I'm still agonizing over which university to go to.","furigana":"だいがく|い|なや"},{"japanese":"多くの農家が人手不足に悩んでいる。","romaji":"Ooku no nouka ga hitode busoku ni nayande iru.","german":"Many farmers are troubled by a labor shortage.","furigana":"おお|のうか|ひとでぶそく|なや"}],"notes":"悩む means \"to struggle with something, suffer from something, go back and forth\": 将来のことで悩む, 人間関係に悩む, どちらにするか悩む. It is an inner, lasting struggle with a problem. 心配する, by contrast, is worry about someone or something (\"to be concerned about\"), and 迷う is indecision between options. The noun 悩み means \"worry, trouble.\"","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は長い間病気に苦しんだ。","romaji":"Kare wa nagai aida byōki ni kurushinda.","german":"He suffered from illness for a long time.","furigana":"かれ|なが|あいだびょうき|くる"},{"japanese":"その国では多くの人が食料不足に苦しんでいる。","romaji":"Sono kuni de wa ooku no hito ga shokuryou busoku ni kurushinde iru.","german":"In that country, many people are suffering from food shortages.","furigana":"くに|おお|ひと|しょくりょうぶそく|くる"},{"japanese":"彼の態度は理解に苦しむ。","romaji":"Kare no taido wa rikai ni kurushimu.","german":"I have trouble understanding his attitude.","furigana":"かれ|たいど|りかい|くる"}],"notes":"苦しむ means \"to suffer, be in agony,\" physically or mentally: 病気・痛み・借金・貧困に苦しむ. The set phrase 理解に苦しむ means \"to be unable to make sense of something.\" The adjective is 苦しい, the transitive counterpart 苦しめる \"to torment.\" 悩む is more inner brooding over a problem, while 苦しむ stresses the suffering itself.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"厳しい訓練に耐えた。","romaji":"Kibishii kunren ni taeta.","german":"I endured the strict training.","furigana":"きび|くんれん|た"},{"japanese":"この建物は大きな地震にも耐えられるように設計されている。","romaji":"Kono tatemono wa ookina jishin ni mo taerareru you ni sekkei sarete iru.","german":"This building is designed to withstand even large earthquakes.","furigana":"たてもの|おお|じしん|た|せっけい"},{"japanese":"彼女は痛みに耐えて、最後まで走り続けた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa itami ni taete, saigo made hashiritsuzuketa.","german":"She endured the pain and kept running to the end.","furigana":"かのじょ|いた|た|さいご|はし|つづ"}],"notes":"耐える means \"to endure, put up with\" and takes に: 痛み・寒さ・厳しい訓練に耐える, 〜に耐えられない. For things it means \"to withstand\": 地震に耐える建物. 我慢する is more everyday and stresses holding oneself back or going without; 耐える sounds more serious and suggests longer strain. For emotional matters, 堪える is sometimes written as well.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの時の決断を今でも後悔している。","romaji":"Ano toki no ketsudan wo ima demo kōkai shite iru.","german":"I still regret the decision I made back then.","furigana":"とき|けつだん|いま|こうかい"},{"japanese":"もっと勉強しておけばよかったと後悔している。","romaji":"Motto benkyou shite okeba yokatta to koukai shite iru.","german":"I regret not having studied more.","furigana":"べんきょう|こうかい"},{"japanese":"やらずに後悔するより、やって後悔したほうがいい。","romaji":"Yarazu ni koukai suru yori, yatte koukai shita hou ga ii.","german":"It's better to do something and regret it than to regret not having done it.","furigana":"こうかい|こうかい"}],"notes":"後悔する means \"to regret,\" referring to one's own past action: 決断を後悔する, 〜たことを後悔する, 〜ばよかったと後悔する. Well known is the proverb 後悔先に立たず \"regret always comes too late.\" 反省する, by contrast, means to recognize one's mistake and want to improve. Note: 公開する \"to make public\" sounds the same.","levelNote":"Derived from 後悔 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自分の行動を反省した。","romaji":"Jibun no kōdō wo hansei shita.","german":"Ich habe mein Verhalten selbstkritisch überdacht.","furigana":"じぶん|こうどう|はんせい"},{"japanese":"試合の後、チームで負けた原因を反省した。","romaji":"Shiai no ato, chiimu de maketa gen'in o hansei shita.","german":"Nach dem Spiel analysierten wir im Team selbstkritisch, warum wir verloren hatten.","furigana":"しあい|あと|ま|げんいん|はんせい"},{"japanese":"息子は「もう二度としません」と深く反省している。","romaji":"Musuko wa mou nido to shimasen to fukaku hansei shite iru.","german":"Mein Sohn bereut es zutiefst und verspricht, es nie wieder zu tun.","furigana":"むすこ|にど|ふか|はんせい"}],"notes":"反省する heißt „die eigenen Fehler selbstkritisch einsehen“, verbunden mit dem Vorsatz, es besser zu machen: 自分の行動を反省する, 深く反省している. Das deutsche „reflektieren“ trifft es nur halb, denn 反省 setzt fast immer einen Fehler voraus; neutrales Nachdenken ist 振り返る oder 考える. 反省会 ist eine kritische Nachbesprechung. 後悔する ist bloßes Bereuen ohne Besserungsabsicht.","levelNote":"Derived from 反省 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自分の無知を恥じた。","romaji":"Jibun no muchi wo hajita.","german":"I was ashamed of my ignorance.","furigana":"じぶん|むち|は"},{"japanese":"優勝チームの名に恥じないプレーをしたい。","romaji":"Yuushou chiimu no na ni hajinai purei o shitai.","german":"I want to play in a way that does justice to the name of the champion team.","furigana":"ゆうしょう|な|は"},{"japanese":"貧しいことは恥じることではない。","romaji":"Mazushii koto wa hajiru koto de wa nai.","german":"Being poor is nothing to be ashamed of.","furigana":"まず|は"}],"notes":"恥じる means \"to be ashamed (of)\" and is literary: 自分の無知を恥じる, 恥じることではない \"nothing to be ashamed of\". The negative 〜に恥じない \"worthy of something\" (名に恥じない) is common. In everyday speech one says 恥ずかしい or 恥ずかしく思う; 恥をかく means \"to be embarrassed, lose face\". The object of shame takes を.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼を恨んでも何も変わらない。","romaji":"Kare wo urande mo nani mo kawaranai.","german":"Resenting him won't change anything.","furigana":"かれ|うら|なに|か"},{"japanese":"彼女は自分を捨てた恋人を長い間恨んでいた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa jibun o suteta koibito o nagai aida urande ita.","german":"She long held a grudge against the lover who had abandoned her.","furigana":"かのじょ|じぶん|す|こいびと|なが|あいだうら"},{"japanese":"試合の日に限って雨が降るなんて、天気を恨みたくなる。","romaji":"Shiai no hi ni kagitte ame ga furu nante, tenki o uramitaku naru.","german":"Rain on the very day of the match - it makes you want to curse the weather.","furigana":"しあい|ひ|かぎ|あめ|ふ|てんき|うら"}],"notes":"恨む means \"to bear a grudge against someone, to resent something\" because of a wrong suffered: 人を恨む, 〜を恨んでいる. As a noun: 恨みを持つ, 恨みを買う \"to make enemies\", 逆恨み \"unjustified resentment\". One can also 恨む fate or the weather, i.e. \"rail against\" it. 憎む is stronger and means \"to hate\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子供の頃からパイロットに憧れていた。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro kara pairotto ni akogarete ita.","german":"I have admired pilots since I was a child.","furigana":"こども|ころ|あこが"},{"japanese":"田舎で育った彼女は、ずっと都会の生活に憧れていた。","romaji":"Inaka de sodatta kanojo wa, zutto tokai no seikatsu ni akogarete ita.","german":"Having grown up in the countryside, she had always longed for city life.","furigana":"いなか|そだ|かのじょ|とかい|せいかつ|あこが"},{"japanese":"部活の先輩は、みんなの憧れの存在だった。","romaji":"Bukatsu no senpai wa, minna no akogare no sonzai datta.","german":"The senior student in the club was an object of admiration for everyone.","furigana":"ぶかつ|せんぱい|あこが|そんざい"}],"notes":"憧れる means \"to long for, to be crazy about, to admire as a role model\" and always takes に: パイロットに憧れる, 都会の生活に憧れる. The noun is very common: 憧れの人 \"great role model, idol\", 憧れの職業 \"dream job\". 尊敬する means, more soberly, \"to respect, to look up to\", and うらやましい is \"to be envious\". Intransitive, so not with を.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"学生時代を懐かしんでいる。","romaji":"Gakusei jidai wo natsukashinde iru.","german":"I look back fondly on my student days.","furigana":"がくせいじだい|なつ"},{"japanese":"祖父は古いアルバムを見ながら、若い頃を懐かしんでいた。","romaji":"Sofu wa furui arubamu o minagara, wakai koro o natsukashinde ita.","german":"My grandfather reminisced about his youth while looking through an old album.","furigana":"そふ|ふる|み|わか|ころ|なつ"},{"japanese":"海外で暮らす姉は、日本の家庭料理を懐かしんでいる。","romaji":"Kaigai de kurasu ane wa, Nihon no katei ryouri o natsukashinde iru.","german":"My older sister, who lives abroad, longs for Japanese home cooking.","furigana":"かいがい|く|あね|にほん|かていりょうり|なつ"}],"notes":"懐かしむ means \"to look back on something with nostalgia, to miss something\" and takes を: 学生時代を懐かしむ, 故郷を懐かしむ. It is rather literary; in conversation the adjective 懐かしい is used much more often (\"Oh, how nice, this brings back memories!\"). Mostly in the forms 懐かしんでいる or 懐かしみながら. 恋しい more strongly stresses missing people or places.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"世の中の不公平さを嘆いた。","romaji":"Yo no naka no fukōhei sa wo nageita.","german":"He lamented the unfairness of the world.","furigana":"よ|なか|ふこうへい|なげ"},{"japanese":"友人の突然の死を深く嘆いた。","romaji":"Yūjin no totsuzen no shi o fukaku nageita.","german":"I deeply grieved the sudden death of my friend.","furigana":"ゆうじん|とつぜん|し|ふか|なげ"},{"japanese":"最近の若者は本を読まないと嘆く人が多い。","romaji":"Saikin no wakamono wa hon o yomanai to nageku hito ga ōi.","german":"Many people lament that young people these days don't read books.","furigana":"さいきん|わかもの|ほん|よ|なげ|ひと|おお"}],"notes":"嘆く means \"to lament, mourn\" and expresses deep grief or serious regret: 死を嘆く, 現状を嘆く, or with a quotation: 〜と嘆く. It is more elevated and serious than everyday complaining, for which one says 愚痴をこぼす or 文句を言う. The adjective 嘆かわしい means \"deplorable\"; 嘆き悲しむ intensifies the sorrow.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"国の将来を憂う声が多い。","romaji":"Kuni no shōrai o ureu koe ga ōi.","german":"There are many voices worrying about the country's future.","furigana":"くに|しょうらい|うれ|こえ|おお"},{"japanese":"環境破壊を憂う若者たちが、駅前で署名を集めていた。","romaji":"Kankyō hakai o ureu wakamonotachi ga, ekimae de shomei o atsumete ita.","german":"Young people concerned about environmental destruction were collecting signatures in front of the station.","furigana":"かんきょうはかい|うれ|わかもの|えきまえ|しょめい|あつ"},{"japanese":"彼は友人の健康を憂い、病院に行くよう何度も勧めた。","romaji":"Kare wa yūjin no kenkō o urei, byōin ni iku yō nando mo susumeta.","german":"He was worried about his friend's health and urged him several times to go to the hospital.","furigana":"かれ|ゆうじん|けんこう|うれ|びょういん|い|なんど|すす"}],"notes":"憂う is a literary, elevated verb for deep concern about something serious or large-scale, often society or the future; the object takes を: 国の将来を憂う, 世を憂う. It is a more recent variant of the ichidan verb 憂える (憂えて, 憂えない), which is traditionally considered more correct; of 憂う, mainly the dictionary form and 憂い before a comma are common. In daily life one says 心配する. Related are the noun 憂い \"worry, sorrow\" and 憂鬱 \"melancholy\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"来月から新しい仕事を始める。","romaji":"Raigetsu kara atarashii shigoto o hajimeru.","german":"I'm starting a new job next month.","furigana":"らいげつ|あたら|しごと|はじ"},{"japanese":"では、会議を始めましょう。","romaji":"Dewa, kaigi o hajimemashō.","german":"Well then, let's start the meeting.","furigana":"かいぎ|はじ"},{"japanese":"雨が降り始めたので、急いで帰りました。","romaji":"Ame ga furihajimeta node, isoide kaerimashita.","german":"It started to rain, so I hurried home.","furigana":"あめ|ふ|はじ|いそ|かえ"}],"notes":"始める is transitive: you start something (仕事を始める, 会議を始めましょう); its intransitive partner 始まる says that something begins by itself (授業が始まる). After the verb stem, 〜始める means \"begin to ...\": 降り始める, 読み始める. Also \"to open a business\": 店を始める. Do not confuse it with 初めて \"for the first time\", which is written with the kanji 初.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 8"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"全ての作業を無事に終えた。","romaji":"Subete no sagyō wo buji ni oeta.","german":"I finished all the work without incident.","furigana":"すべ|さぎょう|ぶじ|お"},{"japanese":"この本を読み終えたら、貸してあげるよ。","romaji":"Kono hon o yomioetara, kashite ageru yo.","german":"When you finish reading this book, I'll lend it to you.","furigana":"ほん|よ|お|か"},{"japanese":"彼は大学を終えて、地元の会社に就職した。","romaji":"Kare wa daigaku o oete, jimoto no kaisha ni shūshoku shita.","german":"He finished university and got a job at a local company.","furigana":"かれ|だいがく|お|じもと|かいしゃ|しゅうしょく"}],"notes":"終える is transitive: 仕事を終える \"to finish the work\"; the intransitive counterpart is 終わる (仕事が終わる). It sounds somewhat more formal than 終わらせる and is often used in reports and news. After the verb stem it forms compounds such as 読み終える or 食べ終える \"to finish reading/eating.\" 学校を終える can also mean \"to complete school.\"","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大事なアナウンスを聞き逃してしまった。","romaji":"Daiji na anaunsu wo kikinogashite shimatta.","german":"I missed an important announcement.","furigana":"だいじ|き|のが"},{"japanese":"今の発言は聞き逃せませんね。","romaji":"Ima no hatsugen wa kikinogasemasen ne.","german":"I can't let that remark pass.","furigana":"いま|はつげん|き|のが"},{"japanese":"好きな番組を聞き逃さないよう、ラジオのタイマーをセットした。","romaji":"Suki na bangumi o kikinogasanai yō, rajio no taimā o setto shita.","german":"I set the radio timer so as not to miss my favorite program.","furigana":"す|ばんぐみ|き|のが"}],"notes":"聞き逃す means failing to catch something because one was inattentive or not there: アナウンスを聞き逃す. To be distinguished is 聞き取れない, where one is listening but acoustically cannot understand. In the negative potential form 聞き逃せない it means \"I can't just let that pass\". Parallel to it is 見逃す \"to overlook, to miss\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の方言が聞き取れなかった。","romaji":"Kare no hōgen ga kikitorenakatta.","german":"I couldn't catch his dialect.","furigana":"かれ|ほうげん|き|と"},{"japanese":"電話の音が小さくて、住所を聞き取れなかった。","romaji":"Denwa no oto ga chiisakute, jūsho o kikitorenakatta.","german":"The phone was so quiet that I couldn't make out the address.","furigana":"でんわ|おと|ちい|じゅうしょ|き|と"},{"japanese":"警察は目撃者から当時の様子を聞き取った。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa mokugekisha kara tōji no yōsu o kikitotta.","german":"The police took down from the eyewitness what the scene had been like at the time.","furigana":"けいさつ|もくげきしゃ|とうじ|ようす|き|と"}],"notes":"聞き取る means to acoustically grasp and understand what is spoken; very common in the potential form 聞き取れる/聞き取れない. The simple 聞く is broader (\"to hear, to listen, to ask\"). 聞き取り is also the name of the listening comprehension part of exams. In official and business language, 聞き取る also means \"to question, to have something explained\" (聞き取り調査 \"interview survey\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"相手の表情から気持ちを読み取った。","romaji":"Aite no hyōjō kara kimochi wo yomitotta.","german":"I read his feelings from the other person's expression.","furigana":"あいて|ひょうじょう|きも|よ|と"},{"japanese":"カメラでコードを読み取ると、地図が表示される。","romaji":"Kamera de kōdo o yomitoru to, chizu ga hyōji sareru.","german":"When you scan the code with the camera, a map is displayed.","furigana":"よ|と|ちず|ひょうじ"},{"japanese":"このグラフから読み取れることを説明してください。","romaji":"Kono gurafu kara yomitoreru koto o setsumei shite kudasai.","german":"Please explain what can be read from this graph.","furigana":"よ|と|せつめい"}],"notes":"読み取る means to read information or intent out of something visible: 表情から気持ちを読み取る, グラフから傾向を読み取る. The typical frame is 〜から〜を読み取る, often in the potential form 読み取れる. Technically it means \"to scan, to read in\" (コードを読み取る, 読み取り機 \"reader\"). For simply reading a text one says 読む; 察する is sensing without visible clues.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先生に言われてレポートを書き直した。","romaji":"Sensei ni iwarete repōto wo kakinaoshita.","german":"The teacher told me to, so I rewrote the report.","furigana":"せんせい|い|か|なお"},{"japanese":"字が汚かったので、宛名を書き直した。","romaji":"Ji ga kitanakatta node, atena o kakinaoshita.","german":"My handwriting was messy, so I rewrote the address.","furigana":"じ|きたな|あてな|か|なお"},{"japanese":"結末が気に入らなくて、小説の最後の章を書き直している。","romaji":"Ketsumatsu ga ki ni iranakute, shōsetsu no saigo no shō o kakinaoshite iru.","german":"I don't like the ending, so I'm rewriting the last chapter of the novel.","furigana":"けつまつ|き|い|しょうせつ|さいご|しょう|か|なお"}],"notes":"書き直す means \"to write once more (better)\": レポートを書き直す, 宛名を書き直す. The suffix 〜直す generally means \"to redo, to correct\", cf. やり直す, 言い直す. 書き換える, by contrast, stresses changing or replacing content (data, laws), whereas 書き直す rather means a new, improved version.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"重要なことをメモに書き留めた。","romaji":"Jūyō na koto wo memo ni kakitometa.","german":"I wrote the important things down in a memo.","furigana":"じゅうよう|か|と"},{"japanese":"祖母から聞いた昔話をノートに書き留めた。","romaji":"Sobo kara kiita mukashibanashi o nōto ni kakitometa.","german":"I wrote down in a notebook the old tales I heard from my grandmother.","furigana":"そぼ|き|むかしばなし|か|と"},{"japanese":"番号を言いますので、書き留めていただけますか。","romaji":"Bangō o iimasu node, kakitomete itadakemasu ka.","german":"I'll give you the number; could you please write it down?","furigana":"ばんごう|い|か|と"}],"notes":"書き留める means to put something in writing so that it is not forgotten: メモに書き留める, ノートに書き留める. The place takes に, the thing noted takes を. In daily life one often just says メモする; 書き留める sounds a little more careful and can also mean recording stories. 書き込む, by contrast, means \"to write into something\" (a form, a forum).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"難しい表現を簡単な言葉に言い換えた。","romaji":"Muzukashii hyōgen wo kantan na kotoba ni iikaeta.","german":"I rephrased the difficult expression in simple words.","furigana":"むずか|ひょうげん|かんたん|ことば|い|か"},{"japanese":"言い換えれば、彼は失敗を恐れているのだ。","romaji":"Iikaereba, kare wa shippai o osorete iru no da.","german":"In other words, he is afraid of failure.","furigana":"い|か|かれ|しっぱい|おそ"},{"japanese":"同じ言葉が続かないように、「大切」を「重要」に言い換えた。","romaji":"Onaji kotoba ga tsuzukanai yō ni, 'taisetsu' o 'jūyō' ni iikaeta.","german":"So that the same word wouldn't repeat, I replaced \"taisetsu\" with \"juyo\".","furigana":"おな|ことば|つづ|たいせつ|じゅうよう|い|か"}],"notes":"言い換える means to say the same thing in other words: AをBに言い換える. The meaning stays the same, only the expression changes; 言い直す, by contrast, rather means correcting oneself or saying it once more. Very common is the conditional form 言い換えれば \"in other words\" at the start of a sentence, equivalent to つまり. The noun 言い換え means \"paraphrase\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"説明に一言付け加えた。","romaji":"Setsumei ni hitokoto tsukekuwaeta.","german":"I added a word to the explanation.","furigana":"せつめい|ひとことつ|くわ"},{"japanese":"最後に一つ付け加えますと、明日の会議は十時からです。","romaji":"Saigo ni hitotsu tsukekuwaemasu to, ashita no kaigi wa jūji kara desu.","german":"One more thing to add at the end: tomorrow's meeting starts at ten.","furigana":"さいご|ひと|つ|くわ|あした|かいぎ|じゅうじ"},{"japanese":"レポートに参考文献のリストを付け加えた。","romaji":"Repōto ni sankō bunken no risuto o tsukekuwaeta.","german":"I added a list of references to the report.","furigana":"さんこうぶんけん|つ|くわ"}],"notes":"付け加える means to add something more to what already exists, especially words or explanations: 一言付け加える, 説明を付け加える. The target takes に. In speeches, 付け加えますと or 付け加えると introduces an addition. 加える is more general (also ingredients, pressure), and 付け足す is more colloquial and stresses attaching something afterwards.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"税金を差し引いた金額が手取りです。","romaji":"Zeikin wo sashihiita kingaku ga tedori desu.","german":"The amount after deducting taxes is the take-home pay.","furigana":"ぜいきん|さ|ひ|きんがく|てど"},{"japanese":"ホテルの料金から、ポイント分が差し引かれた。","romaji":"Hoteru no ryōkin kara, pointo-bun ga sashihikareta.","german":"The value of my points was deducted from the hotel price.","furigana":"りょうきん|ぶん|さ|ひ"},{"japanese":"彼の話は少し差し引いて聞いたほうがいい。","romaji":"Kare no hanashi wa sukoshi sashihiite kiita hō ga ii.","german":"You should take his stories with a grain of salt.","furigana":"かれ|はなし|すこ|さ|ひ|き"}],"notes":"差し引く means \"to deduct\", especially of sums of money: 給料から税金を差し引く, often in the passive 〜から差し引かれる. The noun 差し引き means \"balance, net result\". Figuratively, 差し引いて聞く/考える means to view something with some discount. In school arithmetic one simply says 引く; 控除する is the technical tax term.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"庭の雑草を取り除いた。","romaji":"Niwa no zassō o torinozoita.","german":"I removed the weeds in the garden.","furigana":"にわ|ざっそう|と|のぞ"},{"japanese":"魚の骨を取り除いてから、子どもに食べさせた。","romaji":"Sakana no hone o torinozoite kara, kodomo ni tabesaseta.","german":"I removed the fish bones before letting my child eat it.","furigana":"さかな|ほね|と|のぞ|こ|た"},{"japanese":"患者の不安を取り除くのも、医者の大切な仕事だ。","romaji":"Kanja no fuan o torinozoku no mo, isha no taisetsu na shigoto da.","german":"Relieving patients' anxiety is also an important part of a doctor's job.","furigana":"かんじゃ|ふあん|と|のぞ|いしゃ|たいせつ|しごと"}],"notes":"取り除く means \"to remove, eliminate\" - something disturbing or unwanted is taken out: ゴミ・雑草・原因・不安を取り除く. Transitive with を; the place of origin takes から: スープから脂を取り除く. 除く alone rather means \"to exclude, except\" (日曜日を除く \"except Sundays\"), and 取り外す means \"to detach\" (parts that can be reattached). Very often also abstract, with feelings and problems.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"家の壁を白く塗り替えた。","romaji":"Ie no kabe wo shiroku nurikaeta.","german":"I repainted the walls of the house white.","furigana":"いえ|かべ|しろ|ぬ|か"},{"japanese":"彼女は世界記録を塗り替えた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa sekai kiroku o nurikaeta.","german":"She broke the world record.","furigana":"かのじょ|せかいきろく|ぬ|か"},{"japanese":"この新製品は業界の常識を塗り替えた。","romaji":"Kono shinseihin wa gyōkai no jōshiki o nurikaeta.","german":"This new product rewrote the common sense of the industry.","furigana":"しんせいひん|ぎょうかい|じょうしき|ぬ|か"}],"notes":"塗り替える first means \"to repaint\", often in a different color: 壁を白く塗り替える. Figuratively it means \"to redo completely\": 記録を塗り替える \"to break a record\", 地図を塗り替える \"to change the balance of power\". 塗り直す, by contrast, only means touching up or repainting, usually in the same color.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"チームのメンバーを組み替えた。","romaji":"Chīmu no menbā wo kumikaeta.","german":"I rearranged the members of the team.","furigana":"く|か"},{"japanese":"新学期にはクラスが組み替えられる。","romaji":"Shingakki ni wa kurasu ga kumikaerareru.","german":"The classes will be reshuffled in the new school term.","furigana":"しんがっき|く|か"},{"japanese":"急な出張が入ったので、来週の予定を組み替えた。","romaji":"Kyū na shutchō ga haitta node, raishū no yotei o kumikaeta.","german":"A sudden business trip came up, so I rearranged my plans for next week.","furigana":"きゅう|しゅっちょう|はい|らいしゅう|よてい|く|か"}],"notes":"組み替える means to rearrange the composition of something: クラスを組み替える \"to reassign classes\", 予定を組み替える \"to rearrange a plan\", 予算を組み替える. In biology it is mostly written 組み換える (遺伝子組み換え \"genetic engineering\"). 入れ替える stresses exchanging individual parts, 並べ替える only the order.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"古い電池を新しいものに入れ替えた。","romaji":"Furui denchi wo atarashii mono ni irekaeta.","german":"I replaced the old batteries with new ones.","furigana":"ふる|でんち|あたら|い|か"},{"japanese":"窓を開けて部屋の空気を入れ替えよう。","romaji":"Mado o akete heya no kūki o irekaeyō.","german":"Let's open the window and air out the room.","furigana":"まど|あ|へや|くうき|い|か"},{"japanese":"心を入れ替えて、真面目に働くことにした。","romaji":"Kokoro o irekaete, majime ni hataraku koto ni shita.","german":"I turned over a new leaf and decided to work seriously.","furigana":"こころ|い|か|まじめ|はたら"}],"notes":"入れ替える means \"to exchange, to swap\": AをBに入れ替える \"to replace A with B\"; the intransitive counterpart is 入れ替わる. Fixed expressions are 空気を入れ替える \"to air out\" and 心を入れ替える \"to reform, to become a new person\". 取り替える is used more for replacing a broken part or exchanging with someone; 交換する is more neutral and formal.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"気持ちを切り替えて次の試合に集中した。","romaji":"Kimochi wo kirikaete tsugi no shiai ni shūchū shita.","german":"I switched gears and focused on the next match.","furigana":"きも|き|か|つぎ|しあい|しゅうちゅう"},{"japanese":"画面を日本語表示に切り替えてください。","romaji":"Gamen o Nihongo hyōji ni kirikaete kudasai.","german":"Please switch the display to Japanese.","furigana":"がめん|にほんごひょうじ|き|か"},{"japanese":"来月から電気の契約を別の会社に切り替える予定だ。","romaji":"Raigetsu kara denki no keiyaku o betsu no kaisha ni kirikaeru yotei da.","german":"I plan to switch my electricity contract to another company from next month.","furigana":"らいげつ|でんき|けいやく|べつ|かいしゃ|き|か|よてい"}],"notes":"切り替える means to switch from one state or system to another: AをBに切り替える; intransitive 切り替わる. Typical are 気持ちを切り替える \"to switch mental gears, move on\", 画面/モードを切り替える and changing over contracts or systems. 変える is the general \"to change\"; 切り替える stresses a clear switch between two options. The noun 切り替え means \"switchover\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"難しい仕事を引き受けた。","romaji":"Muzukashii shigoto wo hikiuketa.","german":"I took on the difficult job.","furigana":"むずか|しごと|ひ|う"},{"japanese":"旅行中の猫の世話を隣人が引き受けてくれた。","romaji":"Ryokōchū no neko no sewa o rinjin ga hikiukete kureta.","german":"My neighbor took care of the cat while I was traveling.","furigana":"りょこうちゅう|ねこ|せわ|りんじん|ひ|う"},{"japanese":"結婚式のスピーチを頼まれて、快く引き受けた。","romaji":"Kekkonshiki no supīchi o tanomarete, kokoroyoku hikiuketa.","german":"I was asked to give a speech at a wedding and gladly accepted.","furigana":"けっこんしき|たの|こころよ|ひ|う"}],"notes":"引き受ける means to take on a task, role or responsibility, usually at the request of others: 仕事を引き受ける, 世話を引き受ける. Typical are 快く引き受ける \"to gladly take on\" and 引き受けてくれる. 受け取る, by contrast, only means to receive an object, and 受け入れる to accept a proposal or people. 担当する rather describes a fixed responsibility.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"天気が悪くなったので引き返した。","romaji":"Tenki ga waruku natta node hikikaeshita.","german":"The weather got bad, so I turned back.","furigana":"てんき|わる|ひ|かえ"},{"japanese":"忘れ物に気づいて、駅から家に引き返した。","romaji":"Wasuremono ni kizuite, eki kara ie ni hikikaeshita.","german":"I noticed I had forgotten something and went back home from the station.","furigana":"わす|もの|き|えき|いえ|ひ|かえ"},{"japanese":"計画はもう引き返せないところまで進んでいる。","romaji":"Keikaku wa mō hikikaesenai tokoro made susunde iru.","german":"The plan has advanced to the point of no return.","furigana":"けいかく|ひ|かえ|すす"}],"notes":"引き返す is intransitive and means \"to turn back halfway\": 途中で引き返す, 駅から家に引き返す; with を the path taken back is expressed (来た道を引き返す). 戻る is the general \"to return\", whereas 引き返す stresses turning around before reaching the destination. Figuratively, もう引き返せない means \"there is no turning back\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"困難にも負けずに突き進んだ。","romaji":"Konnan ni mo makezu ni tsukisusunda.","german":"I pressed on without giving in to difficulties.","furigana":"こんなん|ま|つ|すす"},{"japanese":"一度決めたら、自分の信じる道を突き進むだけだ。","romaji":"Ichido kimetara, jibun no shinjiru michi o tsukisusumu dake da.","german":"Once I've decided, I just push forward on the path I believe in.","furigana":"いちどき|じぶん|しん|みち|つ|すす"},{"japanese":"選手たちはゴールに向かって雨の中を突き進んだ。","romaji":"Senshutachi wa gōru ni mukatte ame no naka o tsukisusunda.","german":"The athletes pushed through the rain toward the finish line.","furigana":"せんしゅ|む|あめ|なか|つ|すす"}],"notes":"突き進む is intransitive and means to advance energetically without hesitation, literally and figuratively: 夢に向かって突き進む, 信じる道を突き進む. Depending on context it sounds determined and positive or reckless and blind. It is stronger than 進む; colloquially similar is 突っ走る.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"列に割り込むのは失礼だ。","romaji":"Retsu ni warikomu no wa shitsurei da.","german":"It is rude to cut in line.","furigana":"れつ|わ|こ|しつれい"},{"japanese":"人の話に割り込まないでください。","romaji":"Hito no hanashi ni warikomanaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't interrupt other people when they are talking.","furigana":"ひと|はなし|わ|こ"},{"japanese":"今月の売上は目標を大きく割り込んだ。","romaji":"Kongetsu no uriage wa mokuhyō o ōkiku warikonda.","german":"This month's sales fell well below the target.","furigana":"こんげつ|うりあげ|もくひょう|おお|わ|こ"}],"notes":"割り込む means to push one's way into something: 列に割り込む \"to cut in line\", 話に割り込む \"to interrupt someone\", in traffic 車線に割り込む \"to cut into a lane\". With を, with numbers it means \"to fall below a mark\": 目標を割り込む. The noun 割り込み means \"cutting in\"; colloquially one also says 横入りする.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"塀を飛び越えて庭に入った。","romaji":"Hei wo tobikoete niwa ni haitta.","german":"I jumped over the fence and into the garden.","furigana":"へい|と|こ|にわ|はい"},{"japanese":"鳥たちは海を飛び越えて南の国へ渡っていく。","romaji":"Toritachi wa umi o tobikoete minami no kuni e watatte iku.","german":"Birds fly across the sea to the southern countries.","furigana":"とり|うみ|と|こ|みなみ|くに|わた"},{"japanese":"彼は先輩たちを飛び越えて部長に昇進した。","romaji":"Kare wa senpaitachi o tobikoete buchō ni shōshin shita.","german":"He was promoted to department head, skipping over his seniors.","furigana":"かれ|せんぱい|と|こ|ぶちょう|しょうしん"}],"notes":"飛び越える means \"to jump over something\": 塀を飛び越える; the obstacle takes を. For jumping with the legs, the spelling 跳び越える is also seen. Figuratively it means to skip over borders or sequences: 国境を飛び越える, 先輩を飛び越えて昇進する. 越える alone only means \"to cross, to exceed\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"夜空を見上げて星を眺めた。","romaji":"Yozora wo miagete hoshi wo nagameta.","german":"I looked up at the night sky and gazed at the stars.","furigana":"よぞら|みあ|ほし|なが"},{"japanese":"高いビルを見上げていたら、首が痛くなった。","romaji":"Takai biru o miagete itara, kubi ga itaku natta.","german":"I got a stiff neck from looking up at the tall building.","furigana":"たか|みあ|くび|いた"},{"japanese":"毎朝ごみを拾うとは、見上げた心がけだね。","romaji":"Maiasa gomi o hirou to wa, miageta kokorogake da ne.","german":"Picking up trash every morning - what admirable dedication.","furigana":"まいあさ|ひろ|みあ|こころ"}],"notes":"見上げる means \"to look up at something\": 空を見上げる, ビルを見上げる; the opposite is 見下ろす. Figuratively it means \"to look up to someone, to admire\"; today mainly in the fixed form 見上げた \"admirable\" (見上げた心がけ, 見上げた根性). For \"to revere\" in daily life one rather says 尊敬する.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"展望台から町を見下ろした。","romaji":"Tenbōdai kara machi wo mioroshita.","german":"I looked down at the town from the observation deck.","furigana":"てんぼうだい|まち|みお"},{"japanese":"丘の上のホテルからは海が見下ろせる。","romaji":"Oka no ue no hoteru kara wa umi ga mioroseru.","german":"From the hotel on the hill, you can look down on the sea.","furigana":"おか|うえ|うみ|みお"},{"japanese":"背の高い彼は、いつも人を見下ろすように話す。","romaji":"Se no takai kare wa, itsumo hito o miorosu yō ni hanasu.","german":"The tall man always talks as if he were looking down on people.","furigana":"せ|たか|かれ|ひと|みお|はな"}],"notes":"見下ろす means \"to look down on something from above\": 展望台から町を見下ろす; the opposite is 見上げる. With the potential form one describes views: 海が見下ろせる部屋. The meaning \"to look down on someone, to despise\" is possible but is mostly expressed with 見下す (みくだす).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"椅子から立ち上がって挨拶をした。","romaji":"Isu kara tachiagatte aisatsu wo shita.","german":"I stood up from my chair and gave a greeting.","furigana":"いす|た|あ|あいさつ"},{"japanese":"パソコンがなかなか立ち上がらない。","romaji":"Pasokon ga nakanaka tachiagaranai.","german":"The computer won't start up for a long time.","furigana":"た|あ"},{"japanese":"地震の後、町の人々は力を合わせて立ち上がった。","romaji":"Jishin no ato, machi no hitobito wa chikara o awasete tachiagatta.","german":"After the earthquake, the people of the town joined forces and got back on their feet.","furigana":"じしん|あと|まち|ひとびと|ちから|あ|た|あ"}],"notes":"立ち上がる is intransitive: to get up from sitting or lying (椅子から立ち上がる), as opposed to 起きる \"to wake up, get out of bed.\" Figuratively it means \"to pick oneself up again\" or \"to take action\" (困難から立ち上がる). For computers it means \"to boot up.\" The transitive counterpart 立ち上げる means \"to start, found\" (パソコン/会社を立ち上げる).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"美しい景色を見て立ち止まった。","romaji":"Utsukushii keshiki wo mite tachidomatta.","german":"I stopped when I saw the beautiful scenery.","furigana":"うつく|けしき|み|た|ど"},{"japanese":"名前を呼ばれて、思わず立ち止まった。","romaji":"Namae o yobarete, omowazu tachidomatta.","german":"When someone called my name, I stopped involuntarily.","furigana":"なまえ|よ|おも|た|ど"},{"japanese":"忙しい毎日でも、たまには立ち止まって人生を考えたい。","romaji":"Isogashii mainichi demo, tama ni wa tachidomatte jinsei o kangaetai.","german":"Even in busy daily life, I want to pause once in a while and think about life.","furigana":"いそが|まいにち|た|ど|じんせい|かんが"}],"notes":"立ち止まる is intransitive and is used for people (and animals) who stop while walking: 足を止めて立ち止まる, 思わず立ち止まる. For vehicles and machines one says 止まる. Figuratively, 立ち止まって考える means \"to stop and think\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"疲れて道端に座り込んだ。","romaji":"Tsukarete michibata ni suwarikonda.","german":"I was so tired that I sat down at the roadside.","furigana":"つか|みちばた|すわ|こ"},{"japanese":"知らせを聞いて、彼女はその場に座り込んでしまった。","romaji":"Shirase o kiite, kanojo wa sono ba ni suwarikonde shimatta.","german":"When she heard the news, she sank to the ground on the spot.","furigana":"し|き|かのじょ|ば|すわ|こ"},{"japanese":"住民たちは工事に反対して、現場に座り込んだ。","romaji":"Jūmintachi wa kōji ni hantai shite, genba ni suwarikonda.","german":"Residents held a sit-in at the site in protest against the construction.","furigana":"じゅうみん|こうじ|はんたい|げんば|すわ|こ"}],"notes":"座り込む is intransitive and means to sit down and stay sitting, often from exhaustion or shock: 道端に座り込む, その場に座り込む. The place takes に. It can also mean \"to stage a sit-in\"; the noun 座り込み means \"sit-in blockade\". The neutral 座る only describes sitting down.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"芝生の上に寝転んで空を見た。","romaji":"Shibafu no ue ni nekoronde sora wo mita.","german":"I lay on the lawn and looked at the sky.","furigana":"しばふ|うえ|ねころ|そら|み"},{"japanese":"ソファに寝転んでテレビを見ていたら、母に怒られた。","romaji":"Sofa ni nekoronde terebi o mite itara, haha ni okorareta.","german":"I was lying on the sofa watching TV when my mother scolded me.","furigana":"ねころ|み|はは|おこ"},{"japanese":"暑い日は床に寝転ぶと、背中が冷たくて気持ちいい。","romaji":"Atsui hi wa yuka ni nekorobu to, senaka ga tsumetakute kimochi ii.","german":"On hot days, lying on the floor feels nice, with the coolness on your back.","furigana":"あつ|ひ|ゆか|ねころ|せなか|つめ|きも"}],"notes":"寝転ぶ is intransitive and means to lie down loosely and comfortably, without intending to sleep: 芝生に寝転ぶ, ソファで寝転ぶ. The place takes に (destination) or で (place of the action). Synonymous and somewhat more colloquial is 寝転がる; neutral and polite is 横になる. 寝る, by contrast, mostly means \"to sleep, to go to bed\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"締め切りぎりぎりに滑り込んだ。","romaji":"Shimekiri girigiri ni suberikonda.","german":"I just made it right at the deadline.","furigana":"し|き|すべ|こ"},{"japanese":"ランナーが二塁に滑り込んだ。","romaji":"Rannā ga nirui ni suberikonda.","german":"The runner slid into second base.","furigana":"にるい|すべ|こ"},{"japanese":"発車のベルが鳴る中、電車に滑り込んだ。","romaji":"Hassha no beru ga naru naka, densha ni suberikonda.","german":"As the departure bell rang, I slipped onto the train.","furigana":"はっしゃ|な|なか|でんしゃ|すべ|こ"}],"notes":"滑り込む is intransitive and literally means \"to slide in\": 二塁に滑り込む in baseball, 布団に滑り込む. Very common is the figurative meaning \"to just make it\": 締め切りに滑り込む, 発車間際の電車に滑り込む. The expression 滑り込みセーフ \"just in time\" belongs here. The destination takes に.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鳥が木の枝から飛び立った。","romaji":"Tori ga ki no eda kara tobitatta.","german":"The bird flew off from the tree branch.","furigana":"とり|き|えだ|と|た"},{"japanese":"飛行機は予定通り成田から飛び立った。","romaji":"Hikōki wa yotei dōri Narita kara tobitatta.","german":"The plane took off from Narita as scheduled.","furigana":"ひこうき|よていどお|なりた|と|た"},{"japanese":"卒業生たちは社会へ飛び立っていった。","romaji":"Sotsugyōseitachi wa shakai e tobitatte itta.","german":"The graduates have flown off into society.","furigana":"そつぎょうせい|しゃかい|と|た"}],"notes":"飛び立つ is intransitive and describes birds and aircraft taking off: 〜から飛び立つ, 空へ飛び立つ. 離陸する is the technical term for aircraft, 飛ぶ is simply \"to fly\". Figuratively it means setting out into life or the world: 社会へ飛び立つ, 世界へ飛び立つ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子供の才能を育むことが大切だ。","romaji":"Kodomo no sainō wo hagukumu koto ga taisetsu da.","german":"It is important to nurture children's talents.","furigana":"こども|さいのう|はぐく|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"二人は長い時間をかけて友情を育んできた。","romaji":"Futari wa nagai jikan o kakete yūjō o hagukunde kita.","german":"The two of them have nurtured their friendship over a long time.","furigana":"ふたり|なが|じかん|ゆうじょう|はぐく"},{"japanese":"豊かな森が多くの生き物を育んでいる。","romaji":"Yutaka na mori ga ōku no ikimono o hagukunde iru.","german":"The rich forest nurtures many living things.","furigana":"ゆた|もり|おお|い|もの|はぐく"}],"notes":"育む is an elevated, rather literary verb for \"to nurture, to foster, to let flourish\": 才能を育む, 友情/愛を育む, 自然が命を育む. For the concrete raising of children, animals or plants, daily speech uses 育てる. 育む stresses the careful, long-term growing of something valuable, often abstract.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先生が生徒たちを正しい道に導いた。","romaji":"Sensei ga seitotachi wo tadashii michi ni michibiita.","german":"The teacher guided the students onto the right path.","furigana":"せんせい|せいと|ただ|みち|みちび"},{"japanese":"彼の活躍がチームを勝利に導いた。","romaji":"Kare no katsuyaku ga chīmu o shōri ni michibiita.","german":"His strong performance led the team to victory.","furigana":"かれ|かつやく|しょうり|みちび"},{"japanese":"データから結論を導くのは簡単ではない。","romaji":"Dēta kara ketsuron o michibiku no wa kantan de wa nai.","german":"It is not easy to draw a conclusion from data.","furigana":"けつろん|みちび|かんたん"}],"notes":"導く means to lead someone to a goal or result: AをBに導く, e.g. チームを勝利に導く, 成功に導く. 案内する, by contrast, is the concrete leading and showing of a way, 率いる the leading of a group. In logic and mathematics, 導く (or 導き出す) means \"to derive, deduce\": 結論を導く. The noun 導き means \"guidance\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼がチームを率いて優勝した。","romaji":"Kare ga chīmu wo hikiite yūshō shita.","german":"He led the team to the championship.","furigana":"かれ|ひき|ゆうしょう"},{"japanese":"社長は三千人の社員を率いている。","romaji":"Shachō wa sanzennin no shain o hikiite iru.","german":"The company president heads three thousand employees.","furigana":"しゃちょう|さんぜんにん|しゃいん|ひき"},{"japanese":"先生に率いられて、生徒たちは博物館を見学した。","romaji":"Sensei ni hikiirarete, seitotachi wa hakubutsukan o kengaku shita.","german":"Led by their teacher, the students toured the museum.","furigana":"せんせい|ひき|せいと|はくぶつかん|けんがく"}],"notes":"率いる means to lead a group as its leader: チーム/軍/会社を率いる. In the passive, 〜に率いられて describes who is leading a group. It stresses the position at the head of a group; 導く, by contrast, stresses guiding toward a goal, and colloquially one says 引っ張る. Note: it is an ichidan verb (率いて, 率いない).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"部下を従えて会議に臨んだ。","romaji":"Buka wo shitagaete kaigi ni nozonda.","german":"He attended the meeting accompanied by his subordinates.","furigana":"ぶか|したが|かいぎ|のぞ"},{"japanese":"歌手はバンドを従えてステージに登場した。","romaji":"Kashu wa bando o shitagaete sutēji ni tōjō shita.","german":"The singer appeared on stage with her band behind her.","furigana":"かしゅ|したが|とうじょう"},{"japanese":"その王は周りの国々を次々と従えた。","romaji":"Sono ō wa mawari no kuniguni o tsugitsugi to shitagaeta.","german":"The king subdued the surrounding countries one after another.","furigana":"おう|まわ|くにぐに|つぎつぎ|したが"}],"notes":"従える is the transitive counterpart of 従う \"to follow, to obey\" and means \"to have someone in one's retinue, to lead along\": 部下を従えて, バンドを従えて. It is mostly used in the て-form before a verb of movement or action. In historical texts it also means \"to subjugate\" (国々を従える). It sounds elevated to literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は長年王に仕えた。","romaji":"Kare wa naganen ō ni tsukaeta.","german":"He served the king for many years.","furigana":"かれ|ながねんおう|つか"},{"japanese":"神に仕える身として、正直に生きたい。","romaji":"Kami ni tsukaeru mi to shite, shōjiki ni ikitai.","german":"As someone who serves God, I want to live honestly.","furigana":"かみ|つか|み|しょうじき|い"},{"japanese":"彼女は三代の社長に仕えてきたベテラン秘書だ。","romaji":"Kanojo wa sandai no shachō ni tsukaete kita beteran hisho da.","german":"She is a veteran secretary who has served three company presidents.","furigana":"かのじょ|さんだい|しゃちょう|つか|ひしょ"}],"notes":"仕える is intransitive and means \"to serve (someone)\", with に: 王に仕える, 神に仕える. It sounds old-fashioned or solemn and occurs mainly in historical or religious contexts; for ordinary employment one says 勤める or 働く. Do not confuse it with 使える \"to be usable\" and つかえる \"to get stuck\", which are read the same.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しいアルバイトを三人雇った。","romaji":"Atarashii arubaito wo sannin yatotta.","german":"We hired three new part-timers.","furigana":"あたら|さんにんやと"},{"japanese":"弁護士を雇って裁判に備えた。","romaji":"Bengoshi o yatotte saiban ni sonaeta.","german":"I hired a lawyer to prepare for the trial.","furigana":"べんごし|やと|さいばん|そな"},{"japanese":"今は小さな会社に雇われて働いている。","romaji":"Ima wa chiisana kaisha ni yatowarete hataraite iru.","german":"I'm currently working for a small company.","furigana":"いま|ちい|かいしゃ|やと|はたら"}],"notes":"雇う means \"to employ, hire\": アルバイトを雇う, 弁護士を雇う. In the passive, 〜に雇われる means \"to be employed by someone\"; 雇い主 is the employer. 採用する is more formal and stresses the decision in the application process. With vehicles, 雇う also means \"to charter, rent\" (船を雇う).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この仕事は君に任せるよ。","romaji":"Kono shigoto wa kimi ni makaseru yo.","german":"I'll leave this job to you.","furigana":"しごと|きみ|まか"},{"japanese":"準備は全部やったから、結果は運に任せるしかない。","romaji":"Junbi wa zenbu yatta kara, kekka wa un ni makaseru shika nai.","german":"I've done all the preparation, so all I can do is leave the result to luck.","furigana":"じゅんび|ぜんぶ|けっか|うん|まか"},{"japanese":"その後どうなったかは、ご想像にお任せします。","romaji":"Sono ato dō natta ka wa, go-sōzō ni o-makase shimasu.","german":"I'll leave it to your imagination what happened after that.","furigana":"あと|そうぞう|まか"}],"notes":"任せる means to leave a task or decision to someone: 〜に〜を任せる; 任せて！ \"Leave it to me!\" It can also mean leaving something to chance or its own course: 運に任せる, ご想像にお任せします. 頼む is merely asking, 委ねる the more formal variant. 力に任せて means \"by brute force.\"","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"最終判断を委員長に委ねた。","romaji":"Saishū handan wo iinchō ni yudaneta.","german":"The final decision was left to the committee chairman.","furigana":"さいしゅうはんだん|いいんちょう|ゆだ"},{"japanese":"波の音に身を委ねて、ゆっくり休んだ。","romaji":"Nami no oto ni mi o yudanete, yukkuri yasunda.","german":"I gave myself over to the sound of the waves and rested slowly.","furigana":"なみ|おと|み|ゆだ|やす"},{"japanese":"子供の教育を学校だけに委ねるべきではない。","romaji":"Kodomo no kyōiku o gakkō dake ni yudaneru beki de wa nai.","german":"The education of children should not be left to schools alone.","furigana":"こども|きょういく|がっこう|ゆだ"}],"notes":"委ねる is the formal, literary equivalent of 任せる: 〜に判断を委ねる \"to leave the decision to someone\". It often appears in news, administration and law (判断を委ねる, 権限を委ねる). The expression 身を委ねる means \"to give oneself up, to let oneself drift\" (流れに身を委ねる).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"未来を若者に託す。","romaji":"Mirai wo wakamono ni takusu.","german":"To entrust the future to the young.","furigana":"みらい|わかもの|たく"},{"japanese":"友人に手紙を託して、彼女に届けてもらった。","romaji":"Yūjin ni tegami o takushite, kanojo ni todokete moratta.","german":"I entrusted a letter to a friend so that she would deliver it to her.","furigana":"ゆうじん|てがみ|たく|かのじょ|とど"},{"japanese":"彼は自分の思いをこの歌に託した。","romaji":"Kare wa jibun no omoi o kono uta ni takushita.","german":"He put his feelings into this song.","furigana":"かれ|じぶん|おも|うた|たく"}],"notes":"託す means to entrust something important to someone: 〜に〜を託す, such as 未来/夢/希望を託す. One can also have a message or object conveyed through someone (伝言を託す). Figuratively it means to express feelings in something: 思いを歌に託す. 任せる is more everyday and refers to tasks, 預ける to having things kept.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"強盗が女性のバッグを奪った。","romaji":"Gōtō ga josei no baggu wo ubatta.","german":"A robber snatched the woman's bag.","furigana":"ごうとう|じょせい|うば"},{"japanese":"その事故は多くの命を奪った。","romaji":"Sono jiko wa ōku no inochi o ubatta.","german":"That accident took many lives.","furigana":"じこ|おお|いのち|うば"},{"japanese":"美しい景色に目を奪われた。","romaji":"Utsukushii keshiki ni me o ubawareta.","german":"My eyes were captivated by the beautiful scenery.","furigana":"うつく|けしき|め|うば"}],"notes":"奪う means to take something from someone by force or against their will: 〜から〜を奪う, バッグを奪う. Unlike 盗む \"to steal secretly,\" 奪う stresses violent snatching. Abstract objects are common: 命を奪う, 時間を奪う, 自由を奪う. In the passive, 目/心を奪われる means \"to be captivated, enraptured.\"","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"法律に違反した者は罰せられる。","romaji":"Hōritsu ni ihan shita mono wa basserareru.","german":"Those who violate the law are punished.","furigana":"ほうりつ|いはん|もの|ばっ"},{"japanese":"子供を厳しく罰するだけでは、問題は解決しない。","romaji":"Kodomo o kibishiku bassuru dake de wa, mondai wa kaiketsu shinai.","german":"Just punishing children harshly won't solve the problem.","furigana":"こども|きび|ばっ|もんだい|かいけつ"},{"japanese":"彼は自分を罰するかのように、毎晩遅くまで働いた。","romaji":"Kare wa jibun o bassuru ka no yō ni, maiban osoku made hataraita.","german":"As if to punish himself, he worked late every night.","furigana":"かれ|じぶん|ばっ|まいばんおそ|はたら"}],"notes":"罰する is a formal verb for \"to punish,\" often in a legal context and frequently in the passive 罰せられる. It belongs to the special する class: 罰して, 罰しない, passive 罰せられる. In everyday speech people more often say 罰を与える; 叱る only means \"to scold,\" and 処罰する is more official.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は相手を大声で罵った。","romaji":"Kare wa aite wo ōgoe de nonoshitta.","german":"He loudly abused the other person.","furigana":"かれ|あいて|おおごえ|ののし"},{"japanese":"ネットで知らない人を罵るのはやめよう。","romaji":"Netto de shiranai hito o nonoshiru no wa yameyō.","german":"Let's stop abusing strangers online.","furigana":"し|ひと|ののし"},{"japanese":"負けたチームの監督を、ファンが激しく罵った。","romaji":"Maketa chīmu no kantoku o, fan ga hageshiku nonoshitta.","german":"The fans violently abused the manager of the losing team.","furigana":"ま|かんとく|はげ|ののし"}],"notes":"罵る means to abuse someone fiercely and loudly: 〜を罵る, often with 大声で or 口汚く. It is a strong, rather literary word. 悪口を言う means rather \"to speak ill of someone\", often behind their back; 怒鳴る is \"to yell at\" without necessarily using insulting words. The noun 罵り means \"abuse, insult\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は自分の運命を呪った。","romaji":"Kanojo wa jibun no unmei wo norotta.","german":"She cursed her fate.","furigana":"かのじょ|じぶん|うんめい|のろ"},{"japanese":"この家は呪われているといううわさがある。","romaji":"Kono ie wa norowarete iru to iu uwasa ga aru.","german":"There is a rumor that this house is cursed.","furigana":"いえ|のろ"},{"japanese":"寝坊した自分を呪いながら駅まで走った。","romaji":"Nebō shita jibun o noroinagara eki made hashitta.","german":"I ran to the station cursing myself for oversleeping.","furigana":"ねぼう|じぶん|のろ|えき|はし"}],"notes":"呪う means \"to curse, to put a curse on someone\": 〜を呪う. Literally it appears in ghost and horror stories (呪われた家, 呪い \"curse\"). Figuratively it expresses fierce resentment against one's own fate or oneself: 運命を呪う, 自分の不注意を呪う. Do not confuse it with 罵る, loudly insulting someone.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"友人の結婚を祝った。","romaji":"Yūjin no kekkon wo iwatta.","german":"I celebrated my friend's wedding.","furigana":"ゆうじん|けっこん|いわ"},{"japanese":"家族で祖母の八十歳の誕生日を祝った。","romaji":"Kazoku de sobo no hachijissai no tanjōbi o iwatta.","german":"The family celebrated my grandmother's eightieth birthday.","furigana":"かぞく|そぼ|はちじっさい|たんじょうび|いわ"},{"japanese":"合格を祝って、みんなで乾杯した。","romaji":"Gōkaku o iwatte, minna de kanpai shita.","german":"We all made a toast to celebrate passing the exam.","furigana":"ごうかく|いわ|かんぱい"}],"notes":"祝う means to celebrate a joyful occasion or to congratulate on it: 誕生日を祝う, 合格を祝う. The object is the occasion, not the person; \"to congratulate someone\" is 〜におめでとうと言う. The noun お祝い means \"celebration, congratulations, gift.\" For a party without a special occasion, people more often say パーティーをする; 祝福する is more solemn.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二度と嘘をつかないと誓った。","romaji":"Nido to uso wo tsukanai to chikatta.","german":"I swore never to lie again.","furigana":"にど|うそ|ちか"},{"japanese":"二人は神の前で永遠の愛を誓った。","romaji":"Futari wa kami no mae de eien no ai o chikatta.","german":"The two of them vowed eternal love before God.","furigana":"ふたり|かみ|まえ|えいえん|あい|ちか"},{"japanese":"次の大会では必ず優勝すると心に誓った。","romaji":"Tsugi no taikai de wa kanarazu yūshō suru to kokoro ni chikatta.","german":"I resolved in my heart to win at the next tournament without fail.","furigana":"つぎ|たいかい|かなら|ゆうしょう|こころ|ちか"}],"notes":"誓う means \"to swear, to vow solemnly\": 〜と誓う, 愛を誓う, 神に誓う. The one sworn to takes に, and the content is expressed with と or を; 心に誓う means \"to firmly resolve\". 約束する is the everyday \"to promise\"; 誓う is much more solemn and binding. The noun 誓い means \"oath, vow\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"明日また会うことを約束した。","romaji":"Ashita mata au koto wo yakusoku shita.","german":"We promised to meet again tomorrow.","furigana":"あした|あ|やくそく"},{"japanese":"母に早く帰ると約束したので、今日は飲みに行けない。","romaji":"Haha ni hayaku kaeru to yakusoku shita node, kyō wa nomi ni ikenai.","german":"I promised my mother I would come home early, so I can't go out drinking today.","furigana":"はは|はや|かえ|やくそく|きょう|の|い"},{"japanese":"友達と駅で三時に会う約束をしている。","romaji":"Tomodachi to eki de sanji ni au yakusoku o shite iru.","german":"I have arranged to meet a friend at the station at three o'clock.","furigana":"ともだち|えき|さんじ|あ|やくそく"}],"notes":"約束する means \"to promise\" and \"to arrange to meet\": 〜と約束する (content), 〜に約束する (person), 友達と会う約束をする. The noun 約束 appears in set phrases such as 約束を守る \"to keep a promise\" and 約束を破る \"to break\"; 約束がある means \"to have plans\". 誓う is more solemn, whereas 予約する means \"to reserve\".","levelNote":"Derived from 約束 (N5) (source: N5; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"重大な犯罪を犯した者は厳しく罰せられる。","romaji":"Jūdai na hanzai wo okashita mono wa kibishiku basserareru.","german":"Anyone who commits a serious crime will be severely punished.","furigana":"じゅうだい|はんざい|おか|もの|きび|ばっ"},{"japanese":"同じ過ちを二度と犯さないようにしたい。","romaji":"Onaji ayamachi o nido to okasanai yō ni shitai.","german":"I want to make sure never to make the same mistake twice.","furigana":"おな|あやま|にど|おか"},{"japanese":"知らないうちに法律を犯していることもある。","romaji":"Shiranai uchi ni hōritsu o okashite iru koto mo aru.","german":"Sometimes one breaks a law without knowing it.","furigana":"し|ほうりつ|おか"}],"notes":"犯す means \"to commit\" for crimes and mistakes (罪/過ち/ミスを犯す) and \"to violate, break\" (法を犯す). Read the same are 侵す \"to violate, invade\" (領土/権利を侵す) and 冒す \"to risk, to afflict\" (危険を冒す, 病に冒される); the choice of kanji matters. With a person as the object, 犯す means \"to rape\", so be careful with its use.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"記者が政治家の不正を暴いた。","romaji":"Kisha ga seijika no fusei wo abaita.","german":"A reporter exposed the politician's corruption.","furigana":"きしゃ|せいじか|ふせい|あば"},{"japanese":"探偵はついに犯人の正体を暴いた。","romaji":"Tantei wa tsui ni hannin no shōtai o abaita.","german":"The detective finally uncovered the culprit's true identity.","furigana":"たんてい|はんにん|しょうたい|あば"},{"japanese":"人の秘密を暴くようなことはしたくない。","romaji":"Hito no himitsu o abaku yō na koto wa shitakunai.","german":"I don't want to do anything like exposing other people's secrets.","furigana":"ひと|ひみつ|あば"}],"notes":"暴く means to bring something hidden, usually wrongdoing, to light: 不正/秘密/正体を暴く. It has an exposing, often accusatory undertone. More neutral is 明らかにする; colloquial is ばらす \"to blab\". Literally it can also mean \"to open a grave\" (墓を暴く).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は真実を隠そうとした。","romaji":"Kare wa shinjitsu wo kakusō to shita.","german":"He tried to hide the truth.","furigana":"かれ|しんじつ|かく"},{"japanese":"誕生日のプレゼントをベッドの下に隠した。","romaji":"Tanjōbi no purezento o beddo no shita ni kakushita.","german":"I hid the birthday present under the bed.","furigana":"たんじょうび|した|かく"},{"japanese":"彼女は恥ずかしそうに顔を手で隠した。","romaji":"Kanojo wa hazukashisō ni kao o te de kakushita.","german":"She covered her face with her hands, looking embarrassed.","furigana":"かのじょ|は|かお|て|かく"}],"notes":"隠す is transitive: to hide or conceal something (〜を〜に隠す); the intransitive counterpart is 隠れる \"to hide oneself.\" It applies to objects (プレゼントを隠す) as well as abstract things (真実/気持ちを隠す). The noun 隠し事 means \"secret,\" 隠さずに \"openly, without holding anything back.\"","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"怒りを抑えて冷静に話した。","romaji":"Ikari wo osaete reisei ni hanashita.","german":"I suppressed my anger and spoke calmly.","furigana":"いか|おさ|れいせい|はな"},{"japanese":"今月は出費を抑えないといけない。","romaji":"Kongetsu wa shuppi o osaenai to ikenai.","german":"This month I have to keep my spending down.","furigana":"こんげつ|しゅっぴ|おさ"},{"japanese":"薬で熱を抑えた。","romaji":"Kusuri de netsu o osaeta.","german":"I brought the fever down with medicine.","furigana":"くすり|ねつ|おさ"}],"notes":"抑える means to keep something within limits or hold it back: 怒り/感情を抑える, 費用/出費を抑える, 熱/痛みを抑える. Do not confuse it with the identically read 押さえる \"to hold down, to press, to secure\" (傷口を押さえる, 席を押さえる). Rule of thumb: 抑える for limiting and suppressing, 押さえる for physically holding down.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"長年の目的をついに遂げた。","romaji":"Naganen no mokuteki o tsui ni togeta.","german":"I finally accomplished my long-held goal.","furigana":"ながねん|もくてき|と"},{"japanese":"この町は十年で大きな発展を遂げた。","romaji":"Kono machi wa jūnen de ōkina hatten o togeta.","german":"This town underwent great development in ten years.","furigana":"まち|じゅうねん|おお|はってん|と"},{"japanese":"スマートフォンは驚くほどの進化を遂げてきた。","romaji":"Sumātofon wa odoroku hodo no shinka o togete kita.","german":"Smartphones have undergone astonishing evolution.","furigana":"おどろ|しんか|と"}],"notes":"遂げる is elevated and means \"to accomplish, realize\": 目的を遂げる, 思いを遂げる. Very often it describes reaching a result with nouns such as 発展/成長/進化/変化を遂げる \"to undergo a development\". In everyday speech the compound やり遂げる \"to see through to the end\" is more common; for goals one usually says 目標を達成する.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"患者に応急処置を施した。","romaji":"Kanja ni ōkyū shochi wo hodokoshita.","german":"First aid was administered to the patient.","furigana":"かんじゃ|おうきゅうしょち|ほどこ"},{"japanese":"この寺の壁には美しい彫刻が施されている。","romaji":"Kono tera no kabe ni wa utsukushii chōkoku ga hodokosarete iru.","german":"Beautiful carvings are applied to the walls of this temple.","furigana":"てら|かべ|うつく|ちょうこく|ほどこ"},{"japanese":"表示を見やすくするために、さまざまな工夫を施した。","romaji":"Hyōji o miyasuku suru tame ni, samazama na kufū o hodokoshita.","german":"To make the display easier to read, we incorporated various improvements.","furigana":"ひょうじ|み|くふう|ほどこ"}],"notes":"施す is a formal verb meaning \"to apply, carry out (a measure)\": 治療/処置を施す, 対策を施す. It also means \"to attach, provide with\" (装飾/化粧/工夫を施す), often in the passive 〜が施されている. The older meaning \"to give alms, donate\" survives in 施しを受ける. In everyday speech one mostly says する or 行う.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"赤ちゃんの肌は滑らかだ。","romaji":"Akachan no hada wa nameraka da.","german":"A baby's skin is smooth.","furigana":"あか|はだ|なめ"},{"japanese":"彼は日本語を滑らかに話す。","romaji":"Kare wa Nihongo o nameraka ni hanasu.","german":"He speaks Japanese fluently.","furigana":"かれ|にほんご|なめ|はな"},{"japanese":"このプリンはとても滑らかで、おいしい。","romaji":"Kono purin wa totemo nameraka de, oishii.","german":"This pudding is very smooth and delicious.","furigana":"なめ"}],"notes":"滑らか is a な-adjective: smooth and without bumps (滑らかな肌, 滑らかな表面) or fluent and frictionless (滑らかに話す, 動きが滑らかだ). It is often written in hiragana too: なめらか. すべすべ describes pleasantly smooth skin, つるつる smooth, slippery surfaces; for fluent speaking of a foreign language, 流暢 is more formal.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この靴は丈夫で長持ちする。","romaji":"Kono kutsu wa jōbu de nagamochi suru.","german":"These shoes are sturdy and last long.","furigana":"くつ|じょうぶ|ながも"},{"japanese":"祖母は九十歳になっても体が丈夫です。","romaji":"Sobo wa kyūjussai ni natte mo karada ga jōbu desu.","german":"My grandmother is still in robust health at ninety.","furigana":"そぼ|きゅうじゅっさい|からだ|じょうぶ"},{"japanese":"重い本を入れるなら、もっと丈夫な袋を使ったほうがいい。","romaji":"Omoi hon o ireru nara, motto jōbu na fukuro o tsukatta hō ga ii.","german":"If you're putting in heavy books, you'd better use a sturdier bag.","furigana":"おも|ほん|い|じょうぶ|ふくろ|つか"}],"notes":"丈夫 has two main meanings: for things \"sturdy, durable\" (丈夫な靴, 丈夫な袋), for people \"healthy, strong\", usually as 体が丈夫だ. The combination 丈夫で長持ちする \"sturdy and long-lasting\" is typical. The more massive 頑丈 rather stresses stability against great forces (頑丈な建物), while 丈夫 aims at durability in everyday use. Do not confuse it with 大丈夫 \"all right\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"頑丈な建物は地震にも耐えられる。","romaji":"Ganjō na tatemono wa jishin ni mo taerareru.","german":"A solid building can withstand even an earthquake.","furigana":"がんじょう|たてもの|じしん|た"},{"japanese":"このスーツケースは頑丈に作られているので、落としても壊れない。","romaji":"Kono sūtsukēsu wa ganjō ni tsukurarete iru node, otoshite mo kowarenai.","german":"This suitcase is built so solidly that it won't break even if you drop it.","furigana":"がんじょう|つく|お|こわ"},{"japanese":"彼はラグビー選手らしい頑丈な体をしている。","romaji":"Kare wa ragubī senshu rashii ganjō na karada o shite iru.","german":"He has the sturdy build typical of a rugby player.","furigana":"かれ|せんしゅ|がんじょう|からだ"}],"notes":"頑丈 describes something that is massively built and withstands great stress: 頑丈な建物, 頑丈な金庫, 頑丈な鍵. For people it means a \"strong, stocky\" physique (頑丈な体). Unlike 丈夫, which rather expresses durability in use and health, 頑丈 stresses force and firmness. Adverbially: 頑丈に作られている \"to be solidly built\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"華やかなドレスを着てパーティーに出席した。","romaji":"Hanayaka na doresu wo kite pātī ni shusseki shita.","german":"I attended the party wearing a gorgeous dress.","furigana":"はな|き|しゅっせき"},{"japanese":"花を飾ったら、部屋が一気に華やかになった。","romaji":"Hana o kazattara, heya ga ikki ni hanayaka ni natta.","german":"When I put up flowers, the room instantly became brighter and more festive.","furigana":"はな|かざ|へや|いっき|はな"},{"japanese":"芸能界は華やかに見えるが、実際は厳しい世界だ。","romaji":"Geinōkai wa hanayaka ni mieru ga, jissai wa kibishii sekai da.","german":"The entertainment world looks glamorous, but in reality it is a harsh world.","furigana":"げいのうかい|はな|み|じっさい|きび|せかい"}],"notes":"華やか means \"splendid, brilliant, colorful\" and describes something that attracts attention and looks festive: 華やかなドレス, 華やかな雰囲気, 華やかな世界 (e.g. show business). Unlike 派手, which often sounds negative (\"garish, flashy\"), 華やか is positive. The opposite is 地味 \"plain\". Also typical: 会場が華やかになる \"the hall becomes festive\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女はいつも地味な服装をしている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa itsumo jimi na fukusō wo shite iru.","german":"She always dresses plainly.","furigana":"かのじょ|じみ|ふくそう"},{"japanese":"データの入力は地味な作業だが、とても大切だ。","romaji":"Dēta no nyūryoku wa jimi na sagyō da ga, totemo taisetsu da.","german":"Data entry is unglamorous work, but it is very important.","furigana":"にゅうりょく|じみ|さぎょう|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"このネクタイは私には少し地味すぎるかな。","romaji":"Kono nekutai wa watashi ni wa sukoshi jimi sugiru ka na.","german":"Isn't this tie a bit too plain for me?","furigana":"わたし|すこ|じみ"}],"notes":"地味 means \"plain, inconspicuous, understated\" and is the opposite of 派手. It describes clothes and colors (地味な服, 地味な色), but also people who don't stand out, and tasks that are laborious and not very glamorous (地味な作業). Depending on context it sounds neutral to slightly negative (\"boring, drab\"); when meant positively one rather says 落ち着いた or シンプルな. Common: 地味だけど大切な仕事.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"派手な色のシャツを着ている人が目立つ。","romaji":"Hade na iro no shatsu wo kite iru hito ga medatsu.","german":"The person wearing a shirt in a flashy color stands out.","furigana":"はで|いろ|き|ひと|めだ"},{"japanese":"宝くじが当たってから、彼は派手な生活を送っている。","romaji":"Takarakuji ga atatte kara, kare wa hade na seikatsu o okutte iru.","german":"Since winning the lottery, he has been living an extravagant life.","furigana":"たから|あ|かれ|はで|せいかつ|おく"},{"japanese":"雪の道で派手に転んでしまった。","romaji":"Yuki no michi de hade ni koronde shimatta.","german":"I fell spectacularly on the snowy road.","furigana":"ゆき|みち|はで|ころ"}],"notes":"派手 means \"flashy, garish, showy\" and is the opposite of 地味. It describes colors and clothes (派手な色, 派手な服) but also a lavish lifestyle (派手な生活, 派手にお金を使う). Criticism often resonates, in contrast to the positive 華やか. Adverbially 派手に means \"spectacularly, dramatically\": 派手に転ぶ \"to fall spectacularly\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"田舎の素朴な暮らしに憧れる。","romaji":"Inaka no soboku na kurashi ni akogareru.","german":"I long for the simple life in the countryside.","furigana":"いなか|そぼく|く|あこが"},{"japanese":"素朴な疑問なんですが、どうして日本の電車は時間通りに来るんですか。","romaji":"Soboku na gimon nan desu ga, dōshite Nihon no densha wa jikan dōri ni kuru n desu ka.","german":"This is a naive question, but why do Japanese trains arrive on time?","furigana":"そぼく|ぎもん|にほん|でんしゃ|じかんどお|く"},{"japanese":"このお菓子は昔ながらの素朴な味がする。","romaji":"Kono okashi wa mukashinagara no soboku na aji ga suru.","german":"This sweet has a simple, old-fashioned taste.","furigana":"かし|むかし|そぼく|あじ"}],"notes":"素朴 describes something simple and unadulterated, without sophistication: 素朴な暮らし \"a simple life\", 素朴な味 \"an unpretentious taste\", 素朴な人柄 \"a simple, unspoiled character\". Mostly positive and warm in connotation, unlike 地味, which rather means \"inconspicuous, boring\". Very common is 素朴な疑問 \"a naive, very simple question\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は優雅にワルツを踊った。","romaji":"Kanojo wa yūga ni warutsu wo odotta.","german":"She danced a waltz gracefully.","furigana":"かのじょ|ゆうが|おど"},{"japanese":"湖の上を白鳥が優雅に泳いでいる。","romaji":"Mizuumi no ue o hakuchō ga yūga ni oyoide iru.","german":"Swans are swimming gracefully on the lake.","furigana":"みずうみ|うえ|はくちょう|ゆうが|およ"},{"japanese":"定年後はハワイで優雅に暮らしたい。","romaji":"Teinen-go wa Hawai de yūga ni kurashitai.","german":"After retirement, I want to live a leisurely life in Hawaii.","furigana":"ていねんご|ゆうが|く"}],"notes":"優雅 means \"elegant, graceful\" and describes movements and bearing (優雅に踊る, 優雅な仕草). A second important meaning is \"leisurely, carefree, well-off\": 優雅な生活, 優雅に暮らす - often said with slight envy or irony. Unlike 上品, which stresses good manners and taste, 優雅 puts the accent on ease and leisure.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女の上品な振る舞いに感心した。","romaji":"Kanojo no jōhin na furumai ni kanshin shita.","german":"I was impressed by her refined manners.","furigana":"かのじょ|じょうひん|ふ|ま|かんしん"},{"japanese":"この店の和菓子は甘さが控えめで、上品な味だ。","romaji":"Kono mise no wagashi wa amasa ga hikaeme de, jōhin na aji da.","german":"The Japanese sweets of this shop are modestly sweet and delicate in taste.","furigana":"みせ|わがし|あま|ひか|じょうひん|あじ"},{"japanese":"面接には上品なスーツで行ったほうがいい。","romaji":"Mensetsu ni wa jōhin na sūtsu de itta hō ga ii.","german":"You should go to the interview in an elegant suit.","furigana":"めんせつ|じょうひん|い"}],"notes":"上品 means \"refined, cultured, tasteful\" and is used for manners (上品な振る舞い), speech (上品な言葉遣い), clothing and even the taste of food (上品な味 \"delicate, subtly seasoned\"). The opposite is 下品 \"vulgar\". Unlike 優雅, which stresses grace and leisure, 上品 puts good upbringing in the foreground.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"下品な冗談は控えたほうがいい。","romaji":"Gehin na jōdan wa hikaeta hō ga ii.","german":"You'd better refrain from vulgar jokes.","furigana":"げひん|じょうだん|ひか"},{"japanese":"口を開けたまま食べるのは下品だと言われた。","romaji":"Kuchi o aketa mama taberu no wa gehin da to iwareta.","german":"I was told that eating with your mouth open is vulgar.","furigana":"くち|あ|た|げひん|い"},{"japanese":"金色ばかりの部屋は、豪華というより下品に見える。","romaji":"Kin'iro bakari no heya wa, gōka to iu yori gehin ni mieru.","german":"A room that is all gold looks tasteless rather than luxurious.","furigana":"きんいろ|へや|ごうか|げひん|み"}],"notes":"下品 means \"vulgar, coarse, tasteless\" and is the opposite of 上品. It criticizes manners and speech (下品な冗談, 下品な言葉遣い, 下品な笑い方), but also clothing or design that looks cheap and pushy. It is a clear value judgment; toned down one says あまり上品じゃない.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"穏やかな天気が続いている。","romaji":"Odayaka na tenki ga tsuzuite iru.","german":"The calm weather continues.","furigana":"おだ|てんき|つづ"},{"japanese":"父は穏やかな性格で、怒ったところを見たことがない。","romaji":"Chichi wa odayaka na seikaku de, okotta tokoro o mita koto ga nai.","german":"My father has a gentle temperament; I have never seen him angry.","furigana":"ちち|おだ|せいかく|おこ|み"},{"japanese":"今日は海が穏やかなので、釣りに行こう。","romaji":"Kyō wa umi ga odayaka na node, tsuri ni ikō.","german":"The sea is calm today, so let's go fishing.","furigana":"きょう|うみ|おだ|つ|い"}],"notes":"穏やか means \"calm, peaceful, gentle\" and describes weather and sea (穏やかな天気, 海が穏やかだ), character (穏やかな性格, 穏やかな人) and life circumstances (穏やかな日々). The opposite for the sea is 荒い. Unlike 静か \"quiet, still\", 穏やか is about the absence of agitation or violence. Fixed expression: 心中穏やかではない \"to be inwardly agitated\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"海が荒いので船は出せない。","romaji":"Umi ga arai node fune wa dasenai.","german":"The sea is rough, so the ship can't go out.","furigana":"うみ|あら|ふね|だ"},{"japanese":"彼は金遣いが荒くて、いつもお金がない。","romaji":"Kare wa kanezukai ga arakute, itsumo okane ga nai.","german":"He spends money recklessly and is always broke.","furigana":"かれ|かねづか|あら|かね"},{"japanese":"階段を駆け上がってきた彼は息が荒かった。","romaji":"Kaidan o kakeagatte kita kare wa iki ga arakatta.","german":"He had run up the stairs and was breathing hard.","furigana":"かいだん|か|あ|かれ|いき|あら"}],"notes":"荒い means \"violent, wild, rough\": 波が荒い / 海が荒い \"the sea is stormy\", 息が荒い \"to breathe heavily\", 言葉遣いが荒い \"to have a rough way of speaking\". With 遣い it forms expressions for immoderate handling: 金遣いが荒い \"to be a spendthrift\", 人使いが荒い \"to drive people ruthlessly\". Do not confuse it with the identically read 粗い \"coarse, grainy\" (粗い目, 粗い砂), which concerns structure and fineness.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"緩やかな坂道を登った。","romaji":"Yuruyaka na sakamichi wo nobotta.","german":"I climbed a gentle slope.","furigana":"ゆる|さかみち|のぼ"},{"japanese":"景気は緩やかに回復している。","romaji":"Keiki wa yuruyaka ni kaifuku shite iru.","german":"The economy is recovering gradually.","furigana":"けいき|ゆる|かいふく"},{"japanese":"この川は流れが緩やかなので、子供でも安全に遊べる。","romaji":"Kono kawa wa nagare ga yuruyaka na node, kodomo demo anzen ni asoberu.","german":"This river flows gently, so even children can play in it safely.","furigana":"かわ|なが|ゆる|こども|あんぜん|あそ"}],"notes":"緩やか means \"gentle, gradual, leisurely\". Spatially it describes slight slopes and curves (緩やかな坂, 緩やかなカーブ), in time a slow development: 緩やかに回復する \"to recover gradually\" - very common in economic news. It also means \"loose, mild\" for rules (緩やかな規制). The opposite is 急 \"steep, abrupt\". Compared with the more colloquial 緩い, 緩やか sounds more elevated and neutral to positive.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"急な予定変更に戸惑った。","romaji":"Kyū na yotei henkō ni tomadotta.","german":"I was thrown by the sudden change of plans.","furigana":"きゅう|よていへんこう|とまど"},{"japanese":"急な用事ができたので、今日は早めに帰ります。","romaji":"Kyū na yōji ga dekita node, kyō wa hayame ni kaerimasu.","german":"Something urgent has come up, so I'll leave early today.","furigana":"きゅう|ようじ|きょう|はや|かえ"},{"japanese":"この先は急な坂なので、自転車を降りて歩いてください。","romaji":"Kono saki wa kyū na saka na node, jitensha o orite aruite kudasai.","german":"The road ahead is steep, so please get off your bicycle and walk.","furigana":"さき|きゅう|さか|じてんしゃ|お|ある"}],"notes":"急 as a na-adjective has three core meanings: \"sudden, unexpected\" (急な予定変更, 急に雨が降り出す), \"urgent\" (急な用事) and \"steep, sharp\" (急な坂, 急なカーブ). Adverbially 急に \"suddenly\" is very common. The opposite for slopes is 緩やか. Note: 急いで \"in a hurry\" comes from the verb 急ぐ, not from 急な.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このズボンは腰回りが緩い。","romaji":"Kono zubon wa koshimawari ga yurui.","german":"These pants are loose around the waist.","furigana":"こしまわ|ゆる"},{"japanese":"うちの会社は服装のルールが緩いので、ジーンズでも大丈夫です。","romaji":"Uchi no kaisha wa fukusō no rūru ga yurui node, jīnzu demo daijōbu desu.","german":"The dress code at our company is lax, so jeans are fine.","furigana":"かいしゃ|ふくそう|ゆる|だいじょうぶ"},{"japanese":"ねじが緩くなっているから、締めておいて。","romaji":"Neji ga yuruku natte iru kara, shimete oite.","german":"The screw has come loose, so please tighten it.","furigana":"ゆる|し"}],"notes":"緩い means \"loose, slack\" for clothes, screws or knots (ズボンが緩い, ねじが緩い), \"lax, lenient\" for rules (ルールが緩い, 校則が緩い) and \"gentle\" for slopes (緩い坂). The opposite is きつい. It is more colloquial than 緩やか. Colloquially ゆるい also describes something relaxed and casual (ゆるいキャラ → ゆるキャラ).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この靴はきつくて足が痛い。","romaji":"Kono kutsu wa kitsukute ashi ga itai.","german":"These shoes are tight and hurt my feet.","furigana":"くつ|あし|いた"},{"japanese":"毎日残業が続いて、さすがにきつい。","romaji":"Mainichi zangyō ga tsuzuite, sasuga ni kitsui.","german":"Overtime every day is really tough.","furigana":"まいにちざんぎょう|つづ"},{"japanese":"ちょっと言い方がきつかったかな。後で謝ろう。","romaji":"Chotto iikata ga kitsukatta ka na. Ato de ayamarō.","german":"Maybe the way I said it was a bit harsh. I'll apologize later.","furigana":"い|かた|あと|あやま"}],"notes":"きつい is very versatile: \"tight\" for clothes and shoes (靴がきつい), \"tough, demanding\" for work or schedules (仕事がきつい, スケジュールがきつい), \"sharp, harsh\" for words and character (言い方がきつい, きつい性格) and \"strong\" for smells or alcohol. The opposite for clothing is 緩い. In everyday speech, きつい often replaces 大変 or 辛い as an honest \"this is hard.\" Mostly written in kana.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この薬は苦いが効果がある。","romaji":"Kono kusuri wa nigai ga kōka ga aru.","german":"This medicine is bitter, but it works.","furigana":"くすり|にが|こうか"},{"japanese":"このコーヒーは苦すぎて飲めません。","romaji":"Kono kōhī wa nigasugite nomemasen.","german":"This coffee is too bitter for me to drink.","furigana":"にが|の"},{"japanese":"初めての仕事で失敗したのは苦い思い出です。","romaji":"Hajimete no shigoto de shippai shita no wa nigai omoide desu.","german":"Failing at my first job is a bitter memory.","furigana":"はじ|しごと|しっぱい|にが|おも|で"}],"notes":"苦い describes the bitter taste of medicine, coffee or vegetables such as ゴーヤ. Figuratively it stands for painful experiences: 苦い経験, 苦い思い出, and 苦い顔をする means \"to make a sour face\". To be distinguished are 辛い \"spicy\", 酸っぱい \"sour\" and 渋い \"astringent\"; the same kanji is in 苦手 \"to be bad at something, to dislike\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 34"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このレモンはとても酸っぱい。","romaji":"Kono remon wa totemo suppai.","german":"This lemon is very sour.","furigana":"す"},{"japanese":"この牛乳、ちょっと酸っぱい匂いがするから飲まないほうがいいよ。","romaji":"Kono gyūnyū, chotto suppai nioi ga suru kara nomanai hō ga ii yo.","german":"This milk smells a little sour, so you'd better not drink it.","furigana":"ぎゅうにゅう|す|にお|の"},{"japanese":"母に口が酸っぱくなるほど「片付けなさい」と言われた。","romaji":"Haha ni kuchi ga suppaku naru hodo \"katazukenasai\" to iwareta.","german":"My mother told me to clean up until she was blue in the face.","furigana":"はは|くち|す|かたづ|い"}],"notes":"酸っぱい is the sour taste (レモン, 梅干し, 酢: lemon, pickled plum, vinegar) and also a sourish smell, e.g. of spoiled milk (酸っぱい匂いがする). The related noun is 酸味 \"sour taste, acidity\". Idiom: 口が酸っぱくなるほど言う \"to repeat something until one is blue in the face\". Mostly written with the kanji 酸っぱい; すっぱい in kana is also common.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"渋いお茶が好きだ。","romaji":"Shibui ocha ga suki da.","german":"I like astringent tea.","furigana":"しぶ|ちゃ|す"},{"japanese":"あの俳優は年を取って、ますます渋くなった。","romaji":"Ano haiyū wa toshi o totte, masumasu shibuku natta.","german":"That actor has become more and more cool and refined with age.","furigana":"はいゆう|とし|と|しぶ"},{"japanese":"給料を上げてほしいと言ったら、部長は渋い顔をした。","romaji":"Kyūryō o agete hoshii to ittara, buchō wa shibui kao o shita.","german":"When I asked for a raise, the department manager made a sour face.","furigana":"きゅうりょう|あ|い|ぶちょう|しぶ|かお"}],"notes":"渋い first means \"astringent, tart\" (渋いお茶, 渋い柿). Figuratively it praises a restrained, mature style: 渋い色 \"muted color\", 渋い声 \"distinctive, deep voice\", 渋い趣味 \"refined taste\" - similar to \"cool in a quiet way\". It also appears in 渋い顔 \"a sour face\" and colloquially \"stingy\" (お金に渋い). Distinguish it from 苦い \"bitter\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"秋の紅葉が鮮やかだ。","romaji":"Aki no kōyō ga azayaka da.","german":"The autumn leaves are vivid.","furigana":"あき|こうよう|あざ"},{"japanese":"初めて海外に行った日のことは、今も鮮やかに覚えている。","romaji":"Hajimete kaigai ni itta hi no koto wa, ima mo azayaka ni oboete iru.","german":"I still remember the day of my first trip abroad vividly.","furigana":"はじ|かいがい|い|ひ|いま|あざ|おぼ"},{"japanese":"職人は鮮やかな手つきで魚をさばいた。","romaji":"Shokunin wa azayaka na tetsuki de sakana o sabaita.","german":"The craftsman filleted the fish with brilliant skill.","furigana":"しょくにん|あざ|て|さかな"}],"notes":"鮮やか means \"bright, vivid\" for colors (鮮やかな色, 鮮やかな紅葉) and \"vivid, clear\" for memories (記憶に鮮やかに残る). A second important meaning is \"masterly, brilliant\" for skill: 鮮やかな手つき \"deft hands\", 鮮やかな逆転 \"a brilliant turnaround\". Unlike 派手, 鮮やか is always positive.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の返事は曖昧だった。","romaji":"Kare no henji wa aimai datta.","german":"His reply was vague.","furigana":"かれ|へんじ|あいまい"},{"japanese":"子供の頃の記憶なので、少し曖昧です。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro no kioku na node, sukoshi aimai desu.","german":"It's a childhood memory, so it's a bit hazy.","furigana":"こども|ころ|きおく|すこ|あいまい"},{"japanese":"責任の所在を曖昧にしたまま会議が終わった。","romaji":"Sekinin no shozai o aimai ni shita mama kaigi ga owatta.","german":"The meeting ended with responsibility left unclear.","furigana":"せきにん|しょざい|あいまい|かいぎ|お"}],"notes":"曖昧 means \"unclear, vague, ambiguous\" and describes answers, attitudes and memories: 曖昧な返事, 曖昧な態度をとる, 記憶が曖昧だ. It is often a criticism of a lack of decisiveness; 曖昧にする means \"to leave something unclear\". The opposite is 明確 or 明瞭. Related, but more matter-of-fact, is はっきりしない \"not clear\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"明瞭な説明でよく分かった。","romaji":"Meiryō na setsumei de yoku wakatta.","german":"Thanks to the clear explanation, I understood well.","furigana":"めいりょう|せつめい|わ"},{"japanese":"アナウンサーは発音が明瞭で聞き取りやすい。","romaji":"Anaunsā wa hatsuon ga meiryō de kikitoriyasui.","german":"Announcers have clear pronunciation and are easy to understand.","furigana":"はつおん|めいりょう|き|と"},{"japanese":"報告書は簡単明瞭に書いてください。","romaji":"Hōkokusho wa kantan meiryō ni kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write the report simply and clearly.","furigana":"ほうこくしょ|かんたんめいりょう|か"}],"notes":"明瞭 means \"clear, distinct\" and is rather literary or formal. It describes pronunciation and voice (発音が明瞭だ), explanations and accounts (明瞭な説明, 明瞭会計 \"transparent pricing\"). The fixed phrase 簡単明瞭 means \"short and clear\". The opposite is 不明瞭 or 曖昧. In daily life one usually says はっきりした or 分かりやすい.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"些細なことで喧嘩するのはやめよう。","romaji":"Sasai na koto de kenka suru no wa yameyō.","german":"Let's stop quarreling over trivial things.","furigana":"ささい|けんか"},{"japanese":"些細なミスが大きな事故につながることもある。","romaji":"Sasai na misu ga ōki na jiko ni tsunagaru koto mo aru.","german":"A minor mistake can even lead to a major accident.","furigana":"ささい|おお|じこ"},{"japanese":"彼女は些細な変化にもすぐ気づく。","romaji":"Kanojo wa sasai na henka ni mo sugu kizuku.","german":"She immediately notices even slight changes.","furigana":"かのじょ|ささい|へんか|き"}],"notes":"些細 means \"trivial, insignificant\" and almost always stands before a noun: 些細なこと \"a trifle\", 些細なミス, 些細な違い. Typical is 些細なことで喧嘩する \"to quarrel over trifles\". It sounds somewhat more elevated than ちょっとした or 小さな; because of the rare kanji 些, it is often written in kana too: ささいな.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"膨大な量のデータを分析した。","romaji":"Bōdai na ryō no dēta wo bunseki shita.","german":"I analyzed an enormous amount of data.","furigana":"ぼうだい|りょう|ぶんせき"},{"japanese":"論文を書くために、膨大な資料を読まなければならない。","romaji":"Ronbun o kaku tame ni, bōdai na shiryō o yomanakereba naranai.","german":"To write my thesis, I have to read a vast amount of material.","furigana":"ろんぶん|か|ぼうだい|しりょう|よ"},{"japanese":"この辞書の完成には膨大な時間がかかった。","romaji":"Kono jisho no kansei ni wa bōdai na jikan ga kakatta.","german":"Completing this dictionary took an enormous amount of time.","furigana":"じしょ|かんせい|ぼうだい|じかん"}],"notes":"膨大 means \"huge, enormous\" and describes above all quantities and scale: 膨大な量, 膨大なデータ, 膨大な資料, 膨大な時間. It sounds factual to formal. The similar 莫大, by contrast, is preferred with money and damage (莫大な費用, 莫大な損害). In daily life one often says すごい量 or 大量.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この事業には莫大な費用がかかる。","romaji":"Kono jigyō ni wa bakudai na hiyō ga kakaru.","german":"This project costs an immense amount.","furigana":"じぎょう|ばくだい|ひよう"},{"japanese":"彼は祖父から莫大な財産を相続した。","romaji":"Kare wa sofu kara bakudai na zaisan o sōzoku shita.","german":"He inherited a huge fortune from his grandfather.","furigana":"かれ|そふ|ばくだい|ざいさん|そうぞく"},{"japanese":"台風で農家は莫大な損害を受けた。","romaji":"Taifū de nōka wa bakudai na songai o uketa.","german":"Farmers suffered immense damage from the typhoon.","furigana":"たいふう|のうか|ばくだい|そんがい|う"}],"notes":"莫大 means \"immense, enormous\" and is used above all with money, wealth and damage: 莫大な費用, 莫大な財産, 莫大な損害, 莫大な借金. Unlike 膨大, which describes quantity and scale (data, material), 莫大 stresses value or amount. It sounds formal and almost always stands attributively before a noun.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二人の関係は微妙な状態だ。","romaji":"Futari no kankei wa bimyō na jōtai da.","german":"The relationship between the two of them is in a delicate state.","furigana":"ふたり|かんけい|びみょう|じょうたい"},{"japanese":"この二つの言葉には微妙な違いがある。","romaji":"Kono futatsu no kotoba ni wa bimyō na chigai ga aru.","german":"There is a subtle difference between these two words.","furigana":"ふた|ことば|びみょう|ちが"},{"japanese":"「新しい店のラーメン、どうだった？」「うーん、ちょっと微妙だった。」","romaji":"\"Atarashii mise no rāmen, dō datta?\" \"Ūn, chotto bimyō datta.\"","german":"\"How was the ramen at the new shop?\" \"Hmm, it was so-so.\"","furigana":"あたら|みせ|びみょう"}],"notes":"微妙 originally means \"fine, subtle\": 微妙な違い \"a subtle difference\", 微妙な色合い. From this comes \"delicate, tricky\" (微妙な問題, 微妙な関係). In today's colloquial language, 微妙 is also a polite evasive answer for \"well, not so good\" or \"hard to say\": 「どう？」「うーん、微妙。」. It is worth knowing this nuance so as not to be misunderstood.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は繊細な心の持ち主だ。","romaji":"Kanojo wa sensai na kokoro no mochinushi da.","german":"She has a sensitive heart.","furigana":"かのじょ|せんさい|こころ|も|ぬし"},{"japanese":"和食は味付けが繊細だと言われる。","romaji":"Washoku wa ajitsuke ga sensai da to iwareru.","german":"Japanese food is said to be delicately seasoned.","furigana":"わしょく|あじつ|せんさい|い"},{"japanese":"このガラス細工はとても繊細なので、触らないでください。","romaji":"Kono garasu zaiku wa totemo sensai na node, sawaranaide kudasai.","german":"This glasswork is very delicate, so please don't touch it.","furigana":"ざいく|せんさい|さわ"}],"notes":"繊細 means \"fine, delicate\" for things (繊細な指, 繊細な細工, 繊細な味) and \"sensitive, tender-hearted\" for people (繊細な性格, 繊細な心). For people it can be positive (\"considerate\") or slightly critical (\"touchy, easily hurt\"). Related is 敏感 \"sensitive to stimuli\", which concerns perception more; opposites are 大雑把 \"rough\" and 鈍感 \"insensitive\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は大胆な計画を提案した。","romaji":"Kare wa daitan na keikaku wo teian shita.","german":"He proposed a bold plan.","furigana":"かれ|だいたん|けいかく|ていあん"},{"japanese":"社長は赤字の部門を大胆に整理した。","romaji":"Shachō wa akaji no bumon o daitan ni seiri shita.","german":"The company president boldly restructured the loss-making departments.","furigana":"しゃちょう|あかじ|ぶもん|だいたん|せいり"},{"japanese":"このドレスは背中が大きく開いていて、かなり大胆なデザインだ。","romaji":"Kono doresu wa senaka ga ōkiku hiraite ite, kanari daitan na dezain da.","german":"This dress is open at the back - quite a daring design.","furigana":"せなか|おお|ひら|だいたん"}],"notes":"大胆 means \"bold, daring, fearless\" and describes plans, decisions, statements and designs: 大胆な計画, 大胆な発言, 大胆なデザイン. Adverbially: 大胆に挑戦する \"to take something on boldly\". It is mostly positive but can also mean \"risque, cheeky\". The opposite is 慎重 \"cautious\". Unlike 勇気がある, which stresses personal courage, 大胆 is about the unusual, risky nature of an action.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"慎重に判断する必要がある。","romaji":"Shinchō ni handan suru hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"It is necessary to judge carefully.","furigana":"しんちょう|はんだん|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"雪の日は、いつもより慎重に運転してください。","romaji":"Yuki no hi wa, itsumo yori shinchō ni unten shite kudasai.","german":"On snowy days, please drive more carefully than usual.","furigana":"ゆき|ひ|しんちょう|うんてん"},{"japanese":"彼は慎重な性格なので、すぐには返事をしない。","romaji":"Kare wa shinchō na seikaku na node, sugu ni wa henji o shinai.","german":"He is a cautious person and doesn't reply right away.","furigana":"かれ|しんちょう|せいかく|へんじ"}],"notes":"慎重 means \"cautious, careful, prudent\" in the sense of thoroughly considered action. Very common adverbially: 慎重に判断する, 慎重に検討する, 慎重に運転する. Its counterparts are 大胆 or 軽率 \"rash\". Unlike 注意深い, which stresses attentiveness, 慎重 carries restraint before a decision; a 慎重派 is someone who \"would rather wait and see\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大雑把な計算でも結果は変わらない。","romaji":"Ōzappa na keisan demo kekka wa kawaranai.","german":"The result doesn't change even with a rough calculation.","furigana":"おおざっぱ|けいさん|けっか|か"},{"japanese":"まず計画を大雑把に説明して、細かいことは後で話します。","romaji":"Mazu keikaku o ōzappa ni setsumei shite, komakai koto wa ato de hanashimasu.","german":"I'll first explain the plan roughly and talk about the details later.","furigana":"けいかく|おおざっぱ|せつめい|こま|あと|はな"},{"japanese":"兄は大雑把な性格で、部屋の片付けが苦手だ。","romaji":"Ani wa ōzappa na seikaku de, heya no katazuke ga nigate da.","german":"My older brother is easygoing and bad at tidying his room.","furigana":"あに|おおざっぱ|せいかく|へや|かたづ|にがて"}],"notes":"大雑把 means, for things, \"rough, approximate, in broad strokes\" (大雑把な計算, 大雑把に説明する \"to explain roughly\") and, for people, \"careless, not fussy\" (大雑把な性格). The opposite for character is 几帳面 \"meticulous\". The description of a person is usually slightly critical but can also mean good-naturedly \"easygoing\". More neutral for estimates are おおよそ or だいたい.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は几帳面な性格で部屋がいつもきれいだ。","romaji":"Kare wa kichōmen na seikaku de heya ga itsumo kirei da.","german":"He is so orderly that his room is always clean.","furigana":"かれ|きちょうめん|せいかく|へや"},{"japanese":"母は毎日の支出を几帳面に家計簿につけている。","romaji":"Haha wa mainichi no shishutsu o kichōmen ni kakeibo ni tsukete iru.","german":"My mother meticulously records her daily expenses in a household account book.","furigana":"はは|まいにち|ししゅつ|きちょうめん|かけいぼ"},{"japanese":"田中さんは時間に几帳面で、一度も遅刻したことがない。","romaji":"Tanaka-san wa jikan ni kichōmen de, ichido mo chikoku shita koto ga nai.","german":"Mr. Tanaka is punctual and has never once been late.","furigana":"たなか|じかん|きちょうめん|いちど|ちこく"}],"notes":"几帳面 describes a person who is \"orderly, conscientious, painstakingly exact\": 几帳面な性格, 几帳面な人. One also says 時間に几帳面 \"punctual\" or 几帳面に記録する \"to keep careful records\". The opposite is 大雑把. It is predominantly positive; for exaggerated exactness one rather says 神経質 \"fussy, nervous\". The kanji 几 is rare, so it is often written in kana as きちょうめん.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"素直に謝ったほうがいい。","romaji":"Sunao ni ayamatta hō ga ii.","german":"It's better to apologize sincerely.","furigana":"すなお|あやま"},{"japanese":"褒められたら、素直に喜べばいいんだよ。","romaji":"Homeraretara, sunao ni yorokobeba ii n da yo.","german":"When you're praised, you should just be happy about it.","furigana":"ほ|すなお|よろこ"},{"japanese":"この子は素直で、先生の言うことをよく聞く。","romaji":"Kono ko wa sunao de, sensei no iu koto o yoku kiku.","german":"This child is obedient and listens well to the teacher.","furigana":"こ|すなお|せんせい|い|き"}],"notes":"素直 means \"sincere, open, unaffected\" and for children also \"obedient, docile\" (素直な子). Very common adverbially: 素直に謝る \"to apologize honestly\", 素直に喜ぶ \"to be happy without reservation\", 素直に言うと \"to be honest\". It means accepting or showing feelings or advice without defiance. The opposite is 素直じゃない or ひねくれた \"twisted, contrary\". Unlike 正直 \"honest (not lying)\", 素直 stresses the absence of inner resistance.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"祖父は頑固で自分の意見を変えない。","romaji":"Sofu wa ganko de jibun no iken wo kaenai.","german":"My grandfather is stubborn and doesn't change his opinion.","furigana":"そふ|がんこ|じぶん|いけん|か"},{"japanese":"この洗剤なら、台所の頑固な汚れもすぐ落ちます。","romaji":"Kono senzai nara, daidokoro no ganko na yogore mo sugu ochimasu.","german":"With this detergent, even stubborn kitchen stains come off quickly.","furigana":"せんざい|だいどころ|がんこ|よご|お"},{"japanese":"あのラーメン屋の主人は頑固で、自分のやり方を絶対に変えない。","romaji":"Ano rāmen-ya no shujin wa ganko de, jibun no yarikata o zettai ni kaenai.","german":"The owner of that ramen shop is stubborn and never changes his way of doing things.","furigana":"や|しゅじん|がんこ|じぶん|かた|ぜったい|か"}],"notes":"頑固 means \"stubborn, obstinate\" and describes people who don't deviate from their opinion: 頑固な人, 頑固親父 \"stubborn old man\". Figuratively it stands for persistent things that are hard to remove: 頑固な汚れ \"stubborn dirt\", 頑固な肩こり. The similar 意地っ張り stresses defiance, 強情 willfulness; 頑固 sounds more neutral and can also suggest \"principled\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"寛大な心で人を許すべきだ。","romaji":"Kandai na kokoro de hito wo yurusu beki da.","german":"One should forgive people with a generous heart.","furigana":"かんだい|こころ|ひと|ゆる"},{"japanese":"初めての失敗だったので、会社は寛大な処置をとった。","romaji":"Hajimete no shippai datta node, kaisha wa kandai na shochi o totta.","german":"Since it was his first failure, the company took lenient measures.","furigana":"はじ|しっぱい|かいしゃ|かんだい|しょち"},{"japanese":"彼女は部下のミスに寛大で、いつも優しくアドバイスしている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa buka no misu ni kandai de, itsumo yasashiku adobaisu shite iru.","german":"She is lenient with her subordinates' mistakes and always gives gentle advice.","furigana":"かのじょ|ぶか|かんだい|やさ"}],"notes":"寛大 means \"magnanimous, lenient, tolerant\" in dealing with other people's mistakes: 寛大な心, 寛大な処置 \"lenient treatment, leniency\", 寛大に許す. It does not mean generosity with money - for that, one says 気前がいい. The opposite is 厳しい or 厳格. The expression is rather elevated; in daily life one often says 心が広い \"broad-minded\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の父親はとても厳格だった。","romaji":"Kare no chichioya wa totemo genkaku datta.","german":"His father was very strict.","furigana":"かれ|ちちおや|げんかく"},{"japanese":"この工場では温度が厳格に管理されている。","romaji":"Kono kōjō de wa ondo ga genkaku ni kanri sarete iru.","german":"In this factory, the temperature is strictly controlled.","furigana":"こうじょう|おんど|げんかく|かんり"},{"japanese":"その学校は厳格な校則があることで有名だ。","romaji":"Sono gakkō wa genkaku na kōsoku ga aru koto de yūmei da.","german":"That school is famous for having strict school rules.","furigana":"がっこう|げんかく|こうそく|ゆうめい"}],"notes":"厳格 means \"strict, rigorous\" and describes people (厳格な父親, 厳格な先生) as well as rules and their application (厳格な規則, 厳格に管理する). It sounds more formal and uncompromising than 厳しい, which is much more common in daily life and can also mean \"harsh, difficult\". 厳格 stresses the exact observance of principles without exceptions. Opposite: 寛大 or 緩い.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"勤勉な学生は必ず成功する。","romaji":"Kinben na gakusei wa kanarazu seikō suru.","german":"Diligent students are sure to succeed.","furigana":"きんべん|がくせい|かなら|せいこう"},{"japanese":"祖父は四十年間、勤勉に働いて家族を支えた。","romaji":"Sofu wa yonjūnenkan, kinben ni hataraite kazoku o sasaeta.","german":"My grandfather worked diligently for forty years and supported the family.","furigana":"そふ|よんじゅうねんかん|きんべん|はたら|かぞく|ささ"},{"japanese":"彼の勤勉さが評価されて、リーダーに選ばれた。","romaji":"Kare no kinbensa ga hyōka sarete, rīdā ni erabareta.","german":"His diligence was recognized, and he was chosen as leader.","furigana":"かれ|きんべん|ひょうか|えら"}],"notes":"勤勉 means \"diligent, industrious\" in the sense of steady, dutiful work: 勤勉な学生, 勤勉に働く. It is more elevated and character-oriented than the everyday 真面目 or よく頑張る. Common in attributions like 日本人は勤勉だ. The opposite is 怠惰 \"lazy\". The noun 勤勉さ means \"diligence\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"怠惰な生活を送っていてはいけない。","romaji":"Taida na seikatsu wo okutte ite wa ikenai.","german":"You must not lead an idle life.","furigana":"たいだ|せいかつ|おく"},{"japanese":"夏休みを怠惰に過ごしてしまい、宿題が終わっていない。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi o taida ni sugoshite shimai, shukudai ga owatte inai.","german":"I spent the summer vacation idly and haven't finished my homework.","furigana":"なつやす|たいだ|す|しゅくだい|お"},{"japanese":"成功しないのは才能のせいではなく、怠惰のせいだ。","romaji":"Seikō shinai no wa sainō no sei de wa naku, taida no sei da.","german":"His lack of success is due not to a lack of talent but to laziness.","furigana":"せいこう|さいのう|たいだ"}],"notes":"怠惰 means \"lazy, idle, inactive\" and is literary and clearly judgmental: 怠惰な生活, 怠惰な性格, 怠惰に過ごす. In daily life one usually says 怠け者 \"lazybones\", だらしない \"sloppy\" or ごろごろする \"to laze around\" instead. The opposite is 勤勉. Related verb: 怠ける \"to be lazy, to slack off\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"誠実な態度で仕事に臨むべきだ。","romaji":"Seijitsu na taido de shigoto ni nozomu beki da.","german":"One should approach work with a sincere attitude.","furigana":"せいじつ|たいど|しごと|のぞ"},{"japanese":"お客様からの苦情には、誠実に対応することが大切だ。","romaji":"Okyakusama kara no kujō ni wa, seijitsu ni taiō suru koto ga taisetsu da.","german":"It is important to respond sincerely to complaints from customers.","furigana":"きゃくさま|くじょう|せいじつ|たいおう|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"結婚相手には、お金より誠実な人を選びたい。","romaji":"Kekkon aite ni wa, okane yori seijitsu na hito o erabitai.","german":"As a spouse, I'd rather choose a sincere person than money.","furigana":"けっこんあいて|かね|せいじつ|ひと|えら"}],"notes":"誠実 means \"sincere, honest, conscientious\" and describes people and their behavior toward others: 誠実な人, 誠実な態度, 誠実に対応する \"to respond responsibly\". It stresses reliability and earnestness - important in business relationships, for example. Unlike 正直 \"honest (not lying)\" and 素直 \"open, unaffected\", 誠実 also includes fidelity and a sense of duty. Opposite: 不誠実.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不誠実な対応に失望した。","romaji":"Fuseijitsu na taiō ni shitsubō shita.","german":"I was disappointed by the insincere way they handled it.","furigana":"ふせいじつ|たいおう|しつぼう"},{"japanese":"約束を何度も破るなんて、不誠実だと思う。","romaji":"Yakusoku o nando mo yaburu nante, fuseijitsu da to omou.","german":"I think it's insincere to break promises over and over.","furigana":"やくそく|なんど|やぶ|ふせいじつ|おも"},{"japanese":"事故の説明をしない会社の不誠実な態度が批判された。","romaji":"Jiko no setsumei o shinai kaisha no fuseijitsu na taido ga hihan sareta.","german":"The company's insincere attitude, refusing to explain the accident, was criticized.","furigana":"じこ|せつめい|かいしゃ|ふせいじつ|たいど|ひはん"}],"notes":"不誠実 is the negation of 誠実 and means \"insincere, unscrupulous, irresponsible\". It criticizes above all how one treats others: 不誠実な対応 \"a disingenuous response\" (e.g. by a company to complaints), 不誠実な態度, 不誠実な人 (such as an unfaithful partner). It is a serious accusation, stronger than 適当 \"lax, superficial\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子供の純粋な目に心を打たれた。","romaji":"Kodomo no junsui na me ni kokoro wo utareta.","german":"I was moved by the child's innocent eyes.","furigana":"こども|じゅんすい|め|こころ|う"},{"japanese":"賞のためではなく、純粋に音楽が好きだから演奏している。","romaji":"Shō no tame de wa naku, junsui ni ongaku ga suki da kara ensō shite iru.","german":"I'm not playing for a prize; I play purely because I love music.","furigana":"しょう|じゅんすい|おんがく|す|えんそう"},{"japanese":"この指輪は純粋な金でできている。","romaji":"Kono yubiwa wa junsui na kin de dekite iru.","german":"This ring is made of pure gold.","furigana":"ゆびわ|じゅんすい|きん"}],"notes":"純粋 means \"pure, unmixed\" for substances (純粋な金, 純粋な水) and \"innocent, unspoiled, genuine\" for people and motives (純粋な心, 純粋な気持ち). Also common as 純粋に \"purely, simply\": 純粋に楽しむ \"simply enjoy\". Applied to people it can sound positive or slightly naive. For purity in the sense of cleanliness, use 清潔 instead.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この問題は非常に複雑だ。","romaji":"Kono mondai wa hijō ni fukuzatsu da.","german":"This problem is extremely complex.","furigana":"もんだい|ひじょう|ふくざつ"},{"japanese":"ビザの手続きが複雑で、何度も役所に行った。","romaji":"Biza no tetsuzuki ga fukuzatsu de, nando mo yakusho ni itta.","german":"The visa procedure was so complicated that I had to go to the government office several times.","furigana":"てつづ|ふくざつ|なんど|やくしょ|い"},{"japanese":"親友が遠くに引っ越すと聞いて、複雑な気持ちになった。","romaji":"Shin'yū ga tōku ni hikkosu to kiite, fukuzatsu na kimochi ni natta.","german":"When I heard that my best friend was moving far away, I had mixed feelings.","furigana":"しんゆう|とお|ひ|こ|き|ふくざつ|きも"}],"notes":"複雑 means \"complicated, intricate\" and describes problems, procedures and structures (複雑な問題, 手続きが複雑だ). Very common is 複雑な気持ち \"mixed feelings\", when you are happy and sad at the same time. The opposite is 単純 or 簡単. Unlike 難しい \"difficult\", 複雑 stresses that many parts interlock.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"単調な仕事に飽きてしまった。","romaji":"Tanchō na shigoto ni akite shimatta.","german":"I got tired of the monotonous work.","furigana":"たんちょう|しごと|あ"},{"japanese":"高速道路は景色が単調なので、運転中に眠くなりやすい。","romaji":"Kōsoku dōro wa keshiki ga tanchō na node, unten-chū ni nemuku nariyasui.","german":"The scenery on the expressway is so monotonous that you easily get sleepy while driving.","furigana":"こうそくどうろ|けしき|たんちょう|うんてんちゅう|ねむ"},{"japanese":"彼の話し方は単調で、聞いていると眠くなる。","romaji":"Kare no hanashikata wa tanchō de, kiite iru to nemuku naru.","german":"He speaks so monotonously that you fall asleep listening.","furigana":"かれ|はな|かた|たんちょう|き|ねむ"}],"notes":"単調 means \"monotonous, dull\" and describes work, daily life, landscapes or sounds without variety: 単調な仕事, 単調な生活, 単調なリズム. It is mostly negative (\"boring\"). Unlike 退屈 \"boring\", which expresses the person's feeling, 単調 describes the thing itself. Opposite: 変化に富む \"full of variety\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"多様な文化が共存している。","romaji":"Tayō na bunka ga kyōzon shite iru.","german":"Diverse cultures coexist.","furigana":"たよう|ぶんか|きょうぞん"},{"japanese":"会議では多様な意見が出たが、結論は出なかった。","romaji":"Kaigi de wa tayō na iken ga deta ga, ketsuron wa denakatta.","german":"Many different opinions came up at the meeting, but no conclusion was reached.","furigana":"かいぎ|たよう|いけん|で|けつろん|で"},{"japanese":"最近は在宅勤務など、働き方が多様になっている。","romaji":"Saikin wa zaitaku kinmu nado, hatarakikata ga tayō ni natte iru.","german":"Recently, ways of working are becoming more diverse, for example with working from home.","furigana":"さいきん|ざいたくきんむ|はたら|かた|たよう"}],"notes":"多様 means \"diverse, of various kinds\" and is a typical word in news and discussions of society: 多様な文化, 多様な意見, 多様な働き方. Related are 多様化 \"diversification\" and 多様性 \"diversity\". In everyday speech people more often say いろいろな or さまざまな; 多様 sounds objective and formal.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"均一な品質を保つことが大切だ。","romaji":"Kin'itsu na hinshitsu wo tamotsu koto ga taisetsu da.","german":"It is important to maintain uniform quality.","furigana":"きんいつ|ひんしつ|たも|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"この店では全商品が百円均一で売られている。","romaji":"Kono mise de wa zen shōhin ga hyakuen kin'itsu de urarete iru.","german":"At this store, every item is sold for a flat 100 yen.","furigana":"みせ|ぜんしょうひん|ひゃくえんきんいつ|う"},{"japanese":"小麦粉と砂糖を均一になるまでよく混ぜてください。","romaji":"Komugiko to satō o kin'itsu ni naru made yoku mazete kudasai.","german":"Mix the flour and sugar well until evenly blended.","furigana":"こむぎこ|さとう|きんいつ|ま"}],"notes":"均一 means \"even, uniform\" and describes quality, distribution and prices: 均一な品質, 均一に混ぜる \"mix evenly\". Very well known is 100円均一 (short: 百均) \"100-yen flat price\" for discount stores. The opposite is 不均一 or ばらばら. Unlike 平等 \"equal in rights\", 均一 refers to physical or numerical equality, not to fairness.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"塗装が不均一で仕上がりが悪い。","romaji":"Tosō ga fukin'itsu de shiagari ga warui.","german":"The paint job is uneven and the result is poor.","furigana":"とそう|ふきんいつ|しあ|わる"},{"japanese":"オーブンの中の温度が不均一だと、パンがうまく焼けない。","romaji":"Ōbun no naka no ondo ga fukin'itsu da to, pan ga umaku yakenai.","german":"If the temperature inside the oven is uneven, the bread won't bake well.","furigana":"なか|おんど|ふきんいつ|や"},{"japanese":"地域によって医療サービスの質が不均一だという問題がある。","romaji":"Chiiki ni yotte iryō sābisu no shitsu ga fukin'itsu da to iu mondai ga aru.","german":"There is a problem that the quality of medical services is uneven depending on the region.","furigana":"ちいき|いりょう|しつ|ふきんいつ|もんだい"}],"notes":"不均一 is the opposite of 均一 and means \"uneven, non-uniform\". It is a rather technical word for color, thickness, temperature or distribution: 塗装が不均一, 温度が不均一, 色むらがあって不均一. In everyday speech people often say むらがある \"to be uneven\" or ばらばら. As a noun it also appears in technical texts (不均一性 \"heterogeneity\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"市場で新鮮な魚を買った。","romaji":"Ichiba de shinsen na sakana wo katta.","german":"I bought fresh fish at the market.","furigana":"いちば|しんせん|さかな|か"},{"japanese":"窓を開けて新鮮な空気を入れよう。","romaji":"Mado o akete shinsen na kūki o ireyō.","german":"Let's open the window and let in some fresh air.","furigana":"まど|あ|しんせん|くうき|い"},{"japanese":"日本に来たばかりの頃は、何もかもが新鮮に感じた。","romaji":"Nihon ni kita bakari no koro wa, nanimokamo ga shinsen ni kanjita.","german":"When I had just come to Japan, everything felt new and exciting.","furigana":"にほん|き|ころ|なに|しんせん|かん"}],"notes":"新鮮 means \"fresh\" for food (新鮮な魚, 新鮮な野菜) and air (新鮮な空気). Figuratively it means \"new, refreshing, unjaded\" for impressions and ideas: 新鮮な発想, 何もかもが新鮮に感じる. For freshly baked bread or something just made, people more often say できたて or 焼きたて; 新鮮 stresses the unspoiled state. Opposite for food: 古い or 腐った.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"温かいスープを飲んで体が温まった。","romaji":"Atatakai sūpu wo nonde karada ga atatamatta.","german":"I drank warm soup and my body warmed up.","furigana":"あたた|の|からだ|あたた"},{"japanese":"落ち込んでいたとき、先生の温かい言葉に救われた。","romaji":"Ochikonde ita toki, sensei no atatakai kotoba ni sukuwareta.","german":"When I was down, I was saved by my teacher's kind words.","furigana":"お|こ|せんせい|あたた|ことば|すく"},{"japanese":"料理が温かいうちに召し上がってください。","romaji":"Ryōri ga atatakai uchi ni meshiagatte kudasai.","german":"Please enjoy the food while it is warm.","furigana":"りょうり|あたた|め|あ"}],"notes":"温かい means \"warm\" for things you touch, eat or drink (温かいスープ, 温かい飲み物), and figuratively \"heartfelt, warm-hearted\" (温かい人, 温かい言葉, 温かい家庭). For warm air and weather, 暖かい is written instead (暖かい部屋, 暖かい春). Opposite: 冷たい (things, hearts) or 寒い (weather). Both spellings are read あたたかい; colloquially also あったかい.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"冷たい水で顔を洗った。","romaji":"Tsumetai mizu de kao o aratta.","german":"I washed my face with cold water.","furigana":"つめ|みず|かお|あら"},{"japanese":"暑い日は冷たいお茶が飲みたいです。","romaji":"Atsui hi wa tsumetai ocha ga nomitai desu.","german":"On hot days I want to drink cold tea.","furigana":"あつ|ひ|つめ|ちゃ|の"}],"notes":"冷たい is an i-adjective for something that feels cold. It stands directly before a noun: 冷たい水. 冷たくない and 冷たかった are the negative and past forms. 水で marks the water as the means of washing. For cold weather, 寒い is normally used.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 8"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"惨めな気持ちで家に帰った。","romaji":"Mijime na kimochi de ie ni kaetta.","german":"I went home feeling miserable.","furigana":"みじ|きも|いえ|かえ"},{"japanese":"みんなの前で失敗して、惨めな思いをした。","romaji":"Minna no mae de shippai shite, mijime na omoi o shita.","german":"I failed in front of everyone and felt terribly humiliated.","furigana":"まえ|しっぱい|みじ|おも"},{"japanese":"チームは0対7という惨めな負け方をした。","romaji":"Chīmu wa zero tai nana to iu mijime na makekata o shita.","german":"The team suffered a pitiful 7-0 defeat.","furigana":"たい|みじ|ま|かた"}],"notes":"惨め means \"miserable, pitiful, wretched\" and describes above all one's own feeling of humiliation or defeat: 惨めな気持ち, 惨めな思いをする \"to feel miserable\", 惨めな負け方 \"a humiliating defeat\". Unlike 哀れ, which expresses the pity of an observer, 惨め puts the shame and wretchedness of the situation in the foreground. Similar but weaker: 情けない \"pathetic, shameful\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"哀れな姿を見て涙が出た。","romaji":"Aware na sugata wo mite namida ga deta.","german":"Tears came to my eyes when I saw his pitiful figure.","furigana":"あわ|すがた|み|なみだ|で"},{"japanese":"雨に濡れて震えている子猫を哀れに思い、家に連れて帰った。","romaji":"Ame ni nurete furuete iru koneko o aware ni omoi, ie ni tsurete kaetta.","german":"I felt sorry for the kitten shivering in the rain, so I took it home.","furigana":"あめ|ぬ|ふる|こねこ|あわ|おも|いえ|つ|かえ"},{"japanese":"嘘をつき続けるしかない彼が、哀れにさえ思えた。","romaji":"Uso o tsukitsuzukeru shika nai kare ga, aware ni sae omoeta.","german":"He, who could do nothing but keep lying, even seemed pitiful to me.","furigana":"うそ|つづ|かれ|あわ|おも"}],"notes":"哀れ expresses an observer's pity: \"pitiable, wretched\" (哀れな姿, 哀れな老人), often in the phrase 哀れに思う \"to feel pity\". It can also sound contemptuous, \"pathetic\" (哀れな奴). The word is rather literary; in everyday speech people say かわいそう. Unlike 惨め, which stresses one's own feeling of wretchedness, 哀れ looks from the outside at the suffering of others. Classically related is もののあはれ \"the pathos of the transience of things\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"故郷の料理が恋しい。","romaji":"Kokyō no ryōri ga koishii.","german":"I miss the food from my hometown.","furigana":"こきょう|りょうり|こい"},{"japanese":"寒くなると、温かい鍋料理が恋しくなる。","romaji":"Samuku naru to, atatakai nabe ryōri ga koishiku naru.","german":"When it gets cold, I start craving hot pot dishes.","furigana":"さむ|あたた|なべりょうり|こい"},{"japanese":"留学して半年がたち、家族が恋しくなってきた。","romaji":"Ryūgaku shite hantoshi ga tachi, kazoku ga koishiku natte kita.","german":"Half a year into studying abroad, I'm starting to miss my family.","furigana":"りゅうがく|はんとし|かぞく|こい"}],"notes":"恋しい means \"missed, longed for\": the thing missed is the subject with が, e.g. 故郷が恋しい \"I miss my hometown\", 母の料理が恋しい. With なる it expresses longing setting in: 冬になると鍋が恋しくなる. Unlike 懐かしい, which expresses wistful memory of the past, 恋しい means the wish to have the missed thing back now. Despite the kanji 恋 \"love\", it is by no means only about loved persons.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"怪しい人物が周辺をうろついている。","romaji":"Ayashii jinbutsu ga shūhen wo urotsuite iru.","german":"A suspicious person is lurking around the neighborhood.","furigana":"あや|じんぶつ|しゅうへん"},{"japanese":"空模様が怪しくなってきたから、傘を持っていこう。","romaji":"Soramoyō ga ayashiku natte kita kara, kasa o motte ikō.","german":"The sky is looking threatening, so let's take an umbrella.","furigana":"そらもよう|あや|かさ|も"},{"japanese":"締め切りに間に合うかどうか怪しい。","romaji":"Shimekiri ni maniau ka dō ka ayashii.","german":"It's doubtful whether we'll meet the deadline.","furigana":"し|き|ま|あ|あや"}],"notes":"怪しい is the everyday word for \"suspicious, fishy\": 怪しい人/動き/メール. But it also means \"doubtful, uncertain\" (間に合うかどうか怪しい), \"shaky\" for knowledge (私の英語は怪しい) and \"threatening\" for weather (空が怪しくなる). 疑わしい is more literary and aimed at statements and facts, 不審な is official (police, announcements). The related verb is 怪しむ \"to become suspicious\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の証言は疑わしい。","romaji":"Kare no shōgen wa utagawashii.","german":"His testimony is doubtful.","furigana":"かれ|しょうげん|うたが"},{"japanese":"その薬の効果は科学的に疑わしい。","romaji":"Sono kusuri no kōka wa kagakuteki ni utagawashii.","german":"The effect of that medicine is scientifically questionable.","furigana":"くすり|こうか|かがくてき|うたが"},{"japanese":"疑わしい点があれば、遠慮なく質問してください。","romaji":"Utagawashii ten ga areba, enryo naku shitsumon shite kudasai.","german":"If there is anything doubtful, please feel free to ask.","furigana":"うたが|てん|えんりょ|しつもん"}],"notes":"疑わしい is the adjective form of 疑う \"to doubt, suspect\" and refers to something whose truth or authenticity one must doubt: 疑わしい証言/データ/点. It sounds more objective and literary than the everyday 怪しい and is used more for claims, effects and results than for persons. Well known is the legal principle 疑わしきは罰せず \"in doubt, in favor of the accused\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不審な荷物を見つけたら通報してください。","romaji":"Fushin na nimotsu wo mitsuketara tsūhō shite kudasai.","german":"If you find a suspicious package, please report it.","furigana":"ふしん|にもつ|み|つうほう"},{"japanese":"学校の近くで不審者が目撃された。","romaji":"Gakkō no chikaku de fushinsha ga mokugeki sareta.","german":"A suspicious person was spotted near the school.","furigana":"がっこう|ちか|ふしんしゃ|もくげき"},{"japanese":"彼の態度を不審に思った店員が警察に連絡した。","romaji":"Kare no taido o fushin ni omotta ten'in ga keisatsu ni renraku shita.","german":"The clerk, who found his behavior strange, contacted the police.","furigana":"かれ|たいど|ふしん|おも|てんいん|けいさつ|れんらく"}],"notes":"不審 is a Sino-Japanese noun/な-adjective with an official ring: police, railways and schools warn about 不審な物/荷物 and 不審者 \"suspicious person\"; 挙動不審 means \"suspicious behavior\", 不審火 a fire of unclear cause. 不審に思う means \"to find something strange, become suspicious\". For a distrustful character, on the other hand, say 疑い深い; in everyday speech 怪しい mostly replaces the word.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不気味な音が聞こえてきた。","romaji":"Bukimi na oto ga kikoete kita.","german":"I heard an eerie sound.","furigana":"ぶきみ|おと|き"},{"japanese":"夜の病院は静かで不気味だ。","romaji":"Yoru no byōin wa shizuka de bukimi da.","german":"The hospital is quiet and creepy at night.","furigana":"よる|びょういん|しず|ぶきみ"},{"japanese":"彼は不気味な笑みを浮かべていた。","romaji":"Kare wa bukimi na emi o ukabete ita.","german":"He wore an eerie smile.","furigana":"かれ|ぶきみ|え|う"}],"notes":"不気味 describes an uncanny feeling whose cause one cannot quite pin down: 不気味な音/静けさ/笑み. Unlike 怖い, which expresses direct fear, 不気味 stresses the uneasy and ghostly; close in meaning are 気味が悪い and, intensified, 薄気味悪い. Not to be confused with 気持ち悪い, which rather means \"disgusting, nauseating\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"恐ろしい事故のニュースを見た。","romaji":"Osoroshii jiko no nyūsu wo mita.","german":"I saw news of a terrible accident.","furigana":"おそ|じこ|み"},{"japanese":"一人で夜の山道を歩くのは恐ろしかった。","romaji":"Hitori de yoru no yamamichi o aruku no wa osoroshikatta.","german":"Walking alone on a mountain path at night was frightening.","furigana":"ひとり|よる|やまみち|ある|おそ"},{"japanese":"東京の家賃は恐ろしく高い。","romaji":"Tōkyō no yachin wa osoroshiku takai.","german":"Rent in Tokyo is terribly high.","furigana":"とうきょう|やちん|おそ|たか"}],"notes":"恐ろしい is somewhat more elevated than 怖い and often describes an objective threat: 恐ろしい事故/病気/事件. 怖い, by contrast, expresses the speaker's personal fear and is more colloquial. As an adverb, 恐ろしく \"extremely, terribly\" (恐ろしく高い) intensifies, as does 恐ろしいほど. The noun 恐ろしさ denotes the terribleness.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"おぞましい犯罪が報道された。","romaji":"Ozomashii hanzai ga hōdō sareta.","german":"A heinous crime was reported.","furigana":"はんざい|ほうどう"},{"japanese":"想像するだけでおぞましい。","romaji":"Sōzō suru dake de ozomashii.","german":"Just imagining it is horrifying.","furigana":"そうぞう"},{"japanese":"部屋の隅でゴキブリが大量に見つかり、おぞましい光景だった。","romaji":"Heya no sumi de gokiburi ga tairyō ni mitsukari, ozomashii kōkei datta.","german":"A large number of cockroaches were found in the corner of the room; it was a revolting sight.","furigana":"へや|すみ|たいりょう|み|こうけい"}],"notes":"おぞましい combines horror with physical disgust: 想像するだけでおぞましい \"just imagining it is horrifying\". It is clearly stronger than 気持ち悪い or 嫌な and emotionally colored; 恐ろしい, by contrast, stresses fear. The word is almost always written in kana (the kanji 悍ましい is rare) and is often found in reports on crimes or in descriptions of revolting scenes.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"醜い争いは見たくない。","romaji":"Minikui arasoi wa mitakunai.","german":"I don't want to see an ugly quarrel.","furigana":"みにく|あらそ|み"},{"japanese":"子どもの頃、「醜いアヒルの子」の話が好きだった。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, \"Minikui ahiru no ko\" no hanashi ga suki datta.","german":"As a child, I liked the story of \"The Ugly Duckling\".","furigana":"こ|ころ|みにく|こ|はなし|す"},{"japanese":"自分の利益しか考えない醜い大人にはなりたくない。","romaji":"Jibun no rieki shika kangaenai minikui otona ni wa naritakunai.","german":"I don't want to become an ugly adult who only thinks of his own profit.","furigana":"じぶん|りえき|かんが|みにく|おとな"}],"notes":"醜い means \"ugly\" in appearance, but is very hurtful when applied to people; in everyday speech people more often say 見た目が悪い. More common is the figurative meaning \"unsightly, shameful\": 醜い争い/嫉妬, 醜い姿をさらす. Well known is the fairy tale 醜いアヒルの子 \"The Ugly Duckling\". Note: it is pronounced the same as 見にくい \"hard to see\"; the opposite is 美しい.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"美しい夕焼けに感動した。","romaji":"Utsukushii yūyake ni kandō shita.","german":"I was moved by the beautiful sunset.","furigana":"うつく|ゆうや|かんどう"},{"japanese":"彼女は美しい日本語を話します。","romaji":"Kanojo wa utsukushii nihongo o hanashimasu.","german":"She speaks beautiful Japanese.","furigana":"かのじょ|うつく|にほんご|はな"},{"japanese":"ピアノの美しい音が聞こえてきました。","romaji":"Piano no utsukushii oto ga kikoete kimashita.","german":"I could hear the beautiful sound of a piano.","furigana":"うつく|おと|き"}],"notes":"美しい is \"beautiful\" in an elevated, somewhat literary sense: 美しい景色, 美しい音楽, 美しい日本語, also 美しい心. In everyday conversation one usually says きれい; unlike きれい, however, 美しい never means \"clean\". The noun is 美しさ \"beauty\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 31"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この子犬はとても可愛い。","romaji":"Kono koinu wa totemo kawaii.","german":"This puppy is very cute.","furigana":"こいぬ|かわい"},{"japanese":"可愛い人形を妹にあげました。","romaji":"Kawaii ningyō o imōto ni agemashita.","german":"I gave my younger sister a cute doll.","furigana":"かわい|にんぎょう|いもうと"}],"notes":"可愛い is an i-adjective meaning cute or pretty. It can describe an animal, an object, or someone's appearance. It is often written in hiragana as かわいい; 可愛い is a common kanji spelling with an assigned reading. It needs no な before a noun: 可愛い人形. The ending is the vowel sequence ii; it is not shortened in kawaii. For food, \"sweet\" is 甘い instead.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 7"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は格好良いスーツを着ていた。","romaji":"Kare wa kakkoii sūtsu o kite ita.","german":"He was wearing a sharp suit.","furigana":"かれ|かっこい|き"},{"japanese":"うちの兄、バスケをしている時は本当にかっこいいんだよ。","romaji":"Uchi no ani, basuke o shite iru toki wa hontō ni kakkoii n da yo.","german":"My older brother looks really cool when he's playing basketball.","furigana":"あに|とき|ほんとう"},{"japanese":"自分の失敗を素直に認められる人って、かっこいいと思う。","romaji":"Jibun no shippai o sunao ni mitomerareru hito tte, kakkoii to omou.","german":"I think people who can honestly admit their own mistakes are cool.","furigana":"じぶん|しっぱい|すなお|みと|ひと|おも"}],"notes":"Almost always written in kana: かっこいい (from 格好がいい); the kanji spelling 格好良い is rare, and in casual texts also カッコいい. It means \"cool, good-looking, stylish\" and fits appearance, things and also behavior. Like いい, it is conjugated via よ-: かっこよくない, かっこよかった. The opposite is かっこ悪い, colloquially ダサい. Colloquial; in formal texts people prefer 素敵な or スマートな.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女の才能は同期の中で際立っている。","romaji":"Kanojo no sainō wa dōki no naka de kiwadatte iru.","german":"Her talent stands out among her peers.","furigana":"かのじょ|さいのう|どうき|なか|きわだ"},{"japanese":"白い壁に赤い花が際立って見える。","romaji":"Shiroi kabe ni akai hana ga kiwadatte mieru.","german":"The red flowers stand out against the white wall.","furigana":"しろ|かべ|あか|はな|きわだ|み"},{"japanese":"この地域の料理には際立った特徴がある。","romaji":"Kono chiiki no ryōri ni wa kiwadatta tokuchō ga aru.","german":"The cuisine of this region has distinctive characteristics.","furigana":"ちいき|りょうり|きわだ|とくちょう"}],"notes":"際立つ is intransitive (〜が際立つ) and stresses that something stands out clearly from its surroundings through contrast, mostly in a positive sense and rather literary. Often attributive as 際立った特徴/才能 \"distinctive, outstanding\" or adverbial 際立って \"remarkably\". 目立つ is more neutral and often negative (\"to stand out unpleasantly\"). There is no transitive counterpart; \"to highlight\" is the causative 際立たせる (味を際立たせる).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"赤い服を着ると目立つ。","romaji":"Akai fuku wo kiru to medatsu.","german":"If you wear red clothes, you stand out.","furigana":"あか|ふく|き|めだ"},{"japanese":"彼はあまり目立つのが好きではない。","romaji":"Kare wa amari medatsu no ga suki de wa nai.","german":"He doesn't like to stand out very much.","furigana":"かれ|めだ|す"},{"japanese":"最近、白髪が目立ってきた。","romaji":"Saikin, shiraga ga medatte kita.","german":"Lately, my gray hairs have become noticeable.","furigana":"さいきん|しらが|めだ"}],"notes":"目立つ is intransitive: 〜が目立つ \"something stands out\". It is neutral, but often used for unwanted conspicuousness: 傷/ミス/白髪が目立つ. 目立たない means \"inconspicuous\", 目立ちたがり屋 is someone who likes to be the center of attention. Transitive \"to make conspicuous\" is the causative 目立たせる. Compared with 際立つ, 目立つ sounds more everyday and less laudatory.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"凄まじい爆発音が聞こえた。","romaji":"Susamajii bakuhatsuon ga kikoeta.","german":"I heard a terrific explosion.","furigana":"すさ|ばくはつおん|き"},{"japanese":"台風の凄まじい風で木が倒れた。","romaji":"Taifū no susamajii kaze de ki ga taoreta.","german":"The typhoon's violent winds knocked trees down.","furigana":"たいふう|すさ|かぜ|き|たお"},{"japanese":"彼の集中力は凄まじい。","romaji":"Kare no shūchūryoku wa susamajii.","german":"His concentration is tremendous.","furigana":"かれ|しゅうちゅうりょく|すさ"}],"notes":"凄まじい describes an overwhelming, almost frightening intensity: 凄まじい音/風/勢い. Mostly negative (\"terrible, fierce\"), but also admiring (\"tremendous\"): 凄まじい集中力/人気. It is stronger and more literary than すごい and is often written in kana (すさまじい). 恐ろしい, by contrast, stresses fear, not force.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は目覚ましい成長を遂げた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa mezamashii seichō wo togeta.","german":"She has made remarkable progress.","furigana":"かのじょ|めざ|せいちょう|と"},{"japanese":"新人選手の目覚ましい活躍でチームが優勝した。","romaji":"Shinjin senshu no mezamashii katsuyaku de chīmu ga yūshō shita.","german":"Thanks to the rookie's remarkable performance, the team won the championship.","furigana":"しんじんせんしゅ|めざ|かつやく|ゆうしょう"},{"japanese":"医療技術の進歩は目覚ましい。","romaji":"Iryō gijutsu no shinpo wa mezamashii.","german":"The progress in medical technology is remarkable.","furigana":"いりょうぎじゅつ|しんぽ|めざ"}],"notes":"目覚ましい is purely positive and is used above all for progress and achievement: 目覚ましい進歩/発展/成長/活躍. Typical is 目覚ましい成長を遂げる \"to develop impressively\". For beautiful things or skill, one rather uses 見事な. Despite the same kanji, it has nothing to do in meaning with 目覚まし時計 \"alarm clock\". Rather literary (news, speeches).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"見事な桜が咲き誇っている。","romaji":"Migoto na sakura ga sakihokotte iru.","german":"The cherry trees are in splendid full bloom.","furigana":"みごと|さくら|さ|ほこ"},{"japanese":"彼は難しい技を見事に決めた。","romaji":"Kare wa muzukashii waza o migoto ni kimeta.","german":"He pulled off the difficult technique masterfully.","furigana":"かれ|むずか|わざ|みごと|き"},{"japanese":"作戦は見事に失敗した。","romaji":"Sakusen wa migoto ni shippai shita.","german":"The plan failed completely.","furigana":"さくせん|みごと|しっぱい"}],"notes":"見事 praises beauty or skill: 見事な桜/腕前, as an adverb 見事に \"masterfully\"; the exclamation お見事！ means \"Bravo!\". Ironically, 見事に also means \"thoroughly, utterly\": 見事に失敗した. 立派 praises rather dignity and decency, 素晴らしい is more general. Both the kanji and kana (みごと) spellings are common.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は立派な大人に成長した。","romaji":"Kare wa rippa na otona ni seichō shita.","german":"He has grown into a fine adult.","furigana":"かれ|りっぱ|おとな|せいちょう"},{"japanese":"駅前に立派な図書館が建った。","romaji":"Ekimae ni rippa na toshokan ga tatta.","german":"An impressive library was built in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|りっぱ|としょかん|た"},{"japanese":"他人の物を勝手に使うのは立派な犯罪だ。","romaji":"Tanin no mono o katte ni tsukau no wa rippa na hanzai da.","german":"Using other people's things without permission is flat-out a crime.","furigana":"たにん|もの|かって|つか|りっぱ|はんざい"}],"notes":"立派 has several facets: \"magnificent, imposing\" for buildings and things (立派な家), \"decent, respectable\" for people and behavior (立派な人, 立派に育つ) and, as an intensifier, \"downright, unmistakable\" (それは立派な犯罪だ). Adverbial 立派に means \"properly, impeccably\". 偉い praises rather rank or achievement, 見事 skill and beauty.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"偉大な科学者の業績を学んだ。","romaji":"Idai na kagakusha no gyōseki wo mananda.","german":"I learned about the achievements of a great scientist.","furigana":"いだい|かがくしゃ|ぎょうせき|まな"},{"japanese":"母の存在がどれほど偉大だったか、亡くなってから気づいた。","romaji":"Haha no sonzai ga dorehodo idai datta ka, nakunatte kara kizuita.","german":"Only after she died did I realize how great my mother's presence had been.","furigana":"はは|そんざい|いだい|な|き"},{"japanese":"月面着陸は人類にとって偉大な一歩だった。","romaji":"Getsumen chakuriku wa jinrui ni totte idai na ippo datta.","german":"The moon landing was a great step for mankind.","furigana":"げつめんちゃくりく|じんるい|いだい|いっぽ"}],"notes":"偉大 denotes greatness in the sense of historical importance and dignity: 偉大な人物/科学者/業績/指導者. It never refers to physical size (for that, 大きな, 巨大な). 偉い is the everyday word for \"admirable\" or \"high-ranking\" (and in 偉そう \"arrogant\"), whereas 偉大 sounds elevated and solemn. Predicative: 母の存在は偉大だった.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"壮大な景色が目の前に広がった。","romaji":"Sōdai na keshiki ga me no mae ni hirogatta.","german":"A magnificent view spread out before my eyes.","furigana":"そうだい|けしき|め|まえ|ひろ"},{"japanese":"彼は世界一周という壮大な計画を立てた。","romaji":"Kare wa sekai isshū to iu sōdai na keikaku o tateta.","german":"He made a grand plan to travel around the world.","furigana":"かれ|せかいいっしゅう|そうだい|けいかく|た"},{"japanese":"この映画は壮大なスケールで描かれた歴史物語だ。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa sōdai na sukēru de egakareta rekishi monogatari da.","german":"This movie is a historical epic told on a grand scale.","furigana":"えいが|そうだい|えが|れきしものがたり"}],"notes":"壮大 stresses large scale and ambition: 壮大な景色/計画/夢/物語/スケール. Unlike 雄大, which is used almost only for nature, 壮大 also fits plans, music and films; sometimes ironic for overly big plans. 広大 denotes the vastness of an area, 巨大 the physical size of an object.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"雄大な山脈を眺めた。","romaji":"Yūdai na sanmyaku wo nagameta.","german":"I gazed at the majestic mountain range.","furigana":"ゆうだい|さんみゃく|なが"},{"japanese":"飛行機の窓から雄大な富士山が見えた。","romaji":"Hikōki no mado kara yūdai na Fujisan ga mieta.","german":"I could see the majestic Mt. Fuji from the airplane window.","furigana":"ひこうき|まど|ゆうだい|ふじさん|み"},{"japanese":"北海道の雄大な自然に感動した。","romaji":"Hokkaidō no yūdai na shizen ni kandō shita.","german":"I was moved by the grand nature of Hokkaido.","furigana":"ほっかいどう|ゆうだい|しぜん|かんどう"}],"notes":"雄大 is used almost exclusively for natural phenomena that impress through size and calm grandeur: 雄大な山/川/海/景色, especially 雄大な自然. For plans or stories one does not say 雄大, but 壮大. The word is descriptive and elevated, typical of travel reports and brochures.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"広大な草原が広がっている。","romaji":"Kōdai na sōgen ga hirogatte iru.","german":"A vast grassland stretches out.","furigana":"こうだい|そうげん|ひろ"},{"japanese":"その大学は広大なキャンパスを持っている。","romaji":"Sono daigaku wa kōdai na kyanpasu o motte iru.","german":"That university has a vast campus.","furigana":"だいがく|こうだい|も"},{"japanese":"宇宙は広大で、まだわからないことが多い。","romaji":"Uchū wa kōdai de, mada wakaranai koto ga ōi.","german":"The universe is vast, and there is still much we don't know.","furigana":"うちゅう|こうだい|おお"}],"notes":"広大 describes spatial vastness and extent: 広大な土地/敷地/草原/キャンパス, also 宇宙は広大だ. Not to be confused with 膨大, which denotes large quantities (膨大なデータ \"huge amounts of data\"), and with 巨大, which means the size of a single object. Rather literary; in everyday speech people often simply say 広い.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"巨大なビルが街の中心に建っている。","romaji":"Kyodai na biru ga machi no chūshin ni tatte iru.","german":"A huge building stands in the center of the city.","furigana":"きょだい|まち|ちゅうしん|た"},{"japanese":"巨大な台風が日本に接近している。","romaji":"Kyodai na taifū ga Nihon ni sekkin shite iru.","german":"A huge typhoon is approaching Japan.","furigana":"きょだい|たいふう|にほん|せっきん"},{"japanese":"その巨大企業は市場をほぼ独占している。","romaji":"Sono kyodai kigyō wa shijō o hobo dokusen shite iru.","german":"That giant corporation almost monopolizes the market.","furigana":"きょだいきぎょう|しじょう|どくせん"}],"notes":"巨大 stresses a size far above normal, for things (巨大なビル/岩) as well as for phenomena and organizations (巨大地震, 巨大企業). It often appears directly in compounds without な. 大きな is neutral, 巨大 emphatic; 広大 refers to areas, 膨大 to quantities. The verb 巨大化する means \"to become huge\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"微小な変化も見逃さない。","romaji":"Bishō na henka mo minogasanai.","german":"I don't miss even the tiniest change.","furigana":"びしょう|へんか|みのが"},{"japanese":"微小なゴミも精密機械の故障の原因になる。","romaji":"Bishō na gomi mo seimitsu kikai no koshō no gen'in ni naru.","german":"Even tiny bits of dust can cause precision machines to break down.","furigana":"びしょう|せいみつきかい|こしょう|げんいん"},{"japanese":"微小な地震は毎日のように起きているが、ほとんど感じない。","romaji":"Bishō na jishin wa mainichi no yō ni okite iru ga, hotondo kanjinai.","german":"Tiny earthquakes occur almost every day, but we hardly feel them.","furigana":"びしょう|じしん|まいにち|お|かん"}],"notes":"微小 is a technical word for \"microscopically small, tiny\", typical in science and engineering: 微小な粒子/変化, 微小地震. It stands as 微小な or 微小の before a noun. In everyday speech people more often say ごく小さい or, for amounts and changes, わずかな. 微量, by contrast, denotes a tiny quantity, 極小 the utmost minimum.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"極小なチップが機械に埋め込まれている。","romaji":"Kyokushō na chippu ga kikai ni umekomarete iru.","german":"An extremely small chip is embedded in the machine.","furigana":"きょくしょう|きかい|う|こ"},{"japanese":"このカメラは極小サイズで、ポケットに入る。","romaji":"Kono kamera wa kyokushō saizu de, poketto ni hairu.","german":"This camera is tiny and fits in your pocket.","furigana":"きょくしょう|はい"},{"japanese":"グラフのこの点で関数は極小になる。","romaji":"Gurafu no kono ten de kansū wa kyokushō ni naru.","german":"The function reaches a local minimum at this point of the graph.","furigana":"てん|かんすう|きょくしょう"}],"notes":"極小 (極 \"extremely\" + 小 \"small\") means \"extremely small, minimal\" and is technical and literary. Most often it appears as 極小の or directly in compounds such as 極小サイズ; 極小な is rarer. In mathematics, 極小 means a local minimum (counterpart 極大), whereas 最小 is the absolute minimum. In everyday speech people more often say ごく小さい or 超小型.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人生は有限なので大切に生きるべきだ。","romaji":"Jinsei wa yūgen na node taisetsu ni ikiru beki da.","german":"Life is finite, so we should live it with care.","furigana":"じんせい|ゆうげん|たいせつ|い"},{"japanese":"地球の資源は有限だ。","romaji":"Chikyū no shigen wa yūgen da.","german":"The Earth's resources are limited.","furigana":"ちきゅう|しげん|ゆうげん"},{"japanese":"父は小さな有限会社を経営している。","romaji":"Chichi wa chiisa na yūgen gaisha o keiei shite iru.","german":"My father runs a small limited company (yugen kaisha).","furigana":"ちち|ちい|ゆうげんがいしゃ|けいえい"}],"notes":"有限 means \"limited, finite\" and stands mostly predicatively (資源は有限だ) or as 有限の/有限な before nouns. In everyday speech people often say 限りがある. The opposite is 無限. 有限会社 is the legal form \"limited company\" (can no longer be newly founded since 2006). Not to be confused with the identically read 幽玄, a concept of aesthetics.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これは一時的な措置に過ぎない。","romaji":"Kore wa ichijiteki na sochi ni suginai.","german":"This is nothing more than a temporary measure.","furigana":"いちじてき|そち|す"},{"japanese":"薬を飲むと痛みは一時的に治まる。","romaji":"Kusuri o nomu to itami wa ichijiteki ni osamaru.","german":"When I take the medicine, the pain subsides temporarily.","furigana":"くすり|の|いた|いちじてき|おさ"},{"japanese":"その人気は一時的なものだった。","romaji":"Sono ninki wa ichijiteki na mono datta.","german":"That popularity was only short-lived.","furigana":"にんき|いちじてき"}],"notes":"一時的 means \"temporary, only for a short time\": 一時的な措置/現象/ブーム, adverbial 一時的に (痛みが一時的に治まる). 暫定的, by contrast, stresses \"provisional until a final arrangement\", 臨時 \"extraordinary, unscheduled\". The opposites are 恒久的 and 永続的. Not to be confused with 一時 on its own, which can mean \"for a while\" or \"one o'clock\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"恒久的な平和を実現したい。","romaji":"Kōkyūteki na heiwa wo jitsugen shitai.","german":"I want to bring about lasting peace.","furigana":"こうきゅうてき|へいわ|じつげん"},{"japanese":"その施設は仮設ではなく、恒久的に使われる予定だ。","romaji":"Sono shisetsu wa kasetsu de wa naku, kōkyūteki ni tsukawareru yotei da.","german":"That facility is not temporary; it is planned to be used permanently.","furigana":"しせつ|かせつ|こうきゅうてき|つか|よてい"},{"japanese":"一時的な支援ではなく、恒久的な制度が必要だ。","romaji":"Ichijiteki na shien de wa naku, kōkyūteki na seido ga hitsuyō da.","german":"What we need is not temporary support but a permanent system.","furigana":"いちじてき|しえん|こうきゅうてき|せいど|ひつよう"}],"notes":"恒久的 is formal and belongs to politics, law and administration: 恒久的な平和/対策/施設/制度, adverbial 恒久的に. It stresses lasting validity as opposed to 一時的 or 暫定的. 永久 sounds more like \"eternal, for all time\" (永久歯), 永続的 like \"continuing\". In everyday speech people say the simpler ずっと続く.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"暫定的な合意に達した。","romaji":"Zanteiteki na gōi ni tasshita.","german":"A provisional agreement was reached.","furigana":"ざんていてき|ごうい|たっ"},{"japanese":"新しいリーダーが決まるまで、私が暫定的に担当します。","romaji":"Atarashii rīdā ga kimaru made, watashi ga zanteiteki ni tantō shimasu.","german":"Until a new leader is decided, I will take charge on a provisional basis.","furigana":"あたら|き|わたし|ざんていてき|たんとう"},{"japanese":"これはあくまで暫定的な数字で、後で変わる可能性がある。","romaji":"Kore wa akumade zanteiteki na sūji de, ato de kawaru kanōsei ga aru.","german":"These are only provisional figures and may change later.","furigana":"ざんていてき|すうじ|あと|か|かのうせい"}],"notes":"暫定的 means \"provisional, until a final arrangement\": 暫定的な合意/措置/数字, adverbial 暫定的に. Related are 暫定予算 \"interim budget\" and, in sports, 暫定1位 \"provisionally first\". 一時的 stresses only the short duration, not the later final solution; colloquially people often say 仮の (仮の予定). Typical of news and business language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"最終的な決定は来週行われる。","romaji":"Saishūteki na kettei wa raishū okonawareru.","german":"The final decision will be made next week.","furigana":"さいしゅうてき|けってい|らいしゅうおこな"},{"japanese":"いろいろ迷ったが、最終的に青い車を買った。","romaji":"Iroiro mayotta ga, saishūteki ni aoi kuruma o katta.","german":"I hesitated a lot, but in the end I bought the blue car.","furigana":"まよ|さいしゅうてき|あお|くるま|か"},{"japanese":"最終的な責任は社長にある。","romaji":"Saishūteki na sekinin wa shachō ni aru.","german":"The ultimate responsibility lies with the company president.","furigana":"さいしゅうてき|せきにん|しゃちょう"}],"notes":"最終的 means \"final, ultimate\": 最終的な決定/結果/責任. Very common is the adverb 最終的に \"finally, in the end\", which neutrally describes the result of a process; 結局, by contrast, often sounds like \"in the end after all (contrary to expectation)\". 最後 only denotes the last position in a sequence.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"根本的な改革が必要だ。","romaji":"Konponteki na kaikaku ga hitsuyō da.","german":"A fundamental reform is necessary.","furigana":"こんぽんてき|かいかく|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"彼とは考え方が根本的に違う。","romaji":"Kare to wa kangaekata ga konponteki ni chigau.","german":"My way of thinking is fundamentally different from his.","furigana":"かれ|かんが|かた|こんぽんてき|ちが"},{"japanese":"問題の根本的な原因を明らかにしなければならない。","romaji":"Mondai no konponteki na gen'in o akiraka ni shinakereba naranai.","german":"We must clarify the root cause of the problem.","furigana":"もんだい|こんぽんてき|げんいん|あき"}],"notes":"根本的 (根本 \"root, foundation\") means \"from the ground up, fundamental\": 根本的な原因/解決/改革/見直し, adverbial 根本的に違う/間違っている. 基本的, by contrast, means \"basically, in principle\", 本質的 \"concerning the essence\". Even more formal is 抜本的 (抜本的な改革 \"radical reform\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"本質的な問題を見極める必要がある。","romaji":"Honshitsuteki na mondai wo mikiwameru hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"We need to identify the essential problem.","furigana":"ほんしつてき|もんだい|みきわ|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"二つの方法は本質的に同じだ。","romaji":"Futatsu no hōhō wa honshitsuteki ni onaji da.","german":"The two methods are essentially the same.","furigana":"ふた|ほうほう|ほんしつてき|おな"},{"japanese":"細かい点ではなく、もっと本質的な議論をしよう。","romaji":"Komakai ten de wa naku, motto honshitsuteki na giron o shiyō.","german":"Instead of petty details, let's have a more essential discussion.","furigana":"こま|てん|ほんしつてき|ぎろん"}],"notes":"本質的 (本質 \"essence, core\") means \"essential, concerning the essence\": 本質的な問題/違い/議論, adverbial 本質的に \"essentially\". 根本的 aims at origin and foundation, 実質的 at the actual effect as opposed to the name. The opposite is 表面的. Frequent in discussions, science and business.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"表面的な理解だけでは不十分だ。","romaji":"Hyōmenteki na rikai dake de wa fujūbun da.","german":"A superficial understanding alone is not enough.","furigana":"ひょうめんてき|りかい|ふじゅうぶん"},{"japanese":"二人は表面的には仲が良さそうに見える。","romaji":"Futari wa hyōmenteki ni wa naka ga yosasō ni mieru.","german":"On the surface, the two seem to get along well.","furigana":"ふたり|ひょうめんてき|なか|よ|み"},{"japanese":"職場では表面的な付き合いしかしていない。","romaji":"Shokuba de wa hyōmenteki na tsukiai shika shite inai.","german":"With my colleagues I only have superficial relationships.","furigana":"しょくば|ひょうめんてき|つ|あ"}],"notes":"表面的 means \"superficial, only on the outside\" and is mostly meant critically: 表面的な理解/付き合い/変化. 表面的には means \"on the surface, outwardly\". For a superficial character people more often say 浅はかな or 薄っぺらい. The opposites are 本質的 and 根本的.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"形式的な挨拶を済ませた。","romaji":"Keishikiteki na aisatsu wo sumaseta.","german":"I got the formal greetings out of the way.","furigana":"けいしきてき|あいさつ|す"},{"japanese":"面接は形式的なもので、採用はほぼ決まっている。","romaji":"Mensetsu wa keishikiteki na mono de, saiyō wa hobo kimatte iru.","german":"The interview is a mere formality; the hiring is almost decided.","furigana":"めんせつ|けいしきてき|さいよう|き"},{"japanese":"会議は形式的に開かれただけで、何も決まらなかった。","romaji":"Kaigi wa keishikiteki ni hirakareta dake de, nani mo kimaranakatta.","german":"The meeting was held only for form's sake, and nothing was decided.","furigana":"かいぎ|けいしきてき|ひら|なに|き"}],"notes":"形式的 mostly means \"purely formal, only for form's sake\" and is often critical: 形式的な挨拶/手続き/会議, 形式的なもの \"a pure formality\". It forms a pair with 実質的 \"actual, in substance\". For \"formal, ceremonial\" in clothing or occasions, people say フォーマルな, 正式な or 改まった instead.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"実質的なリーダーは彼だ。","romaji":"Jisshitsuteki na rīdā wa kare da.","german":"The real leader is him.","furigana":"じっしつてき|かれ"},{"japanese":"値段は同じだが、量が減ったので実質的には値上げだ。","romaji":"Nedan wa onaji da ga, ryō ga hetta node jisshitsuteki ni wa neage da.","german":"The price is the same, but the amount has decreased, so in effect it's a price increase.","furigana":"ねだん|おな|りょう|へ|じっしつてき|ねあ"},{"japanese":"物価が上がって、実質的な給料は下がっている。","romaji":"Bukka ga agatte, jisshitsuteki na kyūryō wa sagatte iru.","german":"Prices are rising, so real salaries are falling.","furigana":"ぶっか|あ|じっしつてき|きゅうりょう|さ"}],"notes":"実質的 means \"actual, effective, in substance\" as opposed to 名目 \"in name only\": 実質的なリーダー, 実質的には値上げ \"effectively a price increase\". In economics: 実質賃金 \"real wage\", 実質GDP. 本質的, by contrast, means \"concerning the essence\". It forms an opposing pair with 形式的.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"主観的な意見ではなく、客観的な事実に基づいて判断すべきだ。","romaji":"Shukanteki na iken de wa naku, kyakkanteki na jijitsu ni motozuite handan subeki da.","german":"One should judge based on objective facts, not subjective opinions.","furigana":"しゅかんてき|いけん|きゃっかんてき|じじつ|もと|はんだん"},{"japanese":"味の感じ方は主観的なものだ。","romaji":"Aji no kanjikata wa shukanteki na mono da.","german":"How one perceives taste is something subjective.","furigana":"あじ|かん|かた|しゅかんてき"},{"japanese":"この評価は主観的になりすぎている。","romaji":"Kono hyōka wa shukanteki ni narisugite iru.","german":"This evaluation has become too subjective.","furigana":"ひょうか|しゅかんてき"}],"notes":"主観的 means \"subjective, shaped by one's own view\": 主観的な意見/評価/判断, adverbial 主観的に. It forms a fixed pair with 客観的. The noun 主観 often appears in the qualification あくまで私の主観ですが \"this is just my personal view, but ...\". Register neutral to objective-sounding.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"客観的なデータに基づいて分析した。","romaji":"Kyakkanteki na dēta ni motozuite bunseki shita.","german":"I analyzed it based on objective data.","furigana":"きゃっかんてき|もと|ぶんせき"},{"japanese":"自分のことを客観的に見るのは難しい。","romaji":"Jibun no koto o kyakkanteki ni miru no wa muzukashii.","german":"It is hard to view yourself objectively.","furigana":"じぶん|きゃっかんてき|み|むずか"},{"japanese":"裁判では客観的な証拠が重視される。","romaji":"Saiban de wa kyakkanteki na shōko ga jūshi sareru.","german":"In court, objective evidence is given great weight.","furigana":"さいばん|きゃっかんてき|しょうこ|じゅうし"}],"notes":"客観的 (reading きゃっかん, not きゃくかん) means \"objective, independent of personal feelings\": 客観的なデータ/証拠/事実, especially common adverbially as 客観的に見る/判断する/評価する. The related noun is 客観性 \"objectivity\". The opposite is 主観的.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"幸福は相対的な概念だ。","romaji":"Kōfuku wa sōtaiteki na gainen da.","german":"Happiness is a relative concept.","furigana":"こうふく|そうたいてき|がいねん"},{"japanese":"円高で輸入品の値段が相対的に安くなった。","romaji":"Endaka de yunyūhin no nedan ga sōtaiteki ni yasuku natta.","german":"Because of the strong yen, imported goods have become relatively cheaper.","furigana":"えんだか|ゆにゅうひん|ねだん|そうたいてき|やす"},{"japanese":"この学校の成績は相対的な評価で決まる。","romaji":"Kono gakkō no seiseki wa sōtaiteki na hyōka de kimaru.","german":"Grades at this school are determined by relative evaluation.","furigana":"がっこう|せいせき|そうたいてき|ひょうか|き"}],"notes":"相対的 means \"relative, in relation to something else\": 相対的な概念/評価, adverbial 相対的に \"relatively\". The opposite is 絶対的; at school people distinguish 相対評価 and 絶対評価. In everyday speech, \"relatively (cheap, etc.)\" is mostly 比較的; 相対的 stresses the explicit comparison and sounds more objective.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"絶対的な正義は存在するのか。","romaji":"Zettaiteki na seigi wa sonzai suru no ka.","german":"Does absolute justice exist?","furigana":"ぜったいてき|せいぎ|そんざい"},{"japanese":"彼は自分の判断に絶対的な自信を持っている。","romaji":"Kare wa jibun no handan ni zettaiteki na jishin o motte iru.","german":"He has absolute confidence in his own judgment.","furigana":"かれ|じぶん|はんだん|ぜったいてき|じしん|も"},{"japanese":"社長はその会社で絶対的な権力を握っている。","romaji":"Shachō wa sono kaisha de zettaiteki na kenryoku o nigitte iru.","german":"The president holds absolute power in that company.","furigana":"しゃちょう|かいしゃ|ぜったいてき|けんりょく|にぎ"}],"notes":"絶対的 means \"absolute, unlimited, unquestionable\": 絶対的な権力/自信/存在, also 絶対的に不足している \"absolutely insufficient\". Not to be confused with the colloquial adverb 絶対(に) \"definitely, without fail\" (絶対に行く). The opposite is 相対的. Rather objective and formal.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"肯定的な態度で物事に取り組む。","romaji":"Kōteiteki na taido de monogoto ni torikumu.","german":"Approach things with a positive attitude.","furigana":"こうていてき|たいど|ものごと|と|く"},{"japanese":"部長は私の提案を肯定的に受け止めてくれた。","romaji":"Buchō wa watashi no teian o kōteiteki ni uketomete kureta.","german":"The department head received my proposal favorably.","furigana":"ぶちょう|わたし|ていあん|こうていてき|う|と"},{"japanese":"アンケートでは七割が肯定的な回答だった。","romaji":"Ankēto de wa nanawari ga kōteiteki na kaitō datta.","german":"In the survey, 70 percent gave affirmative answers.","furigana":"ななわり|こうていてき|かいとう"}],"notes":"肯定的 (肯定 \"affirmation\") means \"approving, affirmative, evaluating positively\": 肯定的な意見/回答/評価, adverbial 肯定的に受け止める. For an optimistic basic attitude, people in everyday speech more often say 前向き or ポジティブ. The opposite is 否定的; in grammar, the affirmative form is 肯定形.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"否定的な意見ばかり言わないでほしい。","romaji":"Hiteiteki na iken bakari iwanaide hoshii.","german":"I wish you wouldn't only voice negative opinions.","furigana":"ひていてき|いけん|い"},{"japanese":"専門家はその計画に否定的だ。","romaji":"Senmonka wa sono keikaku ni hiteiteki da.","german":"The experts are negative about the plan.","furigana":"せんもんか|けいかく|ひていてき"},{"japanese":"実験は否定的な結果に終わった。","romaji":"Jikken wa hiteiteki na kekka ni owatta.","german":"The experiment ended with a negative result.","furigana":"じっけん|ひていてき|けっか|お"}],"notes":"否定的 means \"dismissive, negative\": 否定的な意見/見方/結果. Typical is 〜に否定的だ \"to take a negative stance toward something\". For a pessimistic attitude, people in everyday speech more often say ネガティブ or 後ろ向き. The related verb is 否定する \"to deny, negate\"; the opposite is 肯定的.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"能動的な学習が求められている。","romaji":"Nōdōteki na gakushū ga motomerarete iru.","german":"Active learning is called for.","furigana":"のうどうてき|がくしゅう|もと"},{"japanese":"指示を待つのではなく、能動的に動ける人材を採用したい。","romaji":"Shiji o matsu no de wa naku, nōdōteki ni ugokeru jinzai o saiyō shitai.","german":"We want to hire people who don't wait for instructions but can take the initiative.","furigana":"しじ|ま|のうどうてき|うご|じんざい|さいよう"},{"japanese":"子どもが能動的に遊べる環境を作りたい。","romaji":"Kodomo ga nōdōteki ni asoberu kankyō o tsukuritai.","german":"I want to create an environment where children can play on their own initiative.","furigana":"こ|のうどうてき|あそ|かんきょう|つく"}],"notes":"能動的 means \"active, acting on one's own initiative\" as opposed to 受動的: 能動的な学習/姿勢, adverbial 能動的に動く. In everyday speech 積極的 \"proactive, enterprising\" is much more common; 自主的 and 主体的 stress independence. In grammar, the active voice is 能動態. Rather formal (education, personnel).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"受動的な姿勢では成長できない。","romaji":"Judōteki na shisei de wa seichō dekinai.","german":"You can't grow with a passive attitude.","furigana":"じゅどうてき|しせい|せいちょう"},{"japanese":"テレビを受動的に見るだけでは、語学力はあまり伸びない。","romaji":"Terebi o judōteki ni miru dake de wa, gogakuryoku wa amari nobinai.","german":"Just watching TV passively won't improve your language skills much.","furigana":"じゅどうてき|み|ごがくりょく|の"},{"japanese":"彼は会議ではいつも受動的で、自分から発言しない。","romaji":"Kare wa kaigi de wa itsumo judōteki de, jibun kara hatsugen shinai.","german":"He is always passive in meetings and never speaks up on his own.","furigana":"かれ|かいぎ|じゅどうてき|じぶん|はつげん"}],"notes":"受動的 means \"passive, merely reacting\" as opposed to 能動的: 受動的な姿勢/学習, adverbial 受動的に見る. In everyday speech 消極的 \"reserved, not taking initiative\" is more common. In grammar, the passive is 受動態 or, in textbooks, 受身; 受動喫煙 is \"passive smoking\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"独創的なアイデアが評価された。","romaji":"Dokusōteki na aidea ga hyōka sareta.","german":"The original idea was appreciated.","furigana":"どくそうてき|ひょうか"},{"japanese":"彼女の作品はどれも独創的で、すぐに彼女のものだとわかる。","romaji":"Kanojo no sakuhin wa dore mo dokusōteki de, sugu ni kanojo no mono da to wakaru.","german":"Her works are all so original that you can tell right away they're hers.","furigana":"かのじょ|さくひん|どくそうてき|かのじょ"},{"japanese":"このレストランは和食とフランス料理を組み合わせた独創的な料理で有名だ。","romaji":"Kono resutoran wa washoku to Furansu ryōri o kumiawaseta dokusōteki na ryōri de yūmei da.","german":"This restaurant is famous for its original dishes combining Japanese and French cuisine.","furigana":"わしょく|りょうり|く|あ|どくそうてき|りょうり|ゆうめい"}],"notes":"独創的 (独創 \"one's own creation\") praises genuine originality: 独創的なアイデア/作品/発想; the noun is 独創性. 創造的 refers more to the general ability to create something new, whereas 独創的 stresses that something is unique and not imitated. Colloquially people also say ユニーク, which can also mean \"odd\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"革新的な技術が開発された。","romaji":"Kakushinteki na gijutsu ga kaihatsu sareta.","german":"An innovative technology was developed.","furigana":"かくしんてき|ぎじゅつ|かいはつ"},{"japanese":"この会社は次々と革新的な製品を生み出している。","romaji":"Kono kaisha wa tsugitsugi to kakushinteki na seihin o umidashite iru.","german":"This company produces one innovative product after another.","furigana":"かいしゃ|つぎつぎ|かくしんてき|せいひん|う|だ"},{"japanese":"当時、その市長は革新的な政策で注目を集めた。","romaji":"Tōji, sono shichō wa kakushinteki na seisaku de chūmoku o atsumeta.","german":"At that time, the mayor drew attention with his reformist policies.","furigana":"とうじ|しちょう|かくしんてき|せいさく|ちゅうもく|あつ"}],"notes":"革新的 means \"innovative, novel\": 革新的な技術/製品/アイデア. In politics, 革新 stands for \"reform-minded, progressive\" as the counterpart to 保守 (革新的な政策). 画期的 stresses a single epoch-making breakthrough, 先進的 the lead over others. Typical of business, technology and news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の考え方は保守的だ。","romaji":"Kare no kangaekata wa hoshuteki da.","german":"His way of thinking is conservative.","furigana":"かれ|かんが|かた|ほしゅてき"},{"japanese":"保守的な政党が選挙で勝利した。","romaji":"Hoshuteki na seitō ga senkyo de shōri shita.","german":"A conservative party won the election.","furigana":"ほしゅてき|せいとう|せんきょ|しょうり"},{"japanese":"老後の資金は保守的に運用したい。","romaji":"Rōgo no shikin wa hoshuteki ni un'yō shitai.","german":"I want to invest my retirement funds conservatively.","furigana":"ろうご|しきん|ほしゅてき|うんよう"}],"notes":"保守的 means \"conservative, holding on to the existing\" in politics and values (保守的な政党/考え方), but also \"cautious\" for estimates and investments (保守的な見積もり). The opposite is 革新的 or 進歩的. Note: 保守 on its own also means \"maintenance\" in engineering (保守点検).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"画期的な発明が世界を変えた。","romaji":"Kakkiteki na hatsumei ga sekai wo kaeta.","german":"A groundbreaking invention changed the world.","furigana":"かっきてき|はつめい|せかい|か"},{"japanese":"がんの画期的な治療法が見つかった。","romaji":"Gan no kakkiteki na chiryōhō ga mitsukatta.","german":"A groundbreaking cancer treatment was discovered.","furigana":"かっきてき|ちりょうほう|み"},{"japanese":"これは画期的なアイデアだね！","romaji":"Kore wa kakkiteki na aidea da ne!","german":"That's a brilliant idea!","furigana":"かっきてき"}],"notes":"画期的 (reading かっき, not がき) means \"groundbreaking, epoch-making\", something that ushers in a new era: 画期的な発明/発見/治療法. In advertising and everyday speech it is also used exaggeratingly for \"brilliant, handy\" (画期的なアイデア). 革新的 stresses innovation in general.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先進的な医療技術が導入された。","romaji":"Senshinteki na iryō gijutsu ga dōnyū sareta.","german":"Advanced medical technology was introduced.","furigana":"せんしんてき|いりょうぎじゅつ|どうにゅう"},{"japanese":"この町は環境対策において先進的な取り組みをしている。","romaji":"Kono machi wa kankyō taisaku ni oite senshinteki na torikumi o shite iru.","german":"This town is taking progressive initiatives in environmental protection.","furigana":"まち|かんきょうたいさく|せんしんてき|と|く"},{"japanese":"その車は先進的なデザインで若者に人気だ。","romaji":"Sono kuruma wa senshinteki na dezain de wakamono ni ninki da.","german":"That car is popular with young people for its modern design.","furigana":"くるま|せんしんてき|わかもの|にんき"}],"notes":"先進的 means \"advanced, ahead of others\": 先進的な技術/取り組み/デザイン. Related is 先進国 \"developed country\". 最先端 stresses the absolute cutting edge (\"high-tech\"), 進歩的 rather progressive ideas and views. The opposite is 後進的.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"後進的な制度を改善する必要がある。","romaji":"Kōshinteki na seido wo kaizen suru hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"The outdated system needs to be improved.","furigana":"こうしんてき|せいど|かいぜん|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"この国はかつて後進的だと言われていたが、今では急速に発展している。","romaji":"Kono kuni wa katsute kōshinteki da to iwarete ita ga, ima de wa kyūsoku ni hatten shite iru.","german":"This country was once said to be backward, but now it is developing rapidly.","furigana":"くに|こうしんてき|い|いま|きゅうそく|はってん"},{"japanese":"その会社はIT化の面では後進的で、今もファックスを使っている。","romaji":"Sono kaisha wa aitīka no men de wa kōshinteki de, ima mo fakkusu o tsukatte iru.","german":"That company is backward in terms of IT; it still uses fax machines.","furigana":"かいしゃ|か|めん|こうしんてき|いま|つか"}],"notes":"後進的 means \"backward, lagging behind\" and is derogatory; for countries, people now say 発展途上国 or 途上国 instead of 後進国. In everyday speech people more often say 遅れている or 時代遅れ. Note: the noun 後進 on its own mostly means \"younger generation, successors\" (後進を育てる \"to nurture the next generation\"). The opposite is 先進的.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は決して嘘をつかない。","romaji":"Kare wa kesshite uso o tsukanai.","german":"He never tells lies.","furigana":"かれ|けっ|うそ"},{"japanese":"このことは決して忘れません。","romaji":"Kono koto wa kesshite wasuremasen.","german":"I will never forget this.","furigana":"けっ|わす"},{"japanese":"この薬は決して子どもに飲ませないでください。","romaji":"Kono kusuri wa kesshite kodomo ni nomasenaide kudasai.","german":"Please never let children take this medicine.","furigana":"くすり|けっ|こ|の"}],"notes":"決して always occurs with a negation (〜ない, 〜ません, 〜ないでください) and strengthens it to \"never, by no means\": 決して忘れません. It sounds more serious and formal than 絶対に, which also occurs in affirmative sentences. In warnings, 決して〜ないでください is typical, and 決して悪くない means \"not bad at all\". It is often written in kana as けっして.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"宿題はほとんど終わった。","romaji":"Shukudai wa hotondo owatta.","german":"My homework is almost done.","furigana":"しゅくだい|お"},{"japanese":"昨日はほとんど寝ていません。","romaji":"Kinō wa hotondo nete imasen.","german":"I hardly slept yesterday.","furigana":"きのう|ね"},{"japanese":"クラスのほとんどの学生が自転車で来ます。","romaji":"Kurasu no hotondo no gakusei ga jitensha de kimasu.","german":"Most of the students in the class come by bicycle.","furigana":"がくせい|じてんしゃ|き"}],"notes":"With an affirmative verb ほとんど means \"almost, nearly\" (ほとんど終わった), with a negation \"hardly\" (ほとんど寝ていない, ほとんど知らない). As a noun with の it means \"most, the greater part\": ほとんどの学生. It is almost always written in kana. だいたい is more approximate (\"roughly, on the whole\"), while ほとんど is closer to \"completely\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 27"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"週末は大抵家にいます。","romaji":"Shūmatsu wa taitei ie ni imasu.","german":"I'm usually at home on weekends.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|たいていいえ"},{"japanese":"大抵の人はこの方法を知っている。","romaji":"Taitei no hito wa kono hōhō o shitte iru.","german":"Most people know this method.","furigana":"たいてい|ひと|ほうほう|し"},{"japanese":"一人で子どもを三人育てるのは並大抵のことではない。","romaji":"Hitori de kodomo o sannin sodateru no wa namitaitei no koto de wa nai.","german":"Raising three children alone is no small feat.","furigana":"ひとり|こ|さんにんそだ|なみたいてい"}],"notes":"大抵 is mostly written in kana (たいてい). As an adverb it means \"usually, as a rule\" (週末はたいてい家にいる); as 大抵の + noun it means \"most\" (大抵の人). It lies between いつも \"always\" and よく \"often\"; ほとんど emphasizes quantity more. The fixed phrase 並大抵ではない means \"no small matter, extraordinarily hard\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"朝はたいていコーヒーを飲む。","romaji":"Asa wa taitei kōhī o nomu.","german":"In the morning I usually drink coffee.","furigana":"あさ|の"},{"japanese":"会社へはたいてい電車で行きますが、雨の日はバスです。","romaji":"Kaisha e wa taitei densha de ikimasu ga, ame no hi wa basu desu.","german":"I usually go to work by train, but on rainy days I take the bus.","furigana":"かいしゃ|でんしゃ|い|あめ|ひ"},{"japanese":"たいていの店は十時に開きます。","romaji":"Taitei no mise wa jūji ni akimasu.","german":"Most shops open at ten.","furigana":"みせ|じゅうじ|あ"}],"notes":"たいてい describes a habit that holds in most cases: \"usually, as a rule\", so somewhat weaker than いつも and stronger than よく. With の before a noun it stands for \"most\": たいていの店, たいていの人. It is usually written in kana; the kanji spelling 大抵 is rare. Unlike ほとんど, たいてい is about frequency, not quantity.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 28"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ここからおよそ三十分かかります。","romaji":"Koko kara oyoso sanjuppun kakarimasu.","german":"It takes about thirty minutes from here.","furigana":"さんじゅっぷん"},{"japanese":"説明を聞いて、仕事の内容はおよそ分かった。","romaji":"Setsumei o kiite, shigoto no naiyō wa oyoso wakatta.","german":"After hearing the explanation, I roughly understood what the job involved.","furigana":"せつめい|き|しごと|ないよう|わ"},{"japanese":"そんな話はおよそ信じがたい。","romaji":"Sonna hanashi wa oyoso shinjigatai.","german":"Such a story is hardly believable at all.","furigana":"はなし|しん"}],"notes":"およそ (rarely 凡そ) means \"approximately, about\" and stands before numbers (およそ三十分) or with verbs (およそ分かる \"to understand roughly\"). It is somewhat more formal than だいたい; 約 stands only before numbers. With negation, およそ means \"not at all\" (およそ信じがたい). Variants: おおよそ, as a noun \"outline.\"","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"参加者は約百人だった。","romaji":"Sankasha wa yaku hyakunin datta.","german":"There were about a hundred participants.","furigana":"さんかしゃ|やくひゃくにん"},{"japanese":"会議は約二時間続いた。","romaji":"Kaigi wa yaku nijikan tsuzuita.","german":"The meeting lasted about two hours.","furigana":"かいぎ|やくにじかんつづ"},{"japanese":"費用は約三万円を予定しています。","romaji":"Hiyō wa yaku sanman en o yotei shite imasu.","german":"The cost is planned to be about 30,000 yen.","furigana":"ひよう|やくさんまんえん|よてい"}],"notes":"約 stands as a prefix directly before numbers: 約百人, 約2時間. It is factual and literary (news, reports); in speech people often say 百人くらい or だいたい百人. Unlike およそ or だいたい, 約 cannot stand with verbs (not 約分かる). As a kanji it also occurs in 約束 \"promise.\"","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"作業はほぼ完了した。","romaji":"Sagyō wa hobo kanryō shita.","german":"The work is almost complete.","furigana":"さぎょう|かんりょう"},{"japanese":"駅から会社まで、ほぼ毎日歩いています。","romaji":"Eki kara kaisha made, hobo mainichi aruite imasu.","german":"I walk from the station to the company almost every day.","furigana":"えき|かいしゃ|まいにちある"},{"japanese":"二人の身長はほぼ同じだ。","romaji":"Futari no shinchou wa hobo onaji da.","german":"The two are almost the same height.","furigana":"ふたり|しんちょう|おな"}],"notes":"ほぼ means \"almost, nearly\" (ほぼ完成, ほぼ毎日) or \"roughly\" in comparisons and quantities (ほぼ同じ, ほぼ半分). It is neutral to slightly literary and stresses that only a little is missing. ほとんど is similar, but can also stand as a noun (ほとんどの人) and means \"hardly\" with negation (ほとんどない); だいたい is more casual and means \"by and large.\" Almost always written in kana; the kanji 略 and 粗 are very rare.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"まさにその通りです。","romaji":"Masa ni sono tōri desu.","german":"That's exactly right.","furigana":"とお"},{"japanese":"これこそまさに私が探していた本だ。","romaji":"Kore koso masa ni watashi ga sagashite ita hon da.","german":"This is exactly the book I was looking for.","furigana":"わたし|さが|ほん"},{"japanese":"電車がまさに出発しようとしたとき、駅に着いた。","romaji":"Densha ga masa ni shuppatsu shiyō to shita toki, eki ni tsuita.","german":"I arrived at the station just as the train was about to depart.","furigana":"でんしゃ|しゅっぱつ|えき|つ"}],"notes":"まさに (rarely 正に) is an emphatic \"exactly, truly\": まさにその通り \"exactly so,\" これこそまさに… \"this is exactly ...\". With 〜ようとする it means \"just about to\" (まさに出発しようとしていた). It sounds somewhat more formal than ちょうど or 本当に. Not to be confused with まさか \"no way!\"","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"正に奇跡と言える出来事だった。","romaji":"Masa ni kiseki to ieru dekigoto datta.","german":"It was truly an event one could call a miracle.","furigana":"まさ|きせき|い|できごと"},{"japanese":"まさにその通りだと思います。","romaji":"Masa ni sono tōri da to omoimasu.","german":"I think that's exactly right.","furigana":"とお|おも"},{"japanese":"電車がまさに出発しようとしたとき、駅に着いた。","romaji":"Densha ga masa ni shuppatsu shiyō to shita toki, eki ni tsuita.","german":"I arrived at the station just as the train was about to depart.","furigana":"でんしゃ|しゅっぱつ|えき|つ"}],"notes":"まさに stresses that something applies exactly and without reservation: \"exactly, truly, downright.\" Typical are まさに〜だ or まさに〜と言える (まさに奇跡だ) as well as the agreement まさにその通りです. With 〜ようとする or 〜ところ it means \"just about to\" (まさに出発しようとしていた). Mostly written in kana; 正に looks literary. Somewhat more elevated and emphatic than 本当に or ちょうど.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"確かにそれは難しい問題だ。","romaji":"Tashika ni sore wa muzukashii mondai da.","german":"That is certainly a difficult problem.","furigana":"たし|むずか|もんだい"},{"japanese":"確かにこの店は高いけど、料理は最高だ。","romaji":"Tashika ni kono mise wa takai kedo, ryōri wa saikō da.","german":"This restaurant is indeed expensive, but the food is the best.","furigana":"たし|みせ|たか|りょうり|さいこう"},{"japanese":"荷物は確かに受け取りました。","romaji":"Nimotsu wa tashika ni uketorimashita.","german":"I have duly received the package.","furigana":"にもつ|たし|う|と"}],"notes":"確かに confirms a statement: \"certainly, indeed, undoubtedly\". Very common is the concessive use 確かに〜が/けど \"it's true that ..., but\": 確かに高いけど、品質はいい. As a short agreement, people say 確かに！ or 確かにそうですね \"True!\". To be distinguished from the na-adjective 確か (確かな情報 \"reliable information\") and from the sentence-initial 確か \"if I remember correctly\" (確か月曜日だった).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"実に素晴らしい演奏だった。","romaji":"Jitsu ni subarashii ensō datta.","german":"It was a truly wonderful performance.","furigana":"じつ|すば|えんそう"},{"japanese":"参加者は実に千人を超えた。","romaji":"Sankasha wa jitsu ni sennin o koeta.","german":"The participants numbered over a thousand, no less.","furigana":"さんかしゃ|じつ|せんにん|こ"},{"japanese":"彼の説明は実に分かりやすかった。","romaji":"Kare no setsumei wa jitsu ni wakariyasukatta.","german":"His explanation was really easy to understand.","furigana":"かれ|せつめい|じつ|わ"}],"notes":"実に intensifies adjectives and judgments: \"really, truly, extremely.\" It is weightier and more literary than 本当に or とても and sounds slightly stiff in everyday conversation; typical in speeches, reviews and commentary. Before numbers it expresses astonishment at the size: 実に千人 \"no fewer than a thousand.\" Not to be confused with 実は \"to be honest, actually.\"","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"誠にありがとうございます。","romaji":"Makoto ni arigatō gozaimasu.","german":"Thank you very much indeed.","furigana":"まこと"},{"japanese":"ご迷惑をおかけして、誠に申し訳ございません。","romaji":"Gomeiwaku o okake shite, makoto ni mōshiwake gozaimasen.","german":"I sincerely apologize for the inconvenience.","furigana":"めいわく|まこと|もう|わけ"},{"japanese":"誠に恐れ入りますが、こちらにご記入ください。","romaji":"Makoto ni osoreirimasu ga, kochira ni gokinyū kudasai.","german":"I am very sorry to trouble you, but could you please fill this in here?","furigana":"まこと|おそ|い|きにゅう"}],"notes":"誠に is a very polite intensifying adverb: \"sincerely, truly, deeply\". It appears almost always in fixed polite formulas such as 誠にありがとうございます, 誠に申し訳ございません or 誠に恐れ入りますが; in business and in announcements it is standard. In casual conversation it sounds overly formal; there 本当に is enough. The kanji spelling 誠に is common, and the kana まことに is also widespread.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 20"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一旦休憩しましょう。","romaji":"Ittan kyūkei shimashō.","german":"Let's take a break for now.","furigana":"いったんきゅうけい"},{"japanese":"忘れ物をしたので、一旦家に戻ります。","romaji":"Wasuremono o shita node, ittan ie ni modorimasu.","german":"I forgot something, so I'll go back home for a moment.","furigana":"わす|もの|いったんいえ|もど"},{"japanese":"一旦約束したら、必ず守るべきだ。","romaji":"Ittan yakusoku shitara, kanarazu mamoru beki da.","german":"Once you've made a promise, you should keep it.","furigana":"いったんやくそく|かなら|まも"}],"notes":"一旦 has two main uses: \"temporarily, for now\" for an interruption after which things continue (一旦休憩する, 一旦家に帰る), and \"once, as soon as\" in conditional sentences with 〜たら/〜と/〜ば (一旦始めたら最後までやる). In the sense of \"for the time being\" it resembles とりあえず, but stresses the later continuation. Usually written in kana いったん; the kanji form looks more literary.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いったん決めたことは変えない。","romaji":"Ittan kimeta koto wa kaenai.","german":"Once I decide something, I don't change it.","furigana":"き|か"},{"japanese":"作業の途中ですが、いったんデータを保存してください。","romaji":"Sagyō no tochū desu ga, ittan dēta o hozon shite kudasai.","german":"I know you're in the middle of work, but please save your data for now.","furigana":"さぎょう|とちゅう|ほぞん"},{"japanese":"この件はいったん持ち帰って、社内で検討します。","romaji":"Kono ken wa ittan mochikaette, shanai de kentō shimasu.","german":"We'll take this matter back with us for now and consider it internally.","furigana":"けん|も|かえ|しゃない|けんとう"}],"notes":"いったん is the usual kana spelling of 一旦. It means, on the one hand, \"for now, temporarily\" (いったん保存する, いったん持ち帰る), and on the other, in conditional sentences, \"once, as soon as\": いったん〜たら/〜と/〜ば, often with the consequence that something can no longer be undone. In meetings, いったん持ち帰って検討します is a polite way to postpone a decision. Unlike とりあえず, it sounds more orderly and less provisional.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"とりあえずビールをお願いします。","romaji":"Toriaezu bīru o onegai shimasu.","german":"A beer for now, please.","furigana":"ねが"},{"japanese":"詳しい日程は後で決めて、とりあえず会場だけ予約した。","romaji":"Kuwashii nittei wa ato de kimete, toriaezu kaijō dake yoyaku shita.","german":"I decided the detailed schedule later and, for the time being, only reserved the venue.","furigana":"くわ|にってい|あと|き|かいじょう|よやく"},{"japanese":"とりあえず、みんな無事でよかった。","romaji":"Toriaezu, minna buji de yokatta.","german":"Anyway, I'm glad everyone is safe.","furigana":"ぶじ"}],"notes":"とりあえず means \"for now, first of all\": one deals with the nearest matter and postpones the rest, famous as とりあえずビール when ordering at an izakaya. It also means \"at any rate, in any case\" (とりあえず無事でよかった). With customers or superiors it can sound makeshift and unprepared; more formal are まず or ひとまず. Almost always in kana; 取りあえず is rare.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"さっそく始めましょう。","romaji":"Sassoku hajimemashō.","german":"Let's start right away.","furigana":"はじ"},{"japanese":"新しい靴を買ったので、さっそく履いて出かけた。","romaji":"Atarashii kutsu o katta node, sassoku haite dekaketa.","german":"I bought new shoes, so I put them on and went out right away.","furigana":"あたら|くつ|か|は|で"},{"japanese":"さっそくですが、本題に入らせていただきます。","romaji":"Sassoku desu ga, hondai ni hairasete itadakimasu.","german":"If I may, I'll get right to the main topic.","furigana":"ほんだい|はい"}],"notes":"さっそく means \"immediately, right away, without hesitating\" and describes a prompt reaction to something that came before: you receive something, learn something or are asked, and act at once. Typical: さっそく使ってみる and さっそくですが as a transition to the actual topic in meetings. Unlike すぐ, it has a positive coloring of eagerness; for urgent instructions, ただちに fits better. The kanji spelling 早速 is just as common.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"問題が発生したのでただちに対応した。","romaji":"Mondai ga hassei shita node tadachi ni taiō shita.","german":"A problem occurred, so we responded immediately.","furigana":"もんだい|はっせい|たいおう"},{"japanese":"津波警報が出ました。ただちに高い所へ避難してください。","romaji":"Tsunami keihō ga demashita. Tadachi ni takai tokoro e hinan shite kudasai.","german":"A tsunami warning has been issued. Please evacuate to high ground immediately.","furigana":"つなみけいほう|で|たか|ところ|ひなん"},{"japanese":"この数字がただちに失敗を意味するわけではない。","romaji":"Kono sūji ga tadachi ni shippai o imi suru wake de wa nai.","german":"This figure does not necessarily mean failure.","furigana":"すうじ|しっぱい|いみ"}],"notes":"ただちに means \"immediately, without delay\" and is formal: typical in instructions, announcements, news and regulations (ただちに避難してください). It is more urgent and stricter than すぐに; さっそく, on the other hand, stresses eager action in response to something pleasant. With negation, ただちに〜わけではない means \"not immediately, not automatically\" (ただちに健康に影響はない). The kanji spelling 直ちに is also common.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は質問に即座に答えた。","romaji":"Kare wa shitsumon ni sokuza ni kotaeta.","german":"He answered the question immediately.","furigana":"かれ|しつもん|そくざ|こた"},{"japanese":"その提案は、社長に即座に断られた。","romaji":"Sono teian wa, shachō ni sokuza ni kotowarareta.","german":"The proposal was turned down by the president on the spot.","furigana":"ていあん|しゃちょう|そくざ|ことわ"},{"japanese":"地震の揺れを感じたら、即座に机の下に入ってください。","romaji":"Jishin no yure o kanjitara, sokuza ni tsukue no shita ni haitte kudasai.","german":"If you feel an earthquake, immediately get under a desk.","furigana":"じしん|ゆ|かん|そくざ|つくえ|した|はい"}],"notes":"即座に means \"on the spot, promptly, without hesitation\" - the reaction follows a trigger such as a question, a proposal or an event directly. It is somewhat more literary than すぐ(に); similar are 直ちに (formal, esp. in instructions) and その場で \"on the spot\". Before nouns it is 即座の: 即座の判断 \"an immediate decision\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"早速お返事いただきありがとうございます。","romaji":"Sassoku ohenji itadaki arigatō gozaimasu.","german":"Thank you for your prompt reply.","furigana":"さっそく|へんじ"},{"japanese":"教えてもらったレシピを早速作ってみた。","romaji":"Oshiete moratta reshipi o sassoku tsukutte mita.","german":"I tried out the recipe you told me right away.","furigana":"おし|さっそくつく"},{"japanese":"早速ですが、来月のイベントについてご相談があります。","romaji":"Sassoku desu ga, raigetsu no ibento ni tsuite gosōdan ga arimasu.","german":"To get right to the point: I'd like to consult you about next month's event.","furigana":"さっそく|らいげつ|そうだん"}],"notes":"早速 is the kanji spelling of さっそく \"immediately, promptly\"; both forms are common, and in business emails 早速 is often seen. Set phrases are 早速のご返信ありがとうございます \"thank you for your prompt reply\" (with の before a noun) and 早速ですが as an introduction to the topic. It expresses a pleasantly quick reaction, not an obligation like ただちに.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いよいよ明日が試験の日だ。","romaji":"Iyoiyo ashita ga shiken no hi da.","german":"Tomorrow is finally the day of the exam.","furigana":"あした|しけん|ひ"},{"japanese":"夜になって、風はいよいよ強くなった。","romaji":"Yoru ni natte, kaze wa iyoiyo tsuyoku natta.","german":"As night fell, the wind grew stronger and stronger.","furigana":"よる|かぜ|つよ"},{"japanese":"いよいよとなったら、親に相談するつもりだ。","romaji":"Iyoiyo to nattara, oya ni sōdan suru tsumori da.","german":"If it really comes to that, I intend to consult my parents.","furigana":"おや|そうだん"}],"notes":"いよいよ has two core meanings: \"now at last, the time has come\" for a long-awaited moment (いよいよ明日から夏休み) and \"more and more, increasingly\" for an intensification (雨がいよいよ激しくなる). In the first sense there is tension or anticipation, while やっと and ようやく stress relief after a long wait. いよいよとなったら means \"when it gets serious, in an emergency\". Almost always in kana.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"雨が降ったらなおさら行きたくない。","romaji":"Ame ga futtara naosara ikitakunai.","german":"If it rains, I want to go even less.","furigana":"あめ|ふ|い"},{"japanese":"普段でも混むのだから、休日はなおさらだ。","romaji":"Fudan demo komu no da kara, kyūjitsu wa naosara da.","german":"It's crowded even on ordinary days, so on holidays it's all the more so.","furigana":"ふだん|こ|きゅうじつ"},{"japanese":"理由を聞いて、彼の努力がなおさら立派に思えた。","romaji":"Riyū o kiite, kare no doryoku ga naosara rippa ni omoeta.","german":"When I heard the reason, his effort seemed all the more admirable.","furigana":"りゆう|き|かれ|どりょく|りっぱ|おも"}],"notes":"なおさら means \"all the more, even more so\": a situation that already holds applies even more strongly under a further condition. Typical patterns are 〜ならなおさら, 〜たらなおさら and 〜はなおさらだ at the end of a sentence. With a negative it corresponds to \"even less, all the less\" (なおさら行きたくない). Unlike いっそう, which describes a mere increase, なおさら presupposes a comparison with a starting case. Do not confuse it with なお \"furthermore, still\"; the kanji 尚更 is rare.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"現実は想像よりはるかに厳しかった。","romaji":"Genjitsu wa sōzō yori haruka ni kibishikatta.","german":"The reality was far harsher than I had imagined.","furigana":"げんじつ|そうぞう|きび"},{"japanese":"電車で行くほうが車よりはるかに速い。","romaji":"Densha de iku hō ga kuruma yori haruka ni hayai.","german":"Going by train is far faster than by car.","furigana":"でんしゃ|い|くるま|はや"},{"japanese":"山の上から、はるかに海が見えた。","romaji":"Yama no ue kara, haruka ni umi ga mieta.","german":"From the mountaintop, the sea could be seen far away.","furigana":"やま|うえ|うみ|み"}],"notes":"はるかに strengthens comparisons: \"by far, far more\", typical in the pattern A は B よりはるかに〜. It is stronger and somewhat more elevated than ずっと. Originally はるか means \"far away\", hence also spatially \"in the distance\" (はるかに海が見える) and temporally はるか昔 \"long ago\" (then without に). The kanji spelling 遥かに is possible, but kana predominates.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この店の方が断然おいしい。","romaji":"Kono mise no hō ga danzen oishii.","german":"This restaurant is by far tastier.","furigana":"みせ|ほう|だんぜん"},{"japanese":"彼はその計画に断然反対した。","romaji":"Kare wa sono keikaku ni danzen hantai shita.","german":"He firmly opposed the plan.","furigana":"かれ|けいかく|だんぜんはんたい"},{"japanese":"うちのチームは今季、リーグで断然トップだ。","romaji":"Uchi no chīmu wa konki, rīgu de danzen toppu da.","german":"Our team is far and away at the top of the league this season.","furigana":"こんき|だんぜん"}],"notes":"断然 has two uses: as an intensifier in comparisons, \"by far, clearly\" (こっちのほうが断然いい), very common in colloquial speech, and as an adverb of attitude, \"resolutely, categorically\" (断然反対する, 断然断る). In comparisons it resembles ずっと or はるかに but sounds more subjective and emphatic. With nouns: 断然トップ \"far ahead at the top\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"決勝では彼女が圧倒的に強かった。","romaji":"Kesshō de wa kanojo ga attōteki ni tsuyokatta.","german":"In the final, she was overwhelmingly strong.","furigana":"けっしょう|かのじょ|あっとうてき|つよ"},{"japanese":"この商品は若い女性に圧倒的に人気がある。","romaji":"Kono shōhin wa wakai josei ni attōteki ni ninki ga aru.","german":"This product is by far the most popular among young women.","furigana":"しょうひん|わか|じょせい|あっとうてき|にんき"},{"japanese":"反対意見より賛成意見のほうが圧倒的に多かった。","romaji":"Hantai iken yori sansei iken no hō ga attōteki ni ōkatta.","german":"Opinions in favor far outnumbered opinions against.","furigana":"はんたいいけん|さんせいいけん|あっとうてき|おお"}],"notes":"圧倒的に is the adverb form of the na-adjective 圧倒的 \"overwhelming\" and means \"by an overwhelming margin, clearly\". Common combinations: 圧倒的に多い, 圧倒的に強い, 圧倒的に有利だ; attributively 圧倒的な差 or 圧倒的な人気. It sounds stronger than 断然 or はるかに and is equally common in news, sports reports and colloquial speech.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"非常に残念ですが欠席します。","romaji":"Hijō ni zannen desu ga kesseki shimasu.","german":"It is very regrettable, but I will be absent.","furigana":"ひじょう|ざんねん|けっせき"},{"japanese":"今回の試験は非常に難しかったです。","romaji":"Konkai no shiken wa hijō ni muzukashikatta desu.","german":"This exam was extremely difficult.","furigana":"こんかい|しけん|ひじょう|むずか"},{"japanese":"ご協力いただき、非常に助かりました。","romaji":"Gokyōryoku itadaki, hijō ni tasukarimashita.","german":"Thank you for your cooperation; it was a great help.","furigana":"きょうりょく|ひじょう|たす"}],"notes":"非常に means \"extremely, exceedingly\" and sounds matter-of-fact to formal; it fits well in announcements, letters and reports. In casual conversation one rather uses とても or すごく. With a negation (\"not very\"), it is not 非常に but あまり〜ない that is used. 非常 alone means \"emergency\", as in 非常口 \"emergency exit\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"相当な努力が必要だ。","romaji":"Sōtō na doryoku ga hitsuyō da.","german":"Considerable effort is needed.","furigana":"そうとう|どりょく|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"彼は相当疲れているようだ。","romaji":"Kare wa sōtō tsukarete iru yō da.","german":"He seems to be quite tired.","furigana":"かれ|そうとうつか"},{"japanese":"優勝者には十万円相当の賞品が贈られる。","romaji":"Yūshōsha ni wa jūman'en sōtō no shōhin ga okurareru.","german":"The winner will receive a prize worth 100,000 yen.","furigana":"ゆうしょうしゃ|じゅうまんえんそうとう|しょうひん|おく"}],"notes":"相当 as an adverb means \"quite, considerably\" and stresses a surprisingly high degree: 相当疲れている, 相当難しい. It is stronger than かなり and often sounds evaluative. As a na-adjective: 相当な努力 \"considerable effort.\" There is also the noun and する-verb \"to correspond to\": 一万円相当の商品 \"goods worth 10,000 yen,\" 〜に相当する \"to be equivalent to ...\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"予定に若干の変更があります。","romaji":"Yotei ni jakkan no henkō ga arimasu.","german":"There are some changes to the schedule.","furigana":"よてい|じゃっかん|へんこう"},{"japanese":"この店は駅前の店より若干高い。","romaji":"Kono mise wa ekimae no mise yori jakkan takai.","german":"This shop is slightly more expensive than the one in front of the station.","furigana":"みせ|えきまえ|みせ|じゃっかんたか"},{"japanese":"アルバイトを若干名募集しています。","romaji":"Arubaito o jakkanmei boshū shite imasu.","german":"We are recruiting a few part-time workers.","furigana":"じゃっかんめいぼしゅう"}],"notes":"若干 means \"a little, some, a few\" and is rather formal and literary: 若干の変更 \"some changes\", 若干名 \"a few people\" (in job ads). As an adverb it modifies adjectives and verbs: 若干高い \"somewhat more expensive\", 若干遅れる \"to be slightly late\". It sounds more businesslike than 少し or ちょっと; in youth slang, however, 若干 is also used casually for \"a bit\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"気温がわずかに上がった。","romaji":"Kion ga wazuka ni agatta.","german":"The temperature rose slightly.","furigana":"きおん|あ"},{"japanese":"ボールはわずかにゴールを外れた。","romaji":"Bōru wa wazuka ni gōru o hazureta.","german":"The ball missed the goal by a narrow margin.","furigana":"はず"},{"japanese":"ドアがわずかに開いていて、光が漏れていた。","romaji":"Doa ga wazuka ni aite ite, hikari ga morete ita.","german":"The door was open a crack, and light was leaking out.","furigana":"あ|ひかり|も"}],"notes":"わずかに means \"slightly, narrowly, only\": it describes a very small amount or change (わずかに上がる, わずかに残る). For distances it means \"narrowly\" (わずかに届かなかった \"just missed\"). It is somewhat more literary than 少し or ちょっと and typical in news, reports and descriptions. The na-adjective わずか appears attributively: わずかな差, わずか三分 \"only three minutes\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日は比較的暖かい。","romaji":"Kyō wa hikakuteki atatakai.","german":"It's comparatively warm today.","furigana":"きょう|ひかくてきあたた"},{"japanese":"この試験は比較的簡単だと言われている。","romaji":"Kono shiken wa hikakuteki kantan da to iwarete iru.","german":"This exam is said to be relatively easy.","furigana":"しけん|ひかくてきかんたん|い"},{"japanese":"平日の昼間は比較的電車が空いている。","romaji":"Heijitsu no hiruma wa hikakuteki densha ga suite iru.","german":"On weekday daytimes the trains are relatively empty.","furigana":"へいじつ|ひるま|ひかくてきでんしゃ|す"}],"notes":"比較的 means \"comparatively, relatively\" and qualifies a statement against a tacitly assumed standard: 比較的安い, 比較的簡単だ. As an adverb it usually stands directly before adjectives or verbs without に; 比較的に is rarer. Attributively: 比較的新しい技術. It is neutral to slightly formal; colloquially people often say わりと or わりに.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この料理は意外とおいしかった。","romaji":"Kono ryōri wa igai to oishikatta.","german":"This dish was surprisingly delicious.","furigana":"りょうり|いがい"},{"japanese":"やってみたら、意外と簡単だった。","romaji":"Yatte mitara, igai to kantan datta.","german":"When I tried it, it was surprisingly easy.","furigana":"いがい|かんたん"},{"japanese":"彼は無口に見えるけど、意外と話し好きだ。","romaji":"Kare wa mukuchi ni mieru kedo, igai to hanashizuki da.","german":"He seems taciturn, but he is surprisingly talkative.","furigana":"かれ|むくち|み|いがい|はな|ず"}],"notes":"意外と means \"surprisingly, unexpectedly\" and expresses that something is different (mostly better) than thought: 意外と簡単, 意外とおいしい. 意外に is synonymous and somewhat more neutral; 意外と is especially common in colloquial speech. The na-adjective 意外 appears predicatively or attributively: 意外な結果, それは意外だった. More elevated is 思いのほか.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"思いのほか時間がかかった。","romaji":"Omoi no hoka jikan ga kakatta.","german":"It took unexpectedly long.","furigana":"おも|じかん"},{"japanese":"会議は思いのほか早く終わった。","romaji":"Kaigi wa omoi no hoka hayaku owatta.","german":"The meeting ended unexpectedly early.","furigana":"かいぎ|おも|はや|お"},{"japanese":"初めての一人旅は思いのほか楽しかった。","romaji":"Hajimete no hitoritabi wa omoi no hoka tanoshikatta.","german":"My first solo trip was more fun than I expected.","furigana":"はじ|ひとりたび|おも|たの"}],"notes":"思いのほか means \"contrary to expectations, unexpectedly\", literally \"outside of what was thought\". It is synonymous with 意外と, but more elevated and literary; in everyday conversation it sounds slightly literary. Often used with quantities and degrees: 思いのほか時間がかかる, 思いのほか早く終わる. The spelling 思いの外 is also common. Not to be confused with もってのほか \"outrageous, completely unacceptable\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"予想以上に多くの人が集まった。","romaji":"Yosō ijō ni ōku no hito ga atsumatta.","german":"More people gathered than expected.","furigana":"よそういじょう|おお|ひと|あつ"},{"japanese":"新商品が予想以上に売れて、在庫がなくなった。","romaji":"Shinshōhin ga yosō ijō ni urete, zaiko ga nakunatta.","german":"The new product sold better than expected, and the stock ran out.","furigana":"しんしょうひん|よそういじょう|う|ざいこ"},{"japanese":"引っ越しの費用は予想以上にかかった。","romaji":"Hikkoshi no hiyō wa yosō ijō ni kakatta.","german":"The moving costs were more than expected.","furigana":"ひ|こ|ひよう|よそういじょう"}],"notes":"予想以上に means \"more than expected, beyond expectations\" and is composed of 予想 \"expectation, forecast\" and 以上 \"more than\". It refers to quantities and degrees, positive as well as negative: 予想以上に売れる, 予想以上に難しい. Attributively one says 予想以上の結果, predicatively 予想以上だった. Close in meaning is 思った以上に, which sounds more colloquial; the opposite is expressed by 予想を下回る.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼はあたかも何も知らないかのように振る舞った。","romaji":"Kare wa atakamo nani mo shiranai ka no yō ni furumatta.","german":"He behaved as if he knew nothing.","furigana":"かれ|なに|し|ふ|ま"},{"japanese":"その日の空は、あたかも絵のように美しかった。","romaji":"Sono hi no sora wa, atakamo e no yō ni utsukushikatta.","german":"The sky that day was beautiful, as if painted.","furigana":"ひ|そら|え|うつく"},{"japanese":"彼は人の意見を、あたかも自分の考えのように発表した。","romaji":"Kare wa hito no iken o, atakamo jibun no kangae no yō ni happyō shita.","german":"He presented other people's opinions as if they were his own ideas.","furigana":"かれ|ひと|いけん|じぶん|かんが|はっぴょう"}],"notes":"あたかも \"as if, as though\" opens a comparison that is completed with 〜ようだ/〜ように or 〜かのようだ/〜かのように: あたかも〜かのように振る舞う. It is literary and elevated; in conversation people more often say まるで. It often implies that appearances are deceptive (deception, pretense). Almost always in kana; 恰も is rare.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いかにも彼らしい発言だ。","romaji":"Ika ni mo kare rashii hatsugen da.","german":"That's a very typical remark of his.","furigana":"かれ|はつげん"},{"japanese":"彼女はいかにも高そうなバッグを持っていた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa ika ni mo takasō na baggu o motte ita.","german":"She was carrying a bag that looked really expensive.","furigana":"かのじょ|たか|も"},{"japanese":"「これは先生の作品ですか。」「いかにも、その通りです。」","romaji":"\"Kore wa sensei no sakuhin desu ka.\" \"Ika ni mo, sono tōri desu.\"","german":"\"Is this your work?\" \"Indeed, it is.\"","furigana":"せんせい|さくひん|とお"}],"notes":"いかにも has three uses: before 〜らしい \"typical, just like\" (いかにも彼らしい), before 〜そうだ \"evidently, obviously\" (いかにも高そうな時計), and alone as formal agreement, \"indeed, quite right\". A slightly ironic or critical note often resonates (いかにも言い訳らしい). Almost always in kana; 如何にも is rare. Do not confuse it with いかに \"how (much)\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女はさも楽しそうに笑った。","romaji":"Kanojo wa samo tanoshisō ni waratta.","german":"She laughed as if she were really enjoying herself.","furigana":"かのじょ|たの|わら"},{"japanese":"彼はさも自分が考えたかのように話した。","romaji":"Kare wa samo jibun ga kangaeta ka no yō ni hanashita.","german":"He spoke as if he had thought of it himself.","furigana":"かれ|じぶん|かんが|はな"},{"japanese":"兄はさも面倒くさそうに返事をした。","romaji":"Ani wa samo mendōkusasō ni henji o shita.","german":"My older brother replied with obvious reluctance.","furigana":"あに|めんどう|へんじ"}],"notes":"さも expresses that someone clearly puts something on display: \"visibly, just as if\". It almost always combines with 〜そうに/〜そうな or 〜かのように: さも嬉しそうに, さも知っているかのように話す. Often it implies that the impression is exaggerated or feigned; unlike あたかも, the focus is on a person's manner. Rather literary and written in kana.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どうやら雨が降りそうだ。","romaji":"Dōyara ame ga furisō da.","german":"It looks like it's going to rain.","furigana":"あめ|ふ"},{"japanese":"電気が消えている。どうやら店はもう閉まったようだ。","romaji":"Denki ga kiete iru. Dōyara mise wa mō shimatta yō da.","german":"The lights are off. It seems the shop has already closed.","furigana":"でんき|き|みせ|し"},{"japanese":"走ったので、どうやら終電に間に合った。","romaji":"Hashitta node, dōyara shūden ni maniatta.","german":"Because I ran, I just barely made it to the last train.","furigana":"はし|しゅうでん|ま|あ"}],"notes":"どうやら expresses a conclusion drawn from external signs: \"apparently, it seems\". It is almost always closed with 〜ようだ, 〜らしい or colloquially 〜みたいだ; 〜そうだ is also possible. Unlike たぶん or おそらく, the supposition rests on observation, not on a mere estimate. Second meaning: \"somehow, with difficulty\" (どうやら間に合った \"just barely made it\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"おそらく彼は来ないだろう。","romaji":"Osoraku kare wa konai darō.","german":"He probably won't come.","furigana":"かれ|こ"},{"japanese":"この調子だと、工事はおそらく来月まで続くでしょう。","romaji":"Kono chōshi da to, kōji wa osoraku raigetsu made tsuzuku deshō.","german":"At this rate, the construction will probably continue until next month.","furigana":"ちょうし|こうじ|らいげつ|つづ"},{"japanese":"おそらく彼女はもう知っていると思う。","romaji":"Osoraku kanojo wa mō shitte iru to omou.","german":"I think she probably already knows.","furigana":"かのじょ|し|おも"}],"notes":"おそらく means \"probably, most likely\" and is closed with a conjecture form: 〜だろう/〜でしょう, 〜と思う or 〜かもしれない. It is somewhat more formal and sounds more convinced than たぶん; typical in news, reports and cautious assessments. In contrast to どうやら, it does not need concrete signs. The kanji 恐らく is possible, kana is usual.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は間違いなく合格するだろう。","romaji":"Kanojo wa machigai naku gōkaku suru darō.","german":"She will surely pass.","furigana":"かのじょ|まちが|ごうかく"},{"japanese":"これは間違いなく彼の字だ。","romaji":"Kore wa machigai naku kare no ji da.","german":"This is definitely his handwriting.","furigana":"まちが|かれ|じ"},{"japanese":"書類は間違いなく本人に渡してください。","romaji":"Shorui wa machigai naku honnin ni watashite kudasai.","german":"Please be sure to hand the documents to the person in question.","furigana":"しょるい|まちが|ほんにん|わた"}],"notes":"間違いなく means \"without a doubt, absolutely certainly\", literally \"without error\". As a sentence adverb it reinforces a supposition or fact (間違いなく成功する); as a manner adverb it means \"without mistakes, properly\" (間違いなく届ける). It is stronger than おそらく. Predicatively one says 〜に間違いない \"there is no doubt that ...\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"確実に届くように書留で送った。","romaji":"Kakujitsu ni todoku yō ni kakitome de okutta.","german":"I sent it by registered mail so that it will surely arrive.","furigana":"かくじつ|とど|かきとめ|おく"},{"japanese":"毎日練習しているから、確実に上手になっている。","romaji":"Mainichi renshū shite iru kara, kakujitsu ni jōzu ni natte iru.","german":"Because I practice every day, I'm steadily getting better.","furigana":"まいにちれんしゅう|かくじつ|じょうず"},{"japanese":"このままだと、締め切りに確実に遅れる。","romaji":"Kono mama da to, shimekiri ni kakujitsu ni okureru.","german":"If this continues, we will definitely miss the deadline.","furigana":"し|き|かくじつ|おく"}],"notes":"確実に is the adverb form of the na-adjective 確実 \"certain, reliable\": \"with certainty, reliably, steadily\". It describes that something happens without risk or failure (確実に届ける, 確実に合格する) or advances step by step but surely (確実に上達している). Unlike 確かに, it does not confirm a statement but qualifies the action itself. Attributively: 確実な方法.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"無論その提案に賛成です。","romaji":"Muron sono teian ni sansei desu.","german":"Of course I agree with the proposal.","furigana":"むろん|ていあん|さんせい"},{"japanese":"この仕事は、英語は無論、中国語も必要だ。","romaji":"Kono shigoto wa, eigo wa muron, chūgokugo mo hitsuyō da.","german":"This job requires not only English but also Chinese.","furigana":"しごと|えいご|むろん|ちゅうごくご|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"無論、最終的な判断は社長に任せます。","romaji":"Muron, saishūteki na handan wa shachō ni makasemasu.","german":"Of course, we leave the final decision to the president.","furigana":"むろん|さいしゅうてき|はんだん|しゃちょう|まか"}],"notes":"無論 means \"of course, naturally\" and is a literary, somewhat stiff synonym of もちろん. Common in enumerations: A は無論、B も \"not only A, but naturally also B\". In conversation it seems formal or old-fashioned; there people mostly say もちろん. The kana spelling むろん is also widespread.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"健康が大切なのは言うまでもない。","romaji":"Kenkō ga taisetsu na no wa iu made mo nai.","german":"It goes without saying that health is important.","furigana":"けんこう|たいせつ|い"},{"japanese":"言うまでもなく、安全が何よりも優先です。","romaji":"Iu made mo naku, anzen ga nani yori mo yūsen desu.","german":"Needless to say, safety takes priority above all else.","furigana":"い|あんぜん|なに|ゆうせん"},{"japanese":"この店は地元の人は言うまでもなく、観光客にも人気がある。","romaji":"Kono mise wa jimoto no hito wa iu made mo naku, kankōkyaku ni mo ninki ga aru.","german":"This shop is popular not only with locals, but also with tourists.","furigana":"みせ|じもと|ひと|い|かんこうきゃく|にんき"}],"notes":"言うまでもなく means \"of course, it goes without saying\", literally \"without needing to say it\". As a sentence adverb it introduces a generally known fact; with nouns it forms A は言うまでもなく B も \"not only A, but also B\". Predicatively 〜のは言うまでもない stands at the end of a sentence. Slightly formal, typical in speeches, essays and news; in conversation people more often say もちろん.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"もともと料理が好きだった。","romaji":"Motomoto ryōri ga suki datta.","german":"I have always liked cooking.","furigana":"りょうり|す"},{"japanese":"もともとの予定では、三日で帰るはずだった。","romaji":"Motomoto no yotei de wa, mikka de kaeru hazu datta.","german":"According to the original plan, we were supposed to be back in three days.","furigana":"よてい|みっか|かえ"},{"japanese":"だめでもともとだから、応募してみよう。","romaji":"Dame de motomoto da kara, ōbo shite miyō.","german":"I have nothing to lose, so let me just apply.","furigana":"おうぼ"}],"notes":"もともと means \"originally, from the beginning, by nature\": もともと体が弱い, もともと東京の出身だ. Attributively it takes の: もともとの計画 \"the original plan\". There is also the expression だめでもともと (short だめもと) \"you have nothing to lose\". To be distinguished from the prefix 元 \"former\" (元社員): もともと emphasizes the initial state. Both kana and 元々 are common.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 33"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この土地は元々田んぼだった。","romaji":"Kono tochi wa motomoto tanbo datta.","german":"This land was originally a rice field.","furigana":"とち|もともとた"},{"japanese":"彼は元々おとなしい性格だ。","romaji":"Kare wa motomoto otonashii seikaku da.","german":"He is quiet by nature.","furigana":"かれ|もともと|せいかく"},{"japanese":"この企画は元々、社長のアイデアだった。","romaji":"Kono kikaku wa motomoto, shachō no aidea datta.","german":"This project was originally the president's idea.","furigana":"きかく|もともと|しゃちょう"}],"notes":"元々 is the kanji spelling of もともと \"originally, from the start, by nature\"; the repetition mark 々 doubles 元. Both spellings are common. Typical are 元々は〜だった for the former state of a place or plan and 元々〜な性格 for a disposition. Not to be confused with the prefix 元 (もと) \"former\": 元首相 \"ex-prime minister\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ここにはかつて大きな城があった。","romaji":"Koko ni wa katsute ōkina shiro ga atta.","german":"There used to be a large castle here.","furigana":"おお|しろ"},{"japanese":"かつての同僚と偶然駅で会った。","romaji":"Katsute no dōryō to gūzen eki de atta.","german":"I ran into a former colleague at the station by chance.","furigana":"どうりょう|ぐうぜんえき|あ"},{"japanese":"今回の台風は、かつてないほどの被害をもたらした。","romaji":"Konkai no taifū wa, katsute nai hodo no higai o motarashita.","german":"This typhoon caused unprecedented damage.","furigana":"こんかい|たいふう|ひがい"}],"notes":"かつて means \"once, formerly\" and is more elevated than 昔 or 以前; typical in narratives, history texts and news (かつての首都 \"the former capital\"). With a negative it means \"never before\": かつてない規模 \"an unprecedented scale\", いまだかつて〜ない \"never before\". The pronunciation かって is colloquial and considered incorrect.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この件に関してご質問はありますか。","romaji":"Kono ken ni kanshite go-shitsumon wa arimasu ka.","german":"Do you have any questions regarding this matter?","furigana":"けん|かん|しつもん"},{"japanese":"環境問題に関する本を何冊か読んだ。","romaji":"Kankyō mondai ni kansuru hon o nansatsu ka yonda.","german":"I read several books about environmental problems.","furigana":"かんきょうもんだい|かん|ほん|なんさつ|よ"},{"japanese":"料金に関しては、後日メールでお知らせします。","romaji":"Ryōkin ni kanshite wa, gojitsu mēru de oshirase shimasu.","german":"As for the fees, we will inform you by email at a later date.","furigana":"りょうきん|かん|ごじつ|し"}],"notes":"〜に関して means \"regarding, concerning, about\" and names the topic at hand. It is more formal than 〜について and typical in business language, announcements and reports. Before nouns, 〜に関する (環境に関する調査) or 〜に関しての is used; with the topic marker, 〜に関しては \"as for ...\". In content it is mostly interchangeable with について, but for personal conversation topics に関して sounds stiff.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お客様に対して丁寧に対応する。","romaji":"Okyakusama ni taishite teinei ni taiō suru.","german":"We deal politely with customers.","furigana":"きゃくさま|たい|ていねい|たいおう"},{"japanese":"姉が社交的なのに対して、妹はおとなしい。","romaji":"Ane ga shakōteki na no ni taishite, imōto wa otonashii.","german":"While the older sister is sociable, the younger one is quiet.","furigana":"あね|しゃこうてき|たい|いもうと"},{"japanese":"政府の決定に対して、多くの反対意見が出た。","romaji":"Seifu no kettei ni taishite, ōku no hantai iken ga deta.","german":"Many objections were raised against the government's decision.","furigana":"せいふ|けってい|たい|おお|はんたいいけん|で"}],"notes":"〜に対して has two main meanings: \"toward, to\" for the target of an action or attitude (客に対して丁寧だ, 質問に対して答える) and \"in contrast to\" when comparing two things, after sentences with の (兄が背が高いのに対して、弟は低い). It also means \"per, for each\" in ratios (学生十人に対して先生一人). Before nouns: 〜に対する (政府に対する批判). Not to be confused with 〜について \"about (a topic)\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この分野において日本は世界をリードしている。","romaji":"Kono bun'ya ni oite Nihon wa sekai o rīdo shite iru.","german":"Japan is leading the world in this field.","furigana":"ぶんや|にほん|せかい"},{"japanese":"入学式は本校の体育館において行われます。","romaji":"Nyūgakushiki wa honkō no taiikukan ni oite okonawaremasu.","german":"The entrance ceremony will be held in our school's gymnasium.","furigana":"にゅうがくしき|ほんこう|たいいくかん|おこな"},{"japanese":"現代社会における家族の役割について考える。","romaji":"Gendai shakai ni okeru kazoku no yakuwari ni tsuite kangaeru.","german":"We think about the role of the family in modern society.","furigana":"げんだいしゃかい|かぞく|やくわり|かんが"}],"notes":"〜において is the formal counterpart of で and denotes place, time or field: \"in, at, in the field of\". Typical in announcements, official texts and technical language (会議室において, 現代社会において). Before nouns: 〜における (日本における教育). In everyday speech people simply say で; in conversation において sounds overly stiff.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"法律に基づいて判断する。","romaji":"Hōritsu ni motozuite handan suru.","german":"We judge based on the law.","furigana":"ほうりつ|もと|はんだん"},{"japanese":"アンケートの結果に基づいて、サービスを改善しました。","romaji":"Ankēto no kekka ni motozuite, sābisu o kaizen shimashita.","german":"Based on the survey results, we improved our service.","furigana":"けっか|もと|かいぜん"},{"japanese":"これはデータに基づく予測にすぎない。","romaji":"Kore wa dēta ni motozuku yosoku ni suginai.","german":"This is merely a forecast based on data.","furigana":"もと|よそく"}],"notes":"〜に基づいて means \"on the basis of, in accordance with\" and names a binding basis such as data, laws, rules or facts. Before nouns: 〜に基づく or 〜に基づいた. Unlike 〜をもとに, which denotes source material for something new, に基づいて stresses conformity with a norm or body of data. Formal; typical in reports, administration and science. It comes from the verb 基づく \"to be based on\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"実話をもとにした映画です。","romaji":"Jitsuwa o moto ni shita eiga desu.","german":"It's a movie based on a true story.","furigana":"じつわ|えいが"},{"japanese":"集めたデータをもとに、報告書を作成した。","romaji":"Atsumeta dēta o moto ni, hōkokusho o sakusei shita.","german":"I wrote a report based on the data I collected.","furigana":"あつ|ほうこくしょ|さくせい"},{"japanese":"祖母のレシピをもとにして、新しいケーキを作った。","romaji":"Sobo no reshipi o moto ni shite, atarashii kēki o tsukutta.","german":"I made a new cake based on my grandmother's recipe.","furigana":"そぼ|あたら|つく"}],"notes":"〜をもとに means \"on the basis of, starting from\" and names the material or model from which something new arises (実話をもとにした映画, 資料をもとに報告書を書く). Before nouns: 〜をもとにした; variant 〜をもとにして. In contrast to 〜に基づいて, which stresses conformity with a norm, this is about source and inspiration. The kanji 元に or 基に are possible; kana is common.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"インターネットを通じて情報を集める。","romaji":"Intānetto o tsūjite jōhō o atsumeru.","german":"I gather information through the internet.","furigana":"つう|じょうほう|あつ"},{"japanese":"この島は一年を通じて暖かい。","romaji":"Kono shima wa ichinen o tsūjite atatakai.","german":"It is warm on this island throughout the year.","furigana":"しま|いちねん|つう|あたた"},{"japanese":"共通の友人を通じて、彼女と知り合った。","romaji":"Kyōtsū no yūjin o tsūjite, kanojo to shiriatta.","german":"I got to know her through a mutual friend.","furigana":"きょうつう|ゆうじん|つう|かのじょ|し|あ"}],"notes":"〜を通じて has two meanings: \"through, via, by means of\" for a medium or intermediary (友人を通じて知り合う, ネットを通じて) and \"throughout the whole ...\" for a period of time (一年を通じて暖かい). It is somewhat more formal than the largely synonymous 〜を通して, which stresses conscious mediation more. Before nouns: 〜を通じての. It comes from the verb 通じる \"to go through, to connect\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"文化によって習慣が違う。","romaji":"Bunka ni yotte shūkan ga chigau.","german":"Customs differ depending on the culture.","furigana":"ぶんか|しゅうかん|ちが"},{"japanese":"電話はベルによって発明された。","romaji":"Denwa wa Beru ni yotte hatsumei sareta.","german":"The telephone was invented by Bell.","furigana":"でんわ|はつめい"},{"japanese":"大雨によって、多くの電車が運休した。","romaji":"Ōame ni yotte, ōku no densha ga unkyū shita.","german":"Many trains were suspended due to heavy rain.","furigana":"おおあめ|おお|でんしゃ|うんきゅう"}],"notes":"〜によって has several functions: \"depending on\" for differences (人によって考え方が違う), \"by\" for the agent in the passive (ベルによって発明された), \"by means of\" for a means, and \"due to\" for a cause (事故によって遅れる). Before nouns: 〜による (地震による被害); in writing also 〜により. In the passive with persons, everyday speech mostly uses に; によって is used for creations, discoveries and official acts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私にとってそれは大きな挑戦だった。","romaji":"Watashi ni totte sore wa ōkina chōsen datta.","german":"For me, that was a big challenge.","furigana":"わたし|おお|ちょうせん"},{"japanese":"外国人にとって、敬語は難しい。","romaji":"Gaikokujin ni totte, keigo wa muzukashii.","german":"For foreigners, honorific language is difficult.","furigana":"がいこくじん|けいご|むずか"},{"japanese":"家族にとって、この家は思い出の場所だ。","romaji":"Kazoku ni totte, kono ie wa omoide no basho da.","german":"For the family, this house is a place of memories.","furigana":"かぞく|いえ|おも|で|ばしょ"}],"notes":"〜にとって means \"for (someone), from the standpoint of\" and stands before evaluations: 私にとって大切だ, 子どもにとって難しい. The predicate is a judgment (important, difficult, a challenge), not an action. For actions in favor of others use 〜のために (家族のために働く), for attitudes toward someone 〜に対して. With topic: 〜にとっては; before nouns: 〜にとっての.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"能力に応じて給料が決まる。","romaji":"Nōryoku ni ōjite kyūryō ga kimaru.","german":"Salary is determined according to ability.","furigana":"のうりょく|おう|きゅうりょう|き"},{"japanese":"必要に応じて、資料を追加してください。","romaji":"Hitsuyō ni ōjite, shiryō o tsuika shite kudasai.","german":"Please add materials as needed.","furigana":"ひつよう|おう|しりょう|ついか"},{"japanese":"子どもの年齢に応じた本を選びましょう。","romaji":"Kodomo no nenrei ni ōjita hon o erabimashō.","german":"Let's choose books suited to the child's age.","furigana":"こ|ねんれい|おう|ほん|えら"}],"notes":"〜に応じて means \"in accordance with, depending on, according to\": something is adapted to a variable quantity (能力に応じて, 予算に応じて, 必要に応じて \"as needed\"). Unlike 〜によって, which merely states a difference, it stresses deliberate adjustment. Before nouns: 〜に応じた (年齢に応じた指導). It comes from the verb 応じる, which also means \"to comply with a request\" (依頼に応じる).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"経済成長に伴って生活水準が上がった。","romaji":"Keizai seichō ni tomonatte seikatsu suijun ga agatta.","german":"Living standards rose along with economic growth.","furigana":"けいざいせいちょう|ともな|せいかつすいじゅん|あ"},{"japanese":"店舗の移転に伴って、営業時間が変更になります。","romaji":"Tenpo no iten ni tomonatte, eigyō jikan ga henkō ni narimasu.","german":"With the relocation of the store, the business hours will change.","furigana":"てんぽ|いてん|ともな|えいぎょうじかん|へんこう"},{"japanese":"高齢化に伴う問題は年々深刻になっている。","romaji":"Kōreika ni tomonau mondai wa nennen shinkoku ni natte iru.","german":"The problems accompanying an aging society are becoming more serious year by year.","furigana":"こうれいか|ともな|もんだい|ねんねんしんこく"}],"notes":"〜に伴って means \"along with, accompanying, as a result of\": one change brings another in its wake (人口の増加に伴って, 工事に伴って). It is literary and typical of news, reports and notices. Similar is 〜につれて, but that only expresses parallel, gradual developments; に伴って can also name a one-time event as the trigger (移転に伴って). Before nouns: 〜に伴う. It comes from the verb 伴う \"to accompany, to bring with it\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"留学をきっかけに英語が好きになった。","romaji":"Ryūgaku o kikkake ni eigo ga suki ni natta.","german":"Studying abroad made me like English.","furigana":"りゅうがく|えいご|す"},{"japanese":"テレビ番組をきっかけに、その町は有名になった。","romaji":"Terebi bangumi o kikkake ni, sono machi wa yūmei ni natta.","german":"Thanks to a TV program, the town became famous.","furigana":"ばんぐみ|まち|ゆうめい"},{"japanese":"病気をきっかけにして、たばこをやめた。","romaji":"Byōki o kikkake ni shite, tabako o yameta.","german":"My illness was the trigger for quitting smoking.","furigana":"びょうき"}],"notes":"〜をきっかけに means \"triggered by, on the occasion of\": an event gives the impetus to a new development (留学をきっかけに). Variants are 〜をきっかけにして and the more colloquial 〜がきっかけで. It is neutral and very common in everyday speech; the synonymous 〜を契機に is literary and formal. The noun きっかけ on its own means \"occasion, trigger\" (話すきっかけ).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"結婚を契機に生活を見直した。","romaji":"Kekkon o keiki ni seikatsu o minaoshita.","german":"On the occasion of my marriage, I reviewed my lifestyle.","furigana":"けっこん|けいき|せいかつ|みなお"},{"japanese":"震災を契機に、防災への関心が高まった。","romaji":"Shinsai o keiki ni, bōsai e no kanshin ga takamatta.","german":"Since the earthquake disaster, interest in disaster prevention has risen.","furigana":"しんさい|けいき|ぼうさい|かんしん|たか"},{"japanese":"この合併を契機として、会社は海外に進出した。","romaji":"Kono gappei o keiki to shite, kaisha wa kaigai ni shinshutsu shita.","german":"With this merger as a starting point, the company expanded overseas.","furigana":"がっぺい|けいき|かいしゃ|かいがい|しんしゅつ"}],"notes":"〜を契機に means \"on the occasion of, as a turning point\" and is the literary counterpart of 〜をきっかけに. Typical in news, speeches and reports about social or personal turning points (震災を契機に, 就職を契機に). Variants: 〜を契機として, 〜を契機にして. In casual conversation it sounds stiff; there people say 〜がきっかけで.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"やり方次第で結果が変わる。","romaji":"Yarikata shidai de kekka ga kawaru.","german":"The result changes depending on how you do it.","furigana":"かたしだい|けっか|か"},{"japanese":"合格できるかどうかは、これからの努力次第だ。","romaji":"Gōkaku dekiru ka dō ka wa, kore kara no doryoku shidai da.","german":"Whether you can pass depends on your effort from now on.","furigana":"ごうかく|どりょくしだい"},{"japanese":"天気次第で、明日のバーベキューは中止になるかもしれない。","romaji":"Tenki shidai de, ashita no bābekyū wa chūshi ni naru kamoshirenai.","german":"Depending on the weather, tomorrow's barbecue might be canceled.","furigana":"てんきしだい|あした|ちゅうし"}],"notes":"〜次第で means \"depending on\": the result depends decisively on N (天気次第で, 努力次第で). Predicatively: 〜次第だ \"it depends on ...\" (それはあなた次第だ). It stresses more strongly than 〜によって that N is the deciding factor. Caution: after a verb stem, 次第 means \"as soon as\" (分かり次第連絡します).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"成績いかんで進学先が決まる。","romaji":"Seiseki ikan de shingakusaki ga kimaru.","german":"The school you advance to is determined by your grades.","furigana":"せいせき|しんがくさき|き"},{"japanese":"交渉の結果いかんで、計画を変更することもある。","romaji":"Kōshō no kekka ikan de, keikaku o henkō suru koto mo aru.","german":"Depending on the result of the negotiations, the plan may also be changed.","furigana":"こうしょう|けっか|けいかく|へんこう"},{"japanese":"理由のいかんを問わず、遅刻は認められません。","romaji":"Riyū no ikan o towazu, chikoku wa mitomeraremasen.","german":"Regardless of the reason, lateness is not accepted.","furigana":"りゆう|と|ちこく|みと"}],"notes":"〜いかんで means \"depending on\" and is a very formal, literary variant of 〜次第で. It stands directly after nouns or with の: 結果いかんで, 対応のいかんで. Related patterns are 〜いかんによっては \"depending on ..., possibly\" and 〜いかんにかかわらず or 〜いかんを問わず \"regardless of\". The kanji 如何 is possible, kana is usual; the pattern is often already counted as N1 level.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"年齢を問わず誰でも参加できる。","romaji":"Nenrei o towazu dare demo sanka dekiru.","german":"Anyone can participate regardless of age.","furigana":"ねんれい|と|だれ|さんか"},{"japanese":"このアプリは、国内外を問わず多くの人に使われている。","romaji":"Kono apuri wa, kokunaigai o towazu ōku no hito ni tsukawarete iru.","german":"This app is used by many people regardless of whether at home or abroad.","furigana":"こくないがい|と|おお|ひと|つか"},{"japanese":"経験の有無を問わず、応募できます。","romaji":"Keiken no umu o towazu, ōbo dekimasu.","german":"You can apply regardless of whether you have experience.","furigana":"けいけん|うむ|と|おうぼ"}],"notes":"〜を問わず means \"regardless of, irrespective of\" and often follows paired terms or categories: 年齢を問わず, 男女を問わず, 昼夜を問わず \"day and night\". It is formal and typical in job ads, invitations to tender and announcements. Similar are 〜にかかわらず and the more colloquial 〜に関係なく. It is the negative literary form of 問う \"to ask, to take into consideration\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"天候にかかわらず試合は行われる。","romaji":"Tenkō ni kakawarazu shiai wa okonawareru.","german":"The match will be held regardless of the weather.","furigana":"てんこう|しあい|おこな"},{"japanese":"年齢や経験にかかわらず、どなたでも応募できます。","romaji":"Nenrei ya keiken ni kakawarazu, donata demo ōbo dekimasu.","german":"Anyone can apply regardless of age or experience.","furigana":"ねんれい|けいけん|おうぼ"},{"japanese":"参加するかしないかにかかわらず、メールで返事をください。","romaji":"Sanka suru ka shinai ka ni kakawarazu, mēru de henji o kudasai.","german":"Please reply by email, whether or not you will participate.","furigana":"さんか|へんじ"}],"notes":"〜にかかわらず means \"regardless of, no matter whether\": the result applies equally to all cases. It follows nouns that contain a range (天候, 年齢, 性別, 経験の有無) or opposing pairs such as 行くか行かないかにかかわらず. Not to be confused with にもかかわらず \"despite, although\"! Typical for notices, invitations and formal announcements; mostly in kana, less often 関わらず or 拘らず.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"努力したにもかかわらず不合格だった。","romaji":"Doryoku shita ni mo kakawarazu fugōkaku datta.","german":"Although he made an effort, he failed.","furigana":"どりょく|ふごうかく"},{"japanese":"雨にもかかわらず、たくさんの人が集まった。","romaji":"Ame ni mo kakawarazu, takusan no hito ga atsumatta.","german":"Despite the rain, many people gathered.","furigana":"あめ|ひと|あつ"},{"japanese":"お忙しいにもかかわらず、お越しいただきありがとうございます。","romaji":"Oisogashii ni mo kakawarazu, okoshi itadaki arigatō gozaimasu.","german":"Thank you for coming despite your busy schedule.","furigana":"いそが|こ"}],"notes":"AにもかかわらずB means \"despite A / although A\": B happens contrary to expectation, often with surprise, regret or reproach. It attaches to nouns (雨にもかかわらず), and to verbs and adjectives in the plain form; with nouns and な-adjectives also 〜であるにもかかわらず. It is more formal than のに and also stands at the start of a sentence as それにもかかわらず. As a polite formula: お忙しいにもかかわらず \"although you are so busy\". Contrast: にかかわらず \"regardless of\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"予想に反して彼が優勝した。","romaji":"Yosō ni hanshite kare ga yūshō shita.","german":"Contrary to expectations, he won.","furigana":"よそう|はん|かれ|ゆうしょう"},{"japanese":"親の期待に反して、彼は大学に行かずに歌手になった。","romaji":"Oya no kitai ni hanshite, kare wa daigaku ni ikazu ni kashu ni natta.","german":"Contrary to his parents' expectations, he didn't go to university but became a singer.","furigana":"おや|きたい|はん|かれ|だいがく|い|かしゅ"},{"japanese":"規則に反する行為は禁止されています。","romaji":"Kisoku ni hansuru kōi wa kinshi sarete imasu.","german":"Acts that violate the rules are prohibited.","furigana":"きそく|はん|こうい|きんし"}],"notes":"AにはんしてB means \"contrary to A\": the result contradicts an expectation (予想, 期待, 予報) or a will (本人の意志, 親の希望). Before nouns, 〜に反する or 〜に反した is used (規則に反する行為 \"an act against the rules\"). Not to be confused with 〜に対して \"toward, in contrast to\", which merely contrasts two things. Rather written and formal; in conversation people often say 予想と違って.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"川に沿って歩いた。","romaji":"Kawa ni sotte aruita.","german":"I walked along the river.","furigana":"かわ|そ|ある"},{"japanese":"会社の方針に沿って、計画を見直しました。","romaji":"Kaisha no hōshin ni sotte, keikaku o minaoshimashita.","german":"We reviewed the plan in line with the company's policy.","furigana":"かいしゃ|ほうしん|そ|けいかく|みなお"},{"japanese":"マニュアルに沿った対応をしてください。","romaji":"Manyuaru ni sotta taiō o shite kudasai.","german":"Please respond in accordance with the manual.","furigana":"そ|たいおう"}],"notes":"〜に沿って has two uses: spatially \"along\" (川に沿って, 線路に沿って) and abstractly \"in line with, in accordance with\" a guideline (方針に沿って, 計画に沿って, ご希望に沿って). Before nouns, 〜に沿った is used (マニュアルに沿った対応). Similar are 〜に基づいて \"on the basis of\" and 〜に従って \"following\", but に沿って stresses following a line or direction. The base verb is 沿う; not to be confused with 添う \"to accompany\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"開会に先立って挨拶があった。","romaji":"Kaikai ni sakidatte aisatsu ga atta.","german":"There was a greeting before the opening.","furigana":"かいかい|さきだ|あいさつ"},{"japanese":"新製品の発売に先立って、記者会見が開かれた。","romaji":"Shinseihin no hatsubai ni sakidatte, kisha kaiken ga hirakareta.","german":"A press conference was held ahead of the new product's release.","furigana":"しんせいひん|はつばい|さきだ|きしゃかいけん|ひら"},{"japanese":"工事を始めるに先立って、近所の住民に説明会を行います。","romaji":"Kōji o hajimeru ni sakidatte, kinjo no jūmin ni setsumeikai o okonaimasu.","german":"Before construction begins, we will hold an information session for local residents.","furigana":"こうじ|はじ|さきだ|きんじょ|じゅうみん|せつめいかい|おこな"}],"notes":"〜に先立って means \"prior to, in the run-up to\" an important, mostly official event and introduces a preparatory action. It follows event nouns (開会, 試合, 発売, 工事) or verbs in dictionary form (始めるに先立って). Before nouns: 〜に先立つ (試合に先立つ記者会見). Formal; in everyday speech people simply say 〜の前に. The verb 先立つ on its own means \"to precede\", also \"to die before someone\" (親に先立つ).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"とにかくやってみよう。","romaji":"Tonikaku yatte miyō.","german":"Let's just try it."},{"japanese":"遅れてもいいから、とにかく来てください。","romaji":"Okurete mo ii kara, tonikaku kite kudasai.","german":"It's fine if you're late, but please come in any case.","furigana":"おく|き"},{"japanese":"今日はとにかく暑くて、何もする気にならない。","romaji":"Kyō wa tonikaku atsukute, nani mo suru ki ni naranai.","german":"Today it's just so hot that I don't feel like doing anything.","furigana":"きょう|あつ|なに|き"}],"notes":"とにかく sets details and concerns aside and turns attention to the essentials: \"anyway, in any case, just\" (とにかくやってみよう, とにかく来てください). In colloquial speech it also intensifies: とにかく暑い \"just plain hot\". With は, 〜はとにかく means \"setting aside\", for which 〜はともかく is more usual, however. Almost always in kana; the spelling 兎に角 is rare. Very common in conversation, somewhat more casual than ともかく.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 21"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"理由はともかく結果が大事だ。","romaji":"Riyū wa tomokaku kekka ga daiji da.","german":"Setting the reasons aside, the result is what matters.","furigana":"りゆう|けっか|だいじ"},{"japanese":"子どもならともかく、大人がそんなことをするなんて信じられない。","romaji":"Kodomo nara tomokaku, otona ga sonna koto o suru nante shinjirarenai.","german":"It would be one thing for a child, but I can't believe an adult would do such a thing.","furigana":"こ|おとな|しん"},{"japanese":"間に合うかどうか分からないが、ともかく出発しよう。","romaji":"Ma ni au ka dō ka wakaranai ga, tomokaku shuppatsu shiyō.","german":"I don't know whether we'll make it in time, but let's set out anyway.","furigana":"ま|あ|わ|しゅっぱつ"}],"notes":"On its own, ともかく means \"anyway, be that as it may\" (similar to とにかく, somewhat more formal). The most important pattern, however, is AはともかくB \"setting A aside, apart from A\": 味はともかく、値段は安い. Also 〜ならともかく \"it would be one thing if ..., but ...\". Extended also as 〜はともかくとして. Mostly written in kana.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いずれにしても早めに連絡してください。","romaji":"Izure ni shite mo hayame ni renraku shite kudasai.","german":"In any case, please contact us early.","furigana":"はや|れんらく"},{"japanese":"電車でもバスでも、いずれにしても一時間はかかります。","romaji":"Densha demo basu demo, izure ni shite mo ichijikan wa kakarimasu.","german":"Whether by train or bus, either way it takes an hour.","furigana":"でんしゃ|いちじかん"},{"japanese":"原因はまだ分かりませんが、いずれにしても調査が必要です。","romaji":"Gen'in wa mada wakarimasen ga, izure ni shite mo chōsa ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"The cause is still unknown, but in any case an investigation is necessary.","furigana":"げんいん|わ|ちょうさ|ひつよう"}],"notes":"いずれにしても \"in any case, either way\" draws, after several possibilities have been mentioned, the conclusion that holds in every case. Neutral to polite, frequent in conversation and emails; more formal are いずれにせよ and いずれにしろ, more casual どっちにしても. Not to be confused with いずれ on its own \"someday, soon\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いずれにせよ準備は必要だ。","romaji":"Izure ni seyo junbi wa hitsuyō da.","german":"In any case, preparation is necessary.","furigana":"じゅんび|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"延期になるかもしれませんが、いずれにせよ、決まり次第ご連絡いたします。","romaji":"Enki ni naru kamo shiremasen ga, izure ni seyo, kimari shidai gorenraku itashimasu.","german":"It may be postponed, but in any case we will contact you as soon as it is decided.","furigana":"えんき|き|しだい|れんらく"},{"japanese":"事故の原因は調査中だが、いずれにせよ安全対策の見直しは避けられない。","romaji":"Jiko no gen'in wa chōsachū da ga, izure ni seyo anzen taisaku no minaoshi wa sakerarenai.","german":"The cause of the accident is under investigation, but in any case a review of safety measures is unavoidable.","furigana":"じこ|げんいん|ちょうさちゅう|あんぜんたいさく|みなお|さ"}],"notes":"いずれにせよ means the same as いずれにしても \"in any case, either way\". せよ is the literary imperative of する, so the expression sounds more formal and appears mainly in reports, news, speeches and business emails; いずれにしろ lies in between. After open possibilities, it sums up what holds regardless.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どうせ行くなら楽しもう。","romaji":"Dōse iku nara tanoshimō.","german":"If we're going anyway, let's enjoy ourselves.","furigana":"い|たの"},{"japanese":"どうせ今から行っても間に合わないよ。","romaji":"Dōse ima kara itte mo ma ni awanai yo.","german":"Even if we go now, we won't make it in time anyway.","furigana":"いま|い|ま|あ"},{"japanese":"どうせ私の話なんて誰も聞いてくれない。","romaji":"Dōse watashi no hanashi nante dare mo kiite kurenai.","german":"Nobody listens to what I say anyway.","furigana":"わたし|はなし|だれ|き"}],"notes":"どうせ has two nuances: resigned \"anyway, regardless (nothing will change)\" - どうせ無理だ, どうせ私なんか - and \"since ... anyway, might as well ...\" with 〜なら or 〜のだから: どうせ行くなら楽しもう. The tone is colloquial and often pessimistic or defiant, unlike the neutral いずれにしても. Always in kana.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"所詮人間は完璧ではない。","romaji":"Shosen ningen wa kanpeki de wa nai.","german":"After all, human beings are not perfect.","furigana":"しょせんにんげん|かんぺき"},{"japanese":"所詮、ゲームはゲームだ。本気になる必要はない。","romaji":"Shosen, gēmu wa gēmu da. Honki ni naru hitsuyō wa nai.","german":"A game is only a game after all. There's no need to take it seriously.","furigana":"しょせん|ほんき|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"素人の私には所詮無理な話だった。","romaji":"Shirōto no watashi ni wa shosen muri na hanashi datta.","german":"For an amateur like me, it was impossible from the start.","furigana":"しろうと|わたし|しょせんむり|はなし"}],"notes":"所詮 stresses that something is limited by its nature: \"after all, ultimately just\". Typical with 〜にすぎない, 〜でしかない, AはAだ or a negation (所詮無理だ). The tone is resigned or disparaging, stronger and more literary than どうせ; the neutral 結局, by contrast, only describes the outcome. Also in kana しょせん.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"結局のところ自分で決めるしかない。","romaji":"Kekkyoku no tokoro jibun de kimeru shika nai.","german":"In the end, you have no choice but to decide for yourself.","furigana":"けっきょく|じぶん|き"},{"japanese":"いろいろ議論したが、結局のところ予算の問題だ。","romaji":"Iroiro giron shita ga, kekkyoku no tokoro yosan no mondai da.","german":"We discussed a lot, but ultimately it's a question of budget.","furigana":"ぎろん|けっきょく|よさん|もんだい"},{"japanese":"結局のところ、彼が何を考えているのか誰にも分からなかった。","romaji":"Kekkyoku no tokoro, kare ga nani o kangaete iru no ka dare ni mo wakaranakatta.","german":"In the end, nobody knew what he was thinking.","furigana":"けっきょく|かれ|なに|かんが|だれ|わ"}],"notes":"結局のところ \"in the end, bottom line\" sums up the essentials after deliberation or discussion, often with 〜ということだ or 〜しかない. 結局 on its own rather describes how something turned out (結局行かなかった); 結局のところ sounds more summarizing and judgmental. Neutral to formal, also in essays; unlike 所詮, without a resigned undertone.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"例えば東京タワーなどが有名です。","romaji":"Tatoeba Tōkyō Tawā nado ga yūmei desu.","german":"For example, Tokyo Tower is famous.","furigana":"たと|とうきょう|ゆうめい"},{"japanese":"スポーツなら、例えばテニスやサッカーが好きです。","romaji":"Supōtsu nara, tatoeba tenisu ya sakkā ga suki desu.","german":"As for sports, I like, for example, tennis and soccer.","furigana":"たと|す"},{"japanese":"例えば明日雨が降ったら、どうしますか。","romaji":"Tatoeba ashita ame ga futtara, dō shimasu ka.","german":"If, for example, it rains tomorrow, what will you do?","furigana":"たと|あしたあめ|ふ"}],"notes":"例えば introduces an example and is often combined with 〜など or, colloquially, 〜とか: 例えばテニスやサッカーなど. With 〜たら or 〜ば it means \"suppose, let's say\": 例えば雨が降ったら…. It is also often written in kana as たとえば; the noun 例 means \"example\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 27"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"具体的には何をすればいいですか。","romaji":"Gutaiteki ni wa nani o sureba ii desu ka.","german":"What exactly should I do?","furigana":"ぐたいてき|なに"},{"japanese":"業務の効率化を進めます。具体的には、会議の時間を半分にします。","romaji":"Gyōmu no kōritsuka o susumemasu. Gutaiteki ni wa, kaigi no jikan o hanbun ni shimasu.","german":"We will make operations more efficient. Specifically, we will halve the length of meetings.","furigana":"ぎょうむ|こうりつか|すす|ぐたいてき|かいぎ|じかん|はんぶん"},{"japanese":"前の仕事では、具体的には新商品の企画を担当していました。","romaji":"Mae no shigoto de wa, gutaiteki ni wa shinshōhin no kikaku o tantō shite imashita.","german":"In my previous job, I was specifically in charge of planning new products.","furigana":"まえ|しごと|ぐたいてき|しんしょうひん|きかく|たんとう"}],"notes":"具体的には consists of the な-adjective 具体的 \"concrete\" + に + は and, after a general statement, introduces the specification: \"specifically, in detail\". Very frequent in presentations, reports and job interviews and as a follow-up question (具体的にはどういうことですか). Distinction: 例えば gives an example, つまり rephrases. Opposite: 抽象的.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"便利な一方で危険もある。","romaji":"Benri na ippō de kiken mo aru.","german":"It is convenient, but on the other hand there are also dangers.","furigana":"べんり|いっぽう|きけん"},{"japanese":"彼は仕事に厳しい一方で、部下には優しい。","romaji":"Kare wa shigoto ni kibishii ippō de, buka ni wa yasashii.","german":"He is strict at work, but kind to his subordinates.","furigana":"かれ|しごと|きび|いっぽう|ぶか|やさ"},{"japanese":"都市の人口は増えている。その一方で、地方では人口が減り続けている。","romaji":"Toshi no jinkō wa fuete iru. Sono ippō de, chihō de wa jinkō ga heri tsuzukete iru.","german":"The urban population is increasing. On the other hand, the population in rural areas keeps declining.","furigana":"とし|じんこう|ふ|いっぽう|ちほう|じんこう|へ|つづ"}],"notes":"一方で connects two opposing sides: after a clause \"while, on the one hand ... on the other\" (便利な一方で危険もある), at the beginning of a sentence mostly as その一方で \"on the other hand\". Connection: verbs and い-adjectives in plain form, な-adjectives with な, nouns with である. Not to be confused with 〜一方だ \"keeps getting more\" (増える一方だ). More formal is 他方.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"都市は便利だ。他方、自然が少ない。","romaji":"Toshi wa benri da. Tahō, shizen ga sukunai.","german":"The city is convenient. On the other hand, it has little nature.","furigana":"とし|べんり|たほう|しぜん|すく"},{"japanese":"一方は賛成し、他方は反対した。","romaji":"Ippō wa sansei shi, tahō wa hantai shita.","german":"One side agreed, and the other opposed.","furigana":"いっぽう|さんせい|たほう|はんたい"},{"japanese":"新制度は企業の負担を減らす。他方で、利用者にとっては手続きが複雑になる。","romaji":"Shinseido wa kigyō no futan o herasu. Tahō de, riyōsha ni totte wa tetsuzuki ga fukuzatsu ni naru.","german":"The new system reduces the burden on companies. On the other hand, procedures become more complicated for users.","furigana":"しんせいど|きぎょう|ふたん|へ|たほう|りようしゃ|てつづ|ふくざつ"}],"notes":"他方 is, as a noun, \"the other side, the other party\", often in the pair 一方は…、他方は…, and as a conjunction \"on the other hand\" (also 他方で, 他方では). Very formal and literary - typical of essays, reports and news. In conversation one says 一方で or その一方で.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"兄は静かだ。それに対して弟はにぎやかだ。","romaji":"Ani wa shizuka da. Sore ni taishite otōto wa nigiyaka da.","german":"Der ältere Bruder ist ruhig. Demgegenüber ist der jüngere lebhaft.","furigana":"あに|しず|たい|おとうと"},{"japanese":"日本では車は左側を走る。それに対して、ドイツでは右側を走る。","romaji":"Nihon de wa kuruma wa hidarigawa o hashiru. Sore ni taishite, Doitsu de wa migigawa o hashiru.","german":"In Japan fahren Autos links. In Deutschland dagegen fährt man rechts.","furigana":"にほん|くるま|ひだりがわ|はし|たい|みぎがわ|はし"},{"japanese":"部長の提案に多くの社員が反対した。それに対して部長は丁寧に説明した。","romaji":"Buchō no teian ni ōku no shain ga hantai shita. Sore ni taishite buchō wa teinei ni setsumei shita.","german":"Viele Mitarbeiter lehnten den Vorschlag des Abteilungsleiters ab. Daraufhin erklärte er ihn ausführlich.","furigana":"ぶちょう|ていあん|おお|しゃいん|はんたい|たい|ぶちょう|ていねい|せつめい"}],"notes":"それに対して „demgegenüber, im Gegensatz dazu“ stellt am Satzanfang zwei Dinge kontrastierend gegenüber, ohne sie nach Grad zu vergleichen (dafür それに比べて). Wörtlich auch „darauf, als Reaktion darauf“: それに対して部長は説明した. 対して kommt von 対する „gegenüberstehen“. Neutral bis formell, häufig in Berichten und Aufsätzen.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"去年は暑かった。それに比べて今年は涼しい。","romaji":"Kyonen wa atsukatta. Sore ni kurabete kotoshi wa suzushii.","german":"Last year was hot. Compared to that, this year is cool.","furigana":"きょねん|あつ|くら|ことし|すず"},{"japanese":"兄は何でもできる。それに比べて、僕は失敗ばかりだ。","romaji":"Ani wa nan demo dekiru. Sore ni kurabete, boku wa shippai bakari da.","german":"My older brother can do everything. Compared to him, I just keep failing.","furigana":"あに|なん|くら|ぼく|しっぱい"},{"japanese":"東京の家賃は高い。それに比べて地方は安いが、仕事が少ない。","romaji":"Tōkyō no yachin wa takai. Sore ni kurabete chihō wa yasui ga, shigoto ga sukunai.","german":"Rents in Tokyo are high. By comparison, they are cheap in the countryside, but there are few jobs.","furigana":"とうきょう|やちん|たか|くら|ちほう|やす|しごと|すく"}],"notes":"それに比べて \"compared to that\" compares two things in degree or quality (more/less, better/worse), often with evaluation or self-criticism: それに比べて私は.... それに対して, by contrast, stresses pure opposition. In general also 〜に比べて or 〜と比べて after a noun. Neutral, in conversation and text.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"それにしても今日は暑いですね。","romaji":"Sore ni shite mo kyō wa atsui desu ne.","german":"Still, it really is hot today, isn't it?","furigana":"きょう|あつ"},{"japanese":"遅れるのは仕方ない。それにしても、連絡ぐらいはしてほしかった。","romaji":"Okureru no wa shikata nai. Sore ni shite mo, renraku gurai wa shite hoshikatta.","german":"It can't be helped that you're late. Even so, I wish you'd at least have contacted me.","furigana":"おく|しかた|れんらく"},{"japanese":"それにしても、彼女はどうして急に会社を辞めたんだろう。","romaji":"Sore ni shite mo, kanojo wa dōshite kyū ni kaisha o yameta n darō.","german":"Even so, why did she suddenly quit the company?","furigana":"かのじょ|きゅう|かいしゃ|や"}],"notes":"それにしても acknowledges what came before and then qualifies it: \"even so, even considering that\" (遅れるのは仕方ない。それにしても...). In conversation it also introduces a spontaneous impression or astonishment: \"but really, wow ...\" - それにしても暑いですね. Mainly spoken; related to 〜にしても \"even if\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"少しも疲れなかった。それどころか、前より元気になった。","romaji":"Sukoshi mo tsukarenakatta. Sore dokoro ka, mae yori genki ni natta.","german":"I wasn't tired at all. On the contrary, I felt more energetic than before.","furigana":"すこ|つか|まえ|げんき"},{"japanese":"薬を飲んでも熱は下がらなかった。それどころか、ますます上がってしまった。","romaji":"Kusuri o nonde mo netsu wa sagaranakatta. Sore dokoro ka, masumasu agatte shimatta.","german":"Even after taking the medicine, the fever didn't go down. On the contrary, it went up even more.","furigana":"くすり|の|ねつ|さ|あ"},{"japanese":"彼は謝らなかった。それどころか、私のせいだと言い出した。","romaji":"Kare wa ayamaranakatta. Sore dokoro ka, watashi no sei da to iidashita.","german":"He didn't apologize. On the contrary, he started saying it was my fault.","furigana":"かれ|あやま|わたし|い|だ"}],"notes":"それどころか \"on the contrary, even\" mostly follows a negated statement and sets against it something that reverses the expectation or far exceeds it: Aない。それどころかB. Related is 〜どころか \"far from ...\" within a sentence. Distinction: そればかりか adds something in the same direction (\"not only that, but also\"). Neutral, spoken and written.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"それはさておき、本題に入りましょう。","romaji":"Sore wa sateoki, hondai ni hairimashō.","german":"Setting that aside, let's get to the main topic.","furigana":"ほんだい|はい"},{"japanese":"昨日の試合は惜しかったね。それはさておき、来週の旅行の件だけど。","romaji":"Kinō no shiai wa oshikatta ne. Sore wa sateoki, raishū no ryokō no ken da kedo.","german":"Yesterday's game was a close one, wasn't it? Anyway, about next week's trip ...","furigana":"きのう|しあい|お|らいしゅう|りょこう|けん"},{"japanese":"冗談はさておき、真面目な話をしよう。","romaji":"Jōdan wa sateoki, majime na hanashi o shiyō.","german":"Joking aside, let's have a serious talk.","furigana":"じょうだん|まじめ|はなし"}],"notes":"それはさておき \"setting that aside, be that as it may\" ends a digression and leads back to the actual topic or deliberately changes the subject. さておく means \"to leave aside\"; as a pattern, 〜はさておき \"setting ... aside\" (冗談はさておき). Slightly formal; in casual conversation people more often say それはそうと or ところで. Unlike ちなみに, it does not add extra information.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ちなみに、私の趣味は読書です。","romaji":"Chinamini, watashi no shumi wa dokusho desu.","german":"Incidentally, my hobby is reading.","furigana":"わたし|しゅみ|どくしょ"},{"japanese":"会議は三時からです。ちなみに、会場は二階です。","romaji":"Kaigi wa sanji kara desu. Chinami ni, kaijō wa nikai desu.","german":"The meeting starts at three. By the way, the venue is on the second floor.","furigana":"かいぎ|さんじ|かいじょう|にかい"},{"japanese":"このケーキ、おいしいでしょう。ちなみに、駅前の店で買いました。","romaji":"Kono kēki, oishii deshō. Chinamini, ekimae no mise de kaimashita.","german":"This cake is delicious, isn't it? Incidentally, I bought it at the shop in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|みせ|か"}],"notes":"ちなみに \"incidentally, by the way\" attaches additional information that fits thematically with what came before. ところで, by contrast, switches to a new topic, それはさておき returns to the main topic. Frequent in conversations, presentations and blogs; in official announcements なお is more common. Mostly in kana; the spelling 因みに is rare.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"なお、詳細は後日お知らせします。","romaji":"Nao, shōsai wa gojitsu oshirase shimasu.","german":"Furthermore, details will be announced at a later date.","furigana":"しょうさい|ごじつ|し"},{"japanese":"今もなお、多くの人がその事故を覚えている。","romaji":"Ima mo nao, ōku no hito ga sono jiko o oboete iru.","german":"Even now, many people remember that accident.","furigana":"いま|おお|ひと|じこ|おぼ"},{"japanese":"駅から近ければ、なお良いです。","romaji":"Eki kara chikakereba, nao ii desu.","german":"If it's close to the station, even better.","furigana":"えき|ちか|い"}],"notes":"なお has two main uses: at the beginning of a sentence in announcements and letters it introduces a supplementary note: \"Also/Note: ...\" (more formal than ちなみに). As an adverb it means \"still, even more\": 今もなお \"even today\", なお一層 \"even more\", それならなお良い \"even better\". Mostly in kana; 尚 is rare.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"参加は自由です。ただし、事前に申し込みが必要です。","romaji":"Sanka wa jiyū desu. Tadashi, jizen ni mōshikomi ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"Participation is voluntary. However, prior registration is required.","furigana":"さんか|じゆう|じぜん|もう|こ|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"写真は撮れます。ただし、フラッシュは使わないでください。","romaji":"Shashin wa toremasu. Tadashi, furasshu wa tsukawanaide kudasai.","german":"You may take photos. However, please do not use a flash.","furigana":"しゃしん|と|つか"},{"japanese":"入場料は大人千円です。ただし、小学生以下は無料です。","romaji":"Nyūjōryō wa otona sen'en desu. Tadashi, shōgakusei ika wa muryō desu.","german":"Admission is 1,000 yen for adults. However, elementary school children and younger are free.","furigana":"にゅうじょうりょう|おとなせんえん|しょうがくせいいか|むりょう"}],"notes":"ただし introduces a restriction, condition or exception to a statement just made and usually stands at the beginning of a sentence: 〜。ただし、〜. Unlike しかし, which marks a general contrast, ただし does not cancel the previous statement but supplements it with a proviso. Typical for rules, price information and notices; in contracts also 但し, otherwise mostly kana. In conversation people more often say ただ or でも.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は優秀だ。もっとも、欠点もある。","romaji":"Kare wa yūshū da. Mottomo, ketten mo aru.","german":"He is excellent. Admittedly, he has flaws too.","furigana":"かれ|ゆうしゅう|けってん"},{"japanese":"あんなことを言われたら、彼が怒るのももっともだ。","romaji":"Anna koto o iwaretara, kare ga okoru no mo mottomo da.","german":"If I were told something like that, it's only natural that he got angry.","furigana":"い|かれ|おこ"},{"japanese":"お客様のおっしゃることはごもっともです。","romaji":"Okyakusama no ossharu koto wa gomottomo desu.","german":"What you say is perfectly reasonable.","furigana":"きゃくさま"}],"notes":"As a conjunction at the beginning of a sentence, もっとも qualifies what was said before: \"however, admittedly\" (… もっとも、欠点もある). As a na-adjective it means \"justified, understandable, reasonable\": もっともな意見, 怒るのももっともだ \"no wonder he's angry\"; to agree politely, people say ごもっともです. Not to be confused with the identically read 最も \"most\" (最も高い山). Mostly written in kana; the kanji spelling 尤も is rare.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"計画は良い。しかしながら実行は難しい。","romaji":"Keikaku wa yoi. Shikashi nagara jikkō wa muzukashii.","german":"The plan is good. However, carrying it out is difficult.","furigana":"けいかく|よ|じっこう|むずか"},{"japanese":"ご提案には大変感謝しております。しかしながら、今回は見送らせていただきます。","romaji":"Goteian ni wa taihen kansha shite orimasu. Shikashi nagara, konkai wa miokurasete itadakimasu.","german":"We are very grateful for your proposal. However, we will pass on it this time.","furigana":"ていあん|たいへんかんしゃ|こんかい|みおく"},{"japanese":"経済は回復しつつある。しかしながら、賃金はほとんど上がっていない。","romaji":"Keizai wa kaifuku shitsutsu aru. Shikashi nagara, chingin wa hotondo agatte inai.","german":"The economy is recovering. However, wages have hardly risen.","furigana":"けいざい|かいふく|ちんぎん|あ"}],"notes":"しかしながら is the formal, literary variant of しかし \"however, nevertheless\". It appears in speeches, reports, essays and business letters, especially before refusals or qualifications; in everyday speech it seems stiff - there people say でも or けど. ところが additionally expresses an unexpected turn.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"忙しいとはいえ健康管理は大切だ。","romaji":"Isogashii to wa ie kenkō kanri wa taisetsu da.","german":"Even though I'm busy, health care is important.","furigana":"いそが|けんこうかんり|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"春とはいえ、朝晩はまだ寒い。","romaji":"Haru to wa ie, asaban wa mada samui.","german":"Although it's spring, mornings and evenings are still cold.","furigana":"はる|あさばん|さむ"},{"japanese":"試験に合格した。とはいえ、まだ安心はできない。","romaji":"Shiken ni gōkaku shita. To wa ie, mada anshin wa dekinai.","german":"I passed the exam. That said, I still can't relax.","furigana":"しけん|ごうかく|あんしん"}],"notes":"AとはいえB \"A, but nevertheless B\" acknowledges A and qualifies it: what one would expect from A does not quite apply. Connects to nouns and plain forms (春とはいえ, 忙しいとはいえ); at the beginning of a sentence as とはいえ、... \"however\". More written; in conversation people say とは言っても or そうは言っても. Equivalent to とはいうものの.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"簡単だとはいうものの時間はかかる。","romaji":"Kantan da to wa iu mono no jikan wa kakaru.","german":"Although it's said to be easy, it takes time.","furigana":"かんたん|じかん"},{"japanese":"ダイエットを始めたとはいうものの、甘い物はなかなかやめられない。","romaji":"Daietto o hajimeta to wa iu mono no, amai mono wa nakanaka yamerarenai.","german":"Although I've started a diet, I just can't give up sweets.","furigana":"はじ|あま|もの"},{"japanese":"来月から留学する。とはいうものの、まだ住む所も決まっていない。","romaji":"Raigetsu kara ryūgaku suru. To wa iu mono no, mada sumu tokoro mo kimatte inai.","german":"I'm going to study abroad from next month. That said, I haven't even decided where to live yet.","furigana":"らいげつ|りゅうがく|す|ところ|き"}],"notes":"A(と)はいうもののB \"A, but ...\": one concedes A, but reality looks different. ものの means \"although\". At the beginning of a sentence also とはいうものの、... or そうはいうものの \"however\". Same in content as とはいえ, somewhat more literary. It does not mean \"although it is said\" - it is about one's own concession.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は忙しい。だからといって、約束を破っていいわけではない。","romaji":"Kare wa isogashii. Dakara to itte, yakusoku o yabutte ii wake de wa nai.","german":"He is busy. That doesn't mean he's allowed to break his promise.","furigana":"かれ|いそが|やくそく|やぶ"},{"japanese":"テストの点が悪かった。だからといって、諦める必要はない。","romaji":"Tesuto no ten ga warukatta. Dakara to itte, akirameru hitsuyō wa nai.","german":"My test score was bad. That doesn't mean you need to give up.","furigana":"てん|わる|あきら|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"この店は有名だ。だからといって、おいしいとは限らない。","romaji":"Kono mise wa yūmei da. Dakara to itte, oishii to wa kagiranai.","german":"This shop is famous. That doesn't necessarily mean the food is good.","furigana":"みせ|ゆうめい|かぎ"}],"notes":"だからといって stands at the beginning of a sentence, takes up the previous sentence as a reason and rejects an obvious conclusion: \"just because ..., that doesn't mean ...\". Typical sentence endings are 〜わけではない, 〜とは限らない, 〜必要はない or 〜てはいけない. Attached directly to a clause, the form is 〜からといって (高いからといって...); colloquially shortened to だからって.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"行きたくない。そうかといって断れない。","romaji":"Ikitakunai. Sō ka to itte kotowarenai.","german":"I don't want to go. But I can't refuse either.","furigana":"い|ことわ"},{"japanese":"今の仕事は好きではない。そうかといって、すぐに辞めるわけにもいかない。","romaji":"Ima no shigoto wa suki de wa nai. Sō ka to itte, sugu ni yameru wake ni mo ikanai.","german":"I don't like my current job. But I can't just quit right away either.","furigana":"いま|しごと|す|や"},{"japanese":"甘いものは控えている。そうかといって、全く食べないのもつらい。","romaji":"Amai mono wa hikaete iru. Sō ka to itte, mattaku tabenai no mo tsurai.","german":"I'm cutting back on sweets. But not eating any at all is hard too.","furigana":"あま|ひか|まった|た"}],"notes":"そうかといって \"but on the other hand, not that either\" shows that the obvious opposite reaction to A is likewise not possible or not good. A negation or 〜わけにもいかない usually follows. More common and shorter is かといって; the kanji spelling そうかと言って also occurs. Neutral, in conversation and text, fond of dilemmas.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は英語が話せる。そればかりかフランス語もできる。","romaji":"Kare wa eigo ga hanaseru. Sore bakari ka furansugo mo dekiru.","german":"He can speak English. Not only that, he can also speak French.","furigana":"かれ|えいご|はな|ご"},{"japanese":"彼は約束の時間に遅れた。そればかりか、謝りもしなかった。","romaji":"Kare wa yakusoku no jikan ni okureta. Sore bakari ka, ayamari mo shinakatta.","german":"He was late for the appointment. Not only that, he didn't even apologize.","furigana":"かれ|やくそく|じかん|おく|あやま"},{"japanese":"この薬は効果がない。そればかりか、副作用もある。","romaji":"Kono kusuri wa kōka ga nai. Sore bakari ka, fukusayō mo aru.","german":"This medicine has no effect. What's more, it also has side effects.","furigana":"くすり|こうか|ふくさよう"}],"notes":"そればかりか \"not only that, but even; furthermore\" adds a further, often even stronger point in the same direction. The basic pattern is AばかりかBも \"not only A, but also B\". Distinction: それどころか reverses the expectation (\"on the contrary\"). More written; in conversation people say それに or しかも.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は言わばチームの柱だ。","romaji":"Kare wa iwaba chīmu no hashira da.","german":"He is, so to speak, the pillar of the team.","furigana":"かれ|い|はしら"},{"japanese":"祖母にとって、この庭は言わば人生そのものだ。","romaji":"Sobo ni totte, kono niwa wa iwaba jinsei sono mono da.","german":"For my grandmother, this garden is, so to speak, life itself.","furigana":"そぼ|にわ|い|じんせい"},{"japanese":"インターネットは、いわば現代の図書館のようなものだ。","romaji":"Intānetto wa, iwaba gendai no toshokan no yō na mono da.","german":"The internet is, so to speak, something like a modern library.","furigana":"げんだい|としょかん"}],"notes":"言わば \"so to speak, as it were\" introduces a figurative paraphrase or metaphor, often with 〜のようなものだ: 言わば第二の家だ. It comes from 言う plus the conditional ば (\"if one were to say it\") and is mostly written in kana as いわば. Not to be confused with いわゆる \"so-called\" (for established terms); つまり summarizes, and 例えば gives an example.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"電車がなかったので仕方なくタクシーに乗った。","romaji":"Densha ga nakatta node shikata naku takushī ni notta.","german":"There was no train, so I had no choice but to take a taxi.","furigana":"でんしゃ|しかた|の"},{"japanese":"雨で試合が中止になり、仕方なく家でテレビを見た。","romaji":"Ame de shiai ga chūshi ni nari, shikata naku ie de terebi o mita.","german":"The game was canceled due to rain, so I reluctantly watched TV at home.","furigana":"あめ|しあい|ちゅうし|しかた|いえ|み"},{"japanese":"誰もやりたがらないので、仕方なく私がパーティーの準備を引き受けた。","romaji":"Dare mo yaritagaranai node, shikata naku watashi ga pātī no junbi o hikiuketa.","german":"Since no one wanted to do it, I reluctantly took on the party preparations.","furigana":"だれ|しかた|わたし|じゅんび|ひ|う"}],"notes":"仕方なく is the adverbial form of 仕方ない and means \"reluctantly, out of necessity, because there is no other way\". It stands before an action one did not really want: 仕方なく〜した. Synonymous is 仕方なしに; more colloquial しょうがなく, more formal やむを得ず.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不動産の仲介手数料を払った。","romaji":"Fudōsan no chūkai tesūryō o haratta.","german":"I paid the real estate brokerage fee.","furigana":"ふどうさん|ちゅうかいてすうりょう|はら"},{"japanese":"友人の仲介で、今の会社に入った。","romaji":"Yūjin no chūkai de, ima no kaisha ni haitta.","german":"I joined my current company through a friend's mediation.","furigana":"ゆうじん|ちゅうかい|いま|かいしゃ|はい"},{"japanese":"この会社は中古車の売買を仲介している。","romaji":"Kono kaisha wa chūkosha no baibai o chūkai shite iru.","german":"This company brokers the buying and selling of used cars.","furigana":"かいしゃ|ちゅうこしゃ|ばいばい|ちゅうかい"}],"notes":"仲介 is \"mediation\" between two parties, especially in real estate (不動産の仲介, 仲介手数料 \"brokerage fee\", 仲介業者 \"broker\"), in trade and in negotiations. As a する-verb it is transitive: 売買を仲介する; \"through the mediation of\" is 〜の仲介で. Distinction: 紹介 \"to introduce\", 仲裁 \"to arbitrate a dispute\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"将来は一戸建てに住みたい。","romaji":"Shōrai wa ikkodate ni sumitai.","german":"In the future, I want to live in a detached house.","furigana":"しょうらい|いっこだ|す"},{"japanese":"郊外に中古の一戸建てを買いました。","romaji":"Kōgai ni chūko no ikkodate o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a used detached house in the suburbs.","furigana":"こうがい|ちゅうこ|いっこだ|か"},{"japanese":"一戸建ては庭があるのがいいが、手入れが大変だ。","romaji":"Ikkodate wa niwa ga aru no ga ii ga, teire ga taihen da.","german":"A detached house is nice for having a garden, but the upkeep is hard work.","furigana":"いっこだ|にわ|てい|たいへん"}],"notes":"一戸建て is a freestanding single-family house as opposed to apartments in multi-unit buildings such as マンション or アパート. Typical combinations: 一戸建てを買う / 建てる, 新築の一戸建て, 中古の一戸建て. It is shortened to 戸建て, colloquially also 一軒家. Frequent in real estate ads and in talk about dream homes.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"駅前のマンションに引っ越した。","romaji":"Ekimae no manshon ni hikkoshita.","german":"I moved into an apartment in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|ひ|こ"},{"japanese":"高層マンションの最上階からは富士山が見える。","romaji":"Kōsō manshon no saijōkai kara wa Fujisan ga mieru.","german":"You can see Mt. Fuji from the top floor of the high-rise apartment building.","furigana":"こうそう|さいじょうかい|ふじさん|み"},{"japanese":"マンションを買うか、ずっと賃貸にするか迷っている。","romaji":"Manshon o kau ka, zutto chintai ni suru ka mayotte iru.","german":"I can't decide whether to buy a condo or keep renting.","furigana":"か|ちんたい|まよ"}],"notes":"マンション is a false friend: it does not mean a mansion but a multi-story residential building of reinforced concrete or an apartment in one - rented (賃貸マンション) or owned (分譲マンション). アパート, by contrast, is a smaller, more simply built apartment building. Typical: マンションに住む, マンションの〜階, 高層マンション.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 27"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"学生の頃アパートに住んでいた。","romaji":"Gakusei no koro apāto ni sunde ita.","german":"I lived in an apartment when I was a student.","furigana":"がくせい|ころ|す"},{"japanese":"このアパートは三階建てです。","romaji":"Kono apāto wa sangaidate desu.","german":"This apartment building has three floors.","furigana":"さんがいだ"}],"notes":"アパート can mean an apartment building or a single unit in it. アパートに住む describes where one lives. 三階建て gives the number of floors of a building (three). 学生の頃 refers to one's own student days. The dash in アパート makes the a after パ long: apāto.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 22"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい家具を買いに行った。","romaji":"Atarashii kagu o kai ni itta.","german":"I went to buy new furniture.","furigana":"あたら|かぐ|か|い"},{"japanese":"家具付きの部屋を探しています。","romaji":"Kagutsuki no heya o sagashite imasu.","german":"I'm looking for a furnished room.","furigana":"かぐつ|へや|さが"},{"japanese":"地震に備えて、家具を壁に固定した。","romaji":"Jishin ni sonaete, kagu o kabe ni kotei shita.","german":"As a precaution against earthquakes, I secured the furniture to the wall.","furigana":"じしん|そな|かぐ|かべ|こてい"}],"notes":"家具 is the collective term \"furniture\" (cabinet, table, bed, etc.); individual pieces are counted with 点 or つ. Important combinations: 家具付き \"furnished\", 家具を配置する \"to arrange furniture\", 家具を固定する (earthquake protection), 家具店. To distinguish: 家電 are electrical appliances, 道具 tools and utensils.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 27"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"家電量販店でテレビを買った。","romaji":"Kaden ryōhanten de terebi o katta.","german":"I bought a TV at an electronics retailer.","furigana":"かでんりょうはんてん|か"},{"japanese":"引っ越しのとき、古い家電をまとめて処分した。","romaji":"Hikkoshi no toki, furui kaden o matomete shobun shita.","german":"When moving, I disposed of my old appliances all at once.","furigana":"ひ|こ|ふる|かでん|しょぶん"},{"japanese":"最近の家電はスマホで操作できるものが多い。","romaji":"Saikin no kaden wa sumaho de sōsa dekiru mono ga ōi.","german":"Many recent home appliances can be operated with a smartphone.","furigana":"さいきん|かでん|そうさ|おお"}],"notes":"家電 is the short form of 家庭用電気製品 and covers electric household appliances such as refrigerators, washing machines and rice cookers, but also TVs. Frequent: 家電量販店 \"electronics superstore\", 白物家電 \"white goods\", 生活家電. Distinction: 家具 means furniture; 電化製品 is a somewhat older umbrella term.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"冬は暖房なしでは過ごせない。","romaji":"Fuyu wa danbō nashi de wa sugosenai.","german":"You can't get through winter without heating.","furigana":"ふゆ|だんぼう|す"},{"japanese":"寒いから暖房をつけてもいいですか。","romaji":"Samui kara danbō o tsukete mo ii desu ka.","german":"It's cold - may I turn on the heating?","furigana":"さむ|だんぼう"},{"japanese":"この部屋は暖房が効いていて暖かいです。","romaji":"Kono heya wa danbō ga kiite ite atatakai desu.","german":"This room is well heated and nice and warm.","furigana":"へや|だんぼう|き|あたた"}],"notes":"暖房 is the heating of a room or the heating as a system: 暖房をつける/消す, 暖房を入れる, 暖房が効いている \"it is well heated\". The counterpart is 冷房 \"cooling\". The actual device is usually called エアコン, ヒーター or ストーブ; 暖房 emphasizes the function.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 41"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"冷房が効きすぎて寒い。","romaji":"Reibō ga kikisugite samui.","german":"The air conditioning is too strong and it's cold.","furigana":"れいぼう|き|さむ"},{"japanese":"暑いので、冷房をつけましょう。","romaji":"Atsui node, reibō o tsukemashō.","german":"It's hot, so let's turn on the air conditioning.","furigana":"あつ|れいぼう"},{"japanese":"夜は冷房を消して寝ます。","romaji":"Yoru wa reibō o keshite nemasu.","german":"At night I turn off the air conditioning when I sleep.","furigana":"よる|れいぼう|け|ね"}],"notes":"冷房 is room cooling or the air conditioner in cooling mode: 冷房をつける/入れる/消す, 冷房が効いている \"it is well cooled\", 冷房が効きすぎる \"too strongly cooled\". The counterpart is 暖房. The device itself is usually called エアコン; 冷房 emphasizes the function and often appears on signs in trains and shops.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 41"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"部屋の照明を暗くした。","romaji":"Heya no shōmei o kuraku shita.","german":"I dimmed the lighting in the room.","furigana":"へや|しょうめい|くら"},{"japanese":"舞台の照明が消えて、劇が始まった。","romaji":"Butai no shōmei ga kiete, geki ga hajimatta.","german":"The stage lights went out, and the play began.","furigana":"ぶたい|しょうめい|き|げき|はじ"},{"japanese":"寝室には暖かい色の照明が合う。","romaji":"Shinshitsu ni wa atatakai iro no shōmei ga au.","german":"Warm-colored lighting suits a bedroom.","furigana":"しんしつ|あたた|いろ|しょうめい|あ"}],"notes":"照明 denotes the lighting or lamps in a room (照明をつける / 消す, 照明を暗くする, 間接照明 \"indirect lighting\") as well as lighting technology in theater or film (舞台照明). In everyday speech, for switching on and off, people often simply say 電気をつける. As a する-verb \"to illuminate\" it is rare and rather technical.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"週に一回シーツを洗う。","romaji":"Shū ni ikkai shītsu o arau.","german":"I wash the sheets once a week.","furigana":"しゅう|いっかい|あら"},{"japanese":"ホテルでは毎日シーツを替えてくれる。","romaji":"Hoteru de wa mainichi shītsu o kaete kureru.","german":"At the hotel, they change the sheets every day.","furigana":"まいにち|か"},{"japanese":"布団にシーツを敷いてから寝てください。","romaji":"Futon ni shītsu o shiite kara nete kudasai.","german":"Please put a sheet on the futon before you go to sleep.","furigana":"ふとん|し|ね"}],"notes":"シーツ (from English \"sheets\") is the bed sheet, for both beds and futons. Typical verbs: シーツを替える \"to change\", 敷く \"to lay on\", 洗う; counted with 枚. For covers, on the other hand, people say カバー (布団カバー, 枕カバー).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"柔軟剤を入れるとタオルがふわふわになる。","romaji":"Jūnanzai o ireru to taoru ga fuwafuwa ni naru.","german":"Adding fabric softener makes towels fluffy.","furigana":"じゅうなんざい|い"},{"japanese":"柔軟剤の香りが強すぎて、気分が悪くなった。","romaji":"Jūnanzai no kaori ga tsuyosugite, kibun ga waruku natta.","german":"The scent of the fabric softener was so strong that I felt sick.","furigana":"じゅうなんざい|かお|つよ|きぶん|わる"},{"japanese":"洗剤と間違えて柔軟剤を買ってしまった。","romaji":"Senzai to machigaete jūnanzai o katte shimatta.","german":"I mistakenly bought fabric softener instead of detergent.","furigana":"せんざい|まちが|じゅうなんざい|か"}],"notes":"柔軟剤 is composed of 柔軟 \"soft, supple\" and 剤 \"agent\": \"fabric softener\". One says 柔軟剤を入れる / 使う; in Japan people often talk about 柔軟剤の香り \"the scent of the softener\". Not to be confused with 洗剤 \"detergent\"; both are put into the washing machine together.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"粗大ゴミを出すには予約が必要だ。","romaji":"Sodai gomi o dasu ni wa yoyaku ga hitsuyō da.","german":"You need a reservation to put out bulky waste.","furigana":"そだい|だ|よやく|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"古いソファを粗大ゴミに出した。","romaji":"Furui sofa o sodai gomi ni dashita.","german":"I put the old sofa out as bulky waste.","furigana":"ふる|そだい|だ"},{"japanese":"粗大ゴミの回収は月に一度しかない。","romaji":"Sodai gomi no kaishū wa tsuki ni ichido shika nai.","german":"Bulky waste is collected only once a month.","furigana":"そだい|かいしゅう|つき|いちど"}],"notes":"粗大ゴミ is bulky waste such as furniture, bicycles or large appliances. In Japan you usually have to register it beforehand and attach a paid sticker (粗大ゴミ処理券); it is then picked up on a set day. One says 粗大ゴミを出す or 〜を粗大ゴミに出す; in official texts it is often written 粗大ごみ. Jokingly it also refers to a retired husband sitting around uselessly.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"夏はエアコンで電気代が高くなる。","romaji":"Natsu wa eakon de denkidai ga takaku naru.","german":"In summer, the electricity bill goes up because of the air conditioner.","furigana":"なつ|でんきだい|たか"},{"japanese":"電気代を節約するために、使わない電気はこまめに消している。","romaji":"Denkidai o setsuyaku suru tame ni, tsukawanai denki wa komame ni keshite iru.","german":"To save on electricity, I diligently switch off power I'm not using.","furigana":"でんきだい|せつやく|つか|でんき|け"},{"japanese":"今月の電気代は一万円を超えた。","romaji":"Kongetsu no denkidai wa ichiman'en o koeta.","german":"This month's electricity bill exceeded 10,000 yen.","furigana":"こんげつ|でんきだい|いちまんえん|こ"}],"notes":"電気代 is the electricity cost or electricity bill; 代 stands for the price of a service (cf. ガス代, 水道代). Typical: 電気代がかかる / 高い / 上がる, 電気代を払う / 節約する. In contracts and official texts, the more formal 電気料金 is used.","levelNote":"Compound: 電気 (N5) + 代 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"冬はガス代がかさむ。","romaji":"Fuyu wa gasudai ga kasamu.","german":"In winter, the gas bill piles up.","furigana":"ふゆ|だい"},{"japanese":"ガス代はクレジットカードで払っている。","romaji":"Gasudai wa kurejitto kādo de haratte iru.","german":"I pay the gas bill by credit card.","furigana":"だい|はら"},{"japanese":"お風呂を毎日沸かすと、ガス代がかなりかかる。","romaji":"Ofuro o mainichi wakasu to, gasudai ga kanari kakaru.","german":"If you heat the bath every day, it costs quite a lot of gas.","furigana":"ふろ|まいにちわ|だい"}],"notes":"ガス代 is the gas cost or gas bill for cooking, hot water and heating (city gas or propane). Typical: ガス代がかかる / かさむ \"to add up\", ガス代を払う. Formally it is ガス料金. Colloquially ガス代 can also mean gasoline costs; for that, ガソリン代 is clearer.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"水道代は二ヶ月に一回請求される。","romaji":"Suidōdai wa nikagetsu ni ikkai seikyū sareru.","german":"The water bill is charged once every two months.","furigana":"すいどうだい|にかげつ|いっかいせいきゅう"},{"japanese":"シャワーの時間を短くして、水道代を節約している。","romaji":"Shawā no jikan o mijikaku shite, suidōdai o setsuyaku shite iru.","german":"I shorten my showers to save on the water bill.","furigana":"じかん|みじか|すいどうだい|せつやく"},{"japanese":"このアパートは水道代が家賃に含まれている。","romaji":"Kono apāto wa suidōdai ga yachin ni fukumarete iru.","german":"In this apartment, the water bill is included in the rent.","furigana":"すいどうだい|やちん|ふく"}],"notes":"水道代 is the water cost or water bill; in Japan billing is usually every two months, often together with sewage (下水道). Formally it is 水道料金. Typical: 水道代を払う / 節約する, 水道代が家賃に含まれている.","levelNote":"Compound: 水道 (N4) + 代 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"食費を月三万円に抑えている。","romaji":"Shokuhi o tsuki sanman'en ni osaete iru.","german":"I keep my food expenses to 30,000 yen a month.","furigana":"しょくひ|つきさんまんえん|おさ"},{"japanese":"外食が多くて、食費がかさんでいる。","romaji":"Gaishoku ga ōkute, shokuhi ga kasande iru.","german":"I eat out a lot, so my food expenses are piling up.","furigana":"がいしょく|おお|しょくひ"},{"japanese":"寮の家賃には食費も含まれている。","romaji":"Ryō no yachin ni wa shokuhi mo fukumarete iru.","german":"Meals are also included in the dormitory rent.","furigana":"りょう|やちん|しょくひ|ふく"}],"notes":"食費 is household spending on food, i.e. groceries and meals overall; for dormitories also \"board costs\". Typical: 食費を抑える / 節約する / 切り詰める, 食費がかさむ. Distinction: 食事代 is the price of a single meal, 生活費 covers all living costs.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"会社が交通費を負担してくれる。","romaji":"Kaisha ga kōtsūhi o futan shite kureru.","german":"The company covers my transportation costs.","furigana":"かいしゃ|こうつうひ|ふたん"},{"japanese":"面接の交通費は自己負担です。","romaji":"Mensetsu no kōtsūhi wa jiko futan desu.","german":"You bear your own transportation costs to the interview.","furigana":"めんせつ|こうつうひ|じこふたん"},{"japanese":"出張の交通費を経理に精算してもらった。","romaji":"Shutchō no kōtsūhi o keiri ni seisan shite moratta.","german":"The accounting department reimbursed me for my business trip transportation costs.","furigana":"しゅっちょう|こうつうひ|けいり|せいさん"}],"notes":"交通費 are transportation or travel costs, above all commuting costs, which Japanese companies often reimburse (交通費支給, 交通費を負担する). For business trips: 交通費を精算する \"to settle expenses\". Distinction: 運賃 is a single fare, 旅費 the total travel costs including lodging.","levelNote":"Compound: 交通 (N5) + 費 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"通信費を見直して安いプランに変えた。","romaji":"Tsūshinhi o minaoshite yasui puran ni kaeta.","german":"I reviewed my communication costs and switched to a cheaper plan.","furigana":"つうしんひ|みなお|やす|か"},{"japanese":"在宅勤務の通信費は会社が一部負担する。","romaji":"Zaitaku kinmu no tsūshinhi wa kaisha ga ichibu futan suru.","german":"The company partly covers the internet costs for working from home.","furigana":"ざいたくきんむ|つうしんひ|かいしゃ|いちぶふたん"},{"japanese":"家族全員のスマホ代を合わせると、通信費は月二万円になる。","romaji":"Kazoku zen'in no sumahodai o awaseru to, tsūshinhi wa tsuki niman'en ni naru.","german":"Adding up the phones of the whole family, our communication costs come to 20,000 yen a month.","furigana":"かぞくぜんいん|だい|あ|つうしんひ|つきにまんえん"}],"notes":"通信費 are the costs of communication, today mainly mobile phone and internet contracts, formerly also postage and landline. Typical in household budgets and saving tips: 通信費を見直す / 抑える / 節約する. For the mobile phone alone, people colloquially say スマホ代 or 携帯代.","levelNote":"Compound: 通信 (N3) + 費 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"娯楽費を削減することにした。","romaji":"Gorakuhi o sakugen suru koto ni shita.","german":"I decided to cut back on entertainment expenses.","furigana":"ごらくひ|さくげん"},{"japanese":"家計簿をつけたら、娯楽費が思ったより多かった。","romaji":"Kakeibo o tsuketara, gorakuhi ga omotta yori ōkatta.","german":"When I kept a household account book, my entertainment expenses were higher than I thought.","furigana":"かけいぼ|ごらくひ|おも|おお"},{"japanese":"学生のころは娯楽費にほとんどお金を使えなかった。","romaji":"Gakusei no koro wa gorakuhi ni hotondo okane o tsukaenakatta.","german":"When I was a student, I could hardly spend any money on entertainment.","furigana":"がくせい|ごらくひ|かね|つか"}],"notes":"娯楽費 is composed of 娯楽 \"entertainment, amusement\" and 費 \"cost\" and denotes spending on leisure such as movies, games, hobbies and outings - a typical category in a household account book (家計簿). Related are 交際費 \"social expenses\" and 趣味にかけるお金. Typical: 娯楽費を削る / 抑える.","levelNote":"Compound: 娯楽 (N2) + 費 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"雑費として毎月一万円を予算に入れている。","romaji":"Zappi to shite maitsuki ichiman'en o yosan ni irete iru.","german":"I budget 10,000 yen a month for miscellaneous expenses.","furigana":"ざっぴ|まいつきいちまんえん|よさん|い"},{"japanese":"引っ越しでは、思ったより雑費がかかった。","romaji":"Hikkoshi de wa, omotta yori zappi ga kakatta.","german":"The move cost more in miscellaneous expenses than I expected.","furigana":"ひ|こ|おも|ざっぴ"},{"japanese":"文房具などの細かい買い物は雑費として処理する。","romaji":"Bunbōgu nado no komakai kaimono wa zappi to shite shori suru.","german":"Small purchases such as stationery are booked as miscellaneous expenses.","furigana":"ぶんぼうぐ|こま|か|もの|ざっぴ|しょり"}],"notes":"雑費 are \"miscellaneous expenses, incidental costs\" - small, varied items that fit no category of their own. 雑 means \"mixed, various\". Common in household account books, in bookkeeping (a \"miscellaneous\" account) and with moves or trips: 雑費がかかる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今月は出費が多かった。","romaji":"Kongetsu wa shuppi ga ōkatta.","german":"I had a lot of expenses this month.","furigana":"こんげつ|しゅっぴ|おお"},{"japanese":"車の修理代は痛い出費だった。","romaji":"Kuruma no shūridai wa itai shuppi datta.","german":"The car repair was a painful expense.","furigana":"くるま|しゅうりだい|いた|しゅっぴ"},{"japanese":"結婚式が続いて、出費がかさんでいる。","romaji":"Kekkonshiki ga tsuzuite, shuppi ga kasande iru.","german":"With one wedding after another, expenses are piling up.","furigana":"けっこんしき|つづ|しゅっぴ"}],"notes":"出費 means expenses, especially noticeable or unexpected ones: 出費がかさむ \"expenses pile up\", 痛い出費 \"a painful expense\", 出費を抑える. Distinction: 支出 is the bookkeeping counterpart to 収入, 費用 the cost for a particular purpose. As a する-verb it is hardly used.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"無駄遣いをやめなさい。","romaji":"Mudazukai o yamenasai.","german":"Stop wasting money.","furigana":"むだづか"},{"japanese":"給料日の後はつい無駄遣いしてしまう。","romaji":"Kyūryōbi no ato wa tsui mudazukai shite shimau.","german":"After payday I always end up spending money needlessly.","furigana":"きゅうりょうび|あと|むだづか"},{"japanese":"税金の無駄遣いだと、市民から批判が出ている。","romaji":"Zeikin no mudazukai da to, shimin kara hihan ga dete iru.","german":"Citizens are criticizing it as a waste of tax money.","furigana":"ぜいきん|むだづか|しみん|ひはん|で"}],"notes":"無駄遣い is wasting money, also time or resources (時間の無駄遣い, 税金の無駄遣い). As a する-verb: 無駄遣いする, 無駄遣いをやめる. Opposite: 節約. More formal is 浪費. The spelling 無駄使い also occurs.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 22"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"スーパーの特売日に買い物をする。","romaji":"Sūpā no tokubaibi ni kaimono o suru.","german":"I go shopping on the supermarket's sale day.","furigana":"とくばいび|か|もの"},{"japanese":"卵が特売で一パック百円だった。","romaji":"Tamago ga tokubai de hito pakku hyakuen datta.","german":"Eggs were on sale at 100 yen a pack.","furigana":"たまご|とくばい|ひと|ひゃくえん"},{"japanese":"特売品は売り切れるのが早い。","romaji":"Tokubaihin wa urikireru no ga hayai.","german":"Sale items sell out quickly.","furigana":"とくばいひん|う|き|はや"}],"notes":"特売 is a special sale at reduced prices, typical in supermarkets: 特売日 \"sale day\", 特売品 \"sale item\", 本日の特売. One says 特売で買う / 特売になっている. Distinction: セール is more for clothing and department stores, 割引 is the discount itself, 安売り generally means \"cheap sale\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"冬のセールで服を買った。","romaji":"Fuyu no sēru de fuku o katta.","german":"I bought clothes at the winter sale.","furigana":"ふゆ|ふく|か"},{"japanese":"駅前のスーパーで今日から半額セールが始まった。","romaji":"Ekimae no sūpā de kyō kara hangaku sēru ga hajimatta.","german":"A half-price sale started today at the supermarket in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|きょう|はんがく|はじ"},{"japanese":"セール品は返品できませんのでご注意ください。","romaji":"Sēruhin wa henpin dekimasen node go-chūi kudasai.","german":"Please note that sale items cannot be returned.","furigana":"ひん|へんぴん|ちゅうい"}],"notes":"セール is a sale at reduced prices. Typical are 夏のセール, 冬のセール, 半額セール or 期間限定セール; an item is セール中 or a セール品, and you buy something セールで (\"at the sale\"). \"Sales\" in the neutral sense, by contrast, is 販売; a clearance sale is 閉店セール or 在庫一掃セール.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このクーポンを使えば10%引きになる。","romaji":"Kono kūpon o tsukaeba juppāsento biki ni naru.","german":"With this coupon you get 10% off.","furigana":"つか|び"},{"japanese":"会計のときに、スマホのクーポン画面を見せてください。","romaji":"Kaikei no toki ni, sumaho no kūpon gamen o misete kudasai.","german":"Please show the coupon screen on your smartphone when paying.","furigana":"かいけい|がめん|み"},{"japanese":"新規登録すると、500円分のクーポンがもらえます。","romaji":"Shinki tōroku suru to, gohyaku-en bun no kūpon ga moraemasu.","german":"If you register as a new member, you will receive a coupon worth 500 yen.","furigana":"しんきとうろく|えんぶん"}],"notes":"クーポン is a discount coupon or voucher, today often digital in an app (アプリのクーポン). You use it with 使う or 利用する, receive it with もらう, shops hand it out with 配る, and you show it at the register: クーポンを提示する. A gift certificate with a fixed cash value is rather called 商品券 or ギフト券; 割引券 is the Japanese word for a \"discount ticket\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ポイントが貯まったので商品と交換した。","romaji":"Pointo ga tamatta node shōhin to kōkan shita.","german":"I had collected enough points, so I exchanged them for a product.","furigana":"た|しょうひん|こうかん"},{"japanese":"このレポートのポイントは三つあります。","romaji":"Kono repōto no pointo wa mittsu arimasu.","german":"This report has three key points.","furigana":"みっ"},{"japanese":"内閣の支持率が前回より5ポイント下がった。","romaji":"Naikaku no shijiritsu ga zenkai yori go pointo sagatta.","german":"The cabinet's approval rating fell by 5 points from last time.","furigana":"ないかく|しじりつ|ぜんかい|さ"}],"notes":"ポイント has two main areas: in everyday life, the reward points of loyalty cards (ポイントが貯まる \"points accumulate\", ポイントを貯める, ポイントを使う, ポイントカード) and, figuratively, \"the key point\": 話のポイント, ここがポイントだ. It also stands for scoring points and percentage points in statistics (3ポイント上昇). For a point as a dot or for school grades, one says 点 instead.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"レシートは必ず保管してください。","romaji":"Reshīto wa kanarazu hokan shite kudasai.","german":"Please be sure to keep the receipt.","furigana":"かなら|ほかん"},{"japanese":"レシートはいりますか。――いいえ、結構です。","romaji":"Reshīto wa irimasu ka. — Iie, kekkō desu.","german":"Do you need the receipt? - No, thank you.","furigana":"けっこう"},{"japanese":"家計簿をつけるために、レシートを集めている。","romaji":"Kakeibo o tsukeru tame ni, reshīto o atsumete iru.","german":"I collect receipts in order to keep a household account book.","furigana":"かけいぼ|あつ"}],"notes":"レシート is the machine-printed register receipt. You get it with もらう; at the register the staff ask 「レシートはいりますか」 or, more politely, 「レシートはご入用ですか」. For an official receipt, for example for expense reports with the company name, on the other hand, you ask for a 領収書 - this distinction matters in working life.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"現金でお支払いですか。","romaji":"Genkin de oshiharai desu ka.","german":"Will you be paying in cash?","furigana":"げんきん|しはら"},{"japanese":"この店は現金しか使えないので、ATMでお金を下ろしてきた。","romaji":"Kono mise wa genkin shika tsukaenai node, ATM de okane o oroshite kita.","german":"This shop only accepts cash, so I withdrew money from the ATM.","furigana":"みせ|げんきん|つか|かね|お"},{"japanese":"お小遣いをあげると言ったとたん手伝い始めるなんて、現金な子だ。","romaji":"Okozukai o ageru to itta totan tetsudai hajimeru nante, genkin na ko da.","german":"He started helping the moment I said I'd give him pocket money - what a mercenary kid.","furigana":"こづか|い|てつだ|はじ|げんきん|こ"}],"notes":"現金 is \"cash\" as opposed to cashless payment (キャッシュレス, カード). To pay cash is 現金で払う; 現金のみ is posted at shops that take only cash. As a な-adjective, 現金な also means \"calculating, concerned only with one's own advantage\": 現金な人, 現金なやつだ - colloquial and slightly mocking. In everyday speech, cash is also simply called お金 or キャッシュ.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 18 (source: N5; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"クレジットカードは使えますか。","romaji":"Kurejitto kādo wa tsukaemasu ka.","german":"Can I use a credit card?","furigana":"つか"},{"japanese":"クレジットカードで払うと、ポイントが貯まります。","romaji":"Kurejitto kādo de harau to, pointo ga tamarimasu.","german":"If you pay by credit card, you earn points.","furigana":"はら|た"},{"japanese":"財布を落としたので、すぐにクレジットカードを止めてもらった。","romaji":"Saifu o otoshita node, sugu ni kurejitto kādo o tomete moratta.","german":"I lost my wallet, so I had my credit card blocked right away.","furigana":"さいふ|お|と"}],"notes":"クレジットカード is often shortened to カード in conversation: カードで払う, カードは使えますか. The means of payment takes で: クレジットカードで支払う. At the register you often hear the question about 一括払い (\"in one payment\") or 分割払い (\"in installments\"). To be distinguished are デビットカード and rechargeable IC cards such as Suica, which count as 電子マネー.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 10 (source: N5; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"最近は電子マネーで支払うことが多い。","romaji":"Saikin wa denshi manē de shiharau koto ga ōi.","german":"Recently I often pay with electronic money.","furigana":"さいきん|でんし|しはら|おお"},{"japanese":"コンビニで電子マネーにチャージした。","romaji":"Konbini de denshi manē ni chāji shita.","german":"I topped up my electronic money at the convenience store.","furigana":"でんし"},{"japanese":"電子マネーの残高が足りなくて、改札で止められた。","romaji":"Denshi manē no zandaka ga tarinakute, kaisatsu de tomerareta.","german":"My e-money balance was insufficient, so I was stopped at the ticket gate.","furigana":"でんし|ざんだか|た|かいさつ|と"}],"notes":"電子マネー in Japan mainly means prepaid electronic money on IC cards or in a smartphone, such as Suica, PASMO or nanaco. You top up with チャージする and pay with 電子マネーで払う, usually by briefly touching the reader (タッチ). The umbrella term for all cashless payment methods is キャッシュレス決済; QR code payments are mostly not counted as 電子マネー in the narrow sense.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"乗車券は券売機で購入できます。","romaji":"Jōshaken wa kenbaiki de kōnyū dekimasu.","german":"Tickets can be purchased at the ticket machine.","furigana":"じょうしゃけん|けんばいき|こうにゅう"},{"japanese":"新幹線に乗るには、乗車券のほかに特急券も必要です。","romaji":"Shinkansen ni noru ni wa, jōshaken no hoka ni tokkyūken mo hitsuyō desu.","german":"To ride the Shinkansen, you need an express ticket in addition to the basic ticket.","furigana":"しんかんせん|の|じょうしゃけん|とっきゅうけん|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"乗車券を拝見いたします。","romaji":"Jōshaken o haiken itashimasu.","german":"May I see your tickets, please?","furigana":"じょうしゃけん|はいけん"}],"notes":"乗車券 is the official term for a ticket for train and bus; you read it on machines, signs and in announcements. In everyday speech people mostly simply say 切符. At JR, the 乗車券 covers only the basic fare - for express and Shinkansen trains you additionally need a 特急券. For flights it is 航空券, for concerts チケット.","levelNote":"Compound: 乗車 (N2) + 券 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新鮮な食材を市場で買った。","romaji":"Shinsen na shokuzai o ichiba de katta.","german":"I bought fresh ingredients at the market.","furigana":"しんせん|しょくざい|いちば|か"},{"japanese":"このレストランは地元の食材にこだわっている。","romaji":"Kono resutoran wa jimoto no shokuzai ni kodawatte iru.","german":"This restaurant is particular about local ingredients.","furigana":"じもと|しょくざい"},{"japanese":"冷蔵庫に残っている食材で夕飯を作った。","romaji":"Reizōko ni nokotte iru shokuzai de yūhan o tsukutta.","german":"I made dinner with the ingredients left in the fridge.","furigana":"れいぞうこ|のこ|しょくざい|ゆうはん|つく"}],"notes":"食材 are foodstuffs as raw material for cooking, i.e. \"ingredients\" such as vegetables, meat or fish. Typical: 新鮮な食材, 地元の食材, 旬の食材, 食材を生かす \"to bring out the ingredients\". 材料 is more general and also appears in recipe lists for baking or crafts; 食品 means rather processed foods as goods, such as 冷凍食品.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"基本的な調味料を揃えておく。","romaji":"Kihonteki na chōmiryō o soroete oku.","german":"Keep the basic seasonings on hand.","furigana":"きほんてき|ちょうみりょう|そろ"},{"japanese":"調味料を全部入れてから、弱火で10分煮ます。","romaji":"Chōmiryō o zenbu irete kara, yowabi de juppun nimasu.","german":"After adding all the seasonings, simmer for 10 minutes over low heat.","furigana":"ちょうみりょう|ぜんぶい|よわび|ぷんに"},{"japanese":"当店の料理には化学調味料を使っていません。","romaji":"Tōten no ryōri ni wa kagaku chōmiryō o tsukatte imasen.","german":"We do not use chemical seasonings in our dishes.","furigana":"とうてん|りょうり|かがくちょうみりょう|つか"}],"notes":"調味料 are seasonings in the broad sense: salt, sugar, soy sauce, miso, vinegar, mayonnaise, etc. The classic basic seasonings of Japanese cooking are remembered with さしすせそ (砂糖, 塩, 酢, 醤油, 味噌). Hot or aromatic spices such as pepper or cinnamon are more precisely called 香辛料 or スパイス. Typical: 調味料を加える, 調味料で味を調える; 化学調味料 are artificial flavor enhancers.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 34"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ステーキに胡椒を振りかけた。","romaji":"Sutēki ni koshō o furikaketa.","german":"I sprinkled pepper on the steak.","furigana":"こしょう|ふ"},{"japanese":"肉に塩胡椒をして、強火で焼きます。","romaji":"Niku ni shio koshō o shite, tsuyobi de yakimasu.","german":"Season the meat with salt and pepper and grill it over high heat.","furigana":"にく|しおこしょう|つよび|や"},{"japanese":"スープの味が薄かったので、胡椒を少し足した。","romaji":"Sūpu no aji ga usukatta node, koshō o sukoshi tashita.","german":"The soup tasted bland, so I added a little pepper.","furigana":"あじ|うす|こしょう|すこ|た"}],"notes":"胡椒 is mostly written in kana in everyday life (こしょう or コショウ); the kanji is seen more on packaging and menus. You sprinkle it with 振る or かける: 胡椒を振る. Very common is the pair 塩胡椒 \"salt and pepper\", also as a verb 塩胡椒する. Black pepper is 黒胡椒 or ブラックペッパー. Note: ピーマン is green bell pepper, not pepper.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"焼きそばにソースをかける。","romaji":"Yakisoba ni sōsu o kakeru.","german":"I pour sauce over the fried noodles.","furigana":"や"},{"japanese":"とんかつにはソースとからしをつけて食べる。","romaji":"Tonkatsu ni wa sōsu to karashi o tsukete taberu.","german":"Tonkatsu is eaten with sauce and mustard.","furigana":"た"},{"japanese":"パスタのソースを一から手作りした。","romaji":"Pasuta no sōsu o ichi kara tezukuri shita.","german":"I made the pasta sauce completely from scratch.","furigana":"いち|てづく"}],"notes":"ソース is \"sauce\" in general, but without further qualification in Japan it usually means the dark, savory Worcestershire-style sauce poured over とんかつ, お好み焼き or 焼きそば. Other sauces are named more precisely: トマトソース, ホワイトソース, デミグラスソース. You pour it on with かける or dip with つける. The same word is used for the \"source\" of information (ソースはどこ?), which is more internet jargon.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 34"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"昆布と鰹節でだしを取る。","romaji":"Konbu to katsuobushi de dashi o toru.","german":"I make dashi from kelp and bonito flakes.","furigana":"こんぶ|かつおぶし|と"},{"japanese":"このみそ汁はだしがよく効いていておいしい。","romaji":"Kono misoshiru wa dashi ga yoku kiite ite oishii.","german":"This miso soup is delicious, with a strong dashi flavor.","furigana":"しる|き"},{"japanese":"忙しい日はだしの素を使っている。","romaji":"Isogashii hi wa dashi no moto o tsukatte iru.","german":"On busy days, I use instant dashi.","furigana":"いそが|ひ|もと|つか"}],"notes":"だし (kanji 出汁, mostly in kana) is the Japanese basic stock made from 昆布, bonito flakes (鰹節) or dried sardines (煮干し). The set phrase is だしを取る \"to extract stock\"; instant powder is だしの素. だしが効いている means that the stock flavor comes through well. Western meat broth is rather called スープ or ブイヨン. Figuratively, 〜をだしにする means \"to use someone or something as a pretext\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"片栗粉でとろみをつける。","romaji":"Katakuriko de toromi o tsukeru.","german":"I thicken it with potato starch.","furigana":"かたくりこ"},{"japanese":"唐揚げは片栗粉をつけて揚げると、カリッとする。","romaji":"Karaage wa katakuriko o tsukete ageru to, karitto suru.","german":"Karaage turns out crispy if you coat it in potato starch before frying.","furigana":"からあ|かたくりこ|あ"},{"japanese":"水で溶いた片栗粉を少しずつ鍋に加えてください。","romaji":"Mizu de toita katakuriko o sukoshi zutsu nabe ni kuwaete kudasai.","german":"Add the starch dissolved in water to the pot little by little.","furigana":"みず|と|かたくりこ|すこ|なべ|くわ"}],"notes":"片栗粉 is today practically always potato starch (originally it was made from the plant 片栗). It is used for thickening - 片栗粉でとろみをつける, usually as 水溶き片栗粉 \"starch mixed with water\" - and as a coating for crispy fried food such as 唐揚げ. Unlike wheat flour (小麦粉), it makes sauces translucent and glossy.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"パン粉をつけて揚げるとサクサクになる。","romaji":"Panko o tsukete ageru to sakusaku ni naru.","german":"Wenn man es mit Paniermehl frittiert, wird es knusprig.","furigana":"こ|あ"},{"japanese":"小麦粉、卵、パン粉の順につけてください。","romaji":"Komugiko, tamago, panko no jun ni tsukete kudasai.","german":"Wenden Sie es nacheinander in Mehl, Ei und Paniermehl.","furigana":"こむぎこ|たまご|こ|じゅん"},{"japanese":"ハンバーグを作るとき、牛乳に浸したパン粉を入れる。","romaji":"Hanbāgu o tsukuru toki, gyūnyū ni hitashita panko o ireru.","german":"Für Frikadellen gebe ich in Milch eingeweichtes Paniermehl in die Masse.","furigana":"つく|ぎゅうにゅう|ひた|こ|い"}],"notes":"パン粉 ist Paniermehl aus Brot; japanisches Panko ist gröber und luftiger als deutsches Semmelmehl. Die typische Panierreihenfolge lautet 小麦粉、卵、パン粉の順につける, etwa für とんかつ oder コロッケ. Man sagt パン粉をつける oder パン粉をまぶす. In der Hackfleischmasse für ハンバーグ dient パン粉 als Bindemittel. Nicht verwechseln mit 小麦粉 („Weizenmehl“).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"フライパンに油をひいて焼く。","romaji":"Furaipan ni abura o hiite yaku.","german":"Put oil in the frying pan and fry.","furigana":"あぶら|や"},{"japanese":"油で揚げる料理は、後片付けが大変だ。","romaji":"Abura de ageru ryōri wa, atokatazuke ga taihen da.","german":"Cleaning up after deep-fried dishes is hard work.","furigana":"あぶら|あ|りょうり|あとかたづ|たいへん"},{"japanese":"こんなところで油を売っていないで、早く仕事に戻りなさい。","romaji":"Konna tokoro de abura o utte inai de, hayaku shigoto ni modorinasai.","german":"Stop idling around in a place like this and get back to work quickly.","furigana":"あぶら|う|はや|しごと|もど"}],"notes":"油 is mainly liquid oil: cooking oil (サラダ油, ごま油, オリーブ油), but also machine oil. Solid animal fat is usually written 脂 (same reading), e.g. 脂身 or 脂がのった魚. In the kitchen, people say フライパンに油をひく (\"to put oil in the pan\") and 油で揚げる. Idioms: 油を売る \"to loaf around,\" 火に油を注ぐ \"to add fuel to the fire.\"","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"トーストにバターを塗った。","romaji":"Tōsuto ni batā o nutta.","german":"I spread butter on the toast.","furigana":"ぬ"},{"japanese":"冷蔵庫にバターがありません。","romaji":"Reizōko ni batā ga arimasen.","german":"There's no butter in the fridge.","furigana":"れいぞうこ"}],"notes":"バター is butter. を marks it as the thing being spread; に marks the surface or food it is spread on. バターがない reports that there is none. The dash after タ lengthens the a: batā. 塗る is a godan verb with the form 塗った.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ケーキの上に生クリームを絞った。","romaji":"Kēki no ue ni nama kurīmu o shibotta.","german":"I piped fresh cream on top of the cake.","furigana":"うえ|なま|しぼ"},{"japanese":"生クリームを角が立つまで泡立てます。","romaji":"Nama kurīmu o tsuno ga tatsu made awadatemasu.","german":"Whip the cream until stiff peaks form.","furigana":"なま|つの|た|あわだ"},{"japanese":"このパスタは生クリームを使っているので、味がまろやかだ。","romaji":"Kono pasuta wa nama kurīmu o tsukatte iru node, aji ga maroyaka da.","german":"This pasta uses cream, so it tastes mild.","furigana":"なま|つか|あじ"}],"notes":"生クリーム is cream or whipping cream; 生 means \"fresh, unprocessed\" here. You whip it with 泡立てる, pipe it onto cakes with 絞る and stir it into sauces. In stores, real cream is distinguished from vegetable-based substitute cream, which is often sold as ホイップ. クリーム alone can also mean skin cream or custard (カスタードクリーム).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"カルボナーラには卵黄を使う。","romaji":"Karubonāra ni wa ran'ō o tsukau.","german":"Egg yolk is used for carbonara.","furigana":"らんおう|つか"},{"japanese":"卵を卵黄と卵白に分けておいてください。","romaji":"Tamago o ran'ō to ranpaku ni wakete oite kudasai.","german":"Please separate the eggs into yolks and whites.","furigana":"たまご|らんおう|らんぱく|わ"},{"japanese":"マヨネーズは卵黄と油と酢で作られている。","romaji":"Mayonēzu wa ran'ō to abura to su de tsukurarete iru.","german":"Mayonnaise is made from egg yolk, oil and vinegar.","furigana":"らんおう|あぶら|す|つく"}],"notes":"卵黄 is the egg yolk in a rather technical or written register: in recipes, ingredient lists and nutrition texts. In everyday speech people mostly say 黄身; the counterpart is 卵白 or, colloquially, 白身. Typical: 卵黄と卵白に分ける \"to separate yolk and white\", 卵黄だけを使う.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"メレンゲは卵白を泡立てて作る。","romaji":"Merenge wa ranpaku o awadatete tsukuru.","german":"Meringue is made by whipping egg whites.","furigana":"らんぱく|あわだ|つく"},{"japanese":"余った卵白は冷凍して保存できます。","romaji":"Amatta ranpaku wa reitō shite hozon dekimasu.","german":"Leftover egg whites can be frozen and stored.","furigana":"あま|らんぱく|れいとう|ほぞん"},{"japanese":"ダイエット中なので、卵白だけでオムレツを作った。","romaji":"Daietto-chū na node, ranpaku dake de omuretsu o tsukutta.","german":"I'm on a diet, so I made an omelet with only egg whites.","furigana":"ちゅう|らんぱく|つく"}],"notes":"卵白 is the egg white in a technical or recipe register; colloquially people say 白身. Note: \"egg white\" in the sense of the nutrient is not 卵白 but たんぱく質 \"protein\". Typical: 卵白を泡立てる \"to whip egg whites\", 卵白だけを使う; the counterpart is 卵黄.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"朝食にヨーグルトを食べている。","romaji":"Chōshoku ni yōguruto o tabete iru.","german":"I eat yogurt for breakfast.","furigana":"ちょうしょく|た"},{"japanese":"プレーンヨーグルトにはちみつをかけて食べるのが好きだ。","romaji":"Purēn yōguruto ni hachimitsu o kakete taberu no ga suki da.","german":"I like to eat plain yogurt with honey.","furigana":"た|す"},{"japanese":"ヨーグルトはおなかの調子を整えると言われている。","romaji":"Yōguruto wa onaka no chōshi o totonoeru to iwarete iru.","german":"Yogurt is said to regulate the stomach.","furigana":"ちょうし|ととの|い"}],"notes":"ヨーグルト is yogurt. Common combinations: プレーンヨーグルト \"plain yogurt\", 飲むヨーグルト \"drinking yogurt\", ヨーグルトにはちみつをかける. In Japan yogurt is strongly associated with health and digestion (乳酸菌, おなかの調子). You eat it with 食べる; only drinking yogurt is drunk with 飲む.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"寒い日にはおでんが食べたくなる。","romaji":"Samui hi ni wa oden ga tabetaku naru.","german":"On cold days, I crave oden.","furigana":"さむ|ひ|た"},{"japanese":"コンビニで大根と卵のおでんを買った。","romaji":"Konbini de daikon to tamago no oden o katta.","german":"I bought oden with daikon and egg at the convenience store.","furigana":"だいこん|たまご|か"},{"japanese":"おでんの具で一番好きなのは何？","romaji":"Oden no gu de ichiban suki na no wa nani?","german":"What's your favorite oden ingredient?","furigana":"ぐ|いちばんす|なに"}],"notes":"おでん is a winter dish in which ingredients such as 大根, eggs, こんにゃく and fish products (ちくわ, はんぺん) simmer for a long time in a soy-seasoned だし. It is almost always written in hiragana. Typical are おでんを煮る, おでんの具 \"the ingredients\" and the おでん at the convenience store register, where you choose the pieces yourself. People like to eat it with からし (hot mustard).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"母の煮物が懐かしい。","romaji":"Haha no nimono ga natsukashii.","german":"I miss my mother's simmered dishes.","furigana":"はは|にもの|なつ"},{"japanese":"かぼちゃの煮物は甘くて、ご飯によく合う。","romaji":"Kabocha no nimono wa amakute, gohan ni yoku au.","german":"Simmered pumpkin is sweet and goes well with rice.","furigana":"にもの|あま|はん|あ"},{"japanese":"煮物は一晩置くと、味がしみておいしくなる。","romaji":"Nimono wa hitoban oku to, aji ga shimite oishiku naru.","german":"Simmered dishes taste better when left overnight, as the flavor soaks in.","furigana":"にもの|ひとばんお|あじ"}],"notes":"煮物 are dishes simmered in a broth with soy sauce, sugar and mirin, such as 肉じゃが or かぼちゃの煮物 - a classic of home cooking, often associated with nostalgia. One says 煮物を作る; the related verb is 煮る \"to simmer, stew\", intransitive 煮える. The series 〜物 forms dish types by cooking method: 煮物, 焼き物, 炒め物, 揚げ物, 蒸し物.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"野菜の炒め物を作った。","romaji":"Yasai no itamemono o tsukutta.","german":"I made a vegetable stir-fry.","furigana":"やさい|いた|もの|つく"},{"japanese":"炒め物は強火で手早く作るのがコツだ。","romaji":"Itamemono wa tsuyobi de tebayaku tsukuru no ga kotsu da.","german":"The trick to stir-fries is to make them quickly over high heat.","furigana":"いた|もの|つよび|てばや|つく"},{"japanese":"このフライパンは軽くて、炒め物に使いやすい。","romaji":"Kono furaipan wa karukute, itamemono ni tsukaiyasui.","german":"This frying pan is light and easy to use for stir-frying.","furigana":"かる|いた|もの|つか"}],"notes":"炒め物 is a dish fried with little oil over high heat while constantly stirring, such as 野菜炒め or 肉野菜炒め. The verb is 炒める; unlike 焼く \"to grill, pan-fry\" (without much stirring) and 揚げる \"to deep-fry\", 炒める involves constant turning. Common are 炒め物をする and 炒め物に使う (e.g. of a frying pan or an oil).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"揚げ物はカロリーが高い。","romaji":"Agemono wa karorī ga takai.","german":"Deep-fried food is high in calories.","furigana":"あ|もの|たか"},{"japanese":"揚げ物をすると、台所が油で汚れてしまう。","romaji":"Agemono o suru to, daidokoro ga abura de yogorete shimau.","german":"When you deep-fry, the kitchen gets dirty with oil.","furigana":"あ|もの|だいどころ|あぶら|よご"},{"japanese":"夜遅くに揚げ物を食べたら、胃がもたれた。","romaji":"Yoru osoku ni agemono o tabetara, i ga motareta.","german":"When I ate deep-fried food late at night, it sat heavily in my stomach.","furigana":"よるおそ|あ|もの|た|い"}],"notes":"揚げ物 are foods deep-fried in plenty of oil, such as 天ぷら, 唐揚げ, とんかつ or コロッケ. The verb is 揚げる \"to deep-fry\"; deep-fried without breading or batter is 素揚げ. Typical: 揚げ物をする \"to deep-fry\", 揚げ物を控える \"to avoid fried food\", 揚げ物で胃がもたれる \"fried food sits heavily in the stomach\". Not to be confused with 上げる \"to raise\" - same reading, different kanji.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"蒸し物は油を使わないので、ヘルシーだ。","romaji":"Mushimono wa abura o tsukawanai node, herushī da.","german":"Steamed dishes are healthy because they use no oil.","furigana":"む|もの|あぶら|つか"},{"japanese":"会席料理で、焼き物の次に蒸し物が出てきた。","romaji":"Kaiseki ryōri de, yakimono no tsugi ni mushimono ga dete kita.","german":"At the kaiseki meal, a steamed dish came out after the grilled dish.","furigana":"かいせきりょうり|や|もの|つぎ|む|もの|で"},{"japanese":"電子レンジでも簡単な蒸し物が作れる。","romaji":"Denshi renji demo kantan na mushimono ga tsukureru.","german":"You can make easy steamed dishes in the microwave, too.","furigana":"でんし|かんたん|む|もの|つく"}],"notes":"蒸し物 are steamed dishes such as 茶碗蒸し, しゅうまい or steamed vegetables. The verb is 蒸す \"to steam\"; you cook in a 蒸し器 or a bamboo steamer (せいろ). In a Japanese banquet menu (会席料理), 蒸し物 is a course of its own. It always means the dish, not the cooking method - the latter is expressed with the verb: 蒸して作る.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"和食はユネスコの無形文化遺産に登録されている。","romaji":"Washoku wa Yunesuko no mukei bunka isan ni tōroku sarete iru.","german":"Japanese cuisine is registered as an intangible cultural heritage by UNESCO.","furigana":"わしょく|むけいぶんかいさん|とうろく"},{"japanese":"朝ご飯は和食と洋食、どちらになさいますか。","romaji":"Asagohan wa washoku to yōshoku, dochira ni nasaimasu ka.","german":"For breakfast, would you like Japanese or Western food?","furigana":"あさ|はん|わしょく|ようしょく"},{"japanese":"和食はカロリーが低く、健康的だと言われている。","romaji":"Washoku wa karorī ga hikuku, kenkōteki da to iwarete iru.","german":"Japanese food is said to be low in calories and healthy.","furigana":"わしょく|ひく|けんこうてき|い"}],"notes":"和食 is traditional Japanese cuisine: rice, miso soup, fish, pickled vegetables and dishes with a delicate だし flavor. Contrasting terms are 洋食 (\"Western cuisine\") and 中華 (\"Chinese cuisine\"). 和食 is neutral and common in restaurants, cookbooks and media; 日本料理 sounds somewhat more formal and upscale. Typical: 和食の店, 和食派 (\"someone who prefers Japanese food\").","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 44"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この店は洋食が専門だ。","romaji":"Kono mise wa yōshoku ga senmon da.","german":"This restaurant specializes in Western-style food.","furigana":"みせ|ようしょく|せんもん"},{"japanese":"子供の頃、日曜日に家族で洋食屋に行くのが楽しみだった。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, nichiyōbi ni kazoku de yōshokuya ni iku no ga tanoshimi datta.","german":"As a child, I always looked forward to going to a yoshoku restaurant with my family on Sundays.","furigana":"こども|ころ|にちようび|かぞく|ようしょくや|い|たの"},{"japanese":"祖父は洋食より和食のほうが好きだ。","romaji":"Sofu wa yōshoku yori washoku no hō ga suki da.","german":"My grandfather likes Japanese food better than Western food.","furigana":"そふ|ようしょく|わしょく|す"}],"notes":"洋食 refers to Western dishes, but above all to the classics that have become established in Japan in a Japanized form, such as オムライス, ハンバーグ, エビフライ or カレーライス. A 洋食屋 is therefore a cozy restaurant with exactly such dishes, not a French restaurant. For genuine French or Italian cuisine, one rather says フランス料理 or イタリアン. Contrasting terms are 和食 and 中華.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 44"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日の昼は中華を食べに行こう。","romaji":"Kyō no hiru wa chūka o tabe ni ikō.","german":"Let's go eat Chinese for lunch today.","furigana":"きょう|ひる|ちゅうか|た|い"},{"japanese":"横浜の中華街で肉まんを食べた。","romaji":"Yokohama no chūkagai de nikuman o tabeta.","german":"I ate steamed meat buns in Yokohama's Chinatown.","furigana":"よこはま|ちゅうかがい|にく|た"},{"japanese":"和食、洋食、中華の中で、どれが一番好き？","romaji":"Washoku, yōshoku, chūka no naka de, dore ga ichiban suki?","german":"Of Japanese, Western and Chinese food, which do you like best?","furigana":"わしょく|ようしょく|ちゅうか|なか|いちばんす"}],"notes":"中華 is, in everyday speech, the short form of 中華料理 \"Chinese cuisine\": 中華を食べる, 中華料理店, 町中華 (simple neighborhood Chinese restaurants). As a modifier it appears in 中華街 \"Chinatown\", 中華まん or 中華鍋 \"wok\". For the country China itself, people say 中国; 中華 in that sense appears only in fixed names such as 中華人民共和国.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"前菜にサラダを注文した。","romaji":"Zensai ni sarada o chūmon shita.","german":"I ordered a salad as a starter.","furigana":"ぜんさい|ちゅうもん"},{"japanese":"コースは前菜、スープ、メイン、デザートの順で出てきます。","romaji":"Kōsu wa zensai, sūpu, mein, dezāto no jun de dete kimasu.","german":"The course is served in the order of appetizer, soup, main dish and dessert.","furigana":"ぜんさい|じゅん|で"},{"japanese":"前菜の盛り合わせを二人で分けた。","romaji":"Zensai no moriawase o futari de waketa.","german":"The two of us shared an appetizer platter.","furigana":"ぜんさい|も|あ|ふたり|わ"}],"notes":"前菜 is the appetizer of a multi-course meal (コース料理), whether Western, Chinese or Japanese. Typical: 前菜の盛り合わせ \"appetizer platter\", 前菜に〜を頼む. It is usually followed by スープ, メイン or 主菜 and デザート. For small bites to go with drinks at a pub, people say つまみ instead, and for the starter automatically served at an izakaya, お通し.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"主菜は魚か肉から選べます。","romaji":"Shusai wa sakana ka niku kara erabemasu.","german":"You can choose fish or meat as the main dish.","furigana":"しゅさい|さかな|にく|えら"},{"japanese":"今日の主菜は鮭の塩焼きにしよう。","romaji":"Kyō no shusai wa sake no shioyaki ni shiyō.","german":"Let's have salted grilled salmon as today's main dish.","furigana":"きょう|しゅさい|さけ|しおや"},{"japanese":"給食の献立は、主食・主菜・副菜のバランスを考えて作られている。","romaji":"Kyūshoku no kondate wa, shushoku, shusai, fukusai no baransu o kangaete tsukurarete iru.","german":"The school lunch menu is made with a balance of staple, main dish and side dishes in mind.","furigana":"きゅうしょく|こんだて|しゅしょく|しゅさい|ふくさい|かんが|つく"}],"notes":"主菜 is the main dish of a meal, usually with meat, fish, egg or tofu as the protein source. The term comes from nutrition science and cookbooks and belongs to the meal pattern 一汁三菜 (rice, soup, one 主菜 and two 副菜); the rice itself is 主食, not 主菜. In restaurants people more often say メイン or メインディッシュ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"バランスの良い食事には副菜も大切だ。","romaji":"Baransu no yoi shokuji ni wa fukusai mo taisetsu da.","german":"Side dishes are also important for a balanced meal.","furigana":"よ|しょくじ|ふくさい|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"主菜が肉なので、副菜は野菜を中心にした。","romaji":"Shusai ga niku na node, fukusai wa yasai o chūshin ni shita.","german":"Since the main dish is meat, I made the side dish mainly with vegetables.","furigana":"しゅさい|にく|ふくさい|やさい|ちゅうしん"},{"japanese":"週末に副菜を何品か作り置きしておくと便利だ。","romaji":"Shūmatsu ni fukusai o nanpin ka tsukurioki shite oku to benri da.","german":"It's handy to prepare a few side dishes in advance on the weekend.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|ふくさい|なんぴん|つく|お|べんり"}],"notes":"副菜 is the small vegetable, bean or seaweed side dish to the 主菜, such as おひたし, salad or a small 煮物. The term comes from nutrition science and cookbooks; in everyday speech, for everything you eat with rice, people more often say おかず, and for the garnish on the same plate 付け合わせ. Rice counts as 主食, not as 副菜.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"デザートにアイスクリームを頼んだ。","romaji":"Dezāto ni aisu kurīmu o tanonda.","german":"I ordered ice cream for dessert.","furigana":"たの"},{"japanese":"おなかいっぱいだけど、デザートは別腹だよ。","romaji":"Onaka ippai da kedo, dezāto wa betsubara da yo.","german":"I'm full, but there's always room for dessert.","furigana":"べつばら"},{"japanese":"デザートはコーヒーと一緒にお持ちしましょうか。","romaji":"Dezāto wa kōhī to issho ni omochi shimashō ka.","german":"Shall I bring the dessert together with your coffee?","furigana":"いっしょ|も"}],"notes":"デザート is the dessert after a meal, in restaurants often 食後のデザート. Typical: デザートを頼む, デザートにする and the saying デザートは別腹 \"there's always room for dessert\", literally \"dessert goes into a different stomach\". Sweets in general are rather called お菓子 or スイーツ, Japanese sweets 和菓子. \"Desert\" in Japanese is 砂漠, not デザート.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"飲み物は何にしますか。","romaji":"Nomimono wa nani ni shimasu ka.","german":"What would you like to drink?","furigana":"の|もの|なに"},{"japanese":"暑いので、冷たい飲み物を持って行きます。","romaji":"Atsui node, tsumetai nomimono o motte ikimasu.","german":"It's hot, so I'll bring a cold drink.","furigana":"あつ|つめ|の|もの|も|い"}],"notes":"飲み物 is the general word for a drink, whether hot or cold. 飲み物は何にしますか asks which choice one wants. In 飲み物を持って行く, by contrast, the drink is an object one takes along. The word is made up of \"drink\" and \"thing\"; the combination is used as a single noun.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 9"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この地域には多くの旧跡がある。","romaji":"Kono chiiki ni wa ōku no kyūseki ga aru.","german":"There are many historic sites in this area.","furigana":"ちいき|おお|きゅうせき"},{"japanese":"京都の名所旧跡を巡るツアーに参加した。","romaji":"Kyōto no meisho kyūseki o meguru tsuā ni sanka shita.","german":"I joined a tour of Kyoto's famous sights and historic sites.","furigana":"きょうと|めいしょきゅうせき|めぐ|さんか"},{"japanese":"町の旧跡を訪ねて、その歴史について学んだ。","romaji":"Machi no kyūseki o tazunete, sono rekishi ni tsuite mananda.","german":"I visited the historic sites of the town and learned about its history.","furigana":"まち|きゅうせき|たず|れきし|まな"}],"notes":"旧跡 is a place where something of historical importance happened or where traces of it remain. It most often appears in the fixed paired phrase 名所旧跡 (\"famous sights and historic sites\"), typical of travel guides and tourist brochures. The register is written; in conversation people tend to say 歴史のある場所. Archaeological excavation sites and ruins are called 遺跡.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"チェックインは三時からです。","romaji":"Chekkuin wa sanji kara desu.","german":"Check-in is from three o'clock.","furigana":"さんじ"},{"japanese":"空港には出発の二時間前までにチェックインしてください。","romaji":"Kūkō ni wa shuppatsu no nijikan mae made ni chekkuin shite kudasai.","german":"Please check in at the airport no later than two hours before departure.","furigana":"くうこう|しゅっぱつ|にじかんまえ"},{"japanese":"ホテルに着いてすぐチェックインを済ませた。","romaji":"Hoteru ni tsuite sugu chekkuin o sumaseta.","german":"I checked in as soon as I arrived at the hotel.","furigana":"つ|す"}],"notes":"チェックイン is checking in at a hotel or airport. As an intransitive する-verb, the destination takes に: ホテルにチェックインする. Typical: チェックインの手続き, チェックインカウンター, オンラインチェックイン. The counterpart is チェックアウト. For registering at events or doctor's offices, 受付 is more usual.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"チェックアウトは十一時までにお願いします。","romaji":"Chekkuauto wa jūichiji made ni onegai shimasu.","german":"Please check out by eleven o'clock.","furigana":"じゅういちじ|ねが"},{"japanese":"チェックアウトの後も、荷物はフロントで預かってもらえます。","romaji":"Chekkuauto no ato mo, nimotsu wa furonto de azukatte moraemasu.","german":"Even after check-out, the front desk can keep your luggage.","furigana":"あと|にもつ|あず"},{"japanese":"朝八時にホテルをチェックアウトして、駅へ向かった。","romaji":"Asa hachiji ni hoteru o chekkuauto shite, eki e mukatta.","german":"I checked out of the hotel at eight in the morning and headed for the station.","furigana":"あさはちじ|えき|む"}],"notes":"チェックアウト is checking out of a hotel. As a する-verb, the place you leave takes を, as with 出る: ホテルをチェックアウトする. Typical: チェックアウトの時間, レイトチェックアウト (\"late check-out\"), フロントでチェックアウトする. The counterpart is チェックイン. In online shopping, however, the payment process is usually called 購入手続き.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大きなスーツケースを一つ預けた。","romaji":"Ōkina sūtsukēsu o hitotsu azuketa.","german":"I checked one large suitcase.","furigana":"おお|ひと|あず"},{"japanese":"スーツケースに服を入れました。","romaji":"Sūtsukēsu ni fuku o iremashita.","german":"I put my clothes in the suitcase.","furigana":"ふく|い"},{"japanese":"空港で私のスーツケースが出てきませんでした。","romaji":"Kūkō de watashi no sūtsukēsu ga dete kimasen deshita.","german":"My suitcase didn't come out at the airport.","furigana":"くうこう|わたし|で"}],"notes":"スーツケース is the (usually hard-shell) travel or rolling suitcase: スーツケースに服を入れる, スーツケースを預ける \"to check the suitcase\", スーツケースを引く \"to pull the suitcase\". Synonymous terms you hear are キャリーケース or キャリーバッグ. A bag in general is かばん, a backpack リュック.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"パスポートの有効期限を確認してください。","romaji":"Pasupōto no yūkō kigen o kakunin shite kudasai.","german":"Please check the expiration date of your passport.","furigana":"ゆうこうきげん|かくにん"},{"japanese":"空港でパスポートをなくして、大変な目にあった。","romaji":"Kūkō de pasupōto o nakushite, taihen na me ni atta.","german":"I lost my passport at the airport and had a terrible time.","furigana":"くうこう|たいへん|め"},{"japanese":"初めての海外旅行のために、パスポートを申請した。","romaji":"Hajimete no kaigai ryokō no tame ni, pasupōto o shinsei shita.","german":"I applied for a passport for my first trip abroad.","furigana":"はじ|かいがいりょこう|しんせい"}],"notes":"パスポート is the passport; officially and on forms it is called 旅券. Typical combinations: パスポートを申請する (\"to apply for\"), パスポートを更新する (\"to renew\"), パスポートを提示する (\"to show\") and パスポートの有効期限 (\"period of validity\"). An ID in general is 身分証明書; in Japan, a driver's license (免許証) is often used for this.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 14 (source: N5; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"東京への直行便はありますか。","romaji":"Tōkyō e no chokkōbin wa arimasu ka.","german":"Is there a direct flight to Tokyo?","furigana":"とうきょう|ちょっこうびん"},{"japanese":"直行便がないので、ソウルで乗り継ぐことにした。","romaji":"Chokkōbin ga nai node, Souru de noritsugu koto ni shita.","german":"Since there was no direct flight, I decided to transfer in Seoul.","furigana":"ちょっこうびん|の|つ"},{"japanese":"直行便は高いけど、時間を考えるとそのほうが楽だ。","romaji":"Chokkōbin wa takai kedo, jikan o kangaeru to sono hō ga raku da.","german":"Direct flights are expensive, but considering the time, they are easier.","furigana":"ちょっこうびん|たか|じかん|かんが|らく"}],"notes":"直行便 is a direct flight without transfers; the opposite is 乗り継ぎ便 or 経由便 (\"via ...\"). Typical: 〜行きの直行便, 〜への直行便, 直行便で行く. The word can also refer to direct bus or ferry connections. It is based on 直行 (\"to go directly\"), which is also heard in everyday speech: 会社に直行する (\"to go straight to the office\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"グリーン車は座席が広くて快適だ。","romaji":"Gurīnsha wa zaseki ga hirokute kaiteki da.","german":"The seats in the Green Car are spacious and comfortable.","furigana":"しゃ|ざせき|ひろ|かいてき"},{"japanese":"出張のときは、会社がグリーン車の料金を出してくれる。","romaji":"Shutchō no toki wa, kaisha ga gurīnsha no ryōkin o dashite kureru.","german":"On business trips, the company pays the Green Car fare.","furigana":"しゅっちょう|かいしゃ|しゃ|りょうきん|だ"},{"japanese":"グリーン車に乗るには、乗車券のほかにグリーン券が必要だ。","romaji":"Gurīnsha ni noru ni wa, jōshaken no hoka ni gurīnken ga hitsuyō da.","german":"To ride the Green Car, you need a Green Car ticket in addition to your regular ticket.","furigana":"しゃ|の|じょうしゃけん|けん|ひつよう"}],"notes":"グリーン車 is the first-class car on JR; the name comes from the green marking these cars used to have, and today a green four-leaf-clover symbol marks them. You need a グリーン券 in addition to the 乗車券. The ordinary cars are called 普通車, with 自由席 and 指定席; some Shinkansen also have the even more luxurious グランクラス. Typical: グリーン車に乗る, グリーン車を予約する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本語が話せるガイドを頼んだ。","romaji":"Nihongo ga hanaseru gaido o tanonda.","german":"I hired a guide who speaks Japanese.","furigana":"にほんご|はな|たの"},{"japanese":"ガイドさんの説明のおかげで、お寺の歴史がよくわかった。","romaji":"Gaido-san no setsumei no okage de, otera no rekishi ga yoku wakatta.","german":"Thanks to the guide's explanations, I understood the history of the temple well.","furigana":"せつめい|てら|れきし"},{"japanese":"友達が日本に来たとき、東京をガイドしてあげた。","romaji":"Tomodachi ga Nihon ni kita toki, Tōkyō o gaido shite ageta.","german":"When my friend came to Japan, I showed him around Tokyo.","furigana":"ともだち|にほん|き|とうきょう"}],"notes":"ガイド is above all a person who guides tourists (観光ガイド, ガイドさん, ガイド付きツアー); it can also mean a printed guide, usually explicitly ガイドブック. As a transitive する-verb: 町をガイドする (\"to show someone around the town\"). The general Japanese word for \"to guide, to show, to give information\" is 案内する, which is even more common in everyday speech.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 50"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"卒業式で記念品をもらった。","romaji":"Sotsugyōshiki de kinenhin o moratta.","german":"I received a commemorative gift at the graduation ceremony.","furigana":"そつぎょうしき|きねんひん"},{"japanese":"マラソン大会の参加者全員に記念品が配られた。","romaji":"Marason taikai no sankasha zen'in ni kinenhin ga kubarareta.","german":"A commemorative gift was handed out to all participants of the marathon.","furigana":"たいかい|さんかしゃぜんいん|きねんひん|くば"},{"japanese":"退職する先輩に、みんなで記念品を贈った。","romaji":"Taishoku suru senpai ni, minna de kinenhin o okutta.","german":"Everyone together gave a commemorative gift to our senior colleague who was retiring.","furigana":"たいしょく|せんぱい|きねんひん|おく"}],"notes":"記念品 is a commemorative gift for an event, for example at graduation ceremonies, company anniversaries, farewells, or as a participation gift at events; it is usually presented: 記念品を贈る, 記念品をもらう, 記念品を配る. A souvenir brought back from a trip for other people is お土産 instead; a personal keepsake is more often called 思い出の品.","levelNote":"Compound: 記念 (N3) + 品 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"古い観念にとらわれてはいけない。","romaji":"Furui kannen ni torawarete wa ikenai.","german":"You must not be bound by old ideas.","furigana":"ふる|かんねん"},{"japanese":"彼は時間の観念がなくて、いつも遅刻する。","romaji":"Kare wa jikan no kannen ga nakute, itsumo chikoku suru.","german":"He has no sense of time and is always late.","furigana":"かれ|じかん|かんねん|ちこく"},{"japanese":"もう逃げられないと分かり、犯人はついに観念した。","romaji":"Mō nigerarenai to wakari, hannin wa tsui ni kannen shita.","german":"Realizing he could no longer escape, the culprit finally resigned himself to it.","furigana":"に|わ|はんにん|かんねん"}],"notes":"観念 means \"idea, notion, concept\", especially in 固定観念 (\"fixed idea, stereotype\"). With の before it, it denotes a sense of something: 時間の観念がない (\"to have no sense of time\"), 金銭観念 (\"attitude toward money\"). As a する-verb, 観念する means \"to resign oneself to one's fate, to give up\": もう観念しろ! The word is rather literary; in everyday speech people usually say 考え or アイデア for \"idea\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"論拠を明確に示して議論を進めた。","romaji":"Ronkyo o meikaku ni shimeshite giron o susumeta.","german":"He advanced the discussion by clearly presenting his grounds.","furigana":"ろんきょ|めいかく|しめ|ぎろん|すす"},{"japanese":"この論文は、結論は面白いが論拠が弱い。","romaji":"Kono ronbun wa, ketsuron wa omoshiroi ga ronkyo ga yowai.","german":"The conclusion of this paper is interesting, but the supporting argument is weak.","furigana":"ろんぶん|けつろん|おもしろ|ろんきょ|よわ"},{"japanese":"彼は統計データを論拠に、制度の見直しを主張した。","romaji":"Kare wa tōkei dēta o ronkyo ni, seido no minaoshi o shuchō shita.","german":"Using statistical data as his basis, he argued for a review of the system.","furigana":"かれ|とうけい|ろんきょ|せいど|みなお|しゅちょう"}],"notes":"論拠 is the foundation of an argument, that is, the evidence or reasons a claim rests on. It is literary and typical of essays, debates and specialist texts: 論拠を示す, 論拠が弱い, 〜を論拠に主張する. In conversation people say 根拠 (\"basis, evidence\") more often, which is also used outside of arguments (根拠のないうわさ). The claim itself is called 主張.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"犯行の動機はまだ不明だ。","romaji":"Hankō no dōki wa mada fumei da.","german":"The motive for the crime is still unknown.","furigana":"はんこう|どうき|ふめい"},{"japanese":"面接で志望動機を聞かれて、うまく答えられなかった。","romaji":"Mensetsu de shibō dōki o kikarete, umaku kotaerarenakatta.","german":"I was asked in the interview why I was applying and couldn't answer well.","furigana":"めんせつ|しぼうどうき|き|こた"},{"japanese":"日本語を始めた動機は、アニメが好きだったことです。","romaji":"Nihongo o hajimeta dōki wa, anime ga suki datta koto desu.","german":"My motivation for starting Japanese was that I liked anime.","furigana":"にほんご|はじ|どうき|す"}],"notes":"動機 is the reason behind an action. Typical are 犯行の動機 (\"motive for the crime\") in the news and 志望動機 (\"reason for applying\") in interviews and application documents. One says 動機は〜だ, 〜が動機で, 不純な動機 (\"impure motive\"). \"Motivation\" in the sense of drive and willingness, by contrast, is やる気 or モチベーション. The homophone is 動悸 (\"heart palpitations\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この政策の狙いは景気回復にある。","romaji":"Kono seisaku no nerai wa keiki kaifuku ni aru.","german":"The aim of this policy is economic recovery.","furigana":"せいさく|ねら|けいきかいふく"},{"japanese":"狙いを定めて、ボールを蹴った。","romaji":"Nerai o sadamete, bōru o ketta.","german":"I took careful aim and kicked the ball.","furigana":"ねら|さだ|け"},{"japanese":"新商品は狙い通り、若い人の間で人気が出た。","romaji":"Shinshōhin wa nerai dōri, wakai hito no aida de ninki ga deta.","german":"The new product became popular among young people, just as intended.","furigana":"しんしょうひん|ねら|どお|わか|ひと|あいだ|にんき|で"}],"notes":"狙い is the noun of 狙う (\"to aim, to have one's eye on something\"). Concretely it is aiming with a weapon or ball (狙いを定める \"to take careful aim\"); figuratively it is the real intention behind a plan: 〜の狙いは…にある, 狙い通り (\"as intended\"). It often implies that the intention is strategic or hidden: 本当の狙いは何だ? More neutral are 目的 (\"purpose\") and 目標 (\"goal, target\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"相手チームの戦術を分析した。","romaji":"Aite chīmu no senjutsu o bunseki shita.","german":"We analyzed the opposing team's tactics.","furigana":"あいて|せんじゅつ|ぶんせき"},{"japanese":"後半から監督は戦術を変え、守りを固めた。","romaji":"Kōhan kara kantoku wa senjutsu o kae, mamori o katameta.","german":"From the second half, the coach changed tactics and strengthened the defense.","furigana":"こうはん|かんとく|せんじゅつ|か|まも|かた"},{"japanese":"戦略が正しくても、戦術を誤れば勝てない。","romaji":"Senryaku ga tadashikute mo, senjutsu o ayamareba katenai.","german":"Even with the right strategy, you can't win if the tactics are wrong.","furigana":"せんりゃく|ただ|せんじゅつ|あやま|か"}],"notes":"戦術 means tactics, i.e. concrete procedure in a particular situation - in war, sports, election campaigns or business. It must be distinguished from 戦略 (\"strategy\"), the overarching, long-term plan. Typical: 戦術を立てる, 戦術を変える, 戦術を練る, 選挙戦術. More colloquial is 作戦 (\"plan, approach\"), which is also used for private undertakings.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"職業訓練で実践的な技能を身につけた。","romaji":"Shokugyō kunren de jissenteki na ginō o mi ni tsuketa.","german":"I acquired practical skills through vocational training.","furigana":"しょくぎょうくんれん|じっせんてき|ぎのう|み"},{"japanese":"この資格を取るには、筆記試験と技能試験に合格する必要がある。","romaji":"Kono shikaku o toru ni wa, hikki shiken to ginō shiken ni gōkaku suru hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"To obtain this qualification, you must pass a written exam and a practical exam.","furigana":"しかく|と|ひっきしけん|ぎのうしけん|ごうかく|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"その職人は長い年月をかけて技能を磨いてきた。","romaji":"Sono shokunin wa nagai nengetsu o kakete ginō o migaite kita.","german":"That craftsman has honed his skills over many years.","furigana":"しょくにん|なが|ねんげつ|ぎのう|みが"}],"notes":"技能 is a practical skill acquired through practice, especially manual or occupational: 技能を身につける, 技能を磨く, 技能検定 (\"skills test\"), 技能実習生 (\"technical intern\"). 技術 stresses technique as knowledge and procedures, whereas 技能 means an individual's ability to carry something out in practice. In everyday speech you also hear スキル; 能力 is \"ability\" in general.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"失敗から教訓を得ることが大切だ。","romaji":"Shippai kara kyōkun o eru koto ga taisetsu da.","german":"It is important to draw lessons from failure.","furigana":"しっぱい|きょうくん|え|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"震災の教訓を生かして、町の防災計画が見直された。","romaji":"Shinsai no kyōkun o ikashite, machi no bōsai keikaku ga minaosareta.","german":"Drawing on the lessons of the earthquake, the town's disaster prevention plan was revised.","furigana":"しんさい|きょうくん|い|まち|ぼうさいけいかく|みなお"},{"japanese":"この昔話には、うそをついてはいけないという教訓がある。","romaji":"Kono mukashibanashi ni wa, uso o tsuite wa ikenai to iu kyōkun ga aru.","german":"The moral of this old tale is that one must not lie.","furigana":"むかしばなし|きょうくん"}],"notes":"教訓 is a lesson drawn from experience, often from mistakes or disasters. Typical: 〜から教訓を得る/学ぶ, 〜を教訓にする (\"to take something as a lesson\"), 教訓を生かす (\"to make use of the lessons\"). The moral of a story is also called 教訓. A \"lesson\" in the sense of a teaching unit in a textbook, by contrast, is 課 or レッスン.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自分の行動を反省して改めた。","romaji":"Jibun no kōdō o hansei shite aratameta.","german":"Ich habe mein Verhalten reflektiert und geändert.","furigana":"じぶん|こうどう|はんせい|あらた"},{"japanese":"昨日は言いすぎました。深く反省しています。","romaji":"Kinō wa iisugimashita. Fukaku hansei shite imasu.","german":"Ich bin gestern zu weit gegangen. Das tut mir aufrichtig leid.","furigana":"きのう|い|ふか|はんせい"},{"japanese":"イベントの後、反省会を開いて改善点を話し合った。","romaji":"Ibento no ato, hanseikai o hiraite kaizenten o hanashiatta.","german":"Nach der Veranstaltung hielten wir eine Nachbesprechung ab und sprachen über Verbesserungsmöglichkeiten.","furigana":"あと|はんせいかい|ひら|かいぜんてん|はな|あ"}],"notes":"反省 heißt, über das eigene Verhalten nachzudenken, meist mit dem Eingeständnis eines Fehlers und dem Vorsatz zur Besserung – es liegt näher an „einsehen, bereuen“ als am neutralen deutschen „Reflexion“. Als transitives する-Verb: 〜を反省する, 深く反省する; 反省しています ist eine häufige Entschuldigungsformel. Nach Projekten hält man ein 反省会 („Nachbesprechung“) ab. 後悔 dagegen ist bloßes Bedauern ohne Besserungsvorsatz.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"最終的な決定は来週行われる。","romaji":"Saishūteki na kettei wa raishū okonawareru.","german":"The final decision will be made next week.","furigana":"さいしゅうてき|けってい|らいしゅうおこな"},{"japanese":"次のオリンピックの開催地が決定した。","romaji":"Tsugi no Orinpikku no kaisaichi ga kettei shita.","german":"The host city of the next Olympics has been decided.","furigana":"つぎ|かいさいち|けってい"},{"japanese":"会議で新しい方針を決定しました。","romaji":"Kaigi de atarashii hōshin o kettei shimashita.","german":"We decided on a new policy at the meeting.","furigana":"かいぎ|あたら|ほうしん|けってい"}],"notes":"決定 is a (often official) decision or resolution, typical for organizations, committees and the news: 決定を下す (\"to make a decision\"), 正式に決定する, 決定事項. As a する-verb it is both transitive and intransitive: 方針を決定する / 開催地が決定した. For personal everyday decisions, people more often say 決める/決まる; 決心 is a person's inner resolve.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は家族のために多くの犠牲を払った。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kazoku no tame ni ōku no gisei o haratta.","german":"She made many sacrifices for her family.","furigana":"かのじょ|かぞく|おお|ぎせい|はら"},{"japanese":"仕事のために家族との時間を犠牲にしたくない。","romaji":"Shigoto no tame ni kazoku to no jikan o gisei ni shitakunai.","german":"I don't want to sacrifice time with my family for work.","furigana":"しごと|かぞく|じかん|ぎせい"},{"japanese":"その事故で多くの人が犠牲になった。","romaji":"Sono jiko de ōku no hito ga gisei ni natta.","german":"Many people fell victim in that accident.","furigana":"じこ|おお|ひと|ぎせい"}],"notes":"犠牲 is the sacrifice that one makes or that is demanded. Typical: 犠牲を払う (\"to make sacrifices\"), 〜を犠牲にする (\"to sacrifice something\"), 〜の犠牲になる (\"to fall victim to something\", often \"to lose one's life\"). People who die in accidents or disasters are called 犠牲者. The victim of a crime or damage who survives, by contrast, is 被害者.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"成功には大きな代償が伴うこともある。","romaji":"Seikō ni wa ōkina daishō ga tomonau koto mo aru.","german":"Success can sometimes come at a high price.","furigana":"せいこう|おお|だいしょう|ともな"},{"japanese":"若いころ無理をした代償を、今になって払っている。","romaji":"Wakai koro muri o shita daishō o, ima ni natte haratte iru.","german":"Now I am paying the price for overdoing it when I was young.","furigana":"わか|むり|だいしょう|いま|はら"},{"japanese":"便利さの代償に、私たちは多くの個人情報を差し出している。","romaji":"Benrisa no daishō ni, watashitachi wa ōku no kojin jōhō o sashidashite iru.","german":"In exchange for convenience, we hand over a great deal of personal information.","furigana":"べんり|だいしょう|わたし|おお|こじんじょうほう|さ|だ"}],"notes":"代償 is the \"price\" one pays for something, today mostly in a figurative sense: a sacrifice or disadvantage one must accept for a goal or because of a mistake. Typical: 代償を払う \"to pay the price\", 大きな代償 and 〜の代償として \"as the price for ...\". In its original meaning \"compensation in kind\" it is rare; for financial compensation, 補償 or 賠償 is used today. It is rather literary and common in news and commentary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"被害者に対して適切な補償が行われた。","romaji":"Higaisha ni taishite tekisetsu na hoshō ga okonawareta.","german":"Appropriate compensation was paid to the victims.","furigana":"ひがいしゃ|たい|てきせつ|ほしょう|おこな"},{"japanese":"工事で休業した店に、売り上げの補償が出た。","romaji":"Kōji de kyūgyō shita mise ni, uriage no hoshō ga deta.","german":"The shop that closed because of construction work received compensation for lost sales.","furigana":"こうじ|きゅうぎょう|みせ|う|あ|ほしょう|で"},{"japanese":"この保険は、旅行中に盗まれた荷物の損害も補償してくれる。","romaji":"Kono hoken wa, ryokōchū ni nusumareta nimotsu no songai mo hoshō shite kureru.","german":"This insurance also covers damage to luggage stolen during a trip.","furigana":"ほけん|りょこうちゅう|ぬす|にもつ|そんがい|ほしょう"}],"notes":"補償 is compensation for damage or loss suffered, even when no one acted unlawfully, for example in cases of expropriation, construction work, or through insurance. Typical: 補償金, 損失を補償する, 補償を求める, 補償が出る. Read the same but different: 保証 \"guarantee, surety\" and 保障 \"protection, security\" (社会保障). For liability for fault, 賠償 is more often used.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"裁判所は賠償金の支払いを命じた。","romaji":"Saibansho wa baishōkin no shiharai o meijita.","german":"The court ordered payment of compensation.","furigana":"さいばんしょ|ばいしょうきん|しはら|めい"},{"japanese":"事故を起こした会社は、住民に損害を賠償しなければならない。","romaji":"Jiko o okoshita kaisha wa, jūmin ni songai o baishō shinakereba naranai.","german":"The company that caused the accident must compensate the residents for the damage.","furigana":"じこ|お|かいしゃ|じゅうみん|そんがい|ばいしょう"},{"japanese":"借りたカメラを壊して、修理代を賠償するよう求められた。","romaji":"Karita kamera o kowashite, shūridai o baishō suru yō motomerareta.","german":"I broke the camera I had borrowed and was asked to pay for the repairs.","furigana":"か|こわ|しゅうりだい|ばいしょう|もと"}],"notes":"賠償 is compensation that someone must pay because they are responsible for damage - mostly in a legal context. Set combinations: 損害賠償 \"damages\", 損害賠償を請求する \"to claim damages\", 賠償金, 賠償責任 \"liability\"; historically also 戦争賠償 \"war reparations\". Unlike 補償, 賠償 stresses the fault of the payer. As a する-verb it is transitive: 損害を賠償する.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この方法の利点は簡単なことだ。","romaji":"Kono hōhō no riten wa kantan na koto da.","german":"The advantage of this method is that it is simple.","furigana":"ほうほう|りてん|かんたん"},{"japanese":"この車には燃費がいいという利点がある。","romaji":"Kono kuruma ni wa nenpi ga ii to iu riten ga aru.","german":"This car has the advantage of good fuel economy.","furigana":"くるま|ねんぴ|りてん"},{"japanese":"都会に住むことには、利点もあれば欠点もある。","romaji":"Tokai ni sumu koto ni wa, riten mo areba ketten mo aru.","german":"Living in a city has both advantages and disadvantages.","furigana":"とかい|す|りてん|けってん"}],"notes":"利点 is an \"advantage\" of a thing, method or situation, factual and neutral. Typical: 〜の利点は…ことだ, 〜という利点がある, 利点を生かす. For a person's strengths one rather says 長所 or 強み; colloquially 利点 is often replaced by メリット. Antonyms are 欠点, 難点 or デメリット.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この計画にはいくつかの欠点がある。","romaji":"Kono keikaku ni wa ikutsuka no ketten ga aru.","german":"This plan has several drawbacks.","furigana":"けいかく|けってん"},{"japanese":"彼の欠点は、時間を守らないことだ。","romaji":"Kare no ketten wa, jikan o mamoranai koto da.","german":"His fault is that he isn't punctual.","furigana":"かれ|けってん|じかん|まも"},{"japanese":"欠点のない人間なんていないよ。","romaji":"Ketten no nai ningen nante inai yo.","german":"There's no such thing as a person without flaws.","furigana":"けってん|にんげん"}],"notes":"欠点 is a \"weakness\" or \"shortcoming\" - of people as well as of things, plans or products. Typical: 欠点がある, 欠点を直す, 欠点を補う, 欠点のない人はいない. 短所 is used almost only for character traits and usually appears in the pair 長所と短所; 欠陥, by contrast, is a (technical) defect, as in 欠陥商品. Rarely, 欠点 also means a failing grade in an exam.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自分の長所を面接でアピールした。","romaji":"Jibun no chōsho o mensetsu de apīru shita.","german":"I emphasized my strengths in the interview.","furigana":"じぶん|ちょうしょ|めんせつ"},{"japanese":"誰にでも長所と短所がある。","romaji":"Dare ni demo chōsho to tansho ga aru.","german":"Everyone has strengths and weaknesses.","furigana":"だれ|ちょうしょ|たんしょ"},{"japanese":"このカメラの長所は、軽くて持ち運びやすいところだ。","romaji":"Kono kamera no chōsho wa, karukute mochihakobiyasui tokoro da.","german":"The merit of this camera is that it is light and easy to carry.","furigana":"ちょうしょ|かる|も|はこ"}],"notes":"長所 is a \"strength\" or \"merit\", above all in a person's character; the opposite is 短所, often as the pair 長所と短所. A classic question in job interviews: あなたの長所は何ですか. Also typical are 長所を生かす and 長所を伸ばす. For things, 長所 is possible, but for methods or situations 利点 or メリット fits better; 強み stresses a competitive advantage.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"短所を克服するために努力している。","romaji":"Tansho o kokufuku suru tame ni doryoku shite iru.","german":"I am working hard to overcome my weaknesses.","furigana":"たんしょ|こくふく|どりょく"},{"japanese":"面接で「あなたの短所は何ですか」と聞かれた。","romaji":"Mensetsu de \"anata no tansho wa nan desu ka\" to kikareta.","german":"In the interview I was asked, \"What is your weakness?\"","furigana":"めんせつ|たんしょ|なん|き"},{"japanese":"短所も、見方を変えれば長所になる。","romaji":"Tansho mo, mikata o kaereba chōsho ni naru.","german":"Even a weakness can become a strength if you look at it differently.","furigana":"たんしょ|みかた|か|ちょうしょ"}],"notes":"短所 is a \"weakness\" in character or behavior, the opposite of 長所. It often appears in the pair 長所と短所 and is a typical job-interview question. Typical: 短所を直す, 短所を克服する, 短所を補う. For defects in things or plans, 欠点 is more usual; colloquially one also says 弱点 or ダメなところ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この素材の特性を生かして製品を作った。","romaji":"Kono sozai no tokusei o ikashite seihin o tsukutta.","german":"The product was made by taking advantage of the properties of this material.","furigana":"そざい|とくせい|い|せいひん|つく"},{"japanese":"この金属には、熱に強いという特性がある。","romaji":"Kono kinzoku ni wa, netsu ni tsuyoi to iu tokusei ga aru.","german":"This metal has the property of being resistant to heat.","furigana":"きんぞく|ねつ|つよ|とくせい"},{"japanese":"子ども一人一人の特性に合わせた教え方が大切だ。","romaji":"Kodomo hitori hitori no tokusei ni awaseta oshiekata ga taisetsu da.","german":"It is important to teach in a way that suits each child's individual characteristics.","furigana":"こ|ひとりひとり|とくせい|あ|おし|かた|たいせつ"}],"notes":"特性 is a \"characteristic property\" that something has by nature, often of materials and technology, but also of people (for example, learning dispositions of children). Typical: 〜の特性を生かす, 〜という特性がある, 特性に合わせる. 特徴 is instead a conspicuous, distinguishing feature, and 性質 is general nature or constitution. Somewhat technical, less common in everyday conversation.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"水は温度によって性質が変わる。","romaji":"Mizu wa ondo ni yotte seishitsu ga kawaru.","german":"The properties of water change with temperature.","furigana":"みず|おんど|せいしつ|か"},{"japanese":"彼は穏やかな性質で、めったに怒らない。","romaji":"Kare wa odayaka na seishitsu de, metta ni okoranai.","german":"He is of a gentle nature and rarely gets angry.","furigana":"かれ|おだ|せいしつ|おこ"},{"japanese":"問題の性質上、すぐにはお答えできません。","romaji":"Mondai no seishitsu jō, sugu ni wa okotae dekimasen.","german":"Because of the nature of the problem, I cannot answer you right away.","furigana":"もんだい|せいしつじょう|こた"}],"notes":"性質 has two meanings: the nature of a thing (chemical properties, the nature of a problem) and the innate character of a person or animal. For a person's character, 性格 is more usual in everyday speech; 性質 sounds somewhat more fundamental here (\"by nature\"). Typical: 〜という性質がある, 性質が変わる, 問題の性質上 \"because of the nature of the problem\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"理想と現実の間には大きな差がある。","romaji":"Risō to genjitsu no aida ni wa ōkina sa ga aru.","german":"There is a big difference between the ideal and reality.","furigana":"りそう|げんじつ|あいだ|おお|さ"},{"japanese":"彼女は理想が高くて、なかなか結婚相手が見つからない。","romaji":"Kanojo wa risō ga takakute, nakanaka kekkon aite ga mitsukaranai.","german":"Her standards are so high that she just can't find anyone to marry.","furigana":"かのじょ|りそう|たか|けっこんあいて|み"},{"japanese":"自分の理想を実現するために、会社を立ち上げた。","romaji":"Jibun no risō o jitsugen suru tame ni, kaisha o tachiageta.","german":"He founded a company to realize his ideals.","furigana":"じぶん|りそう|じつげん|かいしゃ|た|あ"}],"notes":"理想 is the \"ideal\", often in contrast to 現実 \"reality\": 理想と現実. Typical: 理想の〜 \"dream ..., ideal ...\" (理想の家, 理想の相手), 理想が高い \"to have high standards\", 理想を実現する. The adjective is 理想的(な) \"ideal\". 夢 is more personal and emotional, whereas 理想 is more a standard of how something should be.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 23"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼はいつまでも幻想を抱いている。","romaji":"Kare wa itsu made mo gensō o idaite iru.","german":"He still harbors illusions.","furigana":"かれ|げんそう|いだ"},{"japanese":"「努力すれば必ず報われる」というのは幻想かもしれない。","romaji":"\"Doryoku sureba kanarazu mukuwareru\" to iu no wa gensō kamo shirenai.","german":"The idea that effort will always be rewarded may be an illusion.","furigana":"どりょく|かなら|むく|げんそう"},{"japanese":"雪に包まれた町は、幻想的な雰囲気だった。","romaji":"Yuki ni tsutsumareta machi wa, gensōteki na fun'iki datta.","german":"The snow-covered town had a fantastical atmosphere.","furigana":"ゆき|つつ|まち|げんそうてき|ふんいき"}],"notes":"幻想 is an \"illusion\", a beautiful but unrealistic notion. Typical: 〜に幻想を抱く \"to have illusions about ...\", 幻想が崩れる / 幻想を捨てる. The adjective 幻想的(な), on the other hand, is positive and means \"fairy-tale-like, dreamlike\" (幻想的な景色). By contrast, 空想 is harmless daydreaming and 妄想 is a delusion.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"空想の世界に浸るのが好きだ。","romaji":"Kūsō no sekai ni hitaru no ga suki da.","german":"I like to immerse myself in a world of fantasy.","furigana":"くうそう|せかい|ひた|す"},{"japanese":"子どものころ、空を飛ぶ自分をよく空想した。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, sora o tobu jibun o yoku kūsō shita.","german":"As a child, I often imagined myself flying through the sky.","furigana":"こ|そら|と|じぶん|くうそう"},{"japanese":"ドラゴンは空想上の生き物だ。","romaji":"Doragon wa kūsōjō no ikimono da.","german":"Dragons are imaginary creatures.","furigana":"くうそうじょう|い|もの"}],"notes":"空想 is a harmless \"daydream\", a fantasy about something that does not (yet) exist. As a する-verb it is transitive: 〜を空想する. Typical: 空想の世界, 空想にふける, 空想上の動物; 空想科学 used to be a word for science fiction. 想像 is more neutral and general, \"to imagine\"; 妄想 has a pathological or ridiculous connotation.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"それは彼の妄想にすぎない。","romaji":"Sore wa kare no mōsō ni suginai.","german":"That is nothing but his delusion.","furigana":"かれ|もうそう"},{"japanese":"病気のせいで、誰かに見られているという妄想に苦しんでいる。","romaji":"Byōki no sei de, dareka ni mirarete iru to iu mōsō ni kurushinde iru.","german":"Because of his illness, he suffers from the delusion that someone is watching him.","furigana":"びょうき|だれ|み|もうそう|くる"},{"japanese":"宝くじが当たったら何をしようかと、つい妄想してしまう。","romaji":"Takarakuji ga atattara nani o shiyō ka to, tsui mōsō shite shimau.","german":"I can't help fantasizing about what I would do if I won the lottery.","furigana":"たから|あ|なに|もうそう"}],"notes":"妄想 is a \"delusion\" or \"figment of the imagination\" that has nothing to do with reality. In medicine, for example, 被害妄想 \"persecution delusion\". Colloquially, especially among young people, 妄想(する) also refers self-ironically to uninhibitedly picturing wish scenarios. Distinction: 空想 is harmless daydreaming, 幻想 is a beautiful illusion. Typical: 〜にすぎない, 妄想にとらわれる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"想像以上に難しい問題だった。","romaji":"Sōzō ijō ni muzukashii mondai datta.","german":"It was a more difficult problem than I had imagined.","furigana":"そうぞういじょう|むずか|もんだい"},{"japanese":"十年後の自分を想像してみてください。","romaji":"Jūnen go no jibun o sōzō shite mite kudasai.","german":"Please try to imagine yourself ten years from now.","furigana":"じゅうねんご|じぶん|そうぞう"},{"japanese":"彼が先生になるなんて、想像もしなかった。","romaji":"Kare ga sensei ni naru nante, sōzō mo shinakatta.","german":"I never imagined that he would become a teacher.","furigana":"かれ|せんせい|そうぞう"}],"notes":"想像 is the general word for \"imagination\" and \"to imagine something\"; as a する-verb it is transitive: 〜を想像する. Common: 想像以上 \"more than expected,\" 想像通り, 想像がつく / 想像もつかない \"can / cannot imagine,\" 想像力 \"imagination.\" Not to be confused with the identically read 創造 \"creation.\"","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"芸術は創造の力から生まれる。","romaji":"Geijutsu wa sōzō no chikara kara umareru.","german":"Art is born from the power of creation.","furigana":"げいじゅつ|そうぞう|ちから|う"},{"japanese":"神が世界を創造したと信じている人も多い。","romaji":"Kami ga sekai o sōzō shita to shinjite iru hito mo ōi.","german":"Many people believe that God created the world.","furigana":"かみ|せかい|そうぞう|しん|ひと|おお"},{"japanese":"わが社は新しい価値の創造を目指しています。","romaji":"Waga sha wa atarashii kachi no sōzō o mezashite imasu.","german":"Our company aims at the creation of new value.","furigana":"しゃ|あたら|かち|そうぞう|めざ"}],"notes":"創造 is the \"creating\" of something completely new, also creation by God; as a する-verb it is transitive. It sounds elevated and often appears in art, religion and corporate mission statements: 創造力, 創造性, 創造的(な), 新しい価値の創造. In everyday speech people simply say 作る, and for artistic works 創作. The opposite is 破壊; beware of the homophone 想像 \"imagination\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この小説は実話から着想を得た。","romaji":"Kono shōsetsu wa jitsuwa kara chakusō o eta.","german":"This novel took its inspiration from a true story.","furigana":"しょうせつ|じつわ|ちゃくそう|え"},{"japanese":"着想は面白いが、実現するのは難しそうだ。","romaji":"Chakusō wa omoshiroi ga, jitsugen suru no wa muzukashisō da.","german":"The idea is interesting, but it seems difficult to realize.","furigana":"ちゃくそう|おもしろ|じつげん|むずか"},{"japanese":"この椅子のデザインは、鳥の羽に着想を得たそうだ。","romaji":"Kono isu no dezain wa, tori no hane ni chakusō o eta sō da.","german":"The design of this chair is said to be inspired by bird feathers.","furigana":"いす|とり|はね|ちゃくそう|え"}],"notes":"着想 is the original idea or basic thought from which a work, invention or plan arises. It is almost always found in the phrase 〜から／〜に着想を得る \"to draw inspiration from ...\" or as a subject: 着想が面白い / 着想がユニークだ. Rather literary; in everyday speech people say アイデア or 思いつき. 発想, by contrast, refers more to the way of thinking, how one comes up with ideas.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しいプロジェクトの構想を練っている。","romaji":"Atarashii purojekuto no kōsō o nette iru.","german":"I am working out the concept for a new project.","furigana":"あたら|こうそう|ね"},{"japanese":"新しい駅を中心とした町づくりの構想が発表された。","romaji":"Atarashii eki o chūshin to shita machizukuri no kōsō ga happyō sareta.","german":"A concept for urban development centered on the new station was announced.","furigana":"あたら|えき|ちゅうしん|まち|こうそう|はっぴょう"},{"japanese":"この小説は、構想から完成まで五年かかった。","romaji":"Kono shōsetsu wa, kōsō kara kansei made gonen kakatta.","german":"From the initial concept to completion, this novel took five years.","furigana":"しょうせつ|こうそう|かんせい|ごねん"}],"notes":"構想 is an overall concept or design for a larger undertaking - a novel, an urban project, a political vision - before it is planned concretely. Typical: 構想を練る \"to work out a concept\", 構想を発表する, 構想段階 \"conceptual stage\", often as a suffix: 〜構想. 計画 is the more concrete plan with schedule and dates; 着想 is only the first idea.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"予想に反して、午後から雨が降り出した。","romaji":"Yosō ni hanshite, gogo kara ame ga furidashita.","german":"Contrary to the forecast, it started to rain in the afternoon.","furigana":"よそう|はん|ごご|あめ|ふ|だ"},{"japanese":"試験の結果は予想以上によかった。","romaji":"Shiken no kekka wa yosō ijō ni yokatta.","german":"The exam result was better than expected.","furigana":"しけん|けっか|よそういじょう"},{"japanese":"どのチームが優勝するか予想してみよう。","romaji":"Dono chīmu ga yūshō suru ka yosō shite miyō.","german":"Let's try guessing which team will win the championship.","furigana":"ゆうしょう|よそう"}],"notes":"予想 is the expectation or prediction of how something will turn out; as a する-verb it is transitive: 結果を予想する. Typical: 予想が当たる / 外れる, 予想通り, 予想以上, 予想外, 予想に反して \"contrary to expectations\". 予測 is more objective and data-based (経済予測), 予報 is specifically the weather forecast, and 予期 is more \"to anticipate something\" (予期せぬ出来事).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本は太平洋に面している。","romaji":"Nihon wa Taiheiyō ni men shite iru.","german":"Japan faces the Pacific Ocean.","furigana":"にほん|たいへいよう|めん"},{"japanese":"明日は太平洋側を中心に晴れるでしょう。","romaji":"Ashita wa Taiheiyōgawa o chūshin ni hareru deshō.","german":"Tomorrow it will be sunny, mainly on the Pacific side.","furigana":"あした|たいへいようがわ|ちゅうしん|は"},{"japanese":"祖父は太平洋戦争を経験した世代だ。","romaji":"Sofu wa Taiheiyō Sensō o keiken shita sedai da.","german":"My grandfather belongs to the generation that lived through the Pacific War.","furigana":"そふ|たいへいようせんそう|けいけん|せだい"}],"notes":"太平洋 is the Pacific Ocean; literally something like \"great peaceful ocean\". Note: it is written with 太, not 大 as in 大西洋. Very common in Japan is the distinction between 太平洋側 (Pacific side) and 日本海側 (Sea of Japan side), above all in weather reports. Other combinations: 太平洋沿岸, 太平洋戦争 \"Pacific War\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大西洋を横断する船旅は壮大だ。","romaji":"Taiseiyō o ōdan suru funatabi wa sōdai da.","german":"A sea voyage across the Atlantic is magnificent.","furigana":"たいせいよう|おうだん|ふなたび|そうだい"},{"japanese":"大西洋はヨーロッパとアメリカ大陸の間にある。","romaji":"Taiseiyō wa Yōroppa to Amerika tairiku no aida ni aru.","german":"The Atlantic lies between Europe and the Americas.","furigana":"たいせいよう|たいりく|あいだ"},{"japanese":"ハリケーンは主に大西洋で発生する。","romaji":"Harikēn wa omo ni Taiseiyō de hassei suru.","german":"Hurricanes form mainly over the Atlantic.","furigana":"おも|たいせいよう|はっせい"}],"notes":"大西洋 is the Atlantic Ocean; literally \"great western ocean\" (西洋 = \"the West, the Occident\"). Unlike 太平洋, here it is written with 大, not 太, a common mix-up. Typical: 大西洋を横断する, 北大西洋, 大西洋岸. The other oceans are called 太平洋 and インド洋.","levelNote":"Compound: 大 (N3) + 西洋 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"北極の氷が温暖化で溶けている。","romaji":"Hokkyoku no kōri ga ondanka de tokete iru.","german":"The ice of the Arctic is melting because of global warming.","furigana":"ほっきょく|こおり|おんだんか|と"},{"japanese":"北極星を見つければ、北の方角がわかる。","romaji":"Hokkyokusei o mitsukereba, kita no hōgaku ga wakaru.","german":"If you find the North Star, you can tell which way is north.","furigana":"ほっきょくせい|み|きた|ほうがく"},{"japanese":"人類が初めて北極点に到達したのは二十世紀の初めだ。","romaji":"Jinrui ga hajimete hokkyokuten ni tōtatsu shita no wa nijusseiki no hajime da.","german":"Humans first reached the North Pole in the early twentieth century.","furigana":"じんるい|はじ|ほっきょくてん|とうたつ|にじゅっせいき|はじ"}],"notes":"北極 refers both to the North Pole and to the Arctic as a region. For the exact point, one says 北極点; for the area also 北極圏 \"Arctic Circle region\", and for the sea 北極海. Other words: 北極星 \"North Star\" and ホッキョクグマ \"polar bear\" (usually in katakana). The counterpart is 南極.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"南極にはペンギンが生息している。","romaji":"Nankyoku ni wa pengin ga seisoku shite iru.","german":"Penguins live in Antarctica.","furigana":"なんきょく|せいそく"},{"japanese":"南極大陸の大部分は厚い氷に覆われている。","romaji":"Nankyoku tairiku no daibubun wa atsui kōri ni ōwarete iru.","german":"Most of the Antarctic continent is covered with thick ice.","furigana":"なんきょくたいりく|だいぶぶん|あつ|こおり|おお"},{"japanese":"日本の観測隊は、毎年南極で研究を続けている。","romaji":"Nihon no kansokutai wa, maitoshi nankyoku de kenkyū o tsuzukete iru.","german":"A Japanese research team continues its research in Antarctica every year.","furigana":"にほん|かんそくたい|まいとしなんきょく|けんきゅう|つづ"}],"notes":"南極 refers to the South Pole and to Antarctica as a whole. More precise are 南極点 \"South Pole (point)\", 南極大陸 \"Antarctic continent\" and 南極海. Well known in Japan are the 南極観測隊 \"Antarctic expeditions\" and the station 昭和基地. The counterpart is 北極.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"赤道付近は一年中暑い。","romaji":"Sekidō fukin wa ichinenjū atsui.","german":"It is hot all year round near the equator.","furigana":"せきどうふきん|いちねんじゅうあつ"},{"japanese":"エクアドルという国名は「赤道」という意味だ。","romaji":"Ekuadoru to iu kokumei wa \"sekidō\" to iu imi da.","german":"The name of the country Ecuador means \"equator\".","furigana":"こくめい|せきどう|いみ"},{"japanese":"船が赤道を越えたとき、乗客みんなでお祝いをした。","romaji":"Fune ga sekidō o koeta toki, jōkyaku minna de oiwai o shita.","german":"When the ship crossed the equator, all the passengers celebrated together.","furigana":"ふね|せきどう|こ|じょうきゃく|いわ"}],"notes":"赤道 is the equator, literally \"red road\". Typical: 赤道付近 \"near the equator\", 赤道直下 \"right on the equator\", 赤道を越える. It divides the Earth into 北半球 and 南半球. In astronomy there is also the celestial equator (天の赤道).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"東京の緯度は北緯約三十五度だ。","romaji":"Tōkyō no ido wa hokui yaku sanjūgo do da.","german":"Tokyo's latitude is about 35 degrees north.","furigana":"とうきょう|いど|ほくいやくさんじゅうごど"},{"japanese":"緯度が高い地域ほど、冬は日が短い。","romaji":"Ido ga takai chiiki hodo, fuyu wa hi ga mijikai.","german":"The higher the latitude, the shorter the days in winter.","furigana":"いど|たか|ちいき|ふゆ|ひ|みじか"},{"japanese":"函館とローマは、ほぼ同じ緯度にある。","romaji":"Hakodate to Rōma wa, hobo onaji ido ni aru.","german":"Hakodate and Rome lie at almost the same latitude.","furigana":"|おな|いど"}],"notes":"緯度 is geographic latitude. It is given with 北緯 \"north latitude\" or 南緯 \"south latitude\"; the lines are called 緯線. Typical: 緯度が高い / 低い \"high/low latitude\", 同じ緯度にある. The counterpart is 経度 \"longitude\"; together one speaks of 緯度と経度 or 経緯度.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"経度と緯度で正確な位置を特定できる。","romaji":"Keido to ido de seikaku na ichi o tokutei dekiru.","german":"With longitude and latitude, you can pinpoint an exact position.","furigana":"けいど|いど|せいかく|いち|とくてい"},{"japanese":"経度が十五度違うと、時差は一時間になる。","romaji":"Keido ga jūgo do chigau to, jisa wa ichi jikan ni naru.","german":"A difference of fifteen degrees of longitude makes a one-hour time difference.","furigana":"けいど|じゅうごどちが|じさ|いちじかん"},{"japanese":"日本の標準時は、東経百三十五度の経度を基準にしている。","romaji":"Nihon no hyōjunji wa, tōkei hyaku sanjūgo do no keido o kijun ni shite iru.","german":"Japan's standard time is based on the longitude of 135 degrees east.","furigana":"にほん|ひょうじゅんじ|とうけいひゃくさんじゅうごど|けいど|きじゅん"}],"notes":"経度 is geographic longitude. It is given as 東経 \"east longitude\" and 西経 \"west longitude\", measured from the prime meridian at Greenwich; the lines are called 経線. Japan's standard time is based on 東経135度 (Akashi). The counterpart is 緯度 \"latitude\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この山の標高は三千メートルを超える。","romaji":"Kono yama no hyōkō wa sanzen mētoru o koeru.","german":"The elevation of this mountain exceeds three thousand meters.","furigana":"やま|ひょうこう|さんぜん|こ"},{"japanese":"標高が高くなるほど、気温は下がる。","romaji":"Hyōkō ga takaku naru hodo, kion wa sagaru.","german":"The higher the elevation, the lower the temperature.","furigana":"ひょうこう|たか|きおん|さ"},{"japanese":"この村は標高千メートルの高原にあり、夏も涼しい。","romaji":"Kono mura wa hyōkō sen mētoru no kōgen ni ari, natsu mo suzushii.","german":"This village is on a plateau at an elevation of 1,000 meters, so it is cool even in summer.","furigana":"むら|ひょうこうせん|こうげん|なつ|すず"}],"notes":"標高 is the height of a place above sea level, above all for mountains, passes and plateaus: 標高〇〇メートル, 標高が高い / 低い. 海抜 refers to the same measurement but is used more for low-lying land, coastal places and tsunami signs. 高度 is flight altitude or height in general, and 高さ is the height of an object itself.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この町は海抜五百メートルに位置している。","romaji":"Kono machi wa kaibatsu gohyaku mētoru ni ichi shite iru.","german":"This town is located at an elevation of five hundred meters.","furigana":"まち|かいばつごひゃく|いち"},{"japanese":"海岸近くの電柱に「海抜3m」という表示がある。","romaji":"Kaigan chikaku no denchū ni \"kaibatsu san mētoru\" to iu hyōji ga aru.","german":"A utility pole near the coast has a sign reading \"3 m above sea level\"."},{"japanese":"オランダには、海抜ゼロメートル以下の土地が多い。","romaji":"Oranda ni wa, kaibatsu zero mētoru ika no tochi ga ōi.","german":"In the Netherlands, there is a lot of land at or below sea level.","furigana":"かいばつ|いか|とち|おお"}],"notes":"海抜 is the \"height above sea level\", literally \"rising above the sea\". Its content is the same as 標高, but it is used especially for coasts and low-lying land - in Japan, signs such as 海抜3m stand along roads as tsunami warnings. Typical: 海抜〇〇メートル, 海抜ゼロメートル地帯 \"area at or below sea level\". For mountain peaks, 標高 is more usual.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この部屋の面積は約二十平方メートルだ。","romaji":"Kono heya no menseki wa yaku nijū heihō mētoru da.","german":"Die Fläche dieses Zimmers beträgt etwa zwanzig Quadratmeter.","furigana":"へや|めんせき|やくにじゅうへいほう"},{"japanese":"日本の面積は、ドイツとほぼ同じくらいだ。","romaji":"Nihon no menseki wa, Doitsu to hobo onaji kurai da.","german":"Japan ist flächenmäßig ungefähr so groß wie Deutschland.","furigana":"にほん|めんせき|おな"},{"japanese":"次の三角形の面積を求めなさい。","romaji":"Tsugi no sankakukei no menseki o motomenasai.","german":"Berechne die Fläche des folgenden Dreiecks.","furigana":"つぎ|さんかくけい|めんせき|もと"}],"notes":"面積 ist der Flächeninhalt – von Zimmern, Grundstücken, Ländern oder in der Mathematik. Typisch: 面積が広い / 狭い, 面積を求める „die Fläche berechnen“, 床面積 „Wohnfläche“, 〜平方メートル. Im Alltag sagt man oft einfach 広さ. Nicht verwechseln mit 体積 „Volumen“.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"東京は人口密度が非常に高い。","romaji":"Tōkyō wa jinkō mitsudo ga hijō ni takai.","german":"Tokyo has a very high population density.","furigana":"とうきょう|じんこうみつど|ひじょう|たか"},{"japanese":"北海道は人口密度が低く、豊かな自然が残っている。","romaji":"Hokkaidō wa jinkō mitsudo ga hikuku, yutaka na shizen ga nokotte iru.","german":"Hokkaido has a low population density, and rich nature remains.","furigana":"ほっかいどう|じんこうみつど|ひく|ゆた|しぜん|のこ"},{"japanese":"人口密度の高い地域では、住宅の値段も高くなりやすい。","romaji":"Jinkō mitsudo no takai chiiki de wa, jūtaku no nedan mo takaku nariyasui.","german":"In areas with a high population density, housing prices also tend to be high.","furigana":"じんこうみつど|たか|ちいき|じゅうたく|ねだん|たか"}],"notes":"人口密度 is population density, composed of 人口 \"population\" and 密度 \"density\". One says 人口密度が高い / 低い (not 多い / 少ない). It is usually given as the number of inhabitants per square kilometer (1平方キロメートルあたり). Related terms: 過密 \"overcrowding\" and 過疎 \"depopulation of rural areas\".","levelNote":"Compound: 人口 (N4) + 密度 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本の首都は東京である。","romaji":"Nihon no shuto wa Tōkyō de aru.","german":"Die Hauptstadt Japans ist Tokio.","furigana":"にほん|しゅと|とうきょう"},{"japanese":"首都圏では、朝の電車がとても混雑する。","romaji":"Shutoken de wa, asa no densha ga totemo konzatsu suru.","german":"Im Großraum Tokio sind die Züge morgens extrem voll.","furigana":"しゅとけん|あさ|でんしゃ|こんざつ"},{"japanese":"夏休みに、ドイツの首都ベルリンを訪れた。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi ni, Doitsu no shuto Berurin o otozureta.","german":"In den Sommerferien habe ich Berlin, die Hauptstadt Deutschlands, besucht.","furigana":"なつやす|しゅと|おとず"}],"notes":"首都 ist die Hauptstadt eines Landes. Sehr häufig ist 首都圏 „Großraum Tokio“ (Tokio und die Nachbarpräfekturen), z. B. in Verkehrs- und Wetternachrichten; 首都高 ist die Stadtautobahn Tokios. 都市 bedeutet dagegen allgemein „Großstadt“, 都 (wie in 東京都) ist eine Verwaltungsbezeichnung. Für Hauptstädte von Präfekturen sagt man 県庁所在地.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"都心のマンションは家賃が高い。","romaji":"Toshin no manshon wa yachin ga takai.","german":"Apartments in the city center have high rents.","furigana":"としん|やちん|たか"},{"japanese":"都心まで電車で三十分のところに住んでいる。","romaji":"Toshin made densha de sanjuppun no tokoro ni sunde iru.","german":"I live thirty minutes by train from the city center.","furigana":"としん|でんしゃ|さんじゅっぷん|す"},{"japanese":"昨夜は都心でも五センチの雪が積もった。","romaji":"Sakuya wa toshin demo go senchi no yuki ga tsumotta.","german":"Last night, even central Tokyo got five centimeters of snow.","furigana":"さくや|としん|ご|ゆき|つ"}],"notes":"都心 is the city center, primarily the center of Tokyo; in weather reports, 都心 usually means central Tokyo. It can, however, also be used for other big cities. Typical: 都心に通う, 都心まで〇分, 都心部. 副都心 are the sub-centers such as Shinjuku or Ikebukuro. More general is 中心部, and for entertainment districts 繁華街.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"郊外に引っ越して通勤時間が長くなった。","romaji":"Kōgai ni hikkoshite tsūkin jikan ga nagaku natta.","german":"Nach dem Umzug in die Vorstadt ist die Pendelzeit länger geworden.","furigana":"こうがい|ひ|こ|つうきんじかん|なが"},{"japanese":"郊外は空気がきれいで静かです。","romaji":"Kōgai wa kūki ga kirei de shizuka desu.","german":"Am Stadtrand ist die Luft sauber und es ist ruhig.","furigana":"こうがい|くうき|しず"},{"japanese":"週末は郊外の大きなスーパーに車で行きます。","romaji":"Shūmatsu wa kōgai no ōkina sūpā ni kuruma de ikimasu.","german":"Am Wochenende fahre ich mit dem Auto zu einem großen Supermarkt am Stadtrand.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|こうがい|おお|くるま|い"}],"notes":"郊外 ist das Gebiet am Rand einer Großstadt, meist ruhige Wohngegenden mit Pendlerverkehr: 郊外に住む, 東京の郊外, 郊外の大きな店. Das Gegenteil ist das Stadtzentrum (都心, 町の中心), und richtig ländliche Gegenden heißen 田舎. Anders als das deutsche „Vorstadt“ klingt 郊外 eher neutral bis positiv (Natur, Ruhe).","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 50"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"繁華街は夜になるとネオンで輝く。","romaji":"Hankagai wa yoru ni naru to neon de kagayaku.","german":"The entertainment district glitters with neon at night.","furigana":"はんかがい|よる|かがや"},{"japanese":"駅の東口を出ると、すぐに繁華街が広がっている。","romaji":"Eki no higashiguchi o deru to, sugu ni hankagai ga hirogatte iru.","german":"As soon as you leave the east exit of the station, the busy downtown area spreads out.","furigana":"えき|ひがしぐち|で|はんかがい|ひろ"},{"japanese":"夜遅くに繁華街を一人で歩くのは少し怖い。","romaji":"Yoru osoku ni hankagai o hitori de aruku no wa sukoshi kowai.","german":"Walking alone through the entertainment district late at night is a little scary.","furigana":"よるおそ|はんかがい|ひとり|ある|すこ|こわ"}],"notes":"繁華街 is a lively district with many shops, restaurants and bars, where many people are out and about (繁華 \"bustle\", 街 \"street, district\"). Typical: 駅前の繁華街, 夜の繁華街. 商店街 is instead a smaller, local shopping street, and 歓楽街 stresses nightlife and entertainment establishments more strongly.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"静かな住宅地に家を建てた。","romaji":"Shizuka na jūtakuchi ni ie o tateta.","german":"I built a house in a quiet residential area.","furigana":"しず|じゅうたくち|いえ|た"},{"japanese":"住宅地では、夜遅くに大きな音を出さないでください。","romaji":"Jūtakuchi de wa, yoru osoku ni ōkina oto o dasanaide kudasai.","german":"In residential areas, please do not make loud noises late at night.","furigana":"じゅうたくち|よるおそ|おお|おと|だ"},{"japanese":"この辺りは昔は畑だったが、今は住宅地になっている。","romaji":"Kono atari wa mukashi wa hatake datta ga, ima wa jūtakuchi ni natte iru.","german":"This area used to be fields, but now it is a residential district.","furigana":"あた|むかし|はたけ|いま|じゅうたくち"}],"notes":"住宅地 is a residential area, that is, an area made up mainly of dwellings. Typical: 静かな住宅地, 高級住宅地 \"upscale residential district\", 新興住宅地 \"newly developed housing area\". 住宅街 is almost synonymous but sounds more everyday and stresses the streets; the opposites are 商業地 and 工業地帯.","levelNote":"Compound: 住宅 (N3) + 地 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"駅前は商業地として発展している。","romaji":"Ekimae wa shōgyōchi to shite hatten shite iru.","german":"The area in front of the station is developing as a commercial district.","furigana":"えきまえ|しょうぎょうち|はってん"},{"japanese":"今年は商業地の地価が大きく上がった。","romaji":"Kotoshi wa shōgyōchi no chika ga ōkiku agatta.","german":"This year, land prices in commercial areas rose sharply.","furigana":"ことし|しょうぎょうち|ちか|おお|あ"},{"japanese":"この土地は商業地なので、住宅よりも店を建てる人が多い。","romaji":"Kono tochi wa shōgyōchi na node, jūtaku yori mo mise o tateru hito ga ōi.","german":"Since this land is zoned commercial, more people build shops than homes.","furigana":"とち|しょうぎょうち|じゅうたく|みせ|た|ひと|おお"}],"notes":"商業地 is an area used for trade and business, a rather technical term from urban planning and real estate, for example in land price reports (商業地の地価). The opposites are 住宅地 and 工業地. In everyday speech people more often say 繁華街, 商店街 or ビジネス街.","levelNote":"Compound: 商業 (N2) + 地 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この地域は日本有数の工業地帯だ。","romaji":"Kono chiiki wa Nihon yūsū no kōgyō chitai da.","german":"This region is one of Japan's leading industrial zones.","furigana":"ちいき|にほんゆうすう|こうぎょうちたい"},{"japanese":"社会の授業で、日本の主な工業地帯について習った。","romaji":"Shakai no jugyō de, Nihon no omo na kōgyō chitai ni tsuite naratta.","german":"In social studies class, we learned about Japan's main industrial zones.","furigana":"しゃかい|じゅぎょう|にほん|おも|こうぎょうちたい|なら"},{"japanese":"工業地帯の夜景を船から見るツアーが人気だ。","romaji":"Kōgyō chitai no yakei o fune kara miru tsuā ga ninki da.","german":"Boat tours to see the industrial zone's night view are popular.","furigana":"こうぎょうちたい|やけい|ふね|み|にんき"}],"notes":"工業地帯 is a large industrial area with many factories (地帯 \"zone, belt\"). In Japanese geography class, you learn the major 工業地帯 such as 京浜工業地帯, 中京工業地帯 and 阪神工業地帯. Smaller areas are often called 工業地域 or 工業団地 \"industrial park\". Opposites: 住宅地, 商業地, 農業地帯.","levelNote":"Compound: 工業 (N4) + 地帯 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"横浜は歴史のある港町として有名だ。","romaji":"Yokohama wa rekishi no aru minatomachi to shite yūmei da.","german":"Yokohama is famous as a port town with a long history.","furigana":"よこはま|れきし|みなとまち|ゆうめい"},{"japanese":"港町の市場では、新鮮な魚が安く買える。","romaji":"Minatomachi no ichiba de wa, shinsen na sakana ga yasuku kaeru.","german":"At the market in the port town, you can buy fresh fish cheaply.","furigana":"みなとまち|いちば|しんせん|さかな|やす|か"},{"japanese":"彼は北海道の小さな港町で生まれ育った。","romaji":"Kare wa Hokkaidō no chiisa na minatomachi de umare sodatta.","german":"He was born and raised in a small port town in Hokkaido.","furigana":"かれ|ほっかいどう|ちい|みなとまち|う|そだ"}],"notes":"港町 is a port town, read with the native Japanese reading みなとまち. The word often has a nostalgic or romantic ring (sailors, fish markets, old warehouses) and is popular in songs and travel guides. Formed similarly are 城下町 \"castle town\" and 門前町 \"temple town\". In a factual, official register one says 港湾都市.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"温泉街を浴衣で散歩するのが楽しい。","romaji":"Onsengai o yukata de sanpo suru no ga tanoshii.","german":"It is fun to stroll through the hot spring town in a yukata.","furigana":"おんせんがい|ゆかた|さんぽ|たの"},{"japanese":"温泉街には、旅館や土産物屋がずらりと並んでいる。","romaji":"Onsengai ni wa, ryokan ya miyagemonoya ga zurari to narande iru.","german":"In the hot spring town, inns and souvenir shops line up in a row.","furigana":"おんせんがい|りょかん|みやげものや|なら"},{"japanese":"冬休みに家族で草津の温泉街へ行った。","romaji":"Fuyuyasumi ni kazoku de Kusatsu no onsengai e itta.","german":"During winter vacation, my family went to the Kusatsu hot spring town.","furigana":"ふゆやす|かぞく|くさつ|おんせんがい|い"}],"notes":"温泉街 is the district of a town around the hot springs, with ryokan, bathhouses and souvenir shops. Here 街 is read がい, as in 商店街 or 繁華街. 温泉地, by contrast, generally refers to a spa resort with hot springs. Typical is strolling through the 温泉街 in a yukata, often with 温泉まんじゅう.","levelNote":"Compound: 温泉 (N5) + 街 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"市街地の再開発が進んでいる。","romaji":"Shigaichi no saikaihatsu ga susunde iru.","german":"Redevelopment of the urban area is progressing.","furigana":"しがいち|さいかいはつ|すす"},{"japanese":"市街地では、スピードを落として運転してください。","romaji":"Shigaichi de wa, supīdo o otoshite unten shite kudasai.","german":"In built-up areas, please slow down when driving.","furigana":"しがいち|お|うんてん"},{"japanese":"大雨で川があふれ、市街地の一部が水につかった。","romaji":"Ōame de kawa ga afure, shigaichi no ichibu ga mizu ni tsukatta.","german":"Heavy rain made the river overflow, and part of the urban area was flooded.","furigana":"おおあめ|かわ|しがいち|いちぶ|みず"}],"notes":"市街地 is the built-up urban area, as opposed to 郊外 \"suburbs\" or 山間部 \"mountain areas\". Common in news, urban planning and traffic law: 市街地の再開発, 市街地を走る, 中心市街地 \"city center\". In everyday speech people mostly say 町中 or 街中. 市街 alone appears in compounds such as 旧市街 and 市街戦.","levelNote":"Compound: 市街 (N1) + 地 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"旧市街には歴史的な建物が多い。","romaji":"Kyūshigai ni wa rekishiteki na tatemono ga ōi.","german":"There are many historic buildings in the old town.","furigana":"きゅうしがい|れきしてき|たてもの|おお"},{"japanese":"旧市街は道が狭いので、車では入れない。","romaji":"Kyūshigai wa michi ga semai node, kuruma de wa hairenai.","german":"The old town has such narrow streets that cars can't enter.","furigana":"きゅうしがい|みち|せま|くるま|はい"},{"japanese":"ヨーロッパ旅行では、旧市街を歩くのが一番の楽しみだ。","romaji":"Yōroppa ryokō de wa, kyūshigai o aruku no ga ichiban no tanoshimi da.","german":"On trips to Europe, my greatest pleasure is walking through the old towns.","furigana":"りょこう|きゅうしがい|ある|いちばん|たの"}],"notes":"旧市街 is the \"old town\", the historic core of a city. It is used mainly for European and foreign cities, for example in travel guides. For Japanese cities one rather speaks of 古い町並み, 城下町 or 旧市街地; 下町 is something else, namely the traditional district of ordinary people. The counterpart is 新市街.","levelNote":"Compound: 旧 (N3) + 市街 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新市街には近代的なビルが並んでいる。","romaji":"Shinshigai ni wa kindaiteki na biru ga narande iru.","german":"Modern buildings line the new town.","furigana":"しんしがい|きんだいてき|なら"},{"japanese":"ホテルは新市街にあるので、旧市街まではバスで十分ほどだ。","romaji":"Hoteru wa shinshigai ni aru node, kyūshigai made wa basu de juppun hodo da.","german":"The hotel is in the new town, so it's about ten minutes by bus to the old town.","furigana":"しんしがい|きゅうしがい|じゅっぷん"},{"japanese":"新市街の駅前には、大きなショッピングモールがある。","romaji":"Shinshigai no ekimae ni wa, ōkina shoppingu mōru ga aru.","german":"There is a large shopping mall in front of the station in the new town.","furigana":"しんしがい|えきまえ|おお"}],"notes":"新市街 is the \"new town\", the later-built, modern part of a city, usually as the counterpart to 旧市街. The word appears mainly in travel guides about European or Middle Eastern cities. For Japanese planned towns on the city outskirts, one rather says ニュータウン or 新興住宅地.","levelNote":"Compound: 新 (N1) + 市街 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"市の中心部には大きな公園がある。","romaji":"Shi no chūshinbu ni wa ōkina kōen ga aru.","german":"There is a large park in the center of the city.","furigana":"し|ちゅうしんぶ|おお|こうえん"},{"japanese":"町の中心部は車が多いので、自転車で移動している。","romaji":"Machi no chūshinbu wa kuruma ga ōi node, jitensha de idō shite iru.","german":"There is a lot of traffic in the center of town, so I get around by bicycle.","furigana":"まち|ちゅうしんぶ|くるま|おお|じてんしゃ|いどう"},{"japanese":"地球の中心部は、非常に高温だと考えられている。","romaji":"Chikyū no chūshinbu wa, hijō ni kōon da to kangaerarete iru.","german":"The interior of the Earth is thought to be extremely hot.","furigana":"ちきゅう|ちゅうしんぶ|ひじょう|こうおん|かんが"}],"notes":"中心部 is the central area of something, usually a city (市の中心部, 町の中心部), but also of a body or object (地球の中心部). While 中心 refers more to the center point, 部 stresses the whole area. For the center of Tokyo specifically, one says 都心; the opposite is 周辺部 or 郊外.","levelNote":"Compound: 中心 (N3) + 部 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"周辺部には農地が広がっている。","romaji":"Shūhenbu ni wa nōchi ga hirogatte iru.","german":"Farmland stretches across the outskirts.","furigana":"しゅうへんぶ|のうち|ひろ"},{"japanese":"都市の周辺部では、新しい住宅が次々に建てられている。","romaji":"Toshi no shūhenbu de wa, atarashii jūtaku ga tsugitsugi ni taterarete iru.","german":"On the outskirts of the city, new houses are being built one after another.","furigana":"とし|しゅうへんぶ|あたら|じゅうたく|つぎつぎ|た"},{"japanese":"写真の周辺部が少し暗くなってしまった。","romaji":"Shashin no shūhenbu ga sukoshi kuraku natte shimatta.","german":"The edges of the photo came out a little dark.","furigana":"しゃしん|しゅうへんぶ|すこ|くら"}],"notes":"周辺部 is the outer area or periphery of a region, formed from 周辺 \"surroundings\" and 部 \"part\"; the opposite is 中心部. It sounds factual and appears in urban planning, statistics or technology (for example, the edges of a photo). In everyday speech people usually say 周辺, 周り, or for cities 郊外 \"suburbs\".","levelNote":"Compound: 周辺 (N2) + 部 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二つの国の境界線は川に沿っている。","romaji":"Futatsu no kuni no kyōkaisen wa kawa ni sotte iru.","german":"The boundary line between the two countries follows the river.","furigana":"ふた|くに|きょうかいせん|かわ|そ"},{"japanese":"隣の家との境界がはっきりしないので、測量を頼んだ。","romaji":"Tonari no ie to no kyōkai ga hakkiri shinai node, sokuryō o tanonda.","german":"The boundary with the neighboring house was unclear, so I asked for a survey.","furigana":"となり|いえ|きょうかい|そくりょう|たの"},{"japanese":"夢と現実の境界があいまいになるような映画だった。","romaji":"Yume to genjitsu no kyōkai ga aimai ni naru yō na eiga datta.","german":"It was a movie in which the boundary between dream and reality becomes blurred.","furigana":"ゆめ|げんじつ|きょうかい|えいが"}],"notes":"境界 is a \"boundary\" between two areas: plots of land, regions, but also abstractly (夢と現実の境界). It sounds factual to formal; the boundary line is called 境界線. In everyday speech people often say 境 (さかい) or 境目. For national borders, 国境 (こっきょう) is usual, and for prefectural borders 県境.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この山は二つの県の県境に位置している。","romaji":"Kono yama wa futatsu no ken no kenzakai ni ichi shite iru.","german":"This mountain is located on the border of two prefectures.","furigana":"やま|ふた|けん|けんざかい|いち"},{"japanese":"県境の長いトンネルを抜けると、雪景色が広がっていた。","romaji":"Kenzakai no nagai tonneru o nukeru to, yukigeshiki ga hirogatte ita.","german":"When we came out of the long tunnel at the prefectural border, a snowy landscape spread out before us.","furigana":"けんざかい|なが|ぬ||ひろ"},{"japanese":"連休中は、県境をまたぐ移動を控えるよう呼びかけられた。","romaji":"Renkyūchū wa, kenzakai o matagu idō o hikaeru yō yobikakerareta.","german":"During the holidays, people were urged to refrain from traveling across prefectural borders.","furigana":"れんきゅうちゅう|けんざかい|いどう|ひか|よ"}],"notes":"県境 is the border between two prefectures and is usually read けんざかい; in official and news language one also hears けんきょう. Note: 境 is read さかい here, but きょう in 国境 (こっきょう). Typical: 県境を越える, 県境のトンネル; during the Corona period, 県境をまたぐ移動 \"travel across prefectural borders\" became a catchphrase.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"市境を越えると田園風景が広がる。","romaji":"Shizakai o koeru to den'en fūkei ga hirogaru.","german":"Beyond the city limits, a pastoral landscape spreads out.","furigana":"しざかい|こ|でんえんふうけい|ひろ"},{"japanese":"この川が、二つの市の市境になっている。","romaji":"Kono kawa ga, futatsu no shi no shizakai ni natte iru.","german":"This river forms the boundary between the two cities.","furigana":"かわ|ふた|し|しざかい"},{"japanese":"うちは市境のすぐ近くなので、隣の市の駅のほうが近い。","romaji":"Uchi wa shizakai no sugu chikaku na node, tonari no shi no eki no hō ga chikai.","german":"We live right near the city limits, so the station in the neighboring city is closer.","furigana":"しざかい|ちか|となり|し|えき|ちか"}],"notes":"市境 is the boundary between two cities or municipalities with city status, formed in analogy to 県境 and usually read しざかい. The word is relatively rare and appears mainly in news, on maps or in administrative matters; in conversation people often simply say 市の境. Correspondingly there are 町境 and 区境.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"学校の敷地内は禁煙だ。","romaji":"Gakkō no shikichinai wa kin'en da.","german":"Smoking is prohibited on school grounds.","furigana":"がっこう|しきちない|きんえん"},{"japanese":"この大学は敷地が広くて、移動に自転車が必要なほどだ。","romaji":"Kono daigaku wa shikichi ga hirokute, idō ni jitensha ga hitsuyō na hodo da.","german":"This university's grounds are so large that you need a bicycle to get around.","furigana":"だいがく|しきち|ひろ|いどう|じてんしゃ|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"家を建てる前に、敷地の広さを正確に測った。","romaji":"Ie o tateru mae ni, shikichi no hirosa o seikaku ni hakatta.","german":"Before building the house, I measured the size of the lot accurately.","furigana":"いえ|た|まえ|しきち|ひろ|せいかく|はか"}],"notes":"敷地 is the plot of land on which a building stands or which belongs to an institution, such as a school, company or temple. Very common is 敷地内 \"on the premises\" (敷地内禁煙, 敷地内立入禁止). Also typical: 敷地が広い, 敷地面積. 土地 is more general, \"land, ground\", and 用地 is land earmarked for a specific purpose.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"駅の近くの土地は値段が高い。","romaji":"Eki no chikaku no tochi wa nedan ga takai.","german":"Land near the station is expensive.","furigana":"えき|ちか|とち|ねだん|たか"},{"japanese":"旅先では、その土地の名物を食べるのが楽しみだ。","romaji":"Tabisaki de wa, sono tochi no meibutsu o taberu no ga tanoshimi da.","german":"When traveling, I look forward to eating the local specialties.","furigana":"たびさき|とち|めいぶつ|た|たの"},{"japanese":"この辺りは土地がやせていて、米作りには向かない。","romaji":"Kono atari wa tochi ga yasete ite, komezukuri ni wa mukanai.","german":"The soil around here is poor and not suited to growing rice.","furigana":"あた|とち|こめづく|む"}],"notes":"土地 has two main meanings: \"plot of land, land\" as property or ground (土地を買う, 土地の値段, 土地がやせている) and \"area, place\" (その土地の料理, 土地の人 \"locals\"). Also typical is 土地勘 \"familiarity with an area\". For soil as earth one rather says 土, for the surface of the ground 地面.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 37"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい病院の建設用地を探している。","romaji":"Atarashii byōin no kensetsu yōchi o sagashite iru.","german":"We are looking for a construction site for the new hospital.","furigana":"あたら|びょういん|けんせつようち|さが"},{"japanese":"空港を広げるための用地を確保するのは簡単ではない。","romaji":"Kūkō o hirogeru tame no yōchi o kakuho suru no wa kantan de wa nai.","german":"Securing land to expand the airport is not easy.","furigana":"くうこう|ひろ|ようち|かくほ|かんたん"},{"japanese":"市は道路用地として、その土地を買い取った。","romaji":"Shi wa dōro yōchi to shite, sono tochi o kaitotta.","german":"The city bought up that land as a site for road construction.","furigana":"し|どうろようち|とち|か|と"}],"notes":"用地 is land earmarked for a specific purpose and appears almost always in compounds: 建設用地 \"construction site\", 道路用地, 工場用地, 住宅用地. Typical in official and business language: 用地を確保する, 用地買収 \"land acquisition\". Unlike 敷地, the land is usually not yet built on.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"工場の跡地に公園が作られた。","romaji":"Kōjō no atochi ni kōen ga tsukurareta.","german":"A park was built on the site of the old factory.","furigana":"こうじょう|あとち|こうえん|つく"},{"japanese":"オリンピック会場の跡地をどう利用するかが問題になっている。","romaji":"Orinpikku kaijō no atochi o dō riyō suru ka ga mondai ni natte iru.","german":"How to use the former Olympic venue sites has become an issue.","furigana":"かいじょう|あとち|りよう|もんだい"},{"japanese":"なくなった小学校の跡地に、マンションが建った。","romaji":"Nakunatta shōgakkō no atochi ni, manshon ga tatta.","german":"An apartment building was built on the site of the closed elementary school.","furigana":"しょうがっこう|あとち|た"}],"notes":"跡地 is land on which something used to stand, such as a factory, school or station, that has since been demolished or abandoned (跡 \"trace, remains\"). Typical: 工場跡地, 〜の跡地に…が建つ, 跡地利用 \"reuse of the site\". For historical remains such as castles, 城跡 (しろあと) is more usual; archaeological sites are called 遺跡.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子供たちは空き地でサッカーをしていた。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa akichi de sakkā o shite ita.","german":"The children were playing soccer in a vacant lot.","furigana":"こども|あ|ち"},{"japanese":"隣の空き地に、新しい家が建つらしい。","romaji":"Tonari no akichi ni, atarashii ie ga tatsu rashii.","german":"It seems a new house will be built on the vacant lot next door.","furigana":"となり|あ|ち|あたら|いえ|た"},{"japanese":"空き地にごみを捨てないでください。","romaji":"Akichi ni gomi o sutenaide kudasai.","german":"Please do not dump garbage in the vacant lot.","furigana":"あ|ち|す"}],"notes":"空き地 is an undeveloped, vacant plot of land, especially in residential areas, the classic playground of children (known from \"Doraemon\"). Formed like 空き家 \"vacant house\" or 空き部屋. The spelling 空地 also occurs. A cleared plot ready for building is called 更地; fallow agricultural land is 休耕地 or 荒れ地.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本は山が多い複雑な地形をしている。","romaji":"Nihon wa yama ga ōi fukuzatsu na chikei o shite iru.","german":"Japan has a complex topography with many mountains.","furigana":"にほん|やま|おお|ふくざつ|ちけい"},{"japanese":"この地域は地形の関係で、冬になると雪が多い。","romaji":"Kono chiiki wa chikei no kankei de, fuyu ni naru to yuki ga ōi.","german":"Because of the terrain, this region gets a lot of snow in winter.","furigana":"ちいき|ちけい|かんけい|ふゆ|ゆき|おお"},{"japanese":"登山の前に、地図で地形を確認しておいた。","romaji":"Tozan no mae ni, chizu de chikei o kakunin shite oita.","german":"Before the mountain climb, I checked the terrain on a map.","furigana":"とざん|まえ|ちず|ちけい|かくにん"}],"notes":"地形 refers to the shape of the earth's surface in an area: mountains, valleys, plains, coastlines. Typical are 複雑な地形, 険しい地形, 山がちな地形 and the phrase 〜な地形をしている. Unlike 地質 (\"composition of rock and soil\"), 地形 is only about the visible surface form; 地理 is more generally the \"geography\" of an area. In technical language it appears in 地形図 (\"topographic map\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"建設前に地質調査を行った。","romaji":"Kensetsu mae ni chishitsu chōsa o okonatta.","german":"A geological survey was carried out before construction.","furigana":"けんせつまえ|ちしつちょうさ|おこな"},{"japanese":"この山は地質が複雑で、珍しい石がたくさん見つかる。","romaji":"Kono yama wa chishitsu ga fukuzatsu de, mezurashii ishi ga takusan mitsukaru.","german":"The geology of this mountain is complex, and many unusual stones are found there.","furigana":"やま|ちしつ|ふくざつ|めずら|いし|み"},{"japanese":"大学で地質学を専攻して、火山の研究をしている。","romaji":"Daigaku de chishitsugaku o senkō shite, kazan no kenkyū o shite iru.","german":"I majored in geology at university and am researching volcanoes.","furigana":"だいがく|ちしつがく|せんこう|かざん|けんきゅう"}],"notes":"地質 refers to the makeup of the ground: rock types, soil layers and how they formed. Common in compounds: 地質調査 (\"geological survey\"), 地質学 (\"geology\" as a science), 地質図 (\"geological map\"). It is mostly found in technical or news language, for example regarding construction, earthquakes or volcanoes. It differs from 地形 (\"landform\") in that it concerns the material beneath the surface, not the outer shape; the soil in a garden is simply 土.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いい方法を思い付いた。","romaji":"Ii hōhō o omoitsuita.","german":"I thought of a good method.","furigana":"ほうほう|おも|つ"},{"japanese":"プレゼントに何を買えばいいか、全然思い付かない。","romaji":"Purezento ni nani o kaeba ii ka, zenzen omoitsukanai.","german":"I can't think of at all what to buy as a present.","furigana":"なに|か|ぜんぜんおも|つ"},{"japanese":"シャワーを浴びているときに、新しい企画のアイデアを思い付いた。","romaji":"Shawā o abite iru toki ni, atarashii kikaku no aidea o omoitsuita.","german":"While taking a shower, I came up with an idea for a new project.","furigana":"あ|あたら|きかく|おも|つ"}],"notes":"思い付く (usually written 思いつく) describes the moment when an idea or solution suddenly occurs to you: いい案を思いつく, 名前が思いつかない. The object can take を or, especially in the negative, が. Unlike 考え付く, 思いつく stresses the spontaneous, almost accidental; 思い出す, by contrast, means \"to remember something forgotten\". The noun 思いつき means \"spontaneous idea\", often disparagingly: 思いつきで決める (\"to decide on a whim\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"誰もそんな解決策を考え付かなかった。","romaji":"Dare mo sonna kaiketsusaku o kangaetsukanakatta.","german":"Nobody came up with such a solution.","furigana":"だれ|かいけつさく|かんが|つ"},{"japanese":"考え付く限りの方法を試したが、うまくいかなかった。","romaji":"Kangaetsuku kagiri no hōhō o tameshita ga, umaku ikanakatta.","german":"I tried every method I could think of, but nothing worked.","furigana":"かんが|つ|かぎ|ほうほう|ため"},{"japanese":"子どもは大人が考え付かないような遊び方をする。","romaji":"Kodomo wa otona ga kangaetsukanai yō na asobikata o suru.","german":"Children play in ways that adults would never think of.","furigana":"こ|おとな|かんが|つ|あそ|かた"}],"notes":"考え付く (also 考えつく) means \"to think up, to hit upon something\", as the result of one's own thinking: 方法を考えつく, 誰も考えつかなかったアイデア. Compared with 思いつく (\"to occur to one suddenly\"), it sounds more like deliberate mental effort. Very common are the negative 考えつかない and the phrase 考えつく限り (\"everything one can possibly think of\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"もう一度考え直してみてください。","romaji":"Mō ichido kangaenaoshite mite kudasai.","german":"Please think it over once more.","furigana":"いちどかんが|なお"},{"japanese":"会社を辞めると言ったら、上司に考え直すように言われた。","romaji":"Kaisha o yameru to ittara, jōshi ni kangaenaosu yō ni iwareta.","german":"When I said I would quit the company, my boss told me to reconsider.","furigana":"かいしゃ|や|い|じょうし|かんが|なお|い"},{"japanese":"予算が足りないので、旅行の計画を考え直した。","romaji":"Yosan ga tarinai node, ryokō no keikaku o kangaenaoshita.","german":"Because the budget was insufficient, I reconsidered my travel plans.","furigana":"よさん|た|りょこう|けいかく|かんが|なお"}],"notes":"考え直す means to \"think over again\" a decision or opinion already made, often with the result of changing it. Typical are 計画を考え直す, もう一度考え直す and the request 考え直してください, with which one tries to talk someone out of a decision. The suffix 〜直す generally expresses \"to do anew, to do again (properly)\", as in 書き直す or やり直す. Plain 考える is only \"to think\", and 見直す is more \"to review, to reassess\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女の不安を感じ取ることができた。","romaji":"Kanojo no fuan o kanjitoru koto ga dekita.","german":"I could sense her anxiety.","furigana":"かのじょ|ふあん|かん|と"},{"japanese":"部屋に入った瞬間、何かあったような雰囲気を感じ取った。","romaji":"Heya ni haitta shunkan, nani ka atta yō na fun'iki o kanjitotta.","german":"The moment I entered the room, I sensed an atmosphere as if something had happened.","furigana":"へや|はい|しゅんかん|なに|ふんいき|かん|と"},{"japanese":"この詩からは、作者の深い悲しみが感じ取れる。","romaji":"Kono shi kara wa, sakusha no fukai kanashimi ga kanjitoreru.","german":"The author's deep sorrow can be sensed from this poem.","furigana":"し|さくしゃ|ふか|かな|かん|と"}],"notes":"感じ取る means to \"pick up on, sense\" something unspoken or subtle: 相手の気持ちを感じ取る, 雰囲気を感じ取る, 季節の変化を感じ取る. Compared with plain 感じる (\"to feel\"), 〜取る stresses actively grasping and understanding what one perceives. Often in the potential form: 感じ取れる, 感じ取ることができる. The tone is neutral to slightly literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"間違いに気付いてすぐに訂正した。","romaji":"Machigai ni kizuite sugu ni teisei shita.","german":"I noticed the mistake and corrected it right away.","furigana":"まちが|きづ|ていせい"},{"japanese":"電車を降りてから、傘を忘れたことに気付いた。","romaji":"Densha o orite kara, kasa o wasureta koto ni kizuita.","german":"After getting off the train, I realized I had forgotten my umbrella.","furigana":"でんしゃ|お|かさ|わす|きづ"},{"japanese":"髪を切ったのに、彼は全然気付いてくれなかった。","romaji":"Kami o kitta noni, kare wa zenzen kizuite kurenakatta.","german":"I got a haircut, but he didn't notice at all.","furigana":"かみ|き|かれ|ぜんぜんきづ"}],"notes":"気付く (nowadays mostly written 気づく) means \"to notice, become aware, realize\" and is intransitive: what is noticed takes に - ミスに気づく, 彼の変化に気づく; a whole clause is attached with 〜ことに or 〜と. Unlike 分かる, 気づく stresses the moment when something strikes you; the literary 認識する means a conscious, factual grasp. Equivalent and very common is 気がつく, which can also mean \"to come to.\"","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しいアパートをとても気に入っている。","romaji":"Atarashii apāto o totemo ki ni itte iru.","german":"I like my new apartment very much.","furigana":"あたら|き|い"},{"japanese":"このかばん、色もデザインも気に入ったので買います。","romaji":"Kono kaban, iro mo dezain mo ki ni itta node kaimasu.","german":"I like both the color and the design of this bag, so I'll buy it.","furigana":"いろ|き|い|か"},{"japanese":"誕生日のプレゼント、気に入ってもらえるといいな。","romaji":"Tanjōbi no purezento, ki ni itte moraeru to ii na.","german":"I hope you'll like your birthday present.","furigana":"たんじょうび|き|い"}],"notes":"気に入る means \"to please, to find pleasing.\" The thing liked takes が (この店が気に入った) or, equally common, を (この店を気に入った). For a lasting state, use 気に入っている: \"to be fond of something.\" In contrast to 好き, it stresses that something appeals to you when you get to know or try it. Derived from it are お気に入り (\"favorite\") and 気に入らない (\"not to suit, to displease\"), also toward people: あの人の態度が気に入らない.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"問題の深刻さを認識する必要がある。","romaji":"Mondai no shinkokusa o ninshiki suru hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"It is necessary to recognize the seriousness of the problem.","furigana":"もんだい|しんこく|にんしき|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"パソコンにUSBメモリを差したが、認識されなかった。","romaji":"Pasokon ni USB memori o sashita ga, ninshiki sarenakatta.","german":"I plugged the USB stick into the computer, but it was not recognized.","furigana":"さ|にんしき"},{"japanese":"その件については、私も同じように認識しております。","romaji":"Sono ken ni tsuite wa, watashi mo onaji yō ni ninshiki shite orimasu.","german":"As for that matter, I understand it the same way.","furigana":"けん|わたし|おな|にんしき"}],"notes":"認識する means \"to recognize, be aware of, grasp\" and is a rather literary, factual word: 問題の重要性を認識する, 〜と認識している (\"to understand it as, to assume\"). In companies and news you often hear 認識が甘い (\"to underestimate something\") or 認識の違い (\"difference in understanding\"). In technology it means \"to recognize\" in the sense of recognition: 顔認識, 音声認識, USBが認識されない. For everyday \"to notice\", one rather says 気づく.","levelNote":"Derived from 認識 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自分の責任を自覚しなければならない。","romaji":"Jibun no sekinin o jikaku shinakereba naranai.","german":"You must be aware of your responsibility.","furigana":"じぶん|せきにん|じかく"},{"japanese":"疲れているという自覚はなかったが、医者に休むように言われた。","romaji":"Tsukarete iru to iu jikaku wa nakatta ga, isha ni yasumu yō ni iwareta.","german":"I didn't feel tired myself, but the doctor told me to rest.","furigana":"つか|じかく|いしゃ|やす|い"},{"japanese":"彼は自分がチームのリーダーだと自覚して、行動が変わった。","romaji":"Kare wa jibun ga chīmu no rīdā da to jikaku shite, kōdō ga kawatta.","german":"Since he realized he is the team leader, his behavior has changed.","furigana":"かれ|じぶん|じかく|こうどう|か"}],"notes":"自覚する means to \"be aware of\" or \"become aware of\" one's own situation, role, weakness or responsibility: 責任を自覚する, 自分の弱点を自覚している. The object almost always concerns oneself, which distinguishes it from 認識する, which also grasps external facts. In medicine, 自覚症状 stands for \"subjectively felt symptoms\": 自覚症状がない. The counterpart 無自覚 means \"without noticing it oneself, unconscious\".","levelNote":"Derived from 自覚 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"健康を意識した食生活を心がけている。","romaji":"Kenkō o ishiki shita shokuseikatsu o kokorogakete iru.","german":"I try to eat in a way that is mindful of my health.","furigana":"けんこう|いしき|しょくせいかつ|こころ"},{"japanese":"好きな人が隣に座ると、つい意識してしまって話せない。","romaji":"Suki na hito ga tonari ni suwaru to, tsui ishiki shite shimatte hanasenai.","german":"When the person I like sits next to me, I can't help being self-conscious and can't talk.","furigana":"す|ひと|となり|すわ|いしき|はな"},{"japanese":"発表のときは、ゆっくり話すことを意識してください。","romaji":"Happyō no toki wa, yukkuri hanasu koto o ishiki shite kudasai.","german":"When you give your presentation, please be conscious of speaking slowly.","furigana":"はっぴょう|はな|いしき"}],"notes":"意識する means \"to be aware of something, to pay conscious attention to something\" and also \"to keep thinking about someone.\" With を: 健康を意識する, 周りの目を意識する (\"to worry about what others think\"). Very common is 〜を意識した (\"geared toward ...\"): 若者を意識した商品. Regarding people, it often hints at rivalry or romantic interest: 彼のことを意識してしまう. The noun 意識 also means \"consciousness\" (意識を失う \"to lose consciousness\").","levelNote":"Derived from 意識 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"困難を覚悟して挑戦することにした。","romaji":"Konnan o kakugo shite chōsen suru koto ni shita.","german":"I decided to take on the challenge, prepared for the difficulties.","furigana":"こんなん|かくご|ちょうせん"},{"japanese":"怒られるのを覚悟で、部長に本当のことを話した。","romaji":"Okorareru no o kakugo de, buchō ni hontō no koto o hanashita.","german":"Prepared to be scolded, I told the department head the truth.","furigana":"おこ|かくご|ぶちょう|ほんとう|はな"},{"japanese":"この仕事は残業が多いと覚悟しておいたほうがいい。","romaji":"Kono shigoto wa zangyō ga ōi to kakugo shite oita hō ga ii.","german":"You should be prepared for a lot of overtime in this job.","furigana":"しごと|ざんぎょう|おお|かくご"}],"notes":"覚悟する means to brace oneself inwardly for something unpleasant or to knowingly accept it: 失敗を覚悟する, 反対されるのは覚悟の上だ (\"I had expected that\"). Also typical is 〜を覚悟で (\"at the risk of ...\"). Unlike 決心する and 決意する (\"to resolve\"), it is less about the decision itself than about the readiness to bear the consequences. As a threat or in jest: 覚悟しろ! (\"Prepare yourself!\").","levelNote":"Derived from 覚悟 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"留学することを決意した。","romaji":"Ryūgaku suru koto o ketsui shita.","german":"I resolved to study abroad.","furigana":"りゅうがく|けつい"},{"japanese":"その選手は、けがを理由に引退を決意した。","romaji":"Sono senshu wa, kega o riyū ni intai o ketsui shita.","german":"The player decided to retire because of his injury.","furigana":"せんしゅ|りゆう|いんたい|けつい"},{"japanese":"新年に、毎日日記をつけると決意した。","romaji":"Shinnen ni, mainichi nikki o tsukeru to ketsui shita.","german":"For the new year, I resolved to keep a diary every day.","furigana":"しんねん|まいにちにっき|けつい"}],"notes":"決意する means to \"make a firm decision\", often one that has matured over time: 〜ことを決意する, 〜と決意する. It sounds more serious and solemn than 決心する and likes to appear in speeches, news and narratives, e.g. 引退を決意する (\"to decide to retire\"). The noun occurs in phrases such as 固い決意 (\"firm resolve\") and 決意を新たにする (\"to renew one's resolve\"). For small everyday decisions, one simply says 決める.","levelNote":"Derived from 決意 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"転職する決心がついた。","romaji":"Tenshoku suru kesshin ga tsuita.","german":"I've made up my mind to change jobs.","furigana":"てんしょく|けっしん"},{"japanese":"健康のために、たばこをやめようと決心した。","romaji":"Kenkō no tame ni, tabako o yameyō to kesshin shita.","german":"I decided to quit smoking for my health.","furigana":"けんこう|けっしん"},{"japanese":"彼女にプロポーズしたいが、なかなか決心がつかない。","romaji":"Kanojo ni puropōzu shitai ga, nakanaka kesshin ga tsukanai.","german":"I want to propose to her, but I just can't make up my mind.","furigana":"かのじょ|けっしん"}],"notes":"決心する means \"to resolve, make a decision,\" mostly after some hesitation: 〜ことを決心する, 〜と決心する. Very typical is 決心がつく (\"to finally come to a decision\") and 決心がつかない (\"cannot bring oneself to decide\"). Compared with 決意する, it is somewhat more everyday and stresses the inner decision process; 決める is the neutral base word for \"to decide, set.\"","levelNote":"Derived from 決心 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大量の情報を正確に記憶するのは難しい。","romaji":"Tairyō no jōhō o seikaku ni kioku suru no wa muzukashii.","german":"It is difficult to memorize a large amount of information accurately.","furigana":"たいりょう|じょうほう|せいかく|きおく|むずか"},{"japanese":"その日のことは、はっきりと記憶している。","romaji":"Sono hi no koto wa, hakkiri to kioku shite iru.","german":"I remember that day clearly.","furigana":"ひ|きおく"},{"japanese":"このアプリは、前回入力した住所を記憶してくれる。","romaji":"Kono apuri wa, zenkai nyūryoku shita jūsho o kioku shite kureru.","german":"This app remembers the address you entered last time.","furigana":"ぜんかいにゅうりょく|じゅうしょ|きおく"}],"notes":"記憶する means \"to commit to memory, retain in memory\" and sounds more factual than 覚える. As a verb it often appears in sober or technical contexts (データを記憶する \"to store data\"); in everyday speech the noun is more common: 記憶がある/ない (\"to remember / have no memory of\"), 記憶に残る (\"to stay in memory\"), 記憶力 (\"memory\"). For deliberate rote learning, people more often say 暗記する, and for \"to recall,\" 思い出す.","levelNote":"Derived from 記憶 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"歴史の年号を暗記するのが苦手だ。","romaji":"Rekishi no nengō o anki suru no ga nigate da.","german":"I'm bad at memorizing historical dates.","furigana":"れきし|ねんごう|あんき|にがて"},{"japanese":"スピーチの原稿を全部暗記したので、紙を見ないで話せた。","romaji":"Supīchi no genkō o zenbu anki shita node, kami o minaide hanaseta.","german":"I memorized the whole script of my speech, so I could talk without looking at the paper.","furigana":"げんこう|ぜんぶあんき|かみ|み|はな"},{"japanese":"試験の前の日に、単語を百個暗記した。","romaji":"Shiken no mae no hi ni, tango o hyakko anki shita.","german":"The day before the exam, I memorized a hundred words.","furigana":"しけん|まえ|ひ|たんご|ひゃっこあんき"}],"notes":"暗記する means \"to learn by heart,\" i.e. to commit something to memory word for word: 単語を暗記する, 公式を暗記する. It is a typical school and exam word; 丸暗記 means \"to memorize mechanically without understanding.\" Unlike 覚える (\"to remember, learn\"), 暗記 stresses literal memorizing, and compared with 記憶する the learning process is in the foreground. A common aid is the 暗記カード (\"flashcard\").","levelNote":"Derived from 暗記 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"明日は雨が降ると予想されている。","romaji":"Ashita wa ame ga furu to yosō sarete iru.","german":"Rain is forecast for tomorrow.","furigana":"あした|あめ|ふ|よそう"},{"japanese":"テストは予想以上に難しかった。","romaji":"Tesuto wa yosō ijō ni muzukashikatta.","german":"The test was harder than expected.","furigana":"よそういじょう|むずか"},{"japanese":"どっちのチームが勝つか、みんなで予想しよう。","romaji":"Dotchi no chīmu ga katsu ka, minna de yosō shiyō.","german":"Let's all guess which team will win.","furigana":"か|よそう"}],"notes":"予想する means \"to predict, to expect, to guess\"; you estimate how something will turn out: 試合の結果を予想する, 〜と予想される. Very common are 予想通り (\"as expected\"), 予想外 (\"unexpected\") and 予想以上に (\"more than expected\"). The official weather forecast is 予報 (天気予報); 予想 is the expectation itself. 予測 is similar but more data-based and technical, while 期待 is a hopeful expectation.","levelNote":"Derived from 予想 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"根拠のない憶測をするべきではない。","romaji":"Konkyo no nai okusoku o suru beki de wa nai.","german":"You shouldn't make baseless speculations.","furigana":"こんきょ|おくそく"},{"japanese":"社長の突然の辞任は、さまざまな憶測を呼んだ。","romaji":"Shachō no totsuzen no jinin wa, samazama na okusoku o yonda.","german":"The company president's sudden resignation gave rise to various speculations.","furigana":"しゃちょう|とつぜん|じにん|おくそく|よ"},{"japanese":"事実が分からないうちに、あれこれ憶測するのはやめよう。","romaji":"Jijitsu ga wakaranai uchi ni, arekore okusoku suru no wa yameyō.","german":"Let's stop speculating this and that before we know the facts.","furigana":"じじつ|わ|おくそく"}],"notes":"憶測 (also written 臆測) is a \"conjecture, speculation\" without a secure basis and usually has a critical undertone. The verb 憶測する exists, but the noun is more common in set phrases: 憶測を呼ぶ (\"to give rise to speculation\"), 憶測が飛び交う (\"speculation is rife\"), 憶測で物を言う (\"to talk without any basis\"). More neutral are 推測 (\"guess, inference\") and 予想. Typical of news and written language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この議論は全員の合意を前提としている。","romaji":"Kono giron wa zen'in no gōi o zentei to shite iru.","german":"This discussion presupposes the agreement of everyone.","furigana":"ぎろん|ぜんいん|ごうい|ぜんてい"},{"japanese":"この料金は、二年間契約することを前提としています。","romaji":"Kono ryōkin wa, ninenkan keiyaku suru koto o zentei to shite imasu.","german":"This price assumes a two-year contract.","furigana":"りょうきん|にねんかんけいやく|ぜんてい"},{"japanese":"彼とは結婚を前提として付き合っている。","romaji":"Kare to wa kekkon o zentei to shite tsukiatte iru.","german":"I am going out with him with a view to marriage.","furigana":"かれ|けっこん|ぜんてい|つ|あ"}],"notes":"前提とする means \"to presuppose, to proceed from ...\": 〜を前提とする, adverbially 〜を前提として or shortened 〜を前提に. Well known is 結婚を前提に付き合う (\"to be together with serious intent to marry\"); 〜ことが前提だ means \"the prerequisite is that ...\". The expression is neutral to formal and common in contracts, discussions and business language. 条件 is an explicitly stated \"condition\", whereas 前提 is the basis on which something tacitly builds.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は将来の展開を見通していた。","romaji":"Kare wa shōrai no tenkai o mitōshite ita.","german":"He had foreseen how things would develop in the future.","furigana":"かれ|しょうらい|てんかい|みとお"},{"japanese":"私の嘘なんて、母には全部見通されていた。","romaji":"Watashi no uso nante, haha ni wa zenbu mitōsarete ita.","german":"My mother saw right through all my lies.","furigana":"わたし|うそ|はは|ぜんぶみとお"},{"japanese":"この丘の上からは、町全体を見通すことができる。","romaji":"Kono oka no ue kara wa, machi zentai o mitōsu koto ga dekiru.","german":"From the top of this hill, you can look out over the whole town.","furigana":"おか|うえ|まちぜんたい|みとお"}],"notes":"見通す literally means \"to look far over, to see through\", figuratively \"to look ahead, to foresee\" and \"to see through (someone)\": 将来を見通す, 相手の心を見通す. The noun 見通し is even more common in daily life: 見通しが立たない (\"to be unforeseeable\"), 見通しが甘い (\"to be too optimistic in one's outlook\"), 見通しのいい道 (\"a road with good visibility\"). Compared with 予想する, 見通す stresses a wide view over a whole development; 見抜く is \"to see through, to expose\" in the sense of recognizing deception.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"パスポートの更新を申請した。","romaji":"Pasupōto no kōshin o shinsei shita.","german":"I applied for a passport renewal.","furigana":"こうしん|しんせい"},{"japanese":"大学に奨学金を申請したが、まだ結果が来ない。","romaji":"Daigaku ni shōgakukin o shinsei shita ga, mada kekka ga konai.","german":"I applied to the university for a scholarship, but I haven't heard back yet.","furigana":"だいがく|しょうがくきん|しんせい|けっか|こ"},{"japanese":"有給休暇を申請するときは、一週間前までに上司に伝えてください。","romaji":"Yūkyū kyūka o shinsei suru toki wa, isshūkan mae made ni jōshi ni tsutaete kudasai.","german":"When you apply for paid leave, please tell your supervisor at least a week in advance.","furigana":"ゆうきゅうきゅうか|しんせい|いっしゅうかんまえ|じょうし|つた"}],"notes":"申請する means \"to apply (to an authority or institution)\": ビザを申請する, 奨学金を申請する, 〜に…を申請する. The form is called 申請書, the procedure 申請手続き. Unlike 申し込む (\"to sign up, to apply\", e.g. for courses or events), 申請 is formal and is examined and approved (許可, 受理). 申告する, by contrast, means \"to declare\", for example at customs or for taxes.","levelNote":"Derived from 申請 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"毎年三月に確定申告をしなければならない。","romaji":"Maitoshi sangatsu ni kakutei shinkoku o shinakereba naranai.","german":"Every March, one has to file a tax return.","furigana":"まいとしさんがつ|かくていしんこく"},{"japanese":"税関で申告するものは何もありません。","romaji":"Zeikan de shinkoku suru mono wa nani mo arimasen.","german":"I have nothing to declare at customs.","furigana":"ぜいかん|しんこく|なに"},{"japanese":"副業の収入を申告しなかったため、後で税金を払うことになった。","romaji":"Fukugyō no shūnyū o shinkoku shinakatta tame, ato de zeikin o harau koto ni natta.","german":"Because he did not report his side-job income, he later had to pay taxes.","furigana":"ふくぎょう|しゅうにゅう|しんこく|あと|ぜいきん|はら"}],"notes":"申告する means to \"declare, report\" something to an authority that one is obliged to: 所得を申告する, 税関で申告する. Best known is 確定申告 (\"income tax return\"), which in Japan is filed from mid-February to mid-March. At customs, the question is 申告するものはありますか (\"Do you have anything to declare?\"). Unlike 申請する (\"to apply for\"), you do not ask for anything but communicate facts; 届け出る is the more general \"to report (officially)\".","levelNote":"Derived from 申告 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"引っ越したら住所変更を届け出る必要がある。","romaji":"Hikkoshitara jūsho henkō o todokederu hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"After moving, you need to report your change of address.","furigana":"ひ|こ|じゅうしょへんこう|とど|で|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"財布を盗まれたので、すぐに警察に届け出た。","romaji":"Saifu o nusumareta node, sugu ni keisatsu ni todokedeta.","german":"My wallet was stolen, so I reported it to the police right away.","furigana":"さいふ|ぬす|けいさつ|とど|で"},{"japanese":"病気で休む場合は、学校に届け出てください。","romaji":"Byōki de yasumu baai wa, gakkō ni todokedete kudasai.","german":"If you are absent because of illness, please notify the school.","furigana":"びょうき|やす|ばあい|がっこう|とど|で"}],"notes":"届け出る means to officially \"report, notify, register\" something with an office: 警察に届け出る, 市役所に出生を届け出る. The office takes に, the reported matter を. The related noun 届け出 or 届 appears in 婚姻届 (\"marriage registration\") or 欠席届 (\"notice of absence\"). 届ける alone means \"to deliver, to bring\", but also \"to hand in (lost property)\": 落とし物を交番に届ける.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"レポートを期限までに提出してください。","romaji":"Repōto o kigen made ni teishutsu shite kudasai.","german":"Please submit your report by the deadline.","furigana":"きげん|ていしゅつ"},{"japanese":"履歴書を提出したあとで、間違いに気付いた。","romaji":"Rirekisho o teishutsu shita ato de, machigai ni kizuita.","german":"After submitting my resume, I noticed a mistake.","furigana":"りれきしょ|ていしゅつ|まちが|きづ"},{"japanese":"政府は新しい法案を国会に提出した。","romaji":"Seifu wa atarashii hōan o kokkai ni teishutsu shita.","german":"The government submitted a new bill to the Diet.","furigana":"せいふ|あたら|ほうあん|こっかい|ていしゅつ"}],"notes":"提出する means \"to submit, hand in, present\" and is used for documents, homework, applications or evidence: 宿題を提出する, 書類を役所に提出する. Typical are 提出期限 (\"submission deadline\") and 〜までに提出する. In parliament it means \"to introduce a bill\": 法案を提出する. More colloquial is 出す (\"to hand in\"); 申請する, on the other hand, refers to the application itself, not to handing in papers.","levelNote":"Derived from 提出 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"申請書が正式に受理された。","romaji":"Shinseisho ga seishiki ni juri sareta.","german":"The application was formally accepted.","furigana":"しんせいしょ|せいしき|じゅり"},{"japanese":"部長に辞表を出したが、なかなか受理してもらえない。","romaji":"Buchō ni jihyō o dashita ga, nakanaka juri shite moraenai.","german":"I submitted my resignation to the department head, but he won't accept it.","furigana":"ぶちょう|じひょう|だ|じゅり"},{"japanese":"必要な書類が足りなかったので、申し込みは受理されなかった。","romaji":"Hitsuyō na shorui ga tarinakatta node, mōshikomi wa juri sarenakatta.","german":"Because required documents were missing, the application was not accepted.","furigana":"ひつよう|しょるい|た|もう|こ|じゅり"}],"notes":"受理する means to officially \"accept, receive\" a submitted document and thereby take it up for processing: 申請を受理する, 辞表を受理する (\"to accept a resignation letter\"). It is a formal official and business word and is usually in the passive: 書類が受理された. Important: 受理 only means that something was formally accepted; no decision on approval (許可, 認可) has been made yet. The opposite is 却下 (\"rejection\") or 不受理.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新薬がようやく認可された。","romaji":"Shin'yaku ga yōyaku ninka sareta.","german":"The new drug was finally approved.","furigana":"しんやく|にんか"},{"japanese":"新しい大学の設立が、国に認可された。","romaji":"Atarashii daigaku no setsuritsu ga, kuni ni ninka sareta.","german":"The establishment of the new university was approved by the state.","furigana":"あたら|だいがく|せつりつ|くに|にんか"},{"japanese":"子どもを認可保育園に入れたいが、空きがない。","romaji":"Kodomo o ninka hoikuen ni iretai ga, aki ga nai.","german":"I want to put my child in a licensed daycare center, but there are no openings.","furigana":"こ|にんかほいくえん|い|あ"}],"notes":"認可する means that an authority officially \"approves, permits, licenses\" something: 国が設立を認可する, 認可を受ける/得る. Typical compounds are 認可保育園 (\"state-licensed daycare\") and 無認可 (\"unlicensed\"). Compared with 許可する (\"to allow\", also in everyday use), 認可 is an administrative term for companies, schools, products or fees. For medicines, 承認 is usually used in technical contexts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の提案は会議で却下された。","romaji":"Kare no teian wa kaigi de kyakka sareta.","german":"His proposal was rejected at the meeting.","furigana":"かれ|ていあん|かいぎ|きゃっか"},{"japanese":"ビザの申請が却下されたので、旅行をあきらめた。","romaji":"Biza no shinsei ga kyakka sareta node, ryokō o akirameta.","german":"My visa application was rejected, so I gave up the trip.","furigana":"しんせい|きゃっか|りょこう"},{"japanese":"「週休三日にしようよ。」「却下！」","romaji":"\"Shūkyū mikka ni shiyō yo.\" \"Kyakka!\"","german":"\"Let's make it a three-day weekend.\" \"Rejected!\"","furigana":"しゅうきゅうみっか|きゃっか"}],"notes":"却下する means to \"reject, dismiss\" an application, proposal or demand: 申請を却下する, 提案が却下される, mostly in the passive. It is formal but is also used jokingly in everyday life (却下! \"Rejected!\"). In court, 却下 means dismissal on formal grounds without examining the merits, whereas 棄却 is dismissal after examining the merits. More general and less official is 断る (\"to decline, to refuse\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"裁判所が訴えを棄却した。","romaji":"Saibansho ga uttae o kikyaku shita.","german":"The court dismissed the lawsuit.","furigana":"さいばんしょ|うった|ききゃく"},{"japanese":"高等裁判所は、被告の控訴を棄却した。","romaji":"Kōtō saibansho wa, hikoku no kōso o kikyaku shita.","german":"The High Court dismissed the defendant's appeal.","furigana":"こうとうさいばんしょ|ひこく|こうそ|ききゃく"},{"japanese":"データを分析した結果、最初の仮説は棄却された。","romaji":"Dēta o bunseki shita kekka, saisho no kasetsu wa kikyaku sareta.","german":"As a result of analyzing the data, the initial hypothesis was rejected.","furigana":"ぶんせき|けっか|さいしょ|かせつ|ききゃく"}],"notes":"棄却する is a legal technical term: a court dismisses a lawsuit, appeal or motion as unfounded after examining the merits: 訴えを棄却する, 控訴が棄却される. By contrast, 却下 refers to dismissal as inadmissible on formal grounds. Outside law, the word appears in statistics: 仮説を棄却する (\"to reject a hypothesis\"). In everyday life it practically appears only in the news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"原告は訴訟を取り下げた。","romaji":"Genkoku wa soshō o torisageta.","german":"The plaintiff withdrew the lawsuit.","furigana":"げんこく|そしょう|と|さ"},{"japanese":"反対意見が多かったので、提案を取り下げることにした。","romaji":"Hantai iken ga ōkatta node, teian o torisageru koto ni shita.","german":"Because there was a lot of opposition, I decided to withdraw my proposal.","furigana":"はんたいいけん|おお|ていあん|と|さ"},{"japanese":"就職が決まったので、奨学金の申請を取り下げた。","romaji":"Shūshoku ga kimatta node, shōgakukin no shinsei o torisageta.","german":"Since I had found a job, I withdrew my scholarship application.","furigana":"しゅうしょく|き|しょうがくきん|しんせい|と|さ"}],"notes":"取り下げる means to \"withdraw\" of one's own accord something one has submitted or put forward oneself: 訴えを取り下げる, 申請を取り下げる, 提案を取り下げる. Unlike 却下される (being rejected by the other side), the action here comes from the applicant themselves. The noun is 取り下げ. For taking back a statement or promise, 撤回する is more fitting.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい学生証が発行された。","romaji":"Atarashii gakuseishō ga hakkō sareta.","german":"A new student ID card has been issued.","furigana":"あたら|がくせいしょう|はっこう"},{"japanese":"すみません、領収書を発行していただけますか。","romaji":"Sumimasen, ryōshūsho o hakkō shite itadakemasu ka.","german":"Excuse me, could you issue a receipt?","furigana":"りょうしゅうしょ|はっこう"},{"japanese":"この雑誌は月に一回発行されている。","romaji":"Kono zasshi wa tsuki ni ikkai hakkō sarete iru.","german":"This magazine is published once a month.","furigana":"ざっし|つき|いっかいはっこう"}],"notes":"発行する means \"to issue, publish\" and is used for printed matter (雑誌を発行する), documents (証明書・パスポートを発行する), banknotes, shares and tickets. In everyday speech you often hear 領収書を発行する (\"to issue a receipt\") or パスワードを再発行する. 発行 stresses that an authority officially produces and issues something; 交付 stresses handing it over to a person, 発給 the official issuing of passports and visas. 発行部数 is the \"circulation.\"","levelNote":"Derived from 発行 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"運転免許証が交付された。","romaji":"Unten menkyoshō ga kōfu sareta.","german":"A driver's license was issued.","furigana":"うんてんめんきょしょう|こうふ"},{"japanese":"マイナンバーカードは、市役所の窓口で交付されます。","romaji":"Mainanbā kādo wa, shiyakusho no madoguchi de kōfu saremasu.","german":"My Number cards are issued at the city hall counter.","furigana":"しやくしょ|まどぐち|こうふ"},{"japanese":"市から引っ越し費用の補助金が交付された。","romaji":"Shi kara hikkoshi hiyō no hojokin ga kōfu sareta.","german":"The city granted a subsidy for moving expenses.","furigana":"し|ひ|こ|ひよう|ほじょきん|こうふ"}],"notes":"交付する is an official word for the formal \"handing over, issuing\" of documents or funds by an authority: 免許証を交付する, 補助金を交付する. From the recipient's viewpoint it usually appears in the passive (〜が交付される) or as 交付を受ける. At counters you often read 交付窓口 (\"issuing counter\"). Compared with 発行 (creating and issuing), the focus is on handing over to the entitled person; in everyday speech one simply says 渡す or もらう.","levelNote":"Derived from 交付 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ビザは大使館で発給される。","romaji":"Biza wa taishikan de hakkyū sareru.","german":"Visas are issued at the embassy.","furigana":"たいしかん|はっきゅう"},{"japanese":"パスポートの発給には、通常一週間ほどかかる。","romaji":"Pasupōto no hakkyū ni wa, tsūjō isshūkan hodo kakaru.","german":"Issuing a passport usually takes about a week.","furigana":"はっきゅう|つうじょういっしゅうかん"},{"japanese":"その国は、観光客へのビザの発給を一時停止した。","romaji":"Sono kuni wa, kankōkyaku e no biza no hakkyū o ichiji teishi shita.","german":"That country temporarily suspended the issuance of visas to tourists.","furigana":"くに|かんこうきゃく|はっきゅう|いちじていし"}],"notes":"発給する is an official expression for the \"issuing\" of passports, visas or permits by an authority: ビザを発給する, 旅券の発給. It appears almost only in administrative texts, on embassy websites and in the news, mostly in the passive. Compared with the broader 発行 (also for magazines, receipts, etc.), 発給 is limited to such papers; in everyday speech people often simply say ビザが出る or ビザが下りる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"免許証を更新するために警察署に行った。","romaji":"Menkyoshō o kōshin suru tame ni keisatsusho ni itta.","german":"I went to the police station to renew my driver's license.","furigana":"めんきょしょう|こうしん|けいさつしょ|い"},{"japanese":"住所が変わったら、登録情報を更新してください。","romaji":"Jūsho ga kawattara, tōroku jōhō o kōshin shite kudasai.","german":"If your address changes, please update your registration information.","furigana":"じゅうしょ|か|とうろくじょうほう|こうしん"},{"japanese":"彼女はマラソンの日本記録を更新した。","romaji":"Kanojo wa marason no Nihon kiroku o kōshin shita.","german":"She broke the Japanese marathon record.","furigana":"かのじょ|にほんきろく|こうしん"}],"notes":"更新する means \"to renew, to extend, to update\", translated differently depending on the object: 契約・免許を更新する (\"to renew\"), 情報・ページを更新する (\"to update\"), 記録を更新する (\"to break a record\"). In IT it is the standard word for \"update\" or \"reload\" (ページを更新する); アップデート is also widespread. In contrast to 新しくする (\"to make new\"), 更新 usually means continuing something existing in a new version. Often in the passive: 世界記録が更新された.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい設定を有効にするには再起動が必要だ。","romaji":"Atarashii settei o yūkō ni suru ni wa saikidō ga hitsuyō da.","german":"A restart is required to enable the new settings.","furigana":"あたら|せってい|ゆうこう|さいきどう|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"安全のため、二段階認証を有効にしてください。","romaji":"Anzen no tame, nidankai ninshō o yūkō ni shite kudasai.","german":"For safety, please enable two-factor authentication.","furigana":"あんぜん|にだんかいにんしょう|ゆうこう"},{"japanese":"期限が切れたポイントを、もう一度有効にすることはできません。","romaji":"Kigen ga kireta pointo o, mō ichido yūkō ni suru koto wa dekimasen.","german":"Expired points cannot be reactivated.","furigana":"きげん|き|いちどゆうこう"}],"notes":"有効にする means \"to make valid, to activate, to make effective\" and is the standard word for \"to enable, to turn on\" above all in IT instructions: 機能を有効にする, 設定を有効にする. Opposite: 無効にする (\"to disable\"). Grammatically it is the na-adjective 有効 + にする (\"to make into ...\"); as a state one says 有効になっている. On its own, 有効 means \"valid\" (このチケットは三日間有効だ) or \"effective\" (有効な方法).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"古いパスワードを無効にした。","romaji":"Furui pasuwādo o mukō ni shita.","german":"I invalidated the old password.","furigana":"ふる|むこう"},{"japanese":"通知が多すぎるので、このアプリの通知を無効にした。","romaji":"Tsūchi ga ōsugiru node, kono apuri no tsūchi o mukō ni shita.","german":"There were too many notifications, so I disabled notifications for this app.","furigana":"つうち|おお|つうち|むこう"},{"japanese":"カードをなくしたら、すぐに銀行に連絡して無効にしてもらいましょう。","romaji":"Kādo o nakushitara, sugu ni ginkō ni renraku shite mukō ni shite moraimashō.","german":"If you lose your card, contact the bank right away and have it canceled.","furigana":"ぎんこう|れんらく|むこう"}],"notes":"無効にする means \"to make invalid, to nullify, to disable\": パスワードを無効にする, 契約を無効にする. In software menus it is the counterpart of 有効にする (\"to enable\"). Grammatically: na-adjective 無効 (\"invalid, ineffective\") + にする; the state is called 無効になる (\"to become invalid\"), for example with expired coupons. Note: 無効 (むこう) sounds exactly like 向こう (\"over there, the other side\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい法律が来月から施行される。","romaji":"Atarashii hōritsu ga raigetsu kara shikō sareru.","german":"The new law will take effect next month.","furigana":"あたら|ほうりつ|らいげつ|しこう"},{"japanese":"この規則は、施行されてから一度も変更されていない。","romaji":"Kono kisoku wa, shikō sarete kara ichido mo henkō sarete inai.","german":"This regulation has not been changed even once since it came into force.","furigana":"きそく|しこう|いちど|へんこう"},{"japanese":"政府は、法律の施行に向けて準備を進めている。","romaji":"Seifu wa, hōritsu no shikō ni mukete junbi o susumete iru.","german":"The government is making preparations for the enforcement of the law.","furigana":"せいふ|ほうりつ|しこう|む|じゅんび|すす"}],"notes":"施行する means to put a law or ordinance into effect: 法律を施行する, 〜から施行される. It mostly appears in the passive with a time expression; the noun occurs in 施行日 (\"effective date\"). To distinguish it from 試行 (\"trial run\"), jurists sometimes read it せこう. Distinguish it from 実施 (\"to carry out\", e.g. surveys, exams) and 制定 (\"to enact\"): a law is first 制定 and then 施行. Very formal, typical of news and official texts.","levelNote":"Derived from 施行 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"留学生は授業料を免除された。","romaji":"Ryūgakusei wa jugyōryō o menjo sareta.","german":"The international student was exempted from tuition fees.","furigana":"りゅうがくせい|じゅぎょうりょう|めんじょ"},{"japanese":"日本語能力試験のN2を持っている人は、筆記試験が免除される。","romaji":"Nihongo Nōryoku Shiken no N2 o motte iru hito wa, hikki shiken ga menjo sareru.","german":"Those who hold JLPT N2 are exempt from the written exam.","furigana":"にほんごのうりょくしけん|も|ひと|ひっきしけん|めんじょ"},{"japanese":"収入が少ない場合は、保険料の支払いが免除されることがある。","romaji":"Shūnyū ga sukunai baai wa, hokenryō no shiharai ga menjo sareru koto ga aru.","german":"If income is low, one may be exempted from paying insurance premiums.","furigana":"しゅうにゅう|すく|ばあい|ほけんりょう|しはら|めんじょ"}],"notes":"免除する means to exempt someone from an obligation, fee or exam, or to waive it for them: 授業料を免除する, 試験を免除する. In the passive, the person takes は and the waived item takes を, or the item is the subject with が: 学生は授業料を免除された / 授業料が免除された. Common compounds: 授業料免除, 免除申請, 一部免除 (\"partial exemption\"). It is formal; unlike 許す (\"to forgive, allow\"), it concerns a regulated, official exception.","levelNote":"Derived from 免除 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ひらがなを漢字に変換する。","romaji":"Hiragana o kanji ni henkan suru.","german":"To convert hiragana into kanji.","furigana":"かんじ|へんかん"},{"japanese":"この資料をPDFに変換してから送ってください。","romaji":"Kono shiryō o PDF ni henkan shite kara okutte kudasai.","german":"Please convert these documents to PDF before sending them.","furigana":"しりょう|へんかん|おく"},{"japanese":"変換ミスで、メールに変な漢字が入っていた。","romaji":"Henkan misu de, mēru ni hen na kanji ga haitte ita.","german":"Because of a conversion error, a strange kanji got into the email.","furigana":"へんかん|へん|かんじ|はい"}],"notes":"変換する means \"to convert, to transform\" and refers to changing from one form into another: AをBに変換する. In everyday life it is met mainly on the computer: ひらがなを漢字に変換する (kanji conversion while typing), ファイルをPDFに変換する, 変換ミス (\"error in kanji conversion\"). In technology it is used e.g. as 電気を熱に変換する. Unlike 変更する (\"to change\") and 変える (\"to alter\"), the content stays the same and only the form changes.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"会議の日程を変更した。","romaji":"Kaigi no nittei o henkō shita.","german":"I changed the schedule of the meeting.","furigana":"かいぎ|にってい|へんこう"},{"japanese":"雨のため、試合の会場が変更になりました。","romaji":"Ame no tame, shiai no kaijō ga henkō ni narimashita.","german":"Because of the rain, the venue of the game has been changed.","furigana":"あめ|しあい|かいじょう|へんこう"},{"japanese":"三か月ごとにパスワードを変更するように言われている。","romaji":"Sankagetsu goto ni pasuwādo o henkō suru yō ni iwarete iru.","german":"We are told to change our passwords every three months.","furigana":"さん|げつ|へんこう|い"}],"notes":"変更する means \"to change, alter\" - above all plans, dates, settings and rules: 予定を変更する, パスワードを変更する. In announcements, 〜が変更になりました (\"has been changed\") often appears. Compared with 変える, it is more factual and formal; unlike 変換 (\"to convert the form\") and 改正 (\"to amend a law\"), it is the general word for changes to something that has been fixed. Common compounds: 変更点 (\"change\"), 予定変更, 住所変更.","levelNote":"Derived from 変更 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"憲法を改正するには国民投票が必要だ。","romaji":"Kenpō o kaisei suru ni wa kokumin tōhyō ga hitsuyō da.","german":"A national referendum is required to amend the constitution.","furigana":"けんぽう|かいせい|こくみんとうひょう|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"法律が改正されて、十八歳から成人になった。","romaji":"Hōritsu ga kaisei sarete, jūhassai kara seijin ni natta.","german":"The law was revised, and people now become adults at eighteen.","furigana":"ほうりつ|かいせい|じゅうはっさい|せいじん"},{"japanese":"クラブの規則を改正して、新しい会員も投票できるようにした。","romaji":"Kurabu no kisoku o kaisei shite, atarashii kaiin mo tōhyō dekiru yō ni shita.","german":"We amended the club's rules so that new members can also vote.","furigana":"きそく|かいせい|あたら|かいいん|とうひょう"}],"notes":"改正する means to formally \"amend, revise\" laws, regulations or rules: 法律を改正する, 憲法改正, 〜の一部を改正する. It belongs to legal and news language; the objects are almost always norms. To be distinguished are the two かいてい words 改定 (\"to reset prices, rates\") and 改訂 (\"to revise texts, books\"). 改革 is a more comprehensive \"reform\" of systems.","levelNote":"Derived from 改正 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"来月から料金が改定される。","romaji":"Raigetsu kara ryōkin ga kaitei sareru.","german":"Fees will be revised from next month.","furigana":"らいげつ|りょうきん|かいてい"},{"japanese":"会社は四月に給与規定を改定した。","romaji":"Kaisha wa shigatsu ni kyūyo kitei o kaitei shita.","german":"The company revised its salary regulations in April.","furigana":"かいしゃ|しがつ|きゅうよきてい|かいてい"},{"japanese":"最低賃金が改定され、時給が五十円上がった。","romaji":"Saitei chingin ga kaitei sare, jikyū ga gojū en agatta.","german":"The minimum wage was revised, and the hourly wage went up by 50 yen.","furigana":"さいていちんぎん|かいてい|じきゅう|ごじゅうえんあ"}],"notes":"改定する means to revise established values such as prices, fees, wages or sets of rules: 料金を改定する, 運賃改定, 価格改定のお知らせ. In company announcements it is often the neutral way of saying a price increase. Do not confuse it with the identically read 改訂 (\"to revise texts\", e.g. textbooks) and with 改正 (\"to amend laws\"). Very formal, typical of notices, official letters and news.","levelNote":"Derived from 改定 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"教科書が十年ぶりに改訂された。","romaji":"Kyōkasho ga jūnenburi ni kaitei sareta.","german":"The textbook was revised for the first time in ten years.","furigana":"きょうかしょ|じゅうねん|かいてい"},{"japanese":"この辞書は、新しい言葉を加えて改訂された。","romaji":"Kono jisho wa, atarashii kotoba o kuwaete kaitei sareta.","german":"This dictionary was revised with new words added.","furigana":"じしょ|あたら|ことば|くわ|かいてい"},{"japanese":"マニュアルを改訂したので、新しい版を使ってください。","romaji":"Manyuaru o kaitei shita node, atarashii han o tsukatte kudasai.","german":"We have revised the manual, so please use the new edition.","furigana":"かいてい|あたら|はん|つか"}],"notes":"改訂する means to revise the content of an existing text - a book, dictionary, manual, curriculum - and bring out a new edition: 教科書を改訂する, 辞書の改訂版 (\"revised edition\"). The character 訂 (\"to correct\") points to the written word, as in 訂正. Do not confuse it with the identically read 改定 (\"to revise prices, fees\") and with 改正 (\"to amend laws\"). The word is formal and typical of publishers and educational institutions.","levelNote":"Derived from 改訂 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不公平な制度を是正する必要がある。","romaji":"Fukōhei na seido o zesei suru hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"It is necessary to correct the unfair system.","furigana":"ふこうへい|せいど|ぜせい|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"政府は、地方と都市の格差を是正するための政策を発表した。","romaji":"Seifu wa, chihō to toshi no kakusa o zesei suru tame no seisaku o happyō shita.","german":"The government announced policies to reduce the gap between rural areas and cities.","furigana":"せいふ|ちほう|とし|かくさ|ぜせい|せいさく|はっぴょう"},{"japanese":"残業が多すぎると指摘され、会社は働き方を是正した。","romaji":"Zangyō ga ōsugiru to shiteki sare, kaisha wa hatarakikata o zesei shita.","german":"After being told it had too much overtime, the company changed how its employees work.","furigana":"ざんぎょう|おお|してき|かいしゃ|はたら|かた|ぜせい"}],"notes":"是正する means to correct or remedy a problem or inequality: 格差を是正する, 不平等を是正する, 長時間労働の是正. The object is almost always a social or organizational problem, not an individual mistake - for typos or calculation errors one says 訂正する or 直す. The word is formal and typical of politics, administration and news; authorities issue, for example, a 是正勧告 (\"recommendation to rectify\").","levelNote":"Derived from 是正 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"歯並びを矯正するために歯医者に通っている。","romaji":"Hanarabi o kyōsei suru tame ni haisha ni kayotte iru.","german":"I go to the dentist to have my teeth straightened.","furigana":"はなら|きょうせい|はいしゃ|かよ"},{"japanese":"毎日ストレッチをして、悪い姿勢を矯正している。","romaji":"Mainichi sutoretchi o shite, warui shisei o kyōsei shite iru.","german":"I stretch every day to correct my bad posture.","furigana":"まいにち|わる|しせい|きょうせい"},{"japanese":"左利きを無理に矯正するのは、よくないと言われている。","romaji":"Hidarikiki o muri ni kyōsei suru no wa, yoku nai to iwarete iru.","german":"It is said that forcibly correcting left-handedness is not good.","furigana":"ひだりき|むり|きょうせい|い"}],"notes":"矯正する means to deliberately \"correct, straighten\" something crooked or a bad habit: 歯並びを矯正する, 姿勢を矯正する, 視力を矯正する (\"to correct poor eyesight\", e.g. with glasses). Well known is 歯列矯正 (\"orthodontic treatment, braces\"). Unlike 是正 (social ills) and 訂正 (errors in texts), 矯正 mostly concerns the body or behavior. In the justice system it also refers to the rehabilitation of offenders.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"写真の明るさを補正した。","romaji":"Shashin no akarusa o hosei shita.","german":"I adjusted the brightness of the photo.","furigana":"しゃしん|あか|ほせい"},{"japanese":"このカメラには、手ぶれ補正の機能が付いている。","romaji":"Kono kamera ni wa, tebure hosei no kinō ga tsuite iru.","german":"This camera has an image stabilization function.","furigana":"て|ほせい|きのう|つ"},{"japanese":"政府は災害対策のため、補正予算を組んだ。","romaji":"Seifu wa saigai taisaku no tame, hosei yosan o kunda.","german":"The government drew up a supplementary budget for disaster countermeasures.","furigana":"せいふ|さいがいたいさく|ほせいよさん|く"}],"notes":"補正する means to \"correct, readjust\" values or deviations by supplementing or compensating: 明るさを補正する, 誤差を補正する. It is common in technology and photography (手ぶれ補正 \"image stabilization\", 色補正 \"color correction\") and in budget politics: 補正予算 (\"supplementary budget\"). Unlike 修正 (\"to correct, to change\"), 補正 stresses adjusting to a target value.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"スケジュールを調整して会議に出席した。","romaji":"Sukejūru o chōsei shite kaigi ni shusseki shita.","german":"I adjusted my schedule and attended the meeting.","furigana":"ちょうせい|かいぎ|しゅっせき"},{"japanese":"椅子の高さを調整して、画面を見やすくした。","romaji":"Isu no takasa o chōsei shite, gamen o miyasuku shita.","german":"I adjusted the height of the chair to make the screen easier to see.","furigana":"いす|たか|ちょうせい|がめん|み"},{"japanese":"来週の打ち合わせの日時は、現在調整中です。","romaji":"Raishū no uchiawase no nichiji wa, genzai chōseichū desu.","german":"The date and time of next week's meeting is currently being arranged.","furigana":"らいしゅう|う|あ|にちじ|げんざいちょうせいちゅう"}],"notes":"調整する means \"to adjust, to set, to coordinate\", both technically and organizationally: 音量・温度を調整する (\"to adjust\"), 日程を調整する (\"to coordinate schedules\"), 意見を調整する (\"to bring opinions into line\"). In the business world, 調整中 (\"being arranged\") is a common answer, often also an evasive one. Unlike 調節 (technically regulating amounts or degrees, e.g. 温度調節), 調整 also includes coordinating between people.","levelNote":"Derived from 調整 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい環境に適応するのに時間がかかった。","romaji":"Atarashii kankyō ni tekiō suru no ni jikan ga kakatta.","german":"It took time to adapt to the new environment.","furigana":"あたら|かんきょう|てきおう|じかん"},{"japanese":"この植物は、寒い気候にも適応できる。","romaji":"Kono shokubutsu wa, samui kikō ni mo tekiō dekiru.","german":"This plant can adapt even to cold climates.","furigana":"しょくぶつ|さむ|きこう|てきおう"},{"japanese":"時代の変化に適応できない会社は、生き残れない。","romaji":"Jidai no henka ni tekiō dekinai kaisha wa, ikinokorenai.","german":"Companies that cannot adapt to changing times will not survive.","furigana":"じだい|へんか|てきおう|かいしゃ|い|のこ"}],"notes":"適応する means \"to adapt\" to an environment or situation and is intransitive: 環境に適応する, 変化に適応する. Typical are 適応力 (\"adaptability\") and, in psychology, 適応障害 (\"adjustment disorder\"). Unlike 慣れる (\"to get used to\"), it sounds more factual and stresses active adjustment. Do not confuse it with 適用 (\"to apply a rule\") and 対応 (\"to respond to something\").","levelNote":"Derived from 適応 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"問題に冷静に対処することが重要だ。","romaji":"Mondai ni reisei ni taisho suru koto ga jūyō da.","german":"It is important to deal with problems calmly.","furigana":"もんだい|れいせい|たいしょ|じゅうよう"},{"japanese":"地震が起きたときにどう対処すればいいか、家族で話し合った。","romaji":"Jishin ga okita toki ni dō taisho sureba ii ka, kazoku de hanashiatta.","german":"We talked as a family about how to handle things when an earthquake occurs.","furigana":"じしん|お|たいしょ|かぞく|はな|あ"},{"japanese":"お客様からのクレームには、責任者が対処します。","romaji":"Okyakusama kara no kurēmu ni wa, sekininsha ga taisho shimasu.","german":"The person in charge will deal with complaints from customers.","furigana":"きゃくさま|せきにんしゃ|たいしょ"}],"notes":"対処する means to respond appropriately to a problem or situation: \"to deal with, handle, take measures\": 問題に対処する, トラブルに対処する. The problem takes に; typical adverbs are 冷静に, 迅速に, 適切に. It is neutral to formal. 対応する is similar but broader (also \"to serve customers, correspond to\"), whereas 対処 specifically means coping with a difficulty; 処理する stresses working through tasks or data.","levelNote":"Derived from 対処 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大量のデータを短時間で処理した。","romaji":"Tairyō no dēta o tanjikan de shori shita.","german":"I processed a large amount of data in a short time.","furigana":"たいりょう|たんじかん|しょり"},{"japanese":"今日中に、たまったメールを全部処理しなければならない。","romaji":"Kyōjū ni, tamatta mēru o zenbu shori shinakereba naranai.","german":"I have to deal with all the accumulated emails today.","furigana":"きょうじゅう|ぜんぶしょり"},{"japanese":"この工場では、汚れた水をきれいに処理してから川に流している。","romaji":"Kono kōjō de wa, yogoreta mizu o kirei ni shori shite kara kawa ni nagashite iru.","german":"This factory treats the dirty water until it is clean before releasing it into the river.","furigana":"こうじょう|よご|みず|しょり|かわ|なが"}],"notes":"処理する means to \"handle, deal with, process\" things or tasks: 仕事を処理する, データを処理する, ゴミを処理する (\"to dispose of or treat garbage\"). In IT, 処理 is \"processing, operation\" (処理速度, 処理中). Unlike 対処する (reacting to a problem), the focus is on working through something in a matter-of-fact way; 処分する, on the other hand, means \"to get rid of, dispose of\" or \"to punish.\" The word is neutral and very common in offices, administration and technology.","levelNote":"Derived from 処理 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"古い家具を処分することにした。","romaji":"Furui kagu o shobun suru koto ni shita.","german":"I decided to get rid of the old furniture.","furigana":"ふる|かぐ|しょぶん"},{"japanese":"壊れた家電は、市のルールに従って処分してください。","romaji":"Kowareta kaden wa, shi no rūru ni shitagatte shobun shite kudasai.","german":"Please dispose of broken appliances according to the city's rules.","furigana":"こわ|かでん|し|したが|しょぶん"},{"japanese":"会社は、情報を漏らした社員を処分した。","romaji":"Kaisha wa, jōhō o morashita shain o shobun shita.","german":"The company disciplined the employee who leaked information.","furigana":"かいしゃ|じょうほう|も|しゃいん|しょぶん"}],"notes":"処分する first means \"to get rid of, dispose of, sell off\": 古い家具を処分する, 不用品を処分する. Second, it means \"to punish, take disciplinary action against\": 社員を処分する, 停学処分 (\"temporary suspension from school\"), 懲戒処分 (\"disciplinary action\"). Unlike 捨てる (\"to throw away\"), 処分 is more neutral and also includes selling or giving away. With animals it is a euphemism for \"to kill, put down\".","levelNote":"Derived from 処分 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"期限切れの食品を廃棄した。","romaji":"Kigengire no shokuhin o haiki shita.","german":"Expired food was disposed of.","furigana":"きげんぎ|しょくひん|はいき"},{"japanese":"個人情報が書かれた書類は、シュレッダーで廃棄してください。","romaji":"Kojin jōhō ga kakareta shorui wa, shureddā de haiki shite kudasai.","german":"Please dispose of documents with personal information in a shredder.","furigana":"こじんじょうほう|か|しょるい|はいき"},{"japanese":"古いパソコンを廃棄する前に、データを完全に消しておこう。","romaji":"Furui pasokon o haiki suru mae ni, dēta o kanzen ni keshite okō.","german":"Before we discard the old computer, let's erase the data completely.","furigana":"ふる|はいき|まえ|かんぜん|け"}],"notes":"廃棄する means to officially dispose of or destroy something unusable or superfluous - typically food, files, old appliances or industrial waste: 書類を廃棄する. The word is factual-formal and sounds like a company or authority; in everyday speech people mostly just say 捨てる \"to throw away\". Common derivations are 廃棄物 \"waste\" and 食品廃棄 \"food waste\". In reports the passive 廃棄される often appears.","levelNote":"Derived from 廃棄 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"政府は発言を撤回した。","romaji":"Seifu wa hatsugen o tekkai shita.","german":"The government retracted its statement.","furigana":"せいふ|はつげん|てっかい"},{"japanese":"住民の反対を受けて、市は建設計画を撤回した。","romaji":"Jūmin no hantai o ukete, shi wa kensetsu keikaku o tekkai shita.","german":"In response to residents' opposition, the city withdrew its construction plan.","furigana":"じゅうみん|はんたい|う|し|けんせつけいかく|てっかい"},{"japanese":"さっきの言葉は撤回するよ。言い過ぎた。","romaji":"Sakki no kotoba wa tekkai suru yo. Iisugita.","german":"I take back what I said just now. I went too far.","furigana":"ことば|てっかい|い|す"}],"notes":"撤回する means to officially \"take back, withdraw\" a statement, application or decision already made: 発言を撤回する, 前言を撤回する, 処分を撤回する. It is formal and typical of politics, law and company announcements; the retraction is often demanded: 撤回を求める. Unlike 取り消す (generally \"to undo, to cancel\", also reservations), 撤回 stresses withdrawing a position one has taken.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不要な規制を撤廃するべきだ。","romaji":"Fuyō na kisei o teppai suru beki da.","german":"Unnecessary regulations should be abolished.","furigana":"ふよう|きせい|てっぱい"},{"japanese":"両国は輸入品の関税を撤廃することで合意した。","romaji":"Ryōkoku wa yunyūhin no kanzei o teppai suru koto de gōi shita.","german":"The two countries agreed to eliminate tariffs on imported goods.","furigana":"りょうこく|ゆにゅうひん|かんぜい|てっぱい|ごうい"},{"japanese":"この会社では服装の規則が撤廃され、私服で働けるようになった。","romaji":"Kono kaisha de wa fukusō no kisoku ga teppai sare, shifuku de hatarakeru yō ni natta.","german":"At this company the dress code was abolished, and people can now work in casual clothes.","furigana":"かいしゃ|ふくそう|きそく|てっぱい|しふく|はたら"}],"notes":"撤廃する means to completely \"abolish\" or \"lift\" existing rules, systems or restrictions: 規制を撤廃する, 関税を撤廃する, 差別を撤廃する. It is a formal word from politics, business and the news. Unlike 撤回 (taking back a statement or decision), 撤廃 is directed against existing institutions. Similar is 廃止する \"to abolish\"; 緩和する, by contrast, only means \"to relax, to ease\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"各部署の意見を取りまとめて報告した。","romaji":"Kaku busho no iken o torimatomete hōkoku shita.","german":"I compiled the opinions of each department and reported them.","furigana":"かくぶしょ|いけん|と|ほうこく"},{"japanese":"参加希望者の名前を金曜日までに取りまとめてください。","romaji":"Sanka kibōsha no namae o kin'yōbi made ni torimatomete kudasai.","german":"Please collect the names of those who wish to participate by Friday.","furigana":"さんかきぼうしゃ|なまえ|きんようび|と"},{"japanese":"社長が間に入って、両社の契約を取りまとめた。","romaji":"Shachō ga aida ni haitte, ryōsha no keiyaku o torimatometa.","german":"The company president stepped in and brought the contract between the two firms to a conclusion.","furigana":"しゃちょう|あいだ|はい|りょうしゃ|けいやく|と"}],"notes":"取りまとめる means to \"collect and combine into a whole\" scattered opinions, data or documents: 意見を取りまとめる, 報告書を取りまとめる. A second meaning is to \"bring to a conclusion\" negotiations or a deal: 契約を取りまとめる. Compared with plain まとめる it sounds more formal and stresses the coordinating role; typical in the office and in the news. The noun 取りまとめ is also common, e.g. 取りまとめ役 \"coordinator\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"証拠がないのに断定するのは危険だ。","romaji":"Shōko ga nai noni dantei suru no wa kiken da.","german":"It is dangerous to make definitive claims without evidence.","furigana":"しょうこ|だんてい|きけん"},{"japanese":"警察は火事の原因を放火と断定した。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa kaji no gen'in o hōka to dantei shita.","german":"The police concluded that the fire was arson.","furigana":"けいさつ|かじ|げんいん|ほうか|だんてい"},{"japanese":"現時点では、どちらが正しいか断定できません。","romaji":"Genjiten de wa, dochira ga tadashii ka dantei dekimasen.","german":"At this point, we cannot say definitively which is correct.","furigana":"げんじてん|ただ|だんてい"}],"notes":"断定する means to \"declare\" something as established with certainty or to \"determine definitively\", often in the pattern Xを〜と断定する: 警察は事故と断定した. Common is the negative 断定できない \"cannot be said with certainty\". Unlike 主張する (\"to assert, to advocate one's opinion\"), it concerns a final judgment. In grammar, 断定 denotes the definite form of statement with だ or である.","levelNote":"Derived from 断定 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の無実を確信している。","romaji":"Kare no mujitsu o kakushin shite iru.","german":"I am convinced of his innocence.","furigana":"かれ|むじつ|かくしん"},{"japanese":"ゴールが決まった瞬間、私たちは勝利を確信した。","romaji":"Gōru ga kimatta shunkan, watashitachi wa shōri o kakushin shita.","german":"The moment the goal was scored, we were certain of victory.","furigana":"き|しゅんかん|わたし|しょうり|かくしん"},{"japanese":"正直に言うと、この方法が正しいという確信はない。","romaji":"Shōjiki ni iu to, kono hōhō ga tadashii to iu kakushin wa nai.","german":"Honestly, I'm not confident that this method is right.","furigana":"しょうじき|い|ほうほう|ただ|かくしん"}],"notes":"確信する means \"to be firmly convinced\" that something is true or will happen. Typical patterns are Xを確信する (勝利を確信する) and 〜と確信する; for the ongoing state 確信している is usual. As a noun: 確信がある/ない \"to be sure / not sure\", 確信を持つ. 信じる is simple \"to believe\"; 確信 stresses certainty. Do not confuse it with the homophone 革新 \"innovation\".","levelNote":"Derived from 確信 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自分の過ちを悟って深く反省した。","romaji":"Jibun no ayamachi o satotte fukaku hansei shita.","german":"I realized my mistake and reflected on it deeply.","furigana":"じぶん|あやま|さと|ふか|はんせい"},{"japanese":"彼の表情を見て、もう手遅れだと悟った。","romaji":"Kare no hyōjō o mite, mō teokure da to satotta.","german":"Seeing his expression, I realized it was already too late.","furigana":"かれ|ひょうじょう|み|ておく|さと"},{"japanese":"緊張していることを相手に悟られないよう、笑顔を作った。","romaji":"Kinchō shite iru koto o aite ni satorarenai yō, egao o tsukutta.","german":"I put on a smile so that the other person wouldn't sense that I was nervous.","furigana":"きんちょう|あいて|さと|えがお|つく"}],"notes":"悟る means to inwardly \"realize\" or \"grasp\" something unspoken or deeper: 自分の過ちを悟る, 〜と悟る, 死期を悟る. It sounds more literary than 気づく \"to notice\" or 分かる \"to understand\" and stresses a sudden, often painful insight. In the passive, 悟られる means \"to be seen through\": 相手に悟られないように \"so that the other person doesn't notice\". In Buddhism, 悟りを開く means \"to attain enlightenment\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"沈黙は同意と見なされることがある。","romaji":"Chinmoku wa dōi to minasareru koto ga aru.","german":"Silence may be regarded as consent.","furigana":"ちんもく|どうい|み"},{"japanese":"連絡なしに三回休んだ場合は、退会したものと見なします。","romaji":"Renraku nashi ni sankai yasunda baai wa, taikai shita mono to minashimasu.","german":"If you are absent three times without notice, you will be regarded as having left the club.","furigana":"れんらく|さんかいやす|ばあい|たいかい|み"},{"japanese":"彼はチームの中心選手と見なされている。","romaji":"Kare wa chīmu no chūshin senshu to minasarete iru.","german":"He is regarded as the team's key player.","furigana":"かれ|ちゅうしんせんしゅ|み"}],"notes":"見なす means \"to regard as, consider as\" and appears in the pattern AをBと見なす; the passive AはBと見なされる is very common. It expresses a judgment or a (often rule-based) determination, even when it does not fully match reality: 欠席と見なす \"to treat as absent\". It is often written entirely in kana (みなす). Compared with 考える or 思う, it is more formal and sounds like a rule or assessment.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"組織を再編して効率化を図った。","romaji":"Soshiki o saihen shite kōritsuka o hakatta.","german":"The organization was restructured to improve efficiency.","furigana":"そしき|さいへん|こうりつか|はか"},{"japanese":"来年度から、バスの路線が大幅に再編される予定だ。","romaji":"Rainendo kara, basu no rosen ga ōhaba ni saihen sareru yotei da.","german":"From next fiscal year, the bus routes are scheduled to be extensively reorganized.","furigana":"らいねんど|ろせん|おおはば|さいへん|よてい"},{"japanese":"少子化の影響で、市内の小学校を再編することになった。","romaji":"Shōshika no eikyō de, shinai no shōgakkō o saihen suru koto ni natta.","german":"Because of the declining birthrate, it was decided to reorganize the elementary schools in the city.","furigana":"しょうしか|えいきょう|しない|しょうがっこう|さいへん"}],"notes":"再編する means to \"reorganize\" or \"restructure\" organizations, structures or networks: 組織を再編する, 路線を再編する. It is a formal word from business, politics and administration and often appears as a noun in combinations such as 業界再編 \"industry restructuring\" or 再編成. Unlike 改革 \"reform\", it is about regrouping, merging or splitting parts. In the news, the passive 再編される is common.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"入国制限が緩和された。","romaji":"Nyūkoku seigen ga kanwa sareta.","german":"Entry restrictions were eased.","furigana":"にゅうこくせいげん|かんわ"},{"japanese":"この薬は痛みを緩和する効果がある。","romaji":"Kono kusuri wa itami o kanwa suru kōka ga aru.","german":"This medicine has the effect of alleviating pain.","furigana":"くすり|いた|かんわ|こうか"},{"japanese":"新しい道路ができて、駅前の渋滞がかなり緩和された。","romaji":"Atarashii dōro ga dekite, ekimae no jūtai ga kanari kanwa sareta.","german":"Thanks to the new road, the traffic jam in front of the station has eased considerably.","furigana":"あたら|どうろ|えきまえ|じゅうたい|かんわ"}],"notes":"緩和する means to \"ease\" or \"alleviate\" restrictions, tensions or burdens: 規制を緩和する, 渋滞を緩和する, 痛みを緩和する, 緊張を緩和する. It is used transitively (Xを緩和する); the passive 緩和される is especially common. The word is formal and typical of news, politics and medicine. For rules, the opposite is 強化する \"to tighten\"; 撤廃する goes further and abolishes them completely.","levelNote":"Derived from 緩和 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"工事のためこの道路は通行止めです。","romaji":"Kōji no tame kono dōro wa tsūkōdome desu.","german":"This road is closed to traffic because of construction.","furigana":"こうじ|どうろ|つうこうど"},{"japanese":"この先は一方通行なので、ここからは入れません。","romaji":"Kono saki wa ippō tsūkō na node, koko kara wa hairemasen.","german":"The road ahead is one-way, so you can't enter from here.","furigana":"さき|いっぽうつうこう|はい"},{"japanese":"日本では車は道路の左側を通行する。","romaji":"Nihon de wa kuruma wa dōro no hidarigawa o tsūkō suru.","german":"In Japan, cars drive on the left side of the road.","furigana":"にほん|くるま|どうろ|ひだりがわ|つうこう"}],"notes":"通行 denotes the \"passing\" of people or vehicles at a point, i.e. passage or transit. Common combinations: 通行止め \"road closure,\" 通行人 \"passerby,\" 一方通行 \"one-way street,\" 左側通行 \"left-hand traffic,\" 通行料 \"toll.\" As an intransitive する-verb, the route takes を: 左側を通行する. Compared with 交通 (traffic and transport in general, 交通事故), 通行 means concretely walking or driving through.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"歩行者は横断歩道を渡ってください。","romaji":"Hokōsha wa ōdan hodō o watatte kudasai.","german":"Pedestrians, please use the crosswalk.","furigana":"ほこうしゃ|おうだんほどう|わた"},{"japanese":"日曜日の銀座は歩行者天国になる。","romaji":"Nichiyōbi no Ginza wa hokōsha tengoku ni naru.","german":"On Sundays, Ginza becomes a pedestrian zone.","furigana":"にちようび|ぎんざ|ほこうしゃてんごく"},{"japanese":"交差点で車が歩行者をはねる事故が起きた。","romaji":"Kōsaten de kuruma ga hokōsha o haneru jiko ga okita.","german":"An accident occurred at the intersection in which a car hit a pedestrian.","furigana":"こうさてん|くるま|ほこうしゃ|じこ|お"}],"notes":"歩行者 is the formal expression for \"pedestrian\" and is typical of traffic signs, traffic rules and accident reports; in conversation people more often say 歩いている人. Important combinations: 歩行者天国 \"temporarily car-free pedestrian zone\", 歩行者優先 \"pedestrians have priority\", 歩行者用信号 \"pedestrian signal\". Opposites are 運転者 or ドライバー and 自転車.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自動車産業は日本の経済を支えている。","romaji":"Jidōsha sangyō wa Nihon no keizai o sasaete iru.","german":"The automobile industry supports Japan's economy.","furigana":"じどうしゃさんぎょう|にほん|けいざい|ささ"},{"japanese":"雨の日は、自動車で駅まで行きます。","romaji":"Ame no hi wa, jidōsha de eki made ikimasu.","german":"On rainy days I go to the station by car.","furigana":"あめ|ひ|じどうしゃ|えき|い"}],"notes":"自動車 is the formal term for a car or motor vehicle. In conversation the shorter 車 is often used instead. In 自動車産業 the noun stands before 産業 and means the automobile industry. In 自動車で行く, で marks the means of transport used.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"乗用車の販売台数が増加している。","romaji":"Jōyōsha no hanbai daisū ga zōka shite iru.","german":"Passenger car sales are increasing.","furigana":"じょうようしゃ|はんばいだいすう|ぞうか"},{"japanese":"交差点で乗用車とバイクが衝突した。","romaji":"Kōsaten de jōyōsha to baiku ga shōtotsu shita.","german":"A passenger car and a motorcycle collided at the intersection.","furigana":"こうさてん|じょうようしゃ|しょうとつ"},{"japanese":"この駐車場は乗用車専用で、トラックは止められません。","romaji":"Kono chūshajō wa jōyōsha sen'yō de, torakku wa tomeraremasen.","german":"This parking lot is for passenger cars only; trucks cannot park here.","furigana":"ちゅうしゃじょう|じょうようしゃせんよう|と"}],"notes":"乗用車 is the technical term for \"passenger car\" as opposed to commercial vehicles such as トラック or バス. It is found in statistics, news and accident reports; in everyday speech people simply say 車 or 自動車. It is counted with 台. Combinations: 軽乗用車 \"kei car\", 普通乗用車 \"regular passenger car\", 乗用車専用 \"passenger cars only\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"貨物車が荷物を工場に運んだ。","romaji":"Kamotsusha ga nimotsu o kōjō ni hakonda.","german":"The truck carried the cargo to the factory.","furigana":"かもつしゃ|にもつ|こうじょう|はこ"},{"japanese":"この橋は重量制限があるため、大型の貨物車は通行できない。","romaji":"Kono hashi wa jūryō seigen ga aru tame, ōgata no kamotsusha wa tsūkō dekinai.","german":"Because of the weight limit, large trucks cannot cross this bridge.","furigana":"はし|じゅうりょうせいげん|おおがた|かもつしゃ|つうこう"},{"japanese":"機関車が長い貨物車の列を引いていた。","romaji":"Kikansha ga nagai kamotsusha no retsu o hiite ita.","german":"A locomotive was pulling a long line of freight cars.","furigana":"きかんしゃ|なが|かもつしゃ|れつ|ひ"}],"notes":"貨物車 is a factual, technical collective term for vehicles that carry goods (貨物). On the road it means trucks (formally 貨物自動車), for example in statistics as the opposite of 乗用車; on the railway it denotes a freight car, for which the shorter 貨車 is usually used. In everyday speech people almost always say トラック for a truck.","levelNote":"Compound: 貨物 (N2) + 車 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大型トラックが高速道路を走っている。","romaji":"Ōgata torakku ga kōsoku dōro o hashitte iru.","german":"A large truck is driving on the expressway.","furigana":"おおがた|こうそくどうろ|はし"},{"japanese":"引っ越しのために小さいトラックを借りた。","romaji":"Hikkoshi no tame ni chiisai torakku o karita.","german":"I rented a small truck for the move.","furigana":"ひ|こ|ちい|か"},{"japanese":"父は長距離トラックの運転手をしている。","romaji":"Chichi wa chōkyori torakku no untenshu o shite iru.","german":"My father is a long-distance truck driver.","furigana":"ちち|ちょうきょり|うんてんしゅ"}],"notes":"トラック is the normal everyday word for \"truck, lorry\"; the driver is called トラック運転手 or ドライバー. It is counted with 台; sizes: 軽トラック (short 軽トラ) \"small truck\", 大型トラック \"heavy truck\". Note: a homonym トラック means \"track\" in a stadium (陸上のトラック) or a \"track\" on a CD; context clarifies the meaning. Formal counterparts are 貨物車 or 貨物自動車.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 39"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"パトカーが犯人を追跡していた。","romaji":"Patokā ga hannin o tsuiseki shite ita.","german":"The police car was chasing the criminal.","furigana":"はんにん|ついせき"},{"japanese":"サイレンを鳴らしたパトカーが何台も通り過ぎた。","romaji":"Sairen o narashita patokā ga nandai mo tōrisugita.","german":"Several police cars with sirens blaring passed by.","furigana":"な|なんだい|とお|す"},{"japanese":"夜になると、この辺りはパトカーが巡回している。","romaji":"Yoru ni naru to, kono atari wa patokā ga junkai shite iru.","german":"At night, police cars patrol this area.","furigana":"よる|あた|じゅんかい"}],"notes":"パトカー is the common short form of パトロールカー \"patrol car, police car\". Typical combinations: パトカーが出動する, パトカーに追われる, パトカーが巡回する; the siren is called サイレン. It is counted with 台. Related vehicles are 救急車 \"ambulance\" and 消防車 \"fire engine\"; the police motorcycle is called 白バイ.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 47"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"特急列車で大阪まで二時間半かかる。","romaji":"Tokkyū ressha de Ōsaka made nijikan han kakaru.","german":"It takes two and a half hours to Osaka by limited express train.","furigana":"とっきゅうれっしゃ|おおさか|にじかんはん"},{"japanese":"大雪の影響で、多くの列車が運休となった。","romaji":"Ōyuki no eikyō de, ōku no ressha ga unkyū to natta.","german":"Many trains were cancelled because of the heavy snow.","furigana":"おおゆき|えいきょう|おお|れっしゃ|うんきゅう"},{"japanese":"夜行列車に乗って、朝に北海道に着いた。","romaji":"Yakō ressha ni notte, asa ni Hokkaidō ni tsuita.","german":"I took an overnight train and arrived in Hokkaido in the morning.","furigana":"やこうれっしゃ|の|あさ|ほっかいどう|つ"}],"notes":"列車 is the more formal, railway-technical term for \"train\" as a scheduled train service, typical in announcements, timetables and the news: 普通列車, 特急列車, 夜行列車, 列車が遅れる. In everyday speech people mostly say 電車, especially for local transport; 汽車 is outdated. A single car is 車両. Trains are counted with 本.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新幹線で東京から京都まで約二時間だ。","romaji":"Shinkansen de Tōkyō kara Kyōto made yaku nijikan da.","german":"By Shinkansen, it takes about two hours from Tokyo to Kyoto.","furigana":"しんかんせん|とうきょう|きょうと|やくにじかん"},{"japanese":"新幹線の指定席を予約しておいた。","romaji":"Shinkansen no shiteiseki o yoyaku shite oita.","german":"I reserved a reserved seat on the Shinkansen in advance.","furigana":"しんかんせん|していせき|よやく"},{"japanese":"九州まで新幹線が通って、とても便利になった。","romaji":"Kyūshū made shinkansen ga tōtte, totemo benri ni natta.","german":"Now that the Shinkansen runs all the way to Kyushu, it has become very convenient.","furigana":"きゅうしゅう|しんかんせん|とお|べんり"}],"notes":"新幹線 is Japan's high-speed railway; the word denotes both the line (東海道新幹線) and the individual train. You travel with で (新幹線で行く) and board with に (新幹線に乗る). The train types have names such as のぞみ, ひかり or こだま. For tickets, a distinction is made between 指定席 \"reserved seat\" and 自由席 \"non-reserved seat\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 5 (source: N5; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"空港までモノレールで行ける。","romaji":"Kūkō made monorēru de ikeru.","german":"You can get to the airport by monorail.","furigana":"くうこう|い"},{"japanese":"モノレールの窓から海がよく見える。","romaji":"Monorēru no mado kara umi ga yoku mieru.","german":"You can see the sea well from the monorail window.","furigana":"まど|うみ|み"},{"japanese":"市は渋滞対策としてモノレールの建設を計画している。","romaji":"Shi wa jūtai taisaku to shite monorēru no kensetsu o keikaku shite iru.","german":"The city is planning to build a monorail as a measure against traffic congestion.","furigana":"し|じゅうたいたいさく|けんせつ|けいかく"}],"notes":"モノレール is a \"monorail\"; well known in Japan are, for example, the 東京モノレール to Haneda Airport and the line in Naha on Okinawa. Grammatically it is treated like 電車: モノレールで行く, モノレールに乗る. In urban planning it is considered a space-saving means of transport because it runs above the streets.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"救助のためにヘリコプターが出動した。","romaji":"Kyūjo no tame ni herikoputā ga shutsudō shita.","german":"A helicopter was dispatched for the rescue.","furigana":"きゅうじょ|しゅつどう"},{"japanese":"上空をヘリコプターが何機も飛んでいる。","romaji":"Jōkū o herikoputā ga nanki mo tonde iru.","german":"Several helicopters are flying overhead.","furigana":"じょうくう|なんき|と"},{"japanese":"旅行中にヘリコプターに乗って、上から夜景を楽しんだ。","romaji":"Ryokōchū ni herikoputā ni notte, ue kara yakei o tanoshinda.","german":"On the trip, I rode in a helicopter and enjoyed the night view from above.","furigana":"りょこうちゅう|の|うえ|やけい|たの"}],"notes":"ヘリコプター means \"helicopter\"; colloquially often shortened to ヘリ, e.g. ドクターヘリ \"rescue helicopter\". Typical combinations: ヘリコプターが出動する/飛ぶ/着陸する, ヘリコプターで運ぶ. It is counted formally with 機, in everyday speech also with 台. The landing site is called ヘリポート.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大きな船が港に入ってきた。","romaji":"Ōkina fune ga minato ni haitte kita.","german":"A large ship came into the harbor.","furigana":"おお|ふね|みなと|はい"},{"japanese":"島までは船で一時間かかります。","romaji":"Shima made wa fune de ichijikan kakarimasu.","german":"It takes an hour to get to the island by boat.","furigana":"しま|ふね|いちじかん"},{"japanese":"船に乗ると、すぐ気分が悪くなります。","romaji":"Fune ni noru to, sugu kibun ga waruku narimasu.","german":"As soon as I get on a boat, I feel sick.","furigana":"ふね|の|きぶん|わる"}],"notes":"船 is the general word for ship and boat: 船に乗る, 船で行く, 船が港に着く; 船酔い is \"seasickness\". Small rowing boats are often called ボート, and 舟 (same reading) is written for small, simple boats. In compounds 船 is read せん: 客船 \"passenger ship\", 漁船 \"fishing boat\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"フェリーで島に渡った。","romaji":"Ferī de shima ni watatta.","german":"We crossed to the island by ferry.","furigana":"しま|わた"},{"japanese":"車ごとフェリーに乗って北海道へ行った。","romaji":"Kuruma goto ferī ni notte Hokkaidō e itta.","german":"We took the ferry with our car to Hokkaido.","furigana":"くるま|の|ほっかいどう|い"},{"japanese":"台風のため、今日のフェリーは全便欠航です。","romaji":"Taifū no tame, kyō no ferī wa zenbin kekkō desu.","german":"Because of the typhoon, all ferry services are canceled today.","furigana":"たいふう|きょう|ぜんびんけっこう"}],"notes":"フェリー is a \"ferry\" that carries people and often also cars; in Japan it connects islands with the main islands, sometimes overnight. Typical: フェリーで渡る, フェリーに乗る, フェリー乗り場 \"ferry terminal\", カーフェリー \"car ferry\". More general is 船 \"ship\"; a small boat is ボート.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この航路は毎日運航されている。","romaji":"Kono kōro wa mainichi unkō sarete iru.","german":"This route runs every day.","furigana":"こうろ|まいにちうんこう"},{"japanese":"嵐を避けるため、船は航路を変更した。","romaji":"Arashi o sakeru tame, fune wa kōro o henkō shita.","german":"To avoid the storm, the ship changed its course.","furigana":"あらし|さ|ふね|こうろ|へんこう"},{"japanese":"利用者が減り、その島への航路は廃止された。","romaji":"Riyōsha ga heri, sono shima e no kōro wa haishi sareta.","german":"Ridership fell, and the ferry route to that island was discontinued.","furigana":"りようしゃ|へ|しま|こうろ|はいし"}],"notes":"航路 is the fixed route of ships or aircraft: 定期航路 \"regular service\", 国際航路, 航路を変更する. It is used mainly for shipping; for flight routes, 空路 or 路線 is more common. The matching verb is 運航する \"(of ships/flights) to operate\". The word is formal and typical of news and travel information.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"空路で荷物を送るのは高い。","romaji":"Kūro de nimotsu o okuru no wa takai.","german":"Sending goods by air is expensive.","furigana":"くうろ|にもつ|おく|たか"},{"japanese":"首相は今朝、空路ワシントンへ向かった。","romaji":"Shushō wa kesa, kūro Washinton e mukatta.","german":"The prime minister left for Washington by air this morning.","furigana":"しゅしょう|けさ|くうろ|む"},{"japanese":"道路が通れなくなったため、救援物資は空路で届けられた。","romaji":"Dōro ga tōrenaku natta tame, kyūen busshi wa kūro de todokerareta.","german":"Because the roads were impassable, relief supplies were delivered by air.","furigana":"どうろ|とお|きゅうえんぶっし|くうろ|とど"}],"notes":"空路 means \"air route\" or flight path and is often used adverbially in the sense of \"by air, by plane\": 空路で帰国する; in the news also without で: 空路パリへ向かう. It is formal; in everyday speech people say 飛行機で. Counterparts are 陸路 \"land route\" and 海路 \"sea route\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"陸路で国境を越えた。","romaji":"Rikuro de kokkyō o koeta.","german":"We crossed the border overland.","furigana":"りくろ|こっきょう|こ"},{"japanese":"飛行機が欠航したので、陸路で帰ることにした。","romaji":"Hikōki ga kekkō shita node, rikuro de kaeru koto ni shita.","german":"The flight was canceled, so I decided to go back overland.","furigana":"ひこうき|けっこう|りくろ|かえ"},{"japanese":"昔の旅人は陸路で何日もかけて江戸へ向かった。","romaji":"Mukashi no tabibito wa rikuro de nannichi mo kakete Edo e mukatta.","german":"Travelers in the past took many days to reach Edo overland.","furigana":"むかし|たびびと|りくろ|なんにち|えど|む"}],"notes":"陸路 means \"land route\", that is, travel or transport over land (road, rail) as opposed to 海路 and 空路. It usually stands adverbially with で: 陸路で移動する, 陸路で国境を越える. The word is formal and typical of the news, history and travel reports; in everyday speech people say 車で or 電車で more often.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"海路で物資を輸送した。","romaji":"Kairo de busshi o yusō shita.","german":"Supplies were transported by sea.","furigana":"かいろ|ぶっし|ゆそう"},{"japanese":"島には橋がないので、海路でしか行けない。","romaji":"Shima ni wa hashi ga nai node, kairo de shika ikenai.","german":"There is no bridge to the island, so it can only be reached by sea.","furigana":"しま|はし|かいろ|い"},{"japanese":"「待てば海路の日和あり」と言うから、焦らずにチャンスを待とう。","romaji":"\"Mateba kairo no hiyori ari\" to iu kara, aserazu ni chansu o matō.","german":"As the saying goes, \"good weather comes to those who wait at sea\" - let's wait calmly for our chance.","furigana":"ま|かいろ||い|あせ|ま"}],"notes":"海路 means \"sea route\", i.e. travel or transport by ship: 海路で運ぶ, 海路を行く. It is formal and rather literary; in everyday speech one says 船で. For a concrete shipping line, 航路 is more common. Well known is the proverb 待てば海路の日和あり \"if you wait, there will be fair weather for sailing\", i.e. \"patience pays off\". Counterparts: 陸路, 空路.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"高速道路が渋滞していたので一般道を走った。","romaji":"Kōsoku dōro ga jūtai shite ita node ippandō o hashitta.","german":"The expressway was congested, so we took the ordinary roads.","furigana":"こうそくどうろ|じゅうたい|いっぱんどう|はし"},{"japanese":"高速を降りて、一般道で一時間ほど走った。","romaji":"Kōsoku o orite, ippandō de ichijikan hodo hashitta.","german":"We left the expressway and drove about an hour on regular roads.","furigana":"こうそく|お|いっぱんどう|いちじかん|はし"},{"japanese":"一般道を使えば料金はかからないが、時間は倍かかる。","romaji":"Ippandō o tsukaeba ryōkin wa kakaranai ga, jikan wa bai kakaru.","german":"If you use the regular roads there is no toll, but it takes twice as long.","furigana":"いっぱんどう|つか|りょうきん|じかん|ばい"}],"notes":"一般道 (short for 一般道路) refers to any normal, toll-free road as opposed to the 高速道路 \"expressway\". So it is not only the \"country road\" outside towns but also includes city streets. Typical: 一般道を走る, 一般道で行く, 高速を降りて一般道に入る. The word often appears in car navigation systems, traffic reports and route planning.","levelNote":"Compound: 一般 (N3) + 道 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"国道一号線は東京から大阪まで続いている。","romaji":"Kokudō ichigōsen wa Tōkyō kara Ōsaka made tsuzuite iru.","german":"Die Nationalstraße Nr. 1 führt von Tokio bis Osaka.","furigana":"こくどういちごうせん|とうきょう|おおさか|つづ"},{"japanese":"国道沿いには大きな店やファミレスが並んでいる。","romaji":"Kokudō zoi ni wa ōkina mise ya famiresu ga narande iru.","german":"Entlang der Nationalstraße reihen sich große Geschäfte und Familienrestaurants.","furigana":"こくどうぞ|おお|みせ|なら"},{"japanese":"土砂崩れで国道が通行止めになっている。","romaji":"Dosha kuzure de kokudō ga tsūkōdome ni natte iru.","german":"Wegen eines Erdrutsches ist die Nationalstraße gesperrt.","furigana":"どしゃくず|こくどう|つうこうど"}],"notes":"国道 ist eine vom Staat verwaltete „Nationalstraße“, vergleichbar mit einer deutschen Bundesstraße. Sie wird mit Nummer und 号線 genannt (国道1号線, oft kurz 1号). Anders als das 高速道路 ist sie mautfrei. Die Rangfolge lautet 国道 → 県道 (Präfektur) → 市道 (Stadt). Häufig ist 国道沿い „entlang der Nationalstraße“, etwa für Geschäfte und Restaurants.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"県道を通って目的地に向かった。","romaji":"Kendō o tōtte mokutekichi ni mukatta.","german":"Wir fuhren über die Präfekturstraße zum Ziel.","furigana":"けんどう|とお|もくてきち|む"},{"japanese":"この先の交差点を左に曲がると、県道に出ます。","romaji":"Kono saki no kōsaten o hidari ni magaru to, kendō ni demasu.","german":"Wenn Sie an der nächsten Kreuzung links abbiegen, kommen Sie auf die Präfekturstraße.","furigana":"さき|こうさてん|ひだり|ま|けんどう|で"},{"japanese":"県道の工事が終わって、道がずいぶん広くなった。","romaji":"Kendō no kōji ga owatte, michi ga zuibun hiroku natta.","german":"Die Bauarbeiten an der Präfekturstraße sind fertig, und die Straße ist jetzt viel breiter.","furigana":"けんどう|こうじ|お|みち|ひろ"}],"notes":"県道 ist eine von der Präfektur (県) verwaltete Straße, eine Stufe unter dem 国道 – etwa vergleichbar mit einer deutschen Landesstraße. In Tokio heißt sie 都道, in Hokkaido 道道, in Osaka und Kyoto 府道. Auf Schildern steht sie mit Nummer, z. B. 県道15号. Das Wort kommt vor allem in Wegbeschreibungen und Verkehrsmeldungen vor.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ガソリンスタンドで給油した。","romaji":"Gasorin sutando de kyūyu shita.","german":"I filled up at the gas station.","furigana":"きゅうゆ"},{"japanese":"ガソリンが少ないから、ガソリンスタンドに寄ろう。","romaji":"Gasorin ga sukunai kara, gasorin sutando ni yorō.","german":"We're low on gas, so let's stop at a gas station.","furigana":"すく|よ"},{"japanese":"この近くにガソリンスタンドはありますか。","romaji":"Kono chikaku ni gasorin sutando wa arimasu ka.","german":"Is there a gas station near here?","furigana":"ちか"}],"notes":"ガソリンスタンド is a Japanese-coined word (wasei eigo) for \"gas station\"; for short one also says スタンド. To fill up is ガソリンを入れる in everyday speech, 給油する in more formal speech. At self-service stations the sign says セルフ; at others the staff fill up for you. You stop by with に寄る: ガソリンスタンドに寄る.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"高速道路の料金所で通行料を払った。","romaji":"Kōsoku dōro no ryōkinjo de tsūkōryō o haratta.","german":"I paid the toll at the expressway toll gate.","furigana":"こうそくどうろ|りょうきんじょ|つうこうりょう|はら"},{"japanese":"料金所の手前で渋滞が五キロ続いている。","romaji":"Ryōkinjo no temae de jūtai ga go kiro tsuzuite iru.","german":"Congestion stretches for five kilometers before the toll gate.","furigana":"りょうきんじょ|てまえ|じゅうたい|ご|つづ"},{"japanese":"ETCがあれば、料金所で止まらずに通過できる。","romaji":"ETC ga areba, ryōkinjo de tomarazu ni tsūka dekiru.","german":"With ETC, you can pass through the toll gate without stopping.","furigana":"りょうきんじょ|と|つうか"}],"notes":"料金所 is the \"toll gate\" on expressways and toll roads, where you pay the 通行料. Today people mostly drive through with the electronic ETC system; there are separate ETCレーン next to the 一般レーン. Traffic jams often form before the 料金所 (料金所付近で渋滞). Not to be confused with 料金 alone, \"fee, price\".","levelNote":"Compound: 料金 (N3) + 所 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"サービスエリアで休憩して食事をした。","romaji":"Sābisu eria de kyūkei shite shokuji o shita.","german":"We took a break and ate at the service area.","furigana":"きゅうけい|しょくじ"},{"japanese":"次のサービスエリアでトイレに寄ろう。","romaji":"Tsugi no sābisu eria de toire ni yorō.","german":"Let's stop at the next service area for the restroom.","furigana":"つぎ|よ"},{"japanese":"このサービスエリアは地元の名物が買えるので人気がある。","romaji":"Kono sābisu eria wa jimoto no meibutsu ga kaeru node ninki ga aru.","german":"This service area is popular because you can buy local specialties there.","furigana":"じもと|めいぶつ|か|にんき"}],"notes":"サービスエリア (abbreviated SA) is a large \"rest stop\" on Japanese expressways with restaurants, shops, restrooms and often a gas station. Smaller rest areas are called パーキングエリア (PA). Many service areas sell regional specialties and are themselves a popular excursion destination. Typical: サービスエリアで休憩する, サービスエリアに寄る.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自然との調和を大切にする生活を送りたい。","romaji":"Shizen to no chōwa o taisetsu ni suru seikatsu o okuritai.","german":"I want to lead a life that values harmony with nature.","furigana":"しぜん|ちょうわ|たいせつ|せいかつ|おく"},{"japanese":"この建物は周りの景色とよく調和している。","romaji":"Kono tatemono wa mawari no keshiki to yoku chōwa shite iru.","german":"This building blends well with the surrounding scenery.","furigana":"たてもの|まわ|けしき|ちょうわ"},{"japanese":"調和のとれた食事を心がけています。","romaji":"Chōwa no toreta shokuji o kokorogakete imasu.","german":"I try to eat a balanced diet.","furigana":"ちょうわ|しょくじ|こころ"}],"notes":"調和 means \"harmony\" between different elements: 自然との調和, 色の調和. As an intransitive する-verb, AがBと調和する means \"to harmonize with B, blend in\". Very common is 調和のとれた \"balanced, harmonious\" (調和のとれた食事). The word is rather abstract and elevated; for concord within a group one rather says 和 or 協調.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"社会の秩序を守ることは重要だ。","romaji":"Shakai no chitsujo o mamoru koto wa jūyō da.","german":"It is important to maintain social order.","furigana":"しゃかい|ちつじょ|まも|じゅうよう"},{"japanese":"災害の後も、人々は秩序正しく列に並んでいた。","romaji":"Saigai no ato mo, hitobito wa chitsujo tadashiku retsu ni narande ita.","german":"Even after the disaster, people queued up in an orderly way.","furigana":"さいがい|あと|ひとびと|ちつじょただ|れつ|なら"},{"japanese":"デモが暴力的になり、町の秩序が乱れた。","romaji":"Demo ga bōryokuteki ni nari, machi no chitsujo ga midareta.","german":"The demonstration turned violent, and order in the town was disrupted.","furigana":"ぼうりょくてき|まち|ちつじょ|みだ"}],"notes":"秩序 is the \"order\" of a system or society: 社会の秩序, 秩序を守る/保つ/乱す, 秩序が回復する. It is formal and common in politics and news; 治安, by contrast, refers specifically to public safety. Adverbially, 秩序正しく means \"in an orderly, disciplined way\". For tidiness in a room, one does not say 秩序 but 片付いている or 整理されている.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"政変後、国は混沌とした状態に陥った。","romaji":"Seihengo, kuni wa konton to shita jōtai ni ochiitta.","german":"After the coup, the country fell into a state of chaos.","furigana":"せいへんご|くに|こんとん|じょうたい|おちい"},{"japanese":"会議は意見がまとまらず、混沌としていた。","romaji":"Kaigi wa iken ga matomarazu, konton to shite ita.","german":"The meeting was chaotic because opinions did not come together.","furigana":"かいぎ|いけん|こんとん"},{"japanese":"今後の景気は混沌としていて、予測が難しい。","romaji":"Kongo no keiki wa konton to shite ite, yosoku ga muzukashii.","german":"The future economy is murky and hard to predict.","furigana":"こんご|けいき|こんとん|よそく|むずか"}],"notes":"混沌 is a literary word for \"chaos, complete disorder\" and is usually used as a たる-adjective: 混沌とした状況, 混沌としている. It also describes uncertainty: 先行きが混沌としている \"the future is completely open\". 混乱 stresses confusion caused by a specific event (事故で交通が混乱する); in everyday speech people often say カオス or ごちゃごちゃ. As a noun: 混沌の時代.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"全員の意見が一致した。","romaji":"Zen'in no iken ga icchi shita.","german":"Everyone's opinions coincided.","furigana":"ぜんいん|いけん|いっち"},{"japanese":"現場の指紋が容疑者のものと一致した。","romaji":"Genba no shimon ga yōgisha no mono to icchi shita.","german":"The fingerprints at the scene matched the suspect's.","furigana":"げんば|しもん|ようぎしゃ|いっち"},{"japanese":"同じ日に生まれたなんて、すごい偶然の一致だね。","romaji":"Onaji hi ni umareta nante, sugoi gūzen no icchi da ne.","german":"Being born on the same day - what an amazing coincidence.","furigana":"おな|ひ|う|ぐうぜん|いっち"}],"notes":"一致 means \"agreement, match\"; as an intransitive する-verb: 意見が一致する, AがBと一致する, 指紋が一致する. Important phrases are 全員一致で \"unanimously,\" 偶然の一致 \"coincidence\" and 言行一致 \"words and deeds agree.\" The opposite is 不一致 or the verb 食い違う. The word is neutral and ranges from everyday speech to the news.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"両者の見解に相違がある。","romaji":"Ryōsha no kenkai ni sōi ga aru.","german":"There is a difference in the views of the two parties.","furigana":"りょうしゃ|けんかい|そうい"},{"japanese":"申込書の内容に相違がないか、もう一度確認してください。","romaji":"Mōshikomisho no naiyō ni sōi ga nai ka, mō ichido kakunin shite kudasai.","german":"Please check once more that there are no discrepancies in the contents of the application form.","furigana":"もうしこみしょ|ないよう|そうい|いちどかくにん"},{"japanese":"彼の証言は事実と相違している。","romaji":"Kare no shōgen wa jijitsu to sōi shite iru.","german":"His testimony differs from the facts.","furigana":"かれ|しょうげん|じじつ|そうい"}],"notes":"相違 means \"difference, discrepancy\" between opinions, statements or facts and is formal: 意見の相違, 見解の相違, 事実と相違する. Set phrases are 〜に相違ない \"without doubt\" (the written variant of 違いない) and, on forms, 上記の通り相違ありません \"The above information is correct\". In everyday speech people say 違い; 差異 stresses measurable differences.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"文化的な差異を理解することが大切だ。","romaji":"Bunkateki na sai o rikai suru koto ga taisetsu da.","german":"It is important to understand cultural differences.","furigana":"ぶんかてき|さい|りかい|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"二つのグループの結果に大きな差異は見られなかった。","romaji":"Futatsu no gurūpu no kekka ni ōkina sai wa mirarenakatta.","german":"No significant difference was seen between the results of the two groups.","furigana":"ふた|けっか|おお|さい|み"},{"japanese":"地域によって方言にはさまざまな差異がある。","romaji":"Chiiki ni yotte hōgen ni wa samazama na sai ga aru.","german":"Depending on the region, there are various differences in dialects.","furigana":"ちいき|ほうげん|さい"}],"notes":"差異 is a literary word for \"difference, discrepancy\", especially for objectively ascertainable, often measurable deviations: 〜の間に差異がある, 大きな差異は見られない, 文化的な差異. In everyday speech one says 違い or 差. 相違 stresses rather that statements or opinions do not agree; for social inequality 格差 is more usual.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この二つの事件には類似点が多い。","romaji":"Kono futatsu no jiken ni wa ruijiten ga ōi.","german":"These two cases have many similarities.","furigana":"ふた|じけん|るいじてん|おお"},{"japanese":"類似品にご注意ください。","romaji":"Ruijihin ni go-chūi kudasai.","german":"Please beware of imitation products.","furigana":"るいじひん|ちゅうい"},{"japanese":"他社の商品に類似したデザインが問題になった。","romaji":"Tasha no shōhin ni ruiji shita dezain ga mondai ni natta.","german":"A design resembling a competitor's product became a problem.","furigana":"たしゃ|しょうひん|るいじ|もんだい"}],"notes":"類似 means \"similarity\"; as a する-verb, AがBに類似する, attributively often 類似した or 類似の. Common compounds are 類似点 \"similarity, common point\" and 類似品 \"similar product, imitation\". The word is formal and typical of reports, law and product notices (類似品にご注意ください); in everyday speech one says 似ている.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この二つの事件に関連があるか調べている。","romaji":"Kono futatsu no jiken ni kanren ga aru ka shirabete iru.","german":"We are investigating whether these two incidents are related.","furigana":"ふた|じけん|かんれん|しら"},{"japanese":"環境問題に関連する本を何冊か読んだ。","romaji":"Kankyō mondai ni kanren suru hon o nansatsu ka yonda.","german":"I read several books related to environmental issues.","furigana":"かんきょうもんだい|かんれん|ほん|なんさつ|よ"},{"japanese":"この記事の下に関連記事が表示されます。","romaji":"Kono kiji no shita ni kanren kiji ga hyōji saremasu.","german":"Related articles are displayed below this article.","furigana":"きじ|した|かんれんきじ|ひょうじ"}],"notes":"関連 denotes a factual \"connection, relation\": AとBの関連, Xに関連がある. As a する-verb, Xに関連する means \"to be related to X,\" often attributively: 関連する資料. Very common as a first element: 関連会社 \"affiliated company,\" 関連記事 \"related articles,\" 関連商品. The nearly synonymous 関係 also covers human relationships; 関連 is more factual.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"喫煙と肺がんの因果関係が証明された。","romaji":"Kitsuen to haigan no inga kankei ga shōmei sareta.","german":"The causal relationship between smoking and lung cancer has been proven.","furigana":"きつえん|はい|いんがかんけい|しょうめい"},{"japanese":"事故と道路の設計に因果関係があるかが裁判で争われた。","romaji":"Jiko to dōro no sekkei ni inga kankei ga aru ka ga saiban de arasowareta.","german":"Whether there was a causal link between the accident and the road's design was disputed in court.","furigana":"じこ|どうろ|せっけい|いんがかんけい|さいばん|あらそ"},{"japanese":"人をだましてばかりいた彼が最後にだまされたのは、因果応報だ。","romaji":"Hito o damashite bakari ita kare ga saigo ni damasareta no wa, inga ōhō da.","german":"That he, who had done nothing but deceive people, was finally deceived himself is karmic retribution.","furigana":"ひと|かれ|さいご|いんがおうほう"}],"notes":"因果 means \"cause and effect\" and today occurs mainly in 因果関係 \"causal relationship\", for example in science and law. From Buddhism comes the meaning \"karma, fate\", known from 因果応報 \"every deed meets its reward\". As an adjective, 因果な \"unfortunate\" is old-fashioned and literary. 因果 on its own is rare; for simple reasons people say 原因 and 結果.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"気温と電力消費量の相関を調べた。","romaji":"Kion to denryoku shōhiryō no sōkan o shirabeta.","german":"I investigated the correlation between temperature and electricity consumption.","furigana":"きおん|でんりょくしょうひりょう|そうかん|しら"},{"japanese":"睡眠時間と成績には相関があることが分かった。","romaji":"Suimin jikan to seiseki ni wa sōkan ga aru koto ga wakatta.","german":"It turned out that there is a correlation between sleep time and grades.","furigana":"すいみんじかん|せいせき|そうかん|わ"},{"japanese":"相関関係があっても、因果関係があるとは限らない。","romaji":"Sōkan kankei ga atte mo, inga kankei ga aru to wa kagiranai.","german":"Even if there is a correlation, it does not necessarily mean there is a causal relationship.","furigana":"そうかんかんけい|いんがかんけい|かぎ"}],"notes":"相関 means \"correlation, interrelation\" and is a technical word from statistics and science: AとBの相関, 相関がある/ない, 相関関係, 正の相関/負の相関. As a する-verb (AとBが相関する) it is rarer. Important: 相関関係 is not the same as 因果関係 \"causal relationship\", a frequent reminder in technical texts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"高齢者の社会的な孤立が問題になっている。","romaji":"Kōreisha no shakaiteki na koritsu ga mondai ni natte iru.","german":"The social isolation of elderly people is becoming a problem.","furigana":"こうれいしゃ|しゃかいてき|こりつ|もんだい"},{"japanese":"大雪で道路が使えなくなり、山の村が孤立した。","romaji":"Ōyuki de dōro ga tsukaenaku nari, yama no mura ga koritsu shita.","german":"Heavy snow made the roads unusable, and the mountain village was cut off.","furigana":"おおゆき|どうろ|つか|やま|むら|こりつ"},{"japanese":"転校したばかりで、クラスで孤立しないか心配だ。","romaji":"Tenkō shita bakari de, kurasu de koritsu shinai ka shinpai da.","german":"I've just changed schools and I'm worried about being isolated in the class.","furigana":"てんこう|こりつ|しんぱい"}],"notes":"孤立 means \"isolation\", i.e. being cut off from others - socially, politically or spatially. As an intransitive する-verb: クラスで孤立する, 国際的に孤立する, 大雪で村が孤立する. Common are 社会的孤立 and 孤立感 \"feeling of isolation\". Distinction: 孤独 is the inner feeling of loneliness, 孤立 the objective situation of having no connection to others.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"被災地への連帯を示すために寄付をした。","romaji":"Hisaichi e no rentai o shimesu tame ni kifu o shita.","german":"I made a donation to show solidarity with the disaster area.","furigana":"ひさいち|れんたい|しめ|きふ"},{"japanese":"一人のミスでもチーム全体の連帯責任になる。","romaji":"Hitori no misu demo chīmu zentai no rentai sekinin ni naru.","german":"Even one person's mistake becomes joint responsibility of the whole team.","furigana":"ひとり|ぜんたい|れんたいせきにん"},{"japanese":"部屋を借りるときに、連帯保証人が必要だと言われた。","romaji":"Heya o kariru toki ni, rentai hoshōnin ga hitsuyō da to iwareta.","german":"When I rented an apartment, I was told that I needed a joint guarantor.","furigana":"へや|か|れんたいほしょうにん|ひつよう|い"}],"notes":"連帯 means \"solidarity\", a group standing together: 連帯を示す, 連帯感 \"sense of solidarity\", 〜と連帯する. Important in law and everyday life are 連帯責任 \"joint liability, collective responsibility\" and 連帯保証人 \"joint guarantor\", who is required for rental contracts, for example. The word is formal; 団結 stresses banding together, 協力 practical cooperation.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"国際的な協調が不可欠だ。","romaji":"Kokusaiteki na kyōchō ga fukaketsu da.","german":"International cooperation is indispensable.","furigana":"こくさいてき|きょうちょう|ふかけつ"},{"japanese":"彼は能力は高いが、協調性に欠ける。","romaji":"Kare wa nōryoku wa takai ga, kyōchōsei ni kakeru.","german":"He is very capable but lacks cooperativeness.","furigana":"かれ|のうりょく|たか|きょうちょうせい|か"},{"japanese":"各国が協調して金利を引き下げた。","romaji":"Kakkoku ga kyōchō shite kinri o hikisageta.","german":"The countries acted in concert and lowered interest rates.","furigana":"かっこく|きょうちょう|きんり|ひ|さ"}],"notes":"協調 means \"cooperation, coordination\" in the sense of mutual consideration in order to avoid conflict: 国際協調, 労使協調, 他国と協調する. Important in working life is 協調性 \"teamwork ability\" (協調性がある/に欠ける). Compared with 協力 (concrete helping out), 協調 stresses the harmonious coordination of interests. Not to be confused with the homophone 強調 \"emphasis\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"多文化共生の社会を築いていきたい。","romaji":"Tabunka kyōsei no shakai o kizuite ikitai.","german":"We want to build a society of multicultural coexistence.","furigana":"たぶんかきょうせい|しゃかい|きず"},{"japanese":"クマノミとイソギンチャクは共生している。","romaji":"Kumanomi to isoginchaku wa kyōsei shite iru.","german":"Clownfish and sea anemones live in symbiosis.","furigana":"きょうせい"},{"japanese":"人間と野生動物が共生できる町づくりを考えよう。","romaji":"Ningen to yasei dōbutsu ga kyōsei dekiru machizukuri o kangaeyō.","german":"Let's think about town planning in which humans and wild animals can live together.","furigana":"にんげん|やせいどうぶつ|きょうせい|まち|かんが"}],"notes":"共生 means \"living together, coexistence\" and, in biology, \"symbiosis\". Typical are 自然との共生, 多文化共生 \"multicultural coexistence\" and AとBが共生する. The word is formal and common in discussions of the environment, integration and politics. 共存 \"coexistence\" stresses more the mere existing side by side, 共生 the mutual living together. Not to be confused with the homophone 強制 \"compulsion\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"犯人の心理を分析する。","romaji":"Hannin no shinri o bunseki suru.","german":"I analyze the culprit's state of mind.","furigana":"はんにん|しんり|ぶんせき"},{"japanese":"安くなると買いたくなるのが消費者の心理だ。","romaji":"Yasuku naru to kaitaku naru no ga shōhisha no shinri da.","german":"It's consumer psychology that people want to buy when prices drop.","furigana":"やす|か|しょうひしゃ|しんり"},{"japanese":"試験の前は心理的なプレッシャーが大きい。","romaji":"Shiken no mae wa shinriteki na puresshā ga ōkii.","german":"Before exams, the psychological pressure is great.","furigana":"しけん|まえ|しんりてき|おお"}],"notes":"心理 denotes the \"mental state, way of thinking\" of a person or group: 犯人の心理, 消費者心理, 群集心理 \"mass psychology.\" Very common are 心理的な \"psychological\" (心理的な負担) and 心理状態 \"state of mind.\" The science is 心理学; 心理 alone mostly means the state of mind, not the discipline. 精神 refers more to spirit and mental health.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大学で心理学を専攻している。","romaji":"Daigaku de shinrigaku o senkō shite iru.","german":"I'm majoring in psychology at university.","furigana":"だいがく|しんりがく|せんこう"},{"japanese":"心理学者によると、人は第一印象を数秒で決めるそうだ。","romaji":"Shinrigakusha ni yoru to, hito wa daiichi inshō o sūbyō de kimeru sō da.","german":"According to psychologists, people form a first impression within a few seconds.","furigana":"しんりがくしゃ|ひと|だいいちいんしょう|すうびょう|き"},{"japanese":"心理学の知識はマーケティングにも役立つ。","romaji":"Shinrigaku no chishiki wa māketingu ni mo yakudatsu.","german":"Knowledge of psychology is also useful in marketing.","furigana":"しんりがく|ちしき|やくだ"}],"notes":"心理学 is the science of \"psychology\": 心理学を学ぶ/専攻する, 心理学者 \"psychologist\". Subfields are called, for example, 社会心理学, 発達心理学 or 臨床心理学. To be distinguished are 心理 (a person's psyche, state of mind) and 精神科 (psychiatry as a medical specialty). The word is neutral to academic.","levelNote":"Compound: 心理 (N3) + 学 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"精神科の医師に相談した。","romaji":"Seishinka no ishi ni sōdan shita.","german":"I consulted a psychiatrist.","furigana":"せいしんか|いし|そうだん"},{"japanese":"眠れない日が続いたので、精神科を受診した。","romaji":"Nemurenai hi ga tsuzuita node, seishinka o jushin shita.","german":"I hadn't been able to sleep for days, so I saw a psychiatrist.","furigana":"ねむ|ひ|つづ|せいしんか|じゅしん"},{"japanese":"姉は大学病院の精神科で看護師として働いている。","romaji":"Ane wa daigaku byōin no seishinka de kangoshi to shite hataraite iru.","german":"My older sister works as a nurse in the psychiatry department of a university hospital.","furigana":"あね|だいがくびょういん|せいしんか|かんごし|はたら"}],"notes":"精神科 is the medical specialty or hospital department of \"psychiatry\": 精神科に通う \"to be in psychiatric treatment\", 精神科医 \"psychiatrist\", 精神科を受診する. Because 精神科 sounds off-putting to some, many practices call themselves 心療内科 (psychosomatic medicine) or メンタルクリニック. To be distinguished from 心理学 (the science) and カウンセラー (a counselor without a medical license).","levelNote":"Compound: 精神 (N3) + 科 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鬱になって会社を休んでいる。","romaji":"Utsu ni natte kaisha o yasunde iru.","german":"He has become depressed and is taking time off work.","furigana":"うつ|かいしゃ|やす"},{"japanese":"仕事のストレスから鬱病になる人が増えている。","romaji":"Shigoto no sutoresu kara utsubyō ni naru hito ga fuete iru.","german":"The number of people who develop depression from work stress is increasing.","furigana":"しごと|うつびょう|ひと|ふ"},{"japanese":"冬は日が短いので、鬱っぽくなる人もいる。","romaji":"Fuyu wa hi ga mijikai node, utsuppoku naru hito mo iru.","german":"Because the days are short in winter, some people become a bit depressed.","furigana":"ふゆ|ひ|みじか|うつ|ひと"}],"notes":"鬱 stands for \"depression, low spirits\" and, because of the very complex kanji, is usually written in kana (うつ). The medical diagnosis is called うつ病 (鬱病); うつになる means \"to become depressed\", うつっぽい \"somewhat depressed\". Even more common in everyday speech is 憂鬱 \"gloomy, melancholy\" (月曜日は憂鬱だ). The counterpart 躁 is found in 躁うつ病 \"bipolar disorder\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"責任の重圧に押しつぶされそうだ。","romaji":"Sekinin no jūatsu ni oshitsubusaresō da.","german":"I feel like I'm about to be crushed by the burden of responsibility.","furigana":"せきにん|じゅうあつ|お"},{"japanese":"国民の期待という重圧の中で、彼は金メダルを取った。","romaji":"Kokumin no kitai to iu jūatsu no naka de, kare wa kinmedaru o totta.","german":"Under the pressure of the nation's expectations, he won the gold medal.","furigana":"こくみん|きたい|じゅうあつ|なか|かれ|きん|と"},{"japanese":"新しい社長は会社を立て直すという重圧に耐えている。","romaji":"Atarashii shachō wa kaisha o tatenaosu to iu jūatsu ni taete iru.","german":"The new company president is bearing up under the pressure of rebuilding the company.","furigana":"あたら|しゃちょう|かいしゃ|た|なお|じゅうあつ|た"}],"notes":"重圧 means heavy, above all psychological \"pressure, burden\" that weighs on someone: 責任の重圧, 期待の重圧, 重圧に耐える, 重圧に負ける, 重圧がかかる. It is more formal and stronger than プレッシャー, which is more common in everyday speech. Literally \"heavy pressure\", it is used today almost only figuratively, typically in sports and business reports.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"翻訳の依頼を受けた。","romaji":"Hon'yaku no irai o uketa.","german":"I received a request for a translation.","furigana":"ほんやく|いらい|う"},{"japanese":"専門家に調査を依頼することにした。","romaji":"Senmonka ni chōsa o irai suru koto ni shita.","german":"We decided to commission an expert to do the investigation.","furigana":"せんもんか|ちょうさ|いらい"},{"japanese":"申し訳ありませんが、今回のご依頼はお受けできません。","romaji":"Mōshiwake arimasen ga, konkai no go-irai wa o-uke dekimasen.","german":"I'm sorry, but we cannot accept your request this time.","furigana":"もう|わけ|こんかい|いらい|う"}],"notes":"依頼 means \"request, commission\": you officially ask someone to take care of something. Patterns: Person に Thing を依頼する, 依頼を受ける/断る, 依頼人 \"client.\" It is more formal than 頼む and typical in business correspondence (ご依頼ありがとうございます). 要請 is an even more official request, mostly from authorities; 注文 is an order for goods.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"政府に支援を要請した。","romaji":"Seifu ni shien o yōsei shita.","german":"We requested support from the government.","furigana":"せいふ|しえん|ようせい"},{"japanese":"県の要請を受けて、自衛隊が被災地に派遣された。","romaji":"Ken no yōsei o ukete, jieitai ga hisaichi ni haken sareta.","german":"At the request of the prefecture, the Self-Defense Forces were dispatched to the disaster area.","furigana":"けん|ようせい|う|じえいたい|ひさいち|はけん"},{"japanese":"市は住民に節電への協力を要請している。","romaji":"Shi wa jūmin ni setsuden e no kyōryoku o yōsei shite iru.","german":"The city is asking residents to cooperate in saving electricity.","furigana":"し|じゅうみん|せつでん|きょうりょく|ようせい"}],"notes":"要請 is a formal \"request\" or \"call\", mostly from or to institutions: 政府に支援を要請する, 〜の要請を受けて, 協力を要請する. It sounds more official and urgent than 依頼, but is less binding than 命令 \"order\" or 要求 \"demand\". Typical in news (出動要請, 協力要請); it is not used among friends.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新入社員に大きな期待を寄せている。","romaji":"Shinnyūshain ni ōkina kitai o yosete iru.","german":"We have great expectations of the new employees.","furigana":"しんにゅうしゃいん|おお|きたい|よ"},{"japanese":"期待していたほど、その映画は面白くなかった。","romaji":"Kitai shite ita hodo, sono eiga wa omoshiroku nakatta.","german":"The film wasn't as interesting as I had expected.","furigana":"きたい|えいが|おもしろ"},{"japanese":"ご期待に添えず、申し訳ございません。","romaji":"Go-kitai ni soezu, mōshiwake gozaimasen.","german":"I apologize for not meeting your expectations.","furigana":"きたい|そ|もう|わけ"}],"notes":"期待 is hopeful \"expectation\": you expect something good. Typical are 期待する (transitive with を, also 〜に期待する \"to pin hopes on someone\"), 期待を寄せる/かける \"to place expectations,\" 期待に応える \"to live up to expectations\" and 期待外れ \"disappointing.\" Before nouns, 期待の〜 means \"promising\" (期待の新人). A neutral prediction without hope is 予想, by contrast.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"議案が国会で審議されている。","romaji":"Gian ga kokkai de shingi sarete iru.","german":"The bill is being deliberated in the Diet.","furigana":"ぎあん|こっかい|しんぎ"},{"japanese":"株主総会で、すべての議案が賛成多数で可決された。","romaji":"Kabunushi sōkai de, subete no gian ga sansei tasū de kaketsu sareta.","german":"At the shareholders' meeting, all the proposals were approved by a majority.","furigana":"かぶぬしそうかい|ぎあん|さんせいたすう|かけつ"},{"japanese":"市長は新しい予算の議案を市議会に提出した。","romaji":"Shichō wa atarashii yosan no gian o shigikai ni teishutsu shita.","german":"The mayor submitted the new budget proposal to the city council.","furigana":"しちょう|あたら|よさん|ぎあん|しぎかい|ていしゅつ"}],"notes":"議案 is a motion or bill on which an assembly (parliament, city council, shareholders' meeting) decides. Typical verbs: 議案を提出する \"to submit\", 審議する \"to deliberate\", 可決する \"to pass\", 否決する \"to reject\". 法案 is narrower, only a draft law; 議題 is merely the topic on the agenda, which is not necessarily voted on. A formal word from politics and administration.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"契約書の草案を作成した。","romaji":"Keiyakusho no sōan o sakusei shita.","german":"I prepared a draft of the contract.","furigana":"けいやくしょ|そうあん|さくせい"},{"japanese":"社長のスピーチの草案を秘書が書いた。","romaji":"Shachō no supīchi no sōan o hisho ga kaita.","german":"The secretary wrote the draft of the president's speech.","furigana":"しゃちょう|そうあん|ひしょ|か"},{"japanese":"憲法の草案は何度も修正された。","romaji":"Kenpō no sōan wa nando mo shūsei sareta.","german":"The draft of the constitution was revised many times.","furigana":"けんぽう|そうあん|なんど|しゅうせい"}],"notes":"草案 is the first written draft of an official text, such as a contract, law, speech or charter. One says 草案を作成する/練る/まとめる and 草案を修正する. For everyday things like an essay or letter draft, people say 下書き instead; 原案 stresses the original proposal that is put up for deliberation. A rather formal written word.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"原案のまま承認された。","romaji":"Gen'an no mama shōnin sareta.","german":"It was approved as the original draft stood.","furigana":"げんあん|しょうにん"},{"japanese":"委員会は原案にいくつかの修正を加えた。","romaji":"Iinkai wa gen'an ni ikutsu ka no shūsei o kuwaeta.","german":"The committee made several amendments to the original proposal.","furigana":"いいんかい|げんあん|しゅうせい|くわ"},{"japanese":"このアニメの原案は有名な小説家が考えたものだ。","romaji":"Kono anime no gen'an wa yūmei na shōsetsuka ga kangaeta mono da.","german":"The original concept for this anime was thought up by a famous novelist.","furigana":"げんあん|ゆうめい|しょうせつか|かんが"}],"notes":"原案 is the original proposal, that is, the plan or draft put forward for deliberation before changes are added. Typical: 原案どおり/原案のまま可決される \"to be passed unchanged\", 原案に修正を加える \"to amend the draft\". For films and manga, 原案 appears in the credits for the \"original idea\" on which the work is based. Unlike 草案, the focus is on \"original\" as opposed to the amended version.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"問題解決の方案を考える。","romaji":"Mondai kaiketsu no hōan o kangaeru.","german":"To think of a plan for solving the problem.","furigana":"もんだいかいけつ|ほうあん|かんが"},{"japanese":"業務改善の方案をまとめて、部長に提出した。","romaji":"Gyōmu kaizen no hōan o matomete, buchō ni teishutsu shita.","german":"I put together a proposal for improving operations and submitted it to the department head.","furigana":"ぎょうむかいぜん|ほうあん|ぶちょう|ていしゅつ"},{"japanese":"市は観光客を増やすための具体的な方案を発表した。","romaji":"Shi wa kankōkyaku o fuyasu tame no gutaiteki na hōan o happyō shita.","german":"The city announced a concrete plan to increase tourists.","furigana":"し|かんこうきゃく|ふ|ぐたいてき|ほうあん|はっぴょう"}],"notes":"方案 is a worked-out plan or proposal for how to tackle a problem. The word is rather rare and literary, mostly in compounds such as 改善方案 or 解決方案. In normal Japanese, people say much more often 方策 \"measure\", 対策 \"countermeasure\" or simply 案. Not to be confused with 法案 (same reading) \"bill\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この件について検討させてください。","romaji":"Kono ken ni tsuite kentō sasete kudasai.","german":"Please let me think about this matter.","furigana":"けん|けんとう"},{"japanese":"さまざまな角度から検討した結果、計画を中止することにした。","romaji":"Samazama na kakudo kara kentō shita kekka, keikaku o chūshi suru koto ni shita.","german":"After examining it from various angles, we decided to cancel the plan.","furigana":"かくど|けんとう|けっか|けいかく|ちゅうし"},{"japanese":"引っ越しを検討しているので、物件を見て回っている。","romaji":"Hikkoshi o kentō shite iru node, bukken o mite mawatte iru.","german":"I'm considering moving, so I'm going around looking at properties.","furigana":"ひ|こ|けんとう|ぶっけん|み|まわ"}],"notes":"検討 is the thorough examination and weighing of a matter before deciding; 検討する is transitive (〜を検討する). Common: 検討中 \"under consideration,\" 検討の余地がある \"there is room for discussion,\" 検討した結果 \"after examination.\" In business conversations, 前向きに検討します is often a polite, evasive refusal. Not to be confused with the identically sounding 見当 \"guess, estimate.\"","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"書類審査に合格した。","romaji":"Shorui shinsa ni gōkaku shita.","german":"I passed the document screening.","furigana":"しょるいしんさ|ごうかく"},{"japanese":"空港の入国審査で三十分も待たされた。","romaji":"Kūkō no nyūkoku shinsa de sanjuppun mo matasareta.","german":"I was made to wait a full thirty minutes at immigration control at the airport.","furigana":"くうこう|にゅうこくしんさ|さんじゅっぷん|ま"},{"japanese":"ローンの審査に通らなかったので、車は諦めた。","romaji":"Rōn no shinsa ni tōranakatta node, kuruma wa akirameta.","german":"My loan application was not approved, so I gave up on the car.","furigana":"しんさ|とお|くるま|あきら"}],"notes":"審査 is the formal examination of whether a person or thing meets certain criteria, for example for applications, loans, competitions or entry into a country. Typical: 審査を受ける, 審査に通る/合格する, 審査に落ちる, 入国審査, 書類審査, 審査員 \"judge, jury\". 検査, by contrast, is a technical or medical examination, 試験 a test with questions.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"委員会で審議が行われた。","romaji":"Iinkai de shingi ga okonawareta.","german":"Deliberations were held in the committee.","furigana":"いいんかい|しんぎ|おこな"},{"japanese":"その法案は来週から国会で審議される予定だ。","romaji":"Sono hōan wa raishū kara kokkai de shingi sareru yotei da.","german":"The bill is scheduled to be deliberated in the Diet from next week.","furigana":"ほうあん|らいしゅう|こっかい|しんぎ|よてい"},{"japanese":"十分な審議もないまま、案は可決されてしまった。","romaji":"Jūbun na shingi mo nai mama, an wa kaketsu sarete shimatta.","german":"The proposal was passed without sufficient deliberation.","furigana":"じゅうぶん|しんぎ|あん|かけつ"}],"notes":"審議 is the detailed deliberation of a body on a motion or bill before it votes. Typical: 法案を審議する, 審議が行われる, 審議中, 審議会 \"advisory council\". Compared with 議論 \"discussion\" and 検討 \"examination\", 審議 is clearly limited to official bodies such as parliament, committees or councils. A formal word from politics and news.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ボーナスの査定が行われる。","romaji":"Bōnasu no satei ga okonawareru.","german":"Bonus assessments are carried out.","furigana":"さてい|おこな"},{"japanese":"古い車を売る前に、査定してもらった。","romaji":"Furui kuruma o uru mae ni, satei shite moratta.","german":"Before selling my old car, I had it appraised.","furigana":"ふる|くるま|う|まえ|さてい"},{"japanese":"保険会社が事故の損害額を査定した。","romaji":"Hoken gaisha ga jiko no songaigaku o satei shita.","german":"The insurance company assessed the amount of damage from the accident.","furigana":"ほけんがいしゃ|じこ|そんがいがく|さてい"}],"notes":"査定 is an examining evaluation of a value or performance, especially as a sum of money: used cars, real estate, insurance damages or the performance review of employees (人事査定, ボーナスの査定). One says 査定する, 査定を受ける, 査定額 \"appraised value\". 鑑定, by contrast, is an expert opinion on authenticity, and 評価 is general evaluation.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"専門家に美術品の鑑定を依頼した。","romaji":"Senmonka ni bijutsuhin no kantei o irai shita.","german":"I asked an expert to appraise the artwork.","furigana":"せんもんか|びじゅつひん|かんてい|いらい"},{"japanese":"DNA鑑定の結果、二人は親子だと分かった。","romaji":"DNA kantei no kekka, futari wa oyako da to wakatta.","german":"The result of the DNA test showed that the two are parent and child.","furigana":"かんてい|けっか|ふたり|おやこ|わ"},{"japanese":"祖母の指輪を鑑定してもらったら、本物のダイヤだった。","romaji":"Sobo no yubiwa o kantei shite morattara, honmono no daiya datta.","german":"When I had my grandmother's ring appraised, it turned out to be a real diamond.","furigana":"そぼ|ゆびわ|かんてい|ほんもの"}],"notes":"鑑定 is the expert assessment by a specialist: the authenticity and value of artworks or gemstones, but also DNA-鑑定, 筆跡鑑定 \"handwriting analysis\" and 精神鑑定 \"psychiatric evaluation\" in court. Typical: 鑑定を依頼する, 鑑定してもらう, 鑑定書 \"certificate of authenticity\". For a mere valuation (car, house), 査定 is more usual.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"審判の判定に異議を唱えた。","romaji":"Shinpan no hantei ni igi o tonaeta.","german":"He objected to the referee's call.","furigana":"しんぱん|はんてい|いぎ|とな"},{"japanese":"模試で志望校のA判定が出て、ほっとした。","romaji":"Moshi de shibōkō no ē hantei ga dete, hotto shita.","german":"I was relieved when I got an A rating for my first-choice school on the mock exam.","furigana":"もし|しぼうこう|はんてい|で"},{"japanese":"ビデオで確認した結果、判定が覆った。","romaji":"Bideo de kakunin shita kekka, hantei ga kutsugaetta.","german":"After checking the video, the call was overturned.","furigana":"かくにん|けっか|はんてい|くつがえ"}],"notes":"判定 is the decision made according to set criteria about who has won or into which grade something falls: referee rulings, 判定勝ち \"win on points\", exam ratings such as A判定 on mock exams, or 合否判定 \"pass/fail decision\". 判断, by contrast, is one's own judgment in everyday life, 判決 a court judgment. One says 判定する, 判定が出る, 判定に従う.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この製品は品質認定を受けている。","romaji":"Kono seihin wa hinshitsu nintei o ukete iru.","german":"This product has received quality certification.","furigana":"せいひん|ひんしつにんてい|う"},{"japanese":"父のけがは労働災害と認定された。","romaji":"Chichi no kega wa rōdō saigai to nintei sareta.","german":"My father's injury was recognized as an industrial accident.","furigana":"ちち|ろうどうさいがい|にんてい"},{"japanese":"日本語の認定試験に向けて毎日勉強している。","romaji":"Nihongo no nintei shiken ni mukete mainichi benkyō shite iru.","german":"I study every day for the Japanese certification exam.","furigana":"にほんご|にんていしけん|む|まいにちべんきょう"}],"notes":"認定 is official recognition by an authority or organization that something meets a condition. Typical: 〜と認定する/される (労災と認定される \"to be recognized as a work accident\"), 認定を受ける, 認定試験, 世界遺産に認定される. Unlike 認める (generally \"to recognize, to admit\"), 認定 is official and formal; 資格 is the qualification itself.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"指定された場所に集合してください。","romaji":"Shitei sareta basho ni shūgō shite kudasai.","german":"Please assemble at the designated place.","furigana":"してい|ばしょ|しゅうごう"},{"japanese":"新幹線の指定席を予約した。","romaji":"Shinkansen no shiteiseki o yoyaku shita.","german":"I booked a reserved seat on the Shinkansen.","furigana":"しんかんせん|していせき|よやく"},{"japanese":"荷物の配達日時を指定することができます。","romaji":"Nimotsu no haitatsu nichiji o shitei suru koto ga dekimasu.","german":"You can specify the date and time for the delivery of your package.","furigana":"にもつ|はいたつにちじ|してい"}],"notes":"指定 means to explicitly specify or designate something: place, time, person or category. Very common as the participle 指定された〜 \"specified, given\" and in compounds such as 指定席 \"reserved seat\", 日時指定 \"specified date and time\", 指定校. One says 〜を指定する or 〜に指定される (国宝に指定される \"to be designated a national treasure\"). More general is 決める; 選ぶ stresses the selection.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい部長が任命された。","romaji":"Atarashii buchō ga ninmei sareta.","german":"A new department head was appointed.","furigana":"あたら|ぶちょう|にんめい"},{"japanese":"首相は彼女を外務大臣に任命した。","romaji":"Shushō wa kanojo o gaimu daijin ni ninmei shita.","german":"The prime minister appointed her foreign minister.","furigana":"しゅしょう|かのじょ|がいむだいじん|にんめい"},{"japanese":"大臣の不祥事で、首相の任命責任が問われている。","romaji":"Daijin no fushōji de, shushō no ninmei sekinin ga towarete iru.","german":"Because of the minister's scandal, the prime minister's responsibility for the appointment is being questioned.","furigana":"だいじん|ふしょうじ|しゅしょう|にんめいせきにん|と"}],"notes":"任命 is the official appointment to an office by a higher authority: 〜を〜に任命する, in the passive 〜に任命される. Typical contexts are ministers, ambassadors, judges or leadership posts. 任命責任 is the responsibility of the person who made the appointment. 指名 is the naming of a person, 選任 stresses selection plus installation, and 任命 the formal act.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"委員の選任が行われた。","romaji":"Iin no sennin ga okonawareta.","german":"The selection of committee members was carried out.","furigana":"いいん|せんにん|おこな"},{"japanese":"株主総会で新しい取締役が選任された。","romaji":"Kabunushi sōkai de atarashii torishimariyaku ga sennin sareta.","german":"New directors were elected at the shareholders' meeting.","furigana":"かぶぬしそうかい|あたら|とりしまりやく|せんにん"},{"japanese":"被告は自分で弁護士を選任した。","romaji":"Hikoku wa jibun de bengoshi o sennin shita.","german":"The defendant appointed a lawyer himself.","furigana":"ひこく|じぶん|べんごし|せんにん"}],"notes":"選任 means to select someone and install them in a function, typical in legal and corporate language: 取締役を選任する \"to appoint directors\", 弁護人を選任する \"to appoint defense counsel\", 選任される. It is more formal and rarer than 選ぶ; 任命 stresses appointment by a higher authority, 選任 more the choice by a body or a party.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"教授の推薦で奨学金をもらった。","romaji":"Kyōju no suisen de shōgakukin o moratta.","german":"I received a scholarship on my professor's recommendation.","furigana":"きょうじゅ|すいせん|しょうがくきん"},{"japanese":"彼をリーダー候補として推薦したいと思います。","romaji":"Kare o rīdā kōho to shite suisen shitai to omoimasu.","german":"I would like to recommend him as a candidate for leader.","furigana":"かれ|こうほ|すいせん|おも"},{"japanese":"前の上司に推薦状を書いてもらった。","romaji":"Mae no jōshi ni suisenjō o kaite moratta.","german":"I had my former boss write me a letter of recommendation.","furigana":"まえ|じょうし|すいせんじょう|か"}],"notes":"推薦 is the recommendation of a person or thing to third parties, often of an official nature: 〜を〜に推薦する \"to recommend someone for something,\" 推薦状 \"letter of recommendation,\" 推薦入試 \"admission by recommendation.\" For everyday tips (\"Try this\"), people more often use 勧める or おすすめ. 推薦 sounds more formal and binding.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼がプロジェクトリーダーに指名された。","romaji":"Kare ga purojekuto rīdā ni shimei sareta.","german":"He was nominated as project leader.","furigana":"かれ|しめい"},{"japanese":"授業中に突然先生に指名されて、答えられなかった。","romaji":"Jugyōchū ni totsuzen sensei ni shimei sarete, kotaerarenakatta.","german":"In class I was suddenly called on by the teacher and couldn't answer.","furigana":"じゅぎょうちゅう|とつぜんせんせい|しめい|こた"},{"japanese":"その男は殺人の疑いで指名手配されている。","romaji":"Sono otoko wa satsujin no utagai de shimei tehai sarete iru.","german":"The man is wanted by the police on suspicion of murder.","furigana":"おとこ|さつじん|うたが|しめいてはい"}],"notes":"指名 means to name or select a particular person by name: 〜を〜に指名する, in the passive 〜に指名される. It is also used when a teacher calls on someone in class (先生に指名される), when one requests a particular hairdresser at a salon (指名料), and in 指名手配 \"wanted notice\". Compared with 任命 (formal appointment), 指名 is the mere naming.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"若手社員が部長に抜擢された。","romaji":"Wakate shain ga buchō ni batteki sareta.","german":"A young employee was picked to be department head.","furigana":"わかてしゃいん|ぶちょう|ばってき"},{"japanese":"新人の女優が映画の主役に抜擢された。","romaji":"Shinjin no joyū ga eiga no shuyaku ni batteki sareta.","german":"A newcomer actress was chosen for the lead role in the film.","furigana":"しんじん|じょゆう|えいが|しゅやく|ばってき"},{"japanese":"監督は高校生の選手を代表チームに抜擢した。","romaji":"Kantoku wa kōkōsei no senshu o daihyō chīmu ni batteki shita.","german":"The coach selected a high school student for the national team.","furigana":"かんとく|こうこうせい|せんしゅ|だいひょう|ばってき"}],"notes":"抜擢 means to pick someone out of many candidates for an important role, often surprisingly and bypassing the usual order. Mostly in the passive: 〜に抜擢される (部長に抜擢される, 主役に抜擢される). It stresses the special distinction, whereas 昇進 is normal promotion and 任命 is formal appointment. The kanji 擢 is rare; in the media 抜てき is also written.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"家具の配置を変えた。","romaji":"Kagu no haichi o kaeta.","german":"I changed the arrangement of the furniture.","furigana":"かぐ|はいち|か"},{"japanese":"イベント会場の入り口に警備員を配置した。","romaji":"Ibento kaijō no iriguchi ni keibiin o haichi shita.","german":"Security guards were posted at the entrance of the event venue.","furigana":"かいじょう|い|ぐち|けいびいん|はいち"},{"japanese":"四月から営業部に配置されることになった。","romaji":"Shigatsu kara eigyōbu ni haichi sareru koto ni natta.","german":"From April, I will be assigned to the sales department.","furigana":"しがつ|えいぎょうぶ|はいち"}],"notes":"配置 is the planned arrangement of things or people at particular places: furniture, keyboards, personnel, police or troops. One says 〜を配置する, 〜に配置される, 配置を変える, 人員配置 and 配置転換 \"transfer, reassignment\". 配置 stresses distribution in space or in an organization; 並べる is only \"to line up in a row\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"来月から海外に赴任する。","romaji":"Raigetsu kara kaigai ni funin suru.","german":"From next month, I will take up a post abroad.","furigana":"らいげつ|かいがい|ふにん"},{"japanese":"父は単身赴任で大阪に住んでいる。","romaji":"Chichi wa tanshin funin de Ōsaka ni sunde iru.","german":"My father lives in Osaka alone because of a job transfer, without the family.","furigana":"ちち|たんしんふにん|おおさか|す"},{"japanese":"赴任先の生活にもようやく慣れてきた。","romaji":"Funinsaki no seikatsu ni mo yōyaku narete kita.","german":"I have finally gotten used to life at my new posting.","furigana":"ふにんさき|せいかつ|な"}],"notes":"赴任 means to move to a new place of work because of a transfer and take up the job there; 赴任する is intransitive with に (海外に赴任する). Very common: 単身赴任 \"to be transferred to another place without one's family\", 赴任先 \"place of posting\", 海外赴任. 転勤 is the transfer itself, while 赴任 stresses going there and taking up the post. The counterpart is 帰任.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"三年間の赴任を終えて帰任した。","romaji":"Sannenkan no funin o oete kinin shita.","german":"I returned to my post after finishing a three-year assignment.","furigana":"さんねんかん|ふにん|お|きにん"},{"japanese":"来年の春、東京本社に帰任する予定です。","romaji":"Rainen no haru, Tōkyō honsha ni kinin suru yotei desu.","german":"I am scheduled to return to the Tokyo headquarters next spring.","furigana":"らいねん|はる|とうきょうほんしゃ|きにん|よてい"},{"japanese":"帰任後は、海外での経験を生かして新しい部署で働いている。","romaji":"Kinin-go wa, kaigai de no keiken o ikashite atarashii busho de hataraite iru.","german":"Since returning, I have been working in a new department, making use of my overseas experience.","furigana":"きにんご|かいがい|けいけん|い|あたら|ぶしょ|はたら"}],"notes":"帰任 is the return to one's original workplace, usually to the head office or one's home country after a posting; 帰任する is intransitive (本社に帰任する). It is the counterpart of 赴任 and belongs to company and official language. 帰国 is generally \"returning home to one's country\", while 帰任 stresses returning to the old post.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"業務を外部に委託する。","romaji":"Gyōmu o gaibu ni itaku suru.","german":"We outsource the work to an outside party.","furigana":"ぎょうむ|がいぶ|いたく"},{"japanese":"市は公園の管理を民間企業に委託している。","romaji":"Shi wa kōen no kanri o minkan kigyō ni itaku shite iru.","german":"The city has entrusted the management of the park to a private company.","furigana":"し|こうえん|かんり|みんかんきぎょう|いたく"},{"japanese":"作家の作品をギャラリーで委託販売している。","romaji":"Sakka no sakuhin o gyararī de itaku hanbai shite iru.","german":"The artist's works are sold on consignment at a gallery.","furigana":"さっか|さくひん|いたくはんばい"}],"notes":"委託 means to entrust a task or goods to another company or person by contract: 〜を〜に委託する (業務を外部に委託する). Common in 業務委託 \"commissioned work, outsourcing\", 委託販売 \"consignment sale\" and 委託先 \"contractor\". 依頼 is the general \"to ask, commission\", and 外注 stresses outsourcing to external providers.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"デザインの仕事を外注に出した。","romaji":"Dezain no shigoto o gaichū ni dashita.","german":"I outsourced the design work.","furigana":"しごと|がいちゅう|だ"},{"japanese":"外注費を減らすため、システム開発を社内で行うことにした。","romaji":"Gaichūhi o herasu tame, shisutemu kaihatsu o shanai de okonau koto ni shita.","german":"To reduce outsourcing costs, we decided to do the system development in-house.","furigana":"がいちゅうひ|へ|かいはつ|しゃない|おこな"},{"japanese":"翻訳は外注しているので、少し時間がかかります。","romaji":"Hon'yaku wa gaichū shite iru node, sukoshi jikan ga kakarimasu.","german":"We outsource translation, so it takes a little time.","furigana":"ほんやく|がいちゅう|すこ|じかん"}],"notes":"外注 is giving work to outside companies, that is, \"outsourcing\": 〜を外注する or 外注に出す. Related are 外注先 \"external contractor\" and 外注費 \"outsourcing costs\". The opposite is 内製 \"in-house production\". 委託 is more formal and also common in the public sector; 外注 sounds like everyday business and cost accounting.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"下請け業者に作業を依頼した。","romaji":"Shitauke gyōsha ni sagyō o irai shita.","german":"I asked a subcontractor to do the work.","furigana":"したう|ぎょうしゃ|さぎょう|いらい"},{"japanese":"うちは大手自動車メーカーの下請けをしている小さな工場です。","romaji":"Uchi wa ōte jidōsha mēkā no shitauke o shite iru chiisa na kōjō desu.","german":"We are a small factory working as a subcontractor for a major automaker.","furigana":"おおてじどうしゃ|したう|ちい|こうじょう"},{"japanese":"下請けに無理な値下げを求めるのは問題だ。","romaji":"Shitauke ni muri na nesage o motomeru no wa mondai da.","german":"Demanding unreasonable price cuts from subcontractors is a problem.","furigana":"したう|むり|ねさ|もと|もんだい"}],"notes":"下請け refers both to the subcontract and to the company that carries it out (\"subcontractor\"). Typical: 下請け業者, 下請け会社, 大手の下請けをする, 下請けに出す. In Japan there are frequent reports of 下請けいじめ, the pressure big companies put on small suppliers. The counterpart is 元請け \"main contractor\"; in administrative texts 下請 without け is also written.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"元請け会社が全体の管理を行う。","romaji":"Motouke gaisha ga zentai no kanri o okonau.","german":"The main contractor handles overall management.","furigana":"もとう|がいしゃ|ぜんたい|かんり|おこな"},{"japanese":"うちの会社は仕事のほとんどを元請けからもらっている。","romaji":"Uchi no kaisha wa shigoto no hotondo o motouke kara moratte iru.","german":"Our company gets almost all its work from the main contractor.","furigana":"かいしゃ|しごと|もとう"},{"japanese":"事故の責任は元請けにもあると判断された。","romaji":"Jiko no sekinin wa motouke ni mo aru to handan sareta.","german":"It was decided that the prime contractor also bears responsibility for the accident.","furigana":"じこ|せきにん|もとう|はんだん"}],"notes":"元請け is the main contractor, who receives the order directly from the client or customer and passes parts of it on to 下請け. Typical: 元請け会社, 元請け業者, 元請けから仕事をもらう. The word is used mainly in the construction, IT and manufacturing industries. Counterpart: 下請け \"subcontractor\", and below it also 孫請け \"sub-subcontractor\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"公共事業の入札に参加した。","romaji":"Kōkyō jigyō no nyūsatsu ni sanka shita.","german":"I took part in the bidding for a public works project.","furigana":"こうきょうじぎょう|にゅうさつ|さんか"},{"japanese":"ネットオークションで古いカメラに入札してみた。","romaji":"Netto ōkushon de furui kamera ni nyūsatsu shite mita.","german":"I tried bidding on an old camera in an online auction.","furigana":"ふる|にゅうさつ"},{"japanese":"入札をめぐる不正が発覚し、担当者が逮捕された。","romaji":"Nyūsatsu o meguru fusei ga hakkaku shi, tantōsha ga taiho sareta.","german":"Irregularities surrounding the bidding were discovered, and the person in charge was arrested.","furigana":"にゅうさつ|ふせい|はっかく|たんとうしゃ|たいほ"}],"notes":"入札 is submitting a bid, whether in a public tender (公共事業の入札, 競争入札) or at an auction, also online. 入札する is intransitive; the object takes に: 入札に参加する, オークションに入札する. The one who wins the bid has 落札した. A formal word from business and administration, but familiar in everyday life through online auctions.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"最も安い価格で落札した。","romaji":"Mottomo yasui kakaku de rakusatsu shita.","german":"We won the bid with the lowest price.","furigana":"もっと|やす|かかく|らくさつ"},{"japanese":"オークションで欲しかったゲームを落札できた。","romaji":"Ōkushon de hoshikatta gēmu o rakusatsu dekita.","german":"I managed to win the game I wanted at an auction.","furigana":"ほ|らくさつ"},{"japanese":"有名な画家の絵が十億円で落札された。","romaji":"Yūmei na gaka no e ga jūokuen de rakusatsu sareta.","german":"A painting by a famous artist sold at auction for one billion yen.","furigana":"ゆうめい|がか|え|じゅうおくえん|らくさつ"}],"notes":"落札 is the successful bid: you win the contract in a tender or win something at an auction. 落札する is transitive (絵を落札する), and often 〜円で落札される \"to be sold for ... yen\". Related: 落札者 \"winning bidder\" and 落札価格 \"winning price\". The preceding step is 入札 \"to place a bid\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"両国間で条約が締結された。","romaji":"Ryōkokukan de jōyaku ga teiketsu sareta.","german":"A treaty was concluded between the two countries.","furigana":"りょうこくかん|じょうやく|ていけつ"},{"japanese":"新しい取引先と販売契約を締結しました。","romaji":"Atarashii torihikisaki to hanbai keiyaku o teiketsu shimashita.","german":"We have concluded a sales contract with a new business partner.","furigana":"あたら|とりひきさき|はんばいけいやく|ていけつ"},{"japanese":"労使交渉の結果、新しい協定の締結に至った。","romaji":"Rōshi kōshō no kekka, atarashii kyōtei no teiketsu ni itatta.","german":"As a result of labor-management negotiations, a new agreement was concluded.","furigana":"ろうしこうしょう|けっか|あたら|きょうてい|ていけつ|いた"}],"notes":"締結 is the formal conclusion of a contract or agreement: 契約を締結する, 条約が締結される, 協定の締結. The word belongs to legal, business and news language; in everyday speech people say the simpler 契約を結ぶ. In engineering, 締結 also means joining parts with screws, but learners rarely need this.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"携帯電話の契約を解約した。","romaji":"Keitai denwa no keiyaku o kaiyaku shita.","german":"I canceled my mobile phone contract.","furigana":"けいたいでんわ|けいやく|かいやく"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"引っ越し前に、インターネットの解約を申し込んだ。","romaji":"Hikkoshi mae ni, intānetto no kaiyaku o mōshikonda.","german":"Before moving, I applied to cancel my internet service.","furigana":"ひ|こ|まえ|かいやく|もう|こ"},{"japanese":"定期預金を途中で解約すると、利息が少なくなります。","romaji":"Teiki yokin o tochū de kaiyaku suru to, risoku ga sukunaku narimasu.","german":"If you cancel a fixed-term deposit midway, you will receive less interest.","furigana":"ていきよきん|とちゅう|かいやく|りそく|すく"}],"notes":"解約 is the termination of an ongoing contract, such as for a phone, internet, insurance, subscription or account: 〜を解約する, 解約を申し込む, 解約手数料 \"cancellation fee\". A one-time booking, by contrast, is canceled with 予約をキャンセルする or 取り消す. 解除 is legally the rescission of a contract, often unilaterally because of breach of contract.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"警報が解除された。","romaji":"Keihō ga kaijo sareta.","german":"The alarm was lifted.","furigana":"けいほう|かいじょ"},{"japanese":"指紋でスマホのロックを解除する。","romaji":"Shimon de sumaho no rokku o kaijo suru.","german":"I unlock my smartphone with my fingerprint.","furigana":"しもん|かいじょ"},{"japanese":"相手が約束を守らなかったので、契約を解除した。","romaji":"Aite ga yakusoku o mamoranakatta node, keiyaku o kaijo shita.","german":"Because the other party did not keep the agreement, we cancelled the contract.","furigana":"あいて|やくそく|まも|けいやく|かいじょ"}],"notes":"解除 means to lift an existing state, restriction or bond: 警報が解除される \"the warning is lifted\", 規制を解除する, ロックを解除する \"to unlock\", 契約を解除する \"to withdraw from a contract\". Often used in the passive in news reports. In the contract context, 解除 is usually unilateral and legal, whereas 解約 is the ordinary cancellation of a subscription or contract.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"予約の取消をお願いします。","romaji":"Yoyaku no torikeshi o onegai shimasu.","german":"Please cancel the reservation.","furigana":"よやく|とりけし|ねが"},{"japanese":"前日の取消には、取消料がかかります。","romaji":"Zenjitsu no torikeshi ni wa, torikeshiryō ga kakarimasu.","german":"There is a cancellation fee for cancellations the day before.","furigana":"ぜんじつ|とりけし|とりけしりょう"},{"japanese":"飲酒運転で、彼は免許取消の処分を受けた。","romaji":"Inshu unten de, kare wa menkyo torikeshi no shobun o uketa.","german":"For drunk driving, he received the penalty of license revocation.","furigana":"いんしゅうんてん|かれ|めんきょとりけし|しょぶん|う"}],"notes":"取消 is the noun of 取り消す \"to revoke, to cancel\". The spelling without り appears mainly in forms, official texts and on buttons (取消, 取消料, 免許取消); in regular text 取り消し is usually written. It is used for reservations, orders, permits and statements (発言の取り消し). In everyday speech people also simply say キャンセル for bookings.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不良品だったので返金を求めた。","romaji":"Furyōhin datta node henkin o motometa.","german":"It was a defective product, so I asked for a refund.","furigana":"ふりょうひん|へんきん|もと"},{"japanese":"コンサートが中止になり、チケット代が全額返金された。","romaji":"Konsāto ga chūshi ni nari, chiketto-dai ga zengaku henkin sareta.","german":"The concert was canceled, and the ticket price was refunded in full.","furigana":"ちゅうし|だい|ぜんがくへんきん"},{"japanese":"ご満足いただけない場合は返金いたします。","romaji":"Go-manzoku itadakenai baai wa henkin itashimasu.","german":"If you are not satisfied, we will refund your money.","furigana":"まんぞく|ばあい|へんきん"}],"notes":"返金 is the repayment of money already paid, typically in shopping or for canceled events: 返金する, 返金を求める, 返金に応じる \"to agree to a refund\", 全額返金 \"full refund\". 返済, by contrast, is repaying a loan, and 払い戻し is a similar, somewhat more official word for refunds (e.g. for train tickets).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"図書館に本を返却した。","romaji":"Toshokan ni hon o henkyaku shita.","german":"I returned the book to the library.","furigana":"としょかん|ほん|へんきゃく"},{"japanese":"レンタカーは満タンにしてから返却してください。","romaji":"Rentakā wa mantan ni shite kara henkyaku shite kudasai.","german":"Please return the rental car with a full tank.","furigana":"まん|へんきゃく"},{"japanese":"返却期限を過ぎると、延滞料がかかります。","romaji":"Henkyaku kigen o sugiru to, entairyō ga kakarimasu.","german":"After the return deadline passes, a late fee is charged.","furigana":"へんきゃくきげん|す|えんたいりょう"}],"notes":"返却 is the return of something borrowed or entrusted to the place you got it from: books, rental cars, loaned equipment, graded exams. Typical: 〜を返却する, 返却期限 \"return deadline\", 返却口 \"return slot\". 返す is the everyday word; 返却 sounds more formal and often appears on signs and in rules.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"未払いの請求書が三件ある。","romaji":"Miharai no seikyūsho ga sanken aru.","german":"There are three unpaid invoices.","furigana":"みはら|せいきゅうしょ|さんけん"},{"japanese":"会社に未払いの残業代を請求した。","romaji":"Kaisha ni miharai no zangyōdai o seikyū shita.","german":"I demanded the unpaid overtime pay from the company.","furigana":"かいしゃ|みはら|ざんぎょうだい|せいきゅう"},{"japanese":"家賃が二か月未払いのままだと、退去を求められます。","romaji":"Yachin ga nikagetsu miharai no mama da to, taikyo o motomeraremasu.","german":"If rent stays unpaid for two months, you will be asked to move out.","furigana":"やちん|に|げつみはら|たいきょ|もと"}],"notes":"未払い means \"not yet paid\" and is mostly used attributively with の (未払いの請求書) or predicatively (家賃が未払いだ). Well-known phrases are 未払い金 \"outstanding amount\" and 未払い残業代 \"unpaid overtime\". The prefix 未 \"not yet\" is also found in 未定, 未完成. In forms, 未払 without い is also written.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"納品の催促を受けた。","romaji":"Nōhin no saisoku o uketa.","german":"I received a reminder about the delivery.","furigana":"のうひん|さいそく|う"},{"japanese":"友達に貸したお金を返してほしいと催促した。","romaji":"Tomodachi ni kashita okane o kaeshite hoshii to saisoku shita.","german":"I urged my friend to give back the money I lent.","furigana":"ともだち|か|かね|かえ|さいそく"},{"japanese":"催促するようで申し訳ありませんが、お返事をお待ちしております。","romaji":"Saisoku suru yō de mōshiwake arimasen ga, o-henji o o-machi shite orimasu.","german":"I am sorry if this seems like a reminder, but I am awaiting your reply.","furigana":"さいそく|もう|わけ|へんじ|ま"}],"notes":"催促 is repeatedly urging someone to finally do something that is due: payment, delivery, a reply. One says 〜を催促する (返事を催促する), 〜に催促される, 催促の電話/メール. 督促 is the formal, often written reminder (督促状); 催促 is more general and also common in private life. 促す, by contrast, is more neutral, \"to encourage, to urge\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"支払いは月末までにお願いします。","romaji":"Shiharai wa getsumatsu made ni onegai shimasu.","german":"Please make the payment by the end of the month.","furigana":"しはら|げつまつ|ねが"},{"japanese":"カードでの支払いはできますか。","romaji":"Kaado de no shiharai wa dekimasu ka?","german":"Can I pay by card?","furigana":"しはら"},{"japanese":"毎月の家賃の支払いが大変だ。","romaji":"Maitsuki no yachin no shiharai ga taihen da.","german":"Paying the monthly rent is hard.","furigana":"まいつき|やちん|しはら|たいへん"}],"notes":"支払い is payment, the noun of the verb 支払う. Typical are 支払いをする／済ませる \"to pay\" and 支払いが遅れる \"to be late with payment\"; in a shop people politely ask お支払いはどうなさいますか \"How would you like to pay?\" In forms and compounds, the spelling without い is common: 支払方法, 支払期限. In compounds, the short 払い is also used (前払い \"advance payment,\" 後払い).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"税金の納付期限が近い。","romaji":"Zeikin no nōfu kigen ga chikai.","german":"The deadline for paying taxes is near.","furigana":"ぜいきん|のうふきげん|ちか"},{"japanese":"年金の保険料は、コンビニでも納付できます。","romaji":"Nenkin no hokenryō wa, konbini demo nōfu dekimasu.","german":"You can also pay your pension insurance premiums at a convenience store.","furigana":"ねんきん|ほけんりょう|のうふ"},{"japanese":"納付書をなくしてしまったので、市役所に再発行を頼んだ。","romaji":"Nōfusho o nakushite shimatta node, shiyakusho ni saihakkō o tanonda.","german":"I lost my payment slip, so I asked the city hall to reissue it.","furigana":"のうふしょ|しやくしょ|さいはっこう|たの"}],"notes":"納付 is paying money to the state or public bodies: taxes, pension contributions, fees. Typical: 税金を納付する, 納付期限 \"payment deadline\", 納付書 \"payment slip\". It is an official word; in everyday speech people say 払う, and for taxes also 納める. 納入 can additionally mean \"delivery of goods\", whereas 納付 concerns only money (and documents).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"製品の納入が遅れている。","romaji":"Seihin no nōnyū ga okurete iru.","german":"Delivery of the products is delayed.","furigana":"せいひん|のうにゅう|おく"},{"japanese":"授業料は四月末までに納入してください。","romaji":"Jugyōryō wa shigatsu-matsu made ni nōnyū shite kudasai.","german":"Please pay the tuition fees by the end of April.","furigana":"じゅぎょうりょう|しがつまつ|のうにゅう"},{"japanese":"この会社は自動車メーカーに部品を納入している。","romaji":"Kono kaisha wa jidōsha mēkā ni buhin o nōnyū shite iru.","german":"This company supplies parts to automobile manufacturers.","furigana":"かいしゃ|じどうしゃ|ぶひん|のうにゅう"}],"notes":"納入 has two meanings: paying fees (授業料の納入) and delivering goods to a buyer (部品を納入する, 納入業者 \"supplier\"). In business language it usually means delivery to companies or government offices. 納品 is almost synonymous for goods; 納付 refers only to payments to public bodies.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"税金の徴収が強化された。","romaji":"Zeikin no chōshū ga kyōka sareta.","german":"Tax collection has been stepped up.","furigana":"ぜいきん|ちょうしゅう|きょうか"},{"japanese":"サークルの会費は毎月千円徴収します。","romaji":"Sākuru no kaihi wa maitsuki sen'en chōshū shimasu.","german":"The club fee of 1,000 yen is collected every month.","furigana":"かいひ|まいつきせんえんちょうしゅう"},{"japanese":"給料から所得税が源泉徴収されている。","romaji":"Kyūryō kara shotokuzei ga gensen chōshū sarete iru.","german":"Income tax is withheld directly from salary.","furigana":"きゅうりょう|しょとくぜい|げんせんちょうしゅう"}],"notes":"徴収 is the collecting of money by an authorized body: taxes, fees, membership dues. Typical: 〜を徴収する, 会費を徴収する, 源泉徴収 \"withholding tax\". It is the recipient's perspective; the payer 納める or 納付する. 集める is the neutral everyday word for \"to collect\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"所得に応じて課税される。","romaji":"Shotoku ni ōjite kazei sareru.","german":"You are taxed according to your income.","furigana":"しょとく|おう|かぜい"},{"japanese":"政府は輸入品に新たに課税することを決めた。","romaji":"Seifu wa yunyūhin ni arata ni kazei suru koto o kimeta.","german":"The government decided to impose a new tax on imported goods.","furigana":"せいふ|ゆにゅうひん|あら|かぜい|き"},{"japanese":"この手当は課税の対象にならない。","romaji":"Kono teate wa kazei no taishō ni naranai.","german":"This allowance is not subject to taxation.","furigana":"てあて|かぜい|たいしょう"}],"notes":"課税 is taxation, that is, imposing a tax; the thing taxed takes に: 所得に課税する, 輸入品に課税される. Common compounds: 課税対象 \"taxable\", 非課税 \"tax-free\", 課税所得. From the citizen's point of view, the counterpart is 納税 \"paying taxes\". A formal word from administration and the news.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"原価を下げるために工夫した。","romaji":"Genka o sageru tame ni kufū shita.","german":"I worked out ways to lower costs.","furigana":"げんか|さ|くふう"},{"japanese":"在庫を減らすため、原価割れの値段で売った。","romaji":"Zaiko o herasu tame, genkaware no nedan de utta.","german":"To reduce inventory, we sold at below cost.","furigana":"ざいこ|へ|げんかわ|ねだん|う"},{"japanese":"このラーメンの原価は一杯二百円ぐらいだそうだ。","romaji":"Kono rāmen no genka wa ippai nihyakuen gurai da sō da.","german":"The cost of one bowl of this ramen is said to be about 200 yen.","furigana":"げんか|いっぱいにひゃくえん"}],"notes":"原価 is the cost price of a product, that is, purchase or production cost without profit. Typical: 原価を下げる, 原価計算, 原価率 \"cost ratio\" and 原価割れ \"below cost\" (selling at a loss). Opposites are 売値/販売価格 \"selling price\" and 定価 \"list price\". 実費 is the actual cost incurred for a service.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この本の定価は千円だ。","romaji":"Kono hon no teika wa sen'en da.","german":"The list price of this book is a thousand yen.","furigana":"ほん|ていか|せんえん"},{"japanese":"セールで、定価の三割引きでコートを買った。","romaji":"Sēru de, teika no sanwaribiki de kōto o katta.","german":"I bought a coat at 30 percent off the list price in the sale.","furigana":"ていか|さんわりび|か"},{"japanese":"ネットで探せば、定価より安く買えるかもしれない。","romaji":"Netto de sagaseba, teika yori yasuku kaeru kamo shirenai.","german":"If you search online, you may be able to buy it for less than the list price.","furigana":"さが|ていか|やす|か"}],"notes":"定価 is the regular selling price set by the manufacturer or publisher, e.g. the price printed on books. Typical: 定価で買う, 定価の三割引き \"30% off list price\", 定価より安い. 原価 is the cost price, and 値段/価格 is price in general. In discount promotions, the 定価 is often crossed out with the sale price next to it.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"交渉して値引きしてもらった。","romaji":"Kōshō shite nebiki shite moratta.","german":"I negotiated and got a discount.","furigana":"こうしょう|ねび"},{"japanese":"閉店前のスーパーで、値引きされたお弁当を買った。","romaji":"Heiten mae no sūpā de, nebiki sareta o-bentō o katta.","german":"Just before closing time I bought a marked-down bento at the supermarket.","furigana":"へいてんまえ|ねび|べんとう|か"},{"japanese":"まとめて買うので、少し値引きしてもらえませんか。","romaji":"Matomete kau node, sukoshi nebiki shite moraemasen ka.","german":"I'm buying several at once - could you give me a little discount?","furigana":"か|すこ|ねび"}],"notes":"値引き is a reduction of the price, usually by the seller: 値引きする, 値引きしてもらう, 〜円値引き. In supermarkets, 値引きシール mark reduced goods. Similar are 割引 (a percentage discount, more fixed by rule, e.g. 学生割引) and, colloquially, まける \"to knock down the price\". 値下げ is a general price cut, whereas 値引き is more of a discount in an individual case.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"閉店前は半額になる。","romaji":"Heiten mae wa hangaku ni naru.","german":"Everything is half price before closing time.","furigana":"へいてんまえ|はんがく"},{"japanese":"小学生以下の入場料は半額です。","romaji":"Shōgakusei ika no nyūjōryō wa hangaku desu.","german":"Admission for elementary school children and under is half price.","furigana":"しょうがくせいいか|にゅうじょうりょう|はんがく"},{"japanese":"会社が引っ越し費用の半額を出してくれた。","romaji":"Kaisha ga hikkoshi hiyō no hangaku o dashite kureta.","german":"The company paid half of the moving expenses.","furigana":"かいしゃ|ひ|こ|ひよう|はんがく|だ"}],"notes":"半額 is \"half the amount\", above all half price: 半額になる, 半額で買う, 半額セール, 子どもは半額. For fares and admission, it means \"half fare\". The more general word for \"half\" is 半分; 半額 refers only to sums of money. A discount of 50% is also called 五割引き.","levelNote":"Compound: 額 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"入場は無料です。","romaji":"Nyūjō wa muryō desu.","german":"Admission is free.","furigana":"にゅうじょう|むりょう"},{"japanese":"五千円以上買うと、送料が無料になります。","romaji":"Gosen'en ijō kau to, sōryō ga muryō ni narimasu.","german":"If you buy 5,000 yen or more, shipping is free.","furigana":"ごせんえんいじょうか|そうりょう|むりょう"},{"japanese":"このアプリは無料で使えるけど、広告が多い。","romaji":"Kono apuri wa muryō de tsukaeru kedo, kōkoku ga ōi.","german":"This app is free to use, but it has a lot of ads.","furigana":"むりょう|つか|こうこく|おお"}],"notes":"無料 means \"free of charge, no fee\" and is used as a noun with だ/です or with の: 無料の駐車場, 入場無料, 送料無料 \"free shipping\", 無料で使える. The opposite is 有料. Colloquially one also says ただ (often written タダ), which sounds more casual; on signs and in advertising, however, it is almost always 無料.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 28"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この駐車場は有料です。","romaji":"Kono chūshajō wa yūryō desu.","german":"This parking lot is paid.","furigana":"ちゅうしゃじょう|ゆうりょう"},{"japanese":"レジ袋は有料ですが、お付けしますか。","romaji":"Rejibukuro wa yūryō desu ga, o-tsuke shimasu ka.","german":"Plastic bags cost extra. Would you like one?","furigana":"ぶくろ|ゆうりょう|つ"},{"japanese":"有料道路を使えば、一時間早く着く。","romaji":"Yūryō dōro o tsukaeba, ichijikan hayaku tsuku.","german":"If we take the toll road, we'll arrive an hour earlier.","furigana":"ゆうりょうどうろ|つか|いちじかんはや|つ"}],"notes":"有料 means \"subject to a fee, paid\" and is used with だ/です or with の: 有料の駐車場, 有料道路 \"toll road\", 有料会員, 有料になる. The opposite is 無料. Since 2020, for example, plastic bags in stores in Japan are 有料 (レジ袋の有料化). It says only that something costs money, not how much.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"交通費は実費で支給される。","romaji":"Kōtsūhi wa jippi de shikyū sareru.","german":"Travel expenses are reimbursed at actual cost.","furigana":"こうつうひ|じっぴ|しきゅう"},{"japanese":"参加費は無料ですが、材料費は実費をいただきます。","romaji":"Sankahi wa muryō desu ga, zairyōhi wa jippi o itadakimasu.","german":"Participation is free, but we charge for the materials at cost.","furigana":"さんかひ|むりょう|ざいりょうひ|じっぴ"},{"japanese":"書類の郵送には、実費として三百円かかります。","romaji":"Shorui no yūsō ni wa, jippi to shite sanbyakuen kakarimasu.","german":"300 yen in actual costs is incurred for mailing the documents.","furigana":"しょるい|ゆうそう|じっぴ|さんびゃくえん"}],"notes":"実費 is the cost actually incurred, without markup or profit, e.g. for travel, materials or postage. Typical: 実費で支給する \"to reimburse at actual cost\", 実費負担, 材料費は実費. Do not confuse with 自費 \"at one's own expense\": 実費 is about the amount, 自費 is about who pays.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この治療は自費になります。","romaji":"Kono chiryō wa jihi ni narimasu.","german":"This treatment is at your own expense.","furigana":"ちりょう|じひ"},{"japanese":"会社の研修ではなく、自費でセミナーに参加した。","romaji":"Kaisha no kenshū de wa naku, jihi de seminā ni sanka shita.","german":"I attended the seminar at my own expense, not as company training.","furigana":"かいしゃ|けんしゅう|じひ|さんか"},{"japanese":"祖父は自費出版で自分の詩集を出した。","romaji":"Sofu wa jihi shuppan de jibun no shishū o dashita.","german":"My grandfather published his own poetry collection through self-publishing.","furigana":"そふ|じひしゅっぱん|じぶん|ししゅう|だ"}],"notes":"自費 means that you pay for something out of your own pocket, without a company, the state or insurance: 自費で留学する, 自費出版 \"self-publishing\", 自費診療 \"treatment not covered by health insurance\". Typical are 自費で〜する and 自費になる. Opposites are 公費 \"public funds\" and 経費 \"company expenses\"; 実費, by contrast, concerns the actual amount of the costs.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"研究は公費で賄われている。","romaji":"Kenkyū wa kōhi de makanawarete iru.","german":"The research is funded by public money.","furigana":"けんきゅう|こうひ|まかな"},{"japanese":"議員が公費で家族旅行をしていたことが問題になった。","romaji":"Giin ga kōhi de kazoku ryokō o shite ita koto ga mondai ni natta.","german":"It became a problem that a lawmaker had taken a family trip at public expense.","furigana":"ぎいん|こうひ|かぞくりょこう|もんだい"},{"japanese":"この予防接種は公費で受けられます。","romaji":"Kono yobō sesshu wa kōhi de ukeraremasu.","german":"This vaccination is available at public expense.","furigana":"よぼうせっしゅ|こうひ|う"}],"notes":"公費 is public funds, that is, money from the state or local government derived from taxes: 公費で賄う \"to cover from public funds\", 公費負担, 公費の無駄遣い \"waste of taxpayer money\". The counterpart is 私費 or 自費 \"at one's own expense\". The word is formal and common in news about budgets, health and politicians' scandals.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"医療費の負担が大きい。","romaji":"Iryōhi no futan ga ōkii.","german":"The burden of medical expenses is heavy.","furigana":"いりょうひ|ふたん|おお"},{"japanese":"長時間のパソコン作業は目に負担がかかる。","romaji":"Chōjikan no pasokon sagyō wa me ni futan ga kakaru.","german":"Working at a computer for hours strains the eyes.","furigana":"ちょうじかん|さぎょう|め|ふたん"},{"japanese":"返品の送料はお客様のご負担となります。","romaji":"Henpin no sōryō wa o-kyakusama no go-futan to narimasu.","german":"The customer bears the shipping costs for returns.","furigana":"へんぴん|そうりょう|きゃくさま|ふたん"}],"notes":"負担 is on the one hand a \"load, burden\" (体に負担がかかる, 負担が大きい, 負担になる), and on the other the assuming of costs or responsibility: 〜を負担する (費用を負担する). In business texts the polite form is 送料はお客様のご負担となります. 分担, by contrast, is the dividing of a load among several people.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"費用を分担して支払った。","romaji":"Hiyō o buntan shite shiharatta.","german":"We split the costs and paid.","furigana":"ひよう|ぶんたん|しはら"},{"japanese":"共働きなので、夫婦で家事を分担している。","romaji":"Tomobataraki na node, fūfu de kaji o buntan shite iru.","german":"Since we both work, we share the housework as a couple.","furigana":"ともばたら|ふうふ|かじ|ぶんたん"},{"japanese":"発表の準備は、役割を分担して進めましょう。","romaji":"Happyō no junbi wa, yakuwari o buntan shite susumemashō.","german":"Let's tackle the preparation of the presentation with a clear division of tasks.","furigana":"はっぴょう|じゅんび|やくわり|ぶんたん|すす"}],"notes":"分担 means dividing work, costs or responsibility among several people, each taking a share: 〜を分担する, 家事を分担する, 役割分担 \"division of roles\", 分担して〜する. In contrast to 負担 (bearing a burden), 分担 emphasizes the dividing; 割り勘 specifically means splitting the bill in a restaurant.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新会社に出資した。","romaji":"Shingaisha ni shusshi shita.","german":"I invested in the new company.","furigana":"しんがいしゃ|しゅっし"},{"japanese":"三社が共同で出資して、新しいホテルを建てた。","romaji":"Sansha ga kyōdō de shusshi shite, atarashii hoteru o tateta.","german":"Three companies jointly invested and built a new hotel.","furigana":"さんしゃ|きょうどう|しゅっし|あたら|た"},{"japanese":"出資者に事業の進み具合を報告する必要がある。","romaji":"Shusshisha ni jigyō no susumiguai o hōkoku suru hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"You need to report the progress of the business to the investors.","furigana":"しゅっししゃ|じぎょう|すす|ぐあい|ほうこく|ひつよう"}],"notes":"出資 means to put money or capital into a company or project and thereby become a partner or backer. As a する-verb, the target takes に: 新会社に出資する; the amount can take を: 一億円を出資する. Typical compounds are 出資者 \"backer\", 出資金 \"capital contribution\" and 共同出資 \"joint investment\". Unlike 投資, which generally means \"to invest money to make a profit\" (e.g. in stocks), 出資 stresses participation in the capital of a specific undertaking.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"研究助成金に応募した。","romaji":"Kenkyū joseikin ni ōbo shita.","german":"I applied for a research grant.","furigana":"けんきゅうじょせいきん|おうぼ"},{"japanese":"市は子育て世帯に家賃の一部を助成している。","romaji":"Shi wa kosodate setai ni yachin no ichibu o josei shite iru.","german":"The city subsidizes part of the rent for households raising children.","furigana":"し|こそだ|せたい|やちん|いちぶ|じょせい"},{"japanese":"この映画は文化財団の助成を受けて制作された。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa bunka zaidan no josei o ukete seisaku sareta.","german":"This film was produced with a grant from a cultural foundation.","furigana":"えいが|ぶんかざいだん|じょせい|う|せいさく"}],"notes":"助成 is financial support for projects, research or particular groups from the state, local governments or foundations. It is a word of administrative and application language and appears above all in 助成金 \"grant, subsidy\", 〜の助成を受ける \"to receive funding\" and 〜を助成する \"to subsidize\". 補助 is more general (\"support, aid\", also 補助金), while 援助 means rather help for people or countries in need. In everyday conversation, one mostly hears 助成金 or simply 補助金.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"政府から補助金が出る。","romaji":"Seifu kara hojokin ga deru.","german":"There are subsidies from the government.","furigana":"せいふ|ほじょきん|で"},{"japanese":"子どもが自転車に補助輪をつけて練習している。","romaji":"Kodomo ga jitensha ni hojorin o tsukete renshū shite iru.","german":"The child practices riding a bicycle with training wheels.","furigana":"こ|じてんしゃ|ほじょりん|れんしゅう"},{"japanese":"看護師が高齢の患者の食事を補助している。","romaji":"Kangoshi ga kōrei no kanja no shokuji o hojo shite iru.","german":"The caregiver helps the elderly patient eat.","furigana":"かんごし|こうれい|かんじゃ|しょくじ|ほじょ"}],"notes":"補助 means \"support, help\" in the sense of a supplement: one helps where someone or something is not sufficient alone. Financially it appears in 補助金 \"subsidy, grant\"; it also appears in many compounds meaning \"auxiliary, additional\": 補助輪 \"training wheels\", 補助席 \"fold-down seat\", 補助的な役割 \"supporting role\". As a suru-verb it is transitive: 〜を補助する. 助成 is narrower, limited to public funding, while 援助 emphasizes help for the needy.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"武道で正しく構える。","romaji":"Budō de tadashiku kamaeru.","german":"To take the correct stance in martial arts.","furigana":"ぶどう|ただ|かま"},{"japanese":"駅前に小さな店を構えた。","romaji":"Ekimae ni chiisana mise o kamaeta.","german":"He opened a small shop at the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|ちい|みせ|かま"},{"japanese":"カメラを構えて、鳥が飛び立つのを待った。","romaji":"Kamera o kamaete, tori ga tobitatsu no o matta.","german":"I waited for the bird to take off with my camera at the ready.","furigana":"かま|とり|と|た|ま"}],"notes":"構える has several uses: taking a posture or fighting stance (剣を構える, 正しく構える), holding something ready to fire or shoot (カメラを構える, 銃を構える), and setting up a business or house (店を構える, 家を構える). Intransitively it means \"to tense up, to become formal\": そんなに構えないで \"don't be so stiff\". The noun 構え is \"posture, stance\", 心構え \"mental attitude\". Do not confuse with 構う \"to care about, to mind\" (構わない).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"身だしなみを整えてから出かけた。","romaji":"Midashinami o totonoete kara dekaketa.","german":"I tidied up my appearance and then went out.","furigana":"み|ととの|で"},{"japanese":"試合の前に体調を整えておきたい。","romaji":"Shiai no mae ni taichō o totonoete okitai.","german":"Before the competition I want to get into top physical shape.","furigana":"しあい|まえ|たいちょう|ととの"},{"japanese":"深呼吸して息を整えてから話し始めた。","romaji":"Shinkokyū shite iki o totonoete kara hanashihajimeta.","german":"I took a deep breath, calmed my breathing, and then began to speak.","furigana":"しんこきゅう|いき|ととの|はな|はじ"}],"notes":"整える means \"to put in order, to bring into a clean, balanced state\": 身だしなみを整える, 髪を整える, 部屋を整える. It is just as common for conditions and circumstances: 体調を整える \"to get oneself in good shape\", 息を整える \"to calm one's breathing\", 環境を整える \"to create the conditions\", 準備を整える. The intransitive partner is 整う (準備が整う). The spelling 調える, by contrast, is preferred for \"to procure, to put together\" and for seasoning food (味を調える).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"旅行の支度を調える。","romaji":"Ryokō no shitaku o totonoeru.","german":"To make travel preparations.","furigana":"りょこう|したく|ととの"},{"japanese":"最後に塩とこしょうで味を調えてください。","romaji":"Saigo ni shio to koshō de aji o totonoete kudasai.","german":"Finally, season with salt and pepper.","furigana":"さいご|しお|あじ|ととの"},{"japanese":"結婚式の費用をなんとか調えることができた。","romaji":"Kekkonshiki no hiyō o nantoka totonoeru koto ga dekita.","german":"I somehow managed to raise the money for the wedding.","furigana":"けっこんしき|ひよう|ととの"}],"notes":"調える is a spelling of ととのえる that, according to official recommendation, stands for \"to procure and put together what is needed\" and \"to bring into the desired state\": 旅行の支度を調える, 費用を調える \"to raise the money\", 味を調える \"to season\", 縁談を調える \"to arrange a marriage\". For \"to put in order\" (髪, 服装, 体調) one writes 整える instead, which is much more common overall in daily life. The intransitive partner is 調う (準備が調う).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"王は国を平和に治めた。","romaji":"Ō wa kuni o heiwa ni osameta.","german":"The king ruled the country peacefully.","furigana":"おう|くに|へいわ|おさ"},{"japanese":"彼は長年この村を治めてきた村長だ。","romaji":"Kare wa naganen kono mura o osamete kita sonchō da.","german":"He is the village head who has governed this village for many years.","furigana":"かれ|ながねん|むら|おさ|そんちょう"},{"japanese":"母は大家族を上手に治めていた。","romaji":"Haha wa daikazoku o jōzu ni osamete ita.","german":"My mother managed the large family skillfully.","furigana":"はは|だいかぞく|じょうず|おさ"}],"notes":"治める means \"to rule, to administer, to govern\": 国を治める, 領地を治める, 家を治める \"to run a household\". It sounds elevated and is mostly said of rulers, governments or heads of families. The intransitive partner is 治まる (騒ぎが治まる, 痛みが治まる). Beware of the similarly read verbs: 収める \"to put away, to achieve\" (also recommended for \"to settle a dispute\": 争いを収める), 納める \"to pay, to deliver\", 修める \"to study, to master\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大会で良い成績を収めた。","romaji":"Taikai de yoi seiseki o osameta.","german":"He achieved good results at the tournament.","furigana":"たいかい|よ|せいせき|おさ"},{"japanese":"美しい景色を写真に収めた。","romaji":"Utsukushii keshiki o shashin ni osameta.","german":"I captured the beautiful scenery in a photo.","furigana":"うつく|けしき|しゃしん|おさ"},{"japanese":"書類はすべてこの棚に収めてください。","romaji":"Shorui wa subete kono tana ni osamete kudasai.","german":"Please put all the documents on this shelf.","furigana":"しょるい|たな|おさ"}],"notes":"収める basically means \"to bring something into its place\": 棚に収める \"to put on the shelf,\" 写真に収める \"to capture in a photo,\" 全集に収める \"to include in a collection.\" It is very common with success and results: 成功を収める, 勝利を収める, 好成績を収める \"to do well.\" \"To settle, calm down\" is also written this way: 騒ぎを収める. The intransitive partner is 収まる (箱に収まる, 騒ぎが収まる). Distinguish it from 納める \"to pay, deliver\" and 治める \"to rule.\"","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"税金をきちんと納める。","romaji":"Zeikin o kichinto osameru.","german":"I pay my taxes properly.","furigana":"ぜいきん|おさ"},{"japanese":"注文された部品を期日までに工場に納めた。","romaji":"Chūmon sareta buhin o kijitsu made ni kōjō ni osameta.","german":"I delivered the ordered parts to the factory by the due date.","furigana":"ちゅうもん|ぶひん|きじつ|こうじょう|おさ"},{"japanese":"古いお守りを神社に納めた。","romaji":"Furui omamori o jinja ni osameta.","german":"I dedicated an old amulet to the shrine.","furigana":"ふる|まも|じんじゃ|おさ"}],"notes":"納める mainly means \"to hand over money to an institution\" (税金を納める, 授業料を納める, 会費を納める) and \"to deliver goods\" (商品を納める, 期日までに納める). In addition, one brings offerings or old good-luck charms to a shrine: お守りを神社に納める. As a suffix it means \"to do for the last time, to wrap up\": 仕事納め. The intransitive partner is 納まる. In everyday speech people also say 払う for taxes, but 納める sounds more correct and official.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大学で法学を修めた。","romaji":"Daigaku de hōgaku o osameta.","german":"I studied law at university.","furigana":"だいがく|ほうがく|おさ"},{"japanese":"彼はフランスで料理の技術を修めた。","romaji":"Kare wa Furansu de ryōri no gijutsu o osameta.","german":"He mastered cooking techniques in France.","furigana":"かれ|りょうり|ぎじゅつ|おさ"},{"japanese":"学業を修めるまでは結婚しないと決めている。","romaji":"Gakugyō o osameru made wa kekkon shinai to kimete iru.","german":"I have decided not to marry until I finish my studies.","furigana":"がくぎょう|おさ|けっこん|き"}],"notes":"修める means \"to study a subject thoroughly and master it\" or \"to complete an education\": 学問を修める, 法学を修める, 学業を修める. It is more elevated than 勉強する or 学ぶ and stresses successful completion. A second, more literary use is 身を修める \"to perfect one's conduct\". Not to be confused with the identically read 治める \"to rule\", 収める \"to put away, to achieve\" and 納める \"to pay\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"常に改善に努めている。","romaji":"Tsune ni kaizen ni tsutomete iru.","german":"I am always working to make improvements.","furigana":"つね|かいぜん|つと"},{"japanese":"彼女は涙をこらえて、笑顔でいようと努めた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa namida o koraete, egao de iyō to tsutometa.","german":"Holding back her tears, she tried to keep smiling.","furigana":"かのじょ|なみだ|えがお|つと"},{"japanese":"当店ではお客様の個人情報の保護に努めております。","romaji":"Tōten de wa okyakusama no kojin jōhō no hogo ni tsutomete orimasu.","german":"Our store strives to protect our customers' personal information.","furigana":"とうてん|きゃくさま|こじんじょうほう|ほご|つと"}],"notes":"努める means \"to make every effort to do something\". The goal takes に (改善に努める, 安全の確保に努める) or comes as a clause with 〜よう(に)努める or 〜ようと努める. It is more formal than 頑張る or 努力する and typical of company and official language: 〜に努めております. Not to be confused with 務める \"to serve in a post\" (司会を務める) and 勤める \"to be employed\" (会社に勤める).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"銀行に勤めている。","romaji":"Ginkō ni tsutomete iru.","german":"I work for a bank.","furigana":"ぎんこう|つと"},{"japanese":"姉は来月から病院に勤め始めます。","romaji":"Ane wa raigetsu kara byōin ni tsutomehajimemasu.","german":"My older sister starts working at a hospital next month.","furigana":"あね|らいげつ|びょういん|つと|はじ"}],"notes":"勤める describes an employment relationship. The company or institution stands before に in the usual construction: 銀行に勤める means to work for a bank. 勤めている describes existing employment, while 勤め始める names the start. It is an ichidan verb: つとめます and つとめて. 努める, by contrast, means to make an effort.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"司会を務めることになった。","romaji":"Shikai o tsutomeru koto ni natta.","german":"I was asked to be the master of ceremonies.","furigana":"しかい|つと"},{"japanese":"彼女は映画で初めて主役を務めた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa eiga de hajimete shuyaku o tsutometa.","german":"She played the lead role in a film for the first time.","furigana":"かのじょ|えいが|はじ|しゅやく|つと"},{"japanese":"父は十年間、町内会の会長を務めている。","romaji":"Chichi wa jūnenkan, chōnaikai no kaichō o tsutomete iru.","german":"My father has served as head of the neighborhood association for ten years.","furigana":"ちち|じゅうねんかん|ちょうないかい|かいちょう|つと"}],"notes":"務める means \"to take on or fill a role or post\" and takes を: 司会を務める \"to host\", 議長を務める, 主役を務める \"to play the lead role\", 通訳を務める. It stresses the task, not the workplace: for \"to work at a company\", say 会社に勤める. 努める, in turn, means \"to strive\". The noun 務め means \"duty, task\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は偉大なことを成し遂げた。","romaji":"Kare wa idai na koto o nashitogeta.","german":"He accomplished great things.","furigana":"かれ|いだい|な|と"},{"japanese":"チーム全員の力で、ついに優勝を成し遂げた。","romaji":"Chīmu zen'in no chikara de, tsui ni yūshō o nashitogeta.","german":"With the strength of the whole team, we finally won the championship.","furigana":"ぜんいん|ちから|ゆうしょう|な|と"},{"japanese":"一人では無理でも、仲間がいれば成し遂げられるはずだ。","romaji":"Hitori de wa muri demo, nakama ga ireba nashitogerareru hazu da.","german":"Even if it's impossible alone, with companions it should be possible to accomplish.","furigana":"ひとり|むり|なかま|な|と"}],"notes":"成し遂げる means \"to carry a great, difficult task through to success\": 偉業を成し遂げる, 改革を成し遂げる, 優勝を成し遂げる. It sounds weighty and praising; for everyday tasks, やり遂げる \"to see through to the end\" or 終える is more fitting. 達成する stresses reaching a set goal or number (目標を達成する). The potential form 成し遂げられる is common.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"会議は三時間に亘った。","romaji":"Kaigi wa sanjikan ni watatta.","german":"The meeting lasted three hours.","furigana":"かいぎ|さんじかん|わた"},{"japanese":"工事は二年にわたって続く予定だ。","romaji":"Kōji wa ninen ni watatte tsuzuku yotei da.","german":"The construction work is scheduled to continue for two years.","furigana":"こうじ|にねん|つづ|よてい"},{"japanese":"大雨の被害は県内の広い範囲に亘った。","romaji":"Ōame no higai wa kennai no hiroi han'i ni watatta.","german":"The damage from the heavy rain extended over a wide area of the prefecture.","furigana":"おおあめ|ひがい|けんない|ひろ|はんい|わた"}],"notes":"亘る means \"to extend (in time or space) over something\" and appears almost always in the pattern 〜にわたって / 〜にわたる: 三時間にわたって, 長期にわたる工事, 全国にわたる調査. The kanji spelling 亘る is rare; today this word is mostly written in kana (わたる). The identically read 渡る means \"to cross\" (橋を渡る). The expression is rather written and typical of news and reports.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5 (source: N5; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"水たまりを跨いで歩いた。","romaji":"Mizutamari o mataide aruita.","german":"I stepped over the puddle and walked on.","furigana":"みず|また|ある"},{"japanese":"二度とこの家の敷居をまたぐな！","romaji":"Nido to kono ie no shikii o matagu na!","german":"Don't you ever set foot in this house again!","furigana":"にど|いえ|しきい"},{"japanese":"県をまたいだ移動は控えるように言われた。","romaji":"Ken o mataida idō wa hikaeru yō ni iwareta.","german":"We were told to refrain from traveling across prefectures.","furigana":"けん|いどう|ひか|い"}],"notes":"跨ぐ means \"to step over something in one stride\": 水たまりを跨ぐ, 敷居を跨ぐ \"to cross the threshold\" (二度と敷居をまたぐな \"never darken my door again\"). Figuratively it means \"to extend across a boundary\": 県をまたぐ移動, 年をまたぐ \"to carry over the new year\", 川をまたぐ橋. It is mostly written in kana (またぐ). Unlike 越える, it stresses spanning from one side to the other.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"売上が目標を超えた。","romaji":"Uriage ga mokuhyō o koeta.","german":"Sales exceeded the target.","furigana":"うりあげ|もくひょう|こ"},{"japanese":"気温が三十五度を超える日が続いている。","romaji":"Kion ga sanjūgo-do o koeru hi ga tsuzuite iru.","german":"Days when the temperature exceeds thirty-five degrees continue.","furigana":"きおん|さんじゅうごど|こ|ひ|つづ"},{"japanese":"彼の歌は時代を超えて愛されている。","romaji":"Kare no uta wa jidai o koete aisarete iru.","german":"His songs are loved across the ages.","furigana":"かれ|うた|じだい|こ|あい"}],"notes":"超える is used for exceeding a number, limit or standard: 目標を超える, 三十五度を超える, 限界を超える, 想像を超える. \"To go beyond, transcend\" is also written this way: 時代を超える, 国境を超えた友情. For physically crossing mountains, rivers or borders, 越える is written instead (山を越える). 上回る is more factual and limited to numbers in comparison.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"山を越えて隣の村に行った。","romaji":"Yama o koete tonari no mura ni itta.","german":"I crossed the mountain and went to the neighboring village.","furigana":"やま|こ|となり|むら|い"},{"japanese":"渡り鳥は海を越えて南の国へ飛んでいく。","romaji":"Wataridori wa umi o koete minami no kuni e tonde iku.","german":"Migratory birds fly over the sea to the southern countries.","furigana":"わた|どり|うみ|こ|みなみ|くに|と"},{"japanese":"病状はようやく峠を越えたそうだ。","romaji":"Byōjō wa yōyaku tōge o koeta sō da.","german":"I hear that the patient's condition has finally passed the critical point.","furigana":"びょうじょう|とうげ|こ"}],"notes":"越える is the spatial crossing of something that lies in the way: 山を越える, 国境を越える, 海を越える. Figuratively it stands for getting through a critical phase: 峠を越える \"to be over the hump.\" For numbers and limits, 超える is written (三十度を超える). Related are 越す (年を越す \"to see in the new year\") and 乗り越える \"to overcome\" (困難を乗り越える).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"実績が予想を上回った。","romaji":"Jisseki ga yosō o uwamawatta.","german":"The results exceeded expectations.","furigana":"じっせき|よそう|うわまわ"},{"japanese":"今年の観光客数は去年を大きく上回っている。","romaji":"Kotoshi no kankōkyakusū wa kyonen o ōkiku uwamawatte iru.","german":"The number of tourists this year is clearly above last year's.","furigana":"ことし|かんこうきゃくすう|きょねん|おお|うわまわ"},{"japanese":"賛成が反対をわずかに上回り、案は可決された。","romaji":"Sansei ga hantai o wazuka ni uwamawari, an wa kaketsu sareta.","german":"The yes votes narrowly prevailed, and the motion was passed.","furigana":"さんせい|はんたい|うわまわ|あん|かけつ"}],"notes":"上回る means \"to be above something (numerically), to exceed\" and is used mainly with statistics, results and forecasts: 予想を上回る, 前年を上回る, 平均を大きく上回る. It is factual and typical of news and business reports. Its opposite is 下回る. 超える is broader and emphasizes crossing a limit, while 勝る/優る means qualitative superiority.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今年の売上は昨年を下回った。","romaji":"Kotoshi no uriage wa sakunen o shitamawatta.","german":"This year's sales fell below last year's.","furigana":"ことし|うりあげ|さくねん|したまわ"},{"japanese":"応募者の数が定員を下回ったため、追加で募集することになった。","romaji":"Ōbosha no kazu ga teiin o shitamawatta tame, tsuika de boshū suru koto ni natta.","german":"Because the number of applicants fell short of capacity, additional recruitment will be held.","furigana":"おうぼしゃ|かず|ていいん|したまわ|ついか|ぼしゅう"},{"japanese":"テストの点数が平均を下回ってしまった。","romaji":"Tesuto no tensū ga heikin o shitamawatte shimatta.","german":"My test score unfortunately fell below the average.","furigana":"てんすう|へいきん|したまわ"}],"notes":"下回る means \"to be below, to fall short of\" (in numbers): 前年を下回る, 平均を下回る, 予想を下回る. Like its counterpart 上回る, it is factual and typical of statistics, surveys and business news. For falling below a threshold on the stock market, one also hears 割る or 割り込む. The standard of comparison takes を.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"実力で他の選手に優っている。","romaji":"Jitsuryoku de hoka no senshu ni masatte iru.","german":"He is superior to the other players in skill.","furigana":"じつりょく|ほか|せんしゅ|まさ"},{"japanese":"健康に優る宝はない。","romaji":"Kenkō ni masaru takara wa nai.","german":"There is no greater treasure than health.","furigana":"けんこう|まさ|たから"},{"japanese":"この店のラーメンは、有名店に優るとも劣らない味だ。","romaji":"Kono mise no rāmen wa, yūmeiten ni masaru tomo otoranai aji da.","german":"The ramen here is every bit as good as that of famous shops.","furigana":"みせ|ゆうめいてん|まさ|おと|あじ"}],"notes":"優る means \"to be better than, to be superior to\" and appears in the pattern AはBに優る. The usual spelling is 勝る; 優る is a rarer variant. Fixed expressions are 〜に優るとも劣らない \"is not inferior to ...\" and 〜に優るものはない \"there is nothing better than ...\". Do not confuse with 優れる (すぐれる) \"to be outstanding\", which does not need a direct comparison with に. The tone is rather written.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は数学に秀でている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa sūgaku ni hiidete iru.","german":"She excels at mathematics.","furigana":"かのじょ|すうがく|ひい"},{"japanese":"一芸に秀でた人は、他の分野でも成功することが多い。","romaji":"Ichigei ni hiideta hito wa, hoka no bun'ya demo seikō suru koto ga ōi.","german":"People who excel in one skill are often successful in other fields too.","furigana":"いちげい|ひい|ひと|ほか|ぶんや|せいこう|おお"},{"japanese":"彼は語学に秀でており、五か国語を話せる。","romaji":"Kare wa gogaku ni hiidete ori, gokakokugo o hanaseru.","german":"He excels at languages and can speak five languages.","furigana":"かれ|ごがく|ひい|ご|こくご|はな"}],"notes":"秀でる means \"to stand out clearly in a field\" and takes に: 数学に秀でる, 語学に秀でる, 一芸に秀でる \"to excel in one thing\". It mostly appears as 秀でている or attributively as 秀でた. It sounds elevated and written; in conversation people say 優れている or 得意だ more often. Related is 抜きん出る, which stresses more the distance from others.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は同期の中で抜きん出ている。","romaji":"Kare wa dōki no naka de nukindete iru.","german":"He stands out among his peers.","furigana":"かれ|どうき|なか|ぬ|で"},{"japanese":"彼女の作品は、ほかの応募作より抜きん出ていた。","romaji":"Kanojo no sakuhin wa, hoka no ōbosaku yori nukindete ita.","german":"Her work stood out from the other entries.","furigana":"かのじょ|さくひん|おうぼさく|ぬ|で"},{"japanese":"この会社は技術力で業界の中でも抜きん出た存在だ。","romaji":"Kono kaisha wa gijutsuryoku de gyōkai no naka demo nukindeta sonzai da.","german":"This company is an outstanding presence in its industry thanks to its technological strength.","furigana":"かいしゃ|ぎじゅつりょく|ぎょうかい|なか|ぬ|で|そんざい"}],"notes":"抜きん出る means \"to stand clearly apart from others, to stand out\", literally something like \"to step forward pulled out\". Typical are 〜の中で抜きん出る, 〜より抜きん出る and attributively 抜きん出た才能 / 抜きん出た存在. It stresses a large gap to the competition, like the phrase 群を抜く. It is also written 抜きんでる; the tone is rather written, and in conversation people often say ずば抜けている.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"目の前に高い山がそびえていた。","romaji":"Me no mae ni takai yama ga sobiete ita.","german":"A tall mountain towered before my eyes.","furigana":"め|まえ|たか|やま"},{"japanese":"駅前には新しいタワーマンションがそびえ立っている。","romaji":"Ekimae ni wa atarashii tawā manshon ga sobietatte iru.","german":"A new high-rise apartment building towers in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|あたら|た"},{"japanese":"雲の上にそびえる富士山の姿は本当に美しかった。","romaji":"Kumo no ue ni sobieru Fujisan no sugata wa hontō ni utsukushikatta.","german":"The sight of Fuji rising above the clouds was truly beautiful.","furigana":"くも|うえ|ふじさん|すがた|ほんとう|うつく"}],"notes":"そびえる describes how tall mountains or buildings rise majestically into the sky. It is almost always used in a state: 〜がそびえている, attributively そびえる山, or as the compound そびえ立つ. The place is marked with に: 町の中心に高いビルがそびえる. The word sounds descriptive and literary and is usually written in kana, since the kanji 聳 is not among the Jōyō kanji.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"超高層ビルが聳えている。","romaji":"Chōkōsō biru ga sobiete iru.","german":"A skyscraper towers up.","furigana":"ちょうこうそう|そび"},{"japanese":"城は町を見下ろす丘の上に聳えている。","romaji":"Shiro wa machi o miorosu oka no ue ni sobiete iru.","german":"The castle stands on a hill above the town.","furigana":"しろ|まち|みお|おか|うえ|そび"},{"japanese":"遠くにアルプスの山々が聳えて見える。","romaji":"Tōku ni Arupusu no yamayama ga sobiete mieru.","german":"In the distance you can see the peaks of the Alps rising up.","furigana":"とお|やまやま|そび|み"}],"notes":"聳える is the kanji spelling of そびえる \"to tower, to rise high\", said of mountains, towers and high-rise buildings. Since 聳 is not a Jōyō kanji, this form is seen mainly in literature and elevated texts; in daily life it is usually written そびえる. Grammatically it is almost always used in a state (聳えている) or attributively (聳える塔), often also as 聳え立つ. The place is marked with に.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日が西に傾いてきた。","romaji":"Hi ga nishi ni katamuite kita.","german":"The sun has begun to sink toward the west.","furigana":"ひ|にし|かたむ"},{"japanese":"地震のあと、古い家が少し傾いてしまった。","romaji":"Jishin no ato, furui ie ga sukoshi katamuite shimatta.","german":"After the earthquake, the old house tilts slightly.","furigana":"じしん|ふる|いえ|すこ|かたむ"},{"japanese":"父の会社は不景気で経営が傾いている。","romaji":"Chichi no kaisha wa fukeiki de keiei ga katamuite iru.","german":"My father's company is faltering because of the recession.","furigana":"ちち|かいしゃ|ふけいき|けいえい|かたむ"}],"notes":"傾く first means \"to lean, to stand crooked\" (家が傾く, 船が傾く). Of the sun and moon one says 日が傾く \"the sun is sinking\". Figuratively it means \"to fall into crisis\" (会社が傾く, 経営が傾く) and \"to tend toward an opinion\" (賛成に傾く, 気持ちが傾く). The transitive counterpart is 傾ける: 首を傾ける, 耳を傾ける \"to listen attentively\". 偏る, by contrast, stresses a one-sided distribution.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"栄養が偏った食事をしている。","romaji":"Eiyō ga katayotta shokuji o shite iru.","german":"I am eating an unbalanced diet.","furigana":"えいよう|かたよ|しょくじ"},{"japanese":"荷物が片側に偏らないように積んでください。","romaji":"Nimotsu ga katagawa ni katayoranai yō ni tsunde kudasai.","german":"Please load the luggage so that it does not lean to one side.","furigana":"にもつ|かたがわ|かたよ|つ"},{"japanese":"ネットのニュースだけでは、情報が偏りがちだ。","romaji":"Netto no nyūsu dake de wa, jōhō ga katayorigachi da.","german":"If you read only online news, your information tends to be one-sided.","furigana":"じょうほう|かたよ"}],"notes":"偏る means \"to be distributed one-sidedly, to concentrate too much on one side\". Very common are 栄養が偏る \"to eat a one-sided diet\", 偏った考え方 \"biased way of thinking\" and 〜に偏る \"to be concentrated in ...\" (人口が都市に偏る). The noun is 偏り. Unlike 傾く, which describes a visible tilt, 偏る usually concerns unequal distribution or lack of neutrality. The opposite is バランスがとれている.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"話が本題から逸れてしまった。","romaji":"Hanashi ga hondai kara sorete shimatta.","german":"The conversation strayed from the main topic.","furigana":"はなし|ほんだい|そ"},{"japanese":"ボールはゴールの右に逸れた。","romaji":"Bōru wa gōru no migi ni soreta.","german":"The ball veered off to the right of the goal.","furigana":"みぎ|そ"},{"japanese":"台風が東に逸れたので、予定通り出発できた。","romaji":"Taifū ga higashi ni soreta node, yotei dōri shuppatsu dekita.","german":"The typhoon veered east, so we were able to depart as planned.","furigana":"たいふう|ひがし|そ|よていどお|しゅっぱつ"}],"notes":"逸れる means \"to deviate from the intended direction\": 話が本題から逸れる \"to stray from the topic\", 弾が逸れる \"to miss the target\", 台風が逸れる \"the typhoon turns away\", 道から逸れる. It is mostly written in kana (それる). The transitive counterpart is 逸らす: 目を逸らす \"to look away\", 話を逸らす \"to change the subject\". 外れる, by contrast, stresses missing the mark or coming off, not the movement to the side.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"天気予報が外れた。","romaji":"Tenki yohō ga hazureta.","german":"The weather forecast was wrong.","furigana":"てんきよほう|はず"},{"japanese":"シャツのボタンが一つ外れているよ。","romaji":"Shatsu no botan ga hitotsu hazurete iru yo.","german":"One of the buttons on your shirt is undone.","furigana":"ひと|はず"},{"japanese":"けがのため、彼は代表メンバーから外れた。","romaji":"Kega no tame, kare wa daihyō menbā kara hazureta.","german":"Because of an injury, he was dropped from the national team.","furigana":"かれ|だいひょう|はず"}],"notes":"外れる has three main meanings: \"to come off, to no longer be in place\" (ボタンが外れる, 眼鏡が外れる), \"to miss, not to come true\" (予想が外れる, 天気予報が外れる, 宝くじが外れる \"to draw a blank\") and \"to be excluded, to deviate\" (メンバーから外れる, 道理に外れる). The transitive counterpart is 外す (眼鏡を外す, 席を外す). The opposite of 外れる in the sense of \"to be wrong\" is 当たる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"計画が狂ってしまった。","romaji":"Keikaku ga kurutte shimatta.","german":"The plan went awry.","furigana":"けいかく|くる"},{"japanese":"この時計は少し狂っていて、五分進んでいる。","romaji":"Kono tokei wa sukoshi kurutte ite, gofun susunde iru.","german":"This clock is a bit off and is five minutes fast.","furigana":"とけい|すこ|くる|ごふんすす"},{"japanese":"彼は若いころ、ギャンブルに狂って財産を失った。","romaji":"Kare wa wakai koro, gyanburu ni kurutte zaisan o ushinatta.","german":"When he was young, he went wild over gambling and lost his fortune.","furigana":"かれ|わか|くる|ざいさん|うしな"}],"notes":"狂う originally means \"to lose one's mind\" (気が狂う), but in everyday speech it is mainly used for things that get out of sync: 時計が狂う \"the clock is running wrong,\" 予定が狂う / 計画が狂う \"the plan gets thrown off,\" 調子が狂う \"to get out of rhythm.\" With に it means \"to be crazy about something\": ギャンブルに狂う. The transitive counterpart is 狂わせる. Applied to people, 狂う is drastic; be careful when using it in conversation about persons.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"生活のリズムが乱れている。","romaji":"Seikatsu no rizumu ga midarete iru.","german":"My daily rhythm is out of order.","furigana":"せいかつ|みだ"},{"japanese":"大雪の影響で電車のダイヤが乱れている。","romaji":"Ōyuki no eikyō de densha no daiya ga midarete iru.","german":"Because of the heavy snowfall, train service was disrupted.","furigana":"おおゆき|えいきょう|でんしゃ|みだ"},{"japanese":"風で髪が乱れてしまった。","romaji":"Kaze de kami ga midarete shimatta.","german":"The wind messed up my hair.","furigana":"かぜ|かみ|みだ"}],"notes":"乱れる means \"to fall out of order\", both outwardly and inwardly: 髪が乱れる \"the hair is tousled\", 列が乱れる, 生活のリズムが乱れる, ダイヤが乱れる \"the train timetable is disrupted\", 心が乱れる \"to be inwardly agitated\". The transitive counterpart is 乱す (秩序を乱す \"to disturb the order\"). 狂う emphasizes more the malfunctioning of a mechanism or plan, 荒れる the wild and rough.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"台風で海が荒れている。","romaji":"Taifū de umi ga arete iru.","german":"The sea is rough because of the typhoon.","furigana":"たいふう|うみ|あ"},{"japanese":"冬は空気が乾燥して、手が荒れやすい。","romaji":"Fuyu wa kūki ga kansō shite, te ga areyasui.","german":"In winter the air is dry, and hands easily become chapped.","furigana":"ふゆ|くうき|かんそう|て|あ"},{"japanese":"長い間だれも住んでいないので、庭がすっかり荒れている。","romaji":"Nagai aida dare mo sunde inai node, niwa ga sukkari arete iru.","german":"No one has lived there for a long time, so the garden has become completely overgrown.","furigana":"なが|あいだ|す|にわ|あ"}],"notes":"荒れる means \"to become rough, wild\" and has several set uses: weather and sea (海が荒れる, 天気が荒れる), neglected places (荒れた庭 \"overgrown garden\"), skin (手が荒れる, 肌荒れ \"rough or chapped skin\") and people or situations that get out of control (クラスが荒れる, 会議が荒れる). The transitive counterpart is 荒らす \"to ravage, to rummage through\" (泥棒が部屋を荒らす). 乱れる, by contrast, is more \"to get into disorder\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"痛みで呻いている。","romaji":"Itami de umeite iru.","german":"He is groaning in pain.","furigana":"いた|うめ"},{"japanese":"隣の部屋から誰かがうめく声が聞こえてきた。","romaji":"Tonari no heya kara dareka ga umeku koe ga kikoete kita.","german":"I heard someone groaning from the next room.","furigana":"となり|へや|だれ|こえ|き"},{"japanese":"事故現場では、けが人が地面に倒れてうめいていた。","romaji":"Jiko genba de wa, keganin ga jimen ni taorete umeite ita.","german":"At the accident scene, injured people lay on the ground, groaning.","furigana":"じこげんば|にん|じめん|たお"}],"notes":"呻く means \"to groan or moan in pain or torment\". Typical are 痛みに呻く / 痛みで呻く and the noun うめき声 \"groan\". The word is mostly written in kana (うめく) because the kanji 呻 is not a Jōyō kanji. うなる describes more a deep rumble or growl (also of animals and engines), and 叫ぶ a loud scream. The tone is descriptive, often in narratives and reports.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"緊張で声がふるえている。","romaji":"Kinchō de koe ga furuete iru.","german":"My voice is trembling from nervousness.","furigana":"きんちょう|こえ"},{"japanese":"寒さで体がふるえて止まらなかった。","romaji":"Samusa de karada ga furuete tomaranakatta.","german":"My body shivered from the cold and wouldn't stop.","furigana":"さむ|からだ|と"},{"japanese":"合格の知らせを見たとき、うれしくて手がふるえた。","romaji":"Gōkaku no shirase o mita toki, ureshikute te ga furueta.","german":"When I saw the news that I had passed, my hands trembled with joy.","furigana":"ごうかく|し|み|て"}],"notes":"ふるえる (kanji 震える) means \"to tremble, shake.\" The cause takes で: 寒さで震える, 緊張で声が震える, 怒りで手が震える. It describes involuntary, fine trembling of the body, voice or hands, but also the shaking of objects (窓ガラスが震える). 揺れる, by contrast, means a larger swaying (地震で建物が揺れる). The transitive counterpart is 震わせる (声を震わせる). Written in kana it looks softer; there is no difference in meaning.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"犬は雷に怯えている。","romaji":"Inu wa kaminari ni obiete iru.","german":"The dog is afraid of the thunder.","furigana":"いぬ|かみなり|おび"},{"japanese":"住民は毎晩のように続く地震に怯えている。","romaji":"Jūmin wa maiban no yō ni tsuzuku jishin ni obiete iru.","german":"The residents live in fear of the earthquakes that occur almost every night.","furigana":"じゅうみん|まいばん|つづ|じしん|おび"},{"japanese":"その子は怯えた目で私を見ていた。","romaji":"Sono ko wa obieta me de watashi o mite ita.","german":"The child looked at me with frightened eyes.","furigana":"こ|おび|め|わたし|み"}],"notes":"怯える means \"to be frightened, to be (continually) afraid\"; the trigger is marked with に: 雷に怯える, 将来への不安に怯える. The attributive form 怯えた目 / 怯えた顔 is common. Unlike 怖がる, which describes visible fearful behavior, and 恐れる, which means \"to fear\" in a factual-formal way, 怯える emphasizes an inner, often lasting trembling with fear. The word is also written in kana (おびえる).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"暗闇におびえる子供をなだめた。","romaji":"Kurayami ni obieru kodomo o nadameta.","german":"I calmed the child who was afraid of the dark.","furigana":"くらやみ|こども"},{"japanese":"彼はいつ解雇されるかとおびえながら働いていた。","romaji":"Kare wa itsu kaiko sareru ka to obienagara hataraite ita.","german":"He worked in constant fear of being fired at any moment.","furigana":"かれ|かいこ|はたら"},{"japanese":"物音におびえて、猫がベッドの下に隠れた。","romaji":"Monooto ni obiete, neko ga beddo no shita ni kakureta.","german":"Startled by the noise, the cat hid under the bed.","furigana":"ものおと|ねこ|した|かく"}],"notes":"おびえる is the kana spelling of 怯える \"to be scared, to be frightened\" and is often written this way in daily life. The trigger is marked with に (暗闇におびえる, 物音におびえる); with 〜かとおびえる one expresses what one constantly fears. 怖がる describes more the visible showing of fear, 恐れる the formal \"to fear\". Related is びくびくする \"to flinch fearfully\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"上司の前で萎縮してしまう。","romaji":"Jōshi no mae de ishuku shite shimau.","german":"I get intimidated in front of my boss.","furigana":"じょうし|まえ|いしゅく"},{"japanese":"厳しく叱りすぎると、子どもは萎縮してしまう。","romaji":"Kibishiku shikarisugiru to, kodomo wa ishuku shite shimau.","german":"If you scold children too harshly, they become intimidated.","furigana":"きび|しか|こ|いしゅく"},{"japanese":"使わない筋肉は少しずつ萎縮していく。","romaji":"Tsukawanai kinniku wa sukoshi zutsu ishuku shite iku.","german":"Muscles that aren't used gradually atrophy.","furigana":"つか|きんにく|すこ|いしゅく"}],"notes":"萎縮する literally means \"to wither, to shrink\" and, medically, \"to atrophy\" (筋肉が萎縮する). In everyday life it is used mainly figuratively: one is intimidated by pressure or authority and no longer dares to do anything (上司の前で萎縮する, 失敗を恐れて萎縮する). The economy or activities can also \"slacken\" (消費が萎縮する). Newspapers often write 委縮; 縮こまる is the physical, more colloquial cowering.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"厳しい批判を受けて委縮した。","romaji":"Kibishii hihan o ukete ishuku shita.","german":"I was intimidated by the harsh criticism.","furigana":"きび|ひはん|う|いしゅく"},{"japanese":"規制が強すぎると、企業の活動が委縮するおそれがある。","romaji":"Kisei ga tsuyosugiru to, kigyō no katsudō ga ishuku suru osore ga aru.","german":"If regulations are too strong, there is a risk that corporate activity will be stifled.","furigana":"きせい|つよ|きぎょう|かつどう|いしゅく"},{"japanese":"失敗を恐れて委縮せず、思い切ってやってみなさい。","romaji":"Shippai o osorete ishuku sezu, omoikitte yatte minasai.","german":"Don't be intimidated by the fear of failure; just go for it.","furigana":"しっぱい|おそ|いしゅく|おも|き"}],"notes":"委縮する is a substitute spelling of 萎縮する, used mainly by newspapers because 萎 was for a long time not among the Jōyō kanji. Meaning and use are the same: \"to shrink\" and, figuratively, \"to be intimidated, to slacken\". Typical are 批判を受けて委縮する, 経済活動が委縮する and the admonition 委縮せずに \"without being intimidated\". In books and technical texts, 萎縮 predominates again today.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"寒くて布団の中で縮こまっている。","romaji":"Samukute futon no naka de chijikomatte iru.","german":"It's cold, so I'm huddled up in my futon.","furigana":"さむ|ふとん|なか|ちぢ"},{"japanese":"怒鳴られた新人は、部屋の隅で縮こまっていた。","romaji":"Donarareta shinjin wa, heya no sumi de chijikomatte ita.","german":"The newcomer who had been yelled at was cowering in a corner of the room.","furigana":"どな|しんじん|へや|すみ|ちぢ"},{"japanese":"面接では縮こまらずに、堂々と話しましょう。","romaji":"Mensetsu de wa chijikomarazu ni, dōdō to hanashimashō.","german":"In the interview, don't shrink back; speak confidently.","furigana":"めんせつ|ちぢ|どうどう|はな"}],"notes":"縮こまる means \"to make oneself small, to cower\", from cold (寒さで縮こまる) or from fear and shyness (隅で縮こまる). It describes a visible bodily posture and, figuratively, also timid behavior: 縮こまらずに堂々と. 縮む, by contrast, means \"to shrink\" (セーターが縮む), and 丸まる \"to curl up round\". The more literary 萎縮する describes more the inner state.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"猫がソファの上で丸まっている。","romaji":"Neko ga sofa no ue de marumatte iru.","german":"The cat is curled up on the sofa.","furigana":"ねこ|うえ|まる"},{"japanese":"長時間パソコンを使っていると、背中が丸まってしまう。","romaji":"Chōjikan pasokon o tsukatte iru to, senaka ga marumatte shimau.","german":"When I use the computer for a long time, my back gets rounded.","furigana":"ちょうじかん|つか|せなか|まる"},{"japanese":"古いポスターの端が丸まって、うまく貼れない。","romaji":"Furui posutā no hashi ga marumatte, umaku harenai.","german":"The edges of the old poster are curling, so I can't hang it up properly.","furigana":"ふる|はし|まる|は"}],"notes":"丸まる means \"to become round, to curl up\": 猫が丸まって寝る, 背中が丸まる \"to get a hunched back\", 紙の端が丸まる \"to curl\". The transitive counterpart is 丸める (紙を丸める \"to crumple paper\", 背中を丸める). Not to be confused with the identically read adverb 丸々 (丸々一日 \"a whole day\", 丸々と太った \"plump\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"低い入口で屈んで通った。","romaji":"Hikui iriguchi de kagande tōtta.","german":"I stooped to get through the low entrance.","furigana":"ひく|いりぐち|かが|とお"},{"japanese":"落ちた財布を拾うためにかがんだ。","romaji":"Ochita saifu o hirou tame ni kaganda.","german":"I bent down to pick up the dropped wallet.","furigana":"お|さいふ|ひろ"},{"japanese":"腰が痛くて、かがむのがつらい。","romaji":"Koshi ga itakute, kagamu no ga tsurai.","german":"My back hurts, so bending down is painful.","furigana":"こし|いた"}],"notes":"屈む means \"to bend down, to lean forward, to crouch\" and is mostly written in kana (かがむ). Typical: 屈んで拾う \"to bend down and pick up\", 低い入口で屈む. しゃがむ is crouching fully with bent knees, whereas かがむ stresses more the bending forward of the upper body. The transitive counterpart is 屈める (身を屈める, 腰を屈める).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"恥ずかしくて俯いてしまった。","romaji":"Hazukashikute utsumuite shimatta.","german":"I was so embarrassed that I hung my head.","furigana":"は|うつむ"},{"japanese":"彼女は先生に叱られて、黙ってうつむいていた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa sensei ni shikararete, damatte utsumuite ita.","german":"She was scolded by the teacher and stood there silently with her head bowed.","furigana":"かのじょ|せんせい|しか|だま"},{"japanese":"うつむいて歩いていたら、電柱にぶつかってしまった。","romaji":"Utsumuite aruite itara, denchū ni butsukatte shimatta.","german":"I was walking with my eyes on the ground and bumped into a utility pole.","furigana":"ある|でんちゅう"}],"notes":"俯く (usually written in kana, うつむく) means \"to lower one's head, to look down\", often out of shame, sadness, guilt or shyness. The verb is intransitive and takes no object; the everyday synonym is 下を向く, and the opposite is 顔を上げる or 上を向く. Common expressions are うつむいたまま \"with one's head bowed\" and うつむき加減に \"head slightly lowered\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"試合に負けてうなだれている。","romaji":"Shiai ni makete unadarete iru.","german":"He hangs his head after losing the match.","furigana":"しあい|ま"},{"japanese":"先生に叱られて、彼は黙ってうなだれていた。","romaji":"Sensei ni shikararete, kare wa damatte unadarete ita.","german":"Scolded by the teacher, he stood there in silence with his head bowed.","furigana":"せんせい|しか|かれ|だま"},{"japanese":"不合格の結果を見て、彼女はがっくりとうなだれた。","romaji":"Fugōkaku no kekka o mite, kanojo wa gakkuri to unadareta.","german":"When she saw the result that she had failed, she hung her head in disappointment.","furigana":"ふごうかく|けっか|み|かのじょ"}],"notes":"うなだれる means \"to hang one's head\" - out of disappointment, shame or dejection. Typical: 負けてうなだれる, 叱られてうなだれる, がっくりとうなだれる. The kanji spelling 項垂れる is rare. うつむく means, more neutrally, \"to look down\" (also out of shyness), whereas うなだれる always expresses dejection. It is a descriptive word, common in narrative writing and sports reports.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"突然の出来事に立ちすくんだ。","romaji":"Totsuzen no dekigoto ni tachisukunda.","german":"I froze in the face of the sudden event.","furigana":"とつぜん|できごと|た"},{"japanese":"目の前に大きな熊が現れ、恐怖で立ちすくんでしまった。","romaji":"Me no mae ni ōkina kuma ga araware, kyōfu de tachisukunde shimatta.","german":"A big bear suddenly appeared in front of me, and I stood frozen with fear.","furigana":"め|まえ|おお|くま|あらわ|きょうふ|た"},{"japanese":"火事で焼けた自宅を前に、彼はただ立ちすくむしかなかった。","romaji":"Kaji de yaketa jitaku o mae ni, kare wa tada tachisukumu shika nakatta.","german":"Standing before his house burned down in the fire, all he could do was stand there, stunned.","furigana":"かじ|や|じたく|まえ|かれ|た"}],"notes":"立ちすくむ means \"to stand rooted to the spot out of shock or bewilderment\". The cause is marked with に or で: 突然の出来事に立ちすくむ, 恐怖で立ちすくむ; その場に立ちすくむ is also common. It is composed of 立つ and すくむ \"to freeze\" (足がすくむ \"one's legs give way\"). Unlike たたずむ, which describes standing still calmly and voluntarily, 立ちすくむ is always involuntary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"湖のほとりにたたずんでいた。","romaji":"Mizuumi no hotori ni tatazunde ita.","german":"I stood lingering at the lakeshore.","furigana":"みずうみ"},{"japanese":"駅の前で、一人の女性が雨の中にたたずんでいた。","romaji":"Eki no mae de, hitori no josei ga ame no naka ni tatazunde ita.","german":"In front of the station, a woman stood quietly in the rain.","furigana":"えき|まえ|ひとり|じょせい|あめ|なか"},{"japanese":"森の奥に、小さな神社がひっそりとたたずんでいる。","romaji":"Mori no oku ni, chiisana jinja ga hissori to tatazunde iru.","german":"Deep in the forest, a small shrine stands quietly, hidden from view.","furigana":"もり|おく|ちい|じんじゃ"}],"notes":"たたずむ (kanji 佇む) means \"to stand quietly in one place and linger\", often pensively or melancholically. The place is marked with に: 湖のほとりにたたずむ, 雨の中にたたずむ. Buildings or trees that stand calmly and inconspicuously in a place can also \"たたずむ\": 森の奥にたたずむ寺. The word is literary and descriptive. 立ち止まる only means \"to stop (walking)\", and 立ちすくむ \"to freeze in shock\". The noun たたずまい means \"aura, appearance\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子供たちが野原を駆けている。","romaji":"Kodomotachi ga nohara o kakete iru.","german":"The children are running across the field.","furigana":"こども|のはら|か"},{"japanese":"馬が草原を駆けていく姿は美しい。","romaji":"Uma ga sōgen o kakete iku sugata wa utsukushii.","german":"The sight of a horse galloping across the grassland is beautiful.","furigana":"うま|そうげん|か|すがた|うつく"},{"japanese":"彼は階段を一気に駆け上がった。","romaji":"Kare wa kaidan o ikki ni kakeagatta.","german":"He ran up the stairs in one go.","furigana":"かれ|かいだん|いっき|か|あ"}],"notes":"駆ける means \"to run, to gallop\", but sounds more dashing and literary than the everyday 走る. It is used of people, horses and animals running across an expanse (野原を駆ける, 草原を駆ける); the route is marked with を. It is very productive in compounds: 駆け足, 駆け上がる \"to run up\", 駆け寄る, 駆け付ける, 駆け回る, 駆け抜ける. There is no transitive counterpart.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"倒れた人に駆け寄った。","romaji":"Taoreta hito ni kakeyotta.","german":"I ran over to the person who had collapsed.","furigana":"たお|ひと|か|よ"},{"japanese":"子どもは母親の姿を見つけると、うれしそうに駆け寄った。","romaji":"Kodomo wa hahaoya no sugata o mitsukeru to, ureshisō ni kakeyotta.","german":"When the child spotted its mother, it ran up to her happily.","furigana":"こ|ははおや|すがた|み|か|よ"},{"japanese":"ゴールした選手に、仲間たちが一斉に駆け寄った。","romaji":"Gōru shita senshu ni, nakamatachi ga issei ni kakeyotta.","german":"The teammates all rushed up at once to the athlete who had reached the finish line.","furigana":"せんしゅ|なかま|いっせい|か|よ"}],"notes":"駆け寄る means \"to run up to someone or something\", i.e. to run quickly over a short distance toward a nearby target. The target is marked with に or 〜のそばに: 倒れた人に駆け寄る, 母親に駆け寄る. 駆け付ける, by contrast, stresses hurrying to a place from farther away in response to news (現場に駆け付ける). The verb often shows joy, concern or readiness to help.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"事故の知らせを聞いて駆け付けた。","romaji":"Jiko no shirase o kiite kaketsuketa.","german":"When I heard the news of the accident, I rushed over.","furigana":"じこ|し|き|か|つ"},{"japanese":"通報を受けて、警察がすぐに現場に駆け付けた。","romaji":"Tsūhō o ukete, keisatsu ga sugu ni genba ni kaketsuketa.","german":"After receiving the report, the police rushed to the scene immediately.","furigana":"つうほう|う|けいさつ|げんば|か|つ"},{"japanese":"遠くから友人が結婚式に駆け付けてくれた。","romaji":"Tōku kara yūjin ga kekkonshiki ni kaketsukete kureta.","german":"A friend came all the way from far away to our wedding.","furigana":"とお|ゆうじん|けっこんしき|か|つ"}],"notes":"駆け付ける means \"to come hurriedly to a place (in response to news)\". The destination is marked with に or へ: 現場に駆け付ける, 病院に駆け付ける. It stresses urgency or readiness to help, so it suits police, firefighters and relatives, but also friends who travel specially (結婚式に駆け付けてくれた). It is often written 駆けつける. 駆け寄る, by contrast, is running up to something over a short distance.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"蝶が花の周りを飛び回っている。","romaji":"Chō ga hana no mawari o tobimawatte iru.","german":"A butterfly is flying around the flowers.","furigana":"ちょう|はな|まわ|と|まわ"},{"japanese":"子どもたちが公園を元気に飛び回っている。","romaji":"Kodomotachi ga kōen o genki ni tobimawatte iru.","german":"The children are running around the park energetically.","furigana":"こ|こうえん|げんき|と|まわ"},{"japanese":"彼は営業で毎日あちこち飛び回っている。","romaji":"Kare wa eigyō de mainichi achikochi tobimawatte iru.","german":"He is rushing around all over the place every day for sales work.","furigana":"かれ|えいぎょう|まいにち|と|まわ"}],"notes":"飛び回る literally means \"to fly around\" (蝶が飛び回る), but also \"to jump around, to romp\" (子どもが飛び回る) and, figuratively, \"to travel a lot, to be constantly on the go\": 営業で飛び回る, 世界中を飛び回る. The area covered is marked with を. Related formations are 走り回る \"to run around\" and 駆け回る. The tone is neutral and everyday.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どん底から這い上がった。","romaji":"Donzoko kara haiagatta.","german":"I clawed my way up from rock bottom.","furigana":"ぞこ|は|あ"},{"japanese":"穴に落ちた子犬が、自分で這い上がってきた。","romaji":"Ana ni ochita koinu ga, jibun de haiagatte kita.","german":"The puppy that had fallen into a hole climbed back out by itself.","furigana":"あな|お|こいぬ|じぶん|は|あ"},{"japanese":"最下位から這い上がり、ついに優勝を果たした。","romaji":"Saikai kara haiagari, tsui ni yūshō o hatashita.","german":"Climbing up from last place, the team finally won the championship.","furigana":"さいかい|は|あ|ゆうしょう|は"}],"notes":"這い上がる literally means \"to crawl up, to pull oneself up\" (穴から這い上がる). At least as common is the figurative meaning \"to work one's way back up from a bad situation\": どん底から這い上がる, 最下位から這い上がる. The starting point is marked with から. It is also written はい上がる. 這う alone means \"to crawl, to creep\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"赤ちゃんが床を這い回っている。","romaji":"Akachan ga yuka o haimawatte iru.","german":"The baby is crawling around on the floor.","furigana":"あか|ゆか|は|まわ"},{"japanese":"台所をゴキブリが這い回っていて、ぞっとした。","romaji":"Daidokoro o gokiburi ga haimawatte ite, zotto shita.","german":"Cockroaches were crawling around in the kitchen, and it made my skin crawl.","furigana":"だいどころ|は|まわ"},{"japanese":"落としたコンタクトレンズを探して、床を這い回った。","romaji":"Otoshita kontakuto renzu o sagashite, yuka o haimawatta.","german":"I crawled around on the floor looking for the contact lens I had dropped.","furigana":"お|さが|ゆか|は|まわ"}],"notes":"這い回る means \"to crawl around, to creep about\" and is used for babies, insects and animals, but also for people searching for something on the floor. The surface is marked with を: 床を這い回る. With insects such as cockroaches it often carries a sense of disgust. The base verb is 這う \"to crawl\"; the kana spelling はい回る is also common.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"腹痛でのたうち回った。","romaji":"Fukutsū de notauchimawatta.","german":"I writhed in stomach pain.","furigana":"ふくつう|まわ"},{"japanese":"病室では、患者が激しい痛みにのたうつ姿を何度も見てきた。","romaji":"Byōshitsu de wa, kanja ga hageshii itami ni notautsu sugata o nando mo mite kita.","german":"In the hospital ward I have seen patients writhing in intense pain many times.","furigana":"びょうしつ|かんじゃ|はげ|いた|すがた|なんど|み"},{"japanese":"釣り上げた魚が船の上でのたうっていた。","romaji":"Tsuriageta sakana ga fune no ue de notautte ita.","german":"The fish that had been caught was thrashing about on the boat.","furigana":"つ|あ|さかな|ふね|うえ"}],"notes":"のたうつ means \"to writhe, to twist (in pain)\" and is almost always written in kana. The compound のたうち回る \"to roll about writhing\" is especially common: 激痛でのたうち回る. It describes violent, uncontrolled movements of people or animals (snakes, caught fish). The tone is drastic and descriptive; in conversation, for mild pain one is more likely to say 痛くて動けない.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"借金から抜け出そうともがいている。","romaji":"Shakkin kara nukedasō to mogaite iru.","german":"I struggle to get out of debt.","furigana":"しゃっきん|ぬ|だ"},{"japanese":"溺れかけた子どもが水の中で必死にもがいていた。","romaji":"Oborekaketa kodomo ga mizu no naka de hisshi ni mogaite ita.","german":"The child, who nearly drowned, flailed desperately in the water.","furigana":"おぼ|こ|みず|なか|ひっし"},{"japanese":"患者は激しい痛みにもがき苦しんでいた。","romaji":"Kanja wa hageshii itami ni mogaki kurushinde ita.","german":"The patient writhed in severe pain.","furigana":"かんじゃ|はげ|いた|くる"}],"notes":"もがく is an intransitive verb and first describes physical flailing and writhing, for example in water or from pain (もがき苦しむ \"to writhe in agony\"). Figuratively it means \"to struggle desperately\", typically in the pattern 〜しようともがく. It is almost always written in kana. The closely related あがく emphasizes more the futile thrashing in a hopeless situation (悪あがき).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"最後まで足掻いてみるつもりだ。","romaji":"Saigo made agaite miru tsumori da.","german":"I intend to keep struggling until the end.","furigana":"さいご|あが"},{"japanese":"今さらあがいても結果は変わらないよ。","romaji":"Imasara agaite mo kekka wa kawaranai yo.","german":"Struggling now won't change the result.","furigana":"いま|けっか|か"},{"japanese":"水に落ちた犬が、岸に上がろうと必死にあがいていた。","romaji":"Mizu ni ochita inu ga, kishi ni agarō to hisshi ni agaite ita.","german":"The dog that had fallen into the water was desperately paddling to get up onto the shore.","furigana":"みず|お|いぬ|きし|あ|ひっし"}],"notes":"あがく is intransitive and literally means \"to paw the ground with one's feet, to kick about\"; it is mostly used figuratively for a desperate, often futile struggle against a hopeless situation: 今さらあがいても無駄だ. The noun 悪あがき \"pointless flailing, refusing to give up\" is very common. The kanji spelling 足掻く is rare; kana is normal. Compared with もがく, あがく sounds more strongly \"in vain\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"藁にもすがる気持ちだった。","romaji":"Wara ni mo sugaru kimochi datta.","german":"I was ready to clutch at any straw.","furigana":"わら|きも"},{"japanese":"祖母は杖にすがってゆっくり歩いた。","romaji":"Sobo wa tsue ni sugatte yukkuri aruita.","german":"My grandmother walked slowly, leaning on her cane.","furigana":"そぼ|つえ|ある"},{"japanese":"最後は神にもすがる思いで合格を祈った。","romaji":"Saigo wa kami ni mo sugaru omoi de gōkaku o inotta.","german":"In the end I prayed to pass, turning even to the gods in my desperation.","furigana":"さいご|かみ|おも|ごうかく|いの"}],"notes":"すがる is the usual kana spelling of 縋る. It takes に and means \"to cling to something, to lean on something\" (杖にすがる) or, figuratively, \"to rely desperately on someone, to implore\". Set phrases: 藁にもすがる \"to clutch at any straw\" and 神にもすがる思い \"praying to all the gods in one's distress\". Unlike 頼る, すがる suggests dependence and having no way out.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"困ったときは友人に頼る。","romaji":"Komatta toki wa yūjin ni tayoru.","german":"When I'm in trouble, I turn to my friends.","furigana":"こま|ゆうじん|たよ"},{"japanese":"地図アプリに頼りすぎると、道が覚えられなくなる。","romaji":"Chizu apuri ni tayori sugiru to, michi ga oboerarenaku naru.","german":"If you rely too much on map apps, you won't be able to remember the way.","furigana":"ちず|たよ|みち|おぼ"},{"japanese":"大学に入るとき、叔父を頼って上京した。","romaji":"Daigaku ni hairu toki, oji o tayotte jōkyō shita.","german":"When I entered university, I went up to Tokyo relying on my uncle.","furigana":"だいがく|はい|おじ|たよ|じょうきょう"}],"notes":"頼る means \"to rely on someone or something, to depend on help\" and mostly takes に: 親に頼る, 薬に頼る, 〜に頼りすぎる. With を it means \"to use someone as a point of contact,\" e.g. 知人を頼って上京する \"to go to Tokyo and stay with an acquaintance.\" Not to be confused with 頼む \"to ask, commission.\" Related are 頼りになる \"reliable\" and 頼りない \"unreliable.\"","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"前任者から業務を引き継いだ。","romaji":"Zenninsha kara gyōmu o hikitsuida.","german":"I took over the duties from my predecessor.","furigana":"ぜんにんしゃ|ぎょうむ|ひ|つ"},{"japanese":"異動の前に、後任に仕事を引き継がなければならない。","romaji":"Idō no mae ni, kōnin ni shigoto o hikitsuganakereba naranai.","german":"Before my transfer, I have to hand my work over to my successor.","furigana":"いどう|まえ|こうにん|しごと|ひ|つ"},{"japanese":"兄は父の店を引き継いで、二代目の店主になった。","romaji":"Ani wa chichi no mise o hikitsuide, nidaime no tenshu ni natta.","german":"My older brother took over our father's shop and became the second-generation owner.","furigana":"あに|ちち|みせ|ひ|つ|にだいめ|てんしゅ"}],"notes":"引き継ぐ is transitive: 〜から〜を引き継ぐ \"to take over from someone\" (duties, a post, a business) or 〜に〜を引き継ぐ \"to hand over to someone\". It is typical of the working world; the noun 引き継ぎ is the \"handover\" at a job change or the end of a shift. In contrast, 受け継ぐ is used more for inherited traditions, talents or traits.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"祖父の技術を受け継いだ。","romaji":"Sofu no gijutsu o uketsuida.","german":"I inherited my grandfather's technique.","furigana":"そふ|ぎじゅつ|う|つ"},{"japanese":"この祭りは三百年以上受け継がれてきた伝統だ。","romaji":"Kono matsuri wa sanbyakunen ijō uketsugarete kita dentō da.","german":"This festival is a tradition that has been handed down for over three hundred years.","furigana":"まつ|さんびゃくねんいじょうう|つ|でんとう"},{"japanese":"娘は母親の明るい性格を受け継いでいる。","romaji":"Musume wa hahaoya no akarui seikaku o uketsuide iru.","german":"The daughter inherited her mother's cheerful nature.","furigana":"むすめ|ははおや|あか|せいかく|う|つ"}],"notes":"受け継ぐ is transitive (〜を受け継ぐ) and means \"to inherit, to carry on\" in the sense of traditions, techniques, character traits or the will of a deceased person. Very common in the passive: 代々受け継がれてきた伝統 \"a tradition handed down from generation to generation\". For the legal inheritance of property one says 相続する, for handing over work duties 引き継ぐ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"過大な期待をかけないでほしい。","romaji":"Kadai na kitai o kakenaide hoshii.","german":"Please don't place excessive expectations on me.","furigana":"かだい|きたい"},{"japanese":"社員に過大な負担をかけるべきではない。","romaji":"Shain ni kadai na futan o kakeru beki de wa nai.","german":"Employees should not be given an excessive burden.","furigana":"しゃいん|かだい|ふたん"},{"japanese":"彼は自分の能力を過大評価している。","romaji":"Kare wa jibun no nōryoku o kadai hyōka shite iru.","german":"He overestimates his own abilities.","furigana":"かれ|じぶん|のうりょく|かだいひょうか"}],"notes":"過大な is a rather written-language な-adjective meaning \"too large, excessive\", typically with abstract nouns: 過大な期待, 過大な負担, 過大な要求. The compound 過大評価(する) \"to overestimate\" is very common. The opposite is 過小. In everyday speech one often says simply 大きすぎる; 過度な stresses exceeding a limit in actions (過度な飲酒).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"被害を過小に評価してはいけない。","romaji":"Higai o kashō ni hyōka shite wa ikenai.","german":"The damage must not be underestimated.","furigana":"ひがい|かしょう|ひょうか"},{"japanese":"費用を過小に見積もったせいで、予算が足りなくなった。","romaji":"Hiyō o kashō ni mitsumotta sei de, yosan ga tarinaku natta.","german":"Because we had estimated the costs too low, the budget ran short.","furigana":"ひよう|かしょう|みつ|よさん|た"},{"japanese":"彼女は自分の実力を過小評価しがちだ。","romaji":"Kanojo wa jibun no jitsuryoku o kashō hyōka shigachi da.","german":"She tends to underestimate her own ability.","furigana":"かのじょ|じぶん|じつりょく|かしょうひょうか"}],"notes":"過小 means \"too small, too little\" and is the counterpart of 過大. As the attributive form 過小な it is rather rare; typical are 過小に評価する / 過小に見積もる and above all the compound 過小評価(する) \"to underestimate\". It is written-language; in everyday speech people also say 甘く見る or 小さく見積もる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"適度な運動が健康に良い。","romaji":"Tekido na undō ga kenkō ni yoi.","german":"Moderate exercise is good for your health.","furigana":"てきど|うんどう|けんこう|よ"},{"japanese":"仕事の合間に適度な休憩を取りましょう。","romaji":"Shigoto no aima ni tekido na kyūkei o torimashō.","german":"Let's take an appropriate break between tasks.","furigana":"しごと|あいま|てきど|きゅうけい|と"},{"japanese":"加湿器で部屋を適度な湿度に保っている。","romaji":"Kashitsuki de heya o tekido na shitsudo ni tamotte iru.","german":"I keep the room at a suitable humidity with a humidifier.","furigana":"かしつき|へや|てきど|しつど|たも"}],"notes":"適度な describes the right, moderate amount: neither too much nor too little. Typical: 適度な運動, 適度な休憩, 適度な湿度, and adverbial 適度に (適度に体を動かす). 適切, by contrast, means \"fitting, appropriate to the matter\" (a measure, a choice of words), not \"in the right amount\". Be careful with 適当: in colloquial speech it often sounds like \"sloppy, any old way\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"適切な対応をお願いします。","romaji":"Tekisetsu na taiō o onegai shimasu.","german":"Please respond appropriately.","furigana":"てきせつ|たいおう|ねが"},{"japanese":"状況に応じて適切な言葉を選ぶのは難しい。","romaji":"Jōkyō ni ōjite tekisetsu na kotoba o erabu no wa muzukashii.","german":"It is difficult to choose the right words depending on the situation.","furigana":"じょうきょう|おう|てきせつ|ことば|えら|むずか"},{"japanese":"医師の適切な処置のおかげで、父はすぐに回復した。","romaji":"Ishi no tekisetsu na shochi no okage de, chichi wa sugu ni kaifuku shita.","german":"Thanks to the doctor's appropriate treatment, my father recovered quickly.","furigana":"いし|てきせつ|しょち|ちち|かいふく"}],"notes":"適切な means \"suitable, appropriate, proper\" and judges whether something fits the situation: 適切な対応, 適切な処置, 適切な表現, adverbial 適切に管理する. Opposite: 不適切 (不適切な発言). Unlike 適度, it is not about quantity. 適当 has the same basic meaning, but in speech it is often used pejoratively for \"sloppy\", so 適切 is the safe choice in formal contexts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"妥当な判断だと思う。","romaji":"Datō na handan da to omou.","german":"I think it is a reasonable decision.","furigana":"だとう|はんだん|おも"},{"japanese":"この品質なら、その値段は妥当だと思う。","romaji":"Kono hinshitsu nara, sono nedan wa datō da to omou.","german":"For this quality, I think that price is reasonable.","furigana":"ひんしつ|ねだん|だとう|おも"},{"japanese":"現時点では、計画を延期するのが妥当だろう。","romaji":"Genjiten de wa, keikaku o enki suru no ga datō darō.","german":"For the time being, it would probably be reasonable to postpone the plan.","furigana":"げんじてん|けいかく|えんき|だとう"}],"notes":"妥当な means \"appropriate, justifiable, objectively warranted\" and evaluates judgments, prices or conclusions: 妥当な判断, 妥当な金額, 〜とするのが妥当だ \"it is reasonable to assume that ...\". It is rather formal and typical of discussions and reports. The noun 妥当性 means \"appropriateness, validity\". 適切 stresses fitting the situation, 妥当 the reasonableness of a judgment.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"合理的な方法で問題を解決した。","romaji":"Gōriteki na hōhō de mondai o kaiketsu shita.","german":"I solved the problem in a rational way.","furigana":"ごうりてき|ほうほう|もんだい|かいけつ"},{"japanese":"彼は何でも合理的に考えるタイプだ。","romaji":"Kare wa nan demo gōriteki ni kangaeru taipu da.","german":"He is the type who thinks about everything rationally.","furigana":"かれ|なん|ごうりてき|かんが"},{"japanese":"無駄な会議を減らすのは合理的な判断だ。","romaji":"Muda na kaigi o herasu no wa gōriteki na handan da.","german":"Cutting down on pointless meetings is a reasonable decision.","furigana":"むだ|かいぎ|へ|ごうりてき|はんだん"}],"notes":"合理的な means \"rational, reasonable, expedient\" - without waste and based on reason: 合理的な方法, 合理的に考える, 合理的な判断. Opposite: 非合理的. Related is 合理化 \"rationalization\" (also in the sense of job cuts). 論理的, by contrast, stresses the logical consistency of an argument, 合理的 the practical reasonableness.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"論理的に説明してください。","romaji":"Ronriteki ni setsumei shite kudasai.","german":"Please explain it logically.","furigana":"ろんりてき|せつめい"},{"japanese":"彼女の文章は論理的でわかりやすい。","romaji":"Kanojo no bunshō wa ronriteki de wakariyasui.","german":"Her writing is logical and easy to understand.","furigana":"かのじょ|ぶんしょう|ろんりてき"},{"japanese":"感情的にならず、論理的に話し合おう。","romaji":"Kanjōteki ni narazu, ronriteki ni hanashiaō.","german":"Let's not get emotional, but discuss it logically.","furigana":"かんじょうてき|ろんりてき|はな|あ"}],"notes":"論理的な means \"logical, consistent\" and describes thinking, speaking and texts: 論理的に考える, 論理的に説明する, 論理的思考. Opposite: 非論理的; as a contrast, 感情的 \"emotional\" is common. Unlike 合理的 (\"practically reasonable, efficient\"), it is about a coherent structure of thought. Not to be confused with 理論的 \"theoretical\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"科学的な根拠に基づいている。","romaji":"Kagakuteki na konkyo ni motozuite iru.","german":"It is based on scientific evidence.","furigana":"かがくてき|こんきょ|もと"},{"japanese":"その健康法は科学的に証明されていない。","romaji":"Sono kenkōhō wa kagakuteki ni shōmei sarete inai.","german":"That health method has not been scientifically proven.","furigana":"けんこうほう|かがくてき|しょうめい"},{"japanese":"血液型で性格が決まるという話は、科学的ではない。","romaji":"Ketsuekigata de seikaku ga kimaru to iu hanashi wa, kagakuteki de wa nai.","german":"The claim that blood type determines personality is not scientific.","furigana":"けつえきがた|せいかく|き|はなし|かがくてき"}],"notes":"科学的な means \"scientific\" in the sense of \"based on science and evidence\": 科学的な根拠, 科学的に証明する, 科学的な方法. Opposite: 非科学的 (e.g. about superstition). In everyday speech it often appears negated: 科学的ではない. The noun 科学 refers mainly to the natural sciences; for \"academic\" in general, 学術的 is more usual.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"体系的に知識を整理した。","romaji":"Taikeiteki ni chishiki o seiri shita.","german":"I organized my knowledge systematically.","furigana":"たいけいてき|ちしき|せいり"},{"japanese":"文法を体系的に学びたいなら、この教科書がおすすめだ。","romaji":"Bunpō o taikeiteki ni manabitai nara, kono kyōkasho ga osusume da.","german":"If you want to study grammar systematically, I recommend this textbook.","furigana":"ぶんぽう|たいけいてき|まな|きょうかしょ"},{"japanese":"新入社員のために体系的な研修プログラムを用意している。","romaji":"Shinnyū shain no tame ni taikeiteki na kenshū puroguramu o yōi shite iru.","german":"We have a systematic training program for new employees.","furigana":"しんにゅうしゃいん|たいけいてき|けんしゅう|ようい"}],"notes":"体系的な means \"systematic, brought into an ordered overall system\" and concerns mainly knowledge and learning: 体系的に学ぶ, 体系的に整理する, 体系的な知識. The base word is 体系 \"system, structure\". 組織的, by contrast, refers to the coordinated action of a group, and 計画的 to proceeding according to a plan.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"組織的な犯罪が増えている。","romaji":"Soshikiteki na hanzai ga fuete iru.","german":"Organized crime is increasing.","furigana":"そしきてき|はんざい|ふ"},{"japanese":"会社として組織的に取り組む必要がある。","romaji":"Kaisha to shite soshikiteki ni torikumu hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"The company needs to tackle this as an organization.","furigana":"かいしゃ|そしきてき|と|く|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"地震の後、ボランティアが組織的に被災地を支援した。","romaji":"Jishin no ato, borantia ga soshikiteki ni hisaichi o shien shita.","german":"After the earthquake, volunteers supported the disaster area in an organized way.","furigana":"じしん|あと|そしきてき|ひさいち|しえん"}],"notes":"組織的な means \"organized, coordinated, carried out by an organization\": 組織的な犯罪 \"organized crime\", 組織的な支援, 組織的に取り組む \"to tackle together as an organization\". It stresses that not individuals but a group acts in a planned way. 体系的, by contrast, means ordered knowledge, and 計画的 acting according to a plan.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"計画的に勉強を進めている。","romaji":"Keikakuteki ni benkyō o susumete iru.","german":"I am studying according to a plan.","furigana":"けいかくてき|べんきょう|すす"},{"japanese":"毎月計画的に貯金している。","romaji":"Maitsuki keikakuteki ni chokin shite iru.","german":"I save money according to a plan every month.","furigana":"まいつきけいかくてき|ちょきん"},{"japanese":"警察はこれを計画的な犯行と見ている。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa kore o keikakuteki na hankō to mite iru.","german":"The police regard this as a premeditated crime.","furigana":"けいさつ|けいかくてき|はんこう|み"}],"notes":"計画的な means \"planned, according to plan\": 計画的に貯金する, 計画的に勉強する. For crimes, 計画的な犯行 means \"premeditated, planned act\" as opposed to a spontaneous one (衝動的な犯行). Everyday opposite: 無計画 \"unplanned\". Often used adverbially with に.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"段階的に規制を緩和する。","romaji":"Dankaiteki ni kisei o kanwa suru.","german":"To ease the regulations step by step.","furigana":"だんかいてき|きせい|かんわ"},{"japanese":"新しいシステムは段階的に導入される予定だ。","romaji":"Atarashii shisutemu wa dankaiteki ni dōnyū sareru yotei da.","german":"The new system is scheduled to be introduced in stages.","furigana":"あたら|だんかいてき|どうにゅう|よてい"},{"japanese":"初心者向けに段階的な練習メニューを作った。","romaji":"Shoshinsha muke ni dankaiteki na renshū menyū o tsukutta.","german":"I made a step-by-step practice menu for beginners.","furigana":"しょしんしゃむ|だんかいてき|れんしゅう|つく"}],"notes":"段階的な means \"step by step, in stages\": 段階的に導入する, 段階的に引き上げる, 段階的な廃止. Typical of politics, administration and companies. Unlike 徐々に or 次第に (\"gradually\", smoothly), 段階的 stresses clearly separated steps. Base word: 段階 \"step, phase\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"部分的な修正が必要だ。","romaji":"Bubunteki na shūsei ga hitsuyō da.","german":"A partial correction is necessary.","furigana":"ぶぶんてき|しゅうせい|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"彼の意見には部分的に賛成だ。","romaji":"Kare no iken ni wa bubunteki ni sansei da.","german":"I partly agree with his opinion.","furigana":"かれ|いけん|ぶぶんてき|さんせい"},{"japanese":"その小説は部分的に実話に基づいている。","romaji":"Sono shōsetsu wa bubunteki ni jitsuwa ni motozuite iru.","german":"The novel is partly based on a true story.","furigana":"しょうせつ|ぶぶんてき|じつわ|もと"}],"notes":"部分的な means \"partial\": 部分的な修正, 部分的に賛成する \"to agree in part\", 部分的には正しい. Opposite: 全面的. In everyday speech 一部 is also often used (一部を修正する); 部分的 sounds somewhat more objective. Mostly adverbial with に or attributive with な.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"全面的な見直しが求められている。","romaji":"Zenmenteki na minaoshi ga motomerarete iru.","german":"A comprehensive review is being called for.","furigana":"ぜんめんてき|みなお|もと"},{"japanese":"社長はこの計画を全面的に支持している。","romaji":"Shachō wa kono keikaku o zenmenteki ni shiji shite iru.","german":"The company president fully supports this plan.","furigana":"しゃちょう|けいかく|ぜんめんてき|しじ"},{"japanese":"来年から、駅の構内は全面的に禁煙になる。","romaji":"Rainen kara, eki no kōnai wa zenmenteki ni kin'en ni naru.","german":"From next year, smoking will be banned throughout the station premises.","furigana":"らいねん|えき|こうない|ぜんめんてき|きんえん"}],"notes":"全面的な means \"comprehensive, complete, across the board\": 全面的に支持する, 全面的に協力する, 全面禁煙・全面的な見直し. Opposite: 部分的. It is used mainly adverbially with に and sounds more formal than 全部 or すっかり. Typical of the news, politics and official statements.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"徹底的に調査を行った。","romaji":"Tetteiteki ni chōsa o okonatta.","german":"I carried out a thorough investigation.","furigana":"てっていてき|ちょうさ|おこな"},{"japanese":"年末に家中を徹底的に掃除した。","romaji":"Nenmatsu ni iejū o tetteiteki ni sōji shita.","german":"At the end of the year I cleaned the whole house thoroughly.","furigana":"ねんまつ|いえじゅう|てっていてき|そうじ"},{"japanese":"試験の前に、苦手な分野を徹底的に復習した。","romaji":"Shiken no mae ni, nigate na bun'ya o tetteiteki ni fukushū shita.","german":"Before the exam, I reviewed my weak areas thoroughly.","furigana":"しけん|まえ|にがて|ぶんや|てっていてき|ふくしゅう"}],"notes":"徹底的な means \"thorough, down to the last detail\": 徹底的に調べる, 徹底的に掃除する, 徹底的な調査. The base word is 徹底 (徹底する \"to carry out consistently\", 徹底した管理). It is stronger than よく or しっかり and stresses that nothing is left out. Mostly adverbial with に.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"抜本的な改革が必要だ。","romaji":"Bapponteki na kaikaku ga hitsuyō da.","german":"Fundamental reform is necessary.","furigana":"ばっぽんてき|かいかく|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"少子化の問題には抜本的な対策が求められている。","romaji":"Shōshika no mondai ni wa bapponteki na taisaku ga motomerarete iru.","german":"Fundamental measures are being demanded to deal with the problem of the declining birthrate.","furigana":"しょうしか|もんだい|ばっぽんてき|たいさく|もと"},{"japanese":"赤字を減らすには、経営を抜本的に見直すしかない。","romaji":"Akaji o herasu ni wa, keiei o bapponteki ni minaosu shika nai.","german":"To reduce the deficit, there is no choice but to rethink the management from the ground up.","furigana":"あかじ|へ|けいえい|ばっぽんてき|みなお"}],"notes":"抜本的な literally means \"pulling out the root\", i.e. \"fundamental, going to the root\". Typical combinations: 抜本的な改革, 抜本的な対策, 抜本的に見直す. It belongs to the language of politics and business and sounds more formal than 根本的, which means something similar, \"basic, from the ground up\". \"Radical\" in the sense of extreme, by contrast, is 過激.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"具体的な例を挙げてください。","romaji":"Gutaiteki na rei o agete kudasai.","german":"Please give a concrete example.","furigana":"ぐたいてき|れい|あ"},{"japanese":"もう少し具体的に説明してもらえますか。","romaji":"Mō sukoshi gutaiteki ni setsumei shite moraemasu ka.","german":"Could you explain it a little more concretely?","furigana":"すこ|ぐたいてき|せつめい"},{"japanese":"旅行の具体的な日程はまだ決まっていない。","romaji":"Ryokō no gutaiteki na nittei wa mada kimatte inai.","german":"The specific travel schedule has not been decided yet.","furigana":"りょこう|ぐたいてき|にってい|き"}],"notes":"具体的な means \"concrete, tangible\": 具体的な例, 具体的な計画, 具体的に説明する. Very common in requests for clarification: もう少し具体的に言ってください. Opposite: 抽象的. Related words: 具体例 \"concrete example\", 具体化 \"concretization, making concrete\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"抽象的な議論は避けたい。","romaji":"Chūshōteki na giron wa saketai.","german":"I would like to avoid abstract discussions.","furigana":"ちゅうしょうてき|ぎろん|さ"},{"japanese":"彼の説明は抽象的すぎて、よくわからない。","romaji":"Kare no setsumei wa chūshōteki sugite, yoku wakaranai.","german":"His explanation is so abstract that I can hardly understand it.","furigana":"かれ|せつめい|ちゅうしょうてき"},{"japanese":"抽象的な絵は、見る人によって受ける印象が違う。","romaji":"Chūshōteki na e wa, miru hito ni yotte ukeru inshō ga chigau.","german":"Abstract paintings give a different impression depending on the viewer.","furigana":"ちゅうしょうてき|え|み|ひと|う|いんしょう|ちが"}],"notes":"抽象的な means \"abstract\" and is the opposite of 具体的. It is often used critically for vague, hard-to-grasp statements: 説明が抽象的すぎる. In art one speaks of 抽象画 \"abstract painting\" or 抽象的な絵. Base word: 抽象 \"abstraction\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"理論的には可能だが実行は難しい。","romaji":"Rironteki ni wa kanō da ga jikkō wa muzukashii.","german":"In theory it is possible, but carrying it out is difficult.","furigana":"りろんてき|かのう|じっこう|むずか"},{"japanese":"理論的な研究と実験の両方が必要だ。","romaji":"Rironteki na kenkyū to jikken no ryōhō ga hitsuyō da.","german":"Both theoretical research and experiments are necessary.","furigana":"りろんてき|けんきゅう|じっけん|りょうほう|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"先生はその現象を理論的に説明してくれた。","romaji":"Sensei wa sono genshō o rironteki ni setsumei shite kureta.","german":"The teacher explained the phenomenon to us theoretically.","furigana":"せんせい|げんしょう|りろんてき|せつめい"}],"notes":"理論的な means \"theoretical, based on theory\": 理論的な研究, 理論的に説明する. Very common is 理論的には \"theoretically (but not in practice)\", equivalent to 理論上は. The counterpart is 実践的 or 実際的. Do not confuse it with 論理的 \"logical\" - the two words have the same kanji in reverse order.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"実践的なスキルを身につける。","romaji":"Jissenteki na sukiru o mi ni tsukeru.","german":"To acquire practical skills.","furigana":"じっせんてき|み"},{"japanese":"この講座では実践的な会話を中心に学ぶ。","romaji":"Kono kōza de wa jissenteki na kaiwa o chūshin ni manabu.","german":"In this course you mainly learn practical conversation.","furigana":"こうざ|じっせんてき|かいわ|ちゅうしん|まな"},{"japanese":"理論だけでなく、実践的な経験も大切だ。","romaji":"Riron dake de naku, jissenteki na keiken mo taisetsu da.","german":"Not only theory but also practical experience is important.","furigana":"りろん|じっせんてき|けいけん|たいせつ"}],"notes":"実践的な means \"practice-oriented, practically applicable\" and describes knowledge, skills and teaching: 実践的なスキル, 実践的な英語, 実践的な訓練. The counterpart is 理論的. 実用的, by contrast, means \"practical, useful\" for objects (実用的なカバン). Base word: 実践 \"practice, putting into action\" (実践する \"to put into practice\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"もっと現実的な目標を設定しよう。","romaji":"Motto genjitsuteki na mokuhyō o settei shiyō.","german":"Let's set more realistic goals.","furigana":"げんじつてき|もくひょう|せってい"},{"japanese":"現実的に考えると、今年中の完成は難しい。","romaji":"Genjitsuteki ni kangaeru to, kotoshijū no kansei wa muzukashii.","german":"Realistically speaking, finishing it within this year is difficult.","furigana":"げんじつてき|かんが|ことしじゅう|かんせい|むずか"},{"japanese":"彼は夢を追うより、現実的な道を選んだ。","romaji":"Kare wa yume o ou yori, genjitsuteki na michi o eranda.","german":"Rather than chase his dream, he chose a realistic path.","furigana":"かれ|ゆめ|お|げんじつてき|みち|えら"}],"notes":"現実的な means \"realistic, true to reality\": 現実的な目標, 現実的な案, 現実的に考える \"to think realistically\". It can also mean \"sober, pragmatic\" as a character trait (彼は現実的な人だ). Opposite: 非現実的 or 理想的 in the sense of \"idealistic\". Base word: 現実 \"reality\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その計画は非現実的だ。","romaji":"Sono keikaku wa higenjitsuteki da.","german":"That plan is unrealistic.","furigana":"けいかく|ひげんじつてき"},{"japanese":"一か月で十キロ痩せるなんて、非現実的な目標だ。","romaji":"Ikkagetsu de jukkiro yaseru nante, higenjitsuteki na mokuhyō da.","german":"Losing ten kilos in a month is an unrealistic goal.","furigana":"いっ|げつ|じゅっ|や|ひげんじつてき|もくひょう"},{"japanese":"目の前に、映画のような非現実的な光景が広がっていた。","romaji":"Me no mae ni, eiga no yō na higenjitsuteki na kōkei ga hirogatte ita.","german":"An unreal scene like something out of a movie spread out before my eyes.","furigana":"め|まえ|えいが|ひげんじつてき|こうけい|ひろ"}],"notes":"非現実的な is the negation of 現実的 with the prefix 非 \"non-\". Usually it means \"unrealistic, not feasible\" (非現実的な計画, 非現実的な目標); but it can also mean \"unreal, dreamlike\", for example for landscapes or experiences. Similar: 現実離れした \"detached from reality\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女はいつも楽観的な考え方をする。","romaji":"Kanojo wa itsumo rakkanteki na kangaekata o suru.","german":"She always has an optimistic way of thinking.","furigana":"かのじょ|らっかんてき|かんが|かた"},{"japanese":"専門家は景気の回復について楽観的な見方をしている。","romaji":"Senmonka wa keiki no kaifuku ni tsuite rakkanteki na mikata o shite iru.","german":"Experts take an optimistic view of the economic recovery.","furigana":"せんもんか|けいき|かいふく|らっかんてき|みかた"},{"japanese":"そんなに楽観的に考えていると、後で困るよ。","romaji":"Sonna ni rakkanteki ni kangaete iru to, ato de komaru yo.","german":"If you think about it that optimistically, you'll get into trouble later.","furigana":"らっかんてき|かんが|あと|こま"}],"notes":"楽観的な means \"optimistic\" and describes a view of future developments: 楽観的な見方, 楽観的に考える, 見通しは楽観的だ. Opposite: 悲観的. The verb 楽観する or 楽観視する means \"to view confidently\", often negated: 楽観できない. 楽天的 describes more a fundamentally carefree nature.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あまり悲観的にならないでください。","romaji":"Amari hikanteki ni naranaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't be too pessimistic.","furigana":"ひかんてき"},{"japanese":"彼は自分の将来について悲観的だ。","romaji":"Kare wa jibun no shōrai ni tsuite hikanteki da.","german":"He is pessimistic about his own future.","furigana":"かれ|じぶん|しょうらい|ひかんてき"},{"japanese":"調査では、景気の先行きに悲観的な人が増えている。","romaji":"Chōsa de wa, keiki no sakiyuki ni hikanteki na hito ga fuete iru.","german":"According to the survey, more and more people are pessimistic about the economic outlook.","furigana":"ちょうさ|けいき|さきゆ|ひかんてき|ひと|ふ"}],"notes":"悲観的な means \"pessimistic, gloomy\": 悲観的な見方, 悲観的になる, 将来に悲観的だ. Opposite: 楽観的. The verb 悲観する means \"to see things black, to despair\" (将来を悲観する). Often found in advice: そんなに悲観的にならないで.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"中立的な立場から意見を述べる。","romaji":"Chūritsuteki na tachiba kara iken o noberu.","german":"I will state my opinion from a neutral standpoint.","furigana":"ちゅうりつてき|たちば|いけん|の"},{"japanese":"記者は中立的な態度で報道するべきだ。","romaji":"Kisha wa chūritsuteki na taido de hōdō suru beki da.","german":"Reporters should report in a neutral manner.","furigana":"きしゃ|ちゅうりつてき|たいど|ほうどう"},{"japanese":"友達同士のけんかでは、どちらの味方もせず中立的でいたい。","romaji":"Tomodachi dōshi no kenka de wa, dochira no mikata mo sezu chūritsuteki de itai.","german":"When friends quarrel, I want to take neither side and stay neutral.","furigana":"ともだちどうし|みかた|ちゅうりつてき"}],"notes":"中立的な means \"neutral, impartial\": 中立的な立場, 中立的な態度, 中立的に報道する. The base word is 中立 \"neutrality\", which also stands alone (中立を守る, 中立国 \"neutral country\"). In everyday speech one also says どちらの味方もしない \"to take neither side\". 公平 stresses more \"just, fair\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"漠然とした不安を感じている。","romaji":"Bakuzen to shita fuan o kanjite iru.","german":"I feel a vague sense of unease.","furigana":"ばくぜん|ふあん|かん"},{"japanese":"将来のことを漠然と考えている。","romaji":"Shōrai no koto o bakuzen to kangaete iru.","german":"I'm thinking vaguely about the future.","furigana":"しょうらい|ばくぜん|かんが"},{"japanese":"そんな漠然とした質問には答えにくい。","romaji":"Sonna bakuzen to shita shitsumon ni wa kotaenikui.","german":"It's hard to answer such a vague question.","furigana":"ばくぜん|しつもん|こた"}],"notes":"漠然 is a たる-adjective: attributively 漠然とした \"vague, blurry\" (漠然とした不安, 漠然としたイメージ), adverbially 漠然と (漠然と考える \"to think only vaguely\"). It describes something indefinite, without clear outlines. 曖昧, by contrast, means \"ambiguous, unclear\" - for example an evasive answer. Used mainly in written Japanese.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先行きが不透明な状況だ。","romaji":"Sakiyuki ga futōmei na jōkyō da.","german":"The outlook is uncertain.","furigana":"さきゆ|ふとうめい|じょうきょう"},{"japanese":"政治資金の流れが不透明だと批判されている。","romaji":"Seiji shikin no nagare ga futōmei da to hihan sarete iru.","german":"The flow of political funds is criticized as opaque.","furigana":"せいじしきん|なが|ふとうめい|ひはん"},{"japanese":"中身が見えないように、不透明な袋に入れてください。","romaji":"Nakami ga mienai yō ni, futōmei na fukuro ni irete kudasai.","german":"Please put it in an opaque bag so that the contents can't be seen.","furigana":"なかみ|み|ふとうめい|ふくろ|い"}],"notes":"不透明な literally means \"opaque, not see-through\" (不透明な袋, 不透明なガラス), and figuratively \"unclear, impenetrable\". Very common in the news: 先行き不透明 \"uncertain prospects\", 不透明な資金の流れ \"opaque flows of money\". Opposite: 透明. For unclarity in statements, 曖昧 fits better.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"透明なガラスのコップを使う。","romaji":"Tōmei na garasu no koppu o tsukau.","german":"I use a clear glass cup.","furigana":"とうめい|つか"},{"japanese":"沖縄の海は透明で、底まではっきり見える。","romaji":"Okinawa no umi wa tōmei de, soko made hakkiri mieru.","german":"The sea in Okinawa is so clear that you can see all the way to the bottom.","furigana":"おきなわ|うみ|とうめい|そこ|み"},{"japanese":"選挙の手続きはもっと透明であるべきだ。","romaji":"Senkyo no tetsuzuki wa motto tōmei de aru beki da.","german":"The election procedures should be more transparent.","furigana":"せんきょ|てつづ|とうめい"}],"notes":"透明な means \"see-through, clear\" for glass, water or film (透明なガラス, 透明な水). Figuratively it means \"transparent, traceable\" for procedures and flows of money; for this, the noun 透明性 \"transparency\" is very common. Opposite: 不透明. 透明人間 is the \"invisible man\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"明白な証拠が見つかった。","romaji":"Meihaku na shōko ga mitsukatta.","german":"Clear evidence was found.","furigana":"めいはく|しょうこ|み"},{"japanese":"彼がうそをついているのは明白だ。","romaji":"Kare ga uso o tsuite iru no wa meihaku da.","german":"It is obvious that he is lying.","furigana":"かれ|めいはく"},{"japanese":"これは明白なルール違反だ。","romaji":"Kore wa meihaku na rūru ihan da.","german":"This is a clear violation of the rules.","furigana":"めいはく|いはん"}],"notes":"明白な means \"evident, unambiguous, undeniable\" and sounds more formal than the everyday 明らか: 明白な事実, 明白な証拠, 明白な違反, 〜のは明白だ. Typical of reports, courts and discussions. In conversation, people mostly say はっきりしている or 明らかだ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"顕著な成果を上げた。","romaji":"Kencho na seika o ageta.","german":"He achieved remarkable results.","furigana":"けんちょ|せいか|あ"},{"japanese":"最近、若者の読書離れが顕著になっている。","romaji":"Saikin, wakamono no dokusho banare ga kencho ni natte iru.","german":"Recently, it has become clear that young people are reading less and less.","furigana":"さいきん|わかもの|どくしょばな|けんちょ"},{"japanese":"この薬は特に高齢者に顕著な効果が見られた。","romaji":"Kono kusuri wa toku ni kōreisha ni kencho na kōka ga mirareta.","german":"With this medicine, a marked effect was seen especially in elderly people.","furigana":"くすり|とく|こうれいしゃ|けんちょ|こうか|み"}],"notes":"顕著な means \"strikingly evident, conspicuous, marked\" and describes measurable differences, tendencies or effects: 顕著な効果, 顕著な傾向, 〜が顕著になる / 顕著に表れる. It is a formal word of reports and news; in everyday speech, 目立つ or はっきりした is more usual. Praise in the sense of \"excellent\" is expressed rather by 優れた.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"問題が顕在化してきた。","romaji":"Mondai ga kenzaika shite kita.","german":"The problem has become apparent.","furigana":"もんだい|けんざいか"},{"japanese":"顧客の顕在的なニーズだけでなく、潜在的なニーズにも目を向けるべきだ。","romaji":"Kokyaku no kenzaiteki na nīzu dake de naku, senzaiteki na nīzu ni mo me o mukeru beki da.","german":"We should pay attention not only to customers' manifest needs but also to their latent needs.","furigana":"こきゃく|けんざいてき|せんざいてき|め|む"},{"japanese":"顕在的なリスクより、まだ見えていないリスクのほうが怖い。","romaji":"Kenzaiteki na risuku yori, mada miete inai risuku no hō ga kowai.","german":"More than the risks that are visible, I fear the risks that nobody can see yet.","furigana":"けんざいてき|み|こわ"}],"notes":"顕在的な is a technical word (\"manifest, coming out into the open\") and the counterpart of 潜在的 \"latent\". It appears mainly in marketing and specialist texts, e.g. 顕在的なニーズ \"expressed, conscious needs\" as opposed to 潜在的なニーズ. Much more common is the verb 顕在化する \"to come to light, to become visible\" (問題が顕在化する); 顕在的になる sounds unnatural.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"潜在的なリスクを見逃さない。","romaji":"Senzaiteki na risuku o minogasanai.","german":"Don't overlook latent risks.","furigana":"せんざいてき|みのが"},{"japanese":"彼女には潜在的な能力があると思う。","romaji":"Kanojo ni wa senzaiteki na nōryoku ga aru to omou.","german":"I think she has latent ability.","furigana":"かのじょ|せんざいてき|のうりょく|おも"},{"japanese":"高齢化が進み、この商品には大きな潜在的需要がある。","romaji":"Kōreika ga susumi, kono shōhin ni wa ōki na senzaiteki juyō ga aru.","german":"With the aging of society, there is large potential demand for this product.","furigana":"こうれいか|すす|しょうひん|おお|せんざいてきじゅよう"}],"notes":"潜在的な means \"latent, potential, present beneath the surface\": 潜在的なリスク, 潜在的な需要, 潜在的な能力. It describes something that already exists but is not yet visible or effective. The counterpart is 顕在的. Related words: 潜在能力 \"hidden potential\", 潜在意識 \"subconscious\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これは典型的な例だ。","romaji":"Kore wa tenkeiteki na rei da.","german":"This is a typical example.","furigana":"てんけいてき|れい"},{"japanese":"彼は典型的な日本のサラリーマンだ。","romaji":"Kare wa tenkeiteki na Nihon no sararīman da.","german":"He is a typical Japanese office worker.","furigana":"かれ|てんけいてき|にほん"},{"japanese":"これは詐欺によくある典型的なパターンだ。","romaji":"Kore wa sagi ni yoku aru tenkeiteki na patān da.","german":"This is a typical pattern common in fraud.","furigana":"さぎ|てんけいてき"}],"notes":"典型的な means \"typical, a textbook example of something\": 典型的な例, 典型的な日本人, 典型的なパターン. It is often also used with a slightly critical tone when something fits a cliché. 代表的, by contrast, means \"representative, especially well known\" (代表的な料理). Base word: 典型 \"type, model example\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この建物は時代の象徴的な存在だ。","romaji":"Kono tatemono wa jidai no shōchōteki na sonzai da.","german":"This building is a symbolic presence of its era.","furigana":"たてもの|じだい|しょうちょうてき|そんざい"},{"japanese":"その事件は時代の変化を象徴的に表している。","romaji":"Sono jiken wa jidai no henka o shōchōteki ni arawashite iru.","german":"That incident symbolically expresses the changes of the times.","furigana":"じけん|じだい|へんか|しょうちょうてき|あらわ"},{"japanese":"開会式では、平和を願う象徴的な場面がいくつもあった。","romaji":"Kaikaishiki de wa, heiwa o negau shōchōteki na bamen ga ikutsu mo atta.","german":"At the opening ceremony there were several symbolic moments expressing the wish for peace.","furigana":"かいかいしき|へいわ|ねが|しょうちょうてき|ばめん"}],"notes":"象徴的な means \"symbolic, emblematic, rich in symbolism\": 象徴的な存在, 象徴的な出来事, 〜を象徴的に表す. The base word is 象徴 \"symbol\" (平和の象徴, 象徴する \"to symbolize\"). It describes something that stands in for a larger idea or era; in the sense of \"purely formal, just for show\", it is hardly used.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"特殊な技術が必要とされる。","romaji":"Tokushu na gijutsu ga hitsuyō to sareru.","german":"Special techniques are required.","furigana":"とくしゅ|ぎじゅつ|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"特殊なケースを除いて、返品はできません。","romaji":"Tokushu na kēsu o nozoite, henpin wa dekimasen.","german":"Except in special cases, returns are not possible.","furigana":"とくしゅ|のぞ|へんぴん"},{"japanese":"この部屋のドアは特殊なカギでしか開けられない。","romaji":"Kono heya no doa wa tokushu na kagi de shika akerarenai.","german":"The door of this room can only be opened with a special key.","furigana":"へや|とくしゅ|あ"}],"notes":"特殊な means \"special, particular, of an unusual kind\" and stresses that something deviates from the normal: 特殊な技術, 特殊なケース, 特殊な素材. 特別, by contrast, means \"special, extra\" in the sense of special treatment or appreciation (特別な日, 特別に許可する). Opposite of 特殊: 一般的 or 普通.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"特異な才能の持ち主だ。","romaji":"Tokui na sainō no mochinushi da.","german":"He is a person with a unique talent.","furigana":"とくい|さいのう|も|ぬし"},{"japanese":"この地域は特異な気候で知られている。","romaji":"Kono chiiki wa tokui na kikō de shirarete iru.","german":"This region is known for its peculiar climate.","furigana":"ちいき|とくい|きこう|し"},{"japanese":"その事件は特異なケースとして、新聞に大きく取り上げられた。","romaji":"Sono jiken wa tokui na kēsu to shite, shinbun ni ōkiku toriagerareta.","german":"The case was widely covered in the newspaper as an unusual individual case.","furigana":"じけん|とくい|しんぶん|おお|と|あ"}],"notes":"特異な means \"peculiar, unique, out of the ordinary\": 特異な才能, 特異な性格, 特異な事件. It is a written-language word and stresses the unusual, not necessarily in a positive sense. Well-known compounds: 特異体質 \"special (allergic) constitution\", 特異点 \"singularity\". Note: it is read the same as 得意 \"good at something, strong point\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"異常な暑さが続いている。","romaji":"Ijō na atsusa ga tsuzuite iru.","german":"The unusual heat continues.","furigana":"いじょう|あつ|つづ"},{"japanese":"夜中に異常な音がしたので目が覚めた。","romaji":"Yonaka ni ijō na oto ga shita node me ga sameta.","german":"I woke up in the middle of the night because of a strange noise.","furigana":"よなか|いじょう|おと|め|さ"},{"japanese":"検査の結果、特に異常は見つからなかった。","romaji":"Kensa no kekka, toku ni ijō wa mitsukaranakatta.","german":"The examination found nothing in particular wrong.","furigana":"けんさ|けっか|とく|いじょう|み"}],"notes":"異常な means \"abnormal, unusual, not normal\": 異常な暑さ, 異常な音, 異常気象 \"abnormal weather\". As a noun it appears in medical findings: 異常なし \"no abnormalities\", 異常が見つかる. Opposite: 正常. Compared with 珍しい (\"rare\"), 異常 sounds like a malfunction or danger.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"システムは正常に動作している。","romaji":"Shisutemu wa seijō ni dōsa shite iru.","german":"The system is operating normally.","furigana":"せいじょう|どうさ"},{"japanese":"健康診断の結果、血圧は正常だった。","romaji":"Kenkō shindan no kekka, ketsuatsu wa seijō datta.","german":"In the health checkup, my blood pressure was normal.","furigana":"けんこうしんだん|けっか|けつあつ|せいじょう"},{"japanese":"事故で遅れていた電車は、夕方には正常な運行に戻った。","romaji":"Jiko de okurete ita densha wa, yūgata ni wa seijō na unkō ni modotta.","german":"The trains that had been delayed by the accident returned to normal service by the evening.","furigana":"じこ|おく|でんしゃ|ゆうがた|せいじょう|うんこう|もど"}],"notes":"正常な means \"normal, proper, functioning\" in the sense of a fault-free state: 正常に動作する, 正常な値, 正常に戻る. Opposite: 異常. 普通, by contrast, means \"ordinary, average\", and 通常 \"usually\". 正常 suits technology, medicine and processes, not people in the sense of \"average\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"通常の営業時間は九時から六時までだ。","romaji":"Tsūjō no eigyō jikan wa kuji kara rokuji made da.","german":"The regular business hours are from nine to six.","furigana":"つうじょう|えいぎょうじかん|くじ|ろくじ"},{"japanese":"通常、返事には三日ほどかかります。","romaji":"Tsūjō, henji ni wa mikka hodo kakarimasu.","german":"Normally, a reply takes about three days.","furigana":"つうじょう|へんじ|みっか"},{"japanese":"このセールでは、通常の半額で買える。","romaji":"Kono sēru de wa, tsūjō no hangaku de kaeru.","german":"In this sale, you can buy it for half the regular price.","furigana":"つうじょう|はんがく|か"}],"notes":"通常 means \"usual, normal, regular\" and is more formal than いつも or 普通. Attributively it takes の: 通常の営業時間, 通常の料金. Adverbially it means \"normally\": 通常、〜です. Other patterns: 通常通り \"as usual\", 通常は. Typical of business language, notices and announcements.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日常的な会話ができるようになりたい。","romaji":"Nichijōteki na kaiwa ga dekiru yō ni naritai.","german":"I want to be able to hold everyday conversations.","furigana":"にちじょうてき|かいわ"},{"japanese":"彼女は日常的にジョギングをしている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa nichijōteki ni jogingu o shite iru.","german":"She jogs regularly as part of her daily routine.","furigana":"かのじょ|にちじょうてき"},{"japanese":"この地域では、停電が日常的に起きている。","romaji":"Kono chiiki de wa, teiden ga nichijōteki ni okite iru.","german":"In this area, power outages happen as a matter of routine.","furigana":"ちいき|ていでん|にちじょうてき|お"}],"notes":"日常的な means \"everyday, regular, commonplace\": 日常的な会話, 日常的に使う, 日常的に起きる. It can mean neutrally \"part of everyday life\" or critically \"occurring constantly\" (暴力が日常的に行われる). Base word: 日常 \"everyday life\" (日常生活). Opposite: 非日常的.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"旅行は非日常的な体験を与えてくれる。","romaji":"Ryokō wa hinichijōteki na taiken o ataete kureru.","german":"Travel gives us extraordinary experiences.","furigana":"りょこう|ひにちじょうてき|たいけん|あた"},{"japanese":"高級ホテルで非日常的な時間を過ごした。","romaji":"Kōkyū hoteru de hinichijōteki na jikan o sugoshita.","german":"I spent a time away from everyday life at a luxury hotel.","furigana":"こうきゅう|ひにちじょうてき|じかん|す"},{"japanese":"地震の後、しばらく避難所での非日常的な生活が続いた。","romaji":"Jishin no ato, shibaraku hinanjo de no hinichijōteki na seikatsu ga tsuzuita.","german":"After the earthquake, life in an evacuation shelter continued for a while in a state of emergency.","furigana":"じしん|あと|ひなんじょ|ひにちじょうてき|せいかつ|つづ"}],"notes":"非日常的な means \"out of the ordinary, beyond everyday life\" and is the opposite of 日常的. In advertising for travel, hotels and theme parks it is positive (\"special, exciting\"), often as a noun: 非日常を味わう \"to experience something extraordinary\". In other contexts it can also describe a burdensome state of emergency.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日頃の努力が実を結んだ。","romaji":"Higoro no doryoku ga mi o musunda.","german":"My everyday efforts have borne fruit.","furigana":"ひごろ|どりょく|み|むす"},{"japanese":"日頃の感謝の気持ちを込めて、母に花を贈った。","romaji":"Higoro no kansha no kimochi o komete, haha ni hana o okutta.","german":"As thanks for everything she does for me day to day, I gave my mother flowers.","furigana":"ひごろ|かんしゃ|きも|こ|はは|はな|おく"},{"japanese":"日頃から災害に備えておくことが大切だ。","romaji":"Higoro kara saigai ni sonaete oku koto ga taisetsu da.","german":"It is important to prepare for disasters in everyday life.","furigana":"ひごろ|さいがい|そな|たいせつ"}],"notes":"日頃 is a noun/adverb meaning \"usually, constantly, in daily life\". With の it stands before nouns: 日頃の努力, 日頃の感謝, 日頃の行い \"everyday behavior\". Very common is 日頃から \"already in daily life, always\" (日頃から備える). Also typical in thank-you formulas: 日頃より大変お世話になっております. Intensified: 常日頃.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"普段の生活を大切にしている。","romaji":"Fudan no seikatsu o taisetsu ni shite iru.","german":"I value my everyday life.","furigana":"ふだん|せいかつ|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"今日はスーツではなく、普段の服で来てください。","romaji":"Kyō wa sūtsu de wa naku, fudan no fuku de kite kudasai.","german":"Please don't come in a suit today, but in your regular clothes.","furigana":"きょう|ふだん|ふく|き"},{"japanese":"試験では普段の力を出せなかった。","romaji":"Shiken de wa fudan no chikara o dasenakatta.","german":"I couldn't show my usual strength in the exam.","furigana":"しけん|ふだん|ちから|だ"}],"notes":"普段 refers to the normal, everyday state as opposed to special occasions; as an attribute it takes の: 普段の生活, 普段の服, 普段の自分. Adverbially one says 普段は \"normally\" or 普段から \"already in daily life, constantly\". Compared with いつも, 普段 stresses the contrast with an exceptional situation: 普段は静かな町 \"a town that is normally quiet\". 普段着 means \"everyday clothes\"; the more matter-of-fact 日常 sounds more written.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"恒例の夏祭りが開催された。","romaji":"Kōrei no natsumatsuri ga kaisai sareta.","german":"The traditional summer festival was held.","furigana":"こうれい|なつまつ|かいさい"},{"japanese":"年末には毎年恒例の忘年会が開かれる。","romaji":"Nenmatsu ni wa maitoshi kōrei no bōnenkai ga hirakareru.","german":"At the end of the year, the annual year-end party is held.","furigana":"ねんまつ|まいとしこうれい|ぼうねんかい|ひら"},{"japanese":"家族で初詣に行くのが我が家の恒例だ。","romaji":"Kazoku de hatsumōde ni iku no ga wagaya no kōrei da.","german":"Going to the first shrine visit of the year as a family is a tradition in our house.","furigana":"かぞく|はつもうで|い|わ|や|こうれい"}],"notes":"恒例 refers to something that recurs regularly as a fixed custom or tradition, mostly annually: 恒例の行事, 毎年恒例の花火大会. Typical are 毎年恒例の and 〜が恒例になっている \"has become a tradition\". Unlike 定例 (officially fixed dates such as meetings), 恒例 sounds like custom and anticipation. Slightly elevated, but common in the news and in daily life.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"定例の会議が毎週月曜日にある。","romaji":"Teirei no kaigi ga maishū getsuyōbi ni aru.","german":"The regular meeting is held every Monday.","furigana":"ていれい|かいぎ|まいしゅうげつようび"},{"japanese":"大臣は定例の記者会見で新しい方針を発表した。","romaji":"Daijin wa teirei no kisha kaiken de atarashii hōshin o happyō shita.","german":"The minister announced the new policy at his regular press conference.","furigana":"だいじん|ていれい|きしゃかいけん|あたら|ほうしん|はっぴょう"},{"japanese":"今月の定例会は来週の水曜日に変更になりました。","romaji":"Kongetsu no teireikai wa raishū no suiyōbi ni henkō ni narimashita.","german":"This month's regular meeting has been changed to next Wednesday.","furigana":"こんげつ|ていれいかい|らいしゅう|すいようび|へんこう"}],"notes":"定例 means \"taking place regularly according to a fixed schedule\" and concerns mainly official or business appointments: 定例会議, 定例会見, 定例の打ち合わせ. The counterpart is 臨時 \"unscheduled\" (臨時会議). Unlike 恒例, which sounds like custom and tradition, 定例 is sober and official. It is often used as a prefix directly before the noun without の.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"臨時の休業日が設けられた。","romaji":"Rinji no kyūgyōbi ga mōkerareta.","german":"An extraordinary day off was set.","furigana":"りんじ|きゅうぎょうび|もう"},{"japanese":"花火大会の日は臨時の電車が出ます。","romaji":"Hanabi taikai no hi wa rinji no densha ga demasu.","german":"On the day of the fireworks festival, extra trains will run.","furigana":"はなびたいかい|ひ|りんじ|でんしゃ|で"},{"japanese":"今月は臨時の収入があったので、家族で外食した。","romaji":"Kongetsu wa rinji no shūnyū ga atta node, kazoku de gaishoku shita.","german":"I had some unexpected extra income this month, so we ate out as a family.","furigana":"こんげつ|りんじ|しゅうにゅう|かぞく|がいしょく"}],"notes":"臨時 refers to something that is set up additionally or temporarily on a special occasion rather than according to schedule: 臨時休業 \"closed unexpectedly\", 臨時列車 \"special train\", 臨時ニュース \"news flash\", 臨時収入 \"unexpected extra income\". Opposites are 定例 and 定期 \"regular\". For fixed-term work one says 臨時職員; as a prefix, 臨時 often stands without の.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"緊急の連絡が入った。","romaji":"Kinkyū no renraku ga haitta.","german":"An urgent message came in.","furigana":"きんきゅう|れんらく|はい"},{"japanese":"緊急の場合は、この番号に電話してください。","romaji":"Kinkyū no baai wa, kono bangō ni denwa shite kudasai.","german":"In an emergency, please call this number.","furigana":"きんきゅう|ばあい|ばんごう|でんわ"},{"japanese":"部長は緊急の会議のため、出張を中止した。","romaji":"Buchō wa kinkyū no kaigi no tame, shutchō o chūshi shita.","german":"Because of an urgent meeting, the department head canceled his business trip.","furigana":"ぶちょう|きんきゅう|かいぎ|しゅっちょう|ちゅうし"}],"notes":"緊急 means \"urgent, acute, emergency\" and describes a situation that demands immediate action: 緊急の用事, 緊急事態 \"state of emergency\", 緊急連絡先 \"emergency contact\", 緊急地震速報 \"earthquake early warning\". Compared with the everyday 急ぎの \"in a hurry\", 緊急 is more serious and more formal; 至急 \"immediately\" appears more in requests (至急ご連絡ください). As a prefix, 緊急 often stands without の.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"即座の対応が求められる。","romaji":"Sokuza no taiō ga motomerareru.","german":"An immediate response is required.","furigana":"そくざ|たいおう|もと"},{"japanese":"事故の現場では即座の判断が命を救うこともある。","romaji":"Jiko no genba de wa sokuza no handan ga inochi o sukuu koto mo aru.","german":"At the scene of an accident, a split-second decision can sometimes save lives.","furigana":"じこ|げんば|そくざ|はんだん|いのち|すく"},{"japanese":"誘いを受けたが、彼女は即座に断った。","romaji":"Sasoi o uketa ga, kanojo wa sokuza ni kotowatta.","german":"She was invited, but she declined immediately.","furigana":"さそ|う|かのじょ|そくざ|ことわ"}],"notes":"即座 means \"on the spot, immediately\". Most common is the adverbial form 即座に (即座に答える, 即座に断る); attributively with の it sounds written: 即座の判断, 即座の対応. Close in meaning are すぐ (everyday), 直ちに (formal, in instructions) and 即時 (technical, e.g. 即時払い). 即座 stresses that the reaction occurs at that very moment without any hesitation.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"早急な対策が必要だ。","romaji":"Sakkyū na taisaku ga hitsuyō da.","german":"Urgent measures are necessary.","furigana":"さっきゅう|たいさく|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"この件については早急にご返答いただけますか。","romaji":"Kono ken ni tsuite wa sakkyū ni go-hentō itadakemasu ka.","german":"Could you please reply to me promptly about this matter?","furigana":"けん|さっきゅう|へんとう"},{"japanese":"古くなった橋の早急な修理を住民が求めている。","romaji":"Furuku natta hashi no sakkyū na shūri o jūmin ga motomete iru.","german":"Residents are demanding urgent repair of the old bridge.","furigana":"ふる|はし|さっきゅう|しゅうり|じゅうみん|もと"}],"notes":"早急 means \"urgent, as soon as possible\" and appears mainly in formal texts about measures and solutions: 早急な対策, 早急な対応, adverbial 早急に検討する. The reading さっきゅう is the traditional one; そうきゅう is now also widespread and accepted. Unlike 迅速 (which praises the actual speed of an execution), 早急 expresses that something must be done in a hurry.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"迅速な対応をありがとうございます。","romaji":"Jinsoku na taiō o arigatō gozaimasu.","german":"Thank you for your prompt response.","furigana":"じんそく|たいおう"},{"japanese":"消防隊の迅速な行動で、火事はすぐに消された。","romaji":"Shōbōtai no jinsoku na kōdō de, kaji wa sugu ni kesareta.","german":"Thanks to the fire brigade's swift action, the fire was put out quickly.","furigana":"しょうぼうたい|じんそく|こうどう|かじ|け"},{"japanese":"注文した商品が迅速に届いて助かりました。","romaji":"Chūmon shita shōhin ga jinsoku ni todoite tasukarimashita.","german":"The goods I ordered arrived quickly, which was a great help.","furigana":"ちゅうもん|しょうひん|じんそく|とど|たす"}],"notes":"迅速 means \"swift and prompt\" with regard to actions and procedures, not physical speed: 迅速な対応, 迅速な処理, 迅速に行動する. It is a formal word from business and official language; in everyday speech one says すばやい or 早い. Unlike 急速 (rapid change or development), 迅速 praises the speed of a deliberately carried-out response.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"技術の急速な発展に驚いている。","romaji":"Gijutsu no kyūsoku na hatten ni odoroite iru.","german":"I am amazed at the rapid development of technology.","furigana":"ぎじゅつ|きゅうそく|はってん|おどろ"},{"japanese":"インターネットの普及で、情報が急速に広がるようになった。","romaji":"Intānetto no fukyū de, jōhō ga kyūsoku ni hirogaru yō ni natta.","german":"With the spread of the Internet, information now spreads rapidly.","furigana":"ふきゅう|じょうほう|きゅうそく|ひろ"},{"japanese":"この地域では急速な高齢化が進んでいる。","romaji":"Kono chiiki de wa kyūsoku na kōreika ga susunde iru.","german":"Rapid population aging is progressing in this region.","furigana":"ちいき|きゅうそく|こうれいか|すす"}],"notes":"急速 describes a very fast change or development: 急速な発展, 急速な成長, 急速に広がる, 急速に冷える. It typically appears with processes such as growth, spread or change, not with the actions of people - for those, 迅速 is used. Neutral to slightly formal; 急激, by contrast, stresses a sudden, often undesirable change (急激な変化).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"着実に成果を上げている。","romaji":"Chakujitsu ni seika o agete iru.","german":"He is steadily producing results.","furigana":"ちゃくじつ|せいか|あ"},{"japanese":"工事は予定どおり着実に進んでいる。","romaji":"Kōji wa yotei dōri chakujitsu ni susunde iru.","german":"The construction work is progressing steadily as scheduled.","furigana":"こうじ|よてい|ちゃくじつ|すす"},{"japanese":"語学は毎日の着実な努力が大切だ。","romaji":"Gogaku wa mainichi no chakujitsu na doryoku ga taisetsu da.","german":"In language learning, steady daily effort is important.","furigana":"ごがく|まいにち|ちゃくじつ|どりょく|たいせつ"}],"notes":"着実 means \"steady, step by step and reliable\": progress is made not in leaps but surely. Often adverbial: 着実に進む, 着実に成果を上げる, 着実に力をつける. Unlike 堅実 (cautious, low-risk, solid), 着実 puts the emphasis on even progress. In everyday conversation, similarly, one says こつこつ \"persistently, bit by bit\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"堅実な経営方針で信頼を得ている。","romaji":"Kenjitsu na keiei hōshin de shinrai o ete iru.","german":"The company has earned trust with its sound management policy.","furigana":"けんじつ|けいえいほうしん|しんらい|え"},{"japanese":"彼女は無駄遣いをしない堅実な人だ。","romaji":"Kanojo wa mudazukai o shinai kenjitsu na hito da.","german":"She is a sensible person who doesn't waste money.","furigana":"かのじょ|むだづか|けんじつ|ひと"},{"japanese":"若いうちから堅実に貯金している。","romaji":"Wakai uchi kara kenjitsu ni chokin shite iru.","german":"He has been saving money sensibly since he was young.","furigana":"わか|けんじつ|ちょきん"}],"notes":"堅実 means \"solid, sensible, low-risk\" and praises an attitude that relies on safety rather than speculation: 堅実な経営, 堅実な生活, 堅実な人, 堅実に貯金する. In contrast to 着実 (even progress), 堅実 stresses reliability and caution. It is mostly positive, but can also carry a hint of \"a bit tame, without risk-taking\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二つの作業を同時に進める。","romaji":"Futatsu no sagyō o dōji ni susumeru.","german":"I am carrying out two tasks at the same time.","furigana":"ふた|さぎょう|どうじ|すす"},{"japanese":"ドアが開くと同時に、子どもたちが走り出した。","romaji":"Doa ga hiraku to dōji ni, kodomotachi ga hashiridashita.","german":"The moment the door opened, the children ran out.","furigana":"ひら|どうじ|こ|はし|だ"},{"japanese":"卒業はうれしいと同時に、少し寂しい気もする。","romaji":"Sotsugyō wa ureshii to dōji ni, sukoshi sabishii ki mo suru.","german":"I am glad to graduate, but at the same time I feel a little sad.","furigana":"そつぎょう|どうじ|すこ|さび|き"}],"notes":"同時に means \"at the same time\" and has three uses: As an adverb it describes simultaneous events (二つのことを同時にする); after a verb, with と同時に, it means \"at the same moment as ...\" (ベルが鳴ると同時に). After adjectives or at the start of a sentence it means \"at the same time, on the other hand\": うれしいと同時に寂しい. In conversation one also says いっぺんに; 一斉に, by contrast, means \"all at once\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"順次対応していきます。","romaji":"Junji taiō shite ikimasu.","german":"We will respond in order.","furigana":"じゅんじたいおう"},{"japanese":"ご注文の商品は、入荷しだい順次発送いたします。","romaji":"Go-chūmon no shōhin wa, nyūka shidai junji hassō itashimasu.","german":"The items you ordered will be shipped one after another as soon as they arrive in stock.","furigana":"ちゅうもん|しょうひん|にゅうか|じゅんじはっそう"},{"japanese":"新しいシステムは来月から各支店に順次導入される。","romaji":"Atarashii shisutemu wa raigetsu kara kaku shiten ni junji dōnyū sareru.","german":"The new system will be introduced to each branch in turn from next month.","furigana":"あたら|らいげつ|かくしてん|じゅんじどうにゅう"}],"notes":"順次 means \"one after another, in order, step by step\" and is typical of formal announcements by companies and authorities: 順次発送いたします \"will be shipped gradually\", 順次対応いたします. It usually stands directly before the verb without a particle. In everyday speech one says 順番に or 一つずつ instead; 順次 stresses less a fixed order than working through things step by step.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"順番に名前を呼びます。","romaji":"Junban ni namae o yobimasu.","german":"I will call the names in order.","furigana":"じゅんばん|なまえ|よ"},{"japanese":"トイレは混んでいるので、順番に並んでください。","romaji":"Toire wa konde iru node, junban ni narande kudasai.","german":"The restroom is crowded, so please line up in order.","furigana":"こ|じゅんばん|なら"},{"japanese":"兄弟で順番にゲームを使うことにした。","romaji":"Kyōdai de junban ni gēmu o tsukau koto ni shita.","german":"The siblings decided to take turns using the game.","furigana":"きょうだい|じゅんばん|つか"}],"notes":"順番に means \"in order, one after another\" and is the everyday form: 順番に並ぶ, 順番に話す, 順番に使う. The noun 順番 means \"order, turn\": 順番を待つ \"to wait for one's turn\", 私の順番だ \"it's my turn\". More formal and more written is 順次; 交代で, by contrast, emphasizes taking over from one another in an activity.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 46"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"赤と青を交互に並べた。","romaji":"Aka to ao o kōgo ni narabeta.","german":"I arranged red and blue alternately.","furigana":"あか|あお|こうご|なら"},{"japanese":"肉と野菜を交互に串に刺して焼きます。","romaji":"Niku to yasai o kōgo ni kushi ni sashite yakimasu.","german":"Skewer meat and vegetables alternately and grill them.","furigana":"にく|やさい|こうご|くし|さ|や"},{"japanese":"長距離のドライブなので、父と交互に運転した。","romaji":"Chōkyori no doraibu na node, chichi to kōgo ni unten shita.","german":"Since it was a long drive, my father and I took turns driving.","furigana":"ちょうきょり|ちち|こうご|うんてん"}],"notes":"交互に means \"alternately\" in the sense of a regular switching between two (or more) things: AとBを交互に並べる, 左右の足を交互に出す. Compared with 順番に (in order, several participants) and 交代で (taking turns at an activity), 交互に stresses the A-B-A-B pattern. Neutral style, also in instructions and recipes.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"互いに助け合うことが大切だ。","romaji":"Tagai ni tasukeau koto ga taisetsu da.","german":"It is important to help each other.","furigana":"たが|たす|あ|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"二人は互いに顔を見合わせて笑った。","romaji":"Futari wa tagai ni kao o miawasete waratta.","german":"The two looked at each other and laughed.","furigana":"ふたり|たが|かお|みあ|わら"},{"japanese":"チームのメンバーは互いに競い合いながら成長した。","romaji":"Chīmu no menbā wa tagai ni kisoiai nagara seichō shita.","german":"The team members grew by competing with one another.","furigana":"たが|きそ|あ|せいちょう"}],"notes":"互いに means \"mutually, each other\" and often appears with verbs in 〜合う: 互いに助け合う, 互いに理解し合う. In conversation one more often says お互いに or お互い (お互い様 \"we're in the same boat\"); the bare 互いに seems somewhat more written. The formal synonym 相互に belongs more in specialist texts and contracts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"相互に情報を共有する。","romaji":"Sōgo ni jōhō o kyōyū suru.","german":"We share information with one another.","furigana":"そうご|じょうほう|きょうゆう"},{"japanese":"両国は経済面で相互に協力することで合意した。","romaji":"Ryōkoku wa keizaimen de sōgo ni kyōryoku suru koto de gōi shita.","german":"The two countries agreed to cooperate economically with each other.","furigana":"りょうこく|けいざいめん|そうご|きょうりょく|ごうい"},{"japanese":"気温と湿度は相互に影響し合っている。","romaji":"Kion to shitsudo wa sōgo ni eikyō shiatte iru.","german":"Temperature and humidity influence each other.","furigana":"きおん|しつど|そうご|えいきょう|あ"}],"notes":"相互に means \"reciprocally, mutually\" and is the formal, written counterpart of 互いに. It appears in contracts, reports and specialist texts: 相互に協力する, 相互に影響し合う; as a prefix in compounds such as 相互理解 \"mutual understanding\" or 相互作用 \"interaction\". In conversation, お互いに sounds more natural.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"各自で昼食をとってください。","romaji":"Kakuji de chūshoku o totte kudasai.","german":"Please have lunch on your own.","furigana":"かくじ|ちゅうしょく"},{"japanese":"旅行の費用は各自が負担することになった。","romaji":"Ryokō no hiyō wa kakuji ga futan suru koto ni natta.","german":"It was decided that each person will bear their own travel costs.","furigana":"りょこう|ひよう|かくじ|ふたん"},{"japanese":"貴重品は各自で管理してください。","romaji":"Kichōhin wa kakuji de kanri shite kudasai.","german":"Please look after your own valuables.","furigana":"きちょうひん|かくじ|かんり"}],"notes":"各自 means \"each individual, each for themselves\" and is used mainly in instructions to a group: 各自で準備する, 昼食は各自でお願いします \"please arrange lunch yourselves\", 各自の責任. It stands as a noun with の or adverbially, often with で. Similar are それぞれ (more neutral, also for things) and 一人一人 (stresses the individual person); 各自 sounds like an administrative or school instruction.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"個別に対応させていただきます。","romaji":"Kobetsu ni taiō sasete itadakimasu.","german":"We will respond to you individually.","furigana":"こべつ|たいおう"},{"japanese":"お菓子は一つずつ個別に包装されている。","romaji":"Okashi wa hitotsu zutsu kobetsu ni hōsō sarete iru.","german":"The sweets are individually wrapped, one by one.","furigana":"かし|ひと|こべつ|ほうそう"},{"japanese":"成績について、先生が生徒と個別に話をした。","romaji":"Seiseki ni tsuite, sensei ga seito to kobetsu ni hanashi o shita.","german":"The teacher talked with each student individually about their grades.","furigana":"せいせき|せんせい|せいと|こべつ|はなし"}],"notes":"個別に means \"individually, separately\" - each case or person is dealt with on its own: 個別に対応する, 個別に相談する, 個別に包装する. The counterpart is まとめて or, formally, 一括して \"collectively, all at once\". As a prefix, 個別 forms compounds such as 個別指導 \"one-on-one instruction\" or 個別面談 \"individual meeting\". Neutral to formal, common in business language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"全体的に見れば良い結果だった。","romaji":"Zentaiteki ni mireba yoi kekka datta.","german":"Overall, it was a good result.","furigana":"ぜんたいてき|み|よ|けっか"},{"japanese":"この部屋は全体的に明るい色でまとめられている。","romaji":"Kono heya wa zentaiteki ni akarui iro de matomerarete iru.","german":"This room is done in light colors overall.","furigana":"へや|ぜんたいてき|あか|いろ"},{"japanese":"レポートは全体的によく書けているが、結論が弱い。","romaji":"Repōto wa zentaiteki ni yoku kakete iru ga, ketsuron ga yowai.","german":"The report is well written overall, but the conclusion is weak.","furigana":"ぜんたいてき|か|けつろん|よわ"}],"notes":"全体的に means \"overall, by and large\" and sums up a general impression: 全体的に見ると, 全体的に良い, 全体的に暗い色. A qualification often follows: 全体的にはいいが、細かいミスがある. The counterpart is 部分的に \"partially\". Close in meaning is the somewhat more formal 全般的に; in conversation one also says だいたい.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"部分的に修正する必要がある。","romaji":"Bubunteki ni shūsei suru hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"It needs to be corrected in part.","furigana":"ぶぶんてき|しゅうせい|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"彼の意見は部分的には正しいと思う。","romaji":"Kare no iken wa bubunteki ni wa tadashii to omou.","german":"I think his opinion is correct in part.","furigana":"かれ|いけん|ぶぶんてき|ただ|おも"},{"japanese":"台風で、屋根が部分的に壊れてしまった。","romaji":"Taifū de, yane ga bubunteki ni kowarete shimatta.","german":"The typhoon partially damaged the roof.","furigana":"たいふう|やね|ぶぶんてき|こわ"}],"notes":"部分的に means \"partially, in places\" and restricts a statement to one part: 部分的に修正する, 部分的に正しい, 部分的に壊れる. The counterpart is 全体的に. In everyday speech one often says 一部 (一部だけ, 一部の人); 部分的に is somewhat more objective and typical of reports, evaluations and instructions.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"局所的に大雨が降っている。","romaji":"Kyokushoteki ni ōame ga futte iru.","german":"Heavy rain is falling locally.","furigana":"きょくしょてき|おおあめ|ふ"},{"japanese":"この薬は痛む部分に局所的に使います。","romaji":"Kono kusuri wa itamu bubun ni kyokushoteki ni tsukaimasu.","german":"This medicine is applied locally to the painful area.","furigana":"くすり|いた|ぶぶん|きょくしょてき|つか"},{"japanese":"地震の被害は局所的に集中していた。","romaji":"Jishin no higai wa kyokushoteki ni shūchū shite ita.","german":"The earthquake damage was concentrated in localized areas.","furigana":"じしん|ひがい|きょくしょてき|しゅうちゅう"}],"notes":"局所的に means \"locally, in a limited area\" and comes from technical and news language: in weather reports (局所的に激しい雨 \"locally heavy rain\") and in medicine (局所的に痛む, 局所麻酔 \"local anesthesia\"). In everyday speech one more often says ところどころ or 一部の地域で. Compared with 部分的に, 局所的に refers to a spatially narrowly limited area.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"考え方を根本的に変える必要がある。","romaji":"Kangaekata o konponteki ni kaeru hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"We need to change our way of thinking fundamentally.","furigana":"かんが|かた|こんぽんてき|か|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"この問題を根本的に解決するには時間がかかる。","romaji":"Kono mondai o konponteki ni kaiketsu suru ni wa jikan ga kakaru.","german":"It will take time to solve this problem at its root.","furigana":"もんだい|こんぽんてき|かいけつ|じかん"},{"japanese":"二人の意見は根本的に異なっている。","romaji":"Futari no iken wa konponteki ni kotonatte iru.","german":"The opinions of the two differ fundamentally.","furigana":"ふたり|いけん|こんぽんてき|こと"}],"notes":"根本的に means \"fundamentally, from the ground up\" and aims at the root (根本) of a matter: 根本的に見直す, 根本的に解決する, 根本的に違う. Typical is the contrast with superficial fixes: 一時的な対策ではなく根本的な解決. Close in meaning is 抜本的 (more formal, mainly for reforms); 本質的に, by contrast, describes the essence of a thing, not the extent of a change.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この二つの問題は本質的に同じだ。","romaji":"Kono futatsu no mondai wa honshitsuteki ni onaji da.","german":"These two problems are essentially the same.","furigana":"ふた|もんだい|ほんしつてき|おな"},{"japanese":"人間は本質的に社会的な生き物だ。","romaji":"Ningen wa honshitsuteki ni shakaiteki na ikimono da.","german":"Human beings are by nature social creatures.","furigana":"にんげん|ほんしつてき|しゃかいてき|い|もの"},{"japanese":"彼の提案は本質的な問題を解決していない。","romaji":"Kare no teian wa honshitsuteki na mondai o kaiketsu shite inai.","german":"His proposal does not solve the essential problem.","furigana":"かれ|ていあん|ほんしつてき|もんだい|かいけつ"}],"notes":"本質的に means \"in essence, basically\" and refers to the actual nature (本質) of a thing, regardless of external differences: 本質的に同じ, 本質的に異なる, 本質的な問題 \"the real problem\". The counterpart is 表面的に \"superficially\". It sounds matter-of-fact to academic; it differs from 根本的に in that it describes the essence, not the extent of a change.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"表面的にしか理解していない。","romaji":"Hyōmenteki ni shika rikai shite inai.","german":"I only understand it superficially.","furigana":"ひょうめんてき|りかい"},{"japanese":"あの二人は表面的には仲が良さそうに見える。","romaji":"Ano futari wa hyōmenteki ni wa naka ga yosasō ni mieru.","german":"On the surface, those two seem to get along well.","furigana":"ふたり|ひょうめんてき|なか|よ|み"},{"japanese":"表面的な対策だけでは、事故は防げない。","romaji":"Hyōmenteki na taisaku dake de wa, jiko wa fusegenai.","german":"Mere superficial measures cannot prevent accidents.","furigana":"ひょうめんてき|たいさく|じこ|ふせ"}],"notes":"表面的に means \"superficially, only in outward appearance\": 表面的に理解する, 表面的には仲がいい \"on the surface they get along well\". It often appears with は or しか and hints that things look different underneath. The counterpart is 本質的に or 深く. Close in meaning, but more colloquial and more disparaging, is うわべだけ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"形式的な挨拶を交わした。","romaji":"Keishikiteki na aisatsu o kawashita.","german":"We exchanged formal greetings.","furigana":"けいしきてき|あいさつ|か"},{"japanese":"会議は形式的に開かれただけで、何も決まらなかった。","romaji":"Kaigi wa keishikiteki ni hirakareta dake de, nani mo kimaranakatta.","german":"The meeting was only held as a formality, and nothing was decided.","furigana":"かいぎ|けいしきてき|ひら|なに|き"},{"japanese":"入社前に形式的に面接がありますが、心配はいりません。","romaji":"Nyūsha mae ni keishikiteki ni mensetsu ga arimasu ga, shinpai wa irimasen.","german":"There is an interview before you join the company, but it's just a formality, so don't worry.","furigana":"にゅうしゃまえ|けいしきてき|めんせつ|しんぱい"}],"notes":"形式的に means \"purely formally, for form's sake\": something is done because it is a rule or custom, without real substance - 形式的に確認する, 形式的な手続き, 形式的な挨拶. It often has a slightly critical undertone (\"just pro forma\"). The counterpart is 実質的に \"in substance\". For \"formal\" in the sense of etiquette, one says 改まった or フォーマルな.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"実質的にはリーダーの役割を果たしている。","romaji":"Jisshitsuteki ni wa rīdā no yakuwari o hatashite iru.","german":"In practice, he fulfills the role of leader.","furigana":"じっしつてき|やくわり|は"},{"japanese":"このサービスはポイントを使えば実質的に無料だ。","romaji":"Kono sābisu wa pointo o tsukaeba jisshitsuteki ni muryō da.","german":"This service is effectively free if you use points.","furigana":"つか|じっしつてき|むりょう"},{"japanese":"給料は上がったが、物価も上がったので実質的には変わらない。","romaji":"Kyūryō wa agatta ga, bukka mo agatta node jisshitsuteki ni wa kawaranai.","german":"My salary went up, but prices also went up, so in real terms nothing has changed.","furigana":"きゅうりょう|あ|ぶっか|あ|じっしつてき|か"}],"notes":"実質的に means \"in substance, in fact, effectively\" and contrasts the actual state with the formal one: 実質的に無料, 実質的には部長が決めている. The counterpart is 形式的に or 名目上. Very close is 事実上, which stresses more \"de facto, although not official\". Often with は for emphasis: 実質的には.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"事実上の引退を表明した。","romaji":"Jijitsujō no intai o hyōmei shita.","german":"He announced his de facto retirement.","furigana":"じじつじょう|いんたい|ひょうめい"},{"japanese":"この計画は予算不足で事実上中止になった。","romaji":"Kono keikaku wa yosan busoku de jijitsujō chūshi ni natta.","german":"This plan was effectively canceled due to a lack of budget.","furigana":"けいかく|よさんぶそく|じじつじょうちゅうし"},{"japanese":"この時間に会場に着くのは事実上不可能だ。","romaji":"Kono jikan ni kaijō ni tsuku no wa jijitsujō fukanō da.","german":"It is practically impossible to reach the venue at this time.","furigana":"じかん|かいじょう|つ|じじつじょうふかのう"}],"notes":"事実上 means \"de facto, in fact, practically\": something holds in reality even if it has not been declared officially. Adverbially it stands without a particle (事実上不可能だ), attributively with の (事実上の引退, 事実上のトップ). Very close is 実質的に; 事実上 sounds like news and written language. In everyday speech one more often says ほとんど or 実際には.","levelNote":"Compound: 事実 (N3) + 上 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"名目上は社長だが実権はない。","romaji":"Meimokujō wa shachō da ga jikken wa nai.","german":"He is president in name only and has no real power.","furigana":"めいもくじょう|しゃちょう|じっけん"},{"japanese":"名目上は研修だが、実際はただの観光旅行だった。","romaji":"Meimokujō wa kenshū da ga, jissai wa tada no kankō ryokō datta.","german":"Officially it was training, but in reality it was just a sightseeing trip.","furigana":"めいもくじょう|けんしゅう|じっさい|かんこうりょこう"},{"japanese":"彼は名目上の代表にすぎない。","romaji":"Kare wa meimokujō no daihyō ni suginai.","german":"He is nothing more than a nominal representative.","furigana":"かれ|めいもくじょう|だいひょう"}],"notes":"名目上 means \"nominally, in name only\": a title or purpose exists officially but does not correspond to reality. Typical is the contrast with 実際は or 実権: 名目上は社長だが実権はない. Attributively it takes の (名目上の代表), adverbially often は. The counterpart is 実質的に or 事実上; related is 建前上, which refers more to the official stance than to the title.","levelNote":"Compound: 名 (N3) + 目上 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"建前上は賛成だが本心は反対だ。","romaji":"Tatemaejō wa sansei da ga honshin wa hantai da.","german":"In principle I agree, but in my heart I am opposed.","furigana":"たてまえじょう|さんせい|ほんしん|はんたい"},{"japanese":"建前上、この会社では残業は禁止されている。","romaji":"Tatemaejō, kono kaisha de wa zangyō wa kinshi sarete iru.","german":"Officially, overtime is prohibited at this company.","furigana":"たてまえじょう|かいしゃ|ざんぎょう|きんし"},{"japanese":"建前上は誰でも参加できるが、実際は会員しか来ない。","romaji":"Tatemaejō wa dare demo sanka dekiru ga, jissai wa kaiin shika konai.","german":"In principle anyone can take part, but in reality only members come.","furigana":"たてまえじょう|だれ|さんか|じっさい|かいいん|こ"}],"notes":"建前上 means \"officially, on the face of it\" and refers to 建前, the publicly held stance, as opposed to 本音 \"one's true intention\". Typical is the contrast 建前上は〜だが、本音は〜 or 実際は〜. It is often used with a slightly critical tone when rules or statements apply only formally. Closely related are 表向きは and 名目上 (which refers more to titles or purposes).","levelNote":"Compound: 建前 (N2) + 上 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"原則として月曜日は休みです。","romaji":"Gensoku to shite getsuyōbi wa yasumi desu.","german":"As a rule, we are closed on Mondays.","furigana":"げんそく|げつようび|やす"},{"japanese":"セール品は原則として返品できません。","romaji":"Sēru-hin wa gensoku to shite henpin dekimasen.","german":"Sale items cannot, as a rule, be returned.","furigana":"ひん|げんそく|へんぴん"},{"japanese":"レポートは原則として手書きではなく、パソコンで作成すること。","romaji":"Repōto wa gensoku to shite tegaki de wa naku, pasokon de sakusei suru koto.","german":"As a rule, reports must be created on a computer, not by hand.","furigana":"げんそく|てが|さくせい"}],"notes":"原則として means \"in principle, as a rule\" and announces a rule to which exceptions may exist: 原則として〜とする, 原則として認めない. A note on the exception with ただし often follows. Equivalent are 原則的に and, short, 原則 (原則、返品不可). Matter-of-fact and formal, typical of rules, notices and terms and conditions; in conversation one says 基本的に more often.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今回は例外的に許可する。","romaji":"Konkai wa reigaiteki ni kyoka suru.","german":"I will allow it as an exception this time.","furigana":"こんかい|れいがいてき|きょか"},{"japanese":"今年の冬は例外的に暖かかった。","romaji":"Kotoshi no fuyu wa reigaiteki ni atatakakatta.","german":"This winter was exceptionally warm.","furigana":"ことし|ふゆ|れいがいてき|あたた"},{"japanese":"病気など例外的な場合を除き、欠席は認められない。","romaji":"Byōki nado reigaiteki na baai o nozoki, kesseki wa mitomerarenai.","german":"Except for exceptional cases such as illness, absence is not accepted.","furigana":"びょうき|れいがいてき|ばあい|のぞ|けっせき|みと"}],"notes":"例外的に means \"as an exception\" and is used when a rule is departed from: 例外的に認める, 例外的に許可する, often with 今回は. It can also mean \"exceptionally, unusually\": 例外的に暖かい冬. In conversation 特別に or 今回だけ sounds more natural; 例外的に is more matter-of-fact and stresses that a rule exists. Attributively: 例外的な措置 \"exceptional measure\", 例外的なケース.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"特別に割引をしてもらった。","romaji":"Tokubetsu ni waribiki o shite moratta.","german":"I was given a special discount.","furigana":"とくべつ|わりびき"},{"japanese":"今日はお客様の誕生日なので、特別にケーキを用意しました。","romaji":"Kyō wa okyakusama no tanjōbi na node, tokubetsu ni kēki o yōi shimashita.","german":"Since today is your birthday, we have specially prepared a cake.","furigana":"きょう|きゃくさま|たんじょうび|とくべつ|ようい"},{"japanese":"特別に好きな食べ物はないが、嫌いなものもない。","romaji":"Tokubetsu ni suki na tabemono wa nai ga, kirai na mono mo nai.","german":"I don't have any particular favorite food, but there is nothing I dislike either.","furigana":"とくべつ|す|た|もの|きら"}],"notes":"特別に means \"specially, extra, as an exception\" and sets something apart from the norm: 特別に許可する, 特別に作ってもらう, 特別に安くする. It often implies that it is a favor (今日だけ特別に). Negated, it means \"not particularly\": 特別に好きなわけではない. Unlike 特に (\"above all\", singling out from a group), 特別に describes special treatment.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今年は殊に暑い夏だった。","romaji":"Kotoshi wa koto ni atsui natsu datta.","german":"This year was an especially hot summer.","furigana":"ことし|こと|あつ|なつ"},{"japanese":"この作家の作品の中では、殊に初期の短編が好きだ。","romaji":"Kono sakka no sakuhin no naka de wa, koto ni shoki no tanpen ga suki da.","german":"Among this author's works, I especially like the early short stories.","furigana":"さっか|さくひん|なか|こと|しょき|たんぺん|す"},{"japanese":"冬の京都は観光客が少なく、殊に朝は静かだ。","romaji":"Fuyu no Kyōto wa kankōkyaku ga sukunaku, koto ni asa wa shizuka da.","german":"In winter there are few tourists in Kyōto, and mornings in particular are quiet.","furigana":"ふゆ|きょうと|かんこうきゃく|すく|こと|あさ|しず"}],"notes":"殊に means \"especially, above all\" and is a written-language variant of 特に. It stands before adjectives or highlights one element: 殊に暑い, 殊に印象的だった. In conversation 特に sounds more natural; 殊に is found in essays, speeches and literature, often also in kana (ことに). Do not confuse with 〜ことに after adjectives (残念なことに \"regrettably\"), which is a different pattern.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本料理の中でとりわけ寿司が好きだ。","romaji":"Nihon ryōri no naka de toriwake sushi ga suki da.","german":"Of Japanese food, I especially like sushi.","furigana":"にほんりょうり|なか|すし|す"},{"japanese":"今年の冬はとりわけ寒さが厳しかった。","romaji":"Kotoshi no fuyu wa toriwake samusa ga kibishikatta.","german":"This winter was particularly harsh.","furigana":"ことし|ふゆ|さむ|きび"},{"japanese":"彼はクラスの中でもとりわけ数学がよくできる。","romaji":"Kare wa kurasu no naka demo toriwake sūgaku ga yoku dekiru.","german":"In class he stands out above all in mathematics.","furigana":"かれ|なか|すうがく"}],"notes":"とりわけ means \"particularly, above all\" and singles out one element from a group; it is slightly more elevated than 特に but also common in conversation. It is usually written in kana (取り分け as a noun means \"portioning out food\"). Typical: 中でもとりわけ, とりわけ〜が好きだ, 今年はとりわけ寒い. Synonymous but more written is 殊に.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"果物の中でも桃が一番好きだ。","romaji":"Kudamono no naka demo momo ga ichiban suki da.","german":"Unter den Früchten mag ich Pfirsiche am liebsten.","furigana":"くだもの|なか|もも|いちばんす"},{"japanese":"旅行でいろいろな町を回ったが、中でも金沢が印象に残っている。","romaji":"Ryokō de iroiro na machi o mawatta ga, naka demo Kanazawa ga inshō ni nokotte iru.","german":"Auf der Reise habe ich viele Städte besucht, aber vor allem Kanazawa ist mir in Erinnerung geblieben.","furigana":"りょこう|まち|まわ|なか|かなざわ|いんしょう|のこ"},{"japanese":"うちの社員は皆まじめだが、中でも田中さんは一番頼りになる。","romaji":"Uchi no shain wa mina majime da ga, naka demo Tanaka-san wa ichiban tayori ni naru.","german":"Unsere Mitarbeiter sind alle gewissenhaft, aber am meisten kann man sich auf Herrn Tanaka verlassen.","furigana":"しゃいん|みな|なか|たなか|いちばんたよ"}],"notes":"中でも heißt „vor allem, besonders unter …“ und hebt aus einer genannten Gruppe das herausragende Element hervor: 〜の中でも, 中でも特に. Steht die Gruppe schon im Satz davor, kann es am Satzanfang stehen: 日本の町はどこも好きだが、中でも京都が好きだ. Gegenüber とりわけ und 殊に ist 中でも neutral und alltagstauglich. Das deutsche „unter anderem“ trifft es nicht, da 中でも den Spitzenreiter betont.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"多くの課題の中でなかんずく教育が重要だ。","romaji":"Ōku no kadai no naka de nakanzuku kyōiku ga jūyō da.","german":"Among the many issues, education is especially important.","furigana":"おお|かだい|なか|きょういく|じゅうよう"},{"japanese":"彼は芸術、なかんずく音楽に深い関心を持っていた。","romaji":"Kare wa geijutsu, nakanzuku ongaku ni fukai kanshin o motte ita.","german":"He had a deep interest in art, especially music.","furigana":"かれ|げいじゅつ|おんがく|ふか|かんしん|も"},{"japanese":"アジア諸国、なかんずく中国との関係が重要になっている。","romaji":"Ajia shokoku, nakanzuku Chūgoku to no kankei ga jūyō ni natte iru.","german":"Relations with Asian countries, especially China, are becoming important.","furigana":"しょこく|ちゅうごく|かんけい|じゅうよう"}],"notes":"なかんずく means \"above all, most especially\" and is a very literary, archaic word (derived from 中に就く). It appears almost only in speeches, essays and elevated texts and is written in kana; typical is the form A、なかんずくB \"A, especially B\". In normal conversation it is replaced by 中でも, 特に or とりわけ. For learners it is enough to recognize the word when reading.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"健康が何より大切だ。","romaji":"Kenkō ga nani yori taisetsu da.","german":"Health is more important than anything else.","furigana":"けんこう|なに|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"お元気そうで何よりです。","romaji":"O-genki sō de nani yori desu.","german":"I'm glad to see that you are well.","furigana":"げんき|なに"},{"japanese":"旅行では、何より現地の料理を楽しみにしている。","romaji":"Ryokō de wa, nani yori genchi no ryōri o tanoshimi ni shite iru.","german":"On the trip, I'm looking forward above all to the local food.","furigana":"りょこう|なに|げんち|りょうり|たの"}],"notes":"何より means \"more than anything else, above all\": 何より大切だ, 何より嬉しい; 何よりも strengthens it further. As an expression it means \"the best, the nicest thing\": お元気そうで何よりです \"I'm glad to see you are well\" is a very common polite phrase. Unlike 特に, 何より does not single something out from a named group but places something above everything else. Neutral; usual in conversation as well as in letters.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"節約したつもりが逆に出費が増えた。","romaji":"Setsuyaku shita tsumori ga gyaku ni shuppi ga fueta.","german":"I meant to save money, but my expenses actually went up.","furigana":"せつやく|ぎゃく|しゅっぴ|ふ"},{"japanese":"彼女に謝ろうとしたら、逆に謝られてしまった。","romaji":"Kanojo ni ayamarō to shitara, gyaku ni ayamararete shimatta.","german":"I tried to apologize to her, but she ended up apologizing to me instead.","furigana":"かのじょ|あやま|ぎゃく|あやま"},{"japanese":"逆に言えば、今がチャンスということだ。","romaji":"Gyaku ni ieba, ima ga chansu to iu koto da.","german":"Conversely, this means that now is our chance.","furigana":"ぎゃく|い|いま"}],"notes":"逆に means \"conversely, on the contrary\" and marks that a result or viewpoint runs counter to what was expected: 逆に〜になった, 逆に言えば \"put the other way\". In conversation 逆に is used very often, partly almost as a filler (逆にいいかも \"maybe even better\"). かえって is similar when an action brings about the opposite; 反対に means more the opposite direction or side.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"予想とは反対に売上が伸びた。","romaji":"Yosō to wa hantai ni uriage ga nobita.","german":"Contrary to expectations, sales rose.","furigana":"よそう|はんたい|うりあげ|の"},{"japanese":"子どもが靴を左右反対に履いている。","romaji":"Kodomo ga kutsu o sayū hantai ni haite iru.","german":"The child is wearing the shoes on the wrong feet.","furigana":"こ|くつ|さゆうはんたい|は"},{"japanese":"夏はよく売れるが、反対に冬はほとんど売れない商品だ。","romaji":"Natsu wa yoku ureru ga, hantai ni fuyu wa hotondo urenai shōhin da.","german":"This is a product that sells well in summer but, on the other hand, hardly sells at all in winter.","furigana":"なつ|う|はんたい|ふゆ|う|しょうひん"}],"notes":"反対に means \"conversely, on the contrary, the opposite way\": 予想とは反対に \"contrary to expectations\". It can mean literally a direction or side (靴を左右反対に履く \"to put the shoes on the wrong way round\") or express a contrast between two statements. 逆に is more common in conversation and stresses more the surprising reversal. Note: the noun 反対 also means \"opposition, objection\" (〜に反対する).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"兄とは対照的に弟はおとなしい。","romaji":"Ani to wa taishōteki ni otōto wa otonashii.","german":"In contrast to his older brother, the younger brother is quiet.","furigana":"あに|たいしょうてき|おとうと"},{"japanese":"都市の人口が増えるのとは対照的に、農村の人口は減っている。","romaji":"Toshi no jinkō ga fueru no to wa taishōteki ni, nōson no jinkō wa hette iru.","german":"In contrast to the growing populations of cities, the populations of farming villages are declining.","furigana":"とし|じんこう|ふ|たいしょうてき|のうそん|じんこう|へ"},{"japanese":"白い壁と黒い家具が対照的で美しい。","romaji":"Shiroi kabe to kuroi kagu ga taishōteki de utsukushii.","german":"The white walls and black furniture make a beautiful contrast.","furigana":"しろ|かべ|くろ|かぐ|たいしょうてき|うつく"}],"notes":"対照的に means \"in contrast, completely differently\" and sets two things with clearly opposite characteristics against each other: AとはBは対照的に〜, 対照的な性格. It stresses the visible contrast, not a reversal of expectation (for which 逆に is used). Neutral to slightly written; in conversation one often says 〜とは違って or それに比べて.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"皮肉にも彼の忠告が正しかった。","romaji":"Hiniku ni mo kare no chūkoku ga tadashikatta.","german":"Ironically, his advice turned out to be right.","furigana":"ひにく|かれ|ちゅうこく|ただ"},{"japanese":"安全のために作った規則が、皮肉にも事故の原因になった。","romaji":"Anzen no tame ni tsukutta kisoku ga, hiniku ni mo jiko no gen'in ni natta.","german":"The rule made for safety ironically became the cause of an accident.","furigana":"あんぜん|つく|きそく|ひにく|じこ|げんいん"},{"japanese":"健康に気をつけていた医者が、皮肉にも過労で倒れた。","romaji":"Kenkō ni ki o tsukete ita isha ga, hiniku ni mo karō de taoreta.","german":"The doctor who took such care of his health ironically collapsed from overwork.","furigana":"けんこう|き|いしゃ|ひにく|かろう|たお"}],"notes":"皮肉にも means \"ironically\" and comments that a result paradoxically contradicts what was expected or intended. It usually stands at the beginning of the sentence or before the predicate: 皮肉にも〜結果になった. The noun 皮肉 means \"irony, sarcasm\" (皮肉を言う \"to make snide remarks\"); 皮肉な結果 is \"an ironic result\". Slightly elevated, common in narratives and reports.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"意図的に情報を隠していた。","romaji":"Itoteki ni jōhō o kakushite ita.","german":"He was deliberately hiding information.","furigana":"いとてき|じょうほう|かく"},{"japanese":"デザイナーは意図的に余白を多く残している。","romaji":"Dezainā wa itoteki ni yohaku o ōku nokoshite iru.","german":"The designer deliberately leaves a lot of white space.","furigana":"いとてき|よはく|おお|のこ"},{"japanese":"審判は、選手が意図的にぶつかったと判断した。","romaji":"Shinpan wa, senshu ga itoteki ni butsukatta to handan shita.","german":"The referee judged that the player collided intentionally.","furigana":"しんぱん|せんしゅ|いとてき|はんだん"}],"notes":"意図的に means \"intentionally, deliberately, on purpose\" and stresses that a plan or aim lies behind an action: 意図的に隠す, 意図的に避ける, 意図的なミス. In conversation one mostly says わざと, which often has a negative flavor. The counterpart is 無意識に or うっかり. 意図的に is matter-of-fact and appears in reports, news and analyses, also neutrally for conscious design decisions.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"無意識にため息をついていた。","romaji":"Muishiki ni tameiki o tsuite ita.","german":"I sighed without realizing it.","furigana":"むいしき|いき"},{"japanese":"考え事をしていると、無意識に髪を触ってしまう。","romaji":"Kangaegoto o shite iru to, muishiki ni kami o sawatte shimau.","german":"When I'm thinking, I unconsciously touch my hair.","furigana":"かんが|ごと|むいしき|かみ|さわ"},{"japanese":"毎日通る道なので、無意識のうちに駅に着いていた。","romaji":"Mainichi tōru michi na node, muishiki no uchi ni eki ni tsuite ita.","german":"Since I take this road every day, I had reached the station without noticing.","furigana":"まいにちとお|みち|むいしき|えき|つ"}],"notes":"無意識に means \"unconsciously, involuntarily, without thinking\": 無意識に手が動く, 無意識に口に出す. In conversation 思わず (\"involuntarily\", for sudden reactions) and つい (\"out of habit, inadvertently\") are more common; 無意識に stresses that one does not notice it at all. The counterpart is 意識的に or 意図的に. Also very common is 無意識のうちに \"without realizing it\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"社員が自発的に残業した。","romaji":"Shain ga jihatsuteki ni zangyō shita.","german":"The employees worked overtime of their own accord.","furigana":"しゃいん|じはつてき|ざんぎょう"},{"japanese":"子どもが自発的に勉強するようになって、親は喜んでいる。","romaji":"Kodomo ga jihatsuteki ni benkyō suru yō ni natte, oya wa yorokonde iru.","german":"The parents are pleased that their child now studies on their own initiative.","furigana":"こ|じはつてき|べんきょう|おや|よろこ"},{"japanese":"災害の後、多くの若者が自発的にボランティアに参加した。","romaji":"Saigai no ato, ōku no wakamono ga jihatsuteki ni borantia ni sanka shita.","german":"After the disaster, many young people took part in volunteer work of their own accord.","furigana":"さいがい|あと|おお|わかもの|じはつてき|さんか"}],"notes":"自発的に means \"on one's own initiative, voluntarily, of one's own accord\": 自発的に参加する, 自発的に勉強する. It stresses inner motivation without external prompting. In conversation one often says 自分から or 進んで; 自主的に is very similar but stresses more personal responsibility and self-organization. The counterpart is 強制的に \"by force\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"強制的に退去させられた。","romaji":"Kyōseiteki ni taikyo saserareta.","german":"They were forcibly evicted.","furigana":"きょうせいてき|たいきょ"},{"japanese":"パソコンが固まったので、強制的に電源を切った。","romaji":"Pasokon ga katamatta node, kyōseiteki ni dengen o kitta.","german":"The computer froze, so I forcibly turned off the power.","furigana":"かた|きょうせいてき|でんげん|き"},{"japanese":"学生時代は、全員が強制的に部活に入らされた。","romaji":"Gakusei jidai wa, zen'in ga kyōseiteki ni bukatsu ni hairasareta.","german":"In my school days, everyone was forced to join a club.","furigana":"がくせいじだい|ぜんいん|きょうせいてき|ぶかつ|はい"}],"notes":"強制的に means \"compulsorily, against the will of those concerned\": 強制的に退去させる, 強制的に終了する (e.g. to force-quit a program). Often with passive or causative forms: 強制的に〜させられる. The counterpart is 自発的に or 任意で \"voluntarily\". Matter-of-fact to formal; in conversation one also says 無理やり (more emotional, \"by force\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"天候が悪化したため、試合は中止になった。","romaji":"Tenkō ga akka shita tame, shiai wa chūshi ni natta.","german":"The game was cancelled because the weather worsened.","furigana":"てんこう|あっか|しあい|ちゅうし"},{"japanese":"悪天候のため、飛行機の出発が遅れております。","romaji":"Akutenkou no tame, hikouki no shuppatsu ga okurete orimasu.","german":"Departure of the airplane is delayed due to bad weather.","furigana":"あくてんこう|ひこうき|しゅっぱつ|おく"},{"japanese":"今年は天候に恵まれて、米がよく育った。","romaji":"Kotoshi wa tenkou ni megumarete, kome ga yoku sodatta.","german":"This year we were blessed with good weather, and the rice grew well.","furigana":"ことし|てんこう|めぐ|こめ|そだ"}],"notes":"天候 means \"weather, weather conditions\" and denotes the weather situation over a certain period, mostly in factual contexts: 天候不順 \"unsettled weather,\" 悪天候 \"bad weather,\" 天候に恵まれる \"to be blessed with good weather.\" In everyday speech people mostly say 天気; 天候 sounds more formal and appears in news, announcements, and in agriculture or air traffic. 気候, by contrast, is the long-term climate of a region.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"厳寒の中、作業員たちは黙々と働いていた。","romaji":"Genkan no naka, sagyōin-tachi wa mokumoku to hataraite ita.","german":"In the bitter cold, the workers kept working in silence.","furigana":"げんかん|なか|さぎょういん|もくもく|はたら"},{"japanese":"厳寒期には気温が氷点下二十度まで下がる。","romaji":"Genkanki ni wa kion ga hyoutenka nijuu do made sagaru.","german":"In the coldest season, temperatures fall to minus twenty degrees.","furigana":"げんかんき|きおん|ひょうてんかにじゅうど|さ"},{"japanese":"厳寒の候、皆様いかがお過ごしでしょうか。","romaji":"Genkan no kou, minasama ikaga o-sugoshi deshou ka.","german":"In this bitterly cold season, how are you all?","furigana":"げんかん|こう|みなさま|す"}],"notes":"厳寒 means \"severe cold, bitter cold\" and is a written-language word from news, literature and letters: 厳寒の地, 厳寒期 \"the coldest time of the year\", 厳寒の候 as a formal letter opening in midwinter. In everyday speech one says 厳しい寒さ or simply すごく寒い instead. The counterpart is 酷暑 or 猛暑 \"scorching heat\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"乾季には水不足が深刻な問題になる。","romaji":"Kanki ni wa mizubusoku ga shinkoku na mondai ni naru.","german":"In the dry season, water shortage becomes a serious problem.","furigana":"かんき|みずぶそく|しんこく|もんだい"},{"japanese":"この国を旅行するなら、乾季がおすすめです。","romaji":"Kono kuni o ryokou suru nara, kanki ga osusume desu.","german":"If you travel to this country, I recommend the dry season.","furigana":"くに|りょこう|かんき"},{"japanese":"乾季に入ると、川の水がほとんどなくなる。","romaji":"Kanki ni hairu to, kawa no mizu ga hotondo nakunaru.","german":"When the dry season begins, the river almost completely dries up.","furigana":"かんき|はい|かわ|みず"}],"notes":"乾季 means \"dry season\" and refers mainly to tropical and subtropical regions with a clear alternation between rainy and dry seasons; the counterpart is 雨季. There is also the equally pronounced spelling 乾期, which occurs more in specialist texts. Typical: 乾季に入る \"the dry season begins\", 乾季には〜. For dry air in the Japanese winter, by contrast, one speaks of 乾燥.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"東南アジアの雨季は六月から始まる。","romaji":"Tōnan Ajia no uki wa rokugatsu kara hajimaru.","german":"The rainy season in Southeast Asia begins in June.","furigana":"とうなん|うき|ろくがつ|はじ"},{"japanese":"雨季の間は、毎日午後になると激しい雨が降る。","romaji":"Uki no aida wa, mainichi gogo ni naru to hageshii ame ga furu.","german":"During the rainy season, heavy rain falls every afternoon.","furigana":"うき|あいだ|まいにちごご|はげ|あめ|ふ"},{"japanese":"雨季に入る前に、屋根を修理しておいた。","romaji":"Uki ni hairu mae ni, yane o shuuri shite oita.","german":"Before the rainy season began, I had the roof repaired.","furigana":"うき|はい|まえ|やね|しゅうり"}],"notes":"雨季 means \"rainy season\" in the general, geographical sense, e.g. in Southeast Asia or Africa; the counterpart is 乾季. For the Japanese rainy season in early summer, one almost always uses 梅雨 (つゆ). The spelling 雨期 is equivalent. Typical: 雨季に入る \"the rainy season begins\", 雨季の間, 雨季が終わる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"梅雨前線の影響で各地に大雨が降った。","romaji":"Tsuyu zensen no eikyō de kakuchi ni ōame ga futta.","german":"Heavy rain fell in various places due to the influence of the rainy-season front.","furigana":"つゆぜんせん|えいきょう|かくち|おおあめ|ふ"},{"japanese":"今夜、寒冷前線が通過して気温が一気に下がるそうです。","romaji":"Kon'ya, kanrei zensen ga tsuuka shite kion ga ikki ni sagaru sou desu.","german":"I hear a cold front will pass through tonight and temperatures will drop sharply.","furigana":"こんや|かんれいぜんせん|つうか|きおん|いっき|さ"},{"japanese":"桜前線は今週末に東北地方に到達する見込みだ。","romaji":"Sakura zensen wa konshuumatsu ni Touhoku chihou ni toutatsu suru mikomi da.","german":"The cherry blossom front is expected to reach the Tohoku region this weekend.","furigana":"さくらぜんせん|こんしゅうまつ|とうほくちほう|とうたつ|みこ"}],"notes":"前線 in weather language is the \"front\" between two air masses: 寒冷前線 \"cold front\", 温暖前線 \"warm front\" and above all 梅雨前線, the rainy-season front in early summer. Typical are 前線が停滞する \"the front stalls\" and 前線が通過する \"the front passes through\". Outside weather it means \"front line, the very front\" (前線の兵士) and figuratively \"on the front line\" (営業の最前線). 桜前線, the line of cherry blossoms moving from south to north, is also a fixed term.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"寒波の到来で交通機関に乱れが出た。","romaji":"Kanpa no tōrai de kōtsū kikan ni midare ga deta.","german":"The arrival of the cold wave caused disruptions to transportation.","furigana":"かんぱ|とうらい|こうつうきかん|みだ|で"},{"japanese":"今年一番の寒波で、水道管が凍ってしまった。","romaji":"Kotoshi ichiban no kanpa de, suidoukan ga kootte shimatta.","german":"In the strongest cold wave of the year, the water pipes froze.","furigana":"ことしいちばん|かんぱ|すいどうかん|こお"},{"japanese":"北海道は記録的な寒波に見舞われている。","romaji":"Hokkaidou wa kirokuteki na kanpa ni mimawarete iru.","german":"Hokkaido is being hit by a record-breaking cold wave.","furigana":"ほっかいどう|きろくてき|かんぱ|みま"}],"notes":"寒波 is a \"cold wave\", a spell of very cold air lasting several days, a typical word from weather reports and news. Common combinations are 寒波が到来する／襲来する \"a cold wave arrives\", 寒波に見舞われる \"to be hit by a cold wave\" and 最強寒波 \"the strongest cold wave\". The summer counterpart is 熱波 \"heat wave\"; for a single cold day one says 寒い日 or 冷え込み.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼のスピーチに深い感銘を受けた。","romaji":"Kare no supīchi ni fukai kanmei wo uketa.","german":"I was deeply impressed by his speech.","furigana":"かれ|ふか|かんめい|う"},{"japanese":"この小説は多くの読者に感銘を与えた。","romaji":"Kono shousetsu wa ooku no dokusha ni kanmei o ataeta.","german":"This novel deeply moved many readers.","furigana":"しょうせつ|おお|どくしゃ|かんめい|あた"},{"japanese":"ボランティアの方々の熱意に感銘しました。","romaji":"Borantia no katagata no netsui ni kanmei shimashita.","german":"I was deeply impressed by the enthusiasm of the volunteers.","furigana":"かたがた|ねつい|かんめい"}],"notes":"感銘 refers to a deep, lasting impression that moves one, usually through speeches, books, works or exemplary behavior. The standard combination is 〜に感銘を受ける \"to be deeply impressed by something\", more rarely 感銘を与える \"to impress deeply\"; as a する-verb: 〜に感銘する. It is more elevated and lasting than 感動 \"emotion, being moved\", which describes more spontaneous feelings. For a mere \"first impression\", 印象 is used, not 感銘.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"憎悪からは何も生まれない。","romaji":"Zōo kara wa nani mo umarenai.","german":"Nothing is born from hatred.","furigana":"ぞうお|なに|う"},{"japanese":"戦争は国と国の間に深い憎悪を残した。","romaji":"Sensou wa kuni to kuni no aida ni fukai zouo o nokoshita.","german":"The war left deep hatred between the countries.","furigana":"せんそう|くに|くに|あいだ|ふか|ぞうお|のこ"},{"japanese":"ネット上で特定の人々への憎悪をあおる投稿が問題になっている。","romaji":"Netto-jou de tokutei no hitobito e no zouo o aoru toukou ga mondai ni natte iru.","german":"Posts online that stir up hatred against particular groups have become a problem.","furigana":"じょう|とくてい|ひとびと|ぞうお|とうこう|もんだい"}],"notes":"憎悪 is strong, deep-seated \"hatred\" and a heavy written-language word often found in news, politics and literature: 憎悪犯罪 \"hate crime\", 憎悪の連鎖 \"chain of hatred\", 〜に憎悪を抱く \"to harbor hatred toward someone\". In everyday speech one says 憎しみ or the verb 憎む instead. Unlike 嫌悪 \"disgust\" (rejection, aversion), 憎悪 stresses active hostility toward someone.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は暴力に対して強い嫌悪感を持っている。","romaji":"Kare wa bōryoku ni taishite tsuyoi ken'okan wo motte iru.","german":"He has a strong aversion to violence.","furigana":"かれ|ぼうりょく|たい|つよ|けんおかん|も"},{"japanese":"つい友達に八つ当たりしてしまい、自己嫌悪に陥った。","romaji":"Tsui tomodachi ni yatsuatari shite shimai, jiko ken'o ni ochiitta.","german":"I took my frustration out on a friend and ended up despising myself.","furigana":"ともだち|や|あ|じこけんお|おちい"},{"japanese":"多くの市民が政治家の嘘に嫌悪を示した。","romaji":"Ooku no shimin ga seijika no uso ni ken'o o shimeshita.","german":"Many citizens expressed disgust at the politicians' lies.","furigana":"おお|しみん|せいじか|うそ|けんお|しめ"}],"notes":"嫌悪 is \"disgust, aversion, revulsion\", a strong inner rejection. Very common is the form 嫌悪感 \"feeling of aversion\": 嫌悪感を抱く／覚える, 〜に嫌悪感を示す. Also well known is 自己嫌悪 \"self-loathing\", e.g. 自己嫌悪に陥る. The word is rather elevated; in everyday speech one says 嫌だ or 大嫌い. Unlike 憎悪 \"hatred\", 嫌悪 aims more at rejection than at hostility.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人前で失敗して、強い羞恥心を覚えた。","romaji":"Hitomae de shippai shite, tsuyoi shuuchishin o oboeta.","german":"I felt intense shame when I failed in front of others.","furigana":"ひとまえ|しっぱい|つよ|しゅうちしん|おぼ"},{"japanese":"電車の中で大声で電話するなんて、羞恥心がないのだろうか。","romaji":"Densha no naka de oogoe de denwa suru nante, shuuchishin ga nai no darou ka.","german":"Talking loudly on the phone on the train - don't they have any sense of shame?","furigana":"でんしゃ|なか|おおごえ|でんわ|しゅうちしん"},{"japanese":"外国語を上達させるには、まず羞恥心を捨てることだ。","romaji":"Gaikokugo o joutatsu saseru ni wa, mazu shuuchishin o suteru koto da.","german":"To improve in a foreign language, you first have to throw away your shame.","furigana":"がいこくご|じょうたつ|しゅうちしん|す"}],"notes":"羞恥 is an elevated word for \"shame\" and occurs almost only in compounds, above all 羞恥心 \"sense of shame\": 羞恥心がない \"to be shameless\", 羞恥心を捨てる \"to cast off one's shame\", 羞恥心を覚える. In everyday speech, for embarrassment one mostly says 恥ずかしい or 恥. Unlike 屈辱 \"humiliation\", which is inflicted from outside, 羞恥 is one's own feeling of being ashamed.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの屈辱を忘れることはできない。","romaji":"Ano kutsujoku wo wasureru koto wa dekinai.","german":"I can never forget that humiliation.","furigana":"くつじょく|わす"},{"japanese":"チームは格下の相手に0対5で負けるという屈辱を味わった。","romaji":"Chiimu wa kakushita no aite ni zero tai go de makeru to iu kutsujoku o ajiwatta.","german":"The team suffered the humiliation of losing 0-5 to a weaker opponent.","furigana":"かくした|あいて|たい|ま|くつじょく|あじ"},{"japanese":"みんなの前で叱られるのは屈辱的だった。","romaji":"Minna no mae de shikarareru no wa kutsujokuteki datta.","german":"Being scolded in front of everyone was humiliating.","furigana":"まえ|しか|くつじょくてき"}],"notes":"屈辱 is \"humiliation, disgrace\", the feeling of having one's pride injured, for example by a defeat or belittlement. Typical combinations: 屈辱を味わう \"to suffer humiliation\", 屈辱を受ける, 屈辱を晴らす \"to wipe out the disgrace\" and the adjective 屈辱的な \"humiliating\". In sports, 屈辱の敗戦 \"humiliating defeat\" is a fixed expression. Unlike 恥 or 羞恥 (one's own shame), 屈辱 stresses the affront inflicted from outside.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"何度失敗しても絶望してはいけない。","romaji":"Nando shippai shite mo zetsubō shite wa ikenai.","german":"No matter how many times you fail, you must not despair.","furigana":"なんどしっぱい|ぜつぼう"},{"japanese":"全財産を失い、彼は絶望の淵に立たされた。","romaji":"Zenzaisan o ushinai, kare wa zetsubou no fuchi ni tatasareta.","german":"After losing all his fortune, he stood at the brink of despair.","furigana":"ぜんざいさん|うしな|かれ|ぜつぼう|ふち|た"},{"japanese":"残り1分で3点差なんて、勝つのはもう絶望的だ。","romaji":"Nokori ippun de santen-sa nante, katsu no wa mou zetsubouteki da.","german":"Three goals behind one minute before the end - a victory is practically hopeless.","furigana":"のこ|ぷん|てんさ|か|ぜつぼうてき"}],"notes":"絶望 is \"despair, hopelessness\" and is used as a noun and as an intransitive suru-verb: 将来に絶望する \"to despair of the future\", 絶望に陥る \"to fall into despair\". With の or 的 come descriptions such as 絶望の淵 \"the brink of despair\" and 絶望的な状況 \"a hopeless situation\". The opposite is 希望 \"hope\"; weaker discouragement is expressed more with がっかりする or 落ち込む.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"暗闇に対する恐怖は子供の頃から続いている。","romaji":"Kurayami ni taisuru kyōfu wa kodomo no koro kara tsuzuite iru.","german":"My fear of the dark has continued since childhood.","furigana":"くらやみ|たい|きょうふ|こども|ころ|つづ"},{"japanese":"地震で建物が大きく揺れ、恐怖で声も出なかった。","romaji":"Jishin de tatemono ga ookiku yure, kyoufu de koe mo denakatta.","german":"The building shook violently in the earthquake, and I was so terrified I couldn't even make a sound.","furigana":"じしん|たてもの|おお|ゆ|きょうふ|こえ|で"},{"japanese":"私は高所恐怖症なので、観覧車には乗れません。","romaji":"Watashi wa kousho kyoufushou na node, kanransha ni wa noremasen.","german":"I'm afraid of heights, so I can't ride the Ferris wheel.","furigana":"わたし|こうしょきょうふしょう|かんらんしゃ|の"}],"notes":"恐怖 is \"fear, dread, terror\" in a strong sense, mostly of something concretely threatening. Typical are 恐怖を感じる／覚える, 恐怖に襲われる \"to be seized by fear,\" 〜に対する恐怖 and 〜恐怖症 \"-phobia\" (高所恐怖症). It is stronger and more formal than 怖さ or the adjective 怖い; 不安, by contrast, is diffuse worry, and 心配 is concern about something. As a genre it is ホラー or 恐怖映画.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"電車の遅延に苛立ちを感じた。","romaji":"Densha no chien ni iradachi wo kanjita.","german":"I felt irritated by the train delay.","furigana":"でんしゃ|ちえん|いらだ|かん"},{"japanese":"何度説明しても理解してもらえず、部長は苛立ちを隠せなかった。","romaji":"Nando setsumei shite mo rikai shite moraezu, buchou wa iradachi o kakusenakatta.","german":"No matter how many times he explained, he wasn't understood, and the department head could not hide his irritation.","furigana":"なんどせつめい|りかい|ぶちょう|いらだ|かく"},{"japanese":"対策が遅れる政府への苛立ちが市民の間で募っている。","romaji":"Taisaku ga okureru seifu e no iradachi ga shimin no aida de tsunotte iru.","german":"Frustration with the government, which is slow with countermeasures, is growing among citizens.","furigana":"たいさく|おく|せいふ|いらだ|しみん|あいだ|つの"}],"notes":"苛立ち is the noun of the verb 苛立つ \"to become irritated\" and means \"irritation, impatience, frustration\" when something does not go as wished. Typical: 苛立ちを感じる／覚える, 苛立ちを隠せない \"cannot hide one's irritation\", 〜への苛立ちが募る \"irritation about ... grows\". It is very often written in kana (いらだち, いら立ち). Colloquially one says イライラ; 怒り \"anger\" is stronger and more open.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"戦後の虚無感に多くの作家が苦しんだ。","romaji":"Sengo no kyomu-kan ni ōku no sakka ga kurushinda.","german":"Many writers suffered from the sense of emptiness after the war.","furigana":"せんご|きょむかん|おお|さっか|くる"},{"japanese":"大きな目標を達成した後、なぜか虚無感に襲われた。","romaji":"Ookina mokuhyou o tassei shita ato, naze ka kyomukan ni osowareta.","german":"After achieving a big goal, I was for some reason overcome by a feeling of emptiness.","furigana":"おお|もくひょう|たっせい|あと|きょむかん|おそ"},{"japanese":"彼の作品には虚無的な世界観が漂っている。","romaji":"Kare no sakuhin ni wa kyomuteki na sekaikan ga tadayotte iru.","german":"A nihilistic worldview hangs over his works.","furigana":"かれ|さくひん|きょむてき|せかいかん|ただよ"}],"notes":"虚無 means \"nothingness, emptiness, void\" and is a literary-philosophical word. In everyday life it appears mainly as 虚無感 \"feeling of emptiness and meaninglessness\" (虚無感に襲われる) and in 虚無主義 \"nihilism\" (also ニヒリズム). It does not describe mere boredom like 退屈, but the feeling that everything is meaningless; 空しい／虚しい is the matching adjective.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"親友が引っ越して喪失感に襲われた。","romaji":"Shin'yū ga hikkoshite sōshitsukan ni osowareta.","german":"When my best friend moved away, I was overcome by a sense of loss.","furigana":"しんゆう|ひ|こ|そうしつかん|おそ"},{"japanese":"母を亡くした喪失感から、なかなか立ち直れない。","romaji":"Haha o nakushita soushitsukan kara, nakanaka tachinaorenai.","german":"I can't recover from the sense of loss after losing my mother.","furigana":"はは|な|そうしつかん|た|なお"},{"japanese":"定年退職後、強い喪失感を抱える人も少なくない。","romaji":"Teinen taishoku-go, tsuyoi soushitsukan o kakaeru hito mo sukunaku nai.","german":"Not a few people carry a strong sense of loss after retirement.","furigana":"ていねんたいしょくご|つよ|そうしつかん|かか|ひと|すく"}],"notes":"喪失感 is the \"feeling of loss\", the emptiness that remains when a person, a relationship or something important is lost. Typical are 喪失感に襲われる \"to be overwhelmed by a sense of loss\", 喪失感を抱える, 喪失感から立ち直る \"to recover from the loss\". For pets one speaks of ペットロス. 喪失 alone (記憶喪失 \"memory loss\", 自信喪失) is the objective loss; -感 turns it into the feeling.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"嘘をついた罪悪感がずっと消えなかった。","romaji":"Uso wo tsuita zaiakukan ga zutto kienakatta.","german":"The guilt of having told a lie never went away.","furigana":"うそ|ざいあくかん|き"},{"japanese":"夜中にケーキを食べると罪悪感がある。","romaji":"Yonaka ni keeki o taberu to zaiakukan ga aru.","german":"Eating cake in the middle of the night makes me feel guilty.","furigana":"よなか|た|ざいあくかん"},{"japanese":"仕事を休んで同僚に迷惑をかけ、罪悪感を覚えた。","romaji":"Shigoto o yasunde douryou ni meiwaku o kake, zaiakukan o oboeta.","german":"I took a day off work and caused trouble for my colleagues, and I felt guilty.","furigana":"しごと|やす|どうりょう|めいわく|ざいあくかん|おぼ"}],"notes":"罪悪感 is the \"feeling of guilt\", also over small things such as snacking or declining. Typical combinations: 罪悪感を抱く／覚える, 罪悪感がある, 罪悪感にさいなまれる \"to be tormented by guilt\"; colloquially also 罪悪感なしで食べられる \"can eat without a guilty conscience\". Not to be confused with 罪 \"crime, sin\" itself; for legal guilt one says 有罪.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"古い窓枠を新しいものに交換した。","romaji":"Furui madowaku wo atarashii mono ni kōkan shita.","german":"I replaced the old window frame with a new one.","furigana":"ふる|まどわく|あたら|こうかん"},{"japanese":"冬になると窓枠に結露がたまってカビが生えやすい。","romaji":"Fuyu ni naru to madowaku ni ketsuro ga tamatte kabi ga hae yasui.","german":"In winter, condensation collects on the window frame and mold easily grows.","furigana":"ふゆ|まどわく|けつろ|は"},{"japanese":"猫が窓枠に座って外を眺めている。","romaji":"Neko ga madowaku ni suwatte soto o nagamete iru.","german":"The cat is sitting in the window frame looking outside.","furigana":"ねこ|まどわく|すわ|そと|なが"}],"notes":"窓枠 is the \"window frame\", i.e. the fixed frame around the glass pane or the window opening. Typical in the context of renovation and housing: 窓枠を交換する, 窓枠に結露がたまる \"condensation collects on the window frame\", 窓枠にもたれる \"to lean on the window frame\". The sill on which one puts something is called 窓辺 or 出窓; 枠 alone is generally \"frame, border\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"最近、頻繁に頭痛がする。","romaji":"Saikin, hinpan ni zutsū ga suru.","german":"Recently I get headaches frequently.","furigana":"さいきん|ひんぱん|ずつう"},{"japanese":"この交差点では事故が頻繁に起きている。","romaji":"Kono kousaten de wa jiko ga hinpan ni okite iru.","german":"Accidents happen frequently at this intersection.","furigana":"こうさてん|じこ|ひんぱん|お"},{"japanese":"海外の取引先とは、メールで頻繁に連絡を取っています。","romaji":"Kaigai no torihikisaki to wa, meeru de hinpan ni renraku o totte imasu.","german":"We are in frequent contact with our overseas business partners by e-mail.","furigana":"かいがい|とりひきさき|ひんぱん|れんらく|と"}],"notes":"頻繁に is the adverbial form of the な-adjective 頻繁 and means \"frequently, at short intervals\", often with the undertone that it happens conspicuously or annoyingly often. It is more formal than よく or しょっちゅう and likes to appear in reports, news and explanations: 頻繁に起こる, 頻繁に連絡を取る. Attributively one says 頻繁な＋noun (頻繁な出張). Neutral \"often\" in conversation is mostly よく.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"健康のために定期的に運動している。","romaji":"Kenkō no tame ni teikiteki ni undō shite iru.","german":"I exercise regularly for my health.","furigana":"けんこう|ていきてき|うんどう"},{"japanese":"エレベーターは月に一度、定期的に点検されている。","romaji":"Erebeetaa wa tsuki ni ichido, teikiteki ni tenken sarete iru.","german":"The elevator is inspected regularly, once a month.","furigana":"つき|いちど|ていきてき|てんけん"},{"japanese":"大切なデータは定期的にバックアップを取りましょう。","romaji":"Taisetsu na deeta wa teikiteki ni bakkuappu o torimashou.","german":"Let's back up important data regularly.","furigana":"たいせつ|ていきてき|と"}],"notes":"定期的に means \"regularly, at fixed intervals\" and stresses a scheduled rhythm, e.g. for checks, maintenance or meetings: 定期的に点検する, 定期的に健康診断を受ける. Attributively: 定期的な＋noun. Related are 定期 (定期券 \"commuter pass\", 定期便 \"scheduled service\"). The opposite is 不定期に \"irregularly\"; よく or 頻繁に only say \"often\", without a fixed beat.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このイベントは不定期に開催される。","romaji":"Kono ibento wa futeiki ni kaisai sareru.","german":"This event is held irregularly.","furigana":"ふていき|かいさい"},{"japanese":"このブログは不定期に更新しています。","romaji":"Kono burogu wa futeiki ni koushin shite imasu.","german":"This blog is updated irregularly.","furigana":"ふていき|こうしん"},{"japanese":"その店は不定期に休むので、行く前に確認したほうがいい。","romaji":"Sono mise wa futeiki ni yasumu node, iku mae ni kakunin shita hou ga ii.","german":"That shop closes at irregular times, so it's better to check before you go.","furigana":"みせ|ふていき|やす|い|まえ|かくにん"}],"notes":"不定期に means \"irregularly, without a fixed date\" and is the counterpart of 定期的に. It describes events that recur but not according to a schedule: 不定期に開催される, 不定期に更新する. Attributively one says 不定期の＋noun (不定期のイベント, 不定期便). For \"irregular\" in the sense of an unhealthy daily rhythm, 不規則 is used instead (不規則な生活).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ご質問があれば随時お問い合わせください。","romaji":"Go-shitsumon ga areba zuiji otoiawase kudasai.","german":"If you have any questions, please feel free to contact us at any time.","furigana":"しつもん|ずいじ|と|あ"},{"japanese":"アルバイトスタッフを随時募集しています。","romaji":"Arubaito sutaffu o zuiji boshuu shite imasu.","german":"We are recruiting part-time staff on an ongoing basis.","furigana":"ずいじぼしゅう"},{"japanese":"最新の運行情報は、ホームページで随時お知らせします。","romaji":"Saishin no unkou jouhou wa, hoomupeeji de zuiji oshirase shimasu.","german":"We will announce the latest service information on our website as it becomes available.","furigana":"さいしん|うんこうじょうほう|ずいじ|し"}],"notes":"随時 means \"at any time, as needed, on an ongoing basis\" and is typical of announcements, authorities and business language: 随時受け付ける \"to accept at any time\", 随時募集中 \"recruiting continuously\", 随時更新 \"updated continuously\". Unlike いつでも, it sounds formal and matter-of-fact. It differs from 常時 \"constantly, throughout\" in that 随時 means \"whenever necessary\", not uninterruptedly.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このサービスは常時利用可能です。","romaji":"Kono sābisu wa jōji riyō kanō desu.","german":"This service is available at all times.","furigana":"じょうじりようかのう"},{"japanese":"この施設には看護師が常時待機しています。","romaji":"Kono shisetsu ni wa kangoshi ga jouji taiki shite imasu.","german":"A nurse is on standby at all times at this facility.","furigana":"しせつ|かんごし|じょうじたいき"},{"japanese":"工場内では常時ヘルメットを着用してください。","romaji":"Koujou-nai de wa jouji herumetto o chakuyou shite kudasai.","german":"Please wear a helmet at all times inside the factory.","furigana":"こうじょうない|じょうじ|ちゃくよう"}],"notes":"常時 means \"constantly, continuously, at all times\" and stresses that a state exists without interruption. It is a formal word from technology, administration and notice signs: 常時接続 \"always-on connection\", 常時監視 \"constant monitoring\", 常時点灯 \"permanently lit\"; also attributively with の (常時の警戒). In everyday speech one says いつも or ずっと instead. Compared with 随時 \"as needed\", 常時 means \"without interruption\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は会議の間終始無言だった。","romaji":"Kare wa kaigi no aida shūshi mugon datta.","german":"He kept silent throughout the entire meeting.","furigana":"かれ|かいぎ|あいだしゅうしむごん"},{"japanese":"試合は終始、日本チームのペースで進んだ。","romaji":"Shiai wa shuushi, Nihon chiimu no peesu de susunda.","german":"The game went at the Japanese team's pace from start to finish.","furigana":"しあい|しゅうし|にほん|すす"},{"japanese":"記者会見で社長は言い訳に終始した。","romaji":"Kisha kaiken de shachou wa iiwake ni shuushi shita.","german":"At the press conference, the company president did nothing but make excuses.","furigana":"きしゃかいけん|しゃちょう|い|わけ|しゅうし"}],"notes":"終始 means adverbially \"from beginning to end, the whole time\" and describes consistent behavior during an event: 終始笑顔だった, 試合は終始リードした. It is somewhat more formal than ずっと. As a suru-verb, 〜に終始する means \"to be limited to ..., to consist only of ...\", often critically: 言い訳に終始する \"to do nothing but make excuses\". Also fixed is 終始一貫 \"consistent from beginning to end\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"暫くお待ちください。","romaji":"Shibaraku omachi kudasai.","german":"Please wait a moment.","furigana":"しばら|ま"},{"japanese":"しばらく休んでから、また始めましょう。","romaji":"Shibaraku yasunde kara, mata hajimemashou.","german":"Let's take a break for a while and then start again.","furigana":"やす|はじ"},{"japanese":"田中さん、しばらくですね。お元気でしたか。","romaji":"Tanaka-san, shibaraku desu ne. Ogenki deshita ka.","german":"Mr. Tanaka, it's been a while! How have you been?","furigana":"たなか|げんき"}],"notes":"暫く refers to an indefinite, usually manageable span of time: しばらくお待ちください \"please wait a moment\", しばらく休む \"to take a break for a while\", しばらくの間 \"temporarily, for the time being\". In しばらくぶり or the greeting しばらくですね, however, it is about a long time: \"long time no see\". Compared with ちょっと, しばらく sounds somewhat more formal and often means a longer duration. Almost always written in kana as しばらく; the kanji 暫く is rare.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"来月以降、新しい制度が適用される。","romaji":"Raigetsu ikō, atarashii seido ga tekiyō sareru.","german":"The new system will apply from next month onward.","furigana":"らいげついこう|あたら|せいど|てきよう"},{"japanese":"午後6時以降はこちらの入口をご利用ください。","romaji":"Gogo rokuji ikou wa kochira no iriguchi o go-riyou kudasai.","german":"From 6 p.m. onward, please use this entrance.","furigana":"ごご|じいこう|いりぐち|りよう"},{"japanese":"東京に引っ越して以降、一度も地元に帰っていない。","romaji":"Toukyou ni hikkoshite ikou, ichido mo jimoto ni kaette inai.","german":"Since moving to Tokyo, I have not been back to my hometown even once.","furigana":"とうきょう|ひ|こ|いこう|いちど|じもと|かえ"}],"notes":"以降 follows a time expression directly and means \"from, since, onward\"; the stated point in time is included: 来月以降, 午後6時以降, 2000年以降. After verbs it takes the て-form: 入社して以降. It is more formal than から and stresses the whole period that follows. Similar is 以後 (more \"after that, from now on\"); 以前 is the counterpart \"before, earlier\". Grammatically 以降 is a noun or suffix, not a free adverb.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"事前に予約しないと入れないレストランだ。","romaji":"Jizen ni yoyaku shinai to hairenai resutoran da.","german":"It is a restaurant you can't get into without booking in advance.","furigana":"じぜん|よやく|はい"},{"japanese":"欠席する場合は、事前にご連絡ください。","romaji":"Kesseki suru baai wa, jizen ni go-renraku kudasai.","german":"If you will be absent, please let us know in advance.","furigana":"けっせき|ばあい|じぜん|れんらく"},{"japanese":"面接の前に、事前に会社について調べておいた。","romaji":"Mensetsu no mae ni, jizen ni kaisha ni tsuite shirabete oita.","german":"Before the interview, I looked up the company beforehand.","furigana":"めんせつ|まえ|じぜん|かいしゃ|しら"}],"notes":"事前に means \"in advance, beforehand\", i.e. before a particular event, and sounds more matter-of-fact and formal than 前もって or あらかじめ. Common: 事前に予約する, 事前に連絡する, 事前に申し込む. Attributively: 事前の＋noun (事前の準備, 事前説明会). The counterpart is 事後に \"afterward\"; 直前に, by contrast, means \"immediately before\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"事後に報告しても遅すぎる。","romaji":"Jigo ni hōkoku shite mo ososugiru.","german":"It's too late to report after the fact.","furigana":"じご|ほうこく|おそ"},{"japanese":"急ぎの場合は、事後に申請しても構いません。","romaji":"Isogi no baai wa, jigo ni shinsei shite mo kamaimasen.","german":"In urgent cases, you may apply after the fact.","furigana":"いそ|ばあい|じご|しんせい|かま"},{"japanese":"部下から事後報告ばかりされて、部長は困っている。","romaji":"Buka kara jigo houkoku bakari sarete, buchou wa komatte iru.","german":"The department head is troubled because his subordinates only ever report to him after the fact.","furigana":"ぶか|じごほうこく|ぶちょう|こま"}],"notes":"事後に means \"afterward, after the fact\", i.e. only after the event, and is the counterpart of 事前に. It is a formal word from work and administration; much more common than the form with に are compounds such as 事後報告 \"report after the fact\", 事後承諾 \"approval after the fact\" and 事後処理 \"follow-up handling\". In everyday speech one often simply says 後で or あとから.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"出発の直前に忘れ物に気づいた。","romaji":"Shuppatsu no chokuzen ni wasuremono ni kidzuita.","german":"Just before departure, I noticed I had forgotten something."},{"japanese":"寝る直前にスマホを見ると、眠りが浅くなるそうだ。","romaji":"Neru chokuzen ni sumaho o miru to, nemuri ga asaku naru sou da.","german":"They say that looking at your smartphone right before going to bed makes your sleep shallower.","furigana":"ね|ちょくぜん|み|ねむ|あさ"},{"japanese":"試験の直前になって慌てて勉強を始めた。","romaji":"Shiken no chokuzen ni natte awatete benkyou o hajimeta.","german":"It was only right before the exam that I hurriedly started studying.","furigana":"しけん|ちょくぜん|あわ|べんきょう|はじ"}],"notes":"直前に means \"immediately before, just before\" and usually follows a noun with の or the dictionary form of a verb: 出発の直前に, 寝る直前に. It stresses a very short interval, unlike 事前に \"in advance\" (with planning) or 前に in general. Attributively: 直前の＋noun (直前のキャンセル). The counterpart is 直後に \"immediately after\". 直前 can also mean \"right in front of\" spatially.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地震の直後に津波警報が出された。","romaji":"Jishin no chokugo ni tsunami keihō ga dasareta.","german":"A tsunami warning was issued immediately after the earthquake.","furigana":"じしん|ちょくご|つなみけいほう|だ"},{"japanese":"家を出た直後に雨が降り出した。","romaji":"Ie o deta chokugo ni ame ga furidashita.","german":"It started to rain just after I left the house.","furigana":"いえ|で|ちょくご|あめ|ふ|だ"},{"japanese":"食事の直後に激しい運動をするのはよくない。","romaji":"Shokuji no chokugo ni hageshii undou o suru no wa yoku nai.","german":"It's not good to do intense exercise right after a meal.","furigana":"しょくじ|ちょくご|はげ|うんどう"}],"notes":"直後に means \"immediately after, right after\" and follows a noun with の or the た-form of a verb: 地震の直後に, 家を出た直後に. It stresses that almost no time lies in between, more tightly than 後で or その後. Attributively: 直後の＋noun. The counterpart is 直前に \"immediately before\"; 事後に, by contrast, means only \"after the event\" without closeness.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"食事の最中に電話が鳴った。","romaji":"Shokuji no saichū ni denwa ga natta.","german":"The phone rang in the middle of the meal.","furigana":"しょくじ|さいちゅう|でんわ|な"},{"japanese":"試験の最中に急に気分が悪くなった。","romaji":"Shiken no saichuu ni kyuu ni kibun ga waruku natta.","german":"I suddenly felt sick in the middle of the exam.","furigana":"しけん|さいちゅう|きゅう|きぶん|わる"},{"japanese":"引っ越しの準備をしている最中に、友達が訪ねてきた。","romaji":"Hikkoshi no junbi o shite iru saichuu ni, tomodachi ga tazunete kita.","german":"Just as I was in the middle of preparing for the move, a friend came to visit.","furigana":"ひ|こ|じゅんび|さいちゅう|ともだち|たず"}],"notes":"最中に means \"in the middle of, right while\" and follows a noun with の or the ている-form: 食事の最中に, 話している最中に. It stresses the peak of an ongoing action, often when something unexpected intervenes; therefore it hardly appears with deliberate actions of one's own in the main clause. 間に \"during\" is weaker and more neutral; intensified, one says 真っ最中. The reading もなか, by contrast, denotes a sweet.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"映画の途中で眠ってしまった。","romaji":"Eiga no tochū de nemutte shimatta.","german":"I fell asleep in the middle of the movie.","furigana":"えいが|とちゅう|ねむ"},{"japanese":"会社へ行く途中で、交通事故を見かけた。","romaji":"Kaisha e iku tochuu de, koutsuu jiko o mikaketa.","german":"On my way to work, I saw a traffic accident.","furigana":"かいしゃ|い|とちゅう|こうつうじこ|み"},{"japanese":"一度始めたことは、途中で投げ出さないようにしている。","romaji":"Ichido hajimeta koto wa, tochuu de nagedasanai you ni shite iru.","german":"I try not to give up halfway on something once I've started it.","furigana":"いちどはじ|とちゅう|な|だ"}],"notes":"途中で has two main meanings: spatially \"on the way\" (駅へ行く途中で) and temporally \"in the middle, before the end\" (話の途中で). Very common is 途中でやめる／あきらめる \"to stop/give up halfway\". With で it is used for events and actions; for a fixed place along the way, one says 途中に (途中にコンビニがある). Compared with 最中に, 途中で emphasizes more that something is not yet finished.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 39"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"システムは一時的に停止しています。","romaji":"Shisutemu wa ichijiteki ni teishi shite imasu.","german":"The system is temporarily suspended.","furigana":"いちじてき|ていし"},{"japanese":"薬を飲んだら、痛みが一時的に治まった。","romaji":"Kusuri o nondara, itami ga ichijiteki ni osamatta.","german":"After I took the medicine, the pain subsided temporarily.","furigana":"くすり|の|いた|いちじてき|おさ"},{"japanese":"工事のため、この道路は一時的に通行止めになります。","romaji":"Kouji no tame, kono douro wa ichijiteki ni tsuukoudome ni narimasu.","german":"Because of construction work, this road will be temporarily closed to traffic.","furigana":"こうじ|どうろ|いちじてき|つうこうど"}],"notes":"一時的に is the adverbial form of the な-adjective 一時的 and means \"temporarily, for a short time\", with the expectation that the state will end again. Typical in announcements and explanations: 一時的に停止する, 一時的に閉鎖する, 痛みが一時的に治まる. Attributively: 一時的な＋noun (一時的な措置). Similar is the adverb 一時 (\"for a time\"); opposites are 永久に and 恒久的に \"permanently\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この美しい風景が永久に残ることを願う。","romaji":"Kono utsukushii fūkei ga eikyū ni nokoru koto wo negau.","german":"I hope this beautiful scenery remains forever.","furigana":"うつく|ふうけい|えいきゅう|のこ|ねが"},{"japanese":"一度削除したデータは永久に復元できません。","romaji":"Ichido sakujo shita deeta wa eikyuu ni fukugen dekimasen.","german":"Data that has been deleted once cannot be restored, ever.","furigana":"いちどさくじょ|えいきゅう|ふくげん"},{"japanese":"この会員カードは永久に有効です。","romaji":"Kono kaiin kaado wa eikyuu ni yuukou desu.","german":"This membership card is valid permanently.","furigana":"かいいん|えいきゅう|ゆうこう"}],"notes":"永久に means \"forever, eternally, permanently\". It sounds more matter-of-fact than the more emotional 永遠に and is typical in technology, law and administration: 永久に保存する, 永久に有効, and in compounds such as 永久歯 \"permanent tooth\" and 永久凍土 \"permafrost\". In love songs or vows one hears 永遠に more often. Opposite: 一時的に \"temporarily\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"祖父は生涯にわたって研究を続けた。","romaji":"Sofu wa shōgai ni watatte kenkyū wo tsuzuketa.","german":"My grandfather continued his research throughout his life.","furigana":"そふ|しょうがい|けんきゅう|つづ"},{"japanese":"あの日の先生の言葉は、生涯忘れないだろう。","romaji":"Ano hi no sensei no kotoba wa, shougai wasurenai darou.","german":"I will never forget my teacher's words that day as long as I live.","furigana":"ひ|せんせい|ことば|しょうがいわす"},{"japanese":"彼女は生涯独身を通し、作品づくりに打ち込んだ。","romaji":"Kanojo wa shougai dokushin o tooshi, sakuhin-zukuri ni uchikonda.","german":"She remained single all her life and devoted herself to creating her works.","furigana":"かのじょ|しょうがいどくしん|とお|さくひん|う|こ"}],"notes":"生涯 is first of all a noun \"life, lifetime\" (生涯を閉じる \"to end one's life\", 〜の生涯), but it is also used adverbially for \"all one's life\": 生涯忘れない, 生涯独身を通す. Common are 生涯にわたって \"throughout one's life\" and compounds such as 生涯学習 \"lifelong learning\". 生涯 is more formal than 一生, which is more usual in conversation.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"沖縄は年中暖かい気候だ。","romaji":"Okinawa wa nenjū atatakai kikō da.","german":"Okinawa has a warm climate all year round.","furigana":"おきなわ|ねんじゅうあたた|きこう"},{"japanese":"あの二人は年中けんかしているのに、なぜか別れない。","romaji":"Ano futari wa nenjuu kenka shite iru noni, naze ka wakarenai.","german":"Those two are always fighting, yet somehow they never break up.","furigana":"ふたり|ねんじゅう|わか"},{"japanese":"駅前のスーパーは年中無休なので便利だ。","romaji":"Ekimae no suupaa wa nenjuu mukyuu na node benri da.","german":"The supermarket in front of the station is open every day of the year, which is convenient.","furigana":"えきまえ|ねんじゅうむきゅう|べんり"}],"notes":"年中 has two uses: literally \"all year round\" (年中暖かい, 年中無休 \"open all year without a rest day\") and colloquially \"constantly, all the time,\" often with a slightly complaining tone: 年中忙しい, 年中けんかしている. In the second sense it resembles しょっちゅう or いつも. Not to be confused with 年中行事 \"annual events\"; the reading ねんちゅう refers to the middle kindergarten class.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大学が彼に名誉博士号を授けた。","romaji":"Daigaku ga kare ni meiyo hakushi-gō wo sazuketa.","german":"The university conferred an honorary doctorate on him.","furigana":"だいがく|かれ|めいよはくしごう|さず"},{"japanese":"師匠は長年の修業を終えた弟子に秘伝の技を授けた。","romaji":"Shishou wa naganen no shugyou o oeta deshi ni hiden no waza o sazuketa.","german":"After years of training, the master passed on his secret technique to his disciple.","furigana":"ししょう|ながねん|しゅぎょう|お|でし|ひでん|わざ|さず"},{"japanese":"祖母は私に生活の知恵をたくさん授けてくれた。","romaji":"Sobo wa watashi ni seikatsu no chie o takusan sazukete kureta.","german":"My grandmother gave me a lot of life wisdom to take along on my way.","furigana":"そぼ|わたし|せいかつ|ちえ|さず"}],"notes":"授ける is an elevated transitive verb: someone of higher standing \"confers, grants\" something (〜に〜を授ける), such as titles, prizes or honors (学位を授ける), or \"imparts\" knowledge and skills (知恵を授ける, 技を授ける). The intransitive counterpart is 授かる \"to be conferred, to be blessed with\", very common in 子どもを授かる. In daily life one naturally says あげる or 与える for \"to give\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"困っている人に食べ物を恵んだ。","romaji":"Komatte iru hito ni tabemono wo megunda.","german":"I gave food to people in need.","furigana":"こま|ひと|た|もの|めぐ"},{"japanese":"駅前で「お金を恵んでください」と声をかけられた。","romaji":"Ekimae de \"okane o megunde kudasai\" to koe o kakerareta.","german":"Someone approached me in front of the station: \"Could you spare some change?\"","furigana":"えきまえ|かね|めぐ|こえ"},{"japanese":"当日は天気に恵まれて、運動会は大成功だった。","romaji":"Toujitsu wa tenki ni megumarete, undoukai wa daiseikou datta.","german":"On the day itself we were lucky with the weather, and the sports festival was a great success.","furigana":"とうじつ|てんき|めぐ|うんどうかい|だいせいこう"}],"notes":"恵む means \"to give something out of pity, to give alms\" (〜に〜を恵む) and has a condescending undertone; 恵んでもらう can therefore feel humiliating. Much more common is the passive 恵まれる \"to be blessed with, to be fortunate in\": 天気に恵まれる, 才能に恵まれる, 恵まれた環境. Related are the noun 恵み \"blessing, bounty\" (自然の恵み) and お恵み.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は人生を医療研究に捧げた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa jinsei wo iryō kenkyū ni sasageta.","german":"She dedicated her life to medical research.","furigana":"かのじょ|じんせい|いりょうけんきゅう|ささ"},{"japanese":"式の始めに、犠牲者に黙とうを捧げた。","romaji":"Shiki no hajime ni, giseisha ni mokutou o sasageta.","german":"At the start of the ceremony, a moment of silence was observed for the victims.","furigana":"しき|はじ|ぎせいしゃ|もく|ささ"},{"japanese":"この曲を、天国の母に捧げます。","romaji":"Kono kyoku o, tengoku no haha ni sasagemasu.","german":"I dedicate this song to my mother in heaven.","furigana":"きょく|てんごく|はは|ささ"}],"notes":"捧げる means \"to offer, to dedicate, to devote\" and has a solemn, reverent tone (〜に〜を捧げる). Typical: 人生を〜に捧げる \"to dedicate one's life to ...\", 祈りを捧げる \"to offer a prayer\", 黙とうを捧げる \"to observe a moment of silence\", 花を捧げる. Originally it means \"to hold up with both hands\". Unlike 供える (placing offerings on an altar), 捧げる emphasizes devotion; for mere \"to give\" one says あげる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"亡くなった友人を弔うために集まった。","romaji":"Nakunatta yūjin wo tomurau tame ni atsumatta.","german":"We gathered to mourn our deceased friend.","furigana":"な|ゆうじん|とむら|あつ"},{"japanese":"戦争で亡くなった人々の霊を弔うため、慰霊碑が建てられた。","romaji":"Sensou de nakunatta hitobito no rei o tomurau tame, ireihi ga taterareta.","german":"A memorial monument was erected to mourn the souls of those who died in the war.","furigana":"せんそう|な|ひとびと|れい|とむら|いれいひ|た"},{"japanese":"お盆には寺で先祖を弔う法要が行われる。","romaji":"Obon ni wa tera de senzo o tomurau houyou ga okonawareru.","german":"At Obon, a memorial service for the ancestors is held at the temple.","furigana":"ぼん|てら|せんぞ|とむら|ほうよう|おこな"}],"notes":"弔う means \"to mourn a dead person\" and above all \"to hold a mourning or memorial ceremony for them\", i.e. the ritual act after a death: 死者を弔う, 霊を弔う, 先祖を弔う. By contrast, 悼む is inner mourning and 偲ぶ is fond remembrance. Related words: 弔問 \"condolence visit\", 弔辞 \"funeral address\". The word is elevated and rather written.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"国民は犠牲者の死を深く悼んだ。","romaji":"Kokumin wa giseisha no shi wo fukaku itanda.","german":"The people deeply mourned the deaths of the victims.","furigana":"こくみん|ぎせいしゃ|し|ふか|いた"},{"japanese":"恩師の突然の死を悼み、多くの教え子が葬儀に参列した。","romaji":"Onshi no totsuzen no shi o itami, ooku no oshiego ga sougi ni sanretsu shita.","german":"Mourning the sudden death of their mentor, many former students attended the funeral.","furigana":"おんし|とつぜん|し|いた|おお|おし|ご|そうぎ|さんれつ"},{"japanese":"世界中のファンがその俳優の早すぎる死を悼んでいる。","romaji":"Sekaijuu no fan ga sono haiyuu no hayasugiru shi o itande iru.","german":"Fans around the world are mourning the actor's all-too-early death.","furigana":"せかいじゅう|はいゆう|はや|し|いた"}],"notes":"悼む means \"to mourn, to lament\" and describes the inner pain over a death; the object is usually 死 or the deceased: 〜の死を悼む. It is very formal and often appears in obituaries, news and expressions of condolence (哀悼の意を表す). Not to be confused with the identically sounding 痛む \"to hurt\" and 傷む \"to get damaged\". Compared with 弔う (holding mourning rites), 悼む is the feeling itself.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"故人を偲ぶ会が開かれた。","romaji":"Kojin wo shinobu kai ga hirakareta.","german":"A memorial gathering for the deceased was held.","furigana":"こじん|しの|かい|ひら"},{"japanese":"古いアルバムを開いて、亡き祖母を偲んだ。","romaji":"Furui arubamu o hiraite, naki sobo o shinonda.","german":"I opened the old photo album and remembered my late grandmother.","furigana":"ふる|ひら|な|そぼ|しの"},{"japanese":"この通りには、江戸時代を偲ばせる古い家が残っている。","romaji":"Kono toori ni wa, Edo jidai o shinobaseru furui ie ga nokotte iru.","german":"Old houses remain on this street that call to mind the Edo period.","furigana":"とお|えどじだい|しの|ふる|いえ|のこ"}],"notes":"偲ぶ means \"to remember wistfully, to commemorate\", mostly the deceased or the past: 故人を偲ぶ, 昔を偲ぶ. Set expressions are 〜を偲ぶ会 \"memorial gathering\" and the causative form 〜を偲ばせる \"to make one recall ...\" (昔の面影を偲ばせる町並み). Unlike 思い出す, it is elevated and emotionally colored. Not to be confused with 忍ぶ \"to endure, to hide\", which is read the same.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の勇気ある行動を皆が称えた。","romaji":"Kare no yūki aru kōdō wo mina ga tataeta.","german":"Everyone praised his courageous action.","furigana":"かれ|ゆうき|こうどう|みな|たた"},{"japanese":"試合の後、両チームの選手は互いの健闘を称え合った。","romaji":"Shiai no ato, ryou chiimu no senshu wa tagai no kentou o tataeatta.","german":"After the match, the players of both teams praised each other's good fight.","furigana":"しあい|あと|りょう|せんしゅ|たが|けんとう|たた|あ"},{"japanese":"博士の功績を称えて、記念館が建てられた。","romaji":"Hakase no kouseki o tataete, kinenkan ga taterareta.","german":"A memorial hall was built in honor of the scientist's achievements.","furigana":"はかせ|こうせき|たた|きねんかん|た"}],"notes":"称える means \"to extol, to praise, to honor\" and is used for public, ceremonial praise of achievements or deeds: 功績を称える, 健闘を称える, 勇気を称える. Also common is 称え合う \"to honor one another\". Other spellings are 讃える and 賛える. In everyday life one praises with 褒める, which suits children or small things; 称える, by contrast, sounds elevated.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この神社には学問の神様が祀られている。","romaji":"Kono jinja ni wa gakumon no kamisama ga matsurarete iru.","german":"The god of learning is enshrined in this shrine.","furigana":"じんじゃ|がくもん|かみさま|まつ"},{"japanese":"この村では昔から山の神を祀ってきた。","romaji":"Kono mura de wa mukashi kara yama no kami o matsutte kita.","german":"In this village, the mountain god has been worshipped since ancient times.","furigana":"むら|むかし|やま|かみ|まつ"},{"japanese":"家の神棚には、地元の神社のお札が祀ってある。","romaji":"Ie no kamidana ni wa, jimoto no jinja no ofuda ga matsutte aru.","german":"A talisman from the local shrine is enshrined on the household altar.","furigana":"いえ|かみだな|じもと|じんじゃ|ふだ|まつ"}],"notes":"祀る means \"to worship as a deity, to enshrine in a shrine\" and is very often used in the passive: 〜に〜が祀られている \"... is enshrined in ...\". Objects are gods, ancestors or deified people (菅原道真を祀る神社). The spelling 祭る is also common; 祭り \"festival\" comes from it. For the act of praying itself, one says 拝む or お参りする.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"初日の出を拝むために海岸へ行った。","romaji":"Hatsuhinode wo ogamu tame ni kaigan e itta.","german":"I went to the beach to pay my respects to the first sunrise of the year.","furigana":"はつひ|で|おが|かいがん|い"},{"japanese":"祖母は毎朝、仏壇の前で手を合わせて拝んでいる。","romaji":"Sobo wa maiasa, butsudan no mae de te o awasete ogande iru.","german":"Every morning my grandmother puts her hands together and prays in front of the household Buddhist altar.","furigana":"そぼ|まいあさ|ぶつだん|まえ|て|あ|おが"},{"japanese":"一度でいいから、本物のオーロラを拝んでみたい。","romaji":"Ichido de ii kara, honmono no oorora o ogande mitai.","german":"Even just once, I'd like to get to see real aurora.","furigana":"いちど|ほんもの|おが"}],"notes":"拝む first means \"to pray with folded hands, to bow reverently\" (仏像を拝む, 手を合わせて拝む). Figuratively it means \"to beg earnestly\" (この通り拝むから \"I'm begging you\") and, modestly or jokingly, \"to get to see\": 初日の出を拝む, 一度でいいから拝んでみたい. The elevated compound 拝見する \"to look at\" (謙譲語, humble) comes from the same root. For visiting a shrine itself, one says 参拝する or お参りする.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"正月に家族で神社に参拝した。","romaji":"Shōgatsu ni kazoku de jinja ni sanpai shita.","german":"At New Year, we visited the shrine as a family.","furigana":"しょうがつ|かぞく|じんじゃ|さんぱい"},{"japanese":"神社では「二礼二拍手一礼」で参拝するのが一般的だ。","romaji":"Jinja de wa \"nirei nihakushu ichirei\" de sanpai suru no ga ippanteki da.","german":"At a shrine, it is common to pray with \"two bows, two claps, one bow\".","furigana":"じんじゃ|にれいにはくしゅいちれい|さんぱい|いっぱんてき"},{"japanese":"週末になると、多くの観光客がこの寺に参拝に訪れる。","romaji":"Shuumatsu ni naru to, ooku no kankoukyaku ga kono tera ni sanpai ni otozureru.","german":"On weekends, many tourists visit this temple to pray.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|おお|かんこうきゃく|てら|さんぱい|おとず"}],"notes":"参拝する means \"to visit a shrine or temple in order to pray there\"; the place is marked with に: 神社に参拝する. It is the more formal word for お参りする, which is heard more often in daily life. The first shrine visit of the new year is called 初詣; someone who merely tours a temple without praying does 拝観. Typical combinations: 参拝客 \"visitors\", 参拝の作法 \"etiquette when visiting\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仏壇に花と果物を供えた。","romaji":"Butsudan ni hana to kudamono wo sonaeta.","german":"I offered flowers and fruit at the household Buddhist altar.","furigana":"ぶつだん|はな|くだもの|そな"},{"japanese":"お彼岸にはお墓に線香と花を供えに行く。","romaji":"Ohigan ni wa ohaka ni senkou to hana o sonae ni iku.","german":"At the equinox week, we go to the grave to offer incense and flowers.","furigana":"ひがん|はか|せんこう|はな|そな|い"},{"japanese":"事故現場には、たくさんの花束が供えられていた。","romaji":"Jiko genba ni wa, takusan no hanataba ga sonaerarete ita.","german":"Many bouquets had been laid at the scene of the accident.","furigana":"じこげんば|はなたば|そな"}],"notes":"供える means \"to present as an offering\", i.e. to place flowers, food or incense before gods, Buddha or the deceased: 仏壇に花を供える, お墓に線香を供える. The related noun is お供え \"offering\". Beware of the identically read 備える \"to prepare, to equip\" (地震に備える). Unlike 捧げる, 供える stresses the concrete setting down of offerings.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"紅葉が山を美しく彩っている。","romaji":"Kōyō ga yama wo utsukushiku irodotte iru.","german":"The autumn leaves beautifully color the mountains.","furigana":"こうよう|やま|うつく|いろど"},{"japanese":"色とりどりの料理がパーティーのテーブルを彩った。","romaji":"Irotoridori no ryouri ga paatii no teeburu o irodotta.","german":"Colorful dishes adorned the party table.","furigana":"いろ|りょうり|いろど"},{"japanese":"彼女の歌は80年代の日本の音楽シーンを彩った。","romaji":"Kanojo no uta wa hachijuu nendai no Nihon no ongaku shiin o irodotta.","german":"Her songs colored the Japanese music scene of the 80s.","furigana":"かのじょ|うた|ねんだい|にほん|おんがく|いろど"}],"notes":"彩る means \"to color, to decorate with color\" and is mostly used figuratively: nature, flowers or lights \"adorn\" a place (紅葉が山を彩る), dishes get color (料理を彩る). Figuratively it means \"to shape, to enrich\": 歴史を彩った人物, 青春を彩る思い出. It is an elevated, rather written word; the noun 彩り means \"coloring, a touch of color\" (彩りを添える). In everyday speech one often says 飾る.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"乾燥した肌をクリームで潤した。","romaji":"Kansō shita hada wo kurīmu de uruoshita.","german":"I moisturized my dry skin with cream.","furigana":"かんそう|はだ|うるお"},{"japanese":"山道を歩いたあと、冷たい湧き水でのどを潤した。","romaji":"Yamamichi o aruita ato, tsumetai wakimizu de nodo o uruoshita.","german":"After walking the mountain path, I quenched my thirst with cold spring water.","furigana":"やまみち|ある|つめ|わ|みず|うるお"},{"japanese":"観光客の増加が地元の経済を潤している。","romaji":"Kankoukyaku no zouka ga jimoto no keizai o uruoshite iru.","german":"The increase in tourists is enriching the local economy.","furigana":"かんこうきゃく|ぞうか|じもと|けいざい|うるお"}],"notes":"潤す literally means \"to moisten, to wet\" (のどを潤す \"to wet one's throat\", 肌を潤す) and figuratively \"to enrich, to bring profit\": 地域経済を潤す, 心を潤す. The intransitive counterpart is 潤う \"to become moist; to profit\" (観光で町が潤う). Related is the noun 潤い \"moisture; enrichment\" (生活に潤いを与える). For skin and cosmetics one also hears 保湿する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"音楽を聴いて緊張を和らげた。","romaji":"Ongaku wo kiite kinchō wo yawarageta.","german":"I listened to music to ease the tension.","furigana":"おんがく|き|きんちょう|やわ"},{"japanese":"この薬には痛みを和らげる効果がある。","romaji":"Kono kusuri ni wa itami o yawarageru kouka ga aru.","german":"This medicine relieves pain.","furigana":"くすり|いた|やわ|こうか"},{"japanese":"彼の冗談が、重かった会議の雰囲気を和らげた。","romaji":"Kare no joudan ga, omokatta kaigi no fun'iki o yawarageta.","german":"His joke lightened the gloomy mood in the meeting.","furigana":"かれ|じょうだん|おも|かいぎ|ふんいき|やわ"}],"notes":"和らげる means \"to soften, to ease, to relax\" and is used with pain, tension, atmosphere or expressions: 痛みを和らげる, 緊張を和らげる, 雰囲気を和らげる, 表現を和らげる \"to express oneself more mildly\". The intransitive counterpart is 和らぐ \"to subside, to become milder\" (寒さが和らぐ). Similar but more specialized is 緩和する (more technical: 規制を緩和する). The word is neutral to slightly elevated.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"怒りを鎮めるために深呼吸をした。","romaji":"Ikari wo shizumeru tame ni shinkokyū wo shita.","german":"I took a deep breath to calm my anger.","furigana":"いか|しず|しんこきゅう"},{"japanese":"警察が出動し、ようやく暴動を鎮めた。","romaji":"Keisatsu ga shutsudou shi, youyaku boudou o shizumeta.","german":"The police were dispatched and finally quelled the riot.","furigana":"けいさつ|しゅつどう|ぼうどう|しず"},{"japanese":"昔の人は祭りを行って、神の怒りを鎮めようとした。","romaji":"Mukashi no hito wa matsuri o okonatte, kami no ikari o shizumeyou to shita.","german":"People in the old days held festivals to try to appease the wrath of the gods.","furigana":"むかし|ひと|まつ|おこな|かみ|いか|しず"}],"notes":"鎮める means \"to calm, to soothe\" and for unrest \"to put down, to bring under control\": 怒りを鎮める, 気持ちを鎮める, 暴動を鎮める, 痛みを鎮める; in a religious sense also to appease spirits or gods (霊を鎮める). The intransitive counterpart is 鎮まる. Beware of the identically read verbs: 静める \"to bring to quiet, to make still\" (partly overlapping) and 沈める \"to sink\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"東京は人が多いけれど整然としています。","romaji":"Tōkyō wa hito ga ōi keredo seizen to shite imasu.","german":"Although there are many people in Tokyo, it is well organized.","furigana":"とうきょう|ひと|おお|せいぜん"},{"japanese":"図書館の本棚には、本が整然と並んでいる。","romaji":"Toshokan no hondana ni wa, hon ga seizen to narande iru.","german":"In the library shelves, the books are lined up neatly.","furigana":"としょかん|ほんだな|ほん|せいぜん|なら"},{"japanese":"彼女はいつも理路整然と自分の意見を述べる。","romaji":"Kanojo wa itsumo riro seizen to jibun no iken o noberu.","german":"She always presents her opinion clearly and logically structured.","furigana":"かのじょ|りろせいぜん|じぶん|いけん|の"}],"notes":"整然 describes something \"well-ordered, systematic, neatly lined up\" and is a たる-adjective: it is almost always used as 整然と (adverbially: 整然と並ぶ) or 整然とした + noun (整然とした街並み). Typical for rows, rooms, crowds or arguments (理路整然と話す \"to speak in a logically coherent way\"). The opposite is 雑然 \"untidy, in disorder\". In daily life, for a tidy room one says きちんと or 片付いている instead.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一度でも規則の例外を認めると、悪例を残すことになる。","romaji":"Ichido demo kisoku no reigai o mitomeru to, akurei o nokosu koto ni naru.","german":"If you accept an exception to the rules even once, you will set a bad precedent.","furigana":"いちど|きそく|れいがい|みと|あくれい|のこ"},{"japanese":"この教科書では、分かりにくい文章が悪例として挙げられている。","romaji":"Kono kyoukasho de wa, wakarinikui bunshou ga akurei to shite agerarete iru.","german":"In this textbook, hard-to-understand sentences are given as bad examples.","furigana":"きょうかしょ|わ|ぶんしょう|あくれい|あ"},{"japanese":"その会社の対応は、危機管理の悪例としてよく紹介される。","romaji":"Sono kaisha no taiou wa, kiki kanri no akurei to shite yoku shoukai sareru.","german":"That company's response is often cited as a bad example of crisis management.","furigana":"かいしゃ|たいおう|ききかんり|あくれい|しょうかい"}],"notes":"悪例 means \"bad example\" and above all \"bad precedent\", i.e. something that others might later imitate as an unfavorable model. Typical are 悪例を残す／作る \"to set a bad precedent\" and 悪例として挙げる \"to cite as a negative example\". The word is written-language; in conversation one says 悪い例. The counterpart is 好例 \"good example\"; similar is 悪しき前例.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"強い日差しに当たって肌を傷めてしまった。","romaji":"Tsuyoi hizashi ni atatte hada o itamete shimatta.","german":"I damaged my skin by exposing it to the strong sunlight.","furigana":"つよ|ひざ|あ|はだ|いた"},{"japanese":"パーマとカラーを繰り返したせいで、髪をひどく傷めた。","romaji":"Paama to karaa o kurikaeshita sei de, kami o hidoku itameta.","german":"I badly damaged my hair by repeatedly getting perms and color.","furigana":"く|かえ|かみ|いた"},{"japanese":"荷物を詰め込みすぎて、スーツケースを傷めてしまった。","romaji":"Nimotsu o tsumekomisugite, suutsukeesu o itamete shimatta.","german":"I packed too much and damaged the suitcase.","furigana":"にもつ|つ|こ|いた"}],"notes":"傷める means \"to damage, to harm, to injure (a body part)\", often unintentionally through wrong treatment: 髪を傷める, 肌を傷める, 家具を傷める. The intransitive counterpart is 傷む \"to get damaged, to spoil\" (果物が傷む). For aching body parts one mostly writes 痛める (腰を痛める, 心を痛める); for skin and hair both spellings are common. For deliberate destruction one says 壊す more often.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"事故で多くの貴い命が奪われた。","romaji":"Jiko de ooku no toutoi inochi ga ubawareta.","german":"Many precious lives were lost in the disaster.","furigana":"じこ|おお|とうと|いのち|うば"},{"japanese":"昔は、貴い身分の人しかこの門を通れなかった。","romaji":"Mukashi wa, toutoi mibun no hito shika kono mon o toorenakatta.","german":"In the past, only people of high rank were allowed to pass through this gate.","furigana":"むかし|とうと|みぶん|ひと|もん|とお"},{"japanese":"失敗から学んだことは、私にとって貴い財産だ。","romaji":"Shippai kara mananda koto wa, watashi ni totte toutoi zaisan da.","german":"What I learned from my mistakes is a valuable treasure to me.","furigana":"しっぱい|まな|わたし|とうと|ざいさん"}],"notes":"貴い means \"precious, valuable\" as well as \"noble, exalted, distinguished\" and is an elevated, rather written word. It is read the same as 尊い (also たっとい); the rule of thumb is: 貴い for value and rank (貴い身分, 貴い資料), 尊い for the venerable and sacred (尊い犠牲) - in practice the two are mixed, especially with 命. In daily life one usually says 大切 or 貴重.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「じゃあね、たまには連絡してね。」「もちろん。てか、近いうちにうなぎ丼でも食べに行こうよ。」「いいね。」「よかった。じゃあ、また連絡するね。」「ええ、またね。」","romaji":"\"Jā ne, tama ni wa renraku shite ne.\" \"Mochiron. Teka, chikai uchi ni unagi donburi demo tabe ni ikō yo.\" \"Ii ne.\" \"Yokatta. Jā, mata renraku suru ne.\" \"Ē, mata ne.\"","german":"\"Take care, and keep in touch once in a while.\" \"Of course. Actually, let's go eat unagi donburi sometime soon.\" \"Sounds good.\" \"Great. I'll get in touch again.\" \"Yes, see you.\"","furigana":"れんらく|ちか|どんぶり|た|い|れんらく"},{"japanese":"土用の丑の日には、うなぎ丼を食べる人が多い。","romaji":"Doyō no ushi no hi ni wa, unagi donburi o taberu hito ga ōi.","german":"Many people eat unagi donburi on the Day of the Ox in midsummer.","furigana":"どよう|うし|ひ|どんぶり|た|ひと|おお"},{"japanese":"うなぎ丼は高いので、特別な日にしか食べません。","romaji":"Unagi donburi wa takai node, tokubetsu na hi ni shika tabemasen.","german":"Unagi donburi is expensive, so I only eat it on special days.","furigana":"どんぶり|たか|とくべつ|ひ|た"}],"notes":"うなぎ丼 is a bowl of rice topped with grilled eel (蒲焼) glazed with sweet sauce (タレ). In everyday speech it is mostly shortened to うな丼. It should be distinguished from うな重, which is served in a lacquered box (重箱) and is considered finer. Traditionally, eel is eaten in midsummer on 土用の丑の日 for strength. 丼 on its own is read どんぶり, but in many compounds it is shortened to -どん (牛丼, 天丼).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"親子丼ほど美味しいものはない。","romaji":"Oyako donburi hodo oishii mono wa nai.","german":"There is nothing as delicious as oyakodon.","furigana":"おやこどんぶり|おい"},{"japanese":"昼ご飯に親子丼を作りました。","romaji":"Hirugohan ni oyako donburi o tsukurimashita.","german":"I made oyakodon for lunch.","furigana":"ひる|はん|おやこどんぶり|つく"},{"japanese":"この店の親子丼は卵がとろとろでおいしいです。","romaji":"Kono mise no oyako donburi wa tamago ga torotoro de oishii desu.","german":"The oyakodon at this restaurant is delicious because the egg is so runny.","furigana":"みせ|おやこどんぶり|たまご"}],"notes":"親子丼 literally means \"parent-and-child bowl\": chicken (parent) and egg (child) are cooked with onions in a sweetish broth and served on rice. Colloquially it is often shortened and read おやこどん. Related dishes are 他人丼 (\"stranger bowl\" with beef or pork and egg), カツ丼 and 牛丼. One orders it with 親子丼をください or 親子丼にします.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"男は舐めていた飴を吐き出しました。","romaji":"Otoko wa namete ita ame o hakidashimashita.","german":"The man spat out the candy he had been sucking.","furigana":"おとこ|な|あめ|は|だ"},{"japanese":"工場の煙突が黒い煙を吐き出している。","romaji":"Koujou no entotsu ga kuroi kemuri o hakidashite iru.","german":"The factory chimney is belching black smoke.","furigana":"こうじょう|えんとつ|くろ|けむり|は|だ"},{"japanese":"友達に不満を全部吐き出したら、少しすっきりした。","romaji":"Tomodachi ni fuman o zenbu hakidashitara, sukoshi sukkiri shita.","german":"After I got all my complaints off my chest to my friend, I felt a little better.","furigana":"ともだち|ふまん|ぜんぶは|だ|すこ"}],"notes":"吐き出す (from 吐く \"to expel, to vomit\" + 出す \"out\") stresses that something comes entirely out of the mouth or the inside: spitting out food (ガムを吐き出す), expelling smoke or air (煙を吐き出す) and, figuratively, getting rid of pent-up feelings (不満を吐き出す, 本音を吐き出す). The verb is transitive with を. For \"to throw up\", one rather says simply 吐く or, politely, 戻す.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"奴ら、俺たちは働いてるってのに、呑気に日光浴なんかしてやがるぜ。","romaji":"Yatsura, oretachi wa hataraiteru tte noni, nonki ni nikkouyoku nanka shiteyagaru ze.","german":"Those guys are sunbathing without a care while we're hard at work.","furigana":"やつ|おれ|はたら|のんき|にっこうよく"},{"japanese":"奴らにだけは絶対に負けたくない。","romaji":"Yatsura ni dake wa zettai ni maketakunai.","german":"I never want to lose to them, of all people.","furigana":"やつ|ぜったい|ま"},{"japanese":"奴らがまた来たぞ。気をつけろ。","romaji":"Yatsura ga mata kita zo. Ki o tsukero.","german":"Those guys are here again. Watch out.","furigana":"やつ|き|き"}],"notes":"奴ら is the plural of 奴 \"fellow, guy\" and means, pejoratively, \"those guys over there\". It is rude, colloquial and rather masculine; one hears it mainly in crime stories, manga or among friends when talking about opponents or unpleasant people. It is often written in kana (やつら, ヤツら). Neutrally one says 彼ら or あの人たち, politely あの方々.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"口紅をポケットに入れっぱなしにしてたみたいで、乾燥機の中で溶けちゃったのよね。","romaji":"Kuchibeni o poketto ni ireppanashi ni shi teta mitai de, kansōki no naka de toke chatta no yo ne.","german":"I seem to have left my lipstick in my pocket, and it melted in the dryer.","furigana":"くちべに|い|かんそうき|なか|と"},{"japanese":"出かける前に赤い口紅を塗った。","romaji":"Dekakeru mae ni akai kuchibeni o nutta.","german":"Before going out, she put on red lipstick.","furigana":"で|まえ|あか|くちべに|ぬ"},{"japanese":"コップに口紅がついてしまった。","romaji":"Koppu ni kuchibeni ga tsuite shimatta.","german":"Lipstick got on the glass.","furigana":"くちべに"}],"notes":"口紅 (literally \"mouth red\") is lipstick. Typical combinations are 口紅を塗る or 口紅をつける \"to apply\", 口紅を落とす \"to remove\" and 口紅がつく \"to rub off\" (e.g. on a glass). In modern daily life and in advertising one often says リップ or リップスティック; 口紅 sounds a bit more classic. リップクリーム, by contrast, is lip balm.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"そのスリッパは私のお薦めです。","romaji":"Sono surippa wa watashi no osusume desu.","german":"These slippers are my recommendation.","furigana":"わたし|すす"},{"japanese":"先生の勧めで、日本語能力試験を受けることにした。","romaji":"Sensei no susume de, Nihongo Nōryoku Shiken o ukeru koto ni shita.","german":"On my teacher's advice, I decided to take the JLPT.","furigana":"せんせい|すす|にほんごのうりょくしけん|う"},{"japanese":"店員さんにおすすめを聞いてから注文しよう。","romaji":"Ten'in-san ni osusume o kiite kara chūmon shiyō.","german":"Let's first ask the server for their recommendation and then order.","furigana":"てんいん|き|ちゅうもん"}],"notes":"すすめ is the noun of すすめる. With 薦め one recommends a person or thing (a product, a candidate; cf. 推薦), with 勧め one advises an action: 医者の勧めで \"on the doctor's advice\". In practice it is mostly polite with お and often in hiragana: おすすめ, 今日のおすすめ, おすすめは何ですか. Do not confuse with 進める \"to advance, to move forward\", which is read the same.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お勤め先はどちらですか？","romaji":"O tsutomesaki wa dochira desu ka?","german":"Where do you work?","furigana":"つと|さき"},{"japanese":"母は勤めに出ているので、昼間は家にいません。","romaji":"Haha wa tsutome ni dete iru node, hiruma wa ie ni imasen.","german":"My mother goes out to work, so she isn't home during the day.","furigana":"はは|つと|で|ひるま|いえ"},{"japanese":"子どもを守るのは親の務めだ。","romaji":"Kodomo o mamoru no wa oya no tsutome da.","german":"Protecting children is a parent's duty.","furigana":"こ|まも|おや|つと"}],"notes":"勤め is work as an employee, a regular job: 勤めに出る \"to go to work, to be employed,\" 勤め先 \"place of work,\" 会社勤め \"to be a company employee\"; the verb for it is 勤める (会社に勤める). In the meaning \"duty, task, role,\" 務め is written: 親の務め, 務めを果たす \"to fulfill one's duty\" (verb 務める \"to serve as ...\"). 仕事 is more general and means any work or activity. お勤め can also refer to a Buddhist service.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の父は、電磁場発生装置の特許を持っているんですが、私はそれが何のためのものなのかよく分かりません。","romaji":"Watashi no chichi wa, denjiba hassei sōchi no tokkyo o motte iru n desu ga, watashi wa sore ga nani no tame no mono na no ka yoku wakarimasen.","german":"My father holds a patent for an electromagnetic field generator, but I'm not sure what it's for.","furigana":"わたし|ちち|でんじばはっせいそうち|とっきょ|も|わたし|なに|わ"},{"japanese":"電流が流れると、そのまわりに電磁場が生じる。","romaji":"Denryuu ga nagareru to, sono mawari ni denjiba ga shoujiru.","german":"When a current flows, an electromagnetic field arises around it.","furigana":"でんりゅう|なが|でんじば|しょう"},{"japanese":"強い電磁場は精密機器に影響を与えることがある。","romaji":"Tsuyoi denjiba wa seimitsu kiki ni eikyou o ataeru koto ga aru.","german":"Strong electromagnetic fields can affect precision instruments.","furigana":"つよ|でんじば|せいみつきき|えいきょう|あた"}],"notes":"電磁場 is composed of 電磁 \"electromagnetic\" and 場 \"field\" and is a technical term from physics and engineering. In standards and official texts one also reads 電磁界 (でんじかい). Separately, the electric field is 電場 and the magnetic field 磁場. In everyday speech one more often speaks of 電磁波 \"electromagnetic waves\", for example with mobile phones or microwaves.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は源氏物語を漫画でしか読んだことがありません。","romaji":"Watashi wa genjimonogatari o manga de shika yonda koto ga arimasen.","german":"I have only read The Tale of Genji as a manga.","furigana":"わたし|げんじものがたり|まんが|よ"},{"japanese":"源氏物語は紫式部によって書かれた。","romaji":"Genji monogatari wa Murasaki Shikibu ni yotte kakareta.","german":"The Tale of Genji was written by Murasaki Shikibu.","furigana":"げんじものがたり|むらさきしきぶ|か"},{"japanese":"高校の古文の授業で源氏物語の一部を読みました。","romaji":"Koukou no kobun no jugyou de Genji monogatari no ichibu o yomimashita.","german":"In classical Japanese class at high school, we read a part of The Tale of Genji.","furigana":"こうこう|こぶん|じゅぎょう|げんじものがたり|いちぶ|よ"}],"notes":"源氏物語 is the famous court novel of the Heian period, written around the year 1000 by the court lady 紫式部; it tells of the life and love affairs of the \"shining prince\" 光源氏. 物語 means \"tale\". Japanese students read excerpts in 古文 class; many know it, however, through 現代語訳 (translations into modern Japanese) or manga versions.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 29"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「お早うございます。何にいたしましょうか？」「いつものやつ頼むよ。」「かしこまりました。ビールと枝豆でございますね。」「そうだよ。」","romaji":"Ohayou gozaimasu. Nani ni itashimashou ka? Itsumo no yatsu tanomu yo. Kashikomarimashita. Biiru to edamame de gozaimasu ne. Sou da yo.","german":"\"Good morning. What can I get you?\" \"The usual, please.\" \"Certainly. Beer and edamame, correct?\" \"That's right.\"","furigana":"はよ|なに|たの|えだまめ"},{"japanese":"夏はやっぱりビールと枝豆だね。","romaji":"Natsu wa yappari biiru to edamame da ne.","german":"In summer, it's beer and edamame, after all.","furigana":"なつ|えだまめ"},{"japanese":"枝豆を塩ゆでして、おつまみにした。","romaji":"Edamame o shioyude shite, otsumami ni shita.","german":"I boiled edamame in salted water and had it as a snack to go with drinks.","furigana":"えだまめ|しお"}],"notes":"枝豆 literally means \"branch bean\": unripe green soybeans, which used to be sold with the branch. They are boiled in salted water (塩ゆで) and the beans are squeezed out of the pod (さや). In Japan they are the classic snack with beer in an 居酒屋, especially in summer. Ripe, dried soybeans, by contrast, are called 大豆.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いつもパスワードに母親の旧姓を使うんですよ。","romaji":"Itsumo pasuwādo ni hahaoya no kyūsei o tsukau n desu yo.","german":"I always use my mother's maiden name as a password.","furigana":"ははおや|きゅうせい|つか"},{"japanese":"結婚後も、職場では旧姓を使っています。","romaji":"Kekkon go mo, shokuba de wa kyuusei o tsukatte imasu.","german":"Even after marriage, I use my maiden name at work.","furigana":"けっこんご|しょくば|きゅうせい|つか"},{"japanese":"離婚して旧姓に戻りました。","romaji":"Rikon shite kyuusei ni modorimashita.","german":"After the divorce, I went back to my maiden name.","furigana":"りこん|きゅうせい|もど"}],"notes":"旧姓 (旧 \"former\" + 姓 \"family name\") is the family name before a change of name, usually the birth name of a married woman. Typical are 旧姓は〜です, 旧姓を使う (continuing to use the old name at work, 旧姓使用) and 旧姓に戻る after a divorce. The word is neutral and often appears on forms and identity documents.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の姓名を使用しないでください。","romaji":"Watashi no seimei o shiyō shinaide kudasai.","german":"Please do not use my full name.","furigana":"わたし|せいめい|しよう"},{"japanese":"申込書には姓名を漢字で正確に書いてください。","romaji":"Moushikomisho ni wa seimei o kanji de seikaku ni kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write your full name accurately in kanji on the application form.","furigana":"もうしこみしょ|せいめい|かんじ|せいかく|か"},{"japanese":"姓名判断で名前の画数を調べてもらった。","romaji":"Seimei handan de namae no kakusuu o shirabete moratta.","german":"I had the stroke count of my name checked by a name-divination reading.","furigana":"せいめいはんだん|なまえ|かくすう|しら"}],"notes":"姓名 combines 姓 \"family name\" and 名 \"given name\" into the full name. It is formal and written-language; on forms 氏名 is more common, and in conversation one simply says 名前 or フルネーム. Also well known is 姓名判断, name divination based on the stroke count of the kanji.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"シャツを入れないと、お客さんに甘く見られるかもしれないよ。","romaji":"Shatsu o irenai to, okyakusan ni amaku mirareru kamo shirenai yo.","german":"If you don't tuck in your shirt, customers may not take you seriously.","furigana":"い|きゃく|あま|み"},{"japanese":"山の天気を甘く見てはいけません。","romaji":"Yama no tenki o amaku mite wa ikemasen.","german":"You must not underestimate the weather in the mountains.","furigana":"やま|てんき|あま|み"},{"japanese":"試験を甘く見ていたら、半分も解けなかった。","romaji":"Shiken o amaku mite itara, hanbun mo tokenakatta.","german":"I had taken the exam lightly and couldn't even solve half of it.","furigana":"しけん|あま|み|はんぶん|と"}],"notes":"甘く見る (甘い \"sweet, lenient\" + 見る) means \"to underestimate, to not take seriously\": 〜を甘く見る. Very common as a warning: 〜を甘く見てはいけない or 甘く見ないほうがいい. In the passive, 甘く見られる means \"to not be taken seriously\". Similar are colloquial なめる and written 侮る (あなどる); 甘く考える, by contrast, stresses \"to think too optimistically\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は甘党で、ケーキには目がないの。","romaji":"Watashi wa amatō de, kēki ni wa me ga nai no.","german":"I have a sweet tooth and can't resist cake.","furigana":"わたし|あまとう|め"},{"japanese":"父は辛党だが、母はかなりの甘党だ。","romaji":"Chichi wa karatou da ga, haha wa kanari no amatou da.","german":"My father prefers drinking, but my mother has quite a sweet tooth.","furigana":"ちち|からとう|はは|あまとう"},{"japanese":"甘党の友達のために、駅前のケーキ屋でお土産を買った。","romaji":"Amatou no tomodachi no tame ni, ekimae no keekiya de omiyage o katta.","german":"For my friend with a sweet tooth, I bought a souvenir at the cake shop in front of the station.","furigana":"あまとう|ともだち|えきまえ|や|みやげ|か"}],"notes":"甘党 (literally \"party of the sweet\") is someone who likes eating sweets. Traditionally it is the opposite of 辛党, a person who prefers drinking alcohol; a 甘党 thus prefers cake to sake. Typical are 私は甘党です, かなりの甘党 and the phrase 甘いものに目がない \"cannot resist sweets\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大将が机の角に頭をぶつけて、たんこぶを作っちまったんだよ。","romaji":"Taishou ga tsukue no kaku ni atama o butsukete, tankobu o tsukutchimatta n da yo.","german":"The boss bumped his head on the corner of the desk and got a lump.","furigana":"たいしょう|つくえ|かく|あたま|つく"},{"japanese":"寿司屋の大将に今日のおすすめを聞いた。","romaji":"Sushiya no taishou ni kyou no osusume o kiita.","german":"I asked the owner of the sushi restaurant for today's recommendation.","furigana":"すしや|たいしょう|きょう|き"},{"japanese":"彼の祖父は海軍の大将だった。","romaji":"Kare no sofu wa kaigun no taishou datta.","german":"His grandfather was an admiral in the navy.","furigana":"かれ|そふ|かいぐん|たいしょう"}],"notes":"大将 is originally the highest officer rank, \"general\" or, in the navy, \"admiral\" (陸軍大将, 海軍大将). In everyday life, though, you hear it much more often as a colloquial form of address for the boss or owner of a small eatery, especially at a sushi restaurant: 大将、いつもの！ (\"Boss, the usual!\") In team competitions (kendō, jūdō), the last fighter of a team is also called 大将. お山の大将 mockingly refers to someone who plays the \"king of the hill\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どちらの主将もそんなに強くはなかった。","romaji":"Dochira no shushō mo sonnani tsuyoku wa nakatta.","german":"Neither of the two team captains was particularly strong.","furigana":"しゅしょう|つよ"},{"japanese":"彼女はバレー部の主将に選ばれた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa bareebu no shushou ni erabareta.","german":"She was chosen as captain of the volleyball club.","furigana":"かのじょ|ぶ|しゅしょう|えら"},{"japanese":"主将として、チームをまとめるのは簡単ではない。","romaji":"Shushou to shite, chiimu o matomeru no wa kantan de wa nai.","german":"As captain, it is not easy to hold the team together.","furigana":"しゅしょう|かんたん"}],"notes":"主将 is the captain of a sports team, especially in school and university clubs (野球部の主将, 主将に選ばれる); the vice-captain is called 副主将. In professional sports and in everyday speech, people usually say キャプテン. The older meaning \"commander-in-chief\" is found only in historical texts. Not to be confused with 部長, the organizational head of a club.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"スケート選手たちは、日本スケート連盟について不平を漏らしています。","romaji":"Sukēto senshutachi wa, Nippon sukēto renmei nitsuite fuhei o morashite imasu.","german":"The skaters are complaining about the Japan Skating Federation.","furigana":"せんしゅ|にっぽん|れんめい|ふへい|も"},{"japanese":"国際連盟は第一次世界大戦の後に作られた。","romaji":"Kokusai Renmei wa daiichiji sekai taisen no ato ni tsukurareta.","german":"The League of Nations was founded after the First World War.","furigana":"こくさいれんめい|だいいちじせかいたいせん|あと|つく"},{"japanese":"うちの大学の空手部は学生連盟に加盟している。","romaji":"Uchi no daigaku no karatebu wa gakusei renmei ni kamei shite iru.","german":"Our university's karate club is a member of the student federation.","furigana":"だいがく|からてぶ|がくせいれんめい|かめい"}],"notes":"連盟 is a league or federation in which organizations, clubs or states join together, e.g. 日本スケート連盟 or, historically, 国際連盟 \"League of Nations\". One joins with 連盟に加盟する. Similar are 協会 \"association, society\" (日本サッカー協会), 連合 \"union\" (国際連合 = the UN) and 同盟 \"alliance\" between states. The word is rather formal and often appears in organization names.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この空港に遺失物取扱所があるかどうかをご存知ですか。","romaji":"Kono kūkō ni ishitsubutsu toriatsukaisho ga aru ka dō ka o gozonji desu ka.","german":"Do you know whether there is a lost-and-found office at this airport?","furigana":"くうこう|いしつぶつとりあつかいしょ|ぞんじ"},{"japanese":"警察に財布の遺失届を出しました。","romaji":"Keisatsu ni saifu no ishitsu todoke o dashimashita.","german":"I filed a loss report for my wallet with the police.","furigana":"けいさつ|さいふ|いしつとどけ|だ"},{"japanese":"書類を遺失した場合は、速やかに担当者に報告してください。","romaji":"Shorui o ishitsu shita baai wa, sumiyaka ni tantousha ni houkoku shite kudasai.","german":"If you lose any documents, please report it to the person in charge immediately.","furigana":"しょるい|いしつ|ばあい|すみ|たんとうしゃ|ほうこく"}],"notes":"遺失 is an official word for the \"loss\" of objects. It is found almost only in compounds such as 遺失物 \"lost item, lost property\", 遺失物取扱所 \"lost-and-found office\" and 遺失届 \"loss report\" filed with the police. The verb 遺失する appears only in official texts; in everyday speech people say なくす or 落とす, and lost property is called 落とし物 or 忘れ物.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どうして犯人が遺体の唇を舐めたのかは不明です。","romaji":"Dōshite hannin ga itai no kuchibiru o nameta no ka wa fumei desu.","german":"It is unclear why the culprit licked the lips of the corpse.","furigana":"はんにん|いたい|くちびる|な|ふめい"},{"japanese":"山で行方不明になっていた男性が、遺体で発見された。","romaji":"Yama de yukue fumei ni natte ita dansei ga, itai de hakken sareta.","german":"A man who had gone missing in the mountains was found dead.","furigana":"やま|ゆくえふめい|だんせい|いたい|はっけん"},{"japanese":"葬儀の前に、家族は遺体と最後のお別れをした。","romaji":"Sougi no mae ni, kazoku wa itai to saigo no owakare o shita.","german":"Before the funeral, the family said their last goodbye to the deceased.","furigana":"そうぎ|まえ|かぞく|いたい|さいご|わか"}],"notes":"遺体 is the respectful word for a person's dead body and is used in the news and at funerals (遺体が見つかる, 遺体で発見される \"to be found dead\", polite ご遺体). 死体, by contrast, is matter-of-fact and sounds cold when referring to relatives; it is also used for animals. 遺骨 are the bones or the ashes after cremation.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は歯のしつこい着色汚れを奈良の歯医者さんで落としました。","romaji":"Watashi wa ha no shitsukoi chakushoku yogore o Nara no haishasan de otoshimashita.","german":"I had stubborn stains removed from my teeth at a dentist in Nara.","furigana":"わたし|は|ちゃくしょくよご|なら|はいしゃ|お"},{"japanese":"奈良の公園では、たくさんの鹿が自由に歩いている。","romaji":"Nara no kouen de wa, takusan no shika ga jiyuu ni aruite iru.","german":"In the parks of Nara, many deer walk around freely.","furigana":"なら|こうえん|しか|じゆう|ある"},{"japanese":"修学旅行で奈良と京都に行きました。","romaji":"Shuugaku ryokou de Nara to Kyouto ni ikimashita.","german":"On the school trip we went to Nara and Kyoto.","furigana":"しゅうがくりょこう|なら|きょうと|い"}],"notes":"奈良 refers both to the city 奈良市 and to Nara Prefecture 奈良県 in the Kansai region. From 710 to 784, Nara was Japan's capital as 平城京, hence the name of the 奈良時代. It is known for the Great Buddha at 東大寺 and the tame deer (鹿) of 奈良公園. Together with Kyoto, it is a classic destination for school trips.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"神奈川県では漫画がブームです。","romaji":"Kanagawaken de wa manga ga būmu desu.","german":"There is a manga boom in Kanagawa Prefecture.","furigana":"かながわけん|まんが"},{"japanese":"神奈川県の県庁所在地は横浜市です。","romaji":"Kanagawa-ken no kenchou shozaichi wa Yokohama-shi desu.","german":"The capital of Kanagawa Prefecture is Yokohama.","furigana":"かながわけん|けんちょうしょざいち|よこはまし"},{"japanese":"東京の会社に、神奈川県から電車で通っています。","romaji":"Toukyou no kaisha ni, Kanagawa-ken kara densha de kayotte imasu.","german":"I commute by train from Kanagawa Prefecture to my company in Tokyo.","furigana":"とうきょう|かいしゃ|かながわけん|でんしゃ|かよ"}],"notes":"神奈川県 is in the Kantō region, directly southwest of Tokyo; the prefectural capital (県庁所在地) is 横浜市. Well-known places include 鎌倉 with its Great Buddha and the hot-spring area 箱根. Many residents commute to Tokyo every day. In conversation people often say just 神奈川, for example 神奈川に住んでいます.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は薔薇の花びらを一枚一枚丁寧に手で摘んでいきます。","romaji":"Kanojo wa bara no hanabira o ichimai ichimai teinei ni te de tsunde ikimasu.","german":"She carefully plucks the rose petals one by one by hand.","furigana":"かのじょ||はな|いちまいいちまいていねい|て|つ"},{"japanese":"子どもたちは野原で花を摘んで、母親にプレゼントした。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa nohara de hana o tsunde, hahaoya ni purezento shita.","german":"The children picked flowers in the field and gave them to their mother.","furigana":"こ|のはら|はな|つ|ははおや"},{"japanese":"問題が大きくなる前に、その芽を摘んでおくべきだ。","romaji":"Mondai ga ookiku naru mae ni, sono me o tsunde oku beki da.","german":"You should nip that problem in the bud before it gets bigger.","furigana":"もんだい|おお|まえ|め|つ"}],"notes":"摘む means \"to pick with the fingertips\", for example flowers, herbs, tea leaves or strawberries (花を摘む, 茶を摘む, いちご摘み). The second meaning \"to snip off, to trim\" is found in the expression 芽を摘む \"to nip in the bud\" (悪の芽を摘む, also negative: 才能の芽を摘む). Note: the same kanji is also used for つまむ \"to pinch with the fingers\", which is usually written in kana. For fruit from a tree, people tend to say 採る or もぐ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ちょっと間違いを指摘させてもらってもいいですか。","romaji":"Chotto machigai o shiteki sasete moratte mo ii desu ka.","german":"Would you mind if I pointed out your mistakes?","furigana":"まちが|してき"},{"japanese":"上司に報告書のミスを指摘された。","romaji":"Joushi ni houkokusho no misu o shiteki sareta.","german":"My boss pointed out a mistake in the report to me.","furigana":"じょうし|ほうこくしょ|してき"},{"japanese":"専門家は、温暖化の影響が深刻だと指摘している。","romaji":"Senmonka wa, ondanka no eikyou ga shinkoku da to shiteki shite iru.","german":"Experts point out that the consequences of global warming are serious.","furigana":"せんもんか|おんだんか|えいきょう|しんこく|してき"}],"notes":"指摘 means \"to point something out, to point out (mistakes, problems)\" and is mostly used as a suru-verb: ミスを指摘する, 〜と指摘する \"to point out that ...\". In the passive, 指摘される means \"to have something pointed out to one\", often unpleasantly. In business language ご指摘ありがとうございます is a fixed phrase of thanks for corrections. Unlike 注意する, 指摘 contains no admonition, only the naming of the problem.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は警察官ですが、麻薬の密売組織を摘発するような機会に出くわしたことがありません。","romaji":"Watashi wa keisatsukan desu ga, mayaku no mitsubai soshiki o tekihatsu suru yō na kikai ni dekuwashita koto ga arimasen.","german":"I'm a police officer, but I've never had the chance to bust a drug trafficking ring.","furigana":"わたし|けいさつかん|まやく|みつばいそしき|てきはつ|きかい|で"},{"japanese":"警察は違法カジノを摘発し、経営者を逮捕した。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa ihou kajino o tekihatsu shi, keieisha o taiho shita.","german":"The police raided an illegal casino and arrested the operator.","furigana":"けいさつ|いほう|てきはつ|けいえいしゃ|たいほ"},{"japanese":"その会社は脱税で摘発された。","romaji":"Sono kaisha wa datsuzei de tekihatsu sareta.","german":"The company was exposed for tax evasion.","furigana":"かいしゃ|だつぜい|てきはつ"}],"notes":"摘発する means that the authorities uncover crimes or abuses and take action against them: 警察が密売組織を摘発する, 脱税で摘発される. It is a typical news word. 逮捕 is the arrest of a person, 取り締まる is ongoing control and enforcement, 暴く is the general \"to reveal secrets\"; when an insider reports wrongdoing, people say 内部告発.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"核兵器を使用する前に、説明書に書かれている操作方法を必ずお読みください。","romaji":"Kakuheiki o shiyō suru mae ni, setsumeisho ni kaka rete iru sōsa hōhō o kanarazu o yomi kudasai.","german":"Before using the nuclear weapon, be sure to read the operating instructions in the manual.","furigana":"かくへいき|しよう|まえ|せつめいしょ|か|そうさほうほう|かなら|よ"},{"japanese":"世界から核兵器をなくすことを目指している。","romaji":"Sekai kara kakuheiki o nakusu koto o mezashite iru.","german":"We aim to rid the world of nuclear weapons.","furigana":"せかい|かくへいき|めざ"},{"japanese":"現在、核兵器を保有している国は九か国あると言われている。","romaji":"Genzai, kakuheiki o hoyuu shite iru kuni wa kyuukakoku aru to iwarete iru.","german":"It is said that there are currently nine countries that possess nuclear weapons.","furigana":"げんざい|かくへいき|ほゆう|くに|きゅう|こく|い"}],"notes":"核兵器 are atomic or nuclear weapons; 核 means \"nucleus, core\". Typical combinations: 核兵器を保有する \"to possess\", 核兵器の廃絶 \"abolition\", 核兵器を持たない国. Japan upholds the 非核三原則 \"three non-nuclear principles\". 原爆 refers specifically to the atomic bomb (e.g. Hiroshima). Note: 核家族 means \"nuclear family\" and has nothing to do with weapons.","levelNote":"Compound: 核 (N1) + 兵器 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その国は国際社会の反対を無視して核実験を行った。","romaji":"Sono kuni wa kokusai shakai no hantai o mushi shite kakujikken o okonatta.","german":"The country conducted a nuclear test, ignoring the opposition of the international community.","furigana":"くに|こくさいしゃかい|はんたい|むし|かくじっけん|おこな"},{"japanese":"核実験の影響で、周辺の住民に健康被害が出た。","romaji":"Kakujikken no eikyou de, shuuhen no juumin ni kenkou higai ga deta.","german":"As a result of the nuclear test, residents of the surrounding area suffered health damage.","furigana":"かくじっけん|えいきょう|しゅうへん|じゅうみん|けんこうひがい|で"},{"japanese":"核実験の全面禁止を求める声が高まっている。","romaji":"Kakujikken no zenmen kinshi o motomeru koe ga takamatte iru.","german":"Calls for a total ban on nuclear tests are growing louder.","furigana":"かくじっけん|ぜんめんきんし|もと|こえ|たか"}],"notes":"核実験 is a test of nuclear weapons. People say 核実験を行う or 核実験を実施する; in the news you also read 地下核実験 \"underground nuclear test\" and 核実験の禁止. The word belongs to political and news language. The more general word is 実験 \"experiment\"; 核 as a prefix forms further terms such as 核兵器 and 核戦争.","levelNote":"Compound: 核 (N1) + 実験 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"縄文時代にもおしゃぶりみたいなものはあったのかな。","romaji":"Jōmon jidai ni mo oshaburi mitai na mono wa atta no ka na.","german":"I wonder if there were things like pacifiers in the Jōmon period, too.","furigana":"じょうもんじだい"},{"japanese":"縄文時代の人々は、狩りをしたり木の実を集めたりして暮らしていた。","romaji":"Jōmon jidai no hitobito wa, kari o shitari ki no mi o atsumetari shite kurashite ita.","german":"The people of the Jōmon period lived by hunting and gathering nuts.","furigana":"じょうもんじだい|ひとびと|か|き|み|あつ|く"},{"japanese":"博物館で、縄の模様がついた縄文土器を見た。","romaji":"Hakubutsukan de, nawa no moyō ga tsuita Jōmon doki o mita.","german":"At the museum, I saw Jōmon pottery decorated with cord patterns.","furigana":"はくぶつかん|なわ|もよう|じょうもんどき|み"}],"notes":"縄文 literally means \"cord pattern\" (縄 \"rope\" + 文 \"pattern\") - the pattern pressed into early pottery. Named after it are 縄文土器 \"Jōmon pottery\" and above all the prehistoric 縄文時代 (approx. 14,000-1,000 BC), when people lived by hunting and gathering. The word appears almost only in such compounds (縄文人, 縄文文化). The Jōmon period is followed by the 弥生時代 with wet-rice farming.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"車が移動献血車に衝突するのを見ました。","romaji":"Kuruma ga idō kenketsusha ni shōtotsu suru no o mimashita.","german":"I saw a car crash into a mobile blood donation bus.","furigana":"くるま|いどうけんけつしゃ|しょうとつ|み"},{"japanese":"駅前の献血ルームで献血をしてきた。","romaji":"Ekimae no kenketsu ruumu de kenketsu o shite kita.","german":"I went to donate blood at the blood donation center in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|けんけつ|けんけつ"},{"japanese":"体重が少ない人は献血できないことがある。","romaji":"Taijuu ga sukunai hito wa kenketsu dekinai koto ga aru.","german":"People with low body weight sometimes cannot donate blood.","furigana":"たいじゅう|すく|ひと|けんけつ"}],"notes":"献血 (献 \"to offer\" + 血 \"blood\") is blood donation; as an intransitive する-verb: 献血する, 献血に行く. Blood is donated at a 献血ルーム or in the mobile 献血車. To be distinguished from it are 輸血 \"blood transfusion\" (receiving blood) and 採血 \"drawing blood\" for tests. Formed similarly is 献金 \"monetary donation\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"皆様からこんなにたくさんの献金をいただきました。","romaji":"Minasama kara konnani takusan no kenkin o itadakimashita.","german":"We have received so many donations from all of you.","furigana":"みなさま|けんきん"},{"japanese":"その議員は企業から違法な献金を受け取っていた。","romaji":"Sono giin wa kigyou kara ihou na kenkin o uketotte ita.","german":"The lawmaker had accepted illegal donations from companies.","furigana":"ぎいん|きぎょう|いほう|けんきん|う|と"},{"japanese":"彼は毎月、教会に献金している。","romaji":"Kare wa maitsuki, kyoukai ni kenkin shite iru.","german":"He donates money to the church every month.","furigana":"かれ|まいつき|きょうかい|けんきん"}],"notes":"献金 is a monetary donation, above all to political parties or politicians (政治献金, 企業献金) as well as to churches or religious groups. As a する-verb: 〜に献金する. For general donations to charities, people usually say 寄付 in everyday speech; 募金 is the collecting of donations (e.g. on the street). Formed similarly is 献血 \"blood donation\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本で医者になりたいので、今は医療用語を勉強しています。","romaji":"Nippon de isha ni naritai node, ima wa iryō yōgo o benkyō shite imasu.","german":"I want to become a doctor in Japan, so I'm studying medical terminology now.","furigana":"にっぽん|いしゃ|いま|いりょうようご|べんきょう"},{"japanese":"日本では医療費の一部を保険が負担する。","romaji":"Nihon de wa iryouhi no ichibu o hoken ga futan suru.","german":"In Japan, insurance covers part of the medical expenses.","furigana":"にほん|いりょうひ|いちぶ|ほけん|ふたん"},{"japanese":"医療の進歩によって、平均寿命が延びた。","romaji":"Iryou no shinpo ni yotte, heikin jumyou ga nobita.","german":"Thanks to advances in medical care, average life expectancy has increased.","furigana":"いりょう|しんぽ|へいきんじゅみょう|の"}],"notes":"医療 denotes medical care as a field or system and often serves as a modifier in compounds: 医療費 \"medical expenses,\" 医療機関 \"medical institution,\" 医療保険, 医療ミス \"medical error,\" 医療従事者 \"medical personnel.\" The concrete treatment of a disease is 治療, medicine as a science 医学. You say 医療を受ける \"to receive medical care.\"","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本では、通常は長男が一家の跡を継ぎます。","romaji":"Nippon de wa, tsūjō wa chōnan ga ikka no ato o tsugimasu.","german":"In Japan, the eldest son usually succeeds to the family.","furigana":"にっぽん|つうじょう|ちょうなん|いっか|あと|つ"},{"japanese":"父の店を継ぐかどうか、まだ迷っている。","romaji":"Chichi no mise o tsugu ka dou ka, mada mayotte iru.","german":"I'm still undecided about whether to take over my father's shop.","furigana":"ちち|みせ|つ|まよ"},{"japanese":"その王子は二十歳で王位を継いだ。","romaji":"Sono ouji wa hatachi de oui o tsuida.","german":"The prince succeeded to the throne at the age of twenty.","furigana":"おうじ|はたち|おうい|つ"}],"notes":"継ぐ mainly means \"to succeed, to take over\": 家業を継ぐ \"to take over the family business\", 父の跡を継ぐ, 王位を継ぐ. For the legal inheriting of money or property, people say 相続する instead; traditions or traits are \"inherited\" with 受け継ぐ, and a task is taken over with 引き継ぐ. Less often, 継ぐ means \"to patch\" or \"to add on\". Not to be confused with 注ぐ \"to pour\", which is read the same way.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"学校で明治維新について勉強はしたんですが、名前以外は何にも覚えていません。","romaji":"Gakkou de Meiji ishin nitsuite benkyou wa shita n desu ga, namae igai wa nani ni mo oboete imasen.","german":"I studied the Meiji Restoration at school, but I don't remember anything except the name.","furigana":"がっこう|めいじいしん|べんきょう|なまえいがい|なに|おぼ"},{"japanese":"明治維新によって、日本は近代国家への道を歩み始めた。","romaji":"Meiji ishin ni yotte, Nihon wa kindai kokka e no michi o ayumihajimeta.","german":"With the Meiji Restoration, Japan began its path to a modern nation.","furigana":"めいじいしん|にほん|きんだいこっか|みち|あゆ|はじ"},{"japanese":"選挙で維新の候補者が当選した。","romaji":"Senkyo de Ishin no kouhosha ga tousen shita.","german":"In the election, the Ishin candidate won.","furigana":"せんきょ|いしん|こうほしゃ|とうせん"}],"notes":"維新 means \"renewal, fundamental reform\", but it almost always stands for the 明治維新, the Meiji Restoration of 1868, through which Japan modernized. Derived from this, politicians use the word as a slogan for reforms, and as an abbreviation 維新 refers to the party 日本維新の会. A similar word for revolution is 革命, which, however, emphasizes a violent overthrow.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼氏の部屋、埃がすっごい舞ってて最悪だったんだけど、綺麗な花びらが舞っているんだって思い込むように頑張ってみたさ。ま、無理だったけど。","romaji":"Kareshi no heya, hokori ga suggoi mattete saiaku datta n da kedo, kirei na hanabira ga matte iru n datte omoikomu you ni ganbatte mita sa. Ma, muri datta kedo.","german":"There was an extreme amount of dust whirling around in my friend's room and it was awful, so I tried to imagine they were beautiful petals. But that didn't work.","furigana":"かれし|へや|ほこり|ま|さいあく|きれい|はな|ま|おも|こ|がんば|むり"},{"japanese":"風に桜の花びらが舞っている。","romaji":"Kaze ni sakura no hanabira ga matte iru.","german":"Cherry blossom petals whirl in the wind.","furigana":"かぜ|さくら|はな|ま"},{"japanese":"神社のお祭りで、女の子たちが舞を舞った。","romaji":"Jinja no omatsuri de, onnanoko-tachi ga mai o matta.","german":"At the shrine festival, the girls performed a traditional dance.","furigana":"じんじゃ|まつ|おんな|こ|まい|ま"}],"notes":"舞う means, on the one hand, \"to dance\" in the sense of the traditional, circling Japanese dance (舞を舞う); for modern dances one says 踊る. More common is the meaning \"to whirl through the air, to flutter\": 花びらが舞う, 雪が舞う, ほこりが舞う, 鳥が空を舞う. The verb is intransitive. Many compounds are built on it: 舞い上がる \"to whirl up\", 舞い散る \"to flutter down\", 舞い込む \"to flutter in\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今超貧乏でさ〜。砂糖も切らしてるんだけど、それすら新しいの買えないんだわ。","romaji":"Ima chō binbō de sā. Satō mo kirashiteru n da kedo, sore sura atarashii no kaenai n da wa.","german":"I'm super broke right now. I've run out of sugar and can't even afford to buy more.","furigana":"いまちょうびんぼう|さとう|き|あたら|か"},{"japanese":"超大型の台風が近づいている。","romaji":"Chou-oogata no taifuu ga chikazuite iru.","german":"An extremely large typhoon is approaching.","furigana":"ちょうおおがた|たいふう|ちか"},{"japanese":"病院で超音波の検査を受けた。","romaji":"Byouin de chouonpa no kensa o uketa.","german":"I was examined with ultrasound at the hospital.","furigana":"びょういん|ちょうおんぱ|けんさ|う"}],"notes":"As a prefix before Sino-Japanese nouns, 超 means \"over-, ultra-, super-\": 超大型 \"extremely large\", 超高速, 超音波 \"ultrasound\", 超特急. Colloquially, especially among young people, it is used like a degree adverb before adjectives and verbs: 超おいしい, 超貧乏, often also in katakana チョー - this replaces とても or すごく and is out of place in formal situations. In news reports it also follows numbers: 1万人超 \"over 10,000 people\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"そういえば、私の超自然的な力についてお話したことってありましたっけ？","romaji":"Sō ie ba, watashi no chō shizenteki na chikara nitsuite ohanashi shita koto tte arimashita kke?","german":"By the way, have I ever told you about my supernatural power?","furigana":"わたし|ちょうしぜんてき|ちから|はなし"},{"japanese":"科学では説明できない超自然的な現象を信じますか。","romaji":"Kagaku de wa setsumei dekinai choushizenteki na genshou o shinjimasu ka.","german":"Do you believe in supernatural phenomena that science cannot explain?","furigana":"かがく|せつめい|ちょうしぜんてき|げんしょう|しん"},{"japanese":"この映画は、超自然的な存在と戦う少年の物語だ。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa, choushizenteki na sonzai to tatakau shounen no monogatari da.","german":"This movie is the story of a boy who fights supernatural beings.","furigana":"えいが|ちょうしぜんてき|そんざい|たたか|しょうねん|ものがたり"}],"notes":"超自然的 (超 \"super-\" + 自然 \"nature\" + 的) is a な-adjective: 超自然的な力, 超自然的な現象, 超自然的な存在. It sounds rather matter-of-fact to literary and appears in discussions of religion, the occult or fantasy. In everyday speech people often just say 不思議な; for paranormal phenomena there is also 超常現象.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"僕のパンダロボットは、超音速で空を飛べるんだぜ。","romaji":"Boku no panda robotto wa, chō onsoku de sora o toberu n da ze.","german":"My panda robot can fly through the sky at supersonic speed, you know.","furigana":"ぼく|ちょうおんそく|そら|と"},{"japanese":"コンコルドは超音速で大西洋を横断した旅客機だ。","romaji":"Konkorudo wa chouonsoku de taiseiyou o oudan shita ryokakuki da.","german":"The Concorde was a passenger aircraft that crossed the Atlantic at supersonic speed.","furigana":"ちょうおんそく|たいせいよう|おうだん|りょかくき"},{"japanese":"戦闘機が超音速で飛ぶと、大きな音が地上まで届く。","romaji":"Sentouki ga chouonsoku de tobu to, ookina oto ga chijou made todoku.","german":"When a fighter jet flies at supersonic speed, a loud noise reaches the ground.","furigana":"せんとうき|ちょうおんそく|と|おお|おと|ちじょう|とど"}],"notes":"超音速 is composed of 超 \"super-\" and 音速 \"speed of sound\". Typical is 超音速で飛ぶ as well as compounds such as 超音速機 or 超音速旅客機 (e.g. the Concorde). Speed is given in マッハ. The counterpart \"subsonic\" is 亜音速. The word is technical, but also appears in the news and in science fiction.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お前がそこまで馬鹿じゃなければ、お前がどれだけ馬鹿なのか説明することができるんだけどなあ。","romaji":"Omae ga soko made baka ja nakere ba, omae ga dore dake baka na no ka setsumei suru koto ga dekiru n da kedo nā.","german":"If you weren't that stupid, I could explain to you just how stupid you are.","furigana":"まえ|ばか|まえ|ばか|せつめい"},{"japanese":"人を馬鹿にするのはやめなさい。","romaji":"Hito o baka ni suru no wa yamenasai.","german":"Stop making fun of people.","furigana":"ひと|ばか"},{"japanese":"隣のおじさんは親馬鹿で、会うたびに娘の写真を見せてくる。","romaji":"Tonari no ojisan wa oyabaka de, au tabi ni musume no shashin o misete kuru.","german":"The man next door is a doting father; every time we meet, he shows me pictures of his daughter.","furigana":"となり|おやばか|あ|むすめ|しゃしん|み"}],"notes":"馬鹿 is mostly written in kana (ばか, バカ) and is a noun and a な-adjective: \"idiot\" or \"stupid, silly\" (馬鹿なこと, 馬鹿みたい). How insulting it is depends on tone and region; in Kansai, ばか is often felt to be harsher than アホ. Important phrases: 〜を馬鹿にする \"to make fun of someone,\" 馬鹿馬鹿しい \"ridiculous.\" As a suffix it denotes someone who is crazy about something, e.g. 親馬鹿 \"doting parents.\"","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鹿児島県で、今までで運転した中で一番デッコボコの道路をドライブしました。","romaji":"Kagoshimaken de, ima made de unten shita naka de ichiban dekkoboko no dōro o doraibu shimashita.","german":"In Kagoshima Prefecture, I drove on the bumpiest road I have ever driven on.","furigana":"かごしまけん|いま|うんてん|なか|いちばん|どうろ"},{"japanese":"鹿児島県の桜島は、今も噴火を続けている活火山だ。","romaji":"Kagoshima-ken no Sakurajima wa, ima mo funka o tsuzukete iru kakkazan da.","german":"Sakurajima in Kagoshima Prefecture is an active volcano that is still erupting today.","furigana":"かごしまけん|さくらじま|いま|ふんか|つづ|かっかざん"},{"japanese":"屋久島は鹿児島県にある島です。","romaji":"Yakushima wa Kagoshima-ken ni aru shima desu.","german":"Yakushima is an island in Kagoshima Prefecture.","furigana":"やくしま|かごしまけん|しま"}],"notes":"鹿児島県 is the southernmost prefecture of the island of Kyūshū; its capital is 鹿児島市. Its landmark is the active volcano 桜島 directly across from the city. The prefecture also includes islands such as 屋久島 and 奄美大島. It is known for sweet potato shōchū (芋焼酎) and black pork (黒豚). In conversation people often say just 鹿児島.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"事後承諾になってしまってすみません。","romaji":"Jigo shōdaku ni natte shimatte sumimasen.","german":"Sorry for asking for your approval only after the fact.","furigana":"じごしょうだく"},{"japanese":"未成年の場合は、親の承諾が必要です。","romaji":"Miseinen no baai wa, oya no shoudaku ga hitsuyou desu.","german":"For minors, the consent of the parents is required.","furigana":"みせいねん|ばあい|おや|しょうだく|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"彼女は彼のプロポーズを承諾した。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kare no puropoozu o shoudaku shita.","german":"She accepted his marriage proposal.","furigana":"かのじょ|かれ|しょうだく"}],"notes":"承諾 is formal consent to a request or proposal: 〜の承諾を得る \"to obtain consent\", 〜を承諾する \"to consent\", 親の承諾, 承諾書 \"consent form\". 事後承諾 means asking for consent only afterwards. Similar: 了承 \"to take note of and accept\" (ご了承ください), 承知 \"understood, agreed\" (承知しました) and 同意 \"agreement with an opinion\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コウイチはアメリカ合衆国の大統領になることを渋々受諾した。","romaji":"Kouichi wa Amerikagasshuukoku no daitouryou ni naru koto o shibushibu judaku shita.","german":"Kōichi reluctantly accepted becoming president of the United States.","furigana":"がっしゅうこく|だいとうりょう|しぶしぶじゅだく"},{"japanese":"日本は1945年にポツダム宣言を受諾した。","romaji":"Nihon wa sen kyuuhyaku yonjuugo nen ni Potsudamu sengen o judaku shita.","german":"In 1945, Japan accepted the Potsdam Declaration.","furigana":"にほん|ねん|せんげん|じゅだく"},{"japanese":"彼はA社からの仕事の申し出を受諾した。","romaji":"Kare wa A-sha kara no shigoto no moushide o judaku shita.","german":"He accepted the job offer from Company A.","furigana":"かれ|しゃ|しごと|もう|で|じゅだく"}],"notes":"受諾 is the formal acceptance of an offer, a demand or an appointment: 申し出を受諾する, 就任を受諾する. Historically well known is ポツダム宣言の受諾 (1945). The word is literary and appears in diplomacy, politics and business. In everyday speech people say 受け入れる or 引き受ける; 承諾 emphasizes more the consenting to a request.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"予め管理人の許諾を得てください。","romaji":"Arakajime kanrijin no kyodaku o ete kudasai.","german":"Please obtain the administrator's permission beforehand.","furigana":"あらかじ|かんりじん|きょだく|え"},{"japanese":"この写真を使うには、著作権者の許諾が必要です。","romaji":"Kono shashin o tsukau ni wa, chosakukensha no kyodaku ga hitsuyou desu.","german":"To use this photo, the copyright holder's permission is required.","furigana":"しゃしん|つか|ちょさくけんしゃ|きょだく|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"ソフトをインストールする前に、使用許諾契約に同意してください。","romaji":"Sofuto o insutooru suru mae ni, shiyou kyodaku keiyaku ni doui shite kudasai.","german":"Please agree to the license agreement before installing the software.","furigana":"まえ|しようきょだくけいやく|どうい"}],"notes":"許諾 is permission or authorization, above all in the legal sense for copyright and usage rights: 著作権者の許諾を得る, 無断で使わず許諾を取る, 使用許諾契約 \"license agreement\". General official permits are called 許可; 承諾 is consent to a request. The word is formal and is found mainly in contracts and business texts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「諾否を御一報ください」という言葉は、仕事の手紙やEmailなどでよく使われるフレーズです。","romaji":"Dakuhi o go ippō kudasai toiu kotoba wa, shigoto no tegami ya Email nado de yoku tsukawa reru furēzu desu.","german":"\"Please let us know whether this is acceptable or not\" is a phrase often used in business letters and e-mails.","furigana":"だくひ|ごいっぽう|ことば|しごと|てがみ|つか"},{"japanese":"ご依頼の件について、今月末までに諾否をご回答ください。","romaji":"Goirai no ken ni tsuite, kongetsu matsu made ni dakuhi o gokaitou kudasai.","german":"Regarding your request, please let us know your answer, whether you accept or decline, by the end of this month.","furigana":"いらい|けん|こんげつまつ|だくひ|かいとう"},{"japanese":"契約更新の諾否は、書面で通知いたします。","romaji":"Keiyaku koushin no dakuhi wa, shomen de tsuuchi itashimasu.","german":"Whether the contract will be renewed will be notified to you in writing.","furigana":"けいやくこうしん|だくひ|しょめん|つうち"}],"notes":"諾否 (諾 \"yes\" + 否 \"no\") means \"acceptance or refusal\" and is a very formal written word, typical in business letters: 諾否をお知らせください, 諾否をご回答ください. In conversation, people simply ask whether someone accepts or not. Formed similarly are 可否 \"whether possible or approved\", 賛否 \"pros and cons\" and 出欠 \"attendance or absence\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"印鑑を高級な象牙の物に変えました。","romaji":"Inkan o kōkyū na zōge no mono ni kaemashita.","german":"Ich habe meinen Namensstempel gegen einen teuren aus Elfenbein ausgetauscht.","furigana":"いんかん|こうきゅう|ぞうげ|もの|か"},{"japanese":"象牙の国際的な取引は、条約で禁止されている。","romaji":"Zouge no kokusaiteki na torihiki wa, jouyaku de kinshi sarete iru.","german":"Der internationale Handel mit Elfenbein ist durch ein Abkommen verboten.","furigana":"ぞうげ|こくさいてき|とりひき|じょうやく|きんし"},{"japanese":"大学の研究者は「象牙の塔」にこもっていると批判されることがある。","romaji":"Daigaku no kenkyuusha wa \"zouge no tou\" ni komotte iru to hihan sareru koto ga aru.","german":"Universitätsforschern wird manchmal vorgeworfen, sich in ihrem Elfenbeinturm zu verschanzen.","furigana":"だいがく|けんきゅうしゃ|ぞうげ|とう|ひはん"}],"notes":"象牙 (象 „Elefant“ + 牙 „Stoßzahn“) ist Elfenbein. Früher war es in Japan ein beliebtes Material für Namensstempel (印鑑) und Musikinstrumente; heute ist der internationale Handel durch das Washingtoner Artenschutzabkommen weitgehend verboten (象牙の取引). 象牙色 ist die Farbe „Elfenbein“, und 象牙の塔 steht wie im Deutschen für den „Elfenbeinturm“ der Wissenschaft.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"降参しようと思ったことは今まで一度もないんですか。","romaji":"Kōsan shiyō to omotta koto wa ima made ichido mo nai n desu ka.","german":"Haven't you ever once thought of giving up?","furigana":"こうさん|おも|いま|いちど"},{"japanese":"このなぞなぞ、全然分からない。降参するから答えを教えて。","romaji":"Kono nazonazo, zenzen wakaranai. Kousan suru kara kotae o oshiete.","german":"I have no idea about this riddle at all. I give up, so tell me the answer.","furigana":"ぜんぜんわ|こうさん|こた|おし"},{"japanese":"敵の兵士たちは白い旗を上げて降参した。","romaji":"Teki no heishitachi wa shiroi hata o agete kousan shita.","german":"The enemy soldiers raised a white flag and surrendered.","furigana":"てき|へいし|しろ|はた|あ|こうさん"}],"notes":"降参する means \"to surrender, to capitulate\" and is intransitive: 敵に降参する. In everyday life it is often used jokingly for \"I give up!\", for example with riddles, games or arguments (もう降参！). The formal, military word for capitulation is 降伏; 諦める, by contrast, means \"to give up a plan\". Similarly colloquial is 参った \"I'm beaten\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一つ大きいサイズの靴に換えてもらえませんか。","romaji":"Hitotsu ōkii saizu no kutsu ni kaete moraemasen ka.","german":"Could you exchange these for shoes one size larger?","furigana":"ひと|おお|くつ|か"},{"japanese":"空港で円をユーロに換えました。","romaji":"Kuukou de en o yuuro ni kaemashita.","german":"At the airport I changed yen into euros.","furigana":"くうこう|えん|か"},{"japanese":"窓を開けて、部屋の空気を換えましょう。","romaji":"Mado o akete, heya no kuuki o kaemashou.","german":"Let's open the window and air out the room.","furigana":"まど|あ|へや|くうき|か"}],"notes":"換える means \"to exchange for something of equal value, to convert\": X を Y に換える, e.g. 円をドルに換える, 空気を換える \"to ventilate\". There are also identically read spellings: 変える \"to change\" and 替える \"to replace with something new\" (電池を替える). 換える and 替える overlap considerably; 換える emphasizes the exchange. The kanji is also in 交換 \"exchange\" and 乗り換える \"to transfer\". The intransitive partner is 換わる.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 23"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この病院の病床数はいくつですか？","romaji":"Kono byōin no byōshōsū wa ikutsu desu ka?","german":"How many beds does this hospital have?","furigana":"びょういん|びょうしょうすう"},{"japanese":"感染者が増え、市内の病院の病床が足りなくなった。","romaji":"Kansensha ga fue, shinai no byouin no byoushou ga tarinaku natta.","german":"As the number of infected people rose, hospital beds in the city ran short.","furigana":"かんせんしゃ|ふ|しない|びょういん|びょうしょう|た"},{"japanese":"祖父は病床から家族に手紙を書いた。","romaji":"Sofu wa byoushou kara kazoku ni tegami o kaita.","german":"My grandfather wrote a letter to his family from his sickbed.","furigana":"そふ|びょうしょう|かぞく|てがみ|か"}],"notes":"病床 is the \"sickbed\" and is used in two contexts: officially for the bed capacity of hospitals (病床数, 病床が足りない, 病床がひっ迫する) and in literary use for the time of illness (病床にある \"to be bedridden\", 病床で書いた手紙). In everyday speech, people simply say ベッド for the bed itself.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"叔父はこの学校の校長です。","romaji":"Oji wa kono gakkō no kōchō desu.","german":"My uncle is the principal of this school.","furigana":"おじ|がっこう|こうちょう"},{"japanese":"夏休みは田舎の叔父の家に泊まりました。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi wa inaka no oji no ie ni tomarimashita.","german":"During summer vacation I stayed at my uncle's house in the countryside.","furigana":"なつやす|いなか|おじ|いえ|と"},{"japanese":"叔父は父の三つ下の弟です。","romaji":"Oji wa chichi no mittsu shita no otouto desu.","german":"My uncle is my father's brother, three years younger.","furigana":"おじ|ちち|みっ|した|おとうと"}],"notes":"叔父 is the younger brother of a parent (or the husband of a younger aunt); the older brother is written 伯父, and both are read おじ. With 叔父 you talk about your own uncle; other people's uncles are called おじさん or おじさま, and when addressed directly he is likewise called おじさん. Note: おじさん also means \"middle-aged man\" in general.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"叔母は学校の先生をしています。","romaji":"Oba wa gakkō no sensei o shite imasu.","german":"My aunt is a school teacher.","furigana":"おば|がっこう|せんせい"},{"japanese":"叔母は母の妹で、料理がとても上手だ。","romaji":"Oba wa haha no imouto de, ryouri ga totemo jouzu da.","german":"My aunt is my mother's younger sister and is a very good cook.","furigana":"おば|はは|いもうと|りょうり|じょうず"},{"japanese":"大学に入るまで、叔母の家から学校に通っていた。","romaji":"Daigaku ni hairu made, oba no ie kara gakkou ni kayotte ita.","german":"Until I entered university, I went to school from my aunt's house.","furigana":"だいがく|はい|おば|いえ|がっこう|かよ"}],"notes":"叔母 is the younger sister of a parent (or the wife of a younger uncle); the older sister is written 伯母, and both are read おば. 叔母 is used for your own aunt; other people's aunts are called おばさん or おばさま, and she is addressed directly as おばさん. Not to be confused with おばあさん \"grandmother, old woman\" (long あ).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"へー、この辺って水が湧くんだね。","romaji":"Hē, kono atari tte mizu ga waku n da ne.","german":"Oh, so water springs up around here.","furigana":"|みず|わ"},{"japanese":"話を聞いているうちに、その仕事に興味が湧いてきた。","romaji":"Hanashi o kiite iru uchi ni, sono shigoto ni kyoumi ga waite kita.","german":"As I listened to the story, I became interested in that job.","furigana":"はなし|き|しごと|きょうみ|わ"},{"japanese":"合格したと聞いても、まだ実感が湧かない。","romaji":"Goukaku shita to kiite mo, mada jikkan ga wakanai.","german":"Even though I heard that I passed, it still doesn't feel real.","furigana":"ごうかく|き|じっかん|わ"}],"notes":"湧く is intransitive and first means \"to well up, to gush\" (水が湧く, 温泉が湧く). Very common is the figurative meaning \"to arise\" for feelings and ideas: 興味が湧く, 勇気が湧く, アイデアが湧く, 実感が湧かない \"cannot yet take it in\". Vermin can also \"appear\" (虫が湧く). Not to be confused with 沸く \"to boil\" (お湯が沸く) or \"to get excited\" (会場が沸く), which is read the same way.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一昨日、中学の同窓会に行ってきました。","romaji":"Ototoi, chūgaku no dōsōkai ni itte kimashita.","german":"The day before yesterday I went to my junior high school class reunion.","furigana":"おととい|ちゅうがく|どうそうかい|い"},{"japanese":"卒業して十年ぶりに同窓会が開かれた。","romaji":"Sotsugyou shite juunen buri ni dousoukai ga hirakareta.","german":"A class reunion was held for the first time in ten years since graduation.","furigana":"そつぎょう|じゅうねん|どうそうかい|ひら"},{"japanese":"大学の同窓会から寄付のお願いの手紙が届いた。","romaji":"Daigaku no dousoukai kara kifu no onegai no tegami ga todoita.","german":"A letter asking for a donation arrived from my university's alumni association.","furigana":"だいがく|どうそうかい|きふ|ねが|てがみ|とど"}],"notes":"同窓会 refers both to the alumni association of a school or university and to the gathering of alumni, that is, the class or year reunion. 同窓 literally means \"same window\" and means \"having studied at the same school\". Typical: 同窓会を開く, 同窓会に出る, 中学の同窓会. A reunion of just one class is also called クラス会.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"レントゲン写真には、異常は認められませんでした。","romaji":"Rentogen shashin ni wa, ijou wa mitomeraremasen deshita.","german":"The X-ray images showed nothing unusual.","furigana":"しゃしん|いじょう|みと"},{"japanese":"念のため、胸のレントゲンを撮りましょう。","romaji":"Nen no tame, mune no rentogen o torimashou.","german":"To be safe, we will take an X-ray of your chest.","furigana":"ねん|むね|と"},{"japanese":"転んだ後、手首が痛かったので病院でレントゲンを撮ってもらった。","romaji":"Koronda ato, tekubi ga itakatta node byouin de rentogen o totte moratta.","german":"My wrist hurt after the fall, so I had it X-rayed at the hospital.","furigana":"ころ|あと|てくび|いた|びょういん|と"}],"notes":"レントゲン comes from the name of the physicist Röntgen and in daily life refers to the X-ray examination or image: レントゲンを撮る \"to have an X-ray taken\", レントゲン写真, 胸部レントゲン. In technical texts one says X線 (エックスせん). Less often it is the physical unit roentgen.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"かかりつけの歯科医はいますか？","romaji":"Kakaritsuke no shikai wa imasu ka?","german":"Do you have a regular dentist?","furigana":"しかい"},{"japanese":"会社の歯科検診で虫歯が見つかった。","romaji":"Kaisha no shika kenshin de mushiba ga mitsukatta.","german":"At the company dental checkup, a cavity was found in my teeth.","furigana":"かいしゃ|しかけんしん|むしば|み"},{"japanese":"姉は歯科衛生士として働いている。","romaji":"Ane wa shika eiseishi to shite hataraite iru.","german":"My older sister works as a dental hygienist.","furigana":"あね|しかえいせいし|はたら"}],"notes":"歯科 is dentistry as a field or department and often appears in compounds: 歯科医 \"dentist\", 歯科医院 \"dental clinic\", 歯科検診 \"dental checkup\", 歯科衛生士 \"dental hygienist\", 小児歯科 \"pediatric dentistry\". In conversation one usually says 歯医者 or 歯医者さん instead of 歯科医. Note: it is read the same as the particle しか \"only\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"おい、貴様はだまっていろ！","romaji":"Oi, kisama wa damatte iro!","german":"Hey, you shut up!","furigana":"きさま"},{"japanese":"「貴様、何者だ！」と兵士が叫んだ。","romaji":"\"Kisama, nanimono da!\" to heishi ga sakenda.","german":"\"Who are you, you bastard?!\" the soldier shouted.","furigana":"きさま|なにもの|へいし|さけ"},{"japanese":"漫画では、悪役がよく「貴様」という言葉を使う。","romaji":"Manga de wa, akuyaku ga yoku \"kisama\" to iu kotoba o tsukau.","german":"In manga, villains often use the word 「貴様」.","furigana":"まんが|あくやく|きさま|ことば|つか"}],"notes":"貴様 originally consisted of 貴 \"noble\" and 様 and used to be a polite form of address. Today it is an extremely rude, aggressive form of \"you\" that you hear almost only in anime, manga, war movies or in heated arguments among men. In real life you should not use it. Less extreme, but still rude, are お前 and てめえ; neutrally, people address others by name.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"俺の兄貴は恥知らずの嘘つきだ。何のことだかさっぱり分からないなんて言ってたくせに、俺のベーコンを平らげたのはあいつだったんだよ。","romaji":"Ore no aniki wa hajishirazu no usotsuki da. Nan no koto da ka sappari wakaranai nante itteta kuse ni, ore no beekon o tairageta no wa aitsu datta n da yo.","german":"My brother is a shameless liar. He said he had no idea what I was talking about, but he was the one who ate all my bacon.","furigana":"おれ|あにき|はじし|うそ|なん|わ|い|おれ|たい"},{"japanese":"人の手柄を自分のものにするなんて、恥知らずにもほどがある。","romaji":"Hito no tegara o jibun no mono ni suru nante, hajishirazu ni mo hodo ga aru.","german":"Taking credit for someone else's work - that is really the height of shamelessness.","furigana":"ひと|てがら|じぶん|はじし"},{"japanese":"被害者をばかにするような恥知らずな発言に、多くの人が怒った。","romaji":"Higaisha o baka ni suru you na hajishirazu na hatsugen ni, ooku no hito ga okotta.","german":"Many people were angry at the shameless remark that ridiculed the victims.","furigana":"ひがいしゃ|はじし|はつげん|おお|ひと|おこ"}],"notes":"恥知らず (恥 \"shame\" + 知らず \"not knowing\") means \"shameless\" or \"shameless person\". It is a noun and a な-adjective: 恥知らずな行為, 恥知らずな発言, also 恥知らずの嘘つき; as an insult: この恥知らず！ The expression 恥知らずにもほどがある means \"that is the height of shamelessness\". Somewhat milder are 図々しい \"brazen\" and 厚かましい \"impudent\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"試験地獄に苦しむ日本の学生にとっては、電車で勉強するのは当たり前のことです。","romaji":"Shiken jigoku ni kurushimu Nihon no gakusei ni totte wa, densha de benkyou suru no wa atarimae no koto desu.","german":"For Japanese students suffering under exam hell, studying on the train is perfectly normal.","furigana":"しけんじごく|くる|にほん|がくせい|でんしゃ|べんきょう|あ|まえ"},{"japanese":"三月までは試験地獄が続くので、旅行どころではありません。","romaji":"Sangatsu made wa shiken jigoku ga tsuzuku node, ryokou dokoro de wa arimasen.","german":"The exam hell continues until March - travel is out of the question.","furigana":"さんがつ|しけんじごく|つづ|りょこう"},{"japanese":"やっと試験地獄から抜け出したので、春休みはのんびり過ごすつもりだ。","romaji":"Yatto shiken jigoku kara nukedashita node, haruyasumi wa nonbiri sugosu tsumori da.","german":"I finally escaped the exam hell, and I want to spend the spring break in a totally relaxed way.","furigana":"しけんじごく|ぬ|だ|はるやす|す"}],"notes":"試験地獄 refers to the enormous pressure surrounding entrance exams (入試) for high school and university in Japan, often in connection with cram schools (塾) and studying late into the night. Typical combinations are 試験地獄に苦しむ, 試験地獄を経験する or 試験地獄から抜け出す. The word comes from the press and social criticism; related is 受験戦争 \"exam war\"; students also use it jokingly for their own exam period.","levelNote":"Compound: 試験 (N5) + 地獄 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この薬を飲んだら、船酔いしませんでした。","romaji":"Kono kusuri o nondara, funayoi shimasen deshita.","german":"When I took this medicine, I didn't get seasick.","furigana":"くすり|の|ふなよ"},{"japanese":"フェリーに乗ったら、ひどく船酔いしてしまった。","romaji":"Ferī ni nottara, hidoku funayoi shite shimatta.","german":"I got terribly seasick on the ferry.","furigana":"の|ふなよ"},{"japanese":"船酔いしやすい人は、船の真ん中あたりに座るといいですよ。","romaji":"Funayoi shiyasui hito wa, fune no mannaka atari ni suwaru to ii desu yo.","german":"People who get seasick easily should sit around the middle of the ship.","furigana":"ふなよ|ひと|ふね|ま|なか|すわ"}],"notes":"船酔い means \"seasickness\"; as an intransitive する-verb, 船酔いする means \"to get seasick\". Formed in parallel are 車酔い \"carsickness\" and the umbrella term 乗り物酔い \"motion sickness\"; pills against it are called 酔い止め. 酔う alone usually means \"to get drunk\", but 船に酔う also means \"to get seasick\". Typical are 船酔いしやすい and ひどく船酔いする.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女が彼氏にキムチ鍋を作らせた。","romaji":"Kanojo ga kareshi ni kimuchi nabe o tsukuraseta.","german":"The girlfriend made her boyfriend cook kimchi hot pot.","furigana":"かのじょ|かれし|なべ|つく"},{"japanese":"寒い日は、家族みんなで寄せ鍋を囲みます。","romaji":"Samui hi wa, kazoku minna de yosenabe o kakomimasu.","german":"On cold days, the whole family gathers around a yosenabe hot pot.","furigana":"さむ|ひ|かぞく|よ|なべ|かこ"},{"japanese":"相撲部屋の近くで、本場のちゃんこ鍋を食べてみたい。","romaji":"Sumou beya no chikaku de, honba no chanko nabe o tabete mitai.","german":"I'd like to try authentic chanko nabe near a sumo stable.","furigana":"すもうべや|ちか|ほんば|なべ|た"}],"notes":"〜鍋 is attached to a noun and denotes a stew-type dish cooked at the table in a shared pot: キムチ鍋, 寄せ鍋, もつ鍋, ちゃんこ鍋, 豆乳鍋. Unlike in many compounds, the reading usually stays なべ (寄せ鍋 = よせなべ). On its own, 鍋 stands for \"pot, pan\" or, for short, the dish: 今夜は鍋にしよう. Typical is 鍋を囲む \"to sit around the pot together,\" especially in winter.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"現金払いにしていただけるなら、5パーセント引きにさせていただきます。","romaji":"Genkin barai ni shite itadakeru nara, go paasento biki ni sasete itadakimasu.","german":"If you pay in cash, we will give you a 5 percent discount.","furigana":"げんきんばら|び"},{"japanese":"このパソコンは分割払いで買うこともできます。","romaji":"Kono pasokon wa bunkatsu barai de kau koto mo dekimasu.","german":"You can also buy this computer in installments.","furigana":"ぶんかつばら|か"},{"japanese":"家賃の払いがもう二か月も遅れている。","romaji":"Yachin no harai ga mou nikagetsu mo okurete iru.","german":"The rent is already two months overdue.","furigana":"やちん|はら|に|げつ|おく"}],"notes":"払い is the noun form of 払う. On its own, people usually say 支払い for \"payment\"; 払い appears mainly as a suffix, in which case it takes a voiced initial, 〜ばらい: 現金払い, カード払い, 分割払い, 一括払い, 前払い, 後払い. On its own it occurs in fixed expressions, for example 払いがいい \"pays well, generous\" or 家賃の払いが遅れる. Further, rarer meanings are \"sweeping away\" (厄払い) and the trailing brush stroke when writing kanji.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この油絵はフリマで千五百円で買ったんですよ。","romaji":"Kono aburae wa furima de sen gohyaku en de katta n desu yo.","german":"I bought this oil painting at a flea market for 1,500 yen.","furigana":"あぶらえ|せんごひゃくえん|か"},{"japanese":"父は退職してから油絵を習い始めました。","romaji":"Chichi wa taishoku shite kara aburae o naraihajimemashita.","german":"After retiring, my father started learning oil painting.","furigana":"ちち|たいしょく|あぶらえ|なら|はじ"},{"japanese":"美術館で、有名な画家の油絵をじっくり見てきた。","romaji":"Bijutsukan de, yuumei na gaka no aburae o jikkuri mite kita.","german":"I took my time looking at oil paintings by a famous painter at the museum.","furigana":"びじゅつかん|ゆうめい|がか|あぶらえ|み"}],"notes":"油絵 is an oil painting or oil painting as a technique. One says 油絵を描く (かく or えがく) and 油絵を習う; the paints are called 油絵の具. Contrasting terms are 水彩画 \"watercolor\" and 日本画 \"Japanese-style painting\". In catalogs and museums the more formal 油彩 or 油彩画 is often used.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この間、給食の時に生徒が何故か机の中から甘酢の瓶を取り出したのよ。","romaji":"Kono aida, kyuushoku no toki ni seito ga naze ka tsukue no naka kara amazu no bin o toridashita no yo.","german":"The other day at school lunch, a student for some reason pulled a bottle of sweet vinegar out of his desk.","furigana":"あいだ|きゅうしょく|とき|せいと|なぜ|つくえ|なか|あまず|びん|と|だ"},{"japanese":"鶏の唐揚げに甘酢をかけると、子どもたちがよく食べます。","romaji":"Tori no karaage ni amazu o kakeru to, kodomotachi ga yoku tabemasu.","german":"When I put sweet-and-sour sauce on fried chicken, the kids eat really well.","furigana":"|からあ|あまず|こ|た"},{"japanese":"大根を甘酢に漬けておくと、さっぱりしたおかずになります。","romaji":"Daikon o amazu ni tsukete oku to, sappari shita okazu ni narimasu.","german":"If you pickle daikon radish in sweet vinegar, it becomes a refreshing side dish.","furigana":"だいこん|あまず|つ"}],"notes":"甘酢 is vinegar sweetened with sugar, the base of many sweet-and-sour dishes. Typical are 甘酢漬け (vegetables pickled in sweet vinegar, e.g. ginger served with sushi) and 甘酢あんかけ (topped with a thickened sweet-and-sour sauce). Note the voiced initial: 酢 = す, but 甘酢 = あまず. Related seasoning mixtures are 三杯酢 and 合わせ酢.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ズボンのお尻の部分がいつも最初に破れるんだけど、どうしてだろう。","romaji":"Zubon no oshiri no bubun ga itsumo saisho ni yabureru n da kedo, doushite darou.","german":"The seat of my pants always rips first - I wonder why.","furigana":"しり|ぶぶん|さいしょ|やぶ"},{"japanese":"紙袋が破れて、りんごが道に転がった。","romaji":"Kamibukuro ga yaburete, ringo ga michi ni korogatta.","german":"The paper bag tore and the apples rolled onto the road.","furigana":"かみぶくろ|やぶ|みち|ころ"},{"japanese":"長年の夢が破れて、彼はしばらく何もする気になれなかった。","romaji":"Naganen no yume ga yaburete, kare wa shibaraku nani mo suru ki ni narenakatta.","german":"After his long-held dream was shattered, he didn't feel like doing anything for a while.","furigana":"ながねん|ゆめ|やぶ|かれ|なに|き"}],"notes":"破れる is intransitive: cloth, paper or nets tear or get a hole (袋が破れる, ズボンが破れる); the transitive counterpart is 破る \"to tear\". Figuratively, it means that plans, dreams or negotiations fail (夢が破れる, 交渉が破れる). Not to be confused with 割れる (glass, plates break), 壊れる (a device breaks down) and 敗れる \"to lose (in a competition)\", which is read the same way.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お前にどの娘を紹介するかを選ぶのは難しいよ。","romaji":"Omae ni dono musume o shōkai suru ka o erabu no wa muzukashii yo.","german":"Es ist schwer zu entscheiden, welches Mädchen ich dir vorstellen soll.","furigana":"まえ|むすめ|しょうかい|えら|むずか"},{"japanese":"では、簡単に自己紹介させていただきます。","romaji":"Dewa, kantan ni jiko shoukai sasete itadakimasu.","german":"Dann darf ich mich kurz vorstellen.","furigana":"かんたん|じこしょうかい"},{"japanese":"友達が駅の近くのおいしいラーメン屋を紹介してくれた。","romaji":"Tomodachi ga eki no chikaku no oishii raamen'ya o shoukai shite kureta.","german":"Ein Freund hat mir einen leckeren Ramen-Laden in Bahnhofsnähe empfohlen.","furigana":"ともだち|えき|ちか|や|しょうかい"}],"notes":"紹介する heißt „jemanden vorstellen, bekannt machen“ mit dem Muster AにBを紹介する; sich selbst vorstellen ist 自己紹介する. Es wird auch für das Empfehlen oder Präsentieren von Orten, Produkten und Kultur verwendet (いい店を紹介する, テレビで紹介される). Achtung: Das deutsche „sich etwas vorstellen“ im Sinne von „ausmalen“ ist nicht 紹介 sondern 想像する. Förmliches Empfehlen einer Person für eine Stelle heißt eher 推薦する.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 11 (source: N5; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この病院で予約をするには、お医者さんからの紹介状が要ります。","romaji":"Kono byouin de yoyaku o suru ni wa, oishasan kara no shoukaijou ga irimasu.","german":"You need a referral letter from a doctor to make an appointment at this hospital.","furigana":"びょういん|よやく|いしゃ|しょうかいじょう|い"},{"japanese":"先生、留学先の教授への紹介状を書いていただけませんか。","romaji":"Sensei, ryuugakusaki no kyouju e no shoukaijou o kaite itadakemasen ka.","german":"Professor, could you write me a letter of introduction to the professor at my host university?","furigana":"せんせい|りゅうがくさき|きょうじゅ|しょうかいじょう|か"},{"japanese":"社長の紹介状を持って、新しい取引先を訪ねた。","romaji":"Shachou no shoukaijou o motte, atarashii torihikisaki o tazuneta.","german":"I visited a new business partner with a letter of introduction from the company president.","furigana":"しゃちょう|しょうかいじょう|も|あたら|とりひきさき|たず"}],"notes":"紹介状 is a letter with which you introduce or recommend someone to a third person. In everyday life it is encountered above all as a medical referral: large hospitals require a 紹介状 from the family doctor (病院への紹介状). Typical are 紹介状を書く/書いてもらう and 紹介状を持って行く. For job applications, the formal letter of recommendation is rather 推薦状.","levelNote":"Compound: 紹介 (N4) + 状 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あいつ、あのウェイトレスさんに三回もこのレストランに汁物はないのかって聞きやがったんだぜ。ウェイトレスさんその度にありませんって言ってさ、困惑してたよ。","romaji":"Aitsu, ano weitoresu-san ni sankai mo kono resutoran ni shirumono wa nai no ka tte kikiyagatta n da ze. Weitoresu-san sono tabi ni arimasen tte itte sa, konwaku shiteta yo.","german":"He asked that waitress three times whether this restaurant has soup, and each time she said no. She was baffled.","furigana":"さんかい|しるもの|き|たび|い|こんわく"},{"japanese":"和食の基本は、ご飯と汁物とおかずです。","romaji":"Washoku no kihon wa, gohan to shirumono to okazu desu.","german":"The basics of a Japanese meal are rice, soup and side dishes.","furigana":"わしょく|きほん|はん|しるもの"},{"japanese":"寒い日は温かい汁物が飲みたくなる。","romaji":"Samui hi wa atatakai shirumono ga nomitaku naru.","german":"On cold days you feel like drinking something hot and soupy.","furigana":"さむ|ひ|あたた|しるもの|の"}],"notes":"汁物 is the umbrella term for soups as part of a Japanese meal, such as 味噌汁, お吸い物 or 豚汁; in the classic menu 一汁三菜, exactly one 汁物 is included. Western soups are usually called スープ. In Japanese, you \"drink\" soup: 汁物を飲む. The word sounds rather matter-of-fact (menu, cookbook, cafeteria); in conversation people usually name 味噌汁 or スープ directly.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"昨日の夜酔っ払いに絡まれた時、心臓がドキドキしました。","romaji":"Kinou no yoru yopparai ni karamareta toki, shinzou ga dokidoki shimashita.","german":"My heart was pounding when a drunk picked a fight with me last night.","furigana":"きのう|よるよ|ぱら|から|とき|しんぞう"},{"japanese":"金曜日の終電には、酔っ払いがたくさん乗っている。","romaji":"Kin'youbi no shuuden ni wa, yopparai ga takusan notte iru.","german":"The last train on Friday is full of drunks.","furigana":"きんようび|しゅうでん|よ|ぱら|の"},{"japanese":"駅のホームで酔っ払いが寝ていたので、駅員を呼んだ。","romaji":"Eki no hoomu de yopparai ga nete ita node, ekiin o yonda.","german":"A drunk was sleeping on the station platform, so I called a station attendant.","furigana":"えき|よ|ぱら|ね|えきいん|よ"}],"notes":"酔っ払い is the colloquial, slightly derogatory word for a drunk person; it comes from the verb 酔っ払う \"to get drunk\". For the state, people say 酔っ払っている rather than 酔っ払いだ. Typical: 酔っ払いに絡まれる \"to be hassled by a drunk\". News reports use more formal words like 泥酔者; writing it in kana (よっぱらい) is also common.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二日酔いだし、足もつるし今日は最悪だよ。","romaji":"Futsukayoi da shi, ashi mo tsurushi kyō wa saiaku da yo.","german":"I have a hangover and a leg cramp too. Today is the worst.","furigana":"ふつかよ|あし|きょう|さいあく"},{"japanese":"飲みすぎて二日酔いになり、午前中の会議に集中できなかった。","romaji":"Nomisugite futsukayoi ni nari, gozenchuu no kaigi ni shuuchuu dekinakatta.","german":"I drank too much and had a hangover, so I couldn't concentrate in the morning meeting.","furigana":"の|ふつかよ|ごぜんちゅう|かいぎ|しゅうちゅう"},{"japanese":"二日酔いには、水をたくさん飲むといいらしいよ。","romaji":"Futsukayoi ni wa, mizu o takusan nomu to ii rashii yo.","german":"I hear that drinking lots of water helps with a hangover.","furigana":"ふつかよ|みず|の"}],"notes":"二日酔い literally means \"drunkenness on the second day\", that is, the hangover after too much alcohol. One says 二日酔いになる or simply 二日酔いだ; as a reason it takes 二日酔いで (二日酔いで頭が痛い). Not to be confused with 悪酔い (feeling sick while drinking itself) and 酔い止め, which is a remedy for motion sickness.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 12 (source: N5; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この給油所ではどのくらい働いているんですか？","romaji":"Kono kyuuyujo de wa dono kurai hataraite iru n desu ka?","german":"How long have you been working at this gas station?","furigana":"きゅうゆじょ|はたら"},{"japanese":"高速道路の給油所で満タンにしてから出発しよう。","romaji":"Kousoku douro no kyuuyujo de mantan ni shite kara shuppatsu shiyou.","german":"Let's fill up the tank at the expressway gas station and then set off.","furigana":"こうそくどうろ|きゅうゆじょ|まん|しゅっぱつ"},{"japanese":"台風の影響で、市内の給油所には長い列ができた。","romaji":"Taifuu no eikyou de, shinai no kyuuyujo ni wa nagai retsu ga dekita.","german":"Because of the typhoon, long lines formed at the gas stations in the city.","furigana":"たいふう|えいきょう|しない|きゅうゆじょ|なが|れつ"}],"notes":"給油所 is the formal word for \"gas station\", formed from 給油 \"refueling\" and 所 \"place\". In everyday speech people almost always say ガソリンスタンド or simply スタンド; 給油所 is found on signs, in the news and in official texts, and also for airplanes or ships. The related verb is 給油する \"to refuel\"; 満タンにする means \"to fill up the tank\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は完璧なトリプルアクセルを跳んだのに、スコアはあまり伸びなかった。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kanpeki na toripuru akuseru o tonda noni, sukoa wa amari nobinakatta.","german":"Although she landed a perfect triple axel, her score barely rose.","furigana":"かのじょ|かんぺき|と|の"},{"japanese":"カエルがぴょんと跳んで池に入った。","romaji":"Kaeru ga pyon to tonde ike ni haitta.","german":"The frog hopped with a splash into the pond.","furigana":"と|いけ|はい"},{"japanese":"水たまりを跳び越えようとしたが、失敗して靴がぬれてしまった。","romaji":"Mizutamari o tobikoeyou to shita ga, shippai shite kutsu ga nurete shimatta.","german":"I tried to jump over the puddle, but failed and my shoes got wet.","furigana":"みず|と|こ|しっぱい|くつ"}],"notes":"跳ぶ emphasizes jumping off the ground with the legs: \"to jump, to hop\", whereas 飛ぶ means \"to fly\". In everyday writing 飛ぶ is often also used for jumping, but in sports terms 跳 appears: 縄跳び, 跳び箱, 走り高跳び. With を it takes the obstacle or the jump itself (溝を跳ぶ, トリプルアクセルを跳ぶ); 跳び越える means \"to jump over\". Related is 跳ねる \"to hop, to splash\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5 (source: N5; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"昭和何年生まれですか。","romaji":"Shōwa nannen umare desu ka.","german":"In what year of Showa were you born?","furigana":"しょうわなんねんう"},{"japanese":"この喫茶店は昭和の雰囲気が残っていて、なんだか落ち着く。","romaji":"Kono kissaten wa Shouwa no fun'iki ga nokotte ite, nandaka ochitsuku.","german":"This coffee shop still has a Showa-era atmosphere, and somehow it's calming.","furigana":"きっさてん|しょうわ|ふんいき|のこ|お|つ"},{"japanese":"「女は家にいるべきだ」なんて、その考え方はちょっと昭和だね。","romaji":"\"Onna wa ie ni iru beki da\" nante, sono kangaekata wa chotto Shouwa da ne.","german":"\"Women should stay at home\" - that way of thinking is a bit Showa, isn't it?","furigana":"おんな|いえ|かんが|かた|しょうわ"}],"notes":"昭和 is the era name (元号) of Emperor Hirohito, 1926 to 1989, between 大正 and 平成. Years are given as 昭和〇〇年, especially for birth dates on forms (昭和何年生まれ). In colloquial speech, 昭和 also stands for \"old-fashioned, nostalgic\": 昭和っぽい, 昭和な考え方, 昭和レトロ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私はその漁師を知っていますよ。私の義理の兄ですから。","romaji":"Watashi wa sono ryōshi o shitte imasu yo. Watashi no giri no ani desu kara.","german":"I know that fisherman. He's my brother-in-law.","furigana":"わたし|りょうし|し|わたし|ぎり|あに"},{"japanese":"祖父は五十年間、漁師として毎朝海に出ていた。","romaji":"Sofu wa gojuunenkan, ryoushi to shite maiasa umi ni dete ita.","german":"My grandfather went out to sea every morning as a fisherman for fifty years.","furigana":"そふ|ごじゅうねんかん|りょうし|まいあさうみ|で"},{"japanese":"港では、漁師たちが朝早くから網の準備をしている。","romaji":"Minato de wa, ryoushitachi ga asa hayaku kara ami no junbi o shite iru.","german":"At the harbor, the fishermen start preparing their nets early in the morning.","furigana":"みなと|りょうし|あさはや|あみ|じゅんび"}],"notes":"漁師 is the fisherman as an occupation. Note the reading りょう for 漁 - in most other words it is read ぎょ (漁業, 漁船). 猟師 \"hunter\" is pronounced the same, so context helps in conversation. Related terms are 漁師町 \"fishing village\" and 漁師料理; in statistics and administration, the term is rather 漁業者 or 漁民.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"昨夜漁船を盗まれました。","romaji":"Sakuya gyosen o nusumaremashita.","german":"My fishing boat was stolen last night.","furigana":"さくやぎょせん|ぬす"},{"japanese":"台風が近づいているため、漁船はすべて港に避難している。","romaji":"Taifuu ga chikazuite iru tame, gyosen wa subete minato ni hinan shite iru.","german":"Because a typhoon is approaching, all the fishing boats in the harbor took shelter.","furigana":"たいふう|ちか|ぎょせん|みなと|ひなん"},{"japanese":"夜明け前に、たくさんの漁船が港を出ていった。","romaji":"Yoake mae ni, takusan no gyosen ga minato o dete itta.","german":"Even before sunrise, many fishing boats set out from the harbor.","furigana":"よあ|まえ|ぎょせん|みなと|で"}],"notes":"漁船 is a fishing boat or fishing vessel; 漁 is read ぎょ here, unlike in 漁師 (りょうし). A boat for recreational anglers, by contrast, is called 釣り船. Typical are 漁船が出港する/港に戻る and 漁船で漁に出る. As counters, 隻 is used for larger ships and 艘 for small boats.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"過去の過ちを償うことに、疲れちゃったんだよ。","romaji":"Kako no ayamachi o tsugunau koto ni, tsukarechatta n da yo.","german":"I'm tired of atoning for my past mistakes.","furigana":"かこ|あやま|つぐな|つか"},{"japanese":"彼は十年間刑務所に入って、罪を償った。","romaji":"Kare wa juunenkan keimusho ni haitte, tsumi o tsugunatta.","german":"He spent ten years in prison and paid for his crime.","furigana":"かれ|じゅうねんかんけいむしょ|はい|つみ|つぐな"},{"japanese":"自分のミスで会社に損害を出してしまい、どう償えばいいか分からない。","romaji":"Jibun no misu de kaisha ni songai o dashite shimai, dou tsugunaeba ii ka wakaranai.","german":"I caused the company a loss through my own mistake, and I don't know how to make up for it.","furigana":"じぶん|かいしゃ|そんがい|だ|つぐな|わ"}],"notes":"償う is transitive and means \"to make amends, to atone, to compensate\": 罪を償う \"to pay for a crime\", 過ちを償う, 損害を償う. The noun is 償い (罪の償い). Actually paying for a damaged object is rather 弁償する, legal compensation for damages is 賠償. The word is rather elevated and sounds serious.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"撮影中に漁師さんの網を誤って破いてしまったため、弁償しなくてはならなかった。","romaji":"Satsueichū ni ryōshisan no ami o ayamatte yabuite shimatta tame, benshō shinakute wa naranakatta.","german":"While filming, I accidentally tore a fisherman's net and had to replace it.","furigana":"さつえいちゅう|りょうし|あみ|あやま|やぶ|べんしょう"},{"japanese":"割ってしまったお皿は、もちろん弁償します。","romaji":"Watte shimatta osara wa, mochiron benshou shimasu.","german":"Of course I will replace the plate I broke.","furigana":"わ|さら|べんしょう"},{"japanese":"図書館の本をなくしたら、弁償しなければなりません。","romaji":"Toshokan no hon o nakushitara, benshou shinakereba narimasen.","german":"If you lose a library book, you have to replace it.","furigana":"としょかん|ほん|べんしょう"}],"notes":"弁償 means to make up concretely, with money or a replacement, for damage one has caused: 割った皿を弁償する, なくした本を弁償する. As a suru-verb it is transitive (〜を弁償する). In contrast, 賠償 is legal compensation on a larger scale (損害賠償), 補償 is compensation for losses, e.g. by the state or a company, and 償う is the general, also moral, \"to make amends\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その囚人は、十五年間の刑期を務めた後で釈放されたが、行く当てがなかった。","romaji":"Sono shuujin wa, juugonenkan no keiki o tsutometa ato de shakuhou sareta ga, iku ate ga nakatta.","german":"The prisoner was released after serving a fifteen-year term, but he had nowhere to go.","furigana":"しゅうじん|じゅうごねんかん|けいき|つと|あと|しゃくほう|い|あ"},{"japanese":"彼は刑期を終えて、来月社会に戻る予定だ。","romaji":"Kare wa keiki o oete, raigetsu shakai ni modoru yotei da.","german":"He has finished his sentence and plans to return to society next month.","furigana":"かれ|けいき|お|らいげつしゃかい|もど|よてい"},{"japanese":"刑務所での態度がよかったため、刑期が一年短くなった。","romaji":"Keimusho de no taido ga yokatta tame, keiki ga ichinen mijikaku natta.","german":"Because of his good behavior in prison, his sentence was shortened by a year.","furigana":"けいむしょ|たいど|けいき|いちねんみじか"}],"notes":"刑期 is the duration of a prison sentence, that is, the time in custody, not the punishment itself (that would be 刑 or 刑罰). Typical combinations are 刑期を終える, 刑期を務める, 刑期が短縮される and 刑期満了 \"end of the prison term\". The word belongs to legal and news language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"裁判所は被告に終身刑を言い渡した。","romaji":"Saibansho wa hikoku ni shuushinkei o iiwatashita.","german":"The court sentenced the defendant to life imprisonment.","furigana":"さいばんしょ|ひこく|しゅうしんけい|い|わた"},{"japanese":"その国では死刑が廃止され、最も重い刑は終身刑になった。","romaji":"Sono kuni de wa shikei ga haishi sare, mottomo omoi kei wa shuushinkei ni natta.","german":"In that country, the death penalty was abolished and the heaviest punishment became life imprisonment.","furigana":"くに|しけい|はいし|もっと|おも|けい|しゅうしんけい"},{"japanese":"日本には、仮釈放のない終身刑は今のところない。","romaji":"Nihon ni wa, karishakuhou no nai shuushinkei wa ima no tokoro nai.","german":"Japan currently has no life imprisonment without parole.","furigana":"にほん|かりしゃくほう|しゅうしんけい|いま"}],"notes":"終身刑 is a life sentence; people say 終身刑を言い渡される \"to be sentenced to life imprisonment\" or 終身刑に処される. Japanese criminal law formally has 無期懲役, in which release on parole is possible; 終身刑 therefore often refers to sentences abroad or explicitly to imprisonment without release. The word belongs to legal and news language, often in contrast to 死刑 \"death penalty\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"妻への不信感は募るばかりです。","romaji":"Tsuma e no fushinkan wa tsunoru bakari desu.","german":"My distrust of my wife just keeps growing.","furigana":"つま|ふしんかん|つの"},{"japanese":"連絡が来ないまま日が経つにつれて、不安が募っていった。","romaji":"Renraku ga konai mama hi ga tatsu ni tsurete, fuan ga tsunotte itta.","german":"The more days passed without news, the greater the worry became.","furigana":"れんらく|こ|ひ|た|ふあん|つの"},{"japanese":"地震の被災地のために、駅前で寄付を募っています。","romaji":"Jishin no hisaichi no tame ni, ekimae de kifu o tsunotte imasu.","german":"In front of the station, donations are being solicited for the earthquake areas.","furigana":"じしん|ひさいち|えきまえ|きふ|つの"}],"notes":"募る has two uses. Intransitively, feelings keep growing: 不安が募る, 不満が募る, 思いが募る, often with 〜ばかり or 〜一方. Transitively it means \"to recruit, to collect (donations, participants)\": 寄付を募る, 参加者を募る - close in meaning to 募集する, but more elevated. Both meanings are rather written-language.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"トーフグはまだブロガーを募集中ですよ。","romaji":"Toufugu wa mada burogaa o boshuuchuu desu yo.","german":"Tofugu is still looking for bloggers.","furigana":"ぼしゅうちゅう"},{"japanese":"アルバイト募集の張り紙を見て、すぐに電話した。","romaji":"Arubaito boshuu no harigami o mite, sugu ni denwa shita.","german":"I saw the \"part-time help wanted\" sign and called right away.","furigana":"ぼしゅう|は|がみ|み|でんわ"},{"japanese":"市は来年のマラソン大会の参加者を募集しています。","romaji":"Shi wa rainen no marason taikai no sankasha o boshuu shite imasu.","german":"The city is recruiting participants for next year's marathon.","furigana":"し|らいねん|たいかい|さんかしゃ|ぼしゅう"}],"notes":"募集 means that a company or organization publicly seeks people or submissions: 社員を募集する, アルバイト募集, 参加者募集中, 作品を募集する. A person who responds to such a call 応募する; the conditions of the call are called 募集要項. 募集 is more neutral and everyday than the elevated 募る.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 13"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"応募用紙は裁判所でもらえますよ。","romaji":"Ōbo yōshi wa saibansho de moraemasu yo.","german":"You can get the application form at the court.","furigana":"おうぼようし|さいばんしょ"},{"japanese":"求人広告を見て、その会社に応募した。","romaji":"Kyuujin koukoku o mite, sono kaisha ni oubo shita.","german":"I saw the job advertisement and applied to the company.","furigana":"きゅうじんこうこく|み|かいしゃ|おうぼ"},{"japanese":"写真コンテストには千件以上の応募があった。","romaji":"Shashin kontesuto ni wa senken ijou no oubo ga atta.","german":"Over a thousand entries were received for the photo contest.","furigana":"しゃしん|せんけんいじょう|おうぼ"}],"notes":"応募 is the response to a call for applicants (募集): one applies for a job or takes part in a contest or prize draw, with the pattern 〜に応募する. Common compounds are 応募者, 応募用紙, 応募締め切り. Signing up for courses or events in general is more 申し込む.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あなたにこのプロジェクトの指揮を執ってもらいたいんです。","romaji":"Anata ni kono purojekuto no shiki o totte moraitai n desu.","german":"We want you to take charge of this project.","furigana":"しき|と"},{"japanese":"久しぶりに筆を執って、恩師に手紙を書いた。","romaji":"Hisashiburi ni fude o totte, onshi ni tegami o kaita.","german":"For the first time in a long while, I took up my pen and wrote a letter to my old teacher.","furigana":"ひさ|ふで|と|おんし|てがみ|か"},{"japanese":"祖父は三十年間、高校で教鞭を執っていた。","romaji":"Sofu wa sanjuunenkan, koukou de kyouben o totte ita.","german":"My grandfather taught at a high school for thirty years.","furigana":"そふ|さんじゅうねんかん|こうこう|きょうべん|と"}],"notes":"執る is one of the many spellings of とる and stands for taking on and carrying out a task: 指揮を執る \"to take command\", 筆を執る \"to take up the pen\", 事務を執る, 教鞭を執る \"to work as a teacher\". For ordinary taking, people write 取る, for photos 撮る, for catching 捕る, and for adopting/hiring 採る. The word feels literary and occurs almost only in these fixed combinations.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼が二塁打を打つのは久しぶりだね。いつもはホームランだからな。","romaji":"Kare ga niruida o utsu no wa hisashiburi da ne. Itsumo wa hoomuran da kara na.","german":"It's been a while since he hit a double. Usually he hits home runs.","furigana":"かれ|にるいだ|う|ひさ"},{"japanese":"九回裏、田中選手の三塁打で同点に追いついた。","romaji":"Kyuukai ura, Tanaka senshu no sanruida de douten ni oitsuita.","german":"In the bottom of the ninth, Tanaka's triple tied the game.","furigana":"きゅうかいうら|たなかせんしゅ|さんるいだ|どうてん|お"},{"japanese":"彼は今シーズン、本塁打を三十本も打った。","romaji":"Kare wa kon shiizun, honruida o sanjuppon mo utta.","german":"He hit as many as thirty home runs this season.","furigana":"かれ|こん|ほんるいだ|さんじゅっぽん|う"}],"notes":"塁打 is, in baseball, a hit with which the batter reaches one or more bases. On its own it is rarely used; it mostly appears in compounds: 二塁打 \"double\", 三塁打 \"triple\", 本塁打 \"home run\" (colloquially ホームラン). A simple hit is called ヒット in everyday speech; in statistics, 塁打数 is the total number of bases reached.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの一塁の塁審、試合中に屁をこきまくってたぜ。","romaji":"Ano ichirui no ruishin, shiaichū ni he o kokimakutteta ze.","german":"That first-base umpire was farting like crazy during the game.","furigana":"いちるい|るいしん|しあいちゅう|へ"},{"japanese":"一塁の塁審は、すぐにセーフと判定した。","romaji":"Ichirui no ruishin wa, sugu ni sēfu to hantei shita.","german":"The first-base umpire immediately called him safe.","furigana":"いちるい|るいしん|はんてい"},{"japanese":"プロ野球では、球審のほかに三人の塁審がいる。","romaji":"Puro yakyū de wa, kyūshin no hoka ni sannin no ruishin ga iru.","german":"In professional baseball, there are three base umpires in addition to the home plate umpire.","furigana":"やきゅう|きゅうしん|さんにん|るいしん"}],"notes":"塁審 is the umpire at a base in baseball (塁 \"base\", 審 from 審判 \"umpire\"). The home plate umpire behind the catcher is called 球審 (also 主審). Depending on position, people say 一塁塁審, 二塁塁審, 三塁塁審; the call is called 判定 (アウト・セーフと判定する). The word belongs to sports terminology; generally people say 審判.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"母が認知症を患ってるかもしれないんです。","romaji":"Haha ga ninchishou o wazuratteru kamo shirenai n desu.","german":"My mother may be suffering from dementia.","furigana":"はは|にんちしょう|わずら"},{"japanese":"父は長年、糖尿病を患っている。","romaji":"Chichi wa naganen, tounyoubyou o wazuratte iru.","german":"My father has suffered from diabetes for many years.","furigana":"ちち|ながねん|とうにょうびょう|わずら"},{"japanese":"彼は若い頃に心臓を患い、スポーツをあきらめた。","romaji":"Kare wa wakai koro ni shinzou o wazurai, supootsu o akirameta.","german":"He had heart trouble when he was young and gave up sports.","furigana":"かれ|わか|ころ|しんぞう|わずら"}],"notes":"患う means \"to suffer from an (usually serious or long-lasting) illness\" and takes を: 病気を患う, 心臓を患う; often in the form 患っている. For colds and harmless illnesses, people say 〜にかかる or 〜になる instead. The word is literary and elevated; related are 長患い \"long illness\" and 煩う \"to worry, to be troubled\", which has the same reading.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人員不足でごった返しになっていた病院が急患の受け入れを断ったせいで、男性が死亡したそうなんだけど、でもそれって病院だけのせいじゃないよね。","romaji":"Jin'in fusoku de gottagaeshi ni natte ita byōin ga kyūkan no ukeire o kotowatta sei de, dansei ga shibō shita sō na n da kedo, demo sore tte byōin dake no sei ja nai yo ne.","german":"I hear a man died because a hospital, understaffed and in chaos, refused to take an emergency patient, but that's not only the hospital's fault, is it?","furigana":"じんいんふそく|がえ|びょういん|きゅうかん|う|い|ことわ|だんせい|しぼう|びょういん"},{"japanese":"夜中に急患が次々と運ばれてきて、医者たちは休む暇もなかった。","romaji":"Yonaka ni kyuukan ga tsugitsugi to hakobarete kite, ishatachi wa yasumu hima mo nakatta.","german":"Emergency patients were brought in one after another in the middle of the night, and the doctors had no time to rest.","furigana":"よなか|きゅうかん|つぎつぎ|はこ|いしゃ|やす|ひま"},{"japanese":"急患のため、診察の開始が遅れております。","romaji":"Kyuukan no tame, shinsatsu no kaishi ga okurete orimasu.","german":"Because of an emergency patient, the start of consultations is delayed.","furigana":"きゅうかん|しんさつ|かいし|おく"}],"notes":"急患 is the short form of 急病患者 and refers to an emergency patient who must be treated immediately. Typical are 急患を受け入れる \"to admit an emergency patient\", 急患を断る and 急患が運ばれる. The word comes from hospital routine and news language; 救急患者 means the same, and the emergency room is called 救急外来 or 急患センター.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"母親が病気になったので、私は国に戻ることにしました。","romaji":"Hahaoya ga byōki ni natta node, watashi wa kuni ni modoru koto ni shimashita.","german":"Because my mother became ill, I decided to return to my country.","furigana":"ははおや|びょうき|わたし|くに|もど"},{"japanese":"忘れ物をしたので、家に戻りました。","romaji":"Wasuremono o shita node, ie ni modorimashita.","german":"I had forgotten something, so I went back home.","furigana":"わす|もの|いえ|もど"},{"japanese":"課長は三時に戻ります。","romaji":"Kachō wa sanji ni modorimasu.","german":"The section chief will be back at three o'clock.","furigana":"かちょう|さんじ|もど"}],"notes":"戻る means \"to return to a place or state where one has been before\": 席に戻る, 会社に戻る, 元に戻る \"to become as before again\". 帰る, by contrast, means returning to where one belongs (home, one's homeland). That is why in the office one says 三時に戻ります when one is only away briefly. The transitive partner is 戻す \"to put back, to return\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 20"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"対向するバイクの存在を完璧に見落としていました。","romaji":"Taikō suru baiku no sonzai o kanpeki ni miotoshite imashita.","german":"I completely failed to notice the oncoming motorcycle.","furigana":"たいこう|そんざい|かんぺき|みお"},{"japanese":"この道は狭いので、対向する車とすれ違うときは注意してください。","romaji":"Kono michi wa semai node, taikou suru kuruma to surechigau toki wa chuui shite kudasai.","german":"This road is narrow, so be careful when passing oncoming cars.","furigana":"みち|せま|たいこう|くるま|ちが|ちゅうい"},{"japanese":"対向車のライトがまぶしくて、一瞬前が見えなかった。","romaji":"Taikousha no raito ga mabushikute, isshun mae ga mienakatta.","german":"The headlights of the oncoming car were so bright that I couldn't see ahead for a moment.","furigana":"たいこうしゃ|いっしゅんまえ|み"}],"notes":"対向 means \"to face each other, to come from the opposite direction\" and is used above all in road traffic: 対向車 \"oncoming traffic\", 対向車線 \"oncoming lane\"; as a verb mostly attributive, as in 対向するバイク. Note: 対抗する is read the same way but means \"to compete, to match, to put up resistance\". The word is rather technical and written; in conversation people often say 向こうから来る車.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"学生たちは、権威に対して徹底抗戦を強いられました。","romaji":"Gakuseitachi wa, ken'i ni taishite tettei kousen o shiiraremashita.","german":"The students were forced into all-out resistance against authority.","furigana":"がくせい|けんい|たい|てっていこうせん|し"},{"japanese":"その国の軍は、首都が囲まれても最後まで抗戦を続けた。","romaji":"Sono kuni no gun wa, shuto ga kakomarete mo saigo made kousen o tsuzuketa.","german":"Even when the capital was surrounded, the country's army kept resisting to the end.","furigana":"くに|ぐん|しゅと|かこ|さいご|こうせん|つづ"},{"japanese":"住民たちは工場の建設に反対し、徹底抗戦の構えを見せている。","romaji":"Juumintachi wa koujou no kensetsu ni hantai shi, tettei kousen no kamae o misete iru.","german":"The residents oppose the construction of the factory and are prepared to resist to the bitter end.","furigana":"じゅうみん|こうじょう|けんせつ|はんたい|てっていこうせん|かま|み"}],"notes":"抗戦 is resistance against an attack, originally in the military sense: 抗戦を続ける, 最後まで抗戦する. Figuratively it is used in politics and disputes, above all in 徹底抗戦 \"resistance to the utmost\". General resistance is 抵抗; 交戦, which is read the same way, means \"battle between two sides\". Register: written, news language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"気象庁は六月上旬に梅雨が始まると言っている。","romaji":"Kishōchō wa rokugatsu jōjun ni tsuyu ga hajimaru to itte iru.","german":"The Japan Meteorological Agency says the rainy season will begin in early June.","furigana":"きしょうちょう|ろくがつじょうじゅん|つゆ|はじ|い"},{"japanese":"来月上旬に引っ越す予定なので、今は荷造りで忙しい。","romaji":"Raigetsu joujun ni hikkosu yotei na node, ima wa nizukuri de isogashii.","german":"I'm planning to move early next month, so I'm busy packing right now.","furigana":"らいげつじょうじゅん|ひ|こ|よてい|いま|にづく|いそが"},{"japanese":"ご注文の商品は、三月上旬にお届けする予定です。","romaji":"Gochuumon no shouhin wa, sangatsu joujun ni otodoke suru yotei desu.","german":"The product you ordered is scheduled to be delivered in early March.","furigana":"ちゅうもん|しょうひん|さんがつじょうじゅん|とど|よてい"}],"notes":"上旬 is the first third of the month, that is, the 1st to the 10th day; 中旬 (11th-20th) and 下旬 (21st to the end of the month) belong with it. People say 六月上旬 or 六月の上旬 and use it with に for time expressions. The word is typical for business letters, weather reports and announcements; colloquially people say 月の初め or 初めごろ instead.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"四月の下旬に日本に戻って、六月上旬にまたアメリカに戻ってくる予定です。","romaji":"Shigatsu no gejun ni Nihon ni modotte, rokugatsu joujun ni mata Amerika ni modotte kuru yotei desu.","german":"I plan to return to Japan in late April and come back to the US again in early June.","furigana":"しがつ|げじゅん|にほん|もど|ろくがつじょうじゅん|もど|よてい"},{"japanese":"八月下旬になっても、厳しい暑さが続いている。","romaji":"Hachigatsu gejun ni natte mo, kibishii atsusa ga tsuzuite iru.","german":"Even in late August, the severe heat continues.","furigana":"はちがつげじゅん|きび|あつ|つづ"},{"japanese":"レポートの締め切りは今月の下旬です。","romaji":"Repooto no shimekiri wa kongetsu no gejun desu.","german":"The deadline for the report is the end of this month.","furigana":"し|き|こんげつ|げじゅん"}],"notes":"下旬 is the last third of the month, from the 21st day to the end of the month; counterparts are 上旬 and 中旬. Note the reading げ for 下 (not した or か). People say 四月下旬 or 四月の下旬, and as a point in time with に. It is common in planning, news and weather reports; colloquially also 月末ごろ or 終わりごろ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"予定日は十一月の中旬です。","romaji":"Yoteibi wa jūichigatsu no chūjun desu.","german":"The due date is mid-November.","furigana":"よていび|じゅういちがつ|ちゅうじゅん"},{"japanese":"今年は四月中旬になって、ようやく桜が満開になった。","romaji":"Kotoshi wa shigatsu chuujun ni natte, youyaku sakura ga mankai ni natta.","german":"This year the cherry blossoms did not reach full bloom until mid-April.","furigana":"ことし|しがつちゅうじゅん|さくら|まんかい"},{"japanese":"会議は来月の中旬に延期されました。","romaji":"Kaigi wa raigetsu no chuujun ni enki saremashita.","german":"The meeting has been postponed to the middle of next month.","furigana":"かいぎ|らいげつ|ちゅうじゅん|えんき"}],"notes":"中旬 is the middle third of the month, the 11th to the 20th day, between 上旬 and 下旬. It appears in patterns like 十一月中旬 or 十一月の中旬 and with に as a time expression. Typical for dates in business, news and weather reports; in conversation people also say 半ばごろ or 真ん中ごろ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"感染源を台湾のアヒルの群れだと突き止めました。","romaji":"Kansengen o Taiwan no ahiru no mure da to tsukitomemashita.","german":"We traced the source of the infection to a flock of ducks in Taiwan.","furigana":"かんせんげん|たいわん|む|つ|と"},{"japanese":"去年の夏、家族で台湾へ旅行しました。","romaji":"Kyonen no natsu, kazoku de Taiwan e ryokou shimashita.","german":"Last summer I traveled to Taiwan with my family.","furigana":"きょねん|なつ|かぞく|たいわん|りょこう"},{"japanese":"台湾の夜市で食べた小籠包が忘れられない。","romaji":"Taiwan no yoichi de tabeta shouronpou ga wasurerarenai.","german":"I can't forget the xiaolongbao I ate at a night market in Taiwan.","furigana":"たいわん|よいち|た||わす"}],"notes":"台湾 is the Japanese name for Taiwan; the character 湾 means \"bay\". Common derivations are 台湾人, 台湾料理, 台湾茶 and 台湾旅行. In everyday use the word is politically neutral; official documents sometimes use 中華民国. Because of its proximity and good food, Taiwan is a very popular travel destination for Japanese people.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女の一言が彼の怒りを爆発させました。","romaji":"Kanojo no hitokoto ga kare no ikari o bakuhatsu sasemashita.","german":"Her one remark made his anger explode.","furigana":"かのじょ|ひとこと|かれ|いか|ばくはつ"},{"japanese":"工場でガスが爆発し、数人がけがをした。","romaji":"Koujou de gasu ga bakuhatsu shi, suunin ga kega o shita.","german":"Gas exploded at the factory, and several people were injured.","furigana":"こうじょう|ばくはつ|すうにん"},{"japanese":"その新しいゲームは発売直後から爆発的に売れている。","romaji":"Sono atarashii geemu wa hatsubai chokugo kara bakuhatsuteki ni urete iru.","german":"That new game has been selling explosively since just after its release.","furigana":"あたら|はつばいちょくご|ばくはつてき|う"}],"notes":"爆発 is an explosion; as a する-verb it is intransitive (ガスが爆発する), and transitively it is used as 爆発させる. Figuratively, feelings or dissatisfaction explode: 怒りが爆発する, 不満が爆発する. Very common is 爆発的 \"explosive,\" as in 爆発的な人気 or 爆発的に売れる. Distinction: 破裂 is bursting (tire, balloon, pipe).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"爆弾を分解することは危険だってことは分かってたんだけど、すごく興味があったんだ。","romaji":"Bakudan o bunkai suru koto wa kiken datte koto wa wakatteta n da kedo, sugoku kyoumi ga atta n da.","german":"I knew that dismantling a bomb was dangerous, but I was really curious.","furigana":"ばくだん|ぶんかい|きけん|わ|きょうみ"},{"japanese":"空港で不審な荷物が見つかり、爆弾の可能性があるとして全員が避難した。","romaji":"Kuukou de fushin na nimotsu ga mitsukari, bakudan no kanousei ga aru to shite zen'in ga hinan shita.","german":"A suspicious package was found at the airport, and since it might be a bomb, everyone was evacuated.","furigana":"くうこう|ふしん|にもつ|み|ばくだん|かのうせい|ぜんいん|ひなん"},{"japanese":"大臣の爆弾発言で、国会は大騒ぎになった。","romaji":"Daijin no bakudan hatsugen de, kokkai wa oosawagi ni natta.","german":"The minister's bombshell remark caused an uproar in the Diet.","furigana":"だいじん|ばくだんはつげん|こっかい|おおさわ"}],"notes":"爆弾 is a bomb; typical combinations are 爆弾を仕掛ける \"to plant a bomb\", 爆弾が爆発する, 時限爆弾 \"time bomb\" and 原子爆弾 (short 原爆). Figuratively, 爆弾発言 stands for a \"bombshell\" in the sense of a shocking remark, and 〜に爆弾を抱える means to have a latent problem, such as a vulnerable knee in athletes.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 40"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"母さんと父さんを爆笑させるような、何かいいエイプリルフールの案はある？","romaji":"Kaasan to tousan o bakushou saseru you na, nani ka ii eipuriru fuuru no an wa aru?","german":"Do you have any good April Fools' ideas that would make Mom and Dad burst out laughing?","furigana":"かあ|とう|ばくしょう|なに|あん"},{"japanese":"司会者の冗談で、会場は大爆笑に包まれた。","romaji":"Shikaisha no joudan de, kaijou wa daibakushou ni tsutsumareta.","german":"At the host's joke, the whole hall was filled with roaring laughter.","furigana":"しかいしゃ|じょうだん|かいじょう|だいばくしょう|つつ"},{"japanese":"昨日見た動物の動画、マジで爆笑したよ。","romaji":"Kinou mita doubutsu no douga, maji de bakushou shita yo.","german":"I really cracked up at the animal video I watched yesterday.","furigana":"きのうみ|どうぶつ|どうが|ばくしょう"}],"notes":"爆笑 originally refers to the roaring laughter of a whole group (会場が爆笑する, 爆笑に包まれる). Colloquially it is also said when a single person bursts out laughing (この動画、爆笑した). Common forms are 大爆笑, 爆笑を誘う and 爆笑させる \"to make roar with laughter\". As a する-verb, it is intransitive.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"駅前で自爆テロが起き、多くの人が犠牲になった。","romaji":"Ekimae de jibaku tero ga oki, ooku no hito ga gisei ni natta.","german":"A suicide bombing occurred in front of the station, and many people were killed.","furigana":"えきまえ|じばく|お|おお|ひと|ぎせい"},{"japanese":"余計なことを言って、完全に自爆してしまった。","romaji":"Yokei na koto o itte, kanzen ni jibaku shite shimatta.","german":"I said something unnecessary and totally shot myself in the foot.","furigana":"よけい|い|かんぜん|じばく"},{"japanese":"そのゲームでは、自分が置いた爆弾に当たると自爆してしまう。","romaji":"Sono geemu de wa, jibun ga oita bakudan ni ataru to jibaku shite shimau.","german":"In that game, you blow yourself up if you hit a bomb you placed yourself.","furigana":"じぶん|お|ばくだん|あ|じばく"}],"notes":"自爆 literally means \"to blow oneself up\": in the news for suicide attacks (自爆テロ) or for weapons and machines that destroy themselves (自爆装置). Colloquially it is very common in a figurative sense: to harm oneself through one's own words or actions, to \"score an own goal\" (余計なことを言って自爆した). As a する-verb, it is intransitive.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"そのチョコレート工場は、爆撃を受けて粉々になりました。","romaji":"Sono chokorēto kōjō wa, bakugeki o ukete konagona ni narimashita.","german":"The chocolate factory was reduced to pieces by the bombing.","furigana":"こうじょう|ばくげき|う|こなごな"},{"japanese":"この町は戦争中に何度も爆撃された。","romaji":"Kono machi wa sensouchuu ni nando mo bakugeki sareta.","german":"This town was bombed several times during the war.","furigana":"まち|せんそうちゅう|なんど|ばくげき"},{"japanese":"爆撃機の音を聞くと、祖母は今でも戦争を思い出すそうだ。","romaji":"Bakugekiki no oto o kiku to, sobo wa ima demo sensou o omoidasu sou da.","german":"When my grandmother hears the sound of a bomber, she still remembers the war.","furigana":"ばくげきき|おと|き|そぼ|いま|せんそう|おも|だ"}],"notes":"爆撃 is bombardment from the air by aircraft; people say 〜を爆撃する or, in the passive, 爆撃を受ける/爆撃される. The aircraft for it is called 爆撃機. Synonymous and frequent in the news is 空爆; shelling with artillery, by contrast, is 砲撃, and a general attack is 攻撃.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ボールが地面で弾んで湖に落ちた。","romaji":"Bouru ga jimen de hazunde mizuumi ni ochita.","german":"The ball bounced off the ground and fell into the lake.","furigana":"じめん|はず|みずうみ|お"},{"japanese":"久しぶりに会った友達と、夜遅くまで話が弾んだ。","romaji":"Hisashiburi ni atta tomodachi to, yoru osoku made hanashi ga hazunda.","german":"I chatted late into the night with a friend I hadn't seen in a long time.","furigana":"ひさ|あ|ともだち|よるおそ|はなし|はず"},{"japanese":"サービスがとてもよかったので、チップを弾んだ。","romaji":"Saabisu ga totemo yokatta node, chippu o hazunda.","german":"Because the service was so good, I gave a generous tip.","furigana":"はず"}],"notes":"弾む is intransitive in its basic sense: a ball bounces or rebounds (ボールが弾む). Figuratively, something becomes lively: 話が弾む \"the conversation gets going\", 声が弾む, 心が弾む. 息が弾む means \"to be out of breath\", and transitively チップを弾む means \"to give a generous tip\". The causative form 弾ませる appears e.g. in 声を弾ませる; similar is 跳ねる \"to hop\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"俺は弾丸の雨をとても上手くかわした。","romaji":"Ore wa dangan no ame o totemo umaku kawashita.","german":"I dodged the rain of bullets very skillfully.","furigana":"おれ|だんがん|あめ|うま"},{"japanese":"古い建物の壁には、戦争中の弾丸の跡が残っていた。","romaji":"Furui tatemono no kabe ni wa, sensouchuu no dangan no ato ga nokotte ita.","german":"Bullet marks from the war remained on the wall of the old building.","furigana":"ふる|たてもの|かべ|せんそうちゅう|だんがん|あと|のこ"},{"japanese":"週末に一泊二日の弾丸ツアーでソウルに行ってきた。","romaji":"Shuumatsu ni ippaku futsuka no dangan tsuaa de Souru ni itte kita.","german":"On the weekend I went to Seoul on a whirlwind two-day, one-night trip.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|いっぱくふつか|だんがん|い"}],"notes":"弾丸 is a projectile or rifle bullet; in everyday speech people also say 弾 (たま) or 銃弾. Typical are 弾丸が当たる, 弾丸をよける and 弾丸の跡. Figuratively it stands for extreme speed: 弾丸ツアー is a \"whirlwind trip\" with a very tight schedule, 弾丸のような速さ \"as fast as a bullet\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日の午後に、盲目の人の聴力に関するレポートを提出するつもりです。","romaji":"Kyou no gogo ni, moumoku no hito no chouryoku ni kansuru repooto o teishutsu suru tsumori desu.","german":"This afternoon I intend to submit my report on the hearing ability of blind people.","furigana":"きょう|ごご|もうもく|ひと|ちょうりょく|かん|ていしゅつ"},{"japanese":"年を取ると、高い音に対する聴力が落ちやすい。","romaji":"Toshi o toru to, takai oto ni taisuru chouryoku ga ochiyasui.","german":"As people get older, their hearing for high-pitched sounds tends to decline.","furigana":"とし|と|たか|おと|たい|ちょうりょく|お"},{"japanese":"健康診断で聴力の検査も受けました。","romaji":"Kenkou shindan de chouryoku no kensa mo ukemashita.","german":"I also had a hearing test at my health checkup.","furigana":"けんこうしんだん|ちょうりょく|けんさ|う"}],"notes":"聴力 is the ability to hear, that is, the measurable capacity to hear. Typical are 聴力が落ちる/低下する, 聴力を失う and 聴力検査 \"hearing test\". Parallel to it is 視力 \"eyesight\". 聴覚, by contrast, refers to the sense of hearing as such (聴覚障害).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"電話が壊れてるのかも。だって、聴者から電話が一本もないなんて、おかしいよ。","romaji":"Denwa ga kowareteru no kamo. Datte, chousha kara denwa ga ippon mo nai nante, okashii yo.","german":"Maybe the phone is broken. It's strange that there hasn't been a single call from a listener.","furigana":"でんわ|こわ|ちょうしゃ|でんわ|いっぽん"},{"japanese":"ろう者と聴者が一緒に楽しめる演劇を作りたい。","romaji":"Rousha to chousha ga issho ni tanoshimeru engeki o tsukuritai.","german":"I want to make a play that deaf people and hearing people can enjoy together.","furigana":"しゃ|ちょうしゃ|いっしょ|たの|えんげき|つく"},{"japanese":"最近は、手話を学ぶ聴者が増えている。","romaji":"Saikin wa, shuwa o manabu chousha ga fuete iru.","german":"Recently, more and more hearing people are learning sign language.","furigana":"さいきん|しゅわ|まな|ちょうしゃ|ふ"}],"notes":"聴者 means \"listener\" (of a broadcast, for example), but it is rare in this meaning; more usual are 聴取者, リスナー or 聞き手. Today 聴者 is encountered above all as the opposite of ろう者 \"deaf person\" and refers to hearing people, for example in texts about sign language (手話). The word is literary or technical.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"森くんって、洋楽聴くの？","romaji":"Mori-kun tte, yougaku kiku no?","german":"Mori-kun, do you listen to Western music?","furigana":"もり|ようがくき"},{"japanese":"寝る前にクラシック音楽を聴くと、よく眠れる。","romaji":"Neru mae ni kurashikku ongaku o kiku to, yoku nemureru.","german":"If I listen to classical music before going to bed, I sleep well.","furigana":"ね|まえ|おんがく|き|ねむ"},{"japanese":"学生たちは、教授の講演を熱心に聴いていた。","romaji":"Gakuseitachi wa, kyouju no kouen o nesshin ni kiite ita.","german":"The students listened attentively to the professor's lecture.","furigana":"がくせい|きょうじゅ|こうえん|ねっしん|き"}],"notes":"聴く emphasizes attentive, conscious listening, especially to music, radio, lectures or other people's concerns (音楽を聴く, 講演を聴く, 話をじっくり聴く). 聞く is the general word for \"to hear\" and also for \"to ask\"; for \"to ask\" one never writes 聴く. In practice, 聞く is possible in all cases, while 聴く seems more deliberate and is very common with music.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5 (source: N5; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"誰かがトーフグのオフィスに盗聴器を仕掛けたのですが、私達は犯人も理由も分かりません。","romaji":"Dare ka ga Tōfugu no ofisu ni tōchōki o shikaketa no desu ga, watashitachi wa hannin mo riyū mo wakarimasen.","german":"Someone planted a bug in Tofugu's office, but we don't know who did it or why.","furigana":"だれ|とうちょうき|しか|わたしたち|はんにん|りゆう|わ"},{"japanese":"彼は、自分の電話が盗聴されているのではないかと疑っている。","romaji":"Kare wa, jibun no denwa ga tōchō sarete iru no de wa nai ka to utagatte iru.","german":"He suspects that his phone is being tapped.","furigana":"かれ|じぶん|でんわ|とうちょう|うたが"},{"japanese":"他人の会話を盗聴するのは犯罪です。","romaji":"Tanin no kaiwa o tōchō suru no wa hanzai desu.","german":"Eavesdropping on other people's conversations is a crime.","furigana":"たにん|かいわ|とうちょう|はんざい"}],"notes":"盗聴 means \"secret listening\" (literally \"to steal\" + \"to hear\") and, as a する-verb, is transitive: 電話を盗聴する, passive 盗聴される. The listening device, the \"bug\", is called 盗聴器; you plant it with 仕掛ける. To be distinguished are 盗み聞き(する) and 立ち聞き for everyday eavesdropping at a door or wall, and 傍受 for the interception of radio communications by authorities. Formed similarly: 盗撮 \"secret filming\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"部屋には犯罪の証跡はなかったが、警察は殺人事件ではないかと考えていた。","romaji":"Heya ni wa hanzai no shōseki wa nakatta ga, keisatsu wa satsujin jiken de wa nai ka to kangaete ita.","german":"There was no trace of a crime in the room, but the police suspected it was a murder case.","furigana":"へや|はんざい|しょうせき|けいさつ|さつじんじけん|かんが"},{"japanese":"監査に備えて、システムの操作の証跡を残しておく必要がある。","romaji":"Kansa ni sonaete, shisutemu no sousa no shouseki o nokoshite oku hitsuyou ga aru.","german":"In preparation for the audit, system operations need to be kept traceable.","furigana":"かんさ|そな|そうさ|しょうせき|のこ|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"取引の証跡を確認したところ、不正が見つかった。","romaji":"Torihiki no shouseki o kakunin shita tokoro, fusei ga mitsukatta.","german":"When we checked the evidence trail of the transactions, we found irregularities.","furigana":"とりひき|しょうせき|かくにん|ふせい|み"}],"notes":"証跡 is a trace that serves as proof of something. In everyday speech people say 証拠 \"evidence\" or 痕跡 \"trace\" instead; 証跡 is encountered today above all in IT, auditing and accounting, for example 監査証跡 \"audit trail\" or 操作の証跡を残す \"to keep a traceable record of operations\". The word is literary and technical.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"アイツ、彼女のためにかなりお金を遣ったらしい。","romaji":"Aitsu, kanojo no tame ni kanari okane o tsukatta rashii.","german":"That guy apparently spent quite a lot of money on his girlfriend.","furigana":"かのじょ|かね|つか"},{"japanese":"彼女はいつも周りの人に気を遣っている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa itsumo mawari no hito ni ki o tsukatte iru.","german":"She is always considerate of the people around her.","furigana":"かのじょ|まわ|ひと|き|つか"},{"japanese":"面接では、言葉遣いに気をつけたほうがいい。","romaji":"Mensetsu de wa, kotobazukai ni ki o tsuketa hou ga ii.","german":"In an interview, you should be careful about your choice of words.","furigana":"めんせつ|ことばづか|き"}],"notes":"遣う is an alternative spelling of 使う that is used mainly for immaterial things and money: 気を遣う \"to be considerate\", 心遣い \"thoughtfulness\", 言葉遣い \"choice of words\", 金遣い \"way of handling money\", お小遣い \"pocket money\". For tools, devices and methods, 使う is written. With money (お金を遣う) both spellings are possible, 使う being more common.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5 (source: N5; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この子たちは、みんな病気と闘っているんです。","romaji":"Kono kotachi wa, minna byōki to tatakatte iru n desu.","german":"All of these children are fighting illness.","furigana":"こ|びょうき|たたか"},{"japanese":"労働者たちは、賃上げを求めて会社と闘った。","romaji":"Roudoushatachi wa, chin'age o motomete kaisha to tatakatta.","german":"The workers fought the company for a wage increase.","furigana":"ろうどうしゃ|ちんあ|もと|かいしゃ|たたか"},{"japanese":"眠気と闘いながら、最後まで試験勉強を続けた。","romaji":"Nemuke to tatakai nagara, saigo made shiken benkyou o tsuzuketa.","german":"I kept studying for the exam to the end while fighting off sleepiness.","furigana":"ねむけ|たたか|さいご|しけんべんきょう|つづ"}],"notes":"闘う means \"to fight\" in the sense of a tenacious struggle against difficulties: 病気と闘う, 貧困と闘う, 眠気と闘う, also in labor disputes (会社と闘う). 戦う, by contrast, is the usual word for war and competition (敵と戦う, 試合で戦う). The opponent takes と; the noun is 闘い.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その戦闘ロボットは、メラメラと闘志を燃やしていた。","romaji":"Sono sentō robotto wa, meramera to tōshi o moyashite ita.","german":"The combat robot burned with fighting spirit.","furigana":"せんとう|とうし|も"},{"japanese":"決勝戦を前に、選手たちは闘志を燃やしている。","romaji":"Kesshousen o mae ni, senshutachi wa toushi o moyashite iru.","german":"Ahead of the final, the players are burning with fighting spirit.","furigana":"けっしょうせん|まえ|せんしゅ|とうし|も"},{"japanese":"一回戦で負けても、彼の闘志は少しも衰えなかった。","romaji":"Ikkaisen de makete mo, kare no toushi wa sukoshi mo otoroenakatta.","german":"Even after losing in the first round, his fighting spirit did not weaken in the slightest.","furigana":"いっかいせん|ま|かれ|とうし|すこ|おとろ"}],"notes":"闘志 is fighting spirit or the will to fight, especially in sports and in challenges. Typical combinations are 闘志を燃やす \"to burn with fighting spirit\", 闘志がわく, 闘志満々 and 闘志をむき出しにする. Related are 闘争心 (combativeness, rather aggressive) and the more everyday やる気 and 意欲 \"motivation\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"レース前、各陣営は最後の調整に入りました。","romaji":"Rēsu mae, kaku jin'ei wa saigo no chōsei ni hairimashita.","german":"Before the race, each camp began its final adjustments.","furigana":"まえ|かくじんえい|さいご|ちょうせい|はい"},{"japanese":"選挙が近づき、両陣営の争いが激しくなっている。","romaji":"Senkyo ga chikazuki, ryou jin'ei no arasoi ga hageshiku natte iru.","german":"As the election approaches, the fight between the two camps is getting fiercer.","furigana":"せんきょ|ちか|りょうじんえい|あらそ|はげ"},{"japanese":"冷戦時代、世界は東西の二つの陣営に分かれていた。","romaji":"Reisen jidai, sekai wa touzai no futatsu no jin'ei ni wakarete ita.","german":"During the Cold War, the world was divided into two camps, East and West.","furigana":"れいせんじだい|せかい|とうざい|ふた|じんえい|わ"}],"notes":"陣営 originally refers to a military camp; today it mainly means a \"camp\" of supporters: in politics and election campaigns (与党陣営, 〇〇陣営), in the Cold War (東側陣営) or in sports, for example the team around a racehorse or a boxer. Typical are 両陣営, 各陣営 and 相手陣営. For a tent camp or holiday camp, people do not say 陣営 but キャンプ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"学校では香水が禁止されているにもかかわらず、その男子生徒はいつも甘い香りの香水を付けていて、教師たちには自然な脇の臭いだと言っていた。","romaji":"Gakkou de wa kousui ga kinshi sarete iru ni mo kakawarazu, sono danshi seito wa itsumo amai kaori no kousui o tsukete ite, kyoushitachi ni wa shizen na waki no nioi da to itte ita.","german":"Although perfume was banned at school, that boy always wore a sweet-smelling perfume and told the teachers it was his natural underarm smell.","furigana":"がっこう|こうすい|きんし|だんしせいと|あま|かお|こうすい|つ|きょうし|しぜん|わき|にお|い"},{"japanese":"誕生日に、彼女に香水をプレゼントした。","romaji":"Tanjoubi ni, kanojo ni kousui o purezento shita.","german":"I gave my girlfriend perfume for her birthday.","furigana":"たんじょうび|かのじょ|こうすい"},{"japanese":"エレベーターの中で香水の匂いが強すぎて、気分が悪くなった。","romaji":"Erebeetaa no naka de kousui no nioi ga tsuyosugite, kibun ga waruku natta.","german":"The smell of perfume in the elevator was so strong that I felt sick.","furigana":"なか|こうすい|にお|つよ|きぶん|わる"}],"notes":"香水 is perfume. You put it on with 香水をつける (not 着る); too much of it is 香水をつけすぎる. The scent is called 香水の香り or 香水の匂い, though 匂い can also sound negative. Lighter scents are called コロン or オーデコロン; in Japan, strong perfume in the office or in restaurants is often considered impolite.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"景気が回復する兆候が、少しずつ見えてきた。","romaji":"Keiki ga kaifuku suru choukou ga, sukoshi zutsu miete kita.","german":"Signs of an economic recovery are gradually becoming visible.","furigana":"けいき|かいふく|ちょうこう|すこ|み"},{"japanese":"疲れやすいのは病気の兆候かもしれないので、一度検査を受けたほうがいい。","romaji":"Tsukareyasui no wa byouki no choukou kamo shirenai node, ichido kensa o uketa hou ga ii.","german":"Getting tired easily could be a sign of illness, so you should get checked once.","furigana":"つか|びょうき|ちょうこう|いちどけんさ|う"},{"japanese":"噴火の兆候をとらえるため、火山は24時間観測されている。","romaji":"Funka no choukou o toraeru tame, kazan wa nijuuyo jikan kansoku sarete iru.","german":"The volcano is monitored 24 hours a day in order to detect signs of an eruption.","furigana":"ふんか|ちょうこう|かざん|じかんかんそく"}],"notes":"兆候 is a sign that something is developing or about to occur: 景気回復の兆候, 病気の兆候; in medicine it is also written 徴候. Typical are 兆候が見られる/現れる/ある. 前兆, by contrast, emphasizes the omen shortly before an event (often negative), 症状 is an actually present disease symptom, and 兆し (きざし) is a softer, often positive \"sign\" (春の兆し).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この雲と風は嵐の前兆です。","romaji":"Kono kumo to kaze wa arashi no zenchō desu.","german":"These clouds and this wind are a portent of a storm.","furigana":"くも|かぜ|あらし|ぜんちょう"},{"japanese":"大きな地震の前兆として、動物が騒ぐという話がある。","romaji":"Ookina jishin no zenchou to shite, doubutsu ga sawagu to iu hanashi ga aru.","german":"There is a story that animals become restless as a warning sign of a big earthquake.","furigana":"おお|じしん|ぜんちょう|どうぶつ|さわ|はなし"},{"japanese":"カラスがたくさん鳴くのは不吉な前兆だと、祖母はよく言っていた。","romaji":"Karasu ga takusan naku no wa fukitsu na zenchou da to, sobo wa yoku itte ita.","german":"My grandmother often said that a lot of crows cawing is a bad omen.","furigana":"な|ふきつ|ぜんちょう|そぼ|い"}],"notes":"前兆 is an omen or harbinger that immediately precedes a particular event: 地震の前兆, 嵐の前兆, 病気の前兆. Often it concerns ominous things (不吉な前兆 \"bad omen\"), but also neutrally: 前兆現象. In contrast to 兆候 (sign of a development), with 前兆 it is clear what it points to; more elevated and literary is 予兆.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"トーフグの本社は、オレゴン州という、太平洋に臨むアメリカ北西部の州に位置しています。","romaji":"Tōfugu no honsha wa, Oregon-shū to iu, Taiheiyō ni nozomu Amerika hokuseibu no shū ni ichi shite imasu.","german":"Tofugu's headquarters is located in Oregon, a state in the northwestern United States facing the Pacific Ocean.","furigana":"ほんしゃ|しゅう|たいへいよう|のぞ|ほくせいぶ|しゅう|いち"},{"japanese":"選手たちは十分な準備をして決勝戦に臨んだ。","romaji":"Senshu-tachi wa jūbun na junbi o shite kesshōsen ni nozonda.","german":"The players went into the final well prepared.","furigana":"せんしゅ|じゅうぶん|じゅんび|けっしょうせん|のぞ"},{"japanese":"面接に臨む前に、会社についてよく調べておきましょう。","romaji":"Mensetsu ni nozomu mae ni, kaisha ni tsuite yoku shirabete okimashō.","german":"Before you go into the interview, let's research the company thoroughly.","furigana":"めんせつ|のぞ|まえ|かいしゃ|しら"}],"notes":"臨む is an intransitive verb with に and has two main meanings: spatially \"to face, to look out onto\" (海に臨むホテル) and \"to face a situation, to take part in something\" (試合に臨む, 面接に臨む, 本番に臨む). The form 〜に臨んで means \"on the occasion of, in the face of\". Not to be confused with 望む \"to wish, to hope for\" (transitive with を), which is read the same way. The tone is written to elevated; in everyday speech people rather say 〜に向かう or 〜を受ける.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本の学生の多くは、夏に臨海学校に行く事を経験します。","romaji":"Nippon no gakusei no ōku wa, natsu ni rinkai gakkō ni iku koto o keiken shimasu.","german":"Many Japanese students experience going to a seaside school camp in summer.","furigana":"にっぽん|がくせい|おお|なつ|りんかいがっこう|い|こと|けいけん"},{"japanese":"東京の臨海部には大きな公園や展示場が並んでいる。","romaji":"Tōkyō no rinkaibu ni wa ōkina kōen ya tenjijō ga narande iru.","german":"In the coastal area of Tokyo, large parks and exhibition halls stand side by side.","furigana":"とうきょう|りんかいぶ|おお|こうえん|てんじじょう|なら"},{"japanese":"この地域は臨海工業地帯として発展してきた。","romaji":"Kono chiiki wa rinkai kōgyō chitai to shite hatten shite kita.","german":"This area has developed as a coastal industrial zone.","furigana":"ちいき|りんかいこうぎょうちたい|はってん"}],"notes":"臨海 means \"located by the sea, coastal\" and almost always appears as the first element of a compound or with の: 臨海部 \"coastal area\", 臨海公園, 臨海工業地帯 \"coastal industrial zone\". 臨海学校 is a summer school camp at the sea where students learn, among other things, to swim. The word is matter-of-fact and official; in everyday speech people say 海辺 or 海の近く, and for \"along the coast\" also 沿岸.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"台風の影響で、臨時休校になった。","romaji":"Taifū no eikyō de, rinji kyūkō ni natta.","german":"Because of the typhoon, school was closed temporarily.","furigana":"たいふう|えいきょう|りんじきゅうこう"},{"japanese":"年末は臨時のアルバイトを募集しています。","romaji":"Nenmatsu wa rinji no arubaito o boshū shite imasu.","german":"At the end of the year, we are recruiting temporary part-time workers.","furigana":"ねんまつ|りんじ|ぼしゅう"},{"japanese":"花火大会の日は臨時列車が運行されます。","romaji":"Hanabi taikai no hi wa rinji ressha ga unkō saremasu.","german":"On the day of the fireworks festival, special trains will run.","furigana":"はなびたいかい|ひ|りんじれっしゃ|うんこう"}],"notes":"臨時 refers to something that is not regular but set up for a special occasion or temporarily. It usually appears with の or directly before a noun: 臨時休業 \"closed on short notice\", 臨時列車 \"special train\", 臨時ニュース, 臨時収入 \"unexpected extra income\", 臨時のアルバイト. The opposite is 定期 or 定例. In contrast to 一時的 (limited in time), 臨時 emphasizes what is outside the schedule.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"入院したのを契機に、たばこをやめました。","romaji":"Nyūin shita no o keiki ni, tabako o yamemashita.","german":"I quit smoking after being hospitalized.","furigana":"にゅういん|けいき"},{"japanese":"オリンピックを契機として、海外からの観光客が増えた。","romaji":"Orinpikku o keiki to shite, kaigai kara no kankōkyaku ga fueta.","german":"The Olympics led to an increase in tourists from overseas.","furigana":"けいき|かいがい|かんこうきゃく|ふ"},{"japanese":"今回の事故が契機となって、安全対策が見直された。","romaji":"Konkai no jiko ga keiki to natte, anzen taisaku ga minaosareta.","german":"This accident prompted a review of safety measures.","furigana":"こんかい|じこ|けいき|あんぜんたいさく|みなお"}],"notes":"契機 is the \"occasion, trigger\" for a change. Typical patterns: 〜を契機に(して) / 〜を契機として \"on the occasion of, triggered by\" and 〜が契機となる \"to become the trigger\". Synonymous, but colloquial, is きっかけ; 契機 sounds written and suits reports, news and essays. It differs from 機会 \"opportunity\" in that it emphasizes the turning point or trigger of a development.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ジェームズは、鉛筆削りの技術のレベルを上げるという目標を掲げた。","romaji":"Jeemuzu wa, enpitsu kezuri no gijutsu no reberu o ageru to iu mokuhyou o kakageta.","german":"James set himself a new goal: to take his pencil sharpening to the next level.","furigana":"えんぴつけず|ぎじゅつ|あ|もくひょう|かか"},{"japanese":"祝日には門の前に国旗を掲げる家もある。","romaji":"Shukujitsu ni wa mon no mae ni kokki o kakageru ie mo aru.","german":"On national holidays, some families fly the national flag in front of the gate.","furigana":"しゅくじつ|もん|まえ|こっき|かか|いえ"},{"japanese":"優勝した選手はトロフィーを高く掲げた。","romaji":"Yūshō shita senshu wa torofī o takaku kakageta.","german":"The winner held the trophy high in the air.","furigana":"ゆうしょう|せんしゅ|たか|かか"}],"notes":"掲げる literally means \"to hold up, to hoist, to hang up\": 旗を掲げる, 看板を掲げる, トロフィーを掲げる. Very common is the figurative meaning \"to put a goal, ideal or slogan on one's banner\": 目標を掲げる, スローガンを掲げる, 公約を掲げる. It is transitive with を and has no intransitive partner. Unlike 上げる, 掲げる emphasizes that something is made visible to everyone.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"アヤは、レストランの臨時休業の掲示に余白があると、そこに落書きをします。","romaji":"Aya wa, resutoran no rinji kyūgyō no keiji ni yohaku ga aru to, soko ni rakugaki o shimasu.","german":"When there is blank space on a notice about a restaurant's temporary closure, Aya scribbles on it.","furigana":"りんじきゅうぎょう|けいじ|よはく|らくが"},{"japanese":"試験の結果は来週、掲示板に掲示されます。","romaji":"Shiken no kekka wa raishū, keijiban ni keiji saremasu.","german":"The exam results will be posted on the bulletin board next week.","furigana":"しけん|けっか|らいしゅう|けいじばん|けいじ"},{"japanese":"駅の掲示によると、終電の時間が変わったらしい。","romaji":"Eki no keiji ni yoru to, shūden no jikan ga kawatta rashii.","german":"According to the notice at the station, the time of the last train seems to have changed.","furigana":"えき|けいじ|しゅうでん|じかん|か"}],"notes":"掲示 is a public \"notice\" or the \"posting\" of an announcement, for example at a school, station or city hall. As a する-verb it is transitive (お知らせを掲示する), very often in the passive: 掲示板に掲示される. 掲示板 is the \"bulletin board\" and on the internet also a \"forum.\" Colloquially, a pasted slip of paper is 張り紙; 掲載, by contrast, is publication in a newspaper, magazine or on the web.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今月号掲載の記事はとても面白いね。","romaji":"Kongetsugō keisai no kiji wa totemo omoshiroi ne.","german":"The article in this month's issue is very interesting, isn't it?","furigana":"こんげつごうけいさい|きじ|おもしろ"},{"japanese":"私の書いた投書が新聞に掲載された。","romaji":"Watashi no kaita tōsho ga shinbun ni keisai sareta.","german":"My letter to the editor was printed in the newspaper.","furigana":"わたし|か|とうしょ|しんぶん|けいさい"},{"japanese":"このサイトの写真を無断で掲載することは禁止されています。","romaji":"Kono saito no shashin o mudan de keisai suru koto wa kinshi sarete imasu.","german":"Republishing photos from this website without permission is prohibited.","furigana":"しゃしん|むだん|けいさい|きんし"}],"notes":"掲載 is the \"printing, publishing\" of a text, photo or advertisement in a newspaper, magazine or on a website. Typical: 新聞に掲載される, 記事を掲載する, 無断掲載禁止 \"printing without permission prohibited\", 〜号掲載の記事. Publishing a whole book, by contrast, is 出版 or 刊行; a notice posted on a wall is 掲示. As a suru-verb 掲載 is transitive, often in the passive.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"男は政治家を狙撃するために、そのホテルの部屋が見える場所までやってきた。","romaji":"Otoko wa seijika o sogeki suru tame ni, sono hoteru no heya ga mieru basho made yatte kita.","german":"The man came to a place with a view of the hotel room in order to snipe at the politician.","furigana":"おとこ|せいじか|そげき|へや|み|ばしょ"},{"japanese":"大統領が演説中に狙撃される事件が起きた。","romaji":"Daitōryō ga enzetsu-chū ni sogeki sareru jiken ga okita.","german":"An incident occurred in which the president was shot at during a speech.","furigana":"だいとうりょう|えんぜつちゅう|そげき|じけん|お"},{"japanese":"彼は軍で狙撃の訓練を受けた。","romaji":"Kare wa gun de sogeki no kunren o uketa.","german":"He received sniper training in the military.","furigana":"かれ|ぐん|そげき|くんれん|う"}],"notes":"狙撃 is composed of 狙う \"to aim\" and 撃つ \"to shoot\" and refers to an aimed shot from ambush or from a distance, typically at a specific person. As a transitive する-verb: 〜を狙撃する, in the news mostly in the passive: 〜が狙撃される. The shooter is called 狙撃手 or スナイパー. The word belongs to news and military vocabulary; the general \"to shoot\" is 撃つ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その事件の真相が闇に葬られるのではないかと心配しています。","romaji":"Sono jiken no shinsou ga yami ni houmurareru no de wa nai ka to shinpai shite imasu.","german":"I'm worried that the truth about the incident might be covered up.","furigana":"じけん|しんそう|やみ|ほうむ|しんぱい"},{"japanese":"祖父は故郷の墓に葬られた。","romaji":"Sofu wa kokyō no haka ni hōmurareta.","german":"My grandfather was laid to rest in the family grave in his hometown.","furigana":"そふ|こきょう|はか|ほうむ"},{"japanese":"そのスキャンダルで彼の政治生命は葬られた。","romaji":"Sono sukyandaru de kare no seiji seimei wa hōmurareta.","german":"The scandal ended his political career.","furigana":"かれ|せいじせいめい|ほうむ"}],"notes":"葬る first means \"to bury, to inter\" (墓に葬る), but today it is especially common figuratively, \"to cover up, to sweep under the rug\": 真相が闇に葬られる \"the truth is covered up\". Also \"to finish off, to get out of the way\": 政治生命が葬られる. Mostly in the passive; 葬り去る strengthens it. The neutral word for \"to bury\" is 埋める, the formal burial is 埋葬する.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"火葬のシステムは自動的に停止する訳ではありません。係の人が小窓から骨の様子を見ながら、終わったと思う時に止めるのです。","romaji":"Kasō no shisutemu wa jidōteki ni teishi suru wake de wa arimasen. Kakari no hito ga komado kara hone no yōsu o minagara, owatta to omou toki ni tomeru no desu.","german":"The cremation system does not stop automatically. The person in charge checks the condition of the bones through a small window and stops it when he thinks it's done.","furigana":"かそう|じどうてき|ていし|わけ|かかり|ひと|こまど|ほね|ようす|み|お|おも|とき|と"},{"japanese":"日本では、亡くなった人のほとんどが火葬される。","romaji":"Nihon de wa, nakunatta hito no hotondo ga kasō sareru.","german":"In Japan, almost all of the deceased are cremated.","furigana":"にほん|な|ひと|かそう"},{"japanese":"葬式の後、家族で火葬場へ向かった。","romaji":"Sōshiki no ato, kazoku de kasōba e mukatta.","german":"After the funeral, the family headed to the crematorium together.","furigana":"そうしき|あと|かぞく|かそうば|む"}],"notes":"火葬 is \"burial by fire, cremation\"; as a transitive する-verb it is mostly used in the passive: 火葬される. The counterpart is 土葬 \"burial in the ground\", and the place is called 火葬場 \"crematorium\". In Japan, cremation is practically the rule; afterward the relatives use chopsticks to place the bones in the urn (骨壺). The word is matter-of-fact and neutral and belongs to the word field around 葬式 \"funeral\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この公園は地元では犯罪の温床になっていることで知られているので、近づかない方がいいですよ。","romaji":"Kono kouen wa jimoto de wa hanzai no onshou ni natte iru koto de shirarete iru node, chikazukanai hou ga ii desu yo.","german":"This park is known locally as a hotbed of crime, so you'd better stay away.","furigana":"こうえん|じもと|はんざい|おんしょう|し|ちか|ほう"},{"japanese":"古い習慣が不正の温床になっていると指摘された。","romaji":"Furui shūkan ga fusei no onshō ni natte iru to shiteki sareta.","german":"It was pointed out that old customs have become a breeding ground for wrongdoing.","furigana":"ふる|しゅうかん|ふせい|おんしょう|してき"},{"japanese":"湿った台所はカビや細菌の温床になりやすい。","romaji":"Shimetta daidokoro wa kabi ya saikin no onshō ni nariyasui.","german":"A damp kitchen easily becomes a breeding ground for mold and bacteria.","furigana":"しめ|だいどころ|さいきん|おんしょう"}],"notes":"温床 literally means \"hotbed\" (a heated bed for seedlings), but it is used almost only figuratively: \"breeding ground, hotbed\" for something negative. Fixed patterns: 〜の温床になる / 〜の温床となる, for example 犯罪の温床, 不正の温床, 細菌の温床. It is not used for positive developments. The tone is rather written, as in newspaper reports.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お肌の弾力が無くなってきているの。","romaji":"O hada no danryoku ga nakunatte kite iru no.","german":"The elasticity of my skin is declining.","furigana":"はだ|だんりょく|な"},{"japanese":"このソファーはクッションの弾力がちょうどいい。","romaji":"Kono sofā wa kusshon no danryoku ga chōdo ii.","german":"The cushions of this sofa are just right in their springiness.","furigana":"だんりょく"},{"japanese":"規則をもう少し弾力的に考えてもいいのではないか。","romaji":"Kisoku o mō sukoshi danryokuteki ni kangaete mo ii no de wa nai ka.","german":"Shouldn't the rules be applied a bit more flexibly?","furigana":"きそく|すこ|だんりょくてき|かんが"}],"notes":"弾力 is the \"elasticity, resilience\" of a material or body: 弾力がある, 弾力が失われる, 肌の弾力, ゴムの弾力. Figuratively it means \"flexibility\", above all in 弾力的 \"flexible, adaptable\" (規則を弾力的に運用する). The technical term in physics is 弾性; for a person's mental flexibility one rather says 柔軟.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"最近、ケールの串焼きにはまっています。","romaji":"Saikin, kēru no kushiyaki ni hamatte imasu.","german":"Lately I'm hooked on grilled kale skewers.","furigana":"さいきん|くしや"},{"japanese":"居酒屋で串焼きを何本か注文した。","romaji":"Izakaya de kushiyaki o nanbon ka chūmon shita.","german":"At the izakaya, we ordered a few grilled skewers.","furigana":"いざかや|くしや|なんぼん|ちゅうもん"},{"japanese":"お祭りの屋台でイカの串焼きを買った。","romaji":"Omatsuri no yatai de ika no kushiyaki o katta.","german":"I bought a grilled squid skewer at a food stall at the festival.","furigana":"まつ|やたい|くしや|か"}],"notes":"串焼き is anything grilled on a skewer (串): meat, fish, vegetables. It is counted with 本 (串焼きを三本). 焼き鳥 is the best-known subgroup (chicken), while 串カツ and 串揚げ are deep-fried skewers. Typical places are 居酒屋 and festival stalls (屋台).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"姉ちゃん、抱っこして！","romaji":"Nēchan, dakko shite!","german":"Big sister, pick me up!","furigana":"ねえ|だ"},{"japanese":"赤ちゃんを抱っこしたまま買い物をするのは大変だ。","romaji":"Akachan o dakko shita mama kaimono o suru no wa taihen da.","german":"Shopping with a baby in your arms is exhausting.","furigana":"あか|だ|か|もの|たいへん"},{"japanese":"うちの猫は抱っこされるのが嫌いです。","romaji":"Uchi no neko wa dakko sareru no ga kirai desu.","german":"Our cat doesn't like being picked up.","furigana":"ねこ|だ|きら"}],"notes":"抱っこ is the child-language or familiar word for \"to pick up, to hold in one's arms\", especially with babies, small children and pets: 赤ちゃんを抱っこする. Children simply call out 抱っこ！ or 抱っこして！ \"Pick me up!\". The neutral verb is 抱く (also figuratively: 夢を抱く). Carrying piggyback on the back is おんぶ; the baby carrier is called 抱っこひも.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"唾液は食べ物を消化するのを助けます。","romaji":"Daeki wa tabemono o shōka suru no o tasukemasu.","german":"Saliva helps digest food.","furigana":"だえき|た|もの|しょうか|たす"},{"japanese":"緊張すると唾液が出にくくなる。","romaji":"Kinchō suru to daeki ga denikuku naru.","german":"When you are nervous, less saliva is produced.","furigana":"きんちょう|だえき|で"},{"japanese":"唾液を使ってウイルスの検査をした。","romaji":"Daeki o tsukatte uirusu no kensa o shita.","german":"I took a virus test using saliva.","furigana":"だえき|つか|けんさ"}],"notes":"唾液 is the matter-of-fact, medical word for \"saliva\" and appears in biology, medicine and health reports: 唾液が出る, 唾液の分泌, 唾液検査, 唾液腺 \"salivary gland\". In everyday speech people say つば; saliva running out, for example while sleeping, is よだれ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は緊張して唾がうまく飲み込めなかった。","romaji":"Kare wa kinchō shite tsuba ga umaku nomikomenakatta.","german":"He was so nervous that he could hardly swallow his saliva.","furigana":"かれ|きんちょう|つば|の|こ"},{"japanese":"道に唾を吐くのはマナー違反だ。","romaji":"Michi ni tsuba o haku no wa manā ihan da.","german":"Spitting on the street is bad manners.","furigana":"みち|つば|は|いはん"},{"japanese":"祖父は指に唾をつけて新聞をめくる。","romaji":"Sofu wa yubi ni tsuba o tsukete shinbun o mekuru.","german":"My grandfather wets his finger with spit and turns the newspaper page.","furigana":"そふ|ゆび|つば|しんぶん"}],"notes":"唾 (つば) is the everyday word for \"spit, saliva\"; the technical counterpart is 唾液. Typical expressions: 唾を吐く \"to spit\", 唾を飲み込む \"to swallow\", 生唾を飲む \"to lick one's lips\" (from greed or tension). Idiomatically, 唾をつける (つばをつけておく) also means \"to reserve something for oneself, to stake a claim\". It is often written in kana つば.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"パンがカバンの中で潰れていた。","romaji":"Pan ga kaban no naka de tsuburete ita.","german":"The bread was squashed in my bag.","furigana":"なか|つぶ"},{"japanese":"駅前の本屋が去年潰れてしまった。","romaji":"Ekimae no hon'ya ga kyonen tsuburete shimatta.","german":"The bookstore in front of the station went out of business last year.","furigana":"えきまえ|ほんや|きょねんつぶ"},{"japanese":"カラオケで歌いすぎて声が潰れた。","romaji":"Karaoke de utaisugite koe ga tsubureta.","german":"I sang so much at karaoke that my voice gave out.","furigana":"うた|こえ|つぶ"}],"notes":"潰れる is intransitive and means \"to be crushed, to be flattened\" (箱が潰れる). It is also very common in a figurative sense: businesses \"go bankrupt\" (会社が潰れる), plans \"fall through\" (予定が潰れる), the voice \"gives out\" (声が潰れる) and time \"is used up\" (休みが潰れる). The transitive partner is 潰す \"to crush, to ruin\", also 時間を潰す \"to kill time\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この会社は対外取引に力を入れています。","romaji":"Kono kaisha wa taigai torihiki ni chikara o irete imasu.","german":"This company focuses on foreign trade.","furigana":"かいしゃ|たいがいとりひき|ちから|い"},{"japanese":"政府は対外政策を見直すことにした。","romaji":"Seifu wa taigai seisaku o minaosu koto ni shita.","german":"The government decided to review its foreign policy.","furigana":"せいふ|たいがいせいさく|みなお"},{"japanese":"社内の問題を対外的に説明するのは難しい。","romaji":"Shanai no mondai o taigaiteki ni setsumei suru no wa muzukashii.","german":"It is difficult to explain internal company problems to the outside.","furigana":"しゃない|もんだい|たいがいてき|せつめい|むずか"}],"notes":"対外 means \"toward the outside, toward foreign countries\" and almost always stands before another noun: 対外政策 \"foreign policy\", 対外貿易, 対外関係, 対外援助. The derivation 対外的 (に/な) means more generally \"outwardly\", for example toward the public or other companies. The opposite is 対内 \"inward\". Freely with の (対外のビジネス) it sounds unnatural; people use the compounds.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"犯人は駅に時限爆弾を仕掛けた。","romaji":"Hannin wa eki ni jigen bakudan o shikaketa.","german":"The culprit planted a time bomb at the station.","furigana":"はんにん|えき|じげんばくだん|しか"},{"japanese":"月曜日の一時限目は数学です。","romaji":"Getsuyōbi no ichijigenme wa sūgaku desu.","german":"On Mondays, first period is math.","furigana":"げつようび|いちじげんめ|すうがく"},{"japanese":"この法律は五年間の時限立法だ。","romaji":"Kono hōritsu wa gonenkan no jigen rippō da.","german":"This law is a time-limited law for five years.","furigana":"ほうりつ|ごねんかん|じげんりっぽう"}],"notes":"時限 has two meanings: at school and university the \"class period\" (一時限目 \"first period\", synonymous with the more casual 一時間目) and, generally, a \"time limit\". The second meaning is found in compounds such as 時限爆弾 \"time bomb\" and 時限立法 \"sunset law, time-limited legislation\". A deadline by which something must be done, by contrast, is 期限.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「彼にあんなひどいこと言わなければよかったわ。」「そうね。でも、言ってしまったことはもう元には戻せないわ。」","romaji":"\"Kare ni anna hidoi koto iwanakereba yokatta wa.\" \"Sou ne. Demo, itte shimatta koto wa mou moto ni wa modosenai wa.\"","german":"\"I wish I hadn't said such a terrible thing to him.\" \"I know. But what's been said can't be taken back.\"","furigana":"かれ|い|い|もと|もど"},{"japanese":"読み終わった本は元の場所に戻してください。","romaji":"Yomiowatta hon wa moto no basho ni modoshite kudasai.","german":"Please put the books you've finished reading back in their original place.","furigana":"よ|お|ほん|もと|ばしょ|もど"},{"japanese":"干ししいたけを水で戻してから料理に使う。","romaji":"Hoshi-shiitake o mizu de modoshite kara ryōri ni tsukau.","german":"Rehydrate the dried shiitake in water before using them in cooking.","furigana":"ほ|みず|もど|りょうり|つか"}],"notes":"戻す is transitive: \"to put something back in its place, return it to its former state\" (元の場所に戻す, 元に戻す, 時計の針を戻す). In cooking it means \"to soak, rehydrate\" (乾物を水で戻す), and colloquially also \"to vomit.\" The intransitive partner is 戻る \"to return.\" For giving something back to a person, 返す is more usual (本を返す).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"三百人以上の人がその貿易会社の職に応募したが、誰も採用されなかったそうだよ。","romaji":"Sanbyakunin ijou no hito ga sono bouekigaisha no shoku ni oubo shita ga, dare mo saiyou sarenakatta sou da yo.","german":"More than 300 people applied for a position at that trading company, but apparently no one was hired.","furigana":"さんびゃくにんいじょう|ひと|ぼうえきがいしゃ|しょく|おうぼ|だれ|さいよう"},{"japanese":"父は貿易会社で働いていて、海外出張が多い。","romaji":"Chichi wa bōekigaisha de hataraite ite, kaigai shutchō ga ōi.","german":"My father works at a trading company and often travels abroad on business.","furigana":"ちち|ぼうえきがいしゃ|はたら|かいがいしゅっちょう|おお"},{"japanese":"卒業後は貿易会社に就職して、英語を生かしたい。","romaji":"Sotsugyōgo wa bōekigaisha ni shūshoku shite, Eigo o ikashitai.","german":"After graduating, I want to get a job at a trading company and make use of my English.","furigana":"そつぎょうご|ぼうえきがいしゃ|しゅうしょく|えいご|い"}],"notes":"貿易会社 is a company for import and export. Note the voicing: かいしゃ becomes がいしゃ in the compound (ぼうえきがいしゃ). Large Japanese trading houses that operate in many industries are called 商社 or 総合商社; 貿易会社 is the more general, matter-of-fact term and is also used for smaller firms.","levelNote":"Compound: 貿易 (N4) + 会社 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私が奈良県に住んでいる時は、彼女は親友の一人でしたが、私が大阪に引っ越してからは疎遠になりつつあります。","romaji":"Watashi ga Naraken ni sunde iru toki wa, kanojo wa shin'yū no hitori deshita ga, watashi ga Ōsaka ni hikkoshite kara wa soen ni naritsutsu arimasu.","german":"She was one of my best friends when I lived in Nara Prefecture, but since I moved to Osaka we have been drifting apart.","furigana":"わたし|ならけん|す|とき|かのじょ|しんゆう|ひとり|わたし|おおさか|ひ|こ|そえん"},{"japanese":"奈良県には、古いお寺や神社がたくさんある。","romaji":"Naraken ni wa, furui otera ya jinja ga takusan aru.","german":"In Nara Prefecture there are many old temples and shrines.","furigana":"ならけん|ふる|てら|じんじゃ"},{"japanese":"奈良県は海に面していない県の一つです。","romaji":"Naraken wa umi ni men shite inai ken no hitotsu desu.","german":"Nara is one of the prefectures that does not border the sea.","furigana":"ならけん|うみ|めん|けん|ひと"}],"notes":"奈良県 is in the 近畿 (Kansai) region; the administrative seat is 奈良市. The prefecture is known for the ancient capital 平城京 of the 奈良時代 (710-794), the 東大寺 with the Great Buddha and the tame deer in 奈良公園. 奈良 alone can mean the city or the prefecture; with 県 it is unambiguous. Nara is one of the prefectures without a sea coast.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新宿駅で中央線から山手線に乗り換えて上野駅まで来てください。","romaji":"Shinjuku-eki de Chūōsen kara Yamanotesen ni norikaete Ueno-eki made kite kudasai.","german":"Please change from the Chuo Line to the Yamanote Line at Shinjuku Station and come as far as Ueno Station.","furigana":"しんじゅくえき|ちゅうおうせん||の|か|うえのえき|き"},{"japanese":"次の駅で地下鉄に乗り換えます。","romaji":"Tsugi no eki de chikatetsu ni norikaemasu.","german":"I will transfer to the subway at the next station.","furigana":"つぎ|えき|ちかてつ|の|か"},{"japanese":"安いので、新しい携帯会社に乗り換えました。","romaji":"Yasui node, atarashii keitai gaisha ni norikaemashita.","german":"It was cheaper, so I switched to a new mobile carrier.","furigana":"やす|あたら|けいたいがいしゃ|の|か"}],"notes":"乗り換える means \"to transfer\": the transfer station takes で, the new line に, the old one から: 新宿で山手線に乗り換える. The noun 乗り換え is \"transfer\" (乗り換えが便利だ). Figuratively it means \"to switch\", for example to another provider: 新しい携帯会社に乗り換える. It is built from 乗る \"to board\" and 換える \"to exchange\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"うちの娘は沖縄が大好きで、一体いつ戻ってくるのかさっぱり分かりません。","romaji":"Uchi no musume wa Okinawa ga daisuki de, ittai itsu modotte kuru no ka sappari wakarimasen.","german":"My daughter loves Okinawa so much that I have no idea when she will come back.","furigana":"むすめ|おきなわ|だいす|いったい|もど|わ"},{"japanese":"沖縄は一年中暖かくて、海がとてもきれいです。","romaji":"Okinawa wa ichinenjū atatakakute, umi ga totemo kirei desu.","german":"Okinawa is warm all year round, and the sea is very beautiful.","furigana":"おきなわ|いちねんじゅうあたた|うみ"},{"japanese":"夏休みに家族で沖縄へ旅行に行きました。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi ni kazoku de Okinawa e ryokō ni ikimashita.","german":"During summer vacation, we traveled to Okinawa with the family.","furigana":"なつやす|かぞく|おきなわ|りょこう|い"}],"notes":"沖縄 is Japan's southernmost prefecture (沖縄県), a subtropical island chain, and also the name of its main island (沖縄本島). Until the 19th century, the Kingdom of 琉球 stood here, and its culture lives on in music, cuisine (沖縄料理) and dialect. You go \"to Okinawa\" with へ or に: 沖縄へ行く.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"蛍光灯は明治維新の頃にはまだ無かったんだよね。","romaji":"Keikoutou wa Meiji Ishin no koro ni wa mada nakatta n da yo ne.","german":"Fluorescent lights didn't exist yet at the time of the Meiji Restoration, right?","furigana":"けいこうとう|めいじいしん|ころ|な"},{"japanese":"明治維新によって、日本は近代国家への道を歩み始めた。","romaji":"Meiji Ishin ni yotte, Nihon wa kindai kokka e no michi o ayumihajimeta.","german":"With the Meiji Restoration, Japan set out on the path to becoming a modern nation.","furigana":"めいじいしん|にほん|きんだいこっか|みち|あゆ|はじ"},{"japanese":"明治維新から百五十年以上がたった。","romaji":"Meiji Ishin kara hyakugojūnen ijō ga tatta.","german":"More than 150 years have passed since the Meiji Restoration.","furigana":"めいじいしん|ひゃくごじゅうねんいじょう"}],"notes":"明治維新 refers to the political upheaval around 1868 in which the Tokugawa shogunate ended, power formally returned to the emperor and Japan modernized rapidly. 維新 means \"renewal\"; the word appears almost only in historical contexts, for example 明治維新の頃, 明治維新以後. The period before is called 江戸時代, the period after 明治時代.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今回の舞踏会には、前回よりも明るい色のドレスを着て行くつもりです。","romaji":"Konkai no butōkai ni wa, zenkai yori mo akarui iro no doresu o kite iku tsumori desu.","german":"For this ball, I intend to wear a dress in a brighter color than last time.","furigana":"こんかい|ぶとうかい|ぜんかい|あか|いろ|き|い"},{"japanese":"シンデレラは魔法の力で王子様の舞踏会に出かけた。","romaji":"Shinderera wa mahō no chikara de ōjisama no butōkai ni dekaketa.","german":"With the power of magic, Cinderella went to the prince's ball.","furigana":"まほう|ちから|おうじさま|ぶとうかい|で"},{"japanese":"ウィーンでは冬になると多くの舞踏会が開かれる。","romaji":"Wīn de wa fuyu ni naru to ōku no butōkai ga hirakareru.","german":"In Vienna, many balls are held when winter comes.","furigana":"ふゆ|おお|ぶとうかい|ひら"}],"notes":"舞踏会 is a formal Western-style \"ball\" with ballroom dancing, as known from fairy tales or Vienna: 舞踏会に招かれる, 舞踏会を開く, 仮面舞踏会 \"masked ball\". The word sounds elegant to old-fashioned; a casual dance event is rather a ダンスパーティー. 舞踏 alone can also refer to the dance style Butoh; traditional Japanese dance is called 日本舞踊.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今年の成績は全般的に良かった。","romaji":"Kotoshi no seiseki wa zenpanteki ni yokatta.","german":"This year's grades were good overall.","furigana":"ことし|せいせき|ぜんぱんてき|よ"},{"japanese":"景気は全般的に回復しつつある。","romaji":"Keiki wa zenpanteki ni kaifuku shitsutsu aru.","german":"The economy is recovering across the board.","furigana":"けいき|ぜんぱんてき|かいふく"},{"japanese":"全般的な傾向として、若者の新聞離れが進んでいる。","romaji":"Zenpanteki na keikō to shite, wakamono no shinbun-banare ga susunde iru.","german":"As a general trend, young people are reading newspapers less and less.","furigana":"ぜんぱんてき|けいこう|わかもの|しんぶんばな|すす"}],"notes":"全般的 means \"overall, on the whole, across the board\", that is, relating to all parts of an area: 全般的に良い, 全般的な傾向. It is a な-adjective; adverbially 全般的に. Not to be confused with 一般的 \"generally common, typical\": statements like \"dogs generally fear cats\" require 一般的に. The base word 全般 is used as a suffix: 生活全般 \"everyday life as a whole\".","levelNote":"Compound: 全般 (N2) + 的 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コウイチはビエトに窓を開けるよう頼みましたが、ビエトはコウイチの頼みを拒絶しました。","romaji":"Kouichi wa Bieto ni mado o akeru you tanomimashita ga, Bieto wa Kouichi no tanomi o kyozetsu shimashita.","german":"Koichi asked Viet to open the window, but Viet refused Koichi's request.","furigana":"まど|あ|たの|たの|きょぜつ"},{"japanese":"ちょっと頼みがあるんだけど、聞いてくれる？","romaji":"Chotto tanomi ga aru n da kedo, kiite kureru?","german":"I have a small favor to ask - will you help me?","furigana":"たの|き"},{"japanese":"道に迷った今、このスマホの地図だけが頼みの綱だ。","romaji":"Michi ni mayotta ima, kono sumaho no chizu dake ga tanomi no tsuna da.","german":"Now that I'm lost, the map on my phone is my only salvation.","furigana":"みち|まよ|いま|ちず|たの|つな"}],"notes":"頼み is the noun of 頼む and means, on the one hand, \"request, favor\": 頼みがある, 頼みを聞く \"to grant a request\", 頼みを断る. On the other hand it means \"reliance, hope\", above all in 頼みの綱 \"last resort\". In conversation お願い sounds more polite and is more common; the formal, business word is 依頼.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"もう少し我慢すれば、依頼人が誰だか分かったのに。","romaji":"Mou sukoshi gaman sureba, irainin ga dare da ka wakatta noni.","german":"If you had been a bit more patient, you could have found out who the client was.","furigana":"すこ|がまん|いらいにん|だれ|わ"},{"japanese":"弁護士は依頼人の秘密を守らなければならない。","romaji":"Bengoshi wa irainin no himitsu o mamoranakereba naranai.","german":"A lawyer must protect the secrets of his client.","furigana":"べんごし|いらいにん|ひみつ|まも"},{"japanese":"探偵は依頼人から夫の浮気調査を頼まれた。","romaji":"Tantei wa irainin kara otto no uwaki chōsa o tanomareta.","german":"The client asked the detective to investigate her husband's infidelity.","furigana":"たんてい|いらいにん|おっと|うわきちょうさ|たの"}],"notes":"依頼人 is the \"client, person who commissions\", that is, someone who gives a task to a professional, typically a lawyer (弁護士), detective (探偵) or real estate agent. Synonymous, but somewhat more neutral, is 依頼者. In business, people rather say 顧客, お客様 or クライアント for customers. Note the reading いらいにん, not \"-じん\".","levelNote":"Compound: 依頼 (N3) + 人 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"全く馬鹿らしいにもほどがある！！！","romaji":"Mattaku bakarashii ni mo hodo ga aru!!!","german":"This is absurd beyond all limits!!!","furigana":"まった|ばか"},{"japanese":"こんなことで喧嘩するなんて馬鹿らしい。","romaji":"Konna koto de kenka suru nante bakarashii.","german":"It's ridiculous to fight over something like this.","furigana":"けんか|ばか"},{"japanese":"一時間も並んだのに売り切れなんて、馬鹿らしくてやってられない。","romaji":"Ichijikan mo naranda noni urikire nante, bakarashikute yatterarenai.","german":"I queued for a whole hour and then it was sold out - that's too ridiculous to put up with.","furigana":"いちじかん|なら|う|き|ばか"}],"notes":"馬鹿らしい is an い-adjective meaning \"ridiculous, absurd, not worth the effort\"; it often expresses annoyance or resignation: 〜なんて馬鹿らしい, 馬鹿らしくてやってられない \"this is too stupid for me\". Stronger and more emotional is 馬鹿馬鹿しい; くだらない emphasizes \"worthless, silly\". It is often written in kana too (ばからしい). Colloquial; use with caution in polite speech.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新生児にとっての必需品って何だろう。","romaji":"Shinseiji ni totte no hitsujuhin tte nan darou.","german":"I wonder what the essentials are for a newborn.","furigana":"しんせいじ|ひつじゅひん|なん"},{"japanese":"今やスマートフォンは生活必需品だ。","romaji":"Ima ya sumātofon wa seikatsu hitsujuhin da.","german":"Smartphones are now a daily necessity.","furigana":"いま|せいかつひつじゅひん"},{"japanese":"キャンプに行く前に必需品のリストを作った。","romaji":"Kyanpu ni iku mae ni hitsujuhin no risuto o tsukutta.","german":"Before going camping, I made a list of the essentials.","furigana":"い|まえ|ひつじゅひん|つく"}],"notes":"必需品 are \"indispensable things\" that you cannot do without: 生活必需品 \"daily necessities\" (food, hygiene products), 旅行の必需品, 〜にとっての必需品. In contrast to 日用品 \"household goods\", 必需品 emphasizes indispensability. In conversation people often simply say 必要なもの or 欠かせないもの.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"高齢者たちは、雇用について難しい問題を抱えています。","romaji":"Koureisha-tachi wa, koyou ni tsuite muzukashii mondai o kakaete imasu.","german":"Elderly people face difficult problems regarding employment.","furigana":"こうれいしゃ|こよう|むずか|もんだい|かか"},{"japanese":"電車で高齢者に席を譲った。","romaji":"Densha de kōreisha ni seki o yuzutta.","german":"I gave up my seat to an elderly person on the train.","furigana":"でんしゃ|こうれいしゃ|せき|ゆず"},{"japanese":"この町では一人暮らしの高齢者が増えている。","romaji":"Kono machi de wa hitorigurashi no kōreisha ga fuete iru.","german":"The number of elderly people living alone is increasing in this town.","furigana":"まち|ひとりぐ|こうれいしゃ|ふ"}],"notes":"高齢者 is the matter-of-fact, official term for \"elderly people, seniors\" (in statistics usually from age 65): 高齢者福祉, 一人暮らしの高齢者, 高齢者向け. In personal conversation, お年寄り sounds friendlier; 老人 can come across as blunt to derogatory. Related are 高齢 \"advanced age\" and 高齢化 \"population aging\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本の老齢年金の仕組みについて説明していただけませんか。","romaji":"Nihon no rōrei nenkin no shikumi ni tsuite setsumei shite itadakemasen ka.","german":"Could you explain how the old-age pension system in Japan works?","furigana":"にほん|ろうれいねんきん|しく|せつめい"},{"japanese":"祖父は老齢のため、仕事を引退した。","romaji":"Sofu wa rōrei no tame, shigoto o intai shita.","german":"My grandfather retired from work because of old age.","furigana":"そふ|ろうれい|しごと|いんたい"},{"japanese":"老齢の犬には、柔らかいえさを与えています。","romaji":"Rōrei no inu ni wa, yawarakai esa o ataete imasu.","german":"We feed our old dog soft food.","furigana":"ろうれい|いぬ|やわ|あた"}],"notes":"老齢 means \"advanced age, old age\" and is written and official. It appears above all in compounds such as 老齢年金 \"old-age pension\" and 老齢化, as well as with の or as a reason: 老齢の犬, 老齢のため. Today 高齢 is much more common (高齢者, 高齢化); 老齢 sounds a bit more old-fashioned and emphasizes aging itself.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ここにカバンを置かせてもらっても宜しいですか？","romaji":"Koko ni kaban o okasete moratte mo yoroshii desu ka?","german":"Is it all right if I put my bag down here?","furigana":"お|よろ"},{"japanese":"よろしければ、こちらの席へどうぞ。","romaji":"Yoroshikereba, kochira no seki e douzo.","german":"If you like, please have a seat here.","furigana":"せき"},{"japanese":"先生は「よろしい。では、始めましょう。」と言いました。","romaji":"Sensei wa \"Yoroshii. Dewa, hajimemashou.\" to iimashita.","german":"The teacher said, \"Very well. Then let's begin.\"","furigana":"せんせい|はじ|い"}],"notes":"宜しい is the polite, formal equivalent of いい/よい \"good, all right\". It is typical in questions asking for permission: ～てもよろしいですか \"may I ...?\", and in よろしければ \"if you like\". From the mouth of a superior, よろしい alone means \"Good, agreed\". Not to be confused with よろしく(お願いします), the adverb form in the greeting formula. Mostly written in kana as よろしい.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"おっと。すみません。トランプを繰るのが苦手なんです。","romaji":"Otto. Sumimasen. Toranpu o kuru no ga nigate na n desu.","german":"Oops. Sorry. I'm bad at shuffling cards.","furigana":"く|にがて"},{"japanese":"彼は辞書のページを繰りながら、その言葉の意味を調べた。","romaji":"Kare wa jisho no pēji o kurinagara, sono kotoba no imi o shirabeta.","german":"Turning the pages of the dictionary, he looked up the meaning of the word.","furigana":"かれ|じしょ|く|ことば|いみ|しら"},{"japanese":"祖母は糸車で糸を繰っていた。","romaji":"Sobo wa itoguruma de ito o kutte ita.","german":"My grandmother was reeling thread on a spinning wheel.","furigana":"そぼ|いとぐるま|いと|く"}],"notes":"繰る is a rare, rather written verb for repeated hand movements: \"to turn\" pages (ページを繰る, in everyday speech rather めくる), \"to wind up\" thread (糸を繰る), \"to shuffle\" playing cards (トランプを繰る) and \"to count off\" days. It lives on mainly in compounds: 繰り返す, 繰り上げる, 繰り越す. Caution: 繰る is a godan verb (繰って, 繰らない) and should not be confused with 来る (来て, 来ない).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今年の新年の抱負の一つは、ちゃんと避妊をすること、です。","romaji":"Kotoshi no shinnen no hōfu no hitotsu wa, chanto hinin o suru koto, desu.","german":"One of my New Year's resolutions this year is to use contraception properly.","furigana":"ことし|しんねん|ほうふ|ひと|ひにん"},{"japanese":"避妊の方法について医師に相談した。","romaji":"Hinin no hōhō ni tsuite ishi ni sōdan shita.","german":"I consulted a doctor about contraception methods.","furigana":"ひにん|ほうほう|いし|そうだん"},{"japanese":"飼い猫に避妊手術を受けさせた。","romaji":"Kaineko ni hinin shujutsu o ukesaseta.","german":"We had our pet cat spayed.","furigana":"か|ねこ|ひにんしゅじゅつ|う"}],"notes":"避妊 is \"contraception\"; as a する-verb it is intransitive: 避妊する \"to use contraception\", often with を: 避妊をする. Typical compounds: 避妊具 \"contraceptive\", 避妊薬 or ピル, 避妊手術 \"sterilization\" (for pets, for females; for males people say 去勢). Matter-of-fact medical tone, as in sex education and counseling.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は尿で妊娠反応検査をして、お医者さんから妊娠していると言われました。","romaji":"Kanojo wa nyou de ninshin hannou kensa o shite, oishasan kara ninshin shite iru to iwaremashita.","german":"She took a urine pregnancy test, and the doctor told her she is pregnant.","furigana":"かのじょ|にょう|にんしんはんのうけんさ|いしゃ|にんしん|い"},{"japanese":"妊娠中はお酒を飲んではいけません。","romaji":"Ninshinchū wa osake o nonde wa ikemasen.","german":"You must not drink alcohol during pregnancy.","furigana":"にんしんちゅう|さけ|の"},{"japanese":"妻が妊娠して、来年の春に子どもが生まれる予定です。","romaji":"Tsuma ga ninshin shite, rainen no haru ni kodomo ga umareru yotei desu.","german":"My wife is pregnant; our child will be born next spring.","furigana":"つま|にんしん|らいねん|はる|こ|う|よてい"}],"notes":"妊娠する means \"to become pregnant\"; the state \"to be pregnant\" is expressed with 妊娠している. Typical expressions: 妊娠中 \"during pregnancy\", 妊娠三か月 \"in the third month\", 妊娠検査. The verb is intransitive; the mother is marked with が/は. In daily life one also says 子どもができる or, politely and indirectly, おめでた.","levelNote":"Derived from 妊娠 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"妊婦さんのためのカウンセラーという、臨時の仕事が見つかりました。","romaji":"Ninpu-san no tame no kaunseraa to iu, rinji no shigoto ga mitsukarimashita.","german":"I found a temporary job as a counselor for pregnant women.","furigana":"にんぷ|りんじ|しごと|み"},{"japanese":"電車の優先席は妊婦や高齢者のための席です。","romaji":"Densha no yūsenseki wa ninpu ya kōreisha no tame no seki desu.","german":"The priority seats on trains are seats for pregnant women and elderly people.","furigana":"でんしゃ|ゆうせんせき|にんぷ|こうれいしゃ|せき"},{"japanese":"妊婦健診で赤ちゃんの心音を聞いた。","romaji":"Ninpu kenshin de akachan no shin'on o kiita.","german":"At the prenatal checkup, I heard the baby's heartbeat.","furigana":"にんぷけんしん|あか|しんおん|き"}],"notes":"妊婦 means \"pregnant woman, expectant mother\". In conversation people usually say the polite 妊婦さん. Common combinations: 妊婦健診 \"prenatal checkup\", 妊婦用, 妊婦や高齢者 (e.g. on signs at priority seats). In official language you also find 妊産婦 \"pregnant women and women who have recently given birth\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"偽のベーコンに残された指紋は、マミのものと一致しました。","romaji":"Nise no beekon ni nokosareta shimon wa, Mami no mono to itchi shimashita.","german":"The fingerprints left on the fake bacon matched Mami's.","furigana":"にせ|のこ|しもん|いっち"},{"japanese":"話し合いの結果、二人の意見がようやく一致した。","romaji":"Hanashiai no kekka, futari no iken ga yōyaku itchi shita.","german":"After discussion, their opinions finally coincided.","furigana":"はな|あ|けっか|ふたり|いけん|いっち"},{"japanese":"入力したパスワードが一致しません。","romaji":"Nyūryoku shita pasuwādo ga itchi shimasen.","german":"The password you entered does not match.","furigana":"にゅうりょく|いっち"}],"notes":"一致する means \"to agree, coincide\" and is intransitive: AとBが一致する or AはBと一致する. Common with opinions (意見が一致する), data, fingerprints or passwords (パスワードが一致しません). Set expressions: 全会一致 \"unanimous,\" 一致団結 \"to stand united,\" 偶然の一致 \"coincidence.\" In everyday speech, for small things people often simply say 合う.","levelNote":"Derived from 一致 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日の朝刊の大ニュースには、誰もが多かれ少なかれ興味を抱いているんじゃないかな。","romaji":"Kyō no chōkan no dai nyūsu ni wa, dare mo ga ōkare sukunakare kyōmi o daite iru n ja nai ka na.","german":"I think everyone is more or less interested in the big news in today's morning paper.","furigana":"きょう|ちょうかん|だい|だれ|おお|すく|きょうみ|だ"},{"japanese":"毎朝、コーヒーを飲みながら朝刊を読む。","romaji":"Maiasa, kōhī o nominagara chōkan o yomu.","german":"Every morning I read the morning paper over coffee.","furigana":"まいあさ|の|ちょうかん|よ"},{"japanese":"その事件は明日の朝刊に載るだろう。","romaji":"Sono jiken wa ashita no chōkan ni noru darō.","german":"That incident will probably appear in tomorrow's morning edition.","furigana":"じけん|あした|ちょうかん|の"}],"notes":"朝刊 is the \"morning edition\" of a daily newspaper; the counterpart is 夕刊 \"evening edition\". In Japan, many newspapers are subscribed to as a package of 朝刊 and 夕刊 and delivered to the door in the morning. Typical: 朝刊を読む, 朝刊に載る \"to appear in the morning paper\", 今日の朝刊. The character 刊 means \"publication, edition\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"月刊誌『ベーコン』は、エープリルフールのジョークとして、「ノーベルベーコン賞はマミに授与された。」と報じた。","romaji":"Gekkanshi \"Beekon\" wa, eepuriru fuuru no jooku to shite, \"Nooberu beekon shou wa Mami ni juyo sareta.\" to houjita.","german":"The monthly magazine \"Bacon\" reported, as an April Fools' joke, that \"the Nobel Bacon Prize was awarded to Mami.\"","furigana":"げっかんし|しょう|じゅよ|ほう"},{"japanese":"毎月、料理の月刊誌を買っています。","romaji":"Maitsuki, ryōri no gekkanshi o katte imasu.","german":"I buy a monthly cooking magazine every month.","furigana":"まいつき|りょうり|げっかんし|か"},{"japanese":"この雑誌は来年、週刊から月刊に変わるそうだ。","romaji":"Kono zasshi wa rainen, shūkan kara gekkan ni kawaru sō da.","german":"This magazine is reportedly changing from weekly to monthly next year.","furigana":"ざっし|らいねん|しゅうかん|げっかん|か"}],"notes":"月刊 means \"published monthly\" and usually stands before a noun: 月刊誌 \"monthly magazine\", 月刊『〜』 as part of a title. It belongs to the series 日刊 (daily), 週刊 (weekly), 季刊 (quarterly) and 年刊 (annual). On its own it appears, for example, in 週刊から月刊に変わる \"to change from weekly to monthly\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どの新聞の夕刊でも、株式市場の大暴落が一面記事で報じられた。","romaji":"Dono shinbun no yuukan de mo, kabushiki shijou no dai bouraku ga ichimen kiji de houjirareta.","german":"The evening edition of every newspaper reported the stock market crash on the front page.","furigana":"しんぶん|ゆうかん|かぶしきしじょう|だいぼうらく|いちめんきじ|ほう"},{"japanese":"父は帰りの電車で夕刊を読むのが習慣だ。","romaji":"Chichi wa kaeri no densha de yūkan o yomu no ga shūkan da.","german":"My father makes a habit of reading the evening paper on the train ride home.","furigana":"ちち|かえ|でんしゃ|ゆうかん|よ|しゅうかん"},{"japanese":"最近は夕刊を発行しない新聞社も増えている。","romaji":"Saikin wa yūkan o hakkō shinai shinbunsha mo fuete iru.","german":"Recently, more and more newspaper companies are no longer publishing an evening edition.","furigana":"さいきん|ゆうかん|はっこう|しんぶんしゃ|ふ"}],"notes":"夕刊 is the \"evening edition\" of a daily newspaper, delivered in the afternoon; the counterpart is 朝刊. Many regional newspapers have by now discontinued their 夕刊. There are also independent tabloids sold at kiosks as 夕刊紙. Typical: 夕刊に載る, 夕刊で報じる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"マミはアヤに、ベーコン雑誌を一緒に刊行しないかと尋ねた。","romaji":"Mami wa Aya ni, beekon zasshi o issho ni kankou shinai ka to tazuneta.","german":"Mami asked Aya whether she wanted to put out a bacon magazine together.","furigana":"ざっし|いっしょ|かんこう|たず"},{"japanese":"その出版社は毎年多くの専門書を刊行している。","romaji":"Sono shuppansha wa maitoshi ōku no senmonsho o kankō shite iru.","german":"The publisher brings out numerous specialist books every year.","furigana":"しゅっぱんしゃ|まいとしおお|せんもんしょ|かんこう"},{"japanese":"新しい辞典が来月刊行される予定だ。","romaji":"Atarashii jiten ga raigetsu kankō sareru yotei da.","german":"A new dictionary is due to come out next month.","furigana":"あたら|じてん|らいげつかんこう|よてい"}],"notes":"刊行する means \"to publish, to issue\" books, magazines or series and is transitive: 雑誌を刊行する, often in the passive: 〜が刊行される. The word sounds formal and is a publishing term; more general is 出版する, and for newspapers and ID documents also 発行する. Related: 創刊 \"first issue, founding\", 廃刊 \"discontinuation\", 刊行物 \"publication\".","levelNote":"Derived from 刊行 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"アヤが描いたパグのイラストが、ある日刊紙のマスコットキャラクターに選ばれました。","romaji":"Aya ga egaita pagu no irasuto ga, aru nikkanshi no masukotto kyarakutaa ni erabaremashita.","german":"Aya's illustration of a pug was chosen as the mascot character of a daily newspaper.","furigana":"えが|にっかんし|えら"},{"japanese":"この新聞は日刊ではなく、週に一回発行される。","romaji":"Kono shinbun wa nikkan de wa naku, shū ni ikkai hakkō sareru.","german":"This newspaper is not published daily but once a week.","furigana":"しんぶん|にっかん|しゅう|いっかいはっこう"},{"japanese":"駅の売店で日刊のスポーツ新聞を買った。","romaji":"Eki no baiten de nikkan no supōtsu shinbun o katta.","german":"I bought a daily sports newspaper at the station kiosk.","furigana":"えき|ばいてん|にっかん|しんぶん|か"}],"notes":"日刊 means \"published daily\" and almost always stands before a noun: 日刊紙 \"daily newspaper\", 日刊誌. It forms a series with 週刊 (weekly), 月刊 (monthly) and 季刊 (quarterly). For the newspaper itself, people simply say 新聞 in everyday speech; 日刊 only emphasizes the publication rhythm.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"卒業式は、吹奏楽部の奏楽で始まった。","romaji":"Sotsugyōshiki wa, suisōgakubu no sōgaku de hajimatta.","german":"The graduation ceremony began with a performance by the brass band club.","furigana":"そつぎょうしき|すいそうがくぶ|そうがく|はじ"},{"japanese":"神社の祭りで、雅楽の奏楽が響いていた。","romaji":"Jinja no matsuri de, gagaku no sōgaku ga hibiite ita.","german":"At the shrine festival, gagaku music was resounding.","furigana":"じんじゃ|まつ|ががく|そうがく|ひび"},{"japanese":"教会の奏楽で使うハンドベルを探しているんですが、ありますか。","romaji":"Kyōkai no sōgaku de tsukau handoberu o sagashite iru n desu ga, arimasu ka.","german":"I'm looking for handbells to use for the music at church. Do you have any?","furigana":"きょうかい|そうがく|つか|さが"}],"notes":"奏楽 is an elevated word for \"musical performance\", especially at ceremonies, church services and formal events: 式典の奏楽, オルガンの奏楽, 雅楽の奏楽. In everyday speech, \"to play music\" is 演奏 or 演奏する; 奏楽 is heard more in program booklets and announcements. Also well known is the concert hall 旧東京音楽学校奏楽堂 in Ueno.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日は奥さんが同伴するのかと思ってたよ。","romaji":"Kyou wa okusan ga douhan suru no ka to omotteta yo.","german":"I thought your wife would be accompanying you today.","furigana":"きょう|おく|どうはん|おも"},{"japanese":"未成年者は保護者の同伴が必要です。","romaji":"Miseinensha wa hogosha no dōhan ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"Minors must be accompanied by a guardian.","furigana":"みせいねんしゃ|ほごしゃ|どうはん|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"ペットを同伴して泊まれるホテルを探している。","romaji":"Petto o dōhan shite tomareru hoteru o sagashite iru.","german":"I'm looking for a hotel where you can stay with a pet.","furigana":"どうはん|と|さが"}],"notes":"同伴する means \"to accompany\" or \"to bring someone along\". It occurs in two patterns: Xに同伴する \"to accompany X\" and Xを同伴する \"to take X along\". Typical in rules and invitations: 保護者同伴 \"only with a guardian\", 夫人同伴, ペット同伴可, 同伴者 \"accompanying person\". Formal; in conversation people say 一緒に行く/来る or 連れて行く, and for looking after someone 付き添う.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"アメリカ合衆国は1845年にテキサスを併合しました。","romaji":"Amerika Gasshūkoku wa 1845-nen ni Tekisasu o heigō shimashita.","german":"The United States annexed Texas in 1845.","furigana":"がっしゅうこく|ねん|へいごう"},{"japanese":"その国は隣国の一部を武力で併合した。","romaji":"Sono kuni wa ringoku no ichibu o buryoku de heigō shita.","german":"The country annexed part of its neighboring country by force.","furigana":"くに|りんごく|いちぶ|ぶりょく|へいごう"},{"japanese":"授業で1910年の韓国併合について学んだ。","romaji":"Jugyō de 1910-nen no Kankoku heigō ni tsuite mananda.","german":"In class, we learned about the annexation of Korea in 1910.","furigana":"じゅぎょう|ねん|かんこくへいごう|まな"}],"notes":"併合 means \"annexation, incorporation\": a state or territory is merged into another, mostly unilaterally (領土を併合する). Historically well known is 韓国併合 (1910). For the merger of companies or municipalities on an equal footing, people say 合併 instead. The word is written and belongs to political and historical vocabulary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「おまえの奥さんって、どんな人？」「彼女は何でもバリバリやるタイプだよ。」","romaji":"Omae no okusan tte, donna hito? Kanojo wa nani demo baribari yaru taipu da yo.","german":"\"What kind of person is your wife?\" \"She's the type who does everything energetically.\"","furigana":"おく|ひと|かのじょ|なに"},{"japanese":"田中さんの奥さんに、駅で会いました。","romaji":"Tanaka-san no okusan ni, eki de aimashita.","german":"I met Mr. Tanaka's wife at the station.","furigana":"たなか|おく|えき|あ"}],"notes":"奥さん is a respectful term for another person's wife; it can also address a married woman directly. おくさん is the hiragana spelling with the same reading. When talking about one's own wife, 妻 is the usual neutral term. The first dialogue is phrased very casually with おまえ and だよ; this form of address does not automatically suit polite conversation with strangers.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 9"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"何か命を懸けるほどのものはありますか。","romaji":"Nani ka inochi o kakeru hodo no mono wa arimasu ka.","german":"Is there anything you would stake your life on?","furigana":"なに|いのち|か"},{"japanese":"彼はこの試合に選手生命を懸けている。","romaji":"Kare wa kono shiai ni senshu seimei o kakete iru.","german":"He is staking his career as an athlete on this match.","furigana":"かれ|しあい|せんしゅせいめい|か"},{"japanese":"犯人の逮捕に百万円の賞金が懸けられた。","romaji":"Hannin no taiho ni hyakuman'en no shōkin ga kakerareta.","german":"A reward of one million yen was offered for the arrest of the criminal.","furigana":"はんにん|たいほ|ひゃくまんえん|しょうきん|か"}],"notes":"懸ける is the kanji spelling of かける for \"to stake, to put on the line\" with serious things: 命を懸ける, 人生を懸ける, 選手生命を懸ける, as well as \"to offer (a reward)\": 賞金を懸ける. For gambling and betting one writes 賭ける, and for all everyday meanings (\"to hang, to call\") 掛ける or kana. The intransitive partner is 懸かる: 命が懸かっている \"it is a matter of life and death\". Related: 命懸け \"at the risk of one's life\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5 (source: N5; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"足が泥にはまって、一生懸命抜こうとしてみたんですが、できなかったんです。","romaji":"Ashi ga doro ni hamatte, isshōkenmei nukō to shite mita n desu ga, dekinakatta n desu.","german":"My foot was stuck in the mud and I tried with all my might to pull it out, but I couldn't.","furigana":"あし|どろ|いっしょうけんめいぬ"},{"japanese":"救助隊は行方不明者を懸命に捜している。","romaji":"Kyūjotai wa yukue fumeisha o kenmei ni sagashite iru.","german":"The rescue team is searching desperately for the missing people.","furigana":"きゅうじょたい|ゆくえふめいしゃ|けんめい|さが"},{"japanese":"医師たちの懸命な治療のおかげで、彼は助かった。","romaji":"Ishi-tachi no kenmei na chiryō no okage de, kare wa tasukatta.","german":"Thanks to the doctors' tireless treatment, he was saved.","furigana":"いし|けんめい|ちりょう|かれ|たす"}],"notes":"懸命 is a な-adjective meaning \"with all one's strength, earnestly\"; mostly adverbial 懸命に (懸命に探す) or attributive 懸命な努力. 懸命 alone sounds written and like news language; in everyday speech people almost always use 一生懸命. Similar, but with desperation, is 必死. Not to be confused with 賢明 \"wise, prudent\" (賢明な判断), which is read the same way.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"夫の連れ子にまるで他人のように扱われて、すごくストレスが溜まります。","romaji":"Otto no tsureko ni marude tanin no you ni atsukawarete, sugoku sutoresu ga tamarimasu.","german":"Being treated like a total stranger by my husband's child from a previous marriage builds up a lot of stress.","furigana":"おっと|つ|こ|たにん|あつか|た"},{"japanese":"この店では輸入食品を扱っています。","romaji":"Kono mise de wa yunyū shokuhin o atsukatte imasu.","german":"This shop deals in imported foods.","furigana":"みせ|ゆにゅうしょくひん|あつか"},{"japanese":"壊れやすいので、丁寧に扱ってください。","romaji":"Kowareyasui node, teinei ni atsukatte kudasai.","german":"It breaks easily, so please handle it carefully.","furigana":"こわ|ていねい|あつか"}],"notes":"扱う is transitive and versatile: to \"treat\" people (子ども扱いする, 〜のように扱う, 〜として扱う), to \"handle\" objects (丁寧に扱う), to \"operate\" machines, to \"carry, stock\" goods (この店では〜を扱っている) and to \"deal with\" topics (問題を扱う). The noun 扱い appears in 扱いにくい \"hard to handle\"; more formal is 取り扱う (取扱説明書 \"instruction manual\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どの国が一番素晴らしい犯罪抑止モデルを有していると思われますか。","romaji":"Dono kuni ga ichiban subarashii hanzai yokushi moderu o yuushite iru to omowaremasu ka.","german":"Which country do you think has the best crime deterrence model?","furigana":"くに|いちばんすば|はんざいよくし|ゆう|おも"},{"japanese":"核兵器は戦争の抑止力になると主張する人もいる。","romaji":"Kakuheiki wa sensō no yokushiryoku ni naru to shuchō suru hito mo iru.","german":"Some people claim that nuclear weapons act as a deterrent to war.","furigana":"かくへいき|せんそう|よくしりょく|しゅちょう|ひと"},{"japanese":"防犯カメラの設置は犯罪の抑止につながる。","romaji":"Bōhan kamera no secchi wa hanzai no yokushi ni tsunagaru.","german":"Installing security cameras leads to deterring crime.","furigana":"ぼうはん|せっち|はんざい|よくし"}],"notes":"抑止 means \"deterrence, containment\", that is, preventing something in advance by deterring the act. Frequent in compounds: 抑止力 \"deterrent effect\" (also military: 核の抑止力), 犯罪抑止, 抑止効果. Related are 防止 \"prevention\" (more general) and 抑制 \"restraint, suppression\" of a process that is already under way. Written, from politics and law.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このソフトは描画の速度がとても速い。","romaji":"Kono sofuto wa byōga no sokudo ga totemo hayai.","german":"This software renders graphics very fast.","furigana":"びょうが|そくど|はや"},{"japanese":"子どもの描画には心の状態が表れると言われている。","romaji":"Kodomo no byōga ni wa kokoro no jōtai ga arawareru to iwarete iru.","german":"It is said that children's drawings reflect their state of mind.","furigana":"こ|びょうが|こころ|じょうたい|あらわ|い"},{"japanese":"タブレットの描画アプリでイラストを描いている。","romaji":"Taburetto no byōga apuri de irasuto o kaite iru.","german":"I draw illustrations with a drawing app on my tablet.","furigana":"びょうが|か"}],"notes":"描画 is a matter-of-fact, technical word for \"drawing, depiction\". It is found in psychology and art education (子どもの描画) and above all in IT, where it means \"displaying, rendering\": 描画ソフト, 描画速度, 描画アプリ. In everyday speech people say 絵 or 絵を描く; a sketch is called デッサン or スケッチ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"しばらくすると、ジェームズは円盤を脇に抱えて戻ってきて、UFOを見つけたんだと言いました。","romaji":"Shibaraku suru to, Jēmuzu wa enban o waki ni kakaete modotte kite, UFO o mitsuketa n da to iimashita.","german":"After a while, James came back with a disc under his arm and told us he had found a UFO.","furigana":"えんばん|わき|かか|もど|み|い"},{"japanese":"陸上部で円盤投げをしています。","romaji":"Rikujōbu de enbannage o shite imasu.","german":"I do the discus throw in the track and field club.","furigana":"りくじょうぶ|えんばんな"},{"japanese":"夜空に、光る円盤のようなものが見えた。","romaji":"Yozora ni, hikaru enban no yō na mono ga mieta.","german":"I saw something like a glowing disc in the night sky.","furigana":"よぞら|ひか|えんばん|み"}],"notes":"円盤 is a flat, round disc. In sports, the discus is 円盤 and the discus throw is 円盤投げ. It very often stands for UFOs: 空飛ぶ円盤 \"flying saucer\". In the anime and fan scene, 円盤 is also a casual word for DVDs and Blu-rays (アニメの円盤が売れる). For slices in everyday life, such as of lemons, people say 輪切り instead, and for data media otherwise ディスク.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この建設中のショッピングモールの仮称知ってる？","romaji":"Kono kensetsuchuu no shoppingu mooru no kashou shitteru?","german":"Do you know the provisional name of this shopping mall under construction?","furigana":"けんせつちゅう|かしょうし"},{"japanese":"新しい駅は、仮称「中央駅」として計画が進んでいる。","romaji":"Atarashii eki wa, kashō \"Chūō-eki\" to shite keikaku ga susunde iru.","german":"The new station is being planned under the provisional name \"Central Station\".","furigana":"あたら|えき|かしょう|ちゅうおうえき|けいかく|すす"},{"japanese":"このプロジェクトを、当面は「A計画」と仮称することにした。","romaji":"Kono purojekuto o, tōmen wa \"A keikaku\" to kashō suru koto ni shita.","german":"We decided to provisionally call this project \"Plan A\" for the time being.","furigana":"とうめん|けいかく|かしょう"}],"notes":"仮称 is a \"provisional name\", for example for buildings, stations or projects before their official naming. On plans and in announcements it is often in parentheses: （仮称）〜駅. As a する-verb: 〜を「…」と仮称する \"to provisionally call ...\". The opposite is 正式名称; an unofficial, commonly used name, by contrast, is 通称.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このホラー映画は、一人称の視点から撮影されています。","romaji":"Kono horaa eiga wa, ichininshou no shiten kara satsuei sarete imasu.","german":"This horror movie was filmed from a first-person perspective.","furigana":"えいが|いちにんしょう|してん|さつえい"},{"japanese":"日本語には「私」「僕」「俺」など、一人称がたくさんある。","romaji":"Nihongo ni wa \"watashi\" \"boku\" \"ore\" nado, ichininshō ga takusan aru.","german":"Japanese has many first-person pronouns, such as 「私」, 「僕」 and 「俺」.","furigana":"にほんご|わたし|ぼく|おれ|いちにんしょう"},{"japanese":"この小説は主人公の一人称で書かれている。","romaji":"Kono shōsetsu wa shujinkō no ichininshō de kakarete iru.","german":"This novel is written in the first person of the protagonist.","furigana":"しょうせつ|しゅじんこう|いちにんしょう|か"}],"notes":"一人称 is the grammatical \"first person\"; 二人称 and 三人称 belong with it. In Japanese, 一人称 often also means the words one uses to refer to oneself (私, 僕, 俺, うち etc.), whose choice reveals a lot about gender, age and situation. In literature, film and games it stands for the \"first-person perspective\": 一人称視点, 一人称小説.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「ご一緒してもよろしいですか？」「全然、構いませんよ。どうぞお座りください。」","romaji":"“Goissho shite mo yoroshii desu ka?” “Zenzen, kamaimasen yo. Dōzo osuwari kudasai.”","german":"\"May I join you?\" \"Not at all, I don't mind. Please have a seat.\"","furigana":"いっしょ|ぜんぜん|かま|すわ"},{"japanese":"日曜日は家族と一緒に料理をします。","romaji":"Nichiyōbi wa kazoku to issho ni ryōri o shimasu.","german":"On Sundays I cook together with my family.","furigana":"にちようび|かぞく|いっしょ|りょうり"}],"notes":"一緒 is a noun often used with に to mean \"together\": 家族と一緒に料理をする. と marks the person one does something with. The polite form ご一緒する means to join someone or to do something together. The word is therefore not inflected like an ordinary i-adjective.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私はICレコーダーで会話を内緒で録音します。","romaji":"Watashi wa IC rekōdā de kaiwa o naisho de rokuon shimasu.","german":"I secretly record conversations with my IC recorder.","furigana":"わたし|かいわ|ないしょ|ろくおん"},{"japanese":"このことは、みんなには内緒にしてね。","romaji":"Kono koto wa, minna ni wa naisho ni shite ne.","german":"Please keep this to yourself, okay?","furigana":"ないしょ"},{"japanese":"親に内緒でアルバイトを始めた。","romaji":"Oya ni naisho de arubaito o hajimeta.","german":"I started a part-time job without my parents' knowledge.","furigana":"おや|ないしょ|はじ"}],"notes":"内緒 means \"secret\" in the sense of \"something others should not know\" and is rather colloquial and familiar. Typical patterns: 内緒にする \"to keep secret\", 〜に内緒で \"without the knowledge of ..., secretly\", 内緒の話 / 内緒話 \"confidential talk\". 秘密 is more neutral and is also used for serious or official secrets (企業秘密), whereas 内緒 is more for private trifles among friends and in the family. Grammatically it is a noun (with の or で), not an adjective; it is often also written in kana ないしょ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"そのトンネルのボルトは、何本か緩んできています。","romaji":"Sono tonneru no boruto wa, nanbon ka yurunde kite imasu.","german":"Some bolts in this tunnel have started to loosen.","furigana":"なんぼん|ゆる"},{"japanese":"試験が終わって気が緩んだのか、風邪をひいてしまった。","romaji":"Shiken ga owatte ki ga yurunda no ka, kaze o hiite shimatta.","german":"Maybe because the tension eased after the exam, I caught a cold.","furigana":"しけん|お|き|ゆる|かぜ"},{"japanese":"三月に入って、ようやく寒さが緩んできた。","romaji":"Sangatsu ni haitte, youyaku samusa ga yurunde kita.","german":"Since the beginning of March, the cold is finally easing.","furigana":"さんがつ|はい|さむ|ゆる"}],"notes":"緩む is intransitive: something \"loosens\" by itself, e.g. ねじ / ボルト / 結び目が緩む. The transitive counterpart is 緩める (ベルトを緩める \"to loosen the belt\"), the related adjective is 緩い \"loose, lax\". Figuratively one says 気が緩む \"the tension eases, one becomes careless\", 寒さが緩む \"the cold eases\" or 規制が緩む \"rules are relaxed\". The form 緩んでくる is typical for a gradual easing.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"父の日には時計を贈るつもりです。","romaji":"Chichi no hi ni wa tokei o okuru tsumori desu.","german":"I'm planning to give a watch for Father's Day.","furigana":"ちち|ひ|とけい|おく"},{"japanese":"卒業する先輩に、みんなで花束を贈りました。","romaji":"Sotsugyou suru senpai ni, minna de hanataba o okurimashita.","german":"Everyone gave a bouquet to the senior who was graduating.","furigana":"そつぎょう|せんぱい|はなたば|おく"},{"japanese":"優勝したチームには、会場から大きな拍手が贈られた。","romaji":"Yuushou shita chiimu ni wa, kaijou kara ookina hakushu ga okurareta.","german":"A big round of applause from the venue was given to the winning team.","furigana":"ゆうしょう|かいじょう|おお|はくしゅ|おく"}],"notes":"贈る means \"to give as a gift, to present (ceremonially)\" and is to be distinguished from the identically read 送る \"to send\": 贈る stresses the gift or the honor, 送る the shipping. Pattern: 人に物を贈る, plus the noun 贈り物 \"gift.\" Figuratively also 拍手を贈る \"to give applause,\" 言葉を贈る \"to offer words of farewell/encouragement\" or 賞を贈る \"to award a prize.\" In everyday speech, for gifts among friends people often simply say あげる or プレゼントする.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の弟は優し過ぎて犯罪者達の逃亡を助けてしまい、そのせいで逮捕されてしまいました。","romaji":"Watashi no otouto wa yasashisugite hanzaishatachi no toubou o tasukete shimai, sono sei de taiho sarete shimaimashita.","german":"My little brother is so good-natured that he helped criminals escape and was arrested for it.","furigana":"わたし|おとうと|やさ|す|はんざいしゃたち|とうぼう|たす|たいほ"},{"japanese":"犯人は現在も逃亡中だそうです。","romaji":"Hannin wa genzai mo toubouchuu da sou desu.","german":"The perpetrator is said to be still on the run.","furigana":"はんにん|げんざい|とうぼうちゅう"},{"japanese":"容疑者は事件の翌日に海外へ逃亡した。","romaji":"Yougisha wa jiken no yokujitsu ni kaigai e toubou shita.","german":"The suspect fled abroad the day after the crime.","furigana":"ようぎしゃ|じけん|よくじつ|かいがい|とうぼう"}],"notes":"逃亡 is an escape in which someone gets away or goes into hiding for good, such as a perpetrator, prisoner or soldier; as a suru-verb it is intransitive: 海外へ逃亡する, 逃亡中の犯人 \"the fugitive perpetrator\", 逃亡者 \"fugitive\". The word is written-language and typical of news reports. 逃走, by contrast, emphasizes more the running away from the scene of the crime, and in daily life one simply says 逃げる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"行方不明の英語教師は、足が凍傷になっていたものの、無事に生還しました。","romaji":"Yukue fumei no eigo kyōshi wa, ashi ga tōshō ni natte ita monono, buji ni seikan shimashita.","german":"The missing English teacher returned safely, although his feet had frostbite.","furigana":"ゆくえふめい|えいごきょうし|あし|とうしょう|ぶじ|せいかん"},{"japanese":"遭難した登山者たちは、三日後に全員生還した。","romaji":"Sounan shita tozanshatachi wa, mikka go ni zen'in seikan shita.","german":"The mountain climbers who had been in distress all returned alive after three days.","furigana":"そうなん|とざんしゃ|みっかご|ぜんいんせいかん"},{"japanese":"ランナーがホームに生還し、同点に追いついた。","romaji":"Rannaa ga hoomu ni seikan shi, douten ni oitsuita.","german":"The runner made it home and tied the game.","furigana":"せいかん|どうてん|お"}],"notes":"生還 means \"to return alive\" from great mortal danger, for example after a mountain accident, an accident or from war; as a する-verb it is intransitive: 無事に生還する, 奇跡の生還 \"miraculous rescue\", 生還者 \"survivor\". It is written and typical for the news. 帰還 is the more neutral \"return\" (e.g. of astronauts), and 生き残る means \"to survive\" without the aspect of coming home. In baseball, 生還 also means that a runner reaches home plate and scores.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"沖縄がアメリカから日本に返還されたのは1972年のことです。","romaji":"Okinawa ga Amerika kara Nippon ni henkan sa reta no wa 1972nen no koto desu.","german":"Okinawa was returned from the US to Japan in 1972.","furigana":"おきなわ|にっぽん|へんかん|ねん"},{"japanese":"博物館は、盗まれた美術品を元の持ち主に返還した。","romaji":"Hakubutsukan wa, nusumareta bijutsuhin o moto no mochinushi ni henkan shita.","german":"The museum returned the stolen artwork to its original owner.","furigana":"はくぶつかん|ぬす|びじゅつひん|もと|も|ぬし|へんかん"},{"japanese":"部屋を出るときに、敷金の一部が返還されました。","romaji":"Heya o deru toki ni, shikikin no ichibu ga henkan saremashita.","german":"When I moved out, part of my deposit was refunded to me.","furigana":"へや|で|しききん|いちぶ|へんかん"}],"notes":"返還 is the formal return of something that is due to someone, such as territories, cultural property, deposits or fees: 〜を〜に返還する, very often in the passive 返還される. The word is written-language and typical of politics, law and administration. In daily life one says 返す; for borrowed items (books, rental cars) 返却 is usual, for money also 返金.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"最初は、どんなもんかなと思って湯豆腐を食べてみただけなんですが、そしたら好きになっちゃったんですよね。","romaji":"Saisho wa, donna mon ka na to omotte yudoufu o tabete mita dake na n desu ga, soshitara suki ni nacchatta n desu yo ne.","german":"At first I tried yudofu just out of curiosity, and then I came to love it.","furigana":"さいしょ|おも|ゆどうふ|た|す"},{"japanese":"寒い夜は、湯豆腐で温まるのがいちばんだ。","romaji":"Samui yoru wa, yudoufu de atatamaru no ga ichiban da.","german":"On a cold night, warming up with yudofu is the best.","furigana":"さむ|よる|ゆどうふ|あたた"},{"japanese":"京都の南禅寺の近くで、名物の湯豆腐を食べました。","romaji":"Kyouto no Nanzenji no chikaku de, meibutsu no yudoufu o tabemashita.","german":"Near Nanzen-ji temple in Kyoto, we ate the famous yudofu.","furigana":"きょうと|なんぜんじ|ちか|めいぶつ|ゆどうふ|た"}],"notes":"湯豆腐 is a simple winter dish: tofu is warmed in a pot (often 土鍋) with kombu broth and then dipped in ponzu or soy sauce with 薬味 such as spring onions. It is especially well known from Kyoto, for example around the temple 南禅寺. The counterpart is 冷奴, cold tofu in summer. People say 湯豆腐を食べる or 湯豆腐で温まる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このセキュリティー対策アプリは、AndroidOS及びiOSのどちらでもご利用頂けます。","romaji":"Kono sekyuritī taisaku apuri wa, AndroidOS oyobi iOS no dochira demo goriyō itadakemasu.","german":"This security app is available for both Android OS and iOS.","furigana":"たいさく|およ|りよういただ"},{"japanese":"こちらに氏名及び住所をご記入ください。","romaji":"Kochira ni shimei oyobi jūsho o go-kinyū kudasai.","german":"Please enter your name and address here.","furigana":"しめいおよ|じゅうしょ|きにゅう"},{"japanese":"会議には社長、副社長及び各部の部長が出席した。","romaji":"Kaigi ni wa shachō, fukushachō oyobi kakubu no buchō ga shusseki shita.","german":"The president, the vice president and the department heads attended the meeting.","furigana":"かいぎ|しゃちょう|ふくしゃちょうおよ|かくぶ|ぶちょう|しゅっせき"}],"notes":"及び is a written-language conjunction \"and, as well as\" that connects nouns - typical of forms, laws, contracts and official notices. With three or more items it comes before the last one: A、B及びC. In conversation one says と or や instead. Even more formal is 並びに, which connects whole groups. Often also written in kana as および; not to be confused with the verb 及ぶ \"to extend to\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ルームメートが、私のプライバシーに踏み込みすぎだと思うのよね。","romaji":"Ruumumeeto ga, watashi no puraibashii ni fumikomisugi da to omou no yo ne.","german":"I think my roommate intrudes too much on my privacy.","furigana":"わたし|ふ|こ|おも"},{"japanese":"警察は、犯人が隠れているアパートに踏み込んだ。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa, hannin ga kakurete iru apaato ni fumikonda.","german":"The police stormed the apartment where the culprit was hiding.","furigana":"けいさつ|はんにん|かく|ふ|こ"},{"japanese":"この問題については、もう一歩踏み込んだ議論が必要だ。","romaji":"Kono mondai ni tsuite wa, mou ippo fumikonda giron ga hitsuyou da.","german":"For this problem we need a discussion that goes one step deeper.","furigana":"もんだい|いっぽふ|こ|ぎろん|ひつよう"}],"notes":"踏み込む literally means \"to step into\": 〜に踏み込む (to enter an area, to intrude on foreign terrain), 警察が〜に踏み込む \"the police storm ...\". With を it means \"to press down (a pedal)\": アクセルを踏み込む. Figuratively it stands for \"to intrude too far into private matters\" (プライバシーに踏み込む) or \"to get to the bottom of a matter more deeply\": 踏み込んだ議論 \"a more in-depth discussion\", 一歩踏み込む \"to go one step further\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このイベントの参加者は、一万人を超す見込みだ。","romaji":"Kono ibento no sankasha wa, ichiman-nin o kosu mikomi da.","german":"The number of participants in this event is expected to exceed ten thousand.","furigana":"さんかしゃ|いちまんにん|こ|みこ"},{"japanese":"今年の夏は、気温が三十五度を超す日が多かった。","romaji":"Kotoshi no natsu wa, kion ga sanjuugo-do o kosu hi ga ookatta.","german":"This summer there were many days when the temperature exceeded thirty-five degrees.","furigana":"ことし|なつ|きおん|さんじゅうごど|こ|ひ|おお"},{"japanese":"修理代が予算を大幅に超してしまった。","romaji":"Shuuridai ga yosan o oohaba ni koshite shimatta.","german":"The repair costs went far over the budget.","furigana":"しゅうりだい|よさん|おおはば|こ"}],"notes":"超す means \"to exceed (a number, limit, measure)\" and almost always takes を: 三十五度を超す, 予算を超す, 一万人を超す. It is equivalent to the more common 超える, but sounds somewhat more like written and news language. Distinguish it from the identically read 越す: 越す is used for crossing physically (山を越す), getting through a season (冬を越す) and for 引っ越す \"to move house.\" For \"to go too far,\" 度を越す is usually written.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"クミンシードは、インドカレーには欠かせない香辛料のうちの一つです。","romaji":"Kuminshiido wa, Indo karee ni wa kakasenai koushinryou no uchi no hitotsu desu.","german":"Cumin is one of the spices indispensable in Indian curry.","furigana":"か|こうしんりょう|ひと"},{"japanese":"この料理は香辛料がよく効いていて、少し辛い。","romaji":"Kono ryouri wa koushinryou ga yoku kiite ite, sukoshi karai.","german":"This dish is strongly seasoned and a little spicy.","furigana":"りょうり|こうしんりょう|き|すこ|から"},{"japanese":"昔、ヨーロッパではコショウなどの香辛料がとても高価だった。","romaji":"Mukashi, Yooroppa de wa koshou nado no koushinryou ga totemo kouka datta.","german":"In the past, spices such as pepper were very expensive in Europe.","furigana":"むかし|こうしんりょう|こうか"}],"notes":"香辛料 are spices in the narrower sense, i.e. pepper, cinnamon, cumin, chili and the like; the word is rather written-language, in daily life one mostly says スパイス. To be distinguished from it are 調味料 \"seasonings\" such as salt, sugar, soy sauce or miso, and 薬味 for fresh condiments such as ginger or spring onions. Typical: 香辛料が効いている \"to be strongly spiced\", 香辛料を使う / 加える.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"狩人とは非常に奥深い職業です。","romaji":"Karyuudo to wa hijou ni okubukai shokugyou desu.","german":"The profession of hunter is an extremely deep one.","furigana":"かりゅうど|ひじょう|おくぶか|しょくぎょう"},{"japanese":"茶道は、知れば知るほど奥深い世界だ。","romaji":"Sadou wa, shireba shiru hodo okubukai sekai da.","german":"The tea ceremony is a world that gets deeper the more you know about it.","furigana":"さどう|し|し|おくぶか|せかい"},{"japanese":"その村は、山の奥深いところにある。","romaji":"Sono mura wa, yama no okubukai tokoro ni aru.","german":"The village is located deep in the mountains.","furigana":"むら|やま|おくぶか"}],"notes":"奥深い is mostly used figuratively: a field, an art or a taste is \"profound, multifaceted\" and you always discover something new (奥深い世界, 奥深い味). In conversation, 奥が深い is heard more often for this. Literally it means \"located deep inside\", for example 山の奥深いところ, 奥深い森. It is an い-adjective: 奥深く, 奥深さ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"退屈すぎて欠伸が出てきた。","romaji":"Taikutsu sugite akubi ga dete kita.","german":"It was so boring that I started yawning.","furigana":"たいくつ|あくび|で"},{"japanese":"会議中に大きなあくびをして、部長に注意された。","romaji":"Kaigichuu ni ookina akubi o shite, buchou ni chuui sareta.","german":"I yawned loudly during the meeting and was warned by the department head.","furigana":"かいぎちゅう|おお|ぶちょう|ちゅうい"},{"japanese":"眠そうにあくびをする猫の写真を撮った。","romaji":"Nemusou ni akubi o suru neko no shashin o totta.","german":"I took a photo of a cat yawning sleepily.","furigana":"ねむ|ねこ|しゃしん|と"}],"notes":"欠伸 \"yawn\" is almost always written in kana あくび. Typical combinations: あくびが出る \"to feel a yawn coming\", あくびをする \"to yawn\", あくびをかみ殺す \"to stifle a yawn\", 生あくび \"a suppressed yawn\". As a する-verb it is intransitive; the only object with を is あくび itself (大きなあくびをする).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"キュウリの浅漬けならありますよ。","romaji":"Kyuuri no asazuke nara arimasu yo.","german":"We have lightly pickled cucumbers.","furigana":"あさづ"},{"japanese":"祖母は毎日ぬか漬けを作っている。","romaji":"Sobo wa mainichi nukazuke o tsukutte iru.","german":"My grandmother makes rice-bran pickles every day.","furigana":"そぼ|まいにち|づ|つく"},{"japanese":"試験の前に一夜漬けで勉強しても、すぐに忘れてしまう。","romaji":"Shiken no mae ni ichiyazuke de benkyou shite mo, sugu ni wasurete shimau.","german":"Even if you cram the night before an exam, you forget it right away.","furigana":"しけん|まえ|いちやづ|べんきょう|わす"}],"notes":"〜漬け is a suffix (with rendaku づけ) for \"something pickled in\": ぬか漬け (in rice bran), 浅漬け (lightly pickled), 醤油漬け, 梅酒漬け; on its own, pickled vegetables are called 漬物. Figuratively it means \"completely immersed in something, excessively dependent on something\": 勉強漬け \"nothing but studying\", 薬漬け \"stuffed with medication\". Very common is 一夜漬け \"last-minute studying, cramming overnight\". The related verb is 漬ける \"to pickle\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"心の奥底でほくそ笑みました。","romaji":"Kokoro no okusoko de hokusoemimashita.","german":"Deep down inside, I chuckled to myself.","furigana":"こころ|おくそこ|え"},{"japanese":"彼女は心の奥底では、まだ彼のことを忘れられずにいる。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kokoro no okusoko de wa, mada kare no koto o wasurerarezu ni iru.","german":"Deep in her heart, she still cannot forget him.","furigana":"かのじょ|こころ|おくそこ|かれ|わす"},{"japanese":"記憶の奥底に眠っていた子どもの頃の思い出が、急によみがえった。","romaji":"Kioku no okusoko ni nemutte ita kodomo no koro no omoide ga, kyuu ni yomigaetta.","german":"Childhood memories that had been sleeping deep in my memory suddenly came back.","furigana":"きおく|おくそこ|ねむ|こ|ころ|おも|で|きゅう"}],"notes":"奥底 refers to the \"deepest depth\" of something and is used almost only figuratively: 心の奥底 \"in the depths of one's heart\", 記憶の奥底 \"in the depths of memory\", 奥底に眠る \"to lie dormant deep inside\". It emphasizes feelings or thoughts that one does not show outwardly. Similar, but more adverbial, is 心の底から \"from the bottom of one's heart\". Literally (海の奥底) it is rare.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"膝頭のけがが痛くて、眠ることすらできない。","romaji":"Hizagashira no kega ga itakute, nemuru koto sura dekinai.","german":"The injury on my knee hurts so much that I can't even sleep.","furigana":"ひざがしら|いた|ねむ"},{"japanese":"転んで膝頭をすりむいてしまった。","romaji":"Koronde hizagashira o surimuite shimatta.","german":"I fell and scraped my knee.","furigana":"ころ|ひざがしら"},{"japanese":"正座を続けていたら、膝頭が痛くなってきた。","romaji":"Seiza o tsuzukete itara, hizagashira ga itaku natte kita.","german":"After continuing to kneel in seiza, my knees started to hurt.","furigana":"せいざ|つづ|ひざがしら|いた"}],"notes":"膝頭 is the front of the knee with the kneecap, that is, the spot you fall or kneel on. In everyday speech people often simply say 膝 or, colloquially, 膝の皿; the medical term for the kneecap as a bone is 膝蓋骨. Typical: 膝頭をすりむく \"to scrape one's knee\", 膝頭が痛い, 膝頭をぶつける.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"犬に手を噛まれました。","romaji":"Inu ni te o kamaremashita.","german":"I was bitten on the hand by a dog.","furigana":"いぬ|て|か"},{"japanese":"ご飯はよく噛んで食べなさい。","romaji":"Gohan wa yoku kande tabenasai.","german":"Chew your food well.","furigana":"はん|か|た"},{"japanese":"大事な発表で何回も噛んでしまいました。","romaji":"Daiji na happyō de nankai mo kande shimaimashita.","german":"I stumbled over my words several times during the important presentation.","furigana":"だいじ|はっぴょう|なんかい|か"}],"notes":"噛む means \"to bite\" (犬に噛まれる, very common in the passive) and \"to chew\" (よく噛んで食べる, ガムを噛む). Colloquially it also means \"to misspeak, to stumble over words\": 発表で噛んでしまった. It is mostly written in kana as かむ. Not to be confused with 鼻をかむ \"to blow one's nose\", which sounds the same but is a different word.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は日本の国歌を完璧に歌えます。","romaji":"Watashi wa Nippon no kokka o kanpeki ni utaemasu.","german":"I can sing the Japanese national anthem perfectly.","furigana":"わたし|にっぽん|こっか|かんぺき|うた"},{"japanese":"完璧な人間なんて、どこにもいないよ。","romaji":"Kanpeki na ningen nante, doko ni mo inai yo.","german":"A perfect person exists nowhere.","furigana":"かんぺき|にんげん"},{"japanese":"彼女は完璧主義なので、少しのミスも許さない。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kanpeki shugi na node, sukoshi no misu mo yurusanai.","german":"She is a perfectionist and lets not the slightest mistake slip through.","furigana":"かのじょ|かんぺきしゅぎ|すこ|ゆる"}],"notes":"完璧 is a na-adjective: 完璧な仕事, 完璧に覚える, 完璧だ. It emphasizes \"flawless, without any mistake\", whereas 完全 rather means \"complete, without gaps\" (完全に忘れた \"completely forgot\"). Common are 完璧主義 \"perfectionism\" and 完璧主義者. Watch out when writing: the second character is 璧 (jade disc), not 壁 (wall).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"京都の清水寺は由緒あるお寺だ。","romaji":"Kyouto no Kiyomizudera wa yuisho aru otera da.","german":"Kiyomizu-dera in Kyoto is a temple with a long history.","furigana":"きょうと|きよみずでら|ゆいしょ|てら"},{"japanese":"彼女は由緒正しい家柄の出身だ。","romaji":"Kanojo wa yuisho tadashii iegara no shusshin da.","german":"She comes from a family of distinguished lineage.","furigana":"かのじょ|ゆいしょただ|いえがら|しゅっしん"},{"japanese":"このお祭りの由緒について、神社の人に話を聞いた。","romaji":"Kono omatsuri no yuisho ni tsuite, jinja no hito ni hanashi o kiita.","german":"We had someone from the shrine tell us about the history of this festival.","furigana":"まつ|ゆいしょ|じんじゃ|ひと|はなし|き"}],"notes":"由緒 is the long, venerable history or origin of a place, a family or a custom. It almost always appears in fixed combinations: 由緒ある寺 / 旅館 \"steeped in tradition\", 由緒正しい家柄 \"of distinguished family\". The word is elevated; 歴史 is the general \"history\", and 由来 refers to the origin of a name or thing.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あ、天の川が見える！","romaji":"A, amanogawa ga mieru!","german":"Oh, I can see the Milky Way!","furigana":"あま|がわ|み"},{"japanese":"七夕の伝説では、織姫と彦星は天の川をはさんで離れ離れになっている。","romaji":"Tanabata no densetsu de wa, Orihime to Hikoboshi wa amanogawa o hasande hanarebanare ni natte iru.","german":"According to the Tanabata legend, Orihime and Hikoboshi are separated by the Milky Way.","furigana":"たなばた|でんせつ|おりひめ|ひこぼし|あま|がわ|はな|ばな"},{"japanese":"町の明かりが多いと、天の川はほとんど見えない。","romaji":"Machi no akari ga ooi to, amanogawa wa hotondo mienai.","german":"Where there is a lot of city light, you can hardly see the Milky Way.","furigana":"まち|あ|おお|あま|がわ|み"}],"notes":"天の川 is the everyday and poetic name of the Milky Way, literally \"river of heaven\". It is closely tied to the 七夕 festival: 織姫 and 彦星 are separated by the 天の川 and may meet only once a year. In astronomy, people also speak of 天の川銀河 or generally of 銀河 \"galaxy\". Typical: 天の川が見える, 天の川を見上げる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"まず、大豆をこんがりと炒ることから始めましょう。","romaji":"Mazu, daizu o kongari to iru koto kara hajimemashō.","german":"First, let's start by roasting the soybeans until golden brown.","furigana":"だいず|い|はじ"},{"japanese":"ごまを軽く炒ると、香りがよくなります。","romaji":"Goma o karuku iru to, kaori ga yoku narimasu.","german":"If you lightly roast the sesame seeds, they smell better.","furigana":"かる|い|かお"},{"japanese":"節分の豆まきには、炒った大豆を使います。","romaji":"Setsubun no mamemaki ni wa, itta daizu o tsukaimasu.","german":"For the bean-throwing at Setsubun, roasted soybeans are used.","furigana":"せつぶん|まめ|い|だいず|つか"}],"notes":"炒る means \"to roast without oil\", for example ごま, 豆 or coffee beans (also written 煎る). To be distinguished from it is 炒める \"to stir-fry with oil\" (野菜を炒める). Although it ends in -iru, 炒る is a godan verb: 炒って, 炒らない. Because of the many homophones (居る, 要る, 射る), it is often written in kana いる, and 炒った豆 \"roasted beans\" is a common form.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"昼飯を食う時間がなかったんだ。","romaji":"Hirumeshi o kuu jikan ga nakatta n da.","german":"I didn't have time to eat lunch.","furigana":"ひるめし|く|じかん"},{"japanese":"この車は大きいから、ガソリンをよく食う。","romaji":"Kono kuruma wa ookii kara, gasorin o yoku kuu.","german":"This car is big, so it guzzles a lot of gas.","furigana":"くるま|おお|く"},{"japanese":"窓を開けて寝ていたら、蚊に足を食われた。","romaji":"Mado o akete nete itara, ka ni ashi o kuwareta.","german":"I slept with the window open and got my leg bitten by a mosquito.","furigana":"まど|あ|ね|か|あし|く"}],"notes":"食う is the rough variant of 食べる \"to eat\" (飯を食う), used mainly by men; in polite speech it is avoided. It also has many figurative meanings: 時間を食う / ガソリンを食う \"to take up a lot of time / gas,\" 蚊に食われる \"to be bitten by mosquitoes,\" 食っていく \"to get by, earn a living.\" Set phrases are 食うか食われるか \"eat or be eaten\" and 一杯食わされる \"to be tricked.\"","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"我が社とWaniKaniとのコラボ商品第一弾は、ワニカニのフィギュアです！","romaji":"Wagasha to WaniKani to no korabo shōhin daiichidan wa, Wanikani no figyua desu!","german":"The first product of our collaboration with WaniKani is a WaniKani figure!"},{"japanese":"キャンペーン第一弾として、全商品が二割引きになります。","romaji":"Kyanpēn daiichidan to shite, zenshōhin ga niwaribiki ni narimasu.","german":"As the first round of the campaign, all products will be 20 percent off.","furigana":"だいいちだん|ぜんしょうひん|にわりび"},{"japanese":"この映画はシリーズの第一弾で、来年には続編が公開される。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa shirīzu no daiichidan de, rainen ni wa zokuhen ga kōkai sareru.","german":"This movie is the first installment of the series, and a sequel will be released next year.","furigana":"えいが|だいいちだん|らいねん|ぞくへん|こうかい"}],"notes":"第一弾 refers to the first part of a planned series - a campaign, product line, film series or sequence of events. 弾 originally means \"projectile\" and here serves as a counter for stages: 第二弾, 第三弾, 最終弾. Typical is 〜の第一弾として. The word is very widespread in advertising, press releases and media. For the first date of a regular event, people rather say 第一回, and for the first section of a work 第一部.","levelNote":"Compound: 第一 (N2) + 弾 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"そのコンサートは、東日本大震災の復興祈念のためのものでした。","romaji":"Sono konsaato wa, Higashi Nihon Daishinsai no fukkou kinen no tame no mono deshita.","german":"That concert was held in prayer for the recovery after the Great East Japan Earthquake.","furigana":"ひがしにほんだいしんさい|ふっこうきねん"},{"japanese":"式典の最後に、全員で世界の平和を祈念しました。","romaji":"Shikiten no saigo ni, zen'in de sekai no heiwa o kinen shimashita.","german":"At the end of the ceremony, everyone prayed together for world peace.","furigana":"しきてん|さいご|ぜんいん|せかい|へいわ|きねん"},{"japanese":"皆様のご健康とご多幸を祈念いたしまして、乾杯！","romaji":"Minasama no gokenkou to gotakou o kinen itashimashite, kanpai!","german":"Wishing you all good health and happiness - cheers!","furigana":"みなさま|けんこう|たこう|きねん|かんぱい"}],"notes":"祈念 is a solemn \"praying, fervently wishing\" and is encountered above all in ceremonies, speeches and letters: 平和を祈念する, 復興祈念, and as a closing formula ご健康とご多幸を祈念いたします. Not to be confused with 記念 \"keepsake, commemoration\" (記念日, 記念写真), which is read the same way. In everyday speech people say 祈る; 祈願 is asking for something specific at a shrine or temple (合格祈願).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"俺は、この年になっても、まだくだらないことに惑うよ。","romaji":"Ore wa, kono toshi ni natte mo, mada kudaranai koto ni madou yo.","german":"Even at my age, I still get bewildered by trivial things.","furigana":"おれ|とし|まど"},{"japanese":"火事が起きて、人々は煙の中を逃げ惑った。","romaji":"Kaji ga okite, hitobito wa kemuri no naka o nigemadotta.","german":"When the fire broke out, people fled in a panic through the smoke.","furigana":"かじ|お|ひとびと|けむり|なか|に|まど"},{"japanese":"甘い言葉に惑うことなく、自分で判断しなさい。","romaji":"Amai kotoba ni madou koto naku, jibun de handan shinasai.","german":"Don't be led astray by sweet words; judge for yourself.","furigana":"あま|ことば|まど|じぶん|はんだん"}],"notes":"惑う means \"to be uncertain, confused, not to know what to do\" and also \"to be led astray\"; on its own it is rather written to literary. In everyday speech people mostly say 迷う or 戸惑う, and for \"to be deceived\" the passive of 惑わす is usual (〜に惑わされる). 惑う is very common in compounds such as 逃げ惑う \"to flee in panic in all directions\" and 思い惑う. Also well known is the Confucius quote 四十にして惑わず, hence 不惑 \"forty years old\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どの香りを付けるかを決めるのは難しいですね。毎朝短くとも一時間はそれに時間を費やします。","romaji":"Dono kaori o tsukeru ka o kimeru no wa muzukashii desu ne. Maiasa mijikaku tomo ichijikan wa sore ni jikan o tsuiyashimasu.","german":"It's hard to decide which scent to wear. Every morning I spend at least an hour on it.","furigana":"かお|つ|き|むずか|まいあさみじか|いちじかん|じかん|つい"},{"japanese":"庭の花のいい香りがする。","romaji":"Niwa no hana no ii kaori ga suru.","german":"The flowers in the garden smell nice.","furigana":"にわ|はな|かお"},{"japanese":"入れたてのコーヒーの香りで目が覚めた。","romaji":"Iretate no koohii no kaori de me ga sameta.","german":"The aroma of freshly brewed coffee woke me up.","furigana":"い|かお|め|さ"}],"notes":"香り is a pleasant scent, for example of flowers, coffee, tea or perfume: いい香りがする, 香りが漂う, 香りを楽しむ. 匂い is more neutral and can smell good or bad, and 臭い (also read におい) stands for a stench. The spelling 薫り is literary and used for a figurative \"atmosphere.\" 香りを付ける refers to applying a fragrance.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「ゲッツー」というのは、併殺を意味する和製英語です。","romaji":"\"Gettsuu\" to iu no wa, heisatsu o imi suru wasei eigo desu.","german":"\"ゲッツー\" (from \"get two\") is Japanese-coined English meaning a double play.","furigana":"へいさつ|いみ|わせいえいご"},{"japanese":"ピンチの場面で、ショートが見事な併殺を決めた。","romaji":"Pinchi no bamen de, shooto ga migoto na heisatsu o kimeta.","german":"In the tight situation, the shortstop pulled off a brilliant double play.","furigana":"ばめん|みごと|へいさつ|き"},{"japanese":"四番打者は併殺打に倒れ、チャンスを逃した。","romaji":"Yoban dasha wa heisatsuda ni taore, chansu o nogashita.","german":"The cleanup hitter grounded into a double play and wasted the chance.","furigana":"よばんだしゃ|へいさつだ|たお|のが"}],"notes":"併殺 is the technical term for the double play in baseball, in which two players are out in a single play. It is typical of sports reports; in conversation people mostly say ゲッツー or ダブルプレー. Important combinations: 併殺打 \"a hit that leads to a double play\", 併殺打に倒れる (from the batter's point of view), 併殺に打ち取る / 併殺を決める (from the defense's point of view). As a する-verb it is transitive.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"父親が跡継ぎに弟を選んだという知らせは、彼にとって大きな打撃でした。","romaji":"Chichioya ga atotsugi ni otouto o eranda to iu shirase wa, kare ni totte ookina dageki deshita.","german":"The news that his father had chosen his younger brother as heir was a heavy blow to him.","furigana":"ちちおや|あとつ|おとうと|えら|し|かれ|おお|だげき"},{"japanese":"老舗の旅館なのに、跡継ぎがいなくて困っているそうだ。","romaji":"Shinise no ryokan na noni, atotsugi ga inakute komatte iru sou da.","german":"Although it is a long-established ryokan, they say no successor can be found.","furigana":"しにせ|りょかん|あとつ|こま"},{"japanese":"長男が家の跡継ぎになるのが当たり前だった時代もある。","romaji":"Chounan ga ie no atotsugi ni naru no ga atarimae datta jidai mo aru.","german":"There were times when it was a matter of course for the eldest son to take over the family estate.","furigana":"ちょうなん|いえ|あとつ|あ|まえ|じだい"}],"notes":"跡継ぎ is the successor who takes over a family business, a shop, a farm or \"the house\" (家); the spelling 後継ぎ is also common. Typical: 跡継ぎがいない, 〜の跡継ぎになる, 跡継ぎに選ぶ. 後継者 is more formal and is also usual for offices and positions, 相続人 is the legal \"heir\" to property. The related verb is 跡を継ぐ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"FM802を聞きながら縄跳びをしています。","romaji":"FM 802 o kiki nagara nawatobi o shite imasu.","german":"I'm jumping rope while listening to FM802.","furigana":"き|なわと"},{"japanese":"子どもの頃、休み時間によく縄跳びをして遊んだ。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, yasumi jikan ni yoku nawatobi o shite asonda.","german":"When I was a child, I often played jump rope during recess.","furigana":"こ|ころ|やす|じかん|なわと|あそ"},{"japanese":"ボクサーは毎日、縄跳びでトレーニングをしている。","romaji":"Bokusaa wa mainichi, nawatobi de toreeningu o shite iru.","german":"Boxers train with jump rope every day.","furigana":"まいにち|なわと"}],"notes":"縄跳び refers both to the jump rope and to jumping rope itself: 縄跳びをする \"to jump rope\", 縄跳びで遊ぶ, 縄跳びを買う. Well-known variants are 二重跳び \"double under\" and 大縄跳び, in which several children jump over a long rope swung by two people. It is often also written in kana なわとび, especially in children's texts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あいつ、ビキニ姿のギャルが見れるからっていう理由だけで、市民プールの監視員の仕事に応募したんだよ。","romaji":"Aitsu, bikini sugata no gyaru ga mireru kara tte iu riyuu dake de, shimin puuru no kanshiin no shigoto ni oubo shita n da yo.","german":"That guy applied to be a lifeguard at the municipal pool only because he can see girls in bikinis there.","furigana":"すがた|み|りゆう|しみん|かんしいん|しごと|おうぼ"},{"japanese":"写真コンテストに応募したら、なんと入賞した。","romaji":"Shashin kontesuto ni oubo shitara, nanto nyuushou shita.","german":"I entered a photo contest and actually won a prize.","furigana":"しゃしん|おうぼ|にゅうしょう"},{"japanese":"新商品が当たるキャンペーンに、はがきで応募した。","romaji":"Shinshouhin ga ataru kyanpeen ni, hagaki de oubo shita.","german":"I took part in a prize draw by postcard where you can win a new product.","furigana":"しんしょうひん|あ|おうぼ"}],"notes":"応募する means \"to apply in response to a call, to take part, to send in an entry\" and takes に: 仕事に応募する, コンテストに応募する, 懸賞に応募する. It is the response to a 募集 \"call for applicants, recruitment\" from the other side. Derived are 応募者 \"applicant\", 応募用紙 and 応募締め切り. 申し込む is more general, \"to sign up, to apply for\" (courses, trips), whereas 応募 presupposes that someone is looking for applicants or entries.","levelNote":"Derived from 応募 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日は彼の二百本目の本塁打を見るのが楽しみでしょうがないよ。","romaji":"Kyou wa kare no nihyakuhonme no honruida o miru no ga tanoshimi de shou ga nai yo.","german":"I can't wait to see his 200th home run today.","furigana":"きょう|かれ|にひゃくほんめ|ほんるいだ|み|たの"},{"japanese":"彼は九回裏に逆転の本塁打を放った。","romaji":"Kare wa kyuukai ura ni gyakuten no honruida o hanatta.","german":"In the bottom of the ninth, he hit a game-turning home run.","furigana":"かれ|きゅうかいうら|ぎゃくてん|ほんるいだ|はな"},{"japanese":"シーズン中に最も多く本塁打を打った選手が、本塁打王になる。","romaji":"Shiizunchuu ni mottomo ooku honruida o utta senshu ga, honruidaou ni naru.","german":"The player who hits the most home runs in a season becomes the home run king.","furigana":"ちゅう|もっと|おお|ほんるいだ|う|せんしゅ|ほんるいだおう"}],"notes":"本塁打 is the official, written expression for the home run and is typical of newspapers and statistics; in conversation people say ホームラン. 本塁 is \"home plate\", 打 is the \"hit\". Typical: 本塁打を打つ / 放つ, 満塁本塁打 \"grand slam\", 本塁打王 \"home run king\". It is counted with 本: 二百本目の本塁打.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"すみません、もう不要になったのでSuicaの残高を払い戻したいのですが...。","romaji":"Sumimasen, mō fuyō ni natta node Suica no zandaka o haraimodoshitai no desu ga....","german":"Excuse me, I no longer need my Suica, so I'd like to get the balance refunded...","furigana":"ふよう|ざんだか|はら|もど"},{"japanese":"コンサートが中止になったので、チケット代が払い戻された。","romaji":"Konsāto ga chūshi ni natta node, chiketto-dai ga haraimodosareta.","german":"Because the concert was canceled, the ticket price was refunded.","furigana":"ちゅうし|だい|はら|もど"},{"japanese":"不良品の場合は、代金を全額払い戻します。","romaji":"Furyōhin no baai wa, daikin o zengaku haraimodoshimasu.","german":"In the case of defective goods, we will refund the full price.","furigana":"ふりょうひん|ばあい|だいきん|ぜんがくはら|もど"}],"notes":"払い戻す means \"to refund (money)\" and is transitive: the subject is the one who pays out (company, railway, organizer): チケット代を払い戻す. From the customer's point of view, people say 払い戻してもらう or 払い戻しを受ける; very common is the noun 払い戻し \"refund\" (払い戻し窓口, 払い戻し手続き). The tone is matter-of-fact and administrative; in everyday speech people often say 返金する. General returning is 返す.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの人は言葉遣いがとてもていねいです。","romaji":"Ano hito wa kotobazukai ga totemo teinei desu.","german":"That person is very polite in the way he speaks.","furigana":"ひと|ことばづか"},{"japanese":"面接では、言葉遣いに気をつけたほうがいい。","romaji":"Mensetsu de wa, kotobazukai ni ki o tsuketa hou ga ii.","german":"In an interview, you should watch your language.","furigana":"めんせつ|ことばづか|き"},{"japanese":"息子の言葉遣いが最近乱暴になってきて、心配だ。","romaji":"Musuko no kotobazukai ga saikin ranbou ni natte kite, shinpai da.","german":"My son's way of speaking has become rough lately, and I'm worried.","furigana":"むすこ|ことばづか|さいきんらんぼう|しんぱい"}],"notes":"言葉遣い is the way someone speaks, especially with regard to politeness and tone: 言葉遣いが丁寧だ / 乱暴だ / 悪い, 言葉遣いに気をつける \"to watch one's language\". So it is less about the choice of individual words (for that rather 言葉選び or 表現) than about register and manners. The suffix 〜遣い is read with rendaku づかい, as in 小遣い or 無駄遣い.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お子さんには月々お小遣いはいくら渡しているんですか。","romaji":"Okosan ni wa tsukizuki okozukai wa ikura watashite iru n desu ka.","german":"How much allowance do you give your child per month?","furigana":"こ|つきづき|こづか|わた"},{"japanese":"夫の小遣いは月に三万円です。","romaji":"Otto no kozukai wa tsuki ni sanman-en desu.","german":"My husband's allowance is 30,000 yen a month.","furigana":"おっと|こづか|つき|さんまんえん"},{"japanese":"大学生の頃は、家庭教師をして小遣いを稼いでいた。","romaji":"Daigakusei no koro wa, katei kyoushi o shite kozukai o kaseide ita.","german":"When I was a university student, I earned some pocket money by tutoring.","furigana":"だいがくせい|ころ|かていきょうし|こづか|かせ"}],"notes":"小遣い is money at one's free disposal and is mostly used with お as お小遣い. It applies not only to children: the monthly \"allowance\" a husband gets from the family budget is also called this. Typical: 小遣いをもらう / あげる / 渡す, 小遣い稼ぎ \"earning a bit on the side\", 小遣い帳 \"pocket money notebook\". It is read with rendaku こづかい.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この掲示板は学校のものです。","romaji":"Kono keijiban wa gakkō no mono desu.","german":"This bulletin board belongs to the school.","furigana":"けいじばん|がっこう"},{"japanese":"試験の結果は、明日の朝、掲示板に貼り出されます。","romaji":"Shiken no kekka wa, ashita no asa, keijiban ni haridasaremasu.","german":"The exam results will be posted on the bulletin board tomorrow morning.","furigana":"しけん|けっか|あした|あさ|けいじばん|は|だ"},{"japanese":"ネットの掲示板に、その店の悪い評判が書き込まれていた。","romaji":"Netto no keijiban ni, sono mise no warui hyouban ga kakikomarete ita.","german":"Bad reviews of that shop had been posted on an online forum.","furigana":"けいじばん|みせ|わる|ひょうばん|か|こ"}],"notes":"掲示板 is the bulletin board or notice board in schools, train stations or residential complexes: 掲示板に貼る / 貼り出す \"to post\", 掲示板を見る. Just as often it refers to an online forum (ネットの掲示板) where you write posts (書き込む). A single notice is called 掲示 or お知らせ.","levelNote":"Compound: 掲示 (N3) + 板 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"そいつ、パソコンオタクではあるけど、見た目はイケメンで、今彼女募集中なんだ。どう、会ってみない？","romaji":"Soitsu, pasokon otaku de wa aru kedo, mitame wa ikemen de, ima kanojo boshūchū na n da. Dō, atte minai?","german":"He is a computer geek, but he is good-looking, and he's looking for a girlfriend right now. How about it, want to meet him?","furigana":"み|め|いまかのじょぼしゅうちゅう|あ"},{"japanese":"コンビニの入り口に「アルバイト募集中」と書いてあった。","romaji":"Konbini no iriguchi ni \"arubaito boshuuchuu\" to kaite atta.","german":"A sign saying \"Part-time help wanted\" was posted at the entrance of the convenience store.","furigana":"い|ぐち|ぼしゅうちゅう|か"},{"japanese":"このサークルでは、新しいメンバーを募集中です。","romaji":"Kono saakuru de wa, atarashii menbaa o boshuuchuu desu.","german":"This club is currently recruiting new members.","furigana":"あたら|ぼしゅうちゅう"}],"notes":"募集中 is composed of 募集 \"call for applicants, recruitment\" and 〜中 \"currently in progress\" and means \"currently recruiting, applications welcome\". Typical on notices: アルバイト募集中, 会員募集中, 参加者募集中; also with an object: 新しいメンバーを募集中です. Jokingly and colloquially one says 彼女募集中 / 彼氏募集中 \"looking for a partner\". A person who responds to it 応募する.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 33"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"戻り道がどれだか分からなかったんです。","romaji":"Modorimichi ga dore da ka wakaranakatta n desu.","german":"I didn't know which way was the way back.","furigana":"もど|みち|わ"},{"japanese":"戻り道が分かるように、木に目印を付けながら進んだ。","romaji":"Modorimichi ga wakaru you ni, ki ni mejirushi o tsukenagara susunda.","german":"So that we could find our way back, we marked the trees as we went.","furigana":"もど|みち|わ|き|めじるし|つ|すす"},{"japanese":"戻り道は下り坂なので、行きよりずっと楽だった。","romaji":"Modorimichi wa kudarizaka na node, iki yori zutto raku datta.","german":"The way back was downhill, so it was much easier than the way there.","furigana":"もど|みち|くだ|ざか|い|らく"}],"notes":"戻り道 is the way back to the starting point, for example after a hike or a detour. In everyday speech, 帰り道 \"way home\" is much more common; 戻り道 emphasizes returning to a place where one was before. The counterpart is 行き / 行きの道 \"way there\". Figuratively: もう戻り道はない \"there is no turning back\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コンピュータを出荷時の設定に初期化することでこの問題は解決しますが、バックアップがなければ失われたデータを取り戻すことはできません。","romaji":"Konpyuuta o shukkaji no settei ni shokika suru koto de kono mondai wa kaiketsu shimasu ga, bakkuappu ga nakereba ushinawareta deeta o torimodosu koto wa dekimasen.","german":"You can solve this problem by resetting the computer to factory settings, but without a backup you can't recover the lost data.","furigana":"しゅっかじ|せってい|しょきか|もんだい|かいけつ|うしな|と|もど"},{"japanese":"休みの間にゆっくり休んで、元気を取り戻した。","romaji":"Yasumi no aida ni yukkuri yasunde, genki o torimodoshita.","german":"I rested well during the holidays and got my strength back.","furigana":"やす|あいだ|やす|げんき|と|もど"},{"japanese":"一度失った信頼を取り戻すのは、簡単ではない。","romaji":"Ichido ushinatta shinrai o torimodosu no wa, kantan de wa nai.","german":"Regaining lost trust is not easy.","furigana":"いちどうしな|しんらい|と|もど|かんたん"}],"notes":"取り戻す means \"to get back, to regain\" and takes を. Besides objects or money (盗まれたお金を取り戻す), it is very often used abstractly: 自信 / 元気 / 落ち着き / 信頼を取り戻す, 遅れを取り戻す \"to make up for a delay\". 取り返す is similar but emphasizes more the active retrieving of something taken away; the expression 取り返しがつかない \"irreparable\" exists only with 取り返す.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の家は火災保険に入っていますが、もし誰かが原子爆弾を落として、それで家が燃えちゃった場合はどうなるんでしょうか。それでも保険金は降りるんでしょうか。","romaji":"Watashi no ie wa kasai hoken ni haitte imasu ga, moshi dare ka ga genshi bakudan o otoshite, sorede ie ga moechatta baai wa dou naru n deshou ka. Soredemo hokenkin wa oriru n deshou ka.","german":"My house is insured against fire, but what happens if someone drops an atomic bomb and my house burns down as a result? Will I still get the insurance money?","furigana":"わたし|いえ|かさいほけん|はい|だれ|げんしばくだん|お|いえ|も|ばあい|ほけんきん|お"},{"japanese":"1945年8月6日、広島に原子爆弾が投下された。","romaji":"1945-nen hachigatsu muika, Hiroshima ni genshi bakudan ga touka sareta.","german":"On August 6, 1945, an atomic bomb was dropped on Hiroshima.","furigana":"ねん|がつ|か|ひろしま|げんしばくだん|とうか"},{"japanese":"修学旅行で原爆ドームを訪れ、原子爆弾の恐ろしさを学んだ。","romaji":"Shuugaku ryokou de Genbaku Doomu o otozure, genshi bakudan no osoroshisa o mananda.","german":"On the school trip we visited the Atomic Bomb Dome and learned how terrible the atomic bomb is.","furigana":"しゅうがくりょこう|げんばく|おとず|げんしばくだん|おそ|まな"}],"notes":"原子爆弾 is the atomic bomb; in daily life and the news it is almost always abbreviated to 原爆 (原爆ドーム, 原爆の日). Typical are 原子爆弾を落とす / 投下する and the passive 原子爆弾が投下された. The umbrella term is 核兵器 \"nuclear weapons\", the hydrogen bomb is called 水素爆弾 or 水爆. The word is a noun, not an adjective.","levelNote":"Compound: 原子 (N1) + 爆弾 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"雪の上に熊の足跡を見つけた瞬間、ポケットの中のナイフを握りしめました。","romaji":"Yuki no ue ni kuma no ashiato o mitsuketa shunkan, poketto no naka no naifu o nigirishimemashita.","german":"The moment I found bear tracks in the snow, I tightly gripped the knife in my pocket.","furigana":"ゆき|うえ|くま|あしあと|み|しゅんかん|なか|にぎ"},{"japanese":"警察は犯人の足跡をたどって、裏山に向かった。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa hannin no ashiato o tadotte, urayama ni mukatta.","german":"The police followed the culprit's footprints toward the hill behind the house.","furigana":"けいさつ|はんにん|あしあと|うらやま|む"},{"japanese":"浜辺に残った子どもたちの小さな足跡を、波が消していった。","romaji":"Hamabe ni nokotta kodomotachi no chiisana ashiato o, nami ga keshite itta.","german":"The waves washed away the children's small footprints left on the beach.","furigana":"はまべ|のこ|こ|ちい|あしあと|なみ|け"}],"notes":"足跡（あしあと）are footprints of people or animals: 足跡が残る, 足跡を見つける, 足跡をたどる \"to follow the tracks\". On social networks it also refers to the list of profile visitors (足跡機能). In the figurative sense \"to leave traces, achievements\" (歴史に足跡を残す), the same spelling is often read そくせき and is then literary.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一応忠告しておくと、奴はお前のポジションを狙ってるみたいだぞ。","romaji":"Ichiou chuukoku shite oku to, yatsu wa omae no pojishon o neratteru mitai da zo.","german":"Just a warning: it looks like he's after your position.","furigana":"いちおうちゅうこく|やつ|まえ|ねら"},{"japanese":"今年こそ優勝を狙って、毎日練習を続けている。","romaji":"Kotoshi koso yuushou o neratte, mainichi renshuu o tsuzukete iru.","german":"This year we're going for the title, so we keep practicing every day.","furigana":"ことし|ゆうしょう|ねら|まいにちれんしゅう|つづ"},{"japanese":"観光客の財布を狙うすりに気をつけてください。","romaji":"Kankoukyaku no saifu o nerau suri ni ki o tsukete kudasai.","german":"Beware of pickpockets who target tourists' wallets.","furigana":"かんこうきゃく|さいふ|ねら|き"}],"notes":"狙う takes を and means \"to aim at\" (的を狙う), \"to have one's eye on something or someone\" (財布を狙う, ポジションを狙う) and \"to aim for deliberately\" (優勝を狙う). In contrast to 目指す \"to aim for a goal\", 狙う sounds more strategic and can also express a dishonest intention. The related noun is 狙い \"aim, objective\" (狙いを定める \"to take aim\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ロード・オブ・ザ・リングの邦訳、もう図書館に返却しちゃった？","romaji":"Roodo obu za ringu no houyaku, mou toshokan ni henkyaku shichatta?","german":"Hast du die japanische Übersetzung von Herr der Ringe schon in die Bibliothek zurückgebracht?","furigana":"ほうやく|としょかん|へんきゃく"},{"japanese":"この小説はまだ邦訳されていないので、英語で読むしかない。","romaji":"Kono shousetsu wa mada houyaku sarete inai node, eigo de yomu shika nai.","german":"Dieser Roman ist noch nicht ins Japanische übersetzt, also muss ich ihn auf Englisch lesen.","furigana":"しょうせつ|ほうやく|えいご|よ"},{"japanese":"彼女はドイツの児童書を数多く邦訳してきた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa Doitsu no jidousho o kazuooku houyaku shite kita.","german":"Sie hat zahlreiche deutsche Kinderbücher ins Japanische übertragen.","furigana":"かのじょ|じどうしょ|かずおお|ほうやく"}],"notes":"邦訳 ist die Übersetzung ins Japanische, besonders von Büchern und Fachliteratur; 邦 steht hier für „Japan“ wie in 邦画 und 邦楽. Als する-Verb ist es transitiv und erscheint oft im Passiv: まだ邦訳されていない. Dazu gehören 邦訳版 „japanische Ausgabe“ und 邦題 „japanischer Titel“. 和訳 ist gleichbedeutend, wird aber eher für Übungen im Unterricht gebraucht (英文和訳); allgemein heißt Übersetzen 翻訳.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ちょっと待って。もしかして、連邦税と州税どっちも払わなきゃいけないの？","romaji":"Chotto matte. Moshikashite, renpō zei to shū zei dotchi mo harawanakya ikenai no?","german":"Moment mal. Muss ich etwa sowohl Bundes- als auch Landessteuern zahlen?","furigana":"ま|れんぽうぜい|しゅうぜい|はら"},{"japanese":"ドイツは十六の州からなる連邦国家です。","romaji":"Doitsu wa juuroku no shuu kara naru renpou kokka desu.","german":"Deutschland ist ein Bundesstaat aus sechzehn Ländern.","furigana":"じゅうろく|しゅう|れんぽうこっか"},{"japanese":"アメリカの連邦政府は、首都ワシントンにある。","romaji":"Amerika no renpou seifu wa, shuto Washinton ni aru.","german":"Die US-Bundesregierung hat ihren Sitz in der Hauptstadt Washington.","furigana":"れんぽうせいふ|しゅと"}],"notes":"連邦 bezeichnet einen Bundesstaat bzw. eine Föderation und steht meist als erstes Glied in Zusammensetzungen: 連邦政府 „Bundesregierung“, 連邦制 „Föderalismus“, 連邦税 „Bundessteuer“, 連邦議会. In Staatsnamen erscheint es z. B. in ドイツ連邦共和国 und ロシア連邦. Nicht verwechseln mit 連合 „Bündnis, Union“ (欧州連合 „EU“). Es ist ein Nomen, kein Adjektiv.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大便の最中に呼び鈴が鳴り、男はお尻も拭かずに玄関へ急いだ。","romaji":"Daiben no saichuu ni yobirin ga nari, otoko wa oshiri mo fukazu ni genkan e isoida.","german":"The doorbell rang while he was on the toilet, and he rushed to the door without wiping himself.","furigana":"だいべん|さいちゅう|よ|りん|な|おとこ|しり|ふ|げんかん|いそ"},{"japanese":"玄関の呼び鈴を押したが、誰も出てこなかった。","romaji":"Genkan no yobirin o oshita ga, dare mo dete konakatta.","german":"I pressed the doorbell at the front door, but nobody came out.","furigana":"げんかん|よ|りん|お|だれ|で"},{"japanese":"フロントの呼び鈴を鳴らすと、すぐに係の人が来た。","romaji":"Furonto no yobirin o narasu to, sugu ni kakari no hito ga kita.","german":"When I rang the bell at the front desk, a staff member came right away.","furigana":"よ|りん|な|かかり|ひと|き"}],"notes":"呼び鈴 is a bell with which you call someone, above all the doorbell: 呼び鈴を鳴らす / 押す, 呼び鈴が鳴る. It sounds somewhat old-fashioned; today, for the front door, people mostly say インターホン or チャイム. The desk bell at a reception can also be called 呼び鈴; in restaurants people rather speak of a 呼び出しボタン.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"週の後半に岐阜の両親のとこに顔を見せに行くつもりだよ。","romaji":"Shū no kōhan ni Gifu no ryōshin no toko ni kao o mise ni iku tsumori da yo.","german":"I plan to go and see my parents in Gifu later in the week.","furigana":"しゅう|こうはん|ぎふ|りょうしん|かお|み|い"},{"japanese":"岐阜県の白川郷は、合掌造りの家で有名です。","romaji":"Gifu-ken no Shirakawagou wa, gasshouzukuri no ie de yuumei desu.","german":"Shirakawa-gō in Gifu Prefecture is famous for its gasshō-style houses.","furigana":"ぎふけん||がっしょうづく|いえ|ゆうめい"},{"japanese":"夏の長良川では、岐阜の名物の鵜飼いが見られる。","romaji":"Natsu no Nagaragawa de wa, Gifu no meibutsu no ukai ga mirareru.","german":"In summer, you can see ukai, Gifu's specialty cormorant fishing, on the Nagara River.","furigana":"なつ||ぎふ|めいぶつ||み"}],"notes":"岐阜 is both the prefecture (岐阜県) and its capital (岐阜市) in the 中部地方 region of central Japan. Well known are the World Heritage site 白川郷 with its houses in 合掌造り style, the old town of 高山 and the cormorant fishing 鵜飼い on the river 長良川. The character 阜 appears almost only in this name and is one of the few place-name kanji on the school list.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"アメリカで今、最も隆盛を極めている産業は何ですか。","romaji":"Amerika de ima, mottomo ryuusei o kiwamete iru sangyou wa nan desu ka.","german":"Which industry in the US is flourishing the most right now?","furigana":"いま|もっと|りゅうせい|きわ|さんぎょう|なん"},{"japanese":"江戸時代には、歌舞伎が隆盛を極めた。","romaji":"Edo jidai ni wa, kabuki ga ryuusei o kiwameta.","german":"In the Edo period, kabuki reached its heyday.","furigana":"えどじだい|かぶき|りゅうせい|きわ"},{"japanese":"かつて隆盛を誇った炭鉱の町も、今では人がほとんど住んでいない。","romaji":"Katsute ryuusei o hokotta tankou no machi mo, ima de wa hito ga hotondo sunde inai.","german":"The mining town that once flourished now has hardly anyone living there.","furigana":"りゅうせい|ほこ|たんこう|まち|いま|ひと|す"}],"notes":"隆盛 is the heyday or boom of a culture, industry, religion or house; the word is written. It almost always appears in fixed expressions: 隆盛を極める \"to reach its peak\", 隆盛を誇る \"to be in full bloom\", 〜の隆盛. 繁栄 emphasizes the prosperity of states or cities, 繁盛 the good business of a shop, and in everyday speech people say 盛んだ. It is used practically only as a noun.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いつも壁紙はアヤのイラストにしています。","romaji":"Itsumo kabegami wa Aya no irasuto ni shite imasu.","german":"I always use Aya's illustrations as my wallpaper.","furigana":"かべがみ"},{"japanese":"引っ越す前に、部屋の壁紙を張り替えた。","romaji":"Hikkosu mae ni, heya no kabegami o harikaeta.","german":"Before moving, I replaced the wallpaper in the room.","furigana":"ひ|こ|まえ|へや|かべがみ|は|か"},{"japanese":"パソコンの壁紙を家族の写真に変えた。","romaji":"Pasokon no kabegami o kazoku no shashin ni kaeta.","german":"I changed my computer's wallpaper to a family photo.","furigana":"かべがみ|かぞく|しゃしん|か"}],"notes":"壁紙 is the wallpaper on a wall (壁紙を貼る, 壁紙を張り替える) and likewise the background image on a computer or smartphone (壁紙にする, 壁紙を変える). For the lock screen of a mobile phone, people also colloquially say 待ち受け. The reading has rendaku: かべがみ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その棋士は、この建物に自分専用の練習部屋を構えています。","romaji":"Sono kishi wa, kono tatemono ni jibun sen'yō no renshū heya o kamaete imasu.","german":"That shogi player has his own private practice room in this building.","furigana":"きし|たてもの|じぶんせんよう|れんしゅうへや|かま"},{"japanese":"藤井さんは、十四歳でプロの棋士になった。","romaji":"Fujii-san wa, juuyon-sai de puro no kishi ni natta.","german":"Mr. Fujii became a professional shogi player at the age of fourteen.","furigana":"ふじい|じゅうよんさい|きし"},{"japanese":"将棋の棋士は、一つの対局で何時間も考え続けることがある。","romaji":"Shougi no kishi wa, hitotsu no taikyoku de nanjikan mo kangaetsuzukeru koto ga aru.","german":"Shogi professionals sometimes keep thinking for hours in a single game.","furigana":"しょうぎ|きし|ひと|たいきょく|なんじかん|かんが|つづ"}],"notes":"棋士 is a professional shogi or go player; 棋 stands for board games as in 将棋 and 棋院. Depending on context, people say 将棋の棋士 or 囲碁の棋士, plus プロ棋士 and 女流棋士 \"female professional player\". Hobby players are not called 棋士 but rather 将棋ファン or 愛好家. Not to be confused with 騎士 \"knight\", which is read the same way.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"毎週月曜日にこの棋院に来て、囲碁を練習しています。","romaji":"Maishū getsuyōbi ni kono kiin ni kite, igo o renshū shite imasu.","german":"I come to this go hall every Monday and practice go.","furigana":"まいしゅうげつようび|きいん|き|いご|れんしゅう"},{"japanese":"日本棋院は、囲碁のプロ棋士が所属する団体です。","romaji":"Nihon Kiin wa, igo no puro kishi ga shozoku suru dantai desu.","german":"The Nihon Ki-in is the organization that professional go players belong to.","furigana":"にほんきいん|いご|きし|しょぞく|だんたい"},{"japanese":"棋院で行われたタイトル戦を、インターネットで見た。","romaji":"Kiin de okonawareta taitorusen o, intaanetto de mita.","german":"I watched the title match held at the Ki-in on the internet.","furigana":"きいん|おこな|せん|み"}],"notes":"棋院 is a go association or go hall where games are played and tournaments held. The best known is the 日本棋院 in Tokyo, the association of professional go players; besides it there is the 関西棋院. For shogi, the corresponding institutions are called 将棋会館 and 日本将棋連盟. A small public go club in the neighborhood is rather called 碁会所.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"片目だけ視力が悪いんです。","romaji":"Katame dake shiryoku ga warui n desu.","german":"My eyesight is bad in only one eye.","furigana":"かため|しりょく|わる"},{"japanese":"荷物が重くて、片手では持てません。","romaji":"Nimotsu ga omokute, katate de wa motemasen.","german":"The luggage is heavy; I can't carry it with one hand.","furigana":"にもつ|おも|かたて|も"}],"notes":"片〜 here refers to one part of a pair: 片目 is one eye, 片手 one hand. It stands directly before the component in question, without の. In the first example, the limited eyesight affects only one eye; in the second, one hand is not enough for carrying. The character 〜 is an unspoken placeholder mark. This card practices the prefix かた and not the other readings ひら or へん for a piece or component.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は重いお米の袋を片手でヒョイと持ち上げました。","romaji":"Kare wa omoi okome no fukuro o katate de hyoi to mochiagemashita.","german":"He lifted the heavy bag of rice with one hand, effortlessly.","furigana":"かれ|おも|こめ|ふくろ|かたて|も|あ"},{"japanese":"彼はコーヒーを片手に、新聞を読んでいた。","romaji":"Kare wa koohii o katate ni, shinbun o yonde ita.","german":"He was reading the newspaper with a coffee in one hand.","furigana":"かれ|かたて|しんぶん|よ"},{"japanese":"運転中に片手でスマホを操作するのは危険だ。","romaji":"Untenchuu ni katate de sumaho o sousa suru no wa kiken da.","german":"Operating a smartphone with one hand while driving is dangerous.","furigana":"うんてんちゅう|かたて|そうさ|きけん"}],"notes":"片手 means \"one hand\" as opposed to 両手 \"both hands\": 片手で持つ, 片手に〜を持つ. Very idiomatic is 〜を片手に \"with ... in hand\" (コーヒーを片手に, 地図を片手に). Derived from it is 片手間 \"on the side\" (片手間にできる仕事). The word is a noun, not an adjective.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は片言の日本語でなんとかパスポートの再発行をしてもらいました。","romaji":"Watashi wa katakoto no nihongo de nantoka pasupōto no sai hakkō o shite moraimashita.","german":"With my broken Japanese, I somehow managed to get my passport reissued.","furigana":"わたし|かたこと|にほんご|さいはっこう"},{"japanese":"一歳の娘が、片言で「ママ」と言うようになった。","romaji":"Issai no musume ga, katakoto de \"mama\" to iu you ni natta.","german":"My one-year-old daughter has started saying \"mama\" in her baby talk.","furigana":"いっさい|むすめ|かたこと|い"},{"japanese":"旅行先では、片言の英語でもなんとか通じた。","romaji":"Ryokousaki de wa, katakoto no eigo demo nantoka tsuujita.","german":"On the trip I could somehow communicate even in broken English.","furigana":"りょこうさき|かたこと|えいご|つう"}],"notes":"片言 is broken, halting speech, as spoken by foreigners with little knowledge or by small children: 片言の日本語 / 英語, 片言で話す. For small children it means the first incomplete words (片言を話し始める). It is a noun, mostly used with の or で, not an adjective.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"平仮名は分かりますが、片仮名はまだ覚えきれていません。","romaji":"Hiragana wa wakarimasu ga, katakana wa mada oboekirete imasen.","german":"I know hiragana, but I haven't fully learned katakana yet.","furigana":"ひらがな|わ|かたかな|おぼ"},{"japanese":"外国人の名前は、ふつう片仮名で書きます。","romaji":"Gaikokujin no namae wa, futsuu katakana de kakimasu.","german":"Foreigners' names are normally written in katakana.","furigana":"がいこくじん|なまえ|かたかな|か"},{"japanese":"説明書に片仮名の言葉が多すぎて、祖父には読みにくいらしい。","romaji":"Setsumeisho ni katakana no kotoba ga oosugite, sofu ni wa yominikui rashii.","german":"The manual has so many katakana words that it seems hard for my grandfather to read.","furigana":"せつめいしょ|かたかな|ことば|おお|そふ|よ"}],"notes":"片仮名 is the angular syllabary used mainly for loanwords, foreign names, onomatopoeia and for emphasis. The word itself is usually written in kana: カタカナ. The counterpart is 平仮名 / ひらがな. Typical: 片仮名で書く, 片仮名語 \"words in katakana, loanwords\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"群馬県の実家から帰ってきたら、知らせてくれよな。","romaji":"Gunmaken no jikka kara kaette kitara, shirasete kure yo na.","german":"Let me know when you get back from your parents' house in Gunma Prefecture.","furigana":"ぐんまけん|じっか|かえ|し"},{"japanese":"群馬県の草津温泉は、日本有数の温泉地です。","romaji":"Gunma-ken no Kusatsu Onsen wa, Nihon yuusuu no onsenchi desu.","german":"Kusatsu Onsen in Gunma Prefecture is one of Japan's leading hot spring resorts.","furigana":"ぐんまけん|くさつおんせん|にほんゆうすう|おんせんち"},{"japanese":"群馬県へは、東京から新幹線で一時間ほどで行ける。","romaji":"Gunma-ken e wa, Toukyou kara shinkansen de ichijikan hodo de ikeru.","german":"You can get to Gunma Prefecture from Tokyo in about an hour by Shinkansen.","furigana":"ぐんまけん|とうきょう|しんかんせん|いちじかん|い"}],"notes":"群馬県 is a landlocked prefecture in the 関東 region northwest of Tokyo; its capital is 前橋市. Well known are the hot springs of 草津温泉 and the silk mill 富岡製糸場 (World Heritage site). In conversation people often say just 群馬; the suffix 〜県 makes clear that the prefecture is meant.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"小さな男の子がジャスティン・ビーバーの物真似をし出したので、群集は大笑いをしました。","romaji":"Chiisana otokonoko ga Jasutin Biibaa no monomane o shidashita node, gunshuu wa oowarai o shimashita.","german":"The crowd burst into laughter when the little boy started imitating Justin Bieber.","furigana":"ちい|おとこ|こ|ものまね|だ|ぐんしゅう|おおわら"},{"japanese":"花火が終わると、群集が一斉に駅へ向かった。","romaji":"Hanabi ga owaru to, gunshuu ga issei ni eki e mukatta.","german":"After the fireworks, the crowd streamed to the station all at once.","furigana":"はなび|お|ぐんしゅう|いっせい|えき|む"},{"japanese":"群集心理で、人は普段しないような行動をとることがある。","romaji":"Gunshuu shinri de, hito wa fudan shinai you na koudou o toru koto ga aru.","german":"Out of mass psychology, people sometimes do things they would never otherwise do.","furigana":"ぐんしゅうしんり|ひと|ふだん|こうどう"}],"notes":"群集 is a large gathering, above all a crowd of people; for people, however, the spelling 群衆 (same reading) is much more common. 群集 is usual in 群集心理 \"mass psychology\" and in biology (community). As a する-verb it is intransitive, \"to crowd together, to swarm\". In daily life one rather says 人込み \"crowd, crush\" or 人だかり \"gathering of people\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「わしゃ、お前の真上におるぞ。」と、仙人が雲の上から言いました。","romaji":"\"Washa, omae no maue ni oru zo.\" to, sennin ga kumo no ue kara iimashita.","german":"\"I'm right above you,\" said the hermit from atop a cloud.","furigana":"まえ|まうえ|せんにん|くも|うえ|い"},{"japanese":"山奥で一人で暮らしている祖父は、まるで仙人のようだ。","romaji":"Yamaoku de hitori de kurashite iru sofu wa, marude sennin no yō da.","german":"My grandfather, who lives alone deep in the mountains, is just like a hermit.","furigana":"やまおく|ひとり|く|そふ|せんにん"},{"japanese":"仙人は霞を食べて生きていると言われている。","romaji":"Sennin wa kasumi o tabete ikite iru to iwarete iru.","german":"It is said that hermit immortals live on mist.","furigana":"せんにん|かすみ|た|い|い"}],"notes":"仙人 comes from Taoism: a sage who lives in seclusion in the mountains, possesses supernatural powers and does not age - often depicted as an old man with a white beard riding on clouds. Figuratively, someone who lives far from worldly desires and money is called 仙人: 仙人のような生活. A hermit without magical connotation is called 世捨て人. Well known is the saying that 仙人 live on mist (霞を食べて生きる).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お墓の真後ろに、水仙の花々が咲いていました。","romaji":"O haka no maushiro ni, suisen no hanabana ga saite imashita.","german":"Right behind the grave, narcissus flowers were blooming.","furigana":"はか|まうし|すいせん|はなばな|さ"},{"japanese":"冬の海岸に水仙がたくさん咲いていて、いい香りがしました。","romaji":"Fuyu no kaigan ni suisen ga takusan saite ite, ii kaori ga shimashita.","german":"Many narcissus were blooming on the winter coast and smelled lovely.","furigana":"ふゆ|かいがん|すいせん|さ|かお"},{"japanese":"水仙の球根には毒があるので、ペットに食べさせないでください。","romaji":"Suisen no kyuukon ni wa doku ga aru node, petto ni tabesasenaide kudasai.","german":"Narcissus bulbs are poisonous, so please don't let your pets eat them.","furigana":"すいせん|きゅうこん|どく|た"}],"notes":"水仙 is the narcissus; in Japan it is mainly the white-and-yellow tazetta that blooms in winter to early spring, often in large fields on the coast. People say 水仙が咲く, 水仙の花 and 水仙の香り. The trumpet daffodil with its large trumpet is called ラッパ水仙. In biology and gardening texts the plant is often written in katakana as スイセン; in everyday speech and literature 水仙 is usual.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仙台に行くまでに地図を買っておきます。","romaji":"Sendai ni iku made ni chizu o katte okimasu.","german":"I will buy a map before I go to Sendai.","furigana":"せんだい|い|ちず|か"},{"japanese":"仙台は牛タンが有名なので、ぜひ食べてみてください。","romaji":"Sendai wa gyuutan ga yuumei na node, zehi tabete mite kudasai.","german":"Sendai is famous for its beef tongue, so please be sure to try it.","furigana":"せんだい|ぎゅう|ゆうめい|た"},{"japanese":"東京から仙台までは、新幹線で一時間半ぐらいです。","romaji":"Toukyou kara Sendai made wa, shinkansen de ichijikan han gurai desu.","german":"From Tokyo to Sendai it takes about an hour and a half by Shinkansen.","furigana":"とうきょう|せんだい|しんかんせん|いちじかんはん"}],"notes":"仙台 is the largest city of the 東北 region and the capital of 宮城県. The city has the nickname 杜の都 \"City of Trees\" and is known for grilled beef tongue (牛タン) and the star festival 仙台七夕. In conversation one simply says 仙台; officially the city is called 仙台市; with directional particles: 仙台に行く, 仙台から来た, 仙台出身.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 40"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"帰る前にプリンタの紙を補充しておいてもらえるかな。","romaji":"Kaeru mae ni purinta no kami o hojū shite oite moraeru ka na.","german":"Could you refill the printer paper before you leave?","furigana":"かえ|まえ|かみ|ほじゅう"},{"japanese":"一人辞めたので、アルバイトを一人補充しなければならない。","romaji":"Hitori yameta node, arubaito o hitori hojuu shinakereba naranai.","german":"Because someone quit, we have to fill a part-time position.","furigana":"ひとりや|ひとりほじゅう"},{"japanese":"棚の商品が少なくなったら、すぐに補充してください。","romaji":"Tana no shouhin ga sukunaku nattara, sugu ni hojuu shite kudasai.","german":"When goods on the shelf run low, please restock immediately.","furigana":"たな|しょうひん|すく|ほじゅう"}],"notes":"補充 means to fill up something missing or used up to the intended amount or number again: 紙を補充する, 在庫を補充する, 人員を補充する (to fill staff positions). Transitive with を. To be distinguished is 補給: this is the ongoing supply of something that is consumed, e.g. 水分補給 or 燃料補給. 補足, by contrast, is a supplementary explanation of what has been said.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今度お会いする際に免状をお渡ししますね。","romaji":"Kondo o ai suru sai ni menjō o o watashi shimasu ne.","german":"I'll hand you the certificate the next time we meet.","furigana":"こんど|あ|さい|めんじょう|わた"},{"japanese":"茶道を十年習って、やっと免状をもらいました。","romaji":"Sadou o juunen naratte, yatto menjou o moraimashita.","german":"After studying the tea ceremony for ten years, I finally received my certificate.","furigana":"さどう|じゅうねんなら|めんじょう"},{"japanese":"危険物を扱う仕事をするには、国の免状が必要です。","romaji":"Kikenbutsu o atsukau shigoto o suru ni wa, kuni no menjou ga hitsuyou desu.","german":"To work with hazardous materials, a national license is required.","furigana":"きけんぶつ|あつか|しごと|くに|めんじょう|ひつよう"}],"notes":"免状 is a somewhat old-fashioned, formal term for a certificate confirming an authorization or rank. Today it is heard mainly in traditional arts (茶道や華道の免状, 師範の免状) and for some state qualification certificates. For a driver's license, people say 免許証, for a graduation certificate 卒業証書, and for general certificates 証明書. Typical: 免状をもらう, 免状を取る, 免状を渡す.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ごめん。さすがに今日の今日で、空港まで迎えに行くのは無理だわ。もうちょっと早く言ってくれたらよかったのに。","romaji":"Gomen. Sasuga ni kyō no kyō de, kūkō made mukae ni iku no wa muri da wa. Mō chotto hayaku itte kuretara yokatta noni.","german":"Sorry. There's really no way I can pick you up at the airport on the same day. I wish you had told me a bit earlier.","furigana":"きょう|きょう|くうこう|むか|い|むり|はや|い"},{"japanese":"ごめんください。どなたかいらっしゃいますか。","romaji":"Gomen kudasai. Donata ka irasshaimasu ka.","german":"Hello? Is anyone there?"},{"japanese":"満員電車で毎日二時間も通勤するなんて、もうごめんだ。","romaji":"Man'in densha de mainichi nijikan mo tsuukin suru nante, mou gomen da.","german":"Commuting two hours a day on a packed train - I've had enough of that.","furigana":"まんいんでんしゃ|まいにちにじかん|つうきん"}],"notes":"御免 is almost always written in kana: ごめん is the casual apology among friends and in the family, ごめんなさい is more polite, and toward superiors people say すみません or 申し訳ありません. ごめんください is called out at someone else's front door to make oneself noticed. In addition, 〜はごめんだ expresses that one absolutely does not want something (anymore): \"I've had enough of it\". The kanji spelling 御免 is seen today mainly in expressions like 天下御免.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の祖父は心臓病を患っていたため、兵役を免除されました。","romaji":"Watashi no sofu wa shinzoubyou o wazuratte ita tame, heieki o menjo saremashita.","german":"My grandfather was exempted from military service because of his heart disease.","furigana":"わたし|そふ|しんぞうびょう|わずら|へいえき|めんじょ"},{"japanese":"成績が優秀な学生は、授業料が全額免除されます。","romaji":"Seiseki ga yuushuu na gakusei wa, jugyouryou ga zengaku menjo saremasu.","german":"Students with outstanding grades have their tuition fees waived completely.","furigana":"せいせき|ゆうしゅう|がくせい|じゅぎょうりょう|ぜんがくめんじょ"},{"japanese":"経験者については、最初の研修を免除することにしました。","romaji":"Keikensha ni tsuite wa, saisho no kenshuu o menjo suru koto ni shimashita.","german":"We decided to exempt applicants with work experience from the introductory training.","furigana":"けいけんしゃ|さいしょ|けんしゅう|めんじょ"}],"notes":"免除 means exemption from a duty, payment or exam: 授業料を免除する, 兵役が免除される, 試験の一部が免除になる. It is very often used in the passive, with the exempted person as the topic: 〜は…を免除される. It fits only duties and burdens, not release from captivity (that would be 解放). For tax exemption there is the separate word 免税. Register: official, formal.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"しまった！兄ちゃんが俺の携帯に電話するって言ってたのに、ここ圏外だわ。","romaji":"Shimatta! Niichan ga ore no keitai ni denwa suru tte itteta noni, koko kengai da wa.","german":"Oh no! My brother said he'd call my phone, but there's no signal here.","furigana":"にい|おれ|けいたい|でんわ|い|けんがい"},{"japanese":"山の中に入ると、スマホがすぐ圏外になってしまう。","romaji":"Yama no naka ni hairu to, sumaho ga sugu kengai ni natte shimau.","german":"Once you go into the mountains, your smartphone quickly loses signal.","furigana":"やま|なか|はい|けんがい"},{"japanese":"前回は三位だったのに、今回はトップテン圏外に落ちた。","romaji":"Zenkai wa san'i datta noni, konkai wa toppu ten kengai ni ochita.","german":"Last time he was third, but this time he fell out of the top ten.","furigana":"ぜんかい|さんい|こんかい|けんがい|お"}],"notes":"圏外 literally means \"outside the range\". In everyday life it appears mainly on the mobile phone display: ここは圏外だ, 圏外になる \"no network\". It also means \"outside the ranking or reach\": ランキング圏外, 優勝圏外, 台風の圏外. The opposite is 圏内 (圏内に入る). Colloquially, people say of a person 恋愛対象の圏外 \"out of the question as a partner\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"シンガポールが英語圏だとは思ってもみなかったよ。","romaji":"Shingapōru ga eigoken da to wa omotte mo minakatta yo.","german":"I never thought of Singapore as an English-speaking country.","furigana":"えいごけん|おも"},{"japanese":"英語圏の大学に留学したいと思っています。","romaji":"Eigoken no daigaku ni ryuugaku shitai to omotte imasu.","german":"I'd like to study abroad at a university in an English-speaking country.","furigana":"えいごけん|だいがく|りゅうがく|おも"},{"japanese":"英語圏では、名前を先に言って、名字を後に言います。","romaji":"Eigoken de wa, namae o saki ni itte, myouji o ato ni iimasu.","german":"In the English-speaking world, you say your first name first and your family name after.","furigana":"えいごけん|なまえ|さき|い|みょうじ|あと|い"}],"notes":"英語圏 is the English-speaking sphere, that is, all countries in which English is the main language. The suffix 〜圏 forms many such words: フランス語圏, イスラム圏, 首都圏 (Greater Tokyo), 経済圏. Typical are 英語圏の国, 英語圏に留学する, 英語圏出身の人. Neutral, also common in newspapers and job applications.","levelNote":"Compound: 英語 (N5) + 圏 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"南極圏にはペンギン以外にも動物はいるんですか？","romaji":"Nankyokuken ni wa pengin igai ni mo dōbutsu wa iru n desu ka?","german":"Are there other animals besides penguins in Antarctica?","furigana":"なんきょくけん|いがい|どうぶつ"},{"japanese":"南極圏に入ると、夏は一日中太陽が沈まない。","romaji":"Nankyokuken ni hairu to, natsu wa ichinichijuu taiyou ga shizumanai.","german":"Once you enter the Antarctic Circle area, the sun does not set all day in summer.","furigana":"なんきょくけん|はい|なつ|いちにちじゅうたいよう|しず"},{"japanese":"その観測船は、氷を割りながら南極圏へ進んでいった。","romaji":"Sono kansokusen wa, koori o warinagara nankyokuken e susunde itta.","german":"The research vessel advanced toward Antarctica, breaking through the ice.","furigana":"かんそくせん|こおり|わ|なんきょくけん|すす"}],"notes":"南極圏 is, strictly speaking, the area south of the Antarctic Circle, and in a wider sense the Antarctic region. In everyday speech people usually just say 南極; the continent itself is called 南極大陸. The counterpart is 北極圏 (the Arctic). Typical: 南極圏に入る, 南極圏の動物, 南極圏の観測.","levelNote":"Compound: 南極 (N2) + 圏 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どうして埼玉県に来たんですか。","romaji":"Dōshite Saitamaken ni kita n desu ka.","german":"What brings you to Saitama Prefecture?","furigana":"さいたまけん|き"},{"japanese":"埼玉県から東京の会社まで、毎日電車で通っています。","romaji":"Saitamaken kara Toukyou no kaisha made, mainichi densha de kayotte imasu.","german":"I commute by train every day from Saitama Prefecture to my company in Tokyo.","furigana":"さいたまけん|とうきょう|かいしゃ|まいにちでんしゃ|かよ"},{"japanese":"埼玉県の県庁所在地は、ひらがなで書く「さいたま市」です。","romaji":"Saitamaken no kenchou shozaichi wa, hiragana de kaku \"Saitama-shi\" desu.","german":"The capital of Saitama Prefecture is Saitama City, which is written in hiragana.","furigana":"さいたまけん|けんちょうしょざいち|か|し"}],"notes":"埼玉県 is the prefecture directly north of Tokyo in the 関東 region. Many residents commute to Tokyo, so it is regarded as a typical bedroom-community region. The prefectural capital is written in hiragana: さいたま市. In conversation, people usually drop 県 (埼玉に住んでいる), and the residents are called 埼玉県民.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"映画を見ながら御飯を食べています。","romaji":"Eiga o mi nagara gohan o tabete imasu.","german":"I'm eating while watching a movie.","furigana":"えいが|み|ごはん|た"},{"japanese":"しまった、ご飯を炊くのを忘れてた！","romaji":"Shimatta, gohan o taku no o wasureteta!","german":"Oh no, I forgot to cook the rice!","furigana":"はん|た|わす"},{"japanese":"「ごはんできたよ！」「はーい、今行く。」","romaji":"\"Gohan dekita yo!\" \"Haai, ima iku.\"","german":"\"Dinner's ready!\" \"Okay, I'm coming!\"","furigana":"いまい"}],"notes":"御飯 first means \"cooked rice\" and, by extension, \"meal, food\": 朝ご飯, 昼ご飯, 晩ご飯, ご飯ですよ \"the meal is ready\". Today it is mostly written ご飯 or ごはん; the kanji form 御飯 is rare. Uncooked rice is 米, and in a Western-style restaurant it is often ライス; 飯 (めし) is the rough, masculine variant. To cook rice is ご飯を炊く.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"もう三階に行かれて、新しい部屋を御覧になりましたか？","romaji":"Mou sangai ni ikarete, atarashii heya o goran ni narimashita ka?","german":"Have you already gone up to the third floor and seen the new rooms?","furigana":"さんがい|い|あたら|へや|ごらん"},{"japanese":"社長は昨日の試合をテレビでご覧になったそうです。","romaji":"Shachou wa kinou no shiai o terebi de goran ni natta sou desu.","german":"I hear the president watched yesterday's game on TV.","furigana":"しゃちょう|きのう|しあい|らん"},{"japanese":"先生、この写真をご覧になったことがありますか。","romaji":"Sensei, kono shashin o goran ni natta koto ga arimasu ka.","german":"Professor, have you ever seen this photo?","furigana":"せんせい|しゃしん|らん"}],"notes":"御覧になる is the respectful form (尊敬語) of 見る and is used for the seeing of other, higher-ranking people; today it is mostly written ご覧になる. For one's own seeing, the humble form 拝見する is used. Very common is the request ご覧ください \"please take a look\". 御覧になられる is a double honorific and is considered incorrect. Casually toward children or younger people: 〜てごらん \"try ...\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私のかかりつけのお医者さんは、私が大腸癌検査を受けるのを拒みました。","romaji":"Watashi no kakaritsuke no o ishasan wa, watashi ga daichō gan kensa o ukeru no o kobamimashita.","german":"My family doctor refused to let me have a colorectal cancer screening.","furigana":"わたし|いしゃ|わたし|だいちょうがんけんさ|う|こば"},{"japanese":"彼は質問に答えることを最後まで拒んだ。","romaji":"Kare wa shitsumon ni kotaeru koto o saigo made kobanda.","german":"He refused to answer the questions until the end.","furigana":"かれ|しつもん|こた|さいご|こば"},{"japanese":"その島は高い崖に囲まれていて、人が近づくのを拒んでいるようだ。","romaji":"Sono shima wa takai gake ni kakomarete ite, hito ga chikazuku no o kobande iru you da.","german":"The island is surrounded by high cliffs, as if refusing anyone who approaches.","furigana":"しま|たか|がけ|かこ|ひと|ちか|こば"}],"notes":"拒む is a written, rather strong \"to refuse, to reject\": 要求を拒む, 〜ことを拒む, 〜のを拒む. In everyday speech people rather say 断る, which also covers polite declining; 拒否する is the formal Sino-Japanese variant, 拒絶する even more abrupt. The second meaning is \"to bar, to turn away\": 人の侵入を拒む, 人を拒む険しい山.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は戦場に行く事を拒否したため、臆病者と呼ばれた。","romaji":"Kare wa senjou ni iku koto o kyohi shita tame, okubyoumono to yobareta.","german":"He was called a coward because he had refused to go to the battlefield.","furigana":"かれ|せんじょう|い|こと|きょひ|おくびょうもの|よ"},{"japanese":"知らない番号からの電話は、着信拒否にしている。","romaji":"Shiranai bangou kara no denwa wa, chakushin kyohi ni shite iru.","german":"I blocked calls from unknown numbers.","furigana":"し|ばんごう|でんわ|ちゃくしんきょひ"},{"japanese":"組合は会社の提案を拒否した。","romaji":"Kumiai wa kaisha no teian o kyohi shita.","german":"The union rejected the company's proposal.","furigana":"くみあい|かいしゃ|ていあん|きょひ"}],"notes":"拒否 is a clear, formal rejection or refusal: 要求を拒否する, 〜することを拒否する. Common compounds are 拒否権 \"right of veto\", 着信拒否 \"call blocking\", 登校拒否 \"school refusal\" and 拒否反応 \"rejection reaction\". In conversation 断る is softer and more usual; 拒む is the native Japanese verb with a similar meaning, 拒絶 is even sharper.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"友達はもっと大きい稲田をもっていますよ。","romaji":"Tomodachi wa motto ookii inada o motte imasu yo.","german":"My friend has much larger rice fields.","furigana":"ともだち|おお|いなだ"},{"japanese":"秋になると、稲田が一面金色に染まる。","romaji":"Aki ni naru to, inada ga ichimen kin'iro ni somaru.","german":"In autumn, the rice fields turn golden all around.","furigana":"あき|いなだ|いちめんきんいろ|そ"},{"japanese":"列車の窓から、どこまでも続く稲田が見えた。","romaji":"Ressha no mado kara, doko made mo tsuzuku inada ga mieta.","german":"From the train window, rice fields stretching endlessly could be seen.","furigana":"れっしゃ|まど|つづ|いなだ|み"}],"notes":"稲田 is a rice field in which rice (稲) grows. The word sounds rather literary or descriptive, for example in descriptions of landscapes; in everyday speech people say 田んぼ, and in a technical register 水田. As a family name, 稲田 is likewise read いなだ. Typical: 稲田が広がる, 一面の稲田.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は稲作に没頭しています。","romaji":"Kare wa inasaku ni bottō shite imasu.","german":"He is absorbed in rice farming.","furigana":"かれ|いなさく|ぼっとう"},{"japanese":"この地方は水が豊かなので、昔から稲作が盛んです。","romaji":"Kono chihou wa mizu ga yutaka na node, mukashi kara inasaku ga sakan desu.","german":"Because this region is rich in water, rice farming has been thriving here since long ago.","furigana":"ちほう|みず|ゆた|むかし|いなさく|さか"},{"japanese":"稲作は弥生時代に日本に広まったと言われている。","romaji":"Inasaku wa Yayoi jidai ni Nihon ni hiromatta to iwarete iru.","german":"It is said that rice farming spread across Japan in the Yayoi period.","furigana":"いなさく|やよいじだい|にほん|ひろ|い"}],"notes":"稲作 is wet-rice cultivation as an industry or agricultural technique: 稲作が盛んな地域, 稲作農家, 稲作文化, 稲作に適した気候. It sounds matter-of-fact and often appears in textbooks, the news and history texts. Colloquially people say 米作り; agriculture in general is 農業.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は、絶版になっている古い小説を私に譲ってくれました。","romaji":"Kare wa, zeppan ni natte iru furui shōsetsu o watashi ni yuzutte kuremashita.","german":"He let me have an old novel that is out of print.","furigana":"かれ|ぜっぱん|ふる|しょうせつ|わたし|ゆず"},{"japanese":"電車でお年寄りに席を譲った。","romaji":"Densha de otoshiyori ni seki o yuzutta.","german":"I gave up my seat to an elderly person on the train.","furigana":"でんしゃ|としよ|せき|ゆず"},{"japanese":"両チームとも一歩も譲らず、試合は延長戦になった。","romaji":"Ryou chiimu tomo ippo mo yuzurazu, shiai wa enchousen ni natta.","german":"Neither team gave an inch, and the game went into extra time.","furigana":"りょう|いっぽ|ゆず|しあい|えんちょうせん"}],"notes":"譲る means to leave something of one's own to someone else: AをBに譲る. Set phrases are 席を譲る \"to offer one's seat,\" 道を譲る \"to yield the right of way\" and 一歩も譲らない \"not to give an inch.\" Selling between private individuals is also 譲る (in ads: 譲ります), and handing down property is 財産を譲る. Mutual yielding is 譲り合う; unlike あげる, 譲る stresses that you give something up.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"校長が権力を他の人に移譲したがらないんだよ。","romaji":"Kouchou ga kenryoku o ta no hito ni ijou shitagaranai n da yo.","german":"The principal doesn't want to hand over his power to anyone else.","furigana":"こうちょう|けんりょく|た|ひと|いじょう"},{"japanese":"国から地方自治体への権限の移譲が進められている。","romaji":"Kuni kara chihou jichitai e no kengen no ijou ga susumerarete iru.","german":"The transfer of authority from the state to local governments is being pushed forward.","furigana":"くに|ちほうじちたい|けんげん|いじょう|すす"},{"japanese":"社長は来年、息子に経営権を移譲する予定だ。","romaji":"Shachou wa rainen, musuko ni keieiken o ijou suru yotei da.","german":"The company president plans to transfer management rights to his son next year.","furigana":"しゃちょう|らいねん|むすこ|けいえいけん|いじょう|よてい"}],"notes":"移譲 is the formal transfer of rights, authority or property to someone else: 権限を移譲する, 地方への権限移譲, 経営権の移譲. It belongs to administrative, political and business language. Similar is 委譲 (same reading, transfer of authority to subordinates); 譲渡 emphasizes the sale or assignment of property. Beware of the similar-sounding 以上 and 異常.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ずっと探していた60年代に生産中止になったクラシックカーを見つけた時は、嬉しくて胸が躍りました。","romaji":"Zutto sagashite ita rokujuu nendai ni seisan chuushi ni natta kurashikku kaa o mitsuketa toki wa, ureshikute mune ga odorimashita.","german":"When I found the classic car I had been looking for all this time, which went out of production in the 60s, my heart leapt with joy.","furigana":"さが|ねんだい|せいさんちゅうし|み|とき|うれ|むね|おど"},{"japanese":"明日から夢だったパリ旅行だと思うと、心が躍る。","romaji":"Ashita kara yume datta Pari ryokou da to omou to, kokoro ga odoru.","german":"When I think that my dream trip to Paris starts tomorrow, my heart leaps.","furigana":"あした|ゆめ|りょこう|おも|こころ|おど"},{"japanese":"新聞の一面に「優勝」という大きな文字が躍っていた。","romaji":"Shinbun no ichimen ni \"yuushou\" to iu ookina moji ga odotte ita.","german":"The word \"champion\" stood out in huge letters on the front page of the newspaper.","furigana":"しんぶん|いちめん|ゆうしょう|おお|もじ|おど"}],"notes":"躍る means \"to jump, to leap\" and is used mostly figuratively: 胸が躍る, 心が躍る \"one's heart leaps\" with joy or anticipation. Headlines also \"leap out at the reader\" (見出しに〜の文字が躍る), and fish leap (魚が躍る). Do not confuse it with the identically read 踊る \"to dance\". The verb is intransitive.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この業界では、女性の活躍が目立ちます。","romaji":"Kono gyōkai de wa, josei no katsuyaku ga medachimasu.","german":"In this industry, women's active contribution stands out.","furigana":"ぎょうかい|じょせい|かつやく|めだ"},{"japanese":"彼は今、プロ野球選手としてアメリカで活躍している。","romaji":"Kare wa ima, puro yakyuu senshu to shite Amerika de katsuyaku shite iru.","german":"He is now doing well in America as a professional baseball player.","furigana":"かれ|いま|やきゅうせんしゅ|かつやく"},{"japanese":"新しい職場でのご活躍をお祈りしております。","romaji":"Atarashii shokuba de no go-katsuyaku o oinori shite orimasu.","german":"I wish you every success in your new workplace.","furigana":"あたら|しょくば|かつやく|いの"}],"notes":"活躍 means to be active and successful in a role and to distinguish oneself, not merely \"to take part\". It is mostly an intransitive する-verb: 〜で活躍する, 〜として活躍する. Common are 大活躍, 活躍の場 and the letter formula ご活躍をお祈りします \"I wish you continued success\". Mere participation, by contrast, is 参加.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この授業は、生徒たちの跳躍力を伸ばすことを目的としています。","romaji":"Kono jugyō wa, seitotachi no chōyakuryoku o nobasu koto o mokuteki to shite imasu.","german":"This class aims to improve the students' jumping ability.","furigana":"じゅぎょう|せいと|ちょうやくりょく|の|もくてき"},{"japanese":"走り高跳びや走り幅跳びは、跳躍種目に入ります。","romaji":"Hashiri takatobi ya hashiri habatobi wa, chouyaku shumoku ni hairimasu.","german":"The high jump and the long jump count as jumping events.","furigana":"はし|たかと|はし|はばと|ちょうやくしゅもく|はい"},{"japanese":"カエルは後ろ足を使って大きく跳躍する。","romaji":"Kaeru wa ushiroashi o tsukatte ookiku chouyaku suru.","german":"Frogs leap far using their hind legs.","furigana":"うし|あし|つか|おお|ちょうやく"}],"notes":"跳躍 is a technical, written-language word for \"jump, to jump\", used above all in sports: 跳躍力 \"jumping power\", 跳躍種目 \"jumping events\". In everyday speech one says ジャンプ or 跳ぶ. It differs from the similar 飛躍 in that 跳躍 means the physical jump, whereas 飛躍 usually means, figuratively, a leap forward or a leap in thought.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"犬が飼い主に銃殺されるという悲しい事件がありました。","romaji":"Inu ga kainushi ni juusatsu sareru to iu kanashii jiken ga arimashita.","german":"There was a sad incident in which a dog was shot dead by its owner.","furigana":"いぬ|か|ぬし|じゅうさつ|かな|じけん"},{"japanese":"反乱を起こした兵士たちは、捕まって銃殺された。","romaji":"Hanran o okoshita heishitachi wa, tsukamatte juusatsu sareta.","german":"The soldiers who had staged the rebellion were captured and shot.","furigana":"はんらん|お|へいし|つか|じゅうさつ"},{"japanese":"戦争中、スパイは見つかれば銃殺刑になった。","romaji":"Sensouchuu, supai wa mitsukareba juusatsukei ni natta.","german":"During the war, spies were sentenced to death by firing squad if they were caught.","furigana":"せんそうちゅう|み|じゅうさつけい"}],"notes":"銃殺 means \"to shoot dead\", especially as an execution (銃殺刑 \"death by firing squad\"). It is mostly used in the passive: 〜に銃殺される. In police reports about the shooting of a criminal or an animal, 射殺 is often used; 撃つ is the neutral \"to shoot\". The word belongs in news, historical and crime contexts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"少し待って貰ってもいいですか？ちょっと銃弾を取りに行かなくてはならないので。","romaji":"Sukoshi matte moratte mo ii desu ka? Chotto jūdan o tori ni ikanakute wa naranai node.","german":"Could you wait a moment? I have to go and get my bullets.","furigana":"すこ|ま|もら|じゅうだん|と|い"},{"japanese":"兵士は胸に銃弾を受けて倒れた。","romaji":"Heishi wa mune ni juudan o ukete taoreta.","german":"The soldier was hit in the chest by a bullet and collapsed.","furigana":"へいし|むね|じゅうだん|う|たお"},{"japanese":"現場の壁には、銃弾の跡がいくつも残っていた。","romaji":"Genba no kabe ni wa, juudan no ato ga ikutsu mo nokotte ita.","german":"Several bullet marks remained on the wall at the scene.","furigana":"げんば|かべ|じゅうだん|あと|のこ"}],"notes":"銃弾 is a bullet fired from a firearm. Typical combinations: 銃弾を受ける \"to be hit\", 銃弾に倒れる \"to be killed by a bullet\", 銃弾が当たる, 銃弾の跡 \"bullet hole\". 弾丸 is more general, and in casual speech people simply say 弾 (たま). The register is that of news and narrative.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"しばらく時間はかかったが、ようやく犯行の凶器となった短銃を見つけることができました。","romaji":"Shibaraku jikan wa kakatta ga, yōyaku hankō no kyōki to natta tanjū o mitsukeru koto ga dekimashita.","german":"It took some time, but we were finally able to find the pistol that was used as the weapon in the crime.","furigana":"じかん|はんこう|きょうき|たんじゅう|み"},{"japanese":"警察官は腰に短銃を下げていた。","romaji":"Keisatsukan wa koshi ni tanjuu o sagete ita.","german":"The police officer wore a pistol at his waist.","furigana":"けいさつかん|こし|たんじゅう|さ"},{"japanese":"西部劇では、ガンマンが短銃を素早く抜くシーンがよくある。","romaji":"Seibugeki de wa, ganman ga tanjuu o subayaku nuku shiin ga yoku aru.","german":"In Westerns, there are often scenes where a gunslinger draws his gun in a flash.","furigana":"せいぶげき|たんじゅう|すばや|ぬ"}],"notes":"短銃 is a short handgun, that is, a pistol or revolver. The word sounds somewhat old-fashioned and literary; today the news usually says 拳銃, and colloquially ピストル. The opposite is long guns such as ライフル or 猟銃. Typical: 短銃を持つ, 短銃を抜く, 短銃で撃つ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「それでは、アジェンダの次の項目へ進みましょう。」「ちょっと待って下さい。次へ進む前に、10分間の休憩を取りませんか？」","romaji":"\"Sore de wa, ajenda no tsugi no koumoku e susumimashou.\" \"Chotto matte kudasai. Tsugi e susumu mae ni, juppunkan no kyuukei o torimasen ka?\"","german":"\"Well then, let's move on to the next item on the agenda.\" \"Wait a moment, please. Before we go on, shall we take a ten-minute break?\"","furigana":"つぎ|こうもく|すす|ま|くだ|つぎ|すす|まえ|ぷんかん|きゅうけい|と"},{"japanese":"この申込書は、入力しなければならない項目が多すぎる。","romaji":"Kono moushikomisho wa, nyuuryoku shinakereba naranai koumoku ga oosugiru.","german":"This application form has far too many fields that have to be filled in.","furigana":"もうしこみしょ|にゅうりょく|こうもく|おお"},{"japanese":"この辞書には約十万の項目が収められている。","romaji":"Kono jisho ni wa yaku juuman no koumoku ga osamerarete iru.","german":"This dictionary contains about 100,000 entries.","furigana":"じしょ|やくじゅうまん|こうもく|おさ"}],"notes":"項目 is a single item in a list or outline: an agenda item, an entry, a form field, a heading; in a dictionary also \"entry, headword\". Typical: 項目を追加する, チェック項目, 入力項目, 調査項目; as a counter 5項目. The related term 事項 refers more to the matter or content (注意事項), whereas 項目 is the unit of an outline.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"用紙に必要事項を記載のうえ、郵送をお願い致します。","romaji":"Yōshi ni hitsuyō jikō o kisai no ue, yūsō o onegai itashimasu.","german":"Please fill in the required items on the form and send it to us by mail.","furigana":"ようし|ひつようじこう|きさい|ゆうそう|ねが|いた"},{"japanese":"会議での決定事項は、後でメールで共有します。","romaji":"Kaigi de no kettei jikou wa, ato de meeru de kyouyuu shimasu.","german":"I'll send out the decisions from the meeting by e-mail later.","furigana":"かいぎ|けっていじこう|あと|きょうゆう"},{"japanese":"試験を受ける前に、注意事項をよく読んでください。","romaji":"Shiken o ukeru mae ni, chuui jikou o yoku yonde kudasai.","german":"Please read the notes carefully before the exam.","furigana":"しけん|う|まえ|ちゅういじこう|よ"}],"notes":"事項 refers to individual matters or points, above all in formal texts such as forms, contracts and minutes. It appears almost always in compounds: 必要事項 \"required items\", 注意事項 \"notes\", 決定事項 \"decisions\", 連絡事項 \"announcements\", 禁止事項. In contrast to 項目 (an item in a list outline), 事項 means the content; the more general, softer word is 事柄.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"詳細については、添付の募集要項をご確認くださいませ。","romaji":"Shōsai nitsuite wa, tenpu no boshū yōkō o go kakunin kudasaimase.","german":"For details, please check the attached application guidelines.","furigana":"しょうさい|てんぷ|ぼしゅうようこう|かくにん"},{"japanese":"大学のホームページから入試要項をダウンロードした。","romaji":"Daigaku no hoomupeeji kara nyuushi youkou o daunroodo shita.","german":"I downloaded the entrance exam information from the university website.","furigana":"だいがく|にゅうしようこう"},{"japanese":"応募する前に、要項をよく読んで条件を確認してください。","romaji":"Oubo suru mae ni, youkou o yoku yonde jouken o kakunin shite kudasai.","german":"Before you apply, please read the guidelines carefully and check the requirements.","furigana":"おうぼ|まえ|ようこう|よ|じょうけん|かくにん"}],"notes":"要項 means the essential points and conditions of an undertaking, usually in the form of an official information sheet: 募集要項 \"call for applications, application requirements\", 入試要項 \"entrance exam information\", 応募要項, 実施要項. One reads, distributes or publishes it (要項を確認する, 要項を配布する). Do not confuse it with the identically read 要綱 (outline of a law or program); 要点 simply means the key points.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鋼材不足には早急に対処する必要があります。","romaji":"Kōzai fusoku ni wa sōkyū ni taisho suru hitsuyō ga arimasu.","german":"We need to deal with the steel shortage as quickly as possible.","furigana":"こうざいふそく|そうきゅう|たいしょ|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"このビルには、地震に強い特殊な鋼材が使われている。","romaji":"Kono biru ni wa, jishin ni tsuyoi tokushu na kouzai ga tsukawarete iru.","german":"A special earthquake-resistant steel is used in this building.","furigana":"じしん|つよ|とくしゅ|こうざい|つか"},{"japanese":"鋼材の値上がりで、車の生産コストが上がった。","romaji":"Kouzai no neagari de, kuruma no seisan kosuto ga agatta.","german":"Because of the rise in steel prices, the production costs of cars have gone up.","furigana":"こうざい|ねあ|くるま|せいさん|あ"}],"notes":"鋼材 is steel as a material in processed form, such as beams, sheets or pipes for construction and industry. Typical: 鋼材価格, 建築用鋼材, 鋼材不足, 鋼材メーカー. The component 鋼 means \"steel\" (read はがね when alone); the industry is called 鉄鋼業. Technical vocabulary, frequent in business news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"賃金カットなんかしたら、製鋼所の工員達がストライキを起こすかもしれませんよ。","romaji":"Chingin katto nanka shitara, seikōsho no kōintachi ga sutoraiki o okosu kamo shiremasen yo.","german":"If you cut wages, the workers at the steelworks might go on strike.","furigana":"ちんぎん|せいこうしょ|こういんたち|お"},{"japanese":"この町は明治時代から製鋼業で栄えてきた。","romaji":"Kono machi wa Meiji jidai kara seikougyou de sakaete kita.","german":"This town has prospered from the steel industry since the Meiji era.","furigana":"まち|めいじじだい|せいこうぎょう|さか"},{"japanese":"鉄くずをリサイクルして製鋼する技術が注目されている。","romaji":"Tetsukuzu o risaikuru shite seikou suru gijutsu ga chuumoku sarete iru.","german":"Technology for making steel by recycling scrap iron is attracting attention.","furigana":"てつ|せいこう|ぎじゅつ|ちゅうもく"}],"notes":"製鋼 is the production of steel. The word appears almost only in compounds and technical texts: 製鋼所 \"steelworks\", 製鋼業, 製鋼会社, 製鋼技術; as the verb 製鋼する it is rare. It must be distinguished from 製鉄, the production of pig iron from ore. Beware of words that sound the same, such as 成功 \"success\" and 精巧 \"elaborate, finely made\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どうして囚人はしましまの服を着ているんですか。","romaji":"Doushite shuujin wa shimashima no fuku o kite iru n desu ka.","german":"Why do prisoners wear striped clothes?","furigana":"しゅうじん|ふく|き"},{"japanese":"映画の中で、囚人たちは刑務所からの脱走を計画していた。","romaji":"Eiga no naka de, shuujintachi wa keimusho kara no dassou o keikaku shite ita.","german":"In the film, the prisoners were planning an escape from the prison.","furigana":"えいが|なか|しゅうじん|けいむしょ|だっそう|けいかく"},{"japanese":"その島は昔、囚人を送る場所として使われていた。","romaji":"Sono shima wa mukashi, shuujin o okuru basho to shite tsukawarete ita.","german":"That island was once used as a place to send prisoners.","furigana":"しま|むかし|しゅうじん|おく|ばしょ|つか"}],"notes":"囚人 is a prisoner in jail. The word sounds somewhat old-fashioned or narrative and often occurs in films, novels and historical contexts (囚人服, 囚人を収容する). Officially, one nowadays speaks of 受刑者 (convicted inmate); prisoners of war are 捕虜, and suspects before the verdict are 容疑者.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"死刑囚はこの階段を登って死刑台へと向かうんだ。","romaji":"Shikeishū wa kono kaidan o nobotte shikeidai e to mukau n da.","german":"A condemned prisoner climbs these stairs and heads for the gallows.","furigana":"しけいしゅう|かいだん|のぼ|しけいだい|む"},{"japanese":"法務大臣が死刑囚の死刑執行を命じた。","romaji":"Houmu daijin ga shikeishuu no shikei shikkou o meijita.","german":"The Minister of Justice ordered the execution of the condemned prisoner.","furigana":"ほうむだいじん|しけいしゅう|しけいしっこう|めい"},{"japanese":"彼は、無実を訴え続ける死刑囚についての本を書いた。","romaji":"Kare wa, mujitsu o uttaetsuzukeru shikeishuu ni tsuite no hon o kaita.","german":"He wrote a book about a death-row inmate who keeps protesting his innocence.","furigana":"かれ|むじつ|うった|つづ|しけいしゅう|ほん|か"}],"notes":"死刑囚 is a person sentenced to death who is waiting for the sentence to be carried out, that is, a death-row inmate. The suffix 〜囚 denotes prisoners (女囚, 未決囚). Typical combinations: 死刑判決を受ける, 死刑を執行する, 死刑囚の再審. Legal news vocabulary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"控えの選手には誰がいたっけ？","romaji":"Hikae no senshu ni wa dare ga ita kke?","german":"Who were the substitutes again?","furigana":"ひか|せんしゅ|だれ"},{"japanese":"申込書の控えは、大切に保管しておいてください。","romaji":"Moushikomisho no hikae wa, taisetsu ni hokan shite oite kudasai.","german":"Please keep the copy of the application form in a safe place.","furigana":"もうしこみしょ|ひか|たいせつ|ほかん"},{"japanese":"出演者は本番まで控え室で待っていた。","romaji":"Shutsuensha wa honban made hikaeshitsu de matte ita.","german":"The performers waited in the waiting room until the actual performance.","furigana":"しゅつえんしゃ|ほんばん|ひか|しつ|ま"}],"notes":"控え is the noun of 控える (\"to hold back, to stand by, to note down\") and has several meanings. \"Reserve, substitute\": 控えの選手, 控えに回る. \"Copy, record\": 領収書の控え, お客様控え, 控えを取る. And \"waiting\": 控え室 \"waiting room, dressing room\". Do not confuse it with 控えめ \"modest, moderate\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"父の消息は依然として不明です。","romaji":"Chichi no shousoku wa izen to shite fumei desu.","german":"There is still no news of my father's whereabouts.","furigana":"ちち|しょうそく|いぜん|ふめい"},{"japanese":"台風の影響で、依然として多くの家が停電している。","romaji":"Taifuu no eikyou de, izen to shite ooku no ie ga teiden shite iru.","german":"Because of the typhoon, many houses are still without power.","furigana":"たいふう|えいきょう|いぜん|おお|いえ|ていでん"},{"japanese":"景気は回復してきたが、失業率は依然高いままだ。","romaji":"Keiki wa kaifuku shite kita ga, shitsugyouritsu wa izen takai mama da.","german":"The economy has recovered, but the unemployment rate remains high.","furigana":"けいき|かいふく|しつぎょうりつ|いぜんたか"}],"notes":"依然 means \"still, as before\" and usually appears as 依然として before the predicate: 依然として不明だ. In the news the short form 依然 also occurs (依然高い, 依然、〜が続いている). The word is written-style; in conversation one says まだ or 相変わらず. Beware: the identically read 以前 means \"earlier, before\". A well-known expression: 旧態依然 \"unchanged and outdated\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"三時間前に眉毛をそったばかりなのに、もう眉毛がつながりそうだ。","romaji":"Sanjikan mae ni mayuge o sotta bakari na noni, mō mayuge ga tsunagarisōda.","german":"I shaved my eyebrows only three hours ago, but they already look like they are about to join up again.","furigana":"さんじかんまえ|まゆげ|まゆげ"},{"japanese":"姉は毎朝、鏡の前で眉毛を丁寧に描いている。","romaji":"Ane wa maiasa, kagami no mae de mayuge o teinei ni kaite iru.","german":"My older sister carefully draws on her eyebrows in front of the mirror every morning.","furigana":"あね|まいあさ|かがみ|まえ|まゆげ|ていねい|か"},{"japanese":"彼は困ると、いつも眉毛が八の字になる。","romaji":"Kare wa komaru to, itsumo mayuge ga hachi no ji ni naru.","german":"When he is in trouble, his eyebrows always slant downward in the shape of 八.","furigana":"かれ|こま|まゆげ|はち|じ"}],"notes":"眉毛 are the eyebrows; shorter and somewhat more elevated is 眉 (まゆ). Typical: 眉毛を描く \"to draw in\", 眉毛を整える, 眉毛を抜く, 太い眉毛. In fixed expressions, 眉 is usually used: 眉をひそめる \"to frown\". Do not confuse it with まつげ \"eyelashes\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この宛名は間違っているみたいです。","romaji":"Kono atena wa machigatte iru mitai desu.","german":"This address seems to be wrong.","furigana":"あてな|まちが"},{"japanese":"領収書の宛名は、会社名でお願いします。","romaji":"Ryoushuusho no atena wa, kaishamei de onegai shimasu.","german":"Please make out the receipt to the company name.","furigana":"りょうしゅうしょ|あてな|かいしゃめい|ねが"},{"japanese":"年賀状の宛名を全部手書きするのは大変だ。","romaji":"Nengajou no atena o zenbu tegaki suru no wa taihen da.","german":"Writing all the New Year's cards' addresses by hand is a lot of work.","furigana":"ねんがじょう|あてな|ぜんぶてが|たいへん"}],"notes":"宛名 is the name of the recipient, usually together with the address on envelopes: 宛名を書く, 宛名書き, 宛名ラベル. On receipts, too, the customer name entered is called 宛名 (領収書の宛名). It must be distinguished from 宛先 (destination address, also for e-mails), 住所 (residential address) and 差出人 (sender).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は由緒正しい家系の出身です。","romaji":"Kare wa yuisho tadashii kakei no shusshin desu.","german":"He comes from a family of distinguished lineage.","furigana":"かれ|ゆいしょただ|かけい|しゅっしん"},{"japanese":"この旅館は、江戸時代から続く由緒正しい宿です。","romaji":"Kono ryokan wa, Edo jidai kara tsuzuku yuisho tadashii yado desu.","german":"This inn is a venerable lodging that has existed since the Edo period.","furigana":"りょかん|えどじだい|つづ|ゆいしょただ|やど"},{"japanese":"京都には由緒正しい寺や神社がたくさんある。","romaji":"Kyouto ni wa yuisho tadashii tera ya jinja ga takusan aru.","german":"Kyoto has many venerable temples and shrines.","furigana":"きょうと|ゆいしょただ|てら|じんじゃ"}],"notes":"由緒正しい is composed of 由緒 \"origin, history\" and 正しい \"correct, proper\": \"of venerable, documented origin\". It describes families, temples, shrines, inns or objects with a long, respected tradition: 由緒正しい家柄, 由緒正しい神社. 由緒ある means the same. Elevated style, sometimes also used slightly ironically.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"フグ夫妻は二人とも香川県には住んでいるが、別居中です。","romaji":"Fugu fusai wa futari tomo Kagawaken ni wa sunde iru ga, bekkyochū desu.","german":"Mr. and Mrs. Fugu both live in Kagawa Prefecture, but they are living apart.","furigana":"ふさい|ふたり|かがわけん|す|べっきょちゅう"},{"japanese":"香川県といえば、やっぱり讃岐うどんだよね。","romaji":"Kagawaken to ieba, yappari Sanuki udon da yo ne.","german":"Speaking of Kagawa Prefecture, it's of course Sanuki udon, isn't it?","furigana":"かがわけん|"},{"japanese":"香川県は、日本で一番面積の小さい都道府県です。","romaji":"Kagawaken wa, Nihon de ichiban menseki no chiisai todōfuken desu.","german":"Kagawa Prefecture is the prefecture with the smallest area in Japan.","furigana":"かがわけん|にほん|いちばんめんせき|ちい|とどうふけん"}],"notes":"香川県 lies in the northeast of the island of 四国; the seat of government is 高松市. It is the smallest prefecture of Japan by area. It is famous for 讃岐うどん (讃岐 is the old name of the province) and therefore jokingly calls itself うどん県. 香川 alone can also be a family name; with 県 the prefecture is clearly meant.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は陰謀論が好きなんですが、彼の論理はよく飛躍するんですよね。","romaji":"Kare wa inbōron ga suki na n desu ga, kare no ronri wa yoku hiyaku suru n desu yo ne.","german":"He likes conspiracy theories, but his logic often makes big leaps.","furigana":"かれ|いんぼうろん|す|かれ|ろんり|ひやく"},{"japanese":"インターネットの普及で、通信技術は飛躍的に進歩した。","romaji":"Intaanetto no fukyuu de, tsuushin gijutsu wa hiyakuteki ni shinpo shita.","german":"With the spread of the internet, communication technology has advanced by leaps and bounds.","furigana":"ふきゅう|つうしんぎじゅつ|ひやくてき|しんぽ"},{"japanese":"彼女はこの大会での優勝をきっかけに、世界へと飛躍した。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kono taikai de no yuushou o kikkake ni, sekai e to hiyaku shita.","german":"After winning this tournament, she made her leap onto the world stage.","furigana":"かのじょ|たいかい|ゆうしょう|せかい|ひやく"}],"notes":"飛躍 is almost always used figuratively: as great progress (飛躍的に進歩する, 飛躍を遂げる), as a leap onto a bigger stage (世界へ飛躍する) and as a leap in thought (論理の飛躍, 話が飛躍する). The adverb 飛躍的に \"by leaps and bounds, dramatically\" is very common. For the physical jump, one uses 跳躍 or ジャンプ. Intransitive する-verb.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お母様に宜しくお伝えくださいね。","romaji":"Okāsama ni yoroshiku otsutae kudasai ne.","german":"Please give my regards to your mother.","furigana":"かあさま|よろ|つた"},{"japanese":"初めまして、田中です。よろしくお願いします。","romaji":"Hajimemashite, Tanaka desu. Yoroshiku onegai shimasu.","german":"Nice to meet you, I'm Tanaka. Please treat me kindly.","furigana":"はじ|たなか|ねが"},{"japanese":"資料を送りますので、ご確認よろしくお願いいたします。","romaji":"Shiryou o okurimasu node, gokakunin yoroshiku onegai itashimasu.","german":"I will send the materials, so please check them.","furigana":"しりょう|おく|かくにん|ねが"}],"notes":"よろしく expresses a request for goodwill and can hardly be translated literally. Most often it appears in よろしくお願いします: at introductions \"Nice to meet you,\" in requests \"Thank you in advance,\" at the end of emails; among friends, short よろしく！／よろしくね. 〜によろしく伝えてください means \"Please give my regards to ...\". The old meaning \"appropriate, good\" survives in よろしくやる \"to manage skillfully.\" Almost always written in kana; 宜しく is rare and formal.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どうして日本人はいつも「宜しくお願いします」って言うの？","romaji":"Dōshite nipponjin wa itsumo yoroshiku onegai shimasu tte iu no?","german":"Warum sagen Japaner ständig \"bitte behandeln Sie mich gut\"?","furigana":"にっぽんじん|よろ|ねが|い"},{"japanese":"はじめまして、田中と申します。どうぞよろしくお願いします。","romaji":"Hajimemashite, Tanaka to moushimasu. Douzo yoroshiku onegai shimasu.","german":"Freut mich, ich heiße Tanaka. Auf gute Zusammenarbeit!","furigana":"たなか|もう|ねが"},{"japanese":"資料は明日までに送りますので、ご確認よろしくお願いします。","romaji":"Shiryou wa ashita made ni okurimasu node, go-kakunin yoroshiku onegai shimasu.","german":"Ich schicke Ihnen die Unterlagen bis morgen – bitte sehen Sie sie sich dann an.","furigana":"しりょう|あした|おく|かくにん|ねが"}],"notes":"宜しくお願いします ist eine vielseitige Höflichkeitsformel, für die es im Deutschen keine direkte Entsprechung gibt; geschrieben wird sie fast immer よろしくお願いします. Man sagt sie bei der Vorstellung („auf gute Zusammenarbeit“), wenn man jemanden um etwas bittet, am Ende von E-Mails (ähnlich „vielen Dank im Voraus“) und zum Jahresbeginn (今年もよろしくお願いします). Höflicher ist よろしくお願いいたします, locker よろしく(ね). 〜によろしく heißt „Grüße an …“.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お話中恐れ入りますが、奥様がエレベーターに閉じ込められてしまったようなんです。","romaji":"Ohanashichuu osoreirimasu ga, okusama ga erebeetaa ni tojikomerarete shimatta you na n desu.","german":"Excuse me for interrupting, but it seems your wife has gotten stuck in the elevator.","furigana":"はなしちゅうおそ|い|おくさま|と|こ"},{"japanese":"奥様にもどうぞよろしくお伝えください。","romaji":"Okusama ni mo douzo yoroshiku otsutae kudasai.","german":"Please give my best regards to your wife as well.","furigana":"おくさま|つた"},{"japanese":"社長の奥様は、以前ピアニストをしていたそうです。","romaji":"Shachou no okusama wa, izen pianisuto o shite ita sou desu.","german":"I hear the president's wife used to be a pianist.","furigana":"しゃちょう|おくさま|いぜん"}],"notes":"奥様 is the very polite term for another person's wife, for example that of the person you are talking to or of a superior; slightly less formal is 奥さん. It is never used for one's own wife; for that, 妻 or 家内 are used. 奥様 also serves as a form of address for a married lady, for example by sales staff (\"madam\").","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 49"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の安否が気に懸かります。","romaji":"Kare no anpi ga ki ni kakarimasu.","german":"I am worried about whether he is safe.","furigana":"かれ|あんぴ|き|か"},{"japanese":"この試合には優勝が懸かっている。","romaji":"Kono shiai ni wa yuushou ga kakatte iru.","german":"The championship is at stake in this match.","furigana":"しあい|ゆうしょう|か"},{"japanese":"犯人の逮捕には、多額の賞金が懸かっている。","romaji":"Hannin no taiho ni wa, tagaku no shoukin ga kakatte iru.","german":"A large reward is offered for the arrest of the criminal.","furigana":"はんにん|たいほ|たがく|しょうきん|か"}],"notes":"懸かる is a kanji spelling of かかる for certain meanings: \"to be at stake, to depend on\" (命が懸かっている, 優勝が懸かった試合), \"to be offered\" for rewards (賞金が懸かる) and \"to hang in the sky\" (月が懸かる). \"To be worried\" results only from the expression 気に懸かる, which is mostly written 気にかかる. In everyday writing the verb is mostly written in kana; the transitive counterpart is 懸ける (命を懸ける).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5 (source: N5; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ワイングラスの扱いに気をつけるようにと言われた直後に、案の定一つ割ってしまった。","romaji":"Wain gurasu no atsukai ni ki o tsukeru you ni to iwareta chokugo ni, annojou hitotsu watte shimatta.","german":"Right after I was told to be careful with the wine glasses, I promptly broke one.","furigana":"あつか|き|い|ちょくご|あん|じょうひと|わ"},{"japanese":"店員にひどい扱いを受けたので、二度とその店には行かない。","romaji":"Ten'in ni hidoi atsukai o uketa node, nido to sono mise ni wa ikanai.","german":"I was treated so badly by the staff that I will never go to that shop again.","furigana":"てんいん|あつか|う|にど|みせ|い"},{"japanese":"もう二十歳なのに、親にまだ子ども扱いされている。","romaji":"Mou hatachi na noni, oya ni mada kodomo atsukai sarete iru.","german":"Even though I'm already twenty, my parents still treat me like a child.","furigana":"はたち|おや|こ|あつか"}],"notes":"扱い is the noun of 扱う and means both the handling of things (機械の扱い, 扱いに注意する, 扱いやすい) and the treatment of people (ひどい扱いを受ける). As a suffix it means \"to treat as\": 子ども扱いする, 犯人扱いされる. The more formal variant, e.g. on labels and in instruction manuals, is 取り扱い (取扱説明書).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その話題については、次の記事で取り扱う予定です。","romaji":"Sono wadai nitsuite wa, tsugi no kiji de toriatsukau yotei desu.","german":"I will deal with this topic in the next article.","furigana":"わだい|つぎ|きじ|と|あつか|よてい"},{"japanese":"当店では、海外のワインも多数取り扱っております。","romaji":"Touten de wa, kaigai no wain mo tasuu toriatsukatte orimasu.","german":"In our shop we also carry a wide selection of foreign wines.","furigana":"とうてん|かいがい|たすうと|あつか"},{"japanese":"この薬品は危険なので、慎重に取り扱ってください。","romaji":"Kono yakuhin wa kiken na node, shinchou ni toriatsukatte kudasai.","german":"This chemical is dangerous, please handle it with care.","furigana":"やくひん|きけん|しんちょう|と|あつか"}],"notes":"取り扱う is the more formal variant of 扱う and is transitive with を. It means \"to handle, to operate\" (機械を取り扱う, 慎重に取り扱う), \"to deal with\" topics or cases (問題を取り扱う) and in commerce \"to carry, to offer\" (当店では〜を取り扱っております). The noun 取り扱い appears on labels such as 取扱注意 \"handle with care, fragile\"; an instruction manual is called 取扱説明書.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"結婚相手にどちらの女性を選択するか決めましたか？","romaji":"Kekkon aite ni dochira no josei o sentaku suru ka kimemashita ka?","german":"Haben Sie sich entschieden, welche Frau Sie als Ehepartnerin wählen?","furigana":"けっこんあいて|じょせい|せんたく|き"},{"japanese":"支払い方法を選択してから、「次へ」を押してください。","romaji":"Shiharai houhou o sentaku shite kara, \"tsugi e\" o oshite kudasai.","german":"Wählen Sie die Zahlungsart aus und klicken Sie dann auf „Weiter“.","furigana":"しはら|ほうほう|せんたく|つぎ|お"},{"japanese":"大学では、第二外国語としてドイツ語を選択した。","romaji":"Daigaku de wa, daini gaikokugo to shite Doitsugo o sentaku shita.","german":"An der Uni habe ich Deutsch als zweite Fremdsprache gewählt.","furigana":"だいがく|だいにがいこくご|ご|せんたく"}],"notes":"選択する ist das sinojapanische, förmlichere Gegenstück zu 選ぶ „wählen“. Es ist typisch für Formulare, Computerbedienung (項目を選択する) und Entscheidungen zwischen festen Optionen (選択科目, 選択肢 „Wahlmöglichkeit“). Im Gespräch sagt man meist 選ぶ. Vorsicht: Das gleich gelesene 洗濯 bedeutet „Wäsche waschen“.","levelNote":"Derived from 選択 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"当人達は内緒話をしているつもりのようだが、声がでかいので全部筒抜けだよ。","romaji":"Tōnintachi wa naishobanashi o shite iru tsumori no yō da ga, koe ga dekai node zenbu tsutsunuke da yo.","german":"They try to whisper, but their voices are so loud that I can hear everything.","furigana":"とうにんたち|ないしょばなし|こえ|ぜんぶつつぬ"},{"japanese":"授業中に内緒話をしていたら、先生に注意された。","romaji":"Jugyouchuu ni naishobanashi o shite itara, sensei ni chuui sareta.","german":"When we were whispering to each other in class, we were reprimanded by the teacher.","furigana":"じゅぎょうちゅう|ないしょばなし|せんせい|ちゅうい"},{"japanese":"二人は部屋の隅で何か内緒話をしているようだった。","romaji":"Futari wa heya no sumi de nanika naishobanashi o shite iru you datta.","german":"The two seemed to be discussing something confidential in a corner of the room.","furigana":"ふたり|へや|すみ|なに|ないしょばなし"}],"notes":"内緒話 is a confidential conversation that others should not hear, often quiet or whispered: 内緒話をする. It is based on 内緒 \"secret\" (内緒にする, 内緒で, often in kana ないしょ). For the whispering itself one says ひそひそ話; 秘密 is the more neutral, more serious word for \"secret\". Colloquial.","levelNote":"Compound: 内緒 (N1) + 話 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どうぞネクタイをお緩めになってください。","romaji":"Dōzo nekutai o o yurume ni natte kudasai.","german":"Please feel free to loosen your tie.","furigana":"ゆる"},{"japanese":"カーブの手前でスピードを緩めてください。","romaji":"Kaabu no temae de supiido o yurumete kudasai.","german":"Please reduce your speed before the curve.","furigana":"てまえ|ゆる"},{"japanese":"試験が終わるまでは、気を緩めてはいけない。","romaji":"Shiken ga owaru made wa, ki o yurumete wa ikenai.","german":"You must not let up until the exam is over.","furigana":"しけん|お|き|ゆる"}],"notes":"緩める is transitive (〜を緩める) and means \"to loosen, to release\": ネクタイ, ベルト, ねじを緩める. Figuratively: スピードを緩める \"to slow down\", 気を緩める \"to let one's guard down\", 規制を緩める \"to relax regulations\", 手を緩める \"to ease off\". The intransitive counterpart is 緩む, the adjective 緩い, the opposite 締める.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これは母への贈り物です。","romaji":"Kore wa haha e no okurimono desu.","german":"This is a gift for my mother.","furigana":"はは|おく|もの"},{"japanese":"結婚のお祝いに、贈り物を選んでいます。","romaji":"Kekkon no oiwai ni, okurimono o erande imasu.","german":"I am choosing a gift for a wedding celebration.","furigana":"けっこん|いわ|おく|もの|えら"},{"japanese":"この時計は祖父からの大切な贈り物です。","romaji":"Kono tokei wa sofu kara no taisetsu na okurimono desu.","german":"This watch is a precious gift from my grandfather.","furigana":"とけい|そふ|たいせつ|おく|もの"}],"notes":"贈り物 is a gift with a somewhat formal or ceremonial ring, for example for weddings, birthdays or seasonal gifts; in everyday speech one mostly says プレゼント. The related verb is 贈る \"to give (ceremonially)\", in everyday speech あげる. A souvenir from a trip, by contrast, is お土産. Note: 送る \"to send\" sounds the same but is written differently.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 45"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"若者が沖縄県から出ていくのを食い止めるため、県は新しい仕事を増やそうとしている。","romaji":"Wakamono ga Okinawaken kara dete iku no o kuitomeru tame, ken wa atarashii shigoto o fuyasou to shite iru.","german":"To stop young people from leaving Okinawa, the prefecture is trying to create new jobs.","furigana":"わかもの|おきなわけん|で|く|と|けん|あたら|しごと|ふ"},{"japanese":"沖縄県は日本で一番南にある県です。","romaji":"Okinawaken wa Nihon de ichiban minami ni aru ken desu.","german":"Okinawa Prefecture is the southernmost prefecture in Japan.","furigana":"おきなわけん|にほん|いちばんみなみ|けん"},{"japanese":"沖縄県には、アメリカ軍の基地が多く集まっている。","romaji":"Okinawaken ni wa, Amerikagun no kichi ga ooku atsumatte iru.","german":"Many American military bases are concentrated in Okinawa Prefecture.","furigana":"おきなわけん|ぐん|きち|おお|あつ"}],"notes":"沖縄県 is the southernmost prefecture of Japan and consists of many subtropical islands; the capital is 那覇市. Until the 19th century the area was the Kingdom of 琉球, and after the war it was under American administration until 1972. In conversation people usually say just 沖縄; from there, Japan's main islands are often called 本土.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これは、ある国では「プレゼント」と呼ばれるかもしれないが、この国では「贈賄」である。","romaji":"Kore wa, aru kuni de wa purezento to yobareru kamo shirenai ga, kono kuni de wa zouwai de aru.","german":"In some countries this might be called a \"present\", but in this country it is \"bribery\".","furigana":"くに|よ|くに|ぞうわい"},{"japanese":"建設会社の社長が、市の職員への贈賄の疑いで逮捕された。","romaji":"Kensetsu gaisha no shachou ga, shi no shokuin e no zouwai no utagai de taiho sareta.","german":"The president of a construction company was arrested on suspicion of bribing city employees.","furigana":"けんせつがいしゃ|しゃちょう|し|しょくいん|ぞうわい|うたが|たいほ"},{"japanese":"贈賄側も収賄側も、どちらも罪に問われる。","romaji":"Zouwaigawa mo shuuwaigawa mo, dochira mo tsumi ni towareru.","german":"Both the side that gives the bribe and the side that takes it are held criminally liable.","furigana":"ぞうわいがわ|しゅうわいがわ|つみ|と"}],"notes":"贈賄 is active bribery, that is, giving a bribe; accepting one is 収賄, and both together are 贈収賄 (贈収賄事件). The bribe itself is 賄賂. Typical in the news: 贈賄の疑いで逮捕される, 贈賄罪. Legal and journalistic vocabulary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"逃亡者は逃走中に病気の子どもに会い、自首を決意したそうです。","romaji":"Tōbōsha wa tōsōchū ni byōki no kodomo ni ai, jishu o ketsui shita sō desu.","german":"The fugitive met a sick child during his flight and decided to turn himself in to the police.","furigana":"とうぼうしゃ|とうそうちゅう|びょうき|こ|あ|じしゅ|けつい"},{"japanese":"警察は、国外に逃げた逃亡者の行方を追っている。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa, kokugai ni nigeta toubousha no yukue o otte iru.","german":"The police are searching for the fugitive who escaped abroad.","furigana":"けいさつ|こくがい|に|とうぼうしゃ|ゆくえ|お"},{"japanese":"逃亡者を手伝った人も、罪に問われることがある。","romaji":"Toubousha o tetsudatta hito mo, tsumi ni towareru koto ga aru.","german":"Even helping a fugitive can make one criminally liable.","furigana":"とうぼうしゃ|てつだ|ひと|つみ|と"}],"notes":"逃亡者 is someone who flees from the police, the justice system or from captivity, i.e. a fugitive or escapee. The word has nothing to do with war or economic refugees; these are called 難民 or 避難民. Related are 逃亡 \"flight\" (逃亡中, 国外逃亡) and 逃走. Typical in news reports and crime stories.","levelNote":"Compound: 逃亡 (N1) + 者 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"明日、部活を休んでもいいかどうか、顧問の先生に聞いてみたら？","romaji":"Ashita, bukatsu o yasunde mo ii ka dō ka, komon no sensei ni kiite mitara?","german":"Why don't you ask the teacher who supervises the club whether you can skip club activities tomorrow?","furigana":"あした|ぶかつ|やす|こもん|せんせい|き"},{"japanese":"会社は法律の問題について、顧問弁護士に相談した。","romaji":"Kaisha wa houritsu no mondai ni tsuite, komon bengoshi ni soudan shita.","german":"The company consulted its legal adviser about the legal problem.","furigana":"かいしゃ|ほうりつ|もんだい|こもんべんごし|そうだん"},{"japanese":"父は会社を退職した後も、顧問として週に一度出社している。","romaji":"Chichi wa kaisha o taishoku shita ato mo, komon to shite shuu ni ichido shussha shite iru.","german":"Even after retiring from the company, my father goes in once a week as an adviser.","furigana":"ちち|かいしゃ|たいしょく|あと|こもん|しゅう|いちどしゅっしゃ"}],"notes":"顧問 is an adviser with a fixed function in an organization: 顧問弁護士 \"(company) legal adviser\", 技術顧問, 会社の顧問 (often former executives). At schools, the 顧問 (顧問の先生) is the teacher who supervises a club (部活). It must be distinguished from コンサルタント (an external consultant paid a fee) and 相談役.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"なんてつまらない回顧録なんだ。","romaji":"Nante tsumaranai kaikoroku na n da.","german":"What boring memoirs.","furigana":"かいころく"},{"japanese":"元首相が、政治家時代を振り返る回顧録を出版した。","romaji":"Moto shushou ga, seijika jidai o furikaeru kaikoroku o shuppan shita.","german":"The former prime minister published memoirs looking back on his time as a politician.","furigana":"もとしゅしょう|せいじかじだい|ふ|かえ|かいころく|しゅっぱん"},{"japanese":"祖父は自分の戦争体験を回顧録にまとめた。","romaji":"Sofu wa jibun no sensou taiken o kaikoroku ni matometa.","german":"My grandfather compiled his wartime experiences into memoirs.","furigana":"そふ|じぶん|せんそうたいけん|かいころく"}],"notes":"回顧録 is composed of 回顧 \"retrospection\" and 録 \"record\" and means memoirs, especially of politicians, executives or witnesses of an era. Typical: 回顧録を書く, 回顧録を出版する, 回顧録にまとめる. Similar are 回想録 and 自伝 \"autobiography\"; 回顧録 sounds the most formal of these.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今夜は駐日アメリカ大使主催のパーティーに行かなくちゃいけないんです。","romaji":"Kon'ya wa chūnichi Amerika taishi shusai no pātī ni ikanakucha ikenai n desu.","german":"Ich muss heute Abend zu einer Party, die vom US-Botschafter in Japan ausgerichtet wird.","furigana":"こんや|ちゅうにち|たいししゅさい|い"},{"japanese":"駐日中国大使が首相と会談した。","romaji":"Chūnichi Chūgoku taishi ga shushō to kaidan shita.","german":"Der chinesische Botschafter in Japan führte Gespräche mit dem Premierminister.","furigana":"ちゅうにちちゅうごくたいし|しゅしょう|かいだん"},{"japanese":"彼は駐日フランス大使館で働いている。","romaji":"Kare wa chūnichi Furansu taishikan de hataraite iru.","german":"Er arbeitet an der französischen Botschaft in Japan.","furigana":"かれ|ちゅうにち|たいしかん|はたら"}],"notes":"駐日 bedeutet „in Japan stationiert, in Japan akkreditiert“ und steht vor Bezeichnungen ausländischer Diplomaten und Institutionen: 駐日アメリカ大使, 駐日大使館, 駐日外国人記者. Allein wird es nicht gebraucht. Nach demselben Muster gebildet sind 駐米 „in den USA“ oder 駐独 „in Deutschland“ (駐独日本大使). 駐 „stationiert sein“ kennt man aus 駐在 und 駐車. Typisch für Nachrichten und formelle Texte.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"父は仕事でシンガポールに三年間駐在していました。","romaji":"Chichi wa shigoto de Shingapōru ni sannenkan chūzai shite imashita.","german":"My father was stationed in Singapore for three years for work.","furigana":"ちち|しごと|さんねんかんちゅうざい"},{"japanese":"来月からロンドン駐在が決まりました。","romaji":"Raigetsu kara Rondon chūzai ga kimarimashita.","german":"It has been decided that I will be posted to London from next month.","furigana":"らいげつ|ちゅうざい|き"},{"japanese":"村の駐在所のおまわりさんは、住民の顔をみんな覚えている。","romaji":"Mura no chūzaisho no omawarisan wa, jūmin no kao o minna oboete iru.","german":"The police officer at the village police box remembers the faces of all the residents.","furigana":"むら|ちゅうざいしょ|じゅうみん|かお|おぼ"}],"notes":"駐在 means that someone is sent by a company, agency or state to a place for a longer period, usually abroad, and works there: ロンドンに駐在する, 海外駐在. The posted employee is called 駐在員 \"expat\". Distinctions: 滞在 is any stay (also private or short), 赴任 stresses taking up a post, and for troops one says 駐留. In addition, 駐在所 is a rural police box in which the officer also lives; he is colloquially called 駐在さん.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"戦後、日本には連合国の駐留軍が置かれた。","romaji":"Sengo, Nihon ni wa rengōkoku no chūryūgun ga okareta.","german":"After the war, Allied occupation forces were stationed in Japan.","furigana":"せんご|にほん|れんごうこく|ちゅうりゅうぐん|お"},{"japanese":"駐留軍の基地の周りには、騒音に悩む住民が多い。","romaji":"Chūryūgun no kichi no mawari ni wa, sōon ni nayamu jūmin ga ōi.","german":"Many residents around the bases of the stationed troops suffer from noise.","furigana":"ちゅうりゅうぐん|きち|まわ|そうおん|なや|じゅうみん|おお"},{"japanese":"政府は駐留軍の撤退を求めている。","romaji":"Seifu wa chūryūgun no tettai o motomete iru.","german":"The government is demanding the withdrawal of the stationed troops.","furigana":"せいふ|ちゅうりゅうぐん|てったい|もと"}],"notes":"駐留軍 are troops stationed in a foreign country for a longer period, usually on the basis of an agreement, such as the US forces in Japan (在日米軍). The related verb is 駐留する \"to be stationed\": 米軍が沖縄に駐留する. The word is neutral and belongs to news and historical language; 占領軍 \"occupation army\", by contrast, stresses the occupation. 駐在 is not used for troops but for people such as company employees or diplomats.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日車を電柱にぶつけちゃったんだよね。","romaji":"Kyou kuruma o denchuu ni butsukechatta n da yo ne.","german":"Ich bin heute mit dem Auto gegen einen Strommast gefahren.","furigana":"きょうくるま|でんちゅう"},{"japanese":"台風で電柱が倒れて、停電になった。","romaji":"Taifū de denchū ga taorete, teiden ni natta.","german":"Der Taifun hat einen Strommast umgerissen, und der Strom ist ausgefallen.","furigana":"たいふう|でんちゅう|たお|ていでん"},{"japanese":"電柱に迷子の猫を探すポスターが貼ってあった。","romaji":"Denchū ni maigo no neko o sagasu posutā ga hatte atta.","german":"An einem Strommast hing ein Suchplakat für eine entlaufene Katze.","furigana":"でんちゅう|まいご|ねこ|さが|は"}],"notes":"電柱 ist der Mast für Strom- und Telefonleitungen (電線), wie er in Japan fast überall am Straßenrand steht – im Deutschen je nach Kontext „Strommast“ oder „Telegrafenmast“. Typische Verbindungen: 電柱にぶつかる/ぶつける, 電柱が倒れる, 電柱に貼る. Große Hochspannungsmasten aus Stahlgitter heißen dagegen 鉄塔. Umgangssprachlich sagt man auch 電信柱 (でんしんばしら).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼はその運動を主唱した人物として知られている。","romaji":"Kare wa sono undō o shushō shita jinbutsu to shite shirarete iru.","german":"He is known as the person who championed that movement.","furigana":"かれ|うんどう|しゅしょう|じんぶつ|し"},{"japanese":"この計画は市長の主唱で始まった。","romaji":"Kono keikaku wa shichō no shushō de hajimatta.","german":"This plan was started at the mayor's initiative.","furigana":"けいかく|しちょう|しゅしょう|はじ"},{"japanese":"環境保護を主唱する団体が、新しい法律を求めている。","romaji":"Kankyō hogo o shushō suru dantai ga, atarashii hōritsu o motomete iru.","german":"An organization that champions environmental protection is demanding a new law.","furigana":"かんきょうほご|しゅしょう|だんたい|あたら|ほうりつ|もと"}],"notes":"主唱 means to be the first, or in a leading position, to publicly advocate an idea, doctrine or movement and to promote it: 〜を主唱する. It is a written-language, rather rare word; the person is called 主唱者. The phrase 〜の主唱で means \"on the initiative of ...\". In everyday speech one says 提唱する (\"to propose, to advocate\") or 主張する/訴える instead.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「ふるさと」は昔から歌われている文部省唱歌です。","romaji":"\"Furusato\" wa mukashi kara utawarete iru Monbushō shōka desu.","german":"\"Furusato\" is a long-loved school song from the Ministry of Education's song collection.","furigana":"むかし|うた|もんぶしょうしょうか"},{"japanese":"祖母は今でも子供のころに習った唱歌をよく歌っている。","romaji":"Sobo wa ima demo kodomo no koro ni naratta shōka o yoku utatte iru.","german":"My grandmother still often sings the school songs she learned as a child.","furigana":"そぼ|いま|こども|なら|しょうか|うた"},{"japanese":"戦前の小学校では、音楽の授業を「唱歌」と呼んでいた。","romaji":"Senzen no shōgakkō de wa, ongaku no jugyō o \"shōka\" to yonde ita.","german":"In elementary schools before the war, music class was called \"shōka\".","furigana":"せんぜん|しょうがっこう|おんがく|じゅぎょう|しょうか|よ"}],"notes":"唱歌 today refers above all to the simple, often nostalgic songs sung in schools since the Meiji era, especially the 文部省唱歌 such as 「ふるさと」 or 「春が来た」; often in the pair 童謡・唱歌. Until 1941, the school subject of music was also called 唱歌. For \"singing\" in general one says 歌う or, technically, 歌唱, and for choral singing 合唱.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"花粉症なのに、発表会で独唱しなくちゃいけないんです。","romaji":"Kafunshō na noni, happyōkai de dokushō shinakucha ikenai n desu.","german":"Even though I have hay fever, I have to sing a solo at the recital.","furigana":"かふんしょう|はっぴょうかい|どくしょう"},{"japanese":"卒業式で、彼女は校歌を独唱した。","romaji":"Sotsugyōshiki de, kanojo wa kōka o dokushō shita.","german":"At the graduation ceremony, she sang the school song as a solo.","furigana":"そつぎょうしき|かのじょ|こうか|どくしょう"},{"japanese":"コンサートの第二部では、有名なソプラノ歌手による独唱があった。","romaji":"Konsāto no daini-bu de wa, yūmei na sopurano kashu ni yoru dokushō ga atta.","german":"In the second part of the concert, there was a solo by a famous soprano.","furigana":"だいにぶ|ゆうめい|かしゅ|どくしょう"}],"notes":"独唱 is singing alone, that is, a \"vocal solo\"; the opposites are 合唱 \"choral singing\" and 重唱 \"ensemble singing (duet, trio ...)\". As a する-verb it takes the song as its object (国歌を独唱する) or none. An instrumental solo is 独奏 (ピアノ独奏); in everyday speech, ソロ is often used for both. The soloist is 独唱者.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あなたの歌唱力をみんなに見せつけるために、このビデオをFacebookに投稿してもいいですか？","romaji":"Anata no kashōryoku o minna ni misetsukeru tame ni, kono bideo o Facebook ni tōkō shite mo ii desu ka?","german":"To show everyone your singing talent, may I post this video on Facebook?","furigana":"かしょうりょく|み|とうこう"},{"japanese":"合唱コンクールに向けて、プロの先生に歌唱指導をしてもらった。","romaji":"Gasshō konkūru ni mukete, puro no sensei ni kashō shidō o shite moratta.","german":"For the choir competition, we received singing lessons from a professional teacher.","furigana":"がっしょう|む|せんせい|かしょうしどう"},{"japanese":"開会式で国歌の歌唱が行われた。","romaji":"Kaikaishiki de kokka no kashō ga okonawareta.","german":"The national anthem was sung at the opening ceremony.","furigana":"かいかいしき|こっか|かしょう|おこな"}],"notes":"歌唱 is the technical, rather written-language word for \"singing\" and appears above all in compounds: 歌唱力 \"vocal ability\", 歌唱指導 \"singing lessons\", 歌唱法 \"singing technique\". As the verb 歌唱する it occurs almost only in official contexts (国歌を歌唱する); in everyday speech one simply says 歌う. Do not confuse: 唱歌 are school songs, 合唱 is choral singing, 独唱 is a vocal solo.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"合唱団員は、みんなで手を繋いで歌っています。","romaji":"Gasshōdan'in wa, minna de te o tsunaide utatte imasu.","german":"The choir members all sing hand in hand.","furigana":"がっしょうだんいん|て|つな|うた"},{"japanese":"娘は地元の少年少女合唱団に入っている。","romaji":"Musume wa jimoto no shōnen shōjo gasshōdan ni haitte iru.","german":"My daughter sings in a local children's choir.","furigana":"むすめ|じもと|しょうねんしょうじょがっしょうだん|はい"},{"japanese":"ウィーン少年合唱団が日本で公演を行う。","romaji":"Wīn shōnen gasshōdan ga Nihon de kōen o okonau.","german":"The Vienna Boys' Choir gives concerts in Japan.","furigana":"しょうねんがっしょうだん|にほん|こうえん|おこな"}],"notes":"合唱団 is an organized choir as a group - a school, amateur or children's choir; one joins it (合唱団に入る) or is a member (合唱団員). 合唱 alone, by contrast, refers to the activity \"choral singing\" (合唱する, 合唱コンクール). For pop backing vocals one rather says コーラス, a church choir is called 聖歌隊.","levelNote":"Compound: 合唱 (N1) + 団 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"もっと親孝行したいけど、なかなか時間がないんだよね。","romaji":"Motto oyakōkō shitai kedo, nakanaka jikan ga nai n da yo ne.","german":"I would like to do more for my parents, but I just don't have the time.","furigana":"おやこうこう|じかん"},{"japanese":"初めての給料で両親を温泉旅行に連れて行って、親孝行をした。","romaji":"Hajimete no kyūryō de ryōshin o onsen ryokō ni tsurete itte, oyakōkō o shita.","german":"With my first salary I took my parents on an onsen trip to repay them.","furigana":"はじ|きゅうりょう|りょうしん|おんせんりょこう|つ|い|おやこうこう"},{"japanese":"「孝行のしたい時分に親はなし」ということわざがある。","romaji":"\"Kōkō no shitai jibun ni oya wa nashi\" to iu kotowaza ga aru.","german":"There is a proverb: \"When you want to be good to your parents, they are no longer there.\"","furigana":"こうこう|じぶん|おや"}],"notes":"孝行 is the Confucian-influenced virtue of showing gratitude to one's parents and caring for them. In everyday speech it appears almost always as 親孝行 (親孝行する, 親孝行な息子); the opposite is 親不孝. It is used as a する-verb (親に孝行する) and as a な-adjective (孝行な娘), and can figuratively express care for others as well, e.g. 女房孝行 \"doing something nice for one's wife\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"親を悲しませるような不孝は、絶対にしたくない。","romaji":"Oya o kanashimaseru yō na fukō wa, zettai ni shitakunai.","german":"I never want to commit the kind of unfilial act that makes my parents sad.","furigana":"おや|かな|ふこう|ぜったい"},{"japanese":"親より先に死ぬことは、最大の不孝だと言われる。","romaji":"Oya yori saki ni shinu koto wa, saidai no fukō da to iwareru.","german":"It is said that dying before one's parents is the greatest unfilial act.","furigana":"おや|さき|し|さいだい|ふこう|い"},{"japanese":"長い間帰省もせず、両親には不孝ばかりしてきた。","romaji":"Nagai aida kisei mo sezu, ryōshin ni wa fukō bakari shite kita.","german":"I haven't gone home for a long time and have done nothing but neglect my parents.","furigana":"なが|あいだきせい|りょうしん|ふこう"}],"notes":"不孝 is the opposite of 孝行: behavior by which one hurts one's parents, worries them or neglects them. On its own it sounds rather written; in everyday speech people almost always say 親不孝 (親不孝な息子, 親不孝をする). Do not confuse it with the identically read 不幸 \"misfortune, death in the family\" - only the kanji distinguishes the two. As a な-adjective: 不孝な子.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は二十代で教授になった俊才だ。","romaji":"Kare wa nijūdai de kyōju ni natta shunsai da.","german":"He is an exceptionally talented man who became a professor in his twenties.","furigana":"かれ|にじゅうだい|きょうじゅ|しゅんさい"},{"japanese":"この塾には全国から俊才が集まっている。","romaji":"Kono juku ni wa zenkoku kara shunsai ga atsumatte iru.","german":"Gifted students from all over the country gather at this cram school.","furigana":"じゅく|ぜんこく|しゅんさい|あつ"},{"japanese":"彼女は社内で一番の俊才だと言われている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa shanai de ichiban no shunsai da to iwarete iru.","german":"She is said to be the most gifted person in the company.","furigana":"かのじょ|しゃない|いちばん|しゅんさい|い"}],"notes":"俊才 (also written 駿才) refers to a person of outstanding ability and quick comprehension, an \"exceptional talent\". It is a complimentary, written-language word for biographies, newspaper articles or speeches: 〜の俊才, 俊才が集まる. Close synonyms are 秀才 (\"very bright mind, model student\", more everyday) and 天才 (\"genius\", innate exceptional gift); for the education of gifted children one says 英才教育.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"うちは兼業農家で、父は平日は会社で働き、週末に田んぼの仕事をしている。","romaji":"Uchi wa kengyō nōka de, chichi wa heijitsu wa kaisha de hataraki, shūmatsu ni tanbo no shigoto o shite iru.","german":"We farm part-time: my father works at a company on weekdays and in the rice field on weekends.","furigana":"けんぎょうのうか|ちち|へいじつ|かいしゃ|はたら|しゅうまつ|た|しごと"},{"japanese":"最近は社員の兼業を認める会社が増えてきた。","romaji":"Saikin wa shain no kengyō o mitomeru kaisha ga fuete kita.","german":"Recently more and more companies allow their employees to have side jobs.","furigana":"さいきん|しゃいん|けんぎょう|みと|かいしゃ|ふ"},{"japanese":"彼は作家と大学の講師を兼業している。","romaji":"Kare wa sakka to daigaku no kōshi o kengyō shite iru.","german":"He is both a writer and a part-time lecturer at a university.","furigana":"かれ|さっか|だいがく|こうし|けんぎょう"}],"notes":"兼業 means to carry on another gainful activity besides one's main occupation or to pursue two activities in parallel: AとBを兼業する. Well known is 兼業農家 \"part-time farmer\" as opposed to 専業農家; likewise 兼業主婦 vs. 専業主婦. In the working world it often appears next to 副業 \"side job\"; 副業 emphasizes the subordinate extra income, 兼業 more the simultaneous practice of two occupations (兼業禁止 in an employment contract).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この食事は朝昼兼用にします。","romaji":"Kono shokuji wa asa hiru ken'yō ni shimasu.","german":"This meal is a brunch.","furigana":"しょくじ|あさひるけんよう"},{"japanese":"この傘は晴雨兼用なので、日傘としても使えます。","romaji":"Kono kasa wa seiu ken'yō na node, higasa to shite mo tsukaemasu.","german":"This umbrella is meant for both sun and rain, so you can also use it as a parasol.","furigana":"かさ|せいうけんよう|ひがさ|つか"},{"japanese":"部屋が狭いので、リビングを仕事部屋と兼用している。","romaji":"Heya ga semai node, ribingu o shigotobeya to ken'yō shite iru.","german":"Because the apartment is small, my living room doubles as a study.","furigana":"へや|せま|しごとべや|けんよう"}],"notes":"兼用 means to use one thing for two or more purposes: AをBと兼用する, or as a suffix A・B兼用: 男女兼用 \"unisex\", 晴雨兼用 \"for sun and rain\", 朝昼兼用 \"breakfast and lunch in one\". Before nouns it takes の (兼用のかばん). Distinctions: 兼業 concerns occupations, 兼任 offices, and 共用 emphasizes shared use by several people.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"薬剤を使い終わったら、必ず元の棚に戻してください。","romaji":"Yakuzai o tsukai owattara, kanarazu moto no tana ni modoshite kudasai.","german":"When you have finished using the agent, be sure to put it back on its original shelf.","furigana":"やくざい|つか|お|かなら|もと|たな|もど"},{"japanese":"薬局で薬剤師に薬の飲み方を説明してもらった。","romaji":"Yakkyoku de yakuzaishi ni kusuri no nomikata o setsumei shite moratta.","german":"At the pharmacy, the pharmacist explained to me how to take the medicine.","furigana":"やっきょく|やくざいし|くすり|の|かた|せつめい"},{"japanese":"畑に虫を防ぐための薬剤をまいた。","romaji":"Hatake ni mushi o fusegu tame no yakuzai o maita.","german":"I sprayed an agent to keep insects away on the field.","furigana":"はたけ|むし|ふせ|やくざい"}],"notes":"薬剤 is a technical word for medicines and chemical agents of all kinds - medications, but also pesticides, disinfectants or bleaches. It sounds more technical than 薬 and is found mainly in medicine, agriculture and on product labels; well known is 薬剤師 \"pharmacist\". For chemicals in a laboratory one says 薬品 instead. Grammatically it is a noun.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このパンは防腐剤を使っていないので、早めに食べてください。","romaji":"Kono pan wa bōfuzai o tsukatte inai node, hayame ni tabete kudasai.","german":"This bread contains no preservatives, so please eat it soon.","furigana":"ぼうふざい|つか|はや|た"},{"japanese":"防腐剤無添加の化粧品を探している。","romaji":"Bōfuzai mutenka no keshōhin o sagashite iru.","german":"I am looking for cosmetics without preservatives.","furigana":"ぼうふざいむてんか|けしょうひん|さが"},{"japanese":"木の柱に防腐剤を塗って、腐らないようにした。","romaji":"Ki no hashira ni bōfuzai o nutte, kusaranai yō ni shita.","german":"I applied preservative to the wooden posts so that they would not rot.","furigana":"き|はしら|ぼうふざい|ぬ|くさ"}],"notes":"防腐剤 literally means \"agent against rot\" (防 \"prevent\", 腐 \"rot\", 剤 \"agent\"). It refers to preservatives in food and cosmetics, but also to wood preservatives; on labels one often reads 防腐剤不使用 or 防腐剤無添加. In food law the official term is 保存料; 防腐剤 is the more general everyday term. Disinfectants for wounds are nowadays rather called 消毒薬.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この洗剤は油汚れがよく落ちる。","romaji":"Kono senzai wa abura yogore ga yoku ochiru.","german":"This detergent removes greasy dirt well.","furigana":"せんざい|あぶらよご|お"},{"japanese":"薬局で痛み止めの錠剤を買った。","romaji":"Yakkyoku de itamidome no jōzai o katta.","german":"I bought painkiller tablets at the pharmacy.","furigana":"やっきょく|いた|ど|じょうざい|か"},{"japanese":"ヘビにかまれたら、すぐに解毒剤を打つ必要がある。","romaji":"Hebi ni kamaretara, sugu ni gedokuzai o utsu hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"If you are bitten by a snake, you need to be given an antidote injection immediately.","furigana":"げどくざい|う|ひつよう"}],"notes":"〜剤 is a Sino-Japanese suffix for \"agent, preparation\" and is attached to a kanji word that indicates purpose or form: 洗剤 \"detergent\", 解毒剤 \"antidote\", 防腐剤 \"preservative\", 接着剤 \"adhesive\", 錠剤 \"tablet\", 消毒剤 \"disinfectant\". It cannot be used freely but forms fixed compounds that sound factual and technical. It is not a particle but a noun suffix.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"皇居の内堀に沿って、たくさんの人がジョギングをしている。","romaji":"Kōkyo no uchibori ni sotte, takusan no hito ga jogingu o shite iru.","german":"Many people are jogging along the inner moat of the Imperial Palace.","furigana":"こうきょ|うちぼり|そ|ひと"},{"japanese":"冬の陣のあと、大坂城は外堀だけでなく内堀まで埋められた。","romaji":"Fuyu no jin no ato, Ōsakajō wa sotobori dake de naku uchibori made umerareta.","german":"After the Winter Campaign, not only the outer moat but even the inner moat of Osaka Castle was filled in.","furigana":"ふゆ|じん|おおさかじょう|そとぼり|うちぼり|う"},{"japanese":"内堀の水面に桜の花びらが浮かんでいる。","romaji":"Uchibori no suimen ni sakura no hanabira ga ukande iru.","german":"Cherry blossom petals are floating on the surface of the inner moat.","furigana":"うちぼり|すいめん|さくら|はな|う"}],"notes":"内堀 is the inner castle moat, which directly surrounds the core areas of a Japanese castle (本丸 etc.); its counterpart is 外堀 \"outer moat\". Today the word is known mainly from historic castles and from the Imperial Palace in Tokyo, along whose moats runs the street 内堀通り. 堀 is an artificially dug water-filled ditch; for castle moats the spelling 濠 is also found.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"まず周りの人を説得して、外堀を埋めてから社長に話そう。","romaji":"Mazu mawari no hito o settoku shite, sotobori o umete kara shachō ni hanasō.","german":"Let's first persuade the people around him and so lay the groundwork before we talk to the president.","furigana":"まわ|ひと|せっとく|そとぼり|う|しゃちょう|はな"},{"japanese":"江戸城の外堀は、今でも飯田橋や市ヶ谷のあたりで見ることができる。","romaji":"Edojō no sotobori wa, ima demo Iidabashi ya Ichigaya no atari de miru koto ga dekiru.","german":"You can still see the outer moat of Edo Castle today around Iidabashi and Ichigaya.","furigana":"えどじょう|そとぼり|いま|||み"},{"japanese":"敵の攻撃を防ぐため、城の周りに外堀が掘られた。","romaji":"Teki no kōgeki o fusegu tame, shiro no mawari ni sotobori ga horareta.","german":"To protect against enemy attacks, an outer moat was dug around the castle.","furigana":"てき|こうげき|ふせ|しろ|まわ|そとぼり|ほ"}],"notes":"外堀 is the outer castle moat, which surrounds the entire castle complex including the outer districts; the 内堀 lies inside it. Very common is the expression 外堀を埋める \"to first work on the surroundings in order to make a goal unavoidable\" - after Osaka Castle, whose moats were filled in before the final attack. In Tokyo, remains of the Edo outer moat can still be seen between 飯田橋 and 四ツ谷.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私達は堀川のほとりを並んで歩きました。","romaji":"Watashitachi wa horikawa no hotori o narande arukimashita.","german":"We walked side by side along the canal.","furigana":"わたしたち|ほりかわ|なら|ある"},{"japanese":"江戸時代には、荷物を運ぶために多くの堀川が掘られた。","romaji":"Edo jidai ni wa, nimotsu o hakobu tame ni ōku no horikawa ga horareta.","german":"In the Edo period, many canals were dug to transport goods.","furigana":"えどじだい|にもつ|はこ|おお|ほりかわ|ほ"},{"japanese":"京都には「堀川通」という大きな通りがある。","romaji":"Kyōto ni wa \"Horikawa-dōri\" to iu ōki na tōri ga aru.","german":"In Kyoto there is a big street called Horikawa-dori.","furigana":"きょうと||おお|とお"}],"notes":"堀川 is a watercourse dug by people, that is, a canal, usually built in the Edo period for shipping and the transport of goods. As a generic word it is rather rare and sounds historical; today it is encountered mainly as a place, river or family name (京都の堀川通, 名古屋の堀川). For canals in the modern sense, one says 運河 or 水路.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"天気予報が外れて、巡回中に雨が降り出しました。","romaji":"Tenki yohō ga hazurete, junkaichū ni ame ga furidashimashita.","german":"The weather forecast was wrong, and it started raining while I was on patrol.","furigana":"てんきよほう|はず|じゅんかいちゅう|あめ|ふ|だ"},{"japanese":"この地区では、夜になると警察官が巡回している。","romaji":"Kono chiku de wa, yoru ni naru to keisatsukan ga junkai shite iru.","german":"In this district, police officers patrol at night.","furigana":"ちく|よる|けいさつかん|じゅんかい"},{"japanese":"駅と病院の間を無料の巡回バスが走っている。","romaji":"Eki to byōin no aida o muryō no junkai basu ga hashitte iru.","german":"A free shuttle bus runs between the station and the hospital.","furigana":"えき|びょういん|あいだ|むりょう|じゅんかい|はし"}],"notes":"巡回 means to visit several places in turn along a set route: to go on patrol (警備員が巡回する), rounds in a hospital or traveling exhibitions (巡回展). As a modifier: 巡回バス \"circuit bus, shuttle bus\". It is an intransitive する-verb; the area covered takes を (校内を巡回する). Related are パトロール (almost synonymous for the police) and the native Japanese 見回る.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"交番の巡査に駅までの道を聞いた。","romaji":"Kōban no junsa ni eki made no michi o kiita.","german":"I asked the police officer at the police box the way to the station.","furigana":"こうばん|じゅんさ|えき|みち|き"},{"japanese":"彼は巡査から巡査部長に昇進した。","romaji":"Kare wa junsa kara junsa buchō ni shōshin shita.","german":"He was promoted from patrolman to police sergeant.","furigana":"かれ|じゅんさ|じゅんさぶちょう|しょうしん"},{"japanese":"事件の現場には、すでに数人の巡査が来ていた。","romaji":"Jiken no genba ni wa, sude ni sūnin no junsa ga kite ita.","german":"Several police officers had already arrived at the scene of the incident.","furigana":"じけん|げんば|すうにん|じゅんさ|き"}],"notes":"巡査 is the lowest rank of the Japanese police, roughly \"patrolman, police constable\"; above it follow 巡査長, 巡査部長, 警部補 and so on. The word sounds official; colloquially one says おまわりさん, neutrally 警察官, or for short 警官. Officers of this rank typically work in a 交番 or on patrol.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ちょっと警戒していることがあるんです。","romaji":"Chotto keikai shite iru koto ga aru n desu.","german":"There is something I am a little wary of.","furigana":"けいかい"},{"japanese":"サミットの間、警察は駅や空港の警戒を強めている。","romaji":"Samitto no aida, keisatsu wa eki ya kūkō no keikai o tsuyomete iru.","german":"During the summit, the police stepped up security at stations and airports.","furigana":"あいだ|けいさつ|えき|くうこう|けいかい|つよ"},{"japanese":"うちの猫は知らない人をすごく警戒する。","romaji":"Uchi no neko wa shiranai hito o sugoku keikai suru.","german":"Our cat is very wary of strangers.","furigana":"ねこ|し|ひと|けいかい"}],"notes":"警戒 is attentive watchfulness against a danger or person: 〜を警戒する \"to be on guard against ..., to be wary of ...\", 警戒を強める/緩める, 警戒心が強い \"to be distrustful\". It is typical of news and severe weather warnings (土砂災害に警戒してください, 警戒レベル). In contrast to 注意 (\"attention, care\", general), 警戒 emphasizes a concrete threat; 用心 is the more everyday \"caution\". Grammatically it is a noun or suru-verb, not an adjective.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"記念硬貨を排水溝に落っことしちゃった。","romaji":"Kinen kouka o haisuikou ni okkotoshichatta.","german":"I dropped the commemorative coin down the drain.","furigana":"きねんこうか|はいすいこう|お"},{"japanese":"大雨で道路の排水が追いつかず、水があふれた。","romaji":"Ōame de dōro no haisui ga oitsukazu, mizu ga afureta.","german":"In the heavy rain the street drainage could not keep up, and the water overflowed.","furigana":"おおあめ|どうろ|はいすい|お|みず"},{"japanese":"洗濯機の排水ホースが外れて、床が水浸しになった。","romaji":"Sentakuki no haisui hōsu ga hazurete, yuka ga mizubitashi ni natta.","german":"The washing machine's drain hose came loose, and the floor was flooded.","furigana":"せんたくき|はいすい|はず|ゆか|みずびた"}],"notes":"排水 refers to draining or pumping away water or wastewater: 排水溝 \"drainage ditch, gutter\", 排水口 \"drain (in a basin)\", 排水管 \"drainpipe\"; as a suru-verb: 水を排水する. The counterpart is 給水 \"water supply\"; 下水 is the wastewater or the sewer system itself. In technical language 排水量 also means the \"displacement\" of a ship.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"携帯の電源を入れるには、電源ボタンを五秒間長押しする必要があります。","romaji":"Keitai no dengen o ireru ni wa, dengen botan o gobyōkan nagaoshi suru hitsuyō ga arimasu.","german":"To turn on the mobile phone, you need to hold down the power button for five seconds.","furigana":"けいたい|でんげん|い|でんげん|ごびょうかんながお|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"携帯をどこかに忘れてきちゃった。","romaji":"Keitai o dokoka ni wasurete kichatta.","german":"I left my phone somewhere.","furigana":"けいたい|わす"},{"japanese":"旅行中は、パスポートを常に携帯してください。","romaji":"Ryokōchū wa, pasupōto o tsune ni keitai shite kudasai.","german":"Please carry your passport with you at all times during the trip.","furigana":"りょこうちゅう|つね|けいたい"}],"notes":"携帯 originally means \"to carry on one's person, to bring along\": 傘を携帯する, 携帯用 \"portable, for taking along\", 携帯食. In everyday speech it is mainly the short form of 携帯電話 \"mobile phone\", often also written ケータイ. For smartphones one nowadays mostly says スマホ; 携帯 remains the general word for the mobile phone (携帯の番号). The verb 携帯する sounds formal and often appears in regulations and notices.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 36"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"過敏性大腸症候群に苦しんでいます。","romaji":"Kabinsei daichō shōkōgun ni kurushinde imasu.","german":"I suffer from irritable bowel syndrome.","furigana":"かびんせいだいちょうしょうこうぐん|くる"},{"japanese":"彼は人に何か言われると、すぐ過敏に反応する。","romaji":"Kare wa hito ni nanika iwareru to, sugu kabin ni hannō suru.","german":"He reacts hypersensitively as soon as anyone says something to him.","furigana":"かれ|ひと|なに|い|かびん|はんのう"},{"japanese":"肌が過敏なので、香料の入った石けんは使えません。","romaji":"Hada ga kabin na node, kōryō no haitta sekken wa tsukaemasen.","german":"My skin is so sensitive that I can't use soap containing fragrance.","furigana":"はだ|かびん|こうりょう|はい|せっ|つか"}],"notes":"過敏 means \"oversensitive\", physically as well as mentally: 過敏な肌, 神経過敏, 〜に過敏に反応する. It is frequent in medical terms such as 過敏症 \"hypersensitivity, allergy\" or 過敏性腸症候群 \"irritable bowel syndrome\". In contrast to 敏感 (\"sensitive, perceptive\", neutral to positive), 過敏 is always negative: too much reaction. Grammatically a な-adjective or noun.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ウェイトレスってのは機敏に動くものなのに、彼女はとんでもなく鈍いんだよ。","romaji":"Weitoresu tte no wa kibin ni ugoku mono na noni, kanojo wa tondemonaku nibui n da yo.","german":"Waitresses are supposed to move briskly, but she is incredibly slow.","furigana":"きびん|うご|かのじょ|にぶ"},{"japanese":"猫は機敏な動きでネズミを捕まえた。","romaji":"Neko wa kibin na ugoki de nezumi o tsukamaeta.","german":"The cat caught the mouse with a nimble movement.","furigana":"ねこ|きびん|うご|つか"},{"japanese":"トラブルが起きても、彼女は機敏に対応してくれた。","romaji":"Toraburu ga okite mo, kanojo wa kibin ni taiō shite kureta.","german":"Even when trouble occurred, she responded quickly and sensibly.","furigana":"お|かのじょ|きびん|たいおう"}],"notes":"機敏 is a na-adjective for \"quick, nimble, quick to react\" and describes both physical agility and quick, clever reaction: 機敏な動き, 機敏に動く, 機敏に対応する. It sounds somewhat more written than the everyday 素早い; すばしこい suits small animals and children better. Do not confuse it with 敏感 \"sensitive\". The opposite is 鈍い \"sluggish, slow\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その犬の鋭敏な嗅覚は、何人もの麻薬密売人を捕らえました。","romaji":"Sono inu no eibin na kyūkaku wa, nannin mo no mayaku mitsubaijin o toraemashita.","german":"That dog's keen sense of smell has caught many drug dealers.","furigana":"いぬ|えいびん|きゅうかく|なんにん|まやくみつばいじん|と"},{"japanese":"彼は鋭敏な頭脳で、問題の本質をすぐに理解した。","romaji":"Kare wa eibin na zunō de, mondai no honshitsu o sugu ni rikai shita.","german":"With his sharp mind, he immediately understood the heart of the problem.","furigana":"かれ|えいびん|ずのう|もんだい|ほんしつ|りかい"},{"japanese":"このセンサーは温度の変化に鋭敏に反応する。","romaji":"Kono sensā wa ondo no henka ni eibin ni hannō suru.","german":"This sensor reacts sensitively to changes in temperature.","furigana":"おんど|へんか|えいびん|はんのう"}],"notes":"鋭敏 refers to sharp, fine perception (鋭敏な嗅覚, 聴覚が鋭敏だ) and, figuratively, an alert, sharp mind (鋭敏な頭脳). It is written-language and elevated; in everyday speech people say 鋭い or 敏感 instead. In contrast to 過敏 (\"oversensitive\", negative), 鋭敏 is positive to neutral; even measuring instruments can 鋭敏に反応する (react sensitively).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"犯人は鋭利な刃物で通行人に切りつけた。","romaji":"Hannin wa eiri na hamono de tsūkōnin ni kiritsuketa.","german":"The culprit slashed passersby with a sharp blade.","furigana":"はんにん|えいり|はもの|つうこうにん|き"},{"japanese":"割れたガラスは鋭利なので、素手で触らないでください。","romaji":"Wareta garasu wa eiri na node, sude de sawaranaide kudasai.","german":"Broken glass is sharp, so please do not touch it with bare hands.","furigana":"わ|えいり|すで|さわ"},{"japanese":"彼女は鋭利な分析で、議論の問題点を指摘した。","romaji":"Kanojo wa eiri na bunseki de, giron no mondaiten o shiteki shita.","german":"With a razor-sharp analysis, she pointed out the problems in the discussion.","furigana":"かのじょ|えいり|ぶんせき|ぎろん|もんだいてん|してき"}],"notes":"鋭利 describes a sharp edge or point; 鋭利な刃物 \"a sharp object, a sharp blade\" is practically a set phrase in police and news reports. The word is written-language; in everyday speech people say 鋭い or, for knives, よく切れる. Figuratively it can also describe a razor-sharp mind or a sharp analysis, but 鋭い is more common for that. Grammatically a な-adjective.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その会社は最新鋭のパソコンを購入することを決定しました。","romaji":"Sono kaisha wa saishin'ei no pasokon o kounyuu suru koto o kettei shimashita.","german":"The company decided to purchase the most up-to-date computers.","furigana":"かいしゃ|さいしんえい|こうにゅう|けってい"},{"japanese":"今年の文学賞は、二十代の新鋭作家が受賞した。","romaji":"Kotoshi no bungakushō wa, nijūdai no shin'ei sakka ga jushō shita.","german":"This year's literature prize was won by an up-and-coming young author in his twenties.","furigana":"ことし|ぶんがくしょう|にじゅうだい|しんえいさっか|じゅしょう"},{"japanese":"決勝では、ベテラン選手と新鋭が対戦する。","romaji":"Kesshō de wa, beteran senshu to shin'ei ga taisen suru.","german":"In the final, a veteran player will face a newcomer.","furigana":"けっしょう|せんしゅ|しんえい|たいせん"}],"notes":"新鋭 refers to something new that is at the same time especially capable: for people \"up-and-coming, rising talent\" (新鋭作家, 期待の新鋭), for machines and equipment \"new and high-performance\". Very common is the intensified form 最新鋭 \"state-of-the-art, up to date\" (最新鋭の設備). In contrast to the neutral 新人 \"newcomer\", 新鋭 is complimentary; the counterpart for people is ベテラン. Before nouns it is usually used with の.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼に、部屋にカーペットを敷くよう説得しました。","romaji":"Kare ni, heya ni kāpetto o shiku yō settoku shimashita.","german":"I persuaded him to lay a carpet in his room.","furigana":"かれ|へや|し|せっとく"},{"japanese":"寝る前に、和室に布団を敷いた。","romaji":"Neru mae ni, washitsu ni futon o shiita.","german":"Before going to bed, I laid out the futon in the Japanese-style room.","furigana":"ね|まえ|わしつ|ふとん|し"},{"japanese":"明治時代に、新橋と横浜の間に日本で初めての鉄道が敷かれた。","romaji":"Meiji jidai ni, Shinbashi to Yokohama no aida ni Nihon de hajimete no tetsudō ga shikareta.","german":"In the Meiji era, Japan's first railway was laid between Shinbashi and Yokohama.","furigana":"めいじじだい|しんばし|よこはま|あいだ|にほん|はじ|てつどう|し"}],"notes":"敷く means to spread something flat over a surface or put it underneath: 布団を敷く \"to lay out the futon\", カーペットを敷く, 座布団を敷く. Figuratively: 線路を敷く \"to lay tracks\", 体制/戒厳令を敷く \"to put a system/martial law in place\"; idiomatically 尻に敷く \"to have (a husband) under one's thumb\". For the futon, the opposite is 布団を畳む/上げる. There is no separate intransitive counterpart; one uses 敷いてある or the passive 敷かれる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"丘の上に古い屋敷が建っている。","romaji":"Oka no ue ni furui yashiki ga tatte iru.","german":"There is an old manor house on the hill.","furigana":"おか|うえ|ふる|やしき|た"},{"japanese":"金沢には、江戸時代の武家屋敷が今も残っている。","romaji":"Kanazawa ni wa, Edo jidai no buke yashiki ga ima mo nokotte iru.","german":"In Kanazawa, samurai residences from the Edo period are still preserved today.","furigana":"かなざわ|えどじだい|ぶけやしき|いま|のこ"},{"japanese":"遊園地のお化け屋敷が怖くて、途中で出てしまった。","romaji":"Yūenchi no obake yashiki ga kowakute, tochū de dete shimatta.","german":"The haunted house at the amusement park was so scary that I left in the middle.","furigana":"ゆうえんち|ば|やしき|こわ|とちゅう|で"}],"notes":"屋敷 is a large residential house together with its grounds, an \"estate, mansion\"; for the house of a distinguished family often with the polite お: お屋敷. Fixed compounds are 武家屋敷 \"samurai residence\", 屋敷町 \"villa district\", お化け屋敷 \"haunted house\" and ゴミ屋敷 \"hoarder house\". For an ordinary house one says 家 or 住宅; 邸宅 is an even more elevated word for a mansion.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お座敷のどこかでコオロギが鳴いている。","romaji":"Ozashiki no doko ka de kōrogi ga naite iru.","german":"A cricket is chirping somewhere in the tatami room.","furigana":"ざしき|な"},{"japanese":"居酒屋で「テーブル席とお座敷、どちらがよろしいですか」と聞かれた。","romaji":"Izakaya de \"tēburu seki to ozashiki, dochira ga yoroshii desu ka\" to kikareta.","german":"At the izakaya, I was asked, \"Would you prefer a table seat or a tatami room?\"","furigana":"いざかや|せき|ざしき|き"},{"japanese":"お客さんを座敷に通して、お茶を出した。","romaji":"Okyakusan o zashiki ni tōshite, ocha o dashita.","german":"I showed the guest into the tatami room and served tea.","furigana":"きゃく|ざしき|とお|ちゃ|だ"}],"notes":"座敷 is a room covered with tatami, traditionally the good reception room of a Japanese house into which guests are shown (座敷に通す). In restaurants, (お)座敷 denotes the tatami area where one sits without shoes at a low table, as opposed to テーブル席. 和室 is the neutral general term for a Japanese-style room. For geisha, お座敷 also means the banquet to which they are called (お座敷がかかる).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この賞状は、中村コウイチ殿の優れた功績を称えて与えられます。","romaji":"Kono shōjō wa, Nakamura Kōichi-dono no sugureta kōseki o tataete ataeraremasu.","german":"This certificate is awarded to Mr. Kōichi Nakamura in recognition of his outstanding achievements.","furigana":"しょうじょう|なかむら|どの|すぐ|こうせき|たた|あた"},{"japanese":"通知書の宛名には「田中花子殿」と書かれていました。","romaji":"Tsūchisho no atena ni wa \"Tanaka Hanako-dono\" to kakarete imashita.","german":"On the addressee line of the notice was written \"Ms. Hanako Tanaka\".","furigana":"つうちしょ|あてな|たなかはなこどの|か"}],"notes":"〜殿 is a formal form of address after a name, for example on certificates or in official and business letters. The reading here is どの: 中村コウイチ殿. It is not a general recommendation for every personal form of address; the appropriate form depends on the letter and its institutional use. The sign 〜 is an unspoken placeholder. This card practices the address suffix and not 殿・との in the sense of \"lord\" or \"prince\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"暇つぶしにイケメンの殿様の絵を描きました。","romaji":"Himatsubushi ni ikemen no tonosama no e o egakimashita.","german":"To pass the time, I drew a handsome feudal lord.","furigana":"ひま|とのさま|え|えが"},{"japanese":"江戸時代、殿様は一年おきに江戸と自分の国を行き来した。","romaji":"Edo jidai, tonosama wa ichinen oki ni Edo to jibun no kuni o ikiki shita.","german":"In the Edo period, the lords commuted every year alternately between Edo and their own domains.","furigana":"えどじだい|とのさま|いちねん|えど|じぶん|くに|い|き"},{"japanese":"あの店は客が来るのを待っているだけの殿様商売だ。","romaji":"Ano mise wa kyaku ga kuru no o matte iru dake no tonosama shōbai da.","german":"This shop just waits for customers to come - doing business like a lord of the manor.","furigana":"みせ|きゃく|く|ま|とのさましょうばい"}],"notes":"殿様 is the respectful term for a feudal lord (大名) of the Edo period or, generally, a high-ranking lord; in samurai dramas he is addressed as 殿 or 殿様. Figuratively it refers to an unworldly, spoiled man who is used to being waited on; hence 殿様商売 \"doing business without customer orientation\". Above the 殿様 stood the 将軍 \"shogun\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二番打者が犠打を決めて、ランナーを二塁に進めた。","romaji":"Niban dasha ga gida o kimete, rannā o nirui ni susumeta.","german":"The number two batter laid down a sacrifice hit and moved the runner to second base.","furigana":"にばんだしゃ|ぎだ|き|にるい|すす"},{"japanese":"監督は一点を取るために犠打のサインを出した。","romaji":"Kantoku wa itten o toru tame ni gida no sain o dashita.","german":"To score one run, the manager gave the sign for a sacrifice hit.","furigana":"かんとく|いってん|と|ぎだ|だ"},{"japanese":"彼はシーズン犠打の記録を持っている。","romaji":"Kare wa shīzun gida no kiroku o motte iru.","german":"He holds the season record for sacrifice hits.","furigana":"かれ|ぎだ|きろく|も"}],"notes":"犠打 is a baseball technical term for the \"sacrifice hit\": the batter is deliberately put out so that a runner can advance or score. It includes 犠牲バント (sacrifice bunt) and 犠牲フライ (sacrifice fly, 犠飛 for short). In sports reports it is 犠打を決める \"to successfully lay down a sacrifice hit\". The word is technical and written; fans usually just say 送りバント.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日ゲームをプレイすると、いつもの二倍のコインが獲得できるよ。","romaji":"Kyō gēmu o purei suru to, itsumo no nibai no koin ga kakutoku dekiru yo.","german":"If you play the game today, you can get twice as many coins as usual.","furigana":"きょう|にばい|かくとく"},{"japanese":"その選手はオリンピックで金メダルを獲得した。","romaji":"Sono senshu wa Orinpikku de kin medaru o kakutoku shita.","german":"The athlete won a gold medal at the Olympic Games.","furigana":"せんしゅ|きん|かくとく"},{"japanese":"新しい政党は選挙で十議席を獲得した。","romaji":"Atarashii seitō wa senkyo de jū giseki o kakutoku shita.","german":"The new party won ten seats in the election.","furigana":"あたら|せいとう|せんきょ|じゅうぎせき|かくとく"}],"notes":"獲得 means to win something through effort or in competition: メダル/賞/議席/権利を獲得する, ポイント獲得, 顧客獲得 \"customer acquisition\". It is more written-language than 手に入れる or もらう and typical of news, sports and business. In contrast to 取得 (\"to acquire\" licenses, qualifications, data), 獲得 emphasizes winning against competition.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"畑を荒らしていたイノシシが、わなで捕獲された。","romaji":"Hatake o arashite ita inoshishi ga, wana de hokaku sareta.","german":"The wild boar that had been ravaging the fields was captured with a trap.","furigana":"はたけ|あ|ほかく"},{"japanese":"この海ではマグロの捕獲量が制限されている。","romaji":"Kono umi de wa maguro no hokakuryō ga seigen sarete iru.","german":"In this sea, the catch quota for tuna is limited.","furigana":"うみ|ほかくりょう|せいげん"},{"japanese":"逃げ出したサルを捕獲するのに、三時間もかかった。","romaji":"Nigedashita saru o hokaku suru no ni, sanjikan mo kakatta.","german":"It took a full three hours to recapture the escaped monkey.","furigana":"に|だ|ほかく|さんじかん"}],"notes":"捕獲 is the capturing or taking of animals - game, fish, whales, escaped zoo animals: クマを捕獲する, わなで捕獲する, 捕獲量 \"catch volume\". It is a factual word from official, news and technical language; in daily life one says 捕まえる. For the arrest of people one does not use 捕獲 but 逮捕; prisoners of war are called 捕虜.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ライオンは草むらに隠れて、じっと獲物を狙っている。","romaji":"Raion wa kusamura ni kakurete, jitto emono o neratte iru.","german":"The lion hides in the grass and lies motionless in wait for its prey.","furigana":"くさ|かく|えもの|ねら"},{"japanese":"今日の釣りの獲物は、小さなアジが三匹だけだった。","romaji":"Kyō no tsuri no emono wa, chiisa na aji ga sanbiki dake datta.","german":"The catch from today's fishing was only three small sticklebacks.","furigana":"きょう|つ|えもの|ちい|"},{"japanese":"詐欺グループは、一人暮らしのお年寄りを獲物にしている。","romaji":"Sagi gurūpu wa, hitorigurashi no otoshiyori o emono ni shite iru.","german":"Fraud gangs target elderly people who live alone.","furigana":"さぎ|ひとりぐ|としよ|えもの"}],"notes":"獲物 is the animal that is or is to be taken in hunting or fishing: 獲物を狙う/追う/捕らえる. Figuratively it also refers to victims that swindlers or criminals have targeted (〜を獲物にする), as well as captured things. The reading is えもの. For spoils of war one rather says 戦利品, for the catch when angling also 釣果 (technical).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どれくらいの頻度で家に掃除機をかけますか？","romaji":"Dore kurai no hindo de ie ni sōjiki o kakemasu ka?","german":"How often do you vacuum your house?","furigana":"ひんど|いえ|そうじき"},{"japanese":"この単語は使用頻度が高いので、必ず覚えておきましょう。","romaji":"Kono tango wa shiyō hindo ga takai node, kanarazu oboete okimashō.","german":"This word is used very frequently, so be sure to learn it.","furigana":"たんご|しようひんど|たか|かなら|おぼ"},{"japanese":"最近、地震の頻度が増えているような気がする。","romaji":"Saikin, jishin no hindo ga fuete iru yō na ki ga suru.","german":"I feel that earthquakes have been occurring more often recently.","furigana":"さいきん|じしん|ひんど|ふ|き"}],"notes":"頻度 is the \"frequency\" with which something occurs. It is combined with 高い/低い rather than 多い/少ない: 頻度が高い \"frequent\", 使用頻度, 高頻度. With で it gives the rate: 週に一回の頻度で \"once a week\". In conversation people usually just ask どのくらい or 何回; 頻度 sounds more factual. Related are 頻繁 \"frequent\" (な-adjective) and 回数 \"number of times\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"みんな、頻発する地震に、ストレスが溜まりに溜まっているんだよ。","romaji":"Minna, hinpatsu suru jishin ni, sutoresu ga tamari ni tamatte iru n da yo.","german":"Everyone is under more and more stress because of the repeated earthquakes.","furigana":"ひんぱつ|じしん|た|た"},{"japanese":"この交差点では事故が頻発している。","romaji":"Kono kōsaten de wa jiko ga hinpatsu shite iru.","german":"Accidents occur frequently at this intersection.","furigana":"こうさてん|じこ|ひんぱつ"},{"japanese":"最近、システムのトラブルが頻発して、仕事にならない。","romaji":"Saikin, shisutemu no toraburu ga hinpatsu shite, shigoto ni naranai.","german":"Recently, system troubles keep happening, and I can't get any work done.","furigana":"さいきん|ひんぱつ|しごと"}],"notes":"頻発 means that something - usually undesirable - occurs repeatedly and in clusters: 事故/事件/地震/トラブルが頻発する. It is an intransitive する-verb, and the event takes が; before nouns: 頻発する地震. Typical of the news; in conversation people say よく起きる or しょっちゅう起きる instead. 多発 is almost synonymous but stresses the large number (事故多発地帯), while 頻発 stresses the dense succession.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"魚の多くは、卵を産んで生殖する。","romaji":"Sakana no ōku wa, tamago o unde seishoku suru.","german":"Most fish reproduce by laying eggs.","furigana":"さかな|おお|たまご|う|せいしょく"},{"japanese":"生物の授業で、植物の生殖のしくみを学んだ。","romaji":"Seibutsu no jugyō de, shokubutsu no seishoku no shikumi o mananda.","german":"In biology class, we learned how plants reproduce.","furigana":"せいぶつ|じゅぎょう|しょくぶつ|せいしょく|まな"},{"japanese":"この化学物質は、動物の生殖能力に影響を与えるおそれがある。","romaji":"Kono kagaku busshitsu wa, dōbutsu no seishoku nōryoku ni eikyō o ataeru osore ga aru.","german":"This chemical may affect the reproductive ability of animals.","furigana":"かがくぶっしつ|どうぶつ|せいしょくのうりょく|えいきょう|あた"}],"notes":"生殖 is the biological technical term for \"reproduction\": 有性生殖/無性生殖 \"sexual/asexual reproduction\", 生殖器 \"reproductive organs\", 生殖能力 \"reproductive ability\". It is factual and scientific and sounds stiff in everyday speech. 繁殖, by contrast, means the multiplication of a population (ペットを繁殖させる, カビが繁殖する); for humans, everyday speech uses 出産 or 子供を作る.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"銀行に預けたお金が、利息で少しずつ殖えていく。","romaji":"Ginkō ni azuketa okane ga, risoku de sukoshi zutsu fuete iku.","german":"The money deposited at the bank gradually grows with interest.","furigana":"ぎんこう|あず|かね|りそく|すこ|ふ"},{"japanese":"この細菌は、暖かい場所で急速に殖える。","romaji":"Kono saikin wa, atatakai basho de kyūsoku ni fueru.","german":"These bacteria multiply rapidly in warm places.","furigana":"さいきん|あたた|ばしょ|きゅうそく|ふ"},{"japanese":"池の金魚が殖えて、だんだん狭くなってきた。","romaji":"Ike no kingyo ga fuete, dandan semaku natte kita.","german":"The goldfish in the pond have multiplied, and it is gradually getting crowded.","furigana":"いけ|きんぎょ|ふ|せま"}],"notes":"殖える is a special spelling of ふえる for \"to multiply\" through reproduction or growth - for living things, bacteria or assets and interest. For a general increase (人口, 仕事, 体重) one writes 増える, and today 増える is often used even for multiplication. The verb is intransitive; the transitive counterpart is 殖やす (財産を殖やす). Related are 繁殖 \"reproduction\" and 養殖 \"farming, cultivation\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お兄さんに言われたわ。あなたは意志薄弱だから私と別れることができないけど、本当は別れたいんだって。","romaji":"Oniisan ni iwareta wa. Anata wa ishi hakujaku da kara watashi to wakareru koto ga dekinai kedo, hontou wa wakaretai n da tte.","german":"Your brother told me that you are too weak-willed to break up with me, although you actually want to.","furigana":"にい|い|いしはくじゃく|わたし|わか|ほんとう|わか"},{"japanese":"証拠が薄弱なため、裁判所は訴えを認めなかった。","romaji":"Shōko ga hakujaku na tame, saibansho wa uttae o mitomenakatta.","german":"Because the evidence was too thin, the court dismissed the suit.","furigana":"しょうこ|はくじゃく|さいばんしょ|うった|みと"},{"japanese":"その主張は根拠が薄弱だと言わざるを得ない。","romaji":"Sono shuchō wa konkyo ga hakujaku da to iwazaru o enai.","german":"Unfortunately, it must be said that this claim stands on weak ground.","furigana":"しゅちょう|こんきょ|はくじゃく|い|え"}],"notes":"薄弱 means \"weak, thin, standing on weak ground\" and is combined mainly with abstract things: 根拠が薄弱だ \"the grounds are thin\", 証拠が薄弱, 理由が薄弱. Applied to people it appears almost only in the fixed combination 意志薄弱 \"weak-willed\". It is written-language; in conversation one rather says 弱い or 根拠が乏しい. For physical weakness one does not use 薄弱 but 虚弱 or 弱い.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は薄情者だし、どっちの味方かサッパリ分からないよ。","romaji":"Kare wa hakujōmono da shi, dotchi no mikata ka sappari wakaranai yo.","german":"He is heartless, and I have no idea whose side he is on.","furigana":"かれ|はくじょうもの|みかた|わ"},{"japanese":"困っている友達を見捨てるなんて、薄情だね。","romaji":"Komatte iru tomodachi o misuteru nante, hakujō da ne.","german":"Abandoning a friend in need - how heartless.","furigana":"こま|ともだち|みす|はくじょう"},{"japanese":"引っ越してから一度も連絡しないなんて、薄情な人だと思われるよ。","romaji":"Hikkoshite kara ichido mo renraku shinai nante, hakujō na hito da to omowareru yo.","german":"If you never get in touch after moving away, people will think you are heartless.","furigana":"ひ|こ|いちど|れんらく|はくじょう|ひと|おも"}],"notes":"薄情 is a na-adjective for \"heartless, cold-hearted, disloyal\" - above all for a lack of compassion or loyalty toward people close to one (friends, family, ex-partners). Often used as a reproach: 薄情だ, 薄情な人, 薄情者. 冷たい is more everyday; 冷酷 sounds much harsher (\"cruelly cold\"). The opposite is 情に厚い or 人情がある.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あなたは爆発の衝撃で気絶したのよ。","romaji":"Anata wa bakuhatsu no shōgeki de kizetsu shita no yo.","german":"You fainted from the blast wave of an explosion.","furigana":"ばくはつ|しょうげき|きぜつ"},{"japanese":"彼の突然の引退発表は、ファンに大きな衝撃を与えた。","romaji":"Kare no totsuzen no intai happyō wa, fan ni ōki na shōgeki o ataeta.","german":"His sudden announcement of resignation was a great shock for the fans.","furigana":"かれ|とつぜん|いんたいはっぴょう|おお|しょうげき|あた"},{"japanese":"このケースは衝撃に強いので、スマホを落としても安心です。","romaji":"Kono kēsu wa shōgeki ni tsuyoi node, sumaho o otoshite mo anshin desu.","german":"This case is shockproof, so nothing happens if the smartphone is dropped.","furigana":"しょうげき|つよ|お|あんしん"}],"notes":"衝撃 is both a physical shock or impact (衝撃に強い \"shock-resistant\", 衝撃を吸収する) and a mental shock (衝撃を受ける, 衝撃を与える, 衝撃的な \"shocking\"). In contrast to ショック, which in daily life mainly means the mental shock, 衝撃 sounds more factual and stronger and is typical of news and headlines (衝撃の事実).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"トーフグで仕事をするという栄誉を得たとき、そのオファーに感激して言葉に詰まりました。","romaji":"Tōfugu de shigoto o suru to iu eiyo o eta toki, sono ofā ni kangeki shite kotoba ni tsumarimashita.","german":"When I was given the honor of working at Tofugu, I was so thrilled by the offer that I was at a loss for words.","furigana":"しごと|えいよ|え|かんげき|ことば|つ"},{"japanese":"彼はノーベル賞という最高の栄誉に輝いた。","romaji":"Kare wa Nōberu-shō to iu saikō no eiyo ni kagayaita.","german":"He was crowned with the highest honor, the Nobel Prize.","furigana":"かれ|しょう|さいこう|えいよ|かがや"},{"japanese":"国民栄誉賞は、国民に希望を与えた人に贈られる。","romaji":"Kokumin Eiyoshō wa, kokumin ni kibō o ataeta hito ni okurareru.","german":"The People's Honor Award is presented to people who have given hope to the nation.","furigana":"こくみんえいよしょう|こくみん|きぼう|あた|ひと|おく"}],"notes":"栄誉 is an honor or distinction publicly conferred, and it sounds formal. Typical combinations: 栄誉に輝く \"to be honored with a distinction\", 栄誉を受ける/得る, 栄誉ある賞; well known is the 国民栄誉賞. It should be distinguished from 名誉 \"personal honor, good reputation\" (名誉を傷つける, 名誉教授) and 光栄 for one's own feeling \"it is an honor for me\" (光栄です).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"頑張ったご褒美がもらえることを期待しています。","romaji":"Ganbatta go hōbi ga moraeru koto o kitai shite imasu.","german":"I hope to get a reward for my hard work.","furigana":"がんば|ほうび|きたい"},{"japanese":"テストで百点を取ったご褒美に、母がケーキを買ってくれた。","romaji":"Tesuto de hyakuten o totta gohōbi ni, haha ga kēki o katte kureta.","german":"As a reward for a perfect score on the test, my mother bought me a cake.","furigana":"ひゃくてん|と|ほうび|はは|か"},{"japanese":"一週間がんばった自分へのご褒美に、高いチョコレートを買った。","romaji":"Isshūkan ganbatta jibun e no gohōbi ni, takai chokorēto o katta.","german":"After a tiring week, I treated myself to expensive chocolate.","furigana":"いっしゅうかん|じぶん|ほうび|たか|か"}],"notes":"褒美 is a reward for good performance or good behavior and almost always appears with the polite ご: ご褒美. Typical are ご褒美をもらう/あげる and 〜のご褒美に \"as a reward for ...\". Very popular is 自分へのご褒美 \"a treat for oneself\". The word sounds personal and warm; official prizes are called 賞 or 賞品, payment for work 報酬.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"褒賞を受け取るために舞台に上がろうとした時、彼女は階段につまずきました。","romaji":"Houshou o uketoru tame ni butai ni agarou to shita toki, kanojo wa kaidan ni tsumazukimashita.","german":"When she tried to go up on stage to receive her award, she tripped on the stairs.","furigana":"ほうしょう|う|と|ぶたい|あ|とき|かのじょ|かいだん"},{"japanese":"人命救助をした高校生に、市から褒賞が贈られた。","romaji":"Jinmei kyūjo o shita kōkōsei ni, shi kara hōshō ga okurareta.","german":"The high school student who had saved a life received a commendation from the city.","furigana":"じんめいきゅうじょ|こうこうせい|し|ほうしょう|おく"},{"japanese":"国は長年社会に貢献した人々を褒賞している。","romaji":"Kuni wa naganen shakai ni kōken shita hitobito o hōshō shite iru.","german":"The state honors people who have contributed to society over many years.","furigana":"くに|ながねんしゃかい|こうけん|ひとびと|ほうしょう"}],"notes":"褒賞 is an official reward or commendation for meritorious deeds, granted by the state, authorities or organizations; as a する-verb: 人を褒賞する \"to honor someone\". In contrast to the personal, everyday ご褒美, 褒賞 is formal and official. Beware: the identically read 褒章 refers to the state medals of honor (紫綬褒章 etc.), whereas 褒賞 means the reward or prize in general.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"光っている電球の上に銀色のスクラッチカードを当てると、その銀色の部分の下に何が書かれてるかが透けて見えちゃうことがあるんだよ。","romaji":"Hikatte iru denkyuu no ue ni gin'iro no sukuratchikaado o ateru to, sono gin'iro no bubun no shita ni nani ga kakareteru ka ga sukete miechau koto ga aru n da yo.","german":"If you hold a silver scratch card over a glowing light bulb, you can sometimes see through the silver part to what is written underneath.","furigana":"ひか|でんきゅう|うえ|ぎんいろ|あ|ぎんいろ|ぶぶん|した|なに|か|す|み"},{"japanese":"このシャツは白くて薄いので、下着が透けてしまう。","romaji":"Kono shatsu wa shirokute usui node, shitagi ga sukete shimau.","german":"This shirt is white and thin, so the underwear shows through.","furigana":"しろ|うす|したぎ|す"},{"japanese":"カーテン越しに朝の光が透けて見える。","romaji":"Kāten goshi ni asa no hikari ga sukete mieru.","german":"The morning light shows through the curtain.","furigana":"ご|あさ|ひかり|す|み"}],"notes":"透ける is intransitive: something is so thin or clear that you can see what is behind or beneath it. The form 透けて見える \"to show through, to shimmer through\" is very common; for clothing: 下着が透ける. Figuratively: 本音が透けて見える \"one's true intentions show through\". The transitive counterpart is 透かす (\"to hold up against the light, to let show through\"); related are 透明 and, colloquially, スケスケ \"completely see-through\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"透明人間の男は、バスタオルで自分の体をよーく拭きました。","romaji":"Tōmei ningen no otoko wa, basu taoru de jibun no karada o yōku fukimashita.","german":"The invisible man dried himself thoroughly with a bath towel.","furigana":"とうめいにんげん|おとこ|じぶん|からだ|ふ"},{"japanese":"この湖は水がとても透明で、底まで見える。","romaji":"Kono mizuumi wa mizu ga totemo tōmei de, soko made mieru.","german":"The water of this lake is so clear that you can see all the way to the bottom.","furigana":"みずうみ|みず|とうめい|そこ|み"},{"japanese":"政府には、お金の使い道について透明性が求められている。","romaji":"Seifu ni wa, okane no tsukaimichi ni tsuite tōmeisei ga motomerarete iru.","german":"The government is required to be transparent about how it spends money.","furigana":"せいふ|かね|つか|みち|とうめいせい|もと"}],"notes":"透明 means \"see-through, clear\": 透明なガラス, 透明の袋, 無色透明 \"colorless and clear\"; before nouns with な or の. Figuratively it stands for traceability, above all as 透明性 \"transparency\" in politics and business. 透明人間 is \"the invisible man\". Distinction: 透ける is the verb \"to show through\", and 半透明 means \"translucent, cloudy\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"隣家ではどうやら窓は新聞紙で拭くようです。","romaji":"Rinka de wa dōyara mado wa shinbunshi de fuku yō desu.","german":"It seems that the people in the house next door clean their windows with newspaper.","furigana":"りんか|まど|しんぶんし|ふ"},{"japanese":"夜中に隣家から大きな音が聞こえてきた。","romaji":"Yonaka ni rinka kara ōki na oto ga kikoete kita.","german":"In the middle of the night, a loud noise could be heard from the house next door.","furigana":"よなか|りんか|おお|おと|き"},{"japanese":"火事は隣家にも燃え広がった。","romaji":"Kaji wa rinka ni mo moehirogatta.","german":"The fire spread to the neighboring house as well.","furigana":"かじ|りんか|も|ひろ"}],"notes":"隣家 is the \"neighboring house\" in written, factual language - typical for news, police reports or literature (隣家に燃え移る). In everyday speech people almost always say 隣の家, or お隣(さん) for the neighbors themselves. Grammatically it is a noun that takes の before other nouns (隣家の住人).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"政府は近隣諸国との関係を大切にしている。","romaji":"Seifu wa kinrin shokoku to no kankei o taisetsu ni shite iru.","german":"The government values its relations with neighboring countries.","furigana":"せいふ|きんりんしょこく|かんけい|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"夜遅くまで騒ぐと、近隣の住民に迷惑がかかる。","romaji":"Yoru osoku made sawagu to, kinrin no jūmin ni meiwaku ga kakaru.","german":"If you make noise until late at night, you disturb the residents nearby.","furigana":"よるおそ|さわ|きんりん|じゅうみん|めいわく"},{"japanese":"工事の前に、近隣の方々を対象に説明会を開いた。","romaji":"Kōji no mae ni, kinrin no katagata o taishō ni setsumeikai o hiraita.","german":"Before the construction work, an information session was held for the neighborhood.","furigana":"こうじ|まえ|きんりん|かたがた|たいしょう|せつめいかい|ひら"}],"notes":"近隣 refers to the nearby surroundings or the neighborhood of a place and usually takes の before nouns: 近隣の住民 \"nearby residents\", 近隣諸国 \"neighboring states\", 近隣トラブル \"neighborhood dispute\". It is factual and formal (authorities, news, notices); in conversation people say 近所 instead. 隣国, by contrast, means a single, directly adjacent country.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どうして隣国の肩を持つのさ？日本人じゃないの？","romaji":"Dōshite ringoku no kata o motsu no sa? Nipponjin ja nai no?","german":"Warum ergreifst du Partei für das Nachbarland? Bist du kein Japaner?","furigana":"りんごく|かた|も|にっぽんじん"},{"japanese":"ドイツは九つの隣国と国境を接している。","romaji":"Doitsu wa kokonotsu no ringoku to kokkyō o sesshite iru.","german":"Deutschland grenzt an neun Nachbarländer.","furigana":"ここの|りんごく|こっきょう|せっ"},{"japanese":"隣国との間で、領土をめぐる問題が続いている。","romaji":"Ringoku to no aida de, ryōdo o meguru mondai ga tsuzuite iru.","german":"Mit dem Nachbarland gibt es weiterhin einen Territorialstreit.","furigana":"りんごく|あいだ|りょうど|もんだい|つづ"}],"notes":"隣国 ist ein angrenzendes Land, das „Nachbarland“: 隣国と国境を接する, 隣国との関係. Das Wort klingt sachlich und ist in Nachrichten und Politik üblich; mündlich sagt man oft 隣の国. Für mehrere Länder einer Region sagt man 近隣諸国. Neben りんごく ist selten auch die Lesung りんこく zu hören.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"壁の絵はとても雅致がありますね。どなたの作品ですか？","romaji":"Kabe no e wa totemo gachi ga arimasu ne. Donata no sakuhin desu ka?","german":"This picture on the wall is very tasteful. Who painted it?","furigana":"かべ|え|がち|さくひん"},{"japanese":"この庭では、古い石の灯籠が雅致を添えている。","romaji":"Kono niwa de wa, furui ishi no tōrō ga gachi o soete iru.","german":"In this garden, an old stone lantern lends the whole an elegant touch.","furigana":"にわ|ふる|いし|とうろう|がち|そ"},{"japanese":"彼の手紙は、雅致に富んだ美しい文章で書かれていた。","romaji":"Kare no tegami wa, gachi ni tonda utsukushii bunshō de kakarete ita.","german":"His letter was written in a cultivated, elegant language.","furigana":"かれ|てがみ|がち|と|うつく|ぶんしょう|か"}],"notes":"雅致 refers to a fine, cultivated elegance with artistic taste, especially in works of art, gardens, handwriting or texts: 雅致がある, 雅致に富む, 雅致を添える. It is a rare, elevated literary word. In daily life one rather says 上品, 趣がある or 風情がある; related is 優雅 \"graceful, elegant\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子供ができればすぐ、嬉々として報告してくれるだろう。","romaji":"Kodomo ga dekireba sugu, kiki to shite houkoku shite kureru darou.","german":"As soon as they have a child, they will surely tell us about it with joy.","furigana":"こども|きき|ほうこく"},{"japanese":"子供たちは嬉々として雪の中を走り回っていた。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa kiki to shite yuki no naka o hashirimawatte ita.","german":"The children were running around in the snow gleefully.","furigana":"こども|きき|ゆき|なか|はし|まわ"},{"japanese":"彼は嬉々として新しいゲームの話を始めた。","romaji":"Kare wa kiki to shite atarashii gēmu no hanashi o hajimeta.","german":"He started talking enthusiastically about the new game.","furigana":"かれ|きき|あたら|はなし|はじ"}],"notes":"嬉々 (also written 喜々 or 嬉嬉) describes visible, exuberant joy and appears almost only in the fixed form 嬉々として \"joyfully, with enthusiasm\" before a verb: 嬉々として働く/話す/遊ぶ. It is written-literary; in conversation people say 喜んで or 楽しそうに. It sometimes sounds slightly ironic when someone does something questionable with enthusiasm.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自転車で転んで、膝小僧が痛い。","romaji":"Jitensha de koronde, hizakozō ga itai.","german":"I fell off my bicycle and my knee hurts.","furigana":"じてんしゃ|ころ|ひざこぞう|いた"},{"japanese":"子供のころは、いつも膝小僧に絆創膏を貼っていた。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro wa, itsumo hizakozō ni bansōkō o hatte ita.","german":"As a child, I always had a plaster on my knee.","furigana":"こども|ひざこぞう|ばんそうこう|は"},{"japanese":"ズボンの膝小僧のところに穴が開いてしまった。","romaji":"Zubon no hizakozō no tokoro ni ana ga aite shimatta.","german":"A hole has opened up in the knee of my pants.","furigana":"ひざこぞう|あな|あ"}],"notes":"膝小僧 is a colloquial, affectionate word for the round front of the knee, that is, the area of the kneecap - literally something like \"knee boy\". It is used above all for children and minor injuries: 膝小僧をすりむく \"to scrape one's knee\". The anatomical technical term for the kneecap is 膝蓋骨; in general people simply say 膝.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このチューリップの花の中には妖精が住んでいるんだ。","romaji":"Kono chūrippu no hana no naka ni wa yōsei ga sunde iru n da.","german":"A fairy lives inside this tulip flower.","furigana":"はな|なか|ようせい|す"},{"japanese":"ティンカー・ベルは「ピーター・パン」に出てくる有名な妖精です。","romaji":"Tinkaa Beru wa \"Piitaa Pan\" ni dete kuru yuumei na yousei desu.","german":"Tinker Bell is the famous fairy from \"Peter Pan\".","furigana":"で|ゆうめい|ようせい"},{"japanese":"舞台の上の彼女は、まるで妖精のように軽やかに踊っていた。","romaji":"Butai no ue no kanojo wa, marude yousei no you ni karoyaka ni odotte ita.","german":"On stage, she danced lightly, just like a fairy.","furigana":"ぶたい|うえ|かのじょ|ようせい||おど"}],"notes":"妖精 refers to fairies, elves and similar small nature spirits from European fairy tales and fantasy, e.g. 森の妖精 \"forest fairy\" or 花の妖精 \"flower elf\". Figuratively, 妖精のような describes a delicate, almost unreal appearance, for example in dancers or figure skaters. Japanese ghosts and spooks, by contrast, are called 妖怪 or お化け; angels are 天使.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女には妖しい美しさがある。","romaji":"Kanojo ni wa ayashii utsukushisa ga aru.","german":"She has a bewitching beauty.","furigana":"かのじょ|あや|うつく"},{"japanese":"雨にぬれた夜の街で、ネオンが妖しく光っていた。","romaji":"Ame ni nureta yoru no machi de, neon ga ayashiku hikatte ita.","german":"In the rain-soaked night city, the neon lights glowed mysteriously.","furigana":"あめ|よる|まち|あや|ひか"},{"japanese":"その絵の女性の目には、見る人を引き込む妖しい魅力がある。","romaji":"Sono e no josei no me ni wa, miru hito o hikikomu ayashii miryoku ga aru.","german":"The eyes of the woman in the picture have a bewitching charm that draws in the viewer.","furigana":"え|じょせい|め|み|ひと|ひ|こ|あや|みりょく"}],"notes":"妖しい describes a mysterious, almost uncanny power of attraction: 妖しい美しさ, 妖しい魅力, 妖しく光る. It is a literary word and sounds seductive and dark. Note: the identically read 怪しい means \"suspicious, doubtful\" (怪しい人, 天気が怪しい); in everyday speech this one is almost always meant, while 妖しい appears mainly in novels, song lyrics and descriptions of atmosphere.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"効果が薄まるので、お酒と一緒には飲まないで下さい。","romaji":"Kōka ga usumaru node, o sake to issho ni wa nomanaide kudasai.","german":"Please do not take this together with alcohol, as the effect will be weakened.","furigana":"こうか|うす|さけ|いっしょ|の|くだ"},{"japanese":"氷が溶けて、ジュースの味が薄まってしまった。","romaji":"Koori ga tokete, juusu no aji ga usumatte shimatta.","german":"The ice melted, and the juice has become watered down.","furigana":"こおり|と|あじ|うす"},{"japanese":"事故から時間がたつにつれて、人々の危機感が薄まっている。","romaji":"Jiko kara jikan ga tatsu ni tsurete, hitobito no kikikan ga usumatte iru.","german":"As time passes since the accident, people's sense of crisis is fading.","furigana":"じこ|じかん|ひとびと|ききかん|うす"}],"notes":"薄まる is intransitive: something becomes thinner or weaker by itself, e.g. 味が薄まる, 色が薄まる, 効果が薄まる. The transitive counterpart is 薄める \"to dilute\" (水で薄める). For fading memories or feelings people usually say 薄れる (記憶が薄れる), whereas 薄まる is used more for liquids, concentrations and effects.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その麺棒、どこで買ったの？","romaji":"Sono menbō, doko de katta no?","german":"Where did you buy that rolling pin?","furigana":"めんぼう|か"},{"japanese":"クッキーの生地を麺棒で薄く伸ばしてください。","romaji":"Kukkii no kiji o menbou de usuku nobashite kudasai.","german":"Roll the cookie dough out thin with the rolling pin.","furigana":"きじ|めんぼう|うす|の"},{"japanese":"麺棒がなかったので、ワインの瓶で代わりにした。","romaji":"Menbou ga nakatta node, wain no bin de kawari ni shita.","german":"I didn't have a rolling pin, so I used a wine bottle instead.","furigana":"めんぼう|びん|か"}],"notes":"麺棒 is the rolling pin used to roll out dough: 生地を麺棒でのばす. Beware of the identically read 綿棒 \"cotton swab\" - in writing the two are easy to tell apart, while in conversation only the context helps. In everyday life it is used mainly for baking and for making うどん or そば at home.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一日中、中華麺が食べたくて仕方がない。","romaji":"Ichinichijuu, chuukamen ga tabetakute shikata ga nai.","german":"I've been dying for Chinese noodles all day.","furigana":"いちにちじゅう|ちゅうかめん|た|しかた"},{"japanese":"ラーメンや焼きそばには、中華麺を使います。","romaji":"Raamen ya yakisoba ni wa, chuukamen o tsukaimasu.","german":"Chinese noodles are used for ramen and yakisoba.","furigana":"や|ちゅうかめん|つか"},{"japanese":"スーパーで生の中華麺を買って、家で冷やし中華を作った。","romaji":"Suupaa de nama no chuukamen o katte, ie de hiyashi chuuka o tsukutta.","german":"I bought fresh Chinese noodles at the supermarket and made cold ramen at home.","furigana":"なま|ちゅうかめん|か|いえ|ひ|ちゅうか|つく"}],"notes":"中華麺 are the yellowish, firm wheat noodles used for ラーメン, 焼きそば and 冷やし中華; their color and firmness come from alkaline water (かんすい). They can be distinguished from Japanese noodles such as うどん and そば. The word appears mainly on packaging and in recipes; when ordering a meal, one simply says ラーメン or 中華そば.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この麺つゆが最高においしいよ。","romaji":"Kono mentsuyu ga saikou ni oishii yo.","german":"This noodle sauce is the best!","furigana":"めん|さいこう"},{"japanese":"冷たいそうめんを麺つゆにつけて食べた。","romaji":"Tsumetai soumen o mentsuyu ni tsukete tabeta.","german":"I dipped cold somen noodles in mentsuyu and ate them.","furigana":"つめ|めん|た"},{"japanese":"この麺つゆは濃縮タイプなので、水で三倍に薄めて使ってください。","romaji":"Kono mentsuyu wa noushuku taipu na node, mizu de sanbai ni usumete tsukatte kudasai.","german":"This mentsuyu is a concentrate, so please dilute it with water at a ratio of 1:3 before use.","furigana":"めん|のうしゅく|みず|さんばい|うす|つか"}],"notes":"麺つゆ is a ready-made seasoning sauce made from dashi, soy sauce, mirin and sugar, usually written in kana as めんつゆ. You dip cold noodles such as そうめん or ざるそば into it, or dilute it as a soup for hot noodles; many households also use it as an all-purpose seasoning for 煮物 or egg dishes. Concentrated types (濃縮タイプ) must be diluted with water.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"拉致被害者全員が無事に帰ってきた。","romaji":"Rachi higaisha zen'in ga buji ni kaette kita.","german":"All of the abduction victims have returned safely.","furigana":"らちひがいしゃぜんいん|ぶじ|かえ"},{"japanese":"男は女性を車で拉致した疑いで逮捕された。","romaji":"Otoko wa josei o kuruma de rachi shita utagai de taiho sareta.","german":"The man was arrested on suspicion of abducting a woman in a car.","furigana":"おとこ|じょせい|くるま|らち|うたが|たいほ"},{"japanese":"拉致問題の解決は、日本政府にとって重要な課題だ。","romaji":"Rachi mondai no kaiketsu wa, Nihon seifu ni totte juuyou na kadai da.","german":"Resolving the abduction issue is an important task for the Japanese government.","furigana":"らちもんだい|かいけつ|にほんせいふ|じゅうよう|かだい"}],"notes":"拉致 means to take someone away by force; as a transitive する-verb: 人を拉致する, often in the passive 拉致される. In Japan the word is closely associated with the abductions of Japanese citizens by North Korea (拉致問題, 拉致被害者). 誘拐, by contrast, is kidnapping in the criminal sense, often of a child and with a ransom demand. Register: news and formal language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自分の作品が世に出るなんて、夢みたいだ。","romaji":"Jibun no sakuhin ga yo ni deru nante, yume mitai da.","german":"It's like a dream that my work is going to be published.","furigana":"じぶん|さくひん|よ|で|ゆめ"},{"japanese":"彼女はこのデビュー小説で一気に世に出た。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kono debyuu shousetsu de ikki ni yo ni deta.","german":"With this debut novel, she became known all at once.","furigana":"かのじょ|しょうせつ|いっき|よ|で"},{"japanese":"スマートフォンが初めて世に出たころは、まだ高くて珍しかった。","romaji":"Sumaatofon ga hajimete yo ni deta koro wa, mada takakute mezurashikatta.","german":"When smartphones first came out, they were still expensive and rare.","furigana":"はじ|よ|で|たか|めずら"}],"notes":"世に出る literally means \"to go out into the world\": a person becomes known and makes a name for themselves (この作品で世に出た), or a work or product reaches the public (新製品が世に出る). The transitive counterpart is 世に出す \"to bring out, to make known\". The expression sounds somewhat elevated; in everyday speech people also say 有名になる or 発売される.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"祖父は晩年、体が虚弱になって、ベッドから出られなくなった。","romaji":"Sofu wa bannen, karada ga kyojaku ni natte, beddo kara derarenaku natta.","german":"In his last years, my grandfather became so frail that he could no longer get out of bed.","furigana":"そふ|ばんねん|からだ|きょじゃく|で"},{"japanese":"子どものころは虚弱体質で、よく学校を休んでいた。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro wa kyojaku taishitsu de, yoku gakkou o yasunde ita.","german":"As a child I had a weak constitution and often missed school.","furigana":"こ|きょじゃくたいしつ|がっこう|やす"},{"japanese":"虚弱な高齢者のために、さまざまな介護サービスが用意されている。","romaji":"Kyojaku na koureisha no tame ni, samazama na kaigo saabisu ga youi sarete iru.","german":"Various nursing-care services are available for frail elderly people.","furigana":"きょじゃく|こうれいしゃ|かいご|ようい"}],"notes":"虚弱 describes a weak, susceptible physical constitution; mostly as a な-adjective (虚弱な子ども) or in the fixed expression 虚弱体質. It refers to the body, not to character or willpower - for that people say 意志が弱い. In everyday speech 体が弱い sounds more natural; 虚弱 is more formal and typical of medical or nursing-care texts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"週の前半は出張で香港にいます。","romaji":"Shū no zenhan wa shutchō de Honkon ni imasu.","german":"I am in Hong Kong on a business trip in the first half of the week.","furigana":"しゅう|ぜんはん|しゅっちょう|ほんこん"},{"japanese":"香港の夜景は「百万ドルの夜景」と呼ばれている。","romaji":"Honkon no yakei wa \"hyakuman doru no yakei\" to yobarete iru.","german":"Hong Kong's night view is called the \"million-dollar night view\".","furigana":"ほんこん|やけい|ひゃくまん|やけい|よ"},{"japanese":"子どものころ、香港映画のアクションシーンに夢中になった。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, Honkon eiga no akushon shiin ni muchuu ni natta.","german":"As a child, I was crazy about the action scenes in Hong Kong movies.","furigana":"こ|ほんこんえいが|むちゅう"}],"notes":"香港 is pronounced ホンコン - the reading comes from Cantonese and does not follow the Japanese kanji readings. In texts the kanji spelling is usually used; in katakana it appears rarely. Common combinations are 香港ドル, 香港映画 and 香港国際空港; as a place, like other cities, it takes に (香港にいる) or で (香港で働く).","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 12 (source: N5; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"中村は、葬儀に一人で行くと言って聞かなかった。","romaji":"Nakamura wa, sougi ni hitori de iku to itte kikanakatta.","german":"Nakamura insisted that he would go to the funeral alone.","furigana":"なかむら|そうぎ|ひとり|い|い|き"},{"japanese":"会社を休んで、祖母の葬儀に参列した。","romaji":"Kaisha o yasunde, sobo no sougi ni sanretsu shita.","german":"I took time off work and attended my grandmother's funeral.","furigana":"かいしゃ|やす|そぼ|そうぎ|さんれつ"},{"japanese":"故人の希望により、葬儀は家族だけで行われました。","romaji":"Kojin no kibou ni yori, sougi wa kazoku dake de okonawaremashita.","german":"At the wishes of the deceased, the funeral was held with family only.","furigana":"こじん|きぼう|そうぎ|かぞく|おこな"}],"notes":"葬儀 is the formal word for a funeral or memorial service; in everyday speech people usually say お葬式. Typical are 葬儀に参列する \"to attend a funeral\", 葬儀を行う, and compounds such as 葬儀場 and 葬儀社. The actual farewell ceremony is called 告別式, and the wake on the evening before お通夜.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どうして日本では、不良少年少女は、コンビニの前で群れる傾向があるんでしょうか。","romaji":"Doushite Nihon de wa, furyou shounen shoujo wa, konbini no mae de mureru keikou ga aru n deshou ka.","german":"Why do delinquent teenagers in Japan tend to hang around in groups in front of convenience stores?","furigana":"にほん|ふりょうしょうねんしょうじょ|まえ|む|けいこう"},{"japanese":"公園の池のまわりに、鳩が群れている。","romaji":"Kouen no ike no mawari ni, hato ga murete iru.","german":"Pigeons are flocking around the pond in the park.","furigana":"こうえん|いけ|はと|む"},{"japanese":"彼は人と群れるのが嫌いで、いつも一人で行動している。","romaji":"Kare wa hito to mureru no ga kirai de, itsumo hitori de koudou shite iru.","german":"He doesn't like going along with the crowd and always acts alone.","furigana":"かれ|ひと|む|きら|ひとり|こうどう"}],"notes":"群れる is intransitive: people or animals gather in groups (鳥が群れる, 子どもたちが群れている). For people it often has a derogatory touch of \"following the herd, forming cliques\" (人と群れるのが嫌い). The noun is 群れ \"flock, herd\"; 群がる, by contrast, stresses converging on a target (蟻が砂糖に群がる).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"何時までに御手洗からバスに戻らなくてはいけませんか？行列ができているので、遅れないか心配してるんですが。","romaji":"Nanji made ni otearai kara basu ni modoranakute wa ikemasen ka? Gyouretsu ga dekite iru node, okurenai ka shinpai shiteru n desu ga.","german":"By what time do we have to be back on the bus from the restroom? There is a long line, and I'm worried about being late.","furigana":"なんじ|おてあらい|もど|ぎょうれつ|おく|しんぱい"},{"japanese":"すみません、お手洗いはどこですか。","romaji":"Sumimasen, otearai wa doko desu ka.","german":"Excuse me, where is the restroom?","furigana":"てあら"},{"japanese":"会議が始まる前に、ちょっとお手洗いに行ってきます。","romaji":"Kaigi ga hajimaru mae ni, chotto otearai ni itte kimasu.","german":"I'll go to the restroom quickly before the meeting starts.","furigana":"かいぎ|はじ|まえ|てあら|い"}],"notes":"おてあらい is the polite, neutral word for toilet and is almost always written お手洗い; the spelling 御手洗 is rare and is easily read as みたらし (e.g. みたらし団子) or みたらい (purification basin at a shrine). お手洗い is more polite than トイレ and fits well when asking for directions in shops or restaurants. Formal and indirect is also 化粧室.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5 (source: N5; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"岐阜県に行くには、電車の乗り換えが必要ですか？","romaji":"Gifuken ni iku ni wa, densha no norikae ga hitsuyō desu ka?","german":"Do I need to change trains to get to Gifu Prefecture?","furigana":"ぎふけん|い|でんしゃ|の|か|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"岐阜県は海に面していない内陸の県です。","romaji":"Gifuken wa umi ni men shite inai nairiku no ken desu.","german":"Gifu Prefecture is an inland prefecture with no access to the sea.","furigana":"ぎふけん|うみ|めん|ないりく|けん"},{"japanese":"夏休みに、岐阜県の白川郷を訪れた。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi ni, Gifuken no Shirakawagou o otozureta.","german":"During summer vacation, I visited Shirakawa-go in Gifu Prefecture.","furigana":"なつやす|ぎふけん||おとず"}],"notes":"岐阜県 is an inland prefecture in the 中部 region with no sea access; its capital is 岐阜市. It is known for cormorant fishing (鵜飼) on the 長良川 river and for the World Heritage village 白川郷. As with all prefectures, 県 can be dropped when it is clear that the region is meant (岐阜に住んでいる); 岐阜 alone can, however, also refer to the city.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"国内の雇用者数は、前の年より増加した。","romaji":"Kokunai no koyoushasuu wa, mae no toshi yori zouka shita.","german":"The number of employed people in the country has risen compared with the previous year.","furigana":"こくない|こようしゃすう|まえ|とし|ぞうか"},{"japanese":"非正規雇用者の割合が年々高くなっている。","romaji":"Hiseiki koyousha no wariai ga nennen takaku natte iru.","german":"The proportion of non-regular employees is rising year by year.","furigana":"ひせいきこようしゃ|わりあい|ねんねんたか"},{"japanese":"賃金について、雇用者側と労働組合の話し合いが続いている。","romaji":"Chingin ni tsuite, koyousha-gawa to roudou kumiai no hanashiai ga tsuzuite iru.","german":"Talks on wages continue between the employer side and the union.","furigana":"ちんぎん|こようしゃがわ|ろうどうくみあい|はな|あ|つづ"}],"notes":"雇用者 is ambiguous: in statistics and economic news it usually refers to employees, i.e. workers (雇用者数, 非正規雇用者). In other texts it can also mean the employer, often clarified by 雇用者側 \"employer side\". Anyone who clearly means the employer should rather say 雇用主 or 雇い主; one's own boss is called 社長 or 上司 in conversation.","levelNote":"Compound: 雇用 (N1) + 者 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地面はガラスの破片だらけなので、気をつけて下さい。","romaji":"Jimen wa garasu no hahendarake na node, ki o tsukete kudasai.","german":"The ground is covered with broken glass, so please be careful.","furigana":"じめん|はへん|き|くだ"},{"japanese":"割れたお皿の破片で指を切ってしまった。","romaji":"Wareta osara no hahen de yubi o kitte shimatta.","german":"I cut my finger on a shard of the broken plate.","furigana":"わ|さら|はへん|ゆび|き"},{"japanese":"事故の現場には、車の破片が散らばっていた。","romaji":"Jiko no genba ni wa, kuruma no hahen ga chirabatte ita.","german":"Pieces of the car were scattered over the scene of the accident.","furigana":"じこ|げんば|くるま|はへん|ち"}],"notes":"破片 is a fragment or splinter of something broken: ガラスの破片 \"glass shards\", 皿の破片, 爆弾の破片. Typical are 破片が飛び散る \"fragments fly about\" and 破片だらけ. The colloquial かけら is similar but is also used figuratively (良心のかけらもない); 破片 stays concrete and sounds somewhat more factual.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ごめんなさい。このTシャツのMサイズは売り切れちゃったんです。","romaji":"Gomen nasai. Kono T-shatsu no M saizu wa urikirechatta n desu.","german":"Sorry. This T-shirt in size M is sold out.","furigana":"う|き"},{"japanese":"遅れてごめんなさい。","romaji":"Okurete gomen nasai.","german":"Sorry I'm late.","furigana":"おく"},{"japanese":"ごめんなさい、その日はちょっと用事があって…。","romaji":"Gomen nasai, sono hi wa chotto youji ga atte...","german":"I'm sorry, I already have something on that day...","furigana":"ひ|ようじ"}],"notes":"ごめんなさい is a personal apology used among friends, in the family or toward children; even more casual are ごめん and ごめんね. Toward superiors or customers it sounds too familiar; there one says すみません or 申し訳ありません. Unlike すみません, it is not used to thank someone or to get their attention, but it is often used for a friendly refusal (\"I'm sorry, but ...\"). It is almost always written in kana; the kanji 御免なさい is rarely seen.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"明日なら、家族の墓参りをした後に、その社会福祉施設に寄れなくもないよ。","romaji":"Ashita nara, kazoku no hakamairi o shita ato ni, sono shakai fukushi shisetsu ni yorenaku mo nai yo.","german":"Tomorrow, after visiting the family grave, I might drop by the social welfare facility.","furigana":"あした|かぞく|はかまい|あと|しゃかいふくししせつ|よ"},{"japanese":"大学で社会福祉を専攻しています。","romaji":"Daigaku de shakai fukushi o senkou shite imasu.","german":"I study social welfare at university.","furigana":"だいがく|しゃかいふくし|せんこう"},{"japanese":"高齢化が進み、社会福祉にかかる費用が増え続けている。","romaji":"Koureika ga susumi, shakai fukushi ni kakaru hiyou ga fuetsuzukete iru.","german":"As society ages, the costs of social welfare keep rising.","furigana":"こうれいか|すす|しゃかいふくし|ひよう|ふ|つづ"}],"notes":"社会福祉 is the state and societal care for people who need support, such as the elderly, people with disabilities or children. Common in compounds: 社会福祉施設 \"social welfare facility\", 社会福祉士 \"certified social worker\", 社会福祉協議会. 社会保障 is broader and also includes pensions and health insurance; 福祉 alone is the shortened form (福祉の仕事). Register: formal, administrative.","levelNote":"Compound: 社会 (N4) + 福祉 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は拳銃を派手なスパンコールで飾り付けしてみました。","romaji":"Watashi wa kenjū o hade na supankōru de kazaritsuke shite mimashita.","german":"I once decorated my pistol with flashy sequins.","furigana":"わたし|けんじゅう|はで|かざ|つ"},{"japanese":"犯人は拳銃を持ったまま逃げている。","romaji":"Hannin wa kenjuu o motta mama nigete iru.","german":"The culprit is on the run with a pistol.","furigana":"はんにん|けんじゅう|も|に"},{"japanese":"日本では、一般の人が拳銃を持つことは法律で禁止されている。","romaji":"Nihon de wa, ippan no hito ga kenjuu o motsu koto wa houritsu de kinshi sarete iru.","german":"In Japan, it is prohibited by law for ordinary people to own a handgun.","furigana":"にほん|いっぱん|ひと|けんじゅう|も|ほうりつ|きんし"}],"notes":"拳銃 is the handgun in general, that is, a pistol or revolver; colloquially people also say ピストル. 銃 is the general term for firearms, and 鉄砲 sounds old-fashioned. Typical combinations: 拳銃を持つ, 拳銃を撃つ, 拳銃を向ける; it is counted with 丁. The word occurs mainly in crime-story and news contexts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"思い遣りのつもりだったのかもしれないけど、嘘なんてついてほしくなかった。","romaji":"Omoiyari no tsumori datta no kamo shirenai kedo, uso nante tsuite hoshiku nakatta.","german":"Maybe you meant it kindly, but I didn't want you to lie.","furigana":"おも|や|うそ"},{"japanese":"彼女は思いやりのある優しい人だ。","romaji":"Kanojo wa omoiyari no aru yasashii hito da.","german":"She is a kind, considerate person.","furigana":"かのじょ|おも|やさ|ひと"},{"japanese":"お年寄りに席を譲るのは、ちょっとした思いやりです。","romaji":"Otoshiyori ni seki o yuzuru no wa, chotto shita omoiyari desu.","german":"Offering your seat to elderly people is a small act of consideration.","furigana":"としよ|せき|ゆず|おも"}],"notes":"思い遣り (usually written 思いやり) is the ability to put oneself in others' place and to act considerately. Typical: 思いやりがある/ない, 思いやりのある人, 思いやりに欠ける. It comes from the verb 思いやる \"to think of someone, to sympathize\". Unlike 同情 \"pity\", it stresses not regret but kind consideration in action.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"原爆のことを言ってるんじゃないよ。","romaji":"Genbaku no koto o i teru n ja nai yo.","german":"I'm not talking about atomic bombs."},{"japanese":"1945年8月6日、広島に原爆が落とされた。","romaji":"Sen kyuuhyaku yonjuugo-nen hachigatsu muika, Hiroshima ni genbaku ga otosareta.","german":"On August 6, 1945, an atomic bomb was dropped on Hiroshima.","furigana":"ねん|がつ|か|ひろしま|げんばく|お"},{"japanese":"修学旅行で、長崎の原爆資料館を見学した。","romaji":"Shuugaku ryokou de, Nagasaki no genbaku shiryoukan o kengaku shita.","german":"On the school trip we visited the Atomic Bomb Museum in Nagasaki.","furigana":"しゅうがくりょこう|ながさき|げんばくしりょうかん|けんがく"}],"notes":"原爆 is the short form of 原子爆弾 \"atomic bomb\" and in Japan is almost always connected with Hiroshima and Nagasaki: 原爆が投下される, 原爆ドーム, 原爆資料館. Survivors are called 被爆者. For nuclear weapons in general one says 核兵器; 原爆 refers to the concrete bomb or the historical bombings.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私はよく渋谷駅で外国人を見ます。","romaji":"Watashi wa yoku Shibuyaeki de gaikokujin o mimasu.","german":"I often see foreigners at Shibuya Station.","furigana":"わたし|しぶやえき|がいこくじん|み"},{"japanese":"渋谷のスクランブル交差点は、いつも人でいっぱいだ。","romaji":"Shibuya no sukuranburu kousaten wa, itsumo hito de ippai da.","german":"The scramble crossing in Shibuya is always full of people.","furigana":"しぶや|こうさてん|ひと"},{"japanese":"明日は渋谷のハチ公前で友達と待ち合わせをしている。","romaji":"Ashita wa Shibuya no Hachikou mae de tomodachi to machiawase o shite iru.","german":"Tomorrow I'm meeting a friend in front of the Hachiko statue in Shibuya.","furigana":"あした|しぶや|こうまえ|ともだち|ま|あ"}],"notes":"渋谷 is a ward (渋谷区) and a large business and entertainment district in Tokyo, known for youth culture, fashion and the スクランブル交差点 crossing. 渋谷駅 is one of the busiest stations; the statue ハチ公 in front of it is a classic meeting place (ハチ公前で待ち合わせ). As usual with places: 渋谷で遊ぶ, 渋谷に行く.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"充電するのを忘れたから携帯の電源が切れて、目覚ましが鳴らなかったんだ。","romaji":"Jūden suru no o wasureta kara keitai no dengen ga kirete, mezamashi ga naranakatta n da.","german":"I forgot to charge my phone, so it ran out of battery and the alarm didn't ring.","furigana":"じゅうでん|わす|けいたい|でんげん|き|めざ|な"},{"japanese":"このカフェでは、無料でスマホを充電できます。","romaji":"Kono kafe de wa, muryou de sumaho o juuden dekimasu.","german":"At this cafe you can charge your smartphone for free.","furigana":"むりょう|じゅうでん"},{"japanese":"週末は温泉でゆっくり充電してくるつもりだ。","romaji":"Shuumatsu wa onsen de yukkuri juuden shite kuru tsumori da.","german":"This weekend I plan to recharge my batteries at a hot spring.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|おんせん|じゅうでん"}],"notes":"充電する means \"to charge\" (a battery, a device) and is transitive: スマホを充電する; also, neutrally and intransitively, 充電が切れる \"the battery is dead\", 充電が終わる. Related are 充電器 \"charger\" and 充電中. Figuratively it means \"to recharge one's batteries\" (温泉で充電する, 充電期間). The technical opposite is 放電 \"discharge\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どうしてあの甲斐性なしが彼女の心を取り戻せたのか、全く理解できないね。","romaji":"Doushite ano kaishounashi ga kanojo no kokoro o torimodoseta no ka, mattaku rikai dekinai ne.","german":"I really can't understand how that good-for-nothing managed to win her heart back.","furigana":"かいしょう|かのじょ|こころ|と|もど|まった|りかい"},{"japanese":"今の僕には、家族を養うだけの甲斐性がない。","romaji":"Ima no boku ni wa, kazoku o yashinau dake no kaishou ga nai.","german":"Right now, I don't have what it takes to support a family.","furigana":"いま|ぼく|かぞく|やしな|かいしょう"},{"japanese":"母は、甲斐性のある人と結婚しなさいとよく言っていた。","romaji":"Haha wa, kaishou no aru hito to kekkon shinasai to yoku itte ita.","german":"My mother often told me to marry someone who is able to provide for a family.","furigana":"はは|かいしょう|ひと|けっこん|い"}],"notes":"甲斐性 is the ability to provide energetically for oneself and others, especially to earn enough money to live on. It appears almost only in 甲斐性がある/ない and in the derogatory 甲斐性なし \"good-for-nothing, loser\" - classically about husbands who cannot support their family. The word sounds somewhat old-fashioned and colloquial; do not confuse it with やりがい or 生きがい, which also contain 甲斐.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"手の甲がとてもきれいなことが彼女の自慢です。","romaji":"Te no kou ga totemo kirei na koto ga kanojo no jiman desu.","german":"She is proud of how beautiful the backs of her hands are.","furigana":"て|こう|かのじょ|じまん"},{"japanese":"蚊に手の甲を刺されて、かゆくてたまらない。","romaji":"Ka ni te no kou o sasarete, kayukute tamaranai.","german":"A mosquito bit me on the back of my hand, and it itches terribly.","furigana":"か|て|こう|さ"},{"japanese":"彼は手の甲で額の汗をぬぐった。","romaji":"Kare wa te no kou de hitai no ase o nugutta.","german":"He wiped the sweat from his forehead with the back of his hand.","furigana":"かれ|て|こう|ひたい|あせ"}],"notes":"手の甲 is the back of the hand; 甲 here refers to the curved upper side. The counterpart is 手のひら \"palm\"; analogously, the top of the foot is 足の甲. Typical contexts: 手の甲を刺される, 手の甲で汗をぬぐう, 手の甲にキスをする. One does not say 手の甲を持っている but describes it directly (手の甲がきれい).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この仕事は、誰かのために役に立つことができるので、とてもやり甲斐があります。","romaji":"Kono shigoto wa, dare ka no tame ni yakunitatsu koto ga dekiru node, totemo yarigai ga arimasu.","german":"This job is very rewarding because I can be useful to someone.","furigana":"しごと|だれ|やく|た|がい"},{"japanese":"給料は高くないが、毎日やりがいを感じている。","romaji":"Kyuuryou wa takakunai ga, mainichi yarigai o kanjite iru.","german":"The salary isn't high, but I find my work meaningful every day.","furigana":"きゅうりょう|たか|まいにち|かん"},{"japanese":"転職するなら、もっとやりがいのある仕事がしたい。","romaji":"Tenshoku suru nara, motto yarigai no aru shigoto ga shitai.","german":"If I change jobs, I want to do more fulfilling work.","furigana":"てんしょく|しごと"}],"notes":"やり甲斐 (usually written やりがい) is the feeling that an activity is worthwhile and makes sense. Fixed patterns: やりがいがある, やりがいを感じる, やりがいのある仕事. It is formed from the stem of the verb やる and 〜甲斐 \"worth, reward\", as in 生きがい \"purpose in life\" or 教えがい \"it is worth teaching\". Especially common in the work context and in job interviews.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は小躍りして喜びました。","romaji":"Watashi wa koodori shite yorokobimashita.","german":"I jumped for joy.","furigana":"わたし|こおど|よろこ"},{"japanese":"無名の新人選手が、一気にトップに躍り出た。","romaji":"Mumei no shinjin senshu ga, ikki ni toppu ni odorideta.","german":"An unknown rookie suddenly shot up to the top.","furigana":"むめい|しんじんせんしゅ|いっき|おど|で"},{"japanese":"合格の知らせを聞いて、弟は躍り上がって喜んだ。","romaji":"Goukaku no shirase o kiite, otouto wa odoriagatte yorokonda.","german":"When my younger brother heard that he had passed, he jumped up with joy.","furigana":"ごうかく|し|き|おとうと|おど|あ|よろこ"}],"notes":"躍り means \"leap, hop\" and hardly occurs on its own; it is encountered mainly in compounds such as 小躍り \"jump for joy\" (小躍りして喜ぶ), 躍り出る \"to leap forward, to burst into the lead\" and 躍り上がる \"to jump up\". Do not confuse it with the identically read 踊り \"dance\". Related is the verb 躍る, for instance in 胸が躍る \"one's heart leaps with excitement\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日の会議では、聞き役に徹するつもりです。","romaji":"Kyou no kaigi de wa, kikiyaku ni tessuru tsumori desu.","german":"In today's meeting I want to confine myself entirely to listening.","furigana":"きょう|かいぎ|き|やく|てっ"},{"japanese":"救助隊は夜を徹して、行方不明者を捜し続けた。","romaji":"Kyuujotai wa yoru o tesshite, yukue fumeisha o sagashitsuzuketa.","german":"The rescue team searched for the missing all night long.","furigana":"きゅうじょたい|よる|てっ|ゆくえふめいしゃ|さが|つづ"},{"japanese":"彼は選手をやめた後、裏方に徹してチームを支えた。","romaji":"Kare wa senshu o yameta ato, uragata ni tesshite chiimu o sasaeta.","german":"After his active career, he supported the team exclusively behind the scenes.","furigana":"かれ|せんしゅ|あと|うらがた|てっ|ささ"}],"notes":"徹する means to confine oneself fully and consistently to one role or attitude: 聞き役に徹する \"to only listen\", 裏方に徹する \"to work entirely in the background\", 守りに徹する. With time expressions, 夜を徹して means \"all night long\". The verb is intransitive and takes に; in contrast to 専念する, it emphasizes deliberately doing nothing else. Register: elevated, written and in speeches.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"英語を徹夜で勉強するなんて、いかにもあの子らしいよね。","romaji":"Eigo o tetsuya de benkyō suru nante, ikanimo ano ko rashii yo ne.","german":"It's just like her to study English all night long.","furigana":"えいご|てつや|べんきょう|こ"},{"japanese":"昨日は徹夜でレポートを書いたので、眠くてたまらない。","romaji":"Kinou wa tetsuya de repooto o kaita node, nemukute tamaranai.","german":"I wrote my report all night yesterday, so I'm dying to sleep.","furigana":"きのう|てつや|か|ねむ"},{"japanese":"徹夜するより、早く寝て朝に勉強したほうが効率がいい。","romaji":"Tetsuya suru yori, hayaku nete asa ni benkyou shita hou ga kouritsu ga ii.","german":"Rather than pulling an all-nighter, it is more efficient to go to bed early and study in the morning.","furigana":"てつや|はや|ね|あさ|べんきょう|こうりつ"}],"notes":"徹夜 means not sleeping all night, e.g. in order to study or work: 徹夜する, 徹夜で勉強する, 徹夜明け \"after a night without sleep\". A night shift at work, by contrast, is 夜勤, and simply staying up late is 夜更かし. 徹夜 is a noun or a する-verb, not an adverb; the adverbial \"all night long\" only arises with で.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"事故の原因について、徹底した調査が行われた。","romaji":"Jiko no gen'in ni tsuite, tettei shita chousa ga okonawareta.","german":"A thorough investigation was carried out into the cause of the accident.","furigana":"じこ|げんいん|てってい|ちょうさ|おこな"},{"japanese":"会社は全社員に、個人情報の管理を徹底するよう指示した。","romaji":"Kaisha wa zenshain ni, kojin jouhou no kanri o tettei suru you shiji shita.","german":"The company instructed all employees to strictly enforce the handling of personal information.","furigana":"かいしゃ|ぜんしゃいん|こじんじょうほう|かんり|てってい|しじ"},{"japanese":"年末に、部屋を徹底的に掃除した。","romaji":"Nenmatsu ni, heya o tetteiteki ni souji shita.","german":"At the end of the year, I cleaned my room thoroughly.","furigana":"ねんまつ|へや|てっていてき|そうじ"}],"notes":"徹底 means thoroughness and complete, gap-free enforcement. As a する-verb it is both transitive (ルールを徹底する \"to enforce a rule consistently\") and intransitive (指示が徹底していない \"instructions are not being consistently followed\"). Very common are 徹底した + noun (徹底した調査), the adjective 徹底的な/徹底的に \"thorough, through and through\", and 周知徹底 \"making sure everyone knows\". Register: neutral to formal, typical of business and official language.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この川はこの辺りは高瀬になってるから、きっと足が底に着くと思うよ。","romaji":"Kono kawa wa kono atari wa takase ni natteru kara, kitto ashi ga soko ni tsuku to omou yo.","german":"The river is shallow around here, so I'm sure your feet will reach the bottom.","furigana":"かわ|あた|たかせ|あし|そこ|つ|おも"},{"japanese":"高瀬舟は、浅い川でも荷物を運べるように底が平らに作られていた。","romaji":"Takasebune wa, asai kawa demo nimotsu o hakoberu you ni soko ga taira ni tsukurarete ita.","german":"Takase boats were built with flat bottoms so that they could carry cargo even on shallow rivers.","furigana":"たかせぶね|あさ|かわ|にもつ|はこ|そこ|たい|つく"},{"japanese":"森鴎外の『高瀬舟』は、国語の教科書によく載っている。","romaji":"Mori Ougai no \"Takasebune\" wa, kokugo no kyoukasho ni yoku notte iru.","german":"Mori Ogai's \"Takasebune\" often appears in Japanese school textbooks.","furigana":"|たかせぶね|こくご|きょうかしょ|の"}],"notes":"高瀬 refers to a flat, shallow stretch of a river; today the word is rare and is encountered mainly in 高瀬舟, a flat river boat for freight, and in place names such as the 高瀬川 in Kyoto. It is well known through Mori Ogai's story \"高瀬舟\". For \"shallow spot\", everyday speech uses 浅瀬 or simply 浅い所.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"瓦礫の撤去に関するニュースをネットで読みました。","romaji":"Gareki no tekkyo ni kansuru nyuusu o netto de yomimashita.","german":"I read the news about the clearing of rubble on the internet.","furigana":"がれき|てっきょ|かん|よ"},{"japanese":"駅前に放置された自転車は、市によって撤去されます。","romaji":"Ekimae ni houchi sareta jitensha wa, shi ni yotte tekkyo saremasu.","german":"Bicycles left abandoned in front of the station are removed by the city.","furigana":"えきまえ|ほうち|じてんしゃ|し|てっきょ"},{"japanese":"古くなった看板を撤去する工事が始まった。","romaji":"Furuku natta kanban o tekkyo suru kouji ga hajimatta.","german":"Work to take down the old signboard has begun.","furigana":"ふる|かんばん|てっきょ|こうじ|はじ"}],"notes":"撤去 means the removal or dismantling of objects and installations: 看板を撤去する, 放置自転車の撤去, 瓦礫の撤去. It is transitive and often appears in the passive (撤去される). Do not confuse it with 撤回 \"withdrawal (of a statement)\" and 撤退 \"retreat\"; 除去 refers more to the removal of substances (汚れの除去). Register: formal, the language of authorities and news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ごめん。前言撤回するよ。","romaji":"Gomen. Zengen tekkai suru yo.","german":"Sorry. I take back what I said.","furigana":"ぜんげんてっかい"},{"japanese":"大臣は問題となった発言を撤回し、謝罪した。","romaji":"Daijin wa mondai to natta hatsugen o tekkai shi, shazai shita.","german":"The minister retracted the controversial remark and apologized.","furigana":"だいじん|もんだい|はつげん|てっかい|しゃざい"},{"japanese":"客からの反発を受けて、会社は値上げを撤回した。","romaji":"Kyaku kara no hanpatsu o ukete, kaisha wa neage o tekkai shita.","german":"In response to a backlash from customers, the company withdrew the price increase.","furigana":"きゃく|はんぱつ|う|かいしゃ|ねあ|てっかい"}],"notes":"撤回 is the withdrawal of a statement, decision or demand: 発言を撤回する, 決定を撤回する, 撤回を求める. It is transitive and sounds formal; in everyday speech people also say 取り消す. The set phrase 前言撤回 \"I take back what I said\" is also used jokingly in conversation. Do not confuse it with 撤去 \"removal of objects\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私はその撤兵を恥じた事は一度もありません。","romaji":"Watashi wa sono teppei o hajita koto wa ichido mo arimasen.","german":"I have never been ashamed of that troop withdrawal.","furigana":"わたし|てっぺい|は|こと|いちど"},{"japanese":"停戦の合意により、両国は国境地帯から撤兵した。","romaji":"Teisen no goui ni yori, ryoukoku wa kokkyou chitai kara teppei shita.","german":"Following the ceasefire agreement, both countries withdrew their troops from the border area.","furigana":"ていせん|ごうい|りょうこく|こっきょうちたい|てっぺい"},{"japanese":"政府は、来年までに全部隊を撤兵させる方針を発表した。","romaji":"Seifu wa, rainen made ni zenbutai o teppei saseru houshin o happyou shita.","german":"The government announced a policy of withdrawing all units by next year.","furigana":"せいふ|らいねん|ぜんぶたい|てっぺい|ほうしん|はっぴょう"}],"notes":"撤兵 is the withdrawal of troops from a region: 〜から撤兵する. It is intransitive; the opposite is 派兵 \"to dispatch troops\" or 出兵. 撤退 is more general and refers to any retreat, including in business (市場から撤退する). 撤兵 belongs to the political-military language of news and history.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"君の詩の措辞が素晴らしいことに感動しました。","romaji":"Kimi no shi no soji ga subarashii koto ni kandō shimashita.","german":"I was moved by how wonderful the wording of your poem is.","furigana":"きみ|し|そじ|すば|かんどう"},{"japanese":"俳句では、一語ごとの措辞が作品の印象を大きく左右する。","romaji":"Haiku de wa, ichigo goto no soji ga sakuhin no inshou o ookiku sayuu suru.","german":"In haiku, the choice of each individual word greatly affects the impression of the work.","furigana":"はいく|いちご|そじ|さくひん|いんしょう|おお|さゆう"},{"japanese":"先生は、私の作文の措辞を一つ一つ丁寧に直してくれた。","romaji":"Sensei wa, watashi no sakubun no soji o hitotsu hitotsu teinei ni naoshite kureta.","german":"My teacher carefully corrected the wording of my composition one item at a time.","furigana":"せんせい|わたし|さくぶん|そじ|ひと|ひと|ていねい|なお"}],"notes":"措辞 is an elevated, literary word for word choice and phrasing in a text, especially in poems, haiku and essays. One speaks of 措辞が巧みだ or 措辞が適切だ. In everyday speech people say 言葉遣い, 言い回し or 表現 instead; 措辞 appears almost only in literary criticism and haiku reviews.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"皆様のお力に拠って、成功することができました。感謝の気持ちをこめて、ベーコンをプレゼントさせて頂きます。","romaji":"Minasama no ochikara ni yotte, seikō suru koto ga dekimashita. Kansha no kimochi o komete, bēkon o purezento sasete itadakimasu.","german":"Thanks to everyone's support, I was able to succeed. As a token of my gratitude, I would like to give you some bacon.","furigana":"みなさま|ちから|よ|せいこう|かんしゃ|きも|いただ"},{"japanese":"道路の通行止めは、昨夜の大雨に因るものです。","romaji":"Dōro no tsūkōdome wa, sakuya no ōame ni yoru mono desu.","german":"The road closure is caused by last night's heavy rain.","furigana":"どうろ|つうこうど|さくや|おおあめ|よ"},{"japanese":"この考えは、昔の経験に因るものです。","romaji":"Kono kangae wa, mukashi no keiken ni yoru mono desu.","german":"This idea stems from past experience.","furigana":"かんが|むかし|けいけん|よ"}],"notes":"拠る is read よる here and means to be based on something or to be caused by something. The basis or cause stands before に; によって often names it in a full sentence. よる is usually written in hiragana. For an origin or cause, the rarer spelling 因る is also found. 拠って is the て-form of the godan verb; the identically read words 寄る and 夜 have other meanings.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"我々は生産拠点を海外に移します。","romaji":"Wareware wa seisan kyoten o kaigai ni utsushimasu.","german":"We will move our production base overseas.","furigana":"われわれ|せいさんきょてん|かいがい|うつ"},{"japanese":"そのバンドは大阪を拠点に活動している。","romaji":"Sono bando wa Oosaka o kyoten ni katsudou shite iru.","german":"That band is based in Osaka and performs from there.","furigana":"おおさか|きょてん|かつどう"},{"japanese":"京都のホテルを拠点にして、周辺の観光地を回った。","romaji":"Kyouto no hoteru o kyoten ni shite, shuuhen no kankouchi o mawatta.","german":"Using a hotel in Kyoto as our base, we toured the sightseeing spots in the area.","furigana":"きょうと|きょてん|しゅうへん|かんこうち|まわ"}],"notes":"拠点 is a base or location from which one is active: 〜を拠点に(して)活動する, 拠点を置く, 拠点を移す. Common in business (生産拠点, 営業拠点) and the military (戦略的拠点). 本拠地 stresses the headquarters, e.g. of a sports club, and 基地 is above all a military or technical base.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本人はどうしてペコペコお辞儀ばっかりするんですか？","romaji":"Nihonjin wa doushite pekopeko ojigi bakkari suru n desu ka?","german":"Why do Japanese people keep bowing all the time?","furigana":"にほんじん|じぎ"},{"japanese":"先生に会ったら、きちんとお辞儀をしなさい。","romaji":"Sensei ni attara, kichinto ojigi o shinasai.","german":"When you meet your teacher, bow properly.","furigana":"せんせい|あ|じぎ"},{"japanese":"面接では、部屋に入る前に軽くお辞儀をします。","romaji":"Mensetsu de wa, heya ni hairu mae ni karuku ojigi o shimasu.","german":"In a job interview, you bow lightly before entering the room.","furigana":"めんせつ|へや|はい|まえ|かる|じぎ"}],"notes":"What is meant is the bow, which is practically always used with the polite prefix as お辞儀 (お辞儀をする); 辞儀 without お is obsolete. Depending on depth, one distinguishes 会釈 (a slight nod), 敬礼 and 最敬礼 (a deep bow). Typical are 深くお辞儀をする, 軽くお辞儀をする and the onomatopoeic ペコペコお辞儀する \"to keep bowing subserviently\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"果樹園での仕事は結構退屈です。","romaji":"Kajuen de no shigoto wa kekkō taikutsu desu.","german":"Working in an orchard is rather boring.","furigana":"かじゅえん|しごと|けっこうたいくつ"},{"japanese":"庭に、りんごやみかんなどの果樹を植えた。","romaji":"Niwa ni, ringo ya mikan nado no kaju o ueta.","german":"We planted fruit trees such as apples and mandarins in the garden.","furigana":"にわ|かじゅ|う"},{"japanese":"この地方は気候が温暖で、果樹の栽培が盛んだ。","romaji":"Kono chihou wa kikou ga ondan de, kaju no saibai ga sakan da.","german":"This region has a mild climate, and fruit growing is thriving.","furigana":"ちほう|きこう|おんだん|かじゅ|さいばい|さか"}],"notes":"果樹 is a fruit tree; the word is rather technical or written and appears mainly in 果樹園 \"orchard, fruit plantation\" and 果樹栽培 \"fruit growing\". For the fruit itself, people say 果物 or, technically, 果実. In everyday speech people often say something concrete like りんごの木 or みかんの木 instead of 果樹.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"結婚の記念に、主人と私は庭にサトウカエデを植樹しました。","romaji":"Kekkon no kinen ni, shujin to watashi wa niwa ni satoukaede o shokuju shimashita.","german":"To commemorate our wedding, my husband and I planted a sugar maple in the garden.","furigana":"けっこん|きねん|しゅじん|わたし|にわ|しょくじゅ"},{"japanese":"毎年春になると、地域のボランティアが山で植樹をしている。","romaji":"Maitoshi haru ni naru to, chiiki no borantia ga yama de shokuju o shite iru.","german":"Every spring, local volunteers plant trees in the mountains.","furigana":"まいとしはる|ちいき|やま|しょくじゅ"},{"japanese":"卒業の記念として、校庭に桜が植樹された。","romaji":"Sotsugyou no kinen toshite, koutei ni sakura ga shokuju sareta.","german":"To commemorate graduation, a cherry tree was planted in the schoolyard.","furigana":"そつぎょう|きねん|こうてい|さくら|しょくじゅ"}],"notes":"植樹 is the (usually ceremonial or organized) planting of trees, for example as 記念植樹 or at a 植樹祭 and reforestation campaigns. As a する-verb it takes the tree species as its object (桜を植樹する); 木を植樹する is redundant, since 樹 already means \"tree\". In everyday speech people simply say 木を植える; 植樹 sounds more formal.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は親に育児放棄され、施設で育ちました。","romaji":"Watashi wa oya ni ikuji houki sare, shisetsu de sodachimashita.","german":"My parents neglected me, and I grew up in a children's home.","furigana":"わたし|おや|いくじほうき|しせつ|そだ"},{"japanese":"借金が多かったので、兄は相続を放棄した。","romaji":"Shakkin ga ookatta node, ani wa souzoku o houki shita.","german":"Because the debts were so high, my brother renounced the inheritance.","furigana":"しゃっきん|おお|あに|そうぞく|ほうき"},{"japanese":"日本国憲法第九条は、戦争の放棄を定めている。","romaji":"Nihonkoku kenpou daikyuujou wa, sensou no houki o sadamete iru.","german":"Article 9 of the Japanese Constitution stipulates the renunciation of war.","furigana":"にほんこくけんぽうだいきゅうじょう|せんそう|ほうき|さだ"}],"notes":"放棄 means to consciously give up or waive a right, duty or thing: 権利を放棄する, 相続を放棄する, 試合を放棄する, 責任放棄. Well known is 戦争の放棄 in Article 9 of the Japanese Constitution, as well as 育児放棄 \"child neglect\". It is transitive and formal; 諦める rather expresses the inner giving up of a wish, 捨てる the throwing away.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"恐い虎の顔が描かれたお揃いのTシャツを着たカップルが、今目の前を腕を組みながら歩いています。","romaji":"Kowai tora no kao ga egakareta osoroi no T-shatsu o kita kappuru ga, ima me no mae o ude o kuminagara aruite imasu.","german":"A couple in matching T-shirts with a fierce tiger face printed on them is walking arm in arm right in front of me.","furigana":"こわ|とら|かお|えが|そろ|き|いまめ|まえ|うで|く|ある"},{"japanese":"動物園で虎の赤ちゃんが生まれた。","romaji":"Dōbutsuen de tora no akachan ga umareta.","german":"A tiger cub was born at the zoo.","furigana":"どうぶつえん|とら|あか|う"},{"japanese":"父はお酒を飲みすぎると、虎になって大声で歌い出す。","romaji":"Chichi wa osake o nomisugiru to, tora ni natte ōgoe de utaidasu.","german":"When my father drinks too much, he gets drunk and starts singing at the top of his voice.","furigana":"ちち|さけ|の|とら|おおごえ|うた|だ"}],"notes":"虎 is the tiger; in zoos and biological texts it is often written in katakana トラ. The kanji appears in sayings such as 虎の威を借る狐 \"the fox who borrows the tiger's might\" (to throw one's weight around with borrowed authority). Colloquially, 虎 also means \"a drunk\": 虎になる \"to get uninhibitedly drunk\". Do not confuse it with 寅, the tiger of the zodiac (寅年).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は私の蜂蜜プリンを一口味見しました。","romaji":"Kanojo wa watashi no hachimitsu purin o hitokuchi ajimi shimashita.","german":"She tasted a bite of my honey pudding.","furigana":"かのじょ|わたし|はちみつ|ひとくちあじみ"},{"japanese":"のどが痛いときは、紅茶に蜂蜜を入れて飲みます。","romaji":"Nodo ga itai toki wa, koucha ni hachimitsu o irete nomimasu.","german":"When I have a sore throat, I drink black tea with honey.","furigana":"いた|こうちゃ|はちみつ|い|の"},{"japanese":"一歳未満の赤ちゃんに蜂蜜を与えてはいけません。","romaji":"Issai miman no akachan ni hachimitsu o ataete wa ikemasen.","german":"You must not give honey to babies under one year old.","furigana":"いっさいみまん|あか|はちみつ|あた"}],"notes":"蜂蜜 is honey, composed of 蜂 \"bee\" and 蜜 \"nectar, honey\". It is mostly written in kana as はちみつ or ハチミツ; the kanji are seen more on labels of traditional products. Typical are 蜂蜜を塗る (on bread), 蜂蜜を入れる (in tea) and compounds like 蜂蜜レモン.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"港には、海上自衛隊の艦船が何隻も並んでいた。","romaji":"Minato ni wa, kaijou jieitai no kansen ga nanseki mo narande ita.","german":"Several Maritime Self-Defense Force ships were lined up in the harbor.","furigana":"みなと|かいじょうじえいたい|かんせん|なんせき|なら"},{"japanese":"記念日に、艦船の一般公開が行われた。","romaji":"Kinenbi ni, kansen no ippan koukai ga okonawareta.","german":"On the anniversary, the naval ships were opened to the public.","furigana":"きねんび|かんせん|いっぱんこうかい|おこな"},{"japanese":"今回の訓練には、各国から二十隻以上の艦船が参加した。","romaji":"Konkai no kunren ni wa, kakkoku kara nijusseki ijou no kansen ga sanka shita.","german":"More than twenty naval ships from various countries took part in this exercise.","furigana":"こんかい|くんれん|かっこく|にじゅっせきいじょう|かんせん|さんか"}],"notes":"艦船 is a collective term for warships and other ships of a navy; it is mostly used in a plural sense, as in 艦船が停泊する or 十隻の艦船. It is counted with 隻. A single warship is more often called 軍艦 or 艦, and a formation 艦隊. The word belongs to news and military language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"敵の艦隊からもっと激しい抵抗があると思っていたので、なんだか拍子抜けです。","romaji":"Teki no kantai kara motto hageshii teikō ga aru to omotte ita node, nandaka hyōshinuke desu.","german":"I had expected stronger resistance from the enemy fleet, so I feel somewhat let down.","furigana":"てき|かんたい|はげ|ていこう|おも|ひょうしぬ"},{"japanese":"アメリカ第七艦隊の拠点は横須賀にある。","romaji":"Amerika dainana kantai no kyoten wa Yokosuka ni aru.","german":"The base of the US Seventh Fleet is in Yokosuka.","furigana":"だいななかんたい|きょてん|"},{"japanese":"その提督は、大艦隊を率いて出航した。","romaji":"Sono teitoku wa, daikantai o hiite shukkou shita.","german":"The admiral set sail leading a large fleet.","furigana":"ていとく|だいかんたい||しゅっこう"}],"notes":"艦隊 is a fleet of warships, that is, a formation under common command: 艦隊を率いる, 太平洋艦隊, 連合艦隊. For civilian groups of ships, such as convoys or fishing fleets, people say 船団 instead. The word appears in history, news and also in science fiction (宇宙艦隊).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"軍艦プラモの専門店を開く予定なんだよ。","romaji":"Gunkan puramo no senmonten o hiraku yotei na n da yo.","german":"I plan to open a specialty shop for warship model kits.","furigana":"ぐんかん|せんもんてん|ひら|よてい"},{"japanese":"外国の軍艦が港に入ってきて、町は大騒ぎになった。","romaji":"Gaikoku no gunkan ga minato ni haitte kite, machi wa oosawagi ni natta.","german":"When a foreign warship entered the port, there was great excitement in the town.","furigana":"がいこく|ぐんかん|みなと|はい|まち|おおさわ"},{"japanese":"いくらの軍艦巻きは、形が軍艦に似ているからそう呼ばれる。","romaji":"Ikura no gunkanmaki wa, katachi ga gunkan ni nite iru kara sou yobareru.","german":"Gunkan-maki with salmon roe are so called because their shape resembles a warship.","furigana":"ぐんかんま|かたち|ぐんかん|に|よ"}],"notes":"軍艦 is the general word for a warship. A battleship in the narrower sense is called 戦艦, an aircraft carrier 空母; several warships together are 艦船 or, as a formation, 艦隊. The word is also known from sushi cuisine: 軍艦巻き are rice morsels wrapped in nori whose shape is reminiscent of a warship.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"多分あの潜水艦の中で片方のイヤリングを失くしてしまったの。","romaji":"Tabun ano sensuikan no naka de katahō no iyaringu o nakushite shimatta no.","german":"I probably lost my other earring in that submarine.","furigana":"たぶん|せんすいかん|なか|かたほう|"},{"japanese":"潜水艦は、長い間海の中に潜っていられる。","romaji":"Sensuikan wa, nagai aida umi no naka ni mogutte irareru.","german":"A submarine can stay submerged in the sea for a long time.","furigana":"せんすいかん|なが|あいだうみ|なか|もぐ"},{"japanese":"子どもの頃、潜水艦のプラモデルを作ったことがある。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, sensuikan no puramoderu o tsukutta koto ga aru.","german":"When I was a child, I once built a submarine model kit.","furigana":"こ|ころ|せんすいかん|つく"}],"notes":"潜水艦 is the submarine (潜水 \"diving\" + 艦 \"warship\"). The character 艦 marks military ships, cf. 軍艦, 戦艦, 艦隊. Smaller civilian diving vessels for research or tourism are more often called 潜水艇 or 潜水調査船. Matching verbs: 潜る or 潜航する \"to dive\" and 浮上する \"to surface\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その航空母艦は突然視界から消えたんです。","romaji":"Sono kōkū bokan wa totsuzen shikai kara kieta n desu.","german":"That aircraft carrier suddenly disappeared from view.","furigana":"こうくうぼかん|とつぜんしかい|き"},{"japanese":"航空母艦は、略して「空母」と呼ばれることが多い。","romaji":"Koukuu bokan wa, ryakushite \"kuubo\" to yobareru koto ga ooi.","german":"Aircraft carriers are often called \"kuubo\" for short.","furigana":"こうくうぼかん|りゃく|くうぼ|よ|おお"},{"japanese":"戦闘機が次々と航空母艦の甲板から飛び立った。","romaji":"Sentouki ga tsugitsugi to koukuu bokan no kanpan kara tobitatta.","german":"Fighter planes took off one after another from the deck of the aircraft carrier.","furigana":"せんとうき|つぎつぎ|こうくうぼかん||と|た"}],"notes":"航空母艦 is the full, formal name for an aircraft carrier; in the news and in conversation the short form 空母 is almost always used. Typical combinations are 空母の甲板 \"flight deck\", 原子力空母 and 空母打撃群. The word is composed of 航空 \"aviation\" and 母艦 \"mother ship\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"原子力潜水艦は、略して原潜と呼ばれることもある。","romaji":"Genshiryoku sensuikan wa, ryakushite gensen to yobareru koto mo aru.","german":"Nuclear-powered submarines are sometimes called \"gensen\" for short.","furigana":"げんしりょくせんすいかん|りゃく|げんせん|よ"},{"japanese":"アメリカの原潜が寄港するたびに、反対する市民が集まる。","romaji":"Amerika no gensen ga kikou suru tabi ni, hantai suru shimin ga atsumaru.","german":"Every time an American nuclear submarine makes a port call, protesting citizens gather.","furigana":"げんせん|きこう|はんたい|しみん|あつ"},{"japanese":"原潜は燃料を補給しなくても、長い間海に潜ったまま活動できる。","romaji":"Gensen wa nenryou o hokyuu shinakute mo, nagai aida umi ni mogutta mama katsudou dekiru.","german":"A nuclear submarine can stay submerged and operate for a long time without refueling.","furigana":"げんせん|ねんりょう|ほきゅう|なが|あいだうみ|もぐ|かつどう"}],"notes":"原潜 is the abbreviation of 原子力潜水艦 \"nuclear submarine\" and is used mainly in the news and in headlines (原潜が寄港する, 米原潜). Conventional submarines are simply called 潜水艦. Similar abbreviations with 原 are 原発 \"nuclear power plant\" and 原爆 \"atomic bomb\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"うちの妹は今年の夏に潜水士免許を取得したんだよ。","romaji":"Uchi no imōto wa kotoshi no natsu ni sensuishi menkyo o shutoku shita n da yo.","german":"My sister got her diving license this summer.","furigana":"いもうと|ことし|なつ|せんすいしめんきょ|しゅとく"},{"japanese":"彼は息を止めたまま、二分以上潜水できる。","romaji":"Kare wa iki o tometa mama, nifun ijou sensui dekiru.","german":"He can dive for over two minutes holding his breath.","furigana":"かれ|いき|と|にふんいじょうせんすい"},{"japanese":"海女さんは道具を使わずに潜水して、貝をとる。","romaji":"Ama-san wa dougu o tsukawazu ni sensui shite, kai o toru.","german":"The ama divers dive without equipment and gather shellfish.","furigana":"|どうぐ|つか|せんすい|かい"}],"notes":"潜水 is diving underwater, as an intransitive suru-verb 潜水する. It sounds more factual than the everyday verb 潜る and often appears in compounds: 潜水艦 \"submarine\", 潜水士 \"professional diver\", 潜水服 \"diving suit\". For recreational sport diving one mostly says ダイビング or スキューバダイビング.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"夢ってのは、僕たちの潜在意識を反映しているとよく言われるよね。","romaji":"Yume tte no wa, bokutachi no senzai ishiki o han'ei shite iru to yoku iwareru yo ne.","german":"It's often said that dreams reflect our subconscious, isn't it?","furigana":"ゆめ|ぼく|せんざいいしき|はんえい|い"},{"japanese":"子どものころの経験は、大人になっても潜在意識に残っている。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro no keiken wa, otona ni natte mo senzai ishiki ni nokotte iru.","german":"Experiences from childhood remain in the subconscious even in adulthood.","furigana":"こ|けいけん|おとな|せんざいいしき|のこ"},{"japanese":"この広告は、消費者の潜在意識に働きかけるように作られている。","romaji":"Kono koukoku wa, shouhisha no senzai ishiki ni hatarakikakeru you ni tsukurarete iru.","german":"This advertisement is designed to appeal to consumers' subconscious.","furigana":"こうこく|しょうひしゃ|せんざいいしき|はたら|つく"}],"notes":"潜在意識 is the subconscious, that is, the layer of consciousness that is not directly accessible to us: 潜在意識に働きかける, 潜在意識の中に残る. 潜在 means \"hidden, latent\", cf. 潜在能力 \"hidden abilities\" and 潜在的な. Largely synonymous but shaped by psychoanalysis is 無意識 \"the unconscious\". The word is a noun and belongs to the vocabulary of psychology and self-help.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"父は、僕に拳骨を食らわせるふりをしました。","romaji":"Chichi wa, boku ni genkotsu o kurawaseru furi o shimashita.","german":"My father pretended he was going to hit me with his fist.","furigana":"ちち|ぼく|げんこつ|く"},{"japanese":"いたずらをして、おじいちゃんにげんこつをもらった。","romaji":"Itazura o shite, ojiichan ni genkotsu o moratta.","german":"Because I had been mischievous, Grandpa gave me a knuckle rap."},{"japanese":"彼はげんこつを握りしめて、怒りをこらえていた。","romaji":"Kare wa genkotsu o nigirishimete, ikari o koraete ita.","german":"He clenched his fist and held back his anger.","furigana":"かれ|にぎ|いか"}],"notes":"拳骨 (usually written げんこつ) is the clenched fist, above all as a means of hitting. Typical are げんこつを食らわせる/落とす \"to give a knuckle rap\" and げんこつをもらう \"to get a knuckle rap\" - classically the punishment from a father or teacher. The neutral word for fist is 拳 (こぶし); げんこつ sounds colloquial and somewhat old-fashioned.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一酸化炭素と二酸化炭素の違いは何ですか？","romaji":"Issanka tanso to nisanka tanso no chigai wa nan desu ka?","german":"What is the difference between carbon monoxide and carbon dioxide?","furigana":"いっさんかたんそ|にさんかたんそ|ちが|なん"},{"japanese":"ダイヤモンドは、炭素だけでできている。","romaji":"Daiyamondo wa, tanso dake de dekite iru.","german":"Diamonds consist of nothing but carbon.","furigana":"たんそ"},{"japanese":"世界の多くの国が、脱炭素社会の実現を目指している。","romaji":"Sekai no ooku no kuni ga, datsutanso shakai no jitsugen o mezashite iru.","german":"Many countries in the world are aiming for a climate-neutral society.","furigana":"せかい|おお|くに|だつたんそしゃかい|じつげん|めざ"}],"notes":"炭素 is the chemical element carbon (C). It is most often encountered in compounds like 二酸化炭素 \"carbon dioxide\" and 一酸化炭素 \"carbon monoxide\" as well as in environmental terms like 脱炭素 \"decarbonization\" and 炭素税 \"carbon tax\". 炭 alone, by contrast, is charcoal. In daily life カーボン is also used, e.g. for materials.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今時田畑を機械を使わずに耕すなんて、時間の無駄だよ。","romaji":"Imadoki tahata o kikai o tsukawazu ni tagayasu nante, jikan no muda da yo.","german":"These days, plowing fields without machines is a waste of time.","furigana":"いまどきたはた|きかい|つか|たがや|じかん|むだ"},{"japanese":"大雨で川があふれ、田畑が水につかった。","romaji":"Ooame de kawa ga afure, tahata ga mizu ni tsukatta.","german":"Heavy rain made the river overflow, and the fields were flooded.","furigana":"おおあめ|かわ|たはた|みず"},{"japanese":"祖父は、先祖から受け継いだ田畑を大切に守っている。","romaji":"Sofu wa, senzo kara uketsuida tahata o taisetsu ni mamotte iru.","german":"My grandfather carefully looks after the fields inherited from his ancestors.","furigana":"そふ|せんぞ|う|つ|たはた|たいせつ|まも"}],"notes":"田畑 combines rice paddies (田) and dry fields (畑) and refers to the farmland of a farmer or of an area; the reading たはた is common, and でんぱた also occurs. Typical are 田畑を耕す, 田畑が荒れる and 田畑を手放す. The word sounds somewhat written; in conversation people say 田んぼ or 畑 instead.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"麦畑が見えてきたとたんに、くしゃみが出始めました。","romaji":"Mugibatake ga miete kita totan ni, kushami ga de hajimemashita.","german":"Sobald das Weizenfeld in Sicht kam, fing ich an zu niesen.","furigana":"むぎばたけ|み|ではじ"},{"japanese":"初夏になると、麦畑が一面黄金色に染まります。","romaji":"Shoka ni naru to, mugibatake ga ichimen kogane-iro ni somarimasu.","german":"Im Frühsommer färben sich die Getreidefelder überall goldgelb.","furigana":"しょか|むぎばたけ|いちめんこがねいろ|そ"},{"japanese":"北海道の広い麦畑の中を車で走りました。","romaji":"Hokkaidou no hiroi mugibatake no naka o kuruma de hashirimashita.","german":"Wir sind mit dem Auto durch die weiten Weizenfelder von Hokkaido gefahren.","furigana":"ほっかいどう|ひろ|むぎばたけ|なか|くるま|はし"}],"notes":"麦畑 ist ein Feld mit 麦, also Getreide wie 小麦 „Weizen“ oder 大麦 „Gerste“; im Deutschen passt je nach Kontext „Weizenfeld“, „Gerstenfeld“ oder allgemein „Getreidefeld“. 畑 wird im Kompositum zu ばたけ. Typische Wendungen sind 麦畑が広がる und 一面の麦畑. Ein 畑 ist immer ein Trockenfeld, im Gegensatz zum bewässerten Reisfeld 田んぼ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その建物はじきに警察に包囲されるでしょう。","romaji":"Sono tatemono wa jiki ni keisatsu ni houi sareru deshou.","german":"That building will soon be surrounded by the police.","furigana":"たてもの|けいさつ|ほうい"},{"japanese":"敵軍は城を包囲して、食料が尽きるのを待った。","romaji":"Tekigun wa shiro o houi shite, shokuryou ga tsukiru no o matta.","german":"The enemy army besieged the castle and waited for the food supplies to run out.","furigana":"てきぐん|しろ|ほうい|しょくりょう|つ|ま"},{"japanese":"犯人は警察の包囲網をくぐり抜けて逃げた。","romaji":"Hannin wa keisatsu no houimou o kugurinukete nigeta.","german":"The culprit slipped through the police cordon and escaped.","furigana":"はんにん|けいさつ|ほういもう|ぬ|に"}],"notes":"包囲する means \"to encircle, surround, besiege\" and is transitive: 〜を包囲する. The passive 〜に包囲される (\"to be surrounded by ...\") is very common, for example in police operations; historically it refers to the siege of a castle or city. 包囲網 is the \"dragnet\" or encirclement. In everyday speech people say 囲む or 取り囲む instead; 包囲 sounds like news and military language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"盗人には仁義はない。","romaji":"Nusubito ni wa jingi wa nai.","german":"There is no honor among thieves.","furigana":"ぬすびと|じんぎ"},{"japanese":"祖父は仁義を重んじる人で、受けた恩は必ず返した。","romaji":"Sofu wa jingi o omonjiru hito de, uketa on wa kanarazu kaeshita.","german":"My grandfather was a man who valued honor and decency, and he always repaid favors he had received.","furigana":"そふ|じんぎ|おも|ひと|う|おん|かなら|かえ"},{"japanese":"独立する前に、前の会社の社長に仁義を切っておいた。","romaji":"Dokuritsu suru mae ni, mae no kaisha no shachou ni jingi o kitte oita.","german":"Before going independent, I paid my respects to the president of my former company.","furigana":"どくりつ|まえ|まえ|かいしゃ|しゃちょう|じんぎ|き"}],"notes":"仁義 originally refers to the Confucian virtues 仁 \"humanity\" and 義 \"justice\"; today it mostly means the code of honor or the duty of decency among people, especially in the world of the yakuza. Typical are 仁義を重んじる (\"to value honor and decency\") and 仁義なき (\"without honor\"). 仁義を切る is originally the formal greeting among yakuza, and colloquially also \"to inform someone beforehand as a matter of courtesy\". The word sounds old-fashioned and somewhat rough.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鉱山で働いていた時は、いつも背中が痛かったです。","romaji":"Kouzan de hataraite ita toki wa, itsumo senaka ga itakatta desu.","german":"When I worked in the mine, I constantly had back pain.","furigana":"こうざん|はたら|とき|せなか|いた"},{"japanese":"この町はかつて銅の鉱山で栄えていた。","romaji":"Kono machi wa katsute dou no kouzan de sakaete ita.","german":"This town was once prosperous thanks to its copper mine.","furigana":"まち|どう|こうざん|さか"},{"japanese":"鉱山の事故で作業員が中に閉じ込められた。","romaji":"Kouzan no jiko de sagyouin ga naka ni tojikomerareta.","german":"In a mining accident, workers were trapped underground.","furigana":"こうざん|じこ|さぎょういん|なか|と|こ"}],"notes":"鉱山 is a mine in which ores or minerals are extracted; specific kinds are called 金山, 銀山 or 銅山, a coal mine by contrast 炭鉱. Typical are 鉱山で働く, 鉱山を開発する and 鉱山事故 \"mining accident\". Be careful when listening: 高山 (\"high mountain\") is also read こうざん. A military explosive mine is not 鉱山 but 地雷.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どんな鉱石でも、彼が触ると金に変わってしまうのさ。","romaji":"Donna kōseki de mo, kare ga sawaru to kin ni kawatte shimau no sa.","german":"Any ore he touches turns to gold.","furigana":"こうせき|かれ|さわ|きん|か"},{"japanese":"この地域では昔から鉄の鉱石が採れる。","romaji":"Kono chiiki de wa mukashi kara tetsu no kouseki ga toreru.","german":"Iron ore has been mined in this area since long ago.","furigana":"ちいき|むかし|てつ|こうせき|と"},{"japanese":"博物館で珍しい鉱石の標本を見た。","romaji":"Hakubutsukan de mezurashii kouseki no hyouhon o mita.","german":"At the museum, I saw samples of rare ores.","furigana":"はくぶつかん|めずら|こうせき|ひょうほん|み"}],"notes":"鉱石 is \"ore\", that is, rock from which a metal is extracted; compounds are 鉄鉱石 \"iron ore\" and 金鉱石 \"gold ore\". Typical verbs are 鉱石を採掘する (\"to mine\") and 鉱石が採れる (\"to be obtained\"). It should be distinguished from the scientific term 鉱物 \"mineral\" and from the gemstone 宝石, even though in everyday speech 鉱石 sometimes loosely refers to pretty crystals.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"うーん。この炭鉱が閉山するってのは、本当に残念ですね。","romaji":"Ūn. Kono tankō ga heizan suru tte no wa, hontōni zannen desu ne.","german":"Hmm. It's really a shame that this coal mine is going to close.","furigana":"たんこう|へいざん|ほんとう|ざんねん"},{"japanese":"祖父は若い頃、九州の炭鉱で働いていた。","romaji":"Sofu wa wakai koro, Kyuushuu no tankou de hataraite ita.","german":"In his youth, my grandfather worked in a coal mine in Kyushu.","furigana":"そふ|わか|ころ|きゅうしゅう|たんこう|はたら"},{"japanese":"炭鉱の町として栄えた夕張は、今では観光に力を入れている。","romaji":"Tankou no machi to shite sakaeta Yuubari wa, ima de wa kankou ni chikara o irete iru.","german":"Yubari, which once flourished as a coal-mining town, now focuses on tourism.","furigana":"たんこう|まち|さか||いま|かんこう|ちから|い"}],"notes":"炭鉱 is a coal mine (炭 \"coal\" + 鉱 \"mine, ore\"); ore mines in general are 鉱山. The spelling 炭坑 properly refers to the shaft but is often used with the same meaning. Typical are 炭鉱で働く, 炭鉱の町 and 閉山 (\"closure of a mine\"); miners are called 炭鉱労働者 or, older, 炭鉱夫. In Japan the word is associated with the decline of towns such as Yubari or those in Kyushu.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"マイクラでたくさん鉄鉱石を採掘した。","romaji":"Maikura de takusan tekkōseki o saikutsu shita.","german":"I mined a lot of iron ore in Minecraft.","furigana":"てっこうせき|さいくつ"},{"japanese":"オーストラリアは世界有数の鉄鉱石の輸出国だ。","romaji":"Oosutoraria wa sekai yuusuu no tekkouseki no yushutsukoku da.","german":"Australia is one of the world's leading exporters of iron ore.","furigana":"せかいゆうすう|てっこうせき|ゆしゅつこく"},{"japanese":"この山には良質な鉄鉱が眠っていると言われている。","romaji":"Kono yama ni wa ryoushitsu na tekkou ga nemutte iru to iwarete iru.","german":"It is said that high-quality iron ore lies buried in this mountain.","furigana":"やま|りょうしつ|てっこう|ねむ|い"}],"notes":"鉄鉱 means \"iron ore\" but on its own occurs rather in technical texts; in normal usage people almost always say 鉄鉱石. Typical are 鉄鉱石を輸入する, 鉄鉱石を採掘する and 鉄鉱山 (\"iron ore mine\"). Do not confuse it with the identically read 鉄鋼 てっこう \"iron and steel\", as in 鉄鋼業 \"steel industry\" - that is the processed product, not the raw material.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"父に夏至から11日目のことを何と呼ぶか知っているかと聞かれたので、得意気に「ハゲ症」って答えたんですけど、それって「ハゲ頭の症状」って意味になるみたいで。父は笑って、正しい答えは「半夏生」だよって教えてくれましたけどね。","romaji":"Chichi ni geshi kara 11nichime no koto o nan to yobu ka shitte iru ka to kikareta node, tokuige ni \"Hageshō\" tte kotaeta n desu kedo, sore tte \"hageatama no shōjō\" tte imi ni naru mitai de. Chichi wa waratte, tadashii kotae wa \"Hangeshō\" da yo tte oshiete kuremashita kedo ne.","german":"My father asked me if I knew what the 11th day after the summer solstice is called, and I proudly answered \"hage-sho\", but it seems that means \"bald-head symptom\". He laughed and told me the correct answer is \"hangesho\".","furigana":"ちち|げし|にちめ|なん|よ|し|き|とくいげ|しょう|こた|あたま|しょうじょう|いみ|ちち|わら|ただ|こた|はんげしょう|おし"},{"japanese":"夏至は、一年で最も昼が長い日です。","romaji":"Geshi wa, ichinen de mottomo hiru ga nagai hi desu.","german":"The summer solstice is the day with the longest daylight of the year.","furigana":"げし|いちねん|もっと|ひる|なが|ひ"},{"japanese":"北欧では、夏至の日に盛大なお祭りが行われる。","romaji":"Hokuō de wa, geshi no hi ni seidai na omatsuri ga okonawareru.","german":"In Scandinavia, a big festival is held on the day of the summer solstice.","furigana":"ほくおう|げし|ひ|せいだい|まつ|おこな"}],"notes":"夏至 is the summer solstice around June 21, the day with the longest daylight. Note the unusual reading げし (夏 read as げ). The counterpart is 冬至 (とうじ), on which people in Japan traditionally take a yuzu bath and eat pumpkin; the equinoxes are called 春分 and 秋分. 夏至 is one of the 二十四節気 and falls in the middle of the rainy season in Japan.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どうして日本では冬至の日にカボチャを食べると良いというのですか？","romaji":"Dōshite Nippon de wa tōji no hi ni kabocha o taberu to yoi toiu no desu ka?","german":"Why do they say in Japan that it is good to eat pumpkin on the winter solstice?","furigana":"にっぽん|とうじ|ひ|た|よ"},{"japanese":"冬至は一年で昼がいちばん短い日です。","romaji":"Touji wa ichinen de hiru ga ichiban mijikai hi desu.","german":"The winter solstice is the day with the shortest daylight of the year.","furigana":"とうじ|いちねん|ひる|みじか|ひ"},{"japanese":"冬至の夜には、ゆず湯に入る習慣があります。","romaji":"Touji no yoru ni wa, yuzuyu ni hairu shuukan ga arimasu.","german":"On the night of the winter solstice, it is customary to take a bath with yuzu.","furigana":"とうじ|よる|ゆ|はい|しゅうかん"}],"notes":"冬至 is the winter solstice around December 22, the day with the shortest daylight; the counterpart is 夏至 げし \"summer solstice\". It is usually used as 冬至の日 or 冬至には. In Japan, people traditionally eat pumpkin (かぼちゃ) on this day and take a yuzu bath (ゆず湯). Do not confuse it with the identically read 当時 \"at that time\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このままでは赤字は必至だ。","romaji":"Kono mama de wa akaji wa hisshi da.","german":"If this continues, a deficit is inevitable.","furigana":"あかじ|ひっし"},{"japanese":"増税すれば、景気の悪化は必至だと専門家は見ている。","romaji":"Zouzei sureba, keiki no akka wa hisshi da to senmonka wa mite iru.","german":"Experts believe that a tax increase will inevitably worsen the economy.","furigana":"ぞうぜい|けいき|あっか|ひっし|せんもんか|み"},{"japanese":"主力選手が抜けたので、チームの苦戦は必至だ。","romaji":"Shuryoku senshu ga nuketa node, chiimu no kusen wa hisshi da.","german":"With the key players gone, the team is bound to struggle.","furigana":"しゅりょくせんしゅ|ぬ|くせん|ひっし"}],"notes":"必至 means \"inevitable, certain to occur\" and is usually used predicatively as 〜は必至だ, almost always for undesirable developments: 値上げは必至, 苦戦は必至. It belongs to written and news language. Beware of two common mix-ups: 必死 (same reading) means \"desperate, with all one's might\", and 必須 ひっす means \"indispensable, mandatory\" - 必至 is wrong for \"necessary\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は名門大学を首席で卒業した頭のいい男性なんでしょうが、明らかに白人至上主義者なのでそこが嫌いなんですよ。","romaji":"Kare wa meimon daigaku o shuseki de sotsugyō shita atama no ii dansei na n deshō ga, akiraka ni hakujin shijō shugisha na node soko ga kirai na n desu yo.","german":"He may be a smart man who graduated at the top of his class from a prestigious university, but he is obviously a white supremacist, and I dislike that about him.","furigana":"かれ|めいもんだいがく|しゅせき|そつぎょう|あたま|だんせい|あき|はくじんしじょうしゅぎしゃ|きら"},{"japanese":"お客様の安全を第一に考えることが、我が社の至上命令です。","romaji":"Okyakusama no anzen o daiichi ni kangaeru koto ga, wagasha no shijou meirei desu.","german":"Putting our customers' safety first is our company's supreme imperative.","furigana":"きゃくさま|あんぜん|だいいち|かんが|わ|しゃ|しじょうめいれい"},{"japanese":"家族と過ごす時間は、私にとって至上の喜びだ。","romaji":"Kazoku to sugosu jikan wa, watashi ni totte shijou no yorokobi da.","german":"Time spent with my family is the greatest joy for me.","furigana":"かぞく|す|じかん|わたし|しじょう|よろこ"}],"notes":"至上 means \"the highest, supreme\" and is usually used attributively as 至上の〜 (至上の喜び \"greatest joy\") or in compounds: 至上命令 \"supreme imperative\" and 〜至上主義 \"the ideology of putting X above everything\" (利益至上主義, 学歴至上主義, 白人至上主義). The latter usually sound critical. Do not confuse it with the homophones 史上 \"in history\" (史上初) and 市場 \"market\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"誠意のこもった説明が聞けたので、今回は彼女の言い分を聞いてあげることにしました。","romaji":"Seii no komotta setsumei ga kiketa node, konkai wa kanojo no iibun o kiite ageru koto ni shimashita.","german":"Since I heard an explanation made in good faith, I decided to hear her side this time.","furigana":"せいい|せつめい|き|こんかい|かのじょ|い|ぶん|き"},{"japanese":"ミスをしたなら、まずは誠意を持って謝るべきだ。","romaji":"Misu o shita nara, mazu wa seii o motte ayamaru beki da.","german":"If you made a mistake, you should first apologize sincerely.","furigana":"せいい|も|あやま"},{"japanese":"会社側の対応には誠意が感じられない。","romaji":"Kaishagawa no taiou ni wa seii ga kanjirarenai.","german":"I can't sense any sincerity in the company's response.","furigana":"かいしゃがわ|たいおう|せいい|かん"}],"notes":"誠意 is \"sincerity, good faith\", especially as an attitude shown in apologies, negotiations or complaints: 誠意を持って謝る, 誠意を見せる, 誠意ある対応. A typical reproach is 誠意が感じられない. The character trait \"sincere, honest\", by contrast, is expressed with the な-adjective 誠実. 誠意のこもった〜 means \"carried by sincerity\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今振り返ってみると、ハチ公は絶対的な忠誠心を持ったいい犬だったよな。","romaji":"Ima furikaette miru to, Hachikou wa zettaiteki na chuuseishin o motta ii inu datta yo na.","german":"Looking back now, Hachiko was a good dog with absolute loyalty.","furigana":"いまふ|かえ|こう|ぜったいてき|ちゅうせいしん|も|いぬ"},{"japanese":"騎士たちは王に忠誠を誓った。","romaji":"Kishitachi wa ou ni chuusei o chikatta.","german":"The knights swore loyalty to the king.","furigana":"きし|おう|ちゅうせい|ちか"},{"japanese":"社員に会社への忠誠を求める時代は終わった。","romaji":"Shain ni kaisha e no chuusei o motomeru jidai wa owatta.","german":"The days when employees were required to show loyalty to the company are over.","furigana":"しゃいん|かいしゃ|ちゅうせい|もと|じだい|お"}],"notes":"忠誠 is \"loyalty, allegiance\" toward a lord, state or organization. Typical phrases are 〜に忠誠を誓う (\"to swear loyalty to ...\"), 〜への忠誠 and 忠誠心 (\"loyalty\" as an attitude). The な-adjective 忠実, by contrast, means \"faithful, conscientious, true\" (原文に忠実な翻訳). Do not confuse it with the homophones 中世 \"Middle Ages\" and 中性 \"neutral\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"郷里を訪れるのはお金と時間の無駄です。","romaji":"Kyōri o otozureru no wa okane to jikan no muda desu.","german":"Visiting my hometown is a waste of time and money.","furigana":"きょうり|おとず|かね|じかん|むだ"},{"japanese":"定年後は郷里に戻って畑仕事をするつもりだ。","romaji":"Teinengo wa kyouri ni modotte hatakeshigoto o suru tsumori da.","german":"After retiring, I want to return to my hometown and grow vegetables.","furigana":"ていねんご|きょうり|もど|はたけしごと"},{"japanese":"郷里の母から、毎年りんごが送られてくる。","romaji":"Kyouri no haha kara, maitoshi ringo ga okurarete kuru.","german":"My mother back home sends me apples every year.","furigana":"きょうり|はは|まいとし|おく"}],"notes":"郷里 is the hometown or region one comes from; it sounds somewhat elevated and written-language. Typical are 郷里に帰る, 郷里の両親 and 郷里を離れる. In daily life one rather says 地元, 田舎 or 故郷 (ふるさと), and for the parental home 実家. Returning to one's hometown is called 帰郷.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ほとんどの博打打ちが思っているように、彼女も自分には天分があると思っていたんだが、結局あるポーカーのゲームで全財産を失って、帰郷するはめになったんだ。","romaji":"Hotondo no bakuchiuchi ga omotte iru you ni, kanojo mo jibun ni wa tenbun ga aru to omotte ita n da ga, kekkyoku aru pookaa no geemu de zenzaisan o ushinatte, kikyou suru hame ni natta n da.","german":"Like most gamblers, she believed she had a gift for it, but in the end she lost everything in a game of poker and had to return to her hometown.","furigana":"ばくちう|おも|かのじょ|じぶん|てんぶん|おも|けっきょく|ぜんざいさん|うしな|ききょう"},{"japanese":"十年ぶりに帰郷すると、駅前の様子がすっかり変わっていた。","romaji":"Juunen buri ni kikyou suru to, ekimae no yousu ga sukkari kawatte ita.","german":"When I returned to my hometown for the first time in ten years, the area in front of the station had completely changed.","furigana":"じゅうねん|ききょう|えきまえ|ようす|か"},{"japanese":"大学を卒業した後、彼は帰郷して家業を継いだ。","romaji":"Daigaku o sotsugyou shita ato, kare wa kikyou shite kagyou o tsuida.","german":"After graduating from university, he returned to his hometown and took over the family business.","furigana":"だいがく|そつぎょう|あと|かれ|ききょう|かぎょう|つ"}],"notes":"帰郷する means \"to return to one's home region\" and is intransitive; it is usually used without a destination, otherwise with に. It sounds somewhat written and can mean a visit or a permanent return. For the holiday visit to one's parents at New Year or Obon, 帰省 is the usual word; moving back to one's hometown is colloquially also 地元に戻る. The noun on its own means \"homecoming\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"奴らが山のてっぺんにある俺たちの村を侵攻しようとした時、敵陣が全員高山病に罹ってしまったのには笑えたよ。","romaji":"Yatsura ga yama no teppen ni aru oretachi no mura o shinkō shiyō to shita toki, tekijin ga zen'in kōzanbyō ni kakatte shimatta no ni wa waraeta yo.","german":"Es war lustig, dass das gesamte feindliche Lager an Höhenkrankheit erkrankte, als sie versuchten, unser Dorf hoch oben auf dem Berg zu überfallen.","furigana":"やつ|やま|おれ|むら|しんこう|とき|てきじん|ぜんいんこうざんびょう|かか|わら"},{"japanese":"1939年、ドイツ軍はポーランドに侵攻した。","romaji":"Sen kyuuhyaku sanjuukyuu nen, Doitsugun wa Poorando ni shinkou shita.","german":"1939 marschierte die deutsche Armee in Polen ein.","furigana":"ねん|ぐん|しんこう"},{"japanese":"軍事侵攻を受けて、国連は緊急の会議を開いた。","romaji":"Gunji shinkou o ukete, Kokuren wa kinkyuu no kaigi o hiraita.","german":"Nach der militärischen Invasion berief die UNO eine Dringlichkeitssitzung ein.","furigana":"ぐんじしんこう|う|こくれん|きんきゅう|かいぎ|ひら"}],"notes":"侵攻 ist die militärische Invasion. Als する-Verb steht es meist mit に (A国がB国に侵攻する), seltener mit を; typisch sind auch 軍事侵攻 und 侵攻を受ける. 侵略 betont dagegen die unrechtmäßige Eroberung und ist wertender, 侵入 ist allgemeines „Eindringen“ (auch Einbrecher). Vorsicht vor Homophonen: 進行 „Fortschreiten“, 信仰 „Glaube“, 新興 „aufstrebend“.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このコンピュータウィルスは、大晦日の夜に時計の針が12時を告げると、何千台ものパソコンに侵入するんですよ。","romaji":"Kono konpyūta wirusu wa, ōmisoka no yoru ni tokei no hari ga 12ji o tsugeru to, nanzendai mo no pasokon ni shinnyū suru n desu yo.","german":"This computer virus infects thousands of computers when the clock strikes midnight on New Year's Eve.","furigana":"おおみそか|よる|とけい|はり|じ|つ|なんぜんだい|しんにゅう"},{"japanese":"泥棒は窓を割って家に侵入した。","romaji":"Dorobō wa mado o watte ie ni shinnyū shita.","german":"The burglar broke a window and broke into the house.","furigana":"どろぼう|まど|わ|いえ|しんにゅう"},{"japanese":"関係者以外の侵入を禁止します。","romaji":"Kankeisha igai no shinnyū o kinshi shimasu.","german":"Entry by anyone other than authorized persons is prohibited.","furigana":"かんけいしゃいがい|しんにゅう|きんし"}],"notes":"侵入 is unauthorized or forcible entry; as an intransitive する-verb, the target takes に: 家に侵入する, システムに侵入する. Common compounds are 不法侵入 \"trespassing\" and 侵入者 \"intruder\". Beware of words read the same: 進入 is vehicles entering (車両進入禁止), and 浸入 is water seeping in. 侵略 refers to the military invasion of a country.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"たくさんの人たちが、プライバシーを侵されたことに憤慨しています。","romaji":"Takusan no hitotachi ga, puraibashii o okasareta koto ni fungai shite imasu.","german":"Many people are angry that their privacy was violated.","furigana":"ひと|おか|ふんがい"},{"japanese":"国籍不明の飛行機が領空を侵した。","romaji":"Kokuseki fumei no hikouki ga ryoukuu o okashita.","german":"An aircraft of unknown origin violated the airspace.","furigana":"こくせきふめい|ひこうき|りょうくう|おか"},{"japanese":"検査の結果、彼の体はがんに侵されていた。","romaji":"Kensa no kekka, kare no karada wa gan ni okasarete ita.","german":"The examination revealed that his body was affected by cancer.","furigana":"けんさ|けっか|かれ|からだ|おか"}],"notes":"侵す means \"to invade foreign territory, to violate rights\" and is transitive: 領空を侵す, プライバシーを侵す, 権利を侵す; often in the passive (〜を侵される). For diseases, 〜に侵される \"to be afflicted by\" is used. One must separate it from two verbs read the same: 犯す \"to commit a crime, to make a mistake, to break a rule\" and 冒す \"to take a risk\" (危険を冒す).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"伯父は、自社製品の表示を偽装して逮捕され、現在は刑務所にいます。","romaji":"Oji wa, jisha seihin no hyouji o gisou shite taiho sare, genzai wa keimusho ni imasu.","german":"My uncle was arrested for falsifying the labels on his company's products and is currently in prison.","furigana":"おじ|じしゃせいひん|ひょうじ|ぎそう|たいほ|げんざい|けいむしょ"},{"japanese":"ビザのための偽装結婚が見つかり、二人は逮捕された。","romaji":"Biza no tame no gisou kekkon ga mitsukari, futari wa taiho sareta.","german":"A sham marriage for a visa was discovered, and both were arrested.","furigana":"ぎそうけっこん|み|ふたり|たいほ"},{"japanese":"兵士たちは木の枝で戦車を偽装した。","romaji":"Heishitachi wa ki no eda de sensha o gisou shita.","german":"The soldiers camouflaged the tank with branches.","furigana":"へいし|き|えだ|せんしゃ|ぎそう"}],"notes":"偽装 is \"camouflage, pretense, concealment\"; 偽装する is transitive (〜を偽装する). In the news it appears in compounds such as 産地偽装 (\"false labeling of origin\"), 偽装結婚 (\"sham marriage\") or 偽装工作 (\"cover-up maneuver\"), and militarily for camouflage. Distinctions: 偽造 is the forging of an object (money, a passport), and 変装 is the disguising of a person.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"全く同じ日に、二種類の偽造紙幣が東京で出回り出しました。","romaji":"Mattaku onaji hi ni, ni shurui no gizō shihei ga Tōkyō de demawari dashimashita.","german":"Two kinds of counterfeit money started circulating in Tokyo on the same day.","furigana":"まった|おな|ひ|にしゅるい|ぎぞうしへい|とうきょう|でまわ|だ"},{"japanese":"パスポートを偽造した疑いで男が逮捕された。","romaji":"Pasupooto o gizou shita utagai de otoko ga taiho sareta.","german":"A man was arrested on suspicion of forging a passport.","furigana":"ぎぞう|うたが|おとこ|たいほ"},{"japanese":"彼は上司のサインを偽造して書類を提出した。","romaji":"Kare wa joushi no sain o gizou shite shorui o teishutsu shita.","german":"He forged his superior's signature and submitted the documents.","furigana":"かれ|じょうし|ぎぞう|しょるい|ていしゅつ"}],"notes":"偽造 is the forging of objects, especially money and documents: 紙幣を偽造する, パスポートの偽造, 偽造カード, 偽造品. It is transitive and is often used in the passive or attributively (偽造された書類). Distinctions: 偽装 is disguise or the feigning of facts, 改ざん is the subsequent altering of existing data. Counterfeit brand goods are usually called 偽物 in daily life.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"嘘ですよね？","romaji":"Uso desu yo ne?","german":"You're joking, right?","furigana":"うそ"},{"japanese":"子どもに嘘をついてはいけません。","romaji":"Kodomo ni uso o tsuite wa ikemasen.","german":"You must not lie to children.","furigana":"こ|うそ"},{"japanese":"彼は嘘ばかり言うので、もう信じられません。","romaji":"Kare wa uso bakari iu node, mō shinjiraremasen.","german":"He does nothing but lie, so I can't believe him anymore.","furigana":"かれ|うそ|い|しん"}],"notes":"嘘 is a lie; \"to lie\" is 嘘をつく (not 嘘を言う as the standard), a liar is 嘘つき. As an exclamation うそ！ or 嘘でしょう？ it colloquially means \"Really? No way!\" and is not an accusation. The word is often written in kana as うそ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ウソップは嘘吐きだ。","romaji":"Usoppu wa usotsuki da.","german":"Usopp is a liar.","furigana":"うそつ"},{"japanese":"「嘘つきは泥棒の始まり」と、子どもの頃よく母に言われた。","romaji":"Usotsuki wa dorobou no hajimari to, kodomo no koro yoku haha ni iwareta.","german":"\"A liar will also steal,\" my mother often told me as a child.","furigana":"うそ|どろぼう|はじ|こ|ころ|はは|い"},{"japanese":"約束を破ってばかりいると、嘘つきだと思われるよ。","romaji":"Yakusoku o yabutte bakari iru to, usotsuki da to omowareru yo.","german":"If you constantly break promises, people will think you are a liar.","furigana":"やくそく|やぶ|うそ|おも"}],"notes":"嘘吐き is \"liar\", derived from 嘘をつく \"to lie\"; it is almost always written 嘘つき or うそつき, the kanji 吐き are rare. It can be used directly as an insult (この嘘つき!) and often appears in 〜は嘘つきだ. Well known is the proverb 嘘つきは泥棒の始まり (\"a liar will also steal\"). The word is direct and derogatory; more cautiously one paraphrases with 嘘をつく人.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"生後半年で話せるようになったなんて、凄い！","romaji":"Seigo hantoshi de hanaseru yō ni natta nante, sugoi!","german":"It's amazing that he could talk at just six months old!","furigana":"せいごはんとし|はな|すご"},{"japanese":"昨日の夜はすごい雨でしたね。","romaji":"Kinō no yoru wa sugoi ame deshita ne.","german":"It rained really hard last night, didn't it?","furigana":"きのう|よる|あめ"},{"japanese":"日本語がすごく上手ですね。","romaji":"Nihongo ga sugoku jōzu desu ne.","german":"Your Japanese is really very good!","furigana":"にほんご|じょうず"}],"notes":"すごい is almost always written in kana and expresses a high degree, positive or negative: すごい人 \"an impressive person\", すごい雨 \"heavy rain\". As a reaction, すごい！ or すごいですね is very common. Before adjectives, standard language uses the adverb form すごく (すごく上手); すごい きれい is casual. More formal is 素晴らしい \"wonderful\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"喧嘩しないでよ。","romaji":"Kenka shinaide yo.","german":"Don't fight!","furigana":"けんか"},{"japanese":"昨日、彼女とけんかしてしまいました。","romaji":"Kinō, kanojo to kenka shite shimaimashita.","german":"I had a fight with my girlfriend yesterday.","furigana":"きのう|かのじょ"},{"japanese":"子どものころ、弟とよく喧嘩をしました。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, otōto to yoku kenka o shimashita.","german":"As a child, I often fought with my younger brother.","furigana":"こ|おとうと|けんか"}],"notes":"喧嘩 is a quarrel, ranging from a war of words to a brawl; as a verb 喧嘩する means \"to quarrel, to fight\", the person takes と: 友達と喧嘩する, 喧嘩になる \"it comes to a fight\". Typical are 兄弟喧嘩 and 夫婦喧嘩, a purely verbal quarrel is 口喧嘩, and to make up again is 仲直りする. Because of the difficult kanji, it is mostly written けんか or ケンカ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「ああ、喧しい！だまれ！」","romaji":"Ā, yakamashii! Damare!","german":"\"Ah, so noisy! Shut up!\"","furigana":"やかま"},{"japanese":"工事の音がやかましくて、夜も眠れない。","romaji":"Kouji no oto ga yakamashikute, yoru mo nemurenai.","german":"The construction noise is so loud that I can't sleep even at night.","furigana":"こうじ|おと|よる|ねむ"},{"japanese":"父は言葉遣いにやかましい人だった。","romaji":"Chichi wa kotobazukai ni yakamashii hito datta.","german":"My father was a man who was particular about how one spoke.","furigana":"ちち|ことばづか|ひと"}],"notes":"やかましい is usually written in kana and first means \"noisy, unpleasantly loud\", similar to the more common うるさい; as an exclamation (ああ、やかましい!) it is rude. Second, it means \"strict, fussy\": 〜にやかましい (\"to be fussy about ...\"), 口やかましい (\"constantly nagging\"). As with うるさい, annoyance or nuisance is always implied; neutral \"loud\" is 大きい音, or にぎやか for cheerful bustle.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"トーフグが今年、大躍進を遂げたのは何故だと思いますか。","romaji":"Toofugu ga kotoshi, daiyakushin o togeta no wa naze da to omoimasu ka.","german":"Why do you think Tofugu made such great strides this year?","furigana":"ことし|だいやくしん|と|なぜ|おも"},{"japanese":"今回の選挙で野党が大きく躍進した。","romaji":"Konkai no senkyo de yatou ga ookiku yakushin shita.","german":"The opposition made major gains in this election.","furigana":"こんかい|せんきょ|やとう|おお|やくしん"},{"japanese":"この会社は新製品のヒットで業界三位に躍進した。","romaji":"Kono kaisha wa shinseihin no hitto de gyoukai san'i ni yakushin shita.","german":"With the hit of its new product, the company jumped to third place in the industry.","furigana":"かいしゃ|しんせいひん|ぎょうかいさんい|やくしん"}],"notes":"躍進 is a big leap forward, a rapid rise in rank or importance. As an intransitive する-verb it is often used with a destination (業界三位に躍進する), and as a noun in 大躍進を遂げる. Typical contexts are elections, sports and business. 進歩, by contrast, means gradual progress, and 発展 development and growth in general.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"アヤの最新のトーフグキャラのイラストは、とても躍動感に溢れています。","romaji":"Aya no saishin no Tōfugu kyara no irasuto wa, totemo yakudōkan ni afurete imasu.","german":"Aya's latest illustration of the Tofugu characters is full of dynamism.","furigana":"さいしん|やくどうかん|あふ"},{"japanese":"グラウンドで躍動する選手たちの姿に、観客は大いに盛り上がった。","romaji":"Guraundo de yakudō suru senshutachi no sugata ni, kankyaku wa ōi ni moriagatta.","german":"The audience was greatly excited by the sight of the players moving energetically on the field.","furigana":"やくどう|せんしゅ|すがた|かんきゃく|おお|も|あ"},{"japanese":"春になると、自然の中に生命の躍動を感じる。","romaji":"Haru ni naru to, shizen no naka ni seimei no yakudō o kanjiru.","german":"In spring, you feel the pulse of life in nature.","furigana":"はる|しぜん|なか|せいめい|やくどう|かん"}],"notes":"躍動 refers to powerful, lively movement, the pulsing of energy (躍 \"jump\" + 動 \"move\"). The word is written-language and typical of sports reports, art criticism and advertising: 躍動する選手, 生命の躍動, 躍動感あふれる \"full of dynamism\". As a する-verb it is intransitive. In everyday speech people say 生き生きとした or ダイナミックな instead. Do not confuse it with 活躍 \"to distinguish oneself, to work successfully\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"それが不法投棄になるとは知らなかったんです。","romaji":"Sore ga fuhō tōki ni naru to wa shiranakatta n desu.","german":"I didn't know that it would count as illegal dumping.","furigana":"ふほうとうき|し"},{"japanese":"山の中に古い家電が投棄されていた。","romaji":"Yama no naka ni furui kaden ga touki sarete ita.","german":"Old home appliances had been dumped in the mountains.","furigana":"やま|なか|ふる|かでん|とうき"},{"japanese":"有害物質の海洋投棄は国際条約で禁止されている。","romaji":"Yuugai busshitsu no kaiyou touki wa kokusai jouyaku de kinshi sarete iru.","german":"Dumping hazardous substances into the ocean is banned by international treaty.","furigana":"ゆうがいぶっしつ|かいようとうき|こくさいじょうやく|きんし"}],"notes":"投棄 is the careless throwing away or dumping of things, usually waste; it almost always appears in 不法投棄 (\"illegal dumping\"), ゴミの投棄 or 海洋投棄 (\"ocean dumping\"). The word belongs to official and news language; in everyday speech people simply say 捨てる. 廃棄, by contrast, is orderly disposal or decommissioning, and 放棄 is giving up a right or plan.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"終電に駆け込み乗車をしようとしましたが、目の前でドアが閉まりました。","romaji":"Shūden ni kakekomi jōsha o shiyō to shimashita ga, me no mae de doa ga shimarimashita.","german":"I tried to dash onto the last train, but the door closed right in front of me.","furigana":"しゅうでん|か|こ|じょうしゃ|め|まえ|し"},{"japanese":"急に雨が降ってきたので、近くのコンビニに駆け込んだ。","romaji":"Kyuu ni ame ga futte kita node, chikaku no konbini ni kakekonda.","german":"It suddenly started to rain, so I dashed into a nearby convenience store.","furigana":"きゅう|あめ|ふ|ちか|か|こ"},{"japanese":"財布を落とした彼は、慌てて交番に駆け込んだ。","romaji":"Saifu o otoshita kare wa, awatete kouban ni kakekonda.","german":"Having dropped his wallet, he rushed into the police box in a panic.","furigana":"さいふ|お|かれ|あわ|こうばん|か|こ"}],"notes":"駆け込む means \"to run in, to rush in\" and is intransitive: 〜に駆け込む. It often implies hurrying to find shelter or help (交番に駆け込む, 病院に駆け込む). The noun 駆け込み appears in set expressions such as 駆け込み乗車 (\"boarding at the last second\", expressly prohibited in stations) and 駆け込み需要 (\"last-minute demand\" before a price increase). Distinctions: 飛び込む \"to jump into\", 駆け出す \"to start running\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼に質問したら、急に駆け出した。","romaji":"Kare ni shitsumon shitara, kyū ni kakedashita.","german":"When I asked him a question, he suddenly ran off.","furigana":"かれ|しつもん|きゅう|か|だ"},{"japanese":"信号が青になると、子どもたちは一斉に駆け出した。","romaji":"Shingō ga ao ni naru to, kodomotachi wa issei ni kakedashita.","german":"When the light turned green, the children all started running at once.","furigana":"しんごう|あお|こ|いっせい|か|だ"},{"japanese":"雨が降ってきたので、駅に向かって駆け出した。","romaji":"Ame ga futte kita node, eki ni mukatte kakedashita.","german":"It started to rain, so I ran off toward the station.","furigana":"あめ|ふ|えき|む|か|だ"}],"notes":"駆け出す means \"to start running, to suddenly break into a run\" or \"to run out\" (駆ける \"run\" + 〜出す \"begin; out\"): 家から駆け出す. It is intransitive and used mainly for people and animals; the neutral 走り出す also suits vehicles. The noun 駆け出し \"beginner, newcomer\" is very common: 駆け出しの記者 \"a rookie journalist\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"母に「不孝者」と呼ばれた。","romaji":"Haha ni \"fukōmono\" to yobareta.","german":"My mother called me an \"unfilial child\".","furigana":"はは|ふこうもの|よ"},{"japanese":"親の葬式にも帰らないなんて、とんだ親不孝者だ。","romaji":"Oya no sōshiki ni mo kaeranai nante, tonda oyafukōmono da.","german":"Not even coming home for your parents' funeral - what an unfilial child.","furigana":"おや|そうしき|かえ|おやふこうもの"},{"japanese":"何年も実家に帰らなかった私は、本当に不孝者でした。","romaji":"Nannen mo jikka ni kaeranakatta watashi wa, hontō ni fukōmono deshita.","german":"I didn't go home for years - I was truly an unfilial child.","furigana":"なんねん|じっか|かえ|わたし|ほんとう|ふこうもの"}],"notes":"不孝者 is someone who neglects their duties toward their parents (孝 \"filial piety\"). Today one mostly hears 親不孝者 or 親不孝 (親不孝をする); the opposite is 親孝行. The word is used as a reproach or in self-criticism and sounds somewhat old-fashioned. Do not confuse it with the identically read 不幸 \"misfortune\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"もう手遅れですが、私は本当に親不孝な息子でしたよ。","romaji":"Mō teokure desu ga, watashi wa hontōni oyafukō na musuko deshita yo.","german":"It's already too late, but I was truly an unfilial son.","furigana":"ておく|わたし|ほんとう|おやふこう|むすこ"},{"japanese":"親より先に死ぬことは、最大の親不孝だと言われる。","romaji":"Oya yori saki ni shinu koto wa, saidai no oyafukou da to iwareru.","german":"It is said that dying before your parents is the greatest unfilial act.","furigana":"おや|さき|し|さいだい|おやふこう|い"},{"japanese":"連絡もしないで親に心配をかけるなんて、親不孝だよ。","romaji":"Renraku mo shinaide oya ni shinpai o kakeru nante, oyafukou da yo.","german":"Making your parents worry by not getting in touch is unfilial.","furigana":"れんらく|おや|しんぱい|おやふこう"}],"notes":"親不孝 is the opposite of 親孝行: the behavior of a child who does not honor their parents and causes them worry or grief; it also refers to the ungrateful child themselves (親不孝者). It is used as a noun, as a な-adjective (親不孝な息子) and as a する-verb (親不孝をする). A well-known idea is that dying before one's parents is the greatest 親不孝. \"Disrespect\" in general would be 失礼 or 無礼 instead.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「携帯電話をお借りしてもいいですか？」「ええ、どうぞ。」","romaji":"Keitai denwa o okari shite mo ii desu ka? Ee, douzo.","german":"\"May I borrow your cell phone?\" \"Yes, go ahead.\"","furigana":"けいたいでんわ|か"},{"japanese":"授業中は携帯電話の電源を切ってください。","romaji":"Jugyouchuu wa keitai denwa no dengen o kitte kudasai.","german":"Please turn off your mobile phone during class.","furigana":"じゅぎょうちゅう|けいたいでんわ|でんげん|き"},{"japanese":"日本では、電車の中で携帯電話で話すのはマナー違反とされている。","romaji":"Nihon de wa, densha no naka de keitai denwa de hanasu no wa manaa ihan to sarete iru.","german":"In Japan, talking on a mobile phone on the train is considered bad manners.","furigana":"にほん|でんしゃ|なか|けいたいでんわ|はな|いはん"}],"notes":"携帯電話 is the mobile phone; in everyday speech it is almost always shortened to 携帯 or ケータイ, for smartphones people say スマホ, and for older flip phones ガラケー. Typical are 携帯電話の電源を切る, 携帯電話で話す and 携帯電話を充電する. 携帯 on its own originally means \"to carry with oneself\" (携帯する, 携帯用). The full form sounds rather formal, for example in announcements and house rules.","levelNote":"Compound: 携帯 (N4) + 電話 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"少し前まで、3Dプリンターは最新鋭の機械でした。","romaji":"Sukoshi mae made, surii dii purintaa wa saishin'ei no kikai deshita.","german":"Until recently, 3D printers were state-of-the-art machines."},{"japanese":"その病院には最新鋭の医療機器がそろっている。","romaji":"Sono byouin ni wa saishin'ei no iryou kiki ga sorotte iru.","german":"That hospital is equipped with state-of-the-art medical devices.","furigana":"びょういん|さいしんえい|いりょうきき"},{"japanese":"工場に最新鋭のロボットが導入された。","romaji":"Koujou ni saishin'ei no robotto ga dounyuu sareta.","german":"State-of-the-art robots have been introduced in the factory.","furigana":"こうじょう|さいしんえい|どうにゅう"}],"notes":"最新鋭 is composed of 最新 \"newest\" and 鋭 \"sharp, elite\" and means \"state-of-the-art, at the latest technical level\". It is almost always used attributively as 最新鋭の〜 with machines, weapons, facilities and devices (最新鋭の戦闘機, 最新鋭の設備). For fashion, news or software versions, people simply say 最新; 最新鋭 stresses top technical performance.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"野球用語の犠飛と犠打の意味について教えてもらえませんか？","romaji":"Yakyū yōgo no gihi to gida no imi nitsuite oshiete moraemasen ka?","german":"Could you explain to me what the baseball terms sacrifice fly and sacrifice bunt mean?","furigana":"やきゅうようご|ぎひ|ぎだ|いみ|おし"},{"japanese":"三回に犠飛で一点を先制した。","romaji":"Sankai ni gihi de itten o sensei shita.","german":"They took a one-run lead in the third inning on a sacrifice fly.","furigana":"さんかい|ぎひ|いってん|せんせい"},{"japanese":"彼は九回に同点の犠飛を打った。","romaji":"Kare wa kyuukai ni douten no gihi o utta.","german":"In the ninth inning, he hit a sacrifice fly to tie the game.","furigana":"かれ|きゅうかい|どうてん|ぎひ|う"}],"notes":"犠飛 is the short form of 犠牲フライ, the \"sacrifice fly\" in baseball: the batter is put out but allows a runner to score. The short form appears mainly in sports reports and statistics (犠飛で一点); in speech people usually say 犠牲フライ. The counterpart is 犠打 or 犠牲バント \"sacrifice bunt\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不透明な窓がある女子の更衣室です。","romaji":"Futōmei na mado ga aru joshi no kōishitsu desu.","german":"This is the girls' changing room, which has opaque windows.","furigana":"ふとうめい|まど|じょし|こういしつ"},{"japanese":"景気の先行きは依然として不透明だ。","romaji":"Keiki no sakiyuki wa izen to shite futoumei da.","german":"The economic outlook remains uncertain.","furigana":"けいき|さきゆ|いぜん|ふとうめい"},{"japanese":"政治資金の使い道が不透明だと批判されている。","romaji":"Seiji shikin no tsukaimichi ga futoumei da to hihan sarete iru.","german":"It is criticized that how the political funds are used is not transparent.","furigana":"せいじしきん|つか|みち|ふとうめい|ひはん"}],"notes":"不透明 literally means \"opaque, cloudy\" (glass, liquids, plastic), the opposite of 透明. Very common is the figurative use in news and business: 先行きが不透明 (\"the outlook is uncertain\") or 不透明なお金の流れ (\"opaque money flows\"). Fittingly, \"transparency\" is 透明性. As a な-adjective: 不透明な〜, 不透明だ.","levelNote":"Compound: 不 (N3) + 透明 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"十年前の旧正月のことは、克明に覚えていますよ。","romaji":"Jūnen mae no kyū shōgatsu no koto wa, kokumei ni oboete imasu yo.","german":"I remember the Lunar New Year of ten years ago in detail.","furigana":"じゅうねんまえ|きゅうしょうがつ|こくめい|おぼ"},{"japanese":"彼女は毎日の出来事を日記に克明に記録している。","romaji":"Kanojo wa mainichi no dekigoto o nikki ni kokumei ni kiroku shite iru.","german":"She records the events of each day in detail in her diary.","furigana":"かのじょ|まいにち|できごと|にっき|こくめい|きろく"},{"japanese":"その映画は戦争の悲惨さを克明に描いている。","romaji":"Sono eiga wa sensou no hisansa o kokumei ni egaite iru.","german":"The film depicts the misery of war in minute detail.","furigana":"えいが|せんそう|ひさん|こくめい|えが"}],"notes":"克明 means \"exact, down to the smallest detail\" and is mostly used adverbially: 克明に記録する, 克明に描く, 克明に覚えている. Attributively it appears as 克明な記録 or 克明な描写. It sounds written; in everyday speech people say 詳しく or 細かく. 鮮明, by contrast, stresses the clarity and vividness of a memory or image, not the completeness of its details.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本に到着した時、私はちょっとテンションが高すぎましたよね。","romaji":"Nippon ni tōchaku shita toki, watashi wa chotto tenshon ga taka sugimashita yo ne.","german":"I was too excited when I arrived in Japan, wasn't I?","furigana":"にっぽん|とうちゃく|とき|わたし|たか"},{"japanese":"飛行機は予定より三十分遅れて成田に到着した。","romaji":"Hikouki wa yotei yori sanjuppun okurete Narita ni touchaku shita.","german":"The plane arrived at Narita thirty minutes behind schedule.","furigana":"ひこうき|よてい|さんじゅっぷんおく|なりた|とうちゃく"},{"japanese":"荷物が到着したら、すぐに中身を確認してください。","romaji":"Nimotsu ga touchaku shitara, sugu ni nakami o kakunin shite kudasai.","german":"When the package arrives, please check the contents right away.","furigana":"にもつ|とうちゃく|なかみ|かくにん"}],"notes":"到着 is \"arrival\"; as an intransitive する-verb the destination takes に: 東京に到着する. It is the formal counterpart of 着く and appears in announcements, timetables and e-mails (到着時刻, 到着予定, 到着ロビー). Shipments also \"arrive\": 荷物が到着する. The opposite is 出発; 到達, on the other hand, means \"to reach a goal or level\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この双眼鏡の黒はありますか？","romaji":"Kono sōgankyō no kuro wa arimasu ka?","german":"Do you have these binoculars in black?","furigana":"そうがんきょう|くろ"},{"japanese":"双眼鏡で野鳥を観察するのが趣味です。","romaji":"Sougankyou de yachou o kansatsu suru no ga shumi desu.","german":"My hobby is watching wild birds with binoculars.","furigana":"そうがんきょう|やちょう|かんさつ|しゅみ"},{"japanese":"コンサートには双眼鏡を持っていくと便利だよ。","romaji":"Konsaato ni wa sougankyou o motte iku to benri da yo.","german":"It's handy to bring binoculars to concerts.","furigana":"そうがんきょう|も|べんり"}],"notes":"双眼鏡 means binoculars, literally \"double-eye glass\". It is used with で: 双眼鏡で見る, 双眼鏡でのぞく; it is counted with 台 or simply 一つ. Related words with 鏡 are 望遠鏡 \"telescope\", 顕微鏡 \"microscope\" and 眼鏡 \"glasses\". Typical places of use are bird-watching, concerts, stadiums and theaters.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"介護施設に、どうやら記憶喪失らしいお婆さんがいるんだけど、その人がすっごく面白いのよ。","romaji":"Kaigo shisetsu ni, dōyara kioku sōshitsu rashii obāsan ga iru n da kedo, sono hito ga suggoku omoshiroi no yo.","german":"There is an old woman at the nursing home who apparently has memory loss, and she is really funny.","furigana":"かいごしせつ|きおくそうしつ|ばあ|ひと|おもしろ"},{"japanese":"事故の後、彼はすっかり自信を喪失してしまった。","romaji":"Jiko no ato, kare wa sukkari jishin o soushitsu shite shimatta.","german":"After the accident, he completely lost his self-confidence.","furigana":"じこ|あと|かれ|じしん|そうしつ"},{"japanese":"違反を繰り返すと、資格を喪失することがあります。","romaji":"Ihan o kurikaesu to, shikaku o soushitsu suru koto ga arimasu.","german":"If you repeat violations, you may lose your qualification.","furigana":"いはん|く|かえ|しかく|そうしつ"}],"notes":"喪失 is the formal counterpart of 失う, but only for abstract things: 記憶喪失 \"memory loss\", 自信喪失, 資格喪失, 戦意喪失. As a する-verb it is transitive (自信を喪失する). 喪失感 is the \"feeling of emptiness after a loss\". For lost objects, people say なくす or, formally, 紛失.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この予想は、かなり堅いと思うよ。","romaji":"Kono yosō wa, kanari katai to omou yo.","german":"I think this forecast is quite solid.","furigana":"よそう|かた|おも"},{"japanese":"彼女は口が堅いので、安心して秘密を話せます。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kuchi ga katai node, anshin shite himitsu o hanasemasu.","german":"She keeps secrets well, so you can tell her a secret without worry.","furigana":"かのじょ|くち|かた|あんしん|ひみつ|はな"},{"japanese":"この木は堅くて、加工に時間がかかります。","romaji":"Kono ki wa katakute, kakō ni jikan ga kakarimasu.","german":"This wood is hard, and working it takes a long time.","furigana":"き|かた|かこう|じかん"}],"notes":"堅い describes a solid or reliable quality and can express that a result is considered certain. For a person, 口が堅い describes someone who keeps secrets to themselves. 堅い木 refers to the hardness of wood. 硬い often emphasizes material hardness, 固い often firm cohesion; the spellings overlap but are not equally common in every compound. The i-adjective forms 堅く and 堅くない.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どうしてこの文章を括弧で括ったんですか？","romaji":"Dōshite kono bunshō o kakko de kukutta n desu ka?","german":"Why did you put this sentence in parentheses?","furigana":"ぶんしょう|かっこ|くく"},{"japanese":"古新聞をひもでくくって、資源ごみに出した。","romaji":"Furushinbun o himo de kukutte, shigen gomi ni dashita.","german":"I tied the old newspapers with string and put them out for recycling.","furigana":"ふるしんぶん|しげん|だ"},{"japanese":"簡単な試験だと高をくくっていたら、全然できなかった。","romaji":"Kantan na shiken da to taka o kukutte itara, zenzen dekinakatta.","german":"I had taken the exam lightly, thinking it would be easy, and then couldn't do anything.","furigana":"かんたん|しけん|たか|ぜんぜん"}],"notes":"くくる is usually written in kana and is transitive: 〜を…でくくる (\"to tie ... together with ...\"), e.g. 新聞をひもでくくる, or to put text in brackets (括弧でくくる). Important expressions: 高をくくる (\"to underestimate something\"), 腹をくくる (\"to steel oneself for the worst\") and 一くくりにする (\"to lump together\"). 結ぶ, by contrast, means \"to knot\", and 縛る \"to bind, to tie up\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"パソコンを一括で払うか、分割で払うか迷っている。","romaji":"Pasokon o ikkatsu de harau ka, bunkatsu de harau ka mayotte iru.","german":"I'm wondering whether to pay for the computer in one go or in installments.","furigana":"いっかつ|はら|ぶんかつ|はら|まよ"},{"japanese":"複数のファイルを一括してダウンロードできます。","romaji":"Fukusuu no fairu o ikkatsu shite daunroodo dekimasu.","german":"You can download multiple files at once.","furigana":"ふくすう|いっかつ"},{"japanese":"申し込みは担当部署で一括して受け付けます。","romaji":"Moushikomi wa tantou busho de ikkatsu shite uketsukemasu.","german":"Applications are accepted collectively by the department in charge.","furigana":"もう|こ|たんとうぶしょ|いっかつ|う|つ"}],"notes":"一括 means \"to put everything together and handle it at once\". It is mostly used adverbially as 一括で or 一括して: 一括で支払う \"to pay in a lump sum\", ファイルを一括してダウンロードする. Important compounds are 一括払い (opposite 分割払い \"installment payment\"), 一括購入 and 一括処理. Do not confuse with 一くくりにする \"to lump together\" and 統一 \"to unify\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"物事を総括的にとらえる力がある人材がほしいんですよね。","romaji":"Monogoto o sōkatsuteki ni toraeru chikara ga aru jinzai ga hoshii n desu yo ne.","german":"We want people who have the ability to grasp things comprehensively.","furigana":"ものごと|そうかつてき|ちから|じんざい"},{"japanese":"年度末に、一年間の活動を総括する会議が開かれた。","romaji":"Nendomatsu ni, ichinenkan no katsudou o soukatsu suru kaigi ga hirakareta.","german":"At the end of the fiscal year, a meeting was held to review the year's activities.","furigana":"ねんどまつ|いちねんかん|かつどう|そうかつ|かいぎ|ひら"},{"japanese":"監督は試合後、「守備に課題が残った」と総括した。","romaji":"Kantoku wa shiaigo, shubi ni kadai ga nokotta to soukatsu shita.","german":"After the game, the manager summed up: \"Defense remains an issue.\"","furigana":"かんとく|しあいご|しゅび|かだい|のこ|そうかつ"}],"notes":"総括 means \"to sum up and take stock, to evaluate in retrospect\": 一年間の活動を総括する, 選挙結果を総括する. It is transitive and belongs to the language of companies, politics and sports. 総括的 means \"comprehensive, overall\", 総括責任者 \"person with overall responsibility\". Shortening a text, by contrast, is 要約する, and in everyday speech people say まとめる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"左の括弧を読む時は単に「括弧」と言いますが、右の括弧を読む時は「括弧閉じる」と言います。","romaji":"Hidari no kakko o yomu toki wa tanni kakko to iimasu ga, migi no kakko o yomu toki wa kakko tojiru to iimasu.","german":"When reading the left bracket, you simply say \"kakko\", but when reading the right bracket, you say \"kakko-tojiru\".","furigana":"ひだり|かっこ|よ|とき|たん|かっこ|い|みぎ|かっこ|よ|とき|かっこと|い"},{"japanese":"答えを括弧の中に書きなさい。","romaji":"Kotae o kakko no naka ni kakinasai.","german":"Write the answer inside the parentheses.","furigana":"こた|かっこ|なか|か"},{"japanese":"会話の部分はかぎ括弧でくくってください。","romaji":"Kaiwa no bubun wa kagikakko de kukutte kudasai.","german":"Please put the spoken parts of the conversation in corner brackets.","furigana":"かいわ|ぶぶん|かっこ"}],"notes":"括弧 is the bracket or parenthesis and is often written in kana as かっこ. There are 丸括弧 （）, 角括弧 ［］ and the Japanese quotation marks かぎ括弧 「」. Typical are 括弧に入れる and 括弧でくくる (\"to put in brackets\"); when dictating, people say かっこ and かっことじ. Note: かっこいい \"cool\" comes from 格好 and has nothing to do with 括弧.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"父親は息子に円弧を描くように命令しました。","romaji":"Chichioya wa musuko ni enko o egaku yō ni meirei shimashita.","german":"The father ordered his son to draw an arc.","furigana":"ちちおや|むすこ|えんこ|えが|めいれい"},{"japanese":"コンパスを使って円弧を描きます。","romaji":"Konpasu o tsukatte enko o egakimasu.","german":"You draw a circular arc using a compass.","furigana":"つか|えんこ|えが"},{"japanese":"円弧の長さは半径と中心角から求められる。","romaji":"Enko no nagasa wa hankei to chuushinkaku kara motomerareru.","german":"The length of an arc can be calculated from the radius and the central angle.","furigana":"えんこ|なが|はんけい|ちゅうしんかく|もと"}],"notes":"円弧 is the \"circular arc\" (円 \"circle\" + 弧 \"arc\"), a technical term from geometry and engineering: 円弧を描く, 円弧の長さ. For trajectories and movements, people normally say just 弧: ボールが弧を描く. The word has nothing to do with the bow as a weapon (弓) or the structure (アーチ).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"抗議者の一人が、警察を挑発するために火炎瓶を投げつけました。","romaji":"Kougisha no hitori ga, keisatsu o chouhatsu suru tame ni kaenbin o nagetsukemashita.","german":"One of the protesters threw a Molotov cocktail to provoke the police.","furigana":"こうぎしゃ|ひとり|けいさつ|ちょうはつ|かえんびん|な"},{"japanese":"相手の挑発に乗ってはいけない。","romaji":"Aite no chouhatsu ni notte wa ikenai.","german":"Don't let yourself be provoked by your opponent.","furigana":"あいて|ちょうはつ|の"},{"japanese":"彼女は挑発的な態度で記者の質問に答えた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa chouhatsuteki na taido de kisha no shitsumon ni kotaeta.","german":"She answered the reporters' questions with a provocative attitude.","furigana":"かのじょ|ちょうはつてき|たいど|きしゃ|しつもん|こた"}],"notes":"挑発 is \"provocation\"; 挑発する is transitive: 相手を挑発する. Very common is 挑発に乗る (\"to rise to the bait\"), plus 挑発的な態度 and 挑発行為 (e.g. in a military context). 挑発的 can also mean \"provocative\" for clothing. Do not confuse it with 挑戦 \"challenge, attempt\", which sounds positive.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"クママンは中年の男やもめで、家業で金を掘っています。","romaji":"Kuma man wa chūnen no otokoyamome de, kagyō de kin o hotte imasu.","german":"Kumaman is a middle-aged widower who digs for gold in the family business.","furigana":"ちゅうねん|おとこ|かぎょう|きん|ほ"},{"japanese":"子どもたちは砂浜で大きな穴を掘って遊んだ。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa sunahama de ookina ana o hotte asonda.","german":"The children played on the beach and dug a big hole.","furigana":"こ|すなはま|おお|あな|ほ|あそ"},{"japanese":"秋になると、畑でさつまいもを掘ります。","romaji":"Aki ni naru to, hatake de satsumaimo o horimasu.","german":"In autumn, we dig up sweet potatoes in the field.","furigana":"あき|はたけ|ほ"}],"notes":"掘る is transitive and means \"to dig, to dig up\": 穴を掘る, 井戸を掘る, トンネルを掘る, 芋を掘る, 金を掘る (\"to prospect for gold\"). Important derivatives are 掘り出す (\"to dig out\"), 掘り下げる (\"to delve deeper into a topic\") and 掘り出し物 (\"bargain, find\"). Do not confuse it with the identically read 彫る \"to carve, to engrave, to tattoo\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自分の父親なんだから、善意に解釈してあげるべきよ。例えば、年を取って握力が弱っているせいで、あなたのビンテージのバカラのグラスを割っちゃったんだ、っていう風に。","romaji":"Jibun no chichioya na n da kara, zen'i ni kaishaku shite agerubeki yo. Tatoeba, toshi o totte akuryoku ga yowatte iru sei de, anata no bintēji no Bakara no gurasu o watchatta n da, tte iu fū ni.","german":"He's your father, so you should interpret it charitably. For example, as him breaking your vintage Baccarat glass because his grip strength has weakened with age.","furigana":"じぶん|ちちおや|ぜんい|かいしゃく|たと|とし|と|あくりょく|よわ|わ|ふう"},{"japanese":"体力テストで握力を測った。","romaji":"Tairyoku tesuto de akuryoku o hakatta.","german":"Grip strength was measured at the fitness test.","furigana":"たいりょく|あくりょく|はか"},{"japanese":"彼は握力が強くて、リンゴを片手で握りつぶせる。","romaji":"Kare wa akuryoku ga tsuyokute, ringo o katate de nigiritsubuseru.","german":"He has such a strong grip that he can crush an apple with one hand.","furigana":"かれ|あくりょく|つよ|かたて|にぎ"}],"notes":"握力 is grip or hand strength (握る \"to grip\" + 力 \"strength\"). Typical combinations are 握力が強い・弱い, 握力が落ちる (\"grip strength declines\") and 握力を測る. In Japanese schools it is measured in kilograms with a 握力計 in the 体力テスト; in medicine it is considered an indicator of aging. Muscle strength in general is 筋力.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"カヌーを揺らないで！","romaji":"Kanū o yuranaide!","german":"Don't rock the canoe!","furigana":"ゆ"},{"japanese":"母は眠っている私を揺り起こした。","romaji":"Haha wa nemutte iru watashi o yuriokoshita.","german":"My mother shook me awake from my sleep.","furigana":"はは|ねむ|わたし|ゆ|お"},{"japanese":"その事件は国全体を揺り動かした。","romaji":"Sono jiken wa kuni zentai o yuriugokashita.","german":"The incident shook the whole country.","furigana":"じけん|くにぜんたい|ゆ|うご"}],"notes":"揺る (\"to shake, to rock\", transitive) is rare as an independent verb and sounds archaic; it is alive mainly in compounds such as 揺りかご (\"cradle\"), 揺り起こす (\"to shake awake\") and 揺り動かす (\"to shake, to rouse\"). In everyday speech, \"to rock, to shake\" is 揺らす or 揺する, and intransitive \"to wobble, to sway\" is 揺れる. Also related are 揺るがす (\"to shake\") and 揺るぎない (\"unshakable\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日は一日中書斎で重要書類を探していたんですが、まだ見つからないんですよ。","romaji":"Kyō wa ichinichichū shosai de jūyō shorui o sagashite ita n desu ga, mada mitsukaranai n desu yo.","german":"I've been looking for an important document in my study all day today, but I still haven't found it.","furigana":"きょう|いちにちちゅうしょさい|じゅうようしょるい|さが|み"},{"japanese":"父は休みの日はいつも書斎にこもって本を読んでいる。","romaji":"Chichi wa yasumi no hi wa itsumo shosai ni komotte hon o yonde iru.","german":"On his days off, my father always shuts himself in his study and reads.","furigana":"ちち|やす|ひ|しょさい|ほん|よ"},{"japanese":"新しい家には小さな書斎を作りたい。","romaji":"Atarashii ie ni wa chiisana shosai o tsukuritai.","german":"In our new house, I want to build a small study.","furigana":"あたら|いえ|ちい|しょさい|つく"}],"notes":"書斎 is the home work or study room, classically with a desk and bookshelves. Typical are 書斎にこもる (\"to retreat into one's study\") and 書斎で仕事をする. An office outside the home is 事務所 or オフィス, a child's study room is 勉強部屋; today one also simply says 仕事部屋.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ちょうど斎場に到着するところです。","romaji":"Chōdo saijō ni tōchaku suru tokoro desu.","german":"We are just arriving at the funeral hall.","furigana":"さいじょう|とうちゃく"},{"japanese":"通夜は駅の近くの斎場で行われます。","romaji":"Tsuya wa eki no chikaku no saijou de okonawaremasu.","german":"The wake will be held at the funeral hall near the station.","furigana":"つや|えき|ちか|さいじょう|おこな"},{"japanese":"市の斎場は火葬場と同じ建物の中にある。","romaji":"Shi no saijou wa kasouba to onaji tatemono no naka ni aru.","german":"The city funeral hall is in the same building as the crematorium.","furigana":"し|さいじょう|かそうば|おな|たてもの|なか"}],"notes":"斎場 is a hall for funeral ceremonies such as the 通夜 (\"wake\") and 告別式 (\"farewell ceremony\"), often with an attached crematorium (火葬場). 葬儀場 and the more modern セレモニーホール mean the same. The meaning \"place of a religious ceremony\" is rare. The word sounds factual and formal and appears in funeral notices.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"こちらの料金設定は暫定的ですのでご留意下さいませ。","romaji":"Kochira no ryōkin settei wa zanteiteki desu node go ryūi kudasaimase.","german":"Please note that these price settings are provisional.","furigana":"りょうきんせってい|ざんていてき|りゅういくだ"},{"japanese":"台風の影響で、暫定のダイヤで運行しています。","romaji":"Taifuu no eikyou de, zantei no daiya de unkou shite imasu.","german":"Because of the typhoon, trains are running on a provisional timetable.","furigana":"たいふう|えいきょう|ざんてい|うんこう"},{"japanese":"現時点での暫定順位は三位です。","romaji":"Genjiten de no zantei jun'i wa san'i desu.","german":"As of this moment, the provisional ranking is third place.","furigana":"げんじてん|ざんていじゅんい|さんい"}],"notes":"暫定 means \"provisional, until a final decision is made\". It is used attributively as 暫定の〜, adverbially as 暫定的に, or in compounds such as 暫定政権 (\"interim government\"), 暫定予算 and 暫定順位 (\"provisional ranking\"). The tone is official. 一時的, by contrast, stresses only the temporal limit, and 仮 (\"temporary\") is closer to everyday speech.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"枢軸国は第二次世界大戦中、連合国と戦いました。","romaji":"Sūjikukoku wa dai niji sekai taisenchū, rengōkoku to tatakaimashita.","german":"The Axis powers fought against the Allies during World War II.","furigana":"すうじくこく|だいにじせかいたいせんちゅう|れんごうこく|たたか"},{"japanese":"首相は日米同盟を外交の枢軸と位置づけた。","romaji":"Shushou wa Nichibei doumei o gaikou no suujiku to ichizuketa.","german":"The prime minister positioned the Japan-US alliance as the pivot of diplomacy.","furigana":"しゅしょう|にちべいどうめい|がいこう|すうじく|いち"},{"japanese":"「悪の枢軸」という言葉は、2002年にアメリカの大統領が使った。","romaji":"Aku no suujiku to iu kotoba wa, nisen ni nen ni Amerika no daitouryou ga tsukatta.","german":"The phrase \"axis of evil\" was used by the US president in 2002.","furigana":"あく|すうじく|ことば|ねん|だいとうりょう|つか"}],"notes":"枢軸 literally means \"axis, pivot\", but is used almost only figuratively: the center of power or the linchpin of a policy. Historically, 枢軸国 refers to the \"Axis powers\" of World War II, with 連合国 \"the Allies\" as the counterpart; also well known is 悪の枢軸 \"axis of evil\". The word is written-language; 中枢 stresses the inner core, and 軸 is the neutral \"axis\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どうしてそれが彼女の中枢神経系を刺激したの？","romaji":"Dōshite sore ga kanojo no chūsū shinkeikei o shigeki shita no?","german":"How could that stimulate her central nervous system?","furigana":"かのじょ|ちゅうすうしんけいけい|しげき"},{"japanese":"彼は若くして会社の中枢を担う存在になった。","romaji":"Kare wa wakakushite kaisha no chuusuu o ninau sonzai ni natta.","german":"Even at a young age, he became a pillar of the company.","furigana":"かれ|わか|かいしゃ|ちゅうすう|にな|そんざい"},{"japanese":"東京は日本の政治と経済の中枢だ。","romaji":"Toukyou wa Nihon no seiji to keizai no chuusuu da.","german":"Tokyo is the political and economic center of Japan.","furigana":"とうきょう|にほん|せいじ|けいざい|ちゅうすう"}],"notes":"中枢 is the central control point or load-bearing core of a system or organization: 政府の中枢, 会社の中枢を担う \"to play a central role in a company\". In medicine it stands for the central nervous system: 中枢神経 as opposed to 末梢神経 \"peripheral nervous system\". The word is written-language; in daily life one says 中心.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"なんか車の調子が悪いと思ったら、車輪の中軸部分に不具合が見つかったんだよね。","romaji":"Nanka kuruma no chōshi ga warui to omottara, sharin no chūjiku bubun ni fuguai ga mitsukatta n da yo ne.","german":"I thought something was wrong with the car, and sure enough, a defect was found in the central axle of the wheel.","furigana":"くるま|ちょうし|わる|おも|しゃりん|ちゅうじくぶぶん|ふぐあい|み"},{"japanese":"彼はチームの中軸として活躍している。","romaji":"Kare wa chiimu no chuujiku to shite katsuyaku shite iru.","german":"He is active as a core member of the team.","furigana":"かれ|ちゅうじく|かつやく"},{"japanese":"三番から五番の中軸打線が、今日は一本もヒットを打てなかった。","romaji":"Sanban kara goban no chuujiku dasen ga, kyou wa ippon mo hitto o utenakatta.","german":"The core of the lineup, batters three through five, couldn't get a single hit today.","furigana":"さんばん|ごばん|ちゅうじくだせん|きょう|いっぽん|う"}],"notes":"中軸 is literally the \"central axis\" of a wheel or machine, but more often figuratively the \"key figure\" or \"core\" of a group: チームの中軸, 中軸を担う. In baseball, 中軸打線 denotes the batters numbered three to five, the heart of the batting order. 中心 and 中核 are similar; 中枢 is used more for organizations and systems.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"それぞれの国にはそれぞれのお国柄ってものがあって、私達はそれをお互いに尊重し合うべきだと思います。","romaji":"Sorezore no kuni ni wa sorezore no okunigara tte mono ga atte, watashitachi wa sore o otagai ni sonchou shiau beki da to omoimasu.","german":"Each country has its own national character, and I think we should all respect that in each other.","furigana":"くに|くにがら|わたしたち|たが|そんちょう|あ|おも"},{"japanese":"時間に正確なのは、日本のお国柄なのかもしれない。","romaji":"Jikan ni seikaku na no wa, Nihon no okunigara na no ka mo shirenai.","german":"Being punctual may simply be a Japanese characteristic.","furigana":"じかん|せいかく|にほん|くにがら"},{"japanese":"料理の味付けにもお国柄が出る。","romaji":"Ryouri no ajitsuke ni mo okunigara ga deru.","german":"A country's character also shows in the seasoning of its dishes.","furigana":"りょうり|あじつ|くにがら|で"}],"notes":"国柄 appears almost always with the polite prefix as お国柄: the character, mentality and customs of a country or even a region. Typical are お国柄が出る (\"the character shows\") and お国柄の違い. 国民性 is the more analytical term \"national character\", 土地柄 the character of a locality, and 人柄 the character of a person.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"英語で円滑なコミュニケーションを取るために、最も重要なことは何だと思いますか？","romaji":"Eigo de enkatsu na komyunikēshon o toru tame ni, mottomo jūyō na koto wa nani da to omoimasu ka?","german":"What do you think is most important for a smooth conversation in English?","furigana":"えいご|えんかつ|と|もっと|じゅうよう|なに|おも"},{"japanese":"会議を円滑に進めるために、事前に資料を配っておきます。","romaji":"Kaigi o enkatsu ni susumeru tame ni, jizen ni shiryou o kubatte okimasu.","german":"So that the meeting runs smoothly, I'm distributing the materials in advance.","furigana":"かいぎ|えんかつ|すす|じぜん|しりょう|くば"},{"japanese":"新しいシステムへの移行は円滑に行われた。","romaji":"Atarashii shisutemu e no ikou wa enkatsu ni okonawareta.","german":"The switchover to the new system went smoothly.","furigana":"あたら|いこう|えんかつ|おこな"}],"notes":"円滑 means \"smooth, without friction\" and is a formal na-adjective, typical of business and official language: 円滑に進める, 円滑に進む, 円滑な人間関係, 円滑なコミュニケーション. In daily life one mostly says スムーズに or 順調に. 円滑 emphasizes that processes or relationships function without friction and conflict.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"去年の夏、秋田県の八郎潟にバス釣りに行きました。","romaji":"Kyonen no natsu, Akitaken no Hachirōgata ni basuzuri ni ikimashita.","german":"Last summer, I went bass fishing in Hachirogata in Akita Prefecture.","furigana":"きょねん|なつ|あきたけん|はちろうがた|づ|い"},{"japanese":"新潟という地名には「潟」という字が入っている。","romaji":"Niigata to iu chimei ni wa kata to iu ji ga haitte iru.","german":"The place name Niigata contains the character for \"lagoon\".","furigana":"にいがた|ちめい|かた|じ|はい"},{"japanese":"潮が引いた干潟には、多くの渡り鳥が集まる。","romaji":"Shio ga hiita higata ni wa, ooku no wataridori ga atsumaru.","german":"Many migratory birds gather on the tidal flat at low tide.","furigana":"しお|ひ|ひがた|おお|わた|どり|あつ"}],"notes":"潟 is a lagoon or a bay cut off from the sea, also a tidal flat area. On its own the word is rare; it is encountered mainly in place names such as 新潟 or 八郎潟 (read がた) and in the word 干潟 ひがた \"tidal flat, Wadden Sea\". For an ordinary lake people say 湖, and for a bay 湾 or 入り江.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先生が顕微鏡の焦点の合わせ方について説明をしてくれていた時、私は上の空でした。","romaji":"Sensei ga kenbikyō no shōten no awasekata nitsuite setsumei o shite kurete ita toki, watashi wa uwanosora deshita.","german":"When the teacher was explaining how to focus the microscope, I was absent-minded.","furigana":"せんせい|けんびきょう|しょうてん|あ|かた|せつめい|とき|わたし|うわ|そら"},{"japanese":"今回の選挙では、経済政策が最大の焦点になっている。","romaji":"Konkai no senkyo de wa, keizai seisaku ga saidai no shouten ni natte iru.","german":"In this election, economic policy has become the biggest focus.","furigana":"こんかい|せんきょ|けいざいせいさく|さいだい|しょうてん"},{"japanese":"この本は戦後の女性の生き方に焦点を当てている。","romaji":"Kono hon wa sengo no josei no ikikata ni shouten o atete iru.","german":"This book focuses on how women lived after the war.","furigana":"ほん|せんご|じょせい|い|かた|しょうてん|あ"}],"notes":"焦点 is the optical \"focal point\": 焦点を合わせる (\"to bring into focus\"), 焦点が合う; colloquially people say ピント for this. Very common is the figurative use as \"key point, center of attention\": 〜に焦点を当てる (\"to focus on ...\"), 焦点を絞る (\"to narrow down\"), 〜が焦点となる (\"to be the central issue\"). The word is a noun.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"常にかき混ぜることで、鍋の底が焦げるのを防げるとお母さんが言っていたわ。","romaji":"Tsune ni kakimazeru koto de, nabe no soko ga kogeru no o fusegeru to okaasan ga itte ita wa.","german":"My mom said that by stirring constantly, you can keep the bottom of the pot from burning.","furigana":"つね|ま|なべ|そこ|こ|ふせ|かあ|い"},{"japanese":"魚を焼きすぎて、皮が真っ黒に焦げてしまった。","romaji":"Sakana o yakisugite, kawa ga makkuro ni kogete shimatta.","german":"I grilled the fish too long, and the skin got burned completely black.","furigana":"さかな|や|かわ|ま|くろ|こ"},{"japanese":"アイロンを当てたままにしていたら、シャツが焦げてしまった。","romaji":"Airon o ateta mama ni shite itara, shatsu ga kogete shimatta.","german":"I left the iron on the shirt, and it got scorched.","furigana":"あ|こ"}],"notes":"焦げる is intransitive: the food itself \"burns\" or \"gets charred\" - 魚が焦げる, 鍋の底が焦げる. The transitive counterpart is 焦がす (\"to let burn\"): パンを焦がしてしまった. Fabric can also 焦げる from heat (iron, cigarette). Related are 焦げ目 (\"browned or burned spot\") and 焦げ臭い (\"to smell burnt\"); for a suntan, by contrast, people say 日に焼ける.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不快な気分を紛らすために、好きな音楽を大きな音で聴きました。","romaji":"Fukai na kibun o magirasu tame ni, suki na ongaku o ookina oto de kikimashita.","german":"To dispel my bad mood, I listened to my favorite music at high volume.","furigana":"ふかい|きぶん|まぎ|す|おんがく|おお|おと|き"},{"japanese":"入院中は、退屈を紛らすために毎日本を読んでいた。","romaji":"Nyuuinchuu wa, taikutsu o magirasu tame ni mainichi hon o yonde ita.","german":"In the hospital, I read every day to pass the time.","furigana":"にゅういんちゅう|たいくつ|まぎ|まいにちほん|よ"},{"japanese":"彼は恥ずかしさを笑いで紛らした。","romaji":"Kare wa hazukashisa o warai de magirashita.","german":"He covered up his embarrassment with laughter.","furigana":"かれ|は|わら|まぎ"}],"notes":"紛らす means to \"dispel\" unpleasant feelings or boredom, or to \"distract oneself\" from them: 気を紛らす, 退屈を紛らす, 寂しさを酒で紛らす - the means takes で. It also means \"to gloss over, to cover up\": 照れを笑いで紛らす. The variant 紛らわす is nowadays even more common and has the same meaning. Intransitive is 紛れる (\"to be distracted, to get lost in\"): 気が紛れる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この問題をこれ以上紛糾させたくないんです。","romaji":"Kono mondai o kore ijou funkyuu sasetakunai n desu.","german":"I don't want to complicate this problem any further.","furigana":"もんだい|いじょうふんきゅう"},{"japanese":"予算をめぐって議論が紛糾し、会議は夜遅くまで続いた。","romaji":"Yosan o megutte giron ga funkyuu shi, kaigi wa yoru osoku made tsuzuita.","german":"The discussion over the budget got out of hand, and the meeting went on until late at night.","furigana":"よさん|ぎろん|ふんきゅう|かいぎ|よるおそ|つづ"},{"japanese":"住民説明会は、反対意見が相次いで紛糾した。","romaji":"Juumin setsumeikai wa, hantai iken ga aitsuide funkyuu shita.","german":"The residents' briefing session became chaotic as objection followed objection.","furigana":"じゅうみんせつめいかい|はんたいいけん|あいつ|ふんきゅう"}],"notes":"紛糾 describes a meeting, negotiation or debate \"getting out of hand\" - it becomes tangled and contentious: 会議が紛糾する, 議論が紛糾した. It is intransitive; someone who complicates a matter 紛糾させる it. The word is typical of news and formal reports; in everyday speech people say もめる or ややこしくなる. Unlike 混乱 (\"disorder, confusion\"), 紛糾 always contains an aspect of dispute or conflict.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今は単なる凡人のように見えるかもしれないけど、こう見えてもかつては横綱だったんだよ。","romaji":"Ima wa tannaru bonjin no yō ni mieru kamo shirenai kedo, kō miete mo katsute wa yokozuna datta n da yo.","german":"I may look like an ordinary man now, but I was once a sumo grand champion.","furigana":"いま|たん|ぼんじん|み|み|よこづな"},{"japanese":"彼は二十代の若さで横綱に昇進した。","romaji":"Kare wa nijuudai no wakasa de yokozuna ni shoushin shita.","german":"He rose to yokozuna already in his twenties.","furigana":"かれ|にじゅうだい|わか|よこづな|しょうしん"},{"japanese":"この店のカレーは、まさにカレー界の横綱だ。","romaji":"Kono mise no karee wa, masa ni karee kai no yokozuna da.","german":"The curry at this shop is truly the number one among curries.","furigana":"みせ|かい|よこづな"}],"notes":"横綱 is the highest rank in sumo, the \"grand champion\". One is promoted to it (横綱に昇進する); below it stands the rank 大関. Originally 横綱 is the thick rope that the yokozuna wears around his waist when entering the ring ceremonially. Figuratively it means \"the best of its kind, number one\", often in the pattern 〇〇界の横綱 for a restaurant, a product or a company.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"綱引きは、日本と同じくらいアメリカでも人気がありますか？","romaji":"Tsunahiki wa, Nippon to onaji kurai Amerika de mo ninki ga arimasu ka?","german":"Is tug of war as popular in America as it is in Japan?","furigana":"つなひ|にっぽん|おな|にんき"},{"japanese":"運動会の綱引きで、赤組が白組に勝った。","romaji":"Undoukai no tsunahiki de, akagumi ga shirogumi ni katta.","german":"In the tug of war at the sports day, the red team beat the white team.","furigana":"うんどうかい|つなひ|あかぐみ|しろぐみ|か"},{"japanese":"新しい空港の場所をめぐって、二つの県の綱引きが続いている。","romaji":"Atarashii kuukou no basho o megutte, futatsu no ken no tsunahiki ga tsuzuite iru.","german":"The tug of war between the two prefectures over the site of the new airport continues.","furigana":"あたら|くうこう|ばしょ|ふた|けん|つなひ|つづ"}],"notes":"綱引き is \"tug of war\", a classic of the Japanese school sports day (運動会): 綱引きをする, 綱引きで勝つ. Figuratively it denotes a \"contest of strength, jockeying\" between two parties, for example in politics or business: 与党と野党の綱引き, 主導権をめぐる綱引き. 綱 is a thick, strong rope; an ordinary rope is called ロープ or 縄.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大地震が首都圏の交通網を麻痺させました。","romaji":"Daijishin ga shutoken no koutsuumou o mahi sasemashita.","german":"A major earthquake paralyzed the transportation network of the Tokyo metropolitan area.","furigana":"だいじしん|しゅとけん|こうつうもう|まひ"},{"japanese":"この町は交通網が発達していて、車がなくても生活できる。","romaji":"Kono machi wa koutsuumou ga hattatsu shite ite, kuruma ga nakute mo seikatsu dekiru.","german":"This town has a well-developed transportation network, so you can live without a car.","furigana":"まち|こうつうもう|はったつ|くるま|せいかつ"},{"japanese":"オリンピックに向けて、市内の交通網が整備された。","romaji":"Orinpikku ni mukete, shinai no koutsuumou ga seibi sareta.","german":"In the run-up to the Olympics, the city's transportation network was improved.","furigana":"む|しない|こうつうもう|せいび"}],"notes":"交通網 is the entire \"transportation network\" of a region, consisting of rail, bus and roads. The suffix 網 (\"net\") forms many such words: 通信網, 鉄道網, 道路網. Typical combinations are 交通網が発達している (\"to be well developed\"), 交通網を整備する (\"to build up\") and 交通網が麻痺する (\"to be paralyzed\"). The word belongs rather to written and news language; in conversation people often simply say 交通の便がいい.","levelNote":"Compound: 交通 (N5) + 網 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"台風が島を直撃した瞬間、全ての通信網が途絶えました。","romaji":"Taifuu ga shima o chokugeki shita shunkan, subete no tsuushinmou ga todaemashita.","german":"The moment the typhoon hit the island directly, all communications networks were cut off.","furigana":"たいふう|しま|ちょくげき|しゅんかん|すべ|つうしんもう|とだ"},{"japanese":"災害時でも通信網を維持できるように、対策を進めています。","romaji":"Saigaiji demo tsuushinmou o iji dekiru you ni, taisaku o susumete imasu.","german":"We are advancing measures so that the communications network can be maintained even during disasters.","furigana":"さいがいじ|つうしんもう|いじ|たいさく|すす"},{"japanese":"この国では、山奥の村まで通信網が広がっている。","romaji":"Kono kuni de wa, yamaoku no mura made tsuushinmou ga hirogatte iru.","german":"In this country, the communications network extends even to remote mountain villages.","furigana":"くに|やまおく|むら|つうしんもう|ひろ"}],"notes":"通信網 is the \"communications or telecommunications network\" made up of telephone, mobile and internet connections. Like 交通網, it belongs to the vocabulary of news and technology: 通信網が途絶える (\"to go down\"), 通信網を整備する (\"to build up\"), 通信網が広がる. In everyday speech people mostly say ネットワーク or describe concretely that 電話がつながらない. 網 originally means \"net\", as in a fishing net.","levelNote":"Compound: 通信 (N3) + 網 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あのA型肝炎の患者は愛に飢えており、いつも医者や看護師の気を引こうとしている。","romaji":"Ano A-gata kan'en no kanja wa ai ni uete ori, itsumo isha ya kangoshi no ki o hikō to shite iru.","german":"That hepatitis A patient is starved for love and is always trying to get the attention of the doctors and nurses.","furigana":"がたかんえん|かんじゃ|あい|う|いしゃ|かんごし|き|ひ"},{"japanese":"海外旅行の前に、B型肝炎のワクチンを打った。","romaji":"Kaigai ryokō no mae ni, Bī-gata kan'en no wakuchin o utta.","german":"Before traveling abroad, I got vaccinated against hepatitis B.","furigana":"かいがいりょこう|まえ|がたかんえん|う"},{"japanese":"お酒を飲みすぎると、肝炎になることもある。","romaji":"Osake o nomisugiru to, kan'en ni naru koto mo aru.","german":"If you drink too much alcohol, you can also get hepatitis.","furigana":"さけ|の|かんえん"}],"notes":"肝炎 is inflammation of the liver, hepatitis (肝 from 肝臓 \"liver\" + 炎 \"inflammation\"). One distinguishes A型肝炎, B型肝炎, C型肝炎 as well as ウイルス性肝炎 and アルコール性肝炎. To fall ill is 肝炎になる or 肝炎にかかる. Formed on the same pattern are 胃炎 \"gastritis\", 腸炎 and 皮膚炎. The word belongs to medical terminology but is widely known.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この公園では、芝生の上を歩くのは禁止されています。","romaji":"Kono kōen de wa, shibafu no ue o aruku no wa kinshi sa rete imasu.","german":"Das Betreten des Rasens in diesem Park ist verboten.","furigana":"こうえん|しばふ|うえ|ある|きんし"},{"japanese":"天気がいいので、芝生の上でお弁当を食べましょう。","romaji":"Tenki ga ii node, shibafu no ue de obentou o tabemashou.","german":"Das Wetter ist schön, lass uns auf dem Rasen unser Bento essen.","furigana":"てんき|しばふ|うえ|べんとう|た"},{"japanese":"隣の芝生は青く見えるものだよ。","romaji":"Tonari no shibafu wa aoku mieru mono da yo.","german":"Die Kirschen in Nachbars Garten sind eben immer süßer.","furigana":"となり|しばふ|あお|み"}],"notes":"芝生 ist die „Rasenfläche“ – das gepflegte Gras in Gärten, Parks oder Stadien. Typisch sind 芝生の上に座る, 芝生を刈る („mähen“) und das Schild 芝生に入らないでください („Rasen nicht betreten“). 芝 allein bezeichnet eher die Grasart bzw. die Rasensoden. Die Redewendung 隣の芝生は青い entspricht dem deutschen „Die Kirschen in Nachbars Garten sind immer süßer“.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"東芝のお客様センターから届いたメールは、全文文字化けしていました。","romaji":"Tōshiba no okyakusama sentā kara todoita mēru wa, zenbun mojibake shite imashita.","german":"The email from Toshiba's customer center arrived completely garbled.","furigana":"とうしば|きゃくさま|とど|ぜんぶんもじば"},{"japanese":"この冷蔵庫は東芝の製品です。","romaji":"Kono reizōko wa Tōshiba no seihin desu.","german":"This refrigerator is a Toshiba product.","furigana":"れいぞうこ|とうしば|せいひん"},{"japanese":"東芝は、日本を代表する電機メーカーの一つだ。","romaji":"Tōshiba wa, Nihon o daihyō suru denki mēkā no hitotsu da.","german":"Toshiba is one of Japan's leading electronics manufacturers.","furigana":"とうしば|にほん|だいひょう|でんき|ひと"}],"notes":"東芝 is the Japanese technology company Toshiba, headquartered in Tokyo. The name came from the merger of 東京電気 and 芝浦製作所. The company is known for home appliances, semiconductors and energy technology; \"made by Toshiba\" is 東芝製. In its logo the name is written in Latin letters as TOSHIBA, but in text it is usually written with kanji.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"庭の一部に芝草を植えて、子どもの遊び場にした。","romaji":"Niwa no ichibu ni shibakusa o uete, kodomo no asobiba ni shita.","german":"We planted lawn grass in part of the garden and turned it into a playground for the kids.","furigana":"にわ|いちぶ|しばくさ|う|こ|あそ|ば"},{"japanese":"梅雨の時期は芝草がすぐ伸びるので、こまめに刈る必要がある。","romaji":"Tsuyu no jiki wa shibakusa ga sugu nobiru node, komame ni karu hitsuyou ga aru.","german":"The rainy season makes lawn grass grow quickly, so you need to mow it often.","furigana":"つゆ|じき|しばくさ|の|か|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"ゴルフ場の芝草は、専門の職人が手入れしている。","romaji":"Gorufujou no shibakusa wa, senmon no shokunin ga teire shite iru.","german":"The lawn grass on the golf course is maintained by specialist craftsmen.","furigana":"じょう|しばくさ|せんもん|しょくにん|てい"}],"notes":"芝草 refers to the grass itself that a lawn is made of: \"lawn grass\". It is much rarer than 芝生 (the lawn area) or simply 芝, and appears more in texts about gardening, parks or golf courses. Typical combinations: 芝草を植える, 芝草が伸びる, 芝草を刈る. In everyday speech people almost always say 芝 or 芝生.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"駅員さんに、電車に自転車を持ち込む場合は、持ち運び用の袋の使用が義務づけられているって言われたんだけど、代わりにゴミ袋を使っちゃだめなのかなぁ。","romaji":"Ekiin-san ni, densha ni jitensha o mochikomu baai wa, mochihakobiyō no fukuro no shiyō ga gimuzukerarete iru tte iwareta n da kedo, kawari ni gomibukuro o tsukatcha dame na no ka nā.","german":"The station attendant told me that when taking a bicycle on the train you're required to use a carrying bag, but I wonder if I couldn't use a garbage bag instead.","furigana":"えきいん|でんしゃ|じてんしゃ|も|こ|ばあい|も|はこ|よう|ふくろ|しよう|ぎむ|い|か|ぶくろ|つか"},{"japanese":"燃えるゴミは、指定のゴミ袋に入れて出してください。","romaji":"Moeru gomi wa, shitei no gomibukuro ni irete dashite kudasai.","german":"Please put burnable trash in the designated garbage bag and put it out.","furigana":"も|してい|ぶくろ|い|だ"},{"japanese":"ゴミ袋がいっぱいになったから、口を縛っておいて。","romaji":"Gomibukuro ga ippai ni natta kara, kuchi o shibatte oite.","german":"The garbage bag is full, so tie it up for me.","furigana":"ぶくろ|くち|しば"}],"notes":"ゴミ袋 (also written ごみ袋) is a garbage bag or trash sack; here 袋 is read with a voiced initial, ぶくろ. In many Japanese municipalities, waste has to be sorted by type (燃えるゴミ, 燃えないゴミ) and disposed of in prescribed, sometimes paid, bags (指定のゴミ袋). Typical combinations are ゴミ袋に入れる, ゴミ袋を出す \"put out\" and 口を縛る \"tie up\". General plastic bags are called ビニール袋 or ポリ袋.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"何か両親に親孝行してあげたいと思っています。","romaji":"Nani ka ryōshin ni oyakōkō shite agetai to omotte imasu.","german":"I'd like to do something to show my parents how grateful I am.","furigana":"なに|りょうしん|おやこうこう|おも"},{"japanese":"初めての給料で、両親を温泉旅行に連れて行って親孝行した。","romaji":"Hajimete no kyuuryou de, ryoushin o onsen ryokou ni tsurete itte oyakoukou shita.","german":"With my first paycheck, I took my parents on an onsen trip to repay them.","furigana":"はじ|きゅうりょう|りょうしん|おんせんりょこう|つ|い|おやこうこう"},{"japanese":"田中さんの娘さんは本当に親孝行ですね。","romaji":"Tanaka-san no musume-san wa hontou ni oyakoukou desu ne.","german":"Mrs. Tanaka's daughter is really devoted to her parents, isn't she?","furigana":"たなか|むすめ|ほんとう|おやこうこう"}],"notes":"親孝行 means taking good care of one's own parents and making them happy: \"filial piety, duty toward one's parents\". As a する-verb: 親孝行する or 親孝行をする; as a な-adjective: 親孝行な息子 (\"a son who is good to his parents\"). The opposite is 親不孝. The proverb 親孝行したい時に親はなし warns that parents are often no longer alive by the time you finally want to thank them.","levelNote":"Compound: 親 (N4) + 孝行 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"俺の新しい髪型を見て、あいつプッと吹き出したんだぜ。失礼だよな。","romaji":"Ore no atarashii kamigata o mite, aitsu putto fukidashita n da ze. Shitsurei da yo na.","german":"When he saw my new hairstyle, that guy burst out laughing with a snort. How rude, right?","furigana":"おれ|あたら|かみがた|み|ふ|だ|しつれい"},{"japanese":"割れた水道管から水が勢いよく吹き出した。","romaji":"Wareta suidoukan kara mizu ga ikioi yoku fukidashita.","german":"Water gushed out forcefully from the burst water pipe.","furigana":"わ|すいどうかん|みず|いきお|ふ|だ"},{"japanese":"外に出たとたん、暑さで汗が吹き出してきた。","romaji":"Soto ni deta totan, atsusa de ase ga fukidashite kita.","german":"The moment I stepped outside, sweat broke out from the heat.","furigana":"そと|で|あつ|あせ|ふ|だ"}],"notes":"吹き出す has two main uses: liquid, gas or sweat \"shoots out, gushes forth\" (水が吹き出す, 汗が吹き出す), in which sense it is often also written 噴き出す. It also means someone suddenly \"bursts out\" laughing, often with プッと or 思わず: 思わず吹き出した. Compared with 笑い出す (\"to start laughing\"), 吹き出す is more involuntary and sudden. The trigger is usually in a subordinate clause such as 〜を見て.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"うちはおんぼろで、嵐が来るたびに雨風が吹き込んでくる。","romaji":"Uchi wa onboro de, arashi ga kuru tabi ni amekaze ga fukikonde kuru.","german":"Our house is so rundown that wind and rain blow in every time a storm comes.","furigana":"あらし|く|あめかぜ|ふ|こ"},{"japanese":"留守番電話にメッセージを吹き込んでおきました。","romaji":"Rusuban denwa ni messeeji o fukikonde okimashita.","german":"I left a message on the answering machine.","furigana":"るすばんでんわ|ふ|こ"},{"japanese":"誰が子どもにそんな嘘を吹き込んだんだ？","romaji":"Dare ga kodomo ni sonna uso o fukikonda n da?","german":"Who put such a lie into the child's head?","furigana":"だれ|こ|うそ|ふ|こ"}],"notes":"吹き込む has several meanings: intransitive \"to blow in\" of wind, rain or snow (窓から雨が吹き込む); transitive \"to blow into\" (風船に息を吹き込む, figuratively 新しい命を吹き込む), \"to instill, whisper into someone's mind\" (子どもに変な考えを吹き込む) and \"to record, speak onto a recording\" (留守番電話にメッセージを吹き込む). The target is marked with に in each case. Do not confuse it with 吹き出す (\"to shoot out; to burst out laughing\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"口笛を吹くことにどんな魅力を感じますか。","romaji":"Kuchibue o fuku koto ni donna miryoku o kanjimasu ka.","german":"What do you find appealing about whistling?","furigana":"くちぶえ|ふ|みりょく|かん"},{"japanese":"父は機嫌がいいと、口笛を吹きながら料理をする。","romaji":"Chichi wa kigen ga ii to, kuchibue o fukinagara ryouri o suru.","german":"When my father is in a good mood, he whistles while he cooks.","furigana":"ちち|きげん|くちぶえ|ふ|りょうり"},{"japanese":"夜に口笛を吹くと蛇が出ると、祖母によく言われた。","romaji":"Yoru ni kuchibue o fuku to hebi ga deru to, sobo ni yoku iwareta.","german":"My grandmother often told me that if you whistle at night, snakes will come out.","furigana":"よる|くちぶえ|ふ|へび|で|そぼ|い"}],"notes":"口笛を吹く is the set phrase for \"to whistle\" with the mouth; 口笛 alone is whistling or a whistle sound. 吹く is used here as with wind instruments (笛を吹く, トランペットを吹く). For a referee's whistle, you say 笛を吹く or ホイッスルを鳴らす. In Japan there is a superstition that whistling at night attracts snakes or thieves.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この味噌汁はしょっぱすぎるよ。もうちょっと薄めてもらってもいいかな？","romaji":"Kono misoshiru wa shoppa sugiru yo. Mō chotto usumete moratte mo ii ka na?","german":"This miso soup is too salty. Could you dilute it a little?","furigana":"みそしる|うす"},{"japanese":"この洗剤は、水で十倍に薄めてから使ってください。","romaji":"Kono senzai wa, mizu de juubai ni usumete kara tsukatte kudasai.","german":"Please dilute this detergent ten times with water before use.","furigana":"せんざい|みず|じゅうばい|うす|つか"},{"japanese":"絵の具を水で薄めて、淡い空の色を出した。","romaji":"Enogu o mizu de usumete, awai sora no iro o dashita.","german":"I thinned the paint with water to get a pale sky color.","furigana":"え|ぐ|みず|うす|あわ|そら|いろ|だ"}],"notes":"薄める is transitive: you \"dilute\" a liquid, usually with water: 水で薄める, 十倍に薄める. Colors or flavors are also \"weakened\" this way, and figuratively impressions or statements too (印象を薄める). The intransitive counterpart is 薄まる (\"to become thinner, weaker\"). 薄める concerns concentration and strength; \"to cut thinner\" in the sense of thickness is instead 薄く切る or 薄くする.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"パーティーに行って、憂鬱なんて吹き飛ばしちゃおうぜ。","romaji":"Pātī ni itte, yūutsu nante fukitobashichaō ze.","german":"Let's go to the party and blow away your gloom.","furigana":"い|ゆううつ|ふ|と"},{"japanese":"台風の強い風で、屋根の瓦が吹き飛ばされた。","romaji":"Taifū no tsuyoi kaze de, yane no kawara ga fukitobasareta.","german":"The typhoon's strong wind blew the roof tiles off.","furigana":"たいふう|つよ|かぜ|やね|かわら|ふ|と"},{"japanese":"彼の明るい笑顔が、みんなの緊張を吹き飛ばした。","romaji":"Kare no akarui egao ga, minna no kinchō o fukitobashita.","german":"His bright smile dispelled everyone's tension.","furigana":"かれ|あか|えがお|きんちょう|ふ|と"}],"notes":"吹き飛ばす (吹く + 飛ばす) is transitive: wind or an explosion blows something away, often in the passive: 屋根が吹き飛ばされる. The intransitive counterpart is 吹き飛ぶ (帽子が吹き飛ぶ). Figuratively it means \"to dispel, blow away\" feelings and moods: 不安を吹き飛ばす, 暑さを吹き飛ばす, 疲れを吹き飛ばす – a popular expression in advertising and everyday speech.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この俳優は、万人に人気がある。","romaji":"Kono haiyuu wa, bannin ni ninki ga aru.","german":"This actor is popular with everyone.","furigana":"はいゆう|ばんにん|にんき"},{"japanese":"この映画はかなり暴力的なので、万人向けとは言えない。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa kanari bouryokuteki na node, bannin muke to wa ienai.","german":"This movie is quite violent and therefore not suitable for everyone.","furigana":"えいが|ぼうりょくてき|ばんにんむ|い"},{"japanese":"このラーメンは癖がなくて、万人受けする味だ。","romaji":"Kono raamen wa kuse ga nakute, bannin uke suru aji da.","german":"These ramen taste simple and go over well with everyone.","furigana":"くせ|ばんにんう|あじ"}],"notes":"万人 (ばんにん) means \"all people, everyone\" and sounds somewhat elevated. Common are 万人向け \"suitable for everyone\" and 万人受けする \"to go over well with everyone\". The reading まんにん also occurs in this meaning, but is mainly usual when counting: 一万人 (いちまんにん), 十万人 \"ten thousand, a hundred thousand people\". In daily life one otherwise simply says みんな or 誰にでも.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの犯人を逃がすことはできない。","romaji":"Ano hannin o nigasu koto wa dekinai.","german":"We can't let that criminal get away.","furigana":"はんにん|に"},{"japanese":"釣った魚が小さかったので、川に逃がしてやった。","romaji":"Tsutta sakana ga chiisakatta node, kawa ni nigashite yatta.","german":"The fish I caught was small, so I let it go back into the river.","furigana":"つ|さかな|ちい|かわ|に"},{"japanese":"迷っているうちに、せっかくのチャンスを逃がしてしまった。","romaji":"Mayotte iru uchi ni, sekkaku no chansu o nigashite shimatta.","german":"While I was hesitating, I let a precious chance slip away.","furigana":"まよ|に"}],"notes":"逃がす is the transitive counterpart of 逃げる: you let an animal or a person go \"free\" or \"escape\" – 鳥を逃がす, 犯人を逃がす. Secondly it means \"to miss, let slip\": チャンスを逃がす. In this second sense, the identically written 逃す (のがす) is more common and somewhat more literary. Proverb: 逃がした魚は大きい (\"the fish that got away was always the biggest\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は小柄だけど、とても元気です。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kogara da kedo, totemo genki desu.","german":"She is petite but full of energy.","furigana":"かのじょ|こがら|げんき"},{"japanese":"小柄な体で、あんなに重い荷物を運べるなんてすごい。","romaji":"Kogara na karada de, anna ni omoi nimotsu o hakoberu nante sugoi.","german":"I can't believe he can carry such heavy luggage with his small build.","furigana":"こがら|からだ|おも|にもつ|はこ"},{"japanese":"祖母は小柄の着物を好んで着ていた。","romaji":"Sobo wa kogara no kimono o kononde kite ita.","german":"My grandmother liked best to wear kimono with small patterns.","furigana":"そぼ|こがら|きもの|この|き"}],"notes":"小柄 describes a person with a \"small, petite build\": 小柄な女性, 小柄な体. It is more tactful than 背が低い because it refers to the whole figure and not just the height. The opposite is 大柄 \"large and strongly built\". For fabrics and kimono, 小柄 also means \"small pattern\", then usually with の: 小柄の着物.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女の手は透き通るほど白かった。","romaji":"Kanojo no te wa sukitōru hodo shirokatta.","german":"Her hands were so white they were almost transparent.","furigana":"かのじょ|て|す|とお|しろ"},{"japanese":"沖縄の海は底まで透き通っていて、魚がよく見える。","romaji":"Okinawa no umi wa soko made sukitootte ite, sakana ga yoku mieru.","german":"The sea in Okinawa is clear all the way to the bottom, so you can see the fish well.","furigana":"おきなわ|うみ|そこ|す|とお|さかな|み"},{"japanese":"彼女の透き通った歌声に、会場中が聞き入った。","romaji":"Kanojo no sukitootta utagoe ni, kaijouchuu ga kikiitta.","german":"The whole hall listened intently to her clear singing voice.","furigana":"かのじょ|す|とお|うたごえ|かいじょうちゅう|き|い"}],"notes":"透き通る is intransitive and means \"to be transparent, clear\", for example of water, glass or skin: 透き通った海, 透き通るような肌. Before nouns, 透き通った or 透き通るような is usually used. Figuratively it also describes a \"bright, clear\" voice: 透き通った声. 透明な (\"transparent\") is more matter-of-fact; the spelling 透きとおる also occurs.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"キュウリの漬物、美味しく漬かるといいな。","romaji":"Kyūri no tsukemono, oishiku tsukaru to ii na.","german":"I hope the pickled cucumbers turn out delicious.","furigana":"つけもの|おい|つ"},{"japanese":"この白菜はよく漬かっていて、ご飯に合う。","romaji":"Kono hakusai wa yoku tsukatte ite, gohan ni au.","german":"This napa cabbage is well pickled and goes great with rice.","furigana":"はくさい|つ|はん|あ"},{"japanese":"大雨で、田んぼが水に漬かってしまった。","romaji":"Ooame de, tanbo ga mizu ni tsukatte shimatta.","german":"The heavy rain left the rice paddies submerged in water.","furigana":"おおあめ|た|みず|つ"}],"notes":"漬かる is intransitive: vegetables in salt, vinegar or rice bran are well \"pickled, steeped\": よく漬かった白菜. The transitive counterpart is 漬ける (\"to pickle\"): 野菜を漬ける, and 漬物 comes from it as well. In the sense of \"to be submerged, stand in water\" or \"to get into the bath\" (お風呂に浸かる), 浸かる is usually written; both spellings belong to the same word.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鮭ラーメンなんて初めて食べるよ。","romaji":"Sake rāmen nante hajimete taberu yo.","german":"This is the first time I'm eating salmon ramen!","furigana":"さけ|はじ|た"},{"japanese":"駅の近くにおいしいラーメン屋があります。","romaji":"Eki no chikaku ni oishii rāmen'ya ga arimasu.","german":"There is a delicious ramen restaurant near the station.","furigana":"えき|ちか|や"}],"notes":"ラーメン here refers to the Chinese-influenced noodles or the noodle dish in soup. 鮭ラーメン denotes a variant with salmon; ラーメン屋 a shop that serves ramen. The usual spelling is katakana; the rare form 拉麺 is kept as a search alias. The same katakana form can denote a frame in construction engineering; this other meaning does not belong to this card.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この農場では殺虫剤の使用は厳禁です。","romaji":"Kono nōjō de wa satchūzai no shiyō wa genkin desu.","german":"The use of insecticides is strictly prohibited on this farm.","furigana":"のうじょう|さっちゅうざい|しよう|げんきん"},{"japanese":"ゴキブリが出たので、慌てて殺虫剤をかけた。","romaji":"Gokiburi ga deta node, awatete satchuuzai o kaketa.","german":"A cockroach appeared, so I hurriedly sprayed it with insecticide.","furigana":"で|あわ|さっちゅうざい"},{"japanese":"殺虫剤を使うときは、窓を開けて換気してください。","romaji":"Satchuuzai o tsukau toki wa, mado o akete kanki shite kudasai.","german":"When using insecticide, please open the window and ventilate.","furigana":"さっちゅうざい|つか|まど|あ|かんき"}],"notes":"殺虫剤 is an agent for killing insects (殺 \"kill\", 虫 \"insect\", 剤 \"agent\"). You spray it: 殺虫剤をまく, 殺虫剤をかける, 殺虫剤のスプレー. Distinguish it from 虫除け (\"insect repellent\", which only drives them away) and 農薬 (\"agricultural chemicals\" in general in farming). The suffix 〜剤 forms many names of agents such as 洗剤, 消毒剤 or 防腐剤.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私はキムチ炒飯が大好きです。","romaji":"Watashi wa kimuchi chāhan ga daisuki desu.","german":"I love kimchi fried rice.","furigana":"わたし||だいす"},{"japanese":"冷蔵庫の残り物で炒飯を作った。","romaji":"Reizōko no nokorimono de chāhan o tsukutta.","german":"I made fried rice from the leftovers in the fridge.","furigana":"れいぞうこ|のこ|もの||つく"},{"japanese":"ラーメンと炒飯のセットを一つください。","romaji":"Rāmen to chāhan no setto o hitotsu kudasai.","german":"One set of ramen and fried rice, please.","furigana":"|ひと"}],"notes":"炒飯 is fried rice in the Chinese style; the reading チャーハン comes from Chinese. It is usually written in katakana, and the kanji are seen mainly on the menus of Chinese restaurants. 炒 means \"to stir-fry in a wok\" (cf. 炒める). It is a classic in 中華料理店 and ラーメン屋, often as a set with ramen. Similar is 焼き飯, which is used regionally or in older speech for a comparable dish.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ショッピングモールは週末、買い物客で賑わう。","romaji":"Shoppingu mōru wa shūmatsu, kaimono kyaku de nigiwau.","german":"On weekends the shopping mall is full of shoppers.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|か|ものきゃく|にぎ"},{"japanese":"夏祭りの夜、神社は浴衣姿の人々で賑わっていた。","romaji":"Natsumatsuri no yoru, jinja wa yukata sugata no hitobito de nigiwatte ita.","german":"On the evening of the summer festival, the shrine was full of people in yukata.","furigana":"なつまつ|よる|じんじゃ|ゆかたすがた|ひとびと|にぎ"},{"japanese":"この商店街も、昔はもっと賑わっていたそうだ。","romaji":"Kono shoutengai mo, mukashi wa motto nigiwatte ita sou da.","german":"This shopping street is said to have been much livelier in the past.","furigana":"しょうてんがい|むかし|にぎ"}],"notes":"賑わう is intransitive: a place \"is bustling, full of people\". What fills it is marked with で: 観光客で賑わう, 買い物客で賑わう. Figuratively it also means \"to flourish, to do good business\" (店が賑わう). The related noun is 賑わい (賑わいを取り戻す \"to come back to life\"), the adjective 賑やか \"lively\". Unlike 混む, 賑わう emphasizes cheerful, positive liveliness, not unpleasant crowding.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"アイツはぜんぜん面白くないな。話題といったら軍艦島の都市伝説だけだし。","romaji":"Aitsu wa zenzen omoshirokunai na. Wadai to ittara Gunkanjima no toshi densetsu dake da shi.","german":"That guy is really boring. He talks about nothing but the modern legends surrounding Gunkanjima.","furigana":"おもしろ|わだい|ぐんかんじま|としでんせつ"},{"japanese":"軍艦島は長崎県にある無人島で、世界遺産に登録されている。","romaji":"Gunkanjima wa Nagasaki-ken ni aru mujintou de, sekai isan ni touroku sarete iru.","german":"Gunkanjima is an uninhabited island in Nagasaki Prefecture and is a World Heritage site.","furigana":"ぐんかんじま|ながさきけん|むじんとう|せかいいさん|とうろく"},{"japanese":"ツアーに参加して、軍艦島に残る古いアパートを見学した。","romaji":"Tsuaa ni sanka shite, Gunkanjima ni nokoru furui apaato o kengaku shita.","german":"On a guided tour, I looked at the old apartment blocks that still stand on Gunkanjima.","furigana":"さんか|ぐんかんじま|のこ|ふる|けんがく"}],"notes":"軍艦島 is the nickname of the island 端島 off Nagasaki: with its sea walls and concrete buildings, its silhouette is reminiscent of a \"warship\" (軍艦). Coal was mined there until 1974; today it is an uninhabited island of ruins and part of the UNESCO World Heritage. The name is always read ぐんかんじま, not ぐんかんとう. Typical: 軍艦島に上陸する, 軍艦島ツアーに参加する.","levelNote":"Compound: 軍艦 (N1) + 島 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの男、俺に偽物のグッチの財布を自慢してきたんだぜ。","romaji":"Ano otoko, ore ni nisemono no gutchi no saifu o jiman shite kita n da ze.","german":"That guy bragged to me about his fake Gucci wallet.","furigana":"おとこ|おれ|にせもの|さいふ|じまん"},{"japanese":"ネットで安く買ったバッグは、偽物だった。","romaji":"Netto de yasuku katta baggu wa, nisemono datta.","german":"The bag I bought cheaply online was a fake.","furigana":"やす|か|にせもの"},{"japanese":"この絵が本物か偽物か、専門家でも判断が難しい。","romaji":"Kono e ga honmono ka nisemono ka, senmonka demo handan ga muzukashii.","german":"Even experts find it hard to judge whether this painting is genuine or fake.","furigana":"え|ほんもの|にせもの|せんもんか|はんだん|むずか"}],"notes":"偽物 is a \"fake, imitation\" – the opposite of 本物 (\"genuine, the real thing\"). Before nouns, 偽物の is used: 偽物のブランドバッグ, 偽物の絵. Being palmed off a fake is 偽物をつかまされる. When a person is meant (\"impostor, a false ...\"), 偽者 with the same reading is usually written. More formal are 偽造品 and 模造品; counterfeit money is 偽札.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日付が変わる瞬間に包みを開けてもいい？","romaji":"Hizuke ga kawaru shunkan ni tsutsumi o akete mo ii?","german":"May I open this package at midnight?","furigana":"ひづけ|か|しゅんかん|つつ|あ"},{"japanese":"母から届いた包みには、手作りのお菓子が入っていた。","romaji":"Haha kara todoita tsutsumi ni wa, tezukuri no okashi ga haitte ita.","german":"The package from my mother contained homemade sweets.","furigana":"はは|とど|つつ|てづく|かし|はい"},{"japanese":"彼女は風呂敷の包みを大事そうに抱えていた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa furoshiki no tsutsumi o daiji sou ni kakaete ita.","german":"She was holding a bundle wrapped in a furoshiki cloth carefully in her arms.","furigana":"かのじょ|ふろしき|つつ|だいじ|かか"}],"notes":"包み is the noun of 包む (\"to wrap\") and refers to something wrapped up: a package or bundle in paper or cloth. You open it with 包みを開ける or 包みをほどく. For postal parcels one rather says 小包 or 荷物. 包み紙 is \"wrapping paper\", and as a counter it appears in 一包み (\"one package\"). The polite form お包み also refers to a monetary gift wrapped in paper.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の彼氏の苗字は「御手洗」という珍名です。","romaji":"Watashi no kareshi no myouji wa \"Mitarashi\" to iu chinmei desu.","german":"My boyfriend's surname is the unusual name 「御手洗」.","furigana":"わたし|かれし|みょうじ||ちんめい"},{"japanese":"テストで珍回答を書いてしまい、クラスで笑われた。","romaji":"Tesuto de chinkaitou o kaite shimai, kurasu de warawareta.","german":"I wrote a bizarre answer on the test and the whole class laughed at me.","furigana":"ちんかいとう|か|わら"},{"japanese":"野球の珍プレーを集めた番組は、毎年人気がある。","romaji":"Yakyuu no chinpuree o atsumeta bangumi wa, maitoshi ninki ga aru.","german":"Programs compiling baseball's funniest plays are popular every year.","furigana":"やきゅう|ちん|あつ|ばんぐみ|まいとしにんき"}],"notes":"珍〜 is a Sino-Japanese prefix meaning \"strange, curious, unusual\", often with a humorous tone: 珍名 (\"curious name\"), 珍事件 (\"strange incident\"), 珍回答 (\"bizarre answer\"), 珍プレー (\"funny play\"). In the positive sense of \"rare and precious\" it appears in 珍味 and 珍品. As an independent word, you say 珍しい (\"rare\") or 変な (\"strange\") instead.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は痛風なのでビールを飲まない方がいいのは分かっているんですが、友人からビールに良く合う珍味をもらったので今日ばかりは我慢できませんね。","romaji":"Watashi wa tsūfū na node bīru o nomanai hō ga ii no wa wakatte iru n desu ga, yūjin kara bīru ni yoku au chinmi o moratta node kyō bakari wa gaman dekimasen ne.","german":"I have gout and know I shouldn't drink beer, but a friend gave me delicacies that go well with beer, so I can't resist just for today.","furigana":"わたし|つうふう|の|ほう|わ|ゆうじん|よ|あ|ちんみ|きょう|がまん"},{"japanese":"この店では、全国各地の珍味を味わうことができる。","romaji":"Kono mise de wa, zenkoku kakuchi no chinmi o ajiwau koto ga dekiru.","german":"At this shop you can taste delicacies from all over the country.","furigana":"みせ|ぜんこくかくち|ちんみ|あじ"},{"japanese":"キャビアは世界三大珍味の一つとして知られている。","romaji":"Kyabia wa sekai sandai chinmi no hitotsu to shite shirarete iru.","german":"Caviar is known as one of the world's three great delicacies.","furigana":"せかいさんだいちんみ|ひと|し"}],"notes":"珍味 is a rare, special \"delicacy\". It often means regional specialties or small, often salty bites to go with alcohol, such as イカの塩辛 or からすみ: 酒の珍味, 北海道の珍味. Also well known is 世界三大珍味 (caviar, foie gras, truffles). Unlike ごちそう (\"feast, fine food\"), 珍味 emphasizes the rarity and unusualness of the taste.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"両者の意見が合わず、話し合いは決裂した。","romaji":"Ryōsha no iken ga awazu, hanashiai wa ketsuretsu shita.","german":"The two sides could not agree, and the talks broke down.","furigana":"りょうしゃ|いけん|あ|はな|あ|けつれつ"},{"japanese":"賃金をめぐる労使交渉が決裂し、組合はストライキに入った。","romaji":"Chingin o meguru roushi koushou ga ketsuretsu shi, kumiai wa sutoraiki ni haitta.","german":"The labor-management negotiations over wages broke down, and the union went on strike.","furigana":"ちんぎん|ろうしこうしょう|けつれつ|くみあい|はい"},{"japanese":"首脳会談は、合意に至らないまま決裂に終わった。","romaji":"Shunou kaidan wa, goui ni itaranai mama ketsuretsu ni owatta.","german":"The summit ended in a breakdown without reaching an agreement.","furigana":"しゅのうかいだん|ごうい|いた|けつれつ|お"}],"notes":"決裂 means that negotiations or talks \"fail, are broken off\" because no agreement can be reached: 交渉が決裂する, 会談が決裂した, 決裂に終わる. It is intransitive; the subject is almost always 交渉, 話し合い or 会談, not people. Typical of news language. For the failure of a relationship or a company, 別れる and 破綻する respectively are more fitting.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"電車の中で、突然の激しい腹痛に襲われました。","romaji":"Densha no naka de, totsuzen no hageshii haraita ni osowaremashita.","german":"On the train I was suddenly seized by severe abdominal pain.","furigana":"でんしゃ|なか|とつぜん|はげ|はらいた|おそ"},{"japanese":"最近、山の近くでクマが人を襲う事故が増えている。","romaji":"Saikin, yama no chikaku de kuma ga hito o osou jiko ga fuete iru.","german":"Recently, cases of bears attacking people near mountains have been increasing.","furigana":"さいきん|やま|ちか|ひと|おそ|じこ|ふ"},{"japanese":"昨夜、大型の台風が九州を襲った。","romaji":"Sakuya, oogata no taifuu ga Kyuushuu o osotta.","german":"A powerful typhoon struck Kyushu last night.","furigana":"さくや|おおがた|たいふう|きゅうしゅう|おそ"}],"notes":"襲う is transitive: people, animals or forces of nature \"attack, assault, strike\": クマが人を襲う, 台風が九州を襲う. Very common is the passive 〜に襲われる, also for sudden feelings and conditions: 不安に襲われる, 眠気に襲われる, 激しい腹痛に襲われる. The Sino-Japanese 襲撃する is more formal and only for targeted attacks; 攻撃する is more general and also used figuratively.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"警察の車は、暴走族に襲撃された。","romaji":"Keisatsu no kuruma wa, bousouzoku ni shuugeki sareta.","german":"A police car was attacked by a motorcycle gang.","furigana":"けいさつ|くるま|ぼうそうぞく|しゅうげき"},{"japanese":"武装した集団が夜中に村を襲撃した。","romaji":"Busou shita shuudan ga yonaka ni mura o shuugeki shita.","german":"An armed group raided the village in the middle of the night.","furigana":"ぶそう|しゅうだん|よなか|むら|しゅうげき"},{"japanese":"大使館が襲撃を受けたというニュースが流れた。","romaji":"Taishikan ga shuugeki o uketa to iu nyuusu ga nagareta.","german":"A report came in that the embassy had been attacked.","furigana":"たいしかん|しゅうげき|う|なが"}],"notes":"襲撃 is a targeted, often surprise \"raid, attack\" on people, vehicles or buildings: 村を襲撃する, 襲撃を受ける, 襲撃事件. As a suru-verb it is transitive; the passive 襲撃される is very common. It sounds more formal and more brutal than 襲う and is typical of news reports. 攻撃, by contrast, is the general term for \"attack\", also in sports or as criticism.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"敵の奇襲を予想できなかった。","romaji":"Teki no kishū o yosō dekinakatta.","german":"We did not anticipate the enemy's surprise attack.","furigana":"てき|きしゅう|よそう"},{"japanese":"夜明け前に、部隊は敵の基地を奇襲した。","romaji":"Yoake mae ni, butai wa teki no kichi o kishuu shita.","german":"Before dawn, the unit launched a surprise attack on the enemy base.","furigana":"よあ|まえ|ぶたい|てき|きち|きしゅう"},{"japanese":"相手チームの奇襲作戦に、うちの選手たちは対応できなかった。","romaji":"Aite chiimu no kishuu sakusen ni, uchi no senshutachi wa taiou dekinakatta.","german":"Our players could not cope with the opposing team's surprise tactics.","furigana":"あいて|きしゅうさくせん|せんしゅ|たいおう"}],"notes":"奇襲 is a \"surprise attack\" (奇 \"unexpected\", 襲 \"attack\"). Typical combinations: 奇襲をかける, 奇襲攻撃, 奇襲作戦, 奇襲を受ける. Originally military, it is also used figuratively in sports, games or business when someone catches others off guard with an unexpected tactic. As a する-verb it is transitive: 敵を奇襲する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"僕が空を見上げると、ちょうど空襲が始まるところだった。","romaji":"Boku ga sora o miageru to, chōdo kūshū ga hajimaru tokoro datta.","german":"When I looked up at the sky, an air raid was just about to begin.","furigana":"ぼく|そら|みあ|くうしゅう|はじ"},{"japanese":"祖母は子どもの頃、東京で空襲に遭ったそうだ。","romaji":"Sobo wa kodomo no koro, Toukyou de kuushuu ni atta sou da.","german":"My grandmother reportedly experienced an air raid in Tokyo as a child.","furigana":"そぼ|こ|ころ|とうきょう|くうしゅう|あ"},{"japanese":"空襲警報が鳴ると、人々は急いで地下に逃げた。","romaji":"Kuushuu keihou ga naru to, hitobito wa isoide chika ni nigeta.","german":"When the air-raid siren sounded, people hurriedly fled underground.","furigana":"くうしゅうけいほう|な|ひとびと|いそ|ちか|に"}],"notes":"空襲 is an \"air raid\" with airplanes and bombs. In Japan the word is closely associated with World War II, for example 東京大空襲 (the great air raid on Tokyo in 1945). Typical are 空襲を受ける, 空襲に遭う, 空襲で焼ける and 空襲警報 (\"air-raid warning\"). The verb form 空襲する is possible but rare; it is usually used as a noun. More general is 爆撃 (\"bombing\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"パックマンは、追ってくるモンスターを逆襲して食べ始めた。","romaji":"Pakkuman wa, otte kuru monsutaa o gyakushuu shite tabe hajimeta.","german":"Pac-Man turned on the monsters chasing him and started eating them.","furigana":"お|ぎゃくしゅう|た|はじ"},{"japanese":"前半は押されていたが、後半からチームは逆襲に転じた。","romaji":"Zenhan wa osarete ita ga, kouhan kara chiimu wa gyakushuu ni tenjita.","german":"The team was under pressure in the first half, but turned to a counterattack in the second.","furigana":"ぜんはん|お|こうはん|ぎゃくしゅう|てん"},{"japanese":"いじめられていた主人公が逆襲する物語が人気を集めている。","romaji":"Ijimerarete ita shujinkou ga gyakushuu suru monogatari ga ninki o atsumete iru.","german":"Stories in which a bullied protagonist hits back are gaining popularity.","furigana":"しゅじんこう|ぎゃくしゅう|ものがたり|にんき|あつ"}],"notes":"逆襲 is a \"counterattack\" by someone who had been on the defensive or seemed to be at a disadvantage. Typical are 逆襲に転じる (\"to switch to a counterattack\"), 逆襲に出る and 〜の逆襲 in film or book titles. As a verb you can say 敵を逆襲する. 反撃 is more neutral and mainly military or sporting; 仕返し is a personal \"revenge, payback\" in everyday life.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"年を取ったら長距離のフライトはきつくなるだろうね。","romaji":"Toshi o tottara choukyori no furaito wa kitsuku naru darou ne.","german":"Long-distance flights will become tiring for me as I age.","furigana":"とし|と|ちょうきょり"},{"japanese":"駅からホテルまでの距離はどのくらいですか。","romaji":"Eki kara hoteru made no kyori wa dono kurai desu ka.","german":"How far is it from the station to the hotel?","furigana":"えき|きょり"},{"japanese":"彼とは最近、少し距離を置いている。","romaji":"Kare to wa saikin, sukoshi kyori o oite iru.","german":"Recently I've been keeping a bit of distance from him.","furigana":"かれ|さいきん|すこ|きょり|お"}],"notes":"距離 is the spatial \"distance\" between two points: 駅までの距離, 距離が遠い/近い, 長距離・短距離. Figuratively it refers to the inner \"distance\" between people: 距離を置く \"to keep one's distance\", 距離が縮まる \"to grow closer\", 距離感. For spacing between things in a row, 間隔 fits better, for differences in numbers 差.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女があまりに露骨にものを言うもので、少々面食らいました。","romaji":"Kanojo ga amari ni rokotsu ni mono o iu mono de, shoushou menkuraimashita.","german":"I was quite surprised at how blunt she was.","furigana":"かのじょ|ろこつ|い|しょうしょうめんく"},{"japanese":"残業を頼んだら、部下に露骨に嫌な顔をされた。","romaji":"Zangyou o tanondara, buka ni rokotsu ni iya na kao o sareta.","german":"When I asked for overtime, my coworker made a frown quite openly.","furigana":"ざんぎょう|たの|ぶか|ろこつ|いや|かお"},{"japanese":"その映画は露骨な場面が多いので、子どもには見せられない。","romaji":"Sono eiga wa rokotsu na bamen ga ooi node, kodomo ni wa miserarenai.","german":"The film has many explicit scenes, so it can't be shown to children.","furigana":"えいが|ろこつ|ばめん|おお|こ|み"}],"notes":"露骨 describes something that is shown \"openly, bluntly\", usually with a negative note: 露骨に嫌な顔をする, 露骨な態度, 露骨な差別. As a na-adjective: 露骨な, adverbially 露骨に. For depictions it can also mean \"crude, offensive\": 露骨な表現. Unlike 率直 (\"frank, sincere\"), which is positive, 露骨 criticizes a lack of tact.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの露店で買ったアクセは全部すぐ壊れちゃったよ。","romaji":"Ano roten de katta akuse wa zenbu sugu kowarechatta yo.","german":"All the accessories I bought at that street stall broke right away.","furigana":"ろてん|か|ぜんぶ|こわ"},{"japanese":"お祭りの夜、神社の参道に露店がずらりと並んでいた。","romaji":"Omatsuri no yoru, jinja no sandou ni roten ga zurari to narande ita.","german":"On the night of the festival, stalls were lined up in a row along the approach to the shrine.","furigana":"まつ|よる|じんじゃ|さんどう|ろてん|なら"},{"japanese":"週末になると、この広場に古本の露店が出る。","romaji":"Shuumatsu ni naru to, kono hiroba ni furuhon no roten ga deru.","german":"On weekends, secondhand-book stalls appear in this square.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|ひろば|ふるほん|ろてん|で"}],"notes":"露店 is a \"street stall\" in the open air, for example at festivals or markets: 露店が並ぶ, 露店を出す, 露店で買う. In conversation people often say 屋台 (especially for food stalls) or 出店. Do not confuse it with 露天 (\"open air\") as in 露天風呂 – same reading, different kanji. Vendors who sell this way are called 露天商 or 露店商.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"思い直して、肌の露出が激しいドレスを着るのは控えることにしました。","romaji":"Omoinaoshite, hada no roshutsu ga hageshii doresu o kiru no wa hikaeru koto ni shimashita.","german":"I thought it over and decided to refrain from wearing a dress that shows so much skin.","furigana":"おも|なお|はだ|ろしゅつ|はげ|き|ひか"},{"japanese":"道路工事で、地下の水道管が露出している。","romaji":"Douro kouji de, chika no suidoukan ga roshutsu shite iru.","german":"Because of the road work, the underground water pipe is exposed.","furigana":"どうろこうじ|ちか|すいどうかん|ろしゅつ"},{"japanese":"ドラマのヒット以来、その俳優はテレビへの露出が増えた。","romaji":"Dorama no hitto irai, sono haiyuu wa terebi e no roshutsu ga fueta.","german":"Since the drama became a hit, the actor has been appearing on TV more.","furigana":"いらい|はいゆう|ろしゅつ|ふ"}],"notes":"露出 means that something hidden \"lies open, is exposed\" or is shown. It often concerns skin: 肌の露出, 露出が多い服 (\"revealing clothing\"), 露出度. Pipes, rock or roots can also 露出する. In relation to media it means \"presence, appearances\": テレビへの露出が増える; in photography it is \"exposure\". The verb is usually intransitive, transitive only in phrases like 肌を露出する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"牧師さんはもう今日は教会を出ちゃいましたか？","romaji":"Bokushi-san wa mou kyou wa kyoukai o dechaimashita ka?","german":"Has the pastor already left the church today?","furigana":"ぼくし|きょう|きょうかい|で"},{"japanese":"父は小さな町の教会で牧師をしています。","romaji":"Chichi wa chiisana machi no kyoukai de bokushi o shite imasu.","german":"My father is a pastor at a church in a small town.","furigana":"ちち|ちい|まち|きょうかい|ぼくし"},{"japanese":"結婚式では、牧師の前で二人が永遠の愛を誓った。","romaji":"Kekkonshiki de wa, bokushi no mae de futari ga eien no ai o chikatta.","german":"At the wedding, the two vowed eternal love before the pastor.","furigana":"けっこんしき|ぼくし|まえ|ふたり|えいえん|あい|ちか"}],"notes":"牧師 is a Protestant \"pastor, minister\". For Catholic priests one says 神父, for Buddhist monks 僧侶 or お坊さん, for Shinto priests 神主. The 牧 (\"to tend\") alludes to the image of the shepherd. In conversation one says 牧師さん or addresses him as 先生. Typical: 教会の牧師, 牧師になる, 牧師の説教.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"北海道には、見渡す限りの牧野が広がっている。","romaji":"Hokkaidou ni wa, miwatasu kagiri no bokuya ga hirogatte iru.","german":"In Hokkaido, pastureland stretches as far as the eye can see.","furigana":"ほっかいどう|みわた|かぎ|ぼくや|ひろ"},{"japanese":"この牧野では、夏の間だけ牛を放牧している。","romaji":"Kono bokuya de wa, natsu no aida dake ushi o houboku shite iru.","german":"On this pasture, cattle graze only during the summer.","furigana":"ぼくや|なつ|あいだ|うし|ほうぼく"},{"japanese":"将来は父の牧野を継いで、牛を育てたいと思っている。","romaji":"Shourai wa chichi no bokuya o tsuide, ushi o sodatetai to omotte iru.","german":"In the future I want to take over my father's pasture and raise cattle.","furigana":"しょうらい|ちち|ぼくや|つ|うし|そだ|おも"}],"notes":"牧野 (ぼくや) is \"grazing land\" where livestock graze – a rather literary, technical word from agriculture and geography. In everyday speech people usually say 牧場 (ぼくじょう, \"pasture, ranch, farm with livestock\") or 放牧地. Caution: as a family name 牧野 is read まきの, e.g. the botanist 牧野富太郎.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"氷点下の日々が続いているせいで、牧草が全てやられてしまった。","romaji":"Hyoutenka no hibi ga tsuzuite iru sei de, bokusou ga subete yararete shimatta.","german":"Because of the days of sub-zero temperatures, all the pasture grass has been killed.","furigana":"ひょうてんか|ひび|つづ|ぼくそう|すべ"},{"japanese":"牛たちが牧草をのんびり食べている。","romaji":"Ushitachi ga bokusou o nonbiri tabete iru.","german":"The cows are leisurely eating the grass.","furigana":"うし|ぼくそう|た"},{"japanese":"冬に備えて、牧草を刈って乾かしておく。","romaji":"Fuyu ni sonaete, bokusou o katte kawakashite oku.","german":"To prepare for winter, we cut the pasture grass and let it dry.","furigana":"ふゆ|そな|ぼくそう|か|かわ"}],"notes":"牧草 is \"pasture grass, forage grass\" that livestock eat or that is harvested as hay. Typical: 牧草を食べる, 牧草を刈る, 牧草地 (\"pasture, meadow\"). Dried, it is called 干し草. Unlike 芝 or 芝生 (lawn for looking at and walking on), 牧草 serves as fodder.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今年は年賀状何枚刷ればいいかなあ？","romaji":"Kotoshi wa nengajou nanmai sureba ii ka naa?","german":"How many New Year's cards should we print this year?","furigana":"ことし|ねんがじょうなんまいす"},{"japanese":"このポスターを百枚刷ってもらえますか。","romaji":"Kono posutaa o hyakumai sutte moraemasu ka.","german":"Could you print a hundred copies of this poster for us?","furigana":"ひゃくまいす"},{"japanese":"この印刷所では、一晩で十万部の新聞を刷っている。","romaji":"Kono insatsujo de wa, hitoban de juuman bu no shinbun o sutte iru.","german":"This print shop prints a hundred thousand copies of newspapers in a single night.","furigana":"いんさつじょ|ひとばん|じゅうまんぶ|しんぶん|す"}],"notes":"刷る is transitive and means \"to print\": 年賀状を刷る, ポスターを百枚刷る. It is a godan verb (刷って, 刷らない) and sounds like する (\"to do\") or 擦る (\"to rub\"), so it is almost always written with kanji. In everyday speech and among printers, 印刷する is usually said; 刷る is shorter and typical of print shops, woodblock printing and compounds like 刷り上がる (\"to be finished printing\") or 増刷 (\"reprint\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼はかつては明朗快活な青年だった。","romaji":"Kare wa katsute wa meirō kaikatsu na seinen datta.","german":"He was once a cheerful young man.","furigana":"かれ|めいろうかいかつ|せいねん"},{"japanese":"彼女は明朗な性格で、職場の誰からも好かれている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa meirou na seikaku de, shokuba no dare kara mo sukarete iru.","german":"She has a cheerful nature and is liked by all her colleagues.","furigana":"かのじょ|めいろう|せいかく|しょくば|だれ|す"},{"japanese":"当店は明朗会計ですので、どうぞ安心してお飲みください。","romaji":"Touten wa meirou kaikei desu node, douzo anshin shite onomi kudasai.","german":"We have transparent prices, so you can drink without worry.","furigana":"とうてん|めいろうかいけい|あんしん|の"}],"notes":"明朗 has two meanings: \"cheerful, bright\" as a character trait (明朗な性格, often in the four-character word 明朗快活) and \"clear, transparent, honest\" (明朗会計 \"transparent, fair billing\", 明朗な政治). It is written-language and often appears in references or job advertisements; in conversation one rather says 明るい. As a na-adjective: 明朗な, 明朗だ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"朗報を伝えるためにここに来ました。","romaji":"Rōhō o tsutaeru tame ni koko ni kimashita.","german":"I came here to bring you good news.","furigana":"ろうほう|つた|き"},{"japanese":"息子から大学合格の朗報が届いた。","romaji":"Musuko kara daigaku goukaku no rouhou ga todoita.","german":"Good news arrived from my son that he got into university.","furigana":"むすこ|だいがくごうかく|ろうほう|とど"},{"japanese":"電車の運賃が下がるのは、通勤客にとって朗報だ。","romaji":"Densha no unchin ga sagaru no wa, tsuukinkyaku ni totte rouhou da.","german":"The fall in train fares is good news for commuters.","furigana":"でんしゃ|うんちん|さ|つうきんきゃく|ろうほう"}],"notes":"朗報 is \"good, welcome news\" and sounds more refined than いい知らせ. Typical: 朗報が届く, 朗報を伝える, 朗報を待つ, 〜にとって朗報だ. Common in the media and advertising, for example when something is advantageous for a target group. The opposite is 悲報 (\"sad news\"), which is also used jokingly on the internet.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"荒い潮流に逆らってボートを漕ぐのは簡単ではないが、コテージに戻るにはそれしか方法がない。","romaji":"Arai chouryuu ni sakaratte booto o kogu no wa kantan de wa nai ga, koteeji ni modoru ni wa sore shika houhou ga nai.","german":"Rowing the boat against the rough current is not easy, but it's the only way to get back to the cottage.","furigana":"あら|ちょうりゅう|さか|こ|かんたん|もど|ほうほう"},{"japanese":"この海峡は潮流が速く、船の事故が多い。","romaji":"Kono kaikyou wa chouryuu ga hayaku, fune no jiko ga ooi.","german":"The current in this strait is fast, and there are many shipping accidents.","furigana":"かいきょう|ちょうりゅう|はや|ふね|じこ|おお"},{"japanese":"会社が生き残るには、時代の潮流を読む力が必要だ。","romaji":"Kaisha ga ikinokoru ni wa, jidai no chouryuu o yomu chikara ga hitsuyou da.","german":"For a company to survive, it needs the ability to read the trends of the times.","furigana":"かいしゃ|い|のこ|じだい|ちょうりゅう|よ|ちから|ひつよう"}],"notes":"潮流 literally means the \"tidal current\" in the sea: 潮流が速い, 潮流に逆らう. Even more often it is used figuratively for the \"trend, current\" of an era: 時代の潮流, 世界の潮流に乗る/逆らう. In this sense it is more refined than トレンド or 流れ and typical of newspapers and speeches. In everyday speech, for the ocean current people tend to say 潮の流れ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"すごく可愛いテディーベアを見つけたので、即買いしちゃいました。","romaji":"Sugoku kawaii tedībea o mitsuketa node, sokugai shichaimashita.","german":"I found a super cute teddy bear, so I bought it on the spot.","furigana":"かわい|み|そくが"},{"japanese":"彼は社長の提案に即答した。","romaji":"Kare wa shachō no teian ni sokutō shita.","german":"He answered the president's proposal right away.","furigana":"かれ|しゃちょう|ていあん|そくとう"},{"japanese":"メールを送ったら、即返事が来た。","romaji":"Mēru o okuttara, soku henji ga kita.","german":"When I sent the email, a reply came right away.","furigana":"おく|そくへんじ|き"}],"notes":"即 is a short prefix or adverb meaning \"immediately, on the spot\". As a prefix it forms fixed words such as 即答, 即決 or 即死 and colloquial coinages like 即買い or 即レス. As a free-standing adverb (即返事が来た), 即 sounds casual and informal; neutral expressions are すぐ or すぐに, formal ones 直ちに or 即座に. Do not confuse it with the literary conjunction 即ち \"namely\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"即座の結果は期待しないでください。","romaji":"Sokuza no kekka wa kitai shinaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't expect immediate results.","furigana":"そくざ|けっか|きたい"},{"japanese":"面接官の質問に、彼女は即座に答えた。","romaji":"Mensetsukan no shitsumon ni, kanojo wa sokuza ni kotaeta.","german":"She answered the interviewer's question without hesitation.","furigana":"めんせつかん|しつもん|かのじょ|そくざ|こた"},{"japanese":"無理なお願いをしたら、即座に断られてしまった。","romaji":"Muri na onegai o shitara, sokuza ni kotowararete shimatta.","german":"When I made an unreasonable request, I was refused on the spot.","furigana":"むり|ねが|そくざ|ことわ"}],"notes":"即座 is used almost only adverbially as 即座に (\"immediately, on the spot\"): 即座に答える, 即座に断る, 即座に反応する. Less often it appears attributively as 即座の (即座の判断, 即座の対応). It stresses a reaction without any hesitation and is somewhat more formal than すぐに; even more formal is ただちに. For technical processes, 即時 is preferred.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は潮津波に乗ってサーフィンをしようとしたが、残念ながら即死した。","romaji":"Kare wa shiotsunami ni notte sāfin o shiyō to shita ga, zannen nagara sokushi shita.","german":"He tried to surf on a tidal bore, but unfortunately he died instantly.","furigana":"かれ|しおつなみ|の|ざんねん|そくし"},{"japanese":"トラックと衝突した車の運転手は即死だった。","romaji":"Torakku to shōtotsu shita kuruma no untenshu wa sokushi datta.","german":"The driver of the car that collided with the truck died instantly.","furigana":"しょうとつ|くるま|うんてんしゅ|そくし"},{"japanese":"ヘルメットをかぶっていなかったら、即死していたかもしれない。","romaji":"Herumetto o kabutte inakattara, sokushi shite ita ka mo shirenai.","german":"If he hadn't been wearing a helmet, he might have died on the spot.","furigana":"そくし"}],"notes":"即死 means \"to be dead on the spot, instant death\" and appears almost only in reports about accidents, attacks or disasters. As an intransitive する-verb: 運転手は即死した, often also as a noun: 即死だった. The neutral news word for \"death\" is 死亡; the counterpart for survivors is 重傷を負う \"to be seriously injured\". The tone is matter-of-fact and harsh, typical of police and press language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この回転肉焼き器は、ご注文をいただいたら即日発送いたします。","romaji":"Kono kaiten nikuyakiki wa, gochuumon o itadaitara sokujitsu hassou itashimasu.","german":"We ship this rotisserie grill on the same day we receive your order.","furigana":"かいてんにくや|き|ちゅうもん|そくじつはっそう"},{"japanese":"選挙の結果は即日開票され、夜にはほぼ判明した。","romaji":"Senkyo no kekka wa sokujitsu kaihyou sare, yoru ni wa hobo hanmei shita.","german":"The election votes were counted on the same day, and the result was mostly clear by evening.","furigana":"せんきょ|けっか|そくじつかいひょう|よる|はんめい"},{"japanese":"この部屋は空いているので、即日入居も可能です。","romaji":"Kono heya wa aite iru node, sokujitsu nyuukyo mo kanou desu.","german":"This room is vacant, so you can move in right away.","furigana":"へや|あ|そくじつにゅうきょ|かのう"}],"notes":"即日 means \"on the very same day\" and usually stands without a particle before a Sino-Japanese verbal noun: 即日発送 (\"shipping on the day of ordering\"), 即日開票 (\"vote count on election day\"), 即日入居可 (\"available for immediate move-in\"). Typical of business and official language. More neutral is 当日 (\"on that day\"); in conversation people say その日のうちに.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"即効で目の疲れを取る方法をお教えしましょう。","romaji":"Sokkou de me no tsukare o toru houhou o ooshie shimashou.","german":"Let me show you a way to relieve eye strain right away.","furigana":"そっこう|め|つか|と|ほうほう|おし"},{"japanese":"この頭痛薬は即効性があるので、飲んで十分ほどで効いてくる。","romaji":"Kono zutsuuyaku wa sokkousei ga aru node, nonde juppun hodo de kiite kuru.","german":"This headache medicine is fast-acting; it starts working about ten minutes after you take it.","furigana":"ずつうやく|そっこうせい|の|じゅっぷん|き"},{"japanese":"肥料には即効のものと、ゆっくり効くものがある。","romaji":"Hiryou ni wa sokkou no mono to, yukkuri kiku mono ga aru.","german":"Some fertilizers act immediately, and others act slowly.","furigana":"ひりょう|そっこう|き"}],"notes":"即効 is an \"immediate effect\", especially of medicines and remedies: 即効性がある, 即効薬. Colloquially 即効で is also used as \"immediately, lightning fast\"; for this, 速攻 (same reading, literally \"lightning attack\" in sports) is often written. The opposite is 遅効性 (\"slow-acting\"). Very common in advertising and advice guides: 即効で効く, 即効ダイエット.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"隣の家との境に、低い垣根を作った。","romaji":"Tonari no ie to no sakai ni, hikui kakine o tsukutta.","german":"We built a low hedge on the boundary with the neighbor's house.","furigana":"となり|いえ|さかい|ひく|かきね|つく"},{"japanese":"垣根越しに、お隣さんとしばらく立ち話をした。","romaji":"Kakine goshi ni, otonari-san to shibaraku tachibanashi o shita.","german":"I chatted with the neighbor over the fence for a while.","furigana":"かきねご|となり|た|ばなし"},{"japanese":"専門分野の垣根を越えて、研究者たちが協力している。","romaji":"Senmon bun'ya no kakine o koete, kenkyuushatachi ga kyouryoku shite iru.","german":"Researchers are collaborating across the boundaries of their fields.","furigana":"せんもんぶんや|かきね|こ|けんきゅうしゃ|きょうりょく"}],"notes":"垣根 is an enclosure made of plants, bamboo or wood around a house and garden – a \"hedge, fence\": 垣根を作る, 垣根越しに話す. A wall of stone or concrete is called 塀, a metal fence フェンス. Figuratively, 垣根 means a \"barrier, divide\" between groups or fields: 分野の垣根を越える, 垣根を取り払う.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お城の周りには、昔の高い石垣が今も残っている。","romaji":"Oshiro no mawari ni wa, mukashi no takai ishigaki ga ima mo nokotte iru.","german":"Around the castle, the tall stone walls of old still remain today.","furigana":"しろ|まわ|むかし|たか|いしがき|いま|のこ"},{"japanese":"地震で石垣の一部が崩れてしまった。","romaji":"Jishin de ishigaki no ichibu ga kuzurete shimatta.","german":"Part of the stone wall collapsed in the earthquake.","furigana":"じしん|いしがき|いちぶ|くず"},{"japanese":"古い石垣の上で、猫が気持ちよさそうに昼寝している。","romaji":"Furui ishigaki no ue de, neko ga kimochi yosasou ni hirune shite iru.","german":"A cat is taking a pleasant nap on top of the old stone wall.","furigana":"ふる|いしがき|うえ|ねこ|きも|ひるね"}],"notes":"石垣 is a \"stone wall\" stacked from natural stones, often without mortar. Typical are the mighty castle walls (城の石垣) and the retaining walls of terraced fields. You say 石垣を積む (\"to stack\") and 石垣が崩れる (\"to collapse\"). A smooth wall around a property is more often called 塀. As a place name, the island 石垣島 in Okinawa is well known.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"野球のシーズンが到来する春に近づくにつれて、あの投手の球威は益々増しています。","romaji":"Yakyuu no shiizun ga tourai suru haru ni chikazuku ni tsurete, ano toushu no kyuui wa masumasu mashite imasu.","german":"As spring and the baseball season approach, that pitcher's pitching power keeps increasing.","furigana":"やきゅう|とうらい|はる|ちか|とうしゅ|きゅうい|ますますま"},{"japanese":"九回になっても、彼の球威は全く落ちなかった。","romaji":"Kyuukai ni natte mo, kare no kyuui wa mattaku ochinakatta.","german":"Even in the ninth inning, the force of his pitches did not drop at all.","furigana":"きゅうかい|かれ|きゅうい|まった|お"},{"japanese":"球速はそれほどでもないが、球威のあるピッチャーだ。","romaji":"Kyuusoku wa sore hodo demo nai ga, kyuui no aru picchaa da.","german":"His speed isn't that great, but he's a pitcher whose balls have real power.","furigana":"きゅうそく|きゅうい"}],"notes":"球威 is a baseball term for the \"power, punch\" of a pitch – how heavy and forceful the ball feels when it reaches the batter. Typical: 球威がある, 球威が落ちる/衰える, 球威が増す. It is not the same as 球速 (the measurable speed in km/h): a pitcher can throw fast and still have little 球威. The word appears mainly in sports reports.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"開封前の商品であればいつでも返品可って書いてあるけど、開封後に中身が壊れてたり、その商品が好きじゃなかった場合はどうなんだろう。","romaji":"Kaifuu mae no shouhin de areba itsu demo henpin ka tte kaite aru kedo, kaifuugo ni nakami ga kowarete tari, sono shouhin ga suki ja nakatta baai wa dou na n darou.","german":"It says that unopened products can be returned at any time, but what if the contents are broken after opening, or I don't like the product?","furigana":"かいふうまえ|しょうひん|へんぴんか|か|かいふうご|なかみ|こわ|しょうひん|す|ばあい"},{"japanese":"他人宛ての手紙を勝手に開封してはいけません。","romaji":"Tanin ate no tegami o katte ni kaifuu shite wa ikemasen.","german":"You must not open letters addressed to other people without permission.","furigana":"たにんあ|てがみ|かって|かいふう"},{"japanese":"開封後は冷蔵庫に入れて、お早めにお召し上がりください。","romaji":"Kaifuugo wa reizouko ni irete, ohayame ni omeshiagari kudasai.","german":"After opening, please refrigerate and consume soon.","furigana":"かいふうご|れいぞうこ|い|はや|め|あ"}],"notes":"開封 means to \"open\" something sealed or closed – letters, packages, products: 手紙を開封する, 開封後. Typical on packaging: 開封後はお早めにお召し上がりください (\"consume soon after opening\"). The opposite term is 未開封 (\"unopened\"); 開封済み means \"already opened\". In everyday speech people just say 開ける; 開封 is more matter-of-fact and formal.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"契約書にサインをして封筒に入れてありますが、まだその封書を郵送していません。","romaji":"Keiyakusho ni sain o shite fūtō ni irete arimasu ga, mada sono fūsho o yūsō shite imasen.","german":"I signed the contract and put it in an envelope, but I haven't mailed the sealed letter yet.","furigana":"けいやくしょ|ふうとう|い|ふうしょ|ゆうそう"},{"japanese":"申込書ははがきではなく、封書で送ってください。","romaji":"Moushikomisho wa hagaki de wa naku, fuusho de okutte kudasai.","german":"Please send the application form in a sealed envelope, not on a postcard.","furigana":"もうしこみしょ|ふうしょ|おく"},{"japanese":"銀行から重要な封書が届いたので、すぐに開けてみた。","romaji":"Ginkou kara juuyou na fuusho ga todoita node, sugu ni akete mita.","german":"An important letter arrived from the bank, so I opened it right away.","furigana":"ぎんこう|じゅうよう|ふうしょ|とど|あ"}],"notes":"封書 is a letter in a sealed envelope – the postal term as opposed to the open postcard はがき. Typical are 封書で送る and 封書が届く; government offices, banks and companies deliberately send important notices as 封書. The envelope itself is 封筒, and the letter as content is generally 手紙. The word is rather matter-of-fact and written; in everyday speech people usually just say 手紙.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日は歴史の授業で封建主義について学びました。","romaji":"Kyō wa rekishi no jugyō de hōken shugi nitsuite manabimashita.","german":"Today in history class we learned about feudalism.","furigana":"きょう|れきし|じゅぎょう|ほうけんしゅぎ|まな"},{"japanese":"封建主義の社会では、生まれた身分によって職業が決まっていた。","romaji":"Houken shugi no shakai de wa, umareta mibun ni yotte shokugyou ga kimatte ita.","german":"In a feudal society, the class one was born into determined one's occupation.","furigana":"ほうけんしゅぎ|しゃかい|う|みぶん|しょくぎょう|き"},{"japanese":"うちの会社は上下関係が厳しくて、まるで封建主義の時代みたいだ。","romaji":"Uchi no kaisha wa jouge kankei ga kibishikute, marude houken shugi no jidai mitai da.","german":"Our company has such a strict hierarchy that it's as if we still lived under feudalism.","furigana":"かいしゃ|じょうげかんけい|きび|ほうけんしゅぎ|じだい"}],"notes":"封建主義 refers to feudalism as a social order, in Japan mainly the period from the Kamakura to the Edo period. Closely related are 封建制度 (\"feudal system\") and 封建時代 (\"feudal era\"). In daily life one hears more often the adjective 封建的, with which one pejoratively criticizes rigid hierarchies and old-fashioned ways of thinking, e.g. 封建的な考え方. The word belongs to history, politics and social criticism.","levelNote":"Compound: 封建 (N1) + 主義 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"保険会社から受け取る慰謝料は、税務署に申告する必要がありますか。","romaji":"Hoken kaisha kara uketoru isharyō wa, zeimusho ni shinkoku suru hitsuyō ga arimasu ka.","german":"Do I need to declare the compensation I receive from the insurance company to the tax office?","furigana":"ほけんかいしゃ|う|と|いしゃりょう|ぜいむしょ|しんこく|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"離婚の際、夫は妻に慰謝料を支払うことになった。","romaji":"Rikon no sai, otto wa tsuma ni isharyou o shiharau koto ni natta.","german":"In the divorce, the husband was required to pay his wife compensation.","furigana":"りこん|さい|おっと|つま|いしゃりょう|しはら"},{"japanese":"事故の被害者は、相手の運転手に慰謝料を請求した。","romaji":"Jiko no higaisha wa, aite no untenshu ni isharyou o seikyuu shita.","german":"The accident victim demanded compensation from the other driver.","furigana":"じこ|ひがいしゃ|あいて|うんてんしゅ|いしゃりょう|せいきゅう"}],"notes":"慰謝 actually means \"consolation, comfort\", but today it appears almost only in 慰謝料: money as compensation for emotional suffering, i.e. \"compensation for pain and suffering\" or damages in cases of divorce, accidents or defamation. Typical are 慰謝料を請求する, 慰謝料を支払う and 慰謝料をもらう. An older spelling is 慰藉. Do not confuse it with the identically sounding 医者 \"doctor\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私達は毎週日曜日、セラピー犬たちと一緒に老人ホームを慰問します。","romaji":"Watashitachi wa maishū nichiyōbi, serapī inutachi to issho ni rōjin hōmu o imon shimasu.","german":"Every Sunday we visit a nursing home with therapy dogs.","furigana":"わたしたち|まいしゅうにちようび|いぬ|いっしょ|ろうじん|いもん"},{"japanese":"首相は地震の被災地を慰問し、避難所の人々を励ました。","romaji":"Shushou wa jishin no hisaichi o imon shi, hinanjo no hitobito o hagemashita.","german":"The prime minister visited the earthquake-stricken area and encouraged the people in the shelters.","furigana":"しゅしょう|じしん|ひさいち|いもん|ひなんじょ|ひとびと|はげ"},{"japanese":"子どもたちは入院中の患者に慰問の手紙を書いた。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa nyuuinchuu no kanja ni imon no tegami o kaita.","german":"The children wrote get-well letters to the patients in the hospital.","furigana":"こ|にゅういんちゅう|かんじゃ|いもん|てがみ|か"}],"notes":"慰問する means to visit people in a difficult situation in order to comfort and cheer them up – for example the sick, seniors, disaster victims or soldiers. It is transitive: 被災地を慰問する, 病院を慰問する. Compounds like 慰問団, 慰問品 and 慰問の手紙 are common. Unlike 見舞い, which one does privately for acquaintances, 慰問 sounds more official and is often said of groups, celebrities or the imperial couple.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"うちの会社では、毎年強制参加の慰安旅行があります。","romaji":"Uchi no kaisha de wa, maitoshi kyōsei sanka no ian ryokō ga arimasu.","german":"At my company there's a mandatory recreational trip every year.","furigana":"かいしゃ|まいとしきょうせいさんか|いあんりょこう"},{"japanese":"社員の慰安のために、会社が温泉旅行を計画した。","romaji":"Shain no ian no tame ni, kaisha ga onsen ryokou o keikaku shita.","german":"For the employees' recreation, the company planned a trip to a hot spring.","furigana":"しゃいん|いあん|かいしゃ|おんせんりょこう|けいかく"},{"japanese":"老人ホームで慰安会が開かれ、地元の子どもたちが歌を歌った。","romaji":"Roujin houmu de iankai ga hirakare, jimoto no kodomotachi ga uta o utatta.","german":"An entertainment event was held at the nursing home, and local children sang songs.","furigana":"ろうじん|いあんかい|ひら|じもと|こ|うた|うた"}],"notes":"慰安 means \"recreation, diversion\" and almost always appears in compounds: 慰安旅行 is the classic company outing, 慰安会 an entertainment event for employees or seniors. The reason is given as 社員の慰安のために. Caution: 慰安婦 refers to the women forced into prostitution during World War II and is a politically sensitive word. For personal relaxation, everyday speech prefers 息抜き or 気分転換.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"来る10月22日に、マンションの住人の皆様との懇話会を予定しております。","romaji":"Kitaru 10gatsu 22nichi ni, manshon no jūnin no minasama to no konwakai o yotei shite orimasu.","german":"On October 22, we are planning a social gathering with the residents of the apartment building.","furigana":"きた|がつ|にち|じゅうにん|みなさま|こんわかい|よてい"},{"japanese":"市長は地元の若者と懇話会を開き、町の将来について意見を聞いた。","romaji":"Shichō wa jimoto no wakamono to konwakai o hiraki, machi no shōrai ni tsuite iken o kiita.","german":"The mayor held a discussion with local young people and heard their opinions about the town's future.","furigana":"しちょう|じもと|わかもの|こんわかい|ひら|まち|しょうらい|いけん|き"},{"japanese":"会議の後、先生方と和やかに懇話する時間がありました。","romaji":"Kaigi no ato, senseigata to nagoyaka ni konwa suru jikan ga arimashita.","german":"After the meeting, we had time for a relaxed chat with the teachers.","furigana":"かいぎ|あと|せんせいがた|なご|こんわ|じかん"}],"notes":"懇話 is a refined word for an informal, friendly conversation. On its own it is rare; almost always it appears in 懇話会 \"discussion group, social gathering\", for example of government offices, associations or property managers with residents. In everyday speech people say 雑談 or おしゃべり instead. Similarly built is 懇談会, which sounds somewhat more official (e.g. a talk between parents and teachers).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"飲み放題のパーティーを通じてチームメイトとの懇親を深めました。","romaji":"Nomihōdai no pātī o tsūjite chīmumeito to no konshin o fukamemashita.","german":"Through parties with all-you-can-drink, I deepened my friendship with my teammates.","furigana":"の|ほうだい|つう|こんしん|ふか"},{"japanese":"学会の後、ホテルで懇親会が開かれた。","romaji":"Gakkai no ato, hoteru de konshinkai ga hirakareta.","german":"After the academic conference, a social gathering was held at the hotel.","furigana":"がっかい|あと|こんしんかい|ひら"},{"japanese":"新入社員の懇親会に参加しませんか。","romaji":"Shinnyū shain no konshinkai ni sanka shimasen ka.","german":"Won't you join the get-together for new employees?","furigana":"しんにゅうしゃいん|こんしんかい|さんか"}],"notes":"懇親 refers to friendly, sociable interaction to get to know each other better. It almost always appears in fixed combinations: 懇親会 \"social gathering, get-together\" after conferences or in companies, and 懇親を深める \"to deepen relations\". It is not used for \"friendship\" in general – for that, people say 友情 or 親しさ. The tone is formal; 懇親会 is the usual term on invitations.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先週、市長は色々な職業の人々との懇談を行いました。","romaji":"Senshū, shichō wa iroiro na shokugyō no hitobito to no kondan o okonaimashita.","german":"Last week, the mayor held informal talks with people of various occupations.","furigana":"せんしゅう|しちょう|いろいろ|しょくぎょう|ひとびと|こんだん|おこな"},{"japanese":"来週、学校で保護者懇談会があります。","romaji":"Raishuu, gakkou de hogosha kondankai ga arimasu.","german":"Next week there is a parent-teacher meeting at school.","furigana":"らいしゅう|がっこう|ほごしゃこんだんかい"},{"japanese":"社長は新入社員と昼食をとりながら懇談した。","romaji":"Shachou wa shinnyuu shain to chuushoku o torinagara kondan shita.","german":"The company president chatted informally with the new employees over lunch.","furigana":"しゃちょう|しんにゅうしゃいん|ちゅうしょく|こんだん"}],"notes":"懇談 is a relaxed, open conversation, often between groups that otherwise do not talk directly. As an intransitive する-verb, the partner takes と: 市民と懇談する. Very common is 懇談会, e.g. 保護者懇談会 (\"parents' meeting\") or 三者懇談 (teacher, parents and student). Unlike 会議 (\"meeting, conference\"), it is not about decisions; unlike 雑談 (\"chatting\"), it has an official setting.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女に三度も振られてるっていうのに、お前まだ懲りてないのか。","romaji":"Kanojo ni sando mo furareteru tte iu noni, omae mada koritenai no ka.","german":"You've been turned down by her three times already and still haven't learned your lesson?","furigana":"かのじょ|さんど|ふ|まえ|こ"},{"japanese":"前回の失敗に懲りて、今回は早めに準備を始めた。","romaji":"Zenkai no shippai ni korite, konkai wa hayame ni junbi o hajimeta.","german":"Wiser after the last failure, I started the preparations earlier this time.","furigana":"ぜんかい|しっぱい|こ|こんかい|はや|じゅんび|はじ"},{"japanese":"彼は何度注意されても、懲りずに遅刻してくる。","romaji":"Kare wa nando chuui sarete mo, korizu ni chikoku shite kuru.","german":"No matter how many times you warn him, he is incorrigible and keeps coming late.","furigana":"かれ|なんどちゅうい|こ|ちこく"}],"notes":"懲りる means to have become wise through a bad experience and not want to repeat something. The cause is marked with に: 失敗に懲りる. Very common are the negatives まだ懲りていない (\"still hasn't learned\") and 懲りずに (\"incorrigibly, yet again\") as well as 懲り懲りだ (\"to be sick and tired of it\"). It is intransitive; \"to teach someone a lesson\" is 懲らしめる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"違反者には懲罰が与えられる。","romaji":"Ihansha ni wa choubatsu ga ataerareru.","german":"Violators will be punished.","furigana":"いはんしゃ|ちょうばつ|あた"},{"japanese":"国会で問題発言をした議員が懲罰を受けた。","romaji":"Kokkai de mondai hatsugen o shita giin ga choubatsu o uketa.","german":"The lawmaker who made a problematic remark in the Diet was disciplined.","furigana":"こっかい|もんだいはつげん|ぎいん|ちょうばつ|う"},{"japanese":"その選手は懲罰として三試合の出場停止になった。","romaji":"Sono senshu wa choubatsu to shite san shiai no shutsujou teishi ni natta.","german":"As punishment, the player was suspended for three games.","furigana":"せんしゅ|ちょうばつ|さんしあい|しゅつじょうていし"}],"notes":"懲罰 is a disciplinary punishment within an organization, such as in parliament, in prison, in the military or in sports. It is mostly used as a noun: 懲罰を受ける, 懲罰を与える, 懲罰として; also 懲罰委員会 and 懲罰動議. The general word for \"to punish\" is 罰する or 処罰する; legal penalties are called 刑罰. The word is formal and appears mainly in news and regulations.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"生徒達は一斉に温度計に目をやりました。","romaji":"Seitotachi wa issei ni ondokei ni me o yarimashita.","german":"The students all looked at the thermometer at the same time.","furigana":"せいとたち|いっせい|おんどけい|め"},{"japanese":"試験終了のベルが鳴ると、受験者は一斉にペンを置いた。","romaji":"Shiken shuuryou no beru ga naru to, jukensha wa issei ni pen o oita.","german":"When the bell signaling the end of the exam rang, all the examinees put down their pens at once.","furigana":"しけんしゅうりょう|な|じゅけんしゃ|いっせい|お"},{"japanese":"台風の接近で、市内の小中学校が一斉休校になった。","romaji":"Taifuu no sekkin de, shinai no shouchuugakkou ga issei kyuukou ni natta.","german":"Because of the approaching typhoon, all elementary and junior high schools in the city were closed.","furigana":"たいふう|せっきん|しない|しょうちゅうがっこう|いっせいきゅうこう"}],"notes":"一斉 is almost always used as the adverb 一斉に: many people or things do the same thing at the same time, as if on command. Unlike 同時に, which can link just two events, 一斉に emphasizes the large number. As a prefix it forms compounds like 一斉休校, 一斉送信 (\"mass mailing\") and 一斉検挙 (\"roundup, sweep\"). The word is neutral and common in the news as well as in daily life.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"めっちゃ旨いお好み焼きの店見つけたから、今度一緒に行かへん？","romaji":"Metcha umai okonomiyaki no mise mitsuketa kara, kondo issho ni ikahen?","german":"I found a place with really delicious okonomiyaki. Want to go together sometime?","furigana":"うま|この|や|みせみ|こんどいっしょ|い"},{"japanese":"旨い料理でも、食べすぎないようにしています。","romaji":"Umai ryōri demo, tabesuginai yō ni shite imasu.","german":"Even with delicious food, I try not to eat too much.","furigana":"うま|りょうり|た"}],"notes":"旨い here means \"delicious\" and can also be written うまい. The meaning \"skillful\" belongs to the card 上手い. As an i-adjective, 旨い stands directly before a noun and forms, for example, 旨くない and 旨かった. In the first example, めっちゃ is a colloquial intensifier; 行かへん？ is a form from Kansai speech and serves here as an invitation, similar to the standard 一緒に行かない？. After 店 the object marker を is omitted in casual conversation. In the second example, でも means \"even with\"; 〜ないようにしています describes a behavior kept up deliberately. For the meaning \"delicious\", 甘い with the reading うまい is also attested. Without such a reading hint, 甘い is usually read あまい and then means \"sweet\". For this card, うまい remains the reading; the spelling 旨い or the kana form うまい makes the intended meaning easier to recognize.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あそこで柔和な笑みを浮かべている理髪師は、実はこの辺りのポン引き男どもの親分なんだ。","romaji":"Asoko de nyuuwa na emi o ukabete iru rihatsushi wa, jitsu wa kono atari no ponbiki otokodomo no oyabun na n da.","german":"The barber over there with the gentle smile is actually the boss of the pimps in this area.","furigana":"にゅうわ|え|う|りはつし|じつ|あた|び|おとこ|おやぶん"},{"japanese":"祖父はいつも柔和な表情で孫たちを見守っている。","romaji":"Sofu wa itsumo nyuuwa na hyoujou de magotachi o mimamotte iru.","german":"Grandfather always watches over his grandchildren with a gentle expression.","furigana":"そふ|にゅうわ|ひょうじょう|まご|みまも"},{"japanese":"新しい校長は柔和な人柄で、生徒にも人気がある。","romaji":"Atarashii kouchou wa nyuuwa na hitogara de, seito ni mo ninki ga aru.","german":"The new principal has a gentle personality and is popular with the students too.","furigana":"あたら|こうちょう|にゅうわ|ひとがら|せいと|にんき"}],"notes":"柔和 (reading にゅうわ, not じゅうわ) describes a gentle, mild nature or appearance. As a な-adjective, it typically goes with 顔, 表情, 笑み and 人柄: 柔和な笑顔. It does not fit soft things or materials; for that, use 柔らかい. Compared with 優しい and 穏やか, 柔和 sounds more literary and emphasizes the friendly expression one shows outwardly.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"沼田を裸足で歩いたことはありますか。","romaji":"Numata o hadashi de aruita koto wa arimasu ka.","german":"Have you ever walked barefoot through a marshy rice paddy?","furigana":"ぬまた|はだし|ある"},{"japanese":"この辺りの沼田は水がなかなか抜けず、田植えにも苦労する。","romaji":"Kono atari no numata wa mizu ga nakanaka nukezu, taue ni mo kurou suru.","german":"The marshy paddies around here drain poorly, so even rice planting is a struggle.","furigana":"あた|ぬまた|みず|ぬ|たう|くろう"},{"japanese":"昔は機械が入れない沼田で、腰まで泥につかって稲を刈ったそうだ。","romaji":"Mukashi wa kikai ga hairenai numata de, koshi made doro ni tsukatte ine o katta sou da.","german":"In the past, in marshy paddies where machines couldn't go, people reportedly harvested rice standing waist-deep in mud.","furigana":"むかし|きかい|はい|ぬまた|こし|どろ|いね|か"}],"notes":"沼田 is a technical term from agriculture for a poorly drained, deeply muddy rice field in which one sinks in above the knees; a technical term with the same meaning is 湿田. In everyday language it is rare – far more often you meet 沼田 as a family name or as a city in Gunma Prefecture. For a normal rice field, people say 田んぼ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この黄色い花を咲かせる植物は、沼沢地に生えています。","romaji":"Kono kiiroi hana o sakaseru shokubutsu wa, shoutakuchi ni haete imasu.","german":"This yellow-flowering plant grows in marshland.","furigana":"きいろ|はな|さ|しょくぶつ|しょうたくち|は"},{"japanese":"この地域には大きな沼沢地があり、冬には多くの渡り鳥が訪れる。","romaji":"Kono chiiki ni wa ookina shoutakuchi ga ari, fuyu ni wa ooku no wataridori ga otozureru.","german":"There is a large marshland in this area, which many migratory birds visit in winter.","furigana":"ちいき|おお|しょうたくち|ふゆ|おお|わた|どり|おとず"},{"japanese":"開発で沼沢が埋め立てられ、珍しい生き物が姿を消した。","romaji":"Kaihatsu de shoutaku ga umetaterare, mezurashii ikimono ga sugata o keshita.","german":"The marshes were filled in for development, and rare animals disappeared.","furigana":"かいはつ|しょうたく|う|た|めずら|い|もの|すがた|け"}],"notes":"沼沢 refers to swamp and bog land, i.e. areas with ponds and wet ground. It is a written technical word from geography and biology and usually appears in 沼沢地 (\"marshland\"). In everyday speech people tend to say 沼 (\"pond, marsh\"), 湿地 (\"wetland\") or 湿原 (\"moor\", e.g. 尾瀬). It is encountered more often in texts on nature conservation and plant or bird species.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの一族がこの先もずっと安泰だなんて、誰が信じられるだろう。","romaji":"Ano ichizoku ga kono saki mo zutto antai da nante, dare ga shinjirareru darou.","german":"Who could believe that that clan will remain secure forever?","furigana":"いちぞく|さき|あんたい|だれ|しん"},{"japanese":"大企業に就職したからといって、一生安泰とは限らない。","romaji":"Daikigyou ni shuushoku shita kara to itte, isshou antai to wa kagiranai.","german":"Just because you got a job at a big company doesn't mean you're secure for life.","furigana":"だいきぎょう|しゅうしょく|いっしょうあんたい|かぎ"},{"japanese":"新しい技術のおかげで、会社の経営はしばらく安泰だ。","romaji":"Atarashii gijutsu no okage de, kaisha no keiei wa shibaraku antai da.","german":"Thanks to the new technology, the company's management is stable for the time being.","furigana":"あたら|ぎじゅつ|かいしゃ|けいえい|あんたい"}],"notes":"安泰 describes a secure, stable state with no danger of decline – of states, companies, families, positions or one's own future: 国家の安泰, 会社は安泰だ, 一生安泰. As a な-adjective or noun, it is mostly used predicatively with だ or in 安泰とは限らない. It differs from 安全 (protection from a concrete danger) and 平和 (peace, no war). In conversation, これで老後も安泰だ sounds half jocular.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"奴らはメディアで情報操作を行って、日本を滅ぼそうとしているんだ。","romaji":"Yatsura wa media de jōhō sōsa o okonatte, Nippon o horobosō to shite iru n da.","german":"They are trying to destroy Japan through media manipulation.","furigana":"やつ|じょうほうそうさ|おこな|にっぽん|ほろ"},{"japanese":"織田信長は多くの大名を滅ぼした。","romaji":"Oda Nobunaga wa ōku no daimyō o horoboshita.","german":"Oda Nobunaga destroyed many feudal houses.","furigana":"|おお|だいみょう|ほろ"},{"japanese":"ギャンブルで身を滅ぼした人を何人も知っている。","romaji":"Gyanburu de mi o horoboshita hito o nannin mo shitte iru.","german":"I know several people who ruined themselves through gambling.","furigana":"み|ほろ|ひと|なんにん|し"}],"notes":"滅ぼす is transitive: to \"destroy, to bring to ruin\" something or someone, typically 国を滅ぼす or 敵を滅ぼす. The intransitive partner is 滅びる \"to perish, to go extinct\" (ローマ帝国が滅びた). Figuratively 身を滅ぼす means \"to ruin oneself\", e.g. through gambling or alcohol. Unlike 壊す (to break things), 滅ぼす is about states, peoples, species or entire existences; the tone is rather written and dramatic.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"もしその超巨大火山が噴火をすれば、我々は滅亡を免れないだろう。","romaji":"Moshi sono chou kyodai kazan ga funka o sureba, wareware wa metsubou o manukarenai darou.","german":"If this supervolcano erupted, we probably wouldn't escape destruction.","furigana":"ちょうきょだいかざん|ふんか|われわれ|めつぼう|まぬか"},{"japanese":"平家は壇ノ浦の戦いで滅亡した。","romaji":"Heike wa Dannoura no tatakai de metsubou shita.","german":"The Taira clan perished in the Battle of Dan-no-ura.","furigana":"へいけ|だん|うら|たたか|めつぼう"},{"japanese":"ローマ帝国の滅亡について、レポートを書いている。","romaji":"Rouma teikoku no metsubou ni tsuite, repooto o kaite iru.","german":"I'm writing a term paper on the fall of the Roman Empire.","furigana":"ていこく|めつぼう|か"}],"notes":"滅亡 is the final downfall of a state, dynasty, clan, civilization or humanity: 帝国の滅亡, 人類の滅亡. As an intransitive suru-verb: 国が滅亡する. The native Japanese verb for it is 滅びる, transitive 滅ぼす (\"to destroy\"). For the extinction of animal species one uses 絶滅 instead. Also typical are 滅亡を免れる and 滅亡に追い込まれる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その都市は原爆により壊滅しました。","romaji":"Sono toshi wa genbaku ni yori kaimetsu shimashita.","german":"The city was destroyed by the atomic bomb.","furigana":"とし|げんばく|かいめつ"},{"japanese":"大地震で、海沿いの町は壊滅的な被害を受けた。","romaji":"Daijishin de, umizoi no machi wa kaimetsuteki na higai o uketa.","german":"The seaside town suffered devastating damage in the great earthquake.","furigana":"だいじしん|うみぞ|まち|かいめつてき|ひがい|う"},{"japanese":"警察は長い捜査の末、その犯罪組織を壊滅させた。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa nagai sousa no sue, sono hanzai soshiki o kaimetsu saseta.","german":"After a long investigation, the police wiped out the criminal organization.","furigana":"けいさつ|なが|そうさ|すえ|はんざいそしき|かいめつ"}],"notes":"壊滅 is complete destruction, after which practically nothing is left – for example through war, earthquake or tsunami. It is intransitive: 町が壊滅する; \"to annihilate something\" is 壊滅させる. Very common is the adjective 壊滅的: 壊滅的な被害 (\"devastating damage\"), colloquially also exaggerated as in 数学の成績が壊滅的だ. Unlike 滅亡 (the fall of a state), 壊滅 emphasizes the degree of destruction; unlike 破壊, it does not emphasize the act of destroying.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼氏が道端で立ちションしてるのを見ちゃってさ。もう、完璧に幻滅した。","romaji":"Kareshi ga michibata de tachishon shiteru no o michatte sa. Mou, kanpeki ni genmetsu shita.","german":"I happened to see my boyfriend peeing on the street, and I was completely disillusioned.","furigana":"かれし|みちばた|た|み|かんぺき|げんめつ"},{"japanese":"憧れの作家に会ったら、態度が悪くて幻滅した。","romaji":"Akogare no sakka ni attara, taido ga warukute genmetsu shita.","german":"When I met the writer I admired, he was so rude that I was disillusioned.","furigana":"あこが|さっか|あ|たいど|わる|げんめつ"},{"japanese":"政治に幻滅して、選挙に行かなくなった若者も多い。","romaji":"Seiji ni genmetsu shite, senkyo ni ikanaku natta wakamono mo ooi.","german":"Many young people have become disillusioned with politics and no longer go to vote.","furigana":"せいじ|げんめつ|せんきょ|い|わかもの|おお"}],"notes":"幻滅 is the disillusionment felt when an idealized image shatters: you suddenly see the disappointing reality of a person or thing. The cause takes に: 政治に幻滅する; to disillusion someone is 幻滅させる. It is stronger than がっかりする, which covers any kind of disappointment, and applies above all to people one admired or loved. Very common in conversation as 幻滅した.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"風邪を引いたら、大豆だとか林檎だとか、滋養のある物を食べなさい。","romaji":"Kaze o hiitara, daizu da toka ringo da toka, jiyou no aru mono o tabenasai.","german":"When you catch a cold, eat nourishing food such as soybeans or apples.","furigana":"かぜ|ひ|だいず|りんご|じよう|もの|た"},{"japanese":"このスープは滋養に富んでいて、病み上がりの体にいい。","romaji":"Kono suupu wa jiyou ni tonde ite, yamiagari no karada ni ii.","german":"This soup is rich in nourishment and is good for a body that is just recovering from illness.","furigana":"じよう|と|や|あ|からだ"},{"japanese":"この栄養ドリンクには「滋養強壮」と書いてある。","romaji":"Kono eiyou dorinku ni wa \"jiyou kyousou\" to kaite aru.","german":"This energy drink says 「滋養強壮」 on it.","furigana":"えいよう|じようきょうそう|か"}],"notes":"滋養 means the nutritional value, the strengthening effect of food. Typical phrases are 滋養のある食べ物, 滋養に富む and 滋養をつける. Very well known is 滋養強壮 (\"strengthening and invigoration\"), which appears on the labels of energy drinks and tonics. The word sounds somewhat old-fashioned; in everyday speech people usually say 栄養. A single nutrient, on the other hand, is called 栄養素.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今朝の朝刊で、その鉄鋼会社は世界一大きな高炉の建設を計画していると読んだよ。","romaji":"Kesa no chōkan de, sono tekkō kaisha wa sekaiichi ōkina kōro no kensetsu o keikaku shite iru to yonda yo.","german":"I read in this morning's paper that the steel company is planning to build the world's largest blast furnace.","furigana":"けさ|ちょうかん|てっこうかいしゃ|せかいいちおお|こうろ|けんせつ|けいかく|よ"},{"japanese":"高炉では、鉄鉱石とコークスを使って鉄を作る。","romaji":"Kouro de wa, tekkouseki to kookusu o tsukatte tetsu o tsukuru.","german":"In a blast furnace, iron is made using iron ore and coke.","furigana":"こうろ|てっこうせき|つか|てつ|つく"},{"japanese":"環境対策のため、高炉から電炉に切り替える製鉄所が増えている。","romaji":"Kankyou taisaku no tame, kouro kara denro ni kirikaeru seitetsujo ga fuete iru.","german":"For environmental reasons, a growing number of steelworks are switching from blast furnaces to electric furnaces.","furigana":"かんきょうたいさく|こうろ|でんろ|き|か|せいてつじょ|ふ"}],"notes":"高炉 is the blast furnace in which pig iron is produced from iron ore and coke. The word comes from industrial and business news; 高炉メーカー are the large integrated steel companies. A more general term, also understood in everyday life, is 溶鉱炉. In contrast stands 電炉 (\"electric arc furnace\"), which melts scrap and is considered more climate-friendly.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"うちの子がおしっこ漏らしをしちゃって、ズボンを洗ったから、今それを暖炉の前で乾かしているのよ。","romaji":"Uchi no ko ga oshikko morashi o shichatte, zubon o aratta kara, ima sore o danro no mae de kawakashite iru no yo.","german":"My kid wet his pants, so I washed them and am drying them in front of the fireplace now.","furigana":"こ|も|あら|いま|だんろ|まえ|かわ"},{"japanese":"冬の夜は、家族で暖炉を囲んで話をした。","romaji":"Fuyu no yoru wa, kazoku de danro o kakonde hanashi o shita.","german":"On winter nights, our family sat around the fireplace and talked.","furigana":"ふゆ|よる|かぞく|だんろ|かこ|はなし"},{"japanese":"山小屋に着くと、まず暖炉に薪を入れて火をつけた。","romaji":"Yamagoya ni tsuku to, mazu danro ni maki o irete hi o tsuketa.","german":"When we arrived at the mountain hut, we first put firewood in the fireplace and lit it.","furigana":"やまごや|つ|だんろ|まき|い|ひ"}],"notes":"暖炉 is an open fireplace of the Western kind, in which wood is burned. Typical phrases are 暖炉に火をつける, 暖炉に薪を入れる and 暖炉を囲む (\"to gather around the fireplace\"). In Japanese homes it is rare and seems romantic and European; heating is usually done with a ストーブ or エアコン. The traditional open hearth in a farmhouse is called 囲炉裏.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どうして奴らは原子炉の炉心が溶けてしまったことを隠そうとしたんだ？","romaji":"Doushite yatsura wa genshiro no roshin ga tokete shimatta koto o kakusou to shita n da?","german":"Why did they try to hide that the reactor core had melted?","furigana":"やつ|げんしろ|ろしん|と|かく"},{"japanese":"冷却装置が止まったため、炉心溶融が起きた。","romaji":"Reikyaku souchi ga tomatta tame, roshin youyuu ga okita.","german":"The cooling system stopped, so a core meltdown occurred.","furigana":"れいきゃくそうち|と|ろしんようゆう|お"},{"japanese":"作業員は炉心の温度を常に監視している。","romaji":"Sagyouin wa roshin no ondo o tsune ni kanshi shite iru.","german":"The workers constantly monitor the temperature of the reactor core.","furigana":"さぎょういん|ろしん|おんど|つね|かんし"}],"notes":"炉心 is the core of a nuclear reactor, where the fuel assemblies sit. The word appears almost only in connection with nuclear power, especially in 炉心溶融 (\"core meltdown\", also メルトダウン) and 炉心損傷. It belongs to technical language and to news about power plant accidents. The reactor as a whole is called 原子炉.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"原子炉の建設について、政府に忠告をした人もいました。","romaji":"Genshiro no kensetsu nitsuite, seifu ni chūkoku o shita hito mo imashita.","german":"Some people warned the government about building nuclear reactors.","furigana":"げんしろ|けんせつ|せいふ|ちゅうこく|ひと"},{"japanese":"地震の揺れを感知して、原子炉は自動的に停止した。","romaji":"Jishin no yure o kanchi shite, genshiro wa jidouteki ni teishi shita.","german":"The reactor shut down automatically when it detected the earthquake tremors.","furigana":"じしん|ゆ|かんち|げんしろ|じどうてき|ていし"},{"japanese":"古くなった原子炉を廃炉にするには、何十年もかかる。","romaji":"Furuku natta genshiro o hairo ni suru ni wa, nanjuunen mo kakaru.","german":"It takes decades to decommission an aging reactor.","furigana":"ふる|げんしろ|はいろ|なんじゅうねん"}],"notes":"原子炉 is the nuclear reactor itself; the whole power plant is called 原子力発電所, 原発 for short. Typical phrases are 原子炉を停止する, 原子炉が稼働する and 再稼働 (\"restart\"). The final shutdown is called 廃炉, literally \"to abolish the reactor\". The word is technical and matter-of-fact and appears mainly in the news and energy debates.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あなたのブログのURLをお伺いしてもよろしいですか？","romaji":"Anata no burogu no URL o oukagai shite mo yoroshii desu ka?","german":"May I ask for the URL of your blog?","furigana":"うかが"},{"japanese":"休暇の日程について、部長にお伺いを立てた。","romaji":"Kyuuka no nittei ni tsuite, buchou ni oukagai o tateta.","german":"I asked the department head for permission regarding my vacation dates.","furigana":"きゅうか|にってい|ぶちょう|うかが|た"},{"japanese":"営業部の田中さんは、毎月お得意様にご機嫌伺いの電話をかけている。","romaji":"Eigyoubu no Tanaka-san wa, maitsuki otokuisama ni gokigen ukagai no denwa o kakete iru.","german":"Mr. Tanaka from the sales department phones his regular customers every month to ask after their well-being.","furigana":"えいぎょうぶ|たなか|まいつき|とくいさま|きげんうかが|でんわ"}],"notes":"伺い is the noun form of the humble verb 伺う (\"to ask; to visit\") and almost always appears with お. お伺いを立てる means to ask a superior for permission or instructions. The meaning \"visit\" is contained in ご機嫌伺い (\"courtesy visit, or a call to ask how someone is doing\"), and in 進退伺い the formal offer to resign. お伺いする as a verb is strictly speaking considered double politeness, but is completely normal in business.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"空港に到着するとファンが群がっていて、驚きましたよ。","romaji":"Kūkō ni tōchaku suru to fan ga muragatte ite, odorokimashita yo.","german":"When I arrived at the airport, fans were crowded there - that really surprised me.","furigana":"くうこう|とうちゃく|むら|おどろ"},{"japanese":"落とした飴に蟻が群がっている。","romaji":"Otoshita ame ni ari ga muragatte iru.","german":"Ants are swarming over the candy that fell on the ground.","furigana":"お|あめ|あり|むら"},{"japanese":"セール会場の入口に客が群がっていた。","romaji":"Sēru kaijō no iriguchi ni kyaku ga muragatte ita.","german":"Customers crowded at the entrance to the clearance sale.","furigana":"かいじょう|いりぐち|きゃく|むら"}],"notes":"群がる is intransitive and describes many people or animals \"crowding, flocking, swarming\" at one spot. The target is marked with に: 砂糖に蟻が群がる, スターにファンが群がる. Compared with the neutral 集まる \"to gather\", 群がる emphasizes the dense, often disorderly mass and sounds vivid, sometimes slightly derogatory. Related is the noun 群れ \"herd, swarm\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"俺の投げたライターがテレビに当たって、液晶画面にヒビが入りました。","romaji":"Ore no nageta raitā ga terebi ni atatte, ekishō gamen ni hibi ga hairimashita.","german":"The lighter I threw hit the TV, and the LCD screen cracked.","furigana":"おれ|な|あ|えきしょうがめん|はい"},{"japanese":"スマホを落として、液晶が割れてしまった。","romaji":"Sumaho o otoshite, ekishou ga warete shimatta.","german":"I dropped my smartphone and the screen cracked.","furigana":"お|えきしょう|わ"},{"japanese":"電卓の液晶が薄くなってきたから、電池を替えよう。","romaji":"Dentaku no ekishou ga usuku natte kita kara, denchi o kaeyou.","german":"The calculator's display is getting faint, so I'll change the battery.","furigana":"でんたく|えきしょう|うす|でんち|か"}],"notes":"液晶 is the liquid crystal or the liquid crystal display (LCD). Common in 液晶画面, 液晶テレビ and 液晶ディスプレイ. Colloquially, 液晶 alone often stands for a device's display: スマホの液晶が割れた. Newer technology is called 有機EL (OLED). The word is technical but familiar in daily life.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この水晶は大中小の三つの大きさがあります。","romaji":"Kono suishou wa dai chuu shou no mittsu no ookisa ga arimasu.","german":"This crystal comes in three sizes: large, medium and small.","furigana":"すいしょう|だいちゅうしょう|みっ|おお"},{"japanese":"占い師は水晶玉をのぞき込んで、私の将来について話した。","romaji":"Uranaishi wa suishoudama o nozokikonde, watashi no shourai ni tsuite hanashita.","german":"The fortune teller peered into the crystal ball and talked about my future.","furigana":"うらな|し|すいしょうだま|こ|わたし|しょうらい|はな"},{"japanese":"山梨県は昔から水晶の産地として知られている。","romaji":"Yamanashi-ken wa mukashi kara suishou no sanchi to shite shirarete iru.","german":"Yamanashi Prefecture has long been known as a source of rock crystal.","furigana":"やまなしけん|むかし|すいしょう|さんち|し"}],"notes":"水晶 is rock crystal or quartz as a mineral, literally \"water crystal\". Well known are 水晶玉 (the fortune-teller's \"crystal ball\") and the quartz in watches. \"Crystal\" in the general chemical sense, on the other hand, is 結晶, and crystal glass is クリスタル. In anatomy, 水晶体 is the lens of the eye.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"僕がおならしたって？そんなの濡れ衣だよ。","romaji":"Boku ga onara shita tte? Sonna no nureginu da yo.","german":"You say I farted? That's a false accusation.","furigana":"ぼく|ぬ|ぎぬ"},{"japanese":"彼は万引きの濡れ衣を着せられて、店の事務所に連れて行かれた。","romaji":"Kare wa manbiki no nureginu o kiserarete, mise no jimusho ni tsurete ikareta.","german":"He was falsely accused of shoplifting and taken to the store's office.","furigana":"かれ|まんび|ぬ|ぎぬ|き|みせ|じむしょ|つ|い"},{"japanese":"十年後にようやく真犯人が見つかり、その男性の濡れ衣が晴れた。","romaji":"Juunen go ni youyaku shinhannin ga mitsukari, sono dansei no nureginu ga hareta.","german":"Ten years later the real culprit was finally found, and the man was cleared of the false charge.","furigana":"じゅうねんご|しんはんにん|み|だんせい|ぬ|ぎぬ|は"}],"notes":"濡れ衣 literally means \"wet garment\" and figuratively means a false accusation. The fixed phrases are 濡れ衣を着せる (\"to pin something on someone\"), 濡れ衣を着せられる (\"to be wrongly accused\") and 濡れ衣を晴らす or 濡れ衣が晴れる (\"to be cleared of false suspicion\"). In conversation you retort with それは濡れ衣だ. For a legal miscarriage of justice, people say 冤罪.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ベトナムのコーヒー、凄く甘くて驚きました。","romaji":"Betonamu no kōhī, sugoku amakute odorokimashita.","german":"Vietnamese coffee was very sweet. I was surprised!","furigana":"すご|あま|おどろ"},{"japanese":"昨日の試験はすごく難しかった。","romaji":"Kinou no shiken wa sugoku muzukashikatta.","german":"Yesterday's exam was really hard.","furigana":"きのう|しけん|むずか"},{"japanese":"最近仕事が忙しくて、すごく疲れています。","romaji":"Saikin shigoto ga isogashikute, sugoku tsukarete imasu.","german":"I've been busy with work lately and am really exhausted.","furigana":"さいきんしごと|いそが|つか"}],"notes":"すごく is the adverbial form of すごい and means \"very, totally, insanely\"; it stands before adjectives and verbs: すごく暑い, すごく疲れた. It is colloquial – in formal texts, とても or 非常に is used. The kanji spelling 凄く is rare; normally すごく is written in kana. In conversation you often hear すごい美味しい instead of すごく美味しい, but this is considered non-standard. For emphasis people also say すっごく.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"唐揚げ揚がったよ！","romaji":"Karaage agatta yo!","german":"The fried chicken is ready!","furigana":"からあ|あ"},{"japanese":"油の温度が低いと、天ぷらがカラッと揚がらない。","romaji":"Abura no ondo ga hikui to, tenpura ga karatto agaranai.","german":"If the oil temperature is low, the tempura won't fry up crisp.","furigana":"あぶら|おんど|ひく|てん|あ"},{"japanese":"風の強い日に、空高く凧が揚がっている。","romaji":"Kaze no tsuyoi hi ni, sora takaku tako ga agatte iru.","german":"On a windy day, a kite is flying high in the sky.","furigana":"かぜ|つよ|ひ|そらたか|たこ|あ"}],"notes":"揚がる is the intransitive counterpart of 揚げる (\"to deep-fry\") and means that something is finished frying: 天ぷらが揚がる, カラッと揚がる (\"to turn out nicely crisp\"). With the same spelling, kites and fireworks also rise into the air: 凧が揚がる, 花火が揚がる. For the general \"to rise, go up\", 上がる is written instead. When cooking, you often hear 揚がったよ as a signal that the food is ready.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"父の助力のおかげです。","romaji":"Chichi no joryoku no okage desu.","german":"It's thanks to my father's help.","furigana":"ちち|じょりょく"},{"japanese":"今回の調査では、地元の人々に助力を求めた。","romaji":"Konkai no chousa de wa, jimoto no hitobito ni joryoku o motometa.","german":"For this survey, we asked the local people for their assistance.","furigana":"こんかい|ちょうさ|じもと|ひとびと|じょりょく|もと"},{"japanese":"彼は友人の起業に助力を惜しまなかった。","romaji":"Kare wa yuujin no kigyou ni joryoku o oshimanakatta.","german":"He spared no effort in helping his friend start a business.","furigana":"かれ|ゆうじん|きぎょう|じょりょく|お"}],"notes":"助力 is support given to someone in an undertaking. It is formal and often used as a noun: 助力を求める, 助力を得る, 助力を惜しまない (\"to support with all one's strength\"), politely ご助力. As a する-verb it is intransitive: 〜に助力する. Unlike 協力 (\"cooperation\"), here one side helps the other; 援助 means more financial or material aid, 手伝い everyday help.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼はソファに身を沈めながら、アカデミー賞の授賞式の生中継を観ています。","romaji":"Kare wa sofa ni mi o shizumenagara, Akademīshō no jushōshiki no namachūkei o mite imasu.","german":"He watches the live broadcast of the Oscars while sinking into the sofa.","furigana":"かれ|み|しず|しょう|じゅしょうしき|なまちゅうけい|み"},{"japanese":"敵の潜水艦が輸送船を沈めた。","romaji":"Teki no sensuikan ga yusōsen o shizumeta.","german":"An enemy submarine sank the transport ship.","furigana":"てき|せんすいかん|ゆそうせん|しず"},{"japanese":"チャンピオンは三ラウンドで挑戦者をマットに沈めた。","romaji":"Chanpion wa san-raundo de chōsensha o matto ni shizumeta.","german":"The champion floored the challenger in the third round.","furigana":"さん|ちょうせんしゃ|しず"}],"notes":"沈める is the transitive counterpart of 沈む \"to sink, to go down\": 船を沈める \"to sink a ship\". Figuratively 身を沈める means \"to sink (deep) into an armchair\", and in boxing 相手をマットに沈める \"to send the opponent to the canvas\". Do not confuse with the same-sounding 静める/鎮める \"to calm, to soothe\" (気持ちを静める, 痛みを鎮める).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの携帯ストラップが気色悪すぎて、考えるだけでも吐きそうだわ。","romaji":"Ano keitai sutorappu ga kishokuwarusugite, kangaeru dake demo hakisou da wa.","german":"That phone strap is so gross that I feel sick just thinking about it.","furigana":"けいたい|きしょくわる|かんが|は"},{"japanese":"お土産に、ご当地キャラの携帯ストラップを買った。","romaji":"Omiyage ni, gotouchi kyara no keitai sutorappu o katta.","german":"As a souvenir, I bought a phone strap with the local mascot.","furigana":"みやげ|とうち|けいたい|か"},{"japanese":"最近は携帯ストラップを付けている人がずいぶん減った。","romaji":"Saikin wa keitai sutorappu o tsukete iru hito ga zuibun hetta.","german":"These days, far fewer people attach a strap to their phone.","furigana":"さいきん|けいたい|つ|ひと|へ"}],"notes":"携帯ストラップ is a small charm on a loop that is hung on a cell phone – often with figures, good-luck charms or souvenir motifs like ご当地キャラ. In the days of flip phones it was extremely widespread; today smartphones mostly have no strap hole anymore, and you see phone neck straps (ショルダーストラップ) more often. You say ストラップを付ける, and in conversation also simply ストラップ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その哲学者は強風の中を歩きながら、ベレー帽をしっかりと押さえていた。","romaji":"Sono tetsugakusha wa kyoufuu no naka o arukinagara, bereebou o shikkari to osaete ita.","german":"The philosopher held his beret firmly as he walked through the strong wind.","furigana":"てつがくしゃ|きょうふう|なか|ある|ぼう|お"},{"japanese":"ソクラテスは古代ギリシャの有名な哲学者だ。","romaji":"Sokuratesu wa kodai Girisha no yuumei na tetsugakusha da.","german":"Socrates is a famous philosopher of ancient Greece.","furigana":"こだい|ゆうめい|てつがくしゃ"},{"japanese":"父は酔うと、哲学者みたいに人生について語り出す。","romaji":"Chichi wa you to, tetsugakusha mitai ni jinsei ni tsuite katari dasu.","german":"When my father gets drunk, he starts talking about life like a philosopher.","furigana":"ちち|よ|てつがくしゃ|じんせい|かた|だ"}],"notes":"哲学者 is a philosopher, formed from 哲学 (\"philosophy\") and the person suffix 者 as in 学者 or 科学者. Typical are ギリシャの哲学者 or 有名な哲学者. For a general \"thinker\", people tend to say 思想家. Jokingly, someone who likes to ponder life is also called 哲学者みたい.","levelNote":"Compound: 哲学 (N3) + 者 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の妹は拒食症なのですが、喪服を着ているとより一層痩せ細ってみえました。","romaji":"Watashi no imouto wa kyoshokushou na no desu ga, mofuku o kite iru to yori issou yasehosotte miemashita.","german":"My sister is anorexic, and in mourning clothes she looked even thinner.","furigana":"わたし|いもうと|きょしょくしょう|もふく|き|いっそうや|ほそ"},{"japanese":"急な葬儀だったので、喪服をレンタルした。","romaji":"Kyuu na sougi datta node, mofuku o rentaru shita.","german":"The funeral was sudden, so I rented mourning clothes.","furigana":"きゅう|そうぎ|もふく"},{"japanese":"お通夜には、喪服ではなく地味な服で行ってもいいとされている。","romaji":"Otsuya ni wa, mofuku de wa naku jimi na fuku de itte mo ii to sarete iru.","german":"For the wake, it is said to be acceptable to go in plain clothes rather than mourning clothes.","furigana":"つや|もふく|じみ|ふく|い"}],"notes":"喪服 is the black mourning clothing for funerals and memorial services; 喪 means \"mourning\" as in 喪中 (the mourning period during which one does not send New Year's cards). You say 喪服を着る; in stores it is also called ブラックフォーマル. For the wake お通夜, it is traditionally considered appropriate to also appear in plain, dark clothing, since 喪服 here can seem as if one had expected the death.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"腕相撲の挑戦者は、握力を見せつけるために、右手で林檎を潰してみせました。","romaji":"Udezumō no chōsensha wa, akuryoku o misetsukeru tame ni, migite de ringo o tsubushite misemashita.","german":"The arm-wrestling challenger crushed an apple with his right hand to show off his grip strength.","furigana":"|ちょうせんしゃ|あくりょく|み|みぎて|りんご|つぶ"},{"japanese":"チャンピオンは挑戦者を三ラウンドで倒した。","romaji":"Chanpion wa chousensha o san raundo de taoshita.","german":"The champion knocked out the challenger in the third round.","furigana":"ちょうせんしゃ|さん|たお"},{"japanese":"このクイズ番組では、毎週新しい挑戦者が賞金を目指す。","romaji":"Kono kuizu bangumi de wa, maishuu atarashii chousensha ga shoukin o mezasu.","german":"On this quiz show, new contestants go after the prize money every week.","furigana":"ばんぐみ|まいしゅうあたら|ちょうせんしゃ|しょうきん|めざ"}],"notes":"挑戦者 is the challenger, especially in sports, title fights and quiz shows; the opposite is the チャンピオン or the 王者. Note: 挑戦 does not mean \"challenge\" in the sense of a difficult task (that is 課題), but \"to dare something, to try one's hand at something\": 新しいことに挑戦する. Therefore 挑戦者 generally refers to someone who takes on a test or a record.","levelNote":"Compound: 挑戦 (N3) + 者 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"揚げドーナツ、揚げバナナ、揚げギョーザ…。とにかく揚げ物が大好きなんです。","romaji":"Age doonatsu, age banana, age gyouza... Tonikaku agemono ga daisuki na n desu.","german":"Fried donuts, fried bananas, fried dumplings... I just love fried food.","furigana":"あ|あ|あ|あ|もの|だいす"},{"japanese":"きつねうどんには、甘く煮た揚げがのっている。","romaji":"Kitsune udon ni wa, amaku nita age ga notte iru.","german":"Kitsune udon has sweetly simmered fried tofu on top.","furigana":"あま|に|あ"},{"japanese":"お昼に、かき揚げそばを食べた。","romaji":"Ohiru ni, kakiage soba o tabeta.","german":"For lunch, I ate soba with kakiage (mixed vegetable fritters).","furigana":"ひる|あ|た"}],"notes":"揚げ is the noun form of 揚げる (\"to deep-fry\"). As a prefix it means \"fried\": 揚げパン, 揚げ出し豆腐; as a suffix it forms dish names such as 唐揚げ, かき揚げ and 竜田揚げ. On its own, 揚げ (or お揚げ, especially in western Japan) stands for 油揚げ, fried tofu, for example in きつねうどん. Fried foods in general are called 揚げ物.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"怒ったコウイチは、握り拳で机をバンバン叩いた。","romaji":"Okotta Kouichi wa, nigirikobushi de tsukue o banban tataita.","german":"The angry Koichi pounded the desk with his clenched fist.","furigana":"おこ|にぎ|こぶし|つくえ|たた"},{"japanese":"このラケットは握りが太くて、持ちやすい。","romaji":"Kono raketto wa nigiri ga futokute, mochiyasui.","german":"This racket has a thick grip and is easy to hold.","furigana":"にぎ|ふと|も"},{"japanese":"寿司屋で、握りを一人前注文した。","romaji":"Sushiya de, nigiri o ichininmae chuumon shita.","german":"At the sushi restaurant, I ordered one serving of nigiri.","furigana":"すしや|にぎ|いちにんまえちゅうもん"}],"notes":"握り is the noun form of 握る (\"to grasp, hold tightly\") and has several meanings: the grip of an object (ラケットの握り), the way of gripping, a handful (一握りの米, figuratively ほんの一握りの人 \"just a handful of people\") and short for 握り寿司. It is also contained in 握り拳 (\"fist\") and おにぎり. For door handles, people tend to say 取っ手 or ノブ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"僕の伯父さんは、今まで蜂に刺されたことがなくて、どんなものか試してみたかったから、蜂の巣がある木を揺すったんだそうです。","romaji":"Boku no ojisan wa, ima made hachi ni sasareta koto ga nakute, donna mono ka tameshite mitakatta kara, hachi no su ga aru ki o yusutta n da sou desu.","german":"My uncle apparently shook a tree with a beehive in it because he had never been stung by a bee and wanted to know what it was like.","furigana":"ぼく|おじ|いま|はち|さ|ため|はち|す|き|ゆ"},{"japanese":"いくら肩を揺すっても、弟は起きなかった。","romaji":"Ikura kata o yusutte mo, otouto wa okinakatta.","german":"No matter how much I shook his shoulder, my younger brother didn't wake up.","furigana":"かた|ゆ|おとうと|お"},{"japanese":"母親は赤ちゃんを抱いて、ゆっくり体を揺すった。","romaji":"Hahaoya wa akachan o daite, yukkuri karada o yusutta.","german":"The mother held the baby in her arms and slowly rocked her body.","furigana":"ははおや|あか|だ|からだ|ゆ"}],"notes":"揺する means to move something back and forth with the hand or body: 木を揺する, 肩を揺する, 体を揺する. It is transitive; the intransitive counterpart is 揺れる (\"to sway\"). 揺らす is almost synonymous, 揺さぶる sounds more violent and is also used figuratively (心を揺さぶる). Written in kana, ゆする also means \"to blackmail\". Nervous jiggling of the leg is called 貧乏揺すり.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"うちの子、幼稚園のお泊まり会をすごく楽しみにしてたんだけど、風邪を引いちゃって、今抗生物質を飲ませたところなんです。だから、残念ながら今夜は欠席させて頂きます。","romaji":"Uchi no ko, youchien no otomarikai o sugoku tanoshimi ni shiteta n da kedo, kaze o hiichatte, ima kousei busshitsu o nomaseta tokoro na n desu. Dakara, zannen nagara kon'ya wa kesseki sasete itadakimasu.","german":"My kid had been really looking forward to the sleepover at kindergarten, but caught a cold and I've just given him antibiotics. So unfortunately he will have to miss tonight.","furigana":"こ|ようちえん|と|かい|たの|かぜ|ひ|いまこうせいぶっしつ|の|ざんねん|こんや|けっせき|いただ"},{"japanese":"週末は、泊まりで温泉に行きます。","romaji":"Shuumatsu wa, tomari de onsen ni ikimasu.","german":"This weekend, we're going to a hot spring with an overnight stay.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|と|おんせん|い"},{"japanese":"今夜は泊まりの仕事なので、家には帰れません。","romaji":"Kon'ya wa tomari no shigoto na node, ie ni wa kaeremasen.","german":"I have an overnight shift tonight, so I can't go home.","furigana":"こんや|と|しごと|いえ|かえ"}],"notes":"泊まり is the noun form of 泊まる (\"to stay overnight\"). Very common is 泊まりで (\"with an overnight stay\") as opposed to 日帰り (\"day trip\"). お泊まり sounds childlike or friendly, as in お泊まり会 (\"sleepover\"). In work contexts, 泊まり also means night duty: 今日は泊まりだ. Guests who stay overnight are called 泊まり客.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"甥っ子の誕生日祝いに滑り台を作ってるんですが、少々予定が遅れ気味なんですよね。","romaji":"Oikko no tanjoubi iwai ni suberidai o tsukutteru n desu ga, shoushou yotei ga okuregimi na n desu yo ne.","german":"I'm building a slide for my nephew's birthday, but I'm running a little behind schedule.","furigana":"おい|こ|たんじょうびいわ|すべ|だい|つく|しょうしょうよてい|おく|ぎみ"},{"japanese":"娘は公園の滑り台を何度も滑って遊んだ。","romaji":"Musume wa kouen no suberidai o nando mo subette asonda.","german":"My daughter played on the playground slide, going down again and again.","furigana":"むすめ|こうえん|すべ|だい|なんど|すべ|あそ"},{"japanese":"真夏の滑り台は熱くなっていて、やけどしそうだった。","romaji":"Manatsu no suberidai wa atsuku natte ite, yakedo shisou datta.","german":"In midsummer, the slide had gotten so hot that I almost got burned.","furigana":"まなつ|すべ|だい|あつ"}],"notes":"滑り台 is the slide on a playground, formed from 滑る (\"to slide\") and 台 (\"stand, platform\"). You say 滑り台を滑る or 滑り台で遊ぶ; it is also often written すべり台. Other playground equipment includes ブランコ (\"swing\"), ジャングルジム and 砂場 (\"sandbox\"). In technical language, it also refers to the slipway from which a ship is launched.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いつもトーストを真っ黒に焦がしてしまう。","romaji":"Itsumo toosuto o makkuro ni kogashite shimau.","german":"I always burn my toast completely black.","furigana":"ま|くろ|こ"},{"japanese":"話に夢中になって、鍋を焦がしてしまった。","romaji":"Hanashi ni muchuu ni natte, nabe o kogashite shimatta.","german":"I was so absorbed in the conversation that I burned the pot.","furigana":"はなし|むちゅう|なべ|こ"},{"japanese":"アイロンをかけている途中で、シャツを焦がしてしまった。","romaji":"Airon o kakete iru tochuu de, shatsu o kogashite shimatta.","german":"I scorched the shirt while ironing.","furigana":"とちゅう|こ"}],"notes":"焦がす means to accidentally let something burn or to scorch it: パンを焦がす, 鍋を焦がす, アイロンでシャツを焦がす. It is transitive; the intransitive counterpart is 焦げる (\"to burn\"), and the result is called 焦げ. Deliberate browning is described with 焦げ目をつける. Figuratively, 胸を焦がす means to be consumed by longing or love.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"解熱剤が他の薬に紛れちゃってて、どれがどれだか分かりません。","romaji":"Genetsuzai ga hoka no kusuri ni magirechattete, dore ga dore da ka wakarimasen.","german":"The fever medicine got mixed in with the other medicines, and I no longer know which is which.","furigana":"げねつざい|ほか|くすり|まぎ|わ"},{"japanese":"犯人は人込みに紛れて逃げた。","romaji":"Hannin wa hitogomi ni magirete nigeta.","german":"The culprit disappeared into the crowd and escaped.","furigana":"はんにん|ひとご|まぎ|に"},{"japanese":"音楽を聴いていると、少し気が紛れる。","romaji":"Ongaku o kiite iru to, sukoshi ki ga magireru.","german":"When I listen to music, it takes my mind off things a bit.","furigana":"おんがく|き|すこ|き|まぎ"}],"notes":"紛れる means to get lost in a crowd or to mix in with other things so that one can no longer tell it apart: 書類に紛れる, 人込みに紛れる. From this come figurative meanings: acting under cover of something (闇に紛れて逃げる), being distracted (気が紛れる) and forgetting something in the midst of something else (忙しさに紛れて). It is intransitive; the transitive counterpart is 紛らす or 紛らわす (気を紛らわす).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの古い網戸は本当に気持ち悪いね。","romaji":"Ano furui amido wa hontou ni kimochi warui ne.","german":"That old screen door is really gross.","furigana":"ふる|あみど|ほんとう|きも|わる"},{"japanese":"網戸が破れていて、蚊が入ってくる。","romaji":"Amido ga yaburete ite, ka ga haitte kuru.","german":"The screen door is torn, and mosquitoes are coming in.","furigana":"あみど|やぶ|か|はい"},{"japanese":"夏の夜は、窓を網戸にして寝ると涼しい。","romaji":"Natsu no yoru wa, mado o amido ni shite neru to suzushii.","german":"On summer nights, it's cool to sleep with the window open and only the screen closed.","furigana":"なつ|よる|まど|あみど|ね|すず"}],"notes":"網戸 is the insect screen in front of windows and balcony doors, installed in almost every Japanese home; usually it is a sliding screen door. Typical are 網戸を閉める, 網戸が破れる and 網戸にする – opening the window and leaving only the screen closed. The word is composed of 網 (\"net\") and 戸 (\"door\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"アイツはまだ社会の荒波に揉まれた事のないただの若造だよ。","romaji":"Aitsu wa mada shakai no aranami ni momareta koto no nai tada no wakazou da yo.","german":"That guy is just a youngster who has never been tossed about by the rough waves of society.","furigana":"しゃかい|あらなみ|も|こと|わかぞう"},{"japanese":"冬の日本海は荒波で有名だ。","romaji":"Fuyu no Nihonkai wa aranami de yuumei da.","german":"The Sea of Japan in winter is famous for its rough waves.","furigana":"ふゆ|にほんかい|あらなみ|ゆうめい"},{"japanese":"その会社は不況の荒波を乗り越えて、成長を続けた。","romaji":"Sono kaisha wa fukyou no aranami o norikoete, seichou o tsuzuketa.","german":"The company weathered the rough waves of the recession and kept growing.","furigana":"かいしゃ|ふきょう|あらなみ|の|こ|せいちょう|つづ"}],"notes":"荒波 literally means rough, raging waves on a stormy sea. Equally common is the figurative meaning \"hardships, adversities of life\", especially in 社会の荒波に揉まれる (\"to have to prove oneself in the harsh working world\"), 時代の荒波 and 荒波を乗り越える. The word has a slightly literary ring and appears in speeches and newspaper texts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"恐竜の化石を見つけるのに一番いい方法はなんでしょうか。","romaji":"Kyōryū no kaseki o mitsukeru no ni ichiban ii hōhō wa nan deshō ka.","german":"What is the best way to find dinosaur fossils?","furigana":"きょうりゅう|かせき|み|いちばん|ほうほう"},{"japanese":"息子は恐竜が大好きで、名前を全部覚えている。","romaji":"Musuko wa kyouryuu ga daisuki de, namae o zenbu oboete iru.","german":"My son loves dinosaurs and knows all their names.","furigana":"むすこ|きょうりゅう|だいす|なまえ|ぜんぶおぼ"},{"japanese":"恐竜は約六千六百万年前に絶滅したと考えられている。","romaji":"Kyouryuu wa yaku rokusen roppyakuman nen mae ni zetsumetsu shita to kangaerarete iru.","german":"Dinosaurs are thought to have gone extinct about 66 million years ago.","furigana":"きょうりゅう|やくろくせんろっぴゃくまんねんまえ|ぜつめつ|かんが"}],"notes":"恐竜 literally means \"terrible dragon\" (恐 \"fear\", 竜 \"dragon\") and refers to dinosaurs. Typical combinations are 恐竜の化石, 恐竜の骨 and 恐竜が絶滅する. For mythical dragons, people say 竜 or ドラゴン instead. Well known in Japan is the dinosaur museum in Fukui Prefecture, where many fossils have been found.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この田舎道は、渋滞とは無縁です。","romaji":"Kono inakamichi wa, jūtai to wa muen desu.","german":"This country road is free of traffic jams.","furigana":"いなかみち|じゅうたい|むえん"},{"japanese":"私には、贅沢な暮らしなど無縁だ。","romaji":"Watashi ni wa, zeitaku na kurashi nado muen da.","german":"A life of luxury has nothing to do with me.","furigana":"わたし|ぜいたく|く|むえん"},{"japanese":"一人暮らしの高齢者が増え、「無縁社会」が問題になっている。","romaji":"Hitorigurashi no koureisha ga fue, \"muen shakai\" ga mondai ni natte iru.","german":"As the number of elderly people living alone grows, the \"society without ties\" has become a problem.","furigana":"ひとりぐ|こうれいしゃ|ふ|むえんしゃかい|もんだい"}],"notes":"無縁 means \"without connection to, having nothing to do with\". Typical are AはBと無縁だ or Aには無縁だ: 私には贅沢など無縁だ. Also common is 無縁の: 争いとは無縁の生活. A second meaning is \"without relatives\", e.g. 無縁仏 (a dead person with no surviving relatives) and 無縁社会 (\"society without ties\", with isolated, lonely people). The word is neutral to literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"高卒以下の学歴の男との縁談を私に持って寄越すなんて、一体どういう神経してる訳？","romaji":"Kousotsu ika no gakureki no otoko to no endan o watashi ni motte yokosu nante, ittai dou iu shinkei shiteru wake?","german":"You're seriously offering me a match with a man who has at most a high school diploma? What are you thinking?!","furigana":"こうそついか|がくれき|おとこ|えんだん|わたし|も|よこ|いったい|しんけい|わけ"},{"japanese":"娘に縁談が持ち込まれたが、本人はあまり乗り気ではない。","romaji":"Musume ni endan ga mochikomareta ga, honnin wa amari noriki de wa nai.","german":"A match was proposed for our daughter, but she herself is not particularly taken with it.","furigana":"むすめ|えんだん|も|こ|ほんにん|の|き"},{"japanese":"親戚の紹介で縁談がまとまり、来年の春に結婚することになった。","romaji":"Shinseki no shoukai de endan ga matomari, rainen no haru ni kekkon suru koto ni natta.","german":"The marriage came about through a relative's mediation, and the wedding will be next spring.","furigana":"しんせき|しょうかい|えんだん|らいねん|はる|けっこん"}],"notes":"縁談 is a marriage proposal made through intermediaries, i.e. the talks about a possible marriage that parents, relatives or acquaintances arrange - often in connection with お見合い. Typical are 縁談を持ち込む, 縁談がまとまる (\"to be settled\"), 縁談を断る and 縁談が破談になる. A personal marriage proposal from man to woman, by contrast, is called プロポーズ. The word sounds traditional.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"みんな、結婚式の日に吉日を選びたいのよ。","romaji":"Minna, kekkonshiki no hi ni kichijitsu o erabitai no yo.","german":"Everyone wants to choose an auspicious day for their wedding.","furigana":"けっこんしき|ひ|きちじつ|えら"},{"japanese":"初詣でおみくじを引いたら、「吉」だった。","romaji":"Hatsumoude de omikuji o hiitara, \"kichi\" datta.","german":"When I drew a fortune slip at the New Year's shrine visit, it was 「吉」.","furigana":"はつもうで|ひ|きち"},{"japanese":"この決断が吉と出るか凶と出るかは、まだ分からない。","romaji":"Kono ketsudan ga kichi to deru ka kyou to deru ka wa, mada wakaranai.","german":"It's not yet clear whether this decision will turn out lucky or unlucky.","furigana":"けつだん|きち|で|きょう|で|わ"}],"notes":"吉 (read きち on its own) means \"good luck, good omen\" and is met mainly on おみくじ, the fortune slips at shrines; the grades run roughly from 大吉 through 中吉, 小吉, 吉 and 末吉 down to 凶, though the order varies from shrine to shrine. The phrase 吉と出るか凶と出るか asks whether something will turn out lucky or unlucky. In compounds it is often read きつ or きっ: 不吉, 吉報. 吉日 (\"lucky day\") is read きちじつ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"すごく不吉な夢を見たもんで、あんたのことが心配になってこうして電話をした訳さ。","romaji":"Sugoku fukitsu na yume o mita mon de, anta no koto ga shinpai ni natte kōshite denwa o shita wake sa.","german":"I had such an ominous dream that I worried about you, so I called.","furigana":"ふきつ|ゆめ|み|しんぱい|でんわ|わけ"},{"japanese":"黒猫が前を横切ると不吉だと言う人もいる。","romaji":"Kuroneko ga mae o yokogiru to fukitsu da to iu hito mo iru.","german":"Some say it brings bad luck if a black cat crosses your path.","furigana":"くろねこ|まえ|よこぎ|ふきつ|い|ひと"},{"japanese":"日本では「四」は「死」と同じ音なので、不吉な数字とされている。","romaji":"Nihon de wa \"shi\" wa \"shi\" to onaji oto na node, fukitsu na suuji to sarete iru.","german":"In Japan, the number four is considered unlucky because it sounds the same as the word for \"death\".","furigana":"にほん|し|し|おな|おと|ふきつ|すうじ"}],"notes":"不吉 means \"ominous, bringing bad luck\" and is a na-adjective used with 予感, 夢, 数字 or 前兆: 不吉な予感がする. Colloquially the same is often said with 縁起が悪い; 不吉 sounds more serious and somewhat more literary. Well-known examples from Japan are the numbers 4 and 9, whose pronunciation recalls 死 (\"death\") and 苦 (\"suffering\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"刃物をちらつかせながら、男は低い声で私に「こっちへ来い」と言いました。","romaji":"Hamono o chiratsukasenagara, otoko wa hikui koe de watashi ni \"kotchi e koi\" to iimashita.","german":"Brandishing a knife, the man said to me in a low voice, \"Come here.\"","furigana":"はもの|おとこ|ひく|こえ|わたし|こ|い"},{"japanese":"刃物を使うときは、子どもを近くに来させないでください。","romaji":"Hamono o tsukau toki wa, kodomo o chikaku ni kosasenaide kudasai.","german":"When using bladed tools, please don't let children come near.","furigana":"はもの|つか|こ|ちか|こ"},{"japanese":"飛行機の機内には、刃物を持ち込めない。","romaji":"Hikouki no kinai ni wa, hamono o mochikomenai.","german":"You cannot bring knives onto an airplane.","furigana":"ひこうき|きない|はもの|も|こ"}],"notes":"刃物 is the general term for all objects with a blade – knives, kitchen knives (包丁), scissors, razors. In the news, 刃物を持った男 is the typical phrasing for a knife attacker. Other combinations are 刃物を研ぐ (\"to sharpen\") and 刃物を扱う. The blade itself is called 刃 (は); Seki in Gifu is known as the town of cutlery (刃物の町).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"刃先を俺の首に当てながら、奴は、皆にいつもご機嫌をとられてるってのはどんな気分なのかと聞いてきたんだ。","romaji":"Hasaki o ore no kubi ni atenagara, yatsu wa, mina ni itsumo gokigen o torareteru tte no wa donna kibun na no ka to kiite kita n da.","german":"Holding the blade against my neck, he asked me what it feels like to have everyone always flatter you.","furigana":"はさき|おれ|くび|あ|やつ|みな|きげん|きぶん|き"},{"japanese":"包丁の刃先が欠けてしまったので、新しいのを買った。","romaji":"Houchou no hasaki ga kakete shimatta node, atarashii no o katta.","german":"The tip of my kitchen knife chipped, so I bought a new one.","furigana":"ほうちょう|はさき|か|あたら|か"},{"japanese":"刃先を使って、魚の骨を丁寧に取り除いた。","romaji":"Hasaki o tsukatte, sakana no hone o teinei ni torinozoita.","german":"Using the tip of the knife, I carefully removed the fish bones.","furigana":"はさき|つか|さかな|ほね|ていねい|と|のぞ"}],"notes":"刃先 is the cutting edge or the front tip of a blade, i.e. the part with which one actually cuts or stabs. Typical are 包丁の刃先, ナイフの刃先 and 刃先が欠ける (\"to chip, break off\"). With 刃先を当てる you hold the blade against something. Unlike 刃 (\"blade\" in general) and 刃物 (\"edged tool\"), 刃先 refers only to this front part.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"桃色のバンで母親が迎えにきた時は、恥ずかしかったですね。","romaji":"Momoiro no ban de hahaoya ga mukae ni kita toki wa, hazukashikatta desu ne.","german":"I was embarrassed when my mother came to pick me up in a pink van.","furigana":"ももいろ|ははおや|むか|とき|は"},{"japanese":"春になると、川沿いが桃色の花でいっぱいになる。","romaji":"Haru ni naru to, kawazoi ga momoiro no hana de ippai ni naru.","german":"In spring, the riverside is full of pink flowers.","furigana":"はる|かわぞ|ももいろ|はな"},{"japanese":"恥ずかしさで、彼女の頬が桃色に染まった。","romaji":"Hazukashisa de, kanojo no hoo ga momoiro ni somatta.","german":"Her cheeks flushed pink with embarrassment.","furigana":"は|かのじょ|ほお|ももいろ|そ"}],"notes":"桃色 is the color of peach blossoms, i.e. a light pink – not the orange-yellow of a peach fruit. As a color noun it takes の or に: 桃色の花, 頬が桃色に染まる. In everyday speech people usually say ピンク; 桃色 (also もも色) sounds more traditional and poetic. In the past it also had the secondary meaning \"erotic, amorous\" (桃色遊戯), which is now obsolete.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの映画のその場面がいつも謎なんだよね。","romaji":"Ano eiga no sono bamen ga itsumo nazo na n da yo ne.","german":"That scene in the movie is always a mystery to me.","furigana":"えいが|ばめん|なぞ"},{"japanese":"その事件の謎は、今もまだ解けていない。","romaji":"Sono jiken no nazo wa, ima mo mada tokete inai.","german":"The mystery of the case remains unsolved to this day.","furigana":"じけん|なぞ|いま|と"},{"japanese":"彼女は謎の多い人で、誰も本当の年齢を知らない。","romaji":"Kanojo wa nazo no ooi hito de, dare mo hontou no nenrei o shiranai.","german":"She is a very mysterious person; no one knows her real age.","furigana":"かのじょ|なぞ|おお|ひと|だれ|ほんとう|ねんれい|し"}],"notes":"謎 is a riddle or secret, something inexplicable: 謎を解く (\"to solve a mystery\"), 謎が解ける, 謎に包まれる (\"to be shrouded in mystery\"). As 謎の before a noun it means \"mysterious, enigmatic\": 謎の人物, 謎の死. The children's guessing game is called なぞなぞ. Colloquially, young people also use 謎 for \"strange, incomprehensible\": それ、謎すぎる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子どもはみんな謎々遊びをするのが好きだろう。","romaji":"Kodomo wa minna nazonazo asobi o suru no ga suki darō.","german":"All children probably like playing riddle games.","furigana":"こ|なぞなぞあそ|す"},{"japanese":"娘に謎々を出したら、すぐに答えられてしまった。","romaji":"Musume ni nazonazo o dashitara, sugu ni kotaerarete shimatta.","german":"When I gave my daughter a riddle, she answered right away.","furigana":"むすめ|なぞなぞ|だ|こた"},{"japanese":"「パンはパンでも食べられないパンはなあに？」というなぞなぞは有名だ。","romaji":"\"Pan wa pan demo taberarenai pan wa naani?\" to iu nazonazo wa yūmei da.","german":"The riddle 「パンはパンでも食べられないパンはなあに？」 is famous.","furigana":"た|ゆうめい"}],"notes":"謎々 is a wordplay riddle of the kind asked of children, often with a witty punchline: 「パンはパンでも食べられないパンは？」 Typical are 謎々を出す \"to pose a riddle\" and 謎々を解く／に答える \"to solve\". The word is usually written in kana: なぞなぞ. Do not confuse it with 謎 \"secret, mystery\" (謎の人物) or with クイズ \"quiz\" or パズル \"puzzle, brainteaser\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"暗い部屋で突然肩をたたかれて、思わず絶叫してしまった。","romaji":"Kurai heya de totsuzen kata o tatakarete, omowazu zekkyō shite shimatta.","german":"Someone suddenly tapped my shoulder in the dark room, and I screamed involuntarily.","furigana":"くら|へや|とつぜんかた|おも|ぜっきょう"},{"japanese":"コンサート会場はファンの絶叫に包まれた。","romaji":"Konsāto kaijō wa fan no zekkyō ni tsutsumareta.","german":"The concert hall was filled with the screams of the fans.","furigana":"かいじょう|ぜっきょう|つつ"},{"japanese":"ジェットコースターのような絶叫マシンはどうも苦手です。","romaji":"Jetto kōsutā no yō na zekkyō mashin wa dōmo nigate desu.","german":"I'm not good with thrill rides like roller coasters.","furigana":"ぜっきょう|にがて"}],"notes":"絶叫 is a piercing scream let out at the top of one's lungs, usually from fear, shock or extreme excitement. As an intransitive する-verb, the exclamation takes と: 「助けて！」と絶叫する. It is much stronger than 叫ぶ \"to shout, scream\"; 悲鳴 is more a cry of fear or pain, 歓声 a cheer. Well known is 絶叫マシン for roller coasters and other thrill rides.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"後で何匹釣れたかお知らせしますね。","romaji":"Ato de nanbiki tsureta ka oshirase shimasu ne.","german":"I'll let you know later how many we caught.","furigana":"あと||し"},{"japanese":"川で大きな魚を釣りました。","romaji":"Kawa de ōkina sakana o tsurimashita.","german":"I caught a big fish in the river.","furigana":"かわ|おお|さかな|つ"},{"japanese":"安い値段で客を釣る店には気をつけてください。","romaji":"Yasui nedan de kyaku o tsuru mise ni wa ki o tsukete kudasai.","german":"Be careful of shops that lure customers with cheap prices.","furigana":"やす|ねだん|きゃく|つ|みせ|き"}],"notes":"釣る means \"to catch (with a rod)\": 魚を釣る. \"To go fishing\" is mostly said with the noun: 釣りに行く, 釣りをする. The potential/intransitive form 釣れる means \"to bite, to let itself be caught\": 今日はよく釣れる. Figuratively 釣る means \"to lure, to entice\": 安い値段で客を釣る.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"金髪になりたかったので、コカコーラで髪を脱色しました。","romaji":"Kinpatsu ni naritakatta node, kokakōra de kami o dasshoku shimashita.","german":"I wanted to have blond hair, so I bleached my hair with Coca-Cola.","furigana":"きんぱつ|かみ|だっしょく"},{"japanese":"彼は大学に入ってすぐ、髪を金髪に染めた。","romaji":"Kare wa daigaku ni haitte sugu, kami o kinpatsu ni someta.","german":"Right after entering university, he dyed his hair blond.","furigana":"かれ|だいがく|はい|かみ|きんぱつ|そ"},{"japanese":"駅で金髪の女の子に道を聞かれた。","romaji":"Eki de kinpatsu no onna no ko ni michi o kikareta.","german":"A blond girl asked me for directions at the station.","furigana":"えき|きんぱつ|おんな|こ|みち|き"}],"notes":"金髪 refers to blond or golden hair, whether natural or dyed. As an attribute it takes の (金髪の女性); to dye is 髪を金髪に染める, \"to turn blond\" is 金髪になる. Hair dyed brown is 茶髪; the loanword ブロンド sounds more like natural blond. Grammatically 金髪 is a noun, not an adjective.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「今何してるの？」「髪の毛をセットしてるところ。」","romaji":"\"Ima nani shiteru no?\" \"Kami no ke o setto shiteru tokoro.\"","german":"\"What are you doing right now?\" \"I'm just styling my hair.\"","furigana":"いまなに|かみ|け"},{"japanese":"スープに髪の毛が一本入っていた。","romaji":"Sūpu ni kami no ke ga ippon haitte ita.","german":"There was a hair in the soup.","furigana":"かみ|け|いっぽんはい"},{"japanese":"最近、髪の毛が抜けやすくなって心配だ。","romaji":"Saikin, kami no ke ga nukeyasuku natte shinpai da.","german":"Lately my hair has been falling out easily, and I'm worried.","furigana":"さいきん|かみ|け|ぬ|しんぱい"}],"notes":"髪の毛 is the everyday word for hair on the head and also for a single hair (髪の毛が一本). It is somewhat more colloquial and concrete than 髪, which is used more for the hairstyle as a whole (髪を切る). 毛 alone refers to hair in general, including body hair or animal fur. Typical: 髪の毛が抜ける \"hair falls out\", 髪の毛をセットする \"to style one's hair\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ビートルズみたいな髪型だね。","romaji":"Bītoruzu mitai na kamigata da ne.","german":"You have a Beatles-style hairstyle.","furigana":"かみがた"},{"japanese":"思い切って髪型を変えたら、みんなにすぐ気づかれた。","romaji":"Omoikitte kamigata o kaetara, minna ni sugu kizukareta.","german":"When I took the plunge and changed my hairstyle, everyone noticed right away.","furigana":"おも|き|かみがた|か|き"},{"japanese":"面接にはどんな髪型で行けばいいですか。","romaji":"Mensetsu ni wa donna kamigata de ikeba ii desu ka.","german":"What hairstyle should I wear to the interview?","furigana":"めんせつ|かみがた|い"}],"notes":"髪型 is the hairstyle; the spelling 髪形 is also found. Typical are 髪型を変える \"to change one's hairstyle\", 短い髪型にする \"to get a short hairstyle\" and 髪型が似合う \"the hairstyle suits one\". At the hair salon you often hear the loanword ヘアスタイル as well. Grammatically 髪型 is a simple noun, not an expression.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大砲がズドーンと鳴り響き、男は三人の子どもたちを残したまま逝ってしまいました。","romaji":"Taihō ga zudōn to narihibiki, otoko wa sannin no kodomotachi o nokoshita mama itte shimaimashita.","german":"The cannon boomed, and the man passed away, leaving three children behind.","furigana":"たいほう|な|ひび|おとこ|さんにん|こ|のこ|"},{"japanese":"城の入り口に、江戸時代の大砲が展示されている。","romaji":"Shiro no iriguchi ni, Edo jidai no taihō ga tenji sarete iru.","german":"A cannon from the Edo period is on display at the castle entrance.","furigana":"しろ|い|ぐち|えどじだい|たいほう|てんじ"},{"japanese":"新しく入った外国人選手は、チームの大砲として期待されている。","romaji":"Atarashiku haitta gaikokujin senshu wa, chīmu no taihō to shite kitai sarete iru.","german":"The new foreign player is expected to be the team's big slugger.","furigana":"あたら|はい|がいこくじんせんしゅ|たいほう|きたい"}],"notes":"大砲 is a cannon or heavy artillery piece; you say 大砲を撃つ \"to fire a cannon\" and count guns with 門 (大砲一門). Smaller firearms are 銃 or 鉄砲. In baseball, 大砲 colloquially refers to a home-run hitter who stands for a team's batting power.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どんな時に人は感涙にむせぶと思いますか。","romaji":"Donna toki ni hito wa kanrui ni musebu to omoimasu ka.","german":"When do you think people choke up with tears of emotion?","furigana":"とき|ひと|かんるい|おも"},{"japanese":"優勝が決まった瞬間、監督は感涙を流した。","romaji":"Yūshō ga kimatta shunkan, kantoku wa kanrui o nagashita.","german":"The moment the victory was decided, the manager shed tears of emotion.","furigana":"ゆうしょう|き|しゅんかん|かんとく|かんるい|なが"},{"japanese":"この映画のラストシーンは、まさに感涙ものだった。","romaji":"Kono eiga no rasuto shīn wa, masa ni kanrui mono datta.","german":"The final scene of this movie was truly a tearjerker.","furigana":"えいが|かんるい"}],"notes":"感涙 are tears shed from emotion, gratitude or overwhelming joy – not from sorrow. The word is elevated and literary; fixed phrases are 感涙を流す and 感涙にむせぶ \"to sob with emotion\". Colloquially people say 感動して泣く or 嬉し涙. Informally, 感涙もの means \"beautiful enough to make you cry\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私のお父さんは桃の缶詰工場で働いています。","romaji":"Watashi no otōsan wa momo no kanzume kōjō de hataraite imasu.","german":"My father works at a canned peach factory.","furigana":"わたし|とう|もも|かんづめこうじょう|はたら"},{"japanese":"空き缶はこちらのごみ箱に捨ててください。","romaji":"Akikan wa kochira no gomibako ni sutete kudasai.","german":"Please throw empty cans into this trash bin.","furigana":"あ|かん|ばこ|す"},{"japanese":"帰りにコンビニで缶コーヒーを二本買った。","romaji":"Kaeri ni konbini de kan kōhī o nihon katta.","german":"On the way home, I bought two cans of coffee at the convenience store.","furigana":"かえ|かん|にほんか"}],"notes":"缶 is a can or tin and appears in many compounds: 缶ビール, 缶コーヒー, 空き缶 \"empty can\", 缶切り \"can opener\", 缶詰 \"canned food\". For beverage cans one usually counts with 本, but 缶 itself can also be a counter (ビール3缶). Glass bottles, by contrast, are called 瓶 (びん), plastic bottles ペットボトル.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 26"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼らは、とても美味い缶コーヒーを販売して、大儲けしている。","romaji":"Karera wa, totemo umai kan kōhī o hanbai shite, ōmōke shite iru.","german":"They're making a fortune selling delicious canned coffee.","furigana":"かれ||かん|はんばい|おおもう"},{"japanese":"寒い朝は、自販機で温かい缶コーヒーを買います。","romaji":"Samui asa wa, jihanki de atatakai kan kōhī o kaimasu.","german":"On cold mornings, I buy a hot canned coffee from a vending machine.","furigana":"さむ|あさ|じはんき|あたた|かん|か"},{"japanese":"缶コーヒーは甘すぎるので、あまり飲みません。","romaji":"Kan kōhī wa amasugiru node, amari nomimasen.","german":"Canned coffee is too sweet, so I rarely drink it.","furigana":"かん|あま|の"}],"notes":"缶コーヒー is sweetened or black coffee in a can, as bought everywhere in Japan from vending machines (自動販売機, 自販機 for short) and at convenience stores – hot (ホット) in winter, cold (アイス) in summer. It is a typical everyday drink for commuters and workers. It is counted with 〜本: 缶コーヒーを一本買う. Similarly formed are 缶ビール and 缶ジュース.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お迎えにあがりましたよ、僕の小さなお姫様。","romaji":"Omukae ni agarimashita yo, boku no chiisana ohimesama.","german":"I've come to pick you up, my little princess.","furigana":"むか|ぼく|ちい|ひめさま"},{"japanese":"昔々、ある国に美しいお姫様が住んでいました。","romaji":"Mukashi mukashi, aru kuni ni utsukushii ohimesama ga sunde imashita.","german":"Once upon a time, a beautiful princess lived in a certain country.","furigana":"むかしむかし|くに|うつく|ひめさま|す"},{"japanese":"娘は、お姫様のドレスを着て写真を撮るのが大好きだ。","romaji":"Musume wa, ohimesama no doresu o kite shashin o toru no ga daisuki da.","german":"My daughter loves having her picture taken in a princess dress.","furigana":"むすめ|ひめさま|き|しゃしん|と|だいす"}],"notes":"姫様 \"princess\" is almost always used with the polite prefix お: お姫様. It is the word of fairy tales and the world of children, and also used affectionately for little girls; the official term for daughters of a royal house is 王女, or for the Japanese imperial family 皇女. Also well known is お姫様抱っこ, carrying someone in one's arms \"like a bride\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"光が粒子なのか波なのかを考えていて、昨日は眠れませんでした。","romaji":"Hikari ga ryūshi na no ka nami na no ka o kangaete ite, kinō wa nemuremasendeshita.","german":"I was thinking about whether light is a particle or a wave and couldn't sleep last night.","furigana":"ひかり|りゅうし|なみ|かんが|きのう|ねむ"},{"japanese":"この写真は粒子が粗いので、拡大するとぼやけてしまう。","romaji":"Kono shashin wa ryūshi ga arai node, kakudai suru to boyakete shimau.","german":"The grain of this photo is coarse, so it becomes blurry when enlarged.","furigana":"しゃしん|りゅうし|あら|かくだい"},{"japanese":"空気中の細かい粒子が肺に入ると、健康に悪い影響を与える。","romaji":"Kūkichū no komakai ryūshi ga hai ni hairu to, kenkō ni warui eikyō o ataeru.","german":"When fine particles in the air get into the lungs, they have a bad effect on health.","furigana":"くうきちゅう|こま|りゅうし|はい|はい|けんこう|わる|えいきょう|あた"}],"notes":"粒子 is a technical word for a particle or fine grain: in physics (素粒子 \"elementary particle\", 光の粒子), for fine dust in the air and in photography (粒子が粗い \"coarse-grained\"). In everyday speech, for grains people tend to say 粒 (つぶ). Note: the grammatical \"particle\" is not called 粒子 but 助詞.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今朝の強烈な雷雨で停電になったので、今はインターネットが使えません。","romaji":"Kesa no kyōretsu na raiu de teiden ni natta node, ima wa intānetto ga tsukaemasen.","german":"The violent thunderstorm this morning caused a power outage, so I can't use the internet right now.","furigana":"けさ|きょうれつ|らいう|ていでん|いま|つか"},{"japanese":"天気予報によると、午後から雷雨になるそうだ。","romaji":"Tenki yohō ni yoru to, gogo kara raiu ni naru sō da.","german":"According to the weather forecast, there will be thunderstorms from the afternoon.","furigana":"てんきよほう|ごご|らいう"},{"japanese":"突然の雷雨で、野外コンサートが中止になった。","romaji":"Totsuzen no raiu de, yagai konsāto ga chūshi ni natta.","german":"The open-air concert was canceled because of a sudden thunderstorm.","furigana":"とつぜん|らいう|やがい|ちゅうし"}],"notes":"雷雨 is a thunderstorm with rain – literally \"thunder and rain\". The word belongs more to the language of weather reports and news: 雷雨になる, 激しい雷雨, 雷雨注意報. In conversation people often simply say 雷が鳴って雨が降る; a short summer shower in the afternoon is 夕立. 雷 alone means \"thunder, lightning\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"小包を開けると、中には自動追尾魚雷が入っていた。","romaji":"Kozutsumi o akeru to, naka ni wa jidō tsuibi gyorai ga haitte ita.","german":"When I opened the package, there was a homing torpedo inside.","furigana":"こづつみ|あ|なか|じどうついびぎょらい|はい"},{"japanese":"潜水艦から発射された魚雷が、敵の船に命中した。","romaji":"Sensuikan kara hassha sareta gyorai ga, teki no fune ni meichū shita.","german":"The torpedo fired from the submarine hit the enemy ship.","furigana":"せんすいかん|はっしゃ|ぎょらい|てき|ふね|めいちゅう"},{"japanese":"その戦艦は魚雷を受けて、わずか数分で沈んだ。","romaji":"Sono senkan wa gyorai o ukete, wazuka sūfun de shizunda.","german":"The battleship was hit by a torpedo and sank within just a few minutes.","furigana":"せんかん|ぎょらい|う|すうふん|しず"}],"notes":"魚雷 is a torpedo, literally \"fish mine\". Typical combinations: 魚雷を発射する \"to fire a torpedo\", 魚雷が命中する \"a torpedo hits\", 魚雷を受ける \"to be hit by a torpedo\". Related words with 雷 in the sense of \"explosive device\" are 機雷 \"naval mine\" and 地雷 \"land mine\". The word appears mainly in history, news and war films.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"もし巨大な竜巻がこの地域を襲ったら、管制塔はどうなるんでしょうか。","romaji":"Moshi kyodai na tatsumaki ga kono chiiki o osottara, kanseitō wa dō naru n deshō ka.","german":"If a giant tornado hit this area, what would happen to the control tower?","furigana":"きょだい|たつまき|ちいき|おそ|かんせいとう"},{"japanese":"パイロットは管制塔の指示に従って着陸した。","romaji":"Pairotto wa kanseitō no shiji ni shitagatte chakuriku shita.","german":"The pilot landed following the control tower's instructions.","furigana":"かんせいとう|しじ|したが|ちゃくりく"},{"japanese":"空港の展望デッキから管制塔がよく見える。","romaji":"Kūkō no tenbō dekki kara kanseitō ga yoku mieru.","german":"You can see the control tower well from the airport's observation deck.","furigana":"くうこう|てんぼう|かんせいとう|み"}],"notes":"管制塔 is an airport's control tower, from which air traffic is directed. 管制 means \"control, direction\" (航空管制 \"air traffic control\"), and the air traffic controllers are called 管制官. Typical: 管制塔の指示に従う \"to follow the tower's instructions\". For a playmaker in sports in the figurative sense, people say 司令塔 instead, not 管制塔.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"バベルの塔は、旧約聖書に出てくる話です。","romaji":"Baberu no tō wa, Kyūyaku Seisho ni dete kuru hanashi desu.","german":"Der Turmbau zu Babel ist eine Geschichte aus dem Alten Testament.","furigana":"とう|きゅうやくせいしょ|で|はなし"},{"japanese":"天まで届くバベルの塔を建てようとした人々は、言葉が通じなくなったという。","romaji":"Ten made todoku Baberu no tō o tateyō to shita hitobito wa, kotoba ga tsūjinaku natta to iu.","german":"Die Menschen, die einen Turm zu Babel bis in den Himmel bauen wollten, sollen einander nicht mehr verstanden haben.","furigana":"てん|とど|とう|た|ひとびと|ことば|つう"},{"japanese":"その計画は、いつまでも完成しないバベルの塔のようだ。","romaji":"Sono keikaku wa, itsu made mo kansei shinai Baberu no tō no yō da.","german":"Dieses Projekt ist wie ein Turm zu Babel, der nie fertig wird.","furigana":"けいかく|かんせい|とう"}],"notes":"バベルの塔 ist der Turm zu Babel aus dem Alten Testament (旧約聖書): Die Menschen wollten einen Turm bis in den Himmel bauen, und Gott verwirrte daraufhin ihre Sprache. Im Japanischen wird der Ausdruck wie im Deutschen auch bildlich gebraucht – für ein maßloses, zum Scheitern verurteiltes Vorhaben oder für Sprachverwirrung. Die Teile sind バベル „Babel“, の und 塔 „Turm“.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"エッフェル塔のどんなところが好きでしたか？","romaji":"Efferutō no donna tokoro ga suki deshita ka?","german":"What did you like about the Eiffel Tower?","furigana":"とう|す"},{"japanese":"パリに行ったら、エッフェル塔に登ってみたい。","romaji":"Pari ni ittara, Efferu-tō ni nobotte mitai.","german":"If I go to Paris, I want to climb the Eiffel Tower.","furigana":"い|とう|のぼ"},{"japanese":"ホテルの部屋からエッフェル塔がよく見えました。","romaji":"Hoteru no heya kara Efferu-tō ga yoku miemashita.","german":"We had a great view of the Eiffel Tower from the hotel room.","furigana":"へや|とう|み"}],"notes":"エッフェル塔 is the name of the Parisian landmark; 塔 (とう) means \"tower\" and is attached to the proper name, as in ピサの斜塔. For 東京タワー, on the other hand, the English タワー is used. Typical are エッフェル塔に登る \"to climb the Eiffel Tower\" and エッフェル塔が見える \"the Eiffel Tower is visible\". The spelling mixes katakana for the name with the kanji 塔.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"犯罪者を取り逃がすふりをするのは、ちょっとやり過ぎじゃない？","romaji":"Hanzaisha o torinigasu furi o suru no wa, chotto yari sugi ja nai?","german":"Don't you think pretending you couldn't catch the criminal is a bit overdoing it?","furigana":"はんざいしゃ|と|に|す"},{"japanese":"警察は犯人をあと一歩のところで取り逃がした。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa hannin o ato ippo no tokoro de torinigashita.","german":"The police let the culprit slip away at the last moment.","furigana":"けいさつ|はんにん|いっぽ|と|に"},{"japanese":"せっかくのチャンスを取り逃がしてしまい、とても悔しい。","romaji":"Sekkaku no chansu o torinigashite shimai, totemo kuyashii.","german":"I let a precious chance slip away, and I'm really frustrated.","furigana":"と|に|くや"}],"notes":"取り逃がす means to let someone or something you had almost caught get away: 犯人を取り逃がす \"to let the culprit escape\", figuratively also チャンスを取り逃がす \"to miss a chance\". It is transitive (〜を取り逃がす); the one who escapes is described with 逃げる. Unlike 逃がす \"to set free\" (intentionally), 取り逃がす emphasizes unintentional failure; 逃す (のがす) is the general \"to miss\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「さぁ、そろそろ寝床に就く時間じゃぞ。」子供の頃、毎晩祖父にこう言われました。","romaji":"\"Sā, sorosoro nedoko ni tsuku jikan ja zo.\" Kodomo no koro, maiban sofu ni kō iwaremashita.","german":"\"Well, it's about time to go to bed.\" My grandfather said this to me every night when I was a child.","furigana":"ねどこ|つ|じかん|こども|ころ|まいばんそふ|い"},{"japanese":"猫の寝床に古い毛布を敷いてあげた。","romaji":"Neko no nedoko ni furui mōfu o shiite ageta.","german":"I laid an old blanket in the cat's bed for it.","furigana":"ねこ|ねどこ|ふる|もうふ|し"},{"japanese":"キャンプ場に着いたら、まず寝床を確保しよう。","romaji":"Kyanpujō ni tsuitara, mazu nedoko o kakuho shiyō.","german":"When we arrive at the campsite, let's first secure a place to sleep.","furigana":"じょう|つ|ねどこ|かくほ"}],"notes":"寝床 is the place to sleep, that is, a bed or a laid-out futon, of any kind. Typical phrases: 寝床に入る \"to go to bed\", more elevated 寝床に就く, and 寝床を作る \"to prepare the sleeping place\". In everyday speech people usually just say ベッド or 布団; 寝床 sounds somewhat old-fashioned or is used for animals (犬の寝床). Do not confuse it with 寝室 \"bedroom\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"中々手堅い仕事みたいだから、葬儀屋になるための勉強をしようかなって思ってるんだよね。","romaji":"Nakanaka tegatai shigoto mitai da kara, sougiya ni naru tame no benkyou o shiyou ka natte omotteru n da yo ne.","german":"It seems like a pretty secure job, so I'm thinking of studying to become a funeral director.","furigana":"なかなかてがた|しごと|そうぎや|べんきょう|おも"},{"japanese":"彼は派手さはないが、手堅いプレーでチームを支えている。","romaji":"Kare wa hadesa wa nai ga, tegatai purē de chīmu o sasaete iru.","german":"He isn't flashy, but he supports the team with his solid play.","furigana":"かれ|はで|てがた|ささ"},{"japanese":"今回は冒険せず、手堅い投資を選んだ。","romaji":"Konkai wa bōken sezu, tegatai tōshi o eranda.","german":"This time I didn't take risks and chose a solid investment.","furigana":"こんかい|ぼうけん|てがた|とうし|えら"}],"notes":"手堅い describes something solid, low-risk and dependable: 手堅い仕事 \"a secure job\", 手堅い投資 \"a solid investment\", 手堅いプレー \"solid, error-free play\". Adverbially: 手堅く勝つ \"to win safely\". Closely related is 堅実 \"solid, conscientious\"; 安全 rather means freedom from danger, and 確実 certainty. Applied to people, it praises a careful, reliable way of working.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"昨夜車上荒らしにあって、マックを盗まれました。","romaji":"Sakuya shajou arashi ni atte, Makku o nusumaremashita.","german":"My car was broken into last night and my Mac was stolen.","furigana":"さくやしゃじょうあ|ぬす"},{"japanese":"イノシシが畑を荒らして、野菜がほとんどだめになった。","romaji":"Inoshishi ga hatake o arashite, yasai ga hotondo dame ni natta.","german":"Wild boars ravaged the field, and almost all the vegetables are gone.","furigana":"はたけ|あ|やさい"},{"japanese":"ネットの掲示板を荒らす人がいて困っている。","romaji":"Netto no keijiban o arasu hito ga ite komatte iru.","german":"Someone is trolling our online forum, and it's a real problem.","furigana":"けいじばん|あ|ひと|こま"}],"notes":"荒らす means \"to ravage, to cause damage\": イノシシが畑を荒らす \"wild boars ravage the field\". Also \"to break in, to plunder\" - hence nouns like 車上荒らし \"car break-in\" or 事務所荒らし \"office burglary\". Colloquially, 荒らす on the internet means \"to troll, to spam\" (掲示板を荒らす), a troll is called 荒らし. It is transitive; the intransitive counterpart is 荒れる (海が荒れる, 肌が荒れる).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一寸は一尺の十分の一で、約3.03センチです。","romaji":"Issun wa isshaku no juubun no ichi de, yaku san ten zero san senchi desu.","german":"One sun is one-tenth of a shaku, about 3.03 centimeters."},{"japanese":"一寸法師は、背が一寸しかない男の子の昔話だ。","romaji":"Issunbōshi wa, se ga issun shika nai otoko no ko no mukashibanashi da.","german":"\"Issun-bōshi\" is a folktale about a boy who is only one sun tall.","furigana":"いっすんぼうし|せ|いっすん|おとこ|こ|むかしばなし"},{"japanese":"一寸先は闇というから、少しは貯金しておいたほうがいい。","romaji":"Issun saki wa yami to iu kara, sukoshi wa chokin shite oita hō ga ii.","german":"They say you can't see even an inch ahead, so you'd better save up a little money.","furigana":"いっすんさき|やみ|すこ|ちょきん"}],"notes":"寸 is an old Japanese unit of length: one-tenth of a 尺, about 3.03 cm. Today it is met mainly in fixed words and sayings: 一寸法師 (the \"inch-high\" boy from the fairy tale), 一寸先は闇 \"the future is uncertain\", 寸前 \"just before\" and 寸法 \"measurements\". In traditional crafts, such as with carpenters, measurements are still made in 寸 and 尺. Note: 一寸 can also be read as ateji for ちょっと.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の会社は破産寸前である。","romaji":"Watashi no kaisha wa hasan sunzen dearu.","german":"My company is on the verge of bankruptcy.","furigana":"わたし|かいしゃ|はさんすんぜん"},{"japanese":"電車のドアが閉まる寸前に、なんとか乗ることができた。","romaji":"Densha no doa ga shimaru sunzen ni, nantoka noru koto ga dekita.","german":"I somehow managed to get on the train just before the doors closed.","furigana":"でんしゃ|し|すんぜん|の"},{"japanese":"ゴール寸前で転んでしまい、優勝を逃した。","romaji":"Gōru sunzen de koronde shimai, yūshō o nogashita.","german":"I fell just before the finish line and missed out on the victory.","furigana":"すんぜん|ころ|ゆうしょう|のが"}],"notes":"寸前 means \"immediately before, just before\" and often emphasizes that something (usually negative) almost happened. It stands after nouns as a suffix (破産寸前, 倒産寸前) or after the dictionary form of a verb (閉まる寸前に). More neutral and temporal is 直前 \"right before\"; 寸前 sounds more dramatic, like \"hanging in the balance\". Grammatically it is a noun, not a particle.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"落雷が直撃したのに、奇跡的に無傷だった。","romaji":"Rakurai ga chokugeki shita noni, kisekiteki ni mukizu datta.","german":"Even though I took a direct lightning strike, I miraculously escaped unharmed.","furigana":"らくらい|ちょくげき|きせきてき|むきず"},{"japanese":"落雷のため、電車は現在運転を見合わせています。","romaji":"Rakurai no tame, densha wa genzai unten o miawasete imasu.","german":"Due to a lightning strike, train service is currently suspended.","furigana":"らくらい|でんしゃ|げんざいうんてん|みあ"},{"japanese":"昨日、ゴルフ場で落雷による事故が起きた。","romaji":"Kinō, gorufujō de rakurai ni yoru jiko ga okita.","german":"Yesterday an accident caused by lightning occurred at a golf course.","furigana":"きのう|じょう|らくらい|じこ|お"}],"notes":"落雷 is a lightning strike. The word is rather literary and typical of news and announcements (落雷のため運転見合わせ, 落雷による停電); in conversation people say 雷が落ちる. As an intransitive する-verb, 落雷する is possible but rare. To be struck by lightning is 雷に打たれる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大きなプロジェクトの一翼を担えることになりました。","romaji":"Ōkina purojekuto no ichiyoku o ninaeru koto ni narimashita.","german":"I've been given the chance to take on a part of a big project.","furigana":"おお|いちよく|にな"},{"japanese":"地域の防災活動の一翼を担っているのは、ボランティアの人たちだ。","romaji":"Chiiki no bōsai katsudō no ichiyoku o ninatte iru no wa, borantia no hitotachi da.","german":"Volunteers are the ones who play a part in the area's disaster prevention activities.","furigana":"ちいき|ぼうさいかつどう|いちよく|にな|ひと"},{"japanese":"日本の自動車産業は、経済成長の一翼を担ってきた。","romaji":"Nihon no jidōsha sangyō wa, keizai seichō no ichiyoku o ninatte kita.","german":"Japan's automobile industry has played its part in economic growth.","furigana":"にほん|じどうしゃさんぎょう|けいざいせいちょう|いちよく|にな"}],"notes":"一翼 literally means \"one wing\", but it is used almost only figuratively in the fixed phrase 〜の一翼を担う: \"to take on a part (of a task), to play a role\". The expression is elevated and typical of speeches, corporate texts and news. Related in meaning but more everyday are 役割を果たす and 一部を担当する.","levelNote":"Compound: 翼 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二遊間、外野の両翼のポジションはまだ空いています。","romaji":"Niyūkan, gaiya no ryōyoku no pojishon wa mada aite imasu.","german":"The positions between second base and shortstop and in left and right field are still open.","furigana":"にゆうかん|がいや|りょうよく|あ"},{"japanese":"ワシは両翼を大きく広げて飛び立った。","romaji":"Washi wa ryōyoku o ōkiku hirogete tobitatta.","german":"The eagle spread both wings wide and took off.","furigana":"りょうよく|おお|ひろ|と|た"},{"japanese":"この飛行機は、両翼にエンジンが一つずつ付いている。","romaji":"Kono hikōki wa, ryōyoku ni enjin ga hitotsu zutsu tsuite iru.","german":"This airplane has one engine on each wing.","furigana":"ひこうき|りょうよく|ひと|つ"}],"notes":"両翼 means \"both wings\": of birds and airplanes, but also the two flanks of an army or the side wings of a building. In baseball, 両翼 means the left and right outfield (両翼100メートルの球場). The counterpart is 片翼 \"one wing\". Typical: 両翼を広げる \"to spread the wings\".","levelNote":"Compound: 翼 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"最近は毎朝、裂けるチーズを食べています。","romaji":"Saikin wa maiasa, sakeru chīzu o tabete imasu.","german":"Lately I've been eating string cheese every morning.","furigana":"さいきん|まいあさ|さ|た"},{"japanese":"落雷で大きな木が真っ二つに裂けた。","romaji":"Rakurai de ōkina ki ga mapputatsu ni saketa.","german":"The lightning split the big tree right in two.","furigana":"らくらい|おお|き|ま|ぷた|さ"},{"japanese":"その秘密は、口が裂けても言えない。","romaji":"Sono himitsu wa, kuchi ga sakete mo ienai.","german":"I wouldn't tell that secret for anything.","furigana":"ひみつ|くち|さ|い"}],"notes":"裂ける is intransitive and means \"to rip open, split (along a line or fiber)\": 木が裂ける, 布が裂ける, 地面が裂ける. The transitive counterpart is 裂く (intensified 引き裂く). Paper or clothing getting holes is more 破れる; glass or dishes 割れる. Well known is the idiom 口が裂けても言えない \"I won't say it at any price\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"北斗七星は七つの星で構成されています。","romaji":"Hokuto shichisei wa nanatsu no hoshi de kousei sarete imasu.","german":"The Big Dipper is made up of seven stars.","furigana":"ほくとしちせい|なな|ほし|こうせい"},{"japanese":"北斗七星を目印にすると、北極星が見つけやすい。","romaji":"Hokuto shichisei o mejirushi ni suru to, hokkyokusei ga mitsukeyasui.","german":"If you use the Big Dipper as a guide, it's easy to find the North Star.","furigana":"ほくとしちせい|めじるし|ほっきょくせい|み"},{"japanese":"春の夜空に、北斗七星がはっきり見えた。","romaji":"Haru no yozora ni, Hokuto shichisei ga hakkiri mieta.","german":"I could clearly see the Big Dipper in the spring night sky.","furigana":"はる|よぞら|ほくとしちせい|み"}],"notes":"北斗七星 is the Big Dipper – literally \"the seven stars of the northern dipper\" (北斗). It belongs to the constellation おおぐま座 \"Great Bear\". A typical remark is that with its help one can find the North Star 北極星. The word is neutral and appears in school, astronomy and mythology.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一斗缶は洗剤や石油などを保管するためのものです。","romaji":"Ittokan wa senzai ya sekiyu nado o hokan suru tame no mono desu.","german":"An 18-liter can is used to store things such as detergent and oil.","furigana":"いっとかん|せんざい|せきゆ|ほかん"},{"japanese":"業務用の油は、一斗缶で売られていることが多い。","romaji":"Gyōmuyō no abura wa, ittokan de urarete iru koto ga ōi.","german":"Cooking oil for commercial use is often sold in 18-liter cans.","furigana":"ぎょうむよう|あぶら|いっとかん|う|おお"},{"japanese":"工場の隅に、空の一斗缶がいくつも積まれていた。","romaji":"Kōjō no sumi ni, kara no ittokan ga ikutsu mo tsumarete ita.","german":"In a corner of the factory, many empty 18-liter cans were stacked.","furigana":"こうじょう|すみ|から|いっとかん|つ"}],"notes":"一斗缶 is a square tin can with a capacity of 18 liters. 斗 is an old unit of volume (10 升, about 18 l) that today lives on practically only in this word. 一斗缶 are used for wholesale cooking oil, paint, solvents or kerosene. Note the reading with gemination: いっとかん.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"川底にはたくさんの土砂がたまっている。","romaji":"Kawazoko ni wa takusan no dosha ga tamatte iru.","german":"A lot of sediment has accumulated on the riverbed.","furigana":"かわぞこ|どしゃ"},{"japanese":"大雨で土砂崩れが起き、道路が通れなくなった。","romaji":"Ōame de dosha kuzure ga oki, dōro ga tōrenaku natta.","german":"Heavy rain caused a landslide, and the road became impassable.","furigana":"おおあめ|どしゃくず|お|どうろ|とお"},{"japanese":"トラックが工事現場から土砂を運び出している。","romaji":"Torakku ga kōji genba kara dosha o hakobidashite iru.","german":"Trucks are carrying soil away from the construction site.","furigana":"こうじげんば|どしゃ|はこ|だ"}],"notes":"土砂 means \"earth and sand\", i.e. loose soil, rubble and sediment. It is very common in disaster reports: 土砂崩れ \"landslide\", 土砂災害 \"landslide and debris flow disaster\". At construction sites people speak of 土砂を運ぶ／積む. The word 土砂降り \"downpour\" is written with the same characters, but is a figurative exception.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"将来の選択肢は多い方がいい。","romaji":"Shōrai no sentakushi wa ōi hō ga ii.","german":"The more options you have for the future, the better.","furigana":"しょうらい|せんたくし|おお|ほう"},{"japanese":"次の四つの選択肢から、正しいものを一つ選びなさい。","romaji":"Tsugi no yottsu no sentakushi kara, tadashii mono o hitotsu erabinasai.","german":"Choose the correct answer from the following four options.","furigana":"つぎ|よっ|せんたくし|ただ|ひと|えら"},{"japanese":"今の状況では、断るという選択肢はない。","romaji":"Ima no jōkyō de wa, kotowaru to iu sentakushi wa nai.","german":"In the current situation, declining is not an option.","furigana":"いま|じょうきょう|ことわ|せんたくし"}],"notes":"選択肢 are the possibilities available to choose from – originally the answer options in multiple-choice questions, today quite generally \"option\". Typical: 選択肢が多い／限られる, 選択肢から選ぶ and 〜という選択肢はない \"... is out of the question\". 選択, on the other hand, is the act of choosing itself. In business language, オプション is also used.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その星は地球から約四光年離れている。","romaji":"Sono hoshi wa chikyū kara yaku yon kōnen hanarete iru.","german":"That star is about four light-years from Earth.","furigana":"ほし|ちきゅう|やくよんこうねんはな"},{"japanese":"一光年とは、光が一年間に進む距離のことだ。","romaji":"Ikkōnen to wa, hikari ga ichinenkan ni susumu kyori no koto da.","german":"A light-year is the distance light travels in one year.","furigana":"いっこうねん|ひかり|いちねんかん|すす|きょり"},{"japanese":"彼の技術は、僕なんかより何光年も先を行っている。","romaji":"Kare no gijutsu wa, boku nanka yori nan kōnen mo saki o itte iru.","german":"His skill is light-years ahead of mine.","furigana":"かれ|ぎじゅつ|ぼく|なんこうねん|さき|い"}],"notes":"光年 is a unit of length in astronomy: the distance light travels in a year – so not a time span, even though 年 is in it. It stands directly after numbers (4光年, 何光年) and is usually used with 離れている or 先. In an exaggerated figurative sense people also say 何光年も先を行っている \"to be light-years ahead\". Grammatically 光年 is a noun or counter unit, not a particle.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"核廃絶運動家たちは、核兵器を廃絶することは実現可能だと信じている。","romaji":"Kaku haizetsu undōkatachi wa, kakuheiki o haizetsu suru koto wa jitsugen kanō da to shinjite iru.","german":"Anti-nuclear activists believe that abolishing nuclear weapons is achievable.","furigana":"かくはいぜつうんどうか|かくへいき|はいぜつ|じつげんかのう|しん"},{"japanese":"世界から差別を廃絶するには、教育が欠かせない。","romaji":"Sekai kara sabetsu o haizetsu suru ni wa, kyōiku ga kakasenai.","german":"Education is indispensable for eradicating discrimination from the world.","furigana":"せかい|さべつ|はいぜつ|きょういく|か"},{"japanese":"跡を継ぐ人がいなくなり、その名家は廃絶した。","romaji":"Ato o tsugu hito ga inaku nari, sono meika wa haizetsu shita.","german":"With no one left to carry on, that distinguished family died out.","furigana":"あと|つ|ひと|めいか|はいぜつ"}],"notes":"廃絶 transitively means \"to abolish completely, eliminate\"; it is a formal word, used above all in 核兵器の廃絶 or 核廃絶 \"nuclear disarmament\" and 差別の廃絶. Intransitively it can also mean \"to die out, become extinct\" (家が廃絶する). To distinguish: 廃止 \"to abolish\" (laws, institutions), 撤廃 \"to repeal\" (rules, tariffs), 根絶 \"to eradicate\" (diseases).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"うちに知らない人を泊めるのは嫌です。","romaji":"Uchi ni shiranai hito o tomeru no wa iya desu.","german":"I don't like letting strangers stay at my place.","furigana":"し|ひと|と|いや"},{"japanese":"終電を逃した友達を、一晩うちに泊めてあげた。","romaji":"Shūden o nogashita tomodachi o, hitoban uchi ni tomete ageta.","german":"I let a friend who had missed the last train stay at my place for the night.","furigana":"しゅうでん|のが|ともだち|ひとばん|と"},{"japanese":"台風が来るので、船を港に泊めておいた。","romaji":"Taifū ga kuru node, fune o minato ni tomete oita.","german":"A typhoon was coming, so we left the boat moored in the harbor.","furigana":"たいふう|く|ふね|みなと|と"}],"notes":"泊める is the transitive counterpart of 泊まる: to let someone stay overnight at one's home, to put someone up. Typical: 友達を家に泊める, 一晩泊めてもらう \"to be allowed to stay overnight at someone's place\". Less often it also means \"to anchor a ship\" (船を港に泊める). Do not confuse it with the identically read 止める \"to stop\" and 停める \"to park\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あのカップルの仲をうまく引き裂く事ができたら、お知らせ致します。","romaji":"Ano kappuru no naka o umaku hikisaku koto ga dekitara, oshirase itashimasu.","german":"If I manage to tear that couple apart, I'll let you know.","furigana":"なか|ひ|さ|こと|し|いた"},{"japanese":"怒った彼女は、その手紙をびりびりに引き裂いた。","romaji":"Okotta kanojo wa, sono tegami o biribiri ni hikisaita.","german":"In her anger, she tore the letter to shreds.","furigana":"おこ|かのじょ|てがみ|ひ|さ"},{"japanese":"戦争で引き裂かれた家族が、五十年ぶりに再会した。","romaji":"Sensō de hikisakareta kazoku ga, gojūnen-buri ni saikai shita.","german":"A family torn apart by the war was reunited after fifty years.","furigana":"せんそう|ひ|さ|かぞく|ごじゅうねん|さいかい"}],"notes":"引き裂く is an intensified form of 裂く: to tear something apart by force (手紙を引き裂く, びりびりに引き裂く). Figuratively it means to tear people apart by force: 二人の仲を引き裂く, 戦争で引き裂かれた家族. The intransitive counterpart is 裂ける. For simply tearing paper, people also say 破る in everyday speech.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"飛行機で巨人を襲撃することができるようになるとは、知る由もなかった。","romaji":"Hikōki de kyojin o shūgeki suru koto ga dekiru yō ni naru to wa, shiru yoshi mo nakatta.","german":"They had no idea that they could attack Titans from the air.","furigana":"ひこうき|きょじん|しゅうげき|し|よし"},{"japanese":"武装したグループが警察署を襲撃した。","romaji":"Busō shita gurūpu ga keisatsusho o shūgeki shita.","german":"An armed group raided a police station.","furigana":"ぶそう|けいさつしょ|しゅうげき"},{"japanese":"夜明け前に敵の基地を襲撃する計画が立てられた。","romaji":"Yoake mae ni teki no kichi o shūgeki suru keikaku ga taterareta.","german":"A plan was devised to raid the enemy base before dawn.","furigana":"よあ|まえ|てき|きち|しゅうげき|けいかく|た"}],"notes":"襲撃する means \"to raid, to carry out an attack\", usually by surprise and targeted at a place or person: 基地を襲撃する, 襲撃を受ける \"to be attacked\". The word is written-language and typical of news and war reports. More general is 攻撃 \"attack\" (also in sports); the native verb 襲う sounds more everyday and is also used for animals or forces of nature.","levelNote":"Derived from 襲撃 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本では、遠距離通学や遠距離通勤はよくあることです。","romaji":"Nippon de wa, enkyori tsūgaku ya enkyori tsūkin wa yoku aru koto desu.","german":"In Japan, long commutes to school or work are quite common.","furigana":"にっぽん|えんきょりつうがく|えんきょりつうきん"},{"japanese":"彼女とは今、東京と大阪で遠距離恋愛をしている。","romaji":"Kanojo to wa ima, Tōkyō to Ōsaka de enkyori ren'ai o shite iru.","german":"My girlfriend and I are in a long-distance relationship between Tokyo and Osaka right now.","furigana":"かのじょ|いま|とうきょう|おおさか|えんきょりれんあい"},{"japanese":"遠距離の運転は疲れるので、途中で何度か休憩した。","romaji":"Enkyori no unten wa tsukareru node, tochū de nando ka kyūkei shita.","german":"Long-distance driving is tiring, so we took several breaks on the way.","furigana":"えんきょり|うんてん|つか|とちゅう|なんど|きゅうけい"}],"notes":"遠距離 means \"a great distance, long haul\" and usually appears as the first element of compounds: 遠距離通勤 \"long commute\", 遠距離恋愛 (short 遠恋) \"long-distance relationship\", 遠距離バス. As an attribute, の is used: 遠距離の運転. The opposite is 近距離; 長距離 emphasizes more the length of a route (長距離走 \"long-distance run\"). Grammatically it is a noun, not an adjective.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"タクシーの運ちゃんは大体みんな近距離乗車は嫌がるよね。","romaji":"Takushī no unchan wa daitai minna kinkyori jōsha wa iyagaru yo ne.","german":"Taxi drivers generally dislike short-distance rides, don't they?","furigana":"うん|だいたい|きんきょりじょうしゃ|いや"},{"japanese":"近距離なら、電車より自転車のほうが速い。","romaji":"Kinkyori nara, densha yori jitensha no hō ga hayai.","german":"For short distances, a bicycle is faster than the train.","furigana":"きんきょり|でんしゃ|じてんしゃ|はや"},{"japanese":"その写真は、かなりの近距離から撮影されたものだ。","romaji":"Sono shashin wa, kanari no kinkyori kara satsuei sareta mono da.","german":"That photo was taken from a fairly close distance.","furigana":"しゃしん|きんきょり|さつえい"}],"notes":"近距離 is the \"short distance, close range\". It often appears in compounds like 近距離乗車 \"short ride\" or 近距離通勤, and with から／で: 近距離から撮影する. For \"from the very closest range\" there is 至近距離. The opposite is 遠距離; 短距離, on the other hand, emphasizes the shortness of a route (短距離走 \"sprint\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は高校時代、短距離の選手だった。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kōkō jidai, tankyori no senshu datta.","german":"She was a sprinter in high school.","furigana":"かのじょ|こうこうじだい|たんきょり|せんしゅ"},{"japanese":"短距離なら、飛行機より新幹線のほうが便利です。","romaji":"Tankyori nara, hikōki yori shinkansen no hō ga benri desu.","german":"For short distances, the Shinkansen is more convenient than the airplane.","furigana":"たんきょり|ひこうき|しんかんせん|べんり"},{"japanese":"このドローンは短距離しか飛べない。","romaji":"Kono dorōn wa tankyori shika tobenai.","german":"This drone can only fly short distances.","furigana":"たんきょり|と"}],"notes":"短距離 means \"short distance\" and is the opposite of 長距離. In sports it appears in 短距離走 \"sprint\" and 短距離選手 \"sprinter\", in transport in 短距離便 \"short-haul flight\", in the military in 短距離ミサイル. For \"near, a short way\" in everyday speech, people tend to say 近い; 短距離 sounds matter-of-fact and technical, and often stands as a compound or with の before nouns.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"結婚式の前に入籍をする予定です。","romaji":"Kekkonshiki no mae ni nyūseki o suru yotei desu.","german":"We plan to register our marriage before the wedding ceremony.","furigana":"けっこんしき|まえ|にゅうせき|よてい"},{"japanese":"人気俳優が、一般女性と入籍したと発表した。","romaji":"Ninki haiyū ga, ippan josei to nyūseki shita to happyō shita.","german":"A popular actor announced that he had registered his marriage with a non-celebrity woman.","furigana":"にんきはいゆう|いっぱんじょせい|にゅうせき|はっぴょう"},{"japanese":"二人は先月入籍し、来年の春に結婚式を挙げるそうだ。","romaji":"Futari wa sengetsu nyūseki shi, rainen no haru ni kekkonshiki o ageru sō da.","german":"The two registered their marriage last month and reportedly plan to hold a wedding ceremony next spring.","furigana":"ふたり|せんげつにゅうせき|らいねん|はる|けっこんしき|あ"}],"notes":"入籍 literally means \"to enter into a family register (戸籍)\". Colloquially and in celebrity news, it almost always means a legal marriage, i.e. filing the 婚姻届. Typical: 〜と入籍する, 入籍を発表する; synonymous and more casual is 籍を入れる. The wedding ceremony (結婚式) is separate and can take place before or after.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地球がじきに滅びるんだとしたら、どうしてベーコンを食べるのを我慢しなくちゃいけないの？","romaji":"Chikyū ga jiki ni horobiru n da to shitara, dōshite bēkon o taberu no o gaman shinakucha ikenai no?","german":"If the Earth is going to perish, why should I give up bacon?","furigana":"ちきゅう|ほろ|た|がまん"},{"japanese":"恐竜はなぜ滅びたのか、今も研究が続いている。","romaji":"Kyōryū wa naze horobita no ka, ima mo kenkyū ga tsuzuite iru.","german":"Why the dinosaurs went extinct is still being researched today.","furigana":"きょうりゅう|ほろ|いま|けんきゅう|つづ"},{"japanese":"栄えた古代都市も、やがて戦争で滅びてしまった。","romaji":"Sakaeta kodai toshi mo, yagate sensō de horobite shimatta.","german":"Even the flourishing ancient city eventually perished through war.","furigana":"さか|こだいとし|せんそう|ほろ"}],"notes":"滅びる means \"to perish, to go to ruin, to go extinct\" and is used for empires, peoples, cultures, species or the world: 国が滅びる, 恐竜が滅びた. It is intransitive; transitively it is 滅ぼす \"to destroy\". There is also the synonymous godan form 滅ぶ. For the extinction of species in the technical sense, 絶滅 is usual.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"心理的硬直は問題を解決する妨げになると思われる。","romaji":"Shinriteki kouchoku wa mondai o kaiketsu suru samatage ni naru to omowareru.","german":"Psychological rigidity is thought to hinder solving problems.","furigana":"しんりてきこうちょく|もんだい|かいけつ|さまた|おも"},{"japanese":"緊張のあまり、体が硬直して声が出なかった。","romaji":"Kinchō no amari, karada ga kōchoku shite koe ga denakatta.","german":"I was so nervous that my body froze and I couldn't speak.","furigana":"きんちょう|からだ|こうちょく|こえ|で"},{"japanese":"死後硬直の状態から、死亡時刻が推定された。","romaji":"Shigo kōchoku no jōtai kara, shibō jikoku ga suitei sareta.","german":"The time of death was estimated from the state of rigor mortis.","furigana":"しごこうちょく|じょうたい|しぼうじこく|すいてい"}],"notes":"硬直 is stiffening or becoming rigid: physically (体が硬直する from fear or tension, 死後硬直 \"rigor mortis\") and figuratively for rigid, inflexible structures (硬直した組織, 考え方が硬直している). For the development toward rigidity, people say 硬直化. As an intransitive する-verb it takes が. More everyday for muscles are 固まる or こわばる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「ゴキブリ」という名前を聞くだけで、鳥肌が立ちます。","romaji":"\"Gokiburi\" to iu namae o kiku dake de, torihada ga tachimasu.","german":"I get goose bumps just from hearing the name \"cockroach\".","furigana":"なまえ|き|とりはだ|た"},{"japanese":"寒くて、腕に鳥肌が立っている。","romaji":"Samukute, ude ni torihada ga tatte iru.","german":"It's cold, and I have goose bumps on my arms.","furigana":"さむ|うで|とりはだ|た"},{"japanese":"あの歌手の生の歌声を聞いて、鳥肌が立った。","romaji":"Ano kashu no nama no utagoe o kiite, torihada ga tatta.","german":"Hearing that singer's live voice gave me goose bumps.","furigana":"かしゅ|なま|うたごえ|き|とりはだ|た"}],"notes":"鳥肌 is goose bumps – literally \"bird skin\". It almost always appears in the phrase 鳥肌が立つ \"to get goose bumps\". Originally it referred only to cold, fear or disgust; today it is also said for emotion and enthusiasm (鳥肌が立つほど感動した), which some language purists criticize as misuse. Informally, something thrilling is called 鳥肌もの \"something that gives you goose bumps\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このタオルはとても柔らかくて心地よい肌触りです。","romaji":"Kono taoru wa totemo yawarakakute kokochiyoi hadazawari desu.","german":"This towel is very soft and feels pleasant on the skin.","furigana":"やわ|ここち|はだざわ"},{"japanese":"肌触りのいいパジャマを探しています。","romaji":"Hadazawari no ii pajama o sagashite imasu.","german":"I'm looking for pajamas that feel good against the skin.","furigana":"はだざわ|さが"},{"japanese":"このセーターはチクチクして肌触りが悪い。","romaji":"Kono sētā wa chikuchiku shite hadazawari ga warui.","german":"This sweater is scratchy and feels unpleasant.","furigana":"はだざわ|わる"}],"notes":"肌触り is the feeling a material leaves on the skin: 肌触りがいい／悪い, 肌触りが柔らかい／滑らか. It is used above all for fabrics, clothing, towels and bedding. Related words are 手触り (when touching with the hand), 舌触り (in the mouth) and 歯ごたえ (when chewing). Rarely, it also refers to the way someone comes across to others.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この絵の女性の肌色、すごく綺麗だね。","romaji":"Kono e no josei no hadairo, sugoku kirei da ne.","german":"The skin tone of the woman in this painting is really beautiful.","furigana":"え|じょせい|はだいろ|きれい"},{"japanese":"自分の肌色に合ったファンデーションを選ぶのは難しい。","romaji":"Jibun no hadairo ni atta fandēshon o erabu no wa muzukashii.","german":"It's hard to choose a foundation that matches your own skin tone.","furigana":"じぶん|はだいろ|あ|えら|むずか"},{"japanese":"最近のクレヨンでは、「肌色」の代わりに「うすだいだい」という名前が使われている。","romaji":"Saikin no kureyon de wa, \"hadairo\" no kawari ni \"usudaidai\" to iu namae ga tsukawarete iru.","german":"In today's crayons, the name 「うすだいだい」 is used instead of 「肌色」.","furigana":"さいきん|はだいろ|か|なまえ|つか"}],"notes":"肌色 has two meanings: one's own skin color or skin tone (自分の肌色に合うファンデーション) and, as a color name, a light beige-orange. Because the color name is considered exclusionary, the colored pencil has mostly been called うすだいだい or ペールオレンジ since about 2000. For skin tone, people also say 肌の色. Grammatically 肌色 is a noun, not an adjective (肌色の).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"太平洋上には小舟がひしめきあっていた。","romaji":"Taiheiyōjō ni wa kobune ga hishimekiatte ita.","german":"The Pacific Ocean was crowded with small boats.","furigana":"たいへいようじょう|こぶね"},{"japanese":"私たちは小舟に乗って、ゆっくり川を下った。","romaji":"Watashitachi wa kobune ni notte, yukkuri kawa o kudatta.","german":"We rode in a small boat and slowly went down the river.","furigana":"わたし|こぶね|の|かわ|くだ"},{"japanese":"嵐の海で、小舟は木の葉のように揺れた。","romaji":"Arashi no umi de, kobune wa ko no ha no yō ni yureta.","german":"On the stormy sea, the small boat tossed like a leaf.","furigana":"あらし|うみ|こぶね|こ|は|ゆ"}],"notes":"小舟 is a small boat or skiff, often rowed or sailed; you go with 小舟に乗る. The character 舟 stands for small, simple boats, whereas 船 refers to large ships. In modern everyday speech, for rowboats people tend to say ボート; 小舟 sounds somewhat literary and often appears in narratives and comparisons (木の葉のように揺れる小舟).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"正午になると鐘が12回鳴るんです。","romaji":"Shougo ni naru to kane ga juunikai naru n desu.","german":"The bell rings twelve times at noon.","furigana":"しょうご|かね|かいな"},{"japanese":"大晦日の夜、近くのお寺へ除夜の鐘をつきに行った。","romaji":"Ōmisoka no yoru, chikaku no otera e joya no kane o tsuki ni itta.","german":"On New Year's Eve, we went to a nearby temple to ring the New Year's bell.","furigana":"おおみそか|よる|ちか|てら|じょや|かね|い"},{"japanese":"教会の鐘が鳴り響き、二人の結婚を祝った。","romaji":"Kyōkai no kane ga narihibiki, futari no kekkon o iwatta.","german":"The church bells rang out, celebrating the couple's wedding.","furigana":"きょうかい|かね|な|ひび|ふたり|けっこん|いわ"}],"notes":"鐘 is a large, usually hanging bell, as found in temples and churches. You say 鐘が鳴る \"the bell rings\", 鐘を鳴らす and, specifically for temple bells, 鐘をつく \"to strike the bell\". Small bells are called 鈴, electric bells ベル or チャイム. Do not confuse it with the identically read 金 \"money\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"研究者たちは、どれだけ速く氷山が溶け出しているかについて警鐘を鳴らしている。","romaji":"Kenkyūshatachi wa, dore dake hayaku hyōzan ga toke dashite iru ka nitsuite keishō o narashite iru.","german":"Researchers are sounding the alarm about how fast the icebergs are melting.","furigana":"けんきゅうしゃ|はや|ひょうざん|と|だ|けいしょう|な"},{"japanese":"専門家は、若者のスマホ依存に警鐘を鳴らしている。","romaji":"Senmonka wa, wakamono no sumaho izon ni keishō o narashite iru.","german":"Experts are warning about young people's smartphone addiction.","furigana":"せんもんか|わかもの|いぞん|けいしょう|な"},{"japanese":"今回の事故は、安全管理の甘さに対する警鐘と言える。","romaji":"Konkai no jiko wa, anzen kanri no amasa ni taisuru keishō to ieru.","german":"This accident can be called a wake-up call about lax safety management.","furigana":"こんかい|じこ|あんぜんかんり|あま|たい|けいしょう|い"}],"notes":"警鐘 is originally an alarm or fire bell; today it is used almost only figuratively as \"warning, wake-up call\". The fixed phrase is 〜に警鐘を鳴らす \"to warn about something, to sound the alarm\", often with experts or the media as the subject. The word is literary and typical of news and editorials. Read the same way are 継承 \"succession\" and 敬称 \"title of address, honorific\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「アチョーーーーーー!」と叫び声を上げながら、コウイチはビエトの手から凶器を叩き落とした。","romaji":"\"Achō!\" to sakebigoe o agenagara, Kōichi wa Bieto no te kara kyōki o tatakiotoshita.","german":"Shouting \"Achoooo!\", Koichi knocked the dangerous weapon out of Viet's hand."},{"japanese":"警察は事件現場の近くで凶器を発見した。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa jiken genba no chikaku de kyōki o hakken shita.","german":"The police found the weapon near the crime scene.","furigana":"けいさつ|じけんげんば|ちか|きょうき|はっけん"},{"japanese":"飲酒運転の車は走る凶器だ。","romaji":"Inshu unten no kuruma wa hashiru kyōki da.","german":"A car driven by a drunk driver is a weapon on wheels.","furigana":"いんしゅうんてん|くるま|はし|きょうき"}],"notes":"凶器 is a weapon in the sense of \"weapon used in a crime, dangerous object\", especially in crime and police reports: 凶器はナイフだった, 凶器が見つかる. Unlike 武器 (weapon in general, also in war or in games), 凶器 emphasizes use in an act of violence; everyday objects like a hammer also become a 凶器. Figuratively, 走る凶器 is said of a recklessly driven car.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人類の半分を一掃した凶悪なウィルスの元凶は、ウォッカのつまった西瓜だった。","romaji":"Jinrui no hanbun o issō shita kyōaku na wirusu no genkyō wa, wokka no tsumatta suika datta.","german":"The cause of the terrible virus that wiped out half of humanity was a watermelon filled with vodka."},{"japanese":"睡眠不足が肌荒れの元凶だった。","romaji":"Suimin busoku ga hadaare no genkyō datta.","german":"Lack of sleep was the real cause of my skin problems.","furigana":"すいみんぶそく|はだあ|げんきょう"},{"japanese":"この事件の元凶は、会社の古い体質だと言われている。","romaji":"Kono jiken no genkyō wa, kaisha no furui taishitsu da to iwarete iru.","german":"The root cause of this incident is said to be the company's outdated culture.","furigana":"じけん|げんきょう|かいしゃ|ふる|たいしつ|い"}],"notes":"元凶 refers to the real cause or main culprit of an evil – always with a negative tone: 事件の元凶, 不況の元凶. Compared with the neutral 原因 \"cause\", 元凶 sounds accusing, as if naming a guilty party. Typical pattern: 〜の元凶は…だ. The tone is rather literary (newspapers, commentary); in conversation it is often half-joking.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"誰もが、その凶悪犯が刑務所で大人しく刑期を務めるのは無理だろうと思っていました。","romaji":"Dare mo ga, sono kyōakuhan ga keimusho de otonashiku keiki o tsutomeru no wa muri darō to omotte imashita.","german":"Everyone thought it would be impossible for that violent criminal to serve his sentence quietly in prison.","furigana":"だれ|きょうあくはん|けいむしょ|おとな|けいき|つと|むり|おも"},{"japanese":"最近、凶悪な事件が続いていて不安だ。","romaji":"Saikin, kyōaku na jiken ga tsuzuite ite fuan da.","german":"Violent crimes have been continuing lately, and it worries me.","furigana":"さいきん|きょうあく|じけん|つづ|ふあん"},{"japanese":"その男は凶悪な顔つきをしていたが、実はとても優しい人だった。","romaji":"Sono otoko wa kyōaku na kaotsuki o shite ita ga, jitsu wa totemo yasashii hito datta.","german":"The man had a fierce-looking face, but was actually a very kind person.","furigana":"おとこ|きょうあく|かお|じつ|やさ|ひと"}],"notes":"凶悪 describes extremely brutal, heinous crimes or criminals. It is a な-adjective: 凶悪な事件, 凶悪な顔つき. Very common in the news are the compounds 凶悪犯 \"violent criminal\", 凶悪犯罪 and 凶悪事件. Related are 残酷 \"cruel\" (of an act) and 悪質 \"malicious, vicious\" (more for fraud or violations).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"来週はずっと山で狩りをしている予定です。","romaji":"Raishū wa zutto yama de kari o shite iru yotei desu.","german":"Next week I plan to be hunting in the mountains the whole time.","furigana":"らいしゅう|やま|か|よてい"},{"japanese":"秋になると、家族で紅葉狩りに出かけます。","romaji":"Aki ni naru to, kazoku de momijigari ni dekakemasu.","german":"In autumn, our family goes out to view the autumn leaves.","furigana":"あき|かぞく|もみじが|で"},{"japanese":"中世ヨーロッパでは、多くの女性が魔女狩りの犠牲になった。","romaji":"Chūsei Yōroppa de wa, ōku no josei ga majogari no gisei ni natta.","german":"In medieval Europe, many women fell victim to witch hunts.","furigana":"ちゅうせい|おお|じょせい|まじょが|ぎせい"}],"notes":"狩り is hunting, the nominalization of 狩る: 狩りをする, 狩りに出かける. As a suffix with a voiced initial (〜狩り, がり) it also refers to gathering or picking in nature (いちご狩り, ぶどう狩り, 潮干狩り) and to admiring autumn foliage (紅葉狩り). Figuratively it stands for manhunts against people: 魔女狩り \"witch hunt\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"狩人が週休二日制だってのは初耳だな。","romaji":"Karyūdo ga shūkyū ninichisei datte no wa hatsumimi da na.","german":"It's the first I've heard of hunters having a five-day work week.","furigana":"かりゅうど|しゅうきゅうににちせい|はつみみ"},{"japanese":"赤ずきんの話では、狩人がオオカミのお腹から女の子を助け出す。","romaji":"Akazukin no hanashi de wa, karyūdo ga ōkami no onaka kara onna no ko o tasukedasu.","german":"In the story of Little Red Riding Hood, the hunter rescues the girl from the wolf's belly.","furigana":"あか|はなし|かりゅうど|なか|おんな|こ|たす|だ"},{"japanese":"狩人は朝早く森に入り、じっと獲物を待った。","romaji":"Karyūdo wa asa hayaku mori ni hairi, jitto emono o matta.","german":"The hunter entered the forest early in the morning and waited motionless for his prey.","furigana":"かりゅうど|あさはや|もり|はい|えもの|ま"}],"notes":"狩人 is a hunter; the reading かりゅうど (less often かりうど) is irregular and has to be learned. The word sounds archaic or literary and appears mainly in fairy tales, fantasy and songs. A real, professional hunter is nowadays called 猟師 (りょうし), and in games and advertising also ハンター.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"つい先頃までとても仲良くしてたのに、彼が急によそよそしくなったんだけど、理由が全然分からないの。","romaji":"Tsui sakigoro made totemo nakayoku shiteta noni, kare ga kyuu ni yosoyososhiku natta n da kedo, riyuu ga zenzen wakaranai no.","german":"We were getting along really well until just recently, but he suddenly became distant, and I have no idea why.","furigana":"さきごろ|なかよ|かれ|きゅう|りゆう|ぜんぜんわ"},{"japanese":"先頃発表された調査によると、若者の読書時間が減っているという。","romaji":"Sakigoro happyō sareta chōsa ni yoru to, wakamono no dokusho jikan ga hette iru to iu.","german":"According to a survey announced recently, young people are spending less time reading.","furigana":"さきごろはっぴょう|ちょうさ|わかもの|どくしょじかん|へ"},{"japanese":"先頃はたいへんお世話になり、ありがとうございました。","romaji":"Sakigoro wa taihen osewa ni nari, arigatō gozaimashita.","german":"Thank you very much for your kind help the other day.","furigana":"さきごろ|せわ"}],"notes":"先頃 means \"recently, the other day\" and refers to a specific point in the recent past. It is elevated and literary – typical of news, letters and speeches (先頃発表された調査). In conversation, people prefer 先日 or この間. Unlike 近頃 and この頃, which mean an ongoing period \"lately\", 先頃 designates a point in time.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"近頃は製菓工場で夜勤の仕事をしているんだ。","romaji":"Chikagoro wa seika kōjō de yakin no shigoto o shite iru n da.","german":"Lately I've been working the night shift at a confectionery factory.","furigana":"ちかごろ|せいかこうじょう|やきん|しごと"},{"japanese":"近頃の若者は、新聞をほとんど読まないらしい。","romaji":"Chikagoro no wakamono wa, shinbun o hotondo yomanai rashii.","german":"Young people these days apparently hardly read newspapers.","furigana":"ちかごろ|わかもの|しんぶん|よ"},{"japanese":"近頃、夜になってもなかなか眠れなくて困っています。","romaji":"Chikagoro, yoru ni natte mo nakanaka nemurenakute komatte imasu.","german":"Lately I can hardly fall asleep at night, and it's troubling me.","furigana":"ちかごろ|よる|ねむ|こま"}],"notes":"近頃 means \"lately, nowadays\" and describes an ongoing state or habit of the present. It sounds somewhat more formal or old-fashioned than この頃 and 最近; the famous complaint is 近頃の若者は… \"the youth of today ...\". For a single recent event (moved last week), 最近 or 先日 fits better than 近頃.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの子いつも一人だろう？彼、同じ年頃の友達がいないそうでどうも心配だ。","romaji":"Ano ko itsumo hitori darou? Kare, onaji toshigoro no tomodachi ga inai sou de doumo shinpai da.","german":"That child is always alone, isn't he? He doesn't seem to have friends his own age, so I'm worried.","furigana":"こ|ひとり|かれ|おな|としごろ|ともだち|しんぱい"},{"japanese":"娘もそろそろ年頃なので、親としては少し心配だ。","romaji":"Musume mo sorosoro toshigoro na node, oya to shite wa sukoshi shinpai da.","german":"My daughter is gradually reaching the age where parents start to worry a little.","furigana":"むすめ|としごろ|おや|すこ|しんぱい"},{"japanese":"中学生は、親に反抗したくなる年頃だ。","romaji":"Chūgakusei wa, oya ni hankō shitaku naru toshigoro da.","german":"Middle schoolers are at the age when they want to rebel against their parents.","furigana":"ちゅうがくせい|おや|はんこう|としごろ"}],"notes":"年頃 refers to an approximate age (同じ年頃の子ども \"children of a similar age\") and especially to the age at which one becomes adult or marriageable: 年頃の娘. With a preceding clause it means \"the age when ...\": 親に反抗したい年頃. For the exact age in years one uses 年齢 or 年 instead.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"トーフグはいつ頃Eブックマーケットに参入するつもりですか？","romaji":"Tōfugu wa itsugoro E-bukku māketto ni sannyū suru tsumori desu ka?","german":"About when does Tofugu intend to enter the e-book market?","furigana":"ごろ|さんにゅう"},{"japanese":"荷物はいつ頃届きますか。","romaji":"Nimotsu wa itsugoro todokimasu ka.","german":"About when will the package arrive?","furigana":"にもつ|ごろとど"},{"japanese":"日本に来たのはいつ頃ですか。","romaji":"Nihon ni kita no wa itsugoro desu ka.","german":"About when did you come to Japan?","furigana":"にほん|き|ごろ"}],"notes":"いつ頃 asks for an approximate point in time: \"about when, around what time\". It is softer and less pressing than a plain いつ because no exact answer is demanded – hence popular in business emails: いつ頃お届けできますか. It usually stands without a particle as an adverb; いつ頃に is also possible. It is often written in kana as いつごろ, and the answer correspondingly uses 〜頃 (三時頃).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この頃、風味を出すために砕いたヘーゼルナッツをコーヒーに加えてるんです。","romaji":"Kono goro, fuumi o dasu tame ni kudaita heezerunattsu o koohii ni kuwaeteru n desu.","german":"Lately I've been adding crushed hazelnuts to my coffee to bring out the flavor.","furigana":"ごろ|ふうみ|だ|くだ|くわ"},{"japanese":"この頃、急に寒くなりましたね。","romaji":"Kono goro, kyū ni samuku narimashita ne.","german":"It has suddenly gotten cold these days, hasn't it?","furigana":"ごろ|きゅう|さむ"},{"japanese":"この頃の子どもは、小さいうちからスマホを使っている。","romaji":"Kono goro no kodomo wa, chiisai uchi kara sumaho o tsukatte iru.","german":"Children these days use smartphones from a young age.","furigana":"ごろ|こ|ちい|つか"}],"notes":"この頃 means \"lately, these days\" and describes an ongoing state or habit up to today; often written in kana as このごろ. It is more colloquial than 近頃 and similar to 最近, but does not fit single completed events (not: この頃結婚した). Note: その頃, by contrast, means \"back then\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"若い頃は食べ物について気にしなかったけど、今はお医者さんに糖尿病予備軍だって言われたので、食べ物に気をつけるようになったし、いつもレシピに書かれてるよりも少ない砂糖を入れるようにもなりました。","romaji":"Wakai koro wa tabemono ni tsuite ki ni shinakatta kedo, ima wa oishasan ni tounyoubyou yobigun datte iwareta node, tabemono ni ki o tsukeru you ni natta shi, itsumo reshipi ni kakareteru yori mo sukunai satou o ireru you ni mo narimashita.","german":"When I was young I didn't care about food, but now I do, since my doctor said I'm at risk of diabetes, and I always use less sugar than the recipe says.","furigana":"わか|ころ|た|もの|き|いま|いしゃ|とうにょうびょうよびぐん|い|た|もの|き|か|すく|さとう|い"},{"japanese":"父は若い頃、船乗りとして世界中を旅したそうです。","romaji":"Chichi wa wakai koro, funanori to shite sekaijuu o tabi shita sou desu.","german":"My father reportedly traveled all over the world as a sailor when he was young.","furigana":"ちち|わか|ころ|ふなの|せかいじゅう|たび"},{"japanese":"祖母の若い頃の写真を見て、今の私にそっくりで驚いた。","romaji":"Sobo no wakai koro no shashin o mite, ima no watashi ni sokkuri de odoroita.","german":"Looking at a photo of my grandmother in her youth, I was surprised at how much she looks like me now.","furigana":"そぼ|わか|ころ|しゃしん|み|いま|わたし|おどろ"}],"notes":"若い頃 means \"in one's younger days\", \"when one was young\" and usually stands adverbially with は or に at the start of a sentence: 若い頃は…, 若い頃に…. Attributively with の: 若い頃の写真, 若い頃の夢. After nouns and adjectives, 頃 is read ころ here; ごろ, on the other hand, follows time expressions for \"about\" (3時ごろ). Similarly built is 子供の頃 \"when one was a child\"; 青春 by contrast emphasizes the emotional time of youth, and 若者 are \"young people\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"死者の霊魂に捧げるためのイラストを描いてほしいとアヤに頼みました。","romaji":"Shisha no reikon ni sasageru tame no irasuto o egaite hoshii to Aya ni tanomimashita.","german":"I asked Aya to draw an illustration dedicated to the souls of the dead.","furigana":"ししゃ|れいこん|ささ|えが|たの"},{"japanese":"年末のセールでは、どの店も商魂たくましく客を呼び込んでいる。","romaji":"Nenmatsu no seeru de wa, dono mise mo shoukon takumashiku kyaku o yobikonde iru.","german":"At the year-end sale, all the shops lure customers with enormous business spirit.","furigana":"ねんまつ|みせ|しょうこん|きゃく|よ|こ"},{"japanese":"試合の前、監督は選手たちに闘魂を見せろと言った。","romaji":"Shiai no mae, kantoku wa senshutachi ni toukon o misero to itta.","german":"Before the game, the coach urged the players to show fighting spirit.","furigana":"しあい|まえ|かんとく|せんしゅ|とうこん|み|い"}],"notes":"The reading こん is the Sino-Japanese reading of 魂 and occurs practically only in compounds: 霊魂 \"soul\", 商魂 \"business spirit\", 闘魂 \"fighting spirit\", 鎮魂 \"repose of souls, memorial for the dead\". As an independent word, 魂 is たましい \"soul\"; as a suffix after a noun it is usually read だましい (大和魂, 職人魂 \"craftsman's spirit\"). A free-standing word こん is not used in daily life. Fixed expression: 商魂たくましい \"extremely business-minded\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本で初恋の人にばったり出くわした時は、かなり驚きました。","romaji":"Nihon de hatsukoi no hito ni battari dekuwashita toki wa, kanari odorokimashita.","german":"I was quite surprised when I ran into my first love in Japan.","furigana":"にほん|はつこい|ひと|で|とき|おどろ"},{"japanese":"この仕事を終えるには、かなりの時間がかかりそうだ。","romaji":"Kono shigoto o oeru ni wa, kanari no jikan ga kakarisou da.","german":"It looks like it will take quite a lot of time to finish this job.","furigana":"しごと|お|じかん"},{"japanese":"駅からかなり歩くので、タクシーで行ったほうがいいですよ。","romaji":"Eki kara kanari aruku node, takushii de itta hou ga ii desu yo.","german":"It's quite a walk from the station, so you should take a taxi.","furigana":"えき|ある|い"}],"notes":"かなり means \"quite, considerably\" - clearly more than expected or normal, but not extreme. It stands before adjectives and verbs (かなり難しい, かなり疲れた) and with の before nouns (かなりの時間, かなりの金額). The spelling 可也 is a rare ateji; it is almost always written in kana. By comparison: とても is stronger and more general, 相当 is similar but even more emphatic, and なかなか expresses \"better than expected\" and often has a positive ring.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"魔女はお姫様に、コップを逆さまにしか持てなくする呪いをかけた。","romaji":"Majo wa ohimesama ni, koppu o sakasama ni shika motenaku suru noroi o kaketa.","german":"The witch put a curse on the princess so that she could only hold cups upside down.","furigana":"まじょ|ひめさま|さか|も|のろ"},{"japanese":"古い城には、王家にかけられた呪いの伝説が残っている。","romaji":"Furui shiro ni wa, ouke ni kakerareta noroi no densetsu ga nokotte iru.","german":"In the old castle, the legend of a curse laid on the royal family lives on.","furigana":"ふる|しろ|おうけ|のろ|でんせつ|のこ"},{"japanese":"何年も優勝できないのは呪いのせいだと、ファンは冗談を言っている。","romaji":"Nannen mo yuushou dekinai no wa noroi no sei da to, fan wa joudan o itte iru.","german":"Fans joke that a curse is the reason the team hasn't won a title in years.","furigana":"なんねん|ゆうしょう|のろ|じょうだん|い"}],"notes":"呪い is a \"curse\": 呪いをかける \"to lay a curse\", 呪いが解ける / 呪いを解く \"the curse is broken / to break a curse\". The related verb is 呪う \"to curse\". The same kanji read まじない mean a harmless \"charm, spell\" (おまじない against pain or the like). Do not confuse it with the identically sounding adjective 鈍い（のろい）\"slow, sluggish\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"旧暦の正月といえばいつになりますかね。","romaji":"Kyuureki no shougatsu to ieba itsu ni narimasu ka ne.","german":"When is New Year according to the lunar calendar, anyway?","furigana":"きゅうれき|しょうがつ"},{"japanese":"七夕を旧暦で祝う地方もあります。","romaji":"Tanabata o kyuureki de iwau chihou mo arimasu.","german":"Some regions celebrate Tanabata by the old calendar.","furigana":"たなばた|きゅうれき|いわ|ちほう"},{"japanese":"日本では明治時代に旧暦から新暦に切り替わった。","romaji":"Nihon de wa Meiji jidai ni kyuureki kara shinreki ni kirikawatta.","german":"In Japan, the switch from the old calendar to the new calendar took place in the Meiji period.","furigana":"にほん|めいじじだい|きゅうれき|しんれき|き|か"}],"notes":"旧暦 is Japan's \"old calendar\", a lunisolar calendar that was replaced in 1873 by the Gregorian 新暦. You say 旧暦で数える, 旧暦の正月, 旧暦の7月. Some festivals such as お盆 or 七夕 are still celebrated regionally by the old calendar, or one month later. The purely lunar calendar is technically called 太陰暦 or 陰暦; the Western year count is 西暦.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"昭和は西暦何年に終わったんですか？","romaji":"Shōwa wa seireki nannen ni owatta n desu ka?","german":"In what year of the Western calendar did the Showa era end?","furigana":"しょうわ|せいれきなんねん|お"},{"japanese":"書類の生年月日は、西暦ではなく和暦で書いてください。","romaji":"Shorui no seinengappi wa, seireki de wa naku wareki de kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write your date of birth on the form in the Japanese calendar, not the Western one.","furigana":"しょるい|せいねんがっぴ|せいれき|われき|か"},{"japanese":"このお寺は西暦800年ごろに建てられたと言われている。","romaji":"Kono otera wa seireki happyaku nen goro ni taterareta to iwarete iru.","german":"This temple is said to have been built around the year 800 AD.","furigana":"てら|せいれき|ねん|た|い"}],"notes":"西暦 is the Western (Christian) year count, as opposed to the Japanese 和暦 with era names such as 令和 or 平成. On forms you often have to choose whether to give the date in 西暦 or 和暦; you say 西暦2025年 or ask 西暦で何年ですか. \"Before Christ\" is 紀元前. In everyday conversation, 西暦 is usually omitted and people just say 2025年.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"そのホテルの部屋からは、青く美しい海を眺望することができました。","romaji":"Sono hoteru no heya kara wa, aoku utsukushii umi o choubou suru koto ga dekimashita.","german":"From our hotel room, we could take in a view of the beautiful blue sea.","furigana":"へや|あお|うつく|うみ|ちょうぼう"},{"japanese":"このマンションは高台にあり、眺望が素晴らしい。","romaji":"Kono manshon wa takadai ni ari, choubou ga subarashii.","german":"This apartment building stands on high ground and has a wonderful view.","furigana":"たかだい|ちょうぼう|すば"},{"japanese":"新しいビルが建って、部屋からの眺望が悪くなった。","romaji":"Atarashii biru ga tatte, heya kara no choubou ga waruku natta.","german":"After the new building went up, the view from the room got worse.","furigana":"あたら|た|へや|ちょうぼう|わる"}],"notes":"眺望 is a literary word for \"view, distant vista\" and is used mainly as a noun: 眺望がいい, 眺望が開ける, 部屋からの眺望. It is typical in real-estate and tourism texts (眺望良好, 眺望抜群). As a する-verb (〜を眺望する \"to overlook\") it is rare and elevated. In everyday speech people tend to say 眺め, 景色 or 見晴らし; the related verb is 眺める.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一度しかない人生だろ！なぁ！一緒に全裸でスカイダイビングしに行こうぜ。すんげぇいい経験になると思うんだよね。","romaji":"Ichido shika nai jinsei daro! Nā! Issho ni zenra de sukaidaibingu shi ni ikō ze. Sungē ii keiken ni naru to omou n da yo ne.","german":"You only live once! Come on! Let's go skydiving naked together. I think it'd be a great experience.","furigana":"いちど|じんせい|いっしょ|ぜんら|い|けいけん|おも"},{"japanese":"男が全裸で道路を走り回り、警察に保護された。","romaji":"Otoko ga zenra de dōro o hashirimawari, keisatsu ni hogo sareta.","german":"A man ran around the road naked and was taken into protective custody by the police.","furigana":"おとこ|ぜんら|どうろ|はし|まわ|けいさつ|ほご"},{"japanese":"温泉では全裸で湯に入るのがマナーです。","romaji":"Onsen de wa zenra de yu ni hairu no ga manā desu.","german":"At an onsen, it is the etiquette to enter the water completely naked.","furigana":"おんせん|ぜんら|ゆ|はい"}],"notes":"全裸 means \"stark naked, completely nude\" and sounds more matter-of-fact than the colloquial 素っ裸 or the childish すっぽんぽん. Typical are 全裸で \"naked\" (adverbial) and 全裸の男 \"a naked man\", often in police reports (全裸で発見される). The counterpart is 半裸 \"half-naked\". The simple 裸 covers both and is the everyday word.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"きゃー！たった今、夢が叶っちゃった！半裸のイケメンが淹れたてのコーヒーを飲みながら、私が目を覚ました瞬間に「おはよう」って言ってくれるっていう夢が！","romaji":"Kyaa! Tatta ima, yume ga kanacchatta! Hanra no ikemen ga iretate no koohii o nominagara, watashi ga me o samashita shunkan ni ohayou tte itte kureru tte iu yume ga!","german":"Eek! My dream just came true! The dream where a half-naked hunk drinking freshly brewed coffee says \"good morning\" the moment I wake up!","furigana":"いま|ゆめ|かな|はんら||の|わたし|め|さ|しゅんかん|い|ゆめ"},{"japanese":"工事現場では、暑さのあまり半裸で働いている人もいた。","romaji":"Kouji genba de wa, atsusa no amari hanra de hataraite iru hito mo ita.","german":"At the construction site, some people worked half-naked because of the heat.","furigana":"こうじげんば|あつ|はんら|はたら|ひと"},{"japanese":"祭りでは、男たちが半裸で神輿を担いで町を回る。","romaji":"Matsuri de wa, otokotachi ga hanra de mikoshi o katsuide machi o mawaru.","german":"At the festival, men carry the portable shrine through the town half-naked.","furigana":"まつ|おとこ|はんら|みこし|かつ|まち|まわ"}],"notes":"半裸 means \"half-naked\", usually \"with a bare upper body\". It is a noun and is used adverbially with で (半裸で働く) or attributively with の (半裸の男). The word sounds rather literary, as in news or descriptions; colloquially people say 上半身裸 or simply 裸. The counterpart for \"completely naked\" is 全裸.","levelNote":"Compound: 裸 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"キモイ！今あそこの横断歩道で、裸に袖無しエプロンを着た男を見かけたんだけど。警察に通報した方がいいかな。","romaji":"Kimoi! Ima asoko no ōdan hodō de, hadaka ni sodenashi epuron o kita otoko o mikaketa n da kedo. Keisatsu ni tsūhō shita hō ga ii ka na.","german":"Gross! I just saw a guy at that crosswalk over there wearing nothing but a sleeveless apron. Should I report him to the police?","furigana":"いま|おうだんほどう|はだか|そでな|き|おとこ|み|けいさつ|つうほう|ほう"},{"japanese":"赤ちゃんを裸にして、お風呂に入れた。","romaji":"Akachan o hadaka ni shite, ofuro ni ireta.","german":"I undressed the baby and put him in the bath.","furigana":"あか|はだか|ふろ|い"},{"japanese":"冬になると、木々はすっかり裸になる。","romaji":"Fuyu ni naru to, kigi wa sukkari hadaka ni naru.","german":"When winter comes, the trees become completely bare.","furigana":"ふゆ|きぎ|はだか"}],"notes":"裸 is a noun, not an adjective: 裸になる \"to undress\", 裸で寝る \"to sleep naked\", attributive 裸の王様 \"the naked king\". It also means \"bare, uncovered\" (裸の木) and figuratively \"penniless\" (裸一貫 \"with nothing but oneself\"). Related: 丸裸 \"stark naked\", 裸足（はだし）\"barefoot\", 半裸 \"half-naked\", 全裸 \"completely naked\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は競馬で、中々の大金を賭けるんだよ。","romaji":"Kanojo wa keiba de, nakanaka no taikin o kakeru n da yo.","german":"She bets quite large sums of money on horse races.","furigana":"かのじょ|けいば|なかなか|たいきん|か"},{"japanese":"彼が時間どおりに来ないほうに千円賭けるよ。","romaji":"Kare ga jikan doori ni konai hou ni sen'en kakeru yo.","german":"I'll bet a thousand yen that he won't come on time.","furigana":"かれ|じかん|こ|せんえんか"},{"japanese":"彼は人生を賭けて、この会社を立ち上げた。","romaji":"Kare wa jinsei o kakete, kono kaisha o tachiageta.","german":"He staked his life and founded this company.","furigana":"かれ|じんせい|か|かいしゃ|た|あ"}],"notes":"賭ける means \"to bet, wager\": 〜に〜を賭ける (馬に1万円を賭ける). Figuratively it means \"to put at stake\": 命を賭ける, 人生を賭ける (here also written 懸ける). \"I'd bet on that\" is 賭けてもいい. Do not confuse it with the identically sounding 掛ける \"to hang\", 駆ける \"to run\" and 欠ける \"to be lacking\". The related noun is 賭け \"bet\", gambling in general is 賭け事 or ギャンブル.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あいつとの賭けに勝ったよ。","romaji":"Aitsu to no kake ni katta yo.","german":"I won a bet against him.","furigana":"か|か"},{"japanese":"賭けに負けたので、昼ご飯をおごることになった。","romaji":"Kake ni maketa node, hirugohan o ogoru koto ni natta.","german":"I lost the bet and now have to pay for lunch.","furigana":"か|ま|ひる|はん"},{"japanese":"貯金を全部使って店を開くのは、大きな賭けだった。","romaji":"Chokin o zenbu tsukatte mise o hiraku no wa, ookina kake datta.","german":"Opening a shop with all my savings was a big gamble.","furigana":"ちょきん|ぜんぶつか|みせ|ひら|おお|か"}],"notes":"賭け is the noun of 賭ける: \"bet\" and, figuratively, \"gamble, risk\". Typical are 賭けをする, 賭けに勝つ / 賭けに負ける and 一か八かの賭け \"an all-or-nothing gamble\". With 大きな賭け one describes risky life decisions. Gambling as an activity is rather 賭け事 or ギャンブル, the stake 賭け金.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"慶應義塾に通い始めた初日、思いっきり場違いだと感じました。","romaji":"Keiou Gijuku ni kayoi hajimeta shonichi, omoikkiri bachigai da to kanjimashita.","german":"On my first day attending Keio Gijuku, I felt totally out of place.","furigana":"|かよ|はじ|しょにち|おも|ばちが|かん"},{"japanese":"福沢諭吉が開いた塾は、のちに慶應義塾と名付けられた。","romaji":"Fukuzawa Yukichi ga hiraita juku wa, nochi ni Keiou Gijuku to nazukerareta.","german":"The school opened by Fukuzawa Yukichi was later named Keio Gijuku.","furigana":"ふくざわゆきち|ひら|じゅく||なづ"},{"japanese":"義塾とは、もともと世の中のために開かれた私立の学校のことだ。","romaji":"Gijuku to wa, motomoto yo no naka no tame ni hirakareta shiritsu no gakkou no koto da.","german":"A gijuku was originally a private school established for the good of society.","furigana":"ぎじゅく|よ|なか|ひら|しりつ|がっこう"}],"notes":"義塾 historically refers to a private school founded for the public good (義 \"justice, public good\" + 塾 \"private school\"). Today the word is encountered almost only in proper names, above all 慶應義塾 or 慶應義塾大学. For modern cram schools, people say 塾 or 学習塾, and for private schools in general 私立学校.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は塾生たちの中で一番記憶力が悪かった。","romaji":"Kare wa jukuseitachi no naka de ichiban kiokuryoku ga warukatta.","german":"He had the worst memory of all the cram school students.","furigana":"かれ|じゅくせい|なか|いちばんきおくりょく|わる"},{"japanese":"この塾では、塾生一人ひとりに合わせた授業を行っています。","romaji":"Kono juku de wa, jukusei hitori hitori ni awaseta jugyou o okonatte imasu.","german":"At this cram school, lessons are tailored to each individual student.","furigana":"じゅく|じゅくせいひとり|あ|じゅぎょう|おこな"},{"japanese":"慶應義塾大学の学生は、自分たちのことを塾生と呼ぶ。","romaji":"Keiou Gijuku Daigaku no gakusei wa, jibuntachi no koto o jukusei to yobu.","german":"Students of Keio University call themselves jukusei.","furigana":"|がくせい|じぶん|じゅくせい|よ"}],"notes":"塾生 is a student of a 塾, that is, a private tutoring or cram school; the word reflects the school's perspective (塾生募集 \"students wanted\"). In everyday speech people often simply say 塾に通っている子. For schoolchildren in general, 生徒 is used, for university students 学生. A special case: students of Keio University (慶應義塾) also call themselves 塾生.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"頼むから電卓を叩くのをやめてくれ。気が狂いそうになるだろ！","romaji":"Tanomu kara dentaku o tataku no o yamete kure. Ki ga kuruisou ni naru daro!","german":"Please stop tapping on the calculator. It's driving me crazy!","furigana":"たの|でんたく|たた|き|くる"},{"japanese":"計算が合っているか、電卓で確かめてください。","romaji":"Keisan ga atte iru ka, dentaku de tashikamete kudasai.","german":"Please check with a calculator whether the calculation is correct.","furigana":"けいさん|あ|でんたく|たし"},{"japanese":"この試験には電卓の持ち込みが禁止されている。","romaji":"Kono shiken ni wa dentaku no mochikomi ga kinshi sarete iru.","german":"Calculators are prohibited from being brought into this exam.","furigana":"しけん|でんたく|も|こ|きんし"}],"notes":"電卓 is the abbreviation of 電子式卓上計算機 and means the pocket or desktop calculator. You calculate with 電卓で計算する; 電卓を叩く / 電卓を打つ literally means \"to tap on the calculator\", figuratively \"to crunch the numbers, work out the figures\". 計算機 is the more general umbrella term. The calculator app on a phone is also often called 電卓.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私がお皿を洗うから、あなたは食卓を綺麗にしてくれる？いいかしら？","romaji":"Watashi ga o sara o arau kara, anata wa shokutaku o kirei ni shite kureru? Ii kashira?","german":"I'll wash the dishes, so could you clean the table? Is that okay?","furigana":"わたし|さら|あら|しょくたく|きれい"},{"japanese":"夕食の食卓には、家族の好きな料理が並んだ。","romaji":"Yuushoku no shokutaku ni wa, kazoku no suki na ryouri ga naranda.","german":"The family's favorite dishes were lined up on the dinner table.","furigana":"ゆうしょく|しょくたく|かぞく|す|りょうり|なら"},{"japanese":"週に一度は、家族全員で食卓を囲むようにしている。","romaji":"Shuu ni ichido wa, kazoku zen'in de shokutaku o kakomu you ni shite iru.","german":"I try to have the whole family sit around the table together once a week.","furigana":"しゅう|いちど|かぞくぜんいん|しょくたく|かこ"}],"notes":"食卓 is the \"dining table\" and sounds somewhat more refined than テーブル. It is often figurative: 食卓を囲む \"to eat together at the table\", 食卓に並ぶ / 食卓に上る \"to appear on the table\", 家庭の食卓 \"meals in families\". It is a noun; attributively it takes の (食卓の上, 食卓の塩).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの卓球の音のせいで気が変になりそうだ！","romaji":"Ano takkyuu no oto no sei de ki ga hen ni narisou da!","german":"That table tennis sound is driving me crazy!","furigana":"たっきゅう|おと|き|へん"},{"japanese":"週末は友達と卓球をして汗を流した。","romaji":"Shuumatsu wa tomodachi to takkyuu o shite ase o nagashita.","german":"On the weekend, I played table tennis with friends and worked up a good sweat.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|ともだち|たっきゅう|あせ|なが"},{"japanese":"温泉旅館には、卓球台が置いてあることが多い。","romaji":"Onsen ryokan ni wa, takkyuudai ga oite aru koto ga ooi.","german":"Onsen inns often have a table tennis table.","furigana":"おんせんりょかん|たっきゅうだい|お|おお"}],"notes":"卓球 is \"table tennis\"; you say 卓球をする (not 遊ぶ). Related are 卓球台 \"table tennis table\", 卓球部 \"table tennis club\" and 卓球選手. ピンポン is colloquial and somewhat old-fashioned. In Japan, many onsen inns traditionally have a table tennis table, and \"onsen ping-pong\" is a well-known cliché.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"Excelのスキルに磨きをかけて、仕事が見つかればいいな、と思っています。","romaji":"Excel no sukiru ni migaki o kakete, shigoto ga mitsukareba ii na, to omotte imasu.","german":"I hope to find a job by polishing my Excel skills.","furigana":"みが|しごと|み|おも"},{"japanese":"歯磨きの後は、何も食べないようにしている。","romaji":"Hamigaki no ato wa, nani mo tabenai you ni shite iru.","german":"I try not to eat anything after brushing my teeth.","furigana":"はみが|あと|なに|た"},{"japanese":"駅前で靴磨きを頼んだら、新品のようにぴかぴかになった。","romaji":"Ekimae de kutsumigaki o tanondara, shinpin no you ni pikapika ni natta.","german":"I had my shoes shined in front of the station, and now they sparkle like new.","furigana":"えきまえ|くつみが|たの|しんぴん"}],"notes":"磨き is the noun form of 磨く \"to polish, clean\". On its own it is rare; it mostly appears in compounds like 歯磨き \"brushing teeth\", 靴磨き \"shoe shining\" or in the phrase 〜に磨きをかける \"to refine, improve further\" (腕に磨きをかける, 技術に磨きがかかる). The range of meaning thus extends from literal polishing to honing skills.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ホームレスだからって、俺がまるでばい菌のように人にジロジロ見られるのは、とても腹立たしい。","romaji":"Houmuresu da kara tte, ore ga marude baikin no you ni hito ni jirojiro mirareru no wa, totemo haradatashii.","german":"Just because I'm homeless, people stare at me as if I were vermin - it really makes me angry.","furigana":"おれ|きん|ひと|み|はらだ"},{"japanese":"手にはばい菌がたくさんついているから、食事の前に洗いなさい。","romaji":"Te ni wa baikin ga takusan tsuite iru kara, shokuji no mae ni arainasai.","german":"There are loads of germs on your hands - wash them before eating.","furigana":"て|きん|しょくじ|まえ|あら"},{"japanese":"傷口からばい菌が入らないように、消毒しておきましょう。","romaji":"Kizuguchi kara baikin ga hairanai you ni, shoudoku shite okimashou.","german":"We disinfect the wound so that no germs get in.","furigana":"きずぐち|きん|はい|しょうどく"}],"notes":"ばい菌 is the everyday, often child-friendly word for \"germs, bacteria\": 手にばい菌がつく, 傷口からばい菌が入る. It is mostly written ばい菌 or バイ菌; the full kanji spelling 黴菌 is rare. In technical language one says 細菌 or 菌, for viruses ウイルス. Figuratively someone is ばい菌扱い, i.e. \"treated like vermin\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"トーフグのステッカーが無菌かどうかの問い合わせのメールが来たんだけど。","romaji":"Toufugu no sutekkaa ga mukin ka dou ka no toiawase no meeru ga kita n da kedo.","german":"I got an email asking whether the Tofugu stickers are sterile.","furigana":"むきん|と|あ|き"},{"japanese":"手術は無菌状態の部屋で行われる。","romaji":"Shujutsu wa mukin joutai no heya de okonawareru.","german":"Surgery is performed in a sterile room.","furigana":"しゅじゅつ|むきんじょうたい|へや|おこな"},{"japanese":"無菌パックのご飯は、常温で長く保存できる。","romaji":"Mukin pakku no gohan wa, jouon de nagaku hozon dekiru.","german":"Aseptically packaged rice can be stored for a long time at room temperature.","furigana":"むきん|はん|じょうおん|なが|ほぞん"}],"notes":"無菌 means \"germ-free, sterile\" and is used attributively with の or in compounds: 無菌室, 無菌状態, 無菌パック (aseptically packaged rice). It is not a な-adjective. Related words: 殺菌 \"to kill germs\", 除菌 \"to remove germs\" (advertising language), 滅菌 \"to sterilize completely\" (medical). Register: rather technical, from medicine and food technology.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"トイレを殺菌する一番いい方法は何ですか？","romaji":"Toire o sakkin suru ichiban ii hōhō wa nani desu ka?","german":"Wie desinfiziert man eine Toilette am besten?","furigana":"さっきん|いちばん|ほうほう|なに"},{"japanese":"牛乳は高温で殺菌してから出荷される。","romaji":"Gyuunyuu wa kouon de sakkin shite kara shukka sareru.","german":"Milch wird bei hoher Temperatur entkeimt, bevor sie ausgeliefert wird.","furigana":"ぎゅうにゅう|こうおん|さっきん|しゅっか"},{"japanese":"布団を日光に当てると、殺菌効果があるそうだ。","romaji":"Futon o nikkou ni ateru to, sakkin kouka ga aru sou da.","german":"Futons in die Sonne zu legen soll eine keimtötende Wirkung haben.","furigana":"ふとん|にっこう|あ|さっきんこうか"}],"notes":"殺菌 heißt „Keime abtöten, entkeimen, pasteurisieren“; als する-Verb transitiv: 〜を殺菌する. Häufig sind 殺菌効果, 殺菌作用, 殺菌剤 und 低温殺菌 „Pasteurisierung“. Abgrenzung: 消毒 ist „desinfizieren“ (z. B. Wunden), 除菌 „Keime entfernen“ (Werbung für Reinigungsmittel), 滅菌 „vollständig sterilisieren“ (Medizin). Das deutsche „Sterilisation“ im Sinne von Unfruchtbarmachung heißt dagegen 不妊手術.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"悪霊がいると聞いたので、トンネルの中ではスピードを上げて運転しました。","romaji":"Akuryō ga iru to kiita node, tonneru no naka de wa supīdo o agete unten shimashita.","german":"She drove through the tunnel at high speed because she had heard there was an evil spirit there.","furigana":"あくりょう|き|なか|あ|うんてん"},{"japanese":"その神社では、悪霊を追い払うための儀式が毎年行われる。","romaji":"Sono jinja de wa, akuryou o oiharau tame no gishiki ga maitoshi okonawareru.","german":"At that shrine, a ritual to drive away evil spirits is held every year.","furigana":"じんじゃ|あくりょう|お|はら|ぎしき|まいとしおこな"},{"japanese":"映画の中で、少女は悪霊に取りつかれてしまう。","romaji":"Eiga no naka de, shoujo wa akuryou ni toritsukarete shimau.","german":"In the movie, a girl becomes possessed by an evil spirit.","furigana":"えいが|なか|しょうじょ|あくりょう|と"}],"notes":"悪霊 is an \"evil spirit\" that harms people or possesses them. Typical: 悪霊に取りつかれる \"to be possessed by an evil spirit\", 悪霊を払う / 追い払う \"to drive out\", 悪霊払い \"exorcism\". A neutral spirit of a dead person is 幽霊 or 亡霊; 悪魔 is rather the Western \"devil\", 鬼 the Japanese demon.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は非常に霊感の強い霊能力者で、あの世の人と話をすることができます。","romaji":"Kanojo wa hijou ni reikan no tsuyoi reinouryokusha de, anoyo no hito to hanashi o suru koto ga dekimasu.","german":"She is a medium with a very strong sixth sense and can talk with people in the afterlife.","furigana":"かのじょ|ひじょう|れいかん|つよ|れいのうりょくしゃ|よ|ひと|はなし"},{"japanese":"私は霊感がないので、幽霊を見たことは一度もない。","romaji":"Watashi wa reikan ga nai node, yuurei o mita koto wa ichido mo nai.","german":"I have no sixth sense, so I've never seen a ghost.","furigana":"わたし|れいかん|ゆうれい|み|いちど"},{"japanese":"その詩人は、旅先の風景から霊感を得て名作を書いた。","romaji":"Sono shijin wa, tabisaki no fuukei kara reikan o ete meisaku o kaita.","german":"The poet drew inspiration from the scenery on his travels and wrote a masterpiece.","furigana":"しじん|たびさき|ふうけい|れいかん|え|めいさく|か"}],"notes":"霊感 today mostly means the ability to perceive the supernatural, such as ghosts: 霊感が強い, 霊感がある / ない. The older meaning \"divine inspiration\" is literary (霊感を得る); in everyday speech people say インスピレーション or ひらめき instead. 霊感商法 refers to fraud involving supposedly spiritual goods. A medium is called 霊能力者.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"亡霊はめげずに、人々を怖がらせ続けました。","romaji":"Bourei wa megezu ni, hitobito o kowagarase tsuzukemashita.","german":"The ghost went on scaring people, undaunted.","furigana":"ぼうれい|ひとびと|こわ|つづ"},{"japanese":"戦場の跡には、兵士たちの亡霊が出るといううわさがある。","romaji":"Senjou no ato ni wa, heishitachi no bourei ga deru to iu uwasa ga aru.","german":"There is a rumor that the ghosts of soldiers appear at the old battlefield.","furigana":"せんじょう|あと|へいし|ぼうれい|で"},{"japanese":"過去の亡霊にいつまでもとらわれていてはいけない。","romaji":"Kako no bourei ni itsu made mo torawarete ite wa ikenai.","german":"You shouldn't stay trapped by the ghosts of the past forever.","furigana":"かこ|ぼうれい"}],"notes":"亡霊 is the \"spirit of a deceased person\" and sounds gloomier and more literary than the everyday 幽霊 \"ghost\". Typical: 亡霊が出る / 現れる. Figurative use for something past that won't let go is common: 過去の亡霊, 軍国主義の亡霊 \"the ghost of militarism\". 悪霊, on the other hand, emphasizes the malice of the spirit.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"家族でうちの犬をペット専用霊園に連れて行きました。","romaji":"Kazoku de uchi no inu o petto sen'yō reien ni tsurete ikimashita.","german":"My family took our dog to a pet cemetery.","furigana":"かぞく|いぬ|せんようれいえん|つ|い"},{"japanese":"祖父のお墓は、郊外の霊園にあります。","romaji":"Sofu no ohaka wa, kougai no reien ni arimasu.","german":"My grandfather's grave is in a cemetery on the outskirts.","furigana":"そふ|はか|こうがい|れいえん"},{"japanese":"お彼岸には、霊園が墓参りの人でにぎわう。","romaji":"Ohigan ni wa, reien ga hakamairi no hito de nigiwau.","german":"During Higan, the cemetery is crowded with people visiting graves.","furigana":"ひがん|れいえん|はかまい|ひと"}],"notes":"霊園 is a large, park-like cemetery, often run by a city or privately, and frequently part of names (市営霊園, 〇〇霊園). 墓地 is the general word for \"cemetery\", also on temple grounds; 墓場 sounds colloquial and creepy; お墓 is the individual grave. You say 霊園にお墓を建てる and 霊園へ墓参りに行く.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"最近加湿器の調子が悪いんだよね。","romaji":"Saikin kashitsuki no chōshi ga warui n da yo ne.","german":"My humidifier hasn't been working well lately.","furigana":"さいきんかしつき|ちょうし|わる"},{"japanese":"冬は空気が乾燥するので、寝るときに加湿器をつけている。","romaji":"Fuyu wa kuuki ga kansou suru node, neru toki ni kashitsuki o tsukete iru.","german":"The air is dry in winter, so I run the humidifier when I sleep.","furigana":"ふゆ|くうき|かんそう|ね|かしつき"},{"japanese":"加湿器の水は毎日取り替えないと、カビの原因になります。","romaji":"Kashitsuki no mizu wa mainichi torikaenai to, kabi no gen'in ni narimasu.","german":"If you don't change the water in the humidifier every day, it can cause mold.","furigana":"かしつき|みず|まいにちと|か|げんいん"}],"notes":"加湿器 is a \"humidifier\" (加湿 \"to humidify\" + 器 \"device\"). You say 加湿器をつける / 消す or 加湿器を置く. The counterpart is 除湿機 \"dehumidifier\" – usually written with 機 here; related is 空気清浄機 \"air purifier\". In Japan, the humidifier runs mainly in the dry winter.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"昨日の夜、寝汗をたくさんかいちゃったから、ベッドが何だか湿ってるのよね。","romaji":"Kinō no yoru, nease o takusan kaichatta kara, beddo ga nandaka shimetteru no yo ne.","german":"I sweated a lot in my sleep last night, so my bed feels kind of damp.","furigana":"きのう|よる|ねあせ|なん|しめ"},{"japanese":"梅雨の時期は、洗濯物がなかなか乾かずに湿ったままだ。","romaji":"Tsuyu no jiki wa, sentakumono ga nakanaka kawakazu ni shimetta mama da.","german":"During the rainy season, laundry hardly dries and stays damp.","furigana":"つゆ|じき|せんたくもの|かわ|しめ"},{"japanese":"湿った空気のせいで、髪がまとまらない。","romaji":"Shimetta kūki no sei de, kami ga matomaranai.","german":"Because of the humid air, my hair won't stay in place.","furigana":"しめ|くうき|かみ"}],"notes":"湿る is intransitive: something becomes \"damp, clammy\", mostly in the state 湿っている: 洗濯物がまだ湿っている. Transitive counterparts are 湿らせる and 湿す \"to dampen\". Compared with 濡れる \"to get wet\", 湿る is weaker – only slightly damp. Figuratively it means \"to be gloomy, listless\", as in 湿った雰囲気 or, in baseball, 打線が湿る \"the batting lineup is in a slump\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"嫁いだばかりの頃、息子の嫁はとてもおとなしかった。","romaji":"Totsuida bakari no koro, musuko no yome wa totemo otonashikatta.","german":"Shortly after the wedding, my son's wife was still very reserved.","furigana":"とつ|ころ|むすこ|よめ"},{"japanese":"娘が嫁いでから、家の中が急に静かになった。","romaji":"Musume ga totsuide kara, ie no naka ga kyuu ni shizuka ni natta.","german":"Since our daughter got married and moved out, the house has suddenly become quiet.","furigana":"むすめ|とつ|いえ|なか|きゅう|しず"},{"japanese":"姉は農家に嫁ぎ、今は米作りを手伝っている。","romaji":"Ane wa nouka ni totsugi, ima wa komezukuri o tetsudatte iru.","german":"My older sister married into a farming family and now helps with the rice growing.","furigana":"あね|のうか|とつ|いま|こめづく|てつだ"}],"notes":"嫁ぐ is said of a woman: \"to marry, to marry into a family\". The destination takes に or へ: 農家に嫁ぐ, 遠くへ嫁ぐ. The word reflects the traditional idea that the bride moves into the husband's family, and sounds formal to somewhat old-fashioned; neutrally one says 結婚する, colloquially 嫁に行く. For a man who marries into the woman's family, one says 婿に入る. The verb is intransitive; \"to marry off a daughter\" is 娘を嫁がせる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"祖母に、滝川を泳ぐのは危険だと言われました。","romaji":"Sobo ni, takigawa o oyogu no wa kiken da to iwaremashita.","german":"My grandmother told me that it is very dangerous to swim in a torrent.","furigana":"そぼ|たきがわ|およ|きけん|い"},{"japanese":"雪解けの季節になると、山の滝川は水かさを増して音を立てて流れる。","romaji":"Yukidoke no kisetsu ni naru to, yama no takigawa wa mizukasa o mashite oto o tatete nagareru.","german":"In the snowmelt season, the mountain torrent swells and roars down the valley.","furigana":"ゆきど|きせつ|やま|たきがわ|みず|ま|おと|た|なが"},{"japanese":"滝川の流れは速いので、ボートで下るのは上級者だけだ。","romaji":"Takigawa no nagare wa hayai node, booto de kudaru no wa joukyuusha dake da.","german":"The current of the torrent is so strong that only experienced people can go down it by boat.","furigana":"たきがわ|なが|はや|くだ|じょうきゅうしゃ"}],"notes":"滝川 refers to a rushing river or mountain stream with rapids, literally \"waterfall river\". As a common noun it is rare and rather literary; in everyday speech one says 急流 \"rapid current\" or 渓流 \"mountain stream\". Much more often 滝川 appears as a family or place name (e.g. the city 滝川 in Hokkaidō).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その申し出を断るなんて狂気の沙汰だよ。","romaji":"Sono mōshide o kotowaru nante kyōki no sata da yo.","german":"Refusing that offer is sheer madness.","furigana":"もう|で|ことわ|きょうき|さた"},{"japanese":"彼の目には狂気が宿っていた。","romaji":"Kare no me ni wa kyouki ga yadotte ita.","german":"Madness dwelled in his eyes.","furigana":"かれ|め|きょうき|やど"},{"japanese":"戦争の狂気を描いた映画を見て、言葉を失った。","romaji":"Sensou no kyouki o egaita eiga o mite, kotoba o ushinatta.","german":"After watching the film about the madness of war, I was speechless.","furigana":"せんそう|きょうき|えが|えいが|み|ことば|うしな"}],"notes":"狂気 is a noun meaning \"madness\" and sounds written or dramatic; attributively it takes の (狂気の目), not な. Set phrases are 狂気の沙汰 \"sheer madness\" and 狂気じみた \"seemingly mad\". The opposite is 正気 \"being in one's right mind\". Colloquially one more often says 正気じゃない, 頭がおかしい or クレイジー.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"それは、まるで天空を高く翔る不死鳥のようでした。","romaji":"Sore wa, marude tenkū o takaku kakeru fushichō no yō deshita.","german":"It looked like a phoenix flying high in the air.","furigana":"てんくう|たか|かけ|ふしちょう"},{"japanese":"鷹が大空を翔る姿は、実に美しい。","romaji":"Taka ga oozora o kakeru sugata wa, jitsu ni utsukushii.","german":"The sight of a hawk gliding across the wide sky is truly beautiful.","furigana":"たか|おおぞら|かけ|すがた|じつ|うつく"},{"japanese":"若者よ、夢に向かって世界を翔れ。","romaji":"Wakamono yo, yume ni mukatte sekai o kakere.","german":"You young people, fly out into the world and chase your dreams!","furigana":"わかもの|ゆめ|む|せかい|かけ"}],"notes":"翔る is a poetic verb for \"to fly high through the air, to soar\". The space one flies through is marked with を: 大空を翔る, 天を翔る; also belonging here is 天翔る（あまかける）. In daily life one simply says 飛ぶ. 翔る is found mainly in literature, song lyrics, titles and slogans. Do not confuse with the similar-sounding 駆ける \"to run\", 掛ける and 賭ける.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"母は、父が悪趣味な人形を集めることを禁止しました。","romaji":"Haha wa, chichi ga akushumi na ningyou o atsumeru koto o kinshi shimashita.","german":"My mother forbade my father from collecting tasteless dolls.","furigana":"はは|ちち|あくしゅみ|にんぎょう|あつ|きんし"},{"japanese":"人の失敗を笑いものにするなんて、悪趣味だよ。","romaji":"Hito no shippai o waraimono ni suru nante, akushumi da yo.","german":"Making fun of other people's failures is in bad taste.","furigana":"ひと|しっぱい|わら|あくしゅみ"},{"japanese":"金ぴかの家具ばかりで、正直悪趣味な部屋だと思った。","romaji":"Kinpika no kagu bakari de, shoujiki akushumi na heya da to omotta.","german":"The room was full of gaudy gold furniture, and honestly I thought it was tasteless.","furigana":"きん|かぐ|しょうじきあくしゅみ|へや|おも"}],"notes":"悪趣味 is a な-adjective or noun with two meanings: aesthetically \"tasteless, kitschy\" (悪趣味な服, 悪趣味な家具) and morally \"tactless, nasty\" for behavior (悪趣味な冗談, 悪趣味ないたずら). More casually one says 趣味が悪い. 下品 emphasizes \"vulgar, coarse\". Note: 趣味 here means \"taste\", not \"hobby\".","levelNote":"Compound: 悪 (N2) + 趣味 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この包み紙には点字が施してあるわ。","romaji":"Kono tsutsumigami ni wa tenji ga hodokoshite aru wa.","german":"This wrapping paper has Braille on it.","furigana":"つつ|がみ|てんじ|ほどこ"},{"japanese":"プレゼントの包み紙を破らないように、丁寧に開けた。","romaji":"Purezento no tsutsumigami o yaburanai you ni, teinei ni aketa.","german":"I opened it carefully so as not to tear the wrapping paper of the present.","furigana":"つつ|がみ|やぶ|ていねい|あ"},{"japanese":"飴の包み紙を道に捨てないでください。","romaji":"Ame no tsutsumigami o michi ni sutenaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't throw candy wrappers on the street.","furigana":"あめ|つつ|がみ|みち|す"}],"notes":"包み紙 is \"wrapping paper\" (包む \"to wrap\" + 紙, with voicing to がみ): for gifts, but also for candies or the packaging in a department store. Typical: 包み紙を開ける / 破る / はがす. 包装紙 is more formal and businesslike; decorative gift wrap is often called ラッピングペーパー.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"そろそろ潮時だ。引き上げるぞ。","romaji":"Sorosoro shiodoki da. Hikiageru zo.","german":"It's about time. Let's pull out.","furigana":"しおどき|ひ|あ"},{"japanese":"会社を辞めるなら、今が潮時かもしれない。","romaji":"Kaisha o yameru nara, ima ga shiodoki ka mo shirenai.","german":"If you're going to quit the company, now may be the right moment.","furigana":"かいしゃ|や|いま|しおどき"},{"japanese":"引退の潮時を判断するのは難しい。","romaji":"Intai no shiodoki o handan suru no wa muzukashii.","german":"It is hard to judge the right time to retire.","furigana":"いんたい|しおどき|はんだん|むずか"}],"notes":"潮時 originally means the time of ebb or flood tide; figuratively it means \"the right moment\", especially to end something or withdraw (引退の潮時, そろそろ潮時だ). Typical combinations: 潮時を見る \"to pick the right moment\", 潮時を逃す \"to miss the right moment\". Similar are 頃合い and タイミング; チャンス, by contrast, emphasizes an opportunity to begin something.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"黒潮は正確にはどこを流れているんですか？","romaji":"Kuroshio wa seikaku ni wa doko o nagarete iru n desu ka?","german":"Where exactly does the Kuroshio flow?","furigana":"くろしお|せいかく|なが"},{"japanese":"黒潮の影響で、この地方は冬でも比較的暖かい。","romaji":"Kuroshio no eikyou de, kono chihou wa fuyu demo hikakuteki atatakai.","german":"Thanks to the influence of the Kuroshio, this region is relatively warm even in winter.","furigana":"くろしお|えいきょう|ちほう|ふゆ|ひかくてきあたた"},{"japanese":"カツオは黒潮に乗って日本の近海にやって来る。","romaji":"Katsuo wa Kuroshio ni notte Nihon no kinkai ni yatte kuru.","german":"Bonito come to the waters near Japan riding the Kuroshio.","furigana":"くろしお|の|にほん|きんかい|く"}],"notes":"黒潮 is the warm ocean current that flows north from the south along Japan's Pacific coast; its cold counterpart from the north is the 親潮. 潮 here means \"ocean current\". One says 黒潮が流れる, 黒潮に乗る (fish, drifting objects) and 黒潮の影響. Another name is 日本海流.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"マッチを擦って、ろうそくに火をつけた。","romaji":"Macchi o sutte, rousoku ni hi o tsuketa.","german":"I struck a match and lit the candle.","furigana":"す|ひ"},{"japanese":"競馬で給料を全部擦ってしまった。","romaji":"Keiba de kyuuryou o zenbu sutte shimatta.","german":"I lost my whole salary on horse racing.","furigana":"けいば|きゅうりょう|ぜんぶす"},{"japanese":"転んで膝を擦りむいてしまった。","romaji":"Koronde hiza o surimuite shimatta.","german":"I fell and scraped my knee.","furigana":"ころ|ひざ|す"}],"notes":"擦る（する）means \"to rub, to strike\", above all マッチを擦る \"to strike a match\", and colloquially \"to lose money gambling\" (競馬で給料を擦る, often written スる). The same kanji are also read こする; 目を擦る \"to rub one's eyes\" is always read こする. Compounds: 擦りむく \"to scrape\", 擦り傷 \"scrape, abrasion\". The intransitive counterpart is 擦れる（すれる）\"to chafe, to rub against\". Do not confuse with 掏る \"to pick pockets\" (財布をすられた).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"手頃な値段ですね。","romaji":"Tegoro na nedan desu ne.","german":"That's a reasonable price.","furigana":"てごろ|ねだん"},{"japanese":"この辞書は手頃な大きさで、持ち運びに便利だ。","romaji":"Kono jisho wa tegoro na ookisa de, mochihakobi ni benri da.","german":"This dictionary is a handy size and convenient to carry around.","furigana":"じしょ|てごろ|おお|も|はこ|べんり"},{"japanese":"初心者に手頃なハイキングコースを教えてください。","romaji":"Shoshinsha ni tegoro na haikingu koosu o oshiete kudasai.","german":"Please tell me a hiking course suitable for beginners.","furigana":"しょしんしゃ|てごろ|おし"}],"notes":"手頃 is a な-adjective: literally \"fitting well in the hand\", hence \"handy\" (手頃な大きさ) and \"suitable, reasonable\", especially for prices: 手頃な値段, 手頃な価格. In advertising, お手頃価格 \"affordable price\" is very common. Unlike 安い, 手頃 does not sound \"cheap\" but \"reasonable, affordable\". For abilities or difficulty it means \"just right\" (初心者に手頃なコース).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"未決状態の裁判は、当事者にとって大きなストレスです。","romaji":"Miketsu jōtai no saiban wa, tōjisha nitotte ōkina sutoresu desu.","german":"A pending trial is a great source of stress for the parties involved.","furigana":"みけつじょうたい|さいばん|とうじしゃ|おお"},{"japanese":"未決の書類は、右側の箱に入れておいてください。","romaji":"Miketsu no shorui wa, migigawa no hako ni irete oite kudasai.","german":"Please put the pending documents in the box on the right.","furigana":"みけつ|しょるい|みぎがわ|はこ|い"},{"japanese":"彼は判決が出ないまま、未決の身で半年以上を過ごした。","romaji":"Kare wa hanketsu ga denai mama, miketsu no mi de hantoshi ijou o sugoshita.","german":"He spent more than half a year in custody awaiting trial, with no verdict issued.","furigana":"かれ|はんけつ|で|みけつ|み|はんとしいじょう|す"}],"notes":"未決 means \"not yet decided, pending\" and is used with の or in compounds: 未決の書類, 未決箱 (in-tray for pending items), opposite 既決. In criminal law it means \"not yet sentenced\": 未決囚 \"prisoner awaiting trial\", 未決勾留 \"pretrial detention\". The word is written and official; for everyday plans one says 未定 \"not yet fixed\". Note: a \"draw\" in sports is 引き分け.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は息子がコンビニのお握りを食べることを禁止しました。","romaji":"Watashi wa musuko ga konbini no onigiri o taberu koto o kinshi shimashita.","german":"I forbade my son from eating convenience store rice balls.","furigana":"わたし|むすこ|にぎ|た|きんし"},{"japanese":"母が作ってくれたおにぎりを持って、遠足に行った。","romaji":"Haha ga tsukutte kureta onigiri o motte, ensoku ni itta.","german":"I went on the school excursion carrying the rice balls my mother made for me.","furigana":"はは|つく|も|えんそく|い"},{"japanese":"梅干しと鮭、どっちのおにぎりにする？","romaji":"Umeboshi to sake, docchi no onigiri ni suru?","german":"Which rice ball will you have, pickled plum or salmon?","furigana":"うめぼ|さけ"}],"notes":"お握り is almost always written in kana as おにぎり; it comes from 握る \"to form with the hand\". One says おにぎりを握る / 作る, the filling is called 具 (梅干し, 鮭, ツナマヨ), and it is often wrapped in 海苔. A synonym is おむすび, which sounds somewhat softer or regional (usage differs between eastern and western Japan). Convenience-store onigiri and onigiri in a bento for outings are typical.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これは出来たてホカホカの美味しい揚げ出し豆腐ですよ。","romaji":"Kore wa dekitate hokahoka no oishii agedashi dōfu desu yo.","german":"This is delicious agedashi tofu, freshly made and piping hot.","furigana":"でき|おい|あ|だ|どうふ"},{"japanese":"居酒屋に行くと、いつも揚げ出し豆腐を注文します。","romaji":"Izakaya ni iku to, itsumo agedashi dōfu o chūmon shimasu.","german":"When I go to an izakaya, I always order agedashi tofu.","furigana":"いざかや|い|あ|だ|どうふ|ちゅうもん"},{"japanese":"今夜は揚げ出しナスを作ってみよう。","romaji":"Kon'ya wa agedashi nasu o tsukutte miyō.","german":"Let's try making agedashi eggplant tonight.","furigana":"こんや|あ|だ|つく"}],"notes":"揚げ出し refers to a method of preparation: ingredients are dusted with starch, briefly deep-fried and served in warm dashi broth. The word hardly stands alone; it is almost always 揚げ出し豆腐, more rarely 揚げ出しナス or 揚げ出し餅. It is a classic at izakaya. Do not confuse with 揚げ物 \"fried foods\" in general (tempura, karaage, etc.).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この人形に単四電池を入れてスイッチを入れると、奇妙な叫びを上げるんです。","romaji":"Kono ningyou ni tan'yon denchi o irete suicchi o ireru to, kimyou na sakebi o ageru n desu.","german":"If you put AAA batteries into this doll and switch it on, it lets out a strange scream.","furigana":"にんぎょう|たんよんでんち|い|い|きみょう|さけ|あ"},{"japanese":"彼の歌は、若者たちの心の叫びを表している。","romaji":"Kare no uta wa, wakamonotachi no kokoro no sakebi o arawashite iru.","german":"His songs give voice to the inner outcry of young people.","furigana":"かれ|うた|わかもの|こころ|さけ|あらわ"},{"japanese":"住民の叫びは、ついに政府に届いた。","romaji":"Juumin no sakebi wa, tsui ni seifu ni todoita.","german":"The outcry of the residents finally reached the government.","furigana":"じゅうみん|さけ|せいふ|とど"}],"notes":"叫び is the noun of 叫ぶ \"to shout\". Concretely it appears in 叫びを上げる \"to let out a cry\", but for audible screaming one usually says 叫び声, or 悲鳴 for fear and pain. Very common is the figurative use as \"outcry, cry for help\": 心の叫び, 魂の叫び, 怒りの叫び, 住民の叫び. Munch's painting \"The Scream\" is 「叫び」 in Japanese.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"マミは叫び声を上げながら、ベーコンを上下に動かしました。","romaji":"Mami wa sakebigoe o agenagara, beekon o jouge ni ugokashimashita.","german":"Mami raised a scream while moving the bacon up and down.","furigana":"さけ|ごえ|あ|じょうげ|うご"},{"japanese":"夜中に外から叫び声が聞こえて、目が覚めた。","romaji":"Yonaka ni soto kara sakebigoe ga kikoete, me ga sameta.","german":"I woke up in the middle of the night hearing a scream from outside.","furigana":"よなか|そと|さけ|ごえ|き|め|さ"},{"japanese":"ジェットコースターから、楽しそうな叫び声が響いてくる。","romaji":"Jetto koosutaa kara, tanoshisou na sakebigoe ga hibiite kuru.","german":"Joyful-sounding screams echo from the roller coaster.","furigana":"たの|さけ|ごえ|ひび"}],"notes":"叫び声 is the concretely audible \"scream\" or \"shouting\": 叫び声を上げる, 叫び声が聞こえる, 叫び声が響く. Unlike 叫び, it is hardly used figuratively. A scream of fear or pain is specifically 悲鳴, cheering is 歓声; simply \"loud voice\" is 大声.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このペースじゃ、夕方までにお茶の新芽を摘み終わるのは無理じゃないかな。","romaji":"Kono pēsu ja, yūgata made ni ocha no shinme o tsumi owaru no wa muri ja nai ka na.","german":"At this pace, I don't think we can finish picking the new tea shoots by evening.","furigana":"ゆうがた|ちゃ|しんめ|つ|お|むり"},{"japanese":"春になると、木々の枝から新芽が出始める。","romaji":"Haru ni naru to, kigi no eda kara shinme ga dehajimeru.","german":"When spring comes, new shoots begin to appear on the branches of the trees.","furigana":"はる|きぎ|えだ|しんめ|ではじ"},{"japanese":"鹿が庭の新芽を食べてしまって困っている。","romaji":"Shika ga niwa no shinme o tabete shimatte komatte iru.","german":"Deer have eaten the new shoots in our garden, which is a nuisance.","furigana":"しか|にわ|しんめ|た|こま"}],"notes":"新芽 are \"fresh shoots, young buds\" in spring: 新芽が出る, 新芽が伸びる, 新芽を摘む. With tea, the first tender shoots are processed into 新茶 or 一番茶. More general is 芽 \"sprout, bud\"; a flower bud is つぼみ, young leaves 若葉. The verb 芽吹く describes sprouting.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は墨絵を描き終えると、すぐに壁に掛けて眺めました。","romaji":"Kanojo wa sumie o kakioeru to, sugu ni kabe ni kakete nagamemashita.","german":"When she finished the ink painting, she hung it on the wall at once and gazed at it.","furigana":"かのじょ|すみえ|か|お|かべ|か|なが"},{"japanese":"墨絵は、墨の濃さの違いだけで山や川を表現する。","romaji":"Sumie wa, sumi no kosa no chigai dake de yama ya kawa o hyougen suru.","german":"Ink painting expresses mountains and rivers only through differences in the density of the ink.","furigana":"すみえ|すみ|こ|ちが|やま|かわ|ひょうげん"},{"japanese":"美術館で、雪舟の墨絵の展覧会が開かれている。","romaji":"Bijutsukan de, Sesshuu no sumie no tenrankai ga hirakarete iru.","german":"An exhibition of Sesshū's ink paintings is being held at the art museum.","furigana":"びじゅつかん|せっしゅう|すみえ|てんらんかい|ひら"}],"notes":"墨絵 is a picture painted only with black ink (墨), that is, \"ink painting\". The art-historical technical term is 水墨画; 墨絵 sounds somewhat more everyday and is also used for simple ink drawings. One says 墨絵を描く; attributively 墨絵の世界, 墨絵のような風景 \"a landscape like an ink painting\" (e.g. in mist). Famous master: 雪舟.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その入れ墨には今までいくら費やしているんですか。","romaji":"Sono irezumi ni wa ima made ikura tsuiyashite iru n desu ka.","german":"How much have you spent on that tattoo so far?","furigana":"い|ずみ|いま|つい"},{"japanese":"日本の温泉では、入れ墨のある人の入浴を断るところもある。","romaji":"Nihon no onsen de wa, irezumi no aru hito no nyuuyoku o kotowaru tokoro mo aru.","german":"At some hot springs in Japan, people with tattoos are refused entry to the baths.","furigana":"にほん|おんせん|い|ずみ|ひと|にゅうよく|ことわ"},{"japanese":"彼は腕に龍の入れ墨を入れている。","romaji":"Kare wa ude ni ryuu no irezumi o irete iru.","german":"He has a dragon tattoo on his arm.","furigana":"かれ|うで|りゅう|い|ずみ|い"}],"notes":"入れ墨 is a \"tattoo\"; one says 入れ墨を入れる or 入れ墨を彫る \"to get a tattoo\". Other spellings are 刺青 and 文身. The word is strongly associated with traditional Japanese tattooing and the yakuza milieu; for fashionable tattoos one more often says タトゥー. Many onsen, swimming pools and gyms still refuse entry to tattooed people.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼が嫌いだと言うわけじゃないんだけど、あそこであの発言はちょっと穏当でないなと思ったわけよ。","romaji":"Kare ga kirai da to iu wake ja nai n da kedo, asoko de ano hatsugen wa chotto ontou de nai na to omotta wake yo.","german":"It's not that I dislike him, but I felt that remark there was a bit inappropriate.","furigana":"かれ|きら|い|はつげん|おんとう|おも"},{"japanese":"双方の意見を聞いたうえで、穏当な結論を出すべきだ。","romaji":"Souhou no iken o kiita ue de, ontou na ketsuron o dasu beki da.","german":"After hearing both sides' opinions, a reasonable conclusion should be reached.","furigana":"そうほう|いけん|き|おんとう|けつろん|だ"},{"japanese":"公の場では、もう少し穏当な表現を使ったほうがいい。","romaji":"Ooyake no ba de wa, mou sukoshi ontou na hyougen o tsukatta hou ga ii.","german":"In a public setting, it would be better to use somewhat more measured expressions.","furigana":"おおやけ|ば|すこ|おんとう|ひょうげん|つか"}],"notes":"穏当 is a written-language な-adjective: \"appropriate, moderate, reasonable\" in the sense of \"not offensive, not provoking conflict\". Typical: 穏当な判断, 穏当な処分, 穏当な表現; often negated: 穏当でない / 穏当を欠く \"inappropriate\". 妥当, by contrast, emphasizes \"factually correct, justified\"; 適当 is ambiguous (\"suitable\" or \"sloppy\"). The kanji 穏 means \"calm, peaceful\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"俺は平穏無事に暮らすことが嫌いだ。","romaji":"Ore wa heion buji ni kurasu koto ga kirai da.","german":"I hate living a peaceful, uneventful life.","furigana":"おれ|へいおんぶじ|く|きら"},{"japanese":"事件のあと、町はようやく平穏を取り戻した。","romaji":"Jiken no ato, machi wa youyaku heion o torimodoshita.","german":"After the incident, the town finally regained its calm.","furigana":"じけん|まち|へいおん|と|もど"},{"japanese":"家族と平穏な日々を送れることが、何よりの幸せだ。","romaji":"Kazoku to heion na hibi o okureru koto ga, nani yori no shiawase da.","german":"Being able to spend peaceful days with my family is the greatest happiness of all.","furigana":"かぞく|へいおん|ひび|おく|なに|しあわ"}],"notes":"平穏 means \"calm, peaceful, without incident\" and is a な-adjective or noun: 平穏な日々, 平穏に暮らす, 平穏を取り戻す \"to regain calm\". The doubled form 平穏無事 \"calm and without incident\" is very common. 平和 means rather \"peace\" as opposed to war, 穏やか the gentleness of weather or character. The opposite is 不穏.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今夜の三日月は、何だか不穏な感じがするわ。","romaji":"Kon'ya no mikazuki wa, nandaka fuon na kanji ga suru wa.","german":"Tonight's crescent moon gives off a somewhat ominous feeling.","furigana":"こんや|みかづき|なん|ふおん|かん"},{"japanese":"国境付近で不穏な動きがあると報じられた。","romaji":"Kokkyou fukin de fuon na ugoki ga aru to houjirareta.","german":"It was reported that there is threatening activity near the border.","furigana":"こっきょうふきん|ふおん|うご|ほう"},{"japanese":"会議室には、何となく不穏な空気が漂っていた。","romaji":"Kaigishitsu ni wa, nantonaku fuon na kuuki ga tadayotte ita.","german":"A vaguely tense atmosphere hung over the meeting room.","furigana":"かいぎしつ|なん|ふおん|くうき|ただよ"}],"notes":"不穏 is a な-adjective for a situation in which unrest or danger is developing: 不穏な動き \"threatening activity\", 不穏な空気 \"tense atmosphere\", 不穏な情勢, 不穏な発言. It describes the situation, not one's own feeling; for that one says 不安. 物騒 means rather \"unsafe, dangerous\" (物騒な世の中). Opposite: 平穏.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"被害者は鈍器のようなもので頭を殴られたとみられている。","romaji":"Higaisha wa donki no you na mono de atama o nagurareta to mirarete iru.","german":"The victim is believed to have been hit on the head with something like a blunt object.","furigana":"ひがいしゃ|どんき|あたま|なぐ"},{"japanese":"警察は、凶器として使われた鈍器を探している。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa, kyouki to shite tsukawareta donki o sagashite iru.","german":"The police are searching for the blunt object used as the weapon.","furigana":"けいさつ|きょうき|つか|どんき|さが"},{"japanese":"その辞書は鈍器になりそうなほど分厚い。","romaji":"Sono jisho wa donki ni narisou na hodo buatsui.","german":"That dictionary is so thick it could pass as a blunt instrument.","furigana":"じしょ|どんき|ぶあつ"}],"notes":"鈍器 is a \"blunt object\" used as a weapon (hammer, stone, bottle) and appears almost only in crime news: 鈍器のようなもので殴られる \"to be struck with something like a blunt object\". Opposite: 刃物 \"bladed weapon\"; the weapon used in a crime in general is 凶器. Jokingly, very thick books are called 鈍器 or 鈍器本.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今夜は悪魔と出かける気分じゃないの。","romaji":"Kon'ya wa akuma to dekakeru kibun ja nai no.","german":"I don't feel like going out with the devil tonight.","furigana":"こんや|あくま|で|きぶん"},{"japanese":"悪魔のささやきに負けて、ケーキをもう一つ食べてしまった。","romaji":"Akuma no sasayaki ni makete, keeki o mou hitotsu tabete shimatta.","german":"I gave in to the devil's whisper and ate another piece of cake.","furigana":"あくま|ま|ひと|た"},{"japanese":"彼は普段は優しいが、怒ると悪魔のようになる。","romaji":"Kare wa fudan wa yasashii ga, okoru to akuma no you ni naru.","german":"He is usually gentle, but when he gets angry he turns into a devil.","furigana":"かれ|ふだん|やさ|おこ|あくま"}],"notes":"悪魔 is the \"devil\" or \"demon\" of Western belief (also Satan) and, in Buddhism, Māra. Figuratively: 悪魔のような人 \"a devilish person\", 悪魔のささやき \"the voice of temptation\", 悪魔的 \"diabolical\". 小悪魔 is a flirtatious, seductive woman. Japanese demons are rather called 鬼, evil spirits 悪霊.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"わぁ、A級の新しい魔法を習得中なの？上手くいけば、すごいね！","romaji":"Wā, A-kyū no atarashii mahō o shūtokuchū na no? Umaku ikeba, sugoi ne!","german":"Wow, are you learning a new A-rank spell? If it goes well, that'll be amazing!","furigana":"きゅう|あたら|まほう|しゅうとくちゅう|うま"},{"japanese":"魔女は王子に魔法をかけて、カエルに変えてしまった。","romaji":"Majo wa ōji ni mahō o kakete, kaeru ni kaete shimatta.","german":"The witch cast a spell on the prince and turned him into a frog.","furigana":"まじょ|おうじ|まほう|か"},{"japanese":"「ありがとう」は人を笑顔にする魔法の言葉だ。","romaji":"\"Arigatō\" wa hito o egao ni suru mahō no kotoba da.","german":"\"Thank you\" is a magic word that makes people smile.","furigana":"ひと|えがお|まほう|ことば"}],"notes":"魔法 is \"magic, sorcery, a spell\" in fairy tales, fantasy and games: 魔法を使う \"to use magic\", 魔法をかける \"to cast a spell\", 魔法が解ける \"the spell wears off\". 魔法使い is \"wizard, witch\". To be distinguished from 手品 or マジック, i.e. magic tricks on stage. Figuratively: 魔法のような \"as if by magic\", 魔法の言葉 \"magic word\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「好き嫌いしないの！黒魔術だけじゃなくて白魔術もちゃんと勉強しなさい！」「はいはい。いつも同じこと言ってるよね。」「『はい』は一回でしょ！」","romaji":"Suki kirai shinai no! Kuro majutsu dake ja nakute shiro majutsu mo chanto benkyou shinasai! Hai hai. Itsumo onaji koto itteru yo ne. Hai wa ikkai desho!","german":"\"Don't be picky! Study not only black magic but white magic properly too!\" \"Yeah, yeah. You always say the same thing.\" \"It's one 'yes' only, right!\"","furigana":"す|きら|くろまじゅつ|しろまじゅつ|べんきょう|おな|い|いっかい"},{"japanese":"そのマジシャンは、まるで魔術のような技で観客を驚かせた。","romaji":"Sono majishan wa, marude majutsu no you na waza de kankyaku o odorokaseta.","german":"That magician amazed the audience with tricks that seemed like sorcery.","furigana":"まじゅつ|わざ|かんきゃく|おどろ"},{"japanese":"かつてヨーロッパでは、魔術を使ったと疑われた人々が処刑された。","romaji":"Katsute Yooroppa de wa, majutsu o tsukatta to utagawareta hitobito ga shokei sareta.","german":"In Europe long ago, people suspected of using sorcery were executed.","furigana":"まじゅつ|つか|うたが|ひとびと|しょけい"}],"notes":"魔術 is \"magic, sorcery\" in the occult or fantasy sense: 黒魔術 \"black magic\", 白魔術（しろまじゅつ）\"white magic\", 魔術師 \"magician, sorcerer\". Secondly it refers to large stage illusions. More common and more child-friendly is 魔法 (fairy tales, games: 魔法使い, 魔法をかける); sleight-of-hand tricks are called 手品 or マジック. It is a noun; attributively it takes の (魔術の本).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「ちょっと言ってもいいかな？」「もちろん。」「あのさ…やっぱいいや。」「ほら、白状しなって！」「えっと、実は私魔女なんだよね。」","romaji":"Chotto itte mo ii ka na? Mochiron. Ano sa... yappa ii ya. Hora, hakujou shinatte! Etto, jitsu wa watashi majo na n da yo ne.","german":"\"Can I say something?\" \"Of course.\" \"Well... never mind.\" \"Come on, confess!\" \"Um, actually, I'm a witch.\"","furigana":"い|はくじょう|じつ|わたしまじょ"},{"japanese":"魔女が出てくる絵本を子どもに読んであげた。","romaji":"Majo ga dete kuru ehon o kodomo ni yonde ageta.","german":"I read a picture book with a witch in it to the child.","furigana":"まじょ|で|えほん|こ|よ"},{"japanese":"ヨーロッパの魔女狩りの歴史について、レポートを書いた。","romaji":"Yooroppa no majogari no rekishi ni tsuite, repooto o kaita.","german":"I wrote a report on the history of European witch hunts.","furigana":"まじょが|れきし|か"}],"notes":"魔女 is the \"witch\" of fairy tales, folklore and history: 魔女が出てくる話, 魔女のほうき, 魔女狩り \"witch hunt\" (also figuratively for public hunts for scapegoats). A male sorcerer is called 魔法使い or 魔術師. Colloquially, 美魔女 refers to a woman from about 40 who looks astonishingly young. Also well known is the Ghibli film 「魔女の宅急便」.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「新しい寮生はセクシー？」「ああ、セクシーだよ。フグなんて、すでに彼女に熱を上げているよ。」","romaji":"Atarashii ryōsei wa sekushī? Ā, sekushī da yo. Fugu nante, sudeni kanojo ni netsu o agete iru yo.","german":"\"Is the new dorm resident sexy?\" \"Yeah, he is. Fugu has already fallen for her.\"","furigana":"あたら|りょうせい|かのじょ|ねつ|あ"},{"japanese":"寮生は夜11時までに帰らなければならない。","romaji":"Ryousei wa yoru juuichi-ji made ni kaeranakereba naranai.","german":"Dorm residents must return by 11 p.m."},{"japanese":"この大学では、寮生同士で食事当番を交代でしている。","romaji":"Kono daigaku de wa, ryousei doushi de shokuji touban o koutai de shite iru.","german":"At this university, the dorm residents take turns on meal duty.","furigana":"だいがく|りょうせいどうし|しょくじとうばん|こうたい"}],"notes":"寮生 is someone who lives in a 寮, a dormitory of a school, university or company; at schools this corresponds to a \"boarding student\". Related: 寮生活 \"dorm life\" and 寮のルール. Someone who lives privately as a lodger is a 下宿生; a roommate is called ルームメイト or 同室の人.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あんたの病棟で、かっこいい男は見た？","romaji":"Anta no byōtō de, kakkoii otoko wa mita?","german":"Have you seen any good-looking guys in your ward?","furigana":"びょうとう|おとこ|み"},{"japanese":"祖父は手術のあと、外科病棟に移されました。","romaji":"Sofu wa shujutsu no ato, geka byoutou ni utsusaremashita.","german":"After the surgery, my grandfather was transferred to the surgical ward.","furigana":"そふ|しゅじゅつ|げかびょうとう|うつ"},{"japanese":"小児病棟では、面会時間が決められています。","romaji":"Shouni byoutou de wa, menkai jikan ga kimerarete imasu.","german":"Visiting hours are set in the pediatric ward.","furigana":"しょうにびょうとう|めんかいじかん|き"}],"notes":"病棟 refers to a ward or building wing of a hospital, often with the specialty in front: 外科病棟 \"surgical ward\", 小児病棟 \"pediatric ward\", 精神科病棟, 隔離病棟. One is placed in a ward, or transferred to one (病棟に移される). Do not confuse with 病室 \"patient room\" (a single room) and 病院 \"hospital\" (the whole institution).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"吾輩は猫である。名前はまだ無い。","romaji":"Wagahai wa neko de aru. Namae wa mada nai.","german":"I am a cat. As yet I have no name.","furigana":"わがはい|ねこ|なまえ|な"},{"japanese":"漫画の中で、偉そうな猫が「吾輩に任せろ」と言った。","romaji":"Manga no naka de, erasou na neko ga wagahai ni makasero to itta.","german":"In the manga, a haughty cat said, \"Leave it to me.\"","furigana":"まんが|なか|えら|ねこ|わがはい|まか|い"},{"japanese":"兄はふざけて、自分のことを吾輩と呼んでいる。","romaji":"Ani wa fuzakete, jibun no koto o wagahai to yonde iru.","german":"My older brother jokingly refers to himself as 吾輩.","furigana":"あに|じぶん|わがはい|よ"}],"notes":"吾輩 is an archaic, pompous first-person pronoun for men. It is famous through Natsume Sōseki's novel 吾輩は猫である \"I Am a Cat\", in which a cat calls himself this in a puffed-up, dignified manner. Today it is heard only jokingly or with arrogant characters in manga and anime; in daily life one says 私, 僕 or 俺. Occasionally also written 我輩.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"すごく斬新なアイディアなので、後者よりも最初の案の方がいいと思います。","romaji":"Sugoku zanshin na aidia na node, kōsha yori mo saisho no an no hō ga ii to omoimasu.","german":"It's a very novel idea, so I think the first proposal is better than the latter.","furigana":"ざんしん|こうしゃ|さいしょ|あん|ほう|おも"},{"japanese":"この建物は斬新なデザインで話題になっている。","romaji":"Kono tatemono wa zanshin na dezain de wadai ni natte iru.","german":"This building is attracting attention for its innovative design.","furigana":"たてもの|ざんしん|わだい"},{"japanese":"彼の小説は構成が斬新で、最後まで飽きなかった。","romaji":"Kare no shōsetsu wa kōsei ga zanshin de, saigo made akinakatta.","german":"His novel's structure was innovative, and I never got bored until the end.","furigana":"かれ|しょうせつ|こうせい|ざんしん|さいご|あ"}],"notes":"斬新 means \"novel, original, innovative\" and praises ideas, designs or concepts that break with the familiar: 斬新なデザイン, 斬新な発想. It is a な-adjective. Compared with 新しい (\"new\", purely temporal), 斬新 emphasizes surprising originality; similar but more matter-of-fact is 独創的. The tone is rather elevated and typical of reviews, presentations and advertising.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"僕のお気に入りのアニメキャラクターが斬殺されてしまった。","romaji":"Boku no okiniiri no anime kyarakutaa ga zansatsu sarete shimatta.","german":"My favorite anime character got cut down.","furigana":"ぼく|き|い|ざんさつ"},{"japanese":"幕末には、多くの武士が京都の路上で斬殺された。","romaji":"Bakumatsu ni wa, ooku no bushi ga Kyouto no rojou de zansatsu sareta.","german":"In the late Edo period, many samurai were cut down on the streets of Kyoto.","furigana":"ばくまつ|おお|ぶし|きょうと|ろじょう|ざんさつ"},{"japanese":"男は刃物で被害者を斬殺したとして逮捕された。","romaji":"Otoko wa hamono de higaisha o zansatsu shita to shite taiho sareta.","german":"The man was arrested on suspicion of slashing the victim to death with a blade.","furigana":"おとこ|はもの|ひがいしゃ|ざんさつ|たいほ"}],"notes":"斬殺 means to kill someone with a sword or blade. The word is written-language and appears in historical accounts, novels and police reports, mostly in the passive: 〜に斬殺される. Related formations: 刺殺 \"to stab to death\", 射殺 \"to shoot dead\", 殺害 \"to murder\" (general). In everyday speech one would more likely say 刀で切り殺す.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その侍は、早口言葉が上手く言えなければお前の舌を斬るぞ、と私に言いました。","romaji":"Sono samurai wa, hayakuchi kotoba ga umaku ienakereba omae no shita o kiru zo, to watashi ni iimashita.","german":"That samurai told me that if I couldn't say the tongue twister well, he would cut out my tongue.","furigana":"さむらい|はやくちことば|うま|い|まえ|した|き|わたし|い"},{"japanese":"侍は一刀で敵を斬った。","romaji":"Samurai wa ittou de teki o kitta.","german":"The samurai cut down the enemy with a single stroke.","furigana":"さむらい|いっとう|てき|き"},{"japanese":"その評論家は、政治家の発言を鋭く斬った。","romaji":"Sono hyouronka wa, seijika no hatsugen o surudoku kitta.","german":"The critic sharply cut into the politician's remarks.","furigana":"ひょうろんか|せいじか|はつげん|するど|き"}],"notes":"斬る is a special spelling of きる for striking with a sword or blade, especially for cutting people down. Typical in samurai films: 敵を斬る, 斬りかかる \"to attack with a sword\", 斬り合い \"sword fight\". Figuratively it means \"to criticize sharply\": 世相を斬る. For everyday cutting (paper, vegetables, hair) one always writes 切る. Despite the -iru ending, it is a godan verb.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"それから半世紀の歳月が流れ、彼のことなんてほとんど忘れかけていた時に、私達は小さなパン屋さんで偶然再会したんです。","romaji":"Sorekara han seiki no saigetsu ga nagare, kare no koto nante hotondo wasurekakete ita toki ni, watashitachi wa chiisana pan'yasan de gūzen saikai shita n desu.","german":"Half a century passed after that, and just when I had almost forgotten him, we met again by chance in a small bakery.","furigana":"はんせいき|さいげつ|なが|かれ|わす|とき|わたしたち|ちい|や|ぐうぜんさいかい"},{"japanese":"歳月人を待たず。","romaji":"Saigetsu hito o matazu.","german":"Time waits for no one.","furigana":"さいげつひと|ま"},{"japanese":"この橋は完成までに十年の歳月を要した。","romaji":"Kono hashi wa kansei made ni juunen no saigetsu o youshita.","german":"This bridge took ten years to complete.","furigana":"はし|かんせい|じゅうねん|さいげつ|よう"}],"notes":"歳月 denotes a long span of time, \"the years\", and is elevated to written-language. Typical combinations: 歳月が流れる/経つ \"years pass\", 〜年の歳月をかける/要する \"to take ... years\", 長い歳月. Well known is the proverb 歳月人を待たず \"Time waits for no one\". Equivalent in meaning but a little less elevated is 年月 (としつき/ねんげつ).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"三十歳を過ぎてから、日本語を勉強し始めました。","romaji":"Sanjussai o sugite kara, Nihongo o benkyō shi-hajimemashita.","german":"I started learning Japanese in my thirties.","furigana":"さんじゅっさい|す|にほんご|べんきょう|はじ"},{"japanese":"娘は今年十歳になります。","romaji":"Musume wa kotoshi jussai ni narimasu.","german":"My daughter turns ten this year.","furigana":"むすめ|ことしじゅっさい"}],"notes":"〜歳 follows a number to express an age. The pronunciation changes with some numbers: 一歳 is いっさい, 八歳 is はっさい and 二十歳 is usually はたち. For 十歳, both じっさい and じゅっさい are in use; the examples use じゅっさい. The sign 〜 only marks the place for the number. 歳・さい is a counter here and not the independent reading とし for \"year\" or \"age\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"十分間にどれだけの炭酸入り清涼飲料水を飲めると思いますか？","romaji":"Jūfunkan ni dore dake no tansan'iri seiryōinryōsui o nomeru to omoimasu ka?","german":"How many carbonated soft drinks do you think you can drink in ten minutes?","furigana":"じゅうふんかん|たんさんい|せいりょういんりょうすい|の|おも"},{"japanese":"このミント入りのガムは清涼感があって、眠気覚ましにぴったりだ。","romaji":"Kono minto iri no gamu wa seiryoukan ga atte, nemukezamashi ni pittari da.","german":"This mint chewing gum has a refreshing feel and is perfect for staying awake.","furigana":"い|せいりょうかん|ねむけざ"},{"japanese":"暗いニュースが続く中で、彼の誠実な言葉は一服の清涼剤となった。","romaji":"Kurai nyuusu ga tsuzuku naka de, kare no seijitsu na kotoba wa ippuku no seiryouzai to natta.","german":"In the midst of a stream of gloomy news, his sincere words were a breath of fresh air.","furigana":"くら|つづ|なか|かれ|せいじつ|ことば|いっぷく|せいりょうざい"}],"notes":"清涼 \"cool and refreshing\" appears almost only in compounds: 清涼飲料水 \"soft drink\", 清涼感 \"refreshing feeling\" (advertising language for mint, cosmetics), 清涼剤. Figuratively, 一服の清涼剤 is \"a breath of fresh air, a welcome relief\" in an oppressive situation. As a な-adjective (清涼な空気) it is rare and elevated; in daily life one says 爽やか or 涼しい.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"窓を開けると、心地よい涼風とともに美しいピアノの音色が舞い込んできた。","romaji":"Mado o akeru to, kokochiyoi ryoufuu to tomo ni utsukushii piano no neiro ga maikonde kita.","german":"When I opened the window, the beautiful sound of a piano drifted in along with a pleasant cool breeze.","furigana":"まど|あ|ここち|りょうふう|うつく|ねいろ|ま|こ"},{"japanese":"夕方になると、川から涼風が吹いてきた。","romaji":"Yuugata ni naru to, kawa kara ryoufuu ga fuite kita.","german":"In the evening, a cool breeze came blowing from the river.","furigana":"ゆうがた|かわ|りょうふう|ふ"},{"japanese":"厳しい暑さの中にも、朝晩は涼風が感じられるようになった。","romaji":"Kibishii atsusa no naka ni mo, asaban wa ryoufuu ga kanjirareru you ni natta.","german":"Even in the severe heat, one can now feel a cool breeze in the mornings and evenings.","furigana":"きび|あつ|なか|あさばん|りょうふう|かん"}],"notes":"涼風 \"cool breeze\" is an elevated, literary word, often with a seasonal note (the end of summer). Typical: 涼風が吹く, 涼風を感じる, 涼風が立つ. The same spelling is also read すずかぜ, which sounds a little more poetic; this card teaches りょうふう. In daily life one simply says 涼しい風.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"猿真似をするのはよせ。みっともない。","romaji":"Sarumane o suru no wa yose. Mittomonai.","german":"Stop aping others. It's embarrassing.","furigana":"さるまね"},{"japanese":"他社の商品の猿真似では、お客さんに選んでもらえない。","romaji":"Tasha no shouhin no sarumane de wa, okyakusan ni erande moraenai.","german":"If you just ape other companies' products, customers won't choose you.","furigana":"たしゃ|しょうひん|さるまね|きゃく|えら"},{"japanese":"有名な歌手の猿真似をしても、自分のスタイルは身につかないよ。","romaji":"Yuumei na kashu no sarumane o shite mo, jibun no sutairu wa mi ni tsukanai yo.","german":"Even if you ape a famous singer, you won't develop a style of your own.","furigana":"ゆうめい|かしゅ|さるまね|じぶん|み"}],"notes":"猿真似 is pejorative: thoughtless aping without understanding or contributing anything of one's own. Typical: 〜の猿真似をする, 〜の猿真似にすぎない \"merely a cheap copy of ...\". Neutral by contrast are 真似 \"imitation\" and the elevated 模倣. Criticizing someone this way directly sounds harsh.","levelNote":"Compound: 猿 (N4) + 真似 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"零下十度だろうが二十度だろうが、洗い物はここでします。","romaji":"Reika juudo darou ga nijuudo darou ga, araimono wa koko de shimasu.","german":"Whether it's ten or twenty below zero, I'll do the washing up here.","furigana":"れいかじゅうど|にじゅうど|あら|もの"},{"japanese":"今朝は零下五度まで下がり、道路が凍っていた。","romaji":"Kesa wa reika godo made sagari, douro ga kootte ita.","german":"This morning it dropped to five degrees below zero, and the roads were frozen.","furigana":"けさ|れいかごど|さ|どうろ|こお"},{"japanese":"北海道の冬は、昼間でも零下の日が多い。","romaji":"Hokkaidou no fuyu wa, hiruma demo reika no hi ga ooi.","german":"In Hokkaido's winter, there are many days below zero even in the daytime.","furigana":"ほっかいどう|ふゆ|ひるま|れいか|ひ|おお"}],"notes":"零下 means \"below zero (degrees)\" and comes before the temperature: 零下10度 \"minus 10 degrees\". As an attribute with の: 零下の寒さ, 零下の日. In weather reports 氷点下 is more common today; colloquially one simply says マイナス5度. The counterpart \"above zero\" is 零上, but it is hardly used.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"零時頃、彼女が町中を一人で猛スピードで歩いているのを見かけました。","romaji":"Reijigoro, kanojo ga machinaka o hitori de mō supīdo de aruite iru no o mikakemashita.","german":"Around midnight, I saw her walking alone through town at a furious pace.","furigana":"れいじごろ|かのじょ|まちなか|ひとり|もう|ある|み"},{"japanese":"このチケットは本日の午前零時から販売開始です。","romaji":"Kono chiketto wa honjitsu no gozen reiji kara hanbai kaishi desu.","german":"Ticket sales for this ticket start at midnight today.","furigana":"ほんじつ|ごぜんれいじ|はんばいかいし"},{"japanese":"零時を過ぎると、電車がなくなってしまう。","romaji":"Reiji o sugiru to, densha ga nakunatte shimau.","german":"After midnight, the trains stop running.","furigana":"れいじ|す|でんしゃ"}],"notes":"零時 is \"zero o'clock\" and means midnight in the 24-hour format (午前零時). 午後零時, by contrast, means 12 noon, which in daily life is usually called 正午 or 昼の12時. It is often written 0時 and pronounced れいじ. Typical in timetables and announcements: 零時ちょうど, 零時を過ぎる \"to pass midnight\". Colloquially one more often says 夜中の12時.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"最小公倍数を公分母にするということが全く理解できず、試験で零点を取ってしまった。","romaji":"Saishou koubaisuu o koubunbo ni suru to iu koto ga mattaku rikai dekizu, shiken de reiten o totte shimatta.","german":"I couldn't understand at all about using the least common multiple as the common denominator, and I got zero on the exam.","furigana":"さいしょうこうばいすう|こうぶんぼ|まった|りかい|しけん|れいてん|と"},{"japanese":"名前を書き忘れて、テストが零点になった。","romaji":"Namae o kakiwasurete, tesuto ga reiten ni natta.","german":"I forgot to write my name, so the test was marked zero.","furigana":"なまえ|か|わす|れいてん"},{"japanese":"料理は上手だけど、片付けに関しては零点だね。","romaji":"Ryouri wa jouzu da kedo, katazuke ni kanshite wa reiten da ne.","german":"You're good at cooking, but when it comes to tidying up, you get a zero.","furigana":"りょうり|じょうず|かたづ|かん|れいてん"}],"notes":"零点 mainly means \"zero points\" on tests: 零点を取る, テストが零点になる. In speech 0点 (れいてん or ぜろてん) is more common. Figuratively, 〜としては零点だ means \"a complete zero, a total failure as ...\". In technical language it also means \"zero (root) of a function\" (mathematics) or \"freezing point\", but these meanings are rare for learners.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"囲碁教室に通い始めてから、少し上達しました。","romaji":"Igo kyōshitsu ni kayoi hajimete kara, sukoshi jōtatsu shimashita.","german":"I've improved a little since I started going to a go class.","furigana":"いごきょうしつ|かよ|はじ|すこ|じょうたつ"},{"japanese":"祖父は毎週日曜日に、近所の人と囲碁を打っている。","romaji":"Sofu wa maishuu nichiyoubi ni, kinjo no hito to igo o utte iru.","german":"My grandfather plays go with the neighbors every Sunday.","furigana":"そふ|まいしゅうにちようび|きんじょ|ひと|いご|う"},{"japanese":"囲碁のAIがプロの棋士に勝って、大きな話題になった。","romaji":"Igo no eeai ga puro no kishi ni katte, ooki na wadai ni natta.","german":"It became big news when a go AI beat a professional player.","furigana":"いご|きし|か|おお|わだい"}],"notes":"囲碁 is the board game Go; for short one also says 碁. The matching verb is 打つ: 囲碁を打つ, 碁を打つ (colloquially also 囲碁をする). Related are 碁石 \"stones\", 碁盤 \"board\" and 棋士 \"professional player\". Do not confuse with 将棋 (Japanese chess), for which one says 指す.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今朝俺たちの碁会所を覗き見していた男を取っ捕まえてやったよ。","romaji":"Kesa oretachi no gokaisho o nozokimi shite ita otoko o tottsukamaete yatta yo.","german":"This morning I caught the guy who was peeping into our go salon.","furigana":"けさおれ|ごかいしょ|のぞ|み|おとこ|と|つか"},{"japanese":"定年後、父は駅前の碁会所に通うようになった。","romaji":"Teinen go, chichi wa ekimae no gokaisho ni kayou you ni natta.","german":"After retiring, my father started going to the go salon in front of the station.","furigana":"ていねんご|ちち|えきまえ|ごかいしょ|かよ"},{"japanese":"碁会所では、初めての人でも同じくらいの強さの相手を紹介してもらえる。","romaji":"Gokaisho de wa, hajimete no hito demo onaji kurai no tsuyosa no aite o shoukai shite moraeru.","german":"At a go salon, even first-timers are introduced to an opponent of about the same strength.","furigana":"ごかいしょ|はじ|ひと|おな|つよ|あいて|しょうかい"}],"notes":"碁会所 is an establishment where one pays a fee to play Go against other patrons; traditionally a meeting place for older men. Typical: 碁会所に通う \"to go regularly to a go salon\". Modern establishments often call themselves 囲碁サロン or 囲碁クラブ. A go course with lessons, by contrast, is a 囲碁教室.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"昔の上海とフランス租界について知りたいのなら、彼がそれについては詳しいよ。","romaji":"Mukashi no Shanhai to Furansu sokai nitsuite shiritai no nara, kare ga sore nitsuite wa kuwashii yo.","german":"If you want to know about old Shanghai and the French Concession, he knows a lot about that.","furigana":"むかし||そかい|し|かれ|くわ"},{"japanese":"十九世紀の後半、上海には外国の租界がいくつも作られた。","romaji":"Juukyuu seiki no kouhan, Shanhai ni wa gaikoku no sokai ga ikutsu mo tsukurareta.","german":"In the latter half of the nineteenth century, several foreign concessions were created in Shanghai.","furigana":"じゅうきゅうせいき|こうはん||がいこく|そかい|つく"},{"japanese":"天津には、今も旧租界の洋館が数多く残っている。","romaji":"Tenshin ni wa, ima mo kyuu sokai no youkan ga kazu ooku nokotte iru.","german":"Many Western-style buildings of the former concessions still remain in Tianjin.","furigana":"てんしん|いま|きゅうそかい|ようかん|かずおお|のこ"}],"notes":"租界 is a historical term for the city districts administered by foreign powers in Chinese treaty ports (19th century to the 1940s). Typical: フランス租界, イギリス租界, 上海の共同租界, 旧租界. Related but different: 租借地 \"leased territory\", 植民地 \"colony\" and 居留地 for the foreigners' quarters in Japan (e.g. Yokohama).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一番最初にしなくちゃいけないことが、陶芸用の粘土から気泡を取り除くことです。","romaji":"Ichiban saisho ni shinakucha ikenai koto ga, tōgeiyō no nendo kara kihō o torinozoku koto desu.","german":"The first thing you have to do is remove the air bubbles from the pottery clay.","furigana":"いちばんさいしょ|とうげいよう|ねんど|きほう|と|のぞ"},{"japanese":"スマホに保護フィルムを貼ったら、気泡が入ってしまった。","romaji":"Sumaho ni hogo firumu o hattara, kihou ga haitte shimatta.","german":"After I put a protective film on my smartphone, air bubbles got in.","furigana":"ほご|は|きほう|はい"},{"japanese":"水槽の底から小さな気泡が次々に上がってくる。","romaji":"Suisou no soko kara chiisana kihou ga tsugitsugi ni agatte kuru.","german":"Small air bubbles keep rising one after another from the bottom of the aquarium.","furigana":"すいそう|そこ|ちい|きほう|つぎつぎ|あ"}],"notes":"気泡 is an (air) bubble in liquids or solid materials and sounds technical and matter-of-fact; in daily life one mostly says 泡. Typical: 気泡が入る/できる \"bubbles form\", 気泡を抜く/取り除く. Bubble wrap is 気泡緩衝材, colloquially プチプチ. Do not confuse with 水泡 \"foam\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"データを保存する前にブラウザがクラッシュしてしまったため、長文のメッセージを書いていた努力が水泡に帰してしまった。","romaji":"Deeta o hozon suru mae ni burauza ga kurasshu shite shimatta tame, choubun no messeeji o kaite ita doryoku ga suihou ni kishite shimatta.","german":"My browser crashed before I could save the data, so all my effort writing a long message came to nothing.","furigana":"ほぞん|まえ|ちょうぶん|か|どりょく|すいほう|き"},{"japanese":"大会の中止で、選手たちの四年間の努力は水泡に帰した。","romaji":"Taikai no chuushi de, senshutachi no yonenkan no doryoku wa suihou ni kishita.","german":"Because the tournament was canceled, the players' four years of effort came to nothing.","furigana":"たいかい|ちゅうし|せんしゅ|よねんかん|どりょく|すいほう|き"},{"japanese":"波が岩に当たって、白い水泡が広がった。","romaji":"Nami ga iwa ni atatte, shiroi suihou ga hirogatta.","german":"The waves hit the rocks and white foam spread out.","furigana":"なみ|いわ|あ|しろ|すいほう|ひろ"}],"notes":"水泡 \"foam, water bubble\" is rare and literary in its literal meaning. Almost always it appears in the set phrase 水泡に帰す (帰す is read きす here) \"to come to nothing, to be in vain\": 努力が水泡に帰す. This is elevated; colloquially one says 水の泡になる. Do not confuse with 水疱 (same reading) \"blister\", in everyday speech 水ぶくれ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"発泡スチロールはこの袋に入れてもらってもいい？他のゴミとは分別して捨てるんだよね。","romaji":"Happou suchirooru wa kono fukuro ni irete moratte mo ii? Hoka no gomi to wa bunbetsu shite suteru n da yo ne.","german":"Can I put the styrofoam in this bag? It has to be separated from the other trash, right?","furigana":"はっぽう|ふくろ|い|ほか|ぶんべつ|す"},{"japanese":"発泡酒はビールより税金が安いので、値段も安い。","romaji":"Happoushu wa biiru yori zeikin ga yasui node, nedan mo yasui.","german":"Happoshu has lower taxes than beer, so it is cheaper too.","furigana":"はっぽうしゅ|ぜいきん|やす|ねだん|やす"},{"japanese":"この入浴剤はお湯に入れると発泡して、炭酸ガスが出る。","romaji":"Kono nyuuyokuzai wa oyu ni ireru to happou shite, tansan gasu ga deru.","german":"When you put this bath additive in hot water, it fizzes and releases carbon dioxide.","furigana":"にゅうよくざい|ゆ|い|はっぽう|たんさん|で"}],"notes":"発泡 \"foaming, formation of foam\" is a technical word and mostly appears in compounds: 発泡スチロール \"styrofoam\", 発泡酒 (beer-like drink with little malt), 発泡性ワイン \"sparkling wine\", 発泡剤. As an intransitive verb 発泡する is rather technical; in daily life one says 泡が出る or 泡立つ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"専業主婦になってからというもの、まるで捕虜になって、台所に幽閉されてでもいるかのようだわ。","romaji":"Sengyou shufu ni natte kara to iu mono, marude horyo ni natte, daidokoro ni yuuhei sarete demo iru ka no you da wa.","german":"Ever since I became a full-time housewife, it's as if I've been taken prisoner and confined in the kitchen.","furigana":"せんぎょうしゅふ|ほりょ|だいどころ|ゆうへい"},{"japanese":"王女は城の塔に何年も幽閉されていた。","romaji":"Oujo wa shiro no tou ni nannen mo yuuhei sarete ita.","german":"The princess was confined in the castle tower for years.","furigana":"おうじょ|しろ|とう|なんねん|ゆうへい"},{"japanese":"クーデターの後、前の国王は遠い島に幽閉された。","romaji":"Kuudetaa no ato, mae no kokuou wa tooi shima ni yuuhei sareta.","german":"After the coup, the former king was confined on a distant island.","furigana":"あと|まえ|こくおう|とお|しま|ゆうへい"}],"notes":"幽閉 means to hold someone in a secluded place, cut off from the outside world, classically kings, princesses or deposed rulers: 塔に幽閉する, 〜に幽閉される. The word is written-language and sounds historical or fairy-tale-like. 監禁 is confinement as a crime (kidnapping), 投獄 is putting someone in prison; in daily life one says 閉じ込める.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ある錬金術師が鉛を金に変えようとして失敗した日、外は雨がザーザー降りでした。","romaji":"Aru renkinjutsushi ga namari o kin ni kaeyou to shite shippai shita hi, soto wa ame ga zaazaa buri deshita.","german":"On the day an alchemist tried and failed to turn lead into gold, rain was pouring outside.","furigana":"れんきんじゅつし|なまり|きん|か|しっぱい|ひ|そと|あめ|ぶ"},{"japanese":"中世のヨーロッパでは、多くの学者が錬金術の研究に熱中した。","romaji":"Chuusei no Yooroppa de wa, ooku no gakusha ga renkinjutsu no kenkyuu ni netchuu shita.","german":"In medieval Europe, many scholars were absorbed in the study of alchemy.","furigana":"ちゅうせい|おお|がくしゃ|れんきんじゅつ|けんきゅう|ねっちゅう"},{"japanese":"元手なしで大金を生む「錬金術」のような投資話には、気をつけたほうがいい。","romaji":"Motode nashi de taikin o umu renkinjutsu no you na toushi banashi ni wa, ki o tsuketa hou ga ii.","german":"You should be careful of investment pitches like \"alchemy\" that make big money without capital.","furigana":"もとで|たいきん|う|れんきんじゅつ|とうしばなし|き"}],"notes":"錬金術 is alchemy, the attempt to turn base metals into gold; an alchemist is a 錬金術師. Figuratively it denotes (often critically) a method of making money out of nothing: 現代の錬金術, 〜は一種の錬金術だ. In this meaning it is often put in quotation marks.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お米は私達の主食ですが、だからといって日本人全員が風に靡く美しい金色の稲穂を見た事がある訳ではありません。","romaji":"O kome wa watashitachi no shushoku desu ga, dakaratoitte nipponjin zen'in ga kaze ni nabiku utsukushii kin'iro no inaho o mita koto ga aru wake de wa arimasen.","german":"Rice is our staple food, but that doesn't mean every Japanese person has seen beautiful golden ears of rice swaying in the wind.","furigana":"こめ|わたしたち|しゅしょく|にっぽんじんぜんいん|かぜ|なび|うつく|きんいろ|いなほ|み|こと|わけ"},{"japanese":"秋になると、田んぼの稲穂が黄金色に実る。","romaji":"Aki ni naru to, tanbo no inaho ga koganeiro ni minoru.","german":"In autumn, the ears of rice in the paddies ripen to a golden color.","furigana":"あき|た|いなほ|こがねいろ|みの"},{"japanese":"実るほど頭を垂れる稲穂かな。","romaji":"Minoru hodo koube o tareru inaho kana.","german":"An ear of rice that bows lower the more it ripens.","furigana":"みの||た|いなほ"}],"notes":"稲穂 is the ear of the rice plant (稲 + 穂). Typical: 稲穂が実る \"the ears ripen\", 稲穂が垂れる/揺れる, 黄金色の稲穂. Well known is the proverb 実るほど頭を垂れる稲穂かな: the riper the ear, the lower it bows - true greatness shows in humility. Distinguish 稲 (plant), 米 (grain) and ご飯 (cooked rice).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ローマ帝政時代、皇帝はその村の人達に目をつけていました。","romaji":"Rooma teisei jidai, koutei wa sono mura no hitotachi ni me o tsukete imashita.","german":"During the Roman imperial period, the emperor had his eye on the people of that village.","furigana":"ていせいじだい|こうてい|むら|ひとたち|め"},{"japanese":"ロシアの帝政は、1917年の革命によって終わりを迎えた。","romaji":"Roshia no teisei wa, sen kyuuhyaku juunana nen no kakumei ni yotte owari o mukaeta.","german":"Russia's imperial rule came to an end with the revolution of 1917.","furigana":"ていせい|ねん|かくめい|お|むか"},{"japanese":"帝政時代の建物が、今は博物館として使われている。","romaji":"Teisei jidai no tatemono ga, ima wa hakubutsukan to shite tsukawarete iru.","german":"A building from the imperial era is now used as a museum.","furigana":"ていせいじだい|たてもの|いま|はくぶつかん|つか"}],"notes":"帝政 is rule by an emperor as a form of government, i.e. \"imperial rule, monarchy\". Typical: ローマ帝政 \"Roman imperial period\" (as opposed to the republic), 帝政ロシア \"Tsarist Russia\", 帝政時代, 帝政が崩壊する. Opposites are 共和制 \"republic\" and 王政 \"royal rule\"; 帝国, by contrast, refers to the empire as a state.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"辺りの景色は瞬く間に雪に覆われ、真っ白になった。","romaji":"Atari no keshiki wa matataku ma ni yuki ni ooware, masshiro ni natta.","german":"The surrounding scenery was covered in snow in an instant and turned pure white.","furigana":"あた|けしき|またた|ま|ゆき|おお|ま|しろ"},{"japanese":"冬の夜空に星が瞬いている。","romaji":"Fuyu no yozora ni hoshi ga matataite iru.","german":"Stars are twinkling in the winter night sky.","furigana":"ふゆ|よぞら|ほし|またた"},{"japanese":"遠くの町の明かりが、霧の中でかすかに瞬いていた。","romaji":"Tooku no machi no akari ga, kiri no naka de kasuka ni matataite ita.","german":"The lights of a distant town flickered faintly in the fog.","furigana":"とお|まち|あ|きり|なか|またた"}],"notes":"瞬く mainly means \"to twinkle, to flicker\" of light: 星が瞬く, 明かりが瞬く. The meaning \"to blink\" also exists, but in daily life one says まばたきする for that. Very common is the set phrase 瞬く間に \"in a flash, in no time\". It is intransitive; 瞬く is occasionally also read しばたたく (to blink the eyes).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"蜘蛛の巣にぶつかった瞬間に、人は突然空手の達人へと変身する。","romaji":"Kumo no su ni butsukatta shunkan ni, hito wa totsuzen karate no tatsujin e to henshin suru.","german":"The moment you run into a spider web, you suddenly turn into a karate master.","furigana":"くも|す|しゅんかん|ひと|とつぜんからて|たつじん|へんしん"},{"japanese":"ドアを開けた瞬間、猫が外に飛び出した。","romaji":"Doa o aketa shunkan, neko ga soto ni tobidashita.","german":"The moment I opened the door, the cat dashed outside.","furigana":"あ|しゅんかん|ねこ|そと|と|だ"},{"japanese":"ゴールが決まった瞬間を、カメラが見事にとらえた。","romaji":"Gooru ga kimatta shunkan o, kamera ga migoto ni toraeta.","german":"The camera superbly captured the moment the goal was scored.","furigana":"き|しゅんかん|みごと"}],"notes":"瞬間 is a point in time, the \"instant, moment\". Very common after the past form: 〜た瞬間(に) \"the moment when ...\". Other combinations: 決定的瞬間 \"the decisive moment\", 瞬間的に \"in a flash\". 一瞬, by contrast, emphasizes the short duration (\"for a moment\"), whereas 瞬間 emphasizes the exact point in time.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"言いたかったことを彼が瞬時にズバッと言ってくれたので、とても感心しました。","romaji":"Iitakatta koto o kare ga shunji ni zubatto itte kureta node, totemo kanshin shimashita.","german":"He said exactly what I wanted to say in an instant and straight to the point, and I was very impressed.","furigana":"い|かれ|しゅんじ|い|かんしん"},{"japanese":"このアプリは、撮った写真の文字を瞬時に翻訳してくれる。","romaji":"Kono apuri wa, totta shashin no moji o shunji ni hon'yaku shite kureru.","german":"This app instantly translates the text in the photos you take.","furigana":"と|しゃしん|もじ|しゅんじ|ほんやく"},{"japanese":"運転中は、瞬時の判断が事故を防ぐこともある。","romaji":"Untenchuu wa, shunji no handan ga jiko o fusegu koto mo aru.","german":"While driving, a split-second decision can sometimes prevent an accident.","furigana":"うんてんちゅう|しゅんじ|はんだん|じこ|ふせ"}],"notes":"瞬時 \"instant, split second\" is more elevated than 一瞬 and appears almost always adverbially as 瞬時に \"instantly, immediately\" or attributively as 瞬時の判断 \"split-second decision\". Frequent in technical and news texts: 瞬時に計算する, 瞬時に反応する. In conversation, すぐに or 一瞬で sounds more natural.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"家の前に大量の白菊を並べるなんて、迷惑行為以上のことだよ。だって、白菊は日本では死者を弔う花だからね。言ってる意味が分かりますか？","romaji":"Ie no mae ni tairyou no shiragiku o naraberu nante, meiwaku koui ijou no koto da yo. Datte, shiragiku wa Nihon de wa shisha o tomurau hana da kara ne. Itteru imi ga wakarimasu ka?","german":"Lining up masses of white chrysanthemums in front of someone's house is worse than a nuisance. After all, white chrysanthemums are flowers for mourning the dead in Japan. Do you understand what I mean?","furigana":"いえ|まえ|たいりょう|しらぎく|なら|めいわくこういいじょう|しらぎく|にほん|ししゃ|とむら|はな|い|いみ|わ"},{"japanese":"お墓参りに白菊の花束を持っていった。","romaji":"Ohakamairi ni shiragiku no hanataba o motte itta.","german":"I took a bouquet of white chrysanthemums when visiting the grave.","furigana":"はかまい|しらぎく|はなたば|も"},{"japanese":"葬儀の祭壇は、たくさんの白菊で飾られていた。","romaji":"Sougi no saidan wa, takusan no shiragiku de kazararete ita.","german":"The funeral altar was decorated with many white chrysanthemums.","furigana":"そうぎ|さいだん|しらぎく|かざ"}],"notes":"白菊 is the white chrysanthemum (also read しろぎく). In Japan it is the typical flower for funerals, graves and remembrance of the dead (お墓参り, 葬儀, お盆). For this reason, white chrysanthemums are not given to the sick or on happy occasions. The chrysanthemum in general (菊) is also a symbol of the imperial house.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"四人に一人が広告は誇張されていると思っていることは知っていますが、私は全部が全部誇大広告ではないと思うんです。","romaji":"Yonin ni hitori ga kōkoku wa kochō sarete iru to omotte iru koto wa shitte imasu ga, watashi wa zenbu ga zenbu kodai kōkoku de wa nai to omou n desu.","german":"I know that one in four people think advertising is exaggerated, but I don't think all of it is exaggerated advertising.","furigana":"よにん|ひとり|こうこく|こちょう|おも|し|わたし|ぜんぶ|ぜんぶこだいこうこく|おも"},{"japanese":"その会社は誇大広告で行政から注意を受けた。","romaji":"Sono kaisha wa kodai kōkoku de gyōsei kara chūi o uketa.","german":"The company received a warning from the authorities for exaggerated advertising.","furigana":"かいしゃ|こだいこうこく|ぎょうせい|ちゅうい|う"},{"japanese":"彼の話はいつも誇大だから、話半分に聞いておいたほうがいい。","romaji":"Kare no hanashi wa itsumo kodai da kara, hanashi hanbun ni kiite oita hō ga ii.","german":"His stories are always exaggerated, so you should take them with a grain of salt.","furigana":"かれ|はなし|こだい|はなしはんぶん|き"}],"notes":"誇大 means \"exaggerated, inflated\" and appears mainly in set compounds: 誇大広告 \"exaggerated, misleading advertising\", 誇大表現, 誇大妄想 \"delusions of grandeur\". As a な-adjective: 誇大な宣伝. The related 誇張 denotes the act of exaggerating (誇張する), whereas 誇大 describes the quality. In daily life one simply says 大げさ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"瞬きせずに、この絵を見続けてください。","romaji":"Mabataki sezu ni, kono e o mitsuzukete kudasai.","german":"Don't blink, just keep looking at the picture.","furigana":"まばた|え|みつづ"},{"japanese":"子どもたちは瞬きもせずに手品を見つめていた。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa mabataki mo sezu ni tejina o mitsumete ita.","german":"The children followed the magic trick spellbound, without even blinking.","furigana":"こ|まばた|てじな|み"},{"japanese":"パソコンを長時間使うと瞬きが減って、目が乾きやすくなる。","romaji":"Pasokon o choujikan tsukau to mabataki ga hette, me ga kawakiyasuku naru.","german":"When you sit at a computer for a long time, you blink less and your eyes dry out more easily.","furigana":"ちょうじかんつか|まばた|へ|め|かわ"}],"notes":"瞬き \"blinking, blink of an eye\" is usually written in hiragana まばたき; as an intransitive verb まばたきする. Typical: 瞬きもせずに見つめる \"to stare without blinking\", 瞬きする間に \"in the blink of an eye\". A deliberate wink, by contrast, is ウインク. Do not confuse with 瞬く (またたく) \"to twinkle\"; the same spelling 瞬き can also be read またたき \"twinkling (of stars)\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「お前には捕まらないぞ！」「捕まえたっ！」「うわっ！速い！一瞬だったな。でも、大人げないよ。子どもなんだからもう少し手加減してよ。」","romaji":"Omae ni wa tsukamaranai zo! Tsukamaeta! Uwa! Hayai! Isshun datta na. Demo, otonagenai yo. Kodomo na n da kara mou sukoshi tekagen shite yo.","german":"\"You won't catch me!\" \"Got you!\" \"Whoa! That was fast. It was over in a flash. But that's not fair of you. I'm just a kid, so go a bit easier on me.\""},{"japanese":"一瞬、何が起きたのか分からなかった。","romaji":"Isshun, nani ga okita no ka wakaranakatta.","german":"For a moment, I didn't understand what had happened.","furigana":"いっしゅん|なに|お|わ"},{"japanese":"雲の間から、一瞬だけ富士山が見えた。","romaji":"Kumo no aida kara, isshun dake Fujisan ga mieta.","german":"Mount Fuji was visible between the clouds for just an instant.","furigana":"くも|あいだ|いっしゅん|ふじさん|み"}],"notes":"一瞬 is a very short moment and is used both as a noun and as an adverb: 一瞬、言葉を失った \"for a moment I was speechless\". Typical: 一瞬だけ, 一瞬のうちに/一瞬で \"in no time\", 一瞬の出来事. In contrast to 瞬間 (exact point in time, 〜た瞬間), 一瞬 emphasizes the shortness of the duration.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この短編映画では、日本が世界に誇るロボット産業をテーマとしています。","romaji":"Kono tanpen eiga de wa, Nippon ga sekai ni hokoru robotto sangyō o tēma to shite imasu.","german":"This short film is about the robotics industry that Japan is proud of in the world.","furigana":"たんぺんえいが|にっぽん|せかい|ほこ|さんぎょう"},{"japanese":"この寺は千年以上の歴史を誇っている。","romaji":"Kono tera wa sennen ijou no rekishi o hokotte iru.","german":"This temple can boast a history of over a thousand years.","furigana":"てら|せんねんいじょう|れきし|ほこ"},{"japanese":"彼は自分の成功を誇ることなく、いつも謙虚だ。","romaji":"Kare wa jibun no seikou o hokoru koto naku, itsumo kenkyo da.","german":"He never boasts of his success and always remains modest.","furigana":"かれ|じぶん|せいこう|ほこ|けんきょ"}],"notes":"誇る is transitive: Xを誇る \"to be proud of X, to pride oneself on something\". It is especially common for describing what distinguishes something: 世界一の高さを誇る, 長い歴史を誇る, 日本が世界に誇る技術. For personal pride one rather says 誇りに思う or 誇らしい in conversation. 自慢する is more colloquial and can sound like bragging. Noun: 誇り \"pride\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"レビューで今までで最高のカメラだとか何とか書かれてたけど、それはやっぱり誇張だったね。","romaji":"Rebyuu de ima made de saikou no kamera da toka nantoka kakareteta kedo, sore wa yappari kochou datta ne.","german":"The review said it was the best camera of all time, but that was a bit of an exaggeration.","furigana":"いま|さいこう|なん|か|こちょう"},{"japanese":"彼は話を誇張する癖があるから、半分くらいに聞いておいたほうがいい。","romaji":"Kare wa hanashi o kochou suru kuse ga aru kara, hanbun kurai ni kiite oita hou ga ii.","german":"He tends to exaggerate, so you'd better believe only half of what he says.","furigana":"かれ|はなし|こちょう|くせ|はんぶん|き"},{"japanese":"誇張ではなく、本当に三時間も並んだんです。","romaji":"Kochou de wa naku, hontou ni sanjikan mo naranda n desu.","german":"That's no exaggeration - we really did wait in line for three hours.","furigana":"こちょう|ほんとう|さんじかん|なら"}],"notes":"誇張 is exaggerating in presentation: 話を誇張する, 誇張した表現, 誇張して伝える. Common in denials: 誇張ではなく \"it's no exaggeration\". The word is somewhat formal; in daily life one says 大げさだ, オーバーだ or, in youth slang, 話を盛る.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"庄園制度導入への準備は全て整っています。","romaji":"Shōen seido dōnyū e no junbi wa subete totonotte imasu.","german":"All preparations for introducing the manor system are complete.","furigana":"しょうえんせいどどうにゅう|じゅんび|すべ|ととの"},{"japanese":"平安時代には、貴族や寺社が各地に庄園を持っていた。","romaji":"Heian jidai ni wa, kizoku ya jisha ga kakuchi ni shouen o motte ita.","german":"In the Heian period, nobles and temples and shrines held manors in various places.","furigana":"へいあんじだい|きぞく|じしゃ|かくち|しょうえん|も"},{"japanese":"中世ヨーロッパの庄園では、農民が領主のために畑を耕した。","romaji":"Chuusei Yooroppa no shouen de wa, noumin ga ryoushu no tame ni hatake o tagayashita.","german":"On medieval European manors, peasants cultivated the fields for the lord.","furigana":"ちゅうせい|しょうえん|のうみん|りょうしゅ|はたけ|たがや"}],"notes":"庄園 is a variant of 荘園 (textbook spelling) and denotes a private landed estate or manorial lordship. In Japanese history (Nara to Muromachi periods), such estates belonged to nobles, temples and shrines; this is called the 荘園制度. The word is also used for medieval European manors. Purely a historical technical term.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その証人が法廷で証言するのを、何としてでも阻止するんだ。","romaji":"Sono shōnin ga hōtei de shōgen suru no o, nani to shite demo soshi suru n da.","german":"Do everything to prevent this witness from testifying in court.","furigana":"しょうにん|ほうてい|しょうげん|なに|そし"},{"japanese":"警察は犯人の逃走を阻止した。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa hannin no tousou o soshi shita.","german":"The police prevented the culprit's escape.","furigana":"けいさつ|はんにん|とうそう|そし"},{"japanese":"決勝点を阻止しようと、ゴールキーパーが必死に飛び込んだ。","romaji":"Kesshouten o soshi shiyou to, goorukiipaa ga hisshi ni tobikonda.","german":"To prevent the winning goal, the goalkeeper threw himself desperately at the ball.","furigana":"けっしょうてん|そし|ひっし|と|こ"}],"notes":"阻止 means to actively stop an action or plan before it succeeds: 侵入を阻止する, 計画を阻止する, 〜するのを阻止する. The word is formal and typical of news and sports. 防ぐ is more general (\"to prevent, to ward off\"); 阻害 means only \"to hinder, to impede\", not to prevent completely.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"カフェインを含む飲み物は、睡眠を阻害します。","romaji":"Kafein o fukumu nomimono wa, suimin o sogai shimasu.","german":"Drinks containing caffeine interfere with sleep.","furigana":"ふく|の|もの|すいみん|そがい"},{"japanese":"行き過ぎた規制が、経済の成長を阻害している。","romaji":"Ikisugita kisei ga, keizai no seichou o sogai shite iru.","german":"Excessive regulation is hampering economic growth.","furigana":"い|す|きせい|けいざい|せいちょう|そがい"},{"japanese":"言葉の壁が、外国人社員の昇進を阻害する要因になっている。","romaji":"Kotoba no kabe ga, gaikokujin shain no shoushin o sogai suru youin ni natte iru.","german":"The language barrier is a factor hindering the promotion of foreign employees.","furigana":"ことば|かべ|がいこくじんしゃいん|しょうしん|そがい|よういん"}],"notes":"阻害 means \"to hinder, to impede, to impair\", mostly of developments and processes: 成長を阻害する, 発展を阻害する, 阻害要因 \"inhibiting factor\". Formal, typical of technical and newspaper texts; in everyday language 妨げる or 邪魔する. Unlike 阻止, something is not completely prevented. Do not confuse with the identically read 疎外 \"alienation, exclusion\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どうしたの？どうして黙っているの？","romaji":"Dō shita no? Dōshite damatte iru no?","german":"What's wrong? Why are you so quiet?","furigana":"だま"},{"japanese":"黙って人の物を使わないでください。","romaji":"Damatte hito no mono o tsukawanaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't use other people's things without asking.","furigana":"だま|ひと|もの|つか"},{"japanese":"先生に注意されて、生徒たちは急に黙った。","romaji":"Sensei ni chuui sarete, seitotachi wa kyuu ni damatta.","german":"When the teacher scolded them, the students suddenly fell silent.","furigana":"せんせい|ちゅうい|せいと|きゅう|だま"}],"notes":"黙る is intransitive: \"to fall silent, to keep quiet\"; the state is 黙っている. Very common is 黙って \"without saying anything, without asking\": 黙って帰る, 黙って使う. 黙れ \"Shut up!\" is rude; more polite is 静かにしてください. Related: 黙り込む \"to lapse into silence\", causative 黙らせる \"to silence\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"気まずい沈黙の後、私は塩の入った小瓶を掴みました。","romaji":"Kimazui chinmoku no nochi, watashi wa shio no haitta kobin o tsukamimashita.","german":"There was an awkward silence, and I reached for a salt shaker.","furigana":"き|ちんもく|のち|わたし|しお|はい|こびん|つか"},{"japanese":"記者に質問されても、大臣は沈黙を守った。","romaji":"Kisha ni shitsumon sarete mo, daijin wa chinmoku o mamotta.","german":"Even when asked by journalists, the minister kept silent.","furigana":"きしゃ|しつもん|だいじん|ちんもく|まも"},{"japanese":"長い沈黙を破って、その歌手が新しいアルバムを発表した。","romaji":"Nagai chinmoku o yabutte, sono kashu ga atarashii arubamu o happyou shita.","german":"After a long silence, the singer returned with a new album.","furigana":"なが|ちんもく|やぶ|かしゅ|あたら|はっぴょう"}],"notes":"沈黙 is silence as a state: 沈黙が続く/流れる, 気まずい沈黙. Fixed combinations: 沈黙を守る \"to maintain silence\", 沈黙を破る \"to break the silence\". As a verb, 沈黙する is formal (news, literature); in daily life one says 黙る. Proverb: 沈黙は金 \"silence is golden\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"警察はその地域では多くの犯罪を黙殺しています。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa sono chiiki de wa ōku no hanzai o mokusatsu shite imasu.","german":"The police are turning a blind eye to many crimes in that area.","furigana":"けいさつ|ちいき|おお|はんざい|もくさつ"},{"japanese":"住民の反対意見は、市によって黙殺された。","romaji":"Juumin no hantai iken wa, shi ni yotte mokusatsu sareta.","german":"The residents' objections were ignored by the city.","furigana":"じゅうみん|はんたいいけん|し|もくさつ"},{"japanese":"会社は社員からの改善の提案を黙殺し続けた。","romaji":"Kaisha wa shain kara no kaizen no teian o mokusatsu shitsuzuketa.","german":"The company kept ignoring the improvement proposals from its employees.","furigana":"かいしゃ|しゃいん|かいぜん|ていあん|もくさつ|つづ"}],"notes":"黙殺 means to deliberately pass over something and not react to it - literally \"to kill by silence\". Typical: 意見/要求/抗議を黙殺する, often in the passive 黙殺される. The word is formal and critical (press, politics). 無視する is the neutral everyday word, 見逃す means rather \"to let slide\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"誰かが土俵にパンの欠片を投げ込みました。","romaji":"Dare ka ga dohyō ni pan no kakera o nagekomimashita.","german":"Someone threw a piece of bread into the sumo ring.","furigana":"だれ|どひょう||な|こ"},{"japanese":"力士は土俵に上がる前に、塩をまいた。","romaji":"Rikishi wa dohyou ni agaru mae ni, shio o maita.","german":"Before entering the ring, the wrestler scattered salt.","furigana":"りきし|どひょう|あ|まえ|しお"},{"japanese":"同じ土俵で議論しなければ、話はかみ合わない。","romaji":"Onaji dohyou de giron shinakereba, hanashi wa kamiawanai.","german":"If you don't discuss on the same footing, you end up talking past each other.","furigana":"おな|どひょう|ぎろん|はなし|あ"}],"notes":"土俵 is the sumo ring of packed clay: 土俵に上がる \"to enter the ring\", 土俵を割る \"to be pushed out of the ring\". Figuratively it refers to the arena on which one measures oneself: 同じ土俵に立つ \"to compete on equal terms\", 土俵際 \"at the edge, in a last-ditch situation\". Traditionally women are not allowed to enter the ring.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"水をよく吸収するので、私は綿布を布巾に使います。","romaji":"Mizu o yoku kyūshū suru node, watashi wa menpu o fukin ni tsukaimasu.","german":"Because it absorbs water well, I use cotton cloth as a dishcloth.","furigana":"みず|きゅうしゅう|わたし|めんぷ|ふきん|つか"},{"japanese":"この工場では、昔から綿布を織っている。","romaji":"Kono koujou de wa, mukashi kara menpu o otte iru.","german":"This factory has been weaving cotton cloth since long ago.","furigana":"こうじょう|むかし|めんぷ|お"},{"japanese":"傷口を清潔な綿布で軽く押さえてください。","romaji":"Kizuguchi o seiketsu na menpu de karuku osaete kudasai.","german":"Please press lightly on the wound with a clean cotton cloth.","furigana":"きずぐち|せいけつ|めんぷ|かる|お"}],"notes":"綿布 is cotton fabric or a cloth made of cotton. The word is matter-of-fact and written-language (textile industry, medicine, product descriptions); in daily life one says 綿の布, 木綿 or コットン. Do not confuse: 綿 read めん means the fiber in the fabric, read わた it means cotton wadding or raw cotton.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今まで一度も橋梁を架設したことがありません。","romaji":"Ima made ichido mo kyōryō o kasetsu shita koto ga arimasen.","german":"I have never built a bridge before.","furigana":"いま|いちど||かせつ"},{"japanese":"川に新しい橋を架設する工事が始まった。","romaji":"Kawa ni atarashii hashi o kasetsu suru kouji ga hajimatta.","german":"Construction has begun on a new bridge across the river.","furigana":"かわ|あたら|はし|かせつ|こうじ|はじ"},{"japanese":"山奥の村まで電線が架設されたのは、戦後のことだ。","romaji":"Yamaoku no mura made densen ga kasetsu sareta no wa, sengo no koto da.","german":"It was after the war that electric wires were strung to the village deep in the mountains.","furigana":"やまおく|むら|でんせん|かせつ|せんご"}],"notes":"架設 is a construction and engineering term for erecting something that spans or is laid out: 橋を架設する \"to build a bridge\", 電線/電話線を架設する \"to lay lines\". General building is 建設, installing equipment is 設置. Do not confuse with the identically read 仮設 \"temporary\" (仮設住宅).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"図書館では、必要な本を自分で書架から探して借りることができる。","romaji":"Toshokan de wa, hitsuyou na hon o jibun de shoka kara sagashite kariru koto ga dekiru.","german":"At the library, you can find and borrow the books you need from the shelves yourself.","furigana":"としょかん|ひつよう|ほん|じぶん|しょか|さが|か"},{"japanese":"古い資料は地下の書架に保管されています。","romaji":"Furui shiryou wa chika no shoka ni hokan sarete imasu.","german":"Old materials are stored on the bookshelves in the basement.","furigana":"ふる|しりょう|ちか|しょか|ほかん"},{"japanese":"教授の研究室には、天井まで届く書架が並んでいた。","romaji":"Kyouju no kenkyuushitsu ni wa, tenjou made todoku shoka ga narande ita.","german":"Bookshelves reaching to the ceiling lined the professor's office.","furigana":"きょうじゅ|けんきゅうしつ|てんじょう|とど|しょか|なら"}],"notes":"書架 is the written-language word for \"bookshelf\", typical in libraries and archives: 書架に並ぶ, 書架から本を取り出す, 開架 \"open stacks\" and 閉架 \"closed stacks\". At home one says 本棚; a casual chat about one's own IKEA shelf sounds unnatural with 書架.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あそこの家の息子さん、交通事故に巻き込まれて担架で病院に担ぎ込まれたんだけど、結局植物状態になっちゃったんだって。","romaji":"Asoko no ie no musukosan, koutsuu jiko ni makikomarete tanka de byouin ni katsugikomareta n da kedo, kekkyoku shokubutsu joutai ni nacchatta n da tte.","german":"The son of the family over there was in a traffic accident and was taken to the hospital on a stretcher, but apparently ended up in a persistent vegetative state.","furigana":"いえ|むすこ|こうつうじこ|ま|こ|たんか|びょういん|かつ|こ|けっきょくしょくぶつじょうたい"},{"japanese":"けがをした選手が担架でグラウンドの外に運ばれた。","romaji":"Kega o shita senshu ga tanka de guraundo no soto ni hakobareta.","german":"The injured player was carried off the field on a stretcher.","furigana":"せんしゅ|たんか|そと|はこ"},{"japanese":"救急隊員は二人で担架を持ち上げ、救急車に乗せた。","romaji":"Kyuukyuu taiin wa futari de tanka o mochiage, kyuukyuusha ni noseta.","german":"Two paramedics lifted the stretcher and slid it into the ambulance.","furigana":"きゅうきゅうたいいん|ふたり|たんか|も|あ|きゅうきゅうしゃ|の"}],"notes":"担架 is the stretcher used to carry the injured: 担架で運ぶ/運ばれる, 担架に乗せる. A wheeled stretcher in a hospital is usually called ストレッチャー. Do not confuse with the same-sounding 短歌 (a poem form) and 啖呵 (in 啖呵を切る \"to give someone a sharp piece of one's mind\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は紛れもない田舎っぺの女の子だ。","romaji":"Kanojo wa magire mo nai inakappe no onnanoko da.","german":"She is an unmistakable country girl.","furigana":"かのじょ|まぎ|いなか|おんな|こ"},{"japanese":"これは紛れもない事実です。","romaji":"Kore wa magire mo nai jijitsu desu.","german":"This is an undeniable fact.","furigana":"まぎ|じじつ"},{"japanese":"写真に写っているのは、紛れもなく私の財布だ。","romaji":"Shashin ni utsutte iru no wa, magire mo naku watashi no saifu da.","german":"What appears in the photo is unmistakably my wallet.","furigana":"しゃしん|うつ|まぎ|わたし|さいふ"}],"notes":"紛れもない \"unmistakable, undeniable\" comes from 紛れる \"to be mistaken for, to get lost\": there is nothing it could be confused with. Typically attributive: 紛れもない事実/証拠, adverbial 紛れもなく \"quite clearly, without doubt\". Somewhat elevated; in conversation one often says 間違いなく or 明らかに.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いつ、どうして瞳孔の縮小と拡張が起きるのか、説明できますか？","romaji":"Itsu, dōshite dōkō no shukushō to kakuchō ga okiru no ka, setsumei dekimasu ka?","german":"Can you explain when and why the pupil constricts and dilates?","furigana":"どうこう|しゅくしょう|かくちょう|お|せつめい"},{"japanese":"医者はライトを当てて、患者の瞳孔の反応を確かめた。","romaji":"Isha wa raito o atete, kanja no doukou no hannou o tashikameta.","german":"The doctor shone a light to check the patient's pupil response.","furigana":"いしゃ|あ|かんじゃ|どうこう|はんのう|たし"},{"japanese":"検査で目薬をさすと、瞳孔が開いてしばらくまぶしく感じる。","romaji":"Kensa de megusuri o sasu to, doukou ga hiraite shibaraku mabushiku kanjiru.","german":"When eye drops are used for an examination, the pupils dilate and things look dazzling for a while.","furigana":"けんさ|めぐすり|どうこう|ひら|かん"}],"notes":"瞳孔 is the medical term for the pupil: 瞳孔が開く/拡大する \"to dilate\", 瞳孔が縮む/収縮する \"to constrict\", 瞳孔反射. In daily life and literature one rather says 瞳 (ひとみ), which also stands poetically for \"eyes\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"頭蓋骨の標本には、大きな眼孔が二つ開いている。","romaji":"Zugaikotsu no hyouhon ni wa, ookina gankou ga futatsu aite iru.","german":"The skull specimen has two large eye sockets.","furigana":"ずがいこつ|ひょうほん|おお|がんこう|ふた|あ"},{"japanese":"おもちゃのがいこつは、眼孔の奥が赤く光るようになっている。","romaji":"Omocha no gaikotsu wa, gankou no oku ga akaku hikaru you ni natte iru.","german":"The toy skeleton is made so that the depths of its eye sockets glow red.","furigana":"がんこう|おく|あか|ひか"}],"notes":"眼孔 is the eye socket in the skull. The word is rare and technical; in medicine 眼窩 (がんか) is more common, in daily life one speaks of 目のくぼみ. Do not confuse with the identically read 眼光 \"gaze, gleam of the eyes\" (鋭い眼光).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"夫は、いつも寸暇を惜しんで働いています。","romaji":"Otto wa, itsumo sunka o oshinde hataraite imasu.","german":"My husband is always working, begrudging every spare moment.","furigana":"おっと|すんか|お|はたら"},{"japanese":"彼女は寸暇を惜しんで資格の勉強をしている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa sunka o oshinde shikaku no benkyou o shite iru.","german":"She studies for her certification, making use of every spare moment.","furigana":"かのじょ|すんか|お|しかく|べんきょう"},{"japanese":"忙しくて寸暇もない毎日が続いている。","romaji":"Isogashikute sunka mo nai mainichi ga tsuzuite iru.","german":"Busy days without a moment to spare continue.","furigana":"いそが|すんか|まいにち|つづ"}],"notes":"寸暇 \"a short moment of free time\" (寸 = tiny, 暇 = free time) occurs almost only in the set phrase 寸暇を惜しんで〜: \"to leave no spare minute unused, to use every minute to ...\". Besides that, 寸暇もない \"not to have even a spare minute\". Elevated and written-language; the form 寸暇を惜しまず is a common mistake.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"木綿のハンカチをプレゼントしてもらいました。","romaji":"Momen no hankachi o purezento shite moraimashita.","german":"I received a cotton handkerchief as a present.","furigana":"もめん"},{"japanese":"このシャツは木綿だから、洗っても大丈夫です。","romaji":"Kono shatsu wa momen da kara, aratte mo daijōbu desu.","german":"This shirt is cotton, so it's fine to wash it.","furigana":"もめん|あら|だいじょうぶ"},{"japanese":"スーパーで木綿豆腐を買ってきてください。","romaji":"Sūpā de momen dōfu o katte kite kudasai.","german":"Please buy firm tofu at the supermarket.","furigana":"もめんどうふ|か"}],"notes":"木綿 is cotton as a fabric: 木綿のシャツ, 木綿のハンカチ. Today one often says コットン in everyday speech, and on labels 綿 (めん) appears: 綿100%. Also well known is 木綿豆腐 \"firm tofu\" as opposed to the soft 絹ごし豆腐. The cotton plant or cotton wool itself is more likely called 綿 (わた).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私はその神社で心願成就の御札を買いました。","romaji":"Watashi wa sono jinja de shingan jōju no ofuda o kaimashita.","german":"I bought a charm for the fulfillment of wishes at that shrine.","furigana":"わたし|じんじゃ|しんがんじょうじゅ|おふだ|か"},{"japanese":"長年の心願がかなって、ついに自分の店を開くことができた。","romaji":"Naganen no shingan ga kanatte, tsui ni jibun no mise o hiraku koto ga dekita.","german":"My long-held wish came true, and I was finally able to open my own shop.","furigana":"ながねん|しんがん|じぶん|みせ|ひら"},{"japanese":"絵馬には、合格を願う心願が数多く書かれていた。","romaji":"Ema ni wa, goukaku o negau shingan ga kazu ooku kakarete ita.","german":"Many wishes for passing exams were written on the ema plaques.","furigana":"えま|ごうかく|ねが|しんがん|かずおお|か"}],"notes":"心願 is a fervent wish of the heart, especially a wish addressed to gods or Buddha. Very common is the set phrase 心願成就 \"fulfillment of one's heartfelt wish\" on amulets and votive plaques. Other combinations: 心願がかなう, 長年の心願. In daily life one says 願い; 念願 is a long-cherished wish without religious overtones.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"十九世紀の欧米では、女権の拡大を求める運動が広がった。","romaji":"Juukyuu seiki no Oubei de wa, joken no kakudai o motomeru undou ga hirogatta.","german":"In nineteenth-century Europe and America, a movement demanding the expansion of women's rights spread.","furigana":"じゅうきゅうせいき|おうべい|じょけん|かくだい|もと|うんどう|ひろ"},{"japanese":"平塚らいてうは、日本の女権運動を代表する人物の一人だ。","romaji":"Hiratsuka Raichou wa, Nihon no joken undou o daihyou suru jinbutsu no hitori da.","german":"Hiratsuka Raichō is one of the figures representing the women's rights movement in Japan."},{"japanese":"大学で、女権の歴史についてレポートを書いた。","romaji":"Daigaku de, joken no rekishi ni tsuite repooto o kaita.","german":"I wrote a report on the history of women's rights at university.","furigana":"だいがく|じょけん|れきし|か"}],"notes":"女権 \"women's rights\" is a somewhat old-fashioned, mainly historical term: 女権運動 \"women's rights movement\", 女権拡張 \"expansion of women's rights\". Today one mostly says 女性の権利 or 女性の人権. A \"matriarchal society\" is not called 女権社会 but 母系社会 or 女系社会.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ペティコートは、スカートが肌と擦れることを防ぎます。","romaji":"Petikōto wa, sukāto ga hada to sureru koto o fusegimasu.","german":"Slips prevent skirts from rubbing against the skin.","furigana":"はだ|す|ふせ"},{"japanese":"靴が足に擦れて、かかとが痛い。","romaji":"Kutsu ga ashi ni surete, kakato ga itai.","german":"The shoe chafes, and my heel hurts.","furigana":"くつ|あし|す|いた"},{"japanese":"このジーンズは膝のところが擦れて薄くなっている。","romaji":"Kono jīnzu wa hiza no tokoro ga surete usuku natte iru.","german":"These jeans have worn thin and frayed at the knees.","furigana":"ひざ|す|うす"}],"notes":"擦れる is intransitive: two surfaces \"rub against each other\" (〜と擦れる, 〜に擦れる) or something \"wears off, wears out\": 袖口が擦れる. The transitive partner is 擦る (する) \"to rub\". Figuratively, 擦れた人 describes someone who has lost their naivety and become hardened; the opposite is 擦れていない \"unspoiled\". The word is often written in kana すれる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"トフグのコウイチに万歳三唱をしましょう！万歳！万歳！万歳！","romaji":"Tofugu no Kouichi ni banzai sanshou o shimashou! Banzai! Banzai! Banzai!","german":"Let's give three cheers for Koichi of Tofugu! Banzai! Banzai! Banzai!","furigana":"ばんざいさんしょう|ばんざい|ばんざい|ばんざい"},{"japanese":"当選が決まると、事務所で支持者たちが万歳をした。","romaji":"Tousen ga kimaru to, jimusho de shijishatachi ga banzai o shita.","german":"When the election win was confirmed, supporters cheered \"banzai\" at the office.","furigana":"とうせん|き|じむしょ|しじしゃ|ばんざい"},{"japanese":"合格の知らせを聞いて、思わず万歳と叫んだ。","romaji":"Goukaku no shirase o kiite, omowazu banzai to sakenda.","german":"Hearing the news that I had passed, I involuntarily shouted \"banzai.\"","furigana":"ごうかく|し|き|おも|ばんざい|さけ"}],"notes":"万歳 is the cheer \"Hurrah! Hooray!\", during which one throws both arms up. Typical: 万歳をする, 万歳三唱 \"three cheers of banzai\" at election wins and celebrations, 〜万歳 \"Long live ...!\". Colloquially 万歳(する) also means \"to give up, to throw up one's hands\" (like お手上げ). Read まんざい, by contrast, it is a traditional comedy performance at New Year.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"キッチンペーパーをちょっと湿らせて拭きました。","romaji":"Kitchin peepaa o chotto shimerasete fukimashita.","german":"I dampened a paper towel a little and wiped.","furigana":"しめ|ふ"},{"japanese":"乾燥した部屋では、ぬれタオルを干して空気を湿らせるといい。","romaji":"Kansou shita heya de wa, nure taoru o hoshite kuuki o shimeraseru to ii.","german":"In a dry room, it helps to hang up a wet towel to humidify the air.","furigana":"かんそう|へや|ほ|くうき|しめ"},{"japanese":"のどが渇いていたので、水で少し口を湿らせた。","romaji":"Nodo ga kawaite ita node, mizu de sukoshi kuchi o shimeraseta.","german":"I was thirsty, so I moistened my mouth a little with water.","furigana":"かわ|みず|すこ|くち|しめ"}],"notes":"湿らせる is transitive: \"to moisten something slightly\", e.g. 布/タオルを湿らせる, 空気を湿らせる. The intransitive counterpart is 湿る \"to become damp\" (湿った服); equivalent in meaning but more elevated is 湿す. 濡らす means \"to make wet\" with more water, 湿らせる only a little.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この道は現在砕石舗装工事中です。","romaji":"Kono michi wa genzai saiseki hosō kōjichū desu.","german":"This road is currently under crushed-stone paving work.","furigana":"みち|げんざいさいせきほそうこうじちゅう"},{"japanese":"線路の下には、砕石がたくさん敷かれている。","romaji":"Senro no shita ni wa, saiseki ga takusan shikarete iru.","german":"A lot of crushed stone is laid under the railway tracks.","furigana":"せんろ|した|さいせき|し"},{"japanese":"駐車場に砕石を敷いて、水はけを良くした。","romaji":"Chuushajou ni saiseki o shiite, mizuhake o yoku shita.","german":"I laid crushed stone in the parking lot to improve drainage.","furigana":"ちゅうしゃじょう|さいせき|し|みず|よ"}],"notes":"砕石 is broken-up rock, i.e. crushed stone or gravel as used in road and rail construction: 砕石を敷く, 砕石舗装, 砕石場 \"quarry\". 砂利, by contrast, denotes natural, rather rounded gravel; in daily life both are often called 砂利. A technical word from construction.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の従兄弟は、転んで左足の膝のお皿を粉砕骨折しました。","romaji":"Watashi no itoko wa, koronde hidariashi no hiza no osara o funsai kossetsu shimashita.","german":"My cousin fell and suffered a comminuted fracture of the left kneecap.","furigana":"わたし|いとこ|ころ|ひだりあし|ひざ|さら|ふんさいこっせつ"},{"japanese":"集めたペットボトルは、機械で細かく粉砕されてリサイクルされる。","romaji":"Atsumeta pettobotoru wa, kikai de komakaku funsai sarete risaikuru sareru.","german":"The collected plastic bottles are finely crushed by machine and recycled.","furigana":"あつ|きかい|こま|ふんさい"},{"japanese":"決勝戦で、チームは強豪を5対0で粉砕した。","romaji":"Kesshousen de, chiimu wa kyougou o go tai zero de funsai shita.","german":"In the final, the team crushed the powerhouse 5-0.","furigana":"けっしょうせん|きょうごう|たい|ふんさい"}],"notes":"粉砕 means \"to grind, to smash\": 機械で粉砕する, 粉砕骨折 \"comminuted fracture\". Figuratively, and more in sports and press language, 粉砕する means \"to beat an opponent or plan crushingly\": 強豪を粉砕する. Compared with the native 砕く, it is more formal and sounds like complete destruction.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ええっ！あのセールスマンまだうちの母ちゃんと話をしてるよ。粘るね〜！","romaji":"Ē! Ano sērusuman mada uchi no kāchan to hanashi o shiteru yo. Nebaru nē!","german":"Wow! The salesman is still talking to my mother. How persistent!"},{"japanese":"よく混ぜると、納豆はもっと粘る。","romaji":"Yoku mazeru to, nattō wa motto nebaru.","german":"If you stir natto well, it becomes even stickier.","furigana":"ま|なっとう|ねば"},{"japanese":"試合は負けたけど、最後まで粘ってよく頑張った。","romaji":"Shiai wa maketa kedo, saigo made nebatte yoku ganbatta.","german":"We lost the game, but we fought to the end and didn't give up.","furigana":"しあい|ま|さいご|ねば|がんば"}],"notes":"粘る has two meanings: literally \"to be sticky, to be tough\" (納豆が粘る), figuratively \"to not give up, to stay at it stubbornly\" (最後まで粘る). In the second sense one says e.g. 粘って交渉する or, jokingly, コーヒー一杯で三時間粘る \"to sit for three hours over one coffee\". Compared with 頑張る, 粘る emphasizes tenaciously holding out even when it seems hopeless. Related are 粘り強い \"persevering\" and ねばねば \"sticky\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"粘土を買おうかなと思ってるの？","romaji":"Nendo o kaou ka na to omotteru no?","german":"Are you thinking of buying clay?","furigana":"ねんど|か|おも"},{"japanese":"子どもたちは粘土で動物を作って遊んだ。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa nendo de doubutsu o tsukutte asonda.","german":"The children played making animals out of clay.","furigana":"こ|ねんど|どうぶつ|つく|あそ"},{"japanese":"この地域の粘土は、焼き物に向いていることで有名だ。","romaji":"Kono chiiki no nendo wa, yakimono ni muite iru koto de yuumei da.","german":"The clay of this region is famous for being suited to pottery.","furigana":"ちいき|ねんど|や|もの|む|ゆうめい"}],"notes":"粘土 is both natural clay (粘土質の土, 陶芸用の粘土) and modeling clay for play and crafts (油粘土, 紙粘土). Typical: 粘土をこねる \"to knead clay\", 粘土で〜を作る. The 粘 is also in 粘る \"to be sticky, to be tenacious\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どうして粘着テープを宝石箱に仕舞っているの？","romaji":"Dōshite nenchaku tēpu o hōsekibako ni shimatte iru no?","german":"Why are you keeping adhesive tape in your jewelry box?","furigana":"ねんちゃく|ほうせきばこ|しま"},{"japanese":"このシールは粘着力が弱くて、すぐはがれてしまう。","romaji":"Kono shiiru wa nenchakuryoku ga yowakute, sugu hagarete shimau.","german":"This sticker has weak adhesion and peels off right away.","furigana":"ねんちゃくりょく|よわ"},{"japanese":"あの人はネットで同じ相手に何年も粘着している。","romaji":"Ano hito wa netto de onaji aite ni nannen mo nenchaku shite iru.","german":"That person has been obsessively clinging to the same person online for years.","furigana":"ひと|おな|あいて|なんねん|ねんちゃく"}],"notes":"粘着 denotes the sticking or adhering of a substance to a surface and appears mainly in compounds: 粘着テープ \"adhesive tape\", 粘着力 \"adhesive strength\", 粘着シート \"adhesive sheet\". As an intransitive する-verb (〜に粘着する) it is rather technical; in daily life one says くっつく. Colloquially, especially on the internet, 粘着する means \"to stalk someone persistently, to fixate on someone\", and 粘着質 describes a clingy, resentful character.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"出産後お肌の調子が悪くってさ〜。前に言ってたおススメの化粧品の名前教えてくれない？","romaji":"Shussango ohada no chōshi ga warukutte sā. Mae ni itteta osusume no keshōhin no namae oshiete kurenai?","german":"My skin has been in bad shape since giving birth. Could you tell me the name of the cosmetic you recommended before?","furigana":"しゅっさんご|はだ|ちょうし|わる|まえ|い|けしょうひん|なまえおし"},{"japanese":"寝る前にちゃんと化粧を落としてね。","romaji":"Neru mae ni chanto keshō o otoshite ne.","german":"Be sure to remove your makeup before going to bed.","furigana":"ね|まえ|けしょう|お"},{"japanese":"彼女は毎朝十分で化粧して、会社に行く。","romaji":"Kanojo wa maiasa juppun de keshō shite, kaisha ni iku.","german":"She puts on makeup in ten minutes every morning and goes to work.","furigana":"かのじょ|まいあさじゅっぷん|けしょう|かいしゃ|い"}],"notes":"化粧 is \"makeup, applying makeup\"; 化粧する means \"to put on makeup\", the same as お化粧をする or the casual メイクする. Typical combinations: 化粧を落とす \"to remove makeup\", 化粧が濃い/薄い \"heavily/lightly made up\", 化粧品 \"cosmetics\", 化粧室 as a polite word for \"restroom\". Figuratively it means \"beautification, coating\", such as 雪化粧 \"dusted with snow\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 47"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は偽の売買契約書で、お金を詐取されました。","romaji":"Watashi wa nise no baibai keiyakusho de, okane o sashu saremashita.","german":"I was swindled out of money with a fake sales contract.","furigana":"わたし|にせ|ばいばいけいやくしょ|かね|さしゅ"},{"japanese":"男は高齢者から現金を詐取した疑いで逮捕された。","romaji":"Otoko wa koureisha kara genkin o sashu shita utagai de taiho sareta.","german":"The man was arrested on suspicion of swindling cash from elderly people.","furigana":"おとこ|こうれいしゃ|げんきん|さしゅ|うたが|たいほ"},{"japanese":"彼は経費を水増しして、会社から約百万円を詐取していた。","romaji":"Kare wa keihi o mizumashi shite, kaisha kara yaku hyakuman'en o sashu shite ita.","german":"He had inflated expenses and defrauded the company of about one million yen.","furigana":"かれ|けいひ|みずま|かいしゃ|やくひゃくまんえん|さしゅ"}],"notes":"詐取 is a legal, written-language term for obtaining money or goods by deception: 金を詐取する, often passive 詐取される. The general word for fraud as an offense is 詐欺; 詐取 emphasizes the result, namely the swindled loot. Typical in news: 〜を詐取した疑いで逮捕される. Colloquially one says だまし取る instead.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"庭師は彼女が年齢を詐称していることにすぐに気がつきましたが、何も言いませんでした。","romaji":"Niwashi wa kanojo ga nenrei o sashō shite iru koto ni sugu ni kigatsukimashita ga, nani mo iimasendeshita.","german":"The gardener noticed right away that she was lying about her age, but said nothing.","furigana":"にわし|かのじょ|ねんれい|さしょう|き|なに|い"},{"japanese":"その社員は経歴を詐称して入社していたことが分かった。","romaji":"Sono shain wa keireki o sashou shite nyuusha shite ita koto ga wakatta.","german":"It turned out that the employee had joined the company by misrepresenting his career history.","furigana":"しゃいん|けいれき|さしょう|にゅうしゃ|わ"},{"japanese":"警察官を詐称する男が、お年寄りの家を訪ねて回っているそうです。","romaji":"Keisatsukan o sashou suru otoko ga, otoshiyori no ie o tazunete mawatte iru sou desu.","german":"A man impersonating a police officer is reportedly going around visiting elderly people's homes.","furigana":"けいさつかん|さしょう|おとこ|としよ|いえ|たず|まわ"}],"notes":"詐称 means to give false information about oneself - about name, age, occupation or résumé: 年齢を詐称する, 経歴詐称 \"falsified career history\", 学歴詐称. It is a formal word, often with legal coloring, from the news. Unlike 詐取 (obtaining money by fraud), it is not about loot but about a feigned identity; in everyday speech one says 〜だと嘘をつく or 〜を名乗る.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ホームステイ先のお父さんが、今日の夕飯の際に黒い霧事件について説明をしてくれました。","romaji":"Hōmusuteisaki no otōsan ga, kyō no yūhan no sai ni kuroi kiri jiken nitsuite setsumei o shite kuremashita.","german":"My homestay father explained the Black Mist scandal to me at dinner today.","furigana":"さき|とう|きょう|ゆうはん|さい|くろ|きりじけん|せつめい"},{"japanese":"国民は政界の黒い霧が晴れることを望んでいる。","romaji":"Kokumin wa seikai no kuroi kiri ga hareru koto o nozonde iru.","german":"The public hopes that the dark dealings in the political world will be cleared up.","furigana":"こくみん|せいかい|くろ|きり|は|のぞ"},{"japanese":"市長と建設会社の間には黒い霧があるとうわさされている。","romaji":"Shichou to kensetsu gaisha no aida ni wa kuroi kiri ga aru to uwasa sarete iru.","german":"It is rumored that there are shady dealings between the mayor and a construction company.","furigana":"しちょう|けんせつがいしゃ|あいだ|くろ|きり"}],"notes":"黒い霧 literally means \"black fog\" but is used almost only figuratively: for corruption and dark dealings that remain hidden, especially in politics and sports. Typical are 政界の黒い霧 and 黒い霧を晴らす \"to clear up the allegations\". Well known is the 黒い霧事件, the match-fixing scandal in Japanese professional baseball around 1970. For real dense fog one says 濃い霧 or 濃霧.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"チャリティーオークションで、朝霧から山々が浮き出ている素晴らしく美しい絵を落札しました。","romaji":"Charitī ōkushon de, asagiri kara yamayama ga ukidete iru subarashiku utsukushii e o rakusatsu shimashita.","german":"At a charity auction, I won a wonderfully beautiful painting of mountains emerging from the morning mist.","furigana":"あさぎり|やまやま|う|で|すば|うつく|え|らくさつ"},{"japanese":"朝霧が晴れると、目の前に湖が現れた。","romaji":"Asagiri ga hareru to, me no mae ni mizuumi ga arawareta.","german":"When the morning mist cleared, a lake appeared before my eyes.","furigana":"あさぎり|は|め|まえ|みずうみ|あらわ"},{"japanese":"朝霧の中を自転車で走るのは少し危ない。","romaji":"Asagiri no naka o jitensha de hashiru no wa sukoshi abunai.","german":"Riding a bicycle through the morning mist is a little dangerous.","furigana":"あさぎり|なか|じてんしゃ|はし|すこ|あぶ"}],"notes":"朝霧 is the \"morning mist\" that forms over valleys, rivers and lakes on cool mornings and disappears with the sun. Typical: 朝霧がかかる \"morning mist hangs\", 朝霧が晴れる \"the morning mist lifts\". The word sounds poetic and often appears in landscape descriptions; in weather reports one says plainly 霧. The evening counterpart is 夕霧, light haze is もや.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は花柳小説の有名な著者ですよ。","romaji":"Kare wa karyū shōsetsu no yūmei na chosha desu yo.","german":"He is a famous author of novels set in the pleasure quarters.","furigana":"かれ|かりゅうしょうせつ|ゆうめい|ちょしゃ"},{"japanese":"祖母は若いころ、京都の花柳界で芸者をしていたそうだ。","romaji":"Sobo wa wakai koro, Kyouto no karyuukai de geisha o shite ita sou da.","german":"My grandmother reportedly worked as a geisha in Kyoto's geisha world when she was young.","furigana":"そぼ|わか|きょうと|かりゅうかい|げいしゃ"},{"japanese":"昔は性病のことを花柳病と呼んでいた。","romaji":"Mukashi wa seibyou no koto o karyuubyou to yonde ita.","german":"In the past, venereal disease was called 花柳病.","furigana":"むかし|せいびょう|かりゅうびょう|よ"}],"notes":"花柳 (literally \"flowers and willows\") is an old-fashioned expression for the pleasure quarters with geisha and brothels. Today it survives almost only in compounds: 花柳界 \"the geisha world\" (still used for geisha districts such as in Kyōto), 花柳小説 \"novels from the pleasure quarters\" and the obsolete 花柳病 \"venereal disease\". The word is written-language and has a historical coloring.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"伊勢神宮には、一度行ってみた方がいいよ。","romaji":"Isejingū ni wa, ichido itte mita hō ga ii yo.","german":"You should visit Ise Shrine once.","furigana":"いせじんぐう|いちどい|ほう"},{"japanese":"江戸時代には、多くの人がお伊勢参りに出かけた。","romaji":"Edo jidai ni wa, ooku no hito ga o-Ise mairi ni dekaketa.","german":"In the Edo period, many people went on pilgrimages to Ise.","furigana":"えどじだい|おお|ひと|いせまい|で"},{"japanese":"お祝いの席で、立派な伊勢エビが出された。","romaji":"Oiwai no seki de, rippa na ise ebi ga dasareta.","german":"At the celebration, a magnificent Ise lobster was served.","furigana":"いわ|せき|りっぱ|いせ|だ"}],"notes":"伊勢 is the name of the old province in today's Mie Prefecture and of today's city 伊勢市. Best known is the 伊勢神宮, Japan's most important Shintō shrine; the traditional pilgrimage there is called お伊勢参り. The name is also in 伊勢エビ \"spiny lobster\" and in the bay 伊勢湾 \"Ise Bay\". The name is practically always written in kanji.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"伊達巻きはおせち料理の中で私が好きなものの一つです。","romaji":"Datemaki wa osechi ryouri no naka de watashi ga suki na mono no hitotsu desu.","german":"Datemaki is one of my favorite dishes among osechi cuisine.","furigana":"だてま|りょうり|なか|わたし|す|ひと"},{"japanese":"この眼鏡は度が入っていない伊達眼鏡です。","romaji":"Kono megane wa do ga haitte inai date megane desu.","german":"These glasses are non-prescription fashion glasses.","furigana":"めがね|ど|はい|だてめがね"},{"japanese":"伊達に十年も日本に住んでいるわけじゃないよ。","romaji":"Date ni juunen mo Nihon ni sunde iru wake ja nai yo.","german":"I haven't lived in Japan for ten years for nothing.","furigana":"だて|じゅうねん|にほん|す"}],"notes":"伊達 means on the one hand \"stylishness, elegance, flair\" (伊達男 \"dandy\"), on the other hand \"only for show, out of vanity\": 伊達眼鏡 are glasses without a prescription worn only as an accessory. Very common is the phrase 伊達に〜ない \"not for nothing, not merely for show\": 伊達に年を取っていない \"I haven't gotten older for nothing\". As a name, 伊達 also stands for the lord's family (伊達政宗) and in the dish 伊達巻き.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私はトーフグ営業部の補佐として働きました。","romaji":"Watashi wa Toufugu eigyoubu no hosa toshite hatarakimashita.","german":"I worked as an assistant in the Tofugu sales department.","furigana":"わたし|えいぎょうぶ|ほさ|はたら"},{"japanese":"来月から課長補佐に昇進することになった。","romaji":"Raigetsu kara kachou hosa ni shoushin suru koto ni natta.","german":"From next month, I will be promoted to assistant section manager.","furigana":"らいげつ|かちょうほさ|しょうしん"},{"japanese":"社長を補佐するのが秘書の大切な仕事です。","romaji":"Shachou o hosa suru no ga hisho no taisetsu na shigoto desu.","german":"Assisting the president is an important job of a secretary.","furigana":"しゃちょう|ほさ|ひしょ|たいせつ|しごと"}],"notes":"補佐 means \"support, assistance\" and refers both to the activity (上司を補佐する \"to support one's superior\") and to the person. In job titles it is fixed: 課長補佐 \"assistant section manager\", 大統領補佐官 \"presidential aide\". Unlike the general 手伝う or 助ける, 補佐 is formal and implies a fixed supporting role under a leading person.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大佐にお会いできるのを楽しみにしています。","romaji":"Taisa ni o ai dekiru no o tanoshimi ni shite imasu.","german":"I'm looking forward to meeting the colonel.","furigana":"たいさ|あ|たの"},{"japanese":"彼はアメリカ空軍の大佐として退役した。","romaji":"Kare wa Amerika kuugun no taisa toshite taieki shita.","german":"He retired as a colonel in the U.S. Air Force.","furigana":"かれ|くうぐん|たいさ|たいえき"},{"japanese":"その映画に出てくる大佐は、部下にとても厳しい人物だ。","romaji":"Sono eiga ni dete kuru taisa wa, buka ni totemo kibishii jinbutsu da.","german":"The colonel in that film is a man who is very strict with his subordinates.","furigana":"えいが|で|たいさ|ぶか|きび|じんぶつ"}],"notes":"大佐 is the highest field-grade officer rank: \"colonel\" in the army and air force, \"captain\" in the navy. The series is 少佐 \"major\" → 中佐 \"lieutenant colonel\" → 大佐 \"colonel\". In today's Japan Self-Defense Forces the corresponding rank is called 一佐; 大佐 is therefore heard mainly with foreign armies, in history, films and anime. As a form of address it often stands alone: 大佐！","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その少佐は、大佐に飲み物を奢ると言ってきかなかった。","romaji":"Sono shōsa wa, taisa ni nomimono o ogoru to itte kikanakatta.","german":"The major insisted on treating the colonel to a drink.","furigana":"しょうさ|たいさ|の|もの|おご|い"},{"japanese":"祖父は戦争中、陸軍の少佐だったそうです。","romaji":"Sofu wa sensouchuu, rikugun no shousa datta sou desu.","german":"My grandfather was reportedly an army major during the war.","furigana":"そふ|せんそうちゅう|りくぐん|しょうさ"},{"japanese":"彼は三十代の若さで少佐に昇進した。","romaji":"Kare wa sanjuudai no wakasa de shousa ni shoushin shita.","german":"He was promoted to major at the young age of thirty-something.","furigana":"かれ|さんじゅうだい|わか|しょうさ|しょうしん"}],"notes":"少佐 is the lowest field-grade officer rank: \"major\" (army, air force) or \"lieutenant commander\" (navy). It ranks below 中佐 and 大佐; the characters 少・中・大 also order the ranks in 少尉, 中尉, 大尉. In the Self-Defense Forces the rank is called 三佐, so 少佐 appears mainly in historical or foreign contexts and in fiction.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"中佐は、日本への移住に反対することを決めた。","romaji":"Chūsa wa, Nippon e no ijū ni hantai suru koto o kimeta.","german":"The lieutenant colonel decided to oppose the move to Japan.","furigana":"ちゅうさ|にっぽん|いじゅう|はんたい|き"},{"japanese":"中佐は大佐の一つ下、少佐の一つ上の階級です。","romaji":"Chuusa wa taisa no hitotsu shita, shousa no hitotsu ue no kaikyuu desu.","german":"Lieutenant colonel is the rank one below colonel and one above major.","furigana":"ちゅうさ|たいさ|ひと|した|しょうさ|ひと|うえ|かいきゅう"},{"japanese":"作戦の指揮は、経験豊かな中佐に任された。","romaji":"Sakusen no shiki wa, keiken yutaka na chuusa ni makasareta.","german":"Command of the operation was entrusted to an experienced lieutenant colonel.","furigana":"さくせん|しき|けいけんゆた|ちゅうさ|まか"}],"notes":"中佐 is the middle field-grade officer rank: \"lieutenant colonel\" in the army and air force, \"commander\" in the navy. It stands between 少佐 \"major\" and 大佐 \"colonel\". Like the other 佐 ranks, the word is mainly used today for foreign or historical armed forces; the Self-Defense Forces say 二佐.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この巻尺で胸囲を測るのを手伝ってもらえませんか。","romaji":"Kono makijaku de kyōi o hakaru no o tetsudatte moraemasen ka.","german":"Could you help me measure my chest with this tape measure?","furigana":"まきじゃく|きょうい|はか|てつだ"},{"japanese":"ソファーを買う前に、巻尺で部屋の幅を測っておこう。","romaji":"Sofaa o kau mae ni, makijaku de heya no haba o hakatte okou.","german":"Before buying a sofa, let's measure the width of the room with a tape measure.","furigana":"か|まえ|まきじゃく|へや|はば|はか"},{"japanese":"巻尺を最後まで引き出したら、元に戻らなくなった。","romaji":"Makijaku o saigo made hikidashitara, moto ni modoranaku natta.","german":"When I pulled the tape measure out all the way, it wouldn't retract.","furigana":"まきじゃく|さいご|ひ|だ|もと|もど"}],"notes":"巻尺 is a roll-up tape measure, both the soft tailor's tape and the steel tape from the hardware store. One measures with it: 巻尺で測る. In daily life one mostly says メジャー; 巻尺 sounds somewhat old-fashioned or more technical. The 尺 is the old unit of length (about 30 cm), which can also be found in 尺度 \"scale, yardstick\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"衣食住もままならない彼らの事を哀れに思います。","romaji":"Ishokujū mo mamanaranai karera no koto o aware ni omoimasu.","german":"I feel sorry for them, since they can barely afford food, clothing or shelter.","furigana":"いしょくじゅう|かれ|こと|あわ|おも"},{"japanese":"雨の中で震えている子犬の姿は、なんとも哀れだった。","romaji":"Ame no naka de furuete iru koinu no sugata wa, nantomo aware datta.","german":"The sight of the puppy shivering in the rain was truly pitiful.","furigana":"あめ|なか|ふる|こいぬ|すがた|あわ"},{"japanese":"そんな嘘で人の気を引こうとするなんて、哀れな人だ。","romaji":"Sonna uso de hito no ki o hikou to suru nante, aware na hito da.","german":"Trying to attract people's attention with such lies - what a pathetic person.","furigana":"うそ|ひと|き|ひ|あわ|ひと"}],"notes":"哀れ is, as a noun, \"pity, pathos\" and, as a na-adjective, \"pitiful, wretched\". Typical are 哀れに思う \"to feel pity\", 哀れな姿 \"a miserable sight\" and 哀れむ \"to pity\". Applied to people it can sound condescending (哀れな人 \"a pathetic person\"); more neutral is かわいそう. Classic is もののあわれ, the wistful sensitivity to the transience of things.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お酒を付き合い程度にしか飲まない人のこと、なんだか可哀想だなって思うんだよね。","romaji":"O-sake o tsukiai teido ni shika nomanai hito no koto, nandaka kawaisou da na tte omou n da yo ne.","german":"For some reason, I feel sorry for people who only drink as a social obligation.","furigana":"さけ|つ|あ|ていど|の|ひと|かわいそう|おも"},{"japanese":"雨の中で子犬が震えていて、かわいそうだった。","romaji":"Ame no naka de koinu ga furuete ite, kawaisou datta.","german":"A puppy was shivering in the rain, and I felt sorry for it.","furigana":"あめ|なか|こいぬ|ふる"},{"japanese":"そんな言い方をしたら、彼がかわいそうだよ。","romaji":"Sonna iikata o shitara, kare ga kawaisou da yo.","german":"If you talk like that, it's unfair to him.","furigana":"い|かた|かれ"}],"notes":"かわいそう expresses pity: \"poor, pitiable, one feels sorry for someone\". It is a na-adjective (かわいそうな犬); かわいそうに! means \"You poor thing!\", and 〜がかわいそう can also imply that something is unfair to someone. It is usually written in kana; 可哀想 and 可哀相 are ateji; despite the similar sound, it has nothing to do with かわいい \"cute\". 気の毒 is more distant and polite - toward superiors, かわいそう sounds condescending.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"赤ちゃんは、木綿のミトンをしていたにも関わらず、下唇を引っ掻いてしまった。","romaji":"Akachan wa, momen no miton o shite ita ni mo kakawarazu, shitakuchibiru o hikkaite shimatta.","german":"Even though the baby was wearing cotton mittens, he scratched his lower lip.","furigana":"あか|もめん|かか|したくちびる|ひ|か"},{"japanese":"彼は悔しそうに下唇を噛んだ。","romaji":"Kare wa kuyashisō ni shitakuchibiru o kanda.","german":"He bit his lower lip in frustration.","furigana":"かれ|くや|したくちびる|か"},{"japanese":"「f」の音は、上の歯を下唇に軽く当てて出します。","romaji":"\"F\" no oto wa, ue no ha o shitakuchibiru ni karuku atete dashimasu.","german":"The \"f\" sound is made by lightly touching the upper teeth to the lower lip.","furigana":"おと|うえ|は|したくちびる|かる|あ|だ"}],"notes":"下唇 is the \"lower lip\"; its counterpart is 上唇 \"upper lip\", the base word 唇. Typical expressions: 下唇を噛む \"to bite one's lower lip\" (from anger, disappointment or embarrassment) and 下唇が腫れる \"the lower lip swells\". It is often heard in pronunciation lessons because Japanese has no sounds like \"f\" or \"v\" in which the upper teeth touch the 下唇.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"疲れると、上唇がよく腫れちゃうんですよね。","romaji":"Tsukareru to, uwakuchibiru ga yoku harechau n desu yo ne.","german":"When I'm tired, my upper lip often swells up.","furigana":"つか|うわくちびる|は"},{"japanese":"寒さで上唇が切れて、食べるたびに痛い。","romaji":"Samusa de uwakuchibiru ga kirete, taberu tabi ni itai.","german":"My upper lip cracked from the cold, and it hurts every time I eat.","furigana":"さむ|うわくちびる|き|た|いた"},{"japanese":"彼は考え事をするとき、指で上唇を触る癖がある。","romaji":"Kare wa kangaegoto o suru toki, yubi de uwakuchibiru o sawaru kuse ga aru.","german":"He has a habit of touching his upper lip with his finger when he is thinking.","furigana":"かれ|かんが|ごと|ゆび|うわくちびる|さわ|くせ"}],"notes":"上唇 is the upper lip; note the reading うわくちびる (not うえ〜), parallel to 下唇 したくちびる \"lower lip\". The う〜 prefix is also known from 上着 うわぎ. Typical combinations: 上唇が腫れる \"the upper lip swells\", 上唇を噛む. When no distinction is needed, one simply says 唇.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あなたの家の土塀をよじ登っている人がいたので、それをお知らせするためにお電話させて頂きました。","romaji":"Anata no ie no dobei o yojinobotte iru hito ga ita node, sore o oshirase suru tame ni o denwa sasete itadakimashita.","german":"I'm calling to let you know that someone was climbing over the earthen wall of your house.","furigana":"いえ|どべい|のぼ|ひと|し|でんわ|いただ"},{"japanese":"古いお寺の周りには、長い土塀が続いている。","romaji":"Furui otera no mawari ni wa, nagai dobei ga tsuzuite iru.","german":"A long earthen wall continues around the old temple.","furigana":"ふる|てら|まわ|なが|どべい|つづ"},{"japanese":"地震で土塀の一部が崩れてしまった。","romaji":"Jishin de dobei no ichibu ga kuzurete shimatta.","german":"Part of the earthen wall collapsed in the earthquake.","furigana":"じしん|どべい|いちぶ|くず"}],"notes":"土塀 is a wall of clay or earth, often with a small tiled roof, as seen around temples, old samurai residences and traditional houses. A wall or fence in general is 塀 (ブロック塀 \"concrete block wall\"); 土塀 emphasizes the material and has a historical feel. Typical combinations: 土塀が続く, 土塀が崩れる, 土塀に囲まれる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"iPhoneで軍事飛行機がヘリコプターを撃墜する瞬間を撮影しました。","romaji":"iPhone de gunji hikōki ga herikoputā o gekitsui suru shunkan o satsuei shimashita.","german":"I filmed the moment a military plane shot down a helicopter with my iPhone.","furigana":"ぐんじひこうき|げきつい|しゅんかん|さつえい"},{"japanese":"軍は国境付近で無人機を撃墜したと発表した。","romaji":"Gun wa kokkyō fukin de mujinki o gekitsui shita to happyō shita.","german":"The military announced that it had shot down a drone near the border.","furigana":"ぐん|こっきょうふきん|むじんき|げきつい|はっぴょう"},{"japanese":"その旅客機はミサイルで撃墜されたとみられている。","romaji":"Sono ryokakki wa misairu de gekitsui sareta to mirarete iru.","german":"The passenger plane is believed to have been shot down by a missile.","furigana":"りょかっき|げきつい"}],"notes":"撃墜 means \"to shoot down (an aircraft)\"; as a transitive する-verb: 戦闘機を撃墜する, passive 撃墜される. It applies only to targets in the air such as aircraft, drones or missiles; sinking a ship is 撃沈, shooting a person dead is 射殺. The word belongs to military and news language. For the crash itself one says 墜落.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あのジェット機の墜落は人為的ミスが原因だったと思う？","romaji":"Ano jettoki no tsuiraku wa jin'iteki misu ga gen'in datta to omou?","german":"Do you think the jet crashed due to human error?","furigana":"き|ついらく|じんいてき|げんいん|おも"},{"japanese":"強風にあおられて、ドローンが川に墜落した。","romaji":"Kyoufuu ni aorarete, doroon ga kawa ni tsuiraku shita.","german":"Caught by a gust of wind, the drone fell into the river.","furigana":"きょうふう|かわ|ついらく"},{"japanese":"墜落事故の原因は、まだ分かっていません。","romaji":"Tsuiraku jiko no gen'in wa, mada wakatte imasen.","german":"The cause of the crash is still unclear.","furigana":"ついらくじこ|げんいん|わ"}],"notes":"墜落 is a fall from a great height, typically of airplanes, helicopters or drones: 飛行機が墜落する, 墜落事故. It is an intransitive suru-verb. For people falling from buildings or stairs one rather uses 転落; 落下 is neutral for anything that falls down (落下物). 墜落 sounds dramatic and often appears in news reports.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼には常識が欠如しているとしか思えない。","romaji":"Kare ni wa joushiki ga ketsujo shite iru to shika omoenai.","german":"I can only think that he lacks common sense.","furigana":"かれ|じょうしき|けつじょ|おも"},{"japanese":"安全意識の欠如が、今回の事故につながった。","romaji":"Anzen ishiki no ketsujo ga, konkai no jiko ni tsunagatta.","german":"The lack of safety awareness led to this accident.","furigana":"あんぜんいしき|けつじょ|こんかい|じこ"},{"japanese":"この報告書は具体性が欠如していると指摘された。","romaji":"Kono houkokusho wa gutaisei ga ketsujo shite iru to shiteki sareta.","german":"The report was criticized for lacking specificity.","furigana":"ほうこくしょ|ぐたいせい|けつじょ|してき"}],"notes":"欠如 means that something necessary is completely missing, and is used above all for abstract qualities: 常識の欠如, 責任感が欠如している, 安全意識の欠如. For substances and supplies such as vitamins or water, one speaks instead of 欠乏 (ビタミン欠乏症) or 不足. 欠如 is written-language and often sounds critical. Grammatically: 〜が欠如する or 〜の欠如.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女が突如彼をセクハラで訴え始めたのには理由があります。","romaji":"Kanojo ga totsujo kare o sekuhara de uttae hajimeta no ni wa riyū ga arimasu.","german":"There is a reason for her sudden accusations of sexual harassment.","furigana":"かのじょ|とつじょかれ|うった|はじ|りゆう"},{"japanese":"静かな夜に、突如として大きな爆発音が鳴り響いた。","romaji":"Shizuka na yoru ni, totsujo toshite ookina bakuhatsuon ga narihibiita.","german":"In the quiet night, a loud bang suddenly echoed.","furigana":"しず|よる|とつじょ|おお|ばくはつおん|な|ひび"},{"japanese":"晴れていた空が突如暗くなり、激しい雨が降り出した。","romaji":"Harete ita sora ga totsujo kuraku nari, hageshii ame ga furidashita.","german":"The clear sky suddenly darkened, and it began to rain heavily.","furigana":"は|そら|とつじょくら|はげ|あめ|ふ|だ"}],"notes":"突如 means \"suddenly, abruptly\" and is the written-language, somewhat dramatic variant of 突然. It is often used as 突如として. It is found in news, novels and reports about unexpected events; in casual conversation one rather says 突然 or 急に. Unlike 突然, 突如 is hardly used attributively (突然の雨, but not 突如の雨).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの老婆は絶対に賭博を認めようとしないんだ。","romaji":"Ano rōba wa zettai ni tobaku o mitomeyō to shinai n da.","german":"That old woman absolutely refuses to approve of gambling.","furigana":"ろうば|ぜったい|とばく|みと"},{"japanese":"昔話には、山奥に住む不思議な老婆がよく登場する。","romaji":"Mukashibanashi ni wa, yamaoku ni sumu fushigi na rouba ga yoku toujou suru.","german":"Folktales often feature a mysterious old woman living deep in the mountains.","furigana":"むかしばなし|やまおく|す|ふしぎ|ろうば|とうじょう"},{"japanese":"老婆心ながら、一言だけ言わせてください。","romaji":"Roubashin nagara, hitokoto dake iwasete kudasai.","german":"If I may be a bit meddlesome, let me say just one thing.","furigana":"ろうばしん|ひとこと|い"}],"notes":"老婆 is a written-language word for \"old woman\", as found in novels and fairy tales. About real persons it sounds distant to disrespectful; more polite are お年寄り, 高齢の女性 or おばあさん. The male counterpart is 老人 or 老翁. Fixed is 老婆心 \"unsolicited, well-meant advice\", often in 老婆心ながら \"if I may be so bold as to say\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お正月は、お祖母さんの家に泊まります。","romaji":"Oshōgatsu wa, obāsan no ie ni tomarimasu.","german":"Over New Year I stay at my grandmother's house.","furigana":"しょうがつ|ばあ|いえ|と"},{"japanese":"バスで、荷物を持ったお婆さんに席を譲りました。","romaji":"Basu de, nimotsu o motta obāsan ni seki o yuzurimashita.","german":"On the bus, I gave my seat to an elderly woman carrying luggage.","furigana":"にもつ|も|ばあ|せき|ゆず"}],"notes":"おばあさん can mean a grandmother or an elderly woman. In kanji, a grandmother is usually written お祖母さん, an elderly woman お婆さん; the hiragana spelling is very common. When speaking about one's own grandmother to outsiders, one mostly says 祖母. An unknown elderly woman is better addressed by name or an appropriate form of address, depending on the situation. The long ああ distinguishes おばあさん from おばさん, aunt.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5 (source: N5; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は実は、昔の貨幣の収集家なんです。","romaji":"Watashi wa jitsuha, mukashi no kahei no shūshūka na n desu.","german":"To tell the truth, I'm a collector of old coins and currency.","furigana":"わたし|じつ|むかし|かへい|しゅうしゅうか"},{"japanese":"物々交換の時代には、まだ貨幣は使われていなかった。","romaji":"Butsubutsu koukan no jidai ni wa, mada kahei wa tsukawarete inakatta.","german":"In the age of barter, money was not yet in use.","furigana":"ぶつぶつこうかん|じだい|かへい|つか"},{"japanese":"物価が上がると、貨幣の価値は下がる。","romaji":"Bukka ga agaru to, kahei no kachi wa sagaru.","german":"When prices rise, the value of money falls.","furigana":"ぶっか|あ|かへい|かち|さ"}],"notes":"貨幣 is \"money\" as a means of payment in the economic sense, often specifically \"coinage\". It belongs to technical and written language: 貨幣経済, 貨幣価値, 記念貨幣. Distinguish 通貨 \"currency\" (円, ユーロ), 硬貨 \"coin\" and 紙幣 \"banknote\"; in everyday speech people simply say お金. Collectors speak of 古い貨幣 or 古銭.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は母を憎んだが、母も同様に私のことを憎んでいました。","romaji":"Watashi wa haha o nikunda ga, haha mo dōyō ni watashi no koto o nikunde imashita.","german":"I hated my mother, but my mother hated me just the same.","furigana":"わたし|はは|にく|はは|どうよう|わたし|にく"},{"japanese":"戦争を憎む気持ちは、世界中の人々に共通している。","romaji":"Sensou o nikumu kimochi wa, sekaijuu no hitobito ni kyoutsuu shite iru.","german":"The feeling of hating war is shared by people all over the world.","furigana":"せんそう|にく|きも|せかいじゅう|ひとびと|きょうつう"},{"japanese":"「罪を憎んで人を憎まず」という言葉があります。","romaji":"\"Tsumi o nikunde hito o nikumazu\" to iu kotoba ga arimasu.","german":"There is a saying: \"Hate the sin, not the sinner.\"","furigana":"つみ|にく|ひと|にく|ことば"}],"notes":"憎む means \"to hate, to detest\" and is a strong, rather written word for deep resentment against persons or things such as war and injustice: 〜を憎む. In daily life one mostly says 嫌い / 嫌う for dislike; 憎む expresses genuine hatred. Related are 憎い / 憎らしい \"hateful\" and 憎しみ \"hatred\". Well known is the proverb 罪を憎んで人を憎まず \"Hate the sin, not the sinner\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この愛憎の入り混じった気持ちをコントロールするのはもう無理だよ。","romaji":"Kono aizou no irimajitta kimochi o kontorooru suru no wa mou muri da yo.","german":"Ich kann diese gemischten Gefühle von Liebe und Hass nicht mehr kontrollieren.","furigana":"あいぞう|い|ま|きも|むり"},{"japanese":"この小説は、ある家族の愛憎を描いている。","romaji":"Kono shousetsu wa, aru kazoku no aizou o egaite iru.","german":"Dieser Roman schildert Liebe und Hass innerhalb einer Familie.","furigana":"しょうせつ|かぞく|あいぞう|えが"},{"japanese":"昼のドラマは、ドロドロの愛憎劇が人気だ。","romaji":"Hiru no dorama wa, dorodoro no aizougeki ga ninki da.","german":"Bei den Nachmittagsserien sind verworrene Dramen um Liebe und Hass beliebt.","furigana":"ひる|あいぞうげき|にんき"}],"notes":"愛憎 bedeutet „Liebe und Hass“ zugleich, also eine ambivalente, leidenschaftliche Gefühlslage. Typisch sind 愛憎の入り混じった気持ち „eine Mischung aus Liebe und Hass“, 愛憎劇 „Drama um Liebe und Hass“ und 愛憎を描く. Das Wort ist schriftsprachlich und wird vor allem über Literatur, Filme und Beziehungen gebraucht. Das deutsche „Hassliebe“ trifft es gut, ist aber im Japanischen kein Adjektiv, sondern ein Nomen.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この山小屋でちょっと憩わないか。","romaji":"Kono yamagoya de chotto ikowanai ka.","german":"Why don't we rest a bit in this mountain hut?","furigana":"やまごや|いこ"},{"japanese":"休日には、多くの家族が川辺で憩っている。","romaji":"Kyuujitsu ni wa, ooku no kazoku ga kawabe de ikotte iru.","german":"On holidays, many families relax by the riverside.","furigana":"きゅうじつ|おお|かぞく|かわべ|いこ"},{"japanese":"この公園は、昔から市民の憩いの場になっている。","romaji":"Kono kouen wa, mukashi kara shimin no ikoi no ba ni natte iru.","german":"This park has long been a place of rest for the citizens.","furigana":"こうえん|むかし|しみん|いこ|ば"}],"notes":"憩う means \"to rest, to take a break, to relax\" and is an elevated, rather written verb. It is found in descriptions of peaceful scenes: 公園で家族が憩う. Much more common than the verb is the noun 憩い, especially 憩いの場 \"place of relaxation\" and 憩いのひととき. In conversation one says 休む, くつろぐ or 一休みする instead.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"重ね着しすぎてすごく暑いんだけど、ちょっと私に向かって扇いでくれない？","romaji":"Kasanegi shisugite sugoku atsui n da kedo, chotto watashi ni mukatte aoide kurenai?","german":"I'm really hot from wearing too many layers. Could you fan me a bit?","furigana":"かさ|ぎ|あつ|わたし|む|あお"},{"japanese":"暑かったので、下敷きで顔を扇いだ。","romaji":"Atsukatta node, shitajiki de kao o aoida.","german":"It was hot, so I fanned my face with a pencil board.","furigana":"あつ||かお|あお"},{"japanese":"炭火をうちわで扇いで、火を強くした。","romaji":"Sumibi o uchiwa de aoide, hi o tsuyoku shita.","german":"I fanned the charcoal fire with a fan to make it stronger.","furigana":"すみび|あお|ひ|つよ"}],"notes":"扇ぐ means \"to fan, to wave air toward\": うちわで扇ぐ, 顔を扇ぐ. The tool takes で, the person or thing takes を: 子どもをうちわで扇ぐ. The identically read 仰ぐ means \"to look up, to ask for advice\" - do not confuse. For \"to incite, to stir up\" one mostly uses 煽る (あおる) today. 扇ぐ is often written in kana as あおぐ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私が門扉の前で待っていると、誰かが割り込んできました。","romaji":"Watashi ga monpi no mae de matte iru to, dare ka ga warikonde kimashita.","german":"While I was waiting in front of the gate, someone cut in line.","furigana":"わたし|もんぴ|まえ|ま|だれ|わ|こ"},{"japanese":"夜十時になると、学校の門扉は閉められます。","romaji":"Yoru juuji ni naru to, gakkou no monpi wa shimeraremasu.","german":"At ten at night, the school gate is closed.","furigana":"よるじゅうじ|がっこう|もんぴ|し"},{"japanese":"門扉をしっかり閉めないと、犬が外に出てしまうよ。","romaji":"Monpi o shikkari shimenai to, inu ga soto ni dete shimau yo.","german":"If you don't close the gate firmly, the dog will get out.","furigana":"もんぴ|し|いぬ|そと|で"}],"notes":"門扉 are the leaves or door of a gate, i.e. the movable part that one opens and closes: 門扉を開ける / 閉める. 門 alone is the whole gate, 扉 any door. The word is somewhat formal and appears with houses, schools and company grounds as well as in construction and real estate texts. Do not confuse with 門戸 in 門戸を開く \"to open up, to grant access\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この二つの段落の間にアヤのイラストを挿入するのはどうでしょうか。","romaji":"Kono futatsu no danraku no aida ni Aya no irasuto o sounyuu suru no wa dou deshou ka.","german":"How about inserting Aya's illustration between these two paragraphs?","furigana":"ふた|だんらく|あいだ|そうにゅう"},{"japanese":"カードを挿入して、暗証番号を入力してください。","romaji":"Kaado o sounyuu shite, anshou bangou o nyuuryoku shite kudasai.","german":"Please insert your card and enter your PIN.","furigana":"そうにゅう|あんしょうばんごう|にゅうりょく"},{"japanese":"このドラマの挿入歌が、若者の間で大ヒットしている。","romaji":"Kono dorama no sounyuuka ga, wakamono no aida de daihitto shite iru.","german":"The insert song of this drama is a big hit among young people.","furigana":"そうにゅうか|わかもの|あいだ|だい"}],"notes":"挿入 means \"to insert, to put in\": to place something into an opening or between other parts. Typical are カードを挿入する, 文章に図を挿入する and, in a computer context, 挿入キー \"Insert key\". Fixed are 挿入歌 \"song played in a film/series\" and 挿入句 \"parenthetical phrase\". It is somewhat technical/formal; in daily life one often says 入れる or 差し込む.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"恐らく気づいていないと思うが、コウイチ大統領は全てを掌握しているんだよ。","romaji":"Osoraku kizuite inai to omou ga, Kouichi daitouryou wa subete o shouaku shite iru n da yo.","german":"You probably haven't noticed, but President Koichi has everything under control.","furigana":"おそ|き|おも|だいとうりょう|すべ|しょうあく"},{"japanese":"クーデターで軍が政権を掌握した。","romaji":"Kuudetaa de gun ga seiken o shouaku shita.","german":"The military seized power in a coup.","furigana":"ぐん|せいけん|しょうあく"},{"japanese":"新しい部長は、まず部下をしっかり掌握することから始めた。","romaji":"Atarashii buchou wa, mazu buka o shikkari shouaku suru koto kara hajimeta.","german":"The new department head began by first getting his subordinates firmly in hand.","furigana":"あたら|ぶちょう|ぶか|しょうあく|はじ"}],"notes":"掌握 means to hold something firmly \"in the hand\": to bring or keep power, organizations or people under control. Typical: 政権を掌握する \"to seize the reins of government\", 部下を掌握する \"to have one's people in hand\", 実権を掌握する. Do not confuse with 把握 \"to grasp, to understand\" (状況を把握する); 掌握 emphasizes control and leadership, not mere understanding. The word is formal and often appears in political and economic news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の額から、血が滴り落ちました。","romaji":"Kare no hitai kara, chi ga shitatari ochimashita.","german":"Blood dripped from his forehead.","furigana":"かれ|ひたい|ち|したた|お"},{"japanese":"雨上がりの木の葉から、しずくが滴っている。","romaji":"Ameagari no ko no ha kara, shizuku ga shitatatte iru.","german":"Drops are dripping from the leaves of the trees after the rain.","furigana":"あめあ|こ|は|したた"},{"japanese":"初夏になると、緑滴る山々がとても美しい。","romaji":"Shoka ni naru to, midori shitataru yamayama ga totemo utsukushii.","german":"In early summer, the mountains, dripping with green, are very beautiful.","furigana":"しょか|みどりしたた|やまやま|うつく"}],"notes":"滴る means \"to drip, to trickle\": a liquid falls down in individual drops, e.g. 汗が滴る, 血が滴る, often as 滴り落ちる. It is intransitive and somewhat more literary than ぽたぽた落ちる or 垂れる. Figuratively it describes overflowing freshness or beauty, above all in 緑滴る \"lush green, brimming with green\" and 水も滴るいい男 \"a stunningly handsome man\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"車の窓には水滴がついています。","romaji":"Kuruma no mado ni wa suiteki ga tsuite imasu.","german":"There are water drops on the car window.","furigana":"くるま|まど|すいてき"},{"japanese":"冷たいグラスの表面に、たくさんの水滴がついた。","romaji":"Tsumetai gurasu no hyoumen ni, takusan no suiteki ga tsuita.","german":"Many water droplets formed on the surface of the cold glass.","furigana":"つめ|ひょうめん|すいてき"},{"japanese":"蛇口から水滴が落ちる音が気になって眠れない。","romaji":"Jaguchi kara suiteki ga ochiru oto ga ki ni natte nemurenai.","german":"I can't sleep because the sound of water dripping from the faucet bothers me.","furigana":"じゃぐち|すいてき|お|おと|き|ねむ"}],"notes":"水滴 is a \"water drop\", especially drops that collect on surfaces (condensation) or fall: 水滴がつく, 水滴が落ちる. Similar in meaning is しずく (滴), which sounds softer and more poetic; 水滴 seems more matter-of-fact and is also used in technical texts. For raindrops one says 雨粒 or 雨のしずく.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"忙しいけど、ちゃんと自炊してるよ。実際、昨日の夜はラタトゥイユっていう野菜の煮込み料理を作ったよ。","romaji":"Isogashii kedo, chanto jisui shiteru yo. Jissai, kinō no yoru wa ratatuiyu tte iu yasai no nikomi ryōri o tsukutta yo.","german":"I'm busy, but I do cook for myself properly. In fact, last night I made a vegetable stew called ratatouille.","furigana":"いそが|じすい|じっさい|きのう|よる|やさい|にこ|りょうり|つく"},{"japanese":"一人暮らしを始めてから、節約のために自炊するようになった。","romaji":"Hitorigurashi o hajimete kara, setsuyaku no tame ni jisui suru yō ni natta.","german":"Since I started living alone, I've begun cooking for myself to save money.","furigana":"ひとりぐ|はじ|せつやく|じすい"},{"japanese":"この宿は台所付きで、自炊もできます。","romaji":"Kono yado wa daidokoro-tsuki de, jisui mo dekimasu.","german":"This inn has a kitchen, so you can cook for yourself too.","furigana":"やど|だいどころつ|じすい"}],"notes":"自炊 means \"to cook for oneself, to prepare one's own meals\" and is said mainly of people living alone and students, as opposed to 外食 \"eating out\" or コンビニ弁当. As an intransitive する-verb: 毎日自炊している. Accommodations with cooking facilities are advertised as 自炊可 or 自炊できる. More recently and colloquially, 自炊 also means \"to scan one's own books\" to read them as e-books.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"さすがベテランアナウンサーなだけあって、彼女はとてもよく聞き取れる明瞭な声でその質問に答えました。","romaji":"Sasuga beteran anaunsā na dake atte, kanojo wa totemo yoku kikitoreru meiryō na koe de sono shitsumon ni kotaemashita.","german":"As you'd expect from a veteran announcer, she answered the question in a very clear, easily audible voice.","furigana":"かのじょ|き|と|めいりょう|こえ|しつもん|こた"},{"japanese":"彼の説明は簡潔で明瞭だった。","romaji":"Kare no setsumei wa kanketsu de meiryou datta.","german":"His explanation was concise and clear.","furigana":"かれ|せつめい|かんけつ|めいりょう"},{"japanese":"お客様のために、料金を明瞭に表示してください。","romaji":"Okyakusama no tame ni, ryoukin o meiryou ni hyouji shite kudasai.","german":"For our customers' sake, please display the charges clearly.","furigana":"きゃくさま|りょうきん|めいりょう|ひょうじ"}],"notes":"明瞭 is a na-adjective meaning \"clear, distinct, unambiguous\", e.g. 明瞭な発音, 簡潔明瞭な説明 and 明瞭会計 \"transparent accounting\". The opposite is 不明瞭 \"unclear\". Compared with はっきり or 明らか, 明瞭 is more formal and is used mainly for pronunciation, explanations and differences; the adverbial form is 明瞭に.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お弁当を買ったら、割り箸を二膳つけてもらった。","romaji":"Obentou o kattara, waribashi o nizen tsukete moratta.","german":"When I bought a bento, they gave me two pairs of disposable chopsticks.","furigana":"べんとう|か|わ|ばし|にぜん"},{"japanese":"割り箸をきれいに割るのは、意外と難しい。","romaji":"Waribashi o kirei ni waru no wa, igai to muzukashii.","german":"Splitting disposable chopsticks cleanly is surprisingly difficult.","furigana":"わ|ばし|わ|いがい|むずか"},{"japanese":"環境のために、割り箸を使わずにマイ箸を持ち歩く人が増えている。","romaji":"Kankyou no tame ni, waribashi o tsukawazu ni maibashi o mochiaruku hito ga fuete iru.","german":"For the environment, more and more people carry their own chopsticks instead of using disposable ones.","furigana":"かんきょう|わ|ばし|つか|ばし|も|ある|ひと|ふ"}],"notes":"割り箸 are disposable chopsticks of wood or bamboo that are joined as a pair and snapped apart before eating: 割り箸を割る. One gets them at restaurants, snack shops and when buying a bento at a konbini. Chopsticks are counted with 膳 (一膳, 二膳). Reusable chopsticks are simply 箸, one's own brought along are マイ箸.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"君は今までみた中で一番胴体が長いよ。","romaji":"Kimi wa ima made mita naka de ichiban dōtai ga nagai yo.","german":"You have the longest torso I've ever seen.","furigana":"きみ|いま|なか|いちばんどうたい|なが"},{"japanese":"車輪が出なかったため、飛行機は胴体着陸をした。","romaji":"Sharin ga denakatta tame, hikouki wa doutai chakuriku o shita.","german":"Because the landing gear wouldn't come down, the plane made a belly landing.","furigana":"しゃりん|で|ひこうき|どうたいちゃくりく"},{"japanese":"この人形は、胴体から頭と手足を外すことができる。","romaji":"Kono ningyou wa, doutai kara atama to teashi o hazusu koto ga dekiru.","german":"The head and limbs of this doll can be removed from the torso.","furigana":"にんぎょう|どうたい|あたま|てあし|はず"}],"notes":"胴体 is the torso, i.e. the body without head, arms and legs. Figuratively it is also the fuselage of an aircraft or the hull of a ship, known from 胴体着陸 \"belly landing\". 胴 alone also means \"torso, waist\" (胴回り \"waist circumference\"), 体 the whole body. For the human upper body in daily life one often says 上半身.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"虹色の山葵を購入してみたんですが、味はまんま山葵ですね。","romaji":"Nijiiro no wasabi o kōnyū shite mita n desu ga, aji wa manma wasabi desu ne.","german":"I tried buying rainbow-colored wasabi, but it tastes just like wasabi.","furigana":"にじいろ|わさび|こうにゅう|あじ|わさび"},{"japanese":"シャボン玉が日の光を受けて虹色に光っている。","romaji":"Shabondama ga hi no hikari o ukete nijiiro ni hikatte iru.","german":"The soap bubbles are shining in rainbow colors in the sunlight.","furigana":"だま|ひ|ひかり|う|にじいろ|ひか"},{"japanese":"娘の誕生日に虹色のケーキを作りました。","romaji":"Musume no tanjōbi ni nijiiro no kēki o tsukurimashita.","german":"I made a rainbow-colored cake for my daughter's birthday.","furigana":"むすめ|たんじょうび|にじいろ|つく"}],"notes":"虹色 is \"rainbow-colored, in all the colors of the rainbow\" and is placed before nouns with の: 虹色の傘; adverbially with に: 虹色に光る \"to shimmer in rainbow colors\". Grammatically it is a noun, not an い- or な-adjective. Figuratively it stands for diversity and hope, e.g. 虹色の未来; the rainbow flag is usually called レインボーフラッグ. Similar: 七色 \"seven-colored, colorful\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"部長のお嬢さんは確か歌手志望じゃなかったですっけ？","romaji":"Buchō no ojōsan wa tashika kashu shibō ja nakatta desu kke?","german":"Didn't the department head's daughter want to become a singer, if I remember right?","furigana":"ぶちょう|じょう|たし|かしゅしぼう"},{"japanese":"お嬢さんはおいくつですか。","romaji":"Ojōsan wa oikutsu desu ka.","german":"How old is your daughter?","furigana":"じょう"},{"japanese":"お嬢さん、ハンカチを落としましたよ。","romaji":"Ojōsan, hankachi o otoshimashita yo.","german":"Miss, you dropped your handkerchief.","furigana":"じょう|お"}],"notes":"お嬢さん is above all the polite term for someone else's daughter: 部長のお嬢さん, お嬢さんはおいくつですか. One's own daughter is called 娘. As a form of address for a young woman (\"young lady\") it sounds somewhat old-fashioned and is mostly used by older people. Even more polite is お嬢様, which can also mean \"daughter of a good family\"; the male counterpart is 息子さん.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 22"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"水虫になりたくないなら、スリッパを裸足で履かない方がいいですよ。","romaji":"Mizumushi ni naritakunainara, surippa o hadashi de hakanai hō ga iidesu yo.","german":"If you don't want to get athlete's foot, you'd better not wear slippers barefoot.","furigana":"みずむし|はだし|は|ほう"},{"japanese":"子どもたちは裸足で砂浜を走り回っていた。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa hadashi de sunahama o hashirimawatte ita.","german":"The children were running around the beach barefoot.","furigana":"こ|はだし|すなはま|はし|まわ"},{"japanese":"彼の料理の腕前はプロはだしだ。","romaji":"Kare no ryouri no udemae wa puro hadashi da.","german":"His cooking skill rivals that of a professional.","furigana":"かれ|りょうり|うでまえ"}],"notes":"裸足 means \"barefoot\" and is grammatically a noun: 裸足で歩く \"to walk barefoot\", 裸足の子ども \"a barefoot child\". Without socks but wearing shoes is 素足. As a suffix, usually written in kana, 〜はだし means \"second to no professional\": プロはだし. The kanji spelling 裸足 is a special reading (jukujikun).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"稼ぎがそんなに多くないので、何によくお金を使っているか習慣を見直して節約するために家計簿をつけています。","romaji":"Kasegi ga sonnani ōkunai node, nani ni yoku okane o tsukatte iru ka shūkan o minaoshite setsuyaku suru tame ni kakeibo o tsukete imasu.","german":"Since my income isn't very high, I keep a household account book to review what I often spend money on and to save.","furigana":"かせ|おお|なに|かね|つか|しゅうかん|みなお|せつやく|かけいぼ"},{"japanese":"年末は、お店にとって一番の稼ぎ時だ。","romaji":"Nenmatsu wa, omise ni totte ichiban no kasegidoki da.","german":"The end of the year is the best earning season for shops.","furigana":"ねんまつ|みせ|いちばん|かせ|どき"},{"japanese":"兄は、もっと稼ぎのいい仕事に転職した。","romaji":"Ani wa, motto kasegi no ii shigoto ni tenshoku shita.","german":"My older brother changed jobs to one with better pay.","furigana":"あに|かせ|しごと|てんしょく"}],"notes":"稼ぎ is the noun of 稼ぐ \"to earn\" and denotes income from work: 稼ぎがいい / 少ない. It sounds more everyday than 収入 or 所得. Common compounds: 稼ぎ時 \"peak season, best time to earn\", 出稼ぎ \"working away from home\", 時間稼ぎ \"playing for time\". 稼ぎ手 is the main breadwinner of a family.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鹿狩り用の見張り台は、今は大きなブルーシートで覆われています。","romaji":"Shikagariyō no miharidai wa, ima wa ōkina burū shīto de ōwarete imasu.","german":"The lookout tower for deer hunting is now covered with a big blue tarp.","furigana":"しかが|よう|みは|だい|いま|おお|おお"},{"japanese":"週末に家族でいちご狩りに行きました。","romaji":"Shūmatsu ni kazoku de ichigogari ni ikimashita.","german":"On the weekend, we went strawberry picking as a family.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|かぞく|が|い"},{"japanese":"秋になると、京都へ紅葉狩りに出かける人が多い。","romaji":"Aki ni naru to, Kyōto e momijigari ni dekakeru hito ga ōi.","german":"When autumn comes, many people go to Kyoto to view the autumn leaves.","furigana":"あき|きょうと|もみじが|で|ひと|おお"}],"notes":"〜狩り (read がり as a suffix) first means \"hunt\" (鹿狩り \"deer hunt\"), but far more often \"gathering, picking, enjoying\" as a leisure activity: いちご狩り \"strawberry picking\", ぶどう狩り, 潮干狩り \"shellfish gathering at low tide\", 紅葉狩り \"viewing autumn leaves\". Figuratively it stands for hunting people: 魔女狩り \"witch hunt\". Read on its own, the word is 狩り (かり) \"hunt\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「どうして歯を磨かないの？」「だって、食べ物の味が口に残っているのが好きなんだもん。それっていけないことかしら？」","romaji":"\"Dōshite ha o migakanai no?\" \"Datte, tabemono no aji ga kuchi ni nokotte iru no ga suki na n da mon. Sore tte ikenai koto kashira?\"","german":"\"Why don't you brush your teeth?\" \"Because I like the taste of food lingering in my mouth. Is that wrong?\"","furigana":"は|みが|た|もの|あじ|くち|のこ|す"},{"japanese":"毎食後に歯を磨くようにしています。","romaji":"Maishokugo ni ha o migaku yō ni shite imasu.","german":"I try to brush my teeth after every meal.","furigana":"まいしょくご|は|みが"},{"japanese":"寝る前に、ちゃんと歯を磨いた？","romaji":"Neru mae ni, chanto ha o migaita?","german":"Did you brush your teeth properly before bed?","furigana":"ね|まえ|は|みが"}],"notes":"歯を磨く is the normal expression for \"to brush one's teeth\"; 磨く generally means \"to polish, to shine\". One never says 歯を洗う. Common frames: 寝る前に歯を磨く, 食後に歯を磨く. The noun 歯磨き \"tooth brushing\" also forms 歯磨きする, which means the same and is slightly more casual; the toothbrush is 歯ブラシ, toothpaste 歯磨き粉.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"耳を澄ますと、遠くから波の音が聞こえてきた。","romaji":"Mimi o sumasu to, tooku kara nami no oto ga kikoete kita.","german":"When I listened closely, I could hear the sound of waves from far away.","furigana":"みみ|す|とお|なみ|おと|き"},{"japanese":"彼女は何を言われても、澄ました顔をしている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa nani o iwarete mo, sumashita kao o shite iru.","german":"No matter what is said to her, she keeps a composed, unruffled look.","furigana":"かのじょ|なに|い|す|かお"},{"japanese":"泥水をしばらく置いて澄ましてから、上の水だけを使う。","romaji":"Doromizu o shibaraku oite sumashite kara, ue no mizu dake o tsukau.","german":"Let the muddy water stand for a while to clear, then use only the water on top.","furigana":"どろみず|お|す|うえ|みず|つか"}],"notes":"澄ます is the transitive counterpart of 澄む \"to become clear\" and has several meanings: \"to make clear\" (a liquid), 耳を澄ます \"to prick up one's ears, listen closely\", and, used intransitively, \"to act indifferent, to put on airs\", especially in 澄ました顔 / 澄まし顔. The meaning \"to clear\" is rare; 耳を澄ます is by far the most common. Do not confuse it with the identically pronounced 済ます \"to get done, finish\" (宿題を済ます).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"でも、どうしてハッカーは攻撃の矛先を俺たちに向けてきたんだ？","romaji":"Demo, dōshite hakkā wa kōgeki no hokosaki o oretachi ni mukete kita n da?","german":"But why did the hackers turn their attack on us?","furigana":"こうげき|ほこさき|おれ|む"},{"japanese":"事故の後、世間の批判の矛先は鉄道会社に向けられた。","romaji":"Jiko no ato, seken no hihan no hokosaki wa tetsudou gaisha ni mukerareta.","german":"After the accident, public criticism was aimed at the railway company.","furigana":"じこ|あと|せけん|ひはん|ほこさき|てつどうがいしゃ|む"},{"japanese":"部長の怒りの矛先が、なぜか私に向いてきた。","romaji":"Buchou no ikari no hokosaki ga, nazeka watashi ni muite kita.","german":"For some reason, the department head's anger was directed at me.","furigana":"ぶちょう|いか|ほこさき|わたし|む"}],"notes":"矛先 is literally the tip of a spear (矛), but is used almost only figuratively: the target or thrust of an attack, criticism or anger. Set phrases: 批判の矛先, 矛先を向ける \"to turn against someone\", 矛先を変える / 転じる \"to change the target\", 矛先が向く \"to come into the crosshairs\". The word is neutral to written-language and frequent in the news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"伊達さんがうちの子を見ていてくれたお陰で気兼ねなく買い物ができました。","romaji":"Datesan ga uchi no ko o mite ite kureta okage de kigane naku kaimono ga dekimashita.","german":"Thanks to Mr. Date watching our child, I was able to shop without worry.","furigana":"だて|こ|み|かげ|きが|か|もの"},{"japanese":"皆さんのおかげで、無事に卒業することができました。","romaji":"Minasan no okage de, buji ni sotsugyou suru koto ga dekimashita.","german":"Thanks to everyone, I was able to graduate safely.","furigana":"みな|ぶじ|そつぎょう"},{"japanese":"君が遅れたおかげで、終電に乗り遅れたよ。","romaji":"Kimi ga okureta okage de, shuuden ni noriokureta yo.","german":"Because you were late, I missed the last train.","furigana":"きみ|おく|しゅうでん|の|おく"}],"notes":"おかげで expresses that a positive result is owed to someone or something: noun + のおかげで, verb + おかげで. It is mostly written in kana. For negative causes one uses せいで; ironically, however, おかげで can also stand for something negative (\"thanks to you, I have...\"). The polite phrase おかげさまで answers questions about one's well-being.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"綿棒で犬の耳垢を取ったら何だか粘々していたんですが、それは普通ですか。","romaji":"Menbō de inu no mimiaka o tottara nandaka nebaneba shite ita n desu ga, sore wa futsū desu ka.","german":"When I cleaned the dog's ear wax with a cotton swab, it was somehow sticky. Is that normal?","furigana":"めんぼう|いぬ|みみあか|と|なん|ねばねば|ふつう"},{"japanese":"納豆やオクラのようなねばねばした食べ物は体にいいらしい。","romaji":"Nattō ya okura no yō na nebaneba shita tabemono wa karada ni ii rashii.","german":"Sticky foods like natto and okra are said to be good for the body.","furigana":"なっとう|た|もの|からだ"},{"japanese":"ガムを踏んで、靴の裏がねばねばする。","romaji":"Gamu o funde, kutsu no ura ga nebaneba suru.","german":"I stepped on gum, and the sole of my shoe is sticky.","furigana":"ふ|くつ|うら"}],"notes":"ねばねば is an onomatopoeic word for \"sticky, viscous, stringy\" and is almost always written in kana; 粘々 is rare. As a する-verb: 手がねばねばする; adnominally ねばねばした食べ物 or ねばねばの納豆. Compared with べたべた (\"sticky-damp, greasy\", e.g. of sweat or syrup), ねばねば describes a viscous, stringy mass like natto, okra or mucus. Related verb: 粘る.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"アスファルトは、砂利を石油で固めたものなんですよ。","romaji":"Asufuaruto wa, jari o sekiyu de katameta mono na n desu yo.","german":"Asphalt is gravel bound together with petroleum, you know.","furigana":"じゃり|せきゆ|かた"},{"japanese":"神社の参道には、白い砂利が敷いてある。","romaji":"Jinja no sandou ni wa, shiroi jari ga shiite aru.","german":"White gravel is laid along the approach to the shrine.","furigana":"じんじゃ|さんどう|しろ|じゃり|し"},{"japanese":"砂利道を自転車で走ると、ガタガタ揺れる。","romaji":"Jarimichi o jitensha de hashiru to, gatagata yureru.","german":"Riding a bicycle on a gravel road makes it rattle and shake.","furigana":"じゃりみち|じてんしゃ|はし|ゆ"}],"notes":"砂利 is \"gravel, crushed stone\": small stones of the kind laid on paths, parking lots, railway tracks and shrine grounds. Typical phrases are 砂利道 \"gravel road\" and 砂利を敷く \"to spread gravel\". Fine sand is 砂, larger stones 石. Colloquially and somewhat pejoratively, じゃり (usually in kana) can also mean \"kid, brat\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お前の親父さん、元気？","romaji":"Omae no oyajisan, genki?","german":"How's your old man?","furigana":"まえ|おやじ|げんき"},{"japanese":"うちの親父は、昔は大工だったんだ。","romaji":"Uchi no oyaji wa, mukashi wa daiku datta n da.","german":"My old man used to be a carpenter.","furigana":"おやじ|むかし|だいく"},{"japanese":"あの店の親父さんは無口だけど、料理の腕は確かだ。","romaji":"Ano mise no oyajisan wa mukuchi dakedo, ryouri no ude wa tashika da.","german":"The owner of that shop is taciturn, but his cooking is reliably good.","furigana":"みせ|おやじ|むくち|りょうり|うで|たし"}],"notes":"親父 is a familiar, rather masculine word for one's own father: \"my old man\". It is usually written in kana (おやじ / オヤジ). For someone else's father one says 親父さん or, neutrally, お父さん. It also means \"middle-aged or older man\" (often pejorative, e.g. オヤジギャグ \"dad joke\") and, as 親父さん, the owner of an eatery.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"クッキーが粉々になるまで、伸ばし棒で叩いて砕きました。","romaji":"Kukkī ga konagona ni naru made, nobashi bō de tataite kudakimashita.","german":"I crushed the cookies with a rolling pin until they were in pieces.","furigana":"こなごな|の|ぼう|たた|くだ"},{"japanese":"その一言で、彼の夢は打ち砕かれた。","romaji":"Sono hitokoto de, kare no yume wa uchikudakareta.","german":"With that one remark, his dream was shattered.","furigana":"ひとこと|かれ|ゆめ|う|くだ"},{"japanese":"母は家族の健康にいつも心を砕いてきた。","romaji":"Haha wa kazoku no kenkou ni itsumo kokoro o kudaite kita.","german":"My mother has always worried about the health of the family.","furigana":"はは|かぞく|けんこう|こころ|くだ"}],"notes":"砕く means \"to smash, to break up, to crush\": 氷を砕く, 岩を砕く. Figuratively: to crush hopes or resistance (夢を打ち砕く), to take pains over something (心を砕く \"to go to great pains\") and to explain something in simplified form (砕いて説明する, more often 噛み砕く). The intransitive counterpart is 砕ける \"to break\", as in 砕けた言い方 \"casual way of speaking\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ご飯を炊きながら、妹は私に家計が火の車だとぼやきました。","romaji":"Gohan o takinagara, imouto wa watashi ni kakei ga hi no kuruma da to boyakimashita.","german":"While cooking rice, my younger sister grumbled to me that the household finances were in dire straits.","furigana":"はん|た|いもうと|わたし|かけい|ひ|くるま"},{"japanese":"今夜はお客さんが来るので、五合のご飯を炊いた。","romaji":"Kon'ya wa okyakusan ga kuru node, gogou no gohan o taita.","german":"We have guests tonight, so I cooked five go of rice.","furigana":"こんや|きゃく|く|ごごう|はん|た"},{"japanese":"関西では、大根を薄味で炊くことが多い。","romaji":"Kansai de wa, daikon o usuaji de taku koto ga ooi.","german":"In Kansai, daikon is often simmered in a light seasoning.","furigana":"かんさい|だいこん|うすあじ|た|おお"}],"notes":"炊く mainly means \"to cook rice\": ご飯を炊く, 炊飯器 \"rice cooker\", 炊き込みご飯. For cooking other food in liquid, standard Japanese says 煮る; in the Kansai dialect, however, 炊く is also used for vegetables or fish (大根を炊く). In the general sense of \"to cook\" one uses 料理する or 作る.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は陰では顧客のことを「カモ」と呼んでいる癖に、表ではペコペコ頭を下げておべっかを言っている。","romaji":"Kare wa kage de wa kokyaku no koto o \"kamo\" to yonde iru kuse ni, omote de wa pekopeko atama o sagete obekka o itte iru.","german":"Behind their backs he calls customers \"suckers\", yet to their faces he bows and flatters them.","furigana":"かれ|かげ|こきゃく|よ|くせ|おもて|あたま|さ|い"},{"japanese":"何も知らないくせに、偉そうなことを言うな。","romaji":"Nani mo shiranai kuse ni, erasou na koto o iu na.","german":"You don't know anything, yet you talk big.","furigana":"なに|し|えら|い"},{"japanese":"新人のくせに、毎日定時に帰るなんて生意気だと言われた。","romaji":"Shinjin no kuse ni, mainichi teiji ni kaeru nante namaiki da to iwareta.","german":"I was told it's cheeky for a newcomer to go home on time every day.","furigana":"しんじん|まいにちていじ|かえ|なまいき|い"}],"notes":"くせに connects two statements with reproach: \"although ..., (shamelessly) ...\". It is almost always written in kana and follows noun + の or a verb/adjective in plain form: 子どものくせに, 知らないくせに. Unlike the neutral のに, くせに expresses anger or contempt toward the person and is used only when both clauses have the same subject. Casual variant: くせして.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あなたの国は、帝国主義国家ですか？","romaji":"Anata no kuni wa, teikoku shugi kokka desu ka?","german":"Is your country an imperialist state?","furigana":"くに|ていこくしゅぎこっか"},{"japanese":"十九世紀後半は、帝国主義の時代と呼ばれている。","romaji":"Juukyuu seiki kouhan wa, teikoku shugi no jidai to yobarete iru.","german":"The latter half of the nineteenth century is called the age of imperialism.","furigana":"じゅうきゅうせいきこうはん|ていこくしゅぎ|じだい|よ"},{"japanese":"歴史の授業で、帝国主義と植民地支配の関係について学んだ。","romaji":"Rekishi no jugyou de, teikoku shugi to shokuminchi shihai no kankei nitsuite mananda.","german":"In history class, I learned about the relationship between imperialism and colonial rule.","furigana":"れきし|じゅぎょう|ていこくしゅぎ|しょくみんちしはい|かんけい|まな"}],"notes":"帝国主義 is \"imperialism\", the policy of extending power and rule over other countries. The word belongs to history and politics: 帝国主義の時代, 帝国主義国家, 反帝国主義. Closely connected are 植民地 \"colony\" and 植民地支配 \"colonial rule\". As an attribute, 帝国主義の or 帝国主義的な \"imperialistic\" is used.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"駅にエレベーターができたので、車椅子でも安心して利用できる。","romaji":"Eki ni erebeetaa ga dekita node, kurumaisu demo anshin shite riyou dekiru.","german":"Now that the station has an elevator, wheelchair users can use it with peace of mind.","furigana":"えき|くるまいす|あんしん|りよう"},{"japanese":"祖母は足を骨折してから、車椅子で生活している。","romaji":"Sobo wa ashi o kossetsu shite kara, kurumaisu de seikatsu shite iru.","german":"My grandmother has been living in a wheelchair since she broke her leg.","furigana":"そぼ|あし|こっせつ|くるまいす|せいかつ"},{"japanese":"よろしければ、車椅子を押しましょうか。","romaji":"Yoroshikereba, kurumaisu o oshimashou ka.","german":"If you'd like, shall I push your wheelchair?","furigana":"くるまいす|お"}],"notes":"車椅子 is the \"wheelchair\": 車椅子に乗る / 座る, 車椅子を押す \"to push the wheelchair\", 車椅子で生活する. Barrier-free facilities are called 車椅子対応, e.g. 車椅子対応トイレ. It is also written 車いす. The word is neutral and used equally in daily life, nursing care and transport.","levelNote":"Compound: 車 (N5) + 椅子 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その力士は土俵際で踏ん張り、相手力士に打っ棄りを食らわせた。","romaji":"Sono rikishi wa dohyōgiwa de funbari, aite rikishi ni utchari o kurawaseta.","german":"The wrestler dug in at the edge of the ring and flung his opponent with a last-ditch throw.","furigana":"りきし|どひょうぎわ|ふ||あいてりきし|う||く"},{"japanese":"交渉は土俵際で何とかまとまった。","romaji":"Kōshō wa dohyōgiwa de nantoka matomatta.","german":"The negotiations somehow came together at the last moment.","furigana":"こうしょう|どひょうぎわ|なん"},{"japanese":"相次ぐ問題で、社長は土俵際に追い込まれている。","romaji":"Aitsugu mondai de, shachō wa dohyōgiwa ni oikomarete iru.","german":"Amid a string of problems, the company president is being driven to the brink.","furigana":"あいつ|もんだい|しゃちょう|どひょうぎわ|お|こ"}],"notes":"土俵際 is literally the \"edge of the sumo ring\" (土俵 \"ring\" + 際 \"edge\"), where a wrestler is about to be pushed out: 土俵際で踏ん張る \"to dig in at the edge of the ring\". Figuratively it means \"the last moment, on the verge of failure\" and is popular in political and business news: 土俵際に追い込まれる \"to be pushed into a corner\". Similar is 瀬戸際 \"on the brink of the abyss\".","levelNote":"Compound: 土俵 (N1) + 際 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"そのクイズの答えが当たったのは、ただのまぐれです。","romaji":"Sono kuizu no kotae ga atatta no wa, tada no magure desu.","german":"Getting that quiz answer right was just a fluke.","furigana":"こた|あ"},{"japanese":"一回まぐれで勝っただけで、実力じゃないよ。","romaji":"Ikkai magure de katta dake de, jitsuryoku ja nai yo.","german":"Winning once by a fluke doesn't mean it's real ability.","furigana":"いっかい|か|じつりょく"},{"japanese":"適当に書いた答えが、まぐれ当たりした。","romaji":"Tekitou ni kaita kotae ga, magure atari shita.","german":"The answer I wrote at random turned out to be a lucky hit.","furigana":"てきとう|か|こた|あ"}],"notes":"まぐれ means \"a lucky hit, a pure fluke\" for a success that is not based on skill. It is almost always written in kana; the kanji 紛れ are normally read まぎれ (e.g. 腹立ち紛れ). Typical: ただのまぐれ, まぐれで勝つ, まぐれ当たり. For chance in the general sense one says 偶然; まぐれ is colloquial and always refers to lucky successes.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"脅迫電話を受けた直後に警察に電話をしましたが、彼らは何だか対応したくなさそうでした。","romaji":"Kyōhaku denwa o uketa chokugo ni keisatsu ni denwa o shimashita ga, karera wa nandaka taiō shitakunasasōdeshita.","german":"I called the police right after receiving a threatening phone call, but they didn't seem to want to deal with it.","furigana":"きょうはくでんわ|う|ちょくご|けいさつ|でんわ|かれ|なん|たいおう"},{"japanese":"男は元交際相手を脅迫した疑いで逮捕された。","romaji":"Otoko wa moto kousai aite o kyouhaku shita utagai de taiho sareta.","german":"The man was arrested on suspicion of threatening his former partner.","furigana":"おとこ|もとこうさいあいて|きょうはく|うたが|たいほ"},{"japanese":"会社に脅迫状が届き、イベントは中止になった。","romaji":"Kaisha ni kyouhakujou ga todoki, ibento wa chuushi ni natta.","german":"A threatening letter arrived at the company, and the event was cancelled.","furigana":"かいしゃ|きょうはくじょう|とど|ちゅうし"}],"notes":"脅迫 is threatening or coercing a person, also as a criminal offense: 〜を脅迫する, 脅迫状 \"threatening letter\", 脅迫電話 \"threatening phone call\", 脅迫罪. It is formal and legal; in everyday speech people say 脅す. Distinguish 恐喝 \"extortion (for money)\" and the identically pronounced 強迫 \"compulsion\" (強迫観念 \"obsession\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"約束を守らないと、みんなから相手にされなくなるぞ。いいから、お前が先日会ったっていう、その魔法使いを連れてこいよ！","romaji":"Yakusoku o mamoranai to, minna kara aite ni sarenaku naru zo. Ii kara, omae ga senjitsu atta tte iu, sono mahoutsukai o tsurete koi yo!","german":"If you don't keep your promises, nobody will take you seriously. Anyway, bring me that wizard you met the other day!","furigana":"やくそく|まも|あいて|まえ|せんじつあ|まほうつか|つ"},{"japanese":"子どものころ、魔法使いになるのが夢だった。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, mahoutsukai ni naru no ga yume datta.","german":"As a child, my dream was to become a wizard.","furigana":"こ|まほうつか|ゆめ"},{"japanese":"この物語では、悪い魔法使いがお姫様を塔に閉じ込めてしまう。","romaji":"Kono monogatari de wa, warui mahoutsukai ga ohimesama o tou ni tojikomete shimau.","german":"In this story, an evil wizard locks the princess in a tower.","furigana":"ものがたり|わる|まほうつか|ひめさま|とう|と|こ"}],"notes":"魔法使い is a \"wizard, magician\", a person who commands real magic (魔法), as in fairy tales and fantasy. Witches are specifically 魔女. A stage magician, by contrast, is a 手品師 or マジシャン. The 使い comes from 使う \"to use\" and forms \"someone who commands something\", as in 猛獣使い \"animal tamer\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"帳簿に記帳をし忘れたため、上司に叱られました。","romaji":"Choubo ni kichou o shiwasureta tame, joushi ni shikararemashita.","german":"I was scolded by my boss because I forgot to make entries in the ledger.","furigana":"ちょうぼ|きちょう|わす|じょうし|しか"},{"japanese":"給料が入ったか確かめるために、ATMで通帳に記帳した。","romaji":"Kyuuryou ga haitta ka tashikameru tame ni, eetiiemu de tsuuchou ni kichou shita.","german":"I updated my passbook at the ATM to check whether my salary had come in.","furigana":"きゅうりょう|はい|たし|つうちょう|きちょう"},{"japanese":"受付でお名前を記帳してから、会場にお入りください。","romaji":"Uketsuke de onamae o kichou shite kara, kaijou ni ohairi kudasai.","german":"Please sign in at the reception desk before entering the venue.","furigana":"うけつけ|なまえ|きちょう|かいじょう|はい"}],"notes":"記帳 means \"to enter something in a book\" and has three typical uses: entering transactions in the account books (帳簿に記帳する), having account activity printed in the passbook (通帳に記帳する, at the ATM) and signing the guest book at occasions such as funerals or receptions (受付で記帳する). For \"bookkeeping\" as an activity, 簿記 or 経理 is more usual.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"話の途中で申し訳ありませんが、あなたのメモ帳のことでどうしても質問したいんです。","romaji":"Hanashi no tochū de mōshiwake arimasen ga, anata no memochō no koto de dōshitemo shitsumon shitai n desu.","german":"Sorry to interrupt, but I really want to ask you about your notepad.","furigana":"はなし|とちゅう|もう|わけ|ちょう|しつもん"},{"japanese":"いいアイデアが浮かんだら、すぐメモ帳に書いておくようにしている。","romaji":"Ii aidea ga ukandara, sugu memochou ni kaite oku you ni shite iru.","german":"When a good idea comes to me, I make a point of writing it in my notepad right away.","furigana":"う|ちょう|か"},{"japanese":"パソコンのメモ帳で、メールの下書きを作った。","romaji":"Pasokon no memochou de, meeru no shitagaki o tsukutta.","german":"I drafted the email in Notepad on my computer.","furigana":"ちょう|したが|つく"}],"notes":"メモ帳 is a \"notepad, memo book\" for short notes: メモ帳に書く, メモ帳を持ち歩く. It is also the Japanese name of the Windows program \"Notepad\" and of note apps in general. A larger, bound notebook is more often ノート or 手帳 (pocket diary/planner); メモ alone is a single note.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この電話帳を整理してしまう方がいいと思います。","romaji":"Kono denwachō o seiri shite shimau hō ga ii to omoimasu.","german":"I think it would be better to tidy up this phone book.","furigana":"でんわちょう|せいり|ほう|おも"},{"japanese":"昔は、お店の番号を電話帳で調べたものだ。","romaji":"Mukashi wa, omise no bangou o denwachou de shirabeta mono da.","german":"In the past, we used to look up shops' numbers in the telephone directory.","furigana":"むかし|みせ|ばんごう|でんわちょう|しら"},{"japanese":"スマホの電話帳から、もう使わない連絡先を削除した。","romaji":"Sumaho no denwachou kara, mou tsukawanai renrakusaki o sakujo shita.","german":"I deleted contacts I no longer use from my smartphone's phone book.","furigana":"でんわちょう|つか|れんらくさき|さくじょ"}],"notes":"電話帳 is the printed \"telephone directory\" with names and numbers, e.g. 電話帳で調べる \"to look up in the phone book\". Today it often also means the contact list on a mobile phone (スマホの電話帳), also called 連絡先. The yellow pages were called タウンページ. The 帳 stands, as in 手帳 or 通帳, for a notebook for entries.","levelNote":"Compound: 電話 (N5) + 帳 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"肩がこったので、湿布を貼りました。","romaji":"Kata ga kotta node, shippu o harimashita.","german":"My shoulders were stiff, so I put on a pain-relief patch.","furigana":"かた|しっぷ|は"},{"japanese":"切手を封筒に貼ってください。","romaji":"Kitte o fūtō ni hatte kudasai.","german":"Please stick the stamp on the envelope.","furigana":"きって|ふうとう|は"}],"notes":"貼る means to stick or attach something onto a surface. The object stands before を, the surface before に: 切手を封筒に貼る. It is a godan verb ending in る: 貼ります and 貼って. This spelling is especially suited to sticking something on; the related spelling 張る also appears in meanings such as stretching or setting up.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 21"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"みんなの前で私の事を辱めるのが、どうしてそんなに面白いんですか？","romaji":"Minna no mae de watashi no koto o hazukashimeru no ga, dōshite sonnani omoshiroi n desu ka?","german":"Why is it so amusing to you to humiliate me in front of everyone?","furigana":"まえ|わたし|こと|はずかし|おもしろ"},{"japanese":"彼の行為は学校の名を辱めるものだ。","romaji":"Kare no kōi wa gakkō no na o hazukashimeru mono da.","german":"His conduct brings disgrace on the school's name.","furigana":"かれ|こうい|がっこう|な|はずかし"},{"japanese":"負けた相手を辱めるような態度は、スポーツマンとして許されない。","romaji":"Maketa aite o hazukashimeru yō na taido wa, supōtsuman to shite yurusarenai.","german":"An attitude that humiliates a defeated opponent is unacceptable for a sportsman.","furigana":"ま|あいて|はずかし|たいど|ゆる"}],"notes":"辱める means \"to humiliate, to dishonor, to bring shame upon\" and is transitive: 人を辱める, 名誉を辱める, 家名を辱める. The word is written-language to elevated; in daily life one rather says 恥をかかせる \"to embarrass someone\". Often in the passive: 辱められる \"to be humiliated\". Note: in literature and news, 辱める is also a euphemism for sexual violence, so use it with care. Do not confuse with 恥じる \"to be ashamed\" (intransitive, one's own feeling).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大勢の前で叱られ、彼は大きな恥辱を味わった。","romaji":"Ōzei no mae de shikarare, kare wa ōkina chijoku o ajiwatta.","german":"Scolded in front of many people, he experienced great humiliation.","furigana":"おおぜい|まえ|しか|かれ|おお|ちじょく|あじ"},{"japanese":"選手たちは昨年の大敗の恥辱を晴らそうと、毎日練習に励んだ。","romaji":"Senshu-tachi wa sakunen no taihai no chijoku o harasō to, mainichi renshū ni hagenda.","german":"The players trained every day to wipe out the humiliation of last year's crushing defeat.","furigana":"せんしゅ|さくねん|たいはい|ちじょく|は|まいにちれんしゅう|はげ"},{"japanese":"彼にとって、仲間を裏切ったと言われることほどの恥辱はなかった。","romaji":"Kare ni totte, nakama o uragitta to iwareru koto hodo no chijoku wa nakatta.","german":"For him, there was no greater disgrace than being said to have betrayed his comrades.","furigana":"かれ|なかま|うらぎ|い|ちじょく"}],"notes":"恥辱 is a serious, publicly felt \"disgrace, shame\" and belongs to written language. Typical combinations: 恥辱を受ける, 恥辱を味わう, 恥辱に耐える, 恥辱を晴らす \"to wipe out a disgrace\". In daily life 恥 (\"shame, embarrassment\", 恥をかく) is enough. 屈辱 is the more common term for a \"humiliation\" suffered at the hands of others; 侮辱 is an active \"insult\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"そのラーメン屋の店主は、いつも色あせた綿の鉢巻を締めていました。","romaji":"Sono rāmen'ya no tenshu wa, itsumo iroaseta men no hachimaki o shimete imashita.","german":"The owner of that ramen shop always wore a faded cotton headband.","furigana":"や|てんしゅ|いろ|めん|はちまき|し"},{"japanese":"運動会では、赤組と白組に分かれて鉢巻をします。","romaji":"Undōkai de wa, akagumi to shirogumi ni wakarete hachimaki o shimasu.","german":"At the sports day, we split into red and white teams and wear headbands.","furigana":"うんどうかい|あかぐみ|しろぐみ|わ|はちまき"},{"japanese":"受験生たちは「必勝」と書かれた鉢巻を巻いて、最後の勉強に取り組んだ。","romaji":"Jukensei-tachi wa hisshō to kakareta hachimaki o maite, saigo no benkyō ni torikunda.","german":"The exam takers tied on headbands reading 「必勝」 and tackled their last studying.","furigana":"じゅけんせい|ひっしょう|か|はちまき|ま|さいご|べんきょう|と|く"}],"notes":"鉢巻 (usually written はちまき or 鉢巻き) is a cloth tied around the forehead. One says 鉢巻を締める or 鉢巻を巻く, in short also 鉢巻をする. It stands for determination: exam takers wear 必勝 headbands, at the school sports festival (運動会) there are red and white ones, craftsmen and cooks wear the twisted ねじり鉢巻. Note the verb: 締める (to tie, to tighten), not 絞める (to strangle).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"暗闇で戦う時は、相手の出方を見て、その上でどうするか判断した方がいいよ。","romaji":"Kurayami de tatakau toki wa, aite no dekata o mite, sono ue de dō suru ka handan shita hō ga ii yo.","german":"When fighting in the dark, it's best to see how your opponent moves and then decide what to do.","furigana":"くらやみ|たたか|とき|あいて|でかた|み|うえ|はんだん|ほう"},{"japanese":"停電で部屋が真っ暗闇になり、懐中電灯を探した。","romaji":"Teiden de heya ga makkurayami ni nari, kaichū dentō o sagashita.","german":"The power outage plunged the room into pitch darkness, and I looked for a flashlight.","furigana":"ていでん|へや|ま|くらやみ|かいちゅうでんとう|さが"},{"japanese":"子どもの頃は暗闇が怖くて、電気をつけたまま寝ていた。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro wa kurayami ga kowakute, denki o tsuketa mama nete ita.","german":"As a child, I was afraid of the dark and slept with the light on.","furigana":"こ|ころ|くらやみ|こわ|でんき|ね"}],"notes":"暗闇 is concrete \"darkness, the dark\" as a place or state: 暗闇の中で, 暗闇に目が慣れる \"eyes get used to the dark\", 暗闇が怖い. Intensified: 真っ暗闇 \"pitch dark\". 闇 alone is more literary and often figurative (闇取引 \"black-market dealing\"), 暗さ denotes the degree of darkness. The corresponding adjective is 暗い.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二台の自販機の隙間に、小銭が二、三枚落ちているのを見つけました。","romaji":"Nidai no jihanki no sukima ni, kozeni ga ni, sanmai ochite iru no o mitsukemashita.","german":"I found a couple of coins that had fallen in the gap between the two vending machines.","furigana":"にだい|じはんき|すきま|こぜに|に|さんまいお|み"},{"japanese":"窓の隙間から冷たい風が入ってくる。","romaji":"Mado no sukima kara tsumetai kaze ga haitte kuru.","german":"Cold wind comes in through the gap in the window.","furigana":"まど|すきま|つめ|かぜ|はい"},{"japanese":"通勤の隙間時間を使って、英単語を覚えています。","romaji":"Tsūkin no sukima jikan o tsukatte, eitango o oboete imasu.","german":"I memorize English words in my spare moments during the commute.","furigana":"つうきん|すきまじかん|つか|えいたんご|おぼ"}],"notes":"隙間 is a narrow \"gap, crack, space\" between two things: ドアの隙間, 〜と〜の隙間に, 隙間から光が漏れる. Compounds: 隙間風 \"draft (through cracks)\" and the modern 隙間時間 \"small windows of free time\". For an \"opening, unguarded spot\" one now rather uses 隙 alone (隙を見せる). 間 (あいだ) is more general \"between\", without the idea of a narrow gap.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"長年日本語を独学してきたんですが、今は誰かと日本語の会話をすることに飢えています。","romaji":"Naganen nihongo o dokugaku shite kita n desu ga, ima wa dare ka to nihongo no kaiwa o suru koto ni uete imasu.","german":"I've been teaching myself Japanese for years, and now I'm hungry for Japanese conversation with someone.","furigana":"ながねんにほんご|どくがく|いま|だれ|にほんご|かいわ|う"},{"japanese":"戦争中は食べ物がなく、多くの人が飢えていた。","romaji":"Sensōchū wa tabemono ga naku, ōku no hito ga uete ita.","german":"During the war there was no food, and many people were starving.","furigana":"せんそうちゅう|た|もの|おお|ひと|う"},{"japanese":"その子は親の愛情に飢えていたのかもしれない。","romaji":"Sono ko wa oya no aijō ni uete ita no kamo shirenai.","german":"That child may have been starved of parental affection.","furigana":"こ|おや|あいじょう|う"}],"notes":"飢える is intransitive and literally means \"to starve, to suffer hunger\" (飢えた子どもたち), which sounds rather written; for normal hunger one says お腹が空く. Very common is the figurative meaning with に: 愛情に飢える \"to hunger for love\", 知識に飢える, 刺激に飢える. The noun is 飢え (飢えに苦しむ), \"to starve to death\" is 飢え死にする or 餓死する.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"警察は、その寝たきりの老人は餓死したと考えている。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa, sono netakiri no rōjin wa gashi shita to kangaete iru.","german":"The police believe the bedridden old man starved to death.","furigana":"けいさつ|ね|ろうじん|がし|かんが"},{"japanese":"雨の降らない日が続き、多くの家畜が餓死した。","romaji":"Ame no furanai hi ga tsuzuki, ōku no kachiku ga gashi shita.","german":"With days without rain continuing, many livestock starved to death.","furigana":"あめ|ふ|ひ|つづ|おお|かちく|がし"},{"japanese":"「お腹すいた、餓死しそう！」と弟が大げさに叫んだ。","romaji":"Onaka suita, gashi shisō! to otōto ga ōgesa ni sakenda.","german":"\"I'm hungry, I'm going to starve!\" my younger brother shouted dramatically.","furigana":"なか|がし|おとうと|おお|さけ"}],"notes":"餓死 is \"death by starvation\"; as an intransitive する-verb 餓死する \"to starve to death\". The word sounds matter-of-fact to formal and is typical of news and reports (餓死者 \"those who starved\"). Colloquially one says 飢え死にする, and jokingly one likes to exaggerate with 餓死しそう \"I'm about to starve\". Cf. 飢える \"to hunger\" (without death).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このトウモロコシは畜産用飼料なので、人間用ではありません。","romaji":"Kono tōmorokoshi wa chikusan'yō shiryō na node, ningen'yō de wa arimasen.","german":"This corn is livestock feed, not for human consumption.","furigana":"ちくさんようしりょう|にんげんよう"},{"japanese":"この地方では、畜産が主な産業になっている。","romaji":"Kono chihō de wa, chikusan ga omo na sangyō ni natte iru.","german":"In this region, livestock farming is the main industry.","furigana":"ちほう|ちくさん|おも|さんぎょう"},{"japanese":"祖父は北海道で畜産農家を営んでいます。","romaji":"Sofu wa Hokkaidō de chikusan nōka o itonande imasu.","german":"My grandfather runs a livestock farm in Hokkaido.","furigana":"そふ|ほっかいどう|ちくさんのうか|いとな"}],"notes":"畜産 refers to \"animal husbandry\" as an industry: keeping cattle, pigs and chickens to obtain meat, milk and eggs. Typical are 畜産業, 畜産農家, 畜産物 \"animal products\". Narrower terms: 酪農 \"dairy farming\", 養豚 \"pig farming\"; the animals themselves are 家畜. The tone is factual (business, government, school).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本独特の家畜っていうのはいるんですか？","romaji":"Nihon dokutoku no kachiku tte iu no wa iru n desu ka?","german":"Are there any livestock unique to Japan?","furigana":"にほんどくとく|かちく"},{"japanese":"村の人々は牛や豚などの家畜を飼って暮らしている。","romaji":"Mura no hitobito wa ushi ya buta nado no kachiku o katte kurashite iru.","german":"The villagers make their living raising livestock such as cattle and pigs.","furigana":"むら|ひとびと|うし|ぶた|かちく|か|く"},{"japanese":"犬は人間が最初に家畜化した動物だと言われている。","romaji":"Inu wa ningen ga saisho ni kachikuka shita dōbutsu da to iwarete iru.","german":"The dog is said to have been the first animal humans domesticated.","furigana":"いぬ|にんげん|さいしょ|かちくか|どうぶつ|い"}],"notes":"家畜 means \"livestock, farm animals\", animals that people keep for food or labor: 牛, 豚, 鶏, 馬. One says 家畜を飼う, 家畜の世話をする; 家畜化 is \"domestication\". Pets kept for pleasure are called ペット. The industry is 畜産. Figuratively 家畜 is pejorative (\"treated like cattle\", e.g. 社畜 \"corporate drone\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この畜生め！何でシラチャソースを俺のベッドにぶちまけやがったんだ！","romaji":"Kono chikushō me! Nande shiracha sōsu o ore no beddo ni buchimakeyagatta n da!","german":"You bastard! Why did you spill sriracha sauce all over my bed?!","furigana":"ちくしょう|なん|おれ"},{"japanese":"ちくしょう、また終電に乗り遅れた！","romaji":"Chikushō, mata shūden ni noriokureta!","german":"Damn, I missed the last train again!","furigana":"しゅうでん|の|おく"},{"japanese":"畜生、あと一点で合格だったのに。","romaji":"Chikushō, ato itten de gōkaku datta noni.","german":"Damn it, I was one point away from passing.","furigana":"ちくしょう|いってん|ごうかく"}],"notes":"畜生 is today mainly a coarse curse: \"damn!, shoot!\", usually written in kana as ちくしょう or, casually, ちきしょう. As an insult aimed at a person: この畜生め \"you bastard\". Originally it means \"beast, animal\" in the Buddhist sense (畜生道 \"realm of animals\"). The tone is rough and rather masculine; milder exclamations are くそ or しまった.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"こちらの件に関して、迅速かつ丁寧にご対応頂き誠に有り難うございます。","romaji":"Kochira no ken ni kanshite, jinsoku katsu teinei ni go taiō itadaki makoto ni arigatō gozaimasu.","german":"Thank you very much for your prompt and courteous handling of this matter.","furigana":"けん|かん|じんそく|ていねい|たいおういただ|まこと|あ|がと"},{"japanese":"災害時には迅速な情報提供が求められる。","romaji":"Saigaiji ni wa jinsoku na jōhō teikyō ga motomerareru.","german":"Prompt provision of information is required during disasters.","furigana":"さいがいじ|じんそく|じょうほうていきょう|もと"},{"japanese":"ご注文の商品は迅速に発送いたします。","romaji":"Go-chūmon no shōhin wa jinsoku ni hassō itashimasu.","german":"We will ship the product you ordered promptly.","furigana":"ちゅうもん|しょうひん|じんそく|はっそう"}],"notes":"迅速 is a formal な-adjective for \"rapid, swift, prompt\", used above all in business language, government and news: 迅速な対応 \"prompt handling\", 迅速に処理する. The pairing 迅速かつ丁寧に \"quickly and carefully\" is typical. In everyday speech people say 速い or すぐ instead; 迅速 does not describe the speed of movements but quick action and response.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"中世ヨーロッパの騎士は、馬に乗って剣や槍で戦った。","romaji":"Chūsei Yōroppa no kishi wa, uma ni notte ken ya yari de tatakatta.","german":"Die Ritter im mittelalterlichen Europa kämpften zu Pferd mit Schwert und Lanze.","furigana":"ちゅうせい|きし|うま|の|けん||たたか"},{"japanese":"彼女はいつか白馬の騎士が迎えに来てくれると信じている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa itsuka hakuba no kishi ga mukae ni kite kureru to shinjite iru.","german":"Sie glaubt, dass eines Tages ihr Prinz auf dem weißen Pferd kommt, um sie abzuholen.","furigana":"かのじょ|はくば|きし|むか|き|しん"},{"japanese":"この映画の主人公は、王女を守る若い騎士だ。","romaji":"Kono eiga no shujinkō wa, ōjo o mamoru wakai kishi da.","german":"Die Hauptfigur dieses Films ist ein junger Ritter, der eine Prinzessin beschützt.","furigana":"えいが|しゅじんこう|おうじょ|まも|わか|きし"}],"notes":"騎士 ist der europäische „Ritter“ des Mittelalters; davon 騎士道 „Rittertum, ritterliche Gesinnung“. Der Ausdruck 白馬の騎士 entspricht dem deutschen „Prinzen auf dem weißen Pferd“ (auch: rettender Investor). Berittene Samurai nennt man meist 騎馬武者, nicht 騎士. Die Schachfigur heißt ナイト. Das Wort wirkt historisch oder märchenhaft.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その騎手は、自分の初めてのG1レースの日に朝寝坊をしました。","romaji":"Sono kishu wa, jibun no hajimete no G1 rēsu no hi ni asanebō o shimashita.","german":"That jockey overslept on the day of his first G1 race.","furigana":"きしゅ|じぶん|はじ|ひ|あさねぼう"},{"japanese":"騎手は体重を厳しく管理しなければならない。","romaji":"Kishu wa taijū o kibishiku kanri shinakereba naranai.","german":"Jockeys have to control their weight strictly.","furigana":"きしゅ|たいじゅう|きび|かんり"},{"japanese":"その馬は騎手を振り落として、一頭で走り続けた。","romaji":"Sono uma wa kishu o furiotoshite, ittō de hashiritsuzuketa.","german":"The horse threw off its jockey and kept running on its own.","furigana":"うま|きしゅ|ふ|お|いっとう|はし|つづ"}],"notes":"騎手 is a \"rider\", above all the professional jockey in horse racing (競馬). One says 騎手が乗る or 騎手を務める; a famous jockey is a 名騎手, a female jockey a 女性騎手. Someone who rides as a hobby does 乗馬; that person is then rather a 乗り手 and not a 騎手. The word is matter-of-fact and typical of sports reports.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ついに食料の備蓄が尽きた。","romaji":"Tsuini shokuryō no bichiku ga tsukita.","german":"The food stockpile finally ran out.","furigana":"しょくりょう|びちく|つ"},{"japanese":"久しぶりに会った友達とは、話が尽きなかった。","romaji":"Hisashiburi ni atta tomodachi to wa, hanashi ga tsukinakatta.","german":"I had no end of things to talk about with a friend I hadn't seen in a long time.","furigana":"ひさ|あ|ともだち|はなし|つ"},{"japanese":"この店の魅力は、何といっても料理のおいしさに尽きる。","romaji":"Kono mise no miryoku wa, nan to itte mo ryōri no oishisa ni tsukiru.","german":"The appeal of this restaurant comes down, above all, to how delicious the food is.","furigana":"みせ|みりょく|なん|りょうり|つ"}],"notes":"尽きる is intransitive: something \"comes to an end, is exhausted\": 食料が尽きる, 力が尽きる (力尽きる \"to be at the end of one's strength\"), 運が尽きる. In the negative it expresses abundance: 話が尽きない \"there is no end to the conversation\". With に, 〜に尽きる means \"comes down to ..., nothing beats\": この一言に尽きる. The transitive counterpart is 尽くす \"to exhaust; to do one's utmost\". In everyday speech, for supplies one often simply says なくなる or 切れる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"超理不尽な上司とやっていかなくちゃいけなくて、理想の仕事が一気に悪夢になったよ。","romaji":"Chō rifujin na jōshi to yatte ikanakucha ikenakute, risō no shigoto ga ikkini akumu ni natta yo.","german":"Mein Traumjob wurde auf einen Schlag zum Albtraum, weil ich mit einem so unvernünftigen Chef zurechtkommen musste.","furigana":"ちょうりふじん|じょうし|りそう|しごと|いっき|あくむ"},{"japanese":"客から理不尽なクレームを言われても、店員は笑顔で対応しなければならない。","romaji":"Kyaku kara rifujin na kurēmu o iwarete mo, ten'in wa egao de taiō shinakereba naranai.","german":"Selbst bei völlig unberechtigten Beschwerden von Kunden müssen die Verkäufer freundlich lächelnd reagieren.","furigana":"きゃく|りふじん|い|てんいん|えがお|たいおう"},{"japanese":"何も悪いことをしていないのに怒られるなんて、理不尽だ。","romaji":"Nani mo warui koto o shite inai noni okorareru nante, rifujin da.","german":"Dass ich ausgeschimpft werde, obwohl ich nichts falsch gemacht habe, ist einfach ungerecht.","furigana":"なに|わる|おこ|りふじん"}],"notes":"理不尽 ist ein な-Adjektiv bzw. Nomen für etwas, das gegen Vernunft und Fairness verstößt: 理不尽な要求, 理不尽な上司, 理不尽な扱いを受ける, „理不尽だ！“ als Ausruf der Empörung. Es ist emotional und betont die Ungerechtigkeit; 不合理 ist nüchtern „unlogisch, irrational“, 不条理 philosophisch „absurd“. Im Deutschen passt oft „ungerecht, unzumutbar“ besser als „unvernünftig“.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"社長として、サービスの向上に尽力いたします。","romaji":"Shachō to shite, sābisu no kōjō ni jinryoku itashimasu.","german":"As president, I will do my utmost to improve our service.","furigana":"しゃちょう|こうじょう|じんりょく"},{"japanese":"皆様のご尽力のおかげで、イベントは無事に終わりました。","romaji":"Minasama no go-jinryoku no okage de, ibento wa buji ni owarimashita.","german":"Thanks to everyone's efforts, the event ended safely.","furigana":"みなさま|じんりょく|ぶじ|お"},{"japanese":"彼女は長年、地域の子どもたちの教育に尽力してきた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa naganen, chiiki no kodomo-tachi no kyōiku ni jinryoku shite kita.","german":"She has worked tirelessly for years for the education of local children.","furigana":"かのじょ|ながねん|ちいき|こ|きょういく|じんりょく"}],"notes":"尽力 is a formal する-verb: \"to exert oneself with all one's strength\" for something, usually for others or a cause: 〜に尽力する, 〜のために尽力する. Very common in thanks formulas: ご尽力いただき、ありがとうございます. Unlike 努力 (\"to make an effort\", often for one's own goals), 尽力 emphasizes service to a cause or to others; 協力 is \"cooperation, assistance\". Typical for speeches, business letters and news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先生は、その生徒は鋭い色彩感覚を持っていると判断しました。","romaji":"Sensei wa, sono seito wa surudoi shikisai kankaku o motte iru to handan shimashita.","german":"The teacher concluded that the student had a keen sense of color.","furigana":"せんせい|せいと|するど|しきさいかんかく|も|はんだん"},{"japanese":"この画家の作品は、明るく豊かな色彩で知られている。","romaji":"Kono gaka no sakuhin wa, akaruku yutaka na shikisai de shirarete iru.","german":"This painter's works are known for their bright, rich colors.","furigana":"がか|さくひん|あか|ゆた|しきさい|し"},{"japanese":"そのデモは政治的な色彩が強かった。","romaji":"Sono demo wa seijiteki na shikisai ga tsuyokatta.","german":"The demonstration had a strong political coloring.","furigana":"せいじてき|しきさい|つよ"}],"notes":"色彩 is a refined, rather technical word for \"color, coloring\" as a whole, especially in art, design and description: 色彩豊か, 鮮やかな色彩, 色彩感覚 \"sense of color\". For a single color in everyday speech, one says 色. Figuratively 色彩 means \"tinge, character\": 政治的な色彩が強い.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日迷彩柄のズボンを買いに行ったんだけど、ひとつも見つけられなかったよ。","romaji":"Kyou meisaigara no zubon o kai ni itta n da kedo, hitotsu mo mitsukerarenakatta yo.","german":"I went to buy camouflage pants today, but couldn't find a single pair.","furigana":"きょうめいさいがら|か|い|み"},{"japanese":"兵士たちは迷彩服を着て、森の中に隠れていた。","romaji":"Heishi-tachi wa meisaifuku o kite, mori no naka ni kakurete ita.","german":"The soldiers were hiding in the forest in camouflage uniforms.","furigana":"へいし|めいさいふく|き|もり|なか|かく"},{"japanese":"この車は、砂漠で目立たないように迷彩が塗られている。","romaji":"Kono kuruma wa, sabaku de medatanai yō ni meisai ga nurarete iru.","german":"This vehicle is painted in camouflage so as not to stand out in the desert.","furigana":"くるま|さばく|めだ|めいさい|ぬ"}],"notes":"迷彩 is \"camouflage\" by color patterns, especially in the military: 迷彩服 \"camouflage uniform\", 迷彩を施す. In fashion, 迷彩柄 \"camouflage pattern\" is very common. For animals that adapt their color, one rather says 保護色 or 擬態; disguising a person is 変装.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は素晴らしい水彩画家だそうですよ。","romaji":"Kanojo wa subarashii suisai gaka da sō desu yo.","german":"I hear she is a wonderful watercolor painter.","furigana":"かのじょ|すば|すいさいがか"},{"japanese":"休みの日は、近くの公園で水彩画を描いています。","romaji":"Yasumi no hi wa, chikaku no kōen de suisaiga o kaite imasu.","german":"On days off, I paint watercolors in a nearby park.","furigana":"やす|ひ|ちか|こうえん|すいさいが|か"},{"japanese":"美術館で、明治時代の水彩画の展覧会が開かれている。","romaji":"Bijutsukan de, Meiji jidai no suisaiga no tenrankai ga hirakarete iru.","german":"An exhibition of Meiji-era watercolors is being held at the art museum.","furigana":"びじゅつかん|めいじじだい|すいさいが|てんらんかい|ひら"}],"notes":"水彩画 is a \"watercolor\", a picture painted with watercolors; the verb is 描く (かく or えがく): 水彩画を描く. 水彩 alone denotes the technique or medium (水彩絵の具 \"watercolor paints\"), a 水彩画家 is a watercolor painter. The counterpart is 油絵 or, more formally, 油彩画 \"oil painting\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"油彩画のコツをつかんできました。","romaji":"Yusaiga no kotsu o tsukande kimashita.","german":"I've gotten the knack of oil painting.","furigana":"ゆさいが"},{"japanese":"この作品は、キャンバスに油彩で描かれている。","romaji":"Kono sakuhin wa, kyanbasu ni yusai de egakarete iru.","german":"This work is painted in oil on canvas.","furigana":"さくひん|ゆさい|えが"},{"japanese":"美術大学では、一年目に水彩と油彩の両方を学びます。","romaji":"Bijutsu daigaku de wa, ichinenme ni suisai to yusai no ryōhō o manabimasu.","german":"At art university, you study both watercolor and oil in the first year.","furigana":"びじゅつだいがく|いちねんめ|すいさい|ゆさい|りょうほう|まな"}],"notes":"油彩 is the art-technical term for \"oil painting\" or oil paint as a technique; a single picture is a 油彩画. In daily life one says 油絵 far more often; the paints are 油絵の具. In catalogs and picture descriptions, キャンバスに油彩 \"oil on canvas\" typically appears. Counterpart: 水彩 \"watercolor\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人々は、その試合は八百長だったのではないかと憶測している。","romaji":"Hitobito wa, sono shiai wa yaochō datta no de wa nai ka to okusoku shite iru.","german":"People speculate that the match was fixed.","furigana":"ひとびと|しあい||おくそく"},{"japanese":"憶測で物を言うのはやめてください。","romaji":"Okusoku de mono o iu no wa yamete kudasai.","german":"Please stop speaking based on speculation.","furigana":"おくそく|もの|い"},{"japanese":"社長の突然の辞任は、さまざまな憶測を呼んだ。","romaji":"Shachō no totsuzen no jinin wa, samazama na okusoku o yonda.","german":"The president's sudden resignation gave rise to various speculations.","furigana":"しゃちょう|とつぜん|じにん|おくそく|よ"}],"notes":"憶測 (also 臆測) is a \"conjecture, speculation\" without secure basis and often sounds critical. Typical: 憶測を呼ぶ \"to give rise to speculation\", 憶測が飛び交う \"speculation is flying around\", 憶測で物を言う \"to assert things at random\"; as a verb 〜と憶測する. By contrast, 推測 is a reasoned \"inference\", 予想 a \"forecast\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"水溶性のビタミンCはすぐに体内から排出されるので、私達の体に蓄えることはできないと読みましたよ。","romaji":"Suiyōsei no bitamin C wa sugu ni tainai kara haishutsu sareru node, watashitachi no karada ni takuwaeru koto wa dekinai to yomimashita yo.","german":"I read that water-soluble vitamin C is quickly excreted from the body, so our bodies can't store it.","furigana":"すいようせい|たいない|はいしゅつ|わたしたち|からだ|たくわ|よ"},{"japanese":"ビタミンAは脂溶性だが、ビタミンBは水溶性だ。","romaji":"Bitamin A wa shiyōsei da ga, bitamin B wa suiyōsei da.","german":"Vitamin A is fat-soluble, but vitamin B is water-soluble.","furigana":"しようせい|すいようせい"},{"japanese":"このペンは水溶性のインクなので、水で簡単に落とせます。","romaji":"Kono pen wa suiyōsei no inku na node, mizu de kantan ni otosemasu.","german":"This pen uses water-soluble ink, so it comes off easily with water.","furigana":"すいようせい|みず|かんたん|お"}],"notes":"水溶性 means \"water-soluble\" and is a technical word from chemistry, nutrition and product descriptions. It takes の before nouns (水溶性のビタミン) or stands directly in a compound: 水溶性食物繊維, 水溶性塗料. Opposites are 脂溶性 \"fat-soluble\" and 不溶性 \"insoluble\". The suffix 〜性 forms property words like 安全性 or 可能性.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この消防士さんの人形は、実際に耐火性の服と手袋を身につけています。","romaji":"Kono shōbōshisan no ningyō wa, jissai ni taikasei no fuku to tebukuro o mi ni tsukete imasu.","german":"This firefighter doll actually wears fire-resistant clothes and gloves.","furigana":"しょうぼうし|にんぎょう|じっさい|たいかせい|ふく|てぶくろ|み"},{"japanese":"大切な書類は耐火金庫に保管しています。","romaji":"Taisetsu na shorui wa taika kinko ni hokan shite imasu.","german":"I keep important documents in a fireproof safe.","furigana":"たいせつ|しょるい|たいかきんこ|ほかん"},{"japanese":"この建物は耐火構造なので、火事になっても燃え広がりにくい。","romaji":"Kono tatemono wa taika kōzō na node, kaji ni natte mo moehirogarinikui.","german":"This building has a fire-resistant structure, so fire is unlikely to spread even if one breaks out.","furigana":"たてもの|たいかこうぞう|かじ|も|ひろ"}],"notes":"耐火 means \"fireproof, fire-resistant\" and almost always stands as the first element of compounds or with の: 耐火性 \"fire resistance\", 耐火金庫, 耐火構造, 耐火レンガ. To distinguish: 防火 \"fire prevention\" (防火扉), 耐熱 \"heat-resistant\" (e.g. tableware), 不燃 \"non-combustible\". The word is technical and matter-of-fact.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"海の中の魚達は、みんな太鼓の音に合わせて一斉に踊り出しました。","romaji":"Umi no naka no sakanatachi wa, minna taiko no oto ni awasete issei ni odori dashimashita.","german":"All the fish in the sea started dancing at once to the sound of the drum.","furigana":"うみ|なか|さかなたち|たいこ|おと|あ|いっせい|おど|だ"},{"japanese":"お祭りでみんなと盆踊りを踊りました。","romaji":"Omatsuri de minna to bon'odori o odorimashita.","german":"At the festival, we all danced the Bon dance together.","furigana":"まつ|ぼんおど|おど"},{"japanese":"パーティーで音楽に合わせて朝まで踊った。","romaji":"Pātī de ongaku ni awasete asa made odotta.","german":"At the party, we danced to the music until morning.","furigana":"おんがく|あ|あさ|おど"}],"notes":"踊る means \"to dance\" and is intransitive; however, the dance can appear as an object with を: 盆踊りを踊る, ワルツを踊る. To dance to music is 音楽に合わせて踊る. The noun 踊り tends to mean traditional dance, ダンス modern dance; ダンスをする is a synonym. The identically read 躍る appears in 胸が躍る \"one's heart leaps with joy\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 9"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"クリスなら、さっき階段の踊り場ですれ違ったよ。","romaji":"Kurisu nara, sakki kaidan no odoriba de surechigatta yo.","german":"Chris? I passed him on the stair landing just now.","furigana":"かいだん|おど|ば|ちが"},{"japanese":"階段の踊り場に植木鉢が置いてある。","romaji":"Kaidan no odoriba ni uekibachi ga oite aru.","german":"A flowerpot is placed on the stair landing.","furigana":"かいだん|おど|ば|うえきばち|お"},{"japanese":"景気はここ数か月、踊り場にあると言われている。","romaji":"Keiki wa koko sūkagetsu, odoriba ni aru to iwarete iru.","german":"The economy is said to have been in a plateau for the past few months.","furigana":"けいき|すう|げつ|おど|ば|い"}],"notes":"踊り場 is mainly the \"stair landing\" today: 階段の踊り場で. The literal meaning \"dance floor\" is obsolete (one says ダンスフロア for that). In economic news, 踊り場 figuratively denotes a \"phase of stagnation\" after an upswing: 景気が踊り場にある \"the economy is treading water\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"僕はあまり賢くはないけど、やるときはちゃんとやってみせるよ。","romaji":"Boku wa amari kashikoku wa nai kedo, yaru toki wa chanto yatte miseru yo.","german":"I'm not especially smart, but when it counts, I'll show you I can do it properly.","furigana":"ぼく|かしこ"},{"japanese":"うちの犬はとても賢くて、言葉をたくさん覚えている。","romaji":"Uchi no inu wa totemo kashikokute, kotoba o takusan oboete iru.","german":"Our dog is very clever and has learned a lot of words.","furigana":"いぬ|かしこ|ことば|おぼ"},{"japanese":"セールの時にまとめて買うのが賢い買い方だ。","romaji":"Sēru no toki ni matomete kau no ga kashikoi kaikata da.","german":"Buying in bulk during a sale is the smart way to shop.","furigana":"とき|か|かしこ|か|かた"}],"notes":"賢い means \"clever, bright, wise\" and often praises practical cleverness as well: 賢い選択, 賢い買い物. For animals and children it also means \"well-behaved, good\" (especially Kansai: 賢くしてなさい). 頭がいい is the most neutral word for intelligence, 利口 is similar (お利口さん \"good child\"), while ずる賢い means \"cunning\". It conjugates as an い-adjective: 賢くない, 賢かった.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"古代ギリシャの賢人たちは、人間の生き方について深く考えた。","romaji":"Kodai Girisha no kenjin-tachi wa, ningen no ikikata ni tsuite fukaku kangaeta.","german":"The sages of ancient Greece thought deeply about how people should live.","furigana":"こだい|けんじん|にんげん|い|かた|ふか|かんが"},{"japanese":"「急がば回れ」という言葉には、昔の賢人の知恵が詰まっている。","romaji":"Isogaba maware to iu kotoba ni wa, mukashi no kenjin no chie ga tsumatte iru.","german":"The saying 「急がば回れ」 is packed with the wisdom of sages of old.","furigana":"いそ|まわ|ことば|むかし|けんじん|ちえ|つ"},{"japanese":"世界の問題について話し合うため、各国から「賢人」と呼ばれる専門家が集められた。","romaji":"Sekai no mondai ni tsuite hanashiau tame, kakkoku kara kenjin to yobareru senmonka ga atsumerareta.","german":"Experts called \"sages\" were gathered from each country to discuss the world's problems.","furigana":"せかい|もんだい|はな|あ|かっこく|けんじん|よ|せんもんか|あつ"}],"notes":"賢人 is a \"sage\", a person of great wisdom and virtue. The word is elevated and appears mainly in history, philosophy and fixed expressions like 古代ギリシャの賢人, 七賢人 or 賢人会議 \"council of sages\". In daily life one rather says 賢い人 or 知恵のある人; 賢者 means the same but appears mainly in fantasy and games.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"我が校は百年の光輝ある歴史を持っている。","romaji":"Waga kō wa hyakunen no kōki aru rekishi o motte iru.","german":"Our school has a history of a hundred glorious years.","furigana":"わ|こう|ひゃくねん|こうき|れきし|も"},{"japanese":"夜空の星が、静かな光輝を放っていた。","romaji":"Yozora no hoshi ga, shizuka na kōki o hanatte ita.","german":"The stars in the night sky gave off a quiet brilliance.","furigana":"よぞら|ほし|しず|こうき|はな"},{"japanese":"チームの光輝ある伝統を守るため、選手たちは必死に戦った。","romaji":"Chīmu no kōki aru dentō o mamoru tame, senshu-tachi wa hisshi ni tatakatta.","german":"To uphold the team's glorious tradition, the players fought desperately.","furigana":"こうき|でんとう|まも|せんしゅ|ひっし|たたか"}],"notes":"光輝 is a very elevated, written-language word for \"brilliance, radiance\" and, figuratively, \"glory\". It appears almost only in set phrases: 光輝ある歴史 \"glorious history\", 光輝ある伝統, 光輝を放つ \"to radiate brilliance\". In daily life one says 輝き or 光. Do not confuse with the verb 光り輝く \"to shine brightly\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"例えば、太陽の輝度値はとても高いです。","romaji":"Tatoeba, taiyou no kidochi wa totemo takai desu.","german":"For example, the luminance value of the sun is very high.","furigana":"たと|たいよう|きどち|たか"},{"japanese":"画面の輝度を下げると、バッテリーが長持ちします。","romaji":"Gamen no kido o sageru to, batterī ga nagamochi shimasu.","german":"Lowering the screen brightness makes the battery last longer.","furigana":"がめん|きど|さ|ながも"},{"japanese":"このモニターは高輝度なので、明るい部屋でも見やすい。","romaji":"Kono monitā wa kōkido na node, akarui heya demo miyasui.","german":"This monitor has high brightness, so it is easy to see even in a bright room.","furigana":"こうきど|あか|へや|み"}],"notes":"輝度 is a technical term from physics and engineering: \"luminance\", i.e. how brightly a surface or light source shines. In daily life it is mostly encountered with screens: 画面の輝度を上げる／下げる, 高輝度LED. Colloquially one says 明るさ. To distinguish: 照度 \"illuminance\" (light falling on a surface) and 明度 \"lightness of a color\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この国では大麻の使用は禁止されています。","romaji":"Kono kuni de wa taima no shiyou wa kinshi sarete imasu.","german":"The use of cannabis is prohibited in this country.","furigana":"くに|たいま|しよう|きんし"},{"japanese":"有名な俳優が、大麻を所持していた疑いで逮捕された。","romaji":"Yūmei na haiyū ga, taima o shoji shite ita utagai de taiho sareta.","german":"A famous actor was arrested on suspicion of possessing cannabis.","furigana":"ゆうめい|はいゆう|たいま|しょじ|うたが|たいほ"},{"japanese":"昔の日本では、大麻の繊維から布や縄を作っていた。","romaji":"Mukashi no Nihon de wa, taima no sen'i kara nuno ya nawa o tsukutte ita.","german":"In old Japan, cloth and rope were made from cannabis fibers.","furigana":"むかし|にほん|たいま|せんい|ぬの|なわ|つく"}],"notes":"大麻 denotes both the plant \"hemp\" (fibers for ropes and fabrics) and the drug \"cannabis, marijuana\". In Japan, possession is strictly prohibited, so the word often appears in the news: 大麻を所持する, 大麻取締法. Colloquially also マリファナ. The hemp fiber is technically also called 麻; the meaning \"Shinto offering\" is rare.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その部屋には、小さなロウソクの灯りだけが灯っていました。","romaji":"Sono heya ni wa, chiisana rōsoku no akari dake ga tomotte imashita.","german":"The room was lit only by the light of a small candle.","furigana":"へや|ちい|あか|とも"},{"japanese":"寝る前に、廊下の灯りを消してね。","romaji":"Neru mae ni, rōka no akari o keshite ne.","german":"Please turn off the hallway light before you go to bed.","furigana":"ね|まえ|ろうか|あか|け"},{"japanese":"丘の上から、遠くの町の灯りが美しく見えた。","romaji":"Oka no ue kara, tōku no machi no akari ga utsukushiku mieta.","german":"From the hilltop, the lights of the distant town looked beautiful.","furigana":"おか|うえ|とお|まち|あか|うつく|み"}],"notes":"灯り (also written 明かり) is the \"light\" of a lamp or candle, that is, a light source that you turn on: 灯りをつける／消す, 灯りがともる, 町の灯り \"the lights of the town\". The spelling 灯り emphasizes the lamp, 明かり rather the brightness (月明かり \"moonlight\"). 光 is \"light\" in the physical sense in general; in everyday life, for room lighting one often simply says 電気 (電気を消す).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大量の蛾が、各街灯の周りを飛び交っています。","romaji":"Tairyō no ga ga, kaku gaitō no mawari o tobikatte imasu.","german":"Large numbers of moths are flying around each streetlight.","furigana":"たいりょう||かくがいとう|まわ|と|か"},{"japanese":"この道は街灯が少なくて、夜は一人で歩くのが怖い。","romaji":"Kono michi wa gaitō ga sukunakute, yoru wa hitori de aruku no ga kowai.","german":"This road has few streetlights, and it's scary to walk alone at night.","furigana":"みち|がいとう|すく|よる|ひとり|ある|こわ"},{"japanese":"日が暮れると、通りの街灯が一斉についた。","romaji":"Hi ga kureru to, tōri no gaitō ga issei ni tsuita.","german":"When dusk fell, the streetlights along the street came on all at once.","furigana":"ひ|く|とお|がいとう|いっせい"}],"notes":"街灯 is the \"streetlight\" along public roads: 街灯がつく／ともる, 街灯の下で, 街灯が少ない道. Pronounced the same but meant differently is 外灯 \"outdoor light\" on a house or building. 電灯 is generally \"electric light\", 信号 the \"traffic light\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"うちの嫁さんは全然電灯を消さないんだよね。きっとスイッチがあることを知らないんだと思うよ。","romaji":"Uchi no yomesan wa zenzen dentō o kesanai n da yo ne. Kitto suitchi ga aru koto o shiranai n da to omou yo.","german":"My wife never turns off the lights. I bet she doesn't even know there's a switch.","furigana":"よめ|ぜんぜんでんとう|け|し|おも"},{"japanese":"停電で電灯が全部消えました。","romaji":"Teiden de dentō ga zenbu kiemashita.","german":"All the lights went out because of the power outage.","furigana":"ていでん|でんとう|ぜんぶき"},{"japanese":"地震のときのために、懐中電灯を買っておきました。","romaji":"Jishin no toki no tame ni, kaichū dentō o katte okimashita.","german":"I bought a flashlight in advance in case of an earthquake.","furigana":"じしん|かいちゅうでんとう|か"}],"notes":"電灯 is the electric light or lamp; the word sounds somewhat matter-of-fact to old-fashioned. In everyday speech one mostly says 電気: 電気をつける/消す, or 明かり, ライト. The compound 懐中電灯 \"flashlight\" is very common, and a street lamp is called 街灯.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"冬が長引いたため、今年はうちの庭の桜が遅咲きでした。","romaji":"Fuyu ga nagabiita tame, kotoshi wa uchi no niwa no sakura ga osozaki deshita.","german":"Because winter dragged on, the cherry tree in our garden bloomed late this year.","furigana":"ふゆ|ながび|ことし|にわ|さくら|おそざ"},{"japanese":"この公園には遅咲きの品種の桜もあるので、四月の終わりまで花見が楽しめる。","romaji":"Kono kōen ni wa osozaki no hinshu no sakura mo aru node, shigatsu no owari made hanami ga tanoshimeru.","german":"This park also has late-blooming cherry varieties, so you can enjoy hanami until the end of April.","furigana":"こうえん|おそざ|ひんしゅ|さくら|しがつ|お|はなみ|たの"},{"japanese":"彼は四十歳でデビューした遅咲きの小説家だ。","romaji":"Kare wa yonjussai de debyū shita osozaki no shōsetsuka da.","german":"He is a late-blooming novelist who debuted at the age of forty.","furigana":"かれ|よんじゅっさい|おそざ|しょうせつか"}],"notes":"遅咲き first means \"blooming late\", mostly with の: 遅咲きの桜, 遅咲きの品種. Figuratively it denotes people who succeed only late: 遅咲きの俳優, 遅咲きの才能 \"late bloomer\". The opposite is 早咲き. For the idea \"great talents mature late\" there is the proverb 大器晩成.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"元チャンピオンは三年ぶりに王座に返り咲いた。","romaji":"Moto chanpion wa sannen-buri ni ōza ni kaerizaita.","german":"The former champion returned to the throne after three years.","furigana":"もと|さんねん|おうざ|かえ|ざ"},{"japanese":"前回の選挙で落選した議員の返り咲きが決まった。","romaji":"Zenkai no senkyo de rakusen shita giin no kaerizaki ga kimatta.","german":"The return of the lawmaker who lost in the last election has been decided.","furigana":"ぜんかい|せんきょ|らくせん|ぎいん|かえ|ざ|き"},{"japanese":"暖かい日が続いて、秋にツツジの返り咲きが見られた。","romaji":"Atatakai hi ga tsuzuite, aki ni tsutsuji no kaerizaki ga mirareta.","german":"Warm days continued, and azaleas were seen blooming again in autumn.","furigana":"あたた|ひ|つづ|あき|かえ|ざ|み"}],"notes":"返り咲き is the noun of the verb 返り咲く and means a \"return\" to a former top position: 王座に返り咲く, 首位に返り咲く, 政権に返り咲く. It presupposes that one held that position before; a comeback in a game is 逆転. カムバック and 復帰 (\"return after a break\") are similar. Literally, 返り咲き also denotes the \"second blooming\" of a plant in the same season.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いてぇ。どこに目ぇつけて歩いてるんだ！脇見しながら歩いてんじゃねぇぞ！","romaji":"Itē. Doko ni mē tsukete aruiteru n da! Wakimi shinagara aruiten ja nē zo!","german":"Ouch. Where are you looking? Don't walk while looking away!","furigana":"め|ある|わきみ|ある"},{"japanese":"事故の原因は運転手の脇見運転だった。","romaji":"Jiko no gen'in wa untenshu no wakimi unten datta.","german":"The cause of the accident was the driver's inattention.","furigana":"じこ|げんいん|うんてんしゅ|わきみうんてん"},{"japanese":"ちょっと脇見をしている間に、子どもが見えなくなった。","romaji":"Chotto wakimi o shite iru aida ni, kodomo ga mienaku natta.","german":"While I looked away for a moment, I lost sight of the child.","furigana":"わきみ|あいだ|こ|み"}],"notes":"脇見 means \"to look to the side, to avert one's gaze\", mostly in the sense of inattention. The most important expression is 脇見運転 \"distracted driving\", a frequent accident cause in police reports. As an intransitive する-verb: 脇見しながら歩く. Equivalent in meaning, but more everyday and common especially toward children, is よそ見.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女、オーディションに受かったって言いふらしてるけど、ただの脇役みたいだよ。","romaji":"Kanojo, ōdishon ni ukatta tte iifurashiteru kedo, tada no wakiyaku mitai da yo.","german":"She keeps telling everyone she passed the audition, but it seems to be just a supporting role.","furigana":"かのじょ|う|い|わきやく"},{"japanese":"あの俳優は主役より脇役のほうが光っている。","romaji":"Ano haiyū wa shuyaku yori wakiyaku no hō ga hikatte iru.","german":"That actor shines more in supporting roles than in leads.","furigana":"はいゆう|しゅやく|わきやく|ひか"},{"japanese":"今回のプロジェクトでは、私は脇役に徹して若手を支えるつもりだ。","romaji":"Konkai no purojekuto de wa, watashi wa wakiyaku ni tesshite wakate o sasaeru tsumori da.","german":"In this project, I intend to stick to a supporting role and back up the younger staff.","furigana":"こんかい|わたし|わきやく|てっ|わかて|ささ"}],"notes":"脇役 is the \"supporting role\" in film, theater or drama; the opposite is 主役 \"lead role\". One says 脇役を演じる, 脇役を務める. A very small part is 端役, extras are エキストラ. Figuratively, 脇役に徹する means \"to deliberately stay in the background and support others\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"男は殺人未遂の疑いで逮捕された。","romaji":"Otoko wa satsujin misui no utagai de taiho sareta.","german":"The man was arrested on suspicion of attempted murder.","furigana":"おとこ|さつじんみすい|うたが|たいほ"},{"japanese":"銀行強盗は、警備員に気づかれて未遂に終わった。","romaji":"Ginkō gōtō wa, keibiin ni kizukarete misui ni owatta.","german":"The bank robbery ended in failure after the robber was noticed by a security guard.","furigana":"ぎんこうごうとう|けいびいん|き|みすい|お"},{"japanese":"彼女は若い頃に自殺未遂をしたことがあると、インタビューで語った。","romaji":"Kanojo wa wakai koro ni jisatsu misui o shita koto ga aru to, intabyū de katatta.","german":"In an interview, she said she had attempted suicide when she was young.","furigana":"かのじょ|わか|ころ|じさつみすい|かた"}],"notes":"未遂 denotes an \"attempted, uncompleted act\", mostly a crime or suicide. It often appears as a suffix: 殺人未遂, 強盗未遂, 自殺未遂; the set phrase 未遂に終わる means \"to remain at the attempt stage, to fail\". In law the opposite is 既遂 \"completed\". For a general \"attempt\" in daily life, one says 試み or やってみること, not 未遂.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は社交的でみんなから好かれているので、班長に選ばれました。","romaji":"Kare wa shakouteki de minna kara sukarete iru node, hanchou ni erabaremashita.","german":"He is sociable and liked by everyone, so he was chosen as group leader.","furigana":"かれ|しゃこうてき|す|はんちょう|えら"},{"japanese":"小学校では、給食の班ごとに班長を決めていた。","romaji":"Shōgakkō de wa, kyūshoku no han goto ni hanchō o kimete ita.","german":"In elementary school, we chose a group leader for each school lunch group.","furigana":"しょうがっこう|きゅうしょく|はん|はんちょう|き"},{"japanese":"工場の班長として、十人の作業員をまとめています。","romaji":"Kōjō no hanchō to shite, jūnin no sagyōin o matomete imasu.","german":"As a factory team leader, I lead ten workers.","furigana":"こうじょう|はんちょう|じゅうにん|さぎょういん"}],"notes":"班長 is the \"leader\" of a 班, a small group: in Japanese schools the class groups (cleaning, lunch or project groups), in factories a work group, in the military or police a squad. One says 班長に選ばれる, 班長を務める. In offices, titles like 係長 or 主任 correspond; generally and in modern usage one often says リーダー.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"昨日は一日中石膏を乾式工法の壁に塗布していました。","romaji":"Kinō wa ichinichichū sekkō o kanshiki kōhō no kabe ni tofu shite imashita.","german":"Yesterday I spent the whole day applying plaster to the dry-construction wall.","furigana":"きのう||かんしきこうほう|かべ|とふ"},{"japanese":"一日数回、適量を患部に塗布してください。","romaji":"Ichinichi sūkai, tekiryō o kanbu ni tofu shite kudasai.","german":"Apply an appropriate amount to the affected area several times a day.","furigana":"いちにちすうかい|てきりょう|かんぶ|とふ"},{"japanese":"錆を防ぐため、部品の表面に特殊な油を塗布する。","romaji":"Sabi o fusegu tame, buhin no hyōmen ni tokushu na abura o tofu suru.","german":"To prevent rust, a special oil is applied to the surface of the parts.","furigana":"さび|ふせ|ぶひん|ひょうめん|とくしゅ|あぶら|とふ"}],"notes":"塗布 is a technical する-verb for \"applying\" an ointment, liquid or coating: 患部に塗布する, 表面に塗布する. It is found on package inserts, in instructions for use and in engineering (塗布剤). In daily life one simply says 塗る (薬を塗る, ペンキを塗る). Construction: 物を場所に塗布する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"遅くなって、本当にすみません。トイレの壁の塗装が思ったよりも長引いてしまって。","romaji":"Osoku natte, hontōni sumimasen. Toire no kabe no tosō ga omotta yori mo nagabiite shimatte.","german":"I'm really sorry for being late. The painting of the bathroom wall took longer than expected.","furigana":"おそ|ほんとう|かべ|とそう|おも|ながび"},{"japanese":"車の塗装が、ところどころはげてきた。","romaji":"Kuruma no tosō ga, tokorodokoro hagete kita.","german":"The paint on the car has started to peel in places.","furigana":"くるま|とそう"},{"japanese":"来月、マンションの外壁を塗装する工事が始まります。","romaji":"Raigetsu, manshon no gaiheki o tosō suru kōji ga hajimarimasu.","german":"Next month, work to paint the exterior wall of the apartment building will begin.","furigana":"らいげつ|がいへき|とそう|こうじ|はじ"}],"notes":"塗装 is the \"painting, lacquering\" of surfaces such as walls, cars or buildings, and also the resulting layer of paint: 塗装がはげる \"the paint peels off\", 塗装工事, 再塗装. As a transitive する-verb: 壁を塗装する. It has nothing to do with painting in the artistic sense (絵を描く). In daily life one also says ペンキを塗る.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"刷毛に蛍光塗料を付けました。","romaji":"Hake ni keikō toryō o tsukemashita.","german":"I dipped the brush in fluorescent paint.","furigana":"|けいこうとりょう|つ"},{"japanese":"この塗料は水性なので、においが少なくて室内でも使いやすい。","romaji":"Kono toryō wa suisei na node, nioi ga sukunakute shitsunai demo tsukaiyasui.","german":"This paint is water-based, so it has little odor and is easy to use indoors.","furigana":"とりょう|すいせい|すく|しつない|つか"},{"japanese":"塗料が乾くまで、ドアに触らないでください。","romaji":"Toryō ga kawaku made, doa ni sawaranaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't touch the door until the paint dries.","furigana":"とりょう|かわ|さわ"}],"notes":"塗料 is the umbrella term for \"paint, lacquer, coating material\", i.e. paint for coating surfaces: 水性塗料, 油性塗料, 蛍光塗料, 塗料を塗る. In daily life, wall paint is often called ペンキ; paints for pictures are 絵の具. Color as a hue is 色.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"水平な横線を引くように言ったのに、ちょっと斜めになっています。","romaji":"Suihei na yokosen o hiku you ni itta noni, chotto naname ni natte imasu.","german":"I told you to draw a horizontal line, but it's a little slanted.","furigana":"すいへい|よこせん|ひ|い|なな"},{"japanese":"野菜は斜めに切ると、火が通りやすくなる。","romaji":"Yasai wa naname ni kiru to, hi ga tōriyasuku naru.","german":"If you cut vegetables diagonally, the heat penetrates more easily.","furigana":"やさい|なな|き|ひ|とお"},{"japanese":"駅は、この交差点の斜め向かいにあります。","romaji":"Eki wa, kono kōsaten no naname mukai ni arimasu.","german":"The station is diagonally across from this intersection.","furigana":"えき|こうさてん|なな|む"}],"notes":"斜め means \"slanted, diagonal, oblique\" and is used as a noun, with の, or as an adverb with に: 斜めになる, 斜めに切る, 斜めの線. Location expressions: 斜め前, 斜め後ろ, 斜め向かい \"diagonally opposite\". Figuratively: ご機嫌斜め \"in a bad mood\", 斜めに構える \"to take a cynical attitude\", 斜め読み \"skimming\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"うちの私道は急斜面になってるので、上まで上がってくるにはスノータイヤがいります。","romaji":"Uchi no shidou wa kyuushamen ni natteru node, ue made agatte kuru ni wa sunootaiya ga irimasu.","german":"Our private road is a steep slope, so you need snow tires to get up to the top.","furigana":"しどう|きゅうしゃめん|うえ|あ"},{"japanese":"山の斜面一面に、茶畑が広がっている。","romaji":"Yama no shamen ichimen ni, chabatake ga hirogatte iru.","german":"Tea fields spread across the entire mountain slope.","furigana":"やま|しゃめんいちめん|ちゃばたけ|ひろ"},{"japanese":"大雨のあと、斜面が崩れて道路がふさがった。","romaji":"Ōame no ato, shamen ga kuzurete dōro ga fusagatta.","german":"After the heavy rain, the slope collapsed and blocked the road.","furigana":"おおあめ|しゃめん|くず|どうろ"}],"notes":"斜面 is a \"slanting surface, slope\": 山の斜面, 急な斜面 (急斜面), 斜面を登る, 斜面が崩れる \"a slope gives way\". It describes the surface itself in a rather factual, geographical way. For a rising road or path, one says 坂 or 坂道; 傾斜 is the \"incline\", i.e. the degree of slant.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どうしてこの単語の文字のフォントを斜体に変えたんですか。","romaji":"Doushite kono tango no moji no fonto o shatai ni kaeta n desu ka.","german":"Why did you change the font of this word to italics?","furigana":"たんご|もじ|しゃたい|か"},{"japanese":"本のタイトルは斜体で書くのが一般的です。","romaji":"Hon no taitoru wa shatai de kaku no ga ippanteki desu.","german":"Book titles are generally written in italics.","furigana":"ほん|しゃたい|か|いっぱんてき"},{"japanese":"強調したい部分は、太字か斜体にしてください。","romaji":"Kyōchō shitai bubun wa, futoji ka shatai ni shite kudasai.","german":"Please make the parts you want to emphasize bold or italic.","furigana":"きょうちょう|ぶぶん|ふとじ|しゃたい"}],"notes":"斜体 is \"italic type\" in typography: 斜体にする, 斜体で書く, 斜体の文字. Equivalent in meaning and very common in computer use is イタリック（体）. Related terms: 太字 \"bold type\", 下線 \"underline\". In mathematics, 斜体 also denotes a \"skew field\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ゴングが鳴った瞬間、そのボクサーは相手の顎を思い切り殴り付けた。","romaji":"Gongu ga natta shunkan, sono bokusā wa aite no ago o omoikiri naguritsuketa.","german":"The moment the gong rang, the boxer punched his opponent in the jaw with all his might.","furigana":"な|しゅんかん|あいて|あご|おも|き|なぐ|つ"},{"japanese":"酔っ払いに突然殴られて、けがをした。","romaji":"Yopparai ni totsuzen nagurarete, kega o shita.","german":"I was suddenly hit by a drunk and got injured.","furigana":"よ|ぱら|とつぜんなぐ"},{"japanese":"二人は口論の末、殴り合いのけんかになった。","romaji":"Futari wa kōron no sue, naguriai no kenka ni natta.","german":"After an argument, the two ended up in a fistfight.","furigana":"ふたり|こうろん|すえ|なぐ|あ"}],"notes":"殴る means \"to hit (hard), to beat up\", usually with a fist or an object: 人を殴る, 顔を殴る; passive 殴られる. Compounds: 殴り合い \"brawl\", 殴りかかる \"to lash out at someone\", 殴り付ける \"to strike violently\". 叩く is more neutral (\"to knock, to hit, to clap\", also drumming), ぶつ is colloquial, and 打つ is more \"to hit\" (a ball, a nail).","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 21"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"突然、背後から知らない男に後頭部を殴打されました。","romaji":"Totsuzen, haigo kara shiranai otoko ni koutoubu o ouda saremashita.","german":"Suddenly, an unknown man struck me on the back of the head from behind.","furigana":"とつぜん|はいご|し|おとこ|こうとうぶ|おうだ"},{"japanese":"男は、店員を棒で殴打した疑いで逮捕された。","romaji":"Otoko wa, ten'in o bō de ōda shita utagai de taiho sareta.","german":"The man was arrested on suspicion of beating a store clerk with a stick.","furigana":"おとこ|てんいん|ぼう|おうだ|うたが|たいほ"},{"japanese":"被害者の頭部には、強く殴打された跡が残っていた。","romaji":"Higaisha no tōbu ni wa, tsuyoku ōda sareta ato ga nokotte ita.","german":"Marks of a heavy blow remained on the victim's head.","furigana":"ひがいしゃ|とうぶ|つよ|おうだ|あと|のこ"}],"notes":"殴打 is the formal, legal-factual word for \"hitting, beating\" and appears mainly in news, police reports and court documents: 頭部を殴打する, 殴打される, 殴打事件. In conversation one uses 殴る instead. As a transitive する-verb: 人を棒で殴打する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は友人の後ろ盾に勇気づけられました。","romaji":"Watashi wa yuujin no ushirodate ni yuukizukeraremashita.","german":"I was encouraged by my friend's support.","furigana":"わたし|ゆうじん|うし|だて|ゆうき"},{"japanese":"彼が若くして社長になれたのは、有力な後ろ盾があったからだ。","romaji":"Kare ga wakakushite shachō ni nareta no wa, yūryoku na ushirodate ga atta kara da.","german":"He was able to become president at a young age because he had a powerful backer.","furigana":"かれ|わか|しゃちょう|ゆうりょく|うし|だて"},{"japanese":"その小国は、大国を後ろ盾にして強気な外交を続けている。","romaji":"Sono shōkoku wa, taikoku o ushirodate ni shite tsuyoki na gaikō o tsuzukete iru.","german":"That small country continues its assertive diplomacy with a major power as its backer.","furigana":"しょうこく|たいこく|うし|だて|つよき|がいこう|つづ"}],"notes":"後ろ盾 is literally the \"shield at one's back\" and denotes an influential \"supporter, backing, patron\": 〜の後ろ盾がある, 〜を後ろ盾にする, 〜の後ろ盾となる. It is mostly about power, money or influence (politics, companies, diplomacy). Emotional support in daily life is rather 支え or 応援; 味方 is an \"ally\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コップに水を入れすぎて、テーブルに零れてしまった。","romaji":"Koppu ni mizu o iresugite, tēburu ni koborete shimatta.","german":"I put too much water in the glass, and it spilled onto the table.","furigana":"みず|い|こぼ"},{"japanese":"映画の最後のシーンで、思わず涙がこぼれた。","romaji":"Eiga no saigo no shīn de, omowazu namida ga koboreta.","german":"In the last scene of the movie, tears spilled out involuntarily.","furigana":"えいが|さいご|おも|なみだ"},{"japanese":"孫の写真を見て、祖母の顔に笑みがこぼれた。","romaji":"Mago no shashin o mite, sobo no kao ni emi ga koboreta.","german":"Seeing a photo of her grandchild, a smile spread across the grandmother's face.","furigana":"まご|しゃしん|み|そぼ|かお|え"}],"notes":"零れる is mostly written in kana (こぼれる) and is intransitive: liquid or grains \"overflow, fall out, are spilled\": 水がこぼれる, ご飯がこぼれる. The transitive counterpart is こぼす \"to spill\" (お茶をこぼす), which can also mean \"to complain, to grumble\" (愚痴をこぼす). Figuratively: 涙がこぼれる \"tears well up\", 笑みがこぼれる \"a smile crosses one's face\", 光がこぼれる \"light filters through\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あ、ホッチキスの針、切れちゃった。","romaji":"A, hotchikisu no hari, kirechatta.","german":"Oh, I'm out of staples.","furigana":"はり|き"},{"japanese":"会議の資料は、ホッチキスで左上を留めてください。","romaji":"Kaigi no shiryō wa, hotchikisu de hidariue o tomete kudasai.","german":"Please staple the meeting materials at the top left.","furigana":"かいぎ|しりょう|ひだりうえ|と"},{"japanese":"書類の束が厚すぎて、ホッチキスが通らなかった。","romaji":"Shorui no taba ga atsusugite, hotchikisu ga tōranakatta.","german":"The stack of documents was too thick, and the stapler wouldn't go through.","furigana":"しょるい|たば|あつ|とお"}],"notes":"ホッチキス (also ホチキス) is the common name for the \"stapler\" and goes back to a brand name; commercially it is officially called ステープラー. The staples are called ホッチキスの針 or 芯. Typical: ホッチキスで留める \"to staple together\", 針が切れる \"to run out of staples\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 7 (source: N5; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その時からどれぐらい貨幣価値が変わったか、知っているかい？","romaji":"Sono toki kara dore gurai kahei kachi ga kawatta ka, shitte iru kai?","german":"Do you know how much the value of money has changed since then?","furigana":"とき|かへいかち|か|し"},{"japanese":"江戸時代の一両は、今の貨幣価値で約十万円とも言われている。","romaji":"Edo jidai no ichiryō wa, ima no kahei kachi de yaku jūman'en to mo iwarete iru.","german":"One ryo in the Edo period is said to be worth about 100,000 yen in today's money.","furigana":"えどじだい|いちりょう|いま|かへいかち|やくじゅうまんえん|い"},{"japanese":"インフレが進むと、貨幣価値は下がる。","romaji":"Infure ga susumu to, kahei kachi wa sagaru.","german":"As inflation advances, the value of money falls.","furigana":"すす|かへいかち|さ"}],"notes":"貨幣価値 is the \"value of money\" in the sense of the purchasing power of money at a given time. It is typical when comparing prices across eras: 今の貨幣価値で, 当時の貨幣価値に換算すると. In economics: インフレで貨幣価値が下がる. Distinguish it from 為替レート \"exchange rate\" and 物価 \"price level\".","levelNote":"Compound: 貨幣 (N1) + 価値 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"説明書を読んだんですが、あまりに不明瞭だったので、新モデルの実力についてちょっと不安に思っています。","romaji":"Setsumeisho o yonda n desu ga, amarini fumeiryō datta node, shin moderu no jitsuryoku nitsuite chotto fuan ni omotte imasu.","german":"I read the manual, but it was so unclear that I'm a little worried about the new model's real capabilities.","furigana":"せつめいしょ|よ|ふめいりょう|しん|じつりょく|ふあん|おも"},{"japanese":"電話の声が不明瞭で、何を言っているのか聞き取れなかった。","romaji":"Denwa no koe ga fumeiryō de, nani o itte iru no ka kikitorenakatta.","german":"The voice on the phone was indistinct, and I couldn't make out what was being said.","furigana":"でんわ|こえ|ふめいりょう|なに|い|き|と"},{"japanese":"会計に不明瞭な点があるとして、監査が行われた。","romaji":"Kaikei ni fumeiryō na ten ga aru to shite, kansa ga okonawareta.","german":"An audit was carried out on the grounds that there were unclear points in the accounts.","furigana":"かいけい|ふめいりょう|てん|かんさ|おこな"}],"notes":"不明瞭 is a formal な-adjective for \"indistinct, unclear, opaque\": 不明瞭な発音, 説明が不明瞭だ, 会計が不明瞭 \"opaque bookkeeping\", 責任の所在が不明瞭. Opposite: 明瞭. In everyday speech people tend to say はっきりしない or 曖昧; 不明, by contrast, means \"unknown\" (原因不明).","levelNote":"Compound: 不 (N3) + 明瞭 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一生懸命粘り強く努力すればいつかは報われると思っていましたが、それは間違いでした。","romaji":"Isshoukenmei nebarizuyoku doryoku sureba itsuka wa mukuwareru to omotte imashita ga, sore wa machigai deshita.","german":"I used to think that if I worked hard and persistently, I would be rewarded someday, but I was wrong.","furigana":"いっしょうけんめいねば|づよ|どりょく|むく|おも|まちが"},{"japanese":"この米は粘りがあって、とてもおいしい。","romaji":"Kono kome wa nebari ga atte, totemo oishii.","german":"This rice is sticky and very tasty.","furigana":"こめ|ねば"},{"japanese":"負けていたチームが最後に粘りを見せ、同点に追いついた。","romaji":"Makete ita chīmu ga saigo ni nebari o mise, dōten ni oitsuita.","german":"The losing team showed tenacity at the end and caught up to tie the game.","furigana":"ま|さいご|ねば|み|どうてん|お"}],"notes":"粘り has two meanings: concretely \"stickiness, viscosity\" of foods or materials (納豆の粘り, 粘りのある米), and figuratively \"staying power, tenacity\" (粘りを見せる, 最後まで粘りを発揮する). It is the noun of the verb 粘る \"to be sticky; to refuse to give up\". The adjective 粘り強い \"persistent\" is very common. Similar: 根気 \"patience\", 忍耐 \"endurance\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お婆ちゃんに、嘘をついたことで責められた。","romaji":"Obaachan ni, uso o tsuita koto de semerareta.","german":"I was scolded by Grandma for lying.","furigana":"ばあ|うそ|せ"},{"japanese":"夏休みは、田舎のおばあちゃんの家に遊びに行きます。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi wa, inaka no obāchan no ie ni asobi ni ikimasu.","german":"In summer vacation, I'm going to visit my grandma's house in the countryside.","furigana":"なつやす|いなか|いえ|あそ|い"},{"japanese":"電車で、荷物を持ったおばあちゃんに席を譲った。","romaji":"Densha de, nimotsu o motta obāchan ni seki o yuzutta.","german":"On the train, I gave my seat to an old lady carrying baggage.","furigana":"でんしゃ|にもつ|も|せき|ゆず"}],"notes":"おばあちゃん (kanji 婆 rare) is the familiar form of address and term for \"grandma\", but also for older women in general. When talking to outsiders about one's own grandmother, 祖母 is more polite; おばあさん is more neutral. Addressing unfamiliar older women this way can seem condescending. Watch the vowel length: おばさん (short a) is \"aunt, middle-aged woman\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"候補者のコウイチ氏は、大統領候補討論会の前、憎らしいほど落ち着いていました。","romaji":"Kouhosha no Kouichi-shi wa, daitouryou kouho touronkai no mae, nikurashii hodo ochitsuite imashita.","german":"Candidate Koichi was irritatingly calm before the presidential debate.","furigana":"こうほしゃ|し|だいとうりょうこうほとうろんかい|まえ|にく|お|つ"},{"japanese":"弟はいつも憎らしいことばかり言って、私を怒らせる。","romaji":"Otōto wa itsumo nikurashii koto bakari itte, watashi o okoraseru.","german":"My little brother is always saying nasty things and getting on my nerves.","furigana":"おとうと|にく|い|わたし|おこ"},{"japanese":"「もう、憎らしい人ね」と言いながら、彼女は笑っていた。","romaji":"Mō, nikurashii hito ne to iinagara, kanojo wa waratte ita.","german":"\"Honestly, you're impossible,\" she said, laughing.","furigana":"にく|ひと|い|かのじょ|わら"}],"notes":"憎らしい describes a person or thing that provokes annoyance and dislike: \"detestable, hateful, provoking\". It is often weaker than \"hateful\": 憎らしいことを言う \"to say nasty things\", 憎らしいほど上手い \"annoyingly good\". Between close friends it can be teasing and affectionate (憎らしい人). The stronger 憎い expresses real hatred, whereas 心憎い means \"admirably clever\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"てっきり彼らはお互いに憎しみ合っているものだと思っていました。","romaji":"Tekkiri karera wa otagai ni nikushimiatte iru mono da to omotte imashita.","german":"I was sure they hated each other.","furigana":"かれ|たが|にく|あ|おも"},{"japanese":"彼は家族を奪った犯人に、強い憎しみを抱いている。","romaji":"Kare wa kazoku o ubatta hannin ni, tsuyoi nikushimi o idaite iru.","german":"He harbors intense hatred toward the criminal who took away his family.","furigana":"かれ|かぞく|うば|はんにん|つよ|にく|いだ"},{"japanese":"戦争は新たな憎しみを生むだけだ。","romaji":"Sensō wa arata na nikushimi o umu dake da.","german":"War only breeds new hatred.","furigana":"せんそう|あら|にく|う"}],"notes":"憎しみ is the noun of 憎む \"to hate\" and denotes deep \"hatred\": 憎しみを抱く, 憎しみが募る \"hatred grows\", 憎しみの連鎖 \"cycle of hatred\". The compound verb 憎しみ合う means \"to hate each other\". 恨み, by contrast, is a \"grudge\" over wrongs suffered; 嫌い is mere dislike.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"こんなに誰かのことを憎いと思ったのは生まれて初めてです。","romaji":"Konna ni dareka no koto o nikui to omotta no wa umarete hajimete desu.","german":"It is the first time in my life that I have hated someone this much.","furigana":"だれ|にく|おも|う|はじ"},{"japanese":"贈り物に手書きの手紙まで添えるとは、憎い心遣いだ。","romaji":"Okurimono ni tegaki no tegami made soeru to wa, nikui kokorozukai da.","german":"Even adding a handwritten letter to the gift - what an admirably thoughtful gesture!","furigana":"おく|もの|てが|てがみ|そ|にく|こころづか"}],"notes":"憎い describes strong aversion to a person or thing. As an i-adjective it can stand directly before a noun or be completed with と: 憎いと思う. Used approvingly it also means \"admirable\", as if the skill were almost annoyingly good; context decides between praise and dislike. The unusual spelling 悪い with the reading にくい is attested in dictionaries. Ordinary 悪い \"bad\" is read わるい; for this card 憎い is the clear spelling. The suffix にくい \"hard to do\" is a different usage.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コンサートのチケットは抽選で、三回応募してやっと当たった。","romaji":"Konsāto no chiketto wa chūsen de, sankai ōbo shite yatto atatta.","german":"The concert tickets were by lottery, and I finally won after applying three times.","furigana":"ちゅうせん|さんかいおうぼ|あ"},{"japanese":"抽選の結果は、来週メールでお知らせします。","romaji":"Chūsen no kekka wa, raishū mēru de oshirase shimasu.","german":"We will notify you of the drawing results by email next week.","furigana":"ちゅうせん|けっか|らいしゅう|し"},{"japanese":"応募者が多かったため、参加者は抽選で決めることになった。","romaji":"Ōbosha ga ōkatta tame, sankasha wa chūsen de kimeru koto ni natta.","german":"Because there were so many applicants, it was decided that participants would be chosen by lottery.","furigana":"おうぼしゃ|おお|さんかしゃ|ちゅうせん|き"}],"notes":"抽選 is a \"lottery, drawing\"; as a する-verb \"to draw lots\". Typical: 抽選で当たる／外れる, 抽選に当たる, 抽選で決める, 抽選会. In Japan, sought-after concert tickets or apartments are often allocated by 抽選. More everyday are くじ and くじ引き \"drawing lots\"; 宝くじ is the \"lottery\" with purchased tickets.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"結婚の誓いの言葉を述べている時、自分が間違った男の人と結婚しようとしていることは分かっていました。","romaji":"Kekkon no chikai no kotoba o nobete iru toki, jibun ga machigatta otoko no hito to kekkon shiyō to shite iru koto wa wakatte imashita.","german":"While saying my wedding vows, I knew I was about to marry the wrong man.","furigana":"けっこん|ちか|ことば|の|とき|じぶん|まちが|おとこ|ひと|けっこん|わ"},{"japanese":"彼は二度とお酒を飲まないと誓いを立てた。","romaji":"Kare wa nido to osake o nomanai to chikai o tateta.","german":"He swore an oath never to drink alcohol again.","furigana":"かれ|にど|さけ|の|ちか|た"},{"japanese":"一度立てた誓いを簡単に破るような人は信用できない。","romaji":"Ichido tateta chikai o kantan ni yaburu you na hito wa shin'you dekinai.","german":"You cannot trust someone who breaks an oath once sworn so easily.","furigana":"いちどた|ちか|かんたん|やぶ|ひと|しんよう"}],"notes":"誓い is the noun of 誓う \"to swear\" and denotes a solemn oath or vow, often made to oneself, to God, or to a partner. Typical combinations: 誓いを立てる \"to take an oath\", 誓いを守る \"to keep an oath\", 誓いを破る \"to break an oath\", 結婚の誓い \"wedding vow\". An everyday promise is 約束; 誓い sounds much more solemn and serious. The formal, legal pledge is called 誓約.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"こちらが誓約書となっておりまして、こちらとこちらにサインをして頂く必要があります。","romaji":"Kochira ga seiyakusho to natte orimashite, kochira to kochira ni sain o shite itadaku hitsuyō ga arimasu.","german":"This is the written pledge. You need to sign here and here.","furigana":"せいやくしょ|いただ|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"入社の際、会社の情報を外部に漏らさないことを誓約しました。","romaji":"Nyuusha no sai, kaisha no jouhou o gaibu ni morasanai koto o seiyaku shimashita.","german":"When joining the company, I pledged not to leak company information to outsiders.","furigana":"にゅうしゃ|さい|かいしゃ|じょうほう|がいぶ|も|せいやく"},{"japanese":"選手たちは大会のルールを守ることを誓約した。","romaji":"Senshutachi wa taikai no ruuru o mamoru koto o seiyaku shita.","german":"The athletes pledged to abide by the rules of the tournament.","furigana":"せんしゅ|たいかい|まも|せいやく"}],"notes":"誓約 is a formal, often legally binding pledge or declaration of commitment. It appears most often in 誓約書 \"written pledge\", which one signs when joining a company, signing a lease, or taking an exam. As a する-verb it is transitive with を, mostly in ～ことを誓約する \"to pledge to do ...\". Compared with the personal 誓い and the everyday 約束, 誓約 sounds official and literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"畑は違えど、新規市場を開拓するという点においては、前職とさほど変わりはないよ。","romaji":"Hatake wa chigaedo, shinki shijō o kaitaku suru to iu ten ni oite wa, zenshoku to sahodo kawari wa nai yo.","german":"The field may differ, but in terms of developing new markets, it's not much different from my previous job.","furigana":"はたけ|ちが|しんきしじょう|かいたく|てん|ぜんしょく|か"},{"japanese":"明治時代、多くの人が北海道の開拓に携わった。","romaji":"Meiji jidai, ōku no hito ga Hokkaidō no kaitaku ni tazusawatta.","german":"In the Meiji period, many people took part in the settlement of Hokkaido.","furigana":"めいじじだい|おお|ひと|ほっかいどう|かいたく|たずさ"},{"japanese":"営業部は新しい顧客の開拓に力を入れている。","romaji":"Eigyōbu wa atarashii kokyaku no kaitaku ni chikara o irete iru.","german":"The sales department is focusing on cultivating new customers.","furigana":"えいぎょうぶ|あたら|こきゃく|かいたく|ちから|い"}],"notes":"開拓 originally means \"to clear and open up land\" (荒れ地を開拓する, e.g. the settlement of Hokkaido). Today it is often used figuratively: 新しい市場を開拓する \"to open up a new market\", 顧客を開拓する \"to win new customers\". It is a transitive する-verb with を; 開拓者 \"pioneer\" is derived from it. Compared with 開発 (development of products or areas), 開拓 emphasizes stepping into uncharted territory.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"容疑者は裁判が終わるまで東京拘置所に拘置されることになった。","romaji":"Yougisha wa saiban ga owaru made Toukyou kouchisho ni kouchi sareru koto ni natta.","german":"The suspect is to be held at the Tokyo Detention House until the trial ends.","furigana":"ようぎしゃ|さいばん|お|とうきょうこうちしょ|こうち"},{"japanese":"日本では、死刑囚は刑務所ではなく拘置所に拘置されている。","romaji":"Nihon de wa, shikeishuu wa keimusho de wa naku kouchisho ni kouchi sarete iru.","german":"In Japan, prisoners sentenced to death are held in detention houses, not in prisons.","furigana":"にほん|しけいしゅう|けいむしょ|こうちしょ|こうち"},{"japanese":"長期間の拘置は人権問題だと批判されている。","romaji":"Choukikan no kouchi wa jinken mondai da to hihan sarete iru.","german":"Long-term detention is criticized as a human rights issue.","furigana":"ちょうきかん|こうち|じんけんもんだい|ひはん"}],"notes":"拘置 is a legal term for holding suspects, defendants, or prisoners sentenced to death in a custodial facility, typically a 拘置所 \"detention house\". In the news it mostly appears in the passive: 拘置される \"to be held in custody\". The arrest itself is 逮捕, and actual prison sentences are served in a 刑務所. For police taking drunks or animals into protective care, one says 保護, not 拘置.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"昨日は俺が悪かったよ。本当にごめん。お詫びの印に、今日は草刈りをするよ。","romaji":"Kinou wa ore ga warukatta yo. Hontou ni gomen. Owabi no shirushi ni, kyou wa kusakari o suru yo.","german":"Yesterday was my fault. I'm really sorry. To make up for it, I'll cut the grass today.","furigana":"きのう|おれ|わる|ほんとう|わ|しるし|きょう|くさか"},{"japanese":"町内の人たちが集まって、川沿いの草刈りをしました。","romaji":"Chounai no hitotachi ga atsumatte, kawazoi no kusakari o shimashita.","german":"The neighborhood people got together and cut the grass along the river.","furigana":"ちょうない|ひと|あつ|かわぞ|くさか"},{"japanese":"草刈り機を使えば、広い畑でも一時間で終わります。","romaji":"Kusakariki o tsukaeba, hiroi hatake demo ichijikan de owarimasu.","german":"With a brush cutter, even a large field is done in an hour.","furigana":"くさか|き|つか|ひろ|はたけ|いちじかん|お"}],"notes":"草刈り means cutting grass and weeds, for example with a sickle (鎌) or a brush cutter (草刈り機), along roadsides, in fields, or in a garden; one says 草刈りをする. Neat lawn mowing, by contrast, is 芝刈り (lawn mower: 芝刈り機). Pulling weeds out by hand is 草取り or 草むしり. In residential neighborhoods, communal 草刈り is often a shared duty.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の実家は農家で米を育てているので、今週末は稲の刈り取りを手伝わなきゃいけないんです。","romaji":"Watashi no jikka wa nōka de kome o sodatete iru node, konshūmatsu wa ine no karitori o tetsudawanakya ikenai n desu.","german":"My parents are farmers who grow rice, so I have to help with the rice harvest this weekend.","furigana":"わたし|じっか|のうか|こめ|そだ|こんしゅうまつ|いね|か|と|てつだ"},{"japanese":"伸びすぎた雑草を鎌で刈り取った。","romaji":"Nobisugita zassou o kama de karitotta.","german":"I cut down the overgrown weeds with a sickle.","furigana":"の|ざっそう|かま|か|と"},{"japanese":"問題が大きくなる前に、悪の芽は早いうちに刈り取っておくべきだ。","romaji":"Mondai ga ookiku naru mae ni, aku no me wa hayai uchi ni karitotte oku beki da.","german":"Before the problem grows bigger, one should nip evil in the bud early.","furigana":"もんだい|おお|まえ|あく|め|はや|か|と"}],"notes":"刈り取る is transitive and means \"to mow, cut off, harvest\": 稲を刈り取る \"to harvest rice\", 草を刈り取る \"to mow grass\". Compared with the simple 刈る, it stresses that something is cut off completely and removed; the noun is 刈り取り (稲の刈り取り). Figuratively one says 悪の芽を刈り取る \"to nip evil in the bud\". For harvesting fruit or vegetables, 収穫する or 取る is more usual.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は剛健な昔の武士のような見た目だが、彼の足にある血豆を誤って押してしまった時に、泣き叫んでいました。","romaji":"Kare wa gōken na mukashi no bushi no yō na mitame da ga, kare no ashi ni aru chimame o ayamatte oshite shimatta toki ni, nakisakende imashita.","german":"He looks like a strong warrior of old, but he screamed when I accidentally pressed a blood blister on his foot.","furigana":"かれ|ごうけん|むかし|ぶし|み|め|かれ|あし|ちまめ|あやま|お|とき|な|さけ"},{"japanese":"うちの学校の校訓は「質実剛健」です。","romaji":"Uchi no gakkou no koukun wa \"shitsujitsu gouken\" desu.","german":"Our school's motto is \"simple and sturdy\".","furigana":"がっこう|こうくん|しつじつごうけん"},{"japanese":"彼の書く字は、性格と同じく剛健で力強い。","romaji":"Kare no kaku ji wa, seikaku to onajiku gouken de chikarazuyoi.","german":"His handwriting is bold and forceful, just like his character.","furigana":"かれ|か|じ|せいかく|おな|ごうけん|ちからづよ"}],"notes":"剛健 is a literary word for strength and fortitude of body and spirit, with a touch of masculine austerity. Best known is the four-character expression 質実剛健 \"simple, earnest, and robust\", a popular school motto. As a な-adjective: 剛健な精神, 剛健な気風. For sturdy objects one says 頑丈, and for healthy and resilient people 丈夫 or 強健.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あいつは奴の家族の中で唯一まともな人間だよ。","romaji":"Aitsu wa yatsu no kazoku no naka de yuiitsu matomo na ningen da yo.","german":"He is the only decent person in his family.","furigana":"やつ|かぞく|なか|ゆいいつ|にんげん"},{"japanese":"読書が私の唯一の趣味です。","romaji":"Dokusho ga watashi no yuiitsu no shumi desu.","german":"Reading is my only hobby.","furigana":"どくしょ|わたし|ゆいいつ|しゅみ"},{"japanese":"この島へ行く唯一の方法は船です。","romaji":"Kono shima e iku yuiitsu no hōhō wa fune desu.","german":"The only way to get to this island is by boat.","furigana":"しま|い|ゆいいつ|ほうほう|ふね"}],"notes":"唯一 means \"only, sole\" and stresses that there is exactly one. It takes の before nouns (唯一の趣味, 唯一の方法) or is used adverbially without a particle (唯一まともな人). Compared with ただ一つ or たった一つ, 唯一 sounds somewhat more refined. The reading is ゆいいつ, not ゆういつ. Intensified, 唯一無二 means \"one of a kind, unparalleled\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"母国語でも唯物論について説明できないのに、どうして外国語で説明できるというのか。","romaji":"Bokokugo de mo yuibutsuron ni tsuite setsumei dekinai noni, doushite gaikokugo de setsumei dekiru to iu no ka.","german":"I can't even explain materialism in my native language, so how could I explain it in a foreign language?","furigana":"ぼこくご|ゆいぶつろん|せつめい|がいこくご|せつめい"},{"japanese":"マルクスの思想は唯物論に基づいている。","romaji":"Marukusu no shisou wa yuibutsuron ni motozuite iru.","german":"Marx's thought is based on materialism.","furigana":"しそう|ゆいぶつろん|もと"},{"japanese":"唯物論の立場では、心も脳の働きにすぎないと考える。","romaji":"Yuibutsuron no tachiba de wa, kokoro mo nou no hataraki ni suginai to kangaeru.","german":"From a materialist standpoint, the mind too is merely a function of the brain.","furigana":"ゆいぶつろん|たちば|こころ|のう|はたら|かんが"}],"notes":"唯物論 is philosophical materialism: the doctrine that everything is reducible to matter. Its opposite is 唯心論 \"idealism\"; well known are マルクスの唯物論 and 史的唯物論 \"historical materialism\", and an adherent is called 唯物論者. Materialism in the everyday sense of consumerism and a focus on possessions is 物質主義, not 唯物論. The word belongs to philosophical and academic contexts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その花壇を見ると、いつも母を思い出します。","romaji":"Sono kadan o miru to, itsumo haha o omoidashimasu.","german":"Whenever I see that flower bed, I always think of my mother.","furigana":"かだん|み|はは|おも|だ"},{"japanese":"駅前の花壇にチューリップが植えられました。","romaji":"Ekimae no kadan ni chuurippu ga ueraremashita.","german":"Tulips were planted in the flower bed in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|かだん|う"},{"japanese":"花壇に入らないでください。","romaji":"Kadan ni hairanaide kudasai.","german":"Please do not enter the flower bed.","furigana":"かだん|はい"}],"notes":"花壇 is a laid-out flower bed in gardens, parks, in front of schools or stations. One says 花壇に花を植える \"to plant flowers in the bed\" and 花壇の手入れをする \"to tend the bed\"; on signs you read 花壇に入らないでください. A vegetable bed is not a 花壇 but 畑 or 菜園; a large natural field of flowers is 花畑. For planters, one says プランター or 植木鉢.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その尼僧は、寝起きはいつも顔がむくんでいます。","romaji":"Sono nisō wa, neoki wa itsumo kao ga mukunde imasu.","german":"That nun's face is always puffy when she wakes up.","furigana":"にそう|ねお|かお"},{"japanese":"京都の尼寺では、今も数人の尼僧が修行を続けている。","romaji":"Kyouto no amadera de wa, ima mo suunin no nisou ga shugyou o tsuzukete iru.","german":"At a convent in Kyoto, several nuns still continue their religious training.","furigana":"きょうと|あまでら|いま|すうにん|にそう|しゅぎょう|つづ"},{"japanese":"彼女は四十歳で出家し、尼僧になった。","romaji":"Kanojo wa yonjussai de shukke shi, nisou ni natta.","german":"She renounced the world at forty and became a nun.","furigana":"かのじょ|よんじゅっさい|しゅっけ|にそう"}],"notes":"尼僧 is the literary word for a Buddhist nun; in conversation 尼さん is more common. It can also refer to a Catholic nun, but 修道女 or シスター is more usual for that. A woman becomes a nun with 出家して尼僧になる; a nunnery is an 尼寺. The male counterpart is 僧 or 僧侶, colloquially お坊さん.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"万引きをするのは大概、退屈で孤独を感じている女性だと言う人がいますが、ある調査が男性の方が女性よりも万引きをすることが多い可能性を示唆しているそうですよ。","romaji":"Manbiki o suru no wa taigai, taikutsu de kodoku o kanjite iru josei da to iu hito ga imasu ga, aru chōsa ga dansei no hō ga josei yori mo manbiki o suru koto ga ōi kanōsei o shisa shite iru sō desu yo.","german":"Some say shoplifters are mostly bored, lonely women, but a certain survey reportedly suggests men may shoplift more often than women.","furigana":"まんび|たいがい|たいくつ|こどく|かん|じょせい|い|ひと|ちょうさ|だんせい|ほう|じょせい|まんび|おお|かのうせい|しさ"},{"japanese":"日曜日は大概、家でのんびりしています。","romaji":"Nichiyoubi wa taigai, ie de nonbiri shite imasu.","german":"On Sundays I mostly relax at home.","furigana":"にちようび|たいがい|いえ"},{"japanese":"冗談も大概にしてくれ。","romaji":"Joudan mo taigai ni shite kure.","german":"Enough with the jokes already.","furigana":"じょうだん|たいがい"}],"notes":"大概 is used mainly as an adverb meaning \"mostly, as a rule\", being almost synonymous with 大抵 (たいてい) but sounding somewhat more colloquial. With の it stands before nouns: 大概の人 \"most people\". The phrase ～も大概にしろ \"don't overdo ...\" is important, e.g. 冗談も大概にしろ. Colloquially, あいつも大概だ means \"that guy is pretty bad too\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"金魚は淡水魚だから、水槽に塩を入れてはいけませんよ。","romaji":"Kingyo wa tansuigyo da kara, suisō ni shio o irete wa ikemasen yo.","german":"Goldfish are freshwater fish, so you must not put salt in the tank.","furigana":"きんぎょ|たんすいぎょ|すいそう|しお|い"},{"japanese":"この湖の水は淡水ではなく、少し塩分を含んでいる。","romaji":"Kono mizuumi no mizu wa tansui de wa naku, sukoshi enbun o fukunde iru.","german":"The water of this lake is not fresh water but contains a little salt.","furigana":"みずうみ|みず|たんすい|すこ|えんぶん|ふく"},{"japanese":"地球上の水のうち、淡水はわずか3パーセントほどしかない。","romaji":"Chikyuujou no mizu no uchi, tansui wa wazuka san paasento hodo shika nai.","german":"Only about three percent of the water on Earth is fresh water.","furigana":"ちきゅうじょう|みず|たんすい"}],"notes":"淡水 is the technical term for \"fresh water\", as opposed to 海水 \"seawater\"; in between lies 汽水 \"brackish water\". It is common in compounds: 淡水魚 \"freshwater fish\", 淡水湖 \"freshwater lake\". In everyday speech, non-salty water is more often called 真水 (まみず). Water that tastes \"sweet\" would instead be 甘い水.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お雑煮そろそろ煮えたんじゃないかな。","romaji":"O zōni sorosoro nieta n ja nai ka na.","german":"The New Year's mochi soup should be about done cooking by now.","furigana":"ぞうに|に"},{"japanese":"正月の朝は、家族みんなでお雑煮を食べます。","romaji":"Shougatsu no asa wa, kazoku minna de ozouni o tabemasu.","german":"On New Year's morning, the whole family eats ozoni together.","furigana":"しょうがつ|あさ|かぞく|ぞうに|た"},{"japanese":"お雑煮の味は地域によって全然違います。","romaji":"Ozouni no aji wa chiiki ni yotte zenzen chigaimasu.","german":"How ozoni tastes is completely different from region to region.","furigana":"ぞうに|あじ|ちいき|ぜんぜんちが"}],"notes":"雑煮 is the soup with mochi and vegetables that is traditionally eaten during the first days of the New Year, usually together with おせち料理. In daily speech people almost always say the polite お雑煮. Preparation varies greatly by region: in the Kantō area it is more often a clear broth with grilled square mochi, in the Kansai area often white miso with round mochi. Servings are counted with 杯.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"覆面のヒーローは、ボッロボロのマントを身につけていました。","romaji":"Fukumen no hiiroo wa, borroboro no manto o mi ni tsukete imashita.","german":"The masked hero wore a tattered cape.","furigana":"ふくめん|み"},{"japanese":"覆面をした男が銀行に押し入った。","romaji":"Fukumen o shita otoko ga ginkou ni oshiitta.","german":"A masked man broke into the bank.","furigana":"ふくめん|おとこ|ぎんこう|お|い"},{"japanese":"高速道路で覆面パトカーにスピード違反で捕まった。","romaji":"Kousoku douro de fukumen patokaa ni supiido ihan de tsukamatta.","german":"I was caught for speeding on the expressway by an unmarked police car.","furigana":"こうそくどうろ|ふくめん|いはん|つか"}],"notes":"覆面 is a mask or cloth that covers the face, as worn by robbers or wrestlers (覆面レスラー); one says 覆面をする or 覆面をかぶる. A surgical or face mask is マスク, a theater or carnival mask 仮面. In its second sense, 覆面〜 means \"disguised, anonymous\": 覆面パトカー \"unmarked police car\", 覆面調査 \"mystery shopping\", 覆面作家 \"anonymous author\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ワニだらけの沼で私のカヌーが転覆した時は、どっと冷や汗をかきましたよ。","romaji":"Wanidarake no numa de watashi no kanuu ga tenpuku shita toki wa, dotto hiyaase o kakimashita yo.","german":"I broke out in a cold sweat when my canoe capsized in a swamp full of crocodiles.","furigana":"ぬま|わたし|てんぷく|とき|ひ|あせ"},{"japanese":"強風で漁船が転覆し、乗組員三人が救助された。","romaji":"Kyoufuu de gyosen ga tenpuku shi, norikumiin sannin ga kyuujo sareta.","german":"A fishing boat capsized in strong winds, and three crew members were rescued.","furigana":"きょうふう|ぎょせん|てんぷく|のりくみいんさんにん|きゅうじょ"},{"japanese":"彼らは政府の転覆を計画した疑いで逮捕された。","romaji":"Karera wa seifu no tenpuku o keikaku shita utagai de taiho sareta.","german":"They were arrested on suspicion of plotting to overthrow the government.","furigana":"かれ|せいふ|てんぷく|けいかく|うたが|たいほ"}],"notes":"転覆 means \"capsizing, overturning\" of ships or vehicles, and is then intransitive: 船が転覆する, 列車が転覆する. Figuratively it is the \"overthrow\" of a government: 政府を転覆する, 政権の転覆を図る. The word is rather literary and typical of the news; in daily life one says ひっくり返る. It should be distinguished from 沈没 \"sinking\" and 脱線 \"derailment\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あいつらはフィレンツェのダビデ像の前でのドラマチックな暗殺を謀っているんだ。","romaji":"Aitsura wa Firentse no Dabide-zou no mae de no doramachikku na ansatsu o hakatte iru n da.","german":"Those guys are plotting a dramatic assassination in front of Michelangelo's David in Florence.","furigana":"ぞう|まえ|あんさつ|はか"},{"japanese":"部下たちはひそかに王の暗殺を謀った。","romaji":"Bukatachi wa hisoka ni ou no ansatsu o hakatta.","german":"His subordinates secretly plotted the king's assassination.","furigana":"ぶか|おう|あんさつ|はか"},{"japanese":"しまった、謀られた！","romaji":"Shimatta, hakarareta!","german":"Damn, I've been tricked!","furigana":"はか"}],"notes":"謀る (はかる) means \"to plan something evil, to scheme\", e.g. 暗殺を謀る \"to plot an assassination\" or 謀反を謀る \"to plot a rebellion\"; in the passive, 謀られた means \"I was tricked\". The word is literary or sounds like a historical drama; in everyday speech people say 企む (たくらむ) instead. Do not confuse it with the identically read 図る \"to aim at, attempt\" (解決を図る) and 計る／測る／量る \"to measure\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の娘を止めてもらえませんか。無謀にもスパイ活動をしようとしているんです。","romaji":"Watashi no musume o tomete moraemasen ka. Mubō ni mo supai katsudō o shiyō to shite iru n desu.","german":"Could you stop my daughter? She is recklessly trying to do spy work.","furigana":"わたし|むすめ|と|むぼう|かつどう"},{"japanese":"こんな嵐の中で山に登るなんて無謀だよ。","romaji":"Konna arashi no naka de yama ni noboru nante mubou da yo.","german":"Climbing a mountain in a storm like this is reckless.","furigana":"あらし|なか|やま|のぼ|むぼう"},{"japanese":"準備もせずに起業するのは無謀な挑戦だと言われた。","romaji":"Junbi mo sezu ni kigyou suru no wa mubou na chousen da to iwareta.","german":"I was told that starting a business without preparation is a reckless challenge.","furigana":"じゅんび|きぎょう|むぼう|ちょうせん|い"}],"notes":"無謀 describes an undertaking made without thought or regard for risk: \"reckless, rash, ill-considered\". Typical are 無謀な計画, 無謀な挑戦, 無謀運転 as well as 無謀にも～ \"recklessly ...\". Colloquially one often says 無茶; 軽率 refers more to thoughtless behavior in small matters. 大胆 \"bold\" is positive, whereas 無謀 is clearly critical.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は秘密結社が世界を支配しているという陰謀論を信じています。","romaji":"Kare wa himitsu kessha ga sekai o shihai shite iru toiu inbōron o shinjite imasu.","german":"He believes in a conspiracy theory that a secret society controls the world.","furigana":"かれ|ひみつけっしゃ|せかい|しはい|いんぼうろん|しん"},{"japanese":"その大臣は、ライバルの陰謀にはめられて辞任に追い込まれた。","romaji":"Sono daijin wa, raibaru no inbou ni hamerarete jinin ni oikomareta.","german":"The minister was framed by a rival's plot and forced to resign.","furigana":"だいじん|いんぼう|じにん|お|こ"},{"japanese":"王の弟は、王位を奪おうと陰謀を企てていた。","romaji":"Ou no otouto wa, oui o ubaou to inbou o kuwadatete ita.","german":"The king's younger brother was plotting to seize the throne.","furigana":"おう|おとうと|おうい|うば|いんぼう|くわだ"}],"notes":"陰謀 is a secret intrigue or conspiracy with evil intent. Typical: 陰謀を企てる \"to hatch a plot\", 陰謀にはめられる \"to be framed by a plot\", 陰謀に巻き込まれる, 陰謀論 \"conspiracy theory\". Neutral planning is 計画; 策略 is a stratagem, 謀略 more of a political or military intrigue. The word is neutral to literary and frequent in politics and crime stories.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このアニメは隔週で放送されていて、私はそのオープニング曲とエンディング曲が好きです。","romaji":"Kono anime wa kakushuu de housou sarete ite, watashi wa sono oopuningu kyoku to endingu kyoku ga suki desu.","german":"This anime airs every other week, and I like its opening and ending songs.","furigana":"かくしゅう|ほうそう|わたし|きょく|きょく|す"},{"japanese":"資源ゴミは隔週水曜日に回収されます。","romaji":"Shigen gomi wa kakushuu suiyoubi ni kaishuu saremasu.","german":"Recyclable waste is collected every other Wednesday.","furigana":"しげん|かくしゅうすいようび|かいしゅう"},{"japanese":"父は隔週で病院に通っている。","romaji":"Chichi wa kakushuu de byouin ni kayotte iru.","german":"My father goes to the hospital every other week.","furigana":"ちち|かくしゅう|びょういん|かよ"}],"notes":"隔週 means \"every second week, every two weeks\". It is used adverbially with で (隔週で開かれる) or without a particle before weekdays (隔週土曜日 \"every second Saturday\"), and attributively with の (隔週の会議). Colloquially one also says 一週おきに or 二週間に一回. Do not confuse it with 毎週 \"every week\" and 隔月 \"every other month\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"生理痛程度の陣痛が15分から20分ぐらいの間隔で始まって、今行った方がいいのか病院に電話をしました。","romaji":"Seiritsū teido no jintsū ga jūgo-fun kara nijuppun gurai no kankaku de hajimatte, ima itta hō ga ii no ka byōin ni denwa o shimashita.","german":"Contractions about as strong as period cramps started at intervals of 15 to 20 minutes, so I called the hospital to ask whether I should go now.","furigana":"せいりつうていど|じんつう|ふん|ぷん|かんかく|はじ|いまい|ほう|びょういん|でんわ"},{"japanese":"このバスは十五分間隔で運行しています。","romaji":"Kono basu wa jūgofun kankaku de unkō shite imasu.","german":"This bus runs at 15-minute intervals.","furigana":"じゅうごふんかんかく|うんこう"},{"japanese":"苗は三十センチの間隔をあけて植えてください。","romaji":"Nae wa sanjussenchi no kankaku o akete uete kudasai.","german":"Please plant the seedlings 30 centimeters apart.","furigana":"なえ|さんじゅっ|かんかく|う"}],"notes":"間隔 denotes the \"interval\" or \"gap\" between two things, in space or in time. Typical: 間隔をあける \"to leave a gap\", 一定の間隔で \"at regular intervals\", 15分間隔で \"every 15 minutes\" (for buses, for example). Compared with 距離 \"distance\", 間隔 stresses the space between recurring events or elements in a series. Do not confuse it with the identically read 感覚 \"feeling, sense\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本文化の授業が隔月で開講されるんですが、今月は餅つきをする予定です。","romaji":"Nihon bunka no jugyou ga kakugetsu de kaikou sareru n desu ga, kongetsu wa mochitsuki o suru yotei desu.","german":"We have a Japanese culture class every other month, and this month we plan to pound mochi.","furigana":"にほんぶんか|じゅぎょう|かくげつ|かいこう|こんげつ|もち|よてい"},{"japanese":"この雑誌は隔月で発行されている。","romaji":"Kono zasshi wa kakugetsu de hakkou sarete iru.","german":"This magazine is published every other month.","furigana":"ざっし|かくげつ|はっこう"},{"japanese":"水道料金は隔月で請求されます。","romaji":"Suidou ryoukin wa kakugetsu de seikyuu saremasu.","german":"Water bills are charged every other month.","furigana":"すいどうりょうきん|かくげつ|せいきゅう"}],"notes":"隔月 means \"every other month, every two months\" and is used like 隔週: adverbially with で (隔月で発行される), attributively with の (隔月の会合). For magazines, a bimonthly issue is called 隔月刊. Colloquially one says 二か月に一回 or 一か月おき. In Japan, water bills, for example, are often issued 隔月.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私のアパートでは、浴槽と洗面所はシャワーカーテン一枚で隔てられています。","romaji":"Watashi no apaato de wa, yokusou to senmensho wa shawaa kaaten ichimai de hedaterarete imasu.","german":"In my apartment, the bathtub and the washroom are separated by just a single shower curtain.","furigana":"わたし|よくそう|せんめんしょ|いちまい|へだ"},{"japanese":"道を隔てて向かい側に郵便局があります。","romaji":"Michi o hedatete mukaigawa ni yuubinkyoku ga arimasu.","german":"There is a post office across the road.","furigana":"みち|へだ|む|がわ|ゆうびんきょく"},{"japanese":"二人は二十年の時を隔てて再会した。","romaji":"Futari wa nijuunen no toki o hedatete saikai shita.","german":"The two met again after twenty years.","furigana":"ふたり|にじゅうねん|とき|へだ|さいかい"}],"notes":"隔てる is transitive and means \"to separate, partition off, have something in between\": AとBを隔てる, 壁で隔てる. The て-form with space or time is very common: 道を隔てて \"across the street\", 十年の時を隔てて \"after ten years\". Its intransitive counterpart is 隔たる (二人の意見は大きく隔たっている), with the noun 隔たり \"distance, gap\". Compared with 分ける or 仕切る, 隔てる sounds a bit more literary and stresses the distance.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"豚インフルエンザの流行により、遠征は中止となりました。","romaji":"Buta infuruenza no ryuukou ni yori, ensei wa chuushi to narimashita.","german":"Due to the spread of swine flu, the expedition was canceled.","furigana":"ぶた|りゅうこう|えんせい|ちゅうし"},{"japanese":"チームは来月、ヨーロッパへ遠征する予定です。","romaji":"Chiimu wa raigetsu, Yooroppa e ensei suru yotei desu.","german":"The team plans to travel to Europe for away games next month.","furigana":"らいげつ|えんせい|よてい"},{"japanese":"好きなバンドのライブを見るために、大阪まで遠征した。","romaji":"Suki na bando no raibu o miru tame ni, Oosaka made ensei shita.","german":"To see my favorite band's live show, I traveled all the way to Osaka.","furigana":"す|み|おおさか|えんせい"}],"notes":"遠征 originally meant a military campaign; today it usually refers to a long trip for a specific purpose: expeditions, away trips of sports teams (海外遠征, 遠征試合), or tours. Colloquially, fans also say ライブに遠征する when they travel far for a concert. As a する-verb it is intransitive and takes へ／に／まで: ヨーロッパへ遠征する. A pure research expedition into the unknown is more often 探検.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"陛下、恐れ入りますが会議にご出席のご予定だったのでは。","romaji":"Heika, osoreirimasu ga kaigi ni go shusseki no go yotei datta no de wa.","german":"Your Majesty, with all due respect, weren't you scheduled to attend the meeting?","furigana":"へいか|おそ|い|かいぎ|しゅっせき|よてい"},{"japanese":"天皇陛下が被災地を訪問された。","romaji":"Tennou heika ga hisaichi o houmon sareta.","german":"The Emperor visited the disaster-stricken area.","furigana":"てんのうへいか|ひさいち|ほうもん"},{"japanese":"イギリスの女王陛下は長い間、国民に愛された。","romaji":"Igirisu no joou heika wa nagai aida, kokumin ni aisareta.","german":"The British Queen was loved by her people for a long time.","furigana":"じょおうへいか|なが|あいだ|こくみん|あい"}],"notes":"陛下 is the highest honorific title for emperors, empresses, and reigning monarchs, and is placed after the title: 天皇陛下, 皇后陛下, 女王陛下. In direct address it stands alone: 陛下 \"Your Majesty\". Princes and princesses, by contrast, receive 殿下 \"Highness\", and heads of state or high officials formerly 閣下 \"Excellency\". Sentences about 陛下 normally use honorific language (訪問された, お言葉).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は、歴史民俗資料館の館長です。","romaji":"Kare wa, rekishi minzoku shiryōkan no kanchō desu.","german":"He is the director of the Museum of History and Folklore.","furigana":"かれ|れきしみんぞくしりょうかん|かんちょう"},{"japanese":"大学で民俗学を専攻しています。","romaji":"Daigaku de minzokugaku o senkou shite imasu.","german":"I'm majoring in folklore studies at university.","furigana":"だいがく|みんぞくがく|せんこう"},{"japanese":"村に伝わる民俗芸能を守るため、若者たちが踊りを習っている。","romaji":"Mura ni tsutawaru minzoku geinou o mamoru tame, wakamonotachi ga odori o naratte iru.","german":"Young people are learning the dances in order to preserve the folk performing arts handed down in the village.","furigana":"むら|つた|みんぞくげいのう|まも|わかもの|おど|なら"}],"notes":"民俗 refers to traditional folk customs, practices and ways of life. The science of it, folklore studies, is 民俗学; other compounds are 民俗芸能 \"folk arts, folk dances\" and 民俗資料館 \"local history museum\". Beware of the homophone 民族 (みんぞく) \"people, ethnic group\": 民族衣装 \"traditional costume\", 少数民族 \"ethnic minority\". The word is neutral to technical.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の地元には、桑畑があちこちにあります。","romaji":"Watashi no jimoto ni wa, kuwabatake ga achikochi ni arimasu.","german":"There are mulberry fields all over my hometown.","furigana":"わたし|じもと|くわばたけ"},{"japanese":"昔はこの辺りも一面の桑畑で、蚕を育てる家が多かった。","romaji":"Mukashi wa kono atari mo ichimen no kuwabatake de, kaiko o sodateru ie ga ookatta.","german":"In the past this area was covered with mulberry fields, and many families raised silkworms.","furigana":"むかし|あた|いちめん|くわばたけ|かいこ|そだ|いえ|おお"},{"japanese":"地図で桑畑を表す記号は、今はもう使われていない。","romaji":"Chizu de kuwabatake o arawasu kigou wa, ima wa mou tsukawarete inai.","german":"The map symbol for a mulberry field is no longer used today.","furigana":"ちず|くわばたけ|あらわ|きごう|いま|つか"}],"notes":"桑畑 is a field of mulberry trees (桑), whose leaves serve as feed for silkworms (蚕). Until the mid-20th century, 桑畑 shaped the landscape of many regions such as Nagano or Gunma, when silkworm farming (養蚕) was still important. Today the word appears mainly in stories about the past, in local histories, and in songs. 畑 generally means a dry field, as opposed to the rice paddy 田んぼ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"真珠を身につける人は減ってきています。","romaji":"Shinju o mi ni tsukeru hito wa hette kite imasu.","german":"Fewer and fewer people wear pearls.","furigana":"しんじゅ|み|ひと|へ"},{"japanese":"結婚式には真珠のネックレスをつけていくつもりです。","romaji":"Kekkonshiki ni wa shinju no nekkuresu o tsukete iku tsumori desu.","german":"I'm planning to wear a pearl necklace to the wedding.","furigana":"けっこんしき|しんじゅ"},{"japanese":"三重県は真珠の養殖で有名です。","romaji":"Mie-ken wa shinju no youshoku de yuumei desu.","german":"Mie Prefecture is famous for pearl cultivation.","furigana":"みえけん|しんじゅ|ようしょく|ゆうめい"}],"notes":"真珠 is the pearl; in fashion and jewelry one often also says パール. Typical are 真珠のネックレス \"pearl necklace\", 真珠を身につける \"to wear pearls\" and 養殖真珠 \"cultured pearl\"; Ago Bay in Mie is famous for pearl farming. Pearls are counted with 粒. Also well known is 真珠湾 \"Pearl Harbor\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"年を取ると、どうしても体力が衰えてくる。","romaji":"Toshi o toru to, doushite mo tairyoku ga otoroete kuru.","german":"As you get older, your physical strength inevitably declines.","furigana":"とし|と|たいりょく|おとろ"},{"japanese":"台風の勢いは、上陸後に急速に衰えた。","romaji":"Taifuu no ikioi wa, jouriku go ni kyuusoku ni otoroeta.","german":"The typhoon's strength weakened rapidly after landfall.","furigana":"たいふう|いきお|じょうりくご|きゅうそく|おとろ"},{"japanese":"引退して十年たっても、彼の人気は少しも衰えていない。","romaji":"Intai shite juunen tatte mo, kare no ninki wa sukoshi mo otoroete inai.","german":"Even ten years after retiring, his popularity hasn't declined in the slightest.","furigana":"いんたい|じゅうねん|かれ|にんき|すこ|おとろ"}],"notes":"衰える is intransitive and means \"to decline, grow weaker, deteriorate\". Typical subjects are strength and abilities (体力が衰える, 視力や記憶力が衰える), but also influence, popularity or forces of nature (人気が衰える, 台風の勢いが衰える). The noun 衰え appears in 体力の衰えを感じる. For fading flavor or falling quality, by contrast, one says 味が落ちる; 弱る is more everyday and rather physical.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"母親が老衰で亡くなったとき、妻が「厄介者がいなくなってせいせいした」と独り言を言っているのを私は聞き漏らしませんでした。","romaji":"Hahaoya ga rousui de nakunatta toki, tsuma ga \"yakkaimono ga inaku natte seisei shita\" to hitorigoto o itte iru no o watashi wa kikimorashimasen deshita.","german":"When my mother died of old age, I didn't miss hearing my wife mutter to herself, \"I'm glad that burden is gone.\"","furigana":"ははおや|ろうすい|な|つま|やっかいもの|ひと|ごと|い|わたし|き|も"},{"japanese":"祖父は九十八歳で、老衰のため静かに息を引き取った。","romaji":"Sofu wa kyuujuuhassai de, rousui no tame shizuka ni iki o hikitotta.","german":"My grandfather passed away peacefully at ninety-eight from old age.","furigana":"そふ|きゅうじゅうはっさい|ろうすい|しず|いき|ひ|と"},{"japanese":"近年、死因として老衰の割合が増えている。","romaji":"Kinnen, shiin to shite rousui no wariai ga fuete iru.","german":"In recent years, the proportion of deaths attributed to old age has been increasing.","furigana":"きんねん|しいん|ろうすい|わりあい|ふ"}],"notes":"老衰 denotes the frailty of old age, above all as a natural cause of death: 老衰で亡くなる, 死因は老衰. It is not a general word for growing older; the biological aging process is 老化. The word is neutral to formal and often appears in obituaries and statistics. As a する-verb (老衰する) it is rare.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このカボチャのパイは、焼き上がりでも美味しく召し上がって頂けるのですが、私たちは御賞味の前に少し冷蔵庫に入れて頂くことを推奨しています。","romaji":"Kono kabocha no pai wa, yaki agari demo oishiku meshiagatte itadakeru no desu ga, watashitachi wa go shōmi no mae ni sukoshi reizōko ni irete itadaku koto o suishō shite imasu.","german":"This pumpkin pie tastes good fresh from the oven, but we recommend putting it in the refrigerator for a while before eating.","furigana":"や|あ|おい|め|あ|いただ|わたし|ごしょうみ|まえ|すこ|れいぞうこ|い|いただ|すいしょう"},{"japanese":"このソフトの推奨環境は、メモリ8GB以上です。","romaji":"Kono sofuto no suishou kankyou wa, memori hachi giga ijou desu.","german":"As the recommended environment for this software, at least 8 GB of memory is advised."},{"japanese":"政府は、高齢者にワクチン接種を推奨している。","romaji":"Seifu wa, koureisha ni wakuchin sesshu o suishou shite iru.","german":"The government recommends vaccination for the elderly.","furigana":"せいふ|こうれいしゃ|せっしゅ|すいしょう"}],"notes":"推奨 is an official or professional recommendation and sounds more formal than おすすめ or 勧める. Typical are ～を推奨する, ～することが推奨されている as well as 推奨環境 \"recommended system requirements\" and 推奨年齢. 推薦, by contrast, means to nominate a person or work for something (推薦状 \"letter of recommendation\"). 推奨 is found mainly in manuals, official texts, and technology.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"劣悪な職場環境の会社は、日本ではしばしば「ブラック企業」と呼ばれます。","romaji":"Retsuaku na shokuba kankyou no kaisha wa, Nihon de wa shibashiba \"burakku kigyou\" to yobaremasu.","german":"Companies with terrible working conditions are often called \"black companies\" in Japan.","furigana":"れつあく|しょくばかんきょう|かいしゃ|にほん|きぎょう|よ"},{"japanese":"劣悪な環境で飼われていた犬たちが保護された。","romaji":"Retsuaku na kankyou de kawarete ita inutachi ga hogo sareta.","german":"Dogs that had been kept in wretched conditions were taken into protective care.","furigana":"れつあく|かんきょう|か|いぬ|ほご"},{"japanese":"安い材料を使ったせいで、製品の品質が劣悪になった。","romaji":"Yasui zairyou o tsukatta sei de, seihin no hinshitsu ga retsuaku ni natta.","german":"Because cheap materials were used, the product quality has become poor.","furigana":"やす|ざいりょう|つか|せいひん|ひんしつ|れつあく"}],"notes":"劣悪 means \"very bad, wretched\" and refers above all to conditions and quality: 劣悪な環境, 劣悪な労働条件, 劣悪な品質. It is a strong, rather literary な-adjective, often in news about work, animal keeping, or housing conditions. For cheaply made goods one also says 粗悪; in daily life ひどい or 悪い is often enough.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その詐欺師は劣等感の塊だった。","romaji":"Sono sagishi wa rettōkan no katamari datta.","german":"That swindler was a bundle of inferiority complexes.","furigana":"さぎし|れっとうかん|かたまり"},{"japanese":"兄と比べられてばかりで、ずっと劣等感を抱いていた。","romaji":"Ani to kuraberarete bakari de, zutto rettoukan o idaite ita.","german":"Because I was constantly compared with my older brother, I always felt inferior.","furigana":"あに|くら|れっとうかん|いだ"},{"japanese":"英語が話せないことに劣等感を感じる必要はないよ。","romaji":"Eigo ga hanasenai koto ni rettoukan o kanjiru hitsuyou wa nai yo.","german":"You don't need to feel inferior just because you can't speak English.","furigana":"えいご|はな|れっとうかん|かん|ひつよう"}],"notes":"劣等感 is the feeling of being inferior to others, i.e. a \"sense of inferiority, inferiority complex\". Typical: 劣等感を持つ／抱く／感じる, ～に劣等感がある, 劣等感の塊 \"full of inferiority feelings\". The opposite is 優越感 \"sense of superiority\". Colloquially one often says コンプレックス, which in Japanese almost always has this meaning.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"成功の秘訣は、鋭い勘をもつことです。","romaji":"Seikō no hiketsu wa, surudoi kan o motsu koto desu.","german":"The secret to success is having a sharp intuition.","furigana":"せいこう||するど|かん"},{"japanese":"答えが分からなかったので勘で選んだら、当たった。","romaji":"Kotae ga wakaranakatta node kan de erandara, atatta.","german":"I didn't know the answer, so I picked by instinct, and it turned out right.","furigana":"こた|わ|かん|えら|あ"},{"japanese":"しばらく運転していなかったので、勘を取り戻すのに時間がかかった。","romaji":"Shibaraku unten shite inakatta node, kan o torimodosu no ni jikan ga kakatta.","german":"I hadn't driven for a while, so it took time to get my touch back.","furigana":"うんてん|かん|と|もど|じかん"}],"notes":"勘 is intuitive sense, a \"nose\" for things or a gut feeling. Typical are 勘がいい／鋭い \"to have good instincts\", 勘が当たる／外れる \"to be right／wrong\", 勘で答える \"to answer by gut feeling\" and 勘を取り戻す \"to get the feel for something back\" after a break. The more literary 直感 emphasizes the sudden inspiration, 第六感 the \"sixth sense\". 勘 belongs to casual everyday vocabulary.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"勘弁してよ。今日は忙しかったし、滅茶苦茶疲れているんだ。","romaji":"Kanben shite yo. Kyō wa isogashikatta shi, mechakucha tsukarete iru n da.","german":"Come on, give me a break. I was busy today and I'm totally exhausted.","furigana":"かんべん|きょう|いそが|めちゃくちゃつか"},{"japanese":"遅刻したのは悪かったけど、今回だけは勘弁してください。","romaji":"Chikoku shita no wa warukatta kedo, konkai dake wa kanben shite kudasai.","german":"I'm sorry I was late, but please let me off just this once.","furigana":"ちこく|わる|こんかい|かんべん"},{"japanese":"店内での写真撮影はご勘弁ください。","romaji":"Tennai de no shashin satsuei wa gokanben kudasai.","german":"Please refrain from taking photographs inside the store.","furigana":"てんない|しゃしんさつえい|かんべん"}],"notes":"勘弁 means \"forbearance, pardon\" and is used almost only as 勘弁する in fixed expressions. 勘弁してください means, depending on tone, \"please forgive me\" or \"please spare me that\"; もう勘弁してくれ is an exasperated \"enough already\". Someone who forgives says 今回だけは勘弁してやる. On signs, the polite ご勘弁ください means \"we ask that you refrain from this\". Compared with 許す it sounds more colloquial and pleading.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「すみません、間違えました。違う人と勘違いしました。」「正確には誰と間違えたんですか？お願いします。教えてください。」","romaji":"Sumimasen, machigaemashita. Chigau hito to kanchigai shimashita. Seikaku ni wa dare to machigaeta n desu ka? Onegai shimasu. Oshiete kudasai.","german":"\"Sorry, my mistake. I mistook you for someone else.\" \"Who exactly did you mistake me for? Please. Tell me.\"","furigana":"まちが|ちが|ひと|かんちが|せいかく|だれ|まちが|ねが|おし"},{"japanese":"会議は明日だと勘違いしていた。","romaji":"Kaigi wa ashita da to kanchigai shite ita.","german":"I mistakenly thought the meeting was tomorrow.","furigana":"かいぎ|あした|かんちが"},{"japanese":"彼女が優しくしてくれるのは自分のことが好きだからだと、勘違いしてしまった。","romaji":"Kanojo ga yasashiku shite kureru no wa jibun no koto ga suki da kara da to, kanchigai shite shimatta.","german":"I mistakenly assumed she was being kind to me because she likes me.","furigana":"かのじょ|やさ|じぶん|す|かんちが"}],"notes":"勘違い is an error based on a false assumption: one takes something to be different from what it is. Typical: ～と勘違いする \"to wrongly believe that ...\", AをBと勘違いする \"to mistake A for B\", 日にちを勘違いする \"to get the date wrong\". 誤解, by contrast, is a misunderstanding between people, for example about intentions (誤解を招く). Colloquially, 勘違いしないで also means \"don't get the wrong idea\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これは王妃の首飾りです。","romaji":"Kore wa ōhi no kubikazari desu.","german":"This is the queen's necklace.","furigana":"おうひ|くびかざ"},{"japanese":"マリー・アントワネットは、フランス国王ルイ16世の王妃だった。","romaji":"Marii Antowanetto wa, Furansu kokuou Rui juurokusei no ouhi datta.","german":"Marie Antoinette was the queen consort of King Louis XVI of France.","furigana":"こくおう|せい|おうひ"},{"japanese":"王と王妃が手を振ると、集まった人々から大きな拍手が起こった。","romaji":"Ou to ouhi ga te o furu to, atsumatta hitobito kara ookina hakushu ga okotta.","german":"When the king and queen waved, great applause broke out from the gathered crowd.","furigana":"おう|おうひ|て|ふ|あつ|ひとびと|おお|はくしゅ|お"}],"notes":"王妃 is the consort of a king, i.e. the queen as wife. A queen who reigns in her own right is called 女王, and the consort of an emperor 皇后. Typical in history and fairy tales: 王と王妃, マリー・アントワネット王妃. The word is neutral to literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ダイアナ妃は世界中の人々に愛された。","romaji":"Daiana-hi wa sekaijuu no hitobito ni aisareta.","german":"Princess Diana was loved by people all over the world.","furigana":"ひ|せかいじゅう|ひとびと|あい"},{"japanese":"皇太子妃が病院を訪問された。","romaji":"Koutaishihi ga byouin o houmon sareta.","german":"The Crown Princess visited a hospital.","furigana":"こうたいしひ|びょういん|ほうもん"}],"notes":"〜妃 (ひ) is a suffix placed after the name of the consort of a prince or monarch, for example ダイアナ妃 \"Princess Diana\". It is also found in 王妃 \"queen (consort)\" and 皇太子妃 \"Crown Princess\"; in forms of address and honorific language it is extended to 妃殿下. When the character stands alone, it is read きさき. A princess by birth is called 王女 or 姫.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女のお婆さんが、「あの子の人見知りは哀れむべきものだ」とぼやくのを聞きました。","romaji":"Kanojo no obāsan ga, ano ko no hitomishiri wa awaremubeki mono da to boyaku no o kikimashita.","german":"I overheard her grandmother grumble, \"That child's shyness is pitiable.\"","furigana":"かのじょ|ばあ|こ|ひとみし|あわ|き"},{"japanese":"彼は私を哀れむような目で見た。","romaji":"Kare wa watashi o awaremu you na me de mita.","german":"He looked at me with a pitying look.","furigana":"かれ|わたし|あわ|め|み"},{"japanese":"自分を哀れんでばかりいても、何も変わらないよ。","romaji":"Jibun o awarende bakari ite mo, nani mo kawaranai yo.","german":"Just feeling sorry for yourself won't change anything.","furigana":"じぶん|あわ|なに|か"}],"notes":"哀れむ (also written 憐れむ) means \"to pity, feel compassion for\" and is transitive: 人を哀れむ, 自分を哀れむ. It sounds somewhat literary and can come across as condescending, especially in 哀れむような目で見る. More neutral are 同情する or かわいそうに思う. The noun is 哀れみ (哀れみをかける).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私はうちの食堂を忙しいサラリーマンの憩いの場にしたいんです。","romaji":"Watashi wa uchi no shokudō o isogashii sararīman no ikoi no ba ni shitai n desu.","german":"I want to make my restaurant a place of rest for busy salarymen.","furigana":"わたし|しょくどう|いそが|いこ|ば"},{"japanese":"この公園は、近所のお年寄りの憩いの場になっている。","romaji":"Kono kouen wa, kinjo no otoshiyori no ikoi no ba ni natte iru.","german":"This park has become a place of relaxation for the elderly in the neighborhood.","furigana":"こうえん|きんじょ|としよ|いこ|ば"},{"japanese":"仕事の合間のコーヒータイムが、私にとって唯一の憩いのひとときです。","romaji":"Shigoto no aima no koohii taimu ga, watashi ni totte yuiitsu no ikoi no hitotoki desu.","german":"The coffee break between work is the only moment of relaxation for me.","furigana":"しごと|あいま|わたし|ゆいいつ|いこ"}],"notes":"憩い means \"rest, relaxation, repose\" and appears almost always in fixed combinations such as 憩いの場 \"place of rest\" or 憩いのひととき \"moment of relaxation\". It sounds elevated and friendly, typical of park descriptions or advertising copy. The verb 憩う is literary. In everyday speech, a break is 休憩, and relaxation is くつろぎ or リラックス.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"派手にお金を使っているなぁと思っていたら、詐欺師だったんですね！","romaji":"Hade ni okane o tsukatte iru nā to omotte itara, sagishi datta n desu ne!","german":"I wondered why he was spending money so lavishly, and it turns out he was a swindler!","furigana":"はで|かね|つか|おも|さぎし"},{"japanese":"祖母は電話で詐欺師にだまされて、お金を取られた。","romaji":"Sobo wa denwa de sagishi ni damasarete, okane o torareta.","german":"My grandmother was tricked by a swindler over the phone and had money taken from her.","furigana":"そぼ|でんわ|さぎし|かね|と"},{"japanese":"彼は口がうまくて、まるで詐欺師のようだ。","romaji":"Kare wa kuchi ga umakute, marude sagishi no yō da.","german":"He's such a smooth talker, he's just like a con man.","furigana":"かれ|くち|さぎし"}],"notes":"詐欺師 is a \"swindler, con artist\": 詐欺 means \"fraud\", and 〜師 denotes someone who practices something like a trade. Common is 詐欺師にだまされる \"to be tricked by a swindler\". For phone scams targeting seniors (振り込め詐欺, オレオレ詐欺) people usually speak of a 詐欺グループ or 犯人. Colloquially and somewhat old-fashioned there is also ペテン師.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"花柳界を経験してみるのが夢だったんだ。","romaji":"Karyūkai o keiken shite miru no ga yume datta n da.","german":"It was my dream to experience the geisha world.","furigana":"かりゅうかい|けいけん|ゆめ"},{"japanese":"祖母は若い頃、京都の花柳界で芸者として働いていた。","romaji":"Sobo wa wakai koro, Kyouto no karyuukai de geisha to shite hataraite ita.","german":"In her youth my grandmother worked as a geisha in the Kyoto hanamachi.","furigana":"そぼ|わか|ころ|きょうと|かりゅうかい|げいしゃ|はたら"},{"japanese":"花柳界には、独特のしきたりや言葉がある。","romaji":"Karyuukai ni wa, dokutoku no shikitari ya kotoba ga aru.","german":"The geisha world has its own unique customs and vocabulary.","furigana":"かりゅうかい|どくとく|ことば"}],"notes":"花柳界 literally means the \"world of flowers and willows\", an old image for geisha and courtesans. Today it mainly refers to the geisha world with its teahouses, for example in Kyoto's Gion; a synonym is 花街 (かがい／はなまち). Historically it also included pleasure quarters, but it is not the same as the modern sex industry (風俗). The word is literary and somewhat old-fashioned.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"逮捕された男は、休憩所に不法侵入した疑いがかけられていた。","romaji":"Taiho sareta otoko wa, kyuukeijo ni fuhou shinnyuu shita utagai ga kakerarete ita.","german":"The arrested man was suspected of trespassing in the rest area.","furigana":"たいほ|おとこ|きゅうけいじょ|ふほうしんにゅう|うたが"},{"japanese":"山道の途中に休憩所があるので、そこで一休みしましょう。","romaji":"Yamamichi no tochuu ni kyuukeijo ga aru node, soko de hitoyasumi shimashou.","german":"There is a rest area partway along the mountain path, so let's take a break there.","furigana":"やまみち|とちゅう|きゅうけいじょ|ひとやす"},{"japanese":"イベント会場の休憩所は、すでに人でいっぱいだった。","romaji":"Ibento kaijou no kyuukeijo wa, sude ni hito de ippai datta.","german":"The rest area at the event venue was already full of people.","furigana":"かいじょう|きゅうけいじょ|ひと"}],"notes":"休憩所 is a place to rest: rest areas along hiking trails, rest areas in parks, at events, or in train stations. The reading is きゅうけいじょ, though きゅうけいしょ also occurs. On expressways one usually says specifically サービスエリア or パーキングエリア; a break room at a company is 休憩室. One says 休憩所で休む or 休憩所で一休みする.","levelNote":"Compound: 休憩 (N3) + 所 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ご飯、炊けた？","romaji":"Gohan, taketa?","german":"Is the rice done?","furigana":"はん|た"},{"japanese":"ご飯が炊けたら、すぐにおにぎりを作ろう。","romaji":"Gohan ga taketara, sugu ni onigiri o tsukurou.","german":"As soon as the rice is done, let's make onigiri.","furigana":"はん|た|つく"},{"japanese":"炊飯器が「ご飯が炊けました」と知らせてくれた。","romaji":"Suihanki ga \"gohan ga takemashita\" to shirasete kureta.","german":"The rice cooker announced, \"The rice is done.\"","furigana":"すいはんき|はん|た|し"}],"notes":"炊ける is the intransitive counterpart of 炊く \"to cook rice\": ご飯が炊ける \"the rice is cooked, is ready\". The rice cooker accordingly announces ご飯が炊けました. In standard Japanese 炊く／炊ける is used almost only for rice; for things simmered in broth one says 煮える, and for things grilled or baked 焼ける.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"急に窓ガラスが粉々に砕けた。","romaji":"Kyū ni mado garasu ga konagona ni kudaketa.","german":"Suddenly the window glass shattered into pieces.","furigana":"きゅう|まど|こなごな|くだ"},{"japanese":"大きな波が岩に当たって砕けた。","romaji":"Ookina nami ga iwa ni atatte kudaketa.","german":"Big waves hit the rocks and broke.","furigana":"おお|なみ|いわ|あ|くだ"},{"japanese":"友達と話す時は、もっと砕けた言い方でいいんだよ。","romaji":"Tomodachi to hanasu toki wa, motto kudaketa iikata de ii n da yo.","german":"When you talk with friends, it's fine to speak in a more casual way.","furigana":"ともだち|はな|とき|くだ|い|かた"}],"notes":"砕ける is intransitive and means \"to break into pieces, shatter\": ガラスが粉々に砕ける, 波が岩に当たって砕ける. Its transitive counterpart is 砕く \"to smash, grind\". Unlike 割れる (to crack, split), 砕ける stresses falling apart into many small pieces. The figurative sense \"casual, informal\" is very common: 砕けた言い方, 砕けた態度; also the saying 当たって砕けろ \"just give it a try\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この会社の決算公告、もう見ましたか。","romaji":"Kono kaisha no kessan koukoku, mou mimashita ka.","german":"Have you already seen this company's published financial statements?","furigana":"かいしゃ|けっさんこうこく|み"},{"japanese":"選挙の日程が官報で公告された。","romaji":"Senkyo no nittei ga kanpou de koukoku sareta.","german":"The election date was publicly announced in the official gazette.","furigana":"せんきょ|にってい|かんぽう|こうこく"},{"japanese":"合併について、会社は新聞に公告を出した。","romaji":"Gappei ni tsuite, kaisha wa shinbun ni koukoku o dashita.","german":"Regarding the merger, the company placed a public notice in the newspaper.","furigana":"がっぺい|かいしゃ|しんぶん|こうこく|だ"}],"notes":"公告 is an official or legally required public notice, for example 決算公告 \"publication of annual accounts\" or announcements in the official gazette (官報で公告する). As a する-verb it is transitive: ～を公告する, often passive 公告される. Beware of the homophone 広告 \"advertisement\". General announcements are more often called お知らせ, 告知, or 公表.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この音楽はとても律動的ですね。","romaji":"Kono ongaku wa totemo ritsudōteki desu ne.","german":"This music is very rhythmic.","furigana":"おんがく|りつどうてき"},{"japanese":"心臓は一定の律動で血液を送り出している。","romaji":"Shinzou wa ittei no ritsudou de ketsueki o okuridashite iru.","german":"The heart pumps blood at a steady rhythm.","furigana":"しんぞう|いってい|りつどう|けつえき|おく|だ"},{"japanese":"ダンサーたちの律動的な動きに、観客は目を奪われた。","romaji":"Dansaatachi no ritsudouteki na ugoki ni, kankyaku wa me o ubawareta.","german":"The audience was captivated by the dancers' rhythmic movements.","furigana":"りつどうてき|うご|かんきゃく|め|うば"}],"notes":"律動 is a literary, technical word for \"rhythm\", for example in medicine, music theory, or literature: 心臓の律動, 律動的な動き. In daily life one almost always says リズム. Most common is the derivation 律動的 \"rhythmic\" as a な-adjective; the verb 律動する is rare.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"貧乏ですが、何とか子供の学資は貯えるつもりです。","romaji":"Binbō desu ga, nantoka kodomo no gakushi wa takuwaeru tsumori desu.","german":"We're poor, but I intend to somehow save up for our child's education.","furigana":"びんぼう|なん|こども|がくし|たくわ"},{"japanese":"冬に備えて、食料を貯えておいた。","romaji":"Fuyu ni sonaete, shokuryou o takuwaete oita.","german":"I stored up food in preparation for winter.","furigana":"ふゆ|そな|しょくりょう|たくわ"},{"japanese":"若いうちに力を貯えて、いつか独立したい。","romaji":"Wakai uchi ni chikara o takuwaete, itsuka dokuritsu shitai.","german":"I want to build up strength while I'm young and someday become independent.","furigana":"わか|ちから|たくわ|どくりつ"}],"notes":"貯える means \"to save up, lay in stores, store\" and is transitive: お金を貯える, 食料を貯える. The spelling 蓄える is more common, especially for abstract things like 知識を蓄える, 力を蓄える, or ひげを蓄える \"to grow a beard\". For everyday saving of money one usually says 貯める; 貯える sounds more deliberate, as if setting something aside for later. The noun is 蓄え／貯え \"savings, stock\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"運動会の騎馬戦の練習があるので、明日は早起きしなくてはいけません。","romaji":"Undōkai no kibasen no renshū ga aru node, ashita wa hayaoki shinakute wa ikemasen.","german":"I have a cavalry battle (kibasen) practice for the sports day, so I have to get up early tomorrow.","furigana":"うんどうかい|きばせん|れんしゅう|あした|はやお"},{"japanese":"祭りでは、騎馬の武者が町を行進する。","romaji":"Matsuri de wa, kiba no musha ga machi o koushin suru.","german":"At the festival, mounted warriors parade through town.","furigana":"まつ|きば|むしゃ|まち|こうしん"},{"japanese":"ロンドンでは騎馬警官をよく見かける。","romaji":"Rondon de wa kiba keikan o yoku mikakeru.","german":"In London you often see mounted police officers.","furigana":"きばけいかん|み"}],"notes":"騎馬 means \"on horseback, mounted\" and mostly appears as a modifier: 騎馬隊 \"mounted troop\", 騎馬警官 \"mounted police officer\", 騎馬民族 \"equestrian people\". Well known is 騎馬戦, a school sports game in which three children carry a \"rider\" who tries to capture opponents' headbands. Riding as a hobby or sport is 乗馬. The word sounds literary or historical.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その騎兵の写真、なんだか若かりし頃を思い出すな。","romaji":"Sono kihei no shashin, nandaka wakakarishi koro o omoidasu na.","german":"That photo of the cavalryman somehow reminds me of my younger days.","furigana":"きへい|しゃしん|わか|ころ|おも|だ"},{"japanese":"昔の戦争では、騎兵の突撃が勝敗を左右することも多かった。","romaji":"Mukashi no sensou de wa, kihei no totsugeki ga shouhai o sayuu suru koto mo ookatta.","german":"In past wars, a cavalry charge often decided victory or defeat.","furigana":"むかし|せんそう|きへい|とつげき|しょうはい|さゆう|おお"},{"japanese":"騎兵隊が丘の向こうから現れた。","romaji":"Kiheitai ga oka no mukou kara arawareta.","german":"The cavalry appeared from beyond the hill.","furigana":"きへいたい|おか|む|あらわ"}],"notes":"騎兵 means \"cavalry\" or an individual \"cavalryman\", i.e. soldiers on horseback. Opposing terms are 歩兵 \"infantry\" and 砲兵 \"artillery\"; a unit is called 騎兵隊. The word belongs to military and historical topics and hardly occurs in daily life. 騎馬, by contrast, stresses riding or being mounted in general.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"桑原さんに聞いてみましょう！","romaji":"Kuwabara-san ni kiite mimashou!","german":"Let's ask Kuwabara-san!","furigana":"くわばら|き"},{"japanese":"雷が鳴ると、祖母はいつも「くわばら、くわばら」と唱えていた。","romaji":"Kaminari ga naru to, sobo wa itsumo \"kuwabara, kuwabara\" to tonaete ita.","german":"When it thundered, my grandmother would always chant \"kuwabara, kuwabara\".","furigana":"かみなり|な|そぼ|とな"},{"japanese":"くわばらくわばら。あの人を怒らせたら大変なことになるよ。","romaji":"Kuwabara kuwabara. Ano hito o okorasetara taihen na koto ni naru yo.","german":"God forbid! If you make that person angry, all hell will break loose.","furigana":"ひと|おこ|たいへん"}],"notes":"桑原 literally means \"mulberry field\", but in this meaning it is obsolete; today one says 桑畑. 桑原 is known mainly as a family name and from the incantation くわばらくわばら (usually in kana), which was formerly recited during thunderstorms and is used today in the sense of \"God forbid!\" or \"knock on wood\" when one wants to escape a danger or unpleasantness. The formula sounds old-fashioned to jocular.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"皇太子妃が軍に志願するはずねぇだろうが。","romaji":"Kōtaishihi ga gun ni shigan suru hazu nē darō ga.","german":"There's no way a crown princess would volunteer for the military.","furigana":"こうたいしひ|ぐん|しがん"},{"japanese":"皇太子妃のご訪問を、市民たちは笑顔で迎えた。","romaji":"Kōtaishihi no go-hōmon o, shimintachi wa egao de mukaeta.","german":"The citizens welcomed the Crown Princess's visit with smiles.","furigana":"こうたいしひ|ほうもん|しみん|えがお|むか"},{"japanese":"イギリスの皇太子妃がチャリティー活動に参加した。","romaji":"Igirisu no kōtaishihi ga charitī katsudō ni sanka shita.","german":"The British Crown Princess took part in a charity event.","furigana":"こうたいしひ|かつどう|さんか"}],"notes":"皇太子妃 is the wife of the crown prince (皇太子), i.e. the \"crown princess\" - in Japan, for example, 雅子さま until the accession to the throne in 2019. In the media she is respectfully called 皇太子妃殿下 or referred to with 〜さま. The wife of the present heir (皇嗣) is called 皇嗣妃. 皇太子妃 is also used for foreign royal families.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あいつは悪賢い男で、猫かぶりが大のお得意なんだ。","romaji":"Aitsu wa warugashikoi otoko de, nekokaburi ga dai no otokui na n da.","german":"He's a cunning guy and a master at putting on an innocent act.","furigana":"わるがしこ|おとこ|ねこ|だい|とくい"},{"japanese":"悪賢いキツネは、カラスをだましてチーズを奪った。","romaji":"Warugashikoi kitsune wa, karasu o damashite chiizu o ubatta.","german":"The cunning fox tricked the crow and took the cheese.","furigana":"わるがしこ|うば"},{"japanese":"彼は法律の抜け道を探すのが上手な、悪賢い政治家だ。","romaji":"Kare wa houritsu no nukemichi o sagasu no ga jouzu na, warugashikoi seijika da.","german":"He is a crafty politician who is good at finding loopholes in the law.","furigana":"かれ|ほうりつ|ぬ|みち|さが|じょうず|わるがしこ|せいじか"}],"notes":"悪賢い is an い-adjective made of 悪 \"bad\" and 賢い \"clever\": clever, but in a wicked, self-serving way, i.e. \"cunning, crafty, sly\". Very similar is ずる賢い, which sounds more everyday and stresses sneakiness. Related is 悪知恵が働く \"to be crafty\". Typical: 悪賢い男, 悪賢いやり方, and in fairy tales 悪賢いキツネ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私はそのダイヤモンドの輝きに目がくらんでしまいました。","romaji":"Watashi wa sono daiyamondo no kagayaki ni me ga kurande shimaimashita.","german":"I was dazzled by the brilliance of the diamond.","furigana":"わたし|かがや|め"},{"japanese":"子どもたちの目の輝きを見ると、教師になってよかったと思う。","romaji":"Kodomotachi no me no kagayaki o miru to, kyoushi ni natte yokatta to omou.","german":"When I see the sparkle in the children's eyes, I'm glad I became a teacher.","furigana":"こ|め|かがや|み|きょうし|おも"},{"japanese":"引退した後、彼女はかつての輝きを失ってしまった。","romaji":"Intai shita ato, kanojo wa katsute no kagayaki o ushinatte shimatta.","german":"After retiring, she lost her former brilliance.","furigana":"いんたい|あと|かのじょ|かがや|うしな"}],"notes":"輝き is the noun of 輝く \"to shine, gleam\" and means \"brilliance, radiance, sparkle\". It is used for the glitter of gemstones or stars (ダイヤモンドの輝き, 星の輝き), but also figuratively: 目の輝き \"the sparkle in one's eyes\", かつての輝きを失う \"to lose one's former brilliance\". Typical verbs are 輝きを放つ, 輝きを増す, 輝きを失う. Mere light is 光, and surface shine is more like つや.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この鰐の置物、邪魔なんだけど。","romaji":"Kono wani no okimono, jama na n da kedo.","german":"This crocodile ornament is in the way.","furigana":"わに|おきもの|じゃま"},{"japanese":"動物園のワニは、一日中ほとんど動かなかった。","romaji":"Doubutsuen no wani wa, ichinichijuu hotondo ugokanakatta.","german":"The crocodile at the zoo hardly moved all day.","furigana":"どうぶつえん|いちにちじゅう|うご"},{"japanese":"「因幡の白うさぎ」に出てくる「わに」は、サメのことだと言われている。","romaji":"\"Inaba no shirousagi\" ni dete kuru \"wani\" wa, same no koto da to iwarete iru.","german":"The \"wani\" in the tale of the white hare of Inaba is said to mean sharks.","furigana":"|しろ|で|い"}],"notes":"鰐 (わに) refers to crocodiles and alligators alike; in daily life it is almost always written ワニ in katakana, and the kanji is rare. Compounds include ワニ革 \"crocodile leather\" and ワニ口クリップ \"alligator clip\". In old Japanese, わに also meant \"shark\", for example in the tale of the white hare of Inaba.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日は空気が澄んでいて、立山連峰がはっきり見えます。","romaji":"Kyou wa kuuki ga sunde ite, Tateyama renpou ga hakkiri miemasu.","german":"The air is clear today, and you can see the Tateyama range clearly.","furigana":"きょう|くうき|す||み"},{"japanese":"ホテルの窓から、雪をかぶった連峰を眺めた。","romaji":"Hoteru no mado kara, yuki o kabutta renpou o nagameta.","german":"From the hotel window, I gazed at the snow-capped range.","furigana":"まど|ゆき|れんぽう|なが"},{"japanese":"連峰の最高峰に登るのが、今年の目標です。","romaji":"Renpou no saikouhou ni noboru no ga, kotoshi no mokuhyou desu.","german":"My goal this year is to climb the highest peak of the range.","furigana":"れんぽう|さいこうほう|のぼ|ことし|もくひょう"}],"notes":"連峰 denotes a row of connected peaks seen as a chain, often with proper names: 立山連峰, 八ヶ岳連峰. Typical are expressions like 連峰を望む \"to look out at the mountain range\" or 雪をかぶった連峰. The word is literary and appears mainly in travel guides, weather reports, and the news. 山脈, by contrast, is the geographical term for a long mountain range (奥羽山脈); in daily life one simply says 山々.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は人を説得するのが巧い。","romaji":"Kare wa hito o settoku suru no ga umai.","german":"He is good at persuading people.","furigana":"かれ|ひと|せっとく|うま"},{"japanese":"この作家は会話の書き方が実に巧い。","romaji":"Kono sakka wa kaiwa no kakikata ga jitsu ni umai.","german":"This author is really skillful at writing dialogue.","furigana":"さっか|かいわ|か|かた|じつ|うま"},{"japanese":"巧く言えないけど、何かおかしい気がする。","romaji":"Umaku ienai kedo, nanika okashii ki ga suru.","german":"I can't put it well, but something feels off.","furigana":"うま|い|なに|き"}],"notes":"巧い is the spelling of うまい in the sense of \"skillful, adept\"; the word is mostly written in kana, and 上手い is also common as a kanji spelling. For \"delicious\", 旨い or 美味い is used instead. Typical is 〜が巧い or 〜のが巧い (話すのが巧い), adverbially 巧く \"well, as desired\" (巧く言えない). 上手 is more neutral and polite; うまい sounds colloquial and is also used for one's own abilities.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ある邪悪な魔法使いが、巧妙に偶然を装って女性に近づきました。","romaji":"Aru jaaku na mahōtsukai ga, kōmyō ni gūzen o yosōtte josei ni chikazukimashita.","german":"A certain wicked wizard cleverly approached the woman, pretending it was by chance.","furigana":"じゃあく|まほうつか|こうみょう|ぐうぜん|よそお|じょせい|ちか"},{"japanese":"巧妙な手口の詐欺が増えているので、注意してください。","romaji":"Koumyou na teguchi no sagi ga fuete iru node, chuui shite kudasai.","german":"Fraud using sophisticated methods is increasing, so please be careful.","furigana":"こうみょう|てぐち|さぎ|ふ|ちゅうい"},{"japanese":"宝石は箱の底に巧妙に隠されていた。","romaji":"Houseki wa hako no soko ni koumyou ni kakusarete ita.","german":"The jewel had been cleverly hidden at the bottom of the box.","furigana":"ほうせき|はこ|そこ|こうみょう|かく"}],"notes":"巧妙 is a な-adjective for something cleverly devised and often carries a negative undertone of cunning or deception: 巧妙な手口 \"a sophisticated scheme\", 巧妙な嘘, 巧妙に隠す. The adverbial form is 巧妙に. For neutral skill one rather says 上手, for manual dexterity 器用. The word is rather literary and common in news about fraud and crime.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の邪魔をするな。一人で出来るよ！","romaji":"Watashi no jama o suru na. Hitori de dekiru yo!","german":"Don't get in my way. I can do it by myself!","furigana":"わたし|じゃま|ひとり|でき"},{"japanese":"この箱は邪魔なので、外に出してください。","romaji":"Kono hako wa jama na node, soto ni dashite kudasai.","german":"This box is in the way, so please take it outside.","furigana":"はこ|じゃま|そと|だ"},{"japanese":"こんにちは。お邪魔します。","romaji":"Konnichiwa. Ojama shimasu.","german":"Hello. Excuse me for coming in. (when entering someone's home)","furigana":"じゃま"}],"notes":"邪魔 refers to something that disturbs or is in the way: 邪魔な荷物 \"luggage that is in the way\", 邪魔になる \"to be a nuisance, to be a hindrance\". As a する-verb, the thing disturbed takes を: 勉強の邪魔をする, 人の邪魔をしない. Very common is the polite formula お邪魔します when entering someone else's home (and お邪魔しました when leaving) - here it means \"to visit\". In everyday writing it is often written in kana as じゃま.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"男の子と旅行に行くのは、絶対に駄目ですからね！","romaji":"Otokonoko to ryokō ni iku no wa, zettai ni dame desu kara ne!","german":"You absolutely must not go on a trip with a boy!","furigana":"おとこ|こ|りょこう|い|ぜったい|だめ"},{"japanese":"このパソコンはもう古くてだめです。","romaji":"Kono pasokon wa mou furukute dame desu.","german":"This computer is old now and no good anymore.","furigana":"ふる"},{"japanese":"一生懸命練習したけど、試合はだめでした。","romaji":"Isshoukenmei renshuu shita kedo, shiai wa dame deshita.","german":"I practiced as hard as I could, but the match went badly anyway.","furigana":"いっしょうけんめいれんしゅう|しあい"}],"notes":"駄目 is a な-adjective with the basic sense \"is no good, doesn't work\": この傘はもうだめだ \"the umbrella is done for\", 私は料理がだめです \"I can't cook\". From this follows \"not allowed\": ～てはだめ / ～ちゃだめ \"you must not ...\", and on its own as an exclamation だめ！ \"No! Stop that!\". Also \"futile, failed\": 頑張ったけどだめだった. The word is colloquial-neutral and mostly written in kana as だめ (also ダメ); more politely one prohibits with いけません or ご遠慮ください.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女、唐突に車の修理をしてほしいって俺に頼んできたんだよ。","romaji":"Kanojo, tōtotsu ni kuruma no shūri o shite hoshii tte ore ni tanonde kita n da yo.","german":"She suddenly asked me to repair her car.","furigana":"かのじょ|とうとつ|くるま|しゅうり|おれ|たの"},{"japanese":"唐突ですが、来月で会社を辞めることになりました。","romaji":"Toutotsu desu ga, raigetsu de kaisha o yameru koto ni narimashita.","german":"I'm sorry to spring this on you, but I'll be leaving the company at the end of next month.","furigana":"とうとつ|らいげつ|かいしゃ|や"},{"japanese":"その映画は結末が唐突で、よく分からなかった。","romaji":"Sono eiga wa ketsumatsu ga toutotsu de, yoku wakaranakatta.","german":"The movie's ending was so abrupt that I didn't really understand it.","furigana":"えいが|けつまつ|とうとつ|わ"}],"notes":"唐突 describes something that comes without warning or connection, especially people's remarks and actions: 唐突な質問, 唐突に話題を変える. As a polite opener one says 唐突ですが… \"Excuse me for bringing this up so abruptly\". Unlike 突然, which fits any sudden event (突然雨が降り出した), 唐突 stresses the lack of transition; it is not used for natural events.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この小説は、中世ヨーロッパの宮廷を舞台にしている。","romaji":"Kono shousetsu wa, chuusei Yooroppa no kyuutei o butai ni shite iru.","german":"This novel is set at a court in medieval Europe.","furigana":"しょうせつ|ちゅうせい|きゅうてい|ぶたい"},{"japanese":"雅楽は、もともと宮廷で演奏されていた音楽です。","romaji":"Gagaku wa, motomoto kyuutei de ensou sarete ita ongaku desu.","german":"Gagaku is music that was originally played at the imperial court.","furigana":"ががく|きゅうてい|えんそう|おんがく"},{"japanese":"北京で、かつての宮廷料理を味わった。","romaji":"Pekin de, katsute no kyuutei ryouri o ajiwatta.","german":"In Beijing, I tasted the former imperial court cuisine.","furigana":"|きゅうていりょうり|あじ"}],"notes":"宮廷 is the court of a monarch, as a place and as courtly society, mainly in historical or foreign contexts. It is common in compounds: 宮廷料理, 宮廷音楽, 宮廷画家. For the present Japanese imperial family one rather says 皇室, and for matters inside the Imperial Palace 宮中 (宮中晩餐会). The word is a pure noun and is placed before other nouns with の.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"表彰式には出席せずに帰ってきてしまいました。","romaji":"Hyōshōshiki ni wa shusseki sezu ni kaette kite shimaimashita.","german":"I went home without attending the award ceremony.","furigana":"ひょうしょうしき|しゅっせき|かえ"},{"japanese":"川でおぼれた子どもを助けた男性が、警察から表彰されました。","romaji":"Kawa de oboreta kodomo o tasuketa dansei ga, keisatsu kara hyoushou saremashita.","german":"A man who saved a child drowning in the river was commended by the police.","furigana":"かわ|こ|たす|だんせい|けいさつ|ひょうしょう"},{"japanese":"会社は、十年間一度も休まなかった社員を表彰した。","romaji":"Kaisha wa, juunenkan ichido mo yasumanakatta shain o hyoushou shita.","german":"The company honored an employee who had not taken a single day off in ten years.","furigana":"かいしゃ|じゅうねんかんいちど|やす|しゃいん|ひょうしょう"}],"notes":"表彰する means to officially honor someone for achievements or good deeds; the honor comes from an institution: 警察が市民を表彰する. Very common is the passive 表彰される, along with 表彰式 \"award ceremony\" and 表彰状 \"certificate of commendation\". For receiving a prize oneself, one says 受賞する, and everyday praise is 褒める. The word is neutral to formal and appears often in school, company, and news contexts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この辞書の改訂版が、来月出版されます。","romaji":"Kono jisho no kaiteiban ga, raigetsu shuppan saremasu.","german":"A revised edition of this dictionary will be published next month.","furigana":"じしょ|かいていばん|らいげつしゅっぱん"},{"japanese":"古い教科書ではなく、改訂版を使ってください。","romaji":"Furui kyoukasho de wa naku, kaiteiban o tsukatte kudasai.","german":"Please use the revised edition, not the old textbook.","furigana":"ふる|きょうかしょ|かいていばん|つか"},{"japanese":"マニュアルの改訂版では、いくつかの誤りが直されている。","romaji":"Manyuaru no kaiteiban de wa, ikutsuka no ayamari ga naosarete iru.","german":"The revised edition of the manual corrects several errors.","furigana":"かいていばん|あやま|なお"}],"notes":"改訂版 is the revised new edition of a book, dictionary, textbook or manual; 改訂 means the revision of the content of texts. Beware of the identically read 改定: that is the change of rules or prices (料金改定). Timetables are changed with 改正 (ダイヤ改正). Related terms are 新版, 増補版 \"expanded edition\" and 初版 \"first edition\".","levelNote":"Compound: 改訂 (N1) + 版 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この問題は取締役会に諮る必要があります。","romaji":"Kono mondai wa torishimariyakukai ni hakaru hitsuyō ga arimasu.","german":"Diese Frage müssen wir dem Vorstand zur Beratung vorlegen.","furigana":"もんだい|とりしまりやくかい|はか|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"新しい規則は、来週の会議に諮ってから決めます。","romaji":"Atarashii kisoku wa, raishuu no kaigi ni hakatte kara kimemasu.","german":"Über die neuen Regeln entscheiden wir, nachdem wir sie in der Sitzung nächste Woche besprochen haben.","furigana":"あたら|きそく|らいしゅう|かいぎ|はか|き"},{"japanese":"市は計画案を専門家の委員会に諮った。","romaji":"Shi wa keikakuan o senmonka no iinkai ni hakatta.","german":"Die Stadt legte den Planentwurf einem Expertenausschuss zur Beratung vor.","furigana":"し|けいかくあん|せんもんか|いいんかい|はか"}],"notes":"諮る bedeutet, eine Angelegenheit einem Gremium zur Beratung vorzulegen: AをBに諮る (議案を会議に諮る). Es ist formelles Behörden- und Firmenjapanisch; im Alltag sagt man 相談する. Vorsicht bei den gleich gelesenen Verben: 図る „planen, anstreben“, 計る・測る・量る „messen“, 謀る „etwas aushecken“. Nicht verwechseln mit dem deutschen „jemanden beraten“; dafür steht eher アドバイスする.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"後どれくらいでお客さんに推奨する銘柄が決まりそうですか。","romaji":"Ato dore kurai de okyakusan ni suishō suru meigara ga kimarisō desu ka.","german":"About how long until it's decided which stocks we'll recommend to customers?","furigana":"あと|きゃく|すいしょう|めいがら|き"},{"japanese":"日本酒はどの銘柄が人気ですか。","romaji":"Nihonshu wa dono meigara ga ninki desu ka.","german":"Which brands of sake are popular?","furigana":"にほんしゅ|めいがら|にんき"},{"japanese":"父はいつも同じ銘柄のたばこを吸っている。","romaji":"Chichi wa itsumo onaji meigara no tabako o sutte iru.","german":"My father always smokes the same brand of cigarettes.","furigana":"ちち|おな|めいがら|す"}],"notes":"銘柄 denotes a particular \"brand, variety\" of a product, especially for sake, rice, tea, tobacco, or beef (銘柄米, 銘柄牛). On the stock exchange, 銘柄 is a \"security, issue\", i.e. a single stock: 注目銘柄, 銘柄を選ぶ. For brands of companies, fashion, or electronics one rather says ブランド or メーカー; 銘柄 stresses the variety or designation of origin.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"参加者はめいめい弁当を持ってきてください。","romaji":"Sankasha wa meimei bentou o motte kite kudasai.","german":"Participants, please each bring your own lunch.","furigana":"さんかしゃ|べんとう|も"},{"japanese":"子どもたちは銘々好きな絵を描き始めた。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa meimei suki na e o kakihajimeta.","german":"The children each began drawing whatever they liked.","furigana":"こ|めいめいす|え|か|はじ"},{"japanese":"ケーキを銘々の皿に取り分けた。","romaji":"Keeki o meimei no sara ni toriwaketa.","german":"I divided the cake onto the individual plates.","furigana":"めいめい|さら|と|わ"}],"notes":"銘々 means \"each one for themselves\" and is mostly written in kana, めいめい. It most often stands adverbially before the verb (めいめい好きな物を注文する) or before nouns with の (銘々の皿). Also well known is 銘々皿, small individual plates. それぞれ is more general and also applies to things; 各自 sounds like an instruction or rule (各自で持参すること).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の息子は、ある日突然、堰を切ったように日本語をペラペラ話し始めたんです。","romaji":"Watashi no musuko wa, aru hi totsuzen, seki o kitta yō ni nihongo o perapera hanashi hajimeta n desu.","german":"One day my son suddenly started speaking Japanese fluently, as if a dam had burst inside him.","furigana":"わたし|むすこ|ひとつぜん|せき|き|にほんご|はな|はじ"},{"japanese":"この川では、堰で水の量を調節している。","romaji":"Kono kawa de wa, seki de mizu no ryou o chousetsu shite iru.","german":"On this river, the amount of water is regulated by a weir.","furigana":"かわ|せき|みず|りょう|ちょうせつ"},{"japanese":"母の顔を見たとたん、彼女は堰を切ったように泣き出した。","romaji":"Haha no kao o mita totan, kanojo wa seki o kitta you ni nakidashita.","german":"The moment she saw her mother's face, she burst into tears.","furigana":"はは|かお|み|かのじょ|せき|き|な|だ"}],"notes":"堰 is a weir or small dam in a river or irrigation channel, used to regulate the amount of water; a large dam is called ダム. The word is most often met in the expression 堰を切ったように \"as if a dam had burst\": feelings, tears, or words suddenly pour out (堰を切ったように泣き出す). Related is the verb 堰き止める \"to dam up\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"マストが折れてヨットが漂流し始めた時は、さすがに不安になりました。","romaji":"Masuto ga orete yotto ga hyouryuu shihajimeta toki wa, sasuga ni fuan ni narimashita.","german":"When the mast broke and the yacht began to drift, I did get anxious.","furigana":"お|ひょうりゅう|はじ|とき|ふあん"},{"japanese":"漁船は三日間太平洋を漂流した後、無事に救助された。","romaji":"Gyosen wa mikkakan Taiheiyou o hyouryuu shita ato, buji ni kyuujo sareta.","german":"The fishing boat drifted on the Pacific for three days before being safely rescued.","furigana":"ぎょせん|みっかかんたいへいよう|ひょうりゅう|あと|ぶじ|きゅうじょ"},{"japanese":"議論がまとまらず、計画は漂流したままだ。","romaji":"Giron ga matomarazu, keikaku wa hyouryuu shita mama da.","german":"The discussion is not coming together, and the plan is still adrift.","furigana":"ぎろん|けいかく|ひょうりゅう"}],"notes":"漂流する is intransitive and describes a ship or person floating helplessly on the water: 海を漂流する, 海上を漂流する; also 漂流物 \"flotsam\". Figuratively it also means that a policy or project drifts aimlessly. 漂う is more general, \"to float, drift\" (also for scents), and 漂着 is the end of drifting, landing on a coast.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"傷ついた赤ちゃん鯨が、とある砂浜に漂着するところを想像してみてください。","romaji":"Kizutsuita akachan kujira ga, toaru sunahama ni hyōchaku suru tokoro o sōzō shite mite kudasai.","german":"Imagine an injured baby whale washing up on a sandy beach.","furigana":"きず|あか|くじら|すなはま|ひょうちゃく|そうぞう"},{"japanese":"海岸には、外国から流れてきたごみが大量に漂着している。","romaji":"Kaigan ni wa, gaikoku kara nagarete kita gomi ga tairyou ni hyouchaku shite iru.","german":"A large amount of trash from abroad has washed up on the coast.","furigana":"かいがん|がいこく|なが|たいりょう|ひょうちゃく"},{"japanese":"嵐で流された漁師たちは、一週間後に無人島に漂着した。","romaji":"Arashi de nagasareta ryoushitachi wa, isshuukan go ni mujintou ni hyouchaku shita.","german":"The fishermen who were swept away by the storm drifted ashore on an uninhabited island a week later.","furigana":"あらし|なが|りょうし|いっしゅうかんご|むじんとう|ひょうちゃく"}],"notes":"漂着する means to land on a coast or be washed up after drifting on the water; the place takes に: 島に漂着する. Common are 漂着物 and 漂着ごみ \"washed-up trash\". The word is literary; colloquially one says 流れ着く, and for animals thrown ashore by waves also 打ち上げられる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"社長は、辞めると言っていた部長に翻意を促した。","romaji":"Shachou wa, yameru to itte ita buchou ni hon'i o unagashita.","german":"The company president urged the department head, who had said he would quit, to reconsider.","furigana":"しゃちょう|や|い|ぶちょう|ほんい|うなが"},{"japanese":"仲間に説得され、彼は翻意して現役を続けることにした。","romaji":"Nakama ni settoku sare, kare wa hon'i shite gen'eki o tsuzukeru koto ni shita.","german":"Persuaded by his teammates, he changed his mind and decided to keep playing.","furigana":"なかま|せっとく|かれ|ほんい|げんえき|つづ"},{"japanese":"一度決めたら、彼女が翻意することはまずない。","romaji":"Ichido kimetara, kanojo ga hon'i suru koto wa mazu nai.","german":"Once she has decided, she hardly ever changes her mind.","furigana":"いちどき|かのじょ|ほんい"}],"notes":"翻意 is a formal word for reversing a decision one has made, especially an announced intention such as resignation or quitting. Typical are 翻意する, 翻意を促す \"to urge someone to reconsider\", and 翻意させる. It appears mainly in the news and reports; in daily life one says 気が変わる, 考えを変える, or 思い直す.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このビデオでは、ワニカニ誕生からこれまでの軌跡をたどります。","romaji":"Kono bideo de wa, wani kani tanjō kara kore made no kiseki o tadorimasu.","german":"In this video, we trace the path of WaniKani from its birth to the present.","furigana":"たんじょう|きせき"},{"japanese":"展示会では、画家の五十年にわたる創作の軌跡を振り返ることができる。","romaji":"Tenjikai de wa, gaka no gojuunen ni wataru sousaku no kiseki o furikaeru koto ga dekiru.","german":"The exhibition lets you look back on the painter's fifty-year creative journey.","furigana":"てんじかい|がか|ごじゅうねん|そうさく|きせき|ふ|かえ"},{"japanese":"夜空に、流れ星が美しい軌跡を描いた。","romaji":"Yozora ni, nagareboshi ga utsukushii kiseki o egaita.","german":"A shooting star traced a beautiful arc across the night sky.","furigana":"よぞら|なが|ぼし|うつく|きせき|えが"}],"notes":"軌跡 literally means \"wheel track\", but it is mostly used figuratively: the path a person or organization has traveled, typically 〜の軌跡をたどる or 〜の軌跡を振り返る. Concretely it also denotes the path of a moving object (ボールの軌跡) and, in mathematics, a locus. Do not confuse it with the identically read 奇跡 \"miracle\"; 足跡 rather stresses traces and achievements left behind.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"天皇皇后両陛下が被災地を訪問されました。","romaji":"Tennou kougou ryouheika ga hisaichi o houmon saremashita.","german":"Their Majesties the Emperor and Empress visited the disaster-stricken area.","furigana":"てんのうこうごうりょうへいか|ひさいち|ほうもん"},{"japanese":"歴史の授業で、ナポレオンの皇后ジョゼフィーヌについて学んだ。","romaji":"Rekishi no jugyou de, Naporeon no kougou Jozefiinu ni tsuite mananda.","german":"In history class we learned about Joséphine, Napoleon's empress.","furigana":"れきし|じゅぎょう|こうごう|まな"},{"japanese":"皇后は外国の大使夫妻を笑顔で迎えた。","romaji":"Kougou wa gaikoku no taishi fusai o egao de mukaeta.","german":"The Empress welcomed the foreign ambassador and his wife with a smile.","furigana":"こうごう|がいこく|たいしふさい|えがお|むか"}],"notes":"皇后 is the empress as consort of an emperor; in Japan respectfully 皇后陛下, and together with the Emperor 天皇皇后両陛下. An empress who rules in her own right is a 女帝, a queen is a 女王, and the consort of a king is an 王妃. The word belongs to the formal vocabulary of the news and history; when speaking about the Japanese Empress in the news, honorific forms are used.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"明日から旅行だと思うと、興奮してなかなか眠れなかった。","romaji":"Ashita kara ryokou da to omou to, koufun shite nakanaka nemurenakatta.","german":"Thinking that the trip starts tomorrow, I was so excited that I couldn't sleep for a long time.","furigana":"あした|りょこう|おも|こうふん|ねむ"},{"japanese":"試合終了直前のゴールに、観客は興奮して立ち上がった。","romaji":"Shiai shuuryou chokuzen no gooru ni, kankyaku wa koufun shite tachiagatta.","german":"At the goal just before the end of the match, the spectators jumped to their feet in excitement.","furigana":"しあいしゅうりょうちょくぜん|かんきゃく|こうふん|た|あ"},{"japanese":"少し興奮しているようですね。まず落ち着いて話してください。","romaji":"Sukoshi koufun shite iru you desu ne. Mazu ochitsuite hanashite kudasai.","german":"You seem a little worked up. First calm down and then speak.","furigana":"すこ|こうふん|お|つ|はな"}],"notes":"興奮する is intransitive and means \"to be excited, aroused\", both positively and negatively: from joy, from anger, or physically aroused. Typical are 興奮して眠れない, 興奮を抑える, 興奮気味, and the causative 興奮させる. わくわくする is only joyful anticipation, and 熱狂 is the enthusiasm of a crowd. To calm someone down, one says 興奮しないで、落ち着いて.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女の一言が彼を奮起させ、彼は必死に受験勉強を始めた。","romaji":"Kanojo no hitokoto ga kare o funki sase, kare wa hisshi ni juken benkyou o hajimeta.","german":"A single remark from her spurred him on, and he began studying desperately for the entrance exam.","furigana":"かのじょ|ひとこと|かれ|ふんき|かれ|ひっし|じゅけんべんきょう|はじ"},{"japanese":"前半で大きくリードされたが、チームは後半に奮起して逆転した。","romaji":"Zenhan de ookiku riido sareta ga, chiimu wa kouhan ni funki shite gyakuten shita.","german":"The team was far behind in the first half, but rallied in the second half and turned the game around.","furigana":"ぜんはん|おお|こうはん|ふんき|ぎゃくてん"},{"japanese":"業績の悪化を受け、社長は社員の奮起を求めた。","romaji":"Gyouseki no akka o uke, shachou wa shain no funki o motometa.","german":"In response to worsening business results, the president called on employees to rise to the occasion.","furigana":"ぎょうせき|あっか|う|しゃちょう|しゃいん|ふんき|もと"}],"notes":"奮起 means to rouse oneself and gather fresh strength, often after a setback or a reprimand. As a する-verb it is intransitive (チームが奮起する); to spur someone on is 奮起させる, and to urge someone to rouse themselves is 奮起を促す. Compared with 頑張る, 奮起 is more formal and stresses the moment when someone takes up new fighting spirit; it is common in sports reports and speeches.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"駅前の「さくら亭」というラーメン屋には、いつも行列ができている。","romaji":"Ekimae no \"Sakura-tei\" to iu raamen'ya ni wa, itsumo gyouretsu ga dekite iru.","german":"There is always a line at the ramen shop \"Sakura-tei\" in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|てい|や|ぎょうれつ"},{"japanese":"落語家の名前には、「三遊亭」や「古今亭」のように「亭」がつくものが多い。","romaji":"Rakugoka no namae ni wa, \"San'yuutei\" ya \"Kokontei\" no you ni \"tei\" ga tsuku mono ga ooi.","german":"Many rakugo performers have names with \"-tei\", such as \"San'yūtei\" or \"Kokontei\".","furigana":"らくごか|なまえ|さんゆうてい|ここんてい|てい|おお"},{"japanese":"父は、老舗の洋食屋「みどり亭」で三十年働いている。","romaji":"Chichi wa, shinise no youshokuya \"Midori-tei\" de sanjuunen hataraite iru.","german":"My father has worked for thirty years at the long-established Western-style restaurant \"Midori-tei\".","furigana":"ちち|しにせ|ようしょくや|てい|さんじゅうねんはたら"}],"notes":"〜亭 is a suffix that originally means \"pavilion, arbor\" and today mainly forms names of restaurants and eateries (〇〇亭), especially traditional Japanese ones; an upscale Japanese restaurant is called 料亭. It also forms stage names of rakugo performers (三遊亭, 古今亭) and of some writers (二葉亭四迷). On its own as a word it is hardly used.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"真美がベーコンをあんなに速く食べられることに、びっくり仰天しました。","romaji":"Mami ga beekon o anna ni hayaku taberareru koto ni, bikkuri gyouten shimashita.","german":"I was totally amazed at how fast Mami could eat bacon.","furigana":"まみ|はや|た|ぎょうてん"},{"japanese":"請求書の金額を見て、仰天した。","romaji":"Seikyuusho no kingaku o mite, gyouten shita.","german":"When I saw the amount on the bill, I was astounded.","furigana":"せいきゅうしょ|きんがく|み|ぎょうてん"},{"japanese":"有名歌手の突然の引退発表に、ファンは仰天した。","romaji":"Yuumei kashu no totsuzen no intai happyou ni, fan wa gyouten shita.","german":"Fans were shocked by the famous singer's sudden retirement announcement.","furigana":"ゆうめいかしゅ|とつぜん|いんたいはっぴょう|ぎょうてん"}],"notes":"仰天する means \"to be stunned with amazement\", literally \"to look up at the sky\". The intensified expression びっくり仰天する is very common, rather colloquial and a bit humorous. In headlines, 仰天 stands for sensational news (仰天ニュース). 驚く is the neutral basic word, and 唖然とする describes speechless shock.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"伯母はどうして私達の結婚式に来ようとしなかったんだろう。","romaji":"Oba wa dōshite watashitachi no kekkonshiki ni koyō to shinakatta n darō.","german":"I wonder why my aunt didn't want to come to our wedding.","furigana":"おば|わたしたち|けっこんしき|こ"},{"japanese":"伯母は母より五つ年上で、昔から私をかわいがってくれた。","romaji":"Oba wa haha yori itsutsu toshiue de, mukashi kara watashi o kawaigatte kureta.","german":"My aunt is five years older than my mother and has always doted on me.","furigana":"おば|はは|いつ|としうえ|むかし|わたし"},{"japanese":"夏休みは、田舎の伯母の家に泊まった。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi wa, inaka no oba no ie ni tomatta.","german":"During summer vacation, I stayed at my aunt's house in the countryside.","furigana":"なつやす|いなか|おば|いえ|と"}],"notes":"伯母 is an aunt who is older than one's own parent (the older sister of one's father or mother, or the wife of an older uncle). The younger aunt is written 叔母; both are read おば, and in conversation often simply おばさん. For other people's aunts one politely says おばさま or おばさん. The kanji spelling is needed mainly in writing.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"偶然の一致に思わず笑ってしまった。","romaji":"Gūzen no itchi ni omowazu waratte shimatta.","german":"I couldn't help laughing at the coincidence.","furigana":"ぐうぜん|いっち|おも|わら"},{"japanese":"駅で偶然、高校時代の友達に会った。","romaji":"Eki de guuzen, koukou jidai no tomodachi ni atta.","german":"I ran into a friend from high school at the station by chance.","furigana":"えき|ぐうぜん|こうこうじだい|ともだち|あ"},{"japanese":"彼が同じ日に休みを取ったのは、偶然ではないと思う。","romaji":"Kare ga onaji hi ni yasumi o totta no wa, guuzen de wa nai to omou.","german":"I don't think it's a coincidence that he took the same day off.","furigana":"かれ|おな|ひ|やす|と|ぐうぜん|おも"}],"notes":"偶然 is a noun, a な-adjective and an adverb: 偶然の一致 \"coincidental match\", 偶然に・偶然 \"by chance\" (駅で偶然会った), 偶然にも \"coincidentally\". The opposite is 必然 \"necessity\". たまたま means almost the same, but is only an adverb and more colloquial. The phrase 偶然ではない \"no coincidence\" is also popular.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"汚い水にも怯まず、彼女は淀川で泳いだ。","romaji":"Kitanai mizu ni mo hirumazu, kanojo wa Yodogawa de oyoida.","german":"Undaunted by the dirty water, she swam in the Yodo River.","furigana":"きたな|みず|ひる|かのじょ|よどがわ|およ"},{"japanese":"淀川は琵琶湖から流れ出て、大阪湾に注いでいる。","romaji":"Yodogawa wa Biwako kara nagaredete, Oosakawan ni sosoide iru.","german":"The Yodo River flows out of Lake Biwa and empties into Osaka Bay.","furigana":"よどがわ|びわこ|なが|で|おおさかわん|そそ"},{"japanese":"毎年夏には、淀川の河川敷で大きな花火大会が開かれる。","romaji":"Maitoshi natsu ni wa, Yodogawa no kasenjiki de ookina hanabi taikai ga hirakareru.","german":"Every summer, a large fireworks festival is held on the Yodo River bank.","furigana":"まいとしなつ|よどがわ||おお|はなびたいかい|ひら"}],"notes":"淀川 is the Yodo River in Kansai: it flows out of Lake Biwa, is called 瀬田川 and 宇治川 in its upper course, and empties into Osaka Bay in Osaka. 〜川 is read here with voiced がわ. The 淀 in the name means \"still, stagnant water\", as in the verb 淀む. In Osaka the river is known for its riverside meadows and the big summer fireworks.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"近年その古墳を訪れる人が増えています。","romaji":"Kinnen sono kofun o otozureru hito ga fuete imasu.","german":"In recent years the number of people visiting that burial mound has been increasing.","furigana":"きんねん|こふん|おとず|ひと|ふ"},{"japanese":"堺市には、日本最大の古墳である大仙古墳があります。","romaji":"Sakai-shi ni wa, Nihon saidai no kofun de aru Daisen Kofun ga arimasu.","german":"In Sakai there is the Daisen Kofun, Japan's largest burial mound.","furigana":"|にほんさいだい|こふん|だいせんこふん"},{"japanese":"工事中に古墳が見つかり、建設は一時中止された。","romaji":"Koujichuu ni kofun ga mitsukari, kensetsu wa ichiji chuushi sareta.","german":"A burial mound was discovered during construction, and the building work was temporarily halted.","furigana":"こうじちゅう|こふん|み|けんせつ|いちじちゅうし"}],"notes":"古墳 are ancient burial mounds, especially from the 古墳時代 named after them (roughly the 3rd to 7th century). Famous are the keyhole-shaped 前方後円墳, such as the 大仙古墳 in Sakai. 墓 is an ordinary grave, and 墳墓 is the formal word for a burial site. In the news one often reads 古墳が見つかる, 古墳を発掘する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ツタンカーメンの墳墓は絶対に訪れた方がいいよ。","romaji":"Tsutankāmen no funbo wa zettai ni otozureta hō ga ii yo.","german":"You should definitely visit Tutankhamun's tomb.","furigana":"ふんぼ|ぜったい|おとず|ほう"},{"japanese":"発掘調査で、王族のものとみられる墳墓が見つかった。","romaji":"Hakkutsu chousa de, ouzoku no mono to mirareru funbo ga mitsukatta.","german":"In an excavation, a tomb thought to belong to a member of the royal family was found.","furigana":"はっくつちょうさ|おうぞく|ふんぼ|み"},{"japanese":"墳墓を荒らす行為は、法律で厳しく罰せられる。","romaji":"Funbo o arasu koui wa, houritsu de kibishiku basserareru.","german":"Desecrating tombs is severely punished by law.","furigana":"ふんぼ|あ|こうい|ほうりつ|きび|ばっ"}],"notes":"墳墓 is a formal, literary word for a grave or burial site, mainly in archaeology, law, and elevated language; 墳墓の地 is the homeland where one's ancestors are buried. In daily life one says 墓 or お墓, and for a cemetery 墓地. For the ancient Japanese burial mounds there is the special word 古墳.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼のお兄さんは壮年期に心臓発作を起こして亡くなりました。","romaji":"Kare no oniisan wa sounenki ni shinzou hossa o okoshite nakunarimashita.","german":"His older brother had a heart attack in the prime of life and died.","furigana":"かれ|にい|そうねんき|しんぞうほっさ|お|な"},{"japanese":"この病気は、壮年の男性に多いと言われている。","romaji":"Kono byouki wa, sounen no dansei ni ooi to iwarete iru.","german":"This disease is said to be common in men in their prime.","furigana":"びょうき|そうねん|だんせい|おお|い"},{"japanese":"町の剣道大会には、青年の部と壮年の部がある。","romaji":"Machi no kendou taikai ni wa, seinen no bu to sounen no bu ga aru.","german":"The town's kendo tournament has a youth division and a senior division.","furigana":"まち|けんどうたいかい|せいねん|ぶ|そうねん|ぶ"}],"notes":"壮年 is the time of full vigor in life, roughly from the thirties into the fifties, often as 壮年期 or 壮年層. Unlike 中年 \"middle age\", which sounds neutral to slightly negative, 壮年 stresses energy and capability. In sports, 壮年の部 denotes the older age class. The word is rather literary; counterparts are 青年 and 老年.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"金曜日に彼の壮行会を予定しているんですが、ご都合はいかがですか。","romaji":"Kin'youbi ni kare no soukoukai o yotei shite iru n desu ga, go-tsugou wa ikaga desu ka.","german":"We're planning a send-off party for him on Friday. Are you available?","furigana":"きんようび|かれ|そうこうかい|よてい|つごう"},{"japanese":"オリンピック代表選手の壮行会が、市役所で開かれた。","romaji":"Orinpikku daihyou senshu no soukoukai ga, shiyakusho de hirakareta.","german":"A send-off ceremony for the Olympic team members was held at city hall.","furigana":"だいひょうせんしゅ|そうこうかい|しやくしょ|ひら"},{"japanese":"海外遠征の前に、チームは壮行試合で地元のファンに勝利を見せた。","romaji":"Kaigai ensei no mae ni, chiimu wa soukou jiai de jimoto no fan ni shouri o miseta.","german":"Before the overseas tour, the team gave local fans a victory in a send-off match.","furigana":"かいがいえんせい|まえ|そうこうじあい|じもと|しょうり|み"}],"notes":"壮行 is a ceremonious, encouraging send-off for someone setting out on a great undertaking, such as a competition, an overseas assignment, or an expedition. The word hardly stands alone; it is used almost only in 壮行会 \"send-off party with good wishes\" and 壮行試合 \"send-off match\". A 送別会, by contrast, bids farewell to someone who is leaving and not coming back (transfer, retirement).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このレストランには、食事やその他の細々したものを運搬するためのミニエレベータがあります。","romaji":"Kono resutoran ni wa, shokuji ya sonota no komagoma shita mono o unpan suru tame no mini erebēta ga arimasu.","german":"This restaurant has a mini elevator for transporting meals and other small items.","furigana":"しょくじ|た||うんぱん"},{"japanese":"工事現場では、クレーンで資材を運搬している。","romaji":"Kouji genba de wa, kureen de shizai o unpan shite iru.","german":"At the construction site, materials are being transported by crane.","furigana":"こうじげんば|しざい|うんぱん"},{"japanese":"ピアノの運搬は、専門の業者に頼んだほうが安全です。","romaji":"Piano no unpan wa, senmon no gyousha ni tanonda hou ga anzen desu.","german":"It's safer to ask a specialist company to move a piano.","furigana":"うんぱん|せんもん|ぎょうしゃ|たの|あんぜん"}],"notes":"運搬する is transitive and refers to carrying mostly heavy goods or materials from one place to another, by vehicle, machine or by hand: 資材を運搬する, 運搬費, 運搬車. 運送 is rather the commercial transport business (運送会社), and 輸送 is transport on a large scale by rail, ship or aircraft, also of people. In everyday speech one simply says 運ぶ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"搬送作業時に左肩を脱臼してしまいました。","romaji":"Hansō sagyōji ni hidarikata o dakkyū shite shimaimashita.","german":"I dislocated my left shoulder during the transport work.","furigana":"はんそうさぎょうじ|ひだりかた|だっきゅう"},{"japanese":"事故でけがをした三人が、救急車で病院に搬送された。","romaji":"Jiko de kega o shita sannin ga, kyuukyuusha de byouin ni hansou sareta.","german":"Three people injured in the accident were taken to the hospital by ambulance.","furigana":"じこ|さんにん|きゅうきゅうしゃ|びょういん|はんそう"},{"japanese":"工場では、部品がベルトコンベヤーで次の工程へ搬送される。","romaji":"Koujou de wa, buhin ga beruto konbeyaa de tsugi no koutei e hansou sareru.","german":"In the factory, parts are conveyed to the next process by conveyor belt.","furigana":"こうじょう|ぶひん|つぎ|こうてい|はんそう"}],"notes":"搬送する is transitive and has two main meanings: transporting goods, for example in factories and warehouses (ベルトコンベヤーで搬送する), and taking sick or injured people to a hospital. The latter is very common in the news, almost always in the passive: 病院に搬送される, 救急搬送. In daily speech one says 運ぶ or 病院に運ばれる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日曜の夜七時までに全ての作品を搬出する必要があります。","romaji":"Nichiyō no yoru nanaji made ni subete no sakuhin o hanshutsu suru hitsuyō ga arimasu.","german":"All works must be carried out by 7 p.m. on Sunday.","furigana":"にちよう|よるななじ|すべ|さくひん|はんしゅつ|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"引っ越し業者は、午前中に家具を搬出し、午後に新居へ搬入する予定だ。","romaji":"Hikkoshi gyousha wa, gozenchuu ni kagu o hanshutsu shi, gogo ni shinkyo e hannyuu suru yotei da.","german":"The moving company plans to carry out the furniture in the morning and bring it into the new home in the afternoon.","furigana":"ひ|こ|ぎょうしゃ|ごぜんちゅう|かぐ|はんしゅつ|ごご|しんきょ|はんにゅう|よてい"},{"japanese":"火事の際、美術館の職員は重要な作品から順に搬出した。","romaji":"Kaji no sai, bijutsukan no shokuin wa juuyou na sakuhin kara jun ni hanshutsu shita.","german":"During the fire, museum staff carried out the most important works first.","furigana":"かじ|さい|びじゅつかん|しょくいん|じゅうよう|さくひん|じゅん|はんしゅつ"}],"notes":"搬出する means to carry heavy or valuable objects such as furniture, artworks, or equipment out of a building. Its counterpart is 搬入する \"to carry in\"; both are heard at moves, exhibitions, and events (搬入口, 搬出作業). In daily life one says 運び出す. The word is matter-of-fact and appears mainly in work instructions and technical language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"白熊が木の空洞の中でうたた寝をしていると、近くに木こりがやって来ました。","romaji":"Shirokuma ga ki no kūdō no naka de utatane o shite iru to, chikaku ni kikori ga yattekimashita.","german":"A polar bear was napping in a hollow in a tree when a lumberjack came by.","furigana":"しろくま|き|くうどう|なか|ね|ちか|き|き"},{"japanese":"道路の下に空洞が見つかり、通行止めになった。","romaji":"Douro no shita ni kuudou ga mitsukari, tsuukoudome ni natta.","german":"A cavity was found under the road, and it was closed to traffic.","furigana":"どうろ|した|くうどう|み|つうこうど"},{"japanese":"工場の海外移転で、国内産業の空洞化が進んでいる。","romaji":"Koujou no kaigai iten de, kokunai sangyou no kuudouka ga susunde iru.","german":"As factories move overseas, the hollowing-out of domestic industry is advancing.","furigana":"こうじょう|かいがいいてん|こくないさんぎょう|くうどうか|すす"}],"notes":"空洞 is a cavity inside something: 木の空洞, 地下の空洞; in medicine also cavities in the lung. Figuratively it means emptiness or lack of substance, and very common is 空洞化 \"hollowing-out\", such as 産業の空洞化 through the relocation of industry, or the desolation of city centers. 穴 is a hole in general, and a cave that one can walk into is a 洞窟.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その洞穴でつまずいて、足首を捻挫しました。","romaji":"Sono douketsu de tsumazuite, ashikubi o nenza shimashita.","german":"I tripped in that cave and sprained my ankle.","furigana":"どうけつ|あしくび|ねんざ"},{"japanese":"海岸の崖には、波に削られてできた洞穴がいくつもある。","romaji":"Kaigan no gake ni wa, nami ni kezurarete dekita douketsu ga ikutsu mo aru.","german":"In the cliffs along the coast there are many caves carved out by the waves.","furigana":"かいがん|がけ|なみ|けず|どうけつ"},{"japanese":"この洞穴からは、古代の人々が使っていた土器が見つかっている。","romaji":"Kono douketsu kara wa, kodai no hitobito ga tsukatte ita doki ga mitsukatte iru.","german":"Earthenware used by people in ancient times has been found in this cave.","furigana":"どうけつ|こだい|ひとびと|つか|どき|み"}],"notes":"洞穴 in the reading どうけつ is the literary, rather technical word for a natural cave, for example in geology and archaeology (洞穴遺跡). The same kanji are often read ほらあな in daily life. The most common word for \"cave\" is 洞窟; for an animal's hideout one says 巣穴.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その村の人々は、全員疫病で亡くなりました。","romaji":"Sono mura no hitobito wa, zen'in ekibyou de nakunarimashita.","german":"All the people of that village died of the epidemic.","furigana":"むら|ひとびと|ぜんいんえきびょう|な"},{"japanese":"昔の人々は、疫病が流行ると神社で祈りをささげた。","romaji":"Mukashi no hitobito wa, ekibyou ga hayaru to jinja de inori o sasageta.","german":"In the past, people prayed at shrines when an epidemic broke out.","furigana":"むかし|ひとびと|えきびょう|はや|じんじゃ|いの"},{"japanese":"中世ヨーロッパでは、疫病で人口の三分の一が失われたと言われる。","romaji":"Chuusei Yooroppa de wa, ekibyou de jinkou no sanbun no ichi ga ushinawareta to iwareru.","german":"In medieval Europe, a third of the population is said to have been lost to plague.","furigana":"ちゅうせい|えきびょう|じんこう|さんぶん|いち|うしな|い"}],"notes":"疫病 is a plague that spreads rapidly and sounds historical or literary: 疫病が流行る, 疫病で亡くなる. It can also be read やくびょう, as always in 疫病神 \"bringer of bad luck\". In modern news one mostly says 感染症, 伝染病, or パンデミック. Recently the formula 疫病退散 \"drive away the plague\" became well known again.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"飢えに苦しむ子どもたちの写真を見る度に胸が痛みます。","romaji":"Ue ni kurushimu kodomotachi no shashin o miru tabi ni mune ga itamimasu.","german":"My heart aches every time I see photos of starving children.","furigana":"う|くる|こ|しゃしん|み|たび|むね|いた"},{"japanese":"山で道に迷った二人は、木の実を食べて飢えをしのいだ。","romaji":"Yama de michi ni mayotta futari wa, ki no mi o tabete ue o shinoida.","german":"The two who got lost in the mountains staved off hunger by eating nuts and berries.","furigana":"やま|みち|まよ|ふたり|き|み|た|う"},{"japanese":"戦争中、祖父は飢えと寒さに耐えながら暮らしていたそうだ。","romaji":"Sensouchuu, sofu wa ue to samusa ni taenagara kurashite ita sou da.","german":"During the war, my grandfather is said to have lived enduring hunger and cold.","furigana":"せんそうちゅう|そふ|う|さむ|た|く"}],"notes":"飢え is the noun of 飢える and means serious hunger up to starvation, not just an empty stomach. Typical are 飢えに苦しむ, 飢えをしのぐ \"to stave off hunger\", and 飢え死に \"death by starvation\". Ordinary hunger is 空腹 or お腹が空く, and famine on a large scale is 飢餓. Figuratively the verb is mostly used: 愛情に飢える \"to hunger for love\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"父の弁当は、彩りについて全く考慮がされておらず、全部茶色だったので嫌でしたね。","romaji":"Chichi no bentou wa, irodori ni tsuite mattaku kouryo ga sarete orazu, zenbu chairo datta node iya deshita ne.","german":"I disliked my father's bento because he never considered the color scheme and everything was brown.","furigana":"ちち|べんとう|いろど|まった|こうりょ|ぜんぶちゃいろ|いや"},{"japanese":"サラダにトマトを入れると、彩りがよくなる。","romaji":"Sarada ni tomato o ireru to, irodori ga yoku naru.","german":"Adding tomatoes to a salad makes it more colorful.","furigana":"い|いろど"},{"japanese":"趣味の園芸が、退職後の生活に彩りを添えている。","romaji":"Shumi no engei ga, taishokugo no seikatsu ni irodori o soete iru.","german":"Gardening as a hobby adds color to his life in retirement.","furigana":"しゅみ|えんげい|たいしょくご|せいかつ|いろど|そ"}],"notes":"彩り is the noun of 彩る and means coloring and color coordination, especially when arranging food: 彩りがいい, 彩りを考える. Figuratively, 彩りを添える means \"to add some color or brilliance\", for example to life or a celebration. 色 is simply color, and 色合い is the shade; 彩り stresses the effect of the colors working together.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"俺の連れは今クラブで殴り合いの喧嘩の真っ最中さ。","romaji":"Ore no tsure wa ima kurabu de naguriai no kenka no massaichuu sa.","german":"My buddy is in the middle of a fistfight at the club right now.","furigana":"おれ|つ|いま|なぐ|あ|けんか|ま|さいちゅう"},{"japanese":"口論がエスカレートして、とうとう殴り合いになった。","romaji":"Kouron ga esukareeto shite, toutou naguriai ni natta.","german":"The argument escalated, and finally it came to blows.","furigana":"こうろん|なぐ|あ"},{"japanese":"試合中に選手同士の殴り合いが起き、二人とも退場になった。","romaji":"Shiaichuu ni senshu doushi no naguriai ga oki, futari tomo taijou ni natta.","german":"A fight broke out between players during the match, and both were sent off.","furigana":"しあいちゅう|せんしゅどうし|なぐ|あ|お|ふたり|たいじょう"}],"notes":"殴り合い comes from 殴り合う \"to hit each other\" and denotes a brawl with fists. Typical are 殴り合いのけんか and 殴り合いになる \"to come to blows\". けんか is more general and can also be a verbal fight (口げんか), and 取っ組み合い is a scuffle with grappling and grabbing. The word is neutral to colloquial.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"殴り込みの前に早めの昼食を取りました。","romaji":"Nagurikomi no mae ni hayame no chūshoku o torimashita.","german":"We ate an early lunch before the raid.","furigana":"なぐ|こ|まえ|はや|ちゅうしょく|と"},{"japanese":"映画の最後で、主人公はたった一人で敵の事務所に殴り込みをかける。","romaji":"Eiga no saigo de, shujinkou wa tatta hitori de teki no jimusho ni nagurikomi o kakeru.","german":"At the end of the movie, the hero storms the enemy's office all by himself.","furigana":"えいが|さいご|しゅじんこう|ひとり|てき|じむしょ|なぐ|こ"},{"japanese":"新しい会社が、格安スマホの市場に殴り込みをかけた。","romaji":"Atarashii kaisha ga, kakuyasu sumaho no shijou ni nagurikomi o kaketa.","german":"A new company has barged into the budget smartphone market.","furigana":"あたら|かいしゃ|かくやす|しじょう|なぐ|こ"}],"notes":"殴り込み is a violent raid on the headquarters of a rival group, typical of yakuza and action films; the set phrase is 殴り込みをかける. Figuratively it means forcing one's way vigorously into someone else's market or field: 業界に殴り込みをかける. The word is colloquial and sounds combative; more neutral are 襲撃 \"attack\" and 参入 \"market entry\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"麻布の服が好きなんだけど、結構お値段が高いんだよね。","romaji":"Asanuno no fuku ga suki na n da kedo, kekkou o-nedan ga takai n da yo ne.","german":"I like clothes made of linen, but they're quite expensive.","furigana":"あさぬの|ふく|す|けっこう|ねだん|たか"},{"japanese":"夏は麻布のシーツが涼しくて気持ちいい。","romaji":"Natsu wa asanuno no shiitsu ga suzushikute kimochi ii.","german":"In summer, linen sheets feel cool and pleasant.","furigana":"なつ|あさぬの|すず|きも"},{"japanese":"昔の農家では、麻布で作業着を作っていた。","romaji":"Mukashi no nouka de wa, asanuno de sagyougi o tsukutte ita.","german":"In the old days, farming families made their work clothes from hemp cloth.","furigana":"むかし|のうか|あさぬの|さぎょうぎ|つく"}],"notes":"麻布 in the reading あさぬの is cloth made of hemp or linen; the same kanji are also read まふ, and as a place name in Tokyo they are read あざぶ (Azabu). In fashion today one usually just says 麻 (麻のシャツ) or リネン. The fabric is especially valued in summer because it is cool and breathable.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"伯父は、鬱気で家でふさぎ込んでいる。","romaji":"Oji wa, ukki de ie de fusagikonde iru.","german":"My uncle is moping at home, depressed.","furigana":"おじ|うっき|いえ|こ"},{"japanese":"長雨が続くと、どうも鬱気がたまってしまう。","romaji":"Nagaame ga tsuzuku to, doumo ukki ga tamatte shimau.","german":"When the rain goes on for a long time, I somehow get gloomy.","furigana":"ながあめ|つづ|うっき"},{"japanese":"散歩でもして、鬱気を晴らそう。","romaji":"Sanpo demo shite, ukki o harasou.","german":"Let's take a walk to shake off this gloomy mood.","furigana":"さんぽ|うっき|は"}],"notes":"鬱気 is a rare, literary word for a depressed, melancholy mood. It appears in expressions like 鬱気を晴らす \"to dispel the gloomy mood\" or 鬱気がたまる. In daily life one says far more often 憂鬱, 気が滅入る, or ふさぎ込む; the illness depression is called うつ病 and is not the same thing.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"伯父は銀行員です。","romaji":"Oji wa ginkōin desu.","german":"My uncle is a bank clerk.","furigana":"おじ|ぎんこういん"},{"japanese":"伯父は父の兄で、田舎で農業をしています。","romaji":"Oji wa chichi no ani de, inaka de nougyou o shite imasu.","german":"My uncle is my father's older brother and farms in the countryside.","furigana":"おじ|ちち|あに|いなか|のうぎょう"},{"japanese":"正月には、伯父の家に親戚が集まる。","romaji":"Shougatsu ni wa, oji no ie ni shinseki ga atsumaru.","german":"At New Year, relatives gather at my uncle's house.","furigana":"しょうがつ|おじ|いえ|しんせき|あつ"}],"notes":"伯父 is an uncle who is older than one's own parent (the older brother of one's father or mother, or the husband of an older aunt). The younger uncle is written 叔父; both are read おじ, and in conversation mostly おじさん. When speaking of other people's uncles, one politely says おじさま or おじさん. Do not confuse it with 祖父 おじいさん \"grandfather\", with a long い.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの部屋に明かりが灯るのを見たんだ。","romaji":"Ano heya ni akari ga tomoru no o mita n da.","german":"I saw a light come on in that room.","furigana":"へや|あ|とも|み"},{"japanese":"日が暮れると、通りの街灯が一斉に灯った。","romaji":"Hi ga kureru to, toori no gaitou ga issei ni tomotta.","german":"When the sun went down, the streetlights along the road all came on at once.","furigana":"ひ|く|とお|がいとう|いっせい|とも"},{"japanese":"彼の言葉で、私の心に小さな希望の火が灯った。","romaji":"Kare no kotoba de, watashi no kokoro ni chiisana kibou no hi ga tomotta.","german":"His words kindled a small flame of hope in my heart.","furigana":"かれ|ことば|わたし|こころ|ちい|きぼう|ひ|とも"}],"notes":"灯る is intransitive: a light, lamp, or candle comes on and burns (明かりが灯る, 街灯が灯る); also written 点る. Its transitive counterpart is 灯す \"to light\" (ろうそくを灯す). In daily life, for electric light one mostly says 電気がつく; 灯る sounds more atmospheric and literary. Figuratively: 心に希望の火が灯る.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"豆、ちゃんと煮えてる？","romaji":"Mame, chanto niete ru?","german":"Are the beans cooked through properly?","furigana":"まめ|に"},{"japanese":"じゃがいもが柔らかく煮えたら、火を止めてください。","romaji":"Jagaimo ga yawarakaku nietara, hi o tomete kudasai.","german":"When the potatoes are cooked soft, please turn off the heat.","furigana":"やわ|に|ひ|と"},{"japanese":"鍋の肉がまだ煮えていないから、もう少し待って。","romaji":"Nabe no niku ga mada niete inai kara, mou sukoshi matte.","german":"The meat in the pot isn't cooked yet, so wait a little longer.","furigana":"なべ|にく|に|すこ|ま"}],"notes":"煮える is intransitive: food becomes cooked in liquid (豆が煮える, よく煮えた); its transitive counterpart is 煮る \"to simmer, cook\". For water that boils one says 沸く, and for rice 炊ける. A well-known expression is はらわたが煮えくり返る \"to seethe with rage\". Typical are 煮えるまで待つ and まだ煮えていない \"not cooked yet\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"線路には、何があっても入らないで下さい。","romaji":"Senro ni wa, nani ga atte mo hairanaide kudasai.","german":"Please never go onto the tracks, no matter what.","furigana":"せんろ|なに|はい|くだ"},{"japanese":"線路の近くに住んでいます。","romaji":"Senro no chikaku ni sunde imasu.","german":"I live near the railway tracks.","furigana":"せんろ|ちか|す"},{"japanese":"大雪で線路が埋まり、電車が運休になった。","romaji":"Ooyuki de senro ga umari, densha ga unkyuu ni natta.","german":"Heavy snow buried the tracks, and train service was suspended.","furigana":"おおゆき|せんろ|う|でんしゃ|うんきゅう"}],"notes":"線路 is the track or rail line as a concrete place: 線路を渡る, 線路沿い, 線路に落ちる. Do not confuse it with 路線, the route as a transport connection (バス路線, 路線図); railway lines have names ending in 〜線 (山手線). Platforms are called ホーム. In announcements one often hears 線路内に人が立ち入ったため \"because a person entered the tracks\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"一定 means \"fixed, constant, certain\". Before nouns it appears as 一定の: 一定の条件, 一定の期間, and 一定の効果 means \"a certain effect\". Adverbially one says 一定に保つ \"to keep constant\", and as a state 一定している \"is constant\". Unlike 特定 \"one very specific thing\", 一定 refers to a fixed amount or a constant level.","examples":[{"japanese":"一定の条件を満たす必要があります。","romaji":"Ittei no jōken o mitasu hitsuyō ga arimasu.","german":"Certain conditions must be met.","furigana":"いってい|じょうけん|み|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"温度を一定に保ってください。","romaji":"Ondo o ittei ni tamotte kudasai.","german":"Please keep the temperature constant.","furigana":"おんど|いってい|たも"},{"japanese":"彼の仕事の質は、いつも一定している。","romaji":"Kare no shigoto no shitsu wa, itsumo ittei shite iru.","german":"The quality of his work is always consistent.","furigana":"かれ|しごと|しつ|いってい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"安易 is a な-adjective and mostly meant critically: taken too lightly, without enough thought. Typical are 安易な考え, 安易な判断 and the adverbial 安易に〜する (安易に信じる). For neutral \"easy, simple\" one uses 簡単 or, formally, 容易; 安易, by contrast, criticizes the attitude of someone who takes the easy way out.","examples":[{"japanese":"安易な判断は避けるべきです。","romaji":"An'i na handan wa sakeru beki desu.","german":"Hasty judgments should be avoided.","furigana":"あんい|はんだん|さ"},{"japanese":"安易に約束して後悔しました。","romaji":"An'i ni yakusoku shite kōkai shimashita.","german":"I promised too casually and regretted it.","furigana":"あんい|やくそく|こうかい"},{"japanese":"ネットの情報を安易に信じてはいけない。","romaji":"Netto no jouhou o an'i ni shinjite wa ikenai.","german":"You shouldn't casually believe information on the internet.","furigana":"じょうほう|あんい|しん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"幾分 means \"somewhat, a little, to a certain degree\" and often appears with changes and comparisons: 幾分よくなる, 幾分楽になる, 〜より幾分〜. It is somewhat more formal than 少し and similar to いくらか or やや. As a noun, 幾分か also means \"a part\" (収入の幾分か). Written in kana, いくぶん, it is also common.","examples":[{"japanese":"朝より幾分暖かくなりました。","romaji":"Asa yori ikubun atatakaku narimashita.","german":"It has become somewhat warmer than this morning.","furigana":"あさ|いくぶんあたた"},{"japanese":"説明を聞いて幾分安心しました。","romaji":"Setsumei o kiite ikubun anshin shimashita.","german":"I felt somewhat reassured after hearing the explanation.","furigana":"せつめい|き|いくぶんあんしん"},{"japanese":"収入の幾分かを、毎月貯金に回している。","romaji":"Shuunyuu no ikubun ka o, maitsuki chokin ni mawashite iru.","german":"I put some of my income into savings every month.","furigana":"しゅうにゅう|いくぶん|まいつきちょきん|まわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"言い出す means to bring something up or to be the first to suggest it: 〜と言い出す \"to suddenly say that ...\", 誰が言い出したのか \"who was it that brought it up\". Very common is the negative potential 言い出せない \"cannot bring oneself to say\". 一度言い出したら聞かない describes stubborn people, and 言い出しっぺ is colloquial for \"the one who suggested it\". 話し出す, by contrast, only means \"to begin talking\".","examples":[{"japanese":"誰が最初にその案を言い出したのですか。","romaji":"Dare ga saisho ni sono an o iidashita no desu ka.","german":"Who first brought up that proposal?","furigana":"だれ|さいしょ|あん|い|だ"},{"japanese":"本当のことをなかなか言い出せませんでした。","romaji":"Hontō no koto o nakanaka iidasemasen deshita.","german":"I couldn't bring myself to say the truth for a long time.","furigana":"ほんとう|い|だ"},{"japanese":"うちの父は、一度言い出したら聞かない。","romaji":"Uchi no chichi wa, ichido iidashitara kikanai.","german":"Once my father says he'll do something, he won't listen to anyone.","furigana":"ちち|いちどい|だ|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"受け持つ means to be responsible for a task, a class, or an area: 〜を受け持つ. Typical in schools (三年生を受け持つ, 受け持ちの先生) and at the workplace. 担当する is the more common, matter-of-fact word at work, and 担任 specifically the homeroom teacher. 引き受ける stresses accepting a request, while 受け持つ stresses the ongoing area of responsibility.","examples":[{"japanese":"今年は三年生の授業を受け持っています。","romaji":"Kotoshi wa sannensei no jugyō o ukemotte imasu.","german":"This year I'm in charge of the third-year classes.","furigana":"ことし|さんねんせい|じゅぎょう|う|も"},{"japanese":"私は受付を受け持つことになりました。","romaji":"Watashi wa uketsuke o ukemotsu koto ni narimashita.","german":"I've been assigned to handle the reception.","furigana":"わたし|うけつけ|う|も"},{"japanese":"この地区の配達は、田中さんが受け持っています。","romaji":"Kono chiku no haitatsu wa, Tanaka-san ga ukemotte imasu.","german":"Mr. Tanaka is in charge of deliveries in this district.","furigana":"ちく|はいたつ|たなか|う|も"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"売り切れる is intransitive: the goods are sold out (商品が売り切れる). The transitive 売り切る describes the seller who sells everything. The noun 売り切れ is very common (売り切れ次第終了 \"while supplies last\"). More formally one says 完売する; in shops one hears the polite 売り切れております.","examples":[{"japanese":"人気のパンは午前中に売り切れました。","romaji":"Ninki no pan wa gozenchū ni urikiremashita.","german":"The popular bread was sold out in the morning.","furigana":"にんき|ごぜんちゅう|う|き"},{"japanese":"切符が売り切れる前に予約しましょう。","romaji":"Kippu ga urikireru mae ni yoyaku shimashō.","german":"Let's reserve before the tickets sell out.","furigana":"きっぷ|う|き|まえ|よやく"},{"japanese":"申し訳ございません。その商品は売り切れております。","romaji":"Moushiwake gozaimasen. Sono shouhin wa urikirete orimasu.","german":"We are very sorry. That item is sold out.","furigana":"もう|わけ|しょうひん|う|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"屋外 means \"outdoors, outside\" and is the opposite of 屋内. It sounds more matter-of-fact than 外 and often appears in notices and compounds: 屋外プール, 屋外での使用, 屋外イベント. 野外 stresses open nature or events under the open sky (野外コンサート), and 戸外 is literary. With the place particle: 屋外で.","examples":[{"japanese":"天気がよければ屋外で食事をします。","romaji":"Tenki ga yokereba okugai de shokuji o shimasu.","german":"If the weather is good, we'll eat outdoors.","furigana":"てんき|おくがい|しょくじ"},{"japanese":"この機械は屋外では使えません。","romaji":"Kono kikai wa okugai de wa tsukaemasen.","german":"This machine cannot be used outdoors.","furigana":"きかい|おくがい|つか"},{"japanese":"夏の間だけ、屋外プールが開いている。","romaji":"Natsu no aida dake, okugai puuru ga aite iru.","german":"The outdoor pool is open only during the summer.","furigana":"なつ|あいだ|おくがい|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"回数 is the number of times something happens, not the duration. Typical are 回数が多い／少ない, 回数を増やす／減らす, 回数を重ねる \"to do repeatedly\" and 回数券 \"book of tickets\". 頻度 is more formal and denotes frequency per unit of time; counting uses the counter 〜回.","examples":[{"japanese":"練習の回数を増やしました。","romaji":"Renshū no kaisū o fuyashimashita.","german":"I increased the number of practice sessions.","furigana":"れんしゅう|かいすう|ふ"},{"japanese":"利用できる回数には制限があります。","romaji":"Riyō dekiru kaisū ni wa seigen ga arimasu.","german":"There is a limit on the number of times you can use it.","furigana":"りよう|かいすう|せいげん"},{"japanese":"バスの回数券を買うと、少し安くなる。","romaji":"Basu no kaisuuken o kau to, sukoshi yasuku naru.","german":"If you buy a coupon book of bus tickets, it becomes a little cheaper.","furigana":"かいすうけん|か|すこ|やす"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"外部 denotes the \"outside\" - spatially (建物の外部 \"exterior of a building\") and above all organizationally: everything outside a company, group, or system. Typical are 外部の人・外部の専門家 \"outsiders\", 外部に漏れる/漏らす \"to leak\", and 外部からの侵入 \"intrusion from outside\". The counterpart is 内部. In daily life one simply says 外 for \"outside\"; 外部 sounds matter-of-fact and suits business and official language.","examples":[{"japanese":"外部の専門家に相談しました。","romaji":"Gaibu no senmonka ni sōdan shimashita.","german":"I consulted outside experts.","furigana":"がいぶ|せんもんか|そうだん"},{"japanese":"この情報を外部に伝えないでください。","romaji":"Kono jōhō o gaibu ni tsutaenaide kudasai.","german":"Please do not pass this information on to outsiders.","furigana":"じょうほう|がいぶ|つた"},{"japanese":"建物の外部は来年塗り替える予定です。","romaji":"Tatemono no gaibu wa rainen nurikaeru yotei desu.","german":"The exterior of the building is scheduled to be repainted next year.","furigana":"たてもの|がいぶ|らいねんぬ|か|よてい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"架空 means \"merely invented, not really existing\" and almost always stands with の before a noun: 架空の人物, 架空の町, 架空の話. In the news it often appears in connection with fraud: 架空請求 \"fake billing, fraudulent demand for payment\" or 架空の会社 \"shell company\". The opposite is 実在の \"actually existing\". Rare is the technical meaning \"overhead, laid in the air\" (架空線 \"overhead line\").","examples":[{"japanese":"物語の舞台は架空の町です。","romaji":"Monogatari no butai wa kakū no machi desu.","german":"The story is set in an imaginary town.","furigana":"ものがたり|ぶたい|かくう|まち"},{"japanese":"架空の数字を報告してはいけません。","romaji":"Kakū no sūji o hōkoku shite wa ikemasen.","german":"You must not report made-up figures.","furigana":"かくう|すうじ|ほうこく"},{"japanese":"架空請求のメールが届いたら、無視してください。","romaji":"Kakuu seikyuu no meeru ga todoitara, mushi shite kudasai.","german":"If you receive an email with a fake invoice, please ignore it.","furigana":"かくうせいきゅう|とど|むし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"貸し出し is the noun of 貸し出す \"to lend out\" and is used above all for libraries, rental stations, and banks (loans). Common are 貸し出し期間 \"loan period\", 貸し出し中 \"on loan\", and 貸し出しを行う. On signs and cards the short spelling 貸出 without し is often used (貸出カード). The counterpart from the user's side is 借りる, and returning is 返却.","examples":[{"japanese":"図書館の貸し出し期間は二週間です。","romaji":"Toshokan no kashidashi kikan wa nishūkan desu.","german":"The library's loan period is two weeks.","furigana":"としょかん|か|だ|きかん|にしゅうかん"},{"japanese":"自転車の貸し出しは受付で行っています。","romaji":"Jitensha no kashidashi wa uketsuke de okonatte imasu.","german":"Bicycle rentals are handled at the reception desk.","furigana":"じてんしゃ|か|だ|うけつけ|おこな"},{"japanese":"その本は今、貸し出し中です。","romaji":"Sono hon wa ima, kashidashichuu desu.","german":"That book is currently checked out.","furigana":"ほん|いま|か|だ|ちゅう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"加速 means \"acceleration\"; as a する-verb it is mostly intransitive: 車が加速する. Someone who actively speeds something up often says 〜を加速させる. Figuratively it is used for developments that become faster: 少子化が加速する, 改革を加速させる. The opposite is 減速 \"slowing down\"; the physics term \"acceleration (quantity)\" is 加速度.","examples":[{"japanese":"電車はゆっくり加速しました。","romaji":"Densha wa yukkuri kasoku shimashita.","german":"The train accelerated slowly.","furigana":"でんしゃ|かそく"},{"japanese":"技術の進歩が加速しています。","romaji":"Gijutsu no shinpo ga kasoku shite imasu.","german":"Technological progress is accelerating.","furigana":"ぎじゅつ|しんぽ|かそく"},{"japanese":"政府は改革を加速させる方針です。","romaji":"Seifu wa kaikaku o kasoku saseru houshin desu.","german":"The government intends to speed up the reforms.","furigana":"せいふ|かいかく|かそく|ほうしん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"記号 is an agreed sign or symbol that stands for something: 地図記号, 元素記号, 発音記号, and for passwords the \"special characters\" besides letters and digits. One says 記号を付ける \"to put a mark\" and 〜を表す記号. 文字, by contrast, are written characters, 印 is a simple mark, and マーク is used in daily life for logos and labels.","examples":[{"japanese":"この記号は何を表しますか。","romaji":"Kono kigō wa nani o arawashimasu ka.","german":"What does this symbol represent?","furigana":"きごう|なに|あらわ"},{"japanese":"地図の記号を確認しました。","romaji":"Chizu no kigō o kakunin shimashita.","german":"I checked the map symbols.","furigana":"ちず|きごう|かくにん"},{"japanese":"パスワードには数字と記号を入れてください。","romaji":"Pasuwaado ni wa suuji to kigou o irete kudasai.","german":"Please include numbers and symbols in your password.","furigana":"すうじ|きごう|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"休業 means that a shop, company, or school is temporarily not operating: 本日休業, 臨時休業 \"unscheduled closure\", 休業中. As a する-verb it is intransitive: 店が休業する. For employees there is 育児休業 \"childcare leave\" (short: 育休). To be distinguished: 定休日 is the regular closing day, 閉店 closing in the evening or for good, and 廃業 the final giving up of a business.","examples":[{"japanese":"明日は修理のため休業します。","romaji":"Ashita wa shūri no tame kyūgyō shimasu.","german":"We will be closed tomorrow for repairs.","furigana":"あした|しゅうり|きゅうぎょう"},{"japanese":"休業中は電話でお問い合わせください。","romaji":"Kyūgyōchū wa denwa de otoiawase kudasai.","german":"During the closure, please contact us by phone.","furigana":"きゅうぎょうちゅう|でんわ|と|あ"},{"japanese":"妻の出産に合わせて育児休業を取りました。","romaji":"Tsuma no shussan ni awasete ikuji kyuugyou o torimashita.","german":"I took childcare leave to coincide with my wife's delivery.","furigana":"つま|しゅっさん|あ|いくじきゅうぎょう|と"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"休養 is a longer rest to regain strength or health: 休養を取る, 休養が必要だ, しばらく休養する. It sounds more serious than 休み and is often used for illness, exhaustion, or for athletes and celebrities. 休憩, by contrast, is a short break during work, and 休息 is \"rest\" in general.","examples":[{"japanese":"週末は十分な休養を取りました。","romaji":"Shūmatsu wa jūbun na kyūyō o torimashita.","german":"I got plenty of rest over the weekend.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|じゅうぶん|きゅうよう|と"},{"japanese":"疲れがひどいので休養が必要です。","romaji":"Tsukare ga hidoi node kyūyō ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"I'm badly worn out, so I need rest.","furigana":"つか|きゅうよう|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"監督は選手たちに一週間の休養を与えました。","romaji":"Kantoku wa senshutachi ni isshuukan no kyuuyou o ataemashita.","german":"The manager gave the players a week of rest.","furigana":"かんとく|せんしゅ|いっしゅうかん|きゅうよう|あた"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"恐縮 expresses, in polite business language, that one feels indebted to someone or somewhat embarrassed. Most common is 恐縮ですが or お忙しいところ恐縮ですが as an introduction to a request (\"Sorry to trouble you, but ...\"). In response to praise or hospitality, one says 恐縮です or 恐縮しております (\"That is too great an honor\"). It is much more formal than すみません and belongs to humble language about oneself.","examples":[{"japanese":"恐縮ですが、もう一度ご説明ください。","romaji":"Kyōshuku desu ga, mō ichido gosetsumei kudasai.","german":"I'm sorry to trouble you, but could you explain once more?","furigana":"きょうしゅく|いちど|せつめい"},{"japanese":"丁寧に迎えていただき、恐縮しています。","romaji":"Teinei ni mukaete itadaki, kyōshuku shite imasu.","german":"I am overwhelmed by the kind welcome you gave me.","furigana":"ていねい|むか|きょうしゅく"},{"japanese":"お忙しいところ恐縮ですが、ご確認をお願いいたします。","romaji":"Oisogashii tokoro kyoushuku desu ga, gokakunin o onegai itashimasu.","german":"I apologize for troubling you when you are so busy, but I would ask you to check this.","furigana":"いそが|きょうしゅく|かくにん|ねが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"苦情 is a complaint about noise, service, defects, and so on. Typical: 苦情を言う \"to complain\", 苦情が来る/寄せられる \"complaints come in\", 苦情に対応する \"to handle complaints\". In daily life one often hears the loanword クレーム for it; 文句 is colloquial grumbling and nagging. 苦情 sounds matter-of-fact and suits companies, authorities, and property managers.","examples":[{"japanese":"騒音について苦情が寄せられました。","romaji":"Sōon ni tsuite kujō ga yoseraremashita.","german":"Complaints about the noise have been received.","furigana":"そうおん|くじょう|よ"},{"japanese":"客の苦情に丁寧に対応しました。","romaji":"Kyaku no kujō ni teinei ni taiō shimashita.","german":"I responded politely to the customer's complaint.","furigana":"きゃく|くじょう|ていねい|たいおう"},{"japanese":"店員の態度について店長に苦情を言いました。","romaji":"Ten'in no taido ni tsuite tenchou ni kujou o iimashita.","german":"I complained to the store manager about the clerk's attitude.","furigana":"てんいん|たいど|てんちょう|くじょう|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"形式 is the outer form or prescribed format: 書類の形式, ファイル形式, 面接形式 \"in the form of an interview\". It often stands in contrast to content (内容): 形式だけ \"only for form's sake\", 形式的な挨拶 \"a formal pleasantry\". 形 is the concrete shape of a thing, and 様式 is more a style (architectural style, lifestyle). With で one states in what form something takes place.","examples":[{"japanese":"指定された形式で書いてください。","romaji":"Shitei sareta keishiki de kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write in the specified format.","furigana":"してい|けいしき|か"},{"japanese":"形式だけでなく内容も大切です。","romaji":"Keishiki dake de naku naiyō mo taisetsu desu.","german":"Not only the form but also the content is important.","furigana":"けいしき|ないよう|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"今回の試験は面接形式で行われます。","romaji":"Konkai no shiken wa mensetsu keishiki de okonawaremasu.","german":"This exam will be conducted in interview format.","furigana":"こんかい|しけん|めんせつけいしき|おこな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"警備 is guarding and securing places, buildings, or events against dangers: 〜を警備する (transitive), 警備を強化する, 警備に当たる. The people are called 警備員, and the companies 警備会社. 警護 is personal protection for a specific person, and 警戒 means \"to be alert, on guard\".","examples":[{"japanese":"会場の警備を強化しました。","romaji":"Kaijō no keibi o kyōka shimashita.","german":"We have strengthened security at the venue.","furigana":"かいじょう|けいび|きょうか"},{"japanese":"入口は警備員が確認しています。","romaji":"Iriguchi wa keibiin ga kakunin shite imasu.","german":"Security guards are checking the entrance.","furigana":"いりぐち|けいびいん|かくにん"},{"japanese":"首相の訪問に備えて、多くの警察官が駅を警備しています。","romaji":"Shushou no houmon ni sonaete, ooku no keisatsukan ga eki o keibi shite imasu.","german":"In preparation for the prime minister's visit, many police officers are guarding the station.","furigana":"しゅしょう|ほうもん|そな|おお|けいさつかん|えき|けいび"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"見学 is a visit to learn about something by watching: 工場見学, 社会科見学, 見学会, 〜を見学する. Unlike 見物 (looking at sights for pleasure) or 観光, the aim is learning and information. In school, 見学する also means to just watch in PE class instead of taking part.","examples":[{"japanese":"学校の授業で工場を見学しました。","romaji":"Gakkō no jugyō de kōjō o kengaku shimashita.","german":"In a school class, we toured a factory.","furigana":"がっこう|じゅぎょう|こうじょう|けんがく"},{"japanese":"見学を希望する方は予約してください。","romaji":"Kengaku o kibō suru kata wa yoyaku shite kudasai.","german":"Those who wish to tour are asked to make a reservation.","furigana":"けんがく|きぼう|かた|よやく"},{"japanese":"足をけがしたので、今日の体育は見学します。","romaji":"Ashi o kega shita node, kyou no taiiku wa kengaku shimasu.","german":"I hurt my leg, so I'll just watch gym class today.","furigana":"あし|きょう|たいいく|けんがく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"厳重 is a な-adjective for measures that are strict and leave no gaps: 厳重な警備, 厳重なチェック, adverbially 厳重に管理する/注意する/抗議する. 厳重注意 is an official \"stern warning\". For strict people or teachers one says 厳しい; 厳重 describes the thoroughness of control and protection, not character.","examples":[{"japanese":"個人情報は厳重に管理しています。","romaji":"Kojin jōhō wa genjū ni kanri shite imasu.","german":"We manage personal information with strict care.","furigana":"こじんじょうほう|げんじゅう|かんり"},{"japanese":"事故の後、厳重な点検が行われました。","romaji":"Jiko no ato, genjū na tenken ga okonawaremashita.","german":"After the accident, a thorough inspection was carried out.","furigana":"じこ|あと|げんじゅう|てんけん|おこな"},{"japanese":"遅刻を繰り返したため、上司から厳重注意を受けました。","romaji":"Chikoku o kurikaeshita tame, joushi kara genjuu chuui o ukemashita.","german":"Because I was late repeatedly, I received a stern warning from my boss.","furigana":"ちこく|く|かえ|じょうし|げんじゅうちゅうい|う"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"原料 is the raw material that is processed and transformed during manufacture: 石油を原料とする, 〜の原料は木だ. 材料, by contrast, are materials or ingredients that remain recognizable in the product (cooking ingredients, building materials). On food labels, 原材料 usually appears as the umbrella term. Typical is the phrase 〜を原料にして/として作る.","examples":[{"japanese":"この紙の原料は木です。","romaji":"Kono kami no genryō wa ki desu.","german":"The raw material of this paper is wood.","furigana":"かみ|げんりょう|き"},{"japanese":"原料の価格が上がっています。","romaji":"Genryō no kakaku ga agatte imasu.","german":"The price of raw materials is rising.","furigana":"げんりょう|かかく|あ"},{"japanese":"プラスチックは主に石油を原料として作られています。","romaji":"Purasuchikku wa omo ni sekiyu o genryou to shite tsukurarete imasu.","german":"Plastic is mainly made from petroleum.","furigana":"おも|せきゆ|げんりょう|つく"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 37"},
{"notes":"交通機関 are the means of transport as a system, above all trains, buses, airplanes, and ships. One almost always hears 公共交通機関 \"public transportation\", for example in directions. For disruptions one says 交通機関が乱れる/止まる. For individual vehicles in daily life one says 乗り物; 交通 alone is \"traffic\" in general.","examples":[{"japanese":"公共交通機関を利用してください。","romaji":"Kōkyō kōtsū kikan o riyō shite kudasai.","german":"Please use public transportation.","furigana":"こうきょうこうつうきかん|りよう"},{"japanese":"雪で交通機関が乱れています。","romaji":"Yuki de kōtsū kikan ga midarete imasu.","german":"Transportation is disrupted because of the snow.","furigana":"ゆき|こうつうきかん|みだ"},{"japanese":"この町は交通機関が不便なので、車が必要です。","romaji":"Kono machi wa koutsuu kikan ga fuben na node, kuruma ga hitsuyou desu.","german":"Transportation in this town is inconvenient, so you need a car.","furigana":"まち|こうつうきかん|ふべん|くるま|ひつよう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"公表 means that authorities, companies, or institutions officially make information public, often information not previously known: 結果を公表する, 名前は公表されていない, 公表を控える \"to refrain from disclosure\". 発表 is more general (also presentations, results in school), and 公開 means \"to make accessible\" (films, data, websites). It often appears in the passive in the news.","examples":[{"japanese":"調査の結果が公表されました。","romaji":"Chōsa no kekka ga kōhyō saremashita.","german":"The results of the survey were made public.","furigana":"ちょうさ|けっか|こうひょう"},{"japanese":"会社は来月、新しい計画を公表します。","romaji":"Kaisha wa raigetsu, atarashii keikaku o kōhyō shimasu.","german":"The company will announce its new plan next month.","furigana":"かいしゃ|らいげつ|あたら|けいかく|こうひょう"},{"japanese":"被害者の名前は公表されていません。","romaji":"Higaisha no namae wa kouhyou sarete imasen.","german":"The victims' names have not been made public.","furigana":"ひがいしゃ|なまえ|こうひょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"心当たり is an inkling of what something is about or where something might be, because something fitting comes to mind. Almost always with ある/ない: 〜に心当たりがある/ない, politely asked 心当たりはありませんか. 心当たりを探す means \"to search everywhere one suspects it might be\". Similar is the verb 思い当たる; 見当がつく rather means \"to be able to make a rough estimate\".","examples":[{"japanese":"鍵をなくした場所に心当たりはありますか。","romaji":"Kagi o nakushita basho ni kokoroatari wa arimasu ka.","german":"Do you have any idea where you lost the key?","furigana":"かぎ|ばしょ|こころあ"},{"japanese":"その名前には心当たりがありません。","romaji":"Sono namae ni wa kokoroatari ga arimasen.","german":"I don't recognize that name.","furigana":"なまえ|こころあ"},{"japanese":"心当たりを全部探しましたが、財布は見つかりませんでした。","romaji":"Kokoroatari o zenbu sagashimashita ga, saifu wa mitsukarimasen deshita.","german":"I looked everywhere I could think of, but I couldn't find my wallet.","furigana":"こころあ|ぜんぶさが|さいふ|み"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"作成 is the creation of documents, plans, and data: 資料・書類・計画・報告書を作成する, and digitally アカウントやファイルを作成する. It sounds more matter-of-fact than 作る. Do not confuse it with 作製 (making concrete objects) or with 製作 and 制作 (production of goods and of artistic works, respectively). Often used as a noun: 〜の作成.","examples":[{"japanese":"会議の資料を作成しました。","romaji":"Kaigi no shiryō o sakusei shimashita.","german":"I prepared the materials for the meeting.","furigana":"かいぎ|しりょう|さくせい"},{"japanese":"申請書の作成には時間がかかります。","romaji":"Shinseisho no sakusei ni wa jikan ga kakarimasu.","german":"Preparing the application form takes time.","furigana":"しんせいしょ|さくせい|じかん"},{"japanese":"まずアカウントを作成してから、ログインしてください。","romaji":"Mazu akaunto o sakusei shite kara, roguin shite kudasai.","german":"First create an account, then log in.","furigana":"さくせい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"差し支え is a \"hindrance, problem\" and appears mainly in polite set phrases: 差し支えなければ \"if you don't mind\", 〜ても差し支えありません \"there is no objection to ...\". With に one indicates what the disruption affects: 仕事に差し支えが出る. The verb for it is 差し支える \"to be a hindrance\". The phrases are more formal than 大丈夫 or 構いません.","examples":[{"japanese":"差し支えなければ、お名前を教えてください。","romaji":"Sashitsukae nakereba, onamae o oshiete kudasai.","german":"If you don't mind, please tell me your name.","furigana":"さ|つか|なまえ|おし"},{"japanese":"明日は休んでも差し支えありません。","romaji":"Ashita wa yasunde mo sashitsukae arimasen.","german":"There is no problem if you take tomorrow off.","furigana":"あした|やす|さ|つか"},{"japanese":"睡眠不足が続くと、仕事に差し支えが出ます。","romaji":"Suimin busoku ga tsuzuku to, shigoto ni sashitsukae ga demasu.","german":"If lack of sleep continues, it will interfere with your work.","furigana":"すいみんぶそく|つづ|しごと|さ|つか|で"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"持参 means \"to bring along, take with one\" and appears above all in notices, invitations, and forms: 〜を持参する, politely ご持参ください, 〜をご持参のうえ. In conversation one mostly says 持ってくる/持っていく. Common are 弁当持参 and 各自持参 \"everyone brings their own\".","examples":[{"japanese":"当日は筆記用具を持参してください。","romaji":"Tōjitsu wa hikki yōgu o jisan shite kudasai.","german":"Please bring writing materials on the day.","furigana":"とうじつ|ひっきようぐ|じさん"},{"japanese":"昼食は各自で持参します。","romaji":"Chūshoku wa kakuji de jisan shimasu.","german":"Everyone brings their own lunch.","furigana":"ちゅうしょく|かくじ|じさん"},{"japanese":"印鑑をご持参のうえ、窓口へお越しください。","romaji":"Inkan o gojisan no ue, madoguchi e okoshi kudasai.","german":"Please bring your name seal and come to the counter.","furigana":"いんかん|じさん|まどぐち|こ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"実例 is an example from reality, not an invented one: 実例を挙げる/示す \"to cite a concrete example\". 例 is the general word, 具体例 stresses concreteness, and 事例 is used in reports and business for documented cases (成功事例). 実例 sounds somewhat literary.","examples":[{"japanese":"実例を挙げて説明してください。","romaji":"Jitsurei o agete setsumei shite kudasai.","german":"Please explain by giving a real example.","furigana":"じつれい|あ|せつめい"},{"japanese":"この本には役立つ実例が多く載っています。","romaji":"Kono hon ni wa yakudatsu jitsurei ga ōku notte imasu.","german":"This book contains many useful real-life examples.","furigana":"ほん|やくだ|じつれい|おお|の"},{"japanese":"実例を見ると、この方法の効果がよくわかります。","romaji":"Jitsurei o miru to, kono houhou no kouka ga yoku wakarimasu.","german":"Looking at real examples, you can clearly see the effect of this method.","furigana":"じつれい|み|ほうほう|こうか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"重点 is the point on which one places special weight: 〜に重点を置く \"to put the emphasis on ...\", 重点項目 \"priority item\". Very common is the adverb 重点的に \"in a focused way, with emphasis\". 重要 only means \"important\", while 重点 denotes a deliberately set priority.","examples":[{"japanese":"今回は安全に重点を置きます。","romaji":"Konkai wa anzen ni jūten o okimasu.","german":"This time we'll focus on safety.","furigana":"こんかい|あんぜん|じゅうてん|お"},{"japanese":"重点を決めて学習すると効果的です。","romaji":"Jūten o kimete gakushū suru to kōkateki desu.","german":"Studying with set priorities is effective.","furigana":"じゅうてん|き|がくしゅう|こうかてき"},{"japanese":"苦手な文法を重点的に復習しました。","romaji":"Nigate na bunpou o juutenteki ni fukushuu shimashita.","german":"I intensively reviewed the grammar I'm weak at.","furigana":"にがて|ぶんぽう|じゅうてんてき|ふくしゅう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"周辺 is the area around a place, often as a suffix: 駅周辺, 東京周辺, 周辺地域, 周辺の道路. It sounds more matter-of-fact than 周り. 付近 stresses the immediate proximity, and 近所 is the neighborhood of one's own home. In IT, 周辺機器 means \"peripheral devices\".","examples":[{"japanese":"駅周辺には飲食店が多いです。","romaji":"Eki shūhen ni wa inshokuten ga ōi desu.","german":"There are many restaurants around the station.","furigana":"えきしゅうへん|いんしょくてん|おお"},{"japanese":"工事のため周辺の道路が混んでいます。","romaji":"Kōji no tame shūhen no dōro ga konde imasu.","german":"The surrounding roads are congested because of construction.","furigana":"こうじ|しゅうへん|どうろ|こ"},{"japanese":"パソコンの周辺機器を買いそろえました。","romaji":"Pasokon no shuuhen kiki o kaisoroemashita.","german":"I bought all the computer peripherals I needed.","furigana":"しゅうへんきき|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"出勤 means to show up for work or to be present at the workplace: 〜時に出勤する, 出勤時間, 休日出勤 \"work on a day off\". The opposite is 退勤. 通勤, by contrast, is commuting (the way there), and 出社 specifically means arriving at the company office. As a する-verb it is intransitive.","examples":[{"japanese":"毎朝八時に出勤します。","romaji":"Maiasa hachiji ni shukkin shimasu.","german":"I go to work at eight every morning.","furigana":"まいあさはちじ|しゅっきん"},{"japanese":"明日は出勤する必要がありません。","romaji":"Ashita wa shukkin suru hitsuyō ga arimasen.","german":"I don't need to go to work tomorrow.","furigana":"あした|しゅっきん|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"人手が足りなくて、今週は休日出勤もしました。","romaji":"Hitode ga tarinakute, konshuu wa kyuujitsu shukkin mo shimashita.","german":"We were short-staffed, so I even worked on a day off this week.","furigana":"ひとで|た|こんしゅう|きゅうじつしゅっきん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"正面 is the front side or the spot directly opposite: 正面玄関 \"main entrance\", 駅の正面に \"directly in front of the station\", 真正面 \"right opposite\". 正面から also means \"openly, directly\" in a figurative sense: 問題に正面から向き合う. 前 is more general, and 向かい stresses \"on the other side\". 正面衝突 is a head-on collision.","examples":[{"japanese":"正面の入口から入ってください。","romaji":"Shōmen no iriguchi kara haitte kudasai.","german":"Please enter through the front entrance.","furigana":"しょうめん|いりぐち|はい"},{"japanese":"問題に正面から向き合いました。","romaji":"Mondai ni shōmen kara mukiaimashita.","german":"I faced the problem head-on.","furigana":"もんだい|しょうめん|む|あ"},{"japanese":"駅の正面に大きな銀行があります。","romaji":"Eki no shoumen ni ookina ginkou ga arimasu.","german":"There is a large bank directly in front of the station.","furigana":"えき|しょうめん|おお|ぎんこう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"書籍 is the matter-of-fact, literary word for \"books, publications\" and appears above all in the book trade and in specialist contexts: 書籍売り場, 電子書籍 \"e-book\", 書籍化 \"to be published as a book\". In conversation one simply says 本. 図書 is the term used by libraries (図書館), and 著書 is the book of a specific author.","examples":[{"japanese":"専門書籍を扱う店に行きました。","romaji":"Senmon shoseki o atsukau mise ni ikimashita.","german":"I went to a shop that handles specialized books.","furigana":"せんもんしょせき|あつか|みせ|い"},{"japanese":"電子書籍ならすぐに読めます。","romaji":"Denshi shoseki nara sugu ni yomemasu.","german":"If it's an e-book, you can read it right away.","furigana":"でんししょせき|よ"},{"japanese":"人気のブログが書籍化されました。","romaji":"Ninki no burogu ga shosekika saremashita.","german":"A popular blog was published as a book.","furigana":"にんき|しょせきか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"生年月日 is the full date of birth with year, month, and day, and appears on forms, ID documents, and in identity checks. Note the reading: 月日 is read here as がっぴ, not つきひ. 誕生日, by contrast, is the yearly birthday that one celebrates. Typical: 生年月日を記入する/伺う.","examples":[{"japanese":"生年月日を記入してください。","romaji":"Seinengappi o kinyū shite kudasai.","german":"Please fill in your date of birth.","furigana":"せいねんがっぴ|きにゅう"},{"japanese":"本人確認のため生年月日を伺います。","romaji":"Honnin kakunin no tame seinengappi o ukagaimasu.","german":"We will ask for your date of birth to verify your identity.","furigana":"ほんにんかくにん|せいねんがっぴ|うかが"},{"japanese":"パスワードに生年月日を使うのは危険です。","romaji":"Pasuwaado ni seinengappi o tsukau no wa kiken desu.","german":"Using your date of birth as a password is dangerous.","furigana":"せいねんがっぴ|つか|きけん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"成分 are the components or ingredients of a substance: 有効成分 \"active ingredient\", 成分表示 \"ingredient list\", 〜成分が含まれている. It describes what something is chemically composed of; 原料, by contrast, is the raw material before processing, and 材料 is the material or ingredient used in making something.","examples":[{"japanese":"買う前に成分を確認しました。","romaji":"Kau mae ni seibun o kakunin shimashita.","german":"I checked the ingredients before buying.","furigana":"か|まえ|せいぶん|かくにん"},{"japanese":"この薬には眠くなる成分が含まれています。","romaji":"Kono kusuri ni wa nemuku naru seibun ga fukumarete imasu.","german":"This medicine contains an ingredient that makes you drowsy.","furigana":"くすり|ねむ|せいぶん|ふく"},{"japanese":"温泉の成分は場所によって違います。","romaji":"Onsen no seibun wa basho ni yotte chigaimasu.","german":"The composition of hot spring water differs from place to place.","furigana":"おんせん|せいぶん|ばしょ|ちが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"成立 means that something comes into being or becomes valid: 契約・取引・交渉が成立する, 法律が成立する \"a law is passed\". An argument or theory can also 成立する \"hold up\". The verb is intransitive; to bring something about actively is 成立させる. Do not confuse it with 設立 \"to found an organization\" (transitive).","examples":[{"japanese":"話し合いの結果、契約が成立しました。","romaji":"Hanashiai no kekka, keiyaku ga seiritsu shimashita.","german":"As a result of the talks, the contract was concluded.","furigana":"はな|あ|けっか|けいやく|せいりつ"},{"japanese":"この条件では取引は成立しません。","romaji":"Kono jōken de wa torihiki wa seiritsu shimasen.","german":"Under these conditions, the deal will not go through.","furigana":"じょうけん|とりひき|せいりつ"},{"japanese":"国会で新しい法律が成立しました。","romaji":"Kokkai de atarashii houritsu ga seiritsu shimashita.","german":"A new law was passed in the Diet.","furigana":"こっかい|あたら|ほうりつ|せいりつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"全般 denotes a field in its entirety and mostly stands as a suffix: 仕事全般, 生活全般, 日本文化全般. Common are the adverb 全般的に \"overall, on the whole\" and the phrase 〜全般にわたって \"covering the entire field of ...\". 全体 stresses the whole as a unit, whereas 一般 means \"general, common\" or \"the public\".","examples":[{"japanese":"仕事全般について説明を受けました。","romaji":"Shigoto zenpan ni tsuite setsumei o ukemashita.","german":"I received an explanation of the job as a whole.","furigana":"しごとぜんぱん|せつめい|う"},{"japanese":"日本文化全般に興味があります。","romaji":"Nihon bunka zenpan ni kyōmi ga arimasu.","german":"I'm interested in Japanese culture in general.","furigana":"にほんぶんかぜんぱん|きょうみ"},{"japanese":"今年は全般的に売り上げが良かったです。","romaji":"Kotoshi wa zenpanteki ni uriage ga yokatta desu.","german":"Sales were good overall this year.","furigana":"ことし|ぜんぱんてき|う|あ|よ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"送料 is the shipping cost for parcels or letters, not the price of the goods. Typical phrases in online shopping: 送料無料 \"free shipping\", 送料込み \"shipping included\", 送料別 \"shipping extra\", 送料がかかる \"shipping is charged\". For postage on stamps one also says 郵便料金.","examples":[{"japanese":"送料は商品代金に含まれています。","romaji":"Sōryō wa shōhin daikin ni fukumarete imasu.","german":"Shipping is included in the price of the goods.","furigana":"そうりょう|しょうひんだいきん|ふく"},{"japanese":"海外への送料を教えてください。","romaji":"Kaigai e no sōryō o oshiete kudasai.","german":"Please tell me the shipping cost overseas.","furigana":"かいがい|そうりょう|おし"},{"japanese":"五千円以上お買い上げで送料無料になります。","romaji":"Gosen'en ijou okaiage de souryou muryou ni narimasu.","german":"Shipping is free for purchases of 5,000 yen or more.","furigana":"ごせんえんいじょう|か|あ|そうりょうむりょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"体制 is the organizational structure or system with which something is run: 体制を整える \"to set up a structure\", 新体制, 二十四時間体制 \"around-the-clock system\". Politically it also denotes the ruling system (反体制). Do not confuse it with the identically read 態勢 \"readiness, posture\" and 体勢 \"body posture\"; 制度, by contrast, are established rules and institutions.","examples":[{"japanese":"安全に働ける体制を整えました。","romaji":"Anzen ni hatarakeru taisei o totonoemashita.","german":"We have put in place a setup in which people can work safely.","furigana":"あんぜん|はたら|たいせい|ととの"},{"japanese":"新しい体制で事業を始めます。","romaji":"Atarashii taisei de jigyō o hajimemasu.","german":"We will start the business under a new structure.","furigana":"あたら|たいせい|じぎょう|はじ"},{"japanese":"病院は二十四時間体制で患者を受け入れています。","romaji":"Byouin wa nijuuyojikan taisei de kanja o ukeirete imasu.","german":"The hospital accepts patients around the clock.","furigana":"びょういん|にじゅうよじかんたいせい|かんじゃ|う|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"調節 is regulating or adjusting a measurable quantity to the right level: 温度・音量・高さ・スピードを調節する, 体温調節. 調整 is similar but is used more for coordination between people, schedules, or opinions (日程を調整する). As a する-verb it is transitive with を.","examples":[{"japanese":"部屋の温度を調節してください。","romaji":"Heya no ondo o chōsetsu shite kudasai.","german":"Please adjust the room temperature.","furigana":"へや|おんど|ちょうせつ"},{"japanese":"椅子の高さを調節しました。","romaji":"Isu no takasa o chōsetsu shimashita.","german":"I adjusted the height of the chair.","furigana":"いす|たか|ちょうせつ"},{"japanese":"テレビの音量を調節するボタンが壊れています。","romaji":"Terebi no onryou o chousetsu suru botan ga kowarete imasu.","german":"The button for adjusting the TV volume is broken.","furigana":"おんりょう|ちょうせつ|こわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"追加 means to add something to what already exists: 〜に〜を追加する, 追加注文, 追加料金 \"extra charge\", 追加の説明. The Japanese verb 加える is more general, and 付け加える is used above all for remarks added afterward. 追加 sounds matter-of-fact and is very common in restaurants, online services, and offices.","examples":[{"japanese":"注文を一つ追加したいです。","romaji":"Chūmon o hitotsu tsuika shitai desu.","german":"I'd like to add one more order.","furigana":"ちゅうもん|ひと|ついか"},{"japanese":"説明に具体的な例を追加しました。","romaji":"Setsumei ni gutaiteki na rei o tsuika shimashita.","german":"I added concrete examples to the explanation.","furigana":"せつめい|ぐたいてき|れい|ついか"},{"japanese":"荷物が重量制限を超えたので、追加料金を払いました。","romaji":"Nimotsu ga juuryou seigen o koeta node, tsuika ryoukin o haraimashita.","german":"My baggage exceeded the weight limit, so I paid an additional fee.","furigana":"にもつ|じゅうりょうせいげん|こ|ついかりょうきん|はら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"通路 is a passage or aisle through which one walks: in trains, airplanes, theaters, stores, or underground (地下通路). 通路側の席 is the aisle seat, as opposed to 窓側. 廊下 is specifically the corridor in houses and schools, and 通り is a street. On signs one often reads 通路に物を置かないでください.","examples":[{"japanese":"通路に荷物を置かないでください。","romaji":"Tsūro ni nimotsu o okanaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't leave luggage in the aisle.","furigana":"つうろ|にもつ|お"},{"japanese":"通路側の席を予約しました。","romaji":"Tsūrogawa no seki o yoyaku shimashita.","german":"I reserved an aisle seat.","furigana":"つうろがわ|せき|よやく"},{"japanese":"駅と空港は地下通路でつながっています。","romaji":"Eki to kuukou wa chika tsuuro de tsunagatte imasu.","german":"The station and the airport are connected by an underground passage.","furigana":"えき|くうこう|ちかつうろ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"定員 is the set maximum number of people, for example in courses, vehicles, or elevators. Typical are 定員に達する \"to be full\", 定員を超える, 定員オーバー, and 定員になり次第締め切る \"closing as soon as capacity is reached\". Do not confuse it with the similar-sounding 店員 \"shop clerk\". 定員割れ means that there are too few applicants.","examples":[{"japanese":"この部屋の定員は十人です。","romaji":"Kono heya no teiin wa jūnin desu.","german":"The capacity of this room is ten people.","furigana":"へや|ていいん|じゅうにん"},{"japanese":"定員に達したので募集を終了しました。","romaji":"Teiin ni tasshita node boshū o shūryō shimashita.","german":"Applications were closed because the capacity was reached.","furigana":"ていいん|たっ|ぼしゅう|しゅうりょう"},{"japanese":"エレベーターが定員を超えて、ブザーが鳴りました。","romaji":"Erebeetaa ga teiin o koete, buzaa ga narimashita.","german":"The elevator exceeded its capacity, and the buzzer sounded.","furigana":"ていいん|こ|な"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"定休日 is the regularly recurring closing day of a shop or restaurant, for example 定休日は水曜日です or 月曜定休. A one-off, unscheduled closing day is called 臨時休業. 休日 is generally a day off, independent of any business.","examples":[{"japanese":"この店の定休日は水曜日です。","romaji":"Kono mise no teikyūbi wa suiyōbi desu.","german":"This shop is closed on Wednesdays.","furigana":"みせ|ていきゅうび|すいようび"},{"japanese":"定休日を確認してから出かけましょう。","romaji":"Teikyūbi o kakunin shite kara dekakemashō.","german":"Let's check the closing day before we go out.","furigana":"ていきゅうび|かくにん|で"},{"japanese":"月曜日を定休日にしている美容院が多いです。","romaji":"Getsuyoubi o teikyuubi ni shite iru biyouin ga ooi desu.","german":"Many hair salons are closed on Mondays.","furigana":"げつようび|ていきゅうび|びよういん|おお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"当日 is \"the day in question\", i.e. the day on which a planned event takes place; it can also stand adverbially (当日は…). Typical: 当日券 \"same-day ticket at the box office\", 当日限り有効 \"valid only on the day\", 当日になって \"when the day came\". 今日 and 本日 refer to today, whereas 当日 refers to the day of the event, whenever that is.","examples":[{"japanese":"当日は九時までに集合してください。","romaji":"Tōjitsu wa kuji made ni shūgō shite kudasai.","german":"On the day, please gather by nine o'clock.","furigana":"とうじつ|くじ|しゅうごう"},{"japanese":"当日でも切符を買えます。","romaji":"Tōjitsu de mo kippu o kaemasu.","german":"You can buy tickets even on the day itself.","furigana":"とうじつ|きっぷ|か"},{"japanese":"当日になって急に雨が降り、試合は中止になりました。","romaji":"Toujitsu ni natte kyuu ni ame ga furi, shiai wa chuushi ni narimashita.","german":"On the day itself, it suddenly started raining, and the game was canceled.","furigana":"とうじつ|きゅう|あめ|ふ|しあい|ちゅうし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"盗難 is a theft from the point of view of the victim and is not used with する. Typical: 〜が盗難に遭う \"to be stolen\", 盗難届 \"theft report\", 盗難防止, 盗難事件. The act itself is described with the verbs 盗む or 盗まれる; the thief is called 泥棒. 盗難 sounds matter-of-fact and is typical of the police, insurance, and the news.","examples":[{"japanese":"自転車が盗難に遭いました。","romaji":"Jitensha ga tōnan ni aimashita.","german":"My bicycle was stolen.","furigana":"じてんしゃ|とうなん|あ"},{"japanese":"盗難を防ぐために鍵を掛けます。","romaji":"Tōnan o fusegu tame ni kagi o kakemasu.","german":"I lock up to prevent theft.","furigana":"とうなん|ふせ|かぎ|か"},{"japanese":"警察に盗難届を出しました。","romaji":"Keisatsu ni tounan todoke o dashimashita.","german":"I filed a theft report with the police.","furigana":"けいさつ|とうなんとどけ|だ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"中身 is what is inside something: 箱・財布・かばんの中身. Figuratively it means \"substance, content\", as opposed to the exterior (見た目より中身). 中身がない means \"empty, without substance\". 内容 is the more matter-of-fact word for the content of texts, conversations or contracts; 中身 sounds more everyday and concrete.","examples":[{"japanese":"箱の中身を確認しました。","romaji":"Hako no nakami o kakunin shimashita.","german":"I checked the contents of the box.","furigana":"はこ|なかみ|かくにん"},{"japanese":"見た目より中身が大切です。","romaji":"Mitame yori nakami ga taisetsu desu.","german":"What's inside is more important than appearance.","furigana":"み|め|なかみ|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"長いだけで中身のない会議でした。","romaji":"Nagai dake de nakami no nai kaigi deshita.","german":"It was a meeting that was only long, with no substance at all.","furigana":"なが|なかみ|かいぎ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 41"},
{"notes":"日時 is the combination of date and time, for example for appointments, deliveries, and events: 日時を決める/指定する/変更する, 開催日時. 日付 is just the date, and 日程 is the schedule spanning several days. 日時 is matter-of-fact and typical of invitations and forms.","examples":[{"japanese":"面接の日時を確認してください。","romaji":"Mensetsu no nichiji o kakunin shite kudasai.","german":"Please confirm the date and time of the interview.","furigana":"めんせつ|にちじ|かくにん"},{"japanese":"詳しい日時は後でお知らせします。","romaji":"Kuwashii nichiji wa ato de oshirase shimasu.","german":"We will let you know the detailed date and time later.","furigana":"くわ|にちじ|あと|し"},{"japanese":"配達の日時を指定することができます。","romaji":"Haitatsu no nichiji o shitei suru koto ga dekimasu.","german":"You can specify the date and time of delivery.","furigana":"はいたつ|にちじ|してい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"日用品 are things one needs every day, such as soap, toothpaste, toilet paper, or detergent; in stores there is a 日用品売り場. Food is normally not included. Similar is 生活用品; 雑貨 are more small goods and decorative items. Note: do not confuse にちようひん with 日曜日.","examples":[{"japanese":"近くの店で日用品を買います。","romaji":"Chikaku no mise de nichiyōhin o kaimasu.","german":"I buy daily necessities at a nearby store.","furigana":"ちか|みせ|にちようひん|か"},{"japanese":"引っ越しの前に必要な日用品をそろえました。","romaji":"Hikkoshi no mae ni hitsuyō na nichiyōhin o soroemashita.","german":"Before moving, I gathered the daily necessities I needed.","furigana":"ひ|こ|まえ|ひつよう|にちようひん"},{"japanese":"物価が上がり、日用品の値段も高くなりました。","romaji":"Bukka ga agari, nichiyouhin no nedan mo takaku narimashita.","german":"Prices have risen, and the prices of daily necessities have gone up as well.","furigana":"ぶっか|あ|にちようひん|ねだん|たか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"筆者 is the person who wrote the text at hand; very typical in reading comprehension questions (筆者の考え, 筆者が言いたいこと). In essays, authors also use 筆者 for themselves. 作者 is the creator of a literary or artistic work, 著者 the author of a book, and 作家 a professional writer.","examples":[{"japanese":"筆者の意見をまとめてください。","romaji":"Hissha no iken o matomete kudasai.","german":"Please summarize the author's opinion.","furigana":"ひっしゃ|いけん"},{"japanese":"筆者はこの問題を別の角度から見ています。","romaji":"Hissha wa kono mondai o betsu no kakudo kara mite imasu.","german":"The author looks at this problem from a different angle.","furigana":"ひっしゃ|もんだい|べつ|かくど|み"},{"japanese":"筆者は長年、海外で記者として働いていました。","romaji":"Hissha wa naganen, kaigai de kisha to shite hataraite imashita.","german":"The author worked for many years overseas as a journalist.","furigana":"ひっしゃ|ながねん|かいがい|きしゃ|はたら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"一通り means \"once through everything, on the whole\", without going into depth: 一通り目を通す \"to skim through once\", 一通り説明する. As a noun with の: 一通りの知識 \"basic knowledge\"; 一通りできる means \"to have mastered the essentials\". Do not confuse it with 一通（いっつう）, the counter for a letter. Often also written in hiragana, ひととおり.","examples":[{"japanese":"資料に一通り目を通しました。","romaji":"Shiryō ni hitotōri me o tōshimashita.","german":"I looked through the materials once.","furigana":"しりょう|ひととお|め|とお"},{"japanese":"必要な道具は一通りそろっています。","romaji":"Hitsuyō na dōgu wa hitotōri sorotte imasu.","german":"The necessary tools are all there more or less.","furigana":"ひつよう|どうぐ|ひととお"},{"japanese":"料理は一通りできますが、得意ではありません。","romaji":"Ryouri wa hitotoori dekimasu ga, tokui de wa arimasen.","german":"I can cook the basics, but I'm not good at it.","furigana":"りょうり|ひととお|とくい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"文献 are written sources, especially for research and academic work: 文献を調べる, 参考文献 \"bibliography\", 古い文献 \"old writings\". It is an academic word; in daily life one speaks of 本 or 資料. 資料 also includes data, photos, and documents, while 文献 covers only texts.","examples":[{"japanese":"関連する文献を図書館で探しました。","romaji":"Kanren suru bunken o toshokan de sagashimashita.","german":"I looked for related literature in the library.","furigana":"かんれん|ぶんけん|としょかん|さが"},{"japanese":"報告書の最後に参考文献を載せました。","romaji":"Hōkokusho no saigo ni sankō bunken o nosemashita.","german":"I included references at the end of the report.","furigana":"ほうこくしょ|さいご|さんこうぶんけん|の"},{"japanese":"古い文献によると、この寺は八百年前に建てられました。","romaji":"Furui bunken ni yoru to, kono tera wa happyakunen mae ni tateraremashita.","german":"According to old documents, this temple was built 800 years ago.","furigana":"ふる|ぶんけん|てら|はっぴゃくねんまえ|た"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"分布 is the distribution of something across space or in statistics: 人口分布, 年齢の分布, 分布図. As a する-verb it is intransitive with に: 〜が広い地域に分布している \"is widely distributed\", typical for plants and animals. For actively handing out things, one says 配る or 配布する instead.","examples":[{"japanese":"人口の分布を地図で示しました。","romaji":"Jinkō no bunpu o chizu de shimeshimashita.","german":"I showed the distribution of the population on a map.","furigana":"じんこう|ぶんぷ|ちず|しめ"},{"japanese":"この植物は広い地域に分布しています。","romaji":"Kono shokubutsu wa hiroi chiiki ni bunpu shite imasu.","german":"This plant is distributed over a wide area.","furigana":"しょくぶつ|ひろ|ちいき|ぶんぷ"},{"japanese":"年齢の分布を見ると、若い利用者が多いことがわかります。","romaji":"Nenrei no bunpu o miru to, wakai riyousha ga ooi koto ga wakarimasu.","german":"Looking at the age distribution, you can see that there are many young users.","furigana":"ねんれい|ぶんぷ|み|わか|りようしゃ|おお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"方面 denotes a direction or area, mostly as a suffix: 京都方面行きの電車, 新宿方面. Figuratively it stands for a field or area of expertise: 音楽方面, その方面に詳しい, 各方面から \"from all sides\". 方向 is the pure direction, 方角 the compass direction, and 分野 the field in the narrower sense.","examples":[{"japanese":"京都方面の電車に乗ります。","romaji":"Kyōto hōmen no densha ni norimasu.","german":"I'm taking a train bound for Kyoto.","furigana":"きょうとほうめん|でんしゃ|の"},{"japanese":"彼は音楽方面で活躍しています。","romaji":"Kare wa ongaku hōmen de katsuyaku shite imasu.","german":"He is active in the field of music.","furigana":"かれ|おんがくほうめん|かつやく"},{"japanese":"この件については、各方面から意見が寄せられています。","romaji":"Kono ken ni tsuite wa, kakuhoumen kara iken ga yoserarete imasu.","german":"Opinions on this matter are coming in from all quarters.","furigana":"けん|かくほうめん|いけん|よ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"未満 means \"less than\" and excludes the stated value: 十八歳未満 means persons up to and including 17. 以下, by contrast, includes the value; the counterpart of 未満 is 以上 or 超 (\"over, not including the value\"). Typical in regulations, prices, and statistics: 〜未満の方, 千円未満.","examples":[{"japanese":"十八歳未満の方は入れません。","romaji":"Jūhassai miman no kata wa hairemasen.","german":"People under eighteen years of age cannot enter.","furigana":"じゅうはっさいみまん|かた|はい"},{"japanese":"参加者は百人未満でした。","romaji":"Sankasha wa hyakunin miman deshita.","german":"There were fewer than a hundred participants.","furigana":"さんかしゃ|ひゃくにんみまん"},{"japanese":"千円未満の買い物にはポイントが付きません。","romaji":"Sen'en miman no kaimono ni wa pointo ga tsukimasen.","german":"Purchases under 1,000 yen do not earn points.","furigana":"せんえんみまん|か|もの|つ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"民間 denotes the non-governmental, private or civilian sphere as opposed to the state and authorities: 民間企業, 民間人 \"civilian\", 民間の経験, 民営化 \"privatization\". A second meaning is \"popular, handed down among the people\": 民間療法 \"folk remedy\", 民間信仰. Opposing terms are 公共, 官, or 国営.","examples":[{"japanese":"この施設は民間企業が運営しています。","romaji":"Kono shisetsu wa minkan kigyō ga un'ei shite imasu.","german":"This facility is run by a private company.","furigana":"しせつ|みんかんきぎょう|うんえい"},{"japanese":"民間の経験を行政に生かします。","romaji":"Minkan no keiken o gyōsei ni ikashimasu.","german":"We will make use of private-sector experience in administration.","furigana":"みんかん|けいけん|ぎょうせい|い"},{"japanese":"風邪にはしょうがが効くという民間療法があります。","romaji":"Kaze ni wa shouga ga kiku to iu minkan ryouhou ga arimasu.","german":"There is a folk remedy that says ginger is good for colds.","furigana":"かぜ|き|みんかんりょうほう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"郵送 is sending by post: 書類を郵送する, 郵送で受け付ける, 郵送料. It sounds more matter-of-fact than 送る and stresses the postal route. 配送 is the delivery of goods (also by courier services), and 発送 is sending off in general. Typical in official and business texts, politely 郵送いたします.","examples":[{"japanese":"書類は郵送でも受け付けます。","romaji":"Shorui wa yūsō de mo uketsukemasu.","german":"We also accept documents by mail.","furigana":"しょるい|ゆうそう|う|つ"},{"japanese":"申込書を昨日郵送しました。","romaji":"Mōshikomisho o kinō yūsō shimashita.","german":"I mailed the application form yesterday.","furigana":"もうしこみしょ|きのうゆうそう"},{"japanese":"結果は後日、ご自宅に郵送いたします。","romaji":"Kekka wa gojitsu, gojitaku ni yuusou itashimasu.","german":"We will mail the results to your home at a later date.","furigana":"けっか|ごじつ|じたく|ゆうそう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"予備 denotes something kept ready for an emergency and mostly stands with の before a noun: 予備の電池, 予備の鍵. In fixed compounds it also means \"preparatory, pre-\": 予備知識 \"background knowledge\", 予備校 \"cram school for entrance exams\", 予備日 \"backup date\". Unlike 代わり (\"substitute in place of something\"), 予備 stresses that something extra is on hand in reserve.","examples":[{"japanese":"予備の電池を持って行きます。","romaji":"Yobi no denchi o motte ikimasu.","german":"I'll take spare batteries with me.","furigana":"よび|でんち|も|い"},{"japanese":"会議の前に予備の資料を用意しました。","romaji":"Kaigi no mae ni yobi no shiryō o yōi shimashita.","german":"I prepared extra materials before the meeting.","furigana":"かいぎ|まえ|よび|しりょう|ようい"},{"japanese":"予備知識がなくても楽しめる映画です。","romaji":"Yobi chishiki ga nakute mo tanoshimeru eiga desu.","german":"It's a movie you can enjoy even without any background knowledge.","furigana":"よびちしき|たの|えいが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"欄 is a delimited area in forms, tables, or newspapers. In forms it means \"box, field\": 名前の欄に記入する, 空欄 \"blank field\", 備考欄 \"remarks field\". In newspapers and magazines it stands for a \"column\" or \"section\", often as a suffix: スポーツ欄, 投書欄. The meaning \"railing\" is rare in daily life; for that one says 手すり.","examples":[{"japanese":"名前の欄に記入してください。","romaji":"Namae no ran ni kinyū shite kudasai.","german":"Please fill in the name box.","furigana":"なまえ|らん|きにゅう"},{"japanese":"空いている欄はそのままで結構です。","romaji":"Aite iru ran wa sono mama de kekkō desu.","german":"You can leave empty boxes as they are.","furigana":"あ|らん|けっこう"},{"japanese":"毎朝、新聞のスポーツ欄から読みます。","romaji":"Maiasa, shinbun no supootsu ran kara yomimasu.","german":"Every morning I start reading the newspaper from the sports section.","furigana":"まいあさ|しんぶん|らん|よ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"きっかけ is the impulse or trigger through which something begins; mostly written in kana. Typical are 〜がきっかけで, 〜をきっかけに（して） \"triggered by\", and 〜のきっかけになる. With つかむ or 作る it means \"to find/create an opportunity\", for example to talk to someone. In contrast to 原因 (\"cause\", often negative), きっかけ is neutral and stresses the starting point of a development.","examples":[{"japanese":"旅行が日本語を学ぶきっかけになりました。","romaji":"Ryokō ga nihongo o manabu kikkake ni narimashita.","german":"A trip was what got me started learning Japanese.","furigana":"りょこう|にほんご|まな"},{"japanese":"小さな出会いをきっかけに生活が変わりました。","romaji":"Chiisana deai o kikkake ni seikatsu ga kawarimashita.","german":"My life changed because of a small encounter.","furigana":"ちい|であ|せいかつ|か"},{"japanese":"彼女に話しかけるきっかけがなかなかつかめません。","romaji":"Kanojo ni hanashikakeru kikkake ga nakanaka tsukamemasen.","german":"I just can't find an opening to talk to her.","furigana":"かのじょ|はな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"箇所 is a specific spot in a text, on an object, or at a location: 分からない箇所, 故障した箇所. It sounds more matter-of-fact than 所 and is typical of manuals, reports, and corrections. As a counter it follows numbers: 三箇所（さんかしょ）; in newspapers and on signs one often sees 3か所, 3カ所, or 3ヶ所 instead.","examples":[{"japanese":"分からない箇所に線を引いてください。","romaji":"Wakaranai kasho ni sen o hiite kudasai.","german":"Please underline the parts you don't understand.","furigana":"わ|かしょ|せん|ひ"},{"japanese":"修理が必要な箇所は三つあります。","romaji":"Shūri ga hitsuyō na kasho wa mittsu arimasu.","german":"There are three places that need repair.","furigana":"しゅうり|ひつよう|かしょ|みっ"},{"japanese":"この文章には間違いが三箇所あります。","romaji":"Kono bunshou ni wa machigai ga sankasho arimasu.","german":"There are mistakes in three places in this text.","furigana":"ぶんしょう|まちが|さんかしょ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"何となく describes a feeling or action without a clearly nameable reason: 何となく不安だ \"vaguely uneasy\", 何となく選ぶ \"to choose on a whim\". With 分かる it means \"to understand roughly, vaguely\". It is often written in kana (なんとなく). Similar, but more focused on the inexplicable, is なぜか \"for some reason\"; どことなく rather stresses an atmosphere that is hard to pin down.","examples":[{"japanese":"今日は何となく元気が出ません。","romaji":"Kyō wa nantonaku genki ga demasen.","german":"I somehow have no energy today.","furigana":"きょう|なん|げんき|で"},{"japanese":"何となく選んだ本がとてもおもしろかったです。","romaji":"Nantonaku eranda hon ga totemo omoshirokatta desu.","german":"The book I picked more or less at random was very interesting.","furigana":"なん|えら|ほん"},{"japanese":"彼の言いたいことは何となく分かりました。","romaji":"Kare no iitai koto wa nantonaku wakarimashita.","german":"I vaguely understood what he wanted to say.","furigana":"かれ|い|なん|わ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"せめて expresses a minimum with which one is just barely satisfied: \"at least, at the very least\". It is often combined with だけでも, くらい, or with wish and request forms such as 〜てください, 〜たい, 〜ばいいのに. Unlike 少なくとも, which states a matter-of-fact lower limit, せめて sounds pleading or regretful. It is almost always written in kana.","examples":[{"japanese":"せめて理由だけでも教えてください。","romaji":"Semete riyū dake de mo oshiete kudasai.","german":"At least tell me the reason.","furigana":"りゆう|おし"},{"japanese":"忙しくてもせめて朝ご飯は食べましょう。","romaji":"Isogashikute mo semete asagohan wa tabemashō.","german":"Even if we're busy, let's at least eat breakfast.","furigana":"いそが|あさ|はん|た"},{"japanese":"せめてあと一日休みがあればいいのに。","romaji":"Semete ato ichinichi yasumi ga areba ii no ni.","german":"If only I had at least one more day off!","furigana":"いちにちやす"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"絶えず means \"uninterruptedly, constantly\" and comes from 絶える \"to cease\" with the negative ず. It feels somewhat more literary than いつも or ずっと and suits ongoing changes, noises, or efforts: 絶えず変化する, 絶えず努力する. Equivalent in meaning but even more formal is 常に; colloquially one rather says しょっちゅう or ずっと.","examples":[{"japanese":"社会は絶えず変化しています。","romaji":"Shakai wa taezu henka shite imasu.","german":"Society is constantly changing.","furigana":"しゃかい|た|へんか"},{"japanese":"窓の外から絶えず車の音が聞こえます。","romaji":"Mado no soto kara taezu kuruma no oto ga kikoemasu.","german":"I can hear the sound of cars continuously from outside the window.","furigana":"まど|そと|た|くるま|おと|き"},{"japanese":"彼は絶えず努力を続けてきました。","romaji":"Kare wa taezu doryoku o tsuzukete kimashita.","german":"He has continued to make efforts without ever stopping.","furigana":"かれ|た|どりょく|つづ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"取り入れる has two main uses. Concretely it means \"to bring in, gather in\": 洗濯物を取り入れる \"to bring in the laundry\", 作物を取り入れる \"to bring in the harvest\". Figuratively it means \"to adopt, introduce\": 意見を取り入れる, 新しい技術を取り入れる, often with に for the target (授業に取り入れる). A synonym, but more formal, is 導入する; 受け入れる stresses accepting rather than incorporating.","examples":[{"japanese":"授業に新しい方法を取り入れました。","romaji":"Jugyō ni atarashii hōhō o toriiremashita.","german":"I introduced new methods into my lessons.","furigana":"じゅぎょう|あたら|ほうほう|と|い"},{"japanese":"客の意見を取り入れて改善しました。","romaji":"Kyaku no iken o toriirete kaizen shimashita.","german":"We made improvements by taking customers' opinions on board.","furigana":"きゃく|いけん|と|い|かいぜん"},{"japanese":"雨が降りそうなので、洗濯物を取り入れてください。","romaji":"Ame ga furisou na node, sentakumono o toriirete kudasai.","german":"It looks like rain, so please bring the laundry in.","furigana":"あめ|ふ|せんたくもの|と|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"含める is transitive: someone actively includes something in a group or total. Very common is the te-form 〜を含めて \"including, inclusive of\": 税金を含めて, 私を含めて. The intransitive counterpart 含む means \"to contain\" (この料金は税金を含む), and the passive 含まれる \"to be included\". For prices one also says 税込み for short.","examples":[{"japanese":"税金を含めると千円になります。","romaji":"Zeikin o fukumeru to sen en ni narimasu.","german":"Including tax, it comes to 1,000 yen.","furigana":"ぜいきん|ふく|せんえん"},{"japanese":"子供も含めて全員で十人です。","romaji":"Kodomo mo fukumete zen'in de jūnin desu.","german":"Including the children, there are ten of us in all.","furigana":"こども|ふく|ぜんいん|じゅうにん"},{"japanese":"私を含めて、誰もそのことを知りませんでした。","romaji":"Watashi o fukumete, dare mo sono koto o shirimasen deshita.","german":"Including me, nobody knew about it.","furigana":"わたし|ふく|だれ|し"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"整う is intransitive: something is fully prepared or in good order. Typical are 準備が整う, 条件が整う, 設備が整っている \"to be well equipped\", and 整った顔 \"regular features\". The transitive counterpart is 整える (準備を整える, 服装を整える). Compared with できる or 終わる, 整う sounds somewhat more elevated and stresses that everything necessary is fully in place.","examples":[{"japanese":"出発の準備が整いました。","romaji":"Shuppatsu no junbi ga totonoimashita.","german":"The preparations for departure are complete.","furigana":"しゅっぱつ|じゅんび|ととの"},{"japanese":"必要な条件が整えば始められます。","romaji":"Hitsuyō na jōken ga totonoeba hajimeraremasu.","german":"We can start once the necessary conditions are in place.","furigana":"ひつよう|じょうけん|ととの|はじ"},{"japanese":"この病院は設備が整っています。","romaji":"Kono byouin wa setsubi ga totonotte imasu.","german":"This hospital is well equipped.","furigana":"びょういん|せつび|ととの"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"長引く is intransitive and means that something lasts longer than expected or desired: 会議が長引く, 交渉が長引く, 風邪が長引く. It therefore usually has a negative nuance. There is no transitive counterpart in the narrow sense; someone who deliberately prolongs something 長引かせる (causative) it. More neutral is 長く続く.","examples":[{"japanese":"会議が長引いて帰りが遅くなりました。","romaji":"Kaigi ga nagabiite kaeri ga osoku narimashita.","german":"The meeting dragged on, so I got home late.","furigana":"かいぎ|ながび|かえ|おそ"},{"japanese":"風邪が長引いています。","romaji":"Kaze ga nagabiite imasu.","german":"My cold is dragging on.","furigana":"かぜ|ながび"},{"japanese":"交渉が長引けば、計画に影響が出るでしょう。","romaji":"Koushou ga nagabikeba, keikaku ni eikyou ga deru deshou.","german":"If the negotiations drag on, it will affect the plan.","furigana":"こうしょう|ながび|けいかく|えいきょう|で"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"高める is transitive: one deliberately raises quality, awareness, or ability, for example 能力を高める, 意識を高める, 効果を高める. The intransitive counterpart 高まる describes something increasing on its own (関心が高まる). For concrete heights and prices one rather says 上げる; 高める suits abstract values and sounds somewhat more elevated.","examples":[{"japanese":"練習で会話力を高めたいです。","romaji":"Renshū de kaiwaryoku o takametai desu.","german":"I want to improve my conversation skills through practice.","furigana":"れんしゅう|かいわりょく|たか"},{"japanese":"安全への意識を高める活動です。","romaji":"Anzen e no ishiki o takameru katsudō desu.","german":"It is an activity that raises awareness of safety.","furigana":"あんぜん|いしき|たか|かつどう"},{"japanese":"企業は競争力を高めるために新しい技術に投資しています。","romaji":"Kigyou wa kyousouryoku o takameru tame ni atarashii gijutsu ni toushi shite imasu.","german":"Companies are investing in new technology to enhance their competitiveness.","furigana":"きぎょう|きょうそうりょく|たか|あたら|ぎじゅつ|とうし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"目指す takes its goal with を: 合格を目指す, 医者を目指す \"to aim to become a doctor\", 優勝を目指す. Spatially it means \"to head for, make for\": 頂上を目指して歩く. The form 〜を目指して often introduces a purpose. Unlike 狙う (\"to aim at something\", often strategic or sly), 目指す sounds positive and ambitious.","examples":[{"japanese":"来年の試験合格を目指しています。","romaji":"Rainen no shiken gōkaku o mezashite imasu.","german":"I'm aiming to pass next year's exam.","furigana":"らいねん|しけんごうかく|めざ"},{"japanese":"山の頂上を目指して歩きました。","romaji":"Yama no chōjō o mezashite arukimashita.","german":"We walked toward the summit of the mountain.","furigana":"やま|ちょうじょう|めざ|ある"},{"japanese":"兄は医者を目指して毎日勉強しています。","romaji":"Ani wa isha o mezashite mainichi benkyou shite imasu.","german":"My older brother studies every day because he aims to become a doctor.","furigana":"あに|いしゃ|めざ|まいにちべんきょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"当てはめる is transitive: you apply a rule, pattern or formula to a case, or fit something in, mostly as X を Y に当てはめる. Its intransitive counterpart 当てはまる means \"to apply, to fit\" (この規則は例外にも当てはまる) and often appears in exercises: 当てはまる言葉を選びなさい. A more formal synonym is 適用する.","examples":[{"japanese":"この規則をすべての場合に当てはめることはできません。","romaji":"Kono kisoku o subete no baai ni atehameru koto wa dekimasen.","german":"This rule cannot be applied to every case.","furigana":"きそく|ばあい|あ"},{"japanese":"例に当てはめて考えると分かりやすいです。","romaji":"Rei ni atehamete kangaeru to wakariyasui desu.","german":"It is easier to understand if you apply it to an example.","furigana":"れい|あ|かんが|わ"},{"japanese":"公式に数字を当てはめて計算します。","romaji":"Koushiki ni suuji o atehamete keisan shimasu.","german":"You plug the numbers into the formula and calculate.","furigana":"こうしき|すうじ|あ|けいさん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"売れ行き describes how well a product sells, i.e. sales or demand. Typical uses are 売れ行きがいい・悪い, 売れ行きが好調だ・伸びる・落ちる. It refers to the trend, not to a total; for revenue in money, say 売り上げ. The word is equally common in business news and in everyday business.","examples":[{"japanese":"新商品の売れ行きは好調です。","romaji":"Shinshōhin no ureyuki wa kōchō desu.","german":"Sales of the new product are going well.","furigana":"しんしょうひん|う|ゆ|こうちょう"},{"japanese":"天気によって売れ行きが変わります。","romaji":"Tenki ni yotte ureyuki ga kawarimasu.","german":"Sales change depending on the weather.","furigana":"てんき|う|ゆ|か"},{"japanese":"売れ行きの悪い商品は値下げされます。","romaji":"Ureyuki no warui shouhin wa nesage saremasu.","german":"Products that sell poorly get marked down.","furigana":"う|ゆ|わる|しょうひん|ねさ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"明け方 is the time just before and while it gets light; it is used with に (明け方に目が覚める) or まで (明け方まで続く). 夜明け has the same meaning but is more neutral in everyday speech; 早朝 refers more generally to early morning, including after sunrise. The counterpart in the evening is 夕方 or 日暮れ.","examples":[{"japanese":"明け方に目が覚めました。","romaji":"Akegata ni me ga samemashita.","german":"I woke up at daybreak.","furigana":"あ|がた|め|さ"},{"japanese":"雨は明け方まで続きました。","romaji":"Ame wa akegata made tsuzukimashita.","german":"The rain continued until dawn.","furigana":"あめ|あ|がた|つづ"},{"japanese":"明け方の空はとてもきれいでした。","romaji":"Akegata no sora wa totemo kirei deshita.","german":"The sky at dawn was very beautiful.","furigana":"あ|がた|そら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"青白い is an i-adjective made of 青 and 白 and has two uses: \"pale, pallid\" for an unhealthy complexion (青白い顔をしている) and \"bluish white\" for cold light such as moonlight or screen light. For turning pale suddenly from shock, 顔が青くなる or 真っ青になる are more usual. 顔色が悪い is also a neutral way to say \"pale\".","examples":[{"japanese":"彼の顔は青白く見えました。","romaji":"Kare no kao wa aojiroku miemashita.","german":"His face looked pale.","furigana":"かれ|かお|あおじろ|み"},{"japanese":"月の青白い光が窓から入りました。","romaji":"Tsuki no aojiroi hikari ga mado kara hairimashita.","german":"The pale light of the moon came in through the window.","furigana":"つき|あおじろ|ひかり|まど|はい"},{"japanese":"ずっと家にいるから、青白い顔をしているね。","romaji":"Zutto ie ni iru kara, aojiroi kao o shite iru ne.","german":"You're so pale because you always stay at home.","furigana":"いえ|あおじろ|かお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"申し訳ない expresses deep regret: \"it is inexcusable, I am sorry\". Towards superiors or customers, the polite forms 申し訳ありません or 申し訳ございません are used; 申し訳ないです is more colloquial, and 申し訳ない alone sounds familiar or like an inner thought. 申し訳ないんですが serves to introduce a request. It is much stronger than すみません and is also used to express thanks when you have put someone to trouble.","examples":[{"japanese":"遅れて申し訳ないです。","romaji":"Okurete mōshiwakenai desu.","german":"I am sorry for being late.","furigana":"おく|もう|わけ"},{"japanese":"皆に迷惑をかけて申し訳ないと思っています。","romaji":"Mina ni meiwaku o kakete mōshiwakenai to omotte imasu.","german":"I feel sorry for having caused trouble to everyone.","furigana":"みな|めいわく|もう|わけ|おも"},{"japanese":"申し訳ないんですが、明日休ませてもらえませんか。","romaji":"Moushiwakenai n desu ga, ashita yasumasete moraemasen ka.","german":"I am sorry to ask, but could I take tomorrow off?","furigana":"もう|わけ|あしたやす"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"スケジュール is a time plan or schedule. Typical combinations: スケジュールを立てる \"to draw up\", スケジュールを組む \"to put together\", スケジュールを確認する, スケジュールが詰まっている \"to be fully booked\". A single appointment is 予定; スケジュール means the overall planning across several appointments. In formal texts, 日程 is also used for this.","examples":[{"japanese":"来週のスケジュールを確認しました。","romaji":"Raishū no sukejūru o kakunin shimashita.","german":"I checked next week's schedule.","furigana":"らいしゅう|かくにん"},{"japanese":"無理のないスケジュールを立てましょう。","romaji":"Muri no nai sukejūru o tatemashō.","german":"Let's make a schedule that isn't unreasonable.","furigana":"むり|た"},{"japanese":"今週はスケジュールが詰まっていて、会う時間がありません。","romaji":"Konshuu wa sukejuuru ga tsumatte ite, au jikan ga arimasen.","german":"My schedule is packed this week, so I have no time to meet.","furigana":"こんしゅう|つ|あ|じかん"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 48"},
{"notes":"インタビュー is questioning by the media or for a survey. The person asking says 〜にインタビューする or インタビューをする; the person being questioned says インタビューを受ける or インタビューに答える. For a job interview, Japanese does not use インタビュー but 面接.","examples":[{"japanese":"選手にインタビューをしました。","romaji":"Senshu ni intabyū o shimashita.","german":"I interviewed the athlete.","furigana":"せんしゅ"},{"japanese":"インタビューの内容を記事にまとめました。","romaji":"Intabyū no naiyō o kiji ni matomemashita.","german":"I put the content of the interview together into an article.","furigana":"ないよう|きじ"},{"japanese":"駅前でテレビ局のインタビューを受けました。","romaji":"Ekimae de terebikyoku no intabyuu o ukemashita.","german":"I was interviewed by a TV station in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|きょく|う"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"サンプル is a sample or specimen, such as a free product sample (無料サンプル) or a random sample in statistics and research. In Japan it also refers to the plastic replicas of dishes displayed in front of restaurants (食品サンプル). For cosmetics or food samples in a shop, 試供品 or 試食 are also used; 見本 is the Japanese word for \"sample, model\".","examples":[{"japanese":"商品のサンプルを送っていただきました。","romaji":"Shōhin no sanpuru o okutte itadakimashita.","german":"I was sent a sample of the product.","furigana":"しょうひん|おく"},{"japanese":"少数のサンプルだけでは判断できません。","romaji":"Shōsū no sanpuru dake de wa handan dekimasen.","german":"You can't judge from just a small sample.","furigana":"しょうすう|はんだん"},{"japanese":"店の前に料理のサンプルが並んでいます。","romaji":"Mise no mae ni ryouri no sanpuru ga narande imasu.","german":"Sample dishes are lined up in front of the shop.","furigana":"みせ|まえ|りょうり|なら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"テーマ (aus dem Deutschen „Thema“) ist das Leitthema eines Vortrags, Werks oder einer Untersuchung. Typisch sind テーマを決める, 〜をテーマにする „zum Thema machen“ und 研究テーマ. Gegenüber 話題 („Gesprächsthema“, worüber gerade geredet wird) meint テーマ den zentralen, bewusst gewählten Gegenstand; 題 oder タイトル ist dagegen der Titel.","examples":[{"japanese":"発表のテーマを決めました。","romaji":"Happyō no tēma o kimemashita.","german":"Ich habe das Thema meines Vortrags festgelegt.","furigana":"はっぴょう|き"},{"japanese":"環境をテーマに作文を書きました。","romaji":"Kankyō o tēma ni sakubun o kakimashita.","german":"Ich habe einen Aufsatz zum Thema Umwelt geschrieben.","furigana":"かんきょう|さくぶん|か"},{"japanese":"この映画のテーマは家族の愛です。","romaji":"Kono eiga no teema wa kazoku no ai desu.","german":"Das zentrale Thema dieses Films ist die Liebe in der Familie.","furigana":"えいが|かぞく|あい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"バランス is both physical balance (バランスを崩す \"to lose one's balance\") and balance in a figurative sense: 栄養のバランス, 仕事と生活のバランス. バランスを取る means \"to keep one's balance, to balance out\", バランスがいい \"well balanced\". The native Japanese equivalent is 釣り合い, but it is much rarer.","examples":[{"japanese":"仕事と休みのバランスが大切です。","romaji":"Shigoto to yasumi no baransu ga taisetsu desu.","german":"A balance between work and rest is important.","furigana":"しごと|やす|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"バランスを崩して転びそうになりました。","romaji":"Baransu o kuzushite korobisō ni narimashita.","german":"I lost my balance and almost fell.","furigana":"くず|ころ"},{"japanese":"栄養のバランスを考えて食事を作っています。","romaji":"Eiyou no baransu o kangaete shokuji o tsukutte imasu.","german":"I cook meals with a balanced diet in mind.","furigana":"えいよう|かんが|しょくじ|つく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"レベル means level, grade or standard: レベルが高い・低い, レベルが上がる, 自分のレベルに合う. As a suffix it forms expressions such as 世界レベル \"world class\" or 生活レベル \"standard of living\". For official grades and exams, 級 or 段階 are often used, and for water level 水位 is more usual. Colloquially, 〜レベル also expresses an extent: プロレベル \"at a professional level\".","examples":[{"japanese":"自分のレベルに合う本を選びます。","romaji":"Jibun no reberu ni au hon o erabimasu.","german":"I choose books that suit my level.","furigana":"じぶん|あ|ほん|えら"},{"japanese":"この試験はかなり高いレベルです。","romaji":"Kono shiken wa kanari takai reberu desu.","german":"This exam is at a fairly high level.","furigana":"しけん|たか"},{"japanese":"世界レベルの選手と試合をしました。","romaji":"Sekai reberu no senshu to shiai o shimashita.","german":"I played a match against world-class players.","furigana":"せかい|せんしゅ|しあい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"メニュー refers, in a restaurant, to the menu or the range of dishes offered. メニューを見せてください asks to be allowed to see the menu; メニューが変わる describes a change in the offerings. On a computer, a selection of functions can also be called メニュー. The place or the following verb shows which kind of selection is meant.","examples":[{"kind":"teaching","japanese":"メニューを見せてください。","romaji":"Menyū o misete kudasai.","german":"Please show me the menu.","furigana":"み"},{"kind":"natural","japanese":"季節ごとにメニューが変わります。","romaji":"Kisetsugoto ni menyū ga kawarimasu.","german":"The menu changes with each season.","furigana":"きせつ|か"},{"japanese":"画面のメニューから、言語を選んでください。","romaji":"Gamen no menyū kara, gengo o erande kudasai.","german":"Please select a language from the menu on the screen.","furigana":"がめん|げんご|えら"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 2"},
{"notes":"ロビー is the entrance or waiting hall in hotels, hospitals, theaters or office buildings. One meets there (ロビーで待ち合わせる) or waits (ロビーで待つ). A smaller waiting room, for example at a doctor's, is called 待合室; the entrance area of an apartment building is more likely 玄関 or エントランス.","examples":[{"japanese":"ホテルのロビーで待っています。","romaji":"Hoteru no robī de matte imasu.","german":"I am waiting in the hotel lobby.","furigana":"ま"},{"japanese":"ロビーには自由に使える椅子があります。","romaji":"Robī ni wa jiyū ni tsukaeru isu ga arimasu.","german":"There are chairs in the lobby that you can use freely.","furigana":"じゆう|つか|いす"},{"japanese":"病院のロビーは診察を待つ人でいっぱいでした。","romaji":"Byouin no robii wa shinsatsu o matsu hito de ippai deshita.","german":"The hospital lobby was full of people waiting for their examinations.","furigana":"びょういん|しんさつ|ま|ひと"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 3 (source: N5; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"ロッカー is a lockable compartment or locker, for example at a station (コインロッカー \"coin-operated locker\"), at school, at a gym or in a changing room. You put something in (ロッカーに入れる) and lock it (ロッカーに鍵をかける). The homophone meaning \"rock musician\" is a separate, rarer word.","examples":[{"japanese":"荷物を駅のロッカーに入れました。","romaji":"Nimotsu o eki no rokkā ni iremashita.","german":"I put my luggage in a locker at the station.","furigana":"にもつ|えき|い"},{"japanese":"ロッカーの鍵をなくしました。","romaji":"Rokkā no kagi o nakushimashita.","german":"I lost the locker key.","furigana":"かぎ"},{"japanese":"プールの更衣室で、服をロッカーに入れて着替えました。","romaji":"Puuru no kouishitsu de, fuku o rokkaa ni irete kigaemashita.","german":"In the changing room at the pool, I put my clothes in a locker and changed.","furigana":"こういしつ|ふく|い|きが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"パターン is a recurring pattern, both in data and tasks (問題のパターン, 文型のパターン) and in behavior: いつものパターン \"the usual routine\", ワンパターン \"always the same, unimaginative\". For fabric patterns, 柄 or 模様 are more usual in everyday speech. Typical are 〜のパターンを覚える and 同じパターンで.","examples":[{"japanese":"間違いのパターンを調べました。","romaji":"Machigai no patān o shirabemashita.","german":"I examined the patterns of mistakes.","furigana":"まちが|しら"},{"japanese":"毎日同じパターンで生活しています。","romaji":"Mainichi onaji patān de seikatsu shite imasu.","german":"I live following the same pattern every day.","furigana":"まいにちおな|せいかつ"},{"japanese":"試験問題にはいくつかのパターンがあります。","romaji":"Shiken mondai ni wa ikutsuka no pataan ga arimasu.","german":"The exam questions follow several set patterns.","furigana":"しけんもんだい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"空っぽ is an emphatic, spoken form of 空（から）\"empty\" and means \"completely empty\". It is used as a na-adjective or with の: 空っぽだ, 空っぽの箱, 空っぽになる. Figuratively, 頭が空っぽになる means \"to have a blackout\" or \"to think of nothing\". For rooms or seats that are unoccupied, 空いている is more fitting.","examples":[{"japanese":"冷蔵庫が空っぽです。","romaji":"Reizōko ga karappo desu.","german":"The refrigerator is completely empty.","furigana":"れいぞうこ|から"},{"japanese":"空っぽの箱を片付けました。","romaji":"Karappo no hako o katazukemashita.","german":"I cleared away the empty boxes.","furigana":"から|はこ|かたづ"},{"japanese":"緊張して頭が空っぽになりました。","romaji":"Kinchou shite atama ga karappo ni narimashita.","german":"I was so nervous that my mind went blank.","furigana":"きんちょう|あたま|から"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"人通り refers to how many people pass along a street or place. It almost always appears with 多い・少ない: 人通りが多い \"busy\", 人通りの少ない道 \"a little-used street\". For crowds in a place, 人込み or 混雑 is used instead; 交通 refers to vehicles.","examples":[{"japanese":"この道は夜でも人通りが多いです。","romaji":"Kono michi wa yoru de mo hitodōri ga ōi desu.","german":"This street has a lot of foot traffic even at night.","furigana":"みち|よる|ひとどお|おお"},{"japanese":"人通りの少ない道を避けました。","romaji":"Hitodōri no sukunai michi o sakemashita.","german":"I avoided roads with little foot traffic.","furigana":"ひとどお|すく|みち|さ"},{"japanese":"朝早かったので、まだ人通りはほとんどありませんでした。","romaji":"Asa hayakatta node, mada hitodoori wa hotondo arimasen deshita.","german":"It was early in the morning, so there was hardly anyone about yet.","furigana":"あさはや|ひとどお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Ein i-Adjektiv: 命が危うい beschreibt Lebensgefahr, 成功が危うい einen unsicheren Erfolg. Anders als die alltägliche Warnung 危ない betont 危うい häufig, dass ein Zustand oder Ergebnis auf dem Spiel steht. 危うく＋Vergangenheitsform bedeutet oft, dass etwas beinahe passiert wäre; im Deutschen wird das Geschehen dann nicht als tatsächlich eingetreten übersetzt.","examples":[{"japanese":"このまま赤字が続けば、会社の存続が危うい。","romaji":"Kono mama akaji ga tsuzukeba, kaisha no sonzoku ga ayaui.","german":"Wenn die Verluste so weitergehen, ist der Fortbestand des Unternehmens gefährdet.","furigana":"あかじ|つづ|かいしゃ|そんぞく|あや"},{"japanese":"階段で足を滑らせて、危うく転ぶところだった。","romaji":"Kaidan de ashi o suberasete, ayauku korobu tokoro datta.","german":"Ich rutschte auf der Treppe aus und wäre beinahe gestürzt.","furigana":"かいだん|あし|すべ|あや|ころ"},{"japanese":"二人の関係は危ういバランスの上に成り立っている。","romaji":"Futari no kankei wa ayaui baransu no ue ni naritatte iru.","german":"Die Beziehung der beiden beruht auf einem wackligen Gleichgewicht.","furigana":"ふたり|かんけい|あや|うえ|な|た"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Summarizes the three areas of clothing, food and housing; the order in the Japanese word is fixed. Common are 衣食住に困る (to be unable to secure the basic necessities) and 衣食住を支える (to support the basic necessities). Especially common in texts about living conditions, household costs and social support.","examples":[{"japanese":"衣食住にかかる費用を毎月記録しています。","romaji":"Ishokujū ni kakaru hiyō o maitsuki kiroku shite imasu.","german":"I record the costs of clothing, food and housing every month.","furigana":"いしょくじゅう|ひよう|まいつききろく"},{"japanese":"この団体は、災害で家を失った人々の衣食住を支えている。","romaji":"Kono dantai wa, saigai de ie o ushinatta hitobito no ishokujū o sasaete iru.","german":"This organization supports the clothing, food and shelter of people who lost their homes in the disaster.","furigana":"だんたい|さいがい|いえ|うしな|ひとびと|いしょくじゅう|ささ"},{"japanese":"衣食住に困らない生活ができれば十分です。","romaji":"Ishokujuu ni komaranai seikatsu ga dekireba juubun desu.","german":"It is enough for me if I can live without worrying about clothing, food and housing.","furigana":"いしょくじゅう|こま|せいかつ|じゅうぶん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A one-syllable noun; context distinguishes it from the question particle か. \"To be bitten by a mosquito\" is 蚊に刺される.","examples":[{"japanese":"夜、蚊に刺されて、よく眠れませんでした。","romaji":"Yoru, ka ni sasarete, yoku nemuremasen deshita.","german":"I was bitten by mosquitoes at night and couldn't sleep well.","furigana":"よる|か|さ|ねむ"},{"japanese":"夏は窓を開けると、蚊が入ってきます。","romaji":"Natsu wa mado o akeru to, ka ga haitte kimasu.","german":"In summer, mosquitoes come in when you open the window.","furigana":"なつ|まど|あ|か|はい"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 21"},
{"notes":"Transitive: 運勢を占う \"to read one's fortune\". Also used figuratively for forecasts: 今後を占う. The related noun is 占い（うらない）\"fortune-telling, horoscope\".","examples":[{"japanese":"神社で今年の運勢を占ってもらいました。","romaji":"Jinja de kotoshi no unsei o uranatte moraimashita.","german":"I had my fortune for this year told at the shrine.","furigana":"じんじゃ|ことし|うんせい|うらな"},{"japanese":"この試合は大会の行方を占う大事な一戦です。","romaji":"Kono shiai wa taikai no yukue o uranau daiji na issen desu.","german":"This game is an important match that foretells the outcome of the tournament.","furigana":"しあい|たいかい|ゆくえ|うらな|だいじ|いっせん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Transitive counterpart of 浮かぶ（to float, to come to mind）. Common: 笑みを浮かべる \"to smile\", 涙を浮かべる \"to have tears in one's eyes\", 思い浮かべる \"to imagine\".","examples":[{"japanese":"子どもたちは川に紙の船を浮かべて遊びました。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa kawa ni kami no fune o ukabete asobimashita.","german":"The children floated paper boats on the river and played.","furigana":"こ|かわ|かみ|ふね|う|あそ"},{"japanese":"彼女は目に涙を浮かべて、お礼を言いました。","romaji":"Kanojo wa me ni namida o ukabete, orei o iimashita.","german":"With tears in her eyes, she said thank you.","furigana":"かのじょ|め|なみだ|う|れい|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"I-adjective from 薄（light, faint）and 暗い（dark）: not completely dark but faintly lit, used for rooms, paths or twilight.","examples":[{"japanese":"薄暗い部屋で本を読むと、目が疲れます。","romaji":"Usugurai heya de hon o yomu to, me ga tsukaremasu.","german":"Reading in a dim room tires your eyes.","furigana":"うすぐら|へや|ほん|よ|め|つか"},{"japanese":"五時を過ぎると、外はもう薄暗くなりました。","romaji":"Goji o sugiru to, soto wa mō usuguraku narimashita.","german":"After five o'clock it was already getting dim outside.","furigana":"ごじ|す|そと|うすぐら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Paint as a painting material (watercolors, oil paints), not color as a property, for which 色 is used. One says 絵の具で描く／塗る.","examples":[{"japanese":"絵の具で空を青く塗りました。","romaji":"Enogu de sora o aoku nurimashita.","german":"I painted the sky blue with paint.","furigana":"え|ぐ|そら|あお|ぬ"},{"japanese":"娘の誕生日に二十四色の絵の具を買いました。","romaji":"Musume no tanjōbi ni nijūyon shoku no enogu o kaimashita.","german":"For my daughter's birthday I bought a set of 24 colors of paint.","furigana":"むすめ|たんじょうび|にじゅうよんしょく|え|ぐ|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"I-adjective for stiff, unpracticed movements or a tense, unnatural atmosphere（ぎこちない会話）. It is not an onomatopoeic word, even though it sounds like one.","examples":[{"japanese":"初めて着物を着たので、歩き方がぎこちなかったです。","romaji":"Hajimete kimono o kita node, arukikata ga gikochinakatta desu.","german":"It was my first time wearing a kimono, so my way of walking was awkward.","furigana":"はじ|きもの|き|ある|かた"},{"japanese":"けんかの後、二人の会話は少しぎこちなかった。","romaji":"Kenka no ato, futari no kaiwa wa sukoshi gikochinakatta.","german":"After the quarrel, the conversation between the two was a bit stiff.","furigana":"あと|ふたり|かいわ|すこ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"A color word used as a noun: 紫の花, 紫色. Traditionally a noble color in Japan. A mixed color of red and blue.","examples":[{"japanese":"紫の花がきれいに咲いています。","romaji":"Murasaki no hana ga kirei ni saite imasu.","german":"Purple flowers are blooming beautifully.","furigana":"むらさき|はな|さ"},{"japanese":"赤と青を混ぜると、紫になります。","romaji":"Aka to ao o mazeru to, murasaki ni narimasu.","german":"If you mix red and blue, you get purple.","furigana":"あか|あお|ま|むらさき"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A color word used as a noun: ピンクのシャツ, ピンク色. Traditional equivalent: 桃色（ももいろ）. In some compounds ピンク also hints at the erotic.","examples":[{"japanese":"春になると、ピンクの桜が咲きます。","romaji":"Haru ni naru to, pinku no sakura ga sakimasu.","german":"When spring comes, pink cherry blossoms bloom.","furigana":"はる|さくら|さ"},{"japanese":"姉はピンク色のセーターを着ています。","romaji":"Ane wa pinkuiro no sētā o kite imasu.","german":"My older sister is wearing a pink sweater.","furigana":"あね|いろ|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"As a verb with する: 動画を配信する (to stream), ニュースを配信する (to distribute). Livestream: ライブ配信.","examples":[{"japanese":"試合はネットでライブ配信されます。","romaji":"Shiai wa netto de raibu haishin saremasu.","german":"The match will be live-streamed on the internet.","furigana":"しあい|はいしん"},{"japanese":"登録すると、毎週ニュースが配信されます。","romaji":"Tōroku suru to, maishū nyūsu ga haishin saremasu.","german":"Once you register, news is delivered to you every week.","furigana":"とうろく|まいしゅう|はいしん"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"再 (again) + 起動 (starting up). As a verb with する: パソコンを再起動する.","examples":[{"japanese":"動かないときは、一度再起動してみてください。","romaji":"Ugokanai toki wa, ichido saikidō shite mite kudasai.","german":"If it does not work, please try restarting once.","furigana":"うご|いちどさいきどう"},{"japanese":"更新のあと、スマホが自動で再起動しました。","romaji":"Kōshin no ato, sumaho ga jidō de saikidō shimashita.","german":"After the update, the smartphone restarted automatically.","furigana":"こうしん|じどう|さいきどう"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"An ID card for foreigners living in Japan with a medium- to long-term visa; it must be carried at all times.","examples":[{"japanese":"区役所で在留カードを見せてください。","romaji":"Kuyakusho de zairyū kādo o misete kudasai.","german":"Please show your residence card at the ward office.","furigana":"くやくしょ|ざいりゅう|み"},{"japanese":"在留カードはいつも持っていなければなりません。","romaji":"Zairyū kādo wa itsumo motte inakereba narimasen.","german":"You must always carry your residence card.","furigana":"ざいりゅう|も"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Noun for 通知する (to notify, already N2). An official notice (合格通知), but also the push notification on a phone (スマホの通知).","examples":[{"japanese":"大学から合格の通知が届きました。","romaji":"Daigaku kara gōkaku no tsūchi ga todokimashita.","german":"A notice of acceptance arrived from the university.","furigana":"だいがく|ごうかく|つうち|とど"},{"japanese":"アプリの通知を切りました。","romaji":"Apuri no tsūchi o kirimashita.","german":"I turned off the app notifications.","furigana":"つうち|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Literally \"to put a cat over oneself\": to hide one's true nature behind a well-behaved, gentle facade. Often said of someone who behaves quite differently in front of strangers or superiors than usual. Noun: 猫かぶり.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は先生の前では猫をかぶっている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa sensei no mae de wa neko o kabutte iru.","german":"In front of the teacher, she acts all well-behaved.","furigana":"かのじょ|せんせい|まえ|ねこ"},{"japanese":"最初は猫をかぶっていたけど、だんだん本性が出てきた。","romaji":"Saisho wa neko o kabutte ita kedo, dandan honshō ga dete kita.","german":"At first he put on an act, but gradually his true nature showed.","furigana":"さいしょ|ねこ|ほんしょう|で"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Literally \"relationship of dog and monkey\": in Japanese, dog and monkey are the proverbial enemies, not dog and cat. 犬猿 is read with the on-readings: けんえん.","examples":[{"japanese":"あの二人は犬猿の仲で、会うとすぐにけんかになる。","romaji":"Ano futari wa ken'en no naka de, au to sugu ni kenka ni naru.","german":"Those two are bitter enemies; as soon as they meet, they start fighting.","furigana":"ふたり|けんえん|なか|あ"},{"japanese":"犬猿の仲だった二つの会社が、今回は協力することになった。","romaji":"Ken'en no naka datta futatsu no kaisha ga, konkai wa kyōryoku suru koto ni natta.","german":"Two companies that had long been enemies are going to cooperate this time.","furigana":"けんえん|なか|ふた|かいしゃ|こんかい|きょうりょく"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Literally \"sparrow's tears\": a ridiculously small amount, usually of money (wages, bonus, interest). Slightly complaining or ironic.","examples":[{"japanese":"ボーナスは雀の涙ほどしか出なかった。","romaji":"Bōnasu wa suzume no namida hodo shika denakatta.","german":"The bonus was only a pittance.","furigana":"すずめ|なみだ|で"},{"japanese":"銀行の利息なんて雀の涙だ。","romaji":"Ginkō no risoku nante suzume no namida da.","german":"Bank interest is a pittance.","furigana":"ぎんこう|りそく|すずめ|なみだ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Literally \"to wash one's feet\". Figuratively: to stop a bad or shady activity (crime, gambling), and today also generally \"to hang up one's boots\" from a job.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は悪い仲間から足を洗って、まじめに働いている。","romaji":"Kare wa warui nakama kara ashi o aratte, majime ni hataraite iru.","german":"He has broken with his bad friends and is now working honestly.","furigana":"かれ|わる|なかま|あし|あら|はたら"},{"japanese":"ギャンブルからはもう足を洗った。","romaji":"Gyanburu kara wa mō ashi o aratta.","german":"I have already quit gambling for good.","furigana":"あし|あら"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Literally \"to burn one's hand\". Mostly used with に: 子どもに手を焼く. Similar: 手に負えない (unmanageable).","examples":[{"japanese":"息子のわがままに手を焼いている。","romaji":"Musuko no wagamama ni te o yaite iru.","german":"I have my hands full with my son's selfishness.","furigana":"むすこ|て|や"},{"japanese":"この古いパソコンの設定には手を焼いた。","romaji":"Kono furui pasokon no settei ni wa te o yaita.","german":"I had a lot of trouble setting up this old computer.","furigana":"ふる|せってい|て|や"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Literally \"the teeth do not stand\" - also literal with hard food. Figuratively: to be no match for an opponent or a task.","examples":[{"japanese":"この数学の問題は難しすぎて、全然歯が立たない。","romaji":"Kono sūgaku no mondai wa muzukashisugite, zenzen ha ga tatanai.","german":"This math problem is too hard; I can't make any headway at all.","furigana":"すうがく|もんだい|むずか|ぜんぜんは|た"},{"japanese":"チャンピオンが相手では、歯が立たなかった。","romaji":"Chanpion ga aite de wa, ha ga tatanakatta.","german":"Against the champion, I was no match.","furigana":"あいて|は|た"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Literally \"a hand comes out of the throat\": such great desire that one wants to reach out for it. Mostly used as 喉から手が出るほど欲しい.","examples":[{"japanese":"あのギターは喉から手が出るほど欲しい。","romaji":"Ano gitā wa nodo kara te ga deru hodo hoshii.","german":"I want that guitar desperately.","furigana":"のど|て|で|ほ"},{"japanese":"経験のある人材は、どの会社も喉から手が出るほど欲しがっている。","romaji":"Keiken no aru jinzai wa, dono kaisha mo nodo kara te ga deru hodo hoshigatte iru.","german":"Every company is desperate to get experienced personnel.","furigana":"けいけん|じんざい|かいしゃ|のど|て|で|ほ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Literally \"digging up roots, digging up leaves\". Almost always with 聞く／尋ねる and slightly reproachful: to question someone nosily.","examples":[{"japanese":"初対面なのに、家族のことを根掘り葉掘り聞かれた。","romaji":"Shotaimen na noni, kazoku no koto o nehori hahori kikareta.","german":"Even though we had just met, I was asked about my family in great detail.","furigana":"しょたいめん|かぞく|ねほ|はほ|き"},{"japanese":"記者は事件について根掘り葉掘り質問した。","romaji":"Kisha wa jiken ni tsuite nehori hahori shitsumon shita.","german":"The reporter asked question after question about the incident.","furigana":"きしゃ|じけん|ねほ|はほ|しつもん"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"腕 stands for skill: 腕が上がる = one's abilities improve (料理の腕が上がった). Related: 腕がいい (to be skilled), 腕を磨く (to hone one's skills).","examples":[{"japanese":"毎日練習して、料理の腕が上がった。","romaji":"Mainichi renshū shite, ryōri no ude ga agatta.","german":"I practiced every day, and my cooking skills improved.","furigana":"まいにちれんしゅう|りょうり|うで|あ"},{"japanese":"この一年でテニスの腕がずいぶん上がったね。","romaji":"Kono ichinen de tenisu no ude ga zuibun agatta ne.","german":"Your tennis has improved a lot this past year.","furigana":"いちねん|うで|あ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Literally \"to stick to the nose\": a behavior, way of speaking or smell becomes irritating over time. Typical: 自慢話が鼻につく (the boasting is annoying).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼の自慢話がだんだん鼻についてきた。","romaji":"Kare no jiman banashi ga dandan hana ni tsuite kita.","german":"His boasting has gradually started to get on my nerves.","furigana":"かれ|じまんばなし|はな"},{"japanese":"この香水は強すぎて、少し鼻につく。","romaji":"Kono kōsui wa tsuyosugite, sukoshi hana ni tsuku.","german":"This perfume is too strong and a bit overpowering.","furigana":"こうすい|つよ|すこ|はな"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Literally \"cat's forehead\": a very small area, said especially modestly about one's own garden or plot of land.","examples":[{"japanese":"猫の額ほどの庭で野菜を育てています。","romaji":"Neko no hitai hodo no niwa de yasai o sodatete imasu.","german":"I grow vegetables in a garden the size of a cat's forehead.","furigana":"ねこ|ひたい|にわ|やさい|そだ"},{"japanese":"東京では猫の額のような土地でも高い。","romaji":"Tōkyō de wa neko no hitai no yō na tochi demo takai.","german":"In Tokyo, even a tiny plot of land is expensive.","furigana":"とうきょう|ねこ|ひたい|とち|たか"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Literally \"to stick one's neck in\". Mostly critical: to meddle in things that are none of one's business; less often neutral \"to get deeply involved in something\".","examples":[{"japanese":"他人の問題に首を突っ込まないほうがいい。","romaji":"Tanin no mondai ni kubi o tsukkomanai hō ga ii.","german":"You'd better not poke your nose into other people's problems.","furigana":"たにん|もんだい|くび|つ|こ"},{"japanese":"彼は何にでも首を突っ込みたがる。","romaji":"Kare wa nan ni demo kubi o tsukkomitagaru.","german":"He wants to get involved in everything.","furigana":"かれ|なん|くび|つ|こ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Literally \"fire comes out of the face\": extreme embarrassment, mostly as 顔から火が出るほど恥ずかしい. Mostly about one's own embarrassing moments.","examples":[{"japanese":"みんなの前で転んで、顔から火が出るほど恥ずかしかった。","romaji":"Minna no mae de koronde, kao kara hi ga deru hodo hazukashikatta.","german":"I fell over in front of everyone and was so embarrassed I wanted to sink through the floor.","furigana":"まえ|ころ|かお|ひ|で|は"},{"japanese":"名前を間違えて呼んでしまい、顔から火が出る思いだった。","romaji":"Namae o machigaete yonde shimai, kao kara hi ga deru omoi datta.","german":"I called someone by the wrong name and was mortified.","furigana":"なまえ|まちが|よ|かお|ひ|で|おも"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Someone with long experience in a field: ベテランの先生, ベテラン社員. Not used in the sense of \"war veteran\" (退役軍人).","examples":[{"japanese":"困ったときは、ベテランの先輩に相談するといい。","romaji":"Komatta toki wa, beteran no senpai ni sōdan suru to ii.","german":"When you're in trouble, it's good to consult an experienced senior colleague.","furigana":"こま|せんぱい|そうだん"},{"japanese":"彼女はこの道三十年のベテランです。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kono michi sanjūnen no beteran desu.","german":"She is a veteran with thirty years in this field.","furigana":"かのじょ|みちさんじゅうねん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A tool for gripping and cutting wire; a loanword from the English \"pinchers\".","examples":[{"japanese":"ペンチで針金を切った。","romaji":"Penchi de harigane o kitta.","german":"I cut the wire with pliers.","furigana":"はりがね|き"},{"japanese":"工具箱からペンチを取ってくれる？","romaji":"Kōgubako kara penchi o totte kureru?","german":"Can you get me the pliers from the toolbox?","furigana":"こうぐばこ|と"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A friendly form of address or term for a little boy: 坊や、いくつ？ Said of a young man it can also be condescending (\"greenhorn\").","examples":[{"japanese":"坊や、お母さんはどこ？","romaji":"Bōya, okāsan wa doko?","german":"Hey, little boy, where is your mother?","furigana":"ぼう|かあ"},{"japanese":"あの坊やはまだ三歳だそうだ。","romaji":"Ano bōya wa mada sansai da sō da.","german":"That little boy is said to be only three years old.","furigana":"ぼう|さんさい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Transitive godan verb, more colloquial than 投げる: ボールを放る. Also \"to stop caring\": 仕事を放っておく (to leave work lying).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼はかばんをベッドの上に放った。","romaji":"Kare wa kaban o beddo no ue ni hōtta.","german":"He tossed his bag onto the bed.","furigana":"かれ|うえ|ほう"},{"japanese":"宿題を放っておいて、ゲームばかりしている。","romaji":"Shukudai o hōtte oite, gēmu bakari shite iru.","german":"He leaves his homework untouched and only plays games.","furigana":"しゅくだい|ほう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Keeping and breeding livestock such as cattle or sheep. Related: 牧場 (pasture, ranch), 畜産 (animal husbandry as an industry).","examples":[{"japanese":"この地方では昔から牧畜が盛んだ。","romaji":"Kono chihō de wa mukashi kara bokuchiku ga sakan da.","german":"Livestock farming has long been thriving in this region.","furigana":"ちほう|むかし|ぼくちく|さか"},{"japanese":"雨が少ない土地では、農業より牧畜が向いている。","romaji":"Ame ga sukunai tochi de wa, nōgyō yori bokuchiku ga muite iru.","german":"In lands with little rain, livestock farming suits better than agriculture.","furigana":"あめ|すく|とち|のうぎょう|ぼくちく|む"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Maintaining health: 保健所 (public health center), 保健室 (school infirmary), also the school subject health education. Homophone: 保険 (insurance).","examples":[{"japanese":"市は住民の保健に力を入れている。","romaji":"Shi wa jūmin no hoken ni chikara o irete iru.","german":"The city is putting effort into the health of its residents.","furigana":"し|じゅうみん|ほけん|ちから|い"},{"japanese":"来週の保健の授業では、睡眠について学ぶ。","romaji":"Raishū no hoken no jugyō de wa, suimin ni tsuite manabu.","german":"In next week's health class we will learn about sleep.","furigana":"らいしゅう|ほけん|じゅぎょう|すいみん|まな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A placard for advertising or information, also a poster as wall decoration: ポスターを貼る (to put up).","examples":[{"japanese":"駅にコンサートのポスターが貼ってあった。","romaji":"Eki ni konsāto no posutā ga hatte atta.","german":"There was a concert poster at the station.","furigana":"えき|は"},{"japanese":"部屋の壁に好きな映画のポスターを飾った。","romaji":"Heya no kabe ni suki na eiga no posutā o kazatta.","german":"I put a poster of my favorite movie on the wall of my room.","furigana":"へや|かべ|す|えいが|かざ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 30"},
{"notes":"Polite term for someone else's son: お宅の坊ちゃん. Also pejorative for a naive young man from a wealthy home. Also written 坊っちゃん; known from Natsume Soseki's novel 『坊っちゃん』.","examples":[{"japanese":"お宅の坊ちゃんは、もう小学生ですか。","romaji":"Otaku no botchan wa, mō shōgakusei desu ka.","german":"Is your son already in elementary school?","furigana":"たく|ぼっ|しょうがくせい"},{"japanese":"彼は苦労を知らない坊ちゃんだと言われている。","romaji":"Kare wa kurō o shiranai botchan da to iwarete iru.","german":"He is said to be a spoiled boy who has never known hardship.","furigana":"かれ|くろう|し|ぼっ|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"In Japan usually paid twice a year (summer and winter) in addition to the monthly salary; formally 賞与.","examples":[{"japanese":"冬のボーナスで新しいパソコンを買った。","romaji":"Fuyu no bōnasu de atarashii pasokon o katta.","german":"I bought a new computer with my winter bonus.","furigana":"ふゆ|あたら|か"},{"japanese":"今年は会社の業績が悪く、ボーナスが出なかった。","romaji":"Kotoshi wa kaisha no gyōseki ga waruku, bōnasu ga denakatta.","german":"The company did badly this year, and no bonus was paid.","furigana":"ことし|かいしゃ|ぎょうせき|わる|で"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 28"},
{"notes":"Old, torn cloth or clothing; figuratively, a flaw one wants to hide: ぼろが出る (to give oneself away). Adjective: ぼろい (shabby). The kanji 襤褸 is very rare.","examples":[{"japanese":"物置にあったぼろで、自転車の汚れをふいた。","romaji":"Monooki ni atta boro de, jitensha no yogore o fuita.","german":"I wiped the dirt off the bicycle with a rag that was in the storeroom.","furigana":"ものおき|じてんしゃ|よご"},{"japanese":"知ったかぶりをしていたが、すぐにぼろが出た。","romaji":"Shittakaburi o shite ita ga, sugu ni boro ga deta.","german":"He pretended to know everything, but his ignorance soon showed.","furigana":"し|で"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A prenominal word (連体詞) that stands directly before a noun and emphasizes a small amount: ほんの少し, ほんの気持ちです (just a small token - when handing over a gift).","examples":[{"japanese":"駅はここからほんの五分です。","romaji":"Eki wa koko kara honno gofun desu.","german":"The station is only five minutes from here.","furigana":"えき|ごふん"},{"japanese":"これはほんの気持ちですが、どうぞ。","romaji":"Kore wa honno kimochi desu ga, dōzo.","german":"This is just a small token, but please accept it.","furigana":"きも"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The headquarters of an organization: 本部に連絡する. Opposite: 支部 (branch). For events also 対策本部 (task force, crisis headquarters).","examples":[{"japanese":"詳しいことは本部に問い合わせてください。","romaji":"Kuwashii koto wa honbu ni toiawasete kudasai.","german":"For details, please contact the head office.","furigana":"くわ|ほんぶ|と|あ"},{"japanese":"大会の本部は体育館の入り口にある。","romaji":"Taikai no honbu wa taiikukan no iriguchi ni aru.","german":"The tournament headquarters is at the entrance of the gymnasium.","furigana":"たいかい|ほんぶ|たいいくかん|い|ぐち"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"\"Not good, not bad\": 味はまあまあだ. As a soothing exclamation: まあまあ、落ち着いて (now, now, calm down).","examples":[{"japanese":"テストの結果はまあまあだった。","romaji":"Tesuto no kekka wa māmā datta.","german":"The test result was so-so.","furigana":"けっか"},{"japanese":"まあまあ、そんなに怒らないで。","romaji":"Māmā, sonna ni okoranaide.","german":"Now, now, don't get so angry.","furigana":"おこ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 11 (source: N5; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"The minus sign and sub-zero temperatures (マイナス五度), also figuratively a disadvantage: マイナスになる (to be detrimental). Opposite: プラス.","examples":[{"japanese":"今朝の気温はマイナス三度だった。","romaji":"Kesa no kion wa mainasu sando datta.","german":"This morning the temperature was minus three degrees.","furigana":"けさ|きおん|さんど"},{"japanese":"遅刻は面接でマイナスになる。","romaji":"Chikoku wa mensetsu de mainasu ni naru.","german":"Being late counts against you in an interview.","furigana":"ちこく|めんせつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"To put seeds in the soil: 種を蒔く. Often written in kana as まく. Homophones: 撒く (to scatter, to sprinkle), 巻く (to wind).","examples":[{"japanese":"春になったら、畑に種を蒔こう。","romaji":"Haru ni nattara, hatake ni tane o makō.","german":"When spring comes, let's sow seeds in the field.","furigana":"はる|はたけ|たね|ま"},{"japanese":"庭に花の種を蒔いた。","romaji":"Niwa ni hana no tane o maita.","german":"I sowed flower seeds in the garden.","furigana":"にわ|はな|たね|ま"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Not knowing what to do and hesitating awkwardly: 駅でまごまごする. Similar: おろおろ (anxiously), もたもた (dawdling).","examples":[{"japanese":"初めての駅で出口がわからず、まごまごしてしまった。","romaji":"Hajimete no eki de deguchi ga wakarazu, magomago shite shimatta.","german":"At a station I was visiting for the first time, I couldn't find the exit and wandered around confused.","furigana":"はじ|えき|でぐち"},{"japanese":"まごまごしていると、電車に乗り遅れるよ。","romaji":"Magomago shite iru to, densha ni noriokureru yo.","german":"If you dawdle like that, you'll miss the train.","furigana":"でんしゃ|の|おく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"To meet at a place at an agreed time: 駅で友達と待ち合わせる. Noun: 待ち合わせ (appointment to meet, meeting place).","examples":[{"japanese":"駅の改札で六時に待ち合わせましょう。","romaji":"Eki no kaisatsu de rokuji ni machiawasemashō.","german":"Let's meet at the ticket gate of the station at six.","furigana":"えき|かいさつ|ろくじ|ま|あ"},{"japanese":"友達と待ち合わせたが、なかなか来なかった。","romaji":"Tomodachi to machiawaseta ga, nakanaka konakatta.","german":"I arranged to meet a friend, but he took a long time to come.","furigana":"ともだち|ま|あ|こ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The corner of a street, also generally \"on the street\": 街角インタビュー (street survey). Also written 町角.","examples":[{"japanese":"街角に小さな花屋がある。","romaji":"Machikado ni chiisa na hanaya ga aru.","german":"There is a small flower shop on the street corner.","furigana":"まちかど|ちい|はなや"},{"japanese":"テレビ局が街角で人々に意見を聞いていた。","romaji":"Terebikyoku ga machikado de hitobito ni iken o kiite ita.","german":"A TV station was asking people on the street for their opinions.","furigana":"きょく|まちかど|ひとびと|いけん|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Na-adjective: 真っ暗な部屋. Figuratively: 目の前が真っ暗になる (to see everything black, to despair).","examples":[{"japanese":"停電で家の中が真っ暗になった。","romaji":"Teiden de ie no naka ga makkura ni natta.","german":"The house went pitch dark because of the power outage.","furigana":"ていでん|いえ|なか|ま|くら"},{"japanese":"試験に落ちて、目の前が真っ暗になった。","romaji":"Shiken ni ochite, me no mae ga makkura ni natta.","german":"When I failed the exam, everything went black.","furigana":"しけん|お|め|まえ|ま|くら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Vivid blue (真っ青な空), but also pale in the face from fright: 顔が真っ青になる. Special reading まっさお.","examples":[{"japanese":"今日は雲一つない真っ青な空だ。","romaji":"Kyō wa kumo hitotsu nai massao na sora da.","german":"Today the sky is deep blue without a single cloud.","furigana":"きょう|くもひと|ま|さお|そら"},{"japanese":"事故の知らせを聞いて、彼は真っ青になった。","romaji":"Jiko no shirase o kiite, kare wa massao ni natta.","german":"When he heard about the accident, he turned deathly pale.","furigana":"じこ|し|き|かれ|ま|さお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Mostly as 真っ先に (first of all): 家に帰ると、真っ先に手を洗う.","examples":[{"japanese":"合格の知らせを真っ先に母に伝えた。","romaji":"Gōkaku no shirase o massaki ni haha ni tsutaeta.","german":"I told my mother the news of passing first of all.","furigana":"ごうかく|し|ま|さき|はは|つた"},{"japanese":"地震のとき、真っ先に火を消してください。","romaji":"Jishin no toki, massaki ni hi o keshite kudasai.","german":"In an earthquake, put out the fire first.","furigana":"じしん|ま|さき|ひ|け"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Na-adjective: 真っ白な雪. Figuratively: 頭が真っ白になる (to have a blackout). Also 真っ白い as an i-adjective.","examples":[{"japanese":"朝起きると、庭が雪で真っ白だった。","romaji":"Asa okiru to, niwa ga yuki de masshiro datta.","german":"When I got up in the morning, the garden was pure white with snow.","furigana":"あさお|にわ|ゆき|ま|しろ"},{"japanese":"緊張して、頭が真っ白になった。","romaji":"Kinchō shite, atama ga masshiro ni natta.","german":"I was so nervous that my mind went blank.","furigana":"きんちょう|あたま|ま|しろ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Intransitive godan verb: 話がまとまる (an agreement is reached), 考えがまとまる (one's thoughts get organized). Transitive: まとめる. The kanji 纏まる is rare.","examples":[{"japanese":"長い話し合いの末、ようやく意見がまとまった。","romaji":"Nagai hanashiai no sue, yōyaku iken ga matomatta.","german":"After a long discussion, opinions finally came together.","furigana":"なが|はな|あ|すえ|いけん"},{"japanese":"このクラスはよくまとまっている。","romaji":"Kono kurasu wa yoku matomatte iru.","german":"This class is well united."}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"I-adjective for glaring light, also figuratively radiantly beautiful: まぶしい笑顔. Kanji 眩しい.","examples":[{"japanese":"夏の日差しがまぶしい。","romaji":"Natsu no hizashi ga mabushii.","german":"The summer sunlight is dazzling.","furigana":"なつ|ひざ"},{"japanese":"カーテンを開けたら、朝日がまぶしかった。","romaji":"Kāten o aketara, asahi ga mabushikatta.","german":"When I opened the curtains, the morning sun was dazzling.","furigana":"あ|あさひ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A long-distance run of 42.195 km, also long-distance running in general: マラソン大会 (marathon race, school run).","examples":[{"japanese":"来月、初めてマラソンに出る。","romaji":"Raigetsu, hajimete marason ni deru.","german":"Next month I will run a marathon for the first time.","furigana":"らいげつ|はじ|で"},{"japanese":"テレビでマラソンの中継を見た。","romaji":"Terebi de marason no chūkei o mita.","german":"I watched the broadcast of a marathon on TV.","furigana":"ちゅうけい|み"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 40"},
{"notes":"A longer way than necessary: 回り道をする. Also figuratively a detour in life. Similar: 遠回り.","examples":[{"japanese":"工事中だったので、回り道をして帰った。","romaji":"Kōjichū datta node, mawarimichi o shite kaetta.","german":"There was construction, so I took a detour home.","furigana":"こうじちゅう|まわ|みち|かえ"},{"japanese":"回り道をしたが、今の仕事に出会えてよかった。","romaji":"Mawarimichi o shita ga, ima no shigoto ni deaete yokatta.","german":"I took a detour, but I'm glad I found my current job.","furigana":"まわ|みち|いま|しごと|であ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"How something or someone looks from the outside: 見かけによらず (contrary to appearances). Proverb: 人は見かけによらない (appearances are deceiving).","examples":[{"japanese":"この店は見かけは古いが、料理はとてもおいしい。","romaji":"Kono mise wa mikake wa furui ga, ryōri wa totemo oishii.","german":"This restaurant looks old, but the food is very good.","furigana":"みせ|み|ふる|りょうり"},{"japanese":"彼は見かけによらず、力が強い。","romaji":"Kare wa mikake ni yorazu, chikara ga tsuyoi.","german":"Despite his looks, he is strong.","furigana":"かれ|み|ちから|つよ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Shortened from the English \"(sewing) machine\". ミシンをかける = to sew with the machine.","examples":[{"japanese":"母はミシンで子どもの服を作った。","romaji":"Haha wa mishin de kodomo no fuku o tsukutta.","german":"My mother made children's clothes with the sewing machine.","furigana":"はは|こ|ふく|つく"},{"japanese":"ミシンをかけるのは久しぶりだ。","romaji":"Mishin o kakeru no wa hisashiburi da.","german":"It has been a while since I used the sewing machine.","furigana":"ひさ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A somewhat old-fashioned word for a small shop; today usually 店 or お店. Well known: 店屋物 (food delivered from a restaurant).","examples":[{"japanese":"昔はこの通りに店屋がたくさん並んでいた。","romaji":"Mukashi wa kono tōri ni miseya ga takusan narande ita.","german":"Long ago, many shops lined this street.","furigana":"むかし|とお|みせや|なら"},{"japanese":"近くの店屋で切手を買った。","romaji":"Chikaku no miseya de kitte o katta.","german":"I bought stamps at a nearby shop.","furigana":"ちか|みせや|きって|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The sequence of roads to a destination: 道順を教える (to give directions).","examples":[{"japanese":"駅から会場までの道順を地図で確認した。","romaji":"Eki kara kaijō made no michijun o chizu de kakunin shita.","german":"I checked the route from the station to the venue on the map.","furigana":"えき|かいじょう|みちじゅん|ちず|かくにん"},{"japanese":"すみません、郵便局までの道順を教えてください。","romaji":"Sumimasen, yūbinkyoku made no michijun o oshiete kudasai.","german":"Excuse me, could you tell me the way to the post office?","furigana":"ゆうびんきょく|みちじゅん|おし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Often attributive: 見慣れた景色 (familiar scenery), 見慣れない人 (an unfamiliar face).","examples":[{"japanese":"見慣れた町も、夜は違って見える。","romaji":"Minareta machi mo, yoru wa chigatte mieru.","german":"Even a familiar town looks different at night.","furigana":"みな|まち|よる|ちが|み"},{"japanese":"見慣れない人が家の前に立っていた。","romaji":"Minarenai hito ga ie no mae ni tatte ita.","german":"A person I had never seen was standing in front of the house.","furigana":"みな|ひと|いえ|まえ|た"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"To visit and comfort someone in the hospital: 入院中の友人を見舞う. Also \"to be struck by something\": 台風に見舞われる. Noun: お見舞い.","examples":[{"japanese":"入院している祖父を見舞った。","romaji":"Nyūin shite iru sofu o mimatta.","german":"I visited my grandfather, who is in the hospital.","furigana":"にゅういん|そふ|みま"},{"japanese":"その地方は大雪に見舞われた。","romaji":"Sono chihō wa ōyuki ni mimawareta.","german":"The region was hit by heavy snow.","furigana":"ちほう|おおゆき|みま"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Prefix meaning \"one thousandth\" (ミリグラム, ミリリットル) and short form of ミリメートル (mm).","examples":[{"japanese":"雨が一時間に五十ミリも降った。","romaji":"Ame ga ichijikan ni gojū miri mo futta.","german":"Fifty millimeters of rain fell in one hour.","furigana":"あめ|いちじかん|ごじゅう|ふ"},{"japanese":"このねじは直径三ミリです。","romaji":"Kono neji wa chokkei san miri desu.","german":"This screw has a diameter of three millimeters.","furigana":"ちょっけいさん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Fabric or paper without a pattern: 無地のシャツ. Opposite: 柄物 (patterned).","examples":[{"japanese":"白い無地のシャツを探している。","romaji":"Shiroi muji no shatsu o sagashite iru.","german":"I'm looking for a plain white shirt.","furigana":"しろ|むじ|さが"},{"japanese":"柄物より無地のほうが合わせやすい。","romaji":"Garamono yori muji no hō ga awaseyasui.","german":"Plain items are easier to match than patterned ones.","furigana":"がらもの|むじ|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Mostly as 無数の + noun or 無数に: 無数の星 (countless stars).","examples":[{"japanese":"夜空には無数の星が輝いていた。","romaji":"Yozora ni wa musū no hoshi ga kagayaite ita.","german":"Countless stars were shining in the night sky.","furigana":"よぞら|むすう|ほし|かがや"},{"japanese":"この問題には無数の答えがある。","romaji":"Kono mondai ni wa musū no kotae ga aru.","german":"There are countless answers to this question.","furigana":"もんだい|むすう|こた"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Literary; same meaning as 命じる (N3), but conjugated on the ずる pattern (命ぜられる). Also \"to appoint\": 課長に命ずる.","examples":[{"japanese":"社長は全社員に残業を減らすよう命ずる通知を出した。","romaji":"Shachō wa zenshain ni zangyō o herasu yō meizuru tsūchi o dashita.","german":"The company president issued a notice ordering all employees to reduce overtime.","furigana":"しゃちょう|ぜんしゃいん|ざんぎょう|へ|めい|つうち|だ"},{"japanese":"彼は四月から大阪支店長を命ぜられた。","romaji":"Kare wa shigatsu kara Ōsaka shitenchō o meizerareta.","german":"He was appointed manager of the Osaka branch from April.","furigana":"かれ|しがつ|おおさかしてんちょう|めい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A meter such as 電気のメーター or a taximeter (タクシーのメーター). As a unit of length, メートル is more usual.","examples":[{"japanese":"タクシーのメーターがどんどん上がっていく。","romaji":"Takushī no mētā ga dondon agatte iku.","german":"The taxi meter keeps going up.","furigana":"あ"},{"japanese":"係の人がガスのメーターを確認しに来た。","romaji":"Kakari no hito ga gasu no mētā o kakunin shi ni kita.","german":"A staff member came to check the gas meter.","furigana":"かかり|ひと|かくにん|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"For a clearly perceptible change, mostly with seasons or age: めっきり寒くなった.","examples":[{"japanese":"十一月に入って、めっきり寒くなった。","romaji":"Jūichigatsu ni haitte, mekkiri samuku natta.","german":"With November, it has become noticeably colder.","furigana":"じゅういちがつ|はい|さむ"},{"japanese":"祖父は最近めっきり年を取った。","romaji":"Sofu wa saikin mekkiri toshi o totta.","german":"My grandfather has aged markedly lately.","furigana":"そふ|さいきん|とし|と"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"For happy occasions (めでたい日, めでたく合格する). Polite: おめでたい. Ironically also \"naive, gullible\": おめでたい人.","examples":[{"japanese":"兄の結婚が決まって、本当にめでたい。","romaji":"Ani no kekkon ga kimatte, hontō ni medetai.","german":"My brother's marriage has been decided; it is truly a happy occasion.","furigana":"あに|けっこん|き|ほんとう"},{"japanese":"彼女はめでたく第一志望の大学に合格した。","romaji":"Kanojo wa medetaku daiichi shibō no daigaku ni gōkaku shita.","german":"She happily passed the entrance exam of her first-choice university.","furigana":"かのじょ|だいいちしぼう|だいがく|ごうかく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Short for 模擬試験, e.g. before entrance exams: 模試を受ける. Homophone: もし (if).","examples":[{"japanese":"日曜日に大学入試の模試を受けた。","romaji":"Nichiyōbi ni daigaku nyūshi no moshi o uketa.","german":"On Sunday I took a mock exam for the university entrance exam.","furigana":"にちようび|だいがくにゅうし|もし|う"},{"japanese":"模試の結果がよくて、少し安心した。","romaji":"Moshi no kekka ga yokute, sukoshi anshin shita.","german":"The result of the mock exam was good, which reassured me a little.","furigana":"もし|けっか|すこ|あんしん"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Expresses a weak supposition, often with かもしれない: もしかすると雨になるかもしれない. Same meaning: もしかしたら.","examples":[{"japanese":"もしかすると、彼はもう帰ったかもしれない。","romaji":"Moshika suru to, kare wa mō kaetta kamo shirenai.","german":"Maybe he has already gone home.","furigana":"かれ|かえ"},{"japanese":"もしかすると、この計画は失敗するかもしれない。","romaji":"Moshika suru to, kono keikaku wa shippai suru kamo shirenai.","german":"This plan may possibly fail.","furigana":"けいかく|しっぱい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Na-adjective, often for style and design: モダンな建物. In everyday speech 現代的 is also common.","examples":[{"japanese":"駅前にモダンなホテルができた。","romaji":"Ekimae ni modan na hoteru ga dekita.","german":"A modern hotel has been built in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ"},{"japanese":"和室をモダンな雰囲気に作り変えた。","romaji":"Washitsu o modan na fun'iki ni tsukurikaeta.","german":"We remodeled the tatami room to have a modern atmosphere.","furigana":"わしつ|ふんいき|つく|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Several meanings: fashion model, model of a product (新しいモデル) and a model for a character (小説のモデル).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は雑誌のモデルをしている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa zasshi no moderu o shite iru.","german":"She works as a magazine model.","furigana":"かのじょ|ざっし"},{"japanese":"この車の新しいモデルが来月発売される。","romaji":"Kono kuruma no atarashii moderu ga raigetsu hatsubai sareru.","german":"The new model of this car will be released next month.","furigana":"くるま|あたら|らいげつはつばい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Often figurative: things \"tell\" something, i.e. they bear witness to it: この写真が当時の生活を物語っている.","examples":[{"japanese":"祖母は戦争中の体験を静かに物語った。","romaji":"Sobo wa sensōchū no taiken o shizuka ni monogatatta.","german":"My grandmother quietly told of her experiences during the war.","furigana":"そぼ|せんそうちゅう|たいけん|しず|ものがた"},{"japanese":"この古い道具が、昔の人々の暮らしを物語っている。","romaji":"Kono furui dōgu ga, mukashi no hitobito no kurashi o monogatatte iru.","german":"These old tools tell of the lives of people in the past.","furigana":"ふる|どうぐ|むかし|ひとびと|く|ものがた"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A ruler for measuring, figuratively a standard for judgments: 自分の物差しで人を判断する.","examples":[{"japanese":"物差しで紙の長さを測った。","romaji":"Monosashi de kami no nagasa o hakatta.","german":"I measured the length of the paper with a ruler.","furigana":"ものさ|かみ|なが|はか"},{"japanese":"人を自分の物差しだけで判断してはいけない。","romaji":"Hito o jibun no monosashi dake de handan shite wa ikenai.","german":"You should not judge people only by your own yardstick.","furigana":"ひと|じぶん|ものさ|はんだん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Intensified form of すごい: ものすごい音, ものすごく速い. Kanji 物凄い.","examples":[{"japanese":"外でものすごい音がした。","romaji":"Soto de monosugoi oto ga shita.","german":"There was a tremendous noise outside.","furigana":"そと|おと"},{"japanese":"駅はものすごい人でいっぱいだった。","romaji":"Eki wa monosugoi hito de ippai datta.","german":"The station was full of people, an incredible number.","furigana":"えき|ひと"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Transitive godan verb: 肩を揉む (to massage the shoulders). Idiom: 気を揉む (to worry).","examples":[{"japanese":"疲れた母の肩を揉んであげた。","romaji":"Tsukareta haha no kata o monde ageta.","german":"I massaged my tired mother's shoulders.","furigana":"つか|はは|かた|も"},{"japanese":"紙を手で揉んで柔らかくした。","romaji":"Kami o te de monde yawarakaku shita.","german":"I rubbed the paper in my hands to soften it.","furigana":"かみ|て|も|やわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A public event or celebration: 夏の催し, 催し物. Verb: 催す (to hold an event).","examples":[{"japanese":"週末、公園でいろいろな催しがある。","romaji":"Shūmatsu, kōen de iroiro na moyōshi ga aru.","german":"This weekend there are various events in the park.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|こうえん|もよお"},{"japanese":"市の催しに多くの家族が集まった。","romaji":"Shi no moyōshi ni ōku no kazoku ga atsumatta.","german":"Many families gathered for the city event.","furigana":"し|もよお|おお|かぞく|あつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Formal word for the night time, often on signs: 夜間工事 (night construction), 夜間の外出. Homophone: やかん (kettle).","examples":[{"japanese":"この道路は夜間、通行止めになります。","romaji":"Kono dōro wa yakan, tsūkōdome ni narimasu.","german":"This road is closed to traffic at night.","furigana":"どうろ|やかん|つうこうど"},{"japanese":"夜間の受付は裏口をご利用ください。","romaji":"Yakan no uketsuke wa uraguchi o goriyō kudasai.","german":"For night-time reception, please use the back entrance.","furigana":"やかん|うけつけ|うらぐち|りよう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A kettle for boiling water; mostly in kana, kanji 薬缶. やかんでお湯を沸かす.","examples":[{"japanese":"やかんでお湯を沸かして、お茶をいれた。","romaji":"Yakan de oyu o wakashite, ocha o ireta.","german":"I boiled water in the kettle and made tea.","furigana":"ゆ|わ|ちゃ"},{"japanese":"やかんがピーッと鳴った。","romaji":"Yakan ga pītto natta.","german":"The kettle whistled.","furigana":"な"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 42"},
{"notes":"Especially for theater and kabuki; more general: 俳優. Idiom: 役者が一枚上 (to be a cut above someone).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は歌舞伎の役者として有名だ。","romaji":"Kare wa kabuki no yakusha to shite yūmei da.","german":"He is famous as a kabuki actor.","furigana":"かれ|かぶき|やくしゃ|ゆうめい"},{"japanese":"この映画は役者の演技がすばらしい。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa yakusha no engi ga subarashii.","german":"In this film, the actors' performances are wonderful.","furigana":"えいが|やくしゃ|えんぎ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Formal word for drugs and chemical substances: 医薬品, 化学薬品. In everyday speech one says 薬.","examples":[{"japanese":"実験で使った薬品は決められた場所に戻すこと。","romaji":"Jikken de tsukatta yakuhin wa kimerareta basho ni modosu koto.","german":"Chemicals used in the experiment must be returned to their designated place.","furigana":"じっけん|つか|やくひん|き|ばしょ|もど"},{"japanese":"この工場では薬品を作っている。","romaji":"Kono kōjō de wa yakuhin o tsukutte iru.","german":"This factory produces medicines.","furigana":"こうじょう|やくひん|つく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Travel at night, especially 夜行バス (night bus) and 夜行列車. Also 夜行性 (nocturnal).","examples":[{"japanese":"夜行バスで東京から大阪へ行った。","romaji":"Yakō basu de Tōkyō kara Ōsaka e itta.","german":"I went from Tokyo to Osaka by night bus.","furigana":"やこう|とうきょう|おおさか|い"},{"japanese":"昔は夜行で旅行する人が多かった。","romaji":"Mukashi wa yakō de ryokō suru hito ga ōkatta.","german":"In the past, many people traveled by night train.","furigana":"むかし|やこう|りょこう|ひと|おお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Adverb from やたら + に, also やたらと: やたらに物を買う. Critical, judgmental tone. The kanji 矢鱈 (ateji) is rare.","examples":[{"japanese":"知らない人をやたらに信じてはいけない。","romaji":"Shiranai hito o yatara ni shinjite wa ikenai.","german":"You must not trust strangers indiscriminately.","furigana":"し|ひと|しん"},{"japanese":"最近やたらに眠い。","romaji":"Saikin yatara ni nemui.","german":"Lately I've been sleepy all the time.","furigana":"さいきん|ねむ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Colloquial and often derogatory for a person (あいつは嫌な奴だ), but also for things: もっと安いやつ (a cheaper one). Usually written in kana.","examples":[{"japanese":"あいつは本当にいい奴だ。","romaji":"Aitsu wa hontō ni ii yatsu da.","german":"He's really a good guy.","furigana":"ほんとう|やつ"},{"japanese":"この靴、もう少し大きいやつはありますか。","romaji":"Kono kutsu, mō sukoshi ōkii yatsu wa arimasu ka.","german":"Do you have these shoes in a slightly bigger size?","furigana":"くつ|すこ|おお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Colloquial: to defeat an opponent (敵をやっつける) or to get a job done in haste (仕事をやっつける).","examples":[{"japanese":"ヒーローが悪者をやっつけた。","romaji":"Hīrō ga warumono o yattsuketa.","german":"The hero defeated the villains.","furigana":"わるもの"},{"japanese":"残りの宿題を一時間でやっつけよう。","romaji":"Nokori no shukudai o ichijikan de yattsukeyō.","german":"Let's knock out the rest of the homework in an hour.","furigana":"のこ|しゅくだい|いちじかん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The owner of a rental apartment or house; similar: 大家（おおや）. The reading いえぬし is rarer.","examples":[{"japanese":"家主に家賃を払った。","romaji":"Yanushi ni yachin o haratta.","german":"I paid the rent to the landlord.","furigana":"やぬし|やちん|はら"},{"japanese":"壁に穴をあける前に、家主の許可をもらった。","romaji":"Kabe ni ana o akeru mae ni, yanushi no kyoka o moratta.","german":"Before making a hole in the wall, I got the landlord's permission.","furigana":"かべ|あな|まえ|やぬし|きょか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Transitive godan verb, more colloquial than 破る: 紙を破く.","examples":[{"japanese":"子どもが本のページを破いてしまった。","romaji":"Kodomo ga hon no pēji o yabuite shimatta.","german":"The child tore a page of the book.","furigana":"こ|ほん|やぶ"},{"japanese":"封筒を破いて、中の手紙を出した。","romaji":"Fūtō o yabuite, naka no tegami o dashita.","german":"I tore open the envelope and took out the letter.","furigana":"ふうとう|やぶ|なか|てがみ|だ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The sun in the evening: 夕日が沈む (the sun sets). Opposite: 朝日.","examples":[{"japanese":"海に沈む夕日がとてもきれいだった。","romaji":"Umi ni shizumu yūhi ga totemo kirei datta.","german":"The sun setting into the sea was very beautiful.","furigana":"うみ|しず|ゆうひ"},{"japanese":"夕日に照らされて、町が赤く染まった。","romaji":"Yūhi ni terasarete, machi ga akaku somatta.","german":"Lit by the evening sun, the town was dyed red.","furigana":"ゆうひ|て|まち|あか|そ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Where someone or something has gone (行方がわからない) or how something develops (試合の行方). Special reading ゆくえ.","examples":[{"japanese":"事故のあと、犬の行方がわからなくなった。","romaji":"Jiko no ato, inu no yukue ga wakaranaku natta.","german":"After the accident, the dog's whereabouts became unknown.","furigana":"じこ|いぬ|ゆくえ"},{"japanese":"選挙の行方に注目が集まっている。","romaji":"Senkyo no yukue ni chūmoku ga atsumatte iru.","german":"Attention is focused on how the election will turn out.","furigana":"せんきょ|ゆくえ|ちゅうもく|あつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"To cook food in boiling water: 卵をゆでる, パスタをゆでる. Kanji 茹でる.","examples":[{"japanese":"卵を八分ゆでた。","romaji":"Tamago o happun yudeta.","german":"I boiled the eggs for eight minutes.","furigana":"たまご|はっぷん"},{"japanese":"パスタをゆでている間に、ソースを作る。","romaji":"Pasuta o yudete iru aida ni, sōsu o tsukuru.","german":"While the pasta boils, I make the sauce.","furigana":"あいだ|つく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Words of a specialist field: 専門用語 (technical term), 医学用語.","examples":[{"japanese":"この本は難しい用語が多くて読みにくい。","romaji":"Kono hon wa muzukashii yōgo ga ōkute yominikui.","german":"This book has many difficult terms and is hard to read.","furigana":"ほん|むずか|ようご|おお|よ"},{"japanese":"会議では、誰にでもわかる用語を使ってください。","romaji":"Kaigi de wa, dare ni demo wakaru yōgo o tsukatte kudasai.","german":"In the meeting, please use terms that everyone can understand.","furigana":"かいぎ|だれ|ようご|つか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The capacity of a container or room: 容積を計算する. Similar: 体積 (volume of a solid body).","examples":[{"japanese":"この冷蔵庫は容積が大きい。","romaji":"Kono reizōko wa yōseki ga ōkii.","german":"This refrigerator has a large capacity.","furigana":"れいぞうこ|ようせき|おお"},{"japanese":"箱の容積を計算してください。","romaji":"Hako no yōseki o keisan shite kudasai.","german":"Please calculate the volume of the box.","furigana":"はこ|ようせき|けいさん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A shop for Western-style clothing and accessories; somewhat old-fashioned, today more usually ブティック or 洋服店.","examples":[{"japanese":"商店街の洋品店でネクタイを買った。","romaji":"Shōtengai no yōhinten de nekutai o katta.","german":"I bought a tie at a clothing store in the shopping street.","furigana":"しょうてんがい|ようひんてん|か"},{"japanese":"祖母は昔、洋品店を営んでいた。","romaji":"Sobo wa mukashi, yōhinten o itonande ita.","german":"My grandmother used to run a clothing store.","furigana":"そぼ|むかし|ようひんてん|いとな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Substances that living things, especially plants, need to grow: 土から養分を吸収する. Similar: 栄養.","examples":[{"japanese":"植物は根から養分を吸い上げる。","romaji":"Shokubutsu wa ne kara yōbun o suiageru.","german":"Plants absorb nutrients through their roots.","furigana":"しょくぶつ|ね|ようぶん|す|あ"},{"japanese":"この土は養分が少ない。","romaji":"Kono tsuchi wa yōbun ga sukunai.","german":"This soil is low in nutrients.","furigana":"つち|ようぶん|すく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Someone who always wants more; also as a na-adjective: 欲張りな人. Verb: 欲張る.","examples":[{"japanese":"全部欲しいなんて、欲張りだね。","romaji":"Zenbu hoshii nante, yokubari da ne.","german":"Wanting everything - how greedy you are!","furigana":"ぜんぶほ|よくば"},{"japanese":"欲張りな人は、いつも満足できない。","romaji":"Yokubari na hito wa, itsumo manzoku dekinai.","german":"Greedy people can never be satisfied.","furigana":"よくば|ひと|まんぞく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Colloquial: to send or give something toward the speaker (手紙をよこす). Kanji 寄越す.","examples":[{"japanese":"息子がやっと手紙をよこした。","romaji":"Musuko ga yatto tegami o yokoshita.","german":"My son finally sent a letter.","furigana":"むすこ|てがみ"},{"japanese":"そのペンをこっちによこして。","romaji":"Sono pen o kotchi ni yokoshite.","german":"Hand that pen over here."}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A place where two roads cross; similar: 交差点.","examples":[{"japanese":"次の四つ角を右に曲がってください。","romaji":"Tsugi no yotsukado o migi ni magatte kudasai.","german":"Please turn right at the next intersection.","furigana":"つぎ|よ|かど|みぎ|ま"},{"japanese":"四つ角にパン屋がある。","romaji":"Yotsukado ni pan'ya ga aru.","german":"There is a bakery at the intersection.","furigana":"よ|かど|や"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"To call out to someone or to appeal to people to do something: 市民に節電を呼びかける. Noun: 呼びかけ (appeal).","examples":[{"japanese":"知らない人に後ろから呼びかけられた。","romaji":"Shiranai hito ni ushiro kara yobikakerareta.","german":"A stranger called out to me from behind.","furigana":"し|ひと|うし|よ"},{"japanese":"市は住民に水を大切に使うよう呼びかけた。","romaji":"Shi wa jūmin ni mizu o taisetsu ni tsukau yō yobikaketa.","german":"The city urged residents to use water sparingly.","furigana":"し|じゅうみん|みず|たいせつ|つか|よ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"To call someone to oneself: 先生に呼び出される (to be summoned by the teacher); in IT also to call functions.","examples":[{"japanese":"授業のあと、先生に職員室に呼び出された。","romaji":"Jugyō no ato, sensei ni shokuinshitsu ni yobidasareta.","german":"After class, I was called to the staff room by the teacher.","furigana":"じゅぎょう|せんせい|しょくいんしつ|よ|だ"},{"japanese":"受付で担当者を呼び出してもらった。","romaji":"Uketsuke de tantōsha o yobidashite moratta.","german":"I had the person in charge paged at the reception desk.","furigana":"うけつけ|たんとうしゃ|よ|だ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"As a verb, 来日する - for foreigners coming to Japan: 大統領が来日する. Opposite: 離日.","examples":[{"japanese":"有名な歌手が来月来日する。","romaji":"Yūmei na kashu ga raigetsu rainichi suru.","german":"A famous singer will come to Japan next month.","furigana":"ゆうめい|かしゅ|らいげつらいにち"},{"japanese":"来日して三年になるが、まだ敬語が苦手だ。","romaji":"Rainichi shite sannen ni naru ga, mada keigo ga nigate da.","german":"It has been three years since I came to Japan, but I'm still bad at keigo.","furigana":"らいにち|さんねん|けいご|にがて"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The time of heaviest commuter traffic; often shortened to ラッシュ: 朝のラッシュ.","examples":[{"japanese":"ラッシュアワーの電車はとても込んでいる。","romaji":"Rasshuawā no densha wa totemo konde iru.","german":"Trains during rush hour are very crowded.","furigana":"でんしゃ|こ"},{"japanese":"ラッシュアワーを避けて、早めに家を出た。","romaji":"Rasshuawā o sakete, hayame ni ie o deta.","german":"To avoid the rush hour, I left home early.","furigana":"さ|はや|いえ|で"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Lunch, especially at a restaurant: ランチメニュー, 日替わりランチ (daily lunch special). Neutral: 昼ご飯.","examples":[{"japanese":"会社の近くの店でランチを食べた。","romaji":"Kaisha no chikaku no mise de ranchi o tabeta.","german":"I had lunch at a restaurant near the office.","furigana":"かいしゃ|ちか|みせ|た"},{"japanese":"この店のランチは千円で、コーヒー付きだ。","romaji":"Kono mise no ranchi wa sen'en de, kōhī-tsuki da.","german":"The lunch at this restaurant is 1,000 yen and comes with coffee.","furigana":"みせ|せんえん|つ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Running as a sport: 毎朝ランニングをする. Also short for a sleeveless undershirt (ランニングシャツ).","examples":[{"japanese":"毎朝、公園でランニングをしている。","romaji":"Maiasa, kōen de ranningu o shite iru.","german":"I go running in the park every morning.","furigana":"まいあさ|こうえん"},{"japanese":"暑いので、ランニング一枚で寝た。","romaji":"Atsui node, ranningu ichimai de neta.","german":"Because it was hot, I slept in just a tank top.","furigana":"あつ|いちまい|ね"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"School subject covering physics, chemistry, biology and earth science (地学). The science track in high school and university is called 理系 (opposite: 文系).","examples":[{"japanese":"理科の授業で植物の観察をした。","romaji":"Rika no jugyō de shokubutsu no kansatsu o shita.","german":"In science class we observed plants.","furigana":"りか|じゅぎょう|しょくぶつ|かんさつ"},{"japanese":"弟は理科が得意だ。","romaji":"Otōto wa rika ga tokui da.","german":"My younger brother is good at science.","furigana":"おとうと|りか|とくい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Unit of measurement for liquids (L). For how to count, see the counter リットル.","examples":[{"japanese":"このペットボトルには二リットル入る。","romaji":"Kono pettobotoru ni wa ni rittoru hairu.","german":"This plastic bottle holds two liters.","furigana":"に|はい"},{"japanese":"お風呂には何リットルのお湯が入りますか。","romaji":"Ofuro ni wa nan rittoru no oyu ga hairimasu ka.","german":"How many liters of hot water does the bathtub hold?","furigana":"ふろ|なん|ゆ|はい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Alternative spelling of レポート; especially common for TV reportage (現地からのリポート).","examples":[{"japanese":"記者が現地からリポートしている。","romaji":"Kisha ga genchi kara ripōto shite iru.","german":"A reporter is reporting live from the scene.","furigana":"きしゃ|げんち"},{"japanese":"来週までに実験のリポートを出してください。","romaji":"Raishū made ni jikken no ripōto o dashite kudasai.","german":"Please submit the lab report by next week.","furigana":"らいしゅう|じっけん|だ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A ribbon for gifts or hair: プレゼントにリボンをかける.","examples":[{"japanese":"プレゼントに赤いリボンをかけた。","romaji":"Purezento ni akai ribon o kaketa.","german":"I tied a red ribbon around the present.","furigana":"あか"},{"japanese":"娘は髪にリボンをつけている。","romaji":"Musume wa kami ni ribon o tsukete iru.","german":"My daughter is wearing a ribbon in her hair.","furigana":"むすめ|かみ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A dormitory for students or employees: 学生寮, 社員寮. Similar sounding: 量, 両.","examples":[{"japanese":"大学の寮に住んでいる。","romaji":"Daigaku no ryō ni sunde iru.","german":"I live in a university dormitory.","furigana":"だいがく|りょう|す"},{"japanese":"この会社には独身者のための寮がある。","romaji":"Kono kaisha ni wa dokushinsha no tame no ryō ga aru.","german":"This company has a dormitory for single employees.","furigana":"かいしゃ|どくしんしゃ|りょう"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 8 (source: N5; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"Formal, mainly in 領収書 (receipt) and as 領収する (to receive payment). On receipts: 上記正に領収いたしました.","examples":[{"japanese":"代金を確かに領収しました。","romaji":"Daikin o tashika ni ryōshū shimashita.","german":"We acknowledge receipt of the payment.","furigana":"だいきん|たし|りょうしゅう"},{"japanese":"領収の印をもらうのを忘れた。","romaji":"Ryōshū no in o morau no o wasureta.","german":"I forgot to get the receipt stamp.","furigana":"りょうしゅう|いん|わす"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A coat against rain; also 雨がっぱ (rain cape) or, for short, カッパ.","examples":[{"japanese":"雨の日は自転車に乗るとき、レインコートを着る。","romaji":"Ame no hi wa jitensha ni noru toki, reinkōto o kiru.","german":"On rainy days, I wear a raincoat when riding my bicycle.","furigana":"あめ|ひ|じてんしゃ|の|き"},{"japanese":"子どもに黄色いレインコートを買った。","romaji":"Kodomo ni kiiroi reinkōto o katta.","german":"I bought a yellow raincoat for my child.","furigana":"こ|きいろ|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Group games and activities for relaxation, for example at companies or schools: レクリエーションの時間. Also written レクリェーション.","examples":[{"japanese":"会社の旅行で、夜はレクリエーションの時間があった。","romaji":"Kaisha no ryokō de, yoru wa rekuriēshon no jikan ga atta.","german":"On the company trip, there was a recreation period in the evening.","furigana":"かいしゃ|りょこう|よる|じかん"},{"japanese":"この公園はレクリエーションに最適だ。","romaji":"Kono kōen wa rekuriēshon ni saiteki da.","german":"This park is ideal for recreation.","furigana":"こうえん|さいてき"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Leisure time and outings: レジャー施設 (leisure facility), 夏のレジャー.","examples":[{"japanese":"夏休みには家族でレジャーを楽しんだ。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi ni wa kazoku de rejā o tanoshinda.","german":"During summer vacation, we enjoyed leisure activities as a family.","furigana":"なつやす|かぞく|たの"},{"japanese":"この辺りはレジャー施設が多い。","romaji":"Kono atari wa rejā shisetsu ga ōi.","german":"There are many leisure facilities in this area.","furigana":"あた|しせつ|おお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Fired clay block for building: れんが造りの建物. Kanji 煉瓦, often also written レンガ.","examples":[{"japanese":"東京駅はれんがで造られている。","romaji":"Tōkyō eki wa renga de tsukurarete iru.","german":"Tokyo Station is built of brick.","furigana":"とうきょうえき|つく"},{"japanese":"庭にれんがを並べて花壇を作った。","romaji":"Niwa ni renga o narabete kadan o tsukutta.","german":"I lined up bricks in the garden and made a flower bed.","furigana":"にわ|なら|かだん|つく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Union of states or groups: 国際連合 (United Nations), 欧州連合 (EU). Verb: 連合する.","examples":[{"japanese":"いくつかの小さな政党が連合を組んだ。","romaji":"Ikutsuka no chiisa na seitō ga rengō o kunda.","german":"Several small political parties formed an alliance.","furigana":"ちい|せいとう|れんごう|く"},{"japanese":"近くの町が連合して祭りを開いた。","romaji":"Chikaku no machi ga rengō shite matsuri o hiraita.","german":"Neighboring towns joined together and held a festival.","furigana":"ちか|まち|れんごう|まつ|ひら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Glass lens in cameras or glasses; コンタクトレンズ (contact lens).","examples":[{"japanese":"カメラのレンズを布でふいた。","romaji":"Kamera no renzu o nuno de fuita.","german":"I wiped the camera lens with a cloth.","furigana":"ぬの"},{"japanese":"眼鏡のレンズに傷がついてしまった。","romaji":"Megane no renzu ni kizu ga tsuite shimatta.","german":"The lens of my glasses got scratched.","furigana":"めがね|きず"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Japanese in Latin letters, e.g. for names or computer input (ローマ字入力).","examples":[{"japanese":"名前をローマ字で書いてください。","romaji":"Namae o rōmaji de kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write your name in the Latin alphabet.","furigana":"なまえ|じ|か"},{"japanese":"パソコンではローマ字で日本語を入力する。","romaji":"Pasokon de wa rōmaji de nihongo o nyūryoku suru.","german":"On a computer, you type Japanese in romaji.","furigana":"じ|にほんご|にゅうりょく"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 9 (source: N5; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"Literary; same meaning as 論じる (N3), but conjugated on the ずる pattern. Idiom: 論ずるまでもない (goes without saying, needs no discussion).","examples":[{"japanese":"この本は日本の教育問題を論ずるものだ。","romaji":"Kono hon wa Nihon no kyōiku mondai o ronzuru mono da.","german":"This book discusses Japan's educational problems.","furigana":"ほん|にほん|きょういくもんだい|ろん"},{"japanese":"その案の良し悪しは、論ずるまでもない。","romaji":"Sono an no yoshiashi wa, ronzuru made mo nai.","german":"The merits and demerits of that proposal need no discussion.","furigana":"あん|よ|あ|ろん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Peaceful togetherness (和を大切にする), in mathematics the sum (二つの数の和) and as a prefix \"Japanese\" (和食, 和室).","examples":[{"japanese":"日本の会社では、チームの和が大切にされる。","romaji":"Nihon no kaisha de wa, chīmu no wa ga taisetsu ni sareru.","german":"In Japanese companies, team harmony is valued.","furigana":"にほん|かいしゃ|わ|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"三と五の和は八だ。","romaji":"San to go no wa wa hachi da.","german":"The sum of three and five is eight.","furigana":"さん|ご|わ|はち"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Mostly for dictionaries: 和英辞典 (Japanese-English dictionary). Opposite: 英和.","examples":[{"japanese":"和英辞典で「約束」を英語で何と言うか調べた。","romaji":"Waei jiten de \"yakusoku\" o eigo de nan to iu ka shirabeta.","german":"I looked up in a Japanese-English dictionary how to say 約束 in English.","furigana":"わえいじてん|やくそく|えいご|なん|い|しら"},{"japanese":"この和英は例文が多くて使いやすい。","romaji":"Kono waei wa reibun ga ōkute tsukaiyasui.","german":"This Japanese-English dictionary has many example sentences and is easy to use.","furigana":"わえい|れいぶん|おお|つか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Intransitive: 道が二つに分かれる, 意見が分かれる (opinions are divided). Do not confuse with 別れる (to part from someone).","examples":[{"japanese":"この先で道が二つに分かれている。","romaji":"Kono saki de michi ga futatsu ni wakarete iru.","german":"The road splits in two up ahead.","furigana":"さき|みち|ふた|わ"},{"japanese":"その問題については、専門家の意見が分かれている。","romaji":"Sono mondai ni tsuite wa, senmonka no iken ga wakarete iru.","german":"Experts' opinions are divided on that issue.","furigana":"もんだい|せんもんか|いけん|わ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"\"Compared with what one expects\": 割合に簡単だった. Similar: 割と, 比較的.","examples":[{"japanese":"試験は思ったより割合に簡単だった。","romaji":"Shiken wa omotta yori wariai ni kantan datta.","german":"The exam was comparatively easier than I expected.","furigana":"しけん|おも|わりあい|かんたん"},{"japanese":"今日は割合に暖かい。","romaji":"Kyō wa wariai ni atatakai.","german":"It's fairly warm today.","furigana":"きょう|わりあい|あたた"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Lacquered wooden bowl for soup (お椀). Porcelain bowls are written 碗 (茶碗). Also a counter: 一椀.","examples":[{"japanese":"お椀にみそ汁を入れた。","romaji":"Owan ni misoshiru o ireta.","german":"I put miso soup into the bowl.","furigana":"わん|しる|い"},{"japanese":"このお椀は祖母が大切にしていたものだ。","romaji":"Kono owan wa sobo ga taisetsu ni shite ita mono da.","german":"This bowl was precious to my grandmother.","furigana":"わん|そぼ|たいせつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The week before last week. Likewise: 先々月 (the month before last).","examples":[{"japanese":"先々週の土曜日に引っ越した。","romaji":"Sensenshū no doyōbi ni hikkoshita.","german":"I moved on Saturday the week before last.","furigana":"せんせんしゅう|どようび|ひ|こ"},{"japanese":"その本は先々週から借りたままだ。","romaji":"Sono hon wa sensenshū kara karita mama da.","german":"I've had that book on loan since the week before last.","furigana":"ほん|せんせんしゅう|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Mostly as 転々とする: 職を転々とする (to change jobs frequently), 各地を転々とする.","examples":[{"japanese":"父の仕事の関係で、子どもの頃は各地を転々とした。","romaji":"Chichi no shigoto no kankei de, kodomo no koro wa kakuchi o tenten to shita.","german":"Because of my father's work, I moved around from place to place in childhood.","furigana":"ちち|しごと|かんけい|こ|ころ|かくち|てんてん"},{"japanese":"彼は若い頃、仕事を転々としていた。","romaji":"Kare wa wakai koro, shigoto o tenten to shite ita.","german":"In his youth he drifted from job to job.","furigana":"かれ|わか|ころ|しごと|てんてん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"On its own mainly in set phrases: 機が熟す (the time is ripe), 機を逃す (to miss the opportunity), 機を見る (to wait for the right moment). As a suffix also \"machine\" (洗濯機) and \"aircraft\" (旅客機).","examples":[{"japanese":"計画を発表するには、まだ機が熟していない。","romaji":"Keikaku o happyō suru ni wa, mada ki ga jukushite inai.","german":"The time is not yet ripe to announce the plan.","furigana":"けいかく|はっぴょう|き|じゅく"},{"japanese":"彼は機を見て、社長に新しい提案をした。","romaji":"Kare wa ki o mite, shachō ni atarashii teian o shita.","german":"He waited for the right moment and made a new proposal to the president.","furigana":"かれ|き|み|しゃちょう|あたら|ていあん"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 28"},
{"notes":"Mostly smaller devices and instruments without their own power, for example in medicine, laboratories or sports (器械体操 = apparatus gymnastics). Large, powered machines are called 機械（きかい）.","examples":[{"japanese":"この実験には特別な器械が必要だ。","romaji":"Kono jikken ni wa tokubetsu na kikai ga hitsuyō da.","german":"This experiment requires a special apparatus.","furigana":"じっけん|とくべつ|きかい|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"手術の前に、医者は器械を一つずつ確認した。","romaji":"Shujutsu no mae ni, isha wa kikai o hitotsu zutsu kakunin shita.","german":"Before the operation, the doctor checked the instruments one by one.","furigana":"しゅじゅつ|まえ|いしゃ|きかい|ひと|かくにん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"The engine that pulls cars: 蒸気機関車 (steam locomotive, SL for short), 電気機関車 (electric locomotive).","examples":[{"japanese":"古い機関車が駅に展示されている。","romaji":"Furui kikansha ga eki ni tenji sarete iru.","german":"An old locomotive is on display at the station.","furigana":"ふる|きかんしゃ|えき|てんじ"},{"japanese":"機関車が長い貨物列車を引いて走っていく。","romaji":"Kikansha ga nagai kamotsu ressha o hiite hashitte iku.","german":"The locomotive runs along pulling a long freight train.","furigana":"きかんしゃ|なが|かもつれっしゃ|ひ|はし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Famine after crop failures. Also figuratively a severe shortage: 水飢饉 (water shortage).","examples":[{"japanese":"昔、この地方では大きな飢饉が何度も起きた。","romaji":"Mukashi, kono chihō de wa ōki na kikin ga nando mo okita.","german":"Long ago, great famines occurred many times in this region.","furigana":"むかし|ちほう|おお|ききん|なんど|お"},{"japanese":"雨が降らず、夏には水の飢饉になった。","romaji":"Ame ga furazu, natsu ni wa mizu no kikin ni natta.","german":"It did not rain, so there was an acute water shortage in summer.","furigana":"あめ|ふ|なつ|みず|ききん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Transitive ichidan verb corresponding to 着る: 子どもに服を着せる (to dress a child). Figuratively: 罪を着せる (to pin a crime on someone).","examples":[{"japanese":"母は赤ちゃんに暖かい服を着せた。","romaji":"Haha wa akachan ni atatakai fuku o kiseta.","german":"The mother dressed the baby in warm clothes.","furigana":"はは|あか|あたた|ふく|き"},{"japanese":"彼は自分の失敗の責任を部下に着せた。","romaji":"Kare wa jibun no shippai no sekinin o buka ni kiseta.","german":"He put the blame for his mistake on a subordinate.","furigana":"かれ|じぶん|しっぱい|せきにん|ぶか|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A state of matter alongside 固体 (solid) and 液体 (liquid). Homophone: 期待 (expectation).","examples":[{"japanese":"水は熱すると気体になる。","romaji":"Mizu wa nessuru to kitai ni naru.","german":"Water turns into gas when heated.","furigana":"みず|ねっ|きたい"},{"japanese":"この気体には色もにおいもない。","romaji":"Kono kitai ni wa iro mo nioi mo nai.","german":"This gas has neither color nor smell.","furigana":"きたい|いろ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Military base (米軍基地) or base of operations, e.g. 南極の観測基地 (research station in Antarctica), 秘密基地 (children's secret hideout).","examples":[{"japanese":"島には大きな軍の基地がある。","romaji":"Shima ni wa ōki na gun no kichi ga aru.","german":"There is a large military base on the island.","furigana":"しま|おお|ぐん|きち"},{"japanese":"子どものころ、森に秘密の基地を作った。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, mori ni himitsu no kichi o tsukutta.","german":"As a child, I built a secret hideout in the forest.","furigana":"こ|もり|ひみつ|きち|つく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Something is so full that no room remains: 予定がぎっしり (a full schedule), 本がぎっしり詰まった棚.","examples":[{"japanese":"箱にはりんごがぎっしり詰まっていた。","romaji":"Hako ni wa ringo ga gisshiri tsumatte ita.","german":"The box was packed full of apples.","furigana":"はこ|つ"},{"japanese":"今週は予定がぎっしりで、休む暇がない。","romaji":"Konshū wa yotei ga gisshiri de, yasumu hima ga nai.","german":"My schedule is jam-packed this week; I have no time to rest.","furigana":"こんしゅう|よてい|やす|ひま"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The seats for guests in a theater, stadium or restaurant, and figuratively the audience itself: 客席から拍手が起こった.","examples":[{"japanese":"コンサートが始まる前に、客席はいっぱいになった。","romaji":"Konsāto ga hajimaru mae ni, kyakuseki wa ippai ni natta.","german":"The seats filled up before the concert started.","furigana":"はじ|まえ|きゃくせき"},{"japanese":"歌手が手を振ると、客席から大きな拍手が起こった。","romaji":"Kashu ga te o furu to, kyakuseki kara ōki na hakushu ga okotta.","german":"When the singer waved, loud applause broke out from the audience.","furigana":"かしゅ|て|ふ|きゃくせき|おお|はくしゅ|お"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A room in a house where guests are received; in traditional houses often a tatami room (和室).","examples":[{"japanese":"お客さんを客間に案内した。","romaji":"Okyakusan o kyakuma ni annai shita.","german":"I showed the guest into the reception room.","furigana":"きゃく|きゃくま|あんない"},{"japanese":"祖父の家には広い客間がある。","romaji":"Sofu no ie ni wa hiroi kyakuma ga aru.","german":"My grandfather's house has a large guest room.","furigana":"そふ|いえ|ひろ|きゃくま"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"An armed criminal gang, especially in films about America. The Japanese mafia is called 暴力団 or ヤクザ.","examples":[{"japanese":"映画の中でギャングが銀行を襲った。","romaji":"Eiga no naka de gyangu ga ginkō o osotta.","german":"In the movie, gangsters robbed a bank.","furigana":"えいが|なか|ぎんこう|おそ"},{"japanese":"その町はギャングの争いで危険になった。","romaji":"Sono machi wa gyangu no arasoi de kiken ni natta.","german":"The town became dangerous because of gang fights.","furigana":"まち|あらそ|きけん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The grounds of a university. Many universities have several キャンパス in different parts of the city.","examples":[{"japanese":"このキャンパスには図書館が三つある。","romaji":"Kono kyanpasu ni wa toshokan ga mittsu aru.","german":"This campus has three libraries.","furigana":"としょかん|みっ"},{"japanese":"春になると、キャンパスは新入生でにぎやかになる。","romaji":"Haru ni naru to, kyanpasu wa shinnyūsei de nigiyaka ni naru.","german":"In spring, the campus becomes lively with new students.","furigana":"はる|しんにゅうせい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A class is canceled; as a verb 休講する / 休講になる. Notices about it are posted on the 掲示板 (bulletin board) or sent via app.","examples":[{"japanese":"今日の二時間目は休講です。","romaji":"Kyō no nijikanme wa kyūkō desu.","german":"Second period today is canceled.","furigana":"きょう|にじかんめ|きゅうこう"},{"japanese":"先生が病気で、授業が休講になった。","romaji":"Sensei ga byōki de, jugyō ga kyūkō ni natta.","german":"The teacher was sick, so the class was canceled.","furigana":"せんせい|びょうき|じゅぎょう|きゅうこう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"More formal than 休み or 休憩: sufficient rest, e.g. 十分な休息をとる.","examples":[{"japanese":"医者に十分な休息をとるように言われた。","romaji":"Isha ni jūbun na kyūsoku o toru yō ni iwareta.","german":"The doctor told me to get plenty of rest.","furigana":"いしゃ|じゅうぶん|きゅうそく|い"},{"japanese":"長い仕事のあとには休息が必要だ。","romaji":"Nagai shigoto no ato ni wa kyūsoku ga hitsuyō da.","german":"After long work, you need rest.","furigana":"なが|しごと|きゅうそく|ひつよう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A line of text: 三行目 (the third line), 一行あける (to leave one line blank). Also the rows of the kana table: あ行, か行.","examples":[{"japanese":"最初の行に名前を書いてください。","romaji":"Saisho no gyō ni namae o kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write your name on the first line.","furigana":"さいしょ|ぎょう|なまえ|か"},{"japanese":"この文は長いので、二行に分けた。","romaji":"Kono bun wa nagai node, nigyō ni waketa.","german":"The sentence is long, so I split it into two lines.","furigana":"ぶん|なが|にぎょう|わ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Opposite: 奇数（きすう） (odd number).","examples":[{"japanese":"二、四、六は偶数です。","romaji":"Ni, yon, roku wa gūsū desu.","german":"Two, four and six are even numbers.","furigana":"に|よん|ろく|ぐうすう"},{"japanese":"偶数の日だけ、ごみを出せます。","romaji":"Gūsū no hi dake, gomi o dasemasu.","german":"Garbage can be put out only on even-numbered days.","furigana":"ぐうすう|ひ|だ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The space above the ground: 空中に浮かぶ (to float in the air), 空中ブランコ (circus trapeze).","examples":[{"japanese":"風船が空中に浮かんでいる。","romaji":"Fūsen ga kūchū ni ukande iru.","german":"A balloon is floating in the air.","furigana":"ふうせん|くうちゅう|う"},{"japanese":"選手は空中で一回転して着地した。","romaji":"Senshu wa kūchū de ikkaiten shite chakuchi shita.","german":"The athlete did one rotation in the air and landed.","furigana":"せんしゅ|くうちゅう|いっかいてん|ちゃくち"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Nails are driven in: 釘を打つ. Idiom: 釘を刺す (to warn someone firmly). Often also in kana くぎ.","examples":[{"japanese":"壁に釘を打って、絵を掛けた。","romaji":"Kabe ni kugi o utte, e o kaketa.","german":"I hammered a nail into the wall and hung a picture.","furigana":"かべ|くぎ|う|え|か"},{"japanese":"古い板から錆びた釘を抜いた。","romaji":"Furui ita kara sabita kugi o nuita.","german":"I pulled rusty nails out of the old board.","furigana":"ふる|いた|さ|くぎ|ぬ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Transitive godan verb: 部屋を区切る (to partition a room), 文を区切って読む (to read a sentence in segments). Noun: 区切り (break, section).","examples":[{"japanese":"大きな部屋をカーテンで二つに区切った。","romaji":"Ōki na heya o kāten de futatsu ni kugitta.","german":"I divided the large room into two with a curtain.","furigana":"おお|へや|ふた|くぎ"},{"japanese":"長い文は、意味ごとに区切って読むと分かりやすい。","romaji":"Nagai bun wa, imi goto ni kugitte yomu to wakariyasui.","german":"Long sentences are easier to understand if you read them broken up by units of meaning.","furigana":"なが|ぶん|いみ|くぎ|よ|わ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"\"To comb one's hair\": 髪を櫛でとかす. Often written in kana as くし; a brush is called ブラシ.","examples":[{"japanese":"出かける前に、櫛で髪をとかした。","romaji":"Dekakeru mae ni, kushi de kami o tokashita.","german":"Before going out, I combed my hair.","furigana":"で|まえ|くし|かみ"},{"japanese":"祖母は古い木の櫛を大切にしている。","romaji":"Sobo wa furui ki no kushi o taisetsu ni shite iru.","german":"My grandmother treasures an old wooden comb.","furigana":"そぼ|ふる|き|くし|たいせつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Much effort and ingenuity put into something: 苦心の作 (a painstakingly produced work), 苦心して完成させる.","examples":[{"japanese":"この料理は、祖母が苦心して考えたものだ。","romaji":"Kono ryōri wa, sobo ga kushin shite kangaeta mono da.","german":"My grandmother came up with this dish through much painstaking effort.","furigana":"りょうり|そぼ|くしん|かんが"},{"japanese":"作家は十年の苦心の末に、その小説を書き上げた。","romaji":"Sakka wa jūnen no kushin no sue ni, sono shōsetsu o kakiageta.","german":"After ten years of effort, the author completed the novel.","furigana":"さっか|じゅうねん|くしん|すえ|しょうせつ|か|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Small leftovers and waste: 紙くず (scraps of paper), パンくず (bread crumbs). Often in kana くず. As an insult (人間のくず) it is very offensive.","examples":[{"japanese":"テーブルの上にパンの屑が落ちている。","romaji":"Tēburu no ue ni pan no kuzu ga ochite iru.","german":"Bread crumbs are lying on the table.","furigana":"うえ|くず|お"},{"japanese":"工場では金属の屑も再利用している。","romaji":"Kōjō de wa kinzoku no kuzu mo sairiyō shite iru.","german":"The factory also reuses metal scraps.","furigana":"こうじょう|きんぞく|くず|さいりよう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The fourth finger, on which the wedding ring is usually worn in Japan. The fingers: 親指, 人差し指, 中指, 薬指, 小指.","examples":[{"japanese":"左手の薬指に指輪をしている。","romaji":"Hidarite no kusuriyubi ni yubiwa o shite iru.","german":"She wears a ring on the ring finger of her left hand.","furigana":"ひだりて|くすりゆび|ゆびわ"},{"japanese":"ドアに挟んで、薬指をけがした。","romaji":"Doa ni hasande, kusuriyubi o kega shita.","german":"I caught my ring finger in the door and hurt it.","furigana":"はさ|くすりゆび"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Ichidan verb, more colloquial than 疲れる. Also for things worn out by long use: くたびれた靴. Kanji 草臥れる is rare.","examples":[{"japanese":"一日中歩いて、すっかりくたびれた。","romaji":"Ichinichijū aruite, sukkari kutabireta.","german":"I walked all day and am completely exhausted.","furigana":"いちにちじゅうある"},{"japanese":"父はくたびれたかばんを今も使っている。","romaji":"Chichi wa kutabireta kaban o ima mo tsukatte iru.","german":"My father still uses his worn-out bag.","furigana":"ちち|いま|つか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Upper lip: 上唇, lower lip: 下唇. Lipstick is called 口紅.","examples":[{"japanese":"寒くて唇が紫色になった。","romaji":"Samukute kuchibiru ga murasakiiro ni natta.","german":"It was cold and my lips turned purple.","furigana":"さむ|くちびる|むらさきいろ"},{"japanese":"冬は唇が乾燥しやすい。","romaji":"Fuyu wa kuchibiru ga kansō shiyasui.","german":"In winter, lips dry out easily.","furigana":"ふゆ|くちびる|かんそう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Transitive ichidan verb corresponding to くっつく (to stick): のりで紙をくっつける, 机をくっつける (to push desks together). More colloquial than 付ける.","examples":[{"japanese":"割れたお皿を接着剤でくっつけた。","romaji":"Wareta osara o setchakuzai de kuttsuketa.","german":"I glued the broken plate back together with adhesive.","furigana":"わ|さら|せっちゃくざい"},{"japanese":"グループ活動のために、机を四つくっつけた。","romaji":"Gurūpu katsudō no tame ni, tsukue o yottsu kuttsuketa.","german":"For the group activity, we pushed four desks together.","furigana":"かつどう|つくえ|よっ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"I-adjective: someone repeats the same thing annoyingly often (説明がくどい) or a taste is too strong and heavy (くどい味). Kanji 諄い is rare.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼の説明はくどくて、途中で眠くなった。","romaji":"Kare no setsumei wa kudokute, tochū de nemuku natta.","german":"His explanation was so long-winded that I got sleepy halfway through.","furigana":"かれ|せつめい|とちゅう|ねむ"},{"japanese":"このソースは甘すぎて、少しくどい。","romaji":"Kono sōsu wa amasugite, sukoshi kudoi.","german":"This sauce is too sweet and a little heavy.","furigana":"あま|すこ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Transitive godan verb: 井戸の水を汲む (to draw water from a well). Figuratively: 気持ちを汲む (to sympathize with and take someone's feelings into account). Homophone: 組む (to put together).","examples":[{"japanese":"昔は毎朝、井戸から水を汲んでいた。","romaji":"Mukashi wa maiasa, ido kara mizu o kunde ita.","german":"In the past, we drew water from the well every morning.","furigana":"むかし|まいあさ|いど|みず|く"},{"japanese":"彼女の気持ちを汲んで、何も聞かなかった。","romaji":"Kanojo no kimochi o kunde, nani mo kikanakatta.","german":"Out of consideration for her feelings, I didn't ask anything.","furigana":"かのじょ|きも|く|なに|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"In everyday use, an air conditioner for cooling (today usually called エアコン) and a cooler for drinks (クーラーボックス).","examples":[{"japanese":"暑いので、部屋のクーラーをつけた。","romaji":"Atsui node, heya no kūrā o tsuketa.","german":"Because it was hot, I turned on the air conditioner in the room.","furigana":"あつ|へや"},{"japanese":"クーラーの効きすぎで、体が冷えてしまった。","romaji":"Kūrā no kikisugi de, karada ga hiete shimatta.","german":"The air conditioner was set too cold, and I got chilled.","furigana":"き|からだ|ひ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Usually professional cleaning: クリーニングに出す (to take to the cleaner's); the shop is called クリーニング屋.","examples":[{"japanese":"スーツをクリーニングに出した。","romaji":"Sūtsu o kurīningu ni dashita.","german":"I took my suit to the dry cleaner's.","furigana":"だ"},{"japanese":"コートをクリーニングしてもらったら、新品のようになった。","romaji":"Kōto o kurīningu shite morattara, shinpin no yō ni natta.","german":"After I had the coat cleaned, it looked like new.","furigana":"しんぴん"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 27"},
{"notes":"くるむ means \"to wrap up, to swaddle\". The object being wrapped takes を, the material takes で: 赤ちゃんを毛布でくるむ. This godan verb forms くるんで and くるんだ. 包む is also read くるむ here; the same kanji read つつむ can be a more general verb meaning \"to wrap\".","examples":[{"japanese":"赤ちゃんを暖かい毛布でくるんだ。","romaji":"Akachan o atatakai mōfu de kurunda.","german":"I wrapped the baby in a warm blanket.","furigana":"あか|あたた|もうふ"},{"japanese":"割れないように、コップを新聞紙でくるんだ。","romaji":"Warenai yō ni, koppu o shinbunshi de kurunda.","german":"So that it wouldn't break, I wrapped the glass in newspaper.","furigana":"わ|しんぶんし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Strengthens polite requests and greetings: くれぐれもお体に気をつけてください (please take good care of yourself), くれぐれもよろしくお伝えください.","examples":[{"japanese":"くれぐれもお体を大切にしてください。","romaji":"Kureguremo okarada o taisetsu ni shite kudasai.","german":"Please take good care of yourself.","furigana":"からだ|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"この書類はくれぐれも外に出さないように。","romaji":"Kono shorui wa kureguremo soto ni dasanai yō ni.","german":"Make sure you do not let these documents out of the building.","furigana":"しょるい|そと|だ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Ichidan verb: 犬がボールを咥える (the dog carries the ball in its mouth), たばこを咥える. Also written 銜える. Homophone: 加える (to add).","examples":[{"japanese":"犬が新聞を咥えて持ってきた。","romaji":"Inu ga shinbun o kuwaete motte kita.","german":"The dog brought the newspaper in its mouth.","furigana":"いぬ|しんぶん|くわ|も"},{"japanese":"父はたばこを咥えたまま話していた。","romaji":"Chichi wa tabako o kuwaeta mama hanashite ita.","german":"My father was talking with a cigarette in his mouth.","furigana":"ちち|くわ|はな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The classic ceiling light in Japanese offices and homes, now often replaced by LEDs. Colloquially also used for someone slow to react.","examples":[{"japanese":"台所の蛍光灯が切れてしまった。","romaji":"Daidokoro no keikōtō ga kirete shimatta.","german":"The fluorescent light in the kitchen has burned out.","furigana":"だいどころ|けいこうとう|き"},{"japanese":"蛍光灯をLEDに取り替えた。","romaji":"Keikōtō o eruīdī ni torikaeta.","german":"I replaced the fluorescent light with an LED.","furigana":"けいこうとう|と|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A connected whole: バスの系統 (bus route), 電気系統 (electrical system), 同じ系統の色 (colors of the same group). Also means line of descent.","examples":[{"japanese":"駅前から出るバスは系統が多くて、どれに乗ればいいか分かりにくい。","romaji":"Ekimae kara deru basu wa keitō ga ōkute, dore ni noreba ii ka wakarinikui.","german":"So many bus routes leave from the station square that it is hard to tell which bus to take.","furigana":"えきまえ|で|けいとう|おお|の|わ"},{"japanese":"カーテンを壁と同じ系統の色にした。","romaji":"Kāten o kabe to onaji keitō no iro ni shita.","german":"I chose curtains in the same color family as the walls.","furigana":"かべ|おな|けいとう|いろ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Film, television, music and traditional stage arts: 伝統芸能 (traditional arts such as Kabuki and Noh). 芸能人 = celebrities from the entertainment industry.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は芸能の世界で二十年働いている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa geinō no sekai de nijūnen hataraite iru.","german":"She has worked in show business for twenty years.","furigana":"かのじょ|げいのう|せかい|にじゅうねんはたら"},{"japanese":"この村には古い芸能が今も残っている。","romaji":"Kono mura ni wa furui geinō ga ima mo nokotte iru.","german":"Old folk performing arts still survive in this village.","furigana":"むら|ふる|げいのう|いま|のこ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Horse racing with betting; the racecourse is called 競馬場. Other officially sanctioned racing bets: 競輪 (bicycle racing), 競艇 (boat racing).","examples":[{"japanese":"週末に父と競馬を見に行った。","romaji":"Shūmatsu ni chichi to keiba o mi ni itta.","german":"I went to watch the horse races with my father at the weekend.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|ちち|けいば|み|い"},{"japanese":"彼は競馬で大金を失った。","romaji":"Kare wa keiba de taikin o ushinatta.","german":"He lost a lot of money on horse racing.","furigana":"かれ|けいば|たいきん|うしな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Written style, often in the news; as a verb 激増する. Opposite: 激減 (sharp decrease).","examples":[{"japanese":"この十年で外国人観光客が激増した。","romaji":"Kono jūnen de gaikokujin kankōkyaku ga gekizō shita.","german":"Over the past ten years, the number of foreign tourists has risen sharply.","furigana":"じゅうねん|がいこくじんかんこうきゃく|げきぞう"},{"japanese":"事故の激増を受けて、市は対策を始めた。","romaji":"Jiko no gekizō o ukete, shi wa taisaku o hajimeta.","german":"In response to the sharp increase in accidents, the city began taking countermeasures.","furigana":"じこ|げきぞう|う|し|たいさく|はじ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Formal, typical in announcements and directions: 〇〇駅下車 (get off at 〇〇 Station), 途中下車 (breaking one's journey). Everyday word: 降りる.","examples":[{"japanese":"博物館へは、東駅で下車して徒歩五分です。","romaji":"Hakubutsukan e wa, Higashieki de gesha shite toho gofun desu.","german":"For the museum, get off at East Station and walk five minutes.","furigana":"はくぶつかん|ひがしえき|げしゃ|とほごふん"},{"japanese":"この切符は、途中の駅で下車すると無効になります。","romaji":"Kono kippu wa, tochū no eki de gesha suru to mukō ni narimasu.","german":"This ticket becomes invalid if you get off at a station along the way.","furigana":"きっぷ|とちゅう|えき|げしゃ|むこう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Wastewater and the pipes for it: 下水道 (sewer system). Opposite: 上水 (clean water supply).","examples":[{"japanese":"大雨で下水があふれた。","romaji":"Ōame de gesui ga afureta.","german":"The sewers overflowed because of the heavy rain.","furigana":"おおあめ|げすい"},{"japanese":"油を下水に流さないでください。","romaji":"Abura o gesui ni nagasanaide kudasai.","german":"Please do not pour oil into the drain.","furigana":"あぶら|げすい|なが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The salary paid every month; compare 時給 (hourly wage), 年収 (annual income).","examples":[{"japanese":"新しい会社は月給がいい。","romaji":"Atarashii kaisha wa gekkyū ga ii.","german":"The new company pays a good monthly salary.","furigana":"あたら|かいしゃ|げっきゅう"},{"japanese":"月給の半分を家賃に使っている。","romaji":"Gekkyū no hanbun o yachin ni tsukatte iru.","german":"I spend half of my monthly salary on rent.","furigana":"げっきゅう|はんぶん|やちん|つか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Original region of origin of plants, animals and products: 南米原産, 原産地 (place of origin), 原産国 (country of origin).","examples":[{"japanese":"トマトは南アメリカ原産の野菜だ。","romaji":"Tomato wa Minami Amerika gensan no yasai da.","german":"The tomato is a vegetable native to South America.","furigana":"みなみ|げんさん|やさい"},{"japanese":"この花の原産はアフリカだそうだ。","romaji":"Kono hana no gensan wa Afurika da sō da.","german":"This flower is said to be native to Africa.","furigana":"はな|げんさん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The earliest, untouched beginnings: 原始時代 (primeval age), 原始的 (primitive), 原始の森 (primeval forest). Homophone: 原子 (atom).","examples":[{"japanese":"島には原始のままの森が残っている。","romaji":"Shima ni wa genshi no mama no mori ga nokotte iru.","german":"On the island, forest remains just as it was in primeval times.","furigana":"しま|げんし|もり|のこ"},{"japanese":"人間は原始の昔から火を使ってきた。","romaji":"Ningen wa genshi no mukashi kara hi o tsukatte kita.","german":"Humans have used fire since primeval times.","furigana":"にんげん|げんし|むかし|ひ|つか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Administrative seat of a prefecture; the city where it is located is called 県庁所在地 (prefectural capital). In Tokyo, the equivalent is 都庁.","examples":[{"japanese":"県庁でパスポートを申請した。","romaji":"Kenchō de pasupōto o shinsei shita.","german":"I applied for a passport at the prefectural government office.","furigana":"けんちょう|しんせい"},{"japanese":"県庁は駅から歩いて十分ほどです。","romaji":"Kenchō wa eki kara aruite jippun hodo desu.","german":"The prefectural office is about a ten-minute walk from the station.","furigana":"けんちょう|えき|ある|じっぷん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Strategy game with black and white stones, also called 囲碁; \"to play Go\" is 碁を打つ. Homophone: 五 (five).","examples":[{"japanese":"祖父は毎晩友達と碁を打っている。","romaji":"Sofu wa maiban tomodachi to go o utte iru.","german":"My grandfather plays Go with his friends every evening.","furigana":"そふ|まいばんともだち|ご|う"},{"japanese":"碁のルールは簡単だが、強くなるのは難しい。","romaji":"Go no rūru wa kantan da ga, tsuyoku naru no wa muzukashii.","german":"The rules of Go are simple, but it is hard to get good at it.","furigana":"ご|かんたん|つよ|むずか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Formal godan verb like 問う (te-form 請うて): 許しを請う (to beg forgiveness), 教えを請う (to ask for instruction). Also written 乞う.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は深く頭を下げて許しを請うた。","romaji":"Kare wa fukaku atama o sagete yurushi o kōta.","german":"He bowed deeply and begged for forgiveness.","furigana":"かれ|ふか|あたま|さ|ゆる|こ"},{"japanese":"有名な先生に教えを請うために、遠くから来た。","romaji":"Yūmei na sensei ni oshie o kou tame ni, tōku kara kita.","german":"He came from far away to ask a famous teacher for instruction.","furigana":"ゆうめい|せんせい|おし|こ|とお|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Issue of a magazine (次の号, 最新号), serial number (三号室, 台風十号) and name suffix for ships or trains (のぞみ号).","examples":[{"japanese":"この雑誌の次の号は来月発売される。","romaji":"Kono zasshi no tsugi no gō wa raigetsu hatsubai sareru.","german":"The next issue of this magazine will go on sale next month.","furigana":"ざっし|つぎ|ごう|らいげつはつばい"},{"japanese":"その記事は先月の号に載っていた。","romaji":"Sono kiji wa sengetsu no gō ni notte ita.","german":"That article appeared in last month's issue.","furigana":"きじ|せんげつ|ごう|の"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A worker in a factory; today often replaced by the more neutral 作業員 or 従業員.","examples":[{"japanese":"この工場では五百人の工員が働いている。","romaji":"Kono kōjō de wa gohyakunin no kōin ga hataraite iru.","german":"Five hundred workers are employed at this factory.","furigana":"こうじょう|ごひゃくにん|こういん|はたら"},{"japanese":"父は若いころ、自動車工場の工員だった。","romaji":"Chichi wa wakai koro, jidōsha kōjō no kōin datta.","german":"When he was young, my father was a worker at a car factory.","furigana":"ちち|わか|じどうしゃこうじょう|こういん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Lines or paths cross: 交差する, 交差点 (intersection). Homophone: 考査 (examination).","examples":[{"japanese":"二本の道路がここで交差している。","romaji":"Nihon no dōro ga koko de kōsa shite iru.","german":"Two roads cross here.","furigana":"にほん|どうろ|こうさ"},{"japanese":"線路と道路が交差する所には踏切がある。","romaji":"Senro to dōro ga kōsa suru tokoro ni wa fumikiri ga aru.","german":"There is a railroad crossing where the tracks and the road intersect.","furigana":"せんろ|どうろ|こうさ|ところ|ふみきり"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Introduces the result in a narrative (\"And so ...\") or means \"in this way\". The kanji 斯うして is obsolete. Compare そうして, ああして.","examples":[{"japanese":"こうして二人は結婚することになった。","romaji":"Kōshite futari wa kekkon suru koto ni natta.","german":"And so the two of them ended up getting married.","furigana":"ふたり|けっこん"},{"japanese":"こうして毎日少しずつ練習すれば、きっと上手になる。","romaji":"Kōshite mainichi sukoshi zutsu renshū sureba, kitto jōzu ni naru.","german":"If you practice a little every day like this, you will surely get better.","furigana":"まいにちすこ|れんしゅう|じょうず"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The building of a school; 新校舎 (new building), 旧校舎 (old school building). The school grounds are called 校庭 or キャンパス.","examples":[{"japanese":"古い校舎が来年取り壊される。","romaji":"Furui kōsha ga rainen torikowasareru.","german":"The old school building will be demolished next year.","furigana":"ふる|こうしゃ|らいねんと|こわ"},{"japanese":"新しい校舎にはエレベーターがある。","romaji":"Atarashii kōsha ni wa erebētā ga aru.","german":"The new school building has an elevator.","furigana":"あたら|こうしゃ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Mostly in compounds and set expressions: 公衆電話 (public telephone), 公衆トイレ (public toilet), 公衆の面前で (in full view of the public).","examples":[{"japanese":"公衆の前で大声を出すのはやめなさい。","romaji":"Kōshū no mae de ōgoe o dasu no wa yamenasai.","german":"Stop shouting in front of everyone.","furigana":"こうしゅう|まえ|おおごえ|だ"},{"japanese":"その施設は公衆のために無料で開かれている。","romaji":"Sono shisetsu wa kōshū no tame ni muryō de hirakarete iru.","german":"The facility is open to the public free of charge.","furigana":"しせつ|こうしゅう|むりょう|ひら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Rays of light, also in physics: 太陽光線 (sunbeams), レーザー光線 (laser beam).","examples":[{"japanese":"強い光線は目に悪い。","romaji":"Tsuyoi kōsen wa me ni warui.","german":"Strong light is bad for the eyes.","furigana":"つよ|こうせん|め|わる"},{"japanese":"窓から朝の光線が差し込んでいる。","romaji":"Mado kara asa no kōsen ga sashikonde iru.","german":"The morning light is streaming in through the window.","furigana":"まど|あさ|こうせん|さ|こ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Mostly attributive: 高層ビル (high-rise building), 高層マンション (high-rise apartment building), 高層の建物. In meteorology also \"upper layers of the atmosphere\" (高層の雲).","examples":[{"japanese":"駅前に高層の建物が次々と建てられている。","romaji":"Ekimae ni kōsō no tatemono ga tsugitsugi to taterarete iru.","german":"High-rise buildings are being built one after another in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|こうそう|たてもの|つぎつぎ|た"},{"japanese":"高層の雲が広がると、天気が崩れることが多い。","romaji":"Kōsō no kumo ga hirogaru to, tenki ga kuzureru koto ga ōi.","german":"When high-altitude clouds spread, the weather often turns bad.","furigana":"こうそう|くも|ひろ|てんき|くず|おお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The area in front of the school building for breaks, sports and ceremonies. Homophones: 肯定 (affirmation), 工程 (work process).","examples":[{"japanese":"休み時間に子どもたちが校庭で遊んでいる。","romaji":"Yasumijikan ni kodomotachi ga kōtei de asonde iru.","german":"Children are playing in the schoolyard during break.","furigana":"やす|じかん|こ|こうてい|あそ"},{"japanese":"雨で校庭が使えないので、体育は体育館で行う。","romaji":"Ame de kōtei ga tsukaenai node, taiiku wa taiikukan de okonau.","german":"The schoolyard can't be used because of rain, so PE is held in the gym.","furigana":"あめ|こうてい|つか|たいいく|たいいくかん|おこな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Several groups do something together: 合同練習 (joint practice), 二つの学校が合同で運動会を開く (two schools hold a joint sports day). In geometry also \"congruent\".","examples":[{"japanese":"今年は三つの町が合同で祭りを行う。","romaji":"Kotoshi wa mittsu no machi ga gōdō de matsuri o okonau.","german":"This year three towns are holding the festival jointly.","furigana":"ことし|みっ|まち|ごうどう|まつ|おこな"},{"japanese":"二つのクラブの合同が決まった。","romaji":"Futatsu no kurabu no gōdō ga kimatta.","german":"The merger of the two clubs has been decided.","furigana":"ふた|ごうどう|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Duties in government or public offices: 公務員 (public servant), 公務で出張する (to travel on official business).","examples":[{"japanese":"市長は公務で海外へ出張している。","romaji":"Shichō wa kōmu de kaigai e shucchō shite iru.","german":"The mayor is away on an overseas business trip on official duty.","furigana":"しちょう|こうむ|かいがい|しゅっちょう"},{"japanese":"公務を優先して、個人的な予定を変更した。","romaji":"Kōmu o yūsen shite, kojinteki na yotei o henkō shita.","german":"I gave priority to my official duties and changed my personal plans.","furigana":"こうむ|ゆうせん|こじんてき|よてい|へんこう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Polite prohibition formula on signs and in announcements: 〜はご遠慮ください. Friendlier than 〜禁止 or 〜しないでください.","examples":[{"japanese":"館内での撮影はご遠慮ください。","romaji":"Kannai de no satsuei wa goenryo kudasai.","german":"Please refrain from taking photographs inside the building.","furigana":"かんない|さつえい|えんりょ"},{"japanese":"お電話でのお問い合わせはご遠慮ください。","romaji":"Odenwa de no otoiawase wa goenryo kudasai.","german":"Please refrain from making inquiries by telephone.","furigana":"でんわ|と|あ|えんりょ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Founded and operated by the state: 国立大学, 国立公園, 国立博物館. Compare 県立 (prefectural), 市立 (municipal), 私立 (private).","examples":[{"japanese":"兄は国立の大学に通っている。","romaji":"Ani wa kokuritsu no daigaku ni kayotte iru.","german":"My older brother attends a national university.","furigana":"あに|こくりつ|だいがく|かよ"},{"japanese":"この美術館は国立なので、入場料が安い。","romaji":"Kono bijutsukan wa kokuritsu na node, nyūjōryō ga yasui.","german":"This art museum is state-run, so the admission fee is cheap.","furigana":"びじゅつかん|こくりつ|にゅうじょうりょう|やす"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Intransitive ichidan verb for people and body parts that become stiff from the cold: 手が凍える. Water that freezes, by contrast, is 凍る.","examples":[{"japanese":"外で待っている間に、手が凍えてしまった。","romaji":"Soto de matte iru aida ni, te ga kogoete shimatta.","german":"While I waited outside, my hands went numb with cold.","furigana":"そと|ま|あいだ|て|こご"},{"japanese":"凍えそうな夜に、温かいスープがありがたかった。","romaji":"Kogoesō na yoru ni, atatakai sūpu ga arigatakatta.","german":"On the freezing night, I was grateful for the warm soup.","furigana":"こご|よる|あたた"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A simple seat without a backrest, or a bench. Figuratively: a job one takes only temporarily (腰掛けの仕事).","examples":[{"japanese":"公園の腰掛けに座って休んだ。","romaji":"Kōen no koshikake ni suwatte yasunda.","german":"I sat down on a bench in the park and rested.","furigana":"こうえん|こしか|すわ|やす"},{"japanese":"この仕事は腰掛けのつもりだったが、もう十年になる。","romaji":"Kono shigoto wa koshikake no tsumori datta ga, mō jūnen ni naru.","german":"I meant this job to be temporary, but it has already been ten years.","furigana":"しごと|こしか|じゅうねん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Ichidan verb: to sit on a chair or ledge (ベンチに腰掛ける). To sit on the floor is 座る.","examples":[{"japanese":"疲れたので、ベンチに腰掛けた。","romaji":"Tsukareta node, benchi ni koshikaketa.","german":"Because I was tired, I sat down on a bench.","furigana":"つか|こしか"},{"japanese":"彼はベッドの端に腰掛けて話し始めた。","romaji":"Kare wa beddo no hashi ni koshikakete hanashihajimeta.","german":"He sat down on the edge of the bed and began to talk.","furigana":"かれ|はし|こしか|はな|はじ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The arrangement of kana in five columns and about ten rows (五十音図); dictionaries are sorted in 五十音順 (gojūon order).","examples":[{"japanese":"日本語を学ぶ人は、まず五十音を覚える。","romaji":"Nihongo o manabu hito wa, mazu gojūon o oboeru.","german":"People learning Japanese first memorize the gojūon.","furigana":"にほんご|まな|ひと|ごじゅうおん|おぼ"},{"japanese":"名前は五十音の順に並んでいる。","romaji":"Namae wa gojūon no jun ni narande iru.","german":"The names are arranged in gojūon order.","furigana":"なまえ|ごじゅうおん|じゅん|なら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A route (マラソンのコース), a course of study (初級コース) or a menu with several courses (フルコース). A lane in a swimming pool is also called コース.","examples":[{"japanese":"このハイキングコースは初心者にもやさしい。","romaji":"Kono haikingu kōsu wa shoshinsha ni mo yasashii.","german":"This hiking trail is easy even for beginners.","furigana":"しょしんしゃ"},{"japanese":"記念日にレストランでコースを予約した。","romaji":"Kinenbi ni resutoran de kōsu o yoyaku shita.","german":"For our anniversary, I reserved a set menu at a restaurant.","furigana":"きねんび|よやく"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 45"},
{"notes":"A cook in Western-style restaurants (from Dutch \"kok\"); today often シェフ or 料理人. Homophone: コック (tap, valve).","examples":[{"japanese":"兄はホテルのレストランでコックをしている。","romaji":"Ani wa hoteru no resutoran de kokku o shite iru.","german":"My older brother works as a cook in a hotel restaurant.","furigana":"あに"},{"japanese":"コックが白い帽子をかぶっている。","romaji":"Kokku ga shiroi bōshi o kabutte iru.","german":"The cook is wearing a white hat.","furigana":"しろ|ぼうし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Old, authoritative works: 古典文学, 古典音楽 (classical music = クラシック). At school, 古典 is the subject \"classical Japanese\".","examples":[{"japanese":"『源氏物語』は日本の古典として有名だ。","romaji":"\"Genji monogatari\" wa Nihon no koten to shite yūmei da.","german":"The Tale of Genji is famous as a classic of Japanese literature.","furigana":"げんじものがたり|にほん|こてん|ゆうめい"},{"japanese":"高校で古典の授業が一番苦手だった。","romaji":"Kōkō de koten no jugyō ga ichiban nigate datta.","german":"In high school, classical Japanese was my worst subject.","furigana":"こうこう|こてん|じゅぎょう|いちばんにがて"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Ichidan verb: to give someone a message or item to pass on to a third person. Noun: 言付け (message).","examples":[{"japanese":"母に手紙を言付けて、先生に渡してもらった。","romaji":"Haha ni tegami o kotozukete, sensei ni watashite moratta.","german":"I gave the letter to my mother and had her hand it to the teacher.","furigana":"はは|てがみ|ことづ|せんせい|わた"},{"japanese":"留守だったので、隣の人に荷物を言付けた。","romaji":"Rusu datta node, tonari no hito ni nimotsu o kotozuketa.","german":"Since nobody was home, I left the package with the neighbor.","furigana":"るす|となり|ひと|にもつ|ことづ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Colloquial form of この間（このあいだ）; very common in conversation. The kanji 此間 is obsolete.","examples":[{"japanese":"こないだ駅で先生に会ったよ。","romaji":"Konaida eki de sensei ni atta yo.","german":"I ran into my teacher at the station the other day.","furigana":"えき|せんせい|あ"},{"japanese":"こないだはありがとう。とても楽しかった。","romaji":"Konaida wa arigatō. Totemo tanoshikatta.","german":"Thanks for the other day. I had a lot of fun.","furigana":"たの"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"From Dutch \"gom\": ゴムの木 (rubber tree), 輪ゴム (rubber band), ゴム手袋 (rubber gloves). An eraser is 消しゴム.","examples":[{"japanese":"この靴の底はゴムでできている。","romaji":"Kono kutsu no soko wa gomu de dekite iru.","german":"The soles of these shoes are made of rubber.","furigana":"くつ|そこ"},{"japanese":"髪をゴムで結んだ。","romaji":"Kami o gomu de musunda.","german":"I tied my hair back with a rubber band.","furigana":"かみ|むす"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Choir and part singing: コーラス部 (school choir club). Synonym, rather formal: 合唱.","examples":[{"japanese":"姉は大学でコーラスのサークルに入っている。","romaji":"Ane wa daigaku de kōrasu no sākuru ni haitte iru.","german":"My older sister is in a choir club at university.","furigana":"あね|だいがく|はい"},{"japanese":"歌手の後ろで三人がコーラスをつけた。","romaji":"Kashu no ushiro de sannin ga kōrasu o tsuketa.","german":"Three people sang backing vocals behind the singer.","furigana":"かしゅ|うし|さんにん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Collected items (切手のコレクション) and, in fashion, a season's new collection (春のコレクション).","examples":[{"japanese":"父の趣味は古いカメラのコレクションだ。","romaji":"Chichi no shumi wa furui kamera no korekushon da.","german":"My father's hobby is collecting old cameras.","furigana":"ちち|しゅみ|ふる"},{"japanese":"パリで春のコレクションが発表された。","romaji":"Pari de haru no korekushon ga happyō sareta.","german":"The spring collection was unveiled in Paris.","furigana":"はる|はっぴょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A dark blue, typical for school uniforms and suits: 紺のスーツ. Also 紺色.","examples":[{"japanese":"面接には紺のスーツを着ていった。","romaji":"Mensetsu ni wa kon no sūtsu o kite itta.","german":"I wore a navy blue suit to the interview.","furigana":"めんせつ|こん|き"},{"japanese":"制服は紺で、胸に白いラインが入っている。","romaji":"Seifuku wa kon de, mune ni shiroi rain ga haitte iru.","german":"The school uniform is navy blue with a white stripe on the chest.","furigana":"せいふく|こん|むね|しろ|はい"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 34"},
{"notes":"From French \"concours\"; mainly artistic competitions: ピアノコンクール, 作文コンクール.","examples":[{"japanese":"娘がピアノのコンクールで一位になった。","romaji":"Musume ga piano no konkūru de ichii ni natta.","german":"My daughter won first place in a piano competition.","furigana":"むすめ|いちい"},{"japanese":"来月のコンクールに向けて、毎日練習している。","romaji":"Raigetsu no konkūru ni mukete, mainichi renshū shite iru.","german":"I practice every day for next month's competition.","furigana":"らいげつ|む|まいにちれんしゅう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"As a verb 混合する; attributively: 男女混合 (mixed, men and women), 混合物 (mixture).","examples":[{"japanese":"二つの液体を混合すると、色が変わる。","romaji":"Futatsu no ekitai o kongō suru to, iro ga kawaru.","german":"When the two liquids are mixed, the color changes.","furigana":"ふた|えきたい|こんごう|いろ|か"},{"japanese":"この大会は男女混合のチームで戦う。","romaji":"Kono taikai wa danjo kongō no chīmu de tatakau.","german":"In this tournament, mixed teams of men and women compete.","furigana":"たいかい|だんじょこんごう|たたか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"To be enrolled at a school or university: 在学中 (while enrolled), 在学証明書 (certificate of enrollment).","examples":[{"japanese":"在学中に一年間留学した。","romaji":"Zaigakuchū ni ichinenkan ryūgaku shita.","german":"I studied abroad for a year while I was a student.","furigana":"ざいがくちゅう|いちねんかんりゅうがく"},{"japanese":"現在、この大学には一万人の学生が在学している。","romaji":"Genzai, kono daigaku ni wa ichimannin no gakusei ga zaigaku shite iru.","german":"Currently, ten thousand students are enrolled at this university.","furigana":"げんざい|だいがく|いちまんにん|がくせい|ざいがく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Formal, usually with a slight tone of reproach: 再三注意したのに (although I warned him repeatedly). Stronger: 再三再四.","examples":[{"japanese":"再三注意したのに、彼はまた遅刻した。","romaji":"Saisan chūi shita noni, kare wa mata chikoku shita.","german":"Although I warned him repeatedly, he was late again.","furigana":"さいさんちゅうい|かれ|ちこく"},{"japanese":"会社は再三の要望にやっと応えた。","romaji":"Kaisha wa saisan no yōbō ni yatto kotaeta.","german":"The company finally responded to the repeated requests.","furigana":"かいしゃ|さいさん|ようぼう|こた"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Warning sound of a fire engine, ambulance or factory: サイレンが鳴る.","examples":[{"japanese":"夜中に救急車のサイレンが聞こえた。","romaji":"Yonaka ni kyūkyūsha no sairen ga kikoeta.","german":"In the middle of the night I heard an ambulance siren.","furigana":"よなか|きゅうきゅうしゃ|き"},{"japanese":"試合開始のサイレンが鳴った。","romaji":"Shiai kaishi no sairen ga natta.","german":"The siren signaling the start of the match sounded.","furigana":"しあいかいし|な"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Somewhat old-fashioned word for places that serve alcohol; today mostly 居酒屋 or バー.","examples":[{"japanese":"駅の裏には古い酒場が並んでいる。","romaji":"Eki no ura ni wa furui sakaba ga narande iru.","german":"Old taverns line the back of the station.","furigana":"えき|うら|ふる|さかば|なら"},{"japanese":"仕事の後、同僚と小さな酒場に寄った。","romaji":"Shigoto no ato, dōryō to chiisa na sakaba ni yotta.","german":"After work, I stopped by a small bar with my colleagues.","furigana":"しごと|あと|どうりょう|ちい|さかば|よ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The day before the day before yesterday (おととい). Kanji: 一昨昨日, usually written in kana. Future counterpart: しあさって.","examples":[{"japanese":"さきおとといから熱が下がらない。","romaji":"Sakiototoi kara netsu ga sagaranai.","german":"My fever hasn't gone down since three days ago.","furigana":"ねつ|さ"},{"japanese":"その荷物はさきおとといに届いた。","romaji":"Sono nimotsu wa sakiototoi ni todoita.","german":"That package arrived three days ago.","furigana":"にもつ|とど"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Making objects (模型の作製). For documents and plans, 作成（さくせい）is usually written.","examples":[{"japanese":"この工房では家具の作製をしている。","romaji":"Kono kōbō de wa kagu no sakusei o shite iru.","german":"This workshop makes furniture.","furigana":"こうぼう|かぐ|さくせい"},{"japanese":"展示用の模型を三か月かけて作製した。","romaji":"Tenjiyō no mokei o sankagetsu kakete sakusei shita.","german":"The exhibition model was made over three months.","furigana":"てんじよう|もけい|さん|げつ|さくせい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Voluntary interest group, especially at universities (テニスサークル). Official school clubs are rather called 部 or 部活.","examples":[{"japanese":"大学では写真のサークルに入った。","romaji":"Daigaku de wa shashin no sākuru ni haitta.","german":"At university I joined a photography club.","furigana":"だいがく|しゃしん|はい"},{"japanese":"会社のサークルで毎週サッカーをしている。","romaji":"Kaisha no sākuru de maishū sakkā o shite iru.","german":"I play soccer every week in a company club.","furigana":"かいしゃ|まいしゅう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Transitive godan verb: to feel around in a pocket for something (ポケットを探る) or to carefully find something out (原因を探る, 相手の気持ちを探る).","examples":[{"japanese":"暗い中で、ポケットを探って鍵を出した。","romaji":"Kurai naka de, poketto o sagutte kagi o dashita.","german":"In the dark, I fumbled in my pocket and took out the key.","furigana":"くら|なか|さぐ|かぎ|だ"},{"japanese":"専門家が事故の原因を探っている。","romaji":"Senmonka ga jiko no gen'in o sagutte iru.","german":"Experts are trying to find out the cause of the accident.","furigana":"せんもんか|じこ|げんいん|さぐ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Often written in kana as さじ; especially in recipes: 大さじ (tablespoon), 小さじ (teaspoon). Idiom: 匙を投げる (to give up). In everyday speech, スプーン is usually used.","examples":[{"japanese":"薬を匙ですくって飲ませた。","romaji":"Kusuri o saji de sukutte nomaseta.","german":"I scooped up the medicine with a spoon and gave it to him.","furigana":"くすり|さじ|の"},{"japanese":"赤ちゃんに小さな匙で離乳食を食べさせた。","romaji":"Akachan ni chiisa na saji de rinyūshoku o tabesaseta.","german":"I fed the baby weaning food with a small spoon.","furigana":"あか|ちい|さじ|りにゅうしょく|た"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The result after subtracting: 差し引きゼロ (net zero). Verb: 差し引く (to deduct).","examples":[{"japanese":"収入と支出の差し引きで、一万円残った。","romaji":"Shūnyū to shishutsu no sashihiki de, ichiman'en nokotta.","german":"After subtracting expenses from income, ten thousand yen was left.","furigana":"しゅうにゅう|ししゅつ|さ|ひ|いちまんえんのこ"},{"japanese":"得をした分と損をした分で、差し引きゼロだ。","romaji":"Toku o shita bun to son o shita bun de, sashihiki zero da.","german":"Gains and losses cancel out; on balance it is zero.","furigana":"とく|ぶん|そん|ぶん|さ|ひ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Transitive godan verb: to stick something into an opening, especially flowers (花瓶に花を挿す) or a plug. Many meanings of the same verb are also written 差す.","examples":[{"japanese":"花瓶にバラを一本挿した。","romaji":"Kabin ni bara o ippon sashita.","german":"I put one rose in the vase.","furigana":"かびん|いっぽんさ"},{"japanese":"コンセントにプラグを挿してください。","romaji":"Konsento ni puragu o sashite kudasai.","german":"Please plug the plug into the outlet.","furigana":"さ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A palpably hostile, dangerous atmosphere: 殺気を感じる, 殺気立つ (to become tense and aggressive). Homophone: さっき (a while ago).","examples":[{"japanese":"部屋に入った瞬間、殺気を感じた。","romaji":"Heya ni haitta shunkan, sakki o kanjita.","german":"The moment I entered the room, I sensed a menacing atmosphere.","furigana":"へや|はい|しゅんかん|さっき|かん"},{"japanese":"犯人の目には殺気がこもっていた。","romaji":"Hannin no me ni wa sakki ga komotte ita.","german":"The culprit's eyes were full of murderous intent.","furigana":"はんにん|め|さっき"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Verb: 錆びる (to rust); 錆止め (rust protection). Often also written in kana as さび.","examples":[{"japanese":"古い自転車に錆がついている。","romaji":"Furui jitensha ni sabi ga tsuite iru.","german":"The old bicycle has rust on it.","furigana":"ふる|じてんしゃ|さび"},{"japanese":"錆を落としてから、ペンキを塗った。","romaji":"Sabi o otoshite kara, penki o nutta.","german":"I removed the rust and then painted it.","furigana":"さび|お|ぬ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Transitive godan verb related to 冷める (to get cold): スープを冷ます. Not to be confused with 覚ます (to wake up).","examples":[{"japanese":"熱いお茶を少し冷ましてから飲んだ。","romaji":"Atsui ocha o sukoshi samashite kara nonda.","german":"I let the hot tea cool a little before drinking it.","furigana":"あつ|ちゃ|すこ|さ|の"},{"japanese":"悪いニュースがみんなの興奮を冷ました。","romaji":"Warui nyūsu ga minna no kōfun o samashita.","german":"The bad news dampened everyone's excitement.","furigana":"わる|こうふん|さ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A word coined in Japan (from salary + man) for employees with a fixed salary, especially in company offices. More neutral: 会社員.","examples":[{"japanese":"父は普通のサラリーマンだ。","romaji":"Chichi wa futsū no sararīman da.","german":"My father is an ordinary office worker.","furigana":"ちち|ふつう"},{"japanese":"朝の電車はサラリーマンでいっぱいだ。","romaji":"Asa no densha wa sararīman de ippai da.","german":"The morning trains are full of office workers.","furigana":"あさ|でんしゃ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 20 (source: N5; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"Opposite: アルカリ性 (alkaline), in between 中性 (neutral). 酸性雨 = acid rain. Homophone: 賛成 (agreement).","examples":[{"japanese":"レモンの汁は酸性だ。","romaji":"Remon no shiru wa sansei da.","german":"Lemon juice is acidic.","furigana":"しる|さんせい"},{"japanese":"この土は酸性が強いので、野菜が育ちにくい。","romaji":"Kono tsuchi wa sansei ga tsuyoi node, yasai ga sodachinikui.","german":"This soil is strongly acidic, so vegetables do not grow well.","furigana":"つち|さんせい|つよ|やさい|そだ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Forested mountain land, also as property: 山林を所有する (to own mountain forest). About two thirds of Japan is forested.","examples":[{"japanese":"祖父は広い山林を持っている。","romaji":"Sofu wa hiroi sanrin o motte iru.","german":"My grandfather owns a large piece of mountain forest.","furigana":"そふ|ひろ|さんりん|も"},{"japanese":"大雨で山林の一部が崩れた。","romaji":"Ōame de sanrin no ichibu ga kuzureta.","german":"Part of the mountain forest collapsed because of the heavy rain.","furigana":"おおあめ|さんりん|いちぶ|くず"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Intransitive godan verb related to 仕上げる (to finish): 作品が仕上がる. Noun: 仕上がり (result, finish).","examples":[{"japanese":"注文したスーツは来週仕上がる予定だ。","romaji":"Chūmon shita sūtsu wa raishū shiagaru yotei da.","german":"The suit I ordered is scheduled to be ready next week.","furigana":"ちゅうもん|らいしゅうしあ|よてい"},{"japanese":"レポートがやっと仕上がった。","romaji":"Repōto ga yatto shiagatta.","german":"The report is finally finished.","furigana":"しあ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The day after the day after tomorrow (あさって). Kanji 明明後日, usually in kana. In some regions しあさって refers to a different day; if in doubt, give the date.","examples":[{"japanese":"試験はしあさっての月曜日です。","romaji":"Shiken wa shiasatte no getsuyōbi desu.","german":"The exam is in three days, on Monday.","furigana":"しけん|げつようび"},{"japanese":"しあさってまでに返事をください。","romaji":"Shiasatte made ni henji o kudasai.","german":"Please reply within three days.","furigana":"へんじ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Formal word for temple (in everyday speech お寺); also used for places of worship of other religions: イスラム寺院 (mosque).","examples":[{"japanese":"京都には古い寺院がたくさんある。","romaji":"Kyōto ni wa furui jiin ga takusan aru.","german":"There are many old temples in Kyoto.","furigana":"きょうと|ふる|じいん"},{"japanese":"旅行中に有名なイスラム寺院を訪れた。","romaji":"Ryokōchū ni yūmei na isuramu jiin o otozureta.","german":"During my trip I visited a famous mosque.","furigana":"りょこうちゅう|ゆうめい|じいん|おとず"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"い-adjective, synonymous with しょっぱい (more colloquial). The noun 塩辛 refers to salted seafood.","examples":[{"japanese":"このスープは少し塩辛い。","romaji":"Kono sūpu wa sukoshi shiokarai.","german":"This soup is a little salty.","furigana":"すこ|しおから"},{"japanese":"塩辛いものを食べすぎると、体に悪い。","romaji":"Shiokarai mono o tabesugiru to, karada ni warui.","german":"Eating too many salty things is bad for your health.","furigana":"しおから|た|からだ|わる"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Chairing a meeting or event: 会議の司会をする; the person is called 司会者. Homophone: 視界 (field of vision).","examples":[{"japanese":"今日の会議の司会は田中さんです。","romaji":"Kyō no kaigi no shikai wa Tanaka-san desu.","german":"Ms. Tanaka is chairing today's meeting.","furigana":"きょう|かいぎ|しかい|たなか"},{"japanese":"結婚式で友人の司会をすることになった。","romaji":"Kekkonshiki de yūjin no shikai o suru koto ni natta.","german":"I am going to be the emcee at a friend's wedding.","furigana":"けっこんしき|ゆうじん|しかい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Suffix after a number for the class period in a school timetable: 一時間目 (first period), 三時間目. The question word is 何時間目.","examples":[{"japanese":"一時間目は数学です。","romaji":"Ichijikanme wa sūgaku desu.","german":"First period is math.","furigana":"いちじかんめ|すうがく"},{"japanese":"三時間目の授業が休講になった。","romaji":"Sanjikanme no jugyō ga kyūkō ni natta.","german":"The third period class was canceled.","furigana":"さんじかんめ|じゅぎょう|きゅうこう"}],"levelNote":"Compound: 時間 (N5) + 目 (N5)"},
{"notes":"Weekly schedule of lessons; also the time schedule of an event. Train timetables, by contrast, are called 時刻表.","examples":[{"japanese":"新しい学期の時間割が発表された。","romaji":"Atarashii gakki no jikanwari ga happyō sareta.","german":"The timetable for the new term was announced.","furigana":"あたら|がっき|じかんわり|はっぴょう"},{"japanese":"時間割を見て、明日の教科書をかばんに入れた。","romaji":"Jikanwari o mite, ashita no kyōkasho o kaban ni ireta.","german":"I looked at the timetable and packed tomorrow's textbooks in my bag.","furigana":"じかんわり|み|あした|きょうかしょ|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Formal, typical in business messages: 至急ご連絡ください (please contact us immediately), 大至急 (extremely urgent).","examples":[{"japanese":"至急、事務所に電話してください。","romaji":"Shikyū, jimusho ni denwa shite kudasai.","german":"Please call the office immediately.","furigana":"しきゅう|じむしょ|でんわ"},{"japanese":"これは至急の用件です。","romaji":"Kore wa shikyū no yōken desu.","german":"This is an urgent matter.","furigana":"しきゅう|ようけん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Literally \"discard four, take in five\": up to 4 is rounded down, from 5 up is rounded up. 小数点以下を四捨五入する.","examples":[{"japanese":"小数点以下を四捨五入してください。","romaji":"Shōsūten ika o shishagonyū shite kudasai.","german":"Please round off everything after the decimal point.","furigana":"しょうすうてんいか|ししゃごにゅう"},{"japanese":"三・六を四捨五入すると四になる。","romaji":"San ten roku o shishagonyū suru to yon ni naru.","german":"If you round 3.6, you get 4."}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Constantly, over and over: 始終文句を言う. As a noun in 一部始終 (the whole story).","examples":[{"japanese":"弟は始終ゲームばかりしている。","romaji":"Otōto wa shijū gēmu bakari shite iru.","german":"My younger brother plays nothing but video games all the time.","furigana":"おとうと|しじゅう"},{"japanese":"彼女は会議の間、始終笑顔だった。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kaigi no aida, shijū egao datta.","german":"She was smiling throughout the meeting.","furigana":"かのじょ|かいぎ|あいだ|しじゅうえがお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Studying alone; at school also a period without a teacher (今日は自習だ). 自習室 = study room.","examples":[{"japanese":"放課後、図書館で自習している。","romaji":"Hōkago, toshokan de jishū shite iru.","german":"After school, I study by myself in the library.","furigana":"ほうかご|としょかん|じしゅう"},{"japanese":"先生が休みで、三時間目は自習になった。","romaji":"Sensei ga yasumi de, sanjikanme wa jishū ni natta.","german":"The teacher was absent, so third period became self-study.","furigana":"せんせい|やす|さんじかんめ|じしゅう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Intransitive godan verb: 会場が静まる (the hall falls silent), 風が静まる (the wind dies down). Transitive: 静める. For pain or unrest, 鎮まる is also used.","examples":[{"japanese":"先生が入ってくると、教室は静まった。","romaji":"Sensei ga haitte kuru to, kyōshitsu wa shizumatta.","german":"When the teacher came in, the classroom fell silent.","furigana":"せんせい|はい|きょうしつ|しず"},{"japanese":"夜になって、ようやく風が静まった。","romaji":"Yoru ni natte, yōyaku kaze ga shizumatta.","german":"In the evening, the wind finally died down.","furigana":"よる|かぜ|しず"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The peak time for something: 野球のシーズン, スキーシーズン, 受験シーズン; オフシーズン (off season).","examples":[{"japanese":"スキーのシーズンになると、ホテルは予約でいっぱいだ。","romaji":"Sukī no shīzun ni naru to, hoteru wa yoyaku de ippai da.","german":"When the ski season comes, the hotels are fully booked.","furigana":"よやく"},{"japanese":"プロ野球のシーズンが始まった。","romaji":"Puro yakyū no shīzun ga hajimatta.","german":"The professional baseball season has started.","furigana":"やきゅう|はじ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Physics, chemistry, biology, etc.; compare 社会科学 (social sciences) and 人文科学 (humanities).","examples":[{"japanese":"大学で自然科学を専攻している。","romaji":"Daigaku de shizen kagaku o senkō shite iru.","german":"I am majoring in natural sciences at university.","furigana":"だいがく|しぜんかがく|せんこう"},{"japanese":"自然科学の発展が私たちの生活を変えた。","romaji":"Shizen kagaku no hatten ga watashitachi no seikatsu o kaeta.","german":"The development of natural science has changed our lives.","furigana":"しぜんかがく|はってん|わたし|せいかつ|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Speed per hour: 時速六十キロ (60 km/h). Compare 秒速 (per second), 分速 (per minute).","examples":[{"japanese":"新幹線は時速三百キロで走る。","romaji":"Shinkansen wa jisoku sanbyaku kiro de hashiru.","german":"The Shinkansen runs at 300 kilometers per hour.","furigana":"しんかんせん|じそくさんびゃく|はし"},{"japanese":"この道路では時速四十キロ以下で走ってください。","romaji":"Kono dōro de wa jisoku yonjukkiro ika de hashitte kudasai.","german":"On this road, please drive at no more than 40 km/h.","furigana":"どうろ|じそくよんじゅっ|いか|はし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A spelling of とる used specifically for catching animals, fish or insects (魚を捕る) and in baseball (ボールを捕る). The general \"to take\" is written 取る.","examples":[{"japanese":"子どもの頃、よく川で魚を捕った。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, yoku kawa de sakana o totta.","german":"When I was a child, I often caught fish in the river.","furigana":"こ|ころ|かわ|さかな|と"},{"japanese":"外野手が高く上がったボールを捕った。","romaji":"Gaiyashu ga takaku agatta bōru o totta.","german":"The outfielder caught the high fly ball.","furigana":"がいやしゅ|たか|あ|と"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Internal telephone line in companies and offices: 内線番号 (extension number), 内線で呼ぶ. Opposite: 外線 (outside line).","examples":[{"japanese":"経理部には内線の百二十番でつながります。","romaji":"Keiribu ni wa naisen no hyakunijūban de tsunagarimasu.","german":"You can reach the accounting department at extension 120.","furigana":"けいりぶ|ないせん|ひゃくにじゅうばん"},{"japanese":"内線で課長を呼んでもらえますか。","romaji":"Naisen de kachō o yonde moraemasu ka.","german":"Could you call the section chief on the internal line?","furigana":"ないせん|かちょう|よ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Synthetic fiber, e.g. for bags, stockings and ropes: ナイロンのバッグ.","examples":[{"japanese":"このかばんはナイロンでできていて軽い。","romaji":"Kono kaban wa nairon de dekite ite karui.","german":"This bag is made of nylon and is light.","furigana":"かる"},{"japanese":"ナイロンのロープは丈夫で切れにくい。","romaji":"Nairon no rōpu wa jōbu de kirenikui.","german":"Nylon rope is strong and hard to break.","furigana":"じょうぶ|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"To lose a person through death: 父を亡くす. Losing objects is written 無くす.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は去年、母親を亡くした。","romaji":"Kare wa kyonen, hahaoya o nakushita.","german":"He lost his mother last year.","furigana":"かれ|きょねん|ははおや|な"},{"japanese":"事故で夫を亡くした女性が、体験を語った。","romaji":"Jiko de otto o nakushita josei ga, taiken o katatta.","german":"A woman who had lost her husband in an accident spoke about her experience.","furigana":"じこ|おっと|な|じょせい|たいけん|かた"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Elevated word for \"to do\": 為すべきこと (what must be done), 為す術がない (to be unable to do anything). In everyday speech する is used.","examples":[{"japanese":"自分が為すべきことを考えなさい。","romaji":"Jibun ga nasu beki koto o kangaenasai.","german":"Think about what you ought to do.","furigana":"じぶん|な|かんが"},{"japanese":"大雨の前に、人々は為す術もなかった。","romaji":"Ōame no mae ni, hitobito wa nasu sube mo nakatta.","german":"Faced with the torrential rain, people were helpless.","furigana":"おおあめ|まえ|ひとびと|な|"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"な-adjective: gently inclined (なだらかな坂, なだらかな丘) or running without breaks (なだらかな曲線). Mostly in hiragana.","examples":[{"japanese":"駅から家までは、なだらかな坂が続く。","romaji":"Eki kara ie made wa, nadaraka na saka ga tsuzuku.","german":"From the station to my house, a gentle slope continues.","furigana":"えき|いえ|さか|つづ"},{"japanese":"グラフはなだらかに上がっている。","romaji":"Gurafu wa nadaraka ni agatte iru.","german":"The graph is rising gradually.","furigana":"あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Placeholder for unspecified things (何々という店 = a shop called so-and-so) and an eager question: なになに？(What? Tell me!).","examples":[{"japanese":"申込書には「何々大学卒業」のように書いてください。","romaji":"Mōshikomisho ni wa \"naninani daigaku sotsugyō\" no yō ni kaite kudasai.","german":"On the application form, please write something like \"graduated from so-and-so University\".","furigana":"もうしこみしょ|なになにだいがくそつぎょう|か"},{"japanese":"なになに、面白い話？私にも教えて。","romaji":"Naninani, omoshiroi hanashi? Watashi ni mo oshiete.","german":"What, an interesting story? Tell me too.","furigana":"おもしろ|はなし|わたし|おし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Trees along a road: 桜並木 (row of cherry trees), 並木道 (tree-lined avenue).","examples":[{"japanese":"駅前の通りには並木が続いている。","romaji":"Ekimae no tōri ni wa namiki ga tsuzuite iru.","german":"A row of trees continues along the street in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|とお|なみき|つづ"},{"japanese":"秋になると、並木が黄色く色づく。","romaji":"Aki ni naru to, namiki ga kiiroku irozuku.","german":"In autumn, the trees lining the street turn yellow.","furigana":"あき|なみき|きいろ|いろ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"To follow a model: 前例に倣う (to follow a precedent), 右に倣え (right dress!). Not to be confused with 習う (to learn).","examples":[{"japanese":"私たちも先輩のやり方に倣うことにした。","romaji":"Watashitachi mo senpai no yarikata ni narau koto ni shita.","german":"We decided to follow our seniors' way of doing things.","furigana":"わたし|せんぱい|かた|なら"},{"japanese":"他の国に倣って、新しい制度が作られた。","romaji":"Hoka no kuni ni naratte, atarashii seido ga tsukurareta.","german":"A new system was created following the example of other countries.","furigana":"ほか|くに|なら|あたら|せいど|つく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Transitive counterpart of 鳴る: ベルを鳴らす (to ring the bell), クラクションを鳴らす (to honk the horn). Figuratively: 警鐘を鳴らす (to sound a warning).","examples":[{"japanese":"玄関のベルを鳴らしたが、誰も出てこなかった。","romaji":"Genkan no beru o narashita ga, dare mo dete konakatta.","german":"I rang the doorbell, but nobody came out.","furigana":"げんかん|な|だれ|で"},{"japanese":"後ろの車がクラクションを鳴らした。","romaji":"Ushiro no kuruma ga kurakushon o narashita.","german":"The car behind us honked its horn.","furigana":"うし|くるま|な"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Fruit growing on a plant: 実が生る, 柿が生る. Mostly written in hiragana; not to be confused with なる (to become).","examples":[{"japanese":"庭の木にたくさん柿が生った。","romaji":"Niwa no ki ni takusan kaki ga natta.","german":"The tree in the garden bore lots of persimmons.","furigana":"にわ|き|かき|な"},{"japanese":"このトマトは夏になると実が生る。","romaji":"Kono tomato wa natsu ni naru to mi ga naru.","german":"These tomatoes bear fruit in summer.","furigana":"なつ|み|な"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"With a negative, \"not at all\": なんとも思わない (to not care); without a negative, \"really, extremely\": なんとも美しい. Set phrase: なんとも言えない (indescribable; hard to say).","examples":[{"japanese":"あの人に何を言われても、なんとも思わない。","romaji":"Ano hito ni nani o iwarete mo, nantomo omowanai.","german":"No matter what that person says to me, it doesn't bother me at all.","furigana":"ひと|なに|い|おも"},{"japanese":"この景色はなんとも美しい。","romaji":"Kono keshiki wa nantomo utsukushii.","german":"This scenery is truly beautiful.","furigana":"けしき|うつく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Number in general (ナンバーを振る = to number) and short for ナンバープレート (car license plate): 車のナンバー.","examples":[{"japanese":"事故を起こした車のナンバーを覚えていますか。","romaji":"Jiko o okoshita kuruma no nanbā o oboete imasu ka.","german":"Do you remember the license plate of the car that caused the accident?","furigana":"じこ|お|くるま|おぼ"},{"japanese":"会場では、参加者一人一人にナンバーが振られた。","romaji":"Kaijō de wa, sankasha hitori hitori ni nanbā ga furareta.","german":"At the venue, each participant was assigned a number.","furigana":"かいじょう|さんかしゃひとりひとり|ふ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The north-south axis: 南北に長い国 (a country with great north-south extent). In Japanese the order is \"south-north\". Compare 東西 (east and west).","examples":[{"japanese":"日本は南北に長い国です。","romaji":"Nihon wa nanboku ni nagai kuni desu.","german":"Japan is a country that is long from north to south.","furigana":"にほん|なんぼく|なが|くに"},{"japanese":"この道は町を南北に走っている。","romaji":"Kono michi wa machi o nanboku ni hashitte iru.","german":"This road runs north-south through the town.","furigana":"みち|まち|なんぼく|はし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"式 = way, style: 日本式のトイレ (Japanese-style squat toilet), 日本式の経営. Similar: 和式.","examples":[{"japanese":"この旅館の部屋は日本式で、畳の上で寝ます。","romaji":"Kono ryokan no heya wa nihonshiki de, tatami no ue de nemasu.","german":"The rooms in this inn are Japanese-style; you sleep on tatami.","furigana":"りょかん|へや|にほんしき|たたみ|うえ|ね"},{"japanese":"海外でも日本式の経営を取り入れる会社がある。","romaji":"Kaigai demo nihonshiki no keiei o toriireru kaisha ga aru.","german":"Even overseas, there are companies that adopt Japanese-style management.","furigana":"かいがい|にほんしき|けいえい|と|い|かいしゃ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A casual, somewhat old-fashioned word a man uses for his own wife: うちの女房. More neutral: 妻; for someone else's wife: 奥さん.","examples":[{"japanese":"うちの女房は料理がうまい。","romaji":"Uchi no nyōbō wa ryōri ga umai.","german":"My wife is good at cooking.","furigana":"にょうぼう|りょうり"},{"japanese":"帰りが遅いと女房に怒られるんだ。","romaji":"Kaeri ga osoi to nyōbō ni okorareru n da.","german":"If I come home late, my wife gets angry with me.","furigana":"かえ|おそ|にょうぼう|おこ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"な-adjective: にわか雨 (sudden rain shower), にわかには信じられない (hard to believe right away). Also \"newcomer who only briefly joins in\" (にわかファン).","examples":[{"japanese":"その話はにわかには信じられない。","romaji":"Sono hanashi wa niwaka ni wa shinjirarenai.","german":"I can't readily believe that story.","furigana":"はなし|しん"},{"japanese":"にわかに空が暗くなった。","romaji":"Niwaka ni sora ga kuraku natta.","german":"Suddenly the sky grew dark.","furigana":"そら|くら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Screw: ねじを締める (to tighten), ねじを緩める (to loosen); also the spring of a clock mechanism (ねじを巻く = to wind up). Often also written ネジ.","examples":[{"japanese":"ドライバーでねじを締めた。","romaji":"Doraibā de neji o shimeta.","german":"I tightened the screw with a screwdriver.","furigana":"し"},{"japanese":"古い時計のねじを巻いた。","romaji":"Furui tokei no neji o maita.","german":"I wound up the old clock.","furigana":"ふる|とけい|ま"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Neck jewelry; ネックレスをする／つける (to wear, to put on).","examples":[{"japanese":"誕生日に母からネックレスをもらった。","romaji":"Tanjōbi ni haha kara nekkuresu o moratta.","german":"I received a necklace from my mother for my birthday.","furigana":"たんじょうび|はは"},{"japanese":"今日は白いネックレスをつけている。","romaji":"Kyō wa shiroi nekkuresu o tsukete iru.","german":"Today I am wearing a white necklace.","furigana":"きょう|しろ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Written-style word for \"to heat\": フライパンを熱する, 熱した油. Figuratively: 熱しやすく冷めやすい (quick to get enthusiastic, quick to cool off).","examples":[{"japanese":"フライパンをよく熱してから油を入れる。","romaji":"Furaipan o yoku nesshite kara abura o ireru.","german":"Heat the frying pan well before adding the oil.","furigana":"ねっ|あぶら|い"},{"japanese":"彼は熱しやすく冷めやすい性格だ。","romaji":"Kare wa nesshiyasuku sameyasui seikaku da.","german":"He is quick to get enthusiastic and quick to lose interest.","furigana":"かれ|ねっ|さ|せいかく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Clothing for sleeping; also written 寝巻き. Western-style pajamas are usually called パジャマ.","examples":[{"japanese":"寝間着に着替えて布団に入った。","romaji":"Nemaki ni kigaete futon ni haitta.","german":"I changed into my nightclothes and got into the futon.","furigana":"ねまき|きが|ふとん|はい"},{"japanese":"旅館には浴衣が寝間着として用意されている。","romaji":"Ryokan ni wa yukata ga nemaki to shite yōi sarete iru.","german":"At the ryokan, a yukata is provided as nightwear.","furigana":"りょかん|ゆかた|ねまき|ようい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"In Japan, the fiscal and school year usually runs from April 1 to March 31: 今年度 (this fiscal year), 来年度 (next fiscal year), 年度末 (end of the fiscal year).","examples":[{"japanese":"年度末は仕事がとても忙しい。","romaji":"Nendomatsu wa shigoto ga totemo isogashii.","german":"Work is very busy at the end of the fiscal year.","furigana":"ねんどまつ|しごと|いそが"},{"japanese":"日本の学校では、四月に新しい年度が始まる。","romaji":"Nihon no gakkō de wa, shigatsu ni atarashii nendo ga hajimaru.","german":"In Japanese schools, the new school year begins in April.","furigana":"にほん|がっこう|しがつ|あたら|ねんど|はじ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Products of farming (rice, vegetables, fruit). Compare 水産物 (marine products).","examples":[{"japanese":"この地域は農産物の生産がさかんだ。","romaji":"Kono chiiki wa nōsanbutsu no seisan ga sakan da.","german":"Agricultural production is thriving in this region.","furigana":"ちいき|のうさんぶつ|せいさん"},{"japanese":"道の駅では地元の農産物を買うことができる。","romaji":"Michi no eki de wa jimoto no nōsanbutsu o kau koto ga dekiru.","german":"At the roadside station you can buy local farm products.","furigana":"みち|えき|じもと|のうさんぶつ|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A community shaped by agriculture: 農村地帯 (rural area). Counterpart: 都市 (city).","examples":[{"japanese":"農村では若い人が減っている。","romaji":"Nōson de wa wakai hito ga hette iru.","german":"The number of young people in farming villages is decreasing.","furigana":"のうそん|わか|ひと|へ"},{"japanese":"祖父母は小さな農村で暮らしている。","romaji":"Sofubo wa chiisa na nōson de kurashite iru.","german":"My grandparents live in a small farming village.","furigana":"そふぼ|ちい|のうそん|く"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Chemical agents in agriculture: 農薬を使う (to use pesticides), 無農薬の野菜 (pesticide-free vegetables).","examples":[{"japanese":"この野菜は農薬を使わずに育てた。","romaji":"Kono yasai wa nōyaku o tsukawazu ni sodateta.","german":"These vegetables were grown without pesticides.","furigana":"やさい|のうやく|つか|そだ"},{"japanese":"農薬の使いすぎは環境に悪い。","romaji":"Nōyaku no tsukaisugi wa kankyō ni warui.","german":"Using too many pesticides is bad for the environment.","furigana":"のうやく|つか|かんきょう|わる"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"How much work gets done in a given time: 能率が上がる／下がる (efficiency rises/falls), 能率的 (efficient). Similar: 効率.","examples":[{"japanese":"疲れていると仕事の能率が下がる。","romaji":"Tsukarete iru to shigoto no nōritsu ga sagaru.","german":"When you are tired, your work efficiency drops.","furigana":"つか|しごと|のうりつ|さ"},{"japanese":"新しい機械で作業の能率が上がった。","romaji":"Atarashii kikai de sagyō no nōritsu ga agatta.","german":"The new machine has increased work efficiency.","furigana":"あたら|きかい|さぎょう|のうりつ|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A tool for sawing; Japanese saws cut on the pull stroke. The kanji 鋸 is rare.","examples":[{"japanese":"のこぎりで板を半分に切った。","romaji":"Nokogiri de ita o hanbun ni kitta.","german":"I cut the board in half with a saw.","furigana":"いた|はんぶん|き"},{"japanese":"のこぎりを使うときは手に気をつけて。","romaji":"Nokogiri o tsukau toki wa te ni ki o tsukete.","german":"Be careful of your hands when using a saw.","furigana":"つか|て|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Nothing is left over: 一人残らず (every single person), 残らず食べる (to eat everything).","examples":[{"japanese":"子どもたちは料理を残らず食べた。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa ryōri o nokorazu tabeta.","german":"The children ate up all of the food.","furigana":"こ|りょうり|のこ|た"},{"japanese":"クラスの全員が一人残らず参加した。","romaji":"Kurasu no zen'in ga hitori nokorazu sanka shita.","german":"Every single member of the class took part.","furigana":"ぜんいん|ひとりのこ|さんか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Opposite of 下り: 上り坂 (uphill slope), 上りの電車 (train toward Tokyo or the line's starting station), 上り線 (up line).","examples":[{"japanese":"上りの電車は十分おきに来る。","romaji":"Nobori no densha wa juppun oki ni kuru.","german":"Up trains toward Tokyo come every ten minutes.","furigana":"のぼ|でんしゃ|じゅっぷん|く"},{"japanese":"この先は急な上りが続きます。","romaji":"Kono saki wa kyū na nobori ga tsuzukimasu.","german":"From here on, there is a steep climb.","furigana":"さき|きゅう|のぼ|つづ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Adhesive for paper: 糊で貼る (to glue on); also starch for laundry (シャツに糊をつける). Homophone: 海苔 (seaweed).","examples":[{"japanese":"写真を糊でノートに貼った。","romaji":"Shashin o nori de nōto ni hatta.","german":"I glued the photo into my notebook.","furigana":"しゃしん|のり|は"},{"japanese":"クリーニング店でシャツに糊をつけてもらった。","romaji":"Kurīningu ten de shatsu ni nori o tsukete moratta.","german":"I had my shirt starched at the dry cleaner's.","furigana":"てん|のり"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"If you travel farther than your ticket covers, you pay the difference at the 精算機 (fare adjustment machine): 乗り越し料金.","examples":[{"japanese":"寝てしまって、乗り越しをしてしまった。","romaji":"Nete shimatte, norikoshi o shite shimatta.","german":"I fell asleep and rode past my stop.","furigana":"ね|の|こ"},{"japanese":"乗り越しの場合は、精算機で料金を払ってください。","romaji":"Norikoshi no baai wa, seisanki de ryōkin o haratte kudasai.","german":"If you rode past your stop, please pay the fare at the fare adjustment machine.","furigana":"の|こ|ばあい|せいさんき|りょうきん|はら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A small store in stations, hospitals or schools: 駅の売店, 病院の売店.","examples":[{"japanese":"駅の売店で新聞を買った。","romaji":"Eki no baiten de shinbun o katta.","german":"I bought a newspaper at the station kiosk.","furigana":"えき|ばいてん|しんぶん|か"},{"japanese":"病院の売店は夜八時まで開いている。","romaji":"Byōin no baiten wa yoru hachiji made aite iru.","german":"The shop in the hospital is open until 8 p.m.","furigana":"びょういん|ばいてん|よるはちじ|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Casual farewell among friends and to children; often with a wave. Not used toward superiors.","examples":[{"japanese":"子どもが手を振って「バイバイ」と言った。","romaji":"Kodomo ga te o futte \"baibai\" to itta.","german":"The child waved and said \"bye-bye\".","furigana":"こ|て|ふ|い"},{"japanese":"じゃあ、また明日ね。バイバイ。","romaji":"Jā, mata ashita ne. Baibai.","german":"Well then, see you tomorrow. Bye-bye.","furigana":"あした"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Formal word for commercial transactions: 土地の売買 (land sale), 売買契約 (sales contract). As a verb 売買する.","examples":[{"japanese":"インターネットで中古品を売買する人が増えている。","romaji":"Intānetto de chūkohin o baibai suru hito ga fuete iru.","german":"More and more people are buying and selling used goods on the internet.","furigana":"ちゅうこひん|ばいばい|ひと|ふ"},{"japanese":"土地の売買には契約書が必要だ。","romaji":"Tochi no baibai ni wa keiyakusho ga hitsuyō da.","german":"A contract is needed for buying and selling land.","furigana":"とち|ばいばい|けいやくしょ|ひつよう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Device for weighing: 秤で量る. A bathroom scale is usually called 体重計.","examples":[{"japanese":"小麦粉を秤で二百グラム量った。","romaji":"Komugiko o hakari de nihyaku guramu hakatta.","german":"I weighed out 200 grams of flour on the scale.","furigana":"こむぎこ|はかり|にひゃく|はか"},{"japanese":"郵便局の秤で荷物の重さを調べた。","romaji":"Yūbinkyoku no hakari de nimotsu no omosa o shirabeta.","german":"I checked the weight of the package on the scale at the post office.","furigana":"ゆうびんきょく|はかり|にもつ|おも|しら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Bucket for water or cleaning: バケツに水をくむ. The spelling 馬穴 is an old ateji.","examples":[{"japanese":"バケツに水をくんで、花に水をやった。","romaji":"Baketsu ni mizu o kunde, hana ni mizu o yatta.","german":"I drew water into a bucket and watered the flowers.","furigana":"みず|はな|みず"},{"japanese":"雨漏りがするので、床にバケツを置いた。","romaji":"Amamori ga suru node, yuka ni baketsu o oita.","german":"Because the roof was leaking, I put a bucket on the floor.","furigana":"あまも|ゆか|お"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Ladder: 梯子を上る／かける. Colloquially also \"to go from one bar to another\" (はしご酒). Often in hiragana.","examples":[{"japanese":"梯子を使って屋根に上った。","romaji":"Hashigo o tsukatte yane ni nobotta.","german":"I climbed onto the roof using a ladder.","furigana":"はしご|つか|やね|のぼ"},{"japanese":"梯子を壁にかけて、窓をふいた。","romaji":"Hashigo o kabe ni kakete, mado o fuita.","german":"I leaned the ladder against the wall and wiped the window.","furigana":"はしご|かべ|まど"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Clothing worn directly on the skin (undershirt, etc.). Similar: 下着.","examples":[{"japanese":"赤ちゃんの肌着は綿がいい。","romaji":"Akachan no hadagi wa men ga ii.","german":"Cotton is good for baby underwear.","furigana":"あか|はだぎ|めん"},{"japanese":"寒いので、厚い肌着を着ている。","romaji":"Samui node, atsui hadagi o kite iru.","german":"Because it is cold, I am wearing thick underwear.","furigana":"さむ|あつ|はだぎ|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Deep bowl (料理を鉢に盛る) and flowerpot (植木鉢). Homophones: 八, 蜂.","examples":[{"japanese":"サラダを大きな鉢に盛った。","romaji":"Sarada o ōki na hachi ni motta.","german":"I served the salad in a large bowl.","furigana":"おお|はち|も"},{"japanese":"花を新しい鉢に植え替えた。","romaji":"Hana o atarashii hachi ni uekaeta.","german":"I repotted the flower into a new pot.","furigana":"はな|あたら|はち|う|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The mark ×, which means \"wrong/no\" in Japan; opposite: 丸（まる, ○） = correct. Also shown with the arms: 腕でバツを作る.","examples":[{"japanese":"間違えた答えにバツがついていた。","romaji":"Machigaeta kotae ni batsu ga tsuite ita.","german":"The wrong answers were marked with a cross.","furigana":"まちが|こた"},{"japanese":"彼は腕でバツを作って「だめ」と言った。","romaji":"Kare wa ude de batsu o tsukutte \"dame\" to itta.","german":"He crossed his arms to make an X and said \"no\".","furigana":"かれ|うで|つく|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Versatile loanword: 山をバックに写真を撮る (to take a photo with a mountain as backdrop), 車をバックさせる (to reverse a car), 大企業がバックについている (to have a big company's backing). Not to be confused with バッグ (bag).","examples":[{"japanese":"富士山をバックに写真を撮った。","romaji":"Fujisan o bakku ni shashin o totta.","german":"We took a photo with Mt. Fuji in the background.","furigana":"ふじさん|しゃしん|と"},{"japanese":"駐車場で車をゆっくりバックさせた。","romaji":"Chūshajō de kuruma o yukkuri bakku saseta.","german":"In the parking lot, I slowly backed the car up.","furigana":"ちゅうしゃじょう|くるま"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A conversation to reach an agreement: 話し合いで決める (to decide through discussion), 話し合いがつく (to reach an agreement). Verb: 話し合う.","examples":[{"japanese":"問題は話し合いで解決しよう。","romaji":"Mondai wa hanashiai de kaiketsu shiyō.","german":"Let's solve the problem by talking it over.","furigana":"もんだい|はな|あ|かいけつ"},{"japanese":"長い話し合いの結果、計画は中止になった。","romaji":"Nagai hanashiai no kekka, keikaku wa chūshi ni natta.","german":"After a long discussion, the plan was canceled.","furigana":"なが|はな|あ|けっか|けいかく|ちゅうし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"To approach someone (often a stranger): 知らない人に話しかけられた. Often written in kana: 話しかける.","examples":[{"japanese":"駅で外国人に英語で話しかけられた。","romaji":"Eki de gaikokujin ni eigo de hanashikakerareta.","german":"A foreigner spoke to me in English at the station.","furigana":"えき|がいこくじん|えいご|はな"},{"japanese":"勇気を出して、隣の席の人に話しかけた。","romaji":"Yūki o dashite, tonari no seki no hito ni hanashikaketa.","german":"I mustered up my courage and spoke to the person in the next seat.","furigana":"ゆうき|だ|となり|せき|ひと|はな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The line is busy: 電話が話し中だ; also \"currently talking\": お話し中すみません.","examples":[{"japanese":"何度電話しても話し中だった。","romaji":"Nando denwa shite mo hanashichū datta.","german":"No matter how many times I called, the line was busy.","furigana":"なんどでんわ|はな|ちゅう"},{"japanese":"お話し中すみませんが、お客様がお見えです。","romaji":"Ohanashichū sumimasen ga, okyakusama ga omie desu.","german":"Sorry to interrupt your conversation, but a visitor has arrived.","furigana":"はな|ちゅう|きゃくさま|み"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The woman on her wedding day; groom: 花婿（はなむこ）. 花嫁姿 = the bride in her wedding attire.","examples":[{"japanese":"花嫁は白いドレスを着ていた。","romaji":"Hanayome wa shiroi doresu o kite ita.","german":"The bride was wearing a white dress.","furigana":"はなよめ|しろ|き"},{"japanese":"花嫁の父がスピーチをした。","romaji":"Hanayome no chichi ga supīchi o shita.","german":"The bride's father gave a speech.","furigana":"はなよめ|ちち"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A mechanical spring (ばねが伸びる); figuratively the spring of the legs (足のばね). The kanji 発条 is rare.","examples":[{"japanese":"このおもちゃはばねの力で跳ねる。","romaji":"Kono omocha wa bane no chikara de haneru.","german":"This toy jumps by the force of a spring.","furigana":"ちから|は"},{"japanese":"バスケットボールの選手は足のばねが強い。","romaji":"Basukettobōru no senshu wa ashi no bane ga tsuyoi.","german":"Basketball players have strong spring in their legs.","furigana":"せんしゅ|あし|つよ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"To be popular (若者に流行っている), to be rampant (風邪が流行っている) or to flourish (店が流行る). Noun: 流行（りゅうこう）.","examples":[{"japanese":"今、若者の間でこの歌が流行っている。","romaji":"Ima, wakamono no aida de kono uta ga hayatte iru.","german":"This song is popular among young people right now.","furigana":"いま|わかもの|あいだ|うた|はや"},{"japanese":"学校でインフルエンザが流行っている。","romaji":"Gakkō de infuruenza ga hayatte iru.","german":"The flu is going around at school.","furigana":"がっこう|はや"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"To pay money into an account or to an office: 授業料を払い込む. Noun: 払い込み（払込）.","examples":[{"japanese":"授業料は月末までに銀行で払い込んでください。","romaji":"Jugyōryō wa getsumatsu made ni ginkō de haraikonde kudasai.","german":"Please pay the tuition at the bank by the end of the month.","furigana":"じゅぎょうりょう|げつまつ|ぎんこう|はら|こ"},{"japanese":"コンビニで電気代を払い込んだ。","romaji":"Konbini de denkidai o haraikonda.","german":"I paid the electricity bill at a convenience store.","furigana":"でんきだい|はら|こ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"To be motivated and full of energy: 新しい仕事に張り切っている. Often in te-form: 張り切って準備する.","examples":[{"japanese":"弟は明日の試合に向けて張り切っている。","romaji":"Otōto wa ashita no shiai ni mukete harikitte iru.","german":"My younger brother is raring to go for tomorrow's match.","furigana":"おとうと|あした|しあい|む|は|き"},{"japanese":"母は張り切って、朝からごちそうを作った。","romaji":"Haha wa harikitte, asa kara gochisō o tsukutta.","german":"My mother was full of energy and has been cooking a feast since morning.","furigana":"はは|は|き|あさ|つく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Part of a Japanese address (〜丁目〜番地). Japanese addresses refer to blocks rather than streets.","examples":[{"japanese":"住所の番地を間違えて、手紙が戻ってきた。","romaji":"Jūsho no banchi o machigaete, tegami ga modotte kita.","german":"I got the house number wrong in the address, and the letter came back.","furigana":"じゅうしょ|ばんち|まちが|てがみ|もど"},{"japanese":"この辺りは番地が分かりにくい。","romaji":"Kono atari wa banchi ga wakarinikui.","german":"The lot numbers in this area are hard to figure out.","furigana":"あた|ばんち|わ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Traditionally underpants; in fashion also (women's) trousers (ワイドパンツ). Trousers in general are otherwise called ズボン.","examples":[{"japanese":"旅行用にパンツを三枚と靴下を三足用意した。","romaji":"Ryokōyō ni pantsu o sanmai to kutsushita o sanzoku yōi shita.","german":"For the trip I packed three pairs of underpants and three pairs of socks.","furigana":"りょこうよう|さんまい|くつした|さんぞくようい"},{"japanese":"最近は白いパンツがはやっている。","romaji":"Saikin wa shiroi pantsu ga hayatte iru.","german":"White pants have been in fashion lately.","furigana":"さいきん|しろ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 10 (source: N5; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"Steering wheel of a car (ハンドルを切る = to turn the wheel), handlebar of a bicycle, door handle. A handle in the sense of a grip on a bag or pot is rather 取っ手.","examples":[{"japanese":"雪の道ではハンドルをゆっくり切ってください。","romaji":"Yuki no michi de wa handoru o yukkuri kitte kudasai.","german":"On snowy roads, please steer slowly.","furigana":"ゆき|みち|き"},{"japanese":"自転車のハンドルが曲がってしまった。","romaji":"Jitensha no handoru ga magatte shimatta.","german":"The bicycle handlebar has gotten bent.","furigana":"じてんしゃ|ま"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A place without direct sunlight: 日陰で休む. The shadow of a figure is called 影（かげ）.","examples":[{"japanese":"暑いので、日陰で少し休もう。","romaji":"Atsui node, hikage de sukoshi yasumō.","german":"It's hot, so let's rest in the shade for a bit.","furigana":"あつ|ひかげ|すこ|やす"},{"japanese":"この花は日陰でもよく育つ。","romaji":"Kono hana wa hikage demo yoku sodatsu.","german":"This flower grows well even in the shade.","furigana":"はな|ひかげ|そだ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"To stop someone from leaving or resigning: 帰ろうとする客を引き止める. Also written 引き留める.","examples":[{"japanese":"会社を辞めようとしたが、上司に引き止められた。","romaji":"Kaisha o yameyō to shita ga, jōshi ni hikitomerareta.","german":"I tried to quit the company, but my boss talked me out of it.","furigana":"かいしゃ|や|じょうし|ひ|と"},{"japanese":"長く引き止めてしまって、すみません。","romaji":"Nagaku hikitomete shimatte, sumimasen.","german":"Sorry for keeping you so long.","furigana":"なが|ひ|と"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Handgun; in sports also the starting pistol (スタートのピストル). General term for firearms: 銃.","examples":[{"japanese":"スタートのピストルが鳴った。","romaji":"Sutāto no pisutoru ga natta.","german":"The starting pistol went off.","furigana":"な"},{"japanese":"日本では、ピストルを持つことは原則として禁止されている。","romaji":"Nihon de wa, pisutoru o motsu koto wa gensoku to shite kinshi sarete iru.","german":"In Japan, owning a pistol is in principle prohibited.","furigana":"にほん|も|げんそく|きんし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"ビタミンC, ビタミン剤 (vitamin tablet), ビタミン不足 (vitamin deficiency).","examples":[{"japanese":"果物にはビタミンがたくさん含まれている。","romaji":"Kudamono ni wa bitamin ga takusan fukumarete iru.","german":"Fruit contains a lot of vitamins.","furigana":"くだもの|ふく"},{"japanese":"冬はビタミンCを多めにとるようにしている。","romaji":"Fuyu wa bitamin shī o ōme ni toru yō ni shite iru.","german":"In winter, I try to take more vitamin C.","furigana":"ふゆ|おお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Something fits exactly (予想がぴたりと当たる), sits snugly or stops abruptly (雨がぴたりとやむ). Mostly with と; similar to ぴったり.","examples":[{"japanese":"天気予報がぴたりと当たった。","romaji":"Tenki yohō ga pitari to atatta.","german":"The weather forecast was exactly right.","furigana":"てんきよほう|あ"},{"japanese":"子どもの泣き声がぴたりとやんだ。","romaji":"Kodomo no nakigoe ga pitari to yanda.","german":"The child's crying stopped abruptly.","furigana":"こ|な|ごえ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Literally to get caught on something (枝に引っかかる); figuratively to fall for a trick (詐欺に引っかかる) or to leave an odd impression (その言葉が引っかかる).","examples":[{"japanese":"風船が木の枝に引っかかってしまった。","romaji":"Fūsen ga ki no eda ni hikkakatte shimatta.","german":"The balloon got caught on a tree branch.","furigana":"ふうせん|き|えだ|ひ"},{"japanese":"祖母が電話の詐欺に引っかかりそうになった。","romaji":"Sobo ga denwa no sagi ni hikkakarisō ni natta.","german":"My grandmother almost fell for a telephone scam.","furigana":"そぼ|でんわ|さぎ|ひ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Writing down: 筆記用具 (writing materials), 筆記試験 (written exam) as opposed to 面接 (interview).","examples":[{"japanese":"試験には筆記用具を忘れずに持ってきてください。","romaji":"Shiken ni wa hikki yōgu o wasurezu ni motte kite kudasai.","german":"Please do not forget to bring writing materials to the exam.","furigana":"しけん|ひっきようぐ|わす|も"},{"japanese":"筆記の試験に合格して、次は面接だ。","romaji":"Hikki no shiken ni gōkaku shite, tsugi wa mensetsu da.","german":"I passed the written exam; the interview is next.","furigana":"ひっき|しけん|ごうかく|つぎ|めんせつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Transitive: to turn something over (ホットケーキをひっくり返す), to knock over (コップをひっくり返す) or to completely reverse a situation (試合をひっくり返す). Intransitive: ひっくり返る.","examples":[{"japanese":"フライパンのホットケーキをひっくり返した。","romaji":"Furaipan no hottokēki o hikkurikaeshita.","german":"I flipped the pancake in the frying pan.","furigana":"かえ"},{"japanese":"猫がコップをひっくり返して、水がこぼれた。","romaji":"Neko ga koppu o hikkurikaeshite, mizu ga koboreta.","german":"The cat knocked over the glass and the water spilled.","furigana":"ねこ|かえ|みず"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Intransitive counterpart of ひっくり返す: 車がひっくり返る (a car flips over), 滑ってひっくり返る (to slip and fall on one's back).","examples":[{"japanese":"強い風でテントがひっくり返った。","romaji":"Tsuyoi kaze de tento ga hikkurikaetta.","german":"The tent was blown over by the strong wind.","furigana":"つよ|かぜ|かえ"},{"japanese":"氷の上で滑って、ひっくり返ってしまった。","romaji":"Kōri no ue de subette, hikkurikaette shimatta.","german":"I slipped on the ice and fell over.","furigana":"こおり|うえ|すべ|かえ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"To withdraw (部屋に引っ込む), to be set back (道から引っ込んだ家) or to stay out of it: 引っ込んでろ！(Stay out of it!). Transitive: 引っ込める.","examples":[{"japanese":"父は食事のあと、すぐ自分の部屋に引っ込んだ。","romaji":"Chichi wa shokuji no ato, sugu jibun no heya ni hikkonda.","german":"After the meal, my father immediately withdrew to his room.","furigana":"ちち|しょくじ|じぶん|へや|ひ|こ"},{"japanese":"その店は大通りから少し引っ込んだ所にある。","romaji":"Sono mise wa ōdōri kara sukoshi hikkonda tokoro ni aru.","german":"The shop is located a little set back from the main street.","furigana":"みせ|おおどお|すこ|ひ|こ|ところ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Provisionally, before going further: ひとまず安心した (relieved for now). Similar: とりあえず.","examples":[{"japanese":"手術が成功して、ひとまず安心した。","romaji":"Shujutsu ga seikō shite, hitomazu anshin shita.","german":"The operation was a success, and I am relieved for now.","furigana":"しゅじゅつ|せいこう|あんしん"},{"japanese":"今日の会議はひとまずここで終わりにしましょう。","romaji":"Kyō no kaigi wa hitomazu koko de owari ni shimashō.","german":"Let's end today's meeting here for now.","furigana":"きょう|かいぎ|お"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The pupil of the eye, poetically also the eyes: 澄んだ瞳 (clear eyes). In everyday speech 目 is used more often.","examples":[{"japanese":"暗い所では瞳が大きくなる。","romaji":"Kurai tokoro de wa hitomi ga ōkiku naru.","german":"In the dark, the pupils get larger.","furigana":"くら|ところ|ひとみ|おお"},{"japanese":"その子は大きな瞳で私を見つめた。","romaji":"Sono ko wa ōki na hitomi de watashi o mitsumeta.","german":"The child gazed at me with big eyes.","furigana":"こ|おお|ひとみ|わたし|み"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"To rest briefly: ここで一休みしよう. As a verb 一休みする.","examples":[{"japanese":"疲れたから、ここで一休みしよう。","romaji":"Tsukareta kara, koko de hitoyasumi shiyō.","german":"I'm tired, so let's take a short break here.","furigana":"つか|ひとやす"},{"japanese":"一休みしてから、また作業を始めた。","romaji":"Hitoyasumi shite kara, mata sagyō o hajimeta.","german":"After a short breather, I started working again.","furigana":"ひとやす|さぎょう|はじ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Without outside action: ドアがひとりでに開いた. Similar: 自然に, 勝手に.","examples":[{"japanese":"風もないのに、ドアがひとりでに閉まった。","romaji":"Kaze mo nai noni, doa ga hitorideni shimatta.","german":"Although there was no wind, the door closed by itself.","furigana":"かぜ|し"},{"japanese":"この傷はひとりでに治るでしょう。","romaji":"Kono kizu wa hitorideni naoru deshō.","german":"This wound will probably heal by itself.","furigana":"きず|なお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The day of a planned event (日にちを決める) or the number of days remaining (日にちがない).","examples":[{"japanese":"旅行の日にちがまだ決まっていない。","romaji":"Ryokō no hinichi ga mada kimatte inai.","german":"The date of the trip hasn't been decided yet.","furigana":"りょこう|ひ|き"},{"japanese":"試験まであまり日にちがない。","romaji":"Shiken made amari hinichi ga nai.","german":"There aren't many days left until the exam.","furigana":"しけん|ひ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 28"},
{"notes":"In everyday use for soft plastic: ビニール袋 (plastic bag), ビニール傘 (clear plastic umbrella).","examples":[{"japanese":"雨の日は、ビニールの傘を使っている。","romaji":"Ame no hi wa, binīru no kasa o tsukatte iru.","german":"On rainy days, I use a plastic umbrella.","furigana":"あめ|ひ|かさ|つか"},{"japanese":"濡れた服をビニールの袋に入れた。","romaji":"Nureta fuku o binīru no fukuro ni ireta.","german":"I put the wet clothes in a plastic bag.","furigana":"ぬ|ふく|ふくろ|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The time when the sun goes down; opposite: 日の出 (sunrise). Similar: 日没.","examples":[{"japanese":"冬は日の入りが早い。","romaji":"Fuyu wa hinoiri ga hayai.","german":"In winter, the sun sets early.","furigana":"ふゆ|ひ|い|はや"},{"japanese":"海岸で日の入りを眺めた。","romaji":"Kaigan de hinoiri o nagameta.","german":"We watched the sunset at the beach.","furigana":"かいがん|ひ|い|なが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Reverberation (鐘の響き) and the sound effect of a word or voice: 美しい響きの名前.","examples":[{"japanese":"お寺の鐘の響きが遠くまで聞こえた。","romaji":"Otera no kane no hibiki ga tōku made kikoeta.","german":"The sound of the temple bell could be heard far away.","furigana":"てら|かね|ひび|とお|き"},{"japanese":"「さくら」は響きのいい名前だ。","romaji":"\"Sakura\" wa hibiki no ii namae da.","german":"\"Sakura\" is a nice-sounding name.","furigana":"ひび|なまえ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Care of one's appearance: 美容にいい (good for the skin/beauty), 美容院 (hair salon), 美容師 (hairdresser).","examples":[{"japanese":"よく寝ることは美容にもいい。","romaji":"Yoku neru koto wa biyō ni mo ii.","german":"Getting plenty of sleep is also good for your looks.","furigana":"ね|びよう"},{"japanese":"姉は美容の専門学校に通っている。","romaji":"Ane wa biyō no senmon gakkō ni kayotte iru.","german":"My older sister attends a vocational school for beauty care.","furigana":"あね|びよう|せんもんがっこう|かよ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Cover of a book or magazine: 雑誌の表紙を飾る (to appear on a magazine cover). Homophone: 拍子 (rhythm).","examples":[{"japanese":"この本は表紙の絵がきれいだ。","romaji":"Kono hon wa hyōshi no e ga kirei da.","german":"The cover picture of this book is beautiful.","furigana":"ほん|ひょうし|え"},{"japanese":"その俳優は雑誌の表紙を飾った。","romaji":"Sono haiyū wa zasshi no hyōshi o kazatta.","german":"That actor appeared on the cover of a magazine.","furigana":"はいゆう|ざっし|ひょうし|かざ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Full form of ビル; today almost only in names and written language. In everyday speech ビル is used.","examples":[{"japanese":"駅前に二十階建てのビルディングが建った。","romaji":"Ekimae ni nijukkaidate no birudingu ga tatta.","german":"A twenty-story building was built in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|にじゅっかいだ|た"},{"japanese":"古いビルディングが取り壊されることになった。","romaji":"Furui birudingu ga torikowasareru koto ni natta.","german":"The old building is to be demolished.","furigana":"ふる|と|こわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A public square in a city (駅前広場) or an open area for playing.","examples":[{"japanese":"駅前の広場で祭りが開かれた。","romaji":"Ekimae no hiroba de matsuri ga hirakareta.","german":"A festival was held in the square in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|ひろば|まつ|ひら"},{"japanese":"子どもたちが広場でボール遊びをしている。","romaji":"Kodomotachi ga hiroba de bōru asobi o shite iru.","german":"The children are playing ball in the open space.","furigana":"こ|ひろば|あそ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Mostly 広々とした: 広々とした部屋 (spacious room), 広々とした海 (vast sea).","examples":[{"japanese":"新しい家のリビングは広々としている。","romaji":"Atarashii ie no ribingu wa hirobiro to shite iru.","german":"The living room in the new house is spacious.","furigana":"あたら|いえ|ひろびろ"},{"japanese":"目の前に広々とした海が広がっていた。","romaji":"Me no mae ni hirobiro to shita umi ga hirogatte ita.","german":"A vast sea stretched out before us.","furigana":"め|まえ|ひろびろ|うみ|ひろ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"To make something known (うわさを広める) or to expand (知識を広める). Intransitive: 広まる.","examples":[{"japanese":"日本の文化を世界に広めたい。","romaji":"Nihon no bunka o sekai ni hirometai.","german":"I want to spread Japanese culture around the world.","furigana":"にほん|ぶんか|せかい|ひろ"},{"japanese":"旅行は視野を広めるいい機会だ。","romaji":"Ryokō wa shiya o hiromeru ii kikai da.","german":"Traveling is a good opportunity to broaden one's horizons.","furigana":"りょこう|しや|ひろ|きかい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Paper for letters; the envelope is called 封筒. Also in kana: びんせん.","examples":[{"japanese":"かわいい便箋で友達に手紙を書いた。","romaji":"Kawaii binsen de tomodachi ni tegami o kaita.","german":"I wrote a letter to a friend on cute letter paper.","furigana":"びんせん|ともだち|てがみ|か"},{"japanese":"文房具店で便箋と封筒を買った。","romaji":"Bunbōguten de binsen to fūtō o katta.","german":"I bought letter paper and envelopes at a stationery store.","furigana":"ぶんぼうぐてん|びんせん|ふうとう|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Zipper; colloquially also チャック. ファスナーを閉める／開ける (to zip up/open).","examples":[{"japanese":"かばんのファスナーが壊れて閉まらない。","romaji":"Kaban no fasunā ga kowarete shimaranai.","german":"The zipper on the bag is broken and won't close.","furigana":"こわ|し"},{"japanese":"上着のファスナーを首まで上げた。","romaji":"Uwagi no fasunā o kubi made ageta.","german":"I zipped my jacket up to my neck.","furigana":"うわぎ|くび|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Opposite of 幸運: 不運な事故, 不運にも (unfortunately).","examples":[{"japanese":"不運にも、出発の日に熱が出た。","romaji":"Fuun ni mo, shuppatsu no hi ni netsu ga deta.","german":"Unfortunately, I came down with a fever on the day of departure.","furigana":"ふうん|しゅっぱつ|ひ|ねつ|で"},{"japanese":"彼はけがという不運に負けず、練習を続けた。","romaji":"Kare wa kega to iu fuun ni makezu, renshū o tsuzuketa.","german":"He didn't let the misfortune of his injury beat him and kept training.","furigana":"かれ|ふうん|ま|れんしゅう|つづ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Intransitive: 理解が深まる, 秋が深まる (autumn progresses). Transitive: 深める.","examples":[{"japanese":"話し合いで、お互いの理解が深まった。","romaji":"Hanashiai de, otagai no rikai ga fukamatta.","german":"The discussion deepened our mutual understanding.","furigana":"はな|あ|たが|りかい|ふか"},{"japanese":"秋が深まって、山が赤く色づいてきた。","romaji":"Aki ga fukamatte, yama ga akaku irozuite kita.","german":"Autumn has deepened, and the mountains have begun to turn red.","furigana":"あき|ふか|やま|あか|いろ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"To fill something with air: 風船を膨らます; figurative 期待に胸を膨らます (to be full of expectations). Intransitive: 膨らむ.","examples":[{"japanese":"子どもたちが風船を膨らましている。","romaji":"Kodomotachi ga fūsen o fukuramashite iru.","german":"The children are blowing up balloons.","furigana":"こ|ふうせん|ふく"},{"japanese":"新入生は期待に胸を膨らましている。","romaji":"Shinnyūsei wa kitai ni mune o fukuramashite iru.","german":"The new students are full of expectations.","furigana":"しんにゅうせい|きたい|むね|ふく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"An agreed-upon sign (モールス符号) or a mathematical sign (プラスの符号).","examples":[{"japanese":"点と線の符号で文字を表すのがモールス信号だ。","romaji":"Ten to sen no fugō de moji o arawasu no ga mōrusu shingō da.","german":"Morse code represents letters with signs made of dots and dashes.","furigana":"てん|せん|ふごう|もじ|あらわ|しんごう"},{"japanese":"計算の答えは、符号をよく確認すること。","romaji":"Keisan no kotae wa, fugō o yoku kakunin suru koto.","german":"When you get a calculation result, check the sign carefully.","furigana":"けいさん|こた|ふごう|かくにん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Intransitive: 道が塞がる (blocked), 穴が塞がる (to close up), 手が塞がっている (to have one's hands full). Transitive: 塞ぐ.","examples":[{"japanese":"事故で道が塞がって、車が進めない。","romaji":"Jiko de michi ga fusagatte, kuruma ga susumenai.","german":"The road is blocked by an accident, and the cars cannot move forward.","furigana":"じこ|みち|ふさ|くるま|すす"},{"japanese":"今、手が塞がっているので、ドアを開けてくれる？","romaji":"Ima, te ga fusagatte iru node, doa o akete kureru?","german":"My hands are full right now, so could you open the door?","furigana":"いま|て|ふさ|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"To be silly (授業中にふざける) or to make fun of someone; as an angry exclamation: ふざけるな！ (Stop the nonsense!). The kanji 巫山戯る are ateji.","examples":[{"japanese":"授業中にふざけて、先生に注意された。","romaji":"Jugyōchū ni fuzakete, sensei ni chūi sareta.","german":"I messed around during class and was warned by the teacher.","furigana":"じゅぎょうちゅう|せんせい|ちゅうい"},{"japanese":"子どもたちがふざけ合って笑っている。","romaji":"Kodomotachi ga fuzakeatte waratte iru.","german":"The children are fooling around with each other and laughing.","furigana":"こ|あ|わら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Belonging to something: 付属品 (accessory), 大学付属の病院 (university hospital), 付属高校 (high school affiliated with a university). Also written 附属.","examples":[{"japanese":"このカメラには充電器が付属している。","romaji":"Kono kamera ni wa jūdenki ga fuzoku shite iru.","german":"This camera comes with a charger.","furigana":"じゅうでんき|ふぞく"},{"japanese":"彼女は大学の付属病院で働いている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa daigaku no fuzoku byōin de hataraite iru.","german":"She works at the university hospital.","furigana":"かのじょ|だいがく|ふぞくびょういん|はたら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Lid of a pot or container: 蓋をする (to put a lid on), 蓋を開ける. Often written in kana: ふた.","examples":[{"japanese":"鍋に蓋をして、十分ほど煮てください。","romaji":"Nabe ni futa o shite, juppun hodo nite kudasai.","german":"Put a lid on the pot and simmer for about ten minutes.","furigana":"なべ|ふた|じゅっぷん|に"},{"japanese":"瓶の蓋が固くて開かない。","romaji":"Bin no futa ga katakute akanai.","german":"The lid of the jar is tight and won't open.","furigana":"びん|ふた|かた|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Traffic or communication is interrupted: 大雪で電車が不通になる. Homophone: 普通 (normal).","examples":[{"japanese":"台風の影響で、電車が不通になっている。","romaji":"Taifū no eikyō de, densha ga futsū ni natte iru.","german":"Because of the typhoon, train service is suspended.","furigana":"たいふう|えいきょう|でんしゃ|ふつう"},{"japanese":"地震のあと、電話がしばらく不通だった。","romaji":"Jishin no ato, denwa ga shibaraku futsū datta.","german":"After the earthquake, the phone lines were down for a while.","furigana":"じしん|でんわ|ふつう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Transitive counterpart of ぶつかる: 頭をぶつける (to bump one's head), 車をぶつける; figurative 怒りをぶつける (to vent one's anger). The kanji 打付ける is rare.","examples":[{"japanese":"棚の角に頭をぶつけてしまった。","romaji":"Tana no kado ni atama o butsukete shimatta.","german":"I bumped my head on the corner of the shelf.","furigana":"たな|かど|あたま"},{"japanese":"仕事の不満を家族にぶつけるのはよくない。","romaji":"Shigoto no fuman o kazoku ni butsukeru no wa yokunai.","german":"It is not good to take out your frustration with work on your family.","furigana":"しごと|ふまん|かぞく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Shipping by boat, cheaper but slower than 航空便 (airmail).","examples":[{"japanese":"本は重いので、船便で送った。","romaji":"Hon wa omoi node, funabin de okutta.","german":"The books were heavy, so I sent them by sea mail.","furigana":"ほん|おも|ふなびん|おく"},{"japanese":"船便だと、届くまで一か月ほどかかる。","romaji":"Funabin da to, todoku made ikkagetsu hodo kakaru.","german":"By sea mail, it takes about a month to arrive.","furigana":"ふなびん|とど|いっ|げつ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 11 (source: N5; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"Formal word for parents, e.g. in schools: 父母会 (parents' meeting). Everyday words: 両親, 親.","examples":[{"japanese":"学校で父母会が開かれた。","romaji":"Gakkō de fubokai ga hirakareta.","german":"A parents' meeting was held at school.","furigana":"がっこう|ふぼかい|ひら"},{"japanese":"申込書に父母の名前を書いてください。","romaji":"Mōshikomisho ni fubo no namae o kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write your parents' names on the application form.","furigana":"もうしこみしょ|ふぼ|なまえ|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The lower part of a mountain: 山の麓の村. Opposite: 頂上 (summit). Often written in kana.","examples":[{"japanese":"山の麓に小さな村がある。","romaji":"Yama no fumoto ni chiisa na mura ga aru.","german":"There is a small village at the foot of the mountain.","furigana":"やま|ふもと|ちい|むら"},{"japanese":"麓から頂上まで三時間かかった。","romaji":"Fumoto kara chōjō made sanjikan kakatta.","german":"It took us three hours from the foot to the summit.","furigana":"ふもと|ちょうじょう|さんじかん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A woman's top; ワイシャツ is the men's dress shirt.","examples":[{"japanese":"白いブラウスに紺のスカートを合わせた。","romaji":"Shiroi burausu ni kon no sukāto o awaseta.","german":"I paired a white blouse with a navy skirt.","furigana":"しろ|こん|あ"},{"japanese":"このブラウスはアイロンがいらない。","romaji":"Kono burausu wa airon ga iranai.","german":"This blouse doesn't need ironing."}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"To carry something dangling or to hang it up: 袋をぶら下げて歩く, 風鈴をぶら下げる. Intransitive: ぶら下がる.","examples":[{"japanese":"父は買い物袋をぶら下げて帰ってきた。","romaji":"Chichi wa kaimonobukuro o burasagete kaette kita.","german":"My father came home carrying a shopping bag.","furigana":"ちち|か|ものぶくろ|さ|かえ"},{"japanese":"窓に風鈴をぶら下げた。","romaji":"Mado ni fūrin o burasageta.","german":"I hung a wind chime in the window.","furigana":"まど|ふうりん|さ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A brush for hair, clothes or shoes: ヘアブラシ, 歯ブラシ (toothbrush). ブラシをかける = to brush.","examples":[{"japanese":"出かける前に髪にブラシをかけた。","romaji":"Dekakeru mae ni kami ni burashi o kaketa.","german":"I brushed my hair before going out.","furigana":"で|まえ|かみ"},{"japanese":"靴の泥をブラシで落とした。","romaji":"Kutsu no doro o burashi de otoshita.","german":"I brushed the mud off my shoes.","furigana":"くつ|どろ|お"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Full form; in everyday speech usually shortened to ホーム. Track number: 〜番線.","examples":[{"japanese":"プラットホームで電車を待っている人が多い。","romaji":"Purattohōmu de densha o matte iru hito ga ōi.","german":"Many people are waiting for the train on the platform.","furigana":"でんしゃ|ま|ひと|おお"},{"japanese":"危ないので、プラットホームの端を歩かないでください。","romaji":"Abunai node, purattohōmu no hashi o arukanaide kudasai.","german":"Please do not walk along the edge of the platform, as it is dangerous.","furigana":"あぶ|はし|ある"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Unattached (フリーのカメラマン = freelance photographer), free of charge (フリーの Wi-Fi) or as the suffix \"-free\" (バリアフリー).","examples":[{"japanese":"兄は会社を辞めて、フリーのデザイナーになった。","romaji":"Ani wa kaisha o yamete, furī no dezainā ni natta.","german":"My brother quit his company and became a freelance designer.","furigana":"あに|かいしゃ|や"},{"japanese":"このカフェではフリーのWi-Fiが使える。","romaji":"Kono kafe de wa furī no waifai ga tsukaeru.","german":"Free Wi-Fi is available at this cafe."}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"To turn one's head or body backward: 名前を呼ばれて振り向く.","examples":[{"japanese":"名前を呼ばれて振り向くと、昔の友達がいた。","romaji":"Namae o yobarete furimuku to, mukashi no tomodachi ga ita.","german":"When I turned around after hearing my name called, an old friend was there.","furigana":"なまえ|よ|ふ|む|むかし|ともだち"},{"japanese":"彼女は一度も振り向かずに行ってしまった。","romaji":"Kanojo wa ichido mo furimukazu ni itte shimatta.","german":"She left without looking back even once.","furigana":"かのじょ|いちど|ふ|む|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"In schools, a handed-out worksheet (プリントを配る); also printing and a printout (写真をプリントする).","examples":[{"japanese":"先生が宿題のプリントを配った。","romaji":"Sensei ga shukudai no purinto o kubatta.","german":"The teacher handed out the homework worksheets.","furigana":"せんせい|しゅくだい|くば"},{"japanese":"コンビニで写真をプリントした。","romaji":"Konbini de shashin o purinto shita.","german":"I printed the photos at a convenience store.","furigana":"しゃしん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A decorative pin for clothing: ブローチをつける.","examples":[{"japanese":"祖母からもらったブローチを胸につけた。","romaji":"Sobo kara moratta burōchi o mune ni tsuketa.","german":"I pinned the brooch I got from my grandmother to my chest.","furigana":"そぼ|むね"},{"japanese":"花の形のブローチが店に並んでいる。","romaji":"Hana no katachi no burōchi ga mise ni narande iru.","german":"Flower-shaped brooches are lined up in the shop.","furigana":"はな|かたち|みせ|なら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A fractional number such as ½ (二分の一 = \"one half\"; in Japanese the denominator comes first). Decimal: 小数.","examples":[{"japanese":"小学校で分数の計算を習った。","romaji":"Shōgakkō de bunsū no keisan o naratta.","german":"I learned how to calculate with fractions in elementary school.","furigana":"しょうがっこう|ぶんすう|けいさん|なら"},{"japanese":"分数を小数に直してください。","romaji":"Bunsū o shōsū ni naoshite kudasai.","german":"Please convert the fraction into a decimal.","furigana":"ぶんすう|しょうすう|なお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Pens, notebooks, erasers, etc.: 文房具屋 (stationery shop).","examples":[{"japanese":"新学期の前に文房具をそろえた。","romaji":"Shingakki no mae ni bunbōgu o soroeta.","german":"I got my stationery ready before the new school term.","furigana":"しんがっき|まえ|ぶんぼうぐ"},{"japanese":"日本の文房具は外国人にも人気がある。","romaji":"Nihon no bunbōgu wa gaikokujin ni mo ninki ga aru.","german":"Japanese stationery is popular with foreigners too.","furigana":"にほん|ぶんぼうぐ|がいこくじん|にんき"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The amount of ingredients or work: 砂糖の分量, 仕事の分量. Similar: 量.","examples":[{"japanese":"レシピの分量どおりに作ったら、うまくできた。","romaji":"Reshipi no bunryō dōri ni tsukuttara, umaku dekita.","german":"When I followed the amounts in the recipe exactly, it turned out well.","furigana":"ぶんりょう|つく"},{"japanese":"薬は決められた分量を守って飲んでください。","romaji":"Kusuri wa kimerareta bunryō o mamotte nonde kudasai.","german":"Please take the medicine in exactly the prescribed dose.","furigana":"くすり|き|ぶんりょう|まも|の"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Official end of an event: 閉会式 (closing ceremony), 閉会の挨拶. Opposite: 開会.","examples":[{"japanese":"オリンピックの閉会式をテレビで見た。","romaji":"Orinpikku no heikaishiki o terebi de mita.","german":"I watched the closing ceremony of the Olympics on TV.","furigana":"へいかいしき|み"},{"japanese":"会長のあいさつで大会は閉会した。","romaji":"Kaichō no aisatsu de taikai wa heikai shita.","german":"The tournament was closed with a speech by the chairperson.","furigana":"かいちょう|たいかい|へいかい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Navel; idiom: へそを曲げる (to get in a huff). The kanji 臍 is rare.","examples":[{"japanese":"赤ちゃんのへそをきれいにふいた。","romaji":"Akachan no heso o kirei ni fuita.","german":"I gently wiped the baby's navel clean.","furigana":"あか"},{"japanese":"日本では「雷がなるとへそを取られる」と子どもに言う。","romaji":"Nihon de wa \"kaminari ga naru to heso o torareru\" to kodomo ni iu.","german":"In Japan, children are told, \"If thunder rumbles, your belly button will be taken.\"","furigana":"にほん|かみなり|と|こ|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Prefix meaning \"against, anti-\": 反社会的 (antisocial), 反戦 (anti-war), 反比例 (inversely proportional).","examples":[{"japanese":"反戦のデモに多くの人が参加した。","romaji":"Hansen no demo ni ōku no hito ga sanka shita.","german":"Many people took part in the anti-war demonstration.","furigana":"はんせん|おお|ひと|さんか"},{"japanese":"そんな反社会的な行為は許されない。","romaji":"Sonna hanshakaiteki na kōi wa yurusarenai.","german":"Such antisocial behavior cannot be tolerated.","furigana":"はんしゃかいてき|こうい|ゆる"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Prefix for deputies and secondary things: 副社長 (company vice president), 副作用 (side effect), 副業 (side job).","examples":[{"japanese":"会議には副社長が出席した。","romaji":"Kaigi ni wa fukushachō ga shusseki shita.","german":"The vice president of the company attended the meeting.","furigana":"かいぎ|ふくしゃちょう|しゅっせき"},{"japanese":"この薬には副作用がほとんどない。","romaji":"Kono kusuri ni wa fukusayō ga hotondo nai.","german":"This medicine has almost no side effects.","furigana":"くすり|ふくさよう"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"非 is mainly a negating prefix before Sino-Japanese nouns: 非常識 \"lacking common sense, inconsiderate\", 非公式 \"unofficial\", 非喫煙者 \"non-smoker\". Unlike 不 or 無, which prefix fits depends on the individual word (不可能, 無責任, 非常識), so it has to be learned along with the word. As a standalone noun, 非 means \"fault, wrong\" and appears in fixed expressions such as 自分の非を認める \"to admit one's own fault\" or 非の打ち所がない \"flawless, impeccable\".","examples":[{"japanese":"夜中に電話するなんて非常識だ。","romaji":"Yonaka ni denwa suru nante hijōshiki da.","german":"Calling in the middle of the night is thoughtless.","furigana":"よなか|でんわ|ひじょうしき"},{"japanese":"両国の首相が非公式に会談した。","romaji":"Ryōkoku no shushō ga hikōshiki ni kaidan shita.","german":"The prime ministers of the two countries met informally.","furigana":"りょうこく|しゅしょう|ひこうしき|かいだん"},{"japanese":"彼はすぐに自分の非を認めて謝った。","romaji":"Kare wa sugu ni jibun no hi o mitomete ayamatta.","german":"He immediately admitted his own fault and apologized.","furigana":"かれ|じぶん|ひ|みと|あやま"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A colloquial conjunction at the beginning of a sentence used to add something: \"and one more thing\". Not to be confused with それとも (\"or\" in questions).","examples":[{"japanese":"コーヒーを二つください。それと、ケーキも一つ。","romaji":"Kōhī o futatsu kudasai. Sore to, kēki mo hitotsu.","german":"Two coffees, please. And also a piece of cake.","furigana":"ふた|ひと"},{"japanese":"明日は八時集合です。それと、お弁当を忘れないでください。","romaji":"Ashita wa hachiji shūgō desu. Sore to, obentō o wasurenaide kudasai.","german":"We meet at eight tomorrow. Also, don't forget your lunch box.","furigana":"あした|はちじしゅうごう|べんとう|わす"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Introduces a sentence that contradicts what was expected, often with disappointment or reproach: \"And yet...!\" More neutral: それでも.","examples":[{"japanese":"一生懸命勉強した。それなのに、試験に落ちてしまった。","romaji":"Isshōkenmei benkyō shita. Sore na noni, shiken ni ochite shimatta.","german":"I studied as hard as I could. And yet, I failed the exam.","furigana":"いっしょうけんめいべんきょう|しけん|お"},{"japanese":"彼は約束した。それなのに、また遅れて来た。","romaji":"Kare wa yakusoku shita. Sore na noni, mata okurete kita.","german":"He promised. And yet, he came late again.","furigana":"かれ|やくそく|おく|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Draws a conclusion from what the other person has just said. In casual speech also じゃあ or それじゃ.","examples":[{"japanese":"「雨が降りそうです。」「それなら、傘を持って行きましょう。」","romaji":"\"Ame ga furisō desu.\" \"Sore nara, kasa o motte ikimashō.\"","german":"\"It looks like rain.\" \"In that case, let's take an umbrella.\"","furigana":"あめ|ふ|かさ|も|い"},{"japanese":"時間がないの？それなら、また今度にしよう。","romaji":"Jikan ga nai no? Sore nara, mata kondo ni shiyō.","german":"You don't have time? Then let's do it another time.","furigana":"じかん|こんど"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 35"},
{"notes":"Tire of a car or bicycle. The spelling タイア is rare. Flat tire: パンク.","examples":[{"japanese":"冬になる前にタイヤを替えた。","romaji":"Fuyu ni naru mae ni taiya o kaeta.","german":"I changed the tires before winter came.","furigana":"ふゆ|まえ|か"},{"japanese":"自転車のタイヤに空気を入れてください。","romaji":"Jitensha no taiya ni kūki o irete kudasai.","german":"Please put air in the bicycle tire.","furigana":"じてんしゃ|くうき|い"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 23"},
{"notes":"In general a diagram, in railways the train schedule diagram; in everyday speech usually shortened to ダイヤ (\"timetable\": ダイヤが乱れる).","examples":[{"japanese":"鉄道会社はダイヤグラムを見て列車の間隔を決める。","romaji":"Tetsudō gaisha wa daiyaguramu o mite ressha no kankaku o kimeru.","german":"The railway company decides the intervals between trains by looking at the schedule diagram.","furigana":"てつどうがいしゃ|み|れっしゃ|かんかく|き"},{"japanese":"この本には工程を表すダイヤグラムが載っている。","romaji":"Kono hon ni wa kōtei o arawasu daiyaguramu ga notte iru.","german":"This book contains a diagram showing the work process.","furigana":"ほん|こうてい|あらわ|の"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Almost always with a negative: 大して難しくない (\"not particularly difficult\"). Similar: それほど, あまり.","examples":[{"japanese":"この映画は大して面白くなかった。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa taishite omoshirokunakatta.","german":"This movie wasn't particularly interesting.","furigana":"えいが|たい|おもしろ"},{"japanese":"今日は大して寒くないから、コートはいらない。","romaji":"Kyō wa taishite samukunai kara, kōto wa iranai.","german":"It's not particularly cold today, so I don't need a coat.","furigana":"きょう|たい|さむ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Large and small things together, or size itself: 大小の箱 (boxes of every size), 大小を問わず (regardless of size).","examples":[{"japanese":"大小さまざまな石が庭に並んでいる。","romaji":"Daishō samazama na ishi ga niwa ni narande iru.","german":"Stones of all sizes are lined up in the garden.","furigana":"だいしょう|いし|にわ|なら"},{"japanese":"会社の大小にかかわらず、ルールは同じだ。","romaji":"Kaisha no daishō ni kakawarazu, rūru wa onaji da.","german":"Regardless of the size of the company, the rules are the same.","furigana":"かいしゃ|だいしょう|おな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"As an adverb, formal \"very\" (大層喜ぶ); as a な-adjective \"exaggerated, pompous\" (大層なことを言う).","examples":[{"japanese":"祖母は孫の手紙を大層喜んだ。","romaji":"Sobo wa mago no tegami o taisō yorokonda.","german":"Grandmother was very pleased with her grandchild's letter.","furigana":"そぼ|まご|てがみ|たいそうよろこ"},{"japanese":"そんな大層な話ではありません。","romaji":"Sonna taisō na hanashi de wa arimasen.","german":"It's not such a big deal.","furigana":"たいそう|はなし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A large, old tree. Colloquially also 大きな木.","examples":[{"japanese":"神社の境内に大木が立っている。","romaji":"Jinja no keidai ni taiboku ga tatte iru.","german":"A huge tree stands on the shrine grounds.","furigana":"じんじゃ|けいだい|たいぼく|た"},{"japanese":"台風で大木が倒れた。","romaji":"Taifū de taiboku ga taoreta.","german":"A large tree fell down in the typhoon.","furigana":"たいふう|たいぼく|たお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Title of a book, film or song. Similar: タイトル; also shortened to 題.","examples":[{"japanese":"この本の題名を思い出せない。","romaji":"Kono hon no daimei o omoidasenai.","german":"I can't remember the title of this book.","furigana":"ほん|だいめい|おも|だ"},{"japanese":"作文に題名をつけてください。","romaji":"Sakubun ni daimei o tsukete kudasai.","german":"Please give your essays a title.","furigana":"さくぶん|だいめい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Grammatical term: words like 私, 彼, これ. Figuratively also \"synonym, byword\": 富士山は日本の代名詞だ.","examples":[{"japanese":"「彼」や「これ」は代名詞です。","romaji":"\"Kare\" ya \"kore\" wa daimeishi desu.","german":"\"He\" and \"this\" are pronouns.","furigana":"かれ|だいめいし"},{"japanese":"この町の名前は、今では温泉の代名詞になっている。","romaji":"Kono machi no namae wa, ima de wa onsen no daimeishi ni natte iru.","german":"The name of this town has now become synonymous with hot springs.","furigana":"まち|なまえ|いま|おんせん|だいめいし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The gemstone; often shortened to ダイヤ (ダイヤの指輪). However, ダイヤ also means \"timetable\".","examples":[{"japanese":"ダイヤモンドは一番硬い石だ。","romaji":"Daiyamondo wa ichiban katai ishi da.","german":"Diamond is the hardest stone.","furigana":"いちばんかた|いし"},{"japanese":"母はダイヤモンドの指輪を大切にしている。","romaji":"Haha wa daiyamondo no yubiwa o taisetsu ni shite iru.","german":"My mother treasures her diamond ring.","furigana":"はは|ゆびわ|たいせつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A rotating knob on devices (ダイヤルを回す) and the dial of old telephones; as a verb ダイヤルする \"to dial a number\".","examples":[{"japanese":"ダイヤルを回して音量を上げた。","romaji":"Daiyaru o mawashite onryō o ageta.","german":"I turned the knob to raise the volume.","furigana":"まわ|おんりょう|あ"},{"japanese":"祖父の家にはダイヤル式の電話がある。","romaji":"Sofu no ie ni wa daiyarushiki no denwa ga aru.","german":"My grandfather's house has a rotary-dial telephone.","furigana":"そふ|いえ|しき|でんわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Transplanting rice seedlings into the flooded field, usually in May or June.","examples":[{"japanese":"五月になると、村では田植えが始まる。","romaji":"Gogatsu ni naru to, mura de wa taue ga hajimaru.","german":"When May comes, rice planting begins in the village.","furigana":"ごがつ|むら|たう|はじ"},{"japanese":"子どもたちが田植えを体験した。","romaji":"Kodomotachi ga taue o taiken shita.","german":"The children tried rice planting for themselves.","furigana":"こ|たう|たいけん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A geometric shape; \"oval\" as a property: 楕円形.","examples":[{"japanese":"テーブルは楕円の形をしている。","romaji":"Tēburu wa daen no katachi o shite iru.","german":"The table is oval-shaped.","furigana":"だえん|かたち"},{"japanese":"地球は太陽の周りを楕円を描いて回っている。","romaji":"Chikyū wa taiyō no mawari o daen o egaite mawatte iru.","german":"The Earth orbits the Sun in an elliptical path.","furigana":"ちきゅう|たいよう|まわ|だえん|えが|まわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Transitive: 火を焚く (to make a fire), 落ち葉を焚く. Homophone: 炊く (to cook rice).","examples":[{"japanese":"キャンプで火を焚いて、みんなで温まった。","romaji":"Kyanpu de hi o taite, minna de atatamatta.","german":"At the campsite we made a fire and everyone warmed up.","furigana":"ひ|た|あたた"},{"japanese":"昔はまきを焚いて風呂を沸かした。","romaji":"Mukashi wa maki o taite furo o wakashita.","german":"In the old days, bath water was heated by burning firewood.","furigana":"むかし|た|ふろ|わ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The plant and the material: 竹林 (bamboo grove), 竹の子 (bamboo shoots). In the 松・竹・梅 ranking, the middle grade.","examples":[{"japanese":"この籠は竹で作られている。","romaji":"Kono kago wa take de tsukurarete iru.","german":"This basket is made of bamboo.","furigana":"かご|たけ|つく"},{"japanese":"竹は一日に一メートル以上伸びることもある。","romaji":"Take wa ichinichi ni ichi mētoru ijō nobiru koto mo aru.","german":"Bamboo can sometimes grow more than a meter a day.","furigana":"たけ|いちにち|いち|いじょうの"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"To leave a place in order to travel: 成田を発つ. Homophones: 立つ (to stand), 建つ (to be built).","examples":[{"japanese":"明日の朝、東京を発ちます。","romaji":"Ashita no asa, Tōkyō o tachimasu.","german":"I will leave Tokyo tomorrow morning.","furigana":"あした|あさ|とうきょう|た"},{"japanese":"彼は留学のために日本を発った。","romaji":"Kare wa ryūgaku no tame ni Nihon o tatta.","german":"He left Japan to study abroad.","furigana":"かれ|りゅうがく|にほん|た"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"To describe something with an image: AをBに例える (to compare A to B). Noun: 例え (comparison, parable).","examples":[{"japanese":"人生はよく旅に例えられる。","romaji":"Jinsei wa yoku tabi ni tatoerareru.","german":"Life is often compared to a journey.","furigana":"じんせい|たび|たと"},{"japanese":"この気持ちを何に例えたらいいか分からない。","romaji":"Kono kimochi o nani ni tatoetara ii ka wakaranai.","german":"I don't know what to compare this feeling to.","furigana":"きも|なに|たと|わ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Double...: ダブルベッド, ダブルの部屋 (double room with one bed). The verb ダブる (\"to overlap\") is a separate word.","examples":[{"japanese":"ホテルでダブルの部屋を予約した。","romaji":"Hoteru de daburu no heya o yoyaku shita.","german":"I booked a double room at the hotel.","furigana":"へや|よやく"},{"japanese":"今日はポイントがダブルになる日だ。","romaji":"Kyō wa pointo ga daburu ni naru hi da.","german":"Today is a day when points are doubled.","furigana":"きょう|ひ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A colloquial godan verb from ダブル: appointments overlap (予定がダブる), something exists twice; also \"to repeat a school year\".","examples":[{"japanese":"会議と歯医者の予約がダブってしまった。","romaji":"Kaigi to haisha no yoyaku ga dabutte shimatta.","german":"The meeting and my dentist appointment ended up overlapping.","furigana":"かいぎ|はいしゃ|よやく"},{"japanese":"同じ本を二冊買ってしまい、ダブった。","romaji":"Onaji hon o nisatsu katte shimai, dabutta.","german":"I accidentally bought two copies of the same book, so I have a duplicate.","furigana":"おな|ほん|にさつか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A wall built to store water or generate electricity. The reservoir is called ダム湖.","examples":[{"japanese":"このダムは町に水を送っている。","romaji":"Kono damu wa machi ni mizu o okutte iru.","german":"This dam supplies water to the town.","furigana":"まち|みず|おく"},{"japanese":"雨が少なくて、ダムの水が減っている。","romaji":"Ame ga sukunakute, damu no mizu ga hette iru.","german":"Because there has been little rain, the water in the dam is decreasing.","furigana":"あめ|すく|みず|へ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"\"To sigh\": ため息をつく. A sigh of relief is ほっとため息をつく.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は大きなため息をついた。","romaji":"Kare wa ōki na tameiki o tsuita.","german":"He heaved a big sigh.","furigana":"かれ|おお|いき"},{"japanese":"試験が終わって、ほっとため息が出た。","romaji":"Shiken ga owatte, hotto tameiki ga deta.","german":"The exam was over, and I let out a sigh of relief.","furigana":"しけん|お|いき|で"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Literary; mostly 〜に足る (\"worthy, worth\": 信頼に足る人) or in the negative 取るに足らない (\"insignificant\"). In everyday speech, 足りる is used.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は信頼するに足る人物だ。","romaji":"Kare wa shinrai suru ni taru jinbutsu da.","german":"He is a person worthy of trust.","furigana":"かれ|しんらい|た|じんぶつ"},{"japanese":"それは取るに足らない問題だ。","romaji":"Sore wa toru ni taranai mondai da.","german":"That is a trivial problem.","furigana":"と|た|もんだい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"When no tap water flows, for example during construction work or after an earthquake; as a verb 断水する.","examples":[{"japanese":"工事のため、明日は午前中断水します。","romaji":"Kōji no tame, ashita wa gozenchū dansui shimasu.","german":"Because of construction work, the water will be off tomorrow morning.","furigana":"こうじ|あした|ごぜんちゅうだんすい"},{"japanese":"地震のあと、町は一週間断水が続いた。","romaji":"Jishin no ato, machi wa isshūkan dansui ga tsuzuita.","german":"After the earthquake, the town had no water for a week.","furigana":"じしん|まち|いっしゅうかんだんすい|つづ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Grammatical term. Opposite: 複数 (plural). Japanese nouns usually do not distinguish singular and plural.","examples":[{"japanese":"英語では単数と複数を区別する。","romaji":"Eigo de wa tansū to fukusū o kubetsu suru.","german":"English distinguishes between singular and plural.","furigana":"えいご|たんすう|ふくすう|くべつ"},{"japanese":"この名詞は単数の形で使います。","romaji":"Kono meishi wa tansū no katachi de tsukaimasu.","german":"This noun is used in the singular form.","furigana":"めいし|たんすう|かたち|つか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A large residential area with uniform apartment blocks, often publicly built.","examples":[{"japanese":"私は子どもの頃、団地に住んでいた。","romaji":"Watashi wa kodomo no koro, danchi ni sunde ita.","german":"I lived in a housing complex when I was a child.","furigana":"わたし|こ|ころ|だんち|す"},{"japanese":"この団地には公園と小さなスーパーがある。","romaji":"Kono danchi ni wa kōen to chiisa na sūpā ga aru.","german":"This housing complex has a park and a small supermarket.","furigana":"だんち|こうえん|ちい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Transitive counterpart of 近づく (to approach): to bring something closer, or to bring something nearer to a goal.","examples":[{"japanese":"顔を画面に近づけて、小さい字を読んだ。","romaji":"Kao o gamen ni chikazukete, chiisai ji o yonda.","german":"I brought my face close to the screen and read the small print.","furigana":"かお|がめん|ちか|ちい|じ|よ"},{"japanese":"新しい技術が夢を現実に近づけた。","romaji":"Atarashii gijutsu ga yume o genjitsu ni chikazuketa.","german":"New technology has brought the dream closer to reality.","furigana":"あたら|ぎじゅつ|ゆめ|げんじつ|ちか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Intransitive: to go close to a person or thing, often negated as a warning: 近寄らないでください.","examples":[{"japanese":"危ないから、機械に近寄らないでください。","romaji":"Abunai kara, kikai ni chikayoranaide kudasai.","german":"It's dangerous, so please do not go near the machine.","furigana":"あぶ|きかい|ちかよ"},{"japanese":"猫は人が近寄ると逃げていった。","romaji":"Neko wa hito ga chikayoru to nigete itta.","german":"When someone came near, the cat ran away.","furigana":"ねこ|ひと|ちかよ|に"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Full of strength (力強い声) or encouraging (君がいると力強い = I feel safe with you around).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は力強い声で歌った。","romaji":"Kanojo wa chikarazuyoi koe de utatta.","german":"She sang in a powerful voice.","furigana":"かのじょ|ちからづよ|こえ|うた"},{"japanese":"先輩が一緒に来てくれるので、とても力強い。","romaji":"Senpai ga issho ni kite kureru node, totemo chikarazuyoi.","german":"Since my senior colleague is coming along, I feel very reassured.","furigana":"せんぱい|いっしょ|き|ちからづよ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Transitive counterpart of 縮む (to shrink): 時間を縮める, 差を縮める (to narrow the gap), 首を縮める (to pull in one's head).","examples":[{"japanese":"練習でタイムを五秒縮めた。","romaji":"Renshū de taimu o gobyō chijimeta.","german":"Through practice, I cut five seconds off my time.","furigana":"れんしゅう|ごびょうちぢ"},{"japanese":"寒くて、思わず首を縮めた。","romaji":"Samukute, omowazu kubi o chijimeta.","german":"It was so cold that I instinctively pulled my head into my shoulders.","furigana":"さむ|おも|くび|ちぢ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Of hair or cloth: to become curly. Adjectival use: 縮れた髪 (curly hair), 縮れ麺 (wavy noodles).","examples":[{"japanese":"雨の日は髪が縮れてしまう。","romaji":"Ame no hi wa kami ga chijirete shimau.","german":"On rainy days my hair gets frizzy.","furigana":"あめ|ひ|かみ|ちぢ"},{"japanese":"このラーメンは縮れた麺を使っている。","romaji":"Kono rāmen wa chijireta men o tsukatte iru.","german":"These ramen are made with wavy noodles.","furigana":"ちぢ|めん|つか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A tip; in Japan it is uncommon and is often even refused. Homophone: チップ (computer chip, gambling chip); potato chips are called ポテトチップス.","examples":[{"japanese":"日本のレストランではチップを払う必要はない。","romaji":"Nihon no resutoran de wa chippu o harau hitsuyō wa nai.","german":"You don't need to pay a tip at restaurants in Japan.","furigana":"にほん|はら|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"アメリカのレストランでは、チップを払うのが普通だ。","romaji":"Amerika no resutoran de wa, chippu o harau no ga futsū da.","german":"In American restaurants, it is normal to pay a tip.","furigana":"はら|ふつう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A specific point on the terrain or on a map: 出発地点 (starting point), 折り返し地点 (turnaround point).","examples":[{"japanese":"事故はこの地点で起きた。","romaji":"Jiko wa kono chiten de okita.","german":"The accident happened at this spot.","furigana":"じこ|ちてん|お"},{"japanese":"マラソンの折り返し地点に着いた。","romaji":"Marason no orikaeshi chiten ni tsuita.","german":"I reached the turnaround point of the marathon.","furigana":"お|かえ|ちてん|つ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The name of a place. Many Japanese place names have unusual kanji readings.","examples":[{"japanese":"この地名は読み方が難しい。","romaji":"Kono chimei wa yomikata ga muzukashii.","german":"This place name is hard to read.","furigana":"ちめい|よ|かた|むずか"},{"japanese":"地名にはその土地の歴史が残っている。","romaji":"Chimei ni wa sono tochi no rekishi ga nokotte iru.","german":"The history of a place lives on in its place name.","furigana":"ちめい|とち|れきし|のこ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"い-adjective from 茶色 (brown): 茶色い靴. Before nouns also 茶色の.","examples":[{"japanese":"茶色い犬が公園を走っている。","romaji":"Chairoi inu ga kōen o hashitte iru.","german":"A brown dog is running through the park.","furigana":"ちゃいろ|いぬ|こうえん|はし"},{"japanese":"秋になって、葉っぱが茶色くなった。","romaji":"Aki ni natte, happa ga chairoku natta.","german":"With autumn, the leaves have turned brown.","furigana":"あき|は|ちゃいろ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The middle between two points or times: 中間テスト (midterm exam), 中間地点.","examples":[{"japanese":"駅と学校の中間にコンビニがある。","romaji":"Eki to gakkō no chūkan ni konbini ga aru.","german":"There is a convenience store halfway between the station and the school.","furigana":"えき|がっこう|ちゅうかん"},{"japanese":"二人の意見の中間をとることにした。","romaji":"Futari no iken no chūkan o toru koto ni shita.","german":"We decided to take a middle ground between the two opinions.","furigana":"ふたり|いけん|ちゅうかん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"In der Chemie weder sauer noch alkalisch (中性洗剤), in der Grammatik das Neutrum, bei Kleidung oder Aussehen geschlechtsneutral.","examples":[{"japanese":"この洗剤は中性なので、手に優しい。","romaji":"Kono senzai wa chūsei na node, te ni yasashii.","german":"Dieses Spülmittel ist pH-neutral und schont die Hände.","furigana":"せんざい|ちゅうせい|て|やさ"},{"japanese":"ドイツ語の名詞には男性、女性、中性がある。","romaji":"Doitsugo no meishi ni wa dansei, josei, chūsei ga aru.","german":"Deutsche Nomen sind maskulin, feminin oder neutral.","furigana":"ご|めいし|だんせい|じょせい|ちゅうせい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Mostly in compounds: 中途半端 (half-hearted, unfinished), 中途採用 (hiring of experienced workers outside the new-graduate recruitment cycle), 中途で (halfway).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は大学を中途でやめた。","romaji":"Kare wa daigaku o chūto de yameta.","german":"He dropped out of university partway through.","furigana":"かれ|だいがく|ちゅうと"},{"japanese":"仕事を中途で投げ出してはいけない。","romaji":"Shigoto o chūto de nagedashite wa ikenai.","german":"You must not abandon a job halfway.","furigana":"しごと|ちゅうと|な|だ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Exceeding a limit: 時間超過, 重量超過 (excess weight of luggage); as a verb 超過する.","examples":[{"japanese":"荷物が重量を超過していて、追加料金を払った。","romaji":"Nimotsu ga jūryō o chōka shite ite, tsuika ryōkin o haratta.","german":"My luggage was over the weight limit, so I paid an extra fee.","furigana":"にもつ|じゅうりょう|ちょうか|ついかりょうきん|はら"},{"japanese":"会議は予定の時間を三十分超過した。","romaji":"Kaigi wa yotei no jikan o sanjuppun chōka shita.","german":"The meeting ran thirty minutes over the scheduled time.","furigana":"かいぎ|よてい|じかん|さんじゅっぷんちょうか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The firstborn daughter; eldest son: 長男, second daughter: 次女.","examples":[{"japanese":"田中さんの長女は来年結婚するそうだ。","romaji":"Tanaka-san no chōjo wa rainen kekkon suru sō da.","german":"Mr. Tanaka's eldest daughter is said to be getting married next year.","furigana":"たなか|ちょうじょ|らいねんけっこん"},{"japanese":"私は三人姉妹の長女です。","romaji":"Watashi wa sannin shimai no chōjo desu.","german":"I am the eldest of three sisters.","furigana":"わたし|さんにんしまい|ちょうじょ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Literally \"long and short\": length (音の長短) or strengths and weaknesses (それぞれに長短がある).","examples":[{"japanese":"どの方法にも長短がある。","romaji":"Dono hōhō ni mo chōtan ga aru.","german":"Every method has its pros and cons.","furigana":"ほうほう|ちょうたん"},{"japanese":"日本語では音の長短で意味が変わる。","romaji":"Nihongo de wa oto no chōtan de imi ga kawaru.","german":"In Japanese, the length of a sound changes the meaning.","furigana":"にほんご|おと|ちょうたん|いみ|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The highest point: 人気の頂点 (peak of popularity); in geometry, the corner of a triangle.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は二十代で人気の頂点に立った。","romaji":"Kare wa nijūdai de ninki no chōten ni tatta.","german":"He reached the peak of his popularity in his twenties.","furigana":"かれ|にじゅうだい|にんき|ちょうてん|た"},{"japanese":"三角形には頂点が三つある。","romaji":"Sankakukei ni wa chōten ga mittsu aru.","german":"A triangle has three vertices.","furigana":"さんかくけい|ちょうてん|みっ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A geometric shape; square: 正方形.","examples":[{"japanese":"長方形の面積を計算しなさい。","romaji":"Chōhōkei no menseki o keisan shinasai.","german":"Calculate the area of the rectangle.","furigana":"ちょうほうけい|めんせき|けいさん"},{"japanese":"部屋は細長い長方形だ。","romaji":"Heya wa hosonagai chōhōkei da.","german":"The room is a long, narrow rectangle.","furigana":"へや|ほそなが|ちょうほうけい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Writing chalk for the blackboard (黒板).","examples":[{"japanese":"先生はチョークで黒板に字を書いた。","romaji":"Sensei wa chōku de kokuban ni ji o kaita.","german":"The teacher wrote on the blackboard with chalk.","furigana":"せんせい|こくばん|じ|か"},{"japanese":"子どもたちが道にチョークで絵を描いている。","romaji":"Kodomotachi ga michi ni chōku de e o kaite iru.","german":"The children are drawing pictures on the road with chalk.","furigana":"こ|みち|え|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Without transfer or switchboard: 直通電車 (through train), 直通電話 (direct line).","examples":[{"japanese":"この電車は空港まで直通です。","romaji":"Kono densha wa kūkō made chokutsū desu.","german":"This train goes directly to the airport.","furigana":"でんしゃ|くうこう|ちょくつう"},{"japanese":"部長の直通の番号を教えてください。","romaji":"Buchō no chokutsū no bangō o oshiete kudasai.","german":"Please give me the manager's direct number.","furigana":"ぶちょう|ちょくつう|ばんごう|おし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Electrical direct current (DC); alternating current is 交流.","examples":[{"japanese":"電池は直流の電気を流す。","romaji":"Denchi wa chokuryū no denki o nagasu.","german":"Batteries supply direct current.","furigana":"でんち|ちょくりゅう|でんき|なが"},{"japanese":"この機械は直流でも交流でも使える。","romaji":"Kono kikai wa chokuryū demo kōryū demo tsukaeru.","german":"This device can be used with either direct or alternating current.","furigana":"きかい|ちょくりゅう|こうりゅう|つか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Keeping supplies in storage: 食料を貯蔵する, 貯蔵庫 (storeroom).","examples":[{"japanese":"冬に備えて野菜を貯蔵する。","romaji":"Fuyu ni sonaete yasai o chozō suru.","german":"We store vegetables in preparation for winter.","furigana":"ふゆ|そな|やさい|ちょぞう"},{"japanese":"この倉庫では米を低温で貯蔵している。","romaji":"Kono sōko de wa kome o teion de chozō shite iru.","german":"This warehouse stores rice at low temperatures.","furigana":"そうこ|こめ|ていおん|ちょぞう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"An angle of 90 degrees; in everyday use also \"to turn at a right angle\": 直角に曲がる.","examples":[{"japanese":"正方形の角はすべて直角だ。","romaji":"Seihōkei no kado wa subete chokkaku da.","german":"All the corners of a square are right angles.","furigana":"せいほうけい|かど|ちょっかく"},{"japanese":"道はそこで直角に曲がっている。","romaji":"Michi wa soko de chokkaku ni magatte iru.","german":"The road turns at a right angle there.","furigana":"みち|ちょっかく|ま"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Intransitive: 部屋が散らかっている (the room is messy). Transitive: 散らかす (to make a mess).","examples":[{"japanese":"散らかっていますが、どうぞ入ってください。","romaji":"Chirakatte imasu ga, dōzo haitte kudasai.","german":"It's a bit messy, but please come in.","furigana":"ち|はい"},{"japanese":"机の上に書類が散らかっている。","romaji":"Tsukue no ue ni shorui ga chirakatte iru.","german":"Documents are scattered on the desk.","furigana":"つくえ|うえ|しょるい|ち"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Transitive: to scatter something (花びらを散らす) or to disperse (気を散らす = to get distracted).","examples":[{"japanese":"風が桜の花びらを散らした。","romaji":"Kaze ga sakura no hanabira o chirashita.","german":"The wind scattered the cherry blossom petals.","furigana":"かぜ|さくら|はな|ち"},{"japanese":"仕上げにねぎを散らしてください。","romaji":"Shiage ni negi o chirashite kudasai.","german":"Please sprinkle green onions on top to finish.","furigana":"しあ|ち"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Plain paper for blowing one's nose or as toilet paper; today usually ティッシュ.","examples":[{"japanese":"ちり紙で鼻をかんだ。","romaji":"Chirigami de hana o kanda.","german":"I blew my nose with a tissue.","furigana":"がみ|はな"},{"japanese":"昔はトイレにちり紙が置いてあった。","romaji":"Mukashi wa toire ni chirigami ga oite atta.","german":"In the old days, plain paper was kept in the toilet.","furigana":"むかし|がみ|お"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The opportunity to take care of something while doing something else: ついでの時に (when it happens to be convenient). For the grammar pattern 〜ついでに, see the grammar section.","examples":[{"japanese":"ついでの時に、この本を返してください。","romaji":"Tsuide no toki ni, kono hon o kaeshite kudasai.","german":"Please return this book when you get the chance.","furigana":"とき|ほん|かえ"},{"japanese":"駅まで行くなら、ついでがあるので一緒に行きます。","romaji":"Eki made iku nara, tsuide ga aru node issho ni ikimasu.","german":"If you're going to the station, I'll come along, since it's on my way anyway.","furigana":"えき|い|いっしょ|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"To be generally accepted or to work: 英語が通用する (English is understood), 世界で通用する (to hold up worldwide).","examples":[{"japanese":"この国では英語があまり通用しない。","romaji":"Kono kuni de wa eigo ga amari tsūyō shinai.","german":"English is not widely understood in this country.","furigana":"くに|えいご|つうよう"},{"japanese":"そんな言い訳は通用しないよ。","romaji":"Sonna iiwake wa tsūyō shinai yo.","german":"That kind of excuse won't work.","furigana":"い|わけ|つうよう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"An assignment to fetch or deliver something, and the person who carries it out. Polite/childish: お使い (errand).","examples":[{"japanese":"部長の使いで、書類を届けに来ました。","romaji":"Buchō no tsukai de, shorui o todoke ni kimashita.","german":"I've come on the department head's errand to deliver some documents.","furigana":"ぶちょう|つか|しょるい|とど|き"},{"japanese":"会社から使いの者が来た。","romaji":"Kaisha kara tsukai no mono ga kita.","german":"A messenger came from the company.","furigana":"かいしゃ|つか|もの|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The place where a road or corridor ends, typical in directions: 突き当たりを右に.","examples":[{"japanese":"トイレは廊下の突き当たりにあります。","romaji":"Toire wa rōka no tsukiatari ni arimasu.","german":"The restroom is at the end of the corridor.","furigana":"ろうか|つ|あ"},{"japanese":"この道の突き当たりを左に曲がってください。","romaji":"Kono michi no tsukiatari o hidari ni magatte kudasai.","german":"Please turn left at the end of this road.","furigana":"みち|つ|あ|ひだり|ま"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"To go to the end of a road (道に突き当たる) or to hit an obstacle (問題に突き当たる).","examples":[{"japanese":"まっすぐ行くと、大きな通りに突き当たります。","romaji":"Massugu iku to, ōki na tōri ni tsukiatarimasu.","german":"If you go straight, you will come to a big street.","furigana":"い|おお|とお|つ|あ"},{"japanese":"研究は大きな問題に突き当たった。","romaji":"Kenkyū wa ōki na mondai ni tsukiatatta.","german":"The research has run into a major problem.","furigana":"けんきゅう|おお|もんだい|つ|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The time that has passed, often with feeling: 月日が経つのは早い (time passes quickly). The reading がっぴ means \"date\".","examples":[{"japanese":"月日が経つのは本当に早い。","romaji":"Tsukihi ga tatsu no wa hontō ni hayai.","german":"Time really flies.","furigana":"つきひ|た|ほんとう|はや"},{"japanese":"あれから長い月日が流れた。","romaji":"Are kara nagai tsukihi ga nagareta.","german":"A long time has passed since then.","furigana":"なが|つきひ|なが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Rank or order: 東京に次ぐ大都市圏 (the largest metropolitan area after Tokyo). Homophones: 継ぐ (to inherit, to continue), 注ぐ (to pour).","examples":[{"japanese":"大阪は東京に次ぐ大都市圏だ。","romaji":"Ōsaka wa Tōkyō ni tsugu daitoshiken da.","german":"The Osaka area is the largest metropolitan area after Tokyo.","furigana":"おおさか|とうきょう|つ|だいとしけん"},{"japanese":"イギリスに次いで、フランスでも同じ法律ができた。","romaji":"Igirisu ni tsuide, Furansu demo onaji hōritsu ga dekita.","german":"After Britain, France also enacted the same law.","furigana":"つ|おな|ほうりつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"To stuff something in (ポケットに手を突っ込む), to crash into (車が店に突っ込む) or to question critically. In comedy, the partner's retort (ツッコミ).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼はポケットに手を突っ込んで歩いていた。","romaji":"Kare wa poketto ni te o tsukkonde aruite ita.","german":"He was walking with his hands stuffed in his pockets.","furigana":"かれ|て|つ|こ|ある"},{"japanese":"車が店に突っ込む事故があった。","romaji":"Kuruma ga mise ni tsukkomu jiko ga atta.","german":"There was an accident in which a car crashed into a shop.","furigana":"くるま|みせ|つ|こ|じこ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A thick rope: 綱引き (tug-of-war), 命綱 (lifeline).","examples":[{"japanese":"運動会の綱引きで、みんなで太い綱を引っ張った。","romaji":"Undōkai no tsunahiki de, minna de futoi tsuna o hippatta.","german":"At the sports day tug-of-war, we all pulled on the thick rope.","furigana":"うんどうかい|つなひ|ふと|つな|ひ|ぱ"},{"japanese":"船を綱で岸につないだ。","romaji":"Fune o tsuna de kishi ni tsunaida.","german":"We tied the boat to the shore with a rope.","furigana":"ふね|つな|きし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Usually written in kana つながり: 人とのつながり (relationships with people), 話のつながり (coherence of a story).","examples":[{"japanese":"地域の人との繋がりを大切にしている。","romaji":"Chiiki no hito to no tsunagari o taisetsu ni shite iru.","german":"I value my ties with the people in the community.","furigana":"ちいき|ひと|つな|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"この二つの事件には繋がりがあるようだ。","romaji":"Kono futatsu no jiken ni wa tsunagari ga aru yō da.","german":"There seems to be a connection between these two incidents.","furigana":"ふた|じけん|つな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Transitive: to connect two things (ケーブルを繋げる), also figuratively: 経験を次に繋げる. Often written in kana つなげる.","examples":[{"japanese":"パソコンをテレビに繋げて映画を見た。","romaji":"Pasokon o terebi ni tsunagete eiga o mita.","german":"I connected my computer to the TV and watched a movie.","furigana":"つな|えいが|み"},{"japanese":"この失敗を次の成功に繋げたい。","romaji":"Kono shippai o tsugi no seikō ni tsunagetai.","german":"I want to turn this failure into the next success.","furigana":"しっぱい|つぎ|せいこう|つな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A small round particle: 米の粒, 雨の粒. As a counter for grains: 一粒.","examples":[{"japanese":"ご飯の粒を一つも残さずに食べた。","romaji":"Gohan no tsubu o hitotsu mo nokosazu ni tabeta.","german":"I ate the rice without leaving a single grain.","furigana":"はん|つぶ|ひと|のこ|た"},{"japanese":"大きな雨の粒が窓に当たっている。","romaji":"Ōki na ame no tsubu ga mado ni atatte iru.","german":"Big raindrops are hitting the window.","furigana":"おお|あめ|つぶ|まど|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Shimmering gloss of a surface: 髪の艶, 艶のある声. Often written in kana つや.","examples":[{"japanese":"この家具は古いが、まだ艶がある。","romaji":"Kono kagu wa furui ga, mada tsuya ga aru.","german":"This furniture is old, but it still has a shine.","furigana":"かぐ|ふる|つや"},{"japanese":"彼女の髪は艶があってきれいだ。","romaji":"Kanojo no kami wa tsuya ga atte kirei da.","german":"Her hair is glossy and beautiful.","furigana":"かのじょ|かみ|つや"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Confident and determined, sometimes overconfident. Opposite: 弱気 (timid).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼はいつも強気な発言をする。","romaji":"Kare wa itsumo tsuyoki na hatsugen o suru.","german":"He always makes assertive statements.","furigana":"かれ|つよき|はつげん"},{"japanese":"試合の前は強気でいこう。","romaji":"Shiai no mae wa tsuyoki de ikō.","german":"Let's go in confident before the match.","furigana":"しあい|まえ|つよき"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"To keep balance (重さが釣り合う) or to suit each other (二人は釣り合っている).","examples":[{"japanese":"天秤の左右が釣り合った。","romaji":"Tenbin no sayū ga tsuriatta.","german":"The two sides of the scale balanced.","furigana":"|さゆう|つ|あ"},{"japanese":"この帽子は服とよく釣り合っている。","romaji":"Kono bōshi wa fuku to yoku tsuriatte iru.","german":"This hat goes well with the clothes.","furigana":"ぼうし|ふく|つ|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"To let something hang down from above: 棚を吊る. 吊るす is more common. Homophone: 釣る (to fish).","examples":[{"japanese":"天井からランプを吊った。","romaji":"Tenjō kara ranpu o tsutta.","german":"I hung a lamp from the ceiling.","furigana":"てんじょう|つ"},{"japanese":"腕を骨折して、三角巾で吊っている。","romaji":"Ude o kossetsu shite, sankakukin de tsutte iru.","german":"I broke my arm and am carrying it in a sling.","furigana":"うで|こっせつ|さんかくきん|つ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Transitive: to hang something so that it dangles: 洗濯物を吊るす, 風鈴を吊るす.","examples":[{"japanese":"窓に風鈴を吊るした。","romaji":"Mado ni fūrin o tsurushita.","german":"I hung a wind chime in the window.","furigana":"まど|ふうりん|つ"},{"japanese":"洗濯物を部屋の中に吊るして乾かす。","romaji":"Sentakumono o heya no naka ni tsurushite kawakasu.","german":"I hang the laundry inside the room to dry.","furigana":"せんたくもの|へや|なか|つ|かわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A fall in values or quality: 気温の低下, 体力の低下; as a verb 低下する.","examples":[{"japanese":"年を取ると体力が低下する。","romaji":"Toshi o toru to tairyoku ga teika suru.","german":"Physical strength declines with age.","furigana":"とし|と|たいりょく|ていか"},{"japanese":"気温の低下に注意してください。","romaji":"Kion no teika ni chūi shite kudasai.","german":"Please be careful of the drop in temperature.","furigana":"きおん|ていか|ちゅうい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A vehicle stops briefly: 各駅停車 (local train), 停車中 (stopped). Parking is 駐車.","examples":[{"japanese":"この電車は次の駅に停車します。","romaji":"Kono densha wa tsugi no eki ni teisha shimasu.","german":"This train will stop at the next station.","furigana":"でんしゃ|つぎ|えき|ていしゃ"},{"japanese":"事故のため、電車が一時停車している。","romaji":"Jiko no tame, densha ga ichiji teisha shite iru.","german":"The train is temporarily stopped because of an accident.","furigana":"じこ|でんしゃ|いちじていしゃ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The coming and going of people or things: 人の出入りが多い; 出入り禁止 (no entry).","examples":[{"japanese":"この店は一日中人の出入りが多い。","romaji":"Kono mise wa ichinichijū hito no deiri ga ōi.","german":"Many people come and go in this shop all day.","furigana":"みせ|いちにちじゅうひと|でい|おお"},{"japanese":"工事中のため、関係者以外の出入りは禁止です。","romaji":"Kōjichū no tame, kankeisha igai no deiri wa kinshi desu.","german":"Because of construction work, entry is prohibited for anyone other than authorized personnel.","furigana":"こうじちゅう|かんけいしゃいがい|でい|きんし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A door or opening for going in and out; on signs often written 出入口.","examples":[{"japanese":"出入り口の前に物を置かないでください。","romaji":"Deiriguchi no mae ni mono o okanaide kudasai.","german":"Please do not place things in front of the entrance/exit.","furigana":"でい|ぐち|まえ|もの|お"},{"japanese":"この建物の出入り口は一つしかない。","romaji":"Kono tatemono no deiriguchi wa hitotsu shika nai.","german":"This building has only one entrance and exit.","furigana":"たてもの|でい|ぐち|ひと"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"To tend and keep in good condition: 庭の手入れ, 肌の手入れ; as a verb 手入れする.","examples":[{"japanese":"父は毎週末、庭の手入れをしている。","romaji":"Chichi wa maishūmatsu, niwa no teire o shite iru.","german":"My father tends the garden every weekend.","furigana":"ちち|まいしゅうまつ|にわ|てい"},{"japanese":"この靴はちゃんと手入れすれば長く使える。","romaji":"Kono kutsu wa chanto teire sureba nagaku tsukaeru.","german":"If you take good care of these shoes, you can use them for a long time.","furigana":"くつ|てい|なが|つか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Being finished or the finished result: 出来上がりが楽しみ; in recipes \"done!\" (これで出来上がり).","examples":[{"japanese":"最後にねぎをのせたら出来上がりです。","romaji":"Saigo ni negi o nosetara dekiagari desu.","german":"Put the green onions on top at the end, and it's done.","furigana":"さいご|できあ"},{"japanese":"写真の出来上がりは来週になります。","romaji":"Shashin no dekiagari wa raishū ni narimasu.","german":"The photos will be ready next week.","furigana":"しゃしん|できあ|らいしゅう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Noun and な-adjective: made-up talk (でたらめを言う) or haphazard (でたらめな計画). The kanji 出鱈目 are ateji.","examples":[{"japanese":"そんなでたらめを信じてはいけない。","romaji":"Sonna detarame o shinjite wa ikenai.","german":"You shouldn't believe such nonsense.","furigana":"しん"},{"japanese":"彼の説明はでたらめで、よく分からなかった。","romaji":"Kare no setsumei wa detarame de, yoku wakaranakatta.","german":"His explanation was incoherent, and I could hardly understand it.","furigana":"かれ|せつめい|わ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A bridge made of iron or steel, especially for trains.","examples":[{"japanese":"電車が川の上の鉄橋を渡っている。","romaji":"Densha ga kawa no ue no tekkyō o watatte iru.","german":"The train is crossing the iron bridge over the river.","furigana":"でんしゃ|かわ|うえ|てっきょう|わた"},{"japanese":"この鉄橋は百年前に造られた。","romaji":"Kono tekkyō wa hyakunen mae ni tsukurareta.","german":"This iron bridge was built a hundred years ago.","furigana":"てっきょう|ひゃくねんまえ|つく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"An older word for firearms, especially historical matchlock guns; today usually 銃.","examples":[{"japanese":"鉄砲は十六世紀に日本に伝わった。","romaji":"Teppō wa jūrokuseiki ni Nihon ni tsutawatta.","german":"Firearms were introduced to Japan in the sixteenth century.","furigana":"てっぽう|じゅうろくせいき|にほん|つた"},{"japanese":"昔の武士は刀だけでなく鉄砲も使った。","romaji":"Mukashi no bushi wa katana dake de naku teppō mo tsukatta.","german":"The samurai of old used not only swords but also firearms.","furigana":"むかし|ぶし|かたな|てっぽう|つか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A court for playing tennis; to reserve one is コートを予約する.","examples":[{"japanese":"週末はテニスコートを予約してある。","romaji":"Shūmatsu wa tenisu kōto o yoyaku shite aru.","german":"I have reserved a tennis court for the weekend.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|よやく"},{"japanese":"学校の裏にテニスコートが二面ある。","romaji":"Gakkō no ura ni tenisu kōto ga nimen aru.","german":"There are two tennis courts behind the school.","furigana":"がっこう|うら|にめん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The side closer to the speaker: 駅の手前 (just before the station). For the grammar pattern 〜手前 (\"since one has ...\"), see the grammar section.","examples":[{"japanese":"郵便局は駅の手前にあります。","romaji":"Yūbinkyoku wa eki no temae ni arimasu.","german":"The post office is just before the station.","furigana":"ゆうびんきょく|えき|てまえ"},{"japanese":"手前の棚から順番に片付けてください。","romaji":"Temae no tana kara junban ni katazukete kudasai.","german":"Please tidy up in order, starting with the shelf nearest to you.","furigana":"てまえ|たな|じゅんばん|かたづ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"To pick up or greet someone on arrival: 空港まで出迎えに行く. Verb: 出迎える.","examples":[{"japanese":"空港まで出迎えに行きます。","romaji":"Kūkō made demukae ni ikimasu.","german":"I'll go to the airport to meet you.","furigana":"くうこう|でむか|い"},{"japanese":"駅で友達の出迎えを受けた。","romaji":"Eki de tomodachi no demukae o uketa.","german":"My friend met me at the station.","furigana":"えき|ともだち|でむか|う"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Intransitive, especially of the sun and moon: 日が照る. Transitive: 照らす (to illuminate).","examples":[{"japanese":"朝から日が照って暑い。","romaji":"Asa kara hi ga tette atsui.","german":"The sun has been shining since morning, and it's hot.","furigana":"あさ|ひ|て|あつ"},{"japanese":"雨の後、また太陽が照り始めた。","romaji":"Ame no ato, mata taiyō ga terihajimeta.","german":"After the rain, the sun began to shine again.","furigana":"あめ|あと|たいよう|て|はじ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Scattered in several places: 家が点々とある. Also \"in drops\".","examples":[{"japanese":"山の中に家が点々と見える。","romaji":"Yama no naka ni ie ga tenten to mieru.","german":"Houses can be seen scattered in the mountains.","furigana":"やま|なか|いえ|てんてん|み"},{"japanese":"床に血が点々と落ちていた。","romaji":"Yuka ni chi ga tenten to ochite ita.","german":"Drops of blood were scattered on the floor.","furigana":"ゆか|ち|てんてん|お"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Speed in music, and figuratively in conversation or a story: 話のテンポ.","examples":[{"japanese":"この曲はテンポが速い。","romaji":"Kono kyoku wa tenpo ga hayai.","german":"This piece has a fast tempo.","furigana":"きょく|はや"},{"japanese":"この映画は話のテンポがいい。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa hanashi no tenpo ga ii.","german":"This movie has good pacing.","furigana":"えいが|はなし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"In physics: the flowing current (amperage in アンペア); voltage is 電圧.","examples":[{"japanese":"電流が強すぎると危ない。","romaji":"Denryū ga tsuyosugiru to abunai.","german":"It is dangerous if the current is too strong.","furigana":"でんりゅう|つよ|あぶ"},{"japanese":"この線に電流が流れている。","romaji":"Kono sen ni denryū ga nagarete iru.","german":"Current is flowing through this wire.","furigana":"せん|でんりゅう|なが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Of equal rank (同格に扱う) and, in grammar, apposition.","examples":[{"japanese":"二人は会社の中で同格に扱われている。","romaji":"Futari wa kaisha no naka de dōkaku ni atsukawarete iru.","german":"The two are treated as equals in the company.","furigana":"ふたり|かいしゃ|なか|どうかく|あつか"},{"japanese":"この文の「の」は同格を表している。","romaji":"Kono bun no \"no\" wa dōkaku o arawashite iru.","german":"The \"の\" in this sentence expresses apposition.","furigana":"ぶん|どうかく|あらわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Bodily movement (ゆっくりした動作) and the operation of devices (パソコンの動作が遅い).","examples":[{"japanese":"祖父は動作がゆっくりになった。","romaji":"Sofu wa dōsa ga yukkuri ni natta.","german":"My grandfather has become slow in his movements.","furigana":"そふ|どうさ"},{"japanese":"このパソコンは動作が遅い。","romaji":"Kono pasokon wa dōsa ga osoi.","german":"This computer runs slowly.","furigana":"どうさ|おそ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Direction (東西に走る道) and, figuratively, East and West as cultural spheres: 東西の文化.","examples":[{"japanese":"この道は町を東西に通っている。","romaji":"Kono michi wa machi o tōzai ni tōtte iru.","german":"This road runs east to west through the town.","furigana":"みち|まち|とうざい|とお"},{"japanese":"東西の文化の違いを研究している。","romaji":"Tōzai no bunka no chigai o kenkyū shite iru.","german":"I am studying the differences between Eastern and Western culture.","furigana":"とうざい|ぶんか|ちが|けんきゅう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"An opinion sent to a newspaper or an authority: 新聞に投書する.","examples":[{"japanese":"祖父は新聞によく投書している。","romaji":"Sofu wa shinbun ni yoku tōsho shite iru.","german":"My grandfather often writes letters to the newspaper.","furigana":"そふ|しんぶん|とうしょ"},{"japanese":"市民からの投書で道路が直された。","romaji":"Shimin kara no tōsho de dōro ga naosareta.","german":"The road was repaired because of a letter from a citizen.","furigana":"しみん|とうしょ|どうろ|なお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Appearing on stage or in a story (登場人物 = characters) and the appearance of new products.","examples":[{"japanese":"主人公は物語の後半に登場する。","romaji":"Shujinkō wa monogatari no kōhan ni tōjō suru.","german":"The protagonist appears in the second half of the story.","furigana":"しゅじんこう|ものがたり|こうはん|とうじょう"},{"japanese":"新しいスマホが登場した。","romaji":"Atarashii sumaho ga tōjō shita.","german":"A new smartphone has appeared on the market.","furigana":"あたら|とうじょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A task taken on in turns: 掃除当番, 給食当番 (school lunch duty).","examples":[{"japanese":"今週は私が掃除当番です。","romaji":"Konshū wa watashi ga sōji tōban desu.","german":"It's my turn to clean this week.","furigana":"こんしゅう|わたし|そうじとうばん"},{"japanese":"ゴミ出しは当番制にしている。","romaji":"Gomidashi wa tōbansei ni shite iru.","german":"We take turns taking out the trash.","furigana":"だ|とうばんせい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"To divide into equal parts: ケーキを四等分する. Homophone: 当分 (for the time being).","examples":[{"japanese":"ケーキを六等分した。","romaji":"Kēki o rokutōbun shita.","german":"I cut the cake into six equal pieces.","furigana":"ろくとうぶん"},{"japanese":"もうけは三人で等分しよう。","romaji":"Mōke wa sannin de tōbun shiyō.","german":"Let's split the profit equally among the three of us.","furigana":"さんにん|とうぶん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Fuel for kerosene heaters (石油ストーブ), which are common in Japan in winter.","examples":[{"japanese":"冬になると灯油をよく買う。","romaji":"Fuyu ni naru to tōyu o yoku kau.","german":"I often buy kerosene in winter.","furigana":"ふゆ|とうゆ|か"},{"japanese":"ストーブの灯油がなくなった。","romaji":"Sutōbu no tōyu ga nakunatta.","german":"The heater has run out of kerosene.","furigana":"とうゆ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Asia, especially East Asia, as a cultural sphere; opposite: 西洋 (the West).","examples":[{"japanese":"東洋と西洋では考え方が違うこともある。","romaji":"Tōyō to seiyō de wa kangaekata ga chigau koto mo aru.","german":"Eastern and Western ways of thinking sometimes differ.","furigana":"とうよう|せいよう|かんが|かた|ちが"},{"japanese":"大学で東洋の歴史を学んでいる。","romaji":"Daigaku de tōyō no rekishi o manande iru.","german":"I am studying East Asian history at university.","furigana":"だいがく|とうよう|れきし|まな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"To pass a place by chance: 店の前を通りかかる.","examples":[{"japanese":"店の前を通りかかったら、友達に会った。","romaji":"Mise no mae o tōrikakattara, tomodachi ni atta.","german":"When I happened to pass in front of the shop, I met a friend.","furigana":"みせ|まえ|とお|ともだち|あ"},{"japanese":"通りかかった人が救急車を呼んでくれた。","romaji":"Tōrikakatta hito ga kyūkyūsha o yonde kureta.","german":"A passerby called an ambulance.","furigana":"とお|ひと|きゅうきゅうしゃ|よ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Mostly as 尖った: 尖った鉛筆 (sharp pencil), 神経が尖る (to be on edge).","examples":[{"japanese":"この鉛筆は先がよく尖っている。","romaji":"Kono enpitsu wa saki ga yoku togatte iru.","german":"This pencil is well sharpened.","furigana":"えんぴつ|さき|とが"},{"japanese":"彼は疲れて神経が尖っている。","romaji":"Kare wa tsukarete shinkei ga togatte iru.","german":"He is tired and on edge.","furigana":"かれ|つか|しんけい|とが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"To dissolve something in liquid or to whisk: 卵を溶く, 絵の具を水で溶く. Intransitive: 溶ける.","examples":[{"japanese":"卵を溶いてフライパンに入れた。","romaji":"Tamago o toite furaipan ni ireta.","german":"I beat the eggs and put them into the frying pan.","furigana":"たまご|と|い"},{"japanese":"絵の具を水で溶いて使う。","romaji":"Enogu o mizu de toite tsukau.","german":"You dilute the paint with water before using it.","furigana":"え|ぐ|みず|と|つか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A distinctive quality: 地域の特色, 特色のある学校.","examples":[{"japanese":"この学校は国際教育に特色がある。","romaji":"Kono gakkō wa kokusai kyōiku ni tokushoku ga aru.","german":"This school is distinctive for its international education.","furigana":"がっこう|こくさいきょういく|とくしょく"},{"japanese":"それぞれの地方の特色を生かした料理だ。","romaji":"Sorezore no chihō no tokushoku o ikashita ryōri da.","german":"The dishes make use of the characteristics of each region.","furigana":"ちほう|とくしょく|い|りょうり"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"An especially positive feature. Homophone: 特徴 (feature, neutral).","examples":[{"japanese":"この車の特長は燃費のよさだ。","romaji":"Kono kuruma no tokuchō wa nenpi no yosa da.","german":"The strong point of this car is its fuel efficiency.","furigana":"くるま|とくちょう|ねんぴ"},{"japanese":"自分の特長を面接でアピールした。","romaji":"Jibun no tokuchō o mensetsu de apīru shita.","german":"I emphasized my strengths in the interview.","furigana":"じぶん|とくちょう|めんせつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"To dissolve into something or to fit into a group: 新しい環境に溶け込む.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女はすぐにクラスに溶け込んだ。","romaji":"Kanojo wa sugu ni kurasu ni tokekonda.","german":"She quickly blended in with the class.","furigana":"かのじょ|と|こ"},{"japanese":"砂糖が紅茶に溶け込んでいる。","romaji":"Satō ga kōcha ni tokekonde iru.","german":"The sugar has dissolved in the tea.","furigana":"さとう|こうちゃ|と|こ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"In some places: 所々に雪が残っている. Also written in kana ところどころ.","examples":[{"japanese":"道には所々に水たまりがある。","romaji":"Michi ni wa tokorodokoro ni mizutamari ga aru.","german":"There are puddles here and there on the road.","furigana":"みち|ところどころ|みず"},{"japanese":"彼の話は所々分からなかった。","romaji":"Kare no hanashi wa tokorodokoro wakaranakatta.","german":"I didn't understand his story in places.","furigana":"かれ|はなし|ところどころわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Emphasizes that something happened a long time ago: とっくに終わった. The kanji 疾っくに are rare.","examples":[{"japanese":"宿題はとっくに終わったよ。","romaji":"Shukudai wa tokku ni owatta yo.","german":"I finished my homework long ago.","furigana":"しゅくだい|お"},{"japanese":"電車はとっくに出てしまった。","romaji":"Densha wa tokku ni dete shimatta.","german":"The train left long ago.","furigana":"でんしゃ|で"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Suddenly and in large numbers: 人がどっと押し寄せる, どっと笑う, 疲れがどっと出る. Not to be confused with ドット (dot).","examples":[{"japanese":"試験が終わって、疲れがどっと出た。","romaji":"Shiken ga owatte, tsukare ga dotto deta.","german":"After the exam was over, the fatigue hit me all at once.","furigana":"しけん|お|つか|で"},{"japanese":"セールの初日に客がどっと押し寄せた。","romaji":"Sēru no shonichi ni kyaku ga dotto oshiyoseta.","german":"On the first day of the sale, customers poured in.","furigana":"しょにち|きゃく|お|よ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"留まる／止まる read とどまる means to stay in one place or to remain limited to an area. Both uses are intransitive: 町にとどまる, 一部にとどまる. とどまる is often written in kana. 止まる／とまる is a different reading, for example for a vehicle that stops. とどまる follows the godan conjugation: とどまります, とどまって. With にとどまらない one says that something goes beyond the stated range.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は災害の後も町に留まった。","romaji":"Kare wa saigai no ato mo machi ni todomatta.","german":"He stayed in the town even after the disaster.","furigana":"かれ|さいがい|あと|まち|とど"},{"japanese":"被害は一部の地域に留まった。","romaji":"Higai wa ichibu no chiiki ni todomatta.","german":"The damage was limited to some areas.","furigana":"ひがい|いちぶ|ちいき|とど"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Manners and consideration for others: 電車の中のエチケット. Similar: マナー.","examples":[{"japanese":"電話で大声で話すのはエチケット違反だ。","romaji":"Denwa de ōgoe de hanasu no wa echiketto ihan da.","german":"Talking loudly on the phone is a breach of etiquette.","furigana":"でんわ|おおごえ|はな|いはん"},{"japanese":"せきをするときは口をおさえるのがエチケットです。","romaji":"Seki o suru toki wa kuchi o osaeru no ga echiketto desu.","german":"When coughing, it is good manners to cover your mouth.","furigana":"くち"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"An apron for cooking or working: エプロンをする／つける.","examples":[{"japanese":"料理をするときはエプロンをつけます。","romaji":"Ryōri o suru toki wa epuron o tsukemasu.","german":"I put on an apron when I cook.","furigana":"りょうり"},{"japanese":"店員はみんな緑のエプロンをしている。","romaji":"Ten'in wa minna midori no epuron o shite iru.","german":"All the shop assistants wear green aprons.","furigana":"てんいん|みどり"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Growing flowers, vegetables and fruit, also as a hobby: 園芸店 (garden center). Homophone: 演芸 (variety entertainment).","examples":[{"japanese":"父の趣味は園芸です。","romaji":"Chichi no shumi wa engei desu.","german":"My father's hobby is gardening.","furigana":"ちち|しゅみ|えんげい"},{"japanese":"園芸店でトマトの苗を買った。","romaji":"Engeiten de tomato no nae o katta.","german":"I bought tomato seedlings at a garden center.","furigana":"えんげいてん|なえ|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Mathematics: 円周率 (the number pi). Homophone: 演習 (exercise, practice).","examples":[{"japanese":"円周の長さを求めなさい。","romaji":"Enshū no nagasa o motomenasai.","german":"Find the length of the circumference.","furigana":"えんしゅう|なが|もと"},{"japanese":"地球の円周は約四万キロメートルある。","romaji":"Chikyū no enshū wa yaku yonman kiromētoru aru.","german":"The circumference of the Earth is about 40,000 kilometers.","furigana":"ちきゅう|えんしゅう|やくよんまん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Often for motor or cosmetic oil: エンジンオイル, オリーブオイル. General word: 油.","examples":[{"japanese":"車のオイルを交換した。","romaji":"Kuruma no oiru o kōkan shita.","german":"I changed the oil in my car.","furigana":"くるま|こうかん"},{"japanese":"サラダにオリーブオイルをかけた。","romaji":"Sarada ni orību oiru o kaketa.","german":"I poured olive oil over the salad."}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Receiving visitors, mostly in compounds: 応接室 (reception room), 応接間.","examples":[{"japanese":"お客様を応接室にご案内してください。","romaji":"Okyakusama o ōsetsushitsu ni goannai shite kudasai.","german":"Please show the guest to the reception room.","furigana":"きゃくさま|おうせつしつ|あんない"},{"japanese":"来客の応接に追われて、仕事が進まなかった。","romaji":"Raikyaku no ōsetsu ni owarete, shigoto ga susumanakatta.","german":"I was so busy receiving visitors that my work didn't progress.","furigana":"らいきゃく|おうせつ|お|しごと|すす"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"How one deals with customers, callers or visitors: 電話の応対, 客に応対する.","examples":[{"japanese":"あの店は店員の応対がとてもいい。","romaji":"Ano mise wa ten'in no ōtai ga totemo ii.","german":"The staff at that shop treat customers very well.","furigana":"みせ|てんいん|おうたい"},{"japanese":"新人は電話の応対を練習している。","romaji":"Shinjin wa denwa no ōtai o renshū shite iru.","german":"The new employees are practicing answering the phone.","furigana":"しんじん|でんわ|おうたい|れんしゅう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A large, wide street in a city. Opposite: 路地 (alley).","examples":[{"japanese":"大通りに出ると、タクシーがすぐ見つかる。","romaji":"Ōdōri ni deru to, takushī ga sugu mitsukaru.","german":"If you go out to the main street, you can find a taxi right away.","furigana":"おおどお|で|み"},{"japanese":"大通りの向こうに新しい店ができた。","romaji":"Ōdōri no mukō ni atarashii mise ga dekita.","german":"A new shop has opened across the main street.","furigana":"おおどお|む|あたら|みせ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Another portion of the same food or drink: ご飯のお代わり. As a verb お代わりする. Often written in kana.","examples":[{"japanese":"ご飯のお代わりはいかがですか。","romaji":"Gohan no okawari wa ikaga desu ka.","german":"Would you like another helping of rice?","furigana":"はん|か"},{"japanese":"おいしかったので、スープをお代わりした。","romaji":"Oishikatta node, sūpu o okawari shita.","german":"It was delicious, so I had a second helping of soup.","furigana":"か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Sympathy for another person's misfortune: それはお気の毒に. Not for one's own mistakes (for that, すみません). From the noun 気の毒 (pitiable).","examples":[{"japanese":"「足をけがして、旅行に行けなくなりました。」「それはお気の毒に。」","romaji":"\"Ashi o kega shite, ryokō ni ikenaku narimashita.\" \"Sore wa okinodoku ni.\"","german":"\"I hurt my foot and can't go on the trip.\" \"I'm sorry to hear that.\"","furigana":"あし|りょこう|い|き|どく"},{"japanese":"お気の毒に、彼は試験の日に熱を出してしまった。","romaji":"Okinodoku ni, kare wa shiken no hi ni netsu o dashite shimatta.","german":"Poor thing, he came down with a fever on the day of the exam.","furigana":"き|どく|かれ|しけん|ひ|ねつ|だ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Polite noun from 出かける: お出かけですか (Are you going out?). Also an outing: 週末のお出かけ.","examples":[{"japanese":"あら、今からお出かけですか。","romaji":"Ara, ima kara odekake desu ka.","german":"Oh, are you going out now?","furigana":"いま|で"},{"japanese":"週末のお出かけにぴったりの場所を探している。","romaji":"Shūmatsu no odekake ni pittari no basho o sagashite iru.","german":"I'm looking for a place that's perfect for a weekend outing.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|で|ばしょ|さが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Polite: お先にどうぞ (After you, please). When leaving work: お先に失礼します (I'll be heading out now).","examples":[{"japanese":"どうぞ、お先にお入りください。","romaji":"Dōzo, osaki ni ohairi kudasai.","german":"Please, go in first.","furigana":"さき|はい"},{"japanese":"では、お先にいただきます。","romaji":"Dewa, osaki ni itadakimasu.","german":"Well then, I'll start eating before you.","furigana":"さき"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"な-adjective and noun: おしゃれな服 (stylish clothes), おしゃれをする (to dress up). The kanji お洒落 is rare.","examples":[{"japanese":"駅前におしゃれなカフェができた。","romaji":"Ekimae ni oshare na kafe ga dekita.","german":"A stylish cafe has opened in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ"},{"japanese":"パーティーのために、少しおしゃれをした。","romaji":"Pātī no tame ni, sukoshi oshare o shita.","german":"I dressed up a little for the party.","furigana":"すこ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Polite term for a hired housekeeper. Today often 家事代行 (housework service).","examples":[{"japanese":"その家にはお手伝いさんが二人いる。","romaji":"Sono ie ni wa otetsudaisan ga futari iru.","german":"There are two housekeepers in that house.","furigana":"いえ|てつだ|ふたり"},{"japanese":"お手伝いさんが毎日掃除をしてくれる。","romaji":"Otetsudaisan ga mainichi sōji o shite kureru.","german":"The housekeeper cleans for us every day.","furigana":"てつだ|まいにちそうじ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Transitive godan verb: to scare someone (後ろからおどかす) or to threaten. The kanji 脅かす is also read おびやかす (to endanger).","examples":[{"japanese":"弟が後ろから私をおどかした。","romaji":"Otōto ga ushiro kara watashi o odokashita.","german":"My little brother scared me from behind.","furigana":"おとうと|うし|わたし"},{"japanese":"おどかさないでよ、心臓が止まるかと思った。","romaji":"Odokasanaide yo, shinzō ga tomaru ka to omotta.","german":"Don't scare me like that, I thought my heart would stop.","furigana":"しんぞう|と|おも"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Automated production: 工場のオートメーション化. Today also often 自動化.","examples":[{"japanese":"この工場はほとんどオートメーションで動いている。","romaji":"Kono kōjō wa hotondo ōtomēshon de ugoite iru.","german":"This factory runs almost entirely on automation.","furigana":"こうじょう|うご"},{"japanese":"オートメーションが進んで、人の仕事が減った。","romaji":"Ōtomēshon ga susunde, hito no shigoto ga hetta.","german":"As automation advanced, jobs for people decreased.","furigana":"すす|ひと|しごと|へ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Praying at a shrine, temple or grave: お墓参り (grave visit), 神社にお参りする.","examples":[{"japanese":"正月に家族で神社へお参りに行った。","romaji":"Shōgatsu ni kazoku de jinja e omairi ni itta.","german":"At New Year we went to the shrine as a family to pray.","furigana":"しょうがつ|かぞく|じんじゃ|まい|い"},{"japanese":"お盆には祖父の墓にお参りする。","romaji":"Obon ni wa sofu no haka ni omairi suru.","german":"At Obon we visit my grandfather's grave.","furigana":"ぼん|そふ|はか|まい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A friendly, casual apology for keeping someone waiting, e.g. when serving in a restaurant. More formal: お待たせしました.","examples":[{"japanese":"はい、お待ちどおさま。ラーメン二つです。","romaji":"Hai, omachidōsama. Rāmen futatsu desu.","german":"Here you go, thanks for waiting. Two ramen.","furigana":"ま|ふた"},{"japanese":"お待ちどおさま。さあ、出かけよう。","romaji":"Omachidōsama. Sā, dekakeyō.","german":"Thanks for waiting. Now, let's go.","furigana":"ま|で"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"An occasion for congratulations: おめでたい日 (a joyous day); おめでた = pregnancy/birth. Ironic: gullible (おめでたい人).","examples":[{"japanese":"今日は姉の結婚式という、おめでたい日です。","romaji":"Kyō wa ane no kekkonshiki to iu, omedetai hi desu.","german":"Today is a happy day: my sister's wedding.","furigana":"きょう|あね|けっこんしき|ひ"},{"japanese":"あんな話を信じるなんて、君はおめでたいね。","romaji":"Anna hanashi o shinjiru nante, kimi wa omedetai ne.","german":"Believing a story like that, you really are naive.","furigana":"はなし|しん|きみ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"To nurse a sick person at home: 母を看病する. Professional nursing: 看護, elder care: 介護.","examples":[{"japanese":"熱を出した子どもを一晩中看病した。","romaji":"Netsu o dashita kodomo o hitobanjū kanbyō shita.","german":"I looked after the feverish child all night.","furigana":"ねつ|だ|こ|ひとばんじゅうかんびょう"},{"japanese":"家族の看病のおかげで、早く元気になった。","romaji":"Kazoku no kanbyō no okage de, hayaku genki ni natta.","german":"Thanks to my family's care, I recovered quickly.","furigana":"かぞく|かんびょう|はや|げんき"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Mostly in 漢和辞典: a dictionary that explains kanji and kanji words in Japanese. Homophone: 緩和 (easing, relaxation).","examples":[{"japanese":"読めない漢字を漢和辞典で調べた。","romaji":"Yomenai kanji o kanwa jiten de shirabeta.","german":"I looked up a kanji I couldn't read in a kanji dictionary.","furigana":"よ|かんじ|かんわじてん|しら"},{"japanese":"漢和の辞書は部首で引くことができる。","romaji":"Kanwa no jisho wa bushu de hiku koto ga dekiru.","german":"In a kanji dictionary, you can look things up by radical.","furigana":"かんわ|じしょ|ぶしゅ|ひ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"い-adjective: 思いがけない出会い (an unexpected encounter). Adverb: 思いがけなく.","examples":[{"japanese":"旅先で思いがけない人に会った。","romaji":"Tabisaki de omoigakenai hito ni atta.","german":"On my trip I ran into someone unexpected.","furigana":"たびさき|おも|ひと|あ"},{"japanese":"思いがけないプレゼントに、母はとても喜んだ。","romaji":"Omoigakenai purezento ni, haha wa totemo yorokonda.","german":"My mother was delighted with the unexpected present.","furigana":"おも|はは|よろこ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Adverb meaning \"as much as one can\": 思い切り泣く, 思い切り遊ぶ; colloquially 思いっきり. As a noun: resolve (思い切りがいい).","examples":[{"japanese":"試験が終わったら、思い切り遊びたい。","romaji":"Shiken ga owattara, omoikiri asobitai.","german":"When the exams are over, I want to play to my heart's content.","furigana":"しけん|お|おも|き|あそ"},{"japanese":"ボールを思い切り蹴った。","romaji":"Bōru o omoikiri ketta.","german":"I kicked the ball as hard as I could.","furigana":"おも|き|け"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Spoken variant of 重い, stressing the felt weight; also for mood: 重たい空気.","examples":[{"japanese":"この箱は重たくて、一人では持てない。","romaji":"Kono hako wa omotakute, hitori de wa motenai.","german":"This box is so heavy that I can't carry it alone.","furigana":"はこ|おも|ひとり|も"},{"japanese":"会議室には重たい空気が流れていた。","romaji":"Kaigishitsu ni wa omotai kūki ga nagarete ita.","german":"A heavy atmosphere hung over the meeting room.","furigana":"かいぎしつ|おも|くうき|なが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Polite noun for 休み (明日はお休みです = Tomorrow is a day off) and the short good-night greeting おやすみ (more polite: おやすみなさい).","examples":[{"japanese":"この店は毎週月曜日がお休みです。","romaji":"Kono mise wa maishū getsuyōbi ga oyasumi desu.","german":"This shop is closed every Monday.","furigana":"みせ|まいしゅうげつようび|やす"},{"japanese":"じゃあ、おやすみ。また明日ね。","romaji":"Jā, oyasumi. Mata ashita ne.","german":"Well then, good night. See you tomorrow.","furigana":"あした"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Also the harmonium (足踏みオルガン) common in Japanese schools. Large church organ: パイプオルガン.","examples":[{"japanese":"小学校の教室にはオルガンがあった。","romaji":"Shōgakkō no kyōshitsu ni wa orugan ga atta.","german":"There was an organ in the elementary school classroom.","furigana":"しょうがっこう|きょうしつ"},{"japanese":"教会でパイプオルガンの演奏を聞いた。","romaji":"Kyōkai de paipu orugan no ensō o kiita.","german":"I listened to a pipe organ performance at the church.","furigana":"きょうかい|えんそう|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A heated house for plants. Figurative: 温室育ち (raised in a sheltered, pampered way). Greenhouse effect: 温室効果.","examples":[{"japanese":"温室でトマトを育てている。","romaji":"Onshitsu de tomato o sodatete iru.","german":"I'm growing tomatoes in a greenhouse.","furigana":"おんしつ|そだ"},{"japanese":"植物園の温室には熱帯の花が咲いていた。","romaji":"Shokubutsuen no onshitsu ni wa nettai no hana ga saite ita.","german":"Tropical flowers were blooming in the greenhouse of the botanical garden.","furigana":"しょくぶつえん|おんしつ|ねったい|はな|さ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Placed after the name of a company or department on letters: 〇〇株式会社 御中. For individuals, use 様 instead. Do not combine the two.","examples":[{"japanese":"封筒に「営業部御中」と書いた。","romaji":"Fūtō ni \"Eigyōbu onchū\" to kaita.","german":"I wrote \"Sales Department\" on the envelope.","furigana":"ふうとう|えいぎょうぶおんちゅう|か"},{"japanese":"会社あての手紙には「御中」を付けます。","romaji":"Kaisha ate no tegami ni wa \"onchū\" o tsukemasu.","german":"When addressing a letter to a company, you add 「御中」.","furigana":"かいしゃ|てがみ|おんちゅう|つ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The start of an assembly: 開会式 (opening ceremony), 開会の挨拶. Opposite: 閉会.","examples":[{"japanese":"午前十時に開会します。","romaji":"Gozen jūji ni kaikai shimasu.","german":"The meeting will open at ten in the morning.","furigana":"ごぜんじゅうじ|かいかい"},{"japanese":"市長が開会のあいさつをした。","romaji":"Shichō ga kaikai no aisatsu o shita.","german":"The mayor gave the opening address.","furigana":"しちょう|かいかい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A building for meetings and events: 市民会館, 学生会館. Homophone: 快感 (pleasant sensation).","examples":[{"japanese":"市民会館でコンサートが開かれる。","romaji":"Shimin kaikan de konsāto ga hirakareru.","german":"A concert will be held at the city public hall.","furigana":"しみんかいかん|ひら"},{"japanese":"会館の大ホールは五百人入れる。","romaji":"Kaikan no daihōru wa gohyakunin haireru.","german":"The main hall of the building seats five hundred people.","furigana":"かいかん|だい|ごひゃくにんはい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Swimming and bathing at the beach: 海水浴に行く, 海水浴場 (swimming beach).","examples":[{"japanese":"夏休みに家族で海水浴に行った。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi ni kazoku de kaisuiyoku ni itta.","german":"During the summer vacation, we went swimming in the sea as a family.","furigana":"なつやす|かぞく|かいすいよく|い"},{"japanese":"この海水浴場は七月から開く。","romaji":"Kono kaisuiyokujō wa shichigatsu kara hiraku.","german":"This beach opens in July.","furigana":"かいすいよくじょう|しちがつ|ひら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Technically: a sky almost without clouds. Stronger than 晴れ. Homophone: 改正 (revision).","examples":[{"japanese":"運動会の日は快晴だった。","romaji":"Undōkai no hi wa kaisei datta.","german":"The day of the sports festival was perfectly clear.","furigana":"うんどうかい|ひ|かいせい"},{"japanese":"明日は全国的に快晴になるでしょう。","romaji":"Ashita wa zenkokuteki ni kaisei ni naru deshō.","german":"Tomorrow it will be clear across the country.","furigana":"あした|ぜんこくてき|かいせい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"When a road, railway line or line of communication goes into service: 新しい道路が開通する; also the reopening after a closure.","examples":[{"japanese":"来月、新しいトンネルが開通する。","romaji":"Raigetsu, atarashii tonneru ga kaitsū suru.","german":"A new tunnel will open next month.","furigana":"らいげつ|あたら|かいつう"},{"japanese":"雪で止まっていた道路が、やっと開通した。","romaji":"Yuki de tomatte ita dōro ga, yatto kaitsū shita.","german":"The road that had been closed by snow has finally reopened.","furigana":"ゆき|と|どうろ|かいつう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Written-language word, e.g. in news and official language: 家屋の被害, 日本家屋 (traditional Japanese house).","examples":[{"japanese":"地震で多くの家屋が倒れた。","romaji":"Jishin de ōku no kaoku ga taoreta.","german":"Many houses collapsed in the earthquake.","furigana":"じしん|おお|かおく|たお"},{"japanese":"古い日本家屋を改装してカフェにした。","romaji":"Furui Nihon kaoku o kaisō shite kafe ni shita.","german":"An old Japanese-style house was renovated into a cafe.","furigana":"ふる|にほんかおく|かいそう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Writing down what is heard or read; at school especially kanji writing (漢字の書き取り). Verb: 書き取る.","examples":[{"japanese":"明日は漢字の書き取りテストがある。","romaji":"Ashita wa kanji no kakitori tesuto ga aru.","german":"Tomorrow there is a kanji writing test.","furigana":"あした|かんじ|か|と"},{"japanese":"先生が読んだ文を書き取りで練習した。","romaji":"Sensei ga yonda bun o kakitori de renshū shita.","german":"We practiced with sentences the teacher read aloud, writing them down.","furigana":"せんせい|よ|ぶん|か|と|れんしゅう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Transitive godan verb: 頭をかく (scratch one's head), 汗をかく (sweat), 雪をかく (shovel snow). Usually written in kana.","examples":[{"japanese":"蚊に刺されたところを掻いてしまった。","romaji":"Ka ni sasareta tokoro o kaite shimatta.","german":"I scratched the spot where a mosquito had bitten me.","furigana":"か|さ|か"},{"japanese":"朝から家の前の雪をかいた。","romaji":"Asa kara ie no mae no yuki o kaita.","german":"Since the morning I have been shoveling the snow in front of the house.","furigana":"あさ|いえ|まえ|ゆき"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"な-adjective and adverb: 格別な味, 格別おいしい. With negation: 格別～ない (not particularly).","examples":[{"japanese":"山の上で食べるおにぎりの味は格別だ。","romaji":"Yama no ue de taberu onigiri no aji wa kakubetsu da.","german":"The taste of rice balls eaten on a mountaintop is exceptional.","furigana":"やま|うえ|た|あじ|かくべつ"},{"japanese":"この冬は格別寒さが厳しい。","romaji":"Kono fuyu wa kakubetsu samusa ga kibishii.","german":"This winter the cold is particularly severe.","furigana":"ふゆ|かくべつさむ|きび"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A single room for rent; a rather old-fashioned word (today 貸し部屋 or an apartment). Sign: 貸間あり.","examples":[{"japanese":"家の前に「貸間あり」と書いた紙がはってあった。","romaji":"Ie no mae ni \"kashima ari\" to kaita kami ga hatte atta.","german":"A sign reading \"Room for rent\" was posted in front of the house.","furigana":"いえ|まえ|かしま|か|かみ"},{"japanese":"学生時代は大家さんの家の貸間に住んでいた。","romaji":"Gakusei jidai wa ōyasan no ie no kashima ni sunde ita.","german":"As a student I lived in a rented room in my landlord's house.","furigana":"がくせいじだい|おおや|いえ|かしま|す"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"An entire house for rent. Opposite in housing: 持ち家 (owned home).","examples":[{"japanese":"駅の近くに貸家を探している。","romaji":"Eki no chikaku ni kashiya o sagashite iru.","german":"I'm looking for a house to rent near the station.","furigana":"えき|ちか|かしや|さが"},{"japanese":"この貸家は庭が広くて、家賃も安い。","romaji":"Kono kashiya wa niwa ga hirokute, yachin mo yasui.","german":"This rental house has a big garden, and the rent is cheap.","furigana":"かしや|にわ|ひろ|やちん|やす"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Audio or video cassette; also a game cartridge for consoles (ゲームのカセット). In compounds also a cartridge: カセットコンロ (portable gas stove with a cartridge).","examples":[{"japanese":"昔はカセットで音楽を聞いていた。","romaji":"Mukashi wa kasetto de ongaku o kiite ita.","german":"I used to listen to music on cassette.","furigana":"むかし|おんがく|き"},{"japanese":"子どものころ、ゲームのカセットを友達と交換した。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, gēmu no kasetto o tomodachi to kōkan shita.","german":"As a child, I traded game cartridges with friends.","furigana":"こ|ともだち|こうかん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A line under text: 下線を引く (to underline); in exams often 下線部 (the underlined part).","examples":[{"japanese":"大事なところに下線を引いておいた。","romaji":"Daiji na tokoro ni kasen o hiite oita.","german":"I underlined the important parts.","furigana":"だいじ|かせん|ひ"},{"japanese":"下線の部分を日本語に訳しなさい。","romaji":"Kasen no bubun o nihongo ni yakushinasai.","german":"Translate the underlined part into Japanese.","furigana":"かせん|ぶぶん|にほんご|やく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A solidly clumped mass: 肉の塊, 氷の塊. Figurative: 努力の塊 (the embodiment of diligence). Also written 固まり.","examples":[{"japanese":"スーパーで肉の塊を買って、自分で切った。","romaji":"Sūpā de niku no katamari o katte, jibun de kitta.","german":"I bought a whole chunk of meat at the supermarket and cut it myself.","furigana":"にく|かたまり|か|じぶん|き"},{"japanese":"彼は好奇心の塊のような子どもだ。","romaji":"Kare wa kōkishin no katamari no yō na kodomo da.","german":"He is a child who is brimming with curiosity.","furigana":"かれ|こうきしん|かたまり|こ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Official word for a school class: 学級委員 (class representative), 学級閉鎖 (closing of a class due to illness). In everyday speech: クラス.","examples":[{"japanese":"インフルエンザで学級閉鎖になった。","romaji":"Infuruenza de gakkyū heisa ni natta.","german":"The class was shut down because of influenza.","furigana":"がっきゅうへいさ"},{"japanese":"一つの学級には三十人の生徒がいる。","romaji":"Hitotsu no gakkyū ni wa sanjūnin no seito ga iru.","german":"There are thirty students in one class.","furigana":"ひと|がっきゅう|さんじゅうにん|せいと"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Originally printing type, now mainly printed text: 活字離れ (turning away from reading), 活字中毒 (bookworm; addicted to print).","examples":[{"japanese":"古い印刷所には金属の活字が残っている。","romaji":"Furui insatsujo ni wa kinzoku no katsuji ga nokotte iru.","german":"The old print shop still has metal type.","furigana":"ふる|いんさつじょ|きんぞく|かつじ|のこ"},{"japanese":"彼は活字を読んでいないと落ち着かない。","romaji":"Kare wa katsuji o yonde inai to ochitsukanai.","german":"He gets restless if he isn't reading something in print.","furigana":"かれ|かつじ|よ|お|つ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Without permission or without anyone doing anything: 勝手に使わないで (Don't just use it!); ドアが勝手に開いた (The door opened by itself). Noun: 勝手.","examples":[{"japanese":"人のパソコンを勝手に使わないでください。","romaji":"Hito no pasokon o katte ni tsukawanaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't use other people's computers without asking.","furigana":"ひと|かって|つか"},{"japanese":"風でドアが勝手に閉まった。","romaji":"Kaze de doa ga katte ni shimatta.","german":"The door shut by itself because of the wind.","furigana":"かぜ|かって|し"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A fixed course of study or training: 博士課程 (doctoral program). Homophones: 過程 (process), 家庭 (family; household).","examples":[{"japanese":"兄は大学院の博士課程にいる。","romaji":"Ani wa daigakuin no hakase katei ni iru.","german":"My older brother is in a doctoral program at graduate school.","furigana":"あに|だいがくいん|はかせかてい"},{"japanese":"この学校では三年で全課程を終える。","romaji":"Kono gakkō de wa sannen de zenkatei o oeru.","german":"At this school you complete the entire curriculum in three years.","furigana":"がっこう|さんねん|ぜんかてい|お"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Literary: far away, on the other side: 山の彼方, 空の彼方. Often written in kana.","examples":[{"japanese":"船は海の彼方へ消えていった。","romaji":"Fune wa umi no kanata e kiete itta.","german":"The ship disappeared into the distance over the sea.","furigana":"ふね|うみ|かなた|き"},{"japanese":"山の彼方に夕日が沈んだ。","romaji":"Yama no kanata ni yūhi ga shizunda.","german":"The sun set beyond the mountains.","furigana":"やま|かなた|ゆうひ|しず"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"The rules for how words are written in kana: 現代仮名遣い (present-day rules), 歴史的仮名遣い (older orthography, e.g. けふ for きょう).","examples":[{"japanese":"古い本は歴史的仮名遣いで書かれている。","romaji":"Furui hon wa rekishiteki kanazukai de kakarete iru.","german":"Old books are written in historical kana orthography.","furigana":"ふる|ほん|れきしてきかなづか|か"},{"japanese":"作文の仮名遣いを先生に直された。","romaji":"Sakubun no kanazukai o sensei ni naosareta.","german":"The teacher corrected the kana orthography in my composition.","furigana":"さくぶん|かなづか|せんせい|なお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"As a verb 加熱する: 電子レンジで加熱する. On packaging: 加熱してお召し上がりください (heat before eating).","examples":[{"japanese":"電子レンジで二分加熱してください。","romaji":"Denshi renji de nifun kanetsu shite kudasai.","german":"Please heat it in the microwave for two minutes.","furigana":"でんし|にふんかねつ"},{"japanese":"この肉は十分に加熱してから食べてください。","romaji":"Kono niku wa jūbun ni kanetsu shite kara tabete kudasai.","german":"Please heat this meat thoroughly before eating.","furigana":"にく|じゅうぶん|かねつ|た"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Protective cover (本のカバー, スマホカバー); as a verb カバーする: to make up for a shortcoming (ミスをカバーする). Also a cover version of a song.","examples":[{"japanese":"本にカバーをかけてもらえますか。","romaji":"Hon ni kabā o kakete moraemasu ka.","german":"Could you put a cover on this book for me?","furigana":"ほん"},{"japanese":"先輩が私のミスをカバーしてくれた。","romaji":"Senpai ga watashi no misu o kabā shite kureta.","german":"A senior colleague covered for my mistake.","furigana":"せんぱい|わたし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A curve in the road (急なカーブ) and, in baseball, a pitch that curves.","examples":[{"japanese":"この先は急なカーブがあるので注意してください。","romaji":"Kono saki wa kyū na kābu ga aru node chūi shite kudasai.","german":"Please be careful, there is a sharp curve ahead.","furigana":"さき|きゅう|ちゅうい"},{"japanese":"ピッチャーはカーブで三振を取った。","romaji":"Pitchā wa kābu de sanshin o totta.","german":"The pitcher struck him out with a curveball.","furigana":"さんしん|と"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Transitive ichidan verb related to かぶる: to place something over something (鍋にふたをかぶせる) or to put something on someone (子どもに帽子をかぶせる). Often in kana.","examples":[{"japanese":"子どもに帽子をかぶせて、外に出た。","romaji":"Kodomo ni bōshi o kabusete, soto ni deta.","german":"I put a hat on the child and went outside.","furigana":"こ|ぼうし|そと|で"},{"japanese":"料理にラップを被せて冷蔵庫に入れた。","romaji":"Ryōri ni rappu o kabusete reizōko ni ireta.","german":"I covered the food with plastic wrap and put it in the refrigerator.","furigana":"りょうり|かぶ|れいぞうこ|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Polite form of 構わない (from 構う): 〜ても構いません = it's all right if .... Example: ここに座っても構いませんか.","examples":[{"japanese":"窓を開けても構いませんか。","romaji":"Mado o akete mo kamaimasen ka.","german":"May I open the window?","furigana":"まど|あ|かま"},{"japanese":"「遅れてすみません。」「いいえ、構いませんよ。」","romaji":"\"Okurete sumimasen.\" \"Iie, kamaimasen yo.\"","german":"\"Sorry for being late.\" \"No, it's no problem.\"","furigana":"おく|かま"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Discarded paper; often written 紙くず. Figurative: 紙くず同然 (worthless as scrap paper, e.g. shares).","examples":[{"japanese":"床に落ちた紙屑を拾ってごみ箱に捨てた。","romaji":"Yuka ni ochita kamikuzu o hirotte gomibako ni suteta.","german":"I picked up the scraps of paper that had fallen on the floor and threw them in the trash can.","furigana":"ゆか|お|かみくず|ひろ|ばこ|す"},{"japanese":"会社が倒産して、株が紙屑になった。","romaji":"Kaisha ga tōsan shite, kabu ga kamikuzu ni natta.","german":"The company went bankrupt, and its shares became worthless.","furigana":"かいしゃ|とうさん|かぶ|かみくず"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Razor blade or razor; often in kana かみそり. Electric shaver: 電気かみそり／シェーバー.","examples":[{"japanese":"かみそりでひげをそった。","romaji":"Kamisori de hige o sotta.","german":"I shaved my beard with a razor."},{"japanese":"剃刀の刃で指を切ってしまった。","romaji":"Kamisori no ha de yubi o kitte shimatta.","german":"I cut my finger on the razor blade.","furigana":"かみそり|は|ゆび|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Short for チューインガム: ガムをかむ (to chew gum).","examples":[{"japanese":"眠くならないように、ガムをかんでいる。","romaji":"Nemuku naranai yō ni, gamu o kande iru.","german":"I'm chewing gum so that I don't get sleepy.","furigana":"ねむ"},{"japanese":"道にガムを捨てないでください。","romaji":"Michi ni gamu o sutenaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't throw chewing gum on the street.","furigana":"みち|す"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 28"},
{"notes":"Color (カラー印刷 = color printing) and the character of a group (チームカラー, 学校のカラー). Homophone: カラー (collar).","examples":[{"japanese":"この資料はカラーで印刷してください。","romaji":"Kono shiryō wa karā de insatsu shite kudasai.","german":"Please print these materials in color.","furigana":"しりょう|いんさつ"},{"japanese":"この学校には自由なカラーがある。","romaji":"Kono gakkō ni wa jiyū na karā ga aru.","german":"This school has a free-spirited character.","furigana":"がっこう|じゆう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A traditional card game, especially at New Year: 百人一首のかるた. The word comes from Portuguese (carta).","examples":[{"japanese":"お正月に家族でかるたをした。","romaji":"Oshōgatsu ni kazoku de karuta o shita.","german":"At New Year we played karuta as a family.","furigana":"しょうがつ|かぞく"},{"japanese":"百人一首のかるたの大会に出た。","romaji":"Hyakunin isshu no karuta no taikai ni deta.","german":"I took part in a Hyakunin Isshu karuta tournament.","furigana":"ひゃくにんいっしゅ|たいかい|で"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Transitive godan verb: 孫をかわいがる (spoil one's grandchildren), 部下をかわいがる (nurture subordinates). Kanji: 可愛がる.","examples":[{"japanese":"祖母は孫をとてもかわいがっている。","romaji":"Sobo wa mago o totemo kawaigatte iru.","german":"My grandmother dotes on her grandchildren.","furigana":"そぼ|まご"},{"japanese":"部長は新人をかわいがって、よく食事に連れていく。","romaji":"Buchō wa shinjin o kawaigatte, yoku shokuji ni tsurete iku.","german":"The department head looks after the newcomers and often takes them out for meals.","furigana":"ぶちょう|しんじん|しょくじ|つ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"January 1; 元旦 strictly means the morning of New Year's Day.","examples":[{"japanese":"元日は家族で初詣に行きます。","romaji":"Ganjitsu wa kazoku de hatsumōde ni ikimasu.","german":"On New Year's Day we go to the first shrine visit of the year as a family.","furigana":"がんじつ|かぞく|はつもうで|い"},{"japanese":"この店は元日だけ休みです。","romaji":"Kono mise wa ganjitsu dake yasumi desu.","german":"This shop is closed only on New Year's Day.","furigana":"みせ|がんじつ|やす"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Food in a can: ツナの缶詰. Figuratively: 缶詰になる (to be stuck somewhere, e.g. shut in to work).","examples":[{"japanese":"災害に備えて、缶詰を買っておいた。","romaji":"Saigai ni sonaete, kanzume o katte oita.","german":"I bought canned food in preparation for disasters.","furigana":"さいがい|そな|かんづめ|か"},{"japanese":"締め切り前で、作家はホテルに缶詰になっている。","romaji":"Shimekiri mae de, sakka wa hoteru ni kanzume ni natte iru.","german":"Just before the deadline, the author is shut away in a hotel to write.","furigana":"し|き|まえ|さっか|かんづめ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 42"},
{"notes":"Ordinary disposable battery: 単三乾電池 (AA battery). Rechargeable: 充電池.","examples":[{"japanese":"リモコンの乾電池が切れた。","romaji":"Rimokon no kandenchi ga kireta.","german":"The batteries in the remote control have run out.","furigana":"かんでんち|き"},{"japanese":"単三の乾電池を四本買ってきて。","romaji":"Tansan no kandenchi o yonhon katte kite.","german":"Go buy four AA batteries.","furigana":"たんさん|かんでんち|よんほんか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The region with Tokyo and the surrounding prefectures. Its counterpart in the west: 関西 (Kansai).","examples":[{"japanese":"関東では明日、雪が降る見込みです。","romaji":"Kantō de wa ashita, yuki ga furu mikomi desu.","german":"Snow is expected in the Kanto region tomorrow.","furigana":"かんとう|あした|ゆき|ふ|みこ"},{"japanese":"関東と関西では味付けが違う。","romaji":"Kantō to Kansai de wa ajitsuke ga chigau.","german":"Kanto and Kansai differ in seasoning.","furigana":"かんとう|かんさい|あじつ|ちが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The real object as opposed to a photo, model or copy: 実物大 (life-size).","examples":[{"japanese":"写真で見るより、実物のほうがずっと大きい。","romaji":"Shashin de miru yori, jitsubutsu no hō ga zutto ōkii.","german":"In person, it is much bigger than in the photo.","furigana":"しゃしん|み|じつぶつ|おお"},{"japanese":"博物館で恐竜の骨の実物を見た。","romaji":"Hakubutsukan de kyōryū no hone no jitsubutsu o mita.","german":"At the museum I saw real dinosaur bones.","furigana":"はくぶつかん|きょうりゅう|ほね|じつぶつ|み"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Often in 実用的 (practical, functional) and 実用化 (putting into practical use).","examples":[{"japanese":"このかばんはデザインより実用を重視している。","romaji":"Kono kaban wa dezain yori jitsuyō o jūshi shite iru.","german":"This bag emphasizes practicality over design.","furigana":"じつよう|じゅうし"},{"japanese":"新しい技術が実用の段階に入った。","romaji":"Atarashii gijutsu ga jitsuyō no dankai ni haitta.","german":"The new technology has entered the stage of practical use.","furigana":"あたら|ぎじゅつ|じつよう|だんかい|はい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Stripes on fabric or fur: 縞模様 (striped pattern), 縞のシャツ. Often also written in kana しま.","examples":[{"japanese":"青と白の縞のシャツを買った。","romaji":"Ao to shiro no shima no shatsu o katta.","german":"I bought a blue and white striped shirt.","furigana":"あお|しろ|しま|か"},{"japanese":"シマウマの体には黒い縞がある。","romaji":"Shimauma no karada ni wa kuroi shima ga aru.","german":"Zebras have black stripes on their bodies.","furigana":"からだ|くろ|しま"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Transitive godan verb: to close off acceptance or applications (受付を締め切る) and to shut doors and windows completely (in that sense usually written 閉め切る: 窓を閉め切る). Noun: 締め切り (deadline).","examples":[{"japanese":"寒いので、部屋の窓を全部締め切った。","romaji":"Samui node, heya no mado o zenbu shimekitta.","german":"Because it was cold, I shut all the windows in the room tight.","furigana":"さむ|へや|まど|ぜんぶし|き"},{"japanese":"申し込みは明日の五時で締め切ります。","romaji":"Mōshikomi wa ashita no goji de shimekirimasu.","german":"We will close applications tomorrow at five o'clock.","furigana":"もう|こ|あした|ごじ|し|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Exclamation used when something turns out unexpectedly well; usually in kana, rarely written 占めた. Colloquial, often said to oneself.","examples":[{"japanese":"しめた、まだバスが来ていない。","romaji":"Shimeta, mada basu ga kite inai.","german":"Great, the bus hasn't come yet.","furigana":"き"},{"japanese":"相手が道を間違えたのを見て、「しめた」と思った。","romaji":"Aite ga michi o machigaeta no o mite, \"shimeta\" to omotta.","german":"When I saw that my opponent had taken the wrong road, I thought, \"Got him.\"","furigana":"あいて|みち|まちが|み|おも"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Short for one's own company or a company just mentioned, mainly in business language: 社の方針 (company policy), 社に戻る. As a suffix: 出版社 (publisher), 新聞社. Counter for companies: 三社.","examples":[{"japanese":"一度社に戻ってから、お客様のところへ行きます。","romaji":"Ichido sha ni modotte kara, okyakusama no tokoro e ikimasu.","german":"I'll go back to the office once and then go to the client's.","furigana":"いちどしゃ|もど|きゃくさま|い"},{"japanese":"この件は社の方針に合わない。","romaji":"Kono ken wa sha no hōshin ni awanai.","german":"This matter doesn't fit the company's policy.","furigana":"けん|しゃ|ほうしん|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Sciences of society such as economics, sociology and political science. Alongside: 自然科学 (natural sciences), 人文科学 (humanities).","examples":[{"japanese":"大学では社会科学を専攻しています。","romaji":"Daigaku de wa shakaikagaku o senkō shite imasu.","german":"I'm majoring in social sciences at university.","furigana":"だいがく|しゃかいかがく|せんこう"},{"japanese":"経済学は社会科学の一つだ。","romaji":"Keizaigaku wa shakaikagaku no hitotsu da.","german":"Economics is one of the social sciences.","furigana":"けいざいがく|しゃかいかがく|ひと"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Intransitive godan verb: その場にしゃがむ. For \"to sit down\" in general: 座る.","examples":[{"japanese":"子どもの目の高さまでしゃがんで話した。","romaji":"Kodomo no me no takasa made shagande hanashita.","german":"I crouched down to the child's eye level and talked to him.","furigana":"こ|め|たか|はな"},{"japanese":"花をよく見ようと、その場にしゃがんだ。","romaji":"Hana o yoku miyō to, sono ba ni shaganda.","german":"I crouched down on the spot to get a good look at the flower.","furigana":"はな|み|ば"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Painting or drawing what is in front of you; as a verb 写生する. 写生大会 = outdoor drawing contest.","examples":[{"japanese":"公園で木や花を写生した。","romaji":"Kōen de ki ya hana o shasei shita.","german":"I sketched trees and flowers in the park.","furigana":"こうえん|き|はな|しゃせい"},{"japanese":"美術の時間に写生に出かけた。","romaji":"Bijutsu no jikan ni shasei ni dekaketa.","german":"During art class we went out to sketch.","furigana":"びじゅつ|じかん|しゃせい|で"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Camera shutter: シャッターを切る／押す (to release the shutter). Also the roll-down shutter or rolling door in front of shops: シャッターを下ろす.","examples":[{"japanese":"すみません、シャッターを押していただけますか。","romaji":"Sumimasen, shattā o oshite itadakemasu ka.","german":"Excuse me, could you press the shutter for us?","furigana":"お"},{"japanese":"夜になると、店はシャッターを下ろした。","romaji":"Yoru ni naru to, mise wa shattā o oroshita.","german":"When night fell, the shop pulled down its shutter.","furigana":"よる|みせ|お"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Often used for freelance or well-known journalists; a newspaper reporter is also called 記者.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は戦場を取材するジャーナリストだ。","romaji":"Kanojo wa senjō o shuzai suru jānarisuto da.","german":"She is a journalist who reports from war zones.","furigana":"かのじょ|せんじょう|しゅざい"},{"japanese":"将来はジャーナリストになりたい。","romaji":"Shōrai wa jānarisuto ni naritai.","german":"I want to become a journalist in the future.","furigana":"しょうらい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Transitive godan verb: 飴をしゃぶる (suck on a candy), 指をしゃぶる (suck one's finger).","examples":[{"japanese":"赤ちゃんが指をしゃぶっている。","romaji":"Akachan ga yubi o shabutte iru.","german":"The baby is sucking on its finger.","furigana":"あか|ゆび"},{"japanese":"子どもが飴をしゃぶりながら歩いている。","romaji":"Kodomo ga ame o shaburinagara aruite iru.","german":"A child is walking along sucking on a candy.","furigana":"こ|あめ|ある"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A pun on similar-sounding words; usually in kana しゃれ. だじゃれ = a corny pun. Not to be confused with お洒落（おしゃれ, stylish).","examples":[{"japanese":"父はよくつまらない洒落を言う。","romaji":"Chichi wa yoku tsumaranai share o iu.","german":"My dad often makes lame puns.","furigana":"ちち|しゃれ|い"},{"japanese":"洒落が通じなくて、場が静かになった。","romaji":"Share ga tsūjinakute, ba ga shizuka ni natta.","german":"Nobody got the joke, and the room went quiet.","furigana":"しゃれ|つう|ば|しず"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A hand game with グー (rock), チョキ (scissors) and パー (paper); you call out じゃんけんぽん. Rarely also written じゃん拳.","examples":[{"japanese":"誰が行くか、じゃんけんで決めよう。","romaji":"Dare ga iku ka, janken de kimeyō.","german":"Let's decide who goes by rock-paper-scissors.","furigana":"だれ|い|き"},{"japanese":"じゃんけんに負けて、掃除をすることになった。","romaji":"Janken ni makete, sōji o suru koto ni natta.","german":"I lost at rock-paper-scissors and ended up having to clean.","furigana":"ま|そうじ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Suffix for collections and compilations: 写真集 (photo book), 作品集 (collection of works), 問題集 (problem collection), 詩集 (poetry collection).","examples":[{"japanese":"好きな写真家の写真集を買った。","romaji":"Suki na shashinka no shashinshū o katta.","german":"I bought a photo book by my favorite photographer.","furigana":"す|しゃしんか|しゃしんしゅう|か"},{"japanese":"試験の前に問題集を一冊終わらせた。","romaji":"Shiken no mae ni mondaishū o issatsu owaraseta.","german":"Before the exam, I finished a whole workbook of practice problems.","furigana":"しけん|まえ|もんだいしゅう|いっさつお"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"A gathering of many people for some purpose, also political: 集会を開く. The fundamental right is called 集会の自由 (freedom of assembly).","examples":[{"japanese":"駅前で環境問題についての集会が開かれた。","romaji":"Ekimae de kankyō mondai ni tsuite no shūkai ga hirakareta.","german":"A rally on environmental issues was held in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|かんきょうもんだい|しゅうかい|ひら"},{"japanese":"マンションの住民の集会に出席した。","romaji":"Manshon no jūmin no shūkai ni shusseki shita.","german":"I attended the meeting of the apartment building's residents.","furigana":"じゅうみん|しゅうかい|しゅっせき"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Formal word for a dwelling or place of residence, e.g. in forms and official language; in everyday speech 家 or 住まい.","examples":[{"japanese":"災害で多くの人が住居を失った。","romaji":"Saigai de ōku no hito ga jūkyo o ushinatta.","german":"Many people lost their homes in the disaster.","furigana":"さいがい|おお|ひと|じゅうきょ|うしな"},{"japanese":"この建物は一階が店で、二階が住居になっている。","romaji":"Kono tatemono wa ikkai ga mise de, nikai ga jūkyo ni natte iru.","german":"In this building, the first floor is a shop and the second floor is a residence.","furigana":"たてもの|いっかい|みせ|にかい|じゅうきょ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"As a verb 集合する: to gather. 集合時間 (meeting time), 集合場所 (meeting place). In mathematics: set.","examples":[{"japanese":"明日は朝八時に駅前に集合してください。","romaji":"Ashita wa asa hachiji ni ekimae ni shūgō shite kudasai.","german":"Please assemble in front of the station at eight o'clock tomorrow morning.","furigana":"あした|あさはちじ|えきまえ|しゅうごう"},{"japanese":"全員の集合を待ってから出発した。","romaji":"Zen'in no shūgō o matte kara shuppatsu shita.","german":"We set off only after waiting for everyone to gather.","furigana":"ぜんいん|しゅうごう|ま|しゅっぱつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Practicing characters with a brush or pen; part of the curriculum in elementary schools. The art form itself is rather called 書道.","examples":[{"japanese":"小学生のとき、習字を習っていた。","romaji":"Shōgakusei no toki, shūji o naratte ita.","german":"When I was in elementary school, I took penmanship lessons.","furigana":"しょうがくせい|しゅうじ|なら"},{"japanese":"習字の時間に「春」という字を書いた。","romaji":"Shūji no jikan ni \"haru\" to iu ji o kaita.","german":"In penmanship class I wrote the character \"spring\".","furigana":"しゅうじ|じかん|はる|じ|か"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 48"},
{"notes":"Repairing buildings, furniture or clothing; as a verb 修繕する. For devices usually 修理; in everyday speech also 直す.","examples":[{"japanese":"古い家の屋根を修繕した。","romaji":"Furui ie no yane o shūzen shita.","german":"I repaired the roof of the old house.","furigana":"ふる|いえ|やね|しゅうぜん"},{"japanese":"破れた服を修繕してもらった。","romaji":"Yabureta fuku o shūzen shite moratta.","german":"I had the torn clothes mended.","furigana":"やぶ|ふく|しゅうぜん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Mainly in accident reports: 意識不明の重体 (unconscious and in critical condition). Also written 重態. Homophone: 渋滞 (traffic jam).","examples":[{"japanese":"事故で運転手が重体になった。","romaji":"Jiko de untenshu ga jūtai ni natta.","german":"The driver was critically injured in the accident.","furigana":"じこ|うんてんしゅ|じゅうたい"},{"japanese":"患者は一時重体だったが、今は回復している。","romaji":"Kanja wa ichiji jūtai datta ga, ima wa kaifuku shite iru.","german":"The patient was in critical condition for a while, but is recovering now.","furigana":"かんじゃ|いちじじゅうたい|いま|かいふく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Taking up an office or post: 社長に就任する. Opposite: 辞任 (resignation), 退任 (leaving office).","examples":[{"japanese":"新しい社長が四月に就任した。","romaji":"Atarashii shachō ga shigatsu ni shūnin shita.","german":"The new company president took office in April.","furigana":"あたら|しゃちょう|しがつ|しゅうにん"},{"japanese":"市長は就任してすぐに新しい計画を発表した。","romaji":"Shichō wa shūnin shite sugu ni atarashii keikaku o happyō shita.","german":"Right after taking office, the mayor announced a new plan.","furigana":"しちょう|しゅうにん|あたら|けいかく|はっぴょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Member of a company's management: 重役会議 (board meeting). Today often 役員.","examples":[{"japanese":"父は銀行の重役をしている。","romaji":"Chichi wa ginkō no jūyaku o shite iru.","german":"My father is an executive at a bank.","furigana":"ちち|ぎんこう|じゅうやく"},{"japanese":"会議には会社の重役が全員出席した。","romaji":"Kaigi ni wa kaisha no jūyaku ga zen'in shusseki shita.","german":"All the company's senior executives attended the meeting.","furigana":"かいぎ|かいしゃ|じゅうやく|ぜんいんしゅっせき"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Objective word for weight, e.g. for luggage and goods: 重量オーバー (overweight). In everyday speech: 重さ.","examples":[{"japanese":"荷物の重量は二十キロまでです。","romaji":"Nimotsu no jūryō wa nijukkiro made desu.","german":"The baggage may weigh up to twenty kilograms.","furigana":"にもつ|じゅうりょう|にじゅっ"},{"japanese":"この橋は重量の大きいトラックは通れない。","romaji":"Kono hashi wa jūryō no ōkii torakku wa tōrenai.","german":"Heavy trucks cannot cross this bridge.","furigana":"はし|じゅうりょう|おお|とお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Taking part in a competition, tournament or broadcast: 大会に出場する. Opposite: 欠場 (non-participation).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼はオリンピックに出場した。","romaji":"Kare wa orinpikku ni shutsujō shita.","german":"He took part in the Olympics.","furigana":"かれ|しゅつじょう"},{"japanese":"けがのため、次の試合への出場は難しい。","romaji":"Kega no tame, tsugi no shiai e no shutsujō wa muzukashii.","german":"Because of the injury, playing in the next match is difficult.","furigana":"つぎ|しあい|しゅつじょう|むずか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The handset of a telephone: 受話器を取る (pick up), 受話器を置く (hang up).","examples":[{"japanese":"電話が鳴ったので、受話器を取った。","romaji":"Denwa ga natta node, juwaki o totta.","german":"The phone rang, so I picked up the receiver.","furigana":"でんわ|な|じゅわき|と"},{"japanese":"話し終わって、静かに受話器を置いた。","romaji":"Hanashiowatte, shizuka ni juwaki o oita.","german":"After finishing the conversation, I quietly put down the receiver.","furigana":"はな|お|しず|じゅわき|お"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Mostly 順々に: one after the other. Similar: 順番に; 次々に stresses a rapid succession.","examples":[{"japanese":"名前を呼ばれた人から、順々に部屋に入ってください。","romaji":"Namae o yobareta hito kara, junjun ni heya ni haitte kudasai.","german":"Please enter the room one by one, starting with the person whose name is called.","furigana":"なまえ|よ|ひと|じゅんじゅん|へや|はい"},{"japanese":"先生は生徒の作文を順々に読んでいった。","romaji":"Sensei wa seito no sakubun o junjun ni yonde itta.","german":"The teacher read the students' compositions one after another.","furigana":"せんせい|せいと|さくぶん|じゅんじゅん|よ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"な-adjective and noun: 純情な青年 (an innocent young man). Describes a pure, inexperienced nature, especially in love.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は純情で、すぐに顔が赤くなる。","romaji":"Kare wa junjō de, sugu ni kao ga akaku naru.","german":"He is so innocent that he blushes right away.","furigana":"かれ|じゅんじょう|かお|あか"},{"japanese":"純情な少女の恋を描いた映画を見た。","romaji":"Junjō na shōjo no koi o egaita eiga o mita.","german":"I saw a film depicting the love of an innocent girl.","furigana":"じゅんじょう|しょうじょ|こい|えが|えいが|み"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Mathematical term. The results of the basic operations are called 和 (sum), 差 (difference), 積 (product) and 商 (quotient).","examples":[{"japanese":"十を二で割った商は五です。","romaji":"Jū o ni de watta shō wa go desu.","german":"Ten divided by two gives a quotient of five.","furigana":"じゅう|に|わ|しょう|ご"},{"japanese":"割り算の商と余りを求めなさい。","romaji":"Warizan no shō to amari o motomenasai.","german":"Find the quotient and remainder of the division.","furigana":"わ|ざん|しょう|あま|もと"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A suffix with two meanings: form or state (液状 = liquid, 粉状 = powdery) and written document (招待状 = invitation, 年賀状 = New Year's card).","examples":[{"japanese":"友達の結婚式の招待状が届いた。","romaji":"Tomodachi no kekkonshiki no shōtaijō ga todoita.","german":"An invitation to my friend's wedding arrived.","furigana":"ともだち|けっこんしき|しょうたいじょう|とど"},{"japanese":"この薬は粉状なので、水と一緒に飲んでください。","romaji":"Kono kusuri wa funjō na node, mizu to issho ni nonde kudasai.","german":"This medicine is in powder form, so please take it with water.","furigana":"くすり|ふんじょう|みず|いっしょ|の"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Colloquial short form of しようがない, same meaning as 仕方がない: one has to accept it. With 〜ても: \"there's no use\" (悔やんでもしょうがない). After the て-form also \"terribly, unbearably\": 眠くてしょうがない (I'm terribly sleepy).","examples":[{"japanese":"雨で中止になったのはしょうがない。","romaji":"Ame de chūshi ni natta no wa shōganai.","german":"It can't be helped that it was canceled due to rain.","furigana":"あめ|ちゅうし"},{"japanese":"終わったことを悔やんでもしょうがないよ。","romaji":"Owatta koto o kuyande mo shōganai yo.","german":"There's no use regretting what's already over.","furigana":"お|く"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Top and bottom (上下を逆にする), up-and-down movement (上下に動く) and difference in rank (上下関係 = hierarchy). The reading うえした refers more concretely to \"above and below\".","examples":[{"japanese":"この箱は上下を逆にしないでください。","romaji":"Kono hako wa jōge o gyaku ni shinaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't turn this box upside down.","furigana":"はこ|じょうげ|ぎゃく"},{"japanese":"エレベーターが上下に動いている。","romaji":"Erebētā ga jōge ni ugoite iru.","german":"The elevator is moving up and down.","furigana":"じょうげ|うご"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Railway and official language: 乗車券 (ticket), ご乗車ください. Opposite: 下車 (getting off). In everyday speech: 乗る.","examples":[{"japanese":"ご乗車の際は、足元にご注意ください。","romaji":"Gojōsha no sai wa, ashimoto ni gochūi kudasai.","german":"When boarding, please watch your step.","furigana":"じょうしゃ|さい|あしもと|ちゅうい"},{"japanese":"駆け込み乗車は危ないのでおやめください。","romaji":"Kakekomi jōsha wa abunai node oyame kudasai.","german":"Please refrain from rushing to board at the last moment, as it is dangerous.","furigana":"か|こ|じょうしゃ|あぶ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A number with digits after the decimal point; the decimal point is called 小数点. Homophone: 少数 (small number, minority).","examples":[{"japanese":"答えは小数で書いてください。","romaji":"Kotae wa shōsū de kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write the answer as a decimal.","furigana":"こた|しょうすう|か"},{"japanese":"分数を小数に直す問題が出た。","romaji":"Bunsū o shōsū ni naosu mondai ga deta.","german":"There was a problem about converting fractions into decimals.","furigana":"ぶんすう|しょうすう|なお|もんだい|で"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Rather a small retail shop: 商店街 (shopping street). In everyday speech: 店.","examples":[{"japanese":"この通りには古い商店が並んでいる。","romaji":"Kono tōri ni wa furui shōten ga narande iru.","german":"Old shops line this street.","furigana":"とお|ふる|しょうてん|なら"},{"japanese":"祖父は町で小さな商店を経営していた。","romaji":"Sofu wa machi de chiisa na shōten o keiei shite ita.","german":"My grandfather ran a small shop in town.","furigana":"そふ|まち|ちい|しょうてん|けいえい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"An item won in a contest; a cash prize is called 賞金. Homophone: 商品 (goods).","examples":[{"japanese":"クイズに正解して、賞品をもらった。","romaji":"Kuizu ni seikai shite, shōhin o moratta.","german":"I answered the quiz correctly and received a prize.","furigana":"せいかい|しょうひん"},{"japanese":"一等の賞品は旅行券だった。","romaji":"Ittō no shōhin wa ryokōken datta.","german":"The first prize was a travel voucher.","furigana":"いっとう|しょうひん|りょこうけん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Rather vulgar; politely one says お手洗いに行く, medically 尿（にょう）. 立ち小便 = urinating outdoors.","examples":[{"japanese":"子どもが寝る前に小便に行った。","romaji":"Kodomo ga neru mae ni shōben ni itta.","german":"The child went to pee before going to sleep.","furigana":"こ|ね|まえ|しょうべん|い"},{"japanese":"公園の壁に「ここで小便をするな」と書いてあった。","romaji":"Kōen no kabe ni \"koko de shōben o suru na\" to kaite atta.","german":"On the park wall was written, \"Do not urinate here.\"","furigana":"こうえん|かべ|しょうべん|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"After deducting packaging or breaks: 正味の重さ (net weight), 正味三時間 (three hours net).","examples":[{"japanese":"箱を除いた正味の重さは五キロだ。","romaji":"Hako o nozoita shōmi no omosa wa go kiro da.","german":"The net weight without the box is five kilograms.","furigana":"はこ|のぞ|しょうみ|おも|ご"},{"japanese":"休憩を除くと、正味二時間働いた。","romaji":"Kyūkei o nozoku to, shōmi nijikan hataraita.","german":"Excluding breaks, I actually worked two hours.","furigana":"きゅうけい|のぞ|しょうみにじかんはたら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Former job title at Japanese universities; since 2007 this position has officially been called 准教授（じゅんきょうじゅ）. Still common in older texts.","examples":[{"japanese":"父は昔、大学の助教授だった。","romaji":"Chichi wa mukashi, daigaku no jokyōju datta.","german":"My father used to be an associate professor at a university.","furigana":"ちち|むかし|だいがく|じょきょうじゅ"},{"japanese":"その小説の主人公は若い助教授だ。","romaji":"Sono shōsetsu no shujinkō wa wakai jokyōju da.","german":"The protagonist of that novel is a young associate professor.","furigana":"しょうせつ|しゅじんこう|わか|じょきょうじゅ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"More technical and used on packaging; in everyday speech simply 塩. 食塩水 = salt solution.","examples":[{"japanese":"スープに食塩を少し加えてください。","romaji":"Sūpu ni shokuen o sukoshi kuwaete kudasai.","german":"Please add a little salt to the soup.","furigana":"しょくえん|すこ|くわ"},{"japanese":"この食品には食塩が多く含まれている。","romaji":"Kono shokuhin ni wa shokuen ga ōku fukumarete iru.","german":"This food contains a lot of salt.","furigana":"しょくひん|しょくえん|おお|ふく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Someone who has mastered a trade: 寿司職人, 家具職人. 職人気質 = a craftsman's work ethic.","examples":[{"japanese":"この机は職人が一つ一つ手で作っている。","romaji":"Kono tsukue wa shokunin ga hitotsu hitotsu te de tsukutte iru.","german":"A craftsman makes each of these desks by hand.","furigana":"つくえ|しょくにん|ひと|ひと|て|つく"},{"japanese":"彼は十年の修業を経て、一人前の職人になった。","romaji":"Kare wa jūnen no shugyō o hete, ichininmae no shokunin ni natta.","german":"After ten years of training, he became a full-fledged craftsman.","furigana":"かれ|じゅうねん|しゅぎょう|へ|いちにんまえ|しょくにん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A month is divided into three parts: 初旬／上旬 (1st-10th), 中旬 (11th-20th), 下旬 (21st to the end of the month).","examples":[{"japanese":"来月の初旬に引っ越す予定です。","romaji":"Raigetsu no shojun ni hikkosu yotei desu.","german":"I plan to move at the beginning of next month.","furigana":"らいげつ|しょじゅん|ひ|こ|よてい"},{"japanese":"桜は四月初旬に満開になった。","romaji":"Sakura wa shigatsu shojun ni mankai ni natta.","german":"The cherry blossoms were in full bloom in early April.","furigana":"さくら|しがつしょじゅん|まんかい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Mostly for modern shops or in compounds: コーヒーショップ, ネットショップ. In general: 店.","examples":[{"japanese":"駅の中に新しいショップができた。","romaji":"Eki no naka ni atarashii shoppu ga dekita.","german":"A new shop has opened in the station.","furigana":"えき|なか|あたら"},{"japanese":"このショップはかわいい雑貨を売っている。","romaji":"Kono shoppu wa kawaii zakka o utte iru.","german":"This shop sells cute miscellaneous goods.","furigana":"ざっか|う"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Somewhat more formal than 本屋. Online bookstore: オンライン書店.","examples":[{"japanese":"駅前の書店で新しい辞書を買った。","romaji":"Ekimae no shoten de atarashii jisho o katta.","german":"I bought a new dictionary at the bookstore in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|しょてん|あたら|じしょ|か"},{"japanese":"この本は全国の書店で発売中です。","romaji":"Kono hon wa zenkoku no shoten de hatsubaichū desu.","german":"This book is on sale at bookstores nationwide.","furigana":"ほん|ぜんこく|しょてん|はつばいちゅう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A series of related books, films or products: 人気シリーズ, シリーズ第二弾 (second installment).","examples":[{"japanese":"このシリーズの本は全部読んだ。","romaji":"Kono shirīzu no hon wa zenbu yonda.","german":"I've read all the books in this series.","furigana":"ほん|ぜんぶよ"},{"japanese":"映画のシリーズの最新作が公開された。","romaji":"Eiga no shirīzu no saishinsaku ga kōkai sareta.","german":"The latest film in the series has been released.","furigana":"えいが|さいしんさく|こうかい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Liquid from fruit or food (レモンの汁) and Japanese soup: 味噌汁, 豚汁. Politely also お汁（おしる）.","examples":[{"japanese":"レモンの汁を少しかけて食べる。","romaji":"Remon no shiru o sukoshi kakete taberu.","german":"I eat it with a little lemon juice squeezed over it.","furigana":"しる|すこ|た"},{"japanese":"この汁は昆布でだしを取っている。","romaji":"Kono shiru wa konbu de dashi o totte iru.","german":"The broth for this soup is made from kombu.","furigana":"しる|こんぶ|と"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Wrinkles in skin (目じりのしわ) and creases in fabric or paper: しわになる (to get wrinkled), しわを伸ばす (to smooth out). Kanji: 皺 (rare).","examples":[{"japanese":"祖母の顔には優しいしわがある。","romaji":"Sobo no kao ni wa yasashii shiwa ga aru.","german":"My grandmother's face has gentle wrinkles.","furigana":"そぼ|かお|やさ"},{"japanese":"シャツのしわをアイロンで伸ばした。","romaji":"Shatsu no shiwa o airon de nobashita.","german":"I smoothed out the wrinkles in the shirt with an iron.","furigana":"の"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The innermost part: 鉛筆の芯 (pencil lead), りんごの芯 (apple core), ろうそくの芯 (candle wick). Figurative: 芯が強い (strong inside).","examples":[{"japanese":"鉛筆の芯が折れてしまった。","romaji":"Enpitsu no shin ga orete shimatta.","german":"The pencil lead broke.","furigana":"えんぴつ|しん|お"},{"japanese":"彼女は見た目は優しいが、芯が強い。","romaji":"Kanojo wa mitame wa yasashii ga, shin ga tsuyoi.","german":"She looks gentle, but she has a strong core.","furigana":"かのじょ|み|め|やさ|しん|つよ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Formal or technical word for bed: 寝台車 (sleeping car), 寝台列車 (night train with sleeping cars). In everyday speech: ベッド.","examples":[{"japanese":"夜行列車の寝台で一晩過ごした。","romaji":"Yakō ressha no shindai de hitoban sugoshita.","german":"I spent a night in a berth on an overnight train.","furigana":"やこうれっしゃ|しんだい|ひとばんす"},{"japanese":"病院の寝台に横になって検査を受けた。","romaji":"Byōin no shindai ni yoko ni natte kensa o uketa.","german":"I lay down on the bed at the hospital and had an examination.","furigana":"びょういん|しんだい|よこ|けんさ|う"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Sciences of human beings and their culture, such as literature, history and philosophy. Compare 社会科学 and 自然科学.","examples":[{"japanese":"人文科学には文学や歴史学が含まれる。","romaji":"Jinbunkagaku ni wa bungaku ya rekishigaku ga fukumareru.","german":"The humanities include literature and history.","furigana":"じんぶんかがく|ぶんがく|れきしがく|ふく"},{"japanese":"彼女は人文科学の研究者だ。","romaji":"Kanojo wa jinbunkagaku no kenkyūsha da.","german":"She is a researcher in the humanities.","furigana":"かのじょ|じんぶんかがく|けんきゅうしゃ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Formal, often in news: 人命救助 (lifesaving), 人命にかかわる (life-threatening). Homophone: 人名 (personal name).","examples":[{"japanese":"何よりも人命を優先しなければならない。","romaji":"Nani yori mo jinmei o yūsen shinakereba naranai.","german":"Human life must be given priority above everything else.","furigana":"なに|じんめい|ゆうせん"},{"japanese":"この事故で多くの人命が失われた。","romaji":"Kono jiko de ōku no jinmei ga ushinawareta.","german":"Many lives were lost in this accident.","furigana":"じこ|おお|じんめい|うしな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Strictly the compass heading of a ship or aircraft: 針路を変える. For one's own future (studies, career) one writes 進路.","examples":[{"japanese":"船は針路を南に変えた。","romaji":"Fune wa shinro o minami ni kaeta.","german":"The ship changed its course to the south.","furigana":"ふね|しんろ|みなみ|か"},{"japanese":"台風は針路を東に変えた。","romaji":"Taifū wa shinro o higashi ni kaeta.","german":"The typhoon changed its course to the east.","furigana":"たいふう|しんろ|ひがし|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Catching fish and seafood: 水産業 (fishing industry), 水産物 (marine products).","examples":[{"japanese":"この町は昔から水産で栄えてきた。","romaji":"Kono machi wa mukashi kara suisan de sakaete kita.","german":"This town has prospered on fisheries since long ago.","furigana":"まち|むかし|すいさん|さか"},{"japanese":"兄は大学で水産を学んでいる。","romaji":"Ani wa daigaku de suisan o manande iru.","german":"My older brother is studying fisheries science at university.","furigana":"あに|だいがく|すいさん|まな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Water in gaseous form; the visible steam above hot water is called 湯気（ゆげ）in everyday speech.","examples":[{"japanese":"水を熱すると水蒸気になる。","romaji":"Mizu o nessuru to suijōki ni naru.","german":"When water is heated, it turns into water vapor.","furigana":"みず|ねっ|すいじょうき"},{"japanese":"空気中の水蒸気が冷えて雲ができる。","romaji":"Kūkichū no suijōki ga hiete kumo ga dekiru.","german":"Water vapor in the air cools and forms clouds.","furigana":"くうきちゅう|すいじょうき|ひ|くも"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A reusable bottle for drinks on the go; a disposable plastic bottle is called ペットボトル.","examples":[{"japanese":"遠足には水筒を持っていきましょう。","romaji":"Ensoku ni wa suitō o motte ikimashō.","german":"Let's bring a water bottle on the field trip.","furigana":"えんそく|すいとう|も"},{"japanese":"水筒にお茶を入れて出かけた。","romaji":"Suitō ni ocha o irete dekaketa.","german":"I filled my water bottle with tea and went out.","furigana":"すいとう|ちゃ|い|で"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Water content or fluid intake: 水分をとる (take in fluids), 水分が多い (juicy, watery).","examples":[{"japanese":"暑い日はこまめに水分をとってください。","romaji":"Atsui hi wa komame ni suibun o totte kudasai.","german":"On hot days, please drink fluids frequently.","furigana":"あつ|ひ|すいぶん"},{"japanese":"このすいかは水分が多くて甘い。","romaji":"Kono suika wa suibun ga ōkute amai.","german":"This watermelon is juicy and sweet.","furigana":"すいぶん|おお|あま"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Noun and な-adjective: 水平に置く (place horizontally). Opposite: 垂直 (vertical).","examples":[{"japanese":"棚が水平になっているか確認した。","romaji":"Tana ga suihei ni natte iru ka kakunin shita.","german":"I checked whether the shelf was level.","furigana":"たな|すいへい|かくにん"},{"japanese":"腕を水平に上げてください。","romaji":"Ude o suihei ni agete kudasai.","german":"Please raise your arms horizontally.","furigana":"うで|すいへい|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The line where sea and sky meet; over land one speaks of 地平線.","examples":[{"japanese":"水平線に夕日が沈んでいく。","romaji":"Suiheisen ni yūhi ga shizunde iku.","german":"The evening sun is sinking on the horizon.","furigana":"すいへいせん|ゆうひ|しず"},{"japanese":"水平線の近くに船が一隻見えた。","romaji":"Suiheisen no chikaku ni fune ga isseki mieta.","german":"I could see one ship near the horizon.","furigana":"すいへいせん|ちか|ふね|いっせきみ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Surface of water: 水面に映る (be reflected on the water). Poetically also read みなも.","examples":[{"japanese":"池の水面に月が映っている。","romaji":"Ike no suimen ni tsuki ga utsutte iru.","german":"The moon is reflected on the surface of the pond.","furigana":"いけ|すいめん|つき|うつ"},{"japanese":"魚が水面から跳ねた。","romaji":"Sakana ga suimen kara haneta.","german":"A fish leaped from the surface of the water.","furigana":"さかな|すいめん|は"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A light cloth for the neck or head; a thick winter scarf is called マフラー.","examples":[{"japanese":"母の誕生日にスカーフをプレゼントした。","romaji":"Haha no tanjōbi ni sukāfu o purezento shita.","german":"I gave my mother a scarf for her birthday.","furigana":"はは|たんじょうび"},{"japanese":"風が強いので、スカーフを頭に巻いた。","romaji":"Kaze ga tsuyoi node, sukāfu o atama ni maita.","german":"Since the wind was strong, I tied a scarf around my head.","furigana":"かぜ|つよ|あたま|ま"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A book with many illustrations on one subject: 植物図鑑, 動物図鑑 - very popular with children in Japan.","examples":[{"japanese":"図鑑で鳥の名前を調べた。","romaji":"Zukan de tori no namae o shirabeta.","german":"I looked up the bird's name in the field guide.","furigana":"ずかん|とり|なまえ|しら"},{"japanese":"息子は恐竜の図鑑を毎日見ている。","romaji":"Musuko wa kyōryū no zukan o mainichi mite iru.","german":"My son looks at his dinosaur book every day.","furigana":"むすこ|きょうりゅう|ずかん|まいにちみ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A gap in space or time and a moment of inattention: 隙を見て (seizing a good moment), 隙がない (to show no weakness). Narrow gap: 隙間. Homophone: 好き.","examples":[{"japanese":"相手の隙をついて、ゴールを決めた。","romaji":"Aite no suki o tsuite, gōru o kimeta.","german":"I took advantage of my opponent's opening and scored a goal.","furigana":"あいて|すき|き"},{"japanese":"彼女はいつもきちんとしていて、隙がない。","romaji":"Kanojo wa itsumo kichinto shite ite, suki ga nai.","german":"She is always neat and proper, and shows no weak spot.","furigana":"かのじょ|すき"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Japan's most common timber tree; its pollen (杉花粉) is a main cause of hay fever in spring.","examples":[{"japanese":"山の斜面に杉の木が並んでいる。","romaji":"Yama no shamen ni sugi no ki ga narande iru.","german":"Cedar trees line the mountain slope.","furigana":"やま|しゃめん|すぎ|き|なら"},{"japanese":"春になると杉の花粉が飛ぶ。","romaji":"Haru ni naru to sugi no kafun ga tobu.","german":"When spring comes, cedar pollen flies.","furigana":"はる|すぎ|かふん|と"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Often about food: 好き嫌いが多い (to be very picky), 好き嫌いしないで食べなさい.","examples":[{"japanese":"うちの子は食べ物の好き嫌いが多い。","romaji":"Uchi no ko wa tabemono no sukikirai ga ōi.","german":"Our child is very picky about food.","furigana":"こ|た|もの|す|きら|おお"},{"japanese":"仕事の好き嫌いを言っている場合ではない。","romaji":"Shigoto no sukikirai o itte iru baai de wa nai.","german":"This is no time to be picky about work.","furigana":"しごと|す|きら|い|ばあい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Mostly 好き好きだ or 人の好き好き: that is a matter of taste. Compare 十人十色.","examples":[{"japanese":"どちらの色がいいかは好き好きだ。","romaji":"Dochira no iro ga ii ka wa sukizuki da.","german":"Which color is better is a matter of taste.","furigana":"いろ|す|ず"},{"japanese":"辛い料理が好きかどうかは人の好き好きです。","romaji":"Karai ryōri ga suki ka dō ka wa hito no sukizuki desu.","german":"Whether one likes spicy food is a matter of personal taste.","furigana":"から|りょうり|す|ひと|す|ず"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Mostly for private schools and courses: 英会話スクール, 料理スクール. A regular school is called 学校.","examples":[{"japanese":"駅前のスクールでダンスを習っている。","romaji":"Ekimae no sukūru de dansu o naratte iru.","german":"I'm learning dance at a school in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|なら"},{"japanese":"このスクールには子ども向けのクラスもある。","romaji":"Kono sukūru ni wa kodomo muke no kurasu mo aru.","german":"This school also has classes for children.","furigana":"こ|む"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"In mathematics: triangles, circles, etc. 図形の問題 = geometry problem.","examples":[{"japanese":"三角形や円などの図形の面積を求めた。","romaji":"Sankakkei ya en nado no zukei no menseki o motometa.","german":"We calculated the area of figures such as triangles and circles.","furigana":"さんかっけい|えん|ずけい|めんせき|もと"},{"japanese":"この図形を半分に折ると、ぴったり重なる。","romaji":"Kono zukei o hanbun ni oru to, pittari kasanaru.","german":"If you fold this figure in half, the two halves overlap exactly.","furigana":"ずけい|はんぶん|お|かさ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"As a verb スタートする (to start, to begin). スタートライン = starting line.","examples":[{"japanese":"新しいドラマは来週スタートする。","romaji":"Atarashii dorama wa raishū sutāto suru.","german":"The new drama series starts next week.","furigana":"あたら|らいしゅう"},{"japanese":"スタートの合図で選手が一斉に走り出した。","romaji":"Sutāto no aizu de senshu ga issei ni hashiridashita.","german":"At the start signal, the athletes all began running at once.","furigana":"あいず|せんしゅ|いっせい|はし|だ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 45"},
{"notes":"An outdated term; today people prefer 客室乗務員 or CA (キャビンアテンダント), which also include men.","examples":[{"japanese":"子どものころ、スチュワーデスになるのが夢だった。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, suchuwādesu ni naru no ga yume datta.","german":"When I was a child, my dream was to become a stewardess.","furigana":"こ|ゆめ"},{"japanese":"スチュワーデスが飲み物を配っている。","romaji":"Suchuwādesu ga nomimono o kubatte iru.","german":"The stewardess is handing out drinks.","furigana":"の|もの|くば"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Stage and performance (ステージに立つ), also a phase or game level (次のステージ).","examples":[{"japanese":"歌手がステージに登場すると、会場が盛り上がった。","romaji":"Kashu ga sutēji ni tōjō suru to, kaijō ga moriagatta.","german":"When the singer appeared on stage, the hall came alive.","furigana":"かしゅ|とうじょう|かいじょう|も|あ"},{"japanese":"このゲームは次のステージに進むのが難しい。","romaji":"Kono gēmu wa tsugi no sutēji ni susumu no ga muzukashii.","german":"In this game, it is hard to get to the next stage.","furigana":"つぎ|すす|むずか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Thin nylon stockings or pantyhose; thick tights are called タイツ, socks 靴下.","examples":[{"japanese":"ストッキングが伝線してしまった。","romaji":"Sutokkingu ga densen shite shimatta.","german":"My stockings got a run.","furigana":"でんせん"},{"japanese":"黒いストッキングをはいて出かけた。","romaji":"Kuroi sutokkingu o haite dekaketa.","german":"I put on black stockings and went out.","furigana":"くろ|で"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"As a verb ストップする (to stop, halt); also as an exclamation: ストップ！","examples":[{"japanese":"雪で電車がストップした。","romaji":"Yuki de densha ga sutoppu shita.","german":"The trains stopped because of the snow.","furigana":"ゆき|でんしゃ"},{"japanese":"ちょっとストップ！それ以上進まないで。","romaji":"Chotto sutoppu! Sore ijō susumanaide.","german":"Stop for a moment! Don't go any further.","furigana":"いじょうすす"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Often figurative: 頭脳明晰 (sharp-minded), チームの頭脳 (the brains of the team). The organ itself is usually called 脳.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼はチームの頭脳と呼ばれている。","romaji":"Kare wa chīmu no zunō to yobarete iru.","german":"He is called the brains of the team.","furigana":"かれ|ずのう|よ"},{"japanese":"この仕事には優れた頭脳が必要だ。","romaji":"Kono shigoto ni wa sugureta zunō ga hitsuyō da.","german":"This job requires a sharp mind.","furigana":"しごと|すぐ|ずのう|ひつよう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Loudspeaker, e.g. ワイヤレススピーカー (wireless speaker). Rarely also \"speaker (a person giving a talk)\".","examples":[{"japanese":"スピーカーから音楽が流れている。","romaji":"Supīkā kara ongaku ga nagarete iru.","german":"Music is coming from the speaker.","furigana":"おんがく|なが"},{"japanese":"スピーカーの音が大きすぎる。","romaji":"Supīkā no oto ga ōkisugiru.","german":"The speaker is too loud.","furigana":"おと|おお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Collective term for graphics and tables in reports: 図表を使って説明する.","examples":[{"japanese":"結果を図表にまとめた。","romaji":"Kekka o zuhyō ni matometa.","german":"I summarized the results in charts and tables.","furigana":"けっか|ずひょう"},{"japanese":"この図表を見ると、人口が減っているのが分かる。","romaji":"Kono zuhyō o miru to, jinkō ga hette iru no ga wakaru.","german":"Looking at this chart, you can see that the population is decreasing.","furigana":"ずひょう|み|じんこう|へ|わ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Somewhat more refined than 家: お住まいはどちらですか (Where do you live?). 一人住まい = living alone.","examples":[{"japanese":"お住まいはどちらですか。","romaji":"Osumai wa dochira desu ka.","german":"Where do you live?","furigana":"す"},{"japanese":"駅から近い住まいを探している。","romaji":"Eki kara chikai sumai o sagashite iru.","german":"I'm looking for a place to live close to the station.","furigana":"えき|ちか|す|さが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"な-adjective. Unlike English \"smart\" (clever), it usually means \"slim\" (スマートな体) or \"stylish, smooth\" (スマートな対応); \"intelligent\" only in compounds such as スマートフォン.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は背が高くてスマートだ。","romaji":"Kare wa se ga takakute sumāto da.","german":"He is tall and slim.","furigana":"かれ|せ|たか"},{"japanese":"店員のスマートな対応に感心した。","romaji":"Ten'in no sumāto na taiō ni kanshin shita.","german":"I was impressed by the shop assistant's smooth handling.","furigana":"てんいん|たいおう|かんしん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Casual apology, especially used by men or older people: すまない、遅れた. Polite: すみません. Also \"to have wronged someone\": 彼にすまないことをした.","examples":[{"japanese":"すまない、少し遅れる。","romaji":"Sumanai, sukoshi okureru.","german":"Sorry, I'll be a little late.","furigana":"すこ|おく"},{"japanese":"彼にはすまないことをしたと思っている。","romaji":"Kare ni wa sumanai koto o shita to omotte iru.","german":"I feel I did him wrong.","furigana":"かれ|おも"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Suffix for things that are completed: 支払い済み (paid), 予約済み (reserved), 確認済み (checked).","examples":[{"japanese":"この席はもう予約済みです。","romaji":"Kono seki wa mō yoyakuzumi desu.","german":"This seat has already been reserved.","furigana":"せき|よやくず"},{"japanese":"会費は支払い済みです。","romaji":"Kaihi wa shiharaizumi desu.","german":"The membership fee has already been paid.","furigana":"かいひ|しはら|ず"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"A slide of a presentation or a photographic slide. As a verb スライドする: to slide, to shift.","examples":[{"japanese":"発表で使うスライドを作った。","romaji":"Happyō de tsukau suraido o tsukutta.","german":"I made the slides to use in the presentation.","furigana":"はっぴょう|つか|つく"},{"japanese":"次のスライドをご覧ください。","romaji":"Tsugi no suraido o goran kudasai.","german":"Please look at the next slide.","furigana":"つぎ|らん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Many things or people standing in an impressive line: ずらりと並ぶ. Also written ズラリ.","examples":[{"japanese":"店の前に客がずらりと並んでいた。","romaji":"Mise no mae ni kyaku ga zurari to narande ita.","german":"Customers were lined up in a long row in front of the shop.","furigana":"みせ|まえ|きゃく|なら"},{"japanese":"棚には古い本がずらりと並んでいる。","romaji":"Tana ni wa furui hon ga zurari to narande iru.","german":"Old books are lined up in long rows on the shelf.","furigana":"たな|ふる|ほん|なら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Formal word for surname, e.g. in forms (姓・名). In everyday speech: 名字（みょうじ）.","examples":[{"japanese":"結婚して姓が変わった。","romaji":"Kekkon shite sei ga kawatta.","german":"My surname changed after I got married.","furigana":"けっこん|せい|か"},{"japanese":"ここに姓と名を分けて書いてください。","romaji":"Koko ni sei to mei o wakete kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write your surname and given name separately here.","furigana":"せい|めい|わ|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Creating artistic or media works: 映画を制作する, 番組制作. For industrial manufacturing, 製作 or 製造 is usually written.","examples":[{"japanese":"この映画の制作には三年かかった。","romaji":"Kono eiga no seisaku ni wa sannen kakatta.","german":"This film took three years to make.","furigana":"えいが|せいさく|さんねん"},{"japanese":"彼女は今、新しい作品を制作している。","romaji":"Kanojo wa ima, atarashii sakuhin o seisaku shite iru.","german":"She is currently creating a new work.","furigana":"かのじょ|いま|あたら|さくひん|せいさく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The clean final version after the draft (下書き); as a verb 清書する.","examples":[{"japanese":"下書きを見直してから清書した。","romaji":"Shitagaki o minaoshite kara seisho shita.","german":"I reviewed the draft and then made a fair copy.","furigana":"したが|みなお|せいしょ"},{"japanese":"年賀状は清書する前に鉛筆で書いてみる。","romaji":"Nengajō wa seisho suru mae ni enpitsu de kaite miru.","german":"For New Year's cards, I try writing in pencil before making the fair copy.","furigana":"ねんがじょう|せいしょ|まえ|えんぴつ|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Formal, e.g. in official and news language: 青少年の健全な育成 (healthy development of youth).","examples":[{"japanese":"市は青少年のためのスポーツ施設を作った。","romaji":"Shi wa seishōnen no tame no supōtsu shisetsu o tsukutta.","german":"The city built a sports facility for young people.","furigana":"し|せいしょうねん|しせつ|つく"},{"japanese":"インターネットが青少年に与える影響が心配されている。","romaji":"Intānetto ga seishōnen ni ataeru eikyō ga shinpai sarete iru.","german":"There is concern about the influence of the internet on young people.","furigana":"せいしょうねん|あた|えいきょう|しんぱい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Mathematics: 正の整数 (positive integer). Compare 小数 (decimal), 分数 (fraction).","examples":[{"japanese":"一から十までの整数を全部足してみよう。","romaji":"Ichi kara jū made no seisū o zenbu tashite miyō.","german":"Let's add up all the integers from one to ten.","furigana":"いち|じゅう|せいすう|ぜんぶた"},{"japanese":"三・五は整数ではない。","romaji":"San ten go wa seisū de wa nai.","german":"3.5 is not an integer."}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Formal word for cleaning, especially of facilities and public places: 清掃中 (under cleaning), 清掃員 (cleaner). In everyday speech: 掃除.","examples":[{"japanese":"ただいまトイレは清掃中です。","romaji":"Tadaima toire wa seisōchū desu.","german":"The restroom is currently being cleaned.","furigana":"せいそうちゅう"},{"japanese":"住民が集まって川の清掃をした。","romaji":"Jūmin ga atsumatte kawa no seisō o shita.","german":"The residents gathered and cleaned the river.","furigana":"じゅうみん|あつ|かわ|せいそう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Used mainly for plants; growth of people and animals and economic growth are written 成長.","examples":[{"japanese":"日光が足りないと、植物の生長が遅くなる。","romaji":"Nikkō ga tarinai to, shokubutsu no seichō ga osoku naru.","german":"If there isn't enough sunlight, plants grow more slowly.","furigana":"にっこう|た|しょくぶつ|せいちょう|おそ"},{"japanese":"稲の生長を毎日観察した。","romaji":"Ine no seichō o mainichi kansatsu shita.","german":"I observed the growth of the rice plants every day.","furigana":"いね|せいちょう|まいにちかんさつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Four equal sides and right angles. Rectangle: 長方形.","examples":[{"japanese":"紙を切って、正方形を作った。","romaji":"Kami o kitte, seihōkei o tsukutta.","german":"I cut the paper to make a square.","furigana":"かみ|き|せいほうけい|つく"},{"japanese":"一辺が五センチの正方形の面積を求めなさい。","romaji":"Ippen ga go senchi no seihōkei no menseki o motomenasai.","german":"Find the area of a square with sides of five centimeters.","furigana":"いっぺん|ご|せいほうけい|めんせき|もと"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The main entrance of a school, company or temple. Back gate: 裏門.","examples":[{"japanese":"学校の正門の前で待ち合わせた。","romaji":"Gakkō no seimon no mae de machiawaseta.","german":"We agreed to meet in front of the school's main gate.","furigana":"がっこう|せいもん|まえ|ま|あ"},{"japanese":"夜は正門が閉まるので、裏門を使ってください。","romaji":"Yoru wa seimon ga shimaru node, uramon o tsukatte kudasai.","german":"The main gate closes at night, so please use the back gate.","furigana":"よる|せいもん|し|うらもん|つか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Emphatic form of ぜひ for requests and wishes made with force; also written 是非とも.","examples":[{"japanese":"この仕事はぜひとも成功させたい。","romaji":"Kono shigoto wa zehitomo seikō sasetai.","german":"I really want to make this job a success.","furigana":"しごと|せいこう"},{"japanese":"次回はぜひともご参加ください。","romaji":"Jikai wa zehitomo gosanka kudasai.","german":"Please be sure to take part next time.","furigana":"じかい|さんか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Kurzform von ゼミナール (aus dem Deutschen „Seminar“). An japanischen Universitäten oft eine feste Kleingruppe bei einer Lehrperson.","examples":[{"japanese":"来週のゼミで発表をすることになった。","romaji":"Raishū no zemi de happyō o suru koto ni natta.","german":"Ich soll nächste Woche im Seminar ein Referat halten.","furigana":"らいしゅう|はっぴょう"},{"japanese":"ゼミの仲間と旅行に行った。","romaji":"Zemi no nakama to ryokō ni itta.","german":"Ich bin mit meinen Seminarkollegen verreist.","furigana":"なかま|りょこう|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A binder for construction; with sand and water it becomes mortar (モルタル), and with gravel added, concrete (コンクリート).","examples":[{"japanese":"セメントに砂と水を混ぜてモルタルを作る。","romaji":"Semento ni suna to mizu o mazete morutaru o tsukuru.","german":"Mixing cement with sand and water makes mortar.","furigana":"すな|みず|ま|つく"},{"japanese":"道路の穴をセメントで埋めた。","romaji":"Dōro no ana o semento de umeta.","german":"The hole in the road was filled with cement.","furigana":"どうろ|あな|う"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Seal of a bottle or tub: 栓を抜く (uncork, pull the plug); also ガスの元栓 (main gas valve) and 耳栓 (earplugs).","examples":[{"japanese":"ワインの栓を抜いた。","romaji":"Wain no sen o nuita.","german":"I pulled the cork out of the wine.","furigana":"せん|ぬ"},{"japanese":"お風呂の栓をしてからお湯を入れてください。","romaji":"Ofuro no sen o shite kara oyu o irete kudasai.","german":"Please put in the plug first, and then run the hot water in the bath.","furigana":"ふろ|せん|ゆ|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Spatial (バスの前後), temporal (食事の前後) and, after numbers, approximate: 三十歳前後 (around thirty years old).","examples":[{"japanese":"バスの前後に自転車が止まっている。","romaji":"Basu no zengo ni jitensha ga tomatte iru.","german":"Bicycles are parked in front of and behind the bus.","furigana":"ぜんご|じてんしゃ|と"},{"japanese":"参加者は二十人前後になりそうだ。","romaji":"Sankasha wa nijūnin zengo ni narisō da.","german":"There look to be around twenty participants.","furigana":"さんかしゃ|にじゅうにんぜんご"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Rule by one person or group without checks: 専制政治, 専制君主. Homophone: 先生.","examples":[{"japanese":"国王は長い間専制を続けた。","romaji":"Kokuō wa nagai aida sensei o tsuzuketa.","german":"The king continued despotic rule for a long time.","furigana":"こくおう|なが|あいだせんせい|つづ"},{"japanese":"人々は専制に反対して立ち上がった。","romaji":"Hitobito wa sensei ni hantai shite tachiagatta.","german":"The people rose up against despotism.","furigana":"ひとびと|せんせい|はんたい|た|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Short for センチメートル (cm); for counting see also the counter センチ. Formerly also short for \"sentimental\".","examples":[{"japanese":"この箱の高さは三十センチです。","romaji":"Kono hako no takasa wa sanjussenchi desu.","german":"This box is thirty centimeters high.","furigana":"はこ|たか|さんじゅっ"},{"japanese":"髪を五センチぐらい切ってください。","romaji":"Kami o go senchi gurai kitte kudasai.","german":"Please cut about five centimeters off my hair.","furigana":"かみ|ご|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The foremost position in a line or group: 先頭に立つ (to be at the head, to lead). Homophones: 銭湯 (public bath), 戦闘 (combat).","examples":[{"japanese":"列の先頭の人は朝五時から待っている。","romaji":"Retsu no sentō no hito wa asa goji kara matte iru.","german":"The person at the front of the line has been waiting since five in the morning.","furigana":"れつ|せんとう|ひと|あさごじ|ま"},{"japanese":"マラソンで日本の選手が先頭を走っている。","romaji":"Marason de Nihon no senshu ga sentō o hashitte iru.","german":"In the marathon, a Japanese runner is in the lead.","furigana":"にほん|せんしゅ|せんとう|はし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Introduces a topic one has just remembered; also written そう言えば, colloquially そういや.","examples":[{"japanese":"そういえば、田中さんは元気ですか。","romaji":"Sō ieba, Tanaka-san wa genki desu ka.","german":"By the way, how is Mr. Tanaka doing?","furigana":"たなか|げんき"},{"japanese":"そういえば、明日は母の誕生日だった。","romaji":"Sō ieba, ashita wa haha no tanjōbi datta.","german":"Come to think of it, tomorrow is my mother's birthday.","furigana":"あした|はは|たんじょうび"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"As a verb 増減する: to increase and decrease, to fluctuate.","examples":[{"japanese":"季節によって客の数が増減する。","romaji":"Kisetsu ni yotte kyaku no kazu ga zōgen suru.","german":"The number of customers fluctuates with the season.","furigana":"きせつ|きゃく|かず|ぞうげん"},{"japanese":"人口の増減をグラフで示した。","romaji":"Jinkō no zōgen o gurafu de shimeshita.","german":"I showed the changes in population in a graph.","furigana":"じんこう|ぞうげん|しめ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"そうして connects a sequence in the sense of \"and so, and then\". It can also refer to the manner just described: そうしてできた生地 is the dough made in that way. 然うして is a rare dictionary spelling; the usual form is the kana spelling そうして.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は毎日練習した。そうして、ついに優勝した。","romaji":"Kare wa mainichi renshū shita. Sōshite, tsui ni yūshō shita.","german":"He practiced every day. And so, in the end, he won.","furigana":"かれ|まいにちれんしゅう|ゆうしょう"},{"japanese":"材料を混ぜて、そうしてできた生地を焼く。","romaji":"Zairyō o mazete, sōshite dekita kiji o yaku.","german":"You mix the ingredients and bake the dough made that way.","furigana":"ざいりょう|ま|きじ|や"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5 (source: N5; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"造船所 (shipyard), 造船業 (shipbuilding industry).","examples":[{"japanese":"この町は造船で知られている。","romaji":"Kono machi wa zōsen de shirarete iru.","german":"This town is known for its shipbuilding.","furigana":"まち|ぞうせん|し"},{"japanese":"兄は造船の会社で働いている。","romaji":"Ani wa zōsen no kaisha de hataraite iru.","german":"My older brother works for a shipbuilding company.","furigana":"あに|ぞうせん|かいしゃ|はたら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"い-adjective, stronger than うるさい: 騒々しい音. Also for restless times: 世の中が騒々しい.","examples":[{"japanese":"隣の部屋が騒々しくて眠れない。","romaji":"Tonari no heya ga sōzōshikute nemurenai.","german":"The next room is so noisy that I can't sleep.","furigana":"となり|へや|そうぞう|ねむ"},{"japanese":"最近、世の中が何かと騒々しい。","romaji":"Saikin, yo no naka ga nanika to sōzōshii.","german":"Lately the world has been rather turbulent in many ways.","furigana":"さいきん|よ|なか|なに|そうぞう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Formal; as a verb 増大する: 費用が増大する. Opposite: 減少.","examples":[{"japanese":"医療費の増大が問題になっている。","romaji":"Iryōhi no zōdai ga mondai ni natte iru.","german":"The rise in medical costs has become a problem.","furigana":"いりょうひ|ぞうだい|もんだい"},{"japanese":"需要が増大して、品物が足りなくなった。","romaji":"Juyō ga zōdai shite, shinamono ga tarinaku natta.","german":"Demand rose, and goods ran short.","furigana":"じゅよう|ぞうだい|しなもの|た"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Mostly in 送別会 (farewell party) or 送別の言葉 (words of farewell).","examples":[{"japanese":"転勤する先輩のために送別の会を開いた。","romaji":"Tenkin suru senpai no tame ni sōbetsu no kai o hiraita.","german":"We held a farewell party for our senior colleague who is being transferred.","furigana":"てんきん|せんぱい|そうべつ|かい|ひら"},{"japanese":"卒業生に送別の言葉を贈った。","romaji":"Sotsugyōsei ni sōbetsu no kotoba o okutta.","german":"We offered words of farewell to the graduates.","furigana":"そつぎょうせい|そうべつ|ことば|おく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Surveying land or buildings; as a verb 測量する. 測量士 = land surveyor.","examples":[{"japanese":"家を建てる前に土地を測量した。","romaji":"Ie o tateru mae ni tochi o sokuryō shita.","german":"The land was surveyed before building the house.","furigana":"いえ|た|まえ|とち|そくりょう"},{"japanese":"山の高さを正確に測量するのは難しい。","romaji":"Yama no takasa o seikaku ni sokuryō suru no wa muzukashii.","german":"It is difficult to measure a mountain's height accurately.","furigana":"やま|たか|せいかく|そくりょう|むずか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"More for vehicles and in the phrase 全速力で (at full speed). In general: 速度, スピード.","examples":[{"japanese":"船は速力を上げて港を出た。","romaji":"Fune wa sokuryoku o agete minato o deta.","german":"The ship picked up speed and left the harbor.","furigana":"ふね|そくりょく|あ|みなと|で"},{"japanese":"電車は速力を落として駅に入った。","romaji":"Densha wa sokuryoku o otoshite eki ni haitta.","german":"The train slowed down and entered the station.","furigana":"でんしゃ|そくりょく|お|えき|はい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Ancestors of a family or forebears of a species: 人類の祖先. Similar: 先祖 (more family ancestors).","examples":[{"japanese":"お盆には祖先の霊を迎える。","romaji":"Obon ni wa sosen no rei o mukaeru.","german":"At Obon, we welcome the spirits of our ancestors.","furigana":"ぼん|そせん|れい|むか"},{"japanese":"鳥の祖先は恐竜だと考えられている。","romaji":"Tori no sosen wa kyōryū da to kangaerarete iru.","german":"Birds are thought to have descended from dinosaurs.","furigana":"とり|そせん|きょうりゅう|かんが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"い-adjective for people who often make mistakes or forget things out of haste.","examples":[{"japanese":"そそっかしい弟は、よく傘を忘れる。","romaji":"Sosokkashii otōto wa, yoku kasa o wasureru.","german":"My scatterbrained little brother often forgets his umbrella.","furigana":"おとうと|かさ|わす"},{"japanese":"私はそそっかしくて、違う電車に乗ってしまった。","romaji":"Watashi wa sosokkashikute, chigau densha ni notte shimatta.","german":"I was so careless that I got on the wrong train.","furigana":"わたし|ちが|でんしゃ|の"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Adds something, often as an escalation: 安い。その上、おいしい. Similar: さらに, しかも. Also written in kana そのうえ.","examples":[{"japanese":"この店は安い。その上、とてもおいしい。","romaji":"Kono mise wa yasui. Sono ue, totemo oishii.","german":"This restaurant is cheap. On top of that, it's very tasty.","furigana":"みせ|やす|うえ"},{"japanese":"道に迷い、その上雨まで降ってきた。","romaji":"Michi ni mayoi, sono ue ame made futte kita.","german":"We got lost, and on top of that it even started to rain.","furigana":"みち|まよ|うえあめ|ふ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Refers to a time mentioned earlier, in the past or the future.","examples":[{"japanese":"その頃、私はまだ大阪に住んでいた。","romaji":"Sono koro, watashi wa mada Ōsaka ni sunde ita.","german":"At that time, I still lived in Osaka.","furigana":"ころ|わたし|おおさか|す"},{"japanese":"来月の十日に来てください。その頃には準備ができています。","romaji":"Raigetsu no tōka ni kite kudasai. Sono koro ni wa junbi ga dekite imasu.","german":"Please come on the tenth of next month. By then, preparations will be done.","furigana":"らいげつ|とおか|き|ころ|じゅんび"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Connects cause and effect, rather written style: 大雪が降った。そのため、電車が止まった. As そのために also \"for that purpose\". Kanji: その為.","examples":[{"japanese":"大雪が降った。そのため、電車が止まった。","romaji":"Ōyuki ga futta. Sono tame, densha ga tomatta.","german":"There was heavy snow. Because of that, the trains stopped.","furigana":"おおゆき|ふ|でんしゃ|と"},{"japanese":"この店は材料にこだわっている。そのため、値段は少し高い。","romaji":"Kono mise wa zairyō ni kodawatte iru. Sono tame, nedan wa sukoshi takai.","german":"This shop is particular about its ingredients. For that reason, the prices are a little high.","furigana":"みせ|ざいりょう|ねだん|すこ|たか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A prefix before nouns in the sense of \"total, all\": 総人口 (total population), 総選挙 (general election), 総額 (total amount).","examples":[{"japanese":"旅行の総費用は十万円ほどだった。","romaji":"Ryokō no sōhiyō wa jūman en hodo datta.","german":"The total cost of the trip was about 100,000 yen.","furigana":"りょこう|そうひよう|じゅうまんえん"},{"japanese":"来月、総選挙が行われる。","romaji":"Raigetsu, sōsenkyo ga okonawareru.","german":"A general election will be held next month.","furigana":"らいげつ|そうせんきょ|おこな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"An idea or inspiration; also written アイディア. \"To have an idea\": アイデアが浮かぶ／アイデアを出す.","examples":[{"japanese":"いいアイデアが浮かんだら、すぐメモしてください。","romaji":"Ii aidea ga ukandara, sugu memo shite kudasai.","german":"If a good idea comes to you, please write it down right away.","furigana":"う"},{"japanese":"彼のアイデアで、新しい商品が生まれた。","romaji":"Kare no aidea de, atarashii shōhin ga umareta.","german":"A new product was born from his idea.","furigana":"かれ|あたら|しょうひん|う"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The area right at one's feet: 足元に気をつけてください (watch your step). Also written 足下. Idiom: 足元を見る (to take advantage of someone's predicament).","examples":[{"japanese":"足元が暗いので、気をつけてください。","romaji":"Ashimoto ga kurai node, ki o tsukete kudasai.","german":"It's dark underfoot, so please be careful.","furigana":"あしもと|くら|き"},{"japanese":"猫が私の足元で眠っている。","romaji":"Neko ga watashi no ashimoto de nemutte iru.","german":"The cat is sleeping at my feet.","furigana":"ねこ|わたし|あしもと|ねむ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A somewhat more formal form of あちこち: in various places. The kanji 彼方此方 is rare.","examples":[{"japanese":"公園のあちらこちらで桜が咲いている。","romaji":"Kōen no achirakochira de sakura ga saite iru.","german":"Cherry trees are blooming here and there in the park.","furigana":"こうえん|さくら|さ"},{"japanese":"鍵を探して、家の中をあちらこちら見て回った。","romaji":"Kagi o sagashite, ie no naka o achirakochira mite mawatta.","german":"Looking for the key, I searched all around the house.","furigana":"かぎ|さが|いえ|なか|み|まわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Solid (mostly animal) fat, e.g. in meat: 脂の多い肉. Liquid oil is written 油. Skin oil: 脂.","examples":[{"japanese":"この肉は脂が多くて、少し重い。","romaji":"Kono niku wa abura ga ōkute, sukoshi omoi.","german":"This meat is very fatty and a bit heavy.","furigana":"にく|あぶら|おお|すこ|おも"},{"japanese":"フライパンに残った脂を紙でふき取った。","romaji":"Furaipan ni nokotta abura o kami de fukitotta.","german":"I wiped the fat left in the frying pan with paper.","furigana":"のこ|あぶら|かみ|と"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Transitive godan verb: to hold something briefly over a flame to roast or warm it: 海苔をあぶる, 手をあぶる. Often written in kana.","examples":[{"japanese":"のりを火であぶると香りがよくなる。","romaji":"Nori o hi de aburu to kaori ga yoku naru.","german":"Roasting nori over a flame improves its aroma.","furigana":"ひ|かお"},{"japanese":"寒い夜、たき火で手をあぶった。","romaji":"Samui yoru, takibi de te o abutta.","german":"On the cold night, I warmed my hands at the bonfire.","furigana":"さむ|よる|び|て"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A net for catching or grilling: 魚を網でとる, 網で焼く. Figuratively also a network (in compounds: 通信網).","examples":[{"japanese":"漁師が網を海に投げた。","romaji":"Ryōshi ga ami o umi ni nageta.","german":"The fisherman cast his net into the sea.","furigana":"りょうし|あみ|うみ|な"},{"japanese":"網の上で魚を焼いた。","romaji":"Ami no ue de sakana o yaita.","german":"I grilled fish on a grill.","furigana":"あみ|うえ|さかな|や"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Knitting or crocheting as an activity and the finished piece: 編み物をする (to knit). Verb: 編む.","examples":[{"japanese":"祖母は冬になると編み物をする。","romaji":"Sobo wa fuyu ni naru to amimono o suru.","german":"My grandmother knits when winter comes.","furigana":"そぼ|ふゆ|あ|もの"},{"japanese":"編み物の本を見ながら、マフラーを作った。","romaji":"Amimono no hon o minagara, mafurā o tsukutta.","german":"I made a scarf while looking at a knitting book.","furigana":"あ|もの|ほん|み|つく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A short summary of the plot of a book, film or plan. Kanji: 粗筋.","examples":[{"japanese":"映画を見る前に、あらすじを読んだ。","romaji":"Eiga o miru mae ni, arasuji o yonda.","german":"Before watching the movie, I read the synopsis.","furigana":"えいが|み|まえ|よ"},{"japanese":"この小説のあらすじを三行で書いてください。","romaji":"Kono shōsetsu no arasuji o sangyō de kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write a summary of this novel in three lines.","furigana":"しょうせつ|さんぎょう|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Transitive godan verb, literary: to write and bring out a work. Homophones: 表す (to express), 現す (to show).","examples":[{"japanese":"その学者は多くの本を著した。","romaji":"Sono gakusha wa ōku no hon o arawashita.","german":"That scholar has written many books.","furigana":"がくしゃ|おお|ほん|あらわ"},{"japanese":"彼は旅の経験を一冊の本に著した。","romaji":"Kare wa tabi no keiken o issatsu no hon ni arawashita.","german":"He wrote down his travel experiences in a book.","furigana":"かれ|たび|けいけん|いっさつ|ほん|あらわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Also used adverbially: あれこれ考える (to think it over from every angle). The kanji 彼此 is rare.","examples":[{"japanese":"休みの日は家であれこれ片付けている。","romaji":"Yasumi no hi wa ie de arekore katazukete iru.","german":"On my days off I tidy up this and that around the house.","furigana":"やす|ひ|いえ|かたづ"},{"japanese":"あれこれ悩んだが、結局行くことにした。","romaji":"Arekore nayanda ga, kekkyoku iku koto ni shita.","german":"I agonized over it this way and that, but in the end I decided to go.","furigana":"なや|けっきょくい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Also figurative: アンテナを張る (to keep one's antennae out, to stay up to date).","examples":[{"japanese":"屋根の上に大きなアンテナがある。","romaji":"Yane no ue ni ōki na antena ga aru.","german":"There is a big antenna on the roof.","furigana":"やね|うえ|おお"},{"japanese":"彼女は流行にいつもアンテナを張っている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa ryūkō ni itsumo antena o hatte iru.","german":"She always keeps her antennae out for new trends.","furigana":"かのじょ|りゅうこう|は"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Transitive ichidan verb: 1. to assign someone a task (用事を言いつける); 2. in children's speech, to rat on someone (先生に言いつける).","examples":[{"japanese":"母に買い物を言いつけられた。","romaji":"Haha ni kaimono o iitsukerareta.","german":"My mother told me to go shopping.","furigana":"はは|か|もの|い"},{"japanese":"いじわるすると、先生に言いつけるよ。","romaji":"Ijiwaru suru to, sensei ni iitsukeru yo.","german":"If you're mean, I'll tell the teacher!","furigana":"せんせい|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Meaning in the sense of value or purpose: 意義がある (to be meaningful), 歴史的意義. Homophone: 異議 (objection).","examples":[{"japanese":"この研究には大きな意義がある。","romaji":"Kono kenkyū ni wa ōki na igi ga aru.","german":"This research has great significance.","furigana":"けんきゅう|おお|いぎ"},{"japanese":"ボランティア活動の意義について話し合った。","romaji":"Borantia katsudō no igi ni tsuite hanashiatta.","german":"We discussed the significance of volunteer work.","furigana":"かつどう|いぎ|はな|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Also 生き生きとした／生き生きしている: full of life. Also written in kana as いきいき.","examples":[{"japanese":"子どもたちは生き生きと遊んでいる。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa ikiiki to asonde iru.","german":"The children are playing with great energy.","furigana":"こ|い|い|あそ"},{"japanese":"新しい仕事を始めてから、彼は生き生きしている。","romaji":"Atarashii shigoto o hajimete kara, kare wa ikiiki shite iru.","german":"Since starting his new job, he has been full of life.","furigana":"あたら|しごと|はじ|かれ|い|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Mathematical \"equals\" (二たす三イコール五) and figurative: A イコール B (A is equivalent to B).","examples":[{"japanese":"二たす三イコール五。","romaji":"Ni tasu san ikōru go.","german":"Two plus three equals five.","furigana":"に|さん|ご"},{"japanese":"お金持ちイコール幸せ、というわけではない。","romaji":"Okanemochi ikōru shiawase, to iu wake de wa nai.","german":"Being rich does not necessarily equal being happy.","furigana":"かねも|しあわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Often slightly annoyed: いちいち文句を言う (to nitpick about everything). The kanji 一々 is rare.","examples":[{"japanese":"いちいち説明しなくても分かるよ。","romaji":"Ichiichi setsumei shinakute mo wakaru yo.","german":"You don't need to explain every little thing, I understand.","furigana":"せつめい|わ"},{"japanese":"彼は私のすることにいちいち文句を言う。","romaji":"Kare wa watashi no suru koto ni ichiichi monku o iu.","german":"He complains about everything I do.","furigana":"かれ|わたし|もんく|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Stresses a marked increase compared with before: 一段と寒くなった (it has become noticeably colder still). More literary than もっと.","examples":[{"japanese":"今朝は一段と冷え込んだ。","romaji":"Kesa wa ichidan to hiekonda.","german":"This morning it got noticeably colder still.","furigana":"けさ|いちだん|ひ|こ"},{"japanese":"練習のおかげで、彼の演奏は一段と上手になった。","romaji":"Renshū no okage de, kare no ensō wa ichidan to jōzu ni natta.","german":"Thanks to practice, his playing has become noticeably better.","furigana":"れんしゅう|かれ|えんそう|いちだん|じょうず"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Something happened without one noticing: いつの間にか寝ていた (I had fallen asleep before I knew it). Can also be written entirely in kana.","examples":[{"japanese":"本を読んでいたら、いつの間にか夜になっていた。","romaji":"Hon o yonde itara, itsu no ma ni ka yoru ni natte ita.","german":"While I was reading, it had turned to night before I knew it.","furigana":"ほん|よ|ま|よる"},{"japanese":"いつの間にか、娘は私より背が高くなった。","romaji":"Itsu no ma ni ka, musume wa watashi yori se ga takaku natta.","german":"Before I knew it, my daughter had become taller than me.","furigana":"ま|むすめ|わたし|せ|たか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A mental picture or impression of something: イメージが湧く (an image comes to mind), 会社のイメージ. As a verb イメージする (to imagine).","examples":[{"japanese":"日本に来る前は、東京にどんなイメージを持っていましたか。","romaji":"Nihon ni kuru mae wa, Tōkyō ni donna imēji o motte imashita ka.","german":"What kind of image did you have of Tokyo before coming to Japan?","furigana":"にほん|く|まえ|とうきょう|も"},{"japanese":"その事件で会社のイメージが悪くなった。","romaji":"Sono jiken de kaisha no imēji ga waruku natta.","german":"The incident damaged the company's image.","furigana":"じけん|かいしゃ|わる"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"General word for anything in which something is kept: tin, box, case. More formal: 容器.","examples":[{"japanese":"このクッキーを入れる入れ物はありますか。","romaji":"Kono kukkī o ireru iremono wa arimasu ka.","german":"Do you have a container for these cookies?","furigana":"い|い|もの"},{"japanese":"空いた入れ物は洗って返してください。","romaji":"Aita iremono wa aratte kaeshite kudasai.","german":"Please wash empty containers and return them.","furigana":"あ|い|もの|あら|かえ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Also written ウエートレス. Today the gender-neutral 店員 or ホールスタッフ is often used.","examples":[{"japanese":"ウェイトレスが水を持ってきてくれた。","romaji":"Weitoresu ga mizu o motte kite kureta.","german":"The waitress brought us water.","furigana":"みず|も"},{"japanese":"学生のころ、喫茶店でウェイトレスをしていた。","romaji":"Gakusei no koro, kissaten de weitoresu o shite ita.","german":"When I was a student, I worked as a waitress in a coffee shop.","furigana":"がくせい|きっさてん"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"A tree or shrub planted in a garden, and a potted plant: 植木に水をやる. Gardener: 植木屋.","examples":[{"japanese":"毎朝、植木に水をやっている。","romaji":"Maiasa, ueki ni mizu o yatte iru.","german":"I water the plants every morning.","furigana":"まいあさ|うえき|みず"},{"japanese":"ベランダに小さな植木を置いた。","romaji":"Beranda ni chiisa na ueki o oita.","german":"I put a small potted plant on the balcony.","furigana":"ちい|うえき|お"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The act of receiving (荷物の受け取り) and the acknowledgment of receipt. On forms often written 受取. Verb: 受け取る.","examples":[{"japanese":"荷物の受け取りにはサインが必要です。","romaji":"Nimotsu no uketori ni wa sain ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"A signature is required to receive the package.","furigana":"にもつ|う|と|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"代金の受け取りを確認しました。","romaji":"Daikin no uketori o kakunin shimashita.","german":"We have confirmed receipt of the payment.","furigana":"だいきん|う|と|かくにん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Transitive godan verb: to negate or reject something (うわさを打ち消す); also to mask a sound or a feeling.","examples":[{"japanese":"社長は倒産のうわさを打ち消した。","romaji":"Shachō wa tōsan no uwasa o uchikeshita.","german":"The company president denied the rumor of bankruptcy.","furigana":"しゃちょう|とうさん|う|け"},{"japanese":"このヘッドホンは周りの騒音を打ち消してくれる。","romaji":"Kono heddohon wa mawari no sōon o uchikeshite kureru.","german":"These headphones cancel out the surrounding noise.","furigana":"まわ|そうおん|う|け"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Transitive godan verb, mostly historical or literary: to strike down an enemy, 敵を討つ (to take revenge). Homophones: 打つ (to hit), 撃つ (to shoot).","examples":[{"japanese":"その武士は父の敵を討った。","romaji":"Sono bushi wa chichi no kataki o utta.","german":"The samurai avenged his father by killing the enemy.","furigana":"ぶし|ちち|かたき|う"},{"japanese":"朝廷は反乱軍を討つために大軍を送った。","romaji":"Chōtei wa hanrangun o utsu tame ni taigun o okutta.","german":"The imperial court sent a large army to put down the rebels.","furigana":"ちょうてい|はんらんぐん|う|たいぐん|おく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Almost only in compounds: キャリアウーマン (career woman), ビジネスウーマン. On its own one says 女性.","examples":[{"japanese":"姉はバリバリ働くキャリアウーマンだ。","romaji":"Ane wa baribari hataraku kyaria ūman da.","german":"My older sister is a driven career woman.","furigana":"あね|はたら"},{"japanese":"雑誌で働くウーマンの特集を読んだ。","romaji":"Zasshi de hataraku ūman no tokushū o yonda.","german":"I read a special feature on working women in a magazine.","furigana":"ざっし|はたら|とくしゅう|よ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Literary, often used on forms: 経験の有無 (experience: yes/no). Idiom: 有無を言わせず (without allowing any objection).","examples":[{"japanese":"申込書に、アレルギーの有無を書いてください。","romaji":"Mōshikomisho ni, arerugī no umu o kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write on the application form whether you have any allergies.","furigana":"もうしこみしょ|うむ|か"},{"japanese":"彼は有無を言わせず、私を車に乗せた。","romaji":"Kare wa umu o iwasezu, watashi o kuruma ni noseta.","german":"Without allowing any objection, he put me in the car.","furigana":"かれ|うむ|い|わたし|くるま|の"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Transitive godan verb: to turn something to the other side (シャツを裏返す, カードを裏返す). Intransitive: 裏返る.","examples":[{"japanese":"洗濯する前に、シャツを裏返した。","romaji":"Sentaku suru mae ni, shatsu o uragaeshita.","german":"Before washing it, I turned the shirt inside out.","furigana":"せんたく|まえ|うらがえ"},{"japanese":"カードを裏返して、数字を確かめた。","romaji":"Kādo o uragaeshite, sūji o tashikameta.","german":"I turned the card over and checked the number.","furigana":"うらがえ|すうじ|たし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Sheep's wool as a material: ウールのセーター. The Japanese word is also 羊毛（ようもう）.","examples":[{"japanese":"このセーターはウール百パーセントです。","romaji":"Kono sētā wa ūru hyaku pāsento desu.","german":"This sweater is one hundred percent wool.","furigana":"ひゃく"},{"japanese":"ウールの服は洗濯機で洗わないでください。","romaji":"Ūru no fuku wa sentakuki de arawanaide kudasai.","german":"Please do not wash woollen clothes in the washing machine.","furigana":"ふく|せんたくき|あら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Colloquial for \"very much\": うんと食べる, うんと安い. In writing, 大いに or ずっと are more usual.","examples":[{"japanese":"今日はうんと食べて、元気を出そう。","romaji":"Kyō wa unto tabete, genki o dasō.","german":"Let's eat a lot today and get our energy back.","furigana":"きょう|た|げんき|だ"},{"japanese":"こっちの店のほうがうんと安いよ。","romaji":"Kotchi no mise no hō ga unto yasui yo.","german":"This shop here is much cheaper.","furigana":"みせ|やす"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A filler word used while thinking; also written えーと. Similar: あのう (when addressing someone).","examples":[{"japanese":"ええと、駅はどちらでしたっけ。","romaji":"Ēto, eki wa dochira deshita kke.","german":"Um, where was the station again?","furigana":"えき"},{"japanese":"「今日は何日？」「ええと、十五日です。」","romaji":"\"Kyō wa nannichi?\" \"Ēto, jūgonichi desu.\"","german":"\"What's the date today?\" \"Let me think... it's the 15th.\"","furigana":"きょう|なんにち|じゅうごにち"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 16"},
{"notes":"な-adjective: 活発な子ども, 活発な議論 (lively discussion), 活発に活動する.","examples":[{"japanese":"妹は活発で、外で遊ぶのが大好きだ。","romaji":"Imōto wa kappatsu de, soto de asobu no ga daisuki da.","german":"My younger sister is lively and loves playing outside.","furigana":"いもうと|かっぱつ|そと|あそ|だいす"},{"japanese":"会議では活発な意見交換が行われた。","romaji":"Kaigi de wa kappatsu na iken kōkan ga okonawareta.","german":"A lively exchange of opinions took place at the meeting.","furigana":"かいぎ|かっぱつ|いけんこうかん|おこな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Also written カテゴリ. 〜のカテゴリーに入る (to belong to the category of ...).","examples":[{"japanese":"商品をカテゴリーごとに分けて表示する。","romaji":"Shōhin o kategorī goto ni wakete hyōji suru.","german":"Products are displayed divided by category.","furigana":"しょうひん|わ|ひょうじ"},{"japanese":"この映画はどのカテゴリーにも当てはまらない。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa dono kategorī ni mo atehamaranai.","german":"This movie doesn't fit into any category.","furigana":"えいが|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"As a verb: カムバックする (to make a comeback).","examples":[{"japanese":"けがから一年後、彼は見事にカムバックした。","romaji":"Kega kara ichinen go, kare wa migoto ni kamubakku shita.","german":"One year after his injury, he made a splendid comeback.","furigana":"いちねんご|かれ|みごと"},{"japanese":"そのバンドの十年ぶりのカムバックが話題になった。","romaji":"Sono bando no jūnenburi no kamubakku ga wadai ni natta.","german":"The band's comeback after ten years became a topic of conversation.","furigana":"じゅうねん|わだい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Aus dem Deutschen „Karte“. Heute oft elektronisch: 電子カルテ.","examples":[{"japanese":"医者はカルテを見ながら説明した。","romaji":"Isha wa karute o minagara setsumei shita.","german":"Der Arzt erklärte es mit Blick auf die Krankenakte.","furigana":"いしゃ|み|せつめい"},{"japanese":"病院を変えたので、前の病院からカルテを取り寄せた。","romaji":"Byōin o kaeta node, mae no byōin kara karute o toriyoseta.","german":"Weil ich das Krankenhaus gewechselt habe, habe ich meine Krankenakte vom alten Krankenhaus angefordert.","furigana":"びょういん|か|まえ|びょういん|と|よ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive: to put something in liquid (水に浸ける). Read the same as 漬ける (to pickle, to marinate), which is mainly used for pickled food. Intransitive counterpart: 浸かる (to be immersed).","examples":[{"japanese":"汚れたシャツをしばらく水に浸けておいた。","romaji":"Yogoreta shatsu o shibaraku mizu ni tsukete oita.","german":"I left the dirty shirt soaking in water for a while.","furigana":"よご|みず|つ"},{"japanese":"疲れた足をお湯に浸けると気持ちがいい。","romaji":"Tsukareta ashi o oyu ni tsukeru to kimochi ga ii.","german":"It feels good to soak tired feet in hot water.","furigana":"つか|あし|ゆ|つ|きも"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"In this spelling, 従姉妹 denotes a female cousin. The reading いとこ is often written in kana; the gender then follows from context. 従兄弟 is the corresponding male spelling. In 年上です one describes an age difference, while in 従姉妹に電話する the に names the cousin who is called.","examples":[{"japanese":"従姉妹は私より二歳年上です。","romaji":"Itoko wa watashi yori nisai toshiue desu.","german":"My cousin is two years older than me.","furigana":"いとこ|わたし|にさいとしうえ"},{"japanese":"旅行中に道に迷った時、従姉妹に電話して助けてもらいました。","romaji":"Ryokochū ni michi ni mayotta toki, itoko ni denwa shite tasukete moraimashita.","german":"When I got lost during a trip, I called my cousin and she helped me.","furigana":"|みち|まよ|とき|いとこ|でんわ|たす"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"期 here denotes a delimited period or phase. In business, この期 can refer to the accounting period under consideration; in a project, several phases are distinguished. The word also appears as a suffix, but the emphasis of this card is on the complete standalone word き. The fixed expression この期に及んで uses the other reading ご and does not belong to this reading.","examples":[{"japanese":"この期の売上は、前の期より増えました。","romaji":"Kono ki no uriage wa, mae no ki yori fuemashita.","german":"Sales in this period increased compared with the previous period.","furigana":"き|うりあげ|まえ|き|ふ"},{"japanese":"工事は二つの期に分けて行います。","romaji":"Kōji wa futatsu no ki ni wakete okonaimasu.","german":"The construction work will be carried out in two phases.","furigana":"こうじ|ふた|き|わ|おこな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"再（さい）stands before another word and expresses that something happens once more: 再試験 is a retest, 再放送 a rebroadcast. The prefix stands directly before the following element. It is not a free adverb like もう一度. In 再来週, however, 再 is read さ; that other reading does not belong to this card. No separate accent is attested for the bound prefix.","examples":[{"japanese":"不合格だったので、再試験を受けます。","romaji":"Fugōkaku datta node, saishiken o ukemasu.","german":"Since I failed, I will take the retest.","furigana":"ふごうかく|さいしけん|う"},{"japanese":"この番組の再放送は来週です。","romaji":"Kono bangumi no saihōsō wa raishū desu.","german":"The rerun of this program is next week.","furigana":"ばんぐみ|さいほうそう|らいしゅう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"翌（よく）combines with time expressions such as 月 or 朝 and denotes the next period after a stated point in time. 翌月 is therefore the month after the one mentioned, not automatically next month counted from today. In a narrative, 翌朝 refers to the morning after the evening described. The identically read よく meaning \"often\" or \"well\" is a different word. The accent of the whole compound cannot be derived from the prefix alone.","examples":[{"japanese":"二月に申し込み、翌月から授業を受けました。","romaji":"Nigatsu ni mōshikomi, yokugetsu kara jugyō o ukemashita.","german":"I applied in February and attended classes from the following month.","furigana":"にがつ|もう|こ|よくげつ|じゅぎょう|う"},{"japanese":"昨夜は雪が降り、翌朝には庭が白くなっていました。","romaji":"Sakuya wa yuki ga furi, yokuasa ni wa niwa ga shiroku natte imashita.","german":"It snowed last night, and by the following morning the garden had turned white.","furigana":"さくや|ゆき|ふ|よくあさ|にわ|しろ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"今（こん）stands here at the beginning of a time expression: 今学期 means this semester and 今晩 tonight. The meaning depends on the following element; with a longer span of time it refers to the current period, with a day expression to today. The independent 今（いま）meaning \"now\" has a different reading.","examples":[{"japanese":"今学期は、日本語の授業を二つ取っています。","romaji":"Kongakki wa, Nihongo no jugyō o futatsu totte imasu.","german":"This semester I am taking two Japanese classes.","furigana":"こんがっき|にほんご|じゅぎょう|ふた|と"},{"japanese":"今晩は家で夕食を作ります。","romaji":"Konban wa ie de yūshoku o tsukurimasu.","german":"Tonight I will cook dinner at home.","furigana":"こんばん|いえ|ゆうしょく|つく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"前（ぜん）stands here before a job title or a time period. 前社長 denotes the previous or former company president, 前年度 the previous fiscal year. The prefix is attached directly to the following noun. For the spatial meaning \"in front\" one uses its own reading まえ.","examples":[{"japanese":"前社長から、新しい社長に資料が渡されました。","romaji":"Zenshachō kara, atarashii shachō ni shiryō ga watasaremashita.","german":"The former company president handed the documents over to the new president.","furigana":"ぜんしゃちょう|あたら|しゃちょう|しりょう|わた"},{"japanese":"前年度の売上と今年度の売上を比べました。","romaji":"Zennendo no uriage to konnendo no uriage o kurabemashita.","german":"I compared the sales of the previous fiscal year with those of the current fiscal year.","furigana":"ぜんねんど|うりあげ|こんねんど|うりあげ|くら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"最（さい）bildet hier den höchsten oder äußersten Grad einer Eigenschaft. 最年少 heißt am jüngsten; 最優秀賞 ist eine Auszeichnung für die beste Leistung. Die Bedeutung ergibt sich mit dem folgenden Wort, nicht als frei verwendbares deutsches aller-. Diese Karte übt die Vorsilbe; die eigene Wendung 最たる wird nicht mitgeübt. Trotz der gleichen Lesung ist 再 für erneut eine andere Vorsilbe.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女はチームの最年少の選手です。","romaji":"Kanojo wa chīmu no sainenshō no senshu desu.","german":"Sie ist die jüngste Spielerin im Team.","furigana":"かのじょ|さいねんしょう|せんしゅ"},{"japanese":"私たちの作品が最優秀賞を受賞しました。","romaji":"Watashitachi no sakuhin ga saiyūshūshō o jushō shimashita.","german":"Unser Werk gewann den Preis für die beste Leistung.","furigana":"わたし|さくひん|さいゆうしゅうしょう|じゅしょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"As a noun, 史（し）can mean history or its written account. Used as a standalone word it sounds literary; 史をひもとく means to delve into historical records, and 史を編む to compile a history or chronicle. In everyday language 歴史 is more common. The bound suffix in 日本史 is practiced on a separate card.","examples":[{"japanese":"史をひもとくと、この町の成り立ちがわかります。","romaji":"Shi o himotoku to, kono machi no naritachi ga wakarimasu.","german":"If you delve into history, you can understand how this town came into being.","furigana":"し|まち|な|た"},{"japanese":"この時代の史を編むには、多くの記録を調べる必要があります。","romaji":"Kono jidai no shi o amu ni wa, ōku no kiroku o shiraberu hitsuyō ga arimasu.","german":"To compile a history of this era, you need to examine many records.","furigana":"じだい|し|あ|おお|きろく|しら|ひつよう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"史（し）is used as a suffix after the field whose history is meant. 日本史 is the history of Japan; 会社の百年史 describes the hundred-year history of a company. The suffix attaches directly to the preceding word and is not an honorific title like 氏（し）.","examples":[{"japanese":"大学では日本史を専攻しています。","romaji":"Daigaku de wa Nihonshi o senkō shite imasu.","german":"At university I am majoring in Japanese history.","furigana":"だいがく|にほんし|せんこう"},{"japanese":"会社の百年史を作るため、昔の写真を集めました。","romaji":"Kaisha no hyakunenshi o tsukuru tame, mukashi no shashin o atsumemashita.","german":"To put together the company's hundred-year history, I collected old photos.","furigana":"かいしゃ|ひゃくねんし|つく|むかし|しゃしん|あつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"家（け）is attached after a family name and denotes the family or house as a kinship group. 佐藤家 therefore means the Sato family, not just any house. With a historical family such as 徳川家, \"house\" can also mean a dynasty. The reading いえ for the building and the suffix か in 作家 are other usages.","examples":[{"japanese":"今夜は佐藤家で夕食をごちそうになります。","romaji":"Kon'ya wa Satōke de yūshoku o gochisō ni narimasu.","german":"Tonight I am being treated to dinner at the Sato family's.","furigana":"こんや|さとうけ|ゆうしょく"},{"japanese":"徳川家の歴史について調べています。","romaji":"Tokugawake no rekishi ni tsuite shirabete imasu.","german":"I am researching the history of the Tokugawa house.","furigana":"とくがわけ|れきし|しら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"風（ふう）denotes a kind or manner. こんな風に means \"in this way\"; どんな風に asks how something is done. With に it describes the manner of an action, and with な a corresponding description can stand before a noun. In these phrases the hiragana spelling ふう is also common. The reading かぜ for \"wind\" and the suffix in フランス風 are other usages.","examples":[{"japanese":"どんな風に説明すれば、子どもにも伝わりますか。","romaji":"Donna fū ni setsumei sureba, kodomo ni mo tsutawarimasu ka.","german":"How can I explain it so that even children understand?","furigana":"ふう|せつめい|こ|つた"},{"japanese":"そんな風な考え方は、私には新鮮でした。","romaji":"Sonna fū na kangaekata wa, watashi ni wa shinsen deshita.","german":"That kind of way of thinking was fresh to me.","furigana":"ふう|かんが|かた|わたし|しんせん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"山（さん）appears in names of mountains, such as 富士山（ふじさん）or 阿蘇山（あそさん）. This reading is part of the full name. Other mountain names use やま or ざん, so you cannot automatically put さん on every name. The free noun 山（やま）and the title さん are separate entries.","examples":[{"japanese":"いつか富士山に登りたいです。","romaji":"Itsuka Fujisan ni noboritai desu.","german":"I want to climb Mt. Fuji someday.","furigana":"ふじさん|のぼ"},{"japanese":"阿蘇山についての写真集を図書館で借りました。","romaji":"Asosan ni tsuite no shashinshū o toshokan de karimashita.","german":"I borrowed a photo book about Mt. Aso from the library.","furigana":"|しゃしんしゅう|としょかん|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"名（めい）counts people in polite or formal statements, for example for a reservation or the number of participants. 二名です means \"there are two of us\"; with 十名 you state ten participants. In ordinary conversation 人（にん）is more common. Only the counter is practiced here, not 名 for a given name or the prefix in 名探偵.","examples":[{"japanese":"二名で予約したいのですが、席はありますか。","romaji":"Nimei de yoyaku shitai no desu ga, seki wa arimasu ka.","german":"I would like to make a reservation for two. Do you have seats?","furigana":"にめい|よやく|せき"},{"japanese":"説明会には十名が参加しました。","romaji":"Setsumeikai ni wa jūmei ga sanka shimashita.","german":"Ten people took part in the information session.","furigana":"せつめいかい|じゅうめい|さんか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"色（しょく）follows a number when counting colors. 四色 is read よんしょく and means four colors; 一色 is read いっしょく. The compound counts the different colors, not the number of objects. The free word for \"color\" is read 色（いろ）. Only the counter is practiced here.","examples":[{"japanese":"このプリンターは四色のインクを使います。","romaji":"Kono purintā wa yonshoku no inku o tsukaimasu.","german":"This printer uses ink in four colors.","furigana":"よんしょく|つか"},{"japanese":"一色だけで絵を描くのは難しいです。","romaji":"Isshoku dake de e o kaku no wa muzukashii desu.","german":"It is difficult to paint a picture using only one color.","furigana":"いっしょく|え|か|むずか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"内（ない）is attached directly to a noun and denotes its interior or limits. 館内 means inside a building; 予算内 means within the budget. The context may be spatial or concern a fixed limit. After the compound comes the appropriate particle, such as 館内では or 予算内で. The reading うち and ない meaning \"not existing\" are separate words.","examples":[{"japanese":"館内では、静かに話してください。","romaji":"Kannai de wa, shizuka ni hanashite kudasai.","german":"Please speak quietly inside the building.","furigana":"かんない|しず|はな"},{"japanese":"必要な家具を、予算内でそろえました。","romaji":"Hitsuyō na kagu o, yosannai de soroemashita.","german":"I got the necessary furniture within the budget.","furigana":"ひつよう|かぐ|よさんない"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"外（がい）follows a noun and expresses that something lies outside its limits or does not belong to it. 営業時間外 denotes the time outside business hours; 範囲外 means outside a certain range. It also covers abstract limits such as the scope of a topic. The free word for \"outside\" is read 外（そと）. The suffix is attached directly.","examples":[{"japanese":"営業時間外は、この入口を使えません。","romaji":"Eigyōjikangai wa, kono iriguchi o tsukaemasen.","german":"This entrance cannot be used outside business hours.","furigana":"えいぎょうじかんがい|いりぐち|つか"},{"japanese":"その質問は、今日の授業の範囲外です。","romaji":"Sono shitsumon wa, kyō no jugyō no han'igai desu.","german":"That question is outside the scope of today's lesson.","furigana":"しつもん|きょう|じゅぎょう|はんいがい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Besides the unit of length, メートル has an attested meaning as a measuring device or meter. For such devices メーター is usually used today, e.g. ガスメーター. The term メートル検査員 means someone who inspects measuring devices; here it contains no indication of a length. According to the dictionary, the rare spelling 米 can also carry this meaning. First practice the katakana form and pay attention to the context in older or technical texts.","examples":[{"japanese":"古い記事には、メートル検査員が各家庭の計器を調べたと書かれています。","romaji":"Furui kiji ni wa, mētoru kensain ga kaku katei no keiki o shirabeta to kakarete imasu.","german":"An old article says that a meter inspector examined the devices in each household.","furigana":"ふる|きじ|けんさいん|かくかてい|けいき|しら|か"},{"japanese":"昔の説明書には、ガスのメートルを毎月確認するように書いてあります。","romaji":"Mukashi no setsumeisho ni wa, gasu no mētoru o maitsuki kakunin suru yō ni kaite arimasu.","german":"An old manual says to check the gas meter every month.","furigana":"むかし|せつめいしょ|まいつきかくにん|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5 (source: N5; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"頭（とう）follows a number when counting large animals such as cattle, horses or elephants. 象が三頭いる states the number of animals present; 馬を二頭連れてくる describes animals that someone brings along. For many smaller animals 匹（ひき）is used. The standalone word for \"head\" is 頭（あたま）; the same kanji has a different reading and function here.","examples":[{"japanese":"動物園には象が三頭います。","romaji":"Dōbutsuen ni wa zō ga santō imasu.","german":"There are three elephants at the zoo.","furigana":"どうぶつえん|ぞう|さんとう"},{"japanese":"係の人が馬を二頭連れてきました。","romaji":"Kakari no hito ga uma o nitō tsurete kimashita.","german":"The person in charge brought two horses.","furigana":"かかり|ひと|うま|にとうつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"泊 counts the nights of a stay, while 日 counts the days. 二泊三日 means two nights and three days. With 泊まる you describe the stay; 一泊延ばす means to extend it by one night. Learn the number together with the counter: 二泊 is にはく, and 一泊 becomes いっぱく. The basic reading はく alone does not reveal this sound change.","examples":[{"japanese":"京都に二泊三日で旅行します。","romaji":"Kyōto ni nihaku mikka de ryokō shimasu.","german":"I'm traveling to Kyoto for three days and two nights.","furigana":"きょうと|にはくみっか|りょこう"},{"japanese":"予定が変わったので、ホテルの滞在を一泊延ばしました。","romaji":"Yotei ga kawatta node, hoteru no taizai o ippaku nobashimashita.","german":"Since my plans changed, I extended my hotel stay by one night.","furigana":"よてい|か|たいざい|いっぱくの"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"隻 counts ships. You also encounter it with folding screens: a pair of matching 屏風 is called 一双（いっそう）, and a single screen from it 一隻（いっせき）. So the same unit can denote a number of ships in the harbor and one half of a pair of screens in a museum. Note the small っ in 一隻; two ships is 二隻（にせき）.","examples":[{"japanese":"港には船が二隻停泊しています。","romaji":"Minato ni wa fune ga niseki teihaku shite imasu.","german":"Two ships are anchored in the harbor.","furigana":"みなと|ふね|にせきていはく"},{"japanese":"一対の屏風のうち、一隻だけが展示されています。","romaji":"Ittsui no byōbu no uchi, isseki dake ga tenji sarete imasu.","german":"Of the pair of folding screens, only one is on display.","furigana":"いっつい|びょうぶ|いっせき|てんじ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"In some island names, the final 島 is read とう. One attested example is 硫黄島（いおうとう）. This reading belongs to the full place name; other names may contain しま or じま. The general word for an island is 島（しま）. So with a new island name always learn the whole reading instead of inserting とう everywhere.","examples":[{"japanese":"地図で硫黄島の位置を確認しました。","romaji":"Chizu de Iōtō no ichi o kakunin shimashita.","german":"I checked the location of Iwo Jima on the map.","furigana":"ちず|いおうとう|いち|かくにん"},{"japanese":"研究者たちは硫黄島の火山活動を調べています。","romaji":"Kenkyūshatachi wa Iōtō no kazan katsudō o shirabete imasu.","german":"Researchers are studying the volcanic activity of Iwo Jima.","furigana":"けんきゅうしゃ|いおうとう|かざんかつどう|しら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"早口 has the reading はやぐち in addition to はやくち. Both denote fast talking. 早口で explains the manner of speaking, as in 早口で話す. The word says something about speed, not about whether the person is articulate or speaks many foreign languages. In the first situation the speed prevents understanding; the second recommends a clearer explanation. The identically read place name 早口 belongs to a different usage.","examples":[{"japanese":"受付の説明が早口で、部屋番号を聞き取れませんでした。","romaji":"Uketsuke no setsumei ga hayaguchi de, heya bangō o kikitoremasen deshita.","german":"The explanation at the reception desk was spoken so fast that I couldn't catch the room number.","furigana":"うけつけ|せつめい|はやぐち|へやばんごう|き|と"},{"japanese":"早口で話すより、一つずつはっきり説明したほうが伝わります。","romaji":"Hayaguchi de hanasu yori, hitotsu zutsu hakkiri setsumei shita hō ga tsutawarimasu.","german":"You get your point across better by explaining things clearly one at a time than by talking fast.","furigana":"はやぐち|はな|ひと|せつめい|つた"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"The reading つく is used, among others, in 息をつく \"to catch one's breath, breathe out\", ため息をつく \"to sigh\" and 嘘をつく \"to lie\". The verb is often written in kana in these expressions. The exhaled breath or the lie takes を. The godan forms are つきます, ついて, ついた. For \"to vomit\", the other reading はく is the usual choice; this card practices the expressions with breath and lies.","examples":[{"japanese":"結果を聞いて、彼女はほっと息を吐いた。","romaji":"Kekka o kiite, kanojo wa hotto iki o tsuita.","german":"Hearing the result, she breathed a sigh of relief.","furigana":"けっか|き|かのじょ|いき|つ"},{"japanese":"約束を守れなかった理由について、嘘を吐いてはいけません。","romaji":"Yakusoku o mamorenakatta riyū ni tsuite, uso o tsuite wa ikemasen.","german":"You must not tell lies about the reason you couldn't keep your promise.","furigana":"やくそく|まも|りゆう|うそ|つ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"注す／さす here means adding a liquid. The thing poured takes を, the container or treated object takes に: やかんに水をさす, 機械に油をさす. The verb is often written in kana; 注す emphasizes the pouring. It is transitive and conjugated as a godan verb: さします, さして. Light shining in is described with the other verb 差す／射す.","examples":[{"japanese":"お湯が少なくなったので、やかんに水を注した。","romaji":"Oyu ga sukunaku natta node, yakan ni mizu o sashita.","german":"Since there was little hot water left, I poured water into the kettle.","furigana":"ゆ|すく|みず|さ"},{"japanese":"音がする蝶番に、油を少し注してください。","romaji":"Oto ga suru chōtsugai ni, abura o sukoshi sashite kudasai.","german":"Please put a little oil on the squeaky hinge.","furigana":"おと||あぶら|すこ|さ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"たま describes something that happens only rarely. Before a noun it often takes の, as in たまの休み \"a rare day off\"; たまなこと \"something rare\" is also possible. The spelling 偶 is uncommon; it is usually written in hiragana. The longer たまに means \"occasionally\" as an adverb; \"even number\", by contrast, is 偶数（ぐうすう）.","examples":[{"japanese":"たまの休みには、家でゆっくり本を読みます。","romaji":"Tama no yasumi ni wa, ie de yukkuri hon o yomimasu.","german":"On my rare days off, I read quietly at home.","furigana":"やす|いえ|ほん|よ"},{"japanese":"彼が会議で黙っているのは、たまなことです。","romaji":"Kare ga kaigi de damatte iru no wa, tama na koto desu.","german":"It is rare for him to stay silent in a meeting.","furigana":"かれ|かいぎ|だま"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"ふる denotes a used item, usually with お: 父のお古 is something that previously belonged to the father. As a word element, 古 before a noun can mean \"old\" or \"used\", for example 古新聞 \"old newspapers\". Not every word with 古 is read this way; learn new compounds as a whole. The ordinary adjective \"old\" is 古い／ふるい. For a used item, お古 is used in everyday speech rather than a standalone ふる.","examples":[{"japanese":"このコートは父のお古だが、まだ十分に着られる。","romaji":"Kono kōto wa chichi no ofuru da ga, mada jūbun ni kirareru.","german":"This coat is a hand-me-down from my father, but it is still perfectly wearable.","furigana":"ちち|ふる|じゅうぶん|き"},{"japanese":"古新聞を束ねて、回収の日まで玄関に置いた。","romaji":"Furushinbun o tabanete, kaishū no hi made genkan ni oita.","german":"I tied up the old newspapers and left them in the entrance hall until collection day.","furigana":"ふるしんぶん|たば|かいしゅう|ひ|げんかん|お"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"づらい is attached to the stem before ます: 読みます → 読みづらい. It describes that an action is physically difficult or causes inner resistance. It is usually written in kana and conjugated like an i-adjective: 読みづらかった, 読みづらくない. In this meaning it is used with a verb stem, not as a free づらいな before a noun. 辛い／からい \"spicy\" and 辛い／つらい \"painful, trying\" are other readings.","examples":[{"japanese":"字が薄くて、この手紙は読みづらい。","romaji":"Ji ga usukute, kono tegami wa yomizurai.","german":"The writing is faint, so this letter is hard to read.","furigana":"じ|うす|てがみ|よ"},{"japanese":"助けてもらったばかりなので、断りづらい。","romaji":"Tasukete moratta bakari na node, kotowarizurai.","german":"Since this person has just helped me, it is hard to turn them down.","furigana":"たす|ことわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"同／どう refers to something mentioned earlier in factual texts. Before a noun it can mean \"the same\" or \"the aforementioned\", for example 同校 \"the said school\". In lists or records, 同 also replaces a repeated entry, similar to \"ditto\": 令和五年入学、同七年卒業. You need the preceding context to understand the reference. The question word どう \"how\" has a different meaning. The pronunciation of longer words such as 同校 is learned as a whole.","examples":[{"japanese":"記事では新しい高校を紹介し、同校の校長の話も載せている。","romaji":"Kiji de wa atarashii kōkō o shōkai shi, dōkō no kōchō no hanashi mo nosete iru.","german":"The article introduces a new high school and also includes remarks by that school's principal.","furigana":"きじ|あたら|こうこう|しょうかい|どうこう|こうちょう|はなし|の"},{"japanese":"記録には「令和五年入学、同七年卒業」とある。","romaji":"Kiroku ni wa “Reiwa gonen nyūgaku, dō shichinen sotsugyō” to aru.","german":"The record says: \"Entered in Reiwa 5, graduated in the same era's year 7.\"","furigana":"きろく|れいわごねんにゅうがく|どうしちねんそつぎょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"下 follows a noun that names a condition, an influence or a control. 影響下 means \"under the influence\", 悪条件下 \"under adverse conditions\". The whole word can state the circumstances of an action with で. This か attaches to the preceding word; it does not replace a standalone した as a spatial \"under\". The form is common especially in reports and factual texts.","examples":[{"japanese":"その地域は長い間、隣国の影響下にあった。","romaji":"Sono chiiki wa nagai aida, rinkoku no eikyōka ni atta.","german":"That region was under the influence of its neighboring country for a long time.","furigana":"ちいき|なが|あいだ|りんこく|えいきょうか"},{"japanese":"作業員たちは悪条件下で修理を続けた。","romaji":"Sagyōintachi wa akujōkenka de shūri o tsuzuketa.","german":"The workers continued the repairs under adverse conditions.","furigana":"さぎょういん|あくじょうけんか|しゅうり|つづ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"寺 is used after a number with the reading じ to count temples. This form is found especially in descriptions of temple precincts, pilgrimage routes or historical holdings. A temple as a standalone word is てら. The mere occurrence of 寺 in a temple name is not yet counting: for the counter, a number must precede it.","examples":[{"japanese":"この町には、古い寺が十寺残っている。","romaji":"Kono machi ni wa, furui tera ga jūji nokotte iru.","german":"Ten old temples still remain in this town.","furigana":"まち|ふる|てら|じゅうじのこ"},{"japanese":"本山は、各地の百寺に知らせを送った。","romaji":"Honzan wa, kakuchi no hyakuji ni shirase o okutta.","german":"The head temple sent a notice to a hundred temples in various regions.","furigana":"ほんざん|かくち|ひゃくじ|し|おく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"畳（じょう）follows a number and counts tatami mats; such figures are also used to describe the size of a room. 六畳（ろくじょう）means roughly \"six tatami in size\". The area of one mat varies by region, so 畳 is not a square-meter figure that is the same everywhere. The single mat is 畳（たたみ）. The accent of complete number compounds is not derived from the isolated dictionary entry.","examples":[{"japanese":"六畳の部屋に机と布団を置いた。","romaji":"Rokujō no heya ni tsukue to futon o oita.","german":"I put a desk and a futon in the six-mat room.","furigana":"ろくじょう|へや|つくえ|ふとん|お"},{"japanese":"畳屋さんに畳を八畳注文した。","romaji":"Tatamiyasan ni tatami o hachijō chūmon shita.","german":"I ordered eight tatami mats from the tatami shop.","furigana":"たたみや|たたみ|はちじょうちゅうもん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"五味 denotes, in the traditional classification, sweet, sour, pungent, bitter and salty. This card teaches that older culinary classification. It differs from the five basic tastes of modern taste physiology, which contain umami instead of pungency. The Buddhist classification of dairy products and the family name 五味 are not practiced here. ゴミ \"garbage\" is a different word.","examples":[{"japanese":"料理の先生は、五味には辛さも含まれると説明した。","romaji":"Ryōri no sensei wa, gomi ni wa karasa mo fukumareru to setsumei shita.","german":"The cooking teacher explained that the five traditional flavors include spiciness.","furigana":"りょうり|せんせい|ごみ|から|ふく|せつめい"},{"japanese":"この献立では、五味のバランスを考えて食材を選んだ。","romaji":"Kono kondate de wa, gomi no baransu o kangaete shokuzai o eranda.","german":"For this menu, I chose the ingredients with a balance of the five traditional flavors in mind.","furigana":"こんだて|ごみ|かんが|しょくざい|えら"}],"levelNote":"Compound: 味 (N4)"},
{"notes":"調度 denotes household goods, everyday utensils and smaller pieces of furniture or furnishings. The word sounds elevated or literary and also appears in 調度品. It refers to objects, not to the act of furnishing. 丁度／ちょうど \"exactly, just\" is a different word. The historical meaning \"bow and arrows\" is not learned here.","examples":[{"japanese":"新居に合う調度を少しずつ揃えている。","romaji":"Shinkyo ni au chōdo o sukoshi zutsu soroete iru.","german":"I am gradually gathering furnishings that suit my new home.","furigana":"しんきょ|あ|ちょうど|すこ|そろ"},{"japanese":"展示室には昔の家で使われていた調度が並んでいる。","romaji":"Tenjishitsu ni wa mukashi no ie de tsukawarete ita chōdo ga narande iru.","german":"The exhibition room displays furnishings that were used in houses long ago.","furigana":"てんじしつ|むかし|いえ|つか|ちょうど|なら"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"幾（いく）is a prefix before quantity words and counters. With か it can denote an indefinite number: 幾人か \"several people\". In a question it can mean \"how many\"; in conversation 何人 for \"how many people\" is usually more common than 幾人. 幾度か means \"a few times\". 幾 is not used here on its own as an independent numeral. The words 行く and 幾つ remain separate cards.","examples":[{"japanese":"玄関で幾人かの客が待っていた。","romaji":"Genkan de ikunin ka no kyaku ga matte ita.","german":"Several guests were waiting at the entrance.","furigana":"げんかん|いくにん|きゃく|ま"},{"japanese":"同じ失敗を幾度か繰り返して、やっと原因に気づいた。","romaji":"Onaji shippai o ikudo ka kurikaeshite, yatto gen'in ni kizuita.","german":"After repeating the same mistake a few times, I finally noticed the cause.","furigana":"おな|しっぱい|いくど|く|かえ|げんいん|き"},{"japanese":"会場には幾人の参加者が集まったのでしょうか。","romaji":"Kaijō ni wa ikunin no sankasha ga atsumatta no deshō ka.","german":"I wonder how many participants gathered at the venue?","furigana":"かいじょう|いくにん|さんかしゃ|あつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"持ち（もち）here refers to holding, using or owning, and to taking on a task or expense. After a noun it forms nominal compounds: 片手持ち \"holding with one hand\", 二台持ち \"owning two devices\" and 私持ち \"at my expense\". The particle follows the whole expression. 費用は私持ちだ names the person who pays. This card covers such nominal compounds, not the verb form 持ち from 持つ. The independent meanings \"durability\" and \"tie (draw)\" have separate cards.","examples":[{"japanese":"このカメラは重いので、片手持ちでは安定しない。","romaji":"Kono kamera wa omoi node, katate mochi de wa antei shinai.","german":"This camera is heavy, so it isn't stable when held with one hand.","furigana":"おも|かたても|あんてい"},{"japanese":"仕事用と私用で、携帯電話を二台持ちにしている。","romaji":"Shigotoyō to shiyō de, keitai denwa o nidai mochi ni shite iru.","german":"I carry two mobile phones, one for work and one for private use.","furigana":"しごとよう|しよう|けいたいでんわ|にだいも"},{"japanese":"今日の昼食代は私持ちです。","romaji":"Kyō no chūshokudai wa watashi mochi desu.","german":"Today's lunch is on me.","furigana":"きょう|ちゅうしょくだい|わたしも"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"持ち（もち）here, as an independent noun, refers to how long quality, function or effect is retained. 持ちがいい means \"lasts long\", 持ちが悪い \"doesn't last long\". The word is used for batteries, clothing or foods, for example. It does not denote an owner and is not an attached ending here. 保ち is an occasional spelling for this meaning. In context, it must be clear which property is being retained.","examples":[{"japanese":"この電池は持ちがいいので、旅行に向いている。","romaji":"Kono denchi wa mochi ga ii node, ryokō ni muite iru.","german":"This battery lasts a long time, so it's good for traveling.","furigana":"でんち|も|りょこう|む"},{"japanese":"その布は持ちが悪く、すぐに傷んでしまった。","romaji":"Sono nuno wa mochi ga waruku, sugu ni itande shimatta.","german":"That fabric didn't last well and soon wore out.","furigana":"ぬの|も|わる|いた"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"女（じょ）denotes a woman, a girl or a daughter. As a standalone noun this reading sounds elevated or historical; in everyday speech 女（おんな）is usually used for \"woman\". In compounds じょ is usual, for example 一男一女（いちなんいちじょ）for one son and one daughter. The examples show the older standalone use and the \"daughter\" meaning in a number compound. The name suffix on women's names and the identically written constellation belong to other usages.","examples":[{"japanese":"女は、花を売って暮らしていた。","romaji":"Jo wa, hana o utte kurashite ita.","german":"The woman made her living selling flowers.","furigana":"じょ|はな|う|く"},{"japanese":"夫妻には、一男一女がいる。","romaji":"Fūsai ni wa, ichinan ichijo ga iru.","german":"The couple has a son and a daughter.","furigana":"ふうさい|いちなんいちじょ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"器（き）is here a nominal word-forming element at the end of names of devices, such as 受話器（じゅわき）for a telephone receiver or 電熱器（でんねつき）for an electric heater. In these compounds it is pronounced together with the preceding part. This card covers the device meaning; the standalone 器（うつわ）for a vessel and the elevated meaning \"talent\" are separate usages. The spellings 器 and 機 cannot be freely interchanged for devices.","examples":[{"japanese":"古い受話器から、かすかな声が聞こえた。","romaji":"Furui juwaki kara, kasukana koe ga kikoeta.","german":"A faint voice could be heard from the old telephone receiver.","furigana":"ふる|じゅわき|こえ|き"},{"japanese":"実験では、電熱器で水を温めた。","romaji":"Jikken de wa, dennetsuki de mizu o atatameta.","german":"In the experiment, the water was heated with an electric heater.","furigana":"じっけん|でんねつき|みず|あたた"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A rare, literary noun for comfortable rest or a life of leisure. The reading is いつ; the identically sounding question word いつ \"when\" is a different word. The phrase 佚を以て労を待つ is well known: one rests and awaits the enemy who is already tired. In everyday speech one says 休息 or のんびりする instead. This card covers the standalone sense of rest; other meanings of the character 佚, such as \"to be lost\", do not belong to this word usage.","examples":[{"japanese":"将軍は、佚を以て労を待つ策を取った。","romaji":"Shōgun wa, itsu o motte rō o matsu saku o totta.","german":"The general adopted the strategy of resting and awaiting the exhausted enemy.","furigana":"しょうぐん|いつ|もっ|ろう|ま|さく|と"},{"japanese":"老人は、静かな山里で佚を楽しんだ。","romaji":"Rōjin wa, shizuka na yamazato de itsu o tanoshinda.","german":"The old man enjoyed his leisure in a quiet mountain village.","furigana":"ろうじん|しず|やまざと|いつ|たの"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"しっき is a rarer secondary reading of 湿気 with the same meaning \"humidity\". In everyday speech one almost always says しっけ (separate card), for example 湿気が多い or 湿気を取る; しっき is heard more in formal or older texts and lectures. When speaking, choose しっけ. Do not confuse with the homophone 漆器 \"lacquerware\".","examples":[{"japanese":"梅雨の時期は部屋に湿気がこもりやすい。","romaji":"Tsuyu no jiki wa heya ni shikki ga komoriyasui.","german":"Humidity easily builds up in rooms during the rainy season.","furigana":"つゆ|じき|へや|しっき"},{"japanese":"この地方は夏になると湿気が多くて蒸し暑い。","romaji":"Kono chihou wa natsu ni naru to shikki ga ookute mushiatsui.","german":"In this region it is humid and muggy in summer.","furigana":"ちほう|なつ|しっき|おお|む|あつ"},{"japanese":"本は湿気を避けて保管してください。","romaji":"Hon wa shikki o sakete hokan shite kudasai.","german":"Please store the books away from moisture.","furigana":"ほん|しっき|さ|ほかん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"As a suffix meaning \"etc.\", 等 appears in news, forms and legal texts and is read とう there (東京、大阪等); in everyday language one says など (separate card), written in kana, instead. As a counter, 〜等 denotes classes and ranks: 一等 \"first prize, first class\", 二等, 三等賞. Do not confuse it with 〜等々 \"and so on\" or with 等 as the plural suffix ら (彼等).","examples":[{"japanese":"くじ引きで一等が当たりました。","romaji":"Kujibiki de ittō ga atarimashita.","german":"I won first prize in the drawing.","furigana":"び|いっとう|あ"},{"japanese":"東京、大阪等の大都市では家賃が高い。","romaji":"Tōkyō, Ōsaka tō no daitoshi de wa yachin ga takai.","german":"Rents are high in big cities such as Tokyo and Osaka.","furigana":"とうきょう|おおさかとう|だいとし|やちん|たか"},{"japanese":"昔の列車には一等車と二等車がありました。","romaji":"Mukashi no ressha ni wa ittōsha to nitōsha ga arimashita.","german":"Old trains had first-class and second-class cars.","furigana":"むかし|れっしゃ|いっとうしゃ|にとうしゃ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"〜料 forms words for fees: 入場料 \"admission\", 授業料 \"tuition\", 送料 \"shipping costs\", 手数料 \"handling fee\"; also 無料 \"free of charge\" and 有料 \"paid\". In 材料 and 調味料 it means \"stuff, material\". Similar suffixes are 〜代 (電気代), 〜費 (交通費) and 〜賃 (家賃); 料 hardly stands alone.","examples":[{"japanese":"この美術館の入場料は千円です。","romaji":"Kono bijutsukan no nyūjōryō wa sen'en desu.","german":"The admission fee for this art museum is 1,000 yen.","furigana":"びじゅつかん|にゅうじょうりょう|せんえん"},{"japanese":"三千円以上買うと、送料は無料です。","romaji":"Sanzen'en ijō kau to, sōryō wa muryō desu.","german":"Shipping is free if you buy 3,000 yen or more.","furigana":"さんぜんえんいじょうか|そうりょう|むりょう"},{"japanese":"大学の授業料が毎年上がっている。","romaji":"Daigaku no jugyōryō ga maitoshi agatte iru.","german":"University tuition goes up every year.","furigana":"だいがく|じゅぎょうりょう|まいとしあ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"等々 closes a list and stresses that there is more (パン、飲み物等々); it sounds written and somewhat more emphatic than 〜等. In speech one usually says など or the doubled などなど, which can also be written 等々. Do not confuse it with the identically sounding adverb とうとう \"finally, at last\" (its own card), which is never written 等々.","examples":[{"japanese":"会場ではパン、飲み物、お菓子等々を販売しています。","romaji":"Kaijō de wa pan, nomimono, okashi tōtō o hanbai shite imasu.","german":"Bread, drinks, sweets and so on are sold at the venue.","furigana":"かいじょう|の|もの|かしとうとう|はんばい"},{"japanese":"掃除、洗濯、料理等々、家事は毎日たくさんある。","romaji":"Sōji, sentaku, ryōri tōtō, kaji wa mainichi takusan aru.","german":"Cleaning, laundry, cooking and so on - there is plenty of housework every day.","furigana":"そうじ|せんたく|りょうりとうとう|かじ|まいにち"},{"japanese":"申込書、写真、身分証明書等々の書類をご用意ください。","romaji":"Mōshikomisho, shashin, mibun shōmeisho tōtō no shorui o go-yōi kudasai.","german":"Please prepare documents such as the application form, a photo, ID, etc.","furigana":"もうしこみしょ|しゃしん|みぶんしょうめいしょとうとう|しょるい|ようい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"notes":"Name of a grasshopper species that lives in rice fields (hence 稲子, literally \"rice child\"). In everyday use almost always in kana, in factual texts often in katakana イナゴ; the kanji 稲子 or 蝗 are rare. In general バッタ is used for locusts and grasshoppers; いなご is the smaller species typical of rice fields, which in some regions is eaten as いなごの佃煮.","examples":[{"japanese":"秋の田んぼには、いなごがたくさん跳んでいる。","romaji":"Aki no tanbo ni wa, inago ga takusan tonde iru.","german":"In autumn, many rice grasshoppers are jumping around in the rice fields.","furigana":"あき|た|と"},{"japanese":"長野では、いなごの佃煮を食べる習慣がある。","romaji":"Nagano de wa, inago no tsukudani o taberu shuukan ga aru.","german":"In Nagano there is a custom of eating inago simmered in soy sauce.","furigana":"ながの|つくだに|た|しゅうかん"},{"japanese":"子どものころ、よく友だちとイナゴを捕まえて遊んだ。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, yoku tomodachi to inago o tsukamaete asonda.","german":"As a child, I often played catching grasshoppers with my friends.","furigana":"こ|とも|つか|あそ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"The rare kanji 鸛 is hardly used; こうのとり is usual, or as the animal name コウノトリ in katakana. As in Europe, the stork is regarded as the bringer of babies. Do not confuse with the crane 鶴 (つる), which symbolizes good fortune and long life in Japan.","examples":[{"japanese":"コウノトリが赤ちゃんを運んでくるという言い伝えがある。","romaji":"Kounotori ga akachan o hakonde kuru to iu iitsutae ga aru.","german":"There is a legend that the stork brings babies.","furigana":"あか|はこ|い|つた"},{"japanese":"豊岡市では、コウノトリを野生に戻す取り組みが行われている。","romaji":"Toyooka-shi de wa, kounotori o yasei ni modosu torikumi ga okonawarete iru.","german":"In Toyooka city, efforts are being made to return storks to the wild.","furigana":"|やせい|もど|と|く|おこな"},{"japanese":"田んぼに一羽のこうのとりが降りてきた。","romaji":"Tanbo ni ichiwa no kounotori ga orite kita.","german":"A stork landed in the rice field.","furigana":"た|いちわ|お"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"両〜 stands before pairs and means \"both\": 両手 \"both hands\", 両親 \"parents\", 両側 \"both sides\", 両国 \"both countries\". After a number, 〜両 counts railway cars (十両編成 \"a train of ten cars\"). The standalone noun for \"both\" is 両方; 量 \"quantity\" and 寮 \"dormitory\" are differently written words with the same reading.","examples":[{"japanese":"重いので、両手で持ってください。","romaji":"Omoi node, ryōte de motte kudasai.","german":"It's heavy, so please hold it with both hands.","furigana":"おも|りょうて|も"},{"japanese":"道の両側に桜の木が並んでいます。","romaji":"Michi no ryōgawa ni sakura no ki ga narande imasu.","german":"Cherry trees line both sides of the road.","furigana":"みち|りょうがわ|さくら|き|なら"},{"japanese":"この電車は十両編成です。","romaji":"Kono densha wa jūryō hensei desu.","german":"This train has ten cars.","furigana":"でんしゃ|じゅうりょうへんせい"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"〜領 after a country name denotes a territory belonging to that state: アメリカ領, イギリス領, also abbreviated 英領 or 仏領. The same meaning is found in 領土 \"national territory\", 領海 \"territorial waters\" and 領空 \"airspace\". It is common mainly in news, history and geography.","examples":[{"japanese":"グアムはアメリカ領の島です。","romaji":"Guamu wa Amerika-ryō no shima desu.","german":"Guam is an island that belongs to the USA.","furigana":"りょう|しま"},{"japanese":"香港は1997年までイギリス領だった。","romaji":"Honkon wa 1997-nen made Igirisu-ryō datta.","german":"Hong Kong was British territory until 1997.","furigana":"ほんこん|ねん|りょう"},{"japanese":"許可なく他の国の領空に入ってはいけない。","romaji":"Kyoka naku hoka no kuni no ryōkū ni haitte wa ikenai.","german":"You must not enter another country's airspace without permission.","furigana":"きょか|ほか|くに|りょうくう|はい"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"The Sino-Japanese reading of 港 as a suffix after place names: 横浜港, 神戸港, 博多港. Used on its own, \"harbor\" is always 港（みなと）; こう appears only in compounds such as 空港 \"airport\", 漁港 \"fishing port\" or 入港 \"entering port\", while 港町 is read みなとまち. 島（とう）in island names works similarly.","examples":[{"japanese":"船は朝八時に横浜港に着きます。","romaji":"Fune wa asa hachiji ni Yokohamakou ni tsukimasu.","german":"The ship arrives at Yokohama Port at eight o'clock in the morning.","furigana":"ふね|あさはちじ|よこはまこう|つ"},{"japanese":"神戸港は、昔から外国との貿易で栄えてきた。","romaji":"Koubekou wa, mukashi kara gaikoku to no boueki de sakaete kita.","german":"Kobe Port has prospered on foreign trade since long ago.","furigana":"|むかし|がいこく|ぼうえき|さか"},{"japanese":"台風のため、博多港を出る船はすべて欠航になった。","romaji":"Taifuu no tame, Hakatakou o deru fune wa subete kekkou ni natta.","german":"Because of the typhoon, all ships leaving Hakata Port were canceled.","furigana":"たいふう||で|ふね|けっこう"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"A formal prefix with which a store or organization refers to itself: 当店 \"our store\", 当社 \"our company\", 当ホテル. It often appears on signs, in announcements and on websites. More humble than 当社 is 弊社 \"our (humble) company\"; in the sense of \"in question, said\", 当 is also found in 当日 \"the day in question\" and 当時 \"at that time\".","examples":[{"japanese":"当店ではクレジットカードがご利用いただけます。","romaji":"Tōten de wa kurejitto kādo ga go-riyō itadakemasu.","german":"You can use credit cards at our store.","furigana":"とうてん|りよう"},{"japanese":"当ホテルの朝食は七時からです。","romaji":"Tō hoteru no chōshoku wa shichiji kara desu.","german":"Breakfast at our hotel starts at seven o'clock.","furigana":"とう|ちょうしょく|しちじ"},{"japanese":"当社の製品はすべて日本で作られています。","romaji":"Tōsha no seihin wa subete Nihon de tsukurarete imasu.","german":"All of our company's products are made in Japan.","furigana":"とうしゃ|せいひん|にほん|つく"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"A formal, rather technical noun: in mathematics 正の数 \"positive number\" (opposite 負の数), in logic \"true\", in administrative language 正副 \"original and copy\" or \"principal and deputy\". In everyday life it appears mainly in compounds such as 正社員 \"regular employee\" or 正解 \"correct answer\". Not to be confused with the homophones 背 \"height (of a person)\", 姓 \"family name\" and 〜製 \"made in\".","examples":[{"japanese":"ゼロより大きい数を正の数という。","romaji":"Zero yori ookii kazu o sei no kazu to iu.","german":"Numbers greater than zero are called positive numbers.","furigana":"おお|かず|せい|かず"},{"japanese":"申込書は正副二通を提出してください。","romaji":"Moushikomisho wa seifuku nitsuu o teishutsu shite kudasai.","german":"Please submit the application form in two copies, original and duplicate.","furigana":"もうしこみしょ|せいふくにつう|ていしゅつ"},{"japanese":"彼は来月から正社員として働くことになった。","romaji":"Kare wa raigetsu kara seishain to shite hataraku koto ni natta.","german":"He will start working as a regular employee next month.","furigana":"かれ|らいげつ|せいしゃいん|はたら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"An elevated, literary word for \"life\" in the philosophical sense, especially in the contrast 生と死 \"life and death\" and in the phrase この世に生を受ける \"to be born\". In everyday speech one says 命 \"life (that one can lose)\" or 人生 \"life course, life\". The same character read なま (vocab-n3:3325) means \"raw, fresh, live\", read き (N1 cards) \"pure, undiluted\"; as the suffix せい it is known from 一年生 \"first-year student\".","examples":[{"japanese":"今日の授業では生と死について考えた。","romaji":"Kyou no jugyou de wa sei to shi ni tsuite kangaeta.","german":"In today's class we thought about life and death.","furigana":"きょう|じゅぎょう|せい|し|かんが"},{"japanese":"彼女は1990年に東京で生を受けた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa sen kyuuhyaku kyuujuu-nen ni Toukyou de sei o uketa.","german":"She was born in Tokyo in 1990.","furigana":"かのじょ|ねん|とうきょう|せい|う"},{"japanese":"祖父は九十年の生を静かに終えた。","romaji":"Sofu wa kyuujuu-nen no sei o shizuka ni oeta.","german":"My grandfather quietly ended his ninety years of life.","furigana":"そふ|きゅうじゅうねん|せい|しず|お"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"出 (で) is the noun of the verb 出る: \"coming out, outflow\" (水の出が悪い \"the water doesn't come out well\"), the rising of the sun and moon (日の出, own card) and \"origin\" (農家の出 \"from a farming family\"). After school or place names it serves as a suffix meaning \"graduate of, coming from\" (東大出); more neutral are 出身 and 卒業. Not to be confused with the particle で.","examples":[{"japanese":"この蛇口は水の出が悪い。","romaji":"Kono jaguchi wa mizu no de ga warui.","german":"The water comes out weakly from this faucet.","furigana":"じゃぐち|みず|で|わる"},{"japanese":"彼は東大出のエンジニアだ。","romaji":"Kare wa Tōdai-de no enjinia da.","german":"He is an engineer and a graduate of the University of Tokyo.","furigana":"かれ|とうだいで"},{"japanese":"祖母は農家の出で、料理がとても上手だ。","romaji":"Sobo wa nōka no de de, ryōri ga totemo jōzu da.","german":"My grandmother comes from a farming family and cooks very well.","furigana":"そぼ|のうか|で|りょうり|じょうず"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"とん is onomatopoeic for a short, light knock or setting down, and usually takes と: 肩をとんとたたく \"to tap lightly on the shoulder\", とんと置く. Doubled (とんとん) it describes repeated knocking; the duller, heavier sound is どん. Not to be confused with the katakana card トン \"ton\" and the adverb とんと \"not at all\".","examples":[{"japanese":"誰かが後ろから肩をとんとたたいた。","romaji":"Dareka ga ushiro kara kata o ton to tataita.","german":"Someone tapped me lightly on the shoulder from behind.","furigana":"だれ|うし|かた"},{"japanese":"彼女はコップをテーブルにとんと置いた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa koppu o tēburu ni ton to oita.","german":"She set the glass down on the table with a soft clack.","furigana":"かのじょ|お"},{"japanese":"書類を机の上でとんとそろえた。","romaji":"Shorui o tsukue no ue de ton to soroeta.","german":"He tapped the papers briefly on the desk to line them up.","furigana":"しょるい|つくえ|うえ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"灯 (ひ) is a rather literary word for artificial light, the light of a lamp: 町の灯, 灯がともる \"a light comes on\", 灯を消す. It is common in songs and literature; in everyday life one usually says 明かり/灯り (あかり, own cards) or 電気. 火 \"fire\" is read the same, but 灯 always means shining light.","examples":[{"japanese":"遠くに町の灯が見えてきた。","romaji":"Tōku ni machi no hi ga miete kita.","german":"The lights of the town came into view in the distance.","furigana":"とお|まち|ひ|み"},{"japanese":"日が暮れて、家々の窓に灯がともった。","romaji":"Hi ga kurete, ieie no mado ni hi ga tomotta.","german":"When night fell, lights came on in the windows of the houses.","furigana":"ひ|く|いえいえ|まど|ひ"},{"japanese":"夜の港に船の灯が揺れている。","romaji":"Yoru no minato ni fune no hi ga yurete iru.","german":"The lights of the ships sway in the harbor at night.","furigana":"よる|みなと|ふね|ひ|ゆ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"An older form of インク \"ink\", derived from Dutch. In everyday speech インク is mostly used today (separate card); インキ survives mainly in the printing industry (印刷インキ, 油性インキ) and on product labels of stamp pads or markers. Same meaning as インク, only older or technical; do not confuse with 陰気 \"gloomy\".","examples":[{"japanese":"この工場では新聞用の印刷インキを作っている。","romaji":"Kono kōjō de wa shinbun-yō no insatsu inki o tsukutte iru.","german":"This factory makes printing ink for newspapers.","furigana":"こうじょう|しんぶんよう|いんさつ|つく"},{"japanese":"油性インキで書いた字は、水にぬれても消えない。","romaji":"Yusei inki de kaita ji wa, mizu ni nurete mo kienai.","german":"Characters written with oil-based ink do not disappear even if they get wet.","furigana":"ゆせい|か|じ|みず|き"},{"japanese":"スタンプ台のインキが薄くなってきた。","romaji":"Sutanpu-dai no inki ga usuku natte kita.","german":"The ink in the stamp pad is getting faint.","furigana":"だい|うす"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A colloquial intransitive verb \"to get out of the way\", almost always written in kana, especially as the command どいて！ \"Make room!\" (more politely: どいていただけますか). The place takes を or から: そこをどいて, いすからどく. The same spelling 退く read しりぞく (separate card) is literary and means \"to withdraw, to step down\". The transitive counterpart is どける; do not confuse with 毒 (どく) \"poison\".","examples":[{"japanese":"すみません、ちょっとどいてください。","romaji":"Sumimasen, chotto doite kudasai.","german":"Excuse me, could you please move aside for a moment?"},{"japanese":"邪魔だから、そこをどいてくれない？","romaji":"Jama da kara, soko o doite kurenai?","german":"You're in the way, could you move away from there?","furigana":"じゃま"},{"japanese":"猫がいすの上からなかなかどかない。","romaji":"Neko ga isu no ue kara nakanaka dokanai.","german":"The cat just won't get off the chair.","furigana":"ねこ|うえ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The transitive counterpart of どく: to move something or someone out of the way, e.g. 荷物をどける, 車をどける. Almost always written in kana and rather colloquial; politely, for example, どけていただけますか. The same spelling 退ける read しりぞける (separate card) is literary and means \"to repel, to reject\" (敵を退ける, 要求を退ける).","examples":[{"japanese":"机の上の本をどけて、お皿を置いた。","romaji":"Tsukue no ue no hon o dokete, osara o oita.","german":"I moved the books off the desk and put down the plates.","furigana":"つくえ|うえ|ほん|さら|お"},{"japanese":"すみません、ここに止めてある自転車をどけてもらえますか。","romaji":"Sumimasen, koko ni tomete aru jitensha o dokete moraemasu ka.","german":"Excuse me, could you move the bicycle parked here?","furigana":"と|じてんしゃ"},{"japanese":"大きな石をどけると、下に小さな虫がたくさんいた。","romaji":"Ōkina ishi o dokeru to, shita ni chiisana mushi ga takusan ita.","german":"When I moved the big stone, there were many small insects underneath.","furigana":"おお|いし|した|ちい|むし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A colloquial i-adjective for \"slow, sluggish\", mostly in kana: 仕事がのろい, 足がのろい, 動きがのろい; sounds more disparaging than 遅い. Also \"slow-witted\" (頭の回転がのろい). The same spelling 鈍い read にぶい (separate card) rather means \"dull, insensitive\" (鈍い痛み, 勘が鈍い). Do not confuse with the homophone 呪い \"curse\"; the adverb のろのろ is related.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は仕事がのろいので、いつも残業している。","romaji":"Kare wa shigoto ga noroi node, itsumo zangyō shite iru.","german":"He works so slowly that he is always doing overtime.","furigana":"かれ|しごと|ざんぎょう"},{"japanese":"このパソコンは古くて動きがのろい。","romaji":"Kono pasokon wa furukute ugoki ga noroi.","german":"This computer is old and slow to respond.","furigana":"ふる|うご"},{"japanese":"亀は歩くのがのろいが、最後まで休まなかった。","romaji":"Kame wa aruku no ga noroi ga, saigo made yasumanakatta.","german":"The tortoise walks slowly, but it did not rest until the end.","furigana":"かめ|ある|さいご|やす"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A na-adjective for something shown openly and without disguise, often with a negative flavor: あからさまな嘘 \"a barefaced lie\", あからさまに嫌な顔をする, あからさまな差別. It is almost always written in kana; the spelling 明白 is ateji and is normally read めいはく (card 明白 \"obvious, clear\", factual and neutral). あからさま, by contrast, usually describes behavior that someone does not conceal.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼はあからさまに嫌な顔をした。","romaji":"Kare wa akarasama ni iya na kao o shita.","german":"He made a displeased face, making no attempt to hide it.","furigana":"かれ|いや|かお"},{"japanese":"そんなあからさまな嘘は、誰も信じないよ。","romaji":"Sonna akarasama na uso wa, dare mo shinjinai yo.","german":"Nobody will believe such a blatant lie.","furigana":"うそ|だれ|しん"},{"japanese":"何も買わないと分かると、店員の態度があからさまに変わった。","romaji":"Nani mo kawanai to wakaru to, ten'in no taido ga akarasama ni kawatta.","german":"When the clerk realized I wasn't going to buy anything, her attitude changed blatantly.","furigana":"なに|か|わ|てんいん|たいど|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"いきさつ is a special reading of 経緯 and refers to the circumstances of how something came about: 事件のいきさつ, いきさつを説明する, どういういきさつで \"how did it come about that ...\". The card 経緯/けいい has the same spelling and meaning but sounds factual and formal (reports, news); いきさつ is more everyday and often emphasizes personal background. Because 経緯 is usually read けいい, いきさつ is almost always written in kana.","examples":[{"japanese":"二人が結婚したいきさつを聞きました。","romaji":"Futari ga kekkon shita ikisatsu o kikimashita.","german":"I heard how the two of them came to marry.","furigana":"ふたり|けっこん|き"},{"japanese":"どういういきさつでこの会社に入ったんですか。","romaji":"Dō iu ikisatsu de kono kaisha ni haitta n desu ka.","german":"How did you come to join this company?","furigana":"かいしゃ|はい"},{"japanese":"部長に今までのいきさつを全部説明した。","romaji":"Buchō ni ima made no ikisatsu o zenbu setsumei shita.","german":"I explained the whole background to the department head.","furigana":"ぶちょう|いま|ぜんぶせつめい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"確信 is the firm conviction that something is certainly so: 確信がある \"to be sure\", 確信を持つ \"to be convinced\", 確信が深まる \"the conviction grows firmer\". The content usually precedes with という (成功するという確信). The verb 確信する \"to be firmly convinced\" has its own card. Do not confuse it with the similar-sounding 核心 \"core (of a matter)\" and 革新 \"innovation\".","examples":[{"japanese":"彼が犯人だという確信がある。","romaji":"Kare ga hannin da to iu kakushin ga aru.","german":"I'm convinced that he is the culprit.","furigana":"かれ|はんにん|かくしん"},{"japanese":"練習を続けて、合格できるという確信を持てるようになった。","romaji":"Renshū o tsuzukete, gōkaku dekiru to iu kakushin o moteru yō ni natta.","german":"By continuing to practice, I came to feel sure I could pass.","furigana":"れんしゅう|つづ|ごうかく|かくしん|も"},{"japanese":"その写真を見て、確信が深まった。","romaji":"Sono shashin o mite, kakushin ga fukamatta.","german":"Seeing that photo deepened my conviction.","furigana":"しゃしん|み|かくしん|ふか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"〜圏 forms words for a space or area defined by something: 英語圏 \"English-speaking world\", 首都圏 \"Greater Tokyo area\", 北極圏 \"Arctic, Arctic Circle\", 大気圏 \"atmosphere\". On a mobile phone, 圏外 means \"no signal\", 圏内 \"within coverage\". 圏 is hardly used alone; do not confuse it with the identically pronounced 〜県 \"prefecture\" and 〜権 \"right\".","examples":[{"japanese":"首都圏の電車は朝とても混みます。","romaji":"Shutoken no densha wa asa totemo komimasu.","german":"Trains in the Tokyo metropolitan area are very crowded in the morning.","furigana":"しゅとけん|でんしゃ|あさ|こ"},{"japanese":"英語圏の国に留学したいです。","romaji":"Eigoken no kuni ni ryūgaku shitai desu.","german":"I want to study abroad in an English-speaking country.","furigana":"えいごけん|くに|りゅうがく"},{"japanese":"夏の北極圏では、太陽が沈まない日がある。","romaji":"Natsu no hokkyokuken de wa, taiyō ga shizumanai hi ga aru.","german":"In the Arctic region in summer, there are days when the sun doesn't set.","furigana":"なつ|ほっきょくけん|たいよう|しず|ひ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"〜権 forms words for a right or authority: 選挙権 \"right to vote\", 著作権 \"copyright\", 所有権 \"ownership right\", 人権 \"human rights\". In the meaning \"power, authority\" it appears in 政権 \"governing power\" or 主導権 \"initiative, leadership\". The general word for \"right\" is 権利 (separate card); 権 itself appears almost only as a word component. Do not confuse it with the identically pronounced 〜県 and 〜圏.","examples":[{"japanese":"日本では十八歳から選挙権があります。","romaji":"Nihon de wa jūhassai kara senkyoken ga arimasu.","german":"In Japan, you have the right to vote from age eighteen.","furigana":"にほん|じゅうはっさい|せんきょけん"},{"japanese":"この写真の著作権は撮影者にあります。","romaji":"Kono shashin no chosakuken wa satsueisha ni arimasu.","german":"The copyright of this photograph belongs to the photographer.","furigana":"しゃしん|ちょさくけん|さつえいしゃ"},{"japanese":"会議では若い社員が主導権を握った。","romaji":"Kaigi de wa wakai shain ga shudōken o nigitta.","german":"At the meeting, the young employees took the initiative.","furigana":"かいぎ|わか|しゃいん|しゅどうけん|にぎ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"じょうしょ is the original reading of 情緒; today じょうちょ (separate card) is clearly more common in everyday use. Both are correct and mean the same. じょうしょ is heard mainly in technical language such as psychology and education (情緒障害, 情緒の発達) and in formal speech. Typical combinations are 情緒が不安定だ \"emotionally unstable\" and 〜の情緒が残る \"the atmosphere of ... remains\".","examples":[{"japanese":"音楽は子どもの情緒の発達に良い影響を与える。","romaji":"Ongaku wa kodomo no jōsho no hattatsu ni yoi eikyō o ataeru.","german":"Music has a good influence on the emotional development of children.","furigana":"おんがく|こ|じょうしょ|はったつ|よ|えいきょう|あた"},{"japanese":"最近、彼女は情緒が不安定で、すぐに泣いてしまう。","romaji":"Saikin, kanojo wa jōsho ga fuantei de, sugu ni naite shimau.","german":"Recently she has been emotionally unstable and bursts into tears easily.","furigana":"さいきん|かのじょ|じょうしょ|ふあんてい|な"},{"japanese":"この町には、古い港町の情緒が今も残っている。","romaji":"Kono machi ni wa, furui minatomachi no jōsho ga ima mo nokotte iru.","german":"This town still retains the atmosphere of an old port town.","furigana":"まち|ふる|みなとまち|じょうしょ|いま|のこ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"As a suffix, 高 is usually read だか and denotes a quantity or sum: 売上高 \"sales\", 生産高 \"production volume\", 残高 \"balance\" (separate card). Standing alone, たか appears mainly in the phrase 高が知れている \"is limited, not worth mentioning\", from which also comes the adverb たかが \"merely\". Do not confuse it with the adjective 高い \"high, expensive\" or the homophone 鷹 \"hawk\".","examples":[{"japanese":"今年の売上高は、去年より一割増えた。","romaji":"Kotoshi no uriagedaka wa, kyonen yori ichiwari fueta.","german":"This year's sales rose by ten percent compared with last year.","furigana":"ことし|うりあげだか|きょねん|いちわりふ"},{"japanese":"買い物の前に、口座の残高を確認した。","romaji":"Kaimono no mae ni, kōza no zandaka o kakunin shita.","german":"Before shopping, I checked my account balance.","furigana":"か|もの|まえ|こうざ|ざんだか|かくにん"},{"japanese":"一人でできることなんて、高が知れている。","romaji":"Hitori de dekiru koto nante, taka ga shirete iru.","german":"What one person can do alone is limited.","furigana":"ひとり|たか|し"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"An onomatopoeic adverb for something that flares up suddenly. Most common in カッとなる \"to flare up in anger, to lose one's temper\" (カッとなって言い過ぎた); also for glaring heat (太陽がカッと照りつける) and wide-open eyes (目をカッと見開く). Mostly written in katakana. Do not confuse with the loanword カット \"cut\"; similar but milder is むっとする \"to be annoyed\".","examples":[{"japanese":"彼はすぐカッとなる性格だ。","romaji":"Kare wa sugu katto naru seikaku da.","german":"He is the type who flies off the handle easily.","furigana":"かれ|せいかく"},{"japanese":"真夏の太陽がカッと照りつけている。","romaji":"Manatsu no taiyou ga katto teritsukete iru.","german":"The midsummer sun is blazing down fiercely.","furigana":"まなつ|たいよう|て"},{"japanese":"その音に驚いて、彼は目をカッと見開いた。","romaji":"Sono oto ni odoroite, kare wa me o katto mihiraita.","german":"Startled by the sound, he opened his eyes wide.","furigana":"おと|おどろ|かれ|め|みひら"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"A coin made of copper or a copper alloy, often in a historical or numismatic context (古い銅貨, ローマ時代の銅貨). The general term \"coin\" is 硬貨/こうか; formed in the same way are 金貨 \"gold coin\" and 銀貨 \"silver coin\". Do not confuse with the homophones どうか \"please; somehow\" and 同化 \"assimilation\".","examples":[{"japanese":"祖父の引き出しから古い銅貨が何枚も出てきた。","romaji":"Sofu no hikidashi kara furui douka ga nanmai mo dete kita.","german":"Many old copper coins turned up from my grandfather's drawer.","furigana":"そふ|ひ|だ|ふる|どうか|なんまい|で"},{"japanese":"博物館にローマ時代の銅貨が展示されている。","romaji":"Hakubutsukan ni Rooma jidai no douka ga tenji sarete iru.","german":"Copper coins from the Roman era are on display in the museum.","furigana":"はくぶつかん|じだい|どうか|てんじ"},{"japanese":"昔は銅貨一枚でパンが買えたそうだ。","romaji":"Mukashi wa douka ichimai de pan ga kaeta sou da.","german":"They say that in the old days you could buy bread with one copper coin.","furigana":"むかし|どうかいちまい|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Denotes two things that belong together as a pair or set: 対になる \"to form a pair\", 対の茶碗; as a counter 一対 (いっつい) \"one pair\". The reading たい (see the cards 対/たい), in contrast, stands for \"versus, to\" (3対1, 日本対ブラジル) and \"opposite, counterpart\" in abstract or literary use. For concrete, identically shaped pairs such as vases, figures or tableware, it is read つい; for shoes or socks one rather uses 足 or ペア.","examples":[{"japanese":"神社の入り口に一対の狛犬が置かれている。","romaji":"Jinja no iriguchi ni ittsui no komainu ga okarete iru.","german":"A pair of guardian lions stands at the entrance of the shrine.","furigana":"じんじゃ|い|ぐち|いっつい|こまいぬ|お"},{"japanese":"この二つの花瓶は対になっている。","romaji":"Kono futatsu no kabin wa tsui ni natte iru.","german":"These two vases form a pair.","furigana":"ふた|かびん|つい"},{"japanese":"結婚祝いに対の湯のみをもらった。","romaji":"Kekkon iwai ni tsui no yunomi o moratta.","german":"I received a matching pair of teacups as a wedding present.","furigana":"けっこんいわ|つい|ゆ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"An organized group of people; mostly as a suffix in compounds: 応援団 \"cheering squad\", 劇団 \"theater troupe\", 調査団 \"investigation team\", 選手団 \"team, delegation\", 暴力団 \"yakuza gang\". With numbers: 一団 \"a band, a group\". More general are 集団 \"group, mass\" and 団体 \"group, association\". Do not confuse with 段/だん \"step, rank\" and 壇/だん \"platform\".","examples":[{"japanese":"観光客の一団がバスから降りてきた。","romaji":"Kankoukyaku no ichidan ga basu kara orite kita.","german":"A group of tourists got off the bus.","furigana":"かんこうきゃく|いちだん|お"},{"japanese":"兄は高校時代、応援団に入っていた。","romaji":"Ani wa koukou jidai, ouendan ni haitte ita.","german":"My older brother was in the cheering squad in high school.","furigana":"あに|こうこうじだい|おうえんだん|はい"},{"japanese":"事故の原因を調べるため、調査団が現地に送られた。","romaji":"Jiko no gen'in o shiraberu tame, chousadan ga genchi ni okurareta.","german":"An investigation team was sent to the site to look into the cause of the accident.","furigana":"じこ|げんいん|しら|ちょうさだん|げんち|おく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A suffix for books and notebooks for entering things: 手帳 \"notebook, planner\", 電話帳 \"phone book\", 通帳 \"bankbook\", メモ帳 \"notepad\", 単語帳 \"vocabulary notebook\", 日記帳 \"diary\". Related is 帳簿 \"account book\". 帳 hardly stands alone; the card 帳/とばり \"curtain\" is a different, literary word with the same spelling.","examples":[{"japanese":"来週の予定を手帳に書き込んだ。","romaji":"Raishuu no yotei o techou ni kakikonda.","german":"I wrote next week's schedule in my planner.","furigana":"らいしゅう|よてい|てちょう|か|こ"},{"japanese":"銀行のATMで通帳に記帳した。","romaji":"Ginkou no ATM de tsuuchou ni kichou shita.","german":"I had my passbook updated at the bank ATM.","furigana":"ぎんこう|つうちょう|きちょう"},{"japanese":"新しい単語は単語帳にまとめている。","romaji":"Atarashii tango wa tangochou ni matomete iru.","german":"I collect new words in a vocabulary notebook.","furigana":"あたら|たんご|たんごちょう"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Transitive verb: to clear something out of the way or to set aside (荷物を退ける, 不良品を除けておく). As an auxiliary verb after the て-form, then mostly in kana, it expresses that one boldly accomplishes something difficult (やってのける, 言ってのける). The identically written but different word is 退ける/しりぞける \"to repel, to reject\"; in everyday speech どける is more usual for \"to clear away\", and 除ける/よける means \"to dodge\".","examples":[{"japanese":"机の上の荷物を退けて、地図を広げた。","romaji":"Tsukue no ue no nimotsu o nokete, chizu o hirogeta.","german":"I cleared the things off the desk and spread out the map.","furigana":"つくえ|うえ|にもつ|の|ちず|ひろ"},{"japanese":"彼はその難しい仕事を一人でやってのけた。","romaji":"Kare wa sono muzukashii shigoto o hitori de yatte noketa.","german":"He pulled off that difficult job all by himself.","furigana":"かれ|むずか|しごと|ひとり"},{"japanese":"傷のある商品は除けておいてください。","romaji":"Kizu no aru shouhin wa nokete oite kudasai.","german":"Please set aside the damaged goods.","furigana":"きず|しょうひん|の"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"As a standalone noun it is elevated and rather literary: 一家の長, 各部署の長; in everyday life it appears almost only in compounds such as 社長, 部長, 校長, 課長. The meaning \"advantage, strength\" lives on in 長所 and the phrase 一日の長（いちじつのちょう）\"a small lead in experience\". Do not confuse with the homophones 蝶 \"butterfly\", 兆 \"trillion\", 腸 \"intestine\" or the prefix 超〜.","examples":[{"japanese":"父は一家の長として家族を支えてきた。","romaji":"Chichi wa ikka no chō to shite kazoku o sasaete kita.","german":"My father has supported the family as its head.","furigana":"ちち|いっか|ちょう|かぞく|ささ"},{"japanese":"月曜日の朝、各部署の長が集まって会議を開いた。","romaji":"Getsuyōbi no asa, kaku busho no chō ga atsumatte kaigi o hiraita.","german":"On Monday morning, the heads of every department gathered for a meeting.","furigana":"げつようび|あさ|かくぶしょ|ちょう|あつ|かいぎ|ひら"},{"japanese":"経験の点では、彼の方に一日の長がある。","romaji":"Keiken no ten de wa, kare no hō ni ichijitsu no chō ga aru.","german":"In terms of experience, he has a slight edge.","furigana":"けいけん|てん|かれ|ほう|いちじつ|ちょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A very productive suffix for facilities and offices: 事務所, 役所, 紹介所; after some words it is voiced as じょ: 研究所, 避難所, 発電所. As a counter for places usually written 〜か所: 三か所, 何か所. The card 所/ところ is the standalone noun \"place, spot\"; しょ/じょ occurs only in bound form.","examples":[{"japanese":"父は駅前の事務所で働いている。","romaji":"Chichi wa ekimae no jimusho de hataraite iru.","german":"My father works in an office in front of the station.","furigana":"ちち|えきまえ|じむしょ|はたら"},{"japanese":"市内の三か所に避難所が設けられた。","romaji":"Shinai no sankasho ni hinanjo ga mōkerareta.","german":"Evacuation shelters were set up at three places in the city.","furigana":"しない|さん|しょ|ひなんじょ|もう"},{"japanese":"役所で住民票を取ってきた。","romaji":"Yakusho de jūminhyō o totte kita.","german":"I went to the city office to get a copy of my residence certificate.","furigana":"やくしょ|じゅうみんひょう|と"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"On its own, 書 stands above all for calligraphy as an art work: 書を習う, 書の展覧会; 書道 is the discipline. As a suffix it forms documents: 教科書, 証明書, 申込書, 報告書. For \"book\", 本 is used in everyday speech; 書 in this sense is elevated. Do not confuse with the verb 書く or the prefix 諸〜.","examples":[{"japanese":"祖母は七十歳から書を習い始めた。","romaji":"Sobo wa nanajussai kara sho o naraihajimeta.","german":"My grandmother started learning calligraphy at seventy.","furigana":"そぼ|ななじゅっさい|しょ|なら|はじ"},{"japanese":"美術館で有名な書家の書の展覧会が開かれている。","romaji":"Bijutsukan de yūmei na shoka no sho no tenrankai ga hirakarete iru.","german":"An exhibition of calligraphy by a famous master is being held at the art museum.","furigana":"びじゅつかん|ゆうめい|しょか|しょ|てんらんかい|ひら"},{"japanese":"申込書に名前と住所を記入してください。","romaji":"Mōshikomisho ni namae to jūsho o kinyū shite kudasai.","german":"Please enter your name and address on the application form.","furigana":"もうしこみしょ|なまえ|じゅうしょ|きにゅう"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"A separate entry next to 埋める/うめる: うめる is the everyday word for \"to bury, fill in\", while うずめる sounds more expressive and appears mainly in 顔をうずめる, 骨をうずめる \"to remain in one place for life\" and 群衆が広場をうずめる. Because of the double reading, it is often written in kana. The intransitive counterpart is 埋まる/うずまる.","examples":[{"japanese":"子どもは母親の胸に顔をうずめて泣き出した。","romaji":"Kodomo wa hahaoya no mune ni kao o uzumete nakidashita.","german":"The child buried his face in his mother's chest and began to cry.","furigana":"こ|ははおや|むね|かお|な|だ"},{"japanese":"一万人の観客がコンサート会場を埋めた。","romaji":"Ichiman-nin no kankyaku ga konsāto kaijō o uzumeta.","german":"Ten thousand spectators filled the concert venue.","furigana":"いちまんにん|かんきゃく|かいじょう|うず"},{"japanese":"彼はこの町に骨をうずめる覚悟で移住してきた。","romaji":"Kare wa kono machi ni hone o uzumeru kakugo de ijū shite kita.","german":"He moved to this town resolved to spend the rest of his life here.","furigana":"かれ|まち|ほね|かくご|いじゅう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A small sign or card for marking: 札を掛ける, 札を下げる; in compounds the reading stays ふだ: 名札, 値札, 荷札, 番号札. お札 is ambiguous: おふだ \"talisman from a shrine\" vs. おさつ \"banknote\" (card 札/さつ); the context decides.","examples":[{"japanese":"会議室のドアに「使用中」の札が掛かっている。","romaji":"Kaigishitsu no doa ni \"shiyōchū\" no fuda ga kakatte iru.","german":"A \"In use\" sign is hanging on the meeting room door.","furigana":"かいぎしつ|しようちゅう|ふだ|か"},{"japanese":"受付で番号札を受け取って、順番を待ってください。","romaji":"Uketsuke de bangōfuda o uketotte, junban o matte kudasai.","german":"Please take a numbered ticket at reception and wait your turn.","furigana":"うけつけ|ばんごうふだ|う|と|じゅんばん|ま"},{"japanese":"初詣で神社のお札をいただいてきた。","romaji":"Hatsumōde de jinja no ofuda o itadaite kita.","german":"At the New Year shrine visit, I received an amulet from the shrine.","furigana":"はつもうで|じんじゃ|ふだ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Attached to nouns and almost always written in kana; mostly attributive as 〜じみた or 〜じみている: 子供じみた, 芝居じみた, 年寄りじみた. Sounds disparaging (\"childish, theatrical\"), unlike the neutral 〜らしい or 〜っぽい. Second meaning \"stained with\": 汗じみたシャツ. Do not confuse with the verb 染みる/しみる \"to soak in, to sting\".","examples":[{"japanese":"彼の子供じみた言い訳にはもううんざりだ。","romaji":"Kare no kodomojimita iiwake ni wa mō unzari da.","german":"I'm fed up with his childish excuses.","furigana":"かれ|こども|い|わけ"},{"japanese":"そんな芝居じみたことはやめてくれ。","romaji":"Sonna shibaijimita koto wa yamete kure.","german":"Please stop this theatrical behavior.","furigana":"しばい"},{"japanese":"汗じみたシャツを洗濯機に入れた。","romaji":"Asejimita shatsu o sentakuki ni ireta.","german":"I put the sweat-stained shirt in the washing machine.","furigana":"あせ|せんたくき|い"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Noun for the verb 移転する (separate card): the relocation of a company headquarters, store or office, typical in notices such as 移転のお知らせ, in 本社移転 or 移転先 \"new address\". For a private move of residence, 引っ越し is more usual. In legal use 移転 also means \"transfer\" of property or rights (所有権の移転).","examples":[{"japanese":"事務所の移転に伴い、電話番号が変わります。","romaji":"Jimusho no iten ni tomonai, denwa bangou ga kawarimasu.","german":"With the relocation of the office, the phone number will change.","furigana":"じむしょ|いてん|ともな|でんわばんごう|か"},{"japanese":"店舗移転のお知らせ：来月から駅前の新しいビルで営業いたします。","romaji":"Tenpo iten no oshirase: raigetsu kara ekimae no atarashii biru de eigyou itashimasu.","german":"Notice of store relocation: from next month we will be open in a new building in front of the station.","furigana":"てんぽいてん|し|らいげつ|えきまえ|あたら|えいぎょう"},{"japanese":"土地の所有権の移転には登記が必要だ。","romaji":"Tochi no shoyuuken no iten ni wa touki ga hitsuyou da.","german":"Registration is required for the transfer of ownership of land.","furigana":"とち|しょゆうけん|いてん|とうき|ひつよう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The literary ずる-form of 応じる (separate card, N3) with the same meaning: to respond to a request, demand or invitation (〜に応ずる). Typical in official, corporate and news language, e.g. 要求に応ずる or 取材に応ずる; the written negative is 応ぜず. In conversation 応じる is almost always used.","examples":[{"japanese":"当社はお客様のご要望に応ずる用意があります。","romaji":"Tousha wa okyakusama no goyoubou ni ouzuru youi ga arimasu.","german":"Our company is prepared to meet our customers' requests.","furigana":"とうしゃ|きゃくさま|ようぼう|おう|ようい"},{"japanese":"犯人は警察との交渉に応ぜず、立てこもりを続けた。","romaji":"Hannin wa keisatsu to no koushou ni ouzezu, tatekomori o tsuzuketa.","german":"The culprit did not respond to negotiations with the police and remained barricaded.","furigana":"はんにん|けいさつ|こうしょう|おう|た|つづ"},{"japanese":"社長はマスコミの取材には応ずる考えがないと述べた。","romaji":"Shachou wa masukomi no shuzai ni wa ouzuru kangae ga nai to nobeta.","german":"The company president stated that he has no intention of responding to press interviews.","furigana":"しゃちょう|しゅざい|おう|かんが|の"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Noun for the verb 感激する (separate card): a strong, joyful emotion, often triggered by kindness or a special experience. Fixed phrases are 感激のあまり \"out of sheer emotion\" and 感激の涙. In contrast to 感動 \"being moved by art or achievement\", 感激 emphasizes the personal, emotional surge.","examples":[{"japanese":"憧れの選手に会えて、感激のあまり涙が出た。","romaji":"Akogare no senshu ni aete, kangeki no amari namida ga deta.","german":"Meeting my favorite player, I was moved to tears.","furigana":"あこが|せんしゅ|あ|かんげき|なみだ|で"},{"japanese":"初めて自分の本が書店に並んだときの感激は、今でも忘れられない。","romaji":"Hajimete jibun no hon ga shoten ni naranda toki no kangeki wa, ima demo wasurerarenai.","german":"I still can't forget the emotion when my own book was first displayed in a bookstore.","furigana":"はじ|じぶん|ほん|しょてん|なら|かんげき|いま|わす"},{"japanese":"皆様の温かいご支援に、感激の気持ちでいっぱいです。","romaji":"Minasama no atatakai goshien ni, kangeki no kimochi de ippai desu.","german":"I am filled with deep gratitude for everyone's warm support.","furigana":"みなさま|あたた|しえん|かんげき|きも"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The literary ずる-form of 感じる (separate card, N4) with the same meaning: to feel or perceive something or be moved by it (〜を感ずる, 〜に感ずる). It is found in essays, speeches and older literature; in conversation 感じる is used. Some forms coincide (感じて, 感じた); typical for writing are 感ずる, 感ずれば and 感ぜず.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼の言葉には深い誠意を感ずる。","romaji":"Kare no kotoba ni wa fukai seii o kanzuru.","german":"I sense deep sincerity in his words.","furigana":"かれ|ことば|ふか|せいい|かん"},{"japanese":"責任を感ずるならば、自ら辞任すべきだ。","romaji":"Sekinin o kanzuru naraba, mizukara jinin subeki da.","german":"If he feels responsible, he should resign of his own accord.","furigana":"せきにん|かん|みずか|じにん"},{"japanese":"時代の変化を肌で感ずることが多くなった。","romaji":"Jidai no henka o hada de kanzuru koto ga ooku natta.","german":"I increasingly feel the changes of the times firsthand.","furigana":"じだい|へんか|はだ|かん|おお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"An everyday verb for rubbing and scrubbing with pressure or a back-and-forth motion (目を擦る, 汚れを擦って落とす). It shares its kanji with 擦る/する (separate card), which stands mainly for \"to strike a match\" and \"to gamble away money\"; because of the double reading, こする is usually written in kana. Colloquially also \"to rehash a joke or topic over and over\" (ネタを擦る).","examples":[{"japanese":"子どもが眠そうに目を擦りながら起きてきた。","romaji":"Kodomo ga nemusou ni me o kosurinagara okite kita.","german":"The child came up rubbing his sleepy eyes.","furigana":"こ|ねむ|め|こす|お"},{"japanese":"鍋の焦げ付きは、スポンジで強く擦っても落ちなかった。","romaji":"Nabe no kogetsuki wa, suponji de tsuyoku kosutte mo ochinakatta.","german":"The burnt-on residue in the pot didn't come off even when I scrubbed hard with the sponge.","furigana":"なべ|こ|つ|つよ|こす|お"},{"japanese":"床の汚れをブラシでごしごし擦って落とした。","romaji":"Yuka no yogore o burashi de goshigoshi kosutte otoshita.","german":"I scrubbed the dirt off the floor vigorously with a brush.","furigana":"ゆか|よご|こす|お"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Short form of 逆さま (separate card) with the same meaning. Mostly adverbial in 逆さにする \"to turn upside down\", 逆さになる or 逆さに持つ, attributive with の (逆さの文字) and in compounds such as 逆さ吊り or 逆さ富士 (the reflection of Mt. Fuji in a lake). 逆さま sounds more complete, 逆さ is more concise and usual in compounds.","examples":[{"japanese":"地図を逆さに持っていたので、道に迷ってしまった。","romaji":"Chizu o sakasa ni motte ita node, michi ni mayotte shimatta.","german":"I was holding the map upside down, so I got lost.","furigana":"ちず|さか|も|みち|まよ"},{"japanese":"ケチャップの容器は逆さにして置いておくと使いやすい。","romaji":"Kechappu no youki wa sakasa ni shite oite oku to tsukaiyasui.","german":"A ketchup container is easier to use if you store it upside down.","furigana":"ようき|さか|お|つか"},{"japanese":"壁の絵が逆さに掛かっているのに、誰も気づかなかった。","romaji":"Kabe no e ga sakasa ni kakatte iru no ni, dare mo kizukanakatta.","german":"The picture on the wall was hanging upside down, but nobody noticed.","furigana":"かべ|え|さか|か|だれ|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"An onomatopoeic adverb for complete, often tense or awkward silence: しいんとする or しいんとなる \"to fall dead silent\", often together with 静まり返る. In manga and novels mostly written しーんと or シーン; the shorter form しんと sounds calmer and more literary. Not to be confused with 信徒/しんと \"believer\".","examples":[{"japanese":"校長先生が話し始めると、体育館はしいんと静まり返った。","romaji":"Kouchou sensei ga hanashihajimeru to, taiikukan wa shiin to shizumarikaetta.","german":"When the principal began to speak, the gymnasium fell dead silent.","furigana":"こうちょうせんせい|はな|はじ|たいいくかん|しず|かえ"},{"japanese":"夜中の病院の廊下はしーんとしていて、少し怖かった。","romaji":"Yonaka no byouin no rouka wa shiin to shite ite, sukoshi kowakatta.","german":"The hospital corridor in the middle of the night was silent, and it was a little scary.","furigana":"よなか|びょういん|ろうか|すこ|こわ"},{"japanese":"冗談を言ったつもりが、会議室がしいんとなってしまった。","romaji":"Joudan o itta tsumori ga, kaigishitsu ga shiin to natte shimatta.","german":"I meant it as a joke, but the meeting room fell awkwardly silent.","furigana":"じょうだん|い|かいぎしつ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Describes a feeling that \"soaks in\" deeply: to feel something deeply or to think about something quietly and reflectively (しみじみと感じる, しみじみ思う, しみじみ語り合う). Often with と, attributively as しみじみした気持ち. Almost always written in kana; the kanji 沁み沁み are rare. Related to the verb 染みる \"to soak in, to permeate\".","examples":[{"japanese":"親になって初めて、親のありがたさをしみじみと感じた。","romaji":"Oya ni natte hajimete, oya no arigatasa o shimijimi to kanjita.","german":"Only after becoming a parent did I deeply feel how grateful I should be to my parents.","furigana":"おや|はじ|おや|かん"},{"japanese":"久しぶりに会った友人と、昔のことをしみじみ語り合った。","romaji":"Hisashiburi ni atta yuujin to, mukashi no koto o shimijimi katariatta.","german":"I talked quietly about old times with a friend I hadn't seen in a long time.","furigana":"ひさ|あ|ゆうじん|むかし|かた|あ"},{"japanese":"祖母は古い写真をしみじみと眺めていた。","romaji":"Sobo wa furui shashin o shimijimi to nagamete ita.","german":"My grandmother was gazing wistfully at the old photographs.","furigana":"そぼ|ふる|しゃしん|なが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The literary ずる-form of 生じる (separate card, N3): something arises or occurs (問題が生ずる), more rarely transitive \"to produce, to cause\" (損害を生ずる). Very common in contracts, laws and news, e.g. 〜から生ずる損害; in conversation 生じる or 起こる is used. The written negative is 生ぜず.","examples":[{"japanese":"計画の変更により、追加の費用が生ずる可能性がある。","romaji":"Keikaku no henkou ni yori, tsuika no hiyou ga shouzuru kanousei ga aru.","german":"Due to the change in plans, additional costs may arise.","furigana":"けいかく|へんこう|ついか|ひよう|しょう|かのうせい"},{"japanese":"小さな誤解から大きな対立が生ずることもある。","romaji":"Chiisana gokai kara ookina tairitsu ga shouzuru koto mo aru.","german":"A big conflict can also arise from a small misunderstanding.","furigana":"ちい|ごかい|おお|たいりつ|しょう"},{"japanese":"本契約に関して生ずる一切の紛争は、東京地方裁判所で解決するものとする。","romaji":"Hon keiyaku ni kanshite shouzuru issai no funsou wa, Toukyou chihou saibansho de kaiketsu suru mono to suru.","german":"Any and all disputes arising in connection with this contract shall be settled at the Tokyo District Court.","furigana":"ほんけいやく|かん|しょう|いっさい|ふんそう|とうきょうちほうさいばんしょ|かいけつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The literary ずる-form of 信じる (separate card, N4) with the same meaning: to believe in something, to trust someone (〜を信ずる). Today mainly in fixed phrases such as 信ずる者は救われる, in speeches, legal texts and older literature; in everyday speech 信じる is used. Typical written forms are 信ずれば and 信ぜず.","examples":[{"japanese":"私は彼の無実を信ずる。","romaji":"Watashi wa kare no mujitsu o shinzuru.","german":"I believe in his innocence.","furigana":"わたし|かれ|むじつ|しん"},{"japanese":"「信ずる者は救われる」という言葉がある。","romaji":"\"Shinzuru mono wa sukuwareru\" to iu kotoba ga aru.","german":"There is a saying: \"Those who believe will be saved.\"","furigana":"しん|もの|すく|ことば"},{"japanese":"卒業生の皆さん、自らの信ずる道を進んでください。","romaji":"Sotsugyousei no minasan, mizukara no shinzuru michi o susunde kudasai.","german":"Dear graduates, go forth on the path you believe in.","furigana":"そつぎょうせい|みな|みずか|しん|みち|すす"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A secondary reading of 大分 with the same meaning as だいぶ (separate card): \"quite, considerably\", mostly with changes (だいぶん良くなった, だいぶん寒くなった). Today だいぶ is the standard reading; だいぶん sounds older and more leisurely and survives mainly in western Japan and among older speakers. Both are mostly written in kana, also because 大分 as a place name is read おおいた.","examples":[{"japanese":"薬を飲んだら、熱がだいぶん下がった。","romaji":"Kusuri o nondara, netsu ga daibun sagatta.","german":"After taking the medicine, my fever went down quite a bit.","furigana":"くすり|の|ねつ|さ"},{"japanese":"この辺りも昔と比べて大分変わりましたね。","romaji":"Kono atari mo mukashi to kurabete daibun kawarimashita ne.","german":"This area, too, has changed quite a lot compared with the past, hasn't it?","furigana":"あた|むかし|くら|だいぶんか"},{"japanese":"祖父は今でも「だいぶん寒うなってきたなあ」と言う。","romaji":"Sofu wa ima demo \"daibun samuu natte kita naa\" to iu.","german":"My grandfather still says, \"It's gotten quite cold, hasn't it.\"","furigana":"そふ|いま|さむ|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Noun for the verb 接近する (separate card): spatial approach, especially of storms, vehicles or celestial bodies (台風の接近), but also political or personal rapprochement (両国の接近). Very common in news and warnings, often with 〜に伴い or 〜に注意. In everyday speech 近づく is more usual for \"to come closer\".","examples":[{"japanese":"台風の接近に伴い、交通機関に乱れが出ている。","romaji":"Taifuu no sekkin ni tomonai, koutsuu kikan ni midare ga dete iru.","german":"With the approach of the typhoon, there are disruptions to transportation.","furigana":"たいふう|せっきん|ともな|こうつうきかん|みだ|で"},{"japanese":"大型船の接近を知らせる警報が鳴った。","romaji":"Oogatasen no sekkin o shiraseru keihou ga natta.","german":"An alarm sounded warning of the approach of a large ship.","furigana":"おおがたせん|せっきん|し|けいほう|な"},{"japanese":"両国の急速な接近に、周辺国は警戒を強めている。","romaji":"Ryoukoku no kyuusoku na sekkin ni, shuuhenkoku wa keikai o tsuyomete iru.","german":"In view of the rapid rapprochement between the two countries, neighboring states are becoming more vigilant.","furigana":"りょうこく|きゅうそく|せっきん|しゅうへんこく|けいかい|つよ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"The literary ずる-form of the humble verb 存じる (separate card, N3): \"to know\" or \"to think, to be of the opinion\" from the speaker's standpoint. Today almost only in very formal letters and speeches (〜と存ずる次第です) and in the fixed phrase 知らぬ存ぜぬ \"to feign ignorance\", which contains the old negative 存ぜぬ. In normal polite Japanese, 存じます and 存じません are used.","examples":[{"japanese":"今後とも皆様のご協力を賜りたく存ずる次第でございます。","romaji":"Kongo tomo minasama no gokyouryoku o tamawaritaku zonzuru shidai de gozaimasu.","german":"We humbly hope to receive everyone's continued cooperation in the future.","furigana":"こんご|みなさま|きょうりょく|たまわ|ぞん|しだい"},{"japanese":"彼は記者の質問に対して「知らぬ存ぜぬ」を貫いた。","romaji":"Kare wa kisha no shitsumon ni taishite \"shiranu zonzenu\" o tsuranuita.","german":"In response to the reporters' questions, he stubbornly stuck to claiming he knew nothing.","furigana":"かれ|きしゃ|しつもん|たい|し|ぞん|つらぬ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"断定 is a firm, unambiguous statement or conclusion; typical are 断定を避ける \"to avoid committing oneself\", 断定はできない \"cannot be said with certainty\" and the adjective 断定的な \"categorical\". The verb 断定する \"to state with certainty\" has its own card. In grammar, だ/である is called 断定の助動詞 \"auxiliary verb of assertion\".","examples":[{"japanese":"現時点では、事故の原因について断定はできません。","romaji":"Genjiten de wa, jiko no gen'in ni tsuite dantei wa dekimasen.","german":"At this point, we cannot say anything definite about the cause of the accident.","furigana":"げんじてん|じこ|げんいん|だんてい"},{"japanese":"彼は断定を避けて、「可能性がある」とだけ言った。","romaji":"Kare wa dantei o sakete, \"kanōsei ga aru\" to dake itta.","german":"He avoided making a definite statement and only said, \"It's possible.\"","furigana":"かれ|だんてい|さ|かのうせい|い"},{"japanese":"日本語の「だ」は断定の助動詞と呼ばれる。","romaji":"Nihongo no \"da\" wa dantei no jodōshi to yobareru.","german":"The Japanese \"da\" is called an assertive auxiliary verb.","furigana":"にほんご|だんてい|じょどうし|よ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"直後 means \"immediately after\" and connects with の to a noun (地震の直後) or to a verb in the た-form (家を出た直後に). It is the counterpart of 直前 \"shortly before\" (separate card) and sounds more factual than すぐ後. More rarely it means \"directly behind\" in the spatial sense.","examples":[{"japanese":"地震の直後は、電話がなかなかつながらなかった。","romaji":"Jishin no chokugo wa, denwa ga nakanaka tsunagaranakatta.","german":"Right after the earthquake, it was hard to get phone calls through.","furigana":"じしん|ちょくご|でんわ"},{"japanese":"家を出た直後に、雨が降り出した。","romaji":"Ie o deta chokugo ni, ame ga furidashita.","german":"It started raining right after I left the house.","furigana":"いえ|で|ちょくご|あめ|ふ|だ"},{"japanese":"食事の直後に激しい運動をするのは体によくない。","romaji":"Shokuji no chokugo ni hageshii undō o suru no wa karada ni yokunai.","german":"Doing intense exercise right after a meal is not good for the body.","furigana":"しょくじ|ちょくご|はげ|うんどう|からだ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"通ずる is the literary zuru-form of 通じる (separate card) and has the same meanings: \"to lead to\" (駅に通ずる道), \"to be understood\" and \"to be well versed in\". It is found in texts, speeches and fixed phrases such as 相通ずる \"to have something in common\" or the proverb すべての道はローマに通ず. In conversation 通じる is almost always used; the て-form is 通じて for both, with 通ずれば and 通ぜず also literary.","examples":[{"japanese":"この細い道は、昔は城下町に通ずる街道だった。","romaji":"Kono hosoi michi wa, mukashi wa jōkamachi ni tsūzuru kaidō datta.","german":"This narrow road used to be a highway leading to the castle town.","furigana":"ほそ|みち|むかし|じょうかまち|つう|かいどう"},{"japanese":"二人の作家の考え方には、相通ずるものがある。","romaji":"Futari no sakka no kangaekata ni wa, aitsūzuru mono ga aru.","german":"The ways of thinking of the two writers have something in common.","furigana":"ふたり|さっか|かんが|かた|あいつう"},{"japanese":"言葉は違っても、気持ちは通ずるものだ。","romaji":"Kotoba wa chigatte mo, kimochi wa tsūzuru mono da.","german":"Even if the languages differ, feelings do get through.","furigana":"ことば|ちが|きも|つう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"ドット is a loanword from English \"dot\": the dot in internet addresses (ドットコム), the polka-dot pattern (ドット柄, synonymous with 水玉模様) and the pixel (ドット絵 \"pixel art\"). Not to be confused with the adverb どっと \"all at once, in crowds\" (separate card), which is written in hiragana. For the period at the end of a sentence, one says 点 or 句点 instead.","examples":[{"japanese":"白地に赤いドットの入ったワンピースを買った。","romaji":"Shiroji ni akai dotto no haitta wanpīsu o katta.","german":"I bought a white dress with red polka dots.","furigana":"しろじ|あか|はい|か"},{"japanese":"アドレスを読むときは、「.」を「ドット」と読みます。","romaji":"Adoresu o yomu toki wa, \".\" o \"dotto\" to yomimasu.","german":"When reading out an address, \".\" is pronounced \"dot\".","furigana":"よ|よ"},{"japanese":"昔のゲームは、ドットの粗い絵で作られていた。","romaji":"Mukashi no gēmu wa, dotto no arai e de tsukurarete ita.","german":"Old video games were made of coarsely pixelated pictures.","furigana":"むかし|あら|え|つく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"はきはき describes clear, brisk and confident speaking or demeanor, mostly with 答える, 話す or しゃべる, with or without と. As はきはきする it characterizes a bright, decisive person (はきはきした子). The opposite is, for example, ぐずぐず \"hesitant\" or もごもご \"mumbling indistinctly\"; it is also often seen in katakana ハキハキ.","examples":[{"japanese":"面接では、質問にはきはきと答えましょう。","romaji":"Mensetsu de wa, shitsumon ni hakihaki to kotaemashō.","german":"In the interview, let's answer the questions clearly and crisply.","furigana":"めんせつ|しつもん|こた"},{"japanese":"あの子はいつもはきはきしていて、気持ちがいい。","romaji":"Ano ko wa itsumo hakihaki shite ite, kimochi ga ii.","german":"That child is always so lively and bright, it's a pleasure.","furigana":"こ|きも"},{"japanese":"声が小さいので、もっとはきはき話してください。","romaji":"Koe ga chiisai node, motto hakihaki hanashite kudasai.","german":"You're speaking too quietly, please speak more clearly and distinctly.","furigana":"こえ|ちい|はな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"ぴかぴか (also ピカピカ) describes repeated sparkling or shining (ぴかぴか光る) and something polished spotless or brand-new (ぴかぴかの車, ピカピカにする). As an adjective it takes の, だ or で; ぴかぴかの一年生 is a \"brand-new first-grader\". ぴかっと, by contrast, denotes a single flash, きらきら rather a gentle glitter.","examples":[{"japanese":"夜空に星がぴかぴか光っている。","romaji":"Yozora ni hoshi ga pikapika hikatte iru.","german":"Stars are twinkling in the night sky.","furigana":"よぞら|ほし|ひか"},{"japanese":"新しい靴はぴかぴかで、はくのがもったいない。","romaji":"Atarashii kutsu wa pikapika de, haku no ga mottainai.","german":"The new shoes are so shiny that it feels a waste to wear them.","furigana":"あたら|くつ"},{"japanese":"大掃除をして、台所をピカピカにした。","romaji":"Ōsōji o shite, daidokoro o pikapika ni shita.","german":"I did a big cleaning and made the kitchen sparkling clean.","furigana":"おおそうじ|だいどころ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"無沙汰 denotes a long absence of letters, calls or visits. In everyday speech it appears almost always politely as ご無沙汰 (separate card), e.g. ご無沙汰しています \"I haven't been in touch for a long time\"; the bare 無沙汰 is found more in letters and written language (無沙汰を詫びる). Also very common is 手持ち無沙汰 \"having nothing to do, standing around idly\".","examples":[{"japanese":"忙しさにかまけて、実家にはすっかり無沙汰をしている。","romaji":"Isogashisa ni kamakete, jikka ni wa sukkari busata o shite iru.","german":"Caught up in being busy, I have completely neglected to keep in touch with my parents' home.","furigana":"いそが|じっか|ぶさた"},{"japanese":"日頃の無沙汰をお詫び申し上げます。","romaji":"Higoro no busata o owabi mōshiagemasu.","german":"I apologize for my long silence.","furigana":"ひごろ|ぶさた|わ|もう|あ"},{"japanese":"待ち合わせの相手が来るまで、手持ち無沙汰でスマホを見ていた。","romaji":"Machiawase no aite ga kuru made, temochi-busata de sumaho o mite ita.","german":"Until the person I was meeting arrived, I looked at my phone with nothing to do.","furigana":"ま|あ|あいて|く|ても|ぶさた|み"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"盆 has two meanings: \"tray\" and the summer festival of the dead. Used alone for the latter, one usually says the polite お盆 (separate card); the bare 盆 appears mainly in compounds such as 盆踊り \"Bon dance\", 盆休み \"Bon holidays\" and 盆栽 \"bonsai\" as well as in phrases such as 盆と正月 \"Bon and New Year\" or 覆水盆に返らず \"what is done cannot be undone\".","examples":[{"japanese":"盆と正月には、家族みんなで実家に集まる。","romaji":"Bon to shōgatsu ni wa, kazoku minna de jikka ni atsumaru.","german":"At Obon and New Year, the whole family gathers at my parents' home.","furigana":"ぼん|しょうがつ|かぞく|じっか|あつ"},{"japanese":"夏祭りで、浴衣を着て盆踊りを踊った。","romaji":"Natsumatsuri de, yukata o kite bon'odori o odotta.","german":"At the summer festival, I wore a yukata and danced the Bon dance.","furigana":"なつまつ|ゆかた|き|ぼんおど|おど"},{"japanese":"店員が盆に湯呑みを載せて運んできた。","romaji":"Ten'in ga bon ni yunomi o nosete hakonde kita.","german":"The staff member brought the teacups on a tray.","furigana":"てんいん|ぼん|ゆの|の|はこ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"〜科 is a productive suffix for specialist departments: in hospitals (内科 \"internal medicine\", 外科 \"surgery\", 小児科 \"pediatrics\"), at schools and universities for courses of study (英文科, 普通科), and in biology for families (ネコ科, バラ科). Do not confuse it with 課 (separate card), which denotes lessons and sections in companies and government offices; hospitals and universities have 科. Related is 学科 \"department\".","examples":[{"japanese":"熱が下がらないので、内科を受診した。","romaji":"Netsu ga sagaranai node, naika o jushin shita.","german":"My fever wouldn't go down, so I saw an internal medicine doctor.","furigana":"ねつ|さ|ないか|じゅしん"},{"japanese":"姉は大学の英文科で文学を学んでいる。","romaji":"Ane wa daigaku no eibunka de bungaku o manande iru.","german":"My older sister studies literature in the English department at university.","furigana":"あね|だいがく|えいぶんか|ぶんがく|まな"},{"japanese":"ライオンもトラも、ネコ科の動物だ。","romaji":"Raion mo tora mo, nekoka no dōbutsu da.","german":"Lions and tigers are both members of the cat family.","furigana":"か|どうぶつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"改定 is the renewed setting of rules, prices, rates or laws, typical in 料金改定, 運賃改定, 価格改定 and 〜の改定を行う; 料金改定 is often a euphemism for a price increase. The verb 改定する has its own card. The identically read 改訂 (separate card), by contrast, means the revision of a text or book (改訂版 \"revised edition\"); 海底 \"seabed\" is merely a homophone.","examples":[{"japanese":"来月、電気料金の改定が行われる。","romaji":"Raigetsu, denki ryōkin no kaitei ga okonawareru.","german":"The electricity rates will be revised next month.","furigana":"らいげつ|でんきりょうきん|かいてい|おこな"},{"japanese":"就業規則の改定について、社員向けの説明会が開かれた。","romaji":"Shūgyō kisoku no kaitei ni tsuite, shain-muke no setsumeikai ga hirakareta.","german":"An explanatory meeting for employees was held about the revision of the work regulations.","furigana":"しゅうぎょうきそく|かいてい|しゃいんむ|せつめいかい|ひら"},{"japanese":"鉄道会社は運賃改定を発表した。","romaji":"Tetsudō gaisha wa unchin kaitei o happyō shita.","german":"The railway company announced a fare revision.","furigana":"てつどうがいしゃ|うんちんかいてい|はっぴょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"改良 refers to the improvement of products, processes or varieties, typical in 品種改良 \"(selective) breeding\", 改良を加える \"to improve\" and 改良の余地がある \"to have room for improvement\". The verb 改良する has its own card. Compared with 改善 \"improvement (of conditions, procedures)\", 改良 emphasizes the technical further development of a concrete object; 改革, by contrast, is the fundamental reform of a system.","examples":[{"japanese":"この米は、長年の品種改良によって生まれた。","romaji":"Kono kome wa, naganen no hinshu kairyō ni yotte umareta.","german":"This rice was created through many years of cultivar improvement.","furigana":"こめ|ながねん|ひんしゅかいりょう|う"},{"japanese":"製品にさらに改良を加えて、来年発売する予定だ。","romaji":"Seihin ni sara ni kairyō o kuwaete, rainen hatsubai suru yotei da.","german":"We plan to make further improvements to the product and release it next year.","furigana":"せいひん|かいりょう|くわ|らいねんはつばい|よてい"},{"japanese":"新しいモデルは、エンジンの改良で燃費がよくなった。","romaji":"Atarashii moderu wa, enjin no kairyō de nenpi ga yoku natta.","german":"The new model has better fuel economy thanks to engine improvements.","furigana":"あたら|かいりょう|ねんぴ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Rarely stands alone, but mostly as a word element: 共産主義 \"communism\" (vocab-n2:21), 共産党 \"Communist Party\" (vocab-n3:3013), 共産圏 \"Eastern bloc, communist bloc\", 共産化 \"communization\". In newspaper headlines and election reports, 共産 stands alone as a short form for the Communist Party. The opposing concept is 資本主義 \"capitalism\".","examples":[{"japanese":"冷戦時代、その国は共産圏に属していた。","romaji":"Reisen jidai, sono kuni wa kyousanken ni zokushite ita.","german":"During the Cold War, that country belonged to the communist bloc.","furigana":"れいせんじだい|くに|きょうさんけん|ぞく"},{"japanese":"新聞に「共産、議席を倍増」という見出しが出た。","romaji":"Shinbun ni \"Kyousan, giseki o baizou\" to iu midashi ga deta.","german":"The newspaper ran the headline \"Communists double their seats\".","furigana":"しんぶん|きょうさん|ぎせき|ばいぞう|みだ|で"},{"japanese":"その国は戦後、急速に共産化した。","romaji":"Sono kuni wa sengo, kyuusoku ni kyousanka shita.","german":"After the war, that country rapidly became communist.","furigana":"くに|せんご|きゅうそく|きょうさんか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A suffix for schools in words such as 母校 \"alma mater\", 出身校 \"school one comes from\", 進学校 \"college-preparatory school\", and a counter for schools (3校, 48校 with the sound change はっこう). In publishing it denotes the proofreading stage: 初校 \"first proof\", 再校 \"second proof\", cf. 校正 \"proofreading\". On its own, the word for \"school\" is 学校; do not confuse it with the identically pronounced suffix 〜港 \"harbor\".","examples":[{"japanese":"兄は県内でも有名な進学校に通っている。","romaji":"Ani wa kennai demo yuumei na shingakkou ni kayotte iru.","german":"My older brother attends a college-prep school that is well known even within the prefecture.","furigana":"あに|けんない|ゆうめい|しんがっこう|かよ"},{"japanese":"今年の大会には全国から48校が参加した。","romaji":"Kotoshi no taikai ni wa zenkoku kara yonjuuhakkou ga sanka shita.","german":"Forty-eight schools from across the country took part in this year's tournament.","furigana":"ことし|たいかい|ぜんこく|こう|さんか"},{"japanese":"初校のゲラを来週までにチェックしてください。","romaji":"Shokou no gera o raishuu made ni chekku shite kudasai.","german":"Please check the first proofs by next week.","furigana":"しょこう|らいしゅう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The noun for the verb 固定する \"to fasten\" (vocab-n2:543), very common in compounds: 固定電話 \"landline telephone\", 固定費 \"fixed costs\", 固定給 \"fixed salary\", 固定観念 \"fixed idea, stereotype\". As a noun it takes の or が: ギプスでの固定, 固定が甘い \"not properly fastened\". The opposite is 変動 \"fluctuation\" (固定費 vs. 変動費).","examples":[{"japanese":"最近は固定電話を持たない家庭が増えている。","romaji":"Saikin wa kotei denwa o motanai katei ga fuete iru.","german":"Recently, the number of households without a landline is increasing.","furigana":"さいきん|こていでんわ|も|かてい|ふ"},{"japanese":"家賃などの固定費を見直せば、毎月の出費を減らせる。","romaji":"Yachin nado no koteihi o minaoseba, maitsuki no shuppi o heraseru.","german":"If you review fixed costs such as rent, you can reduce your monthly expenses.","furigana":"やちん|こていひ|みなお|まいつき|しゅっぴ|へ"},{"japanese":"骨折した足はギプスでの固定が必要だ。","romaji":"Kossetsu shita ashi wa gipusu de no kotei ga hitsuyou da.","german":"A broken leg needs to be immobilized in a cast.","furigana":"こっせつ|あし|こてい|ひつよう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A suffix after nouns or verbs, usually written in kana: 三か月ごとの点検 \"inspection every three months\", 日ごと \"day by day\", 会うごとに \"every time we meet\". The adverbial form with に has its own card (〜毎に/ごとに, vocab-n3:2554); this card covers the bare 〜ごと, especially 〜ごとの + noun. Before a noun, by contrast, 毎 is read まい (毎日, 毎週); do not confuse it with 〜ごと \"together with\" (皮ごと \"with the skin\").","examples":[{"japanese":"三か月ごとの定期点検を忘れないでください。","romaji":"Sankagetsu goto no teiki tenken o wasurenaide kudasai.","german":"Don't forget the periodic inspection every three months.","furigana":"さん|げつ|ていきてんけん|わす"},{"japanese":"日ごと寒さが増していく。","romaji":"Higoto samusa ga mashite iku.","german":"It's getting colder day by day.","furigana":"ひ|さむ|ま"},{"japanese":"一雨ごとに暖かくなってきた。","romaji":"Hitoame goto ni atatakaku natte kita.","german":"It gets warmer with each rainfall.","furigana":"ひとあめ|あたた"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The noun for the verb 参照する \"to look up, refer to\" (vocab-n2:573), typical of written language and instructions: 〜を参照のこと \"see ...\", 相互参照 \"cross-reference\", 参照ボタン \"Browse button\" on a computer. Unlike 参考 \"a guide, reference for orientation\", 参照 means looking something up at a specific place.","examples":[{"japanese":"詳しくは次のページを参照のこと。","romaji":"Kuwashiku wa tsugi no peeji o sanshou no koto.","german":"For details, see the next page.","furigana":"くわ|つぎ|さんしょう"},{"japanese":"辞書の相互参照をたどると、関連語が見つかる。","romaji":"Jisho no sougo sanshou o tadoru to, kanrengo ga mitsukaru.","german":"Following the dictionary's cross-references, you can find related words.","furigana":"じしょ|そうごさんしょう|かんれんご|み"},{"japanese":"保存先のフォルダは「参照」ボタンで選べます。","romaji":"Hozonsaki no foruda wa \"sanshou\" botan de erabemasu.","german":"You can choose the destination folder with the \"Browse\" button.","furigana":"ほぞんさき|さんしょう|えら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The noun for the verb 収集する \"to collect\" (vocab-n2:572), often with の or in compounds: 切手の収集 \"stamp collecting\", 情報収集 \"information gathering\", ごみ収集 \"garbage collection\", 収集家 \"collector\". The spelling 蒐集 emphasizes collecting works of art or collectibles and is rare. In everyday speech, for simple gathering one rather says 集める.","examples":[{"japanese":"祖父の趣味は古い切手の収集だ。","romaji":"Sofu no shumi wa furui kitte no shuushuu da.","german":"My grandfather's hobby is collecting old stamps.","furigana":"そふ|しゅみ|ふる|きって|しゅうしゅう"},{"japanese":"この地区のごみ収集は火曜日と金曜日です。","romaji":"Kono chiku no gomi shuushuu wa kayoubi to kin'youbi desu.","german":"Garbage collection in this district is on Tuesdays and Fridays.","furigana":"ちく|しゅうしゅう|かようび|きんようび"},{"japanese":"事件の情報収集に時間がかかっている。","romaji":"Jiken no jouhou shuushuu ni jikan ga kakatte iru.","german":"Gathering information about the incident is taking time.","furigana":"じけん|じょうほうしゅうしゅう|じかん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The noun for the verb 照合する \"to check against\" (vocab-n2:552): something is checked against a template, e.g. 指紋の照合 \"fingerprint matching\", データ照合, 本人照合 \"identity verification\". The verb is used with 〜と照合する; in colloquial speech 照らし合わせる corresponds to it. 比較, by contrast, is general comparing without the intention of checking.","examples":[{"japanese":"現場に残された指紋の照合を急いでいる。","romaji":"Genba ni nokosareta shimon no shougou o isoide iru.","german":"We are hurrying to match the fingerprints left at the scene.","furigana":"げんば|のこ|しもん|しょうごう|いそ"},{"japanese":"入金と請求書の照合は経理の大事な仕事だ。","romaji":"Nyuukin to seikyuusho no shougou wa keiri no daiji na shigoto da.","german":"Matching deposits against invoices is an important part of accounting work.","furigana":"にゅうきん|せいきゅうしょ|しょうごう|けいり|だいじ|しごと"},{"japanese":"パスワードの照合に失敗しました。","romaji":"Pasuwaado no shougou ni shippai shimashita.","german":"Password verification failed.","furigana":"しょうごう|しっぱい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A noun used mainly attributively with の or in compounds: 事前の準備 \"preparation in advance\", 事前登録 \"advance registration\", 事前調査 \"preliminary survey\". The adverbial form 事前に \"in advance\" has its own card (vocab-n2:1323). The opposite is 事後 \"after the fact\" (事後報告); do not confuse it with the homophone 慈善 \"charity\" (vocab-n2:37).","examples":[{"japanese":"事前の準備がしっかりしていたので、会議はスムーズに進んだ。","romaji":"Jizen no junbi ga shikkari shite ita node, kaigi wa sumuuzu ni susunda.","german":"Because the advance preparation was thorough, the meeting went smoothly.","furigana":"じぜん|じゅんび|かいぎ|すす"},{"japanese":"このイベントへの参加には事前登録が必要です。","romaji":"Kono ibento e no sanka ni wa jizen touroku ga hitsuyou desu.","german":"Advance registration is required to take part in this event.","furigana":"さんか|じぜんとうろく|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"事前調査の結果、計画は一部変更された。","romaji":"Jizen chousa no kekka, keikaku wa ichibu henkou sareta.","german":"As a result of the preliminary survey, the plan was partly changed.","furigana":"じぜんちょうさ|けっか|けいかく|いちぶへんこう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The Sino-Japanese reading of 実 as an independent noun, mainly in fixed expressions: 実の親 \"biological parents\", 実を言うと \"to be honest\", 実がない \"lacking substance\", 名より実を取る \"to prefer substance over appearance\". The adverb 実は \"actually, frankly\" has its own card (vocab-n3:3281). 実/み (vocab-n3:correction:mi-fruit), by contrast, is \"fruit\" and is read in expressions such as 実を結ぶ.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は私の実の姉ではなく、義理の姉です。","romaji":"Kanojo wa watashi no jitsu no ane de wa naku, giri no ane desu.","german":"She is not my blood sister but my sister-in-law.","furigana":"かのじょ|わたし|じつ|あね|ぎり|あね"},{"japanese":"実を言うと、まだ宿題が終わっていないんです。","romaji":"Jitsu o iu to, mada shukudai ga owatte inai n desu.","german":"To tell the truth, I haven't finished my homework yet.","furigana":"じつ|い|しゅくだい|お"},{"japanese":"名ばかりで実が伴わない改革だ。","romaji":"Na bakari de jitsu ga tomonawanai kaikaku da.","german":"It's a reform in name only, with no substance behind it.","furigana":"な|じつ|ともな|かいかく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The noun for the verb 対抗する \"to compete with, match\" (vocab-n2:474), frequent in compounds: 対抗意識 \"competitive spirit\", 対抗馬 \"rival candidate\", クラス対抗 \"competition between classes\", 対抗策 \"countermeasure\". 対立 emphasizes the opposition, 対抗 the measuring oneself against an opponent. Do not confuse it with the homophone 対向 \"oncoming\" (対向する, vocab-n2:1437; 対向車).","examples":[{"japanese":"二人は互いに強い対抗意識を持っている。","romaji":"Futari wa tagai ni tsuyoi taikou ishiki o motte iru.","german":"The two have a strong sense of rivalry toward each other.","furigana":"ふたり|たが|つよ|たいこういしき|も"},{"japanese":"来週、クラス対抗のリレー大会がある。","romaji":"Raishuu, kurasu taikou no riree taikai ga aru.","german":"Next week there is an inter-class relay tournament.","furigana":"らいしゅう|たいこう|たいかい"},{"japanese":"次の選挙で現職の対抗馬と目されているのは若手の議員だ。","romaji":"Tsugi no senkyo de genshoku no taikouba to mokusarete iru no wa wakate no giin da.","german":"In the next election, a young legislator is seen as the incumbent's rival candidate.","furigana":"つぎ|せんきょ|げんしょく|たいこうば|もく|わかて|ぎいん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The noun for the verb 短縮する \"to shorten\" (vocab-n2:451), mainly for time and duration: 時間短縮 \"time reduction\", 短縮授業 \"shortened classes\", 短縮営業 \"shortened business hours\", 短縮ダイヤル \"speed dial\". The opposite is 延長 \"extension\". For omitting parts of a text, one rather says 省略.","examples":[{"japanese":"働き方改革で労働時間の短縮が進んでいる。","romaji":"Hatarakikata kaikaku de roudou jikan no tanshuku ga susunde iru.","german":"Working hours are being shortened thanks to work-style reform.","furigana":"はたら|かたかいかく|ろうどうじかん|たんしゅく|すす"},{"japanese":"台風のため、今日は短縮授業になった。","romaji":"Taifuu no tame, kyou wa tanshuku jugyou ni natta.","german":"Because of the typhoon, classes were shortened today.","furigana":"たいふう|きょう|たんしゅくじゅぎょう"},{"japanese":"電話の短縮ダイヤルに母の番号を登録した。","romaji":"Denwa no tanshuku daiyaru ni haha no bangou o touroku shita.","german":"I registered my mother's number on the phone's speed dial.","furigana":"でんわ|たんしゅく|はは|ばんごう|とうろく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A noun for getting out of an enclosed place or danger, often in compounds: 脱出口 \"emergency exit\", 国外脱出 \"fleeing the country\", 脱出ゲーム \"escape room\". As a verb (\"to escape\", 〜から脱出する) there is the separate card 脱出する. Unlike the everyday 逃げる, 脱出 emphasizes getting out of a predicament and sounds factual to journalistic.","examples":[{"japanese":"非常時には、この脱出口を使ってください。","romaji":"Hijōji ni wa, kono dasshutsuguchi o tsukatte kudasai.","german":"In an emergency, please use this exit.","furigana":"ひじょうじ|だっしゅつぐち|つか"},{"japanese":"事故の後、乗客全員の脱出が確認された。","romaji":"Jiko no ato, jōkyaku zen'in no dasshutsu ga kakunin sareta.","german":"After the accident, the escape of all passengers was confirmed.","furigana":"じこ|あと|じょうきゃくぜんいん|だっしゅつ|かくにん"},{"japanese":"週末に友達と脱出ゲームに行った。","romaji":"Shūmatsu ni tomodachi to dasshutsu gēmu ni itta.","german":"On the weekend, I went to an escape room with friends.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|ともだち|だっしゅつ|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Attached directly to a noun, it means \"full of ..., covered all over with ...\", almost always with a negative undertone: 間違いだらけ, 泥だらけ, 傷だらけ, 借金だらけ. The result behaves like a noun or na-adjective (〜だらけだ, 〜だらけの, 〜だらけになる). A neutral \"full of\" would be 〜でいっぱい; 〜まみれ, by contrast, emphasizes something sticking to the surface (汗まみれ). Usually written in kana.","examples":[{"japanese":"子どもたちは泥だらけになって帰ってきた。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa doro-darake ni natte kaette kita.","german":"The children came home covered in mud.","furigana":"こ|どろ|かえ"},{"japanese":"この報告書は間違いだらけで、とても提出できない。","romaji":"Kono hōkokusho wa machigai-darake de, totemo teishutsu dekinai.","german":"This report is full of mistakes and can't possibly be submitted.","furigana":"ほうこくしょ|まちが|ていしゅつ"},{"japanese":"部屋がゴミだらけで、足の踏み場もない。","romaji":"Heya ga gomi-darake de, ashi no fumiba mo nai.","german":"The room is full of trash, with nowhere to step.","furigana":"へや|あし|ふ|ば"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"After a place or time, 〜着 means \"arrival\" and appears on timetables as the counterpart of 〜発: 12時東京着. As a counter it counts suits and garments (スーツ一着, 二着) as well as placings in a race (一着 \"first place\"); note いっちゃく for 一着. Do not confuse it with the verb 着る (きる) or with 到着, which denotes arrival as a process.","examples":[{"japanese":"この新幹線は十二時東京着です。","romaji":"Kono shinkansen wa jūni-ji Tōkyō-chaku desu.","german":"This Shinkansen arrives at Tokyo at twelve o'clock.","furigana":"しんかんせん|じゅうにじとうきょうちゃく"},{"japanese":"面接のためにスーツを一着買った。","romaji":"Mensetsu no tame ni sūtsu o itchaku katta.","german":"I bought a suit for the interview.","furigana":"めんせつ|いっちゃくか"},{"japanese":"彼はマラソン大会で一着になった。","romaji":"Kare wa marason taikai de itchaku ni natta.","german":"He came in first in the marathon.","furigana":"かれ|たいかい|いっちゃく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A noun for the landing of an aircraft or spacecraft, the opposite of 離陸 \"takeoff\". Typical in compounds such as 着陸態勢, 緊急着陸, 月面着陸 and in onboard announcements. The verb \"to land\" (〜に着陸する) is taught by the card 着陸する.","examples":[{"japanese":"当機はまもなく着陸態勢に入ります。","romaji":"Tōki wa mamonaku chakuriku taisei ni hairimasu.","german":"This aircraft will shortly begin its landing approach.","furigana":"とうき|ちゃくりくたいせい|はい"},{"japanese":"エンジンの故障で緊急着陸を余儀なくされた。","romaji":"Enjin no koshō de kinkyū chakuriku o yoginaku sareta.","german":"Because of an engine failure, we were forced to make an emergency landing.","furigana":"こしょう|きんきゅうちゃくりく|よぎ"},{"japanese":"千九百六十九年に人類初の月面着陸が成功した。","romaji":"Sen kyūhyaku rokujūkyū-nen ni jinrui hatsu no getsumen chakuriku ga seikō shita.","german":"In 1969, the first human moon landing succeeded.","furigana":"せんきゅうひゃくろくじゅうきゅうねん|じんるいはつ|げつめんちゃくりく|せいこう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"As a noun or na-adjective \"connoisseur, specialist\": 彼はワインの通だ, 通の食べ方; as a suffix in 日本通, 事情通, 食通. Also a counter for letters, emails and documents: 一通 (いっつう), 二通, 三通. Not to be confused with 通り (とおり, \"street; as ...\") and the verb 通る, which use the same kanji with a different reading.","examples":[{"japanese":"天ぷらは塩で食べるのが通だと言われている。","romaji":"Tenpura wa shio de taberu no ga tsū da to iwarete iru.","german":"They say that connoisseurs eat tempura with salt.","furigana":"てん|しお|た|つう|い"},{"japanese":"今朝、取引先からメールが三通届いた。","romaji":"Kesa, torihikisaki kara mēru ga santsū todoita.","german":"This morning three emails arrived from the business partner.","furigana":"けさ|とりひきさき|さんつうとど"},{"japanese":"彼は日本酒の通で、飲んだだけで銘柄を当てられる。","romaji":"Kare wa nihonshu no tsū de, nonda dake de meigara o aterareru.","german":"He is a sake connoisseur and can guess the brand just by drinking it.","furigana":"かれ|にほんしゅ|つう|の|めいがら|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A noun for reaching a goal, point or level, also abstractly; rather literary. Typical in 到達点, 到達目標, 山頂への到達. Unlike 到着 (arrival of people or vehicles at a place), 到達 emphasizes that a goal is reached after a path or effort. The verb 〜に到達する has its own card.","examples":[{"japanese":"山頂への到達には約六時間かかる。","romaji":"Sanchō e no tōtatsu ni wa yaku roku-jikan kakaru.","german":"Reaching the summit takes about six hours.","furigana":"さんちょう|とうたつ|やくろくじかん"},{"japanese":"今学期の到達目標を先生と確認した。","romaji":"Kongakki no tōtatsu mokuhyō o sensei to kakunin shita.","german":"I confirmed this term's attainment goals with my teacher.","furigana":"こんがっき|とうたつもくひょう|せんせい|かくにん"},{"japanese":"この合意は交渉の最終的な到達点とは言えない。","romaji":"Kono gōi wa kōshō no saishūteki na tōtatsuten to wa ienai.","german":"This agreement cannot be called the final point reached in the negotiations.","furigana":"ごうい|こうしょう|さいしゅうてき|とうたつてん|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A noun for breaking through a barrier, overcoming a hurdle and surpassing a mark. Very frequent in 突破口 \"starting point for a breakthrough\", 予選突破 and in headlines such as 〜万人突破. The verb (〜を突破する) is taught by the card 突破する.","examples":[{"japanese":"交渉の突破口がなかなか見つからない。","romaji":"Kōshō no toppakō ga nakanaka mitsukaranai.","german":"We can't find a breakthrough in the negotiations.","furigana":"こうしょう|とっぱこう|み"},{"japanese":"展示会の来場者数が百万人突破と発表された。","romaji":"Tenjikai no raijōshasū ga hyakuman-nin toppa to happyō sareta.","german":"It was announced that exhibition attendance had topped one million.","furigana":"てんじかい|らいじょうしゃすう|ひゃくまんにんとっぱ|はっぴょう"},{"japanese":"チームは予選突破を目指して毎日練習している。","romaji":"Chīmu wa yosen toppa o mezashite mainichi renshū shite iru.","german":"The team practices every day aiming to get through the qualifying round.","furigana":"よせんとっぱ|めざ|まいにちれんしゅう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A noun for (often formal) agreement or consent: 同意を得る, 同意を求める, 同意書, 保護者の同意. Also \"same meaning\" as in 同意語 \"synonym\". 賛成 expresses support for a proposal, while 同意 is consent, which may also be legally required. The verb 〜に同意する has its own card.","examples":[{"japanese":"手術の前に、こちらの同意書にサインしてください。","romaji":"Shujutsu no mae ni, kochira no dōisho ni sain shite kudasai.","german":"Before the surgery, please sign this consent form.","furigana":"しゅじゅつ|まえ|どういしょ"},{"japanese":"未成年の場合は保護者の同意が必要です。","romaji":"Miseinen no baai wa hogosha no dōi ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"In the case of minors, parental consent is required.","furigana":"みせいねん|ばあい|ほごしゃ|どうい|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"「美しい」と「きれい」はほぼ同意の言葉だ。","romaji":"\"Utsukushii\" to \"kirei\" wa hobo dōi no kotoba da.","german":"\"美しい\" and \"きれい\" are nearly synonymous words.","furigana":"うつく|どうい|ことば"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The noun for pregnancy or conception, factual and medical: 妊娠中, 妊娠三か月, 妊娠検査. Related are 妊婦 (にんぷ, \"pregnant woman\") and 出産 (\"childbirth\"). The verb \"to become pregnant\" (妊娠する) is taught by the card 妊娠する.","examples":[{"japanese":"妊娠中はお酒を控えてください。","romaji":"Ninshinchū wa osake o hikaete kudasai.","german":"Please refrain from alcohol during pregnancy.","furigana":"にんしんちゅう|さけ|ひか"},{"japanese":"姉は今、妊娠五か月だ。","romaji":"Ane wa ima, ninshin go-kagetsu da.","german":"My older sister is now five months pregnant.","furigana":"あね|いま|にんしんご|げつ"},{"japanese":"職場に妊娠を報告するタイミングに悩んでいる。","romaji":"Shokuba ni ninshin o hōkoku suru taimingu ni nayande iru.","german":"I'm worried about when to tell my workplace about the pregnancy.","furigana":"しょくば|にんしん|ほうこく|なや"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The noun for distributing to many people, factual and official: 配布資料 \"handout\", 無料配布 \"free distribution\", チラシの配布. In everyday speech one rather uses the verb 配る; 配達, by contrast, means delivery to an address. The verb 〜を配布する has its own card.","examples":[{"japanese":"配布資料は会議の後で回収します。","romaji":"Haifu shiryō wa kaigi no ato de kaishū shimasu.","german":"The handouts will be collected after the meeting.","furigana":"はいふしりょう|かいぎ|あと|かいしゅう"},{"japanese":"駅前でティッシュの無料配布をしていた。","romaji":"Ekimae de tisshu no muryō haifu o shite ita.","german":"They were giving out free tissues in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|むりょうはいふ"},{"japanese":"アンケート用紙の配布は受付で行います。","romaji":"Ankēto yōshi no haifu wa uketsuke de okonaimasu.","german":"Questionnaire forms are distributed at reception.","furigana":"ようし|はいふ|うけつけ|おこな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"After a place or time, 〜発 means \"departing, leaving from\": 8時発の電車, 博多発東京行き; the counterpart is 〜着. In news, ロンドン発 means \"reported from London\". As a counter it counts shots and blows, with sound changes: 一発 (いっぱつ), 二発 (にはつ), 三発 (さんぱつ). Do not confuse it with 初 (はつ, \"first-\") or 出発, which denotes departure as a process.","examples":[{"japanese":"八時発の特急に乗り遅れてしまった。","romaji":"Hachi-ji-hatsu no tokkyū ni noriokurete shimatta.","german":"I missed the express that departs at eight o'clock.","furigana":"はちじはつ|とっきゅう|の|おく"},{"japanese":"博多発東京行きの新幹線が遅れている。","romaji":"Hakata-hatsu Tōkyō-iki no shinkansen ga okurete iru.","german":"The Shinkansen from Hakata to Tokyo is delayed.","furigana":"|しんかんせん|おく"},{"japanese":"夜中に銃声が二発聞こえた。","romaji":"Yonaka ni jūsei ga ni-hatsu kikoeta.","german":"I heard two gunshots in the middle of the night.","furigana":"よなか|じゅうせい|にはつき"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"〜編 is attached to names and means \"edited by\" (山田編, 編集部編), or it denotes or counts parts and volumes of a work (前編・後編, 第一編, 全五編). The related verb is 編集する \"to edit, compile\"; 長編 \"long work\" is a fixed compound. Do not confuse it with 辺/へん \"vicinity\" and 変/へん \"strange\" (separate cards).","examples":[{"japanese":"この辞書は山田太郎編で、1990年に出版された。","romaji":"Kono jisho wa Yamada Tarō-hen de, 1990-nen ni shuppan sareta.","german":"This dictionary was edited by Yamada Taro and published in 1990.","furigana":"じしょ|やまだたろうへん|ねん|しゅっぱん"},{"japanese":"映画の前編は面白かったが、後編は少し長かった。","romaji":"Eiga no zenpen wa omoshirokatta ga, kōhen wa sukoshi nagakatta.","german":"The first part of the movie was interesting, but the second part was a bit long.","furigana":"えいが|ぜんぺん|おもしろ|こうへん|すこ|なが"},{"japanese":"この報告書は第二編で調査結果をまとめている。","romaji":"Kono hōkokusho wa dai-ni-hen de chōsa kekka o matomete iru.","german":"This report summarizes the survey results in Part Two.","furigana":"ほうこくしょ|だいにへん|ちょうさけっか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"保管 is the noun \"storage, safekeeping\"; as a verb it becomes 保管する (separate card) with を: 書類を保管する. Typical combinations are 保管場所, 保管期間, 冷蔵保管 and 保管料. Unlike 保存 \"preservation, saving (data, food)\", 保管 emphasizes keeping important or others' belongings safely in one's custody.","examples":[{"japanese":"貴重品の保管には十分ご注意ください。","romaji":"Kichōhin no hokan ni wa jūbun go-chūi kudasai.","german":"Please take great care in safeguarding your valuables.","furigana":"きちょうひん|ほかん|じゅうぶん|ちゅうい"},{"japanese":"この薬は冷暗所での保管が必要です。","romaji":"Kono kusuri wa reianjo de no hokan ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"This medicine must be stored in a cool, dark place.","furigana":"くすり|れいあんじょ|ほかん|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"書類の保管期間は五年と決められている。","romaji":"Shorui no hokan kikan wa go-nen to kimerarete iru.","german":"The retention period for documents is set at five years.","furigana":"しょるい|ほかんきかん|ごねん|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"民主 rarely stands alone; mostly it forms compounds such as 民主的 \"democratic\", 民主化 \"democratization\", 民主国家 \"democratic state\" and 民主主義 \"democracy\" (separate card). For the form of government itself, ordinary text uses 民主主義, not just 民主. It is also common in party names such as 自由民主党 or 立憲民主党.","examples":[{"japanese":"代表は民主的な方法で選ぶべきだ。","romaji":"Daihyō wa minshuteki na hōhō de erabu beki da.","german":"Representatives should be chosen by democratic means.","furigana":"だいひょう|みんしゅてき|ほうほう|えら"},{"japanese":"その国では軍事政権が倒れ、民主化が進んだ。","romaji":"Sono kuni de wa gunji seiken ga taore, minshuka ga susunda.","german":"In that country the military regime fell and democratization advanced.","furigana":"くに|ぐんじせいけん|たお|みんしゅか|すす"},{"japanese":"民主国家では、国民が選挙で政府を選ぶ。","romaji":"Minshu kokka de wa, kokumin ga senkyo de seifu o erabu.","german":"In a democratic state, the people choose the government through elections.","furigana":"みんしゅこっか|こくみん|せんきょ|せいふ|えら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"〜問 counts questions and exam problems: 一問, 二問, 三問, 十問 etc.; also 第1問 \"question 1\", 全20問 \"20 questions in total\" and the phrase 一問一答 \"question and answer\". 問 hardly stands alone; the noun for question is 問い (separate card) or 問題. Do not confuse it with 門/もん \"gate\" (separate card).","examples":[{"japanese":"第1問の答えを黒板に書いてください。","romaji":"Dai-ichi-mon no kotae o kokuban ni kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write the answer to question 1 on the blackboard.","furigana":"だい|もん|こた|こくばん|か"},{"japanese":"テストは全50問で、制限時間は60分だ。","romaji":"Tesuto wa zen gojū-mon de, seigen jikan wa rokujuppun da.","german":"The test has 50 questions in total, with a time limit of 60 minutes.","furigana":"ぜん|もん|せいげんじかん|ぷん"},{"japanese":"難しくて、十問中三問しか解けなかった。","romaji":"Muzukashikute, jūmon-chū sanmon shika tokenakatta.","german":"It was difficult, and I could only solve three out of ten questions.","furigana":"むずか|じゅうもんちゅうさんもん|と"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"〜流 is attached to countries, persons or names and means \"-style, in the manner of\" (日本流, アメリカ流, 自己流, 我流), or it denotes a school in traditional arts such as ikebana, tea ceremony or martial arts (小原流). As an independent noun, 流 hardly occurs; in compounds such as 一流 \"first-class\", 交流 \"exchange\" or 電流 \"electric current\" it has other meanings. A similar, somewhat softer suffix for \"style, manner\" is 〜風.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は料理を習ったことがなく、すべて自己流だ。","romaji":"Kare wa ryōri o naratta koto ga naku, subete jikoryū da.","german":"He has never learned to cook; it's all self-taught.","furigana":"かれ|りょうり|なら|じこりゅう"},{"japanese":"日本流のおもてなしを取り入れる海外のホテルが増えている。","romaji":"Nihonryū no omotenashi o toriireru kaigai no hoteru ga fuete iru.","german":"More and more overseas hotels are adopting Japanese-style hospitality.","furigana":"にほんりゅう|と|い|かいがい|ふ"},{"japanese":"祖母は小原流の生け花を四十年続けている。","romaji":"Sobo wa Ohara-ryū no ikebana o yonjū-nen tsuzukete iru.","german":"My grandmother has been practicing Ohara-school flower arranging for forty years.","furigana":"そぼ|おはらりゅう|い|ばな|よんじゅうねんつづ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"大〜 with the Japanese reading おお means \"big, strong, main\" and stands mainly before native Japanese words (大雨, 大雪, 大通り, 大喜び), but also in fixed words such as 大掃除 and 大勢. Before Sino-Japanese words 大/だい (separate card) is usual, e.g. 大問題, 大成功; some words have たい (大会, 大切). There is no firm rule, so it is best to learn the reading with each word.","examples":[{"japanese":"昨日の大雨で電車が止まった。","romaji":"Kinō no ōame de densha ga tomatta.","german":"Trains stopped because of yesterday's heavy rain.","furigana":"きのう|おおあめ|でんしゃ|と"},{"japanese":"年末に家族みんなで大掃除をした。","romaji":"Nenmatsu ni kazoku minna de ōsōji o shita.","german":"At the end of the year, the whole family did a big cleaning.","furigana":"ねんまつ|かぞく|おおそうじ"},{"japanese":"駅前の大通りは買い物客であふれていた。","romaji":"Ekimae no ōdōri wa kaimonokyaku de afurete ita.","german":"The main street in front of the station was overflowing with shoppers.","furigana":"えきまえ|おおどお|か|ものきゃく"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"〜化 macht aus einem Nomen einen Vorgang der Umwandlung, ähnlich wie deutsch „-isierung“ oder „-ifizierung“: 近代化, 自動化, 国際化, 高齢化, 映画化. Mit する werden daraus Verben: 自動化する „automatisieren“, passiv 映画化される „verfilmt werden“. Als eigenständiges Wort kommt 化 nicht vor; nicht verwechseln mit anderen か-Karten wie 家 (Person mit Beruf) oder 下 (unter … Bedingungen).","examples":[{"japanese":"日本では少子高齢化が深刻な問題になっている。","romaji":"Nihon de wa shōshi kōreika ga shinkoku na mondai ni natte iru.","german":"In Japan sind sinkende Geburtenzahlen und Überalterung zu einem ernsten Problem geworden.","furigana":"にほん|しょうしこうれいか|しんこく|もんだい"},{"japanese":"工場の作業の多くが自動化された。","romaji":"Kōjō no sagyō no ōku ga jidōka sareta.","german":"Viele Arbeitsabläufe in der Fabrik wurden automatisiert.","furigana":"こうじょう|さぎょう|おお|じどうか"},{"japanese":"その人気漫画が来年映画化されるそうだ。","romaji":"Sono ninki manga ga rainen eigaka sareru sō da.","german":"Dieser beliebte Manga soll nächstes Jahr verfilmt werden.","furigana":"にんきまんが|らいねんえいがか"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"割 denotes a ratio and above all tenths: 1割 = 10%, 3割 = 30%; this is how proportions (8割の人) and discounts (2割引き) are often expressed in Japan. The same word is in phrases like 割に合わない \"not worth it\" and 〜の割に \"considering that ...\". As a suffix on drinks, 〜割り means \"diluted with\" (水割り, お湯割り). The general word for \"proportion, ratio\" is 割合 (separate card).","examples":[{"japanese":"今日はすべての商品が二割引きです。","romaji":"Kyō wa subete no shōhin ga niwaribiki desu.","german":"Everything is 20 percent off today.","furigana":"きょう|しょうひん|にわりび"},{"japanese":"社員の約八割が在宅勤務を希望している。","romaji":"Shain no yaku hachiwari ga zaitaku kinmu o kibō shite iru.","german":"About 80 percent of employees want to work from home.","furigana":"しゃいん|やくはちわり|ざいたくきんむ|きぼう"},{"japanese":"こんなに働いてこの給料では割に合わない。","romaji":"Konna ni hataraite kono kyūryō de wa wari ni awanai.","german":"Working this much for this salary isn't worth it.","furigana":"はたら|きゅうりょう|わり|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"As a noun, 論 means \"argument, view, thesis\" (様々な論がある, 論を立てる) and belongs more to written and technical language; well known is the proverb 論より証拠 \"proof counts more than words\". More often it is a suffix for theories and treatises: 進化論, 文化論, 日本人論. For debate or dispute use 議論 or 論争, for the conclusion 結論, and for theory in general 理論.","examples":[{"japanese":"論より証拠だ。実際にやってみせよう。","romaji":"Ron yori shōko da. Jissai ni yatte miseyō.","german":"Proof is better than argument. Let me show you by actually doing it.","furigana":"ろん|しょうこ|じっさい"},{"japanese":"ダーウィンの進化論は生物学に大きな影響を与えた。","romaji":"Dāwin no shinkaron wa seibutsugaku ni ōki na eikyō o ataeta.","german":"Darwin's theory of evolution had a great influence on biology.","furigana":"しんかろん|せいぶつがく|おお|えいきょう|あた"},{"japanese":"その問題については様々な論がある。","romaji":"Sono mondai ni tsuite wa samazama na ron ga aru.","german":"There are various views on that problem.","furigana":"もんだい|さまざま|ろん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"限り is the noun of 限る (vocab-n3:2501) and means \"limit\"; but it mostly follows verbs or nouns: できる限り \"as much as possible\", 私の知る限り \"as far as I know\", 〜ない限り \"unless\", 今日限り \"today only\". Fixed phrases are 限りない \"boundless\" and 限りある資源 \"limited resources\". As a grammar pattern it is often also written in kana (かぎり).","examples":[{"japanese":"できる限り早くご連絡いたします。","romaji":"Dekiru kagiri hayaku go-renraku itashimasu.","german":"We will contact you as soon as possible.","furigana":"かぎ|はや|れんらく"},{"japanese":"私の知る限り、彼はまだ独身です。","romaji":"Watashi no shiru kagiri, kare wa mada dokushin desu.","german":"As far as I know, he is still single.","furigana":"わたし|し|かぎ|かれ|どくしん"},{"japanese":"このセール価格は今日限りです。","romaji":"Kono sēru kakaku wa kyō kagiri desu.","german":"This sale price is valid today only.","furigana":"かかく|きょうかぎ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"〜付け (づけ) follows a date and means \"dated\" or \"effective from\": 4月1日付けで異動, 10日付けの手紙, 本日付けで退職. Typical of official, business and newspaper language; often written only as 付 without okurigana. Not to be confused with 日付 (ひづけ, vocab-n3:3291) \"date\" and 〜漬け (づけ, vocab-n2:1532) \"pickled in\".","examples":[{"japanese":"四月一日付けで大阪支社に異動することになった。","romaji":"Shigatsu tsuitachi-zuke de Ōsaka shisha ni idō suru koto ni natta.","german":"I will be transferred to the Osaka branch effective April 1.","furigana":"しがつついたちづ|おおさかししゃ|いどう"},{"japanese":"十月五日付けのお手紙、確かに拝受いたしました。","romaji":"Jūgatsu itsuka-zuke no o-tegami, tashika ni haiju itashimashita.","german":"I have duly received your letter dated October 5.","furigana":"じゅうがついつかづ|てがみ|たし|はいじゅ"},{"japanese":"田中部長は本日付で退職されました。","romaji":"Tanaka buchō wa honjitsu-zuke de taishoku saremashita.","german":"Department head Tanaka retired as of today.","furigana":"たなかぶちょう|ほんじつづけ|たいしょく"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"せっせと describes, onomatopoeically, that someone works tirelessly and busily at something, often at small, repeated tasks: せっせと働く, せっせと貯金する, せっせと運ぶ. It sounds appreciative or affectionate and is also readily used for animals such as アリ. It stands directly before the verb without a particle; synonyms are 一生懸命 and こつこつ \"steadily, step by step\".","examples":[{"japanese":"母は朝からせっせと庭の手入れをしている。","romaji":"Haha wa asa kara sesse to niwa no teire o shite iru.","german":"My mother has been busily tending the garden since morning.","furigana":"はは|あさ|にわ|てい"},{"japanese":"彼は留学のためにせっせとお金を貯めている。","romaji":"Kare wa ryūgaku no tame ni sesse to okane o tamete iru.","german":"He is diligently saving money for studying abroad.","furigana":"かれ|りゅうがく|かね|た"},{"japanese":"アリがせっせと餌を巣に運んでいる。","romaji":"Ari ga sesse to esa o su ni hakonde iru.","german":"The ants are industriously carrying food to their nest.","furigana":"えさ|す|はこ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"〜気味 (ぎみ) is attached to nouns or the verb stem and means \"slightly, somewhat, with a tendency to\": 風邪気味, 疲れ気味, 太り気味, 遅れ気味. Mostly for undesirable states; it combines like a na-adjective (気味だ, 気味の, 気味で). As an independent word, 気味 is read きみ (vocab-n3:3303), for example in 気味が悪い \"creepy\"; ぎみ is the voiced form in the suffix.","examples":[{"japanese":"風邪気味なので、今日は早めに寝ます。","romaji":"Kaze-gimi na node, kyō wa hayame ni nemasu.","german":"I have a bit of a cold, so I'll go to bed early today.","furigana":"かぜぎみ|きょう|はや|ね"},{"japanese":"最近仕事が忙しくて、少し疲れ気味だ。","romaji":"Saikin shigoto ga isogashikute, sukoshi tsukare-gimi da.","german":"Work has been busy lately, so I'm a little tired.","furigana":"さいきんしごと|いそが|すこ|つか|ぎみ"},{"japanese":"電車が遅れ気味で、会議に間に合うか心配だ。","romaji":"Densha ga okure-gimi de, kaigi ni ma ni au ka shinpai da.","german":"The train is running a bit late, and I'm worried whether I'll make it to the meeting.","furigana":"でんしゃ|おく|ぎみ|かいぎ|ま|あ|しんぱい"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"洗面 is the Sino-Japanese word for \"washing one's face\" and appears mainly in compounds: 洗面所 \"washroom\", 洗面台 (vocab-n5:790) \"washbasin\", 洗面器 \"washbowl\", 洗面用具 \"toiletries\". On its own it sounds written (洗面を済ませる); in everyday speech one says 顔を洗う. 洗面する exists but is rare.","examples":[{"japanese":"洗面所はあちらの廊下の突き当たりです。","romaji":"Senmenjo wa achira no rōka no tsukiatari desu.","german":"The washroom is at the end of the corridor over there.","furigana":"せんめんじょ|ろうか|つ|あ"},{"japanese":"朝起きて洗面を済ませてから、朝食をとった。","romaji":"Asa okite senmen o sumasete kara, chōshoku o totta.","german":"After getting up and washing my face, I had breakfast.","furigana":"あさお|せんめん|す|ちょうしょく"},{"japanese":"旅行用の洗面用具をかばんに詰めた。","romaji":"Ryokō-yō no senmen yōgu o kaban ni tsumeta.","german":"I packed the toiletries for the trip into my bag.","furigana":"りょこうよう|せんめんようぐ|つ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"暖か (あたたか) is the na-adjective form alongside the i-adjectives 暖かい (vocab-n5:75) and 温かい (vocab-n2:719); it sounds somewhat more elevated and often stands attributively as 暖かな: 暖かな日差し, 温かな心. The spelling 暖か is used for weather and room temperature, 温か for food, objects and warmth of heart. Colloquially also あったか.","examples":[{"japanese":"窓から暖かな春の日差しが差し込んでいる。","romaji":"Mado kara atataka na haru no hizashi ga sashikonde iru.","german":"Warm spring sunlight is streaming in through the window.","furigana":"まど|あたた|はる|ひざ|さ|こ"},{"japanese":"皆様の温かなご支援に心より感謝申し上げます。","romaji":"Minasama no atataka na go-shien ni kokoro yori kansha mōshiagemasu.","german":"I sincerely thank you all for your warm support.","furigana":"みなさま|あたた|しえん|こころ|かんしゃもう|あ"},{"japanese":"今年の冬は例年より暖かだった。","romaji":"Kotoshi no fuyu wa reinen yori atataka datta.","german":"This winter was warmer than usual.","furigana":"ことし|ふゆ|れいねん|あたた"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"オーバーコート is a loanword from English \"overcoat\" and denotes a long, warm winter coat worn over a suit or other clothing. Today one usually simply says コート. The short form オーバー (vocab-n3:correction:oobaa) is a separate card and mostly means \"excessive\"; the spelling オーバコート is rare.","examples":[{"japanese":"今朝は冷え込んだので、厚いオーバーコートを着て出かけた。","romaji":"Kesa wa hiekonda node, atsui ōbākōto o kite dekaketa.","german":"It was bitterly cold this morning, so I went out wearing a thick overcoat.","furigana":"けさ|ひ|こ|あつ|き|で"},{"japanese":"このオーバーコートは祖父から譲り受けたものだ。","romaji":"Kono ōbākōto wa sofu kara yuzuriuketa mono da.","german":"I inherited this overcoat from my grandfather.","furigana":"そふ|ゆず|う"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"〜遍 (へん) counts how often something happens and corresponds to 〜回 or 〜度; it sounds somewhat more colloquial and is especially common in the Kansai area. Note the sound changes: 一遍 (いっぺん), 三遍 (さんべん), 何遍 (なんべん). Typical are 何遍も \"over and over\" and 一遍に \"all at once\". Not to be confused with 辺 (へん) \"area\" and 変 (へん) \"strange\".","examples":[{"japanese":"何遍言ったら分かるの？","romaji":"Nanben ittara wakaru no?","german":"How many times do I have to tell you before you understand?","furigana":"なんべんい|わ"},{"japanese":"この小説はもう三遍も読み返した。","romaji":"Kono shōsetsu wa mō sanben mo yomikaeshita.","german":"I have already reread this novel three times.","furigana":"しょうせつ|さんべん|よ|かえ"},{"japanese":"一遍でいいから、富士山に登ってみたい。","romaji":"Ippen de ii kara, Fujisan ni nobotte mitai.","german":"Even if it's just once, I'd like to climb Mt. Fuji.","furigana":"いっぺん|ふじさん|のぼ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"御 with the reading おん is a particularly formal honorific prefix that today occurs almost only in fixed words of business and letter language: 御社 \"your company\" (spoken), 御中 as an address suffix for companies or departments, 御礼 (おんれい) in 御礼申し上げます. Otherwise 御 is read お (vocab-n5:correction:o-honorific-prefix, before native Japanese words) or ご (vocab-n5:correction:go-honorific-prefix, before Sino-Japanese words); おん is not productive. In writing, 貴社 is usually used for \"your company\".","examples":[{"japanese":"御社の新しいサービスについて詳しく伺いたいと思います。","romaji":"Onsha no atarashii sābisu ni tsuite kuwashiku ukagaitai to omoimasu.","german":"I would like to hear more details about your company's new service.","furigana":"おんしゃ|あたら|くわ|うかが|おも"},{"japanese":"会社宛ての封筒には「人事部御中」と書きます。","romaji":"Kaisha-ate no fūtō ni wa 'Jinjibu onchū' to kakimasu.","german":"On an envelope addressed to a company, one writes \"Attn: Personnel Department\".","furigana":"かいしゃあ|ふうとう|じんじぶおんちゅう|か"},{"japanese":"日頃のご愛顧に厚く御礼申し上げます。","romaji":"Higoro no go-aiko ni atsuku onrei mōshiagemasu.","german":"We sincerely thank you for your continued patronage.","furigana":"ひごろ|あいこ|あつ|おんれいもう|あ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"歩 (ほ) means \"step\" and serves mainly as a counter for steps, with sound changes: 一歩 (いっぽ, vocab-n5:1032), 二歩 (にほ), 三歩 (さんぽ), 六歩 (ろっぽ), 何歩 (なんぽ). As a standalone noun it is elevated, as in 歩を進める \"to advance\" or 歩を緩める \"to slow one's pace\". Not to be confused with 穂 (ほ) \"ear of grain\" and the 将棋 piece 歩 (ふ).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は二歩下がって、深く頭を下げた。","romaji":"Kare wa niho sagatte, fukaku atama o sageta.","german":"He stepped back two paces and bowed deeply.","furigana":"かれ|にほさ|ふか|あたま|さ"},{"japanese":"ホテルは駅から数歩のところにある。","romaji":"Hoteru wa eki kara sūho no tokoro ni aru.","german":"The hotel is only a few steps from the station.","furigana":"えき|すうほ"},{"japanese":"両国は和平に向けて歩を進めた。","romaji":"Ryōkoku wa wahei ni mukete ho o susumeta.","german":"The two countries took steps toward peace.","furigana":"りょうこく|わへい|む|ほ|すす"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"毎〜 (まい) is a productive prefix \"every, each\" for regularly recurring times and events: 毎週末, 毎日曜, 毎試合, 毎食後. Frequent compounds such as 毎日 (vocab-n5:199), 毎朝 and 毎週 are learned as separate words. Not to be confused with the counter 枚 (まい) for flat objects.","examples":[{"japanese":"毎週末、家族で近くの公園に出かけます。","romaji":"Maishūmatsu, kazoku de chikaku no kōen ni dekakemasu.","german":"Every weekend, our family goes to the nearby park.","furigana":"まいしゅうまつ|かぞく|ちか|こうえん|で"},{"japanese":"この薬は毎食後に一錠飲んでください。","romaji":"Kono kusuri wa maishokugo ni ichijō nonde kudasai.","german":"Take one tablet of this medicine after every meal.","furigana":"くすり|まいしょくご|いちじょうの"},{"japanese":"彼は毎試合のようにゴールを決めている。","romaji":"Kare wa maishiai no yō ni gōru o kimete iru.","german":"He scores a goal in practically every match.","furigana":"かれ|まいしあい|き"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"〜力 (りょく) is the Sino-Japanese suffix reading of 力 and forms words for abilities and strength: 経済力, 想像力, 集中力, 説得力, 影響力. As an independent word, 力 is read ちから (vocab-n4:1398). In some words the suffix is りき, e.g. 馬力, 自力, 他力.","examples":[{"japanese":"この国は経済力では世界有数だ。","romaji":"Kono kuni wa keizairyoku de wa sekai yūsū da.","german":"In economic power, this country is among the world's leading.","furigana":"くに|けいざいりょく|せかいゆうすう"},{"japanese":"子供の想像力には本当に驚かされる。","romaji":"Kodomo no sōzōryoku ni wa hontō ni odorokasareru.","german":"Children's imagination truly amazes me.","furigana":"こども|そうぞうりょく|ほんとう|おどろ"},{"japanese":"疲れていて、集中力が続かない。","romaji":"Tsukarete ite, shūchūryoku ga tsuzukanai.","german":"I'm tired and can't keep my concentration up.","furigana":"つか|しゅうちゅうりょく|つづ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"The Sino-Japanese reading of 画 for pictures and paintings, almost always as a compound element: 日本画, 風景画, 漫画, 画家. On its own, 画 appears mainly in book and manga credits for the illustrator (作・…　画・…). The everyday word for \"picture\" is 絵 (え); the reading かく (separate card), on the other hand, means \"stroke\" as in 画数 or 計画.","examples":[{"japanese":"祖父は若い頃から日本画を描き続けている。","romaji":"Sofu wa wakai koro kara nihonga o egaki tsuzukete iru.","german":"My grandfather has kept painting in the Japanese style since his youth.","furigana":"そふ|わか|ころ|にほんが|えが|つづ"},{"japanese":"美術館で印象派の風景画をじっくり鑑賞した。","romaji":"Bijutsukan de inshōha no fūkeiga o jikkuri kanshō shita.","german":"At the museum I took my time appreciating Impressionist landscape paintings.","furigana":"びじゅつかん|いんしょうは|ふうけいが|かんしょう"},{"japanese":"絵本の表紙には「作・山田花子　画・佐藤一郎」と書いてある。","romaji":"Ehon no hyōshi ni wa \"Saku Yamada Hanako, ga Satō Ichirō\" to kaite aru.","german":"The cover of the picture book says \"Story: Hanako Yamada, Illustrations: Ichiro Sato\".","furigana":"えほん|ひょうし|さく|やまだはなこ|が|さとういちろう|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A frequent prefix in news, business texts and forms that denotes the present office or current state: 現首相, 現政権, 現住所. Counterparts are 元〜 (もと, \"former\": 元会長) and 前〜 (ぜん, \"previous, immediately before\": 前会長). As a separate word for \"now\", 現在 (separate card) is used instead.","examples":[{"japanese":"現政権は来年度の予算案を国会に提出した。","romaji":"Gen-seiken wa rainendo no yosan'an o kokkai ni teishutsu shita.","german":"The current administration has submitted its budget proposal for the next fiscal year to the Diet.","furigana":"げんせいけん|らいねんど|よさんあん|こっかい|ていしゅつ"},{"japanese":"申込書には現住所と電話番号を記入してください。","romaji":"Mōshikomisho ni wa genjūsho to denwa bangō o kinyū shite kudasai.","german":"Please enter your current address and phone number on the application form.","furigana":"もうしこみしょ|げんじゅうしょ|でんわばんごう|きにゅう"},{"japanese":"現会長の任期は来年三月までとなっている。","romaji":"Gen-kaichō no ninki wa rainen sangatsu made to natte iru.","german":"The term of the current chairman runs until March next year.","furigana":"げんかいちょう|にんき|らいねんさんがつ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"A productive suffix for wars, battles and contests: 決勝戦 \"final\", 延長戦 \"extra time\", 選挙戦 \"election campaign\", 情報戦 \"information war\". The standalone word for \"war\" is 戦争 (separate card), for \"battle\" 戦い (たたかい). Not to be confused with the identically sounding cards 千, 線 and 栓.","examples":[{"japanese":"今度の日曜日にサッカーの決勝戦が行われる。","romaji":"Kondo no nichiyōbi ni sakkā no kesshōsen ga okonawareru.","german":"The soccer final will be held this coming Sunday.","furigana":"こんど|にちようび|けっしょうせん|おこな"},{"japanese":"告示を受けて、選挙戦が本格的に始まった。","romaji":"Kokuji o ukete, senkyosen ga honkakuteki ni hajimatta.","german":"With the official announcement, the election campaign has begun in earnest.","furigana":"こくじ|う|せんきょせん|ほんかくてき|はじ"},{"japanese":"延長戦の末、地元チームが劇的な勝利を収めた。","romaji":"Enchōsen no sue, jimoto chīmu ga gekiteki na shōri o osameta.","german":"After extra time, the home team scored a dramatic victory.","furigana":"えんちょうせん|すえ|じもと|げきてき|しょうり|おさ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Most often as an origin suffix on labels: 北海道産, フランス産, 外国産; 国産 is a separate word (separate card). 〜産 applies mainly to agricultural and natural products, 〜製 to manufactured goods (日本製の時計). On its own, 産 means \"fortune\" in written language (産を成す) or \"birth\"; in everyday speech the polite お産 (separate card) is used for the latter.","examples":[{"japanese":"このワインはフランス産で、少し値段が高い。","romaji":"Kono wain wa Furansu-san de, sukoshi nedan ga takai.","german":"This wine is from France and is a little expensive.","furigana":"さん|すこ|ねだん|たか"},{"japanese":"スーパーでは国産と外国産の牛肉が並んで売られている。","romaji":"Sūpā de wa kokusan to gaikokusan no gyūniku ga narande urarete iru.","german":"At the supermarket, domestic and foreign beef are sold side by side.","furigana":"こくさん|がいこくさん|ぎゅうにく|なら|う"},{"japanese":"祖父は一代で巨万の産を成した実業家だった。","romaji":"Sofu wa ichidai de kyoman no san o nashita jitsugyōka datta.","german":"My grandfather was a businessman who amassed a huge fortune in a single generation.","furigana":"そふ|いちだい|きょまん|さん|な|じつぎょうか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"As a prefix, 重 stands for something heavy or serious: 重工業 \"heavy industry\", 重傷 \"serious injury\", 重症 \"serious illness\". As a suffix after Sino-Japanese numbers it means \"-fold\": 二重 \"double\" (separate card), 三重 \"triple\". Not to be confused with 重/え (separate card), which counts layers with native Japanese number forms (一重, 八重); the adjective \"heavy\" is 重い.","examples":[{"japanese":"父は重工業の会社で働いています。","romaji":"Chichi wa jūkōgyō no kaisha de hataraite imasu.","german":"My father works for a heavy industry company.","furigana":"ちち|じゅうこうぎょう|かいしゃ|はたら"},{"japanese":"事故で運転手が重傷を負った。","romaji":"Jiko de untenshu ga jūshō o otta.","german":"The driver was seriously injured in the accident.","furigana":"じこ|うんてんしゅ|じゅうしょう|お"},{"japanese":"この窓は三重ガラスなので、外の音がほとんど聞こえない。","romaji":"Kono mado wa sanjū garasu na node, soto no oto ga hotondo kikoenai.","german":"This window is triple-glazed, so you can hardly hear any noise from outside.","furigana":"まど|さんじゅう|そと|おと|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Rare on its own, mostly as an industry suffix: 製造業 \"manufacturing\", 観光業 \"tourism industry\", 不動産業, サービス業. In the sense of \"studies, coursework\" it is found in 授業 and 卒業. Written the same but a different word is 業/ごう \"karma\" (separate card, Buddhist); for concrete work one says 仕事.","examples":[{"japanese":"兄は製造業の会社に勤めている。","romaji":"Ani wa seizōgyō no kaisha ni tsutomete iru.","german":"My older brother works for a manufacturing company.","furigana":"あに|せいぞうぎょう|かいしゃ|つと"},{"japanese":"この町では観光業が一番大切な産業です。","romaji":"Kono machi de wa kankōgyō ga ichiban taisetsu na sangyō desu.","german":"In this town, tourism is the most important industry.","furigana":"まち|かんこうぎょう|いちばんたいせつ|さんぎょう"},{"japanese":"彼は大学を出たあと、父の家業を継いだ。","romaji":"Kare wa daigaku o deta ato, chichi no kagyō o tsuida.","german":"After graduating from university, he took over his father's family business.","furigana":"かれ|だいがく|で|ちち|かぎょう|つ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"As a prefix, 高 means \"high-\": 高血圧 \"high blood pressure\", 高収入 \"high income\", 高性能 \"high-performance\". After a school name it is the short form of 高校 (separate card): 北高 \"North High School\", 〇〇高の生徒. To be distinguished from 高/たか \"amount, sum\" (売上高, separate card) and from the adjective 高い.","examples":[{"japanese":"父は高血圧なので、塩分を控えている。","romaji":"Chichi wa kōketsuatsu na node, enbun o hikaete iru.","german":"My father has high blood pressure, so he cuts back on salt.","furigana":"ちち|こうけつあつ|えんぶん|ひか"},{"japanese":"高収入の仕事が必ずしも楽しいとは限らない。","romaji":"Kōshūnyū no shigoto ga kanarazushimo tanoshii to wa kagiranai.","german":"A high-paying job isn't necessarily fun.","furigana":"こうしゅうにゅう|しごと|かなら|たの|かぎ"},{"japanese":"甲子園で、地元の北高が決勝に進んだ。","romaji":"Kōshien de, jimoto no Kita-kō ga kesshō ni susunda.","german":"At Koshien, the local Kita High School advanced to the final.","furigana":"こうしえん|じもと|きたこう|けっしょう|すす"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A negating prefix before Sino-Japanese nouns for something that has not yet happened or been achieved: 未成年 \"minor (underage)\", 未完成 \"unfinished\", 未解決 \"unsolved\", 未定 \"not yet decided\". In contrast to 不〜 (不可能, impossible in principle) and 無〜 (無料, without something), 未〜 carries the nuance \"but perhaps later\". Typical of forms, signs and news.","examples":[{"japanese":"未成年者の飲酒は法律で禁止されています。","romaji":"Miseinensha no inshu wa hōritsu de kinshi sarete imasu.","german":"Drinking alcohol by minors is prohibited by law.","furigana":"みせいねんしゃ|いんしゅ|ほうりつ|きんし"},{"japanese":"その事件はまだ未解決のままだ。","romaji":"Sono jiken wa mada mikaiketsu no mama da.","german":"The case is still unsolved.","furigana":"じけん|みかいけつ"},{"japanese":"次回の会議の日程は未定です。","romaji":"Jikai no kaigi no nittei wa mitei desu.","german":"The date of the next meeting has not yet been decided.","furigana":"じかい|かいぎ|にってい|みてい"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Attaches to the stem of i-adjectives and forms nouns for a perceptible, felt quality: 甘み \"sweetness\", 痛み \"pain\", 重み \"weight, significance\", 深み \"depth\". In contrast to 〜さ, which names a measurable degree (甘さ, 重さ), 〜み emphasizes the impression; not every adjective forms a 〜み form. Mostly in kana; the spelling 甘味 is not to be confused with 味/み \"taste\" (separate card).","examples":[{"japanese":"このトマトは甘みが強くておいしい。","romaji":"Kono tomato wa amami ga tsuyokute oishii.","german":"These tomatoes are sweet and delicious.","furigana":"あま|つよ"},{"japanese":"薬を飲んだら、頭の痛みが少し消えた。","romaji":"Kusuri o nondara, atama no itami ga sukoshi kieta.","german":"After I took the medicine, my headache went away a little.","furigana":"くすり|の|あたま|いた|すこ|き"},{"japanese":"社長の言葉には重みがある。","romaji":"Shachō no kotoba ni wa omomi ga aru.","german":"The president's words carry weight.","furigana":"しゃちょう|ことば|おも"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"A suffix attached to nouns, denoting streets or districts with a particular character: 商店街 \"shopping street\", 住宅街 \"residential area\", 中華街 \"Chinatown\", 繁華街 \"entertainment district\". Standing alone, 街 is read まち (card 町/まち, where it appears as a spelling variant); the reading がい occurs only in such compounds. Do not confuse it with 〜外/がい \"outside of\" and 害/がい \"harm\".","examples":[{"japanese":"駅前の商店街はいつも人でにぎわっている。","romaji":"Ekimae no shōtengai wa itsumo hito de nigiwatte iru.","german":"The shopping street in front of the station is always bustling with people.","furigana":"えきまえ|しょうてんがい|ひと"},{"japanese":"この辺りは静かな住宅街です。","romaji":"Kono atari wa shizuka na jūtakugai desu.","german":"This area is a quiet residential district.","furigana":"あた|しず|じゅうたくがい"},{"japanese":"横浜の中華街で肉まんを食べた。","romaji":"Yokohama no chūkagai de nikuman o tabeta.","german":"I ate a meat bun in Yokohama's Chinatown.","furigana":"よこはま|ちゅうかがい|にく|た"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A na-adjective and noun, the opposite of 異常 \"abnormal\": 正常に動く \"to function normally\", 正常な値 \"normal value\", 正常に戻る \"to return to normal\". Typical of technology, medicine and news; for people or things in everyday speech, one rather says 普通. 通常, by contrast, means \"usual, regular (in procedure)\", e.g. 通常営業 \"regular operation\".","examples":[{"japanese":"検査の結果、血圧は正常だった。","romaji":"Kensa no kekka, ketsuatsu wa seijō datta.","german":"The test results showed that my blood pressure was normal.","furigana":"けんさ|けっか|けつあつ|せいじょう"},{"japanese":"システムは現在正常に動いています。","romaji":"Shisutemu wa genzai seijō ni ugoite imasu.","german":"The system is currently operating normally.","furigana":"げんざいせいじょう|うご"},{"japanese":"両国の関係がようやく正常に戻った。","romaji":"Ryōkoku no kankei ga yōyaku seijō ni modotta.","german":"Relations between the two countries have finally returned to normal.","furigana":"りょうこく|かんけい|せいじょう|もど"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A na-adjective for something without variety: 単調な仕事 \"monotonous work\", 単調な生活, 単調なリズム; as a noun 単調さ. It describes the thing itself, whereas 退屈 (たいくつ) emphasizes the feeling of boredom. Do not confuse it with the homophone 短調 \"minor key\"; in mathematics, 単調増加 means \"monotonically increasing\".","examples":[{"japanese":"毎日同じ作業の繰り返しで、仕事が単調だ。","romaji":"Mainichi onaji sagyō no kurikaeshi de, shigoto ga tanchō da.","german":"Every day is the same task repeated, so the work is monotonous.","furigana":"まいにちおな|さぎょう|く|かえ|しごと|たんちょう"},{"japanese":"単調なリズムを聞いていると眠くなる。","romaji":"Tanchō na rizumu o kiite iru to nemuku naru.","german":"Listening to a monotonous rhythm makes me sleepy.","furigana":"たんちょう|き|ねむ"},{"japanese":"この小説は展開が単調で、途中で飽きてしまった。","romaji":"Kono shōsetsu wa tenkai ga tanchō de, tochū de akite shimatta.","german":"This novel's plot is monotonous, and I got bored partway through.","furigana":"しょうせつ|てんかい|たんちょう|とちゅう|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Denotes the habitual everyday as opposed to special occasions: 日頃から \"already in daily life, always\", 日頃の努力 \"daily effort\". A fixed formula in business letters: 日頃のご愛顧に感謝申し上げます. Somewhat more literary than 普段 (separate card), which is more usual in conversation; 日ごろ is a common mixed spelling.","examples":[{"japanese":"日頃から運動するように心がけている。","romaji":"Higoro kara undō suru yō ni kokorogakete iru.","german":"I make a point of exercising regularly.","furigana":"ひごろ|うんどう|こころ"},{"japanese":"合格できたのは日頃の努力のおかげだ。","romaji":"Gōkaku dekita no wa higoro no doryoku no okage da.","german":"I passed thanks to my daily effort.","furigana":"ごうかく|ひごろ|どりょく"},{"japanese":"日ごろお世話になっている方々にお礼の品を送った。","romaji":"Higoro osewa ni natte iru katagata ni orei no shina o okutta.","german":"I sent a thank-you gift to the people who have helped me.","furigana":"ひ|せわ|かたがた|れい|しな|おく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Placed after a noun or the plain form of a verb/adjective: AどころかB means either \"instead of A, even the opposite B\" (褒められるどころか叱られた) or \"not even B, let alone A\" (旅行どころか休みも取れない). Almost always written in kana. At the beginning of a sentence, the conjunction それどころか (\"on the contrary\") is used instead, which has its own card.","examples":[{"japanese":"忙しくて、旅行どころか週末も休めない。","romaji":"Isogashikute, ryokō dokoro ka shūmatsu mo yasumenai.","german":"I'm so busy that I can't take time off even on weekends, let alone travel.","furigana":"いそが|りょこう|しゅうまつ|やす"},{"japanese":"褒められるどころか、先生にひどく叱られた。","romaji":"Homerareru dokoro ka, sensei ni hidoku shikarareta.","german":"Far from being praised, I was scolded severely by the teacher.","furigana":"ほ|せんせい|しか"},{"japanese":"雨はやむどころか、ますます強くなってきた。","romaji":"Ame wa yamu dokoro ka, masumasu tsuyoku natte kita.","german":"Far from stopping, the rain has gotten even heavier.","furigana":"あめ|つよ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"An onomatopoeic adverb for something that happens or spreads suddenly: ぱっと立ち上がる \"to jump up\", ぱっと明るくなる \"to suddenly brighten\", ぱっと見 \"at first glance\". With する it means \"to be striking, splendid\", almost only negated as ぱっとしない \"unremarkable, lackluster, mediocre\". Often also written in katakana パッと. The rare ばっと feels more violent; さっと emphasizes the quick, smooth movement.","examples":[{"japanese":"名前を呼ばれて、彼はぱっと立ち上がった。","romaji":"Namae o yobarete, kare wa patto tachiagatta.","german":"When his name was called, he stood up at once.","furigana":"なまえ|よ|かれ|た|あ"},{"japanese":"電気をつけると、部屋がパッと明るくなった。","romaji":"Denki o tsukeru to, heya ga patto akaruku natta.","german":"When I turned on the light, the room suddenly brightened.","furigana":"でんき|へや|あか"},{"japanese":"最近、天気がぱっとしないね。","romaji":"Saikin, tenki ga patto shinai ne.","german":"The weather hasn't been great lately, has it?","furigana":"さいきん|てんき"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新大統領の施政方針が発表された。","romaji":"Shin daitōryō no shisei hōshin ga happyō sareta.","german":"The new president's policy platform was announced.","furigana":"しんだいとうりょう|しせいほうしん|はっぴょう"},{"japanese":"首相は国会で施政方針演説を行った。","romaji":"Shushō wa kokkai de shisei hōshin enzetsu o okonatta.","german":"The prime minister delivered a policy speech in the Diet.","furigana":"しゅしょう|こっかい|しせいほうしんえんぜつ|おこな"},{"japanese":"沖縄の施政権は１９７２年に日本に返還された。","romaji":"Okinawa no shiseiken wa senkyūhyakunanajūninen ni Nihon ni henkan sareta.","german":"Administrative rights over Okinawa were returned to Japan in 1972.","furigana":"おきなわ|しせいけん|ねん|にほん|へんかん"}],"notes":"施政 is the exercise of governmental power, i.e. the practical running of a state, and occurs almost only in fixed combinations in official and press language. Important are 施政方針 (\"government program\") and 施政方針演説, the prime minister's policy address at the start of the ordinary Diet session, as well as 施政権 (\"administrative authority\"), for example regarding the return of Okinawa. Do not confuse it with the identically read 市政 (\"city administration\") and 姿勢 (\"attitude, posture\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"植民地の統治は長年にわたって続いた。","romaji":"Shokuminchi no tōchi wa naganen ni watatte tsuzuita.","german":"Governance of the colony continued for many years.","furigana":"しょくみんち|とうち|ながねん|つづ"},{"japanese":"当時、この島はイギリスの統治下にあった。","romaji":"Tōji, kono shima wa Igirisu no tōchika ni atta.","german":"At that time, this island was under British rule.","furigana":"とうじ|しま|とうちか"},{"japanese":"不祥事が続き、企業統治の強化が求められている。","romaji":"Fushōji ga tsuzuki, kigyō tōchi no kyōka ga motomerarete iru.","german":"With scandals continuing, strengthening corporate governance is being called for.","furigana":"ふしょうじ|つづ|きぎょうとうち|きょうか|もと"}],"notes":"統治 is the governing and ruling of a country or territory by an authority; as a する-verb it is transitive: 国を統治する. Typical: 植民地統治, 〜の統治下にある (\"to be under the rule of ...\"), 統治者, 統治機構. In business, 企業統治 stands for \"corporate governance\". 支配 is broader and sounds more like oppression, whereas 統治 suggests the orderly exercise of rule.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大国間の覇権争いが激化している。","romaji":"Taikoku kan no haken arasoi ga gekika shite iru.","german":"The struggle for hegemony among the great powers is intensifying.","furigana":"たいこくかん|はけんあらそ|げきか"},{"japanese":"かつてはイギリスが海の覇権を握っていた。","romaji":"Katsute wa Igirisu ga umi no haken o nigitte ita.","german":"Britain once held supremacy over the seas.","furigana":"うみ|はけん|にぎ"},{"japanese":"スマートフォン市場の覇権をめぐって、各社がしのぎを削っている。","romaji":"Sumātofon shijō no haken o megutte, kakusha ga shinogi o kezutte iru.","german":"Companies are battling fiercely for supremacy in the smartphone market.","furigana":"しじょう|はけん|かくしゃ|けず"}],"notes":"覇権 is the supremacy of a state or actor over others. Typical combinations: 覇権を握る (\"to hold supremacy\"), 覇権を争う, 覇権争い, 覇権国家, 覇権主義 (\"hegemonism\"). Besides world politics, it is often used for markets and sports, e.g. 市場の覇権 or 覇権奪回 (\"winning back the title\"). The same kanji 覇 is in 制覇 (\"victory, conquest of a title\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"汚職事件で複数の議員が逮捕された。","romaji":"Oshoku jiken de fukusū no giin ga taiho sareta.","german":"Several lawmakers were arrested in a corruption case.","furigana":"おしょくじけん|ふくすう|ぎいん|たいほ"},{"japanese":"その市長は汚職に手を染めたとして辞職した。","romaji":"Sono shichō wa oshoku ni te o someta to shite jishoku shita.","german":"The mayor resigned after being accused of involvement in corruption.","furigana":"しちょう|おしょく|て|そ|じしょく"},{"japanese":"汚職を防ぐため、公務員の倫理規定が厳しくなった。","romaji":"Oshoku o fusegu tame, kōmuin no rinri kitei ga kibishiku natta.","german":"Ethics rules for public servants have been tightened to prevent corruption.","furigana":"おしょく|ふせ|こうむいん|りんりきてい|きび"}],"notes":"汚職 is corruption by public officials, i.e. bribery and abuse of office by politicians and civil servants. Typical: 汚職事件, 汚職に手を染める (\"to get involved in corruption\"), 汚職で逮捕される, 汚職を摘発する. The bribe itself is 賄賂, and legally one speaks of 贈収賄; 腐敗, by contrast, means the general moral decay of a system.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"政治の腐敗に国民は怒りを感じている。","romaji":"Seiji no fuhai ni kokumin wa ikari o kanjite iru.","german":"The public is angry about political corruption.","furigana":"せいじ|ふはい|こくみん|いか|かん"},{"japanese":"保存温度が高いと、食品の腐敗が進みやすい。","romaji":"Hozon ondo ga takai to, shokuhin no fuhai ga susumiyasui.","german":"When the storage temperature is high, food spoils more easily.","furigana":"ほぞんおんど|たか|しょくひん|ふはい|すす"},{"japanese":"絶対的な権力は必ず腐敗すると言われる。","romaji":"Zettaiteki na kenryoku wa kanarazu fuhai suru to iwareru.","german":"It is said that absolute power inevitably corrupts.","furigana":"ぜったいてき|けんりょく|かなら|ふはい|い"}],"notes":"腐敗 has two meanings: literally \"rot, spoilage\" of food or organic material, and figuratively \"corruption, moral decay\" of politics, organizations or society. As a する-verb it is intransitive: 食品が腐敗する, 権力は腐敗する. In everyday speech, for spoiled food one rather says 腐る; 汚職 denotes concrete cases of bribery, whereas 腐敗 is the state of a whole system (政治の腐敗).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"首相が災害対策の采配を振るった。","romaji":"Shushō ga saigai taisaku no saihai o furutta.","german":"The prime minister took command of the disaster response.","furigana":"しゅしょう|さいがいたいさく|さいはい|ふ"},{"japanese":"監督の采配が見事に当たり、チームは逆転勝ちした。","romaji":"Kantoku no saihai ga migoto ni atari, chīmu wa gyakuten-gachi shita.","german":"The manager's tactics paid off brilliantly, and the team won a comeback victory.","furigana":"かんとく|さいはい|みごと|あ|ぎゃくてんが"},{"japanese":"結婚式の準備は、すべて母が采配を振った。","romaji":"Kekkonshiki no junbi wa, subete haha ga saihai o futta.","german":"Mother ran all the wedding preparations.","furigana":"けっこんしき|じゅんび|はは|さいはい|ふ"}],"notes":"采配 was originally the tasseled baton with which a commander directed his troops; from this came the meaning \"direction, command\". It almost always appears in the phrase 采配を振る or 采配を振るう (\"to take command, pull the strings\"). It is very common in sports for a coach's decisions: 監督の采配, 采配ミス, 采配が当たる. Somewhat elevated, but also used jokingly in everyday life.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"予算案が全会一致で議決された。","romaji":"Yosan'an ga zenkai icchi de giketsu sareta.","german":"The budget proposal was passed unanimously.","furigana":"よさんあん|ぜんかいいっち|ぎけつ"},{"japanese":"株主は株主総会で議決権を行使できる。","romaji":"Kabunushi wa kabunushi sōkai de giketsuken o kōshi dekiru.","german":"Shareholders can exercise their voting rights at the general meeting of shareholders.","furigana":"かぶぬし|かぶぬしそうかい|ぎけつけん|こうし"},{"japanese":"議決には出席者の過半数の賛成が必要だ。","romaji":"Giketsu ni wa shussekisha no kahansū no sansei ga hitsuyō da.","german":"A resolution requires the approval of a majority of those present.","furigana":"ぎけつ|しゅっせきしゃ|かはんすう|さんせい|ひつよう"}],"notes":"議決 is the formal decision-making of a body such as a parliament, town council or general meeting after deliberation; as a する-verb it is transitive: 予算案を議決する. The result can be 可決 (\"approved\") or 否決 (\"rejected\"); 決議 means rather the resolution that has been adopted. Important compounds: 議決権 (\"voting right\", e.g. of shareholders) and 議決機関 (\"decision-making body\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"会期終了に伴い、法案は廃案となった。","romaji":"Kaiki shūryō ni tomonai, hōan wa haian to natta.","german":"With the end of the session, the bill was scrapped.","furigana":"かいきしゅうりょう|ともな|ほうあん|はいあん"},{"japanese":"野党は法案の廃案を求めて抗議した。","romaji":"Yatō wa hōan no haian o motomete kōgi shita.","german":"The opposition protested, demanding the bill's withdrawal.","furigana":"やとう|ほうあん|はいあん|もと|こうぎ"},{"japanese":"予算不足で、新しい公園の計画は廃案になった。","romaji":"Yosan fusoku de, atarashii kōen no keikaku wa haian ni natta.","german":"Due to a lack of budget, the plan for the new park was scrapped.","furigana":"よさんふそく|あたら|こうえん|けいかく|はいあん"}],"notes":"廃案 is a bill or plan that has been discarded or lapses without a decision. In the Japanese parliament, bills not passed by the end of the session automatically become 廃案, unless they are referred to 継続審議 (\"continued deliberation\"). Typical: 廃案になる/となる, 廃案に追い込む, 廃案を求める. Also outside politics for dropped projects: 企画が廃案になる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"立法府の役割は法律を制定することである。","romaji":"Rippōfu no yakuwari wa hōritsu o seitei suru koto de aru.","german":"The role of the legislature is to enact laws.","furigana":"りっぽうふ|やくわり|ほうりつ|せいてい"},{"japanese":"国会は国の唯一の立法機関である。","romaji":"Kokkai wa kuni no yuiitsu no rippō kikan de aru.","german":"The Diet is the sole legislative organ of the state.","furigana":"こっかい|くに|ゆいいつ|りっぽうきかん"},{"japanese":"個人情報を守るための立法が急がれている。","romaji":"Kojin jōhō o mamoru tame no rippō ga isogarete iru.","german":"Legislation to protect personal information is urgently needed.","furigana":"こじんじょうほう|まも|りっぽう|いそ"}],"notes":"立法 is lawmaking, one of the three powers of state alongside 行政 (\"executive\") and 司法 (\"judiciary\"). Typical compounds: 立法府 (\"legislature, parliament\"), 立法権, 立法機関, 議員立法 (\"legislation initiated by members of parliament\"). As a する-verb it is rare; one rather says 法律を制定する or 立法化する (\"to put into law\"). Literary, typical of politics and law.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"司法の独立は民主主義の根幹である。","romaji":"Shihō no dokuritsu wa minshushugi no konkan de aru.","german":"The independence of the judiciary is the foundation of democracy.","furigana":"しほう|どくりつ|みんしゅしゅぎ|こんかん"},{"japanese":"彼は三回目の挑戦で司法試験に合格した。","romaji":"Kare wa sankaime no chōsen de shihō shiken ni gōkaku shita.","german":"He passed the bar examination on his third attempt.","furigana":"かれ|さんかいめ|ちょうせん|しほうしけん|ごうかく"},{"japanese":"この問題は最終的に司法の判断に委ねられた。","romaji":"Kono mondai wa saishūteki ni shihō no handan ni yudanerareta.","german":"This matter was ultimately left to the judgment of the judiciary.","furigana":"もんだい|さいしゅうてき|しほう|はんだん|ゆだ"}],"notes":"司法 is the administration of justice, or the judiciary as a branch of state power, exercised by the courts. Frequent combinations: 司法の独立, 司法権, 司法制度, 司法の場で争う (\"to contest in court\"). Well-known compounds are 司法試験 (\"bar examination\") and 司法書士 (\"judicial scrivener\"). Counterparts in the system of separation of powers are 立法 and 行政.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"三権分立は近代国家の基本原則である。","romaji":"Sanken bunritsu wa kindai kokka no kihon gensoku de aru.","german":"The separation of powers is a basic principle of the modern state.","furigana":"さんけんぶんりつ|きんだいこっか|きほんげんそく"},{"japanese":"三権分立とは、立法・行政・司法の三つの権力を分けることだ。","romaji":"Sanken bunritsu to wa, rippō, gyōsei, shihō no mittsu no kenryoku o wakeru koto da.","german":"The separation of powers means dividing the three powers of legislature, executive and judiciary.","furigana":"さんけんぶんりつ|りっぽう|ぎょうせい|しほう|みっ|けんりょく|わ"},{"japanese":"政府による裁判所への圧力は、三権分立を揺るがすものだと批判された。","romaji":"Seifu ni yoru saibansho e no atsuryoku wa, sanken bunritsu o yurugasu mono da to hihan sareta.","german":"Government pressure on the courts was criticized as undermining the separation of powers.","furigana":"せいふ|さいばんしょ|あつりょく|さんけんぶんりつ|ゆ|ひはん"}],"notes":"三権分立 is composed of 三権 (\"the three powers\": 立法, 行政, 司法) and 分立 (\"to exist separately side by side\") and denotes the separation of powers as described by Montesquieu. In Japan the powers rest with the 国会, 内閣 and 裁判所. The word appears in textbooks, constitutional debates and commentaries, often in phrases like 三権分立を守る or 三権分立を揺るがす.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"首相の辞任により政局が混乱した。","romaji":"Shushō no jinin ni yori seikyoku ga konran shita.","german":"The prime minister's resignation threw the political situation into turmoil.","furigana":"しゅしょう|じにん|せいきょく|こんらん"},{"japanese":"国会は政策の議論より政局に明け暮れている。","romaji":"Kokkai wa seisaku no giron yori seikyoku ni akekurete iru.","german":"The Diet is preoccupied with political maneuvering rather than policy debate.","furigana":"こっかい|せいさく|ぎろん|せいきょく|あ|く"},{"japanese":"内閣不信任案の提出で、政局が一気に緊迫した。","romaji":"Naikaku fushinnin'an no teishutsu de, seikyoku ga ikki ni kinpaku shita.","german":"The submission of a no-confidence motion against the cabinet suddenly heightened political tension.","furigana":"ないかくふしんにんあん|ていしゅつ|せいきょく|いっき|きんぱく"}],"notes":"政局 is the political situation, especially with regard to power struggles, government stability and party tactics. In Japanese political language it is often contrasted with 政策 (\"substantive policy\"): 政局より政策を. Typical: 政局が混乱する, 政局が緊迫する, 政局が流動化する, 政局になる (\"to turn into a power struggle\"). For the general (also international) situation, one rather uses 情勢.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"国際情勢が急速に変化している。","romaji":"Kokusai jōsei ga kyūsoku ni henka shite iru.","german":"The international situation is changing rapidly.","furigana":"こくさいじょうせい|きゅうそく|へんか"},{"japanese":"中東情勢を見守りながら、旅行を延期するか決めるつもりだ。","romaji":"Chūtō jōsei o mimamori nagara, ryokō o enki suru ka kimeru tsumori da.","german":"I'll decide whether to postpone the trip while watching the situation in the Middle East.","furigana":"ちゅうとうじょうせい|みまも|りょこう|えんき|き"},{"japanese":"厳しい経済情勢の中、多くの企業が採用を減らした。","romaji":"Kibishii keizai jōsei no naka, ōku no kigyō ga saiyō o herashita.","german":"Amid the harsh economic conditions, many companies reduced hiring.","furigana":"きび|けいざいじょうせい|なか|おお|きぎょう|さいよう|へ"}],"notes":"情勢 is the overall situation as it develops from many factors, mostly in politics, economy or society: 国際情勢, 中東情勢, 経済情勢, 社会情勢, 選挙情勢. Typical: 情勢を見守る, 情勢が緊迫する, 情勢が悪化する. 状況 is more general and more concrete (\"the situation here and now\"), 事情 means \"circumstances, background\", 形勢 the balance of forces in a contest.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"閣議で新たな経済対策が決定された。","romaji":"Kakugi de arata na keizai taisaku ga kettei sareta.","german":"New economic measures were decided at the cabinet meeting.","furigana":"かくぎ|あら|けいざいたいさく|けってい"},{"japanese":"政府は新しい方針を閣議決定した。","romaji":"Seifu wa atarashii hōshin o kakugi kettei shita.","german":"The government approved a new policy by cabinet decision.","furigana":"せいふ|あたら|ほうしん|かくぎけってい"},{"japanese":"閣議の後、官房長官が記者会見を開いた。","romaji":"Kakugi no ato, kanbō chōkan ga kisha kaiken o hiraita.","german":"After the cabinet meeting, the Chief Cabinet Secretary held a press conference.","furigana":"かくぎ|あと|かんぼうちょうかん|きしゃかいけん|ひら"}],"notes":"閣議 is the meeting of the Japanese cabinet (内閣) chaired by the prime minister; regular meetings (定例閣議) take place on Tuesday and Friday mornings. Very common is 閣議決定 (\"cabinet decision\"), also as a する-verb: 〜を閣議決定する. The ministers are called 閣僚. Typical news language: 閣議で決定する, 閣議後の記者会見.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"委員会で法案に関する質疑が行われた。","romaji":"Iinkai de hōan ni kansuru shitsugi ga okonawareta.","german":"Questioning about the bill took place in committee.","furigana":"いいんかい|ほうあん|かん|しつぎ|おこな"},{"japanese":"発表の後に十分間の質疑応答の時間があります。","romaji":"Happyō no ato ni juppunkan no shitsugi ōtō no jikan ga arimasu.","german":"After the presentation, there will be a ten-minute question-and-answer session.","furigana":"はっぴょう|あと|じゅっぷんかん|しつぎおうとう|じかん"},{"japanese":"野党議員が首相に対して厳しい質疑を行った。","romaji":"Yatō giin ga shushō ni taishite kibishii shitsugi o okonatta.","german":"An opposition lawmaker questioned the prime minister sharply.","furigana":"やとうぎいん|しゅしょう|たい|きび|しつぎ|おこな"}],"notes":"質疑 is the formal asking of questions about a bill, report or lecture in a session; in parliament, e.g. 代表質疑 or 質疑を行う. The word is most often met in 質疑応答 (\"questions and answers\") after presentations, lectures and press conferences. 質問 is the general word for \"question\"; 質疑 emphasizes the official setting and is hardly used for single everyday questions.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は三期連続で代議士を務めている。","romaji":"Kare wa sanki renzoku de daigishi o tsutomete iru.","german":"He has served as a Diet member for three consecutive terms.","furigana":"かれ|さんきれんぞく|だいぎし|つと"},{"japanese":"祖父は地元選出の代議士だった。","romaji":"Sofu wa jimoto senshutsu no daigishi datta.","german":"My grandfather was a Diet member elected from the local district.","furigana":"そふ|じもとせんしゅつ|だいぎし"},{"japanese":"大学を出てから十年間、代議士の秘書として働いた。","romaji":"Daigaku o dete kara jūnenkan, daigishi no hisho to shite hataraita.","german":"For ten years after graduating from university, I worked as a Diet member's secretary.","furigana":"だいがく|で|じゅうねんかん|だいぎし|ひしょ|はたら"}],"notes":"代議士 denotes a member of parliament, in current usage usually a member of the lower house (衆議院議員). The word sounds somewhat old-fashioned or journalistic; neutrally one says 国会議員 or 議員. Typical: 代議士を務める, 地元の代議士, 代議士の秘書. Literally it is \"someone who deliberates on behalf of others\", i.e. a representative of the people.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"参議院選挙が来月実施される。","romaji":"Sangiin senkyo ga raigetsu jisshi sareru.","german":"The House of Councillors election will be held next month.","furigana":"さんぎいんせんきょ|らいげつじっし"},{"japanese":"参議院は解散がなく、三年ごとに半数が改選される。","romaji":"Sangiin wa kaisan ga naku, sannen goto ni hansū ga kaisen sareru.","german":"The House of Councillors cannot be dissolved, and half of its members are re-elected every three years.","furigana":"さんぎいん|かいさん|さんねん|はんすう|かいせん"},{"japanese":"法案は衆議院を通過し、参議院に送られた。","romaji":"Hōan wa Shūgiin o tsūka shi, Sangiin ni okurareta.","german":"The bill passed the House of Representatives and was sent to the House of Councillors.","furigana":"ほうあん|しゅうぎいん|つうか|さんぎいん|おく"}],"notes":"参議院 is the upper house of the Japanese parliament (国会), abbreviated 参院. Its members (参議院議員) serve six-year terms; every three years half of the seats are up for election, and unlike the 衆議院 it cannot be dissolved. Typical: 参議院選挙 (short 参院選), 参議院で可決する, 参議院に送られる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"衆議院が解散され、総選挙が行われる。","romaji":"Shūgiin ga kaisan sare, sōsenkyo ga okonawareru.","german":"The House of Representatives will be dissolved and a general election held.","furigana":"しゅうぎいん|かいさん|そうせんきょ|おこな"},{"japanese":"衆議院議員の任期は四年だ。","romaji":"Shūgiin giin no ninki wa yonen da.","german":"The term of a House of Representatives member is four years.","furigana":"しゅうぎいんぎいん|にんき|よねん"},{"japanese":"予算については衆議院の議決が優先される。","romaji":"Yosan ni tsuite wa Shūgiin no giketsu ga yūsen sareru.","german":"On the budget, the House of Representatives' resolution takes precedence.","furigana":"よさん|しゅうぎいん|ぎけつ|ゆうせん"}],"notes":"衆議院 is the lower house of the Japanese parliament, abbreviated 衆院. The term is four years, but the house can be dissolved early (解散), after which a general election (総選挙) follows. On the budget, treaties and the election of the prime minister, it takes precedence over the 参議院 (衆議院の優越). Typical: 衆議院議員, 衆議院を解散する, 衆議院を通過する.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"各国の首脳が国際会議に出席した。","romaji":"Kakkoku no shunō ga kokusai kaigi ni shusseki shita.","german":"Leaders of various countries attended the international conference.","furigana":"かっこく|しゅのう|こくさいかいぎ|しゅっせき"},{"japanese":"日米首脳会談が東京で開かれた。","romaji":"Nichibei shunō kaidan ga Tōkyō de hirakareta.","german":"A Japan-US summit meeting was held in Tokyo.","furigana":"にちべいしゅのうかいだん|とうきょう|ひら"},{"japanese":"会社の首脳陣が新しい経営方針を説明した。","romaji":"Kaisha no shunōjin ga atarashii keiei hōshin o setsumei shita.","german":"The company's top executives explained the new management policy.","furigana":"かいしゃ|しゅのうじん|あたら|けいえいほうしん|せつめい"}],"notes":"首脳 are the top representatives of a state or organization: in politics the heads of state and government, in companies the top management. Very common: 首脳会談 (\"summit meeting\"), 首脳会議, 日米首脳, 各国首脳, 首脳陣 (\"leadership team\"). Unlike 元首, which formally means the head of state, 首脳 also includes prime ministers and chancellors.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"元首が外国の大使を接見した。","romaji":"Genshu ga gaikoku no taishi o sekken shita.","german":"Das Staatsoberhaupt empfing den ausländischen Botschafter.","furigana":"げんしゅ|がいこく|たいし|せっけん"},{"japanese":"ドイツの元首は首相ではなく大統領だ。","romaji":"Doitsu no genshu wa shushō de wa naku daitōryō da.","german":"Deutschlands Staatsoberhaupt ist nicht der Kanzler, sondern der Bundespräsident.","furigana":"げんしゅ|しゅしょう|だいとうりょう"},{"japanese":"各国の元首が国王の即位式に参列した。","romaji":"Kakkoku no genshu ga kokuō no sokuishiki ni sanretsu shita.","german":"Staatsoberhäupter aus vielen Ländern nahmen an der Krönungsfeier des Königs teil.","furigana":"かっこく|げんしゅ|こくおう|そくいしき|さんれつ"}],"notes":"元首 ist das Staatsoberhaupt, das einen Staat nach außen vertritt, etwa ein König oder Präsident; oft als 国家元首. Es ist ein formaler, staatsrechtlicher Begriff: In Deutschland ist der Bundespräsident 元首, nicht der Kanzler. Ob der japanische Kaiser, laut Verfassung „Symbol“ (象徴) des Staates, als 元首 gilt, ist umstritten. Für Staats- und Regierungschefs bei Gipfeltreffen sagt man dagegen 首脳.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"幼い天皇に代わり摂政が政務を行った。","romaji":"Osanai tennō ni kawari sesshō ga seimu o okonatta.","german":"A regent carried out the affairs of state in place of the young emperor.","furigana":"おさな|てんのう|か|せっしょう|せいむ|おこな"},{"japanese":"聖徳太子は推古天皇の摂政として政治を行ったとされる。","romaji":"Shōtoku Taishi wa Suiko Tennō no sesshō to shite seiji o okonatta to sareru.","german":"Prince Shotoku is said to have governed as regent for Empress Suiko.","furigana":"しょうとくたいし|すいこてんのう|せっしょう|せいじ|おこな"},{"japanese":"平安時代には藤原氏が摂政や関白の地位を独占した。","romaji":"Heian jidai ni wa Fujiwara-shi ga sesshō ya kanpaku no chii o dokusen shita.","german":"In the Heian period, the Fujiwara clan monopolized the positions of regent and chief advisor.","furigana":"へいあんじだい||せっしょう|かんぱく|ちい|どくせん"}],"notes":"摂政 is a regent who rules for a minor or incapacitated ruler, and also the regency itself. In Japanese history, 摂政 was the office for a child on the throne, 関白 that for an adult emperor; the Fujiwara family thus dominated the Heian period (摂関政治). Even today the Imperial Household Law provides for a 摂政; most recently Crown Prince Hirohito was regent in 1921-1926. Do not confuse it with the identically read 殺生 (\"killing of living beings\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"明治時代には多くの勅令が発布された。","romaji":"Meiji jidai ni wa ōku no chokurei ga happu sareta.","german":"Many imperial edicts were promulgated in the Meiji period.","furigana":"めいじじだい|おお|ちょくれい|はっぷ"},{"japanese":"フランス王アンリ４世はナントの勅令でプロテスタントに信仰の自由を認めた。","romaji":"Furansu-ō Anri yonsei wa Nanto no chokurei de purotesutanto ni shinkō no jiyū o mitometa.","german":"French king Henry IV granted Protestants freedom of worship with the Edict of Nantes.","furigana":"おう|せい|ちょくれい|しんこう|じゆう|みと"},{"japanese":"戦後、勅令の多くは政令に置き換えられた。","romaji":"Sengo, chokurei no ōku wa seirei ni okikaerareta.","german":"After the war, most imperial edicts were replaced by cabinet orders.","furigana":"せんご|ちょくれい|おお|せいれい|お|か"}],"notes":"勅令 is an ordinance with the force of law issued by the emperor or monarch. In Japan they existed under the Meiji Constitution; many were issued without the consent of the parliament, and after 1947 they were replaced by 政令 (\"cabinet orders\"). For European history it is the usual translation of \"edict\": ナントの勅令. Distinguish 詔書 (imperial document, proclamation) and 勅語 (imperial address, e.g. 教育勅語).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"終戦の詔書が国民に伝えられた。","romaji":"Shūsen no shōsho ga kokumin ni tsutaerareta.","german":"The imperial rescript on the end of the war was conveyed to the people.","furigana":"しゅうせん|しょうしょ|こくみん|つた"},{"japanese":"衆議院の解散は、天皇の詔書によって行われる。","romaji":"Shūgiin no kaisan wa, tennō no shōsho ni yotte okonawareru.","german":"The dissolution of the House of Representatives is carried out by an imperial edict.","furigana":"しゅうぎいん|かいさん|てんのう|しょうしょ|おこな"},{"japanese":"議長が解散詔書を読み上げると、議員たちは万歳をした。","romaji":"Gichō ga kaisan shōsho o yomiageru to, giin-tachi wa banzai o shita.","german":"When the Speaker read out the dissolution edict, the lawmakers shouted \"banzai\".","furigana":"ぎちょう|かいさんしょうしょ|よ|あ|ぎいん|ばんざい"}],"notes":"詔書 is a formal document in which the emperor publicly announces a declaration of intent. Historically well known are 終戦の詔書 (1945) and the New Year's rescript of 1946, the so-called 人間宣言. To this day, the dissolution of the lower house is carried out by a 解散詔書, which the Speaker of the House reads out. Distinguish: 勅令 is an ordinance with the force of law, 勅語 an oral address by the emperor.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"政府は戒厳令の布告を発した。","romaji":"Seifu wa kaigenrei no fukoku o hasshita.","german":"The government issued a proclamation of martial law.","furigana":"せいふ|かいげんれい|ふこく|はっ"},{"japanese":"その国は隣国に宣戦を布告した。","romaji":"Sono kuni wa ringoku ni sensen o fukoku shita.","german":"That country declared war on its neighbor.","furigana":"くに|りんごく|せんせん|ふこく"},{"japanese":"新社長の発言は、ライバル会社への宣戦布告と受け止められた。","romaji":"Shin shachō no hatsugen wa, raibaru gaisha e no sensen fukoku to uketomerareta.","german":"The new president's remarks were taken as a declaration of war against a rival company.","furigana":"しんしゃちょう|はつげん|がいしゃ|せんせんふこく|う|と"}],"notes":"布告 is an official \"proclamation\" or \"decree\" by which a government announces something publicly: 戒厳令を布告する \"to proclaim martial law\". It is most often met in 宣戦布告 \"declaration of war\" (宣戦を布告する), which is also used figuratively for an open \"challenge\" in everyday life or business. Historically, early Meiji laws were called 太政官布告. For the promulgation of new laws today, one says 公布; general announcements are 発表.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新憲法が公布された日を記念する。","romaji":"Shin kenpō ga kōfu sareta hi o kinen suru.","german":"We commemorate the day the new constitution was promulgated.","furigana":"しんけんぽう|こうふ|ひ|きねん"},{"japanese":"法律は公布の日から一年後に施行される。","romaji":"Hōritsu wa kōfu no hi kara ichinengo ni shikō sareru.","german":"The law takes effect one year after the date of promulgation.","furigana":"ほうりつ|こうふ|ひ|いちねんご|しこう"},{"japanese":"市は新しい条例を公布し、来月から路上喫煙を禁止する。","romaji":"Shi wa atarashii jōrei o kōfu shi, raigetsu kara rojō kitsuen o kinshi suru.","german":"The city promulgated a new ordinance banning smoking on the streets from next month.","furigana":"し|あたら|じょうれい|こうふ|らいげつ|ろじょうきつえん|きんし"}],"notes":"公布 is the official \"promulgation\" of a law, constitution or ordinance, in Japan by publication in the official gazette 官報. It is mostly used in the passive: 法律が公布される. It is almost always thought of together with 施行 \"coming into force\": a law is first 公布 and takes effect at a later date. The Meiji Constitution, by contrast, was called 発布; 布告 is a more general proclamation, 発表 an ordinary announcement.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"条約の批准には国会の承認が必要だ。","romaji":"Jōyaku no hijun ni wa kokkai no shōnin ga hitsuyō da.","german":"Ratification of a treaty requires the Diet's approval.","furigana":"じょうやく|ひじゅん|こっかい|しょうにん|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"その国はまだ協定を批准していない。","romaji":"Sono kuni wa mada kyōtei o hijun shite inai.","german":"That country has not yet ratified the agreement.","furigana":"くに|きょうてい|ひじゅん"},{"japanese":"必要な数の国が批准し、条約はようやく発効した。","romaji":"Hitsuyō na kazu no kuni ga hijun shi, jōyaku wa yōyaku hakkō shita.","german":"The required number of countries ratified, and the treaty finally came into force.","furigana":"ひつよう|かず|くに|ひじゅん|じょうやく|はっこう"}],"notes":"批准 is the \"ratification\" of an international treaty by the state, in Japan after the consent of the parliament 国会. Typical: 条約を批准する, 批准書 \"instrument of ratification\", 批准書を交換する. The order matters: first a treaty is signed by representatives (調印・署名), then ratified, and only then does it enter into force (発効). In everyday life the word practically never occurs.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"首脳同士が条約に調印した。","romaji":"Shunō dōshi ga jōyaku ni chōin shita.","german":"The leaders signed the treaty.","furigana":"しゅのうどうし|じょうやく|ちょういん"},{"japanese":"調印式は来月東京で行われる予定だ。","romaji":"Chōinshiki wa raigetsu Tōkyō de okonawareru yotei da.","german":"The signing ceremony is scheduled to be held in Tokyo next month.","furigana":"ちょういんしき|らいげつとうきょう|おこな|よてい"},{"japanese":"両社は合併契約に調印した。","romaji":"Ryōsha wa gappei keiyaku ni chōin shita.","german":"The two companies signed the merger agreement.","furigana":"りょうしゃ|がっぺいけいやく|ちょういん"}],"notes":"調印 is the solemn \"signing\" of a treaty or important agreement by both sides, originally with signature and seal. It is mostly used with に: 条約に調印する, less often 条約を調印する; also 調印式 \"signing ceremony\". For ordinary signatures on forms, one says 署名 or サイン, not 調印. For state treaties, 調印 is followed by 批准.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"両党は選挙協力の盟約を結んだ。","romaji":"Ryōtō wa senkyo kyōryoku no meiyaku o musunda.","german":"The two parties concluded a pact on electoral cooperation.","furigana":"りょうとう|せんきょきょうりょく|めいやく|むす"},{"japanese":"三人は生涯助け合うという盟約を交わした。","romaji":"Sannin wa shōgai tasukeau to iu meiyaku o kawashita.","german":"The three exchanged a pledge to help one another for life.","furigana":"さんにん|しょうがいたす|あ|めいやく|か"},{"japanese":"盟約を破った国は、周辺国の信頼を失った。","romaji":"Meiyaku o yabutta kuni wa, shūhenkoku no shinrai o ushinatta.","german":"The country that broke the pact lost the trust of its neighbors.","furigana":"めいやく|やぶ|くに|しゅうへんこく|しんらい|うしな"}],"notes":"盟約 is a solemnly sworn \"pact\" or \"covenant\", an elevated, rather literary word. Typical combinations: 盟約を結ぶ \"to conclude a pact\", 盟約を交わす \"to swear to one another\", 盟約を破る \"to break the pact\". It emphasizes the mutual promise, whereas 同盟 denotes the alliance relationship resulting from it. In everyday speech, for promises one says 約束 or 誓い.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"軍事同盟の強化が議論されている。","romaji":"Gunji dōmei no kyōka ga giron sarete iru.","german":"Strengthening the military alliance is being debated.","furigana":"ぐんじどうめい|きょうか|ぎろん"},{"japanese":"日本とアメリカは同盟関係にある。","romaji":"Nihon to Amerika wa dōmei kankei ni aru.","german":"Japan and the United States are in an alliance relationship.","furigana":"にほん|どうめいかんけい"},{"japanese":"小さな国々が同盟を結んで大国に対抗した。","romaji":"Chiisana kuniguni ga dōmei o musunde taikoku ni taikō shita.","german":"Small countries formed an alliance to stand up to a great power.","furigana":"ちい|くにぐに|どうめい|むす|たいこく|たいこう"}],"notes":"同盟 is an \"alliance\" between states, parties or groups: 同盟を結ぶ \"to form an alliance\", 同盟国 \"ally\", 軍事同盟, 日米同盟 \"Japan-US alliance\", 同盟関係にある \"to be allied\". 同盟する as a verb is possible but much rarer than 同盟を結ぶ. Do not confuse it with 連盟 \"federation, league\" as an organization (国際連盟 \"League of Nations\"); 盟約 is the sworn pact itself.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"党内の派閥争いが激しくなっている。","romaji":"Tōnai no habatsu arasoi ga hageshiku natte iru.","german":"Die Flügelkämpfe innerhalb der Partei werden immer heftiger.","furigana":"とうない|はばつあらそ|はげ"},{"japanese":"彼は党内のどの派閥にも属していない。","romaji":"Kare wa tōnai no dono habatsu ni mo zoku shite inai.","german":"Er gehört keinem der Parteiflügel an.","furigana":"かれ|とうない|はばつ|ぞく"},{"japanese":"この会社には出身大学ごとの派閥がある。","romaji":"Kono kaisha ni wa shusshin daigaku goto no habatsu ga aru.","german":"In dieser Firma gibt es Cliquen je nach Herkunftsuniversität.","furigana":"かいしゃ|しゅっしんだいがく|はばつ"}],"notes":"派閥 ist eine Gruppierung innerhalb einer Partei oder Organisation, die sich um eine Führungsfigur schart, etwa die nach ihrem Anführer benannten 〜派 der LDP. Es klingt meist negativ nach Seilschaft und Machtkampf: 派閥争い, 派閥に属する, 派閥を解消する, 派閥の領袖 „Faktionsführer“. Auch in Firmen, Krankenhäusern oder Universitäten gibt es 派閥. Achtung: Die deutsche Bundestags-„Fraktion“ entspricht 会派, nicht 派閥.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"右翼団体がデモ行進を行った。","romaji":"Uyoku dantai ga demo kōshin o okonatta.","german":"Rechte Gruppierungen führten einen Demonstrationszug durch.","furigana":"うよくだんたい|こうしん|おこな"},{"japanese":"右翼の街宣車が駅前で大音量の演説をしていた。","romaji":"Uyoku no gaisensha ga ekimae de daionryō no enzetsu o shite ita.","german":"Vor dem Bahnhof hielt ein Lautsprecherwagen der Rechtsextremen lautstarke Reden.","furigana":"うよく|がいせんしゃ|えきまえ|だいおんりょう|えんぜつ"},{"japanese":"打球は右翼スタンドに飛び込んだ。","romaji":"Dakyū wa uyoku sutando ni tobikonda.","german":"Der Ball landete auf der Tribüne im rechten Feld.","furigana":"だきゅう|うよく|と|こ"}],"notes":"右翼 bedeutet „rechter Flügel, die Rechte“. In Japan bezeichnet es aber oft konkret nationalistische Rechtsaußen-Gruppen, etwa mit ihren Lautsprecherwagen 街宣車, und ist damit stärker als deutsch „rechts“; neutraler sind 右派 oder 保守. Dazu 右翼団体, 右翼的. Im Baseball ist 右翼 das „rechte Außenfeld“ (umgangssprachlich ライト), 右翼手 der rechte Außenfeldspieler; auch der rechte Flügel einer Armee heißt so.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"左翼政党が労働者の権利を主張した。","romaji":"Sayoku seitō ga rōdōsha no kenri o shuchō shita.","german":"Left-wing parties asserted workers' rights.","furigana":"さよくせいとう|ろうどうしゃ|けんり|しゅちょう"},{"japanese":"学生時代、彼は左翼運動に参加していた。","romaji":"Gakusei jidai, kare wa sayoku undō ni sanka shite ita.","german":"In his student days, he took part in the left-wing movement.","furigana":"がくせいじだい|かれ|さよくうんどう|さんか"},{"japanese":"彼は試合で左翼を守っている。","romaji":"Kare wa shiai de sayoku o mamotte iru.","german":"He plays left field in the game.","furigana":"かれ|しあい|さよく|まも"}],"notes":"左翼 means \"left wing, the Left\", especially for socialist and communist currents: 左翼政党, 左翼運動, 新左翼 \"New Left\", 左翼的. 左派 sounds more neutral; in internet slang サヨク is used pejoratively. Like 右翼, it also has the meanings \"left field\" in baseball (colloquially レフト, the player 左翼手) and \"left wing\" of a troop.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"中道路線を掲げる新党が結成された。","romaji":"Chūdō rosen o kakageru shintō ga kessei sareta.","german":"A new party advocating a centrist line was formed.","furigana":"ちゅうどうろせん|かか|しんとう|けっせい"},{"japanese":"選挙の結果、中道右派の連立政権が誕生した。","romaji":"Senkyo no kekka, chūdō uha no renritsu seiken ga tanjō shita.","german":"As a result of the election, a center-right coalition government was born.","furigana":"せんきょ|けっか|ちゅうどううは|れんりつせいけん|たんじょう"},{"japanese":"仏教では、両極端を避ける中道が説かれる。","romaji":"Bukkyō de wa, ryōkyokutan o sakeru chūdō ga tokareru.","german":"Buddhism teaches the middle way, which avoids both extremes.","furigana":"ぶっきょう|りょうきょくたん|さ|ちゅうどう|と"}],"notes":"中道 in politics is the \"center\" between left and right: 中道路線 \"centrist course\", 中道政党, 中道左派 \"center-left\", 中道右派 \"center-right\". In Buddhism it denotes the \"middle way\" that avoids extremes. In elevated style it can also mean \"halfway\" (中道にして倒れる), but today one mostly says 途中 or 道半ば for that. Do not confuse it with 中立 \"neutral, impartial\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"保守勢力が伝統的な価値観の維持を訴えた。","romaji":"Hoshu seiryoku ga dentōteki na kachikan no iji o uttaeta.","german":"Conservative forces called for the preservation of traditional values.","furigana":"ほしゅせいりょく|でんとうてき|かちかん|いじ|うった"},{"japanese":"選挙では保守と革新が激しく争った。","romaji":"Senkyo de wa hoshu to kakushin ga hageshiku arasotta.","german":"In the election, conservatives and progressives fought fiercely.","furigana":"せんきょ|ほしゅ|かくしん|はげ|あらそ"},{"japanese":"エレベーターの保守点検のため、本日は階段をご利用ください。","romaji":"Erebētā no hoshu tenken no tame, honjitsu wa kaidan o go-riyō kudasai.","german":"Due to elevator maintenance inspection, please use the stairs today.","furigana":"ほしゅてんけん|ほんじつ|かいだん|りよう"}],"notes":"保守 has two separate meanings. Politically it is \"conservatism\": 保守派, 保守政党, 保守系, 保守的 \"conservative\", with the opposite 革新 \"progressive\". Technically 保守 means \"maintenance, upkeep\" of facilities and systems: 保守点検, 保守管理, システムを保守する; here it is a transitive する-verb and corresponds to the loanword メンテナンス. 維持 is more general \"maintaining\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"穏健派の議員が妥協案を提示した。","romaji":"Onkenha no giin ga dakyōan o teiji shita.","german":"A moderate lawmaker presented a compromise proposal.","furigana":"おんけんは|ぎいん|だきょうあん|ていじ"},{"japanese":"彼は穏健な考え方の持ち主として知られている。","romaji":"Kare wa onken na kangaekata no mochinushi to shite shirarete iru.","german":"He is known as a person of moderate views.","furigana":"かれ|おんけん|かんが|かた|も|ぬし|し"},{"japanese":"政府はより穏健な改革案を選んだ。","romaji":"Seifu wa yori onken na kaikakuan o eranda.","german":"The government chose a more moderate reform plan.","furigana":"せいふ|おんけん|かいかくあん|えら"}],"notes":"穏健 is a な-adjective for \"moderate, level-headed\" and describes above all opinions, programs and political groups: 穏健な意見, 穏健な路線, 穏健派 \"the moderates\", as opposed to 強硬派 \"hardliners\" or 過激派 \"radicals\". For a calm temperament or mild weather, one says 穏やか instead, for a gentle character 温和. The word is elevated and typical of news and commentary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"過激な主張は社会の分断を招く恐れがある。","romaji":"Kageki na shuchō wa shakai no bundan o maneku osore ga aru.","german":"Radical claims risk causing social division.","furigana":"かげき|しゅちょう|しゃかい|ぶんだん|まね|おそ"},{"japanese":"過激派組織が犯行声明を出した。","romaji":"Kagekiha soshiki ga hankō seimei o dashita.","german":"An extremist organization issued a statement claiming responsibility.","furigana":"かげきはそしき|はんこうせいめい|だ"},{"japanese":"手術の後はしばらく過激な運動を控えてください。","romaji":"Shujutsu no ato wa shibaraku kageki na undō o hikaete kudasai.","german":"After the surgery, please refrain from strenuous exercise for a while.","furigana":"しゅじゅつ|あと|かげき|うんどう|ひか"}],"notes":"過激 is a な-adjective for \"radical, extreme\": 過激な思想, 過激な発言, 過激派 \"extremists\". It can also mean \"excessively strenuous\", e.g. 過激な運動 \"overly hard exercise\", and in the media \"drastic, offensive\" (過激な表現, 過激なシーン). 極端 more neutrally means \"extreme, one-sided\", 激しい \"intense\"; the opposite in politics is 穏健.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"群衆を扇動する行為は法律で禁じられている。","romaji":"Gunshū o sendō suru kōi wa hōritsu de kinjirarete iru.","german":"Acts of inciting crowds are prohibited by law.","furigana":"ぐんしゅう|せんどう|こうい|ほうりつ|きん"},{"japanese":"彼はネット上で暴動を扇動した疑いで逮捕された。","romaji":"Kare wa netto-jō de bōdō o sendō shita utagai de taiho sareta.","german":"He was arrested on suspicion of inciting a riot online.","furigana":"かれ|じょう|ぼうどう|せんどう|うたが|たいほ"},{"japanese":"扇動的な演説に聴衆は熱狂した。","romaji":"Sendōteki na enzetsu ni chōshū wa nekkyō shita.","german":"The audience was thrilled by the inflammatory speech.","furigana":"せんどうてき|えんぜつ|ちょうしゅう|ねっきょう"}],"notes":"扇動 means \"incitement, agitation\" of crowds; also written 煽動. Patterns: 群衆を扇動する, 人々を暴動に扇動する, plus 扇動者 \"agitator\" and 扇動的な演説 \"inflammatory speech\". Colloquially one says 煽る for it. そそのかす, by contrast, is inciting a single person to something bad. Do not confuse it with the identically read 先導 \"to lead the way, guide\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"外国による政治工作の疑惑が浮上した。","romaji":"Gaikoku ni yoru seiji kōsaku no giwaku ga fujō shita.","german":"Suspicions of political manipulation by a foreign country have surfaced.","furigana":"がいこく|せいじこうさく|ぎわく|ふじょう"},{"japanese":"子供たちは紙と木で工作を楽しんだ。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa kami to ki de kōsaku o tanoshinda.","german":"The children enjoyed making crafts with paper and wood.","furigana":"こども|かみ|き|こうさく|たの"},{"japanese":"法案を通すため、野党議員への裏工作が行われた。","romaji":"Hōan o tōsu tame, yatō giin e no urakōsaku ga okonawareta.","german":"Behind-the-scenes maneuvering targeted opposition lawmakers to get the bill passed.","furigana":"ほうあん|とお|やとうぎいん|うらこうさく|おこな"}],"notes":"工作 has three meanings; the context decides. In everyday life it is \"crafts, handiwork\": 夏休みの工作, the school subject 図画工作 (short 図工). Technically it stands for \"machining, manufacture\" as in 工作機械 \"machine tool\". Politically it means covert influence: 政治工作, 裏工作 \"behind-the-scenes machinations\", 工作員 \"agent\". The more neutral 根回し is an informal preliminary understanding, not an intrigue.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"最高裁判所への上告が認められた。","romaji":"Saikō saibansho e no jōkoku ga mitomerareta.","german":"The appeal to the Supreme Court was granted.","furigana":"さいこうさいばんしょ|じょうこく|みと"},{"japanese":"被告側は二審の判決を不服として上告した。","romaji":"Hikoku-gawa wa nishin no hanketsu o fufuku to shite jōkoku shita.","german":"The defense appealed to the Supreme Court, dissatisfied with the second-instance ruling.","furigana":"ひこくがわ|にしん|はんけつ|ふふく|じょうこく"},{"japanese":"最高裁は上告を棄却し、判決が確定した。","romaji":"Saikōsai wa jōkoku o kikyaku shi, hanketsu ga kakutei shita.","german":"The Supreme Court dismissed the appeal, and the judgment became final.","furigana":"さいこうさい|じょうこく|ききゃく|はんけつ|かくてい"}],"notes":"上告 is the legal remedy against a second-instance judgment, usually to the Supreme Court 最高裁: 最高裁に上告する, 上告審, 上告を棄却する \"to dismiss the appeal\". Distinguish 控訴, the \"appeal\" against a first-instance judgment; the umbrella term for both is 上訴. Someone who does not accept a verdict typically files 判決を不服として上告する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"弁護士が被告人の弁護を引き受けた。","romaji":"Bengoshi ga hikokunin no bengo o hikiuketa.","german":"The lawyer took on the defendant's defense.","furigana":"べんごし|ひこくにん|べんご|ひ|う"},{"japanese":"みんなに責められていた私を、彼女だけが弁護してくれた。","romaji":"Minna ni semerarete ita watashi o, kanojo dake ga bengo shite kureta.","german":"Only she defended me when everyone was blaming me.","furigana":"せ|わたし|かのじょ|べんご"},{"japanese":"彼のあの行動には弁護の余地がない。","romaji":"Kare no ano kōdō ni wa bengo no yochi ga nai.","german":"There is no room for defending his behavior.","furigana":"かれ|こうどう|べんご|よち"}],"notes":"弁護 is defense in court (弁護士 \"lawyer\", 弁護人 \"defense counsel\"), but also standing up for someone in everyday life: 友人を弁護する \"to take a friend's part\". Fixed phrases are 弁護を引き受ける/依頼する, 自己弁護 (\"self-justification\") and 弁護の余地がない (\"indefensible\"). 擁護 means rather defending rights or principles (人権擁護); colloquially, for standing up for someone, one often says かばう.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"口頭弁論が来週開かれる予定だ。","romaji":"Kōtō benron ga raishū hirakareru yotei da.","german":"The oral hearing is scheduled for next week.","furigana":"こうとうべんろん|らいしゅうひら|よてい"},{"japanese":"弁護人は最終弁論で被告の無罪を訴えた。","romaji":"Bengonin wa saishū benron de hikoku no muzai o uttaeta.","german":"In the closing argument, defense counsel pleaded for the defendant's innocence.","furigana":"べんごにん|さいしゅうべんろん|ひこく|むざい|うった"},{"japanese":"妹は高校の弁論大会で優勝した。","romaji":"Imōto wa kōkō no benron taikai de yūshō shita.","german":"My younger sister won the high school speech contest.","furigana":"いもうと|こうこう|べんろんたいかい|ゆうしょう"}],"notes":"弁論 is presenting and justifying a standpoint. In court, 口頭弁論 means \"oral hearing\" and 最終弁論 the defense's \"closing argument\"; the prosecution's counterpart is 論告. Outside court it is known from 弁論大会 \"speech contest\" and 弁論部 \"debate club\" at schools. 討論 and 議論 mean \"discussion\" among several people, 演説 a public \"speech\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"検察官が論告で厳罰を求めた。","romaji":"Kensatsukan ga ronkoku de genbatsu o motometa.","german":"The prosecutor demanded a severe penalty in the closing argument.","furigana":"けんさつかん|ろんこく|げんばつ|もと"},{"japanese":"次回の公判で論告求刑が行われる予定だ。","romaji":"Jikai no kōhan de ronkoku kyūkei ga okonawareru yotei da.","german":"The closing argument and sentencing request are scheduled for the next trial session.","furigana":"じかい|こうはん|ろんこくきゅうけい|おこな|よてい"},{"japanese":"検察は論告で、被告に反省の態度が見られないと指摘した。","romaji":"Kensatsu wa ronkoku de, hikoku ni hansei no taido ga mirarenai to shiteki shita.","german":"In its closing argument, the prosecution pointed out that the defendant showed no sign of remorse.","furigana":"けんさつ|ろんこく|ひこく|はんせい|たいど|み|してき"}],"notes":"論告 is the \"prosecution's closing argument\" in a criminal trial, in which it summarizes the evidence. It almost always occurs together with the sentencing request as 論告求刑. The defense's counterpart is 最終弁論. The word belongs to the technical language of the judiciary and court reporting and does not occur in everyday life.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"検察は被告人に懲役十年を求刑した。","romaji":"Kensatsu wa hikokunin ni chōeki jūnen o kyūkei shita.","german":"The prosecution sought ten years' imprisonment for the defendant.","furigana":"けんさつ|ひこくにん|ちょうえきじゅうねん|きゅうけい"},{"japanese":"判決は求刑より二年軽かった。","romaji":"Hanketsu wa kyūkei yori ninen karukatta.","german":"The verdict was two years lighter than the sentence sought.","furigana":"はんけつ|きゅうけい|にねんかる"},{"japanese":"罰金五十万円が求刑された。","romaji":"Bakkin gojūman en ga kyūkei sareta.","german":"A fine of 500,000 yen was requested.","furigana":"ばっきんごじゅうまんえん|きゅうけい"}],"notes":"求刑 is the prosecution's \"sentencing request\": 被告に懲役〜年を求刑する, often as 論告求刑. The verdict is measured against it: 求刑通り \"as requested\", 求刑を上回る／下回る判決. The actual determination by the court is called 量刑. Since 2025, imprisonment is officially called 拘禁刑; in older texts one still reads 懲役 and 禁錮.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"裁判員が量刑の判断に参加する。","romaji":"Saiban'in ga ryōkei no handan ni sanka suru.","german":"Lay judges take part in the sentencing decision.","furigana":"さいばんいん|りょうけい|はんだん|さんか"},{"japanese":"弁護側は量刑が重すぎるとして控訴した。","romaji":"Bengo-gawa wa ryōkei ga omosugiru to shite kōso shita.","german":"The defense appealed, arguing the sentence was too heavy.","furigana":"べんごがわ|りょうけい|おも|こうそ"},{"japanese":"判決文では量刑の理由が詳しく説明された。","romaji":"Hanketsubun de wa ryōkei no riyū ga kuwashiku setsumei sareta.","german":"The reasons for the sentence were explained in detail in the written judgment.","furigana":"はんけつぶん|りょうけい|りゆう|くわ|せつめい"}],"notes":"量刑 is the \"sentencing\" by the court, i.e. the decision on the type and level of punishment. Typical: 量刑が重い／軽い, 量刑の判断, 量刑の理由, 量刑不当 \"unreasonable sentence\" as a ground for appeal. By contrast, 求刑 is the prosecution's request and 判決 the verdict as a whole. Since the introduction of lay judges 裁判員, the word is also frequently used in the news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"死刑の執行が法務大臣の命令で行われた。","romaji":"Shikei no shikkō ga hōmu daijin no meirei de okonawareta.","german":"The execution was carried out by order of the Minister of Justice.","furigana":"しけい|しっこう|ほうむだいじん|めいれい|おこな"},{"japanese":"被告には執行猶予付きの判決が言い渡された。","romaji":"Hikoku ni wa shikkō yūyo tsuki no hanketsu ga iiwatasareta.","german":"A sentence with a suspended execution was handed down to the defendant.","furigana":"ひこく|しっこうゆうよつ|はんけつ|い|わた"},{"japanese":"党の執行部は選挙の敗北を受けて総辞職した。","romaji":"Tō no shikkōbu wa senkyo no haiboku o ukete sōjishoku shita.","german":"The party's executive leadership resigned en masse after the election defeat.","furigana":"とう|しっこうぶ|せんきょ|はいぼく|う|そうじしょく"}],"notes":"執行 is the \"enforcement\" or \"execution\" of something legally or officially decided: 刑を執行する, 死刑の執行, 強制執行 \"compulsory enforcement\", 予算の執行. Very common is 執行猶予 \"suspension of sentence\". 執行部 also denotes the leadership of a party or union, 執行役員 a senior executive officer. 実行 is general \"carrying out\" of a plan, 実施 the \"implementing\" of a measure.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"男は軽犯罪法違反で拘留十日の刑を受けた。","romaji":"Otoko wa keihanzaihō ihan de kōryū tōka no kei o uketa.","german":"The man received a ten-day detention sentence for violating the Minor Offenses Act.","furigana":"おとこ|けいはんざいほういはん|こうりゅうとおか|けい|う"},{"japanese":"拘留は一日以上三十日未満の短い刑だ。","romaji":"Kōryū wa ichinichi ijō sanjūnichi miman no mijikai kei da.","german":"Detention is a short sentence of at least one day and under thirty days.","furigana":"こうりゅう|いちにちいじょうさんじゅうにちみまん|みじか|けい"},{"japanese":"拘留は罰金よりも軽い刑とされている。","romaji":"Kōryū wa bakkin yori mo karui kei to sarete iru.","german":"Detention is considered a lighter punishment than a fine.","furigana":"こうりゅう|ばっきん|かる|けい"}],"notes":"拘留 is a separate, very light penalty in Japanese criminal law: detention of one to at most 29 days, for example for violations of the Minor Offenses Act 軽犯罪法 (拘留又は科料). It is regarded as milder than a fine 罰金. The identically read 勾留, by contrast, is pretrial detention of suspects; newspapers correctly write 勾留 for that, even though in everyday life the two are confused.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不当な身体の拘束は人権侵害である。","romaji":"Futō na shintai no kōsoku wa jinken shingai de aru.","german":"Unjust physical restraint is a violation of human rights.","furigana":"ふとう|しんたい|こうそく|じんけんしんがい"},{"japanese":"この仕事は移動を含めると拘束時間が長い。","romaji":"Kono shigoto wa idō o fukumeru to kōsoku jikan ga nagai.","german":"This job has long bound hours when travel is included.","furigana":"しごと|いどう|ふく|こうそくじかん|なが"},{"japanese":"警察は現場にいた男の身柄を拘束した。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa genba ni ita otoko no migara o kōsoku shita.","german":"The police took into custody the man who was at the scene.","furigana":"けいさつ|げんば|おとこ|みがら|こうそく"}],"notes":"拘束 means \"restraint, restriction of freedom\", in a broader sense \"binding\". With the police: 身柄を拘束する \"to take into custody\", which need not yet be a formal arrest 逮捕. In working life, 拘束時間 is the time one is tied up including breaks and commuting; 法的拘束力 means \"legally binding force\". 束縛 means rather personal constraint, for example in a relationship.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"裁判官が被疑者の勾留を決定した。","romaji":"Saibankan ga higisha no kōryū o kettei shita.","german":"The judge ordered the suspect's detention.","furigana":"さいばんかん|ひぎしゃ|こうりゅう|けってい"},{"japanese":"検察は勾留の延長を請求した。","romaji":"Kensatsu wa kōryū no enchō o seikyū shita.","german":"The prosecution requested an extension of the detention.","furigana":"けんさつ|こうりゅう|えんちょう|せいきゅう"},{"japanese":"容疑者は勾留期限の前日に起訴された。","romaji":"Yōgisha wa kōryū kigen no zenjitsu ni kiso sareta.","german":"The suspect was indicted the day before the detention deadline.","furigana":"ようぎしゃ|こうりゅうきげん|ぜんじつ|きそ"}],"notes":"勾留 is the \"pretrial detention\" a judge orders for suspects or defendants. After the arrest 逮捕 (at most 72 hours), ten days follow at first, which can be extended by another ten: 勾留延長, 勾留期限. It is mostly used in the passive (勾留される) or as a noun. Do not confuse it with the identically read 拘留, a short prison sentence.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"弁護人が保釈を請求した。","romaji":"Bengonin ga hoshaku o seikyū shita.","german":"The defense counsel applied for bail.","furigana":"べんごにん|ほしゃく|せいきゅう"},{"japanese":"彼は保釈金を納めて釈放された。","romaji":"Kare wa hoshakukin o osamete shakuhō sareta.","german":"He paid the bail and was released.","furigana":"かれ|ほしゃくきん|おさ|しゃくほう"},{"japanese":"裁判所は逃亡の恐れがあるとして保釈を認めなかった。","romaji":"Saibansho wa tōbō no osore ga aru to shite hoshaku o mitomenakatta.","german":"The court did not grant bail, citing a risk of flight.","furigana":"さいばんしょ|とうぼう|おそ|ほしゃく|みと"}],"notes":"保釈 is \"release on bail\"; in Japan it is possible only after indictment 起訴, not during the pretrial detention before it. Typical: 保釈を請求する, 保釈を認める／却下する, 保釈金 \"bail money\", 保釈中 \"out on bail\", 被告が保釈される. General \"releasing\" is 釈放, early release from prison is 仮釈放.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"模範囚として仮釈放が認められた。","romaji":"Mohanshū to shite kari shakuhō ga mitomerareta.","german":"Parole was granted for his model-prisoner conduct.","furigana":"もはんしゅう|かりしゃくほう|みと"},{"japanese":"無期刑でも、条件を満たせば仮釈放されることがある。","romaji":"Mukikei demo, jōken o mitaseba kari shakuhō sareru koto ga aru.","german":"Even those serving life sentences may be paroled if they meet the conditions.","furigana":"むきけい|じょうけん|み|かりしゃくほう"},{"japanese":"男は仮釈放中に再び事件を起こした。","romaji":"Otoko wa kari shakuhō-chū ni futatabi jiken o okoshita.","german":"The man committed another crime while on parole.","furigana":"おとこ|かりしゃくほうちゅう|ふたた|じけん|お"}],"notes":"仮釈放 is the early \"conditional release\" of a prisoner under conditions and supervision; in the news it is also called 仮出所. It is mostly used in the passive or as a noun: 仮釈放される, 仮釈放が認められる, 仮釈放中. Do not confuse it with 保釈 \"release on bail\" before the verdict, or with 執行猶予, where one does not have to go to prison at all. 釈放 alone is the general \"release\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"天皇即位の際に恩赦が実施された。","romaji":"Tennō sokui no sai ni onsha ga jisshi sareta.","german":"An amnesty was implemented upon the emperor's accession.","furigana":"てんのうそくい|さい|おんしゃ|じっし"},{"japanese":"彼は恩赦を受けて刑務所から釈放された。","romaji":"Kare wa onsha o ukete keimusho kara shakuhō sareta.","german":"He was released from prison after receiving a pardon.","furigana":"かれ|おんしゃ|う|けいむしょ|しゃくほう"},{"japanese":"恩赦は三権分立に反するという批判もある。","romaji":"Onsha wa sanken bunritsu ni hansuru to iu hihan mo aru.","german":"Some criticize that pardons run counter to the separation of powers.","furigana":"おんしゃ|さんけんぶんりつ|はん|ひはん"}],"notes":"恩赦 is the umbrella term for \"pardon\" and \"amnesty\" by the executive: in Japan the cabinet decides and the emperor attests. Types are 大赦 (amnesty for whole groups of offenses), 特赦 (pardon of individuals), 減刑, 刑の執行の免除 and 復権. Typical: 恩赦を受ける, 恩赦が実施される, 恩赦で釈放される. General amnesties are often granted on state occasions such as an accession to the throne; critics see this as an interference with the judiciary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"政治犯に対する特赦が発令された。","romaji":"Seijihan ni taisuru tokusha ga hatsurei sareta.","german":"A special pardon for political prisoners was issued.","furigana":"せいじはん|たい|とくしゃ|はつれい"},{"japanese":"建国記念日に、受刑者の一部が特赦で釈放された。","romaji":"Kenkoku kinenbi ni, jukeisha no ichibu ga tokusha de shakuhō sareta.","german":"On National Foundation Day, some prisoners were released under a special pardon.","furigana":"けんこくきねんび|じゅけいしゃ|いちぶ|とくしゃ|しゃくほう"},{"japanese":"特赦を受けると、有罪判決の効力が失われる。","romaji":"Tokusha o ukeru to, yūzai hanketsu no kōryoku ga ushinawareru.","german":"When someone receives a special pardon, the effect of the guilty verdict is lost.","furigana":"とくしゃ|う|ゆうざいはんけつ|こうりょく|うしな"}],"notes":"特赦 is a form of 恩赦: the \"special pardon\" of specific persons, through which a guilty verdict loses its effect. By contrast, 大赦 covers all offenders of certain types of offense at once. In foreign news, 特赦 is also used for mass pardons, for example when a state frees prisoners on its national day. Patterns: 特赦を受ける, 特赦で釈放される, 特赦が発令される.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"控訴審で減刑が認められた。","romaji":"Kōsoshin de genkei ga mitomerareta.","german":"A reduction of the sentence was granted on appeal.","furigana":"こうそしん|げんけい|みと"},{"japanese":"多くの市民が減刑を求める嘆願書に署名した。","romaji":"Ōku no shimin ga genkei o motomeru tangansho ni shomei shita.","german":"Many citizens signed a petition seeking a reduced sentence.","furigana":"おお|しみん|げんけい|もと|たんがんしょ|しょめい"},{"japanese":"彼の死刑判決は無期刑に減刑された。","romaji":"Kare no shikei hanketsu wa mukikei ni genkei sareta.","german":"His death sentence was commuted to life imprisonment.","furigana":"かれ|しけいはんけつ|むきけい|げんけい"}],"notes":"減刑 is \"commutation\", the reduction of a penalty already imposed or requested. It is done by a higher court or as a form of 恩赦. The passive and the noun are typical: 減刑される, 減刑が認められる, 減刑を求める嘆願書 \"petition for clemency\". Do not confuse it with 減軽, the statutory mitigation of punishment at sentencing, for example because of diminished responsibility.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"時効成立により免訴の判決が出た。","romaji":"Jikō seiritsu ni yori menso no hanketsu ga deta.","german":"A judgment of dismissal was issued because the statute of limitations had run.","furigana":"じこうせいりつ|めんそ|はんけつ|で"},{"japanese":"大赦があったため、その事件は免訴となった。","romaji":"Taisha ga atta tame, sono jiken wa menso to natta.","german":"Because of an amnesty, the case was dismissed.","furigana":"たいしゃ|じけん|めんそ"},{"japanese":"再審で無罪ではなく免訴が言い渡され、遺族は不満を示した。","romaji":"Saishin de muzai de wa naku menso ga iiwatasare, izoku wa fuman o shimeshita.","german":"At the retrial, a dismissal rather than an acquittal was pronounced, and the bereaved family expressed dissatisfaction.","furigana":"さいしん|むざい|めんそ|い|わた|いぞく|ふまん|しめ"}],"notes":"免訴 is, in criminal proceedings, a judgment that ends the proceedings without deciding on guilt or innocence, for example because of the statute of limitations, amnesty (大赦) or an already final decision in the same matter. It is therefore not an acquittal 無罪. Distinguish 公訴棄却 (dismissal due to procedural defects) and 不起訴 (the prosecution does not file charges at all). Typical: 免訴の判決, 免訴となる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"裁判所は原告の請求を棄却した。","romaji":"Saibansho wa genkoku no seikyū o kikyaku shita.","german":"The court dismissed the plaintiff's claim.","furigana":"さいばんしょ|げんこく|せいきゅう|ききゃく"},{"japanese":"高裁は被告の控訴を棄却した。","romaji":"Kōsai wa hikoku no kōso o kikyaku shita.","german":"The high court dismissed the defendant's appeal.","furigana":"こうさい|ひこく|こうそ|ききゃく"},{"japanese":"有意水準5%で帰無仮説は棄却された。","romaji":"Yūi suijun go pāsento de kimu kasetsu wa kikyaku sareta.","german":"The null hypothesis was rejected at the 5% significance level.","furigana":"ゆういすいじゅん|きむかせつ|ききゃく"}],"notes":"棄却 is the \"dismissal\" of a motion after examination on the merits: 請求を棄却する, 控訴棄却, 上告を棄却する. The important counterpart is 却下, rejection on formal grounds without examining the matter, so to speak turning someone away at the door. In statistics, 棄却 means \"to reject\": 帰無仮説を棄却する \"to reject the null hypothesis\". In everyday speech, for \"to decline\" one rather says 断る or 拒否する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"労使間の紛争で、労働委員会が調停に入った。","romaji":"Rōshikan no funsō de, rōdō iinkai ga chōtei ni haitta.","german":"In the labor-management dispute, the labor relations commission stepped in to mediate.","furigana":"ろうしかん|ふんそう|ろうどういいんかい|ちょうてい|はい"},{"japanese":"妻は家庭裁判所に離婚調停を申し立てた。","romaji":"Tsuma wa katei saibansho ni rikon chōtei o mōshitateta.","german":"My wife filed for divorce mediation at the family court.","furigana":"つま|かていさいばんしょ|りこんちょうてい|もう|た"},{"japanese":"話し合いはまとまらず、調停は不成立に終わった。","romaji":"Hanashiai wa matomarazu, chōtei wa fuseiritsu ni owatta.","german":"The talks did not come together, and the mediation ended without agreement.","furigana":"はな|あ|ちょうてい|ふせいりつ|お"}],"notes":"調停 is \"mediation\" or \"conciliation\": a neutral third party intermediates, and the parties agree by giving way to each other. Especially well known is 離婚調停 before the family court; also 調停を申し立てる, 調停委員, 調停が成立する／不成立に終わる, 調停に入る. In 仲裁 (arbitration), by contrast, an arbitrator decides bindingly; 和解 is the \"settlement\" reached.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"国際仲裁裁判所に提訴された。","romaji":"Kokusai chūsai saibansho ni teiso sareta.","german":"Es wurde beim internationalen Schiedsgericht Klage erhoben.","furigana":"こくさいちゅうさいさいばんしょ|ていそ"},{"japanese":"兄弟げんかの仲裁に入った。","romaji":"Kyōdai genka no chūsai ni haitta.","german":"Ich bin dazwischengegangen, um den Streit unter den Geschwistern zu schlichten.","furigana":"きょうだい|ちゅうさい|はい"},{"japanese":"契約には、紛争は仲裁で解決するという条項がある。","romaji":"Keiyaku ni wa, funsō wa chūsai de kaiketsu suru to iu jōkō ga aru.","german":"Der Vertrag enthält eine Klausel, nach der Streitigkeiten durch ein Schiedsverfahren beigelegt werden.","furigana":"けいやく|ふんそう|ちゅうさい|かいけつ|じょうこう"}],"notes":"仲裁 bedeutet im Recht das „Schiedsverfahren“: Ein Schiedsrichter oder Schiedsgericht 仲裁裁判所 fällt eine verbindliche Entscheidung, anders als beim 調停, wo die Parteien selbst zustimmen müssen. Im Alltag heißt 仲裁 einfach „schlichtend dazwischengehen“: けんかの仲裁に入る. Das deutsche Wort „Arbitrage“ (Ausnutzen von Preisunterschieden) passt dagegen nicht; das wäre アービトラージ oder 裁定取引.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"医師の過失により患者が死亡した。","romaji":"Ishi no kashitsu ni yori kanja ga shibō shita.","german":"The patient died due to the doctor's negligence.","furigana":"いし|かしつ|かんじゃ|しぼう"},{"japanese":"事故の過失割合は八対二と判断された。","romaji":"Jiko no kashitsu wariai wa hachi tai ni to handan sareta.","german":"The fault ratio for the accident was determined to be eight to two.","furigana":"じこ|かしつわりあい|はちたいに|はんだん"},{"japanese":"運転手は過失運転致死の疑いで逮捕された。","romaji":"Untenshu wa kashitsu unten chishi no utagai de taiho sareta.","german":"The driver was arrested on suspicion of causing death by negligent driving.","furigana":"うんてんしゅ|かしつうんてんちし|うたが|たいほ"}],"notes":"Legally, 過失 is \"negligence\": damage resulting from lack of care, not from intent. Important combinations: 過失致死 \"negligent homicide\", 業務上過失, 重過失 \"gross negligence\" and, for traffic accidents, 過失割合 \"fault ratio\". The opposite is 故意 \"intent\". For everyday mistakes one says ミス or 失敗; 過失 sounds legal.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"故意による犯行であることが証明された。","romaji":"Koi ni yoru hankō de aru koto ga shōmei sareta.","german":"It was proven that the crime was committed intentionally.","furigana":"こい|はんこう|しょうめい"},{"japanese":"彼は故意にボールを相手にぶつけたわけではない。","romaji":"Kare wa koi ni bōru o aite ni butsuketa wake de wa nai.","german":"He didn't deliberately hit the other player with the ball.","furigana":"かれ|こい|あいて"},{"japanese":"故意か過失かによって、罪の重さが変わる。","romaji":"Koi ka kashitsu ka ni yotte, tsumi no omosa ga kawaru.","german":"The severity of the crime changes depending on whether it was intentional or negligent.","furigana":"こい|かしつ|つみ|おも|か"}],"notes":"故意 is legally \"intent\", the conscious bringing about of a result, with the opposite 過失 \"negligence\". Adverbially 故意に means \"intentionally, on purpose\", for example with a deliberate foul in sports. Technical term: 未必の故意 \"conditional intent (dolus eventualis)\". In everyday speech, instead of 故意に one rather says わざと, in factual language 意図的に. Do not confuse it with the identically read 恋 \"love\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"共謀罪の適用範囲について議論が続いている。","romaji":"Kyōbō zai no tekiyō han'i ni tsuite giron ga tsuzuite iru.","german":"Debate continues over the scope of application of the conspiracy law.","furigana":"きょうぼうざい|てきようはんい|ぎろん|つづ"},{"japanese":"男は元同僚と共謀して会社の金を横領した。","romaji":"Otoko wa moto dōryō to kyōbō shite kaisha no kane o ōryō shita.","german":"The man conspired with a former colleague to embezzle company funds.","furigana":"おとこ|もとどうりょう|きょうぼう|かいしゃ|かね|おうりょう"},{"japanese":"警察は二人の間に共謀があったとみて捜査している。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa futari no aida ni kyōbō ga atta to mite sōsa shite iru.","german":"The police are investigating on the belief that there was a conspiracy between the two.","furigana":"けいさつ|ふたり|あいだ|きょうぼう|そうさ"}],"notes":"共謀 is the \"joint planning\" of a crime, i.e. conspiracy or complicity. Typical: Aと共謀して〜する, 共謀の疑い, 共謀共同正犯 \"joint principal by agreement\" and 共謀罪, which was controversially introduced in 2017 as \"preparation of terrorist offenses\". 陰謀, by contrast, is a large-scale \"plot\", 結託 joint collusion; colloquially one says ぐるになる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"殺人教唆の罪で起訴された。","romaji":"Satsujin kyōsa no tsumi de kiso sareta.","german":"He was indicted for instigating murder.","furigana":"さつじんきょうさ|つみ|きそ"},{"japanese":"彼は部下に証拠の隠滅を教唆した疑いがある。","romaji":"Kare wa buka ni shōko no inmetsu o kyōsa shita utagai ga aru.","german":"He is suspected of having instigated a subordinate to destroy evidence.","furigana":"かれ|ぶか|しょうこ|いんめつ|きょうさ|うたが"},{"japanese":"教唆犯にも正犯と同じ刑が科される。","romaji":"Kyōsahan ni mo seihan to onaji kei ga kasareru.","german":"The instigator is also subject to the same penalty as the principal offender.","furigana":"きょうさはん|せいはん|おな|けい|か"}],"notes":"教唆 is \"instigation\": inducing someone to commit a crime. Patterns: 人に犯罪を教唆する, 殺人教唆, 自殺教唆, 教唆犯 \"instigator\". Under Japanese law, the instigator faces the same punishment as the principal offender 正犯, while aiding and abetting 幇助 is punished more leniently. In everyday speech one says そそのかす; 扇動 is the inciting of a crowd.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"犯罪の幇助として処罰される可能性がある。","romaji":"Hanzai no hōjo to shite shobatsu sareru kanōsei ga aru.","german":"He may be punished for aiding a crime.","furigana":"はんざい|ほうじょ|しょばつ|かのうせい"},{"japanese":"医師が自殺幇助の罪に問われた。","romaji":"Ishi ga jisatsu hōjo no tsumi ni towareta.","german":"A doctor was charged with assisting a suicide.","furigana":"いし|じさつほうじょ|つみ|と"},{"japanese":"幇助犯の刑は正犯より軽くなる。","romaji":"Hōjohan no kei wa seihan yori karuku naru.","german":"The sentence for an accomplice is lighter than for the principal offender.","furigana":"ほうじょはん|けい|せいはん|かる"}],"notes":"幇助 is \"aiding and abetting\" a crime, i.e. supporting the perpetrator without being a perpetrator oneself: 犯罪を幇助する, 自殺幇助 \"assisted suicide\", 幇助犯 \"accomplice\". Because 幇 is not a standard kanji, newspapers mostly write ほう助. The accomplice's penalty is mitigated compared with that of the principal offender 正犯; with instigation 教唆 this is not so.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"正当防衛が認められ、無罪となった。","romaji":"Seitō bōei ga mitomerare, muzai to natta.","german":"Self-defense was recognized and he was acquitted.","furigana":"せいとうぼうえい|みと|むざい"},{"japanese":"相手が先に殴ってきたので、正当防衛だと主張した。","romaji":"Aite ga saki ni nagutte kita node, seitō bōei da to shuchō shita.","german":"Because the other person had hit him first, he claimed self-defense.","furigana":"あいて|さき|なぐ|せいとうぼうえい|しゅちょう"},{"japanese":"反撃が行き過ぎていたため、正当防衛ではなく過剰防衛とされた。","romaji":"Hangeki ga ikisugite ita tame, seitō bōei de wa naku kajō bōei to sareta.","german":"Because the counterattack went too far, it was ruled excessive defense rather than self-defense.","furigana":"はんげき|い|す|せいとうぼうえい|かじょうぼうえい"}],"notes":"正当防衛 is \"self-defense\": defending against an imminent unlawful attack, which goes unpunished. Literally, 正当 means \"justified\" and 防衛 means \"defense\". Typical: 正当防衛が認められる／成立する, 正当防衛を主張する. If the defense goes too far, it is called 過剰防衛 \"excessive self-defense\". To be distinguished from this is 緊急避難, where a danger is averted at the expense of an uninvolved third party.","levelNote":"Compound: 正当 (N1) + 防衛 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"緊急避難の要件を満たすかが争点となった。","romaji":"Kinkyū hinan no yōken o mitasu ka ga sōten to natta.","german":"The key issue was whether the requirements of necessity were met.","furigana":"きんきゅうひなん|ようけん|み|そうてん"},{"japanese":"近くの公園が緊急避難場所に指定されている。","romaji":"Chikaku no kōen ga kinkyū hinan basho ni shitei sarete iru.","german":"The nearby park is designated as an emergency evacuation site.","furigana":"ちか|こうえん|きんきゅうひなんばしょ|してい"},{"japanese":"今回の減税は、景気を支えるための緊急避難的な措置だ。","romaji":"Konkai no genzei wa, keiki o sasaeru tame no kinkyū hinanteki na sochi da.","german":"This tax cut is an emergency measure to support the economy.","furigana":"こんかい|げんぜい|けいき|ささ|きんきゅうひなんてき|そち"}],"notes":"緊急避難 literally means \"emergency escape\". In criminal law it is \"justifiable necessity\": one averts an acute danger and thereby harms the legal interests of an uninvolved person, for example by entering someone else's house while fleeing a fire. In disaster management it means \"emergency evacuation\": 指定緊急避難場所 \"designated emergency evacuation site\". Figuratively, 緊急避難的な措置 stands for an \"emergency measure, stopgap solution\".","levelNote":"Compound: 緊急 (N1) + 避難 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"検察官が公訴を提起した。","romaji":"Kensatsukan ga kōso o teiki shita.","german":"The prosecutor filed an indictment.","furigana":"けんさつかん|こうそ|ていき"},{"japanese":"殺人罪の公訴時効は2010年に廃止された。","romaji":"Satsujinzai no kōso jikō wa 2010-nen ni haishi sareta.","german":"The statute of limitations for murder was abolished in 2010.","furigana":"さつじんざい|こうそじこう|ねん|はいし"},{"japanese":"裁判所は手続きの違法を理由に公訴を棄却した。","romaji":"Saibansho wa tetsuzuki no ihō o riyū ni kōso o kikyaku shita.","german":"The court dismissed the prosecution because of a procedural illegality.","furigana":"さいばんしょ|てつづ|いほう|りゆう|こうそ|ききゃく"}],"notes":"公訴 is the \"public prosecution\" brought by the public prosecutor's office in criminal proceedings: 公訴を提起する is the formal variant of 起訴する. Important terms are 公訴時効 \"statute of limitations (for prosecution)\" and 公訴棄却 \"dismissal of the prosecution for procedural defects\". Careful when listening: the identically pronounced 控訴 means \"appeal\" against a judgment. In everyday speech one almost always says 起訴.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この問題は民事裁判で解決すべきだ。","romaji":"Kono mondai wa minji saiban de kaiketsu subeki da.","german":"This problem should be settled in a civil trial.","furigana":"もんだい|みんじさいばん|かいけつ"},{"japanese":"警察は民事不介入の原則を理由に動かなかった。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa minji fukainyū no gensoku o riyū ni ugokanakatta.","german":"The police did not act, citing the principle of non-intervention in civil matters.","furigana":"けいさつ|みんじふかいにゅう|げんそく|りゆう|うご"},{"japanese":"刑事では不起訴になったが、遺族は民事で損害賠償を求めた。","romaji":"Keiji de wa fukiso ni natta ga, izoku wa minji de songai baishō o motometa.","german":"No charges were filed in the criminal case, but the bereaved family sought damages in a civil suit.","furigana":"けいじ|ふきそ|いぞく|みんじ|そんがいばいしょう|もと"}],"notes":"民事 refers to everything belonging to civil law, i.e. disputes between private persons or companies: 民事裁判, 民事訴訟, 民事事件, 民事責任. The opposite term is 刑事 \"criminal\". Well known is 民事不介入, the principle that the police do not interfere in private legal disputes. One often reads that someone was not prosecuted criminally, but that damages are being demanded 民事で (in a civil suit).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"刑事事件として捜査が開始された。","romaji":"Keiji jiken to shite sōsa ga kaishi sareta.","german":"An investigation was opened as a criminal case.","furigana":"けいじじけん|そうさ|かいし"},{"japanese":"刑事が聞き込みのために近所を回った。","romaji":"Keiji ga kikikomi no tame ni kinjo o mawatta.","german":"A detective went around the neighborhood to question people.","furigana":"けいじ|き|こ|きんじょ|まわ"},{"japanese":"十四歳未満の子どもは刑事責任を問われない。","romaji":"Jūyon-sai miman no kodomo wa keiji sekinin o towarenai.","german":"Children under fourteen are not held criminally responsible.","furigana":"じゅうよんさいみまん|こ|けいじせきにん|と"}],"notes":"刑事 has two meanings. As a prefix it means \"criminal, criminal-law\": 刑事事件, 刑事裁判, 刑事責任, as opposed to 民事 \"civil\". In everyday life, however, 刑事 is above all the \"detective\", the plainclothes investigator, known from 刑事ドラマ \"crime series\"; as a form of address one hears 刑事さん. The uniformed police officer, by contrast, is called 警察官 or colloquially お巡りさん.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"住民が行政訴訟を起こして許可の取り消しを求めた。","romaji":"Jūmin ga gyōsei soshō o okoshite kyoka no torikeshi o motometa.","german":"The residents filed an administrative lawsuit and demanded that the permit be revoked.","furigana":"じゅうみん|ぎょうせいそしょう|お|きょか|と|け|もと"},{"japanese":"国を相手に行政訴訟を起こすのは簡単ではない。","romaji":"Kuni o aite ni gyōsei soshō o okosu no wa kantan de wa nai.","german":"It is not easy to bring an administrative lawsuit against the state.","furigana":"くに|あいて|ぎょうせいそしょう|お|かんたん"},{"japanese":"行政訴訟で原告が勝訴するケースは少ない。","romaji":"Gyōsei soshō de genkoku ga shōso suru kēsu wa sukunai.","german":"Plaintiffs rarely win in administrative lawsuits.","furigana":"ぎょうせいそしょう|げんこく|しょうそ|すく"}],"notes":"行政訴訟 is a lawsuit against actions of the state or an authority, for example to have a permit or an administrative decision revoked; it is regulated in the 行政事件訴訟法. Typical: 行政訴訟を起こす／提起する, 国を相手に行政訴訟. Alongside 民事訴訟 \"civil lawsuit\" and 刑事訴訟 \"criminal trial\", it is the third major type of proceeding. For citizens, such suits are considered difficult to win.","levelNote":"Compound: 行政 (N1) + 訴訟 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"政令により施行日が定められた。","romaji":"Seirei ni yori shikōbi ga sadamerareta.","german":"The date of entry into force was set by cabinet order.","furigana":"せいれい|しこうび|さだ"},{"japanese":"横浜市は政令指定都市の一つだ。","romaji":"Yokohama-shi wa seirei shitei toshi no hitotsu da.","german":"Yokohama is one of the government-designated cities.","furigana":"よこはまし|せいれいしていとし|ひと"},{"japanese":"政府は政令を改正し、規制を緩和した。","romaji":"Seifu wa seirei o kaisei shi, kisei o kanwa shita.","german":"The government amended the ordinance and eased the regulations.","furigana":"せいふ|せいれい|かいせい|きせい|かんわ"}],"notes":"政令 is a \"government ordinance\" issued by the Cabinet to implement a law. The order of rank is 法律 (from the Diet) > 政令 (Cabinet) > 省令 (an individual ministry); 条例 are local regulations of local governments. Typical: 政令で定める, 政令を改正する. Very common is 政令指定都市, a \"large city designated by government ordinance\" with extended powers, such as Yokohama or Osaka.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"省令で詳細な基準が規定されている。","romaji":"Shōrei de shōsai na kijun ga kitei sarete iru.","german":"Detailed standards are specified in the ministerial ordinance.","furigana":"しょうれい|しょうさい|きじゅん|きてい"},{"japanese":"厚生労働省は省令を改正して基準を見直した。","romaji":"Kōsei rōdōshō wa shōrei o kaisei shite kijun o minaoshita.","german":"The Ministry of Health, Labour and Welfare amended its ordinance and revised the standards.","furigana":"こうせいろうどうしょう|しょうれい|かいせい|きじゅん|みなお"},{"japanese":"細かい手続きは法律ではなく省令に書かれている。","romaji":"Komakai tetsuzuki wa hōritsu de wa naku shōrei ni kakarete iru.","german":"The detailed procedures are written not in the law but in a ministerial ordinance.","furigana":"こま|てつづ|ほうりつ|しょうれい|か"}],"notes":"省令 is a \"ministerial ordinance\" issued by an individual ministry 省, e.g. 厚生労働省令. In the hierarchy of norms it ranks below 法律 and 政令 and usually regulates technical details and procedures: 省令で定める, 省令を改正する. For regulations of agencies and similar bodies, one says 規則; 通達 are internal administrative instructions without the force of law.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"各省庁に通達が出された。","romaji":"Kaku shōchō ni tsūtatsu ga dasareta.","german":"A directive was issued to all government ministries and agencies.","furigana":"かくしょうちょう|つうたつ|だ"},{"japanese":"本社から全支店に、経費削減の通達が届いた。","romaji":"Honsha kara zen shiten ni, keihi sakugen no tsūtatsu ga todoita.","german":"A circular on cost reduction arrived from the head office to all branches.","furigana":"ほんしゃ|ぜんしてん|けいひさくげん|つうたつ|とど"},{"japanese":"文部科学省は各学校に通達を出し、熱中症対策を求めた。","romaji":"Monbu kagakushō wa kaku gakkō ni tsūtatsu o dashi, netchūshō taisaku o motometa.","german":"The Ministry of Education, Culture, Sports, Science and Technology issued a notice to all schools, calling for heatstroke countermeasures.","furigana":"もんぶかがくしょう|かくがっこう|つうたつ|だ|ねっちゅうしょうたいさく|もと"}],"notes":"通達 is an official \"instruction\" or \"circular\" from a higher to subordinate bodies, for example from a ministry to authorities and schools: 通達を出す, 通達に従う, 国税庁の通達. In companies, too, communications from the head office to branches are called 通達. A general notice to citizens is rather called 通知, a simple notification 連絡. The old meaning \"to be well versed in\" is rare today.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"判決文が公開され、法律家の間で議論を呼んだ。","romaji":"Hanketsubun ga kōkai sare, hōritsuka no aida de giron o yonda.","german":"The written judgment was published and sparked discussion among legal experts.","furigana":"はんけつぶん|こうかい|ほうりつか|あいだ|ぎろん|よ"},{"japanese":"裁判長が判決文を読み上げた。","romaji":"Saibanchō ga hanketsubun o yomiageta.","german":"The presiding judge read out the judgment.","furigana":"さいばんちょう|はんけつぶん|よ|あ"},{"japanese":"判決文は百ページを超える長さだった。","romaji":"Hanketsubun wa hyaku pēji o koeru nagasa datta.","german":"The written judgment was over a hundred pages long.","furigana":"はんけつぶん|ひゃく|こ|なが"}],"notes":"判決文 is the written judgment with operative part and reasoning, whereas 判決 means the judgment as a decision. One says 判決文を読み上げる, 判決文の要旨 \"summary of the judgment\" or 判決文が公開される. The operative part of the judgment is called 主文; in death sentences it is often read out only after the reasoning (主文後回し). The reasoning alone is called 判決理由.","levelNote":"Compound: 判決 (N1) + 文 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"行政機関の効率化が求められている。","romaji":"Gyōsei kikan no kōritsuka ga motomerarete iru.","german":"Greater efficiency in administrative agencies is being demanded.","furigana":"ぎょうせいきかん|こうりつか|もと"},{"japanese":"この情報は行政機関の間で共有されている。","romaji":"Kono jōhō wa gyōsei kikan no aida de kyōyū sarete iru.","german":"This information is shared among administrative agencies.","furigana":"じょうほう|ぎょうせいきかん|あいだ|きょうゆう"},{"japanese":"裁判所は行政機関ではなく、司法機関だ。","romaji":"Saibansho wa gyōsei kikan de wa naku, shihō kikan da.","german":"Courts are not administrative agencies but judicial organs.","furigana":"さいばんしょ|ぎょうせいきかん|しほうきかん"}],"notes":"行政機関 is the collective term for state \"administrative agencies\" such as ministries, offices and agencies, and in a wider sense also local government administrations. It stands in contrast to 立法機関 (parliament) and 司法機関 (courts). In everyday speech, one says 役所 or 市役所 for the local office, and in the news 官庁 or 省庁 for the ministries; 当局 means \"the authorities concerned\".","levelNote":"Compound: 行政 (N1) + 機関 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"中央官庁の再編が行われた。","romaji":"Chūō kanchō no saihen ga okonawareta.","german":"A restructuring of the central government agencies was carried out.","furigana":"ちゅうおうかんちょう|さいへん|おこな"},{"japanese":"霞が関には中央官庁が集まっている。","romaji":"Kasumigaseki ni wa chūō kanchō ga atsumatte iru.","german":"Central government ministries are concentrated in Kasumigaseki.","furigana":"かすみ|せき|ちゅうおうかんちょう|あつ"},{"japanese":"大学卒業後、彼は官庁に就職した。","romaji":"Daigaku sotsugyōgo, kare wa kanchō ni shūshoku shita.","german":"After graduating from university, he took a job in a government office.","furigana":"だいがくそつぎょうご|かれ|かんちょう|しゅうしょく"}],"notes":"官庁 denotes state \"government offices\", especially the ministries: 中央官庁 are the central agencies in Tokyo's government district Kasumigaseki, 官庁街 a government quarter. Very common is 官公庁 for national and local government offices together. In everyday speech 役所 is used for the local office; 行政機関 is the legal collective term. The word sounds somewhat formal and slightly bureaucratic.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"省庁間の連携が強化された。","romaji":"Shōchō kan no renkei ga kyōka sareta.","german":"Cooperation between ministries and agencies was strengthened.","furigana":"しょうちょうかん|れんけい|きょうか"},{"japanese":"関係省庁と協議したうえで方針を決める。","romaji":"Kankei shōchō to kyōgi shita ue de hōshin o kimeru.","german":"The policy will be decided after consulting the relevant ministries and agencies.","furigana":"かんけいしょうちょう|きょうぎ|ほうしん|き"},{"japanese":"2001年に中央省庁が大きく再編された。","romaji":"Nisen'ichi-nen ni chūō shōchō ga ōkiku saihen sareta.","german":"In 2001, the central ministries and agencies were extensively reorganized.","furigana":"ねん|ちゅうおうしょうちょう|おお|さいへん"}],"notes":"省庁 is the collective term for the central government bodies: the ministries (〜省 like 財務省) and the agencies attached to them (〜庁 like 気象庁 or 金融庁). Typical are 中央省庁 \"central ministries and agencies\", 関係省庁 \"the ministries concerned\", 省庁間 \"between ministries\" and 省庁再編 \"reorganization of the ministries\" (major reform in 2001). In press jargon, 霞が関 often stands for the ministerial bureaucracy. The word belongs to the language of news and administration.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"全国の都道府県から代表が集まった。","romaji":"Zenkoku no todōfuken kara daihyō ga atsumatta.","german":"Representatives from all the prefectures of the country gathered.","furigana":"ぜんこく|とどうふけん|だいひょう|あつ"},{"japanese":"都道府県別の人口ランキングを調べてみた。","romaji":"Todōfuken-betsu no jinkō rankingu o shirabete mita.","german":"I looked up the population ranking by prefecture.","furigana":"とどうふけんべつ|じんこう|しら"},{"japanese":"日本には四十七の都道府県がある。","romaji":"Nihon ni wa yonjūnana no todōfuken ga aru.","german":"Japan has forty-seven prefectures.","furigana":"にほん|よんじゅうなな|とどうふけん"}],"notes":"都道府県 combines the 47 prefectures of Japan, naming all four types: 1 都 (東京都), 1 道 (北海道), 2 府 (大阪府, 京都府) and 43 県. The word is found mainly in statistics and administrative language: 都道府県別 \"by prefecture\", 都道府県知事 \"prefectural governor\", 都道府県庁所在地 \"prefectural capital\". The level below is the 市町村; together they form the 地方自治体 \"local governments\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"市町村合併により自治体の数が減少した。","romaji":"Shichōson gappei ni yori jichitai no kazu ga genshō shita.","german":"The number of municipalities decreased due to municipal mergers.","furigana":"しちょうそんがっぺい|じちたい|かず|げんしょう"},{"japanese":"ごみの出し方は市町村によって異なる。","romaji":"Gomi no dashikata wa shichōson ni yotte kotonaru.","german":"How to put out garbage differs depending on the municipality.","furigana":"だ|かた|しちょうそん|こと"},{"japanese":"引っ越したら、新しい市町村の役所で転入届を出す。","romaji":"Hikkoshitara, atarashii shichōson no yakusho de tennyū todoke o dasu.","german":"After moving, you submit a move-in notification at the office of your new municipality.","furigana":"ひ|こ|あたら|しちょうそん|やくしょ|てんにゅうとどけ|だ"}],"notes":"市町村 refers to the municipalities of the lowest administrative level: 市 \"city\", 町 \"town\" and 村 \"village\". Forms usually say 市区町村, because the 23 special wards (区) of Tokyo are included as well. Common combinations are 市町村合併 \"municipal merger\", 市町村長 \"mayor\" and 〜は市町村によって異なる \"differs from municipality to municipality\". The higher level is the 都道府県.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"事業開始には行政の許認可が必要だ。","romaji":"Jigyō kaishi ni wa gyōsei no kyoninka ga hitsuyō da.","german":"Government authorization is required to start a business.","furigana":"じぎょうかいし|ぎょうせい|きょにんか|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"許認可の手続きに半年もかかった。","romaji":"Kyoninka no tetsuzuki ni hantoshi mo kakatta.","german":"The permit procedure took as long as six months.","furigana":"きょにんか|てつづ|はんとし"},{"japanese":"官庁は許認可権を通じて業界に強い影響力を持つ。","romaji":"Kanchō wa kyoninkaken o tsūjite gyōkai ni tsuyoi eikyōryoku o motsu.","german":"Government offices hold strong influence over the industry through their authority to grant permits.","furigana":"かんちょう|きょにんかけん|つう|ぎょうかい|つよ|えいきょうりょく|も"}],"notes":"許認可 is a contraction of 許可 \"permission\" and 認可 \"official approval\" and refers collectively to all authorizations that an authority must grant. Typical: 許認可を得る/取る \"to obtain permits\", 許認可を申請する, 許認可権 \"authority to grant permits\", 許認可手続き. It is purely an administrative and newspaper word; it is hardly used as a する-verb, and for a concrete \"to approve\" one says 許可する or 認可する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"会計監査で不正経理が発覚した。","romaji":"Kaikei kansa de fusei keiri ga hakkaku shita.","german":"Accounting fraud was uncovered in the financial audit.","furigana":"かいけいかんさ|ふせいけいり|はっかく"},{"japanese":"毎年、外部の監査法人の監査を受けている。","romaji":"Maitoshi, gaibu no kansa hōjin no kansa o ukete iru.","german":"We are audited every year by an external audit firm.","furigana":"まいとし|がいぶ|かんさほうじん|かんさ|う"},{"japanese":"監査役が取締役の業務を監査する。","romaji":"Kansayaku ga torishimariyaku no gyōmu o kansa suru.","german":"The corporate auditor audits the business conduct of the directors.","furigana":"かんさやく|とりしまりやく|ぎょうむ|かんさ"}],"notes":"監査 is the examining control of accounts or management, especially in companies and organizations: 会計監査 \"financial audit\", 内部監査 \"internal audit\", 監査法人 \"audit firm\", 監査役 \"corporate auditor\". One says 監査を受ける \"to be audited\" and 〜を監査する \"to audit\". To be distinguished are 検査 \"examination, inspection\" (of things, health) and 審査 \"screening, review\" (of applications, candidates); the state audit of the budget is called 会計検査.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"会計検査院が国費の使途を調べた。","romaji":"Kaikei Kensain ga kokuhi no shito o shirabeta.","german":"The Board of Audit examined how public funds were used.","furigana":"かいけいけんさいん|こくひ|しと|しら"},{"japanese":"補助金の使い方について、市が会計検査を受けた。","romaji":"Hojokin no tsukaikata ni tsuite, shi ga kaikei kensa o uketa.","german":"The city underwent an audit concerning the use of subsidies.","furigana":"ほじょきん|つか|かた|し|かいけいけんさ|う"},{"japanese":"会計検査で不適切な支出が指摘された。","romaji":"Kaikei kensa de futekisetsu na shishutsu ga shiteki sareta.","german":"Inappropriate expenditure was pointed out in the audit.","furigana":"かいけいけんさ|ふてきせつ|ししゅつ|してき"}],"notes":"会計検査 in Japan is above all the examination of state revenue and expenditure by the 会計検査院 \"Board of Audit\"; municipalities and recipients of subsidies must also undergo an audit (会計検査を受ける). For audits in private companies, on the other hand, one says 会計監査. It often appears with 指摘 \"pointing out\": 会計検査で〜が指摘された. A technical term from administration and the news.","levelNote":"Compound: 会計 (N3) + 検査 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"来年度の予算編成作業が始まった。","romaji":"Rainendo no yosan hensei sagyō ga hajimatta.","german":"Work on compiling next fiscal year's budget has begun.","furigana":"らいねんど|よさんへんせいさぎょう|はじ"},{"japanese":"財務省は年末に向けて予算編成を進めている。","romaji":"Zaimushō wa nenmatsu ni mukete yosan hensei o susumete iru.","german":"The Ministry of Finance is pushing ahead with budget compilation toward the end of the year.","furigana":"ざいむしょう|ねんまつ|む|よさんへんせい|すす"},{"japanese":"市の予算編成では福祉予算が焦点となった。","romaji":"Shi no yosan hensei de wa fukushi yosan ga shōten to natta.","german":"In the city's budget compilation, welfare spending became the focus.","furigana":"し|よさんへんせい|ふくしよさん|しょうてん"}],"notes":"予算編成 is the drawing up of the budget plan; at the national level, the 財務省 prepares the draft, which the Cabinet approves at the end of the year, but prefectures and municipalities also carry out 予算編成. Typical combinations: 予算編成方針 \"budget guidelines\", 予算編成作業, 予算編成を進める. As a verb one does not say 予算編成する, but 予算を編成する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"歳入の大半は税収で賄われている。","romaji":"Sainyū no taihan wa zeishū de makanawarete iru.","german":"Most of the annual revenue is covered by tax receipts.","furigana":"さいにゅう|たいはん|ぜいしゅう|まかな"},{"japanese":"景気の悪化で、国の歳入が大きく減った。","romaji":"Keiki no akka de, kuni no sainyū ga ōkiku hetta.","german":"Due to the economic downturn, national revenue has fallen sharply.","furigana":"けいき|あっか|くに|さいにゅう|おお|へ"},{"japanese":"市の歳入と歳出のバランスが崩れている。","romaji":"Shi no sainyū to saishutsu no baransu ga kuzurete iru.","german":"The balance between the city's revenues and expenditures has been upset.","furigana":"し|さいにゅう|さいしゅつ|くず"}],"notes":"歳入 is the annual income of a state or municipality in its budget; the counterpart is 歳出 (\"budget expenditure\"). Typical combinations: 歳入が増える/減る, 歳入不足, 歳入を見込む, 国の歳入. For individuals or companies one says 収入 or 売上, not 歳入. 税収 means only the tax revenue, which makes up the largest part of 歳入.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"社会保障費が歳出の最大項目となっている。","romaji":"Shakai hoshōhi ga saishutsu no saidai kōmoku to natte iru.","german":"Social security costs are the largest item of government spending.","furigana":"しゃかいほしょうひ|さいしゅつ|さいだいこうもく"},{"japanese":"政府は歳出削減に取り組んでいる。","romaji":"Seifu wa saishutsu sakugen ni torikunde iru.","german":"The government is working on cutting expenditure.","furigana":"せいふ|さいしゅつさくげん|と|く"},{"japanese":"歳入が歳出を大きく下回り、赤字が続いている。","romaji":"Sainyū ga saishutsu o ōkiku shitamawari, akaji ga tsuzuite iru.","german":"Revenue falls far short of expenditure, and the deficit continues.","furigana":"さいにゅう|さいしゅつ|おお|したまわ|あかじ|つづ"}],"notes":"歳出 is the expenditure of a fiscal year by the state or a local government; the counterpart is 歳入 \"budget revenue\". Typical: 歳出削減 \"expenditure cuts\", 歳出拡大, 歳出規模 \"expenditure volume\", 歳出総額. For expenditure of companies or individuals one uses 支出 or 出費, not 歳出. A word from budget and economic reports.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"選挙区の区割りが見直された。","romaji":"Senkyoku no kuwari ga minaosareta.","german":"The boundaries of the electoral districts were reviewed.","furigana":"せんきょく|くわ|みなお"},{"japanese":"彼は地元の選挙区から立候補した。","romaji":"Kare wa jimoto no senkyoku kara rikkōho shita.","german":"He ran from his home electoral district.","furigana":"かれ|じもと|せんきょく|りっこうほ"},{"japanese":"小選挙区では一人しか当選できない。","romaji":"Shōsenkyoku de wa hitori shika tōsen dekinai.","german":"In a single-member district, only one person can win.","furigana":"しょうせんきょく|ひとり|とうせん"}],"notes":"選挙区 is the electoral district in which candidates run. In the Japanese House of Representatives, one distinguishes 小選挙区 \"single-member district\" and 比例代表 \"proportional representation\"; the redrawing of boundaries is called 区割り. Typical are 地元の選挙区 \"home district\", 〜区から立候補する/出馬する and 選挙区を回る \"to tour the district\". The word 区 alone, by contrast, usually means \"city ward\".","levelNote":"Compound: 選挙 (N4) + 区 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"農村地域は保守政党の票田とされてきた。","romaji":"Nōson chiiki wa hoshu seitō no hyōden to sarete kita.","german":"Rural areas have been regarded as the electoral stronghold of conservative parties.","furigana":"のうそんちいき|ほしゅせいとう|ひょうでん"},{"japanese":"都市部の大票田をめぐって各党が争った。","romaji":"Toshibu no daihyōden o megutte kakutō ga arasotta.","german":"The parties competed over the large pool of voters in urban areas.","furigana":"としぶ|だいひょうでん|かくとう|あらそ"},{"japanese":"野党は与党の票田を切り崩そうとしている。","romaji":"Yatō wa yotō no hyōden o kirikuzusō to shite iru.","german":"The opposition is trying to break into the ruling party's strongholds.","furigana":"やとう|よとう|ひょうでん|き|くず"}],"notes":"票田 literally means \"vote rice field\": an area or group from which a party reliably \"harvests\" votes. Typical are 大票田 \"large voter reservoir\" (e.g. big cities), 票田を固める \"to secure the core electorate\", 票田を切り崩す \"to break into rivals' strongholds\" and 〜の票田とされる. A word of political reporting, rather written.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"候補者の得票数が発表された。","romaji":"Kōhosha no tokuhyōsū ga happyō sareta.","german":"The candidates' vote counts were announced.","furigana":"こうほしゃ|とくひょうすう|はっぴょう"},{"japanese":"彼女は前回を大きく上回る得票率で当選した。","romaji":"Kanojo wa zenkai o ōkiku uwamawaru tokuhyōritsu de tōsen shita.","german":"She was elected with a much higher share of the vote than last time.","furigana":"かのじょ|ぜんかい|おお|うわまわ|とくひょうりつ|とうせん"},{"japanese":"その候補は約三万票を得票した。","romaji":"Sono kōho wa yaku sanmanpyō o tokuhyō shita.","german":"The candidate received about 30,000 votes.","furigana":"こうほ|やくさんまんぴょう|とくひょう"}],"notes":"得票 are the votes that a candidate or party receives, not to be confused with 投票 \"casting a vote, voting\". Most common in compounds: 得票数 \"number of votes\", 得票率 \"share of the vote\". As a する-verb one says 〜票を得票する \"to receive ... votes\"; more colloquial is 〜票を取る or 〜票を獲得する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"選挙の公示日が決定された。","romaji":"Senkyo no kōjibi ga kettei sareta.","german":"The official announcement day of the election was decided.","furigana":"せんきょ|こうじび|けってい"},{"japanese":"衆議院選挙が公示され、選挙戦が始まった。","romaji":"Shūgiin senkyo ga kōji sare, senkyosen ga hajimatta.","german":"The House of Representatives election was officially announced, and the campaign began.","furigana":"しゅうぎいんせんきょ|こうじ|せんきょせん|はじ"},{"japanese":"国土交通省が今年の公示地価を発表した。","romaji":"Kokudo Kōtsūshō ga kotoshi no kōji chika o happyō shita.","german":"The Ministry of Land, Infrastructure, Transport and Tourism announced this year's official land prices.","furigana":"こくどこうつうしょう|ことし|こうじちか|はっぴょう"}],"notes":"公示 is an official public announcement. In election law, it refers specifically to the official proclamation of elections to the House of Representatives and House of Councillors; the official campaign begins afterward. For local and prefectural elections, 告示 is said instead. Other fixed combinations: 公示日 \"announcement day\", 公示地価 \"officially announced land price\". Mostly in the passive: 〜が公示される.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新人議員が党首選に立候補した。","romaji":"Shinjin giin ga tōshusen ni rikkōho shita.","german":"A new lawmaker ran for party leader.","furigana":"しんじんぎいん|とうしゅせん|りっこうほ"},{"japanese":"クラス委員に立候補する人はいませんか。","romaji":"Kurasu iin ni rikkōho suru hito wa imasen ka.","german":"Is there anyone who will run for class representative?","furigana":"いいん|りっこうほ|ひと"},{"japanese":"この都市はオリンピックの開催地に立候補している。","romaji":"Kono toshi wa Orinpikku no kaisaichi ni rikkōho shite iru.","german":"This city is bidding to host the Olympics.","furigana":"とし|かいさいち|りっこうほ"}],"notes":"立候補する is intransitive; the office or election takes に: 選挙に立候補する, 市長に立候補する. It is also used loosely for \"to volunteer\" (学級委員に立候補する) and for cities bidding as host (開催地に立候補する). In the press, 出馬する often appears as a synonym. The person is called 候補者 or 立候補者; if one is proposed by others, that is 推薦.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"候補者が全国各地を遊説して回った。","romaji":"Kōhosha ga zenkoku kakuchi o yūzei shite mawatta.","german":"The candidate traveled around the whole country campaigning.","furigana":"こうほしゃ|ぜんこくかくち|ゆうぜい|まわ"},{"japanese":"首相は遊説先の大阪で演説した。","romaji":"Shushō wa yūzeisaki no Ōsaka de enzetsu shita.","german":"The prime minister gave a speech in Osaka, a stop on his campaign tour.","furigana":"しゅしょう|ゆうぜいさき|おおさか|えんぜつ"},{"japanese":"党首が地方遊説に出発した。","romaji":"Tōshu ga chihō yūzei ni shuppatsu shita.","german":"The party leader set off on a campaign tour of the regions.","furigana":"とうしゅ|ちほうゆうぜい|しゅっぱつ"}],"notes":"遊説 is read ゆうぜい, not ゆうせつ. It refers to a politician traveling around to give speeches in various places, especially during a campaign. Typical: 各地を遊説する, 遊説して回る, 遊説先 \"stop on a campaign tour\", 全国遊説, 地方遊説. A single speech on the street is called 街頭演説.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"首相が審議会に制度改革を諮問した。","romaji":"Shushō ga shingikai ni seido kaikaku o shimon shita.","german":"The prime minister consulted the advisory council on system reform.","furigana":"しゅしょう|しんぎかい|せいどかいかく|しもん"},{"japanese":"この委員会は首相の諮問機関として設置された。","romaji":"Kono iinkai wa shushō no shimon kikan to shite setchi sareta.","german":"This committee was established as an advisory body to the prime minister.","furigana":"いいんかい|しゅしょう|しもんきかん|せっち"},{"japanese":"市長の諮問を受けて、審議会が議論を始めた。","romaji":"Shichō no shimon o ukete, shingikai ga giron o hajimeta.","german":"At the mayor's request, the council began deliberations.","furigana":"しちょう|しもん|う|しんぎかい|ぎろん|はじ"}],"notes":"諮問 is the formal request by an authority or official to an advisory body for an opinion: AがBに〜を諮問する. The answer to it is called 答申; the body consulted is a 諮問機関, often a 審議会. A purely administrative word; in everyday speech one says 相談する or 意見を求める.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"審議会が教育改革に関する答申を提出した。","romaji":"Shingikai ga kyōiku kaikaku ni kansuru tōshin o teishutsu shita.","german":"The council submitted a report on education reform.","furigana":"しんぎかい|きょういくかいかく|かん|とうしん|ていしゅつ"},{"japanese":"審議会は年内に最終答申をまとめる予定だ。","romaji":"Shingikai wa nennai ni saishū tōshin o matomeru yotei da.","german":"The council plans to compile its final report within the year.","furigana":"しんぎかい|ねんない|さいしゅうとうしん|よてい"},{"japanese":"委員会は大臣に対し、制度の廃止を答申した。","romaji":"Iinkai wa daijin ni taishi, seido no haishi o tōshin shita.","german":"The committee recommended to the minister that the system be abolished.","furigana":"いいんかい|だいじん|たい|せいど|はいし|とうしん"}],"notes":"答申 is the reply of an advisory body to a 諮問, i.e. the official report or recommendation to the authority that made the request. Typical: 答申を出す/提出する/まとめる, 中間答申 \"interim report\", 最終答申 \"final report\", 〜に〜を答申する. A formal administrative word; a general report is 報告.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人事院が公務員の給与引き上げを勧告した。","romaji":"Jinjiin ga kōmuin no kyūyo hikiage o kankoku shita.","german":"The National Personnel Authority recommended a pay raise for civil servants.","furigana":"じんじいん|こうむいん|きゅうよひ|あ|かんこく"},{"japanese":"行政は違反業者に改善勧告を出した。","romaji":"Gyōsei wa ihan gyōsha ni kaizen kankoku o dashita.","german":"The administration formally ordered the offending company to make improvements.","furigana":"ぎょうせい|いはんぎょうしゃ|かいぜんかんこく|だ"},{"japanese":"専門家委員会は、その薬の使用を中止するよう勧告した。","romaji":"Senmonka iinkai wa, sono kusuri no shiyō o chūshi suru yō kankoku shita.","german":"The expert committee recommended discontinuing use of the drug.","furigana":"せんもんかいいんかい|くすり|しよう|ちゅうし|かんこく"}],"notes":"勧告 is an official recommendation or call to do something, usually issued by an authority or a body. It is often not legally binding, but carries considerably more weight than 助言 \"advice\" or アドバイス. Typical: 〜を勧告する, 〜するよう勧告する, 勧告に従う, 改善勧告, 人事院勧告 (annual pay recommendation for civil servants).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大臣が所管部署に訓令を発した。","romaji":"Daijin ga shokan busho ni kunrei o hasshita.","german":"The minister issued a directive to the department in charge.","furigana":"だいじん|しょかんぶしょ|くんれい|はっ"},{"japanese":"外務省は大使に対し、交渉を中断するよう訓令した。","romaji":"Gaimushō wa taishi ni taishi, kōshō o chūdan suru yō kunrei shita.","german":"The Ministry of Foreign Affairs instructed the ambassador to suspend the negotiations.","furigana":"がいむしょう|たいし|たい|こうしょう|ちゅうだん|くんれい"},{"japanese":"この規程は省の訓令として定められている。","romaji":"Kono kitei wa shō no kunrei to shite sadamerarete iru.","german":"This regulation is established as an internal directive of the ministry.","furigana":"きてい|しょう|くんれい|さだ"}],"notes":"訓令 is an instruction that a superior authority addresses to subordinate bodies or officials; it takes effect only within the administration, not toward citizens. In diplomacy, it refers to the government's instructions to an envoy. The word is also known from 訓令式, the officially established romanization system (e.g. si instead of shi). Formal and rare outside administration and diplomacy; more general are 指示 or 命令.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"行政の裁量権の範囲が問題となった。","romaji":"Gyōsei no sairyōken no han'i ga mondai to natta.","german":"The extent of the administration's discretionary authority became an issue.","furigana":"ぎょうせい|さいりょうけん|はんい|もんだい"},{"japanese":"細かい点は担当者の裁量に任せる。","romaji":"Komakai ten wa tantōsha no sairyō ni makaseru.","german":"We will leave the details to the discretion of the person in charge.","furigana":"こま|てん|たんとうしゃ|さいりょう|まか"},{"japanese":"裁量労働制では、働く時間を自分で決められる。","romaji":"Sairyō rōdōsei de wa, hataraku jikan o jibun de kimerareru.","german":"Under the discretionary work system, you can decide your working hours yourself.","furigana":"さいりょうろうどうせい|はたら|じかん|じぶん|き"}],"notes":"裁量 is the latitude to decide by one's own judgment. Typical: 〜の裁量に任せる/委ねる \"to leave to someone's discretion\", 〜の裁量で決める, 裁量権 \"discretionary authority\", 自由裁量 \"free discretion\" and 裁量労働制 \"discretionary work system\". As a する-verb it is rare. The word comes from administration and law, but is also common in working life.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この事件は警視庁の管轄に属する。","romaji":"Kono jiken wa Keishichō no kankatsu ni zoku suru.","german":"This case falls under the jurisdiction of the Metropolitan Police Department.","furigana":"じけん|けいしちょう|かんかつ|ぞく"},{"japanese":"その件はうちの管轄外なので、別の窓口に行ってください。","romaji":"Sono ken wa uchi no kankatsugai na node, betsu no madoguchi ni itte kudasai.","german":"That matter is outside our jurisdiction, so please go to another counter.","furigana":"けん|かんかつがい|べつ|まどぐち|い"},{"japanese":"住所を管轄する税務署に申告書を提出する。","romaji":"Jūsho o kankatsu suru zeimusho ni shinkokusho o teishutsu suru.","german":"The tax return is submitted to the tax office with jurisdiction over your address.","furigana":"じゅうしょ|かんかつ|ぜいむしょ|しんこくしょ|ていしゅつ"}],"notes":"管轄 is the jurisdiction of an authority, court or police, often for a particular area: 〜の管轄だ, 〜の管轄に属する, 管轄外 \"outside one's jurisdiction\", 管轄区域, 〜を管轄する税務署/警察署. Related is 所管, which rather stresses the subject-matter responsibility for a field of tasks (e.g. of a ministry). In everyday speech one often hears うちの管轄じゃない \"that's not our department\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この分野は厚生労働省の所管である。","romaji":"Kono bun'ya wa Kōsei Rōdōshō no shokan de aru.","german":"This field is under the jurisdiction of the Ministry of Health, Labour and Welfare.","furigana":"ぶんや|こうせいろうどうしょう|しょかん"},{"japanese":"この法律は文部科学省が所管している。","romaji":"Kono hōritsu wa Monbu Kagakushō ga shokan shite iru.","german":"The Ministry of Education, Culture, Sports, Science and Technology is in charge of this law.","furigana":"ほうりつ|もんぶかがくしょう|しょかん"},{"japanese":"詳しくは所管の官庁に問い合わせてください。","romaji":"Kuwashiku wa shokan no kanchō ni toiawasete kudasai.","german":"For details, please inquire at the competent government office.","furigana":"くわ|しょかん|かんちょう|と|あ"}],"notes":"所管 is the subject-matter responsibility of an authority for a law, a task or an institution: 〜省の所管である, 〜を所管する, 所管官庁 \"competent authority\", 所管事項. Unlike 管轄, which is often thought of spatially (the territory of a court or police station), 所管 is about departments and subject areas. A formal administrative word.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"事業全体を統轄する部署が設置された。","romaji":"Jigyō zentai o tōkatsu suru busho ga setchi sareta.","german":"A department to oversee the entire project was established.","furigana":"じぎょうぜんたい|とうかつ|ぶしょ|せっち"},{"japanese":"営業本部長が全国の支店を統轄している。","romaji":"Eigyō honbuchō ga zenkoku no shiten o tōkatsu shite iru.","german":"The head of sales supervises all the branches in the country.","furigana":"えいぎょうほんぶちょう|ぜんこく|してん|とうかつ"},{"japanese":"各省庁は内閣の統轄の下に置かれている。","romaji":"Kakushōchō wa naikaku no tōkatsu no moto ni okarete iru.","german":"Each ministry is placed under the overall control of the Cabinet.","furigana":"かくしょうちょう|ないかく|とうかつ|もと|お"}],"notes":"統轄 means to direct and supervise several subordinate units as a whole: 〜を統轄する, 〜の統轄の下に置く. The spelling 統轄 is typical of legal and administrative texts; in general usage and in companies, 統括 is usually written, which can mean both \"to bring together\" and \"to direct\" (統括部長 etc.). In conversation, the word sounds very formal.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい駐日大使が着任した。","romaji":"Atarashii chūnichi taishi ga chakunin shita.","german":"The new ambassador to Japan took up his post.","furigana":"あたら|ちゅうにちたいし|ちゃくにん"},{"japanese":"大使館でビザを申請した。","romaji":"Taishikan de biza o shinsei shita.","german":"I applied for a visa at the embassy.","furigana":"たいしかん|しんせい"},{"japanese":"人気女優が観光親善大使に任命された。","romaji":"Ninki joyū ga kankō shinzen taishi ni ninmei sareta.","german":"A popular actress was appointed tourism goodwill ambassador.","furigana":"にんきじょゆう|かんこうしんぜんたいし|にんめい"}],"notes":"大使 is the ambassador, the highest diplomatic representative of a state; the embassy is called 大使館. The country is indicated with 駐〜: 駐日大使 \"ambassador to Japan\", 駐米大使 \"ambassador to the USA\"; formally 特命全権大使. Below it in rank stands the 公使 \"minister (envoy)\", while 領事 handles consular duties. Figuratively there are 親善大使 \"goodwill ambassador\" or 観光大使.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"領事館で在外邦人の保護業務が行われている。","romaji":"Ryōjikan de zaigai hōjin no hogo gyōmu ga okonawarete iru.","german":"Protection services for Japanese nationals abroad are carried out at the consulate.","furigana":"りょうじかん|ざいがいほうじん|ほごぎょうむ|おこな"},{"japanese":"パスポートをなくしたので、領事館に相談した。","romaji":"Pasupōto o nakushita node, ryōjikan ni sōdan shita.","german":"I had lost my passport, so I consulted the consulate.","furigana":"りょうじかん|そうだん"},{"japanese":"彼はニューヨークの総領事として赴任した。","romaji":"Kare wa Nyūyōku no sōryōji to shite funin shita.","german":"He took up his post as consul general in New York.","furigana":"かれ|そうりょうじ|ふにん"}],"notes":"領事 is the consul who, abroad, mainly handles administrative tasks for his own country's citizens (passports, visas, help in emergencies). Common are 領事館 \"consulate\", 総領事 \"consul general\", 総領事館 and 在〜総領事館. In contrast to the 大使 \"ambassador\", who represents the state politically in the capital, consulates are often located in large cities outside the capital.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"平和主義が戦後日本の国是とされてきた。","romaji":"Heiwa shugi ga sengo Nihon no kokuze to sarete kita.","german":"Pacifism has been regarded as the fundamental national principle of postwar Japan.","furigana":"へいわしゅぎ|せんごにほん|こくぜ"},{"japanese":"その国は永世中立を国是としている。","romaji":"Sono kuni wa eisei chūritsu o kokuze to shite iru.","german":"That country has made perpetual neutrality its national principle.","furigana":"くに|えいせいちゅうりつ|こくぜ"},{"japanese":"非核三原則は日本の国是だと言われる。","romaji":"Hikaku sangensoku wa Nihon no kokuze da to iwareru.","german":"The three non-nuclear principles are said to be a national principle of Japan.","furigana":"ひかくさんげんそく|にほん|こくぜ|い"}],"notes":"国是 is the fundamental, long-term guiding principle of a state's policy. Typical are 〜を国是とする \"to make ... one's national principle\" and 〜は〜の国是だ. Unlike 国策 \"concrete national policy\", 国是 means an immovable principle. Analogous terms are 社是 \"company motto\" and 党是 \"party principle\". Very formal, typical of editorials and speeches.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"国権の最高機関は国会である。","romaji":"Kokken no saikō kikan wa kokkai de aru.","german":"The highest organ of state power is the Diet.","furigana":"こっけん|さいこうきかん|こっかい"},{"japanese":"憲法九条は、国権の発動たる戦争を放棄している。","romaji":"Kenpō kyūjō wa, kokken no hatsudō taru sensō o hōki shite iru.","german":"Article 9 of the Constitution renounces war as a sovereign right of the nation.","furigana":"けんぽうきゅうじょう|こっけん|はつどう|せんそう|ほうき"},{"japanese":"明治時代には国権の拡張を唱える人々がいた。","romaji":"Meiji jidai ni wa kokken no kakuchō o tonaeru hitobito ga ita.","german":"In the Meiji era, there were people who advocated expanding national power.","furigana":"めいじじだい|こっけん|かくちょう|とな|ひとびと"}],"notes":"国権 is the power of the state, i.e. the sovereign rights of a state. It is known above all from the Constitution: 国会は国権の最高機関である (Art. 41) and 国権の発動たる戦争 \"war as a sovereign right of the nation\" (Art. 9). Historically, in the Meiji era it stands opposed to 民権 \"people's rights\" (国権論 vs. 民権論). A very formal, legal-political word; in everyday speech one rather says 国の権力 or 主権.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"クーデター後に戒厳令が発令された。","romaji":"Kūdetā go ni kaigenrei ga hatsurei sareta.","german":"Martial law was declared after the coup.","furigana":"ご|かいげんれい|はつれい"},{"japanese":"政府は首都に戒厳令を敷いた。","romaji":"Seifu wa shuto ni kaigenrei o shiita.","german":"The government imposed martial law on the capital.","furigana":"せいふ|しゅと|かいげんれい|し"},{"japanese":"三か月後にようやく戒厳令が解除された。","romaji":"Sankagetsu-go ni yōyaku kaigenrei ga kaijo sareta.","german":"Martial law was finally lifted after three months.","furigana":"さん|げつご|かいげんれい|かいじょ"}],"notes":"戒厳令 is the decree by which the military takes over state authority in an area, i.e. the imposition of martial law. Typical verbs: 戒厳令を敷く (also 布く) \"to impose\", 戒厳令が発令される, 戒厳令を解除する \"to lift\", 戒厳令下で \"under martial law\". Not to be confused with 緊急事態宣言, a civil state of emergency without military rule (as during the coronavirus pandemic). A word from news and history.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"突然の政変により政局が混迷した。","romaji":"Totsuzen no seihen ni yori seikyoku ga konmei shita.","german":"A sudden political upheaval threw the political situation into confusion.","furigana":"とつぜん|せいへん|せいきょく|こんめい"},{"japanese":"その国では軍による政変が起きた。","romaji":"Sono kuni de wa gun ni yoru seihen ga okita.","german":"A military-led change of power occurred in that country.","furigana":"くに|ぐん|せいへん|お"},{"japanese":"政変で首相が退陣し、新政権が発足した。","romaji":"Seihen de shushō ga taijin shi, shin seiken ga hossoku shita.","german":"The prime minister resigned amid the political crisis, and a new government was launched.","furigana":"せいへん|しゅしょう|たいじん|しんせいけん|ほっそく"}],"notes":"政変 is a sudden change in power: from the fall of a cabinet, through a power struggle at court, to a coup. Typical: 政変が起きる/起こる, 政変で失脚する, 宮廷政変 \"palace revolt\". To be distinguished from this are 政局 \"political situation, power games\" and クーデター \"violent coup d'etat\", which is a form of 政変; 革命 means a fundamental overthrow of the system.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"独裁者が反対派の粛清を行った。","romaji":"Dokusaisha ga hantaiha no shukusei o okonatta.","german":"The dictator carried out a purge of the opposition.","furigana":"どくさいしゃ|はんたいは|しゅくせい|おこな"},{"japanese":"スターリンの大粛清で多くの人が処刑された。","romaji":"Sutārin no daishukusei de ōku no hito ga shokei sareta.","german":"Many people were executed in Stalin's Great Purge.","furigana":"だいしゅくせい|おお|ひと|しょけい"},{"japanese":"新しい指導者は、かつての側近を次々と粛清した。","romaji":"Atarashii shidōsha wa, katsute no sokkin o tsugitsugi to shukusei shita.","german":"The new leader purged his former close aides one after another.","furigana":"あたら|しどうしゃ|そっきん|つぎつぎ|しゅくせい"}],"notes":"粛清 is the (often bloody) purge in which a regime or party eliminates opponents and unwelcome members: 〜を粛清する, 〜が粛清される, 大粛清 \"the Great Purge\" under Stalin. Not to be confused with the identically pronounced 粛正 \"strict crackdown, enforcing discipline\", as in 綱紀粛正 \"restoring official discipline\". A word from history and political reporting.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"傀儡政権は大国の意向に従うだけだった。","romaji":"Kairai seiken wa taikoku no ikō ni shitagau dake datta.","german":"The puppet regime merely followed the will of the great power.","furigana":"かいらいせいけん|たいこく|いこう|したが"},{"japanese":"満州国は日本の傀儡国家だった。","romaji":"Manshūkoku wa Nihon no kairai kokka datta.","german":"Manchukuo was a puppet state of Japan.","furigana":"まんしゅうこく|にほん|かいらいこっか"},{"japanese":"新社長は前会長の傀儡にすぎないと言われている。","romaji":"Shin shachō wa zen kaichō no kairai ni suginai to iwarete iru.","german":"The new president is said to be nothing but a puppet of the former chairman.","furigana":"しんしゃちょう|ぜんかいちょう|かいらい|い"}],"notes":"傀儡 originally means \"puppet\", but is today used almost only figuratively: a person or government controlled by others. Typical are 傀儡政権 \"puppet government\", 傀儡国家 \"puppet state\" and 〜の傀儡にすぎない \"is merely a puppet of ...\". For the actual theater puppet one says あやつり人形 or マリオネット today. Derogatory and rather written.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"政敵を陥れるための謀略が暴露された。","romaji":"Seiteki o otoshiireru tame no bōryaku ga bakuro sareta.","german":"A plot to bring down a political rival was exposed.","furigana":"せいてき|おとしい|ぼうりゃく|ばくろ"},{"japanese":"敵の謀略にまんまとはまってしまった。","romaji":"Teki no bōryaku ni manma to hamatte shimatta.","german":"We fell completely into the enemy's trap.","furigana":"てき|ぼうりゃく"},{"japanese":"その事件は情報機関の謀略だったという説がある。","romaji":"Sono jiken wa jōhō kikan no bōryaku datta to iu setsu ga aru.","german":"There is a theory that the incident was a plot by the intelligence agency.","furigana":"じけん|じょうほうきかん|ぼうりゃく|せつ"}],"notes":"謀略 is a secretly devised plan to harm an opponent, especially in politics, war and intelligence work. Typical: 謀略をめぐらす \"to scheme\", 謀略にかかる/はまる \"to fall victim to a plot\", 謀略戦, 謀略説 \"conspiracy theory (about an event)\". Similar are 陰謀 \"conspiracy\" (more focused on secrecy) and 策略 \"ruse, trick\", which sounds more neutral.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"反対派を懐柔して法案を通過させた。","romaji":"Hantaiha o kaijū shite hōan o tsūka saseta.","german":"They won over the opposition and got the bill passed.","furigana":"はんたいは|かいじゅう|ほうあん|つうか"},{"japanese":"政府は補助金を約束して地元住民の懐柔を図った。","romaji":"Seifu wa hojokin o yakusoku shite jimoto jūmin no kaijū o hakatta.","german":"The government tried to win over local residents by promising subsidies.","furigana":"せいふ|ほじょきん|やくそく|じもとじゅうみん|かいじゅう|はか"},{"japanese":"上司はお菓子で部下を懐柔しようとしている。","romaji":"Jōshi wa okashi de buka o kaijū shiyō to shite iru.","german":"The boss is trying to win over his staff with sweets.","furigana":"じょうし|かし|ぶか|かいじゅう"}],"notes":"懐柔 means to bring someone over to one's side by concessions, favors or friendly accommodation, often with calculating intent: 〜を懐柔する, 懐柔策 \"conciliatory policy\", 懐柔を図る, 懐柔工作. In contrast to 説得 \"convincing with arguments\", it is about benefits rather than reasons; なだめる means only \"to calm, soothe\". Rather written, but it can also appear jokingly in everyday speech.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"政治家と利権の結びつきが問題視されている。","romaji":"Seijika to riken no musubitsuki ga mondaishi sarete iru.","german":"The ties between politicians and vested interests are seen as a problem.","furigana":"せいじか|りけん|むす|もんだいし"},{"japanese":"列強は中国で鉄道や鉱山の利権を争った。","romaji":"Rekkyō wa Chūgoku de tetsudō ya kōzan no riken o arasotta.","german":"The great powers competed over railway and mining concessions in China.","furigana":"れっきょう|ちゅうごく|てつどう|こうざん|りけん|あらそ"},{"japanese":"大型開発事業には利権が絡みやすい。","romaji":"Ōgata kaihatsu jigyō ni wa riken ga karamiyasui.","german":"Large development projects easily become entangled with vested interests.","furigana":"おおがたかいはつじぎょう|りけん|から"}],"notes":"利権 are rights or concessions that bring profit, such as 石油利権 \"oil concessions\". In domestic politics the word usually has a negative connotation: lucrative vested interests that politicians, officials and companies flock to. Typical: 利権を握る, 利権に群がる, 利権が絡む \"vested interests are involved\", 利権争い, 利権政治. Not to be confused with 権利 \"right\" - the same characters in reverse order.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"建設族議員が公共事業の予算拡大を主張した。","romaji":"Kensetsu zoku giin ga kōkyō jigyō no yosan kakudai o shuchō shita.","german":"Construction-tribe lawmakers argued for expanding the budget for public works.","furigana":"けんせつぞくぎいん|こうきょうじぎょう|よさんかくだい|しゅちょう"},{"japanese":"農林族議員の反対で改革が骨抜きにされた。","romaji":"Nōrin zoku giin no hantai de kaikaku ga honenuki ni sareta.","german":"The reform was watered down by the resistance of the agriculture-tribe lawmakers.","furigana":"のうりんぞくぎいん|はんたい|かいかく|ほねぬ"},{"japanese":"族議員と官僚の癒着が批判された。","romaji":"Zoku giin to kanryō no yuchaku ga hihan sareta.","german":"The collusion between policy-tribe lawmakers and bureaucrats was criticized.","furigana":"ぞくぎいん|かんりょう|ゆちゃく|ひはん"}],"notes":"族議員 are lawmakers who are closely tied to a particular ministry and its industry and represent its interests in parliament. They are formed with 〜族: 建設族, 道路族, 農林族, 厚労族 etc. The word sounds critical because it hints at clientelism and 癒着 \"collusion\" with the bureaucracy and business. Typical for political commentary in newspapers.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"事務次官が省内の事務を統括している。","romaji":"Jimu jikan ga shōnai no jimu o tōkatsu shite iru.","german":"The administrative vice-minister oversees the administrative affairs within the ministry.","furigana":"じむじかん|しょうない|じむ|とうかつ"},{"japanese":"彼は財務事務次官まで上り詰めた。","romaji":"Kare wa zaimu jimu jikan made noboritsumeta.","german":"He rose all the way to administrative vice-minister of finance.","furigana":"かれ|ざいむじむじかん|のぼ|つ"},{"japanese":"不祥事の責任を取って事務次官が辞任した。","romaji":"Fushōji no sekinin o totte jimu jikan ga jinin shita.","german":"The administrative vice-minister resigned, taking responsibility for the scandal.","furigana":"ふしょうじ|せきにん|と|じむじかん|じにん"}],"notes":"事務次官 is the highest career civil servant of a ministry and heads the administration under the minister - comparable to a permanent secretary. Unlike the 副大臣 and 政務官, who are appointed as politicians, the 事務次官 is at the top of the civil service career: 〜事務次官まで上り詰める. Often with the ministry's name: 財務事務次官, 外務事務次官. It is also shortened to 次官.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"政務官が大臣の代理で会見に臨んだ。","romaji":"Seimukan ga daijin no dairi de kaiken ni nozonda.","german":"The parliamentary secretary attended the press conference on behalf of the minister.","furigana":"せいむかん|だいじん|だいり|かいけん|のぞ"},{"japanese":"彼は当選二回で外務大臣政務官に起用された。","romaji":"Kare wa tōsen nikai de gaimu daijin seimukan ni kiyō sareta.","german":"He was appointed parliamentary secretary of foreign affairs after being elected just twice.","furigana":"かれ|とうせんにかい|がいむだいじんせいむかん|きよう"},{"japanese":"副大臣と政務官が被災地を視察した。","romaji":"Fukudaijin to seimukan ga hisaichi o shisatsu shita.","german":"The vice-minister and the parliamentary secretary inspected the disaster area.","furigana":"ふくだいじん|せいむかん|ひさいち|しさつ"}],"notes":"政務官 is the short form of 大臣政務官, a political post existing since 2001: lawmakers who support the minister in particular areas of responsibility. In the hierarchy: 大臣 > 副大臣 > 政務官. Unlike the 事務次官, the 政務官 is not a civil servant but a politician, often a younger lawmaker at the start of a government career. Typical: 〜大臣政務官に起用される/就任する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"審議会の委員が有識者から選任された。","romaji":"Shingikai no iin ga yūshikisha kara sennin sareta.","german":"The council's members were selected from experts.","furigana":"しんぎかい|いいん|ゆうしきしゃ|せんにん"},{"japanese":"審議会で新しい制度について議論が続いている。","romaji":"Shingikai de atarashii seido ni tsuite giron ga tsuzuite iru.","german":"Discussions about the new system continue in the council.","furigana":"しんぎかい|あたら|せいど|ぎろん|つづ"},{"japanese":"法制審議会が民法改正の要綱をまとめた。","romaji":"Hōsei Shingikai ga minpō kaisei no yōkō o matometa.","german":"The Legislative Council compiled an outline for the reform of the Civil Code.","furigana":"ほうせいしんぎかい|みんぽうかいせい|ようこう"}],"notes":"審議会 is an advisory council of experts and representatives of social groups established at ministries or municipalities. It receives a 諮問 \"request for advice\" and responds with a 答申 \"report\". Many have fixed names, such as 中央教育審議会 (abbreviated 中教審) or 法制審議会. Typical: 審議会に諮る, 審議会で議論する, 審議会の委員. A word from administration and the news.","levelNote":"Compound: 審議 (N1) + 会 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"首相の諮問機関が報告書をまとめた。","romaji":"Shushō no shimon kikan ga hōkokusho o matometa.","german":"The prime minister's advisory body compiled a report.","furigana":"しゅしょう|しもんきかん|ほうこくしょ"},{"japanese":"審議会は大臣の諮問機関であり、その答申に法的拘束力はない。","romaji":"Shingikai wa daijin no shimon kikan de ari, sono tōshin ni hōteki kōsokuryoku wa nai.","german":"The council is an advisory body to the minister, and its reports are not legally binding.","furigana":"しんぎかい|だいじん|しもんきかん|とうしん|ほうてきこうそくりょく"},{"japanese":"市は有識者による諮問機関を新たに設けた。","romaji":"Shi wa yūshikisha ni yoru shimon kikan o arata ni mōketa.","german":"The city set up a new advisory body made up of experts.","furigana":"し|ゆうしきしゃ|しもんきかん|あら|もう"}],"notes":"諮問機関 is a body that prepares opinions at the request (諮問) of an authority or official; the typical example is the 審議会. Its recommendations (答申, 提言) are not legally binding - unlike a 議決機関 \"decision-making body\". Common: 〜の諮問機関, 私的諮問機関 \"informal advisory group\" (e.g. of the prime minister). An administrative term.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"各地の出先機関で申請を受け付けている。","romaji":"Kakuchi no desaki kikan de shinsei o uketsukete iru.","german":"Applications are accepted at field offices in each region.","furigana":"かくち|でさききかん|しんせい|う|つ"},{"japanese":"ハローワークは厚生労働省の出先機関だ。","romaji":"Harōwāku wa Kōsei Rōdōshō no desaki kikan da.","german":"Hello Work is a field office of the Ministry of Health, Labour and Welfare.","furigana":"こうせいろうどうしょう|でさききかん"},{"japanese":"国の出先機関を廃止し、権限を地方に移すべきだという意見がある。","romaji":"Kuni no desaki kikan o haishi shi, kengen o chihō ni utsusu beki da to iu iken ga aru.","german":"Some argue that the state's regional field offices should be abolished and their powers transferred to local governments.","furigana":"くに|でさききかん|はいし|けんげん|ちほう|うつ|いけん"}],"notes":"出先機関 are regional branch offices of central government agencies, especially the ministries' offices in the regions (国の出先機関), e.g. employment offices or regional construction bureaus. 出先 alone means \"the place one is currently visiting\" or \"branch office\". In the debate on decentralization one often hears 出先機関の廃止/統合. An administrative word.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"独立行政法人の運営費が見直された。","romaji":"Dokuritsu gyōsei hōjin no un'eihi ga minaosareta.","german":"The operating costs of the independent administrative agency were reviewed.","furigana":"どくりつぎょうせいほうじん|うんえいひ|みなお"},{"japanese":"国際協力機構は独立行政法人の一つだ。","romaji":"Kokusai Kyōryoku Kikō wa dokuritsu gyōsei hōjin no hitotsu da.","german":"The Japan International Cooperation Agency is one of the independent administrative agencies.","furigana":"こくさいきょうりょくきこう|どくりつぎょうせいほうじん|ひと"},{"japanese":"彼は大学を卒業後、独立行政法人の研究所に就職した。","romaji":"Kare wa daigaku o sotsugyō-go, dokuritsu gyōsei hōjin no kenkyūjo ni shūshoku shita.","german":"After graduating from university, he got a job at a research institute run as an independent administrative agency.","furigana":"かれ|だいがく|そつぎょうご|どくりつぎょうせいほうじん|けんきゅうじょ|しゅうしょく"}],"notes":"独立行政法人 are institutions that were spun off from ministries in 2001 and are legally independent, carrying out public tasks with more autonomy, such as the development agency JICA, national museums or research institutes. Abbreviated, one says 独法. Typical: 独立行政法人化 \"conversion into an independent agency\", 〜は独立行政法人の一つだ. A term from administration and the business press.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"公益法人の認定基準が厳格化された。","romaji":"Kōeki hōjin no nintei kijun ga genkakuka sareta.","german":"The certification criteria for public-interest corporations were tightened.","furigana":"こうえきほうじん|にんていきじゅん|げんかくか"},{"japanese":"この美術館は公益財団法人が運営している。","romaji":"Kono bijutsukan wa kōeki zaidan hōjin ga un'ei shite iru.","german":"This art museum is run by a public interest incorporated foundation.","furigana":"びじゅつかん|こうえきざいだんほうじん|うんえい"},{"japanese":"公益法人には税制上の優遇措置がある。","romaji":"Kōeki hōjin ni wa zeiseijō no yūgū sochi ga aru.","german":"Public-interest corporations enjoy tax preferences.","furigana":"こうえきほうじん|ぜいせいじょう|ゆうぐうそち"}],"notes":"公益法人 are legal entities that serve the public good. Since the 2008 reform there are 公益社団法人 \"public interest incorporated association\" and 公益財団法人 \"public interest incorporated foundation\", which require a 公益認定 \"public-interest certification\" and are tax-privileged. To be distinguished are 一般社団法人, which lack this certification, and NPO法人. A term from law and administration.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"国会が国政調査権を行使して資料を要求した。","romaji":"Kokkai ga kokusei chōsaken o kōshi shite shiryō o yōkyū shita.","german":"The Diet exercised its investigative power and demanded documents.","furigana":"こっかい|こくせいちょうさけん|こうし|しりょう|ようきゅう"},{"japanese":"野党は国政調査権に基づき、関係者の証人喚問を求めた。","romaji":"Yatō wa kokusei chōsaken ni motozuki, kankeisha no shōnin kanmon o motometa.","german":"The opposition, citing the right of investigation, demanded that those involved be summoned as witnesses.","furigana":"やとう|こくせいちょうさけん|もと|かんけいしゃ|しょうにんかんもん|もと"},{"japanese":"国政調査権は憲法第六十二条で認められている。","romaji":"Kokusei chōsaken wa kenpō dai rokujūni-jō de mitomerarete iru.","german":"The right of investigation in government affairs is recognized in Article 62 of the Constitution.","furigana":"こくせいちょうさけん|けんぽうだいろくじゅうにじょう|みと"}],"notes":"国政調査権 is the right, anchored in Art. 62 of the Constitution, of both houses of parliament to investigate government affairs, summon witnesses (証人喚問) and demand documents. Typical: 国政調査権を行使する/発動する, 国政調査権に基づき. One hears the word above all in scandals, when the opposition demands clarification. Technical and formal.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"内閣不信任案が国会に提出された。","romaji":"Naikaku fushinnin'an ga kokkai ni teishutsu sareta.","german":"A no-confidence motion against the Cabinet was submitted to the Diet.","furigana":"ないかくふしんにんあん|こっかい|ていしゅつ"},{"japanese":"不信任案は賛成少数で否決された。","romaji":"Fushinnin'an wa sansei shōsū de hiketsu sareta.","german":"The no-confidence motion was rejected, with few in favor.","furigana":"ふしんにんあん|さんせいしょうすう|ひけつ"},{"japanese":"市議会は市長の不信任を決議した。","romaji":"Shigikai wa shichō no fushinnin o ketsugi shita.","german":"The city council passed a resolution of no confidence in the mayor.","furigana":"しぎかい|しちょう|ふしんにん|けつぎ"}],"notes":"不信任 means \"no confidence, lack of trust\" and is known above all from politics: 内閣不信任案 \"no-confidence motion against the Cabinet\", 不信任決議 \"vote of no confidence\", 不信任案を可決/否決する. If a no-confidence vote is passed in the lower house, the Cabinet must resign or dissolve the house. In the personal sphere, one rather says 不信感 \"distrust\" or 信用できない.","levelNote":"Compound: 不 (N3) + 信任 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大臣に対する問責決議が可決された。","romaji":"Daijin ni taisuru monseki ketsugi ga kaketsu sareta.","german":"A censure resolution against the minister was passed.","furigana":"だいじん|たい|もんせきけつぎ|かけつ"},{"japanese":"問責決議には法的拘束力がない。","romaji":"Monseki ketsugi ni wa hōteki kōsokuryoku ga nai.","german":"A censure resolution is not legally binding.","furigana":"もんせきけつぎ|ほうてきこうそくりょく"},{"japanese":"事故の対応を誤った担当者が社内で問責された。","romaji":"Jiko no taiō o ayamatta tantōsha ga shanai de monseki sareta.","german":"The person in charge who mishandled the response to the accident was held accountable within the company.","furigana":"じこ|たいおう|あやま|たんとうしゃ|しゃない|もんせき"}],"notes":"問責 means to hold someone responsible for a failure. In politics, a 問責決議 is a censure by the upper house against the prime minister or a minister; unlike a 不信任決議 in the lower house, it is not legally binding, but it puts those concerned under political pressure. As a verb, 〜を問責する (rather written); in normal usage one says 責任を問う.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"失言が原因で報道官が更迭された。","romaji":"Shitsugen ga gen'in de hōdōkan ga kōtetsu sareta.","german":"The spokesperson was removed because of a verbal gaffe.","furigana":"しつげん|げんいん|ほうどうかん|こうてつ"},{"japanese":"成績不振で監督が更迭された。","romaji":"Seiseki fushin de kantoku ga kōtetsu sareta.","german":"The manager was dismissed due to poor results.","furigana":"せいせきふしん|かんとく|こうてつ"},{"japanese":"不祥事を受けて、社長は担当役員を更迭した。","romaji":"Fushōji o ukete, shachō wa tantō yakuin o kōtetsu shita.","german":"Following the scandal, the company president removed the executive in charge.","furigana":"ふしょうじ|う|しゃちょう|たんとうやくいん|こうてつ"}],"notes":"更迭 is the replacement of an official by another, usually initiated from above and often after a mistake or scandal - that is, a veiled dismissal. Typical: 〜を更迭する, 〜が更迭される, 事実上の更迭 \"de facto removal\". Neutral is 交代 \"change\", considerably harsher 解任 \"dismissal\"; for a voluntary resignation one says 辞任. Common in political and sports news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"裁判官に対する弾劾裁判が開廷された。","romaji":"Saibankan ni taisuru dangai saiban ga kaitei sareta.","german":"The impeachment trial of the judge was opened.","furigana":"さいばんかん|たい|だんがいさいばん|かいてい"},{"japanese":"日本では国会議員が裁判官となって弾劾裁判を行う。","romaji":"Nihon de wa kokkai giin ga saibankan to natte dangai saiban o okonau.","german":"In Japan, members of the Diet act as judges and conduct impeachment trials.","furigana":"にほん|こっかいぎいん|さいばんかん|だんがいさいばん|おこな"},{"japanese":"アメリカでは上院で大統領の弾劾裁判が開かれた。","romaji":"Amerika de wa jōin de daitōryō no dangai saiban ga hirakareta.","german":"In the United States, an impeachment trial of the president was held in the Senate.","furigana":"じょういん|だいとうりょう|だんがいさいばん|ひら"}],"notes":"弾劾裁判 is a procedure for removal from office. In Japan it is directed only against judges: an 弾劾裁判所 \"impeachment court\" made up of lawmakers from both houses decides on their 罷免 \"removal\". In reports about foreign countries, it means e.g. the impeachment proceedings against a president. 弾劾 alone means \"impeachment, removal from office\" or \"harsh denunciation\" (〜を弾劾する). Legal and formal.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"最高裁が法律を違憲と判断した。","romaji":"Saikōsai ga hōritsu o iken to handan shita.","german":"The Supreme Court ruled the law unconstitutional.","furigana":"さいこうさい|ほうりつ|いけん|はんだん"},{"japanese":"集団的自衛権の行使は違憲だと主張する学者もいる。","romaji":"Shūdanteki jieiken no kōshi wa iken da to shuchō suru gakusha mo iru.","german":"Some scholars argue that exercising the right of collective self-defense is unconstitutional.","furigana":"しゅうだんてきじえいけん|こうし|いけん|しゅちょう|がくしゃ"},{"japanese":"原告たちは国を相手に違憲訴訟を起こした。","romaji":"Genkokutachi wa kuni o aite ni iken soshō o okoshita.","german":"The plaintiffs filed an unconstitutionality suit against the state.","furigana":"げんこく|くに|あいて|いけんそしょう|お"}],"notes":"違憲 is a noun (\"unconstitutionality\"), but is mostly used predicatively: 〜は違憲だ, 違憲と判断する, 違憲判決. Well known is 違憲状態 \"unconstitutional state\", for example with unequally weighted votes (一票の格差). The opposite is 合憲. Note: it is read the same as 意見 \"opinion\" - context decides.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この法律は合憲であるとの判決が出た。","romaji":"Kono hōritsu wa gōken de aru to no hanketsu ga deta.","german":"A ruling came down that this law is constitutional.","furigana":"ほうりつ|ごうけん|はんけつ|で"},{"japanese":"政府は、この措置は合憲だと説明している。","romaji":"Seifu wa, kono sochi wa gōken da to setsumei shite iru.","german":"The government explains that this measure is constitutional.","furigana":"せいふ|そち|ごうけん|せつめい"},{"japanese":"裁判所は法律の合憲性を審査する。","romaji":"Saibansho wa hōritsu no gōkensei o shinsa suru.","german":"Courts review the constitutionality of laws.","furigana":"さいばんしょ|ほうりつ|ごうけんせい|しんさ"}],"notes":"合憲 means \"constitutionality\" and is mostly used predicatively: 〜は合憲だ, 合憲と判断する, 合憲判決. The derived 合憲性 \"constitutionality\" is examined or disputed (合憲性を審査する). The opposite is 違憲. A word of legal and political language that often appears in news about court rulings.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"行政処分に対する司法審査が求められた。","romaji":"Gyōsei shobun ni taisuru shihō shinsa ga motomerareta.","german":"Judicial review of the administrative action was demanded.","furigana":"ぎょうせいしょぶん|たい|しほうしんさ|もと"},{"japanese":"高度に政治的な問題は司法審査の対象外とされることがある。","romaji":"Kōdo ni seijiteki na mondai wa shihō shinsa no taishōgai to sareru koto ga aru.","german":"Highly political questions are sometimes excluded from judicial review.","furigana":"こうど|せいじてき|もんだい|しほうしんさ|たいしょうがい"},{"japanese":"アメリカでは司法審査の制度が判例によって確立した。","romaji":"Amerika de wa shihō shinsa no seido ga hanrei ni yotte kakuritsu shita.","german":"In the United States, the system of judicial review was established by case law.","furigana":"しほうしんさ|せいど|はんれい|かくりつ"}],"notes":"司法審査 is the review of laws or administrative action by the courts. In Japan, one usually speaks more concretely of 違憲審査 or 違憲立法審査権 \"power of judicial review\". Typical: 司法審査の対象となる/外とされる, 司法審査を求める. According to the doctrine of 統治行為 \"acts of state\", highly political questions can be exempted from 司法審査. Legal technical term.","levelNote":"Compound: 司法 (N1) + 審査 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"慣習法も重要な法源の一つである。","romaji":"Kanshūhō mo jūyō na hōgen no hitotsu de aru.","german":"Customary law is also an important source of law.","furigana":"かんしゅうほう|じゅうよう|ほうげん|ひと"},{"japanese":"日本では判例が法源にあたるかどうかについて議論がある。","romaji":"Nihon de wa hanrei ga hōgen ni ataru ka dō ka ni tsuite giron ga aru.","german":"In Japan, there is debate over whether court precedents count as a source of law.","furigana":"にほん|はんれい|ほうげん|ぎろん"},{"japanese":"イスラム法では、コーランが第一の法源とされる。","romaji":"Isuramu-hō de wa, Kōran ga daiichi no hōgen to sareru.","german":"In Islamic law, the Koran is regarded as the primary source of law.","furigana":"ほう|だいいち|ほうげん"}],"notes":"法源 is a term of legal science: the sources from which law arises, such as 成文法 \"written law\", 慣習法 \"customary law\", 判例 \"case law\" and 条理 \"general legal principles\". Typical: 〜は法源の一つである, 〜が法源にあたるか. Note: it is read the same as 方言 \"dialect\". Almost only in textbooks and technical texts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"信義誠実の法理に基づいて判断された。","romaji":"Shingi seijitsu no hōri ni motozuite handan sareta.","german":"The decision was made on the basis of the principle of good faith.","furigana":"しんぎせいじつ|ほうり|もと|はんだん"},{"japanese":"解雇権濫用法理により、合理的な理由のない解雇は無効とされる。","romaji":"Kaikoken ran'yō hōri ni yori, gōriteki na riyū no nai kaiko wa mukō to sareru.","german":"Under the doctrine of abuse of the right to dismiss, a dismissal without reasonable grounds is invalid.","furigana":"かいこけんらんようほうり|ごうりてき|りゆう|かいこ|むこう"},{"japanese":"この判決は法理の上では正しいが、市民の感覚とはずれている。","romaji":"Kono hanketsu wa hōri no ue de wa tadashii ga, shimin no kankaku to wa zurete iru.","german":"This ruling is correct in terms of legal principle, but out of step with the public's sense.","furigana":"はんけつ|ほうり|うえ|ただ|しみん|かんかく"}],"notes":"法理 refers to a legal principle derived from the legal system as a whole or from case law, and often not stated explicitly in the statute. Typical are fixed combinations like 信義則の法理 or 解雇権濫用法理, usually with に基づく or に照らして. Compared with 法律 \"the individual law\" and 原則 \"principle\" in general, 法理 is a technical word from legal science and case law; it hardly appears in everyday life.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"関連法令を遵守する義務がある。","romaji":"Kanren hōrei o junshu suru gimu ga aru.","german":"There is an obligation to comply with the relevant laws and regulations.","furigana":"かんれんほうれい|じゅんしゅ|ぎむ"},{"japanese":"その行為は法令に違反するおそれがあります。","romaji":"Sono kōi wa hōrei ni ihan suru osore ga arimasu.","german":"That act may violate laws and regulations.","furigana":"こうい|ほうれい|いはん"},{"japanese":"法令の改正に合わせて、社内規則を見直した。","romaji":"Hōrei no kaisei ni awasete, shanai kisoku o minaoshita.","german":"We revised our internal company rules in line with the amendments to the laws and regulations.","furigana":"ほうれい|かいせい|あ|しゃないきそく|みなお"}],"notes":"法令 is the collective term for laws (法律) and the administrative ordinances based on them (政令, 省令 etc.), i.e. all rules of law in force. Common: 法令を守る/遵守する, 法令に違反する, 法令に基づく; \"compliance\" in companies is often 法令遵守. In contrast to 法律 (\"a law passed by parliament\"), 法令 also includes regulations of the executive; the tone is official and written.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"判事が被告人に対して判決を言い渡した。","romaji":"Hanji ga hikokunin ni taishite hanketsu o iiwatashita.","german":"The judge handed down the verdict against the defendant.","furigana":"はんじ|ひこくにん|たい|はんけつ|い|わた"},{"japanese":"彼女は判事補を経て、四十歳で判事になった。","romaji":"Kanojo wa hanjiho o hete, yonjussai de hanji ni natta.","german":"After serving as an assistant judge, she became a judge at forty.","furigana":"かのじょ|はんじほ|へ|よんじゅっさい|はんじ"},{"japanese":"米国の連邦判事が、その法律の執行を一時差し止めた。","romaji":"Beikoku no renpō hanji ga, sono hōritsu no shikkō o ichiji sashitometa.","german":"A US federal judge temporarily blocked the enforcement of the law.","furigana":"べいこく|れんぽうはんじ|ほうりつ|しっこう|いちじさ|と"}],"notes":"In Japan, 判事 is actually an official rank designation for professional judges (below it stands the 判事補 \"assistant judge\"); the general word for \"judge\" is 裁判官. In news, 判事 is read mainly in 最高裁判事 and for foreign courts, e.g. 米連邦判事. In everyday speech and conversation, 裁判官 is mostly used; the presiding judge of a panel is called 裁判長.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"検事が冒頭陳述を行った。","romaji":"Kenji ga bōtō chinjutsu o okonatta.","german":"The prosecutor gave the opening statement.","furigana":"けんじ|ぼうとうちんじゅつ|おこな"},{"japanese":"担当の検事から、もう一度事情を聞かれた。","romaji":"Tantō no kenji kara, mō ichido jijō o kikareta.","german":"The prosecutor in charge questioned me once more.","furigana":"たんとう|けんじ|いちどじじょう|き"},{"japanese":"彼は司法試験に合格し、検事の道を選んだ。","romaji":"Kare wa shihō shiken ni gōkaku shi, kenji no michi o eranda.","german":"He passed the bar exam and chose a career as a prosecutor.","furigana":"かれ|しほうしけん|ごうかく|けんじ|みち|えら"}],"notes":"検事 is the public prosecutor, strictly speaking a rank within the prosecution service; the general term for all prosecutors is 検察官, and the authority is called 検察 or 検察庁. In news and crime dramas, 検事 is the usual term for the person: 担当検事, 検事総長 \"Prosecutor General\". The opponent in court is the defense counsel (弁護人, generally 弁護士).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"被疑者の身柄が拘束された。","romaji":"Higisha no migara ga kōsoku sareta.","german":"The suspect was taken into custody.","furigana":"ひぎしゃ|みがら|こうそく"},{"japanese":"警察は被疑者の取り調べを続けている。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa higisha no torishirabe o tsuzukete iru.","german":"The police are continuing to interrogate the suspect.","furigana":"けいさつ|ひぎしゃ|と|しら|つづ"},{"japanese":"被疑者には弁護人を依頼する権利がある。","romaji":"Higisha ni wa bengonin o irai suru kenri ga aru.","german":"A suspect has the right to appoint defense counsel.","furigana":"ひぎしゃ|べんごにん|いらい|けんり"}],"notes":"被疑者 is the technical term for a person suspected of a crime before charges are filed; after indictment, the person becomes a 被告人. Newspapers and TV almost always say 容疑者 instead of 被疑者, whereas 被疑者 is the language of the police, the judiciary and statutes. Typical: 被疑者を逮捕する, 被疑者の取り調べ, 被疑者の身柄を拘束する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"被告人は無罪を主張した。","romaji":"Hikokunin wa muzai o shuchō shita.","german":"The defendant pleaded not guilty.","furigana":"ひこくにん|むざい|しゅちょう"},{"japanese":"公判では被告人質問が行われた。","romaji":"Kōhan de wa hikokunin shitsumon ga okonawareta.","german":"At the trial, the defendant was questioned.","furigana":"こうはん|ひこくにんしつもん|おこな"},{"japanese":"裁判長は被告人に、最後に言いたいことはないかと尋ねた。","romaji":"Saibanchō wa hikokunin ni, saigo ni iitai koto wa nai ka to tazuneta.","german":"The presiding judge asked the defendant whether he had anything to say at the end.","furigana":"さいばんちょう|ひこくにん|さいご|い|たず"}],"notes":"被告人 is the defendant in a criminal trial, i.e. the person against whom charges (起訴) have been filed; before that, the person is called 被疑者 or, in the media, 容疑者. Not to be confused with 被告 (\"defendant\" in a civil suit, the opposing party of the 原告); however, the media also often shorten it to 被告 in criminal trials and attach it to the name (山田被告). Typical: 被告人質問, 被告人に判決を言い渡す.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"強制による自白は証拠能力を持たない。","romaji":"Kyōsei ni yoru jihaku wa shōko nōryoku o motanai.","german":"A confession obtained by coercion has no evidentiary value.","furigana":"きょうせい|じはく|しょうこのうりょく|も"},{"japanese":"容疑者は取り調べで犯行を自白した。","romaji":"Yōgisha wa torishirabe de hankō o jihaku shita.","german":"The suspect confessed to the crime during interrogation.","furigana":"ようぎしゃ|と|しら|はんこう|じはく"},{"japanese":"被告人は公判で自白を翻し、無罪を主張した。","romaji":"Hikokunin wa kōhan de jihaku o hirugaeshi, muzai o shuchō shita.","german":"At trial, the defendant retracted his confession and claimed innocence.","furigana":"ひこくにん|こうはん|じはく|ひるがえ|むざい|しゅちょう"}],"notes":"自白 is a confession, above all in criminal proceedings; as a する-verb it is transitive: 犯行を自白する. Typical combinations: 自白を強要する (\"to force\"), 自白を引き出す, 自白を翻す (\"to retract a confession\"). Close in meaning is 白状する, which is more colloquial and also covers everyday matters (\"to confess, admit\"); 告白, by contrast, is a confession of feelings or secrets, such as a declaration of love.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"被疑者は黙秘権を行使した。","romaji":"Higisha wa mokuhiken o kōshi shita.","german":"The suspect exercised the right to remain silent.","furigana":"ひぎしゃ|もくひけん|こうし"},{"japanese":"取り調べの前に、警察官は黙秘権があることを告げた。","romaji":"Torishirabe no mae ni, keisatsukan wa mokuhiken ga aru koto o tsugeta.","german":"Before the interrogation, the police officer informed him that he had the right to remain silent.","furigana":"と|しら|まえ|けいさつかん|もくひけん|つ"},{"japanese":"年齢を聞かれた母は「黙秘権を行使します」と笑った。","romaji":"Nenrei o kikareta haha wa \"mokuhiken o kōshi shimasu\" to waratta.","german":"When my mother was asked her age, she said with a laugh, \"I'll exercise my right to remain silent.\"","furigana":"ねんれい|き|はは|もくひけん|こうし|わら"}],"notes":"黙秘権 is the right of the suspect or defendant to refuse to testify; fixed combinations are 黙秘権を行使する and 黙秘権を告知する (advisement by the police or court). The silence itself is called 黙秘 (黙秘する, 完全黙秘). In everyday speech one jokingly says 黙秘権を行使します or simply 黙秘します when one does not want to answer an uncomfortable question.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"裁判所が逮捕令状を発行した。","romaji":"Saibansho ga taiho reijō o hakkō shita.","german":"The court issued an arrest warrant.","furigana":"さいばんしょ|たいほれいじょう|はっこう"},{"japanese":"警察は令状を取って、男の自宅を捜索した。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa reijō o totte, otoko no jitaku o sōsaku shita.","german":"The police obtained a warrant and searched the man's home.","furigana":"けいさつ|れいじょう|と|おとこ|じたく|そうさく"},{"japanese":"令状なしでスマートフォンの中身を調べることは許されない。","romaji":"Reijō nashi de sumātofon no nakami o shiraberu koto wa yurusarenai.","german":"It is not permitted to search the contents of a smartphone without a warrant.","furigana":"れいじょう|なかみ|しら|ゆる"}],"notes":"令状 is a written order, today above all the court order in criminal proceedings: 逮捕令状 (usually shortened to 逮捕状 \"arrest warrant\"), 捜索令状 (\"search warrant\"). Typical verbs: 令状を請求する (\"to apply for\", by the police or prosecution), 令状を出す/発付する (by the court), 令状を示す. 令状なしで means \"without a warrant\". Historically, 召集令状 was the draft notice (colloquially 赤紙).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"警察が事件の捜査を進めている。","romaji":"Keisatsu ga jiken no sōsa o susumete iru.","german":"The police are pushing ahead with the investigation of the case.","furigana":"けいさつ|じけん|そうさ|すす"},{"japanese":"警察は詐欺の疑いで捜査に乗り出した。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa sagi no utagai de sōsa ni noridashita.","german":"The police launched an investigation on suspicion of fraud.","furigana":"けいさつ|さぎ|うたが|そうさ|の|だ"},{"japanese":"犯人の手がかりが少なく、捜査は難航している。","romaji":"Hannin no tegakari ga sukunaku, sōsa wa nankō shite iru.","german":"With few clues to the culprit, the investigation is struggling.","furigana":"はんにん|て|すく|そうさ|なんこう"}],"notes":"捜査 is a criminal investigation by the police or prosecution; as a する-verb it is transitive: 事件を捜査する. Common combinations: 捜査を進める, 捜査に乗り出す (\"to launch an investigation\"), 捜査が難航する (\"to stall\"), 捜査線上に浮かぶ (\"to come under the investigators' scrutiny\"). Not to be confused with 捜索 (\"search\" for persons or things) and 調査 (\"investigation, survey\" without criminal connection); 操作 (\"operation, handling\") sounds the same.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"容疑者が書類送検された。","romaji":"Yōgisha ga shorui sōken sareta.","german":"The suspect's case was referred to prosecutors on paper, without an arrest.","furigana":"ようぎしゃ|しょるいそうけん"},{"japanese":"逮捕された男は、翌日送検された。","romaji":"Taiho sareta otoko wa, yokujitsu sōken sareta.","german":"The arrested man was referred to prosecutors the next day.","furigana":"たいほ|おとこ|よくじつそうけん"},{"japanese":"警察は事故を起こした運転手を送検した。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa jiko o okoshita untenshu o sōken shita.","german":"The police referred the driver who caused the accident to the prosecutors.","furigana":"けいさつ|じこ|お|うんてんしゅ|そうけん"}],"notes":"送検 means that the police hand a case over to the public prosecutor's office, which then decides on indictment; in the news mostly in the passive: 容疑者が送検された. With 身柄送検, the arrested suspect is sent along; with 書類送検, only the files go to the prosecutor's office and the suspect remains at large. The word is the language of the police and media; in the law, the procedure is called 送致.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"初公判が来月開かれる予定だ。","romaji":"Hatsu kōhan ga raigetsu hirakareru yotei da.","german":"The first trial hearing is scheduled to be held next month.","furigana":"はつこうはん|らいげつひら|よてい"},{"japanese":"被告は初公判で起訴内容を認めた。","romaji":"Hikoku wa hatsu kōhan de kiso naiyō o mitometa.","german":"The defendant admitted the charges at the first hearing.","furigana":"ひこく|はつこうはん|きそないよう|みと"},{"japanese":"公判はだれでも傍聴することができる。","romaji":"Kōhan wa dare demo bōchō suru koto ga dekiru.","german":"Anyone can attend a public trial as a spectator.","furigana":"こうはん|ぼうちょう"}],"notes":"公判 is the public main hearing in a criminal trial, after the indictment (起訴) and before the verdict. Very common in the news: 初公判 (\"first hearing\"), 公判が開かれる, 公判で起訴内容を認める/否認する. More general is 裁判 (\"court proceedings\", also civil); 公判 is a technical term of criminal procedure.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"DNA鑑定により冤罪が明らかになった。","romaji":"DNA kantei ni yori enzai ga akiraka ni natta.","german":"The miscarriage of justice was revealed by DNA testing.","furigana":"かんてい|えんざい|あき"},{"japanese":"彼は二十年かけて冤罪を晴らした。","romaji":"Kare wa nijūnen kakete enzai o harashita.","german":"It took him twenty years to clear his name of the false charge.","furigana":"かれ|にじゅうねん|えんざい|は"},{"japanese":"満員電車では痴漢の冤罪を恐れて、両手でつり革を持つ人もいる。","romaji":"Man'in densha de wa chikan no enzai o osorete, ryōte de tsurikawa o motsu hito mo iru.","german":"On crowded trains, some people hold the strap with both hands, fearing a false groping accusation.","furigana":"まんいんでんしゃ|ちかん|えんざい|おそ|りょうて|かわ|も|ひと"}],"notes":"冤罪 is a false accusation or the conviction of an innocent person. Typical: 冤罪を晴らす (\"to clear oneself of a false suspicion\"), 冤罪事件, 冤罪を生む, 冤罪の被害者. It is also used outside the courtroom for false suspicions, such as 痴漢冤罪 (falsely accused of groping). Compared with 誤審 (\"misjudgment\" by a judge, also in sports), 冤罪 stresses the injustice done to the person affected.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新証拠の発見により再審が開始された。","romaji":"Shin shōko no hakken ni yori saishin ga kaishi sareta.","german":"A retrial was opened after the discovery of new evidence.","furigana":"しんしょうこ|はっけん|さいしん|かいし"},{"japanese":"弁護団は裁判所に再審を請求した。","romaji":"Bengodan wa saibansho ni saishin o seikyū shita.","german":"The defense team petitioned the court for a retrial.","furigana":"べんごだん|さいばんしょ|さいしん|せいきゅう"},{"japanese":"再審の結果、元死刑囚に無罪が言い渡された。","romaji":"Saishin no kekka, moto shikeishū ni muzai ga iiwatasareta.","german":"As a result of the retrial, the former death-row inmate was acquitted.","furigana":"さいしん|けっか|もとしけいしゅう|むざい|い|わた"}],"notes":"再審 in criminal law is the reopening of a proceeding that has already been finally concluded, usually because of new evidence; typical: 再審を請求する, 再審開始の決定, 再審で無罪となる. Clearly distinct from this are 控訴 and 上告 (appeals against a judgment that is not yet final). The general meaning \"renewed examination\" (sense 2) is rarer and occurs mainly in official and examination contexts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"最高裁が事件を高裁に差し戻した。","romaji":"Saikōsai ga jiken o kōsai ni sashimodoshita.","german":"The Supreme Court remanded the case to the High Court.","furigana":"さいこうさい|じけん|こうさい|さ|もど"},{"japanese":"差し戻し審では、一審と異なる判決が出た。","romaji":"Sashimodoshi shin de wa, isshin to kotonaru hanketsu ga deta.","german":"In the retrial after remand, a different verdict from the first instance was handed down.","furigana":"さ|もど|しん|いっしん|こと|はんけつ|で"},{"japanese":"申請書に不備があり、差し戻しになった。","romaji":"Shinseisho ni fubi ga ari, sashimodoshi ni natta.","german":"The application form was incomplete and was sent back.","furigana":"しんせいしょ|ふび|さ|もど"}],"notes":"差し戻し is the noun for 差し戻す (\"to send back, remand\"). In law it means that a higher court overturns a judgment and sends the case back to the lower court for a new hearing: 高裁に差し戻す, 差し戻し審 (\"hearing after remand\"). In office life it is also said when an application or document is returned for revision (書類が差し戻される). In official texts the spelling 差戻し is also common.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"初犯であることを考慮し執行猶予が付いた。","romaji":"Shohan de aru koto o kōryo shi shikkō yūyo ga tsuita.","german":"Considering that it was his first offense, the sentence was suspended.","furigana":"しょはん|こうりょ|しっこうゆうよ|つ"},{"japanese":"執行猶予付きの判決を受け、彼はその日のうちに釈放された。","romaji":"Shikkō yūyo-tsuki no hanketsu o uke, kare wa sono hi no uchi ni shakuhō sareta.","german":"He received a suspended sentence and was released that same day.","furigana":"しっこうゆうよつ|はんけつ|う|かれ|ひ|しゃくほう"},{"japanese":"執行猶予中に再び罪を犯すと、猶予が取り消されることがある。","romaji":"Shikkō yūyo-chū ni futatabi tsumi o okasu to, yūyo ga torikesareru koto ga aru.","german":"If you commit another crime during the suspension period, the suspension may be revoked.","furigana":"しっこうゆうよちゅう|ふたた|つみ|おか|ゆうよ|と|け"}],"notes":"執行猶予 is the suspension of the execution of a sentence on probation: the sentence is imposed but not carried out if the convicted person does not reoffend within the period. Typical phrases: 執行猶予が付く, 執行猶予付きの判決, in verdicts \"〇年、執行猶予〇年\". The counterpart is 実刑 (\"a sentence without suspension that must be served\"). Colloquially, it is also jokingly used for a grace period.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"被告人に禁固三年の判決が下された。","romaji":"Hikokunin ni kinko sannen no hanketsu ga kudasareta.","german":"The defendant was sentenced to three years' imprisonment without labor.","furigana":"ひこくにん|きんこさんねん|はんけつ|くだ"},{"japanese":"交通事故で人を死なせた運転手に、禁固二年の実刑判決が出た。","romaji":"Kōtsū jiko de hito o shinaseta untenshu ni, kinko ninen no jikkei hanketsu ga deta.","german":"The driver who caused a death in a traffic accident was sentenced to two years' imprisonment without suspension.","furigana":"こうつうじこ|ひと|し|うんてんしゅ|きんこにねん|じっけいはんけつ|で"},{"japanese":"禁固と懲役の違いは、刑務作業の義務があるかどうかだった。","romaji":"Kinko to chōeki no chigai wa, keimu sagyō no gimu ga aru ka dō ka datta.","german":"The difference between imprisonment without labor and imprisonment with labor was whether prison work was required.","furigana":"きんこ|ちょうえき|ちが|けいむさぎょう|ぎむ"}],"notes":"禁固 (officially written 禁錮) was a custodial sentence without an obligation to do prison labor, imposed mainly for negligent offenses such as fatal traffic accidents, e.g. 禁固2年. With 懲役, by contrast, one had to work in prison. Since June 2025, both types of punishment have been merged into the single 拘禁刑; 禁固 is therefore encountered today mainly in older verdicts, reports and texts about the former law.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"懲役五年の実刑判決が言い渡された。","romaji":"Chōeki gonen no jikkei hanketsu ga iiwatasareta.","german":"A sentence of five years' imprisonment with labor, without suspension, was handed down.","furigana":"ちょうえきごねん|じっけいはんけつ|い|わた"},{"japanese":"検察は被告に懲役十年を求刑した。","romaji":"Kensatsu wa hikoku ni chōeki jūnen o kyūkei shita.","german":"The prosecution demanded ten years' imprisonment for the defendant.","furigana":"けんさつ|ひこく|ちょうえきじゅうねん|きゅうけい"},{"japanese":"彼は懲役を終えて出所した後、パン屋で働き始めた。","romaji":"Kare wa chōeki o oete shussho shita ato, pan'ya de hatarakihajimeta.","german":"After serving his sentence and being released, he started working at a bakery.","furigana":"かれ|ちょうえき|お|しゅっしょ|あと|や|はたら|はじ"}],"notes":"懲役 was the most common custodial sentence in Japanese criminal law and involved the obligation of prison labor (刑務作業); typical: 懲役5年, 懲役刑, 懲役〇年を求刑する (the prosecution's sentencing request). With 実刑 the sentence is actually served, with 執行猶予 it is suspended on probation. Since June 2025, 懲役 and 禁固 have been merged into the new 拘禁刑, but the word remains widespread in reports on older cases and in general usage.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"軽微な犯罪に対して科料が科された。","romaji":"Keibi na hanzai ni taishite karyō ga kasareta.","german":"A minor fine was imposed for a petty offense.","furigana":"けいび|はんざい|たい|かりょう|か"},{"japanese":"侮辱罪で起訴され、科料九千円の判決を受けた。","romaji":"Bujokuzai de kiso sare, karyō kyūsen'en no hanketsu o uketa.","german":"He was charged with insult and sentenced to a minor fine of 9,000 yen.","furigana":"ぶじょくざい|きそ|かりょうきゅうせんえん|はんけつ|う"},{"japanese":"科料は罰金よりも軽い刑罰である。","romaji":"Karyō wa bakkin yori mo karui keibatsu de aru.","german":"A minor fine is a lighter punishment than a regular fine.","furigana":"かりょう|ばっきん|かる|けいばつ"}],"notes":"科料 is the lightest monetary penalty in criminal law (1,000 up to under 10,000 yen), for example for insult (侮辱罪) or minor offenses; from 10,000 yen up, one speaks of 罰金. Read the same but different is 過料, an administrative fine without criminal character, e.g. for late notifications. To tell them apart in conversation, 科料 is often read とがりょう and 過料 あやまちりょう. Typical: 科料に処する, 科料を科す.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"犯罪に使用された道具が没収された。","romaji":"Hanzai ni shiyō sareta dōgu ga bosshū sareta.","german":"The tools used in the crime were confiscated.","furigana":"はんざい|しよう|どうぐ|ぼっしゅう"},{"japanese":"授業中にゲームをしていたら、先生に没収された。","romaji":"Jugyōchū ni gēmu o shite itara, sensei ni bosshū sareta.","german":"When I was playing games during class, the teacher confiscated my device.","furigana":"じゅぎょうちゅう|せんせい|ぼっしゅう"},{"japanese":"試合は没収試合となり、相手チームの勝ちになった。","romaji":"Shiai wa bosshū jiai to nari, aite chīmu no kachi ni natta.","german":"The match was ruled a forfeit and awarded to the opposing team.","furigana":"しあい|ぼっしゅうじあい|あいて|か"}],"notes":"没収 is the seizure or taking away of items by an authority; as a する-verb it is transitive, often passive: 財産を没収される. In criminal law it is an additional penalty (forfeiture of instruments or proceeds of a crime); in everyday life it is very common when teachers take something away: 授業中にスマホを没収された. 没収試合 is, in sports, a game that is declared lost because of a rule violation. Close in meaning are 押収 (\"seizure\" as evidence during an investigation) and 差し押さえ (\"attachment, garnishment\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"脱税分に対して追徴課税が行われた。","romaji":"Datsuzeibun ni taishite tsuichō kazei ga okonawareta.","german":"An additional tax was levied on the amount of tax evaded.","furigana":"だつぜいぶん|たい|ついちょうかぜい|おこな"},{"japanese":"申告漏れが見つかり、約百万円を追徴された。","romaji":"Shinkoku more ga mitsukari, yaku hyakuman'en o tsuichō sareta.","german":"Unreported income was discovered, and about one million yen was collected in back payment.","furigana":"しんこくも|み|やくひゃくまんえん|ついちょう"},{"japanese":"裁判所は被告に、受け取った賄賂と同じ額の追徴を命じた。","romaji":"Saibansho wa hikoku ni, uketotta wairo to onaji gaku no tsuichō o meijita.","german":"The court ordered the defendant to pay an amount equal to the bribe he had received.","furigana":"さいばんしょ|ひこく|う|と|わいろ|おな|がく|ついちょう|めい"}],"notes":"追徴 means to demand a missing amount afterward. Most common in 追徴課税 (\"additional tax assessment\") and 追徴金: in criminal law, this is the sum of money levied when confiscation (没収) is no longer possible, for example because bribe money has already been spent. Typical: 〇円を追徴する, 追徴される. The word belongs to the technical language of tax authorities and courts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"軽微な事件は即決裁判で処理される。","romaji":"Keibi na jiken wa sokketsu saiban de shori sareru.","german":"Minor cases are handled in summary trials.","furigana":"けいび|じけん|そっけつさいばん|しょり"},{"japanese":"彼女は家を一目見て、購入を即決した。","romaji":"Kanojo wa ie o hitome mite, kōnyū o sokketsu shita.","german":"She took one look at the house and decided to buy it on the spot.","furigana":"かのじょ|いえ|ひとめみ|こうにゅう|そっけつ"},{"japanese":"社長は新しい企画を即決で承認した。","romaji":"Shachō wa atarashii kikaku o sokketsu de shōnin shita.","german":"The company president approved the new plan on the spot.","furigana":"しゃちょう|あたら|きかく|そっけつ|しょうにん"}],"notes":"即決 means \"to decide immediately\"; as a する-verb it is transitive: 購入を即決する. It is very common in everyday life: その場で即決する, 即決で決まる, 即断即決 (\"quick and decisive\"); in online auctions, 即決価格 is the buy-it-now price. In law, 即決裁判 refers to an expedited procedure for simple, admitted cases. The opposite would be e.g. 保留する (\"to put on hold\") or 検討する (\"to think it over\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"略式起訴により罰金刑が科された。","romaji":"Ryakushiki kiso ni yori bakkinkei ga kasareta.","german":"Durch ein vereinfachtes Anklageverfahren wurde eine Geldstrafe verhängt.","furigana":"りゃくしききそ|ばっきんけい|か"},{"japanese":"時間がないので、式は略式で行われた。","romaji":"Jikan ga nai node, shiki wa ryakushiki de okonawareta.","german":"Weil die Zeit knapp war, wurde die Zeremonie in vereinfachter Form abgehalten.","furigana":"じかん|しき|りゃくしき|おこな"},{"japanese":"通夜には略式の喪服で行っても構わない。","romaji":"Tsuya ni wa ryakushiki no mofuku de itte mo kamawanai.","german":"Zur Totenwache kann man ruhig in schlichter Trauerkleidung gehen.","furigana":"つや|りゃくしき|もふく|い|かま"}],"notes":"略式 bedeutet zunächst allgemein „vereinfacht, verkürzt, nicht förmlich“ und ist das Gegenwort zu 正式: 略式の服装, 略式の喪服, 式を略式で行う. Im Strafrecht bezeichnet es das vereinfachte schriftliche Verfahren ohne Hauptverhandlung: 略式起訴 und 略式命令 entsprechen etwa dem deutschen Strafbefehl und führen meist zu einer Geldstrafe. Es steht vor Nomen mit の oder direkt in Komposita.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"情状酌量が認められ減刑となった。","romaji":"Jōjō shakuryō ga mitomerare genkei to natta.","german":"Extenuating circumstances were recognized and the sentence was reduced.","furigana":"じょうじょうしゃくりょう|みと|げんけい"},{"japanese":"残忍な犯行で、情状酌量の余地はないと検察は主張した。","romaji":"Zannin na hankō de, jōjō shakuryō no yochi wa nai to kensatsu wa shuchō shita.","german":"The prosecution argued that the crime was so brutal that there was no room for leniency.","furigana":"ざんにん|はんこう|じょうじょうしゃくりょう|よち|けんさつ|しゅちょう"},{"japanese":"寝坊じゃなくて電車が止まったなら、情状酌量の余地はあるね。","romaji":"Nebō ja nakute densha ga tomatta nara, jōjō shakuryō no yochi wa aru ne.","german":"If the train stopped and you didn't just oversleep, then there's room for leniency.","furigana":"ねぼう|でんしゃ|と|じょうじょうしゃくりょう|よち"}],"notes":"情状酌量 means that the court takes the special circumstances of a case (motive, remorse, living situation) into account and mitigates the sentence; literally 情状 \"circumstances, state of affairs\" + 酌量 \"to weigh, take into consideration\". Typical: 情状酌量が認められる, 情状酌量の余地がある/ない (\"extenuating circumstances can / cannot be considered\"), 情状酌量を求める. In everyday life, too, one says half jokingly 情状酌量の余地はある when someone deserves leniency.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"裁判官の心証形成に影響を与える証拠が提出された。","romaji":"Saibankan no shinshō keisei ni eikyō o ataeru shōko ga teishutsu sareta.","german":"Evidence was submitted that influences the judge's formation of conviction.","furigana":"さいばんかん|しんしょうけいせい|えいきょう|あた|しょうこ|ていしゅつ"},{"japanese":"遅刻ばかりしていると、上司の心証を害するよ。","romaji":"Chikoku bakari shite iru to, jōshi no shinshō o gaisuru yo.","german":"If you keep being late, you'll end up leaving a bad impression on your boss.","furigana":"ちこく|じょうし|しんしょう|がい"},{"japanese":"面接での態度が悪く、面接官の心証はよくなかったようだ。","romaji":"Mensetsu de no taido ga waruku, mensetsukan no shinshō wa yoku nakatta yō da.","german":"He seems to have made a bad impression on the interviewer because of his poor attitude in the interview.","furigana":"めんせつ|たいど|わる|めんせつかん|しんしょう"}],"notes":"心証 has two uses: in general, the impression someone makes on others through their behavior, especially in 心証を害する (\"to leave a bad impression, to put someone off\") and 心証が良い/悪い; in law, the conviction that the judge forms from the evidence (心証形成, 自由心証主義 \"principle of free evaluation of evidence\"). Compared with 印象 (\"impression\" in the most general sense), 心証 refers to a judgment about a person's credibility or character and sounds more formal.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"民事訴訟では原告に挙証責任がある。","romaji":"Minji soshō de wa genkoku ni kyoshō sekinin ga aru.","german":"In civil proceedings, the burden of proof lies with the plaintiff.","furigana":"みんじそしょう|げんこく|きょしょうせきにん"},{"japanese":"刑事裁判では、検察側が挙証責任を負う。","romaji":"Keiji saiban de wa, kensatsu-gawa ga kyoshō sekinin o ou.","german":"In criminal trials, the prosecution bears the burden of proof.","furigana":"けいじさいばん|けんさつがわ|きょしょうせきにん|お"},{"japanese":"公害訴訟では、挙証責任を企業側に負わせるべきだという意見もある。","romaji":"Kōgai soshō de wa, kyoshō sekinin o kigyō-gawa ni owaseru beki da to iu iken mo aru.","german":"Some argue that in pollution lawsuits, companies should bear the burden of proof.","furigana":"こうがいそしょう|きょしょうせきにん|きぎょうがわ|お|いけん"}],"notes":"挙証責任 is the burden of proof: a party must prove a fact, otherwise it bears the disadvantage. Synonymous and somewhat more common is 立証責任; both are technical language. Typical: 挙証責任を負う, 挙証責任がある, 挙証責任の転換 (\"reversal of the burden of proof\"). In criminal proceedings, it lies in principle with the prosecution (検察側).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"罰金は国庫に納められる。","romaji":"Bakkin wa kokko ni osamerareru.","german":"Fines are paid into the national treasury.","furigana":"ばっきん|こっこ|おさ"},{"japanese":"この事業の費用の半分は国庫が負担する。","romaji":"Kono jigyō no hiyō no hanbun wa kokko ga futan suru.","german":"The national treasury bears half of the cost of this project.","furigana":"じぎょう|ひよう|はんぶん|こっこ|ふたん"},{"japanese":"相続人がいない財産は、最終的に国庫に帰属する。","romaji":"Sōzokunin ga inai zaisan wa, saishūteki ni kokko ni kizoku suru.","german":"Property without heirs ultimately reverts to the national treasury.","furigana":"そうぞくにん|ざいさん|さいしゅうてき|こっこ|きぞく"}],"notes":"国庫 is the state treasury, i.e. the state as the holder of public funds. Typical combinations: 国庫に納める/入る (\"to flow into the treasury\"), 国庫に返納する, 国庫に帰属する (\"to revert to the state\"), 国庫負担, 国庫補助金 (\"state subsidy\"). The word is official and written; in everyday speech one rather says 国のお金 or 税金. Not to be confused with 金庫 (\"safe\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"固定資産税は市町村が賦課する。","romaji":"Kotei shisanzei wa shichōson ga fuka suru.","german":"Fixed asset tax is assessed by the municipality.","furigana":"こていしさんぜい|しちょうそん|ふか"},{"japanese":"電気料金には再エネ賦課金が含まれている。","romaji":"Denki ryōkin ni wa saiene fukakin ga fukumarete iru.","german":"The electricity bill includes a renewable energy surcharge.","furigana":"でんきりょうきん|さい|ふかきん|ふく"},{"japanese":"日本の公的年金は賦課方式で運営されている。","romaji":"Nihon no kōteki nenkin wa fuka hōshiki de un'ei sarete iru.","german":"Japan's public pension is run on a pay-as-you-go basis.","furigana":"にほん|こうてきねんきん|ふかほうしき|うんえい"}],"notes":"賦課 is the official imposition of taxes, levies or fees; as a する-verb it is transitive: 税を賦課する. The authority is the subject, the levy the object. Well known are 賦課金 (e.g. 再エネ賦課金 on the electricity bill) and 賦課方式 (the pay-as-you-go system of pensions). Equivalent in meaning but more everyday are 課す and 課税する; 賦課 is purely administrative language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"担保不動産が競売にかけられた。","romaji":"Tanpo fudōsan ga keibai ni kakerareta.","german":"The mortgaged property was put up for foreclosure auction.","furigana":"たんぽふどうさん|けいばい"},{"japanese":"ローンが払えなくなり、自宅が競売にかけられた。","romaji":"Rōn ga haraenaku nari, jitaku ga keibai ni kakerareta.","german":"He could no longer pay the loan, and his home was put up for foreclosure auction.","furigana":"はら|じたく|けいばい"},{"japanese":"彼は競売物件を安く買って、リフォームして貸している。","romaji":"Kare wa keibai bukken o yasuku katte, rifōmu shite kashite iru.","german":"He buys foreclosure properties cheaply, renovates them and rents them out.","furigana":"かれ|けいばいぶっけん|やす|か|か"}],"notes":"競売 is read けいばい in legal language and mainly means the judicial compulsory auction, for example of real estate whose loan was not repaid; typical: 競売にかける/かけられる, 競売物件, 競売で落札する. General auctions are rather called きょうばい or simply オークション. As a する-verb it is rare; it usually appears as a noun with にかける.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"債務者が弁済金を法務局に供託した。","romaji":"Saimusha ga bensaikin o hōmukyoku ni kyōtaku shita.","german":"The debtor deposited the payment with the Legal Affairs Bureau.","furigana":"さいむしゃ|べんさいきん|ほうむきょく|きょうたく"},{"japanese":"大家が家賃を受け取らないので、家賃を供託した。","romaji":"Ōya ga yachin o uketoranai node, yachin o kyōtaku shita.","german":"Because the landlord would not accept the rent, I deposited it with the deposit office.","furigana":"おおや|やちん|う|と|やちん|きょうたく"},{"japanese":"得票が少なかったため、供託金は没収された。","romaji":"Tokuhyō ga sukunakatta tame, kyōtakukin wa bosshū sareta.","german":"Because he received too few votes, his election deposit was forfeited.","furigana":"とくひょう|すく|きょうたくきん|ぼっしゅう"}],"notes":"供託 is the deposit of money or securities with a state deposit office (供託所, in Japan the 法務局), for example when a creditor refuses payment: 家賃を供託する. Best known is 供託金, the deposit that election candidates must lodge and lose if they get too few votes (供託金が没収される). As a する-verb it is transitive: 〜を法務局に供託する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不動産に抵当権が設定された。","romaji":"Fudōsan ni teitōken ga settei sareta.","german":"A mortgage was registered on the property.","furigana":"ふどうさん|ていとうけん|せってい"},{"japanese":"彼は家を抵当に入れて、銀行からお金を借りた。","romaji":"Kare wa ie o teitō ni irete, ginkō kara okane o karita.","german":"He put his house up as collateral and borrowed money from the bank.","furigana":"かれ|いえ|ていとう|い|ぎんこう|かね|か"},{"japanese":"ローンを完済したので、抵当権を抹消する手続きをした。","romaji":"Rōn o kansai shita node, teitōken o masshō suru tetsuzuki o shita.","german":"Since the loan was paid off, I went through the procedure to cancel the mortgage.","furigana":"かんさい|ていとうけん|まっしょう|てつづ"}],"notes":"抵当 is security for a loan, above all real estate; the right to it is called 抵当権 (\"mortgage, lien on land\"). Typical: 家を抵当に入れる (\"to mortgage a house\"), 抵当権を設定する/抹消する, 抵当に取る. More general is 担保 (\"security\" of any kind); 質 (しち) is the pledge of movable items at the pawnbroker.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"債務不履行により契約が解除された。","romaji":"Saimu furikō ni yori keiyaku ga kaijo sareta.","german":"The contract was terminated due to non-performance of the obligation.","furigana":"さいむふりこう|けいやく|かいじょ"},{"japanese":"会社は多額の債務を抱えて倒産した。","romaji":"Kaisha wa tagaku no saimu o kakaete tōsan shita.","german":"The company went bankrupt carrying large debts.","furigana":"かいしゃ|たがく|さいむ|かか|とうさん"},{"japanese":"国の債務残高は年々増え続けている。","romaji":"Kuni no saimu zandaka wa nennen fuetsuzukete iru.","german":"The national debt keeps growing year after year.","furigana":"くに|さいむざんだか|ねんねんふ|つづ"}],"notes":"債務 is the legal obligation to perform something, usually a monetary debt; the counterpart is 債権 (\"claim\"), and the parties are called 債務者 and 債権者. Typical: 債務を負う, 債務を返済する, 債務不履行 (\"non-performance\"), 債務超過 (\"insolvency, liabilities exceeding assets\"). In everyday life, for debts one rather says 借金; 債務 is matter-of-fact and formal, and common in business and law.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"建物の瑕疵について売主に責任が問われた。","romaji":"Tatemono no kashi ni tsuite urinushi ni sekinin ga towareta.","german":"The seller was held responsible for the defects of the building.","furigana":"たてもの|かし|うりぬし|せきにん|と"},{"japanese":"手続きに瑕疵があったとして、処分は取り消された。","romaji":"Tetsuzuki ni kashi ga atta to shite, shobun wa torikesareta.","german":"The disposition was revoked on the grounds that there was a flaw in the procedure.","furigana":"てつづ|かし|しょぶん|と|け"},{"japanese":"引き渡し後に雨漏りが見つかり、瑕疵として修理を求めた。","romaji":"Hikiwatashi go ni amamori ga mitsukari, kashi to shite shūri o motometa.","german":"A roof leak was found after handover, and we demanded repair as a defect.","furigana":"ひ|わた|ご|あまも|み|かし|しゅうり|もと"}],"notes":"瑕疵 means \"blemish, defect\", above all in law: a defect in a thing (建物の瑕疵) or a legal flaw in a procedure or declaration of intent (手続きに瑕疵がある). The former 瑕疵担保責任 (\"warranty for defects\") has been called 契約不適合責任 in the Civil Code since 2020, but 瑕疵 remains common in construction law and contracts. The characters are difficult; one often sees かし or furigana. In everyday life, one says 欠陥 or 不具合.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"善意の第三者は保護される。","romaji":"Zen'i no daisansha wa hogo sareru.","german":"A bona fide third party is protected.","furigana":"ぜんい|だいさんしゃ|ほご"},{"japanese":"善意で言ったことが、かえって相手を傷つけてしまった。","romaji":"Zen'i de itta koto ga, kaette aite o kizutsukete shimatta.","german":"What I said with good intentions ended up hurting the other person instead.","furigana":"ぜんい|い|あいて|きず"},{"japanese":"人の善意につけ込む詐欺が増えている。","romaji":"Hito no zen'i ni tsukekomu sagi ga fuete iru.","german":"Fraud that takes advantage of people's goodwill is on the rise.","furigana":"ひと|ぜんい|こ|さぎ|ふ"}],"notes":"善意 means in everyday life \"good intention, goodwill\": 善意で, 善意の寄付, 人の善意を利用する. In law it has a quite different, technical meaning: 善意 there means not knowing a certain fact (\"good faith\"), without any moral evaluation, e.g. 善意の第三者. The counterpart is 悪意 (\"knowing the fact\"). For everyday \"goodwill\", 好意 is also common, which expresses more personal affection.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"悪意の占有者には返還義務がある。","romaji":"Akui no sen'yūsha ni wa henkan gimu ga aru.","german":"A possessor in bad faith has a duty to return the item.","furigana":"あくい|せんゆうしゃ|へんかんぎむ"},{"japanese":"悪意のある書き込みがネットで拡散された。","romaji":"Akui no aru kakikomi ga netto de kakusan sareta.","german":"A malicious post spread across the internet.","furigana":"あくい|か|こ|かくさん"},{"japanese":"彼の言葉はきつかったが、悪意はなかったと思う。","romaji":"Kare no kotoba wa kitsukatta ga, akui wa nakatta to omou.","german":"His words were harsh, but I think he meant no harm.","furigana":"かれ|ことば|あくい|おも"}],"notes":"悪意 means in everyday life \"ill will, malice\": 悪意がある/ない, 悪意のある書き込み, 悪意に満ちた. In law, by contrast, it is a technical term: 悪意 means knowing a certain fact (\"bad faith\"), entirely without any intention of harming anyone; the counterpart is 善意. To be distinguished is 故意 (\"intent\"), which refers to intention in committing an act.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"弁護士に訴訟を委任した。","romaji":"Bengoshi ni soshō o inin shita.","german":"I entrusted the lawsuit to a lawyer.","furigana":"べんごし|そしょう|いにん"},{"japanese":"代理人が手続きをする場合は、委任状が必要です。","romaji":"Dairinin ga tetsuzuki o suru baai wa, ininjō ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"If a representative handles the procedure, a power of attorney is required.","furigana":"だいりにん|てつづ|ばあい|いにんじょう|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"社長は決裁の権限を部長に委任した。","romaji":"Shachō wa kessai no kengen o buchō ni inin shita.","german":"The company president delegated the authority to approve to the department head.","furigana":"しゃちょう|けっさい|けんげん|ぶちょう|いにん"}],"notes":"委任 means to transfer a task or authority to someone to carry out at their own discretion; as a する-verb it is transitive: AにBを委任する. Typical: 権限を委任する, 委任状 (\"power of attorney\"), 委任契約. Close in meaning are 委託 (\"commission, outsourcing\", rather business) and 一任 (\"to leave entirely to someone\"); 任せる is the everyday word.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"国有地の払い下げが問題となった。","romaji":"Kokuyūchi no haraisage ga mondai to natta.","german":"The sale of state-owned land became an issue.","furigana":"こくゆうち|はら|さ|もんだい"},{"japanese":"市は使わなくなった土地を民間企業に払い下げた。","romaji":"Shi wa tsukawanaku natta tochi o minkan kigyō ni haraisageta.","german":"The city sold land it no longer used to a private company.","furigana":"し|つか|とち|みんかんきぎょう|はら|さ"},{"japanese":"この店では自衛隊の払い下げ品を扱っている。","romaji":"Kono mise de wa Jieitai no haraisage-hin o atsukatte iru.","german":"This shop deals in surplus equipment from the Self-Defense Forces.","furigana":"みせ|じえいたい|はら|さ|ひん|あつか"}],"notes":"払い下げ is the sale of state or agency property that is no longer needed to private parties; the verb is 払い下げる (transitive: 土地を企業に払い下げる). Common in 国有地の払い下げ, often in connection with scandals over sales at too low a price. 払い下げ品 are decommissioned goods, such as equipment of the Self-Defense Forces. The opposite direction (purchase by the state) is 買い上げ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"予算委員会で激しい論戦が交わされた。","romaji":"Yosan iinkai de hageshii ronsen ga kawasareta.","german":"Heated debate took place in the Budget Committee.","furigana":"よさんいいんかい|はげ|ろんせん|か"},{"japanese":"娘は文化祭の実行委員会に入った。","romaji":"Musume wa bunkasai no jikkō iinkai ni haitta.","german":"My daughter joined the executive committee for the school festival.","furigana":"むすめ|ぶんかさい|じっこういいんかい|はい"},{"japanese":"選挙管理委員会が最終的な投票率を発表した。","romaji":"Senkyo kanri iinkai ga saishūteki na tōhyōritsu o happyō shita.","german":"The election administration commission announced the final voter turnout.","furigana":"せんきょかんりいいんかい|さいしゅうてき|とうひょうりつ|はっぴょう"}],"notes":"委員会 is a committee or commission: in parliament (予算委員会), in government bodies (選挙管理委員会, 教育委員会) and quite commonly in schools and clubs (文化祭の実行委員会). Members are called 委員, the chair 委員長. Typical: 委員会を設置する, 委員会で審議する, 委員会に入る. In contrast to 会議 (\"meeting\"), 委員会 refers to the body itself, but can also mean its meeting.","levelNote":"Compound: 委員 (N3) + 会 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"少数会派が連合して法案に賛成した。","romaji":"Shōsū kaiha ga rengō shite hōan ni sansei shita.","german":"Small parliamentary groups joined forces and voted in favor of the bill.","furigana":"しょうすうかいは|れんごう|ほうあん|さんせい"},{"japanese":"二つの党は、衆議院で統一会派を結成した。","romaji":"Futatsu no tō wa, Shūgiin de tōitsu kaiha o kessei shita.","german":"The two parties formed a joint parliamentary group in the House of Representatives.","furigana":"ふた|とう|しゅうぎいん|とういつかいは|けっせい"},{"japanese":"無所属の議員がその会派に加わった。","romaji":"Mushozoku no giin ga sono kaiha ni kuwawatta.","german":"An independent lawmaker joined that parliamentary group.","furigana":"むしょぞく|ぎいん|かいは|くわ"}],"notes":"会派 is, in the Japanese parliament, a group of lawmakers registered with the respective house, similar to a parliamentary caucus. A 会派 can correspond to one party, but can also include several parties or independents (統一会派を組む/結成する). Speaking time and committee seats depend on the number of members. More generally, 会派 also means \"school, faction\" in religion or art; in politics it is technical.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"造反議員に対して党紀処分が下された。","romaji":"Zōhan giin ni taishite tōki shobun ga kudasareta.","german":"A party disciplinary measure was imposed on the rebel lawmaker.","furigana":"ぞうはんぎいん|たい|とうきしょぶん|くだ"},{"japanese":"党紀に違反したとして、彼は除名された。","romaji":"Tōki ni ihan shita to shite, kare wa jomei sareta.","german":"He was expelled for violating party discipline.","furigana":"とうき|いはん|かれ|じょめい"},{"japanese":"党紀委員会は来週、処分の内容を決める。","romaji":"Tōki iinkai wa raishū, shobun no naiyō o kimeru.","german":"The party's disciplinary committee will decide the punishment next week.","furigana":"とうきいいんかい|らいしゅう|しょぶん|ないよう|き"}],"notes":"党紀 is the discipline and order within a party. Typical: 党紀に違反する, 党紀を乱す, 党紀処分 (\"party punishment\", from a warning to 除名 \"expulsion\"), 党紀委員会. Pronounced the same is 党規 (\"party statutes, party rules\"); 党紀 stresses discipline. Violations often arise when lawmakers vote against the 党議拘束 (\"party whip\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"党大会で新しい綱領が採択された。","romaji":"Tō taikai de atarashii kōryō ga saitaku sareta.","german":"A new platform was adopted at the party convention.","furigana":"とうたいかい|あたら|こうりょう|さいたく"},{"japanese":"党の綱領には、平和主義が掲げられている。","romaji":"Tō no kōryō ni wa, heiwa shugi ga kakagerarete iru.","german":"The party's platform upholds pacifism.","furigana":"とう|こうりょう|へいわしゅぎ|かか"},{"japanese":"新聞社は記者が守るべき倫理綱領を定めている。","romaji":"Shinbunsha wa kisha ga mamoru beki rinri kōryō o sadamete iru.","german":"The newspaper company has set a code of ethics that its reporters must follow.","furigana":"しんぶんしゃ|きしゃ|まも|りんりこうりょう|さだ"}],"notes":"綱領 are the principles or program of an organization, above all the basic platform of a party: 党の綱領, 綱領を採択する/改定する, 綱領に掲げる. Outside politics it appears in 倫理綱領 (\"code of ethics\") of professional associations or newspapers. Compared with 公約 (\"campaign pledge\") and マニフェスト, 綱領 is long-term and fundamental; the word is formal and written.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新党の結党大会が盛大に行われた。","romaji":"Shintō no kettō taikai ga seidai ni okonawareta.","german":"The founding convention of the new party was held on a grand scale.","furigana":"しんとう|けっとうたいかい|せいだい|おこな"},{"japanese":"その党は結党からわずか十年で政権を取った。","romaji":"Sono tō wa kettō kara wazuka jūnen de seiken o totta.","german":"The party took power only ten years after its founding.","furigana":"とう|けっとう|じゅうねん|せいけん|と"},{"japanese":"彼らは消費税の廃止を掲げて結党した。","romaji":"Karera wa shōhizei no haishi o kakagete kettō shita.","german":"They founded a party under the banner of abolishing the consumption tax.","furigana":"かれ|しょうひぜい|はいし|かか|けっとう"}],"notes":"結党 is the founding of a political party; as a する-verb it is mostly intransitive (新党が結党する), but also transitive (新党を結党する). Typical: 結党大会, 結党以来, 結党〇周年. The opposite is 解党 (\"dissolution of a party\"). For groups in general, one says 結成する; 結党 is limited to parties and belongs to the language of the news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"方針の違いから三名が離党を表明した。","romaji":"Hōshin no chigai kara sanmei ga ritō o hyōmei shita.","german":"Three members announced their departure from the party because of differences in policy.","furigana":"ほうしん|ちが|さんめい|りとう|ひょうめい"},{"japanese":"彼は党本部に離党届を提出した。","romaji":"Kare wa tō honbu ni ritō todoke o teishutsu shita.","german":"He submitted his resignation from the party to the party headquarters.","furigana":"かれ|とうほんぶ|りとうとどけ|ていしゅつ"},{"japanese":"離党した議員は、次の選挙に無所属で立候補した。","romaji":"Ritō shita giin wa, tsugi no senkyo ni mushozoku de rikkōho shita.","german":"The lawmaker who left the party ran as an independent in the next election.","furigana":"りとう|ぎいん|つぎ|せんきょ|むしょぞく|りっこうほ"}],"notes":"離党 is voluntarily leaving a party; as a する-verb it is intransitive, yet the party still takes を: 自民党を離党する. Typical: 離党届を出す, 離党を表明する, 離党に追い込まれる. Equivalent in meaning, but sounding somewhat harsher, is 脱党; expulsion by the party is called 除名. The opposite is 入党.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"若者の入党が増加傾向にある。","romaji":"Wakamono no nyūtō ga zōka keikō ni aru.","german":"The number of young people joining parties is on an upward trend.","furigana":"わかもの|にゅうとう|ぞうかけいこう"},{"japanese":"彼は大学生のときに共産党に入党した。","romaji":"Kare wa daigakusei no toki ni Kyōsantō ni nyūtō shita.","german":"He joined the Communist Party when he was a university student.","furigana":"かれ|だいがくせい|きょうさんとう|にゅうとう"},{"japanese":"入党するには、毎年党費を払う必要がある。","romaji":"Nyūtō suru ni wa, maitoshi tōhi o harau hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"To join the party, you have to pay dues every year.","furigana":"にゅうとう|まいとしとうひ|はら|ひつよう"}],"notes":"入党 is joining a political party; as a する-verb it is intransitive with に: 共産党に入党する. Typical: 入党を申し込む, 入党手続き, 入党を勧める. The opposite is 離党; for associations and clubs one says 入会 in general. The word belongs to the language of politics and the news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二つの政党が連立政権を樹立した。","romaji":"Futatsu no seitō ga renritsu seiken o juritsu shita.","german":"Two political parties formed a coalition government.","furigana":"ふた|せいとう|れんりつせいけん|じゅりつ"},{"japanese":"与党は野党の一部と連立を組んだ。","romaji":"Yotō wa yatō no ichibu to renritsu o kunda.","german":"The ruling party formed a coalition with part of the opposition.","furigana":"よとう|やとう|いちぶ|れんりつ|く"},{"japanese":"数学の授業で連立方程式を解いた。","romaji":"Sūgaku no jugyō de renritsu hōteishiki o toita.","german":"We solved simultaneous equations in math class.","furigana":"すうがく|じゅぎょう|れんりつほうていしき|と"}],"notes":"連立 in politics means \"coalition\": 連立政権, 連立を組む, 連立与党, 連立を離脱する (\"to leave the coalition\"). The basic meaning is \"to exist side by side\", hence also the mathematical term 連立方程式 (\"system of equations\"). As a する-verb it is intransitive and rare; it mostly appears as a noun or in compounds. A looser alliance is called 連携 or 協力.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"市民が政府の決定に対して抗議行動を起こした。","romaji":"Shimin ga seifu no kettei ni taishite kōgi kōdō o okoshita.","german":"Citizens staged a protest against the government's decision.","furigana":"しみん|せいふ|けってい|たい|こうぎこうどう|お"},{"japanese":"審判の判定に、監督が激しく抗議した。","romaji":"Shinpan no hantei ni, kantoku ga hageshiku kōgi shita.","german":"The manager protested vehemently against the umpire's call.","furigana":"しんぱん|はんてい|かんとく|はげ|こうぎ"},{"japanese":"外務省は相手国に厳重に抗議した。","romaji":"Gaimushō wa aitekoku ni genjū ni kōgi shita.","german":"The Ministry of Foreign Affairs lodged a strong protest with the other country.","furigana":"がいむしょう|あいてこく|げんじゅう|こうぎ"}],"notes":"抗議 is a protest or objection against a decision or action; as a する-verb it mostly takes に: 審判に抗議する, 決定に抗議する. Typical: 抗議デモ, 抗議文, 厳重に抗議する (diplomatic), 抗議が殺到する. To be distinguished are 反対 (\"being against\", without an active complaint), 異議 (\"formal objection\", 異議あり) and the colloquial 文句を言う (\"to complain, grumble\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大規模なデモが国会議事堂前で行われた。","romaji":"Daikibo na demo ga Kokkai Gijidō mae de okonawareta.","german":"A large demonstration took place in front of the Diet building.","furigana":"だいきぼ|こっかいぎじどうまえ|おこな"},{"japanese":"店員が新しいカメラのデモをしてくれました。","romaji":"Ten'in ga atarashii kamera no demo o shite kuremashita.","german":"The shop assistant gave me a demo of the new camera.","furigana":"てんいん|あたら"}],"notes":"デモ can refer to a protest rally or to a demonstration. In front of a parliament building with many participants, the protest is usually meant. With an app or a device, on the other hand, デモ can show how something works. The katakana form belongs to these nouns; the similar-sounding でも is used differently as a grammatical connective.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"憲法改正には国民投票で過半数の賛成が必要だ。","romaji":"Kenpō kaisei ni wa kokumin tōhyō de kahansū no sansei ga hitsuyō da.","german":"A constitutional amendment requires a majority in favor in a national referendum.","furigana":"けんぽうかいせい|こくみんとうひょう|かはんすう|さんせい|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"イギリスは国民投票でEU離脱を決めた。","romaji":"Igirisu wa kokumin tōhyō de EU ridatsu o kimeta.","german":"The United Kingdom decided to leave the EU by referendum.","furigana":"こくみんとうひょう|りだつ|き"},{"japanese":"日本ではまだ一度も国民投票が行われたことがない。","romaji":"Nihon de wa mada ichido mo kokumin tōhyō ga okonawareta koto ga nai.","german":"A national referendum has never yet been held in Japan.","furigana":"にほん|いちど|こくみんとうひょう|おこな"}],"notes":"国民投票 is a nationwide referendum. In Japan it is provided for only for constitutional amendments (憲法改正の国民投票) and has never been carried out so far; votes at the municipal or prefectural level are called 住民投票. Typical: 国民投票を行う/実施する, 国民投票にかける, 国民投票で可決/否決される. 国民投票 is also used for referendums in other countries.","levelNote":"Compound: 国民 (N3) + 投票 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"スイスは直接民主制を採用している代表的な国だ。","romaji":"Suisu wa chokusetsu minshusei o saiyō shite iru daihyōteki na kuni da.","german":"Switzerland is a typical country that employs direct democracy.","furigana":"ちょくせつみんしゅせい|さいよう|だいひょうてき|くに"},{"japanese":"古代アテネでは直接民主制が行われていた。","romaji":"Kodai Atene de wa chokusetsu minshusei ga okonawarete ita.","german":"Direct democracy was practiced in ancient Athens.","furigana":"こだい|ちょくせつみんしゅせい|おこな"},{"japanese":"住民投票は、直接民主制の考え方を取り入れた制度だ。","romaji":"Jūmin tōhyō wa, chokusetsu minshusei no kangaekata o toriireta seido da.","german":"The residents' referendum is a system that incorporates the idea of direct democracy.","furigana":"じゅうみんとうひょう|ちょくせつみんしゅせい|かんが|かた|と|い|せいど"}],"notes":"直接民主制 is \"direct democracy\" as a system of state or government in which citizens themselves decide on substantive questions; the counterpart is 間接民主制. 直接民主主義 refers more to the idea or principle, while 制 stresses the institution. Typical combinations are 直接民主制を採用する/取り入れる; in Japan, 住民投票 (\"residents' referendum\") and リコール (\"recall\") are regarded as elements of it. A technical word from politics and civics.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本は間接民主制を基本とする国家である。","romaji":"Nihon wa kansetsu minshusei o kihon to suru kokka de aru.","german":"Japan is a state based on indirect democracy.","furigana":"にほん|かんせつみんしゅせい|きほん|こっか"},{"japanese":"間接民主制では、国民は選挙で選んだ代表を通じて政治に参加する。","romaji":"Kansetsu minshusei de wa, kokumin wa senkyo de eranda daihyō o tsūjite seiji ni sanka suru.","german":"In a representative democracy, the people take part in politics through representatives they elect.","furigana":"かんせつみんしゅせい|こくみん|せんきょ|えら|だいひょう|つう|せいじ|さんか"},{"japanese":"人口の多い国では、間接民主制を採るのが一般的だ。","romaji":"Jinkō no ōi kuni de wa, kansetsu minshusei o toru no ga ippanteki da.","german":"In countries with large populations, it is common to adopt representative democracy.","furigana":"じんこう|おお|くに|かんせつみんしゅせい|と|いっぱんてき"}],"notes":"間接民主制 is \"indirect\" or \"representative democracy\": the people elect representatives who decide in parliament. Equivalent are 代議制 and 議会制民主主義; the counterpart is 直接民主制. Typical are 間接民主制を採る/基本とする. The word belongs to the language of politics, law and textbooks; in conversation one rather says 選挙で代表を選ぶ仕組み.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"普通選挙の実現は民主主義の発展に不可欠だった。","romaji":"Futsū senkyo no jitsugen wa minshushugi no hatten ni fukaketsu datta.","german":"The realization of universal suffrage was essential to the development of democracy.","furigana":"ふつうせんきょ|じつげん|みんしゅしゅぎ|はってん|ふかけつ"},{"japanese":"日本で初めて男子の普通選挙が行われたのは1928年だ。","romaji":"Nihon de hajimete danshi no futsū senkyo ga okonawareta no wa 1928-nen da.","german":"The first election with universal male suffrage in Japan was held in 1928.","furigana":"にほん|はじ|だんし|ふつうせんきょ|おこな|ねん"},{"japanese":"当時は女性に選挙権がなく、本当の普通選挙とは言えなかった。","romaji":"Tōji wa josei ni senkyoken ga naku, hontō no futsū senkyo to wa ienakatta.","german":"At that time women had no right to vote, so it could not be called true universal suffrage.","furigana":"とうじ|じょせい|せんきょけん|ほんとう|ふつうせんきょ|い"}],"notes":"普通選挙 is an election in which the right to vote is not tied to property, tax payments or education; the opposite term is 制限選挙. In Japan it was introduced for men aged 25 and over with the 普通選挙法 in 1925; women received the right to vote only in 1945. Abbreviated, one says 普選, e.g. in 普選運動. The word occurs mainly in history and political science.","levelNote":"Compound: 普通 (N5) + 選挙 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"比例代表制により少数政党も議席を獲得できる。","romaji":"Hirei daihyōsei ni yori shōsū seitō mo giseki o kakutoku dekiru.","german":"Under the proportional representation system, even small parties can win seats.","furigana":"ひれいだいひょうせい|しょうすうせいとう|ぎせき|かくとく"},{"japanese":"彼は選挙区で負けたが、比例代表で復活当選した。","romaji":"Kare wa senkyoku de maketa ga, hirei daihyō de fukkatsu tōsen shita.","german":"He lost in his district but was revived through proportional representation.","furigana":"かれ|せんきょく|ま|ひれいだいひょう|ふっかつとうせん"},{"japanese":"衆議院の比例代表では、政党名を書いて投票する。","romaji":"Shūgiin no hirei daihyō de wa, seitōmei o kaite tōhyō suru.","german":"In proportional representation for the House of Representatives, you write the name of a party on the ballot.","furigana":"しゅうぎいん|ひれいだいひょう|せいとうめい|か|とうひょう"}],"notes":"比例代表 is proportional representation, where seats are distributed according to the parties' share of the vote; often shortened to 比例 or 比例区. In the Japanese lower house, it is combined with single-member districts (小選挙区比例代表並立制): someone who loses in the district can still get into parliament via the party list (比例で復活当選). Typical: 比例代表制, 比例名簿, 比例代表で当選する.","levelNote":"Compound: 比例 (N2) + 代表 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"小選挙区制では一つの選挙区から一人が当選する。","romaji":"Shōsenkyokusei de wa hitotsu no senkyoku kara hitori ga tōsen suru.","german":"Under the single-member district system, one candidate is elected from each district.","furigana":"しょうせんきょくせい|ひと|せんきょく|ひとり|とうせん"},{"japanese":"小選挙区では、わずかな票の差で勝敗が決まることもある。","romaji":"Shōsenkyoku de wa, wazuka na hyō no sa de shōhai ga kimaru koto mo aru.","german":"In single-member districts, a small margin of votes can sometimes decide victory or defeat.","furigana":"しょうせんきょく|ひょう|さ|しょうはい|き"},{"japanese":"衆議院は小選挙区比例代表並立制を採用している。","romaji":"Shūgiin wa shōsenkyoku hirei daihyō heiritsusei o saiyō shite iru.","german":"The House of Representatives uses a parallel system of single-member districts and proportional representation.","furigana":"しゅうぎいん|しょうせんきょくひれいだいひょうへいりつせい|さいよう"}],"notes":"小選挙区 is an electoral district in which only one seat is awarded; the candidate with the most votes wins (小選挙区制). The opposites are 中選挙区 and 大選挙区 with several seats. Since 1996, the lower house has been elected under the 小選挙区比例代表並立制. Typical: 小選挙区で当選する/敗れる, 小選挙区に立候補する. The seat won is rather called 小選挙区の議席.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"気候変動に関する議定書が採択された。","romaji":"Kikō hendō ni kansuru giteisho ga saitaku sareta.","german":"Das Protokoll zum Klimawandel wurde verabschiedet.","furigana":"きこうへんどう|かん|ぎていしょ|さいたく"},{"japanese":"京都議定書は1997年に採択された。","romaji":"Kyōto giteisho wa 1997-nen ni saitaku sareta.","german":"Das Kyoto-Protokoll wurde 1997 verabschiedet.","furigana":"きょうとぎていしょ|ねん|さいたく"},{"japanese":"両国の代表が議定書に署名した。","romaji":"Ryōkoku no daihyō ga giteisho ni shomei shita.","german":"Die Vertreter beider Länder unterzeichneten das Protokoll.","furigana":"りょうこく|だいひょう|ぎていしょ|しょめい"}],"notes":"議定書 ist ein völkerrechtliches Protokoll, also ein Zusatz- oder Ergänzungsabkommen zu einem Vertrag, z. B. 京都議定書. Typisch: 議定書に署名する, 議定書を採択する/批准する. Vorsicht: Das deutsche „Protokoll“ im Sinn von Sitzungsniederschrift heißt 議事録, das diplomatische Zeremoniell 儀礼 oder プロトコル. Das Wort ist rein formell und kommt fast nur in Diplomatie und Nachrichten vor.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"武器の禁輸措置が発動された。","romaji":"Buki no kin'yu sochi ga hatsudō sareta.","german":"An arms embargo was imposed.","furigana":"ぶき|きんゆそち|はつどう"},{"japanese":"病気の発生を受けて、牛肉の禁輸が三年間続いた。","romaji":"Byōki no hassei o ukete, gyūniku no kin'yu ga sannenkan tsuzuita.","german":"After the outbreak of the disease, an import ban on beef continued for three years.","furigana":"びょうき|はっせい|う|ぎゅうにく|きんゆ|さんねんかんつづ"},{"japanese":"交渉がまとまり、石油の禁輸がようやく解除された。","romaji":"Kōshō ga matomari, sekiyu no kin'yu ga yōyaku kaijo sareta.","german":"The negotiations came together, and the oil embargo was finally lifted.","furigana":"こうしょう|せきゆ|きんゆ|かいじょ"}],"notes":"禁輸 is the formal word for an embargo, i.e. a state ban on the export or import of certain goods. It mostly appears in fixed combinations such as 禁輸措置 \"embargo measure\", 武器禁輸 \"arms embargo\" or 石油禁輸 \"oil embargo\"; typical verbs are 禁輸措置を発動する \"to impose\" and 禁輸を解除する \"to lift\". More neutral and concrete are 輸出禁止 \"export ban\" and 輸入禁止 \"import ban\". Newspaper and political language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"被災地に救援物資が届けられた。","romaji":"Hisaichi ni kyūen busshi ga todokerareta.","german":"Relief supplies were delivered to the disaster area.","furigana":"ひさいち|きゅうえんぶっし|とど"},{"japanese":"地震の後、水や食料などの物資が不足した。","romaji":"Jishin no ato, mizu ya shokuryō nado no busshi ga fusoku shita.","german":"After the earthquake, there was a shortage of supplies such as water and food.","furigana":"じしん|あと|みず|しょくりょう|ぶっし|ふそく"},{"japanese":"船で島に生活物資を運んでいる。","romaji":"Fune de shima ni seikatsu busshi o hakonde iru.","german":"Daily necessities are carried to the island by ship.","furigana":"ふね|しま|せいかつぶっし|はこ"}],"notes":"物資 refers to goods and materials as supplies, i.e. what is distributed, transported or stored - not the individual product in a shop (for that, 商品). Typical are 救援物資 or 支援物資 \"relief supplies\", 生活物資 \"daily necessities\" and 軍事物資 \"military supplies\". Common verbs: 物資を届ける/輸送する, 物資が不足する. Unlike 資源 \"(natural) resources\", 物資 means goods that are already available and transportable.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"戦時中は食料の配給制度が実施されていた。","romaji":"Senjichū wa shokuryō no haikyū seido ga jisshi sarete ita.","german":"During the war, a food rationing system was in effect.","furigana":"せんじちゅう|しょくりょう|はいきゅうせいど|じっし"},{"japanese":"この映画は大手の会社が配給している。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa ōte no kaisha ga haikyū shite iru.","german":"This film is distributed by a major company.","furigana":"えいが|おおて|かいしゃ|はいきゅう"},{"japanese":"避難所では一人一個ずつおにぎりが配給された。","romaji":"Hinanjo de wa hitori ikko zutsu onigiri ga haikyū sareta.","german":"At the shelter, one rice ball was handed out to each person.","furigana":"ひなんじょ|ひとりいっこ|はいきゅう"}],"notes":"配給 has two main meanings: the allotment of scarce goods (rationing, 食料の配給, 配給制) and, in the film business, distribution (映画を配給する, 配給会社 \"film distributor\"). As a する-verb it is transitive: 〜を配給する. For ordinary handing out, one says 配る in everyday life; 配布 is the distribution of flyers or documents, 配達 is delivery. The rationing sense sounds of wartime and postwar times or of emergencies.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"公営住宅の入居者を募集している。","romaji":"Kōei jūtaku no nyūkyosha o boshū shite iru.","german":"Applicants are being recruited for public housing.","furigana":"こうえいじゅうたく|にゅうきょしゃ|ぼしゅう"},{"japanese":"競馬や競艇は公営ギャンブルと呼ばれている。","romaji":"Keiba ya kyōtei wa kōei gyanburu to yobarete iru.","german":"Horse racing and boat racing are called publicly run gambling.","furigana":"けいば|きょうてい|こうえい|よ"},{"japanese":"この町では公営のバスが一時間に一本しか走っていない。","romaji":"Kono machi de wa kōei no basu ga ichijikan ni ippon shika hashitte inai.","german":"In this town, the municipal bus runs only once an hour.","furigana":"まち|こうえい|いちじかん|いっぽん|はし"}],"notes":"公営 means \"operated by the public sector\", usually by a municipality or prefecture, and almost always stands as a modifier before a noun: 公営住宅 \"public housing\", 公営ギャンブル (horse, boat and bicycle racing), 公営バス, 公営プール. The counterparts are 民営 \"privately operated\" and 国営 \"operated by the central government\"; 市営, 都営 or 県営 name the specific level of operator. Formal administrative vocabulary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"郵政民営化は大きな政治課題だった。","romaji":"Yūsei min'eika wa ōkina seiji kadai datta.","german":"The privatization of the postal service was a major political issue.","furigana":"ゆうせいみんえいか|おお|せいじかだい"},{"japanese":"国鉄は1987年に民営化され、JRになった。","romaji":"Kokutetsu wa 1987-nen ni min'eika sare, JR ni natta.","german":"Japanese National Railways was privatized in 1987 and became JR.","furigana":"こくてつ|ねん|みんえいか"},{"japanese":"水道事業を民営化することには反対の声も多い。","romaji":"Suidō jigyō o min'eika suru koto ni wa hantai no koe mo ōi.","german":"There are many voices opposed to privatizing the water supply.","furigana":"すいどうじぎょう|みんえいか|はんたい|こえ|おお"}],"notes":"民営化 is the transfer of state or public enterprises into private hands; as a transitive する-verb: 〜を民営化する, passive 〜が民営化される. Well-known cases in Japan are the national railways (国鉄 → JR, 1987) and the post (郵政民営化). The opposite is 国有化 \"nationalization\"; the result is called 民営 \"privately operated\", as opposed to 公営 and 国営. Language of political and economic news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"経営不振の三セクが清算された。","romaji":"Keiei fushin no san seku ga seisan sareta.","german":"Das schlecht laufende halbstaatliche Unternehmen wurde abgewickelt.","furigana":"けいえいふしん|さん|せいさん"},{"japanese":"この地方の鉄道は、JRから三セクに経営が移った。","romaji":"Kono chihō no tetsudō wa, JR kara san seku ni keiei ga utsutta.","german":"Der Betrieb dieser Regionalbahn ging von JR auf eine halbstaatliche Gesellschaft über.","furigana":"ちほう|てつどう|さん|けいえい|うつ"},{"japanese":"三セクが運営するテーマパークは赤字続きだ。","romaji":"San seku ga un'ei suru tēma pāku wa akaji tsuzuki da.","german":"Der von einer halbstaatlichen Gesellschaft betriebene Freizeitpark schreibt ständig rote Zahlen.","furigana":"さん|うんえい|あかじつづ"}],"notes":"三セク ist die journalistische Kurzform von 第三セクター: Unternehmen, die gemeinsam von der öffentlichen Hand (erster Sektor) und der Privatwirtschaft (zweiter Sektor) getragen werden. Bekannt sind 三セク鉄道, regionale Bahnlinien, oft ehemalige JR-Strecken, sowie Freizeitanlagen aus der Bubble-Zeit, von denen viele hoch verschuldet sind (三セクの破綻). Achtung: Anders als der deutsche „Dritte Sektor“ meint es keine gemeinnützigen Organisationen (dafür NPO). Häufig in Schlagzeilen und Verwaltungstexten.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"公園の管理を指定管理者に委ねた。","romaji":"Kōen no kanri o shitei kanrisha ni yudaneta.","german":"The management of the park was entrusted to a designated manager.","furigana":"こうえん|かんり|していかんりしゃ|ゆだ"},{"japanese":"この図書館は指定管理者制度を導入している。","romaji":"Kono toshokan wa shitei kanrisha seido o dōnyū shite iru.","german":"This library has introduced the designated manager system.","furigana":"としょかん|していかんりしゃせいど|どうにゅう"},{"japanese":"市は体育館の指定管理者として地元のNPOを選んだ。","romaji":"Shi wa taiikukan no shitei kanrisha to shite jimoto no NPO o eranda.","german":"The city chose a local NPO as the designated manager of the gymnasium.","furigana":"し|たいいくかん|していかんりしゃ|じもと|えら"}],"notes":"指定管理者 is a technical term of Japanese local government law: a company, NPO or similar body commissioned by the municipality to operate a public facility such as a library, gymnasium or park. The system is called 指定管理者制度 (since 2003). Typical: 〜を指定管理者に選定する, 指定管理者が運営する. One reads the word on signs and in tender notices; in conversation one rather says 民間の会社が運営している.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"個人情報保護法が改正された。","romaji":"Kojin jōhō hogohō ga kaisei sareta.","german":"Das Datenschutzgesetz wurde geändert.","furigana":"こじんじょうほうほごほう|かいせい"},{"japanese":"個人情報保護の観点から、住所はお教えできません。","romaji":"Kojin jōhō hogo no kanten kara, jūsho wa ooshie dekimasen.","german":"Aus Datenschutzgründen können wir Ihnen die Adresse nicht mitteilen.","furigana":"こじんじょうほうほご|かんてん|じゅうしょ|おし"},{"japanese":"当社は個人情報保護方針に基づいてデータを管理しています。","romaji":"Tōsha wa kojin jōhō hogo hōshin ni motozuite dēta o kanri shite imasu.","german":"Unser Unternehmen verwaltet Daten gemäß seiner Datenschutzerklärung.","furigana":"とうしゃ|こじんじょうほうほごほうしん|もと|かんり"}],"notes":"個人情報保護 ist der Oberbegriff für den Schutz personenbezogener Daten und entspricht dem deutschen „Datenschutz“. Meist steht es in Zusammensetzungen: 個人情報保護法 „Datenschutzgesetz“, 個人情報保護方針 „Datenschutzerklärung“, 個人情報保護委員会 (die Aufsichtsbehörde). Im Alltag sagt man oft nur 個人情報の保護 oder 個人情報の取り扱い; auf Websites heißt die Datenschutzerklärung auch プライバシーポリシー.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"違反業者に対して行政処分が下された。","romaji":"Ihan gyōsha ni taishite gyōsei shobun ga kudasareta.","german":"An administrative sanction was imposed on the offending business.","furigana":"いはんぎょうしゃ|たい|ぎょうせいしょぶん|くだ"},{"japanese":"飲酒運転で、刑事罰とは別に免許取り消しの行政処分を受けた。","romaji":"Inshu unten de, keijibatsu to wa betsu ni menkyo torikeshi no gyōsei shobun o uketa.","german":"For drunk driving, he received an administrative sanction of license revocation, separate from the criminal penalty.","furigana":"いんしゅうんてん|けいじばつ|べつ|めんきょと|け|ぎょうせいしょぶん|う"},{"japanese":"県は食中毒を出した店に営業停止の行政処分を行った。","romaji":"Ken wa shokuchūdoku o dashita mise ni eigyō teishi no gyōsei shobun o okonatta.","german":"The prefecture issued an administrative order suspending business at the restaurant that caused food poisoning.","furigana":"けん|しょくちゅうどく|だ|みせ|えいぎょうていし|ぎょうせいしょぶん|おこな"}],"notes":"行政処分 is a binding measure by an authority toward a person or company, in everyday life above all sanctions such as business suspension, license revocation or, for traffic violations, 免許停止. Typical verbs: 行政処分を下す/行う/受ける. It is to be distinguished from the criminal 刑事処分; both can occur in parallel for the same violation. A non-binding recommendation by an authority, by contrast, is called 行政指導.","levelNote":"Compound: 行政 (N1) + 処分 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"省庁が企業に対して行政指導を行った。","romaji":"Shōchō ga kigyō ni taishite gyōsei shidō o okonatta.","german":"The ministry issued administrative guidance to the company.","furigana":"しょうちょう|きぎょう|たい|ぎょうせいしどう|おこな"},{"japanese":"不適切な広告について、消費者庁から行政指導を受けた。","romaji":"Futekisetsu na kōkoku ni tsuite, Shōhishachō kara gyōsei shidō o uketa.","german":"The firm received administrative guidance from the Consumer Affairs Agency regarding inappropriate advertising.","furigana":"ふてきせつ|こうこく|しょうひしゃちょう|ぎょうせいしどう|う"},{"japanese":"行政指導には法的な強制力はない。","romaji":"Gyōsei shidō ni wa hōteki na kyōseiryoku wa nai.","german":"Administrative guidance has no legal force.","furigana":"ぎょうせいしどう|ほうてき|きょうせいりょく"}],"notes":"行政指導 consists of recommendations, admonitions or instructions from an authority that are not legally binding but traditionally have great influence on companies in Japan. Typical: 行政指導を行う/受ける, 行政指導に従う. In contrast to 行政処分, a binding administrative act, it is formally based on voluntariness; but those who do not comply often risk a later 行政処分. Technical language from administration and business news.","levelNote":"Compound: 行政 (N1) + 指導 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"国家戦略特区で規制緩和が試行されている。","romaji":"Kokka senryaku tokku de kisei kanwa ga shikō sarete iru.","german":"Deregulation is being tried out in the National Strategic Special Zone.","furigana":"こっかせんりゃくとっく|きせいかんわ|しこう"},{"japanese":"この町は「どぶろく特区」に指定され、農家が酒を造れるようになった。","romaji":"Kono machi wa 'doburoku tokku' ni shitei sare, nōka ga sake o tsukureru yō ni natta.","german":"The town was designated a \"doburoku special zone\", allowing farmers to brew their own rice wine.","furigana":"まち|とっく|してい|のうか|さけ|つく"},{"japanese":"深圳は中国で最初の経済特区の一つだ。","romaji":"Shinsen wa Chūgoku de saisho no keizai tokku no hitotsu da.","german":"Shenzhen is one of China's first special economic zones.","furigana":"|ちゅうごく|さいしょ|けいざいとっく|ひと"}],"notes":"特区 is a short form for various special zones: in Japan it mainly means 構造改革特区 and 国家戦略特区, in which regulations are loosened on a trial basis; for China, 経済特区 \"special economic zone\". Typical: 〜を特区に指定する, 特区で〜を認める. Less often, 特区 is also short for 特別区, the wards of Tokyo; for Hong Kong and Macau, the full form 特別行政区 is more common. A word from political and business news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地方分権改革により自治体の権限が拡大された。","romaji":"Chihō bunken kaikaku ni yori jichitai no kengen ga kakudai sareta.","german":"Powers of local governments were expanded by the decentralization reform.","furigana":"ちほうぶんけんかいかく|じちたい|けんげん|かくだい"},{"japanese":"地方分権を進めるには、税源の移譲が欠かせない。","romaji":"Chihō bunken o susumeru ni wa, zeigen no ijō ga kakasenai.","german":"To advance decentralization, the transfer of tax sources is indispensable.","furigana":"ちほうぶんけん|すす|ぜいげん|いじょう|か"},{"japanese":"日本は長く中央集権の国だったが、地方分権を求める声が強まっている。","romaji":"Nihon wa nagaku chūō shūken no kuni datta ga, chihō bunken o motomeru koe ga tsuyomatte iru.","german":"Japan was long a centralized country, but calls for decentralization are growing louder.","furigana":"にほん|なが|ちゅうおうしゅうけん|くに|ちほうぶんけん|もと|こえ|つよ"}],"notes":"地方分権 refers to the shift of powers and financial resources from the central government to prefectures and municipalities; the opposite is 中央集権 \"centralism\". Typical combinations: 地方分権を進める/推進する, 地方分権改革. Related is 地方自治 \"local self-government\": that is the principle, while 地方分権 is rather the process or the political demand. Language of politics and administration.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"道州制の導入を巡って議論が行われている。","romaji":"Dōshūsei no dōnyū o megutte giron ga okonawarete iru.","german":"Debate is under way over the introduction of the regional system.","furigana":"どうしゅうせい|どうにゅう|めぐ|ぎろん|おこな"},{"japanese":"道州制になれば、県という単位はなくなるかもしれない。","romaji":"Dōshūsei ni nareba, ken to iu tan'i wa nakunaru kamoshirenai.","german":"With a regional system, the prefecture might disappear as a unit.","furigana":"どうしゅうせい|けん|たんい"},{"japanese":"知事の中には道州制に慎重な人も多い。","romaji":"Chiji no naka ni wa dōshūsei ni shinchō na hito mo ōi.","german":"Many governors are cautious about the regional system.","furigana":"ちじ|なか|どうしゅうせい|しんちょう|ひと|おお"}],"notes":"道州制 is a reform plan, discussed for decades but never implemented, to combine the 47 prefectures into a few large regions (about 9 to 13, depending on the draft) to be called 道 or 州 - modeled on 北海道. The regions are to receive far-reaching self-government; this recalls a federal state, but it would not be true federalism. Typical: 道州制を導入する, 道州制の是非. A purely political technical term.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"首長が住民との対話集会を開催した。","romaji":"Shuchō ga jūmin to no taiwa shūkai o kaisai shita.","german":"The head of the local government held a dialogue meeting with residents.","furigana":"しゅちょう|じゅうみん|たいわしゅうかい|かいさい"},{"japanese":"統一地方選挙で、多くの自治体の首長が交代した。","romaji":"Tōitsu chihō senkyo de, ōku no jichitai no shuchō ga kōtai shita.","german":"In the unified local elections, the heads of many municipalities changed.","furigana":"とういつちほうせんきょ|おお|じちたい|しゅちょう|こうたい"},{"japanese":"アラブ首長国連邦の首都はアブダビだ。","romaji":"Arabu shuchōkoku renpō no shuto wa Abudabi da.","german":"The capital of the United Arab Emirates is Abu Dhabi.","furigana":"しゅちょうこくれんぽう|しゅと"}],"notes":"首長 in Japan is above all the collective term for the elected heads of local governments: the 知事 of prefectures and the 市長, 町長 and 村長 of municipalities. In general, it means the \"head\" of an organization and also \"emir\", e.g. in アラブ首長国連邦 \"United Arab Emirates\". Because しゅちょう sounds exactly like 主張 \"assertion\", officials and journalists often say くびちょう. Formal administrative and media language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新知事が就任演説で改革を訴えた。","romaji":"Shin chiji ga shūnin enzetsu de kaikaku o uttaeta.","german":"The new governor called for reforms in his inaugural speech.","furigana":"しんちじ|しゅうにんえんぜつ|かいかく|うった"},{"japanese":"来月、県知事選挙が行われる。","romaji":"Raigetsu, ken chiji senkyo ga okonawareru.","german":"The prefectural governor election will be held next month.","furigana":"らいげつ|けんちじせんきょ|おこな"},{"japanese":"知事は記者会見で、災害対策の予算を増やすと発表した。","romaji":"Chiji wa kisha kaiken de, saigai taisaku no yosan o fuyasu to happyō shita.","german":"The governor announced at a press conference that the budget for disaster preparedness would be increased.","furigana":"ちじ|きしゃかいけん|さいがいたいさく|よさん|ふ|はっぴょう"}],"notes":"知事 is the directly elected governor of a prefecture: Tokyo has a 都知事, Hokkaido a 道知事, Osaka and Kyoto each a 府知事, all the others a 県知事. With a name one says 〇〇知事. Typical combinations: 知事選 \"gubernatorial election\", 知事に就任する, 知事が〜を表明する. At the municipal level the counterpart is 市長 or 町長; the umbrella term for both is 首長.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"法案が本会議で採決に付された。","romaji":"Hōan ga honkaigi de saiketsu ni fusareta.","german":"The bill was put to a vote in the plenary session.","furigana":"ほうあん|ほんかいぎ|さいけつ|ふ"},{"japanese":"法案は委員会で可決され、明日の本会議に送られる。","romaji":"Hōan wa iinkai de kaketsu sare, ashita no honkaigi ni okurareru.","german":"The bill was passed in committee and will be sent to tomorrow's plenary session.","furigana":"ほうあん|いいんかい|かけつ|あした|ほんかいぎ|おく"},{"japanese":"首相は参議院の本会議で野党の質問に答えた。","romaji":"Shushō wa Sangiin no honkaigi de yatō no shitsumon ni kotaeta.","german":"The prime minister answered questions from the opposition in the plenary session of the House of Councillors.","furigana":"しゅしょう|さんぎいん|ほんかいぎ|やとう|しつもん|こた"}],"notes":"本会議 is the full assembly of a parliament (衆議院, 参議院 or a local assembly), as opposed to the committees (委員会). The usual procedure: a bill is deliberated in 委員会 and then voted on in 本会議. Typical: 本会議で可決する/採決する, 本会議に上程する, 衆院本会議. A technical word of parliamentary reporting.","levelNote":"Compound: 本 (N5) + 会議 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"野党が審議打ち切りの動議を提出した。","romaji":"Yatō ga shingi uchikiri no dōgi o teishutsu shita.","german":"The opposition submitted a motion to end deliberations.","furigana":"やとう|しんぎう|き|どうぎ|ていしゅつ"},{"japanese":"株主総会で、ある株主が議長の交代を求める動議を出した。","romaji":"Kabunushi sōkai de, aru kabunushi ga gichō no kōtai o motomeru dōgi o dashita.","german":"At the shareholders' meeting, a shareholder moved to replace the chair.","furigana":"かぶぬしそうかい|かぶぬし|ぎちょう|こうたい|もと|どうぎ|だ"},{"japanese":"その修正動議は賛成少数で否決された。","romaji":"Sono shūsei dōgi wa sansei shōsū de hiketsu sareta.","german":"The amendment motion was rejected, with few in favor.","furigana":"しゅうせいどうぎ|さんせいしょうすう|ひけつ"}],"notes":"動議 is a formal motion made during a meeting (parliament, shareholders' meeting, club meeting), on which deliberation or a vote then takes place. Typical: 動議を提出する/出す, 動議が可決/否決される, 緊急動議 \"emergency motion\", 修正動議 \"motion to amend\". Unlike the general 提案 \"proposal\" or 議案 \"agenda item\", 動議 is a procedural or substantive motion made from within the ongoing meeting.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"法案に附帯決議が付されて可決された。","romaji":"Hōan ni futai ketsugi ga fusarete kaketsu sareta.","german":"The bill was passed with a supplementary resolution attached.","furigana":"ほうあん|ふたいけつぎ|ふ|かけつ"},{"japanese":"委員会は、プライバシーへの配慮を求める附帯決議を採択した。","romaji":"Iinkai wa, puraibashī e no hairyo o motomeru futai ketsugi o saitaku shita.","german":"The committee adopted a supplementary resolution calling for consideration of privacy.","furigana":"いいんかい|はいりょ|もと|ふたいけつぎ|さいたく"},{"japanese":"附帯決議には法的な拘束力がない。","romaji":"Futai ketsugi ni wa hōteki na kōsokuryoku ga nai.","german":"A supplementary resolution is not legally binding.","furigana":"ふたいけつぎ|ほうてき|こうそくりょく"}],"notes":"附帯決議 is a resolution that a parliamentary committee passes together with the adoption of a law, to record concerns, interpretive guidance or demands to the government. It is not legally binding, but carries political weight. Typical: 附帯決議を付す/付ける, 附帯決議を採択する. The spelling 付帯決議 is just as common; 附帯 is the traditional official form.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"野党が牛歩戦術で採決を遅らせようとした。","romaji":"Yatō ga gyūho senjutsu de saiketsu o okuraseyō to shita.","german":"The opposition tried to delay the vote with ox-walk tactics.","furigana":"やとう|ぎゅうほせんじゅつ|さいけつ|おく"},{"japanese":"野党議員たちは牛歩戦術をとり、投票に何時間もかけた。","romaji":"Yatō giin-tachi wa gyūho senjutsu o tori, tōhyō ni nanjikan mo kaketa.","german":"The opposition lawmakers adopted ox-walk tactics and took hours to cast their votes.","furigana":"やとうぎいん|ぎゅうほせんじゅつ|とうひょう|なんじかん"},{"japanese":"最近の国会では、牛歩戦術はあまり見られなくなった。","romaji":"Saikin no kokkai de wa, gyūho senjutsu wa amari mirarenaku natta.","german":"In the recent Diet, ox-walk tactics are hardly seen anymore.","furigana":"さいきん|こっかい|ぎゅうほせんじゅつ|み"}],"notes":"牛歩戦術 literally means \"ox-walk tactics\": in roll-call votes in the Japanese parliament, opposition lawmakers walk extremely slowly to the ballot box in order to drag out the vote. Typical: 牛歩戦術をとる, 牛歩戦術で抵抗する. Comparable is the filibuster (フィリバスター), which, however, works with long speeches. 牛歩 alone means \"very slow progress\". A political technical term, in news and commentary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"与党が強行採決で法案を通過させた。","romaji":"Yotō ga kyōkō saiketsu de hōan o tsūka saseta.","german":"The ruling party pushed the bill through with a forced vote.","furigana":"よとう|きょうこうさいけつ|ほうあん|つうか"},{"japanese":"与党は委員会で法案を強行採決した。","romaji":"Yotō wa iinkai de hōan o kyōkō saiketsu shita.","german":"The ruling party forced a vote on the bill in committee.","furigana":"よとう|いいんかい|ほうあん|きょうこうさいけつ"},{"japanese":"強行採決に抗議して、野党議員が委員長の席に詰め寄った。","romaji":"Kyōkō saiketsu ni kōgi shite, yatō giin ga iinchō no seki ni tsumeyotta.","german":"In protest against the forced vote, opposition lawmakers crowded around the committee chairman's seat.","furigana":"きょうこうさいけつ|こうぎ|やとうぎいん|いいんちょう|せき|つ|よ"}],"notes":"強行採決 is a vote that the majority pushes through despite protest and without sufficient deliberation - a critical term used mainly by the opposition and the media; the government side mostly avoids it. Typical: 〜を強行採決する, 強行採決に踏み切る, 法案を強行採決で可決する. Neutral is 採決 \"vote\"; 強行 means \"to push through by force\".","levelNote":"Compound: 強行 (N1) + 採決 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"政党助成金の使途に対する監視が求められている。","romaji":"Seitō joseikin no shito ni taisuru kanshi ga motomerarete iru.","german":"Monitoring of how public party funding is used is being demanded.","furigana":"せいとうじょせいきん|しと|たい|かんし|もと"},{"japanese":"共産党は政党助成金の受け取りを拒否している。","romaji":"Kyōsantō wa seitō joseikin no uketori o kyohi shite iru.","german":"The Communist Party refuses to accept public party funding.","furigana":"きょうさんとう|せいとうじょせいきん|う|と|きょひ"},{"japanese":"政党助成金は国民一人当たり二百五十円で計算されている。","romaji":"Seitō joseikin wa kokumin hitori atari nihyaku gojū en de keisan sarete iru.","german":"Public party funding is calculated at 250 yen per citizen.","furigana":"せいとうじょせいきん|こくみんひとりあ|にひゃくごじゅうえん|けいさん"}],"notes":"政党助成金 is the state subsidy to political parties under the 政党助成法 of 1995, financed from tax money (calculated as 250 yen per inhabitant). The official legal term is 政党交付金; 政党助成金 is the designation common in the media and in debates. Typical: 政党助成金を受け取る, 政党助成金の使い道. The Communist Party fundamentally refuses to accept it.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"政治資金規正法に基づいて収支報告書が提出された。","romaji":"Seiji shikin kiseihō ni motozuite shūshi hōkokusho ga teishutsu sareta.","german":"A financial report was submitted pursuant to the Political Funds Control Act.","furigana":"せいじしきんきせいほう|もと|しゅうしほうこくしょ|ていしゅつ"},{"japanese":"政治資金パーティーの収入を報告しなかった議員が批判された。","romaji":"Seiji shikin pātī no shūnyū o hōkoku shinakatta giin ga hihan sareta.","german":"Lawmakers who failed to report income from fundraising parties were criticized.","furigana":"せいじしきん|しゅうにゅう|ほうこく|ぎいん|ひはん"},{"japanese":"彼は政治資金を私的な旅行に使ったと報じられた。","romaji":"Kare wa seiji shikin o shiteki na ryokō ni tsukatta to hōjirareta.","german":"It was reported that he used political funds for private trips.","furigana":"かれ|せいじしきん|してき|りょこう|つか|ほう"}],"notes":"政治資金 is all money for political activity: donations, party dues, proceeds from fundraising events (政治資金パーティー) and state subsidies. Income and expenditure are regulated by the 政治資金規正法; a 政治資金収支報告書 must be filed each year. News often concerns scandals such as 政治資金の不記載 or 裏金. A single donation is called 献金.","levelNote":"Compound: 政治 (N5) + 資金 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"贈収賄事件で政治家と企業幹部が逮捕された。","romaji":"Zōshūwai jiken de seijika to kigyō kanbu ga taiho sareta.","german":"A politician and a company executive were arrested in the bribery case.","furigana":"ぞうしゅうわいじけん|せいじか|きぎょうかんぶ|たいほ"},{"japanese":"公共工事の入札をめぐって、贈収賄が行われていた疑いがある。","romaji":"Kōkyō kōji no nyūsatsu o megutte, zōshūwai ga okonawarete ita utagai ga aru.","german":"There is suspicion that bribery was involved in the bidding for public works.","furigana":"こうきょうこうじ|にゅうさつ|ぞうしゅうわい|おこな|うたが"},{"japanese":"贈収賄を防ぐため、公務員は業者からの贈り物を受け取ってはいけない。","romaji":"Zōshūwai o fusegu tame, kōmuin wa gyōsha kara no okurimono o uketotte wa ikenai.","german":"To prevent bribery, civil servants must not accept gifts from companies.","furigana":"ぞうしゅうわい|ふせ|こうむいん|ぎょうしゃ|おく|もの|う|と"}],"notes":"贈収賄 combines 贈賄 \"bribery\" (giving money) and 収賄 \"taking bribes\" (accepting money) and refers to the bribery case as a whole. Typical: 贈収賄事件, 贈収賄の疑いで逮捕される; the individual offenses are called 贈賄罪 and 収賄罪. The bribe itself is 賄賂 (賄賂を贈る/受け取る), colloquially 袖の下. Language of lawyers and the news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"労働委員会が労使紛争の斡旋を行った。","romaji":"Rōdō iinkai ga rōshi funsō no assen o okonatta.","german":"The Labor Relations Commission mediated in the labor dispute.","furigana":"ろうどういいんかい|ろうしふんそう|あっせん|おこな"},{"japanese":"大学のキャリアセンターが卒業生に仕事を斡旋している。","romaji":"Daigaku no kyaria sentā ga sotsugyōsei ni shigoto o assen shite iru.","german":"The university career center finds jobs for graduates.","furigana":"だいがく|そつぎょうせい|しごと|あっせん"},{"japanese":"知人の斡旋で、安くアパートを借りることができた。","romaji":"Chijin no assen de, yasuku apāto o kariru koto ga dekita.","german":"Through an acquaintance's arrangement, I was able to rent an apartment cheaply.","furigana":"ちじん|あっせん|やす|か"}],"notes":"斡旋 means to mediate between two sides or to procure something for someone: 仕事を斡旋する \"to arrange a job\", 労使紛争の斡旋 \"mediation in a labor dispute\". As a する-verb it is transitive (〜を斡旋する); the person arranging it takes の: 知人の斡旋で. Because of the difficult kanji, authorities and media often write あっせん. Unlike the neutral 仲介 (e.g. in real estate) or the mere 紹介 \"introducing\", 斡旋 stresses the active effort of the go-between.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"係争中の土地に新たな開発計画が浮上した。","romaji":"Keisōchū no tochi ni arata na kaihatsu keikaku ga fujō shita.","german":"A new development plan surfaced for the land in dispute.","furigana":"けいそうちゅう|とち|あら|かいはつけいかく|ふじょう"},{"japanese":"同社は元社員と係争中のため、コメントを控えた。","romaji":"Dōsha wa moto shain to keisōchū no tame, komento o hikaeta.","german":"The company declined to comment because it is in a legal dispute with a former employee.","furigana":"どうしゃ|もとしゃいん|けいそうちゅう|ひか"},{"japanese":"両国は国境の係争地をめぐって交渉を続けている。","romaji":"Ryōkoku wa kokkyō no keisōchi o megutte kōshō o tsuzukete iru.","german":"The two countries continue to negotiate over the disputed border area.","furigana":"りょうこく|こっきょう|けいそうち|こうしょう|つづ"}],"notes":"係争 refers to a dispute between parties, usually fought out in court. Most common is 係争中 \"in dispute, sub judice\": 係争中の土地, 〜と係争中である. Other combinations are 係争地 \"disputed territory\" (also between states) and 係争事件. Formal language of lawyers and media; in everyday life one says 争い, 裁判中 or もめている. As a する-verb it hardly occurs.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"国際基準に準拠した法律が制定された。","romaji":"Kokusai kijun ni junkyo shita hōritsu ga seitei sareta.","german":"A law conforming to international standards was enacted.","furigana":"こくさいきじゅん|じゅんきょ|ほうりつ|せいてい"},{"japanese":"このケーブルはUSBの規格に準拠している。","romaji":"Kono kēburu wa USB no kikaku ni junkyo shite iru.","german":"This cable conforms to the USB standard.","furigana":"きかく|じゅんきょ"},{"japanese":"教科書に準拠した問題集を買った。","romaji":"Kyōkasho ni junkyo shita mondaishū o katta.","german":"I bought a workbook that is aligned with the textbook.","furigana":"きょうかしょ|じゅんきょ|もんだいしゅう|か"}],"notes":"準拠 means to follow a norm, rule or template, or to be based on it. It almost always appears as 〜に準拠する or attributively 〜に準拠した + noun: 国際規格に準拠した製品, 教科書準拠の問題集 \"workbook aligned with the textbook\". Intransitive する-verb. Unlike 遵守 \"to observe (laws)\", 準拠 means \"to conform to a norm, to model oneself on\"; compared with the general 基づく, it is more technical. Common in technology, law and education.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"全社員に法令遵守が義務付けられている。","romaji":"Zen shain ni hōrei junshu ga gimuzukerarete iru.","german":"All employees are required to comply with laws and regulations.","furigana":"ぜんしゃいん|ほうれいじゅんしゅ|ぎむづ"},{"japanese":"契約の条件を遵守してください。","romaji":"Keiyaku no jōken o junshu shite kudasai.","german":"Please observe the terms of the contract.","furigana":"けいやく|じょうけん|じゅんしゅ"},{"japanese":"工場では安全基準の遵守が徹底されている。","romaji":"Kōjō de wa anzen kijun no junshu ga tettei sarete iru.","german":"In the factory, strict observance of safety standards is enforced.","furigana":"こうじょう|あんぜんきじゅん|じゅんしゅ|てってい"}],"notes":"遵守 is the formal observing of laws, rules, contracts or deadlines: 法律を遵守する, 法令遵守 (corresponds to コンプライアンス). Transitive する-verb with を. In everyday life one simply says 守る; 遵守 sounds official and written. Newspapers often write 順守, with the same reading. Not to be confused with 準拠 \"to conform to a norm, to be based on something\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この行為は独占禁止法に抵触する恐れがある。","romaji":"Kono kōi wa dokusen kinshihō ni teishoku suru osore ga aru.","german":"This act may violate the Antimonopoly Act.","furigana":"こうい|どくせんきんしほう|ていしょく|おそ"},{"japanese":"他人の写真を無断で使うと、著作権に抵触する場合がある。","romaji":"Tanin no shashin o mudan de tsukau to, chosakuken ni teishoku suru baai ga aru.","german":"Using other people's photos without permission may infringe copyright.","furigana":"たにん|しゃしん|むだん|つか|ちょさくけん|ていしょく|ばあい"},{"japanese":"新しい規則が既存の規定と抵触しないか確認した。","romaji":"Atarashii kisoku ga kizon no kitei to teishoku shinai ka kakunin shita.","german":"We checked whether the new rule conflicts with existing provisions.","furigana":"あたら|きそく|きぞん|きてい|ていしょく|かくにん"}],"notes":"抵触 means to violate a law, rule or provision, or to come into conflict with it; intransitive with に: 法律に抵触する, 著作権に抵触する. Often worded cautiously: 抵触する恐れがある, 抵触しかねない. It sounds more indirect than 違反する \"to violate\". It also denotes contradictions between rules or statements: 規定と抵触する. Formal, legal language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"憲法の解釈を巡って学者の間で論争がある。","romaji":"Kenpō no kaishaku o megutte gakusha no aida de ronsō ga aru.","german":"There is a dispute among scholars over the interpretation of the constitution.","furigana":"けんぽう|かいしゃく|めぐ|がくしゃ|あいだ|ろんそう"},{"japanese":"彼の沈黙を同意と解釈していいのだろうか。","romaji":"Kare no chinmoku o dōi to kaishaku shite ii no darō ka.","german":"Is it right to interpret his silence as agreement?","furigana":"かれ|ちんもく|どうい|かいしゃく"},{"japanese":"この詩はいろいろな解釈ができる。","romaji":"Kono shi wa iroiro na kaishaku ga dekiru.","german":"This poem allows for many interpretations.","furigana":"し|かいしゃく"}],"notes":"解釈 is the interpretation or construal of texts, laws, statements or behavior: 法律の解釈, 解釈の違い, 〜を〜と解釈する \"to take something as ...\". A transitive する-verb. Unlike 説明 \"explanation for others\", 解釈 is one's own reading; translating and interpreting are called 翻訳 and 通訳. It is general-purpose, not only legal: 好意的に解釈する \"to interpret charitably\", 拡大解釈 \"overly broad interpretation\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"時代にそぐわない法律が廃止された。","romaji":"Jidai ni soguwanai hōritsu ga haishi sareta.","german":"A law that no longer suited the times was abolished.","furigana":"じだい|ほうりつ|はいし"},{"japanese":"利用者が少ないため、このバス路線は来年廃止される。","romaji":"Riyōsha ga sukunai tame, kono basu rosen wa rainen haishi sareru.","german":"Because of low ridership, this bus route will be discontinued next year.","furigana":"りようしゃ|すく|ろせん|らいねんはいし"},{"japanese":"死刑を廃止した国は少なくない。","romaji":"Shikei o haishi shita kuni wa sukunakunai.","german":"Quite a few countries have abolished the death penalty.","furigana":"しけい|はいし|くに|すく"}],"notes":"廃止 means to officially and permanently abolish or discontinue an institution, rule or service: 制度を廃止する, 路線の廃止, 死刑廃止. Transitive する-verb; in the news mostly passive: 〜が廃止される. Unlike 中止 \"cancellation or halting of a plan or event\", 廃止 is final; 撤廃 stresses the removal of barriers (関税の撤廃).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新たな環境保護法が制定された。","romaji":"Arata na kankyō hogohō ga seitei sareta.","german":"A new environmental protection law was enacted.","furigana":"あら|かんきょうほごほう|せいてい"},{"japanese":"市は独自の条例を制定し、路上喫煙を禁止した。","romaji":"Shi wa dokuji no jōrei o seitei shi, rojō kitsuen o kinshi shita.","german":"The city enacted its own ordinance and banned smoking on the street.","furigana":"し|どくじ|じょうれい|せいてい|ろじょうきつえん|きんし"},{"japanese":"「山の日」は新しく制定された祝日だ。","romaji":"'Yama no hi' wa atarashiku seitei sareta shukujitsu da.","german":"\"Mountain Day\" is a newly established national holiday.","furigana":"やま|ひ|あたら|せいてい|しゅくじつ"}],"notes":"制定 is the formal enacting of laws, ordinances and statutes, but also the official establishment of holidays or symbols: 法律を制定する, 憲法の制定, 祝日を制定する. Transitive する-verb, in the news mostly passive. The stages of a law: 制定 \"to enact\", 改正 \"to amend\", 廃止 \"to abolish\"; 施行 is the entry into force. Not to be confused with 設定 \"setting, configuring (e.g. on a device)\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"法律の起草に多くの専門家が関わった。","romaji":"Hōritsu no kisō ni ōku no senmonka ga kakawatta.","german":"Many experts were involved in drafting the law.","furigana":"ほうりつ|きそう|おお|せんもんか|かか"},{"japanese":"共同声明は両国の担当者が起草した。","romaji":"Kyōdō seimei wa ryōkoku no tantōsha ga kisō shita.","german":"The joint statement was drafted by representatives of both countries.","furigana":"きょうどうせいめい|りょうこく|たんとうしゃ|きそう"},{"japanese":"会の規約を起草するよう頼まれた。","romaji":"Kai no kiyaku o kisō suru yō tanomareta.","german":"I was asked to draft the club's bylaws.","furigana":"かい|きやく|きそう|たの"}],"notes":"起草 is the drafting of an official text: 法案/条約/声明を起草する, 起草委員会 \"drafting committee\". Transitive する-verb. It stresses the first draft; the result is called 草案 \"draft\". For everyday drafts such as emails or essays, one says 下書き; 立案 is the planning of a measure. Formal language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"裁量権の逸脱が違法と判断された。","romaji":"Sairyōken no itsudatsu ga ihō to handan sareta.","german":"The overstepping of discretionary authority was judged unlawful.","furigana":"さいりょうけん|いつだつ|いほう|はんだん"},{"japanese":"議論が本来のテーマから逸脱してしまった。","romaji":"Giron ga honrai no tēma kara itsudatsu shite shimatta.","german":"The discussion strayed from the original topic.","furigana":"ぎろん|ほんらい|いつだつ"},{"japanese":"彼の行動は社会のルールを大きく逸脱している。","romaji":"Kare no kōdō wa shakai no rūru o ōkiku itsudatsu shite iru.","german":"His behavior deviates greatly from society's rules.","furigana":"かれ|こうどう|しゃかい|おお|いつだつ"}],"notes":"逸脱 is deviating from a norm, rule, role or a given framework: 本来の目的から逸脱する, 常識を逸脱した行動. Intransitive する-verb, mostly with から, in fixed combinations also with を (権限を逸脱する \"to exceed one's authority\"). It sounds formal and judgmental; for simple spatial deviation one says 外れる. In administrative law: 裁量権の逸脱・濫用 \"exceeding and abuse of discretion\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"行政の不作為を理由に国家賠償請求がなされた。","romaji":"Gyōsei no fusakui o riyū ni kokka baishō seikyū ga nasareta.","german":"A claim for state compensation was filed on the grounds of the administration's failure to act.","furigana":"ぎょうせい|ふさくい|りゆう|こっかばいしょうせいきゅう"},{"japanese":"保護すべき子どもを放置するのは、不作為による犯罪になりうる。","romaji":"Hogo subeki kodomo o hōchi suru no wa, fusakui ni yoru hanzai ni nariuru.","german":"Leaving a child who should be protected unattended can be a crime of omission.","furigana":"ほご|こ|ほうち|ふさくい|はんざい"},{"japanese":"作為と不作為の区別は刑法の基本だ。","romaji":"Sakui to fusakui no kubetsu wa keihō no kihon da.","german":"The distinction between acts and omissions is fundamental to criminal law.","furigana":"さくい|ふさくい|くべつ|けいほう|きほん"}],"notes":"不作為 is a legal technical term for failing to perform a required action - the counterpart of 作為 \"active doing\". Typical: 行政の不作為 \"inaction of the authorities\", 不作為犯 \"crime of omission\", 不作為による〜. In everyday life one says 何もしない or 放置する. Not to be confused with 無作為 \"random\" (無作為抽出 \"random sampling\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"証拠不十分のため立件が見送られた。","romaji":"Shōko fujūbun no tame rikken ga miokurareta.","german":"Pursuing charges was passed over for lack of sufficient evidence.","furigana":"しょうこふじゅうぶん|りっけん|みおく"},{"japanese":"警察は社長を詐欺の疑いで立件する方針だ。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa shachō o sagi no utagai de rikken suru hōshin da.","german":"The police plan to build a fraud case against the company president.","furigana":"けいさつ|しゃちょう|さぎ|うたが|りっけん|ほうしん"},{"japanese":"運転手は逮捕されず、在宅のまま立件された。","romaji":"Untenshu wa taiho sarezu, zaitaku no mama rikken sareta.","german":"The driver was not arrested; the case against him was built while he remained at liberty.","furigana":"うんてんしゅ|たいほ|ざいたく|りっけん"}],"notes":"立件 means that the police or prosecution prepare a case as a criminal matter, i.e. gather enough evidence to formally initiate proceedings. Typical: 〜を…の疑いで立件する, 立件を見送る \"to refrain from pursuing charges\". Transitive する-verb, news language. 逮捕 is arrest, 起訴 is the filing of charges; 立件 is possible even without an arrest (在宅のまま立件).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"検察が不起訴処分を決定した。","romaji":"Kensatsu ga fukiso shobun o kettei shita.","german":"The prosecution decided not to indict.","furigana":"けんさつ|ふきそしょぶん|けってい"},{"japanese":"証拠が不十分だとして、男性は不起訴となった。","romaji":"Shōko ga fujūbun da to shite, dansei wa fukiso to natta.","german":"The man was not indicted because of insufficient evidence.","furigana":"しょうこ|ふじゅうぶん|だんせい|ふきそ"},{"japanese":"被害者と示談が成立し、不起訴処分になった。","romaji":"Higaisha to jidan ga seiritsu shi, fukiso shobun ni natta.","german":"After a settlement was reached with the victim, the case was dropped.","furigana":"ひがいしゃ|じだん|せいりつ|ふきそしょぶん"}],"notes":"不起訴 is the prosecution's decision not to bring charges. Typical: 不起訴処分にする, 不起訴となる/になる. Reasons include 嫌疑不十分 \"insufficient suspicion\" and 起訴猶予 \"forgoing indictment despite suspicion\". The opposite is 起訴 \"indictment\". Against a 不起訴, one can apply for review to the 検察審査会. News language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不起訴に不服がある場合、検察審査会に申し立てができる。","romaji":"Fukiso ni fufuku ga aru baai, kensatsu shinsakai ni mōshitate ga dekiru.","german":"If you are dissatisfied with a decision not to indict, you can file a petition with the Prosecution Review Commission.","furigana":"ふきそ|ふふく|ばあい|けんさつしんさかい|もう|た"},{"japanese":"検察審査会は「起訴相当」と議決した。","romaji":"Kensatsu shinsakai wa 'kiso sōtō' to giketsu shita.","german":"The Prosecution Review Commission resolved that an indictment was warranted.","furigana":"けんさつしんさかい|きそそうとう|ぎけつ"},{"japanese":"検察審査会の委員は、くじで選ばれた市民が務める。","romaji":"Kensatsu shinsakai no iin wa, kuji de erabareta shimin ga tsutomeru.","german":"The members of the Prosecution Review Commission are citizens chosen by lot.","furigana":"けんさつしんさかい|いいん|えら|しみん|つと"}],"notes":"検察審査会 is a body of eleven citizens chosen by lot that examines, on request, whether the prosecution's decision not to indict (不起訴) was appropriate. Possible resolutions: 起訴相当 \"indictment warranted\", 不起訴不当 \"non-indictment inappropriate\", 不起訴相当. After a second 起訴相当, a 強制起訴 \"compulsory indictment\" follows. Typical: 検察審査会に申し立てる, 検察審査会が議決する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"裁判員制度により市民が刑事裁判に参加する。","romaji":"Saiban'in seido ni yori shimin ga keiji saiban ni sanka suru.","german":"Durch das Schöffensystem nehmen Bürger an Strafprozessen teil.","furigana":"さいばんいんせいど|しみん|けいじさいばん|さんか"},{"japanese":"裁判員制度が始まって十年以上がたった。","romaji":"Saiban'in seido ga hajimatte jūnen ijō ga tatta.","german":"Seit Einführung des Laienrichtersystems sind über zehn Jahre vergangen.","furigana":"さいばんいんせいど|はじ|じゅうねんいじょう"},{"japanese":"裁判員制度では、選ばれた市民は原則として断ることができない。","romaji":"Saiban'in seido de wa, erabareta shimin wa gensoku to shite kotowaru koto ga dekinai.","german":"Im Laienrichtersystem können ausgewählte Bürger grundsätzlich nicht ablehnen.","furigana":"さいばんいんせいど|えら|しみん|げんそく|ことわ"}],"notes":"裁判員制度 ist das 2009 eingeführte japanische Laienrichtersystem: Bei schweren Straftaten entscheiden sechs per Los bestimmte Bürger (裁判員) gemeinsam mit drei Berufsrichtern über Schuld und Strafmaß. Anders als beim amerikanischen 陪審制, wo die Geschworenen allein über die Schuld befinden, beraten Laien und Richter zusammen – ähnlich dem deutschen Schöffensystem. Typisch: 裁判員に選ばれる, 裁判員裁判.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"陪審制度はアメリカの司法制度の特徴である。","romaji":"Baishin seido wa Amerika no shihō seido no tokuchō de aru.","german":"The jury system is a feature of the American justice system.","furigana":"ばいしんせいど|しほうせいど|とくちょう"},{"japanese":"陪審団は被告に無罪の評決を下した。","romaji":"Baishindan wa hikoku ni muzai no hyōketsu o kudashita.","german":"The jury returned a verdict of not guilty for the defendant.","furigana":"ばいしんだん|ひこく|むざい|ひょうけつ|くだ"},{"japanese":"アメリカで陪審員に選ばれ、一週間裁判所に通った。","romaji":"Amerika de baishin'in ni erabare, isshūkan saibansho ni kayotta.","german":"I was selected as a juror in the United States and went to the courthouse for a week.","furigana":"ばいしんいん|えら|いっしゅうかんさいばんしょ|かよ"}],"notes":"陪審 refers to the jury system or the jury, especially in Anglo-American law: 陪審員 \"juror\", 陪審団 \"the jury (as a group)\", 陪審制 \"jury system\", 陪審の評決 \"jury verdict\". Japan had a jury system from 1928 to 1943; today the 裁判員制度 applies instead, in which lay people judge together with professional judges. Mostly in connection with foreign trials.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"少額訴訟は簡裁で審理される。","romaji":"Shōgaku soshō wa kansai de shinri sareru.","german":"Small claims are heard in summary court.","furigana":"しょうがくそしょう|かんさい|しんり"},{"japanese":"スピード違反で、簡裁から罰金の命令が出た。","romaji":"Supīdo ihan de, kansai kara bakkin no meirei ga deta.","german":"For speeding, the summary court issued a fine order.","furigana":"いはん|かんさい|ばっきん|めいれい|で"},{"japanese":"大家との敷金トラブルを簡裁に訴えた。","romaji":"Ōya to no shikikin toraburu o kansai ni uttaeta.","german":"I took the dispute with my landlord over the deposit to summary court.","furigana":"おおや|しききん|かんさい|うった"}],"notes":"簡裁 is the short form of 簡易裁判所, Japan's lowest court for civil cases with a small amount in dispute (up to 1.4 million yen) and minor criminal cases; 少額訴訟 \"small claims\" are also handled there. The short forms 簡裁, 地裁, 高裁, 最高裁 and 家裁 are common in news and headlines, while formal texts use the full form. Note: it is read the same as 関西. \"Summary court\" is only a rough equivalent.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地裁の判決に不服があれば高裁に控訴できる。","romaji":"Chisai no hanketsu ni fufuku ga areba kōsai ni kōso dekiru.","german":"If you are dissatisfied with a district court ruling, you can appeal to the high court.","furigana":"ちさい|はんけつ|ふふく|こうさい|こうそ"},{"japanese":"東京地裁は原告の訴えを退けた。","romaji":"Tōkyō chisai wa genkoku no uttae o shirizoketa.","german":"The Tokyo District Court dismissed the plaintiff's suit.","furigana":"とうきょうちさい|げんこく|うった|しりぞ"},{"japanese":"住民たちは工事の中止を求めて地裁に提訴した。","romaji":"Jūmintachi wa kōji no chūshi o motomete chisai ni teiso shita.","german":"Residents filed suit in district court seeking a halt to the construction.","furigana":"じゅうみん|こうじ|ちゅうし|もと|ちさい|ていそ"}],"notes":"地裁 is the short form of 地方裁判所, the court of first instance for most civil and criminal cases; every prefecture has one, Hokkaido four. Typical: 東京地裁, 地裁に提訴する, 地裁判決. Against a judgment, one appeals (控訴) to the 高裁 and then to the 最高裁 (上告). \"District court\" is an approximation. News language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"高裁が地裁の判決を覆した。","romaji":"Kōsai ga chisai no hanketsu o kutsugaeshita.","german":"The high court overturned the district court's ruling.","furigana":"こうさい|ちさい|はんけつ|くつがえ"},{"japanese":"被告側は判決を不服として高裁に控訴した。","romaji":"Hikoku-gawa wa hanketsu o fufuku to shite kōsai ni kōso shita.","german":"The defense appealed the ruling to the high court, dissatisfied with the judgment.","furigana":"ひこくがわ|はんけつ|ふふく|こうさい|こうそ"},{"japanese":"大阪高裁は一審の判決を支持した。","romaji":"Ōsaka kōsai wa ichishin no hanketsu o shiji shita.","german":"The Osaka High Court upheld the first-instance ruling.","furigana":"おおさかこうさい|いちしん|はんけつ|しじ"}],"notes":"高裁 is the short form of 高等裁判所, the appellate court above the 地裁 and 家裁. Japan has eight 高裁 (Tokyo, Osaka, Nagoya, Hiroshima, Fukuoka, Sendai, Sapporo, Takamatsu) with branches. Typical: 高裁に控訴する, 高裁判決, 一審判決を覆す/支持する \"to overturn/uphold the first-instance ruling\". News and legal language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"最高裁が画期的な判断を示した。","romaji":"Saikōsai ga kakkiteki na handan o shimeshita.","german":"The Supreme Court delivered a landmark ruling.","furigana":"さいこうさい|かっきてき|はんだん|しめ"},{"japanese":"最高裁はこの法律の規定を違憲と判断した。","romaji":"Saikōsai wa kono hōritsu no kitei o iken to handan shita.","german":"The Supreme Court ruled the provision of this law unconstitutional.","furigana":"さいこうさい|ほうりつ|きてい|いけん|はんだん"},{"japanese":"最高裁で判決が確定し、長い裁判が終わった。","romaji":"Saikōsai de hanketsu ga kakutei shi, nagai saiban ga owatta.","german":"The judgment became final at the Supreme Court, and the long trial was over.","furigana":"さいこうさい|はんけつ|かくてい|なが|さいばん|お"}],"notes":"最高裁 is the short form of 最高裁判所, Japan's Supreme Court in Tokyo. It is the court of last resort and also reviews the constitutionality of laws (違憲審査). Typical: 最高裁に上告する, 最高裁判決, 最高裁が〜を違憲と判断する, 最高裁長官 \"Chief Justice of the Supreme Court\". At House of Representatives elections, citizens also vote on its judges in the 国民審査.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"離婚調停は家裁で行われる。","romaji":"Rikon chōtei wa kasai de okonawareru.","german":"Divorce mediation is conducted at the family court.","furigana":"りこんちょうてい|かさい|おこな"},{"japanese":"万引きをした少年は家裁に送致された。","romaji":"Manbiki o shita shōnen wa kasai ni sōchi sareta.","german":"The juvenile who committed shoplifting was referred to the family court.","furigana":"まんび|しょうねん|かさい|そうち"},{"japanese":"名前を変えたい場合は、家裁の許可が必要だ。","romaji":"Namae o kaetai baai wa, kasai no kyoka ga hitsuyō da.","german":"If you want to change your name, you need the permission of the family court.","furigana":"なまえ|か|ばあい|かさい|きょか|ひつよう"}],"notes":"家裁 is the short form of 家庭裁判所, responsible for family matters such as divorce, inheritance and guardianship, as well as juvenile criminal cases (少年事件). Many family disputes first go through mediation (調停) there. Typical: 家裁に申し立てる, 家裁送致 \"referral to family court\" for juveniles. Note: it is read the same as 火災 \"fire\". News language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不動産の登記は法務局で行う。","romaji":"Fudōsan no tōki wa hōmukyoku de okonau.","german":"Real estate registration is done at the Legal Affairs Bureau.","furigana":"ふどうさん|とうき|ほうむきょく|おこな"},{"japanese":"帰化の申請は法務局で受け付けている。","romaji":"Kika no shinsei wa hōmukyoku de uketsukete iru.","german":"Naturalization applications are accepted at the Legal Affairs Bureau.","furigana":"きか|しんせい|ほうむきょく|う|つ"},{"japanese":"自分で書いた遺言書を法務局に預けた。","romaji":"Jibun de kaita yuigonsho o hōmukyoku ni azuketa.","german":"I deposited my handwritten will with the Legal Affairs Bureau.","furigana":"じぶん|か|ゆいごんしょ|ほうむきょく|あず"}],"notes":"法務局 is the regional branch of the Ministry of Justice (法務省). For citizens, it is above all the office for register entries - land registry (不動産登記) and commercial register (商業登記) - and also for naturalization (帰化), human rights consultation and the custody of wills. Smaller offices are called 地方法務局, 支局 or 出張所. \"Legal Affairs Bureau\" is only a makeshift translation; functionally it corresponds to a land registry office and a company registry court.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"所有権移転の登記を申請した。","romaji":"Shoyūken iten no tōki o shinsei shita.","german":"I applied for registration of the transfer of ownership.","furigana":"しょゆうけんいてん|とうき|しんせい"},{"japanese":"会社を設立したら、まず登記をしなければならない。","romaji":"Kaisha o setsuritsu shitara, mazu tōki o shinakereba naranai.","german":"After founding a company, you first have to register it.","furigana":"かいしゃ|せつりつ|とうき"},{"japanese":"家を買う前に、登記簿で所有者を確認した。","romaji":"Ie o kau mae ni, tōkibo de shoyūsha o kakunin shita.","german":"Before buying the house, I checked the registry to confirm who the owner was.","furigana":"いえ|か|まえ|とうきぼ|しょゆうしゃ|かくにん"}],"notes":"登記 is the official entry of rights and facts into a public register, above all 不動産登記 (land registry) and 商業登記 (commercial register). Transitive する-verb: 〜を登記する; one applies for it with 登記を申請する at the 法務局. The extract is today called 登記事項証明書, formerly 登記簿謄本. Distinction: 登録 is general registration (membership, trademarks, vehicles), 届出 a notification or report.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"公証人の立ち会いで遺言書を作成した。","romaji":"Kōshōnin no tachiai de yuigonsho o sakusei shita.","german":"The will was drawn up in the presence of a notary.","furigana":"こうしょうにん|た|あ|ゆいごんしょ|さくせい"},{"japanese":"外国で使う書類には、公証が必要になることがある。","romaji":"Gaikoku de tsukau shorui ni wa, kōshō ga hitsuyō ni naru koto ga aru.","german":"Documents to be used abroad sometimes require notarization.","furigana":"がいこく|つか|しょるい|こうしょう|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"契約書を公証役場に持って行き、認証を受けた。","romaji":"Keiyakusho o kōshō yakuba ni motte iki, ninshō o uketa.","german":"I took the contract to the notary office and had it certified.","furigana":"けいやくしょ|こうしょうやくば|も|い|にんしょう|う"}],"notes":"公証 is the official certification or recording that a document or fact is genuine. It rarely stands alone and mostly appears in compounds: 公証人 \"notary\", 公証役場 \"notary office\". The deed drawn up by a notary is called 公正証書, a notarial will 公正証書遺言; certifying private documents is called 認証. Technical language from law and administration.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"原告が法廷で意見陳述を行った。","romaji":"Genkoku ga hōtei de iken chinjutsu o okonatta.","german":"The plaintiff gave a statement in court.","furigana":"げんこく|ほうてい|いけんちんじゅつ|おこな"},{"japanese":"初公判で、検察側が冒頭陳述を行った。","romaji":"Hatsukōhan de, kensatsu-gawa ga bōtō chinjutsu o okonatta.","german":"At the first hearing, the prosecution delivered its opening statement.","furigana":"はつこうはん|けんさつがわ|ぼうとうちんじゅつ|おこな"},{"japanese":"弁護士に頼んで陳述書を作成してもらった。","romaji":"Bengoshi ni tanonde chinjutsusho o sakusei shite moratta.","german":"I asked my lawyer to draw up a written statement for me.","furigana":"べんごし|たの|ちんじゅつしょ|さくせい"}],"notes":"陳述 is the formal presentation of facts or views, especially in court or before an authority: 意見陳述 \"statement of opinion (e.g. by the victim)\", 陳述書 \"written statement\", 冒頭陳述 \"opening statement of the prosecution\". Transitive する-verb: 意見を陳述する. In everyday speech one says 述べる; 供述, by contrast, is the testimony of a suspect or witness to the police or in court.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"裁判所が専門家に鑑定を嘱託した。","romaji":"Saibansho ga senmonka ni kantei o shokutaku shita.","german":"The court commissioned an expert to give an opinion.","furigana":"さいばんしょ|せんもんか|かんてい|しょくたく"},{"japanese":"父は定年後も嘱託として同じ会社で働いている。","romaji":"Chichi wa teinen-go mo shokutaku to shite onaji kaisha de hataraite iru.","german":"After retirement, my father keeps working at the same company as a contract employee.","furigana":"ちち|ていねんご|しょくたく|おな|かいしゃ|はたら"},{"japanese":"嘱託医が月に二回、老人ホームを訪れる。","romaji":"Shokutakui ga tsuki ni nikai, rōjin hōmu o otozureru.","german":"A contract doctor visits the nursing home twice a month.","furigana":"しょくたくい|つき|にかい|ろうじん|おとず"}],"notes":"嘱託 has two meanings: formally entrusting someone with a task, especially authorities or courts commissioning experts (〜に鑑定を嘱託する), and, in working life, employment on a contract basis outside the regular staff, often as continued employment after retirement: 嘱託社員, 嘱託として働く, 嘱託医. In the first meaning it is a transitive する-verb. Related: 委託 \"entrusting work to companies, outsourcing\", 依頼 \"request, commission\" in general.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"マイナンバーカードの交付申請が増加している。","romaji":"Mainanbā kādo no kōfu shinsei ga zōka shite iru.","german":"Applications for issuance of the My Number card are increasing.","furigana":"こうふしんせい|ぞうか"},{"japanese":"住民票の写しは市役所の窓口で交付してもらえる。","romaji":"Jūminhyō no utsushi wa shiyakusho no madoguchi de kōfu shite moraeru.","german":"A copy of the residence certificate can be issued at the counter of the city hall.","furigana":"じゅうみんひょう|うつ|しやくしょ|まどぐち|こうふ"},{"japanese":"国から自治体に補助金が交付された。","romaji":"Kuni kara jichitai ni hojokin ga kōfu sareta.","german":"The state granted subsidies to the municipalities.","furigana":"くに|じちたい|ほじょきん|こうふ"}],"notes":"交付 is the official issuing or handing over of documents or funds by an authority: 証明書/免許証を交付する, 交付金 \"allocations, grants\", 地方交付税 \"local allocation tax (fiscal equalization for municipalities)\". Transitive する-verb; from the citizen's perspective 交付を受ける. Distinction: 発行 is publishing or issuing in general (also magazines, tickets), 配布 is distributing to many people; 交付 stresses the official handover by the authorities.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ビザの発給に数週間かかる場合がある。","romaji":"Biza no hakkyū ni sūshūkan kakaru baai ga aru.","german":"Issuance of a visa can take several weeks.","furigana":"はっきゅう|すうしゅうかん|ばあい"},{"japanese":"大使館でビザが発給されるまで、出発できません。","romaji":"Taishikan de biza ga hakkyū sareru made, shuppatsu dekimasen.","german":"I can't depart until the embassy issues the visa.","furigana":"たいしかん|はっきゅう|しゅっぱつ"},{"japanese":"システム障害のため、パスポートの発給が一時停止された。","romaji":"Shisutemu shōgai no tame, pasupōto no hakkyū ga ichiji teishi sareta.","german":"Because of a system failure, the issuance of passports was temporarily suspended.","furigana":"しょうがい|はっきゅう|いちじていし"}],"notes":"発給 is the official term for an authority issuing a document to a person, especially visas, passports and permits: ビザを発給する, 旅券の発給, often in the passive ビザが発給される. The more general 発行 is used for anything that is issued (tickets, magazines, shares, receipts); 発給 fits only where an office hands a document to an applicant. Typical of news, consulates and forms.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"保育所の設置認可が下りた。","romaji":"Hoikusho no setchi ninka ga orita.","german":"Approval to establish the nursery was granted.","furigana":"ほいくしょ|せっちにんか|お"},{"japanese":"新しい薬が国に認可されるまでには、長い時間がかかる。","romaji":"Atarashii kusuri ga kuni ni ninka sareru made ni wa, nagai jikan ga kakaru.","german":"It takes a long time before a new drug is approved by the state.","furigana":"あたら|くすり|くに|にんか|なが|じかん"},{"japanese":"この保育園は認可外なので、補助金があまり出ない。","romaji":"Kono hoikuen wa ninkagai na node, hojokin ga amari denai.","german":"This daycare is unlicensed, so it receives little in subsidies.","furigana":"ほいくえん|にんかがい|ほじょきん|で"}],"notes":"認可 is the official approval by which an authority legally accepts an application: 認可を受ける/得る, 認可が下りる, 国が〜を認可する. The everyday 許可 is any kind of permission (\"may I?\"), 承認 is more the consent or approval of a body or person. Common in fixed expressions such as 認可保育園 and its counterpart 認可外/無認可 (\"without official approval\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"国際規格の認証を取得した。","romaji":"Kokusai kikaku no ninshō o shutoku shita.","german":"We obtained certification to an international standard.","furigana":"こくさいきかく|にんしょう|しゅとく"},{"japanese":"ログインには二段階認証が必要です。","romaji":"Roguin ni wa nidankai ninshō ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"Two-factor authentication is required to log in.","furigana":"にだんかいにんしょう|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"マスクをしていると、顔認証がうまくいかない。","romaji":"Masuku o shite iru to, kao ninshō ga umaku ikanai.","german":"When I wear a mask, face authentication doesn't work well.","furigana":"かおにんしょう"}],"notes":"認証 has two fields of use: in business and administration, certification by an inspection body (ISO認証を取得する); in IT, the authentication of a user (二段階認証, 顔認証, 指紋認証, 認証コード). In both cases a body or system confirms that something is genuine or conforms to a standard. Not to be confused with 証明 (\"proof, evidence\") or 確認 (\"checking\" in the general sense).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"職務を忠実に遂行する義務がある。","romaji":"Shokumu o chūjitsu ni suikō suru gimu ga aru.","german":"There is a duty to carry out one's official duties faithfully.","furigana":"しょくむ|ちゅうじつ|すいこう|ぎむ"},{"japanese":"限られた人数で任務を遂行しなければならない。","romaji":"Kagirareta ninzū de ninmu o suikō shinakereba naranai.","german":"We have to carry out the mission with limited personnel.","furigana":"かぎ|にんずう|にんむ|すいこう"},{"japanese":"彼女は難しい計画を最後まで遂行した。","romaji":"Kanojo wa muzukashii keikaku o saigo made suikō shita.","german":"She saw the difficult plan through to the end.","furigana":"かのじょ|むずか|けいかく|さいご|すいこう"}],"notes":"遂行 is formal and stresses that an assigned task is actually carried out to the end: 任務を遂行する, 職務を遂行する, 計画を遂行する, also 業務遂行能力. 実行 means \"putting into action\" in general, 実施 means \"carrying out (a measure, event)\"; 遂行 sounds like duty and responsibility. In everyday conversation one says やり遂げる instead.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"契約の履行を求めて訴訟を起こした。","romaji":"Keiyaku no rikō o motomete soshō o okoshita.","german":"A lawsuit was filed demanding performance of the contract.","furigana":"けいやく|りこう|もと|そしょう|お"},{"japanese":"首相は選挙で掲げた公約を履行するよう求められている。","romaji":"Shushō wa senkyo de kakageta kōyaku o rikō suru yō motomerarete iru.","german":"The prime minister is being urged to fulfill the campaign pledges he made in the election.","furigana":"しゅしょう|せんきょ|かか|こうやく|りこう|もと"},{"japanese":"期限までに代金を払わず、会社は債務不履行に陥った。","romaji":"Kigen made ni daikin o harawazu, kaisha wa saimu furikō ni ochiitta.","german":"The company did not pay by the deadline and fell into default.","furigana":"きげん|だいきん|はら|かいしゃ|さいむふりこう|おちい"}],"notes":"履行 is the legal term for fulfilling an obligation: 契約を履行する, 義務を履行する, 約束・公約を履行する. The opposite is 不履行, especially 債務不履行 (\"non-performance, breach of obligation\"). 実行 is general \"carrying out\", whereas 履行 stresses that one renders a performance that is owed. Sounds very formal, typical in contracts, court judgments and political commentary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"避難指示が発令された。","romaji":"Hinan shiji ga hatsurei sareta.","german":"An evacuation order was issued.","furigana":"ひなんしじ|はつれい"},{"japanese":"政府は全国に緊急事態宣言を発令した。","romaji":"Seifu wa zenkoku ni kinkyū jitai sengen o hatsurei shita.","german":"The government declared a state of emergency nationwide.","furigana":"せいふ|ぜんこく|きんきゅうじたいせんげん|はつれい"},{"japanese":"今回の人事異動は四月一日付で発令される。","romaji":"Konkai no jinji idō wa shigatsu tsuitachi-zuke de hatsurei sareru.","german":"The current personnel transfers will be officially announced as effective April 1.","furigana":"こんかい|じんじいどう|しがつついたちづけ|はつれい"}],"notes":"発令 means that an authority officially announces an order, warning or personnel decision: 避難指示・警報・緊急事態宣言が発令される, mostly in the passive. In companies and agencies it also stands for the formal announcement of transfers and appointments: 人事異動が四月一日付で発令される. Lifting is called 解除 (警報が解除される). 発表 is neutral \"announce\"; 発令 always has the character of an official command.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"長年の功績が認められ叙勲を受けた。","romaji":"Naganen no kōseki ga mitomerare jokun o uketa.","german":"He was awarded a decoration in recognition of his many years of service.","furigana":"ながねん|こうせき|みと|じょくん|う"},{"japanese":"春の叙勲の受章者が新聞に発表された。","romaji":"Haru no jokun no jushōsha ga shinbun ni happyō sareta.","german":"The recipients of the spring decorations were announced in the newspaper.","furigana":"はる|じょくん|じゅしょうしゃ|しんぶん|はっぴょう"},{"japanese":"祖父は叙勲の知らせを聞いて、とても喜んだ。","romaji":"Sofu wa jokun no shirase o kiite, totemo yorokonda.","german":"My grandfather was delighted to hear the news of his decoration.","furigana":"そふ|じょくん|し|き|よろこ"}],"notes":"叙勲 is the state conferment of an order or decoration; in Japan especially the semiannual honors 春の叙勲 and 秋の叙勲. From the honoree's point of view one says 叙勲を受ける or 受章する; the decoration itself is called 勲章. A very formal word from news and ceremonies, hardly used in everyday life.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"債務者が全額を弁済した。","romaji":"Saimusha ga zengaku o bensai shita.","german":"The debtor repaid the full amount.","furigana":"さいむしゃ|ぜんがく|べんさい"},{"japanese":"本人が払えなかったため、保証人が借金を弁済した。","romaji":"Honnin ga haraenakatta tame, hoshōnin ga shakkin o bensai shita.","german":"Because the borrower couldn't pay, the guarantor paid off the debt.","furigana":"ほんにん|はら|ほしょうにん|しゃっきん|べんさい"},{"japanese":"弁済期を過ぎると、遅延損害金が発生します。","romaji":"Bensaiki o sugiru to, chien songaikin ga hassei shimasu.","german":"After the due date has passed, late-payment damages are incurred.","furigana":"べんさいき|す|ちえんそんがいきん|はっせい"}],"notes":"弁済 is the legal technical term for settling a debt: 債務を弁済する, 全額を弁済する, 弁済期 (\"due date\"), 代位弁済 (payment by a third party, such as a guarantor). In everyday speech, for loans one says 返済 (\"repayment\") or simply 返す. 弁済 is found in contracts, court proceedings and bank documents.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"保険会社が加害者に対して求償権を行使した。","romaji":"Hoken gaisha ga kagaisha ni taishite kyūshōken o kōshi shita.","german":"The insurance company exercised its right of recourse against the perpetrator.","furigana":"ほけんがいしゃ|かがいしゃ|たい|きゅうしょうけん|こうし"},{"japanese":"会社は事故を起こした社員に損害の一部を求償した。","romaji":"Kaisha wa jiko o okoshita shain ni songai no ichibu o kyūshō shita.","german":"The company claimed reimbursement of part of the damage from the employee who caused the accident.","furigana":"かいしゃ|じこ|お|しゃいん|そんがい|いちぶ|きゅうしょう"},{"japanese":"保証人は、支払った分を本人に求償できる。","romaji":"Hoshōnin wa, shiharatta bun o honnin ni kyūshō dekiru.","german":"A guarantor can claim back from the principal debtor the amount he paid.","furigana":"ほしょうにん|しはら|ぶん|ほんにん|きゅうしょう"}],"notes":"求償 is a legal technical term: someone who has paid for another claims the amount back from that person. Typical are 求償権 (\"right of recourse\") and 〜に求償する (\"to seek recourse against someone\"), for instance an insurer against the party causing the damage, or a guarantor against the principal debtor. Unlike 請求 (\"demand\" in general), 求償 presupposes that one has already paid in place of another.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"互いの債権を相殺して精算した。","romaji":"Tagai no saiken o sōsai shite seisan shita.","german":"The mutual claims were offset against each other and settled.","furigana":"たが|さいけん|そうさい|せいさん"},{"japanese":"給料は上がったが、物価の上昇で相殺されてしまった。","romaji":"Kyūryō wa agatta ga, bukka no jōshō de sōsai sarete shimatta.","german":"My salary went up, but it was canceled out by rising prices.","furigana":"きゅうりょう|あ|ぶっか|じょうしょう|そうさい"},{"japanese":"良い点と悪い点が相殺されて、評価は普通だった。","romaji":"Yoi ten to warui ten ga sōsai sarete, hyōka wa futsū datta.","german":"The good and bad points canceled each other out, and the evaluation was average.","furigana":"よ|てん|わる|てん|そうさい|ひょうか|ふつう"}],"notes":"相殺 is read そうさい (not さつ). Legally it is the set-off of mutual claims: 債権を相殺する. In everyday use it means that two effects cancel each other out, mostly in the passive: 〜が〜で相殺される, 効果が相殺される. Similar is 帳消しになる (\"to be wiped out, nullified\"), which sounds more colloquial.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大臣が善処する旨を表明した。","romaji":"Daijin ga zensho suru mune o hyōmei shita.","german":"The minister stated that he would deal with the matter appropriately.","furigana":"だいじん|ぜんしょ|むね|ひょうめい"},{"japanese":"苦情を伝えると、店長は「善処いたします」とだけ答えた。","romaji":"Kujō o tsutaeru to, tenchō wa \"zensho itashimasu\" to dake kotaeta.","german":"When I complained, the store manager only answered, \"We will see what we can do.\"","furigana":"くじょう|つた|てんちょう|ぜんしょ|こた"},{"japanese":"住民は市に対して、騒音問題への早急な善処を求めた。","romaji":"Jūmin wa shi ni taishite, sōon mondai e no sōkyū na zensho o motometa.","german":"The residents urged the city to take prompt action on the noise problem.","furigana":"じゅうみん|し|たい|そうおんもんだい|そうきゅう|ぜんしょ|もと"}],"notes":"善処 means to handle a matter appropriately and take suitable measures: 善処する, 善処いたします, 善処を求める. The reply 善処します from politicians or companies is famous for being polite but noncommittal: one promises to take care of it without committing to anything. Very formal; in everyday speech one says 対応する or 何とかする instead.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"当事者が陳述書を裁判所に提出した。","romaji":"Tōjisha ga chinjutsusho o saibansho ni teishutsu shita.","german":"The party submitted a written statement to the court.","furigana":"とうじしゃ|ちんじゅつしょ|さいばんしょ|ていしゅつ"},{"japanese":"弁護士と相談しながら、事故の経緯を陳述書にまとめた。","romaji":"Bengoshi to sōdan shinagara, jiko no keii o chinjutsusho ni matometa.","german":"In consultation with my lawyer, I summarized the course of the accident in a written statement.","furigana":"べんごし|そうだん|じこ|けいい|ちんじゅつしょ"},{"japanese":"被害者の陳述書が証拠として採用された。","romaji":"Higaisha no chinjutsusho ga shōko to shite saiyō sareta.","german":"The victim's written statement was admitted as evidence.","furigana":"ひがいしゃ|ちんじゅつしょ|しょうこ|さいよう"}],"notes":"陳述書 is, in Japanese civil procedure, a written account of the facts from the standpoint of a party or witness, submitted to the court: 陳述書を作成する・提出する. Unlike a sworn affidavit, it is not made under oath; the sworn statement is called 宣誓供述書. 陳述 alone is a statement (oral or written) before the court, whereas 供述 is the statement of a suspect or witness to the police or public prosecutor.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"弁護士が準備書面を作成して裁判所に提出した。","romaji":"Bengoshi ga junbi shomen o sakusei shite saibansho ni teishutsu shita.","german":"The lawyer drew up a preparatory brief and submitted it to the court.","furigana":"べんごし|じゅんびしょめん|さくせい|さいばんしょ|ていしゅつ"},{"japanese":"次回の期日までに準備書面を出してください。","romaji":"Jikai no kijitsu made ni junbi shomen o dashite kudasai.","german":"Please submit the brief by the next hearing date.","furigana":"じかい|きじつ|じゅんびしょめん|だ"},{"japanese":"相手方の準備書面を読んで、反論の内容を考えた。","romaji":"Aitegata no junbi shomen o yonde, hanron no naiyō o kangaeta.","german":"I read the opposing party's brief and thought about the content of a rebuttal.","furigana":"あいてがた|じゅんびしょめん|よ|はんろん|ないよう|かんが"}],"notes":"準備書面 is, in civil procedure, the brief in which a party sets out its claims and counterarguments before the oral hearing: 準備書面を作成する・提出する, 第一準備書面, 第二準備書面 etc. The complaint is called 訴状, the first reply to the complaint 答弁書; all further briefs are 準備書面. A pure technical term from law firms and courts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"相手方の証拠に対して異議を申し立てた。","romaji":"Aitegata no shōko ni taishite igi o mōshitateta.","german":"An objection was raised against the opposing party's evidence.","furigana":"あいてがた|しょうこ|たい|いぎ|もう|た"},{"japanese":"「この案に異議はありませんか。」「異議なし！」","romaji":"\"Kono an ni igi wa arimasen ka.\" \"Igi nashi!\"","german":"\"Are there any objections to this proposal?\" \"No objections!\"","furigana":"あん|いぎ|いぎ"},{"japanese":"監督は審判の判定に異議を唱えた。","romaji":"Kantoku wa shinpan no hantei ni igi o tonaeta.","german":"The manager protested the umpire's call.","furigana":"かんとく|しんぱん|はんてい|いぎ|とな"}],"notes":"異議 is an objection or dissent against a decision or proposal: 異議を申し立てる (\"to lodge an objection\"), 異議を唱える (\"to raise objections\"), 異議申し立て. In meetings one asks 異議はありませんか and replies curtly 異議なし. Beware of the homophones 意義 (\"meaning, significance\") and 異義 (\"a different meaning\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"被告が時効の抗弁を主張した。","romaji":"Hikoku ga jikō no kōben o shuchō shita.","german":"The defendant raised the defense of the statute of limitations.","furigana":"ひこく|じこう|こうべん|しゅちょう"},{"japanese":"被告は「代金はすでに支払った」と抗弁した。","romaji":"Hikoku wa \"daikin wa sude ni shiharatta\" to kōben shita.","german":"The defendant argued, \"I have already paid the price.\"","furigana":"ひこく|だいきん|しはら|こうべん"},{"japanese":"彼は上司の指示に抗弁することもなく、黙って従った。","romaji":"Kare wa jōshi no shiji ni kōben suru koto mo naku, damatte shitagatta.","german":"He silently obeyed his boss's instructions without so much as objecting.","furigana":"かれ|じょうし|しじ|こうべん|だま|したが"}],"notes":"抗弁 has two uses. Legally it is the defendant's plea that blocks or excludes the claim: 時効の抗弁, 同時履行の抗弁, 抗弁を主張する. In general, 〜に抗弁する means to talk back to someone (often a superior) and justify oneself. 反論 is a neutral \"counterargument\"; 抗弁 sounds more formal and like defending oneself against an accusation.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"判決に不服がある場合は上訴できる。","romaji":"Hanketsu ni fufuku ga aru baai wa jōso dekiru.","german":"If you are dissatisfied with the judgment, you can appeal.","furigana":"はんけつ|ふふく|ばあい|じょうそ"},{"japanese":"上訴の期限を過ぎたので、判決が確定した。","romaji":"Jōso no kigen o sugita node, hanketsu ga kakutei shita.","german":"The appeal deadline passed, so the judgment became final.","furigana":"じょうそ|きげん|す|はんけつ|かくてい"},{"japanese":"検察側は上訴を断念すると発表した。","romaji":"Kensatsu-gawa wa jōso o dannen suru to happyō shita.","german":"The prosecution announced that it would give up on appealing.","furigana":"けんさつがわ|じょうそ|だんねん|はっぴょう"}],"notes":"上訴 is the umbrella term for legal remedies against a judgment that is not yet final. In detail one distinguishes 控訴 (appeal to the next instance), 上告 (final appeal to the Supreme Court) and 抗告 (complaint against rulings). In the news one mostly reads the specific 控訴 or 上告; 上訴 is used when all forms are meant: 上訴する, 上訴権, 上訴期間.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"最高裁が原判決を破棄した。","romaji":"Saikōsai ga gen hanketsu o haki shita.","german":"The Supreme Court reversed the original judgment.","furigana":"さいこうさい|げんはんけつ|はき"},{"japanese":"個人情報が書かれた書類は、シュレッダーで破棄してください。","romaji":"Kojin jōhō ga kakareta shorui wa, shureddā de haki shite kudasai.","german":"Please destroy documents containing personal information with a shredder.","furigana":"こじんじょうほう|か|しょるい|はき"},{"japanese":"相手国が条約を一方的に破棄した。","romaji":"Aitekoku ga jōyaku o ippōteki ni haki shita.","german":"The other country unilaterally abrogated the treaty.","furigana":"あいてこく|じょうやく|いっぽうてき|はき"}],"notes":"破棄 means to destroy something or declare it invalid. Common in everyday use is the destruction of documents and data (書類を破棄する); also the unilateral cancellation of contracts, engagements and agreements (条約を破棄する, 婚約破棄). Legally it denotes the reversal of a judgment by the higher court (原判決を破棄する, 破棄差し戻し). Not to be confused with 廃棄 (\"disposing of\" waste or goods).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"確定判決には従わなければならない。","romaji":"Kakutei hanketsu ni wa shitagawanakereba naranai.","german":"One must comply with a final judgment.","furigana":"かくていはんけつ|したが"},{"japanese":"確定判決を覆すには、再審を請求するしかない。","romaji":"Kakutei hanketsu o kutsugaesu ni wa, saishin o seikyū suru shika nai.","german":"To overturn a final judgment, the only option is to request a retrial.","furigana":"かくていはんけつ|くつがえ|さいしん|せいきゅう"},{"japanese":"確定判決に基づいて、相手の財産が差し押さえられた。","romaji":"Kakutei hanketsu ni motozuite, aite no zaisan ga sashiosaerareta.","german":"On the basis of the final judgment, the opposing party's assets were seized.","furigana":"かくていはんけつ|もと|あいて|ざいさん|さ|お"}],"notes":"確定判決 is a judgment against which no ordinary appeal (控訴, 上告) is possible any more, that is, one that has become \"final and binding\". As a verb one says 判決が確定する (\"the judgment becomes final\"). A 確定判決 can only be challenged through a retrial (再審) and is the basis for enforcement, e.g. 確定判決に基づく強制執行. Technical language from law and the news.","levelNote":"Compound: 確定 (N1) + 判決 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"裁判所が出版差し止めの仮処分を認めた。","romaji":"Saibansho ga shuppan sashitome no kari shobun o mitometa.","german":"The court granted an injunction to halt publication.","furigana":"さいばんしょ|しゅっぱんさ|と|かりしょぶん|みと"},{"japanese":"住民たちは工事の中止を求めて仮処分を申し立てた。","romaji":"Jūmintachi wa kōji no chūshi o motomete kari shobun o mōshitateta.","german":"The residents applied for an injunction demanding that the construction be stopped.","furigana":"じゅうみん|こうじ|ちゅうし|もと|かりしょぶん|もう|た"},{"japanese":"仮処分が決定され、コンサートは中止になった。","romaji":"Kari shobun ga kettei sare, konsāto wa chūshi ni natta.","german":"After the injunction was ordered, the concert was canceled.","furigana":"かりしょぶん|けってい|ちゅうし"}],"notes":"仮処分 is a provisional court order that secures a state of affairs before the main proceedings are decided, for example a ban on sale, construction or publication. Typical phrases: 仮処分を申し立てる (\"to apply for\"), 仮処分を認める/決定する, 〜差し止めの仮処分. While 仮差押え secures monetary claims by seizing assets, 仮処分 concerns other rights and conditions.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"債権者が債務者の財産を仮差押えした。","romaji":"Saikensha ga saimusha no zaisan o kari sashiosae shita.","german":"The creditor had the debtor's assets provisionally attached.","furigana":"さいけんしゃ|さいむしゃ|ざいさん|かりさしおさ"},{"japanese":"銀行口座が仮差押えされて、お金を引き出せなくなった。","romaji":"Ginkō kōza ga kari sashiosae sarete, okane o hikidasenaku natta.","german":"My bank account was provisionally attached, and I can no longer withdraw money.","furigana":"ぎんこうこうざ|かりさしおさ|かね|ひ|だ"},{"japanese":"取引先が倒産しそうなので、裁判所に仮差押えを申し立てた。","romaji":"Torihikisaki ga tōsan shisō na node, saibansho ni kari sashiosae o mōshitateta.","german":"Since our business partner seemed about to go bankrupt, we applied to the court for a provisional attachment.","furigana":"とりひきさき|とうさん|さいばんしょ|かりさしおさ|もう|た"}],"notes":"仮差押え is the provisional seizure of a debtor's assets so that a monetary claim remains secured until judgment: 仮差押えを申し立てる, 口座を仮差押えする, in the passive 仮差押えされる. The final seizure after a judgment is called 差し押さえ. In statutes it is often written without okurigana as 仮差押.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仮処分の後に本案訴訟が提起された。","romaji":"Kari shobun no ato ni hon'an soshō ga teiki sareta.","german":"After the injunction, the main action was filed.","furigana":"かりしょぶん|あと|ほんあんそしょう|ていき"},{"japanese":"訴えは本案の審理に入る前に却下された。","romaji":"Uttae wa hon'an no shinri ni hairu mae ni kyakka sareta.","german":"The suit was dismissed before it reached examination of the merits.","furigana":"うった|ほんあん|しんり|はい|まえ|きゃっか"},{"japanese":"裁判所は本案判決で原告の請求を棄却した。","romaji":"Saibansho wa hon'an hanketsu de genkoku no seikyū o kikyaku shita.","german":"In its judgment on the merits, the court rejected the plaintiff's claim.","furigana":"さいばんしょ|ほんあんはんけつ|げんこく|せいきゅう|ききゃく"}],"notes":"本案 denotes, in procedural law, the main issue, that is, the actual matter in dispute on which a decision is made on the merits: 本案訴訟 (\"main action\", as opposed to 仮処分), 本案審理, 本案判決. If a suit is dismissed already on formal grounds without reaching the merits, this is called 却下; dismissal on the merits is called 棄却. In parliament and meetings, 本案 can also mean \"the present bill\" (本案に賛成).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仮処分の申請には権利の疎明が必要だ。","romaji":"Kari shobun no shinsei ni wa kenri no somei ga hitsuyō da.","german":"An application for an injunction requires a prima facie showing of the right.","furigana":"かりしょぶん|しんせい|けんり|そめい|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"申立人は疎明資料として写真とメールを提出した。","romaji":"Mōshitatenin wa somei shiryō to shite shashin to mēru o teishutsu shita.","german":"The applicant submitted photos and e-mails as materials to substantiate the claim.","furigana":"|そめいしりょう|しゃしん|ていしゅつ"},{"japanese":"この手続きでは証明までは要らず、疎明で足りる。","romaji":"Kono tetsuzuki de wa shōmei made wa irazu, somei de tariru.","german":"This procedure doesn't require full proof; a prima facie showing is enough.","furigana":"てつづ|しょうめい|い|そめい|た"}],"notes":"疎明 is a procedural-law term: the judge only has to be convinced that an assertion is probably true, not that it is proven with certainty. This is sufficient especially in urgent proceedings such as 仮処分 and 仮差押え. Typical: 疎明する, 疎明資料 (\"materials substantiating the claim\"), 〜の疎明が必要だ. The opposite is 証明 (\"full proof\"). Older spelling: 疏明.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"選挙違反の連座制により当選が取り消された。","romaji":"Senkyo ihan no renzasei ni yori tōsen ga torikesareta.","german":"The election was invalidated under the guilt-by-association system for election violations.","furigana":"せんきょいはん|れんざせい|とうせん|と|け"},{"japanese":"元大臣は汚職事件に連座して逮捕された。","romaji":"Moto daijin wa oshoku jiken ni renza shite taiho sareta.","german":"The former minister was arrested for being implicated in the corruption case.","furigana":"もとだいじん|おしょくじけん|れんざ|たいほ"},{"japanese":"昔は、罪人の家族も連座して罰せられることがあった。","romaji":"Mukashi wa, zainin no kazoku mo renza shite basserareru koto ga atta.","german":"In the past, a criminal's family members were sometimes punished along with him.","furigana":"むかし|ざいにん|かぞく|れんざ|ばっ"}],"notes":"連座 means to be drawn into a crime or scandal and held jointly responsible for it: 〜に連座する, 汚職事件に連座して逮捕される. Well known is the 連座制 in election law: if close aides of a candidate commit election offenses, the candidate's election can be invalidated. Historically it also refers to the joint punishment of relatives (guilt by association). It differs from an actual accomplice (共犯) in that one is implicated through one's connection to the act.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"共犯者が逃亡中であると報じられた。","romaji":"Kyōhansha ga tōbōchū de aru to hōjirareta.","german":"It was reported that the accomplice is on the run.","furigana":"きょうはんしゃ|とうぼうちゅう|ほう"},{"japanese":"警察は共犯の可能性も視野に入れて捜査している。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa kyōhan no kanōsei mo shiya ni irete sōsa shite iru.","german":"The police are investigating, also keeping the possibility of an accomplice in view.","furigana":"けいさつ|きょうはん|かのうせい|しや|い|そうさ"},{"japanese":"「お母さんには内緒ね。これで君も共犯だよ。」","romaji":"\"Okaasan ni wa naisho ne. Kore de kimi mo kyouhan da yo.\"","german":"\"Don't tell Mom. Now you're an accomplice too.\"","furigana":"かあ|ないしょ|きみ|きょうはん"}],"notes":"共犯 refers to the commission of a crime jointly by several persons; the person involved is the 共犯者. Legally 共犯 covers the co-perpetrator (共同正犯), the instigator (教唆犯) and the accessory (従犯). The principal offender or mastermind is called 主犯. Colloquially it is used jokingly for someone in on a small secret: これで君も共犯だ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"従犯として懲役二年の判決を受けた。","romaji":"Jūhan to shite chōeki ninen no hanketsu o uketa.","german":"He was sentenced to two years in prison as an accessory.","furigana":"じゅうはん|ちょうえきにねん|はんけつ|う"},{"japanese":"見張り役をしただけでも、従犯に問われることがある。","romaji":"Mihariyaku o shita dake demo, jūhan ni towareru koto ga aru.","german":"Even someone who only acted as a lookout can be charged as an accessory.","furigana":"みは|やく|じゅうはん|と"},{"japanese":"従犯の刑は、正犯の刑より軽くなる。","romaji":"Jūhan no kei wa, seihan no kei yori karuku naru.","german":"The sentence for an accessory is lighter than that for the principal offender.","furigana":"じゅうはん|けい|せいはん|けい|かる"}],"notes":"従犯 is, in Japanese criminal law, the accessory, that is, someone who merely assists another's crime without being a perpetrator himself; 幇助犯 is a synonym. The opposite is 正犯 (\"principal offender\"), the leader is called 主犯. The punishment of a 従犯 is reduced compared with that of a 正犯. A purely technical term from statutes, judgments and court reports.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"累犯者に対しては刑が加重される。","romaji":"Ruihansha ni taishite wa kei ga kajū sareru.","german":"Sentences are aggravated for repeat offenders.","furigana":"るいはんしゃ|たい|けい|かじゅう"},{"japanese":"累犯を防ぐには、出所後の生活支援が欠かせない。","romaji":"Ruihan o fusegu ni wa, shussho-go no seikatsu shien ga kakasenai.","german":"To prevent reoffending, support after release from prison is indispensable.","furigana":"るいはん|ふせ|しゅっしょご|せいかつしえん|か"},{"japanese":"彼は窃盗の累犯で、今回が五回目の逮捕だった。","romaji":"Kare wa settō no ruihan de, konkai ga gokaime no taiho datta.","german":"He is a repeat thief; this was his fifth arrest.","furigana":"かれ|せっとう|るいはん|こんかい|ごかいめ|たいほ"}],"notes":"累犯 is, in criminal law, a new offense within a certain period after serving a prison sentence; the punishment is then increased (累犯加重). A person who relapses in this way is a 累犯者. The more general 再犯 simply means \"another offense, relapse\" and is much more common in everyday life and the media, for example in 再犯防止.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"前科があると就職に不利になることがある。","romaji":"Zenka ga aru to shūshoku ni furi ni naru koto ga aru.","german":"Having a criminal record can be a disadvantage when looking for a job.","furigana":"ぜんか|しゅうしょく|ふり"},{"japanese":"罰金刑でも、有罪になれば前科がつく。","romaji":"Bakkinkei demo, yūzai ni nareba zenka ga tsuku.","german":"Even with a fine, a guilty verdict leaves you with a criminal record.","furigana":"ばっきんけい|ゆうざい|ぜんか"},{"japanese":"逮捕された男には、窃盗の前科が三つあった。","romaji":"Taiho sareta otoko ni wa, settō no zenka ga mittsu atta.","german":"The arrested man had three prior convictions for theft.","furigana":"たいほ|おとこ|せっとう|ぜんか|みっ"}],"notes":"前科 is an earlier criminal conviction: 前科がある, 前科がつく (\"to get a criminal record\"), counted with 犯: 前科三犯 (\"three prior convictions\"). The broader 前歴 also includes arrests or investigations without conviction. 前科者 for a person with a record sounds derogatory. Neutral to formal, common in the news and when talking about job hunting.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"受刑者の更生を支援するプログラムが実施されている。","romaji":"Jukeisha no kōsei o shien suru puroguramu ga jisshi sarete iru.","german":"Programs supporting the rehabilitation of prisoners are being carried out.","furigana":"じゅけいしゃ|こうせい|しえん|じっし"},{"japanese":"彼は過去を反省して更生し、今は真面目に働いている。","romaji":"Kare wa kako o hansei shite kōsei shi, ima wa majime ni hataraite iru.","german":"He reflected on his past, reformed, and now works honestly.","furigana":"かれ|かこ|はんせい|こうせい|いま|まじめ|はたら"},{"japanese":"経営破綻した航空会社が、会社更生法の適用を申請した。","romaji":"Keiei hatan shita kōkū gaisha ga, kaisha kōseihō no tekiyō o shinsei shita.","german":"The bankrupt airline applied for corporate reorganization proceedings.","furigana":"けいえいはたん|こうくうがいしゃ|かいしゃこうせいほう|てきよう|しんせい"}],"notes":"更生 means that a person begins a new, orderly life after misconduct or crimes: 更生する, 更生を支援する, 更生施設. It is also used for the reorganization of a company, especially in 会社更生法 (\"Corporate Reorganization Act\"). Not to be confused with the homophones 更正 (\"correction\", e.g. of a tax assessment) and 厚生 (\"welfare\", as in 厚生労働省).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仮釈放後は保護観察に付される。","romaji":"Kari shakuhō go wa hogo kansatsu ni fusareru.","german":"After release on parole, one is placed under probation.","furigana":"かりしゃくほうご|ほごかんさつ|ふ"},{"japanese":"家庭裁判所の審判で、少年は保護観察処分となった。","romaji":"Katei saibansho no shinpan de, shōnen wa hogo kansatsu shobun to natta.","german":"In a family court hearing, the youth was placed on probation.","furigana":"かていさいばんしょ|しんぱん|しょうねん|ほごかんさつしょぶん"},{"japanese":"保護観察中は、毎月保護司と面接しなければならない。","romaji":"Hogo kansatsuchū wa, maitsuki hogoshi to mensetsu shinakereba naranai.","german":"During probation, you must meet with your probation officer every month.","furigana":"ほごかんさつちゅう|まいつきほごし|めんせつ"}],"notes":"保護観察 is the supervision of convicted persons or juveniles who are not in prison, for instance after parole or with a suspended sentence: 保護観察に付される, 保護観察処分, 保護観察中. They are looked after by full-time 保護観察官 and volunteer 保護司. The suspension of a sentence itself is called 執行猶予; 保護観察 is the supervision associated with it.","levelNote":"Compound: 保護 (N2) + 観察 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"少年法の適用年齢引き下げが議論されている。","romaji":"Shōnenhō no tekiyō nenrei hikisage ga giron sarete iru.","german":"Lowering the age of application of the Juvenile Act is being debated.","furigana":"しょうねんほう|てきようねんれいひ|さ|ぎろん"},{"japanese":"少年法により、未成年の容疑者の実名は報道されない。","romaji":"Shōnenhō ni yori, miseinen no yōgisha no jitsumei wa hōdō sarenai.","german":"Under the Juvenile Act, the real names of minor suspects are not reported.","furigana":"しょうねんほう|みせいねん|ようぎしゃ|じつめい|ほうどう"},{"japanese":"少年法が改正され、十八歳と十九歳は「特定少年」になった。","romaji":"Shōnenhō ga kaisei sare, jūhassai to jūkyūsai wa \"tokutei shōnen\" ni natta.","german":"The Juvenile Act was amended, and 18- and 19-year-olds became \"specified juveniles\".","furigana":"しょうねんほう|かいせい|じゅうはっさい|じゅうきゅうさい|とくていしょうねん"}],"notes":"少年法 is the Japanese law on delinquent juveniles; 少年 here legally means all persons under 20, boys and girls alike. It puts education and protective measures before punishment and in principle forbids publishing the names of juvenile offenders. Since the 2022 reform, 18- and 19-year-olds count as 特定少年 with stricter rules. Typical: 少年法の適用, 少年法改正, 少年法に守られる.","levelNote":"Compound: 少年 (N3) + 法 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"基本的人権は憲法で保障されている。","romaji":"Kihonteki jinken wa kenpō de hoshō sarete iru.","german":"Fundamental human rights are guaranteed by the constitution.","furigana":"きほんてきじんけん|けんぽう|ほしょう"},{"japanese":"日本国憲法の三大原則の一つは、基本的人権の尊重だ。","romaji":"Nihonkoku kenpō no sandai gensoku no hitotsu wa, kihonteki jinken no sonchō da.","german":"One of the three principles of the Japanese Constitution is respect for fundamental human rights.","furigana":"にほんこくけんぽう|さんだいげんそく|ひと|きほんてきじんけん|そんちょう"},{"japanese":"この法案は基本的人権を侵害するおそれがあると批判された。","romaji":"Kono hōan wa kihonteki jinken o shingai suru osore ga aru to hihan sareta.","german":"The bill was criticized for possibly infringing on fundamental human rights.","furigana":"ほうあん|きほんてきじんけん|しんがい|ひはん"}],"notes":"基本的人権 are the fundamental rights to which every person is entitled from birth; the Japanese Constitution names respect for them (基本的人権の尊重) as one of its three principles, alongside 国民主権 and 平和主義. Typical: 基本的人権を保障する, 基本的人権を侵害する. The shorter 人権 is more general and more everyday (人権問題, 人権侵害); 基本的人権 sounds like the constitution and school lessons.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"言論の自由は民主主義社会の柱である。","romaji":"Genron no jiyū wa minshushugi shakai no hashira de aru.","german":"Freedom of speech is a pillar of a democratic society.","furigana":"げんろん|じゆう|みんしゅしゅぎしゃかい|はしら"},{"japanese":"その国では、政府を批判する言論の自由が制限されている。","romaji":"Sono kuni de wa, seifu o hihan suru genron no jiyū ga seigen sarete iru.","german":"In that country, the freedom to criticize the government is restricted.","furigana":"くに|せいふ|ひはん|げんろん|じゆう|せいげん"},{"japanese":"言論の自由があっても、他人を傷つけていいわけではない。","romaji":"Genron no jiyū ga atte mo, tanin o kizutsukete ii wake de wa nai.","german":"Even with freedom of speech, that doesn't mean you may hurt others.","furigana":"げんろん|じゆう|たにん|きず"}],"notes":"言論の自由 is the freedom to express one's opinion in speech and writing. The Constitution speaks more broadly of 表現の自由 (\"freedom of expression\"), which also includes art and the press; for the press there is the separate 報道の自由. Typical combinations: 言論の自由を守る, 言論の自由が制限される/弾圧される. Often also used in discussions about the limits of free speech on the internet.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"憲法が結社の自由を保障している。","romaji":"Kenpō ga kessha no jiyū o hoshō shite iru.","german":"The constitution guarantees freedom of association.","furigana":"けんぽう|けっしゃ|じゆう|ほしょう"},{"japanese":"政党を自由に作れるのは、結社の自由が認められているからだ。","romaji":"Seitō o jiyū ni tsukureru no wa, kessha no jiyū ga mitomerarete iru kara da.","german":"People can freely form political parties because freedom of association is recognized.","furigana":"せいとう|じゆう|つく|けっしゃ|じゆう|みと"},{"japanese":"軍事政権のもとで、集会と結社の自由が奪われた。","romaji":"Gunji seiken no moto de, shūkai to kessha no jiyū ga ubawareta.","german":"Under the military regime, freedom of assembly and association was taken away.","furigana":"ぐんじせいけん|しゅうかい|けっしゃ|じゆう|うば"}],"notes":"結社の自由 is the right to found and join associations such as parties, clubs or labor unions; Article 21 of the Japanese Constitution guarantees it together with freedom of assembly (集会の自由). 結社 alone means \"association, society\" and sounds formal or somewhat old-fashioned (秘密結社 \"secret society\"). Typical: 結社の自由を保障する, 結社の自由を奪う.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"損害賠償請求権の消滅時効は三年である。","romaji":"Songai baishō seikyūken no shōmetsu jikō wa sannen de aru.","german":"The limitation period for claims for damages is three years.","furigana":"そんがいばいしょうせいきゅうけん|しょうめつじこう|さんねん"},{"japanese":"会社を辞めた後でも、未払いの残業代の請求権は残っている。","romaji":"Kaisha o yameta ato demo, miharai no zangyōdai no seikyūken wa nokotte iru.","german":"Even after leaving the company, the right to claim unpaid overtime remains.","furigana":"かいしゃ|や|あと|みはら|ざんぎょうだい|せいきゅうけん|のこ"},{"japanese":"彼は話し合いの結果、請求権を放棄することにした。","romaji":"Kare wa hanashiai no kekka, seikyūken o hōki suru koto ni shita.","german":"After discussions, he decided to waive his claim.","furigana":"かれ|はな|あ|けっか|せいきゅうけん|ほうき"}],"notes":"請求権 is the right to demand something from someone, mostly money: 損害賠償請求権, 代金請求権, 請求権を行使する/放棄する. It can lapse through the statute of limitations (請求権が時効で消滅する). The verb 請求する means \"to demand, bill\"; 権利 alone is more general, whereas 請求権 is the legal claim. Also well known in politics, for example in postwar compensation issues (個人の請求権).","levelNote":"Compound: 請求 (N3) + 権 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不動産の所有権移転登記を行った。","romaji":"Fudōsan no shoyūken iten tōki o okonatta.","german":"Registration of the transfer of ownership of the real estate was carried out.","furigana":"ふどうさん|しょゆうけんいてんとうき|おこな"},{"japanese":"ローンを払い終わるまで、車の所有権はローン会社にある。","romaji":"Rōn o harai owaru made, kuruma no shoyūken wa rōn gaisha ni aru.","german":"Until the loan is paid off, ownership of the car rests with the loan company.","furigana":"はら|お|くるま|しょゆうけん|がいしゃ"},{"japanese":"亡くなった父の土地の所有権をめぐって、兄弟が争っている。","romaji":"Nakunatta chichi no tochi no shoyūken o megutte, kyōdai ga arasotte iru.","german":"The siblings are fighting over ownership of their late father's land.","furigana":"な|ちち|とち|しょゆうけん|きょうだい|あらそ"}],"notes":"所有権 is legal ownership of a thing: 所有権を持つ/取得する/主張する, 所有権が移る, 所有権移転登記 (\"land register entry of the transfer of ownership\"). Legally it must be clearly distinguished from 占有 (\"actual possession\"): someone who rents a car has it in possession but is not the owner. For rights in intellectual works one says 著作権, not 所有権.","levelNote":"Compound: 所有 (N1) + 権 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不法に土地を占有している者に退去を求めた。","romaji":"Fuhō ni tochi o sen'yū shite iru mono ni taikyo o motometa.","german":"The unlawful occupant of the land was asked to leave.","furigana":"ふほう|とち|せんゆう|もの|たいきょ|もと"},{"japanese":"工事で道路を占有するには、警察と役所の許可が必要だ。","romaji":"Kōji de dōro o sen'yū suru ni wa, keisatsu to yakusho no kyoka ga hitsuyō da.","german":"To occupy a road for construction work, permission from the police and the city office is needed.","furigana":"こうじ|どうろ|せんゆう|けいさつ|やくしょ|きょか|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"その会社は国内市場の六割を占有している。","romaji":"Sono kaisha wa kokunai shijō no rokuwari o sen'yū shite iru.","german":"That company holds sixty percent of the domestic market.","furigana":"かいしゃ|こくないしじょう|ろくわり|せんゆう"}],"notes":"占有 means to actually have something in one's control or to occupy a space for oneself: 土地を占有する, 不法占有, 占有権. Legally it is to be distinguished from ownership (所有); one can possess something without being the owner. In economics it appears in 市場占有率 (\"market share\", today often シェア). Not to be confused with 占領 (\"military occupation\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"用益権が設定された土地の利用方法が争われた。","romaji":"Yōekiken ga settei sareta tochi no riyō hōhō ga arasowareta.","german":"The way land encumbered with a usufructuary right may be used was disputed.","furigana":"ようえきけん|せってい|とち|りようほうほう|あらそ"},{"japanese":"地上権は用益権の一つで、他人の土地に建物を建てることができる。","romaji":"Chijōken wa yōekiken no hitotsu de, tanin no tochi ni tatemono o tateru koto ga dekiru.","german":"Superficies is a type of right of use that allows one to build a building on another person's land.","furigana":"ちじょうけん|ようえきけん|ひと|たにん|とち|たてもの|た"},{"japanese":"所有者であっても、用益権を持つ人の利用を勝手に妨げることはできない。","romaji":"Shoyūsha de atte mo, yōekiken o motsu hito no riyō o katte ni samatageru koto wa dekinai.","german":"Even the owner cannot arbitrarily obstruct use by the holder of a usufructuary right.","furigana":"しょゆうしゃ|ようえきけん|も|ひと|りよう|かって|さまた"}],"notes":"用益権 is a right to use someone else's property, above all land, and draw income from it without being the owner. In Japanese civil law this covers the superficies right (地上権), the perpetual tenancy right (永小作権) and the easement (地役権), together called 用益物権. The counterpart is security rights (担保物権) such as the mortgage. A purely technical term from law and real estate.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"職員の越権行為が問題視された。","romaji":"Shokuin no ekken kōi ga mondaishi sareta.","german":"The employee's overstepping of authority was seen as a problem.","furigana":"しょくいん|えっけんこうい|もんだいし"},{"japanese":"部長の許可なく契約を結ぶのは、明らかな越権行為だ。","romaji":"Buchō no kyoka naku keiyaku o musubu no wa, akiraka na ekken kōi da.","german":"Signing a contract without the department head's permission is clearly an act exceeding one's authority.","furigana":"ぶちょう|きょか|けいやく|むす|あき|えっけんこうい"},{"japanese":"副会長が会長に代わって決めるのは越権だと思います。","romaji":"Fukukaichō ga kaichō ni kawatte kimeru no wa ekken da to omoimasu.","german":"I think it is overstepping for the vice chairman to decide in place of the chairman.","furigana":"ふくかいちょう|かいちょう|か|き|えっけん|おも"}],"notes":"越権 is the exceeding of one's own powers and appears almost always as 越権行為 (\"act in excess of authority\"). It can also be used predicatively: それは越権だ (\"that is not your place\"). It fits civil servants as well as employees or volunteers who make decisions they are not responsible for. Related: 権限を超える, colloquially 出しゃばる (\"to butt in\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"中央政府から自治体への権限委譲が課題となっている。","romaji":"Chūō seifu kara jichitai e no kengen ijō ga kadai to natte iru.","german":"Devolving powers from the central government to local governments is a challenge.","furigana":"ちゅうおうせいふ|じちたい|けんげんいじょう|かだい"},{"japanese":"部下に権限委譲したことで、意思決定が速くなった。","romaji":"Buka ni kengen ijō shita koto de, ishi kettei ga hayaku natta.","german":"Since authority was delegated to subordinates, decisions are made faster.","furigana":"ぶか|けんげんいじょう|いしけってい|はや"},{"japanese":"うちの社長は、なかなか権限委譲をしたがらない。","romaji":"Uchi no shachō wa, nakanaka kengen ijō o shitagaranai.","german":"Our company president really doesn't want to delegate authority.","furigana":"しゃちょう|けんげんいじょう"}],"notes":"権限委譲 means handing decision-making authority down to a lower level: in companies from the boss to employees (部下に権限委譲する), in politics from the state to prefectures and municipalities. In an administrative context the spelling 権限移譲 is often seen, with the same pronunciation. The opposite is centralization (中央集権); related is 地方分権 (\"decentralization\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"住民が公金の不正使用に対して住民監査請求を行った。","romaji":"Jūmin ga kōkin no fusei shiyō ni taishite jūmin kansa seikyū o okonatta.","german":"Residents filed a residents' audit request over the misuse of public funds.","furigana":"じゅうみん|こうきん|ふせいしよう|たい|じゅうみんかんさせいきゅう|おこな"},{"japanese":"住民監査請求は、その市に住んでいれば一人でもできる。","romaji":"Jūmin kansa seikyū wa, sono shi ni sunde ireba hitori demo dekiru.","german":"Any resident of the city can file a residents' audit request, even alone.","furigana":"じゅうみんかんさせいきゅう|し|す|ひとり"},{"japanese":"住民監査請求が退けられたため、市民団体は裁判を起こした。","romaji":"Jūmin kansa seikyū ga shirizokerareta tame, shimin dantai wa saiban o okoshita.","german":"Because the residents' audit request was rejected, the citizens' group went to court.","furigana":"じゅうみんかんさせいきゅう|しりぞ|しみんだんたい|さいばん|お"}],"notes":"住民監査請求 is a procedure under the Local Autonomy Act: residents can ask their municipality's audit commissioners (監査委員) to investigate unlawful or inappropriate expenditures and to demand remedies. Any single resident can file one; typical is 住民監査請求を行う/起こす. If it fails, one can then bring a 住民訴訟.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"住民訴訟により自治体の支出が違法と認定された。","romaji":"Jūmin soshō ni yori jichitai no shishutsu ga ihō to nintei sareta.","german":"A residents' lawsuit led to the municipality's spending being found unlawful.","furigana":"じゅうみんそしょう|じちたい|ししゅつ|いほう|にんてい"},{"japanese":"住民訴訟を起こす前に、まず住民監査請求をする必要がある。","romaji":"Jūmin soshō o okosu mae ni, mazu jūmin kansa seikyū o suru hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"Before filing a residents' lawsuit, you must first file a residents' audit request.","furigana":"じゅうみんそしょう|お|まえ|じゅうみんかんさせいきゅう|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"住民訴訟で、前市長に損害を賠償させる判決が出た。","romaji":"Jūmin soshō de, zen shichō ni songai o baishō saseru hanketsu ga deta.","german":"In the residents' lawsuit, the former mayor was ordered to pay damages.","furigana":"じゅうみんそしょう|ぜんしちょう|そんがい|ばいしょう|はんけつ|で"}],"notes":"住民訴訟 is a lawsuit in which residents challenge in court unlawful expenditures or property transactions of their municipality. A prior 住民監査請求 is a prerequisite; only if that fails can one 住民訴訟を起こす/提起する. The plaintiff acts in the public interest, not because of damage to himself. Frequent in local news about mayors and subsidies.","levelNote":"Compound: 住民 (N3) + 訴訟 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"農業への補助金が増額された。","romaji":"Nōgyō e no hojokin ga zōgaku sareta.","german":"Subsidies for agriculture were increased.","furigana":"のうぎょう|ほじょきん|ぞうがく"},{"japanese":"市の補助金を使って、屋根に太陽光パネルを付けた。","romaji":"Shi no hojokin o tsukatte, yane ni taiyōkō paneru o tsuketa.","german":"Using a city subsidy, we installed solar panels on the roof.","furigana":"し|ほじょきん|つか|やね|たいようこう|つ"},{"japanese":"社長は補助金を不正に受け取ったとして逮捕された。","romaji":"Shachō wa hojokin o fusei ni uketotta to shite taiho sareta.","german":"The company president was arrested for improperly receiving subsidies.","furigana":"しゃちょう|ほじょきん|ふせい|う|と|たいほ"}],"notes":"補助金 is a grant, mostly from the state or a local government, that covers part of the cost of a project: 補助金を出す/交付する, 補助金を受ける/申請する, 補助金の不正受給. 助成金 is similar and is often used for funding from foundations or in the employment field; 交付金 is more a general state allocation to municipalities. Neutral, very common in the news and everyday life.","levelNote":"Compound: 補助 (N1) + 金 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地方交付金の配分方法が見直された。","romaji":"Chihō kōfukin no haibun hōhō ga minaosareta.","german":"The method of distributing local grants was reviewed.","furigana":"ちほうこうふきん|はいぶんほうほう|みなお"},{"japanese":"市は国からの交付金で、新しい図書館を建てた。","romaji":"Shi wa kuni kara no kōfukin de, atarashii toshokan o tateta.","german":"The city built a new library with grants from the national government.","furigana":"し|くに|こうふきん|あたら|としょかん|た"},{"japanese":"発電所がある町には、毎年多額の交付金が入る。","romaji":"Hatsudensho ga aru machi ni wa, maitoshi tagaku no kōfukin ga hairu.","german":"Towns with a power plant receive large grants every year.","furigana":"はつでんしょ|まち|まいとしたがく|こうふきん|はい"}],"notes":"交付金 is money that an authority, mostly the state, officially pays out to municipalities or institutions: 交付金を受ける, 国からの交付金, 地方創生交付金. 交付 means \"to hand over officially\" (as in 免許証の交付). Unlike a 補助金, which funds a certain share of costs, 交付金 often leave recipients more leeway. The large fiscal allocation to municipalities is, strictly speaking, called 地方交付税.","levelNote":"Compound: 交付 (N1) + 金 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自治体が公共事業のために起債を行った。","romaji":"Jichitai ga kōkyō jigyō no tame ni kisai o okonatta.","german":"The local government issued bonds for public works.","furigana":"じちたい|こうきょうじぎょう|きさい|おこな"},{"japanese":"その企業は社債を起債して、新工場の資金を集めた。","romaji":"Sono kigyō wa shasai o kisai shite, shin kōjō no shikin o atsumeta.","german":"The company issued corporate bonds to raise funds for a new factory.","furigana":"きぎょう|しゃさい|きさい|しんこうじょう|しきん|あつ"},{"japanese":"市は起債に頼らない財政運営を目指している。","romaji":"Shi wa kisai ni tayoranai zaisei un'ei o mezashite iru.","german":"The city aims for fiscal management that does not rely on bond issuance.","furigana":"し|きさい|たよ|ざいせいうんえい|めざ"}],"notes":"起債 means taking on debt by issuing bonds, whether by the state, municipalities (地方債) or companies (社債): 起債する, 起債を行う, 起債額, 起債許可. The counterpart, repayment, is called 償還. A term from budget and financial reports; in everyday speech one simply says 借金をする.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地方債の償還が財政を圧迫している。","romaji":"Chihōsai no shōkan ga zaisei o appaku shite iru.","german":"The redemption of local bonds is putting pressure on finances.","furigana":"ちほうさい|しょうかん|ざいせい|あっぱく"},{"japanese":"この国債は十年後に元本が償還される。","romaji":"Kono kokusai wa jūnen go ni ganpon ga shōkan sareru.","german":"The principal of this government bond will be redeemed in ten years.","furigana":"こくさい|じゅうねんご|がんぽん|しょうかん"},{"japanese":"償還払いの場合、医療費は後で保険から戻ってくる。","romaji":"Shōkan barai no baai, iryōhi wa ato de hoken kara modotte kuru.","german":"With reimbursement-type payment, the medical costs come back later from insurance.","furigana":"しょうかんばら|ばあい|いりょうひ|あと|ほけん|もど"}],"notes":"償還 is the scheduled repayment of bonds and loans: 国債を償還する, 償還期限 (\"maturity\"), 満期償還. In health care, 償還払い refers to reimbursement when you first pay yourself. 返済 is the general word for repaying private debts; 償還 sounds financial-technical and fits securities and public budgets.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"国の財政状況が悪化の一途をたどっている。","romaji":"Kuni no zaisei jōkyō ga akka no itto o tadotte iru.","german":"The state's fiscal situation is steadily worsening.","furigana":"くに|ざいせいじょうきょう|あっか|いっと"},{"japanese":"市の財政が厳しいため、図書館の開館時間が短くなった。","romaji":"Shi no zaisei ga kibishii tame, toshokan no kaikan jikan ga mijikaku natta.","german":"Because the city's finances are tight, the library's opening hours were shortened.","furigana":"し|ざいせい|きび|としょかん|かいかんじかん|みじか"},{"japanese":"車を買ったので、今月の我が家の財政は火の車だ。","romaji":"Kuruma o katta node, kongetsu no wagaya no zaisei wa hi no kuruma da.","german":"Since we bought a car, our household finances are in dire straits this month.","furigana":"くるま|か|こんげつ|わ|や|ざいせい|ひ|くるま"}],"notes":"財政 is primarily the public finances of the state and municipalities: 国の財政, 財政赤字, 財政難, 財政再建, 財政が厳しい. Half-jokingly it can also refer to the financial situation of a company or household (我が家の財政は火の車). Not to be confused with 経済 (\"economy\") or 金融 (\"finance, banking\"); for the private household budget one normally says 家計.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"社会保障の財源確保が急務とされている。","romaji":"Shakai hoshō no zaigen kakuho ga kyūmu to sarete iru.","german":"Securing funding for social security is considered urgent.","furigana":"しゃかいほしょう|ざいげんかくほ|きゅうむ"},{"japanese":"減税をするなら、その分の財源をどうするのか。","romaji":"Genzei o suru nara, sono bun no zaigen o dō suru no ka.","german":"If taxes are cut, how will the lost revenue be made up?","furigana":"げんぜい|ぶん|ざいげん"},{"japanese":"この町は、宿泊税を新しい財源にしている。","romaji":"Kono machi wa, shukuhakuzei o atarashii zaigen ni shite iru.","german":"The town uses an accommodation tax as a new source of revenue.","furigana":"まち|しゅくはくぜい|あたら|ざいげん"}],"notes":"財源 is the source of money from which expenditures, especially public ones, are paid: 財源を確保する, 財源が不足する, 財源不足, 新たな財源. In political debates people often ask 財源はどうするのか (\"How is this to be financed?\"). 資金 is the money itself, 財源 the origin of the money. Formal, typical in politics and administration.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"各省庁が来年度の概算要求を提出した。","romaji":"Kaku shōchō ga rainendo no gaisan yōkyū o teishutsu shita.","german":"Each ministry submitted its budget request for the next fiscal year.","furigana":"かくしょうちょう|らいねんど|がいさんようきゅう|ていしゅつ"},{"japanese":"防衛省の概算要求は過去最大の額になった。","romaji":"Bōeishō no gaisan yōkyū wa kako saidai no gaku ni natta.","german":"The Ministry of Defense's budget request reached a record amount.","furigana":"ぼうえいしょう|がいさんようきゅう|かこさいだい|がく"},{"japanese":"概算要求の締め切りは、毎年八月末だ。","romaji":"Gaisan yōkyū no shimekiri wa, maitoshi hachigatsu-matsu da.","german":"The deadline for budget requests is the end of August every year.","furigana":"がいさんようきゅう|し|き|まいとしはちがつまつ"}],"notes":"概算要求 is, in Japan's budget process, the preliminary budget request that each ministry submits to the Ministry of Finance by the end of August. The Ministry of Finance then examines the requests (査定), and in December the Cabinet approves the draft budget (予算案). Typical in the news: 概算要求を提出する, 概算要求額, 過去最大の概算要求.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"特別会計の不透明さが批判されている。","romaji":"Tokubetsu kaikei no futōmeisa ga hihan sarete iru.","german":"The opacity of special accounts is being criticized.","furigana":"とくべつかいけい|ふとうめい|ひはん"},{"japanese":"国の予算には、一般会計と特別会計がある。","romaji":"Kuni no yosan ni wa, ippan kaikei to tokubetsu kaikei ga aru.","german":"The national budget consists of the general account and special accounts.","furigana":"くに|よさん|いっぱんかいけい|とくべつかいけい"},{"japanese":"震災の復興費用は、特別会計で管理されている。","romaji":"Shinsai no fukkō hiyō wa, tokubetsu kaikei de kanri sarete iru.","german":"The costs of earthquake reconstruction are managed in a special account.","furigana":"しんさい|ふっこうひよう|とくべつかいけい|かんり"}],"notes":"特別会計 is a special account kept separately from the general account (一般会計) for particular purposes, such as pensions, energy or reconstruction: 年金特別会計, 復興特別会計. Because income and spending there are less visible, it is often criticized in Japan as opaque. Municipalities also keep 特別会計, for example for health insurance or water supply.","levelNote":"Compound: 特別 (N4) + 会計 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"未使用予算の翌年度への繰越が認められた。","romaji":"Mishiyō yosan no yokunendo e no kurikoshi ga mitomerareta.","german":"Carrying unused budget over to the next fiscal year was approved.","furigana":"みしようよさん|よくねんど|くりこし|みと"},{"japanese":"家計簿を見ると、前月からの繰越が三万円ある。","romaji":"Kakeibo o miru to, zengetsu kara no kurikoshi ga sanman'en aru.","german":"Looking at my household account book, there is a carryover of 30,000 yen from last month.","furigana":"かけいぼ|み|ぜんげつ|くりこし|さんまんえん"},{"japanese":"このプランは、使わなかったデータの繰越ができる。","romaji":"Kono puran wa, tsukawanakatta dēta no kurikoshi ga dekiru.","german":"With this plan, unused data can be carried over.","furigana":"つか|くりこし"}],"notes":"繰越 is the carrying of a remaining or balance amount over into the next period: 翌年度への繰越, 前月からの繰越, 繰越金, also with vacation days or mobile data. The related verb is 繰り越す (transitive, \"to carry over\"), intransitive 繰り越される. As a noun it is mostly written without okurigana, 繰越, in forms and ledgers, and also 繰り越し in running text.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"会計出納の正確さが監査で確認された。","romaji":"Kaikei suitō no seikakusa ga kansa de kakunin sareta.","german":"The accuracy of the accounts and cash handling was confirmed in the audit.","furigana":"かいけいすいとう|せいかく|かんさ|かくにん"},{"japanese":"金銭出納帳をつけて、毎月の小遣いを管理している。","romaji":"Kinsen suitōchō o tsukete, maitsuki no kozukai o kanri shite iru.","german":"I keep a cash book to manage my monthly allowance.","furigana":"きんせんすいとうちょう|まいつき|こづか|かんり"},{"japanese":"母は若いころ、銀行の出納係として働いていた。","romaji":"Haha wa wakai koro, ginkō no suitōgakari to shite hataraite ita.","german":"When she was young, my mother worked as a bank teller.","furigana":"はは|わか|ぎんこう|すいとうがかり|はたら"}],"notes":"出納 is read すいとう (not しゅつのう) and refers to receiving and paying out money, or to issuing and accepting items. Typical are 金銭出納帳 (\"cash book\"), 出納係 (\"cashier\", e.g. in a bank or agency) and 出納業務. A formal word from bookkeeping and administration; in everyday speech one says お金の出し入れ instead.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"年頭に首相が施政方針演説を行った。","romaji":"Nentō ni shushō ga shisei hōshin enzetsu o okonatta.","german":"At the start of the year, the prime minister delivered the policy speech.","furigana":"ねんとう|しゅしょう|しせいほうしんえんぜつ|おこな"},{"japanese":"知事は施政方針の中で子育て支援を重点に掲げた。","romaji":"Chiji wa shisei hōshin no naka de kosodate shien o jūten ni kakageta.","german":"In his policy address, the governor made child-rearing support a priority.","furigana":"ちじ|しせいほうしん|なか|こそだ|しえん|じゅうてん|かか"},{"japanese":"野党は施政方針に具体策がないと批判した。","romaji":"Yatō wa shisei hōshin ni gutaisaku ga nai to hihan shita.","german":"The opposition criticized the policy speech for lacking concrete measures.","furigana":"やとう|しせいほうしん|ぐたいさく|ひはん"}],"notes":"施政方針 refers to the guidelines of the government's work and usually appears in 施政方針演説 (\"policy speech\"), which the prime minister gives at the start of the ordinary Diet session in January. Governors and mayors also present their 施政方針 at the start of the fiscal year. Not to be confused with 所信表明演説, the policy speech of a new prime minister or at the start of an extraordinary session. Typical verbs: 施政方針を示す・述べる・掲げる; purely press and political language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新首相が国会で所信表明演説を行った。","romaji":"Shin shushō ga kokkai de shoshin hyōmei enzetsu o okonatta.","german":"The new prime minister delivered a policy speech in the Diet.","furigana":"しんしゅしょう|こっかい|しょしんひょうめいえんぜつ|おこな"},{"japanese":"新しい社長が全社員の前で所信表明を行った。","romaji":"Atarashii shachō ga zen shain no mae de shoshin hyōmei o okonatta.","german":"The new company president stated his views before all employees.","furigana":"あたら|しゃちょう|ぜんしゃいん|まえ|しょしんひょうめい|おこな"},{"japanese":"生徒会長に選ばれた彼女は、短い所信表明でいじめゼロを訴えた。","romaji":"Seitokaichō ni erabareta kanojo wa, mijikai shoshin hyōmei de ijime zero o uttaeta.","german":"The newly elected student council president called for zero bullying in a short statement of her views.","furigana":"せいとかいちょう|えら|かのじょ|みじか|しょしんひょうめい|うった"}],"notes":"所信表明 is the public presentation of one's own convictions and plans (所信 \"conviction, intention\" + 表明 \"declaration\"). In politics it is mostly 所信表明演説 by a new prime minister or at the start of an extraordinary Diet session, in contrast to the 施政方針演説 of the ordinary session in January. Outside politics it is also said of the inaugural speech of a new company president, chairperson or candidate. Verb: 所信表明を行う or 所信を表明する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"国家安全保障会議が設置された。","romaji":"Kokka anzen hoshō kaigi ga setchi sareta.","german":"A National Security Council was established.","furigana":"こっかあんぜんほしょうかいぎ|せっち"},{"japanese":"政府は新たな国家安全保障戦略を閣議決定した。","romaji":"Seifu wa arata na kokka anzen hoshō senryaku o kakugi kettei shita.","german":"The government approved a new national security strategy by Cabinet decision.","furigana":"せいふ|あら|こっかあんぜんほしょうせんりゃく|かくぎけってい"},{"japanese":"サイバー攻撃は国家安全保障上の重大な脅威だ。","romaji":"Saibā kōgeki wa kokka anzen hoshōjō no jūdai na kyōi da.","german":"Cyberattacks are a serious threat to national security.","furigana":"こうげき|こっかあんぜんほしょうじょう|じゅうだい|きょうい"}],"notes":"国家安全保障 is composed of 国家 \"state\" and 安全保障 \"security (guarantee)\". In running text one usually says simply 安全保障 or the abbreviation 安保 (日米安保 \"Japan-U.S. security alliance\"); the long form appears mainly in proper names such as 国家安全保障会議 and 国家安全保障戦略 and in 国家安全保障上の脅威. Not to be confused with 治安, which is internal public safety (crime), whereas 安全保障 is protection against external threats.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"集団的自衛権の行使容認が議論を呼んだ。","romaji":"Shūdanteki jieiken no kōshi yōnin ga giron o yonda.","german":"Allowing the exercise of the right of collective self-defense sparked debate.","furigana":"しゅうだんてきじえいけん|こうしようにん|ぎろん|よ"},{"japanese":"政府は二〇一四年に集団的自衛権の限定的な行使を認めた。","romaji":"Seifu wa nisen jūyo-nen ni shūdanteki jieiken no genteiteki na kōshi o mitometa.","german":"In 2014, the government permitted a limited exercise of the right of collective self-defense.","furigana":"せいふ||しゅうだんてきじえいけん|げんていてき|こうし|みと"},{"japanese":"国連憲章は個別的自衛権と集団的自衛権の両方を認めている。","romaji":"Kokuren kenshō wa kobetsuteki jieiken to shūdanteki jieiken no ryōhō o mitomete iru.","german":"The UN Charter recognizes both the right of individual and collective self-defense.","furigana":"こくれんけんしょう|こべつてきじえいけん|しゅうだんてきじえいけん|りょうほう|みと"}],"notes":"集団的自衛権 is the right to come to the military aid of an attacked ally even if one's own country is not attacked; the counterpart is 個別的自衛権 \"right of individual self-defense\". Fixed combinations: 集団的自衛権を行使する, 〜の行使を容認する and 行使容認 \"permitting the exercise\". In Japan the term is politically highly controversial because of Article 9 and appears almost only in the news and constitutional debates.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"専守防衛の方針は戦後日本の安保政策の基本だ。","romaji":"Senshu bōei no hōshin wa sengo Nihon no anpo seisaku no kihon da.","german":"The exclusively defense-oriented policy is the foundation of postwar Japan's security policy.","furigana":"せんしゅぼうえい|ほうしん|せんごにほん|あんぽせいさく|きほん"},{"japanese":"反撃能力の保有が専守防衛に反するかどうかが争点になった。","romaji":"Hangeki nōryoku no hoyū ga senshu bōei ni hansuru ka dō ka ga sōten ni natta.","german":"Whether possessing a counterstrike capability violates the exclusively defense-oriented policy became the point of contention.","furigana":"はんげきのうりょく|ほゆう|せんしゅぼうえい|はん|そうてん"},{"japanese":"自衛隊は専守防衛に徹するべきだと彼は主張した。","romaji":"Jieitai wa senshu bōei ni tessuru beki da to kare wa shuchō shita.","german":"He argued that the Self-Defense Forces should adhere strictly to an exclusively defensive posture.","furigana":"じえいたい|せんしゅぼうえい|てっ|かれ|しゅちょう"}],"notes":"専守防衛 (専ら守る \"defend exclusively\") is the principle of Japanese security policy of using military force only after an attack and only to the minimum necessary extent. Typical combinations: 専守防衛の原則・方針, 専守防衛に徹する \"to adhere strictly to it\", 専守防衛を逸脱する \"to deviate from the principle\". A term from politics and the press, often discussed together with 集団的自衛権 or 反撃能力.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"憲法は国際紛争の解決手段としての武力行使を禁じている。","romaji":"Kenpō wa kokusai funsō no kaiketsu shudan to shite no buryoku kōshi o kinjite iru.","german":"The constitution prohibits the use of force as a means of settling international disputes.","furigana":"けんぽう|こくさいふんそう|かいけつしゅだん|ぶりょくこうし|きん"},{"japanese":"両国は武力行使を避け、外交による解決を目指すことで合意した。","romaji":"Ryōkoku wa buryoku kōshi o sake, gaikō ni yoru kaiketsu o mezasu koto de gōi shita.","german":"The two countries agreed to avoid the use of force and seek a diplomatic solution.","furigana":"りょうこく|ぶりょくこうし|さ|がいこう|かいけつ|めざ|ごうい"},{"japanese":"国連安保理は武力行使を容認する決議を採択した。","romaji":"Kokuren anpori wa buryoku kōshi o yōnin suru ketsugi o saitaku shita.","german":"The UN Security Council adopted a resolution authorizing the use of force.","furigana":"こくれんあんぽり|ぶりょくこうし|ようにん|けつぎ|さいたく"}],"notes":"武力行使 (武力 \"military force\" + 行使 \"exercise\") refers to the use of armed forces, usually between states; for violence between individuals one says 暴力. Typical: 武力行使に踏み切る \"to resort to force\", 武力行使を辞さない \"not to rule out force\", 武力行使を容認する. Article 9 of the Constitution speaks of 武力による威嚇又は武力の行使; as a verb one mostly uses 武力を行使する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本国憲法は交戦権を認めていない。","romaji":"Nihon koku kenpō wa kōsenken o mitomete inai.","german":"The Constitution of Japan does not recognize the right of belligerency.","furigana":"にほんこくけんぽう|こうせんけん|みと"},{"japanese":"交戦権の解釈をめぐって学者の意見は分かれている。","romaji":"Kōsenken no kaishaku o megutte gakusha no iken wa wakarete iru.","german":"Scholars are divided over the interpretation of the right of belligerency.","furigana":"こうせんけん|かいしゃく|がくしゃ|いけん|わ"},{"japanese":"国際法上、交戦権には敵国の船舶を臨検する権利も含まれる。","romaji":"Kokusaihōjō, kōsenken ni wa tekikoku no senpaku o rinken suru kenri mo fukumareru.","german":"Under international law, the right of belligerency also includes the right to stop and search enemy ships.","furigana":"こくさいほうじょう|こうせんけん|てきこく|せんぱく|りんけん|けんり|ふく"}],"notes":"交戦権 (交戦 \"to engage in combat\" + 権 \"right\") appears almost only in constitutional debate: Article 9, paragraph 2 reads 国の交戦権は、これを認めない. It is disputed whether this means the right to wage war at all, or the rights of a belligerent under international law, such as occupation or the search of enemy ships. Verbs: 交戦権を認める・否認する; strictly legal written language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"非核三原則は日本の国是の一つとされている。","romaji":"Hikaku sangensoku wa Nihon no kokuze no hitotsu to sarete iru.","german":"The Three Non-Nuclear Principles are regarded as one of Japan's national policies.","furigana":"ひかくさんげんそく|にほん|こくぜ|ひと"},{"japanese":"非核三原則とは、核兵器を「持たず、作らず、持ち込ませず」という原則だ。","romaji":"Hikaku sangensoku to wa, kakuheiki o motazu, tsukurazu, mochikomasezu to iu gensoku da.","german":"The Three Non-Nuclear Principles are the principles of not possessing, not producing, and not allowing the introduction of nuclear weapons.","furigana":"ひかくさんげんそく|かくへいき|も|つく|も|こ|げんそく"},{"japanese":"首相は非核三原則を堅持する考えを示した。","romaji":"Shushō wa hikaku sangensoku o kenji suru kangae o shimeshita.","german":"The prime minister indicated that he would uphold the Three Non-Nuclear Principles.","furigana":"しゅしょう|ひかくさんげんそく|けんじ|かんが|しめ"}],"notes":"非核三原則 (非核 \"nuclear-free\" + 三原則 \"three principles\") are the principles formulated in 1967 by Prime Minister 佐藤栄作: 持たず、作らず、持ち込ませず, that is, neither to possess nuclear weapons, nor to produce them, nor to allow their introduction. Typical verbs: 堅持する \"to hold fast to\", 見直す \"to review\". In debates about nuclear deterrence, the third point, 持ち込ませず, is usually the disputed one. A term from politics and the press.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"有事の際の対応計画が策定された。","romaji":"Yūji no sai no taiō keikaku ga sakutei sareta.","german":"A response plan for emergencies was drawn up.","furigana":"ゆうじ|さい|たいおうけいかく|さくてい"},{"japanese":"有事に備えて、食料や水を備蓄しておくべきだ。","romaji":"Yūji ni sonaete, shokuryō ya mizu o bichiku shite oku beki da.","german":"You should stockpile food and water in preparation for an emergency.","furigana":"ゆうじ|そな|しょくりょう|みず|びちく"},{"japanese":"台湾有事は日本の安全保障にも直結する問題だ。","romaji":"Taiwan yūji wa Nihon no anzen hoshō ni mo chokketsu suru mondai da.","german":"A contingency in Taiwan is an issue that directly affects Japan's security as well.","furigana":"たいわんゆうじ|にほん|あんぜんほしょう|ちょっけつ|もんだい"}],"notes":"有事 (literally \"there is an incident\") refers to an emergency, above all war or armed conflict; the opposite is 平時 \"peacetime, normal state\". Typical: 有事の際, 有事に備える, 有事法制 \"emergency legislation\" and as a suffix in 台湾有事 or 朝鮮半島有事. The word sounds formal; in everyday speech one says いざという時 or 緊急時 instead.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"紛争地域からの邦人退避が行われた。","romaji":"Funsō chiiki kara no hōjin taihi ga okonawareta.","german":"The evacuation of Japanese nationals from the conflict zone was carried out.","furigana":"ふんそうちいき|ほうじんたいひ|おこな"},{"japanese":"現地の日本大使館は在留邦人に注意を呼びかけている。","romaji":"Genchi no Nihon taishikan wa zairyū hōjin ni chūi o yobikakete iru.","german":"The local Japanese embassy is urging Japanese residents to be careful.","furigana":"げんち|にほんたいしかん|ざいりゅうほうじん|ちゅうい|よ"},{"japanese":"その事故で邦人の被害は確認されていない。","romaji":"Sono jiko de hōjin no higai wa kakunin sarete inai.","german":"No harm to Japanese nationals has been confirmed in the accident.","furigana":"じこ|ほうじん|ひがい|かくにん"}],"notes":"邦人 refers to Japanese nationals, especially abroad, and is typical of news and official language: 在留邦人 \"Japanese residing abroad\", 邦人保護, 邦人の安否確認. In everyday speech one simply says 日本人. Not to be confused with the homophone 法人 \"legal entity\". The character 邦 (\"one's own country\") is also found in 邦画 \"Japanese film\" and 邦楽 \"Japanese music\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"在外公館がパスポートの更新業務を行っている。","romaji":"Zaigai kōkan ga pasupōto no kōshin gyōmu o okonatte iru.","german":"Overseas diplomatic missions handle passport renewal services.","furigana":"ざいがいこうかん|こうしんぎょうむ|おこな"},{"japanese":"海外で子どもが生まれたら、最寄りの在外公館に出生届を提出します。","romaji":"Kaigai de kodomo ga umaretara, moyori no zaigai kōkan ni shusshō todoke o teishutsu shimasu.","german":"If a child is born abroad, you submit the birth notification to the nearest overseas mission.","furigana":"かいがい|こ|う||ざいがいこうかん|しゅっしょうとどけ|ていしゅつ"},{"japanese":"治安の悪化を受けて、政府は在外公館の警備を強化した。","romaji":"Chian no akka o ukete, seifu wa zaigai kōkan no keibi o kyōka shita.","german":"In response to the worsening security situation, the government strengthened security at its overseas missions.","furigana":"ちあん|あっか|う|せいふ|ざいがいこうかん|けいび|きょうか"}],"notes":"在外公館 is the collective term for Japan's missions abroad: 大使館 \"embassy\", 総領事館 \"consulate general\" and permanent missions to international organizations. A word of official and press language; in everyday speech one names them specifically as 大使館 or 領事館. There, for example, passports are issued and births registered, and Japanese living abroad can vote there (在外公館投票).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その声明は内政干渉に当たると抗議された。","romaji":"Sono seimei wa naisei kanshō ni ataru to kōgi sareta.","german":"It was protested that the statement amounted to interference in internal affairs.","furigana":"せいめい|ないせいかんしょう|あ|こうぎ"},{"japanese":"他国の選挙に口を出すのは明らかな内政干渉だ。","romaji":"Takoku no senkyo ni kuchi o dasu no wa akiraka na naisei kanshō da.","german":"Meddling in another country's elections is clearly interference in its internal affairs.","furigana":"たこく|せんきょ|くち|だ|あき|ないせいかんしょう"},{"japanese":"人権問題への批判を、その国は内政干渉だとして退けた。","romaji":"Jinken mondai e no hihan o, sono kuni wa naisei kanshō da to shite shirizoketa.","german":"The country rejected criticism of its human rights situation as interference in its internal affairs.","furigana":"じんけんもんだい|ひはん|くに|ないせいかんしょう|しりぞ"}],"notes":"内政干渉 (内政 \"domestic affairs\" + 干渉 \"interference\") is almost always used as an accusation: 内政干渉だ, 内政干渉に当たる, 内政干渉として反発する・退ける. The counter-principle is called 内政不干渉 \"non-interference\". Occasionally it is jokingly transferred to everyday life, when someone meddles in family or team matters. Basically a word from diplomacy and the press.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"多国間主義に基づく国際協力が重要視されている。","romaji":"Takokukan shugi ni motozuku kokusai kyōryoku ga jūyōshi sarete iru.","german":"International cooperation based on multilateralism is considered important.","furigana":"たこくかんしゅぎ|もと|こくさいきょうりょく|じゅうようし"},{"japanese":"自国第一主義が広がる中で、多国間主義の意義が問われている。","romaji":"Jikoku daiichi shugi ga hirogaru naka de, takokukan shugi no igi ga towarete iru.","german":"As the \"our own country first\" stance spreads, the significance of multilateralism is being questioned.","furigana":"じこくだいいちしゅぎ|ひろ|なか|たこくかんしゅぎ|いぎ|と"},{"japanese":"首脳たちは多国間主義を堅持することを共同声明で確認した。","romaji":"Shunōtachi wa takokukan shugi o kenji suru koto o kyōdō seimei de kakunin shita.","german":"The leaders confirmed in a joint statement that they would uphold multilateralism.","furigana":"しゅのう|たこくかんしゅぎ|けんじ|きょうどうせいめい|かくにん"}],"notes":"多国間主義 (多国間 \"among many countries\" + 主義 \"-ism\") stands for the approach of solving problems together within frameworks such as the UN or the WTO. Opposite concepts are 単独行動主義 \"unilateralism\" and, as a slogan, 自国第一主義 \"our own country first\". A word from diplomacy and editorials; typical combinations: 多国間主義を堅持する・推進する, 多国間主義に基づく.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二国間の貿易協定が締結された。","romaji":"Nikokukan no bōeki kyōtei ga teiketsu sareta.","german":"A bilateral trade agreement was concluded.","furigana":"にこくかん|ぼうえききょうてい|ていけつ"},{"japanese":"両首脳は二国間関係の改善で一致した。","romaji":"Ryō shunō wa nikokukan kankei no kaizen de itchi shita.","german":"The two leaders agreed to improve bilateral relations.","furigana":"りょうしゅのう|にこくかんかんけい|かいぜん|いっち"},{"japanese":"この問題は二国間ではなく、国際的な枠組みで話し合うべきだ。","romaji":"Kono mondai wa nikokukan de wa naku, kokusaiteki na wakugumi de hanashiau beki da.","german":"This problem should be discussed not bilaterally but in an international framework.","furigana":"もんだい|にこくかん|こくさいてき|わくぐ|はな|あ"}],"notes":"二国間 is used attributively with の (二国間の協定) or directly in compounds: 二国間関係, 二国間協議, 二国間会談. Adverbially one says 二国間で話し合う. The counterpart is 多国間 \"multilateral\"; when naming the two countries concretely, one says, e.g., 日米間 or 日中間. A word of diplomatic and business language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"渉外事務を担当する部署が新設された。","romaji":"Shōgai jimu o tantō suru busho ga shinsetsu sareta.","german":"A department in charge of external affairs was newly established.","furigana":"しょうがいじむ|たんとう|ぶしょ|しんせつ"},{"japanese":"彼は大学の渉外担当として、企業との連携を進めている。","romaji":"Kare wa daigaku no shōgai tantō to shite, kigyō to no renkei o susumete iru.","german":"As the university's external liaison, he is advancing cooperation with companies.","furigana":"かれ|だいがく|しょうがいたんとう|きぎょう|れんけい|すす"},{"japanese":"渉外弁護士として、海外企業との契約交渉を手がけています。","romaji":"Shōgai bengoshi to shite, kaigai kigyō to no keiyaku kōshō o tegakete imasu.","german":"As an international lawyer, I handle contract negotiations with foreign companies.","furigana":"しょうがいべんごし|かいがいきぎょう|けいやくこうしょう|て"}],"notes":"渉外 (literally \"to negotiate with the outside\") refers to an organization's contact with the outside, for example with authorities, business partners, customers or foreign bodies. Typical: 渉外担当 \"liaison officer\", 渉外部, 渉外事務, in banks 渉外係 for field-service client managers and 渉外弁護士 for lawyers with cross-border cases. Not the same as 外交 \"diplomacy between states\" or 広報 \"public relations\". Homophones: 生涯, 障害.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"国会議員は会期中、不逮捕特権を有する。","romaji":"Kokkai giin wa kaikichū, futaiho tokken o yūsuru.","german":"Members of the Diet have immunity from arrest during the session.","furigana":"こっかいぎいん|かいきちゅう|ふたいほとっけん|ゆう"},{"japanese":"議員の不逮捕特権は、政府による不当な弾圧を防ぐためにある。","romaji":"Giin no futaiho tokken wa, seifu ni yoru futō na dan'atsu o fusegu tame ni aru.","german":"The immunity of legislators from arrest exists to prevent unjust suppression by the government.","furigana":"ぎいん|ふたいほとっけん|せいふ|ふとう|だんあつ|ふせ"},{"japanese":"国会の許諾があれば会期中でも逮捕できるため、不逮捕特権は絶対的なものではない。","romaji":"Kokkai no kyodaku ga areba kaikichū demo taiho dekiru tame, futaiho tokken wa zettaiteki na mono de wa nai.","german":"Because they can be arrested even during a session with the Diet's consent, immunity from arrest is not absolute.","furigana":"こっかい|きょだく|かいきちゅう|たいほ|ふたいほとっけん|ぜったいてき"}],"notes":"不逮捕特権 (不逮捕 \"non-arrest\" + 特権 \"privilege\"): Under Article 50 of the Constitution, members of the Diet may be arrested during the session only with the consent of their chamber (逮捕許諾); diplomats also enjoy 不逮捕特権. To be distinguished from 免責特権 (Article 51), which exempts legislators from liability for speeches and votes in the Diet. Typical: 不逮捕特権を有する・持つ; legal technical language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"議員の発言に対する免責特権が保障されている。","romaji":"Giin no hatsugen ni taisuru menseki tokken ga hoshō sarete iru.","german":"Legislators' immunity for their statements is guaranteed.","furigana":"ぎいん|はつげん|たい|めんせきとっけん|ほしょう"},{"japanese":"自己破産の手続きで、借金の免責が認められた。","romaji":"Jiko hasan no tetsuzuki de, shakkin no menseki ga mitomerareta.","german":"In the personal bankruptcy procedure, the discharge of his debts was granted.","furigana":"じこはさん|てつづ|しゃっきん|めんせき|みと"},{"japanese":"このアプリの利用規約には長い免責事項が書かれている。","romaji":"Kono apuri no riyō kiyaku ni wa nagai menseki jikō ga kakarete iru.","german":"The terms of use of this app contain a long disclaimer.","furigana":"りようきやく|なが|めんせきじこう|か"}],"notes":"免責 (免 \"to exempt\" + 責 \"responsibility\") is exemption from liability or obligations. Common compounds: 免責事項 \"disclaimer\", 免責条項 \"exemption clause\", 免責特権 \"immunity (indemnity)\" of legislators and, in insolvency law, 免責決定 \"discharge of debts\". Typical is 〜の免責が認められる; as a する-verb (免責する, mostly 免責される) it is rather legal; in everyday speech one says 責任を負わない.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"被告人が裁判所に出頭した。","romaji":"Hikokunin ga saibansho ni shuttō shita.","german":"The defendant appeared in court.","furigana":"ひこくにん|さいばんしょ|しゅっとう"},{"japanese":"事件の翌日、容疑者が自ら警察署に出頭した。","romaji":"Jiken no yokujitsu, yōgisha ga mizukara keisatsusho ni shuttō shita.","german":"The day after the incident, the suspect turned himself in at the police station.","furigana":"じけん|よくじつ|ようぎしゃ|みずか|けいさつしょ|しゅっとう"},{"japanese":"税務署から出頭を求める通知が届いた。","romaji":"Zeimusho kara shuttō o motomeru tsūchi ga todoita.","german":"A notice arrived from the tax office requiring me to appear in person.","furigana":"ぜいむしょ|しゅっとう|もと|つうち|とど"}],"notes":"出頭 means to appear in person, on order, before a court, the police or an authority: 裁判所に出頭する, 出頭を命じる・求める, 出頭命令. The second important meaning is \"to turn oneself in\": 警察に出頭する. Legally to be distinguished from this is 自首, voluntary surrender before the crime or the culprit is known, which mitigates punishment; someone who is already wanted merely \"turns himself in\" (出頭). Intransitive する-verb, the place takes に.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"証人が裁判所から召喚状を受け取った。","romaji":"Shōnin ga saibansho kara shōkanjō o uketotta.","german":"The witness received a summons from the court.","furigana":"しょうにん|さいばんしょ|しょうかんじょう|う|と"},{"japanese":"国会は事件の関係者を証人として召喚した。","romaji":"Kokkai wa jiken no kankeisha o shōnin to shite shōkan shita.","german":"The Diet summoned those involved in the scandal as witnesses.","furigana":"こっかい|じけん|かんけいしゃ|しょうにん|しょうかん"},{"japanese":"ゲームで強力なモンスターを召喚した。","romaji":"Gēmu de kyōryoku na monsutā o shōkan shita.","german":"In the game, I summoned a powerful monster.","furigana":"きょうりょく|しょうかん"}],"notes":"召喚 means to formally call someone in, above all to summon them in court or in parliament: 証人を召喚する, 召喚に応じる, 召喚状 \"summons (document)\". Transitive する-verb; from the summoned person's perspective it is in the passive: 裁判所に召喚される. From fantasy and games one knows the second meaning, \"to summon (a creature)\" (召喚魔法, 召喚獣). Not to be confused with the homophone 召還 \"to recall (an ambassador)\": 大使を召還する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"出頭に応じない証人に対して勾引状が発せられた。","romaji":"Shuttō ni ōjinai shōnin ni taishite kōinjō ga hasserareta.","german":"A warrant for compulsory appearance was issued against the witness who did not appear.","furigana":"しゅっとう|おう|しょうにん|たい|こういんじょう|はっ"},{"japanese":"正当な理由なく召喚に応じない被告人は、勾引されることがある。","romaji":"Seitō na riyū naku shōkan ni ōjinai hikokunin wa, kōin sareru koto ga aru.","german":"A defendant who does not respond to a summons without good reason may be brought in by force.","furigana":"せいとう|りゆう|しょうかん|おう|ひこくにん|こういん"},{"japanese":"勾引された被告人は、勾留されない限り二十四時間以内に釈放される。","romaji":"Kōin sareta hikokunin wa, kōryū sarenai kagiri nijūyo-jikan inai ni shakuhō sareru.","german":"A defendant who has been brought in by force is released within 24 hours unless detention is ordered.","furigana":"こういん|ひこくにん|こうりゅう|かぎ|にじゅうよじかんいない|しゃくほう"}],"notes":"勾引 is a technical term of the Code of Criminal Procedure: the court has a defendant or witness who fails to obey a summons (召喚) without good reason brought in by force; the document is called 勾引状. Unlike 逮捕 \"arrest\", it serves only to bring the person before the court; afterward 勾留 \"pretrial detention\" may follow. Mostly in the passive: 勾引される. In newspapers the substitute spelling 拘引 is also found.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"弁護士が拘置所で被疑者と接見した。","romaji":"Bengoshi ga kōchisho de higisha to sekken shita.","german":"The lawyer met with the suspect at the detention center.","furigana":"べんごし|こうちしょ|ひぎしゃ|せっけん"},{"japanese":"証拠隠滅のおそれがあるとして、家族との接見が禁止された。","romaji":"Shōko inmetsu no osore ga aru to shite, kazoku to no sekken ga kinshi sareta.","german":"Contact with his family was banned on the grounds of a risk of destroying evidence.","furigana":"しょうこいんめつ|かぞく|せっけん|きんし"},{"japanese":"ローマ教皇はバチカンで各国の大使と接見した。","romaji":"Rōma kyōkō wa Bachikan de kakkoku no taishi to sekken shita.","german":"The Pope received the ambassadors of various countries in audience at the Vatican.","furigana":"きょうこう|かっこく|たいし|せっけん"}],"notes":"接見 has two uses: formally, the reception of a visitor by a high-ranking person (an audience), and in criminal proceedings, the conversation of a detained person with a lawyer or relatives: 弁護士と接見する, 接見交通権, 接見禁止. Mind the direction: 接見 is said from the side of the receiving, higher-ranking person; someone who is admitted to a high-ranking person has a 謁見. In general language, visiting someone in prison is called 面会. Intransitive する-verb with と.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"裁判を待つ間、被告人は拘置所に収容されている。","romaji":"Saiban o matsu aida, hikokunin wa kōchisho ni shūyō sarete iru.","german":"While awaiting trial, the defendant is held in a detention center.","furigana":"さいばん|ま|あいだ|ひこくにん|こうちしょ|しゅうよう"},{"japanese":"死刑囚は刑務所ではなく拘置所に収容される。","romaji":"Shikeishū wa keimusho de wa naku kōchisho ni shūyō sareru.","german":"Death-row inmates are held in a detention center rather than a prison.","furigana":"しけいしゅう|けいむしょ|こうちしょ|しゅうよう"},{"japanese":"弁護士は毎日のように拘置所へ通い、依頼人と接見した。","romaji":"Bengoshi wa mainichi no yō ni kōchisho e kayoi, irainin to sekken shita.","german":"The lawyer went to the detention center almost every day and met with his client.","furigana":"べんごし|まいにち|こうちしょ|かよ|いらいにん|せっけん"}],"notes":"拘置所 is the state facility for detainees whose judgment is not yet final; in Japan, prisoners sentenced to death are also held there. The counterpart is 刑務所 \"prison\" for convicted prisoners serving sentences. To be distinguished from this is 留置場 \"police holding cell\" at the police station, where arrested persons are held first. Typical: 拘置所に収容される, 東京拘置所.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"矯正施設での処遇改善が求められている。","romaji":"Kyōsei shisetsu de no shogū kaizen ga motomerarete iru.","german":"Improving treatment in correctional facilities is being called for.","furigana":"きょうせいしせつ|しょぐうかいぜん|もと"},{"japanese":"子どもの歯並びを矯正するために、歯医者に通っている。","romaji":"Kodomo no hanarabi o kyōsei suru tame ni, haisha ni kayotte iru.","german":"I take my child to the dentist to have his teeth straightened.","furigana":"こ|はなら|きょうせい|はいしゃ|かよ"},{"japanese":"レーシック手術で視力を矯正した。","romaji":"Rēshikku shujutsu de shiryoku o kyōsei shita.","german":"I had my eyesight corrected with LASIK surgery.","furigana":"しゅじゅつ|しりょく|きょうせい"}],"notes":"矯正 means to straighten something crooked or faulty: 歯列矯正 \"orthodontic treatment\", 視力矯正, 姿勢を矯正する. In the penal system it stands for the rehabilitation of offenders: 矯正施設 (prisons, juvenile training schools), 矯正教育. Unlike 修正 \"correction of a text or plan\" and 訂正 \"correction of an error\", 矯正 concerns bodies, habits or behavior. Not to be confused with the homophone 強制 \"compulsion\"; transitive する-verb (〜を矯正する).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"判決確定後に受刑者が刑務所に収監された。","romaji":"Hanketsu kakutei go ni jukeisha ga keimusho ni shūkan sareta.","german":"After the judgment became final, the convict was sent to prison.","furigana":"はんけつかくていご|じゅけいしゃ|けいむしょ|しゅうかん"},{"japanese":"体調不良を理由に、収監が延期された。","romaji":"Taichō furyō o riyū ni, shūkan ga enki sareta.","german":"His incarceration was postponed for health reasons.","furigana":"たいちょうふりょう|りゆう|しゅうかん|えんき"},{"japanese":"収監中の兄に毎月手紙を書いている。","romaji":"Shūkanchū no ani ni maitsuki tegami o kaite iru.","german":"I write a letter every month to my brother, who is in prison.","furigana":"しゅうかんちゅう|あに|まいつきてがみ|か"}],"notes":"収監 refers to being committed to a detention facility, usually after a final judgment: 刑務所に収監される, 収監中, 収監を免れる. Compared with 逮捕 \"arrest\" and 勾留 \"pretrial detention\", 収監 marks the start of imprisonment or the imprisonment itself; the opposite is 釈放 or 出所. Almost always passive or from the authorities' viewpoint (〜を収監する); a word of press language; neutrally one says 刑務所に入る.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"出所後の社会復帰支援が重要だ。","romaji":"Shussho go no shakai fukki shien ga jūyō da.","german":"Support for reintegration into society after release from prison is important.","furigana":"しゅっしょご|しゃかいふっきしえん|じゅうよう"},{"japanese":"彼は刑期を終えて、先月出所した。","romaji":"Kare wa keiki o oete, sengetsu shussho shita.","german":"He finished his sentence and was released last month.","furigana":"かれ|けいき|お|せんげつしゅっしょ"},{"japanese":"その記事には情報の出所が書かれていない。","romaji":"Sono kiji ni wa jōhō no shussho ga kakarete inai.","german":"The article does not state the source of the information.","furigana":"きじ|じょうほう|しゅっしょ|か"}],"notes":"出所 has two main meanings: in the penal system \"release from custody\" as an intransitive する-verb (刑務所から出所する, 出所後), and \"origin, source\" (情報の出所, 出所不明). In the meaning \"source\" one also reads it colloquially as でどころ (うわさの出どころ). The opposite of release is 入所 or 収監; officially one also speaks of 釈放, and the older 出獄 is hardly used any more.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"模範的な態度により仮出獄が認められた。","romaji":"Mohanteki na taido ni yori kari shutsugoku ga mitomerareta.","german":"Parole was granted because of exemplary behavior.","furigana":"もはんてき|たいど|かりしゅつごく|みと"},{"japanese":"仮出獄中に再び罪を犯し、刑務所に戻された。","romaji":"Kari shutsugokuchū ni futatabi tsumi o okashi, keimusho ni modosareta.","german":"While on parole, he committed another crime and was returned to prison.","furigana":"かりしゅつごくちゅう|ふたた|つみ|おか|けいむしょ|もど"},{"japanese":"現在の法律では、仮出獄は仮釈放と呼ばれている。","romaji":"Genzai no hōritsu de wa, kari shutsugoku wa karishakuhō to yobarete iru.","german":"Under current law, parole is called \"karishakuhō\".","furigana":"げんざい|ほうりつ|かりしゅつごく|かりしゃくほう|よ"}],"notes":"仮出獄 (仮 \"provisional\" + 出獄 \"release from prison\") is the conditional early release of a prisoner. This is the older statutory term; since the penal reform of the mid-2000s, the law calls it 仮釈放, and the press and everyday speech today almost only use 仮釈放. Typical: 仮出獄が認められる・許可される, 仮出獄中. The word is found mainly in older texts and novels.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"知的財産権の保護が国際的に強化されている。","romaji":"Chiteki zaisan ken no hogo ga kokusaiteki ni kyōka sarete iru.","german":"Protection of intellectual property rights is being strengthened internationally.","furigana":"ちてきざいさんけん|ほご|こくさいてき|きょうか"},{"japanese":"他社の知的財産権を侵害しないよう注意が必要だ。","romaji":"Tasha no chiteki zaisanken o shingai shinai yō chūi ga hitsuyō da.","german":"Care is needed not to infringe other companies' intellectual property rights.","furigana":"たしゃ|ちてきざいさんけん|しんがい|ちゅうい|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"特許権や著作権は知的財産権の一種だ。","romaji":"Tokkyoken ya chosakuken wa chiteki zaisanken no isshu da.","german":"Patent rights and copyrights are a kind of intellectual property right.","furigana":"とっきょけん|ちょさくけん|ちてきざいさんけん|いっしゅ"}],"notes":"知的財産権 is the umbrella term for rights in intellectual creations: 特許権 \"patent right\", 著作権 \"copyright\", 商標権 \"trademark right\", 意匠権 \"design right\" etc. In business and the media it is often shortened to 知財 (知財戦略, 知財部). Typical combinations: 知的財産権を侵害する \"to infringe\", 〜の保護, 〜を取得する. Technical language from law and business.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"製品のデザインを意匠登録した。","romaji":"Seihin no dezain o ishō tōroku shita.","german":"We registered the product's design as an industrial design.","furigana":"せいひん|いしょうとうろく"},{"japanese":"店内には職人が意匠を凝らした家具が並んでいる。","romaji":"Tennai ni wa shokunin ga ishō o korashita kagu ga narande iru.","german":"The shop displays furniture that craftsmen have made with great ingenuity.","furigana":"てんない|しょくにん|いしょう|こ|かぐ|なら"},{"japanese":"その会社は他社の意匠権を侵害したとして訴えられた。","romaji":"Sono kaisha wa tasha no ishōken o shingai shita to shite uttaerareta.","german":"The company was sued for allegedly infringing a competitor's design right.","furigana":"かいしゃ|たしゃ|いしょうけん|しんがい|うった"}],"notes":"意匠 means, on the one hand, the aesthetic form of a product, and in law the \"registered design\": 意匠権 \"design right\", 意匠登録 \"design registration\". On the other hand, in elevated language it stands for an artful design or idea, above all in the fixed phrase 意匠を凝らす \"to design with great ingenuity\". In everyday speech one mostly says デザイン for design; 意匠 sounds technical or literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"企業間の価格協定が独占禁止法に違反した。","romaji":"Kigyōkan no kakaku kyōtei ga dokusen kinshihō ni ihan shita.","german":"The price agreement between companies violated the Antimonopoly Act.","furigana":"きぎょうかん|かかくきょうてい|どくせんきんしほう|いはん"},{"japanese":"大手通信会社が独占禁止法違反の疑いで立ち入り検査を受けた。","romaji":"Ōte tsūshin gaisha ga dokusen kinshihō ihan no utagai de tachiiri kensa o uketa.","german":"A major telecommunications company underwent an on-site inspection on suspicion of violating the Antimonopoly Act.","furigana":"おおてつうしんがいしゃ|どくせんきんしほういはん|うたが|た|い|けんさ|う"},{"japanese":"取引先に不当な値下げを強いることは、独占禁止法で禁じられている。","romaji":"Torihikisaki ni futō na nesage o shiiru koto wa, dokusen kinshihō de kinjirarete iru.","german":"Forcing business partners into unjustified price cuts is prohibited under the Antimonopoly Act.","furigana":"とりひきさき|ふとう|ねさ|し|どくせんきんしほう|きん"}],"notes":"独占禁止法 is the usual name of Japan's competition law (officially 私的独占の禁止及び公正取引の確保に関する法律), often abbreviated in the news to 独禁法. It prohibits monopolization, cartels (カルテル, 談合) and the abuse of a dominant market position; the authority responsible is the 公正取引委員会. Typical: 独占禁止法に違反する, 独占禁止法違反の疑い.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"公正取引委員会がカルテルの調査に乗り出した。","romaji":"Kōsei torihiki iinkai ga karuteru no chōsa ni noridashita.","german":"The Fair Trade Commission launched an investigation into the cartel.","furigana":"こうせいとりひきいいんかい|ちょうさ|の|だ"},{"japanese":"公正取引委員会は、その合併を認める判断を示した。","romaji":"Kōsei torihiki iinkai wa, sono gappei o mitomeru handan o shimeshita.","german":"The Fair Trade Commission indicated that it would approve the merger.","furigana":"こうせいとりひきいいんかい|がっぺい|みと|はんだん|しめ"},{"japanese":"公正取引委員会は大手コンビニに排除措置命令を出した。","romaji":"Kōsei torihiki iinkai wa ōte konbini ni haijo sochi meirei o dashita.","german":"The Fair Trade Commission issued a cease-and-desist order to a major convenience store chain.","furigana":"こうせいとりひきいいんかい|おおて|はいじょそちめいれい|だ"}],"notes":"公正取引委員会 is Japan's antitrust and competition authority, which oversees compliance with the 独占禁止法; in the news mostly 公取委, colloquially also 公取. Typical actions: 立ち入り検査 \"on-site inspection\", 排除措置命令 \"cease-and-desist order\", 課徴金納付命令 \"surcharge payment order\" and the review of mergers (合併審査). The name literally means \"Fair Trade Commission\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"確定申告の期限は三月十五日までだ。","romaji":"Kakutei shinkoku no kigen wa sangatsu jūgonichi made da.","german":"The deadline for the final tax return is March 15.","furigana":"かくていしんこく|きげん|さんがつじゅうごにち"},{"japanese":"税関で申告するものはありますか。","romaji":"Zeikan de shinkoku suru mono wa arimasu ka.","german":"Do you have anything to declare at customs?","furigana":"ぜいかん|しんこく"},{"japanese":"所得を少なく申告して、追徴課税された。","romaji":"Shotoku o sukunaku shinkoku shite, tsuichō kazei sareta.","german":"He underreported his income and was hit with additional taxes.","furigana":"しょとく|すく|しんこく|ついちょうかぜい"}],"notes":"申告 is the obligatory declaration or statement to an authority: 確定申告 \"income tax return\", 税関で申告する \"to declare at customs\", 所得を申告する. As a する-verb it is transitive (〜を申告する). 自己申告 means \"self-declaration\", 過少申告 \"underreporting\". Unlike 届け出 \"notification (e.g. of moving, a birth)\", 申告 mostly concerns taxes, customs and other declarations required by law.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"国税局の査察部が脱税の調査を行った。","romaji":"Kokuzeikyoku no sasatsubu ga datsuzei no chōsa o okonatta.","german":"The National Tax Bureau's investigation division conducted an inquiry into tax evasion.","furigana":"こくぜいきょく|ささつぶ|だつぜい|ちょうさ|おこな"},{"japanese":"国際原子力機関が核施設の査察を行った。","romaji":"Kokusai genshiryoku kikan ga kaku shisetsu no sasatsu o okonatta.","german":"The International Atomic Energy Agency inspected the nuclear facilities.","furigana":"こくさいげんしりょくきかん|かくしせつ|ささつ|おこな"},{"japanese":"消防の抜き打ち査察で、非常口の不備が見つかった。","romaji":"Shōbō no nukiuchi sasatsu de, hijōguchi no fubi ga mitsukatta.","german":"A surprise fire department inspection found deficiencies in the emergency exits.","furigana":"しょうぼう|ぬ|う|ささつ|ひじょうぐち|ふび|み"}],"notes":"査察 is an official inspection of whether regulations are being observed: 国税局の査察部 \"tax investigation division\" (colloquially マルサ), 核査察 \"nuclear inspection\" by the IAEA, 消防査察 \"fire inspection\". Typical: 査察に入る, 査察を受ける, 抜き打ち査察 \"surprise inspection\". Unlike 視察 \"an inspection tour for information\", 査察 has the character of control or investigation; 監査 is the audit of accounts and management.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"海上保安庁が不審船の臨検を行った。","romaji":"Kaijō hoanchō ga fushinsen no rinken o okonatta.","german":"The Japan Coast Guard boarded and inspected a suspicious vessel.","furigana":"かいじょうほあんちょう|ふしんせん|りんけん|おこな"},{"japanese":"労働基準監督官が工場に臨検に入り、長時間労働の実態を調べた。","romaji":"Rōdō kijun kantokukan ga kōjō ni rinken ni hairi, chōjikan rōdō no jittai o shirabeta.","german":"A labor standards inspector went to the factory on an inspection and investigated the reality of long working hours.","furigana":"ろうどうきじゅんかんとくかん|こうじょう|りんけん|はい|ちょうじかんろうどう|じったい|しら"},{"japanese":"公海上での臨検は、国際法で厳しく制限されている。","romaji":"Kōkaijō de no rinken wa, kokusaihō de kibishiku seigen sarete iru.","german":"Boarding and searching on the high seas is strictly limited by international law.","furigana":"こうかいじょう|りんけん|こくさいほう|きび|せいげん"}],"notes":"臨検 (臨む \"to go to the spot\" + 検 \"to inspect\") is an official inspection directly on site with a right of entry, for example the search of a ship at sea by the coast guard or navy, or the visit by a labor standards inspector to a workplace (労働基準監督官の臨検). Typical: 臨検を行う, 臨検に入る, 船舶を臨検する. A pure technical term from law and administration; more general are 立ち入り検査 or 査察.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"処分に不服がある場合は行政不服審査を請求できる。","romaji":"Shobun ni fufuku ga aru baai wa gyōsei fufuku shinsa o seikyū dekiru.","german":"If you are dissatisfied with a disposition, you can request an administrative appeal review.","furigana":"しょぶん|ふふく|ばあい|ぎょうせいふふくしんさ|せいきゅう"},{"japanese":"行政不服審査は裁判より費用がかからず、手続きも簡単だ。","romaji":"Gyōsei fufuku shinsa wa saiban yori hiyō ga kakarazu, tetsuzuki mo kantan da.","german":"An administrative appeal is cheaper than a lawsuit, and the procedure is simpler.","furigana":"ぎょうせいふふくしんさ|さいばん|ひよう|てつづ|かんたん"},{"japanese":"行政不服審査法の改正で、審査請求の期間が三か月に延びた。","romaji":"Gyōsei fufuku shinsahō no kaisei de, shinsa seikyū no kikan ga sankagetsu ni nobita.","german":"With the amendment of the Administrative Appeal Act, the period for requesting review was extended to three months.","furigana":"ぎょうせいふふくしんさほう|かいせい|しんさせいきゅう|きかん|さん|げつ|の"}],"notes":"行政不服審査 is the procedure in which citizens object to an administrative act (処分) to the administration itself, without going to court; the counterpart is the 行政訴訟 \"administrative lawsuit\". The legal basis is the 行政不服審査法; the usual form of the application is called 審査請求. A technical term from administration and law, found in official notices and agency guides.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不許可処分に対して審査請求を行った。","romaji":"Fukyoka shobun ni taishite shinsa seikyū o okonatta.","german":"A request for review was filed against the denial of permission.","furigana":"ふきょかしょぶん|たい|しんさせいきゅう|おこな"},{"japanese":"特許を取るには、出願から三年以内に審査請求をしなければならない。","romaji":"Tokkyo o toru ni wa, shutsugan kara sannen inai ni shinsa seikyū o shinakereba naranai.","german":"To obtain a patent, you must file a request for examination within three years of filing the application.","furigana":"とっきょ|と|しゅつがん|さんねんいない|しんさせいきゅう"},{"japanese":"年金の不支給決定に納得できず、審査請求をした。","romaji":"Nenkin no fushikyū kettei ni nattoku dekizu, shinsa seikyū o shita.","german":"I could not accept the decision to deny my pension, so I filed a request for review.","furigana":"ねんきん|ふしきゅうけってい|なっとく|しんさせいきゅう"}],"notes":"審査請求 is the formal request for review, above all the appeal against an administrative act under the 行政不服審査法 (〜に対して審査請求をする). In patent law, 出願審査請求 is the request for substantive examination of a patent application. Typical: 審査請求を行う・する, 審査請求が棄却される \"is rejected\". To be distinguished from a mere 申請 \"application (for a benefit)\": 審査請求 is directed against a decision already made or requests an examination.","levelNote":"Compound: 審査 (N1) + 請求 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大臣が官邸で執務を行っている。","romaji":"Daijin ga kantei de shitsumu o okonatte iru.","german":"The minister is carrying out his duties at the official residence.","furigana":"だいじん|かんてい|しつむ|おこな"},{"japanese":"市長は執務室で来客と面会した。","romaji":"Shichō wa shitsumushitsu de raikyaku to menkai shita.","german":"The mayor met a visitor in his office.","furigana":"しちょう|しつむしつ|らいきゃく|めんかい"},{"japanese":"当事務所の執務時間は平日九時から十七時までです。","romaji":"Tō jimusho no shitsumu jikan wa heijitsu kuji kara jūshichiji made desu.","german":"The office hours of our firm are from 9:00 to 17:00 on weekdays.","furigana":"とうじむしょ|しつむじかん|へいじつくじ|じゅうしちじ"}],"notes":"執務 (執る \"to carry out\" + 務 \"duty\") is the performance of official business, especially by officeholders, agencies and law offices: 執務室 \"office room\", 執務時間 \"office hours\", 執務中. As an intransitive する-verb: 官邸で執務する. Compared with 勤務 \"duty, work (for an employer)\" and 業務 \"business activity\", 執務 is more formal and related to public offices.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"陳腐な政策では現代の課題に対処できない。","romaji":"Chinpu na seisaku de wa gendai no kadai ni taisho dekinai.","german":"Stale policies cannot cope with today's challenges.","furigana":"ちんぷ|せいさく|げんだい|かだい|たいしょ"},{"japanese":"その映画は結末が陳腐で、がっかりした。","romaji":"Sono eiga wa ketsumatsu ga chinpu de, gakkari shita.","german":"The ending of that movie was so hackneyed that I was disappointed.","furigana":"えいが|けつまつ|ちんぷ"},{"japanese":"技術の進歩が速く、去年の製品がもう陳腐化している。","romaji":"Gijutsu no shinpo ga hayaku, kyonen no seihin ga mō chinpuka shite iru.","german":"Technology advances so fast that last year's products are already obsolete.","furigana":"ぎじゅつ|しんぽ|はや|きょねん|せいひん|ちんぷか"}],"notes":"陳腐 (陳 \"old\" + 腐 \"rotten\") is a な-adjective for things that have become worn out and uninteresting through frequent use: 陳腐な表現 \"hackneyed phrase\", 陳腐なセリフ, 陳腐な発想. 陳腐化 means \"becoming obsolete\", for example of technology or products. Unlike 古い (merely \"old\"), 陳腐 always carries a negative judgment: banal, clichéd, without originality. Literary, frequent in reviews and commentary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"機密情報の漏洩が安全保障上の問題を引き起こした。","romaji":"Kimitsu jōhō no rōei ga anzen hoshōjō no mondai o hikiokoshita.","german":"The leak of classified information caused a security problem.","furigana":"きみつじょうほう|ろうえい|あんぜんほしょうじょう|もんだい|ひ|お"},{"japanese":"この書類には会社の機密が含まれているので、持ち出し禁止です。","romaji":"Kono shorui ni wa kaisha no kimitsu ga fukumarete iru node, mochidashi kinshi desu.","german":"This document contains company secrets, so it must not be taken out.","furigana":"しょるい|かいしゃ|きみつ|ふく|も|だ|きんし"},{"japanese":"取引先と機密保持契約を結んだ。","romaji":"Torihikisaki to kimitsu hoji keiyaku o musunda.","german":"We signed a confidentiality agreement with our business partner.","furigana":"とりひきさき|きみつほじけいやく|むす"}],"notes":"機密 means \"secrets\" of states, agencies or companies, that is, strictly confidential information: 機密情報, 機密文書, 国家機密, 企業機密. Typical verbs are 機密を漏らす/守る; 機密保持契約 is a confidentiality agreement (NDA). 秘密 is more general and also personal (\"my secret\"), 極秘 means \"top secret\", 内密 \"privately, discreetly\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"公益通報者保護法が改正され、保護対象が拡大された。","romaji":"Kōeki tsūhōsha hogohō ga kaisei sare, hogo taishō ga kakudai sareta.","german":"The Whistleblower Protection Act was amended and the scope of protection was expanded.","furigana":"こうえきつうほうしゃほごほう|かいせい|ほごたいしょう|かくだい"},{"japanese":"社内の不正を知り、外部の窓口に公益通報した。","romaji":"Shanai no fusei o shiri, gaibu no madoguchi ni kōeki tsūhō shita.","german":"Upon learning of wrongdoing within the company, he reported it to an external contact point.","furigana":"しゃない|ふせい|し|がいぶ|まどぐち|こうえきつうほう"},{"japanese":"公益通報を理由に従業員を解雇することは禁じられている。","romaji":"Kōeki tsūhō o riyū ni jūgyōin o kaiko suru koto wa kinjirarete iru.","german":"It is prohibited to dismiss an employee on the grounds of a whistleblower report.","furigana":"こうえきつうほう|りゆう|じゅうぎょういん|かいこ|きん"}],"notes":"公益通報 is the legal term for reporting violations of the law in one's own company or agency, that is, whistleblowing; the basis is the 公益通報者保護法 \"Whistleblower Protection Act\". In everyday speech and the press one mostly says 内部告発. Typical: 公益通報窓口 \"reporting desk\", 公益通報者 \"whistleblower\", 公益通報を受ける. 通報 alone means \"report (to the police/an authority)\", e.g. 警察に通報する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"労働者が不当解雇を理由に訴訟を起こした。","romaji":"Rōdōsha ga futō kaiko o riyū ni soshō o okoshita.","german":"The worker filed a lawsuit on the grounds of unfair dismissal.","furigana":"ろうどうしゃ|ふとうかいこ|りゆう|そしょう|お"},{"japanese":"裁判で不当解雇が認められ、職場に復帰できた。","romaji":"Saiban de futō kaiko ga mitomerare, shokuba ni fukki dekita.","german":"The court recognized the dismissal as unfair, and I was able to return to my workplace.","furigana":"さいばん|ふとうかいこ|みと|しょくば|ふっき"},{"japanese":"妊娠を理由にした解雇は不当解雇に当たる。","romaji":"Ninshin o riyū ni shita kaiko wa futō kaiko ni ataru.","german":"Dismissal on the grounds of pregnancy constitutes unfair dismissal.","furigana":"にんしん|りゆう|かいこ|ふとうかいこ|あ"}],"notes":"不当解雇 (不当 \"unjustified\" + 解雇 \"dismissal\") is a dismissal without objectively justified reason, which is invalid under Japanese labor law. Typical: 不当解雇で訴える, 不当解雇に当たる, 不当解雇が認められる. Colloquially one says クビ for dismissal; technical terms for other kinds of dismissal are 整理解雇 \"redundancy dismissal\" and 懲戒解雇 \"disciplinary dismissal\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"労働基準法に違反した企業に是正勧告が出された。","romaji":"Rōdō kijunhō ni ihan shita kigyō ni zesei kankoku ga dasareta.","german":"A corrective recommendation was issued to the company that violated the Labor Standards Act.","furigana":"ろうどうきじゅんほう|いはん|きぎょう|ぜせいかんこく|だ"},{"japanese":"労働基準法では、一日の労働時間は原則八時間までと定められている。","romaji":"Rōdō kijunhō de wa, ichinichi no rōdō jikan wa gensoku hachijikan made to sadamerarete iru.","german":"The Labor Standards Act stipulates that daily working hours are in principle limited to eight hours.","furigana":"ろうどうきじゅんほう|いちにち|ろうどうじかん|げんそくはちじかん|さだ"},{"japanese":"アルバイトにも労働基準法が適用される。","romaji":"Arubaito ni mo rōdō kijunhō ga tekiyō sareru.","german":"The Labor Standards Act also applies to part-time workers.","furigana":"ろうどうきじゅんほう|てきよう"}],"notes":"労働基準法 is Japan's central labor protection law and sets minimum standards such as working hours, overtime premiums, leave and notice periods for dismissal. Abbreviated 労基法; the supervising authority is the 労働基準監督署 (colloquially 労基). Typical: 労働基準法に違反する, 労働基準法違反, 〜が適用される. Together with the 労働組合法 and the 労働関係調整法 it forms the 労働三法.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"労働組合が賃上げを求めて団体交渉を行った。","romaji":"Rōdō kumiai ga chin'age o motomete dantai kōshō o okonatta.","german":"The labor union held collective bargaining demanding a pay raise.","furigana":"ろうどうくみあい|ちんあ|もと|だんたいこうしょう|おこな"},{"japanese":"会社側は団体交渉に応じなかった。","romaji":"Kaishagawa wa dantai kōshō ni ōjinakatta.","german":"The company side did not agree to collective bargaining.","furigana":"かいしゃがわ|だんたいこうしょう|おう"},{"japanese":"春闘の団体交渉で、ベースアップが合意された。","romaji":"Shuntō no dantai kōshō de, bēsu appu ga gōi sareta.","german":"In the spring wage negotiations, a base pay increase was agreed on.","furigana":"しゅんとう|だんたいこうしょう|ごうい"}],"notes":"団体交渉 is negotiation between a labor union and the employer over wages and working conditions, abbreviated 団交. The right to it (団体交渉権) belongs under Article 28 of the Constitution to the 労働三権, alongside 団結権 and 団体行動権. Typical: 団体交渉を行う・申し入れる, 団体交渉に応じる, 団体交渉を拒否する. The employer may not refuse without a valid reason.","levelNote":"Compound: 団体 (N3) + 交渉 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"労働者の争議権は憲法で保障されている。","romaji":"Rōdōsha no sōgiken wa kenpō de hoshō sarete iru.","german":"Workers' right to strike is guaranteed by the Constitution.","furigana":"ろうどうしゃ|そうぎけん|けんぽう|ほしょう"},{"japanese":"日本の公務員には争議権が認められていない。","romaji":"Nihon no kōmuin ni wa sōgiken ga mitomerarete inai.","german":"Japanese public employees are not granted the right to strike.","furigana":"にほん|こうむいん|そうぎけん|みと"},{"japanese":"組合は争議権を行使して、ストライキに踏み切った。","romaji":"Kumiai wa sōgiken o kōshi shite, sutoraiki ni fumikitta.","german":"The union exercised its right to strike and went on strike.","furigana":"くみあい|そうぎけん|こうし|ふ|き"}],"notes":"争議権 (争議 \"labor dispute\" + 権 \"right\") is the workers' right to take industrial action, above all to strike; in the constitutional context one also speaks of 団体行動権. Colloquially it is called スト権. Typical: 争議権を行使する, 争議権が認められる・制限される. In Japan, public employees (公務員) have no right to strike. Related: 労働争議 \"labor dispute\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"組合活動を理由とした解雇は不当労働行為にあたる。","romaji":"Kumiai katsudō o riyū to shita kaiko wa futō rōdō kōi ni ataru.","german":"Dismissal on the grounds of union activity constitutes an unfair labor practice.","furigana":"くみあいかつどう|りゆう|かいこ|ふとうろうどうこうい"},{"japanese":"組合は、団体交渉の拒否は不当労働行為だとして労働委員会に救済を申し立てた。","romaji":"Kumiai wa, dantai kōshō no kyohi wa futō rōdō kōi da to shite rōdō iinkai ni kyūsai o mōshitateta.","german":"The union petitioned the Labor Relations Commission for relief, arguing that the refusal of collective bargaining was an unfair labor practice.","furigana":"くみあい|だんたいこうしょう|きょひ|ふとうろうどうこうい|ろうどういいんかい|きゅうさい|もう|た"},{"japanese":"組合に入らないことを採用の条件にするのも不当労働行為だ。","romaji":"Kumiai ni hairanai koto o saiyō no jōken ni suru no mo futō rōdō kōi da.","german":"Making non-membership in a union a condition of hiring is also an unfair labor practice.","furigana":"くみあい|はい|さいよう|じょうけん|ふとうろうどうこうい"}],"notes":"不当労働行為 is a technical term from the 労働組合法: anti-union actions by the employer, such as discrimination because of union work, refusal of 団体交渉 or interference in the union. Those affected can apply for relief (救済を申し立てる) to the 労働委員会 \"Labor Relations Commission\". Typical: 不当労働行為に当たる. This does not mean every violation of labor law; violations of working hour rules are 労働基準法違反.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"領土問題が国際司法裁判所に付託された。","romaji":"Ryōdo mondai ga kokusai shihō saibansho ni futaku sareta.","german":"The territorial dispute was referred to the International Court of Justice.","furigana":"りょうどもんだい|こくさいしほうさいばんしょ|ふたく"},{"japanese":"国際司法裁判所はオランダのハーグにある。","romaji":"Kokusai shihō saibansho wa Oranda no Hāgu ni aru.","german":"The International Court of Justice is located in The Hague, the Netherlands.","furigana":"こくさいしほうさいばんしょ"},{"japanese":"国際司法裁判所は日本の調査捕鯨を条約違反と判断した。","romaji":"Kokusai shihō saibansho wa Nihon no chōsa hogei o jōyaku ihan to handan shita.","german":"The International Court of Justice found Japan's research whaling to be in violation of the convention.","furigana":"こくさいしほうさいばんしょ|にほん|ちょうさほげい|じょうやくいはん|はんだん"}],"notes":"国際司法裁判所 (ICJ) is the principal judicial organ of the United Nations, based in The Hague, and decides disputes between states, for example over borders or treaties. Not to be confused with the 国際刑事裁判所, which prosecutes individuals for international crimes. Typical: 国際司法裁判所に提訴する・付託する, 〜の判決. 司法 means \"administration of justice\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"戦争犯罪の容疑者が国際刑事裁判所に起訴された。","romaji":"Sensō hanzai no yōgisha ga kokusai keiji saibansho ni kiso sareta.","german":"A suspect in war crimes was indicted by the International Criminal Court.","furigana":"せんそうはんざい|ようぎしゃ|こくさいけいじさいばんしょ|きそ"},{"japanese":"国際刑事裁判所は、ある国の大統領に逮捕状を出した。","romaji":"Kokusai keiji saibansho wa, aru kuni no daitōryō ni taihojō o dashita.","german":"The International Criminal Court issued an arrest warrant for the president of a country.","furigana":"こくさいけいじさいばんしょ|くに|だいとうりょう|たいほじょう|だ"},{"japanese":"国家間の紛争を扱う国際司法裁判所と違い、国際刑事裁判所は個人の犯罪を裁く。","romaji":"Kokkakan no funsō o atsukau kokusai shihō saibansho to chigai, kokusai keiji saibansho wa kojin no hanzai o sabaku.","german":"Unlike the International Court of Justice, which handles disputes between states, the International Criminal Court tries the crimes of individuals.","furigana":"こっかかん|ふんそう|あつか|こくさいしほうさいばんしょ|ちが|こくさいけいじさいばんしょ|こじん|はんざい|さば"}],"notes":"国際刑事裁判所 (ICC) is a permanent court in The Hague that prosecutes individuals for genocide, war crimes and crimes against humanity. In contrast, the 国際司法裁判所 decides disputes between states. Typical: 国際刑事裁判所に起訴される, 〜が逮捕状を出す. 刑事 here means \"criminal\" (cf. 刑事裁判 \"criminal trial\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"難民認定の申請手続きが簡素化された。","romaji":"Nanmin nintei no shinsei tetsuzuki ga kansoka sareta.","german":"The application procedure for refugee recognition was simplified.","furigana":"なんみんにんてい|しんせいてつづ|かんそか"},{"japanese":"日本は難民認定率が低いと批判されている。","romaji":"Nihon wa nanmin nintei ritsu ga hikui to hihan sarete iru.","german":"Japan is criticized for its low refugee recognition rate.","furigana":"にほん|なんみんにんていりつ|ひく|ひはん"},{"japanese":"難民認定はされなかったが、人道的な配慮から在留が許可された。","romaji":"Nanmin nintei wa sarenakatta ga, jindōteki na hairyo kara zairyū ga kyoka sareta.","german":"He was not recognized as a refugee, but was permitted to stay on humanitarian grounds.","furigana":"なんみんにんてい|じんどうてき|はいりょ|ざいりゅう|きょか"}],"notes":"難民認定 is the official recognition as a refugee under the Geneva Refugee Convention; in Japan the immigration authority (入管) decides. Typical: 難民認定を申請する, 難民認定される, 難民認定率 \"recognition rate\". Those who are rejected can still receive a residence permit on humanitarian grounds (在留特別許可). Political asylum in the narrow sense is called 亡命; in the Japanese procedure, 難民認定 is the technical term.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不法滞在者が強制送還された。","romaji":"Fuhō taizaisha ga kyōsei sōkan sareta.","german":"Illegal residents were deported.","furigana":"ふほうたいざいしゃ|きょうせいそうかん"},{"japanese":"強制送還を拒む外国人の長期収容が問題になっている。","romaji":"Kyōsei sōkan o kobamu gaikokujin no chōki shūyō ga mondai ni natte iru.","german":"The prolonged detention of foreigners who refuse deportation has become a problem.","furigana":"きょうせいそうかん|こば|がいこくじん|ちょうきしゅうよう|もんだい"},{"japanese":"彼は強制送還され、五年間は日本に入国できない。","romaji":"Kare wa kyōsei sōkan sare, gonenkan wa Nihon ni nyūkoku dekinai.","german":"He was deported and cannot enter Japan for five years.","furigana":"かれ|きょうせいそうかん|ごねんかん|にほん|にゅうこく"}],"notes":"強制送還 (強制 \"compulsion\" + 送還 \"sending back\") is the forced removal of foreigners to their home country. Legally the procedure is called 退去強制; 強制送還 is the expression usual in the press and everyday speech. Mostly passive: 強制送還される; typical combinations: 不法滞在で強制送還される, 強制送還を拒む. When someone leaves voluntarily after a violation, one speaks of 出国命令.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"入国管理体制が強化された。","romaji":"Nyūkoku kanri taisei ga kyōka sareta.","german":"The immigration control system was strengthened.","furigana":"にゅうこくかんりたいせい|きょうか"},{"japanese":"入国管理局は二〇一九年に出入国在留管理庁に改組された。","romaji":"Nyūkoku kanrikyoku wa nisen jūkyū-nen ni shutsunyūkoku zairyū kanrichō ni kaiso sareta.","german":"The Immigration Bureau was reorganized in 2019 into the Immigration Services Agency.","furigana":"にゅうこくかんりきょく||しゅつにゅうこくざいりゅうかんりちょう|かいそ"},{"japanese":"観光客の急増で、入国管理の現場は人手不足だ。","romaji":"Kankōkyaku no kyūzō de, nyūkoku kanri no genba wa hitode busoku da.","german":"Because of the sharp rise in tourists, immigration control is short of staff.","furigana":"かんこうきゃく|きゅうぞう|にゅうこくかんり|げんば|ひとでぶそく"}],"notes":"入国管理 refers to the state control and administration of foreigners' entry and stay; the agency is called 入管 for short (officially 出入国在留管理庁 since 2019, previously 入国管理局). The actual passport check at the airport is called 入国審査, the customs check 税関. Typical: 入国管理体制, 入国管理の厳格化. A word of administrative and press language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"道路建設のために土地の収用が行われた。","romaji":"Dōro kensetsu no tame ni tochi no shūyō ga okonawareta.","german":"Land was expropriated for road construction.","furigana":"どうろけんせつ|とち|しゅうよう|おこな"},{"japanese":"住民の反対で、空港用地の強制収用は難航した。","romaji":"Jūmin no hantai de, kūkō yōchi no kyōsei shūyō wa nankō shita.","german":"Because of opposition from residents, the forced expropriation of land for the airport made slow progress.","furigana":"じゅうみん|はんたい|くうこうようち|きょうせいしゅうよう|なんこう"},{"japanese":"市は土地を収用し、所有者に補償金を支払った。","romaji":"Shi wa tochi o shūyō shi, shoyūsha ni hoshōkin o shiharatta.","german":"The city expropriated the land and paid compensation to the owner.","furigana":"し|とち|しゅうよう|しょゆうしゃ|ほしょうきん|しはら"}],"notes":"収用 is the sovereign expropriation, especially of land for public projects against compensation (補償): 土地収用法, 土地を収用する, 強制収用. Transitive する-verb, often passive. Important is the homophone 収容 \"to accommodate, house\" (prisoners, injured people, refugees): 病院に収容される. In short: 収用 takes away property, 収容 puts people or things somewhere.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"都市計画法に基づく用途地域が指定された。","romaji":"Toshi keikakuhō ni motozuku yōto chiiki ga shitei sareta.","german":"Land use zones were designated under the City Planning Act.","furigana":"としけいかくほう|もと|ようとちいき|してい"},{"japanese":"駅前の再開発は市の都市計画に沿って進められている。","romaji":"Ekimae no saikaihatsu wa shi no toshi keikaku ni sotte susumerarete iru.","german":"The redevelopment in front of the station is proceeding in line with the city's urban plan.","furigana":"えきまえ|さいかいはつ|し|としけいかく|そ|すす"},{"japanese":"大学で都市計画を専攻しています。","romaji":"Daigaku de toshi keikaku o senkō shite imasu.","german":"I major in urban planning at university.","furigana":"だいがく|としけいかく|せんこう"}],"notes":"都市計画 is urban planning as a field and also the concrete plan of a municipality adopted by the authorities. Typical combinations: 都市計画を策定する \"to draw up an urban plan\", 都市計画決定 \"plan decision\", 都市計画道路 \"planned (not yet built) road\" and the 都市計画法 \"City Planning Act\". For community-oriented, rather informal urban development, one says まちづくり instead. Register: administrative and technical language.","levelNote":"Compound: 都市 (N3) + 計画 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"用途地域の変更に対して住民が反対した。","romaji":"Yōto chiiki no henkō ni taishite jūmin ga hantai shita.","german":"Gegen die Änderung der Nutzungszone protestierten die Einwohner.","furigana":"ようとちいき|へんこう|たい|じゅうみん|はんたい"},{"japanese":"家を建てる前に、土地の用途地域を確認してください。","romaji":"Ie o tateru mae ni, tochi no yōto chiiki o kakunin shite kudasai.","german":"Prüfen Sie vor dem Hausbau, in welcher Nutzungszone das Grundstück liegt.","furigana":"いえ|た|まえ|とち|ようとちいき|かくにん"},{"japanese":"この辺りの用途地域は住居専用なので、大きな工場は建てられない。","romaji":"Kono atari no yōto chiiki wa jūkyo sen'yō na node, ōkina kōjō wa taterarenai.","german":"Hier ist reines Wohngebiet ausgewiesen, deshalb darf keine große Fabrik gebaut werden.","furigana":"あた|ようとちいき|じゅうきょせんよう|おお|こうじょう|た"}],"notes":"用途地域 ist ein Fachbegriff der Stadtplanung: Im 都市計画 wird das Gemeindegebiet in Nutzungszonen eingeteilt (z. B. 住居地域, 商業地域, 工業地域), die festlegen, welche Gebäude dort gebaut werden dürfen. Typisch sind 用途地域を指定する／変更する und Angaben wie 用途地域：第一種低層住居専用地域 in Immobilienanzeigen. Entspricht etwa dem deutschen „Baugebiet“ im Bebauungsplan; Register: Verwaltungs- und Immobiliensprache.","levelNote":"Compound: 用途 (N2) + 地域 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"建築基準法に違反した建物に是正命令が出された。","romaji":"Kenchiku kijunhō ni ihan shita tatemono ni zesei meirei ga dasareta.","german":"A corrective order was issued for the building that violated the Building Standards Act.","furigana":"けんちくきじゅんほう|いはん|たてもの|ぜせいめいれい|だ"},{"japanese":"1981年の建築基準法改正で、耐震基準が大幅に厳しくなった。","romaji":"Sen kyūhyaku hachijūichi-nen no kenchiku kijunhō kaisei de, taishin kijun ga ōhaba ni kibishiku natta.","german":"With the 1981 amendment of the Building Standards Act, seismic standards became considerably stricter.","furigana":"ねん|けんちくきじゅんほうかいせい|たいしんきじゅん|おおはば|きび"},{"japanese":"この増築には、建築基準法上の確認申請が必要です。","romaji":"Kono zōchiku ni wa, kenchiku kijunhō-jō no kakunin shinsei ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"This extension requires a confirmation application under the Building Standards Act.","furigana":"ぞうちく|けんちくきじゅんほうじょう|かくにんしんせい|ひつよう"}],"notes":"建築基準法 is Japan's central building law (1950). It sets minimum standards for earthquake safety, fire protection and building density (建ぺい率, 容積率). Typical combinations: 建築基準法に違反する／適合する, 建築基準法上の〜 \"under the Building Standards Act\", 建築基準法の改正. Among specialists also abbreviated 建基法; register: legal and technical language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大規模開発には環境影響評価が義務付けられている。","romaji":"Daikibo kaihatsu ni wa kankyō eikyō hyōka ga gimuzukerarete iru.","german":"Environmental impact assessment is mandatory for large-scale developments.","furigana":"だいきぼかいはつ|かんきょうえいきょうひょうか|ぎむづ"},{"japanese":"風力発電所の建設に先立ち、環境影響評価が実施された。","romaji":"Fūryoku hatsudensho no kensetsu ni sakidachi, kankyō eikyō hyōka ga jisshi sareta.","german":"An environmental impact assessment was carried out before the construction of the wind power plant.","furigana":"ふうりょくはつでんしょ|けんせつ|さきだ|かんきょうえいきょうひょうか|じっし"},{"japanese":"住民説明会で環境影響評価書の内容が説明された。","romaji":"Jūmin setsumeikai de kankyō eikyō hyōkasho no naiyō ga setsumei sareta.","german":"The content of the environmental impact statement was explained at the residents' briefing.","furigana":"じゅうみんせつめいかい|かんきょうえいきょうひょうかしょ|ないよう|せつめい"}],"notes":"環境影響評価 is the official term for environmental impact assessment, regulated in the 環境影響評価法 (1997). In the media and everyday speech one mostly says 環境アセスメント or briefly アセス. Typical combinations: 環境影響評価を実施する／行う, 環境影響評価の対象となる, 環境影響評価書 \"environmental impact statement\". Register: administrative and technical language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"罹災証明書の発行を自治体に申請した。","romaji":"Risai shōmeisho no hakkō o jichitai ni shinsei shita.","german":"I applied to the municipality for the issuance of a disaster victim certificate.","furigana":"りさいしょうめいしょ|はっこう|じちたい|しんせい"},{"japanese":"地震で家が半壊し、罹災証明を取りに市役所へ行った。","romaji":"Jishin de ie ga hankai shi, risai shōmei o tori ni shiyakusho e itta.","german":"My house was half destroyed in the earthquake, so I went to city hall to get a disaster certificate.","furigana":"じしん|いえ|はんかい|りさいしょうめい|と|しやくしょ|い"},{"japanese":"支援金を受け取るには罹災証明書が必要です。","romaji":"Shienkin o uketoru ni wa risai shōmeisho ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"A disaster victim certificate is required to receive the support payment.","furigana":"しえんきん|う|と|りさいしょうめいしょ|ひつよう"}],"notes":"罹災証明 is the municipality's official certificate of damage to a dwelling from an earthquake, flood, fire etc.; the degree of damage (全壊, 半壊 etc.) is determined in it. It is a prerequisite for relief payments, tax relief and emergency housing. One mostly speaks of the document, 罹災証明書; since 罹 is not a Jōyō kanji, authorities often write り災証明. 罹災 is the official word for \"affected by a disaster\"; in everyday speech one says 被災.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"行政の違法行為に対して国家賠償請求が行われた。","romaji":"Gyōsei no ihō kōi ni taishite kokka baishō seikyū ga okonawareta.","german":"A claim for state compensation was filed over the administration's unlawful act.","furigana":"ぎょうせい|いほうこうい|たい|こっかばいしょうせいきゅう|おこな"},{"japanese":"元患者らは国に国家賠償を求めて提訴した。","romaji":"Moto kanja-ra wa kuni ni kokka baishō o motomete teiso shita.","german":"Former patients sued the state for state compensation.","furigana":"もとかんじゃ|くに|こっかばいしょう|もと|ていそ"},{"japanese":"裁判所は国家賠償法に基づき、県に約500万円の支払いを命じた。","romaji":"Saibansho wa kokka baishōhō ni motozuki, ken ni yaku gohyakuman-en no shiharai o meijita.","german":"Based on the State Redress Act, the court ordered the prefecture to pay about 5 million yen.","furigana":"さいばんしょ|こっかばいしょうほう|もと|けん|やく|まんえん|しはら|めい"}],"notes":"国家賠償 is the liability of the state and public bodies for damage unlawfully caused by public officials (国家賠償法, Art. 17 of the Constitution). Typical: 国家賠償を求める, 国家賠償請求訴訟 (short 国賠訴訟). To be distinguished from 損失補償: 賠償 compensates damage caused unlawfully, 補償 compensates for lawful interventions. Rarely also \"war reparations\" (for that, 戦争賠償 is more usual).","levelNote":"Compound: 国家 (N3) + 賠償 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"土地の収用に対して正当な損失補償がなされた。","romaji":"Tochi no shūyō ni taishite seitō na sonshitsu hoshō ga nasareta.","german":"Just compensation was paid for the expropriation of the land.","furigana":"とち|しゅうよう|たい|せいとう|そんしつほしょう"},{"japanese":"道路拡張で立ち退く住民に損失補償が支払われた。","romaji":"Dōro kakuchō de tachinoku jūmin ni sonshitsu hoshō ga shiharawareta.","german":"Compensation was paid to residents displaced by the road widening.","furigana":"どうろかくちょう|た|の|じゅうみん|そんしつほしょう|しはら"},{"japanese":"休業を要請するなら、損失補償もセットで考えるべきだ。","romaji":"Kyūgyō o yōsei suru nara, sonshitsu hoshō mo setto de kangaeru beki da.","german":"If you are going to ask businesses to close, you should consider compensation as part of the package.","furigana":"きゅうぎょう|ようせい|そんしつほしょう|かんが"}],"notes":"損失補償 is the compensation the public authorities pay for lawful interventions that demand a special sacrifice from individuals, for instance with expropriation (土地収用) for road building (Art. 29 of the Constitution: 正当な補償). Typical: 損失補償を行う／受ける／求める. To be distinguished from 損害賠償 or 国家賠償, the damages for unlawful action. During the coronavirus period, 損失補償 for officially ordered business closures was much discussed.","levelNote":"Compound: 損失 (N1) + 補償 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"行政改革により省庁の統廃合が行われた。","romaji":"Gyōsei kaikaku ni yori shōchō no tōhaigō ga okonawareta.","german":"Ministries were merged and abolished through administrative reform.","furigana":"ぎょうせいかいかく|しょうちょう|とうはいごう|おこな"},{"japanese":"新しい知事は行政改革を最優先課題に掲げた。","romaji":"Atarashii chiji wa gyōsei kaikaku o saiyūsen kadai ni kakageta.","german":"The new governor made administrative reform his top priority.","furigana":"あたら|ちじ|ぎょうせいかいかく|さいゆうせんかだい|かか"},{"japanese":"行政改革の名のもとで職員数が大幅に削減された。","romaji":"Gyōsei kaikaku no na no moto de shokuinsū ga ōhaba ni sakugen sareta.","german":"In the name of administrative reform, the number of staff was drastically cut.","furigana":"ぎょうせいかいかく|な|しょくいんすう|おおはば|さくげん"}],"notes":"行政改革 refers to the reform of public administration: merging agencies, cutting staff, more efficient procedures. In headlines often shortened to 行革. Typical combinations: 行政改革を進める／断行する, 行政改革担当大臣. To be distinguished from 規制改革 (reform of regulations) and 構造改革 (restructuring of entire economic and social structures). Register: political and media language.","levelNote":"Compound: 行政 (N1) + 改革 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"規制改革会議が新産業の育成策を提言した。","romaji":"Kisei kaikaku kaigi ga shin sangyō no ikuseisaku o teigen shita.","german":"The Regulatory Reform Council proposed measures to foster new industries.","furigana":"きせいかいかくかいぎ|しんさんぎょう|いくせいさく|ていげん"},{"japanese":"政府はライドシェア解禁に向けた規制改革を進めている。","romaji":"Seifu wa raidoshea kaikin ni muketa kisei kaikaku o susumete iru.","german":"The government is pushing regulatory reform toward lifting the ban on ridesharing.","furigana":"せいふ|かいきん|む|きせいかいかく|すす"},{"japanese":"医療分野の規制改革には業界団体の反発が強い。","romaji":"Iryō bun'ya no kisei kaikaku ni wa gyōkai dantai no hanpatsu ga tsuyoi.","german":"Industry groups strongly oppose regulatory reform in the medical field.","furigana":"いりょうぶんや|きせいかいかく|ぎょうかいだんたい|はんぱつ|つよ"}],"notes":"規制改革 is the revision of government regulations to enable competition and new business models. It overlaps with 規制緩和 \"deregulation, relaxation\", but is broader and can also include new rules. Typical: 規制改革を推進する, 〜分野の規制改革, the government panel 規制改革推進会議. Register: political and business language.","levelNote":"Compound: 規制 (N3) + 改革 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"経済の構造改革が成長戦略の柱とされた。","romaji":"Keizai no kōzō kaikaku ga seichō senryaku no hashira to sareta.","german":"Structural reform of the economy was made a pillar of the growth strategy.","furigana":"けいざい|こうぞうかいかく|せいちょうせんりゃく|はしら"},{"japanese":"小泉内閣は「聖域なき構造改革」を掲げ、郵政民営化を進めた。","romaji":"Koizumi naikaku wa seiiki naki kōzō kaikaku o kakage, yūsei min'eika o susumeta.","german":"The Koizumi cabinet advanced postal privatization under the banner of \"structural reform without sacred cows\".","furigana":"こいずみないかく|せいいき|こうぞうかいかく|かか|ゆうせいみんえいか|すす"},{"japanese":"業績不振の会社は構造改革として工場を二つ閉鎖した。","romaji":"Gyōseki fushin no kaisha wa kōzō kaikaku to shite kōjō o futatsu heisa shita.","german":"The struggling company closed two factories as part of restructuring.","furigana":"ぎょうせきふしん|かいしゃ|こうぞうかいかく|こうじょう|ふた|へいさ"}],"notes":"構造改革 means the thorough overhaul of economic or social structures; well known is the slogan 聖域なき構造改革 of the Koizumi government (2001-2006). At company level it means \"restructuring\" and is often a euphemism for plant closures and job cuts. Typical: 構造改革を断行する／進める, 構造改革特区. Broader than 行政改革 or 規制改革.","levelNote":"Compound: 構造 (N2) + 改革 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"少子化により小学校の統廃合が進んでいる。","romaji":"Shōshika ni yori shōgakkō no tōhaigō ga susunde iru.","german":"Due to the declining birthrate, the consolidation of elementary schools is progressing.","furigana":"しょうしか|しょうがっこう|とうはいごう|すす"},{"japanese":"銀行は来年までに支店を約30か所統廃合する方針だ。","romaji":"Ginkō wa rainen made ni shiten o yaku sanjukkasho tōhaigō suru hōshin da.","german":"The bank plans to merge or close about 30 branches by next year.","furigana":"ぎんこう|らいねん|してん|やく|しょとうはいごう|ほうしん"},{"japanese":"病院の統廃合で、近くに救急外来がなくなった。","romaji":"Byōin no tōhaigō de, chikaku ni kyūkyū gairai ga nakunatta.","german":"Because the hospitals were consolidated, there is no longer an emergency outpatient department nearby.","furigana":"びょういん|とうはいごう|ちか|きゅうきゅうがいらい"}],"notes":"統廃合 combines 統合 \"merging\" and 廃止 \"abolishing\": facilities are brought together, with some closed altogether. Typical for schools, hospitals, bank branches and agencies: 〜の統廃合が進む, 〜を統廃合する (transitive する-verb). To be distinguished from 合併 \"merger\" (of companies or municipalities) and 統合 \"integration\" without closures. Register: administrative and news language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"広域連合が複数の市町村にまたがる事務を処理している。","romaji":"Kōiki rengō ga fukusū no shichōson ni matagaru jimu o shori shite iru.","german":"The wide-area union handles affairs that span multiple municipalities.","furigana":"こういきれんごう|ふくすう|しちょうそん|じむ|しょり"},{"japanese":"関西広域連合は防災や観光で府県の連携を進めている。","romaji":"Kansai kōiki rengō wa bōsai ya kankō de fuken no renkei o susumete iru.","german":"The Kansai Wide-Area Union promotes cooperation among the prefectures in disaster prevention and tourism.","furigana":"かんさいこういきれんごう|ぼうさい|かんこう|ふけん|れんけい|すす"},{"japanese":"75歳以上の医療保険は、都道府県ごとの広域連合が運営している。","romaji":"Nanajūgo-sai ijō no iryō hoken wa, todōfuken goto no kōiki rengō ga un'ei shite iru.","german":"Medical insurance for people aged 75 and over is run by wide-area unions at the prefectural level.","furigana":"さいいじょう|いりょうほけん|とどうふけん|こういきれんごう|うんえい"}],"notes":"広域連合 is a legal form under local government law (地方自治法): prefectures, cities and towns join together to carry out supra-local tasks jointly. Well-known examples are the 後期高齢者医療広域連合 (health insurance for people aged 75 and over) and the 関西広域連合. To be distinguished from the 一部事務組合, a special-purpose association for individual tasks such as waste disposal. 広域 means \"wide-area, supra-regional\"; register: administrative language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"事業仕分けにより無駄な支出が削減された。","romaji":"Jigyō shiwake ni yori muda na shishutsu ga sakugen sareta.","german":"Wasteful spending was cut through the program review.","furigana":"じぎょうしわ|むだ|ししゅつ|さくげん"},{"japanese":"事業仕分けでは、スーパーコンピューターの予算も議論の対象になった。","romaji":"Jigyō shiwake de wa, sūpākonpyūtā no yosan mo giron no taishō ni natta.","german":"In the program review, the budget for the supercomputer also became a subject of debate.","furigana":"じぎょうしわ|よさん|ぎろん|たいしょう"},{"japanese":"その補助金は事業仕分けで「廃止」と判定された。","romaji":"Sono hojokin wa jigyō shiwake de haishi to hantei sareta.","german":"That subsidy was judged \"to be abolished\" in the program review.","furigana":"ほじょきん|じぎょうしわ|はいし|はんてい"}],"notes":"事業仕分け is a procedure in which public programs are reviewed in open session and classified as \"continue\", \"cut\" or \"abolish\". It became known in 2009-2010 under the DPJ government; 仕分け literally means \"sorting\". Typical: 事業仕分けの対象になる, 事業仕分けで「廃止」と判定される. Today it is carried out mainly by municipalities; register: political and media language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい条例案に対してパブリックコメントが募集された。","romaji":"Atarashii jōreian ni taishite paburikku komento ga boshū sareta.","german":"Public comments were solicited on the new draft ordinance.","furigana":"あたら|じょうれいあん|たい|ぼしゅう"},{"japanese":"私も規制案についてパブリックコメントを提出した。","romaji":"Watashi mo kiseian ni tsuite paburikku komento o teishutsu shita.","german":"I also submitted a public comment on the draft regulation.","furigana":"わたし|きせいあん|ていしゅつ"},{"japanese":"パブリックコメントには一か月で二千件以上の意見が寄せられた。","romaji":"Paburikku komento ni wa ikkagetsu de nisen-ken ijō no iken ga yoserareta.","german":"More than 2,000 comments were received in the public comment period of one month.","furigana":"いっ|げつ|にせんけんいじょう|いけん|よ"}],"notes":"パブリックコメント is the official consultation procedure (意見公募手続) in which authorities publish drafts of regulations and anyone can comment on them. The word means both the procedure and the individual comment; colloquially it is shortened to パブコメ. Typical: パブリックコメントを募集する／実施する, パブコメを出す／提出する, パブリックコメントに寄せられた意見.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"オンブズマン制度が行政の透明性向上に貢献している。","romaji":"Onbuzuman seido ga gyōsei no tōmeisei kōjō ni kōken shite iru.","german":"The ombudsman system contributes to improving administrative transparency.","furigana":"せいど|ぎょうせい|とうめいせいこうじょう|こうけん"},{"japanese":"市民オンブズマンが議員の政務活動費の使い道を調査した。","romaji":"Shimin onbuzuman ga giin no seimu katsudōhi no tsukaimichi o chōsa shita.","german":"A citizens' ombudsman group investigated how legislators spent their political activity funds.","furigana":"しみん|ぎいん|せいむかつどうひ|つか|みち|ちょうさ"},{"japanese":"区の対応に納得できず、オンブズマンに苦情を申し立てた。","romaji":"Ku no taiō ni nattoku dekizu, onbuzuman ni kujō o mōshitateta.","german":"Not satisfied with the ward's response, I filed a complaint with the ombudsman.","furigana":"く|たいおう|なっとく|くじょう|もう|た"}],"notes":"オンブズマン comes from Swedish and has two uses in Japan: the official ombudsperson of a municipality who examines citizens' complaints about the administration (行政オンブズマン), and above all citizens' groups called 市民オンブズマン that monitor public spending, request the release of information and file lawsuits. Typical: オンブズマンに苦情を申し立てる, オンブズマン制度. Gender-neutral also オンブズパーソン.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"電子政府の推進により行政手続きのオンライン化が進んでいる。","romaji":"Denshi seifu no suishin ni yori gyōsei tetsuzuki no onrainka ga susunde iru.","german":"As e-government is promoted, administrative procedures are increasingly moving online.","furigana":"でんしせいふ|すいしん|ぎょうせいてつづ|か|すす"},{"japanese":"エストニアは電子政府の先進国として知られている。","romaji":"Esutonia wa denshi seifu no senshinkoku to shite shirarete iru.","german":"Estonia is known as a leader in e-government.","furigana":"でんしせいふ|せんしんこく|し"},{"japanese":"電子政府の総合窓口サイトから、さまざまな申請をオンラインで行える。","romaji":"Denshi seifu no sōgō madoguchi saito kara, samazama na shinsei o onrain de okonaeru.","german":"Many kinds of applications can be made online from the e-government portal site.","furigana":"でんしせいふ|そうごうまどぐち|しんせい|おこな"}],"notes":"電子政府 refers to digitized administration in which citizens handle applications and inquiries online; the central portal is called e-Gov. Typical: 電子政府の推進／実現, 電子政府ランキング. For the municipal level one says 電子自治体; newer texts prefer デジタル・ガバメント or 行政のデジタル化 (since 2021 with the デジタル庁). Register: administrative and media language.","levelNote":"Compound: 電子 (N4) + 政府 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"マイナンバー制度は社会保障と税の一体管理を目的としている。","romaji":"Mainanbā seido wa shakai hoshō to zei no ittai kanri o mokuteki to shite iru.","german":"The My Number system aims at unified management of social security and taxes.","furigana":"せいど|しゃかいほしょう|ぜい|いったいかんり|もくてき"},{"japanese":"アルバイトを始めるとき、会社にマイナンバーを提出した。","romaji":"Arubaito o hajimeru toki, kaisha ni mainanbā o teishutsu shita.","german":"When I started a part-time job, I submitted my My Number to the company.","furigana":"はじ|かいしゃ|ていしゅつ"},{"japanese":"マイナンバーカードがあれば、コンビニで住民票を取れる。","romaji":"Mainanbā kādo ga areba, konbini de jūminhyō o toreru.","german":"With a My Number card, you can get a copy of your residence certificate at a convenience store.","furigana":"じゅうみんひょう|と"}],"notes":"マイナンバー is the twelve-digit personal identification number assigned to all residents of Japan since 2016 (社会保障・税番号制度). To be distinguished: マイナンバー is the number itself, マイナンバーカード (short マイナカード) the ID card with photo and chip, which also serves, for example, as a health insurance card (マイナ保険証). Typical: マイナンバーを提出する／記入する, マイナンバー制度.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"行政の怠慢が被害の拡大を招いた。","romaji":"Gyōsei no taiman ga higai no kakudai o maneita.","german":"The administration's negligence led to the spread of the damage.","furigana":"ぎょうせい|たいまん|ひがい|かくだい|まね"},{"japanese":"警備員の職務怠慢が事故の原因とされた。","romaji":"Keibiin no shokumu taiman ga jiko no gen'in to sareta.","german":"The security guard's neglect of duty was identified as the cause of the accident.","furigana":"けいびいん|しょくむたいまん|じこ|げんいん"},{"japanese":"確認を怠ったのは明らかに私の怠慢です。申し訳ありません。","romaji":"Kakunin o okotatta no wa akiraka ni watashi no taiman desu. Mōshiwake arimasen.","german":"Failing to check was clearly my negligence. I am sorry.","furigana":"かくにん|おこた|あき|わたし|たいまん|もう|わけ"}],"notes":"怠慢 is the neglect of duties that one really ought to fulfill; as a noun and as a な-adjective (怠慢な態度). Typical: 職務怠慢 \"dereliction of duty\", 行政の怠慢, 怠慢だと批判される, in sports 怠慢プレー \"lackluster play\". Related to the verb 怠る \"to neglect\". To be distinguished from 怠惰 \"laziness\" as a trait; 怠慢 always criticizes a concrete omission. Register: formal, judgmental.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"瀆職罪で公務員が起訴された。","romaji":"Tokushoku zai de kōmuin ga kiso sareta.","german":"A public official was indicted on charges of malfeasance in office.","furigana":"とくしょくざい|こうむいん|きそ"},{"japanese":"収賄などの瀆職事件が相次ぎ、市政への信頼が揺らいだ。","romaji":"Shūwai nado no tokushoku jiken ga aitsugi, shisei e no shinrai ga yuraida.","german":"After a series of corruption cases such as bribery, trust in the city government was shaken.","furigana":"しゅうわい|とくしょくじけん|あいつ|しせい|しんらい|ゆ"},{"japanese":"公務員による瀆職行為は厳しく処罰されるべきだ。","romaji":"Kōmuin ni yoru tokushoku kōi wa kibishiku shobatsu sareru beki da.","german":"Malfeasance by public officials should be severely punished.","furigana":"こうむいん|とくしょくこうい|きび|しょばつ"}],"notes":"瀆職 literally means \"to defile one's office\": official misconduct by civil servants, especially bribery and abuse of power. The word is outdated and survives mainly in criminal law as 瀆職罪 (collective term for 職権濫用罪, 収賄罪 etc.); in the news today one says 汚職. Since 瀆 is not a Jōyō kanji, one also sees 涜職. Register: very formal, legal.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"会社役員が特別背任の罪で逮捕された。","romaji":"Kaisha yakuin ga tokubetsu hainin no tsumi de taiho sareta.","german":"A company executive was arrested for aggravated breach of trust.","furigana":"かいしゃやくいん|とくべつはいにん|つみ|たいほ"},{"japanese":"元社長は、知人の会社に無担保で融資させたとして背任の罪に問われている。","romaji":"Moto shachō wa, chijin no kaisha ni mutanpo de yūshi saseta to shite hainin no tsumi ni towarete iru.","german":"The former president is accused of breach of trust for having had an acquaintance's company granted an unsecured loan.","furigana":"もとしゃちょう|ちじん|かいしゃ|むたんぽ|ゆうし|はいにん|つみ|と"},{"japanese":"顧客の信頼を裏切る背任行為は許されない。","romaji":"Kokyaku no shinrai o uragiru hainin kōi wa yurusarenai.","german":"Breach of trust that betrays customers' confidence is unacceptable.","furigana":"こきゃく|しんらい|うらぎ|はいにんこうい|ゆる"}],"notes":"背任 is the criminal offense of breach of trust: someone who manages another's affairs (e.g. a director or an employee) acts for his own or another's benefit against his duties and thereby harms the principal. For directors the aggravated 特別背任 applies. To be distinguished from 横領 \"embezzlement\", the appropriation of another's property. Typical: 背任罪, 背任の疑いで逮捕される, 背任行為. Register: legal, news language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"巧妙な詐欺の手口が社会問題となっている。","romaji":"Kōmyō na sagi no teguchi ga shakai mondai to natte iru.","german":"Sophisticated fraud methods have become a social problem.","furigana":"こうみょう|さぎ|てぐち|しゃかいもんだい"},{"japanese":"祖母が電話の詐欺に遭いそうになった。","romaji":"Sobo ga denwa no sagi ni aisō ni natta.","german":"My grandmother almost fell for a phone scam.","furigana":"そぼ|でんわ|さぎ|あ"},{"japanese":"写真と実物が全然違う。こんなの詐欺だよ！","romaji":"Shashin to jitsubutsu ga zenzen chigau. Konna no sagi da yo!","german":"The photo and the real thing are completely different. This is a scam!","furigana":"しゃしん|じつぶつ|ぜんぜんちが|さぎ"}],"notes":"詐欺 is fraud in everyday life as well as in criminal law (詐欺罪). Typical: 詐欺に遭う／引っかかる \"to fall victim to fraud\", 詐欺を働く \"to commit fraud\", 詐欺師 \"fraudster\", 特殊詐欺 or オレオレ詐欺 (phone scam impersonating a relative). Colloquially one expresses indignation about a misleading product with こんなの詐欺だ. The related verb is だます; ぺてん and いかさま are more slangy.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"法廷での偽証は重い罪に問われる。","romaji":"Hōtei de no gishō wa omoi tsumi ni towareru.","german":"Perjury in court is punished as a serious crime.","furigana":"ほうてい|ぎしょう|おも|つみ|と"},{"japanese":"その証人は偽証したとして起訴された。","romaji":"Sono shōnin wa gishō shita to shite kiso sareta.","german":"The witness was indicted for perjury.","furigana":"しょうにん|ぎしょう|きそ"},{"japanese":"証人喚問では、偽証すれば罪に問われることが最初に告げられる。","romaji":"Shōnin kanmon de wa, gishō sureba tsumi ni towareru koto ga saisho ni tsugerareru.","german":"In a Diet witness examination, one is first told that perjury is punishable.","furigana":"しょうにんかんもん|ぎしょう|つみ|と|さいしょ|つ"}],"notes":"偽証 is the deliberately false statement of a person testifying as a witness under oath (宣誓); punishable as 偽証罪, also in witness examinations in parliament (証人喚問). As a する-verb: 偽証する. Lying in general is 嘘をつく; 虚偽の証言 is the paraphrase. Register: legal, news language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"市民団体が企業の違法行為を告発した。","romaji":"Shimin dantai ga kigyō no ihō kōi o kokuhatsu shita.","german":"A citizens' group filed a complaint against the company's illegal acts.","furigana":"しみんだんたい|きぎょう|いほうこうい|こくはつ"},{"japanese":"社員の内部告発で、データの改ざんが明るみに出た。","romaji":"Shain no naibu kokuhatsu de, dēta no kaizan ga akarumi ni deta.","german":"A whistleblower from among the employees brought the data falsification to light.","furigana":"しゃいん|ないぶこくはつ|かい|あか|で"},{"japanese":"この映画は戦争の悲惨さを告発している。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa sensō no hisansa o kokuhatsu shite iru.","german":"This film exposes the misery of war.","furigana":"えいが|せんそう|ひさん|こくはつ"}],"notes":"告発 has two uses: legally, the criminal complaint filed by third parties (刑事告発), whereas the victim himself files a 告訴; and in general, the public denunciation of abuses, e.g. 内部告発 \"whistleblowing\" or a film that 告発する something. Construction: 〜を告発する. Note: indictment by the public prosecutor is called 起訴, not 告発. Register: formal, news language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"違法薬物の密輸組織が摘発された。","romaji":"Ihō yakubutsu no mitsuyu soshiki ga tekihatsu sareta.","german":"A drug smuggling ring was busted.","furigana":"いほうやくぶつ|みつゆそしき|てきはつ"},{"japanese":"国税局が大手企業の脱税を摘発した。","romaji":"Kokuzeikyoku ga ōte kigyō no datsuzei o tekihatsu shita.","german":"The National Tax Agency exposed a major company's tax evasion.","furigana":"こくぜいきょく|おおてきぎょう|だつぜい|てきはつ"},{"japanese":"無許可で営業していた店が警察に摘発された。","romaji":"Mukyoka de eigyō shite ita mise ga keisatsu ni tekihatsu sareta.","german":"A shop operating without a license was raided by the police.","furigana":"むきょか|えいぎょう|みせ|けいさつ|てきはつ"}],"notes":"摘発 means that authorities such as the police, customs or tax investigators uncover violations of the law and take action against them, something like a \"raid\" or \"bust\". Mostly in the passive: 〜が摘発される; also 一斉摘発 \"mass crackdown\", 摘発件数. To be distinguished: 告発 is a complaint by citizens, 検挙 the police identifying and apprehending an offender, 逮捕 the arrest. Register: news and official language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"連続窃盗犯が検挙された。","romaji":"Renzoku settōhan ga kenkyo sareta.","german":"The serial thief was apprehended.","furigana":"れんぞくせっとうはん|けんきょ"},{"japanese":"この地域の窃盗事件の検挙率は年々下がっている。","romaji":"Kono chiiki no settō jiken no kenkyoritsu wa nennen sagatte iru.","german":"The clearance rate for theft cases in this area is falling year by year.","furigana":"ちいき|せっとうじけん|けんきょりつ|ねんねんさ"},{"japanese":"高速道路でスピード違反の車が次々と検挙された。","romaji":"Kōsoku dōro de supīdo ihan no kuruma ga tsugitsugi to kenkyo sareta.","german":"Speeding cars were caught one after another on the expressway.","furigana":"こうそくどうろ|いはん|くるま|つぎつぎ|けんきょ"}],"notes":"検挙 is a police technical term: an offender is identified and proceedings are initiated, with or without arrest. 検挙 is therefore broader than 逮捕 \"arrest\". Typical: 〜が検挙される, 検挙率 \"clearance rate\", 検挙件数, also with traffic violations (スピード違反で検挙される). Register: official and news language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"公安委員会が治安維持に関する方針を決定した。","romaji":"Kōan iinkai ga chian iji ni kansuru hōshin o kettei shita.","german":"The Public Safety Commission decided policy on maintaining public order.","furigana":"こうあんいいんかい|ちあんいじ|かん|ほうしん|けってい"},{"japanese":"運転免許証は都道府県の公安委員会が交付している。","romaji":"Unten menkyoshō wa todōfuken no kōan iinkai ga kōfu shite iru.","german":"Driver's licenses are issued by the prefectural Public Safety Commissions.","furigana":"うんてんめんきょしょう|とどうふけん|こうあんいいんかい|こうふ"},{"japanese":"公安警察は過激派の動向を監視している。","romaji":"Kōan keisatsu wa kagekiha no dōkō o kanshi shite iru.","german":"The public security police are monitoring the movements of extremists.","furigana":"こうあんけいさつ|かげきは|どうこう|かんし"}],"notes":"公安 means \"public safety and order\" and appears mainly in fixed agency names: 公安委員会 (the oversight commission of the police, which e.g. issues driver's licenses), 国家公安委員会, 公安条例. As an abbreviation it stands for 公安警察 \"security police\" or 公安調査庁 (domestic intelligence agency). The general security situation, by contrast, is 治安 (治安がいい), not 公安. Register: official and news language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自衛隊が災害救援活動に派遣された。","romaji":"Jieitai ga saigai kyūen katsudō ni haken sareta.","german":"The Self-Defense Forces were dispatched for disaster relief operations.","furigana":"じえいたい|さいがいきゅうえんかつどう|はけん"},{"japanese":"兄は高校を卒業して自衛隊に入った。","romaji":"Ani wa kōkō o sotsugyō shite jieitai ni haitta.","german":"My older brother joined the Self-Defense Forces after graduating from high school.","furigana":"あに|こうこう|そつぎょう|じえいたい|はい"},{"japanese":"自衛隊を憲法にどう位置づけるかは長年の論点だ。","romaji":"Jieitai o kenpō ni dō ichizukeru ka wa naganen no ronten da.","german":"How to position the Self-Defense Forces in the Constitution has been a point of debate for many years.","furigana":"じえいたい|けんぽう|いち|ながねん|ろんてん"}],"notes":"自衛隊 is the official name of Japan's armed forces; because of Article 9 of the Constitution, the word 軍 \"army\" is avoided. There are three branches: 陸上自衛隊 (short 陸自), 海上自衛隊 and 航空自衛隊; a member is called 自衛官. Typical: 自衛隊を派遣する, 自衛隊に入る／入隊する, 災害派遣. Register: neutral, also used in everyday speech.","levelNote":"Compound: 自衛 (N2) + 隊 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"核兵器が抑止力として機能するかが議論されている。","romaji":"Kaku heiki ga yokushiryoku to shite kinō suru ka ga giron sarete iru.","german":"Whether nuclear weapons function as a deterrent is being debated.","furigana":"かくへいき|よくしりょく|きのう|ぎろん"},{"japanese":"防犯カメラの設置は犯罪の抑止力になる。","romaji":"Bōhan kamera no setchi wa hanzai no yokushiryoku ni naru.","german":"Installing security cameras acts as a deterrent to crime.","furigana":"ぼうはん|せっち|はんざい|よくしりょく"},{"japanese":"政府は同盟国との連携で抑止力を高める方針だ。","romaji":"Seifu wa dōmeikoku to no renkei de yokushiryoku o takameru hōshin da.","german":"The government intends to strengthen deterrence through cooperation with allies.","furigana":"せいふ|どうめいこく|れんけい|よくしりょく|たか|ほうしん"}],"notes":"抑止力 is the ability to keep someone from an action through the threat of countermeasures. In security policy: 核抑止力, 抑止力を高める／強化する, 抑止力として機能する. Also outside the military: punishments or security cameras are a 犯罪の抑止力. The verb for it is 抑止する; to be distinguished from 抑制 \"containment, restraint\" without the idea of deterrence. Register: formal.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"核軍縮に関する条約が締結された。","romaji":"Kaku gunshuku ni kansuru jōyaku ga teiketsu sareta.","german":"A treaty on nuclear disarmament was concluded.","furigana":"かくぐんしゅく|かん|じょうやく|ていけつ"},{"japanese":"冷戦の終結後、各国で軍縮が進んだ。","romaji":"Reisen no shūketsu-go, kakkoku de gunshuku ga susunda.","german":"After the end of the Cold War, disarmament progressed in many countries.","furigana":"れいせん|しゅうけつご|かっこく|ぐんしゅく|すす"},{"japanese":"軍拡競争ではなく軍縮こそが平和への道だと彼は訴えた。","romaji":"Gunkaku kyōsō de wa naku gunshuku koso ga heiwa e no michi da to kare wa uttaeta.","german":"He appealed that disarmament, not an arms race, is the path to peace.","furigana":"ぐんかくきょうそう|ぐんしゅく|へいわ|みち|かれ|うった"}],"notes":"軍縮 is the short form of 軍備縮小 \"arms reduction, disarmament\". Typical: 核軍縮, 軍縮条約, 軍縮会議, 軍縮を進める, 軍縮交渉. The opposite is 軍拡 (軍備拡張) \"arms buildup\", as in 軍拡競争 \"arms race\". To be distinguished from 不拡散, which aims to prevent the transfer of weapons to new states. Register: political and news language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"核不拡散条約の遵守が各国に求められている。","romaji":"Kaku fukakusan jōyaku no junshu ga kakkoku ni motomerarete iru.","german":"All countries are required to comply with the nuclear nonproliferation treaty.","furigana":"かくふかくさんじょうやく|じゅんしゅ|かっこく|もと"},{"japanese":"日本は軍縮と不拡散の両面で外交努力を続けている。","romaji":"Nihon wa gunshuku to fukakusan no ryōmen de gaikō doryoku o tsuzukete iru.","german":"Japan continues its diplomatic efforts in both disarmament and nonproliferation.","furigana":"にほん|ぐんしゅく|ふかくさん|りょうめん|がいこうどりょく|つづ"},{"japanese":"大量破壊兵器の不拡散に向けて、輸出管理が強化された。","romaji":"Tairyō hakai heiki no fukakusan ni mukete, yushutsu kanri ga kyōka sareta.","german":"Export controls were tightened toward the nonproliferation of weapons of mass destruction.","furigana":"たいりょうはかいへいき|ふかくさん|む|ゆしゅつかんり|きょうか"}],"notes":"不拡散 means \"nonproliferation\" (拡散 \"proliferation\" with the negating 不) and appears almost always in combinations such as 核不拡散, 核不拡散条約 (NPT, \"Treaty on the Non-Proliferation of Nuclear Weapons\") or 大量破壊兵器の不拡散. Frequently in the pair 軍縮・不拡散: 軍縮 reduces existing arsenals, 不拡散 prevents further states or groups from acquiring weapons. Register: technical and diplomatic language.","levelNote":"Compound: 不 (N3) + 拡散 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"売主には瑕疵担保責任が課される。","romaji":"Urinushi ni wa kashi tanpo sekinin ga kasareru.","german":"The seller is subject to liability for defects.","furigana":"うりぬし|かしたんぽせきにん|か"},{"japanese":"購入した家で雨漏りが見つかり、売主に瑕疵担保責任を追及した。","romaji":"Kōnyū shita ie de amamori ga mitsukari, urinushi ni kashi tanpo sekinin o tsuikyū shita.","german":"A leaky roof was found in the house I bought, so I held the seller liable for defects.","furigana":"こうにゅう|いえ|あまも|み|うりぬし|かしたんぽせきにん|ついきゅう"},{"japanese":"民法改正で、瑕疵担保責任は契約不適合責任に改められた。","romaji":"Minpō kaisei de, kashi tanpo sekinin wa keiyaku futekigō sekinin ni aratamerareta.","german":"With the civil code reform, liability for defects was replaced by liability for nonconformity to the contract.","furigana":"みんぽうかいせい|かしたんぽせきにん|けいやくふてきごうせきにん|あらた"}],"notes":"瑕疵担保責任 is the seller's liability for hidden defects (隠れた瑕疵) of the item sold; 瑕疵 is the legal word for \"defect\". Typical: 瑕疵担保責任を負う, 〜を免責する, 瑕疵担保責任を追及する. Since the civil code reform of 2020, the institution is officially called 契約不適合責任; the old term, however, remains widespread in real estate and construction contracts and in common usage. Register: legal.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"取締役には善管注意義務が課されている。","romaji":"Torishimariyaku ni wa zenkan chūi gimu ga kasarete iru.","german":"Directors are subject to the duty of care of a good manager.","furigana":"とりしまりやく|ぜんかんちゅういぎむ|か"},{"japanese":"株主は、無謀な投資で損失を出した役員を善管注意義務違反で訴えた。","romaji":"Kabunushi wa, mubō na tōshi de sonshitsu o dashita yakuin o zenkan chūi gimu ihan de uttaeta.","german":"The shareholders sued the executives who had incurred losses through reckless investments for breach of the duty of care.","furigana":"かぶぬし|むぼう|とうし|そんしつ|だ|やくいん|ぜんかんちゅういぎむいはん|うった"},{"japanese":"借りた部屋は善管注意義務を守って使わなければならない。","romaji":"Karita heya wa zenkan chūi gimu o mamotte tsukawanakereba naranai.","german":"A rented room must be used in compliance with the duty of care.","furigana":"か|へや|ぜんかんちゅういぎむ|まも|つか"}],"notes":"善管注意義務 is the short form of 善良な管理者の注意義務, the duty of care of a \"good manager\" in civil law. It applies to everyone who looks after another's affairs or property: agents, custodians, tenants and above all board members of a company. It is stricter than the care applied to one's own affairs. Typical: 善管注意義務を負う／怠る, 善管注意義務違反. Register: legal.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"信義則に反する行為は法的保護を受けない。","romaji":"Shingisoku ni hansuru kōi wa hōteki hogo o ukenai.","german":"Acts that violate the principle of good faith receive no legal protection.","furigana":"しんぎそく|はん|こうい|ほうてきほご|う"},{"japanese":"契約書に書かれていなくても、信義則上、説明する義務があるとされた。","romaji":"Keiyakusho ni kakarete inakute mo, shingisoku-jō, setsumei suru gimu ga aru to sareta.","german":"Even though it was not written in the contract, a duty to explain was found based on good faith.","furigana":"けいやくしょ|か|しんぎそくじょう|せつめい|ぎむ"},{"japanese":"交渉を一方的に打ち切ったのは信義則違反だと判断された。","romaji":"Kōshō o ippōteki ni uchikitta no wa shingisoku ihan da to handan sareta.","german":"The unilateral breaking off of negotiations was judged a violation of good faith.","furigana":"こうしょう|いっぽうてき|う|き|しんぎそくいはん|はんだん"}],"notes":"信義則 is the short form of 信義誠実の原則, the principle of good faith in the Japanese Civil Code (Art. 1, para. 2). Typical: 信義則に反する, 信義則違反, 信義則上の義務 (e.g. duties to inform or protect that are not in the contract). The same article contains the prohibition of 権利濫用. Register: legal, hardly used in everyday life.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"権利濫用の法理により請求が認められなかった。","romaji":"Kenri ran'yō no hōri ni yori seikyū ga mitomerarenakatta.","german":"The claim was not allowed under the doctrine of abuse of rights.","furigana":"けんりらんよう|ほうり|せいきゅう|みと"},{"japanese":"わずかな越境を理由に建物の撤去を求めるのは権利濫用に当たる。","romaji":"Wazuka na ekkyō o riyū ni tatemono no tekkyo o motomeru no wa kenri ran'yō ni ataru.","german":"Demanding the demolition of a building over a slight encroachment across the boundary constitutes abuse of rights.","furigana":"えっきょう|りゆう|たてもの|てっきょ|もと|けんりらんよう|あ"},{"japanese":"会社による解雇は権利濫用として無効と判断された。","romaji":"Kaisha ni yoru kaiko wa kenri ran'yō to shite mukō to handan sareta.","german":"The company's dismissal was judged invalid as an abuse of rights.","furigana":"かいしゃ|かいこ|けんりらんよう|むこう|はんだん"}],"notes":"権利濫用 refers to the exercise of a right that does exist, but so unreasonably that it is not protected; the Civil Code says in Art. 1, para. 3: 権利の濫用は、これを許さない. Typical: 権利濫用に当たる, 権利濫用として無効, 権利濫用の法理 (e.g. with dismissals: 解雇権の濫用). In newspapers also written 権利乱用. Register: legal.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本法では原則として自力救済は禁じられている。","romaji":"Nihonhō de wa gensoku to shite jiriki kyūsai wa kinjirarete iru.","german":"In Japanese law, self-help is in principle prohibited.","furigana":"にほんほう|げんそく|じりききゅうさい|きん"},{"japanese":"家賃を滞納した入居者を大家が勝手に締め出すのは、自力救済に当たる。","romaji":"Yachin o tainō shita nyūkyosha o ōya ga katte ni shimedasu no wa, jiriki kyūsai ni ataru.","german":"If a landlord arbitrarily locks out a tenant who is behind on rent, that is self-help.","furigana":"やちん|たいのう|にゅうきょしゃ|おおや|かって|し|だ|じりききゅうさい|あ"},{"japanese":"貸したお金が返ってこなくても、相手の物を勝手に持ち帰るのは自力救済として違法になりうる。","romaji":"Kashita okane ga kaette konakute mo, aite no mono o katte ni mochikaeru no wa jiriki kyūsai to shite ihō ni nariuru.","german":"Even if the money you lent is not returned, taking the other person's belongings on your own may be illegal as self-help.","furigana":"か|かね|かえ|あいて|もの|かって|も|かえ|じりききゅうさい|いほう"}],"notes":"自力救済 is the arbitrary enforcement of a claim without a court, for example when a landlord simply changes the lock over unpaid rent. In Japanese law the 自力救済の禁止 applies: self-help is permitted only in narrow exceptional cases, otherwise it can lead to damages. To be distinguished from 正当防衛 \"self-defense\" against a present attack. Typical: 自力救済に当たる, 自力救済は禁じられている. Register: legal.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"裁判所が破産管財人を選任した。","romaji":"Saibansho ga hasan kanzainin o sennin shita.","german":"The court appointed a bankruptcy trustee.","furigana":"さいばんしょ|はさんかんざいにん|せんにん"},{"japanese":"破産管財人が会社の資産を売却し、債権者に配当した。","romaji":"Hasan kanzainin ga kaisha no shisan o baikyaku shi, saikensha ni haitō shita.","german":"The bankruptcy trustee sold the company's assets and distributed the proceeds to the creditors.","furigana":"はさんかんざいにん|かいしゃ|しさん|ばいきゃく|さいけんしゃ|はいとう"},{"japanese":"管財人には倒産手続きに詳しい弁護士が就任した。","romaji":"Kanzainin ni wa tōsan tetsuzuki ni kuwashii bengoshi ga shūnin shita.","german":"A lawyer well versed in insolvency proceedings took office as trustee.","furigana":"かんざいにん|とうさんてつづ|くわ|べんごし|しゅうにん"}],"notes":"管財人 is a person appointed by the court, mostly a lawyer, who administers another's assets. Most common are the 破産管財人 \"bankruptcy trustee\" in bankruptcy and the 更生管財人 in the 会社更生 reorganization procedure. Typical: 管財人を選任する, 管財人に就任する, 管財人の管理下に置かれる. To be distinguished from the 清算人, who handles a voluntary liquidation. Register: legal, business news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"経営難に陥った企業が民事再生法の適用を申請した。","romaji":"Keieinan ni ochiitta kigyō ga minji saiseihō no tekiyō o shinsei shita.","german":"A company in financial difficulty applied for civil rehabilitation proceedings.","furigana":"けいえいなん|おちい|きぎょう|みんじさいせいほう|てきよう|しんせい"},{"japanese":"民事再生は、会社をなくさずに立て直すための手続きだ。","romaji":"Minji saisei wa, kaisha o nakusazu ni tatenaosu tame no tetsuzuki da.","german":"Civil rehabilitation is a procedure for rebuilding a company without dissolving it.","furigana":"みんじさいせい|かいしゃ|た|なお|てつづ"},{"japanese":"取引先が民事再生を申し立てたので、代金の回収が難しくなった。","romaji":"Torihikisaki ga minji saisei o mōshitateta node, daikin no kaishū ga muzukashiku natta.","german":"Because our business partner filed for civil rehabilitation, it has become difficult to collect payment.","furigana":"とりひきさき|みんじさいせい|もう|た|だいきん|かいしゅう|むずか"}],"notes":"民事再生 is the rehabilitation procedure under the 民事再生法: a struggling company (or an individual) continues to exist and restructures according to a plan, usually under its previous management. Typical: 民事再生法の適用を申請する, 民事再生を申し立てる, 民事再生手続き. In contrast, 破産 means dissolution and liquidation of the assets; 会社更生 is a stricter procedure for large stock companies. 倒産 is the general umbrella term for a company's bankruptcy.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大企業が会社更生手続きを申請した。","romaji":"Daikigyō ga kaisha kōsei tetsuzuki o shinsei shita.","german":"A large company filed for corporate reorganization proceedings.","furigana":"だいきぎょう|かいしゃこうせいてつづ|しんせい"},{"japanese":"日本航空は2010年に会社更生法の適用を申請した。","romaji":"Nihon Kōkū wa nisen-jū-nen ni kaisha kōseihō no tekiyō o shinsei shita.","german":"Japan Airlines applied for corporate reorganization under the Corporate Reorganization Act in 2010.","furigana":"にほんこうくう|ねん|かいしゃこうせいほう|てきよう|しんせい"},{"japanese":"破産ではなく会社更生を選べば、事業を続けながら再建を目指せる。","romaji":"Hasan de wa naku kaisha kōsei o erabeba, jigyō o tsuzukenagara saiken o mezaseru.","german":"If you choose corporate reorganization rather than bankruptcy, you can aim for rebuilding while continuing the business.","furigana":"はさん|かいしゃこうせい|えら|じぎょう|つづ|さいけん|めざ"}],"notes":"会社更生 is the court-supervised reorganization procedure for (mostly large) stock companies under the 会社更生法, comparable to Chapter 11 in the U.S. The company is not broken up as in 破産, but continues under a 管財人 appointed by the court; the old management usually steps down. For smaller firms there is the 民事再生. Typical in the news: 会社更生法の適用を申請する, 会社更生手続き.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"解散した法人の清算手続きが進められている。","romaji":"Kaisan shita hōjin no seisan tetsuzuki ga susumerarete iru.","german":"The liquidation procedure of the dissolved corporation is progressing.","furigana":"かいさん|ほうじん|せいさんてつづ|すす"},{"japanese":"借金をすべて清算して、新しい生活を始めた。","romaji":"Shakkin o subete seisan shite, atarashii seikatsu o hajimeta.","german":"He settled all his debts and started a new life.","furigana":"しゃっきん|せいさん|あたら|せいかつ|はじ"},{"japanese":"彼女は過去を清算するために、故郷を離れた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kako o seisan suru tame ni, kokyō o hanareta.","german":"To leave her past behind, she left her hometown.","furigana":"かのじょ|かこ|せいさん|こきょう|はな"}],"notes":"清算 has three meanings: to settle debts or accounts finally (借金を清算する), to dissolve and wind up a company (会社の清算, 清算人), and, figuratively, to draw a line under something (過去を清算する, 関係を清算する). Transitive する-verb: 〜を清算する. Not to be confused with the identically read 精算 \"exact settlement\", for example when paying the fare difference at a 精算機 \"fare adjustment machine\". Register: neutral to formal.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"省庁の内規に基づいて処分が行われた。","romaji":"Shōchō no naiki ni motozuite shobun ga okonawareta.","german":"Disciplinary action was taken on the basis of the ministry's internal rules.","furigana":"しょうちょう|ないき|もと|しょぶん|おこな"},{"japanese":"当社の内規で、取引先からの贈り物は受け取れないことになっています。","romaji":"Tōsha no naiki de, torihikisaki kara no okurimono wa uketorenai koto ni natte imasu.","german":"Under our internal rules, we are not allowed to accept gifts from business partners.","furigana":"とうしゃ|ないき|とりひきさき|おく|もの|う|と"},{"japanese":"選考基準は大学の内規で定められており、公表されていない。","romaji":"Senkō kijun wa daigaku no naiki de sadamerarete ori, kōhyō sarete inai.","german":"The selection criteria are set in the university's internal rules and are not made public.","furigana":"せんこうきじゅん|だいがく|ないき|さだ|こうひょう"}],"notes":"内規 are the internal rules of an organization, often unpublished or even unwritten. Typical: 内規で定める, 内規に従う／違反する, 社の内規. Unlike 規則 or 規約, 内規 sounds \"in-house\" and is sometimes criticized for a lack of transparency. The legally required set of rules for employees is called 就業規則. Register: formal.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"職務規程に従って業務を遂行する。","romaji":"Shokumu kitei ni shitagatte gyōmu o suikō suru.","german":"I carry out my work in accordance with the job regulations.","furigana":"しょくむきてい|したが|ぎょうむ|すいこう"},{"japanese":"職務規程に違反したとして、社員が処分を受けた。","romaji":"Shokumu kitei ni ihan shita to shite, shain ga shobun o uketa.","german":"An employee was disciplined for violating the job regulations.","furigana":"しょくむきてい|いはん|しゃいん|しょぶん|う"},{"japanese":"新しい部署の職務規程を読んで、自分の権限を確認した。","romaji":"Atarashii busho no shokumu kitei o yonde, jibun no kengen o kakunin shita.","german":"I read the job regulations of the new department and confirmed my authority.","furigana":"あたら|ぶしょ|しょくむきてい|よ|じぶん|けんげん|かくにん"}],"notes":"職務規程 is a written set of rules that defines tasks, authority and conduct at work, e.g. the 職務権限規程 \"authority regulations\" in companies or the 弁護士職務基本規程 for lawyers. 規程 denotes the whole set of rules as a document, whereas the identically read 規定 is a single provision. Typical: 職務規程に従う／違反する, 職務規程を定める. Register: formal, administrative language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"公務員は服務規律を遵守しなければならない。","romaji":"Kōmuin wa fukumu kiritsu o junshu shinakereba naranai.","german":"Public employees must comply with the rules of service discipline.","furigana":"こうむいん|ふくむきりつ|じゅんしゅ"},{"japanese":"服務規律違反で、警察官が減給処分を受けた。","romaji":"Fukumu kiritsu ihan de, keisatsukan ga genkyū shobun o uketa.","german":"A police officer received a pay cut for violating service discipline.","furigana":"ふくむきりついはん|けいさつかん|げんきゅうしょぶん|う"},{"japanese":"就業規則の服務規律には、副業の禁止も定められている。","romaji":"Shūgyō kisoku no fukumu kiritsu ni wa, fukugyō no kinshi mo sadamerarete iru.","german":"The code of conduct in the work rules also prohibits side jobs.","furigana":"しゅうぎょうきそく|ふくむきりつ|ふくぎょう|きんし|さだ"}],"notes":"服務規律 are the rules of conduct for civil servants and employees on duty: punctuality, confidentiality, prohibition of side jobs, appropriate demeanor. In companies, 服務規律 usually forms a separate chapter of the 就業規則. Typical: 服務規律を守る／遵守する, 服務規律違反, 服務規律の徹底. While 職務規程 regulates tasks and authority, 服務規律 is about behavior. Register: formal.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不祥事を起こした職員に懲戒処分が下された。","romaji":"Fushōji o okoshita shokuin ni chōkai shobun ga kudasareta.","german":"Disciplinary action was taken against the employee who caused the scandal.","furigana":"ふしょうじ|お|しょくいん|ちょうかいしょぶん|くだ"},{"japanese":"飲酒運転をした教員は懲戒処分を受けた。","romaji":"Inshu unten o shita kyōin wa chōkai shobun o uketa.","german":"The teacher who drove drunk received disciplinary action.","furigana":"いんしゅうんてん|きょういん|ちょうかいしょぶん|う"},{"japanese":"公務員の懲戒処分には、免職、停職、減給、戒告の四種類がある。","romaji":"Kōmuin no chōkai shobun ni wa, menshoku, teishoku, genkyū, kaikoku no yon-shurui ga aru.","german":"There are four types of disciplinary action for public employees: dismissal, suspension, pay cut and reprimand.","furigana":"こうむいん|ちょうかいしょぶん|めんしょく|ていしょく|げんきゅう|かいこく|よんしゅるい"}],"notes":"懲戒処分 is formal disciplinary action against civil servants or employees. For civil servants there are four levels: 戒告 \"reprimand\", 減給 \"pay cut\", 停職 \"suspension\" and 免職 \"dismissal\"; companies also have, among others, 譴責 and 懲戒解雇. Mere admonitions such as 訓告 or 厳重注意 do not count. Typical: 懲戒処分を受ける／下す, 懲戒処分とする. Register: administrative and news language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"軽微な違反に対して訓戒処分が行われた。","romaji":"Keibi na ihan ni taishite kunkai shobun ga okonawareta.","german":"An admonition was given for the minor violation.","furigana":"けいび|いはん|たい|くんかいしょぶん|おこな"},{"japanese":"遅刻を繰り返した社員に、上司が訓戒を与えた。","romaji":"Chikoku o kurikaeshita shain ni, jōshi ga kunkai o ataeta.","german":"The boss gave an admonition to the employee who was repeatedly late.","furigana":"ちこく|く|かえ|しゃいん|じょうし|くんかい|あた"},{"japanese":"祖父の訓戒は、今も私の心に残っている。","romaji":"Sofu no kunkai wa, ima mo watashi no kokoro ni nokotte iru.","german":"My grandfather's admonitions stay in my heart even now.","furigana":"そふ|くんかい|いま|わたし|こころ|のこ"}],"notes":"訓戒 is an instructive admonition so that someone does not repeat a mistake. In civil service law, 訓戒 (similarly 訓告) is not a formal disciplinary punishment for civil servants but a step below 戒告; in some company work rules, however, it is a sanction level of its own. Outside work it can also mean the \"admonishing lessons\" of elders. Typical: 訓戒を与える／受ける, 訓戒処分. Register: formal.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"職務怠慢により戒告処分を受けた。","romaji":"Shokumu taiman ni yori kaikoku shobun o uketa.","german":"A reprimand was issued for neglect of duty.","furigana":"しょくむたいまん|かいこくしょぶん|う"},{"japanese":"市は、無断欠勤を繰り返した職員を戒告とした。","romaji":"Shi wa, mudan kekkin o kurikaeshita shokuin o kaikoku to shita.","german":"The city gave a reprimand to the employee who had repeatedly been absent without notice.","furigana":"し|むだんけっきん|く|かえ|しょくいん|かいこく"},{"japanese":"市は空き家の所有者に、取り壊しを求める戒告書を送った。","romaji":"Shi wa akiya no shoyūsha ni, torikowashi o motomeru kaikokusho o okutta.","german":"The city sent the owner of the vacant house a formal warning demanding demolition.","furigana":"し|あ|や|しょゆうしゃ|と|こわ|もと|かいこくしょ|おく"}],"notes":"戒告 is the mildest formal disciplinary punishment for civil servants: responsibility is established in writing, without direct effects on pay, but promotion and bonus may suffer. To be distinguished from 訓告／訓戒, which are not formal punishments. In administrative enforcement law, 戒告 also means the formal warning before a substitute execution (代執行). Typical: 戒告処分を受ける, 〜を戒告とする. Register: administrative language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不祥事の責任を取り減給処分が科された。","romaji":"Fushōji no sekinin o tori genkyū shobun ga kasareta.","german":"A pay cut was imposed to take responsibility for the scandal.","furigana":"ふしょうじ|せきにん|と|げんきゅうしょぶん|か"},{"japanese":"遅刻が多いと減給されることもある。","romaji":"Chikoku ga ōi to genkyū sareru koto mo aru.","german":"If you are late often, you may get a pay cut.","furigana":"ちこく|おお|げんきゅう"},{"japanese":"業績悪化で、全社員が一律10％の減給となった。","romaji":"Gyōseki akka de, zen shain ga ichiritsu juppāsento no genkyū to natta.","german":"Because of deteriorating business performance, all employees received a uniform 10 percent pay cut.","furigana":"ぎょうせきあっか|ぜんしゃいん|いちりつ|げんきゅう"}],"notes":"減給 is a pay cut, both as disciplinary punishment (減給処分, for civil servants up to one fifth for at most one year) and for economic reasons. Typical: 減給になる／となる, 減給される, 減給処分を受ける. Similar is 減俸, which is more used for executives and athletes; 減額 is a general \"reduction of an amount\". Register: neutral to formal.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不正行為により六ヶ月の停職処分を受けた。","romaji":"Fusei kōi ni yori rokkagetsu no teishoku shobun o uketa.","german":"He received a six-month suspension for misconduct.","furigana":"ふせいこうい|ろっかげつ|ていしょくしょぶん|う"},{"japanese":"部下へのパワハラが認定され、課長は停職3か月の処分となった。","romaji":"Buka e no pawahara ga nintei sare, kachō wa teishoku sankagetsu no shobun to natta.","german":"After the abuse of power against his subordinates was established, the section chief was suspended for three months.","furigana":"ぶか|にんてい|かちょう|ていしょく|げつ|しょぶん"},{"japanese":"停職中は給料が支払われない。","romaji":"Teishokuchū wa kyūryō ga shiharawarenai.","german":"No salary is paid during a suspension.","furigana":"ていしょくちゅう|きゅうりょう|しはら"}],"notes":"停職 is a temporary suspension from duty as a disciplinary penalty; for civil servants it lasts from one day to one year, without pay. Typical: 停職処分, 停職〇か月, 停職中. Do not confuse it with 休職 \"leave of absence\" (e.g. because of illness, not a penalty); in companies 停職 roughly corresponds to 出勤停止. Register: administrative and news language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"懲戒免職により公務員としての身分を失った。","romaji":"Chōkai menshoku ni yori kōmuin to shite no mibun o ushinatta.","german":"The disciplinary dismissal cost him his status as a civil servant.","furigana":"ちょうかいめんしょく|こうむいん|みぶん|うしな"},{"japanese":"公金を横領した職員は懲戒免職となった。","romaji":"Kōkin o ōryō shita shokuin wa chōkai menshoku to natta.","german":"The employee who had embezzled public funds was dismissed from service as a disciplinary measure.","furigana":"こうきん|おうりょう|しょくいん|ちょうかいめんしょく"},{"japanese":"組織の再編で、一部の職員が分限免職となった。","romaji":"Soshiki no saihen de, ichibu no shokuin ga bungen menshoku to natta.","german":"In the course of the reorganization, some employees were dismissed for official reasons.","furigana":"そしき|さいへん|いちぶ|しょくいん|ぶんげんめんしょく"}],"notes":"免職 is dismissal from a public office. 懲戒免職 is the most severe disciplinary penalty (usually without a pension), 分限免職 is a dismissal without punitive character, e.g. due to inability to work or job cuts, and 依願免職 is a dismissal at one's own request. In private companies one says 解雇 or 懲戒解雇. Typical: 免職になる／となる, 免職される. Register: administrative and news language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"世界恐慌は多くの国に深刻な影響を与えた。","romaji":"Sekai kyōkō wa ōku no kuni ni shinkoku na eikyō o ataeta.","german":"The Great Depression had a serious impact on many countries.","furigana":"せかいきょうこう|おお|くに|しんこく|えいきょう|あた"},{"japanese":"銀行への取り付け騒ぎが金融恐慌に発展した。","romaji":"Ginkō e no toritsuke sawagi ga kin'yū kyōkō ni hatten shita.","german":"The run on the banks developed into a financial panic.","furigana":"ぎんこう|と|つ|さわ|きんゆうきょうこう|はってん"},{"japanese":"突然の爆発音に、乗客は恐慌状態に陥った。","romaji":"Totsuzen no bakuhatsuon ni, jōkyaku wa kyōkō jōtai ni ochiitta.","german":"At the sudden sound of an explosion, panic broke out among the passengers.","furigana":"とつぜん|ばくはつおん|じょうきゃく|きょうこうじょうたい|おちい"}],"notes":"恐慌 has two meanings: generally \"panic, terror\" (恐慌をきたす, 恐慌状態に陥る) and, in economics, a severe crisis with bank collapses and company bankruptcies, e.g. 世界恐慌 (from 1929), 金融恐慌, 昭和恐慌. 恐慌 is much stronger than 不況 \"recession, slump\" or, colloquially, 不景気. In everyday speech, one usually says パニック for panic. Register: formal, history and economics.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"好況期には雇用が増加する傾向がある。","romaji":"Kōkyōki ni wa koyō ga zōka suru keikō ga aru.","german":"In boom periods, employment tends to increase.","furigana":"こうきょうき|こよう|ぞうか|けいこう"},{"japanese":"半導体業界は空前の好況に沸いている。","romaji":"Handōtai gyōkai wa kūzen no kōkyō ni waite iru.","german":"The semiconductor industry is enjoying an unprecedented boom.","furigana":"はんどうたいぎょうかい|くうぜん|こうきょう|わ"},{"japanese":"好況が続いても、給料はなかなか上がらない。","romaji":"Kōkyō ga tsuzuite mo, kyūryō wa nakanaka agaranai.","german":"Even if the boom continues, wages hardly rise.","furigana":"こうきょう|つづ|きゅうりょう|あ"}],"notes":"好況 denotes a good economic situation, a boom; the opposite is 不況. It sounds more literary than 好景気 or 景気がいい. Typical: 好況が続く, 好況に沸く \"to boom\", 好況期, 空前の好況. For a single business that is doing well, one does not say 好況 but 繁盛する. Register: economic and news language.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"金融機関の規制が強化された。","romaji":"Kin'yū kikan no kisei ga kyōka sareta.","german":"Regulation of financial institutions was tightened.","furigana":"きんゆうきかん|きせい|きょうか"},{"japanese":"日本銀行は金融緩和を続ける方針を示した。","romaji":"Nippon Ginkō wa kin'yū kanwa o tsuzukeru hōshin o shimeshita.","german":"The Bank of Japan indicated that it will continue its monetary easing policy.","furigana":"にっぽんぎんこう|きんゆうかんわ|つづ|ほうしん|しめ"},{"japanese":"兄は大学を出てから金融業界で働いている。","romaji":"Ani wa daigaku o dete kara kin'yū gyōkai de hataraite iru.","german":"My older brother has worked in the financial industry since leaving university.","furigana":"あに|だいがく|で|きんゆうぎょうかい|はたら"}],"notes":"金融 refers to the money and credit system as a whole and mostly appears in compounds: 金融機関 \"financial institution\", 金融市場, 金融政策 \"monetary policy\", 金融危機, 金融緩和 \"monetary easing\" and 金融引き締め \"tightening\". As a modifier it also works with の (金融の専門家). It is to be distinguished from 財政 \"public finances, government budget\"; 経済 is the economy in general. Matter-of-fact news and technical language.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"投機的な取引がバブルを引き起こした。","romaji":"Tōkiteki na torihiki ga baburu o hikiokoshita.","german":"Speculative trading caused the bubble.","furigana":"とうきてき|とりひき|ひ|お"},{"japanese":"株を投機目的で短期間に売買する人も多い。","romaji":"Kabu o tōki mokuteki de tankikan ni baibai suru hito mo ōi.","german":"Many people also buy and sell stocks in the short term for speculative purposes.","furigana":"かぶ|とうきもくてき|たんきかん|ばいばい|ひと|おお"},{"japanese":"土地への投機が地価の高騰を招いた。","romaji":"Tochi e no tōki ga chika no kōtō o maneita.","german":"Speculation in land drove up land prices.","furigana":"とち|とうき|ちか|こうとう|まね"}],"notes":"投機 is \"speculation\": one buys stocks, land or commodities in order to profit from price fluctuations in the short term. Typical are 投機的な取引, 投機目的, 投機マネー and 投機に走る. In contrast, 投資 denotes a longer-term, value-oriented \"investment\"; 投機 therefore often sounds critical. Do not confuse it with the identically read 投棄 (\"illegal dumping\", e.g. 不法投棄).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"利潤を最大化するための戦略を立てる。","romaji":"Rijun wo saidaika suru tame no senryaku wo tateru.","german":"Devise a strategy to maximize profit.","furigana":"りじゅん|さいだいか|せんりゃく|た"},{"japanese":"企業は利潤だけを追求すべきではない。","romaji":"Kigyō wa rijun dake o tsuikyū subeki de wa nai.","german":"Companies should not pursue profit alone.","furigana":"きぎょう|りじゅん|ついきゅう"},{"japanese":"経済学の授業で利潤と費用の関係を学んだ。","romaji":"Keizaigaku no jugyō de rijun to hiyō no kankei o mananda.","german":"In economics class we learned about the relationship between profit and costs.","furigana":"けいざいがく|じゅぎょう|りじゅん|ひよう|かんけい|まな"}],"notes":"利潤 is profit in the economic, rather abstract sense, e.g. in 利潤を追求する \"to pursue profit\" or 利潤の最大化. In business reports and everyday speech one usually says 利益 (with indicators such as 営業利益), colloquially 儲け \"earnings, profit\". 利潤 sounds theoretical and is often used critically when companies chase only profit.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"元本保証のある金融商品を選んだ。","romaji":"Ganpon hoshō no aru kin'yū shōhin wo eranda.","german":"I chose a financial product with a principal guarantee.","furigana":"がんぽんほしょう|きんゆうしょうひん|えら"},{"japanese":"この投資信託は元本割れのリスクがあります。","romaji":"Kono tōshi shintaku wa ganpon ware no risuku ga arimasu.","german":"This investment fund carries the risk that you get back less than you paid in.","furigana":"とうししんたく|がんぽんわ"},{"japanese":"毎月、利息だけでなく元本も少しずつ返済している。","romaji":"Maigetsu, risoku dake de naku ganpon mo sukoshi zutsu hensai shite iru.","german":"Every month I repay not only the interest but also a little of the principal.","furigana":"まいげつ|りそく|がんぽん|すこ|へんさい"}],"notes":"元本 is the amount originally deposited or borrowed, from which interest and returns are calculated: \"capital, principal\". Typical are 元本保証 \"principal guarantee\", 元本割れ \"falling below the amount paid in\" (a loss on funds) and 元本を返済する. For loans one also says 元金, hence 元利 \"principal and interest\"; the counterpart is 利息 or 利子 \"interest\". Language of finance and law.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"利率の引き下げが消費を刺激すると期待されている。","romaji":"Riritsu no hikisage ga shōhi wo shigeki suru to kitai sarete iru.","german":"A cut in interest rates is expected to stimulate consumption.","furigana":"りりつ|ひ|さ|しょうひ|しげき|きたい"},{"japanese":"この定期預金の利率は年0.3パーセントです。","romaji":"Kono teiki yokin no riritsu wa nen rei ten san pāsento desu.","german":"The interest rate on this time deposit is 0.3 percent per year."},{"japanese":"住宅ローンを組む前に、銀行ごとの利率を比較した。","romaji":"Jūtaku rōn o kumu mae ni, ginkō goto no riritsu o hikaku shita.","german":"Before taking out a home loan, I compared the interest rates of the banks.","furigana":"じゅうたく|く|まえ|ぎんこう|りりつ|ひかく"}],"notes":"利率 is the interest rate as a percentage, especially for loans, bonds and savings: 利率が高い／低い, 利率を引き上げる／引き下げる, 年利率. The more common, more general word for \"interest (level)\" is 金利 (金利が上がる, 低金利); 利息 and 利子, on the other hand, denote the amount of interest actually paid. 利率 sounds somewhat more technical and often appears in contract documents.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"家計の貯蓄率が年々低下している。","romaji":"Kakei no chochikuritsu ga nennen teika shite iru.","german":"The household savings rate is falling year by year.","furigana":"かけい|ちょちくりつ|ねんねんていか"},{"japanese":"ボーナスの半分を貯蓄に回すことにした。","romaji":"Bōnasu no hanbun o chochiku ni mawasu koto ni shita.","german":"I decided to put half of my bonus into savings.","furigana":"はんぶん|ちょちく|まわ"},{"japanese":"老後に備えて、若いうちから貯蓄しておいたほうがいい。","romaji":"Rōgo ni sonaete, wakai uchi kara chochiku shite oita hō ga ii.","german":"It is better to save from a young age to prepare for old age.","furigana":"ろうご|そな|わか|ちょちく"}],"notes":"貯蓄 is saving or the accumulated assets in a factual, economic sense, also as a する-verb (老後のために貯蓄する). Common: 貯蓄率 \"savings rate\", 貯蓄に回す \"to put aside as savings\", 貯蓄から投資へ. In everyday speech one usually says 貯金 or 貯金する; 貯蓄 is broader, also covering insurance and securities, and sounds more formal. 預金 is specifically a bank deposit.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"第三者割当増資により資金調達を行った。","romaji":"Daisansha wariate zōshi ni yori shikin chōtatsu wo okonatta.","german":"Funds were raised through a third-party allotment capital increase.","furigana":"|しきんちょうたつ|おこな"},{"japanese":"大手商社が経営難の航空会社の増資を引き受けた。","romaji":"Ōte shōsha ga keieinan no kōkū gaisha no zōshi o hikiuketa.","german":"A major trading company underwrote the capital increase of the struggling airline.","furigana":"おおてしょうしゃ|けいえいなん|こうくうがいしゃ|ぞうし|ひ|う"},{"japanese":"新工場の建設資金を確保するため、来年増資する予定だ。","romaji":"Shin kōjō no kensetsu shikin o kakuho suru tame, rainen zōshi suru yotei da.","german":"To secure funds for the construction of the new factory, a capital increase is planned for next year.","furigana":"しんこうじょう|けんせつしきん|かくほ|らいねんぞうし|よてい"}],"notes":"増資 is the increase of a company's share capital, usually by issuing new shares; as a する-verb 増資する. Typical compounds: 第三者割当増資 \"capital increase by allotment to third parties\", 公募増資 \"capital increase by public offering\"; whoever takes up the new shares 増資を引き受ける. The opposite is 減資; raising money in general is called 資金調達. Purely the language of business and news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"累積赤字を解消するために減資を実施した。","romaji":"Ruiseki akaji wo kaishō suru tame ni genshi wo jisshi shita.","german":"A capital reduction was carried out to eliminate the accumulated deficit.","furigana":"るいせきあかじ|かいしょう|げんし|じっし"},{"japanese":"再建計画では100％減資が行われ、既存の株は価値を失った。","romaji":"Saiken keikaku de wa hyaku pāsento genshi ga okonaware, kizon no kabu wa kachi o ushinatta.","german":"In the restructuring plan, the capital was reduced by 100%, and the existing shares lost their value.","furigana":"さいけんけいかく|げんし|おこな|きぞん|かぶ|かち|うしな"},{"japanese":"税負担を軽くするため、資本金を1億円以下に減資する企業が増えている。","romaji":"Zei futan o karuku suru tame, shihonkin o ichioku en ika ni genshi suru kigyō ga fuete iru.","german":"More and more companies are reducing their share capital to 100 million yen or less in order to ease their tax burden.","furigana":"ぜいふたん|かる|しほんきん|おくえんいか|げんし|きぎょう|ふ"}],"notes":"減資 is the reduction of share capital; as a する-verb 減資する. It often serves to offset accumulated losses on the books, or to qualify as a small business for tax purposes with smaller capital. Collocations: 減資を行う／実施する, 100％減資 (complete reduction, in which the old shares become worthless). Opposite: 増資. Technical language from corporate law and business news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"関連会社との取引は適正価格で行われている。","romaji":"Kanren gaisha to no torihiki wa tekisei kakaku de okonawarete iru.","german":"Transactions with affiliated companies are conducted at fair prices.","furigana":"かんれんがいしゃ|とりひき|てきせいかかく|おこな"},{"japanese":"父は定年後、元の会社の関連会社で働いている。","romaji":"Chichi wa teinengo, moto no kaisha no kanren gaisha de hataraite iru.","german":"After retiring, my father works at an affiliate of his former company.","furigana":"ちち|ていねんご|もと|かいしゃ|かんれんがいしゃ|はたら"},{"japanese":"子会社と関連会社では、決算での扱いが異なる。","romaji":"Kogaisha to kanren gaisha de wa, kessan de no atsukai ga kotonaru.","german":"Subsidiaries and affiliated companies are treated differently in the financial statements.","furigana":"こがいしゃ|かんれんがいしゃ|けっさん|あつか|こと"}],"notes":"関連会社 is, in accounting law, a company in which a firm holds a significant stake without controlling it (usually about 20-50%): an \"associate company\". To be distinguished from it are 子会社 \"subsidiary\" (control) and 親会社 \"parent company\". In everyday speech, however, any firm of the same corporate group is often loosely called 関連会社 or グループ会社. Here 会社 is read がいしゃ.","levelNote":"Compound: 関連 (N3) + 会社 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"持株会社体制に移行してグループ経営を強化する。","romaji":"Mochikabu gaisha taisei ni ikō shite gurūpu keiei wo kyōka suru.","german":"Strengthen group management by shifting to a holding company structure.","furigana":"もちかぶがいしゃたいせい|いこう|けいえい|きょうか"},{"japanese":"両社は経営統合し、新たに持株会社を設立した。","romaji":"Ryōsha wa keiei tōgō shi, arata ni mochikabu gaisha o setsuritsu shita.","german":"The two companies merged their management and established a new holding company.","furigana":"りょうしゃ|けいえいとうごう|あら|もちかぶがいしゃ|せつりつ"},{"japanese":"そのスーパーは昨年、大手流通グループの持株会社の傘下に入った。","romaji":"Sono sūpā wa sakunen, ōte ryūtsū gurūpu no mochikabu gaisha no sanka ni haitta.","german":"The supermarket came under the umbrella of the holding company of a major retail group last year.","furigana":"さくねん|おおてりゅうつう|もちかぶがいしゃ|さんか|はい"}],"notes":"持株会社 is a company whose main purpose is to hold shares of other companies and control them: a \"holding\". In company names it now often appears as 〜ホールディングス; 純粋持株会社 only holds stakes, 事業持株会社 additionally runs its own business. Typical: 持株会社を設立する, 持株会社体制に移行する, 持株会社の傘下に入る. 持株 alone is \"share ownership\" (持株比率 \"shareholding ratio\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"株主総会で新たな取締役が選任された。","romaji":"Kabunushi sōkai de arata na torishimariyaku ga sennin sareta.","german":"New directors were elected at the shareholders' meeting.","furigana":"かぶぬしそうかい|あら|とりしまりやく|せんにん"},{"japanese":"国連総会で各国の首脳が演説した。","romaji":"Kokuren sōkai de kakkoku no shunō ga enzetsu shita.","german":"Heads of state and government gave speeches at the UN General Assembly.","furigana":"こくれんそうかい|かっこく|しゅのう|えんぜつ"},{"japanese":"マンションの管理組合の総会は毎年五月に開かれる。","romaji":"Manshon no kanri kumiai no sōkai wa maitoshi gogatsu ni hirakareru.","german":"The general meeting of our apartment building's management association is held every May.","furigana":"かんりくみあい|そうかい|まいとしごがつ|ひら"}],"notes":"総会 is the assembly of all members of an organization, which decides on important matters. Mostly in compounds: 株主総会 \"general shareholders' meeting\", 国連総会 \"UN General Assembly\", 定期総会 and 臨時総会, in schools 生徒総会. Verbs: 総会を開く／開催する, 総会で承認される／決議する. An ordinary meeting is 会議 or 集会; 総会 emphasizes that all members take part with voting rights.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"代表取締役が辞任を表明した。","romaji":"Daihyō torishimariyaku ga jinin wo hyōmei shita.","german":"Der Vorstandsvorsitzende hat seinen Rücktritt erklärt.","furigana":"だいひょうとりしまりやく|じにん|ひょうめい"},{"japanese":"今年から社外取締役を二人増やすことになった。","romaji":"Kotoshi kara shagai torishimariyaku o futari fuyasu koto ni natta.","german":"Ab diesem Jahr gibt es zwei externe Vorstandsmitglieder mehr.","furigana":"ことし|しゃがいとりしまりやく|ふたりふ"},{"japanese":"彼は四十歳の若さで取締役に就任した。","romaji":"Kare wa yonjussai no wakasa de torishimariyaku ni shūnin shita.","german":"Er wurde schon mit vierzig Jahren in den Vorstand berufen.","furigana":"かれ|よんじゅっさい|わか|とりしまりやく|しゅうにん"}],"notes":"取締役 ist ein Mitglied des Leitungsgremiums einer japanischen Aktiengesellschaft; zusammen bilden sie den 取締役会. 代表取締役 ist das vertretungsberechtigte Mitglied, meist zugleich der 社長. Weitere Titel: 専務取締役, 常務取締役, 社外取締役 „externes Vorstandsmitglied“. Gewählt wird auf der 株主総会 (取締役に選任される／就任する). Ein genaues deutsches Gegenstück fehlt; je nach Kontext „Vorstandsmitglied“ oder „Direktor“. 役員 ist der Oberbegriff für Führungskräfte einschließlich 監査役.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"監査役が会計処理の不正を指摘した。","romaji":"Kansayaku ga kaikei shori no fusei wo shiteki shita.","german":"Der Rechnungsprüfer wies auf Unregelmäßigkeiten in der Buchführung hin.","furigana":"かんさやく|かいけいしょり|ふせい|してき"},{"japanese":"監査役は、取締役が法律に従って仕事をしているかをチェックする。","romaji":"Kansayaku wa, torishimariyaku ga hōritsu ni shitagatte shigoto o shite iru ka o chekku suru.","german":"Der Rechnungsprüfer kontrolliert, ob sich die Vorstandsmitglieder bei ihrer Arbeit an die Gesetze halten.","furigana":"かんさやく|とりしまりやく|ほうりつ|したが|しごと"},{"japanese":"弁護士の田中さんが当社の社外監査役に就任した。","romaji":"Bengoshi no Tanaka-san ga tōsha no shagai kansayaku ni shūnin shita.","german":"Rechtsanwalt Tanaka wurde zum externen Rechnungsprüfer unseres Unternehmens bestellt.","furigana":"べんごし|たなか|とうしゃ|しゃがいかんさやく|しゅうにん"}],"notes":"監査役 ist ein gesetzliches Kontrollorgan der japanischen Aktiengesellschaft, das die Amtsführung der 取締役 und die Rechnungslegung überwacht; üblich sind Übersetzungen wie „Rechnungsprüfer“ oder „Corporate Auditor“. Es entspricht nicht dem deutschen Aufsichtsrat, der den Vorstand bestellt, und ist auch kein externer Wirtschaftsprüfer – der heißt 公認会計士 bzw. als Organ 会計監査人. Mehrere 監査役 bilden den 監査役会; sie werden auf der 株主総会 gewählt, oft auch als 社外監査役.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"定款を変更するには株主総会の決議が必要だ。","romaji":"Teikan wo henkō suru ni wa kabunushi sōkai no ketsugi ga hitsuyō da.","german":"A resolution of the shareholders' meeting is required to amend the articles of incorporation.","furigana":"ていかん|へんこう|かぶぬしそうかい|けつぎ|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"会社を設立するには、まず定款を作成しなければならない。","romaji":"Kaisha o setsuritsu suru ni wa, mazu teikan o sakusei shinakereba naranai.","german":"To establish a company, you must first draw up the articles of incorporation.","furigana":"かいしゃ|せつりつ|ていかん|さくせい"},{"japanese":"定款に記載されていない事業を始めることはできない。","romaji":"Teikan ni kisai sarete inai jigyō o hajimeru koto wa dekinai.","german":"You cannot start a business that is not listed in the articles of incorporation.","furigana":"ていかん|きさい|じぎょう|はじ"}],"notes":"定款 are the articles of association of a legal entity (stock company, foundation, association), which set out its purpose, name, seat and organs. Typical: 定款を作成する, 定款を変更する, 定款に定める \"to stipulate in the articles\", 定款の認証 (notarial certification at founding). Pre-formulated customer terms, on the other hand, are called 約款, and the rules of an association or service are often 規約. Purely legal, formal language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"保険約款の内容を十分に確認してから契約した。","romaji":"Hoken yakkan no naiyō wo jūbun ni kakunin shite kara keiyaku shita.","german":"I signed the contract after thoroughly checking the insurance terms.","furigana":"ほけんやっかん|ないよう|じゅうぶん|かくにん|けいやく"},{"japanese":"約款に同意しないと、このサービスは利用できません。","romaji":"Yakkan ni dōi shinai to, kono sābisu wa riyō dekimasen.","german":"You cannot use this service unless you agree to the terms and conditions.","furigana":"やっかん|どうい|りよう"},{"japanese":"荷物の破損については、運送約款に基づいて補償されます。","romaji":"Nimotsu no hason ni tsuite wa, unsō yakkan ni motozuite hoshō saremasu.","german":"Damage to luggage is compensated in accordance with the carriage terms.","furigana":"にもつ|はそん|うんそうやっかん|もと|ほしょう"}],"notes":"約款 are the pre-formulated contract terms of a company, identical for all customers: \"standard terms and conditions\", for insurance \"insurance terms\". Typical: 保険約款, 運送約款, 約款に同意する, 約款に基づいて. A single clause is called 条項. While 定款 is the internal charter of a company, 約款 governs the relationship with customers; for online services one usually reads 利用規約 \"terms of use\". Formal legal and business language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"景況感が改善し、企業の設備投資が活発化している。","romaji":"Keikyōkan ga kaizen shi, kigyō no setsubi tōshi ga kappatsuka shite iru.","german":"Business sentiment has improved and companies' capital investment is picking up.","furigana":"けいきょうかん|かいぜん|きぎょう|せつびとうし|かっぱつか"},{"japanese":"中小企業の景況は依然として厳しい。","romaji":"Chūshō kigyō no keikyō wa izen to shite kibishii.","german":"The business conditions of small and medium-sized enterprises remain difficult.","furigana":"ちゅうしょうきぎょう|けいきょう|いぜん|きび"},{"japanese":"商工会議所が地域の景況調査の結果を発表した。","romaji":"Shōkō kaigisho ga chiiki no keikyō chōsa no kekka o happyō shita.","german":"The chamber of commerce and industry announced the results of its regional business conditions survey.","furigana":"しょうこうかいぎしょ|ちいき|けいきょうちょうさ|けっか|はっぴょう"}],"notes":"景況 is the general business or economic situation and occurs almost only in business reports. Very common is 景況感 \"economic sentiment, business climate\", that is, how companies judge the situation, as well as 景況調査 \"business conditions survey\". In everyday speech one says 景気 (景気がいい／悪い); 景況 is the formal, analytical counterpart and is more often associated with 厳しい, 改善する or 悪化する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"インフレーションを抑制するために金融引き締めが行われた。","romaji":"Infurēshon wo yokusei suru tame ni kin'yū hikishime ga okonawareta.","german":"Monetary tightening was carried out to curb inflation.","furigana":"よくせい|きんゆうひ|し|おこな"},{"japanese":"急激なインフレーションで国民の生活が苦しくなった。","romaji":"Kyūgeki na infurēshon de kokumin no seikatsu ga kurushiku natta.","german":"Rapid inflation made life hard for the people.","furigana":"きゅうげき|こくみん|せいかつ|くる"},{"japanese":"インフレーションとは、物価が継続的に上がる現象のことだ。","romaji":"Infurēshon to wa, bukka ga keizokuteki ni agaru genshō no koto da.","german":"Inflation is the phenomenon of prices rising continuously.","furigana":"ぶっか|けいぞくてき|あ|げんしょう"}],"notes":"インフレーション is the long form; in news and conversation people almost always say the short form インフレ (インフレが進む, インフレ率, インフレ対策). The long form sounds textbook-like and appears in definitions or technical texts, also in cosmology (インフレーション理論). The opposite is デフレーション／デフレ; a mere price rise is called 物価上昇, and inflation with a stagnating economy スタグフレーション.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"デフレーションから脱却するための政策が求められている。","romaji":"Defurēshon kara dakkyaku suru tame no seisaku ga motomerarete iru.","german":"Policies to escape deflation are called for.","furigana":"だっきゃく|せいさく|もと"},{"japanese":"デフレーションが続くと、企業の売り上げが伸びにくくなる。","romaji":"Defurēshon ga tsuzuku to, kigyō no uriage ga nobinikuku naru.","german":"If deflation continues, it becomes hard for companies to grow their sales.","furigana":"つづ|きぎょう|う|あ|の"},{"japanese":"日本は長い間、デフレーションに苦しんできた。","romaji":"Nihon wa nagai aida, defurēshon ni kurushinde kita.","german":"Japan has suffered from deflation for a long time.","furigana":"にほん|なが|あいだ|くる"}],"notes":"デフレーション is the long form; the short form デフレ is usual, e.g. in デフレ経済, デフレスパイラル and above all デフレから脱却する \"to overcome deflation\", a perennial theme of Japanese economic policy since the 1990s. Opposite: インフレーション／インフレ. Deflation is a price phenomenon and not the same as 不況 \"recession\", even though the two often occur together.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"通信業界は少数の企業による寡占状態にある。","romaji":"Tsūshin gyōkai wa shōsū no kigyō ni yoru kasen jōtai ni aru.","german":"The telecommunications industry is in a state of oligopoly by a few companies.","furigana":"つうしんぎょうかい|しょうすう|きぎょう|かせんじょうたい"},{"japanese":"スマートフォンのOS市場は二社による寡占が進んでいる。","romaji":"Sumātofon no OS shijō wa nisha ni yoru kasen ga susunde iru.","german":"The smartphone OS market is increasingly dominated by two companies.","furigana":"しじょう|にしゃ|かせん|すす"},{"japanese":"寡占市場では、価格競争が起きにくい。","romaji":"Kasen shijō de wa, kakaku kyōsō ga okinikui.","german":"In an oligopolistic market, price competition is unlikely to occur.","furigana":"かせんしじょう|かかくきょうそう|お"}],"notes":"寡占 denotes a market dominated by a few large suppliers: an \"oligopoly\". Typical: 寡占状態, 寡占市場, 寡占化が進む; as a transitive する-verb 上位三社が市場を寡占する. To be distinguished from it is 独占 \"monopoly\" (a single supplier). The kanji 寡 means \"few\" as in 寡黙 \"taciturn\". Technical and news language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"競争の激化により弱小企業が淘汰されている。","romaji":"Kyōsō no gekika ni yori jakushō kigyō ga tōta sarete iru.","german":"As competition intensifies, small and weak companies are being weeded out.","furigana":"きょうそう|げきか|じゃくしょうきぎょう|とうた"},{"japanese":"時代の変化に対応できない店は、いずれ淘汰されるだろう。","romaji":"Jidai no henka ni taiō dekinai mise wa, izure tōta sareru darō.","german":"Shops that cannot adapt to changing times will sooner or later be weeded out.","furigana":"じだい|へんか|たいおう|みせ|とうた"},{"japanese":"キリンの長い首は自然淘汰の結果だと説明されることが多い。","romaji":"Kirin no nagai kubi wa shizen tōta no kekka da to setsumei sareru koto ga ōi.","german":"The giraffe's long neck is often explained as the result of natural selection.","furigana":"なが|くび|しぜんとうた|けっか|せつめい|おお"}],"notes":"淘汰 means that what is weak or useless is sorted out and only the capable remains. Mostly passive: 淘汰される \"to be pushed out, weeded out\", e.g. 市場から淘汰される; also 淘汰が進む. In biology there is 自然淘汰 \"natural selection\" (also 自然選択). The tone is sober and harsh; for dismissing individual employees one rather says 解雇 or リストラ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"異業種からの参入が業界の競争を激化させている。","romaji":"Igyōshu kara no sannyū ga gyōkai no kyōsō wo gekika sasete iru.","german":"Entry from other industries is intensifying competition in the industry.","furigana":"いぎょうしゅ|さんにゅう|ぎょうかい|きょうそう|げきか"},{"japanese":"大手IT企業が電力事業に参入すると発表した。","romaji":"Ōte IT kigyō ga denryoku jigyō ni sannyū suru to happyō shita.","german":"A major IT company announced that it will enter the electric power business.","furigana":"おおて|きぎょう|でんりょくじぎょう|さんにゅう|はっぴょう"},{"japanese":"この業界は参入障壁が高く、新規参入は難しい。","romaji":"Kono gyōkai wa sannyū shōheki ga takaku, shinki sannyū wa muzukashii.","german":"This industry has high barriers to entry, so new entry is difficult.","furigana":"ぎょうかい|さんにゅうしょうへき|たか|しんきさんにゅう|むずか"}],"notes":"参入 is entry into a market or industry; an intransitive する-verb with に: 市場に参入する. Common: 新規参入 \"new entry, new entrant\", 参入障壁 \"market entry barrier\"; the opposite is 撤退する \"to withdraw\". Do not confuse it with 参加 \"to take part\" (in events) or 加入 \"to join\" (insurance, club). The old meaning \"to appear at court\" is rare today.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"採算の取れない部門を整理する方針だ。","romaji":"Saisan no torenai bumon wo seiri suru hōshin da.","german":"The policy is to wind up unprofitable divisions.","furigana":"さいさん|と|ぶもん|せいり|ほうしん"},{"japanese":"この値段では採算が合わないので、値上げせざるを得ない。","romaji":"Kono nedan de wa saisan ga awanai node, neage sezaru o enai.","german":"At this price it is not profitable, so we have no choice but to raise prices.","furigana":"ねだん|さいさん|あ|ねあ|え"},{"japanese":"あの店は採算を度外視して、地元の子どもたちに安く食事を出している。","romaji":"Ano mise wa saisan o dogaishi shite, jimoto no kodomotachi ni yasuku shokuji o dashite iru.","german":"That restaurant serves cheap meals to local children, disregarding profit.","furigana":"みせ|さいさん|どがいし|じもと|こ|やす|しょくじ|だ"}],"notes":"採算 is the calculation of whether something is financially worthwhile: \"profitability\". Almost always in set phrases: 採算が取れる／合う \"to pay off\", 採算が取れない／合わない, 採算を度外視して \"without regard for profit\", 不採算部門 \"unprofitable division\", 採算性. A pure profit amount would be 利益; 採算 emphasizes the balance of costs and returns. Neutral, also used orally in business.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"営業利益が過去最高を更新した。","romaji":"Eigyō rieki ga kako saikō wo kōshin shita.","german":"Operating profit hit a record high.","furigana":"えいぎょうりえき|かこさいこう|こうしん"},{"japanese":"原材料費の高騰で、営業利益が前年より二割減った。","romaji":"Genzairyōhi no kōtō de, eigyō rieki ga zennen yori niwari hetta.","german":"Due to soaring raw material costs, operating profit fell by 20% from the previous year.","furigana":"げんざいりょうひ|こうとう|えいぎょうりえき|ぜんねん|にわりへ"},{"japanese":"売上は伸びたが、広告費がかさんで営業利益は横ばいだった。","romaji":"Uriage wa nobita ga, kōkokuhi ga kasande eigyō rieki wa yokobai datta.","german":"Sales grew, but with rising advertising costs operating profit was flat.","furigana":"うりあげ|の|こうこくひ|えいぎょうりえき|よこ"}],"notes":"営業利益 is the profit from the core business: sales minus cost of goods sold (売上原価) and selling, general and administrative expenses (販売費及び一般管理費). In the Japanese income statement it follows 売上総利益 \"gross profit\" and comes before 経常利益 and 純利益. Common: 営業利益率 \"operating margin\", 営業利益が増える／減る; an operating loss is called 営業損失 or 営業赤字.","levelNote":"Compound: 営業 (N4) + 利益 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"経常利益は前期比で一割減少した。","romaji":"Keijō rieki wa zenki hi de ichiwari genshō shita.","german":"Der ordentliche Gewinn sank gegenüber dem vorigen Geschäftsjahr um zehn Prozent.","furigana":"けいじょうりえき|ぜんきひ|いちわりげんしょう"},{"japanese":"円安の影響で、経常利益が予想を大きく上回った。","romaji":"En'yasu no eikyō de, keijō rieki ga yosō o ōkiku uwamawatta.","german":"Dank des schwachen Yen übertraf der ordentliche Gewinn die Prognosen deutlich.","furigana":"えんやす|えいきょう|けいじょうりえき|よそう|おお|うわまわ"},{"japanese":"本業は赤字だったが、受取配当金のおかげで経常利益は黒字になった。","romaji":"Hongyō wa akaji datta ga, uketori haitōkin no okage de keijō rieki wa kuroji ni natta.","german":"Das Kerngeschäft schrieb rote Zahlen, doch dank der Dividendeneinnahmen war der ordentliche Gewinn positiv.","furigana":"ほんぎょう|あかじ|うけとりはいとうきん|けいじょうりえき|くろじ"}],"notes":"経常利益 ist eine typisch japanische Gewinnkennzahl: 営業利益 plus nicht-operative Erträge (営業外収益, z. B. Zinsen und Dividenden) minus nicht-operative Aufwendungen (営業外費用); außerordentliche Posten (特別利益／特別損失) sind noch nicht enthalten. Im Deutschen entspricht es etwa dem „Ergebnis der gewöhnlichen Geschäftstätigkeit“. In Schlagzeilen kurz 経常益, z. B. 経常最高益. Nicht verwechseln mit 経常収支 „Leistungsbilanz“ eines Staates.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"純利益が大幅に増加し、株価も上昇した。","romaji":"Jun rieki ga ōhaba ni zōka shi, kabuka mo jōshō shita.","german":"Net profit rose sharply, and the share price went up as well.","furigana":"じゅんりえき|おおはば|ぞうか|かぶか|じょうしょう"},{"japanese":"工場の売却益が加わり、純利益は前年の二倍になった。","romaji":"Kōjō no baikyakueki ga kuwawari, jun rieki wa zennen no nibai ni natta.","german":"With the addition of gains from the sale of a factory, net profit doubled from the previous year.","furigana":"こうじょう|ばいきゃくえき|くわ|じゅんりえき|ぜんねん|にばい"},{"japanese":"売上は好調でも、税金や利息を払うと純利益はわずかしか残らない。","romaji":"Uriage wa kōchō demo, zeikin ya risoku o harau to jun rieki wa wazuka shika nokoranai.","german":"Even if sales are strong, only a small net profit remains after paying taxes and interest.","furigana":"うりあげ|こうちょう|ぜいきん|りそく|はら|じゅんりえき|のこ"}],"notes":"純利益 is the profit left after deducting all costs, extraordinary items and taxes: \"net profit, net income\"; formally in full 当期純利益. In the news 最終利益 or 最終損益 is often used with the same meaning, and a loss is called 純損失 or 最終赤字. Colloquially, gross profit is spoken of as 粗利; 純利益 is the figure at the very bottom of the income statement.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"設備の減価償却費を計上する。","romaji":"Setsubi no genka shōkyakuhi wo keijō suru.","german":"Record the depreciation expense of the equipment.","furigana":"せつび|げんかしょうきゃくひ|けいじょう"},{"japanese":"パソコンは四年で減価償却するのが一般的だ。","romaji":"Pasokon wa yonen de genka shōkyaku suru no ga ippanteki da.","german":"Computers are generally depreciated over four years.","furigana":"よねん|げんかしょうきゃく|いっぱんてき"},{"japanese":"減価償却が終わった機械でも、まだ十分に使える。","romaji":"Genka shōkyaku ga owatta kikai demo, mada jūbun ni tsukaeru.","german":"Even a fully depreciated machine can still be used well.","furigana":"げんかしょうきゃく|お|きかい|じゅうぶん|つか"}],"notes":"減価償却 is the accounting allocation of the acquisition cost of durable assets such as machines, buildings or vehicles over their useful life (耐用年数): \"depreciation\". Typical: 減価償却費 \"depreciation expense\", 減価償却する, 減価償却が終わる, 定額法／定率法 \"straight-line／declining-balance method\"; in everyday office speech often just 償却. Unlike 値下がり (price decline on the market), it is a purely accounting term.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"複式簿記の知識は経理担当者に必須だ。","romaji":"Fukushiki boki no chishiki wa keiri tantōsha ni hissu da.","german":"Knowledge of double-entry bookkeeping is essential for accounting staff.","furigana":"ふくしきぼき|ちしき|けいりたんとうしゃ|ひっす"},{"japanese":"就職に有利だと思って、簿記の資格を取った。","romaji":"Shūshoku ni yūri da to omotte, boki no shikaku o totta.","german":"Thinking it would help in job hunting, I obtained a bookkeeping certificate.","furigana":"しゅうしょく|ゆうり|おも|ぼき|しかく|と"},{"japanese":"小さな店でも、簿記の基本を知っておくと便利だ。","romaji":"Chiisana mise demo, boki no kihon o shitte oku to benri da.","german":"Even for a small shop, it is useful to know the basics of bookkeeping.","furigana":"ちい|みせ|ぼき|きほん|し|べんり"}],"notes":"簿記 is the technique or subject of bookkeeping, that is, the systematic recording of all business transactions. Typical: 複式簿記 \"double-entry bookkeeping\", 単式簿記, 簿記を習う／勉強する, 簿記の資格 or 簿記検定 (a very widespread professional qualification). The concrete keeping of books is rather 帳簿をつける. 会計 is accounting in general, 経理 bookkeeping as an activity or department in a company.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"貸借対照表から企業の財務状況が読み取れる。","romaji":"Taishaku taishōhyō kara kigyō no zaimu jōkyō ga yomitoreru.","german":"The company's financial situation can be read from the balance sheet.","furigana":"たいしゃくたいしょうひょう|きぎょう|ざいむじょうきょう|よ|と"},{"japanese":"貸借対照表の左側には資産、右側には負債と純資産が記載される。","romaji":"Taishaku taishōhyō no hidarigawa ni wa shisan, migigawa ni wa fusai to junshisan ga kisai sareru.","german":"On the left side of the balance sheet, assets are listed; on the right, liabilities and net assets.","furigana":"たいしゃくたいしょうひょう|ひだりがわ|しさん|みぎがわ|ふさい|じゅんしさん|きさい"},{"japanese":"決算の時期に貸借対照表を作成して、税務署に提出した。","romaji":"Kessan no jiki ni taishaku taishōhyō o sakusei shite, zeimusho ni teishutsu shita.","german":"At closing time, I prepared the balance sheet and submitted it to the tax office.","furigana":"けっさん|じき|たいしゃくたいしょうひょう|さくせい|ぜいむしょ|ていしゅつ"}],"notes":"貸借対照表 is a company's balance sheet as of a reporting date: on the left the assets (資産), on the right the liabilities (負債) and equity (純資産). In practice one often says バランスシート or just B/S. Together with 損益計算書 and キャッシュフロー計算書 it forms the 財務諸表 \"financial statements\". Typical: 貸借対照表を作成する, 貸借対照表に計上する. Technical language of accounting.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"損益計算書に基づいて業績を分析する。","romaji":"Son'eki keisansho ni motozuite gyōseki wo bunseki suru.","german":"Analyze business performance based on the income statement.","furigana":"そんえきけいさんしょ|もと|ぎょうせき|ぶんせき"},{"japanese":"損益計算書を見ると、売上は増えたが利益は減っていることが分かる。","romaji":"Son'eki keisansho o miru to, uriage wa fueta ga rieki wa hette iru koto ga wakaru.","german":"Looking at the income statement, you can see that sales have increased but profit has decreased.","furigana":"そんえきけいさんしょ|み|うりあげ|ふ|りえき|へ|わ"},{"japanese":"貸借対照表が一時点の財政状態を示すのに対し、損益計算書は一定期間の経営成績を示す。","romaji":"Taishaku taishōhyō ga ichijiten no zaisei jōtai o shimesu no ni taishi, son'eki keisansho wa ittei kikan no keiei seiseki o shimesu.","german":"Whereas the balance sheet shows the financial position at a single point in time, the income statement shows the business results over a given period.","furigana":"たいしゃくたいしょうひょう|いちじてん|ざいせいじょうたい|しめ|たい|そんえきけいさんしょ|いっていきかん|けいえいせいせき|しめ"}],"notes":"損益計算書 is the income statement (profit and loss statement), in practice also called P/L. For a period it shows sales, costs and the profit levels 売上総利益 → 営業利益 → 経常利益 → 当期純利益. Whereas 貸借対照表 shows the position at a reporting date, 損益計算書 describes the result of a period. Typical: 損益計算書を作成する／読む. Technical language of accounting.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"資金繰りが悪化し、経営危機に直面している。","romaji":"Shikinguri ga akka shi, keiei kiki ni chokumen shite iru.","german":"Cash flow has worsened, and the company is facing a management crisis.","furigana":"しきんぐ|あっか|けいえいきき|ちょくめん"},{"japanese":"取引先の倒産で入金が遅れ、資金繰りに苦しんでいる。","romaji":"Torihikisaki no tōsan de nyūkin ga okure, shikinguri ni kurushinde iru.","german":"Because a business partner went bankrupt, payments are late and we are struggling with cash flow.","furigana":"とりひきさき|とうさん|にゅうきん|おく|しきんぐ|くる"},{"japanese":"社長は毎月の資金繰りに頭を悩ませている。","romaji":"Shachō wa maitsuki no shikinguri ni atama o nayamasete iru.","german":"The president racks his brains every month over how to manage the cash flow.","furigana":"しゃちょう|まいつき|しきんぐ|あたま|なや"}],"notes":"資金繰り is the ongoing juggling of incoming and outgoing payments so that a firm stays solvent at all times: \"liquidity planning, cash position\". Typical: 資金繰りが苦しい／厳しい／悪化する, 資金繰りに困る, 資金繰りに行き詰まる (often a precursor to insolvency), 資金繰りをつける. 繰り comes from 繰る \"to handle one after another\" and is read ぐり here. To be distinguished from 資金調達, the raising of new capital.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新規事業のための資金調達に奔走している。","romaji":"Shinki jigyō no tame no shikin chōtatsu ni honsō shite iru.","german":"He is busy running around raising funds for the new business.","furigana":"しんきじぎょう|しきんちょうたつ|ほんそう"},{"japanese":"そのベンチャー企業は、クラウドファンディングで資金調達に成功した。","romaji":"Sono benchā kigyō wa, kuraudo fandingu de shikin chōtatsu ni seikō shita.","german":"The venture company succeeded in raising funds through crowdfunding.","furigana":"きぎょう|しきんちょうたつ|せいこう"},{"japanese":"銀行からの融資だけでなく、社債の発行も資金調達の手段の一つだ。","romaji":"Ginkō kara no yūshi dake de naku, shasai no hakkō mo shikin chōtatsu no shudan no hitotsu da.","german":"Besides bank loans, issuing bonds is also one means of raising funds.","furigana":"ぎんこう|ゆうし|しゃさい|はっこう|しきんちょうたつ|しゅだん|ひと"}],"notes":"資金調達 is the procurement of capital, for example through bank loans (融資), new shares (増資), bonds (社債) or クラウドファンディング. Typical: 資金調達を行う, 資金調達に成功する／苦労する, 資金調達の手段. As a verb one usually says 資金を調達する. Unlike 資金繰り, which concerns ongoing solvency, this is about raising money for projects. Business and news language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"設備投資を増やして生産能力を拡大する。","romaji":"Setsubi tōshi wo fuyashite seisan nōryoku wo kakudai suru.","german":"Increase capital investment to expand production capacity.","furigana":"せつびとうし|ふ|せいさんのうりょく|かくだい"},{"japanese":"景気の先行きが不透明なため、多くの企業が設備投資を控えている。","romaji":"Keiki no sakiyuki ga futōmei na tame, ōku no kigyō ga setsubi tōshi o hikaete iru.","german":"Because the economic outlook is uncertain, many companies are holding back on capital investment.","furigana":"けいき|さきゆ|ふとうめい|おお|きぎょう|せつびとうし|ひか"},{"japanese":"工場の老朽化が進み、大規模な設備投資が避けられなくなった。","romaji":"Kōjō no rōkyūka ga susumi, daikibo na setsubi tōshi ga sakerarenaku natta.","german":"As the factory has aged, large-scale capital investment has become unavoidable.","furigana":"こうじょう|ろうきゅうか|すす|だいきぼ|せつびとうし|さ"}],"notes":"設備投資 is investment in physical assets such as factories, machines or IT systems: \"capital investment\". As 民間設備投資 (private capital investment) it is an important economic indicator. Typical: 設備投資を増やす／抑える／控える, 設備投資が活発になる, 設備投資額. 投資 alone also covers financial investments such as stocks; 研究開発投資 is spending on research and development.","levelNote":"Compound: 設備 (N4) + 投資 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"内需の低迷が経済成長の足かせになっている。","romaji":"Naiju no teimei ga keizai seichō no ashikase ni natte iru.","german":"Weak domestic demand is a drag on economic growth.","furigana":"ないじゅ|ていめい|けいざいせいちょう|あし"},{"japanese":"政府は内需拡大のために減税を打ち出した。","romaji":"Seifu wa naiju kakudai no tame ni genzei o uchidashita.","german":"The government announced tax cuts to boost domestic demand.","furigana":"せいふ|ないじゅかくだい|げんぜい|う|だ"},{"japanese":"鉄道や小売りなどの内需型企業は、円高の影響を受けにくい。","romaji":"Tetsudō ya kouri nado no naijugata kigyō wa, endaka no eikyō o ukenikui.","german":"Domestically oriented companies such as railways and retailers are hardly affected by a strong yen.","furigana":"てつどう|こう|ないじゅがたきぎょう|えんだか|えいきょう|う"}],"notes":"内需 is the short form of 国内需要, demand within the country through consumption and investment: \"domestic demand\". Typical: 内需拡大, 内需主導の成長, 内需が低迷する／回復する, 内需型企業 \"companies oriented toward the domestic market\". The counterpart is 外需 \"foreign demand\". A word of economic news and statistics.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"外需に依存した経済構造からの転換が求められている。","romaji":"Gaiju ni izon shita keizai kōzō kara no tenkan ga motomerarete iru.","german":"A shift away from an economic structure dependent on external demand is called for.","furigana":"がいじゅ|いぞん|けいざいこうぞう|てんかん|もと"},{"japanese":"世界経済の減速で外需が落ち込み、輸出企業の業績が悪化した。","romaji":"Sekai keizai no gensoku de gaiju ga ochikomi, yushutsu kigyō no gyōseki ga akka shita.","german":"With the slowdown in the global economy, external demand collapsed and the results of export companies worsened.","furigana":"せかいけいざい|げんそく|がいじゅ|お|こ|ゆしゅつきぎょう|ぎょうせき|あっか"},{"japanese":"今期の成長は、内需よりも外需に支えられた。","romaji":"Konki no seichō wa, naiju yori mo gaiju ni sasaerareta.","german":"Growth in this period was supported more by external demand than by domestic demand.","furigana":"こんき|せいちょう|ないじゅ|がいじゅ|ささ"}],"notes":"外需 is demand from abroad, essentially for exports: \"foreign demand\". Typical: 外需依存, 外需頼み, 外需主導, 外需が落ち込む／伸びる; in GDP statistics 外需 denotes the contribution of net exports. The counterpart is 内需. Formal economic language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"金融危機の際に銀行の国有化が行われた。","romaji":"Kin'yū kiki no sai ni ginkō no kokuyūka ga okonawareta.","german":"Banks were nationalized during the financial crisis.","furigana":"きんゆうきき|さい|ぎんこう|こくゆうか|おこな"},{"japanese":"政府は経営破綻した電力会社を一時的に国有化する方針だ。","romaji":"Seifu wa keiei hatan shita denryoku gaisha o ichijiteki ni kokuyūka suru hōshin da.","german":"The government plans to temporarily nationalize the bankrupt power company.","furigana":"せいふ|けいえいはたん|でんりょくがいしゃ|いちじてき|こくゆうか|ほうしん"},{"japanese":"その国では、石油産業が国有化された後、再び民営化された。","romaji":"Sono kuni de wa, sekiyu sangyō ga kokuyūka sareta ato, futatabi min'eika sareta.","german":"In that country, the oil industry was first nationalized and later privatized again.","furigana":"くに|せきゆさんぎょう|こくゆうか|あと|ふたた|みんえいか"}],"notes":"国有化 is the transfer of companies, land or resources into state ownership: \"nationalization\". As a transitive する-verb: 銀行を国有化する, passive 国有化される. The opposite is 民営化 \"privatization\". 国営 means \"state-run\" (国営企業), 国有 \"state-owned\" (国有地). Formal language from politics and economics.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"規制緩和が新たなビジネスチャンスを生み出した。","romaji":"Kisei kanwa ga arata na bijinesu chansu wo umidashita.","german":"Deregulation has created new business opportunities.","furigana":"きせいかんわ|あら|う|だ"},{"japanese":"政府は外国人観光客を増やすため、ビザの規制緩和を進めている。","romaji":"Seifu wa gaikokujin kankōkyaku o fuyasu tame, biza no kisei kanwa o susumete iru.","german":"To increase the number of foreign tourists, the government is easing visa regulations.","furigana":"せいふ|がいこくじんかんこうきゃく|ふ|きせいかんわ|すす"},{"japanese":"規制緩和で競争が激しくなり、航空運賃が安くなった。","romaji":"Kisei kanwa de kyōsō ga hageshiku nari, kōkū unchin ga yasuku natta.","german":"Deregulation intensified competition, and airfares fell.","furigana":"きせいかんわ|きょうそう|はげ|こうくううんちん|やす"}],"notes":"規制緩和 is composed of 規制 \"regulation\" and 緩和 \"easing\" and means the dismantling of government rules: \"deregulation\". Typical: 規制緩和を進める, 規制緩和によって, 規制緩和策; as a verb form one usually says 規制を緩和する. The opposite is 規制強化. A common buzzword in politics and economic news.","levelNote":"Compound: 規制 (N3) + 緩和 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"付加価値の高い製品を開発することが重要だ。","romaji":"Fukakachi no takai seihin wo kaihatsu suru koto ga jūyō da.","german":"It is important to develop products with high added value.","furigana":"ふかかち|たか|せいひん|かいはつ|じゅうよう"},{"japanese":"地元の果物をジャムに加工して、付加価値をつけて売っている。","romaji":"Jimoto no kudamono o jamu ni kakō shite, fukakachi o tsukete utte iru.","german":"We process local fruit into jam and sell it with added value.","furigana":"じもと|くだもの|かこう|ふかかち|う"},{"japanese":"ヨーロッパの多くの国では、付加価値税の税率が二十パーセント前後だ。","romaji":"Yōroppa no ōku no kuni de wa, fukakachizei no zeiritsu ga nijū pāsento zengo da.","german":"In many European countries, the value-added tax rate is around twenty percent.","furigana":"おお|くに|ふかかちぜい|ぜいりつ|にじゅう|ぜんご"}],"notes":"付加価値 is the value additionally added to a product or service: in economics \"value added\", in marketing \"added value\". Typical: 付加価値が高い, 高付加価値の製品, 付加価値を高める／つける. In tax terms: 付加価値税 \"value-added tax\"; in Japan the corresponding tax is called 消費税. Neutral, very common in business and advertising.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"景気循環の各局面に応じた政策が必要だ。","romaji":"Keiki junkan no kaku kyokumen ni ōjita seisaku ga hitsuyō da.","german":"Policies adapted to each phase of the business cycle are necessary.","furigana":"けいきじゅんかん|かくきょくめん|おう|せいさく|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"建設業は景気循環の影響を受けやすい。","romaji":"Kensetsugyō wa keiki junkan no eikyō o ukeyasui.","german":"The construction industry is easily affected by the business cycle.","furigana":"けんせつぎょう|けいきじゅんかん|えいきょう|う"},{"japanese":"景気循環から見ると、今は回復期に入ったと言える。","romaji":"Keiki junkan kara miru to, ima wa kaifukuki ni haitta to ieru.","german":"Seen from the business cycle, one can say that we have now entered the recovery phase.","furigana":"けいきじゅんかん|み|いま|かいふくき|はい|い"}],"notes":"景気循環 is the recurring alternation of upswing (好況) and downswing (不況) with phases such as 回復, 拡張 and 後退: \"business cycle\". Typical: 景気循環の局面, 景気循環の影響を受ける, 景気循環株 \"cyclical stocks\". 景気 alone is the current economic situation (景気がいい); 景気循環 emphasizes the ups and downs over years. Technical language of economics.","levelNote":"Compound: 景気 (N3) + 循環 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今年の経済成長率は二パーセントと予測されている。","romaji":"Kotoshi no keizai seichōritsu wa ni pāsento to yosoku sarete iru.","german":"This year's economic growth rate is forecast at two percent.","furigana":"ことし|けいざいせいちょうりつ|に|よそく"},{"japanese":"高度経済成長期の日本では、経済成長率が年十パーセントを超えることもあった。","romaji":"Kōdo keizai seichōki no Nihon de wa, keizai seichōritsu ga nen jū pāsento o koeru koto mo atta.","german":"During Japan's period of high economic growth, the growth rate sometimes exceeded ten percent a year.","furigana":"こうどけいざいせいちょうき|にほん|けいざいせいちょうりつ|ねんじゅう|こ"},{"japanese":"新興国の経済成長率は、先進国より高い傾向がある。","romaji":"Shinkōkoku no keizai seichōritsu wa, senshinkoku yori takai keikō ga aru.","german":"The economic growth rate of emerging countries tends to be higher than that of developed countries.","furigana":"しんこうこく|けいざいせいちょうりつ|せんしんこく|たか|けいこう"}],"notes":"経済成長率 is the percentage change in economic output, usually GDP compared with the previous year; one distinguishes 実質経済成長率 \"real\" and 名目経済成長率 \"nominal\". Typical: 経済成長率が鈍化する／プラスに転じる／マイナスになる, 経済成長率の見通しを上方修正／下方修正する. In context, the short 成長率 is often enough. News and technical language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"国内総生産は前年比で微増にとどまった。","romaji":"Kokunai sōseisan wa zennenhi de bizō ni todomatta.","german":"Gross domestic product rose only slightly compared with the previous year.","furigana":"こくないそうせいさん|ぜんねんひ|びぞう"},{"japanese":"国内総生産は、一定期間に国内で生み出された付加価値の合計である。","romaji":"Kokunai sōseisan wa, ittei kikan ni kokunai de umidasareta fukakachi no gōkei de aru.","german":"Gross domestic product is the total of the value added generated domestically in a given period.","furigana":"こくないそうせいさん|いっていきかん|こくない|う|だ|ふかかち|ごうけい"},{"japanese":"この島国では、観光業が国内総生産の半分近くを占めている。","romaji":"Kono shimaguni de wa, kankōgyō ga kokunai sōseisan no hanbun chikaku o shimete iru.","german":"In this island nation, tourism accounts for nearly half of gross domestic product.","furigana":"しまぐに|かんこうぎょう|こくないそうせいさん|はんぶんちか|し"}],"notes":"国内総生産 is the official term for gross domestic product, that is, the value of all goods and services produced domestically. In news and conversation people almost always say GDP; one distinguishes 実質 \"real\" and 名目 \"nominal\". To be distinguished from it are 国民総生産 (GNP) and 国民総所得 (GNI), which measure the income of residents. Formal, rather written technical language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"長期的な経営戦略を策定する必要がある。","romaji":"Chōkiteki na keiei senryaku wo sakutei suru hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"It is necessary to formulate a long-term business strategy.","furigana":"ちょうきてき|けいえいせんりゃく|さくてい|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"新社長は就任早々、経営戦略を大きく見直した。","romaji":"Shin shachō wa shūnin sōsō, keiei senryaku o ōkiku minaoshita.","german":"The new president thoroughly revised the business strategy right after taking office.","furigana":"しんしゃちょう|しゅうにんそうそう|けいえいせんりゃく|おお|みなお"},{"japanese":"大学院で経営戦略を専攻し、今はコンサルタントとして働いている。","romaji":"Daigakuin de keiei senryaku o senkō shi, ima wa konsarutanto to shite hataraite iru.","german":"I majored in business strategy in graduate school and now work as a consultant.","furigana":"だいがくいん|けいえいせんりゃく|せんこう|いま|はたら"}],"notes":"経営戦略 is the long-term strategy with which a company wants to reach its goals and hold its own in competition. Typical: 経営戦略を立てる／策定する／見直す. 経営 means \"business management\"; related are 経営方針 \"management policy\" and 経営計画 \"concrete business plan with figures\". Also used as a field of study and consulting. Business language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"事業の多角化によりリスクを分散させる。","romaji":"Jigyō no takakuka ni yori risuku wo bunsan saseru.","german":"Spread risk through diversification of the business.","furigana":"じぎょう|たかくか|ぶんさん"},{"japanese":"その鉄道会社は、不動産や百貨店などへと事業を多角化してきた。","romaji":"Sono tetsudō gaisha wa, fudōsan ya hyakkaten nado e to jigyō o takakuka shite kita.","german":"That railway company has diversified its business into real estate, department stores and more.","furigana":"てつどうがいしゃ|ふどうさん|ひゃっかてん|じぎょう|たかくか"},{"japanese":"多角化を急ぎすぎて本業がおろそかになり、業績が悪化した。","romaji":"Takakuka o isogisugite hongyō ga orosoka ni nari, gyōseki ga akka shita.","german":"Diversification was pushed too hastily, the core business was neglected, and results worsened.","furigana":"たかくか|いそ|ほんぎょう|ぎょうせき|あっか"}],"notes":"多角化 means that a company moves into new business fields: \"diversification\". Typical: 事業の多角化, 多角化経営, 多角化を進める／図る; as a する-verb 事業を多角化する. To be distinguished from it is 多様化 \"variety, diversification\" in general (価値観の多様化); spreading financial investments is called 分散投資. Business and news language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"業界再編の動きが加速している。","romaji":"Gyōkai saihen no ugoki ga kasoku shite iru.","german":"Moves toward industry restructuring are accelerating.","furigana":"ぎょうかいさいへん|うご|かそく"},{"japanese":"少子化に伴い、市内の小学校が再編されることになった。","romaji":"Shōshika ni tomonai, shinai no shōgakkō ga saihen sareru koto ni natta.","german":"Due to the declining birthrate, the city's elementary schools will be reorganized.","furigana":"しょうしか|ともな|しない|しょうがっこう|さいへん"},{"japanese":"新しい部長は、営業部を三つのチームに再編した。","romaji":"Atarashii buchō wa, eigyōbu o mittsu no chīmu ni saihen shita.","german":"The new department head reorganized the sales department into three teams.","furigana":"あたら|ぶちょう|えいぎょうぶ|みっ|さいへん"}],"notes":"再編 is the reorganization of organizations, industries or systems: \"restructuring, reorganization\". As a transitive する-verb: 組織を再編する, passive 再編される. Typical: 業界再編, 組織再編, 政界再編, 再編が進む. Unlike リストラ, which mainly means job cuts, 再編 emphasizes putting units together anew, for example through mergers or consolidations.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"事業部門の分社化により経営効率を高める。","romaji":"Jigyō bumon no bunshaka ni yori keiei kōritsu wo takameru.","german":"Improve management efficiency by spinning off business divisions into separate companies.","furigana":"じぎょうぶもん|ぶんしゃか|けいえいこうりつ|たか"},{"japanese":"同社は来年四月にゲーム事業を分社化すると発表した。","romaji":"Dōsha wa rainen shigatsu ni gēmu jigyō o bunshaka suru to happyō shita.","german":"The company announced that it will spin off its game business next April.","furigana":"どうしゃ|らいねんしがつ|じぎょう|ぶんしゃか|はっぴょう"},{"japanese":"分社化された後も、社員の給料や待遇は変わらなかった。","romaji":"Bunshaka sareta ato mo, shain no kyūryō ya taigū wa kawaranakatta.","german":"Even after the spin-off, employees' salaries and treatment remained unchanged.","furigana":"ぶんしゃか|あと|しゃいん|きゅうりょう|たいぐう|か"}],"notes":"分社化 means separating a business division from the company and continuing it as an independent company, usually as a subsidiary: a \"spin-off\". As a する-verb: ゲーム事業を分社化する, passive 分社化される. To be distinguished from it is 子会社化, in which an existing firm is made a subsidiary through acquisition; 会社分割 is the legal technical term for the split. Business and news language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大規模なリストラにより数千人が解雇された。","romaji":"Daikibo na risutora ni yori sūsennin ga kaiko sareta.","german":"Thousands were laid off in a large-scale restructuring.","furigana":"だいきぼ|すうせんにん|かいこ"},{"japanese":"父は五十歳のときにリストラされ、転職を余儀なくされた。","romaji":"Chichi wa gojussai no toki ni risutora sare, tenshoku o yoginaku sareta.","german":"My father was laid off at fifty in a round of job cuts and was forced to change jobs.","furigana":"ちち|ごじゅっさい|てんしょく|よぎ"},{"japanese":"リストラの噂が広がって、社内の雰囲気が暗くなっている。","romaji":"Risutora no uwasa ga hirogatte, shanai no fun'iki ga kuraku natte iru.","german":"As rumors of layoffs spread, the mood inside the company has darkened.","furigana":"うわさ|ひろ|しゃない|ふんいき|くら"}],"notes":"リストラ is the short form of リストラクチャリング \"restructuring\", but in Japanese it mainly means job cuts and layoffs. The firm リストラする (社員をリストラする); those affected are リストラされる or リストラにあう. It sounds softer and more everyday than 解雇 \"dismissal\" or, colloquially, クビ. For neutral reorganization without job cuts, one rather says 再編 or 構造改革.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"業務の合理化により大幅なコスト削減を達成した。","romaji":"Gyōmu no gōrika ni yori ōhaba na kosuto sakugen wo tassei shita.","german":"Significant cost reductions were achieved by streamlining operations.","furigana":"ぎょうむ|ごうりか|おおはば|さくげん|たっせい"},{"japanese":"書類の手続きを電子化して、事務作業を合理化した。","romaji":"Shorui no tetsuzuki o denshika shite, jimu sagyō o gōrika shita.","german":"We digitized paper procedures and streamlined the office work.","furigana":"しょるい|てつづ|でんしか|じむさぎょう|ごうりか"},{"japanese":"彼は自分の失敗を「運が悪かった」と合理化しようとした。","romaji":"Kare wa jibun no shippai o un ga warukatta to gōrika shiyō to shita.","german":"He tried to rationalize his failure as \"just bad luck\".","furigana":"かれ|じぶん|しっぱい|うん|わる|ごうりか"}],"notes":"合理化 means, in a business context, making processes more efficient and cheaper: \"rationalization\"; transitive する-verb: 業務を合理化する. Typical: 業務の合理化, 合理化を進める／図る, 合理化策; in the past often associated with job cuts. 効率化 \"increasing efficiency\" is very similar but more neutral. In psychology, 合理化 also means \"to talk oneself into something, to justify\" (自分の失敗を合理化する).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"産業構造の転換が進んでいる。","romaji":"Sangyō kōzō no tenkan ga susunde iru.","german":"The transformation of the industrial structure is progressing.","furigana":"さんぎょうこうぞう|てんかん|すす"},{"japanese":"この町は、炭鉱の閉山をきっかけに産業構造が大きく変わった。","romaji":"Kono machi wa, tankō no heizan o kikkake ni sangyō kōzō ga ōkiku kawatta.","german":"The industrial structure of this town changed greatly after the coal mine closed.","furigana":"まち|たんこう|へいざん|さんぎょうこうぞう|おお|か"},{"japanese":"日本の産業構造は、製造業中心からサービス業中心へと移ってきた。","romaji":"Nihon no sangyō kōzō wa, seizōgyō chūshin kara sābisugyō chūshin e to utsutte kita.","german":"Japan's industrial structure has shifted from being manufacturing-centered to service-centered.","furigana":"にほん|さんぎょうこうぞう|せいぞうぎょうちゅうしん|ぎょうちゅうしん|うつ"}],"notes":"産業構造 denotes the composition of an economy by its sectors, such as 第一次産業 (agriculture, fishing), 第二次産業 (industry) and 第三次産業 (services). Typical: 産業構造の転換／変化, 産業構造が変わる, 産業構造の高度化. In reference to a region one also speaks of 地域の産業構造. Technical language from economics and politics.","levelNote":"Compound: 産業 (N4) + 構造 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自動車産業は日本の基幹産業の一つだ。","romaji":"Jidōsha sangyō wa Nihon no kikan sangyō no hitotsu da.","german":"The automobile industry is one of Japan's key industries.","furigana":"じどうしゃさんぎょう|にほん|きかんさんぎょう|ひと"},{"japanese":"この地域では、農業と漁業が昔から基幹産業となっている。","romaji":"Kono chiiki de wa, nōgyō to gyogyō ga mukashi kara kikan sangyō to natte iru.","german":"In this region, agriculture and fishing have long been the key industries.","furigana":"ちいき|のうぎょう|ぎょぎょう|むかし|きかんさんぎょう"},{"japanese":"基幹産業である鉄鋼業の不振が、町全体の経済に影響している。","romaji":"Kikan sangyō de aru tekkōgyō no fushin ga, machi zentai no keizai ni eikyō shite iru.","german":"The slump in the steel industry, the key industry, is affecting the economy of the whole town.","furigana":"きかんさんぎょう|てっこうぎょう|ふしん|まちぜんたい|けいざい|えいきょう"}],"notes":"基幹産業 is an industry that forms the backbone of a national or regional economy: a \"key industry\". 基幹 means \"mainstay, core\" as in 基幹システム or 基幹病院. Typical: 〜の基幹産業, 地域の基幹産業, 基幹産業として発展する. 主要産業 \"major industry\" is similar but weaker; 成長産業 and 新興産業 are industries of the future. Formal language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"斜陽産業からの転換を模索する企業が増えている。","romaji":"Shayō sangyō kara no tenkan wo mosaku suru kigyō ga fuete iru.","german":"More and more companies are looking to move away from declining industries.","furigana":"しゃようさんぎょう|てんかん|もさく|きぎょう|ふ"},{"japanese":"かつての石炭産業は、エネルギー転換で斜陽産業となった。","romaji":"Katsute no sekitan sangyō wa, enerugī tenkan de shayō sangyō to natta.","german":"The once-thriving coal industry became a declining industry with the energy transition.","furigana":"せきたんさんぎょう|てんかん|しゃようさんぎょう"},{"japanese":"斜陽産業と言われる業界でも、工夫次第で生き残ることはできる。","romaji":"Shayō sangyō to iwareru gyōkai demo, kufū shidai de ikinokoru koto wa dekiru.","german":"Even in an industry said to be in decline, survival is possible with ingenuity.","furigana":"しゃようさんぎょう|い|ぎょうかい|くふうしだい|い|のこ"}],"notes":"斜陽産業 literally means \"industry of the setting sun\" (斜陽 \"evening sun\") and denotes an industry in decline: a \"shrinking industry\". The image was popularized by the novel 『斜陽』 by 太宰治; also 斜陽化 \"decline\". The opposites are 成長産業 and 新興産業. The expression is judgmental and can sound disparaging to those affected; news and business language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"IT関連の新興産業が雇用創出に貢献している。","romaji":"IT kanren no shinkō sangyō ga koyō sōshutsu ni kōken shite iru.","german":"Emerging IT-related industries are contributing to job creation.","furigana":"かんれん|しんこうさんぎょう|こようそうしゅつ|こうけん"},{"japanese":"政府は新興産業の育成に力を入れている。","romaji":"Seifu wa shinkō sangyō no ikusei ni chikara o irete iru.","german":"The government is putting effort into nurturing emerging industries.","furigana":"せいふ|しんこうさんぎょう|いくせい|ちから|い"},{"japanese":"宇宙ビジネスは、今最も注目されている新興産業の一つだ。","romaji":"Uchū bijinesu wa, ima mottomo chūmoku sarete iru shinkō sangyō no hitotsu da.","german":"The space business is one of the most closely watched emerging industries right now.","furigana":"うちゅう|いまもっと|ちゅうもく|しんこうさんぎょう|ひと"}],"notes":"新興産業 are newly emerging, rapidly growing industries: an \"emerging industry\". 新興 means \"newly arising, emerging\" as in 新興国 \"emerging country\", 新興企業 \"young company, start-up\" or 新興住宅地 \"new housing development\". Typical: 新興産業の育成, 新興産業が台頭する. Closely related is 成長産業; the opposite is 斜陽産業. Formal economic language.","levelNote":"Compound: 新興 (N1) + 産業 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"系列関係の見直しが進んでいる。","romaji":"Keiretsu kankei no minaoshi ga susunde iru.","german":"The review of keiretsu relationships is progressing.","furigana":"けいれつかんけい|みなお|すす"},{"japanese":"このレストランはあのホテルの系列だから、サービスがいい。","romaji":"Kono resutoran wa ano hoteru no keiretsu da kara, sābisu ga ii.","german":"This restaurant is affiliated with that hotel, so the service is good.","furigana":"けいれつ"},{"japanese":"データを時系列に並べると、変化がよく分かる。","romaji":"Dēta o jikeiretsu ni naraberu to, henka ga yoku wakaru.","german":"If you arrange the data in time series, you can see the changes well.","furigana":"じけいれつ|なら|へんか|わ"}],"notes":"系列 generally means \"series, sequence, related line\" (時系列 \"time series\"). In a business context it denotes the Japanese keiretsu: corporate groups linked by mutual shareholdings and fixed supplier relationships, for example a car maker and its suppliers. Very common in 系列会社, 系列店 \"affiliated branch\" and with television stations (〜系列の局 \"station of the ... network\"). Neutral; compare 関連会社 and グループ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コンプライアンス体制の強化が企業に求められている。","romaji":"Konpuraiansu taisei no kyōka ga kigyō ni motomerarete iru.","german":"Companies are required to strengthen their compliance systems.","furigana":"たいせい|きょうか|きぎょう|もと"},{"japanese":"取引先のコンプライアンス違反が発覚し、契約を打ち切った。","romaji":"Torihikisaki no konpuraiansu ihan ga hakkaku shi, keiyaku o uchikitta.","german":"A compliance violation by a business partner came to light, and we terminated the contract.","furigana":"とりひきさき|いはん|はっかく|けいやく|う|き"},{"japanese":"最近のテレビはコンプライアンスが厳しくて、昔のような番組は作れない。","romaji":"Saikin no terebi wa konpuraiansu ga kibishikute, mukashi no yō na bangumi wa tsukurenai.","german":"Compliance is so strict on TV these days that you can't make programs like the old ones.","furigana":"さいきん|きび|むかし|ばんぐみ|つく"}],"notes":"コンプライアンス in Japanese companies mainly means 法令遵守 \"observance of laws\", in a wider sense also of rules, ethics and social norms. Typical combinations: コンプライアンスを徹底する \"to enforce compliance thoroughly\", コンプライアンス違反 \"compliance violation\", コンプライアンス研修, コンプライアンス意識. Colloquially it is shortened to コンプラ, for example when saying that television has to be more careful nowadays.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コーポレートガバナンスの改善が株価に好影響を与えた。","romaji":"Kōporēto gabanansu no kaizen ga kabuka ni kōeikyō wo ataeta.","german":"The improvement of corporate governance had a positive effect on the share price.","furigana":"かいぜん|かぶか|こうえいきょう|あた"},{"japanese":"不祥事が続き、会社のガバナンスが機能していないと批判された。","romaji":"Fushōji ga tsuzuki, kaisha no gabanansu ga kinō shite inai to hihan sareta.","german":"After a series of scandals, the company was criticized for governance that is not functioning.","furigana":"ふしょうじ|つづ|かいしゃ|きのう|ひはん"},{"japanese":"国立大学でもガバナンス改革が進められている。","romaji":"Kokuritsu daigaku demo gabanansu kaikaku ga susumerarete iru.","german":"Governance reforms are also being pushed forward at national universities.","furigana":"こくりつだいがく|かいかく|すす"}],"notes":"ガバナンス denotes the steering and control of an organization, not operational management (for that, 経営). It most often appears in コーポレートガバナンス, in Japanese 企業統治. Typical: ガバナンスを強化する \"to strengthen governance\", ガバナンスが効いている／機能している \"oversight is working\", ガバナンスの欠如 \"lack of oversight\". It is also applied to states, universities or IT (ITガバナンス).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"輸出の増加がGDP成長に大きく寄与した。","romaji":"Yushutsu no zōka ga GDP seichō ni ōkiku kiyo shita.","german":"The increase in exports contributed greatly to GDP growth.","furigana":"ゆしゅつ|ぞうか|せいちょう|おお|きよ"},{"japanese":"この研究は新しい治療法の確立に寄与した。","romaji":"Kono kenkyū wa atarashii chiryōhō no kakuritsu ni kiyo shita.","german":"This study contributed to the establishment of a new treatment.","furigana":"けんきゅう|あたら|ちりょうほう|かくりつ|きよ"},{"japanese":"地域の発展に寄与できる人材を育てたい。","romaji":"Chiiki no hatten ni kiyo dekiru jinzai o sodatetai.","german":"I want to train people who can contribute to the development of the region.","furigana":"ちいき|はってん|きよ|じんざい|そだ"}],"notes":"寄与 means \"contribution\" and is almost always used as an intransitive する-verb with に: XがYに寄与する \"X contributes to Y\". It sounds literary and often appears with causes and factors (exports, research, measures). 貢献 is very similar but rather emphasizes the meritorious effort of people (社会に貢献する); in everyday speech one simply says 役に立つ. In statistics, 寄与度 is the \"contribution to growth\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"公共投資の乗数効果が低下している。","romaji":"Kōkyō tōshi no jōsū kōka ga teika shite iru.","german":"The multiplier effect of public investment is declining.","furigana":"こうきょうとうし|じょうすうこうか|ていか"},{"japanese":"減税よりも公共事業のほうが乗数効果が大きいと主張する経済学者もいる。","romaji":"Genzei yori mo kōkyō jigyō no hō ga jōsū kōka ga ōkii to shuchō suru keizaigakusha mo iru.","german":"Some economists claim that public works have a larger multiplier effect than tax cuts.","furigana":"げんぜい|こうきょうじぎょう|じょうすうこうか|おお|しゅちょう|けいざいがくしゃ"},{"japanese":"観光客の消費は乗数効果によって地域経済全体に波及する。","romaji":"Kankōkyaku no shōhi wa jōsū kōka ni yotte chiiki keizai zentai ni hakyū suru.","german":"Tourists' spending spreads through the entire regional economy via the multiplier effect.","furigana":"かんこうきゃく|しょうひ|じょうすうこうか|ちいきけいざいぜんたい|はきゅう"}],"notes":"乗数効果 (乗数 \"multiplier\" + 効果 \"effect\") is a concept of macroeconomics: additional spending, such as government investment, raises national income by a multiple. Typical are 乗数効果が大きい／小さい, 乗数効果が低下する and 財政支出の乗数効果. Do not confuse it with 相乗効果 \"synergy effect\", which is also used in everyday speech for things that reinforce each other.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"可処分所得の増加が消費拡大につながる。","romaji":"Kashobun shotoku no zōka ga shōhi kakudai ni tsunagaru.","german":"An increase in disposable income leads to expanded consumption.","furigana":"かしょぶんしょとく|ぞうか|しょうひかくだい"},{"japanese":"社会保険料の負担増で、若い世帯の可処分所得が減っている。","romaji":"Shakai hokenryō no futanzō de, wakai setai no kashobun shotoku ga hette iru.","german":"Due to higher social insurance burdens, the disposable income of young households is decreasing.","furigana":"しゃかいほけんりょう|ふたんぞう|わか|せたい|かしょぶんしょとく|へ"},{"japanese":"給料が上がっても物価も上がれば、実質的な可処分所得は増えない。","romaji":"Kyūryō ga agatte mo bukka mo agareba, jisshitsuteki na kashobun shotoku wa fuenai.","german":"Even if salaries rise, if prices rise too, real disposable income does not increase.","furigana":"きゅうりょう|あ|ぶっか|あ|じっしつてき|かしょぶんしょとく|ふ"}],"notes":"可処分所得 literally means \"income that one can dispose of\" (可処分 \"disposable\"): income after deduction of taxes and social contributions. It is a technical word from statistics and economic policy; in everyday speech one talks of 手取り \"take-home pay\". Typical: 可処分所得が増える／減る／伸び悩む, 世帯の可処分所得, 実質可処分所得 \"real disposable income\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新興国の購買力が急速に高まっている。","romaji":"Shinkōkoku no kōbairyoku ga kyūsoku ni takamatte iru.","german":"The purchasing power of emerging countries is rising rapidly.","furigana":"しんこうこく|こうばいりょく|きゅうそく|たか"},{"japanese":"円安で日本人の海外での購買力が落ちた。","romaji":"En'yasu de Nihonjin no kaigai de no kōbairyoku ga ochita.","german":"Because of the weak yen, the purchasing power of Japanese people abroad has fallen.","furigana":"えんやす|にほんじん|かいがい|こうばいりょく|お"},{"japanese":"購買力平価で比べると、両国の生活水準の差はそれほど大きくない。","romaji":"Kōbairyoku heika de kuraberu to, ryōkoku no seikatsu suijun no sa wa sorehodo ōkiku nai.","german":"Compared by purchasing power parity, the difference in living standards between the two countries is not so large.","furigana":"こうばいりょくへいか|くら|りょうこく|せいかつすいじゅん|さ|おお"}],"notes":"購買力 is the \"purchasing power\" of individuals, households or whole countries; 購買 is the formal word for \"purchase\". Typical: 購買力が高い／低い, 購買力が高まる／落ちる／低下する. The technical term 購買力平価 means \"purchasing power parity\". Do not confuse it with 購買意欲 \"desire to buy\": 購買力 is the ability, 購買意欲 the willingness.","levelNote":"Compound: 購買 (N1) + 力 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"需要の価格弾力性を分析して価格設定を行う。","romaji":"Juyō no kakaku danryokusei wo bunseki shite kakaku settei wo okonau.","german":"Pricing is done by analyzing the price elasticity of demand.","furigana":"じゅよう|かかくだんりょくせい|ぶんせき|かかくせってい|おこな"},{"japanese":"予算の運用に弾力性を持たせる。","romaji":"Yosan no un'yō ni danryokusei o motaseru.","german":"Build flexibility into the use of the budget.","furigana":"よさん|うんよう|だんりょくせい|も"},{"japanese":"この素材は弾力性に優れ、長時間座っても疲れにくい。","romaji":"Kono sozai wa danryokusei ni sugure, chōjikan suwatte mo tsukarenikui.","german":"This material has excellent elasticity, so you hardly tire even when sitting for a long time.","furigana":"そざい|だんりょくせい|すぐ|ちょうじかんすわ|つか"}],"notes":"弾力性 is \"elasticity\" as a property: physically in materials, in economics as 価格弾力性 \"price elasticity\" or 所得弾力性, e.g. 需要の弾力性が大きい／小さい. Figuratively it means \"leeway, flexibility\" of rules or budgets (弾力性を持たせる); the adjective 弾力的 (弾力的に運用する) is more common for this. For mental flexibility one rather says 柔軟性.","levelNote":"Compound: 弾力 (N1) + 性 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"国際競争力を強化するために技術革新を推進する。","romaji":"Kokusai kyōsōryoku wo kyōka suru tame ni gijutsu kakushin wo suishin suru.","german":"Promote technological innovation to strengthen international competitiveness.","furigana":"こくさいきょうそうりょく|きょうか|ぎじゅつかくしん|すいしん"},{"japanese":"円高で輸出企業の価格競争力が低下した。","romaji":"Endaka de yushutsu kigyō no kakaku kyōsōryoku ga teika shita.","german":"The strong yen reduced the price competitiveness of export companies.","furigana":"えんだか|ゆしゅつきぎょう|かかくきょうそうりょく|ていか"},{"japanese":"地方の大学が生き残るには、独自の競争力が必要だ。","romaji":"Chihō no daigaku ga ikinokoru ni wa, dokuji no kyōsōryoku ga hitsuyō da.","german":"For regional universities to survive, they need their own competitive strengths.","furigana":"ちほう|だいがく|い|のこ|どくじ|きょうそうりょく|ひつよう"}],"notes":"競争力 is the \"competitiveness\" of companies, products, countries or institutions. Typical combinations: 競争力がある／ない, 競争力を高める／強化する／失う, 競争力が低下する. Common compounds are 国際競争力 \"international competitiveness\" and 価格競争力 \"price competitiveness\"; attributively one says 競争力のある製品 \"a competitive product\".","levelNote":"Compound: 競争 (N4) + 力 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"過当競争により利益率が低下している。","romaji":"Katō kyōsō ni yori riekiritsu ga teika shite iru.","german":"Profit margins are falling because of excessive competition.","furigana":"かとうきょうそう|りえきりつ|ていか"},{"japanese":"コンビニ業界は過当競争に陥っている。","romaji":"Konbini gyōkai wa katō kyōsō ni ochiitte iru.","german":"The convenience store industry has fallen into cutthroat competition.","furigana":"ぎょうかい|かとうきょうそう|おちい"},{"japanese":"値下げ合戦による過当競争で、多くの店が閉店に追い込まれた。","romaji":"Nesage gassen ni yoru katō kyōsō de, ōku no mise ga heiten ni oikomareta.","german":"Many shops were driven to close by cutthroat competition in a price-cutting war.","furigana":"ねさ|がっせん|かとうきょうそう|おお|みせ|へいてん|お|こ"}],"notes":"過当競争 is \"excessive, ruinous competition\" in which too many suppliers fight over the same market until hardly any of them make a profit. 過当 means \"beyond the appropriate measure\". Typical combinations are 過当競争に陥る, 過当競争が激化する and 過当競争を招く. The neutral 競争激化 only describes tougher competition, without the negative judgment \"harmful\". Business language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"価格競争に巻き込まれて収益が悪化した。","romaji":"Kakaku kyōsō ni makikomarete shūeki ga akka shita.","german":"Earnings worsened after being drawn into price competition.","furigana":"かかくきょうそう|ま|こ|しゅうえき|あっか"},{"japanese":"コンビニ業界では弁当の価格競争が激しくなっている。","romaji":"Konbini gyōkai de wa bentō no kakaku kyōsō ga hageshiku natte iru.","german":"Price competition over lunch boxes is intensifying in the convenience store industry.","furigana":"ぎょうかい|べんとう|かかくきょうそう|はげ"},{"japanese":"当社は価格競争を避け、品質で勝負する方針だ。","romaji":"Tōsha wa kakaku kyōsō o sake, hinshitsu de shōbu suru hōshin da.","german":"Our company avoids price competition and aims to compete on quality.","furigana":"とうしゃ|かかくきょうそう|さ|ひんしつ|しょうぶ|ほうしん"}],"notes":"価格競争 is competition on price, often in the sense of a \"price war\". Typical: 価格競争が激しい／激化する, 価格競争に巻き込まれる \"to be drawn into a price war\", 価格競争に陥る, 価格競争を避ける. More colloquial are 値下げ競争 and 安売り競争. Do not confuse it with 価格競争力 \"price competitiveness\".","levelNote":"Compound: 価格 (N3) + 競争 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"市場占有率を拡大するための販売戦略を練る。","romaji":"Shijō sen'yūritsu wo kakudai suru tame no hanbai senryaku wo neru.","german":"Work out a sales strategy to expand market share.","furigana":"しじょうせんゆうりつ|かくだい|はんばいせんりゃく|ね"},{"japanese":"同社は国内で約四割の市場占有率を誇る。","romaji":"Dōsha wa kokunai de yaku yonwari no shijō sen'yūritsu o hokoru.","german":"The company boasts a domestic market share of about 40 percent.","furigana":"どうしゃ|こくない|やくよんわり|しじょうせんゆうりつ|ほこ"},{"japanese":"海外メーカーの参入で、市場占有率が年々低下している。","romaji":"Kaigai mēkā no sannyū de, shijō sen'yūritsu ga nennen teika shite iru.","german":"Market share is falling year by year because of the entry of foreign manufacturers.","furigana":"かいがい|さんにゅう|しじょうせんゆうりつ|ねんねんていか"}],"notes":"市場占有率 is the formal expression for \"market share\"; 市場 is read しじょう here (market in the economic sense), not いちば \"marketplace\". In the media and in companies, the loanword シェア (市場シェア) is now more common. Typical: 市場占有率が高い, 市場占有率を拡大する／伸ばす, 〜%の市場占有率を誇る \"to boast a market share of ...\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"参入障壁が高いため新規企業が少ない。","romaji":"Sannyū shōheki ga takai tame shinki kigyō ga sukunai.","german":"There are few new companies because barriers to entry are high.","furigana":"さんにゅうしょうへき|たか|しんききぎょう|すく"},{"japanese":"規制緩和によって参入障壁が下がり、多くのベンチャー企業が生まれた。","romaji":"Kisei kanwa ni yotte sannyū shōheki ga sagari, ōku no benchā kigyō ga umareta.","german":"Deregulation lowered barriers to entry, and many start-ups were born.","furigana":"きせいかんわ|さんにゅうしょうへき|さ|おお|きぎょう|う"},{"japanese":"大手は特許を武器に参入障壁を築いている。","romaji":"Ōte wa tokkyo o buki ni sannyū shōheki o kizuite iru.","german":"The major companies use their patents as a weapon to build barriers to entry.","furigana":"おおて|とっきょ|ぶき|さんにゅうしょうへき|きず"}],"notes":"参入障壁 is composed of 参入 \"market entry\" and 障壁 \"barrier\" and denotes everything that makes it harder for new suppliers to enter: high initial investment, licenses, patents or strong brands. Typical: 参入障壁が高い／低い, 参入障壁を築く／設ける, 参入障壁を下げる／取り除く. Related is 新規参入 \"new entrant, market entry\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"規模の経済を活かしてコスト削減を図る。","romaji":"Kibo no keizai wo ikashite kosuto sakugen wo hakaru.","german":"Make use of economies of scale to reduce costs.","furigana":"きぼ|けいざい|い|さくげん|はか"},{"japanese":"生産量が増えるほど規模の経済が働き、一個当たりのコストが下がる。","romaji":"Seisanryō ga fueru hodo kibo no keizai ga hataraki, ikko atari no kosuto ga sagaru.","german":"The larger the production volume, the more economies of scale come into play and the lower the cost per unit.","furigana":"せいさんりょう|ふ|きぼ|けいざい|はたら|いっこあ|さ"},{"japanese":"二つの銀行は規模の経済を求めて合併した。","romaji":"Futatsu no ginkō wa kibo no keizai o motomete gappei shita.","german":"The two banks merged in pursuit of economies of scale.","furigana":"ふた|ぎんこう|きぼ|けいざい|もと|がっぺい"}],"notes":"規模の経済 translates \"economies of scale\": as production volume grows, unit costs fall. 経済 here means \"economy, saving\", not \"the economy\". Typical: 規模の経済が働く \"economies of scale take effect\", 規模の経済を活かす／追求する. In business language one often says the pseudo-loanword スケールメリット; the counterpart in theory is 範囲の経済 \"economies of scope\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"産業集積による外部経済の効果が認められる。","romaji":"Sangyō shūseki ni yoru gaibu keizai no kōka ga mitomerareru.","german":"The effects of positive externalities from industrial clustering are recognized.","furigana":"さんぎょうしゅうせき|がいぶけいざい|こうか|みと"},{"japanese":"駅ができると周辺の地価が上がるのは、外部経済の典型的な例だ。","romaji":"Eki ga dekiru to shūhen no chika ga agaru no wa, gaibu keizai no tenkeiteki na rei da.","german":"That land prices around a station rise when it is built is a typical example of a positive externality.","furigana":"えき|しゅうへん|ちか|あ|がいぶけいざい|てんけいてき|れい"},{"japanese":"工場の排煙による公害は外部経済ではなく、外部不経済と呼ばれる。","romaji":"Kōjō no haien ni yoru kōgai wa gaibu keizai de wa naku, gaibu fukeizai to yobareru.","german":"Pollution from factory smoke is not called a positive externality but a negative externality.","furigana":"こうじょう|はいえん|こうがい|がいぶけいざい|がいぶふけいざい|よ"}],"notes":"外部経済 is a technical term of economics: a positive side effect of economic activity that benefits third parties without their paying for it (e.g. rising land values because of a new station). The opposite, that is, negative effects such as environmental pollution, is called 外部不経済. The neutral umbrella term for both is 外部性 or 外部効果.","levelNote":"Compound: 外部 (N2) + 経済 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"各国の経済水準が収斂する傾向にある。","romaji":"Kakkoku no keizai suijun ga shūren suru keikō ni aru.","german":"The economic levels of the individual countries tend to converge.","furigana":"かっこく|けいざいすいじゅん|しゅうれん|けいこう"},{"japanese":"議論は最終的に二つの案に収斂した。","romaji":"Giron wa saishūteki ni futatsu no an ni shūren shita.","german":"The discussion eventually converged on two proposals.","furigana":"ぎろん|さいしゅうてき|ふた|あん|しゅうれん"},{"japanese":"イルカと魚の体形が似ているのは、収斂進化の一例だ。","romaji":"Iruka to sakana no taikei ga nite iru no wa, shūren shinka no ichirei da.","german":"The similar body shapes of dolphins and fish are an example of convergent evolution.","furigana":"さかな|たいけい|に|しゅうれんしんか|いちれい"}],"notes":"収斂 is a literary word for \"convergence, coming together\", often also written 収れん. As an intransitive する-verb it takes に: Yに収斂する \"to converge on Y, to settle on Y\", e.g. for economic data, opinions or in biology (収斂進化 \"convergent evolution\"). The close synonym 収束 is more common in mathematics and everyday speech and also means \"subsiding, containment\" (感染の収束). In addition, 収斂 means \"astringent\" as in 収れん化粧水.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"所得の再分配機能を強化すべきだ。","romaji":"Shotoku no saibunpai kinō wo kyōka subeki da.","german":"The income redistribution function should be strengthened.","furigana":"しょとく|さいぶんぱいきのう|きょうか"},{"japanese":"税と社会保障による所得の再分配で、格差はある程度縮小する。","romaji":"Zei to shakai hoshō ni yoru shotoku no saibunpai de, kakusa wa aru teido shukushō suru.","german":"Inequality is reduced to some extent by income redistribution through taxes and social security.","furigana":"ぜい|しゃかいほしょう|しょとく|さいぶんぱい|かくさ|ていどしゅくしょう"},{"japanese":"富の再分配をめぐって、与野党の意見が対立している。","romaji":"Tomi no saibunpai o megutte, yoyatō no iken ga tairitsu shite iru.","german":"The ruling and opposition parties are at odds over the redistribution of wealth.","furigana":"とみ|さいぶんぱい|よやとう|いけん|たいりつ"}],"notes":"再分配 is the \"redistribution\" of income or wealth, usually through taxes and social benefits: 所得の再分配, 所得再分配, 富の再分配. It appears mainly as a noun, less often as 再分配する. The very similar 再配分 is also used, especially for the reallocation of resources or budgets (資源の再配分).","levelNote":"Compound: 再 (N2) + 分配 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"累進課税制度が所得格差の是正に寄与する。","romaji":"Ruishin kazei seido ga shotoku kakusa no zesei ni kiyo suru.","german":"The progressive tax system contributes to correcting income inequality.","furigana":"るいしんかぜいせいど|しょとくかくさ|ぜせい|きよ"},{"japanese":"日本の所得税は累進課税なので、所得が高いほど税率も高くなる。","romaji":"Nihon no shotokuzei wa ruishin kazei na node, shotoku ga takai hodo zeiritsu mo takaku naru.","german":"Since Japan's income tax is progressive, the higher the income, the higher the tax rate.","furigana":"にほん|しょとくぜい|るいしんかぜい|しょとく|たか|ぜいりつ|たか"},{"japanese":"富裕層への累進課税を強化すべきだという声が高まっている。","romaji":"Fuyūsō e no ruishin kazei o kyōka subeki da to iu koe ga takamatte iru.","german":"Calls for stronger progressive taxation of the wealthy are growing louder.","furigana":"ふゆうそう|るいしんかぜい|きょうか|こえ|たか"}],"notes":"累進課税 consists of 累進 \"rising step by step\" and 課税 \"taxation\": the higher the income, the higher the tax rate. Typical combinations are 累進課税制度, 累進税率 and 累進課税を強化する. The opposite is a regressive effect (逆進性), in which low earners are burdened proportionally more; a uniform rate is called 比例税 or 一律の税率.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"消費税は逆進性があるとの批判がある。","romaji":"Shōhizei wa gyakushinsei ga aru to no hihan ga aru.","german":"There is criticism that the consumption tax is regressive.","furigana":"しょうひぜい|ぎゃくしんせい|ひはん"},{"japanese":"食料品に軽減税率を適用して、逆進性の緩和を図る。","romaji":"Shokuryōhin ni keigen zeiritsu o tekiyō shite, gyakushinsei no kanwa o hakaru.","german":"A reduced tax rate is applied to food to ease regressivity.","furigana":"しょくりょうひん|けいげんぜいりつ|てきよう|ぎゃくしんせい|かんわ|はか"},{"japanese":"たばこ税は低所得者の負担割合が大きく、逆進性が強いと言われる。","romaji":"Tabakozei wa teishotokusha no futan wariai ga ōkiku, gyakushinsei ga tsuyoi to iwareru.","german":"The tobacco tax places a larger share of the burden on low-income earners and is said to be strongly regressive.","furigana":"ぜい|ていしょとくしゃ|ふたんわりあい|おお|ぎゃくしんせい|つよ|い"}],"notes":"逆進性 (逆進 \"advancing in reverse\" + 性 \"-ity\") describes the regressive effect of a levy: low earners bear a higher burden in proportion to their income. The word comes up mainly in the debate on the consumption tax: 消費税の逆進性, 逆進性がある／強い, 逆進性を緩和する \"to ease the regressive effect\". The opposite is 累進性 \"progressivity\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"財務諸表を精査して投資判断を下す。","romaji":"Zaimu shohyō wo seisa shite tōshi handan wo kudasu.","german":"Make investment decisions after scrutinizing the financial statements.","furigana":"ざいむしょひょう|せいさ|とうしはんだん|くだ"},{"japanese":"上場企業は財務諸表を公表することが義務づけられている。","romaji":"Jōjō kigyō wa zaimu shohyō o kōhyō suru koto ga gimuzukerarete iru.","german":"Listed companies are obliged to publish their financial statements.","furigana":"じょうじょうきぎょう|ざいむしょひょう|こうひょう|ぎむ"},{"japanese":"財務諸表の読み方を知っていれば、会社の健康状態がわかる。","romaji":"Zaimu shohyō no yomikata o shitte ireba, kaisha no kenkō jōtai ga wakaru.","german":"If you know how to read financial statements, you can tell the health of a company.","furigana":"ざいむしょひょう|よ|かた|し|かいしゃ|けんこうじょうたい"}],"notes":"財務諸表 (諸表 \"the various statements\") is the collective term for a company's financial reports, above all 貸借対照表 \"balance sheet\", 損益計算書 \"income statement\" and キャッシュ・フロー計算書. Typical: 財務諸表を作成する／開示する／公表する／分析する, 財務諸表を読む. In everyday speech one also says 決算書.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自己資本比率の低下が経営リスクを高めている。","romaji":"Jiko shihon hiritsu no teika ga keiei risuku wo takamete iru.","german":"The decline in the equity ratio is increasing management risk.","furigana":"じこしほんひりつ|ていか|けいえい|たか"},{"japanese":"銀行は一定の自己資本を確保しなければならない。","romaji":"Ginkō wa ittei no jiko shihon o kakuho shinakereba naranai.","german":"Banks must secure a certain amount of equity.","furigana":"ぎんこう|いってい|じこしほん|かくほ"},{"japanese":"自己資本だけで設備投資を賄うのは難しい。","romaji":"Jiko shihon dake de setsubi tōshi o makanau no wa muzukashii.","german":"It is difficult to finance capital investment with equity alone.","furigana":"じこしほん|せつびとうし|まかな|むずか"}],"notes":"自己資本 is \"equity\" as opposed to 他人資本 \"borrowed capital\" (i.e. 負債, debt). Very common are the compounds 自己資本比率 \"equity ratio\" and 自己資本利益率 \"return on equity (ROE)\". On the balance sheet, the corresponding item is now usually called 純資産 \"net assets\".","levelNote":"Compound: 自己 (N3) + 資本 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"他人資本への依存度が高すぎる。","romaji":"Tanin shihon e no izondo ga takasugiru.","german":"Dependence on borrowed capital is too high.","furigana":"たにんしほん|いぞんど|たか"},{"japanese":"自己資本と他人資本を合わせたものを総資本という。","romaji":"Jiko shihon to tanin shihon o awaseta mono o sōshihon to iu.","german":"Equity and borrowed capital together are called total capital.","furigana":"じこしほん|たにんしほん|あ|そうしほん"},{"japanese":"銀行からの借入金は他人資本に当たる。","romaji":"Ginkō kara no kariirekin wa tanin shihon ni ataru.","german":"Bank loans count as borrowed capital.","furigana":"ぎんこう|かりいれきん|たにんしほん|あ"}],"notes":"他人資本 is a term from bookkeeping and business administration: \"borrowed capital\", that is, money a company has received from third parties and must repay (loans, bonds, liabilities). The counterpart is 自己資本 (\"equity\"); together the two make up 総資本. On balance sheets it is mostly called 負債. 他人 has nothing personal here, but means \"from outside\".","levelNote":"Compound: 他人 (N3) + 資本 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"流動資産を増やして短期の支払い能力を確保する。","romaji":"Ryūdō shisan wo fuyashite tanki no shiharai nōryoku wo kakuho suru.","german":"Increase current assets to secure short-term solvency.","furigana":"りゅうどうしさん|ふ|たんき|しはら|のうりょく|かくほ"},{"japanese":"現金や売掛金、在庫などは流動資産に含まれる。","romaji":"Genkin ya urikakekin, zaiko nado wa ryūdō shisan ni fukumareru.","german":"Cash, accounts receivable and inventory are included in current assets.","furigana":"げんきん|うりかけきん|ざいこ|りゅうどうしさん|ふく"},{"japanese":"流動資産が流動負債を大きく上回っており、財務は安定している。","romaji":"Ryūdō shisan ga ryūdō fusai o ōkiku uwamawatte ori, zaimu wa antei shite iru.","german":"Current assets greatly exceed current liabilities, and the finances are stable.","furigana":"りゅうどうしさん|りゅうどうふさい|おお|うわまわ|ざいむ|あんてい"}],"notes":"流動資産 are \"current assets\": assets that turn into cash within a year, such as 現金, 預金, 売掛金 \"receivables\" and 在庫 \"inventory\". The counterpart is 固定資産 \"fixed assets\". For liquidity analysis they are compared with 流動負債; the ratio of the two is called 流動比率.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"固定資産の評価額を見直した。","romaji":"Kotei shisan no hyōkagaku wo minaoshita.","german":"The appraised value of the fixed assets was reviewed.","furigana":"こていしさん|ひょうかがく|みなお"},{"japanese":"家を買うと、毎年固定資産税を払わなければならない。","romaji":"Ie o kau to, maitoshi kotei shisanzei o harawanakereba naranai.","german":"When you buy a house, you have to pay fixed asset tax every year.","furigana":"いえ|か|まいとしこていしさんぜい|はら"},{"japanese":"工場の機械や土地は固定資産として計上される。","romaji":"Kōjō no kikai ya tochi wa kotei shisan to shite keijō sareru.","german":"A factory's machinery and land are recorded as fixed assets.","furigana":"こうじょう|きかい|とち|こていしさん|けいじょう"}],"notes":"固定資産 are \"fixed assets\": assets used over the long term, such as 土地 \"land\", 建物 and 機械 (有形固定資産) as well as software or patents (無形固定資産). The counterpart is 流動資産. Even outside bookkeeping, every homeowner knows the word from 固定資産税, the annual \"property tax\".","levelNote":"Compound: 固定 (N1) + 資産 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"流動負債の返済期限が迫っている。","romaji":"Ryūdō fusai no hensai kigen ga sematte iru.","german":"The repayment deadline for the current liabilities is approaching.","furigana":"りゅうどうふさい|へんさいきげん|せま"},{"japanese":"買掛金や短期借入金は流動負債に分類される。","romaji":"Kaikakekin ya tanki kariirekin wa ryūdō fusai ni bunrui sareru.","german":"Accounts payable and short-term loans are classified as current liabilities.","furigana":"かいかけきん|たんきかりいれきん|りゅうどうふさい|ぶんるい"},{"japanese":"流動負債が流動資産を上回ると、資金繰りが苦しくなるおそれがある。","romaji":"Ryūdō fusai ga ryūdō shisan o uwamawaru to, shikinguri ga kurushiku naru osore ga aru.","german":"If current liabilities exceed current assets, cash flow may become difficult.","furigana":"りゅうどうふさい|りゅうどうしさん|うわまわ|しきんぐ|くる"}],"notes":"流動負債 are \"current liabilities\", that is, debts that fall due within a year: 買掛金 \"accounts payable\", 短期借入金 \"short-term loans\", 未払金. The counterpart is 固定負債. If they exceed 流動資産, a bottleneck in 資金繰り \"cash flow management\" threatens.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"固定負債を減らして財務体質を改善する。","romaji":"Kotei fusai wo herashite zaimu taishitsu wo kaizen suru.","german":"Reduce long-term liabilities to improve the financial structure.","furigana":"こていふさい|へ|ざいむたいしつ|かいぜん"},{"japanese":"社債や長期借入金は固定負債に当たる。","romaji":"Shasai ya chōki kariirekin wa kotei fusai ni ataru.","german":"Bonds and long-term loans count as long-term liabilities.","furigana":"しゃさい|ちょうきかりいれきん|こていふさい|あ"},{"japanese":"新工場の建設資金を長期の借り入れで調達したため、固定負債が膨らんだ。","romaji":"Shin kōjō no kensetsu shikin o chōki no kariire de chōtatsu shita tame, kotei fusai ga fukuranda.","german":"Because the construction funds for the new factory were raised through long-term borrowing, long-term liabilities have swelled.","furigana":"しんこうじょう|けんせつしきん|ちょうき|か|い|ちょうたつ|こていふさい|ふく"}],"notes":"固定負債 are \"long-term liabilities\" that fall due only after more than a year, e.g. 社債 \"bonds\", 長期借入金 \"long-term loans\" or pension provisions. The counterpart is 流動負債. Typical: 固定負債が膨らむ／増える, 固定負債を減らす.","levelNote":"Compound: 固定 (N1) + 負債 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"連結決算でグループ全体の業績を開示する。","romaji":"Renketsu kessan de gurūpu zentai no gyōseki wo kaiji suru.","german":"Disclose the performance of the entire group in consolidated financial statements.","furigana":"れんけつけっさん|ぜんたい|ぎょうせき|かいじ"},{"japanese":"同社の今期の連結決算は、純利益が過去最高となった。","romaji":"Dōsha no konki no renketsu kessan wa, junrieki ga kako saikō to natta.","german":"In the company's consolidated results for the current term, net profit hit a record high.","furigana":"どうしゃ|こんき|れんけつけっさん|じゅんりえき|かこさいこう"},{"japanese":"単体では赤字だが、連結決算では黒字を確保した。","romaji":"Tantai de wa akaji da ga, renketsu kessan de wa kuroji o kakuho shita.","german":"It is in the red on a standalone basis, but it secured a profit in the consolidated results.","furigana":"たんたい|あかじ|れんけつけっさん|くろじ|かくほ"}],"notes":"連結決算 are the \"consolidated financial statements\" that present parent and subsidiary companies (連結子会社) as one unit. The counterpart is 単体決算 or 個別決算 \"standalone statements\". In business news one often reads 連結決算で過去最高益 \"record profit in the consolidated results\". 決算 alone means \"closing of accounts, financial statements\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"企業の内部留保が過去最高を更新した。","romaji":"Kigyō no naibu ryūho ga kako saikō wo kōshin shita.","german":"Companies' retained earnings hit a new record high.","furigana":"きぎょう|ないぶりゅうほ|かこさいこう|こうしん"},{"japanese":"労働組合は、内部留保を賃上げに回すよう求めた。","romaji":"Rōdō kumiai wa, naibu ryūho o chin'age ni mawasu yō motometa.","german":"The labor union demanded that retained earnings be used for wage increases.","furigana":"ろうどうくみあい|ないぶりゅうほ|ちんあ|まわ|もと"},{"japanese":"不況に備えて、内部留保を厚くしておく企業が多い。","romaji":"Fukyō ni sonaete, naibu ryūho o atsuku shite oku kigyō ga ōi.","german":"Many companies build up their retained earnings to prepare for a recession.","furigana":"ふきょう|そな|ないぶりゅうほ|あつ|きぎょう|おお"}],"notes":"内部留保 are the \"retained earnings\" kept within the company, not paid out as dividends. The word is politically charged: companies are criticized for 内部留保をため込む \"hoarding reserves\" instead of using them for wages or investment (賃上げに回す). Other combinations: 内部留保を厚くする \"to build up\", 内部留保を取り崩す \"to draw down\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"保有株式の含み益が大幅に増加した。","romaji":"Hoyū kabushiki no fukumieki ga ōhaba ni zōka shita.","german":"The unrealized gains on held shares increased significantly.","furigana":"ほゆうかぶしき|ふく|えき|おおはば|ぞうか"},{"japanese":"株価が上がって、持っている株に十万円の含み益が出ている。","romaji":"Kabuka ga agatte, motte iru kabu ni jūman'en no fukumieki ga dete iru.","german":"Because share prices rose, the shares I hold show an unrealized gain of 100,000 yen.","furigana":"かぶか|あ|も|かぶ|じゅうまんえん|ふく|えき|で"},{"japanese":"含み益があるうちに売って、利益を確定した。","romaji":"Fukumieki ga aru uchi ni utte, rieki o kakutei shita.","german":"I sold while I still had an unrealized gain and locked in the profit.","furigana":"ふく|えき|う|りえき|かくてい"}],"notes":"含み益 (含み \"what is contained, hidden\" + 益 \"profit\") is the paper gain on stocks, real estate etc. whose market value is above the purchase price while one has not yet sold them. Typical: 含み益が出る／膨らむ, 含み益を抱える; it is realized by selling (利益を確定する). The opposite is 含み損. Companies use the word just as private investors do.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不動産の含み損が経営を圧迫している。","romaji":"Fudōsan no fukumizon ga keiei wo appaku shite iru.","german":"Unrealized losses on real estate are weighing on management.","furigana":"ふどうさん|ふく|ぞん|けいえい|あっぱく"},{"japanese":"買った株が値下がりして、含み損を抱えている。","romaji":"Katta kabu ga nesagari shite, fukumizon o kakaete iru.","german":"The shares I bought have fallen in price, and I am sitting on an unrealized loss.","furigana":"か|かぶ|ねさ|ふく|ぞん|かか"},{"japanese":"含み損が膨らむ前に、思い切って損切りした。","romaji":"Fukumizon ga fukuramu mae ni, omoikitte songiri shita.","german":"Before the unrealized loss grew further, I made up my mind and cut my losses.","furigana":"ふく|ぞん|ふく|まえ|おも|き|そんぎ"}],"notes":"含み損 is the \"paper loss\": the market value of held shares or real estate is below the purchase price, but the loss has not yet been realized by selling. Typical: 含み損を抱える \"to sit on paper losses\", 含み損が膨らむ, 含み損が経営を圧迫する. Someone who sells at a loss to avoid worse does 損切り. The opposite is 含み益.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"貸倒引当金を積み増しした。","romaji":"Kashidaore hikiatekin wo tsumimashi shita.","german":"The allowance for doubtful accounts was increased.","furigana":"|つ|ま"},{"japanese":"夏のボーナスに備えて、賞与引当金を計上した。","romaji":"Natsu no bōnasu ni sonaete, shōyo hikiatekin o keijō shita.","german":"A bonus provision was recorded in preparation for the summer bonus.","furigana":"なつ|そな|しょうよひきあてきん|けいじょう"},{"japanese":"裁判で負ける可能性が高いため、損失に備えて引当金を積んだ。","romaji":"Saiban de makeru kanōsei ga takai tame, sonshitsu ni sonaete hikiatekin o tsunda.","german":"Since losing in court is likely, a provision was set aside against the loss.","furigana":"さいばん|ま|かのうせい|たか|そんしつ|そな|ひきあてきん|つ"}],"notes":"引当金 is a \"provision\" on the balance sheet for future expenses or losses that are already foreseeable. Common types: 貸倒引当金 \"for bad debts\", 退職給付引当金 \"for pensions\", 賞与引当金 \"for bonuses\". Typical verbs: 引当金を計上する／積む／積み増す \"to set up, to increase\" and 引当金を取り崩す \"to release\". In bookkeeping it is written without okurigana, although the verb is 引き当てる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不良債権の償却を加速させる。","romaji":"Furyō saiken no shōkyaku wo kasoku saseru.","german":"Accelerate the write-off of non-performing loans.","furigana":"ふりょうさいけん|しょうきゃく|かそく"},{"japanese":"パソコンは四年かけて減価償却する。","romaji":"Pasokon wa yonen kakete genka shōkyaku suru.","german":"Computers are depreciated over four years.","furigana":"よねん|げんかしょうきゃく"},{"japanese":"敷金のうち一か月分は償却されるので、退去しても返ってこない。","romaji":"Shikikin no uchi ikkagetsubun wa shōkyaku sareru node, taikyo shite mo kaette konai.","german":"One month's worth of the deposit is retained, so it is not returned when you move out.","furigana":"しききん|いっ|げつぶん|しょうきゃく|たいきょ|かえ"}],"notes":"償却 is a transitive する-verb of bookkeeping: \"to depreciate\" (spread the loss in value of assets over the years, mostly as 減価償却) and \"to write off\" (不良債権を償却する \"to write off bad loans\"). The meaning \"redemption, repayment\" also exists, but for bonds one usually says 償還. In everyday life one meets the word in rental contracts: 敷金償却 is the part of the deposit that is not refunded.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"特別損失として計上された金額は莫大だ。","romaji":"Tokubetsu sonshitsu to shite keijō sareta kingaku wa bakudai da.","german":"The amount recorded as an extraordinary loss is enormous.","furigana":"とくべつそんしつ|けいじょう|きんがく|ばくだい"},{"japanese":"同社は三期連続で赤字を計上した。","romaji":"Dōsha wa sanki renzoku de akaji o keijō shita.","german":"The company posted a loss for the third consecutive term.","furigana":"どうしゃ|さんきれんぞく|あかじ|けいじょう"},{"japanese":"新しい図書館の建設費が来年度予算に計上された。","romaji":"Atarashii toshokan no kensetsuhi ga rainendo yosan ni keijō sareta.","german":"The construction cost of the new library was included in next year's budget.","furigana":"あたら|としょかん|けんせつひ|らいねんどよさん|けいじょう"}],"notes":"計上 means \"to enter an amount in the accounts, to book, to report\" and is a transitive する-verb. Typical patterns: XをYとして計上する \"to book X as Y\", 赤字／利益／損失を計上する \"to report a loss/profit\", 予算に計上する \"to include in the budget\". It is very often used in the passive (計上される). For private household account books, one says 家計簿につける instead.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"適時開示のルールを遵守しなければならない。","romaji":"Tekiji kaiji no rūru wo junshu shinakereba naranai.","german":"The rules on timely disclosure must be observed.","furigana":"てきじかいじ|じゅんしゅ"},{"japanese":"自分のカルテの開示を病院に求めた。","romaji":"Jibun no karute no kaiji o byōin ni motometa.","german":"I asked the hospital for disclosure of my medical records.","furigana":"じぶん|かいじ|びょういん|もと"},{"japanese":"会社は事故に関する情報を速やかに開示すべきだ。","romaji":"Kaisha wa jiko ni kansuru jōhō o sumiyaka ni kaiji subeki da.","german":"The company should promptly disclose information about the accident.","furigana":"かいしゃ|じこ|かん|じょうほう|すみ|かいじ"}],"notes":"開示 is the \"disclosure\" of information or documents, mostly to those entitled to it or on the basis of an obligation: 情報を開示する, 情報開示, 決算を開示する, 適時開示 \"timely disclosure\". With 開示請求 one requests access, for example to one's own personal data or government records. 公開 generally means \"make public\", 公表 \"announce publicly\"; 開示 emphasizes releasing information that had been withheld until then.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"カルテルの形成は独占禁止法で禁じられている。","romaji":"Karuteru no keisei wa dokusen kinshihō de kinjirarete iru.","german":"Die Bildung von Kartellen ist durch das Kartellgesetz verboten.","furigana":"けいせい|どくせんきんしほう|きん"},{"japanese":"大手メーカー数社が価格カルテルを結んでいた疑いで、立ち入り検査を受けた。","romaji":"Ōte mēkā sūsha ga kakaku karuteru o musunde ita utagai de, tachiiri kensa o uketa.","german":"Wegen des Verdachts auf ein Preiskartell wurden mehrere große Hersteller durchsucht.","furigana":"おおて|すうしゃ|かかく|むす|うたが|た|い|けんさ|う"},{"japanese":"メキシコの麻薬カルテルによる暴力が深刻化している。","romaji":"Mekishiko no mayaku karuteru ni yoru bōryoku ga shinkokuka shite iru.","german":"Die Gewalt der mexikanischen Drogenkartelle verschärft sich.","furigana":"まやく|ぼうりょく|しんこくか"}],"notes":"カルテル stammt aus dem Deutschen „Kartell“ und bezeichnet Absprachen zwischen Konkurrenten über Preise, Mengen oder Absatzgebiete. Typisch: カルテルを結ぶ „ein Kartell bilden“, 価格カルテル, 国際カルテル. In Japan ist sie durch das 独占禁止法 (kurz 独禁法) verboten; zuständig ist die Wettbewerbsbehörde 公正取引委員会. Bei öffentlichen Ausschreibungen spricht man eher von 談合. In den Nachrichten kommt außerdem 麻薬カルテル „Drogenkartell“ vor.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不正競争防止法により企業秘密が保護される。","romaji":"Fusei kyōsō bōshihō ni yori kigyō himitsu ga hogo sareru.","german":"Trade secrets are protected by the Unfair Competition Prevention Act.","furigana":"ふせいきょうそうぼうしほう|きぎょうひみつ|ほご"},{"japanese":"有名ブランドのロゴをまねた商品の販売は、不正競争に当たる。","romaji":"Yūmei burando no rogo o maneta shōhin no hanbai wa, fusei kyōsō ni ataru.","german":"Selling goods that imitate the logo of a famous brand constitutes unfair competition.","furigana":"ゆうめい|しょうひん|はんばい|ふせいきょうそう|あ"},{"japanese":"他社の商標に似たドメイン名を取得するのも、不正競争になり得る。","romaji":"Tasha no shōhyō ni nita domeinmei o shutoku suru no mo, fusei kyōsō ni nariuru.","german":"Acquiring a domain name similar to another company's trademark can also constitute unfair competition.","furigana":"たしゃ|しょうひょう|に|めい|しゅとく|ふせいきょうそう|う"}],"notes":"不正競争 (不正 \"illegitimate\" + 競争 \"competition\") is a legal term and appears mostly in 不正競争防止法 \"Unfair Competition Prevention Act\" or 不正競争行為. It covers, for example, the theft of trade secrets, imitation of well-known brands or misleading labeling. To be distinguished from 過当競争 \"excessive (but legal) competition\"; the opposite is 公正な競争.","levelNote":"Compound: 不正 (N3) + 競争 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"知的財産権の保護が国際的な課題となっている。","romaji":"Chiteki zaisanken no hogo ga kokusaiteki na kadai to natte iru.","german":"The protection of intellectual property rights has become an international issue.","furigana":"ちてきざいさんけん|ほご|こくさいてき|かだい"},{"japanese":"当社の知的財産を守るため、新しい技術はすべて特許を出願している。","romaji":"Tōsha no chiteki zaisan o mamoru tame, atarashii gijutsu wa subete tokkyo o shutsugan shite iru.","german":"To protect our intellectual property, we file patents for all new technologies.","furigana":"とうしゃ|ちてきざいさん|まも|あたら|ぎじゅつ|とっきょ|しゅつがん"},{"japanese":"他人の作品を無断で使うと、知的財産の侵害になることがある。","romaji":"Tanin no sakuhin o mudan de tsukau to, chiteki zaisan no shingai ni naru koto ga aru.","german":"Using other people's works without permission can be an infringement of intellectual property.","furigana":"たにん|さくひん|むだん|つか|ちてきざいさん|しんがい"}],"notes":"知的財産 (知的 \"intellectual\" + 財産 \"property, assets\") is \"intellectual property\"; in companies and the media it is often abbreviated to 知財 (知財部 \"patent department\"). The rights to it are called 知的財産権, e.g. 特許権, 著作権 and 商標権. Typical: 知的財産を守る／保護する／侵害する, 知的財産戦略.","levelNote":"Compound: 知的 (N1) + 財産 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"景気後退局面での財政出動が議論されている。","romaji":"Keiki kōtai kyokumen de no zaisei shutsudō ga giron sarete iru.","german":"Fiscal stimulus during a recession phase is being discussed.","furigana":"けいきこうたいきょくめん|ざいせいしゅつどう|ぎろん"},{"japanese":"アメリカ経済が景気後退に陥るとの懸念が広がっている。","romaji":"Amerika keizai ga keiki kōtai ni ochiiru to no kenen ga hirogatte iru.","german":"Concern is spreading that the US economy will fall into a recession.","furigana":"けいざい|けいきこうたい|おちい|けねん|ひろ"},{"japanese":"景気後退で求人が減り、就職活動が厳しくなった。","romaji":"Keiki kōtai de kyūjin ga heri, shūshoku katsudō ga kibishiku natta.","german":"Because of the downturn, job offers decreased and job hunting became harder.","furigana":"けいきこうたい|きゅうじん|へ|しゅうしょくかつどう|きび"}],"notes":"景気後退 (景気 \"economy\" + 後退 \"decline\") is an \"economic downturn\" or \"recession\", that is, a phase. Typical: 景気後退に陥る／入る, 景気後退局面, 景気後退の懸念 \"recession worries\". The loanword リセッション is synonymous; 不況 and colloquially 不景気 describe more the state of the slump. Opposites: 景気回復 \"recovery\", 景気拡大 \"expansion\".","levelNote":"Compound: 景気 (N3) + 後退 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大規模な景気刺激策が打ち出された。","romaji":"Daikibo na keiki shigekisaku ga uchidasareta.","german":"Large-scale economic stimulus measures were announced.","furigana":"だいきぼ|けいきしげきさく|う|だ"},{"japanese":"政府は減税を中心とした景気刺激策を検討している。","romaji":"Seifu wa genzei o chūshin to shita keiki shigekisaku o kentō shite iru.","german":"The government is considering a stimulus package centered on tax cuts.","furigana":"せいふ|げんぜい|ちゅうしん|けいきしげきさく|けんとう"},{"japanese":"景気刺激策の効果は一時的なものに過ぎないという批判もある。","romaji":"Keiki shigekisaku no kōka wa ichijiteki na mono ni suginai to iu hihan mo aru.","german":"There is also criticism that the effect of stimulus measures is only temporary.","furigana":"けいきしげきさく|こうか|いちじてき|す|ひはん"}],"notes":"景気刺激策 (景気 \"economy\" + 刺激 \"stimulus\" + 策 \"measure\") is an \"economic stimulus program\", for example tax cuts, public works or cash payments to citizens. Typical verbs: 景気刺激策を打ち出す \"to announce\", 講じる \"to take\", 実施する. In Japanese news such a package is often called, more neutrally, 経済対策, e.g. 総合経済対策.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大規模な金融緩和が景気回復を後押しした。","romaji":"Daikibo na kin'yū kanwa ga keiki kaifuku wo atooshi shita.","german":"Large-scale monetary easing supported the economic recovery.","furigana":"だいきぼ|きんゆうかんわ|けいきかいふく|あとお"},{"japanese":"日銀は金融緩和を続ける方針を維持した。","romaji":"Nichigin wa kin'yū kanwa o tsuzukeru hōshin o iji shita.","german":"The Bank of Japan maintained its policy of continuing monetary easing.","furigana":"にちぎん|きんゆうかんわ|つづ|ほうしん|いじ"},{"japanese":"金融緩和で円安が進み、輸入品の値段が上がった。","romaji":"Kin'yū kanwa de en'yasu ga susumi, yunyūhin no nedan ga agatta.","german":"Monetary easing weakened the yen, and the prices of imported goods rose.","furigana":"きんゆうかんわ|えんやす|すす|ゆにゅうひん|ねだん|あ"}],"notes":"金融緩和 is \"monetary easing\": the central bank lowers interest rates or increases the money supply to stimulate the economy. Typical: 金融緩和を行う／続ける／拡大する／縮小する, 金融緩和策, 大規模金融緩和. The opposite is 金融引き締め; a special form is 量的緩和. 緩和 alone means \"easing\", cf. 規制緩和 \"deregulation\".","levelNote":"Compound: 金融 (N3) + 緩和 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"インフレ対策として金融引き締めが実施された。","romaji":"Infure taisaku to shite kin'yū hikishime ga jisshi sareta.","german":"Monetary tightening was carried out as a measure against inflation.","furigana":"たいさく|きんゆうひ|し|じっし"},{"japanese":"金融引き締めで住宅ローンの金利が上がり、家を買う人が減った。","romaji":"Kin'yū hikishime de jūtaku rōn no kinri ga agari, ie o kau hito ga hetta.","german":"Monetary tightening raised mortgage interest rates, and fewer people bought homes.","furigana":"きんゆうひ|し|じゅうたく|きんり|あ|いえ|か|ひと|へ"},{"japanese":"市場は、急な金融引き締めが景気を冷やすことを警戒している。","romaji":"Shijō wa, kyū na kin'yū hikishime ga keiki o hiyasu koto o keikai shite iru.","german":"Markets are wary that abrupt monetary tightening will cool the economy.","furigana":"しじょう|きゅう|きんゆうひ|し|けいき|ひ|けいかい"}],"notes":"金融引き締め is \"monetary tightening\", usually through interest rate hikes (利上げ) or a reduction of the money supply, in order to curb inflation. Typical: 金融引き締めに転じる \"to switch to\", 金融引き締めに踏み切る, 金融引き締めを強化する. The verb behind it is 引き締める \"to tighten, to draw tight\" (also 気を引き締める \"to pull oneself together\"); in official texts also written 金融引締め. Opposite: 金融緩和.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"中央銀行が量的緩和政策を継続する方針を示した。","romaji":"Chūō ginkō ga ryōteki kanwa seisaku wo keizoku suru hōshin wo shimeshita.","german":"The central bank indicated its intention to continue its quantitative easing policy.","furigana":"ちゅうおうぎんこう|りょうてきかんわせいさく|けいぞく|ほうしん|しめ"},{"japanese":"日本は2001年に世界で初めて量的緩和を導入した。","romaji":"Nihon wa 2001-nen ni sekai de hajimete ryōteki kanwa o dōnyū shita.","german":"In 2001, Japan became the first country in the world to introduce quantitative easing.","furigana":"にほん|ねん|せかい|はじ|りょうてきかんわ|どうにゅう"},{"japanese":"量的緩和の縮小が始まると、株価は大きく下がった。","romaji":"Ryōteki kanwa no shukushō ga hajimaru to, kabuka wa ōkiku sagatta.","german":"When quantitative easing began to be scaled back, stock prices fell sharply.","furigana":"りょうてきかんわ|しゅくしょう|はじ|かぶか|おお|さ"}],"notes":"量的緩和 is \"quantitative easing\": the central bank buys government bonds and other securities on a large scale to increase the money supply when interest rate cuts are no longer possible. Typical: 量的緩和を導入する／拡大する／縮小する／終了する. From 2013, the Bank of Japan pursued the so-called 量的・質的金融緩和. 金融緩和 is the umbrella term; the winding down is called 量的緩和の縮小 or テーパリング.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"公定歩合の引き上げが市場に影響を与えた。","romaji":"Kōtei buai no hikiage ga shijō ni eikyō wo ataeta.","german":"The raising of the official discount rate affected the market.","furigana":"こうていぶあい|ひ|あ|しじょう|えいきょう|あた"},{"japanese":"公定歩合の引き下げで、企業は資金を借りやすくなった。","romaji":"Kōtei buai no hikisage de, kigyō wa shikin o kariyasuku natta.","german":"The cut in the discount rate made it easier for companies to borrow funds.","furigana":"こうていぶあい|ひ|さ|きぎょう|しきん|か"},{"japanese":"公定歩合は2006年に「基準割引率および基準貸付利率」と名前が変わった。","romaji":"Kōtei buai wa 2006-nen ni \"kijun waribikiritsu oyobi kijun kashitsuke riritsu\" to namae ga kawatta.","german":"In 2006, the official discount rate was renamed \"the basic discount rate and basic loan rate\".","furigana":"こうていぶあい|ねん|きじゅんわりびきりつ|きじゅんかしつけりりつ|なまえ|か"}],"notes":"公定歩合 was for a long time the most important policy interest rate of the Bank of Japan, at which it lent money to banks. Since 2006 it has officially been called 基準割引率および基準貸付利率, and the policy rate target is now a different rate; the word is therefore found mainly in historical contexts or older texts. 歩合 means \"rate, percentage\" (also 歩合制 \"commission basis\"). Typical: 公定歩合を引き上げる／引き下げる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"マイナス金利政策が銀行の収益を圧迫している。","romaji":"Mainasu kinri seisaku ga ginkō no shūeki wo appaku shite iru.","german":"The negative interest rate policy is squeezing banks' earnings.","furigana":"きんりせいさく|ぎんこう|しゅうえき|あっぱく"},{"japanese":"日銀は2016年にマイナス金利を導入し、2024年に解除した。","romaji":"Nichigin wa 2016-nen ni mainasu kinri o dōnyū shi, 2024-nen ni kaijo shita.","german":"The Bank of Japan introduced negative interest rates in 2016 and lifted them in 2024.","furigana":"にちぎん|ねん|きんり|どうにゅう|ねん|かいじょ"},{"japanese":"マイナス金利の影響で、住宅ローンの金利が過去最低の水準まで下がった。","romaji":"Mainasu kinri no eikyō de, jūtaku rōn no kinri ga kako saitei no suijun made sagatta.","german":"Because of negative interest rates, mortgage rates fell to record lows.","furigana":"きんり|えいきょう|じゅうたく|きんり|かこさいてい|すいじゅん|さ"}],"notes":"マイナス金利 (マイナス \"minus\" + 金利 \"interest\") is the \"negative interest rate\": banks pay interest on deposits at the central bank instead of receiving it. The Bank of Japan introduced the マイナス金利政策 in 2016 and ended it in 2024. Typical: マイナス金利を導入する／解除する, マイナス金利の影響. Related: ゼロ金利 \"zero interest rate\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大型の財政出動により景気を下支えする。","romaji":"Ōgata no zaisei shutsudō ni yori keiki wo shitazasae suru.","german":"Support the economy through large-scale fiscal stimulus.","furigana":"おおがた|ざいせいしゅつどう|けいき|したざさ"},{"japanese":"財政出動を求める声がある一方で、財政赤字の拡大を心配する声もある。","romaji":"Zaisei shutsudō o motomeru koe ga aru ippō de, zaisei akaji no kakudai o shinpai suru koe mo aru.","german":"While some call for fiscal stimulus, others worry about a growing budget deficit.","furigana":"ざいせいしゅつどう|もと|こえ|いっぽう|ざいせいあかじ|かくだい|しんぱい|こえ"},{"japanese":"災害からの復興のため、政府は異例の規模の財政出動に踏み切った。","romaji":"Saigai kara no fukkō no tame, seifu wa irei no kibo no zaisei shutsudō ni fumikitta.","german":"For reconstruction after the disaster, the government went ahead with fiscal stimulus on an unprecedented scale.","furigana":"さいがい|ふっこう|せいふ|いれい|きぼ|ざいせいしゅつどう|ふ|き"}],"notes":"財政出動 denotes the use of additional government spending to support the economy, for example through public works or subsidies. 出動 actually means \"to turn out\" (like the fire brigade or troops) and sounds like an emergency deployment. Typical: 財政出動を行う／求める, 大規模な財政出動, 財政出動に踏み切る. Opposites are 財政再建 \"fiscal consolidation\" and 緊縮財政 \"austerity\"; government spending in general is called 財政支出.","levelNote":"Compound: 財政 (N1) + 出動 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"法人税の税収が大幅に増加した。","romaji":"Hōjinzei no zeishū ga ōhaba ni zōka shita.","german":"Corporate tax revenue increased significantly.","furigana":"ほうじんぜい|ぜいしゅう|おおはば|ぞうか"},{"japanese":"景気の回復で、国の税収は過去最高を記録した。","romaji":"Keiki no kaifuku de, kuni no zeishū wa kako saikō o kiroku shita.","german":"Thanks to the economic recovery, the state's tax revenue hit a record.","furigana":"けいき|かいふく|くに|ぜいしゅう|かこさいこう|きろく"},{"japanese":"人口が減っている町では、税収不足が深刻だ。","romaji":"Jinkō ga hette iru machi de wa, zeishū busoku ga shinkoku da.","german":"In towns with a shrinking population, the shortage of tax revenue is serious.","furigana":"じんこう|へ|まち|ぜいしゅうぶそく|しんこく"}],"notes":"税収 is \"tax revenue\" from the point of view of the state or a municipality; what a citizen pays, on the other hand, is 税金. Typical: 税収が増える／減る／落ち込む, 税収が過去最高を更新する, 税収不足 \"tax shortfall\", 法人税収. Total government revenue including borrowing is called 歳入.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"国債の大量発行が財政悪化の一因となっている。","romaji":"Kokusai no tairyō hakkō ga zaisei akka no ichiin to natte iru.","german":"The massive issuance of government bonds is one cause of worsening public finances.","furigana":"こくさい|たいりょうはっこう|ざいせいあっか|いちいん"},{"japanese":"個人向け国債は元本割れの心配がなく、安全な投資先だ。","romaji":"Kojin-muke kokusai wa ganpon ware no shinpai ga naku, anzen na tōshisaki da.","german":"Government bonds for individuals are a safe investment with no worry of losing principal.","furigana":"こじんむ|こくさい|がんぽんわ|しんぱい|あんぜん|とうしさき"},{"japanese":"長期国債の利回りが上昇した。","romaji":"Chōki kokusai no rimawari ga jōshō shita.","german":"The yield on long-term government bonds rose.","furigana":"ちょうきこくさい|りまわ|じょうしょう"}],"notes":"国債 is the \"government bond\" of the central government and, in a wider sense, the national debt. Typical: 国債を発行する／購入する／償還する, 赤字国債 \"deficit bonds\", 国債の利回り \"yield\", 個人向け国債 \"government bonds for individual investors\". Careful when listening: it sounds exactly like 国際 \"international\". 公債 is the umbrella term that also includes local government bonds (地方債).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"公債残高が過去最高に達した。","romaji":"Kōsai zandaka ga kako saikō ni tasshita.","german":"The outstanding balance of public bonds reached a record high.","furigana":"こうさいざんだか|かこさいこう|たっ"},{"japanese":"来年度予算の公債依存度は三割を超えている。","romaji":"Rainendo yosan no kōsai izondo wa sanwari o koete iru.","german":"More than 30 percent of next year's budget depends on public bonds.","furigana":"らいねんどよさん|こうさいいぞんど|さんわり|こ"},{"japanese":"公債費が増えて、ほかの政策に使えるお金が減っている。","romaji":"Kōsaihi ga fuete, hoka no seisaku ni tsukaeru okane ga hette iru.","german":"As debt service costs grow, the money available for other policies is shrinking.","furigana":"こうさいひ|ふ|せいさく|つか|かね|へ"}],"notes":"公債 is the umbrella term for \"public bonds\" or public debt: government bonds (国債) and local government bonds (地方債). The word is common mainly in budget and statistics terms: 公債残高 \"outstanding debt\", 公債依存度 \"share of debt financing in the budget\", 公債費 \"debt service\". In everyday speech one rather says 国債 or simply 国の借金.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"社債を発行して設備投資の資金を調達した。","romaji":"Shasai wo hakkō shite setsubi tōshi no shikin wo chōtatsu shita.","german":"Funds for capital investment were raised by issuing corporate bonds.","furigana":"しゃさい|はっこう|せつびとうし|しきん|ちょうたつ"},{"japanese":"社債は株と違って、満期になれば元本が返ってくる。","romaji":"Shasai wa kabu to chigatte, manki ni nareba ganpon ga kaette kuru.","german":"Unlike stocks, with corporate bonds the principal is returned at maturity.","furigana":"しゃさい|かぶ|ちが|まんき|がんぽん|かえ"},{"japanese":"業績悪化で、その会社の社債の格付けが引き下げられた。","romaji":"Gyōseki akka de, sono kaisha no shasai no kakuzuke ga hikisagerareta.","german":"Due to worsening results, the rating of that company's bonds was lowered.","furigana":"ぎょうせきあっか|かいしゃ|しゃさい|かくづ|ひ|さ"}],"notes":"社債 is a \"corporate bond\": a company borrows money from investors and repays it with interest. Unlike 株式 \"shares\", it is debt capital. Typical: 社債を発行する／引き受ける／償還する, 社債の格付け \"rating\", 転換社債 \"convertible bond\". Compare 国債 (state) and 地方債 (local governments).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"手形の不渡りが連鎖倒産を引き起こした。","romaji":"Tegata no fuwatari ga rensa tōsan o hikiokoshita.","german":"The dishonoring of the bills triggered a chain of bankruptcies.","furigana":"てがた|ふわた|れんさとうさん|ひ|お"},{"japanese":"取引先に約束手形を振り出した。","romaji":"Torihikisaki ni yakusoku tegata o furidashita.","german":"We issued a promissory note to our business partner.","furigana":"とりひきさき|やくそくてがた|ふ|だ"},{"japanese":"記念に横綱の手形をもらった。","romaji":"Kinen ni yokozuna no tegata o moratta.","german":"As a souvenir, I received a yokozuna's handprint.","furigana":"きねん|よこづな|てがた"}],"notes":"手形 is above all a \"bill of exchange\": 約束手形 (promissory note) and 為替手形 (draft). One says 手形を振り出す \"to issue\", 手形を割り引く \"to discount\"; if it is not honored, this is called 不渡り, which often means the end for companies. In Japan, paper bills are increasingly being replaced by electronic procedures. Literally, 手形 is also a \"handprint\", such as the signed handprints of sumo wrestlers. 空手形 figuratively means \"empty promise\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"小切手を振り出して代金を支払った。","romaji":"Kogitte wo furidashite daikin wo shiharatta.","german":"I wrote a check and paid the amount with it.","furigana":"こぎって|ふ|だ|だいきん|しはら"},{"japanese":"受け取った小切手を銀行で現金に換えた。","romaji":"Uketotta kogitte o ginkō de genkin ni kaeta.","german":"I cashed the check I received at the bank.","furigana":"う|と|こぎって|ぎんこう|げんきん|か"},{"japanese":"日本では、個人が小切手を使うことはほとんどない。","romaji":"Nihon de wa, kojin ga kogitte o tsukau koto wa hotondo nai.","german":"In Japan, individuals almost never use checks.","furigana":"にほん|こじん|こぎって|つか"}],"notes":"小切手 is the \"check\"; it is read こぎって with a voiced ぎ, not to be confused with 切手 \"postage stamp\". Writing a check is colloquially 小切手を切る, formally 小切手を振り出す; cashing it is 小切手を現金化する or 現金に換える. In Japan, individuals hardly use checks, but they still occur in business dealings.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"有価証券の売買により多額の利益を得た。","romaji":"Yūka shōken no baibai ni yori tagaku no rieki wo eta.","german":"Large profits were made by trading securities.","furigana":"ゆうかしょうけん|ばいばい|たがく|りえき|え"},{"japanese":"上場企業は毎年、有価証券報告書を提出しなければならない。","romaji":"Jōjō kigyō wa maitoshi, yūka shōken hōkokusho o teishutsu shinakereba naranai.","german":"Listed companies must submit a securities report every year.","furigana":"じょうじょうきぎょう|まいとし|ゆうかしょうけんほうこくしょ|ていしゅつ"},{"japanese":"銀行は余った資金の一部を有価証券で運用している。","romaji":"Ginkō wa amatta shikin no ichibu o yūka shōken de un'yō shite iru.","german":"Banks invest part of their surplus funds in securities.","furigana":"ぎんこう|あま|しきん|いちぶ|ゆうかしょうけん|うんよう"}],"notes":"有価証券 (有価 \"having value\" + 証券 \"certificate, instrument\") is the legal and accounting term for \"securities\" such as stocks, bonds and investment fund units. Very common is 有価証券報告書, the mandatory annual report of listed companies (short 有報). Typical: 有価証券を保有する／売却する, 有価証券の売買. In everyday speech one rather says 株 or 証券.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先物取引でリスクヘッジを行う。","romaji":"Sakimono torihiki de risuku hejji wo okonau.","german":"Hedge risk with futures trading.","furigana":"さきものとりひき|おこな"},{"japanese":"原油先物の価格が一日で5%以上急騰した。","romaji":"Gen'yu sakimono no kakaku ga ichinichi de 5-pāsento ijō kyūtō shita.","german":"The price of crude oil futures soared more than 5 percent in a single day.","furigana":"げんゆさきもの|かかく|いちにち|いじょうきゅうとう"},{"japanese":"あのチームは無名の若手選手を先物買いして成功した。","romaji":"Ano chīmu wa mumei no wakate senshu o sakimonogai shite seikō shita.","german":"That team succeeded by betting early on unknown young players.","furigana":"むめい|わかてせんしゅ|さきものが|せいこう"}],"notes":"先物 (先 \"future\" + 物 \"goods\") denotes \"futures\": buying or selling at a price fixed today for a later date. Typical: 先物取引, 先物市場, 原油先物, 日経平均先物. The opposite is 現物 \"spot goods\". Figuratively, 先物買い means \"betting early on future potential\", for example on young talents.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"資産を信託銀行に預けて運用してもらう。","romaji":"Shisan wo shintaku ginkō ni azukete un'yō shite morau.","german":"I entrust my assets to a trust bank and have it manage them.","furigana":"しさん|しんたくぎんこう|あず|うんよう"},{"japanese":"毎月一万円ずつ投資信託を積み立てている。","romaji":"Maitsuki ichiman'en zutsu tōshi shintaku o tsumitatete iru.","german":"I put 10,000 yen into an investment trust every month.","furigana":"まいつきいちまんえん|とうししんたく|つ|た"},{"japanese":"選挙で国民の信託を受けた政府には、それに応える責任がある。","romaji":"Senkyo de kokumin no shintaku o uketa seifu ni wa, sore ni kotaeru sekinin ga aru.","german":"A government that received the people's mandate in the election has a responsibility to live up to it.","furigana":"せんきょ|こくみん|しんたく|う|せいふ|こた|せきにん"}],"notes":"信託 (信 \"trust\" + 託 \"to entrust\") is a \"trust\": assets are transferred to someone for management for the benefit of a beneficiary. Common in compounds: 信託銀行 \"trust bank\", 投資信託 (short 投信) \"investment fund\", 家族信託. As a transitive する-verb (XをYに信託する) it is very formal; in everyday speech one says 預ける or 任せる. Politically, 国民の信託 stands for the \"mandate of the people\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"証券会社が社債の引受業務を行った。","romaji":"Shōken gaisha ga shasai no hikiuke gyōmu wo okonatta.","german":"The securities firm handled the underwriting of the corporate bonds.","furigana":"しょうけんがいしゃ|しゃさい|ひきうけぎょうむ|おこな"},{"japanese":"経営再建のため、取引先の銀行が新株の引受先になった。","romaji":"Keiei saiken no tame, torihikisaki no ginkō ga shinkabu no hikiukesaki ni natta.","german":"For the management turnaround, the business partner's bank became the subscriber of the new shares.","furigana":"けいえいさいけん|とりひきさき|ぎんこう|しんかぶ|ひきうけさき"},{"japanese":"郵便局の窓口では、小包の引受は午後七時までです。","romaji":"Yūbinkyoku no madoguchi de wa, kozutsumi no hikiuke wa gogo shichiji made desu.","german":"At the post office counter, parcels are accepted until 7 p.m.","furigana":"ゆうびんきょく|まどぐち|こづつみ|ひきうけ|ごごしちじ"}],"notes":"引受 is the noun of 引き受ける \"to take over, to take on\". In finance, it denotes the taking over of a securities issue by a bank or securities firm (underwriting): 引受業務, 引受手数料, 引受先 \"taker (e.g. of the new shares in a capital increase)\". Further technical meanings are the acceptance of a bill of exchange (手形の引受) and the acceptance of postal items (郵便物の引受). In everyday writing it is mostly 引き受け with okurigana; the short spelling 引受 is typical of technical language, forms and compounds.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新株の発行により資金を調達した。","romaji":"Shinkabu no hakkō ni yori shikin wo chōtatsu shita.","german":"Funds were raised through the issuance of new shares.","furigana":"しんかぶ|はっこう|しきん|ちょうたつ"},{"japanese":"領収書を発行していただけますか。","romaji":"Ryōshūsho o hakkō shite itadakemasu ka?","german":"Could you issue me a receipt, please?","furigana":"りょうしゅうしょ|はっこう"},{"japanese":"この雑誌は毎月十日に発行される。","romaji":"Kono zasshi wa maitsuki tōka ni hakkō sareru.","german":"This magazine is published on the tenth of every month.","furigana":"ざっし|まいつきとおか|はっこう"}],"notes":"発行 is a transitive する-verb: 株式・社債・紙幣を発行する \"to issue, to float\", but also 領収書・パスポート・証明書を発行する \"to issue (a document)\" and 雑誌・新聞を発行する \"to publish\". In the passive one says 新株が発行される. For books, 出版 \"to publish\" is more usual; 発行 emphasizes the formal act of issuing, for example with magazines and documents. Not to be confused with the identically read 発効 \"coming into force\" and 発酵 \"fermentation\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"電子決済の普及が進んでいる。","romaji":"Denshi kessai no fukyū ga susunde iru.","german":"The spread of electronic payment is progressing.","furigana":"でんしけっさい|ふきゅう|すす"},{"japanese":"代金はクレジットカードで決済しました。","romaji":"Daikin wa kurejitto kādo de kessai shimashita.","german":"I paid the amount by credit card.","furigana":"だいきん|けっさい"},{"japanese":"月末までに取引先との決済を済ませなければならない。","romaji":"Getsumatsu made ni torihikisaki to no kessai o sumasenakereba naranai.","german":"We have to complete settlement with our business partners by the end of the month.","furigana":"げつまつ|とりひきさき|けっさい|す"}],"notes":"決済 means the settling of a payment or the processing of a transaction; as a transitive する-verb: 代金をカードで決済する. Typical compounds are 電子決済, キャッシュレス決済, スマホ決済, 決済手段 and 決済日. In everyday speech one usually says simply 払う or 支払い; 決済 sounds more technical and is common in online shops and in business. Beware of the homophone 決裁 \"approval by a superior\" (部長の決裁を仰ぐ).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"為替レートの急変動が企業業績に影響している。","romaji":"Kawase rēto no kyū hendō ga kigyō gyōseki ni eikyō shite iru.","german":"Sudden fluctuations in the exchange rate are affecting corporate performance.","furigana":"かわせ|きゅうへんどう|きぎょうぎょうせき|えいきょう"},{"japanese":"両替する前に、今日の為替レートを確認した。","romaji":"Ryōgae suru mae ni, kyō no kawase rēto o kakunin shita.","german":"Before exchanging money, I checked today's exchange rate.","furigana":"りょうがえ|まえ|きょう|かわせ|かくにん"},{"japanese":"空港より市内の銀行のほうが為替レートがいい。","romaji":"Kūkō yori shinai no ginkō no hō ga kawase rēto ga ii.","german":"The exchange rate at banks in the city is better than at the airport.","furigana":"くうこう|しない|ぎんこう|かわせ"}],"notes":"為替レート is the everyday expression for the exchange rate; in news and technical texts one also hears 為替相場 or, specifically, 円相場. 為替 alone means \"foreign exchange, money transfer\" and has the irregular reading かわせ. Typical combinations: 為替レートが変動する, 為替レートを確認する, 有利な為替レートで両替する. A rising yen is called 円高, a falling one 円安.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"造幣局で記念硬貨が製造されている。","romaji":"Zōheikyoku de kinen kōka ga seizō sarete iru.","german":"Commemorative coins are produced at the mint.","furigana":"ぞうへいきょく|きねんこうか|せいぞう"},{"japanese":"大阪の造幣局では、毎年春に桜の通り抜けが行われる。","romaji":"Ōsaka no Zōheikyoku de wa, maitoshi haru ni sakura no tōrinuke ga okonawareru.","german":"At the mint in Osaka, a cherry blossom walk-through is held every spring.","furigana":"おおさか|ぞうへいきょく|まいとしはる|さくら|とお|ぬ|おこな"},{"japanese":"日本の造幣技術は、偽造防止の面で高く評価されている。","romaji":"Nihon no zōhei gijutsu wa, gizō bōshi no men de takaku hyōka sarete iru.","german":"Japan's minting technology is highly regarded for its protection against counterfeiting.","furigana":"にほん|ぞうへいぎじゅつ|ぎぞうぼうし|めん|たか|ひょうか"}],"notes":"造幣 means the minting or production of coin money and occurs almost only in compounds, above all in 造幣局, Japan's state mint, based in Osaka. Other combinations are 造幣技術 and 造幣事業. Banknotes are not printed by the 造幣局 but by the 国立印刷局. The word is literary; in everyday speech one rather says 硬貨を作る or 貨幣の製造.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"国債の利回りが過去最低を記録した。","romaji":"Kokusai no rimawari ga kako saitei wo kiroku shita.","german":"The yield on government bonds hit a record low.","furigana":"こくさい|りまわ|かこさいてい|きろく"},{"japanese":"この投資信託の利回りは年三パーセントほどだ。","romaji":"Kono tōshi shintaku no rimawari wa nen san pāsento hodo da.","german":"The yield on this investment fund is about three percent a year.","furigana":"とうししんたく|りまわ|ねんさん"},{"japanese":"利回りの高さだけで商品を選ぶのは危険だ。","romaji":"Rimawari no takasa dake de shōhin o erabu no wa kiken da.","german":"It is dangerous to choose a product based only on the height of its yield.","furigana":"りまわ|たか|しょうひん|えら|きけん"}],"notes":"利回り is the yield, that is, the annual return in relation to the capital invested, usually in percent: 利回りが高い・低い, 利回りが上昇する, 配当利回り \"dividend yield\", 国債の利回り. To be distinguished from it are 金利 or 利率 \"interest rate\" and 利息・利子 \"interest as an amount\". For bonds, price and 利回り move in opposite directions. The spelling with okurigana り is standard.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"年金基金の運用成績が悪化している。","romaji":"Nenkin kikin no un'yō seiseki ga akka shite iru.","german":"The investment results of the pension fund have worsened.","furigana":"ねんきんききん|うんようせいせき|あっか"},{"japanese":"規則は状況に応じて柔軟に運用すべきだ。","romaji":"Kisoku wa jōkyō ni ōjite jūnan ni un'yō subeki da.","german":"Rules should be applied flexibly depending on the situation.","furigana":"きそく|じょうきょう|おう|じゅうなん|うんよう"},{"japanese":"新しいシステムの運用は来月から始まる。","romaji":"Atarashii shisutemu no un'yō wa raigetsu kara hajimaru.","german":"The new system goes into operation next month.","furigana":"あたら|うんよう|らいげつ|はじ"}],"notes":"運用 means to put something to practical use and handle it; as a transitive する-verb: 資金・資産を運用する \"to invest and manage money\", 規則・法律を運用する \"to apply, implement rules\" and システムを運用する \"to operate a system\". To be distinguished from it are 利用 \"to use\", 適用 \"to apply a rule to a case\" and 応用 \"to transfer knowledge to something new\". Common in 資産運用, 運用成績 and 運用開始.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ポートフォリオの分散によりリスクを軽減する。","romaji":"Pōtoforio no bunsan ni yori risuku wo keigen suru.","german":"Durch eine Streuung des Portfolios wird das Risiko verringert.","furigana":"ぶんさん|けいげん"},{"japanese":"年に一度はポートフォリオを見直したほうがいい。","romaji":"Nen ni ichido wa pōtoforio o minaoshita hō ga ii.","german":"Man sollte sein Portfolio mindestens einmal im Jahr überprüfen.","furigana":"ねん|いちど|みなお"},{"japanese":"デザイナーの面接には、ポートフォリオを持参してください。","romaji":"Dezainā no mensetsu ni wa, pōtoforio o jisan shite kudasai.","german":"Bitte bringen Sie zum Vorstellungsgespräch als Designer Ihre Arbeitsmappe mit.","furigana":"めんせつ|じさん"}],"notes":"ポートフォリオ ist im Finanzwesen die Zusammensetzung aller Anlagen eines Investors: ポートフォリオを組む „ein Portfolio zusammenstellen“, ポートフォリオを見直す „überprüfen“, ポートフォリオに組み入れる „ins Portfolio aufnehmen“. Typisch ist die Verbindung mit 分散 „Streuung“. Daneben bezeichnet das Wort wie im Deutschen die Arbeitsmappe von Designern, Fotografen oder Architekten bei Bewerbungen.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"デリバティブ取引のリスク管理が重要だ。","romaji":"Deribatibu torihiki no risuku kanri ga jūyō da.","german":"Risk management in derivatives trading is important.","furigana":"とりひき|かんり|じゅうよう"},{"japanese":"デリバティブで巨額の損失を出した銀行もある。","romaji":"Deribatibu de kyogaku no sonshitsu o dashita ginkō mo aru.","german":"Some banks have suffered huge losses on derivatives.","furigana":"きょがく|そんしつ|だ|ぎんこう"},{"japanese":"個人投資家がデリバティブに手を出すのは危険だと言われる。","romaji":"Kojin tōshika ga deribatibu ni te o dasu no wa kiken da to iwareru.","german":"It is said to be dangerous for individual investors to get involved in derivatives.","furigana":"こじんとうしか|て|だ|きけん|い"}],"notes":"デリバティブ are financial products whose value is derived from an underlying asset such as stocks, interest rates or currencies; the Japanese technical term is 金融派生商品. Typical combinations: デリバティブ取引, デリバティブ商品, デリバティブで損失を出す. Derivatives include 先物 \"futures\", オプション and スワップ, among others. The word belongs to business and news language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ストックオプションを役員に付与する。","romaji":"Sutokku opushon wo yakuin ni fuyo suru.","german":"Stock options are granted to executives.","furigana":"やくいん|ふよ"},{"japanese":"カーナビはオプションで、別料金がかかります。","romaji":"Kānabi wa opushon de, betsu ryōkin ga kakarimasu.","german":"The car navigation system is an option and costs extra.","furigana":"べつりょうきん"},{"japanese":"プットオプションを買って、株価の下落に備えた。","romaji":"Putto opushon o katte, kabuka no geraku ni sonaeta.","german":"I bought put options to hedge against falling share prices.","furigana":"か|かぶか|げらく|そな"}],"notes":"オプション has two areas of use. In everyday life it is an extra that can be chosen for an additional charge, for example on cars or trips: オプションで付ける, オプション料金, オプショナルツアー. In finance it is the right to buy or sell an underlying asset at a fixed price: コールオプション, プットオプション, ストックオプション, オプション取引. For \"choice, alternative\" in the general sense one usually says 選択肢.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"金利スワップにより変動金利リスクを回避する。","romaji":"Kinri suwappu ni yori hendō kinri risuku wo kaihi suru.","german":"Avoid the risk of floating interest rates through an interest rate swap.","furigana":"きんり|へんどうきんり|かいひ"},{"japanese":"両国の中央銀行が通貨スワップ協定を結んだ。","romaji":"Ryōkoku no chūō ginkō ga tsūka suwappu kyōtei o musunda.","german":"The central banks of both countries concluded a currency swap agreement.","furigana":"りょうこく|ちゅうおうぎんこう|つうか|きょうてい|むす"},{"japanese":"変動金利を固定金利にスワップして、支払額を安定させた。","romaji":"Hendō kinri o kotei kinri ni suwappu shite, shiharaigaku o antei saseta.","german":"By swapping floating rates for fixed rates, payment amounts were stabilized.","furigana":"へんどうきんり|こていきんり|しはらいがく|あんてい"}],"notes":"スワップ is in finance an exchange transaction in which two parties swap payment streams: 金利スワップ \"interest rate swap\", 通貨スワップ \"currency swap\" (also as an agreement between central banks). As a する-verb: 変動金利を固定金利にスワップする. In foreign exchange trading one also knows the スワップポイント (interest rate differential). For ordinary \"swapping\" in everyday life, one says 交換する or 取り替える, not スワップ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"為替リスクをヘッジするために先物取引を利用する。","romaji":"Kawase risuku wo hejji suru tame ni sakimono torihiki wo riyō suru.","german":"Use futures trading to hedge exchange rate risk.","furigana":"かわせ|さきものとりひき|りよう"},{"japanese":"為替ヘッジ付きの投資信託を選んだ。","romaji":"Kawase hejji tsuki no tōshi shintaku o eranda.","german":"I chose an investment fund with currency hedging.","furigana":"かわせ|つ|とうししんたく|えら"},{"japanese":"リスクヘッジとして、複数の仕入れ先と契約している。","romaji":"Risuku hejji to shite, fukusū no shiiresaki to keiyaku shite iru.","german":"As a hedge against risk, we have contracts with multiple suppliers.","furigana":"ふくすう|しい|さき|けいやく"}],"notes":"ヘッジ is protection against the risk of loss; as a transitive する-verb: 為替リスクをヘッジする. Common combinations are 為替ヘッジ, ヘッジ付き \"currency-hedged\" and ヘッジファンド. In business Japanese, リスクヘッジ has also become established in the general sense of \"precaution against risks\", for example with suppliers or personnel. For funds one distinguishes 為替ヘッジあり \"with currency hedging\" and 為替ヘッジなし \"without\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"市場間の価格差を利用した裁定取引が行われている。","romaji":"Shijōkan no kakakusa wo riyō shita saitei torihiki ga okonawarete iru.","german":"Arbitrage trades exploiting price differences between markets are being carried out.","furigana":"しじょうかん|かかくさ|りよう|さいていとりひき|おこな"},{"japanese":"価格差は、裁定取引によってすぐに解消される。","romaji":"Kakakusa wa, saitei torihiki ni yotte sugu ni kaishō sareru.","german":"Price differences are quickly eliminated by arbitrage.","furigana":"かかくさ|さいていとりひき|かいしょう"},{"japanese":"コンピューターを使った高速の裁定取引が増えている。","romaji":"Konpyūtā o tsukatta kōsoku no saitei torihiki ga fuete iru.","german":"High-speed computer-driven arbitrage is increasing.","furigana":"つか|こうそく|さいていとりひき|ふ"}],"notes":"裁定取引 is arbitrage: one exploits price differences of the same good between markets by buying cheap and selling dear at the same time. 裁定 actually means \"decision, arbitral ruling\"; an arbitration proceeding in the legal sense, however, is called 仲裁. In stock exchange jargon one also hears アービトラージ or the shortened 裁定, e.g. 裁定買い・裁定売り. The word is technical language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"信用取引による損失が膨らんでいる。","romaji":"Shin'yō torihiki ni yoru sonshitsu ga fukurande iru.","german":"Losses from margin trading are mounting.","furigana":"しんようとりひき|そんしつ|ふく"},{"japanese":"信用取引では、手持ちの資金の約三倍まで取引できる。","romaji":"Shin'yō torihiki de wa, temochi no shikin no yaku sanbai made torihiki dekiru.","german":"With margin trading, you can trade up to about three times your own capital.","furigana":"しんようとりひき|ても|しきん|やくさんばい|とりひき"},{"japanese":"初心者はいきなり信用取引を始めないほうがいい。","romaji":"Shoshinsha wa ikinari shin'yō torihiki o hajimenai hō ga ii.","german":"Beginners should not start margin trading right away.","furigana":"しょしんしゃ|しんようとりひき|はじ"}],"notes":"信用取引 is securities trading on credit: one deposits collateral with the broker (委託保証金) and borrows money or shares in order to trade more than one's own capital allows. Buying on credit is 信用買い, short selling 信用売り or 空売り; if the price falls sharply, a 追証 \"margin call\" threatens. In contrast stands 現物取引 \"cash trading with one's own money\". The general term \"credit transaction\" outside the stock market is rather 掛け取引.","levelNote":"Compound: 信用 (N3) + 取引 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"空売り規制が株価の急落を防いだ。","romaji":"Karauri kisei ga kabuka no kyūraku o fuseida.","german":"Short-selling restrictions prevented a sharp fall in share prices.","furigana":"からう|きせい|かぶか|きゅうらく|ふせ"},{"japanese":"値下がりを見込んで、その銘柄を空売りした。","romaji":"Nesagari o mikonde, sono meigara o karauri shita.","german":"Expecting the price to fall, he sold the stock short.","furigana":"ねさ|みこ|めいがら|からう"},{"japanese":"空売りで損をするのは、株価が予想に反して上がったときだ。","romaji":"Karauri de son o suru no wa, kabuka ga yosō ni hanshite agatta toki da.","german":"You lose on a short sale when the share price rises contrary to expectations.","furigana":"からう|そん|かぶか|よそう|はん|あ"}],"notes":"空売り (read から, not くう) is \"short selling\": one sells borrowed shares in the expectation of falling prices and buys them back more cheaply later (買い戻し). As a する-verb the security takes を: 株を空売りする. Common combinations: 空売り規制, 空売り比率, 空売りを仕掛ける. The counterpart is 空買い. A technical word from the stock market and business news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仕手筋による株価操作が問題になっている。","romaji":"Shitesuji ni yoru kabuka sōsa ga mondai ni natte iru.","german":"Price manipulation by speculators has become a problem.","furigana":"してすじ|かぶかそうさ|もんだい"},{"japanese":"二つのグループの間で、激しい仕手戦が繰り広げられた。","romaji":"Futatsu no gurūpu no aida de, hageshii shitesen ga kurihirogerareta.","german":"A fierce speculative battle was fought between the two groups.","furigana":"ふた|あいだ|はげ|してせん|く|ひろ"},{"japanese":"能では、主人公を演じる役を仕手という。","romaji":"Nō de wa, shujinkō o enjiru yaku o shite to iu.","german":"In Noh, the role of the protagonist is called shite.","furigana":"のう|しゅじんこう|えん|やく|して"}],"notes":"仕手 literally means \"the one who acts\". On the stock exchange it denotes big speculators who deliberately move individual share prices with a lot of capital; mostly in compounds such as 仕手筋 \"speculator circles\", 仕手株 and 仕手戦 \"speculative battle\". In Noh theater, 仕手 is the main role, usually written in katakana シテ; the supporting role is called ワキ. The stock market sense has a negative tint and belongs to the business press.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"相場操縦は金融商品取引法で禁止されている。","romaji":"Sōba sōjū wa kin'yū shōhin torihikihō de kinshi sarete iru.","german":"Market manipulation is prohibited under the Financial Instruments and Exchange Act.","furigana":"そうばそうじゅう|きんゆうしょうひんとりひきほう|きんし"},{"japanese":"監視委員会は、相場操縦の疑いで個人投資家を告発した。","romaji":"Kanshi iinkai wa, sōba sōjū no utagai de kojin tōshika o kokuhatsu shita.","german":"The surveillance commission filed a criminal complaint against an individual investor on suspicion of market manipulation.","furigana":"かんしいいんかい|そうばそうじゅう|うたが|こじんとうしか|こくはつ"},{"japanese":"見せかけの大量注文で株価を動かすのも、相場操縦に当たる。","romaji":"Misekake no tairyō chūmon de kabuka o ugokasu no mo, sōba sōjū ni ataru.","german":"Moving the share price with fake large orders also constitutes market manipulation.","furigana":"み|たいりょうちゅうもん|かぶか|うご|そうばそうじゅう|あ"}],"notes":"相場操縦 is the legal technical term for market manipulation, that is, artificially influencing prices, for example through fake orders or collusive trades; 相場 \"market price, quotation\" + 操縦 \"to steer\". It is prohibited under the 金融商品取引法 (until 2007, the 証券取引法). In the press and in everyday speech 株価操作 is more common. Spreading false rumors to influence prices is legally a separate offense called 風説の流布.","levelNote":"Compound: 相場 (N1) + 操縦 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"インサイダー取引の摘発が強化されている。","romaji":"Insaidā torihiki no tekihatsu ga kyōka sarete iru.","german":"Prosecution of insider trading is being stepped up.","furigana":"とりひき|てきはつ|きょうか"},{"japanese":"未公表の情報で自社の株を売買すると、インサイダー取引になる。","romaji":"Mikōhyō no jōhō de jisha no kabu o baibai suru to, insaidā torihiki ni naru.","german":"Trading your own company's shares using undisclosed information constitutes insider trading.","furigana":"みこうひょう|じょうほう|じしゃ|かぶ|ばいばい|とりひき"},{"japanese":"元役員がインサイダー取引の疑いで逮捕された。","romaji":"Moto yakuin ga insaidā torihiki no utagai de taiho sareta.","german":"A former executive was arrested on suspicion of insider trading.","furigana":"もとやくいん|とりひき|うたが|たいほ"}],"notes":"インサイダー取引 is trading in securities by exploiting non-public company information (未公表の重要事実); the statute speaks of 内部者取引. Typical combinations: インサイダー取引の疑いで逮捕される, インサイダー取引を摘発する, インサイダー取引規制. In Japan it is also punishable to pass such information on to others in order to encourage them to trade.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"時価総額で世界トップクラスの企業となった。","romaji":"Jika sōgaku de sekai toppu kurasu no kigyō to natta.","german":"It has become one of the world's top companies by market capitalization.","furigana":"じかそうがく|せかい|きぎょう"},{"japanese":"時価総額は、株価に発行済み株式数を掛けて計算する。","romaji":"Jika sōgaku wa, kabuka ni hakkōzumi kabushikisū o kakete keisan suru.","german":"Market capitalization is calculated by multiplying the share price by the number of issued shares.","furigana":"じかそうがく|かぶか|はっこうず|かぶしきすう|か|けいさん"},{"japanese":"株価の急落で、時価総額が一日で一兆円も減った。","romaji":"Kabuka no kyūraku de, jika sōgaku ga ichinichi de itchō en mo hetta.","german":"Because of the sharp fall in share prices, market capitalization shrank by one trillion yen in a single day.","furigana":"かぶか|きゅうらく|じかそうがく|いちにち|いっちょうえん|へ"}],"notes":"時価総額 is the market capitalization or market value of a company: share price x number of issued shares. 時価 means \"current market price\" (on a sushi restaurant menu also \"market price of the day\"), 総額 \"total amount\". Typical are 時価総額ランキング, 時価総額が〜兆円に達する and 時価総額で世界一. The term is also used for entire markets, e.g. 東証の時価総額.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"株価指数が年初来高値を更新した。","romaji":"Kabuka shisū ga nenshorai takane o kōshin shita.","german":"The stock index hit a new year-to-date high.","furigana":"かぶかしすう|ねんしょらいたかね|こうしん"},{"japanese":"TOPIXは日本を代表する株価指数の一つだ。","romaji":"TOPIX wa Nihon o daihyō suru kabuka shisū no hitotsu da.","german":"TOPIX is one of the leading stock indexes of Japan.","furigana":"にほん|だいひょう|かぶかしすう|ひと"},{"japanese":"株価指数に連動する投資信託を毎月積み立てている。","romaji":"Kabuka shisū ni rendō suru tōshi shintaku o maitsuki tsumitatete iru.","german":"I save into a fund tracking a stock index every month.","furigana":"かぶかしすう|れんどう|とうししんたく|まいつきつ|た"}],"notes":"株価指数 is a \"stock index\" that summarizes the price movement of many shares in a single number. The best known in Japan are 日経平均株価 and TOPIX (東証株価指数). Typical verbs: 株価指数が上昇する/下落する, 高値を更新する; 株価指数に連動する means \"tracking an index\" (index fund). 指数 alone also means \"index\" in general (物価指数) or, in mathematics, \"exponent\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"出来高が急増し、市場の活況を示している。","romaji":"Dekidaka ga kyūzō shi, shijō no kakkyō wo shimeshite iru.","german":"Trading volume surged, indicating a lively market.","furigana":"できだか|きゅうぞう|しじょう|かっきょう|しめ"},{"japanese":"今年は天候に恵まれ、米の出来高が平年を上回った。","romaji":"Kotoshi wa tenkō ni megumare, kome no dekidaka ga heinen o uwamawatta.","german":"Thanks to good weather this year, the rice harvest exceeded the average year.","furigana":"ことし|てんこう|めぐ|こめ|できだか|へいねん|うわまわ"},{"japanese":"この工場では、給料は出来高払いだ。","romaji":"Kono kōjō de wa, kyūryō wa dekidakabarai da.","german":"In this factory, wages are paid by piecework.","furigana":"こうじょう|きゅうりょう|できだかばら"}],"notes":"出来高 is the quantity actually achieved: on the stock exchange the trading volume, that is, the number of shares traded (出来高が増える・膨らむ・細る), in agriculture the harvest amount (米の出来高). With 払い or 制 it denotes performance-based or piece-rate pay: 出来高払い, 出来高制. To be distinguished from trading volume is 売買代金, the turnover in money.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"値嵩株の動向が指数に大きな影響を与える。","romaji":"Negasakabu no dōkō ga shisū ni ōkina eikyō wo ataeru.","german":"The movement of high-priced stocks has a large influence on the index.","furigana":"ねがさかぶ|どうこう|しすう|おお|えいきょう|あた"},{"japanese":"値嵩株は、一単元を買うだけでも数百万円かかることがある。","romaji":"Negasakabu wa, ichi tangen o kau dake de mo sūhyakuman en kakaru koto ga aru.","german":"With high-priced stocks, even a single trading unit can cost several million yen.","furigana":"ねがさかぶ|いちたんげん|か|すうひゃくまんえん"},{"japanese":"今日は値嵩株が買われ、日経平均を押し上げた。","romaji":"Kyō wa negasakabu ga kaware, Nikkei heikin o oshiageta.","german":"Today high-priced stocks were bought, pushing up the Nikkei average.","furigana":"きょう|ねがさかぶ|か|にっけいへいきん|お|あ"}],"notes":"値嵩株 are shares with a high price per unit; 値 \"price\" + 嵩 \"bulk, volume\" (cf. 値が嵩む \"to become expensive\"). Because the Nikkei 225 is price-weighted, a few 値嵩株 move the index particularly strongly. The opposite is 低位株 \"low-priced stocks\". Note: 値嵩株 says nothing about the quality of the company; first-class blue chips are called 優良株.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"優良株を中心にポートフォリオを構成する。","romaji":"Yūryōkabu wo chūshin ni pōtoforio wo kōsei suru.","german":"Build a portfolio centered on blue-chip stocks.","furigana":"ゆうりょうかぶ|ちゅうしん|こうせい"},{"japanese":"長期投資なら、業績の安定した優良株がおすすめだ。","romaji":"Chōki tōshi nara, gyōseki no antei shita yūryōkabu ga osusume da.","german":"For long-term investing, blue chips with stable results are recommended.","furigana":"ちょうきとうし|ぎょうせき|あんてい|ゆうりょうかぶ"},{"japanese":"不況の時でも、優良株は比較的値下がりしにくい。","romaji":"Fukyō no toki de mo, yūryōkabu wa hikakuteki nesagari shinikui.","german":"Even in a recession, blue chips are relatively unlikely to fall in price.","furigana":"ふきょう|とき|ゆうりょうかぶ|ひかくてきねさ"}],"notes":"優良株 are shares of solid, profitable large companies, that is, blue chips; 優良 means \"excellent, first-class\" (cf. 優良企業, 優良ドライバー). In stock market jargon one also hears ブルーチップ or 主力株. Not to be confused with 値嵩株, which only means a high price per share, and with the risky 仕手株. Typical: 優良株に投資する, 優良株中心のポートフォリオ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仕手株に手を出して大損した。","romaji":"Shitekabu ni te wo dashite ōzon shita.","german":"I got involved in speculative stocks and lost a lot.","furigana":"してかぶ|て|だ|おおぞん"},{"japanese":"仕手株は短期間で急騰し、その後急落することが多い。","romaji":"Shitekabu wa tankikan de kyūtō shi, sono ato kyūraku suru koto ga ōi.","german":"Speculative stocks often soar in a short time and then plunge.","furigana":"してかぶ|たんきかん|きゅうとう|あときゅうらく|おお"},{"japanese":"ネットの掲示板で、仕手株の情報が出回っている。","romaji":"Netto no keijiban de, shitekabu no jōhō ga demawatte iru.","german":"Tips on speculative stocks are circulating on online message boards.","furigana":"けいじばん|してかぶ|じょうほう|でまわ"}],"notes":"仕手株 are shares deliberately driven up by big speculators (仕手筋); mostly small, thinly traded stocks with extreme price swings. The phrase 仕手株に手を出す \"to get involved in speculative stocks\" is typical and sounds like a warning. The opposite term is 優良株. Similar but more neutral is 投機株 \"speculative stock\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新規公開株の抽選に応募した。","romaji":"Shinki kōkai kabu no chūsen ni ōbo shita.","german":"I applied for the lottery for IPO shares.","furigana":"しんきこうかいかぶ|ちゅうせん|おうぼ"},{"japanese":"その会社は来年三月に新規公開を予定している。","romaji":"Sono kaisha wa rainen sangatsu ni shinki kōkai o yotei shite iru.","german":"The company plans to go public next March.","furigana":"かいしゃ|らいねんさんがつ|しんきこうかい|よてい"},{"japanese":"新規公開した株が、初日に公開価格の二倍の値をつけた。","romaji":"Shinki kōkai shita kabu ga, shonichi ni kōkai kakaku no nibai no ne o tsuketa.","german":"The newly listed stock traded at twice the offering price on the first day.","furigana":"しんきこうかい|かぶ|しょにち|こうかいかかく|にばい|ね"}],"notes":"新規公開 is the short form of 新規株式公開, the IPO; in the media one often also reads simply IPO. Shares from an IPO are called 新規公開株; in Japan their allocation to individual investors is often done by lottery (抽選). As a する-verb: 株式を新規公開する. Closely related is 新規上場, which emphasizes admission to exchange trading. Outside the financial world, 新規公開 can also simply mean \"first release\" (e.g. of a website).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"東京証券取引所に上場を果たした。","romaji":"Tōkyō shōken torihikijo ni jōjō wo hatashita.","german":"The company achieved a listing on the Tokyo Stock Exchange.","furigana":"とうきょうしょうけんとりひきじょ|じょうじょう|は"},{"japanese":"上場企業には、決算を公表する義務がある。","romaji":"Jōjō kigyō ni wa, kessan o kōhyō suru gimu ga aru.","german":"Listed companies are obliged to publish their financial results.","furigana":"じょうじょうきぎょう|けっさん|こうひょう|ぎむ"},{"japanese":"上場すれば、会社の知名度も信用も上がる。","romaji":"Jōjō sureba, kaisha no chimeido mo shin'yō mo agaru.","german":"Once listed, a company's name recognition and credibility both rise.","furigana":"じょうじょう|かいしゃ|ちめいど|しんよう|あ"}],"notes":"上場 is the listing on a stock exchange; as a する-verb: 会社が東証に上場する \"to go public\" or 株式を上場する. Typical are 上場企業 \"listed company\", 上場を果たす, 上場を目指す and 東証プライム市場に上場. The second meaning \"to stage a play\" is rare and formal; for that one normally says 上演. The opposite is 上場廃止.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"経営悪化により上場廃止となった。","romaji":"Keiei akka ni yori jōjō haishi to natta.","german":"It was delisted because of deteriorating business conditions.","furigana":"けいえいあっか|じょうじょうはいし"},{"japanese":"上場廃止が決まると、株は整理銘柄に指定される。","romaji":"Jōjō haishi ga kimaru to, kabu wa seiri meigara ni shitei sareru.","german":"Once a delisting is decided, the stock is designated as a security on liquidation watch.","furigana":"じょうじょうはいし|き|かぶ|せいりめいがら|してい"},{"japanese":"経営陣による買収で、自ら上場廃止を選ぶ会社もある。","romaji":"Keieijin ni yoru baishū de, mizukara jōjō haishi o erabu kaisha mo aru.","german":"Some companies choose to delist voluntarily through a management buyout.","furigana":"けいえいじん|ばいしゅう|みずか|じょうじょうはいし|えら|かいしゃ"}],"notes":"上場廃止 is the removal of a stock from exchange trading (delisting). Usual are 上場廃止になる・となる, 上場廃止が決まる and 上場廃止基準. Before delisting, a stock in Japan is classified as 整理銘柄 and is still traded for a while. Besides forced delisting due to bankruptcy or violations, there is voluntary delisting, for example after a takeover by management (MBO) or a parent company.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"公募増資により広く資金を集めた。","romaji":"Kōbo zōshi ni yori hiroku shikin wo atsumeta.","german":"Funds were collected widely through a public-offering capital increase.","furigana":"こうぼぞうし|ひろ|しきん|あつ"},{"japanese":"市の新しいキャラクターの名前を公募することになった。","romaji":"Shi no atarashii kyarakutā no namae o kōbo suru koto ni natta.","german":"It was decided to hold a public call for names for the city's new mascot.","furigana":"し|あたら|なまえ|こうぼ"},{"japanese":"この大学では、教員を公募で採用している。","romaji":"Kono daigaku de wa, kyōin o kōbo de saiyō shite iru.","german":"This university hires faculty through open recruitment.","furigana":"だいがく|きょういん|こうぼ|さいよう"}],"notes":"公募 first means \"to invite publicly, to seek publicly\"; as a transitive する-verb: 作品・職員・名前を公募する, 公募で選ぶ. In finance it is the public offering of securities to a broad circle of investors: 公募増資, 公募価格, 公募投信. The opposite term is 私募 \"private placement\". In contrast to the general 募集 \"to recruit, to seek\", 公募 emphasizes the public call, open to everyone.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私募債を発行して機関投資家から資金を調達した。","romaji":"Shibosai wo hakkō shite kikan tōshika kara shikin wo chōtatsu shita.","german":"Funds were raised from institutional investors by issuing privately placed bonds.","furigana":"しぼさい|はっこう|きかんとうしか|しきん|ちょうたつ"},{"japanese":"私募ファンドは、一般の個人投資家には販売されない。","romaji":"Shibo fando wa, ippan no kojin tōshika ni wa hanbai sarenai.","german":"Privately placed funds are not sold to ordinary individual investors.","furigana":"しぼ|いっぱん|こじんとうしか|はんばい"},{"japanese":"公募ではなく私募にすれば、発行の手続きが簡単になる。","romaji":"Kōbo de wa naku shibo ni sureba, hakkō no tetsuzuki ga kantan ni naru.","german":"With a private placement instead of a public offering, the issuance procedure becomes simpler.","furigana":"こうぼ|しぼ|はっこう|てつづ|かんたん"}],"notes":"私募 is a private placement: securities are not offered publicly but only to a small circle or professional investors. Typical in compounds such as 私募債, 私募ファンド and 私募投信. The opposite term is 公募. Because the strict disclosure obligations of a public offering do not apply, the procedure is simpler; in return, the securities are usually not accessible to ordinary individual investors.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"格付け機関が国債の格下げを発表した。","romaji":"Kakuzuke kikan ga kokusai no kakusage wo happyō shita.","german":"The rating agency announced a downgrade of the government bonds.","furigana":"かくづ|きかん|こくさい|かくさ|はっぴょう"},{"japanese":"格付けの高い会社ほど、低い金利でお金を借りられる。","romaji":"Kakuzuke no takai kaisha hodo, hikui kinri de okane o karirareru.","german":"The higher a company's rating, the lower the interest rate at which it can borrow money.","furigana":"かくづ|たか|かいしゃ|ひく|きんり|かね|か"},{"japanese":"その店はミシュランの格付けで三つ星を獲得した。","romaji":"Sono mise wa Mishuran no kakuzuke de mittsuboshi o kakutoku shita.","german":"The restaurant earned three stars in the Michelin rating.","furigana":"みせ|かくづ|みっ|ぼし|かくとく"}],"notes":"格付け is classification by rank or quality; as a transitive する-verb: 〜を格付けする. In finance it means the rating of bonds, countries or companies by 格付け機関・格付け会社 \"rating agencies\": 格付けが高い・低い, 格付けを引き下げる. It is also used for rankings of all kinds, such as the stars of the Michelin Guide. Upgrade and downgrade are called 格上げ and 格下げ; the spelling 格付 without け occurs in technical texts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"格下げにより社債の利回りが急上昇した。","romaji":"Kakusage ni yori shasai no rimawari ga kyūjōshō shita.","german":"The downgrade caused the yield on corporate bonds to soar.","furigana":"かくさ|しゃさい|りまわ|きゅうじょうしょう"},{"japanese":"その会社は業績悪化を理由に、二段階格下げされた。","romaji":"Sono kaisha wa gyōseki akka o riyū ni, nidankai kakusage sareta.","german":"The company was downgraded by two notches because of worsening results.","furigana":"かいしゃ|ぎょうせきあっか|りゆう|にだんかいかくさ"},{"japanese":"部長から課長に格下げされるなんて、納得できない。","romaji":"Buchō kara kachō ni kakusage sareru nante, nattoku dekinai.","german":"I can't accept being demoted from department head to section chief.","furigana":"ぶちょう|かちょう|かくさ|なっとく"}],"notes":"格下げ is a lowering in rank or rating; mostly passive: 〜が格下げされる, with the target in に (BBBに格下げ) or the extent (一段階格下げ). In finance it refers to ratings, in general to status and standing. For the demotion of people at work, 降格 is the usual, more factual term; 格下げ sounds more like an insult here. Opposite: 格上げ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"格上げを受けて株価が上昇した。","romaji":"Kakuage wo ukete kabuka ga jōshō shita.","german":"Following the upgrade, the share price rose.","furigana":"かくあ|う|かぶか|じょうしょう"},{"japanese":"この寺の本堂は、国宝に格上げされた。","romaji":"Kono tera no hondō wa, kokuhō ni kakuage sareta.","german":"The main hall of this temple was upgraded to a national treasure.","furigana":"てら|ほんどう|こくほう|かくあ"},{"japanese":"その大会は、今年から国際大会に格上げされた。","romaji":"Sono taikai wa, kotoshi kara kokusai taikai ni kakuage sareta.","german":"The tournament was upgraded to an international competition this year.","furigana":"たいかい|ことし|こくさいたいかい|かくあ"}],"notes":"格上げ is a raising in rank or rating, mostly passive: 〜に格上げされる. Besides ratings in finance (格付けの格上げ), it is used for status upgrades of all kinds: cultural properties, competitions, government agencies (庁から省への格上げ). For promotions of people, one says 昇進 or 昇格 instead. Opposite: 格下げ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ヘッジファンドが市場に大きな影響を及ぼしている。","romaji":"Hejji fando ga shijō ni ōkina eikyō wo oyoboshite iru.","german":"Hedge funds exert a great influence on the market.","furigana":"しじょう|おお|えいきょう|およ"},{"japanese":"毎月一万円ずつ、インデックスファンドに積み立てている。","romaji":"Maitsuki ichiman en zutsu, indekkusu fando ni tsumitatete iru.","german":"Every month I put 10,000 yen into an index fund.","furigana":"まいつきいちまんえん|つ|た"},{"japanese":"投資ファンドが、その老舗旅館を買収した。","romaji":"Tōshi fando ga, sono shinise ryokan o baishū shita.","german":"An investment fund acquired the long-established ryokan.","furigana":"とうし|しにせりょかん|ばいしゅう"}],"notes":"ファンド is a fund, that is, a pool of investors' money that is professionally invested: 投資ファンド, ヘッジファンド, インデックスファンド, 買収ファンド. For funds sold to the public, the formal term is 投資信託; in everyday speech, however, many people simply say ファンド. In the news, ファンド often denotes activist or takeover funds that buy companies. A charitable fund or foundation, on the other hand, is rather 基金.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"投資信託を通じて分散投資を行う。","romaji":"Tōshi shintaku wo tsūjite bunsan tōshi wo okonau.","german":"Diversified investing is done through investment trusts.","furigana":"とうししんたく|つう|ぶんさんとうし|おこな"},{"japanese":"銀行の窓口で投資信託を勧められた。","romaji":"Ginkō no madoguchi de tōshi shintaku o susumerareta.","german":"An investment trust was recommended to me at the bank counter.","furigana":"ぎんこう|まどぐち|とうししんたく|すす"},{"japanese":"投資信託は、元本が保証されていない。","romaji":"Tōshi shintaku wa, ganpon ga hoshō sarete inai.","german":"With investment trusts, the principal is not guaranteed.","furigana":"とうししんたく|がんぽん|ほしょう"}],"notes":"投資信託 is the investment fund for the general public: the money of many investors is pooled and invested by a fund company (運用会社). The usual short form is 投信. It is sold through banks and securities firms; typical combinations are 投資信託を購入する, 投資信託を積み立てる and 投資信託の基準価額 \"net asset value\". Colloquially, people often simply say ファンド.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"証券取引所が取引を一時停止した。","romaji":"Shōken torihikijo ga torihiki wo ichiji teishi shita.","german":"The stock exchange temporarily suspended trading.","furigana":"しょうけんとりひきじょ|とりひき|いちじていし"},{"japanese":"東京証券取引所は、日本橋の兜町にある。","romaji":"Tōkyō shōken torihikijo wa, Nihonbashi no Kabutochō ni aru.","german":"The Tokyo Stock Exchange is located in Kabutocho in Nihonbashi.","furigana":"とうきょうしょうけんとりひきじょ|にほんばし|かぶとちょう"},{"japanese":"証券取引所に上場するには、厳しい審査に通らなければならない。","romaji":"Shōken torihikijo ni jōjō suru ni wa, kibishii shinsa ni tōranakereba naranai.","german":"To be listed on a stock exchange, a company must pass a strict examination.","furigana":"しょうけんとりひきじょ|じょうじょう|きび|しんさ|とお"}],"notes":"証券取引所 is the securities exchange; 証券 \"securities\" + 取引所 \"trading place\". The best known is the 東京証券取引所, short 東証, in the Kabutocho district of Nihonbashi. In the news it is often shortened to 取引所. The ending 所 is mostly read じょ here, but one also hears しょ. Typical: 証券取引所に上場する, 証券取引所が取引を停止する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"注文が約定し、株式の売買が成立した。","romaji":"Chūmon ga yakujō shi, kabushiki no baibai ga seiritsu shita.","german":"The order was executed and the share trade was completed.","furigana":"ちゅうもん|やくじょう|かぶしき|ばいばい|せいりつ"},{"japanese":"代金の受け渡しは、約定日の二営業日後だ。","romaji":"Daikin no ukewatashi wa, yakujōbi no ni eigyōbi go da.","german":"Payment of the purchase price is two business days after the trade date.","furigana":"だいきん|う|わた|やくじょうび|にえいぎょうびご"},{"japanese":"ローンの利息は、約定した利率に基づいて計算される。","romaji":"Rōn no risoku wa, yakujō shita riritsu ni motozuite keisan sareru.","german":"Loan interest is calculated based on the agreed interest rate.","furigana":"りそく|やくじょう|りりつ|もと|けいさん"}],"notes":"約定 is a formal expression for \"agreement, contractual stipulation\": 約定書, 約定利率, 〜を約定する. In securities trading it means the execution of an order, that is, the completion of the trade; then intransitive: 注文が約定する. Important terms are 約定日 \"trade date\" and 約定価格 \"execution price\". In everyday speech one says 契約 for contracts and 約束 for promises.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"指値注文を出して希望価格での売買を待つ。","romaji":"Sashine chūmon wo dashite kibō kakaku de no baibai wo matsu.","german":"Place a limit order and wait for a trade at the desired price.","furigana":"さしねちゅうもん|だ|きぼうかかく|ばいばい|ま"},{"japanese":"千円の指値で買い注文を入れた。","romaji":"Sen en no sashine de kai chūmon o ireta.","german":"I placed a buy order with a limit of 1,000 yen.","furigana":"せんえん|さしね|か|ちゅうもん|い"},{"japanese":"株価が指値まで下がらず、注文は成立しなかった。","romaji":"Kabuka ga sashine made sagarazu, chūmon wa seiritsu shinakatta.","german":"The share price did not fall to the limit, so the order was not filled.","furigana":"かぶか|さしね|さ|ちゅうもん|せいりつ"}],"notes":"指値 is the limit price set by the investor; literally \"designated price\". Mostly in 指値注文 \"limit order\" or 〜円の指値で買う・売る. If the price is not reached, the trade does not take place. The opposite term is 成行 \"market order, at best\". The spelling 指し値 also occurs.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"成行注文ですぐに約定した。","romaji":"Nariyuki chūmon de sugu ni yakujō shita.","german":"It was executed immediately with a market order.","furigana":"なりゆきちゅうもん|やくじょう"},{"japanese":"成行で売ると、思ったより安い値段で約定することがある。","romaji":"Nariyuki de uru to, omotta yori yasui nedan de yakujō suru koto ga aru.","german":"If you sell at market, you sometimes get filled at a lower price than expected.","furigana":"なりゆき|う|おも|やす|ねだん|やくじょう"},{"japanese":"今は事の成り行きを見守るしかない。","romaji":"Ima wa koto no nariyuki o mimamoru shika nai.","german":"All we can do now is watch how things develop.","furigana":"いま|こと|な|ゆ|みまも"}],"notes":"成行 has two meanings. On the stock exchange it is the short form of 成行注文 \"market order\": the order is executed immediately at the current price (成行で買う・売る); the opposite term is 指値. In general, なりゆき means \"course, development, outcome\" and is then mostly written 成り行き: 事の成り行き, 成り行きに任せる \"to let things take their course\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"売買代金が一兆円を超える活況だった。","romaji":"Baibai daikin ga itchōen wo koeru kakkyō datta.","german":"The market was lively, with a trading value of over one trillion yen.","furigana":"ばいばいだいきん|いっちょうえん|こ|かっきょう"},{"japanese":"出来高と売買代金は、市場の活気を測る目安になる。","romaji":"Dekidaka to baibai daikin wa, shijō no kakki o hakaru meyasu ni naru.","german":"Trading volume and trading value are yardsticks of how lively a market is.","furigana":"できだか|ばいばいだいきん|しじょう|かっき|はか|めやす"},{"japanese":"土地の売買代金のうち、一割を手付金として契約時に支払った。","romaji":"Tochi no baibai daikin no uchi, ichiwari o tetsukekin to shite keiyakuji ni shiharatta.","german":"Of the purchase price of the land, ten percent was paid as a deposit at the time of contract.","furigana":"とち|ばいばいだいきん|いちわり||けいやくじ|しはら"}],"notes":"売買代金 is the monetary amount of a purchase or sale. On the stock exchange it denotes the trading value in yen, in contrast to 出来高, the number of shares traded; both are considered yardsticks of market activity. In contracts, for example for real estate, 売買代金 is simply the purchase price. Typical: 売買代金が膨らむ, 売買代金が〜兆円を超える.","levelNote":"Compound: 売買 (N2) + 代金 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大納会で日経平均株価が年初来高値を更新した。","romaji":"Dainōkai de Nikkei heikin kabuka ga nenshorai takane o kōshin shita.","german":"On the last trading day of the year, the Nikkei average hit a new year-to-date high.","furigana":"だいのうかい|にっけいへいきんかぶか|ねんしょらいたかね|こうしん"},{"japanese":"今年の大納会は12月30日に行われる。","romaji":"Kotoshi no dainōkai wa jūnigatsu sanjūnichi ni okonawareru.","german":"This year's last trading day is on December 30.","furigana":"ことし|だいのうかい|がつ|にち|おこな"},{"japanese":"大納会から大発会までの間、株の取引は休みになる。","romaji":"Dainōkai kara daihakkai made no aida, kabu no torihiki wa yasumi ni naru.","german":"Between the last trading day of the year and the first of the new year, stock trading is closed.","furigana":"だいのうかい|だいはっかい|あいだ|かぶ|とりひき|やす"}],"notes":"大納会 is the last trading day of the year on the stock exchange, normally December 30 in Japan; the counterpart is 大発会, the first trading day in January. Typical: 大納会を迎える, 大納会の終値 \"the year's closing price\". 納会 alone generally denotes the last meeting or celebration of a year or season. The word occurs almost only in business news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大発会は祝賀ムードの中で取引が始まった。","romaji":"Daihakkai wa shukuga mūdo no naka de torihiki ga hajimatta.","german":"On the first trading day of the year, trading began in a celebratory mood.","furigana":"だいはっかい|しゅくが|なか|とりひき|はじ"},{"japanese":"大発会には、晴れ着姿の女性たちが参加するのが恒例だ。","romaji":"Daihakkai ni wa, haregi sugata no josei-tachi ga sanka suru no ga kōrei da.","german":"It is customary for women in formal kimono to attend the first trading day of the year.","furigana":"だいはっかい|は|ぎすがた|じょせい|さんか|こうれい"},{"japanese":"大発会の日経平均は、三年ぶりに値上がりした。","romaji":"Daihakkai no Nikkei heikin wa, sannen buri ni neagari shita.","german":"The Nikkei average on the first trading day of the year rose for the first time in three years.","furigana":"だいはっかい|にっけいへいきん|さんねん|ねあ"}],"notes":"大発会 is the first trading day of the year on the Japanese stock exchanges, usually in early January; the counterpart on the last trading day of the year is called 大納会. At the Tokyo exchange there is a ceremony for it, at which women in formal kimono traditionally appear. In the news, reports focus mainly on the price movement: 大発会の日経平均. The word is stock market jargon and hardly used otherwise.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"転換社債を株式に転換する権利を行使した。","romaji":"Tenkan shasai wo kabushiki ni tenkan suru kenri wo kōshi shita.","german":"The right to convert the convertible bond into shares was exercised.","furigana":"てんかんしゃさい|かぶしき|てんかん|けんり|こうし"},{"japanese":"転換社債は、株価が上がれば株式に換えて利益を得られる。","romaji":"Tenkan shasai wa, kabuka ga agareba kabushiki ni kaete rieki o erareru.","german":"With a convertible bond, you can swap into shares when the price rises and make a profit.","furigana":"てんかんしゃさい|かぶか|あ|かぶしき|か|りえき|え"},{"japanese":"資金調達のため、転換社債を発行すると発表した。","romaji":"Shikin chōtatsu no tame, tenkan shasai o hakkō suru to happyō shita.","german":"To raise funds, the company announced it would issue convertible bonds.","furigana":"しきんちょうたつ|てんかんしゃさい|はっこう|はっぴょう"}],"notes":"転換社債 is the convertible bond: a corporate bond that the holder can exchange for shares at a fixed price; 転換 \"conversion\" + 社債 \"corporate bond\". Legally it is now called 転換社債型新株予約権付社債, but in practice people continue to speak of 転換社債 or, in short, CB. Typical: 転換社債を発行する, 株式に転換する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"劣後債は利回りが高いがリスクも大きい。","romaji":"Retsugosai wa rimawari ga takai ga risuku mo ōkii.","german":"Subordinated bonds have a high yield but also a high risk.","furigana":"れつごさい|りまわ|たか|おお"},{"japanese":"劣後債は、会社が破綻したとき返済の順位が後になる。","romaji":"Retsugosai wa, kaisha ga hatan shita toki hensai no jun'i ga ato ni naru.","german":"If the company goes bankrupt, subordinated bonds are repaid after other debts.","furigana":"れつごさい|かいしゃ|はたん|へんさい|じゅんい|あと"},{"japanese":"銀行は自己資本を増やすために劣後債を発行した。","romaji":"Ginkō wa jiko shihon o fuyasu tame ni retsugosai o hakkō shita.","german":"The bank issued subordinated bonds to increase its equity.","furigana":"ぎんこう|じこしほん|ふ|れつごさい|はっこう"}],"notes":"劣後債 is a subordinated bond: in case of insolvency it is served only after the other liabilities; 劣後 means \"to rank behind, to be subordinate\". In compensation it offers higher interest. Banks often issue 劣後債 because they are partly counted as equity. Related: 劣後ローン \"subordinated loan\"; opposite term 優先 \"senior, preferential\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仕組債のリスクを十分に理解してから投資すべきだ。","romaji":"Shikumisai no risuku wo jūbun ni rikai shite kara tōshi subeki da.","german":"You should fully understand the risks of structured bonds before investing.","furigana":"しくみさい|じゅうぶん|りかい|とうし"},{"japanese":"銀行の勧めで仕組債を買った高齢者が、大きな損失を出した。","romaji":"Ginkō no susume de shikumisai o katta kōreisha ga, ōkina sonshitsu o dashita.","german":"An elderly customer who bought a structured bond on the bank's recommendation suffered a large loss.","furigana":"ぎんこう|すす|しくみさい|か|こうれいしゃ|おお|そんしつ|だ"},{"japanese":"仕組債は、条件によっては元本を大きく割り込むことがある。","romaji":"Shikumisai wa, jōken ni yotte wa ganpon o ōkiku warikomu koto ga aru.","german":"Depending on the conditions, structured bonds can fall well below the amount invested.","furigana":"しくみさい|じょうけん|がんぽん|おお|わ|こ"}],"notes":"仕組債 is a structured bond with built-in derivatives, so that interest and repayment depend on share prices, indexes or exchange rates. Also written 仕組み債. The often high interest is tempting, but considerable losses of capital threaten. In Japan, the sale to elderly retail customers came under criticism from the financial regulator.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"利付債の利息は半年ごとに支払われる。","romaji":"Ritsukisai no risoku wa hantoshi goto ni shiharawareru.","german":"The interest on the coupon bond is paid every six months.","furigana":"りつきさい|りそく|はんとし|しはら"},{"japanese":"十年物の利付国債を購入した。","romaji":"Jūnenmono no ritsuki kokusai o kōnyū shita.","german":"I bought a ten-year interest-bearing government bond.","furigana":"じゅうねんもの|りつきこくさい|こうにゅう"},{"japanese":"利付債は、満期まで持てば額面と利息を受け取れる。","romaji":"Ritsukisai wa, manki made moteba gakumen to risoku o uketoreru.","german":"If you hold a coupon bond to maturity, you receive the face value and the interest.","furigana":"りつきさい|まんき|も|がくめん|りそく|う|と"}],"notes":"利付債 is a bond with regular interest payments (coupon bond); 利 \"interest\" + 付 \"provided with\" + 債 \"bond\". The interest is usually paid semiannually; at maturity the face value is returned. The opposite term is 割引債 \"zero-coupon bond\". A well-known example: 利付国債 \"interest-bearing government bond\" such as the ten-year 10年利付国債.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"割引債は額面より低い価格で発行される。","romaji":"Waribikisai wa gakumen yori hikui kakaku de hakkō sareru.","german":"Diskontanleihen werden unter dem Nennwert ausgegeben.","furigana":"わりびきさい|がくめん|ひく|かかく|はっこう"},{"japanese":"割引債には利息の支払いがなく、額面との差額が利益になる。","romaji":"Waribikisai ni wa risoku no shiharai ga naku, gakumen to no sagaku ga rieki ni naru.","german":"Nullkuponanleihen zahlen keine Zinsen; der Gewinn ist die Differenz zum Nennwert.","furigana":"わりびきさい|りそく|しはら|がくめん|さがく|りえき"},{"japanese":"子どもの学費のために、満期が十八年後の割引債を買った。","romaji":"Kodomo no gakuhi no tame ni, manki ga jūhachinen go no waribikisai o katta.","german":"Für die Ausbildung meines Kindes habe ich eine Nullkuponanleihe gekauft, die in achtzehn Jahren fällig wird.","furigana":"こ|がくひ|まんき|じゅうはちねんご|わりびきさい|か"}],"notes":"割引債 ist eine Anleihe ohne laufende Zinszahlung: Sie wird unter dem Nennwert ausgegeben (割引 „Abschlag, Rabatt“) und bei Fälligkeit zum vollen Nennwert zurückgezahlt; die Differenz ist der Ertrag. Im Deutschen entspricht das der Nullkupon- oder Abzinsungsanleihe; im Japanischen sagt man auch ゼロクーポン債. Gegenbegriff ist 利付債.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"個人向け国債は、額面一万円から買える。","romaji":"Kojin muke kokusai wa, gakumen ichiman en kara kaeru.","german":"Government bonds for individuals can be bought from a face value of 10,000 yen.","furigana":"こじんむ|こくさい|がくめんいちまんえん|か"},{"japanese":"彼のお世辞を額面通りに受け取ってはいけない。","romaji":"Kare no oseji o gakumen dōri ni uketotte wa ikenai.","german":"You shouldn't take his flattery at face value.","furigana":"かれ|せじ|がくめんどお|う|と"},{"japanese":"額面では月給三十万円だが、手取りは二十四万円ぐらいだ。","romaji":"Gakumen de wa gekkyū sanjūman en da ga, tedori wa nijūyonman en gurai da.","german":"On paper my monthly salary is 300,000 yen, but my take-home pay is about 240,000.","furigana":"がくめん|げっきゅうさんじゅうまんえん|てど|にじゅうよんまんえん"}],"notes":"額面 is the amount printed on a security, coin or banknote, the face value; the opposite is 時価 \"market value\". Very common is the figurative phrase 額面通りに受け取る \"to take at face value\". For salary, 額面 denotes the gross amount, in contrast to 手取り \"net\". Par-value shares no longer exist in Japan since 2001, but for bonds the 額面 is central.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"満期を迎えた定期預金を解約した。","romaji":"Manki wo mukaeta teiki yokin wo kaiyaku shita.","german":"I closed the time deposit that had reached maturity.","furigana":"まんき|むか|ていきよきん|かいやく"},{"japanese":"保険が満期になったら、満期保険金が受け取れる。","romaji":"Hoken ga manki ni nattara, manki hokenkin ga uketoreru.","german":"When the insurance matures, you can receive the maturity benefit.","furigana":"ほけん|まんき|まんきほけんきん|う|と"},{"japanese":"彼は刑期を満期まで務めて出所した。","romaji":"Kare wa keiki o manki made tsutomete shussho shita.","german":"He served his sentence to the full term and was released.","furigana":"かれ|けいき|まんき|つと|しゅっしょ"}],"notes":"満期 is the end of a fixed term: for time deposits, bonds and insurance the maturity (満期を迎える, 満期になる, 満期日), for insurance also 満期保険金 \"maturity benefit\". For prison sentences it means serving the full sentence: 満期出所. For contracts or deadlines in general, one rather says 期限が切れる or 満了.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"低金利を利用して社債の繰上償還を行った。","romaji":"Teikinri wo riyō shite shasai no kuriage shōkan wo okonatta.","german":"Taking advantage of low interest rates, the corporate bonds were redeemed early.","furigana":"ていきんり|りよう|しゃさい|くりあげしょうかん|おこな"},{"japanese":"繰上償還されると、予定していた利息が受け取れなくなる。","romaji":"Kuriage shōkan sareru to, yotei shite ita risoku ga uketorenaku naru.","german":"With early repayment, the investor misses out on the interest they had expected.","furigana":"くりあげしょうかん|よてい|りそく|う|と"},{"japanese":"資産規模が小さくなった投資信託が、繰上償還されることになった。","romaji":"Shisan kibo ga chiisaku natta tōshi shintaku ga, kuriage shōkan sareru koto ni natta.","german":"An investment trust whose assets had shrunk will be redeemed early.","furigana":"しさんきぼ|ちい|とうししんたく|くりあげしょうかん"}],"notes":"繰上償還 is early repayment or redemption before the scheduled date; 繰上 \"to move up\" + 償還 \"repayment, redemption\". It concerns bonds that the issuer buys back earlier, and investment funds that are dissolved early because their volume is too small. For private loans such as mortgages, one says 繰上返済 instead. The spellings 繰り上げ償還 and 繰上げ償還 also occur.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"手形の不渡りを二回出すと銀行取引が停止される。","romaji":"Tegata no fuwatari wo nikai dasu to ginkō torihiki ga teishi sareru.","german":"If a bill is dishonored twice, banking transactions are suspended.","furigana":"てがた|ふわた|にかいだ|ぎんこうとりひき|ていし"},{"japanese":"取引先が不渡りを出し、売掛金が回収できなくなった。","romaji":"Torihikisaki ga fuwatari o dashi, urikakekin ga kaishū dekinaku natta.","german":"A business partner dishonored a bill, and we could no longer collect our receivables.","furigana":"とりひきさき|ふわた|だ|うりかけきん|かいしゅう"},{"japanese":"口座の残高不足で、小切手が不渡りになった。","romaji":"Kōza no zandaka busoku de, kogitte ga fuwatari ni natta.","german":"The check bounced because of insufficient funds in the account.","furigana":"こうざ|ざんだかぶそく|こぎって|ふわた"}],"notes":"不渡り denotes the non-payment of a bill of exchange or check for lack of funds. Typical are 不渡りを出す \"to let a bill bounce\", 不渡りになる and 不渡り手形. Anyone who has 不渡り twice within six months is excluded from banking transactions (銀行取引停止処分), which for companies practically means bankruptcy. The word therefore often appears in the news in connection with insolvencies.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"経営不安の噂で取り付け騒ぎが起きた。","romaji":"Keiei fuan no uwasa de toritsuke sawagi ga okita.","german":"Rumors of financial trouble triggered a run on the bank.","furigana":"けいえいふあん|うわさ|と|つ|さわ|お"},{"japanese":"ネット上のデマが広がり、客が窓口に殺到する取り付け騒ぎになった。","romaji":"Netto-jō no dema ga hirogari, kyaku ga madoguchi ni sattō suru toritsuke sawagi ni natta.","german":"A false report spread online, and it turned into a bank run, with customers storming the counters.","furigana":"じょう|ひろ|きゃく|まどぐち|さっとう|と|つ|さわ"},{"japanese":"預金保険制度は、取り付け騒ぎを防ぐ役割も果たしている。","romaji":"Yokin hoken seido wa, toritsuke sawagi o fusegu yakuwari mo hatashite iru.","german":"The deposit insurance system also serves to prevent bank runs.","furigana":"よきんほけんせいど|と|つ|さわ|ふせ|やくわり|は"}],"notes":"取り付け騒ぎ is a bank run: fearing a bankruptcy, customers rush to a bank to withdraw their deposits. 取り付け here means mass withdrawal, and 騒ぎ means \"uproar\". Typical: 取り付け騒ぎが起きる・起こる. Do not confuse it with 取り付け \"mounting, installation\" from 取り付ける. Rumors or false reports often set off the rush.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"預金保険制度により一定額まで預金が保護される。","romaji":"Yokin hoken seido ni yori itteigaku made yokin ga hogo sareru.","german":"Under the deposit insurance system, deposits are protected up to a certain amount.","furigana":"よきんほけんせいど|いっていがく|よきん|ほご"},{"japanese":"銀行が破綻しても、預金保険で元本一千万円までとその利息は守られる。","romaji":"Ginkō ga hatan shite mo, yokin hoken de ganpon issenman en made to sono risoku wa mamorareru.","german":"Even if a bank fails, principal up to ten million yen and its interest is protected by deposit insurance.","furigana":"ぎんこう|はたん|よきんほけん|がんぽんいっせんまんえん|りそく|まも"},{"japanese":"外貨預金は、預金保険の対象外だ。","romaji":"Gaika yokin wa, yokin hoken no taishōgai da.","german":"Foreign currency deposits are not covered by deposit insurance.","furigana":"がいかよきん|よきんほけん|たいしょうがい"}],"notes":"預金保険 is deposit insurance, which protects bank deposits in the event of a bank failure; in Japan it is run by the 預金保険機構. Up to 10 million yen of principal plus interest is protected per person per bank; the limited payout is called ペイオフ. Not protected are, among other things, foreign currency deposits. Mostly used in 預金保険制度 or 預金保険の対象.","levelNote":"Compound: 預金 (N1) + 保険 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不良債権の処理が銀行経営の課題だ。","romaji":"Furyō saiken no shori ga ginkō keiei no kadai da.","german":"Disposing of non-performing loans is a challenge for bank management.","furigana":"ふりょうさいけん|しょり|ぎんこうけいえい|かだい"},{"japanese":"バブル崩壊後、多くの銀行が巨額の不良債権を抱えた。","romaji":"Baburu hōkai go, ōku no ginkō ga kyogaku no furyō saiken o kakaeta.","german":"After the collapse of the bubble, many banks were left holding huge amounts of bad debt.","furigana":"ほうかいご|おお|ぎんこう|きょがく|ふりょうさいけん|かか"},{"japanese":"不良債権比率が下がり、銀行の経営は健全化した。","romaji":"Furyō saiken hiritsu ga sagari, ginkō no keiei wa kenzenka shita.","german":"The non-performing loan ratio fell, and the banks' management became sound again.","furigana":"ふりょうさいけんひりつ|さ|ぎんこう|けいえい|けんぜんか"}],"notes":"不良債権 are non-performing or bad loans whose repayment has become uncertain; 不良 \"bad, defective\" + 債権 \"claim, receivable\". The word is closely tied to the Japanese banking crisis after the burst of the bubble economy. Typical: 不良債権を抱える, 不良債権処理 \"disposal of bad loans\", 不良債権比率. Do not confuse it with 債券 \"bond\", which is read the same way.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"銀行の貸し渋りが中小企業の経営を圧迫している。","romaji":"Ginkō no kashishiburi ga chūshō kigyō no keiei o appaku shite iru.","german":"Die zurückhaltende Kreditvergabe der Banken setzt kleine und mittlere Unternehmen unter Druck.","furigana":"ぎんこう|か|しぶ|ちゅうしょうきぎょう|けいえい|あっぱく"},{"japanese":"景気が悪くなると、銀行の貸し渋りが起きやすい。","romaji":"Keiki ga waruku naru to, ginkō no kashishiburi ga okiyasui.","german":"Wenn die Konjunktur nachlässt, kommt es leicht zu einer Kreditklemme.","furigana":"けいき|わる|ぎんこう|か|しぶ|お"},{"japanese":"融資を断られて、貸し渋りを身をもって感じた。","romaji":"Yūshi o kotowararete, kashishiburi o mi o motte kanjita.","german":"Als mein Kreditantrag abgelehnt wurde, bekam ich die Kreditklemme am eigenen Leib zu spüren.","furigana":"ゆうし|ことわ|か|しぶ|み|かん"}],"notes":"貸し渋り beschreibt, dass Banken „zögern, Kredite zu vergeben“, etwa in Krisen; im Deutschen spricht man von Kreditklemme. Das zugrunde liegende Verb ist 貸し渋る; 〜渋る heißt „sich sträuben, etwas zu tun“ (出し渋る, 売り渋る). Noch drastischer ist 貸し剥がし, das Zurückfordern bereits gewährter Kredite. Typisch: 銀行の貸し渋り, 貸し渋りが起きる/続く.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"貸し剥がしにより資金繰りが行き詰まった。","romaji":"Kashihagashi ni yori shikinguri ga ikizumatta.","german":"The calling in of loans left the company's cash flow in a bind.","furigana":"か|は|しきんぐ|い|づ"},{"japanese":"不況になると、銀行の貸し剥がしに苦しむ中小企業が増える。","romaji":"Fukyō ni naru to, ginkō no kashihagashi ni kurushimu chūshō kigyō ga fueru.","german":"In a recession, more and more small and medium-sized businesses suffer from banks calling in their loans.","furigana":"ふきょう|ぎんこう|か|は|くる|ちゅうしょうきぎょう|ふ"},{"japanese":"政府は銀行に、貸し渋りや貸し剥がしをしないよう求めた。","romaji":"Seifu wa ginkō ni, kashishiburi ya kashihagashi o shinai yō motometa.","german":"The government asked the banks not to withhold new loans or call in existing ones.","furigana":"せいふ|ぎんこう|か|しぶ|か|は|もと"}],"notes":"貸し剥がし refers to a bank demanding early repayment of loans already granted, or refusing to renew them; literally something like \"lend and strip away\". Distinguish it from 貸し渋り \"reluctance to lend\", where new loans are refused; in the press the two often appear together (貸し渋り・貸し剥がし). It is a critical buzzword from business journalism and politics, coined during the banking crisis of the late 1990s; typical are 貸し剥がしに遭う and 貸し剥がしに苦しむ. Also often written 貸しはがし.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"与信審査を厳格化して貸倒れリスクを低減する。","romaji":"Yoshin shinsa o genkakuka shite kashidaore risuku o teigen suru.","german":"We tighten credit screening to reduce the risk of bad debt.","furigana":"よしんしんさ|げんかくか|かしだお|ていげん"},{"japanese":"クレジットカードの与信枠を超えたため、決済できなかった。","romaji":"Kurejitto kādo no yoshinwaku o koeta tame, kessai dekinakatta.","german":"The payment could not be processed because the credit card limit was exceeded.","furigana":"よしんわく|こ|けっさい"},{"japanese":"新しい取引先については、まず与信管理の担当者が経営状態を調べる。","romaji":"Atarashii torihikisaki ni tsuite wa, mazu yoshin kanri no tantōsha ga keiei jōtai o shiraberu.","german":"For new business partners, the person in charge of credit management first checks their financial condition.","furigana":"あたら|とりひきさき|よしんかんり|たんとうしゃ|けいえいじょうたい|しら"}],"notes":"与信 is the technical term for granting credit to a customer or business partner, including payment terms in trade and card limits. It appears almost always in compounds: 与信審査 \"credit screening\", 与信枠 or 与信限度額 \"credit limit\", 与信管理 \"credit risk management\". Unlike 信用 \"trust, creditworthiness\" and 融資 \"financing, a concrete loan\", 与信 means the extending of credit as a process or framework. Rare in everyday life, but standard in banks, accounting and sales.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"債務不履行に陥り、信用が失墜した。","romaji":"Saimu furikō ni ochiiri, shin'yō ga shittsui shita.","german":"A default occurred and the company's credibility collapsed.","furigana":"さいむふりこう|おちい|しんよう|しっつい"},{"japanese":"その国の国債が債務不履行になるのではないかと心配されている。","romaji":"Sono kuni no kokusai ga saimu furikō ni naru no de wa nai ka to shinpai sarete iru.","german":"There are fears that the country's government bonds may go into default.","furigana":"くに|こくさい|さいむふりこう|しんぱい"},{"japanese":"納期を守らなかったことが債務不履行に当たるとして、損害賠償を求められた。","romaji":"Nōki o mamoranakatta koto ga saimu furikō ni ataru to shite, songai baishō o motomerareta.","german":"Missing the delivery deadline counted as non-performance, and damages were demanded.","furigana":"のうき|まも|さいむふりこう|あ|そんがいばいしょう|もと"}],"notes":"債務不履行 means that an obligation is not fulfilled. In finance this is a \"default\", often also called デフォルト: 債務不履行に陥る, 債務不履行を起こす. In civil law it means \"non-performance\" of a contract in general, e.g. late delivery, and gives rise to 損害賠償 (\"damages\"). 不履行 alone also appears in 契約不履行 or 約束の不履行.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"信用格付けの低下により資金調達コストが増加した。","romaji":"Shin'yō kakuzuke no teika ni yori shikin chōtatsu kosuto ga zōka shita.","german":"The decline in the credit rating increased funding costs.","furigana":"しんようかくづ|ていか|しきんちょうたつ|ぞうか"},{"japanese":"大手格付け会社が日本国債の信用格付けを一段階引き下げた。","romaji":"Ōte kakuzuke gaisha ga Nihon kokusai no shin'yō kakuzuke o ichidankai hikisageta.","german":"A major rating agency lowered the credit rating of Japanese government bonds by one notch.","furigana":"おおてかくづ|がいしゃ|にほんこくさい|しんようかくづ|いちだんかいひ|さ"},{"japanese":"社債を買う前に、発行企業の信用格付けを確認しておこう。","romaji":"Shasai o kau mae ni, hakkō kigyō no shin'yō kakuzuke o kakunin shite okō.","german":"Before buying corporate bonds, check the issuing company's credit rating.","furigana":"しゃさい|か|まえ|はっこうきぎょう|しんようかくづ|かくにん"}],"notes":"信用格付け is the credit rating that rating agencies (格付け会社) assign to countries, companies or bonds. In the news it is often shortened to 格付け; downgrading and upgrading are 格付けを引き下げる/引き上げる, or 格下げ/格上げ for short. For the creditworthiness of private individuals, one more often says 信用情報 or 信用スコア. The spelling 信用格付 without け also occurs in technical texts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"市場の流動性が低下して売買が成立しにくくなった。","romaji":"Shijō no ryūdōsei ga teika shite baibai ga seiritsu shinikuku natta.","german":"Market liquidity declined, making trades harder to execute.","furigana":"しじょう|りゅうどうせい|ていか|ばいばい|せいりつ"},{"japanese":"中央銀行は市場に大量の流動性を供給した。","romaji":"Chūō ginkō wa shijō ni tairyō no ryūdōsei o kyōkyū shita.","german":"The central bank supplied the market with large amounts of liquidity.","furigana":"ちゅうおうぎんこう|しじょう|たいりょう|りゅうどうせい|きょうきゅう"},{"japanese":"不動産は株に比べて流動性が低く、すぐには現金化できない。","romaji":"Fudōsan wa kabu ni kurabete ryūdōsei ga hikuku, sugu ni wa genkinka dekinai.","german":"Real estate is less liquid than stocks and cannot be turned into cash right away.","furigana":"ふどうさん|かぶ|くら|りゅうどうせい|ひく|げんきんか"}],"notes":"In finance, 流動性 means \"liquidity\": how easily an asset can be converted into money, or how actively a market is traded: 流動性が高い/低い, 流動性を供給する (central bank), 流動性リスク. For a company's ability to pay in everyday terms, one more often says 資金繰り; 流動性 is the technical term. Outside finance it means \"fluidity, mobility\", e.g. 雇用の流動性 \"labor market mobility\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"銀行の自己資本比率が国際基準を下回っている。","romaji":"Ginkō no jiko shihon hiritsu ga kokusai kijun o shitamawatte iru.","german":"The bank's capital adequacy ratio is below the international standard.","furigana":"ぎんこう|じこしほんひりつ|こくさいきじゅん|したまわ"},{"japanese":"自己資本比率が高い会社は、不況にも強いと言われる。","romaji":"Jiko shihon hiritsu ga takai kaisha wa, fukyō ni mo tsuyoi to iwareru.","german":"Companies with a high equity ratio are said to withstand recessions well.","furigana":"じこしほんひりつ|たか|かいしゃ|ふきょう|つよ|い"},{"japanese":"増資によって自己資本比率を三十パーセントまで引き上げた。","romaji":"Zōshi ni yotte jiko shihon hiritsu o sanjū pāsento made hikiageta.","german":"Through a capital increase, the equity ratio was raised to thirty percent.","furigana":"ぞうし|じこしほんひりつ|さんじゅう|ひ|あ"}],"notes":"自己資本比率 is the share of equity (自己資本) in total capital; the counterpart of equity is 他人資本 \"borrowed capital\". For companies a high ratio is seen as a sign of stability (自己資本比率が高い); for banks it is internationally regulated as a capital requirement (BIS規制). Typical verbs: 自己資本比率を引き上げる, 〜が基準を下回る. A purely technical term from balance sheets and banking supervision.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"資本金一億円以上の企業が対象となる。","romaji":"Shihonkin ichiokuen ijō no kigyō ga taishō to naru.","german":"Companies with capital of 100 million yen or more are covered.","furigana":"しほんきんいちおくえんいじょう|きぎょう|たいしょう"},{"japanese":"この会社は資本金わずか百万円で設立された。","romaji":"Kono kaisha wa shihonkin wazuka hyakuman'en de setsuritsu sareta.","german":"This company was founded with a capital of only one million yen.","furigana":"かいしゃ|しほんきん|ひゃくまんえん|せつりつ"},{"japanese":"事業拡大のため、資本金を三億円に増やした。","romaji":"Jigyō kakudai no tame, shihonkin o san'okuen ni fuyashita.","german":"To expand the business, the capital was increased to 300 million yen.","furigana":"じぎょうかくだい|しほんきん|さんおくえん|ふ"}],"notes":"資本金 is the capital registered in a company's commercial register, i.e. its stated capital or share capital: 資本金〇〇円の会社. Increase and reduction are 増資 and 減資. Distinguish it from 資本 \"capital\" as a general economic concept and 資金 \"funds, money\" for a specific purpose. Laws and subsidy programs often define small and medium-sized enterprises by their 資本金.","levelNote":"Compound: 資本 (N3) + 金 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"法定準備金を積み立てる義務がある。","romaji":"Hōtei junbikin o tsumitateru gimu ga aru.","german":"There is an obligation to set aside legal reserves.","furigana":"ほうていじゅんびきん|つ|た|ぎむ"},{"japanese":"会社は利益の一部を準備金として積み立てている。","romaji":"Kaisha wa rieki no ichibu o junbikin to shite tsumitatete iru.","german":"The company sets aside part of its profits as reserves.","furigana":"かいしゃ|りえき|いちぶ|じゅんびきん|つ|た"},{"japanese":"赤字を埋めるため、準備金を取り崩すことになった。","romaji":"Akaji o umeru tame, junbikin o torikuzusu koto ni natta.","german":"To cover the deficit, the reserves had to be drawn down.","furigana":"あかじ|う|じゅんびきん|と|くず"}],"notes":"準備金 are \"reserves\" that a company, bank or organization sets aside for future purposes. In Japanese company law one distinguishes 資本準備金 and 利益準備金, together 法定準備金. Typical verbs: 準備金を積み立てる, 準備金を取り崩す (\"to draw down\"). Similar but more general is 積立金; 引当金, by contrast, are provisions for expected costs.","levelNote":"Compound: 準備 (N5) + 金 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"繰越剰余金から配当金を支払う。","romaji":"Kurikoshi jōyokin kara haitōkin o shiharau.","german":"Dividends are paid out of the carried-over surplus.","furigana":"くりこしじょうよきん|はいとうきん|しはら"},{"japanese":"株主総会で剰余金の配当が決議された。","romaji":"Kabunushi sōkai de jōyokin no haitō ga ketsugi sareta.","german":"The shareholders' meeting resolved to pay a dividend out of surplus.","furigana":"かぶぬしそうかい|じょうよきん|はいとう|けつぎ"},{"japanese":"前年度の剰余金の一部は、借金の返済に充てられる。","romaji":"Zennendo no jōyokin no ichibu wa, shakkin no hensai ni aterareru.","german":"Part of the previous year's surplus will be used to repay debt.","furigana":"ぜんねんど|じょうよきん|いちぶ|しゃっきん|へんさい|あ"}],"notes":"On the balance sheet, 剰余金 is the part of equity that exceeds the 資本金; one distinguishes 資本剰余金 \"capital surplus\" and 利益剰余金 \"retained earnings\". Dividends are paid out of the 剰余金 (剰余金の配当). In the national budget it means the surplus after the close of the fiscal year (決算剰余金). For an everyday \"surplus\", one more often says 余り or 黒字.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"租税回避地への資金移動が問題視されている。","romaji":"Sozei kaihichi e no shikin idō ga mondaishi sarete iru.","german":"Die Verlagerung von Geldern in Steueroasen wird als Problem betrachtet.","furigana":"そぜいかいひち|しきんいどう|もんだいし"},{"japanese":"日本とドイツの間には二重課税を防ぐ租税条約がある。","romaji":"Nihon to Doitsu no aida ni wa nijū kazei o fusegu sozei jōyaku ga aru.","german":"Zwischen Japan und Deutschland gibt es ein Abkommen, das Doppelbesteuerung verhindert.","furigana":"にほん|あいだ|にじゅうかぜい|ふせ|そぜいじょうやく"},{"japanese":"大学で租税法の講義を取っている。","romaji":"Daigaku de sozeihō no kōgi o totte iru.","german":"An der Uni belege ich eine Vorlesung im Steuerrecht.","furigana":"だいがく|そぜいほう|こうぎ|と"}],"notes":"租税 ist das formelle, juristisch-wissenschaftliche Wort für Steuern als System; im Alltag sagt man 税金 oder 税. Es erscheint vor allem in festen Fachbegriffen: 租税法 „Steuerrecht“, 租税条約 „Steuerabkommen“, 租税回避 „Steuervermeidung“, 租税負担率 „Steuerlastquote“. Für eine konkrete Steuer, die man zahlt, verwendet man 租税 praktisch nie.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"徴税コストの削減が行政改革の課題だ。","romaji":"Chōzei kosuto no sakugen ga gyōsei kaikaku no kadai da.","german":"Reducing tax collection costs is a challenge for administrative reform.","furigana":"ちょうぜい|さくげん|ぎょうせいかいかく|かだい"},{"japanese":"江戸時代の農民は、厳しい徴税に苦しんだ。","romaji":"Edo jidai no nōmin wa, kibishii chōzei ni kurushinda.","german":"Farmers in the Edo period suffered under harsh tax collection.","furigana":"えどじだい|のうみん|きび|ちょうぜい|くる"},{"japanese":"国はデジタル化で徴税の効率を高めようとしている。","romaji":"Kuni wa dejitaruka de chōzei no kōritsu o takameyō to shite iru.","german":"The state is trying to make tax collection more efficient through digitalization.","furigana":"くに|か|ちょうぜい|こうりつ|たか"}],"notes":"徴税 is the collection of taxes by the state. Distinguish 課税 \"taxation, imposing a tax\", 徴税 \"actually collecting\" and 納税 \"paying taxes\" by the citizen. Typical are 徴税権, 徴税コスト, 徴税を強化する; the verb 徴税する exists, but 税を徴収する is more common. A formal word from administration, history and politics.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"抵当権の実行により不動産が競売にかけられた。","romaji":"Teitōken no jikkō ni yori fudōsan ga keibai ni kakerareta.","german":"Durch die Vollstreckung des Pfandrechts wurde die Immobilie zwangsversteigert.","furigana":"ていとうけん|じっこう|ふどうさん|けいばい"},{"japanese":"住宅ローンを組むと、銀行が家に抵当権を設定する。","romaji":"Jūtaku rōn o kumu to, ginkō ga ie ni teitōken o settei suru.","german":"Nimmt man einen Immobilienkredit auf, lässt die Bank eine Hypothek auf das Haus eintragen.","furigana":"じゅうたく|く|ぎんこう|いえ|ていとうけん|せってい"},{"japanese":"ローンを完済したので、抵当権の抹消手続きをした。","romaji":"Rōn o kansai shita node, teitōken no masshō tetsuzuki o shita.","german":"Da der Kredit abbezahlt war, ließ ich die Hypothek löschen.","furigana":"かんさい|ていとうけん|まっしょうてつづ"}],"notes":"抵当権 ist das Recht eines Gläubigers, meist einer Bank, an einer Immobilie als Sicherheit, ohne dass sie ihm übergeben wird – also eine Hypothek bzw. ein Grundpfandrecht. Beim Hauskredit lässt die Bank es eintragen (抵当権を設定する), nach der Tilgung wird es gelöscht (抵当権を抹消する). 抵当 allein heißt „Pfand, Sicherheit“: 家を抵当に入れる. Anders als im Deutschen meint 抵当権 nie den Kredit selbst (das ist 住宅ローン); ein Pfandrecht mit Übergabe der Sache, etwa beim Pfandhaus, heißt 質権.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"債権者集会で弁済計画が承認された。","romaji":"Saikensha shūkai de bensai keikaku ga shōnin sareta.","german":"The repayment plan was approved at the creditors' meeting.","furigana":"さいけんしゃしゅうかい|べんさいけいかく|しょうにん"},{"japanese":"会社が倒産し、債権者が事務所に押し寄せた。","romaji":"Kaisha ga tōsan shi, saikensha ga jimusho ni oshiyoseta.","german":"The company went bankrupt, and creditors flooded into the office.","furigana":"かいしゃ|とうさん|さいけんしゃ|じむしょ|お|よ"},{"japanese":"債権者の同意がなければ、借金の減額は認められない。","romaji":"Saikensha no dōi ga nakereba, shakkin no gengaku wa mitomerarenai.","german":"Without the creditors' consent, a reduction of the debt is not permitted.","furigana":"さいけんしゃ|どうい|しゃっきん|げんがく|みと"}],"notes":"債権者 is the \"creditor\", i.e. a person who holds a claim (債権); the counterpart is 債務者 \"debtor\". Beware of the homophone 債券 \"bond, security\" - 債権 is the claim, 債券 the traded paper. Typical combinations are 債権者集会 \"creditors' meeting\", 債権者に返済する, 債権者の同意. Legal and economic register; in casual speech one more often says 貸した人 or 貸し手.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"債務者の返済能力を慎重に審査する。","romaji":"Saimusha no hensai nōryoku o shinchō ni shinsa suru.","german":"The debtor's ability to repay is carefully examined.","furigana":"さいむしゃ|へんさいのうりょく|しんちょう|しんさ"},{"japanese":"債務者が返済を三か月滞らせたため、裁判所に訴えた。","romaji":"Saimusha ga hensai o sankagetsu todokoraseta tame, saibansho ni uttaeta.","german":"The debtor fell three months behind on repayment, so we sued in court.","furigana":"さいむしゃ|へんさい|さん||さいばんしょ|うった"},{"japanese":"多重債務者を支援する相談窓口が設けられた。","romaji":"Tajū saimusha o shien suru sōdan madoguchi ga mōkerareta.","german":"A consultation desk was set up to support people with multiple debts.","furigana":"たじゅうさいむしゃ|しえん|そうだんまどぐち|もう"}],"notes":"債務者 is the \"debtor\", i.e. a person who has an obligation (債務); the counterpart is 債権者 \"creditor\". 債務 is more legal and broader than 借金 \"monetary debt\": in law it can be any duty to perform. Common are 多重債務者 \"over-indebted person with several lenders\" and 債務者が返済を滞らせる. In everyday speech one more often says 借りた人 or 借り手.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"外貨準備高が過去最高に達した。","romaji":"Gaika junbidaka ga kako saikō ni tasshita.","german":"Foreign exchange reserves reached a record high.","furigana":"がいかじゅんびだか|かこさいこう|たっ"},{"japanese":"政府は円買い介入のために外貨準備の一部を取り崩した。","romaji":"Seifu wa enkai kainyū no tame ni gaika junbi no ichibu o torikuzushita.","german":"The government drew down part of its foreign exchange reserves to intervene by buying yen.","furigana":"せいふ|えんか|かいにゅう|がいかじゅんび|いちぶ|と|くず"},{"japanese":"外貨準備が少ない国は、通貨危機に陥りやすい。","romaji":"Gaika junbi ga sukunai kuni wa, tsūka kiki ni ochiiriyasui.","german":"Countries with small foreign exchange reserves easily fall into currency crises.","furigana":"がいかじゅんび|すく|くに|つうかきき|おちい"}],"notes":"外貨準備 are the foreign exchange reserves that the state and central bank hold in foreign currency; the amount is called 外貨準備高. They serve mainly for interventions in the currency market and as a buffer in currency crises: 外貨準備を取り崩す \"to draw on the reserves\", 外貨準備が減少する. 外貨 alone simply means \"foreign currency\", e.g. in the private 外貨預金 \"foreign currency account\". A technical term from business news.","levelNote":"Compound: 外貨 (N1) + 準備 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"急激な円高に対して為替介入が実施された。","romaji":"Kyūgeki na endaka ni taishite kawase kainyū ga jisshi sareta.","german":"Currency intervention was carried out against the sharp rise of the yen.","furigana":"きゅうげき|えんだか|たい|かわせかいにゅう|じっし"},{"japanese":"財務省は円安を食い止めるため、為替介入に踏み切った。","romaji":"Zaimushō wa en'yasu o kuitomeru tame, kawase kainyū ni fumikitta.","german":"The Ministry of Finance decided to intervene in the currency market to halt the yen's decline.","furigana":"ざいむしょう|えんやす|く|と|かわせかいにゅう|ふ|き"},{"japanese":"市場では為替介入への警戒感が強まっている。","romaji":"Shijō de wa kawase kainyū e no keikaikan ga tsuyomatte iru.","german":"In the market, wariness of currency intervention is growing.","furigana":"しじょう|かわせかいにゅう|けいかいかん|つよ"}],"notes":"為替介入 is the authorities' intervention in the foreign exchange market; in Japan the Ministry of Finance decides and the Bank of Japan carries it out. One says 為替介入を行う/実施する or 為替介入に踏み切る \"to decide to intervene\"; depending on direction 円買い介入 or 円売り介入, and together with other countries 協調介入. Note the special reading 為替 = かわせ. A technical term from business news.","levelNote":"Compound: 為替 (N2) + 介入 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"通貨供給量の増加がインフレ圧力を高めている。","romaji":"Tsūka kyōkyūryō no zōka ga infure atsuryoku o takamete iru.","german":"The increase in the money supply is raising inflationary pressure.","furigana":"つうかきょうきゅうりょう|ぞうか|あつりょく|たか"},{"japanese":"日銀は通貨供給量を増やして景気を刺激しようとした。","romaji":"Nichigin wa tsūka kyōkyūryō o fuyashite keiki o shigeki shiyō to shita.","german":"The Bank of Japan tried to stimulate the economy by increasing the money supply.","furigana":"にちぎん|つうかきょうきゅうりょう|ふ|けいき|しげき"},{"japanese":"通貨供給量の伸びが前年に比べて鈍化した。","romaji":"Tsūka kyōkyūryō no nobi ga zennen ni kurabete donka shita.","german":"The growth of the money supply has slowed compared with the previous year.","furigana":"つうかきょうきゅうりょう|の|ぜんねん|くら|どんか"}],"notes":"通貨供給量 is the \"money supply\", i.e. the money in circulation. Officially the statistic is now called マネーストック (formerly マネーサプライ); 通貨供給量 is the Japanese paraphrase used in news and textbooks. Typical are 通貨供給量を増やす/絞る and 通貨供給量の伸び. Distinguish it from マネタリーベース \"monetary base\", the money provided directly by the central bank.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"公開市場操作により短期金利を誘導する。","romaji":"Kōkai shijō sōsa ni yori tanki kinri o yūdō suru.","german":"Short-term interest rates are steered through open market operations.","furigana":"こうかいしじょうそうさ|たんききんり|ゆうどう"},{"japanese":"日銀は公開市場操作で国債を大量に買い入れた。","romaji":"Nichigin wa kōkai shijō sōsa de kokusai o tairyō ni kaiireta.","german":"The Bank of Japan bought large amounts of government bonds in open market operations.","furigana":"にちぎん|こうかいしじょうそうさ|こくさい|たいりょう|か|い"},{"japanese":"経済学の試験で、公開市場操作の仕組みを説明する問題が出た。","romaji":"Keizaigaku no shiken de, kōkai shijō sōsa no shikumi o setsumei suru mondai ga deta.","german":"On the economics exam there was a question asking to explain how open market operations work.","furigana":"けいざいがく|しけん|こうかいしじょうそうさ|しく|せつめい|もんだい|で"}],"notes":"公開市場操作 refers to the buying and selling of securities, mainly government bonds, by the central bank in order to steer the money supply and short-term interest rates. In the news and in the market it is usually shortened to オペ: 買いオペ \"buying operation\" (money is supplied), 売りオペ \"selling operation\" (money is withdrawn). The full term is typical of textbooks and official texts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"預金準備率の変更が金融政策の手段として用いられる。","romaji":"Yokin junbiritsu no henkō ga kin'yū seisaku no shudan to shite mochiirareru.","german":"Changes to the reserve requirement ratio are used as a tool of monetary policy.","furigana":"よきんじゅんびりつ|へんこう|きんゆうせいさく|しゅだん|もち"},{"japanese":"中国の中央銀行が預金準備率の引き下げを発表した。","romaji":"Chūgoku no chūō ginkō ga yokin junbiritsu no hikisage o happyō shita.","german":"China's central bank announced a cut in the reserve requirement ratio.","furigana":"ちゅうごく|ちゅうおうぎんこう|よきんじゅんびりつ|ひ|さ|はっぴょう"},{"japanese":"預金準備率を引き上げると、銀行が貸し出せるお金が減る。","romaji":"Yokin junbiritsu o hikiageru to, ginkō ga kashidaseru okane ga heru.","german":"If the reserve requirement ratio is raised, the money banks can lend decreases.","furigana":"よきんじゅんびりつ|ひ|あ|ぎんこう|か|だ|かね|へ"}],"notes":"預金準備率 is the \"reserve requirement ratio\": the share of deposits that banks must hold with the central bank. Technically it is also called 準備率 or 支払準備率 in Japan. Typical are 預金準備率を引き上げる/引き下げる; a cut frees up more money for lending. The Bank of Japan has hardly used this instrument for a long time; in the news it appears mainly in connection with China's central bank.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"金融政策の方向性が市場の注目を集めている。","romaji":"Kin'yū seisaku no hōkōsei ga shijō no chūmoku o atsumete iru.","german":"Die Ausrichtung der Geldpolitik zieht die Aufmerksamkeit des Marktes auf sich.","furigana":"きんゆうせいさく|ほうこうせい|しじょう|ちゅうもく|あつ"},{"japanese":"日銀は金融政策決定会合で、大規模な金融緩和の継続を決めた。","romaji":"Nichigin wa kin'yū seisaku kettei kaigō de, daikibo na kin'yū kanwa no keizoku o kimeta.","german":"Auf ihrer geldpolitischen Sitzung beschloss die Bank of Japan, die massive Lockerung fortzusetzen.","furigana":"にちぎん|きんゆうせいさくけっていかいごう|だいきぼ|きんゆうかんわ|けいぞく|き"},{"japanese":"物価が上がり続け、金融政策の転換を求める声が強まった。","romaji":"Bukka ga agaritsuzuke, kin'yū seisaku no tenkan o motomeru koe ga tsuyomatta.","german":"Da die Preise weiter stiegen, wurden die Rufe nach einer geldpolitischen Wende lauter.","furigana":"ぶっか|あ|つづ|きんゆうせいさく|てんかん|もと|こえ|つよ"}],"notes":"金融政策 ist die Geldpolitik der Zentralbank (日銀の金融政策), also die Steuerung von Zinsen und Geldmenge. Das Gegenstück ist 財政政策 „Fiskalpolitik“ der Regierung über Ausgaben und Steuern – Vorsicht: deutsches „Finanzpolitik“ entspricht 財政政策, nicht 金融政策. Wichtige Verbindungen: 金融緩和 „Lockerung“, 金融引き締め „Straffung“, 金融政策決定会合 „geldpolitische Sitzung“, 金融政策を転換する.","levelNote":"Compound: 金融 (N3) + 政策 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"財政政策と金融政策の連携が重要だ。","romaji":"Zaisei seisaku to kin'yū seisaku no renkei ga jūyō da.","german":"Coordination between fiscal and monetary policy is important.","furigana":"ざいせいせいさく|きんゆうせいさく|れんけい|じゅうよう"},{"japanese":"景気対策として、政府は公共事業を増やす財政政策をとった。","romaji":"Keiki taisaku to shite, seifu wa kōkyō jigyō o fuyasu zaisei seisaku o totta.","german":"To stimulate the economy, the government adopted a fiscal policy of increasing public works.","furigana":"けいきたいさく|せいふ|こうきょうじぎょう|ふ|ざいせいせいさく"},{"japanese":"借金が増えすぎて、積極的な財政政策をとる余地がない。","romaji":"Shakkin ga fuesugite, sekkyokuteki na zaisei seisaku o toru yochi ga nai.","german":"Debt has grown so much that there is no room for an expansionary fiscal policy.","furigana":"しゃっきん|ふ|せっきょくてき|ざいせいせいさく|よち"}],"notes":"財政政策 is the government's fiscal policy: steering the economy through public spending and taxes. Its counterpart is 金融政策 \"monetary policy\" of the central bank. Typical expressions: 財政政策をとる, 積極財政 \"expansionary fiscal policy\" vs. 緊縮財政 \"austerity\", 財政出動 \"government spending program\". 財政 alone means \"public finances\", e.g. 財政赤字 \"budget deficit\".","levelNote":"Compound: 財政 (N1) + 政策 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一般会計の総額が百兆円を超えた。","romaji":"Ippan kaikei no sōgaku ga hyakuchōen o koeta.","german":"The total of the general account has exceeded 100 trillion yen.","furigana":"いっぱんかいけい|そうがく|ひゃくちょうえん|こ"},{"japanese":"来年度の一般会計予算案が閣議決定された。","romaji":"Rainendo no ippan kaikei yosan'an ga kakugi kettei sareta.","german":"The Cabinet approved the draft general account budget for the next fiscal year.","furigana":"らいねんど|いっぱんかいけいよさんあん|かくぎけってい"},{"japanese":"一般会計の歳出のうち、社会保障費が約三分の一を占める。","romaji":"Ippan kaikei no saishutsu no uchi, shakai hoshōhi ga yaku sanbun no ichi o shimeru.","german":"Social security costs account for about one third of the general account expenditure.","furigana":"いっぱんかいけい|さいしゅつ|しゃかいほしょうひ|やくさんぶん|いち|し"}],"notes":"一般会計 is the \"general account\", i.e. the core budget of the state or a municipality, from which regular expenses are paid. Alongside it there are 特別会計 \"special accounts\" for specific purposes such as pensions or government debt. Typical are 一般会計予算, 一般会計の歳出 \"expenditure\" and 歳入 \"revenue\". For a company's bookkeeping, one says 経理 or 会計 instead.","levelNote":"Compound: 一般 (N3) + 会計 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"償還期限が到来した国債の借り換えを行う。","romaji":"Shōkan kigen ga tōrai shita kokusai no karikae o okonau.","german":"Government bonds that have reached maturity are refinanced.","furigana":"しょうかんきげん|とうらい|こくさい|か|か|おこな"},{"japanese":"この社債の償還期限は十年後だ。","romaji":"Kono shasai no shōkan kigen wa jūnengo da.","german":"This corporate bond matures in ten years.","furigana":"しゃさい|しょうかんきげん|じゅうねんご"},{"japanese":"返済が難しくなり、銀行と償還期限の延長を交渉した。","romaji":"Hensai ga muzukashiku nari, ginkō to shōkan kigen no enchō o kōshō shita.","german":"Repayment became difficult, so we negotiated an extension of the redemption deadline with the bank.","furigana":"へんさい|むずか|ぎんこう|しょうかんきげん|えんちょう|こうしょう"}],"notes":"償還期限 is the deadline by which a bond or loan must be repaid - for bonds, the \"maturity\". 償還 means \"redemption, repayment\" and is used mainly for bonds and public loans. Typical are 償還期限を迎える, 償還期限が到来する and 償還期限を延長する. Similar is 満期 \"maturity, expiry\", which also applies to savings contracts and insurance; for ordinary loans one more often says 返済期限.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"経営危機に陥った銀行に公的資金が注入された。","romaji":"Keiei kiki ni ochiitta ginkō ni kōteki shikin ga chūnyū sareta.","german":"Public funds were injected into the bank that had fallen into a management crisis.","furigana":"けいえいきき|おちい|ぎんこう|こうてきしきん|ちゅうにゅう"},{"japanese":"公的資金を使う以上、使い道をきちんと説明する責任がある。","romaji":"Kōteki shikin o tsukau ijō, tsukaimichi o kichinto setsumei suru sekinin ga aru.","german":"As long as public funds are used, there is a responsibility to explain properly how they are spent.","furigana":"こうてきしきん|つか|いじょう|つか|みち|せつめい|せきにん"},{"japanese":"この研究の費用の多くは公的資金で賄われている。","romaji":"Kono kenkyū no hiyō no ōku wa kōteki shikin de makanawarete iru.","german":"Most of the cost of this research is covered by public funds.","furigana":"けんきゅう|ひよう|おお|こうてきしきん|まかな"}],"notes":"公的資金 are \"public funds\", i.e. money from taxes or government budgets. Especially well known is the phrase 公的資金を注入する/投入する for the state bailout of banks or companies. The opposite is 民間資金 (\"private funds\"). 公金 specifically means money managed by public bodies (公金横領 \"embezzlement of public funds\"). The language of politics and business news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"銀行の信用創造機能が経済成長を支えている。","romaji":"Ginkō no shin'yō sōzō kinō ga keizai seichō o sasaete iru.","german":"The credit creation function of banks supports economic growth.","furigana":"ぎんこう|しんようそうぞうきのう|けいざいせいちょう|ささ"},{"japanese":"預金と貸し出しが繰り返されることで、信用創造が起こる。","romaji":"Yokin to kashidashi ga kurikaesareru koto de, shin'yō sōzō ga okoru.","german":"As deposits and lending are repeated, credit creation occurs.","furigana":"よきん|か|だ|く|かえ|しんようそうぞう|お"},{"japanese":"貸し出しが減ると、信用創造の効果も弱まる。","romaji":"Kashidashi ga heru to, shin'yō sōzō no kōka mo yowamaru.","german":"When lending decreases, the effect of credit creation also weakens.","furigana":"か|だ|へ|しんようそうぞう|こうか|よわ"}],"notes":"信用創造 describes how commercial banks create additional book money through lending: a loan is deposited again, lent out again, and so the money supply multiplies. 信用 here means \"credit\", not \"trust\". The term comes from economics and textbooks, often as 信用創造機能 or 信用創造のしくみ. Neutral and technical; hardly used in everyday life.","levelNote":"Compound: 信用 (N3) + 創造 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"金融仲介機能の低下が問題視されている。","romaji":"Kin'yū chūkai kinō no teika ga mondaishi sarete iru.","german":"The decline in the financial intermediation function is seen as a problem.","furigana":"きんゆうちゅうかいきのう|ていか|もんだいし"},{"japanese":"銀行は、預金者から集めたお金を企業に貸す金融仲介の役割を担っている。","romaji":"Ginkō wa, yokinsha kara atsumeta okane o kigyō ni kasu kin'yū chūkai no yakuwari o ninatte iru.","german":"Banks play the role of financial intermediation, lending money collected from depositors to companies.","furigana":"ぎんこう|よきんしゃ|あつ|かね|きぎょう|か|きんゆうちゅうかい|やくわり|にな"},{"japanese":"地方銀行には、地域経済を支える金融仲介機能の強化が求められている。","romaji":"Chihō ginkō ni wa, chiiki keizai o sasaeru kin'yū chūkai kinō no kyōka ga motomerarete iru.","german":"Regional banks are required to strengthen their financial intermediation function that supports the local economy.","furigana":"ちほうぎんこう|ちいきけいざい|ささ|きんゆうちゅうかいきのう|きょうか|もと"}],"notes":"金融仲介 is the intermediary role of banks and other institutions between savers who invest money and companies or households that need money. It mostly appears in 金融仲介機能 or 金融仲介機関. Banks provide 間接金融 \"indirect financing\", while in 直接金融 companies finance themselves directly in the market via stocks and bonds. 仲介 alone means \"mediation, brokering\", e.g. 不動産の仲介 for a real estate agent.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"複雑な金融商品のリスクを十分に説明する義務がある。","romaji":"Fukuzatsu na kin'yū shōhin no risuku o jūbun ni setsumei suru gimu ga aru.","german":"There is an obligation to explain sufficiently the risks of complex financial products.","furigana":"ふくざつ|きんゆうしょうひん|じゅうぶん|せつめい|ぎむ"},{"japanese":"銀行の窓口で、元本保証のない金融商品を勧められた。","romaji":"Ginkō no madoguchi de, ganpon hoshō no nai kin'yū shōhin o susumerareta.","german":"At the bank counter, I was recommended a financial product without a principal guarantee.","furigana":"ぎんこう|まどぐち|がんぽんほしょう|きんゆうしょうひん|すす"},{"japanese":"老後に備えて、どの金融商品に投資すべきか迷っている。","romaji":"Rōgo ni sonaete, dono kin'yū shōhin ni tōshi subeki ka mayotte iru.","german":"I am unsure which financial product I should invest in to prepare for retirement.","furigana":"ろうご|そな|きんゆうしょうひん|とうし|まよ"}],"notes":"金融商品 is the collective term for financial products such as stocks, bonds, investment trusts (投資信託), deposits or insurance; 商品 here means \"product\", not \"goods\". Typical combinations: 金融商品を販売する/購入する, 金融商品に投資する, and the 金融商品取引法 \"Financial Instruments and Exchange Act\". Important in consultations is the risk of 元本割れ \"loss of the invested capital\". Neutral, common in banking and consumer contexts.","levelNote":"Compound: 金融 (N3) + 商品 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"金融工学を応用した新しい金融商品が開発された。","romaji":"Kin'yū kōgaku o ōyō shita atarashii kin'yū shōhin ga kaihatsu sareta.","german":"Mithilfe der Finanzmathematik wurden neue Finanzprodukte entwickelt.","furigana":"きんゆうこうがく|おうよう|あたら|きんゆうしょうひん|かいはつ"},{"japanese":"大学院で金融工学を学び、外資系の証券会社に就職した。","romaji":"Daigakuin de kin'yū kōgaku o manabi, gaishikei no shōken gaisha ni shūshoku shita.","german":"Nach einem Masterstudium in Finanzmathematik fing er bei einer ausländischen Wertpapierfirma an.","furigana":"だいがくいん|きんゆうこうがく|まな|がいしけい|しょうけんがいしゃ|しゅうしょく"},{"japanese":"金融工学を駆使した複雑な商品が、金融危機の一因になった。","romaji":"Kin'yū kōgaku o kushi shita fukuzatsu na shōhin ga, kin'yū kiki no ichiin ni natta.","german":"Komplexe, mit Finanzmathematik konstruierte Produkte waren eine der Ursachen der Finanzkrise.","furigana":"きんゆうこうがく|くし|ふくざつ|しょうひん|きんゆうきき|いちいん"}],"notes":"金融工学 ist die quantitative Finanzmathematik: Mathematik, Statistik und Informatik zur Bewertung von Derivaten, zur Risikosteuerung und zur Konstruktion von Finanzprodukten. Typisch sind 金融工学を駆使する „Finanzmathematik voll ausnutzen“ und 金融工学の専門家. Nicht verwechseln mit deutscher „Finanzwissenschaft“, die Staatsfinanzen untersucht und 財政学 heißt. Nach der Finanzkrise von 2008 wurde 金融工学 in Japan oft kritisch erwähnt.","levelNote":"Compound: 金融 (N3) + 工学 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"世界的な金融危機が実体経済にも波及した。","romaji":"Sekaiteki na kin'yū kiki ga jittai keizai ni mo hakyū shita.","german":"The global financial crisis spilled over into the real economy as well.","furigana":"せかいてき|きんゆうきき|じったいけいざい|はきゅう"},{"japanese":"金融危機を防ぐため、各国の中央銀行が協調して資金を供給した。","romaji":"Kin'yū kiki o fusegu tame, kakkoku no chūō ginkō ga kyōchō shite shikin o kyōkyū shita.","german":"To prevent a financial crisis, the central banks of various countries jointly supplied funds.","furigana":"きんゆうきき|ふせ|かっこく|ちゅうおうぎんこう|きょうちょう|しきん|きょうきゅう"},{"japanese":"父は金融危機のときに仕事を失い、家族は苦労した。","romaji":"Chichi wa kin'yū kiki no toki ni shigoto o ushinai, kazoku wa kurō shita.","german":"My father lost his job during the financial crisis, and the family had a hard time.","furigana":"ちち|きんゆうきき|しごと|うしな|かぞく|くろう"}],"notes":"金融危機 is a \"financial crisis\" in which banks and credit markets begin to wobble. Typical are 金融危機が起きる/発生する, 金融危機に陥る and 金融危機を乗り切る; the 2008 crisis is called 世界金融危機 or リーマン・ショック. Distinguish 通貨危機 \"currency crisis\", 財政危機 \"government fiscal crisis\" and the broader 経済危機 \"economic crisis\".","levelNote":"Compound: 金融 (N3) + 危機 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不動産バブルの崩壊が経済に深刻な打撃を与えた。","romaji":"Fudōsan baburu no hōkai ga keizai ni shinkoku na dageki o ataeta.","german":"The collapse of the real estate bubble dealt a serious blow to the economy.","furigana":"ふどうさん|ほうかい|けいざい|しんこく|だげき|あた"},{"japanese":"バブルの頃は、会社の経費で毎晩のように飲みに行っていたそうだ。","romaji":"Baburu no koro wa, kaisha no keihi de maiban no yō ni nomi ni itte ita sō da.","german":"I hear that during the bubble years they went out drinking almost every night on company expenses.","furigana":"ころ|かいしゃ|けいひ|まいばん|の|い"},{"japanese":"株価の急上昇はバブルではないかと心配する声もある。","romaji":"Kabuka no kyūjōshō wa baburu de wa nai ka to shinpai suru koe mo aru.","german":"Some people worry that the sharp rise in stock prices is a bubble.","furigana":"かぶか|きゅうじょうしょう|しんぱい|こえ"}],"notes":"Economically, バブル is a \"speculative bubble\": 不動産バブル, ITバブル; it bursts with バブルがはじける or バブルが崩壊する. Without any addition, バブル in Japan usually means the bubble economy of the late 1980s to early 1990s (バブル経済, バブル期): バブルの頃, バブル世代. For an actual soap or air bubble one more often says 泡; バブル is used for that only in loanwords such as バブルバス.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"海外への投融資を拡大する方針だ。","romaji":"Kaigai e no tōyūshi o kakudai suru hōshin da.","german":"The policy is to expand investment and lending overseas.","furigana":"かいがい|とうゆうし|かくだい|ほうしん"},{"japanese":"政府は財政投融資を使って、地方の空港整備を進めた。","romaji":"Seifu wa zaisei tōyūshi o tsukatte, chihō no kūkō seibi o susumeta.","german":"The government used the Fiscal Investment and Loan Program to push forward the development of regional airports.","furigana":"せいふ|ざいせいとうゆうし|つか|ちほう|くうこうせいび|すす"},{"japanese":"その銀行は再生可能エネルギー分野への投融資を増やしている。","romaji":"Sono ginkō wa saisei kanō enerugī bun'ya e no tōyūshi o fuyashite iru.","german":"That bank is increasing its investment and lending in the renewable energy sector.","furigana":"ぎんこう|さいせいかのう|ぶんや|とうゆうし|ふ"}],"notes":"投融資 combines 投資 \"investment, equity stake\" and 融資 \"lending\" in one word: money is partly put in as capital and partly lent. Typical for the state, development banks and corporate groups: 投融資を行う, 投融資を拡大する, 海外投融資. A fixed term is 財政投融資, the government's investment and loan program, often called the \"second budget\". Written and technical language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"資金洗浄対策の国際的な枠組みが強化されている。","romaji":"Shikin senjō taisaku no kokusaiteki na wakugumi ga kyōka sarete iru.","german":"The international framework for combating money laundering is being strengthened.","furigana":"しきんせんじょうたいさく|こくさいてき|わくぐ|きょうか"},{"japanese":"暴力団が架空の会社を使って資金洗浄をしていた疑いがある。","romaji":"Bōryokudan ga kakū no kaisha o tsukatte shikin senjō o shite ita utagai ga aru.","german":"A yakuza group is suspected of laundering money through shell companies.","furigana":"ぼうりょくだん|かくう|かいしゃ|つか|しきんせんじょう|うたが"},{"japanese":"銀行で多額の現金を送金しようとしたら、資金洗浄防止のため目的を聞かれた。","romaji":"Ginkō de tagaku no genkin o sōkin shiyō to shitara, shikin senjō bōshi no tame mokuteki o kikareta.","german":"When I tried to send a large sum of cash at the bank, I was asked the purpose in order to prevent money laundering.","furigana":"ぎんこう|たがく|げんきん|そうきん|しきんせんじょうぼうし|もくてき|き"}],"notes":"資金洗浄 is the formal, legal word for \"money laundering\"; in the media and everyday speech one hears マネーロンダリング or shortened マネロン more often. Typical are 資金洗浄を行う, 資金洗浄に関与する and 資金洗浄対策 or 資金洗浄防止 \"anti-money-laundering measures\". 洗浄 literally means \"washing, rinsing\", as in 胃洗浄 \"stomach pumping\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"フィンテックの発展が金融サービスを変革している。","romaji":"Fintekku no hatten ga kin'yū sābisu o henkaku shite iru.","german":"The development of fintech is transforming financial services.","furigana":"はってん|きんゆう|へんかく"},{"japanese":"大学の友人がフィンテック企業を立ち上げた。","romaji":"Daigaku no yūjin ga fintekku kigyō o tachiageta.","german":"A friend from university started a fintech company.","furigana":"だいがく|ゆうじん|きぎょう|た|あ"},{"japanese":"地方銀行もフィンテックを活用して、スマホで口座を開けるようにした。","romaji":"Chihō ginkō mo fintekku o katsuyō shite, sumaho de kōza o hirakeru yō ni shita.","german":"Regional banks, too, are making use of fintech so that people can open an account on their smartphones.","furigana":"ちほうぎんこう|かつよう|こうざ|ひら"}],"notes":"フィンテック is formed from \"finance\" and \"technology\" and means financial services using digital technology, such as smartphone payments, robo-advisors or crypto-assets. It is written in katakana, and in technical texts also as FinTech. Typical are フィンテック企業 \"fintech company\" and フィンテックを活用する. A neutral term from the business press and the corporate world.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"電子マネーの普及により現金取引が減少している。","romaji":"Denshi manē no fukyū ni yori genkin torihiki ga genshō shite iru.","german":"The spread of electronic money is reducing cash transactions.","furigana":"でんし|ふきゅう|げんきんとりひき|げんしょう"},{"japanese":"コンビニでは、交通系の電子マネーで支払うことが多い。","romaji":"Konbini de wa, kōtsūkei no denshi manē de shiharau koto ga ōi.","german":"At convenience stores, I often pay with transit-type electronic money.","furigana":"こうつうけい|でんし|しはら|おお"},{"japanese":"電子マネーの残高が足りなかったので、駅でチャージした。","romaji":"Denshi manē no zandaka ga tarinakatta node, eki de chāji shita.","german":"My electronic money balance was too low, so I topped it up at the station.","furigana":"でんし|ざんだか|た|えき"}],"notes":"In Japan, 電子マネー mainly means rechargeable prepaid systems on IC cards or smartphones, such as Suica, PASMO, nanaco or WAON: 電子マネーで払う, 電子マネーにチャージする \"to top up\", 電子マネーの残高 \"balance\". Strictly speaking, credit cards and QR code payments do not count; all of them together are called キャッシュレス決済. Neutral and everyday.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"キャッシュレス決済の比率が年々上昇している。","romaji":"Kyasshuresu kessai no hiritsu ga nennen jōshō shite iru.","german":"The share of cashless payments is rising year by year.","furigana":"けっさい|ひりつ|ねんねんじょうしょう"},{"japanese":"この店は完全キャッシュレスで、現金は使えません。","romaji":"Kono mise wa kanzen kyasshuresu de, genkin wa tsukaemasen.","german":"This shop is completely cashless; cash cannot be used.","furigana":"みせ|かんぜん|げんきん|つか"},{"japanese":"政府は観光地のキャッシュレス化を進めている。","romaji":"Seifu wa kankōchi no kyasshuresuka o susumete iru.","german":"The government is promoting the shift to cashless payments in tourist areas.","furigana":"せいふ|かんこうち|か|すす"}],"notes":"キャッシュレス \"cashless\" usually stands directly before a noun: キャッシュレス決済 \"cashless payment\", キャッシュレス社会; with 化 it forms キャッシュレス化 \"shift to cashless payment\". Adverbially one says キャッシュレスで払う; キャッシュレスな店 is possible but less common. The counterpart is 現金払い \"cash payment\". Neutral, very common in daily life and politics.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"通関手続きの電子化が進められている。","romaji":"Tsūkan tetsuzuki no denshika ga susumerarete iru.","german":"The digitalization of customs clearance procedures is being advanced.","furigana":"つうかんてつづ|でんしか|すす"},{"japanese":"輸入した部品の通関に時間がかかり、生産が遅れた。","romaji":"Yunyū shita buhin no tsūkan ni jikan ga kakari, seisan ga okureta.","german":"Customs clearance of the imported parts took a long time, and production was delayed.","furigana":"ゆにゅう|ぶひん|つうかん|じかん|せいさん|おく"},{"japanese":"荷物は昨日通関して、明日には届く予定だ。","romaji":"Nimotsu wa kinō tsūkan shite, ashita ni wa todoku yotei da.","german":"The package cleared customs yesterday and is due to arrive tomorrow.","furigana":"にもつ|きのうつうかん|あした|とど|よてい"}],"notes":"通関 is the \"customs clearance\" of goods on import or export: 通関手続き, 通関業者 \"customs broker\", 通関書類. As an intransitive する-verb: 荷物が通関する \"the shipment clears customs\". Do not confuse it with 税関 \"customs office, customs authority\": 税関 is the agency, 通関 the process. For travelers at the airport one more often speaks of 税関検査.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"船荷証券を提示して貨物を受け取る。","romaji":"Funani shōken o teiji shite kamotsu o uketoru.","german":"The cargo is received upon presentation of the bill of lading.","furigana":"ふなにしょうけん|ていじ|かもつ|う|と"},{"japanese":"港では、クレーンで船荷を降ろす作業が続いていた。","romaji":"Minato de wa, kurēn de funani o orosu sagyō ga tsuzuite ita.","german":"At the port, the work of unloading the ship's cargo with cranes continued.","furigana":"みなと|ふなに|お|さぎょう|つづ"},{"japanese":"嵐で船荷の一部が海に落ちてしまった。","romaji":"Arashi de funani no ichibu ga umi ni ochite shimatta.","german":"Part of the cargo fell into the sea in the storm.","furigana":"あらし|ふなに|いちぶ|うみ|お"}],"notes":"船荷 is goods transported by ship, the \"ship's cargo\"; 船 is often read ふな in compounds, as in 船便. The word is technical and appears above all in 船荷証券 \"bill of lading\". One says 船荷を積む/降ろす. More general are 積み荷 \"load\" (for any vehicle) and 貨物 \"freight, cargo\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"荷受人が貨物の状態を確認する。","romaji":"Niukenin ga kamotsu no jōtai o kakunin suru.","german":"The consignee checks the condition of the cargo.","furigana":"にうけにん|かもつ|じょうたい|かくにん"},{"japanese":"倉庫では、午前中に荷受を済ませてから出荷作業に入る。","romaji":"Sōko de wa, gozenchū ni niuke o sumasete kara shukka sagyō ni hairu.","german":"At the warehouse, goods receiving is finished in the morning before shipping work begins.","furigana":"そうこ|ごぜんちゅう|にうけ|す|しゅっかさぎょう|はい"},{"japanese":"荷受の際に箱が破れていたので、写真を撮っておいた。","romaji":"Niuke no sai ni hako ga yaburete ita node, shashin o totte oita.","german":"The box was torn when the goods were received, so I took a photo.","furigana":"にうけ|さい|はこ|やぶ|しゃしん|と"}],"notes":"荷受 is the \"acceptance of goods\", i.e. goods receiving in logistics and trade. It mostly appears in compounds: 荷受人 \"consignee\" (counterpart 荷送人 \"consignor\"), 荷受け場 \"receiving area\". In general text 荷受け with け is more often written; the form without okurigana is common in forms and technical texts. At wholesale markets, the wholesalers who take over goods from producers are also called 荷受.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仲買人が生産者と小売業者の間を取り持つ。","romaji":"Nakagainin ga seisansha to kōri gyōsha no aida o torimotsu.","german":"The broker mediates between producers and retailers.","furigana":"なかがいにん|せいさんしゃ|こうりぎょうしゃ|あいだ|と|も"},{"japanese":"祖父は戦前、米の仲買を営んでいた。","romaji":"Sofu wa senzen, kome no nakagai o itonande ita.","german":"Before the war, my grandfather ran a rice brokerage.","furigana":"そふ|せんぜん|こめ|なかがい|いとな"},{"japanese":"市場の競りでは、仲買人たちが次々に値を付けていく。","romaji":"Shijō no seri de wa, nakagainintachi ga tsugitsugi ni ne o tsukete iku.","german":"At the market auction, the brokers call out one bid after another.","furigana":"しじょう|せ|なかがいにん|つぎつぎ|ね|つ"}],"notes":"仲買 is intermediary trade or brokerage, where someone buys between seller and buyer and resells; the person is called 仲買人. The word has a traditional ring and is met mainly in connection with markets, auctions and historical contexts (米の仲買); at today's wholesale markets the official term is 仲卸. For modern brokering in general, one uses 仲介 or ブローカー.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"期末に棚卸を実施して在庫を確認する。","romaji":"Kimatsu ni tanaoroshi o jisshi shite zaiko o kakunin suru.","german":"A stocktake is carried out at the end of the period to confirm inventory.","furigana":"きまつ|たなおろし|じっし|ざいこ|かくにん"},{"japanese":"年末の棚卸で、帳簿と在庫の数が合わなかった。","romaji":"Nenmatsu no tanaoroshi de, chōbo to zaiko no kazu ga awanakatta.","german":"In the year-end stocktake, the books and the actual stock did not match.","furigana":"ねんまつ|たなおろし|ちょうぼ|ざいこ|かず|あ"},{"japanese":"転職の前に、自分のスキルを棚卸ししてみた。","romaji":"Tenshoku no mae ni, jibun no sukiru o tanaoroshi shite mita.","german":"Before changing jobs, I tried taking stock of my own skills.","furigana":"てんしょく|まえ|じぶん|たなおろ"}],"notes":"棚卸 is the \"stocktaking\": at the end of a period the stock of goods is counted and valued (棚卸をする, 棚卸資産 \"inventories\" on the balance sheet). It is more often written 棚卸し or たな卸し. Figuratively, one now says スキルの棚卸し or キャリアの棚卸し for \"taking stock of one's own skills\"; the older meaning \"listing someone's faults\" has become rare.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仕入先との価格交渉が難航している。","romaji":"Shiiresaki to no kakaku kōshō ga nankō shite iru.","german":"Price negotiations with the suppliers are struggling.","furigana":"しいれさき|かかくこうしょう|なんこう"},{"japanese":"円安で輸入品の仕入れ値が上がり、値上げせざるを得ない。","romaji":"En'yasu de yunyūhin no shiirene ga agari, neage sezaru o enai.","german":"Because of the weak yen, the purchase price of imported goods has gone up, and we have no choice but to raise prices.","furigana":"えんやす|ゆにゅうひん|しい|ね|あ|ねあ|え"},{"japanese":"料理長は毎朝、市場へ魚の仕入れに行く。","romaji":"Ryōrichō wa maiasa, ichiba e sakana no shiire ni iku.","german":"The head chef goes to the market every morning to buy fish.","furigana":"りょうりちょう|まいあさ|いちば|さかな|しい|い"}],"notes":"仕入 is the purchase of goods or materials for resale or production. In running text one mostly writes 仕入れ; the verb is the transitive ichidan verb 仕入れる; without okurigana it appears mainly in compounds and in bookkeeping: 仕入先 \"supplier\", 仕入値 \"purchase price\". Unlike 購入 \"purchase\" in general, 仕入 always concerns business purchasing of goods; 調達 \"procurement\" is broader.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"資産を時価で評価する会計基準が導入された。","romaji":"Shisan o jika de hyōka suru kaikei kijun ga dōnyū sareta.","german":"An accounting standard that values assets at market value was introduced.","furigana":"しさん|じか|ひょうか|かいけいきじゅん|どうにゅう"},{"japanese":"この店のウニは時価と書いてあって、値段が分からない。","romaji":"Kono mise no uni wa jika to kaite atte, nedan ga wakaranai.","german":"The sea urchin at this restaurant is marked \"market price\", so I don't know how much it costs.","furigana":"みせ|じか|か|ねだん|わ"},{"japanese":"その会社の時価総額は一兆円を超えた。","romaji":"Sono kaisha no jika sōgaku wa itchōen o koeta.","german":"The company's market capitalization has exceeded one trillion yen.","furigana":"かいしゃ|じかそうがく|いっちょうえん|こ"}],"notes":"時価 is the current market or daily value: 時価で評価する \"to value at market value\", 時価総額 \"market capitalization\". Opposites are 簿価 \"book value\" and 定価 \"list price\". At sushi restaurants, expensive fish are often marked only 時価 \"market price\" instead of a price - notorious because you only learn the amount at the register.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"原油相場の高騰が物価上昇の要因だ。","romaji":"Gen'yu sōba no kōtō ga bukka jōshō no yōin da.","german":"The surge in crude oil prices is a factor in rising prices.","furigana":"げんゆそうば|こうとう|ぶっかじょうしょう|よういん"},{"japanese":"結婚式のご祝儀は、友人なら三万円が相場だ。","romaji":"Kekkonshiki no goshūgi wa, yūjin nara sanman'en ga sōba da.","german":"As a wedding gift of money, 30,000 yen is the going rate for a friend.","furigana":"けっこんしき|しゅうぎ|ゆうじん|さんまんえん|そうば"},{"japanese":"夏休みの宿題は最終日にやるものと相場が決まっている。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi no shukudai wa saishūbi ni yaru mono to sōba ga kimatte iru.","german":"It is a well-known fact that summer vacation homework is done on the last day.","furigana":"なつやす|しゅくだい|さいしゅうび|そうば|き"}],"notes":"相場 is first of all the \"market price, quotation\": 為替相場 \"exchange rate\", 株式相場, 相場が上がる/下がる. It also denotes the \"customary amount\", for example for monetary gifts: ご祝儀の相場は三万円. The fixed phrase 〜と相場が決まっている means \"it is generally taken for granted that ...\". 相場を張る \"to speculate\" is rather old-fashioned. Unlike 値段 \"price of an item\", 相場 always means a generally valid market value or yardstick.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"荷為替手形を銀行に持ち込んで買取を依頼した。","romaji":"Nigawase tegata o ginkō ni mochikonde kaitori o irai shita.","german":"I brought the documentary bill to the bank and asked them to purchase it.","furigana":"にがわせてがた|ぎんこう|も|こ|かいとり|いらい"},{"japanese":"輸出業者は、船積み書類を付けた荷為替手形で代金を回収する。","romaji":"Yushutsu gyōsha wa, funazumi shorui o tsuketa nigawase tegata de daikin o kaishū suru.","german":"The exporter collects payment through a documentary bill of exchange with attached shipping documents.","furigana":"ゆしゅつぎょうしゃ|ふなづ|しょるい|つ|にがわせてがた|だいきん|かいしゅう"},{"japanese":"貿易実務の授業で、荷為替の仕組みを図で説明してもらった。","romaji":"Bōeki jitsumu no jugyō de, nigawase no shikumi o zu de setsumei shite moratta.","german":"In the trade practice class, we had the mechanism of documentary bills explained with a diagram.","furigana":"ぼうえきじつむ|じゅぎょう|にがわせ|しく|ず|せつめい"}],"notes":"荷為替 is a term from foreign trade financing: the exporter draws a bill of exchange (為替手形) on the importer, attaches the shipping documents such as 船荷証券 and 保険証券, and has his bank purchase it - this is the 荷為替手形 \"documentary bill\". It is often combined with a 信用状 \"letter of credit\". The reading is にがわせ with voiced が. A purely technical term.","levelNote":"Compound: 荷 (N1) + 為替 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"信用状を開設して貿易代金の決済を保証する。","romaji":"Shin'yōjō o kaisetsu shite bōeki daikin no kessai o hoshō suru.","german":"We open a letter of credit to guarantee settlement of the trade payment.","furigana":"しんようじょう|かいせつ|ぼうえきだいきん|けっさい|ほしょう"},{"japanese":"初めての取引先なので、信用状による決済を条件にした。","romaji":"Hajimete no torihikisaki na node, shin'yōjō ni yoru kessai o jōken ni shita.","german":"Since it was a new business partner, we made settlement by letter of credit a condition.","furigana":"はじ|とりひきさき|しんようじょう|けっさい|じょうけん"},{"japanese":"書類の記載が信用状の条件と一致せず、支払いが拒否された。","romaji":"Shorui no kisai ga shin'yōjō no jōken to itchi sezu, shiharai ga kyohi sareta.","german":"The payment was refused because the entries in the documents did not match the terms of the letter of credit.","furigana":"しょるい|きさい|しんようじょう|じょうけん|いっち|しはら|きょひ"}],"notes":"信用状 is the \"letter of credit\", in practice often written L/C: the importer's bank commits to pay against presentation of the prescribed documents. Typical: 信用状を開設する \"to open a letter of credit\", 信用状を発行する, 信用状の条件, 信用状付き取引. 状 here means \"letter, document\", as in 招待状. A purely technical term of foreign trade.","levelNote":"Compound: 信用 (N3) + 状 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"船荷証券は貨物の所有権を表す有価証券だ。","romaji":"Funani shōken wa kamotsu no shoyūken o arawasu yūka shōken da.","german":"A bill of lading is a negotiable instrument representing ownership of the cargo.","furigana":"ふなにしょうけん|かもつ|しょゆうけん|あらわ|ゆうかしょうけん"},{"japanese":"船会社は貨物を船に積んだ後、船荷証券を発行する。","romaji":"Funagaisha wa kamotsu o fune ni tsunda ato, funani shōken o hakkō suru.","german":"After loading the cargo onto the ship, the shipping company issues a bill of lading.","furigana":"ふながいしゃ|かもつ|ふね|つ|あと|ふなにしょうけん|はっこう"},{"japanese":"船荷証券の到着が遅れて、港で貨物を引き取れなかった。","romaji":"Funani shōken no tōchaku ga okurete, minato de kamotsu o hikitorenakatta.","german":"The bill of lading arrived late, so we couldn't collect the cargo at the port.","furigana":"ふなにしょうけん|とうちゃく|おく|みなと|かもつ|ひ|と"}],"notes":"船荷証券 is the \"bill of lading\" (often written B/L). The shipping company issues it after loading; it confirms receipt of the goods and documents the right to delivery, so it is a negotiable instrument (有価証券). Typical: 船荷証券を発行する/提示する/裏書する. For air freight there is instead the 航空運送状 (air waybill), which is not a negotiable instrument.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"保険証券を輸出書類に添付する。","romaji":"Hoken shōken o yushutsu shorui ni tenpu suru.","german":"The insurance policy is attached to the export documents.","furigana":"ほけんしょうけん|ゆしゅつしょるい|てんぷ"},{"japanese":"事故の連絡をする際は、保険証券の番号を伝えてください。","romaji":"Jiko no renraku o suru sai wa, hoken shōken no bangō o tsutaete kudasai.","german":"When reporting an accident, please tell us your insurance policy number.","furigana":"じこ|れんらく|さい|ほけんしょうけん|ばんごう|つた"},{"japanese":"生命保険の保険証券を、金庫に大切に保管している。","romaji":"Seimei hoken no hoken shōken o, kinko ni taisetsu ni hokan shite iru.","german":"I keep my life insurance policy carefully in a safe.","furigana":"せいめいほけん|ほけんしょうけん|きんこ|たいせつ|ほかん"}],"notes":"保険証券 is the \"insurance policy\", the document by which the insurer confirms the contract - for life, car or cargo insurance. In foreign trade it is one of the shipping documents in CIF transactions. Do not confuse it with 保険証, the health insurance card. Typical: 保険証券番号, 保険証券を保管する/紛失する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"貿易収支が赤字に転落した。","romaji":"Bōeki shūshi ga akaji ni tenraku shita.","german":"The trade balance fell into deficit.","furigana":"ぼうえきしゅうし|あかじ|てんらく"},{"japanese":"円安で輸出が伸び、貿易収支は三年ぶりに黒字になった。","romaji":"En'yasu de yushutsu ga nobi, bōeki shūshi wa sannenburi ni kuroji ni natta.","german":"Exports grew thanks to the weak yen, and the trade balance turned to a surplus for the first time in three years.","furigana":"えんやす|ゆしゅつ|の|ぼうえきしゅうし|さんねん|くろじ"},{"japanese":"エネルギー価格の上昇が貿易収支を悪化させている。","romaji":"Enerugī kakaku no jōshō ga bōeki shūshi o akka sasete iru.","german":"Rising energy prices are worsening the trade balance.","furigana":"かかく|じょうしょう|ぼうえきしゅうし|あっか"}],"notes":"貿易収支 is the \"trade balance\": exports of goods minus imports of goods. Typical are 貿易収支が黒字/赤字になる, 赤字に転落する, 改善する/悪化する; one also says 貿易黒字 and 貿易赤字 for short. The trade balance is part of the broader 経常収支 \"current account\", which also includes services and investment income. 収支 alone means \"income and expenditure\".","levelNote":"Compound: 貿易 (N4) + 収支 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"経常収支の黒字幅が縮小している。","romaji":"Keijō shūshi no kurojihaba ga shukushō shite iru.","german":"The current account surplus is shrinking.","furigana":"けいじょうしゅうし|くろじはば|しゅくしょう"},{"japanese":"日本は貿易赤字でも、海外からの投資収益が多いため経常収支は黒字だ。","romaji":"Nihon wa bōeki akaji demo, kaigai kara no tōshi shūeki ga ōi tame keijō shūshi wa kuroji da.","german":"Even with a trade deficit, Japan's current account is in surplus because of its large investment income from abroad.","furigana":"にほん|ぼうえきあかじ|かいがい|とうししゅうえき|おお|けいじょうしゅうし|くろじ"},{"japanese":"観光客の増加でサービス収支が改善し、経常収支の黒字が拡大した。","romaji":"Kankōkyaku no zōka de sābisu shūshi ga kaizen shi, keijō shūshi no kuroji ga kakudai shita.","german":"The service balance improved with the increase in tourists, and the current account surplus widened.","furigana":"かんこうきゃく|ぞうか|しゅうし|かいぜん|けいじょうしゅうし|くろじ|かくだい"}],"notes":"経常収支 is the \"current account\": it comprises 貿易収支 \"trade balance\", サービス収支 \"services balance\" and the primary and secondary income (所得収支). Japan often has a current account surplus despite a trade deficit, because its investment income from abroad is high. Typical: 経常収支が黒字/赤字, 黒字幅が拡大/縮小する. 経常 means \"ordinary, recurring\", as in 経常利益.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"資本収支の動向が為替相場に影響を与える。","romaji":"Shihon shūshi no dōkō ga kawase sōba ni eikyō o ataeru.","german":"Developments in the capital account affect exchange rates.","furigana":"しほんしゅうし|どうこう|かわせそうば|えいきょう|あた"},{"japanese":"海外企業の買収が増え、資本収支に大きな変化が見られた。","romaji":"Kaigai kigyō no baishū ga fue, shihon shūshi ni ōki na henka ga mirareta.","german":"With more acquisitions of foreign companies, a major change was seen in the capital account.","furigana":"かいがいきぎょう|ばいしゅう|ふ|しほんしゅうし|おお|へんか|み"},{"japanese":"教科書では、国際収支は経常収支と資本収支に分けて説明されている。","romaji":"Kyōkasho de wa, kokusai shūshi wa keijō shūshi to shihon shūshi ni wakete setsumei sarete iru.","german":"In the textbook, the balance of payments is explained as divided into the current account and the capital account.","furigana":"きょうかしょ|こくさいしゅうし|けいじょうしゅうし|しほんしゅうし|わ|せつめい"}],"notes":"資本収支 is the \"capital account\", i.e. cross-border capital flows such as direct investment and securities purchases. Together with the 経常収支 it forms the classic breakdown of the balance of payments. In official Japanese statistics it was replaced in 2014 by 金融収支 \"financial account\" and 資本移転等収支; in textbooks and older texts, however, 資本収支 is still common.","levelNote":"Compound: 資本 (N3) + 収支 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"国際収支の均衡を保つことが経済安定の基盤だ。","romaji":"Kokusai shūshi no kinkō o tamotsu koto ga keizai antei no kiban da.","german":"Maintaining balance in the balance of payments is the foundation of economic stability.","furigana":"こくさいしゅうし|きんこう|たも|けいざいあんてい|きばん"},{"japanese":"財務省と日銀は、毎月国際収支統計を公表している。","romaji":"Zaimushō to Nichigin wa, maitsuki kokusai shūshi tōkei o kōhyō shite iru.","german":"The Ministry of Finance and the Bank of Japan publish balance of payments statistics every month.","furigana":"ざいむしょう|にちぎん|まいつきこくさいしゅうしとうけい|こうひょう"},{"japanese":"国際収支とは、一国が外国と行ったすべての取引を記録したものだ。","romaji":"Kokusai shūshi to wa, ikkoku ga gaikoku to okonatta subete no torihiki o kiroku shita mono da.","german":"The balance of payments is a record of all the transactions a country has carried out with foreign countries.","furigana":"こくさいしゅうし|いっこく|がいこく|おこな|とりひき|きろく"}],"notes":"国際収支 is the \"balance of payments\": the overall account of all economic transactions between a country and the rest of the world. It is divided into sub-accounts such as 経常収支 \"current account\" and 金融収支 or, formerly, 資本収支 \"capital account\". Typical are 国際収支統計 and 国際収支の均衡. 収支 alone means \"income and expenditure\", e.g. 家計の収支.","levelNote":"Compound: 国際 (N5) + 収支 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二国間の貿易摩擦が深刻化している。","romaji":"Nikokukan no bōeki masatsu ga shinkokuka shite iru.","german":"Trade friction between the two countries is becoming serious.","furigana":"にこくかん|ぼうえきまさつ|しんこくか"},{"japanese":"一九八〇年代には、半導体をめぐる日米貿易摩擦が大きな問題になった。","romaji":"Sen kyūhyaku hachijū nendai ni wa, handōtai o meguru Nichibei bōeki masatsu ga ōki na mondai ni natta.","german":"In the 1980s, the Japan-US trade friction over semiconductors became a major problem.","furigana":"|はんどうたい|にちべいぼうえきまさつ|おお|もんだい"},{"japanese":"貿易摩擦を避けるため、その自動車メーカーは現地生産を始めた。","romaji":"Bōeki masatsu o sakeru tame, sono jidōsha mēkā wa genchi seisan o hajimeta.","german":"To avoid trade friction, that automaker started local production.","furigana":"ぼうえきまさつ|さ|じどうしゃ|げんちせいさん|はじ"}],"notes":"貿易摩擦 is \"trade tension\" between countries, triggered by trade imbalances, tariffs or import restrictions. Typical: 貿易摩擦が起きる/激化する/深刻化する, 貿易摩擦を解消する. Historically well known is the 日米貿易摩擦 of the 1980s over cars and semiconductors. An escalation to mutual punitive tariffs is called 貿易戦争 \"trade war\". 摩擦 literally means \"friction\", also used figuratively in 文化摩擦.","levelNote":"Compound: 貿易 (N4) + 摩擦 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"保護貿易主義の台頭が世界経済のリスクとなっている。","romaji":"Hogo bōeki shugi no taitō ga sekai keizai no risuku to natte iru.","german":"The rise of protectionism is becoming a risk for the world economy.","furigana":"ほごぼうえきしゅぎ|たいとう|せかいけいざい"},{"japanese":"不況になると、各国は保護貿易に傾きやすい。","romaji":"Fukyō ni naru to, kakkoku wa hogo bōeki ni katamukiyasui.","german":"In a recession, countries easily lean toward protectionism.","furigana":"ふきょう|かっこく|ほごぼうえき|かたむ"},{"japanese":"国内の農家を守るため、政府は輸入米に高い関税をかける保護貿易政策をとってきた。","romaji":"Kokunai no nōka o mamoru tame, seifu wa yunyūmai ni takai kanzei o kakeru hogo bōeki seisaku o totte kita.","german":"To protect domestic farmers, the government has pursued a protectionist policy of imposing high tariffs on imported rice.","furigana":"こくない|のうか|まも|せいふ|ゆにゅうまい|たか|かんぜい|ほごぼうえきせいさく"}],"notes":"保護貿易 is a trade policy that protects domestic industry from foreign competition through tariffs, import quotas or subsidies; the opposite is 自由貿易 \"free trade\". The attitude behind it is called 保護貿易主義 \"protectionism\". Typical are 保護貿易政策 and 保護貿易に傾く/転じる. Technical language, common in business news and in civics class.","levelNote":"Compound: 保護 (N2) + 貿易 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自由貿易の推進が経済発展に寄与する。","romaji":"Jiyū bōeki no suishin ga keizai hatten ni kiyo suru.","german":"Promoting free trade contributes to economic development.","furigana":"じゆうぼうえき|すいしん|けいざいはってん|きよ"},{"japanese":"日本は多くの国と自由貿易協定を結んでいる。","romaji":"Nihon wa ōku no kuni to jiyū bōeki kyōtei o musunde iru.","german":"Japan has concluded free trade agreements with many countries.","furigana":"にほん|おお|くに|じゆうぼうえききょうてい|むす"},{"japanese":"保護貿易か自由貿易か、授業で議論した。","romaji":"Hogo bōeki ka jiyū bōeki ka, jugyō de giron shita.","german":"We debated protectionism versus free trade in class.","furigana":"ほごぼうえき|じゆうぼうえき|じゅぎょう|ぎろん"}],"notes":"自由貿易 is trade without tariffs, quantity restrictions or other government intervention; the opposite is 保護貿易 \"protectionism\". Typical combinations are 自由貿易を推進する \"to promote free trade\", 自由貿易体制 \"free trade regime\" and above all 自由貿易協定 \"free trade agreement\" (FTA). Attributively it takes の; the word belongs to the language of newspapers, politics and economics.","levelNote":"Compound: 自由 (N4) + 貿易 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"両国は貿易障壁の撤廃で合意した。","romaji":"Ryōkoku wa bōeki shōheki no teppai de gōi shita.","german":"The two countries agreed to remove trade barriers.","furigana":"りょうこく|ぼうえきしょうへき|てっぱい|ごうい"},{"japanese":"高い関税は代表的な貿易障壁だ。","romaji":"Takai kanzei wa daihyōteki na bōeki shōheki da.","german":"High tariffs are a typical trade barrier.","furigana":"たか|かんぜい|だいひょうてき|ぼうえきしょうへき"},{"japanese":"複雑な検査制度が、事実上の貿易障壁になっている。","romaji":"Fukuzatsu na kensa seido ga, jijitsujō no bōeki shōheki ni natte iru.","german":"The complicated inspection system has effectively become a trade barrier.","furigana":"ふくざつ|けんさせいど|じじつじょう|ぼうえきしょうへき"}],"notes":"貿易障壁 are \"barriers to trade\" that make international movement of goods more difficult. One distinguishes 関税障壁 (tariffs) and 非関税障壁 (e.g. quotas, strict standards, complicated inspection procedures). Typical verbs: 貿易障壁を撤廃する/取り除く, 貿易障壁を設ける, 貿易障壁が高い. The word belongs to the language of economic policy and the news; 障壁 alone means \"barrier, obstacle\" (言葉の障壁).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ダンピング輸出に対して反ダンピング関税が課された。","romaji":"Danpingu yushutsu ni taishite han danpingu kanzei ga kasareta.","german":"Anti-dumping duties were imposed on dumped exports.","furigana":"ゆしゅつ|たい|はん|かんぜい|か"},{"japanese":"中国製の鉄鋼がダンピングされているとして、調査が始まった。","romaji":"Chūgokusei no tekkō ga danpingu sarete iru to shite, chōsa ga hajimatta.","german":"An investigation began on the grounds that Chinese steel is being dumped.","furigana":"ちゅうごくせい|てっこう|ちょうさ|はじ"},{"japanese":"公共工事でのダンピング受注が問題になっている。","romaji":"Kōkyō kōji de no danpingu juchū ga mondai ni natte iru.","german":"Underbidding in public works contracts has become a problem.","furigana":"こうきょうこうじ|じゅちゅう|もんだい"}],"notes":"ダンピング refers to selling goods below the production cost or domestic price, especially in exports, in order to drive competitors out of the market; the official Japanese term for it is 不当廉売. Fixed combinations are 反ダンピング関税 (also アンチダンピング関税) \"anti-dumping duty\" and, domestically, ダンピング受注 \"accepting orders at dumping prices\" in tenders. Unlike the neutral 安売り \"cheap sale\", ダンピング implies unfair intent. Rarely also used as a する-verb (ダンピングする).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"輸入急増に対してセーフガードを発動した。","romaji":"Yunyū kyūzō ni taishite sēfugādo o hatsudō shita.","german":"Safeguard measures were invoked in response to the surge in imports.","furigana":"ゆにゅうきゅうぞう|たい|はつどう"},{"japanese":"牛肉の輸入が急に増えたため、セーフガードが発動された。","romaji":"Gyūniku no yunyū ga kyū ni fueta tame, sēfugādo ga hatsudō sareta.","german":"Because beef imports suddenly increased, the safeguard was triggered.","furigana":"ぎゅうにく|ゆにゅう|きゅう|ふ|はつどう"},{"japanese":"政府は、セーフガードの発動は慎重に判断すると述べた。","romaji":"Seifu wa, sēfugādo no hatsudō wa shinchō ni handan suru to nobeta.","german":"The government said it would judge carefully whether to invoke the safeguard.","furigana":"せいふ|はつどう|しんちょう|はんだん|の"}],"notes":"セーフガード is above all the WTO's trade-law safeguard clause, under which a country may temporarily raise tariffs or limit quantities when imports suddenly rise sharply; the official term is 緊急輸入制限. The fixed verb for it is 発動する: セーフガードを発動する \"to invoke the safeguard\", passive セーフガードが発動される. In other fields (IT, finance), セーフガード can mean \"safeguard, protective mechanism\" in general.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"原産地証明書の提出が求められる。","romaji":"Gensanchi shōmeisho no teishutsu ga motomerareru.","german":"Submission of a certificate of origin is required.","furigana":"げんさんちしょうめいしょ|ていしゅつ|もと"},{"japanese":"トマトの原産地は南アメリカだそうです。","romaji":"Tomato no gensanchi wa Minami Amerika da sō desu.","german":"The tomato is said to originate from South America.","furigana":"げんさんち|みなみ"},{"japanese":"スーパーで肉の原産地表示を確認した。","romaji":"Sūpā de niku no gensanchi hyōji o kakunin shita.","german":"I checked the country-of-origin label on the meat at the supermarket.","furigana":"にく|げんさんちひょうじ|かくにん"}],"notes":"原産地 is the place or country where something originally comes from: for plants and animals the natural homeland (トマトの原産地は南米), for goods the place of manufacture or production. On foods one reads 原産地表示 \"origin labeling\"; when a country is explicitly meant, one says 原産国 \"country of origin\". The simple 産地 refers more to the present growing or production area (りんごの産地), whereas 原産地 stresses the origin.","levelNote":"Compound: 原産 (N2) + 地 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二国間通商条約の改定交渉が始まった。","romaji":"Nikokukan tsūshō jōyaku no kaitei kōshō ga hajimatta.","german":"Negotiations to revise the bilateral commercial treaty have begun.","furigana":"にこくかんつうしょうじょうやく|かいていこうしょう|はじ"},{"japanese":"江戸時代の末、日本は欧米諸国と通商条約を結んだ。","romaji":"Edo jidai no sue, Nihon wa Ōbei shokoku to tsūshō jōyaku o musunda.","german":"At the end of the Edo period, Japan concluded commercial treaties with Western countries.","furigana":"えどじだい|すえ|にほん|おうべいしょこく|つうしょうじょうやく|むす"},{"japanese":"不平等な通商条約の改正は、明治政府の大きな課題だった。","romaji":"Fubyōdō na tsūshō jōyaku no kaisei wa, Meiji seifu no ōki na kadai datta.","german":"Revising the unequal commercial treaties was a major task for the Meiji government.","furigana":"ふびょうどう|つうしょうじょうやく|かいせい|めいじせいふ|おお|かだい"}],"notes":"通商条約 is a formal treaty between states on trade and navigation; 通商 is the formal word for trade between countries, 条約 a treaty under international law. One says 通商条約を結ぶ／締結する, 改正する \"to revise\". The word is especially well known from history (the 日米修好通商条約 of 1858, the \"unequal treaties\"); for modern arrangements one hears 貿易協定 or 経済連携協定 more often.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"最恵国待遇の原則に基づいて関税率が適用される。","romaji":"Saikeikoku taigū no gensoku ni motozuite kanzeiritsu ga tekiyō sareru.","german":"Tariff rates are applied based on the principle of most-favored-nation treatment.","furigana":"さいけいこくたいぐう|げんそく|もと|かんぜいりつ|てきよう"},{"japanese":"両国は互いに最恵国待遇を与えることで合意した。","romaji":"Ryōkoku wa tagai ni saikeikoku taigū o ataeru koto de gōi shita.","german":"The two countries agreed to grant each other most-favored-nation treatment.","furigana":"りょうこく|たが|さいけいこくたいぐう|あた|ごうい"},{"japanese":"WTOに加盟すると、最恵国待遇を受けられるようになる。","romaji":"WTO ni kamei suru to, saikeikoku taigū o ukerareru yō ni naru.","german":"Joining the WTO makes it possible to receive most-favored-nation treatment.","furigana":"かめい|さいけいこくたいぐう|う"}],"notes":"最恵国待遇 literally means \"treatment of the most favored nation\": a state must grant trade advantages that it gives to one country to all treaty partners equally. It is a basic WTO principle; typical combinations are 最恵国待遇を与える \"to grant\", を受ける \"to receive\" and 最恵国待遇の原則. The counterpart for equal treatment with domestic persons is 内国民待遇. Purely technical language from trade law and the news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"発展途上国からの輸入品に特恵関税が適用される。","romaji":"Hatten tojōkoku kara no yunyūhin ni tokkei kanzei ga tekiyō sareru.","german":"Preferential tariffs are applied to imports from developing countries.","furigana":"はってんとじょうこく|ゆにゅうひん|とっけいかんぜい|てきよう"},{"japanese":"この国からの輸入品は、特恵関税の対象外になった。","romaji":"Kono kuni kara no yunyūhin wa, tokkei kanzei no taishōgai ni natta.","german":"Imports from this country are no longer eligible for the preferential tariff.","furigana":"くに|ゆにゅうひん|とっけいかんぜい|たいしょうがい"},{"japanese":"原産地証明があれば、特恵関税で輸入できます。","romaji":"Gensanchi shōmei ga areba, tokkei kanzei de yunyū dekimasu.","german":"With a certificate of origin, you can import at the preferential tariff.","furigana":"げんさんちしょうめい|とっけいかんぜい|ゆにゅう"}],"notes":"特恵関税 is a reduced preferential tariff that a country grants to certain partners, above all developing countries under the 一般特恵関税制度 (GSP). Watch the reading: 特恵 is read とっけい. Typical are 特恵関税を適用する／が適用される and 特恵関税の対象（外）になる. Unlike 最恵国待遇, which treats all partners equally, 特恵関税 deliberately favors individual countries.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"関税同盟を結成して域内の自由貿易を実現する。","romaji":"Kanzei dōmei o kessei shite ikinai no jiyū bōeki o jitsugen suru.","german":"Eine Zollunion gründen und den Freihandel innerhalb der Region verwirklichen.","furigana":"かんぜいどうめい|けっせい|いきない|じゆうぼうえき|じつげん"},{"japanese":"19世紀のドイツ関税同盟は、国家統一への一歩となった。","romaji":"Jūkyū seiki no Doitsu kanzei dōmei wa, kokka tōitsu e no ippo to natta.","german":"Der Deutsche Zollverein des 19. Jahrhunderts wurde zu einem Schritt auf dem Weg zur nationalen Einigung.","furigana":"せいき|かんぜいどうめい|こっかとういつ|いっぽ"},{"japanese":"関税同盟の加盟国は、域外に対して共通の関税をかける。","romaji":"Kanzei dōmei no kameikoku wa, ikigai ni taishite kyōtsū no kanzei o kakeru.","german":"Die Mitgliedstaaten einer Zollunion erheben gegenüber Drittländern einen gemeinsamen Zoll.","furigana":"かんぜいどうめい|かめいこく|いきがい|たい|きょうつう|かんぜい"}],"notes":"関税同盟 ist eine Zollunion: Die Mitglieder schaffen die Zölle untereinander ab und erheben gegenüber Drittländern einen gemeinsamen Außenzoll. Darin unterscheidet sie sich vom 自由貿易地域 „Freihandelszone“, in der jedes Land seine eigenen Außenzölle behält. Man sagt 関税同盟を結成する／結ぶ, に加盟する. Historisch bekannt ist die ドイツ関税同盟 „Deutscher Zollverein“.","levelNote":"Compound: 関税 (N1) + 同盟 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"経済連携協定の発効により関税が段階的に撤廃される。","romaji":"Keizai renkei kyōtei no hakkō ni yori kanzei ga dankaiteki ni teppai sareru.","german":"With the entry into force of the economic partnership agreement, tariffs will be abolished in stages.","furigana":"けいざいれんけいきょうてい|はっこう|かんぜい|だんかいてき|てっぱい"},{"japanese":"日本とEUの経済連携協定で、ワインが安く買えるようになった。","romaji":"Nihon to EU no keizai renkei kyōtei de, wain ga yasuku kaeru yō ni natta.","german":"Thanks to the economic partnership agreement between Japan and the EU, wine can now be bought cheaply.","furigana":"にほん|けいざいれんけいきょうてい|やす|か"},{"japanese":"経済連携協定に基づいて、インドネシアから看護師候補者を受け入れている。","romaji":"Keizai renkei kyōtei ni motozuite, Indoneshia kara kangoshi kōhosha o ukeirete iru.","german":"Under the economic partnership agreement, nurse candidates are accepted from Indonesia.","furigana":"けいざいれんけいきょうてい|もと|かんごしこうほしゃ|う|い"}],"notes":"経済連携協定 (EPA) goes beyond a pure free trade agreement (自由貿易協定, FTA): besides reducing tariffs, it also regulates investment, services, intellectual property and the movement of people. 連携 means \"cooperation, linkage\". Typical are 経済連携協定を締結する／結ぶ, が発効する \"takes effect\" and ～に基づいて. The media mostly use the abbreviation EPA.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"多国間交渉が難航し、合意に至らなかった。","romaji":"Takokukan kōshō ga nankō shi, gōi ni itaranakatta.","german":"The multilateral negotiations dragged on and no agreement was reached.","furigana":"たこくかんこうしょう|なんこう|ごうい|いた"},{"japanese":"多国間交渉では、すべての国の利害を調整するのが難しい。","romaji":"Takokukan kōshō de wa, subete no kuni no rigai o chōsei suru no ga muzukashii.","german":"In multilateral negotiations, it is difficult to reconcile the interests of all countries.","furigana":"たこくかんこうしょう|くに|りがい|ちょうせい|むずか"},{"japanese":"政府は二国間ではなく、多国間交渉の場で解決を目指す方針だ。","romaji":"Seifu wa nikokukan de wa naku, takokukan kōshō no ba de kaiketsu o mezasu hōshin da.","german":"The government's policy is to aim for a solution in multilateral negotiations rather than bilaterally.","furigana":"せいふ|にこくかん|たこくかんこうしょう|ば|かいけつ|めざ|ほうしん"}],"notes":"多国間交渉 are negotiations in which many states take part at the same time, such as the WTO trade rounds; the counterpart is 二国間交渉 \"bilateral negotiations\". Typical verbs are 多国間交渉に臨む \"to enter negotiations\", が難航する \"stall\", が決裂する \"break down\" and がまとまる \"come to a conclusion\". The word belongs to the language of politics and the news; 多国間 alone means \"multilateral\" (多国間協定, 多国間主義).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二国間協定に基づいて投資保護が保障される。","romaji":"Nikokukan kyōtei ni motozuite tōshi hogo ga hoshō sareru.","german":"Investment protection is guaranteed under the bilateral agreement.","furigana":"にこくかんきょうてい|もと|とうしほご|ほしょう"},{"japanese":"両国は漁業に関する二国間協定を結んだ。","romaji":"Ryōkoku wa gyogyō ni kansuru nikokukan kyōtei o musunda.","german":"The two countries concluded a bilateral agreement on fisheries.","furigana":"りょうこく|ぎょぎょう|かん|にこくかんきょうてい|むす"},{"japanese":"二国間協定により、ワーキングホリデーの制度が始まった。","romaji":"Nikokukan kyōtei ni yori, wākingu horidē no seido ga hajimatta.","german":"The working holiday program began through a bilateral agreement.","furigana":"にこくかんきょうてい|せいど|はじ"}],"notes":"二国間協定 is an agreement between exactly two states; the counterpart is 多国間協定. In newspapers it is often written 2国間協定. 協定 is used more for concrete arrangements in a specific field (fisheries, air traffic, investment), 条約 for formal treaties between states. Typical: 二国間協定を結ぶ／締結する, ～に基づいて, により.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先端技術の輸出規制が強化されている。","romaji":"Sentan gijutsu no yushutsu kisei ga kyōka sarete iru.","german":"Export controls on advanced technology are being tightened.","furigana":"せんたんぎじゅつ|ゆしゅつきせい|きょうか"},{"japanese":"半導体材料の輸出規制が、両国の関係を悪化させた。","romaji":"Handōtai zairyō no yushutsu kisei ga, ryōkoku no kankei o akka saseta.","german":"Export controls on semiconductor materials worsened relations between the two countries.","furigana":"はんどうたいざいりょう|ゆしゅつきせい|りょうこく|かんけい|あっか"},{"japanese":"不作のため、政府は米の輸出規制に踏み切った。","romaji":"Fusaku no tame, seifu wa kome no yushutsu kisei ni fumikitta.","german":"Because of the poor harvest, the government decided to restrict rice exports.","furigana":"ふさく|せいふ|こめ|ゆしゅつきせい|ふ|き"}],"notes":"輸出規制 covers all government restrictions on exports, such as licensing requirements for security-relevant technology or export bans during shortages. Typical verbs are 輸出規制を強化する \"to tighten\", を緩和する \"to relax\", をかける／課す and に踏み切る \"to decide to ...\". 規制 is broader than 制限 \"limitation\"; the counterpart is 輸入規制. Voluntary restrictions are called 輸出自主規制.","levelNote":"Compound: 輸出 (N4) + 規制 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"農産物の輸入制限をめぐる対立が続いている。","romaji":"Nōsanbutsu no yunyū seigen o meguru tairitsu ga tsuzuite iru.","german":"The dispute over import restrictions on agricultural products continues.","furigana":"のうさんぶつ|ゆにゅうせいげん|たいりつ|つづ"},{"japanese":"政府は牛肉の輸入制限を段階的に撤廃した。","romaji":"Seifu wa gyūniku no yunyū seigen o dankaiteki ni teppai shita.","german":"The government abolished the import restrictions on beef in stages.","furigana":"せいふ|ぎゅうにく|ゆにゅうせいげん|だんかいてき|てっぱい"},{"japanese":"輸入制限がかかると、国内の価格が上がりやすい。","romaji":"Yunyū seigen ga kakaru to, kokunai no kakaku ga agariyasui.","german":"When import restrictions are imposed, domestic prices tend to rise.","furigana":"ゆにゅうせいげん|こくない|かかく|あ"}],"notes":"輸入制限 is the limiting of imports, for example through quantity quotas (輸入割当), bans or safeguard clauses (セーフガード). Typical verbs are 輸入制限を設ける／かける, を撤廃する \"to abolish\", を緩和する \"to relax\"; intransitive 輸入制限がかかる. 輸入規制 is somewhat broader and also includes health and safety requirements.","levelNote":"Compound: 輸入 (N4) + 制限 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"輸入割当制度により輸入量が制限されている。","romaji":"Yunyū wariate seido ni yori yunyūryō ga seigen sarete iru.","german":"The import volume is restricted by the import quota system.","furigana":"ゆにゅうわりあてせいど|ゆにゅうりょう|せいげん"},{"japanese":"この会社は毎年、政府から輸入割当を受けている。","romaji":"Kono kaisha wa maitoshi, seifu kara yunyū wariate o ukete iru.","german":"This company receives an import quota from the government every year.","furigana":"かいしゃ|まいとし|せいふ|ゆにゅうわりあて|う"},{"japanese":"輸入割当の枠を拡大するよう、相手国から求められた。","romaji":"Yunyū wariate no waku o kakudai suru yō, aitekoku kara motomerareta.","german":"The partner country asked us to expand the import quota.","furigana":"ゆにゅうわりあて|わく|かくだい|あいてこく|もと"}],"notes":"輸入割当 is an import quota: the state decides what quantity of a good may be imported and allocates it to importers. The spelling varies between 輸入割当, 輸入割当て and 輸入割り当て; in official documents 輸入割当 (IQ) is usually used. Typical are 輸入割当を受ける \"to receive a quota\", 輸入割当制度 and 輸入割当の枠 \"quota framework\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"原産地規則の厳格な適用が貿易に影響している。","romaji":"Gensanchi kisoku no genkaku na tekiyō ga bōeki ni eikyō shite iru.","german":"The strict application of the rules of origin is affecting trade.","furigana":"げんさんちきそく|げんかく|てきよう|ぼうえき|えいきょう"},{"japanese":"部品の多くを第三国から輸入しているため、原産地規則を満たせない。","romaji":"Buhin no ōku o daisangoku kara yunyū shite iru tame, gensanchi kisoku o mitasenai.","german":"We import many parts from third countries, so we cannot meet the rules of origin.","furigana":"ぶひん|おお|だいさんごく|ゆにゅう|げんさんちきそく|み"},{"japanese":"協定ごとに原産地規則が異なるので、担当者は混乱している。","romaji":"Kyōtei goto ni gensanchi kisoku ga kotonaru node, tantōsha wa konran shite iru.","german":"The rules of origin differ for each agreement, so the staff in charge are confused.","furigana":"きょうてい|げんさんちきそく|こと|たんとうしゃ|こんらん"}],"notes":"原産地規則 are the rules of origin of a trade agreement: they determine when a good counts as a product of a treaty country and so receives tariff benefits, for example through a minimum share of domestic value added. The typical verb is 原産地規則を満たす \"to meet\"; also 原産地規則が厳しい／異なる. Technical language from customs and foreign trade.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"貿易統計に基づいて通商政策を立案する。","romaji":"Bōeki tōkei ni motozuite tsūshō seisaku o ritsuan suru.","german":"Trade policy is drafted based on the trade statistics.","furigana":"ぼうえきとうけい|もと|つうしょうせいさく|りつあん"},{"japanese":"財務省が発表した貿易統計によると、先月は貿易赤字だった。","romaji":"Zaimushō ga happyō shita bōeki tōkei ni yoru to, sengetsu wa bōeki akaji datta.","german":"According to the trade statistics released by the Ministry of Finance, there was a trade deficit last month.","furigana":"ざいむしょう|はっぴょう|ぼうえきとうけい|せんげつ|ぼうえきあかじ"},{"japanese":"卒業論文のために、過去二十年の貿易統計を調べた。","romaji":"Sotsugyō ronbun no tame ni, kako nijū-nen no bōeki tōkei o shirabeta.","german":"For my graduation thesis, I looked up the trade statistics of the past twenty years.","furigana":"そつぎょうろんぶん|かこにじゅうねん|ぼうえきとうけい|しら"}],"notes":"貿易統計 are the official statistics on imports and exports; in Japan the Ministry of Finance (財務省) publishes them monthly. Typical frames are 貿易統計によると \"according to the trade statistics\", 貿易統計を発表する／公表する and 貿易統計に基づいて. In the news, terms such as 貿易黒字 \"trade surplus\" and 貿易赤字 \"trade deficit\" usually follow.","levelNote":"Compound: 貿易 (N4) + 統計 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"加工貿易に依存した経済構造を見直す必要がある。","romaji":"Kakō bōeki ni izon shita keizai kōzō o minaosu hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"The economic structure that depends on processing trade needs to be reviewed.","furigana":"かこうぼうえき|いぞん|けいざいこうぞう|みなお|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"資源の少ない日本は、加工貿易で経済成長を遂げた。","romaji":"Shigen no sukunai Nihon wa, kakō bōeki de keizai seichō o togeta.","german":"Japan, with few resources, achieved economic growth through processing trade.","furigana":"しげん|すく|にほん|かこうぼうえき|けいざいせいちょう|と"},{"japanese":"加工貿易とは、原料を輸入して製品に加工し、輸出することだ。","romaji":"Kakō bōeki to wa, genryō o yunyū shite seihin ni kakō shi, yushutsu suru koto da.","german":"Processing trade means importing raw materials, processing them into products, and exporting them.","furigana":"かこうぼうえき|げんりょう|ゆにゅう|せいひん|かこう|ゆしゅつ"}],"notes":"加工貿易 refers to the trade model of importing raw materials, processing them into finished goods at home and exporting those. The word is known from Japanese school lessons, because it long counted as the foundation of the economy of resource-poor Japan. Typical are 加工貿易で発展する／成長する and 加工貿易に依存する. Do not confuse it with 中継貿易, where goods are resold without processing.","levelNote":"Compound: 加工 (N1) + 貿易 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"シンガポールは中継貿易の拠点として発展した。","romaji":"Shingapōru wa chūkei bōeki no kyoten to shite hatten shita.","german":"Singapore developed as a base for entrepot trade.","furigana":"ちゅうけいぼうえき|きょてん|はってん"},{"japanese":"琉球王国は、中国と東南アジアを結ぶ中継貿易で栄えた。","romaji":"Ryūkyū ōkoku wa, Chūgoku to Tōnan Ajia o musubu chūkei bōeki de sakaeta.","german":"The Ryukyu Kingdom flourished on entrepot trade linking China and Southeast Asia.","furigana":"|ちゅうごく|とうなん|むす|ちゅうけいぼうえき|さか"},{"japanese":"香港は今も中継貿易の重要な港だ。","romaji":"Honkon wa ima mo chūkei bōeki no jūyō na minato da.","german":"Hong Kong is still an important port for entrepot trade.","furigana":"ほんこん|いま|ちゅうけいぼうえき|じゅうよう|みなと"}],"notes":"中継貿易 is transit or intermediary trade: goods are brought into a country and resold unprocessed to a third country; 中継 means \"relaying, relay\". Typical are 中継貿易で栄える \"to flourish through entrepot trade\" and 中継貿易の拠点／港. Classic examples are Singapore, Hong Kong and the historical Ryukyu Kingdom. Unlike 加工貿易, no processing takes place.","levelNote":"Compound: 中継 (N1) + 貿易 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"海外での委託加工によりコストを削減する。","romaji":"Kaigai de no itaku kakō ni yori kosuto o sakugen suru.","german":"Costs are reduced through commissioned processing overseas.","furigana":"かいがい|いたくかこう|さくげん"},{"japanese":"当社は大手メーカーから部品の委託加工を請け負っている。","romaji":"Tōsha wa ōte mēkā kara buhin no itaku kakō o ukeotte iru.","german":"Our company takes on commissioned processing of parts from major manufacturers.","furigana":"とうしゃ|おおて|ぶひん|いたくかこう|う|お"},{"japanese":"委託加工では、原材料は依頼主が支給する。","romaji":"Itaku kakō de wa, genzairyō wa irainushi ga shikyū suru.","german":"In commissioned processing, the client supplies the raw materials.","furigana":"いたくかこう|げんざいりょう|いらいぬし|しきゅう"}],"notes":"委託加工 is contract processing: the client (依頼主) supplies raw materials or parts, another company processes them for a processing fee (加工賃) and delivers the result back. Typical are 委託加工を請け負う \"to take on\", 委託加工に出す \"to put out for contract processing\" and 委託加工貿易 for cross-border manufacturing. 外注 \"outsourcing\" is more general and covers any kind of subcontracting.","levelNote":"Compound: 委託 (N1) + 加工 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"総合商社が資源開発に積極的に投資している。","romaji":"Sōgō shōsha ga shigen kaihatsu ni sekkyokuteki ni tōshi shite iru.","german":"General trading companies are actively investing in resource development.","furigana":"そうごうしょうしゃ|しげんかいはつ|せっきょくてき|とうし"},{"japanese":"兄は商社に勤めていて、海外出張が多い。","romaji":"Ani wa shōsha ni tsutomete ite, kaigai shutchō ga ōi.","german":"My older brother works at a trading company and often goes on business trips abroad.","furigana":"あに|しょうしゃ|つと|かいがいしゅっちょう|おお"},{"japanese":"小さな専門商社が、ヨーロッパから家具を輸入している。","romaji":"Chiisa na senmon shōsha ga, Yōroppa kara kagu o yunyū shite iru.","german":"A small specialized trading company imports furniture from Europe.","furigana":"ちい|せんもんしょうしゃ|かぐ|ゆにゅう"}],"notes":"商社 is a trading house that mediates import, export and trade in goods; a typically Japanese form of enterprise. A distinction is made between 総合商社 \"general trading company\" (large conglomerates with business from raw materials to foodstuffs) and 専門商社 \"specialized trading company\" for particular industries. Typical: 商社に勤める \"to work at a trading company\", 商社マン \"trading company employee\". A neutral term in everyday use.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"全国に販売網を構築して市場シェアを拡大する。","romaji":"Zenkoku ni hanbaimō o kōchiku shite shijō shea o kakudai suru.","german":"We will build a sales network nationwide and expand our market share.","furigana":"ぜんこく|はんばいもう|こうちく|しじょう|かくだい"},{"japanese":"あの会社はアジア全域に販売網を持っている。","romaji":"Ano kaisha wa Ajia zen'iki ni hanbaimō o motte iru.","german":"That company has a sales network all over Asia.","furigana":"かいしゃ|ぜんいき|はんばいもう|も"},{"japanese":"地元の小さな酒蔵が、ネットを使って販売網を広げた。","romaji":"Jimoto no chiisa na sakagura ga, netto o tsukatte hanbaimō o hirogeta.","german":"A small local sake brewery expanded its sales network using the internet.","furigana":"じもと|ちい|さかぐら|つか|はんばいもう|ひろ"}],"notes":"販売網 is a company's sales network made up of branches, dealers and sales channels; 網 means \"net\". Typical verbs are 販売網を築く／構築する \"to build\", を広げる \"to expand\" and を持つ. Related words are 販路 \"sales channel, outlet\" (販路を開拓する) and 流通網 \"distribution network\", which stresses logistics more.","levelNote":"Compound: 販売 (N3) + 網 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"供給網の寸断が生産活動に支障をきたした。","romaji":"Kyōkyūmō no sundan ga seisan katsudō ni shishō o kitashita.","german":"The disruption of the supply network hindered production activity.","furigana":"きょうきゅうもう|すんだん|せいさんかつどう|ししょう"},{"japanese":"半導体不足で、世界の供給網が混乱した。","romaji":"Handōtai busoku de, sekai no kyōkyūmō ga konran shita.","german":"Because of the semiconductor shortage, global supply chains were thrown into confusion.","furigana":"はんどうたいぶそく|せかい|きょうきゅうもう|こんらん"},{"japanese":"政府は重要物資の供給網を国内に戻す方針を示した。","romaji":"Seifu wa jūyō busshi no kyōkyūmō o kokunai ni modosu hōshin o shimeshita.","german":"The government indicated a policy of bringing supply chains for important goods back to Japan.","furigana":"せいふ|じゅうようぶっし|きょうきゅうもう|こくない|もど|ほうしん|しめ"}],"notes":"供給網 is the Japanese word for supply chain or supply network, i.e. the chain from raw material suppliers through manufacturers to retail. In newspapers it often has the same meaning as the loanword サプライチェーン, which is more common in companies. Typical combinations are 供給網が寸断される \"is cut off\", が混乱する and 供給網を見直す／強化する.","levelNote":"Compound: 供給 (N3) + 網 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"サプライチェーンの多元化によりリスクを分散する。","romaji":"Sapurai chēn no tagenka ni yori risuku o bunsan suru.","german":"Risk is spread by diversifying the supply chain.","furigana":"たげんか|ぶんさん"},{"japanese":"コロナ禍で、多くの企業がサプライチェーンを見直した。","romaji":"Korona-ka de, ōku no kigyō ga sapurai chēn o minaoshita.","german":"During the COVID pandemic, many companies reviewed their supply chains.","furigana":"か|おお|きぎょう|みなお"},{"japanese":"当社はサプライチェーン全体で二酸化炭素の排出を減らす目標を立てた。","romaji":"Tōsha wa sapurai chēn zentai de nisanka tanso no haishutsu o herasu mokuhyō o tateta.","german":"Our company set a goal of reducing carbon dioxide emissions across the entire supply chain.","furigana":"とうしゃ|ぜんたい|にさんかたんそ|はいしゅつ|へ|もくひょう|た"}],"notes":"サプライチェーン is the loanword for the supply chain from raw material procurement to the end customer; its Japanese counterpart 供給網 sounds more official. Very common in business language, also in compounds such as サプライチェーン・マネジメント (SCM). Typical verbs are サプライチェーンを見直す \"to review\", を強化する and が寸断される. It is written with or without a middle dot (サプライ・チェーン).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"商品先物市場で原油の取引が活発だ。","romaji":"Shōhin sakimono shijō de gen'yu no torihiki ga kappatsu da.","german":"Crude oil trading is active on the commodity futures market.","furigana":"しょうひんさきものしじょう|げんゆ|とりひき|かっぱつ"},{"japanese":"彼は商品先物取引で大きな損失を出した。","romaji":"Kare wa shōhin sakimono torihiki de ōki na sonshitsu o dashita.","german":"He incurred large losses in commodity futures trading.","furigana":"かれ|しょうひんさきものとりひき|おお|そんしつ|だ"},{"japanese":"商品先物の電話勧誘には注意してください。","romaji":"Shōhin sakimono no denwa kan'yū ni wa chūi shite kudasai.","german":"Please be careful about telephone solicitations for commodity futures.","furigana":"しょうひんさきもの|でんわかんゆう|ちゅうい"}],"notes":"商品先物 are futures contracts on commodities such as oil, gold or grain; 商品 here means \"commodity\" in the exchange sense, 先物 \"futures contract\". It mostly appears in compounds: 商品先物取引 \"commodity futures trading\", 商品先物市場 \"commodity futures market\". The counterpart is 金融先物 \"financial futures\". Because of the high risk, the word is often associated for private individuals with aggressive sales calls (勧誘).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"約定書に署名して取引条件を確定する。","romaji":"Yakujōsho ni shomei shite torihiki jōken o kakutei suru.","german":"Sign the written agreement to finalize the terms of the transaction.","furigana":"やくじょうしょ|しょめい|とりひきじょうけん|かくてい"},{"japanese":"融資を受ける際、銀行取引約定書を提出した。","romaji":"Yūshi o ukeru sai, ginkō torihiki yakujōsho o teishutsu shita.","german":"When I took out the loan, I submitted a bank transaction agreement.","furigana":"ゆうし|う|さい|ぎんこうとりひきやくじょうしょ|ていしゅつ"},{"japanese":"約定書の内容をよく読んでから印鑑を押してください。","romaji":"Yakujōsho no naiyō o yoku yonde kara inkan o oshite kudasai.","german":"Please read the contents of the agreement carefully before you put your seal on it.","furigana":"やくじょうしょ|ないよう|よ|いんかん|お"}],"notes":"約定書 is a written agreement recording the terms of a transaction; 約定 (やくじょう) means \"agreement, arrangement\" and, in securities trading, also \"execution of an order\". The word is clearly more formal and rarer than the general 契約書 \"contract\" and appears mainly in fixed expressions such as 銀行取引約定書 or 約定書を交わす \"to exchange an agreement\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"随意契約の透明性を高めるべきだ。","romaji":"Zuii keiyaku no tōmeisei o takameru beki da.","german":"The transparency of non-competitive contracts should be increased.","furigana":"ずいいけいやく|とうめいせい|たか"},{"japanese":"その工事は入札を行わず、随意契約で発注された。","romaji":"Sono kōji wa nyūsatsu o okonawazu, zuii keiyaku de hatchū sareta.","german":"The construction work was ordered by non-competitive contract, without a tender.","furigana":"こうじ|にゅうさつ|おこな|ずいいけいやく|はっちゅう"},{"japanese":"少額の購入なら、随意契約が認められている。","romaji":"Shōgaku no kōnyū nara, zuii keiyaku ga mitomerarete iru.","german":"For small-value purchases, non-competitive contracts are permitted.","furigana":"しょうがく|こうにゅう|ずいいけいやく|みと"}],"notes":"随意契約 is a term from public procurement: an agency awards a contract directly to a chosen partner without a tender; 随意 means \"at will, at one's discretion\". The counterpart is 競争入札 or 一般競争入札 \"open competitive bidding\". Typical are 随意契約を結ぶ and 随意契約で発注する; in the news it often appears in connection with a lack of transparency. Short form in bureaucratic jargon: 随契.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"代金引換で商品を配達する。","romaji":"Daikin hikikae de shōhin o haitatsu suru.","german":"Goods are delivered cash on delivery.","furigana":"だいきんひきかえ|しょうひん|はいたつ"},{"japanese":"支払い方法は、クレジットカードか代金引換を選べます。","romaji":"Shiharai hōhō wa, kurejitto kādo ka daikin hikikae o erabemasu.","german":"As a payment method, you can choose credit card or cash on delivery.","furigana":"しはら|ほうほう|だいきんひきかえ|えら"},{"japanese":"代金引換の場合、別に手数料がかかります。","romaji":"Daikin hikikae no baai, betsu ni tesūryō ga kakarimasu.","german":"With cash on delivery, a separate fee is charged.","furigana":"だいきんひきかえ|ばあい|べつ|てすうりょう"}],"notes":"代金引換 is payment by cash on delivery: the recipient pays for the goods to the delivery person upon delivery. In everyday speech one almost always says the short form 代引き（だいびき）; 代金引換 appears more in order forms and terms of business. Typical are 代金引換で送る／支払う and 代金引換手数料 \"cash-on-delivery fee\". Do not confuse it with 着払い, where the recipient pays only the shipping costs.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"掛け売りの回収率が低下している。","romaji":"Kakeuri no kaishūritsu ga teika shite iru.","german":"The collection rate on credit sales is declining.","furigana":"か|う|かいしゅうりつ|ていか"},{"japanese":"昔の酒屋は、なじみの客に掛け売りをしていた。","romaji":"Mukashi no sakaya wa, najimi no kyaku ni kakeuri o shite ita.","german":"Liquor stores in the old days sold on credit to regular customers.","furigana":"むかし|さかや|きゃく|か|う"},{"japanese":"当店では掛け売りはお断りしております。","romaji":"Tōten de wa kakeuri wa okotowari shite orimasu.","german":"We do not accept credit sales at our store.","furigana":"とうてん|か|う|ことわ"}],"notes":"掛け売り is selling on credit or \"on account\": the customer takes the goods right away and pays later, for example at the end of the month. 掛け here stands for \"credit, running a tab\"; the outstanding claim is called 売掛金, and the buyer's side 掛け買い. The opposite is 現金売り \"cash sale\". Also written 掛売り; possible as a する-verb (掛け売りする). Colloquially one says ツケで売る.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"初回取引は前払いをお願いしている。","romaji":"Shokai torihiki wa maebarai o onegai shite iru.","german":"We ask for prepayment on the first transaction.","furigana":"しょかいとりひき|まえばら|ねが"},{"japanese":"この語学学校では、授業料を三か月分前払いする。","romaji":"Kono gogaku gakkō de wa, jugyōryō o sankagetsu-bun maebarai suru.","german":"At this language school, tuition is paid three months in advance.","furigana":"ごがくがっこう|じゅぎょうりょう|さん|げつぶんまえばら"},{"japanese":"給料の一部を前払いしてもらえないか、社長に頼んだ。","romaji":"Kyūryō no ichibu o maebarai shite moraenai ka, shachō ni tanonda.","german":"I asked the president whether I could get part of my salary paid in advance.","furigana":"きゅうりょう|いちぶ|まえばら|しゃちょう|たの"}],"notes":"前払い is payment in advance, before goods are delivered or a service is provided; the opposite is 後払い. As a する-verb: 授業料を前払いする. Fixed combinations are 前払い金 \"advance payment, down payment\", 前払い制 and 前払いをお願いする. In formal texts 前納 is also used. For salary one speaks of 給料の前払い \"salary advance\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"後払い方式の決済サービスが普及している。","romaji":"Atobarai hōshiki no kessai sābisu ga fukyū shite iru.","german":"Zahlungsdienste mit nachträglicher Bezahlung verbreiten sich.","furigana":"あとばら|ほうしき|けっさい|ふきゅう"},{"japanese":"通販で、商品が届いてから払える後払いを選んだ。","romaji":"Tsūhan de, shōhin ga todoite kara haraeru atobarai o eranda.","german":"Beim Onlinekauf habe ich die Option gewählt, erst nach Lieferung zu bezahlen.","furigana":"つうはん|しょうひん|とど|はら|あとばら|えら"},{"japanese":"修理代は後払いで結構です。","romaji":"Shūridai wa atobarai de kekkō desu.","german":"Die Reparatur können Sie auch hinterher bezahlen.","furigana":"しゅうりだい|あとばら|けっこう"}],"notes":"後払い bedeutet, erst nach Erhalt der Ware oder Leistung zu bezahlen; Gegenteil ist 前払い. Heute begegnet es vor allem beim Onlinekauf: 後払い決済 „Bezahlung nach Erhalt“ (auch „Buy now, pay later“), コンビニ後払い. Als する-Verb möglich (後払いする), häufiger aber 後払いで払う／後払いにする. Das deutsche „Nachzahlung“ im Sinne einer zusätzlichen Zahlung heißt dagegen 追加払い oder 追徴.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"高額商品は分割払いで購入できる。","romaji":"Kōgaku shōhin wa bunkatsu barai de kōnyū dekiru.","german":"Expensive items can be purchased in installments.","furigana":"こうがくしょうひん|ぶんかつばら|こうにゅう"},{"japanese":"パソコンを12回の分割払いで買った。","romaji":"Pasokon o jūni-kai no bunkatsu barai de katta.","german":"I bought the computer in 12 installments.","furigana":"かい|ぶんかつばら|か"},{"japanese":"分割払いにすると、手数料がかかることがある。","romaji":"Bunkatsu barai ni suru to, tesūryō ga kakaru koto ga aru.","german":"Paying in installments may incur fees.","furigana":"ぶんかつばら|てすうりょう"}],"notes":"分割払い is payment in installments; the opposite is 一括払い. Typical are 分割払いで買う, 分割払いにする and phrases like 12回の分割払い \"in twelve installments\"; one also says 分割で払う for short. With credit cards, one chooses at the register between 一回払い, 分割払い and リボ払い (revolving payment). Installment payments often come with fees (手数料).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一括払いの場合は割引が適用される。","romaji":"Ikkatsu barai no baai wa waribiki ga tekiyō sareru.","german":"A discount is applied for payment in full.","furigana":"いっかつばら|ばあい|わりびき|てきよう"},{"japanese":"「お支払いは一括払いでよろしいですか。」","romaji":"\"Oshiharai wa ikkatsu barai de yoroshii desu ka.\"","german":"\"Would you like to pay in a single payment?\"","furigana":"しはら|いっかつばら"},{"japanese":"車を一括払いで買うほどの貯金はない。","romaji":"Kuruma o ikkatsu barai de kau hodo no chokin wa nai.","german":"I don't have enough savings to buy a car in one lump sum.","furigana":"くるま|いっかつばら|か|ちょきん"}],"notes":"一括払い means paying the entire amount at once; the opposite is 分割払い. With credit cards it is also called 一回払い: the amount is charged in full on the next statement, so it is not necessarily paid \"immediately\". Typical are 一括払いで買う／支払う and 一括払いにする. 一括 alone means \"bundled together, in one piece\" (一括購入, 一括処理).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不動産の仲介手数料は法律で上限が定められている。","romaji":"Fudōsan no chūkai tesūryō wa hōritsu de jōgen ga sadamerarete iru.","german":"The upper limit of real estate brokerage fees is set by law.","furigana":"ふどうさん|ちゅうかいてすうりょう|ほうりつ|じょうげん|さだ"},{"japanese":"部屋を借りるとき、家賃一か月分の仲介手数料を払った。","romaji":"Heya o kariru toki, yachin ikkagetsu-bun no chūkai tesūryō o haratta.","german":"When I rented the room, I paid a brokerage fee equal to one month's rent.","furigana":"へや|か|やちんいっ|げつぶん|ちゅうかいてすうりょう|はら"},{"japanese":"このサイトは仲介手数料が無料なので人気がある。","romaji":"Kono saito wa chūkai tesūryō ga muryō na node ninki ga aru.","german":"This site is popular because it has no brokerage fee.","furigana":"ちゅうかいてすうりょう|むりょう|にんき"}],"notes":"仲介手数料 is the fee an intermediary receives for bringing a deal about, most commonly the agent's commission when renting or buying real estate. For rental apartments in Japan, it is limited by law to one month's rent (plus tax). Typical are 仲介手数料を払う, がかかる and 仲介手数料無料 \"no brokerage fee\". 仲介 means \"mediation\", 手数料 \"fee\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"倉庫の保管料が上昇し、コスト増の要因となっている。","romaji":"Sōko no hokanryō ga jōshō shi, kosuto zō no yōin to natte iru.","german":"Warehouse storage fees are rising and have become a factor in higher costs.","furigana":"そうこ|ほかんりょう|じょうしょう|ぞう|よういん"},{"japanese":"荷物を一週間預けたら、保管料を請求された。","romaji":"Nimotsu o isshūkan azuketara, hokanryō o seikyū sareta.","german":"After I left my luggage for a week, I was charged a storage fee.","furigana":"にもつ|いっしゅうかんあず|ほかんりょう|せいきゅう"},{"japanese":"引き取りが遅れると、一日ごとに保管料がかかります。","romaji":"Hikitori ga okureru to, ichinichi goto ni hokanryō ga kakarimasu.","german":"If pickup is delayed, a storage fee is charged for each day.","furigana":"ひ|と|おく|いちにち|ほかんりょう"}],"notes":"保管料 is the fee for keeping items in storage, such as goods in a warehouse, luggage, vehicles or documents; -料 forms fee words, as in 使用料 or 入場料. Typical are 保管料がかかる \"a storage fee is charged\", 保管料を払う／請求する. In warehousing it appears alongside 荷役料 \"handling fee\". The related verb is 保管する \"to keep, to store\".","levelNote":"Compound: 保管 (N1) + 料 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"港湾の荷役作業が自動化されている。","romaji":"Kōwan no niyaku sagyō ga jidōka sarete iru.","german":"Port cargo handling work is being automated.","furigana":"こうわん|にやくさぎょう|じどうか"},{"japanese":"台風の影響で、港の荷役が一時止まった。","romaji":"Taifū no eikyō de, minato no niyaku ga ichiji tomatta.","german":"Because of the typhoon, cargo handling at the port stopped temporarily.","furigana":"たいふう|えいきょう|みなと|にやく|いちじと"},{"japanese":"倉庫ではフォークリフトを使って荷役を行う。","romaji":"Sōko de wa fōkurifuto o tsukatte niyaku o okonau.","german":"At the warehouse, loading and unloading is done using forklifts.","furigana":"そうこ|つか|にやく|おこな"}],"notes":"荷役 is the loading and unloading of freight, especially in ports, on ships and in warehouses; it is read にやく. Typical are 荷役作業 \"cargo handling work\", 荷役業者, 荷役機械 and 荷役を行う. As an intransitive する-verb (荷役する) it is rare. Do not confuse it with 荷造り \"packing\" or 荷物 \"luggage\"; 荷役 is a technical word from logistics and shipping.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"通関業者に輸入手続きを委託した。","romaji":"Tsūkan gyōsha ni yunyū tetsuzuki o itaku shita.","german":"We entrusted the import procedures to a customs broker.","furigana":"つうかんぎょうしゃ|ゆにゅうてつづ|いたく"},{"japanese":"通関業者が書類を準備してくれたので、手続きはすぐ終わった。","romaji":"Tsūkan gyōsha ga shorui o junbi shite kureta node, tetsuzuki wa sugu owatta.","german":"The customs broker prepared the documents, so the procedures were finished quickly.","furigana":"つうかんぎょうしゃ|しょるい|じゅんび|てつづ|お"},{"japanese":"通関業者で働くなら、通関士の資格があると有利だ。","romaji":"Tsūkan gyōsha de hataraku nara, tsūkanshi no shikaku ga aru to yūri da.","german":"If you want to work at a customs brokerage, a customs specialist qualification gives you an edge.","furigana":"つうかんぎょうしゃ|はたら|つうかんし|しかく|ゆうり"}],"notes":"通関業者 is a licensed customs service provider who handles customs declarations and other formalities on behalf of importers and exporters; they are often freight forwarders. Licensing is governed by the 通関業法, and the qualified specialist within the firm is called 通関士. Typical are 通関業者に依頼する／委託する. 通関 means \"customs clearance\", 業者 \"tradesperson, firm\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"保税倉庫に貨物を一時的に保管する。","romaji":"Hozei sōko ni kamotsu o ichijiteki ni hokan suru.","german":"Cargo is temporarily stored in a bonded warehouse.","furigana":"ほぜいそうこ|かもつ|いちじてき|ほかん"},{"japanese":"輸入した貨物は、関税を払うまで保税倉庫に置かれる。","romaji":"Yunyū shita kamotsu wa, kanzei o harau made hozei sōko ni okareru.","german":"Imported cargo is kept in a bonded warehouse until the customs duty is paid.","furigana":"ゆにゅう|かもつ|かんぜい|はら|ほぜいそうこ|お"},{"japanese":"空港の保税倉庫で、ワインを関税なしで保管している。","romaji":"Kūkō no hozei sōko de, wain o kanzei nashi de hokan shite iru.","german":"Wine is stored duty-free in the bonded warehouse at the airport.","furigana":"くうこう|ほぜいそうこ|かんぜい|ほかん"}],"notes":"保税倉庫 is a bonded warehouse in which imported goods may be stored without duty until they are imported or re-exported; 保税 means \"under customs bond, duty suspended\". The umbrella term for such areas is 保税地域. Typical are 保税倉庫に保管する／搬入する. A technical word from customs and logistics.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自由貿易地域の設立により域内の貿易が活発化した。","romaji":"Jiyū bōeki chiiki no setsuritsu ni yori ikinai no bōeki ga kappatsuka shita.","german":"The establishment of the free trade area has stimulated trade within the region.","furigana":"じゆうぼうえきちいき|せつりつ|いきない|ぼうえき|かっぱつか"},{"japanese":"ASEAN自由貿易地域では、域内の関税がほぼゼロになった。","romaji":"Asean jiyū bōeki chiiki de wa, ikinai no kanzei ga hobo zero ni natta.","german":"In the ASEAN Free Trade Area, tariffs within the region have fallen to almost zero.","furigana":"じゆうぼうえきちいき|いきない|かんぜい"},{"japanese":"沖縄には、企業が関税なしで原料を輸入できる自由貿易地域がある。","romaji":"Okinawa ni wa, kigyō ga kanzei nashi de genryō o yunyū dekiru jiyū bōeki chiiki ga aru.","german":"In Okinawa, there is a free trade zone where companies can import raw materials without tariffs.","furigana":"おきなわ|きぎょう|かんぜい|げんりょう|ゆにゅう|じゆうぼうえきちいき"}],"notes":"自由貿易地域 has two meanings: a free trade area between several states that abolish tariffs among themselves (ASEAN自由貿易地域), and a designated area within a country into which goods may be imported duty-free and processed (as in Okinawa). Unlike a 関税同盟 \"customs union\", in a free trade area each country keeps its own external tariffs. Typical: 自由貿易地域を設ける／設立する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"経済特区に外国企業を誘致している。","romaji":"Keizai tokku ni gaikoku kigyō o yūchi shite iru.","german":"Foreign companies are being invited to the special economic zone.","furigana":"けいざいとっく|がいこくきぎょう|ゆうち"},{"japanese":"深圳は1980年に経済特区に指定され、急速に発展した。","romaji":"Shinsen wa sen kyūhyaku hachijū-nen ni keizai tokku ni shitei sare, kyūsoku ni hatten shita.","german":"Shenzhen was designated a special economic zone in 1980 and developed rapidly.","furigana":"|ねん|けいざいとっく|してい|きゅうそく|はってん"},{"japanese":"経済特区では、税金や規制の面で優遇が受けられる。","romaji":"Keizai tokku de wa, zeikin ya kisei no men de yūgū ga ukerareru.","german":"In special economic zones, preferential treatment in taxes and regulations is available.","furigana":"けいざいとっく|ぜいきん|きせい|めん|ゆうぐう|う"}],"notes":"経済特区 (short for 経済特別区) is a special economic zone in which taxes, tariffs and regulations are relaxed to attract investment; Shenzhen in China is famous. Typical combinations are 経済特区に指定する／される, 経済特区を設ける and 企業を誘致する \"to attract companies\". In Japan, similar arrangements are more often called 構造改革特区 or 国家戦略特区; in general it is shortened to 特区.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"海外直接投資の増加が国内雇用に影響を与えている。","romaji":"Kaigai chokusetsu tōshi no zōka ga kokunai koyō ni eikyō o ataete iru.","german":"The increase in foreign direct investment is affecting domestic employment.","furigana":"かいがいちょくせつとうし|ぞうか|こくないこよう|えいきょう|あた"},{"japanese":"その企業はベトナムに工場を建て、直接投資を拡大している。","romaji":"Sono kigyō wa Betonamu ni kōjō o tate, chokusetsu tōshi o kakudai shite iru.","german":"The company is building factories in Vietnam and expanding its direct investment.","furigana":"きぎょう|こうじょう|た|ちょくせつとうし|かくだい"},{"japanese":"政府は外国からの直接投資を呼び込むため、法人税を下げた。","romaji":"Seifu wa gaikoku kara no chokusetsu tōshi o yobikomu tame, hōjinzei o sageta.","german":"To attract direct investment from abroad, the government lowered the corporate tax.","furigana":"せいふ|がいこく|ちょくせつとうし|よ|こ|ほうじんぜい|さ"}],"notes":"直接投資 is investment by which one founds a company abroad or takes a stake that gives influence over management, for example by building factories. Common are 海外直接投資 / 対外直接投資 \"outward direct investment\" and 対内直接投資 \"inward direct investment\" (FDI). The opposite term is 間接投資 (portfolio investment). Typical: 直接投資を拡大する／呼び込む.","levelNote":"Compound: 直接 (N4) + 投資 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"間接投資を通じて新興国の成長に参加する。","romaji":"Kansetsu tōshi o tsūjite shinkōkoku no seichō ni sanka suru.","german":"Take part in the growth of emerging economies through portfolio investment.","furigana":"かんせつとうし|つう|しんこうこく|せいちょう|さんか"},{"japanese":"経営に関わらない株式の取得は、間接投資に分類される。","romaji":"Keiei ni kakawaranai kabushiki no shutoku wa, kansetsu tōshi ni bunrui sareru.","german":"Acquiring shares without involvement in management is classified as portfolio investment.","furigana":"けいえい|かか|かぶしき|しゅとく|かんせつとうし|ぶんるい"},{"japanese":"投資信託を通じた間接投資なら、少額から始められる。","romaji":"Tōshi shintaku o tsūjita kansetsu tōshi nara, shōgaku kara hajimerareru.","german":"With indirect investment through investment trusts, you can start with a small amount.","furigana":"とうししんたく|つう|かんせつとうし|しょうがく|はじ"}],"notes":"In economics, 間接投資 refers to investment in foreign stocks or bonds without influence on management, i.e. portfolio investment (also 証券投資); the opposite term is 直接投資. In retail-investor jargon, 間接投資 also means investing through funds (投資信託) instead of buying individual stocks directly. Typical: 間接投資を通じて, 間接投資に分類される.","levelNote":"Compound: 間接 (N2) + 投資 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"現地法人を設立して海外市場に本格参入する。","romaji":"Genchi hōjin o setsuritsu shite kaigai shijō ni honkaku sannyū suru.","german":"Set up a local subsidiary and enter the overseas market in earnest.","furigana":"げんちほうじん|せつりつ|かいがいしじょう|ほんかくさんにゅう"},{"japanese":"彼はタイの現地法人に社長として赴任した。","romaji":"Kare wa Tai no genchi hōjin ni shachō to shite funin shita.","german":"He was posted to the Thai local subsidiary as its president.","furigana":"かれ|げんちほうじん|しゃちょう|ふにん"},{"japanese":"支店と違って、現地法人は現地の法律に基づく独立した会社だ。","romaji":"Shiten to chigatte, genchi hōjin wa genchi no hōritsu ni motozuku dokuritsu shita kaisha da.","german":"Unlike a branch, a local subsidiary is an independent company under local law.","furigana":"してん|ちが|げんちほうじん|げんち|ほうりつ|もと|どくりつ|かいしゃ"}],"notes":"現地法人 is a company of a foreign firm established under the law of the host country, usually a subsidiary. Unlike a 支店 \"branch\" or a 駐在員事務所 \"representative office\", it is an independent legal entity. Typical are 現地法人を設立する, 現地法人に赴任する \"to be posted to the overseas subsidiary\" and a country name in front (タイの現地法人).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"現地企業との合弁事業でリスクを分担する。","romaji":"Genchi kigyō to no gōben jigyō de risuku o buntan suru.","german":"Share the risks through a joint venture with a local company.","furigana":"げんちきぎょう|ごうべんじぎょう|ぶんたん"},{"japanese":"両社は電池の合弁事業を立ち上げると発表した。","romaji":"Ryōsha wa denchi no gōben jigyō o tachiageru to happyō shita.","german":"The two companies announced that they would launch a joint venture for batteries.","furigana":"りょうしゃ|でんち|ごうべんじぎょう|た|あ|はっぴょう"},{"japanese":"経営方針の違いから、合弁事業は三年で解消された。","romaji":"Keiei hōshin no chigai kara, gōben jigyō wa sannen de kaishō sareta.","german":"Because of differences in management policy, the joint venture was dissolved after three years.","furigana":"けいえいほうしん|ちが|ごうべんじぎょう|さんねん|かいしょう"}],"notes":"合弁事業 is a joint venture in which two or more companies pool capital; 合弁 means \"joint capital participation\". The company founded for it is called 合弁会社. Typical verbs are 合弁事業を立ち上げる \"to launch\", を解消する \"to dissolve\" and 合弁で設立する. In business language ジョイントベンチャー (JV) is also common.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"知的所有権の保護が国際貿易の重要な課題だ。","romaji":"Chiteki shoyūken no hogo ga kokusai bōeki no jūyō na kadai da.","german":"Protecting intellectual property rights is an important issue in international trade.","furigana":"ちてきしょゆうけん|ほご|こくさいぼうえき|じゅうよう|かだい"},{"japanese":"他社の知的所有権を侵害したとして訴えられた。","romaji":"Tasha no chiteki shoyūken o shingai shita to shite uttaerareta.","german":"The company was sued for infringing another company's intellectual property rights.","furigana":"たしゃ|ちてきしょゆうけん|しんがい|うった"},{"japanese":"特許や著作権は、知的所有権の代表的な例だ。","romaji":"Tokkyo ya chosakuken wa, chiteki shoyūken no daihyōteki na rei da.","german":"Patents and copyrights are typical examples of intellectual property rights.","furigana":"とっきょ|ちょさくけん|ちてきしょゆうけん|だいひょうてき|れい"}],"notes":"知的所有権 are rights to intellectual creations such as patents (特許権), copyrights (著作権) and trademark rights (商標権). Today the official standard term is 知的財産権, shortened to 知財; 知的所有権 is the older translation, but it is still found in texts on international trade. Typical verbs are 知的所有権を侵害する \"to infringe\" and を保護する \"to protect\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"模倣品の流通を阻止するための対策が強化されている。","romaji":"Mohōhin no ryūtsū o soshi suru tame no taisaku ga kyōka sarete iru.","german":"Measures to stop the distribution of counterfeit goods are being strengthened.","furigana":"もほうひん|りゅうつう|そし|たいさく|きょうか"},{"japanese":"ネットで買ったブランドバッグが模倣品だった。","romaji":"Netto de katta burando baggu ga mohōhin datta.","german":"The designer bag I bought online turned out to be a counterfeit.","furigana":"か|もほうひん"},{"japanese":"税関で大量の模倣品が見つかり、没収された。","romaji":"Zeikan de tairyō no mohōhin ga mitsukari, bosshū sareta.","german":"A large quantity of counterfeit goods was found at customs and confiscated.","furigana":"ぜいかん|たいりょう|もほうひん|み|ぼっしゅう"}],"notes":"模倣品 are imitations that copy the design, brand or styling of an original product. In official texts it often appears as the pair 模倣品・海賊版 \"counterfeits and pirated copies\"; 偽造品 stresses the criminal forgery, and colloquially one says 偽物 or コピー商品. Typical are 模倣品が出回る \"are in circulation\", 模倣品を取り締まる and 模倣品対策.","levelNote":"Compound: 模倣 (N1) + 品 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"並行輸入品は正規品より安く手に入る。","romaji":"Heikō yunyūhin wa seikihin yori yasuku te ni hairu.","german":"Parallel-imported goods can be obtained more cheaply than genuine authorized products.","furigana":"へいこうゆにゅうひん|せいきひん|やす|て|はい"},{"japanese":"並行輸入品は、メーカーの保証が受けられない場合がある。","romaji":"Heikō yunyūhin wa, mēkā no hoshō ga ukerarenai baai ga aru.","german":"Parallel-imported goods may not be covered by the manufacturer's warranty.","furigana":"へいこうゆにゅうひん|ほしょう|う|ばあい"},{"japanese":"その店は海外ブランドの化粧品を並行輸入して安く売っている。","romaji":"Sono mise wa kaigai burando no keshōhin o heikō yunyū shite yasuku utte iru.","german":"That store parallel-imports foreign brand cosmetics and sells them cheaply.","furigana":"みせ|かいがい|けしょうひん|へいこうゆにゅう|やす|う"}],"notes":"並行輸入 is the import of genuine brand goods bypassing the official distribution channels (正規代理店); the goods imported this way are called 並行輸入品, as opposed to 正規品. They are not counterfeits (模倣品), but warranty and customer service may be limited. As a transitive する-verb: 化粧品を並行輸入する.","levelNote":"Compound: 並行 (N2) + 輸入 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"半製品を輸入して国内で最終加工を行う。","romaji":"Hanseihin o yunyū shite kokunai de saishū kakō o okonau.","german":"Import semi-finished products and carry out the final processing in Japan.","furigana":"はんせいひん|ゆにゅう|こくない|さいしゅうかこう|おこな"},{"japanese":"半製品の在庫が増えて、倉庫がいっぱいになった。","romaji":"Hanseihin no zaiko ga fuete, sōko ga ippai ni natta.","german":"Stock of semi-finished products increased until the warehouse was full.","furigana":"はんせいひん|ざいこ|ふ|そうこ"},{"japanese":"この工場では、半製品を組み立てて完成品にしている。","romaji":"Kono kōjō de wa, hanseihin o kumitatete kanseihin ni shite iru.","german":"In this factory, semi-finished products are assembled into finished products.","furigana":"こうじょう|はんせいひん|く|た|かんせいひん"}],"notes":"半製品 are semi-finished goods: products that have gone through part of manufacturing and are to be processed further, but can also be sold as such. In accounting they are distinguished from 仕掛品 \"work in process\", which are not yet saleable. The sequence is 原材料 → 半製品 → 製品／完成品. A technical word from industry and accounting.","levelNote":"Compound: 製品 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"国際見本市に出展して新製品を紹介する。","romaji":"Kokusai mihon'ichi ni shutten shite shinseihin o shōkai suru.","german":"Exhibit at an international trade fair and introduce new products.","furigana":"こくさいみほんいち|しゅってん|しんせいひん|しょうかい"},{"japanese":"来月、東京で家具の見本市が開かれる。","romaji":"Raigetsu, Tōkyō de kagu no mihon'ichi ga hirakareru.","german":"A furniture trade fair will be held in Tokyo next month.","furigana":"らいげつ|とうきょう|かぐ|みほんいち|ひら"},{"japanese":"見本市で知り合った会社と取引を始めた。","romaji":"Mihon'ichi de shiriatta kaisha to torihiki o hajimeta.","german":"We started doing business with a company we met at the trade fair.","furigana":"みほんいち|し|あ|かいしゃ|とりひき|はじ"}],"notes":"見本市 is a trade fair where companies exhibit products to business customers; note the reading みほんいち. Typical are 見本市に出展する \"to exhibit\", 見本市が開かれる／開催される and 国際見本市. In current usage 展示会 and トレードショー are also common; 博覧会, by contrast, means a large exhibition for the general public (expo).","levelNote":"Compound: 見本 (N2) + 市 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"商品見本を送付して品質を確認してもらう。","romaji":"Shōhin mihon o sōfu shite hinshitsu o kakunin shite morau.","german":"Send a product sample and have them confirm the quality.","furigana":"しょうひんみほん|そうふ|ひんしつ|かくにん"},{"japanese":"商品見本を請求したら、翌日には届いた。","romaji":"Shōhin mihon o seikyū shitara, yokujitsu ni wa todoita.","german":"When I requested a product sample, it arrived the next day.","furigana":"しょうひんみほん|せいきゅう|よくじつ|とど"},{"japanese":"見本市のブースに商品見本を並べた。","romaji":"Mihon'ichi no būsu ni shōhin mihon o narabeta.","german":"We displayed product samples at the trade fair booth.","furigana":"みほんいち|しょうひんみほん|なら"}],"notes":"商品見本 is a product sample that is sent or shown to customers or dealers to check quality and appearance. In everyday speech one usually just says サンプル or 見本; 商品見本 sounds more businesslike. Typical verbs are 商品見本を送る／送付する, を請求する \"to request\" and を並べる \"to display\". Free samples for end customers are more often called 試供品.","levelNote":"Compound: 商品 (N3) + 見本 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"徹底した品質管理が信頼の基盤だ。","romaji":"Tettei shita hinshitsu kanri ga shinrai no kiban da.","german":"Thorough quality control is the foundation of trust.","furigana":"てってい|ひんしつかんり|しんらい|きばん"},{"japanese":"品質管理を徹底するため、すべての製品を検査している。","romaji":"Hinshitsu kanri o tettei suru tame, subete no seihin o kensa shite iru.","german":"To ensure strict quality control, we inspect every product.","furigana":"ひんしつかんり|てってい|せいひん|けんさ"},{"japanese":"姉は食品メーカーの品質管理部で働いている。","romaji":"Ane wa shokuhin mēkā no hinshitsu kanribu de hataraite iru.","german":"My older sister works in the quality control department of a food manufacturer.","furigana":"あね|しょくひん|ひんしつかんりぶ|はたら"}],"notes":"品質管理 is quality control or quality management in production, QC in English. Typical combinations are 品質管理を徹底する \"to carry out thoroughly\", を強化する and 品質管理部 \"quality control department\". Related is 品質保証 \"quality assurance\" (QA), which stresses the guarantee to the customer more. Neutral; commonplace in industry and the food sector.","levelNote":"Compound: 品質 (N1) + 管理 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"クレーム対応の迅速さが顧客満足度に影響する。","romaji":"Kurēmu taiō no jinsokusa ga kokyaku manzokudo ni eikyō suru.","german":"The speed of complaint handling affects customer satisfaction.","furigana":"たいおう|じんそく|こきゃくまんぞくど|えいきょう"},{"japanese":"隣の席の客が、料理に髪の毛が入っていたとクレームをつけた。","romaji":"Tonari no seki no kyaku ga, ryōri ni kaminoke ga haitte ita to kurēmu o tsuketa.","german":"The customer at the next table complained that there was a hair in the food.","furigana":"となり|せき|きゃく|りょうり|かみ|け|はい"},{"japanese":"新製品について、お客様から同じクレームが何件も来ている。","romaji":"Shinseihin ni tsuite, okyakusama kara onaji kurēmu ga nanken mo kite iru.","german":"The same complaint about the new product has come in from customers many times.","furigana":"しんせいひん|きゃくさま|おな|なんけん|き"}],"notes":"In Japanese, クレーム almost always means a customer complaint, often with a demand for replacement - not a legal \"claim\" as in English (for that: 請求, 損害賠償請求). Typical are クレームをつける \"to complain\", クレームが来る/入る and クレーム対応 \"complaint handling\"; someone who constantly complains without justification is a クレーマー. More neutral and general is 苦情 (also for noise among neighbors), stronger is 抗議 \"protest\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不可抗力条項により納期の遅延が免責された。","romaji":"Fuka kōryoku jōkō ni yori nōki no chien ga menseki sareta.","german":"The delay in delivery was exempted from liability under the force majeure clause.","furigana":"ふかこうりょくじょうこう|のうき|ちえん|めんせき"},{"japanese":"台風による遅延は不可抗力なので、補償の対象になりません。","romaji":"Taifū ni yoru chien wa fuka kōryoku na node, hoshō no taishō ni narimasen.","german":"Delays caused by typhoons are force majeure and therefore not eligible for compensation.","furigana":"たいふう|ちえん|ふかこうりょく|ほしょう|たいしょう"},{"japanese":"突然子どもが飛び出してきたのだから、あの事故は不可抗力だった。","romaji":"Totsuzen kodomo ga tobidashite kita no da kara, ano jiko wa fuka kōryoku datta.","german":"Since the child suddenly ran out into the road, that accident was unavoidable.","furigana":"とつぜんこ|と|だ|じこ|ふかこうりょく"}],"notes":"不可抗力 is \"force majeure\": an event that cannot be prevented despite all care (natural disasters, war, strikes). In contracts it appears in 不可抗力条項 \"force majeure clause\" and in phrases like 不可抗力による損害 or 不可抗力の場合は免責とする. In everyday speech too, one says 〜は不可抗力だった \"that could not be helped\" to clear someone of blame. 天災, by contrast, refers to the natural disaster itself. Register: written/legal, slightly elevated in daily life.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"特許使用のロイヤルティーを支払う。","romaji":"Tokkyo shiyō no roiyarutī wo shiharau.","german":"Pay royalties for the use of the patent.","furigana":"とっきょしよう|しはら"},{"japanese":"加盟店は毎月、売上の5%をロイヤルティーとして本部に納める。","romaji":"Kameiten wa maitsuki, uriage no go pāsento o roiyarutī to shite honbu ni osameru.","german":"Franchisees pay 5% of their sales to headquarters every month as royalties.","furigana":"かめいてん|まいつき|うりあげ|ほんぶ|おさ"},{"japanese":"そのキャラクターを商品に使うなら、ロイヤルティーが発生します。","romaji":"Sono kyarakutā o shōhin ni tsukau nara, roiyarutī ga hassei shimasu.","german":"If you use that character on your products, royalties will be incurred.","furigana":"しょうひん|つか|はっせい"}],"notes":"ロイヤルティー is the license fee for using patents, trademarks, characters or a franchise system: ロイヤルティーを支払う/受け取る, ロイヤルティーが発生する \"royalties are incurred\". The spelling varies widely; ロイヤリティー is at least as common. Note: the same katakana form also means \"loyalty\" (顧客ロイヤルティー \"customer loyalty\") - context decides. Japanese equivalents are 使用料 or 特許料; authors' royalties for books are called 印税.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"技術ライセンスの供与により海外展開を図る。","romaji":"Gijutsu raisensu no kyōyo ni yori kaigai tenkai wo hakaru.","german":"Seek overseas expansion by granting technology licenses.","furigana":"ぎじゅつ|きょうよ|かいがいてんかい|はか"},{"japanese":"このソフトはライセンスが切れたので、更新しなければならない。","romaji":"Kono sofuto wa raisensu ga kireta node, kōshin shinakereba naranai.","german":"The license for this software has expired, so I have to renew it.","furigana":"き|こうしん"},{"japanese":"沖縄でダイビングのライセンスを取った。","romaji":"Okinawa de daibingu no raisensu o totta.","german":"I got my diving license in Okinawa.","furigana":"おきなわ|と"}],"notes":"ライセンス is a license in the broad sense: for software, patents, trademarks (ライセンス契約, ライセンス料, ライセンスを取得する/供与する \"to obtain/grant\", ライセンスが切れる \"to expire\"). Colloquially also for private certificates such as a diving license. Official permits, by contrast, are usually called 免許 (運転免許 \"driver's license\") or 許可, and professional qualifications 資格. Register: neutral, especially in business and IT.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"フランチャイズ方式で店舗網を急速に拡大した。","romaji":"Furanchaizu hōshiki de tenpomō wo kyūsoku ni kakudai shita.","german":"The store network was expanded rapidly using the franchise model.","furigana":"ほうしき|てんぽもう|きゅうそく|かくだい"},{"japanese":"会社を辞めて、コンビニのフランチャイズに加盟した。","romaji":"Kaisha o yamete, konbini no furanchaizu ni kamei shita.","german":"He quit his company and joined a convenience store franchise.","furigana":"かいしゃ|や|かめい"},{"japanese":"フランチャイズ契約を結ぶ前に、本部の支援内容をよく確認したほうがいい。","romaji":"Furanchaizu keiyaku o musubu mae ni, honbu no shien naiyō o yoku kakunin shita hō ga ii.","german":"Before signing a franchise contract, it is better to check carefully what support the headquarters provides.","furigana":"けいやく|むす|まえ|ほんぶ|しえんないよう|かくにん"}],"notes":"フランチャイズ refers to the franchise system: the headquarters (本部) provides the brand and know-how, and the franchisee (加盟店, オーナー) pays fees. Typical: フランチャイズ契約, フランチャイズ店, フランチャイズ方式, 〜のフランチャイズに加盟する \"to become a franchisee of ...\"; abbreviated FC in technical texts. In sports, フランチャイズ also refers to a pro team's home location, though in everyday speech 本拠地 is more commonly used for that.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本における独占販売権を取得した。","romaji":"Nihon ni okeru dokusen hanbaiken wo shutoku shita.","german":"We acquired the exclusive sales rights in Japan.","furigana":"にほん|どくせんはんばいけん|しゅとく"},{"japanese":"その会社は、イタリアのある高級ワインの国内独占販売権を持っている。","romaji":"Sono kaisha wa, Itaria no aru kōkyū wain no kokunai dokusen hanbaiken o motte iru.","german":"The company holds the exclusive domestic sales rights for a certain Italian luxury wine.","furigana":"かいしゃ|こうきゅう|こくないどくせんはんばいけん|も"},{"japanese":"売上目標を達成できなければ、独占販売権を失う契約になっている。","romaji":"Uriage mokuhyō o tassei dekinakereba, dokusen hanbaiken o ushinau keiyaku ni natte iru.","german":"The contract says we lose the exclusive sales rights if we fail to achieve our sales targets.","furigana":"うりあげもくひょう|たっせい|どくせんはんばいけん|うしな|けいやく"}],"notes":"独占販売権 consists of 独占 \"monopoly, exclusivity\" and 販売権 \"sales right\": the exclusive right to sell a product in a territory. Typical: 〜の独占販売権を取得する/持つ/与える/失う, often with a territory such as 日本における/国内の. The holder is frequently a 総代理店. The counterpart is a non-exclusive sales right (非独占の販売権). Register: contract and business language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"アジア地域の総代理店として事業を展開する。","romaji":"Ajia chiiki no sō dairiten to shite jigyō wo tenkai suru.","german":"Develop business as the sole agent for the Asian region.","furigana":"ちいき|そうだいりてん|じぎょう|てんかい"},{"japanese":"この時計は、スイスのメーカーの日本総代理店を通じて輸入されている。","romaji":"Kono tokei wa, Suisu no mēkā no Nihon sō dairiten o tsūjite yunyū sarete iru.","german":"These watches are imported through the Japanese sole agent of a Swiss manufacturer.","furigana":"とけい|にほんそうだいりてん|つう|ゆにゅう"},{"japanese":"修理のご依頼は、総代理店のサポート窓口までお願いします。","romaji":"Shūri no go-irai wa, sō dairiten no sapōto madoguchi made onegai shimasu.","german":"For repair requests, please contact the support desk of the sole agent.","furigana":"しゅうり|いらい|そうだいりてん|まどぐち|ねが"}],"notes":"総代理店 consists of 総 \"general, overall\" and 代理店 \"agency, agent\": the company that a (usually foreign) manufacturer appoints as sole general representative for a country or region, often with 独占販売権. Below it stand ordinary 販売代理店 or 特約店. On packaging and websites one often reads 日本総代理店 or 輸入総代理店. Register: business language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"電子商取引の拡大が従来の小売業に影響を及ぼしている。","romaji":"Denshi shōtorihiki no kakudai ga jūrai no kourigyō ni eikyō o oyoboshite iru.","german":"The expansion of e-commerce is affecting conventional retail.","furigana":"でんししょうとりひき|かくだい|じゅうらい|こうりぎょう|えいきょう|およ"},{"japanese":"電子商取引を利用して、海外の客にも商品を売っている。","romaji":"Denshi shōtorihiki o riyō shite, kaigai no kyaku ni mo shōhin o utte iru.","german":"Using e-commerce, we sell our products to customers overseas as well.","furigana":"でんししょうとりひき|りよう|かいがい|きゃく|しょうひん|う"},{"japanese":"電子商取引では、個人情報の保護が大きな課題だ。","romaji":"Denshi shōtorihiki de wa, kojin jōhō no hogo ga ōki na kadai da.","german":"In e-commerce, protecting personal information is a major issue.","furigana":"でんししょうとりひき|こじんじょうほう|ほご|おお|かだい"}],"notes":"電子商取引 is the technical term for \"e-commerce\", i.e. trade over the internet between companies or with end customers. In official and business texts the abbreviation EC or eコマース often appears; in everyday speech one simply says ネット通販 or ネットショッピング. Typical combinations: 電子商取引の市場規模, 電子商取引を利用する, 電子商取引の拡大. 商取引 alone means \"commercial transaction\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"インターネットを通じた越境取引が増加している。","romaji":"Intānetto wo tsūjita ekkyō torihiki ga zōka shite iru.","german":"Cross-border trade over the internet is increasing.","furigana":"つう|えっきょうとりひき|ぞうか"},{"japanese":"越境取引では、関税や配送日数を事前に確認しておく必要がある。","romaji":"Ekkyō torihiki de wa, kanzei ya haisō nissū o jizen ni kakunin shite oku hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"In cross-border transactions, you need to check tariffs and delivery times in advance.","furigana":"えっきょうとりひき|かんぜい|はいそうにっすう|じぜん|かくにん|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"暗号資産の越境取引に対する規制が、各国で強化されている。","romaji":"Angō shisan no ekkyō torihiki ni taisuru kisei ga, kakkoku de kyōka sarete iru.","german":"Regulation of cross-border cryptoasset transactions is being tightened in many countries.","furigana":"あんごうしさん|えっきょうとりひき|たい|きせい|かっこく|きょうか"}],"notes":"越境取引 combines 越境 \"crossing a border\" with 取引 \"deal, transaction\": cross-border transactions, especially in online commerce (越境EC \"cross-border e-commerce\") and in finance (越境取引の規制). On its own, 越境 often means illegally crossing a border, but in compounds it is neutral. More general are 国際取引 or 貿易 \"foreign trade\". Register: press and technical language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"卸売市場の近代化が課題となっている。","romaji":"Oroshiuri shijō no kindaika ga kadai to natte iru.","german":"Modernizing the wholesale market has become an issue.","furigana":"おろしうりしじょう|きんだいか|かだい"},{"japanese":"豊洲の卸売市場では、毎朝マグロの競りが行われる。","romaji":"Toyosu no oroshiuri shijō de wa, maiasa maguro no seri ga okonawareru.","german":"At the Toyosu wholesale market, tuna auctions are held every morning.","furigana":"|おろしうりしじょう|まいあさ|せ|おこな"},{"japanese":"料理人たちは新鮮な野菜を求めて、早朝から卸売市場に足を運ぶ。","romaji":"Ryōrinin-tachi wa shinsen na yasai o motomete, sōchō kara oroshiuri shijō ni ashi o hakobu.","german":"Chefs go to the wholesale market from early morning in search of fresh vegetables.","furigana":"りょうりにん|しんせん|やさい|もと|そうちょう|おろしうりしじょう|あし|はこ"}],"notes":"卸売市場 is the wholesale market where producers, wholesalers (卸売業者) and intermediate wholesalers (仲卸) trade fish, vegetables, fruit or flowers; the 中央卸売市場 in 豊洲 (formerly 築地) is well known. 市場 is read しじょう here, but in everyday speech one often says simply 市場（いちば） or 魚市場. The counterpart of wholesale 卸売 is retail 小売.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"水産物の競りが早朝に行われる。","romaji":"Suisanbutsu no seri ga sōchō ni okonawareru.","german":"The auction of marine products takes place early in the morning.","furigana":"すいさんぶつ|せ|そうちょう|おこな"},{"japanese":"新年の初競りで、マグロ一本に一億円以上の値がついた。","romaji":"Shinnen no hatsuseri de, maguro ippon ni ichioku-en ijō no ne ga tsuita.","german":"At the first auction of the New Year, a single tuna fetched over 100 million yen.","furigana":"しんねん|はつせ|いっぽん|いちおくえんいじょう|ね"},{"japanese":"競り落としたばかりのウニを、仲卸が店に運び込んだ。","romaji":"Seriotoshita bakari no uni o, nakaoroshi ga mise ni hakobikonda.","german":"The intermediate wholesaler carried the sea urchin he had just won at auction into his shop.","furigana":"せ|お|なかおろし|みせ|はこ|こ"}],"notes":"競り is the auction customary at fish and vegetable markets; also written せり or セリ. Typical: 競りにかける \"to auction\", 競りが行われる, 競り落とす \"to win at auction\" and 初競り, the first auction of the year, often with spectacular tuna prices. For art, online or forced auctions, one more often says オークション or 競売（きょうばい）. The underlying verb 競る means \"to compete, to outbid each other\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"中間業者を排除して流通コストを削減する。","romaji":"Chūkan gyōsha wo haijo shite ryūtsū kosuto wo sakugen suru.","german":"Cut out the middlemen and reduce distribution costs.","furigana":"ちゅうかんぎょうしゃ|はいじょ|りゅうつう|さくげん"},{"japanese":"中間業者を通さないので、農家から直接安く買える。","romaji":"Chūkan gyōsha o tōsanai node, nōka kara chokusetsu yasuku kaeru.","german":"Since no middleman is involved, you can buy directly from the farmer cheaply.","furigana":"ちゅうかんぎょうしゃ|とお|のうか|ちょくせつやす|か"},{"japanese":"中間業者が何社も入っているせいで、現場の作業員の賃金が低い。","romaji":"Chūkan gyōsha ga nansha mo haitte iru sei de, genba no sagyōin no chingin ga hikui.","german":"Because several intermediary companies are involved, the wages of workers on site are low.","furigana":"ちゅうかんぎょうしゃ|なんしゃ|はい|げんば|さぎょういん|ちんぎん|ひく"}],"notes":"中間業者 are the firms between manufacturer and end customer, i.e. middlemen. The word mostly appears in connection with cost cutting: 中間業者を排除する/通さない/省く, colloquially 中抜き \"cutting out the middleman\", and 中間マージン \"middleman margin\". 仲介業者 is more of a broker (e.g. in real estate), and ブローカー often sounds derogatory. Register: neutral to written.","levelNote":"Compound: 中間 (N2) + 業者 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"メーカーの直販サイトで購入する方が安い。","romaji":"Mēkā no chokuhan saito de kōnyū suru hō ga yasui.","german":"It is cheaper to buy from the manufacturer's direct sales site.","furigana":"ちょくはん|こうにゅう|ほう|やす"},{"japanese":"この農園では、収穫したりんごを消費者に直販している。","romaji":"Kono nōen de wa, shūkaku shita ringo o shōhisha ni chokuhan shite iru.","german":"This farm sells the apples it harvests directly to consumers.","furigana":"のうえん|しゅうかく|しょうひしゃ|ちょくはん"},{"japanese":"直販に切り替えてから、利益率が大きく改善した。","romaji":"Chokuhan ni kirikaete kara, riekiritsu ga ōkiku kaizen shita.","german":"Since switching to direct sales, the profit margin has improved greatly.","furigana":"ちょくはん|き|か|りえきりつ|おお|かいぜん"}],"notes":"直販 is the short form of 直接販売: manufacturers or producers sell directly to customers without wholesale and retail. Typical: 直販サイト \"manufacturer's online shop\", 直販店, 産地直販, also as a する-verb: 野菜を直販する. Very similar is 直売, which you know especially from farm stands (直売所); 通販, by contrast, refers to the mail-order channel, not the seller.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"通信販売の売上が年々増加している。","romaji":"Tsūshin hanbai no uriage ga nennen zōka shite iru.","german":"Mail-order sales are increasing year by year.","furigana":"つうしんはんばい|うりあげ|ねんねんぞうか"},{"japanese":"祖母はテレビの通信販売で健康器具を買った。","romaji":"Sobo wa terebi no tsūshin hanbai de kenkō kigu o katta.","german":"My grandmother bought an exercise machine through TV shopping.","furigana":"そぼ|つうしんはんばい|けんこうきぐ|か"},{"japanese":"通信販売にはクーリング・オフ制度が適用されないので、注意が必要だ。","romaji":"Tsūshin hanbai ni wa kūringu ofu seido ga tekiyō sarenai node, chūi ga hitsuyō da.","german":"Be careful: the cooling-off system does not apply to mail-order sales.","furigana":"つうしんはんばい|せいど|てきよう|ちゅうい|ひつよう"}],"notes":"通信販売 is mail order: ordering by catalog, telephone, television or internet, with delivery to your home. In daily life one almost always uses the short form 通販 (ネット通販, テレビ通販, 通販で買う); the full form appears in legal texts and formal notices. Important: in Japan no statutory right of withdrawal (クーリング・オフ) applies to 通信販売 - unlike with 訪問販売; the seller's return policy is what counts.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 27 (source: N4; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"訪問販売における消費者保護が強化された。","romaji":"Hōmon hanbai ni okeru shōhisha hogo ga kyōka sareta.","german":"Consumer protection in door-to-door sales has been strengthened.","furigana":"ほうもんはんばい|しょうひしゃほご|きょうか"},{"japanese":"訪問販売で高い浄水器を買わされた。","romaji":"Hōmon hanbai de takai jōsuiki o kawasareta.","german":"I was made to buy an expensive water purifier from a door-to-door salesperson.","furigana":"ほうもんはんばい|たか|じょうすいき|か"},{"japanese":"玄関に訪問販売お断りのステッカーを貼った。","romaji":"Genkan ni hōmon hanbai okotowari no sutekkā o hatta.","german":"I put a sticker on the front door saying no door-to-door sales.","furigana":"げんかん|ほうもんはんばい|ことわ|は"}],"notes":"訪問販売 is selling at the customer's door or in the customer's home, a legal term in the Act on Specified Commercial Transactions (特定商取引法), for which a cooling-off right (クーリング・オフ) applies. Typical: 訪問販売員, 悪質な訪問販売, 訪問販売を断る, and on door signs 訪問販売お断り. Do not confuse it with 直販 \"direct sales\" by the manufacturer or 通信販売 \"mail-order sales\".","levelNote":"Compound: 訪問 (N3) + 販売 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"委託販売方式で在庫リスクを軽減する。","romaji":"Itaku hanbai hōshiki de zaiko risuku wo keigen suru.","german":"Reduce inventory risk through the consignment sales model.","furigana":"いたくはんばいほうしき|ざいこ|けいげん"},{"japanese":"手作りのアクセサリーを、近所の雑貨店で委託販売してもらっている。","romaji":"Tezukuri no akusesarī o, kinjo no zakkaten de itaku hanbai shite moratte iru.","german":"A neighborhood general store sells my handmade accessories on consignment.","furigana":"てづく|きんじょ|ざっかてん|いたくはんばい"},{"japanese":"委託販売なので、売れ残った商品は返品できる。","romaji":"Itaku hanbai na node, urenokotta shōhin wa henpin dekiru.","german":"Since it is consignment, unsold items can be returned.","furigana":"いたくはんばい|う|のこ|しょうひん|へんぴん"}],"notes":"委託販売 is selling on consignment: the owner (委託者) leaves goods with a dealer who sells them for a commission; unsold goods are returned, and the inventory risk stays with the owner. Typical: 委託販売で売る, 〜を委託販売してもらう, 委託販売契約, 委託販売手数料. Common in bookselling, at second-hand shops and for handmade goods. The opposite term is 買い取り \"purchase on one's own account\".","levelNote":"Compound: 委託 (N1) + 販売 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"再販売価格維持は原則として独占禁止法で禁止されている。","romaji":"Saihanbai kakaku iji wa gensoku to shite dokusen kinshihō de kinshi sarete iru.","german":"Resale price maintenance is in principle prohibited under the Antimonopoly Act.","furigana":"さいはんばいかかくいじ|げんそく|どくせんきんしほう|きんし"},{"japanese":"書籍や新聞は例外として、再販売価格維持が認められている。","romaji":"Shoseki ya shinbun wa reigai to shite, saihanbai kakaku iji ga mitomerarete iru.","german":"Books and newspapers are an exception, and resale price maintenance is permitted.","furigana":"しょせき|しんぶん|れいがい|さいはんばいかかくいじ|みと"},{"japanese":"メーカーが小売店に値引きを禁じれば、再販売価格維持に当たるおそれがある。","romaji":"Mēkā ga kouriten ni nebiki o kinjireba, saihanbai kakaku iji ni ataru osore ga aru.","german":"If a manufacturer forbids retailers to discount, it may amount to resale price maintenance.","furigana":"こうりてん|ねび|きん|さいはんばいかかくいじ|あ"}],"notes":"再販売価格維持 is a term from competition law: the manufacturer dictates the resale price to the dealer (vertical price fixing). In Japan this is in principle prohibited under the 独占禁止法; exceptions apply to books, newspapers and sound recordings (再販制度, 再販 for short). Common phrases: 再販売価格維持行為, 再販売価格の拘束. Register: very formal, legal.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"販売促進キャンペーンにより売上が二割増加した。","romaji":"Hanbai sokushin kyanpēn ni yori uriage ga niwari zōka shita.","german":"The sales promotion campaign increased sales by twenty percent.","furigana":"はんばいそくしん|うりあげ|にわりぞうか"},{"japanese":"販売促進のために、店頭で試食会を開いた。","romaji":"Hanbai sokushin no tame ni, tentō de shishokukai o hiraita.","german":"To promote sales, we held a tasting event at the store.","furigana":"はんばいそくしん|てんとう|ししょくかい|ひら"},{"japanese":"来期は販売促進費を削って、広告に予算を回すことになった。","romaji":"Raiki wa hanbai sokushinhi o kezutte, kōkoku ni yosan o mawasu koto ni natta.","german":"Next term, the sales promotion budget will be cut and the money diverted to advertising.","furigana":"らいき|はんばいそくしんひ|けず|こうこく|よさん|まわ"}],"notes":"販売促進 is sales promotion in marketing: promotions, coupons, samples, tastings, displays. In everyday business one almost always hears the short form 販促 (販促品 \"promotional items\", 販促キャンペーン); the full form appears more in texts, e.g. 販売促進費, 販売促進活動, 〜の販売促進を図る. In Japanese, 販促 is usually distinguished from 広告 \"advertising in media\"; as a loanword プロモーション is also common.","levelNote":"Compound: 販売 (N3) + 促進 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"海外に新たな販路を開拓する。","romaji":"Kaigai ni arata na hanro wo kaitaku suru.","german":"Develop new sales channels overseas.","furigana":"かいがい|あら|はんろ|かいたく"},{"japanese":"ネット通販で販路を広げた結果、全国から注文が来るようになった。","romaji":"Netto tsūhan de hanro o hirogeta kekka, zenkoku kara chūmon ga kuru yō ni natta.","german":"As a result of expanding our sales channels through online shopping, orders now come in from all over the country.","furigana":"つうはん|はんろ|ひろ|けっか|ぜんこく|ちゅうもん|く"},{"japanese":"大口の取引先を失って、販路の確保が急務になった。","romaji":"Ōguchi no torihikisaki o ushinatte, hanro no kakuho ga kyūmu ni natta.","german":"After losing a major customer, securing sales channels became urgent.","furigana":"おおぐち|とりひきさき|うしな|はんろ|かくほ|きゅうむ"}],"notes":"販路 is the sales channel or sales market through which a company sells its goods. Fixed combinations: 販路を開拓する \"to open up\", 販路を拡大する/広げる, 販路を確保する, 新たな販路, 海外販路. 流通経路, by contrast, means the whole distribution chain from manufacturer to customer, and 市場 the market as a whole. Register: business language, frequent in the news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大型店の出店により周辺の商圏が変化した。","romaji":"Ōgataten no shutten ni yori shūhen no shōken ga henka shita.","german":"The opening of a large store changed the surrounding trade area.","furigana":"おおがたてん|しゅってん|しゅうへん|しょうけん|へんか"},{"japanese":"コンビニの商圏は、一般に半径500メートル程度と言われる。","romaji":"Konbini no shōken wa, ippan ni hankei gohyaku mētoru teido to iwareru.","german":"The trade area of a convenience store is generally said to be about a 500-meter radius.","furigana":"しょうけん|いっぱん|はんけい|ていど|い"},{"japanese":"出店前に、商圏内の人口や競合店を調査した。","romaji":"Shutten mae ni, shōkennai no jinkō ya kyōgōten o chōsa shita.","german":"Before opening, we surveyed the population and competing stores in the trade area.","furigana":"しゅってんまえ|しょうけんない|じんこう|きょうごうてん|ちょうさ"}],"notes":"商圏 is the catchment area of a shop or town, i.e. the area customers come from. Typical: 商圏が広い/狭い, 商圏人口, 商圏を分析する/調査する, 〜を商圏とする, 半径〜キロの商圏. Do not confuse it with 商店街 \"shopping street\" or the identically read 商権 \"commercial rights\". Register: technical language from retail and site planning.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"取引条件について相互に合意した。","romaji":"Torihiki jōken ni tsuite sōgo ni gōi shita.","german":"Both sides agreed on the terms of the transaction.","furigana":"とりひきじょうけん|そうご|ごうい"},{"japanese":"新しい仕入れ先から、より有利な取引条件が提示された。","romaji":"Atarashii shiiresaki kara, yori yūri na torihiki jōken ga teiji sareta.","german":"A new supplier offered us more favorable terms.","furigana":"あたら|しい|さき|ゆうり|とりひきじょうけん|ていじ"},{"japanese":"下請け企業に一方的に不利な取引条件を押し付けることは禁止されている。","romaji":"Shitauke kigyō ni ippōteki ni furi na torihiki jōken o oshitsukeru koto wa kinshi sarete iru.","german":"It is prohibited to impose one-sidedly unfavorable terms on subcontractors.","furigana":"したう|きぎょう|いっぽうてき|ふり|とりひきじょうけん|お|つ|きんし"}],"notes":"取引条件 are the conditions of a specific deal: price, payment terms (支払条件), delivery date (納期), quantities. Typical: 取引条件を提示する/交渉する/見直す/変更する, 有利な/不利な取引条件. The \"general terms and conditions\" as standard text, by contrast, are called 約款 or 利用規約; 契約条件 stresses the contract clauses. Register: business language.","levelNote":"Compound: 取引 (N1) + 条件 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二国間の取引高が過去最高を更新した。","romaji":"Nikokukan no torihikidaka ga kako saikō wo kōshin shita.","german":"The bilateral trading volume has set a new record.","furigana":"にこくかん|とりひきだか|かこさいこう|こうしん"},{"japanese":"円安の影響で、今月は為替の取引高が急増した。","romaji":"En'yasu no eikyō de, kongetsu wa kawase no torihikidaka ga kyūzō shita.","german":"Due to the weak yen, currency trading volume surged this month.","furigana":"えんやす|えいきょう|こんげつ|かわせ|とりひきだか|きゅうぞう"},{"japanese":"大手スーパーとの取引高は、当社の売上の三割を占める。","romaji":"Ōte sūpā to no torihikidaka wa, tōsha no uriage no sanwari o shimeru.","german":"Business volume with the major supermarket chain accounts for thirty percent of our sales.","furigana":"おおて|とりひきだか|とうしゃ|うりあげ|さんわり|し"}],"notes":"取引高 (with voiced だか) is the trading or business volume between partners, countries or in a market: 取引高が増える/減る, 取引高が過去最高を更新する, 〜との取引高. Similar is 取扱高 \"handled volume\". For the trading volume of individual stocks, one more often says 出来高 (number of shares) or 売買代金 (value). Register: business and press language.","levelNote":"Compound: 取引 (N1) + 高 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"リベートの供与が不正取引の温床になる場合がある。","romaji":"Ribēto no kyōyo ga fusei torihiki no onshō ni naru baai ga aru.","german":"The granting of rebates can become a breeding ground for improper transactions.","furigana":"きょうよ|ふせいとりひき|おんしょう|ばあい"},{"japanese":"年間の販売目標を達成した店には、メーカーからリベートが支払われる。","romaji":"Nenkan no hanbai mokuhyō o tassei shita mise ni wa, mēkā kara ribēto ga shiharawareru.","german":"Stores that achieve their annual sales target are paid a rebate by the manufacturer.","furigana":"ねんかん|はんばいもくひょう|たっせい|みせ|しはら"},{"japanese":"担当者が業者からリベートを受け取っていたことが発覚した。","romaji":"Tantōsha ga gyōsha kara ribēto o uketotte ita koto ga hakkaku shita.","german":"It came to light that the person in charge had been receiving kickbacks from a contractor.","furigana":"たんとうしゃ|ぎょうしゃ|う|と|はっかく"}],"notes":"リベート has two sides: legitimate is the after-the-fact rebate a manufacturer pays to dealers depending on sales volume (formally 販売奨励金); in scandal reports, however, it means \"bribe, kickback\" (リベートを受け取る/渡す). Unlike 値引き or 割引, it is not a price reduction at purchase but a later payment. Register: business language, negative in the second sense.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"原材料費の上昇を販売価格に転嫁する。","romaji":"Genzairyōhi no jōshō wo hanbai kakaku ni tenka suru.","german":"Pass the rise in raw material costs on to the selling price.","furigana":"げんざいりょうひ|じょうしょう|はんばいかかく|てんか"},{"japanese":"当店の販売価格はすべて税込みです。","romaji":"Tōten no hanbai kakaku wa subete zeikomi desu.","german":"All selling prices in our store include tax.","furigana":"とうてん|はんばいかかく|ぜいこ"},{"japanese":"円安が続けば、輸入品の販売価格を見直さざるを得ない。","romaji":"En'yasu ga tsuzukeba, yunyūhin no hanbai kakaku o minaosazaru o enai.","german":"If the yen stays weak, we will have no choice but to review the selling prices of imported goods.","furigana":"えんやす|つづ|ゆにゅうひん|はんばいかかく|みなお|え"}],"notes":"販売価格 is the price at which a product is sold. Distinguish 定価 \"fixed list price\", 希望小売価格 \"suggested retail price\", 仕入れ価格 \"purchase price\" and the colloquial 売値. Typical: 販売価格を設定する/引き上げる/改定する/見直す, 〜を販売価格に転嫁する \"to pass on to the selling price\". Register: business language, also on price tags and notices.","levelNote":"Compound: 販売 (N3) + 価格 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"メーカーと卸の間の仕切り価格を見直す。","romaji":"Mēkā to oroshi no aida no shikiri kakaku wo minaosu.","german":"Review the wholesale price between manufacturers and wholesalers.","furigana":"おろし|あいだ|しき|かかく|みなお"},{"japanese":"仕切り価格が上がったのに、店頭価格は据え置いたままだ。","romaji":"Shikiri kakaku ga agatta noni, tentō kakaku wa sueoita mama da.","german":"Although the wholesale price has gone up, the in-store price has been left unchanged.","furigana":"しき|かかく|あ|てんとうかかく|す|お"},{"japanese":"大量に仕入れる代わりに、仕切り価格を下げてもらった。","romaji":"Tairyō ni shiireru kawari ni, shikiri kakaku o sagete moratta.","german":"In exchange for buying in bulk, we got them to lower the wholesale price.","furigana":"たいりょう|しい|か|しき|かかく|さ"}],"notes":"仕切り価格 (also 仕切価格) is an industry term for the price at which the manufacturer supplies the wholesale trade, or the wholesaler supplies the retailer - the transfer price to the trade. 仕切り comes from 仕切る \"to settle accounts\". The difference between 仕切り価格 and the shop price is the dealer's margin (マージン). Closer to everyday speech is 卸値 \"wholesale price\"; an intra-group \"transfer price\", on the other hand, is called 移転価格.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"希望小売価格を据え置く方針を発表した。","romaji":"Kibō kouri kakaku o sueoku hōshin o happyō shita.","german":"They announced a policy of keeping the suggested retail price unchanged.","furigana":"きぼうこうりかかく|す|お|ほうしん|はっぴょう"},{"japanese":"このカメラは希望小売価格より2万円も安く買えた。","romaji":"Kono kamera wa kibō kouri kakaku yori niman'en mo yasuku kaeta.","german":"I was able to buy this camera for as much as 20,000 yen below the suggested retail price.","furigana":"きぼうこうりかかく|まんえん|やす|か"},{"japanese":"希望小売価格とは、メーカーが小売店に示す目安の値段のことだ。","romaji":"Kibō kouri kakaku to wa, mēkā ga kouriten ni shimesu meyasu no nedan no koto da.","german":"The suggested retail price is the guideline price a manufacturer gives to retailers.","furigana":"きぼうこうりかかく|こうりてん|しめ|めやす|ねだん"}],"notes":"希望小売価格 is the manufacturer's \"suggested retail price\"; in full it is often メーカー希望小売価格. Retailers like to advertise discounts from it: 希望小売価格の3割引き. Typical verbs: 希望小売価格を設定する/据え置く/改定する. When a manufacturer gives up making a suggestion, it is called オープン価格. 小売 (こうり) means \"retail\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"メーカー希望小売価格を廃止し、オープン価格に移行した。","romaji":"Mēkā kibō kouri kakaku o haishi shi, ōpun kakaku ni ikō shita.","german":"The manufacturer's suggested retail price was abolished, and they moved to open pricing.","furigana":"きぼうこうりかかく|はいし|かかく|いこう"},{"japanese":"この商品はオープン価格なので、店によって値段が違う。","romaji":"Kono shōhin wa ōpun kakaku na node, mise ni yotte nedan ga chigau.","german":"This product has an open price, so the price differs from store to store.","furigana":"しょうひん|かかく|みせ|ねだん|ちが"},{"japanese":"家電製品の多くはオープン価格で販売されている。","romaji":"Kaden seihin no ōku wa ōpun kakaku de hanbai sarete iru.","german":"Many home appliances are sold at open prices.","furigana":"かでんせいひん|おお|かかく|はんばい"}],"notes":"オープン価格 means that the manufacturer states no recommended selling price and each retailer sets the price freely. This is especially common for electronics and home appliances in Japan; in catalogs it then simply says 価格：オープン. The counterpart is (メーカー)希望小売価格. Typical: オープン価格に移行する, オープン価格で販売する. It does not mean \"an open, negotiable price\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"売掛金の回収期間を短縮して資金繰りを改善する。","romaji":"Urikakekin no kaishū kikan wo tanshuku shite shikinguri wo kaizen suru.","german":"Improve cash flow by shortening the collection period for accounts receivable.","furigana":"うりかけきん|かいしゅうきかん|たんしゅく|しきんぐ|かいぜん"},{"japanese":"取引先が倒産し、売掛金が回収できなくなった。","romaji":"Torihikisaki ga tōsan shi, urikakekin ga kaishū dekinaku natta.","german":"A client went bankrupt, and we can no longer collect the accounts receivable.","furigana":"とりひきさき|とうさん|うりかけきん|かいしゅう"},{"japanese":"月末に売掛金の残高を確認して、請求漏れがないかチェックする。","romaji":"Getsumatsu ni urikakekin no zandaka o kakunin shite, seikyū more ga nai ka chekku suru.","german":"At the end of the month, I check the accounts receivable balance to make sure no invoice has been missed.","furigana":"げつまつ|うりかけきん|ざんだか|かくにん|せいきゅうも"}],"notes":"売掛金 is an accounting term: money that customers still owe after a sale on credit (掛け売り) - accounts receivable. The counterpart is 買掛金 \"accounts payable\". Typical: 売掛金を回収する \"to collect\", 売掛金の回収期間, 未回収の売掛金, 売掛金が焦げ付く \"to become uncollectible\". 掛け alone means \"on credit\" (掛けで買う). Register: accounting.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"買掛金の支払期限を延長して資金繰りに余裕を持たせる。","romaji":"Kaikakekin no shiharai kigen wo enchō shite shikinguri ni yoyū wo motaseru.","german":"Extend the payment term of accounts payable to give cash flow some breathing room.","furigana":"かいかけきん|しはらいきげん|えんちょう|しきんぐ|よゆう|も"},{"japanese":"うちでは、月末にまとめて買掛金を支払っている。","romaji":"Uchi de wa, getsumatsu ni matomete kaikakekin o shiharatte iru.","german":"We pay our accounts payable all together at the end of the month.","furigana":"げつまつ|かいかけきん|しはら"},{"japanese":"仕入れが増えたため、貸借対照表の買掛金も膨らんだ。","romaji":"Shiire ga fueta tame, taishaku taishōhyō no kaikakekin mo fukuranda.","german":"As purchasing increased, the accounts payable on the balance sheet also swelled.","furigana":"しい|ふ|たいしゃくたいしょうひょう|かいかけきん|ふく"}],"notes":"買掛金 is the counterpart of 売掛金: amounts a company still owes its suppliers after a purchase on credit (掛けで仕入れる) - accounts payable. Typical: 買掛金を支払う/決済する, 買掛金の支払期限, 買掛金が膨らむ. Other outstanding payments, for example for equipment, are called 未払金, and bank loans 借入金. Register: accounting.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"在庫の回転率を高めて効率的な経営を行う。","romaji":"Zaiko no kaitenritsu wo takamete kōritsuteki na keiei wo okonau.","german":"Raise the inventory turnover rate and run the business efficiently.","furigana":"ざいこ|かいてんりつ|たか|こうりつてき|けいえい|おこな"},{"japanese":"この店はランチの回転率が高いので、安くても利益が出る。","romaji":"Kono mise wa ranchi no kaitenritsu ga takai node, yasukute mo rieki ga deru.","german":"This shop has a high turnover at lunch, so it makes a profit even at low prices.","furigana":"みせ|かいてんりつ|たか|やす|りえき|で"},{"japanese":"駐車場の回転率を上げるため、一日の最大料金を廃止した。","romaji":"Chūshajō no kaitenritsu o ageru tame, ichinichi no saidai ryōkin o haishi shita.","german":"To raise the turnover of the parking lot, the daily maximum fee was abolished.","furigana":"ちゅうしゃじょう|かいてんりつ|あ|いちにち|さいだいりょうきん|はいし"}],"notes":"回転率 indicates how often something \"turns over\" in a period: in business administration 在庫回転率 \"inventory turnover\" and 資本回転率, and in restaurants and parking lots how often seats or spaces are refilled (客席回転率). Typical: 回転率が高い/低い, 回転率を上げる/高める. Colloquially 回転 is often enough: この店は回転が速い. Register: technical, but also common in everyday speech.","levelNote":"Compound: 回転 (N3) + 率 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"死に筋商品を早期に処分して在庫コストを削減する。","romaji":"Shinisuji shōhin wo sōki ni shobun shite zaiko kosuto wo sakugen suru.","german":"Dispose of slow-selling items early to reduce inventory costs.","furigana":"し|すじしょうひん|そうき|しょぶん|ざいこ|さくげん"},{"japanese":"POSデータで死に筋を洗い出し、棚から外した。","romaji":"POS dēta de shinisuji o araidashi, tana kara hazushita.","german":"We identified the slow sellers from POS data and removed them from the shelves.","furigana":"し|すじ|あら|だ|たな|はず"},{"japanese":"売れ筋だった商品も、流行が過ぎればすぐ死に筋になる。","romaji":"Uresuji datta shōhin mo, ryūkō ga sugireba sugu shinisuji ni naru.","german":"Even a product that was a hot seller quickly becomes a slow seller once the fad is over.","furigana":"う|すじ|しょうひん|りゅうこう|す|し|すじ"}],"notes":"死に筋 is retail jargon for items that hardly sell - literally something like \"dead line\", where 筋 means \"type, line\". The opposite is 売れ筋. Typical: 死に筋商品, 死に筋をカットする/外す/洗い出す, often in connection with checkout data. More neutral are 不人気商品 or 売れ行きの悪い商品; 売れ残り, by contrast, are concrete leftover items. Register: retail jargon, sounds somewhat harsh.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"売れ筋商品の品揃えを充実させる。","romaji":"Uresuji shōhin no shinazoroe wo jūjitsu saseru.","german":"Expand the lineup of best-selling products.","furigana":"う|すじしょうひん|しなぞろ|じゅうじつ"},{"japanese":"店員さんに今の売れ筋を聞いてから選んだ。","romaji":"Ten'in-san ni ima no uresuji o kiite kara eranda.","german":"I asked the clerk what is selling well right now before choosing.","furigana":"てんいん|いま|う|すじ|き|えら"},{"japanese":"その炊飯器は、通販サイトの売れ筋ランキングで一位になった。","romaji":"Sono suihanki wa, tsūhan saito no uresuji rankingu de ichii ni natta.","german":"That rice cooker took first place in the online shop's best-seller ranking.","furigana":"すいはんき|つうはん|う|すじ|いちい"}],"notes":"売れ筋 are the items that are currently selling well (売れる + 筋 \"line, type\"). Typical: 売れ筋商品, 売れ筋ランキング, and in a shop 今の売れ筋はどれですか. The opposite is 死に筋. Similar are 人気商品 and ヒット商品; ベストセラー is used mainly for books. Register: neutral, very common in shops and online stores.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"品揃えの豊富さが集客の鍵だ。","romaji":"Shinazoroe no hōfusa ga shūkyaku no kagi da.","german":"A wide product selection is the key to attracting customers.","furigana":"しなぞろ|ほうふ|しゅうきゃく|かぎ"},{"japanese":"この本屋は専門書の品揃えがいいので、よく利用している。","romaji":"Kono hon'ya wa senmonsho no shinazoroe ga ii node, yoku riyō shite iru.","german":"This bookstore has a good selection of specialist books, so I often use it.","furigana":"ほんや|せんもんしょ|しなぞろ|りよう"},{"japanese":"駅前のスーパーは品揃えが少なくて、欲しい物がなかなか見つからない。","romaji":"Ekimae no sūpā wa shinazoroe ga sukunakute, hoshii mono ga nakanaka mitsukaranai.","german":"The supermarket in front of the station has a small selection, and I can hardly find what I want.","furigana":"えきまえ|しなぞろ|すく|ほ|もの|み"}],"notes":"品揃え is the assortment or selection of goods in a shop; also written 品ぞろえ. Typical: 品揃えが豊富だ/いい/少ない, 品揃えを充実させる/増やす, 〜の品揃えが自慢だ. It does not fit for a range of options one chooses between in general - for that one says 選択肢. Register: neutral, very common in daily life.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"棚割りの最適化が売上向上に貢献する。","romaji":"Tanawari no saitekika ga uriage kōjō ni kōken suru.","german":"Optimizing shelf allocation contributes to higher sales.","furigana":"たなわ|さいてきか|うりあげこうじょう|こうけん"},{"japanese":"春の新商品に合わせて、飲料売り場の棚割りを変更した。","romaji":"Haru no shinshōhin ni awasete, inryō uriba no tanawari o henkō shita.","german":"In line with the spring new products, we changed the shelf allocation in the beverage section.","furigana":"はる|しんしょうひん|あ|いんりょうう|ば|たなわ|へんこう"},{"japanese":"メーカーの営業は、自社商品を目線の高さに置いてもらえるよう、棚割りの交渉をする。","romaji":"Mēkā no eigyō wa, jisha shōhin o mesen no takasa ni oite moraeru yō, tanawari no kōshō o suru.","german":"Manufacturers' sales reps negotiate over shelf allocation so that their products are placed at eye level.","furigana":"えいぎょう|じしゃしょうひん|めせん|たか|お|たなわ|こうしょう"}],"notes":"棚割り (also 棚割) is retail jargon for shelf planning: which products stand on which shelf, in what quantity and at what height - places at eye level count as the ゴールデンゾーン \"golden zone\". Typical: 棚割りを変更する/見直す/作成する, 棚割り表 \"planogram\". The display itself is called 陳列. Manufacturers negotiate with retailers for good spots in the 棚割り.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"消費者物価指数が前年比で上昇した。","romaji":"Shōhisha bukka shisū ga zennenhi de jōshō shita.","german":"The consumer price index rose compared with the previous year.","furigana":"しょうひしゃぶっかしすう|ぜんねんひ|じょうしょう"},{"japanese":"消費者物価指数は毎月、総務省から発表される。","romaji":"Shōhisha bukka shisū wa maitsuki, Sōmushō kara happyō sareru.","german":"The consumer price index is released every month by the Ministry of Internal Affairs and Communications.","furigana":"しょうひしゃぶっかしすう|まいつき|そうむしょう|はっぴょう"},{"japanese":"年金の支給額は、消費者物価指数などをもとに毎年改定される。","romaji":"Nenkin no shikyūgaku wa, shōhisha bukka shisū nado o moto ni maitoshi kaitei sareru.","german":"Pension payments are revised every year based on the consumer price index and other factors.","furigana":"ねんきん|しきゅうがく|しょうひしゃぶっかしすう|まいとしかいてい"}],"notes":"消費者物価指数 is the consumer price index (物価 \"price level\", 指数 \"index\"), often simply CPI in the news. In Japan it is published monthly by the 総務省; the core measure of inflation is the index 生鮮食品を除く総合 (all items excluding fresh food). Typical: 消費者物価指数が上昇する/下落する, 前年同月比〜％上昇. For prices between companies there is the 企業物価指数 (formerly 卸売物価指数). Register: business and press language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"卸売物価指数の動向が消費者物価に先行する。","romaji":"Oroshiuri bukka shisū no dōkō ga shōhisha bukka ni senkō suru.","german":"Trends in the wholesale price index lead consumer prices.","furigana":"おろしうりぶっかしすう|どうこう|しょうひしゃぶっか|せんこう"},{"japanese":"日本では、卸売物価指数は2002年に企業物価指数へと名称が変わった。","romaji":"Nihon de wa, oroshiuri bukka shisū wa nisen-ni-nen ni kigyō bukka shisū e to meishō ga kawatta.","german":"In Japan, the wholesale price index was renamed the corporate goods price index in 2002.","furigana":"にほん|おろしうりぶっかしすう|ねん|きぎょうぶっかしすう|めいしょう|か"},{"japanese":"原油高を受けて、卸売物価指数が大きく上昇した。","romaji":"Gen'yudaka o ukete, oroshiuri bukka shisū ga ōkiku jōshō shita.","german":"Following high crude oil prices, the wholesale price index rose sharply.","furigana":"げんゆだか|う|おろしうりぶっかしすう|おお|じょうしょう"}],"notes":"卸売物価指数 is the wholesale price index, which measures prices in trade between companies; it is regarded as a leading indicator for consumer prices (消費者物価指数). In Japan the Bank of Japan renamed it 企業物価指数 in 2002, so the old name now appears mainly in older texts or for other countries. Typical: 卸売物価指数が上昇する, 〜の動向. Register: economic technical language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本独自の商慣行が外国企業の参入を阻んでいる。","romaji":"Nihon dokuji no shōkankō ga gaikoku kigyō no sannyū wo habande iru.","german":"Japan's own distinctive business practices are keeping foreign companies from entering.","furigana":"にほんどくじ|しょうかんこう|がいこくきぎょう|さんにゅう|はば"},{"japanese":"返品自由という業界の商慣行を見直す動きが出てきた。","romaji":"Henpin jiyū to iu gyōkai no shōkankō o minaosu ugoki ga dete kita.","german":"Moves have emerged to review the industry practice of free returns.","furigana":"へんぴんじゆう|ぎょうかい|しょうかんこう|みなお|うご|で"},{"japanese":"公正取引委員会は、下請けいじめにつながる商慣行の是正を求めた。","romaji":"Kōsei Torihiki Iinkai wa, shitauke ijime ni tsunagaru shōkankō no zesei o motometa.","german":"The Fair Trade Commission called for correction of business practices that lead to bullying of subcontractors.","furigana":"こうせいとりひきいいんかい|したう|しょうかんこう|ぜせい|もと"}],"notes":"商慣行 are the business practices actually followed in an industry or country. The word often appears critically: 不透明な商慣行, 商慣行を見直す/改める/是正する, 日本独自の商慣行. The nearly synonymous 商慣習 stresses the traditional custom and is also a legal term (商慣習法 \"commercial customary law\"). Register: business and press language.","levelNote":"Compound: 商 (N2) + 慣行 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"商慣習の違いが国際取引の障壁になることがある。","romaji":"Shōkanshū no chigai ga kokusai torihiki no shōheki ni naru koto ga aru.","german":"Differences in commercial customs can become a barrier to international trade.","furigana":"しょうかんしゅう|ちが|こくさいとりひき|しょうへき"},{"japanese":"日本には、お中元やお歳暮を取引先に贈る商慣習がある。","romaji":"Nihon ni wa, ochūgen ya oseibo o torihikisaki ni okuru shōkanshū ga aru.","german":"In Japan, there is a commercial custom of sending gifts to business partners at midsummer and year-end.","furigana":"にほん|ちゅうげん|せいぼ|とりひきさき|おく|しょうかんしゅう"},{"japanese":"契約書に定めがない場合は、商慣習に従って判断される。","romaji":"Keiyakusho ni sadame ga nai baai wa, shōkanshū ni shitagatte handan sareru.","german":"Where the contract has no provision, judgment is made in accordance with commercial custom.","furigana":"けいやくしょ|さだ|ばあい|しょうかんしゅう|したが|はんだん"}],"notes":"商慣習 is commercial custom, i.e. what customarily applies in trade even without a written rule. Legally it matters because contracts are interpreted according to 商慣習 where there are gaps (商慣習法 \"commercial customary law\"). Typical: 商慣習の違い, 商慣習に従う, 〜という商慣習がある. The nearly synonymous 商慣行 stresses the lived, often criticized practice. Register: written, business and law.","levelNote":"Compound: 商 (N2) + 慣習 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"流通経路の短縮によりコストを削減する。","romaji":"Ryūtsū keiro no tanshuku ni yori kosuto wo sakugen suru.","german":"Reduce costs by shortening the distribution channel.","furigana":"りゅうつうけいろ|たんしゅく|さくげん"},{"japanese":"警察は偽ブランド品の流通経路を調べている。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa nise burando hin no ryūtsū keiro o shirabete iru.","german":"The police are investigating the distribution channels of counterfeit brand goods.","furigana":"けいさつ|にせ|ひん|りゅうつうけいろ|しら"},{"japanese":"産地から店頭までの流通経路が明確な食品は、消費者に信頼されやすい。","romaji":"Sanchi kara tentō made no ryūtsū keiro ga meikaku na shokuhin wa, shōhisha ni shinrai sare yasui.","german":"Foods whose distribution path from the place of origin to the store is clear tend to earn consumers' trust.","furigana":"さんち|てんとう|りゅうつうけいろ|めいかく|しょくひん|しょうひしゃ|しんらい"}],"notes":"流通経路 is the path of a product from the manufacturer through wholesale (卸) and retail (小売) to the consumer. Typical: 流通経路を短縮する/確保する/把握する, 複雑な流通経路; also for smuggled or counterfeit goods: 偽造品の流通経路を調べる. A synonym is 流通チャネル; 販路 means more narrowly the sales channel from the seller's point of view. Register: business language.","levelNote":"Compound: 流通 (N1) + 経路 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ECの普及が流通革命を引き起こしている。","romaji":"EC no fukyū ga ryūtsū kakumei o hikiokoshite iru.","german":"The spread of e-commerce is causing a distribution revolution.","furigana":"ふきゅう|りゅうつうかくめい|ひ|お"},{"japanese":"スーパーマーケットの登場は、日本に流通革命をもたらした。","romaji":"Sūpāmāketto no tōjō wa, Nihon ni ryūtsū kakumei o motarashita.","german":"The appearance of supermarkets brought a distribution revolution to Japan.","furigana":"とうじょう|にほん|りゅうつうかくめい"},{"japanese":"問屋を通さない直接販売は、一種の流通革命だった。","romaji":"Ton'ya o tōsanai chokusetsu hanbai wa, isshu no ryūtsū kakumei datta.","german":"Direct sales that bypass wholesalers were a kind of distribution revolution.","furigana":"とんや|とお|ちょくせつはんばい|いっしゅ|りゅうつうかくめい"}],"notes":"流通革命 is a fundamental upheaval of the distribution channels between manufacturer and consumer. 流通 here means \"distribution, movement of goods\" (wholesale, retail, logistics). The term became popular in Japan in the 1960s, when supermarkets bypassed traditional wholesalers; today it is often used for online commerce and direct sales. Typical: 流通革命が起きる/を引き起こす/をもたらす.","levelNote":"Compound: 流通 (N1) + 革命 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"国際物流の効率化がグローバル競争の鍵だ。","romaji":"Kokusai butsuryū no kōritsuka ga gurōbaru kyōsō no kagi da.","german":"Improving the efficiency of international logistics is the key to global competition.","furigana":"こくさいぶつりゅう|こうりつか|きょうそう|かぎ"},{"japanese":"コロナ禍では、港の混雑で国際物流が大きく混乱した。","romaji":"Korona-ka de wa, minato no konzatsu de kokusai butsuryū ga ōkiku konran shita.","german":"During the COVID pandemic, congestion at ports threw international logistics into great disarray.","furigana":"か|みなと|こんざつ|こくさいぶつりゅう|おお|こんらん"},{"japanese":"大学を卒業してから、国際物流の会社に就職した。","romaji":"Daigaku o sotsugyō shite kara, kokusai butsuryū no kaisha ni shūshoku shita.","german":"After graduating from university, I got a job at an international logistics company.","furigana":"だいがく|そつぎょう|こくさいぶつりゅう|かいしゃ|しゅうしょく"}],"notes":"国際物流 is international logistics: transport, storage, customs clearance and documents across borders. 物流 refers to the physical flow of goods, whereas 流通 means the whole distribution including trade. Typical: 国際物流会社, 国際物流網, 国際物流の混乱/効率化; service providers are often called フォワーダー \"forwarders\". Register: business language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コンテナ輸送の需要が世界的に増加している。","romaji":"Kontena yusō no juyō ga sekaiteki ni zōka shite iru.","german":"Demand for container shipping is increasing worldwide.","furigana":"ゆそう|じゅよう|せかいてき|ぞうか"},{"japanese":"港には色とりどりのコンテナが山積みになっていた。","romaji":"Minato ni wa irotoridori no kontena ga yamazumi ni natte ita.","german":"Colorful containers were piled high at the port.","furigana":"みなと|いろ|やまづ"},{"japanese":"引っ越しまでの間、家具をレンタルのコンテナに預けている。","romaji":"Hikkoshi made no aida, kagu o rentaru no kontena ni azukete iru.","german":"Until the move, I am keeping my furniture in a rented container.","furigana":"ひ|こ|あいだ|かぐ|あず"}],"notes":"コンテナ (also コンテナー) is above all the standardized freight container: コンテナ船, コンテナ輸送, コンテナターミナル, コンテナを積む/降ろす; counted with 本 or 個. There are also rental storage containers (トランクルーム, レンタルコンテナ) and, in IT, software containers. An ordinary household \"container\", by contrast, is called 容器 or 入れ物.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"混載便を利用して小口の貨物を輸送する。","romaji":"Konsaibin wo riyō shite koguchi no kamotsu wo yusō suru.","german":"Use consolidated shipping to transport small cargo.","furigana":"こんさいびん|りよう|こぐち|かもつ|ゆそう"},{"japanese":"荷物が少ないなら、コンテナを借り切るより混載にしたほうが安い。","romaji":"Nimotsu ga sukunai nara, kontena o karikiru yori konsai ni shita hō ga yasui.","german":"If you have little cargo, consolidated shipping is cheaper than chartering a whole container.","furigana":"にもつ|すく|か|き|こんさい|やす"},{"japanese":"このトラックは、冷凍食品と常温の商品を混載できる。","romaji":"Kono torakku wa, reitō shokuhin to jōon no shōhin o konsai dekiru.","german":"This truck can carry frozen food and room-temperature goods together.","furigana":"れいとうしょくひん|じょうおん|しょうひん|こんさい"}],"notes":"混載 means loading goods from different shippers (or of different kinds) together in one container, truck or aircraft - consolidated shipment. Typical: 混載便, 混載貨物, 混載輸送, as a する-verb 荷物を混載する. The opposite is a full load or charter (貸切, チャーター便). For small quantities 混載 is cheaper, but often takes longer. Register: logistics terminology.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"船積み期限までに貨物を港に搬入する。","romaji":"Funazumi kigen made ni kamotsu wo minato ni hannyū suru.","german":"Deliver the cargo to the port by the shipping deadline.","furigana":"ふなづ|きげん|かもつ|みなと|はんにゅう"},{"japanese":"船積みが済んだら、すぐに船積み書類を買い手に送ってください。","romaji":"Funazumi ga sundara, sugu ni funazumi shorui o kaite ni okutte kudasai.","german":"Once loading is done, please send the shipping documents to the buyer right away.","furigana":"ふなづ|す|ふなづ|しょるい|か|て|おく"},{"japanese":"横浜港で、輸出用の自動車を船積みする様子を見学した。","romaji":"Yokohama-kō de, yushutsuyō no jidōsha o funazumi suru yōsu o kengaku shita.","german":"At Yokohama port, I watched cars for export being loaded onto a ship.","furigana":"よこはまこう|ゆしゅつよう|じどうしゃ|ふなづ|ようす|けんがく"}],"notes":"船積み (also 船積) is the loading of goods onto a ship for shipment; as a transitive する-verb: 貨物を船積みする. Typical in foreign trade: 船積み書類 \"shipping documents\", 船積み港, 船積み日, 船積み期限. The opposite is unloading the cargo: 荷揚げ, 陸揚げ or 揚げ荷. More general is 積み込み \"loading\" of vehicles of any kind. Register: trade and logistics language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"揚げ荷作業が天候の影響で遅延した。","romaji":"Ageni sagyō ga tenkō no eikyō de chien shita.","german":"Unloading work was delayed by the weather.","furigana":"あ|にさぎょう|てんこう|えいきょう|ちえん"},{"japanese":"揚げ荷の数量が、船荷証券の記載と一致しなかった。","romaji":"Ageni no sūryō ga, funani shōken no kisai to itchi shinakatta.","german":"The quantity of discharged cargo did not match the entry on the bill of lading.","furigana":"あ|に|すうりょう|ふなにしょうけん|きさい|いっち"},{"japanese":"揚げ荷の中に破損した箱が見つかり、保険会社に連絡した。","romaji":"Ageni no naka ni hason shita hako ga mitsukari, hoken gaisha ni renraku shita.","german":"Damaged boxes were found among the unloaded cargo, so we contacted the insurance company.","furigana":"あ|に|なか|はそん|はこ|み|ほけんがいしゃ|れんらく"}],"notes":"揚げ荷 (also 揚荷) is port terminology: the cargo unloaded (\"discharged\") from a ship, or the unloading itself; 揚げる here means \"to bring ashore\". The counterpart is 積み荷 \"loaded cargo\". Typical: 揚げ荷作業, 揚げ荷役. In general usage 荷揚げ (also on construction sites) and 陸揚げ are much more common. Register: technical language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"航空貨物の取扱量が増加している。","romaji":"Kōkū kamotsu no toriatsukai ryō ga zōka shite iru.","german":"The volume of air cargo handled is increasing.","furigana":"こうくうかもつ|とりあつかいりょう|ぞうか"},{"japanese":"急ぎの部品なので、船便ではなく航空貨物で送った。","romaji":"Isogi no buhin na node, funabin de wa naku kōkū kamotsu de okutta.","german":"The parts were urgent, so we sent them by air cargo rather than by sea.","furigana":"いそ|ぶひん|ふなびん|こうくうかもつ|おく"},{"japanese":"成田空港の航空貨物ターミナルで、輸入品の検査が行われている。","romaji":"Narita Kūkō no kōkū kamotsu tāminaru de, yunyūhin no kensa ga okonawarete iru.","german":"Imported goods are inspected at the air cargo terminal at Narita Airport.","furigana":"なりたくうこう|こうくうかもつ|ゆにゅうひん|けんさ|おこな"}],"notes":"航空貨物 is air cargo, i.e. goods carried by aircraft, as opposed to passengers (旅客). Typical: 航空貨物で送る, 航空貨物便, 航空貨物運賃, 航空貨物ターミナル. Air cargo is fast but more expensive than sea freight (海上輸送, 船便). For parcels and letters, one simply says 航空便 \"airmail\" in everyday speech. Register: logistics and trade language.","levelNote":"Compound: 航空 (N3) + 貨物 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"海上輸送は大量輸送に適している。","romaji":"Kaijō yusō wa tairyō yusō ni tekishite iru.","german":"Sea transport is suited to bulk transport.","furigana":"かいじょうゆそう|たいりょうゆそう|てき"},{"japanese":"海上輸送は時間がかかるが、航空便よりずっと安い。","romaji":"Kaijō yusō wa jikan ga kakaru ga, kōkūbin yori zutto yasui.","german":"Sea transport takes time, but it is much cheaper than air freight.","furigana":"かいじょうゆそう|じかん|こうくうびん|やす"},{"japanese":"運河が通れなくなり、世界の海上輸送に大きな影響が出た。","romaji":"Unga ga tōrenaku nari, sekai no kaijō yusō ni ōki na eikyō ga deta.","german":"With the canal blocked, global sea transport was greatly affected.","furigana":"うんが|とお|せかい|かいじょうゆそう|おお|えいきょう|で"}],"notes":"海上輸送 is the transport of goods by ship over the sea. It is slow but cheapest for large quantities; its counterparts are 航空輸送 and 陸上輸送. Typical: 海上輸送のコスト/ルート, 海上輸送が滞る, as a する-verb 原油を海上輸送する. The shipping industry is called 海運; for parcels in everyday speech one says 船便. Register: business and press language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"陸上輸送網の整備が物流効率を向上させる。","romaji":"Rikujō yusō mō no seibi ga butsuryū kōritsu wo kōjō saseru.","german":"Improving the land transport network raises logistics efficiency.","furigana":"りくじょうゆそうもう|せいび|ぶつりゅうこうりつ|こうじょう"},{"japanese":"港に着いたコンテナは、陸上輸送で内陸の工場まで運ばれる。","romaji":"Minato ni tsuita kontena wa, rikujō yusō de nairiku no kōjō made hakobareru.","german":"Containers that arrive at the port are carried by land transport to factories inland.","furigana":"みなと|つ|りくじょうゆそう|ないりく|こうじょう|はこ"},{"japanese":"運転手不足で、陸上輸送のコストが上がっている。","romaji":"Untenshu busoku de, rikujō yusō no kosuto ga agatte iru.","german":"Costs of land transport are rising because of a shortage of drivers.","furigana":"うんてんしゅぶそく|りくじょうゆそう|あ"}],"notes":"陸上輸送 is transport over land by truck or rail, as opposed to 海上輸送 and 航空輸送. The short form 陸送 is met especially in vehicle transfers (陸送会社). Typical: 陸上輸送網, 陸上輸送のコスト, 陸上輸送に切り替える. Do not confuse it with 陸上 in the sense of \"track and field\" (陸上競技). Register: business and logistics language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"複合輸送により効率的な配送を実現する。","romaji":"Fukugō yusō ni yori kōritsuteki na haisō wo jitsugen suru.","german":"Achieve efficient delivery through multimodal transport.","furigana":"ふくごうゆそう|こうりつてき|はいそう|じつげん"},{"japanese":"海と鉄道を組み合わせた複合輸送で、ヨーロッパまで二十日ほどで届く。","romaji":"Umi to tetsudō o kumiawaseta fukugō yusō de, Yōroppa made hatsuka hodo de todoku.","german":"With multimodal transport combining sea and rail, goods reach Europe in about twenty days.","furigana":"うみ|てつどう|く|あ|ふくごうゆそう|はつか|とど"},{"japanese":"複合輸送では、一つの業者が全区間の責任を負う。","romaji":"Fukugō yusō de wa, hitotsu no gyōsha ga zen kukan no sekinin o ou.","german":"In multimodal transport, a single operator takes responsibility for the entire route.","furigana":"ふくごうゆそう|ひと|ぎょうしゃ|ぜんくかん|せきにん|お"}],"notes":"複合輸送 (複合 \"combined, composite\" + 輸送) refers to transport in which several modes such as ship, rail, truck or aircraft are combined, usually under a single contract and one responsible company (国際複合輸送, 複合一貫輸送). Related is モーダルシフト \"modal shift\", the shift from trucks to rail or ships. Register: logistics terminology.","levelNote":"Compound: 複合 (N1) + 輸送 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"傭船契約を結んで大量の原油を輸送する。","romaji":"Yōsen keiyaku wo musunde tairyō no gen'yu wo yusō suru.","german":"Conclude a charter contract and transport large quantities of crude oil.","furigana":"ようせんけいやく|むす|たいりょう|げんゆ|ゆそう"},{"japanese":"自社船だけでは足りず、外国の船会社から貨物船を傭船した。","romaji":"Jishasen dake de wa tarizu, gaikoku no funagaisha kara kamotsusen o yōsen shita.","german":"Our own ships were not enough, so we chartered a freighter from a foreign shipping company.","furigana":"じしゃせん|た|がいこく|ふながいしゃ|かもつせん|ようせん"},{"japanese":"燃料価格の上昇で、傭船料が大幅に値上がりした。","romaji":"Nenryō kakaku no jōshō de, yōsenryō ga ōhaba ni neagari shita.","german":"Charter rates rose sharply because of higher fuel prices.","furigana":"ねんりょうかかく|じょうしょう|ようせんりょう|おおはば|ねあ"}],"notes":"傭船 is the chartering of a ship, or the chartered ship itself. Since 傭 is not a jōyō kanji, 用船 is often written instead. Typical: 傭船契約 \"charter contract\" (定期傭船 \"time charter\", 航海傭船 \"voyage charter\"), 傭船料 \"charter rate\", as a する-verb 貨物船を傭船する. In everyday speech, for example for buses or flights, one says チャーター. Register: shipping terminology.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"海上保険に加入して輸送中のリスクに備える。","romaji":"Kaijō hoken ni kanyū shite yusōchū no risuku ni sonaeru.","german":"Take out marine insurance to prepare for risks during transport.","furigana":"かいじょうほけん|かにゅう|ゆそうちゅう|そな"},{"japanese":"CIF条件では、売り手が海上保険を手配する。","romaji":"CIF jōken de wa, urite ga kaijō hoken o tehai suru.","german":"Under CIF terms, the seller arranges the marine insurance.","furigana":"じょうけん|う|て|かいじょうほけん|てはい"},{"japanese":"海上保険は、ロンドンのコーヒーハウスから発展したと言われる。","romaji":"Kaijō hoken wa, Rondon no kōhī hausu kara hatten shita to iwareru.","german":"Marine insurance is said to have developed from the coffeehouses of London.","furigana":"かいじょうほけん|はってん|い"}],"notes":"海上保険 is marine insurance; it covers ships (船舶保険) and above all cargo at sea (貨物海上保険). Typical: 海上保険に加入する, 海上保険を付ける/掛ける, 海上保険料. In foreign trade the delivery terms determine who takes it out - under CIF, the seller. 貨物保険 also covers air and land transport, and 損害保険 is the umbrella term \"property and casualty insurance\". Register: technical language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"貨物保険の付保条件を確認する。","romaji":"Kamotsu hoken no fuho jōken wo kakunin suru.","german":"Check the insurance terms of the cargo insurance.","furigana":"かもつほけん|ふほじょうけん|かくにん"},{"japanese":"輸送中に商品が濡れてしまったので、貨物保険で補償してもらった。","romaji":"Yusōchū ni shōhin ga nurete shimatta node, kamotsu hoken de hoshō shite moratta.","german":"The goods got wet in transit, so we were compensated under the cargo insurance.","furigana":"ゆそうちゅう|しょうひん|ぬ|かもつほけん|ほしょう"},{"japanese":"高価な美術品を送るときは、必ず貨物保険を掛けておく。","romaji":"Kōka na bijutsuhin o okuru toki wa, kanarazu kamotsu hoken o kakete oku.","german":"When sending expensive artworks, we always take out cargo insurance.","furigana":"こうか|びじゅつひん|おく|かなら|かもつほけん|か"}],"notes":"貨物保険 is transport or freight insurance for goods in transit, whether by ship, plane or truck. Typical phrases: 貨物保険を掛ける/付ける, 貨物保険で補償される, 貨物保険の付保条件; 付保 is the formal word for \"providing insurance cover\". Unlike 海上保険, it does not cover the ship itself, but it does cover land and air transport as well. Register: trade and insurance language.","levelNote":"Compound: 貨物 (N2) + 保険 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"荷受人が貨物を引き取る際に書類を提示する。","romaji":"Niukenin ga kamotsu wo hikitoru sai ni shorui wo teiji suru.","german":"The consignee presents the documents when taking delivery of the cargo.","furigana":"にうけにん|かもつ|ひ|と|さい|しょるい|ていじ"},{"japanese":"送り状には、荷送人と荷受人の住所を正確に書いてください。","romaji":"Okurijō ni wa, niokurinin to niukenin no jūsho o seikaku ni kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write the addresses of the shipper and the consignee correctly on the waybill.","furigana":"おく|じょう|におくりにん|にうけにん|じゅうしょ|せいかく|か"},{"japanese":"荷受人が不在だったため、貨物は倉庫で保管されている。","romaji":"Niukenin ga fuzai datta tame, kamotsu wa sōko de hokan sarete iru.","german":"Because the consignee was absent, the cargo is being kept in the warehouse.","furigana":"にうけにん|ふざい|かもつ|そうこ|ほかん"}],"notes":"荷受人 (荷 \"cargo\" + 受ける + 人) is the recipient of a shipment, as named on the waybill (送り状) or bill of lading (船荷証券). The counterpart is 荷送人 \"shipper\"; 荷主 is the client or owner of the goods. In everyday speech, for parcels and mail one more often says 受取人 or お届け先. Register: technical and form language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"荷送人が輸出書類を準備する責任がある。","romaji":"Niokurinin ga yushutsu shorui o junbi suru sekinin ga aru.","german":"The shipper is responsible for preparing the export documents.","furigana":"におくりにん|ゆしゅつしょるい|じゅんび|せきにん"},{"japanese":"送り状には荷送人と荷受人の住所を正確に書いてください。","romaji":"Okurijō ni wa niokurinin to niukenin no jūsho o seikaku ni kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write the addresses of the shipper and the consignee correctly on the waybill.","furigana":"おく|じょう|におくりにん|にうけにん|じゅうしょ|せいかく|か"},{"japanese":"荷物が配達できない場合は、荷送人に返送されます。","romaji":"Nimotsu ga haitatsu dekinai baai wa, niokurinin ni hensō saremasu.","german":"If the package cannot be delivered, it will be returned to the sender.","furigana":"にもつ|はいたつ|ばあい|におくりにん|へんそう"}],"notes":"荷送人 (also 荷送り人) is the \"sender\" of a freight or parcel shipment, especially in logistics, insurance and foreign trade; its counterpart is 荷受人 (\"consignee\"). 荷主 similarly refers to the owner or client of the cargo. On letters and in everyday speech, 差出人 or 送り主 is used instead. Typical on forms: 荷送人の住所・氏名.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"乙仲に通関手続きを依頼する。","romaji":"Otsunaka ni tsūkan tetsuzuki wo irai suru.","german":"Ask a freight forwarder to handle customs clearance.","furigana":"おつなか|つうかんてつづ|いらい"},{"japanese":"輸出の書類作りは、付き合いの長い乙仲に任せている。","romaji":"Yushutsu no shoruizukuri wa, tsukiai no nagai otsunaka ni makasete iru.","german":"We leave the export paperwork to a freight forwarder we have worked with for a long time.","furigana":"ゆしゅつ|しょるいづく|つ|あ|なが|おつなか|まか"},{"japanese":"乙仲に頼んで、来週の船にコンテナを一本確保してもらった。","romaji":"Otsunaka ni tanonde, raishū no fune ni kontena o ippon kakuho shite moratta.","german":"I asked the forwarder to secure us one container slot on next week's ship.","furigana":"おつなか|たの|らいしゅう|ふね|いっぽんかくほ"}],"notes":"乙仲 is an abbreviation of 乙種海運仲立業, a former legal category, and is still common in port and foreign-trade jargon: it means the sea freight forwarder who handles booking, documents and customs clearance. Outsiders more often say 海貨業者, 通関業者 or フォワーダー. Typical: 乙仲に依頼する/頼む/任せる. Register: industry jargon.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"輸出代金を為替手形で回収する。","romaji":"Yushutsu daikin o kawase tegata de kaishū suru.","german":"Export proceeds are collected by bill of exchange.","furigana":"ゆしゅつだいきん|かわせてがた|かいしゅう"},{"japanese":"輸出業者は信用状に基づいて為替手形を振り出した。","romaji":"Yushutsu gyōsha wa shin'yōjō ni motozuite kawase tegata o furidashita.","german":"The exporter drew a bill of exchange based on the letter of credit.","furigana":"ゆしゅつぎょうしゃ|しんようじょう|もと|かわせてがた|ふ|だ"},{"japanese":"銀行が為替手形を引き受けたので、期日に支払われることが確定した。","romaji":"Ginkō ga kawase tegata o hikiuketa node, kijitsu ni shiharawareru koto ga kakutei shita.","german":"Since the bank had accepted the bill of exchange, it was certain that it would be paid on the due date.","furigana":"ぎんこう|かわせてがた|ひ|う|きじつ|しはら|かくてい"}],"notes":"為替手形 is a drawn bill of exchange (draft): the drawer instructs a third party (usually a bank or the buyer) to pay a beneficiary. Typical verbs: 為替手形を振り出す \"to draw a bill\", 為替手形を引き受ける \"to accept\", 為替手形で回収する \"to collect by bill of exchange\". In foreign trade it appears mainly as 荷為替手形 together with a letter of credit (信用状). Do not confuse it with 約束手形, where the drawer himself promises payment. 為替 has the irregular reading かわせ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"約束手形で支払いを行うことが商慣行となっている。","romaji":"Yakusoku tegata de shiharai o okonau koto ga shōkankō to natte iru.","german":"It has become standard business practice to make payments by promissory note.","furigana":"やくそくてがた|しはら|おこな|しょうかんこう"},{"japanese":"取引先から受け取った約束手形が不渡りになった。","romaji":"Torihikisaki kara uketotta yakusoku tegata ga fuwatari ni natta.","german":"The promissory note we received from our business partner was dishonored.","furigana":"とりひきさき|う|と|やくそくてがた|ふわた"},{"japanese":"資金繰りが苦しく、約束手形の期日までに現金を用意できない。","romaji":"Shikinguri ga kurushiku, yakusoku tegata no kijitsu made ni genkin o yōi dekinai.","german":"Our cash flow is so tight that we cannot prepare the cash by the due date of the promissory note.","furigana":"しきんぐ|くる|やくそくてがた|きじつ|げんきん|ようい"}],"notes":"約束手形 is the promissory note: the drawer himself promises to pay a sum on a set day. In the Japanese business world, 手形 alone usually means exactly this note; the short form is 約手. Typical are 約束手形を振り出す \"to issue\", 手形が落ちる \"is honored\" and 手形が不渡りになる \"bounces\" - bouncing twice effectively means bankruptcy. By contrast, with a 為替手形 the drawer instructs a third party to pay. Paper notes in Japan are increasingly being replaced by electronically recorded claims (でんさい).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"手形の裏書により第三者に譲渡できる。","romaji":"Tegata no uragaki ni yori daisansha ni jōto dekiru.","german":"A bill can be transferred to a third party by endorsement.","furigana":"てがた|うらがき|だいさんしゃ|じょうと"},{"japanese":"この小切手を換金するには、裏書が必要です。","romaji":"Kono kogitte o kankin suru ni wa, uragaki ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"An endorsement is needed to cash this check.","furigana":"こぎって|かんきん|うらがき|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"新しい調査結果が彼の説を裏書きする形になった。","romaji":"Atarashii chōsa kekka ga kare no setsu o uragaki suru katachi ni natta.","german":"The new research results ended up backing his theory.","furigana":"あたら|ちょうさけっか|かれ|せつ|うらが|かたち"}],"notes":"裏書 (also 裏書き) literally means \"writing on the back\": by signing the back of a bill or check, one transfers it to someone else (手形に裏書する, 裏書して譲渡する). As a する-verb it is transitive. Figuratively, 〜を裏書きする in elevated language means \"to confirm, to substantiate\": 事実が証言を裏書きする. In this sense 裏付ける and 裏付け are much more common in everyday speech.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"割引手形で資金化して運転資金を確保する。","romaji":"Waribiki tegata de shikinka shite unten shikin o kakuho suru.","german":"Secure working capital by turning bills into cash through discounting.","furigana":"わりびきてがた|しきんか|うんてんしきん|かくほ"},{"japanese":"割引手形が不渡りになると、会社は銀行からその手形を買い戻さなければならない。","romaji":"Waribiki tegata ga fuwatari ni naru to, kaisha wa ginkō kara sono tegata o kaimodosanakereba naranai.","german":"If a discounted bill is dishonored, the company must buy it back from the bank.","furigana":"わりびきてがた|ふわた|かいしゃ|ぎんこう|てがた|か|もど"},{"japanese":"決算書には割引手形の残高も注記されている。","romaji":"Kessansho ni wa waribiki tegata no zandaka mo chūki sarete iru.","german":"The balance of discounted bills is also noted in the financial statements.","furigana":"けっさんしょ|わりびきてがた|ざんだか|ちゅうき"}],"notes":"割引手形 is a bill that a company has converted into cash at the bank before maturity, minus interest. The procedure itself is called 手形割引 or 手形を割り引く; 割引手形 refers to the instrument, or to the stock of such bills on the balance sheet. If a discounted bill is dishonored, the company remains liable and has to buy it back (買い戻す) from the bank. A purely technical term from accounting and banking.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"支払条件の変更を取引先に申し入れた。","romaji":"Shiharai jōken no henkō o torihikisaki ni mōshiireta.","german":"We proposed a change in payment terms to our business partner.","furigana":"しはらいじょうけん|へんこう|とりひきさき|もう|い"},{"japanese":"支払条件は月末締め翌月末払いとなっております。","romaji":"Shiharai jōken wa getsumatsu-jime yokugetsumatsu-barai to natte orimasu.","german":"Our payment terms are closing at the end of the month, payment at the end of the following month.","furigana":"しはらいじょうけん|げつまつじ|よくげつまつばら"},{"japanese":"見積書には価格だけでなく支払条件も明記してください。","romaji":"Mitsumorisho ni wa kakaku dake de naku shiharai jōken mo meiki shite kudasai.","german":"Please state not only the price but also the payment terms in the quotation.","furigana":"みつもりしょ|かかく|しはらいじょうけん|めいき"}],"notes":"支払条件 (also 支払い条件) are the payment terms of a contract: due date, payment period, installments, cash discount. Typical: 支払条件を提示する, 支払条件を交渉する, 支払条件を変更する. A very Japanese formula is 月末締め翌月末払い \"closing at the end of the month, payment at the end of the following month\". Distinguish from 支払方法 \"method of payment\" (transfer, cash, card): 条件 mainly concerns timing and modalities.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"契約書で引渡条件を確認してください。","romaji":"Keiyakusho de hikiwatashi jōken o kakunin shite kudasai.","german":"Please check the delivery terms in the contract.","furigana":"けいやくしょ|ひきわたしじょうけん|かくにん"},{"japanese":"今回の取引の引渡条件はFOBです。","romaji":"Konkai no torihiki no hikiwatashi jōken wa FOB desu.","german":"The delivery term for this transaction is FOB.","furigana":"こんかい|とりひき|ひきわたしじょうけん"},{"japanese":"引渡条件によって、運賃や保険料をどちらが払うかが決まる。","romaji":"Hikiwatashi jōken ni yotte, unchin ya hokenryō o dochira ga harau ka ga kimaru.","german":"The delivery terms determine who pays the freight and insurance.","furigana":"ひきわたしじょうけん|うんちん|ほけんりょう|はら|き"}],"notes":"引渡条件 (also 引渡し条件 or 引き渡し条件) are the \"delivery terms\" of a sales or trade contract: when and where the goods are handed over and who bears freight, insurance and risk. In foreign trade they are usually fixed with the Incoterms (インコタームズ), such as FOB or CIF. 引き渡し alone means \"handover\", and in law also the \"extradition\" of a suspect. Typical: 引渡条件を確認する/取り決める.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"船腹不足により運賃が高騰している。","romaji":"Senpuku fusoku ni yori unchin ga kōtō shite iru.","german":"Freight rates are soaring due to a shortage of shipping space.","furigana":"せんぷくふそく|うんちん|こうとう"},{"japanese":"繁忙期に備えて早めに船腹を確保した。","romaji":"Hanbōki ni sonaete hayame ni senpuku o kakuho shita.","german":"We secured shipping space early in preparation for the peak season.","furigana":"はんぼうき|そな|はや|せんぷく|かくほ"},{"japanese":"座礁した船は船腹に大きな穴が開いていた。","romaji":"Zashō shita fune wa senpuku ni ōki na ana ga aite ita.","german":"The grounded ship had a large hole in its hull.","furigana":"ざしょう|ふね|せんぷく|おお|あな|あ"}],"notes":"船腹 literally means \"ship's belly\", i.e. the side of the hull. In trade and logistics it mainly stands for available shipping space or tonnage: 船腹を確保する \"to book cargo space\", 船腹不足 \"shortage of cargo space\", 船腹過剰 \"overcapacity\", 船腹量 \"tonnage\". The physical hold inside a ship is more often called 船倉. A word of technical and business language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"船舶が港に係留されて貨物の積み下ろしが行われる。","romaji":"Senpaku ga minato ni keiryū sarete kamotsu no tsumioroshi ga okonawareru.","german":"The ship is moored at the port and the cargo is loaded and unloaded.","furigana":"せんぱく|みなと|けいりゅう|かもつ|つ|お|おこな"},{"japanese":"台風に備えて、漁船をしっかりと係留した。","romaji":"Taifū ni sonaete, gyosen o shikkari to keiryū shita.","german":"In preparation for the typhoon, the fishing boats were moored securely.","furigana":"たいふう|そな|ぎょせん|けいりゅう"},{"japanese":"引退した帆船が博物館として岸壁に係留されている。","romaji":"Intai shita hansen ga hakubutsukan to shite ganpeki ni keiryū sarete iru.","german":"A retired sailing ship is moored at the quay as a museum.","furigana":"いんたい|はんせん|はくぶつかん|がんぺき|けいりゅう"}],"notes":"係留 (older spelling 繋留, often written けい留 in regulations) means \"to make fast with lines\": 船を岸壁に係留する, very often in the passive 係留されている. Unlike 停泊 \"to lie at anchor, to be in port\", 係留 emphasizes the tying up. Balloons (係留気球) and, in ordinances, leashed dogs can also be 係留. Written and technical; in everyday speech one more often says つなぐ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"農産物の輸入には検疫手続きが必要だ。","romaji":"Nōsanbutsu no yunyū ni wa ken'eki tetsuzuki ga hitsuyō da.","german":"Für den Import landwirtschaftlicher Produkte sind Quarantäneverfahren erforderlich.","furigana":"のうさんぶつ|ゆにゅう|けんえきてつづ|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"帰国した際、空港で検疫を受けた。","romaji":"Kikoku shita sai, kūkō de ken'eki o uketa.","german":"Bei meiner Rückkehr wurde ich am Flughafen einer Gesundheitskontrolle unterzogen.","furigana":"きこく|さい|くうこう|けんえき|う"},{"japanese":"輸入された牛肉が検疫で止められている。","romaji":"Yunyū sareta gyūniku ga ken'eki de tomerarete iru.","german":"Das importierte Rindfleisch wird bei der Quarantänekontrolle zurückgehalten.","furigana":"ゆにゅう|ぎゅうにく|けんえき|と"}],"notes":"検疫 ist die gesundheitliche Kontrolle an der Grenze – bei Menschen, Tieren, Pflanzen und Lebensmitteln: 検疫を受ける „sich der Kontrolle unterziehen“, 検疫所, 動物検疫, 植物検疫, 検疫で止められる. Achtung: Das deutsche „Quarantäne“ im Sinn von Absonderung kranker Personen heißt meist 隔離; 検疫 ist die Einreise- bzw. Einfuhrprüfung, die eine Absonderung anordnen kann. Fachlich-amtlicher Ausdruck.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"植物防疫法に基づいて輸入植物が検査される。","romaji":"Shokubutsu bōekihō ni motozuite yunyū shokubutsu ga kensa sareru.","german":"Imported plants are inspected under the Plant Protection Act.","furigana":"しょくぶつぼうえきほう|もと|ゆにゅうしょくぶつ|けんさ"},{"japanese":"海外から苗木を持ち込む場合は、植物防疫所で検査を受けなければならない。","romaji":"Kaigai kara naegi o mochikomu baai wa, shokubutsu bōekisho de kensa o ukenakereba naranai.","german":"If you bring seedlings in from abroad, you must have them inspected at a plant protection station.","furigana":"かいがい|なえぎ|も|こ|ばあい|しょくぶつぼうえきしょ|けんさ|う"},{"japanese":"新しい害虫の侵入を防ぐため、植物防疫の体制が強化された。","romaji":"Atarashii gaichū no shinnyū o fusegu tame, shokubutsu bōeki no taisei ga kyōka sareta.","german":"The plant quarantine system was strengthened to prevent the entry of new pests.","furigana":"あたら|がいちゅう|しんにゅう|ふせ|しょくぶつぼうえき|たいせい|きょうか"}],"notes":"植物防疫 is state plant protection against pests and plant diseases: both import inspection of plants (植物検疫) and control measures inside the country. The word appears almost only in fixed combinations such as 植物防疫法 \"Plant Protection Act\", 植物防疫所 \"plant protection station\" (at ports and airports) and 植物防疫官. 防疫 alone means epidemic prevention in general. Official language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"特恵関税の適用には原産地証明が必要だ。","romaji":"Tokkei kanzei no tekiyō ni wa gensanchi shōmei ga hitsuyō da.","german":"A certificate of origin is required to apply the preferential tariff.","furigana":"とっけいかんぜい|てきよう|げんさんちしょうめい|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"商工会議所で原産地証明を取得した。","romaji":"Shōkō kaigisho de gensanchi shōmei o shutoku shita.","german":"We obtained a certificate of origin from the chamber of commerce.","furigana":"しょうこうかいぎしょ|げんさんちしょうめい|しゅとく"},{"japanese":"原産地証明書の記載に誤りがあり、通関が遅れた。","romaji":"Gensanchi shōmeisho no kisai ni ayamari ga ari, tsūkan ga okureta.","german":"Due to an error in the certificate of origin, customs clearance was delayed.","furigana":"げんさんちしょうめいしょ|きさい|あやま|つうかん|おく"}],"notes":"原産地証明 is proof of the country in which goods were produced; the document itself is precisely called 原産地証明書. It is needed for preferential tariffs from free trade agreements (特恵関税, EPA) and in Japan is usually issued by the chamber of commerce (商工会議所). Typical: 原産地証明を取得する, 発給する, 提出する. To be distinguished from 原産地表示, the indication of origin on the label.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"商業送り状に商品の詳細と価格を記載する。","romaji":"Shōgyō okurijō ni shōhin no shōsai to kakaku o kisai suru.","german":"The commercial invoice lists the product details and prices.","furigana":"しょうぎょうおく|じょう|しょうひん|しょうさい|かかく|きさい"},{"japanese":"通関には商業送り状と船荷証券の提出が求められる。","romaji":"Tsūkan ni wa shōgyō okurijō to funani shōken no teishutsu ga motomerareru.","german":"Customs clearance requires the submission of the commercial invoice and the bill of lading.","furigana":"つうかん|しょうぎょうおく|じょう|ふなにしょうけん|ていしゅつ|もと"},{"japanese":"商業送り状の金額と信用状の金額が一致しなかった。","romaji":"Shōgyō okurijō no kingaku to shin'yōjō no kingaku ga itchi shinakatta.","german":"The amount on the commercial invoice did not match the amount in the letter of credit.","furigana":"しょうぎょうおく|じょう|きんがく|しんようじょう|きんがく|いっち"}],"notes":"商業送り状 is the formal official term for the commercial invoice in exports (description of goods, quantity, price, delivery terms). In practice one usually says インボイス or 商業インボイス. 送り状 alone is ambiguous: it can also mean the waybill or shipping label of a parcel service (宅配便の送り状). Typical: 商業送り状を作成する, 〜に記載する, 船荷証券と一緒に提出する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"貨物に荷印を付けて出荷する。","romaji":"Kamotsu ni nijirushi o tsukete shukka suru.","german":"Ship the cargo with shipping marks attached.","furigana":"かもつ|にじるし|つ|しゅっか"},{"japanese":"荷印が消えかけていて、どの荷物か判別できなかった。","romaji":"Nijirushi ga kiekakete ite, dono nimotsu ka hanbetsu dekinakatta.","german":"The marking had almost faded, so we couldn't tell which shipment it was.","furigana":"にじるし|き|にもつ|はんべつ"},{"japanese":"荷印は船積書類の記載と一致させる必要がある。","romaji":"Nijirushi wa funazumi shorui no kisai to itchi saseru hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"Shipping marks must match the entries in the shipping documents.","furigana":"にじるし||きさい|いっち|ひつよう"}],"notes":"荷印 are the markings on crates and cartons in shipping: consignee abbreviation, port of destination, case number, etc. One says 荷印を付ける or 荷印を表示する; in practice it is often also called ケースマーク or シッピングマーク. The details must match the shipping documents so that the shipment can be identified unambiguously. A purely technical logistics term.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"集荷センターで荷物を集約して効率的に配送する。","romaji":"Shūka sentā de nimotsu o shūyaku shite kōritsuteki ni haisō suru.","german":"Pool packages at the collection center and deliver them efficiently.","furigana":"しゅうか|にもつ|しゅうやく|こうりつてき|はいそう"},{"japanese":"宅配便の集荷を午後に依頼した。","romaji":"Takuhaibin no shūka o gogo ni irai shita.","german":"I requested a parcel pickup for the afternoon.","furigana":"たくはいびん|しゅうか|ごご|いらい"},{"japanese":"農協が地元の農家から米を集荷している。","romaji":"Nōkyō ga jimoto no nōka kara kome o shūka shite iru.","german":"The agricultural cooperative collects rice from local farmers.","furigana":"のうきょう|じもと|のうか|こめ|しゅうか"}],"notes":"集荷 is the collecting of cargo: on the one hand, the pickup of parcels by a courier service (集荷を依頼する, 集荷に来てもらう), on the other hand the purchasing and collecting of agricultural products, for example by a cooperative (米を集荷する). Opposite terms are 出荷 \"shipment\" and 配達 \"delivery\"; together they are called 集配. Neutral, and very common in everyday parcel shipping.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仕向地までの輸送費用を見積もる。","romaji":"Shimukechi made no yusō hiyō o mitsumoru.","german":"Estimate the transport costs to the destination.","furigana":"しむけち|ゆそうひよう|みつ"},{"japanese":"仕向地によっては、輸出許可が必要な品目がある。","romaji":"Shimukechi ni yotte wa, yushutsu kyoka ga hitsuyō na hinmoku ga aru.","german":"Depending on the destination, some items require an export license.","furigana":"しむけち|ゆしゅつきょか|ひつよう|ひんもく"},{"japanese":"船荷証券の仕向地欄にハンブルクと記入した。","romaji":"Funani shōken no shimukechi-ran ni Hanburuku to kinyū shita.","german":"I wrote Hamburg in the destination field of the bill of lading.","furigana":"ふなにしょうけん|しむけちらん|きにゅう"}],"notes":"仕向地 (also 仕向け地) is the destination or destination country of a shipment of goods in foreign trade; likewise 仕向港 \"port of destination\" and 仕向国. It appears mainly in documents, contracts and export controls. In everyday speech one says 目的地 or 届け先. Do not confuse it with the verb 仕向ける \"to induce someone to do something\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"中継港での積替えにより輸送日数が増加する。","romaji":"Chūkeikō de no tsumikae ni yori yusō nissū ga zōka suru.","german":"Transshipment at a transit port increases the transport time.","furigana":"ちゅうけいこう|つみか|ゆそうにっすう|ぞうか"},{"japanese":"信用状では積替えが禁止されている。","romaji":"Shin'yōjō de wa tsumikae ga kinshi sarete iru.","german":"Transshipment is prohibited under the letter of credit.","furigana":"しんようじょう|つみか|きんし"},{"japanese":"貨物はシンガポールで積替えの上、ヨーロッパへ輸送される。","romaji":"Kamotsu wa Shingapōru de tsumikae no ue, Yōroppa e yusō sareru.","german":"The cargo will be transshipped in Singapore and then transported to Europe.","furigana":"かもつ|つみか|うえ|ゆそう"}],"notes":"積替え (usually written 積み替え) is the reloading of cargo from one means of transport to another, for example at a transit port. The related verb is transitive: 荷物を積み替える. Letters of credit often specify whether 積替え is allowed (積替え可) or prohibited (積替え禁止). The spelling without み (積替え) is typical of technical and form texts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"荷役の遅延により滞船料が発生した。","romaji":"Niyaku no chien ni yori taisenryō ga hassei shita.","german":"Demurrage was incurred due to delays in cargo handling.","furigana":"にやく|ちえん|たいせんりょう|はっせい"},{"japanese":"滞船料は一日あたりの金額で契約に定められている。","romaji":"Taisenryō wa ichinichi atari no kingaku de keiyaku ni sadamerarete iru.","german":"Demurrage is set in the contract as a daily amount.","furigana":"たいせんりょう|いちにち|きんがく|けいやく|さだ"},{"japanese":"港の混雑で荷揚げが遅れ、荷主が滞船料を負担することになった。","romaji":"Minato no konzatsu de niage ga okure, ninushi ga taisenryō o futan suru koto ni natta.","german":"Unloading was delayed by port congestion, and the shipper ended up bearing the demurrage.","furigana":"みなと|こんざつ|にあ|おく|にぬし|たいせんりょう|ふたん"}],"notes":"滞船料 is demurrage in maritime freight law: if the charterer needs longer than the agreed laytime for loading or unloading, he pays the shipowner a daily rate. Typical: 滞船料が発生する, 滞船料を支払う, 滞船料を負担する. The counterpart for faster work is 早出料. For containers, people nowadays mostly say デマレージ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"予定より早く荷役が完了し、早出料が支払われた。","romaji":"Yotei yori hayaku niyaku ga kanryō shi, hayaderyō ga shiharawareta.","german":"Cargo handling was completed ahead of schedule, and despatch money was paid.","furigana":"よてい|はや|にやく|かんりょう|はやでりょう|しはら"},{"japanese":"早出料は滞船料の半額とするのが一般的だ。","romaji":"Hayaderyō wa taisenryō no hangaku to suru no ga ippanteki da.","german":"Despatch money is generally half of the demurrage.","furigana":"はやでりょう|たいせんりょう|はんがく|いっぱんてき"},{"japanese":"傭船契約に早出料の規定がなかったため、交渉が長引いた。","romaji":"Yōsen keiyaku ni hayaderyō no kitei ga nakatta tame, kōshō ga nagabiita.","german":"Because the charter agreement had no provision for despatch money, the negotiations dragged on.","furigana":"ようせんけいやく|はやでりょう|きてい|こうしょう|ながび"}],"notes":"早出料 is despatch money: if loading or unloading is faster than agreed, the shipowner pays the charterer a bonus for the time saved. It is the counterpart to 滞船料 \"demurrage\" and is usually half of its daily rate. In maritime trade it is read はやでりょう (not そうしゅつりょう); 早出 alone means \"early start of work, early shift\" in everyday speech. A purely technical term.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不積みが発生し、納期に遅れが生じた。","romaji":"Fuzumi ga hassei shi, nōki ni okure ga shōjita.","german":"A short shipment occurred, and the delivery date was delayed.","furigana":"ふづ|はっせい|のうき|おく|しょう"},{"japanese":"契約数量に満たない不積みの分については、不積運賃を支払わなければならない。","romaji":"Keiyaku sūryō ni mitanai fuzumi no bun ni tsuite wa, fuzumi unchin o shiharawanakereba naranai.","german":"For the portion of short shipment below the contract quantity, dead freight must be paid.","furigana":"けいやくすうりょう|み|ふづ|ぶん||しはら"},{"japanese":"港のストライキで貨物の一部が不積みとなった。","romaji":"Minato no sutoraiki de kamotsu no ichibu ga fuzumi to natta.","german":"Due to a port strike, part of the cargo was not loaded.","furigana":"みなと|かもつ|いちぶ|ふづ"}],"notes":"不積み refers in the sea freight business to the case where the contractually promised cargo is not loaded, wholly or partly. For the unused cargo space the charterer owes the shipowner dead freight, in Japanese 不積運賃. Typical: 不積みが発生する, 一部が不積みとなる. For cargo or passengers that simply no longer fit on board, the general-language word is 積み残し. A rare technical term.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"過積みは安全上の問題があるため禁止されている。","romaji":"Kazumi wa anzenjō no mondai ga aru tame kinshi sarete iru.","german":"Overloading is prohibited for safety reasons.","furigana":"かづ|あんぜんじょう|もんだい|きんし"},{"japanese":"過積載のトラックが高速道路で取り締まりを受けた。","romaji":"Kasekisai no torakku ga kōsoku dōro de torishimari o uketa.","german":"An overloaded truck was cracked down on on the expressway.","furigana":"かせきさい|こうそくどうろ|と|し|う"},{"japanese":"船の過積みが転覆事故の原因とみられている。","romaji":"Fune no kazumi ga tenpuku jiko no gen'in to mirarete iru.","german":"Overloading of the ship is thought to be the cause of the capsizing accident.","furigana":"ふね|かづ|てんぷくじこ|げんいん"}],"notes":"過積み means \"overloading\", i.e. more cargo than permitted. The word is rather rare; the standard in law, media and police language is 過積載 (e.g. 過積載のトラック, 過積載で取り締まられる). Both typically appear with 禁止, 取り締まり or as an accident cause (事故の原因). To load a vehicle too heavily is 積みすぎる or 積み過ぎる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"保税地域内では関税が課されない。","romaji":"Hozei chiiki nai de wa kanzei ga kasarenai.","german":"No customs duties are imposed within a bonded area.","furigana":"ほぜいちいきない|かんぜい|か"},{"japanese":"輸入した部品は通関前に保税倉庫で保管されている。","romaji":"Yunyū shita buhin wa tsūkan mae ni hozei sōko de hokan sarete iru.","german":"The imported parts are stored in a bonded warehouse before customs clearance.","furigana":"ゆにゅう|ぶひん|つうかんまえ|ほぜいそうこ|ほかん"},{"japanese":"貨物を保税のまま別の港へ運送した。","romaji":"Kamotsu o hozei no mama betsu no minato e unsō shita.","german":"The cargo was transported in bond to another port.","furigana":"かもつ|ほぜい|べつ|みなと|うんそう"}],"notes":"保税 means \"under customs supervision, not yet cleared\": goods are stored or transported without duty having been paid yet. The word appears almost only as the first element of compounds: 保税地域 \"bonded area\", 保税倉庫 \"bonded warehouse\", 保税運送 \"transport in bond\", 保税工場. Standing alone it occurs in 保税のまま \"still uncleared\". Customs and logistics technical language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"1971年、アメリカは輸入品に一律10%の課徴金を課した。","romaji":"Sen kyūhyaku nanajūichi-nen, Amerika wa yunyūhin ni ichiritsu juppāsento no kachōkin o kashita.","german":"In 1971, the United States imposed a flat 10% surcharge on imports.","furigana":"ねん|ゆにゅうひん|いちりつ|かちょうきん|か"},{"japanese":"カルテルを結んだ企業に公正取引委員会が課徴金の納付を命じた。","romaji":"Karuteru o musunda kigyō ni Kōsei Torihiki Iinkai ga kachōkin no nōfu o meijita.","german":"The Fair Trade Commission ordered the companies that formed the cartel to pay a surcharge.","furigana":"むす|きぎょう|こうせいとりひきいいんかい|かちょうきん|のうふ|めい"},{"japanese":"課徴金を逃れるため、違反を自主申告する企業も多い。","romaji":"Kachōkin o nogareru tame, ihan o jishu shinkoku suru kigyō mo ōi.","german":"Many companies self-report violations to escape the surcharge.","furigana":"かちょうきん|のが|いはん|じしゅしんこく|きぎょう|おお"}],"notes":"課徴金 is a levy imposed by the state that is not counted as a tax. Today it mainly means the fine imposed by the competition authority (公正取引委員会) in cartel cases: 課徴金納付命令, 課徴金を課す, 課徴金減免制度 \"leniency program\". There is also the 輸入課徴金 \"import surcharge\". Unlike 罰金, it is an administrative sanction, not a criminal penalty. Official and press language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"補助金付き輸出品に相殺関税を課す。","romaji":"Hojokin tsuki yushutsuhin ni sōsai kanzei o kasu.","german":"Impose a countervailing duty on subsidized export goods.","furigana":"ほじょきんつ|ゆしゅつひん|そうさいかんぜい|か"},{"japanese":"政府は外国産鉄鋼に対する相殺関税の発動を決定した。","romaji":"Seifu wa gaikokusan tekkō ni taisuru sōsai kanzei no hatsudō o kettei shita.","german":"The government decided to impose countervailing duties on foreign steel.","furigana":"せいふ|がいこくさんてっこう|たい|そうさいかんぜい|はつどう|けってい"},{"japanese":"相殺関税を課すには、補助金による国内産業への損害を立証する必要がある。","romaji":"Sōsai kanzei o kasu ni wa, hojokin ni yoru kokunai sangyō e no songai o risshō suru hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"To impose a countervailing duty, it must be proven that subsidies are harming the domestic industry.","furigana":"そうさいかんぜい|か|ほじょきん|こくないさんぎょう|そんがい|りっしょう|ひつよう"}],"notes":"相殺関税 is the countervailing duty against subsidized imports: it offsets the price advantage that foreign producers gain through state aid. Typical: 相殺関税を課す, 相殺関税を発動する. To be distinguished from 反ダンピング関税 (against selling below value) and 報復関税 (retaliation). Note the reading: 相殺 is read そうさい here, not そうさつ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"報復関税の応酬が両国の経済関係を悪化させた。","romaji":"Hōfuku kanzei no ōshū ga ryōkoku no keizai kankei o akka saseta.","german":"The exchange of retaliatory tariffs worsened the economic relations between the two countries.","furigana":"ほうふくかんぜい|おうしゅう|りょうこく|けいざいかんけい|あっか"},{"japanese":"相手国が報復関税を発動すれば、輸出企業への打撃は避けられない。","romaji":"Aitekoku ga hōfuku kanzei o hatsudō sureba, yushutsu kigyō e no dageki wa sakerarenai.","german":"If the other country imposes retaliatory tariffs, a blow to exporters is unavoidable.","furigana":"あいてこく|ほうふくかんぜい|はつどう|ゆしゅつきぎょう|だげき|さ"},{"japanese":"大統領は報復関税をちらつかせて交渉を有利に進めようとした。","romaji":"Daitōryō wa hōfuku kanzei o chiratsukasete kōshō o yūri ni susumeyō to shita.","german":"The president dangled the threat of retaliatory tariffs to gain an advantage in the negotiations.","furigana":"だいとうりょう|ほうふくかんぜい|こうしょう|ゆうり|すす"}],"notes":"報復関税 is a retaliatory or punitive tariff that a state imposes in response to trade measures by another. Typical: 報復関税を発動する, 報復関税を課す, 報復関税の応酬 \"tit-for-tat tariffs\", 報復関税をちらつかせる \"to threaten tariffs\". Unlike 相殺関税, it is not directed against subsidies but is politically motivated. Common in news about trade wars (貿易戦争).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"従価税は商品価格に対して一定の割合で課される。","romaji":"Jūkazei wa shōhin kakaku ni taishite ittei no wariai de kasareru.","german":"An ad valorem duty is levied at a fixed percentage of the product price.","furigana":"じゅうかぜい|しょうひんかかく|たい|いってい|わりあい|か"},{"japanese":"日本の輸入関税の多くは従価税で、税率はパーセントで示される。","romaji":"Nihon no yunyū kanzei no ōku wa jūkazei de, zeiritsu wa pāsento de shimesareru.","german":"Most of Japan's import tariffs are ad valorem, and the rate is expressed in percent.","furigana":"にほん|ゆにゅうかんぜい|おお|じゅうかぜい|ぜいりつ|しめ"},{"japanese":"消費税も価格に比例するので、従価税の一種だ。","romaji":"Shōhizei mo kakaku ni hirei suru node, jūkazei no isshu da.","german":"The consumption tax is also proportional to the price, so it is a kind of ad valorem tax.","furigana":"しょうひぜい|かかく|ひれい|じゅうかぜい|いっしゅ"}],"notes":"従価税 is a levy based on the value (price) of the goods and expressed as a percentage, that is, an ad valorem duty or tax. The counterpart is 従量税, which is calculated by quantity or weight. 従 means \"according to, following\" here: \"tax according to the price\". Most import tariffs, and also the consumption tax, are 従価税. Technical language of tax and customs theory.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"従量税は商品の数量や重量に基づいて課される。","romaji":"Jūryōzei wa shōhin no sūryō ya jūryō ni motozuite kasareru.","german":"A specific duty is levied based on the quantity or weight of the goods.","furigana":"じゅうりょうぜい|しょうひん|すうりょう|じゅうりょう|もと|か"},{"japanese":"酒税やたばこ税は量に応じて課される従量税だ。","romaji":"Shuzei ya tabakozei wa ryō ni ōjite kasareru jūryōzei da.","german":"Alcohol and tobacco taxes are specific taxes levied according to quantity.","furigana":"しゅぜい|ぜい|りょう|おう|か|じゅうりょうぜい"},{"japanese":"従量税は価格が下がっても税額が変わらない。","romaji":"Jūryōzei wa kakaku ga sagatte mo zeigaku ga kawaranai.","german":"With a specific tax, the tax amount does not change even if the price falls.","furigana":"じゅうりょうぜい|かかく|さ|ぜいがく|か"}],"notes":"従量税 is a levy calculated by the quantity, weight or volume of the goods (e.g. yen per kilogram or liter), regardless of price. The counterpart is 従価税, which is based on value. In Japan, for example, alcohol and tobacco taxes are 従量税. When prices fall, a specific duty has a relatively stronger effect, because the amount stays the same. Technical language of tax and customs theory.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"関税率表に基づいて適用税率を確認する。","romaji":"Kanzeiritsuhyō ni motozuite tekiyō zeiritsu o kakunin suru.","german":"Check the applicable tariff rate based on the tariff schedule.","furigana":"かんぜいりつひょう|もと|てきようぜいりつ|かくにん"},{"japanese":"輸入する商品の品目番号を関税率表で調べた。","romaji":"Yunyū suru shōhin no hinmoku bangō o kanzeiritsuhyō de shirabeta.","german":"I looked up the tariff number of the imported product in the tariff schedule.","furigana":"ゆにゅう|しょうひん|ひんもくばんごう|かんぜいりつひょう|しら"},{"japanese":"関税率表は毎年改正されるので、最新版を確認してください。","romaji":"Kanzeiritsuhyō wa maitoshi kaisei sareru node, saishinban o kakunin shite kudasai.","german":"The tariff schedule is revised every year, so please check the latest edition.","furigana":"かんぜいりつひょう|まいとしかいせい|さいしんばん|かくにん"}],"notes":"関税率表 is the customs tariff: the official list that assigns a duty rate to each commodity number (HSコード or 統計品目番号). In Japan, the currently valid version is called 実行関税率表; it contains, among other things, the general rate (基本税率), the WTO rate and the rates from free trade agreements. Typical: 関税率表で調べる, 関税率表に基づく, 関税率表を改正する. Technical language of customs and foreign trade.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"輸出入銀行が貿易金融を支援している。","romaji":"Yushutsunyū ginkō ga bōeki kin'yū o shien shite iru.","german":"The export-import bank supports trade finance.","furigana":"ゆしゅつにゅうぎんこう|ぼうえききんゆう|しえん"},{"japanese":"かつての日本輸出入銀行は、現在の国際協力銀行の前身である。","romaji":"Katsute no Nihon Yushutsunyū Ginkō wa, genzai no Kokusai Kyōryoku Ginkō no zenshin de aru.","german":"The former Export-Import Bank of Japan is the predecessor of today's Japan Bank for International Cooperation.","furigana":"にほんゆしゅつにゅうぎんこう|げんざい|こくさいきょうりょくぎんこう|ぜんしん"},{"japanese":"プラント輸出の資金を輸出入銀行から借り入れた。","romaji":"Puranto yushutsu no shikin o yushutsunyū ginkō kara kariireta.","german":"We borrowed the funds for the plant export from the export-import bank.","furigana":"ゆしゅつ|しきん|ゆしゅつにゅうぎんこう|か|い"}],"notes":"輸出入銀行 is a state export credit bank that promotes exports, imports and overseas investment with loans and guarantees. Usually a country name precedes it: 米国輸出入銀行 \"Export-Import Bank of the United States\". The former 日本輸出入銀行 was merged in 1999 with a development fund to form today's 国際協力銀行 (JBIC). Reading: 輸出入 = ゆしゅつにゅう, not ゆしゅついり.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"貿易保険に加入して海外取引のリスクに備える。","romaji":"Bōeki hoken ni kanyū shite kaigai torihiki no risuku ni sonaeru.","german":"Take out trade insurance to prepare for the risks of overseas transactions.","furigana":"ぼうえきほけん|かにゅう|かいがいとりひき|そな"},{"japanese":"相手国で政変が起きたが、貿易保険で損失の大部分が補償された。","romaji":"Aitekoku de seihen ga okita ga, bōeki hoken de sonshitsu no daibubun ga hoshō sareta.","german":"There was a coup in the partner country, but most of the loss was covered by trade insurance.","furigana":"あいてこく|せいへん|お|ぼうえきほけん|そんしつ|だいぶぶん|ほしょう"},{"japanese":"輸入者の代金不払いに備えて、貿易保険をかけておいた。","romaji":"Yunyūsha no daikin fubarai ni sonaete, bōeki hoken o kakete oita.","german":"We took out trade insurance in case the importer fails to pay.","furigana":"ゆにゅうしゃ|だいきんふばら|そな|ぼうえきほけん"}],"notes":"貿易保険 insures exporters and investors against risks that ordinary insurance does not cover: the buyer's default, war, foreign exchange restrictions in the partner country. In Japan, it is mainly offered by the state-run 日本貿易保険 (NEXI). Typical: 貿易保険をかける, 貿易保険に加入する, 貿易保険でカバーする. Do not confuse it with cargo insurance for the goods themselves (貨物海上保険).","levelNote":"Compound: 貿易 (N4) + 保険 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"政府は農産物の市場開放を進めている。","romaji":"Seifu wa nōsanbutsu no shijō kaihō o susumete iru.","german":"The government is pushing ahead with opening the market for agricultural products.","furigana":"せいふ|のうさんぶつ|しじょうかいほう|すす"},{"japanese":"日本は外国から市場開放を強く求められてきた。","romaji":"Nihon wa gaikoku kara shijō kaihō o tsuyoku motomerarete kita.","german":"Japan has been strongly urged by other countries to open its market.","furigana":"にほん|がいこく|しじょうかいほう|つよ|もと"},{"japanese":"市場開放によって、安い輸入品が増えた。","romaji":"Shijō kaihō ni yotte, yasui yunyūhin ga fueta.","german":"Market opening has increased cheap imports.","furigana":"しじょうかいほう|やす|ゆにゅうひん|ふ"}],"notes":"市場開放 is the opening of a domestic market to foreign goods, services or companies, for example by lowering tariffs and import restrictions. Typical: 市場開放を求める/迫る/進める, 農産物の市場開放. Related are 自由化 (\"liberalization\", e.g. 米の自由化) and 規制緩和 (\"deregulation\"); the opposite is 保護主義. 市場 is read しじょう here; いちば is a concrete marketplace.","levelNote":"Compound: 市場 (N3) + 開放 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新興企業の市場参入が相次いでいる。","romaji":"Shinkō kigyō no shijō sannyū ga aitsuide iru.","german":"New companies are entering the market one after another.","furigana":"しんこうきぎょう|しじょうさんにゅう|あいつ"},{"japanese":"規制が厳しく、市場参入のハードルが高い。","romaji":"Kisei ga kibishiku, shijō sannyū no hādoru ga takai.","german":"Regulations are strict, so the hurdles for market entry are high.","furigana":"きせい|きび|しじょうさんにゅう|たか"},{"japanese":"外国企業の市場参入を制限する法律がある。","romaji":"Gaikoku kigyō no shijō sannyū o seigen suru hōritsu ga aru.","german":"There are laws that restrict the market entry of foreign companies.","furigana":"がいこくきぎょう|しじょうさんにゅう|せいげん|ほうりつ"}],"notes":"市場参入 is a company's \"market entry\" into a new market or industry. As a verb, one usually says 市場に参入する (参入 is intransitive), less often 市場参入する. Related terms: 新規参入 (\"new entrant\"), 参入障壁 (\"barrier to entry\"), and as the opposite 撤退 (\"withdrawal from the market\"). By contrast, 市場開放 means the opening of a market by the state.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"国際分業の進展により各国の相互依存が深まっている。","romaji":"Kokusai bungyō no shinten ni yori kakkoku no sōgo izon ga fukamatte iru.","german":"With the progress of the international division of labor, countries' mutual dependence is deepening.","furigana":"こくさいぶんぎょう|しんてん|かっこく|そうごいぞん|ふか"},{"japanese":"部品はアジアで作り、組み立ては欧州で行うという国際分業が定着している。","romaji":"Buhin wa Ajia de tsukuri, kumitate wa Ōshū de okonau to iu kokusai bungyō ga teichaku shite iru.","german":"An international division of labor has become established in which parts are made in Asia and assembled in Europe.","furigana":"ぶひん|つく|く|た|おうしゅう|おこな|こくさいぶんぎょう|ていちゃく"},{"japanese":"パンデミックで国際分業の弱点が明らかになった。","romaji":"Pandemikku de kokusai bungyō no jakuten ga akiraka ni natta.","german":"The pandemic revealed the weaknesses of the international division of labor.","furigana":"こくさいぶんぎょう|じゃくてん|あき"}],"notes":"国際分業 is the international division of labor: each country specializes in certain goods or production stages and exchanges through trade. Typical: 国際分業が進む / 進展する, 国際分業体制, 国際分業に組み込まれる. Subtypes are 垂直分業 (raw materials for finished goods, or production stages) and 水平分業 (finished goods for finished goods). Economic language, common in newspapers and textbooks.","levelNote":"Compound: 国際 (N5) + 分業 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"比較優位に基づいて貿易パターンが決まる。","romaji":"Hikaku yūi ni motozuite bōeki patān ga kimaru.","german":"Trade patterns are determined based on comparative advantage.","furigana":"ひかくゆうい|もと|ぼうえき|き"},{"japanese":"リカードは比較優位の理論で自由貿易の利点を説明した。","romaji":"Rikādo wa hikaku yūi no riron de jiyū bōeki no riten o setsumei shita.","german":"Ricardo explained the benefits of free trade with the theory of comparative advantage.","furigana":"ひかくゆうい|りろん|じゆうぼうえき|りてん|せつめい"},{"japanese":"この国は労働集約的な製品に比較優位がある。","romaji":"Kono kuni wa rōdō shūyakuteki na seihin ni hikaku yūi ga aru.","german":"This country has a comparative advantage in labor-intensive products.","furigana":"くに|ろうどうしゅうやくてき|せいひん|ひかくゆうい"}],"notes":"比較優位 is comparative advantage in Ricardo's sense: a country should produce what it makes relatively most cheaply compared with other goods, even if another country can make everything more cheaply. Typical: 〜に比較優位がある, 比較優位を持つ, 比較優位に基づく. To be distinguished from 絶対優位 \"absolute advantage\". A technical term of economics, also used figuratively in the business press.","levelNote":"Compound: 比較 (N3) + 優位 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"交易条件の悪化が経済に悪影響を与えている。","romaji":"Kōeki jōken no akka ga keizai ni akueikyō o ataete iru.","german":"The deterioration in the terms of trade is having a negative impact on the economy.","furigana":"こうえきじょうけん|あっか|けいざい|あくえいきょう|あた"},{"japanese":"原油価格の高騰で、資源を輸入する日本の交易条件は悪化した。","romaji":"Gen'yu kakaku no kōtō de, shigen o yunyū suru Nihon no kōeki jōken wa akka shita.","german":"Due to soaring crude oil prices, the terms of trade of Japan, which imports resources, worsened.","furigana":"げんゆかかく|こうとう|しげん|ゆにゅう|にほん|こうえきじょうけん|あっか"},{"japanese":"交易条件は輸出物価と輸入物価の比率で表される。","romaji":"Kōeki jōken wa yushutsu bukka to yunyū bukka no hiritsu de arawasareru.","german":"The terms of trade are expressed as the ratio of export prices to import prices.","furigana":"こうえきじょうけん|ゆしゅつぶっか|ゆにゅうぶっか|ひりつ|あらわ"}],"notes":"交易条件 are the terms of trade: the ratio of a country's export prices to its import prices. If import prices (for oil, say) rise more than export prices, 交易条件が悪化する; in the opposite case, 改善する. 交易 is an older word for the exchange of goods and survives mainly in this technical term and in historical contexts (南蛮交易); in the modern sense one otherwise says 貿易.","levelNote":"Compound: 交易 (N1) + 条件 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"外国為替管理法に基づいて資金の移動が規制される。","romaji":"Gaikoku kawase kanrihō ni motozuite shikin no idō ga kisei sareru.","german":"Capital movements are regulated under the Foreign Exchange Control Act.","furigana":"がいこくかわせかんりほう|もと|しきん|いどう|きせい"},{"japanese":"その国は通貨危機を受けて外国為替管理を強化した。","romaji":"Sono kuni wa tsūka kiki o ukete gaikoku kawase kanri o kyōka shita.","german":"The country tightened its foreign exchange controls following the currency crisis.","furigana":"くに|つうかきき|う|がいこくかわせかんり|きょうか"},{"japanese":"厳しい外国為替管理のため、利益を本国に送金できなかった。","romaji":"Kibishii gaikoku kawase kanri no tame, rieki o hongoku ni sōkin dekinakatta.","german":"Because of strict foreign exchange controls, we could not remit the profits to the home country.","furigana":"きび|がいこくかわせかんり|りえき|ほんごく|そうきん"}],"notes":"外国為替管理 is state control of foreign exchange and capital transactions, e.g. permission requirements for transfers abroad or fixed exchange rates. Typical: 外国為替管理を強化する / 緩和する / 撤廃する; shorter, 為替管理. In Japan this was governed by the 外国為替管理法, which was liberalized in 1998 into the 外国為替及び外国貿易法 (short: 外為法). Technical language of economics and law.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"多角的貿易体制の維持が世界経済の安定に不可欠だ。","romaji":"Takakuteki bōeki taisei no iji ga sekai keizai no antei ni fukaketsu da.","german":"Maintaining the multilateral trading system is essential for the stability of the world economy.","furigana":"たかくてきぼうえきたいせい|いじ|せかいけいざい|あんてい|ふかけつ"},{"japanese":"ウルグアイ・ラウンドは多角的貿易交渉の一つである。","romaji":"Uruguai Raundo wa takakuteki bōeki kōshō no hitotsu de aru.","german":"The Uruguay Round is one of the multilateral trade negotiations.","furigana":"たかくてきぼうえきこうしょう|ひと"},{"japanese":"二国間協定が増えると、多角的貿易の枠組みが弱まるという懸念がある。","romaji":"Nikokukan kyōtei ga fueru to, takakuteki bōeki no wakugumi ga yowamaru to iu kenen ga aru.","german":"There is concern that an increase in bilateral agreements will weaken the framework of multilateral trade.","furigana":"にこくかんきょうてい|ふ|たかくてきぼうえき|わくぐ|よわ|けねん"}],"notes":"多角的貿易 (also 多角貿易) is multilateral trade, that is, trade under common rules among many countries rather than only between two partners (二国間). It almost always appears in fixed combinations: 多角的貿易体制 \"multilateral trading system\" (GATT/WTO) and 多角的貿易交渉 \"multilateral trade round\" such as the Uruguay Round. Language of politics, diplomacy and the business press.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先進国間での水平分業が拡大している。","romaji":"Senshinkokukan de no suihei bungyō ga kakudai shite iru.","german":"The horizontal division of labor among developed countries is expanding.","furigana":"せんしんこくかん|すいへいぶんぎょう|かくだい"},{"japanese":"スマートフォン業界では、設計と製造を別の会社が担う水平分業が進んでいる。","romaji":"Sumātofon gyōkai de wa, sekkei to seizō o betsu no kaisha ga ninau suihei bungyō ga susunde iru.","german":"In the smartphone industry, a horizontal division of labor is advancing in which different companies handle design and manufacturing.","furigana":"ぎょうかい|せっけい|せいぞう|べつ|かいしゃ|にな|すいへいぶんぎょう|すす"},{"japanese":"垂直統合から水平分業へと、半導体産業の構造が変わった。","romaji":"Suichoku tōgō kara suihei bungyō e to, handōtai sangyō no kōzō ga kawatta.","german":"The structure of the semiconductor industry has changed from vertical integration to a horizontal division of labor.","furigana":"すいちょくとうごう|すいへいぶんぎょう|はんどうたいさんぎょう|こうぞう|か"}],"notes":"水平分業 is the horizontal division of labor. In trade theory it means the exchange of finished goods between countries at a similar level of development; in the business world, the splitting of value creation among specialized firms, for example development at one and manufacturing at another. Opposite terms: 垂直分業 or, at the company level, 垂直統合 \"vertical integration\". Typical: 水平分業が進む, 水平分業型のビジネスモデル.","levelNote":"Compound: 水平 (N2) + 分業 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先進国と発展途上国の間の垂直分業が変化している。","romaji":"Senshinkoku to hatten tojōkoku no aida no suichoku bungyō ga henka shite iru.","german":"The vertical division of labor between developed and developing countries is changing.","furigana":"せんしんこく|はってんとじょうこく|あいだ|すいちょくぶんぎょう|へんか"},{"japanese":"かつては原料を輸入して製品を輸出する垂直分業が一般的だった。","romaji":"Katsute wa genryō o yunyū shite seihin o yushutsu suru suichoku bungyō ga ippanteki datta.","german":"In the past, a vertical division of labor in which raw materials were imported and products exported was common.","furigana":"げんりょう|ゆにゅう|せいひん|ゆしゅつ|すいちょくぶんぎょう|いっぱんてき"},{"japanese":"植民地時代の垂直分業は、途上国の工業化を妨げたと言われる。","romaji":"Shokuminchi jidai no suichoku bungyō wa, tojōkoku no kōgyōka o samatageta to iwareru.","german":"The vertical division of labor of the colonial era is said to have hindered the industrialization of developing countries.","furigana":"しょくみんちじだい|すいちょくぶんぎょう|とじょうこく|こうぎょうか|さまた|い"}],"notes":"垂直分業 is the vertical division of labor: countries or firms take on different stages of the same value chain. The classic pattern is \"developing countries supply raw materials, industrial countries supply finished goods\"; today it also covers the distribution of parts manufacturing and final assembly among different countries. The opposite term is 水平分業. Typical: 垂直分業から水平分業へ, 垂直分業の構造.","levelNote":"Compound: 垂直 (N2) + 分業 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先進国間の産業内貿易が拡大している。","romaji":"Senshinkokukan no sangyōnai bōeki ga kakudai shite iru.","german":"Der intraindustrielle Handel zwischen Industrieländern nimmt zu.","furigana":"せんしんこくかん|さんぎょうないぼうえき|かくだい"},{"japanese":"日本とドイツが互いに自動車を輸出し合うのは産業内貿易の典型例だ。","romaji":"Nihon to Doitsu ga tagai ni jidōsha o yushutsu shiau no wa sangyōnai bōeki no tenkeirei da.","german":"Dass Japan und Deutschland sich gegenseitig Autos liefern, ist ein typisches Beispiel für intraindustriellen Handel.","furigana":"にほん|たが|じどうしゃ|ゆしゅつ|あ|さんぎょうないぼうえき|てんけいれい"},{"japanese":"産業内貿易は、比較優位だけでは説明しにくい。","romaji":"Sangyōnai bōeki wa, hikaku yūi dake de wa setsumei shinikui.","german":"Intraindustrieller Handel lässt sich mit dem komparativen Vorteil allein schwer erklären.","furigana":"さんぎょうないぼうえき|ひかくゆうい|せつめい"}],"notes":"産業内貿易 ist intraindustrieller Handel: Zwei Länder tauschen Güter derselben Branche aus, etwa Autos gegen Autos. Er beruht weniger auf komparativem Vorteil als auf Produktdifferenzierung und Skaleneffekten. Gegenbegriff ist 産業間貿易 „interindustrieller Handel“ (z. B. Rohstoffe gegen Maschinen). Typisch: 産業内貿易が拡大する, 産業内貿易の比率. Fachbegriff der Außenwirtschaftslehre.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"外国貿易の自由化が経済成長を促進する。","romaji":"Gaikoku bōeki no jiyūka ga keizai seichō o sokushin suru.","german":"Liberalizing foreign trade promotes economic growth.","furigana":"がいこくぼうえき|じゆうか|けいざいせいちょう|そくしん"},{"japanese":"江戸時代、外国貿易は主に長崎に限られていた。","romaji":"Edo jidai, gaikoku bōeki wa omo ni Nagasaki ni kagirarete ita.","german":"In the Edo period, foreign trade was largely limited to Nagasaki.","furigana":"えどじだい|がいこくぼうえき|おも|ながさき|かぎ"},{"japanese":"島国の日本にとって、外国貿易は経済の生命線だ。","romaji":"Shimaguni no Nihon ni totte, gaikoku bōeki wa keizai no seimeisen da.","german":"For the island nation of Japan, foreign trade is the lifeline of the economy.","furigana":"しまぐに|にほん|がいこくぼうえき|けいざい|せいめいせん"}],"notes":"外国貿易 is foreign trade, that is, the exchange of goods with other countries. In present-day Japanese, 貿易 alone usually suffices, since it already means \"foreign trade\" by itself; 外国貿易 sounds formal and appears mainly in laws (外国為替及び外国貿易法), statistics and historical texts, or in contrast to 内国貿易 \"domestic trade\". Typical: 外国貿易の自由化, 外国貿易に依存する.","levelNote":"Compound: 外国 (N5) + 貿易 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"内国貿易の活性化が地域経済の発展に寄与する。","romaji":"Naikoku bōeki no kasseika ga chiiki keizai no hatten ni kiyo suru.","german":"Revitalizing domestic trade contributes to the development of regional economies.","furigana":"ないこくぼうえき|かっせいか|ちいきけいざい|はってん|きよ"},{"japanese":"経済学では、国内での取引を内国貿易、国境を越える取引を外国貿易と呼んで区別する。","romaji":"Keizaigaku de wa, kokunai de no torihiki o naikoku bōeki, kokkyō o koeru torihiki o gaikoku bōeki to yonde kubetsu suru.","german":"In economics, trade within a country is called domestic trade and cross-border trade is called foreign trade, to distinguish them.","furigana":"けいざいがく|こくない|とりひき|ないこくぼうえき|こっきょう|こ|とりひき|がいこくぼうえき|よ|くべつ"},{"japanese":"明治期には、鉄道の開通で内国貿易が急速に拡大した。","romaji":"Meiji-ki ni wa, tetsudō no kaitsū de naikoku bōeki ga kyūsoku ni kakudai shita.","german":"In the Meiji period, domestic trade expanded rapidly with the opening of the railways.","furigana":"めいじき|てつどう|かいつう|ないこくぼうえき|きゅうそく|かくだい"}],"notes":"内国貿易 is domestic trade, that is, trade within a country's borders, and is the counterpart of 外国貿易. The word is rare today and sounds technical or historical; in contemporary language one more often says 国内取引, 国内商業 or 国内流通. It is found mainly in economics textbooks and in texts on economic history.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"補償貿易は現金を介さない取引形態だ。","romaji":"Hoshō bōeki wa genkin o kaisanai torihiki keitai da.","german":"Compensation trade is a form of transaction that does not involve cash.","furigana":"ほしょうぼうえき|げんきん|かい|とりひきけいたい"},{"japanese":"設備を提供し、その工場で生産された製品で代金を回収する補償貿易が行われた。","romaji":"Setsubi o teikyō shi, sono kōjō de seisan sareta seihin de daikin o kaishū suru hoshō bōeki ga okonawareta.","german":"A compensation trade deal was made: equipment was supplied, and payment was recovered with products made at that factory.","furigana":"せつび|ていきょう|こうじょう|せいさん|せいひん|だいきん|かいしゅう|ほしょうぼうえき|おこな"},{"japanese":"外貨不足の国との取引では、補償貿易が選ばれることがある。","romaji":"Gaika busoku no kuni to no torihiki de wa, hoshō bōeki ga erabareru koto ga aru.","german":"In transactions with countries short of foreign currency, compensation trade is sometimes chosen.","furigana":"がいかぶそく|くに|とりひき|ほしょうぼうえき|えら"}],"notes":"補償貿易 is a form of compensation deal: a supplier provides machines or an entire plant and is paid not in foreign currency but with the products made there (buy-back). The term became known mainly through trade with China in the 1970s and 80s. Related are バーター貿易 \"barter trade\" and カウンタートレード as the umbrella term. Technical language of foreign trade.","levelNote":"Compound: 補償 (N2) + 貿易 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"公理は証明なしに受け入れられる基本的な命題である。","romaji":"Kōri wa shōmei nashi ni ukeirerareru kihonteki na meidai de aru.","german":"An axiom is a basic proposition accepted without proof.","furigana":"こうり|しょうめい|う|い|きほんてき|めいだい"},{"japanese":"数学では、いくつかの公理をもとに定理を証明していく。","romaji":"Sūgaku de wa, ikutsu ka no kōri o moto ni teiri o shōmei shite iku.","german":"In mathematics, theorems are proven on the basis of a few axioms.","furigana":"すうがく|こうり|ていり|しょうめい"},{"japanese":"「人はいつか死ぬ」というのは、誰も疑わない公理のようなものだ。","romaji":"\"Hito wa itsuka shinu\" to iu no wa, dare mo utagawanai kōri no yō na mono da.","german":"That everyone eventually dies is a sort of axiom nobody questions.","furigana":"ひと|し|だれ|うたが|こうり"}],"notes":"公理 is an \"axiom\" in mathematics and logic: a basic assumption presupposed without proof, from which 定理 (\"theorems\") are derived. To be distinguished from 定義 (\"definition\") and 原理 (\"principle\"). Outside technical language, 公理 also means a generally accepted, self-evident truth, as in …は公理のようなものだ. Typical: 公理を立てる, 公理系 (\"axiom system\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この命題の真偽を論理的に判断してください。","romaji":"Kono meidai no shingi o ronriteki ni handan shite kudasai.","german":"Please judge the truth or falsity of this proposition logically.","furigana":"めいだい|しんぎ|ろんりてき|はんだん"},{"japanese":"「すべての鳥は飛べる」という命題は、ペンギンの存在によって偽となる。","romaji":"Subete no tori wa toberu to iu meidai wa, pengin no sonzai ni yotte gi to naru.","german":"The proposition \"all birds can fly\" is false because of the existence of penguins.","furigana":"とり|と|めいだい|そんざい|ぎ"},{"japanese":"コスト削減が経営陣にとっての至上命題となっている。","romaji":"Kosuto sakugen ga keieijin ni totte no shijō meidai to natte iru.","german":"Cost reduction has become the top priority for management.","furigana":"さくげん|けいえいじん|しじょうめいだい"}],"notes":"命題 is, in logic and philosophy, a statement that can be true or false: 命題が真である / 偽である, 命題の逆・裏・対偶 \"converse, inverse, contrapositive\". It also means \"task, central challenge\", especially in 至上命題 \"top priority\", which is very common in business and politics (originally 至上命令). Written language; in everyday speech one more often says 主張 or 説 for assertions.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"理論を実証するためにデータを収集した。","romaji":"Riron o jisshō suru tame ni dēta o shūshū shita.","german":"We collected data to verify the theory.","furigana":"りろん|じっしょう|しゅうしゅう"},{"japanese":"自動運転バスの実証実験が市内で始まった。","romaji":"Jidō unten basu no jisshō jikken ga shinai de hajimatta.","german":"A field trial of self-driving buses has started in the city.","furigana":"じどううんてん|じっしょうじっけん|しない|はじ"},{"japanese":"この研究は、運動が記憶力を高めることを実証的に示した。","romaji":"Kono kenkyū wa, undō ga kiokuryoku o takameru koto o jisshōteki ni shimeshita.","german":"This study demonstrated empirically that exercise improves memory.","furigana":"けんきゅう|うんどう|きおくりょく|たか|じっしょうてき|しめ"}],"notes":"実証 means \"to demonstrate through facts, data or experiments\"; as a する-verb it is transitive: 仮説を実証する, 効果が実証される. Common are 実証的 \"empirical\", 実証研究 and 実証実験 \"pilot test, field test\" (e.g. for new technology). To be distinguished: 証明 is more general and also mathematical, 立証 emphasizes proving in court or against doubts, 検証 is checking. Written and scientific language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一つの反証によって仮説が覆されることがある。","romaji":"Hitotsu no hanshō ni yotte kasetsu ga kutsugaesareru koto ga aru.","german":"A single counterexample can overturn a hypothesis.","furigana":"ひと|はんしょう|かせつ|くつがえ"},{"japanese":"弁護側は検察の主張に対して反証を挙げた。","romaji":"Bengogawa wa kensatsu no shuchō ni taishite hanshō o ageta.","german":"The defense presented evidence to the contrary against the prosecution's claims.","furigana":"べんごがわ|けんさつ|しゅちょう|たい|はんしょう|あ"},{"japanese":"ポパーは、反証可能性こそが科学の条件だと考えた。","romaji":"Popā wa, hanshō kanōsei koso ga kagaku no jōken da to kangaeta.","german":"Popper considered falsifiability to be the very condition of science.","furigana":"はんしょうかのうせい|かがく|じょうけん|かんが"}],"notes":"反証 is counter-evidence, that is, proof that refutes a claim or hypothesis. Typical: 反証を挙げる / 示す, 反証となる, 反証が見つかる; as a する-verb it is transitive (仮説を反証する), but less common. Important in the philosophy of science: 反証可能性 \"falsifiability\" (Popper). To be distinguished from 反論 \"verbal objection\": 反証 is a piece of evidence, not an argument. Academic and legal language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"論理的な推論に基づいて結論を導いた。","romaji":"Ronriteki na suiron ni motozuite ketsuron o michibiita.","german":"A conclusion was drawn based on logical inference.","furigana":"ろんりてき|すいろん|もと|けつろん|みちび"},{"japanese":"限られたデータから推論すると、売上は来年も伸びるだろう。","romaji":"Kagirareta dēta kara suiron suru to, uriage wa rainen mo nobiru darō.","german":"Inferring from the limited data, sales will probably keep growing next year.","furigana":"かぎ|すいろん|うりあげ|らいねん|の"},{"japanese":"学習済みのAIモデルを使った推論は、スマートフォン上でも実行できる。","romaji":"Gakushūzumi no ēai moderu o tsukatta suiron wa, sumātofon jō de mo jikkō dekiru.","german":"Inference using trained AI models can also be run on a smartphone.","furigana":"がくしゅうず|つか|すいろん|じょう|じっこう"}],"notes":"推論 is inferential thinking, or a conclusion obtained from it: 推論する (transitive), 推論を立てる, 〜から推論する, 論理的な推論. To be distinguished from 推測 \"conjecture\" (less rigorous) and 推理 \"deduction by piecing clues together\", typical of criminal cases. In AI, 推論 is the technical term for inference, that is, applying a trained model. Written, academic language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"帰納法は個別の事例から一般法則を導く方法である。","romaji":"Kinōhō wa kobetsu no jirei kara ippan hōsoku o michibiku hōhō de aru.","german":"The inductive method derives general laws from individual cases.","furigana":"きのうほう|こべつ|じれい|いっぱんほうそく|みちび|ほうほう"},{"japanese":"多くの観察結果から帰納して、一般的な傾向を見いだした。","romaji":"Ōku no kansatsu kekka kara kinō shite, ippanteki na keikō o miidashita.","german":"From many observations, a general tendency was found by induction.","furigana":"おお|かんさつけっか|きのう|いっぱんてき|けいこう|み"},{"japanese":"帰納的に得られた結論は、新しい事例によって覆る可能性がある。","romaji":"Kinōteki ni erareta ketsuron wa, atarashii jirei ni yotte kutsugaeru kanōsei ga aru.","german":"Conclusions obtained inductively may be overturned by new cases.","furigana":"きのうてき|え|けつろん|あたら|じれい|くつがえ|かのうせい"}],"notes":"帰納 is induction: reasoning from the particular case to the general. It appears almost always in 帰納法 \"inductive method\" or 帰納的 \"inductive\"; 帰納する exists but is rarer. The opposite term is 演繹 \"deduction\". Beware of the homophone 機能 \"function\" - in writing the kanji distinguish them clearly. Academic language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"演繹的推論では前提から結論が必然的に導かれる。","romaji":"En'ekiteki suiron de wa zentei kara ketsuron ga hitsuzenteki ni michibikareru.","german":"In deductive reasoning, the conclusion follows necessarily from the premises.","furigana":"えんえきてきすいろん|ぜんてい|けつろん|ひつぜんてき|みちび"},{"japanese":"数学の証明は、公理から定理を演繹する作業だ。","romaji":"Sūgaku no shōmei wa, kōri kara teiri o en'eki suru sagyō da.","german":"A mathematical proof is the work of deducing theorems from axioms.","furigana":"すうがく|しょうめい|こうり|ていり|えんえき|さぎょう"},{"japanese":"名探偵は、わずかな手がかりから演繹的に犯人を割り出した。","romaji":"Meitantei wa, wazuka na tegakari kara en'ekiteki ni hannin o waridashita.","german":"The master detective deductively identified the culprit from a few clues.","furigana":"めいたんてい|て|えんえきてき|はんにん|わ|だ"}],"notes":"演繹 is deduction: reasoning from general premises compellingly to the particular case. Common in 演繹法, 演繹的 \"deductive\" and as a transitive する-verb: 公理から定理を演繹する. The opposite term is 帰納 \"induction\". The second kanji 繹 hardly occurs in other words; the reading is えんえき. Academic language; in everyday speech, 導き出す is more usual.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"有機化合物の合成には高度な技術が必要だ。","romaji":"Yūki kagōbutsu no gōsei ni wa kōdo na gijutsu ga hitsuyō da.","german":"The synthesis of organic compounds requires advanced techniques.","furigana":"ゆうきかごうぶつ|ごうせい|こうど|ぎじゅつ|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"2枚の写真を合成して、一枚の風景写真を作った。","romaji":"Nimai no shashin o gōsei shite, ichimai no fūkei shashin o tsukutta.","german":"I combined two photos to make a single landscape photo.","furigana":"まい|しゃしん|ごうせい|いちまい|ふうけいしゃしん|つく"},{"japanese":"このカーナビは合成音声で道案内をする。","romaji":"Kono kānabi wa gōsei onsei de michi annai o suru.","german":"This car navigation system gives directions with a synthesized voice.","furigana":"ごうせいおんせい|みちあんない"}],"notes":"合成 means \"to put together from several parts into something new\", as a transitive する-verb: 化合物を合成する, 画像を合成する. Typical compounds: 合成樹脂 \"synthetic resin\", 合成繊維 \"synthetic fiber\", 合成音声 \"synthetic voice\", 合成写真 \"photo montage\", 光合成 \"photosynthesis\". The opposite term is 分解 \"decomposition\". Neutral, equally common in technology and everyday speech.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"植物から有効成分を抽出する技術が進歩した。","romaji":"Shokubutsu kara yūkō seibun o chūshutsu suru gijutsu ga shinpo shita.","german":"The technology for extracting active ingredients from plants has advanced.","furigana":"しょくぶつ|ゆうこうせいぶん|ちゅうしゅつ|ぎじゅつ|しんぽ"},{"japanese":"調査対象は、有権者名簿から無作為に抽出した千人だ。","romaji":"Chōsa taishō wa, yūkensha meibo kara musakui ni chūshutsu shita sennin da.","german":"The survey subjects were a thousand people randomly selected from the voter register.","furigana":"ちょうさたいしょう|ゆうけんしゃめいぼ|むさくい|ちゅうしゅつ|せんにん"},{"japanese":"条件に合うデータだけを抽出して表にまとめた。","romaji":"Jōken ni au dēta dake o chūshutsu shite hyō ni matometa.","german":"I extracted only the data matching the conditions and compiled it into a table.","furigana":"じょうけん|あ|ちゅうしゅつ|ひょう"}],"notes":"抽出 means \"to pull out\": chemically, to extract a substance (植物から成分を抽出する), but also to filter out data (条件に合うデータを抽出する) and, in statistics, to draw a sample, especially in 無作為抽出 \"random sampling\". As a する-verb it is transitive. For extracting mineral resources, one says 採掘 or 採取 instead. Technical and written language, very common in IT.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"蒸留によって混合物から純粋な物質を分離する。","romaji":"Jōryū ni yotte kongōbutsu kara junsui na busshitsu o bunri suru.","german":"Pure substances are separated from mixtures by distillation.","furigana":"じょうりゅう|こんごうぶつ|じゅんすい|ぶっしつ|ぶんり"},{"japanese":"実験には蒸留水を使ってください。","romaji":"Jikken ni wa jōryūsui o tsukatte kudasai.","german":"Please use distilled water for the experiment.","furigana":"じっけん|じょうりゅうすい|つか"},{"japanese":"ウイスキーや焼酎は、醸造した酒を蒸留して造る蒸留酒だ。","romaji":"Uisukī ya shōchū wa, jōzō shita sake o jōryū shite tsukuru jōryūshu da.","german":"Whisky and shochu are distilled spirits made by distilling fermented drinks.","furigana":"しょうちゅう|じょうぞう|さけ|じょうりゅう|つく|じょうりゅうしゅ"}],"notes":"蒸留 is distillation: vaporizing a liquid and condensing the vapor again in order to separate substances. As a する-verb it is transitive: 海水を蒸留する. Well-known compounds are 蒸留水 \"distilled water\" and 蒸留酒 \"spirits\", as opposed to 醸造酒 \"brewed / fermented drinks\" such as beer or sake. On whisky labels one often still finds the older spelling 蒸溜, e.g. in 蒸溜所 \"distillery\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"触媒は化学反応の速度を変化させる物質である。","romaji":"Shokubai wa kagaku hannō no sokudo o henka saseru busshitsu de aru.","german":"Ein Katalysator ist eine Substanz, die die Geschwindigkeit chemischer Reaktionen verändert.","furigana":"しょくばい|かがくはんのう|そくど|へんか|ぶっしつ"},{"japanese":"車の排気ガスに含まれる有害物質は、触媒で浄化される。","romaji":"Kuruma no haiki gasu ni fukumareru yūgai busshitsu wa, shokubai de jōka sareru.","german":"Die Schadstoffe im Autoabgas werden von einem Katalysator gereinigt.","furigana":"くるま|はいき|ふく|ゆうがいぶっしつ|しょくばい|じょうか"},{"japanese":"その事件が、社会を変える触媒となった。","romaji":"Sono jiken ga, shakai o kaeru shokubai to natta.","german":"Dieser Vorfall wurde zum Katalysator für gesellschaftlichen Wandel.","furigana":"じけん|しゃかい|か|しょくばい"}],"notes":"触媒 ist ein Katalysator, der eine chemische Reaktion beschleunigt, ohne selbst verbraucht zu werden: 触媒として働く, 触媒を用いる, 光触媒 „Photokatalysator“. Auch der Abgaskatalysator im Auto heißt 触媒. Übertragen wie im Deutschen: 〜の触媒となる „Katalysator für etwas sein“, etwa für gesellschaftlichen Wandel. Fachsprache, übertragen eher gehoben.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"化学反応の過程を詳細に観察した。","romaji":"Kagaku hannō no katei o shōsai ni kansatsu shita.","german":"We observed the process of the chemical reaction in detail.","furigana":"かがくはんのう|かてい|しょうさい|かんさつ"},{"japanese":"新商品に対する客の反応は上々だった。","romaji":"Shinshōhin ni taisuru kyaku no hannō wa jōjō datta.","german":"Customers' reaction to the new product was excellent.","furigana":"しんしょうひん|たい|きゃく|はんのう|じょうじょう"},{"japanese":"名前を呼んでも、患者は反応しなかった。","romaji":"Namae o yonde mo, kanja wa hannō shinakatta.","german":"Even when his name was called, the patient did not respond.","furigana":"なまえ|よ|かんじゃ|はんのう"}],"notes":"反応 is any reaction: chemical (化学反応), physical (アレルギー反応, 陽性反応 \"positive test result\") and social (客の反応, 反応が鈍い \"barely reacts\"). As a する-verb it is intransitive with に: 刺激に反応する, 名前に反応する. Unlike 返事 \"reply\", it is not about answering back but about the effect of a stimulus. Neutral and very common.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鉄が酸化すると錆が生じる。","romaji":"Tetsu ga sanka suru to sabi ga shōjiru.","german":"When iron oxidizes, rust forms.","furigana":"てつ|さんか|さび|しょう"},{"japanese":"切ったリンゴを置いておくと、酸化して茶色くなる。","romaji":"Kitta ringo o oite oku to, sanka shite chairoku naru.","german":"If you leave a cut apple out, it oxidizes and turns brown.","furigana":"き|お|さんか|ちゃいろ"},{"japanese":"このワインには酸化防止剤が含まれている。","romaji":"Kono wain ni wa sanka bōshizai ga fukumarete iru.","german":"This wine contains antioxidants.","furigana":"さんかぼうしざい|ふく"}],"notes":"酸化 refers to chemical \"oxidation\"; as a する-verb it is mostly intransitive: 鉄が酸化する \"iron oxidizes\". To stress the causing of it, one says 酸化させる. The opposite term is 還元 \"reduction\"; together, 酸化還元反応 \"redox reaction\". In everyday life it turns up with food: 酸化した油 \"rancid oil\", 酸化防止剤 \"antioxidant\" on ingredient lists.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"酸化還元反応は電子の授受を伴う。","romaji":"Sanka kangen hannō wa denshi no juju o tomonau.","german":"Redox reactions involve the transfer of electrons.","furigana":"さんかかんげんはんのう|でんし|じゅじゅ|ともな"},{"japanese":"研究成果を地域社会に還元することが目標だ。","romaji":"Kenkyū seika o chiiki shakai ni kangen suru koto ga mokuhyō da.","german":"The goal is to give the research results back to the local community.","furigana":"けんきゅうせいか|ちいきしゃかい|かんげん|もくひょう"},{"japanese":"このカードで払うと、五パーセントがポイントで還元されます。","romaji":"Kono kādo de harau to, go pāsento ga pointo de kangen saremasu.","german":"If you pay with this card, five percent is returned to you as points.","furigana":"はら|ご|かんげん"}],"notes":"還元 has two fields of use: in chemistry, \"reduction\" as the counterpart of 酸化; in everyday life, \"giving back\" profit or benefit to others. The latter typically uses the transitive pattern AをBに還元する: 利益を社会に還元する, 成果を地域に還元する. Very common today is ポイント還元 \"rebate in points\" when paying. In the sense of \"tracing back to something fundamental\" it is likewise 〜に還元する: 問題を個人の責任に還元する.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"砂糖は水に容易に溶解する。","romaji":"Satō wa mizu ni yōi ni yōkai suru.","german":"Sugar dissolves easily in water.","furigana":"さとう|みず|ようい|ようかい"},{"japanese":"粉末を水に溶解させてから服用してください。","romaji":"Funmatsu o mizu ni yōkai sasete kara fukuyō shite kudasai.","german":"Please dissolve the powder in water before taking it.","furigana":"ふんまつ|みず|ようかい|ふくよう"},{"japanese":"工場では鉄くずを高温で溶解し、新しい鋼材を作っている。","romaji":"Kōjō de wa tetsukuzu o kōon de yōkai shi, atarashii kōzai o tsukutte iru.","german":"At the factory, scrap iron is melted at high temperature to make new steel.","furigana":"こうじょう|てつ|こうおん|ようかい|あたら|こうざい|つく"}],"notes":"溶解 is the technical term for a substance \"dissolving\" in a liquid and sounds much more formal than the everyday verb 溶ける; as a する-verb it is mostly intransitive: 砂糖が水に溶解する, with the solvent marked by に. Transitively, one often says 溶解させる. It also refers to the melting of metal (溶解炉 \"melting furnace\"). Beware of the homophone 妖怪 \"ghost, demon\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"液体が凝固して固体になる温度を凝固点という。","romaji":"Ekitai ga gyōko shite kotai ni naru ondo o gyōkoten to iu.","german":"The temperature at which a liquid solidifies into a solid is called the freezing point.","furigana":"えきたい|ぎょうこ|こたい|おんど|ぎょうこてん"},{"japanese":"傷口では血液が凝固して、出血が止まる。","romaji":"Kizuguchi de wa ketsueki ga gyōko shite, shukketsu ga tomaru.","german":"At a wound, the blood coagulates and the bleeding stops.","furigana":"きずぐち|けつえき|ぎょうこ|しゅっけつ|と"},{"japanese":"豆腐は豆乳ににがりを加えて凝固させて作る。","romaji":"Tōfu wa tōnyū ni nigari o kuwaete gyōko sasete tsukuru.","german":"Tofu is made by adding nigari to soy milk to make it coagulate.","furigana":"とうふ|とうにゅう|くわ|ぎょうこ|つく"}],"notes":"凝固 refers to a liquid becoming solid, whether by cooling or by coagulation as with blood: 血液が凝固する. It is an intransitive する-verb in a technical style; in everyday speech one more often says 固まる or, for water, 凍る. Typical compounds are 凝固点 \"freezing point, solidification point\" and 血液凝固 \"blood coagulation\". The opposite term is 融解 \"melting\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"金属の融点は種類によって大きく異なる。","romaji":"Kinzoku no yūten wa shurui ni yotte ōkiku kotonaru.","german":"The melting point of metals varies greatly depending on the type.","furigana":"きんぞく|ゆうてん|しゅるい|おお|こと"},{"japanese":"タングステンは融点が非常に高いので、電球のフィラメントに使われた。","romaji":"Tangusuten wa yūten ga hijō ni takai node, denkyū no firamento ni tsukawareta.","german":"Because tungsten has a very high melting point, it was used for light bulb filaments.","furigana":"ゆうてん|ひじょう|たか|でんきゅう|つか"},{"japanese":"チョコレートは融点が体温に近いので、口の中ですぐ溶ける。","romaji":"Chokorēto wa yūten ga taion ni chikai node, kuchi no naka de sugu tokeru.","german":"Chocolate's melting point is close to body temperature, so it melts right away in your mouth.","furigana":"ゆうてん|たいおん|ちか|くち|なか|と"}],"notes":"融点 is the \"melting point\", the temperature at which a solid becomes liquid (融解する). Typical: 融点が高い・低い, 融点に達する. The counterpart is 凝固点 \"freezing point\"; for water both are at 0 °C. Do not confuse it with 沸点 \"boiling point\". Purely technical; in everyday speech it is paraphrased as 溶ける温度.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"水の沸点は標準気圧で摂氏百度である。","romaji":"Mizu no futten wa hyōjun kiatsu de sesshi hyaku do de aru.","german":"The boiling point of water is 100 degrees Celsius at standard pressure.","furigana":"みず|ふってん|ひょうじゅんきあつ|せっしひゃくど"},{"japanese":"山の上では沸点が下がるので、お湯が百度にならない。","romaji":"Yama no ue de wa futten ga sagaru node, oyu ga hyaku do ni naranai.","german":"On a mountaintop the boiling point drops, so water does not reach 100 degrees.","furigana":"やま|うえ|ふってん|さ|ゆ|ひゃくど"},{"japanese":"うちの部長は沸点が低くて、すぐ怒鳴る。","romaji":"Uchi no buchō wa futten ga hikukute, sugu donaru.","german":"Our department head has a low boiling point and starts yelling right away.","furigana":"ぶちょう|ふってん|ひく|どな"}],"notes":"沸点 is the \"boiling point\": 沸点が高い・低い, 沸点に達する; the related verb is 沸騰する \"to boil\". Colloquially, 沸点が低い is also used figuratively for people who quickly get angry (\"to have a short fuse\"). Do not confuse it with 融点 \"melting point\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"物質の密度は単位体積あたりの質量で定義される。","romaji":"Busshitsu no mitsudo wa tan'i taiseki atari no shitsuryō de teigi sareru.","german":"The density of a substance is defined as mass per unit volume.","furigana":"ぶっしつ|みつど|たんいたいせき|しつりょう|ていぎ"},{"japanese":"東京は人口密度が非常に高い。","romaji":"Tōkyō wa jinkō mitsudo ga hijō ni takai.","german":"Tokyo has an extremely high population density.","furigana":"とうきょう|じんこうみつど|ひじょう|たか"},{"japanese":"短い研修だったが、とても密度の濃い三日間だった。","romaji":"Mijikai kenshū datta ga, totemo mitsudo no koi mikkakan datta.","german":"It was a short training course, but it was three very intense days.","furigana":"みじか|けんしゅう|みつど|こ|みっかかん"}],"notes":"密度 is \"density\" in physics (mass per volume), but it is just as often used figuratively: 人口密度 \"population density\", 密度の濃い時間 \"intense, substantial time\". Typical: 密度が高い・低い, 密度が濃い. Related is the adjective 密 \"dense, close\", but 密度 always refers to a measurable or comparable quantity.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"質量とエネルギーは等価であることが証明された。","romaji":"Shitsuryō to enerugī wa tōka de aru koto ga shōmei sareta.","german":"It has been proven that mass and energy are equivalent.","furigana":"しつりょう|とうか|しょうめい"},{"japanese":"月の上では体重は軽くなるが、質量は変わらない。","romaji":"Tsuki no ue de wa taijū wa karuku naru ga, shitsuryō wa kawaranai.","german":"On the Moon you become lighter, but your mass does not change.","furigana":"つき|うえ|たいじゅう|かる|しつりょう|か"},{"japanese":"ブラックホールの中には、太陽の何百万倍もの質量を持つものがある。","romaji":"Burakku hōru no naka ni wa, taiyō no nanbyakuman bai mo no shitsuryō o motsu mono ga aru.","german":"Some black holes have a mass millions of times that of the Sun.","furigana":"なか|たいよう|なんびゃくまんばい|しつりょう|も"}],"notes":"質量 is the \"mass\" of a body in physics and is clearly distinguished from 重さ or 重量 \"weight\": on the Moon the weight changes, but the 質量 stays the same. Typical: 質量が大きい, 質量保存の法則 \"law of conservation of mass\". Rarely it also means \"quality and quantity\". In everyday speech, 重さ is almost always said instead of 質量.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"万有引力の法則はニュートンによって発見された。","romaji":"Ban'yū inryoku no hōsoku wa Nyūton ni yotte hakken sareta.","german":"The law of universal gravitation was discovered by Newton.","furigana":"ばんゆういんりょく|ほうそく|はっけん"},{"japanese":"潮の満ち引きは主に月の引力によって起こる。","romaji":"Shio no michihiki wa omo ni tsuki no inryoku ni yotte okoru.","german":"The ebb and flow of the tides is caused mainly by the Moon's gravitational pull.","furigana":"しお|み|ひ|おも|つき|いんりょく|お"},{"japanese":"磁石のN極とS極の間には引力が働く。","romaji":"Jishaku no enu kyoku to esu kyoku no aida ni wa inryoku ga hataraku.","german":"An attractive force acts between the north and south poles of a magnet.","furigana":"じしゃく|きょく|きょく|あいだ|いんりょく|はたら"}],"notes":"引力 is in general the \"attractive force\" between bodies; 万有引力 is gravitation in the Newtonian sense. Distinctions: 重力 \"gravity\" is the force that pulls downward on Earth, 磁力 the magnetic force; the opposite is 斥力 \"repulsive force\". Typical: 引力が働く, 月の引力. Figuratively, 引力 can also mean a person's attractiveness, but one usually says 魅力 for that.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"可視光線は電磁波の一種である。","romaji":"Kashi kōsen wa denjiha no isshu de aru.","german":"Visible light is a kind of electromagnetic wave.","furigana":"かしこうせん|でんじは|いっしゅ"},{"japanese":"電子レンジは電磁波を使って食べ物を温める。","romaji":"Denshi renji wa denjiha o tsukatte tabemono o atatameru.","german":"A microwave oven heats food using electromagnetic waves.","furigana":"でんし|でんじは|つか|た|もの|あたた"},{"japanese":"医療機器への電磁波の影響を防ぐため、病院内では携帯電話の使用が制限されている。","romaji":"Iryō kiki e no denjiha no eikyō o fusegu tame, byōin nai de wa keitai denwa no shiyō ga seigen sarete iru.","german":"To prevent electromagnetic waves from affecting medical equipment, the use of mobile phones is restricted in hospitals.","furigana":"いりょうきき|でんじは|えいきょう|ふせ|びょういんない|けいたいでんわ|しよう|せいげん"}],"notes":"電磁波 is the \"electromagnetic wave\", an umbrella term for radio waves, microwaves, light, X-rays, etc. In technical explanations it often appears in the pattern 〜は電磁波の一種だ; in everyday life it comes up mainly regarding mobile phones, microwave ovens and health questions: 電磁波を出す, 電磁波の影響. Unlike 音波 \"sound wave\", it needs no medium.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"光子は光のエネルギーの最小単位である。","romaji":"Kōshi wa hikari no enerugī no saishō tan'i de aru.","german":"A photon is the smallest unit of light energy.","furigana":"こうし|ひかり|さいしょうたんい"},{"japanese":"原子が光子を吸収すると、電子はより高いエネルギー状態に移る。","romaji":"Genshi ga kōshi o kyūshū suru to, denshi wa yori takai enerugī jōtai ni utsuru.","german":"When an atom absorbs a photon, the electron moves to a higher energy state.","furigana":"げんし|こうし|きゅうしゅう|でんし|たか|じょうたい|うつ"},{"japanese":"太陽電池は光子のエネルギーを電気に変える装置だ。","romaji":"Taiyō denchi wa kōshi no enerugī o denki ni kaeru sōchi da.","german":"A solar cell is a device that converts the energy of photons into electricity.","furigana":"たいようでんち|こうし|でんき|か|そうち"}],"notes":"光子 is the \"photon\", the quantum of light; in technical jargon the loanword form フォトン is also common. The word belongs almost exclusively to physics and technology: 光子を放出する・吸収する, 光子のエネルギー. Beware of homophones: こうし is also 講師 \"lecturer\", 格子 \"lattice\" and 子牛 \"calf\"; as a girl's given name, 光子 is usually read みつこ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"量子力学はミクロの世界を記述する理論である。","romaji":"Ryōshi rikigaku wa mikuro no sekai o kijutsu suru riron de aru.","german":"Quantum mechanics is a theory that describes the microscopic world.","furigana":"りょうしりきがく|せかい|きじゅつ|りろん"},{"japanese":"量子コンピューターは、従来のコンピューターでは解けない問題を解けるかもしれない。","romaji":"Ryōshi konpyūtā wa, jūrai no konpyūtā de wa tokenai mondai o tokeru ka mo shirenai.","german":"Quantum computers may be able to solve problems that conventional computers cannot.","furigana":"りょうし|じゅうらい|と|もんだい|と"},{"japanese":"量子の世界では、私たちの常識が通用しない。","romaji":"Ryōshi no sekai de wa, watashitachi no jōshiki ga tsūyō shinai.","german":"In the quantum world, our common sense does not apply.","furigana":"りょうし|せかい|わたし|じょうしき|つうよう"}],"notes":"量子 \"quantum\" hardly ever stands alone but almost always in compounds: 量子力学 \"quantum mechanics\", 量子論 \"quantum theory\", 量子コンピューター, 量子もつれ \"entanglement\". As a modifier it is placed directly in front or linked with の (量子の世界). Beware of the homophones 漁師 \"fisherman\" and 猟師 \"hunter\", which are also read りょうし.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"放射性同位体は医学的診断にも利用される。","romaji":"Hōshasei dōitai wa igakuteki shindan ni mo riyō sareru.","german":"Radioactive isotopes are also used for medical diagnosis.","furigana":"ほうしゃせいどういたい|いがくてきしんだん|りよう"},{"japanese":"炭素の同位体を調べると、化石の年代を推定できる。","romaji":"Tanso no dōitai o shiraberu to, kaseki no nendai o suitei dekiru.","german":"By examining carbon isotopes, the age of fossils can be estimated.","furigana":"たんそ|どういたい|しら|かせき|ねんだい|すいてい"},{"japanese":"水素には三つの同位体がある。","romaji":"Suiso ni wa mittsu no dōitai ga aru.","german":"Hydrogen has three isotopes.","furigana":"すいそ|みっ|どういたい"}],"notes":"同位体 \"isotope\" refers to atoms of the same element with different numbers of neutrons; アイソトープ is used with the same meaning. Common combinations: 放射性同位体 \"radioisotope\", 安定同位体 \"stable isotope\", 炭素の同位体. The word is purely technical and is usually attached to an element with 〜の同位体.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"放射能汚染の影響は長期にわたって続く。","romaji":"Hōshanō osen no eikyō wa chōki ni watatte tsuzuku.","german":"The effects of radioactive contamination continue over the long term.","furigana":"ほうしゃのうおせん|えいきょう|ちょうき|つづ"},{"japanese":"事故の後、住民たちは自宅周辺の放射能を測定した。","romaji":"Jiko no ato, jūmintachi wa jitaku shūhen no hōshanō o sokutei shita.","german":"After the accident, residents measured the radioactivity around their homes.","furigana":"じこ|あと|じゅうみん|じたくしゅうへん|ほうしゃのう|そくてい"},{"japanese":"原発で放射能漏れが起きたというニュースが流れた。","romaji":"Genpatsu de hōshanō more ga okita to iu nyūsu ga nagareta.","german":"News broke that radioactivity had leaked at the nuclear power plant.","furigana":"げんぱつ|ほうしゃのうも|お|なが"}],"notes":"放射能 is, strictly speaking, \"radioactivity\", that is, the property of a substance to emit radiation; the radiation itself is called 放射線, the radiating substance 放射性物質. In colloquial speech, however, 放射能 is often used for all three, as in 放射能漏れ \"leak of radioactivity\". Typical are 放射能汚染 \"radioactive contamination\" and 放射能を測る.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"核分裂反応は莫大なエネルギーを放出する。","romaji":"Kakubunretsu hannō wa bakudai na enerugī o hōshutsu suru.","german":"Nuclear fission reactions release enormous energy.","furigana":"かくぶんれつはんのう|ばくだい|ほうしゅつ"},{"japanese":"原子力発電所は、ウランの核分裂で生じる熱を利用している。","romaji":"Genshiryoku hatsudensho wa, uran no kakubunretsu de shōjiru netsu o riyō shite iru.","german":"Nuclear power plants use the heat produced by the fission of uranium.","furigana":"げんしりょくはつでんしょ|かくぶんれつ|しょう|ねつ|りよう"},{"japanese":"細胞分裂では、まず核分裂が起こり、その後に細胞質が分かれる。","romaji":"Saibō bunretsu de wa, mazu kakubunretsu ga okori, sono ato ni saibōshitsu ga wakareru.","german":"In cell division, nuclear division occurs first, and then the cytoplasm divides.","furigana":"さいぼうぶんれつ|かくぶんれつ|お|あと|さいぼうしつ|わ"}],"notes":"核分裂 \"nuclear fission\" is the process on which nuclear power plants (原子力発電) and the atomic bomb are based. Typical: 核分裂反応, 核分裂を起こす, ウランの核分裂. The opposite term is 核融合 \"nuclear fusion\". In biology, 核分裂 can also mean the division of the cell nucleus; the division of the whole cell is called 細胞分裂.","levelNote":"Compound: 核 (N1) + 分裂 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"核融合は次世代のエネルギー源として期待されている。","romaji":"Kakuyūgō wa jisedai no enerugīgen to shite kitai sarete iru.","german":"Nuclear fusion is expected to be the energy source of the next generation.","furigana":"かくゆうごう|じせだい|げん|きたい"},{"japanese":"太陽は水素の核融合によって輝いている。","romaji":"Taiyō wa suiso no kakuyūgō ni yotte kagayaite iru.","german":"The Sun shines thanks to the nuclear fusion of hydrogen.","furigana":"たいよう|すいそ|かくゆうごう|かがや"},{"japanese":"核融合発電の実用化には、まだ多くの課題が残っている。","romaji":"Kakuyūgō hatsuden no jitsuyōka ni wa, mada ōku no kadai ga nokotte iru.","german":"Many challenges remain before fusion power generation can be put to practical use.","furigana":"かくゆうごうはつでん|じつようか|おお|かだい|のこ"}],"notes":"核融合 \"nuclear fusion\" is the merging of light atomic nuclei, as in the interior of the Sun. Typical phrases: 核融合反応, 核融合発電 \"fusion power\", 核融合炉 \"fusion reactor\", 核融合を起こす. The counterpart is 核分裂 \"nuclear fission\". Note: 融合 alone means \"merging\" in general (文化の融合); company mergers, on the other hand, are called 合併.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"音の大きさは波の振幅に比例する。","romaji":"Oto no ōkisa wa nami no shinpuku ni hirei suru.","german":"The loudness of a sound is proportional to the amplitude of the wave.","furigana":"おと|おお|なみ|しんぷく|ひれい"},{"japanese":"振り子の振幅が小さいとき、周期は振幅によらずほぼ一定だ。","romaji":"Furiko no shinpuku ga chiisai toki, shūki wa shinpuku ni yorazu hobo ittei da.","german":"When the amplitude of a pendulum is small, its period is nearly constant regardless of amplitude.","furigana":"ふ|こ|しんぷく|ちい|しゅうき|しんぷく|いってい"},{"japanese":"地震計の記録では、揺れの振幅が急に大きくなっていた。","romaji":"Jishinkei no kiroku de wa, yure no shinpuku ga kyū ni ōkiku natte ita.","german":"According to the seismograph record, the amplitude of the shaking suddenly increased.","furigana":"じしんけい|きろく|ゆ|しんぷく|きゅう|おお"}],"notes":"振幅 is the \"amplitude\", the maximum displacement of an oscillation or wave: 振幅が大きい・小さい, 振り子の振幅, 波の振幅. The word is technical; for everyday fluctuations (prices, moods) one more often says 振れ幅 or 変動幅. Do not confuse it with 周期 \"period\" and 振動数 \"frequency\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"共鳴現象は物理学のさまざまな分野で観察される。","romaji":"Kyōmei genshō wa butsurigaku no samazama na bun'ya de kansatsu sareru.","german":"Resonance phenomena are observed in various fields of physics.","furigana":"きょうめいげんしょう|ぶつりがく|ぶんや|かんさつ"},{"japanese":"ギターの胴は弦の音を共鳴させて大きくする。","romaji":"Gitā no dō wa gen no oto o kyōmei sasete ōkiku suru.","german":"The body of a guitar amplifies the sound of the strings through resonance.","furigana":"どう|げん|おと|きょうめい|おお"},{"japanese":"多くの若者が、彼女の環境保護の訴えに共鳴した。","romaji":"Ōku no wakamono ga, kanojo no kankyō hogo no uttae ni kyōmei shita.","german":"Many young people resonated with her appeal for environmental protection.","furigana":"おお|わかもの|かのじょ|かんきょうほご|うった|きょうめい"}],"notes":"In physics, 共鳴 is \"resonance\", the sympathetic vibration of a body (共鳴する, 共鳴現象, 共鳴箱 \"resonance box\"). At least as common is the figurative meaning \"to find a response, to sympathize with someone's ideas\" in the pattern 〜に共鳴する: 彼の考えに共鳴する. Unlike 共感 \"empathy, feeling along with\", 共鳴 emphasizes agreement with views or goals. Intransitive する-verb; transitively, 共鳴させる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"光がプリズムを通過する際に屈折が起こる。","romaji":"Hikari ga purizumu o tsūka suru sai ni kussetsu ga okoru.","german":"Refraction occurs when light passes through a prism.","furigana":"ひかり|つうか|さい|くっせつ|お"},{"japanese":"水に入れたストローが曲がって見えるのは、光が屈折するからだ。","romaji":"Mizu ni ireta sutorō ga magatte mieru no wa, hikari ga kussetsu suru kara da.","german":"A straw in water looks bent because light is refracted.","furigana":"みず|い|ま|み|ひかり|くっせつ"},{"japanese":"彼の作品には、少年時代の屈折した感情が表れている。","romaji":"Kare no sakuhin ni wa, shōnen jidai no kussetsu shita kanjō ga arawarete iru.","german":"His works show the twisted feelings of his boyhood.","furigana":"かれ|さくひん|しょうねんじだい|くっせつ|かんじょう|あらわ"}],"notes":"In optics, 屈折 is the \"refraction\" of light (光が屈折する, 屈折率 \"refractive index\"). The basic meaning is \"bending, a bend\", and figuratively 屈折した describes a psyche or feelings that are not straightforward: 屈折した感情, 屈折した性格 \"warped, twisted\". In grammar it means \"inflection\" (屈折語 \"inflecting language\"). As a する-verb it is intransitive.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"回折現象は波動の性質を示す重要な証拠である。","romaji":"Kaisetsu genshō wa hadō no seishitsu o shimesu jūyō na shōko de aru.","german":"The diffraction phenomenon is important evidence of the wave nature of waves.","furigana":"かいせつげんしょう|はどう|せいしつ|しめ|じゅうよう|しょうこ"},{"japanese":"塀の向こうの声が聞こえるのは、音波が回折するからだ。","romaji":"Hei no mukō no koe ga kikoeru no wa, onpa ga kaisetsu suru kara da.","german":"The reason you can hear voices beyond a wall is that sound waves diffract.","furigana":"へい|む|こえ|き|おんぱ|かいせつ"},{"japanese":"X線回折を使って、結晶の構造を調べた。","romaji":"Ekkusu sen kaisetsu o tsukatte, kesshō no kōzō o shirabeta.","german":"We examined the crystal structure using X-ray diffraction.","furigana":"せんかいせつ|つか|けっしょう|こうぞう|しら"}],"notes":"回折 \"diffraction\" describes how waves travel around obstacles or through narrow slits into the shadow region. Typical: 回折する, 回折現象, X線回折 \"X-ray diffraction\". Purely technical; to be distinguished from 屈折 \"refraction\" (change of direction on passing into another medium) and 反射 \"reflection\". Do not confuse it with the homophone 解説 \"commentary, explanation\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二つの波が重なると干渉縞が現れる。","romaji":"Futatsu no nami ga kasanaru to kanshō jima ga arawareru.","german":"When two waves overlap, interference fringes appear.","furigana":"ふた|なみ|かさ||あらわ"},{"japanese":"母は私の交友関係にいちいち干渉してくる。","romaji":"Haha wa watashi no kōyū kankei ni ichiichi kanshō shite kuru.","german":"My mother meddles in my friendships at every turn.","furigana":"はは|わたし|こうゆうかんけい|かんしょう"},{"japanese":"他国の内政に干渉すべきではない。","romaji":"Takoku no naisei ni kanshō subeki de wa nai.","german":"One should not interfere in the internal affairs of other countries.","furigana":"たこく|ないせい|かんしょう"}],"notes":"In everyday speech, 干渉 mainly means \"interference\" in other people's affairs, usually negative: 〜に干渉する, 内政干渉 \"interference in internal affairs\", 過干渉 \"excessive interference\" (e.g. by parents). In physics it is the \"interference\" of waves (干渉縞 \"interference fringes\", 電波干渉). More neutral is 介入 \"intervention\"; colloquially one says 口出しする \"to butt in\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"偏光フィルターは特定の方向の光だけを通す。","romaji":"Henkō firutā wa tokutei no hōkō no hikari dake o tōsu.","german":"A polarizing filter lets through only light of a specific direction.","furigana":"へんこう|とくてい|ほうこう|ひかり|とお"},{"japanese":"偏光サングラスをかけると、水面の反射が抑えられて魚が見やすくなる。","romaji":"Henkō sangurasu o kakeru to, suimen no hansha ga osaerarete sakana ga miyasuku naru.","german":"Wearing polarized sunglasses reduces reflections on the water surface, making fish easier to see.","furigana":"へんこう|すいめん|はんしゃ|おさ|さかな|み"},{"japanese":"液晶画面には偏光板が使われている。","romaji":"Ekishō gamen ni wa henkōban ga tsukawarete iru.","german":"Polarizing plates are used in LCD screens.","furigana":"えきしょうがめん|へんこうばん|つか"}],"notes":"偏光 is \"polarized light\" or the \"polarization\" of light. In everyday life it appears mainly in 偏光サングラス \"polarized sunglasses\", 偏光フィルター and 偏光板 \"polarizing film\" in displays. Do not confuse it with the homophones 変更 \"change\" and 偏向 \"bias\" (偏向報道 \"biased reporting\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"蛍光物質は紫外線を吸収して可視光を放出する。","romaji":"Keikō busshitsu wa shigaisen o kyūshū shite kashikō o hōshutsu suru.","german":"Fluorescent substances absorb ultraviolet light and emit visible light.","furigana":"けいこうぶっしつ|しがいせん|きゅうしゅう|かしこう|ほうしゅつ"},{"japanese":"大事なところに蛍光ペンで線を引いた。","romaji":"Daiji na tokoro ni keikō pen de sen o hiita.","german":"I drew lines over the important parts with a highlighter.","furigana":"だいじ|けいこう|せん|ひ"},{"japanese":"事務所の蛍光灯をLEDに取り替えた。","romaji":"Jimusho no keikōtō o LED ni torikaeta.","german":"We replaced the fluorescent lights in the office with LEDs.","furigana":"じむしょ|けいこうとう|と|か"}],"notes":"蛍光 \"fluorescence\" (literally \"firefly light\") is known mainly from everyday compounds: 蛍光灯 \"fluorescent lamp\", 蛍光ペン \"highlighter\", 蛍光色 \"neon color\". In technical use: 蛍光を発する, 蛍光物質. To be distinguished is 燐光 \"phosphorescence\", which keeps glowing after the light source is switched off.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"放射線は適切に管理しないと健康に害を及ぼす。","romaji":"Hōshasen wa tekisetsu ni kanri shinai to kenkō ni gai o oyobosu.","german":"Radiation harms health if it is not properly controlled.","furigana":"ほうしゃせん|てきせつ|かんり|けんこう|がい|およ"},{"japanese":"祖父はがんの放射線治療を受けている。","romaji":"Sofu wa gan no hōshasen chiryō o ukete iru.","german":"My grandfather is receiving radiation therapy for cancer.","furigana":"そふ|ほうしゃせんちりょう|う"},{"japanese":"レントゲン検査で浴びる放射線の量はごくわずかだ。","romaji":"Rentogen kensa de abiru hōshasen no ryō wa goku wazuka da.","german":"The amount of radiation received in an X-ray examination is very small.","furigana":"けんさ|あ|ほうしゃせん|りょう"}],"notes":"放射線 is \"radiation\" in the sense of ionizing radiation (X-rays, gamma, alpha and beta rays). Typical: 放射線を浴びる \"to be exposed to radiation\", 放射線量 \"radiation dose\", 放射線治療 \"radiation therapy\". Distinction: 放射能 is the ability of a substance to emit 放射線. For heat or solar radiation, one does not use 放射線 but 熱放射 or 日射.","levelNote":"Compound: 放射 (N1) + 線 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"電子は負の電荷を持つ素粒子である。","romaji":"Denshi wa fu no denka o motsu soryūshi de aru.","german":"Electrons are elementary particles with a negative charge.","furigana":"でんし|ふ|でんか|も|そりゅうし"},{"japanese":"同じ種類の電荷どうしは反発し合う。","romaji":"Onaji shurui no denka dōshi wa hanpatsu shiau.","german":"Charges of the same kind repel each other.","furigana":"おな|しゅるい|でんか|はんぱつ|あ"},{"japanese":"下敷きでこすった髪の毛は、電荷を帯びて逆立つ。","romaji":"Shitajiki de kosutta kami no ke wa, denka o obite sakadatsu.","german":"Hair rubbed with a plastic sheet picks up an electric charge and stands on end.","furigana":"|かみ|け|でんか|お|さかだ"}],"notes":"電荷 is the \"electric charge\" of a particle or body: 正の電荷・負の電荷, 電荷を帯びる \"to be charged\", 電荷を持つ. It is a purely technical term; in everyday speech one more often says 静電気 \"static electricity\". Do not confuse it with the homophone 殿下 \"His/Her Highness\" (form of address for princes) or with 充電 \"charging a battery\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"電位差が電流を流す原動力となる。","romaji":"Den'isa ga denryū o nagasu gendōryoku to naru.","german":"The potential difference is the driving force for electric current.","furigana":"でんいさ|でんりゅう|なが|げんどうりょく"},{"japanese":"神経細胞は膜電位の変化によって信号を伝える。","romaji":"Shinkei saibō wa maku den'i no henka ni yotte shingō o tsutaeru.","german":"Nerve cells transmit signals through changes in membrane potential.","furigana":"しんけいさいぼう|まくでんい|へんか|しんごう|つた"},{"japanese":"回路では、地面の電位を基準のゼロとすることが多い。","romaji":"Kairo de wa, jimen no den'i o kijun no zero to suru koto ga ōi.","german":"In circuits, the potential of the ground is often taken as the reference zero.","furigana":"かいろ|じめん|でんい|きじゅん|おお"}],"notes":"電位 is the \"electric potential\" at a point; the difference between two points, 電位差 \"potential difference\", corresponds to electric voltage (電圧). Typical: 電位が高い・低い, in biology 膜電位 \"membrane potential\". Purely technical; in everyday speech one says 電圧.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"電気抵抗は導体の材質と長さに依存する。","romaji":"Denki teikō wa dōtai no zaishitsu to nagasa ni izon suru.","german":"Electrical resistance depends on the material and length of the conductor.","furigana":"でんきていこう|どうたい|ざいしつ|なが|いぞん"},{"japanese":"犯人は抵抗せずに逮捕された。","romaji":"Hannin wa teikō sezu ni taiho sareta.","german":"The culprit was arrested without resisting.","furigana":"はんにん|ていこう|たいほ"},{"japanese":"知らない人と二人で食事をするのは、まだ少し抵抗がある。","romaji":"Shiranai hito to futari de shokuji o suru no wa, mada sukoshi teikō ga aru.","german":"I still feel a bit reluctant about having a meal alone with someone I don't know.","furigana":"し|ひと|ふたり|しょくじ|すこ|ていこう"}],"notes":"抵抗 is first of all \"resistance\" against pressure or force: 〜に抵抗する, 抵抗運動 \"resistance movement\". Very common is the psychological meaning 〜に抵抗がある \"to have reservations, to feel reluctant\", e.g. 人前で話すのに抵抗がある. In physics and engineering it refers to air or frictional resistance (空気抵抗) and electrical resistance, also the component. Similar is 反抗, which rather means defiant rebellion against parents or teachers.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"銅は優れた導体として広く使用されている。","romaji":"Dō wa sugureta dōtai to shite hiroku shiyō sarete iru.","german":"Copper is widely used as an excellent conductor.","furigana":"どう|すぐ|どうたい|ひろ|しよう"},{"japanese":"人間の体も導体なので、感電することがある。","romaji":"Ningen no karada mo dōtai na node, kanden suru koto ga aru.","german":"The human body is also a conductor, so you can get an electric shock.","furigana":"にんげん|からだ|どうたい|かんでん"},{"japanese":"半導体の不足で、車の生産が遅れている。","romaji":"Handōtai no fusoku de, kuruma no seisan ga okurete iru.","german":"Because of the semiconductor shortage, car production is delayed.","furigana":"はんどうたい|ふそく|くるま|せいさん|おく"}],"notes":"導体 is a \"conductor\" of electricity or heat: 電気の導体, 良導体 \"good conductor\". The opposite term is 絶縁体 \"insulator\"; in between lies 半導体 \"semiconductor\", which appears even more often than 導体 itself in business news. The matching verb is 電気を通す \"to conduct electricity\"; a person as a \"leader\", on the other hand, is called 指導者 or 責任者.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ゴムは電気を通さない絶縁体である。","romaji":"Gomu wa denki o tōsanai zetsuentai de aru.","german":"Rubber is an insulator that does not conduct electricity.","furigana":"でんき|とお|ぜつえんたい"},{"japanese":"電線は絶縁体で覆われているので、触っても感電しない。","romaji":"Densen wa zetsuentai de ōwarete iru node, sawatte mo kanden shinai.","german":"Electric wires are covered with an insulator, so you will not get a shock when you touch them.","furigana":"でんせん|ぜつえんたい|おお|さわ|かんでん"},{"japanese":"純粋な水はほぼ絶縁体だが、不純物が混ざると電気を通す。","romaji":"Junsui na mizu wa hobo zetsuentai da ga, fujunbutsu ga mazaru to denki o tōsu.","german":"Pure water is almost an insulator, but it conducts electricity when impurities are mixed in.","furigana":"じゅんすい|みず|ぜつえんたい|ふじゅんぶつ|ま|でんき|とお"}],"notes":"絶縁体 is an \"insulator\", a substance that does not conduct electricity (or heat), such as rubber, glass or plastic. The opposite term is 導体 \"conductor\". The base word 絶縁 means \"insulation\" (絶縁テープ \"insulating tape\"), but figuratively also \"breaking off a relationship\": 親と絶縁する \"to cut ties with one's parents\". For thermal insulation of a house, one more often says 断熱材.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"超伝導状態では電気抵抗がゼロになる。","romaji":"Chōdendō jōtai de wa denki teikō ga zero ni naru.","german":"In the superconducting state, electrical resistance becomes zero.","furigana":"ちょうでんどうじょうたい|でんきていこう"},{"japanese":"リニアモーターカーは超伝導磁石の力で車体を浮かせる。","romaji":"Rinia mōtā kā wa chōdendō jishaku no chikara de shatai o ukaseru.","german":"The maglev train floats its car body using the force of superconducting magnets.","furigana":"ちょうでんどうじしゃく|ちから|しゃたい|う"},{"japanese":"室温で超伝導を示す物質が見つかれば、世界は大きく変わるだろう。","romaji":"Shitsuon de chōdendō o shimesu busshitsu ga mitsukareba, sekai wa ōkiku kawaru darō.","german":"If a substance that shows superconductivity at room temperature were found, the world would change greatly.","furigana":"しつおん|ちょうでんどう|しめ|ぶっしつ|み|せかい|おお|か"}],"notes":"超伝導 \"superconductivity\" is the state in which a material has no electrical resistance at very low temperature. 超電導 is an equivalent spelling, especially in engineering (超電導リニア). Typical: 超伝導状態, 超伝導体 \"superconductor\", 超伝導磁石, 超伝導を示す. Purely technical.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"慣性の法則は物体の運動の基本原理である。","romaji":"Kansei no hōsoku wa buttai no undō no kihon genri de aru.","german":"The law of inertia is a basic principle of the motion of bodies.","furigana":"かんせい|ほうそく|ぶったい|うんどう|きほんげんり"},{"japanese":"電車が急に止まると、慣性で体が前に倒れそうになる。","romaji":"Densha ga kyū ni tomaru to, kansei de karada ga mae ni taoresō ni naru.","german":"When the train suddenly stops, your body nearly falls forward because of inertia.","furigana":"でんしゃ|きゅう|と|かんせい|からだ|まえ|たお"},{"japanese":"エンジンを切っても、船は慣性でしばらく進み続けた。","romaji":"Enjin o kitte mo, fune wa kansei de shibaraku susumitsuzuketa.","german":"Even after the engine was turned off, the boat kept moving for a while by inertia.","furigana":"き|ふね|かんせい|すす|つづ"}],"notes":"慣性 is the \"inertia\" of a body, its tendency to stay at rest or in motion: 慣性の法則 \"law of inertia\", 慣性が働く, 慣性で進む \"to keep moving by inertia\". For a person's \"carrying on out of habit\", 惰性 is used instead (惰性で続ける). Beware of homophones: 完成 \"completion\", 歓声 \"cheer\", 感性 \"sensibility\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自由落下する物体の加速度は約九点八メートル毎秒毎秒である。","romaji":"Jiyū rakka suru buttai no kasokudo wa yaku kyū ten hachi mētoru mai byō mai byō de aru.","german":"The acceleration of a freely falling object is about 9.8 meters per second squared.","furigana":"じゆうらっか|ぶったい|かそくど|やくきゅうてんはち|まいびょうまいびょう"},{"japanese":"車が急発進すると、乗っている人は大きな加速度を感じる。","romaji":"Kuruma ga kyūhasshin suru to, notte iru hito wa ōki na kasokudo o kanjiru.","german":"When a car starts off abruptly, the occupants feel a strong acceleration.","furigana":"くるま|きゅうはっしん|の|ひと|おお|かそくど|かん"},{"japanese":"ネットの利用者は加速度的に増えている。","romaji":"Netto no riyōsha wa kasokudoteki ni fuete iru.","german":"The number of internet users is growing at an accelerating pace.","furigana":"りようしゃ|かそくどてき|ふ"}],"notes":"加速度 is \"acceleration\" as a physical quantity (change of velocity per time): 加速度が大きい, 重力加速度 \"gravitational acceleration\". The process of speeding up, by contrast, is 加速 (加速する). Figuratively, 加速度的に \"at an increasing pace, rapidly\" is very common: 加速度的に増える.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"遠心力を利用して物質を分離する装置を遠心分離機という。","romaji":"Enshinryoku o riyō shite busshitsu o bunri suru sōchi o enshin bunriki to iu.","german":"A device that uses centrifugal force to separate substances is called a centrifuge.","furigana":"えんしんりょく|りよう|ぶっしつ|ぶんり|そうち|えんしんぶんりき"},{"japanese":"洗濯機は遠心力を使って、洗濯物の水を飛ばす。","romaji":"Sentakuki wa enshinryoku o tsukatte, sentakumono no mizu o tobasu.","german":"The washing machine uses centrifugal force to spin the water out of the laundry.","furigana":"せんたくき|えんしんりょく|つか|せんたくもの|みず|と"},{"japanese":"急カーブでは遠心力がかかって、体が外側に押される。","romaji":"Kyū kābu de wa enshinryoku ga kakatte, karada ga sotogawa ni osareru.","german":"On sharp curves, centrifugal force acts and your body is pushed outward.","furigana":"きゅう|えんしんりょく|からだ|そとがわ|お"}],"notes":"遠心力 \"centrifugal force\" is the outward-acting force in a rotating motion: 遠心力が働く・かかる. The opposite term is 求心力 \"centripetal force\", which figuratively also means the \"cohesion, binding power\" of a leader (首相の求心力が低下する). The device is called 遠心分離機 \"centrifuge\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"摩擦力がなければ歩くことさえできない。","romaji":"Masatsuryoku ga nakereba aruku koto sae dekinai.","german":"Without frictional force, we couldn't even walk.","furigana":"まさつりょく|ある"},{"japanese":"雨の日は路面の摩擦力が小さくなるので、ブレーキが効きにくい。","romaji":"Ame no hi wa romen no masatsuryoku ga chiisaku naru node, burēki ga kikinikui.","german":"On rainy days, the friction of the road surface decreases, so the brakes are less effective.","furigana":"あめ|ひ|ろめん|まさつりょく|ちい|き"},{"japanese":"床にワックスを塗ると、摩擦力が減って滑りやすくなる。","romaji":"Yuka ni wakkusu o nuru to, masatsuryoku ga hette suberiyasuku naru.","german":"When you wax a floor, friction decreases and it becomes slippery.","furigana":"ゆか|ぬ|まさつりょく|へ|すべ"}],"notes":"摩擦力 is the \"frictional force\" between two surfaces: 摩擦力が働く, 摩擦力が大きい・小さい, 摩擦力を減らす. Often plain 摩擦 \"friction\" suffices, which figuratively also means \"friction, tension\" (貿易摩擦 \"trade friction\"); 摩擦力, by contrast, is always meant physically.","levelNote":"Compound: 摩擦 (N2) + 力 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"弾性体は外力を取り除くと元の形に戻る。","romaji":"Dansei tai wa gairyoku o torinozoku to moto no katachi ni modoru.","german":"An elastic body returns to its original shape when the external force is removed.","furigana":"だんせいたい|がいりょく|と|のぞ|もと|かたち|もど"},{"japanese":"天然ゴムは弾性に富んだ素材だ。","romaji":"Tennen gomu wa dansei ni tonda sozai da.","german":"Natural rubber is a material rich in elasticity.","furigana":"てんねん|だんせい|と|そざい"},{"japanese":"ばねを弾性限界を超えて引っ張ると、元に戻らなくなる。","romaji":"Bane o dansei genkai o koete hipparu to, moto ni modoranaku naru.","german":"If you pull a spring beyond its elastic limit, it will no longer return to its original shape.","furigana":"だんせいげんかい|こ|ひ|ぱ|もと|もど"}],"notes":"弾性 is \"elasticity\", the ability of a material to return to its original shape after deformation: 弾性がある, 弾性に富む, 弾性体, 弾性限界 \"elastic limit\". The word is technical; in everyday speech one more often says 弾力 (弾力のある肌 \"firm, springy skin\"). Note: the homophone 男性 \"man\" sounds exactly the same; context decides.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"蜂蜜は水に比べて粘性が非常に高い。","romaji":"Hachimitsu wa mizu ni kurabete nensei ga hijō ni takai.","german":"Honey has a very high viscosity compared to water.","furigana":"はちみつ|みず|くら|ねんせい|ひじょう|たか"},{"japanese":"寒くなると、エンジンオイルの粘性が高くなる。","romaji":"Samuku naru to, enjin oiru no nensei ga takaku naru.","german":"When it gets cold, the viscosity of engine oil increases.","furigana":"さむ|ねんせい|たか"},{"japanese":"火山の溶岩は、粘性によって流れ方が大きく違う。","romaji":"Kazan no yōgan wa, nensei ni yotte nagarekata ga ōkiku chigau.","german":"Volcanic lava flows very differently depending on its viscosity.","furigana":"かざん|ようがん|ねんせい|なが|かた|おお|ちが"}],"notes":"粘性 \"viscosity\" describes how thickly a liquid flows: 粘性が高い・低い, 粘性のある液体. It is technical; in everyday speech one says 粘り気がある or, onomatopoeically, ねばねばする. The related technical term 粘度 denotes the measured viscosity value, for example for motor oil.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"気体分子は拡散によって均一に広がる。","romaji":"Kitai bunshi wa kakusan ni yotte kin'itsu ni hirogaru.","german":"Gas molecules spread out evenly by diffusion.","furigana":"きたいぶんし|かくさん|きんいつ|ひろ"},{"japanese":"そのデマはSNSであっという間に拡散された。","romaji":"Sono dema wa SNS de atto iu ma ni kakusan sareta.","german":"The false rumor spread across social media in no time.","furigana":"ま|かくさん"},{"japanese":"部屋に置いたアロマの香りが、ゆっくり拡散していく。","romaji":"Heya ni oita aroma no kaori ga, yukkuri kakusan shite iku.","german":"The scent of the aroma oil placed in the room slowly spreads.","furigana":"へや|お|かお|かくさん"}],"notes":"拡散 means \"spreading, dissemination\" and in physics \"diffusion\"; as a する-verb it is mostly intransitive (においが拡散する), but today it is also transitive: 情報を拡散する \"to spread information on social media\", 拡散希望 \"please share\". Other combinations: 核拡散 \"nuclear proliferation\", 光の拡散. To be distinguished is 普及, the positive spread of technology or goods.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"結晶構造には特定の対称性が見られる。","romaji":"Kesshō kōzō ni wa tokutei no taishōsei ga mirareru.","german":"A certain symmetry can be seen in the crystal structure.","furigana":"けっしょうこうぞう|とくてい|たいしょうせい|み"},{"japanese":"人間の顔は完全な左右対称ではない。","romaji":"Ningen no kao wa kanzen na sayū taishō de wa nai.","german":"The human face is not perfectly symmetrical left to right.","furigana":"にんげん|かお|かんぜん|さゆうたいしょう"},{"japanese":"この庭園は、池を中心に対称に設計されている。","romaji":"Kono teien wa, ike o chūshin ni taishō ni sekkei sarete iru.","german":"This garden is designed symmetrically around the pond.","furigana":"ていえん|いけ|ちゅうしん|たいしょう|せっけい"}],"notes":"対称 is \"symmetry\": 左右対称 \"mirror-symmetric\", 点対称 \"point-symmetric\", 対称的な形; the property is called 対称性. Beware of three often confused homophones: 対象 \"object, target group\" (子どもを対象にする), 対照 \"contrast, comparison\" (対照的な性格) and 対称 \"symmetry\". The rare meaning \"second person\" (grammar) plays hardly any role in everyday life.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二つの波の位相がずれると干渉が弱まる。","romaji":"Futatsu no nami no isō ga zureru to kanshō ga yowamaru.","german":"When the phases of two waves are shifted, the interference weakens.","furigana":"ふた|なみ|いそう|かんしょう|よわ"},{"japanese":"ノイズキャンセリングは、逆位相の音をぶつけて騒音を打ち消す仕組みだ。","romaji":"Noizu kyanseringu wa, gyaku isō no oto o butsukete sōon o uchikesu shikumi da.","german":"Noise cancelling works by countering noise with sound in opposite phase to cancel it out.","furigana":"ぎゃくいそう|おと|そうおん|う|け|しく"},{"japanese":"大学で位相幾何学の講義を取った。","romaji":"Daigaku de isō kikagaku no kōgi o totta.","german":"I took a lecture on topology at university.","furigana":"だいがく|いそうきかがく|こうぎ|と"}],"notes":"位相 denotes in physics the \"phase\" of an oscillation or wave: 位相がずれる \"to be out of phase\", 位相差 \"phase difference\", 逆位相 \"opposite phase\". In mathematics it stands for \"topology\" (位相幾何学, 位相空間), in linguistics for language variants by group or situation (位相語). Purely technical; the phases of the moon are called 月の満ち欠け in everyday speech.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この関数はx軸に漸近するが交わらない。","romaji":"Kono kansū wa ekkusu jiku ni zenkin suru ga majiwaranai.","german":"This function approaches the x-axis asymptotically but never crosses it.","furigana":"かんすう|じく|ぜんきん|まじ"},{"japanese":"双曲線には二本の漸近線がある。","romaji":"Sōkyokusen ni wa nihon no zenkinsen ga aru.","german":"A hyperbola has two asymptotes.","furigana":"そうきょくせん|にほん|ぜんきんせん"},{"japanese":"サンプル数が増えると、推定値は真の値に漸近していく。","romaji":"Sanpuru sū ga fueru to, suiteichi wa shin no atai ni zenkin shite iku.","german":"As the sample size increases, the estimate gradually approaches the true value.","furigana":"すう|ふ|すいていち|しん|あたい|ぜんきん"}],"notes":"漸近 means \"to approach gradually (arbitrarily closely)\", mainly in mathematics: a curve approaches a straight line ever closer without reaching it (〜に漸近する). The line itself is called 漸近線 \"asymptote\"; adverbially there is 漸近的に \"asymptotically\", e.g. in statistics. The character 漸 \"gradually\" is known from 漸次 and 漸く (usually written ようやく). Outside technical language it is practically unused.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"微分を用いて関数の変化率を求めることができる。","romaji":"Bibun o mochiite kansū no henkaritsu o motomeru koto ga dekiru.","german":"Using differentiation, you can find the rate of change of a function.","furigana":"びぶん|もち|かんすう|へんかりつ|もと"},{"japanese":"この式をxで微分すると、2xになる。","romaji":"Kono shiki o x de bibun suru to, 2x ni naru.","german":"Differentiating this expression with respect to x gives 2x.","furigana":"しき|びぶん"},{"japanese":"高校のとき、微分積分が苦手だった。","romaji":"Kōkō no toki, bibun sekibun ga nigate datta.","german":"In high school, I was bad at calculus.","furigana":"こうこう|びぶんせきぶん|にがて"}],"notes":"微分 is \"differentiation, derivative\" and, as a subject, \"differential calculus\": 微分係数 \"differential coefficient\", 導関数 \"derivative function\". As a する-verb it is transitive: 〜をxで微分する \"to differentiate with respect to x\". Together with 積分 it forms 微分積分 (short: 微積) \"calculus\", a classic subject of upper secondary school and the first year of university.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"面積を求めるために定積分を計算した。","romaji":"Menseki o motomeru tame ni teisekibun o keisan shita.","german":"I calculated a definite integral to find the area.","furigana":"めんせき|もと|ていせきぶん|けいさん"},{"japanese":"速度を時間で積分すると、移動距離が求められる。","romaji":"Sokudo o jikan de sekibun suru to, idō kyori ga motomerareru.","german":"Integrating velocity over time gives the distance traveled.","furigana":"そくど|じかん|せきぶん|いどうきょり|もと"},{"japanese":"微分より積分のほうが計算が面倒だ。","romaji":"Bibun yori sekibun no hō ga keisan ga mendō da.","german":"Integrals are more troublesome to calculate than derivatives.","furigana":"びぶん|せきぶん|けいさん|めんどう"}],"notes":"積分 is \"integration\" or, as a subject, \"integral calculus\"; the result, the \"integral\", is also called 積分. Typical: 定積分 \"definite integral\", 不定積分 \"indefinite integral\". As a する-verb it is transitive: 〜をxで積分する \"to integrate with respect to x\". Together with 微分 it forms 微分積分 \"calculus\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"量子力学では粒子の位置は確率的にしか予測できない。","romaji":"Ryōshi rikigaku de wa ryūshi no ichi wa kakuritsuteki ni shika yosoku dekinai.","german":"In quantum mechanics, the position of a particle can only be predicted probabilistically.","furigana":"りょうしりきがく|りゅうし|いち|かくりつてき|よそく"},{"japanese":"明日の降水確率は七十パーセントだ。","romaji":"Ashita no kōsui kakuritsu wa nanajū pāsento da.","german":"Tomorrow the chance of precipitation is seventy percent.","furigana":"あした|こうすいかくりつ|ななじゅう"},{"japanese":"宝くじで一等が当たる確率は、ほとんどゼロに近い。","romaji":"Takarakuji de ittō ga ataru kakuritsu wa, hotondo zero ni chikai.","german":"The probability of winning first prize in the lottery is close to zero.","furigana":"たから|いっとう|あ|かくりつ|ちか"}],"notes":"確率 is \"probability\", both mathematically and in everyday use: 確率が高い・低い, 〜する確率, 降水確率 \"chance of rain\", 確率は五分五分 \"fifty-fifty\". Adverbial: 確率的に \"probabilistically\". Distinguish 可能性 \"possibility\", which does without a numerical value; with 確率 one thinks of percentages or fractions.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"アボガドロ定数は化学において基本的な定数である。","romaji":"Abogadoro teisū wa kagaku ni oite kihonteki na teisū de aru.","german":"The Avogadro constant is a fundamental constant in chemistry.","furigana":"ていすう|かがく|きほんてき|ていすう"},{"japanese":"次の式で、aとbは定数、xは変数とする。","romaji":"Tsugi no shiki de, a to b wa teisū, x wa hensū to suru.","german":"In the following expression, let a and b be constants and x a variable.","furigana":"つぎ|しき|ていすう|へんすう"},{"japanese":"国会では議員定数の削減が議論されている。","romaji":"Kokkai de wa giin teisū no sakugen ga giron sarete iru.","german":"A reduction in the number of Diet seats is being discussed in the Diet.","furigana":"こっかい|ぎいんていすう|さくげん|ぎろん"}],"notes":"In mathematics and science, 定数 is a \"constant\" (opposite term 変数 \"variable\"), e.g. プランク定数. In politics and administration it denotes a \"fixed number\": 議員定数 \"number of parliamentary seats\", 定数割れ \"falling below the fixed number\", 定数を削減する. Do not confuse it with 定員 \"maximum permitted number (of people, seats)\" for buses or courses.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"三次元座標系で物体の位置を記述する。","romaji":"Sanjigen zahyōkei de buttai no ichi o kijutsu suru.","german":"Describe the position of an object in a three-dimensional coordinate system.","furigana":"さんじげんざひょうけい|ぶったい|いち|きじゅつ"},{"japanese":"グラフ上の点Aの座標を求めなさい。","romaji":"Gurafu jō no ten A no zahyō o motomenasai.","german":"Find the coordinates of point A on the graph.","furigana":"じょう|てん|ざひょう|もと"},{"japanese":"遭難者のスマホから送られた座標をもとに、救助隊が向かった。","romaji":"Sōnansha no sumaho kara okurareta zahyō o moto ni, kyūjotai ga mukatta.","german":"The rescue team set out based on the coordinates sent from the missing person's smartphone.","furigana":"そうなんしゃ|おく|ざひょう|きゅうじょたい|む"}],"notes":"座標 are \"coordinates\", numerical specifications of a position: 座標を求める, x座標・y座標, 座標軸 \"coordinate axis\", 座標系 \"coordinate system\". It is also used for location data on maps or via GPS (緯度と経度の座標). Figuratively, in literary use it can mean \"point of orientation\" (人生の座標).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"弦理論では十次元以上の空間が仮定される。","romaji":"Gen riron de wa jū jigen ijō no kūkan ga katei sareru.","german":"String theory assumes space with ten or more dimensions.","furigana":"げんりろん|じゅうじげんいじょう|くうかん|かてい"},{"japanese":"あの選手のプレーは、ほかの選手とは次元が違う。","romaji":"Ano senshu no purē wa, hoka no senshu to wa jigen ga chigau.","german":"That player plays on a different level from other players.","furigana":"せんしゅ|せんしゅ|じげん|ちが"},{"japanese":"彼は二次元のキャラクターにしか興味がないらしい。","romaji":"Kare wa nijigen no kyarakutā ni shika kyōmi ga nai rashii.","german":"He apparently is only interested in two-dimensional characters.","furigana":"かれ|にじげん|きょうみ"}],"notes":"次元 is the \"dimension\" in mathematics and physics: 三次元 \"three-dimensional\", 四次元. In everyday speech the figurative use as \"level, plane\" is very common: 次元が違う \"to play in a completely different league\", 次元の低い話 \"talk at a low level\", 別次元 \"a completely different dimension\". In pop culture, 二次元 stands for anime and manga characters, 三次元 for real people.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"真空中では音が伝わらない。","romaji":"Shinkū chū de wa oto ga tsutawaranai.","german":"Sound does not travel in a vacuum.","furigana":"しんくうちゅう|おと|つた"},{"japanese":"肉を真空パックにして冷凍した。","romaji":"Niku o shinkū pakku ni shite reitō shita.","german":"I vacuum-packed the meat and froze it.","furigana":"にく|しんくう|れいとう"},{"japanese":"独裁者の死後、国内に権力の真空が生まれた。","romaji":"Dokusaisha no shigo, kokunai ni kenryoku no shinkū ga umareta.","german":"After the dictator's death, a power vacuum arose in the country.","furigana":"どくさいしゃ|しご|こくない|けんりょく|しんくう|う"}],"notes":"真空 is the \"vacuum\", airless space: 真空状態, 真空中で, 真空パック \"vacuum packaging\", 真空管 \"vacuum tube\". Figuratively it denotes an emptiness, especially a \"power vacuum\" (権力の真空, 政治的真空). In everyday life the word is mainly encountered with vacuum-packed foods.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"飽和溶液にはこれ以上溶質が溶けない。","romaji":"Hōwa yōeki ni wa kore ijō yōshitsu ga tokenai.","german":"No more solute can dissolve in a saturated solution.","furigana":"ほうわようえき|いじょうようしつ|と"},{"japanese":"スマートフォン市場はすでに飽和状態にある。","romaji":"Sumātofon shijō wa sude ni hōwa jōtai ni aru.","german":"The smartphone market is already saturated.","furigana":"しじょう|ほうわじょうたい"},{"japanese":"観光客の増加で、この町の宿泊施設は飽和している。","romaji":"Kankōkyaku no zōka de, kono machi no shukuhaku shisetsu wa hōwa shite iru.","german":"Because of the increase in tourists, the accommodations in this town are full.","furigana":"かんこうきゃく|ぞうか|まち|しゅくはくしせつ|ほうわ"}],"notes":"飽和 is \"saturation\": chemically 飽和溶液 \"saturated solution\", physically 飽和水蒸気量 \"saturation vapor content\". Very common is the figurative use for markets, cities or capacities that are \"full\": 市場が飽和する, 飽和状態 \"state of saturation\". As a する-verb it is intransitive. For being full after a meal, one says 満腹 instead.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"化学反応によって沈殿物が生成された。","romaji":"Kagaku hannō ni yotte chindenbutsu ga seisei sareta.","german":"A precipitate was formed by the chemical reaction.","furigana":"かがくはんのう|ちんでんぶつ|せいせい"},{"japanese":"果肉が底に沈殿するので、よく振ってからお飲みください。","romaji":"Kaniku ga soko ni chinden suru node, yoku futte kara onomi kudasai.","german":"The pulp settles at the bottom, so please shake well before drinking.","furigana":"かにく|そこ|ちんでん|ふ|の"},{"japanese":"浄水場では、まず泥を沈殿させてから水をろ過する。","romaji":"Jōsuijō de wa, mazu doro o chinden sasete kara mizu o roka suru.","german":"At the water treatment plant, the mud is first allowed to settle, and then the water is filtered.","furigana":"じょうすいじょう|どろ|ちんでん|みず|か"}],"notes":"沈殿 denotes the \"settling\" of solid particles in a liquid and the resulting \"precipitate, sediment\": 沈殿する, 沈殿物, 沈殿が生じる. In everyday life it turns up with drinks (底に沈殿する) and in water treatment. The older spelling 沈澱 still occurs. As a する-verb it is intransitive, transitively 沈殿させる; \"precipitation\" in the sense of rain, on the other hand, is 降水.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"濾過によって不純物を取り除くことができる。","romaji":"Roka ni yotte fujunbutsu o torinozoku koto ga dekiru.","german":"Impurities can be removed by filtration.","furigana":"ろか|ふじゅんぶつ|と|のぞ"},{"japanese":"腎臓は血液を濾過して、尿をつくる。","romaji":"Jinzō wa ketsueki o roka shite, nyō o tsukuru.","german":"The kidneys filter the blood and produce urine.","furigana":"じんぞう|けつえき|ろか|にょう"},{"japanese":"水槽の水は、濾過装置できれいに保たれている。","romaji":"Suisō no mizu wa, roka sōchi de kirei ni tamotarete iru.","german":"The aquarium water is kept clean by a filtration device.","furigana":"すいそう|みず|ろかそうち|たも"}],"notes":"濾過 is \"filtration\", the filtering out of solids: 〜を濾過する, 濾過装置. Because 濾 is not a Jōyō kanji, it is usually written ろ過 today. In everyday speech, for coffee or broth one more often says こす \"to strain\" or フィルターを通す. It is also known from medicine: 腎臓が血液を濾過する \"the kidneys filter the blood\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"塩化ナトリウムは立方体の結晶構造を持つ。","romaji":"Enka natoriumu wa rippōtai no kesshō kōzō o motsu.","german":"Sodium chloride has a cubic crystal structure.","furigana":"えんか|りっぽうたい|けっしょうこうぞう|も"},{"japanese":"雪の結晶は六角形をしている。","romaji":"Yuki no kesshō wa rokkakukei o shite iru.","german":"Snow crystals are hexagonal.","furigana":"ゆき|けっしょう|ろっかくけい"},{"japanese":"この作品は彼女の十年間の努力の結晶だ。","romaji":"Kono sakuhin wa kanojo no jūnenkan no doryoku no kesshō da.","german":"This work is the crystallization of her ten years of effort.","furigana":"さくひん|かのじょ|じゅうねんかん|どりょく|けっしょう"}],"notes":"結晶 is the technical term for \"crystal\" and \"crystallization\"; as an intransitive する-verb: 塩が結晶する. Common combinations are 結晶構造, 結晶化 and 雪の結晶 \"snow crystal\". Figuratively, it denotes the \"fruit\" of long effort: 努力の結晶, 汗の結晶, and 愛の結晶 poetically stands for a shared child. The mineral \"rock crystal\" is called 水晶 instead.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"金属の腐食を防ぐために防錆処理を施す。","romaji":"Kinzoku no fushoku o fusegu tame ni bōsei shori o hodokosu.","german":"Anti-rust treatment is applied to prevent metal corrosion.","furigana":"きんぞく|ふしょく|ふせ||ほどこ"},{"japanese":"海の近くでは、車の部品が腐食しやすい。","romaji":"Umi no chikaku de wa, kuruma no buhin ga fushoku shiyasui.","german":"Near the sea, car parts corrode easily.","furigana":"うみ|ちか|くるま|ぶひん|ふしょく"},{"japanese":"古い水道管が腐食して、水漏れが起きた。","romaji":"Furui suidōkan ga fushoku shite, mizumore ga okita.","german":"The old water pipes corroded, and a leak occurred.","furigana":"ふる|すいどうかん|ふしょく|みずも|お"}],"notes":"腐食 (older spelling 腐蝕) refers to the chemical decomposition of material, above all the \"corrosion\" of metals: 金属が腐食する (intransitive), less often transitive 酸が金属を腐食する. In everyday speech, for iron one more often says 錆びる (\"to rust\"), for food 腐る (\"to spoil\"). Technical terms include 腐食防止, 耐腐食性 and, in art, 腐食銅版画 (\"etching\"). For erosion by water or wind, 浸食 is used instead.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"水は最も一般的な溶媒として知られている。","romaji":"Mizu wa mottomo ippanteki na yōbai to shite shirarete iru.","german":"Water is known as the most common solvent.","furigana":"みず|もっと|いっぱんてき|ようばい|し"},{"japanese":"塩水では、水が溶媒、塩が溶質である。","romaji":"Shiomizu de wa, mizu ga yōbai, shio ga yōshitsu de aru.","german":"In salt water, water is the solvent and salt is the solute.","furigana":"しおみず|みず|ようばい|しお|ようしつ"},{"japanese":"この物質は水には溶けないが、有機溶媒にはよく溶ける。","romaji":"Kono busshitsu wa mizu ni wa tokenai ga, yūki yōbai ni wa yoku tokeru.","german":"This substance does not dissolve in water, but dissolves well in organic solvents.","furigana":"ぶっしつ|みず|と|ゆうきようばい|と"}],"notes":"溶媒 is the \"solvent\" in which a substance (溶質) is dissolved; together they form the solution (溶液). Typical combinations are 有機溶媒 \"organic solvent\" and 溶媒に溶かす \"to dissolve in a solvent\". The word belongs to the technical language of chemistry; for technical solvents such as thinner in everyday life, one more often says 溶剤 or シンナー.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"溶質の濃度が高いほど浸透圧も高くなる。","romaji":"Yōshitsu no nōdo ga takai hodo shintō atsu mo takaku naru.","german":"The higher the concentration of the solute, the higher the osmotic pressure.","furigana":"ようしつ|のうど|たか|しんとうあつ|たか"},{"japanese":"砂糖水では砂糖が溶質にあたる。","romaji":"Satōmizu de wa satō ga yōshitsu ni ataru.","german":"In sugar water, sugar is the solute.","furigana":"さとうみず|さとう|ようしつ"},{"japanese":"温度が下がると、溶けきれなくなった溶質が結晶として出てくる。","romaji":"Ondo ga sagaru to, tokekirenaku natta yōshitsu ga kesshō to shite dete kuru.","german":"When the temperature drops, the solute that can no longer stay dissolved comes out as crystals.","furigana":"おんど|さ|と|ようしつ|けっしょう|で"}],"notes":"溶質 is the \"solute\" of a solution and the opposite term to 溶媒 \"solvent\". Typical are 溶質の濃度 \"concentration of the solute\" as well as 溶質が溶ける or 溶質が析出する \"the solute precipitates\". It is a purely technical word from chemistry class and practically never occurs in everyday life.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"触媒反応を利用して工業的に重要な物質を製造する。","romaji":"Shokubai hannō o riyō shite kōgyōteki ni jūyō na busshitsu o seizō suru.","german":"Industrially important substances are produced using catalytic reactions.","furigana":"しょくばいはんのう|りよう|こうぎょうてき|じゅうよう|ぶっしつ|せいぞう"},{"japanese":"自動車の排ガスは触媒反応によって無害な物質に変えられる。","romaji":"Jidōsha no haigasu wa shokubai hannō ni yotte mugai na busshitsu ni kaerareru.","german":"Car exhaust gases are converted into harmless substances by catalytic reactions.","furigana":"じどうしゃ|はい|しょくばいはんのう|むがい|ぶっしつ|か"},{"japanese":"体内の多くの触媒反応は酵素によって進められている。","romaji":"Tainai no ōku no shokubai hannō wa kōso ni yotte susumerarete iru.","german":"Many catalytic reactions in the body are driven by enzymes.","furigana":"たいない|おお|しょくばいはんのう|こうそ|すす"}],"notes":"触媒反応 is composed of 触媒 \"catalyst\" and 反応 \"reaction\". Typical verbs are 触媒反応が起こる/進む and 触媒反応を利用する; no する-verb is formed from it. While 触媒 alone is also used figuratively as a \"catalyst for change\", 触媒反応 remains a technical term of chemistry and biochemistry (enzymes are 生体触媒).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"電解によって水を水素と酸素に分解できる。","romaji":"Denkai ni yotte mizu o suiso to sanso ni bunkai dekiru.","german":"Water can be decomposed into hydrogen and oxygen by electrolysis.","furigana":"でんかい|みず|すいそ|さんそ|ぶんかい"},{"japanese":"スポーツドリンクには電解質が含まれている。","romaji":"Supōtsu dorinku ni wa denkaishitsu ga fukumarete iru.","german":"Sports drinks contain electrolytes.","furigana":"でんかいしつ|ふく"},{"japanese":"この工場では食塩水を電解して塩素を作っている。","romaji":"Kono kōjō de wa shokuensui o denkai shite enso o tsukutte iru.","german":"In this factory, chlorine is produced by electrolyzing salt water.","furigana":"こうじょう|しょくえんすい|でんかい|えんそ|つく"}],"notes":"電解 is the short form of 電気分解 \"electrolysis\"; in school lessons and everyday speech, the full 電気分解 is mostly used. The short form lives on mainly in compounds: 電解質 \"electrolyte\" (also on sports drinks), 電解液 \"electrolyte solution\", 電解コンデンサー. As a transitive する-verb: 水を電解する \"to electrolyze water\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"酸は水中で電離して水素イオンを放出する。","romaji":"San wa suichū de denri shite suiso ion o hōshutsu suru.","german":"Acids ionize in water and release hydrogen ions.","furigana":"さん|すいちゅう|でんり|すいそ|ほうしゅつ"},{"japanese":"強い酸ほど水溶液の中でよく電離する。","romaji":"Tsuyoi san hodo suiyōeki no naka de yoku denri suru.","german":"The stronger an acid is, the more it ionizes in aqueous solution.","furigana":"つよ|さん|すいようえき|なか|でんり"},{"japanese":"電離層は短波の電波を反射する。","romaji":"Denrisō wa tanpa no denpa o hansha suru.","german":"The ionosphere reflects shortwave radio waves.","furigana":"でんりそう|たんぱ|でんぱ|はんしゃ"}],"notes":"電離 refers to a substance breaking up into ions, especially electrolytic dissociation in solution; as an intransitive する-verb: 塩化ナトリウムが水中で電離する. Besides the more general loanword イオン化, 電離 is the classic technical term. Important compounds are 電離度 \"degree of dissociation\", 電離層 \"ionosphere\" and 電離放射線 \"ionizing radiation\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"酸と塩基が反応して中和が起こると塩と水が生成される。","romaji":"San to enki ga hannō shite chūwa ga okoru to en to mizu ga seisei sareru.","german":"When an acid and a base react and neutralization occurs, salt and water are produced.","furigana":"さん|えんき|はんのう|ちゅうわ|お|えん|みず|せいせい"},{"japanese":"制酸剤は胃酸を中和して胸やけを抑える。","romaji":"Seisanzai wa isan o chūwa shite muneyake o osaeru.","german":"Antacids neutralize stomach acid and relieve heartburn.","furigana":"せいさんざい|いさん|ちゅうわ|むね|おさ"},{"japanese":"畑の酸性の土を石灰で中和する。","romaji":"Hatake no sansei no tsuchi o sekkai de chūwa suru.","german":"Acidic soil in the field is neutralized with lime.","furigana":"はたけ|さんせい|つち|せっかい|ちゅうわ"}],"notes":"中和 is the \"neutralization\" of acid and base; the result is salt and water. As a する-verb it is intransitive (酸とアルカリが中和する) and often also transitive (胃酸を中和する \"to neutralize stomach acid\"). Figuratively it means \"to render harmless, to balance out\", as in 毒を中和する or 中和剤 \"neutralizing agent\". In everyday life one encounters the word with medicines, garden lime or cleaning agents.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ヒトの細胞には四十六本の染色体がある。","romaji":"Hito no saibō ni wa yonjū roku hon no senshokutai ga aru.","german":"Human cells have forty-six chromosomes.","furigana":"さいぼう|よんじゅうろくほん|せんしょくたい"},{"japanese":"性別はX染色体とY染色体の組み合わせで決まる。","romaji":"Seibetsu wa ekkusu senshokutai to wai senshokutai no kumiawase de kimaru.","german":"Sex is determined by the combination of the X and Y chromosomes.","furigana":"せいべつ|せんしょくたい|せんしょくたい|く|あ|き"},{"japanese":"ダウン症は二十一番染色体が一本多いことで起こる。","romaji":"Daunshō wa nijūichi-ban senshokutai ga ippon ōi koto de okoru.","german":"Down syndrome occurs when there is one extra copy of chromosome 21.","furigana":"しょう|にじゅういちばんせんしょくたい|いっぽんおお|お"}],"notes":"染色体 literally means \"stainable body\" and is the \"chromosome\". It is counted with 本 (四十六本) or in pairs with 対 (二十三対). Common compounds are 性染色体 \"sex chromosome\", X染色体 and 染色体異常 \"chromosomal abnormality\". Not to be confused with 遺伝子 \"gene\", which is a section on the chromosome.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"突然変異は進化の原動力の一つである。","romaji":"Totsuzen hen'i wa shinka no gendōryoku no hitotsu de aru.","german":"Mutation is one of the driving forces of evolution.","furigana":"とつぜんへんい|しんか|げんどうりょく|ひと"},{"japanese":"ウイルスは突然変異を繰り返して性質が変わっていく。","romaji":"Uirusu wa totsuzen hen'i o kurikaeshite seishitsu ga kawatte iku.","german":"Viruses mutate repeatedly, and their properties change.","furigana":"とつぜんへんい|く|かえ|せいしつ|か"},{"japanese":"両親とも運動が苦手なのに、息子は突然変異のように足が速い。","romaji":"Ryōshin tomo undō ga nigate na no ni, musuko wa totsuzen hen'i no yō ni ashi ga hayai.","german":"Both his parents are bad at sports, yet their son is a fast runner, like a mutation.","furigana":"りょうしん|うんどう|にがて|むすこ|とつぜんへんい|あし|はや"}],"notes":"突然変異 literally means \"sudden change\" and is the biological term for \"mutation\"; as an intransitive する-verb: 遺伝子が突然変異する. In technical language, for example about viruses, plain 変異 is often used (変異株 \"virus variant\"). Colloquially it is used jokingly for someone who totally \"takes after no one\": 突然変異みたいな天才.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"個体群の密度は環境要因によって変動する。","romaji":"Kotaigun no mitsudo wa kankyō yōin ni yotte hendō suru.","german":"The population density varies depending on environmental factors.","furigana":"こたいぐん|みつど|かんきょうよういん|へんどう"},{"japanese":"この島のシカの個体群は、天敵がいないため急速に増えた。","romaji":"Kono shima no shika no kotaigun wa, tenteki ga inai tame kyūsoku ni fueta.","german":"The deer population on this island grew rapidly because there are no natural predators.","furigana":"しま|こたいぐん|てんてき|きゅうそく|ふ"},{"japanese":"道路で分断された森では、カエルの個体群が孤立してしまう。","romaji":"Dōro de bundan sareta mori de wa, kaeru no kotaigun ga koritsu shite shimau.","german":"In forests divided by roads, frog populations become isolated.","furigana":"どうろ|ぶんだん|もり|こたいぐん|こりつ"}],"notes":"個体群 is the ecological technical term for \"population\": all individuals (個体) of a species in a certain area. Typical are 個体群の密度 and 個体群が増える/減る. In everyday speech one more often says 個体数 \"number of individuals\" or 群れ \"herd, flock\". For the human population, 人口 is used, not 個体群.","levelNote":"Compound: 個体 (N2) + 群 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"生物は環境に適応することで生存競争を勝ち抜く。","romaji":"Seibutsu wa kankyō ni tekiō suru koto de seizon kyōsō o kachinuku.","german":"Living things win the struggle for survival by adapting to their environment.","furigana":"せいぶつ|かんきょう|てきおう|せいぞんきょうそう|か|ぬ"},{"japanese":"留学して半年、ようやく現地の生活に適応できた。","romaji":"Ryūgaku shite hantoshi, yōyaku genchi no seikatsu ni tekiō dekita.","german":"Six months after starting to study abroad, I was finally able to adapt to life there.","furigana":"りゅうがく|はんとし|げんち|せいかつ|てきおう"},{"japanese":"子どもは大人より新しい環境への適応が早い。","romaji":"Kodomo wa otona yori atarashii kankyō e no tekiō ga hayai.","german":"Children adapt to a new environment faster than adults.","furigana":"こ|おとな|あたら|かんきょう|てきおう|はや"}],"notes":"適応 is \"adaptation\" to the environment or circumstances; as an intransitive する-verb with に: 環境に適応する, 新しい職場に適応する. Common compounds are 適応力 \"adaptability\" and 適応障害 \"adjustment disorder\". 順応 is very similar, but emphasizes gradual getting used to something (目が暗さに順応する). Not to be confused with 適用 \"to apply (a rule)\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"実験では複数の仮定を設けて検証を行う。","romaji":"Jikken de wa fukusū no katei o mōkete kenshō o okonau.","german":"In experiments, several assumptions are set up and tested.","furigana":"じっけん|ふくすう|かてい|もう|けんしょう|おこな"},{"japanese":"仮定の話には答えられません。","romaji":"Katei no hanashi ni wa kotaeraremasen.","german":"I can't answer hypothetical questions.","furigana":"かてい|はなし|こた"},{"japanese":"一日に百人の客が来ると仮定して、売り上げを計算してみよう。","romaji":"Ichinichi ni hyakunin no kyaku ga kuru to katei shite, uriage o keisan shite miyō.","german":"Let's assume a hundred customers come per day and calculate the sales.","furigana":"いちにち|ひゃくにん|きゃく|く|かてい|う|あ|けいさん"}],"notes":"仮定 is an \"assumption\" that one provisionally treats as given; as a transitive する-verb mostly with と: 〜と仮定する \"assuming that ...\". Typical are 仮定に基づく, 仮定を設ける and 仮定の話 \"a hypothetical question\". 仮説, by contrast, is a scientific hypothesis meant to be tested, and 前提 the \"premise\" on which something is built. In grammar, the conditional form is called 仮定形.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"生物は体内の恒常性を維持する仕組みを持つ。","romaji":"Seibutsu wa tainai no kōjōsei o iji suru shikumi o motsu.","german":"Living things have mechanisms to maintain internal homeostasis.","furigana":"せいぶつ|たいない|こうじょうせい|いじ|しく|も"},{"japanese":"この部署では人手不足が恒常的に続いている。","romaji":"Kono busho de wa hitode busoku ga kōjōteki ni tsuzuite iru.","german":"In this department, a shortage of staff continues on a constant basis.","furigana":"ぶしょ|ひとでぶそく|こうじょうてき|つづ"},{"japanese":"残業が恒常化しているのは問題だ。","romaji":"Zangyō ga kōjōka shite iru no wa mondai da.","german":"It is a problem that overtime has become the norm.","furigana":"ざんぎょう|こうじょうか|もんだい"}],"notes":"恒常 means \"constancy, steadiness\" and almost never stands alone. As an adjective one uses 恒常的な/恒常的に \"constant, permanent\"; in addition there are 恒常性 \"homeostasis\" and 恒常化する \"to become the norm\". The word is written and technical; in everyday speech one says いつも, 常に or ずっと.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この理論の正当性を立証するにはさらなる実験が必要だ。","romaji":"Kono riron no seitōsei o risshō suru ni wa saranaru jikken ga hitsuyō da.","german":"Further experiments are needed to prove the validity of this theory.","furigana":"りろん|せいとうせい|りっしょう|じっけん|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"検察は被告の犯行を立証できなかった。","romaji":"Kensatsu wa hikoku no hankō o risshō dekinakatta.","german":"The prosecution was unable to prove the defendant's crime.","furigana":"けんさつ|ひこく|はんこう|りっしょう"},{"japanese":"工場の排水と健康被害の因果関係を立証するのは難しい。","romaji":"Kōjō no haisui to kenkō higai no inga kankei o risshō suru no wa muzukashii.","german":"It is difficult to prove a causal relationship between factory wastewater and health damage.","furigana":"こうじょう|はいすい|けんこうひがい|いんがかんけい|りっしょう|むずか"}],"notes":"立証 is the \"proof\" of a fact with evidence; as a transitive する-verb: 無罪を立証する, 因果関係を立証する. It is especially common in law (立証責任 \"burden of proof\"). 証明 is more general and usual for mathematical proofs, 実証 emphasizes empirical confirmation through experiments, 論証 logical argumentation.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"実験結果に基づいて詳細な考察を行った。","romaji":"Jikken kekka ni motozuite shōsai na kōsatsu o okonatta.","german":"A detailed discussion was carried out based on the experimental results.","furigana":"じっけんけっか|もと|しょうさい|こうさつ|おこな"},{"japanese":"レポートは「目的・方法・結果・考察」の順に書きなさい。","romaji":"Repōto wa \"mokuteki, hōhō, kekka, kōsatsu\" no jun ni kakinasai.","german":"Write the report in the order of purpose, method, results and discussion.","furigana":"もくてき|ほうほう|けっか|こうさつ|じゅん|か"},{"japanese":"本論文では、少子化の原因を経済的な側面から考察する。","romaji":"Honronbun de wa, shōshika no gen'in o keizaiteki na sokumen kara kōsatsu suru.","german":"This paper examines the causes of the declining birthrate from an economic perspective.","furigana":"ほんろんぶん|しょうしか|げんいん|けいざいてき|そくめん|こうさつ"}],"notes":"考察 is the thorough thinking through and investigation of a topic; as a transitive する-verb: 原因を考察する. Typical are 考察を加える and 考察を深める. In lab reports and theses, the section following the results, the \"discussion\", is called 考察. 検討, by contrast, is examining and weighing with a decision in view, and 観察 is mere \"observation\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"厳密な論証によってこの定理が成り立つことを示した。","romaji":"Genmitsu na ronshō ni yotte kono teiri ga naritatsu koto o shimeshita.","german":"It was shown through rigorous argumentation that this theorem holds.","furigana":"げんみつ|ろんしょう|ていり|な|た|しめ"},{"japanese":"彼の主張は面白いが、論証が不十分だ。","romaji":"Kare no shuchō wa omoshiroi ga, ronshō ga fujūbun da.","german":"His claim is interesting, but the argumentation is insufficient.","furigana":"かれ|しゅちょう|おもしろ|ろんしょう|ふじゅうぶん"},{"japanese":"筆者は歴史資料をもとに、その説の誤りを論証している。","romaji":"Hissha wa rekishi shiryō o moto ni, sono setsu no ayamari o ronshō shite iru.","german":"Based on historical sources, the author demonstrates that the theory is mistaken.","furigana":"ひっしゃ|れきししりょう|せつ|あやま|ろんしょう"}],"notes":"論証 is logical \"demonstration\" through arguments; as a transitive する-verb: 〜を論証する, 〜ことを論証する. Typical are 論証が不十分だ and 厳密な論証. 立証 emphasizes proof with evidence (e.g. in court), 実証 confirmation by experiments; for mathematical proofs, 証明 is the usual word. The word is academic and written.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"天体観測によって新しい小惑星が発見された。","romaji":"Tentai kansoku ni yotte atarashii shōwakusei ga hakken sareta.","german":"A new asteroid was discovered through astronomical observation.","furigana":"てんたいかんそく|あたら|しょうわくせい|はっけん"},{"japanese":"今日、東京で観測史上最高の気温を記録した。","romaji":"Kyō, Tōkyō de kansoku shijō saikō no kion o kiroku shita.","german":"Today Tokyo recorded its highest temperature since records began.","furigana":"きょう|とうきょう|かんそくしじょうさいこう|きおん|きろく"},{"japanese":"すぐに景気が回復するというのは希望的観測にすぎない。","romaji":"Sugu ni keiki ga kaifuku suru to iu no wa kibōteki kansoku ni suginai.","german":"The notion that the economy will recover soon is nothing but wishful thinking.","furigana":"けいき|かいふく|きぼうてきかんそく"}],"notes":"観測 is measuring observation of natural phenomena, mostly with instruments: 天体観測, 気象観測, 地震を観測する. Frequent in weather reports: 観測史上最高 \"highest value since records began\". 観察, by contrast, is close looking, such as at plants in class. Figuratively, 観測 means \"assessment, forecast\", especially in 希望的観測 \"wishful thinking\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"統計学では標本から母集団の特性を推定する。","romaji":"Tōkeigaku de wa hyōhon kara boshūdan no tokusei o suitei suru.","german":"In statistics, the characteristics of the population are estimated from a sample.","furigana":"とうけいがく|ひょうほん|ぼしゅうだん|とくせい|すいてい"},{"japanese":"夏休みの宿題で昆虫の標本を作った。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi no shukudai de konchū no hyōhon o tsukutta.","german":"For my summer vacation homework, I made an insect specimen collection.","furigana":"なつやす|しゅくだい|こんちゅう|ひょうほん|つく"},{"japanese":"博物館には恐竜の骨格標本が展示されている。","romaji":"Hakubutsukan ni wa kyōryū no kokkaku hyōhon ga tenji sarete iru.","german":"A dinosaur skeleton specimen is on display at the museum.","furigana":"はくぶつかん|きょうりゅう|こっかくひょうほん|てんじ"}],"notes":"標本 is a preserved \"specimen\" for collections and teaching (昆虫標本, 骨格標本) and, in statistics, the \"sample\" (標本調査). Figuratively it denotes a typical model example: 彼は真面目人間の標本だ. サンプル is the more general loanword, also for product samples, and 検体 the medical test sample.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"化学分析には高純度の試薬を使用する必要がある。","romaji":"Kagaku bunseki ni wa kōjundo no shiyaku o shiyō suru hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"Chemical analysis requires the use of high-purity reagents.","furigana":"かがくぶんせき|こうじゅんど|しやく|しよう|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"試薬を加えると、溶液の色が青く変わった。","romaji":"Shiyaku o kuwaeru to, yōeki no iro ga aoku kawatta.","german":"When the reagent was added, the color of the solution turned blue.","furigana":"しやく|くわ|ようえき|いろ|あお|か"},{"japanese":"使い終わった試薬は流しに捨てずに、決められた容器に入れてください。","romaji":"Tsukaiowatta shiyaku wa nagashi ni sutezu ni, kimerareta yōki ni irete kudasai.","german":"Please do not pour used reagents down the sink, but put them in the designated containers.","furigana":"つか|お|しやく|なが|す|き|ようき|い"}],"notes":"試薬 is a \"reagent\", that is, a chemical with which one detects or tests something. Typical are 試薬を加える, 試薬瓶 and 検査試薬 (e.g. in rapid tests). 薬品 is the general expression for \"chemicals\", while 薬 usually refers to medicines. The word belongs to lab and technical language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"細胞培養によって組織の再生を研究している。","romaji":"Saibō baiyō ni yotte soshiki no saisei o kenkyū shite iru.","german":"We are studying tissue regeneration through cell culture.","furigana":"さいぼうばいよう|そしき|さいせい|けんきゅう"},{"japanese":"患者から採った細菌を培養して原因を調べる。","romaji":"Kanja kara totta saikin o baiyō shite gen'in o shiraberu.","german":"Bacteria taken from the patient are cultured to find the cause.","furigana":"かんじゃ|と|さいきん|ばいよう|げんいん|しら"},{"japanese":"培養肉がスーパーに並ぶ日も近いかもしれない。","romaji":"Baiyōniku ga sūpā ni narabu hi mo chikai kamo shirenai.","german":"The day when cultured meat lines supermarket shelves may be near.","furigana":"ばいようにく|なら|ひ|ちか"}],"notes":"培養 is the cultivation of microorganisms, cells or tissue under controlled conditions; as a transitive する-verb: 細菌を培養する. Typical compounds are 細胞培養, 培養液 \"culture medium\" and 培養肉 \"lab-grown meat\". For growing plants one usually says 栽培, for fish farming 養殖; in the figurative sense of \"fostering abilities\", 培養 is rare, where 養う or 育む are more usual.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コンピューター上の仮想実験で理論の妥当性を検討した。","romaji":"Konpyūtā-jō no kasō jikken de riron no datōsei o kentō shita.","german":"We examined the validity of the theory with a virtual experiment on a computer.","furigana":"じょう|かそうじっけん|りろん|だとうせい|けんとう"},{"japanese":"仮想通貨の価格は一日で大きく変動することがある。","romaji":"Kasō tsūka no kakaku wa ichinichi de ōkiku hendō suru koto ga aru.","german":"The price of cryptocurrencies can fluctuate greatly in a single day.","furigana":"かそうつうか|かかく|いちにち|おお|へんどう"},{"japanese":"ゴーグルをつけると、仮想空間の中を自由に歩き回れる。","romaji":"Gōguru o tsukeru to, kasō kūkan no naka o jiyū ni arukimawareru.","german":"With the goggles on, you can walk around freely in virtual space.","furigana":"かそうくうかん|なか|じゆう|ある|まわ"}],"notes":"仮想 means \"imagined, assumed\" and today mainly \"virtual\". It mostly stands as the first element of compounds: 仮想現実 \"virtual reality\", 仮想空間, 仮想通貨 \"cryptocurrency\", 仮想敵国 \"hypothetical enemy state\". Attributively one uses 仮想の or 仮想的な; the する-verb 仮想する \"to imagine, to suppose\" is rare. 仮定, by contrast, is an assumption in an argument, 空想 a fantasy or daydream.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"検体を採取して成分を分析する。","romaji":"Kentai o saishu shite seibun o bunseki suru.","german":"Collect a specimen and analyze its components.","furigana":"けんたい|さいしゅ|せいぶん|ぶんせき"},{"japanese":"PCR検査の検体として、鼻の奥の粘液を採取します。","romaji":"PCR kensa no kentai to shite, hana no oku no nen'eki o saishu shimasu.","german":"As the specimen for the PCR test, mucus from the back of the nose is collected.","furigana":"けんさ|けんたい|はな|おく|ねんえき|さいしゅ"},{"japanese":"検体の取り違えによって、誤った診断が下された。","romaji":"Kentai no torichigae ni yotte, ayamatta shindan ga kudasareta.","german":"A wrong diagnosis was made because of a mix-up of specimens.","furigana":"けんたい|と|ちが|あやま|しんだん|くだ"}],"notes":"検体 is the \"specimen\" in medicine and the lab, e.g. blood, urine or a swab: 検体を採取する, 検体を提出する, 検体検査. 標本, by contrast, is a preserved preparation, and サンプル is the general word for \"sample\". Careful when listening: 献体 (same reading) is the \"body donation\" for medical education.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"測定には必ず誤差が伴うため統計的処理が必要だ。","romaji":"Sokutei ni wa kanarazu gosa ga tomonau tame tōkeiteki shori ga hitsuyō da.","german":"Measurements always involve error, so statistical processing is necessary.","furigana":"そくてい|かなら|ごさ|ともな|とうけいてきしょり|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"千円くらいの違いなら誤差の範囲だよ。","romaji":"Sen'en kurai no chigai nara gosa no han'i da yo.","german":"A difference of about a thousand yen is within the margin of error.","furigana":"せんえん|ちが|ごさ|はんい"},{"japanese":"この時計は一か月に数秒の誤差しか出ない。","romaji":"Kono tokei wa ikkagetsu ni sūbyō no gosa shika denai.","german":"This watch deviates by only a few seconds a month.","furigana":"とけい|いっ|げつ|すうびょう|ごさ|で"}],"notes":"誤差 is the unavoidable \"deviation\" between a measured or calculated value and the true value, not an \"error\" in the sense of a mistake (for that: 間違い, ミス). Typical are 誤差が出る/生じる, 誤差を小さくする and 誤差の範囲内 \"within the margin of error\". Colloquially, one says 「そのくらいは誤差だ」 for \"that is negligible\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"標準偏差はデータのばらつきを表す指標である。","romaji":"Hyōjun hensa wa dēta no baratsuki o arawasu shihyō de aru.","german":"Standard deviation is an indicator of the dispersion of data.","furigana":"ひょうじゅんへんさ|あらわ|しひょう"},{"japanese":"この高校は偏差値が高いことで有名だ。","romaji":"Kono kōkō wa hensachi ga takai koto de yūmei da.","german":"This high school is famous for its high academic standing.","furigana":"こうこう|へんさち|たか|ゆうめい"},{"japanese":"平均からの偏差を二乗して足し合わせる。","romaji":"Heikin kara no hensa o nijō shite tashiawaseru.","german":"Square the deviations from the mean and add them up.","furigana":"へいきん|へんさ|にじょう|た|あ"}],"notes":"偏差 is the \"deviation\" of a value from the mean or target value. Best known are 標準偏差 \"standard deviation\" and 偏差値, the standardized score by which schools and universities in Japan are ranked (偏差値が高い). Unlike 誤差 (measurement inaccuracy), 偏差 describes a systematic difference or dispersion; colloquially one says ずれ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"回帰分析を用いてデータの傾向を予測する。","romaji":"Kaiki bunseki o mochiite dēta no keikō o yosoku suru.","german":"Use regression analysis to predict data trends.","furigana":"かいきぶんせき|もち|けいこう|よそく"},{"japanese":"最近の若者の間では、昭和のレトロな文化への回帰が見られる。","romaji":"Saikin no wakamono no aida de wa, Shōwa no retoro na bunka e no kaiki ga mirareru.","german":"Among young people recently, a return to the retro culture of the Showa era can be seen.","furigana":"さいきん|わかもの|あいだ|しょうわ|ぶんか|かいき|み"},{"japanese":"サケは生まれた川に回帰して産卵する。","romaji":"Sake wa umareta kawa ni kaiki shite sanran suru.","german":"Salmon return to the river where they were born to spawn.","furigana":"う|かわ|かいき|さんらん"}],"notes":"回帰 first means \"return, recurrence\" to a starting point; as an intransitive する-verb with に: 原点に回帰する \"to return to one's roots\", サケが生まれた川に回帰する. In statistics it is \"regression\" (回帰分析, 回帰直線), and in geography it is part of 北回帰線 \"Tropic of Cancer\". 復帰, by contrast, is a return to a former place, such as 職場復帰.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"測定値に温度補正を加えて正確な結果を得る。","romaji":"Sokuteichi ni ondo hosei o kuwaete seikaku na kekka o eru.","german":"Apply temperature correction to the measured values to obtain accurate results.","furigana":"そくていち|おんどほせい|くわ|せいかく|けっか|え"},{"japanese":"政府は景気対策のために補正予算を組んだ。","romaji":"Seifu wa keiki taisaku no tame ni hosei yosan o kunda.","german":"The government drew up a supplementary budget for economic stimulus.","furigana":"せいふ|けいきたいさく|ほせいよさん|く"},{"japanese":"この写真は明るさを少し補正したほうがいい。","romaji":"Kono shashin wa akarusa o sukoshi hosei shita hō ga ii.","german":"You should adjust the brightness of this photo a little.","furigana":"しゃしん|あか|すこ|ほせい"}],"notes":"補正 is the subsequent correcting or adjusting of a value so that it is right; as a transitive する-verb: データを補正する, 写真の色を補正する. Very common is 補正予算 \"supplementary budget\"; in fashion there is 体型補正 \"figure-shaping\". 修正 and 訂正 correct mistakes (訂正 mainly in texts), 較正 is the calibration of a measuring instrument.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"理論値と実測値の乖離が大きい場合は仮説を見直す。","romaji":"Riron chi to jissoku chi no kairi ga ōkii baai wa kasetsu o minaosu.","german":"If the discrepancy between the theoretical and measured values is large, the hypothesis is reviewed.","furigana":"りろんち|じっそくち|かいり|おお|ばあい|かせつ|みなお"},{"japanese":"政治家の発言は国民の感覚から乖離している。","romaji":"Seijika no hatsugen wa kokumin no kankaku kara kairi shite iru.","german":"The politicians' statements are out of touch with the public's sentiment.","furigana":"せいじか|はつげん|こくみん|かんかく|かいり"},{"japanese":"理想と現実の乖離に悩む新入社員は多い。","romaji":"Risō to genjitsu no kairi ni nayamu shinnyū shain wa ōi.","german":"Many new employees are troubled by the gap between ideal and reality.","furigana":"りそう|げんじつ|かいり|なや|しんにゅうしゃいん|おお"}],"notes":"乖離 refers to the gaping apart of two things that should actually agree; as an intransitive する-verb: AとBが乖離する, 現実から乖離する. Typical are 理想と現実の乖離 and 民意との乖離. The word is written; in everyday speech one says ずれ, 差 or 隔たり. Since 乖 is not a Jōyō kanji, newspapers sometimes write かい離.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"無限級数が収束するかどうかを判定する。","romaji":"Mugen kyūsū ga shūsoku suru ka dō ka o hantei suru.","german":"Determine whether the infinite series converges.","furigana":"むげんきゅうすう|しゅうそく|はんてい"},{"japanese":"騒動はようやく収束に向かっている。","romaji":"Sōdō wa yōyaku shūsoku ni mukatte iru.","german":"The disturbance is finally subsiding.","furigana":"そうどう|しゅうそく|む"},{"japanese":"政府は事態の早期収束を目指している。","romaji":"Seifu wa jitai no sōki shūsoku o mezashite iru.","german":"The government is aiming to bring the situation under control early.","furigana":"せいふ|じたい|そうきしゅうそく|めざ"}],"notes":"収束 is \"convergence\" in mathematics (級数が収束する) and the focusing of light in optics. In the news it usually means that a crisis or unrest \"subsides, comes under control\": 事態が収束する, 混乱を収束させる, 収束に向かう. Do not confuse it with the identically read 終息 \"complete end\" (e.g. of an epidemic). The opposite term in mathematics is 発散.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この級数は発散するため和を定義できない。","romaji":"Kono kyūsū wa hassan suru tame wa o teigi dekinai.","german":"Because this series diverges, its sum cannot be defined.","furigana":"きゅうすう|はっさん|わ|ていぎ"},{"japanese":"カラオケで歌ってストレスを発散した。","romaji":"Karaoke de utatte sutoresu o hassan shita.","german":"I relieved my stress by singing at karaoke.","furigana":"うた|はっさん"},{"japanese":"運動すると体から熱が発散される。","romaji":"Undō suru to karada kara netsu ga hassan sareru.","german":"When you exercise, the body gives off heat.","furigana":"うんどう|からだ|ねつ|はっさん"}],"notes":"発散 means \"to give off outward, to emit\": 熱を発散する, においが発散する. In everyday life it is known mainly from ストレスを発散する and ストレス発散 \"stress relief\", that is, letting out pent-up feelings. In mathematics it is the \"divergence\" of a series, the opposite term to 収束. It is used both transitively and intransitively.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"還流冷却器を使って溶媒の蒸発を防ぐ。","romaji":"Kanryū reikyakuki o tsukatte yōbai no jōhatsu o fusegu.","german":"A reflux condenser is used to prevent evaporation of the solvent.","furigana":"かんりゅうれいきゃくき|つか|ようばい|じょうはつ|ふせ"},{"japanese":"海外子会社の利益が国内に還流すれば、景気にもよい影響がある。","romaji":"Kaigai kogaisha no rieki ga kokunai ni kanryū sureba, keiki ni mo yoi eikyō ga aru.","german":"If the profits of overseas subsidiaries flow back into the country, it will also have a positive effect on the economy.","furigana":"かいがいこがいしゃ|りえき|こくない|かんりゅう|けいき|えいきょう"},{"japanese":"心臓に戻る血液の還流が悪くなると、足がむくみやすい。","romaji":"Shinzō ni modoru ketsueki no kanryū ga waruku naru to, ashi ga mukumiyasui.","german":"When the return flow of blood to the heart worsens, the legs swell easily.","furigana":"しんぞう|もど|けつえき|かんりゅう|わる|あし"}],"notes":"還流 is \"flowing back\" to the starting point; as an intransitive する-verb: 〜に還流する. In the lab, 還流冷却器 \"reflux condenser\" is known; in business 資金の還流 \"return flow of capital\"; in medicine 静脈還流 \"venous return\". 逆流, by contrast, is flowing in the wrong direction, and the identically read 環流 denotes a circulating current.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"液体の表面から分子が蒸発する現象を研究した。","romaji":"Ekitai no hyōmen kara bunshi ga jōhatsu suru genshō o kenkyū shita.","german":"I studied the phenomenon of molecules evaporating from the surface of a liquid.","furigana":"えきたい|ひょうめん|ぶんし|じょうはつ|げんしょう|けんきゅう"},{"japanese":"夏は水たまりがすぐ蒸発してしまう。","romaji":"Natsu wa mizutamari ga sugu jōhatsu shite shimau.","german":"In summer, puddles evaporate quickly.","furigana":"なつ|みず|じょうはつ"},{"japanese":"父は借金を残して蒸発した。","romaji":"Chichi wa shakkin o nokoshite jōhatsu shita.","german":"My father left behind debts and vanished without a trace.","furigana":"ちち|しゃっきん|のこ|じょうはつ"}],"notes":"蒸発 is the \"evaporation\" or \"vaporization\" of a liquid; as an intransitive する-verb: 水が蒸発する, transitive with 蒸発させる. 沸騰, by contrast, is \"boiling\", 気化 the general technical term for the transition to gas. Colloquially, 蒸発 also means that a person \"disappears without a trace\", for example to escape debts or family.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ドライアイスは昇華して直接気体になる。","romaji":"Dorai aisu wa shōka shite chokusetsu kitai ni naru.","german":"Dry ice sublimates and turns directly into gas.","furigana":"しょうか|ちょくせつきたい"},{"japanese":"冷凍庫に長く入れておいた氷が小さくなるのは、昇華が起きているからだ。","romaji":"Reitōko ni nagaku irete oita kōri ga chiisaku naru no wa, shōka ga okite iru kara da.","german":"The reason ice left in the freezer for a long time gets smaller is that sublimation is occurring.","furigana":"れいとうこ|なが|い|こおり|ちい|しょうか|お"},{"japanese":"彼は失恋の痛みを名曲に昇華させた。","romaji":"Kare wa shitsuren no itami o meikyoku ni shōka saseta.","german":"He transformed the pain of his heartbreak into a masterpiece of a song.","furigana":"かれ|しつれん|いた|めいきょく|しょうか"}],"notes":"昇華 is \"sublimation\" in chemistry, the direct transition from solid to gas: ドライアイスが昇華する. In psychology it means the sublimation of drives, and figuratively the raising of something to a higher level, mostly in the pattern AをBに昇華させる: 悲しみを芸術に昇華させる. This figurative use is elevated written language and common in reviews and interviews.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"水蒸気が冷やされると凝縮して水滴になる。","romaji":"Suijōki ga hiyasareru to gyōshuku shite suiteki ni naru.","german":"When water vapor is cooled, it condenses into water droplets.","furigana":"すいじょうき|ひ|ぎょうしゅく|すいてき"},{"japanese":"この映画には監督の思いが二時間に凝縮されている。","romaji":"Kono eiga ni wa kantoku no omoi ga nijikan ni gyōshuku sarete iru.","german":"The director's thoughts are condensed into two hours in this film.","furigana":"えいが|かんとく|おも|にじかん|ぎょうしゅく"},{"japanese":"干したトマトはうまみが凝縮されていて味が濃い。","romaji":"Hoshita tomato wa umami ga gyōshuku sarete ite aji ga koi.","german":"Dried tomatoes have a concentrated umami and a rich flavor.","furigana":"ほ|ぎょうしゅく|あじ|こ"}],"notes":"凝縮 is physically the \"condensation\" of gas into liquid: 水蒸気が凝縮する. The fogging up of windows, however, is called 結露 in everyday speech. Very common is the figurative sense \"to condense, to concentrate\", mostly in the passive: うまみが凝縮されている, 魅力が凝縮された一冊. It is used both transitively and intransitively.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"活性炭は有害物質を吸着する能力がある。","romaji":"Kasseitan wa yūgai busshitsu o kyūchaku suru nōryoku ga aru.","german":"Activated carbon has the ability to adsorb harmful substances.","furigana":"かっせいたん|ゆうがいぶっしつ|きゅうちゃく|のうりょく"},{"japanese":"冷蔵庫に炭を入れておくと、においを吸着してくれる。","romaji":"Reizōko ni sumi o irete oku to, nioi o kyūchaku shite kureru.","german":"If you put charcoal in the refrigerator, it absorbs odors.","furigana":"れいぞうこ|すみ|い|きゅうちゃく"},{"japanese":"タコは吸盤で岩にしっかり吸着する。","romaji":"Tako wa kyūban de iwa ni shikkari kyūchaku suru.","german":"An octopus clings firmly to rocks with its suckers.","furigana":"きゅうばん|いわ|きゅうちゃく"}],"notes":"吸着 is \"adsorption\" in chemistry: substances accumulate on a surface, e.g. with 活性炭 or 吸着剤. In everyday speech it also means \"to cling by suction, to adhere\" (吸盤で吸着する). Although JMdict lists it as intransitive, 〜を吸着する (\"to adsorb something\") is very common. 吸収, by contrast, is \"absorption\", taking up into the interior.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"X線は物質を透過する性質を持つ。","romaji":"Ekkusu sen wa busshitsu o tōka suru seishitsu o motsu.","german":"X-rays have the property of penetrating matter.","furigana":"せん|ぶっしつ|とうか|せいしつ|も"},{"japanese":"このカーテンは光をほとんど透過しない。","romaji":"Kono kāten wa hikari o hotondo tōka shinai.","german":"These curtains let almost no light through.","furigana":"ひかり|とうか"},{"japanese":"背景を透過させた画像をロゴに使う。","romaji":"Haikei o tōka saseta gazō o rogo ni tsukau.","german":"I use an image with a transparent background for the logo.","furigana":"はいけい|とうか|がぞう|つか"}],"notes":"透過 is the \"penetration\" or \"letting through\" of light, radiation or substances: 光を透過する, X線が透過する. Important compounds are 透過率 \"transmittance\" and 透過性. 透明, by contrast, describes the property \"transparent\", and 浸透 the slow penetration of liquid. In image editing, 透過 means \"transparent background\" (透過PNG).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"空が青いのは大気中での光の散乱による。","romaji":"Sora ga aoi no wa taiki chū de no hikari no sanran ni yoru.","german":"The sky is blue because of the scattering of light in the atmosphere.","furigana":"そら|あお|たいきちゅう|ひかり|さんらん"},{"japanese":"事故現場には車の部品が散乱していた。","romaji":"Jiko genba ni wa kuruma no buhin ga sanran shite ita.","german":"Car parts were scattered all over the accident site.","furigana":"じこげんば|くるま|ぶひん|さんらん"},{"japanese":"強風で公園にごみが散乱している。","romaji":"Kyōfū de kōen ni gomi ga sanran shite iru.","german":"Strong winds have scattered trash all over the park.","furigana":"きょうふう|こうえん|さんらん"}],"notes":"散乱 is physically the \"scattering\" of light or particles (光の散乱). In everyday speech it means that things \"lie scattered about\": 床にごみが散乱している, 書類が散乱する; it is intransitive. For a messy room, one colloquially says 散らかっている instead, and 拡散 is \"spreading\" (e.g. of posts on social media).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"電子が励起されるとより高いエネルギー準位に遷移する。","romaji":"Denshi ga reiki sareru to yori takai enerugī jun'i ni sen'i suru.","german":"When electrons are excited, they transition to a higher energy level.","furigana":"でんし|れいき|たか|じゅんい|せんい"},{"japanese":"レーザーは励起された原子が光を放出する仕組みを利用している。","romaji":"Rēzā wa reiki sareta genshi ga hikari o hōshutsu suru shikumi o riyō shite iru.","german":"Lasers use the mechanism by which excited atoms emit light.","furigana":"れいき|げんし|ひかり|ほうしゅつ|しく|りよう"},{"japanese":"蛍光物質は紫外線で励起されると光を発する。","romaji":"Keikō busshitsu wa shigaisen de reiki sareru to hikari o hassuru.","german":"Fluorescent substances emit light when excited by ultraviolet light.","furigana":"けいこうぶっしつ|しがいせん|れいき|ひかり|はっ"}],"notes":"励起 is a technical term of physics and chemistry for the \"excitation\" of atoms, molecules or electrons to a higher energy level. Typical are 励起状態 \"excited state\" (opposite term 基底状態 \"ground state\") and the passive form 励起される. The figurative sense \"to enliven\" is very rare. Despite the shared kanji, it has nothing to do with 励ます \"to encourage\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"電子が高いエネルギー準位から低い準位へ遷移すると、光が出る。","romaji":"Denshi ga takai enerugī jun'i kara hikui jun'i e sen'i suru to, hikari ga deru.","german":"When an electron moves from a higher energy level to a lower one, light is emitted.","furigana":"でんし|たか|じゅんい|ひく|じゅんい|せんい|ひかり|で"},{"japanese":"このボタンを押すと、次の画面に遷移する。","romaji":"Kono botan o osu to, tsugi no gamen ni sen'i suru.","german":"When you press this button, it moves on to the next screen.","furigana":"お|つぎ|がめん|せんい"},{"japanese":"山火事の跡地では、草原から森林へと植生が遷移していく。","romaji":"Yamakaji no atochi de wa, sōgen kara shinrin e to shokusei ga sen'i shite iku.","german":"On land after a forest fire, the vegetation gradually shifts from grassland to forest.","furigana":"やまかじ|あとち|そうげん|しんりん|しょくせい|せんい"}],"notes":"遷移 is a technical word for the \"transition\" from one state to another, mainly in physics, chemistry and computer science: 状態遷移, 画面遷移 (\"screen transition\" in apps), 遷移金属 (\"transition metals\"), electron transitions between energy levels. In ecology 遷移 means \"succession\" (植生の遷移). As a verb it is intransitive: AからBへ遷移する. For states of matter one says 相転移 or, specifically, 融解, 蒸発; in everyday speech 移り変わる or 変わる suffices.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"太陽からの輻射エネルギーが地球を温めている。","romaji":"Taiyō kara no fukusha enerugī ga chikyū o atatamete iru.","german":"The radiant energy from the Sun warms the Earth.","furigana":"たいよう|ふくしゃ|ちきゅう|あたた"},{"japanese":"夏のアスファルトからの輻射熱で、地面の近くはとても暑い。","romaji":"Natsu no asufaruto kara no fukushanetsu de, jimen no chikaku wa totemo atsui.","german":"Because of radiant heat from the asphalt in summer, it is very hot near the ground.","furigana":"なつ|ふくしゃねつ|じめん|ちか|あつ"},{"japanese":"輻射式の暖房は風が出ないので、ほこりが舞い上がらない。","romaji":"Fukushashiki no danbō wa kaze ga denai node, hokori ga maiagaranai.","german":"Radiant heating produces no air movement, so dust is not stirred up.","furigana":"ふくしゃしき|だんぼう|かぜ|で|ま|あ"}],"notes":"輻射 is an older technical term for \"radiation\", especially thermal radiation. Because 輻 is not a Jōyō kanji, textbooks today mostly say 放射 (放射熱, 放射エネルギー). 輻射 is still alive in 輻射熱 \"radiant heat\" and 輻射式暖房 \"radiant heating\". It has nothing to do with radioactivity (放射線, 放射能) in everyday speech.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"金属は熱伝導率が高い物質である。","romaji":"Kinzoku wa netsudendōritsu ga takai busshitsu de aru.","german":"Metals are substances with high thermal conductivity.","furigana":"きんぞく|ねつでんどうりつ|たか|ぶっしつ"},{"japanese":"銅の鍋は熱伝導がよく、料理にむらが出にくい。","romaji":"Dō no nabe wa netsudendō ga yoku, ryōri ni mura ga denikui.","german":"Copper pots conduct heat well, so food is less likely to cook unevenly.","furigana":"どう|なべ|ねつでんどう|りょうり|で"},{"japanese":"熱伝導の悪い素材で作られたコップは、熱い飲み物を入れても持ちやすい。","romaji":"Netsudendō no warui sozai de tsukurareta koppu wa, atsui nomimono o irete mo mochiyasui.","german":"Cups made of material with poor heat conduction are easy to hold even with hot drinks in them.","furigana":"ねつでんどう|わる|そざい|つく|あつ|の|もの|い|も"}],"notes":"熱伝導 is \"heat conduction\", one of the three kinds of heat transfer alongside 対流 \"convection\" and 放射 \"radiation\". It is most often encountered in 熱伝導率 \"thermal conductivity\" and in phrases like 熱伝導がよい/悪い. It is a pure noun; as a verb one says 熱が伝わる or 熱を伝える.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大気の対流が天候のパターンを形成する。","romaji":"Taiki no tairyū ga tenkō no patān o keisei suru.","german":"Atmospheric convection shapes weather patterns.","furigana":"たいき|たいりゅう|てんこう|けいせい"},{"japanese":"鍋の水を温めると対流が起こり、全体が温まる。","romaji":"Nabe no mizu o atatameru to tairyū ga okori, zentai ga atatamaru.","german":"When you heat the water in a pot, convection occurs and the whole thing warms up.","furigana":"なべ|みず|あたた|たいりゅう|お|ぜんたい|あたた"},{"japanese":"飛行機は対流圏の上のほうを飛んでいる。","romaji":"Hikōki wa tairyūken no ue no hō o tonde iru.","german":"Airplanes fly in the upper part of the troposphere.","furigana":"ひこうき|たいりゅうけん|うえ|と"}],"notes":"対流 is \"convection\", that is, heat transport by flowing liquids or gases. Typical are 対流が起こる/生じる and 対流式ストーブ. In meteorology 対流圏 \"troposphere\" is important; strong rising air currents form thunderclouds (積乱雲). Alongside 伝導 \"conduction\" and 放射 \"radiation\", it is one of the three kinds of heat transfer.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"孤立系のエントロピーは時間とともに増大する。","romaji":"Koritsukei no entoropī wa jikan to tomo ni zōdai suru.","german":"The entropy of an isolated system increases over time.","furigana":"こりつけい|じかん|ぞうだい"},{"japanese":"情報理論では、エントロピーは情報の不確かさを表す。","romaji":"Jōhō riron de wa, entoropī wa jōhō no futashikasa o arawasu.","german":"In information theory, entropy expresses the uncertainty of information.","furigana":"じょうほうりろん|じょうほう|ふたし|あらわ"},{"japanese":"何度片付けても、部屋のエントロピーは増える一方だ。","romaji":"Nando katazukete mo, heya no entoropī wa fueru ippō da.","german":"No matter how many times I tidy up, the entropy of my room only increases.","furigana":"なんどかたづ|へや|ふ|いっぽう"}],"notes":"エントロピー is, in thermodynamics, the measure of the disorder of a system; according to the second law, it does not decrease in an isolated system (孤立系): エントロピー増大の法則. In information theory it denotes uncertainty or information content. Typical: エントロピーが増大する/減少する. Half jokingly, the word is also used in everyday speech for increasing chaos.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先行研究に触発されて新たな実験を計画した。","romaji":"Senkō kenkyū ni shokuhatsu sarete arata na jikken o keikaku shita.","german":"Inspired by previous research, I planned a new experiment.","furigana":"せんこうけんきゅう|しょくはつ|あら|じっけん|けいかく"},{"japanese":"友人の活躍に触発されて、私も英語の勉強を始めた。","romaji":"Yūjin no katsuyaku ni shokuhatsu sarete, watashi mo eigo no benkyō o hajimeta.","german":"Spurred by my friend's success, I also started studying English.","furigana":"ゆうじん|かつやく|しょくはつ|わたし|えいご|べんきょう|はじ"},{"japanese":"一つの小さな事件が大規模なデモを触発した。","romaji":"Hitotsu no chiisa na jiken ga daikibo na demo o shokuhatsu shita.","german":"A single small incident triggered massive demonstrations.","furigana":"ひと|ちい|じけん|だいきぼ|しょくはつ"}],"notes":"触発 means that something gives an impetus or sets something off. Most common is the passive 〜に触発される \"to be inspired, spurred on by something\"; actively it means \"to trigger\": 事件が暴動を触発する. The basic sense \"ignition by touch\" is found in 触発機雷 \"contact mine\". 刺激を受ける is similar but more general; 触発 emphasizes that one's own activity arises from it.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"数値解析を用いて複雑な方程式を解く。","romaji":"Sūchi kaiseki o mochiite fukuzatsu na hōteishiki o toku.","german":"Solve complex equations using numerical analysis.","furigana":"すうちかいせき|もち|ふくざつ|ほうていしき|と"},{"japanese":"防犯カメラの映像を解析して、犯人を特定した。","romaji":"Bōhan kamera no eizō o kaiseki shite, hannin o tokutei shita.","german":"By analyzing the surveillance camera footage, the culprit was identified.","furigana":"ぼうはん|えいぞう|かいせき|はんにん|とくてい"},{"japanese":"大学では解析学の授業が一番難しかった。","romaji":"Daigaku de wa kaisekigaku no jugyō ga ichiban muzukashikatta.","german":"At university, the analysis course was the hardest.","furigana":"だいがく|かいせきがく|じゅぎょう|いちばんむずか"}],"notes":"解析 is a systematic, mostly technical or computational \"analysis\" of data and structures; as a transitive する-verb: データを解析する. Typical are 遺伝子解析, 画像解析, 数値解析 and, in computer science, 構文解析 \"parsing\"; 解析学 is the mathematical subject \"analysis\". 分析 is more general and is also used for markets, politics or psychology.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仮説検定を行って統計的有意差を確認した。","romaji":"Kasetsu kentei o okonatte tōkeiteki yūisa o kakunin shita.","german":"We performed hypothesis testing and confirmed a statistically significant difference.","furigana":"かせつけんてい|おこな|とうけいてきゆういさ|かくにん"},{"japanese":"仮説検定では、まず帰無仮説と対立仮説を立てる。","romaji":"Kasetsu kentei de wa, mazu kimu kasetsu to tairitsu kasetsu o tateru.","german":"In hypothesis testing, you first set up a null hypothesis and an alternative hypothesis.","furigana":"かせつけんてい|きむかせつ|たいりつかせつ|た"},{"japanese":"新薬に効果があるかどうかを仮説検定で確かめる。","romaji":"Shin'yaku ni kōka ga aru ka dō ka o kasetsu kentei de tashikameru.","german":"Use hypothesis testing to check whether the new drug is effective.","furigana":"しんやく|こうか|かせつけんてい|たし"}],"notes":"仮説検定 is statistical \"hypothesis testing\". One sets up a 帰無仮説 \"null hypothesis\" and a 対立仮説 \"alternative hypothesis\" and checks with a 有意水準 \"significance level\" whether the null hypothesis is rejected. Concrete procedures are called 検定 for short (t検定, カイ二乗検定); in everyday speech, however, 検定 alone also means \"exam, certificate\" (漢字検定). The usual phrase is 仮説検定を行う.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"帰無仮説が棄却されれば対立仮説が支持される。","romaji":"Kimu kasetsu ga kikyaku sarereba tairitsu kasetsu ga shiji sareru.","german":"If the null hypothesis is rejected, the alternative hypothesis is supported.","furigana":"きむかせつ|ききゃく|たいりつかせつ|しじ"},{"japanese":"p値が0.05より小さかったので、帰無仮説を棄却した。","romaji":"P-chi ga rei-ten-zero-go yori chiisakatta node, kimu kasetsu o kikyaku shita.","german":"Since the p-value was smaller than 0.05, the null hypothesis was rejected."},{"japanese":"この実験の帰無仮説は「新しい肥料には効果がない」というものだ。","romaji":"Kono jikken no kimu kasetsu wa \"atarashii hiryō ni wa kōka ga nai\" to iu mono da.","german":"The null hypothesis of this experiment is that \"the new fertilizer has no effect\".","furigana":"じっけん|きむかせつ|あたら|ひりょう|こうか"}],"notes":"帰無仮説 is the \"null hypothesis\"; 帰無 literally means \"to return to nothing\", because it is set up in the expectation of rejecting it. The fixed verbs are 帰無仮説を棄却する \"to reject\" and 帰無仮説を採択する \"to accept\" or 棄却できない. The opposite term is 対立仮説 \"alternative hypothesis\". A purely technical statistics term.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"統計的に有意な差が認められた。","romaji":"Tōkeiteki ni yūi na sa ga mitomerareta.","german":"A statistically significant difference was found.","furigana":"とうけいてき|ゆうい|さ|みと"},{"japanese":"喫煙者の発症率は非喫煙者より有意に高かった。","romaji":"Kitsuensha no hasshōritsu wa hikitsuensha yori yūi ni takakatta.","german":"The incidence rate among smokers was significantly higher than among non-smokers.","furigana":"きつえんしゃ|はっしょうりつ|ひきつえんしゃ|ゆうい|たか"},{"japanese":"二つのグループの間に有意差は見られなかった。","romaji":"Futatsu no gurūpu no aida ni yūisa wa mirarenakatta.","german":"No significant difference was seen between the two groups.","furigana":"ふた|あいだ|ゆういさ|み"}],"notes":"有意 means \"significant\" in statistics and is used as a な-adjective: 有意な差, 有意に高い. Important compounds are 有意差 \"significant difference\" and 有意水準 \"significance level\". The meaning \"intentional\" is rare. Do not confuse it with the everyday word 有意義 \"meaningful, worthwhile\" (有意義な時間).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"定量分析によって物質の正確な含有量を測定する。","romaji":"Teiryō bunseki ni yotte busshitsu no seikaku na gan'yūryō o sokutei suru.","german":"The exact content of a substance is measured by quantitative analysis.","furigana":"ていりょうぶんせき|ぶっしつ|せいかく|がんゆうりょう|そくてい"},{"japanese":"目標はできるだけ定量的に設定したほうがいい。","romaji":"Mokuhyō wa dekiru dake teiryōteki ni settei shita hō ga ii.","german":"It is better to set goals as quantitatively as possible.","furigana":"もくひょう|ていりょうてき|せってい"},{"japanese":"顧客の満足度を定量化するのは簡単ではない。","romaji":"Kokyaku no manzokudo o teiryōka suru no wa kantan de wa nai.","german":"It is not easy to quantify customer satisfaction.","furigana":"こきゃく|まんぞくど|ていりょうか|かんたん"}],"notes":"定量 literally means \"fixed quantity\"; in science and business, however, it appears almost only as a first element with the meaning \"quantitative\": 定量分析, 定量的な評価, 定量化する \"to quantify\". The opposite term is 定性 \"qualitative\". For the dose of medicines, 用量 is used instead.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"定性分析は物質の種類を特定する手法である。","romaji":"Teisei bunseki wa busshitsu no shurui o tokutei suru shuhō de aru.","german":"Qualitative analysis is a technique for identifying the types of substances.","furigana":"ていせいぶんせき|ぶっしつ|しゅるい|とくてい|しゅほう"},{"japanese":"アンケートの数字だけでなく、インタビューによる定性的な情報も大切だ。","romaji":"Ankēto no sūji dake de naku, intabyū ni yoru teiseiteki na jōhō mo taisetsu da.","german":"Not only the numbers from questionnaires but also qualitative information from interviews is important.","furigana":"すうじ|ていせいてき|じょうほう|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"この試験紙は定性的な検査にしか使えず、濃度までは分からない。","romaji":"Kono shikenshi wa teiseiteki na kensa ni shika tsukaezu, nōdo made wa wakaranai.","german":"This test strip can only be used for qualitative tests and cannot tell you the concentration.","furigana":"しけんし|ていせいてき|けんさ|つか|のうど|わ"}],"notes":"定性 means \"qualitative\" and never stands alone, only as a first element: 定性分析, 定性調査, 定性的な情報. It is the opposite term to 定量 \"quantitative\": 定性 asks \"what/what kind\", 定量 \"how much\". Beware when listening: 訂正 \"correction\" is read ていせい in exactly the same way.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"質量分析法を用いて未知の化合物を同定した。","romaji":"Shitsuryō bunsekihō o mochiite michi no kagōbutsu o dōtei shita.","german":"The unknown compound was identified using mass spectrometry.","furigana":"しつりょうぶんせきほう|もち|みち|かごうぶつ|どうてい"},{"japanese":"採集した昆虫を図鑑で調べて種を同定した。","romaji":"Saishū shita konchū o zukan de shirabete shu o dōtei shita.","german":"I looked up the collected insects in a field guide and identified the species.","furigana":"さいしゅう|こんちゅう|ずかん|しら|しゅ|どうてい"},{"japanese":"DNA鑑定によって遺体の身元が同定された。","romaji":"DNA kantei ni yotte itai no mimoto ga dōtei sareta.","german":"The identity of the body was established through DNA analysis.","furigana":"かんてい|いたい|みもと|どうてい"}],"notes":"同定 is the technical \"identification\" or \"determination\", that is, establishing which species, substance or person something is; as a transitive する-verb: 化合物を同定する, 種を同定する. It belongs to biology, chemistry and forensics. In everyday speech and in the news, 特定する is mostly used (犯人を特定する); 識別 is mere \"distinguishing\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"測定機器は定期的に較正する必要がある。","romaji":"Sokutei kiki wa teikiteki ni kōsei suru hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"Measuring instruments need to be calibrated regularly.","furigana":"そくていきき|ていきてき|こうせい|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"体重計は使う前に較正しておかないと、正しい値が出ない。","romaji":"Taijūkei wa tsukau mae ni kōsei shite okanai to, tadashii atai ga denai.","german":"A scale will not give correct values unless it is calibrated before use.","furigana":"たいじゅうけい|つか|まえ|こうせい|ただ|あたい|で"},{"japanese":"放射線測定器の較正は専門の機関に依頼している。","romaji":"Hōshasen sokuteiki no kōsei wa senmon no kikan ni irai shite iru.","german":"We have a specialist institution carry out the calibration of the radiation measuring devices.","furigana":"ほうしゃせんそくていき|こうせい|せんもん|きかん|いらい"}],"notes":"較正 is the \"calibration\" of measuring instruments against a standard; as a transitive する-verb: 機器を較正する, plus 較正曲線 \"calibration curve\". 校正 is often written for this too, but it mainly means \"proofreading\". In engineering the loanword キャリブレーション is also common. Do not confuse it with 補正, the correcting of the measured values themselves.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"蚊はマラリアの媒介となる昆虫である。","romaji":"Ka wa mararia no baikai to naru konchū de aru.","german":"Mosquitoes are insects that act as vectors of malaria.","furigana":"か|ばいかい|こんちゅう"},{"japanese":"この病気はダニによって媒介される。","romaji":"Kono byōki wa dani ni yotte baikai sareru.","german":"This disease is transmitted by ticks.","furigana":"びょうき|ばいかい"},{"japanese":"言語を媒介として、人は考えを共有する。","romaji":"Gengo o baikai to shite, hito wa kangae o kyōyū suru.","german":"Through language, people share their thoughts.","furigana":"げんご|ばいかい|ひと|かんが|きょうゆう"}],"notes":"媒介 is the \"mediating\" between two things or the \"transmission\" of pathogens; as a transitive する-verb: 蚊が病気を媒介する, often in the passive 〜によって媒介される. Abstractly one says 〜を媒介として \"mediated by, through\". 仲介, by contrast, is business brokerage (不動産の仲介), and 媒体 is the \"medium\" in the sense of a data carrier or the press.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"水の表面張力によって小さな虫は水面に浮くことができる。","romaji":"Mizu no hyōmen chōryoku ni yotte chiisana mushi wa suimen ni uku koto ga dekiru.","german":"Thanks to the surface tension of water, small insects can float on the water surface.","furigana":"みず|ひょうめんちょうりょく|ちい|むし|すいめん|う"},{"japanese":"水滴が丸くなるのは表面張力が働くからだ。","romaji":"Suiteki ga maruku naru no wa hyōmen chōryoku ga hataraku kara da.","german":"Water droplets become round because surface tension acts.","furigana":"すいてき|まる|ひょうめんちょうりょく|はたら"},{"japanese":"洗剤には水の表面張力を下げる働きがある。","romaji":"Senzai ni wa mizu no hyōmen chōryoku o sageru hataraki ga aru.","german":"Detergent has the effect of lowering the surface tension of water.","furigana":"せんざい|みず|ひょうめんちょうりょく|さ|はたら"}],"notes":"表面張力 is the physics term for the \"surface tension\" of liquids. Typical are 表面張力が働く (\"surface tension acts\"), 表面張力が大きい／小さい, and 表面張力で〜 or 表面張力によって〜 for the cause. It explains, for example, round water droplets or insects on water; detergent 表面張力を下げる (\"lowers the surface tension\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"毛管現象によって植物は根から水を吸い上げる。","romaji":"Mōkan genshō ni yotte shokubutsu wa ne kara mizu o suiageru.","german":"Through capillary action, plants draw water up from their roots.","furigana":"もうかんげんしょう|しょくぶつ|ね|みず|す|あ"},{"japanese":"タオルが水を吸うのも毛管現象の一つだ。","romaji":"Taoru ga mizu o suu no mo mōkan genshō no hitotsu da.","german":"A towel soaking up water is also an example of capillary action.","furigana":"みず|す|もうかんげんしょう|ひと"},{"japanese":"細いガラス管を水に立てると、毛管現象で水が上がってくる。","romaji":"Hosoi garasukan o mizu ni tateru to, mōkan genshō de mizu ga agatte kuru.","german":"If you stand a thin glass tube in water, the water rises in it by capillary action.","furigana":"ほそ|かん|みず|た|もうかんげんしょう|みず|あ"}],"notes":"毛管現象 is the technical term for \"capillary action\": liquid rises on its own in narrow tubes, pores or fibers. The long form 毛細管現象 means the same and is at least as common. Typical frames are 毛管現象によって〜 and 毛管現象で水が上がる／吸い上げられる. The term explains everyday things such as an absorbent towel or a candle wick.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"水は比熱が高いため温まりにくく冷めにくい。","romaji":"Mizu wa hinetsu ga takai tame atatamarinikuku samenikui.","german":"Water has a high specific heat capacity, so it heats up and cools down slowly.","furigana":"みず|ひねつ|たか|あたた|さ"},{"japanese":"砂は比熱が小さいので、日中すぐに熱くなる。","romaji":"Suna wa hinetsu ga chīsai node, nitchū sugu ni atsuku naru.","german":"Sand has a low specific heat capacity, so it gets hot quickly during the day.","furigana":"すな|ひねつ|ちい|にっちゅう|あつ"},{"japanese":"陸と海の比熱の違いが海風を生む。","romaji":"Riku to umi no hinetsu no chigai ga umikaze o umu.","german":"The difference in specific heat between land and sea produces the sea breeze.","furigana":"りく|うみ|ひねつ|ちが|うみかぜ|う"}],"notes":"比熱 means \"specific heat capacity\", i.e. the amount of heat needed to raise 1 g of a substance by 1 °C. One says 比熱が大きい／高い or 比熱が小さい／低い. Do not confuse it with 熱容量 (\"heat capacity\" of a whole body) - 比熱 always refers to a unit of mass. Typical in physics lessons and climatology, for example in the contrast between land and sea.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"氷が融解する際に潜熱が吸収される。","romaji":"Kōri ga yūkai suru sai ni sennetsu ga kyūshū sareru.","german":"Latent heat is absorbed when ice melts.","furigana":"こおり|ゆうかい|さい|せんねつ|きゅうしゅう"},{"japanese":"汗が蒸発するとき潜熱を奪うので、体が冷える。","romaji":"Ase ga jōhatsu suru toki sennetsu o ubau node, karada ga hieru.","german":"When sweat evaporates it takes away latent heat, so the body cools down.","furigana":"あせ|じょうはつ|せんねつ|うば|からだ|ひ"},{"japanese":"台風は海から蒸発した水蒸気の潜熱をエネルギー源にしている。","romaji":"Taifū wa umi kara jōhatsu shita suijōki no sennetsu o enerugīgen ni shite iru.","german":"Typhoons use the latent heat of water vapor evaporated from the sea as their energy source.","furigana":"たいふう|うみ|じょうはつ|すいじょうき|せんねつ|げん"}],"notes":"潜熱 is \"latent heat\" (heat of transformation), which is absorbed or released during phase changes such as melting or evaporation without a change in temperature. Typical combinations: 潜熱を吸収する／放出する, 融解潜熱 (\"heat of fusion\"), 蒸発潜熱 (\"heat of vaporization\"). The opposite term is 顕熱 (\"sensible heat\"). Purely technical; the figurative meaning \"hidden heat\" is rare.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"熱力学第二法則はエントロピーの増大を述べている。","romaji":"Netsurikigaku dai ni hōsoku wa entoropī no zōdai o nobete iru.","german":"The second law of thermodynamics states that entropy increases.","furigana":"ねつりきがくだいにほうそく|ぞうだい|の"},{"japanese":"大学で熱力学の講義を受けている。","romaji":"Daigaku de netsurikigaku no kōgi o ukete iru.","german":"I am taking a lecture on thermodynamics at university.","furigana":"だいがく|ねつりきがく|こうぎ|う"},{"japanese":"永久機関は熱力学的に不可能だとされている。","romaji":"Eikyū kikan wa netsurikigakuteki ni fukanō da to sarete iru.","german":"A perpetual motion machine is considered thermodynamically impossible.","furigana":"えいきゅうきかん|ねつりきがくてき|ふかのう"}],"notes":"熱力学 is \"thermodynamics\" as a branch of physics. It often appears in fixed combinations: 熱力学第一法則／第二法則 (\"first/second law of thermodynamics\"), 熱力学的に (\"thermodynamically\"), 熱力学温度. It is built from 熱 (\"heat\") and 力学 (\"mechanics\"), like 流体力学 or 量子力学. Technical language from university study and engineering.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"流体力学は液体と気体の運動を研究する学問である。","romaji":"Ryūtai rikigaku wa ekitai to kitai no undō o kenkyū suru gakumon de aru.","german":"Fluid mechanics is the science that studies the motion of liquids and gases.","furigana":"りゅうたいりきがく|えきたい|きたい|うんどう|けんきゅう|がくもん"},{"japanese":"新幹線の先端の形は流体力学に基づいて設計されている。","romaji":"Shinkansen no sentan no katachi wa ryūtai rikigaku ni motozuite sekkei sarete iru.","german":"The shape of the Shinkansen's nose is designed based on fluid mechanics.","furigana":"しんかんせん|せんたん|かたち|りゅうたいりきがく|もと|せっけい"},{"japanese":"飛行機が飛ぶ仕組みは流体力学で説明できる。","romaji":"Hikōki ga tobu shikumi wa ryūtai rikigaku de setsumei dekiru.","german":"How an airplane flies can be explained by fluid mechanics.","furigana":"ひこうき|と|しく|りゅうたいりきがく|せつめい"}],"notes":"流体力学 is \"fluid mechanics\" or \"fluid dynamics\", the study of the motion of liquids and gases (流体 = \"fluid\"). Typical: 流体力学に基づいて設計する, 流体力学的な観点から, 計算流体力学 (\"computational fluid dynamics, CFD\"). Related terms from the same field are 乱流, 層流 and 渦. Technical language from engineering, meteorology and physics.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"高速で流れる流体には乱流が発生しやすい。","romaji":"Kōsoku de nagareru ryūtai ni wa ranryū ga hassei shiyasui.","german":"Turbulence easily occurs in fluids flowing at high speed.","furigana":"こうそく|なが|りゅうたい|らんりゅう|はっせい"},{"japanese":"川の流れが速くなると、層流から乱流に変わる。","romaji":"Kawa no nagare ga hayaku naru to, sōryū kara ranryū ni kawaru.","german":"When the river's flow gets faster, it changes from laminar to turbulent flow.","furigana":"かわ|なが|はや|そうりゅう|らんりゅう|か"},{"japanese":"車の後ろには乱流が生じ、空気抵抗の原因になる。","romaji":"Kuruma no ushiro ni wa ranryū ga shōji, kūki teikō no gen'in ni naru.","german":"Turbulence forms behind a car and causes air resistance.","furigana":"くるま|うし|らんりゅう|しょう|くうきていこう|げんいん"}],"notes":"乱流 is \"turbulent flow\" in liquids or gases, the opposite of 層流 (\"laminar flow\"). Typical are 乱流が発生する／生じる and 層流から乱流に変わる. For turbulence on an airplane, everyday speech usually says 乱気流 - 乱流 is the more general technical term. It is not used in the figurative sense (\"political turbulence\"); there 混乱 or 波乱 fit.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"層流では流体が整然と流れる。","romaji":"Sōryū de wa ryūtai ga seizen to nagareru.","german":"In laminar flow, the fluid flows in an orderly way.","furigana":"そうりゅう|りゅうたい|せいぜん|なが"},{"japanese":"蛇口から水を静かに出すと、層流になって透明な棒のように見える。","romaji":"Jaguchi kara mizu o shizuka ni dasu to, sōryū ni natte tōmei na bō no yō ni mieru.","german":"If you let water run gently from a faucet, it becomes laminar and looks like a transparent rod.","furigana":"じゃぐち|みず|しず|だ|そうりゅう|とうめい|ぼう|み"},{"japanese":"クリーンルームでは層流で空気を流し、ほこりを防ぐ。","romaji":"Kurīn rūmu de wa sōryū de kūki o nagashi, hokori o fusegu.","german":"In clean rooms, air is passed through in laminar flow to keep out dust.","furigana":"そうりゅう|くうき|なが|ふせ"}],"notes":"層流 is \"laminar flow\", in which a fluid moves in orderly, parallel layers (層 = \"layer\"). The opposite term is 乱流. Typical: 層流になる, 層流を保つ, 層流から乱流へ移る. Purely technical; the word is encountered for example in clean-room ventilation or airfoils (層流翼).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"流体中に発生する渦の構造を解析した。","romaji":"Ryūtai chū ni hassei suru uzu no kōzō o kaiseki shita.","german":"I analyzed the structure of the vortices forming in the fluid.","furigana":"りゅうたいちゅう|はっせい|うず|こうぞう|かいせき"},{"japanese":"鳴門海峡では大きな渦が見られる。","romaji":"Naruto kaikyō de wa ōkina uzu ga mirareru.","german":"Large whirlpools can be seen in the Naruto Strait.","furigana":"|おお|うず|み"},{"japanese":"優勝が決まり、会場は興奮の渦に包まれた。","romaji":"Yūshō ga kimari, kaijō wa kōfun no uzu ni tsutsumareta.","german":"When the victory was decided, the venue was engulfed in a whirl of excitement.","furigana":"ゆうしょう|き|かいじょう|こうふん|うず|つつ"}],"notes":"渦 is a \"vortex\" or \"whirlpool\" in water, air or smoke. Typical phrases: 渦を巻く (\"to swirl, to spiral\"), 渦ができる, 渦に巻き込まれる (\"to get caught in a whirlpool\"). Figuratively it stands for a whirl of events or emotions: 混乱の渦, 興奮の渦, plus 渦中 (\"in the midst of events\"). The 渦潮 (\"tidal whirlpools\") of Naruto are well known.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"揺動散逸定理は、揺動と散逸の関係を示している。","romaji":"Yōdō san'itsu teiri wa, yōdō to san'itsu no kankei o shimeshite iru.","german":"The fluctuation-dissipation theorem shows the relationship between fluctuation and dissipation.","furigana":"ようどうさんいつていり|ようどう|さんいつ|かんけい|しめ"},{"japanese":"このアームはモーターで左右に揺動する。","romaji":"Kono āmu wa mōtā de sayū ni yōdō suru.","german":"This arm swings left and right, driven by a motor.","furigana":"さゆう|ようどう"},{"japanese":"船の揺動を抑える装置を取り付けた。","romaji":"Fune no yōdō o osaeru sōchi o toritsuketa.","german":"We installed a device that reduces the rolling of the ship.","furigana":"ふね|ようどう|おさ|そうち|と|つ"}],"notes":"揺動 is a technical term of written language for \"swaying, swinging back and forth\". In engineering it describes a pendulum or swiveling motion (揺動運動, 揺動する), in physics the \"fluctuation\" of a quantity, especially in 揺動散逸定理 (\"fluctuation-dissipation theorem\"). In everyday speech and popular physics, 揺れ or 揺らぎ is usually used instead, e.g. 量子揺らぎ (\"quantum fluctuation\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"元素の吸収スペクトルを分析して組成を調べる。","romaji":"Genso no kyūshū supekutoru o bunseki shite sosei o shiraberu.","german":"The composition is investigated by analyzing the absorption spectrum of the elements.","furigana":"げんそ|きゅうしゅう|ぶんせき|そせい|しら"},{"japanese":"プリズムを通すと、太陽光が虹のようなスペクトルに分かれる。","romaji":"Purizumu o tōsu to, taiyōkō ga niji no yō na supekutoru ni wakareru.","german":"When sunlight passes through a prism, it splits into a rainbow-like spectrum.","furigana":"とお|たいようこう|にじ|わ"},{"japanese":"星のスペクトルから、その星の温度が分かる。","romaji":"Hoshi no supekutoru kara, sono hoshi no ondo ga wakaru.","german":"A star's temperature can be determined from its spectrum.","furigana":"ほし|ほし|おんど|わ"}],"notes":"スペクトル (from French spectre) is the \"spectrum\" in physics and chemistry: light, radiation or a signal broken down by wavelength or frequency. Common compounds: 吸収スペクトル, 発光スペクトル, 周波数スペクトル, スペクトル分析. For \"spectrum\" in the figurative sense (\"a broad spectrum of opinions\"), one usually uses 幅広い or 範囲; the English form スペクトラム appears mainly in technical terms such as 自閉スペクトラム症.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"電子顕微鏡でナノスケールの構造を観察する。","romaji":"Denshi kenbikyō de nano sukēru no kōzō o kansatsu suru.","german":"Nanoscale structures are observed with an electron microscope.","furigana":"でんしけんびきょう|こうぞう|かんさつ"},{"japanese":"ウイルスは小さすぎて、電子顕微鏡でなければ見えない。","romaji":"Uirusu wa chīsasugite, denshi kenbikyō de nakereba mienai.","german":"Viruses are so small that they can only be seen with an electron microscope.","furigana":"ちい|でんしけんびきょう|み"},{"japanese":"この研究所には電子顕微鏡が三台ある。","romaji":"Kono kenkyūjo ni wa denshi kenbikyō ga sandai aru.","german":"This research institute has three electron microscopes.","furigana":"けんきゅうじょ|でんしけんびきょう|さんだい"}],"notes":"電子顕微鏡 is the \"electron microscope\", as opposed to the 光学顕微鏡 (\"optical microscope\"). In professional circles it is abbreviated to 電顕; one distinguishes 走査型電子顕微鏡 (SEM) and 透過型電子顕微鏡 (TEM). Typical frames: 電子顕微鏡で観察する／撮影する, 電子顕微鏡写真 (\"electron micrograph\"). The devices are counted with 台.","levelNote":"Compound: 電子 (N4) + 顕微鏡 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"分光法は物質の組成分析に広く用いられる。","romaji":"Bunkōhō wa busshitsu no sosei bunseki ni hiroku mochiirareru.","german":"Spectroscopy is widely used for analyzing the composition of substances.","furigana":"ぶんこうほう|ぶっしつ|そせいぶんせき|ひろ|もち"},{"japanese":"プリズムを使うと、太陽の光を分光することができる。","romaji":"Purizumu o tsukau to, taiyō no hikari o bunkō suru koto ga dekiru.","german":"With a prism, you can split sunlight into its spectrum.","furigana":"つか|たいよう|ひかり|ぶんこう"},{"japanese":"星の光を分光して、その星の成分を調べる。","romaji":"Hoshi no hikari o bunkō shite, sono hoshi no seibun o shiraberu.","german":"They split the light of a star into its spectrum to investigate its components.","furigana":"ほし|ひかり|ぶんこう|ほし|せいぶん|しら"}],"notes":"分光 means the splitting of light into its spectrum, for example with a prism or grating: 光を分光する. The method or science is called 分光法 or 分光学 (\"spectroscopy\"), the device 分光器 (\"spectroscope\"), the analysis 分光分析. The word on its own is found almost only in physics, chemistry and astronomy; in everyday speech one would rather say 虹のように光を分ける.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"試料を薄切りにして顕微鏡で観察した。","romaji":"Shiryō o usugiri ni shite kenbikyō de kansatsu shita.","german":"I cut the specimen into thin slices and observed it under a microscope.","furigana":"しりょう|うすぎ|けんびきょう|かんさつ"},{"japanese":"川の水を試料として採取し、水質を分析した。","romaji":"Kawa no mizu o shiryō to shite saishu shi, suishitsu o bunseki shita.","german":"We collected river water as a sample and analyzed the water quality.","furigana":"かわ|みず|しりょう|さいしゅ|すいしつ|ぶんせき"},{"japanese":"月の岩石の試料が博物館で展示されている。","romaji":"Tsuki no ganseki no shiryō ga hakubutsukan de tenji sarete iru.","german":"Samples of lunar rock are on display at the museum.","furigana":"つき|がんせき|しりょう|はくぶつかん|てんじ"}],"notes":"試料 is the \"sample\" or \"test material\" in laboratories, analysis and materials testing: 試料を採取する (\"to take a sample\"), 試料を分析する, 血液試料. Do not confuse it with the identically read 資料 (\"documents, materials, data\") and 飼料 (\"animal feed\") - in speech only the context clarifies. In medical practice, 検体 is preferred for blood or urine samples, and colloquially also サンプル.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"高分子の架橋反応によって材料の強度が増す。","romaji":"Kōbunshi no kakyō hannō ni yotte zairyō no kyōdo ga masu.","german":"The cross-linking reaction of polymers increases the strength of the material.","furigana":"こうぶんし|かきょうはんのう|ざいりょう|きょうど|ま"},{"japanese":"島と本土を結ぶ架橋工事が始まった。","romaji":"Shima to hondo o musubu kakyō kōji ga hajimatta.","german":"Construction of a bridge connecting the island and the mainland has begun.","furigana":"しま|ほんど|むす|かきょうこうじ|はじ"},{"japanese":"この研究は理論と実践の架橋を目指している。","romaji":"Kono kenkyū wa riron to jissen no kakyō o mezashite iru.","german":"This research aims to bridge theory and practice.","furigana":"けんきゅう|りろん|じっせん|かきょう|めざ"}],"notes":"架橋 has two specialist fields: literally \"bridge building\" (橋を架ける), e.g. 架橋工事, and in chemistry the \"cross-linking\" of polymer chains (架橋反応, 架橋剤, 架橋する). Figuratively it also means \"building a bridge\" between two fields, e.g. 理論と実践の架橋. The word is technical written language; in everyday speech one simply says 橋を架ける for building a bridge and 橋渡し(をする) for \"to mediate\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"集積回路には数十億個のトランジスタが搭載されている。","romaji":"Shūseki kairo ni wa sūjū oku ko no toranjisuta ga tōsai sarete iru.","german":"Integrated circuits carry billions of transistors.","furigana":"しゅうせきかいろ|すうじゅうおくこ|とうさい"},{"japanese":"スマートフォンの中には小さな集積回路がたくさん入っている。","romaji":"Sumātofon no naka ni wa chīsana shūseki kairo ga takusan haitte iru.","german":"A smartphone contains many small integrated circuits.","furigana":"なか|ちい|しゅうせきかいろ|はい"},{"japanese":"彼は半導体メーカーで集積回路の設計をしている。","romaji":"Kare wa handōtai mēkā de shūseki kairo no sekkei o shite iru.","german":"He designs integrated circuits at a semiconductor manufacturer.","furigana":"かれ|はんどうたい|しゅうせきかいろ|せっけい"}],"notes":"集積回路 is the \"integrated circuit\" (IC), i.e. many electronic components on one semiconductor chip. In everyday speech one usually says IC or ICチップ, and LSI (大規模集積回路) for very large circuits. Typical: 集積回路を設計する／製造する, 集積回路の集積度 (\"integration density\"). 集積 means \"accumulation, integration\", 回路 \"circuit\" - cf. 電子回路.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"フィードバック制御によって温度を一定に保つ。","romaji":"Fīdobakku seigyo ni yotte ondo o ittei ni tamotsu.","german":"The temperature is kept constant by feedback control.","furigana":"せいぎょ|おんど|いってい|たも"},{"japanese":"ブレーキが故障し、車は制御不能になった。","romaji":"Burēki ga koshō shi, kuruma wa seigyo funō ni natta.","german":"The brakes failed and the car went out of control.","furigana":"こしょう|くるま|せいぎょふのう"},{"japanese":"怒りの感情を制御するのは簡単ではない。","romaji":"Ikari no kanjō o seigyo suru no wa kantan de wa nai.","german":"It is not easy to control feelings of anger.","furigana":"いか|かんじょう|せいぎょ|かんたん"}],"notes":"制御 is the \"control, regulation\" of machines and systems: 制御する, 自動制御, 温度制御, 制御装置, 制御不能 (\"out of control\"). Figuratively also used for reining in emotions or people (感情を制御する), although コントロールする or 抑える sounds more everyday there. Distinction: 管理 is \"administration, management\", 操作 is \"operation\" by a human - 制御 stresses the deliberate steering of a process. Transitive する-verb with を.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"精密な計測技術が品質管理の基盤となっている。","romaji":"Seimitsu na keisoku gijutsu ga hinshitsu kanri no kiban to natte iru.","german":"Precise measurement technology is the foundation of quality control.","furigana":"せいみつ|けいそくぎじゅつ|ひんしつかんり|きばん"},{"japanese":"このアプリで毎日の歩数を計測している。","romaji":"Kono apuri de mainichi no hosū o keisoku shite iru.","german":"I measure my daily step count with this app.","furigana":"まいにち|ほすう|けいそく"},{"japanese":"ゴールの瞬間にタイムが自動で計測される。","romaji":"Gōru no shunkan ni taimu ga jidō de keisoku sareru.","german":"The time is measured automatically at the moment of crossing the finish line.","furigana":"しゅんかん|じどう|けいそく"}],"notes":"計測 is \"measuring, measurement\" with instruments, especially when exact numerical values are recorded: 計測する, 計測器 (\"measuring instrument\"), 計測技術, 計測データ. Compared with the general 測定, 計測 sounds somewhat more technical and is often used for continuous or automatic measurements (タイムを計測する, 歩数を計測する). In everyday speech one simply says 測る／計る. Transitive する-verb with を.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"精密機械の製造には高度な技術力が求められる。","romaji":"Seimitsu kikai no seizō ni wa kōdo na gijutsuryoku ga motomerareru.","german":"Manufacturing precision machinery requires advanced technical skill.","furigana":"せいみつきかい|せいぞう|こうど|ぎじゅつりょく|もと"},{"japanese":"健康診断で異常が見つかり、精密検査を受けることになった。","romaji":"Kenkō shindan de ijō ga mitsukari, seimitsu kensa o ukeru koto ni natta.","german":"An abnormality was found at my health checkup, so I am going to have a detailed examination.","furigana":"けんこうしんだん|いじょう|み|せいみつけんさ|う"},{"japanese":"この時計は非常に精密に作られている。","romaji":"Kono tokei wa hijō ni seimitsu ni tsukurarete iru.","german":"This watch is made extremely precisely.","furigana":"とけい|ひじょう|せいみつ|つく"}],"notes":"精密 is a な-adjective or noun for \"precise, very exact, down to the detail\". It often stands as a modifier before nouns: 精密機械 (\"precision machine\"), 精密検査 (\"thorough medical examination\"), 精密な地図／計算. Compared with 正確 (\"correct, error-free\"), 精密 stresses fineness and level of detail; the similar 緻密 is used more for plans and thinking. Adverbially: 精密に作る／調べる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鋳造技術は古代から金属加工に用いられてきた。","romaji":"Chūzō gijutsu wa kodai kara kinzoku kakō ni mochiirarete kita.","german":"Casting technology has been used for metalworking since ancient times.","furigana":"ちゅうぞうぎじゅつ|こだい|きんぞくかこう|もち"},{"japanese":"奈良の大仏は銅で鋳造された。","romaji":"Nara no daibutsu wa dō de chūzō sareta.","german":"The Great Buddha of Nara was cast in bronze.","furigana":"なら|だいぶつ|どう|ちゅうぞう"},{"japanese":"江戸時代には各地で銭が鋳造された。","romaji":"Edo jidai ni wa kakuchi de zeni ga chūzō sareta.","german":"In the Edo period, coins were cast in many places.","furigana":"えどじだい|かくち|ぜに|ちゅうぞう"}],"notes":"鋳造 is the \"casting\" of metal into a mold (鋳型): 鋳造する, 鋳造品 (\"casting, cast part\"), 鋳造所 (\"foundry\"). Historically it also stands for minting coins: 貨幣を鋳造する. Its counterpart in metal forming is 鍛造 (\"forging\") - the kanji 鋳 (\"to cast\") and 鍛 (\"to forge\") are easily confused. The related simple verb is 鋳る (いる), known from 鋳物 (\"cast goods\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"アーク溶接は金属を高温で接合する技術である。","romaji":"Āku yōsetsu wa kinzoku o kōon de setsugō suru gijutsu de aru.","german":"Arc welding is a technique for joining metals at high temperature.","furigana":"ようせつ|きんぞく|こうおん|せつごう|ぎじゅつ"},{"japanese":"父は町工場で溶接の仕事をしている。","romaji":"Chichi wa machikōba de yōsetsu no shigoto o shite iru.","german":"My father works as a welder at a small town factory.","furigana":"ちち|まちこうば|ようせつ|しごと"},{"japanese":"壊れた門の金具を溶接して直してもらった。","romaji":"Kowareta mon no kanagu o yōsetsu shite naoshite moratta.","german":"I had the broken metal fitting on the gate welded and repaired.","furigana":"こわ|もん|かなぐ|ようせつ|なお"}],"notes":"溶接 is \"welding\", i.e. joining metal parts by melting them: 溶接する, 溶接工 (\"welder\"), 溶接機, 溶接部 (\"weld\"). Typical methods: アーク溶接 (\"arc welding\"), ガス溶接, スポット溶接. To be distinguished from はんだ付け (\"soldering\") and 接着 (\"gluing\"). Transitive する-verb: AとBを溶接する or AをBに溶接する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"レンズの研磨には高い精度が要求される。","romaji":"Renzu no kenma ni wa takai seido ga yōkyū sareru.","german":"Grinding lenses requires high precision.","furigana":"けんま|たか|せいど|ようきゅう"},{"japanese":"歯医者で歯の表面を研磨してもらった。","romaji":"Haisha de ha no hyōmen o kenma shite moratta.","german":"I had the surface of my teeth polished at the dentist.","furigana":"はいしゃ|は|ひょうめん|けんま"},{"japanese":"原石を研磨すると、美しい宝石になる。","romaji":"Genseki o kenma suru to, utsukushii hōseki ni naru.","german":"When a rough stone is polished, it becomes a beautiful gem.","furigana":"げんせき|けんま|うつく|ほうせき"}],"notes":"研磨 means \"grinding, polishing\" of surfaces such as lenses, metal, teeth or gemstones: 研磨する, 研磨剤 (\"abrasive, polishing agent\"), 研磨機. It is more technical than 磨く; in everyday speech one says 包丁を研ぐ (\"to sharpen a knife\") or 靴を磨く. In elevated language, 研磨 can also mean \"honing\" one's own skills, but 研鑽 or 切磋琢磨 are more usual for that.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"切削加工では不要な部分を刃物で除去する。","romaji":"Sessaku kakō de wa fuyō na bubun o hamono de jokyo suru.","german":"In machining, unnecessary portions are removed with a cutting tool.","furigana":"せっさくかこう|ふよう|ぶぶん|はもの|じょきょ"},{"japanese":"切削工具の刃が摩耗すると、加工の精度が落ちる。","romaji":"Sessaku kōgu no ha ga mamō suru to, kakō no seido ga ochiru.","german":"When the blade of a cutting tool wears out, machining accuracy drops.","furigana":"せっさくこうぐ|は|まもう|かこう|せいど|お"},{"japanese":"アルミのブロックを切削して、スマートフォンのケースを作る。","romaji":"Arumi no burokku o sessaku shite, sumātofon no kēsu o tsukuru.","german":"Smartphone cases are made by machining aluminum blocks.","furigana":"せっさく|つく"}],"notes":"切削 is \"machining\", i.e. the chip-removing processing of metal, wood or plastic with cutting tools (turning, milling, drilling). Typical: 切削加工, 切削工具, 切削油 (\"cutting oil\"), 切削する. Purely technical language of manufacturing engineering; everyday \"to cut\" is 切る, \"to shave off, to plane\" is 削る. Related are 研削 (\"grinding\") and 掘削 (\"excavation, drilling\" in earthworks).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"旋盤を使って円筒形の部品を加工する。","romaji":"Senban o tsukatte entōkei no buhin o kakō suru.","german":"Cylindrical parts are machined using a lathe.","furigana":"せんばん|つか|えんとうけい|ぶひん|かこう"},{"japanese":"祖父は四十年間、旋盤工として働いた。","romaji":"Sofu wa yonjūnenkan, senbankō to shite hataraita.","german":"My grandfather worked as a lathe operator for forty years.","furigana":"そふ|よんじゅうねんかん|せんばんこう|はたら"},{"japanese":"工場にNC旋盤を二台導入した。","romaji":"Kōjō ni NC senban o nidai dōnyū shita.","german":"The factory introduced two NC lathes.","furigana":"こうじょう|せんばん|にだいどうにゅう"}],"notes":"旋盤 is the \"lathe\": the workpiece rotates while a fixed cutting tool removes material. Typical: 旋盤で削る／加工する, 旋盤工 (\"lathe operator\"), NC旋盤 (\"CNC lathe\"). To be distinguished from the フライス盤 (\"milling machine\"), in which the tool rotates. The machines are counted with 台.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"数値制御工作機械によって複雑な形状の部品が製造できる。","romaji":"Sūchi seigyo kōsaku kikai ni yotte fukuzatsu na keijō no buhin ga seizō dekiru.","german":"Numerically controlled machine tools can manufacture parts with complex shapes.","furigana":"すうちせいぎょこうさくきかい|ふくざつ|けいじょう|ぶひん|せいぞう"},{"japanese":"工作機械の受注が前年より大きく増えた。","romaji":"Kōsaku kikai no juchū ga zennen yori ōkiku fueta.","german":"Orders for machine tools have increased greatly compared with the previous year.","furigana":"こうさくきかい|じゅちゅう|ぜんねん|おお|ふ"},{"japanese":"この町には工作機械の部品を作る工場が多い。","romaji":"Kono machi ni wa kōsaku kikai no buhin o tsukuru kōjō ga ōi.","german":"There are many factories in this town that make parts for machine tools.","furigana":"まち|こうさくきかい|ぶひん|つく|こうじょう|おお"}],"notes":"工作機械 is the umbrella term for \"machine tools\", i.e. machines that process metal and other materials - e.g. 旋盤, フライス盤 or ボール盤. Typical: 工作機械メーカー, 数値制御工作機械 or NC工作機械, 工作機械の受注. Do not confuse it with 工具 (\"hand tool\") or 機械工作 (\"machining\" as an activity). Japan is among the largest manufacturers, so the word often appears in business news.","levelNote":"Compound: 工作 (N1) + 機械 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"油圧シリンダーは大きな力を発生させることができる。","romaji":"Yuatsu shirindā wa ōkina chikara o hassei saseru koto ga dekiru.","german":"Hydraulic cylinders can generate great force.","furigana":"ゆあつ|おお|ちから|はっせい"},{"japanese":"工事現場で油圧ショベルが土を掘っている。","romaji":"Kōji genba de yuatsu shoberu ga tsuchi o hotte iru.","german":"A hydraulic excavator is digging soil at the construction site.","furigana":"こうじげんば|ゆあつ|つち|ほ"},{"japanese":"油圧の警告灯がついたら、すぐに車を止めてください。","romaji":"Yuatsu no keikokutō ga tsuitara, sugu ni kuruma o tomete kudasai.","german":"If the oil pressure warning light comes on, please stop the car immediately.","furigana":"ゆあつ|けいこくとう|くるま|と"}],"notes":"油圧 literally means \"oil pressure\" and in engineering stands for power transmission with oil, i.e. \"hydraulics\": 油圧シリンダー, 油圧ショベル (\"hydraulic excavator\"), 油圧式ブレーキ. In cars, 油圧 also means engine oil pressure (油圧計, 油圧警告灯). The pressure of other liquids is generally called 液圧, of water 水圧; the pneumatic counterpart is 空気圧.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"空圧機器は圧縮空気を動力源として利用する。","romaji":"Kūatsu kiki wa asshuku kūki o dōryokugen to shite riyō suru.","german":"Pneumatic equipment uses compressed air as its power source.","furigana":"くうあつきき|あっしゅくくうき|どうりょくげん|りよう"},{"japanese":"このロボットハンドは空圧で動いている。","romaji":"Kono robotto hando wa kūatsu de ugoite iru.","german":"This robot hand is operated by air pressure.","furigana":"くうあつ|うご"},{"japanese":"油圧に比べて、空圧は力が小さいが動きが速い。","romaji":"Yuatsu ni kurabete, kūatsu wa chikara ga chīsai ga ugoki ga hayai.","german":"Compared with hydraulics, pneumatics produces less force but moves faster.","furigana":"ゆあつ|くら|くうあつ|ちから|ちい|うご|はや"}],"notes":"空圧 is the engineering short form of 空気圧 and stands for \"compressed air, pneumatics\": 空圧機器, 空圧シリンダー, 空圧で動く. It is the counterpart to 油圧 (\"hydraulics\") - pneumatics is faster and cleaner, but weaker. In everyday speech, tire pressure is 空気圧 (タイヤの空気圧) and atmospheric air pressure is 気圧; 空圧 remains technical language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"歯車の噛み合わせによって回転運動を伝達する。","romaji":"Haguruma no kamiawase ni yotte kaiten undō o dentatsu suru.","german":"Rotary motion is transmitted by the meshing of gears.","furigana":"はぐるま|か|あ|かいてんうんどう|でんたつ"},{"japanese":"古い時計を開けると、小さな歯車がたくさん入っていた。","romaji":"Furui tokei o akeru to, chīsana haguruma ga takusan haitte ita.","german":"When I opened the old clock, it was full of small gears.","furigana":"ふる|とけい|あ|ちい|はぐるま|はい"},{"japanese":"自分は会社の歯車の一つにすぎないと感じることがある。","romaji":"Jibun wa kaisha no haguruma no hitotsu ni suginai to kanjiru koto ga aru.","german":"Sometimes I feel that I am just one cog in the company's machine.","furigana":"じぶん|かいしゃ|はぐるま|ひと|かん"}],"notes":"歯車 is the \"gear, cogwheel\": 歯車が噛み合う (\"the gears mesh\"), 歯車が回る. Figuratively it denotes a \"cog in the machine\" of an organization, often with a resigned undertone: 会社の歯車にすぎない. If a previously smooth process goes awry, one says 歯車が狂う. In engineering, ギア is also used for gears and gear stages.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"軸受は回転する軸を支持する機械要素である。","romaji":"Jikuuke wa kaiten suru jiku o shiji suru kikai yōso de aru.","german":"Lager sind Maschinenelemente, die rotierende Wellen stützen.","furigana":"じくうけ|かいてん|じく|しじ|きかいようそ"},{"japanese":"軸受が摩耗して、モーターから異音がする。","romaji":"Jikuuke ga mamō shite, mōtā kara ion ga suru.","german":"Das Lager ist verschlissen, deshalb macht der Motor seltsame Geräusche.","furigana":"じくうけ|まもう|いおん"},{"japanese":"自転車の車輪には玉軸受が使われている。","romaji":"Jitensha no sharin ni wa tama jikuuke ga tsukawarete iru.","german":"In Fahrradrädern werden Kugellager verwendet.","furigana":"じてんしゃ|しゃりん|たまじくうけ|つか"}],"notes":"軸受 ist das „Lager“ (Wellen- oder Achslager) im Maschinenbau, das eine drehende Welle (軸) trägt. Auch 軸受け geschrieben; in Industrie und Alltag sagt man oft ベアリング, besonders für Kugellager (玉軸受 bzw. ボールベアリング). Typisch: 軸受が摩耗する, すべり軸受 („Gleitlager“), 転がり軸受 („Wälzlager“). Achtung: Das deutsche „Lager“ im Sinne von Warenlager ist 倉庫 bzw. 在庫.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"適切な潤滑は機械の寿命を延ばす。","romaji":"Tekisetsu na junkatsu wa kikai no jumyō o nobasu.","german":"Proper lubrication extends the life of machines.","furigana":"てきせつ|じゅんかつ|きかい|じゅみょう|の"},{"japanese":"自転車のチェーンに潤滑油を差した。","romaji":"Jitensha no chēn ni junkatsuyu o sashita.","german":"I oiled the bicycle chain.","furigana":"じてんしゃ|じゅんかつゆ|さ"},{"japanese":"彼女は明るい性格で、職場の潤滑油のような存在だ。","romaji":"Kanojo wa akarui seikaku de, shokuba no junkatsuyu no yō na sonzai da.","german":"With her cheerful personality, she is like the lubricant of the workplace.","furigana":"かのじょ|あか|せいかく|しょくば|じゅんかつゆ|そんざい"}],"notes":"潤滑 means \"lubrication\" and, more generally, \"smoothness (absence of friction)\": 潤滑油 (\"lubricating oil\"), 潤滑剤 (\"lubricant\"), 潤滑不良. The figurative use of 潤滑油 for a person or thing that makes cooperation easier is very common: 彼はチームの潤滑油だ. For \"smooth\" in the sense of processes, on the other hand, one usually says 円滑な (円滑に進む), not 潤滑な.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この素材は耐久性に優れている。","romaji":"Kono sozai wa taikyūsei ni sugurete iru.","german":"This material has excellent durability.","furigana":"そざい|たいきゅうせい|すぐ"},{"japanese":"新しい車の耐久テストが行われた。","romaji":"Atarashii kuruma no taikyū tesuto ga okonawareta.","german":"An endurance test was carried out on the new car.","furigana":"あたら|くるま|たいきゅう|おこな"},{"japanese":"鈴鹿で八時間の耐久レースが開かれる。","romaji":"Suzuka de hachijikan no taikyū rēsu ga hirakareru.","german":"An eight-hour endurance race is held at Suzuka.","furigana":"すずか|はちじかん|たいきゅう|ひら"}],"notes":"耐久 means \"endurance, durability\" and almost never stands alone, but in compounds: 耐久性 (\"durability, longevity\"), 耐久力 (\"stamina, resilience\"), 耐久テスト (\"endurance test\"), 耐久レース (\"endurance race\"), 耐久消費財 (\"durable consumer goods\"). Typical is 耐久性に優れる／が高い. For a person's athletic stamina, 持久力 is more common.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"耐熱合金はジェットエンジンの部品に使用される。","romaji":"Tainetsu gōkin wa jetto enjin no buhin ni shiyō sareru.","german":"Heat-resistant alloys are used for jet engine parts.","furigana":"たいねつごうきん|ぶひん|しよう"},{"japanese":"耐熱ガラスの容器なら、電子レンジでも使える。","romaji":"Tainetsu garasu no yōki nara, denshi renji de mo tsukaeru.","german":"Containers made of heat-resistant glass can be used in the microwave too.","furigana":"たいねつ|ようき|でんし|つか"},{"japanese":"このプラスチックの耐熱温度は百度だ。","romaji":"Kono purasuchikku no tainetsu ondo wa hyakudo da.","german":"The heat resistance temperature of this plastic is 100 degrees.","furigana":"たいねつおんど|ひゃくど"}],"notes":"耐熱 means \"heat-resistant\" and usually stands as a prefix or with の before nouns: 耐熱ガラス, 耐熱容器, 耐熱性, 耐熱温度 (\"maximum heat-resistance temperature\"). In daily life it is often seen on kitchen utensils and packaging, e.g. 耐熱温度120℃. It belongs to the series 耐水 (\"waterproof\"), 耐震 (\"earthquake-resistant\"), 耐火 (\"fireproof\") - 耐火 stresses fire, 耐熱 high temperatures.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ステンレス鋼は耐食性が高い金属である。","romaji":"Sutenresu kō wa taishokusei ga takai kinzoku de aru.","german":"Stainless steel is a metal with high corrosion resistance.","furigana":"こう|たいしょくせい|たか|きんぞく"},{"japanese":"海の近くでは、耐食性の高い材料を使う必要がある。","romaji":"Umi no chikaku de wa, taishokusei no takai zairyō o tsukau hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"Near the sea, you need to use materials with high corrosion resistance.","furigana":"うみ|ちか|たいしょくせい|たか|ざいりょう|つか|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"チタンは軽くて耐食性に優れている。","romaji":"Chitan wa karukute taishokusei ni sugurete iru.","german":"Titanium is light and highly corrosion-resistant.","furigana":"かる|たいしょくせい|すぐ"}],"notes":"耐食 means \"corrosion-resistant\" and is purely materials-science terminology. It almost always appears in compounds: 耐食性 (\"corrosion resistance\"), 耐食性が高い／に優れる, 耐食合金, 耐食鋼. The spelling 耐蝕 also occurs. In everyday speech one would rather say 錆びにくい (\"hardly rusts\"); active \"corrosion protection\" is 防食 or 防錆.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鍛造によって金属の内部組織が緻密になる。","romaji":"Tanzō ni yotte kinzoku no naibu soshiki ga chimitsu ni naru.","german":"Forging makes the internal structure of the metal dense.","furigana":"たんぞう|きんぞく|ないぶそしき|ちみつ"},{"japanese":"日本刀は鉄を何度も打って鍛造される。","romaji":"Nihontō wa tetsu o nando mo utte tanzō sareru.","german":"Japanese swords are forged by hammering iron over and over.","furigana":"にほんとう|てつ|なんど|う|たんぞう"},{"japanese":"鍛造の部品は鋳造の部品より強度が高い。","romaji":"Tanzō no buhin wa chūzō no buhin yori kyōdo ga takai.","german":"Forged parts are stronger than cast parts.","furigana":"たんぞう|ぶひん|ちゅうぞう|ぶひん|きょうど|たか"}],"notes":"鍛造 is \"forging\": metal is heated and shaped by hammering or pressing, which makes it denser and stronger. As a する-verb it is transitive: 鉄を鍛造する. The contrast with 鋳造 (\"casting\"), in which liquid metal is poured into a mold, is important. Typical: 鍛造品, 鍛造部品, 鍛造ホイール, 刀の鍛造. The related verb is 鍛える (\"to forge; to train\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"焼入れ処理によって鋼の硬度が大幅に上がる。","romaji":"Yakiire shori ni yotte hagane no kōdo ga ōhaba ni agaru.","german":"Quenching greatly increases the hardness of steel.","furigana":"やきい|しょり|はがね|こうど|おおはば|あ"},{"japanese":"日本刀は焼入れによって刃の部分だけが硬くなる。","romaji":"Nihontō wa yakiire ni yotte ha no bubun dake ga kataku naru.","german":"In a Japanese sword, quenching makes only the edge hard.","furigana":"にほんとう|やきい|は|ぶぶん|かた"},{"japanese":"鋼を八百度以上に熱してから、水で急に冷やして焼入れする。","romaji":"Hagane o happyakudo ijō ni nesshite kara, mizu de kyū ni hiyashite yakiire suru.","german":"You heat the steel to over 800 degrees, then cool it rapidly in water to quench it.","furigana":"はがね|はっぴゃくどいじょう|ねっ|みず|きゅう|ひ|やきい"}],"notes":"焼入れ is the \"hardening\" (quenching) of steel: the metal is heated strongly and then cooled quickly in water or oil. Also written 焼き入れ; as a verb 焼入れする or 焼きを入れる. It is usually followed by 焼戻し (\"tempering\") to reduce brittleness. Colloquially, 焼きを入れる also means \"to straighten someone out, to give someone a good talking-to\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"焼戻しを行うことで焼入れ後の脆さを改善する。","romaji":"Yakimodoshi o okonau koto de yakiire go no morosa o kaizen suru.","german":"Tempering improves the brittleness left after quenching.","furigana":"やきもど|おこな|やきい|ご|もろ|かいぜん"},{"japanese":"焼戻しの温度によって、鋼の硬さと粘り強さが変わる。","romaji":"Yakimodoshi no ondo ni yotte, hagane no katasa to nebarizuyosa ga kawaru.","german":"The hardness and toughness of steel change depending on the tempering temperature.","furigana":"やきもど|おんど|はがね|かた|ねば|づよ|か"},{"japanese":"焼入れした刃物は、必ず焼戻ししてから仕上げる。","romaji":"Yakiire shita hamono wa, kanarazu yakimodoshi shite kara shiageru.","german":"Quenched blades are always tempered before being finished.","furigana":"やきい|はもの|かなら|やきもど|しあ"}],"notes":"焼戻し is the \"tempering\" of hardened steel: after 焼入れ, the metal is heated again to a moderate temperature to reduce brittleness and stress and gain toughness. Also written 焼き戻し; verb: 焼戻しする. Do not confuse it with 焼なまし or 焼鈍 (\"annealing\"). The four basic heat-treatment processes are 焼入れ・焼戻し・焼なまし・焼ならし.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"焼鈍は金属の内部応力を除去する熱処理である。","romaji":"Shōdon wa kinzoku no naibu ōryoku o jokyo suru netsu shori de aru.","german":"Annealing is a heat treatment that removes internal stress in metal.","furigana":"しょうどん|きんぞく|ないぶおうりょく|じょきょ|ねつしょり"},{"japanese":"冷間加工で硬くなった銅線を焼鈍して柔らかくする。","romaji":"Reikan kakō de kataku natta dōsen o shōdon shite yawarakaku suru.","german":"Copper wire hardened by cold working is annealed to soften it.","furigana":"れいかんかこう|かた|どうせん|しょうどん|やわ"},{"japanese":"鋼板は焼鈍炉でゆっくり冷やされる。","romaji":"Kōhan wa shōdonro de yukkuri hiyasareru.","german":"Steel sheets are cooled slowly in an annealing furnace.","furigana":"こうはん|しょうどんろ|ひ"}],"notes":"焼鈍 is the written technical term for the \"annealing\" (soft annealing) of metal: it is heated and cooled slowly so that it becomes softer and internal stresses disappear. In the workshop one usually says 焼なまし (also written 焼鈍し); しょうどん is the Sino-Japanese reading used in technical texts and in compounds such as 焼鈍炉 (\"annealing furnace\"). The counterpart is 焼入れ, which makes metal hard.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"圧延によって金属板の厚みを均一にする。","romaji":"Atsuen ni yotte kinzoku ban no atsumi o kin'itsu ni suru.","german":"Rolling makes the thickness of the metal plate uniform.","furigana":"あつえん|きんぞくばん|あつ|きんいつ"},{"japanese":"アルミホイルは、アルミニウムを薄く圧延して作られる。","romaji":"Arumihoiru wa, aruminiumu o usuku atsuen shite tsukurareru.","german":"Aluminum foil is made by rolling aluminum thin.","furigana":"うす|あつえん|つく"},{"japanese":"製鉄所では熱間圧延と冷間圧延の両方を行っている。","romaji":"Seitetsujo de wa nekkan atsuen to reikan atsuen no ryōhō o okonatte iru.","german":"The steelworks does both hot rolling and cold rolling.","furigana":"せいてつじょ|ねっかんあつえん|れいかんあつえん|りょうほう|おこな"}],"notes":"圧延 is the \"rolling\" of metal between rotating rolls to shape it into sheets, plates or profiles: 圧延する, 圧延機 (\"rolling mill\"), 熱間圧延 (\"hot rolling\"), 冷間圧延 (\"cold rolling\"), 圧延鋼板. Technical language of the steel and aluminum industries. Related forming processes are 鍛造 (\"forging\"), 押出し (\"extrusion\") and 引抜き (\"drawing\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"引抜き加工で細い金属線を製造する。","romaji":"Hikinuki kakō de hosoi kinzoku sen o seizō suru.","german":"Thin metal wire is produced by drawing.","furigana":"ひきぬ|かこう|ほそ|きんぞくせん|せいぞう"},{"japanese":"銅線は引抜きを何度も繰り返して細くしていく。","romaji":"Dōsen wa hikinuki o nando mo kurikaeshite hosoku shite iku.","german":"Copper wire is made thinner by repeating the drawing many times.","furigana":"どうせん|ひきぬ|なんど|く|かえ|ほそ"},{"japanese":"ライバル会社による引抜きで、優秀な社員が三人辞めた。","romaji":"Raibaru gaisha ni yoru hikinuki de, yūshū na shain ga sannin yameta.","german":"Because a rival company poached them, three capable employees quit.","furigana":"がいしゃ|ひきぬ|ゆうしゅう|しゃいん|さんにんや"}],"notes":"引抜き literally means \"pulling out\". In metalworking it is the \"drawing\" of wire, pipes or rods through a die, which reduces the cross-section: 引抜き加工, 引抜き管. Far more common in everyday life is the meaning \"poaching\" of staff or players: ライバル会社による引き抜き. In this meaning it is usually written 引き抜き; the corresponding verb is 引き抜く (\"to pull out; to poach\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"プレス加工で金属板を所定の形状に成形する。","romaji":"Puresu kakō de kinzoku ban o shotei no keijō ni seikei suru.","german":"Metal sheets are formed into the specified shape by press working.","furigana":"かこう|きんぞくばん|しょてい|けいじょう|せいけい"},{"japanese":"自動車のボディーは大型のプレス機で成形される。","romaji":"Jidōsha no bodī wa ōgata no puresuki de seikei sareru.","german":"Car bodies are formed with large presses.","furigana":"じどうしゃ|おおがた|き|せいけい"},{"japanese":"会社は新製品についてプレスリリースを出した。","romaji":"Kaisha wa shinseihin ni tsuite puresu rirīsu o dashita.","german":"The company issued a press release about the new product.","furigana":"かいしゃ|しんせいひん|だ"}],"notes":"プレス in engineering means the \"pressing, stamping\" of sheet metal and also the \"press\" as a machine: プレス加工, プレス機, プレス金型, プレスする. In manufacturing, プレス加工 is so common that プレス alone often means this process. In everyday speech the word has other meanings: the \"press\" as media (プレスリリース, プレス発表) and the pressing of clothes (ズボンをプレスする).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"放電加工は硬い材料でも精密に加工できる。","romaji":"Hōden kakō wa katai zairyō de mo seimitsu ni kakō dekiru.","german":"Electrical discharge machining can precisely machine even hard materials.","furigana":"ほうでんかこう|かた|ざいりょう|せいみつ|かこう"},{"japanese":"金型の細かい形状は放電加工で仕上げる。","romaji":"Kanagata no komakai keijō wa hōden kakō de shiageru.","german":"Fine contours of molds are finished by electrical discharge machining.","furigana":"かながた|こま|けいじょう|ほうでんかこう|しあ"},{"japanese":"ワイヤー放電加工機を使えば、複雑な形も正確に切断できる。","romaji":"Waiyā hōden kakōki o tsukaeba, fukuzatsu na katachi mo seikaku ni setsudan dekiru.","german":"With a wire EDM machine, even complicated shapes can be cut accurately.","furigana":"ほうでんかこうき|つか|ふくざつ|かたち|せいかく|せつだん"}],"notes":"放電加工 is \"electrical discharge machining\" (EDM): material is removed by electrical discharges (放電) between an electrode and the workpiece. This allows precise machining even of very hard, conductive materials such as hardened steel, for example when making 金型. Typical: 放電加工機, ワイヤー放電加工 (\"wire EDM\"), 形彫り放電加工 (\"sinker EDM\"). Purely technical language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"レーザー加工は微細な切断やマーキングに適している。","romaji":"Rēzā kakō wa bisai na setsudan ya mākingu ni tekishite iru.","german":"Laser machining is suited to fine cutting and marking.","furigana":"かこう|びさい|せつだん|てき"},{"japanese":"名前をレーザー加工で革の財布に刻んでもらった。","romaji":"Namae o rēzā kakō de kawa no saifu ni kizande moratta.","german":"I had my name engraved on a leather wallet by laser.","furigana":"なまえ|かこう|かわ|さいふ|きざ"},{"japanese":"この工場ではレーザー加工機で薄い鉄板を切断している。","romaji":"Kono kōjō de wa rēzā kakōki de usui teppan o setsudan shite iru.","german":"This factory cuts thin steel plates with a laser machine.","furigana":"こうじょう|かこうき|うす|てっぱん|せつだん"}],"notes":"レーザー加工 is \"laser machining\" with focused laser light: cutting (レーザー切断), welding (レーザー溶接), drilling and marking (レーザーマーキング). It works contact-free and very finely and is used for sheet metal, electronics and even personalized gift items. In industrial standards, the spelling レーザ without the long-vowel mark is also common. Typical: レーザー加工機, レーザー加工で切る／彫る.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"プラスチック製品の多くは射出成形で製造される。","romaji":"Purasuchikku seihin no ōku wa shashutsu seikei de seizō sareru.","german":"Many plastic products are made by injection molding.","furigana":"せいひん|おお|しゃしゅつせいけい|せいぞう"},{"japanese":"このおもちゃの部品は、射出成形で一度に大量に作られる。","romaji":"Kono omocha no buhin wa, shashutsu seikei de ichido ni tairyō ni tsukurareru.","german":"The parts of this toy are made in large quantities at once by injection molding.","furigana":"ぶひん|しゃしゅつせいけい|いちど|たいりょう|つく"},{"japanese":"射出成形では、金型の温度管理が品質を大きく左右する。","romaji":"Shashutsu seikei de wa, kanagata no ondo kanri ga hinshitsu o ōkiku sayū suru.","german":"In injection molding, the temperature control of the mold greatly affects quality.","furigana":"しゃしゅつせいけい|かながた|おんどかんり|ひんしつ|おお|さゆう"}],"notes":"射出成形 is \"injection molding\": molten plastic is injected (射出) under high pressure into a 金型 and shaped (成形) there. Typical: 射出成形機, 射出成形品, 射出成形で作る. The spelling 射出成型 also occurs, but 成形 is the usual form. Related processes are 押出成形 (\"extrusion\") and ブロー成形 (\"blow molding\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"精密な金型が製品の品質を左右する。","romaji":"Seimitsu na kanagata ga seihin no hinshitsu o sayū suru.","german":"Precision molds determine the quality of the product.","furigana":"せいみつ|かながた|せいひん|ひんしつ|さゆう"},{"japanese":"新しい金型を作るには数か月かかる。","romaji":"Atarashii kanagata o tsukuru ni wa sūkagetsu kakaru.","german":"It takes several months to make a new die.","furigana":"あたら|かながた|つく|すう|げつ"},{"japanese":"プラスチックのコップは、金型に溶かした樹脂を入れて作る。","romaji":"Purasuchikku no koppu wa, kanagata ni tokashita jushi o irete tsukuru.","german":"Plastic cups are made by putting molten resin into a mold.","furigana":"かながた|と|じゅし|い|つく"}],"notes":"金型 is a \"metal mold\" or \"die\" in the sense of manufacturing engineering: stamping and pressing dies as well as die-casting and injection molds. Typical: 金型を作る／設計する, 金型メーカー, プレス金型, 射出成形用金型. The reading is かながた, not きんがた. The more general term is 型 (\"mold, form\"); 鋳型 is specifically the casting mold in 鋳造.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"メッキなどの表面処理で耐食性を向上させる。","romaji":"Mekki nado no hyōmen shori de taishokusei o kōjō saseru.","german":"Corrosion resistance is improved by surface treatments such as plating.","furigana":"ひょうめんしょり|たいしょくせい|こうじょう"},{"japanese":"このフライパンには焦げ付きにくい表面処理が施されている。","romaji":"Kono furaipan ni wa kogetsukinikui hyōmen shori ga hodokosarete iru.","german":"This frying pan has a surface treatment that resists burning on.","furigana":"こ|つ|ひょうめんしょり|ほどこ"},{"japanese":"塗装の前に、部品の表面処理をしっかり行う。","romaji":"Tosō no mae ni, buhin no hyōmen shori o shikkari okonau.","german":"Before painting, the surfaces of the parts are thoroughly treated.","furigana":"とそう|まえ|ぶひん|ひょうめんしょり|おこな"}],"notes":"表面処理 is the \"surface treatment\" of materials to improve corrosion protection, hardness, appearance or adhesion. It includes メッキ (\"plating\"), 塗装 (\"painting\"), アルマイト (\"anodizing\"), 蒸着 or 溶射. Typical: 表面処理を施す, 表面処理加工, 表面処理技術. Technical language of the metal, plastics and electronics industries, but also found in product descriptions.","levelNote":"Compound: 表面 (N3) + 処理 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"保護被膜を施して金属の劣化を防ぐ。","romaji":"Hogo himaku o hodokoshite kinzoku no rekka o fusegu.","german":"A protective coating is applied to prevent metal deterioration.","furigana":"ほごひまく|ほどこ|きんぞく|れっか|ふせ"},{"japanese":"アルミニウムの表面には薄い酸化被膜ができ、さびを防ぐ。","romaji":"Aruminiumu no hyōmen ni wa usui sanka himaku ga deki, sabi o fusegu.","german":"A thin oxide film forms on the surface of aluminum and prevents rust.","furigana":"ひょうめん|うす|さんかひまく|ふせ"},{"japanese":"ワックスで車のボディーに被膜を作る。","romaji":"Wakkusu de kuruma no bodī ni himaku o tsukuru.","german":"Wax is used to form a coating on the car body.","furigana":"くるま|ひまく|つく"}],"notes":"被膜 is a thin \"coating, layer, film\" covering a surface: 保護被膜, 酸化被膜 (\"oxide layer\"), 被膜を形成する／施す. In biology it also means \"capsule, envelope\". The identically read 皮膜 is very similar and is also used for coatings; in engineering both occur. More everyday are コーティング and 膜.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"真空蒸着によって薄膜を形成する技術が開発された。","romaji":"Shinkū jōchaku ni yotte hakumaku o keisei suru gijutsu ga kaihatsu sareta.","german":"A technique for forming thin films by vacuum deposition was developed.","furigana":"しんくうじょうちゃく|はくまく|けいせい|ぎじゅつ|かいはつ"},{"japanese":"ポテトチップスの袋の内側にはアルミが蒸着されている。","romaji":"Poteto chippusu no fukuro no uchigawa ni wa arumi ga jōchaku sarete iru.","german":"The inside of a potato chip bag is vapor-coated with aluminum.","furigana":"ふくろ|うちがわ|じょうちゃく"},{"japanese":"カメラのレンズには反射を防ぐ膜が蒸着してある。","romaji":"Kamera no renzu ni wa hansha o fusegu maku ga jōchaku shite aru.","german":"An anti-reflection coating is vapor-deposited on camera lenses.","furigana":"はんしゃ|ふせ|まく|じょうちゃく"}],"notes":"蒸着 is \"vapor deposition\": a material is evaporated in a vacuum and condenses as a thin layer on a surface. Typical: 真空蒸着, 蒸着膜, アルミ蒸着, 蒸着する／蒸着される. It is found in mirrors, lens coatings, semiconductors and snack packaging. In pop culture, 蒸着 is known as the transformation call from the series 宇宙刑事ギャバン.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"溶射技術で部品の表面に耐摩耗性の皮膜を形成する。","romaji":"Yōsha gijutsu de buhin no hyōmen ni taimamōsei no himaku o keisei suru.","german":"Thermal spraying forms a wear-resistant coating on the surface of parts.","furigana":"ようしゃぎじゅつ|ぶひん|ひょうめん|たいまもうせい|ひまく|けいせい"},{"japanese":"橋の鉄骨に亜鉛を溶射して、さびを防いでいる。","romaji":"Hashi no tekkotsu ni aen o yōsha shite, sabi o fuseide iru.","german":"Zinc is thermally sprayed onto the steel frame of the bridge to prevent rust.","furigana":"はし|てっこつ|あえん|ようしゃ|ふせ"},{"japanese":"タービンの羽根にはセラミックが溶射されている。","romaji":"Tābin no hane ni wa seramikku ga yōsha sarete iru.","german":"The turbine blades are thermally sprayed with ceramic.","furigana":"はね|ようしゃ"}],"notes":"溶射 is \"thermal spraying\": metal, ceramic or the like is melted and sprayed as a fine jet onto a surface, where it forms a protective layer. Typical: 溶射皮膜, プラズマ溶射, 溶射する, 溶射加工. It is used for wear, heat and corrosion protection, e.g. on turbine blades or bridges. Do not confuse it with 溶接 (\"welding\") - only the first kanji 溶 (\"to melt\") is the same.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"非破壊検査で部品内部の欠陥を検出する。","romaji":"Hi hakai kensa de buhin naibu no kekkan o kenshutsu suru.","german":"Internal defects in parts are detected by non-destructive testing.","furigana":"ひはかいけんさ|ぶひんないぶ|けっかん|けんしゅつ"},{"japanese":"飛行機は定期的に非破壊検査を受けている。","romaji":"Hikōki wa teikiteki ni hihakai kensa o ukete iru.","german":"Airplanes undergo non-destructive testing regularly.","furigana":"ひこうき|ていきてき|ひはかいけんさ|う"},{"japanese":"古いトンネルのコンクリートを非破壊検査で調べた。","romaji":"Furui tonneru no konkurīto o hihakai kensa de shirabeta.","german":"We examined the concrete of the old tunnel by non-destructive testing.","furigana":"ふる|ひはかいけんさ|しら"}],"notes":"非破壊検査 is \"non-destructive testing\": components are examined without being taken apart or damaged - for example with 超音波, X-rays, magnetic particles or penetrants. The word is composed of 非 (\"non-\"), 破壊 (\"destruction\") and 検査 (\"inspection\"). Typical for aircraft, bridges, pipelines and power plants: 非破壊検査を行う／実施する. In technical language also 非破壊試験.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"超音波を利用して金属内部の亀裂を検出する。","romaji":"Chōonpa o riyō shite kinzoku naibu no kiretsu o kenshutsu suru.","german":"Ultrasound is used to detect cracks inside metal.","furigana":"ちょうおんぱ|りよう|きんぞくないぶ|きれつ|けんしゅつ"},{"japanese":"病院で超音波検査を受けたら、胆石が見つかった。","romaji":"Byōin de chōonpa kensa o uketara, tanseki ga mitsukatta.","german":"I had an ultrasound examination at the hospital and gallstones were found.","furigana":"びょういん|ちょうおんぱけんさ|う|たんせき|み"},{"japanese":"コウモリは超音波を出して、周りの様子を知る。","romaji":"Kōmori wa chōonpa o dashite, mawari no yōsu o shiru.","german":"Bats emit ultrasound to sense their surroundings.","furigana":"ちょうおんぱ|だ|まわ|ようす|し"}],"notes":"超音波 is \"ultrasound\", i.e. sound above the range of human hearing. In everyday life it is encountered mainly in medicine: 超音波検査 (\"ultrasound examination\"), colloquially エコー (エコー検査). Other combinations: 超音波洗浄機 (\"ultrasonic cleaner\"), 超音波を出す／当てる. Do not confuse it with 超音速 (\"supersonic speed\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"各種センサーが環境データをリアルタイムで収集する。","romaji":"Kakushu sensā ga kankyō dēta o riaru taimu de shūshū suru.","german":"Various sensors collect environmental data in real time.","furigana":"かくしゅ|かんきょう|しゅうしゅう"},{"japanese":"玄関のライトは人感センサーで自動的につく。","romaji":"Genkan no raito wa jinkan sensā de jidōteki ni tsuku.","german":"The entrance light comes on automatically with a motion sensor.","furigana":"げんかん|じんかん|じどうてき"},{"japanese":"指紋センサーに指を当てて、スマホのロックを解除する。","romaji":"Shimon sensā ni yubi o atete, sumaho no rokku o kaijo suru.","german":"I put my finger on the fingerprint sensor to unlock my smartphone.","furigana":"しもん|ゆび|あ|かいじょ"}],"notes":"センサー is a \"sensor, detector\" and is very common in daily life as well as in engineering: 人感センサー (\"motion sensor\"), 温度センサー, 指紋センサー, センサーが反応する／作動する. A lamp with a motion detector is called a センサーライト. In technical standards the spelling センサ without the long-vowel mark is also found. The Japanese technical word 検出器 (\"detector\") is more formal and less common.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"圧電変換器は振動を電気信号に変換する。","romaji":"Atsuden henkanki wa shindō o denki shingō ni henkan suru.","german":"A piezoelectric transducer converts vibrations into electrical signals.","furigana":"あつでんへんかんき|しんどう|でんきしんごう|へんかん"},{"japanese":"AD変換器は、アナログ信号をデジタル信号に変える。","romaji":"AD henkanki wa, anarogu shingō o dejitaru shingō ni kaeru.","german":"An A/D converter changes analog signals into digital signals.","furigana":"へんかんき|しんごう|しんごう|か"},{"japanese":"古いゲーム機をテレビにつなぐには、映像信号の変換器が必要だ。","romaji":"Furui gēmuki o terebi ni tsunagu ni wa, eizō shingō no henkanki ga hitsuyō da.","german":"To connect an old game console to a TV, you need a video signal converter.","furigana":"ふる|き|えいぞうしんごう|へんかんき|ひつよう"}],"notes":"変換器 is a technical device that converts a quantity or signal into another form: \"transducer, converter\". Typical in compounds: AD変換器 (\"A/D converter\"), 圧電変換器, 周波数変換器. In everyday speech one often says コンバーター or 変換アダプター; when traveling, the device for voltage is called 変圧器 (\"transformer\"). The corresponding verb is 変換する (\"to convert\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"微弱な信号を増幅して処理可能な大きさにする。","romaji":"Bijaku na shingō o zōfuku shite shori kanō na ōkisa ni suru.","german":"A weak signal is amplified to a size that can be processed.","furigana":"びじゃく|しんごう|ぞうふく|しょりかのう|おお"},{"japanese":"ギターの音はアンプで増幅されてスピーカーから出る。","romaji":"Gitā no oto wa anpu de zōfuku sarete supīkā kara deru.","german":"The guitar's sound is amplified by the amp and comes out of the speaker.","furigana":"おと|ぞうふく|で"},{"japanese":"ネットのうわさが人々の不安を増幅させた。","romaji":"Netto no uwasa ga hitobito no fuan o zōfuku saseta.","german":"Rumors on the internet amplified people's anxiety.","furigana":"ひとびと|ふあん|ぞうふく"}],"notes":"増幅 is the \"amplification\" of a signal or vibration: 信号を増幅する, 増幅器 (\"amplifier\", in everyday speech アンプ), 増幅回路. Figuratively it is often used for feelings or problems that build up: 不安が増幅する, 誤解を増幅させる. It is used transitively (〜を増幅する) and intransitively (〜が増幅する). Distinction: 強化 is \"strengthening\" in the sense of building up (警備を強化する), not of signals.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"周波数変調は雑音に強い通信方式である。","romaji":"Shūhasū henchō wa zatsuon ni tsuyoi tsūshin hōshiki de aru.","german":"Frequency modulation is a communication method that is resistant to noise.","furigana":"しゅうはすうへんちょう|ざつおん|つよ|つうしんほうしき"},{"japanese":"犯人は声を変調して電話をかけてきた。","romaji":"Hannin wa koe o henchō shite denwa o kakete kita.","german":"The culprit called with a modulated voice.","furigana":"はんにん|こえ|へんちょう|でんわ"},{"japanese":"最近、体に変調を感じたので病院に行った。","romaji":"Saikin, karada ni henchō o kanjita node byōin ni itta.","german":"I felt something off in my body recently, so I went to the hospital.","furigana":"さいきん|からだ|へんちょう|かん|びょういん|い"}],"notes":"変調 has two faces: in engineering it is the \"modulation\" of a signal (振幅変調 AM, 周波数変調 FM); 変調する is transitive there (信号を変調する). In everyday life, on the other hand, it means a \"disturbance, irregularity\" in the normal state, mainly in fixed phrases such as 体調に変調を来す \"to get thrown off in terms of health\" or 変調を感じる. The counterpart in engineering is 復調 \"demodulation\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"受信機で変調された信号を復調して元の情報を取り出す。","romaji":"Jushinki de henchō sareta shingō o fukuchō shite moto no jōhō o toridasu.","german":"The receiver demodulates the modulated signal to extract the original information.","furigana":"じゅしんき|へんちょう|しんごう|ふくちょう|もと|じょうほう|と|だ"},{"japanese":"けがから戻ったエースが、ようやく復調の兆しを見せている。","romaji":"Kega kara modotta ēsu ga, yōyaku fukuchō no kizashi o misete iru.","german":"The ace, back from injury, is finally showing signs of regaining form.","furigana":"もど|ふくちょう|きざ|み"},{"japanese":"輸出の回復で、景気は緩やかに復調している。","romaji":"Yushutsu no kaifuku de, keiki wa yuruyaka ni fukuchō shite iru.","german":"Thanks to the recovery in exports, the economy is gradually rebounding.","furigana":"ゆしゅつ|かいふく|けいき|ゆる|ふくちょう"}],"notes":"復調 is \"demodulation\" in engineering, the counterpart to 変調: 信号を復調する. The word is read far more often in sports and economic news in the sense of \"getting back into form, recovering\": エースが復調する, 景気が復調する. In this meaning 復調する is intransitive; 回復 is more general (also illness), while 復調 stresses the return to one's usual performance.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"通信データの暗号化によってセキュリティを確保する。","romaji":"Tsūshin dēta no angōka ni yotte sekyuriti o kakuho suru.","german":"Security is ensured by encrypting communication data.","furigana":"つうしん|あんごうか|かくほ"},{"japanese":"大切なファイルは暗号化して保存しましょう。","romaji":"Taisetsu na fairu wa angōka shite hozon shimashō.","german":"Let's save important files in encrypted form.","furigana":"たいせつ|あんごうか|ほぞん"},{"japanese":"このアプリのメッセージはすべて暗号化されている。","romaji":"Kono apuri no messēji wa subete angōka sarete iru.","german":"All messages in this app are encrypted.","furigana":"あんごうか"}],"notes":"暗号化 is \"encryption\", from 暗号 \"code, cipher\" and the suffix 化 \"-ization\". As a する-verb it is transitive: データを暗号化する; the state is 暗号化されている. The counterpart is 復号 or 復号化 \"decryption with the key\", while 解読 means cracking a code without the key. Related: 暗号資産 \"cryptocurrency\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"正しい鍵を使って暗号文を復号する。","romaji":"Tadashii kagi o tsukatte angōbun o fukugō suru.","german":"The ciphertext is decrypted using the correct key.","furigana":"ただ|かぎ|つか|あんごうぶん|ふくごう"},{"japanese":"パスワードを忘れたので、ファイルを復号できない。","romaji":"Pasuwādo o wasureta node, fairu o fukugō dekinai.","german":"I forgot the password, so I cannot decrypt the file.","furigana":"わす|ふくごう"},{"japanese":"暗号化と復号に同じ鍵を使う方式を共通鍵暗号という。","romaji":"Angōka to fukugō ni onaji kagi o tsukau hōshiki o kyōtsūkagi angō to iu.","german":"A method that uses the same key for encryption and decryption is called symmetric-key cryptography.","furigana":"あんごうか|ふくごう|おな|かぎ|つか|ほうしき|きょうつうかぎあんごう"}],"notes":"復号 is the technical word for \"decryption, decoding\" with the matching key: 暗号文を復号する. In everyday IT speech one also hears 復号化, analogous to 暗号化, although specialists often prefer the shorter 復号. Do not confuse it with 解読, the deciphering or cracking of a code without the key (暗号を解読する), nor with the identically read 複合 \"combination, complex\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"光ファイバーによるデータ伝送は高速で大容量である。","romaji":"Hikari faibā ni yoru dēta densō wa kōsoku de daiyōryō de aru.","german":"Data transmission over optical fiber is fast and has high capacity.","furigana":"ひかり|でんそう|こうそく|だいようりょう"},{"japanese":"この古い規格は伝送速度が遅いのが欠点だ。","romaji":"Kono furui kikaku wa densō sokudo ga osoi no ga ketten da.","german":"The drawback of this old standard is its slow transmission speed.","furigana":"ふる|きかく|でんそうそくど|おそ|けってん"},{"japanese":"決勝戦の映像は衛星を通じて世界中に伝送された。","romaji":"Kesshōsen no eizō wa eisei o tsūjite sekaijū ni densō sareta.","german":"Footage of the final was transmitted around the world via satellite.","furigana":"けっしょうせん|えいぞう|えいせい|つう|せかいじゅう|でんそう"}],"notes":"伝送 is a technical term for the \"transmission\" of signals, data or energy over lines and by radio: データ伝送, 伝送速度, 伝送路. As a する-verb it is transitive (映像を伝送する). In contrast, 伝達 means the passing on of information between people (情報を伝達する), and 送信 the sending of a message or signal.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"光ファイバーは通信インフラの根幹を成す技術である。","romaji":"Hikari faibā wa tsūshin infura no konkan o nasu gijutsu de aru.","german":"Optical fiber is a technology that forms the backbone of communications infrastructure.","furigana":"ひかり|つうしん|こんかん|な|ぎじゅつ"},{"japanese":"マンションに光ファイバーが引かれて、ネットが速くなった。","romaji":"Manshon ni hikari faibā ga hikarete, netto ga hayaku natta.","german":"Since optical fiber was laid in our apartment building, the internet has become much faster.","furigana":"ひかり|ひ|はや"},{"japanese":"光ファイバーは髪の毛ほどの細さのガラスでできている。","romaji":"Hikari faibā wa kami no ke hodo no hososa no garasu de dekite iru.","german":"An optical fiber is made of glass about as thin as a hair.","furigana":"ひかり|かみ|け|ほそ"}],"notes":"光ファイバー is \"optical fiber\", a hair-thin light guide made of glass or plastic; in standards and technical texts the short spelling 光ファイバ without the long-vowel mark is common. For a home fiber internet connection one usually says 光回線 or simply 光 (光にする \"to switch to fiber\"). Typical combinations: 光ファイバーケーブル, 光ファイバー通信.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"クラウド基盤の整備がデジタル変革を支えている。","romaji":"Kuraudo kiban no seibi ga dejitaru henkaku o sasaete iru.","german":"Developing cloud infrastructure supports digital transformation.","furigana":"きばん|せいび|へんかく|ささ"},{"japanese":"まずは仕事を見つけて、生活の基盤を固めたい。","romaji":"Mazu wa shigoto o mitsukete, seikatsu no kiban o katametai.","german":"First, I want to find a job and solidify the foundation of my life.","furigana":"しごと|み|せいかつ|きばん|かた"},{"japanese":"この政党は農村部に強い支持基盤を持っている。","romaji":"Kono seitō wa nōsonbu ni tsuyoi shiji kiban o motte iru.","german":"This party has a strong base of support in rural areas.","furigana":"せいとう|のうそんぶ|つよ|しじきばん|も"}],"notes":"基盤 is the supporting \"foundation, base\" of something larger: 生活の基盤, 経営基盤, 支持基盤 \"base of support\", in IT also \"platform, infrastructure\" (クラウド基盤). Typical verbs are 基盤を築く, 基盤を固める, 基盤となる. 基礎 is rather the groundwork on which one builds (基礎知識), while 基盤 emphasizes the stable base of a system. The circuit board is usually written 基板.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しいアルゴリズムをソフトウェアに実装した。","romaji":"Atarashii arugorizumu o sofutowea ni jissō shita.","german":"I implemented the new algorithm in the software.","furigana":"あたら|じっそう"},{"japanese":"この機能は次のアップデートで実装される予定です。","romaji":"Kono kinō wa tsugi no appudēto de jissō sareru yotei desu.","german":"This feature is scheduled to be implemented in the next update.","furigana":"きのう|つぎ|じっそう|よてい"},{"japanese":"工場では部品を基板に実装する工程を自動化した。","romaji":"Kōjō de wa buhin o kiban ni jissō suru kōtei o jidōka shita.","german":"At the factory, we automated the process of mounting parts onto circuit boards.","furigana":"こうじょう|ぶひん|きばん|じっそう|こうてい|じどうか"}],"notes":"実装 means \"implementation\" in software (機能を実装する \"to build in a function\") and in electronics the \"mounting, assembly\" of components on a circuit board (部品を基板に実装する). As a する-verb it is transitive, often with に for the target. 導入, in contrast, is the introduction of a system in an organization; in politics and research one has recently also heard 社会実装 \"putting into practice in society\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"組込みシステムは家電製品から自動車まで幅広く使われている。","romaji":"Kumikomi shisutemu wa kaden seihin kara jidōsha made habahiroku tsukawarete iru.","german":"Embedded systems are widely used, from home appliances to cars.","furigana":"くみこ|かでんせいひん|じどうしゃ|はばひろ|つか"},{"japanese":"組込みエンジニアの求人が年々増えている。","romaji":"Kumikomi enjinia no kyūjin ga nennen fuete iru.","german":"Job openings for embedded engineers are increasing year by year.","furigana":"くみこ|きゅうじん|ねんねんふ"},{"japanese":"表計算ソフトの組込み関数を使えば、合計はすぐに出せる。","romaji":"Hyōkeisan sofuto no kumikomi kansū o tsukaeba, gōkei wa sugu ni daseru.","german":"If you use the spreadsheet's built-in functions, you can get the total right away.","furigana":"ひょうけいさん|くみこ|かんすう|つか|ごうけい|だ"}],"notes":"組込み is the noun form of 組み込む \"to build in, to incorporate\" and in IT stands mostly before nouns in the sense of \"embedded, built-in\": 組込みシステム \"embedded system\", 組込み関数 \"built-in function\", 組込みエンジニア. The spelling varies: 組込み is usual in technical terms, otherwise mostly 組み込み. For hardware permanently built into a device, one rather says 内蔵 (カメラ内蔵).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ファームウェアの更新で機器の性能が改善される。","romaji":"Fāmuwea no kōshin de kiki no seinō ga kaizen sareru.","german":"Updating the firmware improves the device's performance.","furigana":"こうしん|きき|せいのう|かいぜん"},{"japanese":"更新中はルーターの電源を切らないでください。ファームウェアが壊れるおそれがあります。","romaji":"Kōshinchū wa rūtā no dengen o kiranaide kudasai. Fāmuwea ga kowareru osore ga arimasu.","german":"Do not turn off the router's power during the update. The firmware may be corrupted.","furigana":"こうしんちゅう|でんげん|き|こわ"},{"japanese":"ファームウェアの不具合で、プリンターが起動しなくなった。","romaji":"Fāmuwea no fuguai de, purintā ga kidō shinaku natta.","german":"Because of a firmware bug, the printer no longer starts up.","furigana":"ふぐあい|きどう"}],"notes":"ファームウェア is \"firmware\", the control software permanently stored in a device (router, printer, camera). It is updated with ファームウェアを更新する or ファームウェアをアップデートする. Besides ファームウェア, the spelling ファームウエア also occurs. To be distinguished from ソフトウェア (application programs) and ハードウェア (the devices themselves).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"システムの冗長性を高めて障害に備える。","romaji":"Shisutemu no jōchōsei o takamete shōgai ni sonaeru.","german":"We increase the redundancy of the system to prepare for failures.","furigana":"じょうちょうせい|たか|しょうがい|そな"},{"japanese":"このデータセンターは電源の冗長性を確保している。","romaji":"Kono dēta sentā wa dengen no jōchōsei o kakuho shite iru.","german":"This data center ensures redundancy of its power supply.","furigana":"でんげん|じょうちょうせい|かくほ"},{"japanese":"言語には冗長性があるので、少し聞き逃しても意味は通じる。","romaji":"Gengo ni wa jōchōsei ga aru node, sukoshi kikinogashite mo imi wa tsūjiru.","german":"Language is redundant, so the meaning gets across even if you miss a little.","furigana":"げんご|じょうちょうせい|すこ|き|のが|いみ|つう"}],"notes":"冗長性 is \"redundancy\". The base word 冗長 is negative in everyday speech: 冗長な文章 is a \"long-winded, verbose text\". In engineering, however, 冗長性 is positive: extra reserves that step in when something fails (冗長性を確保する, 冗長性を高める); creating such reserves is called 冗長化. In language and information theory, too, one speaks of the 冗長性 of a message.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"サーバーの負荷を分散させて安定性を向上させる。","romaji":"Sābā no fuka o bunsan sasete anteisei o kōjō saseru.","german":"We distribute the server load to improve stability.","furigana":"ふか|ぶんさん|あんていせい|こうじょう"},{"japanese":"膝に負荷がかからない運動を選んでください。","romaji":"Hiza ni fuka ga kakaranai undō o erande kudasai.","german":"Please choose exercises that do not put strain on your knees.","furigana":"ひざ|ふか|うんどう|えら"},{"japanese":"環境への負荷が少ない製品を選ぶ人が増えている。","romaji":"Kankyō e no fuka ga sukunai seihin o erabu hito ga fuete iru.","german":"More and more people are choosing products with a low environmental impact.","furigana":"かんきょう|ふか|すく|せいひん|えら|ひと|ふ"}],"notes":"負荷 is the \"load, burden\" acting on a system, a machine or the body. Typical are 負荷がかかる (intransitive) and 負荷をかける (transitive), plus 負荷が大きい/小さい, 環境負荷 \"environmental impact\" and 負荷テスト \"load test\". 負担, on the other hand, is the (financial or emotional) burden a person bears (費用を負担する); 負荷 sounds more technical.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"半導体工場では帯電防止対策が不可欠である。","romaji":"Handōtai kōjō de wa taiden bōshi taisaku ga fukaketsu de aru.","german":"Measures against static charge are essential in semiconductor factories.","furigana":"はんどうたいこうじょう|たいでんぼうしたいさく|ふかけつ"},{"japanese":"冬は空気が乾燥しているので、服が帯電しやすい。","romaji":"Fuyu wa kūki ga kansō shite iru node, fuku ga taiden shiyasui.","german":"In winter the air is dry, so clothes easily build up static.","furigana":"ふゆ|くうき|かんそう|ふく|たいでん"},{"japanese":"下敷きをこすると帯電して、髪の毛が吸い寄せられる。","romaji":"Shitajiki o kosuru to taiden shite, kami no ke ga suiyoserareru.","german":"When you rub a plastic pencil board, it becomes charged and attracts hair.","furigana":"|たいでん|かみ|け|す|よ"}],"notes":"帯電 is the technical word for \"electric (mostly static) charge\"; as a する-verb it is intransitive: 静電気で帯電する. In everyday speech one rather says 静電気 (\"static electricity\", 静電気が起きる). Common combinations are 帯電防止 \"antistatic\" (帯電防止スプレー) and 帯電しやすい素材.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"高性能なCPUには効率的な放熱設計が必要だ。","romaji":"Kōseinō na shīpīyū ni wa kōritsuteki na hōnetsu sekkei ga hitsuyō da.","german":"High-performance CPUs require an efficient heat dissipation design.","furigana":"こうせいのう|こうりつてき|ほうねつせっけい|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"ノートパソコンを布団の上で使うと、放熱がうまくいかない。","romaji":"Nōto pasokon o futon no ue de tsukau to, hōnetsu ga umaku ikanai.","german":"If you use a laptop on a futon, heat dissipation doesn't work well.","furigana":"ふとん|うえ|つか|ほうねつ"},{"japanese":"このヒーターは放熱効率が高く、部屋がすぐに暖まる。","romaji":"Kono hītā wa hōnetsu kōritsu ga takaku, heya ga sugu ni atatamaru.","german":"This heater has high heat-radiation efficiency, so the room warms up quickly.","furigana":"ほうねつこうりつ|たか|へや|あたた"}],"notes":"放熱 means \"heat dissipation, heat release\": a component or device gives off heat to its surroundings. As a する-verb it is intransitive; typical are 放熱性, 放熱設計, 放熱板 \"heat sink\" and 放熱ファン. 発熱 is the generation of heat, 冷却 active cooling, 排熱 waste heat or its removal to the outside.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"フィードバック制御はシステムの安定性を保証する。","romaji":"Fīdobakku seigyo wa shisutemu no anteisei o hoshō suru.","german":"Feedback control guarantees the stability of the system.","furigana":"せいぎょ|あんていせい|ほしょう"},{"japanese":"上司から企画書についてのフィードバックをもらった。","romaji":"Jōshi kara kikakusho ni tsuite no fīdobakku o moratta.","german":"I got feedback on my proposal from my boss.","furigana":"じょうし|きかくしょ"},{"japanese":"お客様のフィードバックを新製品の開発に生かしています。","romaji":"Okyakusama no fīdobakku o shinseihin no kaihatsu ni ikashite imasu.","german":"We make use of customer feedback in developing new products.","furigana":"きゃくさま|しんせいひん|かいはつ|い"}],"notes":"フィードバック in control engineering is \"feedback\" in the sense of a loop (フィードバック制御 \"control with feedback\"). In working life, however, the meaning \"response, feedback\" is far more common: フィードバックをもらう, 部下にフィードバックする, お客様のフィードバックを生かす. The acoustic squeal of a microphone feeding back is usually called ハウリング in Japanese.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"帯域幅の拡大により大容量の通信が実現した。","romaji":"Taiiki haba no kakudai ni yori daiyōryō no tsūshin ga jitsugen shita.","german":"Expanding the bandwidth has made high-capacity communication possible.","furigana":"たいいきはば|かくだい|だいようりょう|つうしん|じつげん"},{"japanese":"高画質の動画を配信するには、十分な帯域幅が必要だ。","romaji":"Kōgashitsu no dōga o haishin suru ni wa, jūbun na taiiki haba ga hitsuyō da.","german":"Streaming high-quality video requires sufficient bandwidth.","furigana":"こうがしつ|どうが|はいしん|じゅうぶん|たいいきはば|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"会議中は帯域幅を節約するため、カメラをオフにしてください。","romaji":"Kaigichū wa taiiki haba o setsuyaku suru tame, kamera o ofu ni shite kudasai.","german":"Please turn off your camera during the meeting to save bandwidth.","furigana":"かいぎちゅう|たいいきはば|せつやく"}],"notes":"帯域幅 is the \"bandwidth\" of a frequency range or a data connection. In everyday IT speech one often says the shorter 帯域 (帯域が足りない \"not enough bandwidth\") or simply バンド幅. Typical are 帯域幅を確保する and 帯域幅を消費する. Not to be confused with 幅 alone, which means any spatial width.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"分散処理によって大規模なデータ分析が効率的に行える。","romaji":"Bunsan shori ni yotte daikibo na dēta bunseki ga kōritsuteki ni okonaeru.","german":"Distributed processing allows large-scale data analysis to be done efficiently.","furigana":"ぶんさんしょり|だいきぼ|ぶんせき|こうりつてき|おこな"},{"japanese":"アクセスが増えたので、1台のサーバーから分散処理に切り替えた。","romaji":"Akusesu ga fueta node, ichidai no sābā kara bunsan shori ni kirikaeta.","german":"As traffic increased, we switched from a single server to distributed processing.","furigana":"ふ|だい|ぶんさんしょり|き|か"},{"japanese":"世界中のボランティアのパソコンを使った分散処理で、病気の研究が進められた。","romaji":"Sekaijū no borantia no pasokon o tsukatta bunsan shori de, byōki no kenkyū ga susumerareta.","german":"Disease research advanced through distributed processing using volunteers' PCs around the world.","furigana":"せかいじゅう|つか|ぶんさんしょり|びょうき|けんきゅう|すす"}],"notes":"分散処理 is \"distributed processing\": a task is split among several computers on a network. The opposite is 集中処理 (everything on one central computer). 並列処理, by contrast, stresses simultaneous computation, also within a single computer. One says 分散処理する or 分散処理を行う; related is 負荷分散 \"load balancing\".","levelNote":"Compound: 分散 (N1) + 処理 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"並列処理を活用して計算速度を飛躍的に向上させる。","romaji":"Heiretsu shori o katsuyō shite keisan sokudo o hiyakuteki ni kōjō saseru.","german":"We use parallel processing to dramatically increase computation speed.","furigana":"へいれつしょり|かつよう|けいさんそくど|ひやくてき|こうじょう"},{"japanese":"GPUは単純な計算の並列処理が得意だ。","romaji":"Jī pī yū wa tanjun na keisan no heiretsu shori ga tokui da.","german":"GPUs are good at processing simple calculations in parallel.","furigana":"たんじゅん|けいさん|へいれつしょり|とくい"},{"japanese":"人間の脳は目や耳からの情報を並列処理している。","romaji":"Ningen no nō wa me ya mimi kara no jōhō o heiretsu shori shite iru.","german":"The human brain processes information from the eyes and ears in parallel.","furigana":"にんげん|のう|め|みみ|じょうほう|へいれつしょり"}],"notes":"並列処理 is \"parallel processing\": several computation steps run at the same time on several cores or processors. Specialists distinguish 並列 (truly simultaneous) from 並行 (concurrent, interleaved); in everyday speech this blurs. The opposite is 逐次処理 \"sequential processing\". Figuratively one also says that the brain 並列処理する much information.","levelNote":"Compound: 並列 (N1) + 処理 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"サーバーの仮想化によりハードウェア資源を効率的に利用する。","romaji":"Sābā no kasōka ni yori hādowea shigen o kōritsuteki ni riyō suru.","german":"Server virtualization makes efficient use of hardware resources.","furigana":"かそうか|しげん|こうりつてき|りよう"},{"japanese":"仮想化ソフトを使えば、1台のパソコンで複数のOSを動かせる。","romaji":"Kasōka sofuto o tsukaeba, ichidai no pasokon de fukusū no ō esu o ugokaseru.","german":"With virtualization software, you can run multiple operating systems on one PC.","furigana":"かそうか|つか|だい|ふくすう|うご"},{"japanese":"デスクトップを仮想化して、自宅からも同じ環境で働けるようにした。","romaji":"Desukutoppu o kasōka shite, jitaku kara mo onaji kankyō de hatarakeru yō ni shita.","german":"We virtualized the desktops so that people can work in the same environment from home too.","furigana":"かそうか|じたく|おな|かんきょう|はたら"}],"notes":"仮想化 is \"virtualization\": physical resources such as servers, storage or desktops are reproduced by software. As a する-verb it is transitive (サーバーを仮想化する). The base word 仮想 \"virtual, hypothetical\" is known from 仮想現実 \"virtual reality\", 仮想通貨 and 仮想マシン.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"量子コンピューターは従来の計算機では解けない問題に挑む。","romaji":"Ryōshi konpyūtā wa jūrai no keisanki de wa tokenai mondai ni idomu.","german":"Quantum computers take on problems that conventional computers cannot solve.","furigana":"りょうし|じゅうらい|けいさんき|と|もんだい|いど"},{"japanese":"量子コンピューターが実用化されれば、今の暗号は破られるかもしれない。","romaji":"Ryōshi konpyūtā ga jitsuyōka sarereba, ima no angō wa yaburareru kamo shirenai.","german":"If quantum computers become practical, today's encryption might be broken.","furigana":"りょうし|じつようか|いま|あんごう|やぶ"},{"japanese":"その企業は量子コンピューターの開発に巨額の投資をしている。","romaji":"Sono kigyō wa ryōshi konpyūtā no kaihatsu ni kyogaku no tōshi o shite iru.","german":"That company is investing huge sums in developing quantum computers.","furigana":"きぎょう|りょうし|かいはつ|きょがく|とうし"}],"notes":"量子コンピューター is the \"quantum computer\", which computes with quantum bits (量子ビット). In technical texts the spelling without the long-vowel mark, 量子コンピュータ, is common. Typical combinations: 量子コンピューターの実用化 \"practical use\", 量子コンピューターを開発する. In contrast, conventional computers are called 古典コンピューター or simply 従来のコンピューター.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"深層学習は画像認識や自然言語処理に革命をもたらした。","romaji":"Shinsō gakushū wa gazō ninshiki ya shizen gengo shori ni kakumei o motarashita.","german":"Deep learning has revolutionized image recognition and natural language processing.","furigana":"しんそうがくしゅう|がぞうにんしき|しぜんげんごしょり|かくめい"},{"japanese":"深層学習には大量のデータと計算資源が必要だ。","romaji":"Shinsō gakushū ni wa tairyō no dēta to keisan shigen ga hitsuyō da.","german":"Deep learning requires large amounts of data and computing resources.","furigana":"しんそうがくしゅう|たいりょう|けいさんしげん|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"今学期は大学で深層学習の講義を取っている。","romaji":"Kongakki wa daigaku de shinsō gakushū no kōgi o totte iru.","german":"This semester I'm taking a lecture on deep learning at university.","furigana":"こんがっき|だいがく|しんそうがくしゅう|こうぎ|と"}],"notes":"深層学習 is the Japanese technical term for \"deep learning\", a subfield of machine learning (機械学習) with multilayer neural networks (ニューラルネットワーク). In the media and in practice, the loanword ディープラーニング is heard at least as often; 深層学習 sounds somewhat more formal and likes to appear in textbooks and official texts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"機械学習アルゴリズムがデータからパターンを学習する。","romaji":"Kikai gakushū arugorizumu ga dēta kara patān o gakushū suru.","german":"Machine learning algorithms learn patterns from data.","furigana":"きかいがくしゅう|がくしゅう"},{"japanese":"迷惑メールの判定には機械学習が使われている。","romaji":"Meiwaku mēru no hantei ni wa kikai gakushū ga tsukawarete iru.","german":"Machine learning is used to detect spam email.","furigana":"めいわく|はんてい|きかいがくしゅう|つか"},{"japanese":"機械学習を勉強するなら、まず統計の基礎を固めたほうがいい。","romaji":"Kikai gakushū o benkyō suru nara, mazu tōkei no kiso o katameta hō ga ii.","german":"If you want to study machine learning, you should first solidify the basics of statistics.","furigana":"きかいがくしゅう|べんきょう|とうけい|きそ|かた"}],"notes":"機械学習 is \"machine learning\": computers learn patterns from data instead of being hard-coded. It is a subfield of 人工知能 (AI) and in turn includes 深層学習. Typical combinations: 機械学習モデル, 機械学習を使う, 機械学習で予測する. The loanword マシンラーニング exists too, but is rarer than 機械学習.","levelNote":"Compound: 機械 (N5) + 学習 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ロボット工学の発展により産業用ロボットが普及した。","romaji":"Robotto kōgaku no hatten ni yori sangyōyō robotto ga fukyū shita.","german":"With the development of robotics, industrial robots have become widespread.","furigana":"こうがく|はってん|さんぎょうよう|ふきゅう"},{"japanese":"兄は大学でロボット工学を専攻している。","romaji":"Ani wa daigaku de robotto kōgaku o senkō shite iru.","german":"My older brother is majoring in robotics at university.","furigana":"あに|だいがく|こうがく|せんこう"},{"japanese":"アシモフは小説の中で「ロボット工学三原則」を示した。","romaji":"Ashimofu wa shōsetsu no naka de “robotto kōgaku sangensoku” o shimeshita.","german":"Asimov set out the \"Three Laws of Robotics\" in his novels.","furigana":"しょうせつ|なか|こうがくさんげんそく|しめ"}],"notes":"ロボット工学 is \"robotics\", from ロボット and 工学 \"engineering\" (as in 機械工学, 電子工学). One studies it (ロボット工学を専攻する) or does research in it; the loanword ロボティクス is also used, mainly in company names and specialist circles. Well known is Asimov's ロボット工学三原則 \"the Three Laws of Robotics\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自律制御技術によりドローンが自動で障害物を回避する。","romaji":"Jiritsu seigyo gijutsu ni yori dorōn ga jidō de shōgaibutsu o kaihi suru.","german":"Thanks to autonomous control technology, drones automatically avoid obstacles.","furigana":"じりつせいぎょぎじゅつ|じどう|しょうがいぶつ|かいひ"},{"japanese":"探査機は地球からの指示を待たずに、自律制御で着陸した。","romaji":"Tansaki wa chikyū kara no shiji o matazu ni, jiritsu seigyo de chakuriku shita.","german":"The probe landed under autonomous control, without waiting for instructions from Earth.","furigana":"たんさき|ちきゅう|しじ|ま|じりつせいぎょ|ちゃくりく"},{"japanese":"この倉庫では自律制御のロボットが荷物を運んでいる。","romaji":"Kono sōko de wa jiritsu seigyo no robotto ga nimotsu o hakonde iru.","german":"In this warehouse, autonomously controlled robots carry the packages.","furigana":"そうこ|じりつせいぎょ|にもつ|はこ"}],"notes":"自律制御 is \"autonomous control\": a vehicle or robot perceives its surroundings and decides for itself, without human remote control. Do not confuse it with the identically read 自立 \"independence, self-reliance\" (経済的に自立する); 自律 stresses self-governance by one's own rules. 自動制御 is more general \"automatic control\" according to fixed specifications. Typical: 自律制御で飛行する, 自律制御システム.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ギアボックスは動力伝達の効率を最適化する装置である。","romaji":"Giabokkusu wa dōryoku dentatsu no kōritsu o saitekika suru sōchi de aru.","german":"A gearbox is a device that optimizes the efficiency of power transmission.","furigana":"どうりょくでんたつ|こうりつ|さいてきか|そうち"},{"japanese":"自転車ではチェーンが動力伝達の役割を果たしている。","romaji":"Jitensha de wa chēn ga dōryoku dentatsu no yakuwari o hatashite iru.","german":"On a bicycle, the chain plays the role of power transmission.","furigana":"じてんしゃ|どうりょくでんたつ|やくわり|は"},{"japanese":"動力伝達のロスを減らせば、燃費が良くなる。","romaji":"Dōryoku dentatsu no rosu o heraseba, nenpi ga yoku naru.","german":"If you reduce power transmission losses, fuel economy improves.","furigana":"どうりょくでんたつ|へ|ねんぴ|よ"}],"notes":"動力伝達 is the \"power transmission\" from the engine to wheels or tools, from 動力 \"driving force\" and 伝達 \"transmission, conveyance\". The word appears mainly in mechanical-engineering and vehicle texts: 動力伝達装置, 動力伝達機構, 動力伝達の効率. The components for it are e.g. 歯車, チェーン, ベルト and 変速機; the loanword for the whole drivetrain is パワートレイン.","levelNote":"Compound: 動力 (N1) + 伝達 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"機械の異常振動は故障の前兆である可能性がある。","romaji":"Kikai no ijō shindō wa koshō no zenchō de aru kanōsei ga aru.","german":"Abnormal vibration of a machine may be a sign of impending failure.","furigana":"きかい|いじょうしんどう|こしょう|ぜんちょう|かのうせい"},{"japanese":"マナーモードだったので、電話の振動に気づかなかった。","romaji":"Manā mōdo datta node, denwa no shindō ni kizukanakatta.","german":"My phone was in silent mode, so I didn't notice it vibrating.","furigana":"でんわ|しんどう|き"},{"japanese":"振り子が振動する周期は、糸の長さで決まる。","romaji":"Furiko ga shindō suru shūki wa, ito no nagasa de kimaru.","german":"The period of a pendulum's oscillation is determined by the length of the string.","furigana":"ふ|こ|しんどう|しゅうき|いと|なが|き"}],"notes":"振動 is an \"oscillation, vibration\" of machines, pendulums, phones or strings; as a する-verb it is intransitive: 機械が振動する. In everyday speech, for noticeable shaking one rather says 揺れ/揺れる. Do not confuse it with the identically read 震動, which stands for shocks such as in earthquakes. Typical: 振動が伝わる, 振動を抑える, 振動数 \"frequency\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"構造物の固有周波数で共振が発生すると破壊につながる。","romaji":"Kōzōbutsu no koyū shūhasū de kyōshin ga hassei suru to hakai ni tsunagaru.","german":"If resonance occurs at a structure's natural frequency, it can lead to destruction.","furigana":"こうぞうぶつ|こゆうしゅうはすう|きょうしん|はっせい|はかい"},{"japanese":"脱水のときに洗濯機が共振して、床まで揺れた。","romaji":"Dassui no toki ni sentakuki ga kyōshin shite, yuka made yureta.","german":"During the spin cycle the washing machine went into resonance, and even the floor shook.","furigana":"だっすい|せんたくき|きょうしん|ゆか|ゆ"},{"japanese":"兵士が橋を渡るとき歩調を乱すのは、共振を避けるためだ。","romaji":"Heishi ga hashi o wataru toki hochō o midasu no wa, kyōshin o sakeru tame da.","german":"Soldiers break step when crossing a bridge in order to avoid resonance.","furigana":"へいし|はし|わた|ほちょう|みだ|きょうしん|さ"}],"notes":"共振 is mechanical or electrical \"resonance\": a body vibrates along when an external vibration hits its natural frequency. One says 共振が起きる/発生する or 共振する (intransitive). For sound and, figuratively, \"to find resonance, to sympathize\", 共鳴 is used instead (彼の考えに共鳴する); 共振 remains almost always technical and physical.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"振動の減衰特性を考慮して緩衝装置を設計する。","romaji":"Shindō no gensui tokusei o kōryo shite kanshō sōchi o sekkei suru.","german":"Shock absorbers are designed taking the damping characteristics of vibrations into account.","furigana":"しんどう|げんすいとくせい|こうりょ|かんしょうそうち|せっけい"},{"japanese":"電波は距離が離れるほど減衰する。","romaji":"Denpa wa kyori ga hanareru hodo gensui suru.","german":"Radio waves attenuate the farther the distance.","furigana":"でんぱ|きょり|はな|げんすい"},{"japanese":"鐘の音は次第に減衰して、やがて聞こえなくなった。","romaji":"Kane no oto wa shidai ni gensui shite, yagate kikoenaku natta.","german":"The sound of the bell gradually faded until it could no longer be heard.","furigana":"かね|おと|しだい|げんすい|き"}],"notes":"減衰 means \"damping, attenuation, decay\" of a quantity such as a vibration, signal or sound; as a する-verb it is intransitive: 信号が減衰する. Typical are 減衰特性, 減衰率 and 減衰が大きい. 減少, on the other hand, is a decrease in a number or amount (人口が減少する), and 衰退 the decline of an industry or culture.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"部品に加わる応力を有限要素法で解析する。","romaji":"Buhin ni kuwawaru ōryoku o yūgen yōsohō de kaiseki suru.","german":"The stress applied to the part is analyzed with the finite element method.","furigana":"ぶひん|くわ|おうりょく|ゆうげんようそほう|かいせき"},{"japanese":"穴の周りには応力が集中しやすい。","romaji":"Ana no mawari ni wa ōryoku ga shūchū shiyasui.","german":"Stress tends to concentrate around holes.","furigana":"あな|まわ|おうりょく|しゅうちゅう"},{"japanese":"温度の変化によって、レールに応力が生じる。","romaji":"Ondo no henka ni yotte, rēru ni ōryoku ga shōjiru.","german":"Changes in temperature cause stress in the rails.","furigana":"おんど|へんか|おうりょく|しょう"}],"notes":"応力 is the \"mechanical stress\" in mechanics and materials science, i.e. the internal force per unit area in a loaded component. Typical are 応力がかかる/生じる, 応力集中 \"stress concentration\" and 引張応力/圧縮応力. Do not confuse it with 圧力 \"pressure\" or with ストレス, which in everyday speech means mental strain.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"応力によって材料に歪みが生じる。","romaji":"Ōryoku ni yotte zairyō ni hizumi ga shōjiru.","german":"Stress causes strain in the material.","furigana":"おうりょく|ざいりょう|ひず|しょう"},{"japanese":"このスピーカーは大音量でも音の歪みが少ない。","romaji":"Kono supīkā wa daiongyō de mo oto no hizumi ga sukunai.","german":"This speaker has little sound distortion even at high volume.","furigana":"|おと|ひず|すく"},{"japanese":"急速な経済成長のひずみが、公害という形で現れた。","romaji":"Kyūsoku na keizai seichō no hizumi ga, kōgai to iu katachi de arawareta.","german":"The strains of rapid economic growth appeared in the form of pollution.","furigana":"きゅうそく|けいざいせいちょう|こうがい|かたち|あらわ"}],"notes":"歪み (ひずみ) is technically the \"strain, deformation\" of a material under stress and in acoustics the \"distortion\" of a signal (音のひずみ). Figuratively it denotes \"imbalances, distortions\" in society or the economy (社会のひずみ). It is usually written in kana. The same written form can also be read ゆがみ, which rather means visible bending or distortion (顔のゆがみ, 画像のゆがみ).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"微小な亀裂が疲労破壊の起点となることがある。","romaji":"Bishō na kiretsu ga hirō hakai no kiten to naru koto ga aru.","german":"A tiny crack can become the starting point of fatigue failure.","furigana":"びしょう|きれつ|ひろうはかい|きてん"},{"japanese":"地震で建物の壁に大きな亀裂が入った。","romaji":"Jishin de tatemono no kabe ni ōki na kiretsu ga haitta.","german":"The earthquake caused a large crack in the wall of the building.","furigana":"じしん|たてもの|かべ|おお|きれつ|はい"},{"japanese":"その一言で、二人の友情に亀裂が生じた。","romaji":"Sono hitokoto de, futari no yūjō ni kiretsu ga shōjita.","german":"That one remark caused a rift in the two's friendship.","furigana":"ひとこと|ふたり|ゆうじょう|きれつ|しょう"}],"notes":"亀裂 is a \"crack, fissure\" in walls, roads, ice or material; the basic phrases are 亀裂が入る or 亀裂が生じる. Figuratively it describes a \"rift\" in relationships or organizations: 二人の関係に亀裂が入る. In everyday speech, for small cracks one rather says ひび (ひびが入る); 亀裂 sounds more literary and often larger.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"靭性の高い材料は衝撃に対して強い。","romaji":"Jinsei no takai zairyō wa shōgeki ni taishite tsuyoi.","german":"Materials with high toughness are strong against impact.","furigana":"じんせい|たか|ざいりょう|しょうげき|たい|つよ"},{"japanese":"ガラスは硬いが靭性が低いので、衝撃で割れやすい。","romaji":"Garasu wa katai ga jinsei ga hikui node, shōgeki de wareyasui.","german":"Glass is hard but has low toughness, so it breaks easily under impact.","furigana":"かた|じんせい|ひく|しょうげき|わ"},{"japanese":"寒冷地の橋には、低温でも靭性が落ちにくい鋼材が使われる。","romaji":"Kanreichi no hashi ni wa, teion de mo jinsei ga ochinikui kōzai ga tsukawareru.","german":"Steel whose toughness does not easily drop even at low temperatures is used for bridges in cold regions.","furigana":"かんれいち|はし|ていおん|じんせい|お|こうざい|つか"}],"notes":"靭性 is the \"toughness\" of a material, i.e. its ability to absorb energy without breaking. The standard spelling is 靱性; 靭性 is the widespread variant. The distinction is important: 硬度 \"hardness\" and 強度 \"strength\" are different things; the opposite term is 脆性 \"brittleness\". Typical: 靭性が高い/低い, 破壊靭性.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ダイヤモンドはすべての天然鉱物の中で最高の硬度を持つ。","romaji":"Daiyamondo wa subete no tennen kōbutsu no naka de saikō no kōdo o motsu.","german":"Diamond has the highest hardness of all natural minerals.","furigana":"てんねんこうぶつ|なか|さいこう|こうど|も"},{"japanese":"この地域の水は硬度が高く、石けんが泡立ちにくい。","romaji":"Kono chiiki no mizu wa kōdo ga takaku, sekken ga awadachinikui.","german":"The water in this area has high hardness, so soap does not lather easily.","furigana":"ちいき|みず|こうど|たか|せっ|あわだ"},{"japanese":"鉛筆の芯の硬度は、HやBなどの記号で表される。","romaji":"Enpitsu no shin no kōdo wa, eichi ya bī nado no kigō de arawasareru.","german":"The hardness of pencil leads is indicated by symbols such as H and B.","furigana":"えんぴつ|しん|こうど|きごう|あらわ"}],"notes":"硬度 is the \"hardness\" of a material (モース硬度, ビッカース硬度) and also \"water hardness\" (硬度の高い水 = 硬水, opposite 軟水). One says 硬度が高い/低い and 硬度を測る. To be distinguished from 強度 \"strength, load capacity\" and 靭性 \"toughness\": a material can be very hard and yet brittle.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"炭素繊維は引張強度が非常に高い材料である。","romaji":"Tanso sen'i wa hippari kyōdo ga hijō ni takai zairyō de aru.","german":"Carbon fiber is a material with very high tensile strength.","furigana":"たんそせんい|ひっぱりきょうど|ひじょう|たか|ざいりょう"},{"japanese":"試験片を両端から引っ張って、引張強度を測定する。","romaji":"Shikenhen o ryōtan kara hippatte, hippari kyōdo o sokutei suru.","german":"The test piece is pulled from both ends to measure its tensile strength.","furigana":"しけんへん|りょうたん|ひ|ぱ|ひっぱりきょうど|そくてい"},{"japanese":"クレーンのワイヤーには、引張強度の高い鋼が使われている。","romaji":"Kurēn no waiyā ni wa, hippari kyōdo no takai hagane ga tsukawarete iru.","german":"Steel with high tensile strength is used for crane cables.","furigana":"ひっぱりきょうど|たか|はがね|つか"}],"notes":"引張強度 is \"tensile strength\", i.e. the maximum tensile stress a material withstands before it tears. In technical texts 引張 is written without okurigana; in standards the quantity is usually called 引張強さ, formerly also 抗張力. Counterparts are 圧縮強度 \"compressive strength\" and せん断強度 \"shear strength\". Typical: 引張強度が高い, 引張強度を測定する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コンクリートは圧縮力に対して強い耐性を示す。","romaji":"Konkurīto wa asshukuryoku ni taishite tsuyoi taisei o shimesu.","german":"Concrete shows strong resistance to compressive force.","furigana":"あっしゅくりょく|たい|つよ|たいせい|しめ"},{"japanese":"メールで送る前に、ファイルを圧縮してください。","romaji":"Mēru de okuru mae ni, fairu o asshuku shite kudasai.","german":"Please compress the file before sending it by email.","furigana":"おく|まえ|あっしゅく"},{"japanese":"予算の都合で、工期を半年から三か月に圧縮した。","romaji":"Yosan no tsugō de, kōki o hantoshi kara sankagetsu ni asshuku shita.","german":"Due to budget constraints, the construction period was shortened from six months to three.","furigana":"よさん|つごう|こうき|はんとし|さん|げつ|あっしゅく"}],"notes":"圧縮 means \"compression\" (gas, air, concrete under pressure) and, in IT, \"data compression\": ファイルを圧縮する, counterpart 解凍 or 展開 \"to unpack\". Figuratively it also means \"to cut back, to tighten\": 予算を圧縮する, 日程を圧縮する. As a する-verb it is transitive. In construction, 圧縮力 \"compressive force\" is the opposite of 引張力 \"tensile force\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ボルト接合部には剪断力が作用する。","romaji":"Boruto setsugōbu ni wa sendanryoku ga sayō suru.","german":"Shear force acts on bolted joints.","furigana":"せつごうぶ|せんだんりょく|さよう"},{"japanese":"地震の揺れで、柱にせん断破壊が起きた。","romaji":"Jishin no yure de, hashira ni sendan hakai ga okita.","german":"The earthquake's shaking caused shear failure in the columns.","furigana":"じしん|ゆ|はしら|だんはかい|お"},{"japanese":"はさみは、二枚の刃で紙を剪断する道具だ。","romaji":"Hasami wa, nimai no ha de kami o sendan suru dōgu da.","german":"Scissors are a tool that shears paper with two blades.","furigana":"にまい|は|かみ|せんだん|どうぐ"}],"notes":"剪断 is \"shear\" in mechanics: two forces act in parallel but offset from each other, as with scissors. Since 剪 is not a Jōyō kanji, textbooks and standards usually write せん断 (せん断力, せん断破壊, せん断応力). As a する-verb it is transitive. Counterparts are 引張 \"tension\" and 圧縮 \"compression\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"梁の撓みを計算して安全な設計を行う。","romaji":"Hari no tawami o keisan shite anzen na sekkei o okonau.","german":"The deflection of the beam is calculated to produce a safe design.","furigana":"はり|たわ|けいさん|あんぜん|せっけい|おこな"},{"japanese":"本を詰め込みすぎて、棚の板にたわみが出てきた。","romaji":"Hon o tsumekomisugite, tana no ita ni tawami ga dete kita.","german":"I stuffed in too many books, and the shelf board has started to sag.","furigana":"ほん|つ|こ|たな|いた|で"},{"japanese":"釣りざおのたわみ具合で、魚の大きさがだいたいわかる。","romaji":"Tsurizao no tawami guai de, sakana no ōkisa ga daitai wakaru.","german":"From how much the fishing rod bends, you can roughly tell the size of the fish.","furigana":"つ|ぐあい|さかな|おお"}],"notes":"撓み (たわみ) is the \"deflection\" of a beam, board or rod under load; the corresponding verb is 撓む \"to bend, to give way\" (intransitive). The kanji is rare, so it is usually written たわみ. Typical: たわみが生じる/出る, たわみを計算する, たわみ量. Unlike 曲がり, with たわみ the deformation is usually elastic and returns after the load is removed.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ボルトの締め付けトルクを規定値に管理する。","romaji":"Boruto no shimetsuke toruku o kiteichi ni kanri suru.","german":"The tightening torque of bolts is controlled to the specified value.","furigana":"し|つ|きていち|かんり"},{"japanese":"電気自動車は発進した瞬間から大きなトルクを出せる。","romaji":"Denki jidōsha wa hasshin shita shunkan kara ōki na toruku o daseru.","german":"Electric cars can produce large torque from the moment they start moving.","furigana":"でんきじどうしゃ|はっしん|しゅんかん|おお|だ"},{"japanese":"タイヤを交換したら、トルクレンチでナットを締めてください。","romaji":"Taiya o kōkan shitara, torukurenchi de natto o shimete kudasai.","german":"After changing the tires, please tighten the nuts with a torque wrench.","furigana":"こうかん|し"}],"notes":"トルク is \"torque\", i.e. the force with which something is turned about an axis. Typical are トルクが大きい/強い, トルクを出す, 最大トルク, 締め付けトルク \"tightening torque\" and トルクレンチ \"torque wrench\". In a car context, トルク (pulling force) is distinguished from 馬力 \"horsepower, output\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"エンジンの回転数を最適な範囲に維持する。","romaji":"Enjin no kaitensū o saitekina han'i ni iji suru.","german":"The engine's rotation speed is kept within the optimal range.","furigana":"かいてんすう|さいてき|はんい|いじ"},{"japanese":"回転数が上がりすぎると、エンジンを傷めてしまう。","romaji":"Kaitensū ga agarisugiru to, enjin o itamete shimau.","german":"If the rpm gets too high, it damages the engine.","furigana":"かいてんすう|あ|いた"},{"japanese":"脱水の回転数を下げると、服がしわになりにくい。","romaji":"Dassui no kaitensū o sageru to, fuku ga shiwa ni narinikui.","german":"If you lower the spin speed, clothes wrinkle less easily.","furigana":"だっすい|かいてんすう|さ|ふく"}],"notes":"回転数 is the \"rotational speed\" or \"number of revolutions\" of a motor, wheel, hard disk or spin drum, usually given per minute (毎分〜回転, rpm). Typical: 回転数が上がる/下がる, 回転数を上げる/抑える, エンジンの回転数. 回転 alone is the rotation itself; 速度 would be the speed of the vehicle.","levelNote":"Compound: 回転 (N3) + 数 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"液体冷却システムで高性能プロセッサの温度を管理する。","romaji":"Ekitai reikyaku shisutemu de kōseinō purosessa no ondo o kanri suru.","german":"A liquid cooling system manages the temperature of high-performance processors.","furigana":"えきたいれいきゃく|こうせいのう|おんど|かんり"},{"japanese":"事故で原子炉を冷却するための水が不足した。","romaji":"Jiko de genshiro o reikyaku suru tame no mizu ga fusoku shita.","german":"Water for cooling the reactor ran short because of the accident.","furigana":"じこ|げんしろ|れいきゃく|みず|ふそく"},{"japanese":"けんかばかりの二人には、少し冷却期間が必要だ。","romaji":"Kenka bakari no futari ni wa, sukoshi reikyaku kikan ga hitsuyō da.","german":"The two do nothing but fight, so they need a bit of a cooling-off period.","furigana":"ふたり|すこ|れいきゃくきかん|ひつよう"}],"notes":"冷却 is \"cooling\" in technical contexts: 冷却装置, 冷却水, 水冷/空冷による冷却 (\"cooling by water or air\"). As a する-verb it is both transitive and intransitive (エンジンを冷却する / 冷却される). Figuratively it means a \"cooling off\" between people or states, e.g. 冷却期間 \"cooling-off period\". In everyday speech, for drinks or food one simply says 冷やす.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"エンジンの吸気効率を改善してパワーを向上させる。","romaji":"Enjin no kyūki kōritsu o kaizen shite pawā o kōjō saseru.","german":"Improving the engine's intake efficiency increases power.","furigana":"きゅうきこうりつ|かいぜん|こうじょう"},{"japanese":"吸気口がほこりで詰まると、エアコンの効きが悪くなる。","romaji":"Kyūkikō ga hokori de tsumaru to, eakon no kiki ga waruku naru.","german":"If the air intake gets clogged with dust, the air conditioner works worse.","furigana":"きゅうきこう|つ|き|わる"},{"japanese":"深呼吸では、吸気よりも呼気をゆっくり長くするとよい。","romaji":"Shinkokyū de wa, kyūki yori mo koki o yukkuri nagaku suru to yoi.","german":"In deep breathing, it is good to make the exhalation slower and longer than the inhalation.","furigana":"しんこきゅう|きゅうき|こき|なが"}],"notes":"吸気 is the \"intake\" of air in engines and devices (吸気口 \"air intake\", 吸気効率) and in medicine \"inhalation\" or the inhaled air. The counterpart in engineering is 排気 \"exhaust\", in breathing 呼気 \"exhalation, exhaled air\". In everyday speech, for breathing in one simply says 息を吸う.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"リチウムイオン蓄電池は電気自動車の核心技術である。","romaji":"Richiumu ion chikudenchi wa denki jidōsha no kakushin gijutsu de aru.","german":"Lithium-ion storage batteries are the core technology of electric vehicles.","furigana":"ちくでんち|でんきじどうしゃ|かくしんぎじゅつ"},{"japanese":"停電に備えて、家庭用の蓄電池を導入した。","romaji":"Teiden ni sonaete, kateiyō no chikudenchi o dōnyū shita.","german":"We installed a home storage battery in preparation for power outages.","furigana":"ていでん|そな|かていよう|ちくでんち|どうにゅう"},{"japanese":"昼間に発電した電気を蓄電池にためて、夜に使う。","romaji":"Hiruma ni hatsuden shita denki o chikudenchi ni tamete, yoru ni tsukau.","german":"Electricity generated during the day is stored in the battery and used at night.","furigana":"ひるま|はつでん|でんき|ちくでんち|よる|つか"}],"notes":"蓄電池 is a \"storage battery, rechargeable battery\", i.e. a store of electrical energy (蓄 \"to store\" + 電池). In everyday speech, for phones and cars one rather says バッテリー, for small rechargeables 充電池. 蓄電池 is heard mainly with power storage for homes and grids: 家庭用蓄電池, 蓄電池に電気をためる. The opposite term is 一次電池 \"non-rechargeable battery\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"太陽電池の変換効率は年々向上している。","romaji":"Taiyō denchi no henkan kōritsu wa nennen kōjō shite iru.","german":"The conversion efficiency of solar cells improves year by year.","furigana":"たいようでんち|へんかんこうりつ|ねんねんこうじょう"},{"japanese":"この電卓は太陽電池で動くので、電池交換がいらない。","romaji":"Kono dentaku wa taiyō denchi de ugoku node, denchi kōkan ga iranai.","german":"This calculator runs on a solar cell, so there's no need to replace batteries.","furigana":"でんたく|たいようでんち|うご|でんちこうかん"},{"japanese":"人工衛星は太陽電池パネルで電力を得ている。","romaji":"Jinkō eisei wa taiyō denchi paneru de denryoku o ete iru.","german":"Satellites get their power from solar cell panels.","furigana":"じんこうえいせい|たいようでんち|でんりょく|え"}],"notes":"太陽電池 is the \"solar cell\", which converts light directly into electricity. The whole module on the roof is usually called 太陽光パネル or ソーラーパネル, and power generation as a whole 太陽光発電. Although 電池 is in the name, a solar cell does not store electricity. Typical: 太陽電池で動く, 太陽電池の変換効率.","levelNote":"Compound: 太陽 (N4) + 電池 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"燃料電池は水素と酸素から電気を生成する装置である。","romaji":"Nenryō denchi wa suiso to sanso kara denki o seisei suru sōchi de aru.","german":"A fuel cell is a device that generates electricity from hydrogen and oxygen.","furigana":"ねんりょうでんち|すいそ|さんそ|でんき|せいせい|そうち"},{"japanese":"燃料電池車は走行中に水しか出さない。","romaji":"Nenryō denchi sha wa sōkōchū ni mizu shika dasanai.","german":"Fuel cell vehicles emit only water while driving.","furigana":"ねんりょうでんちしゃ|そうこうちゅう|みず|だ"},{"japanese":"我が家では家庭用燃料電池で、発電とお湯の供給を同時に行っている。","romaji":"Wagaya de wa kateiyō nenryō denchi de, hatsuden to oyu no kyōkyū o dōji ni okonatte iru.","german":"In our house, a home fuel cell generates electricity and supplies hot water at the same time.","furigana":"わ|や|かていようねんりょうでんち|はつでん|ゆ|きょうきゅう|どうじ|おこな"}],"notes":"燃料電池 is the \"fuel cell\", which generates electricity through a chemical reaction of hydrogen and oxygen. Important combinations are 燃料電池車 \"fuel cell vehicle\" and 家庭用燃料電池, which in Japan supplies electricity and hot water at once. Unlike 蓄電池, it is not charged but supplied with fuel.","levelNote":"Compound: 燃料 (N1) + 電池 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"高圧送電によって長距離の電力輸送が可能になる。","romaji":"Kōatsu sōden ni yotte chōkyori no denryoku yusō ga kanō ni naru.","german":"High-voltage transmission makes long-distance power transport possible.","furigana":"こうあつそうでん|ちょうきょり|でんりょくゆそう|かのう"},{"japanese":"台風で送電線が切れ、広い地域が停電した。","romaji":"Taifū de sōdensen ga kire, hiroi chiiki ga teiden shita.","german":"A typhoon cut the power lines, and a wide area lost power.","furigana":"たいふう|そうでんせん|き|ひろ|ちいき|ていでん"},{"japanese":"工事のため、午前中は一時的に送電を停止します。","romaji":"Kōji no tame, gozenchū wa ichijiteki ni sōden o teishi shimasu.","german":"Due to construction work, power transmission will be temporarily suspended during the morning.","furigana":"こうじ|ごぜんちゅう|いちじてき|そうでん|ていし"}],"notes":"送電 is the \"transmission of electricity\" from the power plant via high-voltage lines; as a する-verb transitive/intransitive (電力を送電する, 送電を止める). Typical: 送電線 \"power line\", 送電網 \"power grid\", 送電を再開する. The chain consists of 発電 \"generation\", 送電 \"transmission\" and 配電 \"distribution to households\"; the everyday opposite of 送電 is 停電 \"power outage\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"変圧器で電圧を昇降させて効率的に送電する。","romaji":"Hen'atsuki de den'atsu o shōkō sasete kōritsuteki ni sōden suru.","german":"Voltage is stepped up and down with transformers to transmit power efficiently.","furigana":"へんあつき|でんあつ|しょうこう|こうりつてき|そうでん"},{"japanese":"海外で日本の家電を使うときは、変圧器が必要な場合がある。","romaji":"Kaigai de Nihon no kaden o tsukau toki wa, hen'atsuki ga hitsuyō na baai ga aru.","german":"When using Japanese appliances abroad, a transformer may be necessary.","furigana":"かいがい|にほん|かでん|つか|へんあつき|ひつよう|ばあい"},{"japanese":"電柱の上の変圧器から、各家庭に電気が送られている。","romaji":"Denchū no ue no hen'atsuki kara, kaku katei ni denki ga okurarete iru.","german":"Electricity is sent to each household from the transformer on top of the utility pole.","furigana":"でんちゅう|うえ|へんあつき|かくかてい|でんき|おく"}],"notes":"変圧器 is the \"transformer\", which raises or lowers alternating voltage (電圧を上げる/下げる); colloquially also トランス. In a travel context, the voltage converter for electrical appliances abroad is also called 変圧器 - not to be confused with a mere plug adapter (変換プラグ). Typical: 変圧器を通す, 変圧器が必要だ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"整流器は交流を直流に変換する電子部品である。","romaji":"Seiryūki wa kōryū o chokuryū ni henkan suru denshi buhin de aru.","german":"A rectifier is an electronic component that converts alternating current to direct current.","furigana":"せいりゅうき|こうりゅう|ちょくりゅう|へんかん|でんしぶひん"},{"japanese":"ダイオードを使って交流を整流する回路を作った。","romaji":"Daiōdo o tsukatte kōryū o seiryū suru kairo o tsukutta.","german":"I built a circuit that rectifies alternating current using diodes.","furigana":"つか|こうりゅう|せいりゅう|かいろ|つく"},{"japanese":"この扇風機は風を整流して、やわらかい風を送る。","romaji":"Kono senpūki wa kaze o seiryū shite, yawarakai kaze o okuru.","german":"This fan straightens the airflow to deliver a gentle breeze.","furigana":"せんぷうき|かぜ|せいりゅう|かぜ|おく"}],"notes":"整流 means \"rectification\" in electrical engineering, i.e. the conversion of alternating current (交流) into direct current (直流): 交流を整流する. The device is called 整流器, and the diode acts as a 整流素子. In fluid engineering, 整流 also denotes \"straightening\" an air or water flow to avoid eddies. As a する-verb it is transitive.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"感電事故を防ぐために適切な接地が不可欠である。","romaji":"Kanden jiko o fusegu tame ni tekisetsu na setchi ga fukaketsu de aru.","german":"Proper grounding is essential to prevent electric shock accidents.","furigana":"かんでんじこ|ふせ|てきせつ|せっち|ふかけつ"},{"japanese":"洗濯機は必ず接地してから使ってください。","romaji":"Sentakuki wa kanarazu setchi shite kara tsukatte kudasai.","german":"Please be sure to ground the washing machine before using it.","furigana":"せんたくき|かなら|せっち|つか"},{"japanese":"このタイヤは接地面が広く、雨の日でも滑りにくい。","romaji":"Kono taiya wa setchimen ga hiroku, ame no hi de mo suberinikui.","german":"This tire has a wide contact patch, so it does not slip easily even on rainy days.","furigana":"せっちめん|ひろ|あめ|ひ|すべ"}],"notes":"接地 is \"grounding\" (earthing) in electrical engineering; in everyday speech it is mostly called アース (アース線をつなぐ). As a する-verb: 機器を接地する. The second meaning is \"ground contact\": 接地面 \"contact patch\" of a tire, or the touchdown of an airplane or a foot while running. 着地, in contrast, stresses landing after a jump.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"高電圧部品には十分な絶縁処理を施す必要がある。","romaji":"Kōden'atsu buhin ni wa jūbun na zetsuen shori o hodokosu hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"High-voltage components need sufficient insulation treatment.","furigana":"こうでんあつぶひん|じゅうぶん|ぜつえんしょり|ほどこ|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"電線の絶縁が劣化して、ショートしてしまった。","romaji":"Densen no zetsuen ga rekka shite, shōto shite shimatta.","german":"The insulation of the electric wire deteriorated and caused a short circuit.","furigana":"でんせん|ぜつえん|れっか"},{"japanese":"彼は父親と絶縁して、もう十年も会っていない。","romaji":"Kare wa chichioya to zetsuen shite, mō jūnen mo atte inai.","german":"He has cut ties with his father and hasn't seen him for ten years.","furigana":"かれ|ちちおや|ぜつえん|じゅうねん|あ"}],"notes":"絶縁 has two meanings: technically electrical or thermal \"insulation\" (絶縁体 \"insulator\", 絶縁テープ, 絶縁処理), and in everyday life the \"severing of ties, break in relations\": 親と絶縁する, 絶縁状 \"letter by which one ends a relationship\". In the second sense the other party takes と. For the thermal insulation of houses, 断熱 is usually said; 隔離 is the isolation of people, for example during illness.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"病理検査で組織の異常を顕微鏡レベルで確認する。","romaji":"Byōri kensa de soshiki no ijō o kenbikyō reberu de kakunin suru.","german":"Pathological examination confirms tissue abnormalities at the microscopic level.","furigana":"びょうりけんさ|そしき|いじょう|けんびきょう|かくにん"},{"japanese":"姉は大学病院で病理医として働いている。","romaji":"Ane wa daigaku byōin de byōrii to shite hataraite iru.","german":"My older sister works as a pathologist at a university hospital.","furigana":"あね|だいがくびょういん|びょうりい|はたら"},{"japanese":"いじめは現代社会の病理の一つだと言われる。","romaji":"Ijime wa gendai shakai no byōri no hitotsu da to iwareru.","german":"Bullying is said to be one of the pathologies of modern society.","furigana":"げんだいしゃかい|びょうり|ひと|い"}],"notes":"病理 is \"pathology\", the study of the causes and processes of diseases. It often appears as a first element: 病理学 (the field), 病理検査, 病理医, 病理解剖. Figuratively, media and sociology speak of 社会の病理 \"pathological phenomena of society\". To be distinguished from 病状 \"a patient's condition\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"抗原検査でウイルスの存在を迅速に確認できる。","romaji":"Kōgen kensa de uirusu no sonzai o jinsoku ni kakunin dekiru.","german":"An antigen test can quickly confirm the presence of a virus.","furigana":"こうげんけんさ|そんざい|じんそく|かくにん"},{"japanese":"抗原と抗体が結びつくことで、免疫反応が起こる。","romaji":"Kōgen to kōtai ga musubitsuku koto de, men'eki hannō ga okoru.","german":"An immune response occurs when antigens and antibodies bind.","furigana":"こうげん|こうたい|むす|めんえきはんのう|お"},{"japanese":"旅行の前に抗原検査を受けたら、陰性だった。","romaji":"Ryokō no mae ni kōgen kensa o uketara, insei datta.","german":"I took an antigen test before my trip, and it was negative.","furigana":"りょこう|まえ|こうげんけんさ|う|いんせい"}],"notes":"抗原 is the technical term for \"antigen\", a substance against which the body forms an immune response; its counterpart is 抗体 (\"antibody\"), together in the expression 抗原抗体反応. Since the Covid era, 抗原検査 (\"rapid antigen test\") has also become common in everyday life, usually in contrast to PCR検査. For allergies, one speaks of the 抗原 as the trigger, colloquially more often アレルゲン.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"良性腫瘍は転移せず生命を脅かすことは少ない。","romaji":"Ryōsei shuyō wa ten'i sezu seimei o obiyakasu koto wa sukunai.","german":"Benign tumors do not metastasize and are rarely life-threatening.","furigana":"りょうせいしゅよう|てんい|せいめい|おびや|すく"},{"japanese":"検査の結果、ポリープは良性だと分かってほっとした。","romaji":"Kensa no kekka, porīpu wa ryōsei da to wakatte hotto shita.","german":"I was relieved to learn from the test results that the polyp was benign.","furigana":"けんさ|けっか|りょうせい|わ"},{"japanese":"良性か悪性かを調べるために、組織の一部を取って検査します。","romaji":"Ryōsei ka akusei ka o shiraberu tame ni, soshiki no ichibu o totte kensa shimasu.","german":"We will take part of the tissue and test it to find out whether it is benign or malignant.","furigana":"りょうせい|あくせい|しら|そしき|いちぶ|と|けんさ"}],"notes":"良性 means \"benign\" in the medical sense and is used mainly with tumors, polyps or cysts: attributively as 良性の腫瘍 or directly as the compound 良性腫瘍, predicatively as 良性だ／良性です. The opposite is 悪性, and in a diagnostic conversation the question 良性か悪性か often comes up. For a good-natured character, 良性 is not used; one uses 人がいい or 善良な.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"悪性腫瘍は周囲の組織に浸潤する傾向がある。","romaji":"Akusei shuyō wa shūi no soshiki ni shinjun suru keikō ga aru.","german":"Malignant tumors tend to invade the surrounding tissue.","furigana":"あくせいしゅよう|しゅうい|そしき|しんじゅん|けいこう"},{"japanese":"父の腫瘍は悪性だと告げられ、家族全員がショックを受けた。","romaji":"Chichi no shuyō wa akusei da to tsugerare, kazoku zen'in ga shokku o uketa.","german":"When we were told that my father's tumor was malignant, the whole family was shocked.","furigana":"ちち|しゅよう|あくせい|つ|かぞくぜんいん|う"},{"japanese":"物価が急激に上がり、悪性インフレへの懸念が強まっている。","romaji":"Bukka ga kyūgeki ni agari, akusei infure e no kenen ga tsuyomatte iru.","german":"Prices are rising sharply, and concern about vicious inflation is growing.","furigana":"ぶっか|きゅうげき|あ|あくせい|けねん|つよ"}],"notes":"悪性 means \"malignant\" and is the opposite of 良性; 悪性腫瘍 is the formal term for cancer, plus 悪性リンパ腫 or 悪性貧血. Outside medicine it describes things that spread badly, such as 悪性インフレ (\"runaway inflation\"). Do not confuse it with 悪質な, which describes malicious human actions (悪質な詐欺 \"vicious fraud\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"がん細胞が血液を通じて他の臓器に転移する。","romaji":"Gan saibō ga ketsueki o tsūjite hoka no zōki ni ten'i suru.","german":"Cancer cells metastasize to other organs through the blood.","furigana":"さいぼう|けつえき|つう|ほか|ぞうき|てんい"},{"japanese":"検査の結果、がんはリンパ節に転移していなかった。","romaji":"Kensa no kekka, gan wa rinpasetsu ni ten'i shite inakatta.","german":"The test results showed that the cancer had not spread to the lymph nodes.","furigana":"けんさ|けっか|せつ|てんい"},{"japanese":"肝臓への転移が見つかり、治療方針を変更することになった。","romaji":"Kanzō e no ten'i ga mitsukari, chiryō hōshin o henkō suru koto ni natta.","german":"A metastasis to the liver was found, so the treatment plan was changed.","furigana":"かんぞう|てんい|み|ちりょうほうしん|へんこう"}],"notes":"転移 is medically \"metastasis\": as an intransitive する-verb がんが肺に転移する, as a noun 肺への転移, 転移がある, 遠隔転移 (\"distant metastasis\"). Do not confuse it with the reversed 移転 (\"relocation\" of an office or shop). In specialist fields, 転移 has other meanings such as 相転移 (\"phase transition\"), transfer of learning, or transference in psychoanalysis.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"腫瘍が周囲の組織に浸潤しているかを調べる。","romaji":"Shuyō ga shūi no soshiki ni shinjun shite iru ka o shiraberu.","german":"We examine whether the tumor has infiltrated the surrounding tissue.","furigana":"しゅよう|しゅうい|そしき|しんじゅん|しら"},{"japanese":"手術で、がんが筋肉の層まで浸潤していたことが分かった。","romaji":"Shujutsu de, gan ga kinniku no sō made shinjun shite ita koto ga wakatta.","german":"Surgery revealed that the cancer had invaded as far as the muscle layer.","furigana":"しゅじゅつ|きんにく|そう|しんじゅん|わ"},{"japanese":"レントゲンで肺に浸潤影が見られた。","romaji":"Rentogen de hai ni shinjun'ei ga mirareta.","german":"An infiltrate was seen in the lung on the X-ray.","furigana":"はい|しんじゅんえい|み"}],"notes":"浸潤 is \"infiltration\" in medicine: a tumor grows into neighboring tissue (〜に浸潤する), in contrast to 転移, the spread to distant organs. In X-ray findings one encounters 浸潤影 (\"infiltrate, shadowing\"). Originally the word means \"soaking through with liquid\" and, figuratively, the gradual penetration of ideas into society, but that is literary; in everyday speech one says 浸透 for it.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"熟練の外科医が執刀して難易度の高い手術を成功させた。","romaji":"Jukuren no gekai ga shittō shite nan'ido no takai shujutsu o seikō saseta.","german":"A skilled surgeon performed the operation and made the highly difficult surgery a success.","furigana":"じゅくれん|げかい|しっとう|なんいど|たか|しゅじゅつ|せいこう"},{"japanese":"今回の手術は、院長が自ら執刀することになった。","romaji":"Konkai no shujutsu wa, inchō ga mizukara shittō suru koto ni natta.","german":"The hospital director will perform this surgery himself.","furigana":"こんかい|しゅじゅつ|いんちょう|みずか|しっとう"},{"japanese":"手術の前日、執刀医から詳しい説明を受けた。","romaji":"Shujutsu no zenjitsu, shittōi kara kuwashii setsumei o uketa.","german":"The day before the surgery, I received a detailed explanation from the operating surgeon.","furigana":"しゅじゅつ|ぜんじつ|しっとうい|くわ|せつめい|う"}],"notes":"執刀 literally means \"to hold the knife\" and denotes that a doctor operates as the responsible surgeon: 医師が執刀する, 〇〇医師の執刀で手術が行われる, plus 執刀医 (\"operating surgeon\"). The general word is 手術する; the patient, on the other hand, says 手術を受ける, never 執刀. The word belongs to the medical and journalistic register.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"全身麻酔は大手術において不可欠な処置である。","romaji":"Zenshin masui wa dai shujutsu ni oite fukaketsu na shochi de aru.","german":"General anesthesia is an indispensable procedure in major surgery.","furigana":"ぜんしんますい|だいしゅじゅつ|ふかけつ|しょち"},{"japanese":"歯医者で麻酔をしてもらったので、全然痛くなかった。","romaji":"Haisha de masui o shite moratta node, zenzen itakunakatta.","german":"I had anesthesia at the dentist, so it didn't hurt at all.","furigana":"はいしゃ|ますい|ぜんぜんいた"},{"japanese":"麻酔が切れると痛みが出るので、痛み止めを飲んでください。","romaji":"Masui ga kireru to itami ga deru node, itamidome o nonde kudasai.","german":"Pain comes when the anesthesia wears off, so please take the painkiller.","furigana":"ますい|き|いた|で|いた|ど|の"}],"notes":"麻酔 covers both \"general anesthesia\" and local \"numbing\": 全身麻酔 (\"general anesthesia\") and 局所麻酔 (\"local anesthesia\"). Typical combinations are 麻酔をかける／麻酔をする, 麻酔が効く (\"takes effect\"), 麻酔が切れる (\"wears off\") and 麻酔から覚める (\"to wake up from anesthesia\"). The specialist is called 麻酔科医. The word is also perfectly normal in everyday life at the dentist.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"傷口を丁寧に縫合して感染リスクを低減する。","romaji":"Kizuguchi o teinei ni hōgō shite kansen risuku o teigen suru.","german":"The wound is carefully sutured to reduce the risk of infection.","furigana":"きずぐち|ていねい|ほうごう|かんせん|ていげん"},{"japanese":"医師は額の傷を五針縫合した。","romaji":"Ishi wa hitai no kizu o gohari hōgō shita.","german":"The doctor closed the wound on the forehead with five stitches.","furigana":"いし|ひたい|きず|ごはりほうごう"},{"japanese":"縫合した部分は、一週間後に抜糸します。","romaji":"Hōgō shita bubun wa, isshūkan go ni basshi shimasu.","german":"The stitches will be removed after one week.","furigana":"ほうごう|ぶぶん|いっしゅうかんご|ばっし"}],"notes":"縫合 is the medical word for sewing up a wound or tissue: 傷口を縫合する, 縫合糸 (\"suture material\"), 縫合不全 (\"suture failure\"). In everyday speech one simply says 縫う, especially with a stitch count: 三針縫った. The removal of the stitches afterwards is called 抜糸. For sewing clothes, only 縫う is used, never 縫合.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"外傷の応急処置としてまず止血を行う。","romaji":"Gaishō no ōkyū shochi to shite mazu shiketsu o okonau.","german":"As first aid for external injuries, one first stops the bleeding.","furigana":"がいしょう|おうきゅうしょち|しけつ|おこな"},{"japanese":"ガーゼで傷口を強く押さえて止血した。","romaji":"Gāze de kizuguchi o tsuyoku osaete shiketsu shita.","german":"I pressed hard on the wound with gauze and stopped the bleeding.","furigana":"きずぐち|つよ|お|しけつ"},{"japanese":"出血がひどく、なかなか止血できなかった。","romaji":"Shukketsu ga hidoku, nakanaka shiketsu dekinakatta.","german":"The bleeding was so severe that it could not be stopped for a long time.","furigana":"しゅっけつ|しけつ"}],"notes":"止血 is the \"stopping of bleeding\": 止血する, 止血を行う, 止血剤 (\"hemostatic agent\"), 止血帯 (\"tourniquet\"). In first aid, 圧迫止血 (\"stopping bleeding by pressure\") is a fixed term. In everyday speech one rather says 血を止める or intransitively 血が止まる; 止血 sounds medical and technical.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大量出血の患者に緊急輸血が行われた。","romaji":"Tairyō shukketsu no kanja ni kinkyū yuketsu ga okonawareta.","german":"An emergency transfusion was performed on the patient with heavy bleeding.","furigana":"たいりょうしゅっけつ|かんじゃ|きんきゅうゆけつ|おこな"},{"japanese":"手術の前に、輸血が必要になる場合があると説明された。","romaji":"Shujutsu no mae ni, yuketsu ga hitsuyō ni naru baai ga aru to setsumei sareta.","german":"Before the surgery, I was told that a blood transfusion might become necessary.","furigana":"しゅじゅつ|まえ|ゆけつ|ひつよう|ばあい|せつめい"},{"japanese":"宗教上の理由で輸血を拒否する患者もいる。","romaji":"Shūkyōjō no riyū de yuketsu o kyohi suru kanja mo iru.","german":"Some patients refuse blood transfusions for religious reasons.","furigana":"しゅうきょうじょう|りゆう|ゆけつ|きょひ|かんじゃ"}],"notes":"輸血 is the \"blood transfusion\": 患者に輸血する, 輸血を受ける, 輸血用血液. The counterpart on the donor side is 献血 (\"blood donation\"), which is often confused in everyday life. In informed-consent discussions one hears 輸血が必要になる場合がある; refusal for religious reasons is called 輸血拒否.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"臓器移植は末期臓器不全の患者にとって最後の希望である。","romaji":"Zōki ishoku wa makki zōki fuzen no kanja ni totte saigo no kibō de aru.","german":"Organ transplantation is the last hope for patients with end-stage organ failure.","furigana":"ぞうきいしょく|まっきぞうきふぜん|かんじゃ|さいご|きぼう"},{"japanese":"兄から腎臓の移植を受けることになった。","romaji":"Ani kara jinzō no ishoku o ukeru koto ni natta.","german":"I am going to receive a kidney transplant from my older brother.","furigana":"あに|じんぞう|いしょく|う"},{"japanese":"春になったら、苗を畑に移植する。","romaji":"Haru ni nattara, nae o hatake ni ishoku suru.","german":"When spring comes, I will transplant the seedlings to the field.","furigana":"はる|なえ|はたけ|いしょく"}],"notes":"移植 is transitive: 臓器を移植する, the recipient says 移植を受ける; plus 臓器移植, 骨髄移植, 移植手術 and ドナー (\"donor\"). Originally it means the transplanting of plants (苗を移植する), for which 植え替える is also said in everyday speech. In IT, 移植 also stands for porting software to another platform.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"臓器移植後の拒絶反応を免疫抑制剤で抑える。","romaji":"Zōki ishoku go no kyozetsu hannō o men'eki yokuseizai de osaeru.","german":"Rejection after an organ transplant is suppressed with immunosuppressants.","furigana":"ぞうきいしょくご|きょぜつはんのう|めんえきよくせいざい|おさ"},{"japanese":"移植した腎臓に拒絶反応が起きた。","romaji":"Ishoku shita jinzō ni kyozetsu hannō ga okita.","german":"The transplanted kidney suffered a rejection reaction.","furigana":"いしょく|じんぞう|きょぜつはんのう|お"},{"japanese":"新しい評価制度には、社員から強い拒絶反応が出た。","romaji":"Atarashii hyōka seido ni wa, shain kara tsuyoi kyozetsu hannō ga deta.","german":"The new evaluation system provoked strong resistance from employees.","furigana":"あたら|ひょうかせいど|しゃいん|つよ|きょぜつはんのう|で"}],"notes":"拒絶反応 is the body's \"rejection reaction\" against a transplant: 拒絶反応が起きる／出る, 拒絶反応を抑える (e.g. with 免疫抑制剤). Figuratively it denotes a violent, almost instinctive rejection of ideas or changes: 〜に拒絶反応を示す. The figurative use is common in newspapers and conversation.","levelNote":"Compound: 拒絶 (N1) + 反応 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"薬理学では薬物が生体に及ぼす作用を研究する。","romaji":"Yakurigaku de wa yakubutsu ga seitai ni oyobosu sayō o kenkyū suru.","german":"Pharmacology studies the effects of drugs on living organisms.","furigana":"やくりがく|やくぶつ|せいたい|およ|さよう|けんきゅう"},{"japanese":"この成分の薬理作用は、まだ十分に解明されていない。","romaji":"Kono seibun no yakuri sayō wa, mada jūbun ni kaimei sarete inai.","german":"The pharmacological action of this ingredient has not yet been fully elucidated.","furigana":"せいぶん|やくりさよう|じゅうぶん|かいめい"},{"japanese":"漢方薬の薬理効果を科学的に検証する研究が進んでいる。","romaji":"Kanpōyaku no yakuri kōka o kagakuteki ni kenshō suru kenkyū ga susunde iru.","german":"Research that scientifically verifies the pharmacological effects of Kampo medicines is progressing.","furigana":"かんぽうやく|やくりこうか|かがくてき|けんしょう|けんきゅう|すす"}],"notes":"薬理 denotes how a drug acts in the body and occurs almost only in compounds: 薬理作用 (\"pharmacological action\"), 薬理効果, 薬理学 (\"pharmacology\" as a subject). On its own it is rare, e.g. in lecture titles. Do not confuse it with 薬学 (\"pharmacy\", the course of study of pharmacists) or the more everyday 薬効 (\"efficacy of a medicine\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"抗生物質の投与によって細菌感染を治療する。","romaji":"Kōsei busshitsu no tōyo ni yotte saikin kansen o chiryō suru.","german":"Bacterial infections are treated by administering antibiotics.","furigana":"こうせいぶっしつ|とうよ|さいきんかんせん|ちりょう"},{"japanese":"痛みが強いため、患者に鎮痛剤を投与した。","romaji":"Itami ga tsuyoi tame, kanja ni chintsūzai o tōyo shita.","german":"Because the pain was severe, the patient was given a painkiller.","furigana":"いた|つよ|かんじゃ|ちんつうざい|とうよ"},{"japanese":"この薬は、子どもへの投与が認められていない。","romaji":"Kono kusuri wa, kodomo e no tōyo ga mitomerarete inai.","german":"This drug is not approved for administration to children.","furigana":"くすり|こ|とうよ|みと"}],"notes":"投与 is the technical word for \"administering\" a medication: 患者に薬を投与する, where the recipient takes に; plus 投与量 (\"dose\"), 経口投与 (\"oral administration\"), 静脈内投与. In everyday speech one says 薬を出す, 飲ませる or 注射を打つ instead. Similar is 投薬 (\"medication\"), which refers more to giving drugs as a form of treatment.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"薬剤師が処方箋に基づいて調剤を行う。","romaji":"Yakuzaishi ga shohōsen ni motozuite chōzai o okonau.","german":"A pharmacist dispenses medication based on the prescription.","furigana":"やくざいし|しょほうせん|もと|ちょうざい|おこな"},{"japanese":"病院で処方箋をもらい、近くの調剤薬局で薬を受け取った。","romaji":"Byōin de shohōsen o morai, chikaku no chōzai yakkyoku de kusuri o uketotta.","german":"I got a prescription at the hospital and picked up the medicine at a nearby dispensing pharmacy.","furigana":"びょういん|しょほうせん|ちか|ちょうざいやっきょく|くすり|う|と"},{"japanese":"調剤には時間がかかるので、少々お待ちください。","romaji":"Chōzai ni wa jikan ga kakaru node, shōshō omachi kudasai.","german":"Dispensing takes time, so please wait a moment.","furigana":"ちょうざい|じかん|しょうしょう|ま"}],"notes":"調剤 is the compounding and handing out of medicines according to a prescription, the work of the 薬剤師: 処方箋に基づいて調剤する. The doctor 処方する (\"prescribes\"), the pharmacist 調剤する. A pharmacy that fills prescriptions is called 調剤薬局, as opposed to a drugstore (ドラッグストア) with over-the-counter products.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この薬は一日三回食後に内服してください。","romaji":"Kono kusuri wa ichinichi sankai shokugo ni naifuku shite kudasai.","german":"Please take this medicine orally three times a day after meals.","furigana":"くすり|いちにちさんかいしょくご|ないふく"},{"japanese":"現在内服している薬があれば教えてください。","romaji":"Genzai naifuku shite iru kusuri ga areba oshiete kudasai.","german":"Please tell me if you are currently taking any medicine.","furigana":"げんざいないふく|くすり|おし"},{"japanese":"内服薬と塗り薬が一つずつ処方された。","romaji":"Naifukuyaku to nurigusuri ga hitotsu zutsu shohō sareta.","german":"I was prescribed one oral medicine and one ointment.","furigana":"ないふくやく|ぬ|ぐすり|ひと|しょほう"}],"notes":"内服 is \"internal use\", i.e. taking a medicine by mouth: 薬を内服する, 内服薬. It sounds medical and formal and appears on package inserts or in doctors' questions; patients themselves say 薬を飲む. The opposite is 外用 (\"external use\"); a medicine only as needed is called 頓服.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"外用薬は皮膚に直接塗布して使用する。","romaji":"Gaiyōyaku wa hifu ni chokusetsu tofu shite shiyō suru.","german":"External medicines are applied directly to the skin.","furigana":"がいようやく|ひふ|ちょくせつとふ|しよう"},{"japanese":"この軟膏は外用のみです。口に入れないでください。","romaji":"Kono nankō wa gaiyō nomi desu. Kuchi ni irenaide kudasai.","german":"This ointment is for external use only. Do not put it in your mouth.","furigana":"なんこう|がいよう|くち|い"},{"japanese":"腰の痛みに、外用薬として湿布が処方された。","romaji":"Koshi no itami ni, gaiyōyaku to shite shippu ga shohō sareta.","german":"For my lower back pain, I was prescribed a medicated patch as an external medicine.","furigana":"こし|いた|がいようやく|しっぷ|しょほう"}],"notes":"外用 means \"external use\" and appears mainly in 外用薬, 外用剤 or the warning 外用のみ (\"for external use only\"). The opposite is 内服. In everyday speech one speaks more concretely of 塗り薬 (\"ointment\") or 貼り薬／湿布 (\"patch, compress\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この疾患は早期発見であれば予後は良好である。","romaji":"Kono shikkan wa sōki hakken de areba yogo wa ryōkō de aru.","german":"If this disease is detected early, the prognosis is good.","furigana":"しっかん|そうきはっけん|よご|りょうこう"},{"japanese":"医師から、父の病気は予後が厳しいと告げられた。","romaji":"Ishi kara, chichi no byōki wa yogo ga kibishii to tsugerareta.","german":"The doctor told us that my father's illness has a grim prognosis.","furigana":"いし|ちち|びょうき|よご|きび|つ"},{"japanese":"新しい治療法によって、患者の予後が大きく改善した。","romaji":"Atarashii chiryōhō ni yotte, kanja no yogo ga ōkiku kaizen shita.","german":"The new treatment has greatly improved patients' prognosis.","furigana":"あたら|ちりょうほう|かんじゃ|よご|おお|かいぜん"}],"notes":"予後 is the medical \"prognosis\", i.e. the expected further course of a disease: 予後が良い／悪い, 予後良好, 予後不良. It is not used for weather, the economy or elections; there one says 予報, 予測 or 見通し. The older meaning \"recovery after an illness\" is rare today.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"化学療法によってがんが寛解状態に入った。","romaji":"Kagaku ryōhō ni yotte gan ga kankai jōtai ni haitta.","german":"Chemotherapy has put the cancer into remission.","furigana":"かがくりょうほう|かんかいじょうたい|はい"},{"japanese":"白血病が寛解し、三年ぶりに職場に復帰した。","romaji":"Hakketsubyō ga kankai shi, sannen buri ni shokuba ni fukki shita.","german":"After his leukemia went into remission, he returned to work for the first time in three years.","furigana":"はっけつびょう|かんかい|さんねん|しょくば|ふっき"},{"japanese":"うつ病は寛解と再発を繰り返すことがある。","romaji":"Utsubyō wa kankai to saihatsu o kurikaesu koto ga aru.","german":"Depression can alternate between remission and relapse.","furigana":"びょう|かんかい|さいはつ|く|かえ"}],"notes":"寛解 is \"remission\": the symptoms have disappeared or declined markedly, but a return is possible. It is therefore deliberately distinguished from 完治 (\"complete cure\"). Typical are 寛解する, 寛解状態, 寛解期, 完全寛解; the word is used for cancer, leukemia, autoimmune and mental illnesses.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"治療後も定期的に検査して再発を監視する。","romaji":"Chiryō go mo teikiteki ni kensa shite saihatsu o kanshi suru.","german":"Even after treatment, regular checkups are done to monitor for recurrence.","furigana":"ちりょうご|ていきてき|けんさ|さいはつ|かんし"},{"japanese":"腰痛が再発して、また病院に通い始めた。","romaji":"Yōtsū ga saihatsu shite, mata byōin ni kayoi hajimeta.","german":"My back pain has come back, and I have started going to the hospital again.","furigana":"ようつう|さいはつ|びょういん|かよ|はじ"},{"japanese":"同じ事故の再発を防ぐため、作業手順を見直した。","romaji":"Onaji jiko no saihatsu o fusegu tame, sagyō tejun o minaoshita.","german":"To prevent a recurrence of the same accident, we reviewed the work procedures.","furigana":"おな|じこ|さいはつ|ふせ|さぎょうてじゅん|みなお"}],"notes":"再発 is intransitive: 病気が再発する (\"occurs again\"). Besides illnesses it often appears with accidents, mistakes and scandals, especially in the fixed phrase 再発防止 (\"prevention of recurrence\") in apologies and reports. Do not confuse it with 再発行 (\"reissuing\" of a card or document).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"糖尿病の合併症として腎障害や網膜症がある。","romaji":"Tōnyōbyō no gappeishō to shite jinshōgai ya mōmakushō ga aru.","german":"Complications of diabetes include kidney damage and retinopathy.","furigana":"とうにょうびょう|がっぺいしょう|じんしょうがい|もうまくしょう"},{"japanese":"手術には、感染症などの合併症が起こるリスクがあります。","romaji":"Shujutsu ni wa, kansenshō nado no gappeishō ga okoru risuku ga arimasu.","german":"Surgery carries the risk of complications such as infection.","furigana":"しゅじゅつ|かんせんしょう|がっぺいしょう|お"},{"japanese":"高齢者はインフルエンザから肺炎などの合併症を起こしやすい。","romaji":"Kōreisha wa infuruenza kara haien nado no gappeishō o okoshiyasui.","german":"Elderly people easily develop complications such as pneumonia from influenza.","furigana":"こうれいしゃ|はいえん|がっぺいしょう|お"}],"notes":"合併症 is a \"complication\" in the medical sense, i.e. an additional disease resulting from an illness or procedure: 〜の合併症, 合併症を起こす／引き起こす, 合併症のリスク. It is not used for complications in projects or in life; there one uses 問題, トラブル or 面倒なこと. The word 合併 alone means \"merger\" (会社の合併).","levelNote":"Compound: 合併 (N1) + 症 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"患者の既往歴を詳しく聴取して治療方針を決定する。","romaji":"Kanja no kiōreki o kuwashiku chōshu shite chiryō hōshin o kettei suru.","german":"The patient's medical history is taken in detail to decide the treatment plan.","furigana":"かんじゃ|きおうれき|くわ|ちょうしゅ|ちりょうほうしん|けってい"},{"japanese":"問診票に既往歴とアレルギーの有無を記入してください。","romaji":"Monshinhyō ni kiōreki to arerugī no umu o kinyū shite kudasai.","german":"Please fill in your medical history and any allergies on the questionnaire.","furigana":"もんしんひょう|きおうれき|うむ|きにゅう"},{"japanese":"父には心臓病の既往歴があるので、毎年検査を受けている。","romaji":"Chichi ni wa shinzōbyō no kiōreki ga aru node, maitoshi kensa o ukete iru.","german":"My father has a history of heart disease, so he gets checked every year.","furigana":"ちち|しんぞうびょう|きおうれき|まいとしけんさ|う"}],"notes":"既往歴 is the list of a patient's earlier illnesses, i.e. the \"medical history\": 〜の既往歴がある, 既往歴を聴取する. A single earlier illness is called 既往症, the illnesses in the family 家族歴. In everyday speech one rather says 病歴 or 前に〜をしたことがある.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"問診によって患者の自覚症状を把握する。","romaji":"Monshin ni yotte kanja no jikaku shōjō o haaku suru.","german":"The patient's subjective symptoms are grasped through the medical interview.","furigana":"もんしん|かんじゃ|じかくしょうじょう|はあく"},{"japanese":"受付で問診票に記入してから、お待ちください。","romaji":"Uketsuke de monshinhyō ni kinyū shite kara, omachi kudasai.","german":"Please fill out the medical questionnaire at reception and then wait.","furigana":"うけつけ|もんしんひょう|きにゅう|ま"},{"japanese":"問診では、いつから咳が出ているかを聞かれた。","romaji":"Monshin de wa, itsu kara seki ga dete iru ka o kikareta.","german":"In the interview, I was asked since when I had had a cough.","furigana":"もんしん|せき|で|き"}],"notes":"問診 is the doctor's questioning of the patient about complaints and history, the first step of the examination (診察). It includes 問診票 (\"medical questionnaire\") and 問診する. It forms a series with 視診 (\"inspection\"), 触診 (\"palpation\"), 聴診 (\"auscultation\") and 打診 (\"percussion\"). 問診 is the conversation itself, 既往歴 its content.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"触診によって腹部の異常を確認する。","romaji":"Shokushin ni yotte fukubu no ijō o kakunin suru.","german":"Abnormalities in the abdomen are confirmed by palpation.","furigana":"しょくしん|ふくぶ|いじょう|かくにん"},{"japanese":"医師がおなかを触診して、痛む場所を確かめた。","romaji":"Ishi ga onaka o shokushin shite, itamu basho o tashikameta.","german":"The doctor palpated my abdomen to locate the painful spot.","furigana":"いし|しょくしん|いた|ばしょ|たし"},{"japanese":"月に一度、乳房の自己触診をすることが勧められている。","romaji":"Tsuki ni ichido, nyūbō no jiko shokushin o suru koto ga susumerarete iru.","german":"It is recommended to perform a breast self-exam once a month.","furigana":"つき|いちど|にゅうぼう|じこしょくしん|すす"}],"notes":"触診 is the doctor's \"palpation\" with the hands: おなかを触診する, 触診で〜が分かる. It belongs to the series 問診, 視診, 触診, 聴診. Also well known is 自己触診 (\"self-examination\"), e.g. for breast cancer screening. In everyday speech one simply says 触る or 押さえてみる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"聴診器で心音や呼吸音を確認する。","romaji":"Chōshinki de shin'on ya kokyūon o kakunin suru.","german":"Heart and breath sounds are checked with a stethoscope.","furigana":"ちょうしんき|しんおん|こきゅうおん|かくにん"},{"japanese":"聴診で心臓に雑音があることが分かった。","romaji":"Chōshin de shinzō ni zatsuon ga aru koto ga wakatta.","german":"Auscultation revealed a murmur in the heart.","furigana":"ちょうしん|しんぞう|ざつおん|わ"},{"japanese":"背中から聴診しますので、服を少し上げてください。","romaji":"Senaka kara chōshin shimasu node, fuku o sukoshi agete kudasai.","german":"I will listen to your back, so please lift your shirt a little.","furigana":"せなか|ちょうしん|ふく|すこ|あ"}],"notes":"聴診 is the \"listening\" to the heart, lungs or intestines with the 聴診器 (\"stethoscope\"): 胸を聴診する, 聴診器を当てる. It belongs to the series 問診, 触診, 聴診. In everyday speech the process is rather described as 胸の音を聞く; 聴診 itself is medical vocabulary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"打診によって胸腔内の異常を推測する。","romaji":"Dashin ni yotte kyōkūnai no ijō o suisoku suru.","german":"Percussion is used to infer abnormalities in the chest cavity.","furigana":"だしん||いじょう|すいそく"},{"japanese":"医師は患者のおなかを指で打診した。","romaji":"Ishi wa kanja no onaka o yubi de dashin shita.","german":"The doctor percussed the patient's abdomen with his fingers.","furigana":"いし|かんじゃ|ゆび|だしん"},{"japanese":"部長から、来月の大阪への異動を打診された。","romaji":"Buchō kara, raigetsu no Ōsaka e no idō o dashin sareta.","german":"My department head sounded me out about a transfer to Osaka next month.","furigana":"ぶちょう|らいげつ|おおさか|いどう|だしん"}],"notes":"打診 has two meanings. Medically it is \"percussion\": the doctor taps the chest or abdomen to recognize changes from the sound (胸部を打診する). Much more common, however, is the figurative meaning \"to sound out, to inquire non-bindingly\": 〜に出演を打診する, 意向を打診する, 異動を打診される. With it one asks cautiously before an official offer is made. As a する-verb it is transitive.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"腫瘍の性質を調べるために生検を実施する。","romaji":"Shuyō no seishitsu o shiraberu tame ni seiken o jisshi suru.","german":"A biopsy is performed to examine the nature of the tumor.","furigana":"しゅよう|せいしつ|しら|せいけん|じっし"},{"japanese":"生検の結果が出るまで、一週間ほどかかります。","romaji":"Seiken no kekka ga deru made, isshūkan hodo kakarimasu.","german":"It takes about a week until the biopsy results come out.","furigana":"せいけん|けっか|で|いっしゅうかん"},{"japanese":"胃カメラの際に、念のため生検も行った。","romaji":"I kamera no sai ni, nen no tame seiken mo okonatta.","german":"During the gastroscopy, a biopsy was also taken just in case.","furigana":"い|さい|ねん|せいけん|おこな"}],"notes":"生検 is the short form of 生体組織検査 and means \"biopsy\", i.e. the taking and examination of tissue: 生検を行う／実施する, 生検を受ける, 生検の結果, 針生検 (\"needle biopsy\"). It is technical language; doctors often explain it to patients in other words: 組織を少し取って調べる. Typically, 生検 clarifies whether a finding is 良性 or 悪性.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"内視鏡検査で消化管内部の異常を観察する。","romaji":"Naishikyō kensa de shōkakan naibu no ijō o kansatsu suru.","german":"Abnormalities inside the digestive tract are observed by endoscopy.","furigana":"ないしきょうけんさ|しょうかかんないぶ|いじょう|かんさつ"},{"japanese":"内視鏡を使った手術なら、傷口が小さくて済む。","romaji":"Naishikyō o tsukatta shujutsu nara, kizuguchi ga chiisakute sumu.","german":"With surgery using an endoscope, the wound can be kept small.","furigana":"ないしきょう|つか|しゅじゅつ|きずぐち|ちい|す"},{"japanese":"大腸の内視鏡検査の前日は、消化のよい食事をとってください。","romaji":"Daichō no naishikyō kensa no zenjitsu wa, shōka no yoi shokuji o totte kudasai.","german":"The day before a colonoscopy, please eat easily digestible food.","furigana":"だいちょう|ないしきょうけんさ|ぜんじつ|しょうか|しょくじ"}],"notes":"内視鏡 is the \"endoscope\"; the examination is called 内視鏡検査 (\"endoscopy\"), e.g. 胃の内視鏡検査 or 大腸内視鏡検査. In everyday speech, a gastroscopy is usually called 胃カメラ. 内視鏡手術 means minimally invasive procedures; the device is inserted: 内視鏡を入れる／挿入する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"造影剤を使ったCT検査で血管の状態を確認する。","romaji":"Zōeizai o tsukatta shītī kensa de kekkan no jōtai o kakunin suru.","german":"The condition of the blood vessels is checked with a contrast-enhanced CT scan.","furigana":"ぞうえいざい|つか|けんさ|けっかん|じょうたい|かくにん"},{"japanese":"造影剤を注射すると、体が少し熱く感じることがあります。","romaji":"Zōeizai o chūsha suru to, karada ga sukoshi atsuku kanjiru koto ga arimasu.","german":"When the contrast agent is injected, your body may feel a little warm.","furigana":"ぞうえいざい|ちゅうしゃ|からだ|すこ|あつ|かん"},{"japanese":"心臓の血管を調べるために、カテーテルで血管造影を行った。","romaji":"Shinzō no kekkan o shiraberu tame ni, katēteru de kekkan zōei o okonatta.","german":"An angiogram was performed using a catheter to examine the blood vessels of the heart.","furigana":"しんぞう|けっかん|しら|けっかんぞうえい|おこな"}],"notes":"造影 is the imaging of vessels or organs with the help of a contrast agent in X-ray, CT or MRI. The word appears almost only in compounds: 造影剤 (\"contrast agent\"), 造影CT, 造影検査, 血管造影 (\"angiography\"). Before such examinations, patients are asked about allergies to 造影剤.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"MRIやCTなどの画像診断技術が発展している。","romaji":"Emuāruai ya shītī nado no gazō shindan gijutsu ga hatten shite iru.","german":"Diagnostic imaging technologies such as MRI and CT are advancing.","furigana":"がぞうしんだんぎじゅつ|はってん"},{"japanese":"画像診断の結果、骨折は見つからなかった。","romaji":"Gazō shindan no kekka, kossetsu wa mitsukaranakatta.","german":"Diagnostic imaging found no fracture.","furigana":"がぞうしんだん|けっか|こっせつ|み"},{"japanese":"AIを使った画像診断で、医師の見落としを減らす試みが進んでいる。","romaji":"Ēai o tsukatta gazō shindan de, ishi no miotoshi o herasu kokoromi ga susunde iru.","german":"Attempts to reduce missed findings by doctors through AI-based diagnostic imaging are progressing.","furigana":"つか|がぞうしんだん|いし|みお|へ|こころ|すす"}],"notes":"画像診断 is \"diagnostic imaging\", i.e. the assessment of X-ray, CT, MRI or ultrasound images (レントゲン, CT, MRI, エコー). 画像検査 refers more to taking the images themselves, 画像診断 to their interpretation by specialist physicians (放射線科医, 画像診断医). The phrase 画像診断の結果 is common; recently also AIによる画像診断.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"画像検査で臓器に病変が認められた。","romaji":"Gazō kensa de zōki ni byōhen ga mitomerareta.","german":"A lesion in the organ was identified on imaging.","furigana":"がぞうけんさ|ぞうき|びょうへん|みと"},{"japanese":"内視鏡で、胃に小さな病変が見つかった。","romaji":"Naishikyō de, i ni chiisana byōhen ga mitsukatta.","german":"A small lesion was found in the stomach during the endoscopy.","furigana":"ないしきょう|い|ちい|びょうへん|み"},{"japanese":"病変の範囲が広いため、手術ですべて取り除くのは難しい。","romaji":"Byōhen no han'i ga hiroi tame, shujutsu de subete torinozoku no wa muzukashii.","german":"Since the lesion covers a wide area, it is difficult to remove all of it by surgery.","furigana":"びょうへん|はんい|ひろ|しゅじゅつ|と|のぞ|むずか"}],"notes":"病変 denotes a \"pathological change\" or \"lesion\" in tissue, often before it is determined which disease is involved: 病変が見つかる／認められる, 病変の範囲, 前がん病変, 皮膚病変. It is not the same as 病気 (\"illness\") or 病状 (\"condition of a disease\"). The word belongs in medical findings and conversations with doctors.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"血流の途絶により組織が壊死に陥った。","romaji":"Ketsuryū no tozetsu ni yori soshiki ga eshi ni ochiitta.","german":"The tissue became necrotic because blood flow was cut off.","furigana":"けつりゅう|とぜつ|そしき|えし|おちい"},{"japanese":"ひどい凍傷で、足の指が壊死してしまった。","romaji":"Hidoi tōshō de, ashi no yubi ga eshi shite shimatta.","german":"Due to severe frostbite, his toes became necrotic.","furigana":"とうしょう|あし|ゆび|えし"},{"japanese":"毒ヘビにかまれると、傷の周りが壊死することがある。","romaji":"Dokuhebi ni kamareru to, kizu no mawari ga eshi suru koto ga aru.","german":"After a venomous snake bite, the tissue around the wound can die.","furigana":"どく|きず|まわ|えし"}],"notes":"壊死 is the \"dying off\" of body tissue, necrosis. The reading え is unusual, since 壊 is otherwise mostly read かい. As a する-verb it is intransitive: 組織が壊死する, also 壊死に陥る. Causes include circulatory disorders, frostbite or poisons. Related is 壊疽 (\"gangrene\"); in everyday speech one says, simplified, 組織が死ぬ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"心筋の虚血が狭心症の原因となる。","romaji":"Shinkin no kyoketsu ga kyōshinshō no gen'in to naru.","german":"Ischemia of the heart muscle is the cause of angina pectoris.","furigana":"しんきん|きょけつ|きょうしんしょう|げんいん"},{"japanese":"虚血の状態が長く続くと、組織は壊死してしまう。","romaji":"Kyoketsu no jōtai ga nagaku tsuzuku to, soshiki wa eshi shite shimau.","german":"If the ischemic state continues for a long time, the tissue becomes necrotic.","furigana":"きょけつ|じょうたい|なが|つづ|そしき|えし"},{"japanese":"父は虚血性心疾患で、定期的に通院している。","romaji":"Chichi wa kyoketsusei shinshikkan de, teikiteki ni tsūin shite iru.","german":"My father has ischemic heart disease and goes to the hospital regularly.","furigana":"ちち|きょけつせいしんしっかん|ていきてき|つういん"}],"notes":"虚血 is \"ischemia\", reduced blood supply to a tissue, usually due to narrowed or blocked vessels. Common in 虚血性心疾患 (\"ischemic heart disease\": 狭心症 and 心筋梗塞), 脳虚血 and 一過性脳虚血発作. Do not confuse it with 貧血, which means \"anemia\" or, colloquially, a fainting spell.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"血栓が脳血管を塞ぐと脳梗塞を引き起こす。","romaji":"Kessen ga nō kekkan o fusagu to nō kōsoku o hikiokosu.","german":"When a thrombus blocks a cerebral blood vessel, it causes a cerebral infarction.","furigana":"けっせん|のうけっかん|ふさ|のうこうそく|ひ|お"},{"japanese":"長時間飛行機に座っていると、足に血栓ができることがある。","romaji":"Chōjikan hikōki ni suwatte iru to, ashi ni kessen ga dekiru koto ga aru.","german":"If you sit on a plane for a long time, a blood clot can form in your leg.","furigana":"ちょうじかんひこうき|すわ|あし|けっせん"},{"japanese":"血栓を防ぐため、血液をサラサラにする薬を飲んでいる。","romaji":"Kessen o fusegu tame, ketsueki o sarasara ni suru kusuri o nonde iru.","german":"I take a blood-thinning medicine to prevent blood clots.","furigana":"けっせん|ふせ|けつえき|くすり|の"}],"notes":"血栓 is a \"blood clot\" (thrombus) that forms in a vessel: 血栓ができる, 血栓が詰まる, 血栓を溶かす, 血栓症. It is well known through the エコノミークラス症候群 (\"economy class syndrome\") after long sitting. If a clot detaches and blocks a vessel elsewhere, it is called 塞栓; the resulting tissue death is a 梗塞 (脳梗塞, 心筋梗塞).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"肺塞栓症は静脈血栓が肺動脈を閉塞する疾患である。","romaji":"Hai sokusenshō wa jōmyaku kessen ga hai dōmyaku o heisoku suru shikkan de aru.","german":"Pulmonary embolism is a disease in which a venous thrombus blocks the pulmonary artery.","furigana":"はいそくせんしょう|じょうみゃくけっせん|はいどうみゃく|へいそく|しっかん"},{"japanese":"血栓が血流に乗って移動し、別の場所で塞栓を起こすことがある。","romaji":"Kessen ga ketsuryū ni notte idō shi, betsu no basho de sokusen o okosu koto ga aru.","german":"A blood clot can travel with the bloodstream and cause an embolism in another place.","furigana":"けっせん|けつりゅう|の|いどう|べつ|ばしょ|そくせん|お"},{"japanese":"出血を止めるために、カテーテルによる動脈塞栓術が行われた。","romaji":"Shukketsu o tomeru tame ni, katēteru ni yoru dōmyaku sokusenjutsu ga okonawareta.","german":"To stop the bleeding, arterial embolization was performed with a catheter.","furigana":"しゅっけつ|と|どうみゃくそくせんじゅつ|おこな"}],"notes":"塞栓 is actually the \"embolus\", a plug of clot, air or fat that travels with the blood and blocks a vessel; the clinical picture \"embolism\" is called 塞栓症 (肺塞栓症, 脳塞栓症). Unlike 血栓, the blockage does not form at the site. 塞栓術 is deliberate \"embolization\", e.g. to treat bleeding or tumors.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"動脈硬化は心血管疾患の主要なリスク因子である。","romaji":"Dōmyaku kōka wa shin kekkan shikkan no shuyō na risuku inshi de aru.","german":"Arteriosklerose ist ein Hauptrisikofaktor für Herz-Kreislauf-Erkrankungen.","furigana":"どうみゃくこうか|しんけっかんしっかん|しゅよう|いんし"},{"japanese":"健康診断で、年齢の割に動脈硬化が進んでいると言われた。","romaji":"Kenkō shindan de, nenrei no wari ni dōmyaku kōka ga susunde iru to iwareta.","german":"Beim Gesundheitscheck sagte man mir, meine Arterienverkalkung sei für mein Alter weit fortgeschritten.","furigana":"けんこうしんだん|ねんれい|わり|どうみゃくこうか|すす|い"},{"japanese":"組織が大きくなりすぎて、会社全体が動脈硬化を起こしている。","romaji":"Soshiki ga ōkiku narisugite, kaisha zentai ga dōmyaku kōka o okoshite iru.","german":"Die Firma ist zu groß geworden und völlig verkrustet.","furigana":"そしき|おお|かいしゃぜんたい|どうみゃくこうか|お"}],"notes":"動脈硬化 ist die „Arteriosklerose“ oder „Arterienverkalkung“: 動脈硬化が進む, 動脈硬化を予防する, Risikofaktoren sind 高血圧, 喫煙 und 糖尿病. Übertragen beschreibt es wie das deutsche „verkalkt“ oder „verkrustet“ die Erstarrung von Organisationen oder Denkweisen: 組織が動脈硬化を起こしている. Diese Bildsprache ist in Zeitungen und Reden geläufig.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不整脈の種類によっては生命に関わる場合がある。","romaji":"Fuseimyaku no shurui ni yotte wa seimei ni kakawaru baai ga aru.","german":"Depending on the type of arrhythmia, it can be life-threatening.","furigana":"ふせいみゃく|しゅるい|せいめい|かか|ばあい"},{"japanese":"最近、時々脈が飛ぶので調べたら、軽い不整脈だった。","romaji":"Saikin, tokidoki myaku ga tobu node shirabetara, karui fuseimyaku datta.","german":"Recently my pulse sometimes skips, so I had it checked, and it was a mild arrhythmia.","furigana":"さいきん|ときどきみゃく|と|しら|かる|ふせいみゃく"},{"japanese":"ストレスや睡眠不足が不整脈の引き金になることもある。","romaji":"Sutoresu ya suimin busoku ga fuseimyaku no hikigane ni naru koto mo aru.","german":"Stress and lack of sleep can also trigger arrhythmia.","furigana":"すいみんぶそく|ふせいみゃく|ひ|がね"}],"notes":"不整脈 is \"arrhythmia\" (heart rhythm disorder): 不整脈がある／出る／見つかる. It includes e.g. 頻脈 (\"pulse too fast\"), 徐脈 (\"pulse too slow\") and 心房細動 (\"atrial fibrillation\"). Patients often describe the feeling colloquially as 脈が飛ぶ (\"the pulse skips\") or ドキドキする.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"慢性心不全の患者には生活習慣の改善が重要である。","romaji":"Mansei shin fuzen no kanja ni wa seikatsu shūkan no kaizen ga jūyō de aru.","german":"Improving lifestyle habits is important for patients with chronic heart failure.","furigana":"まんせいしんふぜん|かんじゃ|せいかつしゅうかん|かいぜん|じゅうよう"},{"japanese":"祖父は心不全で亡くなった。","romaji":"Sofu wa shin fuzen de nakunatta.","german":"My grandfather died of heart failure.","furigana":"そふ|しんふぜん|な"},{"japanese":"心不全が悪化すると、少し歩いただけで息切れするようになる。","romaji":"Shin fuzen ga akka suru to, sukoshi aruita dake de ikigire suru yō ni naru.","german":"If heart failure worsens, you get out of breath after walking just a little.","furigana":"しんふぜん|あっか|すこ|ある|いきぎ"}],"notes":"心不全 is \"heart failure\": the heart no longer pumps enough blood. One distinguishes 慢性心不全 and 急性心不全; in obituaries one often reads 心不全で死去. The pattern 〜不全 (\"failure, insufficiency\") recurs in 腎不全, 呼吸不全 and 多臓器不全. Do not confuse it with 心筋梗塞 (\"heart attack\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"慢性腎不全が進行すると透析治療が必要になる。","romaji":"Mansei jin fuzen ga shinkō suru to tōseki chiryō ga hitsuyō ni naru.","german":"When chronic kidney failure progresses, dialysis treatment becomes necessary.","furigana":"まんせいじんふぜん|しんこう|とうせきちりょう|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"糖尿病が原因で腎不全になる人は少なくない。","romaji":"Tōnyōbyō ga gen'in de jin fuzen ni naru hito wa sukunaku nai.","german":"Quite a few people develop kidney failure because of diabetes.","furigana":"とうにょうびょう|げんいん|じんふぜん|ひと|すく"},{"japanese":"急性腎不全は、早く治療すれば回復することも多い。","romaji":"Kyūsei jin fuzen wa, hayaku chiryō sureba kaifuku suru koto mo ōi.","german":"Acute kidney failure often resolves if treated early.","furigana":"きゅうせいじんふぜん|はや|ちりょう|かいふく|おお"}],"notes":"腎不全 is \"kidney failure\"; 腎 is the technical syllable for \"kidney\" (腎臓, 腎機能). One distinguishes 急性腎不全, which is often curable, and 慢性腎不全, which in the final stage (末期腎不全) requires 透析 or a 腎移植. Typical verbs are 腎不全になる and, more formally, 腎不全に陥る.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人工透析は腎機能を代替する治療法である。","romaji":"Jinkō tōseki wa jin kinō o daitai suru chiryōhō de aru.","german":"Artificial dialysis is a treatment that replaces kidney function.","furigana":"じんこうとうせき|じんきのう|だいたい|ちりょうほう"},{"japanese":"父は週に三回、病院で透析を受けている。","romaji":"Chichi wa shū ni sankai, byōin de tōseki o ukete iru.","german":"My father goes to the hospital for dialysis three times a week.","furigana":"ちち|しゅう|さんかい|びょういん|とうせき|う"},{"japanese":"透析は一回四時間ほどかかるので、仕事との両立が大変だ。","romaji":"Tōseki wa ikkai yojikan hodo kakaru node, shigoto to no ryōritsu ga taihen da.","german":"Dialysis takes about four hours each time, so balancing it with work is hard.","furigana":"とうせき|いっかいよじかん|しごと|りょうりつ|たいへん"}],"notes":"透析 is \"dialysis\"; in medical practice mostly 人工透析 or 血液透析 (\"hemodialysis\") and 腹膜透析 (\"peritoneal dialysis\"). Patients say 透析を受ける or 透析に通う; a dialysis patient is a 透析患者. In chemistry, 透析 also denotes the separation process through a membrane (transitive: 溶液を透析する).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"慢性的なアルコール摂取が肝硬変の原因となりうる。","romaji":"Manseiteki na arukōru sesshu ga kankōhen no gen'in to nariuru.","german":"Chronic alcohol consumption can cause liver cirrhosis.","furigana":"まんせいてき|せっしゅ|かんこうへん|げんいん"},{"japanese":"肝炎を放っておくと、肝硬変に進むことがある。","romaji":"Kan'en o hōtte oku to, kankōhen ni susumu koto ga aru.","german":"If hepatitis is left untreated, it can progress to cirrhosis.","furigana":"かんえん|ほう|かんこうへん|すす"},{"japanese":"叔父は肝硬変と診断され、お酒をきっぱりやめた。","romaji":"Oji wa kankōhen to shindan sare, osake o kippari yameta.","german":"My uncle was diagnosed with liver cirrhosis and quit drinking completely.","furigana":"おじ|かんこうへん|しんだん|さけ"}],"notes":"肝硬変 is \"liver cirrhosis\": 肝 (\"liver\") and 硬変 (\"hardening\"). Common causes are chronic hepatitis (B型肝炎, C型肝炎) and alcohol; a consequent risk is 肝がん. Typical are 肝硬変になる, 肝硬変と診断される and 肝炎から肝硬変に進む. A precursor is often 脂肪肝 (\"fatty liver\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"膠原病は免疫系が自己の組織を攻撃する疾患群である。","romaji":"Kōgenbyō wa men'eki kei ga jiko no soshiki o kōgeki suru shikkan gun de aru.","german":"Collagen diseases are a group of disorders in which the immune system attacks the body's own tissue.","furigana":"こうげんびょう|めんえきけい|じこ|そしき|こうげき|しっかんぐん"},{"japanese":"関節の痛みと微熱が続き、検査で膠原病と分かった。","romaji":"Kansetsu no itami to binetsu ga tsuzuki, kensa de kōgenbyō to wakatta.","german":"Joint pain and a low-grade fever continued, and tests showed it was a collagen disease.","furigana":"かんせつ|いた|びねつ|つづ|けんさ|こうげんびょう|わ"},{"japanese":"膠原病は若い女性に多い病気として知られている。","romaji":"Kōgenbyō wa wakai josei ni ōi byōki to shite shirarete iru.","german":"Collagen diseases are known to be common in young women.","furigana":"こうげんびょう|わか|じょせい|おお|びょうき|し"}],"notes":"膠原病 is a collective term for systemic autoimmune diseases of the connective tissue (\"collagen diseases\"), such as 全身性エリテマトーデス, 強皮症 or, in a broader sense, 関節リウマチ. 膠原 means \"collagen\" (膠 = \"glue\"). Caution: it sounds exactly like 抗原 (\"antigen\") but has nothing to do with it. It is treated in the department of 膠原病内科 or リウマチ科.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自己免疫疾患では免疫系が誤って正常な細胞を攻撃する。","romaji":"Jiko men'eki shikkan de wa men'eki kei ga ayamatte seijō na saibō o kōgeki suru.","german":"In autoimmune diseases, the immune system mistakenly attacks normal cells.","furigana":"じこめんえきしっかん|めんえきけい|あやま|せいじょう|さいぼう|こうげき"},{"japanese":"この病気は、自己免疫の仕組みによって起こると考えられている。","romaji":"Kono byōki wa, jiko men'eki no shikumi ni yotte okoru to kangaerarete iru.","german":"This disease is thought to be caused by an autoimmune mechanism.","furigana":"びょうき|じこめんえき|しく|お|かんが"},{"japanese":"姉は自己免疫性の甲状腺の病気で、薬を飲み続けている。","romaji":"Ane wa jiko men'ekisei no kōjōsen no byōki de, kusuri o nomitsuzukete iru.","german":"My sister has an autoimmune thyroid disease and keeps taking medication.","furigana":"あね|じこめんえきせい|こうじょうせん|びょうき|くすり|の|つづ"}],"notes":"自己免疫 is \"autoimmunity\", i.e. an immune reaction against the body itself. The word appears almost always in compounds: 自己免疫疾患 (\"autoimmune disease\"), 自己免疫性肝炎, 自己免疫性甲状腺疾患, plus 自己抗体 (\"autoantibody\"). 自己 is the formal \"self\", 免疫 \"immunity\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"mRNAワクチンは新型感染症に対する革新的な予防手段である。","romaji":"Emuāruenuē wakuchin wa shingata kansenshō ni taisuru kakushinteki na yobō shudan de aru.","german":"mRNA-Impfstoffe sind ein innovatives Präventionsmittel gegen neuartige Infektionskrankheiten.","furigana":"しんがたかんせんしょう|たい|かくしんてき|よぼうしゅだん"},{"japanese":"インフルエンザのワクチンを打ってもらった。","romaji":"Infuruenza no wakuchin o utte moratta.","german":"Ich habe mich gegen Grippe impfen lassen.","furigana":"う"},{"japanese":"子どもは決められた時期にワクチンを接種する必要がある。","romaji":"Kodomo wa kimerareta jiki ni wakuchin o sesshu suru hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"Kinder müssen zu festgelegten Zeitpunkten geimpft werden.","furigana":"こ|き|じき|せっしゅ|ひつよう"}],"notes":"ワクチン (vom deutschen „Vakzin“) ist der „Impfstoff“. Umgangssprachlich sagt man ワクチンを打つ（打ってもらう）, formell ワクチンを接種する; die Impfung selbst heißt ワクチン接種 oder allgemeiner 予防接種. Gebräuchlich sind auch インフルエンザワクチン, mRNAワクチン und 定期接種 für die Pflichtimpfungen von Kindern. Selten bezeichnet das Wort auch Antivirensoftware.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"抗生物質の乱用は耐性菌の発生を促進する。","romaji":"Kōsei busshitsu no ran'yō wa taisei kin no hassei o sokushin suru.","german":"Overuse of antibiotics promotes the emergence of resistant bacteria.","furigana":"こうせいぶっしつ|らんよう|たいせいきん|はっせい|そくしん"},{"japanese":"風邪はウイルスが原因なので、抗生物質は効かない。","romaji":"Kaze wa uirusu ga gen'in na node, kōsei busshitsu wa kikanai.","german":"Colds are caused by viruses, so antibiotics don't work.","furigana":"かぜ|げんいん|こうせいぶっしつ|き"},{"japanese":"抗生物質は、症状が治まっても最後まで飲み切ってください。","romaji":"Kōsei busshitsu wa, shōjō ga osamatte mo saigo made nomikitte kudasai.","german":"Please finish the antibiotics to the end, even if your symptoms subside.","furigana":"こうせいぶっしつ|しょうじょう|おさ|さいご|の|き"}],"notes":"抗生物質 is the \"antibiotic\": 抗生物質を飲む, 抗生物質を出す／処方する, 抗生物質が効く. In clinics the short form 抗生剤 is often heard; in technical language today mostly 抗菌薬. They do not work against viruses, including ordinary colds; wrong use promotes 耐性菌.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"耐性菌の出現は世界的な公衆衛生上の脅威である。","romaji":"Taiseikin no shutsugen wa sekaiteki na kōshū eisei jō no kyōi de aru.","german":"The emergence of drug-resistant bacteria is a worldwide public health threat.","furigana":"たいせいきん|しゅつげん|せかいてき|こうしゅうえいせいじょう|きょうい"},{"japanese":"抗生物質を安易に使うと、耐性菌が生まれやすくなる。","romaji":"Kōsei busshitsu o an'i ni tsukau to, taiseikin ga umareyasuku naru.","german":"If antibiotics are used carelessly, resistant bacteria arise more easily.","furigana":"こうせいぶっしつ|あんい|つか|たいせいきん|う"},{"japanese":"院内で耐性菌の感染が広がり、病棟が一時閉鎖された。","romaji":"Innai de taiseikin no kansen ga hirogari, byōtō ga ichiji heisa sareta.","german":"An infection with resistant bacteria spread in the hospital, and the ward was temporarily closed.","furigana":"いんない|たいせいきん|かんせん|ひろ|びょうとう|いちじへいさ"}],"notes":"耐性菌 are \"resistant bacteria\", bacteria against which antibiotics no longer work: 耐性 (\"resistance, tolerance\") + 菌 (\"bacterium\"). Technically it is also 薬剤耐性菌 or 多剤耐性菌 (\"multidrug-resistant bacteria\"), a well-known example being MRSA. Typical verbs are 耐性菌が生まれる／出現する／広がる; particularly feared is 院内感染 (\"hospital-acquired infection\") by 耐性菌.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"化学療法はがん治療の主要な柱の一つである。","romaji":"Kagaku ryōhō wa gan chiryō no shuyō na hashira no hitotsu de aru.","german":"Chemotherapy is one of the main pillars of cancer treatment.","furigana":"かがくりょうほう|ちりょう|しゅよう|はしら|ひと"},{"japanese":"手術の後、再発を防ぐために化学療法を受けた。","romaji":"Shujutsu no ato, saihatsu o fusegu tame ni kagaku ryōhō o uketa.","german":"After the surgery, I received chemotherapy to prevent a recurrence.","furigana":"しゅじゅつ|あと|さいはつ|ふせ|かがくりょうほう|う"},{"japanese":"化学療法の副作用で、髪の毛が抜けてしまった。","romaji":"Kagaku ryōhō no fukusayō de, kami no ke ga nukete shimatta.","german":"My hair fell out because of the side effects of chemotherapy.","furigana":"かがくりょうほう|ふくさよう|かみ|け|ぬ"}],"notes":"化学療法 is \"chemotherapy\"; in conversation with patients one often says 抗がん剤治療, among doctors also shortened to 化療. Typical are 化学療法を受ける／行う, 化学療法の副作用 (hair loss, nausea). Together with 手術 and 放射線療法 it forms the three classic pillars of cancer treatment.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"放射線療法は腫瘍細胞を標的にして破壊する治療法である。","romaji":"Hōshasen ryōhō wa shuyō saibō o hyōteki ni shite hakai suru chiryōhō de aru.","german":"Radiation therapy is a treatment that targets and destroys tumor cells.","furigana":"ほうしゃせんりょうほう|しゅようさいぼう|ひょうてき|はかい|ちりょうほう"},{"japanese":"放射線療法は、毎日少しずつ数週間にわたって行われる。","romaji":"Hōshasen ryōhō wa, mainichi sukoshi zutsu sūshūkan ni watatte okonawareru.","german":"Radiation therapy is delivered in small daily doses over several weeks.","furigana":"ほうしゃせんりょうほう|まいにちすこ|すうしゅうかん|おこな"},{"japanese":"手術が難しい場所なので、放射線療法を選んだ。","romaji":"Shujutsu ga muzukashii basho na node, hōshasen ryōhō o eranda.","german":"Because the tumor is in a place where surgery is difficult, we chose radiation therapy.","furigana":"しゅじゅつ|むずか|ばしょ|ほうしゃせんりょうほう|えら"}],"notes":"放射線療法 is \"radiation therapy\"; in daily life and on clinic signs, 放射線治療 means the same and is more common. Irradiation is described with 放射線を当てる or, technically, 照射する. It is often chosen when surgery is difficult, and combined with 化学療法.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"免疫療法は体の免疫力を活用してがんと戦う新しい治療法である。","romaji":"Men'eki ryōhō wa karada no men'ekiryoku o katsuyō shite gan to tatakau atarashii chiryōhō de aru.","german":"Immunotherapy is a new treatment that uses the body's immune power to fight cancer.","furigana":"めんえきりょうほう|からだ|めんえきりょく|かつよう|たたか|あたら|ちりょうほう"},{"japanese":"スギ花粉症の治療として、舌下免疫療法を始めた。","romaji":"Sugi kafunshō no chiryō to shite, zekka men'eki ryōhō o hajimeta.","german":"I started sublingual immunotherapy to treat my cedar pollen allergy.","furigana":"かふんしょう|ちりょう|ぜっかめんえきりょうほう|はじ"},{"japanese":"免疫療法の一部は、まだ効果が十分に証明されていない。","romaji":"Men'eki ryōhō no ichibu wa, mada kōka ga jūbun ni shōmei sarete inai.","german":"The effectiveness of some immunotherapies has not yet been sufficiently proven.","furigana":"めんえきりょうほう|いちぶ|こうか|じゅうぶん|しょうめい"}],"notes":"免疫療法 denotes treatments that use or modulate the immune system. In cancer medicine one speaks of がん免疫療法 (e.g. with 免疫チェックポイント阻害薬), for allergies of アレルゲン免疫療法 or 舌下免疫療法 (\"sublingual immunotherapy\"). Since private clinics also offer unproven 免疫療法, the word often appears in the media with critical remarks.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"緩和ケアは患者の苦痛を軽減し生活の質を向上させる。","romaji":"Kanwa kea wa kanja no kutsū o keigen shi seikatsu no shitsu o kōjō saseru.","german":"Palliative care reduces the patient's suffering and improves quality of life.","furigana":"かんわ|かんじゃ|くつう|けいげん|せいかつ|しつ|こうじょう"},{"japanese":"緩和ケアは、末期の患者だけのものではない。","romaji":"Kanwa kea wa, makki no kanja dake no mono de wa nai.","german":"Palliative care is not only for terminal patients.","furigana":"かんわ|まっき|かんじゃ"},{"japanese":"母は最期の数か月を緩和ケア病棟で穏やかに過ごした。","romaji":"Haha wa saigo no sūkagetsu o kanwa kea byōtō de odayaka ni sugoshita.","german":"My mother spent her last few months peacefully in a palliative care ward.","furigana":"はは|さいご|すう|げつ|かんわ|びょうとう|おだ|す"}],"notes":"緩和ケア is \"palliative care\": 緩和 (\"relief\") + ケア. It relieves pain and emotional burden and today often begins already at diagnosis, not only at the end of life. Typical are 緩和ケアを受ける, 緩和ケア病棟 (\"palliative care ward\") and ホスピス. 緩和 alone is also known from 規制緩和 (\"deregulation\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新薬の安全性は臨床試験を通じて確認される。","romaji":"Shin'yaku no anzensei wa rinshō shiken o tsūjite kakunin sareru.","german":"The safety of new drugs is confirmed through clinical trials.","furigana":"しんやく|あんぜんせい|りんしょうしけん|つう|かくにん"},{"japanese":"新しい薬の臨床試験に参加する患者を募集している。","romaji":"Atarashii kusuri no rinshō shiken ni sanka suru kanja o boshū shite iru.","german":"We are recruiting patients to participate in a clinical trial of a new drug.","furigana":"あたら|くすり|りんしょうしけん|さんか|かんじゃ|ぼしゅう"},{"japanese":"このワクチンは、臨床試験の最終段階に入った。","romaji":"Kono wakuchin wa, rinshō shiken no saishū dankai ni haitta.","german":"This vaccine has entered the final stage of clinical trials.","furigana":"りんしょうしけん|さいしゅうだんかい|はい"}],"notes":"臨床試験 is a \"clinical trial\" on humans: 臨床試験を行う, 臨床試験に参加する, 臨床試験の段階. 臨床 means \"at the bedside, clinical\". Trials for drug approval are specifically called 治験; this word is seen more often in everyday life, e.g. in ads for 治験ボランティア.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"疫学調査によって感染症の拡大パターンが明らかになった。","romaji":"Ekigaku chōsa ni yotte kansenshō no kakudai patān ga akiraka ni natta.","german":"An epidemiological survey revealed the pattern of spread of the infectious disease.","furigana":"えきがくちょうさ|かんせんしょう|かくだい|あき"},{"japanese":"疫学の研究によると、喫煙者は肺がんになるリスクが高い。","romaji":"Ekigaku no kenkyū ni yoru to, kitsuensha wa haigan ni naru risuku ga takai.","german":"According to epidemiological research, smokers have a higher risk of lung cancer.","furigana":"えきがく|けんきゅう|きつえんしゃ|はい|たか"},{"japanese":"大学で公衆衛生を学び、疫学を専門にしている。","romaji":"Daigaku de kōshū eisei o manabi, ekigaku o senmon ni shite iru.","german":"I studied public health at university and specialize in epidemiology.","furigana":"だいがく|こうしゅうえいせい|まな|えきがく|せんもん"}],"notes":"疫学 is \"epidemiology\"; 疫 means \"epidemic\" as in 疫病 and 免疫. Typical are 疫学調査 (e.g. the contact tracing by public health centers), 疫学的, 疫学者 and 疫学の研究. Today epidemiology studies not only infections but also associations such as smoking and cancer.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"高齢者の罹患率は若年層と比べて高い。","romaji":"Kōreisha no rikanritsu wa jakunen sō to kurabete takai.","german":"The morbidity rate among the elderly is higher than among young people.","furigana":"こうれいしゃ|りかんりつ|じゃくねんそう|くら|たか"},{"japanese":"父は五十代で糖尿病に罹患した。","romaji":"Chichi wa gojūdai de tōnyōbyō ni rikan shita.","german":"My father contracted diabetes in his fifties.","furigana":"ちち|ごじゅうだい|とうにょうびょう|りかん"},{"japanese":"感染症に罹患した職員は、回復するまで出勤できない。","romaji":"Kansenshō ni rikan shita shokuin wa, kaifuku suru made shukkin dekinai.","german":"Staff who have contracted an infectious disease cannot come to work until they recover.","furigana":"かんせんしょう|りかん|しょくいん|かいふく|しゅっきん"}],"notes":"罹患 is the medical-statistical technical term for \"to fall ill with something\"; as a する-verb the disease takes に: がんに罹患する. In everyday speech one says 病気にかかる instead. It is common in compounds such as 罹患率 (\"morbidity rate, incidence\") or 罹患者 (\"affected persons\"). Since 罹 is not a Jōyō kanji, authorities often write り患.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"糖尿病の有病率は世界的に増加傾向にある。","romaji":"Tōnyōbyō no yūbyōritsu wa sekaiteki ni zōka keikō ni aru.","german":"The prevalence of diabetes is trending upward worldwide.","furigana":"とうにょうびょう|ゆうびょうりつ|せかいてき|ぞうかけいこう"},{"japanese":"この地域では高血圧の有病率が全国平均より高い。","romaji":"Kono chiiki de wa kōketsuatsu no yūbyōritsu ga zenkoku heikin yori takai.","german":"In this region, the prevalence of hypertension is higher than the national average.","furigana":"ちいき|こうけつあつ|ゆうびょうりつ|ぜんこくへいきん|たか"},{"japanese":"調査の結果、花粉症の有病率は十年前の約二倍になっていた。","romaji":"Chōsa no kekka, kafunshō no yūbyōritsu wa jūnen mae no yaku nibai ni natte ita.","german":"The survey found that the prevalence of hay fever had roughly doubled compared with ten years ago.","furigana":"ちょうさ|けっか|かふんしょう|ゆうびょうりつ|じゅうねんまえ|やくにばい"}],"notes":"有病率 is an epidemiological technical term: the share of the population suffering from a disease at a given point in time. To be distinguished from 罹患率, which measures new cases in a period. Typical are 〜の有病率, 有病率が高い／低い and 有病率が増加する. The word is encountered almost only in statistics, reports and news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この感染症の致死率は約二パーセントと報告されている。","romaji":"Kono kansenshō no chishiritsu wa yaku ni pāsento to hōkoku sarete iru.","german":"The case fatality rate of this infectious disease is reported to be about two percent.","furigana":"かんせんしょう|ちしりつ|やくに|ほうこく"},{"japanese":"エボラ出血熱は致死率が非常に高いことで知られている。","romaji":"Ebora shukketsunetsu wa chishiritsu ga hijō ni takai koto de shirarete iru.","german":"Ebola hemorrhagic fever is known for its extremely high case fatality rate.","furigana":"しゅっけつねつ|ちしりつ|ひじょう|たか|し"},{"japanese":"早期に治療を始めれば、致死率を大きく下げられる。","romaji":"Sōki ni chiryō o hajimereba, chishiritsu o ōkiku sagerareru.","german":"If treatment is started early, the fatality rate can be reduced significantly.","furigana":"そうき|ちりょう|はじ|ちしりつ|おお|さ"}],"notes":"致死率 denotes the share of those who fall ill with a disease and die of it (case fatality). Do not confuse it with 死亡率, which gives mortality relative to the whole population. Common are 致死率が高い／低い, 致死率を下げる and statements such as 致死率〇パーセント. Related is 致死量 (\"lethal dose\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この病気の潜伏期間は通常二週間から四週間である。","romaji":"Kono byōki no senpuku kikan wa tsūjō ni shūkan kara yon shūkan de aru.","german":"The incubation period of this disease is usually two to four weeks.","furigana":"びょうき|せんぷくきかん|つうじょうにしゅうかん|よんしゅうかん"},{"japanese":"潜伏期間中でも、他の人にうつす可能性がある。","romaji":"Senpuku kikanchū demo, hoka no hito ni utsusu kanōsei ga aru.","german":"Even during the incubation period, it is possible to pass it on to other people.","furigana":"せんぷくきかんちゅう|ほか|ひと|かのうせい"},{"japanese":"潜伏期間を過ぎても症状が出なければ、感染していないと考えられる。","romaji":"Senpuku kikan o sugite mo shōjō ga denakereba, kansen shite inai to kangaerareru.","german":"If no symptoms appear even after the incubation period has passed, the person is considered not to be infected.","furigana":"せんぷくきかん|す|しょうじょう|で|かんせん|かんが"}],"notes":"潜伏期間 is the time between infection and the appearance of symptoms; the shorter form 潜伏期 is also used. 潜伏 on its own means \"to lie hidden\" and is also used for culprits who have gone into hiding. Typical expressions: 潜伏期間は〇日, 潜伏期間中, 潜伏期間を過ぎる. Common in health information and the news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"病原体の特定が効果的な治療法の開発に不可欠である。","romaji":"Byōgentai no tokutei ga kōkateki na chiryōhō no kaihatsu ni fukaketsu de aru.","german":"Identifying the pathogen is essential for developing an effective treatment.","furigana":"びょうげんたい|とくてい|こうかてき|ちりょうほう|かいはつ|ふかけつ"},{"japanese":"ウイルスや細菌など、病気を引き起こすものをまとめて病原体と呼ぶ。","romaji":"Uirusu ya saikin nado, byōki o hikiokosu mono o matomete byōgentai to yobu.","german":"Viruses, bacteria and anything else that causes disease are collectively called pathogens.","furigana":"さいきん|びょうき|ひ|お|びょうげんたい|よ"},{"japanese":"手術室では病原体が持ち込まれないよう厳しく管理されている。","romaji":"Shujutsushitsu de wa byōgentai ga mochikomarenai yō kibishiku kanri sarete iru.","german":"In operating rooms, strict controls are in place so that no pathogens are brought in.","furigana":"しゅじゅつしつ|びょうげんたい|も|こ|きび|かんり"}],"notes":"病原体 is the umbrella term for anything that causes disease: viruses, bacteria, fungi, parasites. 病原菌, by contrast, refers only to bacteria or germs. Common combinations: 病原体を特定する, 病原体に感染する, 病原体が侵入する. The word belongs to technical language; in everyday speech people tend to say ウイルス or 菌.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"インフルエンザは主に飛沫感染によって広がる。","romaji":"Infuruenza wa omo ni himatsu kansen ni yotte hirogaru.","german":"Influenza spreads mainly through droplet infection.","furigana":"おも|ひまつかんせん|ひろ"},{"japanese":"飛沫感染を防ぐため、咳が出るときはマスクをしてください。","romaji":"Himatsu kansen o fusegu tame, seki ga deru toki wa masuku o shite kudasai.","german":"To prevent droplet infection, please wear a mask when you have a cough.","furigana":"ひまつかんせん|ふせ|せき|で"},{"japanese":"会話の際の飛沫感染を減らすため、テーブルに仕切りが設置された。","romaji":"Kaiwa no sai no himatsu kansen o herasu tame, tēburu ni shikiri ga setchi sareta.","german":"Partitions were installed on the tables to reduce droplet infection during conversation.","furigana":"かいわ|さい|ひまつかんせん|へ|しき|せっち"}],"notes":"飛沫感染 is infection via droplets released when coughing, sneezing or speaking; 飛沫 on its own means \"spray, droplets\". Its sister terms are 接触感染 (\"contact infection\") and 空気感染 (\"airborne transmission\"). Typical: 飛沫感染で広がる, 飛沫感染を防ぐ. Since the Covid period the word has also become very familiar in everyday announcements.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"手洗いは接触感染を予防する最も基本的な方法である。","romaji":"Tearai wa sesshoku kansen o yobō suru mottomo kihonteki na hōhō de aru.","german":"Hand washing is the most basic way to prevent contact infection.","furigana":"てあら|せっしょくかんせん|よぼう|もっと|きほんてき|ほうほう"},{"japanese":"ドアノブやつり革を介した接触感染にも注意が必要だ。","romaji":"Doanobu ya tsurikawa o kaishita sesshoku kansen ni mo chūi ga hitsuyō da.","german":"Caution is also needed against contact infection via doorknobs and train straps.","furigana":"かわ|かい|せっしょくかんせん|ちゅうい|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"この細菌は主に接触感染でうつるので、タオルの共用は避けてください。","romaji":"Kono saikin wa omo ni sesshoku kansen de utsuru node, taoru no kyōyō wa sakete kudasai.","german":"This bacterium is spread mainly by contact infection, so please avoid sharing towels.","furigana":"さいきん|おも|せっしょくかんせん|きょうよう|さ"}],"notes":"接触感染 is transmission through direct touch or via objects such as doorknobs and towels. It usually appears alongside 飛沫感染 and 空気感染 when routes of transmission are explained. Typical frames: 接触感染でうつる, 接触感染を防ぐ／予防する. Register: technical language, but also common in hygiene advice.","levelNote":"Compound: 接触 (N1) + 感染 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"手術器具は使用前に徹底的に滅菌される。","romaji":"Shujutsu kigu wa shiyō mae ni tetteiteki ni mekkin sareru.","german":"Surgical instruments are thoroughly sterilized before use.","furigana":"しゅじゅつきぐ|しようまえ|てっていてき|めっきん"},{"japanese":"このガーゼは滅菌済みなので、袋から出してそのまま使えます。","romaji":"Kono gāze wa mekkinzumi na node, fukuro kara dashite sono mama tsukaemasu.","german":"This gauze is already sterilized, so you can take it out of the bag and use it as it is.","furigana":"めっきんず|ふくろ|だ|つか"},{"japanese":"研究室では、使用した培地を高温高圧で滅菌してから廃棄する。","romaji":"Kenkyūshitsu de wa, shiyō shita baichi o kōon kōatsu de mekkin shite kara haiki suru.","german":"In the laboratory, used culture medium is sterilized at high temperature and pressure before being discarded.","furigana":"けんきゅうしつ|しよう|ばいち|こうおんこうあつ|めっきん|はいき"}],"notes":"滅菌 means to kill all microorganisms completely, which makes it stricter than 殺菌 (\"killing germs\"), 消毒 (\"disinfecting\") or the advertising word 除菌 (\"reducing germs\"). As a する-verb it is transitive: 器具を滅菌する, often in the passive 滅菌される. Very common is 滅菌済み (\"sterile-packed\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先天性心疾患は出生時から存在する心臓の異常である。","romaji":"Sentensei shin shikkan wa shusseiji kara sonzai suru shinzō no ijō de aru.","german":"Congenital heart disease is a heart abnormality that is present from birth.","furigana":"せんてんせいしんしっかん|しゅっせいじ|そんざい|しんぞう|いじょう"},{"japanese":"息子は先天性の心臓病で、生まれてすぐに手術を受けた。","romaji":"Musuko wa sentensei no shinzōbyō de, umarete sugu ni shujutsu o uketa.","german":"My son has a congenital heart disease and had surgery right after he was born.","furigana":"むすこ|せんてんせい|しんぞうびょう|う|しゅじゅつ|う"},{"japanese":"この病気は先天性のものではなく、生活習慣が原因だ。","romaji":"Kono byōki wa sentensei no mono de wa naku, seikatsu shūkan ga gen'in da.","german":"This disease is not congenital; its cause is lifestyle.","furigana":"びょうき|せんてんせい|せいかつしゅうかん|げんいん"}],"notes":"先天性 is a noun that stands attributively with の or directly before a disease name: 先天性の病気, 先天性心疾患. It is mainly medical; for general talents people say 先天的な才能 or 生まれつき. The opposite is 後天性 (\"acquired\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"後天性免疫不全症候群は免疫系を破壊する疾患である。","romaji":"Kōtensei men'eki fuzen shōkōgun wa men'eki kei o hakai suru shikkan de aru.","german":"Acquired immunodeficiency syndrome is a disease that destroys the immune system.","furigana":"こうてんせいめんえきふぜんしょうこうぐん|めんえきけい|はかい|しっかん"},{"japanese":"彼の難聴は先天性ではなく、事故による後天性のものだ。","romaji":"Kare no nanchō wa sentensei de wa naku, jiko ni yoru kōtensei no mono da.","german":"His hearing loss is not congenital but acquired, caused by an accident.","furigana":"かれ|なんちょう|せんてんせい|じこ|こうてんせい"},{"japanese":"後天性の病気の多くは、生活習慣を見直すことで予防できる。","romaji":"Kōtensei no byōki no ōku wa, seikatsu shūkan o minaosu koto de yobō dekiru.","german":"Many acquired diseases can be prevented by reviewing one's lifestyle.","furigana":"こうてんせい|びょうき|おお|せいかつしゅうかん|みなお|よぼう"}],"notes":"後天性 means \"acquired only after birth\" and is the opposite of 先天性. It appears with の or as the first element of disease names: 後天性の難聴, 後天性免疫不全症候群 (AIDS). Outside medicine, the な-adjective 後天的 (後天的な能力) is used more often.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"遺伝性疾患は親から子へと受け継がれる遺伝子異常による。","romaji":"Idensei shikkan wa oya kara ko e to uketsugareru idenshi ijō ni yoru.","german":"Hereditary diseases are caused by genetic abnormalities passed from parents to children.","furigana":"いでんせいしっかん|おや|こ|う|つ|いでんしいじょう"},{"japanese":"この病気には遺伝性があると言われている。","romaji":"Kono byōki ni wa idensei ga aru to iwarete iru.","german":"This disease is said to be hereditary.","furigana":"びょうき|いでんせい|い"},{"japanese":"母の病気は遺伝性のものではないと、医師に説明された。","romaji":"Haha no byōki wa idensei no mono de wa nai to, ishi ni setsumei sareta.","german":"The doctor explained that my mother's illness is not hereditary.","furigana":"はは|びょうき|いでんせい|いし|せつめい"}],"notes":"遺伝性 denotes the property of being \"hereditary\". It mostly stands before nouns, directly or with の: 遺伝性疾患 (\"hereditary disease\"), 遺伝性の病気; also 遺伝性がある/ない. The form 遺伝性な is rare. Distinguish it from 遺伝的 (\"genetic, by inheritance\", e.g. 遺伝的な要因) and 先天性 (\"congenital\"), which does not necessarily mean inherited. Medical terminology, also used in health articles.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"基礎代謝率は年齢とともに低下する傾向がある。","romaji":"Kiso taisha ritsu wa nenrei to tomo ni teika suru keikō ga aru.","german":"The basal metabolic rate tends to decline with age.","furigana":"きそたいしゃりつ|ねんれい|ていか|けいこう"},{"japanese":"運動して筋肉を増やすと、代謝が上がって太りにくくなる。","romaji":"Undō shite kinniku o fuyasu to, taisha ga agatte futorinikuku naru.","german":"If you exercise and build muscle, your metabolism goes up and you gain weight less easily.","furigana":"うんどう|きんにく|ふ|たいしゃ|あ|ふと"},{"japanese":"赤ちゃんは新陳代謝が活発なので、汗をたくさんかく。","romaji":"Akachan wa shinchin taisha ga kappatsu na node, ase o takusan kaku.","german":"Babies have an active metabolism, so they sweat a lot.","furigana":"あか|しんちんたいしゃ|かっぱつ|あせ"}],"notes":"代謝 is metabolism; in everyday speech you hear especially 代謝がいい／悪い, 代謝を上げる and 基礎代謝 (\"basal metabolic rate\"). Even more common is 新陳代謝, which is also used figuratively for the renewal of an organization. In this second sense of \"renewal\", 代謝 can occur as an intransitive する-verb, but this is rare.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"甲状腺ホルモンは代謝の調節に重要な役割を果たす。","romaji":"Kōjōsen horumon wa taisha no chōsetsu ni jūyō na yakuwari o hatasu.","german":"Thyroid hormones play an important role in regulating metabolism.","furigana":"こうじょうせん|たいしゃ|ちょうせつ|じゅうよう|やくわり|は"},{"japanese":"思春期にはホルモンのバランスが大きく変化する。","romaji":"Shishunki ni wa horumon no baransu ga ōkiku henka suru.","german":"During puberty, the hormone balance changes greatly.","furigana":"ししゅんき|おお|へんか"},{"japanese":"今夜は駅前の店でホルモンを焼いて食べよう。","romaji":"Kon'ya wa ekimae no mise de horumon o yaite tabeyō.","german":"Let's grill and eat offal at the place near the station tonight.","furigana":"こんや|えきまえ|みせ|や|た"}],"notes":"ホルモン is the hormone; typical are ホルモンバランス, ホルモンが分泌される and 女性ホルモン／成長ホルモン. Caution: in a food context ホルモン means grilled offal (ホルモン焼き), which is a popular dish in Japan. The word is always written in katakana.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 47 (source: N4; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"内分泌系はホルモンを通じて体の機能を調節する。","romaji":"Naibunpitsu kei wa horumon o tsūjite karada no kinō o chōsetsu suru.","german":"The endocrine system regulates bodily functions through hormones.","furigana":"ないぶんぴつけい|つう|からだ|きのう|ちょうせつ"},{"japanese":"甲状腺の病気は内分泌内科で診てもらえる。","romaji":"Kōjōsen no byōki wa naibunpitsu naika de mite moraeru.","german":"Thyroid diseases can be treated at an endocrinology department.","furigana":"こうじょうせん|びょうき|ないぶんぴつないか|み"},{"japanese":"一部の化学物質は内分泌の働きを乱すおそれがある。","romaji":"Ichibu no kagaku busshitsu wa naibunpitsu no hataraki o midasu osore ga aru.","german":"Some chemical substances may disrupt the workings of the endocrine system.","furigana":"いちぶ|かがくぶっしつ|ないぶんぴつ|はたら|みだ"}],"notes":"内分泌 is internal secretion, that is, the release of hormones directly into the blood; the opposite is 外分泌 (e.g. sweat, saliva). It mostly appears in compounds: 内分泌系 (\"hormone system\"), 内分泌内科 (\"endocrinology\"), 内分泌かく乱物質 (\"hormonally active pollutants\"). In technical language the reading ないぶんぴ is also heard.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ストレスは自律神経のバランスを崩す原因となる。","romaji":"Sutoresu wa jiritsu shinkei no baransu o kuzusu gen'in to naru.","german":"Stress can cause the balance of the autonomic nervous system to break down.","furigana":"じりつしんけい|くず|げんいん"},{"japanese":"夜更かしが続いて、自律神経が乱れているようだ。","romaji":"Yofukashi ga tsuzuite, jiritsu shinkei ga midarete iru yō da.","german":"I keep staying up late, so my autonomic nervous system seems to be out of balance.","furigana":"よふ|つづ|じりつしんけい|みだ"},{"japanese":"姉は自律神経失調症と診断され、しばらく仕事を休んでいる。","romaji":"Ane wa jiritsu shinkei shitchōshō to shindan sare, shibaraku shigoto o yasunde iru.","german":"My older sister was diagnosed with autonomic dysfunction and has been off work for a while.","furigana":"あね|じりつしんけいしっちょうしょう|しんだん|しごと|やす"}],"notes":"自律神経 is the vegetative (autonomic) nervous system, which consists of the 交感神経 and the 副交感神経. In everyday speech 自律神経が乱れる (\"to get out of balance\") is very common, for example with stress or lack of sleep, as is 自律神経を整える. The condition 自律神経失調症 (\"autonomic dysfunction\") is a common diagnosis in Japan.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"交感神経が興奮すると心拍数が増加する。","romaji":"Kōkan shinkei ga kōfun suru to shinpakusū ga zōka suru.","german":"When the sympathetic nervous system is excited, the heart rate increases.","furigana":"こうかんしんけい|こうふん|しんぱくすう|ぞうか"},{"japanese":"緊張すると交感神経が働いて、手に汗をかく。","romaji":"Kinchō suru to kōkan shinkei ga hataraite, te ni ase o kaku.","german":"When I get nervous, my sympathetic nerves kick in and my hands sweat.","furigana":"きんちょう|こうかんしんけい|はたら|て|あせ"},{"japanese":"寝る前にスマホを見ると交感神経が刺激され、眠りにくくなる。","romaji":"Neru mae ni sumaho o miru to kōkan shinkei ga shigeki sare, nemurinikuku naru.","german":"Looking at your phone before bed stimulates the sympathetic nervous system and makes it hard to fall asleep.","furigana":"ね|まえ|み|こうかんしんけい|しげき|ねむ"}],"notes":"交感神経 is the sympathetic nervous system, the part of the 自律神経 that works during tension, stress and activity. Typical expressions: 交感神経が働く, 交感神経が優位になる, 交感神経が刺激される. Its counterpart is 副交感神経. The word often appears in health guides and TV programs.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"副交感神経が優位になるとリラックス状態になる。","romaji":"Fuku kōkan shinkei ga yūi ni naru to rirakkusu jōtai ni naru.","german":"When the parasympathetic nervous system becomes dominant, you enter a relaxed state.","furigana":"ふくこうかんしんけい|ゆうい|じょうたい"},{"japanese":"ぬるめのお風呂にゆっくり入ると、副交感神経が働きやすくなる。","romaji":"Nurume no ofuro ni yukkuri hairu to, fuku kōkan shinkei ga hatarakiyasuku naru.","german":"Soaking slowly in a lukewarm bath makes it easier for the parasympathetic nervous system to work.","furigana":"ふろ|はい|ふくこうかんしんけい|はたら"},{"japanese":"深呼吸には副交感神経を高める効果があると言われる。","romaji":"Shinkokyū ni wa fuku kōkan shinkei o takameru kōka ga aru to iwareru.","german":"Deep breathing is said to have the effect of boosting the parasympathetic nervous system.","furigana":"しんこきゅう|ふくこうかんしんけい|たか|こうか|い"}],"notes":"副交感神経 is the parasympathetic nervous system, the \"rest nerve\" of the 自律神経, active during relaxation, sleep and digestion. Typical are 副交感神経が優位になる, 副交感神経が働く and 副交感神経を高める. It is almost always mentioned in contrast to 交感神経, for example in relaxation tips.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"セロトニンは気分の調節に関わる神経伝達物質である。","romaji":"Serotonin wa kibun no chōsetsu ni kakawaru shinkei dentatsu busshitsu de aru.","german":"Serotonin is a neurotransmitter involved in regulating mood.","furigana":"きぶん|ちょうせつ|かか|しんけいでんたつぶっしつ"},{"japanese":"ドーパミンなどの神経伝達物質は、やる気や快感に関係している。","romaji":"Dōpamin nado no shinkei dentatsu busshitsu wa, yaruki ya kaikan ni kankei shite iru.","german":"Neurotransmitters such as dopamine are related to motivation and pleasure.","furigana":"しんけいでんたつぶっしつ|き|かいかん|かんけい"},{"japanese":"この薬は脳内の神経伝達物質の量を調整して、うつ症状を改善する。","romaji":"Kono kusuri wa nōnai no shinkei dentatsu busshitsu no ryō o chōsei shite, utsu shōjō o kaizen suru.","german":"This medicine improves depressive symptoms by adjusting the amount of neurotransmitters in the brain.","furigana":"くすり|のうない|しんけいでんたつぶっしつ|りょう|ちょうせい|しょうじょう|かいぜん"}],"notes":"神経伝達物質 is the technical term for messenger substances such as ドーパミン, セロトニン or アドレナリン that transmit signals between nerve cells. Typical verbs: 神経伝達物質が放出される, 〜を分泌する, 〜の量を調整する. This long compound belongs in technical texts and popular-science articles; in conversation people often simply say 脳内物質.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"薬物は特定の受容体に結合して作用を発揮する。","romaji":"Yakubutsu wa tokutei no juyōtai ni ketsugō shite sayō o hakki suru.","german":"Drugs exert their effect by binding to specific receptors.","furigana":"やくぶつ|とくてい|じゅようたい|けつごう|さよう|はっき"},{"japanese":"舌の表面には、味を感じる受容体が数多く並んでいる。","romaji":"Shita no hyōmen ni wa, aji o kanjiru juyōtai ga kazuōku narande iru.","german":"Many receptors that sense taste are lined up on the surface of the tongue.","furigana":"した|ひょうめん|あじ|かん|じゅようたい|かずおお|なら"},{"japanese":"ウイルスは細胞表面の受容体を足がかりにして侵入する。","romaji":"Uirusu wa saibō hyōmen no juyōtai o ashigakari ni shite shinnyū suru.","german":"Viruses use receptors on the cell surface as a foothold to invade.","furigana":"さいぼうひょうめん|じゅようたい|あし|しんにゅう"}],"notes":"受容体 is the receptor to which a substance such as a hormone, drug or virus binds; レセプター is also used with the same meaning. Typical expressions: 受容体に結合する, 〜の受容体, 受容体を介して. It also refers to sensory receptors, for example for taste or light. Purely technical language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"抗炎症薬は痛みと腫れを軽減する効果がある。","romaji":"Kō enshōyaku wa itami to hare o keigen suru kōka ga aru.","german":"Anti-inflammatory drugs have the effect of reducing pain and swelling.","furigana":"こうえんしょうやく|いた|は|けいげん|こうか"},{"japanese":"ショウガには抗炎症作用があると言われている。","romaji":"Shōga ni wa kō enshō sayō ga aru to iwarete iru.","german":"Ginger is said to have an anti-inflammatory effect.","furigana":"こうえんしょうさよう|い"},{"japanese":"医師は腫れを抑えるために抗炎症剤の塗り薬を処方した。","romaji":"Ishi wa hare o osaeru tame ni kō enshōzai no nurigusuri o shohō shita.","german":"To reduce the swelling, the doctor prescribed an anti-inflammatory ointment.","furigana":"いし|は|おさ|こうえんしょうざい|ぬ|ぐすり|しょほう"}],"notes":"抗炎症 is made up of the prefix 抗 (\"anti-, against\") and 炎症 (\"inflammation\") and almost never stands alone but as a first element: 抗炎症作用 (\"anti-inflammatory effect\"), 抗炎症薬／抗炎症剤 (\"anti-inflammatory agent\"). The same pattern forms 抗菌, 抗ウイルス or 抗酸化. Typical of package inserts, health articles and technical texts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鎮痛剤は痛みの信号を遮断して苦痛を和らげる。","romaji":"Chintsūzai wa itami no shingō o shadan shite kutsū o yawarageru.","german":"Painkillers relieve suffering by blocking pain signals.","furigana":"ちんつうざい|いた|しんごう|しゃだん|くつう|やわ"},{"japanese":"歯医者で抜歯した後、鎮痛剤を二日分もらった。","romaji":"Haisha de basshi shita ato, chintsūzai o futsukabun moratta.","german":"After having a tooth pulled at the dentist, I was given two days' worth of painkillers.","furigana":"はいしゃ|ばっし|あと|ちんつうざい|ふつかぶん"},{"japanese":"この湿布には鎮痛効果のある成分が含まれている。","romaji":"Kono shippu ni wa chintsū kōka no aru seibun ga fukumarete iru.","german":"This poultice contains an ingredient with a pain-relieving effect.","furigana":"しっぷ|ちんつうこうか|せいぶん|ふく"}],"notes":"鎮痛 means \"pain relief\" and appears almost always in compounds: 鎮痛剤／鎮痛薬 (\"painkiller\"), 鎮痛作用, 鎮痛効果, 解熱鎮痛剤. In conversation people usually say 痛み止め instead of 鎮痛剤. As a する-verb, 鎮痛する hardly occurs; people rather say 痛みを抑える／和らげる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"解熱剤を投与して高熱を下げる処置を行った。","romaji":"Genetsuzai o tōyo shite kōnetsu o sageru shochi o okonatta.","german":"We gave a fever reducer to bring down the high fever.","furigana":"げねつざい|とうよ|こうねつ|さ|しょち|おこな"},{"japanese":"解熱剤を飲んだら、一時間ほどで熱が下がった。","romaji":"Genetsuzai o nondara, ichijikan hodo de netsu ga sagatta.","german":"After I took a fever reducer, my temperature went down in about an hour.","furigana":"げねつざい|の|いちじかん|ねつ|さ"},{"japanese":"解熱してから二日たつまでは、登園を控えてください。","romaji":"Genetsu shite kara futsuka tatsu made wa, tōen o hikaete kudasai.","german":"Please keep your child away from nursery until two days after the fever has gone down.","furigana":"げねつ|ふつか|とうえん|ひか"}],"notes":"解熱 means \"fever reduction\" and mostly appears in 解熱剤 (\"fever-reducing medicine\") or 解熱鎮痛剤. As an intransitive する-verb, 解熱する means \"the fever goes down\", typical in doctors' instructions and school rules (解熱後二日). In everyday speech people say 熱が下がる, and 熱冷まし for the medicine. Watch the reading: げねつ, not かいねつ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"利尿薬は体内の余分な水分を排出させる。","romaji":"Rinyōyaku wa tainai no yobun na suibun o haishutsu saseru.","german":"Diuretics make the body excrete excess water.","furigana":"りにょうやく|たいない|よぶん|すいぶん|はいしゅつ"},{"japanese":"コーヒーには利尿作用があるので、飲みすぎるとトイレが近くなる。","romaji":"Kōhī ni wa rinyō sayō ga aru node, nomisugiru to toire ga chikaku naru.","german":"Coffee has a diuretic effect, so if you drink too much you need the toilet more often.","furigana":"りにょうさよう|の|ちか"},{"japanese":"医師は足のむくみを取るために利尿剤を処方した。","romaji":"Ishi wa ashi no mukumi o toru tame ni rinyōzai o shohō shita.","german":"The doctor prescribed a diuretic to get rid of the swelling in my legs.","furigana":"いし|あし|と|りにょうざい|しょほう"}],"notes":"利尿 is the excretion of urine or its promotion and occurs practically only in compounds: 利尿作用 (\"diuretic effect\"), 利尿剤／利尿薬 (\"diuretic\"). In everyday life you mostly hear コーヒーには利尿作用がある. Do not confuse it with 排尿 (\"urination\"), which denotes the act itself.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"糖尿病患者は血糖値を厳密に管理する必要がある。","romaji":"Tōnyōbyō kanja wa kettōchi o genmitsu ni kanri suru hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"Diabetic patients need to manage their blood sugar level strictly.","furigana":"とうにょうびょうかんじゃ|けっとうち|げんみつ|かんり|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"食後に血糖値が急に上がるのを防ぐため、野菜から先に食べている。","romaji":"Shokugo ni kettōchi ga kyū ni agaru no o fusegu tame, yasai kara saki ni tabete iru.","german":"To prevent my blood sugar level from rising sharply after meals, I eat vegetables first.","furigana":"しょくご|けっとうち|きゅう|あ|ふせ|やさい|さき|た"},{"japanese":"健康診断で血糖値が高めだと言われた。","romaji":"Kenkō shindan de kettōchi ga takame da to iwareta.","german":"At my health checkup I was told that my blood sugar level is somewhat high.","furigana":"けんこうしんだん|けっとうち|たか|い"}],"notes":"血糖値 is the blood sugar value; typical are 血糖値が上がる／下がる, 血糖値が高い, 血糖値を測る and 血糖値の急上昇 after eating. The word is not only medical but constantly appears in health checkups, advertising and diet guides. 血糖 alone (\"blood sugar\") is rarer than 血糖値.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"高コレステロールは動脈硬化のリスク因子である。","romaji":"Kō koresuterōru wa dōmyaku kōka no risuku inshi de aru.","german":"High cholesterol is a risk factor for arteriosclerosis.","furigana":"こう|どうみゃくこうか|いんし"},{"japanese":"健康診断で、コレステロール値が高いと言われた。","romaji":"Kenkō shindan de, koresuterōru-chi ga takai to iwareta.","german":"At my health checkup I was told that my cholesterol level is high.","furigana":"けんこうしんだん|ち|たか|い"},{"japanese":"悪玉コレステロールを減らすために、食事に気をつけている。","romaji":"Akudama koresuterōru o herasu tame ni, shokuji ni ki o tsukete iru.","german":"I am careful about my diet in order to reduce bad cholesterol.","furigana":"あくだま|へ|しょくじ|き"}],"notes":"コレステロール is \"cholesterol\"; in everyday speech it is usually about blood values: コレステロール値が高い, コレステロールを下げる. Colloquially a distinction is made between 善玉コレステロール (HDL, \"good\") and 悪玉コレステロール (LDL, \"bad\"). 高コレステロール (血症) is an elevated cholesterol level. The word is ubiquitous in health topics and is always written in katakana.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"中枢神経系は脳と脊髄から構成される。","romaji":"Chūsū shinkei kei wa nō to sekizui kara kōsei sareru.","german":"The central nervous system consists of the brain and spinal cord.","furigana":"ちゅうすうしんけいけい|のう|せきずい|こうせい"},{"japanese":"アルコールは中枢神経の働きを抑える作用がある。","romaji":"Arukōru wa chūsū shinkei no hataraki o osaeru sayō ga aru.","german":"Alcohol has the effect of suppressing the activity of the central nervous system.","furigana":"ちゅうすうしんけい|はたら|おさ|さよう"},{"japanese":"中枢神経が損傷を受けると、回復には長い時間がかかる。","romaji":"Chūsū shinkei ga sonshō o ukeru to, kaifuku ni wa nagai jikan ga kakaru.","german":"If the central nervous system is damaged, recovery takes a long time.","furigana":"ちゅうすうしんけい|そんしょう|う|かいふく|なが|じかん"}],"notes":"中枢神経 refers to the brain and spinal cord; more precisely the system is called 中枢神経系. 中枢 on its own means \"center, control point\" and is also used figuratively (政治の中枢). Typical expressions: 中枢神経に作用する, 中枢神経を抑制する／刺激する. The opposite is 末梢神経.","levelNote":"Compound: 中枢 (N1) + 神経 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"糖尿病は末梢神経障害を引き起こすことがある。","romaji":"Tōnyōbyō wa masshō shinkei shōgai o hikiokosu koto ga aru.","german":"Diabetes can cause peripheral neuropathy.","furigana":"とうにょうびょう|まっしょうしんけいしょうがい|ひ|お"},{"japanese":"手足のしびれは、末梢神経の異常が原因のことがある。","romaji":"Teashi no shibire wa, masshō shinkei no ijō ga gen'in no koto ga aru.","german":"Numbness in the hands and feet can be caused by an abnormality of the peripheral nerves.","furigana":"てあし|まっしょうしんけい|いじょう|げんいん"},{"japanese":"末梢神経は脳や脊髄から全身へと網の目のように広がっている。","romaji":"Masshō shinkei wa nō ya sekizui kara zenshin e to aminome no yō ni hirogatte iru.","german":"The peripheral nerves spread from the brain and spinal cord throughout the body like a net.","furigana":"まっしょうしんけい|のう|せきずい|ぜんしん|あみ|め|ひろ"}],"notes":"末梢神経 are the nerves outside the brain and spinal cord, which branch out to the hands and feet; the opposite is 中枢神経. Common is 末梢神経障害 (\"neuropathy\"), often linked with しびれ (\"numbness, tingling\"). 末梢 alone means \"tip, periphery\"; 末梢的 means \"peripheral, of minor importance\" in the figurative sense. Since 梢 is not a Jōyō kanji, 末しょう is occasionally written.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"脳卒中の治療は発症後の迅速な対応が鍵となる。","romaji":"Nōsotchū no chiryō wa hasshō go no jinsoku na taiō ga kagi to naru.","german":"In the treatment of stroke, prompt action after onset is the key.","furigana":"のうそっちゅう|ちりょう|はっしょうご|じんそく|たいおう|かぎ"},{"japanese":"祖父は脳卒中で倒れ、救急車で運ばれた。","romaji":"Sofu wa nōsotchū de taore, kyūkyūsha de hakobareta.","german":"My grandfather collapsed from a stroke and was taken away by ambulance.","furigana":"そふ|のうそっちゅう|たお|きゅうきゅうしゃ|はこ"},{"japanese":"脳卒中の後遺症で、右手がうまく動かない。","romaji":"Nōsotchū no kōishō de, migite ga umaku ugokanai.","german":"As an after-effect of the stroke, I can't move my right hand well.","furigana":"のうそっちゅう|こういしょう|みぎて|うご"}],"notes":"脳卒中 is the umbrella term for strokes and includes 脳梗塞 (\"cerebral infarction\"), 脳出血 (\"cerebral hemorrhage\") and くも膜下出血. Typical expressions: 脳卒中で倒れる (\"to suffer a stroke\"), 脳卒中を起こす, 脳卒中の後遺症 (\"after-effects\"). The word is medical, but also common in everyday life and in the news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"認知症の早期発見には記憶力テストが有効である。","romaji":"Ninchishō no sōki hakken ni wa kiokuryoku tesuto ga yūkō de aru.","german":"A memory test is effective for the early detection of dementia.","furigana":"にんちしょう|そうきはっけん|きおくりょく|ゆうこう"},{"japanese":"母が認知症になってから、家族で交代して介護している。","romaji":"Haha ga ninchishō ni natte kara, kazoku de kōtai shite kaigo shite iru.","german":"Since my mother developed dementia, the family has been taking turns caring for her.","furigana":"はは|にんちしょう|かぞく|こうたい|かいご"},{"japanese":"ただの物忘れなのか、認知症の始まりなのか心配だ。","romaji":"Tada no monowasure na no ka, ninchishō no hajimari na no ka shinpai da.","german":"I worry whether it is just forgetfulness or the beginning of dementia.","furigana":"ものわす|にんちしょう|はじ|しんぱい"}],"notes":"認知症 has been the official term for dementia since 2004 and replaced 痴呆, which was felt to be derogatory. Typical are 認知症になる, 認知症が進む (\"to progress\"), 認知症の母 and 認知症の介護. In everyday speech it is often deliberately distinguished from harmless forgetfulness (物忘れ).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"てんかんは脳の異常な電気活動によって発作が起きる疾患である。","romaji":"Tenkan wa nō no ijō na denki katsudō ni yotte hossa ga okiru shikkan de aru.","german":"Epilepsy is a disease in which seizures occur due to abnormal electrical activity in the brain.","furigana":"のう|いじょう|でんきかつどう|ほっさ|お|しっかん"},{"japanese":"彼はてんかんの持病があるので、薬を毎日欠かさず飲んでいる。","romaji":"Kare wa tenkan no jibyō ga aru node, kusuri o mainichi kakasazu nonde iru.","german":"He has chronic epilepsy, so he takes his medicine every day without fail.","furigana":"かれ|じびょう|くすり|まいにちか|の"},{"japanese":"電車の中で男性がてんかんの発作を起こし、乗客が駅員を呼んだ。","romaji":"Densha no naka de dansei ga tenkan no hossa o okoshi, jōkyaku ga ekiin o yonda.","german":"A man had an epileptic seizure on the train, and passengers called a station attendant.","furigana":"でんしゃ|なか|だんせい|ほっさ|お|じょうきゃく|えきいん|よ"}],"notes":"てんかん (\"epilepsy\") is almost always written in hiragana, because the kanji 癲癇 are very difficult and not Jōyō. Typical expressions: てんかんの発作, てんかん発作を起こす, てんかんの持病がある. Beware of homophones such as 転換 (\"change over\") or 展観; context decides.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"細菌性肺炎は抗生物質で治療することができる。","romaji":"Saikinsei haien wa kōsei busshitsu de chiryō suru koto ga dekiru.","german":"Bacterial pneumonia can be treated with antibiotics.","furigana":"さいきんせいはいえん|こうせいぶっしつ|ちりょう"},{"japanese":"風邪をこじらせて肺炎になり、一週間入院した。","romaji":"Kaze o kojirasete haien ni nari, isshūkan nyūin shita.","german":"I let my cold drag on, developed pneumonia, and was hospitalized for a week.","furigana":"かぜ|はいえん|いっしゅうかんにゅういん"},{"japanese":"高齢者は、食べ物が気管に入って起こる誤嚥性肺炎に注意が必要だ。","romaji":"Kōreisha wa, tabemono ga kikan ni haitte okoru goensei haien ni chūi ga hitsuyō da.","german":"Elderly people need to be careful about aspiration pneumonia, which occurs when food enters the windpipe.","furigana":"こうれいしゃ|た|もの|きかん|はい|お|ごえんせいはいえん|ちゅうい|ひつよう"}],"notes":"肺炎 is pneumonia; typical are 肺炎になる, 肺炎を起こす and 風邪をこじらせて肺炎になる (\"a neglected cold turns into pneumonia\"). In older people, 誤嚥性肺炎 (\"aspiration pneumonia\") is a frequent topic. The word is an everyday one, not a purely technical term.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"結核は結核菌の空気感染によって広がる感染症である。","romaji":"Kekkaku wa kekkakukin no kūki kansen ni yotte hirogaru kansenshō de aru.","german":"Tuberculosis is an infectious disease spread by airborne transmission of the tubercle bacillus.","furigana":"けっかく|けっかくきん|くうきかんせん|ひろ|かんせんしょう"},{"japanese":"昔、結核は治らない病気として恐れられていた。","romaji":"Mukashi, kekkaku wa naoranai byōki to shite osorerarete ita.","german":"In the past, tuberculosis was feared as an incurable disease.","furigana":"むかし|けっかく|なお|びょうき|おそ"},{"japanese":"長引く咳が心配で検査を受けたら、結核だとわかった。","romaji":"Nagabiku seki ga shinpai de kensa o uketara, kekkaku da to wakatta.","german":"I was worried about a persistent cough and got tested, and it turned out to be tuberculosis.","furigana":"ながび|せき|しんぱい|けんさ|う|けっかく"}],"notes":"結核 is tuberculosis; the pathogen is called 結核菌. Typical are 結核にかかる, 結核で亡くなる and 結核の検査. It was once regarded as a national disease that cost many writers their lives; today it is rare, but has not disappeared. The second sense \"concretion\" is purely technical and rare.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"敗血症は感染が全身に広がった生命に関わる重篤な状態である。","romaji":"Haiketsushō wa kansen ga zenshin ni hirogatta seimei ni kakawaru jūtoku na jōtai de aru.","german":"Sepsis is a serious, life-threatening condition in which an infection has spread throughout the body.","furigana":"はいけつしょう|かんせん|ぜんしん|ひろ|せいめい|かか|じゅうとく|じょうたい"},{"japanese":"傷口から細菌が入り、敗血症を起こして集中治療室に運ばれた。","romaji":"Kizuguchi kara saikin ga hairi, haiketsushō o okoshite shūchū chiryōshitsu ni hakobareta.","german":"Bacteria entered through the wound, and he developed sepsis and was taken to the intensive care unit.","furigana":"きずぐち|さいきん|はい|はいけつしょう|お|しゅうちゅうちりょうしつ|はこ"},{"japanese":"敗血症は早期に治療を始めないと、命を落とすこともある。","romaji":"Haiketsushō wa sōki ni chiryō o hajimenai to, inochi o otosu koto mo aru.","german":"If treatment for sepsis is not started early, it can even cost a person's life.","furigana":"はいけつしょう|そうき|ちりょう|はじ|いのち|お"}],"notes":"敗血症 is sepsis, colloquially \"blood poisoning\": an infection spreads through the blood to the whole body. Typical are 敗血症を起こす, 敗血症になる, 敗血症で亡くなる and 敗血症性ショック. The word is medical, but appears in news reports about causes of death.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鉄欠乏性貧血は鉄分の不足によって起こる。","romaji":"Tetsu ketsubōsei hinketsu wa tetsubun no fusoku ni yotte okoru.","german":"Iron-deficiency anemia is caused by a lack of iron.","furigana":"てつけつぼうせいひんけつ|てつぶん|ふそく|お"},{"japanese":"朝礼の途中で貧血を起こして倒れてしまった。","romaji":"Chōrei no tochū de hinketsu o okoshite taorete shimatta.","german":"I fainted from anemia in the middle of the morning assembly.","furigana":"ちょうれい|とちゅう|ひんけつ|お|たお"},{"japanese":"最近疲れやすいのは、貧血気味だからかもしれない。","romaji":"Saikin tsukareyasui no wa, hinketsugimi dakara kamoshirenai.","german":"The reason I tire easily lately might be that I am a bit anemic.","furigana":"さいきんつか|ひんけつぎみ"}],"notes":"貧血 is medically anemia (\"lack of blood\"), e.g. 鉄欠乏性貧血. Colloquially, however, 貧血 (貧血を起こす, 貧血で倒れる) also stands for sudden dizziness and fainting, even when medically what is meant is rather 立ちくらみ or circulation problems. Common is 貧血気味 (\"slightly anemic\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"白血病は骨髄での異常な白血球増殖によって起こる。","romaji":"Hakketsubyō wa kotsuzui de no ijō na hakkekkyū zōshoku ni yotte okoru.","german":"Leukemia is caused by abnormal proliferation of white blood cells in the bone marrow.","furigana":"はっけつびょう|こつずい|いじょう|はっけっきゅうぞうしょく|お"},{"japanese":"彼女は十代で白血病と診断されたが、治療を経て今は元気に働いている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa jūdai de hakketsubyō to shindan sareta ga, chiryō o hete ima wa genki ni hataraite iru.","german":"She was diagnosed with leukemia in her teens, but after treatment she is now working in good health.","furigana":"かのじょ|じゅうだい|はっけつびょう|しんだん|ちりょう|へ|いま|げんき|はたら"},{"japanese":"白血病の患者を救うため、骨髄バンクへのドナー登録が呼びかけられている。","romaji":"Hakketsubyō no kanja o sukuu tame, kotsuzui banku e no donā tōroku ga yobikakerarete iru.","german":"Calls are being made to register as donors with the bone marrow bank in order to save leukemia patients.","furigana":"はっけつびょう|かんじゃ|すく|こつずい|とうろく|よ"}],"notes":"白血病 is leukemia, literally \"white-blood disease\"; a distinction is made between 急性 (\"acute\") and 慢性 (\"chronic\") forms. Typical are 白血病と診断される, 白血病を発症する and 白血病の治療. The word often comes up in connection with 骨髄移植 and donor registration (骨髄バンク).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"骨髄移植はある種の血液疾患に対する有効な治療法である。","romaji":"Kotsuzui ishoku wa aru shu no ketsueki shikkan ni taisuru yūkō na chiryōhō de aru.","german":"Knochenmarktransplantation ist eine wirksame Behandlungsmethode für bestimmte Bluterkrankungen.","furigana":"こつずいいしょく|しゅ|けつえきしっかん|たい|ゆうこう|ちりょうほう"},{"japanese":"血液は主に骨の中にある骨髄で作られる。","romaji":"Ketsueki wa omo ni hone no naka ni aru kotsuzui de tsukurareru.","german":"Blut wird hauptsächlich im Knochenmark im Inneren der Knochen gebildet.","furigana":"けつえき|おも|ほね|なか|こつずい|つく"},{"japanese":"裏切られた恨みは骨髄に徹している。","romaji":"Uragirareta urami wa kotsuzui ni tesshite iru.","german":"Der Groll über den Verrat sitzt mir bis ins Mark.","furigana":"うらぎ|うら|こつずい|てっ"}],"notes":"骨髄 ist das Knochenmark, in dem das Blut gebildet wird; geläufig sind 骨髄移植 („Knochenmarktransplantation“), 骨髄バンク und 骨髄検査. Übertragen bedeutet es „das tiefste Innere“, etwa in der literarischen Wendung 恨み骨髄に徹する („Groll bis ins Mark“). Ähnlich bildhaft ist das deutsche „bis ins Mark“.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"幹細胞研究は再生医療の発展に大きく貢献している。","romaji":"Kan saibō kenkyū wa saisei iryō no hatten ni ōkiku kōken shite iru.","german":"Stem cell research is contributing greatly to the development of regenerative medicine.","furigana":"かんさいぼうけんきゅう|さいせいいりょう|はってん|おお|こうけん"},{"japanese":"iPS細胞は、さまざまな細胞になれる幹細胞の一種だ。","romaji":"iPS saibō wa, samazama na saibō ni nareru kansaibō no isshu da.","german":"iPS cells are a kind of stem cell that can become various kinds of cells.","furigana":"さいぼう|さいぼう|かんさいぼう|いっしゅ"},{"japanese":"造血幹細胞は骨髄の中で血液の細胞を作り出している。","romaji":"Zōketsu kansaibō wa kotsuzui no naka de ketsueki no saibō o tsukuridashite iru.","german":"Hematopoietic stem cells produce blood cells inside the bone marrow.","furigana":"ぞうけつかんさいぼう|こつずい|なか|けつえき|さいぼう|つく|だ"}],"notes":"幹細胞 is the stem cell; 幹 means \"trunk (of a tree)\", i.e. the cell from which other cells \"branch off\". Well-known types are iPS細胞, ES細胞 and 造血幹細胞 (\"blood-forming stem cells\"). Typical frames: 幹細胞研究, 幹細胞を培養する／移植する. Technical language, but frequent in science news.","levelNote":"Compound: 幹 (N1) + 細胞 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"再生医療は損傷した組織や臓器を修復する革新的な分野である。","romaji":"Saisei iryō wa sonshō shita soshiki ya zōki o shūfuku suru kakushinteki na bun'ya de aru.","german":"Regenerative medicine is an innovative field that repairs damaged tissues and organs.","furigana":"さいせいいりょう|そんしょう|そしき|ぞうき|しゅうふく|かくしんてき|ぶんや"},{"japanese":"再生医療が実用化されれば、臓器移植を待つ人が減るかもしれない。","romaji":"Saisei iryō ga jitsuyōka sarereba, zōki ishoku o matsu hito ga heru kamoshirenai.","german":"If regenerative medicine becomes practical, fewer people may have to wait for organ transplants.","furigana":"さいせいいりょう|じつようか|ぞうきいしょく|ま|ひと|へ"},{"japanese":"この病院では、iPS細胞を使った再生医療の臨床研究が進められている。","romaji":"Kono byōin de wa, iPS saibō o tsukatta saisei iryō no rinshō kenkyū ga susumerarete iru.","german":"At this hospital, clinical research on regenerative medicine using iPS cells is being carried out.","furigana":"びょういん|さいぼう|つか|さいせいいりょう|りんしょうけんきゅう|すす"}],"notes":"再生医療 is regenerative medicine, which restores damaged tissue or organs using cells. 再生 means \"restoration, regeneration\" (also \"playback\" for videos), 医療 \"medical care\". In Japan the word is closely tied to iPS cell research; typical are 再生医療の実用化 and 再生医療の研究.","levelNote":"Compound: 再生 (N1) + 医療 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"遺伝子治療は遺伝性疾患の根本的な治療を目指す。","romaji":"Idenshi chiryō wa idensei shikkan no konponteki na chiryō o mezasu.","german":"Gene therapy aims at a fundamental cure of hereditary diseases.","furigana":"いでんしちりょう|いでんせいしっかん|こんぽんてき|ちりょう|めざ"},{"japanese":"遺伝子治療を受けたことで、少年は自分の足で歩けるようになった。","romaji":"Idenshi chiryō o uketa koto de, shōnen wa jibun no ashi de arukeru yō ni natta.","german":"Thanks to gene therapy, the boy became able to walk on his own legs.","furigana":"いでんしちりょう|う|しょうねん|じぶん|あし|ある"},{"japanese":"遺伝子治療には、倫理的な問題を指摘する声もある。","romaji":"Idenshi chiryō ni wa, rinriteki na mondai o shiteki suru koe mo aru.","german":"Some voices point out ethical problems with gene therapy.","furigana":"いでんしちりょう|りんりてき|もんだい|してき|こえ"}],"notes":"遺伝子治療 is gene therapy, i.e. treatment by introducing or altering genes (遺伝子). Typical are 遺伝子治療を受ける, 遺伝子治療薬 and 遺伝子治療の臨床試験. Do not confuse it with 遺伝性 (\"hereditary\"), which describes the disease. In debates, ethical questions are often pointed out.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"内科は薬物療法を中心とした診療科である。","romaji":"Naika wa yakubutsu ryōhō o chūshin to shita shinryōka de aru.","german":"Internal medicine is a medical specialty centered on drug therapy.","furigana":"ないか|やくぶつりょうほう|ちゅうしん|しんりょうか"},{"japanese":"熱と咳が続くので、近所の内科を受診した。","romaji":"Netsu to seki ga tsuzuku node, kinjo no naika o jushin shita.","german":"Since my fever and cough continued, I went to a nearby internal medicine clinic.","furigana":"ねつ|せき|つづ|きんじょ|ないか|じゅしん"},{"japanese":"内科と外科のどちらに行けばいいか、受付で聞いてみた。","romaji":"Naika to geka no dochira ni ikeba ii ka, uketsuke de kiite mita.","german":"I asked at the reception whether I should go to internal medicine or surgery.","furigana":"ないか|げか|い|うけつけ|き"}],"notes":"内科 is internal medicine, or the internal medicine department or practice. In Japan, with a cold, fever or stomach problems one typically goes to a 内科, rather than to a \"family doctor\"; one says 内科を受診する or 内科にかかる. The counterpart is 外科; the doctor is called 内科医.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"外科的介入が必要な場合は迅速な判断が求められる。","romaji":"Gekateki kainyū ga hitsuyō na baai wa jinsoku na handan ga motomerareru.","german":"When a surgical intervention is necessary, a quick decision is required.","furigana":"げかてきかいにゅう|ひつよう|ばあい|じんそく|はんだん|もと"},{"japanese":"兄は大学病院で外科医として働いている。","romaji":"Ani wa daigaku byōin de gekai to shite hataraite iru.","german":"My older brother works as a surgeon at a university hospital.","furigana":"あに|だいがくびょういん|げかい|はたら"},{"japanese":"指を深く切ってしまったので、外科で縫ってもらった。","romaji":"Yubi o fukaku kitte shimatta node, geka de nutte moratta.","german":"I had cut my finger deeply, so I had it stitched up in surgery.","furigana":"ゆび|ふか|き|げか|ぬ"}],"notes":"外科 is surgery as a field and as a department; one goes there with injuries or when an operation is needed. Derivatives: 外科医 (\"surgeon\"), 外科手術, 外科的 (\"surgical\"). Note the reading: げか, not がいか. The counterpart is 内科; specialties are, for example, 整形外科 or 脳外科.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"整形外科では骨折や関節疾患を専門に治療する。","romaji":"Seikei geka de wa kossetsu ya kansetsu shikkan o senmon ni chiryō suru.","german":"Orthopedics specializes in treating fractures and joint diseases.","furigana":"せいけいげか|こっせつ|かんせつしっかん|せんもん|ちりょう"},{"japanese":"転んで手首を痛めたので、整形外科でレントゲンを撮ってもらった。","romaji":"Koronde tekubi o itameta node, seikei geka de rentogen o totte moratta.","german":"I hurt my wrist in a fall, so I had an X-ray taken at the orthopedic clinic.","furigana":"ころ|てくび|いた|せいけいげか|と"},{"japanese":"腰痛がひどくて、週に一度整形外科に通っている。","romaji":"Yōtsū ga hidokute, shū ni ichido seikei geka ni kayotte iru.","german":"My lower back pain is severe, so I go to the orthopedic clinic once a week.","furigana":"ようつう|しゅう|いちどせいけいげか|かよ"}],"notes":"整形外科 is orthopedics: bone fractures, joints, back and knee pain. A trap for learners: cosmetic surgery is called 美容整形, and the short 整形 alone colloquially usually means cosmetic surgery; plastic and reconstructive surgery is 形成外科. Typical: 整形外科に通う, 整形外科でレントゲンを撮る.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"緑内障は眼科で定期的に検査すべき疾患である。","romaji":"Ryokunaishō wa ganka de teikiteki ni kensa subeki shikkan de aru.","german":"Glaucoma is a disease that should be checked regularly at an ophthalmologist.","furigana":"りょくないしょう|がんか|ていきてき|けんさ|しっかん"},{"japanese":"目がかゆくて充血しているので、眼科に行った。","romaji":"Me ga kayukute jūketsu shite iru node, ganka ni itta.","german":"My eyes were itchy and bloodshot, so I went to the eye doctor.","furigana":"め|じゅうけつ|がんか|い"},{"japanese":"コンタクトレンズを作る前に、眼科で検査を受ける必要がある。","romaji":"Kontakuto renzu o tsukuru mae ni, ganka de kensa o ukeru hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"Before getting contact lenses, you need to have an examination at an eye clinic.","furigana":"つく|まえ|がんか|けんさ|う|ひつよう"}],"notes":"眼科 is ophthalmology or the eye doctor's practice; the doctor is called 眼科医, colloquially also 目医者. People say 眼科に行く, 眼科で診てもらう or 眼科を受診する. 眼 is read がん here, as in 眼球 or 近眼.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"産科医は出産時の母体と胎児の安全を守る。","romaji":"Sankai wa shussan ji no botai to taiji no anzen o mamoru.","german":"Obstetricians protect the safety of mother and fetus during childbirth.","furigana":"さんかい|しゅっさんじ|ぼたい|たいじ|あんぜん|まも"},{"japanese":"地方では産科医が不足していて、出産できる病院が減っている。","romaji":"Chihō de wa sankai ga fusoku shite ite, shussan dekiru byōin ga hette iru.","german":"In rural areas there is a shortage of obstetricians, and the number of hospitals where one can give birth is decreasing.","furigana":"ちほう|さんかい|ふそく|しゅっさん|びょういん|へ"},{"japanese":"妻の出産予定日が近いので、産科の病棟を下見しておいた。","romaji":"Tsuma no shussan yoteibi ga chikai node, sanka no byōtō o shitami shite oita.","german":"My wife's due date is near, so I checked out the obstetrics ward beforehand.","furigana":"つま|しゅっさんよていび|ちか|さんか|びょうとう|したみ"}],"notes":"産科 is obstetrics or the maternity department; it is mostly combined with gynecology as 産婦人科, and this name is more common in everyday speech. The doctor is called 産科医; the shortage of 産科医 in rural areas is a well-known social issue. Typical: 産科の病棟, 産科を受診する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"小児科は子どもの発育と疾患を専門とする診療科である。","romaji":"Shōnika wa kodomo no hatsuiku to shikkan o senmon to suru shinryōka de aru.","german":"Pediatrics is the medical department that specializes in children's development and diseases.","furigana":"しょうにか|こ|はついく|しっかん|せんもん|しんりょうか"},{"japanese":"子どもが夜中に高い熱を出したので、朝一番で小児科に連れて行った。","romaji":"Kodomo ga yonaka ni takai netsu o dashita node, asa ichiban de shōnika ni tsurete itta.","german":"My child ran a high fever in the middle of the night, so I took him to the pediatrician first thing in the morning.","furigana":"こ|よなか|たか|ねつ|だ|あさいちばん|しょうにか|つ|い"},{"japanese":"小児科の待合室には、絵本やおもちゃが置いてある。","romaji":"Shōnika no machiaishitsu ni wa, ehon ya omocha ga oite aru.","german":"The waiting room of the pediatrics clinic has picture books and toys.","furigana":"しょうにか|まちあいしつ|えほん|お"}],"notes":"小児科 is pediatrics or the pediatrician's practice; 小児 (\"small child, child\") is a formal word for 子ども. People say 小児科に連れて行く, 小児科にかかる; the doctor is called 小児科医. In Japan it mostly treats children up to about the end of junior high school.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"皮膚科ではアトピー性皮膚炎やニキビの治療を行う。","romaji":"Hifuka de wa atopīsei hifuen ya nikibi no chiryō o okonau.","german":"Dermatology treats atopic dermatitis and acne.","furigana":"ひふか|せいひふえん|ちりょう|おこな"},{"japanese":"虫に刺されたところが腫れてきたので、皮膚科で薬をもらった。","romaji":"Mushi ni sasareta tokoro ga harete kita node, hifuka de kusuri o moratta.","german":"The spot where an insect bit me started to swell, so I got medicine at the dermatologist.","furigana":"むし|さ|は|ひふか|くすり"},{"japanese":"皮膚科の先生に、日焼け止めを毎日塗るよう勧められた。","romaji":"Hifuka no sensei ni, hiyakedome o mainichi nuru yō susumerareta.","german":"The dermatologist recommended that I apply sunscreen every day.","furigana":"ひふか|せんせい|ひや|ど|まいにちぬ|すす"}],"notes":"皮膚科 is dermatology or the skin doctor's practice; you go there with rash, acne, itching or insect bites. Typical: 皮膚科に行く, 皮膚科で薬をもらう, 皮膚科の先生. For purely cosmetic treatments there is a separate 美容皮膚科.","levelNote":"Compound: 皮膚 (N3) + 科 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"泌尿器科は腎臓や膀胱などの疾患を専門に扱う。","romaji":"Hinyōkika wa jinzō ya bōkō nado no shikkan o senmon ni atsukau.","german":"Urology specializes in diseases of the kidneys, bladder and so on.","furigana":"ひにょうきか|じんぞう|ぼうこう|しっかん|せんもん|あつか"},{"japanese":"尿に血が混じっていたので、泌尿器科を受診した。","romaji":"Nyō ni chi ga majitte ita node, hinyōkika o jushin shita.","german":"There was blood in my urine, so I went to see a urologist.","furigana":"にょう|ち|ま|ひにょうきか|じゅしん"},{"japanese":"健康診断で前立腺の数値が高く、泌尿器科での再検査を勧められた。","romaji":"Kenkō shindan de zenritsusen no sūchi ga takaku, hinyōkika de no saikensa o susumerareta.","german":"My prostate value was high at the health checkup, and I was advised to be retested at urology.","furigana":"けんこうしんだん|ぜんりつせん|すうち|たか|ひにょうきか|さいけんさ|すす"}],"notes":"泌尿器科 is urology: kidneys, urinary tract, bladder and prostate. 泌尿器 means \"urinary organs\"; 泌 is read ひ here, whereas in 分泌 (ぶんぴつ) it is read ぴつ. Typical: 泌尿器科を受診する, 泌尿器科で検査を受ける. The subject is regarded as somewhat embarrassing, so the visit is often put off for a long time.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"耳鼻咽喉科では難聴や副鼻腔炎の治療を行う。","romaji":"Jibiinkōka de wa nanchō ya fuku bikūen no chiryō o okonau.","german":"Otolaryngology treats hearing loss and sinusitis.","furigana":"じびいんこうか|なんちょう||ちりょう|おこな"},{"japanese":"子どもが耳を痛がるので、耳鼻咽喉科に連れて行った。","romaji":"Kodomo ga mimi o itagaru node, jibiinkōka ni tsurete itta.","german":"My child complained of ear pain, so I took him to the ENT clinic.","furigana":"こ|みみ|いた|じびいんこうか|つ|い"},{"japanese":"花粉症の季節になると、耳鼻咽喉科はいつも混んでいる。","romaji":"Kafunshō no kisetsu ni naru to, jibiinkōka wa itsumo konde iru.","german":"During hay fever season, the ENT clinic is always crowded.","furigana":"かふんしょう|きせつ|じびいんこうか|こ"}],"notes":"耳鼻咽喉科 is otolaryngology (ear, nose and throat medicine); 耳 \"ear\", 鼻 \"nose\", 咽喉 \"pharynx and throat\". In everyday speech people almost always say the short 耳鼻科; the full form appears on signs and in official texts. Typical: 耳鼻咽喉科を受診する, 耳鼻咽喉科に連れて行く. It is especially crowded there during the hay fever season.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"脳卒中後のリハビリテーションは機能回復に不可欠である。","romaji":"Nōsotchū go no rihabiritēshon wa kinō kaifuku ni fukaketsu de aru.","german":"Rehabilitation after a stroke is essential for functional recovery.","furigana":"のうそっちゅうご|きのうかいふく|ふかけつ"},{"japanese":"膝の手術の後、三か月間リハビリテーションを続けた。","romaji":"Hiza no shujutsu no ato, sankagetsukan rihabiritēshon o tsuzuketa.","german":"After my knee surgery, I continued rehabilitation for three months.","furigana":"ひざ|しゅじゅつ|あと|さん|げつかん|つづ"},{"japanese":"退院後も、週二回リハビリテーションに通っている。","romaji":"Taiingo mo, shū nikai rihabiritēshon ni kayotte iru.","german":"Even after leaving the hospital, I go to rehabilitation twice a week.","furigana":"たいいんご|しゅうにかい|かよ"}],"notes":"リハビリテーション is rehabilitation after illness or injury; in everyday speech people almost always say the short リハビリ (リハビリをする／受ける, リハビリに通う). The long form appears in technical texts and in names of clinics and departments. Figuratively, リハビリ is also used for athletes after an injury or a long break.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"理学療法は運動機能の回復を促進する治療法である。","romaji":"Rigaku ryōhō wa undō kinō no kaifuku o sokushin suru chiryōhō de aru.","german":"Physical therapy is a treatment that promotes recovery of motor function.","furigana":"りがくりょうほう|うんどうきのう|かいふく|そくしん|ちりょうほう"},{"japanese":"理学療法士の指導のもとで、歩く訓練を毎日行っている。","romaji":"Rigaku ryōhōshi no shidō no moto de, aruku kunren o mainichi okonatte iru.","german":"Under the guidance of a physical therapist, I do walking training every day.","furigana":"りがくりょうほうし|しどう|ある|くんれん|まいにちおこな"},{"japanese":"肩の痛みが続くので、理学療法を受けることになった。","romaji":"Kata no itami ga tsuzuku node, rigaku ryōhō o ukeru koto ni natta.","german":"My shoulder pain continues, so I am going to receive physical therapy.","furigana":"かた|いた|つづ|りがくりょうほう|う"}],"notes":"理学療法 is physiotherapy: exercises, massage, heat or electrical treatment to restore mobility. The specialist is called 理学療法士 (PT). Distinguish it from 作業療法 (\"occupational therapy\"), which trains everyday activities. In everyday speech people usually just say リハビリ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"作業療法は日常生活動作の自立を支援する。","romaji":"Sagyō ryōhō wa nichijō seikatsu dōsa no jiritsu o shien suru.","german":"Occupational therapy supports independence in activities of daily living.","furigana":"さぎょうりょうほう|にちじょうせいかつどうさ|じりつ|しえん"},{"japanese":"作業療法では、料理や着替えなど日常の動作を練習する。","romaji":"Sagyō ryōhō de wa, ryōri ya kigae nado nichijō no dōsa o renshū suru.","german":"In occupational therapy, you practice everyday movements such as cooking and changing clothes.","furigana":"さぎょうりょうほう|りょうり|きが|にちじょう|どうさ|れんしゅう"},{"japanese":"母は作業療法士として、精神科の病院で働いている。","romaji":"Haha wa sagyō ryōhōshi to shite, seishinka no byōin de hataraite iru.","german":"My mother works at a psychiatric hospital as an occupational therapist.","furigana":"はは|さぎょうりょうほうし|せいしんか|びょういん|はたら"}],"notes":"作業療法 is occupational therapy; 作業 means \"activity\" here, that is, cooking, dressing, handicrafts or writing as practice for everyday life. The specialist is called 作業療法士 (OT). Unlike 理学療法, which trains basic movements such as walking, 作業療法 aims at independence in daily life and is also used in psychiatry.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"血漿中にはさまざまな蛋白質や電解質が含まれている。","romaji":"Kesshōchū ni wa samazama na tanpakushitsu ya denkaishitsu ga fukumarete iru.","german":"Plasma contains various proteins and electrolytes.","furigana":"けっしょうちゅう|たんぱくしつ|でんかいしつ|ふく"},{"japanese":"血液の約55%は血漿でできている。","romaji":"Ketsueki no yaku gojūgo pāsento wa kesshō de dekite iru.","german":"About 55% of blood is made up of plasma.","furigana":"けつえき|やく|けっしょう"},{"japanese":"今日は献血ルームで血漿成分献血をした。","romaji":"Kyō wa kenketsu rūmu de kesshō seibun kenketsu o shita.","german":"Today I gave a plasma donation at the blood donation room.","furigana":"きょう|けんけつ|けっしょうせいぶんけんけつ"}],"notes":"血漿 is \"blood plasma\", the liquid part of the blood without the blood cells (血球). Do not confuse it with 血清 (\"serum\"), which is plasma without clotting factors. Typical combinations: 血漿成分献血 (\"plasma donation\"), 血漿製剤 (\"plasma preparation\"), 血漿タンパク. The character 漿 is rare; texts sometimes write 血しょう. Technical word of medicine and biology.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"血小板は出血時に血液凝固を促進する重要な役割を持つ。","romaji":"Kesshōban wa shukketsu ji ni ketsueki gyōko o sokushin suru jūyō na yakuwari o motsu.","german":"Platelets play an important role in promoting blood clotting when bleeding occurs.","furigana":"けっしょうばん|しゅっけつじ|けつえきぎょうこ|そくしん|じゅうよう|やくわり|も"},{"japanese":"抗がん剤の影響で血小板が減り、あざができやすくなった。","romaji":"Kōganzai no eikyō de kesshōban ga heri, aza ga dekiyasuku natta.","german":"Because of the effects of anticancer drugs, my platelets decreased and I started bruising easily.","furigana":"こう|ざい|えいきょう|けっしょうばん|へ"},{"japanese":"今日は献血ルームで血小板の成分献血をした。","romaji":"Kyō wa kenketsu rūmu de kesshōban no seibun kenketsu o shita.","german":"Today I donated platelets at the blood donation room.","furigana":"きょう|けんけつ|けっしょうばん|せいぶんけんけつ"}],"notes":"血小板 are the platelets (thrombocytes), which initiate blood clotting after injuries. Typical are 血小板が減る／少ない, 血小板の数 and 血小板減少症. When donating blood, one can specifically donate platelets (血小板の成分献血). Literally: \"small plates in the blood\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"赤血球は酸素を全身の組織に運搬する。","romaji":"Sekkekkyū wa sanso o zenshin no soshiki ni unpan suru.","german":"Red blood cells carry oxygen to the tissues throughout the body.","furigana":"せっけっきゅう|さんそ|ぜんしん|そしき|うんぱん"},{"japanese":"血液検査で赤血球の数が少ないと言われました。","romaji":"Ketsueki kensa de sekkekkyū no kazu ga sukunai to iwaremashita.","german":"In my blood test I was told that my red blood cell count is low.","furigana":"けつえきけんさ|せっけっきゅう|かず|すく|い"},{"japanese":"顕微鏡で見ると、赤血球は真ん中がへこんだ円盤の形をしている。","romaji":"Kenbikyō de miru to, sekkekkyū wa mannaka ga hekonda enban no katachi o shite iru.","german":"Under a microscope, red blood cells have the shape of a disc that is dented in the middle.","furigana":"けんびきょう|み|せっけっきゅう|ま|なか|えんばん|かたち"}],"notes":"赤血球 is the medical term for \"red blood cells\"; the building blocks are 赤 \"red\", 血 \"blood\" and 球 \"sphere\". Typical combinations are 赤血球数 \"red blood cell count\", 赤血球が減る/少ない (e.g. in anemia, 貧血) and 赤血球が酸素を運ぶ. Its counterparts are 白血球 \"white blood cells\" and 血小板 \"platelets\". The word is neutral and appears equally in biology class, in doctor's letters and in health articles.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"白血球は体内に侵入した病原体を攻撃する。","romaji":"Hakkekkyū wa tainai ni shinnyū shita byōgentai o kōgeki suru.","german":"White blood cells attack pathogens that have invaded the body.","furigana":"はっけっきゅう|たいない|しんにゅう|びょうげんたい|こうげき"},{"japanese":"白血球の数値が高いので、どこかに炎症があるかもしれません。","romaji":"Hakkekkyū no sūchi ga takai node, dokoka ni enshō ga aru kamo shiremasen.","german":"Your white blood cell count is high, so there may be inflammation somewhere.","furigana":"はっけっきゅう|すうち|たか|えんしょう"},{"japanese":"抗がん剤の副作用で白血球が減ってしまった。","romaji":"Kōganzai no fukusayō de hakkekkyū ga hette shimatta.","german":"My white blood cells decreased due to the side effects of anticancer drugs.","furigana":"こう|ざい|ふくさよう|はっけっきゅう|へ"}],"notes":"白血球 are the \"white blood cells\" (白 \"white\", 血 \"blood\", 球 \"sphere\"), the body's defense cells. In everyday speech you hear the word mainly with blood values: 白血球が多い/増えている points to inflammation or infection, 白血球が少ない/減少する occurs for example after chemotherapy. Do not confuse it with 白血病 \"leukemia\", which differs by only one character. The counterpart is 赤血球.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"リンパ節は免疫系の重要な構成要素である。","romaji":"Rinpa setsu wa men'eki kei no jūyō na kōsei yōso de aru.","german":"Lymph nodes are an important component of the immune system.","furigana":"せつ|めんえきけい|じゅうよう|こうせいようそ"},{"japanese":"風邪をひいて、首のリンパが腫れている。","romaji":"Kaze o hiite, kubi no rinpa ga harete iru.","german":"I caught a cold and the lymph nodes in my neck are swollen.","furigana":"かぜ|くび|は"},{"japanese":"足のむくみには、リンパの流れをよくするマッサージが効果的だ。","romaji":"Ashi no mukumi ni wa, rinpa no nagare o yoku suru massāji ga kōkateki da.","german":"For swollen legs, massage that improves the flow of lymph is effective.","furigana":"あし|なが|こうかてき"}],"notes":"リンパ is \"lymph\" or the lymphatic system; the word comes from Dutch/Latin and is today written almost only in katakana (the old kanji spelling 淋巴 is rare). It mostly appears in compounds: リンパ節 \"lymph node\", リンパ液 \"lymph fluid\", リンパ球 \"lymphocyte\", リンパ腫 \"lymphoma\". Colloquially people say リンパが腫れる and mean swollen lymph nodes; in cosmetics and wellness, リンパマッサージ \"lymphatic drainage\" is a common word.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"血漿の膠質浸透圧は組織間の水分移動を調節する。","romaji":"Kesshō no kōshitsu shintō atsu wa soshiki kan no suibun idō o chōsetsu suru.","german":"The colloid osmotic pressure of plasma regulates the movement of water between tissues.","furigana":"けっしょう|こうしつしんとうあつ|そしきかん|すいぶんいどう|ちょうせつ"},{"japanese":"アルブミンが減ると膠質浸透圧が下がり、むくみが生じやすくなる。","romaji":"Arubumin ga heru to kōshitsu shintō atsu ga sagari, mukumi ga shōjiyasuku naru.","german":"When albumin decreases, the colloid osmotic pressure drops and swelling occurs more easily.","furigana":"へ|こうしつしんとうあつ|さ|しょう"},{"japanese":"看護学校の試験で膠質浸透圧についての問題が出た。","romaji":"Kango gakkō no shiken de kōshitsu shintō atsu ni tsuite no mondai ga deta.","german":"A question about colloid osmotic pressure came up in the nursing school exam.","furigana":"かんごがっこう|しけん|こうしつしんとうあつ|もんだい|で"}],"notes":"膠質浸透圧 is a physiological technical term: 膠質 \"colloid\" (literally \"glue-like substance\") + 浸透圧 \"osmotic pressure\". It refers to the pressure generated mainly by plasma proteins such as albumin (アルブミン) in the blood vessels, which keeps water inside the vessel. Synonymous but somewhat more modern is コロイド浸透圧. Typical: 膠質浸透圧が低下する → 浮腫 \"edema\" develops. The word occurs practically only in textbooks and clinical texts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"凝固因子の欠乏は血友病の原因となる。","romaji":"Gyōko inshi no ketsubō wa ketsuyūbyō no gen'in to naru.","german":"A deficiency of clotting factors causes hemophilia.","furigana":"ぎょうこいんし|けつぼう|けつゆうびょう|げんいん"},{"japanese":"肝臓の機能が落ちると、凝固因子が十分に作られなくなる。","romaji":"Kanzō no kinō ga ochiru to, gyōko inshi ga jūbun ni tsukurarenaku naru.","german":"When liver function declines, clotting factors are no longer produced in sufficient amounts.","furigana":"かんぞう|きのう|お|ぎょうこいんし|じゅうぶん|つく"},{"japanese":"手術の前に凝固因子製剤を投与した。","romaji":"Shujutsu no mae ni gyōko inshi seizai o tōyo shita.","german":"A clotting factor preparation was administered before the surgery.","furigana":"しゅじゅつ|まえ|ぎょうこいんしせいざい|とうよ"}],"notes":"凝固因子 are the \"clotting factors\" in the blood: 凝固 \"coagulation, solidification\" + 因子 \"factor\". In medicine they are usually named with Roman numerals (第VIII因子 \"factor VIII\"). Typical combinations: 凝固因子が欠乏する/不足する, 凝固因子製剤 \"clotting factor preparation\". The related verb is 血液が凝固する \"the blood clots\"; colloquially people rather say 血が固まる. Purely technical vocabulary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"腹膜播種はがんが腹腔内に広がった状態を指す。","romaji":"Fukumaku hashu wa gan ga fukukūnai ni hirogatta jōtai o sasu.","german":"Peritoneal dissemination refers to a state in which cancer has spread within the abdominal cavity.","furigana":"ふくまくはしゅ||ひろ|じょうたい|さ"},{"japanese":"春になると、畑で大豆の播種が始まる。","romaji":"Haru ni naru to, hatake de daizu no hashu ga hajimaru.","german":"When spring comes, the sowing of soybeans begins in the fields.","furigana":"はる|はたけ|だいず|はしゅ|はじ"},{"japanese":"検査の結果、がんが胸膜に播種していることがわかった。","romaji":"Kensa no kekka, gan ga kyōmaku ni hashu shite iru koto ga wakatta.","german":"The test results showed that the cancer had disseminated to the pleura.","furigana":"けんさ|けっか|きょうまく|はしゅ"}],"notes":"播種 literally means \"to sow seeds\" (播 = sow, 種 = seed) and is used technically in agriculture and horticulture: 播種機 (\"seeder\"), 播種期; in everyday speech people say 種まき. In medicine it denotes the \"spreading\" of cancer cells into a body cavity, for example 腹膜播種 or がんが胸膜に播種する; also 播種性 (\"disseminated\"). As a する-verb it is transitive (種を播種する) or, in medicine, intransitive.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"腹腔鏡手術は従来の開腹手術に比べて回復が早い。","romaji":"Fukukūkyō shujutsu wa jūrai no kaifuku shujutsu ni kurabete kaifuku ga hayai.","german":"Laparoscopic surgery allows faster recovery than conventional open abdominal surgery.","furigana":"ふくくうきょうしゅじゅつ|じゅうらい|かいふくしゅじゅつ|くら|かいふく|はや"},{"japanese":"胆嚢の手術は、腹腔鏡を使って行われることが多い。","romaji":"Tannō no shujutsu wa, fukukūkyō o tsukatte okonawareru koto ga ōi.","german":"Gallbladder surgery is often performed using a laparoscope.","furigana":"たんのう|しゅじゅつ|ふくくうきょう|つか|おこな|おお"},{"japanese":"腹腔鏡手術なので、傷は小さくて済みます。","romaji":"Fukukūkyō shujutsu na node, kizu wa chiisakute sumimasu.","german":"Since it is laparoscopic surgery, the wounds will be small.","furigana":"ふくくうきょうしゅじゅつ|きず|ちい|す"}],"notes":"腹腔鏡 is the \"laparoscope\", an endoscope for the abdominal cavity. In medicine 腹腔 is mostly read ふくくう (otherwise also ふっこう). Very common is 腹腔鏡手術 or 腹腔鏡下手術 (\"laparoscopic surgery, keyhole surgery\"), as opposed to 開腹手術 (\"open abdominal surgery\"). The umbrella term is 内視鏡 (\"endoscope\"). Typical: 腹腔鏡で手術する, 腹腔鏡を挿入する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"心臓カテーテル検査で冠動脈の狭窄を確認する。","romaji":"Shinzō katēteru kensa de kan dōmyaku no kyōsaku o kakunin suru.","german":"A cardiac catheterization confirms the narrowing of the coronary arteries.","furigana":"しんぞう|けんさ|かんどうみゃく||かくにん"},{"japanese":"手術の後、しばらく尿道にカテーテルが入っていた。","romaji":"Shujutsu no ato, shibaraku nyōdō ni katēteru ga haitte ita.","german":"After the surgery, a catheter remained in my urethra for a while.","furigana":"しゅじゅつ|あと|にょうどう|はい"},{"japanese":"看護師がカテーテルをゆっくり抜いてくれた。","romaji":"Kangoshi ga katēteru o yukkuri nuite kureta.","german":"The nurse slowly removed the catheter for me.","furigana":"かんごし|ぬ"}],"notes":"カテーテル is the \"catheter\", a thin tube that is inserted into vessels, urinary tract and so on. People say カテーテルを入れる/挿入する and カテーテルを抜く. Common compounds: 心臓カテーテル検査 \"cardiac catheterization\" (in hospital jargon shortened to カテ), 尿道カテーテル \"urinary catheter\", カテーテル治療. The word is completely commonplace in the hospital; patients use it too.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"冠動脈にステントを留置して血流を回復させる。","romaji":"Kan dōmyaku ni sutento o ryūchi shite ketsuryū o kaifuku saseru.","german":"A stent is placed in the coronary artery to restore blood flow.","furigana":"かんどうみゃく|りゅうち|けつりゅう|かいふく"},{"japanese":"父は心筋梗塞で倒れ、ステントを二本入れた。","romaji":"Chichi wa shinkin kōsoku de taore, sutento o nihon ireta.","german":"My father collapsed from a heart attack and had two stents put in.","furigana":"ちち|しんきんこうそく|たお|にほんい"},{"japanese":"ステント治療なら胸を開かずに済むそうだ。","romaji":"Sutento chiryō nara mune o hirakazu ni sumu sō da.","german":"I hear that with stent treatment you don't need to have your chest opened.","furigana":"ちりょう|むね|ひら|す"}],"notes":"ステント is a \"stent\", a small mesh tube that keeps narrowed vessels or ducts open. The typical technical verb is ステントを留置する \"to place a stent (and leave it there)\"; in conversation people simply say ステントを入れる. Common in ステント治療 and 冠動脈ステント. Mostly in connection with cardiac catheter procedures (カテーテル治療) for angina pectoris or heart attack.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"重症患者に人工呼吸器を装着して呼吸を補助する。","romaji":"Jūshō kanja ni jinkō kokyūki o sōchaku shite kokyū o hojo suru.","german":"A ventilator is attached to severely ill patients to assist their breathing.","furigana":"じゅうしょうかんじゃ|じんこうこきゅうき|そうちゃく|こきゅう|ほじょ"},{"japanese":"祖父は一週間、人工呼吸器につながれていた。","romaji":"Sofu wa isshūkan, jinkō kokyūki ni tsunagarete ita.","german":"My grandfather was connected to a ventilator for a week.","furigana":"そふ|いっしゅうかん|じんこうこきゅうき"},{"japanese":"感染症の流行で、病院の人工呼吸器が足りなくなった。","romaji":"Kansenshō no ryūkō de, byōin no jinkō kokyūki ga tarinaku natta.","german":"Because of the epidemic of an infectious disease, the hospital ran out of ventilators.","furigana":"かんせんしょう|りゅうこう|びょういん|じんこうこきゅうき|た"}],"notes":"人工呼吸器 is the \"ventilator\": 人工呼吸 \"artificial respiration\" + 器 \"device\". People say 人工呼吸器を装着する/つける and 人工呼吸器を外す; 人工呼吸器につながれる means \"to be hooked up to a ventilator\". Do not confuse it with 人工呼吸 alone, which also means mouth-to-mouth resuscitation in first aid. In hospital routine the loanword ベンチレーター is also heard.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"心室細動には直ちに除細動を行う必要がある。","romaji":"Shinshitsu saidō ni wa tadachi ni josaidō o okonau hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"Defibrillation must be performed immediately for ventricular fibrillation.","furigana":"しんしつさいどう|ただ|じょさいどう|おこな|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"救急隊が到着する前に、駅員がAEDで除細動をかけた。","romaji":"Kyūkyūtai ga tōchaku suru mae ni, ekiin ga ēīdī de josaidō o kaketa.","german":"Before the ambulance crew arrived, a station attendant applied defibrillation with an AED.","furigana":"きゅうきゅうたい|とうちゃく|まえ|えきいん|じょさいどう"},{"japanese":"救命講習では、除細動器の使い方も練習した。","romaji":"Kyūmei kōshū de wa, josaidōki no tsukaikata mo renshū shita.","german":"In the life-saving course, we also practiced how to use a defibrillator.","furigana":"きゅうめいこうしゅう|じょさいどうき|つか|かた|れんしゅう"}],"notes":"除細動 is \"defibrillation\": literally the removal (除) of fibrillation (細動, ventricular or atrial fibrillation). People say 除細動を行う/かける. The device is called 除細動器; the publicly installed lay defibrillator is the AED (自動体外式除細動器), which in everyday speech is almost always just called \"AED\". The word itself is technical language from emergency medicine and first-aid courses.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"心肺蘇生法は救急医療の基本的な手技である。","romaji":"Shinpai soseihō wa kyūkyū iryō no kihonteki na shugi de aru.","german":"Cardiopulmonary resuscitation is a basic skill in emergency medicine.","furigana":"しんぱいそせいほう|きゅうきゅういりょう|きほんてき|しゅぎ"},{"japanese":"心臓が止まっていた男性は、救急隊の処置で奇跡的に蘇生した。","romaji":"Shinzō ga tomatte ita dansei wa, kyūkyūtai no shochi de kisekiteki ni sosei shita.","german":"The man whose heart had stopped was miraculously revived by the treatment of the ambulance crew.","furigana":"しんぞう|と|だんせい|きゅうきゅうたい|しょち|きせきてき|そせい"},{"japanese":"新しい市長のもとで、さびれた商店街が蘇生しつつある。","romaji":"Atarashii shichō no moto de, sabireta shōtengai ga sosei shitsutsu aru.","german":"Under the new mayor, the run-down shopping street is being revived.","furigana":"あたら|しちょう|しょうてんがい|そせい"}],"notes":"蘇生 means \"resuscitation\" and can be used as an intransitive する-verb: 患者が蘇生した \"the patient was resuscitated / came round\". Common in technical use are 心肺蘇生(法) \"cardiopulmonary resuscitation\" and 蘇生措置 \"resuscitation measures\"; 蘇生させる means \"to resuscitate someone\". Figuratively it can also mean the revival of a town or company, but this is elevated. The everyday word is 生き返る; 復活 rather means a comeback.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"重篤な患者は集中治療室で二十四時間体制の監視を受ける。","romaji":"Jūtoku na kanja wa shūchū chiryōshitsu de nijūyo jikan taisei no kanshi o ukeru.","german":"Critically ill patients receive 24-hour monitoring in the intensive care unit.","furigana":"じゅうとく|かんじゃ|しゅうちゅうちりょうしつ|にじゅうよじかんたいせい|かんし|う"},{"japanese":"事故の被害者は意識不明のまま集中治療室に運ばれた。","romaji":"Jiko no higaisha wa ishiki fumei no mama shūchū chiryōshitsu ni hakobareta.","german":"The accident victim was taken to the intensive care unit unconscious.","furigana":"じこ|ひがいしゃ|いしきふめい|しゅうちゅうちりょうしつ|はこ"},{"japanese":"母は集中治療室から一般病棟に移ることができた。","romaji":"Haha wa shūchū chiryōshitsu kara ippan byōtō ni utsuru koto ga dekita.","german":"My mother was able to move from the intensive care unit to a general ward.","furigana":"はは|しゅうちゅうちりょうしつ|いっぱんびょうとう|うつ"}],"notes":"集中治療室 is the \"intensive care unit\" (literally \"room for concentrated treatment\"). In everyday speech and in the news people usually say the short ICU (アイシーユー); there are also specialized units such as CCU (cardiac) or NICU (newborns). Typical: 集中治療室に運ばれる/入る \"to be taken to / enter the intensive care unit\", 集中治療室で治療を受ける, 集中治療室から一般病棟に移る \"to be moved from intensive care to a normal ward\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"バイタルサインの継続的な監視で患者の状態変化を早期に察知する。","romaji":"Baitaru sain no keizokuteki na kanshi de kanja no jōtai henka o sōki ni sacchi suru.","german":"Continuous monitoring of vital signs allows changes in the patient's condition to be detected early.","furigana":"けいぞくてき|かんし|かんじゃ|じょうたいへんか|そうき|さっち"},{"japanese":"手術後の患者のバイタルサインは安定しています。","romaji":"Shujutsugo no kanja no baitaru sain wa antei shite imasu.","german":"The patient's vital signs after the surgery are stable.","furigana":"しゅじゅつご|かんじゃ|あんてい"},{"japanese":"救急隊員はまずバイタルサインを確認した。","romaji":"Kyūkyū taiin wa mazu baitaru sain o kakunin shita.","german":"The paramedic first checked the vital signs.","furigana":"きゅうきゅうたいいん|かくにん"}],"notes":"バイタルサイン are the \"vital signs\": pulse, blood pressure, respiration, body temperature (often also level of consciousness). In nursing jargon this is shortened to バイタル: バイタルを測る/チェックする. Typical combinations: バイタルサインが安定している \"the vital signs are stable\", バイタルサインを確認する. The word belongs to the technical language of nursing, emergency services and clinics.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"予防医学は疾病の予防と健康増進を目的とする分野である。","romaji":"Yobō igaku wa shippei no yobō to kenkō zōshin o mokuteki to suru bun'ya de aru.","german":"Preventive medicine is a field aimed at preventing disease and promoting health.","furigana":"よぼういがく|しっぺい|よぼう|けんこうぞうしん|もくてき|ぶんや"},{"japanese":"予防医学の観点から、毎年の健康診断をおすすめします。","romaji":"Yobō igaku no kanten kara, maitoshi no kenkō shindan o osusume shimasu.","german":"From the standpoint of preventive medicine, we recommend an annual health checkup.","furigana":"よぼういがく|かんてん|まいとし|けんこうしんだん"},{"japanese":"医療費を抑えるため、国は予防医学に力を入れ始めた。","romaji":"Iryōhi o osaeru tame, kuni wa yobō igaku ni chikara o irehajimeta.","german":"To hold down medical costs, the government has begun putting effort into preventive medicine.","furigana":"いりょうひ|おさ|くに|よぼういがく|ちから|い|はじ"}],"notes":"予防医学 is \"preventive medicine\": 予防 \"prevention\" + 医学 \"medicine (as a science)\". It denotes a field of study, not an individual measure; for concrete preventive care people say 予防 or 健康診断 \"health checkup\". Typical: 予防医学の観点から \"from the viewpoint of preventive medicine\", 予防医学に力を入れる. Related is 治療医学 \"curative medicine\" as its opposite. Rather formal, in technical texts, politics and health journalism.","levelNote":"Compound: 予防 (N3) + 医学 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"公衆衛生の向上は社会全体の健康レベルを引き上げる。","romaji":"Kōshū eisei no kōjō wa shakai zentai no kenkō reberu o hikiageru.","german":"Improving public health raises the health level of society as a whole.","furigana":"こうしゅうえいせい|こうじょう|しゃかいぜんたい|けんこう|ひ|あ"},{"japanese":"上下水道の整備は、公衆衛生の歴史で大きな転機となった。","romaji":"Jōgesuidō no seibi wa, kōshū eisei no rekishi de ōki na tenki to natta.","german":"The development of water and sewage systems was a major turning point in the history of public health.","furigana":"じょうげすいどう|せいび|こうしゅうえいせい|れきし|おお|てんき"},{"japanese":"ごみの放置は公衆衛生上の問題になる。","romaji":"Gomi no hōchi wa kōshū eiseijō no mondai ni naru.","german":"Leaving garbage lying around becomes a public health problem.","furigana":"ほうち|こうしゅうえいせいじょう|もんだい"}],"notes":"公衆衛生 means \"public health\" in the sense of hygiene and health protection of the population: 公衆 \"the public\" + 衛生 \"hygiene\". It covers infection control, water supply, food hygiene and so on. Typical: 公衆衛生の向上, 公衆衛生上の問題 \"a public health problem\", 公衆衛生学 as a subject. Rather formal; in everyday speech people simply say 衛生 or 衛生面.","levelNote":"Compound: 公衆 (N2) + 衛生 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"手術前にインフォームドコンセントを取得することは倫理的義務である。","romaji":"Shujutsu mae ni infōmudo konsento o shutoku suru koto wa rinriteki gimu de aru.","german":"Obtaining informed consent before surgery is an ethical obligation.","furigana":"しゅじゅつまえ|しゅとく|りんりてきぎむ"},{"japanese":"治験に参加する前に、医師からインフォームドコンセントの説明を受けた。","romaji":"Chiken ni sanka suru mae ni, ishi kara infōmudo konsento no setsumei o uketa.","german":"Before taking part in the clinical trial, I received an explanation of informed consent from the doctor.","furigana":"ちけん|さんか|まえ|いし|せつめい|う"},{"japanese":"インフォームドコンセントが不十分だったとして、患者側が病院を訴えた。","romaji":"Infōmudo konsento ga fujūbun datta to shite, kanja-gawa ga byōin o uttaeta.","german":"The patient's side sued the hospital, claiming that informed consent had been insufficient.","furigana":"ふじゅうぶん|かんじゃがわ|びょういん|うった"}],"notes":"インフォームドコンセント is the patient's \"informed consent\" after the doctor's explanation, taken over from English. Typical: インフォームドコンセントを得る/取る, インフォームドコンセントが不十分だ. Because the word is long and incomprehensible to many patients, authorities recommend the Japanese paraphrase 説明と同意; in conversation people often simply say 十分な説明を受ける. It belongs to medical ethics, clinics and law.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"臨床研究の成果が新たな治療法の開発につながった。","romaji":"Rinshō kenkyū no seika ga arata na chiryōhō no kaihatsu ni tsunagatta.","german":"The results of clinical research led to the development of a new treatment.","furigana":"りんしょうけんきゅう|せいか|あら|ちりょうほう|かいはつ"},{"japanese":"この新薬はまだ臨床試験の段階だ。","romaji":"Kono shin'yaku wa mada rinshō shiken no dankai da.","german":"This new drug is still at the clinical trial stage.","furigana":"しんやく|りんしょうしけん|だんかい"},{"japanese":"研究ばかりでなく、臨床の現場でも十年働いてきた。","romaji":"Kenkyū bakari de naku, rinshō no genba de mo jūnen hataraite kita.","german":"Not only in research, I have also worked ten years in clinical practice.","furigana":"けんきゅう|りんしょう|げんば|じゅうねんはたら"}],"notes":"臨床 literally means \"at the sickbed\" (臨 \"to face something\" + 床 \"bed\") and denotes practical work with patients as opposed to basic research (基礎). It almost always stands attributively: 臨床研究, 臨床試験 \"clinical trial\", 臨床医 \"clinician\", 臨床の現場 \"clinical practice\". As a noun there is also 臨床に携わる \"to be engaged in patient care\". Also well known is 臨床心理士 \"clinical psychologist\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"AIを活用した創薬により新薬開発の期間が短縮されつつある。","romaji":"Ēai o katsuyō shita sōyaku ni yori shin'yaku kaihatsu no kikan ga tanshuku saretsutsu aru.","german":"Drug discovery using AI is shortening the time needed for new drug development.","furigana":"かつよう|そうやく|しんやくかいはつ|きかん|たんしゅく"},{"japanese":"彼女は大学で学んだ後、製薬会社で創薬研究に取り組んでいる。","romaji":"Kanojo wa daigaku de manda ato, seiyaku-gaisha de sōyaku kenkyū ni torikunde iru.","german":"After studying at university, she works on drug discovery research at a pharmaceutical company.","furigana":"かのじょ|だいがく||あと|せいやくがいしゃ|そうやくけんきゅう|と|く"},{"japanese":"創薬には十年以上の時間と巨額の費用がかかる。","romaji":"Sōyaku ni wa jūnen ijō no jikan to kyogaku no hiyō ga kakaru.","german":"Drug discovery takes more than ten years and an enormous amount of money.","furigana":"そうやく|じゅうねんいじょう|じかん|きょがく|ひよう"}],"notes":"創薬 means \"drug development, drug discovery research\" (創 \"create\" + 薬 \"medicine\") and denotes the whole process from the search for drug candidates to the new medicine. It is a relatively young technical word from the pharmaceutical industry and science policy: 創薬研究, 創薬ベンチャー, AI創薬. In everyday speech people rather say 新薬の開発 or 薬を開発する; 創薬 itself is not used as a する-verb.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"分子標的治療薬はがん細胞を選択的に攻撃する。","romaji":"Bunshi hyōteki chiryōyaku wa gan saibō o sentakuteki ni kōgeki suru.","german":"Molecularly targeted drugs selectively attack cancer cells.","furigana":"ぶんしひょうてきちりょうやく|さいぼう|せんたくてき|こうげき"},{"japanese":"この病気にはまだ有効な治療薬がない。","romaji":"Kono byōki ni wa mada yūkō na chiryōyaku ga nai.","german":"There is still no effective treatment drug for this disease.","furigana":"びょうき|ゆうこう|ちりょうやく"},{"japanese":"インフルエンザの治療薬は、発症から早めに飲むほど効果がある。","romaji":"Infuruenza no chiryōyaku wa, hasshō kara hayame ni nomu hodo kōka ga aru.","german":"The earlier you take a flu medicine after onset, the more effective it is.","furigana":"ちりょうやく|はっしょう|はや|の|こうか"}],"notes":"治療薬 is a \"drug for treating\" a particular disease, often with the disease name before it: 糖尿病の治療薬, インフルエンザ治療薬, 分子標的治療薬. It is distinct from 予防薬 \"preventive drug\" and ワクチン \"vaccine\". 特効薬, by contrast, is a \"miracle cure\" that works instantly. The general everyday word is simply 薬; 治療薬 sounds more technical and occurs in the news and in conversations with doctors.","levelNote":"Compound: 治療 (N3) + 薬 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"根本的な治療法がない場合は対症療法で症状を管理する。","romaji":"Konponteki na chiryōhō ga nai baai wa taishō ryōhō de shōjō o kanri suru.","german":"When there is no fundamental cure, symptoms are managed with symptomatic treatment.","furigana":"こんぽんてき|ちりょうほう|ばあい|たいしょうりょうほう|しょうじょう|かんり"},{"japanese":"風邪薬は対症療法なので、熱やせきを和らげるだけです。","romaji":"Kazegusuri wa taishō ryōhō na node, netsu ya seki o yawarageru dake desu.","german":"Cold medicine is symptomatic treatment, so it only eases the fever and cough.","furigana":"かぜぐすり|たいしょうりょうほう|ねつ|やわ"},{"japanese":"補助金を配るだけでは対症療法にすぎず、少子化は止まらない。","romaji":"Hojokin o kubaru dake de wa taishō ryōhō ni sugizu, shōshika wa tomaranai.","german":"Merely handing out subsidies is only a stopgap measure, and the declining birthrate will not stop.","furigana":"ほじょきん|くば|たいしょうりょうほう|しょうしか|と"}],"notes":"対症療法 is \"symptomatic treatment\", which treats only the symptoms (症), not the cause (対 = directed at); the opposite is 原因療法 or 根治療法. Figuratively it also means \"stopgap, patchwork\", for example with political measures: 対症療法にすぎない \"is merely fighting symptoms\". Note: the spelling 対処療法 is a very widespread mistake; the correct one is 対症 \"against the symptom\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"外科手術によってがんの根治を目指す。","romaji":"Geka shujutsu ni yotte gan no konchi o mezasu.","german":"We aim at a radical cure of the cancer through surgery.","furigana":"げかしゅじゅつ|こんち|めざ"},{"japanese":"この病気は薬だけでは根治しにくい。","romaji":"Kono byōki wa kusuri dake de wa konchi shinikui.","german":"This disease is hard to cure completely with medicine alone.","furigana":"びょうき|くすり|こんち"},{"japanese":"早期に発見できれば、根治手術が可能です。","romaji":"Sōki ni hakken dekireba, konchi shujutsu ga kanō desu.","german":"If it can be found early, a radical operation is possible.","furigana":"そうき|はっけん|こんちしゅじゅつ|かのう"}],"notes":"根治 means \"complete cure, healing at the root\" (根 \"root\" + 治 \"heal\"); the reading こんじ is also widespread. As a する-verb: 病気を根治する \"to cure a disease completely\" or intransitive 病気が根治する. Typical are 根治を目指す, 根治手術, 根治療法 (the opposite of 対症療法). 完治 also means \"completely cured\", but emphasizes the result, whereas 根治 rather emphasizes the removal of the cause. Also figuratively: 不正を根治する \"to eliminate abuses from the ground up\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"腸内細菌叢のバランスは全身の健康に影響を及ぼす。","romaji":"Chōnai saikinsō no baransu wa zenshin no kenkō ni eikyō o oyobosu.","german":"The balance of the gut microbiota affects the health of the whole body.","furigana":"ちょうないさいきんそう|ぜんしん|けんこう|えいきょう|およ"},{"japanese":"ヨーグルトや納豆は腸内細菌を増やすと言われている。","romaji":"Yōguruto ya nattō wa chōnai saikin o fuyasu to iwarete iru.","german":"Yogurt and natto are said to increase intestinal bacteria.","furigana":"なっとう|ちょうないさいきん|ふ|い"},{"japanese":"抗生物質を飲むと、腸内細菌まで減ってしまうことがある。","romaji":"Kōsei busshitsu o nomu to, chōnai saikin made hette shimau koto ga aru.","german":"Taking antibiotics can sometimes reduce even the intestinal bacteria.","furigana":"こうせいぶっしつ|の|ちょうないさいきん|へ"}],"notes":"腸内細菌 are the \"intestinal bacteria\" (腸 \"intestine\" + 内 \"inside\" + 細菌 \"bacteria\"). The totality is called 腸内細菌叢 technically, and 腸内フローラ \"gut flora\" in popular usage. Common expressions: 腸内細菌のバランス, 善玉菌 \"good bacteria\" and 悪玉菌 \"bad bacteria\", 腸内環境を整える \"to bring the intestinal environment into balance\". The word is very widespread in advertising for yogurt and dietary supplements.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"胃の粘膜はピロリ菌の感染によって損傷を受けることがある。","romaji":"I no nenmaku wa pirori kin no kansen ni yotte sonshō o ukeru koto ga aru.","german":"The mucous membrane of the stomach can be damaged by infection with Helicobacter pylori.","furigana":"い|ねんまく|きん|かんせん|そんしょう|う"},{"japanese":"冬は空気が乾燥して、鼻やのどの粘膜が弱りやすい。","romaji":"Fuyu wa kūki ga kansō shite, hana ya nodo no nenmaku ga yowariyasui.","german":"In winter the air is dry, and the mucous membranes of the nose and throat easily weaken.","furigana":"ふゆ|くうき|かんそう|はな|ねんまく|よわ"},{"japanese":"熱いものを急いで食べて、口の中の粘膜をやけどした。","romaji":"Atsui mono o isoide tabete, kuchi no naka no nenmaku o yakedo shita.","german":"I ate something hot in a hurry and burned the mucous membrane in my mouth.","furigana":"あつ|いそ|た|くち|なか|ねんまく"}],"notes":"粘膜 is the \"mucous membrane\" (粘 \"sticky\" + 膜 \"membrane\"). Mostly with a body part before it: 胃の粘膜 \"gastric mucosa\", 鼻の粘膜, 口の中の粘膜. Typical: 粘膜が荒れる \"the mucous membrane is irritated\", 粘膜が乾燥する, 粘膜を保護する. Do not confuse it with 鼓膜 \"eardrum\" or 角膜 \"cornea\". The word is medical, but also familiar from advertising for lozenges and nasal sprays.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"関節の軟骨が摩耗すると変形性関節症になる。","romaji":"Kansetsu no nankotsu ga mamō suru to henkeisei kansetsu shō ni naru.","german":"When the cartilage in a joint wears down, osteoarthritis develops.","furigana":"かんせつ|なんこつ|まもう|へんけいせいかんせつしょう"},{"japanese":"年を取って膝の軟骨がすり減り、階段がつらくなった。","romaji":"Toshi o totte hiza no nankotsu ga suriheri, kaidan ga tsuraku natta.","german":"As I got older, the cartilage in my knees wore away, and stairs became hard.","furigana":"とし|と|ひざ|なんこつ|へ|かいだん"},{"japanese":"居酒屋で軟骨の唐揚げを注文した。","romaji":"Izakaya de nankotsu no karaage o chūmon shita.","german":"At the izakaya I ordered fried cartilage.","furigana":"いざかや|なんこつ|からあ|ちゅうもん"}],"notes":"軟骨 is \"cartilage\" (軟 \"soft\" + 骨 \"bone\"), e.g. 関節の軟骨, 耳の軟骨, 鼻の軟骨. Typical: 軟骨がすり減る \"the cartilage wears away\". In addition, 軟骨 (often in kana なんこつ) is also a dish: fried or grilled chicken cartilage, popular in izakaya and as yakitori (軟骨の唐揚げ). The counterpart in the body is 硬骨 \"bone (in the narrow sense)\", in everyday speech simply 骨.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"前十字靭帯の損傷はスポーツ選手に多い怪我である。","romaji":"Zen jūji jintai no sonshō wa supōtsu senshu ni ōi kega de aru.","german":"Anterior cruciate ligament injury is a common injury among athletes.","furigana":"ぜんじゅうじじんたい|そんしょう|せんしゅ|おお|けが"},{"japanese":"足首をひねって靭帯を痛めてしまった。","romaji":"Ashikubi o hinette jintai o itamete shimatta.","german":"I twisted my ankle and hurt a ligament.","furigana":"あしくび|じんたい|いた"},{"japanese":"そのエースは膝の靭帯を断裂し、今シーズンは絶望となった。","romaji":"Sono ēsu wa hiza no jintai o danretsu shi, kon shīzun wa zetsubō to natta.","german":"The ace tore a ligament in his knee, and his season is hopeless.","furigana":"ひざ|じんたい|だんれつ|こん|ぜつぼう"}],"notes":"靭帯 is the \"ligament\" that connects bones at joints. The official spelling is 靱帯; 靭帯 is the variant common in the media, and both are read じんたい. Typical combinations: 靭帯を痛める/損傷する, 靭帯が切れる/断裂する \"a ligament tears\", 前十字靭帯 \"anterior cruciate ligament\". Distinguish it from 腱 \"tendon\", which connects muscle and bone. Frequent in sports news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"アキレス腱の断裂は急激な運動で起こりうる。","romaji":"Akiresu ken no danretsu wa kyūgeki na undō de okoriuru.","german":"Rupture of the Achilles tendon can occur from sudden exercise.","furigana":"けん|だんれつ|きゅうげき|うんどう|お"},{"japanese":"毎日スマホを使いすぎて、手首の腱を痛めた。","romaji":"Mainichi sumaho o tsukaisugite, tekubi no ken o itameta.","german":"I used my smartphone too much every day and hurt a tendon in my wrist.","furigana":"まいにち|つか|てくび|けん|いた"},{"japanese":"守備の弱さがこのチームのアキレス腱だ。","romaji":"Shubi no yowasa ga kono chīmu no Akiresu ken da.","german":"Weak defense is this team's Achilles heel.","furigana":"しゅび|よわ|けん"}],"notes":"腱 is the \"tendon\" that connects a muscle to bone; by contrast, 靭帯 connects bone to bone. 腱 rarely stands alone, mostly in compounds: アキレス腱 \"Achilles tendon\" (also figuratively \"Achilles heel, weak point\"), 腱鞘炎 \"tendon sheath inflammation\", 腱を切る \"to tear a tendon\". Because the kanji is difficult, the media sometimes also write けん in kana.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"椎間板ヘルニアは腰痛の主要な原因の一つである。","romaji":"Tsuikanban herunia wa yōtsū no shuyō na gen'in no hitotsu de aru.","german":"A herniated disc is one of the main causes of lower back pain.","furigana":"ついかんばん|ようつう|しゅよう|げんいん|ひと"},{"japanese":"重い荷物を持ち上げた時に、腰の椎間板を痛めてしまった。","romaji":"Omoi nimotsu o mochiageta toki ni, koshi no tsuikanban o itamete shimatta.","german":"When I lifted a heavy load, I hurt a disc in my lower back.","furigana":"おも|にもつ|も|あ|とき|こし|ついかんばん|いた"},{"japanese":"MRIで見ると、椎間板が少しつぶれているのが分かります。","romaji":"Emu-āru-ai de miru to, tsuikanban ga sukoshi tsuburete iru no ga wakarimasu.","german":"On the MRI you can see that the disc is slightly compressed.","furigana":"み|ついかんばん|すこ|わ"}],"notes":"椎間板 is the \"intervertebral disc\" between two vertebrae (椎 \"vertebra\" + 間 \"between\" + 板 \"plate\"). By far the most common context is 椎間板ヘルニア \"slipped disc\", in everyday speech often just called ヘルニア. Further combinations: 腰椎の椎間板 \"disc of the lumbar spine\", 椎間板がすり減る/つぶれる. The word is medical, but patients with back problems know it well.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"骨粗鬆症は骨密度の低下によって骨折リスクが高まる疾患である。","romaji":"Kotsusoshōshō wa kotsumitsudo no teika ni yotte kossetsu risuku ga takamaru shikkan de aru.","german":"Osteoporosis is a disease in which a decrease in bone density raises the risk of fracture.","furigana":"こつそしょうしょう|こつみつど|ていか|こっせつ|たか|しっかん"},{"japanese":"祖母は骨粗鬆症で、転んだだけで足の骨が折れた。","romaji":"Sobo wa kotsusoshōshō de, koronda dake de ashi no hone ga oreta.","german":"My grandmother has osteoporosis, and she broke a leg bone just from falling.","furigana":"そぼ|こつそしょうしょう|ころ|あし|ほね|お"},{"japanese":"骨粗鬆症を予防するには、カルシウムと適度な運動が大切だ。","romaji":"Kotsusoshōshō o yobō suru ni wa, karushiumu to tekido na undō ga taisetsu da.","german":"To prevent osteoporosis, calcium and moderate exercise are important.","furigana":"こつそしょうしょう|よぼう|てきど|うんどう|たいせつ"}],"notes":"骨粗鬆症 is \"osteoporosis\", literally \"disease of bones that become coarse (粗) and loose (鬆)\". Because 鬆 is not an everyday kanji, media and brochures mostly write 骨粗しょう症. Typical combinations: 骨粗鬆症になる, 骨粗鬆症を予防する, 骨粗鬆症による骨折. Closely linked to 骨密度 \"bone density\"; women after menopause (閉経後) in particular are affected.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"高脂血症は心血管疾患のリスクを増大させる。","romaji":"Kōshiketsushō wa shin kekkan shikkan no risuku o zōdai saseru.","german":"Hyperlipidemia increases the risk of cardiovascular disease.","furigana":"こうしけつしょう|しんけっかんしっかん|ぞうだい"},{"japanese":"健康診断で高脂血症と言われ、食事を見直すことにした。","romaji":"Kenkō shindan de kōshiketsushō to iware, shokuji o minaosu koto ni shita.","german":"At my health checkup I was told I have hyperlipidemia, so I decided to review my diet.","furigana":"けんこうしんだん|こうしけつしょう|い|しょくじ|みなお"},{"japanese":"高血圧や高脂血症は、自覚症状がないまま進むことが多い。","romaji":"Kōketsuatsu ya kōshiketsushō wa, jikaku shōjō ga nai mama susumu koto ga ōi.","german":"High blood pressure and hyperlipidemia often progress without any subjective symptoms.","furigana":"こうけつあつ|こうしけつしょう|じかくしょうじょう|すす|おお"}],"notes":"高脂血症 is \"hyperlipidemia\", i.e. too-high blood fat values (高 \"high\" + 脂 \"fat\" + 血 \"blood\" + 症 \"disease\"). In the Japanese guidelines the term was largely replaced in 2007 by 脂質異常症 \"dyslipidemia\", but 高脂血症 is still widespread in everyday speech and among patients. Typically in a series with 高血圧 and 糖尿病 as 生活習慣病 \"lifestyle diseases\". Colloquially people often simply say コレステロールが高い.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"痛風は尿酸値が高い状態が続くと発症する。","romaji":"Tsūfū wa nyōsan chi ga takai jōtai ga tsuzuku to hasshō suru.","german":"Gout develops when a high uric acid level continues.","furigana":"つうふう|にょうさんち|たか|じょうたい|つづ|はっしょう"},{"japanese":"夜中に足の親指が急に腫れて、痛風の発作だと分かった。","romaji":"Yonaka ni ashi no oyayubi ga kyū ni harete, tsūfū no hossa da to wakatta.","german":"The big toe suddenly swelled in the middle of the night, and I realized it was a gout attack.","furigana":"よなか|あし|おやゆび|きゅう|は|つうふう|ほっさ|わ"},{"japanese":"ビールが好きな部長は、痛風になってからお酒を控えている。","romaji":"Bīru ga suki na buchō wa, tsūfū ni natte kara osake o hikaete iru.","german":"The department head, who likes beer, has been cutting back on alcohol since he got gout.","furigana":"す|ぶちょう|つうふう|さけ|ひか"}],"notes":"痛風 is \"gout\", literally \"pain-wind\"; the popular explanation of the name is that it hurts even when just a breath of wind touches the spot. The cause is a too-high 尿酸値 \"uric acid level\"; typical is the sudden 痛風発作 \"gout attack\", often in the big toe. People say 痛風になる/を発症する. Triggers are said to be プリン体 \"purines\" in beer and organ meats, which is why the word often comes up in conversations about drinking habits.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"甲状腺機能亢進症は代謝が異常に活発になる疾患である。","romaji":"Kōjōsen kinō kōshinshō wa taisha ga ijō ni kappatsu ni naru shikkan de aru.","german":"Hyperthyroidism is a disease in which metabolism becomes abnormally active."},{"japanese":"疲れやすいので検査したら、甲状腺の働きが弱っていた。","romaji":"Tsukareyasui node kensa shitara, kōjōsen no hataraki ga yowatte ita.","german":"I tire easily, so I had a test, and my thyroid function was weak.","furigana":"つか|けんさ|こうじょうせん|はたら|よわ"},{"japanese":"首の前が腫れているので、甲状腺の検査を受けてください。","romaji":"Kubi no mae ga harete iru node, kōjōsen no kensa o ukete kudasai.","german":"The front of your neck is swollen, so please have your thyroid examined.","furigana":"くび|まえ|は|こうじょうせん|けんさ|う"}],"notes":"甲状腺 is the \"thyroid gland\" (甲状 \"shield-shaped\" + 腺 \"gland\"). Common compounds: 甲状腺ホルモン, 甲状腺機能亢進症 \"overactive thyroid\" (well known is バセドウ病 \"Graves' disease\"), 甲状腺機能低下症 \"underactive thyroid\", 甲状腺がん. People say 甲状腺が腫れる \"the thyroid is enlarged\". The word is medical, but generally known.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"膵臓はインスリンを分泌する内分泌機能を持つ臓器である。","romaji":"Suizō wa insurin o bunpitsu suru naibunpitsu kinō o motsu zōki de aru.","german":"The pancreas is an organ with the endocrine function of secreting insulin.","furigana":"すいぞう|ぶんぴつ|ないぶんぴつきのう|も|ぞうき"},{"japanese":"膵臓がんは早期発見が難しいと言われている。","romaji":"Suizō gan wa sōki hakken ga muzukashii to iwarete iru.","german":"Pancreatic cancer is said to be difficult to detect early.","furigana":"すいぞう|そうきはっけん|むずか|い"},{"japanese":"お酒の飲みすぎで膵臓を悪くする人もいる。","romaji":"Osake no nomisugi de suizō o waruku suru hito mo iru.","german":"Some people damage their pancreas by drinking too much alcohol.","furigana":"さけ|の|すいぞう|わる|ひと"}],"notes":"膵臓 is the \"pancreas\"; it produces digestive juices (膵液) and insulin. Important combinations: 膵臓がん \"pancreatic cancer\" (considered hard to detect early), 膵炎 \"pancreatitis\", 膵臓の機能. Because of the difficult kanji, the media sometimes write すい臓. The word is widespread in health topics and the news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"副腎はストレスホルモンであるコルチゾールを分泌する。","romaji":"Fukujin wa sutoresu horumon de aru koruchizōru o bunpitsu suru.","german":"The adrenal glands secrete cortisol, a stress hormone.","furigana":"ふくじん|ぶんぴつ"},{"japanese":"副腎は左右の腎臓の上にある小さな臓器だ。","romaji":"Fukujin wa sayū no jinzō no ue ni aru chiisa na zōki da.","german":"The adrenal glands are small organs located above the left and right kidneys.","furigana":"ふくじん|さゆう|じんぞう|うえ|ちい|ぞうき"},{"japanese":"湿疹に副腎皮質ホルモンの塗り薬を処方された。","romaji":"Shisshin ni fukujin hishitsu horumon no nurigusuri o shohō sareta.","german":"I was prescribed an adrenal corticosteroid ointment for my eczema.","furigana":"しっしん|ふくじんひしつ|ぬ|ぐすり|しょほう"}],"notes":"副腎 is the \"adrenal gland\" (副 \"secondary\" + 腎 \"kidney\"), which sits on top of the kidneys and produces hormones such as cortisol and adrenaline. Common compounds: 副腎皮質 \"adrenal cortex\", 副腎皮質ホルモン \"corticosteroids\" (also for steroid ointments, ステロイド), 副腎髄質. The word is purely medical; do not confuse it with 腎臓 \"kidney\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"胆嚢炎は胆石によって引き起こされることが多い。","romaji":"Tannōen wa tanseki ni yotte hikiokosareru koto ga ōi.","german":"Cholecystitis is often caused by gallstones.","furigana":"たんのうえん|たんせき|ひ|お|おお"},{"japanese":"胆石があるので、胆嚢を取る手術を受けた。","romaji":"Tanseki ga aru node, tannō o toru shujutsu o uketa.","german":"I have gallstones, so I had surgery to remove my gallbladder.","furigana":"たんせき|たんのう|と|しゅじゅつ|う"},{"japanese":"胆嚢は、肝臓で作られた胆汁をためておく臓器だ。","romaji":"Tannō wa, kanzō de tsukurareta tanjū o tamete oku zōki da.","german":"The gallbladder is an organ that stores bile produced in the liver.","furigana":"たんのう|かんぞう|つく|たんじゅう|ぞうき"}],"notes":"胆嚢 is the \"gallbladder\", which stores the bile (胆汁) produced in the liver. Because of the difficult character 嚢, it is often written 胆のう. Typical words: 胆嚢炎 (\"gallbladder inflammation\"), 胆石 (\"gallstone\"), 胆嚢摘出 (\"removal of the gallbladder\"). 胆 alone also stands for \"courage\" (大胆, 胆力). Medical terminology, but familiar in conversations with doctors.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"脾臓は老化した赤血球の破壊と免疫機能に関与する。","romaji":"Hizō wa rōka shita sekkekkyū no hakai to men'eki kinō ni kan'yo suru.","german":"The spleen is involved in destroying aged red blood cells and in immune function.","furigana":"ひぞう|ろうか|せっけっきゅう|はかい|めんえききのう|かんよ"},{"japanese":"交通事故で脾臓が破裂し、緊急手術になった。","romaji":"Kōtsū jiko de hizō ga haretsu shi, kinkyū shujutsu ni natta.","german":"My spleen ruptured in a traffic accident, and I had emergency surgery.","furigana":"こうつうじこ|ひぞう|はれつ|きんきゅうしゅじゅつ"},{"japanese":"脾臓は胃の左後ろ側にある。","romaji":"Hizō wa i no hidari ushirogawa ni aru.","german":"The spleen is located behind and to the left of the stomach.","furigana":"ひぞう|い|ひだりうし|がわ"}],"notes":"脾臓 is the \"spleen\" (脾 \"spleen\" + 臓 \"organ\"). Typical contexts are 脾臓が腫れる \"the spleen is enlarged\" (脾腫) and, after accidents, 脾臓破裂 \"ruptured spleen\" or 脾臓を摘出する \"to remove the spleen\". In everyday life the word hardly plays a role; you encounter it in anatomy, in conversations with doctors and in accident reports. Because of the rare kanji, ひ臓 is also found.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"横隔膜の収縮によって肺に空気が取り込まれる。","romaji":"Ōkakumaku no shūshuku ni yotte hai ni kūki ga torikomareru.","german":"Air is drawn into the lungs through contraction of the diaphragm.","furigana":"おうかくまく|しゅうしゅく|はい|くうき|と|こ"},{"japanese":"しゃっくりは横隔膜のけいれんで起こる。","romaji":"Shakkuri wa ōkakumaku no keiren de okoru.","german":"Hiccups are caused by spasms of the diaphragm.","furigana":"おうかくまく|お"},{"japanese":"歌うときは横隔膜を意識して、お腹から声を出しましょう。","romaji":"Utau toki wa ōkakumaku o ishiki shite, onaka kara koe o dashimashō.","german":"When you sing, be aware of your diaphragm and project your voice from your belly.","furigana":"うた|おうかくまく|いしき|なか|こえ|だ"}],"notes":"横隔膜 is the \"diaphragm\", the membrane (膜) that separates (隔) crosswise (横) the chest cavity from the abdominal cavity. Typical: 横隔膜が収縮する, 横隔膜を使って呼吸する (abdominal breathing, 腹式呼吸). Hiccups (しゃっくり) are explained as 横隔膜のけいれん. In 焼肉, the beef diaphragm is known as ハラミ or サガリ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"網膜剥離は早急に治療しないと失明の恐れがある。","romaji":"Mōmaku hakuri wa sōkyū ni chiryō shinai to shitsumei no osore ga aru.","german":"Retinal detachment carries the risk of blindness if not treated promptly.","furigana":"もうまくはくり|そうきゅう|ちりょう|しつめい|おそ"},{"japanese":"目に入った光は、レンズを通って網膜に像を結ぶ。","romaji":"Me ni haitta hikari wa, renzu o tōtte mōmaku ni zō o musubu.","german":"Light entering the eye passes through the lens and forms an image on the retina.","furigana":"め|はい|ひかり|とお|もうまく|ぞう|むす"},{"japanese":"糖尿病の人は、定期的に網膜の検査を受けたほうがいい。","romaji":"Tōnyōbyō no hito wa, teikiteki ni mōmaku no kensa o uketa hō ga ii.","german":"People with diabetes should have their retinas examined regularly.","furigana":"とうにょうびょう|ひと|ていきてき|もうまく|けんさ|う"}],"notes":"網膜 is the \"retina\" of the eye (網 \"net\" + 膜 \"membrane\"). Common combinations: 網膜剥離 \"retinal detachment\", 網膜に映る \"to be projected on the retina\", 糖尿病網膜症 \"diabetic retinopathy\". Distinguish it from 角膜 \"cornea\" (at the front) and 水晶体 \"lens\". Figuratively, 網膜に焼きつく is used for an image that has burned itself into the memory.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"角膜移植によって視力を回復させることが可能である。","romaji":"Kakumaku ishoku ni yotte shiryoku o kaifuku saseru koto ga kanō de aru.","german":"Vision can be restored through a corneal transplant.","furigana":"かくまくいしょく|しりょく|かいふく|かのう"},{"japanese":"コンタクトレンズを付けたまま寝て、角膜に傷がついた。","romaji":"Kontakuto renzu o tsuketa mama nete, kakumaku ni kizu ga tsuita.","german":"I slept with my contact lenses in and scratched my cornea.","furigana":"つ|ね|かくまく|きず"},{"japanese":"レーシックは角膜をレーザーで削って視力を矯正する手術だ。","romaji":"Rēshikku wa kakumaku o rēzā de kezutte shiryoku o kyōsei suru shujutsu da.","german":"LASIK is a surgery that corrects vision by reshaping the cornea with a laser.","furigana":"かくまく|けず|しりょく|きょうせい|しゅじゅつ"}],"notes":"角膜 is the \"cornea\" of the eye (角 \"horn\" + 膜 \"membrane\"); for the callus on the feet, by contrast, people say 角質 or たこ. Typical combinations: 角膜移植 \"corneal transplant\", 角膜に傷がつく \"the cornea gets injured\", 角膜炎. Frequent in connection with contact lenses and laser surgery such as レーシック. Do not confuse it with 網膜 \"retina\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"白内障は水晶体が白く濁る眼疾患である。","romaji":"Hakunaishō wa suishōtai ga shiroku nigoru ganshikkan de aru.","german":"A cataract is an eye disease in which the lens becomes cloudy and white.","furigana":"はくないしょう|すいしょうたい|しろ|にご|がんしっかん"},{"japanese":"水晶体の厚さが変わることで、目はピントを合わせている。","romaji":"Suishōtai no atsusa ga kawaru koto de, me wa pinto o awasete iru.","german":"The eye focuses by changing the thickness of the lens.","furigana":"すいしょうたい|あつ|か|め|あ"},{"japanese":"手術で、濁った水晶体を人工のレンズに取り替えた。","romaji":"Shujutsu de, nigotta suishōtai o jinkō no renzu ni torikaeta.","german":"In the surgery, the clouded lens was replaced with an artificial lens.","furigana":"しゅじゅつ|にご|すいしょうたい|じんこう|と|か"}],"notes":"水晶体 is the \"lens of the eye\"; literally \"crystal body\". For cameras and glasses, by contrast, people say レンズ. The eye focuses as the ciliary muscle changes the thickness of the 水晶体. In a cataract (白内障) the lens becomes cloudy: 水晶体が濁る. Other parts of the eye are 角膜 (\"cornea\") and 網膜 (\"retina\"). A technical word, but one that often comes up in conversations with doctors about the eyes.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"中耳炎が進行すると鼓膜が穿孔することがある。","romaji":"Chūjien ga shinkō suru to komaku ga senkō suru koto ga aru.","german":"As otitis media progresses, the eardrum may perforate.","furigana":"ちゅうじえん|しんこう|こまく|"},{"japanese":"耳かきで奥まで掃除しすぎて、鼓膜を傷つけてしまった。","romaji":"Mimikaki de oku made sōji shisugite, komaku o kizutsukete shimatta.","german":"I cleaned too deep with an ear pick and damaged my eardrum.","furigana":"みみ|おく|そうじ|こまく|きず"},{"japanese":"ライブのスピーカーの前にいたら、鼓膜が破れそうなほどうるさかった。","romaji":"Raibu no supīkā no mae ni itara, komaku ga yaburesō na hodo urusakatta.","german":"When I stood in front of the speakers at the concert, it was so loud that my eardrums felt like they would burst.","furigana":"まえ|こまく|やぶ"}],"notes":"鼓膜 is the \"eardrum\" (鼓 \"drum\" + 膜 \"membrane\"). People say 鼓膜が破れる \"the eardrum bursts\" (technically 鼓膜が穿孔する) and 鼓膜を傷つける. Colloquially and exaggerating: 鼓膜が破れそうな音/声 \"ear-splittingly loud\". Related are 中耳 \"middle ear\" and 中耳炎 \"otitis media\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"蝸牛は音の振動を神経信号に変換する器官である。","romaji":"Kagyū wa oto no shindō o shinkei shingō ni henkan suru kikan de aru.","german":"The cochlea is the organ that converts sound vibrations into nerve signals.","furigana":"かぎゅう|おと|しんどう|しんけいしんごう|へんかん|きかん"},{"japanese":"大きな音を聞き続けると、蝸牛の細胞が傷ついて難聴になることがある。","romaji":"Ōki na oto o kikitsuzukeru to, kagyū no saibō ga kizutsuite nanchō ni naru koto ga aru.","german":"If you keep listening to loud sounds, the cells of the cochlea can be damaged, leading to hearing loss.","furigana":"おお|おと|き|つづ|かぎゅう|さいぼう|きず|なんちょう"},{"japanese":"蝸牛という名前は、その渦巻きの形がかたつむりに似ていることから来ている。","romaji":"Kagyū to iu namae wa, sono uzumaki no katachi ga katatsumuri ni nite iru koto kara kite iru.","german":"The name 蝸牛 comes from the fact that its spiral shape resembles a snail.","furigana":"かぎゅう|なまえ|うずま|かたち|に|き"}],"notes":"蝸牛 literally means \"snail\"; in the reading かぎゅう it is the anatomical technical term for the \"cochlea\" in the inner ear. The animal itself is called かたつむり (same kanji, different reading) or でんでんむし in everyday speech. Common in technical use: 蝸牛管 \"cochlear duct\", 蝸牛の有毛細胞 \"hair cells of the cochlea\"; the cochlear implant, by contrast, is called 人工内耳. The reading かぎゅう occurs practically only in medical and literary texts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大脳皮質は認知や感覚処理を司る脳の領域である。","romaji":"Dainō hishitsu wa ninchi ya kankaku shori o tsukasadoru nō no ryōiki de aru.","german":"The cerebral cortex is the brain region that governs cognition and sensory processing.","furigana":"だいのうひしつ|にんち|かんかくしょり|つかさど|のう|りょういき"},{"japanese":"人間は他の動物に比べて大脳皮質が非常に発達している。","romaji":"Ningen wa hoka no dōbutsu ni kurabete dainō hishitsu ga hijō ni hattatsu shite iru.","german":"Compared with other animals, humans have a highly developed cerebral cortex.","furigana":"にんげん|ほか|どうぶつ|くら|だいのうひしつ|ひじょう|はったつ"},{"japanese":"脳卒中で大脳皮質の一部が傷つき、言葉が出にくくなった。","romaji":"Nōsotchū de dainō hishitsu no ichibu ga kizutsuki, kotoba ga denikuku natta.","german":"A stroke damaged part of my cerebral cortex, and words became hard to produce.","furigana":"のうそっちゅう|だいのうひしつ|いちぶ|きず|ことば|で"}],"notes":"大脳皮質 is the \"cerebral cortex\": 大脳 \"cerebrum\" + 皮質 \"cortex\". It is divided into lobes, e.g. 前頭葉 \"frontal lobe\" and 側頭葉 \"temporal lobe\". Typical combinations: 大脳皮質が〜を司る \"is responsible for ...\", 大脳皮質の働き, 大脳皮質が発達する. A technical term from neurology, psychology and popular-science books about the brain.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"小脳は運動の協調やバランスの維持に関与する。","romaji":"Shōnō wa undō no kyōchō ya baransu no iji ni kan'yo suru.","german":"The cerebellum is involved in motor coordination and maintaining balance.","furigana":"しょうのう|うんどう|きょうちょう|いじ|かんよ"},{"japanese":"自転車の乗り方を体が覚えているのは、小脳のおかげだ。","romaji":"Jitensha no norikata o karada ga oboete iru no wa, shōnō no okage da.","german":"It is thanks to the cerebellum that the body remembers how to ride a bicycle.","furigana":"じてんしゃ|の|かた|からだ|おぼ|しょうのう"},{"japanese":"小脳に梗塞が起きて、まっすぐ歩けなくなった。","romaji":"Shōnō ni kōsoku ga okite, massugu arukenaku natta.","german":"I had an infarction in my cerebellum and became unable to walk straight.","furigana":"しょうのう|こうそく|お|ある"}],"notes":"小脳 is the \"cerebellum\" (小 \"small\" + 脳 \"brain\"), the counterpart of 大脳 \"cerebrum\"; in addition there is 脳幹 \"brain stem\". It controls balance and coordination of movement; failures lead, for example, to ふらつき \"staggering\". Typical: 小脳の働き, 小脳梗塞 \"cerebellar infarction\". Well known is the disease 脊髄小脳変性症. A technical word, but generally known from biology class.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"海馬は記憶の形成と空間認識に重要な役割を果たす。","romaji":"Kaiba wa kioku no keisei to kūkan ninshiki ni jūyō na yakuwari o hatasu.","german":"The hippocampus plays an important role in memory formation and spatial recognition.","furigana":"かいば|きおく|けいせい|くうかんにんしき|じゅうよう|やくわり|は"},{"japanese":"強いストレスが続くと、海馬が小さくなるという研究がある。","romaji":"Tsuyoi sutoresu ga tsuzuku to, kaiba ga chiisaku naru to iu kenkyū ga aru.","german":"There is research showing that the hippocampus shrinks when strong stress continues.","furigana":"つよ|つづ|かいば|ちい|けんきゅう"},{"japanese":"寝ている間に、海馬が一日の記憶を整理しているらしい。","romaji":"Nete iru aida ni, kaiba ga ichinichi no kioku o seiri shite iru rashii.","german":"It seems that while we sleep, the hippocampus organizes the day's memories.","furigana":"ね|あいだ|かいば|いちにち|きおく|せいり"}],"notes":"海馬 is the \"hippocampus\" in anatomy, the brain region for memory formation; the name (literally \"sea horse\") comes from its shape. Typical: 海馬が萎縮する \"the hippocampus shrinks\" (e.g. in Alzheimer's), 海馬の働き. In addition, 海馬 can also mean \"seahorse\" or \"walrus\", but in everyday speech today people say タツノオトシゴ and セイウチ for these. Popular-science books about learning and memory use the word often.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"扁桃体は恐怖や不安などの感情処理に関わる。","romaji":"Hentōtai wa kyōfu ya fuan nado no kanjō shori ni kakawaru.","german":"The amygdala is involved in processing emotions such as fear and anxiety.","furigana":"へんとうたい|きょうふ|ふあん|かんじょうしょり|かか"},{"japanese":"ヘビを見た瞬間、考えるより先に扁桃体が反応する。","romaji":"Hebi o mita shunkan, kangaeru yori saki ni hentōtai ga hannō suru.","german":"The moment you see a snake, the amygdala reacts before you can think.","furigana":"み|しゅんかん|かんが|さき|へんとうたい|はんのう"},{"japanese":"不安障害の人は扁桃体が過剰に働いていると考えられている。","romaji":"Fuan shōgai no hito wa hentōtai ga kajō ni hataraite iru to kangaerarete iru.","german":"People with anxiety disorders are thought to have an overactive amygdala.","furigana":"ふあんしょうがい|ひと|へんとうたい|かじょう|はたら|かんが"}],"notes":"扁桃体 is the \"amygdala\" in the brain, named after its almond shape (扁桃 \"almond\"). It processes mainly fear and emotional reactions: 扁桃体が反応する/過剰に働く. Do not confuse it with 扁桃 or 扁桃腺 \"tonsils (in the throat)\", as in 扁桃腺が腫れる \"the tonsils are swollen\". The word appears in psychology, neuroscience and popular books about stress.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"シナプスは神経細胞間の信号伝達の接合部である。","romaji":"Shinapusu wa shinkei saibō kan no shingō dentatsu no setsugōbu de aru.","german":"A synapse is the junction for signal transmission between nerve cells.","furigana":"しんけいさいぼうかん|しんごうでんたつ|せつごうぶ"},{"japanese":"同じことを何度も練習すると、シナプスのつながりが強くなる。","romaji":"Onaji koto o nando mo renshū suru to, shinapusu no tsunagari ga tsuyoku naru.","german":"When you practice the same thing many times, the synaptic connections become stronger.","furigana":"おな|なんど|れんしゅう|つよ"},{"japanese":"赤ちゃんの脳では、毎秒たくさんのシナプスが作られている。","romaji":"Akachan no nō de wa, maibyō takusan no shinapusu ga tsukurarete iru.","german":"In a baby's brain, many synapses are formed every second.","furigana":"あか|のう|まいびょう|つく"}],"notes":"シナプス is the \"synapse\", the junction between nerve cells (ニューロン). Typical: シナプスを形成する \"to form synapses\", シナプスの結合が強まる, シナプス伝達 \"synaptic transmission\". Popular texts about learning often say that repetition strengthens the シナプス. The word is neurological technical vocabulary, but familiar through nonfiction books and school biology.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ヒトの脳には約千億個のニューロンが存在する。","romaji":"Hito no nō ni wa yaku sen oku ko no nyūron ga sonzai suru.","german":"The human brain contains about one hundred billion neurons.","furigana":"のう|やくせんおくこ|そんざい"},{"japanese":"ニューロンは電気信号を使って情報を伝えている。","romaji":"Nyūron wa denki shingō o tsukatte jōhō o tsutaete iru.","german":"Neurons transmit information using electrical signals.","furigana":"でんきしんごう|つか|じょうほう|つた"},{"japanese":"AIの仕組みは、脳のニューロンのつながりをまねて作られた。","romaji":"Ēai no shikumi wa, nō no nyūron no tsunagari o manete tsukurareta.","german":"The mechanism of AI was made by imitating the connections of neurons in the brain.","furigana":"しく|のう|つく"}],"notes":"ニューロン is the \"neuron\", the loanword for the nerve cell; the Japanese word for it is 神経細胞, which is equally common in textbooks. Typical: ニューロンが発火する \"a neuron fires\", ニューロン同士がつながる, ミラーニューロン \"mirror neuron\". In IT it also stands for the nodes of artificial neural networks (ニューラルネットワーク).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"脳の可塑性によって損傷後も機能回復の可能性がある。","romaji":"Nō no kasosei ni yotte sonshō go mo kinō kaifuku no kanōsei ga aru.","german":"Thanks to the plasticity of the brain, functional recovery is possible even after damage.","furigana":"のう|かそせい|そんしょうご|きのうかいふく|かのうせい"},{"japanese":"粘土は可塑性が高いので、自由に形を変えられる。","romaji":"Nendo wa kasosei ga takai node, jiyū ni katachi o kaerareru.","german":"Clay has high plasticity, so it can be freely reshaped.","furigana":"ねんど|かそせい|たか|じゆう|かたち|か"},{"japanese":"子どもの脳は可塑性に富み、外国語の音もすぐに身につける。","romaji":"Kodomo no nō wa kasosei ni tomi, gaikokugo no oto mo sugu ni mi ni tsukeru.","german":"Children's brains are rich in plasticity, so they pick up the sounds of foreign languages quickly.","furigana":"こ|のう|かそせい|と|がいこくご|おと|み"}],"notes":"可塑性 is \"plasticity, formability\": 可 \"possible\" + 塑 \"to mold\" + 性 \"property\". Originally a term from physics and materials science (粘土の可塑性 \"formability of clay\"), it is today very common as 脳の可塑性 \"neural plasticity\", the ability of the brain to reorganize itself. Figuratively, one also speaks of the 可塑性 of children or systems. Typical: 可塑性がある/高い. Written-language and technical.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"心不全によって下肢に浮腫が生じることがある。","romaji":"Shin fuzen ni yotte kashi ni fushu ga shōjiru koto ga aru.","german":"Heart failure can cause edema in the lower limbs.","furigana":"しんふぜん|かし|ふしゅ|しょう"},{"japanese":"医師はすねを指で押して、浮腫の有無を確認した。","romaji":"Ishi wa sune o yubi de oshite, fushu no umu o kakunin shita.","german":"The doctor pressed the shin with a finger to check for the presence of edema.","furigana":"いし|ゆび|お|ふしゅ|うむ|かくにん"},{"japanese":"腎臓の病気では、顔やまぶたに浮腫が出ることが多い。","romaji":"Jinzō no byōki de wa, kao ya mabuta ni fushu ga deru koto ga ōi.","german":"With kidney disease, edema often appears on the face and eyelids.","furigana":"じんぞう|びょうき|かお|ふしゅ|で|おお"}],"notes":"浮腫 (ふしゅ) is the medical technical term for \"edema, water retention\". The same kanji are read むくみ in everyday speech (also written 浮腫み), and that is how patients talk: 足がむくむ \"the legs are swollen\". Technical use: 浮腫が生じる/出現する, 下肢浮腫, 肺水腫 as a special form in the lung. So the reading ふしゅ is doctors' and textbook language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"高熱による熱性痙攣は小児に多く見られる。","romaji":"Kōnetsu ni yoru nessei keiren wa shōni ni ōku mirareru.","german":"Febrile convulsions caused by high fever are common in children.","furigana":"こうねつ|ねっせいけいれん|しょうに|おお|み"},{"japanese":"疲れているせいか、最近まぶたがよく痙攣する。","romaji":"Tsukarete iru sei ka, saikin mabuta ga yoku keiren suru.","german":"Maybe because I'm tired, my eyelid has been twitching a lot lately.","furigana":"つか|さいきん|けいれん"},{"japanese":"男性は突然倒れて痙攣を起こし、救急車で運ばれた。","romaji":"Dansei wa totsuzen taorete keiren o okoshi, kyūkyūsha de hakobareta.","german":"The man suddenly collapsed and had convulsions, and he was taken away by ambulance.","furigana":"だんせい|とつぜんたお|けいれん|お|きゅうきゅうしゃ|はこ"}],"notes":"痙攣 is a \"cramp, twitch\" and is also used as an intransitive する-verb: 足が痙攣する. Typical: 痙攣を起こす \"to have a cramp / convulsion\", 熱性痙攣 \"febrile convulsion\", まぶたが痙攣する \"the eyelid twitches\". Because of the difficult kanji, it is very often written in kana as けいれん. For a calf cramp, people in everyday speech rather say 足がつる; 痙攣 is more general and also medical.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"血行障害が進行すると組織が壊疽に陥る危険がある。","romaji":"Kekkō shōgai ga shinkō suru to soshiki ga eso ni ochiiru kiken ga aru.","german":"If circulatory disorders progress, there is a danger that the tissue falls into gangrene.","furigana":"けっこうしょうがい|しんこう|そしき|えそ|おちい|きけん"},{"japanese":"糖尿病が悪化して足の指が壊疽を起こし、切断することになった。","romaji":"Tōnyōbyō ga akka shite ashi no yubi ga eso o okoshi, setsudan suru koto ni natta.","german":"My diabetes worsened and the toes developed gangrene, so they had to be amputated.","furigana":"とうにょうびょう|あっか|あし|ゆび|えそ|お|せつだん"},{"japanese":"凍傷を放置すると、指が壊疽になることもある。","romaji":"Tōshō o hōchi suru to, yubi ga eso ni naru koto mo aru.","german":"If frostbite is left untreated, fingers can also become gangrenous.","furigana":"とうしょう|ほうち|ゆび|えそ"}],"notes":"壊疽 is \"gangrene\", the death of tissue due to lack of blood flow or infection (壊 \"destroy\" + 疽 \"ulcer\"). Typical: 壊疽を起こす, 壊疽になる, 壊疽に陥る, 糖尿病性壊疽 \"diabetic gangrene\". Related is 壊死 \"necrosis\", which generally denotes the death of tissue; 壊疽 is a special form of it, often with blackening. Purely medical; in an emergency an amputation (切断) may become necessary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"虫垂の穿孔により腹膜炎を併発することがある。","romaji":"Chūsui no senkō ni yori fukumakuen o heihatsu suru koto ga aru.","german":"Perforation of the appendix can lead to concurrent peritonitis.","furigana":"ちゅうすい||ふくまくえん|へいはつ"},{"japanese":"盲腸を我慢しすぎて、腹膜炎になりかけた。","romaji":"Mōchō o gaman shisugite, fukumakuen ni narikaketa.","german":"I put up with my appendicitis for too long and nearly got peritonitis.","furigana":"もうちょう|がまん|ふくまくえん"},{"japanese":"腹膜炎は命に関わるので、すぐに手術が必要です。","romaji":"Fukumakuen wa inochi ni kakawaru node, sugu ni shujutsu ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"Peritonitis is life-threatening, so surgery is needed immediately.","furigana":"ふくまくえん|いのち|かか|しゅじゅつ|ひつよう"}],"notes":"腹膜炎 is \"peritonitis\": 腹膜 \"peritoneum\" + 炎 \"inflammation\". It often arises as a complication, for example when an inflamed appendix (虫垂, colloquially 盲腸) bursts. Typical: 腹膜炎を起こす/併発する, 腹膜炎になる. The classic warning is that a neglected appendicitis (盲腸) can lead to 腹膜炎. A medical technical term, but generally known.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"急性肺水腫は心不全の重篤な合併症である。","romaji":"Kyūsei hai suishu wa shin fuzen no jūtoku na gappeishō de aru.","german":"Acute pulmonary edema is a serious complication of heart failure.","furigana":"きゅうせいはいすいしゅ|しんふぜん|じゅうとく|がっぺいしょう"},{"japanese":"高い山に急いで登ると、肺水腫になる危険がある。","romaji":"Takai yama ni isoide noboru to, hai suishu ni naru kiken ga aru.","german":"If you climb a high mountain quickly, there is a risk of getting pulmonary edema.","furigana":"たか|やま|いそ|のぼ|はいすいしゅ|きけん"},{"japanese":"息苦しさを訴えて受診したところ、肺水腫と診断された。","romaji":"Ikigurushisa o uttaete jushin shita tokoro, hai suishu to shindan sareta.","german":"I went to see a doctor complaining of difficulty breathing and was diagnosed with pulmonary edema.","furigana":"いきぐる|うった|じゅしん|はいすいしゅ|しんだん"}],"notes":"肺水腫 is \"pulmonary edema\": 肺 \"lung\" + 水腫 \"dropsy, edema\". A typical cause is 心不全 \"heart failure\"; it is then called 心原性肺水腫. Further combinations: 肺水腫を起こす, 急性肺水腫. Also well known is 高地肺水腫 \"high-altitude pulmonary edema\" in mountaineering. Related is 浮腫 \"edema\" in general. Purely a technical term.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自然気胸は若い男性に比較的多く見られる。","romaji":"Shizen kikyō wa wakai dansei ni hikakuteki ōku mirareru.","german":"Spontaneous pneumothorax is relatively common in young men.","furigana":"しぜんききょう|わか|だんせい|ひかくてきおお|み"},{"japanese":"彼は気胸で一週間入院した。","romaji":"Kare wa kikyō de isshūkan nyūin shita.","german":"He was hospitalized for a week with a pneumothorax.","furigana":"かれ|ききょう|いっしゅうかんにゅういん"},{"japanese":"急に胸が痛くなり、病院で気胸と診断された。","romaji":"Kyū ni mune ga itaku nari, byōin de kikyō to shindan sareta.","german":"I suddenly got a pain in my chest and was diagnosed with a pneumothorax at the hospital.","furigana":"きゅう|むね|いた|びょういん|ききょう|しんだん"}],"notes":"気胸 is the medical technical term for a \"pneumothorax\": air enters the pleural space and the lung partially collapses. Typical are 気胸になる, 気胸を起こす and 気胸と診断される; the spontaneous form is called 自然気胸, the dangerous tension form 緊張性気胸. In everyday speech it is often paraphrased as 肺に穴が開く \"there is a hole in the lung\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"長期臥床の患者には褥瘡の予防が重要である。","romaji":"Chōki gashō no kanja ni wa jokusō no yobō ga jūyō de aru.","german":"Preventing pressure sores is important for patients who are bedridden for a long time.","furigana":"ちょうきがしょう|かんじゃ|じょくそう|よぼう|じゅうよう"},{"japanese":"二時間ごとに体の向きを変えて、褥瘡ができないようにしています。","romaji":"Nijikan goto ni karada no muki o kaete, jokusō ga dekinai yō ni shite imasu.","german":"We change your position every two hours so that no pressure sores form.","furigana":"にじかん|からだ|む|か|じょくそう"},{"japanese":"祖母のかかとに小さな褥瘡が見つかった。","romaji":"Sobo no kakato ni chiisana jokusō ga mitsukatta.","german":"A small pressure sore was found on my grandmother's heel.","furigana":"そぼ|ちい|じょくそう|み"}],"notes":"褥瘡 is the medical and nursing technical term for \"decubitus, pressure ulcer\". In everyday speech people almost always say 床ずれ; 褥瘡 is heard mainly in hospitals, nursing care and technical texts (褥瘡ケア, 褥瘡予防). Typical combinations are 褥瘡ができる \"a pressure sore forms\" and 褥瘡を防ぐ. Because of the rare character 褥, government offices often write じょく瘡.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"高齢者の誤嚥性肺炎は死因の上位に位置する。","romaji":"Kōreisha no goensei haien wa shiin no jōi ni ichi suru.","german":"Aspiration pneumonia in the elderly ranks high among causes of death.","furigana":"こうれいしゃ|ごえんせいはいえん|しいん|じょうい|いち"},{"japanese":"父は食事中に誤嚥しないよう、とろみをつけた飲み物を飲んでいる。","romaji":"Chichi wa shokujichū ni goen shinai yō, toromi o tsuketa nomimono o nonde iru.","german":"My father drinks thickened drinks so that he doesn't aspirate during meals.","furigana":"ちち|しょくじちゅう|ごえん|の|もの|の"},{"japanese":"飲み込む力が弱くなると、唾液でも誤嚥することがある。","romaji":"Nomikomu chikara ga yowaku naru to, daeki demo goen suru koto ga aru.","german":"When the ability to swallow weakens, a person can aspirate even saliva.","furigana":"の|こ|ちから|よわ|だえき|ごえん"}],"notes":"誤嚥 denotes \"swallowing the wrong way\" in the medical sense: food, liquid or saliva enters the windpipe instead of the esophagus. As a する-verb: 食べ物を誤嚥する; a feared consequence is 誤嚥性肺炎 \"aspiration pneumonia\". In everyday speech people say むせる or 気管に入る. Do not confuse it with 誤飲 \"accidentally swallowing\" an object into the stomach, for example when a child swallows a coin.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"長時間の不動は深部静脈血栓塞栓症のリスクを高める。","romaji":"Chōjikan no fudō wa shinbu jōmyaku kessen sokusenshō no risuku o takameru.","german":"Long periods of immobility raise the risk of deep vein thromboembolism.","furigana":"ちょうじかん|ふどう|しんぶじょうみゃくけっせんそくせんしょう|たか"},{"japanese":"長時間のフライトの後、肺塞栓症を発症した。","romaji":"Chōjikan no furaito no ato, hai sokusenshō o hasshō shita.","german":"After a long flight, I developed a pulmonary embolism.","furigana":"ちょうじかん|あと|はいそくせんしょう|はっしょう"},{"japanese":"心房細動は脳塞栓症の主な原因の一つだ。","romaji":"Shinbō saidō wa nō sokusenshō no omo na gen'in no hitotsu da.","german":"Atrial fibrillation is one of the main causes of cerebral embolism.","furigana":"しんぼうさいどう|のうそくせんしょう|おも|げんいん|ひと"}],"notes":"塞栓症 is an \"embolism\": a blood clot, fat or air (塞栓) is carried along in the blood and blocks a vessel. It mostly appears in compounds: 肺塞栓症 \"pulmonary embolism\", 脳塞栓症 \"cerebral embolism\", 血栓塞栓症 \"thromboembolism\". The pulmonary embolism after sitting for a long time on a plane is popularly known as エコノミークラス症候群. Purely technical; in everyday speech people rather say 血管が詰まる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"腸閉塞は激しい腹痛と嘔吐を伴う急性疾患である。","romaji":"Chōheisoku wa hageshii fukutsū to ōto o tomonau kyūsei shikkan de aru.","german":"Intestinal obstruction is an acute disease accompanied by severe abdominal pain and vomiting.","furigana":"ちょうへいそく|はげ|ふくつう|おうと|ともな|きゅうせいしっかん"},{"japanese":"祖父は以前の手術の癒着が原因で腸閉塞を起こした。","romaji":"Sofu wa izen no shujutsu no yuchaku ga gen'in de chōheisoku o okoshita.","german":"My grandfather developed an intestinal obstruction caused by adhesions from a previous surgery.","furigana":"そふ|いぜん|しゅじゅつ|ゆちゃく|げんいん|ちょうへいそく|お"},{"japanese":"腸閉塞の疑いで、すぐに緊急入院することになった。","romaji":"Chōheisoku no utagai de, sugu ni kinkyū nyūin suru koto ni natta.","german":"With suspected intestinal obstruction, I was admitted to the hospital as an emergency right away.","furigana":"ちょうへいそく|うたが|きんきゅうにゅういん"}],"notes":"腸閉塞 is an \"intestinal obstruction\": the intestinal contents can no longer be transported onward. In hospital routine you often hear the loanword イレウス, used largely with the same meaning. Typical are 腸閉塞になる and 腸閉塞を起こす; a frequent cause is adhesions (癒着) after abdominal surgery. Key symptoms are 腹痛, 嘔吐 and absent bowel movements.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"膀胱炎は女性に多く見られる尿路感染症である。","romaji":"Bōkōen wa josei ni ōku mirareru nyōro kansenshō de aru.","german":"Cystitis is a urinary tract infection commonly seen in women.","furigana":"ぼうこうえん|じょせい|おお|み|にょうろかんせんしょう"},{"japanese":"膀胱に尿がたまると、トイレに行きたくなる。","romaji":"Bōkō ni nyō ga tamaru to, toire ni ikitaku naru.","german":"When urine accumulates in the bladder, you want to go to the toilet.","furigana":"ぼうこう|にょう|い"},{"japanese":"検査の前に膀胱をいっぱいにしておいてください。","romaji":"Kensa no mae ni bōkō o ippai ni shite oite kudasai.","german":"Please fill your bladder before the examination.","furigana":"けんさ|まえ|ぼうこう"}],"notes":"膀胱 is the \"urinary bladder\", an anatomical term that also comes up in everyday life when talking about complaints. Common compounds are 膀胱炎 \"bladder infection\", 膀胱がん \"bladder cancer\" and 過活動膀胱 \"overactive bladder\". Because of the rare characters, guides often write ぼうこう. For frequent urges to urinate, people in everyday speech simply say トイレが近い.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"前立腺がんは高齢男性に多い悪性腫瘍である。","romaji":"Zenritsusen gan wa kōrei dansei ni ōi akusei shuyō de aru.","german":"Prostate cancer is a malignant tumor common in elderly men.","furigana":"ぜんりつせん|こうれいだんせい|おお|あくせいしゅよう"},{"japanese":"父は前立腺肥大で、夜中に何度もトイレに起きる。","romaji":"Chichi wa zenritsusen hidai de, yonaka ni nando mo toire ni okiru.","german":"My father has an enlarged prostate and gets up to go to the toilet many times in the night.","furigana":"ちち|ぜんりつせんひだい|よなか|なんど|お"},{"japanese":"五十歳を過ぎたら、前立腺の検査を受けたほうがいい。","romaji":"Gojussai o sugitara, zenritsusen no kensa o uketa hō ga ii.","german":"Once you pass fifty, you should have your prostate examined.","furigana":"ごじゅっさい|す|ぜんりつせん|けんさ|う"}],"notes":"前立腺 is the \"prostate\", a gland below the bladder in men. Important compounds are 前立腺がん \"prostate cancer\" and 前立腺肥大(症) \"benign prostatic enlargement\"; the latter is the typical reason for nighttime urges to urinate in older men. At screening examinations you hear 前立腺の検査 or PSA検査. A technical term, but generally familiar in health topics.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子宮頸がんはワクチン接種によって予防できる。","romaji":"Shikyū keigan wa wakuchin sesshu ni yotte yobō dekiru.","german":"Cervical cancer can be prevented by vaccination.","furigana":"しきゅうけい|せっしゅ|よぼう"},{"japanese":"赤ちゃんは子宮の中で約四十週かけて育つ。","romaji":"Akachan wa shikyū no naka de yaku yonjusshū kakete sodatsu.","german":"A baby grows in the womb for about forty weeks.","furigana":"あか|しきゅう|なか|やくよんじゅっしゅう|そだ"},{"japanese":"姉は子宮筋腫の手術を受けた。","romaji":"Ane wa shikyū kinshu no shujutsu o uketa.","german":"My older sister had surgery for uterine fibroids.","furigana":"あね|しきゅうきんしゅ|しゅじゅつ|う"}],"notes":"子宮 is the \"uterus\"; the word is both a technical term and a normal everyday word. Important compounds are 子宮頸がん \"cervical cancer\", 子宮体がん \"endometrial cancer\", 子宮筋腫 \"fibroid\", 子宮内膜症 \"endometriosis\" and 子宮外妊娠 \"ectopic pregnancy\". Attributively it takes の: 子宮の中の赤ちゃん.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"卵巣がんは初期症状が乏しいため発見が遅れやすい。","romaji":"Ransō gan wa shoki shōjō ga toboshii tame hakken ga okureyasui.","german":"Ovarian cancer is easily detected late because early symptoms are scarce.","furigana":"らんそう|しょきしょうじょう|とぼ|はっけん|おく"},{"japanese":"超音波検査で右の卵巣に小さな腫れが見つかった。","romaji":"Chōonpa kensa de migi no ransō ni chiisana hare ga mitsukatta.","german":"A small swelling was found in the right ovary on the ultrasound.","furigana":"ちょうおんぱけんさ|みぎ|らんそう|ちい|は|み"},{"japanese":"卵巣は女性ホルモンを分泌する器官でもある。","romaji":"Ransō wa josei horumon o bunpitsu suru kikan de mo aru.","german":"The ovaries are also organs that secrete female hormones.","furigana":"らんそう|じょせい|ぶんぴつ|きかん"}],"notes":"卵巣 is the \"ovary\". It is a paired organ, so people say 右の卵巣, 左の卵巣 or 両側の卵巣. Common compounds are 卵巣がん \"ovarian cancer\", 卵巣嚢腫 \"ovarian cyst\" and 卵巣機能 \"ovarian function\". The male counterpart is 精巣 \"testis\". A technical term, but generally understandable in health topics.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"胎盤は母体から胎児へ栄養と酸素を供給する器官である。","romaji":"Taiban wa botai kara taiji e eiyō to sanso o kyōkyū suru kikan de aru.","german":"The placenta is the organ that supplies nutrients and oxygen from the mother to the fetus.","furigana":"たいばん|ぼたい|たいじ|えいよう|さんそ|きょうきゅう|きかん"},{"japanese":"赤ちゃんが生まれた後、しばらくして胎盤が出てきた。","romaji":"Akachan ga umareta ato, shibaraku shite taiban ga dete kita.","german":"Some time after the baby was born, the placenta came out.","furigana":"あか|う|あと|たいばん|で"},{"japanese":"検査で前置胎盤と分かり、帝王切開になった。","romaji":"Kensa de zenchi taiban to wakari, teiō sekkai ni natta.","german":"The test showed placenta previa, so I ended up having a cesarean section.","furigana":"けんさ|ぜんちたいばん|わ|ていおうせっかい"}],"notes":"胎盤 is the \"placenta, afterbirth\". Typical combinations are 胎盤が出る (expulsion after birth), 前置胎盤 \"placenta previa\" and 胎盤早期剥離 \"placental abruption\". In cosmetics and advertising language, the loanword プラセンタ is usually used for placenta extracts. 胎盤 itself is a technical term of obstetrics.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"羊水検査は胎児の染色体異常を診断する方法である。","romaji":"Yōsui kensa wa taiji no senshokutai ijō o shindan suru hōhō de aru.","german":"Amniocentesis is a method of diagnosing chromosomal abnormalities in the fetus.","furigana":"ようすいけんさ|たいじ|せんしょくたいいじょう|しんだん|ほうほう"},{"japanese":"健診で羊水の量が少し少ないと言われた。","romaji":"Kenshin de yōsui no ryō ga sukoshi sukunai to iwareta.","german":"At the checkup I was told that the amount of amniotic fluid is a little low.","furigana":"けんしん|ようすい|りょう|すこ|すく|い"},{"japanese":"夜中に破水して、羊水が流れ出てきた。","romaji":"Yonaka ni hasui shite, yōsui ga nagaredete kita.","german":"My water broke in the middle of the night, and the amniotic fluid flowed out.","furigana":"よなか|はすい|ようすい|なが|で"}],"notes":"羊水 is the \"amniotic fluid\" in which the fetus floats in the uterus. Important combinations are 羊水検査 \"amniotic fluid test\", 羊水が少ない / 多い and 羊水が流れ出る. The rupture of the membranes itself is called 破水 (破水する). A technical word of obstetrics, but generally known to pregnant women.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"逆子の場合は帝王切開が選択されることが多い。","romaji":"Sakago no baai wa teiō sekkai ga sentaku sareru koto ga ōi.","german":"In the case of a breech baby, a cesarean section is often chosen.","furigana":"さかご|ばあい|ていおうせっかい|せんたく|おお"},{"japanese":"長女は帝王切開で生まれました。","romaji":"Chōjo wa teiō sekkai de umaremashita.","german":"My eldest daughter was born by cesarean section.","furigana":"ちょうじょ|ていおうせっかい|う"},{"japanese":"赤ちゃんの心拍が下がったため、緊急帝王切開が行われた。","romaji":"Akachan no shinpaku ga sagatta tame, kinkyū teiō sekkai ga okonawareta.","german":"Because the baby's heartbeat dropped, an emergency cesarean section was performed.","furigana":"あか|しんぱく|さ|きんきゅうていおうせっかい|おこな"}],"notes":"帝王切開 is the \"cesarean section\"; 帝王 \"emperor\" + 切開 \"incision\" is a calque of the European term. Typical combinations are 帝王切開で産む / 生まれる, 帝王切開になる (when one becomes necessary), 緊急帝王切開 and 予定帝王切開. The opposite is 自然分娩 or 経腟分娩. Among hospital staff and mothers the short form 帝切 (ていせつ) is also widespread.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"陣痛が規則的になったら分娩の開始が近い。","romaji":"Jintsū ga kisokuteki ni nattara bunben no kaishi ga chikai.","german":"Once contractions become regular, the start of delivery is near.","furigana":"じんつう|きそくてき|ぶんべん|かいし|ちか"},{"japanese":"陣痛の間隔が十分になったら、病院に電話してください。","romaji":"Jintsū no kankaku ga juppun ni nattara, byōin ni denwa shite kudasai.","german":"When the interval between contractions becomes ten minutes, please call the hospital.","furigana":"じんつう|かんかく|じゅっぷん|びょういん|でんわ"},{"japanese":"妻は十二時間も陣痛に耐えて、元気な男の子を産んだ。","romaji":"Tsuma wa jūni jikan mo jintsū ni taete, genki na otoko no ko o unda.","german":"My wife endured contractions for twelve hours and gave birth to a healthy baby boy.","furigana":"つま|じゅうにじかん|じんつう|た|げんき|おとこ|こ|う"}],"notes":"陣痛 are \"labor pains, contractions\". Typical combinations are 陣痛が始まる / 来る, 陣痛が強くなる, 陣痛の間隔 \"contraction interval\" and 陣痛促進剤 \"labor-inducing drug\". Irregular practice contractions are called 前駆陣痛. The word is in general use, not only in hospitals; for childbirth as a whole people say 出産 or お産.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"無痛分娩は硬膜外麻酔を用いた出産方法である。","romaji":"Mutsū bunben wa kōmaku gai masui o mochiita shussan hōhō de aru.","german":"Painless delivery is a childbirth method that uses epidural anesthesia.","furigana":"むつうぶんべん|こうまくがいますい|もち|しゅっさんほうほう"},{"japanese":"夫は分娩室に入って出産に立ち会った。","romaji":"Otto wa bunbenshitsu ni haitte shussan ni tachiatta.","german":"My husband went into the delivery room and was present at the birth.","furigana":"おっと|ぶんべんしつ|はい|しゅっさん|た|あ"},{"japanese":"この病院では年間約千件の分娩を扱っている。","romaji":"Kono byōin de wa nenkan yaku senken no bunben o atsukatte iru.","german":"This hospital handles about a thousand deliveries a year.","furigana":"びょういん|ねんかんやくせんけん|ぶんべん|あつか"}],"notes":"分娩 is the medical term for \"delivery, childbirth\"; in everyday speech people rather say 出産 or お産. Typical are compounds such as 分娩室 \"delivery room\", 分娩台, 自然分娩 and 無痛分娩 \"birth with epidural anesthesia\". As a する-verb (分娩する) it sounds clinical; normal are 出産する or 産む.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新生児のスクリーニング検査で先天性疾患を早期発見する。","romaji":"Shinseiji no sukurīningu kensa de sentensei shikkan o sōki hakken suru.","german":"Screening tests for newborns detect congenital diseases early.","furigana":"しんせいじ|けんさ|せんてんせいしっかん|そうきはっけん"},{"japanese":"新生児は一日の大半を寝て過ごす。","romaji":"Shinseiji wa ichinichi no taihan o nete sugosu.","german":"Newborns spend most of the day sleeping.","furigana":"しんせいじ|いちにち|たいはん|ね|す"},{"japanese":"早産だったため、娘はしばらく新生児集中治療室に入っていた。","romaji":"Sōzan datta tame, musume wa shibaraku shinseiji shūchū chiryōshitsu ni haitte ita.","german":"Because it was a premature birth, my daughter spent some time in the neonatal intensive care unit.","furigana":"そうざん|むすめ|しんせいじしゅうちゅうちりょうしつ|はい"}],"notes":"新生児 is a \"newborn\", medically a child in the first 28 days of life. Compounds: 新生児室, 新生児集中治療室 (NICU), 新生児黄疸 \"neonatal jaundice\". In everyday speech people rather say 生まれたばかりの赤ちゃん. Distinguish 乳児 \"infant\" (up to about one year) and 幼児 \"toddler\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"藻類はバイオ燃料の原料として注目されている。","romaji":"Sōrui wa baio nenryō no genryō to shite chūmoku sarete iru.","german":"Algae are attracting attention as a raw material for biofuel.","furigana":"そうるい|ねんりょう|げんりょう|ちゅうもく"},{"japanese":"池の水が緑色なのは、藻類が増えているからだ。","romaji":"Ike no mizu ga midoriiro na no wa, sōrui ga fuete iru kara da.","german":"The pond water is green because algae are increasing.","furigana":"いけ|みず|みどりいろ|そうるい|ふ"},{"japanese":"海の藻類は光合成によって大量の酸素を作り出している。","romaji":"Umi no sōrui wa kōgōsei ni yotte tairyō no sanso o tsukuridashite iru.","german":"Algae in the sea produce large amounts of oxygen through photosynthesis.","furigana":"うみ|そうるい|こうごうせい|たいりょう|さんそ|つく|だ"}],"notes":"藻類 is the biological collective term for \"algae\", from microscopic green algae to large seaweeds. In everyday speech people say 藻 (も) for algae in a pond or aquarium and 海藻 for edible seaweed such as わかめ or 昆布. 藻類 sounds scientific and often appears in compounds such as 微細藻類 \"microalgae\" or in expressions like 藻類が増える / 繁殖する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"菌類は分解者として生態系で重要な役割を果たす。","romaji":"Kinrui wa bunkaisha to shite seitaikei de jūyō na yakuwari o hatasu.","german":"Fungi play an important role in ecosystems as decomposers.","furigana":"きんるい|ぶんかいしゃ|せいたいけい|じゅうよう|やくわり|は"},{"japanese":"キノコもカビも菌類の仲間だ。","romaji":"Kinoko mo kabi mo kinrui no nakama da.","german":"Mushrooms and molds are both members of the fungi.","furigana":"きんるい|なかま"},{"japanese":"パンやビールを作る酵母も菌類の一種だ。","romaji":"Pan ya bīru o tsukuru kōbo mo kinrui no isshu da.","german":"Yeast, which is used to make bread and beer, is also a kind of fungus.","furigana":"つく|こうぼ|きんるい|いっしゅ"}],"notes":"菌類 is the biological umbrella term for \"fungi\" in the broad sense: yeasts, molds and mushrooms. In everyday speech people say キノコ for edible mushrooms and カビ for mold; 菌類 is the scientific word. Do not confuse it with 細菌 \"bacteria\"; the single character 菌 colloquially means \"germs\" in general.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"酵素は特定の基質にのみ作用する選択性を持つ。","romaji":"Kōso wa tokutei no kishitsu ni nomi sayō suru sentakusei o motsu.","german":"Enzymes have the selectivity to act only on specific substrates.","furigana":"こうそ|とくてい|きしつ|さよう|せんたくせい|も"},{"japanese":"酵素は基質と結合して、反応を進める。","romaji":"Kōso wa kishitsu to ketsugō shite, hannō o susumeru.","german":"An enzyme binds to its substrate and moves the reaction forward.","furigana":"こうそ|きしつ|けつごう|はんのう|すす"},{"japanese":"軟骨の細胞は、自ら作った基質に囲まれている。","romaji":"Nankotsu no saibō wa, mizukara tsukutta kishitsu ni kakomarete iru.","german":"Cartilage cells are surrounded by a matrix they have made themselves.","furigana":"なんこつ|さいぼう|みずか|つく|きしつ|かこ"}],"notes":"基質 is above all a term of biochemistry: the substance on which an enzyme acts, i.e. the \"substrate\". Typical combinations are 基質特異性 \"substrate specificity\", 酵素と基質 and 基質と結合する. In histology 基質 also means \"matrix, ground substance\" (細胞外基質). Do not confuse it with the identically pronounced 気質 \"temperament\"; a substrate in electronics is called 基板.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"酵素は生体内の化学反応を促進する蛋白質である。","romaji":"Kōso wa seitainai no kagaku hannō o sokushin suru tanpakushitsu de aru.","german":"Enzymes are proteins that promote chemical reactions in living organisms.","furigana":"こうそ|せいたいない|かがくはんのう|そくしん|たんぱくしつ"},{"japanese":"唾液に含まれる酵素がでんぷんを分解する。","romaji":"Daeki ni fukumareru kōso ga denpun o bunkai suru.","german":"The enzyme contained in saliva breaks down starch.","furigana":"だえき|ふく|こうそ|ぶんかい"},{"japanese":"この洗剤には汚れを落とす酵素が入っている。","romaji":"Kono senzai ni wa yogore o otosu kōso ga haitte iru.","german":"This detergent contains enzymes that remove dirt.","furigana":"せんざい|よご|お|こうそ|はい"}],"notes":"酵素 is an \"enzyme\". Typical combinations are 酵素が働く, 酵素の働き, 消化酵素 \"digestive enzyme\" and 酵素反応. In everyday life the word also appears in advertising, for example for detergents (酵素入り洗剤) or drinks (酵素ドリンク). Do not confuse it with 酵母 \"yeast\", which is a living organism.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"リボソームはメッセンジャーRNAの情報に基づいて蛋白質を合成する。","romaji":"Ribosōmu wa messenjā āruenuē no jōhō ni motozuite tanpakushitsu o gōsei suru.","german":"Ribosomes synthesize proteins based on the information of messenger RNA.","furigana":"じょうほう|もと|たんぱくしつ|ごうせい"},{"japanese":"一部の抗生物質は、細菌のリボソームの働きを妨げる。","romaji":"Ichibu no kōsei busshitsu wa, saikin no ribosōmu no hataraki o samatageru.","german":"Some antibiotics interfere with the function of bacterial ribosomes.","furigana":"いちぶ|こうせいぶっしつ|さいきん|はたら|さまた"},{"japanese":"リボソームは小胞体の表面にも付着している。","romaji":"Ribosōmu wa shōhōtai no hyōmen ni mo fuchaku shite iru.","german":"Ribosomes are also attached to the surface of the endoplasmic reticulum.","furigana":"しょうほうたい|ひょうめん|ふちゃく"}],"notes":"リボソーム is the \"ribosome\", the cell structure at which proteins are built. A purely biological technical term; the spelling リボゾーム also occurs. Typical combinations are リボソームで合成される and リボソームRNA. In everyday life the word appears almost only in connection with antibiotics that inhibit bacterial ribosomes.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ミトコンドリアは細胞のエネルギー産生を担う細胞小器官である。","romaji":"Mitokondoria wa saibō no enerugī sansei o ninau saibō shōkikan de aru.","german":"Mitochondria are the cell organelles responsible for producing the cell's energy.","furigana":"さいぼう|さんせい|にな|さいぼうしょうきかん"},{"japanese":"ミトコンドリアはよく「細胞の発電所」と呼ばれる。","romaji":"Mitokondoria wa yoku saibō no hatsudensho to yobareru.","german":"Mitochondria are often called the \"power plants of the cell\".","furigana":"さいぼう|はつでんしょ|よ"},{"japanese":"ミトコンドリアのDNAは母親からだけ受け継がれる。","romaji":"Mitokondoria no dīenuē wa hahaoya kara dake uketsugareru.","german":"Mitochondrial DNA is inherited only from the mother.","furigana":"ははおや|う|つ"}],"notes":"ミトコンドリア is the \"mitochondrion\"; Japanese does not mark number, so the word also stands for \"mitochondria\". In popular science it is often called 細胞の発電所 \"power plant of the cell\". Typical combinations are ミトコンドリアDNA and ミトコンドリアの機能. A technical term, but quite well known through health and fitness topics.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"真核細胞は核膜に囲まれた明瞭な核を持つ。","romaji":"Shinkaku saibō wa kakumaku ni kakomareta meiryō na kaku o motsu.","german":"Eukaryotic cells have a distinct nucleus surrounded by a nuclear membrane.","furigana":"しんかくさいぼう|かくまく|かこ|めいりょう|かく|も"},{"japanese":"動物や植物の細胞は、すべて真核細胞である。","romaji":"Dōbutsu ya shokubutsu no saibō wa, subete shinkaku saibō de aru.","german":"The cells of animals and plants are all eukaryotic cells.","furigana":"どうぶつ|しょくぶつ|さいぼう|しんかくさいぼう"},{"japanese":"試験では真核細胞と原核細胞の違いを説明させる問題がよく出る。","romaji":"Shiken de wa shinkaku saibō to genkaku saibō no chigai o setsumei saseru mondai ga yoku deru.","german":"Exam questions often ask you to explain the difference between eukaryotic and prokaryotic cells.","furigana":"しけん|しんかくさいぼう|げんかくさいぼう|ちが|せつめい|もんだい|で"}],"notes":"真核細胞 is the \"eukaryotic cell\": 真 \"true\" + 核 \"nucleus\" + 細胞 \"cell\", i.e. a cell with a true cell nucleus enclosed by a nuclear membrane. The opposite is 原核細胞; organisms made of such cells are called 真核生物. Purely technical, typical of biology class and textbooks.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"細菌は核膜を持たない原核細胞から成る生物である。","romaji":"Saikin wa kakumaku o motanai genkaku saibō kara naru seibutsu de aru.","german":"Bacteria are organisms made up of prokaryotic cells that have no nuclear membrane.","furigana":"さいきん|かくまく|も|げんかくさいぼう|な|せいぶつ"},{"japanese":"原核細胞にはミトコンドリアなどの細胞小器官がない。","romaji":"Genkaku saibō ni wa mitokondoria nado no saibō shōkikan ga nai.","german":"Prokaryotic cells have no organelles such as mitochondria.","furigana":"げんかくさいぼう|さいぼうしょうきかん"},{"japanese":"原核細胞は真核細胞よりずっと小さい。","romaji":"Genkaku saibō wa shinkaku saibō yori zutto chiisai.","german":"Prokaryotic cells are much smaller than eukaryotic cells.","furigana":"げんかくさいぼう|しんかくさいぼう|ちい"}],"notes":"原核細胞 is the \"prokaryotic cell\": 原 \"original\" + 核 \"nucleus\" + 細胞 \"cell\", i.e. a cell without a true, membrane-enclosed cell nucleus. This includes bacteria and archaea, together called 原核生物. The opposite is 真核細胞. A purely technical biology term.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"DNAの二重螺旋構造はワトソンとクリックによって発見された。","romaji":"Dīenuē no nijū rasen kōzō wa Watoson to Kurikku ni yotte hakken sareta.","german":"The double helix structure of DNA was discovered by Watson and Crick.","furigana":"にじゅうらせんこうぞう|はっけん"},{"japanese":"二重螺旋の二本の鎖は、塩基どうしの結合でつながっている。","romaji":"Nijū rasen no nihon no kusari wa, enki dōshi no ketsugō de tsunagatte iru.","german":"The two strands of the double helix are connected by bonds between bases.","furigana":"にじゅうらせん|にほん|くさり|えんき|けつごう"},{"japanese":"科学館の入り口には、DNAの二重螺旋をかたどった大きな模型が飾られている。","romaji":"Kagakukan no iriguchi ni wa, dīenuē no nijū rasen o katadotta ōki na mokei ga kazararete iru.","german":"A large model shaped like the DNA double helix is displayed at the entrance of the science museum.","furigana":"かがくかん|い|ぐち|にじゅうらせん|おお|もけい|かざ"}],"notes":"二重螺旋 is the \"double helix\", mostly as the 二重螺旋構造 of DNA. Because 螺 and 旋 are difficult characters, 二重らせん is very often written. 螺旋 alone means \"spiral, helix\", e.g. in 螺旋階段 \"spiral staircase\". Technical, but generally known through the history of the discovery of DNA.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"転写はDNAの情報がRNAにコピーされる過程である。","romaji":"Tensha wa dīenuē no jōhō ga āruenuē ni kopī sareru katei de aru.","german":"Transcription is the process in which the information of DNA is copied into RNA.","furigana":"てんしゃ|じょうほう|かてい"},{"japanese":"型紙の線を布に転写してから裁断する。","romaji":"Katagami no sen o nuno ni tensha shite kara saidan suru.","german":"I transfer the lines of the pattern onto the fabric before cutting.","furigana":"かたがみ|せん|ぬの|てんしゃ|さいだん"},{"japanese":"子どもがお菓子のおまけの転写シールを腕に貼って遊んでいる。","romaji":"Kodomo ga okashi no omake no tensha shīru o ude ni hatte asonde iru.","german":"The child is playing with a transfer sticker from a candy giveaway stuck on his arm.","furigana":"こ|かし|てんしゃ|うで|は|あそ"}],"notes":"転写 generally means \"to transfer, to copy\": text or an image is copied from a template onto something else. In biology it is the technical term for \"transcription\" (DNAからRNAへの転写); the next step, translation into protein, is called 翻訳. In everyday life it appears with decals (転写シール) or when transferring sewing patterns. As a transitive する-verb: 情報を転写する, passive 転写される.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ヒトのゲノムは約三十億の塩基対から構成されている。","romaji":"Hito no genomu wa yaku sanjū oku no enkitsui kara kōsei sarete iru.","german":"The human genome consists of about three billion base pairs.","furigana":"やくさんじゅうおく|えんきつい|こうせい"},{"japanese":"アデニンはチミンと、グアニンはシトシンと塩基対をつくる。","romaji":"Adenin wa chimin to, guanin wa shitoshin to enkitsui o tsukuru.","german":"Adenine forms a base pair with thymine, and guanine with cytosine.","furigana":"えんきつい"},{"japanese":"たった一つの塩基対の違いが病気の原因になることもある。","romaji":"Tatta hitotsu no enkitsui no chigai ga byōki no gen'in ni naru koto mo aru.","german":"A difference of just a single base pair can cause disease.","furigana":"ひと|えんきつい|ちが|びょうき|げんいん"}],"notes":"塩基対 is a \"base pair\": two complementary bases of DNA (A-T, G-C) joined by hydrogen bonds. It also serves as a unit of measurement for the length of DNA, for example 約三十億塩基対. Composition: 塩基 \"base\" + 対 \"pair\", read つい here as in 一対. Purely technical.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ナノテクノロジーは原子レベルで物質を操作する技術である。","romaji":"Nano tekunorojī wa genshi reberu de busshitsu o sōsa suru gijutsu de aru.","german":"Nanotechnology is a technology that manipulates matter at the atomic level.","furigana":"げんし|ぶっしつ|そうさ|ぎじゅつ"},{"japanese":"この日焼け止めにはナノテクノロジーを応用した成分が使われている。","romaji":"Kono hiyakedome ni wa nano tekunorojī o ōyō shita seibun ga tsukawarete iru.","german":"This sunscreen uses an ingredient that applies nanotechnology.","furigana":"ひや|ど|おうよう|せいぶん|つか"},{"japanese":"大学でナノテクノロジーを専攻したいと考えている。","romaji":"Daigaku de nano tekunorojī o senkō shitai to kangaete iru.","german":"I am thinking of majoring in nanotechnology at university.","furigana":"だいがく|せんこう|かんが"}],"notes":"ナノテクノロジー is \"nanotechnology\", i.e. engineering in the range of billionths of a meter (ナノメートル). In headlines and conversation it is often abbreviated to ナノテク. Typical combinations are ナノテクノロジーを応用する, ナノテクノロジーの研究 and ナノテクノロジー分野. A loanword of technical and newspaper language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"三次元印刷技術は試作品の製造期間を大幅に短縮した。","romaji":"Sanjigen insatsu gijutsu wa shisakuhin no seizō kikan o ōhaba ni tanshuku shita.","german":"3D printing technology has greatly shortened the time needed to manufacture prototypes.","furigana":"さんじげんいんさつぎじゅつ|しさくひん|せいぞうきかん|おおはば|たんしゅく"},{"japanese":"三次元印刷で作った義手は、従来品よりずっと安い。","romaji":"Sanjigen insatsu de tsukutta gishu wa, jūraihin yori zutto yasui.","german":"Prosthetic hands made by 3D printing are much cheaper than conventional ones.","furigana":"さんじげんいんさつ|つく|ぎしゅ|じゅうらいひん|やす"},{"japanese":"工場では三次元印刷を使って部品を一つずつ作っている。","romaji":"Kōjō de wa sanjigen insatsu o tsukatte buhin o hitotsuzutsu tsukutte iru.","german":"At the factory, parts are made one by one using 3D printing.","furigana":"こうじょう|さんじげんいんさつ|つか|ぶひん|ひと|つく"}],"notes":"三次元印刷 is a rare, formal expression for \"3D printing\". In normal usage people almost always say 3Dプリント or speak of the device as 3Dプリンター; in industry and standards the process is called 積層造形 \"additive manufacturing\". 三次元印刷 is found mainly in written, factual texts. Composition: 三次元 \"three-dimensional\" + 印刷 \"printing\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"炭素繊維強化複合材料は航空機の軽量化に貢献している。","romaji":"Tanso sen'i kyōka fukugō zairyō wa kōkūki no keiryōka ni kōken shite iru.","german":"Carbon fiber reinforced composite materials contribute to making aircraft lighter.","furigana":"たんそせんいきょうかふくごうざいりょう|こうくうき|けいりょうか|こうけん"},{"japanese":"このテニスラケットは軽くて丈夫な複合材料でできている。","romaji":"Kono tenisu raketto wa karukute jōbu na fukugō zairyō de dekite iru.","german":"This tennis racket is made of a lightweight and strong composite material.","furigana":"かる|じょうぶ|ふくごうざいりょう"},{"japanese":"鉄筋コンクリートも、広い意味では複合材料の一つだ。","romaji":"Tekkin konkurīto mo, hiroi imi de wa fukugō zairyō no hitotsu da.","german":"Reinforced concrete is also a kind of composite material in the broad sense.","furigana":"てっきん|ひろ|いみ|ふくごうざいりょう|ひと"}],"notes":"複合材料 is a \"composite material\": a material made of two or more components that together have better properties than individually. The best-known example is 炭素繊維強化プラスチック, but in a broader sense reinforced concrete also belongs here. Typical are 複合材料を使う / 採用する and 複合材料製の…; 複合素材 is used similarly but is somewhat more general-language. Technical language of engineering and materials science.","levelNote":"Compound: 複合 (N1) + 材料 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ニッケル基超合金はタービン翼に使用される耐熱材料である。","romaji":"Nikkeruki chōgōkin wa tābin yoku ni shiyō sareru tainetsu zairyō de aru.","german":"Nickel-based superalloys are heat-resistant materials used for turbine blades.","furigana":"きちょうごうきん|よく|しよう|たいねつざいりょう"},{"japanese":"新しい超合金は、千度を超える温度でも強度を保つ。","romaji":"Atarashii chōgōkin wa, sendo o koeru ondo demo kyōdo o tamotsu.","german":"The new superalloy retains its strength even at temperatures exceeding a thousand degrees.","furigana":"あたら|ちょうごうきん|せんど|こ|おんど|きょうど|たも"},{"japanese":"子どものころ、超合金のロボットのおもちゃが宝物だった。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, chōgōkin no robotto no omocha ga takaramono datta.","german":"When I was a child, my superalloy robot toy was my treasure.","furigana":"こ|ちょうごうきん|たからもの"}],"notes":"超合金 is a \"superalloy\", mostly nickel- or cobalt-based, that stays strong even at very high temperatures; it is used in jet engines and gas turbines. Many Japanese people, however, first associate the word with toys: 超合金 is also the name of a well-known series of robot figures made of die-cast metal. Composition: 超 \"super-\" + 合金 \"alloy\". Reading note: in タービン翼 \"turbine blade\", 翼 is read よく in technical language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"水の電気分解で水素燃料を製造する技術が研究されている。","romaji":"Mizu no denki bunkai de suiso nenryō o seizō suru gijutsu ga kenkyū sarete iru.","german":"Technology for producing hydrogen fuel by the electrolysis of water is being researched.","furigana":"みず|でんきぶんかい|すいそねんりょう|せいぞう|ぎじゅつ|けんきゅう"},{"japanese":"理科の実験で水を電気分解すると、水素と酸素が発生した。","romaji":"Rika no jikken de mizu o denki bunkai suru to, suiso to sanso ga hassei shita.","german":"In a science experiment, when we electrolyzed water, hydrogen and oxygen were produced.","furigana":"りか|じっけん|みず|でんきぶんかい|すいそ|さんそ|はっせい"},{"japanese":"アルミニウムは、原料を電気分解して作られる。","romaji":"Aruminiumu wa, genryō o denki bunkai shite tsukurareru.","german":"Aluminum is made by electrolyzing the raw material.","furigana":"げんりょう|でんきぶんかい|つく"}],"notes":"電気分解 is \"electrolysis\": a substance is broken down with electric current. As a transitive する-verb: 水を電気分解する \"to electrolyze water\", passive 電気分解される. In technical texts it is often shortened to 電解 (電解質 \"electrolyte\", 電解液). A classic topic in chemistry class and today frequent in reports about hydrogen production.","levelNote":"Compound: 電気 (N5) + 分解 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"熱交換器は産業プロセスの省エネルギーに不可欠な装置である。","romaji":"Netsu kōkanki wa sangyō purosesu no shō enerugī ni fukaketsu na sōchi de aru.","german":"The heat exchanger is a device indispensable for energy saving in industrial processes.","furigana":"ねつこうかんき|さんぎょう|しょう|ふかけつ|そうち"},{"japanese":"エアコンの熱交換器にほこりがたまると、効率が落ちる。","romaji":"Eakon no netsu kōkanki ni hokori ga tamaru to, kōritsu ga ochiru.","german":"If dust accumulates on the air conditioner's heat exchanger, its efficiency drops.","furigana":"ねつこうかんき|こうりつ|お"},{"japanese":"この換気システムは熱交換器を使って、室内の暖かさを逃がさない。","romaji":"Kono kanki shisutemu wa netsu kōkanki o tsukatte, shitsunai no atatakasa o nigasanai.","german":"This ventilation system uses a heat exchanger so that the warmth of the room is not lost.","furigana":"かんき|ねつこうかんき|つか|しつない|あたた|に"}],"notes":"熱交換器 is a \"heat exchanger\", a device that transfers heat from one medium to another without the two mixing. It is found in air conditioners, boilers, car radiators and ventilation systems. Composition: 熱 \"heat\" + 交換 \"exchange\" + 器 \"device\". For home ventilation you often read 熱交換型換気 or simply 熱交換. Technical language of engineering.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コンデンサの誘電体は電荷の蓄積能力を高める。","romaji":"Kondensa no yūdentai wa denka no chikuseki nōryoku o takameru.","german":"The dielectric of a capacitor increases its ability to store charge.","furigana":"ゆうでんたい|でんか|ちくせきのうりょく|たか"},{"japanese":"電子レンジは、食品のような誘電体に電磁波を当てて温める。","romaji":"Denshi renji wa, shokuhin no yō na yūdentai ni denjiha o atete atatameru.","german":"A microwave oven heats a dielectric such as food by applying electromagnetic waves to it.","furigana":"でんし|しょくひん|ゆうでんたい|でんじは|あ|あたた"},{"japanese":"ガラスやセラミックスは代表的な誘電体だ。","romaji":"Garasu ya seramikkusu wa daihyōteki na yūdentai da.","german":"Glass and ceramics are typical dielectrics.","furigana":"だいひょうてき|ゆうでんたい"}],"notes":"誘電体 is a \"dielectric\": an insulator that can be polarized in an electric field. It matters above all in capacitors (コンデンサ) and in high-frequency engineering. Related terms are 誘電率 \"permittivity\" and 絶縁体 \"insulator\", the more general term without emphasis on polarization. Purely technical.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"圧電素子は機械的変形を電気信号に変換する。","romaji":"Atsuden soshi wa kikaiteki henkei o denki shingō ni henkan suru.","german":"A piezoelectric element converts mechanical deformation into an electrical signal.","furigana":"あつでんそし|きかいてきへんけい|でんきしんごう|へんかん"},{"japanese":"ガスコンロの点火には圧電素子が使われている。","romaji":"Gasu konro no tenka ni wa atsuden soshi ga tsukawarete iru.","german":"A piezoelectric element is used to ignite gas stoves.","furigana":"てんか|あつでんそし|つか"},{"japanese":"床に圧電素子を敷いて、人が歩くときの振動で発電する実験が行われた。","romaji":"Yuka ni atsuden soshi o shiite, hito ga aruku toki no shindō de hatsuden suru jikken ga okonawareta.","german":"An experiment was carried out in which piezoelectric elements were laid on the floor to generate electricity from the vibrations of people walking.","furigana":"ゆか|あつでんそし|し|ひと|ある|しんどう|はつでん|じっけん|おこな"}],"notes":"圧電素子 is a \"piezo element\": a component that generates an electric voltage under pressure and, conversely, vibrates or deforms under voltage. Composition: 圧 \"pressure\" + 電 \"electricity\" + 素子 \"component\". ピエゾ素子 is synonymous; the phenomenon itself is called 圧電効果. Applications are lighters, gas stoves, ultrasonic sensors and small speakers. Technical language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"アインシュタインは光電効果の理論的説明でノーベル賞を受賞した。","romaji":"Ainshutain wa kōden kōka no rironteki setsumei de nōberu shō o jushō shita.","german":"Einstein received the Nobel Prize for his theoretical explanation of the photoelectric effect.","furigana":"こうでんこうか|りろんてきせつめい|しょう|じゅしょう"},{"japanese":"光電効果では、光の強さではなく振動数によって電子が飛び出すかどうかが決まる。","romaji":"Kōden kōka de wa, hikari no tsuyosa de wa naku shindōsū ni yotte denshi ga tobidasu ka dō ka ga kimaru.","german":"In the photoelectric effect, it is the frequency, not the intensity, of the light that determines whether electrons are ejected.","furigana":"こうでんこうか|ひかり|つよ|しんどうすう|でんし|と|だ|き"},{"japanese":"太陽電池は光電効果を利用して電気を作っている。","romaji":"Taiyō denchi wa kōden kōka o riyō shite denki o tsukutte iru.","german":"Solar cells produce electricity using the photoelectric effect.","furigana":"たいようでんち|こうでんこうか|りよう|でんき|つく"}],"notes":"光電効果 is the \"photoelectric effect\": light releases electrons from a material. Composition: 光 \"light\" + 電 \"electricity\" + 効果 \"effect\". It is famous through Einstein's light quantum hypothesis (光量子仮説); as the internal photoelectric effect (内部光電効果) it is used in photocells and solar cells. A purely technical physics term.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"超音波診断は非侵襲的で安全な画像検査法である。","romaji":"Chōonpa shindan wa hishinshūteki de anzen na gazō kensahō de aru.","german":"Ultrasound diagnosis is a non-invasive and safe imaging examination method.","furigana":"ちょうおんぱしんだん|ひしんしゅうてき|あんぜん|がぞうけんさほう"},{"japanese":"妊婦健診では超音波診断で赤ちゃんの成長を確認する。","romaji":"Ninpu kenshin de wa chōonpa shindan de akachan no seichō o kakunin suru.","german":"At prenatal checkups, the baby's growth is confirmed by ultrasound diagnosis.","furigana":"にんぷけんしん|ちょうおんぱしんだん|あか|せいちょう|かくにん"},{"japanese":"超音波診断の結果、胆石が見つかった。","romaji":"Chōonpa shindan no kekka, tanseki ga mitsukatta.","german":"Gallstones were found as a result of the ultrasound diagnosis.","furigana":"ちょうおんぱしんだん|けっか|たんせき|み"}],"notes":"超音波診断 is \"ultrasound diagnostics\". In everyday speech people mostly say エコー or エコー検査, and in hospitals also 超音波検査; 超音波診断 sounds more formal and appears in technical texts and device names (超音波診断装置). Typical areas of use are prenatal care, abdominal organs and the heart (心エコー). 超音波 alone is \"ultrasound\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"磁気共鳴画像法は軟部組織の描出に優れている。","romaji":"Jiki kyōmei gazōhō wa nanbu soshiki no byōshutsu ni sugurete iru.","german":"Magnetic resonance imaging is excellent at depicting soft tissue.","furigana":"じききょうめいがぞうほう|なんぶそしき|びょうしゅつ|すぐ"},{"japanese":"磁気共鳴画像で脳に小さな異常が見つかった。","romaji":"Jiki kyōmei gazō de nō ni chiisana ijō ga mitsukatta.","german":"A small abnormality was found in my brain on the MRI.","furigana":"じききょうめいがぞう|のう|ちい|いじょう|み"},{"japanese":"磁気共鳴画像の検査中は大きな音がするので、耳栓を渡された。","romaji":"Jiki kyōmei gazō no kensachū wa ōki na oto ga suru node, mimisen o watasareta.","german":"The MRI exam makes loud noises, so I was handed earplugs.","furigana":"じききょうめいがぞう|けんさちゅう|おお|おと|みみせん|わた"}],"notes":"磁気共鳴画像 is the image or procedure of \"magnetic resonance imaging\"; the procedure is precisely called 磁気共鳴画像法. In everyday speech practically everyone says MRI or MRI検査; the long Japanese form is read almost only in technical literature and official texts. Composition: 磁気 \"magnetism\" + 共鳴 \"resonance\" + 画像 \"image\". Unlike CT, the method works without X-rays.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"陽電子断層撮影はがん細胞の代謝活動を検出する。","romaji":"Yōdenshi dansō satsuei wa gan saibō no taisha katsudō o kenshutsu suru.","german":"Positron emission tomography detects the metabolic activity of cancer cells.","furigana":"ようでんしだんそうさつえい|さいぼう|たいしゃかつどう|けんしゅつ"},{"japanese":"人間ドックで陽電子断層撮影を受けた。","romaji":"Ningen dokku de yōdenshi dansō satsuei o uketa.","german":"I had a PET scan at my comprehensive medical checkup.","furigana":"にんげん|ようでんしだんそうさつえい|う"},{"japanese":"陽電子断層撮影では、ブドウ糖に似た薬を注射してから撮影する。","romaji":"Yōdenshi dansō satsuei de wa, budōtō ni nita kusuri o chūsha shite kara satsuei suru.","german":"In PET imaging, a drug similar to glucose is injected before the scan.","furigana":"ようでんしだんそうさつえい|とう|に|くすり|ちゅうしゃ|さつえい"}],"notes":"陽電子断層撮影 denotes \"positron emission tomography\"; the full technical name is 陽電子放射断層撮影. In everyday speech people almost always say PET検査 or PET-CT. Composition: 陽電子 \"positron\" + 断層撮影 \"cross-sectional imaging, tomography\". The method is used mainly to look for cancer, because tumor cells take up a lot of sugar.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"薬物動態学は薬が体内でどのように吸収・分布・代謝・排泄されるかを研究する。","romaji":"Yakubutsu dōtaigaku wa kusuri ga tainai de dono yō ni kyūshū bunpu taisha haisetsu sareru ka o kenkyū suru.","german":"Pharmacokinetics studies how a drug is absorbed, distributed, metabolized and excreted in the body.","furigana":"やくぶつどうたいがく|くすり|たいない|きゅうしゅう|ぶんぷ|たいしゃ||けんきゅう"},{"japanese":"高齢者では腎機能の低下により薬物動態が変化しやすい。","romaji":"Kōreisha de wa jinkinō no teika ni yori yakubutsu dōtai ga henka shiyasui.","german":"In the elderly, pharmacokinetics easily changes because of declining kidney function.","furigana":"こうれいしゃ|じんきのう|ていか|やくぶつどうたい|へんか"},{"japanese":"新薬の治験では、まず健康な人で薬物動態を調べる。","romaji":"Shin'yaku no chiken de wa, mazu kenkō na hito de yakubutsu dōtai o shiraberu.","german":"In clinical trials of new drugs, pharmacokinetics is first examined in healthy people.","furigana":"しんやく|ちけん|けんこう|ひと|やくぶつどうたい|しら"}],"notes":"薬物動態 denotes \"pharmacokinetics\": how a drug is absorbed, distributed, metabolized and excreted in the body. The associated science is called 薬物動態学. Typical combinations are 薬物動態を調べる / 評価する. Distinguish it from 薬力学 \"pharmacodynamics\", i.e. what the drug does in the body. Purely technical.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"電子回路の設計にはシミュレーションソフトが不可欠である。","romaji":"Denshi kairo no sekkei ni wa shimyurēshon sofuto ga fukaketsu de aru.","german":"Simulation software is indispensable for designing electronic circuits.","furigana":"でんしかいろ|せっけい|ふかけつ"},{"japanese":"趣味で簡単な電子回路を組んで、ラジオを作った。","romaji":"Shumi de kantan na denshi kairo o kunde, rajio o tsukutta.","german":"As a hobby, I built a simple electronic circuit and made a radio.","furigana":"しゅみ|かんたん|でんしかいろ|く|つく"},{"japanese":"水に濡れて電子回路がショートし、スマホが動かなくなった。","romaji":"Mizu ni nurete denshi kairo ga shōto shi, sumaho ga ugokanaku natta.","german":"The electronic circuit shorted after getting wet, and my smartphone stopped working.","furigana":"みず|ぬ|でんしかいろ|うご"}],"notes":"電子回路 is an \"electronic circuit\" with components such as transistors, resistors and capacitors. Typical combinations are 電子回路を設計する / 組む and 電子回路図 \"circuit diagram\". Distinguish 電気回路, the more general \"electric circuit\", and 基板, the circuit board on which the circuit sits. Technical language, but familiar among hobbyists and students.","levelNote":"Compound: 電子 (N4) + 回路 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"サイバーセキュリティの強化は企業の重要課題である。","romaji":"Saibā sekyuriti no kyōka wa kigyō no jūyō kadai de aru.","german":"Strengthening cybersecurity is an important issue for companies.","furigana":"きょうか|きぎょう|じゅうようかだい"},{"japanese":"政府はサイバーセキュリティの専門家を増やす方針だ。","romaji":"Seifu wa saibā sekyuriti no senmonka o fuyasu hōshin da.","german":"The government plans to increase the number of cybersecurity experts.","furigana":"せいふ|せんもんか|ふ|ほうしん"},{"japanese":"社員向けにサイバーセキュリティの研修が行われた。","romaji":"Shainmuke ni saibā sekyuriti no kenshū ga okonawareta.","german":"Cybersecurity training was held for employees.","furigana":"しゃいんむ|けんしゅう|おこな"}],"notes":"サイバーセキュリティ is \"cybersecurity\", the protection of computers, networks and data from attacks. The spelling with a long vowel, サイバーセキュリティー, is equally common. Typical combinations are サイバーセキュリティを強化する and サイバーセキュリティ対策. Related are サイバー攻撃 \"cyberattack\" and 情報セキュリティ \"information security\". Language of the media, government offices and companies.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"生分解性プラスチックは環境負荷の低減に貢献する。","romaji":"Sei bunkaisei purasuchikku wa kankyō fuka no teigen ni kōken suru.","german":"Biodegradable plastics contribute to reducing environmental impact.","furigana":"せいぶんかいせい|かんきょうふか|ていげん|こうけん"},{"japanese":"このストローは生分解性の素材でできている。","romaji":"Kono sutorō wa seibunkaisei no sozai de dekite iru.","german":"This straw is made of a biodegradable material.","furigana":"せいぶんかいせい|そざい"},{"japanese":"生分解性といっても、海の中ではなかなか分解されないものもある。","romaji":"Seibunkaisei to itte mo, umi no naka de wa nakanaka bunkai sarenai mono mo aru.","german":"Even if something is called biodegradable, some things are not readily broken down in the sea.","furigana":"せいぶんかいせい|うみ|なか|ぶんかい"}],"notes":"生分解性 is \"biodegradability\": the property of a substance to be broken down by microorganisms into water and carbon dioxide. It mostly stands attributively before a noun, directly or with の: 生分解性プラスチック, 生分解性の素材 \"biodegradable material\". Composition: 生 \"biological\" + 分解 \"decomposition\" + 性 \"property, -ability\". A technical word, but frequent in environmental reports.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"炭素繊維は鋼鉄の約十倍の強度を持つ軽量素材である。","romaji":"Tanso sen'i wa kōtetsu no yaku jū bai no kyōdo o motsu keiryō sozai de aru.","german":"Carbon fiber is a lightweight material with about ten times the strength of steel.","furigana":"たんそせんい|こうてつ|やくじゅうばい|きょうど|も|けいりょうそざい"},{"japanese":"このロードバイクのフレームは炭素繊維でできている。","romaji":"Kono rōdo baiku no furēmu wa tanso sen'i de dekite iru.","german":"The frame of this road bike is made of carbon fiber.","furigana":"たんそせんい"},{"japanese":"炭素繊維の需要は、自動車や風力発電の分野でも伸びている。","romaji":"Tanso sen'i no juyō wa, jidōsha ya fūryoku hatsuden no bun'ya demo nobite iru.","german":"Demand for carbon fiber is also growing in the fields of automobiles and wind power generation.","furigana":"たんそせんい|じゅよう|じどうしゃ|ふうりょくはつでん|ぶんや|の"}],"notes":"炭素繊維 is \"carbon fiber\", very light and at the same time extremely strong. In advertising and everyday speech people often say カーボン or カーボンファイバー (カーボン製の自転車). Typical are 炭素繊維強化プラスチック \"carbon-fiber-reinforced plastic\" and 炭素繊維製の…. Since Japanese manufacturers are world leaders, the word often appears in business news.","levelNote":"Compound: 炭素 (N1) + 繊維 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"プラズマは固体・液体・気体に次ぐ物質の第四の状態である。","romaji":"Purazuma wa kotai ekitai kitai ni tsugu busshitsu no dai yon no jōtai de aru.","german":"Plasma is the fourth state of matter after solid, liquid and gas.","furigana":"こたい|えきたい|きたい|つ|ぶっしつ|だいよん|じょうたい"},{"japanese":"雷やオーロラは自然界で見られるプラズマの例だ。","romaji":"Kaminari ya ōrora wa shizenkai de mirareru purazuma no rei da.","german":"Lightning and auroras are examples of plasma seen in nature.","furigana":"かみなり|しぜんかい|み|れい"},{"japanese":"核融合炉では、超高温のプラズマを磁場で閉じ込める。","romaji":"Kakuyūgōro de wa, chōkōon no purazuma o jiba de tojikomeru.","german":"In a fusion reactor, ultra-high-temperature plasma is confined with a magnetic field.","furigana":"かくゆうごうろ|ちょうこうおん|じば|と|こ"}],"notes":"プラズマ is \"plasma\" in the physical sense: an ionized gas, often called the fourth state of matter. Natural examples are lightning, auroras and the sun; technically one speaks of プラズマ切断 \"plasma cutting\" or 核融合プラズマ \"fusion plasma\". Note: blood plasma is normally called 血漿 in Japanese, not プラズマ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"暗黒物質は宇宙の質量の大部分を占めると考えられている。","romaji":"Ankoku busshitsu wa uchū no shitsuryō no daibubun o shimeru to kangaerarete iru.","german":"Dark matter is thought to make up most of the mass of the universe.","furigana":"あんこくぶっしつ|うちゅう|しつりょう|だいぶぶん|し|かんが"},{"japanese":"暗黒物質の正体は、今もまだ分かっていない。","romaji":"Ankoku busshitsu no shōtai wa, ima mo mada wakatte inai.","german":"The true nature of dark matter is still not known today.","furigana":"あんこくぶっしつ|しょうたい|いま|わ"},{"japanese":"地下深くの実験施設で、暗黒物質を検出しようとしている。","romaji":"Chika fukaku no jikken shisetsu de, ankoku busshitsu o kenshutsu shiyō to shite iru.","german":"At an experimental facility deep underground, researchers are trying to detect dark matter.","furigana":"ちかふか|じっけんしせつ|あんこくぶっしつ|けんしゅつ"}],"notes":"暗黒物質 is \"dark matter\": invisible matter that makes itself noticeable only through its gravity. In popular media people often say ダークマター; 暗黒物質 is the somewhat more formal, technical expression. The counterpart 暗黒エネルギー or ダークエネルギー is \"dark energy\". Typical expressions are 暗黒物質の正体 \"what dark matter actually is\" and 暗黒物質を検出する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"重力波の直接検出はアインシュタインの予言を裏付けた。","romaji":"Jūryokuha no chokusetsu kenshutsu wa Ainshutain no yogen o urazuketa.","german":"The direct detection of gravitational waves confirmed Einstein's prediction.","furigana":"じゅうりょくは|ちょくせつけんしゅつ|よげん|うらづ"},{"japanese":"二つのブラックホールが合体したときの重力波が観測された。","romaji":"Futatsu no burakkuhōru ga gattai shita toki no jūryokuha ga kansoku sareta.","german":"Gravitational waves from the merger of two black holes were observed.","furigana":"ふた|がったい|じゅうりょくは|かんそく"},{"japanese":"重力波の観測によって、宇宙を見る新しい方法が生まれた。","romaji":"Jūryokuha no kansoku ni yotte, uchū o miru atarashii hōhō ga umareta.","german":"The observation of gravitational waves has created a new way of looking at the universe.","furigana":"じゅうりょくは|かんそく|うちゅう|み|あたら|ほうほう|う"}],"notes":"重力波 are \"gravitational waves\": distortions of spacetime that propagate at the speed of light, for example when two black holes merge. Typical combinations are 重力波を観測する / 検出する and 重力波望遠鏡. For this purpose Japan operates the underground detector KAGRA in Gifu. In meteorology, 重力波 also denotes \"gravity waves\" in the atmosphere.","levelNote":"Compound: 重力 (N2) + 波 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"学士号だけでは研究職に就くのは難しい。","romaji":"Gakushi-gō dake de wa kenkyūshoku ni tsuku no wa muzukashii.","german":"It is difficult to get a research position with only a bachelor's degree.","furigana":"がくしごう|けんきゅうしょく|つ|むずか"},{"japanese":"彼女は文学の学士を取ったあと、出版社に就職した。","romaji":"Kanojo wa bungaku no gakushi o totta ato, shuppansha ni shūshoku shita.","german":"After earning a bachelor's degree in literature, she got a job at a publishing company.","furigana":"かのじょ|ぶんがく|がくし|と|しゅっぱんしゃ|しゅうしょく"},{"japanese":"医学部には、学士を持つ社会人が入学できる制度がある。","romaji":"Igakubu ni wa, gakushi o motsu shakaijin ga nyūgaku dekiru seido ga aru.","german":"The medical school has a system that allows working adults who hold a bachelor's degree to enroll.","furigana":"いがくぶ|がくし|も|しゃかいじん|にゅうがく|せいど"}],"notes":"学士 denotes the \"bachelor's\" degree or a person with a university degree. The degree itself is called 学士号, the course of study 学士課程; above it come 修士 \"master's\" and 博士 \"doctorate\". On a résumé one writes, for example, 学士(経済学). In everyday speech people usually say 大卒 or 大学卒業 instead of 学士; 学士 sounds official.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その学説は後の研究によって覆された。","romaji":"Sono gakusetsu wa nochi no kenkyū ni yotte kutsugaesareta.","german":"That theory was overturned by later research.","furigana":"がくせつ|のち|けんきゅう|くつがえ"},{"japanese":"この問題については、学者の間で学説が分かれている。","romaji":"Kono mondai ni tsuite wa, gakusha no aida de gakusetsu ga wakarete iru.","german":"On this problem, scholars are divided in their theories.","furigana":"もんだい|がくしゃ|あいだ|がくせつ|わ"},{"japanese":"彼は当時としては大胆な学説を唱えた。","romaji":"Kare wa tōji to shite wa daitan na gakusetsu o tonaeta.","german":"He advanced a theory that was bold for its time.","furigana":"かれ|とうじ|だいたん|がくせつ|とな"}],"notes":"学説 is an \"academic theory, doctrine\", as represented by a scholar or a school. Typical combinations are 学説を唱える \"to advance a theory\", 学説が分かれる \"scholarly opinions are divided\" and 新しい学説; the prevailing view is called 通説. Compared with 理論, 学説 emphasizes the position that is held; 仮説 is a still unproven \"hypothesis\". Written language.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"京都学派の哲学は独自の発展を遂げた。","romaji":"Kyōto gakuha no tetsugaku wa dokuji no hatten o togeta.","german":"The philosophy of the Kyoto School achieved its own development.","furigana":"きょうとがくは|てつがく|どくじ|はってん|と"},{"japanese":"経済学には、ケインズ学派をはじめ多くの学派がある。","romaji":"Keizaigaku ni wa, Keinzu gakuha o hajime ōku no gakuha ga aru.","german":"In economics there are many schools, starting with the Keynesian school.","furigana":"けいざいがく|がくは|おお|がくは"},{"japanese":"彼はどの学派にも属さない独自の立場をとった。","romaji":"Kare wa dono gakuha ni mo zokusanai dokuji no tachiba o totta.","german":"He took a unique position, belonging to no school of thought.","furigana":"かれ|がくは|ぞく|どくじ|たちば"}],"notes":"学派 is a \"school\" in the sense of a scholarly or philosophical movement whose representatives share a common doctrine. It mostly stands after a place or person name: 京都学派, フランクフルト学派, ケインズ学派. Typical combinations are 学派に属する and 学派を形成する. Distinguish 流派, a school in art and martial arts (such as ikebana), and 派閥, a political \"faction, clique\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"幅広い教養を身につけることが大切だ。","romaji":"Habahiroi kyōyō o mi ni tsukeru koto ga taisetsu da.","german":"It is important to acquire a broad general education.","furigana":"はばひろ|きょうよう|み|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"彼女は教養があって、話していて楽しい。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kyōyō ga atte, hanashite ite tanoshii.","german":"She is cultured, and it is fun to talk with her.","furigana":"かのじょ|きょうよう|はな|たの"},{"japanese":"一年生のときは教養科目を中心に履修した。","romaji":"Ichinensei no toki wa kyōyō kamoku o chūshin ni rishū shita.","german":"In my first year, I mainly took liberal arts courses.","furigana":"いちねんせい|きょうようかもく|ちゅうしん|りしゅう"}],"notes":"教養 is broad cultural and intellectual education that makes a person \"cultivated\" - not specialized knowledge and not the formal degree (that is 学歴). Typical are 教養がある／ない, 教養のある人 and 教養を身につける. At universities, 一般教養 or 教養科目 denotes the general-education basic courses of the first years. Compared with 知識 (\"knowledge\"), 教養 also emphasizes judgment, taste and manners.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"音楽の素養がある人は語学の習得も早い。","romaji":"Ongaku no soyō ga aru hito wa gogaku no shūtoku mo hayai.","german":"People with a grounding in music also learn languages quickly.","furigana":"おんがく|そよう|ひと|ごがく|しゅうとく|はや"},{"japanese":"祖母は茶道や書道の素養がある人だった。","romaji":"Sobo wa sadō ya shodō no soyō ga aru hito datta.","german":"My grandmother was a person with accomplishment in tea ceremony and calligraphy.","furigana":"そぼ|さどう|しょどう|そよう|ひと"},{"japanese":"この仕事には統計学の素養が求められる。","romaji":"Kono shigoto ni wa tōkeigaku no soyō ga motomerareru.","german":"This job requires a grounding in statistics.","furigana":"しごと|とうけいがく|そよう|もと"}],"notes":"素養 is a foundation in a particular field acquired through practice or instruction, often since childhood: 音楽の素養, 漢文の素養, 数学の素養. Almost always in the frames 〜の素養がある／ない or 素養を身につける. Unlike 教養 (\"broad general education\"), 素養 refers to a concrete subject or art; unlike 素質 (\"innate aptitude, talent\"), it is learned. Rather elevated, written-leaning style.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今学期は五科目を履修する予定だ。","romaji":"Kongakki wa go-kamoku o rishū suru yotei da.","german":"I plan to take five subjects this semester.","furigana":"こんがっき|ごかもく|りしゅう|よてい"},{"japanese":"履修登録の締め切りは来週の金曜日です。","romaji":"Rishū tōroku no shimekiri wa raishū no kin'yōbi desu.","german":"The deadline for course registration is next Friday.","furigana":"りしゅうとうろく|し|き|らいしゅう|きんようび"},{"japanese":"教職課程を履修すれば、教員免許が取れる。","romaji":"Kyōshoku katei o rishū sureba, kyōin menkyo ga toreru.","german":"If you complete the teacher-training course, you can obtain a teaching license.","furigana":"きょうしょくかてい|りしゅう|きょういんめんきょ|と"}],"notes":"履修 is the technical term in schools and universities for enrolling in and completing courses: 科目を履修する (transitive, with を). Common compounds are 履修登録 (\"course registration\"), 履修科目 and 履修単位. In contrast to 受講 (\"to attend a class\"), 履修 means the official enrollment with credit for assessed work; 修了 is the completion of an entire program. In everyday speech people often just say 授業を取る.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"社会人でも聴講生として大学の授業を受けられる。","romaji":"Shakaijin demo chōkōsei toshite daigaku no jugyō o ukerareru.","german":"Even working adults can attend university classes as auditors.","furigana":"しゃかいじん|ちょうこうせい|だいがく|じゅぎょう|う"},{"japanese":"この公開講座は誰でも無料で聴講できます。","romaji":"Kono kōkai kōza wa dare demo muryō de chōkō dekimasu.","german":"Anyone can attend this public lecture series free of charge.","furigana":"こうかいこうざ|だれ|むりょう|ちょうこう"},{"japanese":"先生にお願いして、大学院の講義を聴講させてもらった。","romaji":"Sensei ni onegai shite, daigakuin no kōgi o chōkō sasete moratta.","german":"I asked the professor and was allowed to sit in on a graduate-school lecture.","furigana":"せんせい|ねが|だいがくいん|こうぎ|ちょうこう"}],"notes":"聴講 means to listen to a lecture or talk, especially without being formally enrolled: 講義を聴講する. Very common is 聴講生 (\"auditor\"); on posters you read 聴講無料 (\"admission free\") or 聴講料. A regular student who attends a course for credit would 受講する or 履修する; 聴講 emphasizes mere listening.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"経済的な理由で退学を余儀なくされた。","romaji":"Keizai-teki na riyū de taigaku o yogi naku sareta.","german":"I was forced to drop out for financial reasons.","furigana":"けいざいてき|りゆう|たいがく|よぎ"},{"japanese":"彼は校則違反で退学処分を受けた。","romaji":"Kare wa kōsoku ihan de taigaku shobun o uketa.","german":"He was expelled for violating school rules.","furigana":"かれ|こうそくいはん|たいがくしょぶん|う"},{"japanese":"大学を退学して、自分で会社を始めた。","romaji":"Daigaku o taigaku shite, jibun de kaisha o hajimeta.","german":"He dropped out of university and started his own company.","furigana":"だいがく|たいがく|じぶん|かいしゃ|はじ"}],"notes":"退学 is the final departure from a school or university without a degree, whether voluntary or as a punishment: 大学を退学する (intransitive する-verb; を marks the place being left). The punishment is called 退学処分 (\"expulsion from school\"), passive 退学になる; the form is the 退学届. Colloquially people often say 中退 (from 中途退学). Distinguish it from 休学 (\"leave of absence\", you stay enrolled) and 卒業 (\"graduation\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"病気療養のため一年間休学した。","romaji":"Byōki ryōyō no tame ichi-nenkan kyūgaku shita.","german":"I took a year's leave of absence to recover from an illness.","furigana":"びょうきりょうよう|いちねんかんきゅうがく"},{"japanese":"留学のために大学を一年休学するつもりだ。","romaji":"Ryūgaku no tame ni daigaku o ichi-nen kyūgaku suru tsumori da.","german":"I intend to take a year off from university to study abroad.","furigana":"りゅうがく|だいがく|いちねんきゅうがく"},{"japanese":"休学中の学生も図書館は利用できます。","romaji":"Kyūgaku-chū no gakusei mo toshokan wa riyō dekimasu.","german":"Students on a leave of absence can also use the library.","furigana":"きゅうがくちゅう|がくせい|としょかん|りよう"}],"notes":"休学 is the official, time-limited interruption of school attendance or studies during which you remain enrolled: 大学を一年休学する. You submit a 休学届, are then 休学中 and later return with 復学. In contrast to 退学 (\"final withdrawal\"), 休学 is temporary; for being absent on individual days, one says 欠席 or 学校を休む instead.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"現在も大学院に在籍している。","romaji":"Genzai mo daigakuin ni zaiseki shite iru.","german":"I am still enrolled in graduate school now.","furigana":"げんざい|だいがくいん|ざいせき"},{"japanese":"彼はこの会社に十年間在籍していた。","romaji":"Kare wa kono kaisha ni jū-nenkan zaiseki shite ita.","german":"He was on the staff of this company for ten years.","furigana":"かれ|かいしゃ|じゅうねんかんざいせき"},{"japanese":"このクラブには約五十人の選手が在籍している。","romaji":"Kono kurabu ni wa yaku gojū-nin no senshu ga zaiseki shite iru.","german":"About fifty players are on this club's roster.","furigana":"やくごじゅうにん|せんしゅ|ざいせき"}],"notes":"在籍 means to be officially listed in a register, at a school but also at a company, team or club: 〜に在籍する (intransitive, with に). Common are 在籍者数, 在籍期間 and 在籍証明書. 在学 refers only to schools and universities, 所属 emphasizes belonging to a group; 在籍 highlights the formal status.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"学籍番号を忘れずに記入してください。","romaji":"Gakuseki bangō o wasurezu ni kinyū shite kudasai.","german":"Please don't forget to enter your student ID number.","furigana":"がくせきばんごう|わす|きにゅう"},{"japanese":"休学中でも学籍は残ります。","romaji":"Kyūgaku-chū demo gakuseki wa nokorimasu.","german":"Your student status remains even during a leave of absence.","furigana":"きゅうがくちゅう|がくせき|のこ"},{"japanese":"授業料を長く滞納すると、学籍を失うことがある。","romaji":"Jugyōryō o nagaku tainō suru to, gakuseki o ushinau koto ga aru.","german":"If you leave tuition unpaid for a long time, you may lose your student status.","furigana":"じゅぎょうりょう|なが|たいのう|がくせき|うしな"}],"notes":"学籍 is the official entry as a pupil or student in a school's register, that is, student status itself. It most often appears in 学籍番号 (\"student ID number\") and 学籍簿 (\"student register\"). One says 学籍を置く (\"to be enrolled\") and 学籍を失う; the process of being enrolled, on the other hand, is expressed with 在籍する. A purely administrative term.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は生涯を学究に捧げた。","romaji":"Kare wa shōgai o gakkyū ni sasageta.","german":"He devoted his whole life to scholarship.","furigana":"かれ|しょうがい|がっきゅう|ささ"},{"japanese":"兄は典型的な学究肌で、出世にはまったく関心がない。","romaji":"Ani wa tenkei-teki na gakkyū-hada de, shusse ni wa mattaku kanshin ga nai.","german":"My older brother is the typical scholarly type and has no interest in career advancement at all.","furigana":"あに|てんけいてき|がっきゅうはだ|しゅっせ|かんしん"},{"japanese":"退職後も学究の徒として研究を続けている。","romaji":"Taishoku-go mo gakkyū no to toshite kenkyū o tsuzukete iru.","german":"Even after retirement, he continues his research as a man of learning.","furigana":"たいしょくご|がっきゅう|と|けんきゅう|つづ"}],"notes":"学究 refers to a person who devotes himself entirely to study and research, and also to this devotion itself. Typical are 学究肌 (\"scholarly temperament\"), 学究の徒 (\"disciple of learning\") and 学究生活. Unlike 学者 (\"scholar\" as a profession), 学究 emphasizes unworldly devotion, sometimes with a slight undertone of being out of touch with real life. Elevated, literary style.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"碩学の先生に師事できたことは幸運だった。","romaji":"Sekigaku no sensei ni shiji dekita koto wa kōun datta.","german":"It was fortunate that I could study under a great scholar.","furigana":"せきがく|せんせい|しじ|こううん"},{"japanese":"彼は東洋史の碩学として知られている。","romaji":"Kare wa tōyōshi no sekigaku toshite shirarete iru.","german":"He is known as an eminent authority on Oriental history.","furigana":"かれ|とうようし|せきがく|し"},{"japanese":"記念講演には当代きっての碩学が招かれた。","romaji":"Kinen kōen ni wa tōdai kitte no sekigaku ga manekareta.","german":"One of the foremost scholars of our time was invited to give the commemorative lecture.","furigana":"きねんこうえん|とうだい|せきがく|まね"}],"notes":"碩学 is a very elevated, respectful word for a scholar of exceptional learning, roughly \"an eminent authority\". It appears mainly in obituaries, commemorative volumes and introductions: 当代きっての碩学, 〜の碩学として知られる, 碩学の士. 博学, by contrast, describes the quality (\"well-read, learned\"), and 学者 is the neutral term for the profession. Rare in everyday speech.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"博学多才な人物として知られている。","romaji":"Hakugaku tasai na jinbutsu toshite shirarete iru.","german":"He is known as a person of wide learning and many talents.","furigana":"はくがくたさい|じんぶつ|し"},{"japanese":"祖父は博学で、何を聞いても答えてくれた。","romaji":"Sofu wa hakugaku de, nani o kiite mo kotaete kureta.","german":"My grandfather was very learned and could answer any question I asked.","furigana":"そふ|はくがく|なに|き|こた"},{"japanese":"彼の博学ぶりには教授たちも驚いた。","romaji":"Kare no hakugaku-buri ni wa kyōju-tachi mo odoroita.","german":"Even the professors were amazed at his erudition.","furigana":"かれ|はくがく|きょうじゅ|おどろ"}],"notes":"博学 means \"broadly learned, very well-read\" and is used as a noun and as a な-adjective: 博学な人, 博学ぶり (\"his erudition\"). A set phrase is 博学多才 (\"learned and versatilely gifted\"). Almost synonymous is 博識, which emphasizes more the amount of knowledge; colloquially one says 物知り. 碩学, on the other hand, refers to the person himself and is even more elevated.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"蒙昧な時代から啓蒙の時代へと移行した。","romaji":"Mōmai na jidai kara keimō no jidai e to ikō shita.","german":"The era shifted from an age of ignorance to an age of enlightenment.","furigana":"もうまい|じだい|けいもう|じだい|いこう"},{"japanese":"民衆を無知蒙昧と決めつけるのは傲慢だ。","romaji":"Minshū o muchi mōmai to kimetsukeru no wa gōman da.","german":"It is arrogant to dismiss the common people as ignorant and backward.","furigana":"みんしゅう|むちもうまい|き|ごうまん"},{"japanese":"当時の人々は蒙昧で、病気を悪霊のせいだと信じていた。","romaji":"Tōji no hitobito wa mōmai de, byōki o akuryō no sei da to shinjite ita.","german":"People at that time were unenlightened and believed that illnesses were caused by evil spirits.","furigana":"とうじ|ひとびと|もうまい|びょうき|あくりょう|しん"}],"notes":"蒙昧 means \"ignorant, unenlightened\" and is used mainly as a な-adjective; its counterpart is 啓蒙 (\"enlightenment\", literally \"to open up the darkness\"). Most common is the set phrase 無知蒙昧 (\"utterly ignorant and unenlightened\"). The word is literary and strongly pejorative; applied to specific people it sounds condescending. In everyday speech one rather says 無知.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"啓蒙思想は近代日本に大きな影響を与えた。","romaji":"Keimō shisō wa kindai Nihon ni ōkina eikyō o ataeta.","german":"Enlightenment thought had a great influence on modern Japan.","furigana":"けいもうしそう|きんだいにほん|おお|えいきょう|あた"},{"japanese":"彼女は長年、環境保護の啓蒙活動に取り組んでいる。","romaji":"Kanojo wa naganen, kankyō hogo no keimō katsudō ni torikunde iru.","german":"For many years she has been engaged in educational campaigns for environmental protection.","furigana":"かのじょ|ながねん|かんきょうほご|けいもうかつどう|と|く"},{"japanese":"一般の読者を啓蒙するために、やさしい解説書を書いた。","romaji":"Ippan no dokusha o keimō suru tame ni, yasashii kaisetsusho o kaita.","german":"He wrote an easy-to-follow guidebook to educate the general reader.","furigana":"いっぱん|どくしゃ|けいもう|かいせつしょ|か"}],"notes":"啓蒙 is enlightenment in the sense of conveying knowledge to the uninformed; as a する-verb it is transitive: 市民を啓蒙する. Common are 啓蒙活動 (\"educational campaign\"), 啓蒙書 (\"popular-science book\") and, historically, 啓蒙思想 and 啓蒙主義 for the European Enlightenment. Because 啓蒙 can mean condescendingly \"enlightening the ignorant\", government agencies today often prefer 啓発 (\"raising awareness\"). The counterpart is 蒙昧.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人格の陶冶こそ教育の本来の目的である。","romaji":"Jinkaku no tōya koso kyōiku no honrai no mokuteki de aru.","german":"The cultivation of character is the true purpose of education.","furigana":"じんかく|とうや|きょういく|ほんらい|もくてき"},{"japanese":"厳しい修行によって心身を陶冶した。","romaji":"Kibishii shugyō ni yotte shinshin o tōya shita.","german":"Through strict training he forged both body and mind.","furigana":"きび|しゅぎょう|しんしん|とうや"},{"japanese":"この学校はスポーツを通じた人格の陶冶を重視している。","romaji":"Kono gakkō wa supōtsu o tsūjita jinkaku no tōya o jūshi shite iru.","german":"This school places great emphasis on the cultivation of character through sports.","furigana":"がっこう|つう|じんかく|とうや|じゅうし"}],"notes":"陶冶 literally comes from \"pottery making and metal casting\" and figuratively means the shaping of character and abilities. It is a strongly literary term from pedagogy and philosophy, used almost only in 人格の陶冶 or 人格を陶冶する (transitive). More everyday words are 人格形成 or 育成; 涵養 emphasizes rather the gradual growth of a quality.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"道徳心の涵養に努めることが求められる。","romaji":"Dōtoku-shin no kan'yō ni tsutomeru koto ga motomerareru.","german":"We are expected to strive to cultivate a moral sense.","furigana":"どうとくしん|かんよう|つと|もと"},{"japanese":"読書を通じて豊かな感性を涵養したい。","romaji":"Dokusho o tsūjite yutaka na kansei o kan'yō shitai.","german":"I want to cultivate a rich sensibility through reading.","furigana":"どくしょ|つう|ゆた|かんせい|かんよう"},{"japanese":"森林には水源を涵養する働きがある。","romaji":"Shinrin ni wa suigen o kan'yō suru hataraki ga aru.","german":"Forests have the function of recharging water sources.","furigana":"しんりん|すいげん|かんよう|はたら"}],"notes":"涵養 means to let a quality or ability grow slowly and naturally, like water seeping into the ground. Typical of curricula and official documents: 道徳心の涵養, 豊かな情操を涵養する (transitive). A second meaning is groundwater recharge: 水源涵養, 水源涵養林 (\"water-source protection forest\"). In contrast to 育成 or 養成 (\"to train deliberately\"), 涵養 emphasizes gradual growth.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"恩師の薫陶を受けて立派な学者になった。","romaji":"Onshi no kuntō o ukete rippa na gakusha ni natta.","german":"Under the guidance of his revered teacher, he became an excellent scholar.","furigana":"おんし|くんとう|う|りっぱ|がくしゃ"},{"japanese":"先生のご薫陶のおかげで、今日の私があります。","romaji":"Sensei no go-kuntō no okage de, kyō no watashi ga arimasu.","german":"I owe who I am today to your guidance, professor.","furigana":"せんせい|くんとう|きょう|わたし"},{"japanese":"名監督の薫陶を受けた選手たちが、各地で指導者として活躍している。","romaji":"Mei-kantoku no kuntō o uketa senshu-tachi ga, kakuchi de shidōsha toshite katsuyaku shite iru.","german":"Players shaped by the great coach are now active as coaches themselves in many places.","furigana":"めいかんとく|くんとう|う|せんしゅ|かくち|しどうしゃ|かつやく"}],"notes":"薫陶 is made up of \"fumigating with fragrance\" and \"pottery making\": a revered teacher shapes his students through his personality, as fragrance seeps into clothes. Almost always in the form 〜の薫陶を受ける, politely ご薫陶; the phrase 薫陶よろしきを得る praises a successful upbringing. The word stands for the influence of a respected person, not for mere teaching (指導), and is very elevated, for example in speeches and words of thanks.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"定年まで教鞭を執った。","romaji":"Teinen made kyōben o totta.","german":"He taught until his retirement.","furigana":"ていねん|きょうべん|と"},{"japanese":"彼女は現在、アメリカの大学で日本文学の教鞭を執っている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa genzai, Amerika no daigaku de Nihon bungaku no kyōben o totte iru.","german":"She currently teaches Japanese literature at a university in America.","furigana":"かのじょ|げんざい|だいがく|にほんぶんがく|きょうべん|と"},{"japanese":"卒業後、地元の高校で教鞭を執ることになった。","romaji":"Sotsugyō-go, jimoto no kōkō de kyōben o toru koto ni natta.","german":"After graduating, he ended up teaching at a local high school.","furigana":"そつぎょうご|じもと|こうこう|きょうべん|と"}],"notes":"教鞭 is literally the teacher's pointer or rod, but it is used practically only in the set phrase 教鞭を執る (\"to teach, to work as a teacher\"), usually with a place: 大学で教鞭を執る. It is elevated and suitable for résumés, introductions and obituaries; in everyday speech one says 教える or 教師をしている. 執る is often also written 取る.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"師弟関係は日本文化において重要な意味を持つ。","romaji":"Shitei kankei wa Nihon bunka ni oite jūyō na imi o motsu.","german":"The master-disciple relationship carries an important meaning in Japanese culture.","furigana":"していかんけい|にほんぶんか|じゅうよう|いみ|も"},{"japanese":"二人は三十年来の師弟の間柄だ。","romaji":"Futari wa sanjū-nen rai no shitei no aidagara da.","german":"The two have been in a teacher-student relationship for thirty years.","furigana":"ふたり|さんじゅうねんらい|してい|あいだがら"},{"japanese":"決勝戦は師弟対決となった。","romaji":"Kesshōsen wa shitei taiketsu to natta.","german":"The final turned into a clash between master and pupil.","furigana":"けっしょうせん|していたいけつ"}],"notes":"師弟 combines \"master/teacher and pupil\" as a pair and emphasizes the personal bond, for example in art, martial arts or scholarship: 師弟関係, 師弟愛, 師弟の絆, 師弟の間柄. Careful, 子弟 (\"sons, young people\", e.g. 良家の子弟) is read the same way. For the relationship in school in general, one rather says 先生と生徒.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は有名な画家の門下に入った。","romaji":"Kare wa yūmei na gaka no monka ni haitta.","german":"He became a pupil of a famous painter.","furigana":"かれ|ゆうめい|がか|もんか|はい"},{"japanese":"芥川龍之介は夏目漱石の門下の一人だった。","romaji":"Akutagawa Ryūnosuke wa Natsume Sōseki no monka no hitori datta.","german":"Akutagawa Ryunosuke was one of Natsume Soseki's pupils.","furigana":"|なつめそうせき|もんか|ひとり"},{"japanese":"この道場の門下生は百人を超える。","romaji":"Kono dōjō no monkasei wa hyaku-nin o koeru.","german":"This dojo has over a hundred students.","furigana":"どうじょう|もんかせい|ひゃくにん|こ"}],"notes":"門下 refers to the body of pupils of a master, literally \"under the gate\" of his house. Typical are 〜の門下に入る (\"to become someone's pupil\"), 〜門下の作家 and 門下生 (\"pupil, disciple\"), e.g. 漱石門下. Compared with 弟子, 門下 is more elevated and emphasizes belonging to a school or tradition, for example in art, literature, martial arts or scholarship.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"若い頃から名匠に師事して技を磨いた。","romaji":"Wakai koro kara meishō ni shiji shite waza o migaita.","german":"From a young age he studied under a master craftsman and refined his skills.","furigana":"わか|ころ|めいしょう|しじ|わざ|みが"},{"japanese":"大学院では山田教授に師事し、言語学を学んだ。","romaji":"Daigakuin de wa Yamada kyōju ni shiji shi, gengogaku o mananda.","german":"In graduate school I studied linguistics under Professor Yamada.","furigana":"だいがくいん|やまだきょうじゅ|しじ|げんごがく|まな"},{"japanese":"彼女は五歳からあるピアニストに師事している。","romaji":"Kanojo wa go-sai kara aru pianisuto ni shiji shite iru.","german":"She has been studying under a certain pianist since the age of five.","furigana":"かのじょ|ごさい|しじ"}],"notes":"師事する means to go to someone as a teacher and study under him; the teacher takes に: 〜に師事する (intransitive). It is elevated and typical of the résumés of artists, musicians and scholars: 〜に師事し、〜を学ぶ. More colloquial is 〜に弟子入りする; 門下に入る is similar. Do not confuse it with 指示 (\"instruction\") or 支持 (\"support\"), which are read the same way.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この学舎から多くの人材が巣立った。","romaji":"Kono manabiya kara ōku no jinzai ga sudatta.","german":"Many talented people have left this school to make their way in the world.","furigana":"まなびや|おお|じんざい|すだ"},{"japanese":"三年間過ごしたこの学舎とも、今日でお別れです。","romaji":"Sannenkan sugoshita kono manabiya to mo, kyō de owakare desu.","german":"Today I say goodbye to this school where I spent three years.","furigana":"さんねんかんす|まなびや|きょう|わか"},{"japanese":"同窓会で懐かしい学舎を久しぶりに訪れた。","romaji":"Dōsōkai de natsukashii manabiya o hisashiburi ni otozureta.","german":"At the reunion I visited my beloved old school for the first time in a long while.","furigana":"どうそうかい|なつ|まなびや|ひさ|おとず"}],"notes":"学舎 in the reading まなびや is a poetic, nostalgic word for the school as a place, especially one's own former school. It sounds like school songs, graduation speeches and anniversary publications: 懐かしい学舎, 学舎を巣立つ. In factual language, for example for buildings, 学舎 is also read がくしゃ, or one says 校舎; in everyday speech simply 学校.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"最高学府で学ぶ機会を得た。","romaji":"Saikō gakufu de manabu kikai o eta.","german":"I got the opportunity to study at the highest institution of learning.","furigana":"さいこうがくふ|まな|きかい|え"},{"japanese":"東京大学は日本の最高学府と呼ばれることが多い。","romaji":"Tōkyō daigaku wa Nihon no saikō gakufu to yobareru koto ga ōi.","german":"The University of Tokyo is often called Japan's highest institution of learning.","furigana":"とうきょうだいがく|にほん|さいこうがくふ|よ|おお"},{"japanese":"最高学府を出たからといって、仕事ができるとは限らない。","romaji":"Saikō gakufu o deta kara to itte, shigoto ga dekiru to wa kagiranai.","german":"Just because someone graduated from a top university doesn't mean they are good at their job.","furigana":"さいこうがくふ|で|しごと|かぎ"}],"notes":"学府 is an elevated word for a seat of learning and occurs almost only in the fixed combination 最高学府, which designates the \"university\" as the highest educational institution; sometimes it specifically means the University of Tokyo. In the sense of \"bastion of scholarship\" there is also 学問の府. In everyday speech one says 大学.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"学窓を巣立ってから二十年が経った。","romaji":"Gakusō o sudatte kara nijū-nen ga tatta.","german":"Twenty years have passed since I left school.","furigana":"がくそう|すだ|にじゅうねん|た"},{"japanese":"学窓を離れて社会に出ると、学ぶ機会は減りがちだ。","romaji":"Gakusō o hanarete shakai ni deru to, manabu kikai wa herigachi da.","german":"Once you leave school and go out into society, opportunities to learn tend to decrease.","furigana":"がくそう|はな|しゃかい|で|まな|きかい|へ"},{"japanese":"学窓時代の友人とは今でも連絡を取り合っている。","romaji":"Gakusō jidai no yūjin to wa ima demo renraku o toriatte iru.","german":"I still keep in touch with friends from my student days.","furigana":"がくそうじだい|ゆうじん|いま|れんらく|と|あ"}],"notes":"学窓 is literally the \"window of the school\" and stands elegantly for school or university as a phase of life. It occurs almost only in fixed phrases: 学窓を巣立つ (\"to graduate and go out into the world\"), 学窓を離れる and 学窓時代 (\"school or student days\"). Typical of speeches, letters and reminiscences; in everyday speech one says 学生時代.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"明治時代に近代的な学制が導入された。","romaji":"Meiji jidai ni kindai-teki na gakusei ga dōnyū sareta.","german":"A modern school system was introduced in the Meiji period.","furigana":"めいじじだい|きんだいてき|がくせい|どうにゅう"},{"japanese":"戦後の学制改革で、六・三・三・四制が始まった。","romaji":"Sengo no gakusei kaikaku de, roku san san yon sei ga hajimatta.","german":"With the postwar educational reform, the 6-3-3-4 system began.","furigana":"せんご|がくせいかいかく|ろく|さん|さん|よんせい|はじ"},{"japanese":"九月入学への学制変更が議論されている。","romaji":"Kugatsu nyūgaku e no gakusei henkō ga giron sarete iru.","german":"A change of the school system to a September start is being discussed.","furigana":"くがつにゅうがく|がくせいへんこう|ぎろん"}],"notes":"学制 is the legally established school and education system of a country, such as the Japanese 六・三・三・四制. Common are 学制改革 (\"reform of the education system\") and, historically, 学制 as a proper name for the education ordinance of 1872 (明治五年), with which universal schooling began. Unlike 教育課程 (\"curriculum\"), 学制 concerns the structure: school levels, duration, start of the school year.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"教育課程の改革が議論されている。","romaji":"Kyōiku katei no kaikaku ga giron sarete iru.","german":"Reform of the curriculum is being discussed.","furigana":"きょういくかてい|かいかく|ぎろん"},{"japanese":"この学校は独自の教育課程を編成している。","romaji":"Kono gakkō wa dokuji no kyōiku katei o hensei shite iru.","german":"This school draws up its own curriculum.","furigana":"がっこう|どくじ|きょういくかてい|へんせい"},{"japanese":"小学校の教育課程に英語が正式に加わった。","romaji":"Shōgakkō no kyōiku katei ni eigo ga seishiki ni kuwawatta.","german":"English was officially added to the elementary school curriculum.","furigana":"しょうがっこう|きょういくかてい|えいご|せいしき|くわ"}],"notes":"教育課程 is the official term for the curriculum of a school or school level; in Japan it is drawn up on the basis of the 学習指導要領 (\"national curriculum guidelines\"): 教育課程を編成する. In everyday and business language people mostly say カリキュラム. Do not confuse it with 教職課程, the teacher-training program at universities.","levelNote":"Compound: 教育 (N4) + 課程 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"英語は全学年の必修科目である。","romaji":"Eigo wa zen-gakunen no hisshū kamoku de aru.","german":"English is a required subject for all grades.","furigana":"えいご|ぜんがくねん|ひっしゅうかもく"},{"japanese":"この授業は必修だから、落とすと卒業できないよ。","romaji":"Kono jugyō wa hisshū dakara, otosu to sotsugyō dekinai yo.","german":"This class is compulsory, so if you fail it you can't graduate.","furigana":"じゅぎょう|ひっしゅう|お|そつぎょう"},{"japanese":"小学校でプログラミング教育が必修化された。","romaji":"Shōgakkō de puroguramingu kyōiku ga hisshūka sareta.","german":"Programming education has been made compulsory in elementary schools.","furigana":"しょうがっこう|きょういく|ひっしゅうか"}],"notes":"必修 designates what must be taken as a requirement in a curriculum: 必修科目, 必修単位, 必修の授業; the counterpart is 選択 or 選択科目. 必修化 means \"to make compulsory\". Do not confuse it with 必須 (\"indispensable\"), which is used generally for the essential, e.g. 必須条件. 必修 is limited to school and training.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"選択科目の中から三つを選ばなければならない。","romaji":"Sentaku kamoku no naka kara mittsu o erabanakereba naranai.","german":"You must choose three from among the elective subjects.","furigana":"せんたくかもく|なか|みっ|えら"},{"japanese":"選択科目で中国語を取ることにした。","romaji":"Sentaku kamoku de Chūgokugo o toru koto ni shita.","german":"I decided to take Chinese as an elective.","furigana":"せんたくかもく|ちゅうごくご|と"},{"japanese":"三年生になると、選択科目の数が増える。","romaji":"Sannensei ni naru to, sentaku kamoku no kazu ga fueru.","german":"From third year on, the number of electives increases.","furigana":"さんねんせい|せんたくかもく|かず|ふ"}],"notes":"選択科目 is an elective that you freely choose from an offering; its counterpart is 必修科目 (\"required subject\"). One says 選択科目を取る or, more formally, 選択科目を履修する. At universities one often also distinguishes 選択必修科目 (\"restricted elective\"), where you must choose from a certain group.","levelNote":"Compound: 選択 (N3) + 科目 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい教授法を取り入れて授業を改善した。","romaji":"Atarashii kyōjuhō o toriirete jugyō o kaizen shita.","german":"I improved my classes by adopting new teaching methods.","furigana":"あたら|きょうじゅほう|と|い|じゅぎょう|かいぜん"},{"japanese":"大学で日本語教授法の授業を取っている。","romaji":"Daigaku de Nihongo kyōjuhō no jugyō o totte iru.","german":"At university I'm taking a class on methods of teaching Japanese.","furigana":"だいがく|にほんごきょうじゅほう|じゅぎょう|と"},{"japanese":"コミュニカティブ・アプローチは、会話を重視する教授法だ。","romaji":"Komyunikatibu apurōchi wa, kaiwa o jūshi suru kyōjuhō da.","german":"The communicative approach is a teaching method that puts emphasis on conversation.","furigana":"かいわ|じゅうし|きょうじゅほう"}],"notes":"In 教授法, 教授 does not mean \"professor\" but \"teaching, instructing\": it is the \"teaching method\" or instructional methodology of a subject. Particularly well known is 日本語教授法, the pedagogy of teaching Japanese as a foreign language. Related are 指導法 (\"method of guidance\") and 教育法 (\"method of education\"); colloquially one says 教え方. Typical: 教授法を学ぶ/取り入れる/工夫する.","levelNote":"Compound: 教授 (N3) + 法 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"査読付きの学術誌に論文が掲載された。","romaji":"Sadoku-tsuki no gakujutsu-shi ni ronbun ga keisai sareta.","german":"The paper was published in a peer-reviewed academic journal.","furigana":"さどくつ|がくじゅつし|ろんぶん|けいさい"},{"japanese":"投稿した論文がやっと査読を通った。","romaji":"Tōkō shita ronbun ga yatto sadoku o tōtta.","german":"The paper I submitted finally passed peer review.","furigana":"とうこう|ろんぶん|さどく|とお"},{"japanese":"彼女は国際学会の査読者を務めている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kokusai gakkai no sadokusha o tsutomete iru.","german":"She serves as a reviewer for an international academic conference.","furigana":"かのじょ|こくさいがっかい|さどくしゃ|つと"}],"notes":"査読 is the expert evaluation of a submitted scholarly article by fellow specialists: 論文を査読する (transitive). Common are 査読付き論文／査読付きの学術誌 (\"peer-reviewed\"), 査読者 (\"reviewer\") and 査読を通る (\"to pass peer review\"). General checking of texts, on the other hand, is called 校閲 (\"copyediting\") or simply チェック.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"研究成果を大学の紀要に発表した。","romaji":"Kenkyū seika o daigaku no kiyō ni happyō shita.","german":"I published my research results in the university bulletin.","furigana":"けんきゅうせいか|だいがく|きよう|はっぴょう"},{"japanese":"この論文は紀要に載ったもので、査読は受けていない。","romaji":"Kono ronbun wa kiyō ni notta mono de, sadoku wa ukete inai.","german":"This paper appeared in the in-house journal and has not been peer-reviewed.","furigana":"ろんぶん|きよう|の|さどく|う"},{"japanese":"研究所の紀要は毎年三月に刊行される。","romaji":"Kenkyūjo no kiyō wa maitoshi sangatsu ni kankō sareru.","german":"The institute's bulletin is published every March.","furigana":"けんきゅうじょ|きよう|まいとしさんがつ|かんこう"}],"notes":"紀要 is the in-house series of a university, faculty or research institute in which mainly its own staff publish: 大学の紀要, 研究紀要, 紀要論文. In Japanese scholarship, contributions in a 紀要 are often considered less weighty than those in a peer-reviewed academic journal (学術誌), because they usually do not go through strict 査読. A purely academic technical term.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"学術的な価値の高い研究として評価された。","romaji":"Gakujutsu-teki na kachi no takai kenkyū toshite hyōka sareta.","german":"The research was evaluated as having high academic value.","furigana":"がくじゅつてき|かち|たか|けんきゅう|ひょうか"},{"japanese":"学術論文は決まった形式で書かなければならない。","romaji":"Gakujutsu ronbun wa kimatta keishiki de kakanakereba naranai.","german":"Academic papers must be written in a fixed format.","furigana":"がくじゅつろんぶん|き|けいしき|か"},{"japanese":"この遺跡は学術上きわめて貴重だ。","romaji":"Kono iseki wa gakujutsu-jō kiwamete kichō da.","german":"This archaeological site is extremely valuable academically.","furigana":"いせき|がくじゅつじょう|きちょう"}],"notes":"学術 denotes science and research as an institutional field, mostly as the first element of compounds: 学術論文, 学術誌, 学術用語, 学術調査, 学術会議. With 的 it becomes 学術的 (\"scholarly, academic\"). 学問 rather means learning and the individual discipline, 科学 (natural) science; 学術 sounds official and abstract.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"最新の知見に基づいて理論を修正した。","romaji":"Saishin no chiken ni motozuite riron o shūsei shita.","german":"The theory was revised on the basis of the latest findings.","furigana":"さいしん|ちけん|もと|りろん|しゅうせい"},{"japanese":"今回の調査から、新たな知見が得られた。","romaji":"Konkai no chōsa kara, arata na chiken ga erareta.","german":"New findings were obtained from this survey.","furigana":"こんかい|ちょうさ|あら|ちけん|え"},{"japanese":"海外勤務を通じて、ビジネスの知見を広げた。","romaji":"Kaigai kinmu o tsūjite, bijinesu no chiken o hirogeta.","german":"Through working abroad, he broadened his business know-how.","furigana":"かいがいきんむ|つう|ちけん|ひろ"}],"notes":"知見 refers to insights gained through research, observation or experience, especially of a specialist kind. Typical phrases: 新たな知見を得る, 知見を広げる／深める, 最新の知見, 専門的な知見. In contrast to 知識 (\"knowledge\" in general), 知見 emphasizes insights from investigation and experience; 見解, on the other hand, is a \"view, statement of opinion\". Formal, typical of reports and scholarship.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は日本美術に造詣が深い。","romaji":"Kanojo wa Nihon bijutsu ni zōkei ga fukai.","german":"She has deep knowledge of Japanese art.","furigana":"かのじょ|にほんびじゅつ|ぞうけい|ふか"},{"japanese":"社長はクラシック音楽にも造詣が深いことで知られている。","romaji":"Shachō wa kurashikku ongaku ni mo zōkei ga fukai koto de shirarete iru.","german":"The company president is also known for his deep knowledge of classical music.","furigana":"しゃちょう|おんがく|ぞうけい|ふか|し"},{"japanese":"中世史に造詣の深い専門家に意見を求めた。","romaji":"Chūseishi ni zōkei no fukai senmonka ni iken o motometa.","german":"I asked a specialist well versed in medieval history for his opinion.","furigana":"ちゅうせいし|ぞうけい|ふか|せんもんか|いけん|もと"}],"notes":"造詣 is used almost exclusively in the set phrase 〜に造詣が深い (\"to be very well versed in something\"), attributively 〜に造詣の深い人. It means a deep understanding acquired over years, often in art, culture or a field of study. The phrase is appreciative and elevated; one does not use it about oneself. More neutral is 〜に詳しい.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼はワインについて蘊蓄を傾けた。","romaji":"Kare wa wain ni tsuite unchiku o katamuketa.","german":"He held forth with his knowledge about wine.","furigana":"かれ|うんちく|かたむ"},{"japanese":"部長は飲むたびにうんちくを語り始める。","romaji":"Buchō wa nomu tabi ni unchiku o katari hajimeru.","german":"Every time he drinks, the department head starts expounding his knowledge.","furigana":"ぶちょう|の|かた|はじ"},{"japanese":"ラーメンのうんちくを延々と聞かされて、うんざりした。","romaji":"Rāmen no unchiku o en'en to kikasarete, unzari shita.","german":"I had to listen endlessly to ramen trivia and got fed up.","furigana":"えんえん|き"}],"notes":"蘊蓄 is accumulated, deep knowledge; it is mostly written うんちく in kana. In elevated usage, 蘊蓄を傾ける means \"to bring all one's knowledge to bear\". Today うんちく is mainly colloquial and slightly mocking: うんちくを語る／垂れる (\"to show off with detailed knowledge, to hold forth\") about wine, ramen or cars, plus うんちく好き. Neutral would be 知識, appreciative 造詣.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"博識な彼女の話はいつも興味深い。","romaji":"Hakushiki na kanojo no hanashi wa itsumo kyōmi-bukai.","german":"Conversations with her, who is so widely learned, are always interesting.","furigana":"はくしき|かのじょ|はなし|きょうみぶか"},{"japanese":"彼は歴史について驚くほど博識だ。","romaji":"Kare wa rekishi ni tsuite odoroku hodo hakushiki da.","german":"He knows an astonishing amount about history.","furigana":"かれ|れきし|おどろ|はくしき"},{"japanese":"その博識ぶりから、「歩く百科事典」と呼ばれている。","romaji":"Sono hakushiki-buri kara, \"aruku hyakka jiten\" to yobarete iru.","german":"Because of his enormous knowledge, he is called a \"walking encyclopedia\".","furigana":"はくしき|ある|ひゃっかじてん|よ"}],"notes":"博識 means \"very well-read, broadly informed\" and is a noun and a な-adjective: 博識な人, 博識ぶり. It is almost synonymous with 博学; 博識 emphasizes more the amount of knowledge about many things, 博学 thorough scholarship. Colloquially one says 物知り. Praising, rather elevated style.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"無学を恥じて独学で勉強を始めた。","romaji":"Mugaku o hajite dokugaku de benkyō o hajimeta.","german":"Ashamed of my lack of education, I started studying on my own.","furigana":"むがく|は|どくがく|べんきょう|はじ"},{"japanese":"祖父は無学だったが、人生の知恵は誰よりも持っていた。","romaji":"Sofu wa mugaku datta ga, jinsei no chie wa dare yori mo motte ita.","german":"My grandfather had no schooling, but he had more life wisdom than anyone.","furigana":"そふ|むがく|じんせい|ちえ|だれ|も"},{"japanese":"無学な私には難しい話ですが、一つ質問させてください。","romaji":"Mugaku na watashi ni wa muzukashii hanashi desu ga, hitotsu shitsumon sasete kudasai.","german":"This is a difficult topic for an uneducated person like me, but please allow me to ask one question.","furigana":"むがく|わたし|むずか|はなし|ひと|しつもん"}],"notes":"無学 means not having received any (school) education; noun and な-adjective: 無学な人. Said about others it sounds pejorative, so today one hears it mainly modestly about oneself (無学な私には…) or in stories about earlier generations. 無知, by contrast, means ignorance in general or in a particular matter; colloquially one says 学がない.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"独学で三か国語を習得した。","romaji":"Dokugaku de san-kakokugo o shūtoku shita.","german":"He mastered three languages through self-study.","furigana":"どくがく|さん|こくご|しゅうとく"},{"japanese":"彼は独学で司法試験に合格した。","romaji":"Kare wa dokugaku de shihō shiken ni gōkaku shita.","german":"He passed the bar exam through self-study.","furigana":"かれ|どくがく|しほうしけん|ごうかく"},{"japanese":"独学には限界を感じて、ピアノ教室に通い始めた。","romaji":"Dokugaku ni wa genkai o kanjite, piano kyōshitsu ni kayoi hajimeta.","german":"Feeling the limits of self-study, I started going to a piano school.","furigana":"どくがく|げんかい|かん|きょうしつ|かよ|はじ"}],"notes":"独学 is learning without a teacher or school, usually with で: 独学で学ぶ／身につける／合格する; as a する-verb it is transitive (英語を独学する), attributively 独学の画家. Antonyms are 師事する or 学校に通う. Similar is 自習, which however rather means independent practice within a course, e.g. 自習時間.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"勉学に励む学生を支援する制度がある。","romaji":"Bengaku ni hagemu gakusei o shien suru seido ga aru.","german":"There is a system that supports students who work hard at their studies.","furigana":"べんがく|はげ|がくせい|しえん|せいど"},{"japanese":"アルバイトを減らして、勉学に専念することにした。","romaji":"Arubaito o herashite, bengaku ni sennen suru koto ni shita.","german":"I decided to cut back on part-time work and concentrate on my studies.","furigana":"へ|べんがく|せんねん"},{"japanese":"学生の本分は勉学である。","romaji":"Gakusei no honbun wa bengaku de aru.","german":"A student's essential duty is study.","furigana":"がくせい|ほんぶん|べんがく"}],"notes":"勉学 is an elevated word for serious learning and studying as a life task, above all of pupils and students. Typical are 勉学に励む (\"to study diligently\"), 勉学に専念する and 勉学の場. For concrete everyday studying (studying for a test tonight) one says 勉強; 勉学 sounds like speeches, scholarship conditions and school regulations.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"修学旅行で京都の寺院を巡った。","romaji":"Shūgaku ryokō de Kyōto no jiin o megutta.","german":"On the school trip, we toured temples in Kyoto.","furigana":"しゅうがくりょこう|きょうと|じいん|めぐ"},{"japanese":"経済的に苦しい学生の修学を支援する制度がある。","romaji":"Keizai-teki ni kurushii gakusei no shūgaku o shien suru seido ga aru.","german":"There is a program that supports the education of financially struggling students.","furigana":"けいざいてき|くる|がくせい|しゅうがく|しえん|せいど"},{"japanese":"この学科の修学年限は四年と定められている。","romaji":"Kono gakka no shūgaku nengen wa yo-nen to sadamerarete iru.","german":"The standard period of study for this department is set at four years.","furigana":"がっか|しゅうがくねんげん|よねん|さだ"}],"notes":"修学 means \"to learn, to receive an education\", but occurs almost only in compounds: 修学旅行 (\"school trip\", the multi-day school excursion in middle and high school), 修学年限 (\"standard period of study\") and 修学支援 (\"educational support\"). Careful: 就学 (\"school entry, school attendance\") is read the same way and concerns entry into compulsory schooling.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"就学率の向上は社会発展の指標となる。","romaji":"Shūgaku-ritsu no kōjō wa shakai hatten no shihyō to naru.","german":"A rise in the school enrollment rate is an indicator of social development.","furigana":"しゅうがくりつ|こうじょう|しゃかいはってん|しひょう"},{"japanese":"娘は来年、就学年齢になる。","romaji":"Musume wa rainen, shūgaku nenrei ni naru.","german":"My daughter reaches school age next year.","furigana":"むすめ|らいねん|しゅうがくねんれい"},{"japanese":"就学前の子どもを持つ家庭に支援金が支給される。","romaji":"Shūgaku-mae no kodomo o motsu katei ni shienkin ga shikyū sareru.","german":"Support money is paid to families with preschool-age children.","furigana":"しゅうがくまえ|こ|も|かてい|しえんきん|しきゅう"}],"notes":"就学 is entering school or attending school within the framework of compulsory education, a term from administration and education policy: 就学年齢, 就学前の子ども (\"preschool children\"), 就学率, 就学援助 (\"school assistance\"), 就学通知. As a する-verb it is intransitive: 小学校に就学する. 修学 (learning, esp. 修学旅行) is read the same way; in everyday speech one says 入学 or 学校に上がる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"片道一時間の通学は体力的に厳しい。","romaji":"Katamichi ichi-jikan no tsūgaku wa tairyoku-teki ni kibishii.","german":"A one-hour commute to school each way is physically demanding.","furigana":"かたみちいちじかん|つうがく|たいりょくてき|きび"},{"japanese":"毎朝自転車で通学しています。","romaji":"Maiasa jitensha de tsūgaku shite imasu.","german":"I go to school by bicycle every morning.","furigana":"まいあさじてんしゃ|つうがく"},{"japanese":"この道は小学生の通学路なので、スピードを落としてください。","romaji":"Kono michi wa shōgakusei no tsūgakuro na node, supīdo o otoshite kudasai.","german":"This road is a school route for elementary school children, so please slow down.","furigana":"みち|しょうがくせい|つうがくろ|お"}],"notes":"通学 is the regular trip to school or university, i.e. commuting: 電車で通学する (intransitive), 自転車通学. Common are 通学路 (\"school route\" as a road), 通学時間 and 通学定期 (\"student commuter pass\"). For working people one says 通勤; 登校, by contrast, means showing up at school on a particular day (登校日, 不登校).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"療養後に復学して無事に卒業した。","romaji":"Ryōyō-go ni fukugaku shite buji ni sotsugyō shita.","german":"After recovering, he returned to school and graduated without problems.","furigana":"りょうようご|ふくがく|ぶじ|そつぎょう"},{"japanese":"留学を終えて、来月から復学する予定です。","romaji":"Ryūgaku o oete, raigetsu kara fukugaku suru yotei desu.","german":"My study abroad is over, so I plan to return to my university next month.","furigana":"りゅうがく|お|らいげつ|ふくがく|よてい"},{"japanese":"停学期間が明けて、彼はようやく復学した。","romaji":"Teigaku kikan ga akete, kare wa yōyaku fukugaku shita.","german":"After his suspension ended, he was finally able to return to school.","furigana":"ていがくきかん|あ|かれ|ふくがく"}],"notes":"復学 is the return to school or university after an interruption, for example after 休学 (\"leave of absence\") or 停学 (\"temporary suspension\"): 復学する (intransitive), 大学に復学する. In administrative language there are 復学届 and 復学手続き. After a 退学, on the other hand, re-admission (再入学) is usually necessary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"家庭の事情で他校に転学した。","romaji":"Katei no jijō de takō ni tengaku shita.","german":"He transferred to another school for family reasons.","furigana":"かてい|じじょう|たこう|てんがく"},{"japanese":"アメリカの大学に転学するには、成績証明書が必要だ。","romaji":"Amerika no daigaku ni tengaku suru ni wa, seiseki shōmeisho ga hitsuyō da.","german":"To transfer to an American university, you need a transcript.","furigana":"だいがく|てんがく|せいせきしょうめいしょ|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"転学を希望する学生は、学務課に相談してください。","romaji":"Tengaku o kibō suru gakusei wa, gakumuka ni sōdan shite kudasai.","german":"Students who wish to transfer, please consult the academic affairs office.","furigana":"てんがく|きぼう|がくせい|がくむか|そうだん"}],"notes":"転学 is the change to another school, especially to another university, or to another faculty: 他大学に転学する (intransitive). It is a formal, administrative term; for changes between elementary, middle and high schools, people say 転校 in everyday speech. A change of faculty within the same university is more precisely called 転学部.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"進学率の地域格差が問題視されている。","romaji":"Shingaku-ritsu no chiiki kakusa ga mondai-shi sarete iru.","german":"Regional disparities in the rate of advancement to higher education are seen as a problem.","furigana":"しんがくりつ|ちいきかくさ|もんだいし"},{"japanese":"高校を卒業したら、東京の大学に進学したい。","romaji":"Kōkō o sotsugyō shitara, Tōkyō no daigaku ni shingaku shitai.","german":"After graduating from high school, I want to go on to a university in Tokyo.","furigana":"こうこう|そつぎょう|とうきょう|だいがく|しんがく"},{"japanese":"進学か就職か、まだ決められずにいる。","romaji":"Shingaku ka shūshoku ka, mada kimerarezu ni iru.","german":"I still can't decide between further study and a job.","furigana":"しんがく|しゅうしょく|き"}],"notes":"進学 is the transition to the next higher level of education, especially from high school to university: 大学に進学する (intransitive, with に). Common are 進学率 (\"advancement rate, university enrollment rate\"), 進学校 (\"high school with a high university-entry rate\"), 進学塾 and 進学か就職か (\"study or work\"). 入学, by contrast, is the concrete entry into a particular school.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"天才児が飛び級で大学に入学した。","romaji":"Tensaiji ga tobikyū de daigaku ni nyūgaku shita.","german":"The child prodigy skipped grades and entered university.","furigana":"てんさいじ|と|きゅう|だいがく|にゅうがく"},{"japanese":"日本の学校には、飛び級の制度がほとんどない。","romaji":"Nihon no gakkō ni wa, tobikyū no seido ga hotondo nai.","german":"Japanese schools have almost no system for skipping grades.","furigana":"にほん|がっこう|と|きゅう|せいど"},{"japanese":"彼女は二年飛び級して、十五歳で高校を卒業した。","romaji":"Kanojo wa ni-nen tobikyū shite, jūgo-sai de kōkō o sotsugyō shita.","german":"She skipped two grades and graduated from high school at fifteen.","furigana":"かのじょ|にねんと|きゅう|じゅうごさい|こうこう|そつぎょう"}],"notes":"飛び級 is skipping one or more grade levels because of exceptional performance: 飛び級する (intransitive), 飛び級で大学に入る, 二年飛び級する. In Japan this is hardly possible; only in exceptional cases is there 飛び入学 (\"early admission to university\"). The word therefore often comes up in connection with foreign school systems. The opposite is 留年 or 落第 (\"to repeat a year\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"及第点ぎりぎりで試験に合格した。","romaji":"Kyūdai-ten girigiri de shiken ni gōkaku shita.","german":"I passed the exam with barely the minimum score.","furigana":"きゅうだいてん|しけん|ごうかく"},{"japanese":"新人の企画としては、まあ及第点だろう。","romaji":"Shinjin no kikaku toshite wa, mā kyūdai-ten darō.","german":"For a newcomer's proposal, it's probably passable.","furigana":"しんじん|きかく|きゅうだいてん"},{"japanese":"昔の中国では、科挙に及第するのは極めて難しかった。","romaji":"Mukashi no Chūgoku de wa, kakyo ni kyūdai suru no wa kiwamete muzukashikatta.","german":"In ancient China, it was extremely difficult to pass the imperial examination.","furigana":"むかし|ちゅうごく|かきょ|きゅうだい|きわ|むずか"}],"notes":"及第 means to pass an exam or to meet the requirements for the next year; the opposite is 落第. As a verb (試験に及第する) it sounds old-fashioned today; normally one says 合格する. Mainly alive is 及第点 (\"passing mark\"), also figuratively: 及第点をつける／及第点だ (\"quite decent, adequate\"). Historically one speaks of 及第 in the Chinese civil service examination (科挙).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"落第を恐れて必死に勉強した。","romaji":"Rakudai o osorete hisshi ni benkyō shita.","german":"I studied desperately for fear of failing.","furigana":"らくだい|おそ|ひっし|べんきょう"},{"japanese":"このままだと落第して、もう一年やり直しになるぞ。","romaji":"Kono mama da to rakudai shite, mō ichi-nen yarinaoshi ni naru zo.","german":"If things go on like this, you'll fail and have to repeat the year.","furigana":"らくだい|いちねん|なお"},{"japanese":"客を待たせてばかりでは、サービスとしては落第だ。","romaji":"Kyaku o matasete bakari de wa, sābisu toshite wa rakudai da.","german":"If you keep making customers wait, you fail as a service.","furigana":"きゃく|ま|らくだい"}],"notes":"落第 means failing an exam or not being promoted and having to repeat the class: 落第する (intransitive). At universities, 留年 is mostly used today, and for exams 不合格 or 試験に落ちる. Figuratively, 落第 also means \"to fall short of expectations\": 落第点, 〜としては落第だ. The opposite is 及第.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は首席で大学を卒業した。","romaji":"Kanojo wa shuseki de daigaku o sotsugyō shita.","german":"She graduated from university at the top of her class.","furigana":"かのじょ|しゅせき|だいがく|そつぎょう"},{"japanese":"彼は首席で入学したが、その後は成績が伸びなかった。","romaji":"Kare wa shuseki de nyūgaku shita ga, sono go wa seiseki ga nobinakatta.","german":"He entered the school with the top score, but his grades didn't improve after that.","furigana":"かれ|しゅせき|にゅうがく|ご|せいせき|の"},{"japanese":"彼女はオーケストラの首席奏者に選ばれた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa ōkesutora no shuseki sōsha ni erabareta.","german":"She was chosen as principal player of the orchestra.","furigana":"かのじょ|しゅせきそうしゃ|えら"}],"notes":"首席 is the first person in a ranking. In education it appears mainly as 首席で卒業する／入学する (\"to graduate as the top of the class, or to pass the entrance exam with the top score\"). It also designates the highest position: 首席代表, 首席補佐官, and in an orchestra 首席奏者 (\"principal player\"). Do not confuse it with the identically read 主席, the title of a head of state or party (国家主席).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"優等の成績で学位を授与された。","romaji":"Yūtō no seiseki de gakui o juyo sareta.","german":"The degree was conferred with honors.","furigana":"ゆうとう|せいせき|がくい|じゅよ"},{"japanese":"兄は昔から優等生で、いつも先生に褒められていた。","romaji":"Ani wa mukashi kara yūtōsei de, itsumo sensei ni homerarete ita.","german":"My older brother has always been a model student and was constantly praised by the teachers.","furigana":"あに|むかし|ゆうとうせい|せんせい|ほ"},{"japanese":"彼の答えはいかにも優等生的で、本音が見えない。","romaji":"Kare no kotae wa ika ni mo yūtōsei-teki de, honne ga mienai.","german":"His answer is so much like a model student's that you can't see what he really thinks.","furigana":"かれ|こた|ゆうとうせいてき|ほんね|み"}],"notes":"優等 means \"outstanding, of the highest rank\" and occurs mainly in compounds: 優等生 (\"model student\"), 優等賞, 優等の成績. 優等生 is also used figuratively and slightly ironically: 優等生的な答え (\"a well-behaved, conformist answer\"). The opposite is 劣等. A degree \"with honors\" is rarely described this way in Japan; more usual are 首席で卒業 or 成績優秀.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"劣等感を克服して自信を取り戻した。","romaji":"Rettō-kan o kokufuku shite jishin o torimodoshita.","german":"He overcame his inferiority complex and regained his self-confidence.","furigana":"れっとうかん|こくふく|じしん|と|もど"},{"japanese":"優秀な兄と比べられて、ずっと劣等感を抱いていた。","romaji":"Yūshū na ani to kuraberarete, zutto rettōkan o idaite ita.","german":"Because I was always compared with my gifted older brother, I felt inferior all the time.","furigana":"ゆうしゅう|あに|くら|れっとうかん|いだ"},{"japanese":"子どもの頃は劣等生だったが、今では大学で教えている。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro wa rettōsei datta ga, ima de wa daigaku de oshiete iru.","german":"He was a poor student as a child, but now he teaches at a university.","furigana":"こ|ころ|れっとうせい|いま|だいがく|おし"}],"notes":"劣等 means \"inferior, of low rank\" and appears almost only in compounds: 劣等感 (\"inferiority complex\", 劣等感を抱く／持つ) and 劣等生 (\"poor student\"). The opposite is 優等 (優等生). Used alone, 劣等 is strongly pejorative and rare; 劣った or 劣る are more usual.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"学力低下の原因を探る調査が行われた。","romaji":"Gakuryoku teika no gen'in o saguru chōsa ga okonawareta.","german":"A survey was conducted to explore the causes of the decline in academic ability.","furigana":"がくりょくていか|げんいん|さぐ|ちょうさ|おこな"},{"japanese":"塾に通って、一年で学力をかなり伸ばした。","romaji":"Juku ni kayotte, ichi-nen de gakuryoku o kanari nobashita.","german":"By attending cram school, he greatly improved his academic ability in a year.","furigana":"じゅく|かよ|いちねん|がくりょく|の"},{"japanese":"全国学力テストの結果が発表された。","romaji":"Zenkoku gakuryoku tesuto no kekka ga happyō sareta.","german":"The results of the national academic achievement test were announced.","furigana":"ぜんこくがくりょく|けっか|はっぴょう"}],"notes":"学力 is the level of attainment acquired at school, that is, knowledge and skills in school subjects - not the ability to learn. Typical: 学力が高い／低い, 学力をつける／伸ばす, 基礎学力, 学力低下 and 学力テスト (e.g. the nationwide 全国学力テスト). 能力 is more general, 実力 means the ability that one can actually produce, for example in an exam.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"学童保育の充実が求められている。","romaji":"Gakudō hoiku no jūjitsu ga motomerarete iru.","german":"Improvement of after-school care for schoolchildren is being called for.","furigana":"がくどうほいく|じゅうじつ|もと"},{"japanese":"通学路では学童の安全を最優先にしてください。","romaji":"Tsūgakuro de wa gakudō no anzen o saiyūsen ni shite kudasai.","german":"On the way to school, please give top priority to the safety of schoolchildren.","furigana":"つうがくろ|がくどう|あんぜん|さいゆうせん"},{"japanese":"共働きなので、娘は放課後に学童に通っている。","romaji":"Tomobataraki na node, musume wa hōkago ni gakudō ni kayotte iru.","german":"Since both of us work, our daughter goes to the after-school program.","furigana":"ともばたら|むすめ|ほうかご|がくどう|かよ"}],"notes":"学童 designates children of elementary-school age and is a rather official word that mainly appears in compounds: 学童保育 \"after-school care\", 学童期 \"elementary-school age\", 学童用 \"for schoolchildren\". In everyday speech one says 小学生 or simply 子ども. Colloquially 学童 is also the short form of 学童保育, that is, the \"after-school program\": 学童に行く, 学童に預ける. Do not confuse it with 児童 (official term for children; in school likewise elementary pupils) and 生徒 (middle and high school students).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一年間の浪人生活を経て志望校に合格した。","romaji":"Ichi-nenkan no rōnin seikatsu o hete shibōkō ni gōkaku shita.","german":"Nach einem Jahr als Prüfungswiederholer bestand ich die Aufnahmeprüfung meiner Wunschuniversität.","furigana":"いちねんかん|ろうにんせいかつ|へ|しぼうこう|ごうかく"},{"japanese":"今年は全部落ちたので、浪人して来年もう一度受けるつもりだ。","romaji":"Kotoshi wa zenbu ochita node, rōnin shite rainen mō ichido ukeru tsumori da.","german":"Ich bin dieses Jahr überall durchgefallen, also lerne ich ein Jahr weiter und versuche es nächstes Jahr noch einmal.","furigana":"ことし|ぜんぶお|ろうにん|らいねん|いちどう"},{"japanese":"主君を失った武士たちは浪人となった。","romaji":"Shukun o ushinatta bushitachi wa rōnin to natta.","german":"Die Samurai, die ihren Herrn verloren hatten, wurden zu herrenlosen Rōnin.","furigana":"しゅくん|うしな|ぶし|ろうにん"}],"notes":"浪人 hat heute vor allem die Bedeutung „Schulabgänger, der nach einer nicht bestandenen Aufnahmeprüfung ein Jahr lang für den nächsten Versuch lernt“. Als intransitives する-Verb: 浪人する; dazu 浪人生 „Prüfungswiederholer“, 一浪 / 二浪 „ein / zwei Jahre Wartezeit“ und 浪人生活. Die ursprüngliche Bedeutung ist der herrenlose Samurai der Edo-Zeit (浪人になる), aus der auch das deutsche Lehnwort „Ronin“ stammt. Gelegentlich bedeutet es auch „Arbeitsloser“ (就職浪人 „wer nach dem Studium noch keine Stelle hat“).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"合否の結果は来週発表される。","romaji":"Gōhi no kekka wa raishū happyō sareru.","german":"The result of whether you passed or failed will be announced next week.","furigana":"ごうひ|けっか|らいしゅうはっぴょう"},{"japanese":"合否はメールで本人に通知されます。","romaji":"Gōhi wa mēru de honnin ni tsūchi saremasu.","german":"You will be notified personally by e-mail whether you passed.","furigana":"ごうひ|ほんにん|つうち"},{"japanese":"面接での印象が合否を左右することもある。","romaji":"Mensetsu de no inshō ga gōhi o sayū suru koto mo aru.","german":"Sometimes the impression made in the interview decides whether you are accepted.","furigana":"めんせつ|いんしょう|ごうひ|さゆう"}],"notes":"合否 combines 合格 \"passed\" and 否 \"not\" and designates the result of an exam or selection as a decision of \"pass or not\". It is a formal word from announcements and official language: 合否を発表する, 合否を通知する, 合否判定, 合否に関わる. In conversation one rather asks 受かった？ or 結果はどうだった？. Similarly built are 成否 \"success or failure\" and 可否 \"whether permissible or not\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"偏差値だけで学校を選ぶべきではない。","romaji":"Hensachi dake de gakkō o erabu beki de wa nai.","german":"You shouldn't choose a school based on the standard score alone.","furigana":"へんさち|がっこう|えら"},{"japanese":"夏の模試で偏差値が六十を超えた。","romaji":"Natsu no moshi de hensachi ga rokujū o koeta.","german":"In the summer mock exam, my deviation score went over 60.","furigana":"なつ|もし|へんさち|ろくじゅう|こ"},{"japanese":"この大学は偏差値が高く、合格するのは難しい。","romaji":"Kono daigaku wa hensachi ga takaku, gōkaku suru no wa muzukashii.","german":"This university has a high deviation score, so getting in is difficult.","furigana":"だいがく|へんさち|たか|ごうかく|むずか"}],"notes":"偏差値 is a statistical standard score (mean 50, standard deviation 10) that is used in Japan above all for mock exams (模試) and for ranking schools and universities. Typical: 偏差値が高い / 低い, 偏差値が上がる, 偏差値60の大学. The word often stands for the whole performance-oriented selection system; 偏差値教育 and 偏差値偏重 are critical terms. The term \"standard deviation\" itself, on the other hand, is 標準偏差.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"教員免許を取得するために教育実習が必要だ。","romaji":"Kyōin menkyo o shutoku suru tame ni kyōiku jisshū ga hitsuyō da.","german":"A teaching practicum is required to obtain a teaching license.","furigana":"きょういんめんきょ|しゅとく|きょういくじっしゅう|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"教員免許は持っているが、今は会社員として働いている。","romaji":"Kyōin menkyo wa motte iru ga, ima wa kaishain toshite hataraite iru.","german":"I have a teaching license, but I currently work as an office worker.","furigana":"きょういんめんきょ|も|いま|かいしゃいん|はたら"},{"japanese":"教員免許の更新制度は二〇二二年に廃止された。","romaji":"Kyōin menkyo no kōshin seido wa nisen nijūni-nen ni haishi sareta.","german":"The requirement to renew teaching licenses was abolished in 2022.","furigana":"きょういんめんきょ|こうしんせいど||はいし"}],"notes":"教員免許 is the state teaching qualification; officially the document is called 教員免許状. One says 教員免許を取る / 取得する / 持っている; for this you need certain courses of study and a school practicum (教育実習). 教員 is the formal word for teaching staff, while in everyday speech one says 先生 or 教師. The type is named more precisely, for example 小学校の教員免許 or 英語の教員免許.","levelNote":"Compound: 教員 (N1) + 免許 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"授業の副読本として推薦図書が配られた。","romaji":"Jugyō no fukudokuhon toshite suisen tosho ga kubarareta.","german":"A list of recommended books was handed out as supplementary reading for the class.","furigana":"じゅぎょう|ふくどくほん|すいせんとしょ|くば"},{"japanese":"市が作成した副読本で地域の歴史を学んだ。","romaji":"Shi ga sakusei shita fukudokuhon de chiiki no rekishi o mananda.","german":"We learned the history of our region from a supplementary booklet produced by the city.","furigana":"し|さくせい|ふくどくほん|ちいき|れきし|まな"},{"japanese":"道徳の時間には副読本の話を読んでから話し合う。","romaji":"Dōtoku no jikan ni wa fukudokuhon no hanashi o yonde kara hanashiau.","german":"In moral education class, we read a story from the supplementary reader and then discuss it.","furigana":"どうとく|じかん|ふくどくほん|はなし|よ|はな|あ"}],"notes":"副読本 is made up of 副 \"secondary, supplementary\" and 読本 \"reader\": a companion booklet or supplementary book that is used in class alongside the official textbook (教科書). Typical are booklets published by municipalities on local studies or books for moral education (道徳の副読本). Not to be confused with 参考書, which is a study aid that you buy yourself for exam preparation. Usage: 副読本として使う / 配る.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"デジタル教材の開発が進んでいる。","romaji":"Dejitaru kyōzai no kaihatsu ga susunde iru.","german":"The development of digital teaching materials is progressing.","furigana":"きょうざい|かいはつ|すす"},{"japanese":"田中先生はいつも手作りの教材で授業をする。","romaji":"Tanaka sensei wa itsumo tezukuri no kyōzai de jugyō o suru.","german":"Ms. Tanaka always teaches with handmade materials.","furigana":"たなかせんせい|てづく|きょうざい|じゅぎょう"},{"japanese":"このドラマは日本語学習の教材としても使える。","romaji":"Kono dorama wa Nihongo gakushū no kyōzai toshite mo tsukaeru.","german":"This drama can also be used as material for learning Japanese.","furigana":"にほんごがくしゅう|きょうざい|つか"}],"notes":"教材 covers everything used for teaching or learning: textbooks, worksheets, videos, apps. 教科書, by contrast, is only the official school textbook; 教具 are physical aids such as blackboards or models. Common patterns: 〜を教材にする / 教材として使う, 手作りの教材, デジタル教材, 教材費 \"cost of materials\", 教材研究 \"lesson preparation\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自由な校風で知られる学校を選んだ。","romaji":"Jiyū na kōfū de shirareru gakkō o eranda.","german":"I chose a school known for its liberal atmosphere.","furigana":"じゆう|こうふう|し|がっこう|えら"},{"japanese":"伝統を重んじる校風が今も受け継がれている。","romaji":"Dentō o omonjiru kōfū ga ima mo uketsugarete iru.","german":"The school's tradition-minded spirit has been handed down to this day.","furigana":"でんとう|おも|こうふう|いま|う|つ"},{"japanese":"入学前に見学に行って、校風が自分に合うか確かめた。","romaji":"Nyūgaku mae ni kengaku ni itte, kōfū ga jibun ni au ka tashikameta.","german":"Before enrolling, I visited the school to see whether its atmosphere suited me.","furigana":"にゅうがくまえ|けんがく|い|こうふう|じぶん|あ|たし"}],"notes":"校風 is the spirit or atmosphere of a school: its traditions, values and the way people treat each other. Typical: 自由な校風, 伝統を重んじる校風, 校風に合う / 合わない. Distinguish it from 校則 (concrete school rules), 校訓 (a formulated school motto) and the analogously formed 社風 \"company culture\". The word is neutral to slightly elevated.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"質実剛健がこの学校の校訓である。","romaji":"Shitsujitsu gōken ga kono gakkō no kōkun de aru.","german":"Simplicity and fortitude are this school's motto.","furigana":"しつじつごうけん|がっこう|こうくん"},{"japanese":"体育館の壁には校訓が大きく掲げられている。","romaji":"Taiikukan no kabe ni wa kōkun ga ōkiku kakagerarete iru.","german":"The school motto hangs large on the wall of the gymnasium.","furigana":"たいいくかん|かべ|こうくん|おお|かか"},{"japanese":"卒業式で校長が校訓の意味を改めて語った。","romaji":"Sotsugyōshiki de kōchō ga kōkun no imi o aratamete katatta.","german":"At the graduation ceremony, the principal explained the meaning of the school motto once again.","furigana":"そつぎょうしき|こうちょう|こうくん|いみ|あらた|かた"}],"notes":"校訓 is the official guiding maxim of a school, often a four-character phrase such as 質実剛健 or 自主自律. It is 掲げる \"displayed, held high\", for example in the assembly hall or the classroom, and quoted at ceremonies. Distinguish: 校則 \"school rules\", 校風 \"school atmosphere\", 校歌 \"school song\"; analogously formed are 社訓 \"company motto\" and 家訓 \"family motto\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"学則に違反した場合は処分の対象となる。","romaji":"Gakusoku ni ihan shita baai wa shobun no taishō to naru.","german":"Violations of the school regulations are subject to disciplinary action.","furigana":"がくそく|いはん|ばあい|しょぶん|たいしょう"},{"japanese":"学則で、在学期間は最長八年と定められている。","romaji":"Gakusoku de, zaigaku kikan wa saichō hachi-nen to sadamerarete iru.","german":"The regulations set the maximum period of enrollment at eight years.","furigana":"がくそく|ざいがくきかん|さいちょうはちねん|さだ"},{"japanese":"この高校では、学則によりアルバイトは許可制になっている。","romaji":"Kono kōkō de wa, gakusoku ni yori arubaito wa kyokasei ni natte iru.","german":"At this high school, part-time jobs require permission under the school regulations.","furigana":"こうこう|がくそく|きょかせい"}],"notes":"学則 is the official, written regulation of a school or university: admission, length of study, credits, degrees and disciplinary measures. Typical: 学則に定める, 学則で定められている, 学則に違反する. The everyday rules of conduct for pupils (hairstyle, uniform, ban on mobile phones), by contrast, are called 校則; at universities one speaks of 学則. The word is formal.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"課外活動を通じて社会性を養う。","romaji":"Kagai katsudō o tsūjite shakai-sei o yashinau.","german":"We cultivate social skills through extracurricular activities.","furigana":"かがいかつどう|つう|しゃかいせい|やしな"},{"japanese":"課外授業で近くの工場を見学した。","romaji":"Kagai jugyō de chikaku no kōjō o kengaku shita.","german":"On an extracurricular outing, we visited a nearby factory.","furigana":"かがいじゅぎょう|ちか|こうじょう|けんがく"},{"japanese":"三年生は放課後に受験対策の課外講座を受けている。","romaji":"Sannensei wa hōkago ni juken taisaku no kagai kōza o ukete iru.","german":"Third-year students take extracurricular exam-prep courses after school.","furigana":"さんねんせい|ほうかご|じゅけんたいさく|かがいこうざ|う"}],"notes":"課外 means \"outside the regular curriculum\" (課 \"lesson, workload\" + 外 \"outside\") and appears almost always in compounds or with の: 課外活動 \"extracurricular activities, clubs\", 課外授業 \"extra class, field trip\", 課外講座 \"supplementary course\". Do not confuse it with 放課後 \"after class\" (time) and 校外 \"outside the school grounds\" (place). For school clubs one usually says 部活 in everyday speech.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"学芸員の資格を取るために博物館で実習した。","romaji":"Gakugeiin no shikaku o toru tame ni hakubutsukan de jisshū shita.","german":"I did a practicum at a museum to obtain a curator's qualification.","furigana":"がくげいいん|しかく|と|はくぶつかん|じっしゅう"},{"japanese":"小学校の学芸会で劇の主役を演じた。","romaji":"Shōgakkō no gakugeikai de geki no shuyaku o enjita.","german":"At the elementary school's arts festival, I played the lead in a play.","furigana":"しょうがっこう|がくげいかい|げき|しゅやく|えん"},{"japanese":"新聞の学芸欄に新刊の書評が載った。","romaji":"Shinbun no gakugei-ran ni shinkan no shohyō ga notta.","german":"A review of a newly published book appeared in the newspaper's culture section.","furigana":"しんぶん|がくげいらん|しんかん|しょひょう|の"}],"notes":"学芸 means \"the sciences and the arts\" and hardly occurs alone, but almost only in fixed compounds: 学芸員 \"curator (museum)\", 学芸会 \"elementary-school event with plays and music\", 学芸欄 / 学芸部 \"culture section or arts desk of a newspaper\". It also appears in names such as 東京学芸大学. For \"art and science\" in general, one rather says 学問と芸術 today.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"初級教程を終えてから中級に進む。","romaji":"Shokyū kyōtei o oete kara chūkyū ni susumu.","german":"After finishing the beginner course, you move on to the intermediate level.","furigana":"しょきゅうきょうてい|お|ちゅうきゅう|すす"},{"japanese":"教習所では、決められた教程に沿って学科と技能を学ぶ。","romaji":"Kyōshūjo de wa, kimerareta kyōtei ni sotte gakka to ginō o manabu.","german":"At the driving school, you learn theory and practice following a fixed curriculum.","furigana":"きょうしゅうじょ|き|きょうてい|そ|がっか|ぎのう|まな"},{"japanese":"この教程は基礎から段階的に学べるように作られている。","romaji":"Kono kyōtei wa kiso kara dankai-teki ni manaberu yō ni tsukurarete iru.","german":"This course is designed so that you can learn step by step from the basics.","furigana":"きょうてい|きそ|だんかいてき|まな|つく"}],"notes":"教程 is a formal word for a course of instruction built up in stages or the corresponding textbook series. You encounter it in driving schools (教習所の教程), in the training plans of the police or military, and in the titles of music and language textbooks (e.g. ピアノ教程). For the curriculum of a school, one rather says カリキュラム or 教育課程; 課程 alone designates a course of study (博士課程). Typical: 教程に沿って進める, 初級教程.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"寺の書院から美しい庭を眺めた。","romaji":"Tera no shoin kara utsukushii niwa o nagameta.","german":"From the temple's shoin, we gazed at the beautiful garden.","furigana":"てら|しょいん|うつく|にわ|なが"},{"japanese":"書院造は現代の和室の原型となった。","romaji":"Shoin-zukuri wa gendai no washitsu no genkei to natta.","german":"The shoin-zukuri style became the prototype of the modern Japanese tatami room.","furigana":"|げんだい|わしつ|げんけい"},{"japanese":"祖父は書院にこもって漢詩を書いていた。","romaji":"Sofu wa shoin ni komotte kanshi o kaite ita.","german":"My grandfather would shut himself in his study and write Chinese poetry.","furigana":"そふ|しょいん|かんし|か"}],"notes":"書院 was originally a study and writing room in temples and samurai residences, later a formal reception room. From it developed the architectural style 書院造 of the Muromachi period with 床の間 and 違い棚, the model of today's 和室. Today the word is encountered mainly in architecture and history and in publishers' names (〜書院). An ordinary study is called 書斎; in a Chinese context, 書院 also designates a Confucian academy.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"福沢諭吉は慶應義塾という私塾を開いた。","romaji":"Fukuzawa Yukichi wa Keiō Gijuku to iu shijuku o hiraita.","german":"Fukuzawa Yukichi opened a private school called Keio Gijuku.","furigana":"ふくざわゆきち||しじゅく|ひら"},{"japanese":"吉田松陰の松下村塾は幕末の有名な私塾だ。","romaji":"Yoshida Shōin no Shōka Sonjuku wa bakumatsu no yūmei na shijuku da.","german":"Yoshida Shoin's Shokasonjuku is a famous private academy of the late Edo period.","furigana":"よしだしょういん|しょうかそんじゅく|ばくまつ|ゆうめい|しじゅく"},{"japanese":"退職した元教師が自宅で小さな私塾を開いている。","romaji":"Taishoku shita moto kyōshi ga jitaku de chiisana shijuku o hiraite iru.","german":"A retired former teacher runs a small private school at home.","furigana":"たいしょく|もときょうし|じたく|ちい|しじゅく|ひら"}],"notes":"私塾 is a school run by a private individual. Historically it means the private academies of the Edo period in which a scholar gathered pupils around him, such as 松下村塾 or 適塾; they stood alongside 藩校 (domain schools) and 寺子屋 (elementary schools). Today it designates small-scale private teaching, for example in one's own home. A state-recognized private school, on the other hand, is a 私立学校, and a cram school is simply 塾 in everyday speech.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"寺子屋は庶民の教育機関として機能した。","romaji":"Terakoya wa shomin no kyōiku kikan toshite kinō shita.","german":"Terakoya functioned as educational institutions for the common people.","furigana":"てらこや|しょみん|きょういくきかん|きのう"},{"japanese":"寺子屋では読み書きそろばんが教えられた。","romaji":"Terakoya de wa yomi kaki soroban ga oshierareta.","german":"At terakoya, reading, writing and the abacus were taught.","furigana":"てらこや|よ|か|おし"},{"japanese":"地域の大人が子どもに勉強を教える「現代の寺子屋」が人気だ。","romaji":"Chiiki no otona ga kodomo ni benkyō o oshieru \"gendai no terakoya\" ga ninki da.","german":"A \"modern terakoya\", where local adults help children with their studies, is popular.","furigana":"ちいき|おとな|こ|べんきょう|おし|げんだい|てらこや|にんき"}],"notes":"寺子屋 were the elementary schools for the common people in the Edo period, in which children (寺子) learned reading, writing and arithmetic with the abacus (読み書きそろばん). Despite the name, they by no means took place only in temples; teachers also included samurai, doctors or townspeople. They are regarded as the reason for the high literacy in premodern Japan. Today 寺子屋 is also used figuratively for free learning offerings in the neighborhood, often in quotation marks.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"藩校では武士の子弟が儒学を学んだ。","romaji":"Hankō de wa bushi no shitei ga jugaku o mananda.","german":"At domain schools, the sons of samurai studied Confucianism.","furigana":"はんこう|ぶし|してい|じゅがく|まな"},{"japanese":"会津の日新館は代表的な藩校の一つだ。","romaji":"Aizu no Nisshinkan wa daihyō-teki na hankō no hitotsu da.","german":"The Nisshinkan in Aizu is one of the representative domain schools.","furigana":"あいづ|にっしんかん|だいひょうてき|はんこう|ひと"},{"japanese":"藩校の伝統を受け継ぐ高校が今も各地にある。","romaji":"Hankō no dentō o uketsugu kōkō ga ima mo kakuchi ni aru.","german":"There are still high schools in many places that carry on the tradition of a domain school.","furigana":"はんこう|でんとう|う|つ|こうこう|いま|かくち"}],"notes":"藩校 (also 藩学) were the schools that the feudal lords (大名) of the individual domains (藩) established in the Edo period for the children of their retainers. Taught were mainly the Confucian classics, later also martial arts, medicine and Western sciences. Well-known examples are 日新館 in Aizu or 明倫館 in Hagi. Distinguish from 寺子屋 (for the common people) and 私塾 (private academies). A purely historical term.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"儒学の教えは日本の教育思想に深く根付いている。","romaji":"Jugaku no oshie wa Nihon no kyōiku shisō ni fukaku nezuite iru.","german":"Confucian teachings are deeply rooted in Japanese educational thought.","furigana":"じゅがく|おし|にほん|きょういくしそう|ふか|ねづ"},{"japanese":"江戸幕府は朱子学を中心とする儒学を重んじた。","romaji":"Edo bakufu wa shushigaku o chūshin to suru jugaku o omonjita.","german":"The Edo shogunate valued Confucianism, centered on Neo-Confucianism (Shushigaku).","furigana":"えどばくふ|しゅしがく|ちゅうしん|じゅがく|おも"},{"japanese":"彼女は大学院で中国の儒学思想を研究している。","romaji":"Kanojo wa daigakuin de Chūgoku no jugaku shisō o kenkyū shite iru.","german":"She studies Chinese Confucian thought in graduate school.","furigana":"かのじょ|だいがくいん|ちゅうごく|じゅがくしそう|けんきゅう"}],"notes":"儒学 is the teaching of Confucius (孔子) as scholarship and learning, that is, the study of the Confucian classics. 儒教, by contrast, means Confucianism as an ethical-religious system and social influence (儒教的な価値観). Related are 儒学者 \"Confucian scholar\" as well as the schools 朱子学 and 陽明学. Typical: 儒学を学ぶ / 重んじる, 儒学の教え.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日々の研鑽を怠らないことが大切だ。","romaji":"Hibi no kensan o okotaranai koto ga taisetsu da.","german":"It is important not to neglect daily study.","furigana":"ひび|けんさん|おこた|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"長年の研鑽を積んで、ようやく一人前の職人になった。","romaji":"Naganen no kensan o tsunde, yōyaku ichininmae no shokunin ni natta.","german":"After many years of diligent training, he finally became a full-fledged craftsman.","furigana":"ながねん|けんさん|つ|いちにんまえ|しょくにん"},{"japanese":"若手の研究者たちが海外で研鑽に励んでいる。","romaji":"Wakate no kenkyūshatachi ga kaigai de kensan ni hagende iru.","german":"Young researchers are devoting themselves to study abroad.","furigana":"わかて|けんきゅうしゃ|かいがい|けんさん|はげ"}],"notes":"研鑽 means to deepen and hone knowledge or skill through persistent study (研 \"to polish\", 鑽 \"to drill\"). It is an elevated, literary word, typical in fixed combinations: 研鑽を積む, 研鑽に励む, 日々の研鑽, 自己研鑽 \"self-improvement\". Because 鑽 is not a jōyō kanji, newspapers often write 研さん. More everyday are 勉強 or 努力; 修業, by contrast, emphasizes practical training under a master.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仲間と切磋琢磨して成長した。","romaji":"Nakama to sessa takuma shite seichō shita.","german":"I grew by spurring each other on with my friends.","furigana":"なかま|せっさたくま|せいちょう"},{"japanese":"ライバルと切磋琢磨することは大切だ。","romaji":"Raibaru to sessa takuma suru koto wa taisetsu da.","german":"It is important to improve through mutual competition with rivals.","furigana":"せっさたくま|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"若手社員が互いに切磋琢磨できる職場を作りたい。","romaji":"Wakate shain ga tagai ni sessa takuma dekiru shokuba o tsukuritai.","german":"I want to create a workplace where young employees can spur each other on.","furigana":"わかてしゃいん|たが|せっさたくま|しょくば|つく"}],"notes":"切磋琢磨 comes from the image of working bone and ivory (切・磋) and jade and stone (琢・磨): by cutting and polishing, something becomes ever finer. Today it mainly means \"to spur each other on and get better together\", i.e. friendly rivalry. Typical is the form with と or 互いに: 仲間と切磋琢磨する, 互いに切磋琢磨し合う. It sounds positive and rather elevated and is readily used in speeches, school and company texts; in everyday speech one says more simply 競い合う or 刺激し合う.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は文武両道の学生だ。","romaji":"Kare wa bunbu ryōdō no gakusei da.","german":"He is a student who excels in both academics and sports.","furigana":"かれ|ぶんぶりょうどう|がくせい"},{"japanese":"この高校は文武両道を教育方針に掲げている。","romaji":"Kono kōkō wa bunbu ryōdō o kyōiku hōshin ni kakagete iru.","german":"This high school has put excellence in both study and sports forward as its educational policy.","furigana":"こうこう|ぶんぶりょうどう|きょういくほうしん|かか"},{"japanese":"江戸時代の武士には、文武両道が求められた。","romaji":"Edo jidai no bushi ni wa, bunbu ryōdō ga motomerareta.","german":"Samurai of the Edo period were expected to excel in both literary and martial arts.","furigana":"えどじだい|ぶし|ぶんぶりょうどう|もと"}],"notes":"文武両道 means \"both paths, that of the pen (文) and that of the military profession (武)\": originally the samurai was required to master both education and martial arts. Today it mostly means that someone is equally good in school or studies and in sports. Typical are 文武両道の生徒 and 文武両道を目指す／掲げる, for instance as a school motto. The expression is positive and slightly formal.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不登校の生徒への支援体制を整えた。","romaji":"Futōkō no seito e no shien taisei o totonoeta.","german":"A support system for students who refuse to attend school was set up.","furigana":"ふとうこう|せいと|しえんたいせい|ととの"},{"japanese":"息子は中学二年の秋から不登校になった。","romaji":"Musuko wa chūgaku ninen no aki kara futōkō ni natta.","german":"Our son has not been going to school since the autumn of his second year of middle school.","furigana":"むすこ|ちゅうがくにねん|あき|ふとうこう"},{"japanese":"不登校の子どもが通えるフリースクールが増えている。","romaji":"Futōkō no kodomo ga kayoeru furī sukūru ga fuete iru.","german":"The number of free schools that children who don't attend regular school can go to is increasing.","furigana":"ふとうこう|こ|かよ|ふ"}],"notes":"不登校 designates longer absence from school for psychological or social reasons, not because of illness or poverty; the Ministry of Education counts pupils with at least 30 days of absence per year. Typical: 不登校になる, 不登校の児童・生徒, 不登校気味. The older expression 登校拒否 \"school refusal\" is avoided today because it implies a conscious refusal. Occasional skipping school, by contrast, is called サボる or ずる休み.","levelNote":"Compound: 不 (N3) + 登校 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いじめの根絶に向けた取り組みが強化されている。","romaji":"Ijime no konzetsu ni muketa torikumi ga kyōka sarete iru.","german":"Efforts to eradicate bullying are being strengthened.","furigana":"こんぜつ|む|と|く|きょうか"},{"japanese":"彼は小学生のころ、ひどいいじめに遭った。","romaji":"Kare wa shōgakusei no koro, hidoi ijime ni atta.","german":"He was badly bullied when he was in elementary school.","furigana":"かれ|しょうがくせい|あ"},{"japanese":"職場でのいじめに悩んで、会社を辞めた。","romaji":"Shokuba de no ijime ni nayande, kaisha o yameta.","german":"I quit my job because I was suffering from bullying at work.","furigana":"しょくば|なや|かいしゃ|や"}],"notes":"いじめ is the noun for the transitive verb いじめる \"to torment, to bully\" (〜をいじめる) and is almost always written in kana. Someone who is bullied いじめに遭う or いじめを受ける; the perpetrator is the いじめっ子, the victim the いじめられっ子. The word applies to school as well as the workplace (職場のいじめ), where パワハラ is often said. Harmless teasing, by contrast, is called からかう.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"学級崩壊は教師にとって深刻な問題だ。","romaji":"Gakkyū hōkai wa kyōshi ni totte shinkoku na mondai da.","german":"Classroom breakdown is a serious problem for teachers.","furigana":"がっきゅうほうかい|きょうし|しんこく|もんだい"},{"japanese":"担任が替わってから、そのクラスは学級崩壊の状態に陥った。","romaji":"Tannin ga kawatte kara, sono kurasu wa gakkyū hōkai no jōtai ni ochiitta.","german":"After the homeroom teacher changed, that class fell into a state of classroom breakdown.","furigana":"たんにん|か|がっきゅうほうかい|じょうたい|おちい"},{"japanese":"学級崩壊を防ぐため、二人の教師で授業をする学校もある。","romaji":"Gakkyū hōkai o fusegu tame, futari no kyōshi de jugyō o suru gakkō mo aru.","german":"To prevent classroom breakdown, some schools have two teachers teach each lesson.","furigana":"がっきゅうほうかい|ふせ|ふたり|きょうし|じゅぎょう|がっこう"}],"notes":"学級崩壊 is a term in use since the late 1990s for a class in which teaching is no longer possible: pupils walk around, don't listen, ignore the teacher. It concerns mainly elementary schools. 学級 is the official word for \"school class\" (in everyday speech クラス), 崩壊 means \"collapse\". Typical: 学級崩壊が起きる, 学級崩壊の状態に陥る, 学級崩壊を防ぐ. Distinguish from 校内暴力 \"violence in schools\".","levelNote":"Compound: 学級 (N2) + 崩壊 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"飛び入学の制度を利用して十六歳で大学に入った。","romaji":"Tobi nyūgaku no seido o riyō shite jūroku-sai de daigaku ni haitta.","german":"He used the early-admission system and entered university at sixteen.","furigana":"と|にゅうがく|せいど|りよう|じゅうろくさい|だいがく|はい"},{"japanese":"彼女は学部三年から大学院に飛び入学した。","romaji":"Kanojo wa gakubu sannen kara daigakuin ni tobi nyūgaku shita.","german":"She moved to graduate school early, after only the third year of her undergraduate studies.","furigana":"かのじょ|がくぶさんねん|だいがくいん|と|にゅうがく"},{"japanese":"日本では飛び入学の例はまだ少ない。","romaji":"Nihon de wa tobi nyūgaku no rei wa mada sukunai.","german":"In Japan there are still few cases of early admission.","furigana":"にほん|と|にゅうがく|れい|すく"}],"notes":"飛び入学 designates early transfer to the next educational level without completing the current one: from high school to university or from undergraduate study to graduate school. In Japan this has been possible at some universities since 1998, but is rare. Do not confuse it with 飛び級 \"skipping a grade\" within the same school, which is not provided for in Japanese compulsory schooling. Typical: 飛び入学で入る, 飛び入学制度.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"詰め込み教育への批判が高まっている。","romaji":"Tsumekomi kyōiku e no hihan ga takamatte iru.","german":"Criticism of cram-style education is growing.","furigana":"つ|こ|きょういく|ひはん|たか"},{"japanese":"詰め込みで覚えた知識は、試験が終わるとすぐ忘れてしまう。","romaji":"Tsumekomi de oboeta chishiki wa, shiken ga owaru to sugu wasurete shimau.","german":"Knowledge learned by cramming is forgotten as soon as the exam is over.","furigana":"つ|こ|おぼ|ちしき|しけん|お|わす"},{"japanese":"予定の詰め込みすぎで体調を崩した。","romaji":"Yotei no tsumekomisugi de taichō o kuzushita.","german":"I got sick from packing in too many appointments.","furigana":"よてい|つ|こ|たいちょう|くず"}],"notes":"詰め込み is the noun for the transitive verb 詰め込む \"to stuff in, to pack full\". In an educational context it stands for cramming knowledge: 詰め込み教育, 詰め込み式 / 詰め込み型の勉強, with ゆとり教育 as the opposite. Beyond that it applies to anything overloaded: 詰め込みすぎ \"too much at once\" with suitcases, appointments or texts. Studying through the night shortly before an exam is called 一夜漬け.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ゆとり教育の功罪が議論されている。","romaji":"Yutori kyōiku no kōzai ga giron sarete iru.","german":"The merits and demerits of yutori education are being debated.","furigana":"きょういく|こうざい|ぎろん"},{"japanese":"ゆとり教育で授業時間と学習内容が大幅に減らされた。","romaji":"Yutori kyōiku de jugyō jikan to gakushū naiyō ga ōhaba ni herasareta.","german":"With yutori education, class hours and the content of learning were drastically reduced.","furigana":"きょういく|じゅぎょうじかん|がくしゅうないよう|おおはば|へ"},{"japanese":"彼はゆとり教育を受けた世代だが、「ゆとり世代」と呼ばれるのを嫌がる。","romaji":"Kare wa yutori kyōiku o uketa sedai da ga, \"yutori sedai\" to yobareru no o iyagaru.","german":"He belongs to the generation that went through yutori education, but he dislikes being called part of the \"yutori generation\".","furigana":"かれ|きょういく|う|せだい|せだい|よ|いや"}],"notes":"ゆとり教育 designates the Japanese education policy of roughly 2002 to 2010, which, as a response to 詰め込み教育, cut learning content and class hours, introduced the five-day week and promoted cross-curricular learning. ゆとり means \"leeway, composure\". After weaker PISA results it was rolled back (脱ゆとり). The generation affected is called ゆとり世代, often used pejoratively.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"生涯学習の重要性が広く認識されるようになった。","romaji":"Shōgai gakushū no jūyō-sei ga hiroku ninshiki sareru yō ni natta.","german":"The importance of lifelong learning has come to be widely recognized.","furigana":"しょうがいがくしゅう|じゅうようせい|ひろ|にんしき"},{"japanese":"母は市の生涯学習センターで書道を習っている。","romaji":"Haha wa shi no shōgai gakushū sentā de shodō o naratte iru.","german":"My mother is learning calligraphy at the city's lifelong learning center.","furigana":"はは|し|しょうがいがくしゅう|しょどう|なら"},{"japanese":"定年後は生涯学習として大学の公開講座に通うつもりだ。","romaji":"Teinen-go wa shōgai gakushū toshite daigaku no kōkai kōza ni kayou tsumori da.","german":"After retirement, I plan to attend the university's open lectures as lifelong learning.","furigana":"ていねんご|しょうがいがくしゅう|だいがく|こうかいこうざ|かよ"}],"notes":"生涯学習 (\"learning throughout life\", 生涯 \"lifetime\") is an education-policy term for all learning activities after school: community courses, hobbies, continuing education in old age. Typical are 生涯学習センター, 生涯学習講座 and 生涯学習として〜を学ぶ. For job-related continuing education, one rather says リカレント教育 or リスキリング; 社会教育 is the administrative term for adult education.","levelNote":"Compound: 生涯 (N2) + 学習 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本の識字率は世界でも最高水準にある。","romaji":"Nihon no shikiji-ritsu wa sekai demo saikō suijun ni aru.","german":"Japan's literacy rate is among the highest in the world.","furigana":"にほん|しきじりつ|せかい|さいこうすいじゅん"},{"japanese":"識字率の向上は貧困の削減につながる。","romaji":"Shikiji-ritsu no kōjō wa hinkon no sakugen ni tsunagaru.","german":"Improving the literacy rate leads to a reduction in poverty.","furigana":"しきじりつ|こうじょう|ひんこん|さくげん"},{"japanese":"江戸時代の日本は識字率が高かったと言われている。","romaji":"Edo jidai no Nihon wa shikiji-ritsu ga takakatta to iwarete iru.","german":"Japan in the Edo period is said to have had a high literacy rate.","furigana":"えどじだい|にほん|しきじりつ|たか|い"}],"notes":"識字率 is made up of 識字 \"being able to read and write\" and 率 \"rate\" and is a technical term from statistics, development policy and history. Typical: 識字率が高い / 低い, 識字率が向上する, 識字率を上げる. Related is 識字教育 \"literacy education\". The older word 文盲 for illiteracy is considered discriminatory today; one says 非識字.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"教養課程では幅広い分野を学ぶ。","romaji":"Kyōyō katei de wa habahiroi bun'ya o manabu.","german":"In the general education course, you study a wide range of fields.","furigana":"きょうようかてい|はばひろ|ぶんや|まな"},{"japanese":"教養課程を終えると、三年生から専門課程に進む。","romaji":"Kyōyō katei o oeru to, sannensei kara senmon katei ni susumu.","german":"After finishing the general education course, you move on to the specialized course from the third year.","furigana":"きょうようかてい|お|さんねんせい|せんもんかてい|すす"},{"japanese":"東大では最初の二年間、全員が教養課程で学ぶ。","romaji":"Tōdai de wa saisho no ninenkan, zen'in ga kyōyō katei de manabu.","german":"At the University of Tokyo, everyone studies in the general education course for the first two years.","furigana":"とうだい|さいしょ|にねんかん|ぜんいん|きょうようかてい|まな"}],"notes":"教養課程 is the general-education stage of the first one or two years at Japanese universities, in which courses from many areas are taken before moving on to specialized study (専門課程). 教養 here means \"general education\", 課程 \"stage of study\". The corresponding courses are called 教養科目 or 一般教養, in student slang abbreviated 般教. Typical: 教養課程で学ぶ, 教養課程を終える.","levelNote":"Compound: 教養 (N2) + 課程 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"副専攻として哲学を選んだ。","romaji":"Fukusenkō toshite tetsugaku o eranda.","german":"Als Nebenfach wählte ich Philosophie.","furigana":"ふくせんこう|てつがく|えら"},{"japanese":"主専攻は経済学で、副専攻は日本語です。","romaji":"Shusenkō wa keizaigaku de, fukusenkō wa Nihongo desu.","german":"Mein Hauptfach ist Wirtschaftswissenschaft, mein Nebenfach Japanisch.","furigana":"しゅせんこう|けいざいがく|ふくせんこう|にほんご"},{"japanese":"日本の大学には副専攻制度がないところも多い。","romaji":"Nihon no daigaku ni wa fukusenkō seido ga nai tokoro mo ōi.","german":"Viele japanische Universitäten haben gar kein Nebenfachsystem.","furigana":"にほん|だいがく|ふくせんこうせいど|おお"}],"notes":"副専攻 ist das Nebenfach an der Universität; das Hauptfach heißt 主専攻 oder einfach 専攻. Typisch: 副専攻として〜を選ぶ / 取る, 副専攻制度. In Japan ist das System weniger verbreitet als in den USA oder Deutschland, viele Universitäten bieten es gar nicht an. Das Grundwort 専攻 kann auch als Verb stehen: 経済学を専攻する „Wirtschaft studieren“.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"学位論文の審査に三人の教授が当たる。","romaji":"Gakui ronbun no shinsa ni san-nin no kyōju ga ataru.","german":"Three professors are in charge of reviewing the dissertation.","furigana":"がくいろんぶん|しんさ|さんにん|きょうじゅ|あ"},{"japanese":"一月末までに学位論文を提出しなければならない。","romaji":"Ichigatsu-matsu made ni gakui ronbun o teishutsu shinakereba naranai.","german":"I have to submit my thesis by the end of January.","furigana":"いちがつまつ|がくいろんぶん|ていしゅつ"},{"japanese":"彼の学位論文は後に本として出版された。","romaji":"Kare no gakui ronbun wa nochi ni hon toshite shuppan sareta.","german":"His dissertation was later published as a book.","furigana":"かれ|がくいろんぶん|のち|ほん|しゅっぱん"}],"notes":"学位論文 is the umbrella term for any paper with which an academic degree is earned: 卒業論文 (卒論, bachelor's thesis), 修士論文 (修論, master's thesis) and 博士論文 (博論, doctoral dissertation). The word is formal and appears in examination regulations and library catalogs; in conversation people usually name the specific kind. Typical: 学位論文を提出する / 審査する / 執筆する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"口頭試問で研究内容について質疑応答した。","romaji":"Kōtō shimon de kenkyū naiyō ni tsuite shitsugi ōtō shita.","german":"In the oral examination, we had a question-and-answer session about the research content.","furigana":"こうとうしもん|けんきゅうないよう|しつぎおうとう"},{"japanese":"この学校の入試では、筆記試験のあとに口頭試問がある。","romaji":"Kono gakkō no nyūshi de wa, hikki shiken no ato ni kōtō shimon ga aru.","german":"In this school's entrance exam, there is an oral examination after the written exam.","furigana":"がっこう|にゅうし|ひっきしけん|こうとうしもん"},{"japanese":"博士論文の口頭試問を無事に終えて、ほっとした。","romaji":"Hakase ronbun no kōtō shimon o buji ni oete, hotto shita.","german":"I was relieved when I got through the oral defense of my dissertation.","furigana":"はかせろんぶん|こうとうしもん|ぶじ|お"}],"notes":"口頭試問 (\"oral examination questions\") is a formal term for an oral exam, especially the defense of a thesis or dissertation and the oral parts of entrance exams. Typical: 口頭試問を受ける / 行う, 口頭試問に合格する. More general is 口頭試験; 面接 is an interview or selection interview, 筆記試験 the written exam.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"答案用紙に名前を書き忘れた。","romaji":"Tōan yōshi ni namae o kakiwasureta.","german":"I forgot to write my name on the answer sheet.","furigana":"とうあんようし|なまえ|か|わす"},{"japanese":"時間内に答案を書き終えることができなかった。","romaji":"Jikan-nai ni tōan o kakioeru koto ga dekinakatta.","german":"I couldn't finish writing my answers within the time limit.","furigana":"じかんない|とうあん|か|お"},{"japanese":"来週の授業で答案が返却される。","romaji":"Raishū no jugyō de tōan ga henkyaku sareru.","german":"The graded exams will be returned in class next week.","furigana":"らいしゅう|じゅぎょう|とうあん|へんきゃく"}],"notes":"答案 is what one hands in in writing during an exam: the completed exam paper or the answers on it. The blank sheet is called 答案用紙. Typical: 答案を書く / 提出する / 採点する / 返却する, 模範答案 \"model answer\". To be distinguished from 解答 (the solution to a problem, also in an answer booklet) and 回答 (a reply to a survey or inquiry).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"百枚以上の答案を採点するのは大変だ。","romaji":"Hyaku-mai ijō no tōan o saiten suru no wa taihen da.","german":"It is tough to grade over a hundred exam papers.","furigana":"ひゃくまいいじょう|とうあん|さいてん|たいへん"},{"japanese":"あの先生は採点が厳しいことで有名だ。","romaji":"Ano sensei wa saiten ga kibishii koto de yūmei da.","german":"That teacher is famous for being a strict grader.","furigana":"せんせい|さいてん|きび|ゆうめい"},{"japanese":"フィギュアスケートの採点方法はとても複雑だ。","romaji":"Figyua sukēto no saiten hōhō wa totemo fukuzatsu da.","german":"The scoring method in figure skating is very complicated.","furigana":"さいてんほうほう|ふくざつ"}],"notes":"採点 is the awarding of points, that is, correcting and grading exams, but also the scoring by judges in sports or competitions. As a transitive する-verb: 答案を採点する. Typical: 採点が甘い / 厳しい \"to grade leniently / strictly\", 採点基準, 自動採点; karaoke machines have a 採点機能. 評価 is broader (overall assessment), 成績 the result.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"成績表を見て親が驚いた。","romaji":"Seiseki-hyō o mite oya ga odoroita.","german":"When the parents saw the report card, they were surprised.","furigana":"せいせきひょう|み|おや|おどろ"},{"japanese":"大学の成績表はウェブで確認できる。","romaji":"Daigaku no seiseki-hyō wa webu de kakunin dekiru.","german":"University grade reports can be checked online.","furigana":"だいがく|せいせきひょう|かくにん"},{"japanese":"塾から模試の成績表が送られてきた。","romaji":"Juku kara moshi no seiseki-hyō ga okurarete kita.","german":"The results sheet for the mock exam was sent from the cram school.","furigana":"じゅく|もし|せいせきひょう|おく"}],"notes":"成績表 is an overview of grades or results achieved. In elementary and middle schools the report card is usually called 通知表 (older 通信簿); 成績表 is used more generally and at universities. An officially certified transcript for applications, on the other hand, is a 成績証明書. Typical: 成績表をもらう / 見る / 確認する.","levelNote":"Compound: 成績 (N5) + 表 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"本試験に落ちたが追試で合格した。","romaji":"Hon shiken ni ochita ga tsuishi de gōkaku shita.","german":"I failed the main exam but passed the makeup exam.","furigana":"ほんしけん|お|ついし|ごうかく"},{"japanese":"病気で期末試験を休んだ学生は、追試を受けることができる。","romaji":"Byōki de kimatsu shiken o yasunda gakusei wa, tsuishi o ukeru koto ga dekiru.","german":"Students who missed the final exam because of illness can take a makeup exam.","furigana":"びょうき|きまつしけん|やす|がくせい|ついし|う"},{"japanese":"その実験結果は、ほかの研究者による追試で確認されなかった。","romaji":"Sono jikken kekka wa, hoka no kenkyūsha ni yoru tsuishi de kakunin sarenakatta.","german":"The experimental result was not confirmed in replication studies by other researchers.","furigana":"じっけんけっか|けんきゅうしゃ|ついし|かくにん"}],"notes":"追試 is the short form of 追試験 and in school designates a makeup exam for those who were absent; colloquially it is often also used for the retake exam after failing (more precisely 再試 / 再試験). Typical: 追試を受ける, 追試になる. In science, 追試 means the replication of an experiment by others (実験を追試する, 追試で確認される).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不合格になった科目を再履修しなければならない。","romaji":"Fugōkaku ni natta kamoku o sai-rishū shinakereba naranai.","german":"You must retake subjects that you failed.","furigana":"ふごうかく|かもく|さいりしゅう"},{"japanese":"再履修のクラスは一年生と一緒なので、少し気まずい。","romaji":"Sai-rishū no kurasu wa ichinensei to issho na node, sukoshi kimazui.","german":"In the retake class I sit with first-years, which is a bit awkward.","furigana":"さいりしゅう|いちねんせい|いっしょ|すこ|き"},{"japanese":"再履修の学生は優先的に履修登録ができる。","romaji":"Sai-rishū no gakusei wa yūsen-teki ni rishū tōroku ga dekiru.","german":"Students retaking a course can register for it with priority.","furigana":"さいりしゅう|がくせい|ゆうせんてき|りしゅうとうろく"}],"notes":"再履修 is a university term: 再 \"again\" + 履修 \"to take a course\", that is, to take a failed course once more. As a する-verb: 科目を再履修する; students say 再履 (さいり) for short. Distinguish from 留年 \"repeating a whole year\" and 追試 / 再試 (only the exam is repeated).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"教育勅語は戦前の教育の基本理念を示した。","romaji":"Kyōiku chokugo wa senzen no kyōiku no kihon rinen o shimeshita.","german":"The Imperial Rescript on Education set out the basic philosophy of prewar education.","furigana":"きょういくちょくご|せんぜん|きょういく|きほんりねん|しめ"},{"japanese":"教育勅語は一八九〇年に発布された。","romaji":"Kyōiku chokugo wa sen happyaku kyūjū-nen ni happu sareta.","german":"The Imperial Rescript on Education was promulgated in 1890.","furigana":"きょういくちょくご||はっぷ"},{"japanese":"戦後、教育勅語は国会の決議で効力を失った。","romaji":"Sengo, kyōiku chokugo wa kokkai no ketsugi de kōryoku o ushinatta.","german":"After the war, the Imperial Rescript on Education lost its force by a resolution of the Diet.","furigana":"せんご|きょういくちょくご|こっかい|けつぎ|こうりょく|うしな"}],"notes":"教育勅語 is the education edict issued in 1890 in the name of the Meiji Emperor (勅語 \"words of the Emperor\"). It stressed loyalty to the Emperor, filial piety and obedience and was solemnly read aloud in schools until 1945. In 1948 it was declared invalid by parliamentary resolutions. Today the word appears in historical texts and in political debates about moral education; it is a historical technical term.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"義務教育の年限を延長すべきだという意見がある。","romaji":"Gimu kyōiku no nengen o enchō subeki da to iu iken ga aru.","german":"There are opinions that the duration of compulsory education should be extended.","furigana":"ぎむきょういく|ねんげん|えんちょう|いけん"},{"japanese":"日本の義務教育は小学校と中学校の九年間だ。","romaji":"Nihon no gimu kyōiku wa shōgakkō to chūgakkō no kyū-nenkan da.","german":"Compulsory education in Japan is nine years, covering elementary and middle school.","furigana":"にほん|ぎむきょういく|しょうがっこう|ちゅうがっこう|きゅうねんかん"},{"japanese":"義務教育では教科書が無償で配られる。","romaji":"Gimu kyōiku de wa kyōkasho ga mushō de kubarareru.","german":"In compulsory education, textbooks are handed out free of charge.","furigana":"ぎむきょういく|きょうかしょ|むしょう|くば"}],"notes":"義務教育 is compulsory schooling; in Japan it covers nine years (6 years 小学校, 3 years 中学校). Legally, the obligation lies with the parents to send their children to school. Typical: 義務教育を受ける / 終える, 義務教育課程, 義務教育の無償. High school (高校) is not part of it, although almost everyone attends.","levelNote":"Compound: 義務 (N3) + 教育 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"高等教育の無償化が政策として検討されている。","romaji":"Kōtō kyōiku no mushō-ka ga seisaku toshite kentō sarete iru.","german":"Making higher education free of charge is being considered as a policy.","furigana":"こうとうきょういく|むしょうか|せいさく|けんとう"},{"japanese":"高等教育を受けた人の割合は年々増えている。","romaji":"Kōtō kyōiku o uketa hito no wariai wa nennen fuete iru.","german":"The proportion of people with higher education is increasing year by year.","furigana":"こうとうきょういく|う|ひと|わりあい|ねんねんふ"},{"japanese":"高等教育機関には大学のほか、短大や専門学校も含まれる。","romaji":"Kōtō kyōiku kikan ni wa daigaku no hoka, tandai ya senmon gakkō mo fukumareru.","german":"Institutions of higher education include, besides universities, junior colleges and vocational schools.","furigana":"こうとうきょういくきかん|だいがく|たんだい|せんもんがっこう|ふく"}],"notes":"高等教育 is tertiary education: universities, junior colleges (短大), colleges of technology (高専, from the 4th year) and vocational schools. Caution: despite its name, 高等学校 (高校) is the high school and belongs to 中等教育. The three levels are called 初等教育, 中等教育 and 高等教育. Typical: 高等教育を受ける, 高等教育機関, 高等教育の無償化.","levelNote":"Compound: 高等 (N2) + 教育 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この作家の散文は簡潔で美しい。","romaji":"Kono sakka no sanbun wa kanketsu de utsukushii.","german":"This author's prose is concise and beautiful.","furigana":"さっか|さんぶん|かんけつ|うつく"},{"japanese":"彼は詩人として出発したが、のちに散文に転じた。","romaji":"Kare wa shijin toshite shuppatsu shita ga, nochi ni sanbun ni tenjita.","german":"He started out as a poet but later turned to prose.","furigana":"かれ|しじん|しゅっぱつ|さんぶん|てん"},{"japanese":"彼の話はいつも散文的で、夢がない。","romaji":"Kare no hanashi wa itsumo sanbun-teki de, yume ga nai.","german":"What he says is always prosaic and without any dreams.","furigana":"かれ|はなし|さんぶんてき|ゆめ"}],"notes":"散文 is prose, that is, text without meter or syllable pattern, in contrast to 韻文. It often appears in literary criticism: 散文詩 \"prose poem\", 散文作品, 散文家. Derived from it is 散文的 \"prosaic\", figuratively also \"sober, unimaginative\". For a single novel or story, one says concretely 小説 or 随筆.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"韻文と散文の違いを理解することが大切だ。","romaji":"Inbun to sanbun no chigai o rikai suru koto ga taisetsu da.","german":"It is important to understand the difference between verse and prose.","furigana":"いんぶん|さんぶん|ちが|りかい|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"昔は歴史や法律も、覚えやすいように韻文で書かれることがあった。","romaji":"Mukashi wa rekishi ya hōritsu mo, oboeyasui yō ni inbun de kakareru koto ga atta.","german":"In the past, even history and laws were sometimes written in verse so that they would be easy to remember.","furigana":"むかし|れきし|ほうりつ|おぼ|いんぶん|か"},{"japanese":"この作品は韻文で書かれた悲劇だ。","romaji":"Kono sakuhin wa inbun de kakareta higeki da.","german":"This work is a tragedy written in verse.","furigana":"さくひん|いんぶん|か|ひげき"}],"notes":"韻文 is metrical language, that is, text with a fixed rhythm, syllable pattern or rhyme (韻 \"rhyme, sound\"). In Japanese poetry it relies less on rhyme than on syllable count, for example the 五七調 in 和歌 and 俳句. The opposite is 散文. The term is literary-scholarly and formal; in everyday speech one simply says 詩. \"To rhyme\" is 韻を踏む.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本の詩歌には四季の移ろいが詠まれている。","romaji":"Nihon no shiika ni wa shiki no utsuroi ga yomarete iru.","german":"In Japanese poetry, the changing of the four seasons is sung.","furigana":"にほん|しいか|しき|うつ|よ"},{"japanese":"彼女は幼いころから詩歌に親しんで育った。","romaji":"Kanojo wa osanai koro kara shiika ni shitashinde sodatta.","german":"She grew up familiar with poetry from early childhood.","furigana":"かのじょ|おさな|しいか|した|そだ"},{"japanese":"図書館の詩歌の棚で古い歌集を見つけた。","romaji":"Toshokan no shiika no tana de furui kashū o mitsuketa.","german":"On the poetry shelf of the library, I found an old poetry collection.","furigana":"としょかん|しいか|たな|ふる|かしゅう|み"}],"notes":"詩歌 (しいか, less often しか) is an elevated collective term for poetry: originally Chinese poems (漢詩) and Japanese song poetry (和歌), today lyric poetry in general including 短歌 and 俳句. It designates the genre, not a single poem; for that one says 詩 or 歌. Typical: 詩歌に親しむ, 詩歌を詠む, 詩歌集.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"万葉集には四千首以上の和歌が収められている。","romaji":"Man'yōshū ni wa yonsen-shu ijō no waka ga osamerarete iru.","german":"The Man'yōshū contains over four thousand waka poems.","furigana":"まんようしゅう|よんせんしゅいじょう|わか|おさ"},{"japanese":"和歌は五・七・五・七・七の三十一音から成る。","romaji":"Waka wa go, shichi, go, shichi, shichi no sanjūichi-on kara naru.","german":"A waka consists of 31 syllables in the pattern 5-7-5-7-7.","furigana":"わか|ご|しち|ご|しち|しち|さんじゅういちおん|な"},{"japanese":"平安時代の貴族は和歌を詠んで思いを伝え合った。","romaji":"Heian jidai no kizoku wa waka o yonde omoi o tsutaeatta.","german":"The aristocrats of the Heian period composed waka to convey their feelings to one another.","furigana":"へいあんじだい|きぞく|わか|よ|おも|つた|あ"}],"notes":"和歌 (\"Japanese song\") is the classical Japanese poetic form in contrast to the Chinese 漢詩; usually the 短歌 with 31 morae in the pattern 5-7-5-7-7 is meant. Poems are 詠む (よむ), not 書く, and counted with 首: 一首, 百人一首. Famous collections are the 万葉集 and the 古今和歌集. The modern form is called 短歌.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の抒情詩は繊細な感情を表現している。","romaji":"Kare no jojōshi wa sensai na kanjō o hyōgen shite iru.","german":"His lyric poetry expresses delicate emotions.","furigana":"かれ|じょじょうし|せんさい|かんじょう|ひょうげん"},{"japanese":"叙事詩が出来事を語るのに対し、抒情詩は詩人の心を歌う。","romaji":"Jojishi ga dekigoto o kataru no ni taishi, jojōshi wa shijin no kokoro o utau.","german":"While the epic narrates events, lyric poetry sings of the poet's heart.","furigana":"じょじし|できごと|かた|たい|じょじょうし|しじん|こころ|うた"},{"japanese":"若いころは恋の抒情詩をよく書いていた。","romaji":"Wakai koro wa koi no jojōshi o yoku kaite ita.","german":"When I was young, I often wrote love poems.","furigana":"わか|こい|じょじょうし|か"}],"notes":"抒情詩 is lyric poetry in the narrower sense: poems that express the feelings and moods of the poet (抒情 \"expression of feeling\"). Because 抒 is not a jōyō kanji, today it is often written 叙情詩. Opposites are 叙事詩 \"epic\" (narrates events) and 劇詩 \"dramatic poetry\". Related is the adjective 抒情的 / 叙情的 \"lyrical, emotional\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"芭蕉の紀行文は文学的価値が高い。","romaji":"Bashō no kikō-bun wa bungaku-teki kachi ga takai.","german":"Bashō's travel writings have high literary value.","furigana":"|きこうぶん|ぶんがくてきかち|たか"},{"japanese":"週末はよくテレビの紀行番組を見ている。","romaji":"Shūmatsu wa yoku terebi no kikō bangumi o mite iru.","german":"On weekends I often watch travel programs on TV.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|きこうばんぐみ|み"},{"japanese":"彼女はアジア各地を旅し、その体験を紀行にまとめた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa Ajia kakuchi o tabi shi, sono taiken o kikō ni matometa.","german":"She traveled around Asia and compiled her experiences into a travelogue.","furigana":"かのじょ|かくち|たび|たいけん|きこう"}],"notes":"紀行 (\"record of a journey\") is a literary term for travel accounts. It rarely stands alone; typical are 紀行文 \"travel prose\", 紀行番組 \"travel documentary on TV\" and titles like 〜紀行. The best-known example is Bashō's おくのほそ道. In everyday speech, one rather says 旅行記 or simply 旅の記録.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"シェイクスピアの戯曲は今なお上演されている。","romaji":"Sheikusupia no gikyoku wa ima nao jōen sarete iru.","german":"Shakespeare's plays are still performed today.","furigana":"ぎきょく|いま|じょうえん"},{"japanese":"彼女は小説だけでなく、戯曲も書いている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa shōsetsu dake de naku, gikyoku mo kaite iru.","german":"She writes not only novels but also plays.","furigana":"かのじょ|しょうせつ|ぎきょく|か"},{"japanese":"その小説は戯曲に書き直され、舞台で上演された。","romaji":"Sono shōsetsu wa gikyoku ni kakinaosare, butai de jōen sareta.","german":"The novel was rewritten as a play and performed on stage.","furigana":"しょうせつ|ぎきょく|か|なお|ぶたい|じょうえん"}],"notes":"戯曲 is a stage play as a literary text, that is, the written drama. The performance itself is called 劇 or 芝居; 脚本 is the script (also for film), 台本 the actors' working script. The author is a 劇作家. Typical: 戯曲を書く, 戯曲化する \"to dramatize\", 戯曲集.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"五年の歳月をかけて長編小説を書き上げた。","romaji":"Go-nen no saigetsu o kakete chōhen shōsetsu o kakiageta.","german":"He completed a full-length novel over five years.","furigana":"ごねん|さいげつ|ちょうへんしょうせつ|か|あ"},{"japanese":"監督にとって初めての長編アニメ映画が公開された。","romaji":"Kantoku ni totte hajimete no chōhen anime eiga ga kōkai sareta.","german":"The director's first feature-length animated film has been released.","furigana":"かんとく|はじ|ちょうへん|えいが|こうかい"},{"japanese":"長編よりも短編のほうが気軽に読めて好きだ。","romaji":"Chōhen yori mo tanpen no hō ga kigaru ni yomete suki da.","german":"I like short stories better than novels because they are easy to read.","furigana":"ちょうへん|たんぺん|きがる|よ|す"}],"notes":"長編 (also 長篇) designates a voluminous work and usually comes before the genre: 長編小説 \"novel\", 長編映画 \"feature film\", 長編アニメ. Used alone it usually means a novel: 初の長編. The gradation is 長編 – 中編 – 短編 – 掌編.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"短編集が文学賞の候補に挙がった。","romaji":"Tanpen-shū ga bungaku-shō no kōho ni agatta.","german":"A short story collection was nominated for a literary prize.","furigana":"たんぺんしゅう|ぶんがくしょう|こうほ|あ"},{"japanese":"通勤電車の中で短編小説を一つ読んだ。","romaji":"Tsūkin densha no naka de tanpen shōsetsu o hitotsu yonda.","german":"On the commuter train I read a short story.","furigana":"つうきんでんしゃ|なか|たんぺんしょうせつ|ひと|よ"},{"japanese":"彼の短編映画が国際映画祭で賞を取った。","romaji":"Kare no tanpen eiga ga kokusai eigasai de shō o totta.","german":"His short film won an award at an international film festival.","furigana":"かれ|たんぺんえいが|こくさいえいがさい|しょう|と"}],"notes":"短編 (also 短篇) denotes a short work and mostly stands before the genre: 短編小説 \"short story\", 短編映画 \"short film\", 短編集 \"story collection\". Used alone, it mostly means a short story. Opposites are 中編 and 長編; even shorter is 掌編.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"千字以内の掌編小説のコンテストに応募した。","romaji":"Sen-ji inai no shōhen shōsetsu no kontesuto ni ōbo shita.","german":"I entered a contest for flash fiction under a thousand characters.","furigana":"せんじいない|しょうへんしょうせつ|おうぼ"},{"japanese":"川端康成の『掌の小説』は掌編小説の代表作だ。","romaji":"Kawabata Yasunari no \"Tenohira no shōsetsu\" wa shōhen shōsetsu no daihyōsaku da.","german":"Kawabata Yasunari's \"Palm-of-the-Hand Stories\" is a representative work of flash fiction.","furigana":"|てのひら|しょうせつ|しょうへんしょうせつ|だいひょうさく"},{"japanese":"寝る前に掌編を一つ読むのが日課だ。","romaji":"Neru mae ni shōhen o hitotsu yomu no ga nikka da.","german":"It is my daily routine to read one flash fiction piece before bed.","furigana":"ね|まえ|しょうへん|ひと|よ|にっか"}],"notes":"掌編 (\"work the size of a palm\", 掌 \"palm of the hand\") is a very short story, often only one to a few pages; mostly as 掌編小説. A well-known example is Kawabata's collection 掌の小説. A similar, more entertaining term is ショートショート, coined by 星新一. The word is literary and rare; the gradation is 長編 – 中編 – 短編 – 掌編.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"宮沢賢治の童話は大人にも人気がある。","romaji":"Miyazawa Kenji no dōwa wa otona ni mo ninki ga aru.","german":"Miyazawa Kenji's children's stories are popular with adults too.","furigana":"みやざわけんじ|どうわ|おとな|にんき"},{"japanese":"寝る前に子どもに童話を読み聞かせる。","romaji":"Neru mae ni kodomo ni dōwa o yomikikaseru.","german":"I read my child a children's story before bedtime.","furigana":"ね|まえ|こ|どうわ|よ|き"},{"japanese":"『人魚姫』はアンデルセンの童話の一つだ。","romaji":"\"Ningyo-hime\" wa Anderusen no dōwa no hitotsu da.","german":"\"The Little Mermaid\" is one of Andersen's fairy tales.","furigana":"にんぎょひめ|どうわ|ひと"}],"notes":"童話 are stories for children, usually written by a single author: アンデルセン童話, グリム童話, the tales of 宮沢賢治. Traditional folktales such as 桃太郎 are more often called 昔話 or 民話. おとぎ話 also means \"fairy tale\" and is used figuratively for unrealistic ideas. Typical: 童話を読み聞かせる, 童話作家, 創作童話.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"口頭による伝承が文字化される前に消滅する恐れがある。","romaji":"Kōtō ni yoru denshō ga moji-ka sareru mae ni shōmetsu suru osore ga aru.","german":"There is a danger that oral traditions will disappear before they are written down.","furigana":"こうとう|でんしょう|もじか|まえ|しょうめつ|おそ"},{"japanese":"この村には、竜にまつわる伝承が今も残っている。","romaji":"Kono mura ni wa, ryū ni matsuwaru denshō ga ima mo nokotte iru.","german":"A legend about a dragon still survives in this village.","furigana":"むら|りゅう|でんしょう|いま|のこ"},{"japanese":"職人の技を若い世代に伝承するのは簡単ではない。","romaji":"Shokunin no waza o wakai sedai ni denshō suru no wa kantan de wa nai.","german":"Passing on the craftsmen's skills to the younger generation is not easy.","furigana":"しょくにん|わざ|わか|せだい|でんしょう|かんたん"}],"notes":"伝承 designates both the process of handing down from generation to generation (伝承する, transitive: 技を次の世代に伝承する) and the handed-down material itself, that is, legends and folk traditions (村に残る伝承). Typical combinations are 口頭伝承 \"oral tradition\", 民間伝承 \"folk tradition\" and 〜にまつわる伝承. Distinguish 伝説 \"legend\" (a concrete tale) and 伝統 \"tradition\" as a custom or practice; 伝承 emphasizes the passing on.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"東北地方の民話を語り継ぐ活動が行われている。","romaji":"Tōhoku chihō no minwa o katari-tsugu katsudō ga okonawarete iru.","german":"There are activities to keep telling the folktales of the Tōhoku region.","furigana":"とうほくちほう|みんわ|かた|つ|かつどう|おこな"},{"japanese":"祖母は寝る前によく民話を聞かせてくれた。","romaji":"Sobo wa neru mae ni yoku minwa o kikasete kureta.","german":"My grandmother often told me folktales before bed.","furigana":"そぼ|ね|まえ|みんわ|き"},{"japanese":"この絵本は、日本各地の民話をもとにしている。","romaji":"Kono ehon wa, Nihon kakuchi no minwa o moto ni shite iru.","german":"This picture book is based on folktales from all over Japan.","furigana":"えほん|にほんかくち|みんわ"}],"notes":"民話 is the collective term for folk narratives passed on orally among the people: fairy tales, legends, comic tales. In everyday speech one rather says 昔話 \"old story, fairy tale\" (those that begin with むかしむかし); 民話 sounds somewhat more factual and is used in folklore studies, libraries and educational projects. 童話, on the other hand, are children's stories, including ones written by authors such as Andersen's. Typical: 民話を語り継ぐ, 〜地方の民話, 民話集.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"歴史上の人物にまつわる逸話を集めた本を読んだ。","romaji":"Rekishi-jō no jinbutsu ni matsuwaru itsuwa o atsumeta hon o yonda.","german":"I read a book collecting anecdotes about historical figures.","furigana":"れきしじょう|じんぶつ|いつわ|あつ|ほん|よ"},{"japanese":"彼の優しさを物語る逸話が数多く残っている。","romaji":"Kare no yasashisa o monogataru itsuwa ga kazu ōku nokotte iru.","german":"Numerous anecdotes that speak of his kindness survive.","furigana":"かれ|やさ|ものがた|いつわ|かずおお|のこ"},{"japanese":"社長はスピーチで創業当時の逸話を披露した。","romaji":"Shachō wa supīchi de sōgyō tōji no itsuwa o hirō shita.","german":"In his speech, the company president shared an anecdote from the founding days.","furigana":"しゃちょう|そうぎょうとうじ|いつわ|ひろう"}],"notes":"逸話 is a little-known, often revealing anecdote about a person that is missing from official accounts (逸 \"escaped, hidden\"). Typical are 〜にまつわる逸話, 逸話が残る, 逸話を残す and 〜を物語る逸話 \"an anecdote that shows ...\". In conversation one mostly says エピソード, which also fits one's own experiences; 逸話 sounds more written and almost always refers to famous people or events. Do not confuse it with 挿話 \"inserted episode\" within a narrative.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"本筋とは関係のない挿話が物語に深みを与えている。","romaji":"Honsuji to wa kankei no nai sōwa ga monogatari ni fukami o ataete iru.","german":"Episodes unrelated to the main plot give the story depth.","furigana":"ほんすじ|かんけい|そうわ|ものがたり|ふか|あた"},{"japanese":"講演の途中に挟まれた挿話が聴衆の笑いを誘った。","romaji":"Kōen no tochū ni hasamareta sōwa ga chōshū no warai o sasotta.","german":"A story inserted in the middle of the lecture made the audience laugh.","furigana":"こうえん|とちゅう|はさ|そうわ|ちょうしゅう|わら|さそ"},{"japanese":"この長編小説には、主人公の少年時代を描いた挿話がいくつか含まれている。","romaji":"Kono chōhen shōsetsu ni wa, shujinkō no shōnen jidai o egaita sōwa ga ikutsu ka fukumarete iru.","german":"This novel contains several inserted episodes depicting the hero's boyhood.","furigana":"ちょうへんしょうせつ|しゅじんこう|しょうねんじだい|えが|そうわ|ふく"}],"notes":"挿話 (挿 \"to insert\") is a side story inserted into a larger narrative, speech or work that does not belong to the main thread (本筋). The word is literary and appears mainly in literary criticism and descriptions of works; in everyday speech one says エピソード. In contrast to 逸話, which means an anecdote about a person, 挿話 emphasizes the position within a larger whole. Typical: 挿話を挟む, 挿話が含まれる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"著名な批評家が序文を寄せた。","romaji":"Chomei na hihyō-ka ga jobun o yoseta.","german":"A famous critic contributed the preface.","furigana":"ちょめい|ひひょうか|じょぶん|よ"},{"japanese":"序文には、この本を書いたきっかけが述べられている。","romaji":"Jobun ni wa, kono hon o kaita kikkake ga noberarete iru.","german":"The preface describes how the author came to write this book.","furigana":"じょぶん|ほん|か|の"},{"japanese":"忙しい人は序文だけ読んでも、本の全体像がつかめる。","romaji":"Isogashii hito wa jobun dake yonde mo, hon no zentaizō ga tsukameru.","german":"Even busy people can grasp the overall picture of the book just by reading the preface.","furigana":"いそが|ひと|じょぶん|よ|ほん|ぜんたいぞう"}],"notes":"序文 is the preface at the beginning of a book. The common, less formal word is まえがき (also はしがき); 序文 sounds more elevated and is often used when someone other than the author writes the preface: 序文を寄せる \"to contribute a preface\". The counterpart is 跋文 or あとがき. In scholarly works the introductory chapter is rather called 序論 or 序章, not 序文.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"跋文には著者の執筆の動機が記されていた。","romaji":"Batsubun ni wa chosha no shippitsu no dōki ga shirusarete ita.","german":"The afterword recorded the author's motivation for writing.","furigana":"ばつぶん|ちょしゃ|しっぴつ|どうき|しる"},{"japanese":"恩師がこの句集に跋文を寄せてくれた。","romaji":"Onshi ga kono kushū ni batsubun o yosete kureta.","german":"My former teacher contributed an afterword to this haiku collection.","furigana":"おんし|くしゅう|ばつぶん|よ"},{"japanese":"跋文は本の最後に置かれ、序文と対になる文章だ。","romaji":"Batsubun wa hon no saigo ni okare, jobun to tsui ni naru bunshō da.","german":"The afterword is placed at the end of the book and is the counterpart to the preface.","furigana":"ばつぶん|ほん|さいご|お|じょぶん|つい|ぶんしょう"}],"notes":"跋文 is the afterword at the end of a book and the counterpart to 序文. The word is very literary; the character 跋 is known almost only from this word. It appears mainly in traditional works and in poetry and haiku collections (歌集, 句集), often written by a teacher or friend: 跋文を寄せる. In an ordinary book the afterword is called あとがき. It is not to be equated with \"epilogue\" as the closing part of a story (エピローグ).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"文庫版の巻末に解説が付されている。","romaji":"Bunko-ban no kanmatsu ni kaisetsu ga fusarete iru.","german":"An explanatory note is attached at the end of the paperback edition.","furigana":"ぶんこばん|かんまつ|かいせつ|ふ"},{"japanese":"専門家がニュースの背景をわかりやすく解説した。","romaji":"Senmonka ga nyūsu no haikei o wakariyasuku kaisetsu shita.","german":"An expert explained the background of the news in an easy-to-understand way.","furigana":"せんもんか|はいけい|かいせつ"},{"japanese":"野球中継では元選手が解説を担当している。","romaji":"Yakyū chūkei de wa moto senshu ga kaisetsu o tantō shite iru.","german":"In baseball broadcasts, a former player handles the commentary.","furigana":"やきゅうちゅうけい|もとせんしゅ|かいせつ|たんとう"}],"notes":"解説 is a knowledgeable explanation for an audience: commentary on news or sports broadcasts, explanatory texts in a museum, or the afterword essay in a paperback (巻末解説). As a transitive する-verb: 〜を解説する. 説明 is the general everyday word for \"to explain\" (also for directions or excuses); 解説 presupposes specialist knowledge. Derivatives: 解説者 \"commentator\", 解説書 \"handbook, companion book\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"古典作品には詳細な註釈が欠かせない。","romaji":"Koten sakuhin ni wa shōsai na chūshaku ga kakasenai.","german":"Detailed annotations are indispensable for classical works.","furigana":"こてんさくひん|しょうさい|ちゅうしゃく|か"},{"japanese":"この本は難しい語に註釈が付いているので読みやすい。","romaji":"Kono hon wa muzukashii go ni chūshaku ga tsuite iru node yomiyasui.","german":"This book is easy to read because difficult words come with notes.","furigana":"ほん|むずか|ご|ちゅうしゃく|つ|よ"},{"japanese":"多くの学者が源氏物語に註釈を加えてきた。","romaji":"Ōku no gakusha ga Genji monogatari ni chūshaku o kuwaete kita.","german":"Many scholars have added commentary to the Tale of Genji.","furigana":"おお|がくしゃ|げんじものがたり|ちゅうしゃく|くわ"}],"notes":"註釈 is the older spelling of 注釈; today one mostly writes 注釈, while 註 is still found in classical and scholarly texts. It means explanatory notes on a text, from individual word explanations to detailed commentary: 註釈を付ける, 註釈を加える, 註釈書. A footnote in the narrower sense is 脚注, a single note simply 注. Figuratively, 注釈 also means an explanatory remark: 注釈なしで \"without further explanation\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"校訂版では原文の誤りが修正されている。","romaji":"Kōtei-ban de wa genbun no ayamari ga shūsei sarete iru.","german":"In the critical edition, errors in the original text have been corrected.","furigana":"こうていばん|げんぶん|あやま|しゅうせい"},{"japanese":"江戸時代の写本を比べて本文を校訂する。","romaji":"Edo jidai no shahon o kurabete honbun o kōtei suru.","german":"He compares Edo-period manuscripts and establishes a critical text.","furigana":"えどじだい|しゃほん|くら|ほんぶん|こうてい"},{"japanese":"この全集は、専門家による厳密な校訂を経て出版された。","romaji":"Kono zenshū wa, senmonka ni yoru genmitsu na kōtei o hete shuppan sareta.","german":"This complete works edition was published after rigorous critical editing by specialists.","furigana":"ぜんしゅう|せんもんか|げんみつ|こうてい|へ|しゅっぱん"}],"notes":"校訂 is the philological work of producing the most correct text possible by comparing different manuscripts and editions; the result is called 校訂本 or 校訂版. Not to be confused with 校正 \"proofreading\" (finding printing errors), 校閲 \"review of content\", and the homophone 改訂 \"revision by the author\" (改訂版 \"revised edition\"). As a transitive する-verb: 本文を校訂する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"翻訳ではなく原典に当たることが研究の基本だ。","romaji":"Hon'yaku de wa naku genten ni ataru koto ga kenkyū no kihon da.","german":"Consulting the original source rather than the translation is the foundation of research.","furigana":"ほんやく|げんてん|あ|けんきゅう|きほん"},{"japanese":"いつかシェイクスピアを原典で読んでみたい。","romaji":"Itsuka Sheikusupia o genten de yonde mitai.","german":"Someday I'd like to read Shakespeare in the original.","furigana":"げんてん|よ"},{"japanese":"引用するときは必ず原典を明記してください。","romaji":"In'yō suru toki wa kanarazu genten o meiki shite kudasai.","german":"When quoting, please always state the original source.","furigana":"いんよう|かなら|げんてん|めいき"}],"notes":"原典 is the original text or the primary source on which translations, quotations or adaptations are based. Set phrases: 原典に当たる \"to consult the original source\", 原典で読む \"to read in the original\", 原典を明記する. Distinguish: 原作 is the source of a film adaptation or manga, 原文 the exact wording of a sentence in the original, 出典 the source citation of a quotation.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この全集は初版を底本としている。","romaji":"Kono zenshū wa shohan o teihon to shite iru.","german":"This complete works edition uses the first edition as its base text.","furigana":"ぜんしゅう|しょはん|ていほん"},{"japanese":"どの写本を底本に選ぶかで、本文が少し変わってくる。","romaji":"Dono shahon o teihon ni erabu ka de, honbun ga sukoshi kawatte kuru.","german":"Depending on which manuscript is chosen as the base text, the text changes slightly.","furigana":"しゃほん|ていほん|えら|ほんぶん|すこ|か"},{"japanese":"底本の誤りと思われる箇所には注を付けた。","romaji":"Teihon no ayamari to omowareru kasho ni wa chū o tsuketa.","german":"I added notes to passages that seem to be errors in the base text.","furigana":"ていほん|あやま|おも|かしょ|ちゅう|つ"}],"notes":"底本 is a technical term of textual scholarship: the edition or manuscript that serves as the textual basis for an edition, translation or copy. Typical is 〜を底本とする / 〜を底本にする. Beware the homophone 定本 (ていほん) \"authoritative, definitive edition\"; to avoid confusion, 底本 is sometimes read そこほん in specialist jargon. The rare meaning \"draft\" plays hardly any role in current usage.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"貴重な写本が図書館に所蔵されている。","romaji":"Kichō na shahon ga toshokan ni shozō sarete iru.","german":"Valuable manuscripts are held in the library.","furigana":"きちょう|しゃほん|としょかん|しょぞう"},{"japanese":"印刷技術が広まる前は、本は写本によって伝えられた。","romaji":"Insatsu gijutsu ga hiromaru mae wa, hon wa shahon ni yotte tsutaerareta.","german":"Before printing technology spread, books were transmitted by handwritten copies.","furigana":"いんさつぎじゅつ|ひろ|まえ|ほん|しゃほん|つた"},{"japanese":"修道士たちは何年もかけて聖書の写本を作った。","romaji":"Shūdōshi-tachi wa nannen mo kakete seisho no shahon o tsukutta.","german":"The monks spent years making copies of the Bible.","furigana":"しゅうどうし|なんねん|せいしょ|しゃほん|つく"}],"notes":"写本 is a hand-copied book, that is, a manuscript in the sense of a codex, in contrast to the printed book (刊本, 版本) and the original (原本). Before the spread of printing, texts were transmitted through 写本, which is why there are often several divergent 写本 of one work. An ordinary copy is called 写し or コピー; the manuscript of a present-day author is 原稿.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"作家の稿本には多くの推敲の跡が見られる。","romaji":"Sakka no kōhon ni wa ōku no suikō no ato ga mirareru.","german":"The writer's manuscript shows many traces of revision.","furigana":"さっか|こうほん|おお|すいこう|あと|み"},{"japanese":"作者自筆の稿本が古書店で見つかった。","romaji":"Sakusha jihitsu no kōhon ga kosho-ten de mitsukatta.","german":"The author's autograph manuscript was found in a used bookstore.","furigana":"さくしゃじひつ|こうほん|こしょてん|み"},{"japanese":"この稿本は出版されないまま、遺族の手元に残されていた。","romaji":"Kono kōhon wa shuppan sarenai mama, izoku no temoto ni nokosarete ita.","german":"This manuscript was never published and remained in the possession of the bereaved family.","furigana":"こうほん|しゅっぱん|いぞく|てもと|のこ"}],"notes":"稿本 is an elevated, technical word for the handwritten manuscript or draft of a work, especially when it has survived as a document. In everyday speech and in publishing, one says 原稿; a provisional draft is 草稿. Common in literary and archival contexts: 自筆稿本 \"autograph manuscript\". Do not confuse it with the homophone 校本 \"collated edition\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"何度も推敲を重ねて文章を練り上げた。","romaji":"Nando mo suikō o kasanete bunshō o neriageta.","german":"He refined the text through repeated revisions.","furigana":"なんど|すいこう|かさ|ぶんしょう|ね|あ"},{"japanese":"提出する前に、もう一度レポートを推敲しなさい。","romaji":"Teishutsu suru mae ni, mō ichido repōto o suikō shinasai.","german":"Polish your report once more before submitting it.","furigana":"ていしゅつ|まえ|いちど|すいこう"},{"japanese":"推敲の段階で、半分近くの文を削った。","romaji":"Suikō no dankai de, hanbun chikaku no bun o kezutta.","german":"During revision, I cut nearly half of the sentences.","furigana":"すいこう|だんかい|はんぶんちか|ぶん|けず"}],"notes":"推敲 is the repeated polishing of the wording of one's own text. The word goes back to the Chinese poet Jia Dao, who wavered for a long time over whether to write \"push the gate\" (推) or \"knock at the gate\" (敲) in his verse. Typical: 推敲を重ねる, 文章を推敲する (transitive). Distinguish 校正 \"proofreading for errors\" and 添削 \"correcting someone else's text\", for example by a teacher.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"三年がかりの大作がようやく脱稿した。","romaji":"San-nen-gakari no taisaku ga yōyaku dakkō shita.","german":"The magnum opus, three years in the making, is finally finished.","furigana":"さんねん|たいさく|だっこう"},{"japanese":"作家は締め切りの前日にやっと長編を脱稿した。","romaji":"Sakka wa shimekiri no zenjitsu ni yatto chōhen o dakkō shita.","german":"The writer finally finished her novel the day before the deadline.","furigana":"さっか|し|き|ぜんじつ|ちょうへん|だっこう"},{"japanese":"脱稿したら、しばらく旅行に行くつもりだ。","romaji":"Dakkō shitara, shibaraku ryokō ni iku tsumori da.","german":"Once the manuscript is finished, I plan to go on a trip for a while.","furigana":"だっこう|りょこう|い"}],"notes":"脱稿 (脱 \"to come out\", 稿 \"manuscript\") means to finish writing a manuscript, a word from the world of authors, journalists and scholars. It is used both transitively (長編を脱稿する) and intransitively (原稿が脱稿する). Related terms: 起稿 \"to begin writing\" and 入稿 \"to hand the manuscript to the publisher or printer\". In everyday speech one simply says 書き上げる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"研究の成果を一冊の本として上梓した。","romaji":"Kenkyū no seika o issatsu no hon toshite jōshi shita.","german":"I published my research results as a book.","furigana":"けんきゅう|せいか|いっさつ|ほん|じょうし"},{"japanese":"このたび、初めての小説を上梓いたしました。","romaji":"Konotabi, hajimete no shōsetsu o jōshi itashimashita.","german":"I am pleased to announce that my first novel has been published.","furigana":"はじ|しょうせつ|じょうし"},{"japanese":"先生は八十歳を記念して自伝を上梓された。","romaji":"Sensei wa hachijussai o kinen shite jiden o jōshi sareta.","german":"To commemorate his eightieth birthday, the professor published his autobiography.","furigana":"せんせい|はちじゅっさい|きねん|じでん|じょうし"}],"notes":"上梓 literally means \"to put on catalpa wood (梓)\", because printing blocks used to be cut from this wood; today it means, in an elevated way, \"to publish a book\". It is used for books, mostly in polite announcements about one's own works or those of respected persons: 〜を上梓する, 上梓いたしました. Neutral are 出版 and 刊行; 上梓 does not fit magazines or newspapers.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その本は絶版になっていて入手困難だ。","romaji":"Sono hon wa zeppan ni natte ite nyūshu konnan da.","german":"That book is out of print and hard to obtain.","furigana":"ほん|ぜっぱん|にゅうしゅこんなん"},{"japanese":"売れ行きが落ちて、その辞書は去年絶版になった。","romaji":"Ureyuki ga ochite, sono jisho wa kyonen zeppan ni natta.","german":"Sales dropped, and that dictionary went out of print last year.","furigana":"う|ゆ|お|じしょ|きょねんぜっぱん"},{"japanese":"絶版の本を古本屋でやっと見つけた。","romaji":"Zeppan no hon o furuhon'ya de yatto mitsuketa.","german":"I finally found the out-of-print book in a secondhand bookstore.","furigana":"ぜっぱん|ほん|ふるほんや|み"}],"notes":"絶版 is the state in which a book is no longer printed and the publisher has permanently taken it out of its list: 絶版になる, 絶版にする, 絶版の本. To be distinguished from it is 品切れ \"temporarily unavailable\" (often 品切れ重版未定). The term for revival is 復刊. For CDs and other products, one says 廃盤.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"読者の要望に応えて名著が復刊された。","romaji":"Dokusha no yōbō ni kotaete meicho ga fukkan sareta.","german":"In response to readers' requests, the classic was reissued.","furigana":"どくしゃ|ようぼう|こた|めいちょ|ふっかん"},{"japanese":"休刊していた雑誌が十年ぶりに復刊した。","romaji":"Kyūkan shite ita zasshi ga jūnen-buri ni fukkan shita.","german":"The magazine that had been suspended resumed publication after ten years.","furigana":"きゅうかん|ざっし|じゅうねん|ふっかん"},{"japanese":"絶版になった漫画の復刊を求める声が多い。","romaji":"Zeppan ni natta manga no fukkan o motomeru koe ga ōi.","german":"Many voices call for a reissue of the out-of-print manga.","furigana":"ぜっぱん|まんが|ふっかん|もと|こえ|おお"}],"notes":"復刊 means to bring out an out-of-print (絶版) book again, but also that a discontinued magazine or newspaper appears again. Transitive: 名著を復刊する; for periodicals also intransitive: 雑誌が復刊する. Distinguish 重版 or 再版 \"reprint of a title that is still available\" and 復刻 \"faithful new edition, facsimile\". Counterpart for periodicals: 休刊 \"temporary suspension\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"独自の文体を確立した作家として評価されている。","romaji":"Dokuji no buntai o kakuritsu shita sakka toshite hyōka sarete iru.","german":"He is acclaimed as a writer who established a style of his own.","furigana":"どくじ|ぶんたい|かくりつ|さっか|ひょうか"},{"japanese":"論文と手紙では、ふさわしい文体が違う。","romaji":"Ronbun to tegami de wa, fusawashii buntai ga chigau.","german":"A thesis and a letter call for different styles.","furigana":"ろんぶん|てがみ|ぶんたい|ちが"},{"japanese":"この作家の文体はリズムがあって読みやすい。","romaji":"Kono sakka no buntai wa rizumu ga atte yomiyasui.","german":"This author's style has rhythm and is easy to read.","furigana":"さっか|ぶんたい|よ"}],"notes":"文体 has two meanings: an author's personal writing style (独自の文体, 簡潔な文体) and the linguistic form of a text, such as 口語体/文語体 or です・ます体 and だ・である体. In the second meaning, one speaks of keeping the 文体 of a text consistent (文体を統一する). 作風 is the artistic style of a work as a whole (themes, atmosphere), not just the language; スタイル is more general and more colloquial.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"巧みな修辞を駆使した名文である。","romaji":"Takumi na shūji o kushi shita meibun de aru.","german":"It is a fine piece of writing that makes skillful use of rhetorical devices.","furigana":"たく|しゅうじ|くし|めいぶん"},{"japanese":"彼の演説は修辞に凝りすぎて、中身が伝わらない。","romaji":"Kare no enzetsu wa shūji ni korisugite, nakami ga tsutawaranai.","german":"His speech is so overloaded with rhetoric that the content doesn't come across.","furigana":"かれ|えんぜつ|しゅうじ|こ|なかみ|つた"},{"japanese":"修辞学は古代ギリシャで発達した。","romaji":"Shūjigaku wa kodai Girisha de hattatsu shita.","german":"Rhetoric as a discipline developed in ancient Greece.","furigana":"しゅうじがく|こだい|はったつ"}],"notes":"修辞 is the artful shaping of language, that is, stylistic devices and rhetorical turns; the discipline is called 修辞学, an individual device 修辞法. Typical: 修辞を駆使する, 修辞に凝る. In present-day usage レトリック is more common and can also mean pejoratively \"mere rhetoric, wordplay\". Technical term: 修辞疑問 \"rhetorical question\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"直喩は「ように」などの語を用いた表現法だ。","romaji":"Chokuyu wa \"yō ni\" nado no go o mochiita hyōgenhō da.","german":"Das Simile ist eine Ausdrucksform, die Wörter wie 'wie' verwendet.","furigana":"ちょくゆ|ご|もち|ひょうげんほう"},{"japanese":"国語の授業で、直喩と隠喩の違いを習った。","romaji":"Kokugo no jugyō de, chokuyu to in'yu no chigai o naratta.","german":"Im Japanischunterricht haben wir den Unterschied zwischen Vergleich und Metapher gelernt.","furigana":"こくご|じゅぎょう|ちょくゆ|いんゆ|ちが|なら"},{"japanese":"彼の詩は直喩が多くて、イメージがつかみやすい。","romaji":"Kare no shi wa chokuyu ga ōkute, imēji ga tsukamiyasui.","german":"Seine Gedichte enthalten viele Vergleiche, deshalb kann man sich die Bilder leicht vorstellen.","furigana":"かれ|し|ちょくゆ|おお"}],"notes":"直喩 ist der Fachbegriff der Rhetorik für den ausdrücklichen Vergleich, also ein Bild mit Vergleichswort wie まるで〜のようだ oder みたいだ: まるで雪のような肌. Das Gegenstück ist 隠喩 (auch 暗喩) „Metapher“, bei der das Vergleichswort fehlt; Oberbegriff für beides ist 比喩. Das Wort gehört in den Literatur- und Deutschunterricht (国語) und in Textanalysen; im Alltag sagt man eher einfach たとえ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"童話では動物に擬人法が多く用いられる。","romaji":"Dōwa de wa dōbutsu ni gijinhō ga ōku mochiirareru.","german":"In fairy tales, personification is often applied to animals.","furigana":"どうわ|どうぶつ|ぎじんほう|おお|もち"},{"japanese":"「風がささやく」は擬人法を使った表現だ。","romaji":"\"Kaze ga sasayaku\" wa gijinhō o tsukatta hyōgen da.","german":"\"The wind whispers\" is an expression that uses personification.","furigana":"かぜ|ぎじんほう|つか|ひょうげん"},{"japanese":"作文で擬人法を使うと、文章が生き生きする。","romaji":"Sakubun de gijinhō o tsukau to, bunshō ga ikiiki suru.","german":"If you use personification in an essay, the writing becomes more lively.","furigana":"さくぶん|ぎじんほう|つか|ぶんしょう|い|い"}],"notes":"擬人法 is personification as a stylistic device: human actions or feelings are attributed to things, animals or natural phenomena, for example 風がささやく or 花が笑う. One says 擬人法を使う／用いる. To be distinguished from it is 擬人化（する）, which means humanizing in general, for example with mascots or anime characters. 擬人法 belongs to the technical language of rhetoric and composition teaching.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"和歌の韻律は五七五七七の音数律に基づく。","romaji":"Waka no inritsu wa go-shichi-go-shichi-shichi no onsū-ritsu ni motozuku.","german":"The meter of waka is based on the 5-7-5-7-7 syllable pattern.","furigana":"わか|いんりつ|ごしちごしちしち|おんすうりつ|もと"},{"japanese":"翻訳では原詩の韻律を再現するのが難しい。","romaji":"Hon'yaku de wa genshi no inritsu o saigen suru no ga muzukashii.","german":"In translation, it is difficult to reproduce the meter of the original poem.","furigana":"ほんやく|げんし|いんりつ|さいげん|むずか"},{"japanese":"音声学では、イントネーションやアクセントを韻律と呼ぶ。","romaji":"Onseigaku de wa, intonēshon ya akusento o inritsu to yobu.","german":"In phonetics, intonation and accent are called prosody.","furigana":"おんせいがく|いんりつ|よ"}],"notes":"韻律 is, in poetry, the meter or rhythm of a poem, that is, the rules for syllable count, stress or rhyme. Japanese poetry such as waka and haiku is based on the number of morae (音数律), not on stress. In linguistics, 韻律 also means \"prosody\", that is, intonation, accent and rhythm of spoken language. Related: 押韻 \"rhyme\", 韻を踏む \"to rhyme\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"漢詩では押韻の規則が厳格に守られる。","romaji":"Kanshi de wa ōin no kisoku ga genkaku ni mamorareru.","german":"In Chinese poetry, the rules of rhyme are strictly observed.","furigana":"かんし|おういん|きそく|げんかく|まも"},{"japanese":"ラップでは押韻のうまさが評価される。","romaji":"Rappu de wa ōin no umasa ga hyōka sareru.","german":"In rap, skill at rhyming is appreciated.","furigana":"おういん|ひょうか"},{"japanese":"この詩は各行の終わりで押韻している。","romaji":"Kono shi wa kakugyō no owari de ōin shite iru.","german":"This poem rhymes at the end of each line.","furigana":"し|かくぎょう|お|おういん"}],"notes":"押韻 is the technical term for \"rhyme, rhyming\"; in everyday speech one says 韻を踏む \"to rhyme\". It occurs mainly in literary studies, in Chinese poetry (漢詩) and today in rap. Subtypes: 脚韻 \"end rhyme\" and 頭韻 \"alliteration\". As a する-verb it is mostly used intransitively (各行が押韻している). Classical Japanese poetry has hardly any rhymes; it relies on syllable count (韻律).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"句読点の打ち方で文章の印象が変わる。","romaji":"Kutōten no uchikata de bunshō no inshō ga kawaru.","german":"The way you punctuate changes the impression a text gives.","furigana":"くとうてん|う|かた|ぶんしょう|いんしょう|か"},{"japanese":"小学生の作文は句読点が少なくて読みにくい。","romaji":"Shōgakusei no sakubun wa kutōten ga sukunakute yominikui.","german":"Elementary school pupils' compositions have little punctuation and are hard to read.","furigana":"しょうがくせい|さくぶん|くとうてん|すく|よ"},{"japanese":"最近、句読点のないメッセージを送る若者が増えている。","romaji":"Saikin, kutōten no nai messēji o okuru wakamono ga fuete iru.","german":"Recently, more and more young people send messages without any punctuation.","furigana":"さいきん|くとうてん|おく|わかもの|ふ"}],"notes":"句読点 is the umbrella term for Japanese punctuation marks: 句点 \"。\" (end of sentence) and 読点 \"、\" (comma); they are put in place with 打つ or 付ける: 句読点を打つ. Note the reading: 読 is read とう here. In a broader sense the word can also include other punctuation marks. In some technical and official documents, the marks \"．，\" are used instead of \"。、\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"詳細な書誌情報を付した研究書を出版した。","romaji":"Shōsai na shoshi jōhō o fushita kenkyū-sho o shuppan shita.","german":"I published a research book with detailed bibliographic information attached.","furigana":"しょうさい|しょしじょうほう|ふ|けんきゅうしょ|しゅっぱん"},{"japanese":"図書館の検索システムで、本の書誌情報を確認した。","romaji":"Toshokan no kensaku shisutemu de, hon no shoshi jōhō o kakunin shita.","german":"I checked the book's bibliographic information in the library search system.","furigana":"としょかん|けんさく|ほん|しょしじょうほう|かくにん"},{"japanese":"彼は漱石研究の書誌をまとめた。","romaji":"Kare wa Sōseki kenkyū no shoshi o matometa.","german":"He compiled a bibliography of Sōseki studies.","furigana":"かれ|そうせきけんきゅう|しょし"}],"notes":"書誌 designates a bibliography in the sense of a list of books on an author or topic, but also the description of books themselves; the discipline is called 書誌学. It most often appears in compounds such as 書誌情報 or 書誌データ \"bibliographic data\" (author, title, publisher, year), for example in library catalogs. The list of references at the end of a paper, by contrast, is called 参考文献.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の蔵書は一万冊を超える。","romaji":"Kare no zōsho wa ichiman-satsu o koeru.","german":"His book collection exceeds ten thousand volumes.","furigana":"かれ|ぞうしょ|いちまんさつ|こ"},{"japanese":"この図書館は約五十万冊の蔵書を誇る。","romaji":"Kono toshokan wa yaku gojūman-satsu no zōsho o hokoru.","german":"This library boasts a collection of about 500,000 volumes.","furigana":"としょかん|やくごじゅうまんさつ|ぞうしょ|ほこ"},{"japanese":"引っ越しのたびに蔵書の多さに悩まされる。","romaji":"Hikkoshi no tabi ni zōsho no ōsa ni nayamasareru.","german":"Every time I move, I'm troubled by how many books I own.","furigana":"ひ|こ|ぞうしょ|おお|なや"}],"notes":"蔵書 are the books owned by a person or an institution, both a private book collection and the holdings of a library. Typical: 蔵書が〜冊ある, 〜冊の蔵書を誇る, 蔵書を整理する. Derivatives: 蔵書家 \"book collector\", 蔵書印 \"ownership stamp\", 蔵書検索 \"catalog search\". The word is neutral to elevated; in everyday speech one also simply says 持っている本.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"古書に旧所有者の蔵書印が押されていた。","romaji":"Kosho ni kyū-shoyūsha no zōsho-in ga osarete ita.","german":"The secondhand book bore the ownership stamp of its former owner.","furigana":"こしょ|きゅうしょゆうしゃ|ぞうしょいん|お"},{"japanese":"図書館の本には、どれも蔵書印が押してある。","romaji":"Toshokan no hon ni wa, dore mo zōsho-in ga oshite aru.","german":"All the books in the library carry a library stamp.","furigana":"としょかん|ほん|ぞうしょいん|お"},{"japanese":"祖父は自分で彫った蔵書印を愛用していた。","romaji":"Sofu wa jibun de hotta zōsho-in o aiyō shite ita.","german":"My grandfather loved to use an ownership stamp he had carved himself.","furigana":"そふ|じぶん|ほ|ぞうしょいん|あいよう"}],"notes":"蔵書印 is a stamp with which an owner or a library marks books as its property; it is pressed with 押す: 蔵書印を押す. A pasted-in bookplate sheet, by contrast, is called 蔵書票. In book research, 蔵書印 are important because they show whose hands an old book has passed through. The word belongs to the technical language of libraries and antiquarian bookshops.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"文庫本は手頃な価格で名作が読める。","romaji":"Bunko-bon wa tegoro na kakaku de meisaku ga yomeru.","german":"With pocket paperbacks, you can read masterpieces at affordable prices.","furigana":"ぶんこぼん|てごろ|かかく|めいさく|よ"},{"japanese":"教室の後ろに学級文庫がある。","romaji":"Kyōshitsu no ushiro ni gakkyū bunko ga aru.","german":"There is a small class library at the back of the classroom.","furigana":"きょうしつ|うし|がっきゅうぶんこ"},{"japanese":"あの小説は来月文庫化されるそうだ。","romaji":"Ano shōsetsu wa raigetsu bunko-ka sareru sō da.","german":"That novel is said to come out as a paperback next month.","furigana":"しょうせつ|らいげつぶんこか"}],"notes":"文庫 has two main meanings: a book collection or library (学級文庫 \"class library\", also in names like 東洋文庫) and, as the short form of 文庫本, the small Japanese paperback in A6 format. Publishers' series are also called this, for example 岩波文庫. 文庫化 means that a book first published in hardcover (単行本) comes out as a paperback. 新書 is another, somewhat taller format, mostly for nonfiction.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"夏目漱石の全集が新装版で発売された。","romaji":"Natsume Sōseki no zenshū ga shinsō-ban de hatsubai sareta.","german":"A new-format edition of Natsume Sōseki's complete works has been released.","furigana":"なつめそうせき|ぜんしゅう|しんそうばん|はつばい"},{"japanese":"大学の図書館で宮沢賢治全集を借りた。","romaji":"Daigaku no toshokan de Miyazawa Kenji zenshū o karita.","german":"I borrowed the complete works of Miyazawa Kenji from the university library.","furigana":"だいがく|としょかん|みやざわけんじぜんしゅう|か"},{"japanese":"このCDは、ベートーヴェンの交響曲全集だ。","romaji":"Kono shīdī wa, Bētōven no kōkyōkyoku zenshū da.","german":"This CD is the complete set of Beethoven's symphonies.","furigana":"こうきょうきょくぜんしゅう"}],"notes":"全集 is the complete edition of all of an author's works (漱石全集) or a comprehensive series on a subject (世界文学全集, 美術全集); it often stands as a suffix directly after the name. By contrast, 選集 contains only a selection, and 著作集 contains collected writings without claiming to be complete. In music, too, one speaks of e.g. 交響曲全集 \"the complete symphonies\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"現代詩の選集に彼女の作品も収録された。","romaji":"Gendai-shi no senshū ni kanojo no sakuhin mo shūroku sareta.","german":"Her work was also included in the anthology of modern poetry.","furigana":"げんだいし|せんしゅう|かのじょ|さくひん|しゅうろく"},{"japanese":"全集は高すぎるので、まず選集から読み始めた。","romaji":"Zenshū wa takasugiru node, mazu senshū kara yomihajimeta.","german":"The complete works were too expensive, so I started with a selected edition first.","furigana":"ぜんしゅう|たか|せんしゅう|よ|はじ"},{"japanese":"没後五十年を記念して、詩人の選集が編まれた。","romaji":"Botsugo gojūnen o kinen shite, shijin no senshū ga amareta.","german":"To mark the fiftieth anniversary of his death, a selection of the poet's works was compiled.","furigana":"ぼつごごじゅうねん|きねん|しじん|せんしゅう|あ"}],"notes":"選集 is a selected edition: chosen works of one author (〜選集) or an anthology on a theme. The counterpart is 全集 \"complete works\"; collections by several authors are now often also called アンソロジー. Typical: 選集を編む \"to compile a selection\", 選集に収録される. Caution: 選集 sounds exactly like 先週 \"last week\"; in classical texts the spelling 撰集 is also found.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"古今和歌集は代表的な詞華集である。","romaji":"Kokin Wakashū wa daihyō-teki na shikashū de aru.","german":"Das Kokin Wakashū ist eine repräsentative poetische Anthologie.","furigana":"こきんわかしゅう|だいひょうてき|しかしゅう"},{"japanese":"この詞華集には、百人の詩人の名作が収められている。","romaji":"Kono shikashū ni wa, hyakunin no shijin no meisaku ga osamerarete iru.","german":"Diese Anthologie versammelt Meisterwerke von hundert Dichtern.","furigana":"しかしゅう|ひゃくにん|しじん|めいさく|おさ"},{"japanese":"英詩の詞華集を一冊、旅に持って行った。","romaji":"Eishi no shikashū o issatsu, tabi ni motte itta.","german":"Ich habe eine Anthologie englischer Lyrik mit auf die Reise genommen.","furigana":"えいし|しかしゅう|いっさつ|たび|も|い"}],"notes":"詞華集 (詞華 „schöne Worte, Blüten der Sprache“) ist ein sehr literarisches Wort für eine Gedicht- oder Textanthologie, die besonders gelungene Stücke versammelt – wie das deutsche „Blütenlese“ oder lateinisch „Florilegium“. Es kommt vor allem in Literaturgeschichte und Übersetzungstiteln vor. Im heutigen Alltag sagt man アンソロジー oder 選集; für japanische Gedichtsammlungen sind 歌集 und 句集 üblich.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"処女歌集が高い評価を受けた。","romaji":"Shojo kashū ga takai hyōka o uketa.","german":"Her first poetry collection received high praise.","furigana":"しょじょかしゅう|たか|ひょうか|う"},{"japanese":"万葉集は日本最古の歌集だ。","romaji":"Man'yōshū wa Nihon saiko no kashū da.","german":"The Man'yōshū is Japan's oldest waka collection.","furigana":"まんようしゅう|にほんさいこ|かしゅう"},{"japanese":"音楽の授業で、歌集を見ながら合唱した。","romaji":"Ongaku no jugyō de, kashū o minagara gasshō shita.","german":"In music class, we sang in chorus while looking at the songbook.","furigana":"おんがく|じゅぎょう|かしゅう|み|がっしょう"}],"notes":"歌集 is above all a collection of 和歌 or 短歌 (poems in the 5-7-5-7-7 pattern), whether a classical anthology such as the 万葉集 or the volume of a modern tanka poet. To be distinguished: 句集 \"haiku collection\" and 詩集 \"poetry volume\" with free, modern verse. In addition, 歌集 can designate a songbook with lyrics and music, for example at school. 処女歌集 means \"one's first own tanka volume\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"句集を出版するのが俳人の夢だ。","romaji":"Kushū o shuppan suru no ga haijin no yume da.","german":"Publishing a haiku collection is the dream of every haiku poet.","furigana":"くしゅう|しゅっぱん|はいじん|ゆめ"},{"japanese":"芭蕉の句集を読んで、季語を調べた。","romaji":"Bashō no kushū o yonde, kigo o shirabeta.","german":"I read Bashō's haiku collection and looked up the seasonal words.","furigana":"|くしゅう|よ|きご|しら"},{"japanese":"祖母は八十歳で初めての句集を自費出版した。","romaji":"Sobo wa hachijussai de hajimete no kushū o jihi shuppan shita.","german":"My grandmother self-published her first haiku collection at eighty.","furigana":"そぼ|はちじゅっさい|はじ|くしゅう|じひしゅっぱん"}],"notes":"句集 is a collection of haiku (俳句), each counted as one 句; senryū collections can also be called this. Haiku poets (俳人) typically publish their works as a 句集; one says 句集を出す, 句集を編む. Counterparts are 歌集 for tanka and 詩集 for modern poetry. Many amateur poets bring out their 句集 through self-publishing (自費出版).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"作家の書簡集から創作の過程がうかがえる。","romaji":"Sakka no shokan-shū kara sōsaku no katei ga ukagaeru.","german":"The author's collected letters offer a glimpse of the creative process.","furigana":"さっか|しょかんしゅう|そうさく|かてい"},{"japanese":"首相は大統領に書簡を送った。","romaji":"Shushō wa daitōryō ni shokan o okutta.","german":"The prime minister sent a letter to the president.","furigana":"しゅしょう|だいとうりょう|しょかん|おく"},{"japanese":"これは漱石が弟子に宛てた書簡だ。","romaji":"Kore wa Sōseki ga deshi ni ateta shokan da.","german":"This is a letter that Sōseki addressed to one of his disciples.","furigana":"そうせき|でし|あ|しょかん"}],"notes":"書簡 is the formal, literary word for \"letter\"; in everyday speech one says 手紙. It appears in diplomacy (首相に書簡を送る, 公開書簡 \"open letter\"), in literary studies (書簡集 \"collection of letters\", 書簡体小説 \"epistolary novel\") and in official contexts. A personal letter from a head of state is called 親書. One says 書簡を送る, 書簡を宛てる, 書簡を交わす.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"土佐日記は日記文学の嚆矢とされる。","romaji":"Tosa Nikki wa nikki bungaku no kōshi to sareru.","german":"The Tosa Diary is regarded as the pioneer of diary literature.","furigana":"とさにっき|にっきぶんがく|こうし"},{"japanese":"平安時代には、女性による日記文学が数多く書かれた。","romaji":"Heian jidai ni wa, josei ni yoru nikki bungaku ga kazu ōku kakareta.","german":"In the Heian period, many works of diary literature were written by women.","furigana":"へいあんじだい|じょせい|にっきぶんがく|かずおお|か"},{"japanese":"日記文学の魅力は、作者の心の動きが細かく描かれている点にある。","romaji":"Nikki bungaku no miryoku wa, sakusha no kokoro no ugoki ga komakaku egakarete iru ten ni aru.","german":"The appeal of diary literature lies in the fact that the movements of the author's heart are depicted in detail.","furigana":"にっきぶんがく|みりょく|さくしゃ|こころ|うご|こま|えが|てん"}],"notes":"日記文学 is a genre term in Japanese literary history for literarily shaped diaries, above all from the Heian period: 土佐日記, 蜻蛉日記, 紫式部日記, 更級日記. They were mostly written in kana, often by court ladies, and contain recollections, poems and partly invented elements; they are thus not mere records like a private 日記. The term appears mainly in school and literary studies.","levelNote":"Compound: 日記 (N5) + 文学 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本文学には私小説という独特のジャンルがある。","romaji":"Nihon bungaku ni wa shishōsetsu to iu dokutoku no janru ga aru.","german":"In der japanischen Literatur gibt es das eigentümliche Genre des Ich-Romans.","furigana":"にほんぶんがく|ししょうせつ|どくとく"},{"japanese":"彼の作品は、自身の貧しい生活を描いた私小説だ。","romaji":"Kare no sakuhin wa, jishin no mazushii seikatsu o egaita shishōsetsu da.","german":"Seine Werke sind Ich-Romane, die sein eigenes Leben in Armut schildern.","furigana":"かれ|さくひん|じしん|まず|せいかつ|えが|ししょうせつ"},{"japanese":"私小説は自然主義文学から生まれたと言われる。","romaji":"Shishōsetsu wa shizen shugi bungaku kara umareta to iwareru.","german":"Der Ich-Roman soll aus der naturalistischen Literatur hervorgegangen sein.","furigana":"ししょうせつ|しぜんしゅぎぶんがく|う|い"}],"notes":"私小説 (auch わたくししょうせつ gelesen) ist eine typisch japanische Gattung, die im frühen 20. Jahrhundert aus dem Naturalismus entstand: Der Autor schildert sein eigenes Leben, oft schonungslos und mit Schwächen und Skandalen, fast ohne Fiktionalisierung. Als Ausgangspunkt gilt 田山花袋『蒲団』. Anders als der deutsche Begriff „Ich-Roman“ nahelegt, ist nicht die Ich-Perspektive entscheidend, sondern die Gleichsetzung von Autor und Hauptfigur. Gegenbegriff im weiteren Sinn: 大衆文学.","levelNote":"Compound: 私 (N5) + 小説 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"純文学と大衆文学の境界は曖昧になっている。","romaji":"Jun bungaku to taishū bungaku no kyōkai wa aimai ni natte iru.","german":"The boundary between pure literature and popular literature is becoming blurred.","furigana":"じゅんぶんがく|たいしゅうぶんがく|きょうかい|あいまい"},{"japanese":"大衆文学の賞としては直木賞がよく知られている。","romaji":"Taishū bungaku no shō toshite wa Naoki-shō ga yoku shirarete iru.","german":"The Naoki Prize is the best-known award for popular literature.","furigana":"たいしゅうぶんがく|しょう|なおきしょう|し"},{"japanese":"時代小説や推理小説は大衆文学に分類される。","romaji":"Jidai shōsetsu ya suiri shōsetsu wa taishū bungaku ni bunrui sareru.","german":"Historical novels and detective novels are classified as popular literature.","furigana":"じだいしょうせつ|すいりしょうせつ|たいしゅうぶんがく|ぶんるい"}],"notes":"大衆文学 is literature for a broad audience with a focus on entertainment: historical novels, crime fiction, romance. The opposite is 純文学 \"pure, artistic literature\"; the most important prize for 大衆文学 is the 直木賞, for 純文学 the 芥川賞. Today in everyday speech one often says エンタメ小説; the older 通俗小説 sounds pejorative.","levelNote":"Compound: 大衆 (N1) + 文学 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"芥川賞は純文学の新人に贈られる賞だ。","romaji":"Akutagawa-shō wa jun bungaku no shinjin ni okurareru shō da.","german":"The Akutagawa Prize is an award given to new writers of pure literature.","furigana":"|じゅんぶんがく|しんじん|おく|しょう"},{"japanese":"純文学は難しそうで、なかなか手が出ない。","romaji":"Jun bungaku wa muzukashisō de, nakanaka te ga denai.","german":"Serious literature seems difficult, and I can hardly bring myself to try it.","furigana":"じゅんぶんがく|むずか|て|で"},{"japanese":"彼女は純文学と大衆小説の両方を書く作家だ。","romaji":"Kanojo wa jun bungaku to taishū shōsetsu no ryōhō o kaku sakka da.","german":"She is a writer who writes both serious literature and popular novels.","furigana":"かのじょ|じゅんぶんがく|たいしゅうしょうせつ|りょうほう|か|さっか"}],"notes":"純文学 designates artistically ambitious literature in which language and inner life matter more than plot and entertainment. The opposite is 大衆文学 or 大衆小説, or today エンタメ小説. The distinction is reflected in the prizes: the 芥川賞 is for 純文学, the 直木賞 for popular literature. Typical are 純文学作家 and 純文学を読む; the word sounds somewhat elevated and is often also used slightly ironically for \"heavy fare\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女のデビュー作は文壇に衝撃を与えた。","romaji":"Kanojo no debyū-saku wa bundan ni shōgeki o ataeta.","german":"Her debut work sent shock waves through the literary world.","furigana":"かのじょ|さく|ぶんだん|しょうげき|あた"},{"japanese":"彼は二十歳で文壇にデビューした。","romaji":"Kare wa hatachi de bundan ni debyū shita.","german":"He made his debut in the literary world at twenty.","furigana":"かれ|はたち|ぶんだん"},{"japanese":"その作家は文壇の付き合いを嫌い、田舎で暮らした。","romaji":"Sono sakka wa bundan no tsukiai o kirai, inaka de kurashita.","german":"The writer disliked socializing in literary circles and lived in the countryside.","furigana":"さっか|ぶんだん|つ|あ|きら|いなか|く"}],"notes":"文壇 designates the literary world as a social community of writers, critics and publishers, the literary scene. Typical: 文壇にデビューする, 文壇に登場する, 文壇の大御所 \"grand old figure of the literary scene\". Following the same pattern there are 画壇 (painting), 歌壇 (tanka) and 論壇 (journalism and commentary). The word sounds somewhat old-fashioned but is common in arts pages and literary history.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"明治の文豪たちは近代日本語の形成に貢献した。","romaji":"Meiji no bungō-tachi wa kindai Nihongo no keisei ni kōken shita.","german":"The literary giants of the Meiji era contributed to the formation of modern Japanese.","furigana":"めいじ|ぶんごう|きんだいにほんご|けいせい|こうけん"},{"japanese":"この旅館には、かつて多くの文豪が泊まった。","romaji":"Kono ryokan ni wa, katsute ōku no bungō ga tomatta.","german":"Many literary giants once stayed at this inn.","furigana":"りょかん|おお|ぶんごう|と"},{"japanese":"ロシアの文豪トルストイは『戦争と平和』を書いた。","romaji":"Roshia no bungō Torusutoi wa Sensō to Heiwa o kaita.","german":"The great Russian writer Tolstoy wrote \"War and Peace\".","furigana":"ぶんごう|せんそう|へいわ|か"}],"notes":"文豪 is an honorific expression for a great, universally recognized writer, usually a classic such as 夏目漱石 or トルストイ. It is hardly used for living authors or newcomers; neutral are 作家 or 小説家. Typical combinations: 明治の文豪, ロシアの文豪, 文豪ゆかりの宿 \"an inn associated with a great writer\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"生涯にわたって著述活動を続けた。","romaji":"Shōgai ni watatte chojutsu katsudō o tsuzuketa.","german":"He continued his writing activities throughout his life.","furigana":"しょうがい|ちょじゅつかつどう|つづ"},{"japanese":"彼は退職後、著述に専念している。","romaji":"Kare wa taishoku-go, chojutsu ni sennen shite iru.","german":"Since retiring, he has devoted himself to writing.","furigana":"かれ|たいしょくご|ちょじゅつ|せんねん"},{"japanese":"書類の職業欄に「著述業」と書いた。","romaji":"Shorui no shokugyō-ran ni chojutsugyō to kaita.","german":"I wrote \"writer\" in the occupation field of the form.","furigana":"しょるい|しょくぎょうらん|ちょじゅつぎょう|か"}],"notes":"著述 is the writing of books, especially nonfiction and scholarly books, as well as the resulting work. Typical: 著述活動 \"writing activity\", 著述に専念する, 著述業 as an occupational title \"freelance author\". 執筆 refers rather to the writing of a specific text, 著書 to an author's finished book. As a transitive する-verb (〜を著述する) it is rare; usually the noun is used.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"現在新作の執筆に取りかかっている。","romaji":"Genzai shinsaku no shippitsu ni torikakatte iru.","german":"I am currently starting work on a new book.","furigana":"げんざいしんさく|しっぴつ|と"},{"japanese":"この記事の執筆者は元新聞記者だ。","romaji":"Kono kiji no shippitsusha wa moto shinbun kisha da.","german":"The author of this article is a former newspaper reporter.","furigana":"きじ|しっぴつしゃ|もとしんぶんきしゃ"},{"japanese":"彼女は雑誌に毎月エッセイを執筆している。","romaji":"Kanojo wa zasshi ni maitsuki essei o shippitsu shite iru.","german":"She writes an essay for a magazine every month.","furigana":"かのじょ|ざっし|まいつき|しっぴつ"}],"notes":"執筆 is writing a text for publication - a novel, article, column - and sounds more formal than 書く. Typical: 〜の執筆に取りかかる, 執筆中, 原稿を執筆する, 雑誌に執筆する \"to write for a magazine\". Derivatives: 執筆者 \"author, writer\", 執筆活動. For private letters or notes, one does not say 執筆 but simply 書く.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"淡々とした筆致で日常を描いている。","romaji":"Tantan to shita hitchi de nichijō o egaite iru.","german":"He depicts everyday life in a detached style.","furigana":"たんたん|ひっち|にちじょう|えが"},{"japanese":"著者はユーモアあふれる筆致で失敗談をつづっている。","romaji":"Chosha wa yūmoa afureru hitchi de shippaidan o tsuzutte iru.","german":"The author recounts his failures in a style full of humor.","furigana":"ちょしゃ|ひっち|しっぱいだん"},{"japanese":"力強い筆致で書かれた書道作品が展示されている。","romaji":"Chikarazuyoi hitchi de kakareta shodō sakuhin ga tenji sarete iru.","german":"Calligraphy works written with powerful brushstrokes are on display.","furigana":"ちからづよ|ひっち|か|しょどうさくひん|てんじ"}],"notes":"筆致 is the \"touch\" of a work: originally the brushwork in calligraphy and painting, figuratively the tone and the way a text is written. A descriptive adjective almost always comes before it: 軽妙な筆致 \"light-footed style\", 淡々とした筆致, 力強い筆致, and usually で描く / でつづる follows. In contrast to 文体, which describes the linguistic form, 筆致 emphasizes the impression and attitude; the word is typical of reviews.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"病のため擱筆を余儀なくされた。","romaji":"Yamai no tame kakuhitsu o yogi naku sareta.","german":"Illness forced him to lay down his pen.","furigana":"やまい|かくひつ|よぎ"},{"japanese":"以上をもって擱筆する。","romaji":"Ijō o motte kakuhitsu suru.","german":"With this, I lay down my pen.","furigana":"いじょう|かくひつ"},{"japanese":"夜明け近くになって、ようやく擱筆した。","romaji":"Yoake chikaku ni natte, yōyaku kakuhitsu shita.","german":"It was only near dawn that he finally put down his pen.","furigana":"よあ|ちか|かくひつ"}],"notes":"擱筆 (擱 \"to put down\", 筆 \"brush\") means to lay down the pen, that is, to stop writing, mostly at the end of a letter, preface or essay: 以上をもって擱筆する \"with this I end my remarks\". More rarely it means to give up writing altogether. The word is very literary and intransitive; the opposite is 起筆. Distinguish: 脱稿 \"to finish a manuscript\" and 絶筆 \"last work before death\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"未完の絶筆が死後に出版された。","romaji":"Mikan no zeppitsu ga shigo ni shuppan sareta.","german":"The unfinished final work was published posthumously.","furigana":"みかん|ぜっぴつ|しご|しゅっぱん"},{"japanese":"この絵は画家の絶筆となった。","romaji":"Kono e wa gaka no zeppitsu to natta.","german":"This painting became the painter's last work.","furigana":"え|がか|ぜっぴつ"},{"japanese":"病床で書かれたこの手紙が、彼の絶筆となった。","romaji":"Byōshō de kakareta kono tegami ga, kare no zeppitsu to natta.","german":"This letter, written on his sickbed, became his last piece of writing.","furigana":"びょうしょう|か|てがみ|かれ|ぜっぴつ"}],"notes":"絶筆 is the last work that a writer, painter or calligrapher created before his death; the typical phrase is 〜が絶筆となる \"became his last work\". More rarely it means to give up writing altogether. Related are 遺稿 \"posthumous manuscripts\" and 遺作 \"last, often posthumously published work\"; 絶筆 emphasizes that nothing was produced afterwards.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"遺稿を整理して遺族が出版した。","romaji":"Ikō o seiri shite izoku ga shuppan shita.","german":"The bereaved family organized the posthumous manuscripts and published them.","furigana":"いこう|せいり|いぞく|しゅっぱん"},{"japanese":"書斎から未発表の遺稿が見つかった。","romaji":"Shosai kara mihappyō no ikō ga mitsukatta.","german":"An unpublished manuscript was found in the study.","furigana":"しょさい|みはっぴょう|いこう|み"},{"japanese":"没後、彼の遺稿集が刊行された。","romaji":"Botsugo, kare no ikōshū ga kankō sareta.","german":"After his death, a collection of his posthumous writings was published.","furigana":"ぼつご|かれ|いこうしゅう|かんこう"}],"notes":"遺稿 are manuscripts left behind by a deceased person that remained unpublished during his lifetime; collected, they appear as 遺稿集. Typical: 遺稿を整理する, 遺稿が見つかる, 遺稿を出版する. Distinguish: 遺作 \"last work\" (also paintings, films), 絶筆 \"the last thing written\". Beware, many homophones: 以降, 意向, 移行.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"スピーチの原稿を代筆してもらった。","romaji":"Supīchi no genkō o daihitsu shite moratta.","german":"I had someone ghostwrite the speech manuscript for me.","furigana":"げんこう|だいひつ"},{"japanese":"目の不自由な祖母に代わって、手紙を代筆した。","romaji":"Me no fujiyū na sobo ni kawatte, tegami o daihitsu shita.","german":"I wrote a letter on behalf of my visually impaired grandmother.","furigana":"め|ふじゆう|そぼ|か|てがみ|だいひつ"},{"japanese":"芸能人の自伝の多くは代筆だと言われている。","romaji":"Geinōjin no jiden no ōku wa daihitsu da to iwarete iru.","german":"Many celebrity autobiographies are said to be ghostwritten.","furigana":"げいのうじん|じでん|おお|だいひつ|い"}],"notes":"代筆 means to write something in place of another: a letter for someone who cannot write, a form, or a speech or a book as a ghostwriter. Transitive: 手紙を代筆する; one asks for it with 代筆を頼む or 代筆してもらう. For professional ghostwriting one also says ゴーストライター. With signatures and official documents, 代筆 can be legally delicate.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"詩の末尾で韻を踏むことで音楽的な効果が生まれる。","romaji":"Shi no matsubi de in o fumu koto de ongaku-teki na kōka ga umareru.","german":"Rhyming at the end of a poem creates a musical effect.","furigana":"し|まつび|いん|ふ|おんがくてき|こうか|う"},{"japanese":"この歌詞は韻を踏んでいて覚えやすい。","romaji":"Kono kashi wa in o funde ite oboeyasui.","german":"This song's lyrics rhyme, so they are easy to remember.","furigana":"かし|いん|ふ|おぼ"},{"japanese":"漢詩では、同じ韻の漢字を句末に置く。","romaji":"Kanshi de wa, onaji in no kanji o kumatsu ni oku.","german":"In Chinese poems, characters of the same rhyme class are placed at the end of lines.","furigana":"かんし|おな|いん|かんじ|くまつ|お"}],"notes":"韻 is rhyme and occurs almost always in the set phrase 韻を踏む \"to rhyme\"; the technical term for it is 押韻. In Chinese phonology, 韻 also designates the rhyming final of a character, according to which characters are divided into rhyme classes, which is important for 漢詩. In compounds, 韻 also stands for aftersound or mood: 余韻 \"lingering resonance\", 韻律 \"meter\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本文学史の講義を受講している。","romaji":"Nihon bungakushi no kōgi o jukō shite iru.","german":"I'm taking a lecture on the history of Japanese literature.","furigana":"にほんぶんがくし|こうぎ|じゅこう"},{"japanese":"この作品は文学史に残る傑作だ。","romaji":"Kono sakuhin wa bungakushi ni nokoru kessaku da.","german":"This work is a masterpiece that will go down in literary history.","furigana":"さくひん|ぶんがくし|のこ|けっさく"},{"japanese":"文学史の教科書で、作家と作品名を覚えた。","romaji":"Bungakushi no kyōkasho de, sakka to sakuhin-mei o oboeta.","german":"I learned authors and work titles from the literary history textbook.","furigana":"ぶんがくし|きょうかしょ|さっか|さくひんめい|おぼ"}],"notes":"文学史 is the history of literature as a subject and as an account: 日本文学史, 文学史の講義, 文学史の教科書. A common phrase is 文学史に残る \"to go down in literary history\" or 文学史に名を残す. The word is neutral and familiar from school; in Japanese language class in Japan, one learns authors, works and periods in the subject 文学史.","levelNote":"Compound: 文学 (N5) + 史 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"文芸批評の手法を学ぶことで作品理解が深まる。","romaji":"Bungei hihyō no shuhō o manabu koto de sakuhin rikai ga fukamaru.","german":"Learning the methods of literary criticism deepens one's understanding of works.","furigana":"ぶんげいひひょう|しゅほう|まな|さくひんりかい|ふか"},{"japanese":"彼は小説家であると同時に、鋭い文芸批評でも知られる。","romaji":"Kare wa shōsetsuka de aru to dōji ni, surudoi bungei hihyō de mo shirareru.","german":"He is a novelist and at the same time known for his sharp literary criticism.","furigana":"かれ|しょうせつか|どうじ|するど|ぶんげいひひょう|し"},{"japanese":"その文芸批評は、作品を社会の問題と結びつけて論じている。","romaji":"Sono bungei hihyō wa, sakuhin o shakai no mondai to musubitsukete ronjite iru.","german":"That critique discusses the work in connection with social problems.","furigana":"ぶんげいひひょう|さくひん|しゃかい|もんだい|むす|ろん"}],"notes":"文芸批評 is literary criticism as a discipline and as a text form that interprets and evaluates works. Synonymous and today somewhat more common is 文芸評論; the person who writes it is a 批評家 or 評論家. The review of a single new publication, on the other hand, is called 書評 \"book review\". Typical: 文芸批評の手法, 文芸批評を書く, 〜の文芸批評で知られる.","levelNote":"Compound: 文芸 (N2) + 批評 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"文脈から単語の意味を推測する。","romaji":"Bunmyaku kara tango no imi o suisoku suru.","german":"I infer the meaning of a word from its context.","furigana":"ぶんみゃく|たんご|いみ|すいそく"},{"japanese":"その発言は文脈を無視して切り取られた。","romaji":"Sono hatsugen wa bunmyaku o mushi shite kiritorareta.","german":"That remark was taken out of context.","furigana":"はつげん|ぶんみゃく|むし|き|と"},{"japanese":"日本の外交を歴史的な文脈の中で考える。","romaji":"Nihon no gaikō o rekishi-teki na bunmyaku no naka de kangaeru.","german":"I think about Japanese diplomacy within its historical context.","furigana":"にほん|がいこう|れきしてき|ぶんみゃく|なか|かんが"}],"notes":"文脈 is first of all the linguistic context of a text: 文脈から意味を推測する, 文脈に合わない. In a broader sense it means the background or circumstances of a matter: 歴史的な文脈, 〜という文脈で語られる. A common criticism is 文脈を無視して切り取る \"to take out of context\". Close in meaning are コンテキスト, 前後関係 and 脈絡 (more \"logical coherence\": 脈絡のない話).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"含蓄のある言葉に心を打たれた。","romaji":"Ganchiku no aru kotoba ni kokoro o utareta.","german":"I was moved by the meaningful words.","furigana":"がんちく|ことば|こころ|う"},{"japanese":"祖父の言葉は短いが含蓄に富んでいる。","romaji":"Sofu no kotoba wa mijikai ga ganchiku ni tonde iru.","german":"Grandfather's words are short but rich in meaning.","furigana":"そふ|ことば|みじか|がんちく|と"},{"japanese":"この詩は一見単純だが、含蓄が深い。","romaji":"Kono shi wa ikken tanjun da ga, ganchiku ga fukai.","german":"This poem seems simple at first glance, but it has great depth.","furigana":"し|いっけんたんじゅん|がんちく|ふか"}],"notes":"含蓄 is the deeper, unspoken content of words or texts, what resonates between the lines. It is positive and appears almost always in fixed combinations: 含蓄のある言葉 \"suggestive, meaningful words\", 含蓄に富む, 含蓄が深い. To be distinguished is 含み (含みのある言い方), which rather means a hidden intention or hint. The word is literary and elevated.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この物語には政治的な寓意が隠されている。","romaji":"Kono monogatari ni wa seiji-teki na gūi ga kakusarete iru.","german":"A political allegory is hidden in this story.","furigana":"ものがたり|せいじてき|ぐうい|かく"},{"japanese":"イソップ物語の一つ一つには、教訓的な寓意が込められている。","romaji":"Isoppu monogatari no hitotsu hitotsu ni wa, kyōkun-teki na gūi ga komerarete iru.","german":"Each of Aesop's fables carries an instructive moral.","furigana":"ものがたり|ひと|ひと|きょうくんてき|ぐうい|こ"},{"japanese":"その絵に描かれた砂時計は、人生の短さを表す寓意だと言われている。","romaji":"Sono e ni egakareta sunadokei wa, jinsei no mijikasa o arawasu gūi da to iwarete iru.","german":"The hourglass painted in the picture is said to be an allegory of the brevity of life.","furigana":"え|えが|すなどけい|じんせい|みじか|あらわ|ぐうい|い"}],"notes":"寓意 is the real message hidden in a story, picture or symbol: \"allegory\", \"moral\", \"allegorical meaning\". Typical are 寓意が込められている／隠されている, 寓意を読み取る and 寓意的な作品. Related is 寓話 \"fable, parable\" as a text genre; 比喩 is any figurative expression (metaphor, simile), without a lesson necessarily lying behind it. Literary, especially in literary and art criticism.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"諧謔に富んだ文章が読者を楽しませる。","romaji":"Kaigyaku ni tonda bunshō ga dokusha o tanoshimaseru.","german":"Writing full of wit entertains readers.","furigana":"かいぎゃく|と|ぶんしょう|どくしゃ|たの"},{"japanese":"漱石の小説には、鋭い社会批判の中にも諧謔がちりばめられている。","romaji":"Sōseki no shōsetsu ni wa, surudoi shakai hihan no naka ni mo kaigyaku ga chiribamerarete iru.","german":"In Sōseki's novels, even sharp social criticism is studded with humor.","furigana":"そうせき|しょうせつ|するど|しゃかいひはん|なか|かいぎゃく"},{"japanese":"彼は諧謔を交えたスピーチで会場を和ませた。","romaji":"Kare wa kaigyaku o majieta supīchi de kaijō o nagomaseta.","german":"He lightened the mood in the hall with a speech laced with wit.","furigana":"かれ|かいぎゃく|まじ|かいじょう|なご"}],"notes":"諧謔 is an elevated, literary expression for witty jokes, banter and humorous irony. Typical are 諧謔に富む, 諧謔を交える, 諧謔精神 and 諧謔的な文体. In everyday speech one says ユーモア or 冗談 instead; 諧謔 appears mainly in literary criticism and demanding texts and sounds more intellectual, often with a touch of self-irony or satire. It is practically always written in kanji.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"哀愁を帯びた旋律が作品の雰囲気を醸し出している。","romaji":"Aishū o obita senritsu ga sakuhin no fun'iki o kamoshidashite iru.","german":"A melancholy melody creates the atmosphere of the work.","furigana":"あいしゅう|お|せんりつ|さくひん|ふんいき|かも|だ"},{"japanese":"夕暮れの港町には、どこか哀愁が漂っている。","romaji":"Yūgure no minatomachi ni wa, dokoka aishū ga tadayotte iru.","german":"A certain wistfulness hangs over the harbor town at dusk.","furigana":"ゆうぐ|みなとまち|あいしゅう|ただよ"},{"japanese":"彼の背中には、定年を迎えた男の哀愁が感じられた。","romaji":"Kare no senaka ni wa, teinen o mukaeta otoko no aishū ga kanjirareta.","german":"His back conveyed the pathos of a man who had just reached retirement.","furigana":"かれ|せなか|ていねん|むか|おとこ|あいしゅう|かん"}],"notes":"哀愁 is a quiet, wistful sadness that is perceived more as a mood or aura than as acute pain. Typical combinations are 哀愁が漂う, 哀愁を帯びる, 哀愁を感じる and 哀愁のある声. Unlike 悲しみ (concrete grief), 哀愁 often has an aesthetic, almost beautiful overtone; 郷愁 is specifically longing for home or the past. Somewhat elevated, used in descriptions of music, film and moods; jokingly also 背中に哀愁が漂う about tired men.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"叙情豊かな詩が読者の心に響く。","romaji":"Jojō yutaka na shi ga dokusha no kokoro ni hibiku.","german":"Poems rich in lyrical feeling resonate in readers' hearts.","furigana":"じょじょうゆた|し|どくしゃ|こころ|ひび"},{"japanese":"彼女の歌は、叙情的なメロディーで多くのファンを魅了している。","romaji":"Kanojo no uta wa, jojō-teki na merodī de ōku no fan o miryō shite iru.","german":"Her songs captivate many fans with their lyrical melodies.","furigana":"かのじょ|うた|じょじょうてき|おお|みりょう"},{"japanese":"授業で、叙事詩と叙情詩の違いについて学んだ。","romaji":"Jugyō de, jojishi to jojōshi no chigai ni tsuite mananda.","german":"In class, we learned about the difference between epic and lyric poetry.","furigana":"じゅぎょう|じょじし|じょじょうし|ちが|まな"}],"notes":"叙情 designates the expression of personal feelings and moods in poetry, music or painting, the \"lyrical\" in the sense of the emotional. It mostly appears in compounds: 叙情詩 \"lyric poem\", 叙情的 \"lyrical, emotional\", 叙情豊か. The opposite is 叙事 \"factual narration of events\" (叙事詩 \"epic\"). The original spelling is 抒情; 叙情 is the replacement spelling in use today; both are correct. Literary, from literary and art criticism.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"複雑な構文を正確に分析する能力が求められる。","romaji":"Fukuzatsu na kōbun o seikaku ni bunseki suru nōryoku ga motomerareru.","german":"The ability to accurately analyze complex sentence structures is required.","furigana":"ふくざつ|こうぶん|せいかく|ぶんせき|のうりょく|もと"},{"japanese":"受験英語では、重要な構文を例文ごと覚えることが多い。","romaji":"Juken eigo de wa, jūyō na kōbun o reibun goto oboeru koto ga ōi.","german":"In exam English, you often memorize important constructions together with example sentences.","furigana":"じゅけんえいご|じゅうよう|こうぶん|れいぶん|おぼ|おお"},{"japanese":"プログラムが動かないのは、構文エラーがあるからだ。","romaji":"Puroguramu ga ugokanai no wa, kōbun erā ga aru kara da.","german":"The program doesn't run because it contains a syntax error.","furigana":"うご|こうぶん"}],"notes":"構文 is the structure of a sentence or a fixed grammatical construction. In school teaching, 構文 often means sentence patterns such as \"so ... that\" in English (英語の構文を覚える). In linguistics and computer science it stands for \"syntax\": 構文解析 \"parsing\", 構文エラー \"syntax error\". Distinction: 文法 is grammar as a whole, 文型 a sentence pattern or sentence type, 文体 style. Neutral to technical.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この言葉の語源をたどるとラテン語に行き着く。","romaji":"Kono kotoba no gogen o tadoru to Raten-go ni ikitsuku.","german":"If you trace the etymology of this word, you end up at Latin.","furigana":"ことば|ごげん|ご|い|つ"},{"japanese":"「ありがとう」の語源は「有り難し」だと言われている。","romaji":"Arigatō no gogen wa arigatashi da to iwarete iru.","german":"The origin of \"arigatō\" is said to be the old \"arigatashi\" (hard to come by).","furigana":"ごげん|あ|がた|い"},{"japanese":"語源を知ると、単語が覚えやすくなる。","romaji":"Gogen o shiru to, tango ga oboeyasuku naru.","german":"Knowing the origin of a word makes it easier to remember.","furigana":"ごげん|し|たんご|おぼ"}],"notes":"語源 is the origin of an individual word or expression. Typical are 語源をたどる／調べる, 〜の語源は…だ, …に語源がある, as well as 語源説 \"theory about a word's origin\" and 語源学 \"etymology (as a field)\". 由来 and 起源 are more general and also apply to customs, names or the origin of life; 語源 refers only to words. Neutral and also common in everyday speech.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"動詞の語幹に接辞を付けて活用形を作る。","romaji":"Dōshi no gokan ni setsuji o tsukete katsuyō-kei o tsukuru.","german":"Conjugated forms are made by attaching affixes to the verb stem.","furigana":"どうし|ごかん|せつじ|つ|かつようけい|つく"},{"japanese":"「高い」の語幹は「高」で、「い」が活用語尾である。","romaji":"Takai no gokan wa taka de, i ga katsuyō gobi de aru.","german":"In \"takai\", \"taka\" is the stem and \"i\" is the inflectional ending.","furigana":"たか|ごかん|たか|かつようごび"},{"japanese":"形容動詞は、語幹に「な」を付けて名詞を修飾する。","romaji":"Keiyōdōshi wa, gokan ni na o tsukete meishi o shūshoku suru.","german":"A na-adjective modifies a noun by attaching \"na\" to its stem.","furigana":"けいようどうし|ごかん|つ|めいし|しゅうしょく"}],"notes":"語幹 is the unchanging part of an inflectable word; the changing part is called 活用語尾 \"inflectional ending\" (語尾 for short). In Japanese school grammar, for example, the stem of 高い is 高, of 静かだ it is 静か. Not to be confused with 語根 \"root\" (etymological core) and 語源 \"word origin\". A technical grammar term, neutral.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「お」や「ご」は日本語の代表的な接頭辞だ。","romaji":"\"O\" ya \"go\" wa Nihongo no daihyō-teki na settōji da.","german":"\"O\" and \"go\" are typical Japanese prefixes.","furigana":"にほんご|だいひょうてき|せっとうじ"},{"japanese":"「不」「無」「非」は否定の意味を表す接頭辞だ。","romaji":"\"Fu\" \"mu\" \"hi\" wa hitei no imi o arawasu settōji da.","german":"\"Fu\", \"mu\" and \"hi\" are prefixes that express negation.","furigana":"ふ|む|ひ|ひてい|いみ|あらわ|せっとうじ"},{"japanese":"英語の「un-」は接頭辞で、単語の意味を反対にする。","romaji":"Eigo no \"un-\" wa settōji de, tango no imi o hantai ni suru.","german":"The English \"un-\" is a prefix that reverses the meaning of a word.","furigana":"えいご|せっとうじ|たんご|いみ|はんたい"}],"notes":"接頭辞 is the linguistic word for \"prefix\", that is, an element that comes before a word, like お／ご or the negations 不・無・非・未. In school grammar 接頭語 is also common; both mean practically the same. The counterpart is 接尾辞 \"suffix\". Typical phrases: 接頭辞が付く／接頭辞を付ける.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「的」は名詞に付く接尾辞である。","romaji":"\"Teki\" wa meishi ni tsuku setsubiji de aru.","german":"\"Teki\" is a suffix attached to nouns.","furigana":"てき|めいし|つ|せつびじ"},{"japanese":"人を表す「たち」や「さん」も接尾辞の一種だ。","romaji":"Hito o arawasu \"tachi\" ya \"san\" mo setsubiji no isshu da.","german":"\"Tachi\" and \"san\", which refer to people, are also a kind of suffix.","furigana":"ひと|あらわ|せつびじ|いっしゅ"},{"japanese":"英語では「-ness」という接尾辞を付けると名詞になる。","romaji":"Eigo de wa \"-ness\" to iu setsubiji o tsukeru to meishi ni naru.","german":"In English, adding the suffix \"-ness\" turns a word into a noun.","furigana":"えいご|せつびじ|つ|めいし"}],"notes":"接尾辞 is the technical word for \"suffix\", an element that attaches to the end of a word: 的, 化, 性 make new words from nouns, さん・様・たち attach to names or persons. In school grammar there is also 接尾語 with the same meaning. The counterpart is 接頭辞 \"prefix\". One says 接尾辞が付く or 名詞に接尾辞を付ける.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"口語と文語では文法が異なる。","romaji":"Kōgo to bungo de wa bunpō ga kotonaru.","german":"Colloquial and literary language differ in grammar.","furigana":"こうご|ぶんご|ぶんぽう|こと"},{"japanese":"古文の教科書には、原文の横に口語訳が付いている。","romaji":"Kobun no kyōkasho ni wa, genbun no yoko ni kōgoyaku ga tsuite iru.","german":"In classical Japanese textbooks, a translation into modern Japanese is given next to the original text.","furigana":"こぶん|きょうかしょ|げんぶん|よこ|こうごやく|つ"},{"japanese":"「じゃん」は口語的な表現なので、レポートでは使わないほうがいい。","romaji":"Jan wa kōgo-teki na hyōgen na node, repōto de wa tsukawanai hō ga ii.","german":"\"Jan\" is colloquial, so you should not use it in reports.","furigana":"こうごてき|ひょうげん|つか"}],"notes":"口語 has two meanings: \"spoken colloquial language\" and, in the history of language, \"modern Japanese\" in contrast to the classical 文語. Typical are 口語的な表現, 口語体 and 口語訳 \"rendering of a classical text into modern Japanese\". In everyday speech, one rather says 話し言葉 for spoken language; 口語 is the more technical term. Neutral to technical.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"文語体で書かれた古典を読み解く。","romaji":"Bungo-tai de kakareta koten o yomitoku.","german":"We decipher classics written in classical literary language.","furigana":"ぶんごたい|か|こてん|よ|と"},{"japanese":"明治憲法は文語で書かれているので、現代人には読みにくい。","romaji":"Meiji kenpō wa bungo de kakarete iru node, gendaijin ni wa yominikui.","german":"The Meiji Constitution is written in classical literary language and is therefore hard for people today to read.","furigana":"めいじけんぽう|ぶんご|か|げんだいじん|よ"},{"japanese":"高校の古典の授業で、文語文法を一から学んだ。","romaji":"Kōkō no koten no jugyō de, bungo bunpō o ichi kara mananda.","german":"In high school classics class, I learned classical grammar from scratch.","furigana":"こうこう|こてん|じゅぎょう|ぶんごぶんぽう|いち|まな"}],"notes":"文語 means above all the classical written language based on Heian-period Japanese, which was used for laws, newspapers and literature until the Meiji period (文語体, 文語文法). The opposite is 口語 \"modern (spoken) Japanese\". For \"written language\" in the modern sense, that is, the written style of present-day texts, one rather says 書き言葉. A technical term, literary.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"標準語と方言のバイリンガルである人は多い。","romaji":"Hyōjungo to hōgen no bairingaru de aru hito wa ōi.","german":"Many people are bilingual in the standard language and a dialect.","furigana":"ひょうじゅんご|ほうげん|ひと|おお"},{"japanese":"東京に出てきてから、標準語で話すようにしている。","romaji":"Tōkyō ni dete kite kara, hyōjungo de hanasu yō ni shite iru.","german":"Since moving to Tokyo, I've been trying to speak standard Japanese.","furigana":"とうきょう|で|ひょうじゅんご|はな"},{"japanese":"ニュースのアナウンサーは、標準語のアクセントで読む訓練を受ける。","romaji":"Nyūsu no anaunsā wa, hyōjungo no akusento de yomu kunren o ukeru.","german":"News announcers are trained to read with standard-language accent.","furigana":"ひょうじゅんご|よ|くんれん|う"}],"notes":"標準語 is the standardized language; in Japanese it is the standard variety based on the speech of Tokyo. The opposite is 方言 \"dialect\"; typical are 標準語で話す and 標準語に直す. Specialists and NHK prefer to speak of 共通語 today, because 標準語 sounds like a prescribed \"correct\" language; in everyday speech, however, 標準語 is the more common word. Neutral.","levelNote":"Compound: 標準 (N2) + 語 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"英語が国際的な共通語として機能している。","romaji":"Eigo ga kokusai-teki na kyōtsūgo toshite kinō shite iru.","german":"English functions as an international common language.","furigana":"えいご|こくさいてき|きょうつうご|きのう"},{"japanese":"地方出身の学生も、授業では共通語で話している。","romaji":"Chihō shusshin no gakusei mo, jugyō de wa kyōtsūgo de hanashite iru.","german":"Even students from the provinces speak standard Japanese in class.","furigana":"ちほうしゅっしん|がくせい|じゅぎょう|きょうつうご|はな"},{"japanese":"多民族国家では、異なる民族をつなぐ共通語が必要になる。","romaji":"Taminzoku kokka de wa, kotonaru minzoku o tsunagu kyōtsūgo ga hitsuyō ni naru.","german":"In multi-ethnic states, a common language that connects different ethnic groups is needed.","furigana":"たみんぞくこっか|こと|みんぞく|きょうつうご|ひつよう"}],"notes":"共通語 has two meanings: a lingua franca between speakers of different languages (e.g. 国際共通語としての英語) and the standard variety of Japanese understood nationwide. In the second meaning, it is the more neutral technical term for what is called 標準語 in everyday speech: it describes the language actually used in common, without a claim to a norm. The opposite is 方言. Neutral, common in the media and linguistics.","levelNote":"Compound: 共通 (N3) + 語 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"母語の影響で外国語の発音が難しいことがある。","romaji":"Bogo no eikyō de gaikokugo no hatsuon ga muzukashii koto ga aru.","german":"Because of the influence of one's native language, the pronunciation of a foreign language can be difficult.","furigana":"ぼご|えいきょう|がいこくご|はつおん|むずか"},{"japanese":"彼の母語はスペイン語だが、日本語もほぼ完璧に話せる。","romaji":"Kare no bogo wa Supeingo da ga, Nihongo mo hobo kanpeki ni hanaseru.","german":"His native language is Spanish, but he also speaks Japanese almost perfectly.","furigana":"かれ|ぼご|ご|にほんご|かんぺき|はな"},{"japanese":"この講座の講師は、全員英語の母語話者だ。","romaji":"Kono kōza no kōshi wa, zen'in Eigo no bogo washa da.","german":"All the instructors of this course are native speakers of English.","furigana":"こうざ|こうし|ぜんいんえいご|ぼごわしゃ"}],"notes":"母語 is the language one first acquired as a child. It is deliberately distinguished from 母国語 \"language of one's home country\", because 母国語 equates country and language, which often does not fit multilingual people or children of immigrants. Linguistics and education therefore prefer 母語; in everyday speech both are heard. Common: 母語話者 \"native speaker\" (colloquially ネイティブ). Neutral to technical.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本語には英語由来の外来語が非常に多い。","romaji":"Nihongo ni wa Eigo yurai no gairaigo ga hijō ni ōi.","german":"Im Japanischen gibt es sehr viele Fremdwörter englischen Ursprungs.","furigana":"にほんご|えいごゆらい|がいらいご|ひじょう|おお"},{"japanese":"外来語は、ふつうカタカナで書かれる。","romaji":"Gairaigo wa, futsū katakana de kakareru.","german":"Fremdwörter werden normalerweise in Katakana geschrieben.","furigana":"がいらいご|か"},{"japanese":"「アルバイト」はドイツ語から入った外来語だ。","romaji":"Arubaito wa Doitsugo kara haitta gairaigo da.","german":"„Arubaito“ ist ein Lehnwort aus dem Deutschen.","furigana":"ご|はい|がいらいご"}],"notes":"外来語 sind Wörter, die aus anderen Sprachen – vor allem aus europäischen Sprachen seit dem 16. Jahrhundert – ins Japanische übernommen wurden; sie stehen meist in Katakana. Obwohl 漢語 ursprünglich aus dem Chinesischen stammen, zählt man sie nicht zu den 外来語; neben 和語 und 漢語 bilden sie die dritte Schicht des Wortschatzes. Abzugrenzen ist 和製英語 – scheinbar englische Wörter, die in Japan gebildet wurden (z. B. サラリーマン). Neutral; 借用語 ist der linguistische Oberbegriff.","levelNote":"Compound: 外来 (N2) + 語 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"和語は日本語固有の語彙である。","romaji":"Wago wa Nihongo koyū no goi de aru.","german":"Wago are words native to the Japanese language.","furigana":"わご|にほんごこゆう|ごい"},{"japanese":"「始める」は和語で、「開始する」は同じ意味の漢語だ。","romaji":"Hajimeru wa wago de, kaishi suru wa onaji imi no kango da.","german":"\"Hajimeru\" is a native Japanese word, and \"kaishi suru\" is the Sino-Japanese word with the same meaning.","furigana":"はじ|わご|かいし|おな|いみ|かんご"},{"japanese":"和語を多く使うと、文章がやわらかい印象になる。","romaji":"Wago o ōku tsukau to, bunshō ga yawarakai inshō ni naru.","german":"Using many native Japanese words gives a text a softer impression.","furigana":"わご|おお|つか|ぶんしょう|いんしょう"}],"notes":"和語 (also やまとことば) are the originally Japanese words that were not borrowed from Chinese or other languages; in kanji they are mostly written with the kun reading (山, 話す, 美しい) or in hiragana. Together with 漢語 and 外来語 they form the three layers of the vocabulary. 和語 sound softer and more everyday, 漢語 more concise and formal; there are often pairs like 決める／決定する. A technical term from linguistics and language teaching.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"学術用語には漢語が多く使われる。","romaji":"Gakujutsu yōgo ni wa kango ga ōku tsukawareru.","german":"Sino-Japanese words are often used in technical terminology.","furigana":"がくじゅつようご|かんご|おお|つか"},{"japanese":"漢語は音読みで読まれ、和語に比べて硬い印象を与える。","romaji":"Kango wa on'yomi de yomare, wago ni kurabete katai inshō o ataeru.","german":"Sino-Japanese words are read with the on reading and give a stiffer impression than native words.","furigana":"かんご|おんよ|よ|わご|くら|かた|いんしょう|あた"},{"japanese":"子ども向けの文章では、難しい漢語をなるべく避けている。","romaji":"Kodomo-muke no bunshō de wa, muzukashii kango o narubeku sakete iru.","german":"In texts for children, difficult Sino-Japanese words are avoided as much as possible.","furigana":"こ|む|ぶんしょう|むずか|かんご|さ"}],"notes":"漢語 are words of Chinese origin or kanji compounds formed on the Chinese pattern with the on reading (勉強, 経済, 開始). Words coined in Japan, such as 哲学, also count; they are called 和製漢語. 漢語 sound more formal and concise than 和語 and dominate technical, official and newspaper language. Rarely, 漢語 also means \"the Chinese language\"; today one says 中国語 for that. A technical term, neutral.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「消しゴム」は和語と外来語が混じった混種語だ。","romaji":"\"Keshigomu\" wa wago to gairaigo ga majitta konshugo da.","german":"\"Keshigomu\" is a hybrid word mixing a native Japanese word and a loanword.","furigana":"け|わご|がいらいご|ま|こんしゅご"},{"japanese":"日本語の語種は、和語・漢語・外来語・混種語の四つに分けられる。","romaji":"Nihongo no goshu wa, wago, kango, gairaigo, konshugo no yottsu ni wakerareru.","german":"The Japanese vocabulary can be divided by origin into four groups: native, Sino-Japanese, loanwords and hybrid words.","furigana":"にほんご|ごしゅ|わご|かんご|がいらいご|こんしゅご|よっ|わ"},{"japanese":"「歯ブラシ」や「省エネ」のような混種語は、日常会話にもよく出てくる。","romaji":"\"Haburashi\" ya \"shōene\" no yō na konshugo wa, nichijō kaiwa ni mo yoku dete kuru.","german":"Hybrid words such as \"haburashi\" (toothbrush) and \"shōene\" (energy saving) also come up often in everyday conversation.","furigana":"は|しょう|こんしゅご|にちじょうかいわ|で"}],"notes":"混種語 is a term in the Japanese classification of words by origin (語種): the vocabulary is divided into 和語 (native), 漢語 (Sino-Japanese), 外来語 (Western loanwords) and 混種語. A 混種語 is composed of parts of different origins, for example 消し＋ゴム or 歯＋ブラシ. The word appears almost only in textbooks, grammar explanations and linguistics.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「矛盾」は中国の故事に由来する故事成語だ。","romaji":"\"Mujun\" wa Chūgoku no koji ni yurai suru koji seigo da.","german":"\"Mujun\" (contradiction) is an idiom derived from an old Chinese anecdote.","furigana":"むじゅん|ちゅうごく|こじ|ゆらい|こじせいご"},{"japanese":"国語の授業で、故事成語の由来を調べて発表した。","romaji":"Kokugo no jugyō de, koji seigo no yurai o shirabete happyō shita.","german":"In Japanese class, we researched and presented the origins of idioms from classical stories.","furigana":"こくご|じゅぎょう|こじせいご|ゆらい|しら|はっぴょう"},{"japanese":"「蛇足」や「五十歩百歩」も、よく使われる故事成語だ。","romaji":"\"Dasoku\" ya \"gojippo hyappo\" mo, yoku tsukawareru koji seigo da.","german":"\"Dasoku\" (a superfluous addition) and \"gojippo hyappo\" (six of one, half a dozen of the other) are also commonly used idioms from old stories.","furigana":"だそく|ごじっぽひゃっぽ|つか|こじせいご"}],"notes":"故事成語 are fixed expressions that go back to an incident or story from ancient China (故事 \"handed-down story\" + 成語 \"set phrase\"), such as 矛盾, 蛇足, 五十歩百歩 or 四面楚歌. They partly overlap with 四字熟語 but differ from ことわざ (folk proverbs) and 慣用句 (idioms without a clear source). Typical is 故事成語の由来を調べる; the word belongs to school teaching and educated language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本語には同音異義語が非常に多い。","romaji":"Nihongo ni wa dōon igigo ga hijō ni ōi.","german":"Japanese has a great many homophones.","furigana":"にほんご|どうおんいぎご|ひじょう|おお"},{"japanese":"「公園」と「講演」は同音異義語なので、話すときは文脈で区別する。","romaji":"Kōen to kōen wa dōon igigo na node, hanasu toki wa bunmyaku de kubetsu suru.","german":"\"Kōen\" (park) and \"kōen\" (lecture) are homophones, so when speaking you tell them apart from context.","furigana":"こうえん|こうえん|どうおんいぎご|はな|ぶんみゃく|くべつ"},{"japanese":"同音異義語が多いので、パソコンで変換ミスをしやすい。","romaji":"Dōon igigo ga ōi node, pasokon de henkan misu o shiyasui.","german":"Because there are so many homophones, it is easy to make conversion errors on a computer.","furigana":"どうおんいぎご|おお|へんかん"}],"notes":"同音異義語 are words with the same pronunciation (the same kana spelling) but different meanings and usually different kanji, e.g. 公園／講演／後援. Because of the many Sino-Japanese words, Japanese is especially rich in them, which makes puns (だじゃれ) possible and easily leads to errors in kana-to-kanji conversion. Related terms: 同訓異字 \"same kun reading, different kanji\" (会う／合う) and 多義語 \"a word with several meanings\". Technical term, neutral.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"類義語の微妙なニュアンスの違いを理解する。","romaji":"Ruigigo no bimyō na nyuansu no chigai o rikai suru.","german":"I understand the subtle differences in nuance between synonyms.","furigana":"るいぎご|びみょう|ちが|りかい"},{"japanese":"「美しい」と「きれい」は類義語だが、使い方が少し違う。","romaji":"Utsukushii to kirei wa ruigigo da ga, tsukaikata ga sukoshi chigau.","german":"\"Utsukushii\" and \"kirei\" are synonyms, but they are used a little differently.","furigana":"うつく|るいぎご|つか|かた|すこ|ちが"},{"japanese":"レポートで同じ言葉が続くときは、類義語辞典で言い換えを探す。","romaji":"Repōto de onaji kotoba ga tsuzuku toki wa, ruigigo jiten de iikae o sagasu.","german":"When the same word keeps recurring in a report, I look for alternatives in a thesaurus.","furigana":"おな|ことば|つづ|るいぎごじてん|い|か|さが"}],"notes":"類義語 are words with a similar but not completely identical meaning, such as 上がる／登る. 同義語, by contrast, means words that are strictly identical in meaning; in language teaching 類義語 is usually used, because true synonyms are rare. The opposite term is 対義語 or 反対語 \"antonym\". A thesaurus is called 類語辞典 or 類義語辞典. Neutral, from linguistics and teaching.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「善」の対義語は「悪」である。","romaji":"\"Zen\" no taigigo wa \"aku\" de aru.","german":"The antonym of \"good\" is \"evil\".","furigana":"ぜん|たいぎご|あく"},{"japanese":"「上がる」の対義語は何ですか。","romaji":"\"Agaru\" no taigigo wa nan desu ka.","german":"What is the opposite of \"agaru\" (to rise)?","furigana":"あ|たいぎご|なん"},{"japanese":"この辞書には類義語だけでなく、対義語も載っている。","romaji":"Kono jisho ni wa ruigigo dake de naku, taigigo mo notte iru.","german":"This dictionary lists not only synonyms but also antonyms.","furigana":"じしょ|るいぎご|たいぎご|の"}],"notes":"対義語 is the word for \"antonym\"; equivalent are 反対語 (more everyday) and 反意語／反義語 (technical). The opposite term is 類義語 or 同義語 \"synonym\". A typical question: 〜の対義語は何ですか. The word often appears in dictionaries and exam questions; in conversation people usually just say 反対の言葉.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本語の音韻体系は比較的単純である。","romaji":"Nihongo no on'in taikei wa hikaku-teki tanjun de aru.","german":"The phonological system of Japanese is relatively simple.","furigana":"にほんご|おんいんたいけい|ひかくてきたんじゅん"},{"japanese":"古代の日本語には、現代にはない音韻の区別があった。","romaji":"Kodai no Nihongo ni wa, gendai ni wa nai on'in no kubetsu ga atta.","german":"Ancient Japanese had phonological distinctions that no longer exist today.","furigana":"こだい|にほんご|げんだい|おんいん|くべつ"},{"japanese":"彼は大学院で日本語の音韻論を研究している。","romaji":"Kare wa daigakuin de Nihongo no on'inron o kenkyū shite iru.","german":"He is studying Japanese phonology in graduate school.","furigana":"かれ|だいがくいん|にほんご|おんいんろん|けんきゅう"}],"notes":"音韻 refers to the sounds of a language as a system of meaning-distinguishing units, i.e. the phonological level. It mostly appears in compounds: 音韻論 \"phonology\", 音韻体系 \"sound system\", 音韻変化 \"sound change\". Distinctions: 音声 concerns the sounds as actually articulated, the physical sounds (音声学 \"phonetics\"), while 音素 is the single smallest unit of this system. Technical term; hardly used in everyday life.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本語は開音節言語に分類される。","romaji":"Nihongo wa kai-onsetsu gengo ni bunrui sareru.","german":"Japanese is classified as an open-syllable language.","furigana":"にほんご|かいおんせつげんご|ぶんるい"},{"japanese":"「にっぽん」は二音節だが、拍で数えると四拍になる。","romaji":"Nippon wa ni-onsetsu da ga, haku de kazoeru to yon-haku ni naru.","german":"\"Nippon\" has two syllables, but counted in morae it has four.","furigana":"におんせつ|はく|かぞ|よんはく"},{"japanese":"英単語を覚えるときは、音節ごとに区切って発音してみよう。","romaji":"Eitango o oboeru toki wa, onsetsu goto ni kugitte hatsuon shite miyō.","german":"When you learn English words, try pronouncing them syllable by syllable.","furigana":"えいたんご|おぼ|おんせつ|くぎ|はつおん"}],"notes":"音節 is the syllable as a phonetic unit made up of a vowel as the nucleus and possibly surrounding consonants. For Japanese, the distinction from 拍 (mora) is important: long vowels, ん and the small っ each form a mora of their own, but not a syllable of their own – とうきょう has two syllables but four morae. Typical terms are 開音節 \"open syllable\" (ends in a vowel), 閉音節 \"closed syllable\" and 一音節ずつ. Technical term, also used neutrally in language teaching.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"音素の区別が言語によって異なる。","romaji":"Onso no kubetsu ga gengo ni yotte kotonaru.","german":"The distinction between phonemes varies from language to language.","furigana":"おんそ|くべつ|げんご|こと"},{"japanese":"英語のrとlは別々の音素だが、日本語では区別されない。","romaji":"Eigo no r to l wa betsubetsu no onso da ga, Nihongo de wa kubetsu sarenai.","german":"In English r and l are different phonemes, but in Japanese they are not distinguished.","furigana":"えいご|べつべつ|おんそ|にほんご|くべつ"},{"japanese":"「かさ」と「かた」は、一つの音素だけが違う。","romaji":"Kasa to kata wa, hitotsu no onso dake ga chigau.","german":"\"Kasa\" and \"kata\" differ in only a single phoneme.","furigana":"ひと|おんそ|ちが"}],"notes":"音素 is the smallest meaning-distinguishing sound unit of a language, e.g. /k/ or /a/. Whether two sounds are different 音素 is shown by minimal pairs such as かさ／かた. Distinctions: 音節 is the syllable, 拍 the mora, 音韻 the sound system as a whole; 形態素, by contrast, is the smallest meaning-bearing unit. Purely a technical term of linguistics.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本語のリズムは拍に基づいている。","romaji":"Nihongo no rizumu wa haku ni motozuite iru.","german":"The rhythm of Japanese is based on morae.","furigana":"にほんご|はく|もと"},{"japanese":"ワルツは、一小節が三拍でできている。","romaji":"Warutsu wa, isshōsetsu ga sanpaku de dekite iru.","german":"In a waltz, one measure consists of three beats.","furigana":"いっしょうせつ|さんぱく"},{"japanese":"一拍置いてから、彼はゆっくりと答えた。","romaji":"Ippaku oite kara, kare wa yukkuri to kotaeta.","german":"After pausing for a beat, he answered slowly.","furigana":"いっぱくお|かれ|こた"}],"notes":"拍 is the \"beat\" in a musical measure and also the linguistic term for the mora, the basic rhythmic unit of Japanese; it also serves as a counter (三拍, 四拍). In Japanese every kana, including ん, っ and a long vowel, counts as one 拍: きって has three 拍. In linguistics one also says モーラ. Well-known derivations are 拍手 \"applause\" and 拍子 \"beat, rhythm\"; the phrase 一拍置く means \"to pause briefly\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"中国語は声調言語であり四つの声調がある。","romaji":"Chūgokugo wa seichō gengo de ari yottsu no seichō ga aru.","german":"Chinese is a tonal language with four tones.","furigana":"ちゅうごくご|せいちょうげんご|よっ|せいちょう"},{"japanese":"声調を間違えると、まったく別の意味になってしまう。","romaji":"Seichō o machigaeru to, mattaku betsu no imi ni natte shimau.","german":"If you get the tone wrong, the word takes on a completely different meaning.","furigana":"せいちょう|まちが|べつ|いみ"},{"japanese":"ベトナム語には、中国語よりも多い六つの声調がある。","romaji":"Betonamugo ni wa, Chūgokugo yori mo ōi muttsu no seichō ga aru.","german":"Vietnamese has six tones, more than Chinese.","furigana":"ご|ちゅうごくご|おお|むっ|せいちょう"}],"notes":"声調 refers in linguistics to the meaning-distinguishing pitch contour of a syllable in tonal languages such as Chinese, Thai or Vietnamese (声調言語 \"tonal language\"). Japanese, by contrast, has no 声調 but a pitch accent at word level (アクセント). The older meaning \"tone of voice\" is rare and literary. For sentence melody one says イントネーション or 抑揚. Technical term, written language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"東京方言と関西方言ではアクセントの型が異なる。","romaji":"Tōkyō hōgen to Kansai hōgen de wa akusento no kata ga kotonaru.","german":"The accent patterns differ between the Tokyo and Kansai dialects.","furigana":"とうきょうほうげん|かんさいほうげん|かた|こと"},{"japanese":"彼の日本語には、少しフランス語のアクセントがある。","romaji":"Kare no Nihongo ni wa, sukoshi Furansugo no akusento ga aru.","german":"His Japanese has a slight French accent.","furigana":"かれ|にほんご|すこ|ご"},{"japanese":"白いシャツに、赤いスカーフでアクセントをつけた。","romaji":"Shiroi shatsu ni, akai sukāfu de akusento o tsuketa.","german":"I added an accent to the white shirt with a red scarf.","furigana":"しろ|あか"}],"notes":"In Japanese, アクセント mainly means the pitch accent of a word (高低アクセント): 箸 and 橋 differ only in their アクセント. In English, by contrast, it means stress accent (強弱アクセント). A foreign-language accent is also called アクセント; for the regional coloring of a dialect, one more often says 訛り. In fashion and design it means \"accent, eye-catcher\": アクセントをつける. Neutral.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"疑問文ではイントネーションが上昇する。","romaji":"Gimon-bun de wa intonēshon ga jōshō suru.","german":"In questions the intonation rises.","furigana":"ぎもんぶん|じょうしょう"},{"japanese":"関西出身の彼は、標準語を話してもイントネーションでわかる。","romaji":"Kansai shusshin no kare wa, hyōjungo o hanashite mo intonēshon de wakaru.","german":"He is from Kansai, and you can tell from his intonation even when he speaks standard Japanese.","furigana":"かんさいしゅっしん|かれ|ひょうじゅんご|はな"},{"japanese":"「そうですか」はイントネーション次第で、驚きにも皮肉にも聞こえる。","romaji":"Sō desu ka wa intonēshon shidai de, odoroki ni mo hiniku ni mo kikoeru.","german":"Depending on the intonation, \"sō desu ka\" can sound surprised or sarcastic.","furigana":"しだい|おどろ|ひにく|き"}],"notes":"イントネーション is the pitch movement over a whole sentence or utterance, such as the rise at the end of a question. It differs from the アクセント, which fixes the pitch within a single word. In everyday use, however, イントネーション is also used loosely for the typical speech melody of a dialect (関西のイントネーション). A Japanese synonym is 抑揚 \"intonation, modulation\". Neutral.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"形態素解析は自然言語処理の基礎となる。","romaji":"Keitaiso kaiseki wa shizen gengo shori no kiso to naru.","german":"Morphological analysis is the foundation of natural language processing.","furigana":"けいたいそかいせき|しぜんげんごしょり|きそ"},{"japanese":"「食べさせられた」は、いくつかの形態素に分けることができる。","romaji":"Tabesaserareta wa, ikutsu ka no keitaiso ni wakeru koto ga dekiru.","german":"\"Tabesaserareta\" can be divided into several morphemes.","furigana":"た|けいたいそ|わ"},{"japanese":"「本棚」は「本」と「棚」という二つの形態素からできている。","romaji":"Hondana wa hon to tana to iu futatsu no keitaiso kara dekite iru.","german":"\"Hondana\" (bookshelf) is made up of the two morphemes \"hon\" and \"tana\".","furigana":"ほんだな|ほん|たな|ふた|けいたいそ"}],"notes":"形態素 is the smallest meaning-bearing unit of a language: 食べさせられた, for example, consists of 食べ, させ, られ and た. To be distinguished are 音素, the smallest meaning-distinguishing sound unit without a meaning of its own, and 単語, which can consist of several 形態素. Particularly well known is 形態素解析 \"morphological analysis\": since Japanese is written without spaces, programs use it to split texts into words. Technical term from linguistics and computer science.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"膠着語では語形変化が接辞の付加で行われる。","romaji":"Kōchakugo de wa gokei henka ga setsuji no fuka de okonawareru.","german":"In agglutinierenden Sprachen erfolgt die Flexion durch Anfügen von Affixen.","furigana":"こうちゃくご|ごけいへんか|せつじ|ふか|おこな"},{"japanese":"ドイツ語の名詞は、格によって語形変化する。","romaji":"Doitsugo no meishi wa, kaku ni yotte gokei henka suru.","german":"Deutsche Nomen werden je nach Kasus dekliniert.","furigana":"ご|めいし|かく|ごけいへんか"},{"japanese":"英語は語形変化が少ないので、語順が重要になる。","romaji":"Eigo wa gokei henka ga sukunai node, gojun ga jūyō ni naru.","german":"Da das Englische kaum flektiert, kommt es stark auf die Wortstellung an.","furigana":"えいご|ごけいへんか|すく|ごじゅん|じゅうよう"}],"notes":"語形変化 ist der Oberbegriff für jede Formänderung eines Wortes, also sowohl die Deklination von Nomen als auch die Konjugation von Verben. Als intransitives する-Verb: 名詞が格によって語形変化する. In der japanischen Grammatik spricht man für Verben und Adjektive meist konkreter von 活用; 語形変化 begegnet vor allem beim Vergleich von Sprachen und in der Linguistik. Fachsprachlich, neutral.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ラテン語は典型的な屈折語である。","romaji":"Raten-go wa tenkei-teki na kussetsugo de aru.","german":"Latein ist eine typische flektierende Sprache.","furigana":"ご|てんけいてき|くっせつご"},{"japanese":"ドイツ語も、名詞や動詞の形が複雑に変わる屈折語の一つだ。","romaji":"Doitsugo mo, meishi ya dōshi no katachi ga fukuzatsu ni kawaru kussetsugo no hitotsu da.","german":"Auch Deutsch gehört zu den flektierenden Sprachen, in denen sich Nomen und Verben vielfältig verändern.","furigana":"ご|めいし|どうし|かたち|ふくざつ|か|くっせつご|ひと"},{"japanese":"屈折語では、一つの語尾が性・数・格など複数の意味を同時に表す。","romaji":"Kussetsugo de wa, hitotsu no gobi ga sei, sū, kaku nado fukusū no imi o dōji ni arawasu.","german":"In flektierenden Sprachen drückt eine einzige Endung gleichzeitig mehrere Merkmale wie Genus, Numerus und Kasus aus.","furigana":"くっせつご|ひと|ごび|せい|すう|かく|ふくすう|いみ|どうじ|あらわ"}],"notes":"屈折語 ist einer der klassischen morphologischen Sprachtypen neben 膠着語 und 孤立語: Grammatische Beziehungen werden durch Endungen ausgedrückt, die mit dem Stamm verschmelzen und oft mehrere Funktionen zugleich tragen. Typische Beispiele sind Latein, Griechisch, Russisch und Deutsch. 屈折 bedeutet wörtlich „Biegung, Brechung“ und steht auch für die Lichtbrechung (光の屈折) oder eine „verbogene“ Psyche (屈折した心理). Reiner Fachbegriff.","levelNote":"Compound: 屈折 (N1) + 語 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本語は膠着語に分類される。","romaji":"Nihongo wa kōchakugo ni bunrui sareru.","german":"Japanese is classified as an agglutinative language.","furigana":"にほんご|こうちゃくご|ぶんるい"},{"japanese":"膠着語では、「食べ・させ・られ・た」のように意味の単位を順につなげていく。","romaji":"Kōchakugo de wa, tabe, sase, rare, ta no yō ni imi no tan'i o jun ni tsunagete iku.","german":"In agglutinative languages, units of meaning are strung together one after another, as in tabe-sase-rare-ta.","furigana":"こうちゃくご|た|いみ|たんい|じゅん"},{"japanese":"トルコ語や韓国語も、日本語と同じ膠着語だ。","romaji":"Torukogo ya Kankokugo mo, Nihongo to onaji kōchakugo da.","german":"Turkish and Korean are also agglutinative languages, like Japanese.","furigana":"ご|かんこくご|にほんご|おな|こうちゃくご"}],"notes":"膠着語 literally means \"glued language\" (膠 \"glue\", 着 \"stick\"): grammatical elements such as particles and auxiliary suffixes are attached one after another to an unchanging stem, and each usually carries only one function. These include Japanese, Korean, Turkish, Finnish and Hungarian. The opposite terms are 屈折語 \"inflecting language\" and 孤立語 \"isolating language\". Purely a technical term of linguistic typology.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"中国語は孤立語の代表例である。","romaji":"Chūgokugo wa koritsugo no daihyō-rei de aru.","german":"Chinese is a representative example of an isolating language.","furigana":"ちゅうごくご|こりつご|だいひょうれい"},{"japanese":"孤立語では語の形が変わらないため、語順で文法関係を表す。","romaji":"Koritsugo de wa go no katachi ga kawaranai tame, gojun de bunpō kankei o arawasu.","german":"In isolating languages words do not change form, so grammatical relations are expressed by word order.","furigana":"こりつご|ご|かたち|か|ごじゅん|ぶんぽうかんけい|あらわ"},{"japanese":"ベトナム語も孤立語で、時制は動詞の前に置く語で示す。","romaji":"Betonamugo mo koritsugo de, jisei wa dōshi no mae ni oku go de shimesu.","german":"Vietnamese is also isolating; tense is indicated by a word placed before the verb.","furigana":"ご|こりつご|じせい|どうし|まえ|お|ご|しめ"}],"notes":"孤立語 is a language whose words do not change form; grammatical relations are expressed by word order and separate function words. Classic examples are Chinese and Vietnamese. Not to be confused with a genetically isolated language with no known relatives, such as Basque – for that one says 孤立した言語. Together with 膠着語 and 屈折語 it forms the classic typology. Purely a technical term.","levelNote":"Compound: 孤立 (N1) + 語 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本語の基本語順はSOV型である。","romaji":"Nihongo no kihon gojun wa SOV-gata de aru.","german":"The basic word order in Japanese is SOV.","furigana":"にほんご|きほんごじゅん|がた"},{"japanese":"日本語は語順が比較的自由で、「昨日私は」とも「私は昨日」とも言える。","romaji":"Nihongo wa gojun ga hikaku-teki jiyū de, kinō watashi wa to mo watashi wa kinō to mo ieru.","german":"Word order in Japanese is relatively free: you can say 「昨日私は」 as well as 「私は昨日」.","furigana":"にほんご|ごじゅん|ひかくてきじゆう|きのうわたし|わたし|きのう|い"},{"japanese":"英作文では、語順を間違えると意味が通じなくなる。","romaji":"Eisakubun de wa, gojun o machigaeru to imi ga tsūjinaku naru.","german":"When writing in English, a sentence becomes unintelligible if the word order is wrong.","furigana":"えいさくぶん|ごじゅん|まちが|いみ|つう"}],"notes":"語順 is the order of words or sentence elements. Typical are 語順が自由だ, 語順を入れ替える, 基本語順 and type labels such as SOV型／SVO型. In Japanese, particles mark the roles, so the order before the predicate is quite free; what is fixed, however, is that the predicate comes last and modifiers come first. Neutral, common in language teaching.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本語では述語が文末に来る。","romaji":"Nihongo de wa jutsugo ga bunmatsu ni kuru.","german":"In Japanese, the predicate comes at the end of the sentence.","furigana":"にほんご|じゅつご|ぶんまつ|く"},{"japanese":"「象は鼻が長い」の述語は「長い」だ。","romaji":"Zō wa hana ga nagai no jutsugo wa nagai da.","german":"In the sentence \"Zō wa hana ga nagai\", \"nagai\" is the predicate.","furigana":"ぞう|はな|なが|じゅつご|なが"},{"japanese":"日本語では、動詞だけでなく形容詞も単独で述語になれる。","romaji":"Nihongo de wa, dōshi dake de naku keiyōshi mo tandoku de jutsugo ni nareru.","german":"In Japanese, not only verbs but also adjectives can form a predicate on their own.","furigana":"にほんご|どうし|けいようし|たんどく|じゅつご"}],"notes":"述語 is the predicate, that is, the part of the sentence that says what the subject does, what it is like, or what it is: a verb, an adjective, or noun + だ. It forms a pair with 主語 (主語と述語, 主述関係); a common writing mistake is that 主語と述語がねじれている, that is, they do not match. In logic, 述語 stands for \"predicate\" as in 述語論理. A technical term from grammar and teaching.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本語では主語が省略されることが多い。","romaji":"Nihongo de wa shugo ga shōryaku sareru koto ga ōi.","german":"In Japanese, the subject is often omitted.","furigana":"にほんご|しゅご|しょうりゃく|おお"},{"japanese":"この文には主語がないが、文脈から「私」だとわかる。","romaji":"Kono bun ni wa shugo ga nai ga, bunmyaku kara watashi da to wakaru.","german":"This sentence has no subject, but from the context you can tell it is \"I\".","furigana":"ぶん|しゅご|ぶんみゃく|わたし"},{"japanese":"長い文を書くときは、主語と述語が対応しているか確認しよう。","romaji":"Nagai bun o kaku toki wa, shugo to jutsugo ga taiō shite iru ka kakunin shiyō.","german":"When you write long sentences, check whether the subject and predicate correspond.","furigana":"なが|ぶん|か|しゅご|じゅつご|たいおう|かくにん"}],"notes":"主語 is the subject of a sentence; in Japanese it is usually marked with が and very often omitted when it is clear from the context. Important: は marks the topic (主題), not necessarily the subject - in 象は鼻が長い, 象 is the topic and 鼻 is the subject. Typical: 主語を省略する, 主語と述語. In internet slang, 主語が大きい means \"to generalize too broadly\" (e.g. \"the Japanese\" instead of \"I\"). Neutral, a grammar term.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"他動詞は目的語を取る動詞である。","romaji":"Tadōshi wa mokutekigo o toru dōshi de aru.","german":"A transitive verb is a verb that takes an object.","furigana":"たどうし|もくてきご|と|どうし"},{"japanese":"「パンを食べる」の目的語は「パン」である。","romaji":"Pan o taberu no mokutekigo wa pan de aru.","german":"In 「パンを食べる」 the object is 「パン」.","furigana":"た|もくてきご"},{"japanese":"英語では、目的語は動詞のすぐ後ろに置かれる。","romaji":"Eigo de wa, mokutekigo wa dōshi no sugu ushiro ni okareru.","german":"In English the object is placed right after the verb.","furigana":"えいご|もくてきご|どうし|うし|お"}],"notes":"目的語 is the object of a sentence; the direct object is marked in Japanese with を, the indirect object (間接目的語) usually with に. With some predicates the object takes が: 日本語がわかる, 水が飲みたい. Motion verbs with を such as 道を歩く or 空を飛ぶ, on the other hand, have no 目的語 in the strict sense; they remain intransitive. Grammatical term, neutral.","levelNote":"Compound: 目的 (N4) + 語 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"修飾語は被修飾語の前に置かれる。","romaji":"Shūshokugo wa hi-shūshokugo no mae ni okareru.","german":"The modifier is placed before the word it modifies.","furigana":"しゅうしょくご|ひしゅうしょくご|まえ|お"},{"japanese":"「赤い花」では、「赤い」が「花」を修飾する修飾語だ。","romaji":"Akai hana de wa, akai ga hana o shūshoku suru shūshokugo da.","german":"In 「赤い花」, 「赤い」 is the modifier that modifies 「花」.","furigana":"あか|はな|あか|はな|しゅうしょく|しゅうしょくご"},{"japanese":"修飾語が長すぎると、文がわかりにくくなる。","romaji":"Shūshokugo ga nagasugiru to, bun ga wakarinikuku naru.","german":"If the modifiers are too long, the sentence becomes hard to understand.","furigana":"しゅうしょくご|なが|ぶん"}],"notes":"修飾語 is a word or expression that describes another word (被修飾語) more closely. One distinguishes 連体修飾語, which modifies a noun (attribute: 赤い花), and 連用修飾語, which modifies a verb or adjective (adverbial: ゆっくり歩く). In Japanese the 修飾語 always comes before the word it modifies, even when it is a whole relative clause. Grammatical term, neutral.","levelNote":"Compound: 修飾 (N1) + 語 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「れる」「られる」は受身を表す助動詞だ。","romaji":"\"Reru\" \"rareru\" wa ukemi o arawasu jodōshi da.","german":"'Reru' und 'rareru' sind Hilfsverben, die das Passiv ausdrücken.","furigana":"うけみ|あらわ|じょどうし"},{"japanese":"「ます」は丁寧な意味を添える助動詞だ。","romaji":"\"Masu\" wa teinei na imi o soeru jodōshi da.","german":"„Masu“ ist ein Auxiliar, das dem Satz Höflichkeit verleiht.","furigana":"ていねい|いみ|そ|じょどうし"},{"japanese":"英語の「can」や「will」も助動詞と呼ばれる。","romaji":"Eigo no \"can\" ya \"will\" mo jodōshi to yobareru.","german":"Auch englisch „can“ und „will“ bezeichnet man als Hilfsverben.","furigana":"えいご|じょどうし|よ"}],"notes":"助動詞 ist in der japanischen Schulgrammatik eine eigene Wortart: flektierende Hilfswörter, die an Verben, Adjektive oder Nomen treten und Bedeutungen wie Passiv, Kausativ, Verneinung, Wunsch oder Vergangenheit hinzufügen – etwa れる・られる, せる・させる, ない, たい, た, ます sowie だ・です. Das ist also mehr als ein deutsches „Hilfsverb“. Für Englisch oder Deutsch bezeichnet 助動詞 dagegen Modal- und Hilfsverben wie can oder will. Man sagt 動詞に助動詞が付く.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「開く（ひらく）」には自動詞と他動詞の両方の用法がある。","romaji":"\"Hiraku\" ni wa jidōshi to tadōshi no ryōhō no yōhō ga aru.","german":"「開く（ひらく）」 can be used both as an intransitive and as a transitive verb."},{"japanese":"「窓が閉まる」の「閉まる」は自動詞だ。","romaji":"\"Mado ga shimaru\" no \"shimaru\" wa jidōshi da.","german":"「閉まる」 in 「窓が閉まる」 is an intransitive verb.","furigana":"まど|し|し|じどうし"},{"japanese":"自動詞と他動詞のペアは、日本語学習者がよく間違えるところだ。","romaji":"Jidōshi to tadōshi no pea wa, Nihongo gakushūsha ga yoku machigaeru tokoro da.","german":"Pairs of intransitive and transitive verbs are a place where learners of Japanese often make mistakes.","furigana":"じどうし|たどうし|にほんごがくしゅうしゃ|まちが"}],"notes":"自動詞 is the \"intransitive verb\", the counterpart of 他動詞. Japanese has many pairs: ドアが開く（あく）／ドアを開ける, 窓が閉まる／窓を閉める; the 自動詞 takes が and describes a process without an acting person. Caution: motion verbs such as 歩く or 渡る take を for the path (公園を歩く) and are still 自動詞. Some verbs such as 開く（ひらく） are both.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自動詞と他動詞の対応関係を覚えることが重要だ。","romaji":"Jidōshi to tadōshi no taiō kankei o oboeru koto ga jūyō da.","german":"It is important to learn the correspondences between intransitive and transitive verbs.","furigana":"じどうし|たどうし|たいおうかんけい|おぼ|じゅうよう"},{"japanese":"「ドアを開ける」の「開ける」は他動詞で、「ドアが開く」の「開く」は自動詞だ。","romaji":"Doa o akeru no akeru wa tadōshi de, doa ga aku no aku wa jidōshi da.","german":"「開ける」 in 「ドアを開ける」 is transitive, while 「開く」 in 「ドアが開く」 is intransitive.","furigana":"あ|あ|たどうし|あ|あ|じどうし"},{"japanese":"辞書では、他動詞に「他」という記号が付いていることが多い。","romaji":"Jisho de wa, tadōshi ni ta to iu kigō ga tsuite iru koto ga ōi.","german":"In dictionaries, transitive verbs are often marked with the symbol 「他」.","furigana":"じしょ|たどうし|た|きごう|つ|おお"}],"notes":"他動詞 is a transitive verb that takes a direct object with を; its counterpart is 自動詞. Many verbs form pairs: 開ける／開く, 壊す／壊れる, 出す／出る – the 他動詞 emphasizes the agent, the 自動詞 the process. Note: verbs of moving through a space such as 道を歩く or 空を飛ぶ do take を but are 自動詞. In dictionaries 他動詞 is often marked with 他. Grammatical term.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"使役表現は相手に何かをさせることを意味する。","romaji":"Shieki hyōgen wa aite ni nanika o saseru koto o imi suru.","german":"Causative expressions mean making or letting someone do something.","furigana":"しえきひょうげん|あいて|なに|いみ"},{"japanese":"使役受身の「飲まされた」は、無理に飲まされたという不満を表す。","romaji":"Shieki ukemi no nomasareta wa, muri ni nomasareta to iu fuman o arawasu.","german":"The causative passive 「飲まされた」 expresses resentment at having been forced to drink.","furigana":"しえきうけみ|の|むり|の|ふまん|あらわ"},{"japanese":"かつては牛や馬を農作業に使役していた。","romaji":"Katsute wa ushi ya uma o nōsagyō ni shieki shite ita.","german":"In the past, cattle and horses were put to work in farming.","furigana":"うし|うま|のうさぎょう|しえき"}],"notes":"使役 is the causative in grammar, i.e. \"to make or let someone do something\": 使役形 (書かせる, 食べさせる), 使役文, 使役受身 (書かされる, 食べさせられる) for \"to be forced to\". The person who is to act is marked with に or を. The original meaning \"to put someone to work\" lives on in 使役する and 使役動物 \"working animal\", but it is literary and rare. Technical term.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本語の受身には迷惑の受身という用法がある。","romaji":"Nihongo no ukemi ni wa meiwaku no ukemi to iu yōhō ga aru.","german":"The Japanese passive also has a usage called the adversative passive.","furigana":"にほんご|うけみ|めいわく|うけみ|ようほう"},{"japanese":"交渉では受身にならず、こちらから提案すべきだ。","romaji":"Kōshō de wa ukemi ni narazu, kochira kara teian subeki da.","german":"In negotiations you should not stay on the defensive but make proposals yourself.","furigana":"こうしょう|うけみ|ていあん"},{"japanese":"柔道では、まず受身の練習から始める。","romaji":"Jūdō de wa, mazu ukemi no renshū kara hajimeru.","german":"In judo you begin with practicing how to fall.","furigana":"じゅうどう|うけみ|れんしゅう|はじ"}],"notes":"受身 (also 受け身) is the passive in grammar: 受身形 (書かれる, 食べられる), 受身文. A Japanese particularity is 迷惑の受身, the indirect passive for inconveniences (雨に降られた, 隣の人にたばこを吸われた). Outside grammar, 受身 means \"passive, defensive attitude\" (受身になる, 受身の姿勢) and, in judo, \"breakfall\" (受身を取る). Neutral.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"可能形の作り方は動詞のグループによって異なる。","romaji":"Kanō-kei no tsukurikata wa dōshi no gurūpu ni yotte kotonaru.","german":"How the potential form is made differs depending on the verb group.","furigana":"かのうけい|つく|かた|どうし|こと"},{"japanese":"「話す」の可能形は「話せる」、「食べる」の可能形は「食べられる」だ。","romaji":"Hanasu no kanō-kei wa hanaseru, taberu no kanō-kei wa taberareru da.","german":"The potential form of 「話す」 is 「話せる」, and that of 「食べる」 is 「食べられる」.","furigana":"はな|かのうけい|はな|た|かのうけい|た"},{"japanese":"最近は「見れる」のような「ら抜き」の可能形もよく耳にする。","romaji":"Saikin wa mireru no yō na ranuki no kanō-kei mo yoku mimi ni suru.","german":"Recently you often hear \"ra-dropped\" potential forms such as 「見れる」 too.","furigana":"さいきん|み|ぬ|かのうけい|みみ"}],"notes":"可能形 is the potential form of the verb: 書く→書ける, 食べる→食べられる, 来る→来られる, する→できる. The object often switches from を to が: 日本語が話せる. Colloquially widespread are ら抜き言葉 such as 見れる instead of 見られる, which are considered incorrect in formal texts. The term comes from Japanese-as-a-foreign-language teaching; school grammar speaks of 可能動詞 or the auxiliary verb られる.","levelNote":"Compound: 可能 (N3) + 形 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"意志形は話し手の意図を表す。","romaji":"Ishi-kei wa hanashite no ito o arawasu.","german":"The volitional form expresses the speaker's intention.","furigana":"いしけい|はな|て|いと|あらわ"},{"japanese":"「行く」の意志形は「行こう」、「食べる」は「食べよう」となる。","romaji":"Iku no ishi-kei wa ikō, taberu wa tabeyō to naru.","german":"The volitional form of 「行く」 is 「行こう」, and that of 「食べる」 is 「食べよう」.","furigana":"い|いしけい|い|た|た"},{"japanese":"意志形に「と思う」を付けると、「旅行しようと思う」のように予定を表せる。","romaji":"Ishi-kei ni to omou o tsukeru to, ryokō shiyō to omou no yō ni yotei o arawaseru.","german":"If you attach 「と思う」 to the volitional form, you can express plans, as in 「旅行しようと思う」.","furigana":"いしけい|おも|つ|りょこう|おも|よてい|あらわ"}],"notes":"意志形 (also 意向形) is the volitional form: 行く→行こう, 食べる→食べよう, する→しよう, 来る→来よう. On its own it means \"let's ...\" or \"I will ...\" (一緒に行こう); with と思う it expresses an intention (旅行しようと思う); with とする it means \"to try\" or \"to be about to\" (出かけようとしたとき). The term comes from Japanese-as-a-foreign-language teaching; school grammar speaks of 未然形＋う／よう.","levelNote":"Compound: 意志 (N3) + 形 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"命令形は日常会話ではあまり使われない。","romaji":"Meirei-kei wa nichijō kaiwa de wa amari tsukawarenai.","german":"The imperative is rarely used in everyday conversation.","furigana":"めいれいけい|にちじょうかいわ|つか"},{"japanese":"「急げ」や「やめろ」は命令形で、かなり乱暴に聞こえる。","romaji":"Isoge ya yamero wa meirei-kei de, kanari ranbō ni kikoeru.","german":"「急げ」 and 「やめろ」 are imperatives and sound quite rough.","furigana":"いそ|めいれいけい|らんぼう|き"},{"japanese":"交通標識の「止まれ」は、命令形が使われる代表的な例だ。","romaji":"Kōtsū hyōshiki no tomare wa, meirei-kei ga tsukawareru daihyō-teki na rei da.","german":"The traffic sign 「止まれ」 is a typical example of the use of the imperative.","furigana":"こうつうひょうしき|と|めいれいけい|つか|だいひょうてき|れい"}],"notes":"命令形 is the imperative form: 書く→書け, 食べる→食べろ, する→しろ, 来る→来い. It sounds very direct and rough and in everyday life is used almost only among close friends, in sports, in emergencies, on signs (止まれ) or in quoted commands. Polite requests are formed with 〜てください or 〜なさい. In exams and instructions, the written variant ending in よ appears: 次の問いに答えよ. Grammatical term, also one of the six conjugation forms of school grammar.","levelNote":"Compound: 命令 (N3) + 形 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仮定形は条件を表す文型に用いられる。","romaji":"Kateikei wa jōken o arawasu bunkei ni mochiirareru.","german":"The conditional form is used in sentence patterns that express conditions.","furigana":"かていけい|じょうけん|あらわ|ぶんけい|もち"},{"japanese":"「行く」の仮定形は「行け」で、「ば」を付けると「行けば」になる。","romaji":"\"Iku\" no kateikei wa \"ike\" de, \"ba\" o tsukeru to \"ikeba\" ni naru.","german":"The conditional form of 「行く」 is 「行け」, and adding 「ば」 gives 「行けば」.","furigana":"い|かていけい|い|つ|い"},{"japanese":"古典文法では、仮定形の代わりに已然形を学ぶ。","romaji":"Koten bunpō de wa, kateikei no kawari ni izenkei o manabu.","german":"In classical grammar one learns the realis form (已然形) instead of the conditional form.","furigana":"こてんぶんぽう|かていけい|か|いぜんけい|まな"}],"notes":"仮定形 is one of the six conjugation forms (活用形) of Japanese school grammar: 未然形, 連用形, 終止形, 連体形, 仮定形, 命令形. It is the form before ば: 行け（ば）, 高けれ（ば）, 静かなら（ば）. In the West the result is usually called the ば-form or conditional. In classical grammar this position is occupied by the 已然形, which has a different function.","levelNote":"Compound: 仮定 (N2) + 形 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"辞書に載っている動詞の形は終止形である。","romaji":"Jisho ni notte iru dōshi no katachi wa shūshi-kei de aru.","german":"The verb form listed in the dictionary is the terminal form.","furigana":"じしょ|の|どうし|かたち|しゅうしけい"},{"japanese":"「らしい」や伝聞の「そうだ」は、動詞の終止形に接続する。","romaji":"Rashii ya denbun no sō da wa, dōshi no shūshi-kei ni setsuzoku suru.","german":"「らしい」 and the hearsay 「そうだ」 attach to the terminal form of a verb.","furigana":"でんぶん|どうし|しゅうしけい|せつぞく"},{"japanese":"古文では、「あり」のように現代語と終止形が異なる動詞がある。","romaji":"Kobun de wa, ari no yō ni gendaigo to shūshi-kei ga kotonaru dōshi ga aru.","german":"In classical Japanese there are verbs such as 「あり」 whose terminal form differs from modern Japanese.","furigana":"こぶん|げんだいご|しゅうしけい|こと|どうし"}],"notes":"終止形 is one of the six conjugation forms (活用形) of Japanese school grammar: the form with which a sentence ends (書く。高い。). For modern verbs and adjectives it is identical to the 連体形 and the dictionary form; differences exist with だ (終止形 だ, 連体形 な) and in classical Japanese (あり／ある). It is followed by, among others, らしい, だろう and the hearsay そうだ. In Japanese-as-a-foreign-language teaching one mostly says 辞書形.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"連体形は名詞を修飾する際に使われる。","romaji":"Rentai-kei wa meishi o shūshoku suru sai ni tsukawareru.","german":"The attributive form is used when modifying a noun.","furigana":"れんたいけい|めいし|しゅうしょく|さい|つか"},{"japanese":"現代語では、動詞の終止形と連体形は同じ形をしている。","romaji":"Gendaigo de wa, dōshi no shūshi-kei to rentai-kei wa onaji katachi o shite iru.","german":"In modern Japanese, the terminal form and the attributive form of a verb have the same shape.","furigana":"げんだいご|どうし|しゅうしけい|れんたいけい|おな|かたち"},{"japanese":"「静かだ」の連体形は「静かな」で、「静かな部屋」のように使う。","romaji":"Shizuka da no rentai-kei wa shizuka na de, shizuka na heya no yō ni tsukau.","german":"The attributive form of 「静かだ」 is 「静かな」, used as in 「静かな部屋」.","furigana":"しず|れんたいけい|しず|しず|へや|つか"}],"notes":"連体形 is the conjugation form that connects to a noun (体言) – literally \"the form that links with a 体言\". For modern verbs and い-adjectives it is the same as the 終止形; it becomes clear with な-adjectives and the copula: 静かだ→静かな部屋, 学生だ→学生なので. It also stands before ので and のに. In classical Japanese it often differs from the 終止形 and plays a role in 係り結び (after ぞ, なむ, や, か). Grammatical term.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"連用形は他の語と接続する際に使う形だ。","romaji":"Ren'yō-kei wa hoka no go to setsuzoku suru sai ni tsukau katachi da.","german":"Die Verbindungsform wird beim Anschluss an andere Wörter verwendet.","furigana":"れんようけい|ほか|ご|せつぞく|さい|つか|かたち"},{"japanese":"「遊び」や「考え」のように、連用形がそのまま名詞になることがある。","romaji":"Asobi ya kangae no yō ni, ren'yō-kei ga sono mama meishi ni naru koto ga aru.","german":"Wie bei „asobi“ oder „kangae“ kann die Verbindungsform direkt als Nomen dienen.","furigana":"あそ|かんが|れんようけい|めいし"},{"japanese":"書き言葉では、「て」を使わずに連用形で文をつなぐことがある。","romaji":"Kakikotoba de wa, te o tsukawazu ni ren'yō-kei de bun o tsunagu koto ga aru.","german":"In der Schriftsprache verbindet man Sätze manchmal ohne „te“, direkt mit der Verbindungsform.","furigana":"か|ことば|つか|れんようけい|ぶん"}],"notes":"連用形 ist die Konjugationsform, die sich an 用言 (Verben, Adjektive) und viele Hilfsverben anschließt: 書き(ます), 書い(た), 高く(なる). Sie entspricht dem „ます-Stamm“ im Fremdsprachenunterricht. Weitere Funktionen: Nominalisierung (遊ぶ→遊び), zusammengesetzte Verben (書き始める) und in der Schriftsprache das Verbinden von Sätzen ohne て (連用中止法: 朝起き、顔を洗う). Nicht mit dem deutschen „Konjunktiv“ verwechseln – es ist keine Modusform. Grammatikbegriff.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"未然形は否定や受身の接辞と結合する。","romaji":"Mizen-kei wa hitei ya ukemi no setsuji to ketsugō suru.","german":"The irrealis form combines with negative and passive affixes.","furigana":"みぜんけい|ひてい|うけみ|せつじ|けつごう"},{"japanese":"「書かない」の「書か」は、「書く」の未然形だ。","romaji":"Kakanai no kaka wa, kaku no mizen-kei da.","german":"The 「書か」 in 「書かない」 is the irrealis form of 「書く」.","furigana":"か|か|か|みぜんけい"},{"japanese":"古文では、未然形に「ば」が付くと仮定の意味になる。","romaji":"Kobun de wa, mizen-kei ni ba ga tsuku to katei no imi ni naru.","german":"In classical Japanese, the irrealis form plus 「ば」 gives a hypothetical meaning.","furigana":"こぶん|みぜんけい|つ|かてい|いみ"}],"notes":"未然形 literally means \"not-yet-realized form\". It is followed by ない (書かない), the passive れる／られる, the causative せる／させる and, in school grammar, also う／よう (書こう). In classical Japanese, 未然形＋ば expresses a hypothetical condition, whereas 已然形＋ば denotes a condition that has already occurred. In modern Japanese the 仮定形 has taken its place. Grammatical term of school and classical grammar; in foreign-language teaching one speaks rather of the ない-form.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"已然形は古典文法における活用形の一つだ。","romaji":"Izen-kei wa koten bunpō ni okeru katsuyō-kei no hitotsu da.","german":"The realis form is one of the conjugation forms in classical grammar.","furigana":"いぜんけい|こてんぶんぽう|かつようけい|ひと"},{"japanese":"已然形に「ば」が付くと、すでに起きた事柄を条件として表す。","romaji":"Izen-kei ni ba ga tsuku to, sude ni okita kotogara o jōken toshite arawasu.","german":"When 「ば」 is attached to the realis form, it expresses something that has already happened as a condition.","furigana":"いぜんけい|つ|お|ことがら|じょうけん|あらわ"},{"japanese":"係助詞「こそ」があると、文末は已然形で結ばれる。","romaji":"Kakarijoshi koso ga aru to, bunmatsu wa izen-kei de musubareru.","german":"When the emphasizing particle 「こそ」 is present, the sentence ends in the realis form.","furigana":"かかりじょし|ぶんまつ|いぜんけい|むす"}],"notes":"已然形 literally means \"already-so form\" and is one of the six conjugation forms of classical grammar. 已然形＋ば denotes a condition that has already occurred (\"since, when\"), in contrast to 未然形＋ば \"if\"; in addition, a sentence ends in the 已然形 after こそ (係り結び). In modern Japanese the 仮定形 has taken its place (書けば = \"if\"). Beware of the kanji: 已 \"already\" is not 己 \"oneself\" or 巳 \"snake\". Technical term of classical-Japanese teaching.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"語用論は言葉の使用と文脈の関係を研究する。","romaji":"Goyōron wa kotoba no shiyō to bunmyaku no kankei o kenkyū suru.","german":"Pragmatics studies the relationship between language use and context.","furigana":"ごようろん|ことば|しよう|ぶんみゃく|かんけい|けんきゅう"},{"japanese":"「ちょっと寒いね」が窓を閉めてという依頼になるのは、語用論で説明される。","romaji":"Chotto samui ne ga mado o shimete to iu irai ni naru no wa, goyōron de setsumei sareru.","german":"Pragmatics explains how 「ちょっと寒いね」 can function as a request to close the window.","furigana":"さむ|まど|し|いらい|ごようろん|せつめい"},{"japanese":"敬語の使い分けは、語用論の重要な研究テーマだ。","romaji":"Keigo no tsukaiwake wa, goyōron no jūyō na kenkyū tēma da.","german":"The appropriate choice of honorific speech is an important research topic in pragmatics.","furigana":"けいご|つか|わ|ごようろん|じゅうよう|けんきゅう"}],"notes":"語用論 is pragmatics, the branch of linguistics that studies how meaning arises in concrete use and context – for example indirect requests, implicatures (含意), speech acts (発話行為) and politeness (ポライトネス). Distinctions: 意味論 studies literal, context-independent meaning, 統語論 sentence structure. For Japanese, with its many indirect ways of expressing things, 語用論 is especially central. Academic technical term.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"意味論では語や文の意味を体系的に分析する。","romaji":"Imiron de wa go ya bun no imi o taikei-teki ni bunseki suru.","german":"Semantics systematically analyzes the meanings of words and sentences.","furigana":"いみろん|ご|ぶん|いみ|たいけいてき|ぶんせき"},{"japanese":"「独身」と「結婚していない」が同じ意味かどうかは、意味論の問題だ。","romaji":"Dokushin to kekkon shite inai ga onaji imi ka dō ka wa, imiron no mondai da.","german":"Whether 「独身」 and 「結婚していない」 mean the same thing is a question of semantics.","furigana":"どくしん|けっこん|おな|いみ|いみろん|もんだい"},{"japanese":"彼は哲学科で、言語の意味論を専門にしている。","romaji":"Kare wa tetsugakuka de, gengo no imiron o senmon ni shite iru.","german":"He is in the philosophy department and specializes in the semantics of language.","furigana":"かれ|てつがくか|げんご|いみろん|せんもん"}],"notes":"意味論 is semantics, the study of the meaning of words and sentences. It examines, for instance, meaning relations such as 類義語 and 対義語 or the truth conditions of sentences (形式意味論). Distinctions: 語用論 asks what a speaker means in a situation, 統語論 deals with sentence structure. Also used in philosophy and computer science (プログラム言語の意味論). Academic technical term.","levelNote":"Compound: 意味 (N5) + 論 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"統語論は文の構造と規則を研究する分野だ。","romaji":"Tōgoron wa bun no kōzō to kisoku o kenkyū suru bun'ya da.","german":"Syntax is the field that studies sentence structure and rules.","furigana":"とうごろん|ぶん|こうぞう|きそく|けんきゅう|ぶんや"},{"japanese":"統語論の立場から見ると、この文は二通りに解釈できる。","romaji":"Tōgoron no tachiba kara miru to, kono bun wa futatōri ni kaishaku dekiru.","german":"From a syntactic point of view, this sentence can be interpreted in two ways.","furigana":"とうごろん|たちば|み|ぶん|ふたとお|かいしゃく"},{"japanese":"チョムスキーの生成文法は、統語論の研究に大きな影響を与えた。","romaji":"Chomusukī no seisei bunpō wa, tōgoron no kenkyū ni ōki na eikyō o ataeta.","german":"Chomsky's generative grammar had a major influence on syntax research.","furigana":"せいせいぶんぽう|とうごろん|けんきゅう|おお|えいきょう|あた"}],"notes":"統語論 is syntax as an academic discipline, i.e. the study of sentence structure and the rules by which words are combined into sentences; 構文論 is equivalent. The adjective is 統語的 \"syntactic\". Distinctions: 構文 is a concrete sentence construction, 形態論 studies the structure of words, 意味論 meaning. Academic technical term.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"音韻論では音の体系的なパターンを分析する。","romaji":"On'inron de wa oto no taikei-teki na patān o bunseki suru.","german":"Phonology analyzes systematic patterns of sound.","furigana":"おんいんろん|おと|たいけいてき|ぶんせき"},{"japanese":"音韻論の授業で、日本語の「ん」がいくつもの音で発音されることを学んだ。","romaji":"On'inron no jugyō de, Nihongo no n ga ikutsu mo no oto de hatsuon sareru koto o mananda.","german":"In my phonology class I learned that Japanese 「ん」 is pronounced as several different sounds.","furigana":"おんいんろん|じゅぎょう|にほんご|おと|はつおん|まな"},{"japanese":"音声学が実際の発音を扱うのに対し、音韻論は音の体系を扱う。","romaji":"Onseigaku ga jissai no hatsuon o atsukau no ni taishi, on'inron wa oto no taikei o atsukau.","german":"Whereas phonetics deals with actual pronunciation, phonology deals with the sound system.","furigana":"おんせいがく|じっさい|はつおん|あつか|たい|おんいんろん|おと|たいけい|あつか"}],"notes":"音韻論 is phonology, the study of the sound system of a language: which sounds distinguish meaning (音素), how syllables and morae (拍) are built, and what role アクセント plays. Distinction: 音声学 \"phonetics\" describes the actual pronunciation and acoustics of sounds. The base word 音韻 denotes the sound system itself. Academic technical term.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"形態論は語の内部構造を研究する。","romaji":"Keitairon wa go no naibu kōzō o kenkyū suru.","german":"Morphology studies the internal structure of words.","furigana":"けいたいろん|ご|ないぶこうぞう|けんきゅう"},{"japanese":"日本語の形態論では、動詞の活用が中心的なテーマになる。","romaji":"Nihongo no keitairon de wa, dōshi no katsuyō ga chūshin-teki na tēma ni naru.","german":"In the morphology of Japanese, verb conjugation is a central topic.","furigana":"にほんご|けいたいろん|どうし|かつよう|ちゅうしんてき"},{"japanese":"形態論と統語論の境界は、言語によってははっきりしないこともある。","romaji":"Keitairon to tōgoron no kyōkai wa, gengo ni yotte wa hakkiri shinai koto mo aru.","german":"The boundary between morphology and syntax is not always clear, depending on the language.","furigana":"けいたいろん|とうごろん|きょうかい|げんご"}],"notes":"形態論 is morphology, the study of the internal structure of words: 形態素, word formation (語形成) and inflection (語形変化, 活用). In Japanese, verb conjugation and compounds such as 本棚 are typical topics. The base word 形態 generally means \"form, shape\"; in biology, morphology is called 形態学. Academic technical term.","levelNote":"Compound: 形態 (N1) + 論 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"音声学の知識は外国語教育に不可欠だ。","romaji":"Onseigaku no chishiki wa gaikokugo kyōiku ni fukaketsu da.","german":"Knowledge of phonetics is indispensable for foreign-language teaching.","furigana":"おんせいがく|ちしき|がいこくごきょういく|ふかけつ"},{"japanese":"大学で音声学を専攻して、母音の発音を研究しています。","romaji":"Daigaku de onseigaku o senkō shite, boin no hatsuon o kenkyū shite imasu.","german":"I majored in phonetics at university and am researching the pronunciation of vowels.","furigana":"だいがく|おんせいがく|せんこう|ぼいん|はつおん|けんきゅう"},{"japanese":"音声学の授業で、自分の声を録音して分析した。","romaji":"Onseigaku no jugyō de, jibun no koe o rokuon shite bunseki shita.","german":"In phonetics class I recorded my own voice and analyzed it.","furigana":"おんせいがく|じゅぎょう|じぶん|こえ|ろくおん|ぶんせき"}],"notes":"音声学 is phonetics, the study of the production, transmission and perception of speech sounds. To be distinguished from it is 音韻論 \"phonology\", which examines which sounds distinguish meaning in a language. Typical are 音声学を専攻する, 音声学の授業 and the adjective 音声学的な／音声学的に \"phonetic(ally)\". Note: 音声 alone in everyday use mostly means just \"sound, audio\" (音声ガイド, 音声入力).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"言語学は人間の言語を科学的に研究する学問だ。","romaji":"Gengogaku wa ningen no gengo o kagaku-teki ni kenkyū suru gakumon da.","german":"Linguistics is the discipline that studies human language scientifically.","furigana":"げんごがく|にんげん|げんご|かがくてき|けんきゅう|がくもん"},{"japanese":"言語学を勉強しても、外国語が上手に話せるとは限らない。","romaji":"Gengogaku o benkyō shite mo, gaikokugo ga jōzu ni hanaseru to wa kagiranai.","german":"Studying linguistics does not mean you will necessarily speak foreign languages well.","furigana":"げんごがく|べんきょう|がいこくご|じょうず|はな|かぎ"},{"japanese":"有名な言語学者の講演を聞きに行った。","romaji":"Yūmei na gengogakusha no kōen o kiki ni itta.","german":"I went to hear a lecture by a famous linguist.","furigana":"ゆうめい|げんごがくしゃ|こうえん|き|い"}],"notes":"言語学 is linguistics as an academic subject; someone who practices it is a 言語学者. Not to be confused with 語学: that denotes the practical learning of foreign languages (語学が得意 \"to be good at languages\", 語学学校 \"language school\"). Typical combinations are 言語学を専攻する, 言語学的に分析する and subfields such as 社会言語学 or 応用言語学.","levelNote":"Compound: 言語 (N3) + 学 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"応用言語学は語学教育の理論的基盤を提供する。","romaji":"Ōyō gengogaku wa gogaku kyōiku no riron-teki kiban o teikyō suru.","german":"Applied linguistics provides the theoretical basis for language teaching.","furigana":"おうようげんごがく|ごがくきょういく|りろんてききばん|ていきょう"},{"japanese":"日本語教師になるために、大学院で応用言語学を学んでいる。","romaji":"Nihongo kyōshi ni naru tame ni, daigakuin de ōyō gengogaku o manande iru.","german":"To become a Japanese teacher, I am studying applied linguistics in a master's program.","furigana":"にほんごきょうし|だいがくいん|おうようげんごがく|まな"},{"japanese":"応用言語学の観点から、この教科書の問題点を分析した。","romaji":"Ōyō gengogaku no kanten kara, kono kyōkasho no mondaiten o bunseki shita.","german":"From the perspective of applied linguistics, I analyzed the weaknesses of this textbook.","furigana":"おうようげんごがく|かんてん|きょうかしょ|もんだいてん|ぶんせき"}],"notes":"応用言語学 is applied linguistics: it uses linguistic findings for practical questions such as foreign-language teaching, 第二言語習得 \"second-language acquisition\", translation or language testing. The counterpart is 理論言語学 \"theoretical linguistics\". The word belongs in academic contexts, e.g. 応用言語学の修士課程 or 応用言語学の観点から.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"社会言語学は言語と社会の関係を研究する。","romaji":"Shakai gengogaku wa gengo to shakai no kankei o kenkyū suru.","german":"Sociolinguistics studies the relationship between language and society.","furigana":"しゃかいげんごがく|げんご|しゃかい|かんけい|けんきゅう"},{"japanese":"若者言葉の変化は、社会言語学の面白いテーマだ。","romaji":"Wakamono kotoba no henka wa, shakai gengogaku no omoshiroi tēma da.","german":"The change in youth language is an interesting topic in sociolinguistics.","furigana":"わかものことば|へんか|しゃかいげんごがく|おもしろ"},{"japanese":"社会言語学の調査で、敬語の使い方が世代によって違うことが分かった。","romaji":"Shakai gengogaku no chōsa de, keigo no tsukaikata ga sedai ni yotte chigau koto ga wakatta.","german":"A sociolinguistic survey showed that the use of honorific speech differs by generation.","furigana":"しゃかいげんごがく|ちょうさ|けいご|つか|かた|せだい|ちが|わ"}],"notes":"社会言語学 is sociolinguistics, which examines how language and society are connected: dialects, the use of 敬語 depending on social relationship, youth language (若者言葉), differences between men's and women's speech, or multilingualism. One says 社会言語学の研究, 社会言語学的な視点 or 社会言語学者. It is purely a technical term from scholarship and teaching.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"比較言語学によって言語間の系統関係が明らかにされた。","romaji":"Hikaku gengogaku ni yotte gengo-kan no keitō kankei ga akiraka ni sareta.","german":"Durch die vergleichende Sprachwissenschaft wurden Verwandtschaftsbeziehungen zwischen Sprachen aufgedeckt.","furigana":"ひかくげんごがく|げんごかん|けいとうかんけい|あき"},{"japanese":"比較言語学の方法で、印欧祖語の単語が再建された。","romaji":"Hikaku gengogaku no hōhō de, In'ō sogo no tango ga saiken sareta.","german":"Mit der Methode der vergleichenden Sprachwissenschaft wurden Wörter des Urindogermanischen rekonstruiert.","furigana":"ひかくげんごがく|ほうほう|いんおうそご|たんご|さいけん"},{"japanese":"19世紀のドイツでは比較言語学が盛んだった。","romaji":"Jūkyū seiki no Doitsu de wa hikaku gengogaku ga sakan datta.","german":"Im Deutschland des 19. Jahrhunderts blühte die vergleichende Sprachwissenschaft.","furigana":"せいき|ひかくげんごがく|さか"}],"notes":"比較言語学 ist die vergleichende (historische) Sprachwissenschaft: Sie vergleicht verwandte Sprachen systematisch, um einen gemeinsamen Ursprung nachzuweisen und eine 祖語 „Ursprache“ wie das 印欧祖語 „Urindogermanisch“ zu rekonstruieren (再建する). Typisch ist 比較言語学の方法 „komparative Methode“. Abzugrenzen ist 言語類型論, das Sprachen nach ihrer Bauweise vergleicht, unabhängig von Verwandtschaft.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本語がどの語族に属するかは議論が続いている。","romaji":"Nihongo ga dono gozoku ni zoku suru ka wa giron ga tsuzuite iru.","german":"Zu welcher Sprachfamilie das Japanische gehört, wird weiterhin diskutiert.","furigana":"にほんご|ごぞく|ぞく|ぎろん|つづ"},{"japanese":"ドイツ語もヒンディー語も、インド・ヨーロッパ語族に属している。","romaji":"Doitsugo mo Hindīgo mo, Indo-Yōroppa gozoku ni zoku shite iru.","german":"Sowohl Deutsch als auch Hindi gehören zur indogermanischen Sprachfamilie.","furigana":"ご|ご|ごぞく|ぞく"},{"japanese":"フィンランド語はウラル語族の言語なので、ほかの北欧の言語とは系統が違う。","romaji":"Finrandogo wa Uraru gozoku no gengo na node, hoka no Hokuō no gengo to wa keitō ga chigau.","german":"Finnisch gehört zur uralischen Sprachfamilie und ist daher mit den anderen nordischen Sprachen nicht verwandt.","furigana":"ご|ごぞく|げんご|ほくおう|げんご|けいとう|ちが"}],"notes":"語族 bezeichnet eine Sprachfamilie, also eine Gruppe von Sprachen mit gemeinsamem Ursprung, z. B. インド・ヨーロッパ語族 oder ウラル語族. Die Zugehörigkeit drückt man mit 〜語族に属する aus; eine Untergruppe innerhalb eines 語族 heißt 語派 (ゲルマン語派). Ob Japanisch zu einem größeren 語族 gehört, ist umstritten; oft spricht man vom 日琉語族 „japonische Sprachfamilie“ aus Japanisch und den Ryūkyū-Sprachen.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ゲルマン語派にはドイツ語や英語が含まれる。","romaji":"Geruman goha ni wa Doitsu-go ya Eigo ga fukumareru.","german":"Zum germanischen Sprachzweig gehören Deutsch und Englisch.","furigana":"ごは|ご|えいご|ふく"},{"japanese":"フランス語やイタリア語は、ラテン語から生まれたロマンス語派の言語だ。","romaji":"Furansugo ya Itariago wa, Ratengo kara umareta Romansu goha no gengo da.","german":"Französisch und Italienisch sind romanische Sprachen, die aus dem Lateinischen hervorgegangen sind.","furigana":"ご|ご|ご|う|ごは|げんご"},{"japanese":"ロシア語とポーランド語は同じスラブ語派に属するので、単語がよく似ている。","romaji":"Roshiago to Pōrandogo wa onaji Surabu goha ni zoku suru node, tango ga yoku nite iru.","german":"Russisch und Polnisch gehören zum selben slawischen Zweig, deshalb ähneln sich viele Wörter.","furigana":"ご|ご|おな|ごは|ぞく|たんご|に"}],"notes":"語派 ist die Ebene unterhalb des 語族: ein Zweig näher verwandter Sprachen innerhalb einer Sprachfamilie, etwa ゲルマン語派, ロマンス語派 oder スラブ語派 innerhalb der indogermanischen Familie. Man sagt 〜語派に属する oder 〜語派の言語. Das Wort ist ein Fachbegriff der Sprachwissenschaft und kommt im Alltag kaum vor.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"言語類型論は世界の言語を構造的に分類する。","romaji":"Gengo ruikeiron wa sekai no gengo o kōzō-teki ni bunrui suru.","german":"Linguistic typology classifies the world's languages by structural features.","furigana":"げんごるいけいろん|せかい|げんご|こうぞうてき|ぶんるい"},{"japanese":"言語類型論から見ると、日本語と韓国語はよく似た特徴を持っている。","romaji":"Gengo ruikeiron kara miru to, Nihongo to Kankokugo wa yoku nita tokuchō o motte iru.","german":"From a typological point of view, Japanese and Korean share very similar features.","furigana":"げんごるいけいろん|み|にほんご|かんこくご|に|とくちょう|も"},{"japanese":"言語類型論の授業で、語順による言語の分類を学んだ。","romaji":"Gengo ruikeiron no jugyō de, gojun ni yoru gengo no bunrui o mananda.","german":"In the typology class we learned to classify languages by their word order.","furigana":"げんごるいけいろん|じゅぎょう|ごじゅん|げんご|ぶんるい|まな"}],"notes":"言語類型論 is linguistic typology: it groups languages by the way they are built, regardless of whether they are related. Classic categories are word-order types such as SOV型 and SVO型 as well as 膠着語 \"agglutinative language\", 屈折語 \"inflecting language\" and 孤立語 \"isolating language\"; Japanese is thus regarded as an SOV language and a 膠着語. The counterpart is 比較言語学, which asks about common descent.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"方言学の調査で地域ごとの言語差が記録された。","romaji":"Hōgengaku no chōsa de chiiki-goto no gengo-sa ga kiroku sareta.","german":"Dialectological surveys documented regional language differences.","furigana":"ほうげんがく|ちょうさ|ちいき|げんごさ|きろく"},{"japanese":"方言学者たちは、お年寄りの話し言葉を録音して記録している。","romaji":"Hōgengakusha-tachi wa, otoshiyori no hanashikotoba o rokuon shite kiroku shite iru.","german":"Dialectologists record the speech of elderly people and document it.","furigana":"ほうげんがくしゃ|としよ|はな|ことば|ろくおん|きろく"},{"japanese":"方言学の授業で、「ありがとう」の言い方を地図にまとめた。","romaji":"Hōgengaku no jugyō de, 'arigatō' no iikata o chizu ni matometa.","german":"In the dialectology class we put the different ways of saying 「ありがとう」 together on a map.","furigana":"ほうげんがく|じゅぎょう|い|かた|ちず"}],"notes":"方言学 is dialectology, which studies the regional varieties of a language, such as differences in vocabulary, pronunciation and accent. Typical are 方言調査 \"dialect survey\" and 方言地図 \"dialect map\"; well known in Japan is the 方言周圏論 of 柳田国男, according to which old words have been preserved in concentric circles around the old center 京都. The base word 方言 \"dialect\" is an everyday word, whereas 方言学 is a technical term.","levelNote":"Compound: 方言 (N2) + 学 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"故郷の訛りが抜けない。","romaji":"Kokyō no namari ga nukenai.","german":"Den Akzent meiner Heimat werde ich nicht los.","furigana":"こきょう|なま|ぬ"},{"japanese":"彼の英語には少しドイツ語なまりがある。","romaji":"Kare no Eigo ni wa sukoshi Doitsugo namari ga aru.","german":"Sein Englisch hat einen leichten deutschen Akzent.","furigana":"かれ|えいご|すこ|ご"},{"japanese":"祖母は東北なまりが強くて、最初は何を言っているのか分からなかった。","romaji":"Sobo wa Tōhoku namari ga tsuyokute, saisho wa nani o itte iru no ka wakaranakatta.","german":"Meine Großmutter hat einen starken Tōhoku-Akzent; anfangs habe ich gar nicht verstanden, was sie sagt.","furigana":"そぼ|とうほく|つよ|さいしょ|なに|い|わ"}],"notes":"訛り (meist in Kana: なまり) ist die regionale oder fremdsprachliche Färbung der Aussprache, also das, was man im Deutschen „Akzent“ nennt. Typisch sind 訛りがある, 訛りが強い, 訛りが抜けない und als Suffix 東北なまり, 関西なまり oder ドイツ語なまりの英語. 方言 bezeichnet dagegen das ganze Dialektsystem mit eigenem Wortschatz und eigener Grammatik. Das Verb dazu ist 訛る; das Lehnwort アクセント meint im Japanischen meist den Tonhöhenakzent, nicht den „Akzent“ im Sinne von 訛り.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"俗語は正式な場面では使うべきではない。","romaji":"Zokugo wa seishiki na bamen de wa tsukau beki de wa nai.","german":"Slang should not be used in formal situations.","furigana":"ぞくご|せいしき|ばめん|つか"},{"japanese":"この辞書では、俗語に「俗」という記号が付いている。","romaji":"Kono jisho de wa, zokugo ni 'zoku' to iu kigō ga tsuite iru.","german":"In this dictionary, slang words are marked with the symbol 「俗」.","furigana":"じしょ|ぞくご|ぞく|きごう|つ"},{"japanese":"「ヤバい」はもともと俗語だったが、今では広く使われている。","romaji":"'Yabai' wa motomoto zokugo datta ga, ima de wa hiroku tsukawarete iru.","german":"「ヤバい」 was originally slang, but today it is used widely.","furigana":"ぞくご|いま|ひろ|つか"}],"notes":"俗語 denotes casual, colloquial words and slang that do not belong to standard or written language. The word itself is matter-of-fact and descriptive and appears above all in dictionaries and discussions of language (俗語的な表現; related is 俗に言う \"as one says colloquially\"); in casual conversation one rather says スラング. To be distinguished are 口語 \"spoken language\" as the opposite of 文語 and 隠語 \"secret language of a group\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"業界の隠語は部外者には理解しにくい。","romaji":"Gyōkai no ingo wa bugaisha ni wa rikai shinikui.","german":"Industry jargon is hard for outsiders to understand.","furigana":"ぎょうかい|いんご|ぶがいしゃ|りかい"},{"japanese":"「ホシ」は警察の隠語で、犯人のことだ。","romaji":"'Hoshi' wa keisatsu no ingo de, hannin no koto da.","german":"「ホシ」 is police slang and means the culprit.","furigana":"けいさつ|いんご|はんにん"},{"japanese":"店員たちは、お客さんに分からないように隠語で話していた。","romaji":"Ten'in-tachi wa, okyakusan ni wakaranai yō ni ingo de hanashite ita.","german":"The shop staff talked in code so that the customers would not understand.","furigana":"てんいん|きゃく|わ|いんご|はな"}],"notes":"隠語 is a secret or special language that a certain group uses so that outsiders do not understand – for example police, crooks, but also merchants or restaurant staff. Well-known examples are ホシ for the culprit or デカ for a detective. Less secretive is 業界用語 \"industry jargon\"; 専門用語 and 術語 are official technical terms, 俗語 general slang. Typical combinations: 隠語を使う, 〜の隠語で.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"専門的な術語を平易な言葉で説明する。","romaji":"Senmon-teki na jutsugo o heii na kotoba de setsumei suru.","german":"Man erklärt Fachbegriffe in einfachen Worten.","furigana":"せんもんてき|じゅつご|へいい|ことば|せつめい"},{"japanese":"論文の最初で、使用する術語をきちんと定義しておく。","romaji":"Ronbun no saisho de, shiyō suru jutsugo o kichinto teigi shite oku.","german":"Am Anfang einer Arbeit definiert man die verwendeten Fachbegriffe sorgfältig.","furigana":"ろんぶん|さいしょ|しよう|じゅつご|ていぎ"},{"japanese":"この分野の術語には、ドイツ語から入ったものが多い。","romaji":"Kono bun'ya no jutsugo ni wa, Doitsugo kara haitta mono ga ōi.","german":"Viele Fachbegriffe dieses Gebiets stammen aus dem Deutschen.","furigana":"ぶんや|じゅつご|ご|はい|おお"}],"notes":"術語 ist ein Fachterminus eines bestimmten Wissensgebiets und klingt gehoben und wissenschaftlich. Im Alltag und in den meisten Texten sagt man stattdessen 専門用語, in Fachkreisen auch einfach 用語. Achtung, Homophon: 述語（じゅつご）ist das „Prädikat“ in der Grammatik. Typische Verbindungen: 術語を定義する, 医学の術語.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"明治時代に多くの造語が作られた。","romaji":"Meiji jidai ni ōku no zōgo ga tsukurareta.","german":"Many coined words were created in the Meiji period.","furigana":"めいじじだい|おお|ぞうご|つく"},{"japanese":"「哲学」は、明治時代に西周が作った造語だ。","romaji":"'Tetsugaku' wa, Meiji jidai ni Nishi Amane ga tsukutta zōgo da.","german":"「哲学」 is a coined word created by Nishi Amane in the Meiji period.","furigana":"てつがく|めいじじだい||つく|ぞうご"},{"japanese":"この作家は造語が得意で、作品には新しい言葉がたくさん出てくる。","romaji":"Kono sakka wa zōgo ga tokui de, sakuhin ni wa atarashii kotoba ga takusan dete kuru.","german":"This author is good at coining words, and many new words appear in his works.","furigana":"さっか|ぞうご|とくい|さくひん|あたら|ことば|で"}],"notes":"造語 means both the forming of new words (造語する, 造語力 \"word-formation ability\") and the word created in this way. Many words that are everyday today, such as 哲学 \"philosophy\", arose in the Meiji period as 造語 for Western concepts; 和製英語 such as サラリーマン are also a kind of 造語. In contrast to 新語, 造語 emphasizes the deliberate creation; 新語 only says that a word is new.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ラテン語は死語とされるが学術分野では今も使われる。","romaji":"Raten-go wa shigo to sareru ga gakujutsu bun'ya de wa ima mo tsukawareru.","german":"Latin is regarded as a dead language, but it is still used in academic fields.","furigana":"ご|しご|がくじゅつぶんや|いま|つか"},{"japanese":"「ナウい」なんて、今ではすっかり死語だよ。","romaji":"'Naui' nante, ima de wa sukkari shigo da yo.","german":"Nobody says 「ナウい」 anymore; it is completely obsolete now.","furigana":"いま|しご"},{"japanese":"若い社員に話したら、その言葉はもう死語ですよと笑われた。","romaji":"Wakai shain ni hanashitara, sono kotoba wa mō shigo desu yo to warawareta.","german":"When I used the word with a young colleague, he laughed and said it was already an obsolete word.","furigana":"わか|しゃいん|はな|ことば|しご|わら"}],"notes":"死語 has two meanings: technically a \"dead language\" that nobody speaks as a native language anymore (ラテン語, サンスクリット語), and colloquially an \"obsolete word\" that has gone out of fashion. In everyday life one hears almost only the second: 「ナウい」はもう死語だ or 死語になる. For endangered languages one says 消滅危機言語, not 死語.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"古語辞典を引いて古典作品を読む。","romaji":"Kogo jiten o hiite koten sakuhin o yomu.","german":"I read classical works by consulting a dictionary of classical Japanese.","furigana":"こごじてん|ひ|こてんさくひん|よ"},{"japanese":"「あはれ」は古語で、しみじみとした深い感動を表す。","romaji":"'Aware' wa kogo de, shimijimi to shita fukai kandō o arawasu.","german":"「あはれ」 is an old word expressing deep, quiet emotion.","furigana":"こご|ふか|かんどう|あらわ"},{"japanese":"古語の中には、現代語と意味が違うものもある。","romaji":"Kogo no naka ni wa, gendaigo to imi ga chigau mono mo aru.","german":"Some old words have meanings different from those in modern Japanese.","furigana":"こご|なか|げんだいご|いみ|ちが"}],"notes":"古語 are words and expressions of earlier stages of the language, above all of classical Japanese, that are no longer in use today or meant something different back then (e.g. をかし \"charming\"). A 古語辞典 is the dictionary for classical Japanese that students use in the subject 古典. The opposite term is 現代語 \"modern language\"; a word that has only recently gone out of fashion is, by contrast, colloquially called 死語.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"毎年新語や流行語が辞書に追加される。","romaji":"Maitoshi shingo ya ryūkōgo ga jisho ni tsuika sareru.","german":"Every year new words and buzzwords are added to the dictionary.","furigana":"まいとししんご|りゅうこうご|じしょ|ついか"},{"japanese":"インターネットの普及で、たくさんの新語が生まれた。","romaji":"Intānetto no fukyū de, takusan no shingo ga umareta.","german":"With the spread of the Internet, many new words have come into being.","furigana":"ふきゅう|しんご|う"},{"japanese":"今日の授業では、まず新語の意味と読み方を確認しましょう。","romaji":"Kyō no jugyō de wa, mazu shingo no imi to yomikata o kakunin shimashō.","german":"In today's class, let's first check the meaning and reading of the new vocabulary.","furigana":"きょう|じゅぎょう|しんご|いみ|よ|かた|かくにん"}],"notes":"新語 is a newly emerged word, i.e. a neologism, for example from technology or society (新語が生まれる, 新語を辞書に収録する). In class, 新語 also denotes the new vocabulary of a lesson. 造語, by contrast, emphasizes the process of forming words, and 流行語 a buzzword that may quickly disappear again; the annual prize 新語・流行語大賞 combines both.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"流行語大賞はその年を象徴する言葉を選ぶ。","romaji":"Ryūkōgo taishō wa sono toshi o shōchō suru kotoba o erabu.","german":"The Buzzword Award chooses the word that symbolizes the year.","furigana":"りゅうこうごたいしょう|とし|しょうちょう|ことば|えら"},{"japanese":"ドラマの主人公のセリフが、その年の流行語になった。","romaji":"Dorama no shujinkō no serifu ga, sono toshi no ryūkōgo ni natta.","german":"A line spoken by the main character of a drama became the buzzword of the year.","furigana":"しゅじんこう|とし|りゅうこうご"},{"japanese":"流行語はすぐに古くなるので、使い方に気をつけたほうがいい。","romaji":"Ryūkōgo wa sugu ni furuku naru node, tsukaikata ni ki o tsuketa hō ga ii.","german":"Buzzwords quickly become dated, so you should be careful how you use them.","furigana":"りゅうこうご|ふる|つか|かた|き"}],"notes":"流行語 is a buzzword or catchphrase that suddenly gets used everywhere at a certain time and often disappears just as quickly. Typical are 流行語になる and 〜年の流行語. Every year in December the 新語・流行語大賞 is awarded, colloquially shortened to 流行語大賞. A buzzword that nobody uses any more becomes, colloquially, a 死語.","levelNote":"Compound: 流行 (N3) + 語 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日常会話では略語が頻繁に使われる。","romaji":"Nichijō kaiwa de wa ryakugo ga hinpan ni tsukawareru.","german":"Abbreviations are used frequently in everyday conversation.","furigana":"にちじょうかいわ|りゃくご|ひんぱん|つか"},{"japanese":"「就活」は「就職活動」の略語だ。","romaji":"'Shūkatsu' wa 'shūshoku katsudō' no ryakugo da.","german":"「就活」 is an abbreviation of 「就職活動」.","furigana":"しゅうかつ|しゅうしょくかつどう|りゃくご"},{"japanese":"報告書には略語が多くて、新人には読みにくい。","romaji":"Hōkokusho ni wa ryakugo ga ōkute, shinjin ni wa yominikui.","german":"The report contains so many abbreviations that it is hard for new employees to read.","furigana":"ほうこくしょ|りゃくご|おお|しんじん|よ"}],"notes":"略語 is an abbreviated word: in Japanese often formed by shortening and combining (スマホ from スマートフォン, 就活 from 就職活動) or as an initialism such as NHK. In conversation, abbreviations are usually explained with 〜の略だ \"is short for ...\"; the verb for it is 略す. Typical combinations: 略語を使う, 略語が多い.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「出鱈目」は当て字の一例である。","romaji":"\"Detarame\" wa ateji no ichirei de aru.","german":"「出鱈目」 is an example of ateji.","furigana":"でたらめ|あ|じ|いちれい"},{"japanese":"「珈琲」は「コーヒー」の当て字だ。","romaji":"\"Kōhī\" wa \"kōhī\" no ateji da.","german":"「珈琲」 is an ateji for 「コーヒー」.","furigana":"|あ|じ"},{"japanese":"最近は、子どもの名前に当て字を使う親も多い。","romaji":"Saikin wa, kodomo no namae ni ateji o tsukau oya mo ōi.","german":"Recently, many parents use ateji for their children's names.","furigana":"さいきん|こ|なまえ|あ|じ|つか|おや|おお"}],"notes":"当て字 are kanji used only for their sound (or by free association) for a word, without fitting its actual meaning: 出鱈目, 珈琲 for コーヒー, 亜米利加 for America. In a broader sense the term also covers a spelling by meaning with no fitting reading. Today the word often comes up in connection with unusual given names: 名前に当て字を使う. Variant spellings are あて字 and 宛字.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「大人」を「おとな」と読むのは熟字訓だ。","romaji":"\"Otona\" o \"otona\" to yomu no wa jukujikun da.","german":"Reading 「大人」 as 「おとな」 is a jukujikun.","furigana":"おとな|よ|じゅくじくん"},{"japanese":"「今日」や「明日」も熟字訓の例だ。","romaji":"\"Kyō\" ya \"ashita\" mo jukujikun no rei da.","german":"「今日」 and 「明日」 are also examples of jukujikun.","furigana":"きょう|あした|じゅくじくん|れい"},{"japanese":"熟字訓は一字ずつ読みを分けられないので、覚えるしかない。","romaji":"Jukujikun wa ichiji zutsu yomi o wakerarenai node, oboeru shika nai.","german":"Jukujikun cannot be divided into readings for individual characters, so you just have to memorize them.","furigana":"じゅくじくん|いちじ|よ|わ|おぼ"}],"notes":"熟字訓 is a reading that belongs only to the whole kanji compound and cannot be divided among the individual characters: 大人＝おとな, 今日＝きょう, 明日＝あした, 時雨＝しぐれ. The characters fit the meaning, not the sound – this is the difference from 当て字 in the narrower sense. One says 熟字訓で読む; lists of such readings are in the appendix of the 常用漢字表.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"音読みは中国語の発音に由来する。","romaji":"On'yomi wa Chūgokugo no hatsuon ni yurai suru.","german":"The on reading derives from Chinese pronunciation.","furigana":"おんよ|ちゅうごくご|はつおん|ゆらい"},{"japanese":"「山」の音読みは「サン」で、訓読みは「やま」だ。","romaji":"'Yama' no on'yomi wa 'san' de, kun'yomi wa 'yama' da.","german":"The on reading of 「山」 is 「サン」 and its kun reading is 「やま」.","furigana":"やま|おんよ|くんよ"},{"japanese":"熟語の中の漢字は、たいてい音読みで読む。","romaji":"Jukugo no naka no kanji wa, taitei on'yomi de yomu.","german":"Kanji within compounds are usually read with the on reading.","furigana":"じゅくご|なか|かんじ|おんよ|よ"}],"notes":"音読み is the Sino-Japanese reading of a kanji, going back to the Chinese pronunciation at the time of borrowing (depending on the period 呉音, 漢音 or 唐音). It appears above all in kanji compounds (山 as サン in 富士山), while 訓読み is the native reading; as a する-verb, 音読みする means \"to read with the on reading\". Not to be confused with 音読（おんどく）\"reading aloud\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"訓読みは漢字に日本語の意味を当てた読み方だ。","romaji":"Kun'yomi wa kanji ni Nihongo no imi o ateta yomikata da.","german":"The kun reading is the way of reading in which a Japanese meaning is assigned to a kanji.","furigana":"くんよ|かんじ|にほんご|いみ|あ|よ|かた"},{"japanese":"「生きる」「生まれる」「生える」のように、「生」には訓読みがたくさんある。","romaji":"'Ikiru', 'umareru', 'haeru' no yō ni, 'sei' ni wa kun'yomi ga takusan aru.","german":"The kanji 「生」 has many kun readings, as in 「生きる」, 「生まれる」 and 「生える」.","furigana":"い|う|は|せい|くんよ"},{"japanese":"名前の漢字は、音読みか訓読みか分かりにくいことが多い。","romaji":"Namae no kanji wa, on'yomi ka kun'yomi ka wakarinikui koto ga ōi.","german":"With kanji in names, it is often hard to tell whether they are read with the on or kun reading.","furigana":"なまえ|かんじ|おんよ|くんよ|わ|おお"}],"notes":"訓読み is the native Japanese reading of a kanji: a Japanese word (和語) of the same meaning was assigned to the character, e.g. 山 → やま. It typically occurs with standalone kanji and with verbs and adjectives with okurigana (食べる, 高い), while compounds mostly have 音読み. Not to be confused with 訓読（くんどく）, the reading of Chinese texts (漢文) in Japanese word order.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"漢字は表意文字の代表例である。","romaji":"Kanji wa hyōi moji no daihyō-rei de aru.","german":"Kanji are a representative example of ideograms.","furigana":"かんじ|ひょういもじ|だいひょうれい"},{"japanese":"表意文字である漢字は、形から意味を推測できることがある。","romaji":"Hyōi moji de aru kanji wa, katachi kara imi o suisoku dekiru koto ga aru.","german":"Because kanji are ideograms, you can sometimes guess their meaning from their form.","furigana":"ひょういもじ|かんじ|かたち|いみ|すいそく"},{"japanese":"古代エジプトのヒエログリフには、表意文字と表音文字の両方が含まれている。","romaji":"Kodai Ejiputo no hierogurifu ni wa, hyōi moji to hyōon moji no ryōhō ga fukumarete iru.","german":"Ancient Egyptian hieroglyphs include both ideograms and phonograms.","furigana":"こだい|ひょういもじ|ひょうおんもじ|りょうほう|ふく"}],"notes":"表意文字 is a written character that represents a meaning rather than a sound, i.e. an \"ideogram\"; the opposite term is 表音文字 \"phonogram\". Kanji are traditionally called 表意文字, but in the specialist literature they are more precisely called 表語文字 \"logograms\", because each character represents a whole word or morpheme together with its pronunciation. Typical: 表意文字である, 表意文字の一種.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ひらがなとカタカナは表音文字である。","romaji":"Hiragana to katakana wa hyōon moji de aru.","german":"Hiragana and katakana are phonograms.","furigana":"ひょうおんもじ"},{"japanese":"アルファベットは表音文字だが、英語のつづりと発音は一致しないことが多い。","romaji":"Arufabetto wa hyōon moji da ga, Eigo no tsuzuri to hatsuon wa itchi shinai koto ga ōi.","german":"The alphabet is a phonetic script, but in English spelling and pronunciation often do not match.","furigana":"ひょうおんもじ|えいご|はつおん|いっち|おお"},{"japanese":"ハングルは、15世紀に作られた表音文字だ。","romaji":"Hanguru wa, jūgo seiki ni tsukurareta hyōon moji da.","german":"Hangul is a phonetic script created in the 15th century.","furigana":"せいき|つく|ひょうおんもじ"}],"notes":"表音文字 is a written character that represents a sound rather than a meaning; the opposite term is 表意文字. One distinguishes 音節文字 \"syllabary\" such as hiragana and katakana and 音素文字 \"alphabet\" such as the Latin alphabet. Not to be confused with 発音記号 \"phonetic transcription\" in the sense of pronunciation notation such as the IPA.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本語の書記体系は世界で最も複雑だと言われる。","romaji":"Nihongo no shoki taikei wa sekai de mottomo fukuzatsu da to iwareru.","german":"The Japanese writing system is said to be the most complex in the world.","furigana":"にほんご|しょきたいけい|せかい|もっと|ふくざつ|い"},{"japanese":"韓国語の書記体系は、20世紀に漢字からハングル中心へと変わった。","romaji":"Kankokugo no shoki taikei wa, nijusseiki ni kanji kara hanguru chūshin e to kawatta.","german":"The Korean writing system changed in the 20th century from kanji to being centered on hangul.","furigana":"かんこくご|しょきたいけい|せいき|かんじ|ちゅうしん|か"},{"japanese":"文字を持たない言語のために、新しい書記体系が作られることもある。","romaji":"Moji o motanai gengo no tame ni, atarashii shoki taikei ga tsukurareru koto mo aru.","german":"New writing systems are sometimes created for languages that have no script.","furigana":"もじ|も|げんご|あたら|しょきたいけい|つく"}],"notes":"書記体系 is the technical term for the writing system of a language, i.e. the totality of the characters used and their rules; the Japanese 書記体系 combines kanji, hiragana, katakana and in part Latin letters. In everyday life one rather says 文字 or 表記 (日本語の表記); 書記体系 sounds scholarly. Not to be confused with 書記 alone, which means \"clerk, secretary\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"正書法の統一は国語政策の重要な課題だ。","romaji":"Seishohō no tōitsu wa kokugo seisaku no jūyō na kadai da.","german":"Die Vereinheitlichung der Orthographie ist eine wichtige Aufgabe der Sprachpolitik.","furigana":"せいしょほう|とういつ|こくごせいさく|じゅうよう|かだい"},{"japanese":"ドイツでは1990年代に正書法の改革が行われた。","romaji":"Doitsu de wa senkyūhyaku kyūjū-nendai ni seishohō no kaikaku ga okonawareta.","german":"In Deutschland wurde in den 1990er-Jahren eine Rechtschreibreform durchgeführt.","furigana":"ねんだい|せいしょほう|かいかく|おこな"},{"japanese":"英語の正書法は不規則で、学習者を悩ませる。","romaji":"Eigo no seishohō wa fukisoku de, gakushūsha o nayamaseru.","german":"Die englische Rechtschreibung ist unregelmäßig und bereitet Lernenden Kopfzerbrechen.","furigana":"えいご|せいしょほう|ふきそく|がくしゅうしゃ|なや"}],"notes":"正書法 ist die Orthographie, also die verbindlichen Regeln der Rechtschreibung einer Sprache. Das Japanische hat streng genommen kein einheitliches 正書法, sondern Richtlinien wie 現代仮名遣い oder 送り仮名の付け方, weshalb man im Alltag eher von 表記 „Schreibweise“ spricht. Typisch sind 正書法に従う und 正書法の改革 „Rechtschreibreform“, etwa die deutsche Reform der 1990er-Jahre.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"送り仮名の付け方にはルールがある。","romaji":"Okurigana no tsukekata ni wa rūru ga aru.","german":"There are rules for how to attach okurigana.","furigana":"おく|がな|つ|かた"},{"japanese":"「行う」と「行なう」のように、送り仮名が二通りある言葉もある。","romaji":"'Okonau' to 'okonau' no yō ni, okurigana ga futatōri aru kotoba mo aru.","german":"Some words, like 行う and 行なう, have two possible okurigana forms.","furigana":"おこな||おく|がな|ふたとお|ことば"},{"japanese":"漢字テストで送り仮名を間違えて、点数を引かれた。","romaji":"Kanji tesuto de okurigana o machigaete, tensū o hikareta.","german":"I wrote the okurigana wrong on a kanji test and lost points.","furigana":"かんじ|おく|がな|まちが|てんすう|ひ"}],"notes":"送り仮名 are the kana that follow a kanji and spell out the rest of the word, above all the inflectional ending: 書く, 新しい, 静かだ. The official rules are laid down in the guidelines 送り仮名の付け方; nevertheless there are permitted double forms such as 行う／行なう or 表す／表わす. One says 送り仮名を付ける, 送り仮名を間違える. Not to be confused with 振り仮名, the small reading aid above or beside a kanji.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"難しい漢字には振り仮名が振ってある。","romaji":"Muzukashii kanji ni wa furigana ga futte aru.","german":"Furigana are provided for difficult kanji.","furigana":"むずか|かんじ|ふ|がな|ふ"},{"japanese":"子ども向けの本には、すべての漢字にふりがなが付いている。","romaji":"Kodomo-muke no hon ni wa, subete no kanji ni furigana ga tsuite iru.","german":"In children's books, every kanji has furigana.","furigana":"こ|む|ほん|かんじ|つ"},{"japanese":"申込書の名前の欄には、ふりがなも書いてください。","romaji":"Mōshikomisho no namae no ran ni wa, furigana mo kaite kudasai.","german":"Please also write your name in furigana in the name field of the application form.","furigana":"もうしこみしょ|なまえ|らん|か"}],"notes":"振り仮名 (usually written in kana: ふりがな) is the small reading aid in kana above or beside a kanji. The typical verb is 振る or 付ける: ふりがなを振る, ふりがなが振ってある; in printing, ルビ is also used. On forms, フリガナ in katakana means you should enter the reading of your name in katakana. Not to be confused with 送り仮名, the kana endings after a kanji.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"常用漢字表には二千百三十六字が収録されている。","romaji":"Jōyō kanji-hyō ni wa nisen-hyaku-sanjūroku-ji ga shūroku sarete iru.","german":"The jōyō kanji list contains 2,136 characters.","furigana":"じょうようかんじひょう|にせんひゃくさんじゅうろくじ|しゅうろく"},{"japanese":"新聞では、常用漢字以外の漢字にふりがなを付けることが多い。","romaji":"Shinbun de wa, jōyō kanji igai no kanji ni furigana o tsukeru koto ga ōi.","german":"Newspapers often add furigana to kanji that are not jōyō kanji.","furigana":"しんぶん|じょうようかんじいがい|かんじ|つ|おお"},{"japanese":"「鬱」は2010年に常用漢字に加えられた。","romaji":"'Utsu' wa nisen-jū-nen ni jōyō kanji ni kuwaerareta.","german":"The kanji 「鬱」 was added to the jōyō kanji in 2010.","furigana":"うつ|ねん|じょうようかんじ|くわ"}],"notes":"常用漢字 are the 2136 kanji of the official list 常用漢字表 (introduced in 1981, expanded in 2010), which serves as a guideline for government offices, newspapers and school textbooks. Kanji outside the list are called 表外漢字; for given names there are in addition the 人名用漢字, and the characters learned in elementary school are called 教育漢字. Newspapers often add ふりがな to non-常用漢字 or write the word in kana.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人名に使われる異体字が混乱を招くことがある。","romaji":"Jinmei ni tsukawareru itaiji ga konran o maneku koto ga aru.","german":"Variant characters used in personal names can cause confusion.","furigana":"じんめい|つか|いたいじ|こんらん|まね"},{"japanese":"名字の「高」を、異体字の「はしごだか」で書く人もいる。","romaji":"Myōji no 'taka' o, itaiji no 'hashigodaka' de kaku hito mo iru.","german":"Some people write the 「高」 in their surname with the variant 「はしごだか」.","furigana":"みょうじ|たか|いたいじ|か|ひと"},{"japanese":"パソコンでは表示できない異体字もある。","romaji":"Pasokon de wa hyōji dekinai itaiji mo aru.","german":"Some variant characters cannot be displayed on a computer.","furigana":"ひょうじ|いたいじ"}],"notes":"異体字 are different written forms of one and the same character with the same meaning and reading, e.g. 髙 beside 高, or 齋 and 斎 in the name 斎藤. These also include the old full forms (旧字体) such as 國 for 国. In everyday life one meets them above all in family names, where they often cause problems at government offices or on computers; 字体, by contrast, denotes the shape of a character in general.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「峠」は日本で作られた国字の一つだ。","romaji":"\"Tōge\" wa Nihon de tsukurareta kokuji no hitotsu da.","german":"「峠」 is one of the kokuji created in Japan.","furigana":"とうげ|にほん|つく|こくじ|ひと"},{"japanese":"「畑」や「込」のような国字には、音読みがないものが多い。","romaji":"\"Hatake\" ya \"komu\" no yō na kokuji ni wa, on'yomi ga nai mono ga ōi.","german":"Many kokuji such as 「畑」 and 「込」 have no on reading.","furigana":"はたけ||こくじ|おんよ|おお"},{"japanese":"魚へんの漢字には、「鰯」のような国字がたくさんある。","romaji":"Uohen no kanji ni wa, \"iwashi\" no yō na kokuji ga takusan aru.","german":"Among kanji with the fish radical there are many kokuji like 「鰯」.","furigana":"うお|かんじ||こくじ"}],"notes":"国字 has several meanings (\"national script\", formerly also \"kana\"); in a learning context it almost always means kanji created in Japan itself (also 和製漢字), such as 峠, 畑, 込, 働 or many fish names like 鰯. Most 国字 have only a kun reading; some, like 働（ドウ）, have acquired an on reading. Typical: 〜は国字の一つだ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"正しい筆順で漢字を書くことが大切だ。","romaji":"Tadashii hitsujun de kanji o kaku koto ga taisetsu da.","german":"It is important to write kanji in the correct stroke order.","furigana":"ただ|ひつじゅん|かんじ|か|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"「右」と「左」は、似ているが筆順が違う。","romaji":"'Migi' to 'hidari' wa, nite iru ga hitsujun ga chigau.","german":"「右」 and 「左」 look similar, but their stroke order is different.","furigana":"みぎ|ひだり|に|ひつじゅん|ちが"},{"japanese":"分からない漢字の筆順は、アプリで確認できる。","romaji":"Wakaranai kanji no hitsujun wa, apuri de kakunin dekiru.","german":"You can check the stroke order of kanji you don't know in an app.","furigana":"わ|かんじ|ひつじゅん|かくにん"}],"notes":"筆順 is the fixed order of strokes when writing a kanji or kana. In everyday life and at school one mostly says 書き順; 筆順 sounds somewhat more technical and appears e.g. in dictionaries and textbooks. Typical are 筆順を守る, 正しい筆順で書く and 筆順を間違える. Similar-looking characters can have different stroke orders, such as 右 and 左.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"部首から漢字の意味を推測できることがある。","romaji":"Bushu kara kanji no imi o suisoku dekiru koto ga aru.","german":"Sometimes you can infer the meaning of a kanji from its radical.","furigana":"ぶしゅ|かんじ|いみ|すいそく"},{"japanese":"「海」や「池」の部首は「さんずい」で、水に関係がある。","romaji":"'Umi' ya 'ike' no bushu wa 'sanzui' de, mizu ni kankei ga aru.","german":"The radical of 海 and 池 is \"sanzui\", and it is related to water.","furigana":"うみ|いけ|ぶしゅ|みず|かんけい"},{"japanese":"漢和辞典では、部首から漢字を探すことができる。","romaji":"Kanwa jiten de wa, bushu kara kanji o sagasu koto ga dekiru.","german":"In a kanji dictionary, you can look up characters by their radical.","furigana":"かんわじてん|ぶしゅ|かんじ|さが"}],"notes":"部首 is the radical by which kanji are classified in dictionaries, e.g. さんずい (氵) for water or にんべん (亻) for people. Many 部首 have their own names, which depend on their position: 〜へん on the left, 〜かんむり on top, 〜あし at the bottom. Typical are 部首で引く \"to look up by radical\" and 部首索引 \"radical index\". Note: a kanji has only one 部首, even if it consists of several components.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"画数の多い漢字は書くのが大変だ。","romaji":"Kakusū no ōi kanji wa kaku no ga taihen da.","german":"Kanji with many strokes are hard to write.","furigana":"かくすう|おお|かんじ|か|たいへん"},{"japanese":"読み方が分からない漢字は、画数で辞書を引くといい。","romaji":"Yomikata ga wakaranai kanji wa, kakusū de jisho o hiku to ii.","german":"If you don't know the reading of a kanji, it's best to look it up by stroke count.","furigana":"よ|かた|わ|かんじ|かくすう|じしょ|ひ"},{"japanese":"子どもの名前を決めるとき、画数を気にする親も多い。","romaji":"Kodomo no namae o kimeru toki, kakusū o ki ni suru oya mo ōi.","german":"Many parents pay attention to stroke count when choosing their child's name.","furigana":"こ|なまえ|き|かくすう|き|おや|おお"}],"notes":"画数 is the number of strokes of a kanji; the total number is more precisely called 総画数. Typical are 画数が多い／少ない and 画数で引く \"to look up by stroke count\" when you know neither the reading nor the radical. In Japan 画数 also plays a role in choosing names, because 姓名判断 \"name divination\" reads good or bad fortune from the stroke count. The counter for single strokes is 〜画 (十画の漢字).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"楷書や行書など様々な書体がある。","romaji":"Kaisho ya gyōsho nado samazama na shotai ga aru.","german":"There are various styles such as regular script and semi-cursive script.","furigana":"かいしょ|ぎょうしょ|さまざま|しょたい"},{"japanese":"発表のスライドには、読みやすい書体を選んだほうがいい。","romaji":"Happyō no suraido ni wa, yomiyasui shotai o eranda hō ga ii.","german":"For presentation slides, it is best to choose an easy-to-read typeface.","furigana":"はっぴょう|よ|しょたい|えら"},{"japanese":"ポスターのタイトルだけ、書体を太いゴシックに変えた。","romaji":"Posutā no taitoru dake, shotai o futoi goshikku ni kaeta.","german":"I changed the typeface to a bold gothic only for the title of the poster.","furigana":"しょたい|ふと|か"}],"notes":"書体 has two meanings: in printing and on computers the typeface, such as 明朝体 or ゴシック体 (today often also フォント), and in calligraphy the script style such as 楷書, 行書, 草書, 隷書 and 篆書. Typical are 書体を選ぶ, 書体を変える. Not to be confused with 字体, which means the form of an individual character (新字体／旧字体).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"正式な文書は楷書で書くのが望ましい。","romaji":"Seishiki na bunsho wa kaisho de kaku no ga nozomashii.","german":"It is desirable to write formal documents in regular script.","furigana":"せいしき|ぶんしょ|かいしょ|か|のぞ"},{"japanese":"申込書には、楷書ではっきりと記入してください。","romaji":"Mōshikomisho ni wa, kaisho de hakkiri to kinyū shite kudasai.","german":"Please fill in the application form clearly in block script.","furigana":"もうしこみしょ|かいしょ|きにゅう"},{"japanese":"書道を始めた人は、まず楷書から練習する。","romaji":"Shodō o hajimeta hito wa, mazu kaisho kara renshū suru.","german":"People who start calligraphy first practice regular script.","furigana":"しょどう|はじ|ひと|かいしょ|れんしゅう"}],"notes":"楷書 is the regular script in which every stroke is written clearly and separately; it corresponds to printed kanji and is learned first at school. On forms one often reads 楷書で記入してください \"please fill in in clear block script\". The more flowing counterparts are 行書 \"semi-cursive\" and 草書 \"grass script\"; together the three styles are called 楷行草.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"草書は流れるような筆致が特徴だ。","romaji":"Sōsho wa nagareru yō na hitchi ga tokuchō da.","german":"Cursive script is characterized by a flowing brushwork.","furigana":"そうしょ|なが|ひっち|とくちょう"},{"japanese":"草書で書かれた古い手紙は、専門家でないと読めない。","romaji":"Sōsho de kakareta furui tegami wa, senmonka de nai to yomenai.","german":"Old letters written in cursive script can only be read by experts.","furigana":"そうしょ|か|ふる|てがみ|せんもんか|よ"},{"japanese":"ひらがなは、万葉仮名の草書体から生まれた。","romaji":"Hiragana wa, man'yōgana no sōshotai kara umareta.","german":"Hiragana arose from the cursive forms of man'yōgana.","furigana":"まんようがな|そうしょたい|う"}],"notes":"草書 is the heavily abbreviated, flowing \"grass script\" in which strokes are run together; for the untrained it is hardly legible. Hiragana arose from 草書 forms of 万葉仮名. By comparison: 楷書 is the regular script and 行書 the semi-cursive in between; as a style name one also says 草書体.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"行書は楷書と草書の中間的な書体だ。","romaji":"Gyōsho wa kaisho to sōsho no chūkan-teki na shotai da.","german":"Semi-cursive script is a style between regular script and cursive script.","furigana":"ぎょうしょ|かいしょ|そうしょ|ちゅうかんてき|しょたい"},{"japanese":"祖父は年賀状の宛名をいつも行書で書く。","romaji":"Sofu wa nengajō no atena o itsumo gyōsho de kaku.","german":"My grandfather always writes the addresses on New Year's cards in semi-cursive script.","furigana":"そふ|ねんがじょう|あてな|ぎょうしょ|か"},{"japanese":"行書は楷書より速く書けるので、日常の手書きによく使われる。","romaji":"Gyōsho wa kaisho yori hayaku kakeru node, nichijō no tegaki ni yoku tsukawareru.","german":"Semi-cursive can be written faster than regular script, so it is often used for everyday handwriting.","furigana":"ぎょうしょ|かいしょ|はや|か|にちじょう|てが|つか"}],"notes":"行書 is the semi-cursive: strokes are partly joined and simplified, but the characters remain easy to read. It lies between 楷書 and 草書 and is the typical style for quick but neat handwriting, e.g. in letters, addresses on New Year's cards or signatures. One says 行書で書く or 行書体.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"万葉仮名は漢字を音だけで使う表記法だ。","romaji":"Man'yōgana wa kanji o oto dake de tsukau hyōkihō da.","german":"Man'yōgana is a way of writing in which kanji are used for their sound alone.","furigana":"まんようがな|かんじ|おと|つか|ひょうきほう"},{"japanese":"『万葉集』の歌は、主に万葉仮名で書かれている。","romaji":"'Man'yōshū' no uta wa, omo ni man'yōgana de kakarete iru.","german":"The poems of the 『万葉集』 are mainly written in man'yōgana.","furigana":"まんようしゅう|うた|おも|まんようがな|か"},{"japanese":"「あ」は「安」、「い」は「以」という万葉仮名から生まれた。","romaji":"'A' wa 'an', 'i' wa 'i' to iu man'yōgana kara umareta.","german":"The 「あ」 arose from the man'yōgana 「安」 and the 「い」 from 「以」.","furigana":"あん|い|まんようがな|う"}],"notes":"万葉仮名 are kanji that were used for their sound value alone and not for their meaning in order to write Japanese, e.g. 安 for あ or 以 for い. The name comes from the poetry collection 万葉集, in which this system is used extensively. Hiragana (from the cursive form) and katakana (from parts of the characters) later arose from simplified 万葉仮名. The word is a technical term of language and writing history.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"変体仮名は蕎麦屋の看板などで見かける。","romaji":"Hentaigana wa soba-ya no kanban nado de mikakeru.","german":"Hentaigana can be seen on signs of soba restaurants and the like.","furigana":"へんたいがな|そばや|かんばん|み"},{"japanese":"明治時代の手紙は変体仮名が多くて、とても読みにくい。","romaji":"Meiji jidai no tegami wa hentaigana ga ōkute, totemo yominikui.","german":"Letters from the Meiji period contain many hentaigana and are very hard to read.","furigana":"めいじじだい|てがみ|へんたいがな|おお|よ"},{"japanese":"1900年に仮名の字体が一つに統一され、それ以外の形が変体仮名と呼ばれるようになった。","romaji":"Senkyūhyaku-nen ni kana no jitai ga hitotsu ni tōitsu sare, sore igai no katachi ga hentaigana to yobareru yō ni natta.","german":"In 1900 the kana forms were standardized into one each, and the other forms came to be called hentaigana.","furigana":"ねん|かな|じたい|ひと|とういつ|いがい|かたち|へんたいがな|よ"}],"notes":"変体仮名 are hiragana forms that derive from different source kanji than the present-day standard characters. Only in 1900 was a single form fixed for each syllable by a school ordinance; all the others have been considered 変体仮名 since. Today they are seen mainly on traditional shop signs (e.g. at soba restaurants), in old documents and in calligraphy.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"丁寧な言い回しを心がけることが大切だ。","romaji":"Teinei na iimawashi o kokorogakeru koto ga taisetsu da.","german":"It is important to take care to use polite phrasing.","furigana":"ていねい|い|まわ|こころ|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"彼女は独特な言い回しをするので、話を聞いていて面白い。","romaji":"Kanojo wa dokutoku na iimawashi o suru node, hanashi o kiite ite omoshiroi.","german":"She has a distinctive way of putting things, so it is fun to listen to her.","furigana":"かのじょ|どくとく|い|まわ|はなし|き|おもしろ"},{"japanese":"この言い回しは、日本語として少し不自然だ。","romaji":"Kono iimawashi wa, Nihongo to shite sukoshi fushizen da.","german":"This phrasing is a little unnatural as Japanese.","furigana":"い|まわ|にほんご|すこ|ふしぜん"}],"notes":"言い回し is the way one formulates something, i.e. a particular turn of phrase or manner of expression. Typical are 独特な言い回し, 回りくどい言い回し \"roundabout phrasing\", 言い回しを工夫する and 言い回しが上手. 表現 is more general \"expression\", 言葉遣い concerns rather word choice and politeness, and 言い方 is the everyday equivalent of 言い回し.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"語調を和らげて話すと相手も落ち着く。","romaji":"Gochō o yawaragete hanasu to aite mo ochitsuku.","german":"If you speak in a softened tone, the other person calms down too.","furigana":"ごちょう|やわ|はな|あいて|お|つ"},{"japanese":"彼は急に語調を強めて反論した。","romaji":"Kare wa kyū ni gochō o tsuyomete hanron shita.","german":"He suddenly raised his tone and argued back.","furigana":"かれ|きゅう|ごちょう|つよ|はんろん"},{"japanese":"文章の語調をそろえるため、すべて「です・ます」に統一した。","romaji":"Bunshō no gochō o soroeru tame, subete 'desu, masu' ni tōitsu shita.","german":"To make the tone of the text uniform, I unified everything into 「です・ます」.","furigana":"ぶんしょう|ごちょう|とういつ"}],"notes":"語調 is the tone in which something is said or written: the strength, sharpness or softness of the manner of expression. Typical are 語調を強める, 語調を和らげる, 語調が荒い and 激しい語調で. 口調 is almost synonymous and more common in everyday use; 語気 emphasizes the vehemence more (語気を荒らげる). 語調 can also refer to the style of a text.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"言語障害の早期発見と治療が重要だ。","romaji":"Gengo shōgai no sōki hakken to chiryō ga jūyō da.","german":"Early detection and treatment of speech disorders is important.","furigana":"げんごしょうがい|そうきはっけん|ちりょう|じゅうよう"},{"japanese":"事故の後遺症で、軽い言語障害が残った。","romaji":"Jiko no kōishō de, karui gengo shōgai ga nokotta.","german":"A mild speech disorder remained as an after-effect of the accident.","furigana":"じこ|こういしょう|かる|げんごしょうがい|のこ"},{"japanese":"言語聴覚士は、言語障害のある人のリハビリを担当する。","romaji":"Gengo chōkakushi wa, gengo shōgai no aru hito no rihabiri o tantō suru.","german":"Speech-language therapists are in charge of rehabilitation for people with speech disorders.","furigana":"げんごちょうかくし|げんごしょうがい|ひと|たんとう"}],"notes":"言語障害 is the umbrella term for disorders of speech or language, such as 構音障害 \"articulation disorder\", 吃音 \"stuttering\" or 失語症 \"aphasia\". It is a medical, matter-of-fact term; such disorders are treated by 言語聴覚士 \"speech-language therapists\". Typical are 言語障害がある, 言語障害が残る. In official texts 障害 is sometimes also written 障がい.","levelNote":"Compound: 言語 (N3) + 障害 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"脳卒中の後遺症で失語症になることがある。","romaji":"Nōsocchū no kōishō de shitsugo-shō ni naru koto ga aru.","german":"Aphasia can occur as an after-effect of a stroke.","furigana":"のうそっちゅう|こういしょう|しつごしょう"},{"japanese":"父は脳梗塞で失語症になり、言葉がうまく出てこなくなった。","romaji":"Chichi wa nōkōsoku de shitsugo-shō ni nari, kotoba ga umaku dete konaku natta.","german":"My father developed aphasia from a cerebral infarction, and words no longer come out properly.","furigana":"ちち|のうこうそく|しつごしょう|ことば|で"},{"japanese":"失語症の人と話すときは、ゆっくり短い文で話すとよい。","romaji":"Shitsugo-shō no hito to hanasu toki wa, yukkuri mijikai bun de hanasu to yoi.","german":"When talking with a person with aphasia, it is good to speak slowly in short sentences.","furigana":"しつごしょう|ひと|はな|みじか|ぶん|はな"}],"notes":"失語症 is aphasia: a disorder acquired through brain damage, mostly after a stroke, in which speaking, understanding, reading or writing are impaired. Typical are 失語症になる, 失語症の患者. To be distinguished is 構音障害, in which only pronunciation is disturbed while language ability itself is preserved; 失語症 is part of the umbrella term 言語障害.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"吃音は適切な訓練で改善できる場合が多い。","romaji":"Kitsuon wa tekisetsu na kunren de kaizen dekiru baai ga ōi.","german":"Stuttering can often be improved with appropriate training.","furigana":"きつおん|てきせつ|くんれん|かいぜん|ばあい|おお"},{"japanese":"彼は子どもの頃から吃音に悩んできた。","romaji":"Kare wa kodomo no koro kara kitsuon ni nayande kita.","german":"He has suffered from stuttering since childhood.","furigana":"かれ|こ|ころ|きつおん|なや"},{"japanese":"吃音のある人が話しているときは、最後まで待って聞くことが大切だ。","romaji":"Kitsuon no aru hito ga hanashite iru toki wa, saigo made matte kiku koto ga taisetsu da.","german":"When a person who stutters is speaking, it is important to wait and listen until the end.","furigana":"きつおん|ひと|はな|さいご|ま|き|たいせつ"}],"notes":"吃音 is the matter-of-fact, medical term for stuttering; the diagnosis is also called 吃音症. The everyday verb どもる is now regarded as pejorative and is avoided in respectful speech. Typical are 吃音がある, 吃音に悩む and 吃音のある人.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二言語併用の環境で育った子供は、認知能力が高いという研究もある。","romaji":"Ni-gengo heiyō no kankyō de sodatta kodomo wa, ninchi nōryoku ga takai to iu kenkyū mo aru.","german":"Einigen Studien zufolge haben Kinder, die in einer zweisprachigen Umgebung aufwachsen, höhere kognitive Fähigkeiten.","furigana":"にげんごへいよう|かんきょう|そだ|こども|にんちのうりょく|たか|けんきゅう"},{"japanese":"カナダの一部の地域では、英語とフランス語の二言語併用が一般的だ。","romaji":"Kanada no ichibu no chiiki de wa, Eigo to Furansugo no ni-gengo heiyō ga ippan-teki da.","german":"In manchen Regionen Kanadas ist der gleichzeitige Gebrauch von Englisch und Französisch selbstverständlich.","furigana":"いちぶ|ちいき|えいご|ご|にげんごへいよう|いっぱんてき"},{"japanese":"家では日本語、学校ではドイツ語という二言語併用の生活を送っている。","romaji":"Ie de wa Nihongo, gakkō de wa Doitsugo to iu ni-gengo heiyō no seikatsu o okutte iru.","german":"Ich lebe zweisprachig: zu Hause Japanisch, in der Schule Deutsch.","furigana":"いえ|にほんご|がっこう|ご|にげんごへいよう|せいかつ|おく"}],"notes":"二言語併用 ist ein Fachbegriff für Bilingualismus, also den gleichzeitigen Gebrauch zweier Sprachen durch eine Person oder eine Gemeinschaft (併用 „gleichzeitige Verwendung“). Er erscheint in Linguistik, Bildungs- und Sprachpolitik; im Alltag sagt man fast immer バイリンガル (バイリンガルの子ども). Bei mehr als zwei Sprachen spricht man von 多言語併用 oder 多言語使用.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"多民族国家では言語政策が重要な課題となる。","romaji":"Ta-minzoku kokka de wa gengo seisaku ga jūyō na kadai to naru.","german":"In multi-ethnic countries, language policy becomes an important issue.","furigana":"たみんぞくこっか|げんごせいさく|じゅうよう|かだい"},{"japanese":"明治政府の言語政策によって、標準語が全国に広められた。","romaji":"Meiji seifu no gengo seisaku ni yotte, hyōjungo ga zenkoku ni hiromerareta.","german":"Through the Meiji government's language policy, the standard language was spread throughout the country.","furigana":"めいじせいふ|げんごせいさく|ひょうじゅんご|ぜんこく|ひろ"},{"japanese":"シンガポールは、英語を共通語とする言語政策をとっている。","romaji":"Shingapōru wa, Eigo o kyōtsūgo to suru gengo seisaku o totte iru.","german":"Singapore pursues a language policy that makes English the common language.","furigana":"えいご|きょうつうご|げんごせいさく"}],"notes":"言語政策 is the language policy of a state or organization: determining official languages (公用語), language teaching, and the promotion or suppression of minority languages. Typical are 言語政策をとる \"to pursue a language policy\" and 〜の言語政策. For Japan's policy regarding its own language (kanji lists, writing rules), one traditionally says 国語政策.","levelNote":"Compound: 言語 (N3) + 政策 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"少数民族の言語権を保障することが求められる。","romaji":"Shōsū minzoku no gengoken o hoshō suru koto ga motomerareru.","german":"Guaranteeing the language rights of ethnic minorities is called for.","furigana":"しょうすうみんぞく|げんごけん|ほしょう|もと"},{"japanese":"母語で教育を受けることは、基本的な言語権の一つだと考えられている。","romaji":"Bogo de kyōiku o ukeru koto wa, kihon-teki na gengoken no hitotsu da to kangaerarete iru.","german":"Receiving education in one's mother tongue is considered one of the basic language rights.","furigana":"ぼご|きょういく|う|きほんてき|げんごけん|ひと|かんが"},{"japanese":"アイヌ語の復興は、言語権の観点からも注目されている。","romaji":"Ainugo no fukkō wa, gengoken no kanten kara mo chūmoku sarete iru.","german":"The revival of the Ainu language is also drawing attention from the standpoint of language rights.","furigana":"ご|ふっこう|げんごけん|かんてん|ちゅうもく"}],"notes":"言語権 are the linguistic rights of a person or a group, such as the right to speak one's own language, to be taught in it, or to use it in court and before authorities. The term appears above all in discussions about minorities and indigenous languages; typical are 言語権を保障する \"to guarantee language rights\" and 言語権の侵害. It is a technical term from law and language policy.","levelNote":"Compound: 言語 (N3) + 権 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"母語の言語習得は生後数年間に急速に進む。","romaji":"Bogo no gengo shūtoku wa seigo sūnenkan ni kyūsoku ni susumu.","german":"Acquisition of the mother tongue progresses rapidly during the first few years of life.","furigana":"ぼご|げんごしゅうとく|せいごすうねんかん|きゅうそく|すす"},{"japanese":"大学院で第二言語習得の研究をしている。","romaji":"Daigakuin de dai-ni gengo shūtoku no kenkyū o shite iru.","german":"I am researching second language acquisition in graduate school.","furigana":"だいがくいん|だいにげんごしゅうとく|けんきゅう"},{"japanese":"子どもの言語習得には、周りの大人との会話が欠かせない。","romaji":"Kodomo no gengo shūtoku ni wa, mawari no otona to no kaiwa ga kakasenai.","german":"Conversation with the adults around them is indispensable for children's language acquisition.","furigana":"こ|げんごしゅうとく|まわ|おとな|かいわ|か"}],"notes":"言語習得 is the technical term for \"language acquisition\", especially in linguistics, psychology and pedagogy. One distinguishes 母語の言語習得 (first-language acquisition) and 第二言語習得 (second-language acquisition, often as a research field). 習得 emphasizes making something one's own until one has mastered it; in everyday life one rather says 言葉を覚える or 語学を勉強する. As a verb: 言語を習得する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"語彙力を高めるには多読が効果的だ。","romaji":"Goi-ryoku o takameru ni wa tadoku ga kōka-teki da.","german":"Extensive reading is effective for increasing vocabulary.","furigana":"ごいりょく|たか|たどく|こうかてき"},{"japanese":"本をたくさん読む人は、やはり語彙力がある。","romaji":"Hon o takusan yomu hito wa, yahari goi-ryoku ga aru.","german":"People who read a lot of books really do have a large vocabulary.","furigana":"ほん|よ|ひと|ごいりょく"},{"japanese":"ライブが最高すぎて、語彙力がなくなった。","romaji":"Raibu ga saikō sugite, goi-ryoku ga nakunatta.","german":"The live show was so amazing that I lost all my words.","furigana":"さいこう|ごいりょく"}],"notes":"語彙力 is the extent and command of one's own vocabulary, i.e. how many words one knows and can use aptly. Typical are 語彙力がある／ない, 語彙力を高める／鍛える and 語彙力が豊富. Colloquially and jokingly, one says 語彙力がなくなる when one is lost for words out of enthusiasm. 語彙 alone is the vocabulary itself, 語彙力 the ability.","levelNote":"Compound: 語彙 (N1) + 力 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"読解力の向上は全ての学習の基盤となる。","romaji":"Dokkai-ryoku no kōjō wa subete no gakushū no kiban to naru.","german":"Improving reading comprehension forms the foundation of all learning.","furigana":"どっかいりょく|こうじょう|すべ|がくしゅう|きばん"},{"japanese":"国際調査で、日本の生徒の読解力の低下が話題になった。","romaji":"Kokusai chōsa de, Nihon no seito no dokkai-ryoku no teika ga wadai ni natta.","german":"An international survey made the decline in Japanese students' reading comprehension a topic of discussion.","furigana":"こくさいちょうさ|にほん|せいと|どっかいりょく|ていか|わだい"},{"japanese":"日本語能力試験では、読解力と聴解力の両方が問われる。","romaji":"Nihongo nōryoku shiken de wa, dokkai-ryoku to chōkai-ryoku no ryōhō ga towareru.","german":"The Japanese-Language Proficiency Test tests both reading and listening comprehension.","furigana":"にほんごのうりょくしけん|どっかいりょく|ちょうかいりょく|りょうほう|と"}],"notes":"読解力 is the ability to read texts and understand them correctly, i.e. reading comprehension or reading literacy. Typical are 読解力がある, 読解力を身につける／伸ばす and 読解力の低下, for instance in discussions about PISA results. Together with 聴解力 \"listening comprehension\" and 作文力 \"writing ability\" it forms a series of competence terms; 読解 alone is reading and understanding, or the reading task in an exam.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"聴解力を鍛えるためにポッドキャストを聞いている。","romaji":"Chōkai-ryoku o kitaeru tame ni poddokyasuto o kiite iru.","german":"I listen to podcasts to train my listening comprehension.","furigana":"ちょうかいりょく|きた|き"},{"japanese":"聴解力が足りなくて、試験の点数が伸びなかった。","romaji":"Chōkai-ryoku ga tarinakute, shiken no tensū ga nobinakatta.","german":"My listening comprehension was lacking, so my exam score did not go up.","furigana":"ちょうかいりょく|た|しけん|てんすう|の"},{"japanese":"毎日少しでも日本語を聞くことが、聴解力を伸ばす近道だ。","romaji":"Mainichi sukoshi demo Nihongo o kiku koto ga, chōkai-ryoku o nobasu chikamichi da.","german":"Listening to even a little Japanese every day is the shortcut to improving listening comprehension.","furigana":"まいにちすこ|にほんご|き|ちょうかいりょく|の|ちかみち"}],"notes":"聴解力 is listening comprehension, especially in foreign-language learning and exams; the exam section is called 聴解 in the JLPT. Typical are 聴解力を鍛える／伸ばす and 聴解力が足りない. In everyday life one often says the more casual リスニング力 or 聞き取る力. The counterpart for reading is 読解力.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"作文力は継続的な練習で向上する。","romaji":"Sakubun-ryoku wa keizoku-teki na renshū de kōjō suru.","german":"Writing ability improves with continuous practice.","furigana":"さくぶんりょく|けいぞくてき|れんしゅう|こうじょう"},{"japanese":"日記を毎日書いていたら、少しずつ作文力がついてきた。","romaji":"Nikki o mainichi kaite itara, sukoshi zutsu sakubun-ryoku ga tsuite kita.","german":"Since I started writing a diary every day, my writing has gradually improved.","furigana":"にっき|まいにちか|すこ|さくぶんりょく"},{"japanese":"この塾では、小学生の作文力を伸ばす授業をしている。","romaji":"Kono juku de wa, shōgakusei no sakubun-ryoku o nobasu jugyō o shite iru.","german":"This cram school offers classes that develop elementary school students' writing skills.","furigana":"じゅく|しょうがくせい|さくぶんりょく|の|じゅぎょう"}],"notes":"作文力 is the ability to write compositions or texts; the term comes from school and language teaching, where 作文 is the \"composition\". For adults and in working life one rather speaks of 文章力 \"writing skill, style\" (文章力がある). Typical are 作文力をつける, 作文力を伸ばす; together with 読解力 and 聴解力 it denotes the basic skills in language teaching.","levelNote":"Compound: 作文 (N5) + 力 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"会話力を伸ばすにはネイティブとの交流が不可欠だ。","romaji":"Kaiwa-ryoku o nobasu ni wa neitibu to no kōryū ga fukaketsu da.","german":"Interacting with native speakers is indispensable for improving conversational ability.","furigana":"かいわりょく|の|こうりゅう|ふかけつ"},{"japanese":"一年間留学して、会話力がかなり上がった。","romaji":"Ichinenkan ryūgaku shite, kaiwa-ryoku ga kanari agatta.","german":"After a year studying abroad, my speaking ability improved considerably.","furigana":"いちねんかんりゅうがく|かいわりょく|あ"},{"japanese":"この会社の面接では、英語の会話力も評価される。","romaji":"Kono kaisha no mensetsu de wa, eigo no kaiwa-ryoku mo hyōka sareru.","german":"In this company's interviews, English speaking ability is also evaluated.","furigana":"かいしゃ|めんせつ|えいご|かいわりょく|ひょうか"}],"notes":"会話力 is the ability to converse orally in a language, formed with the suffix 〜力 \"ability\" like 読解力 \"reading comprehension\" or 聴解力 \"listening comprehension\". Typical combinations are 会話力を伸ばす/高める/つける and 会話力が上がる; for foreign languages one often says 英会話力. The more general 語学力 means foreign-language proficiency as a whole, including reading and writing. Neutral, common in education, job applications and language-school advertising.","levelNote":"Compound: 会話 (N4) + 力 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本語には「さん」「様」など多様な敬称がある。","romaji":"Nihongo ni wa \"san\" \"sama\" nado tayō na keishō ga aru.","german":"Japanese has a variety of honorific titles such as 「さん」 and 「様」.","furigana":"にほんご|さま|たよう|けいしょう"},{"japanese":"自分の名前に「さん」などの敬称を付けてはいけない。","romaji":"Jibun no namae ni \"san\" nado no keishō o tsukete wa ikenai.","german":"You must not attach an honorific such as 「さん」 to your own name.","furigana":"じぶん|なまえ|けいしょう|つ"},{"japanese":"出席者名簿の最後に「敬称略」と書いておいた。","romaji":"Shussekisha meibo no saigo ni \"keishō-ryaku\" to kaite oita.","german":"I wrote 「敬称略」 at the end of the attendee list.","furigana":"しゅっせきしゃめいぼ|さいご|けいしょうりゃく|か"}],"notes":"敬称 are honorific forms of address attached to names or designations: さん, 様, 殿, 先生, and 御中 for companies. One says 敬称を付ける; one never adds a 敬称 to one's own name. In lists of participants one often finds 敬称略 \"honorifics omitted\". Not to be confused with 尊敬語, the respectful verb forms of 敬語.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"卑語の使用は教養のなさを示すとされる。","romaji":"Higo no shiyō wa kyōyō no nasa o shimesu to sareru.","german":"Using vulgar language is said to show a lack of education.","furigana":"ひご|しよう|きょうよう|しめ"},{"japanese":"テレビの放送では卑語は使えない。","romaji":"Terebi no hōsō de wa higo wa tsukaenai.","german":"Vulgar words cannot be used on television broadcasts.","furigana":"ほうそう|ひご|つか"},{"japanese":"卑語と俗語の境界は意外にあいまいだ。","romaji":"Higo to zokugo no kyōkai wa igai ni aimai da.","german":"The boundary between vulgar words and slang is surprisingly vague.","furigana":"ひご|ぞくご|きょうかい|いがい"}],"notes":"卑語 is a linguistic or written-language term for vulgar, coarse or obscene words. It stands beside 俗語 \"slang, casual colloquial language\" and 隠語 \"secret language, jargon\"; 卑語 emphasizes the low and offensive. In everyday life one rather says 汚い言葉 or 下品な言葉. Typical are 卑語を使う and 卑語を避ける.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「瑞穂の国」は日本の美称である。","romaji":"\"Mizuho no kuni\" wa Nihon no bishō de aru.","german":"「瑞穂の国」 is a poetic name for Japan.","furigana":"みずほ|くに|にほん|びしょう"},{"japanese":"「花の都」はパリの美称として知られている。","romaji":"\"Hana no miyako\" wa Pari no bishō to shite shirarete iru.","german":"「花の都」 is known as a poetic name for Paris.","furigana":"はな|みやこ|びしょう|し"},{"japanese":"古い和歌には、土地や山の美称がよく出てくる。","romaji":"Furui waka ni wa, tochi ya yama no bishō ga yoku dete kuru.","german":"In old waka poems, poetic names for places and mountains often appear.","furigana":"ふる|わか|とち|やま|びしょう|で"}],"notes":"美称 is an embellishing, praising or poetic name for something, such as 瑞穂の国 for Japan or 花の都 for Paris. It mostly appears in the pattern AはBの美称だ. For euphemistic paraphrases of unpleasant things (亡くなる instead of 死ぬ), by contrast, one rather says 婉曲表現; for the polite お in お茶 there is the term 美化語. The word is literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本語では婉曲表現が多用される。","romaji":"Nihongo de wa enkyoku hyōgen ga tayō sareru.","german":"Euphemistic expressions are used often in Japanese.","furigana":"にほんご|えんきょくひょうげん|たよう"},{"japanese":"「考えておきます」は、断るときの婉曲表現として使われることが多い。","romaji":"\"Kangaete okimasu\" wa, kotowaru toki no enkyoku hyōgen to shite tsukawareru koto ga ōi.","german":"「考えておきます」 is often used as a euphemism when declining.","furigana":"かんが|ことわ|えんきょくひょうげん|つか|おお"},{"japanese":"「亡くなる」は「死ぬ」の婉曲表現だ。","romaji":"\"Nakunaru\" wa \"shinu\" no enkyoku hyōgen da.","german":"「亡くなる」 is a euphemism for 「死ぬ」.","furigana":"な|し|えんきょくひょうげん"}],"notes":"婉曲表現 is an expression that says something unpleasant or delicate indirectly and in a softened way, e.g. 亡くなる instead of 死ぬ or 考えておきます as a polite refusal. The element 婉曲 is also used alone as a な-adjective: 婉曲な言い方, 婉曲に断る. Colloquially, 遠回しな言い方 \"beating around the bush\" corresponds to it. Typical: 婉曲表現を使う/用いる; a slightly elevated term, often used in linguistics.","levelNote":"Compound: 婉曲 (N1) + 表現 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本語は擬音語が非常に豊富な言語だ。","romaji":"Nihongo wa giongo ga hijō ni hōfu na gengo da.","german":"Japanese is a language extremely rich in onomatopoeia.","furigana":"にほんご|ぎおんご|ひじょう|ほうふ|げんご"},{"japanese":"「ガチャン」や「ドンドン」は擬音語の例だ。","romaji":"\"Gachan\" ya \"dondon\" wa giongo no rei da.","german":"「ガチャン」 and 「ドンドン」 are examples of onomatopoeia.","furigana":"ぎおんご|れい"},{"japanese":"漫画では擬音語がカタカナで大きく描かれている。","romaji":"Manga de wa giongo ga katakana de ōkiku egakarete iru.","german":"In manga, sound-effect words are drawn large in katakana.","furigana":"まんが|ぎおんご|おお|えが"}],"notes":"擬音語 are sound-imitating words for actual noises such as ドンドン \"boom-boom\" or ガチャン \"clang\"; animal calls and human voices are more precisely called 擬声語. To be distinguished from them are 擬態語, which depict states or movements without sound (きらきら, じろじろ). As an umbrella term for all of them, オノマトペ is common today. 擬音語 are mostly written in katakana, especially in manga.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「きらきら」「ふわふわ」は擬態語の例だ。","romaji":"\"Kirakira\" \"fuwafuwa\" wa gitaigo no rei da.","german":"「きらきら」 and 「ふわふわ」 are examples of mimetic words.","furigana":"ぎたいご|れい"},{"japanese":"「にこにこ笑う」の「にこにこ」は擬態語で、実際の音は表していない。","romaji":"\"Nikoniko warau\" no \"nikoniko\" wa gitaigo de, jissai no oto wa arawashite inai.","german":"「にこにこ」 in 「にこにこ笑う」 is a mimetic word and does not represent an actual sound.","furigana":"わら|ぎたいご|じっさい|おと|あらわ"},{"japanese":"日本語は擬態語が豊富で、外国人にはなかなか難しい。","romaji":"Nihongo wa gitaigo ga hōfu de, gaikokujin ni wa nakanaka muzukashii.","german":"Japanese is rich in mimetic words, which is quite difficult for foreigners.","furigana":"にほんご|ぎたいご|ほうふ|がいこくじん|むずか"}],"notes":"擬態語 are sound-symbolic words for states, movements or impressions that make no noise at all: きらきら, ふわふわ, じろじろ, にこにこ. The counterpart is 擬音語 or 擬声語 for real sounds (ワンワン, ドンドン); both together are called オノマトペ. Feeling words such as いらいら are sometimes set apart as 擬情語. In a sentence, 擬態語 mostly stand adverbially, often with と or as a 〜する verb.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"詩の翻訳では意訳が必要になることが多い。","romaji":"Shi no hon'yaku de wa iyaku ga hitsuyō ni naru koto ga ōi.","german":"In translating poetry, a free translation is often necessary.","furigana":"し|ほんやく|いやく|ひつよう|おお"},{"japanese":"このことわざは直訳しても伝わらないので、意訳しました。","romaji":"Kono kotowaza wa chokuyaku shite mo tsutawaranai node, iyaku shimashita.","german":"This proverb doesn't come across when translated literally, so I translated it freely.","furigana":"ちょくやく|つた|いやく"},{"japanese":"映画の字幕は文字数に制限があるため、意訳が多い。","romaji":"Eiga no jimaku wa mojisū ni seigen ga aru tame, iyaku ga ōi.","german":"Movie subtitles have a limit on the number of characters, so they are often freely translated.","furigana":"えいが|じまく|もじすう|せいげん|いやく|おお"}],"notes":"意訳 is the free translation that renders the content rather than the word order; as a transitive する-verb: 文を意訳する. The opposite terms are 直訳 \"literal translation\" and the even stricter 逐語訳 \"word-for-word translation\". Typical in discussions about literature, subtitles and song lyrics; translating too freely is criticized with 意訳しすぎ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"直訳すると不自然な文になることがある。","romaji":"Chokuyaku suru to fushizen na bun ni naru koto ga aru.","german":"A literal translation can result in unnatural sentences.","furigana":"ちょくやく|ふしぜん|ぶん"},{"japanese":"「猫の手も借りたい」を英語に直訳しても、意味は通じない。","romaji":"\"Neko no te mo karitai\" o eigo ni chokuyaku shite mo, imi wa tsūjinai.","german":"Even if you translate 「猫の手も借りたい」 literally into English, the meaning doesn't come across.","furigana":"ねこ|て|か|えいご|ちょくやく|いみ|つう"},{"japanese":"「いただきます」は直訳が難しい言葉だ。","romaji":"\"Itadakimasu\" wa chokuyaku ga muzukashii kotoba da.","german":"「いただきます」 is a word that is difficult to translate literally.","furigana":"ちょくやく|むずか|ことば"}],"notes":"直訳 is the literal translation that sticks closely to the words and sentence structure of the original; as a transitive する-verb: 英文を直訳する. Very common is 直訳すると \"translated literally, this means ...\". The opposite is 意訳 \"free translation\"; 逐語訳 is even stricter, word for word. Clumsy translationese is called 直訳調.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"誤訳によって外交問題に発展した事例もある。","romaji":"Goyaku ni yotte gaikō mondai ni hatten shita jirei mo aru.","german":"There are cases where a mistranslation developed into a diplomatic problem.","furigana":"ごやく|がいこうもんだい|はってん|じれい"},{"japanese":"翻訳書に誤訳が見つかり、出版社が訂正した。","romaji":"Hon'yakusho ni goyaku ga mitsukari, shuppansha ga teisei shita.","german":"A mistranslation was found in the translated book, and the publisher corrected it.","furigana":"ほんやくしょ|ごやく|み|しゅっぱんしゃ|ていせい"},{"japanese":"読者から誤訳を指摘されて、恥ずかしかった。","romaji":"Dokusha kara goyaku o shiteki sarete, hazukashikatta.","german":"I was embarrassed when a reader pointed out a mistranslation.","furigana":"どくしゃ|ごやく|してき|は"}],"notes":"誤訳 is a wrong translation or translation error; as a transitive する-verb it means \"to translate wrongly\": 一文を誤訳する. Typical combinations are 誤訳がある/見つかる, 誤訳を指摘する and 誤訳を訂正する. Colloquially one also says 翻訳ミス. Not to be confused with 誤字 \"typo, wrong character\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"逐語訳では原文のニュアンスが失われがちだ。","romaji":"Chikugoyaku de wa genbun no nyuansu ga ushinaware-gachi da.","german":"Nuances of the original tend to be lost in a word-for-word translation.","furigana":"ちくごやく|げんぶん|うしな"},{"japanese":"古典の授業では、まず逐語訳をしてから全体の意味を考える。","romaji":"Koten no jugyō de wa, mazu chikugoyaku o shite kara zentai no imi o kangaeru.","german":"In classical-literature class, you first translate word for word and then think about the meaning as a whole.","furigana":"こてん|じゅぎょう|ちくごやく|ぜんたい|いみ|かんが"},{"japanese":"契約書は誤解を避けるため、逐語訳に近い形で訳された。","romaji":"Keiyakusho wa gokai o sakeru tame, chikugoyaku ni chikai katachi de yakusareta.","german":"To avoid misunderstandings, the contract was translated in a form close to word for word.","furigana":"けいやくしょ|ごかい|さ|ちくごやく|ちか|かたち|やく"}],"notes":"逐語訳 is the word-for-word translation in which each word is rendered in sequence (逐 \"in sequence\", 語 \"word\"). It is narrower and more technical than 直訳, which generally means \"literal translation\"; the opposite term is 意訳 \"free translation\". As an adverb: 逐語的に訳す. Typical in foreign-language and classical-literature teaching as well as with legal texts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"言語能力は環境と教育によって大きく左右される。","romaji":"Gengo nōryoku wa kankyō to kyōiku ni yotte ōkiku sayū sareru.","german":"Linguistic ability is greatly influenced by environment and education.","furigana":"げんごのうりょく|かんきょう|きょういく|おお|さゆう"},{"japanese":"子どもの言語能力は三歳ごろに急速に発達する。","romaji":"Kodomo no gengo nōryoku wa sansai goro ni kyūsoku ni hattatsu suru.","german":"Children's language ability develops rapidly around the age of three.","furigana":"こ|げんごのうりょく|さんさい|きゅうそく|はったつ"},{"japanese":"脳卒中のあと、祖父は一時的に言語能力が低下した。","romaji":"Nōsotchū no ato, sofu wa ichijiteki ni gengo nōryoku ga teika shita.","german":"After the stroke, my grandfather's language ability declined temporarily.","furigana":"のうそっちゅう|そふ|いちじてき|げんごのうりょく|ていか"}],"notes":"言語能力 denotes a person's linguistic ability in general, for instance in psychology, pedagogy and medicine: 言語能力が高い/低い, 言語能力が発達する/低下する. In linguistics it is also the technical term for Chomsky's \"linguistic competence\". For foreign-language skills one rather says 語学力 in everyday life, and 会話力 for speaking.","levelNote":"Compound: 言語 (N3) + 能力 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"言語接触によって借用語が生まれる。","romaji":"Gengo sesshoku ni yotte shakuyōgo ga umareru.","german":"Loanwords arise through language contact.","furigana":"げんごせっしょく|しゃくようご|う"},{"japanese":"言語接触の結果、ピジンやクレオールが生まれることがある。","romaji":"Gengo sesshoku no kekka, pijin ya kureōru ga umareru koto ga aru.","german":"As a result of language contact, pidgins and creoles can arise.","furigana":"げんごせっしょく|けっか|う"},{"japanese":"この地域の方言は、移民の言葉との言語接触で大きく変化した。","romaji":"Kono chiiki no hōgen wa, imin no kotoba to no gengo sesshoku de ōkiku henka shita.","german":"The dialect of this region changed greatly through language contact with the speech of immigrants.","furigana":"ちいき|ほうげん|いみん|ことば|げんごせっしょく|おお|へんか"}],"notes":"言語接触 is a technical term of linguistics for contact between languages or their speakers, which can lead to borrowings (借用語), mixed languages such as ピジン and クレオール, or to language shift. Typical frames are 〜との言語接触 and 言語接触によって/の結果. Hardly used in everyday life; there one rather speaks of 外国語の影響.","levelNote":"Compound: 言語 (N3) + 接触 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「パン」はポルトガル語からの借用語だ。","romaji":"\"Pan\" wa Porutogaru-go kara no shakuyōgo da.","german":"「パン」 is a loanword from Portuguese.","furigana":"ご|しゃくようご"},{"japanese":"日本語の漢語も、もとは中国語からの借用語だ。","romaji":"Nihongo no kango mo, moto wa Chūgokugo kara no shakuyōgo da.","german":"Japanese kango too are originally loanwords from Chinese.","furigana":"にほんご|かんご|ちゅうごくご|しゃくようご"},{"japanese":"英語には「tsunami」や「sushi」など、日本語からの借用語がある。","romaji":"Eigo ni wa \"tsunami\" ya \"sushi\" nado, Nihongo kara no shakuyōgo ga aru.","german":"English has loanwords from Japanese, such as 「tsunami」 and 「sushi」.","furigana":"えいご|にほんご|しゃくようご"}],"notes":"借用語 is the linguistic umbrella term for \"loanword\": any word taken over from another language, including the 漢語 from Chinese. 外来語, by contrast, in ordinary usage means above all newer words from Western languages, mostly written in katakana. Typical: 〜語からの借用語. In everyday life one hears almost only 外来語; 借用語 sounds technical.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本語のローマ字表記は翻字の一形態だ。","romaji":"Nihongo no rōmaji hyōki wa honji no ichi keitai da.","german":"Romanization of Japanese is a form of transliteration.","furigana":"にほんご|じひょうき|ほんじ|いちけいたい"},{"japanese":"古文書のくずし字を、現代の文字に翻字する。","romaji":"Komonjo no kuzushiji o, gendai no moji ni honji suru.","german":"I transliterate the cursive kuzushiji of old documents into modern characters.","furigana":"こもんじょ|じ|げんだい|もじ|ほんじ"},{"japanese":"ロシア語の人名をラテン文字に翻字すると、表記が何通りもある。","romaji":"Roshiago no jinmei o raten moji ni honji suru to, hyōki ga nantōri mo aru.","german":"If you transliterate Russian personal names into the Latin alphabet, there are several possible spellings.","furigana":"ご|じんめい|もじ|ほんじ|ひょうき|なんとお"}],"notes":"翻字 is transliteration, i.e. the character-faithful transfer of a text from one script to another; as a transitive する-verb: ラテン文字に翻字する. To be distinguished from it are 翻訳 \"translation\" (content into another language) and 音訳 \"transcription by sound\". In Japanese philology, 翻字 also denotes the transfer of cursive script (くずし字) of old manuscripts into present-day characters. Technical.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"文字コードの違いで文字化けが起きた。","romaji":"Moji kōdo no chigai de mojibake ga okita.","german":"Garbled text occurred because of a difference in character encoding.","furigana":"もじ|ちが|もじば|お"},{"japanese":"添付ファイルを開いたら、文字化けしていて読めなかった。","romaji":"Tenpu fairu o hiraitara, mojibake shite ite yomenakatta.","german":"When I opened the attachment, the text was garbled and I couldn't read it.","furigana":"てんぷ|ひら|もじば|よ"},{"japanese":"ブラウザの設定を変えたら、文字化けが直った。","romaji":"Burauza no settei o kaetara, mojibake ga naotta.","german":"After I changed the browser settings, the garbled text was fixed.","furigana":"せってい|か|もじば|なお"}],"notes":"文字化け denotes unreadable character salad caused by a wrong character encoding; the word has even been borrowed into English. As an intransitive する-verb: メールが文字化けする, 文字化けしている. Typical are 文字化けが起きる and 文字化けを直す. Neutral and everyday in an IT context; almost always written with kanji and け in hiragana.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"事実を客観的に叙述することが求められる。","romaji":"Jijitsu o kyakkan-teki ni jojutsu suru koto ga motomerareru.","german":"Facts are required to be described objectively.","furigana":"じじつ|きゃっかんてき|じょじゅつ|もと"},{"japanese":"筆者は自らの戦争体験を淡々と叙述している。","romaji":"Hissha wa mizukara no sensō taiken o tantan to jojutsu shite iru.","german":"The author narrates his own war experiences in a matter-of-fact way.","furigana":"ひっしゃ|みずか|せんそうたいけん|たんたん|じょじゅつ"},{"japanese":"この形容詞は叙述用法でしか使えない。","romaji":"Kono keiyōshi wa jojutsu yōhō de shika tsukaenai.","german":"This adjective can only be used predicatively.","furigana":"けいようし|じょじゅつようほう|つか"}],"notes":"叙述する means to present and narrate facts or events in order in language; transitive with を: 事実を叙述する. It is literary and sounds more matter-of-fact than 描写 \"vivid depiction, portrayal\"; 記述 emphasizes more the setting down in writing. In grammar, 叙述 means \"predication\", e.g. 叙述用法 \"predicative use\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"心理描写が巧みな作家として知られている。","romaji":"Shinri byōsha ga takumi na sakka toshite shirarete iru.","german":"He is known as a writer skilled at psychological portrayal.","furigana":"しんりびょうしゃ|たく|さっか|し"},{"japanese":"この映画は暴力描写が多いので、子どもには向かない。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa bōryoku byōsha ga ōi node, kodomo ni wa mukanai.","german":"This movie has a lot of depictions of violence and is not suitable for children.","furigana":"えいが|ぼうりょくびょうしゃ|おお|こ|む"},{"japanese":"作者は雨の夜の町を細かく描写している。","romaji":"Sakusha wa ame no yoru no machi o komakaku byōsha shite iru.","german":"The author describes the town on a rainy night in detail.","furigana":"さくしゃ|あめ|よる|まち|こま|びょうしゃ"}],"notes":"描写 is the vivid depiction of scenes, people or feelings in literature, film or art; as a transitive する-verb: 町の様子を描写する. Common compounds are 心理描写, 情景描写, 人物描写 and 暴力描写. In contrast to the matter-of-fact 叙述, 描写 emphasizes the pictorial; 描写が細かい/リアルだ praises the level of detail. Neutral, very common in reviews and teaching.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"巧みに張られた伏線が物語の結末で回収される。","romaji":"Takumi ni harareta fukusen ga monogatari no ketsumatsu de kaishū sareru.","german":"Skillfully laid foreshadowing is paid off at the end of the story.","furigana":"たく|は|ふくせん|ものがたり|けつまつ|かいしゅう"},{"japanese":"最終回ですべての伏線がきれいに回収された。","romaji":"Saishūkai de subete no fukusen ga kirei ni kaishū sareta.","german":"In the final episode, all the foreshadowing was neatly paid off.","furigana":"さいしゅうかい|ふくせん|かいしゅう"},{"japanese":"彼は「来週忙しくなるかも」と言って、飲み会を断るための伏線を張っていた。","romaji":"Kare wa \"raishū isogashiku naru kamo\" to itte, nomikai o kotowaru tame no fukusen o hatte ita.","german":"He said \"I might be busy next week,\" laying the groundwork for turning down the drinking party.","furigana":"かれ|らいしゅういそが|い|の|かい|ことわ|ふくせん|は"}],"notes":"伏線 is a hint hidden early in a story that later turns out to be significant. The fixed combinations are 伏線を張る \"to lay foreshadowing\" and 伏線を回収する \"to pay it off\". In a figurative sense it also means a precautionary preparation in everyday life, e.g. in order to have an excuse later. Similar is 布石, but it rather denotes a strategic setting of the course.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"予想外の結末に読者は驚かされた。","romaji":"Yosō-gai no ketsumatsu ni dokusha wa odorokasareta.","german":"The unexpected ending surprised the readers.","furigana":"よそうがい|けつまつ|どくしゃ|おどろ"},{"japanese":"長く続いた交渉は、思いがけない結末を迎えた。","romaji":"Nagaku tsuzuita kōshō wa, omoigakenai ketsumatsu o mukaeta.","german":"The long-running negotiations came to an unexpected conclusion.","furigana":"なが|つづ|こうしょう|おも|けつまつ|むか"},{"japanese":"結末は言わないで！まだ読んでいる途中なんだから。","romaji":"Ketsumatsu wa iwanaide! Mada yonde iru tochū nan dakara.","german":"Don't tell me the ending! I'm still in the middle of reading it.","furigana":"けつまつ|い|よ|とちゅう"}],"notes":"結末 is the ending or outcome of a story, but also of a real event such as a case or dispute. Typical are 結末を迎える \"to come to an end\", 意外な結末 and 悲しい結末. In contrast to 結論 \"conclusion of an argument\" and 結果 \"result\", 結末 emphasizes the outcome of a sequence of events. Neutral; also when spoiling: 結末を言わないで.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"起承転結を意識して文章を構成する。","romaji":"Kishōtenketsu o ishiki shite bunshō o kōsei suru.","german":"I structure the text with the ki-shō-ten-ketsu structure in mind.","furigana":"きしょうてんけつ|いしき|ぶんしょう|こうせい"},{"japanese":"四コマ漫画は、起承転結の形で作られていることが多い。","romaji":"Yonkoma manga wa, kishōtenketsu no katachi de tsukurarete iru koto ga ōi.","german":"Four-panel manga are often made in the ki-shō-ten-ketsu form.","furigana":"よん|まんが|きしょうてんけつ|かたち|つく|おお"},{"japanese":"彼の話は起承転結がなくて、何が言いたいのか分からない。","romaji":"Kare no hanashi wa kishōtenketsu ga nakute, nani ga iitai no ka wakaranai.","german":"His stories have no ki-shō-ten-ketsu structure, so I can't tell what he wants to say.","furigana":"かれ|はなし|きしょうてんけつ|なに|い|わ"}],"notes":"起承転結 denotes the four-part structure of introduction (起), development (承), twist (転) and conclusion (結). It originally comes from Chinese four-line poetry; today it serves as a model for essays, stories and yonkoma manga. Typical: 起承転結を意識する, 起承転結がはっきりしている. Colloquially, 起承転結がない criticizes an aimless, unstructured narrative.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この場面は後の展開への布石だ。","romaji":"Kono bamen wa nochi no tenkai e no fuseki da.","german":"This scene is groundwork for later developments.","furigana":"ばめん|のち|てんかい|ふせき"},{"japanese":"海外進出への布石として、現地に事務所を開いた。","romaji":"Kaigai shinshutsu e no fuseki to shite, genchi ni jimusho o hiraita.","german":"As groundwork for expanding overseas, they opened an office locally.","furigana":"かいがいしんしゅつ|ふせき|げんち|じむしょ|ひら"},{"japanese":"囲碁では序盤の布石が勝敗を大きく左右する。","romaji":"Igo de wa joban no fuseki ga shōhai o ōkiku sayū suru.","german":"In go, the opening moves in the early game greatly affect victory or defeat.","furigana":"いご|じょばん|ふせき|しょうはい|おお|さゆう"}],"notes":"布石 comes from the game of go, where it denotes the strategic placement of stones in the opening. In a figurative sense it is a preparatory step with which one sets the course for the future: 〜への布石, 布石を打つ. Unlike 伏線, a hidden hint in a narrative, 布石 emphasizes planned, strategic action. Frequent in business and political reports.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この小説の主題は人間の孤独である。","romaji":"Kono shōsetsu no shudai wa ningen no kodoku de aru.","german":"The theme of this novel is human loneliness.","furigana":"しょうせつ|しゅだい|にんげん|こどく"},{"japanese":"このアニメの主題歌は大ヒットした。","romaji":"Kono anime no shudaika wa dai-hitto shita.","german":"The theme song of this anime was a huge hit.","furigana":"しゅだいか|だい"},{"japanese":"作者がこの作品で伝えたかった主題について論じなさい。","romaji":"Sakusha ga kono sakuhin de tsutaetakatta shudai ni tsuite ronjinasai.","german":"Discuss the theme the author wanted to convey in this work.","furigana":"さくしゃ|さくひん|つた|しゅだい|ろん"}],"notes":"主題 is the central theme or motif of a work, such as a novel, film or piece of music. In everyday life one often says テーマ; 主題 sounds more analytical and is typical in literature classes and criticism. Well-known compound: 主題歌 \"theme song\". Not to be confused with 題名 \"title\" or 話題 \"topic of conversation\"; in grammar, 主題 is the topic of a sentence (with は).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"物語の梗概を簡潔にまとめた。","romaji":"Monogatari no kōgai o kanketsu ni matometa.","german":"I summarized the synopsis of the story concisely.","furigana":"ものがたり|こうがい|かんけつ"},{"japanese":"卒業論文には、二千字程度の梗概を添付すること。","romaji":"Sotsugyō ronbun ni wa, nisen-ji teido no kōgai o tenpu suru koto.","german":"A summary of about two thousand characters must be attached to the graduation thesis.","furigana":"そつぎょうろんぶん|にせんじていど|こうがい|てんぷ"},{"japanese":"書評の冒頭で、まず作品の梗概が紹介されている。","romaji":"Shohyō no bōtō de, mazu sakuhin no kōgai ga shōkai sarete iru.","german":"At the start of the book review, the synopsis of the work is introduced first.","furigana":"しょひょう|ぼうとう|さくひん|こうがい|しょうかい"}],"notes":"梗概 is a brief summary of the content, for example of a novel, play or academic paper. It is literary and formal; in everyday life one says あらすじ, and for essays and reports also 要約 or 概要. Typical are 梗概をまとめる, 論文の梗概 and 梗概を提出する. The word is used almost only in writing.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"紙面の制約上、詳細は割愛する。","romaji":"Shimen no seiyaku-jō, shōsai wa katsuai suru.","german":"Owing to space limitations, the details are omitted.","furigana":"しめん|せいやくじょう|しょうさい|かつあい"},{"japanese":"その事件は翌朝の新聞の紙面を大きく飾った。","romaji":"Sono jiken wa yokuasa no shinbun no shimen o ōkiku kazatta.","german":"The incident was splashed across the next morning's newspaper pages.","furigana":"じけん|よくあさ|しんぶん|しめん|おお|かざ"},{"japanese":"新聞各紙は選挙の特集に多くの紙面を割いた。","romaji":"Shinbun kakushi wa senkyo no tokushū ni ōku no shimen o saita.","german":"The newspapers devoted a lot of space to special coverage of the election.","furigana":"しんぶんかくし|せんきょ|とくしゅう|おお|しめん|"}],"notes":"紙面 denotes the surface of a printed page, above all the available space in a newspaper. Common are 紙面の都合で/紙面の制約上 \"due to space limitations\", 紙面を割く \"to devote space\" and 紙面を飾る \"to make headlines\". For magazines one writes 誌面, for screens 画面. Rather literary and typical of the press and publishing.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"言文一致運動は明治時代に始まった。","romaji":"Genbun itchi undō wa Meiji jidai ni hajimatta.","german":"The genbun itchi movement began in the Meiji period.","furigana":"げんぶんいっちうんどう|めいじじだい|はじ"},{"japanese":"二葉亭四迷の『浮雲』は、言文一致体で書かれた初期の小説として知られる。","romaji":"Futabatei Shimei no \"Ukigumo\" wa, genbun itchi-tai de kakareta shoki no shōsetsu to shite shirareru.","german":"Futabatei Shimei's 『浮雲』 is known as an early novel written in the genbun itchi style.","furigana":"ふたばていしめい|うきぐも|げんぶんいっちたい|か|しょき|しょうせつ|し"},{"japanese":"言文一致が進んだおかげで、私たちは話すように書くことができる。","romaji":"Genbun itchi ga susunda okage de, watashitachi wa hanasu yō ni kaku koto ga dekiru.","german":"Thanks to the progress of genbun itchi, we can write the way we speak.","furigana":"げんぶんいっち|すす|わたし|はな|か"}],"notes":"言文一致 denotes the approximation of written language to spoken language, especially the reform movement (言文一致運動) of the Meiji period that replaced the classical 文語体 with the modern 口語体. Literary pioneers were 二葉亭四迷 with 浮雲 and 山田美妙. The style is called 言文一致体. A technical term from the history of language and literature.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"文語体で書かれた文章は格調高い印象を与える。","romaji":"Bungo-tai de kakareta bunshō wa kakuchō takai inshō o ataeru.","german":"Texts written in the classical literary style give an impression of dignity.","furigana":"ぶんごたい|か|ぶんしょう|かくちょうたか|いんしょう|あた"},{"japanese":"明治憲法は文語体で書かれているので、今の人には読みにくい。","romaji":"Meiji kenpō wa bungo-tai de kakarete iru node, ima no hito ni wa yominikui.","german":"The Meiji Constitution is written in the classical literary style, so it is hard for people today to read.","furigana":"めいじけんぽう|ぶんごたい|か|いま|ひと|よ"},{"japanese":"文語体の「なり」は、現代語の「である」に当たる。","romaji":"Bungo-tai no \"nari\" wa, gendaigo no \"de aru\" ni ataru.","german":"The classical 「なり」 corresponds to the modern 「である」.","furigana":"ぶんごたい|げんだいご|あ"}],"notes":"文語体 is the writing style that follows the classical written language (文語) with its own grammar, recognizable by forms such as 〜なり, 〜けり or 〜べし. The opposite term is 口語体, which is based on modern spoken language. Until the 20th century, laws and official texts were written in 文語体; today it is encountered in classics, poems and set formulas. Technical term, neutral.","levelNote":"Compound: 文語 (N1) + 体 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"現代の小説は、ほとんどが口語体で書かれている。","romaji":"Gendai no shōsetsu wa, hotondo ga kōgo-tai de kakarete iru.","german":"Modern novels are almost all written in the colloquial style.","furigana":"げんだい|しょうせつ|こうごたい|か"},{"japanese":"刑法は1995年に文語体から口語体に改められた。","romaji":"Keihō wa sen kyūhyaku kyūjūgo-nen ni bungo-tai kara kōgo-tai ni aratamerareta.","german":"The Penal Code was rewritten from the classical to the colloquial style in 1995.","furigana":"けいほう|ねん|ぶんごたい|こうごたい|あらた"},{"japanese":"口語体とはいえ、話し言葉をそのまま書くわけではない。","romaji":"Kōgo-tai to wa ie, hanashikotoba o sono mama kaku wake de wa nai.","german":"Even in the colloquial style, you don't simply write down speech as it is.","furigana":"こうごたい|はな|ことば|か"}],"notes":"口語体 is the writing style based on modern spoken language, i.e. today's normal written Japanese with です/ます or だ/である. The opposite term is the classical 文語体; 口語体 therefore does not mean \"casual\" or \"slang\". For truly colloquial speech one says 話し言葉 or くだけた言い方. Technical term from grammar and the history of language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"明治以前の公文書は候文で書かれていた。","romaji":"Meiji izen no kōbunsho wa sōrōbun de kakarete ita.","german":"Official documents before the Meiji period were written in sōrō-bun.","furigana":"めいじいぜん|こうぶんしょ|そうろうぶん|か"},{"japanese":"戦前までは、手紙を候文で書く人も少なくなかった。","romaji":"Senzen made wa, tegami o sōrōbun de kaku hito mo sukunaku nakatta.","german":"Until before the war, not a few people wrote their letters in sōrō-bun.","furigana":"せんぜん|てがみ|そうろうぶん|か|ひと|すく"},{"japanese":"「御座候」は候文によく出てくる表現だ。","romaji":"\"Gozasōrō\" wa sōrōbun ni yoku dete kuru hyōgen da.","german":"「御座候」 is an expression that often appears in sōrō-bun.","furigana":"ござそうろう|そうろうぶん|で|ひょうげん"}],"notes":"候文 is the historical formal style of letters and documents in which the auxiliary verb 候 (そうろう) takes the role of ある or ます, as in 〜に候 or 御座候. It shaped letters and official documents from the Kamakura period to the early Shōwa period. Today it is encountered only in historical sources that are deciphered in class or in research. Historical technical term.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"漢文の訓読は日本独自の読解法だ。","romaji":"Kanbun no kundoku wa Nihon dokuji no dokkaihō da.","german":"Kundoku reading of kanbun is a reading method unique to Japan.","furigana":"かんぶん|くんどく|にほんどくじ|どっかいほう"},{"japanese":"高校の国語の授業では、古文と漢文も学ぶ。","romaji":"Kōkō no kokugo no jugyō de wa, kobun to kanbun mo manabu.","german":"In high-school Japanese class, you also study classical Japanese and kanbun.","furigana":"こうこう|こくご|じゅぎょう|こぶん|かんぶん|まな"},{"japanese":"昔の武士は、教養として漢文の読み書きができた。","romaji":"Mukashi no bushi wa, kyōyō to shite kanbun no yomikaki ga dekita.","german":"The samurai of old could read and write kanbun as part of their education.","furigana":"むかし|ぶし|きょうよう|かんぶん|よ|か"}],"notes":"漢文 denotes classical Chinese texts, as they have been read, and also composed, in Japan for centuries. At school, 漢文 is, alongside 古文 \"classical Japanese\", part of the subject 国語. It is usually read by 訓読, i.e. with reading marks in Japanese word order; the result is called 書き下し文. Not to be confused with 中国語, modern Chinese.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"漢文を日本語の語順で訓読する。","romaji":"Kanbun o Nihongo no gojun de kundoku suru.","german":"Kanbun is read through kundoku in Japanese word order.","furigana":"かんぶん|にほんご|ごじゅん|くんどく"},{"japanese":"訓読した文を、仮名交じりで書いたものを書き下し文という。","romaji":"Kundoku shita bun o, kanamajiri de kaita mono o kakikudashibun to iu.","german":"A kundoku-read text written out mixed with kana is called kakikudashi-bun.","furigana":"くんどく|ぶん|かなま|か|か|くだ|ぶん"},{"japanese":"中国の人は漢文を訓読せず、そのまま中国語の発音で読む。","romaji":"Chūgoku no hito wa kanbun o kundoku sezu, sono mama Chūgokugo no hatsuon de yomu.","german":"Chinese people don't read kanbun by kundoku but directly with Chinese pronunciation.","furigana":"ちゅうごく|ひと|かんぶん|くんどく|ちゅうごくご|はつおん|よ"}],"notes":"訓読 is the reading of a classical Chinese text (漢文) in Japanese: with the help of 返り点 and 送り仮名, the Chinese word order is rearranged into Japanese; as a transitive する-verb: 漢文を訓読する. The result is called 書き下し文. In addition, 訓読 means the kun reading of a kanji, in everyday life usually called 訓読み. Technical term from school and philology.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"返り点を頼りに漢文を読み下す。","romaji":"Kaeriten o tayori ni kanbun o yomikudasu.","german":"Relying on the kaeriten, I read the kanbun out in Japanese.","furigana":"かえ|てん|たよ|かんぶん|よ|くだ"},{"japanese":"レ点は、下の字を先に読んでから一字上に返ることを示す返り点だ。","romaji":"Reten wa, shita no ji o saki ni yonde kara ichiji ue ni kaeru koto o shimesu kaeriten da.","german":"The re mark is a kaeriten that indicates that you read the lower character first and then go back up one character.","furigana":"てん|した|じ|さき|よ|いちじうえ|かえ|しめ|かえ|てん"},{"japanese":"次の白文に、返り点と送り仮名を付けなさい。","romaji":"Tsugi no hakubun ni, kaeriten to okurigana o tsukenasai.","german":"Add kaeriten and okurigana to the following unpunctuated text.","furigana":"つぎ|はくぶん|かえ|てん|おく|がな|つ"}],"notes":"返り点 are small marks at the lower left of the characters of a 漢文 text that show in which order one has to jump back when reading it in Japanese (訓読). The most important are レ点, 一二点 and 上下点. Typical: 返り点を付ける, 返り点に従って読む. Together with 送り仮名 and punctuation they belong to the 訓点; a text without them is called 白文.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"白文で漢文を読むには高い漢文力が必要だ。","romaji":"Hakubun de kanbun o yomu ni wa takai kanbun-ryoku ga hitsuyō da.","german":"Reading kanbun as hakubun requires a high level of kanbun skill.","furigana":"はくぶん|かんぶん|よ|たか|かんぶんりょく|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"教科書の漢文には訓点が付いているが、原典は白文だ。","romaji":"Kyōkasho no kanbun ni wa kunten ga tsuite iru ga, genten wa hakubun da.","german":"The kanbun in textbooks has reading marks attached, but the originals are hakubun.","furigana":"きょうかしょ|かんぶん|くんてん|つ|げんてん|はくぶん"},{"japanese":"試験で、白文に返り点を付ける問題が出た。","romaji":"Shiken de, hakubun ni kaeriten o tsukeru mondai ga deta.","german":"The exam included a question asking you to add kaeriten to a hakubun.","furigana":"しけん|はくぶん|かえ|てん|つ|もんだい|で"}],"notes":"白文 is a classical Chinese text without Japanese reading aids (訓点), i.e. without 返り点, 送り仮名 and usually without punctuation. The counterpart is a text provided with 訓点 or the rewritten 書き下し文. 白文で読む is regarded as a sign of high kanbun proficiency. Technical term from school teaching and philology; not to be confused with 白紙 \"blank sheet\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"懐かしい学び舎を訪れて感慨深かった。","romaji":"Natsukashii manabiya o otozurete kangaibukakatta.","german":"It was deeply moving to visit my nostalgic old school.","furigana":"なつ|まな|や|おとず|かんがいぶか"},{"japanese":"校長先生は「この学び舎を巣立つ皆さんへ」と語りかけた。","romaji":"Kōchō sensei wa \"kono manabiya o sudatsu minasan e\" to katarikaketa.","german":"The principal addressed the graduates, saying 「この学び舎を巣立つ皆さんへ」.","furigana":"こうちょうせんせい|まな|や|すだ|みな|かた"},{"japanese":"百年の歴史を持つ木造の学び舎が、今年取り壊される。","romaji":"Hyakunen no rekishi o motsu mokuzō no manabiya ga, kotoshi torikowasareru.","german":"The hundred-year-old wooden schoolhouse will be torn down this year.","furigana":"ひゃくねん|れきし|も|もくぞう|まな|や|ことしと|こわ"}],"notes":"学び舎 is a poetic, emotional word for the school or school building, literally \"house of learning\". It appears in school songs, graduation speeches, anniversary publications and the memories of former pupils, often with 懐かしい, 母校 or 巣立つ. In ordinary conversation one says 学校 or 校舎; the Sino-Japanese reading 学舎（がくしゃ） is also elevated.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"教頭先生が校長の代理を務めた。","romaji":"Kyōtō sensei ga kōchō no dairi o tsutometa.","german":"The vice principal stood in for the principal.","furigana":"きょうとうせんせい|こうちょう|だいり|つと"},{"japanese":"遅刻が多くて、教頭先生に呼び出された。","romaji":"Chikoku ga ōkute, kyōtō sensei ni yobidasareta.","german":"I was summoned by the vice principal for being late too often.","furigana":"ちこく|おお|きょうとうせんせい|よ|だ"},{"japanese":"保護者からの苦情には、まず教頭が対応する。","romaji":"Hogosha kara no kujō ni wa, mazu kyōtō ga taiō suru.","german":"Complaints from parents are handled first by the vice principal.","furigana":"ほごしゃ|くじょう|きょうとう|たいおう"}],"notes":"教頭 is the vice principal at elementary, middle and high schools; he or she supports the 校長 and represents him or her in his or her absence. Pupils and parents address the person as 教頭先生. Some schools have a 副校長 instead or in addition. Universities have no 教頭; the offices there are called 学長 and 副学長.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"学部長の挨拶で入学式が始まった。","romaji":"Gakubuchō no aisatsu de nyūgaku-shiki ga hajimatta.","german":"The entrance ceremony began with the dean's address.","furigana":"がくぶちょう|あいさつ|にゅうがくしき|はじ"},{"japanese":"彼女は来年度から文学部の学部長を務める。","romaji":"Kanojo wa rainendo kara bungakubu no gakubuchō o tsutomeru.","german":"She will serve as dean of the Faculty of Letters from next academic year.","furigana":"かのじょ|らいねんど|ぶんがくぶ|がくぶちょう|つと"},{"japanese":"提出期限を延ばすには、学部長の許可が必要だ。","romaji":"Teishutsu kigen o nobasu ni wa, gakubuchō no kyoka ga hitsuyō da.","german":"You need the dean's permission to extend the submission deadline.","furigana":"ていしゅつきげん|の|がくぶちょう|きょか|ひつよう"}],"notes":"学部長 is the dean of a faculty (学部) at a university, e.g. 文学部長 or 法学部長. Above this position is the 学長 \"university president\"; the head of a graduate school (研究科) is called 研究科長. Typical: 学部長を務める, 学部長に選ばれる, 学部長の許可. Neutral, usual in the university context.","levelNote":"Compound: 学部 (N2) + 長 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"学長が卒業生に激励の言葉を贈った。","romaji":"Gakuchō ga sotsugyōsei ni gekirei no kotoba o okutta.","german":"The president gave words of encouragement to the graduates.","furigana":"がくちょう|そつぎょうせい|げきれい|ことば|おく"},{"japanese":"新しい学長が就任し、大学改革を宣言した。","romaji":"Atarashii gakuchō ga shūnin shi, daigaku kaikaku o sengen shita.","german":"The new president took office and declared university reform.","furigana":"あたら|がくちょう|しゅうにん|だいがくかいかく|せんげん"},{"japanese":"学長選挙には三人の教授が立候補した。","romaji":"Gakuchō senkyo ni wa sannin no kyōju ga rikkōho shita.","german":"Three professors ran in the presidential election.","furigana":"がくちょうせんきょ|さんにん|きょうじゅ|りっこうほ"}],"notes":"学長 is the president of a university, i.e. the supreme head of the institution. Some old universities, such as the University of Tokyo, use the title 総長 instead. At schools the head is called 校長, at faculties 学部長. Typical: 学長に就任する, 学長選挙, 学長の式辞.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"助教として大学で研究と教育に従事している。","romaji":"Jokyō toshite daigaku de kenkyū to kyōiku ni jūji shite iru.","german":"I am engaged in research and teaching at a university as an assistant professor.","furigana":"じょきょう|だいがく|けんきゅう|きょういく|じゅうじ"},{"japanese":"彼女は博士号を取ってすぐ、母校の助教に採用された。","romaji":"Kanojo wa hakasegō o totte sugu, bokō no jokyō ni saiyō sareta.","german":"She was hired as an assistant professor at her alma mater right after earning her doctorate.","furigana":"かのじょ|はかせごう|と|ぼこう|じょきょう|さいよう"},{"japanese":"助教から准教授に昇進するまで五年かかった。","romaji":"Jokyō kara junkyōju ni shōshin suru made gonen kakatta.","german":"It took five years to be promoted from assistant professor to associate professor.","furigana":"じょきょう|じゅんきょうじゅ|しょうしん|ごねん"}],"notes":"助教 has been, since 2007, the lowest rank of the Japanese academic career: 教授 \"professor\", 准教授 \"associate professor\", 講師 \"lecturer\", then 助教. The post partly replaced the former 助手 \"assistant\" and is often fixed-term, with teaching and one's own research. Not to be confused with 助教授, the old title for today's 准教授.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"退職後に名誉教授の称号を授与された。","romaji":"Taishoku-go ni meiyo kyōju no shōgō o juyo sareta.","german":"After retirement, he was awarded the title of professor emeritus.","furigana":"たいしょくご|めいよきょうじゅ|しょうごう|じゅよ"},{"japanese":"ニュース番組に、京都大学の名誉教授が解説者として出演した。","romaji":"Nyūsu bangumi ni, Kyōto Daigaku no meiyo kyōju ga kaisetsusha to shite shutsuen shita.","german":"A professor emeritus of Kyoto University appeared on the news program as a commentator.","furigana":"ばんぐみ|きょうとだいがく|めいよきょうじゅ|かいせつしゃ|しゅつえん"},{"japanese":"祖父は名誉教授になった今も、毎日図書館で研究を続けている。","romaji":"Sofu wa meiyo kyōju ni natta ima mo, mainichi toshokan de kenkyū o tsuzukete iru.","german":"Even now that he is a professor emeritus, my grandfather continues his research in the library every day.","furigana":"そふ|めいよきょうじゅ|いま|まいにちとしょかん|けんきゅう|つづ"}],"notes":"名誉教授 is an honorary title that a university confers on professors of merit after retirement; it corresponds to \"professor emeritus\". Typical: 名誉教授の称号を授与される, 名誉教授になる. In the media, experts are often introduced as 〜大学名誉教授. The title carries no teaching obligation.","levelNote":"Compound: 名誉 (N1) + 教授 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人文科学研究科に所属している。","romaji":"Jinbun kagaku kenkyūka ni shozoku shite iru.","german":"I belong to the Graduate School of Humanities.","furigana":"じんぶんかがくけんきゅうか|しょぞく"},{"japanese":"卒業後は、大学院の経済学研究科に進学するつもりだ。","romaji":"Sotsugyō-go wa, daigakuin no keizaigaku kenkyūka ni shingaku suru tsumori da.","german":"After graduating, I intend to go on to the Graduate School of Economics.","furigana":"そつぎょうご|だいがくいん|けいざいがくけんきゅうか|しんがく"},{"japanese":"修士論文の提出時期は、研究科によって異なる。","romaji":"Shūshi ronbun no teishutsu jiki wa, kenkyūka ni yotte kotonaru.","german":"The deadline for submitting the master's thesis differs depending on the graduate school.","furigana":"しゅうしろんぶん|ていしゅつじき|けんきゅうか|こと"}],"notes":"研究科 is the subject department of a graduate school (大学院), such as 法学研究科 or 人文科学研究科; for master's and doctoral study it corresponds to what 学部 is for undergraduate study. Typical: 〜研究科に所属する/進学する, 研究科長 \"head of the graduate school\". Neutral, usual in university administration and in résumés.","levelNote":"Compound: 研究 (N4) + 科 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"論旨を明確にして論文を構成すべきだ。","romaji":"Ronshi o meikaku ni shite ronbun o kōsei subeki da.","german":"You should clarify the line of argument and structure the paper around it.","furigana":"ろんし|めいかく|ろんぶん|こうせい"},{"japanese":"この文章の論旨として、最も適当なものを選びなさい。","romaji":"Kono bunshō no ronshi to shite, mottomo tekitō na mono o erabinasai.","german":"Choose the option that best captures the main argument of this text.","furigana":"ぶんしょう|ろんし|もっと|てきとう|えら"},{"japanese":"彼の発言は、途中から論旨がずれてしまった。","romaji":"Kare no hatsugen wa, tochū kara ronshi ga zurete shimatta.","german":"Partway through, his remarks strayed from the main argument.","furigana":"かれ|はつげん|とちゅう|ろんし"}],"notes":"論旨 is the core and train of thought of an argument in a text or lecture. Typical are 論旨が明快だ/一貫している, 論旨がずれる and 論旨をつかむ; the set phrase 論旨明快 praises a clear argument. 要旨 is the brief summary of the content, 論点 the point at issue. Literary, frequent in exams and scholarship.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"主張の典拠を明示することが学術論文の基本だ。","romaji":"Shuchō no tenkyo o meiji suru koto ga gakujutsu ronbun no kihon da.","german":"Clearly stating the sources of one's claims is fundamental to academic papers.","furigana":"しゅちょう|てんきょ|めいじ|がくじゅつろんぶん|きほん"},{"japanese":"このことわざの典拠は、中国の古典にある。","romaji":"Kono kotowaza no tenkyo wa, Chūgoku no koten ni aru.","german":"The source of this proverb lies in a Chinese classic.","furigana":"てんきょ|ちゅうごく|こてん"},{"japanese":"典拠のないうわさを、事実のように書いてはいけない。","romaji":"Tenkyo no nai uwasa o, jijitsu no yō ni kaite wa ikenai.","german":"You must not write unsourced rumors as if they were facts.","furigana":"てんきょ|じじつ|か"}],"notes":"典拠 is the reliable source or authority on which a statement, quotation or phrase rests. Typical: 典拠を示す/明示する, 典拠がある/ない, 〜の典拠は…にある. The more common 出典 specifically denotes the place where a quotation is found; 根拠 is generally \"reason, basis\". Literary, in scholarly and editorial work.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"引用する際は出典を明記しなければならない。","romaji":"In'yō suru sai wa shutten o meiki shinakereba naranai.","german":"When citing, the source must be clearly stated.","furigana":"いんよう|さい|しゅってん|めいき"},{"japanese":"この一文は、夏目漱石の小説から引用したものだ。","romaji":"Kono ichibun wa, Natsume Sōseki no shōsetsu kara in'yō shita mono da.","german":"This sentence is quoted from a novel by Natsume Soseki.","furigana":"いちぶん|なつめそうせき|しょうせつ|いんよう"},{"japanese":"社長はスピーチで、よく偉人の言葉を引用する。","romaji":"Shachō wa supīchi de, yoku ijin no kotoba o in'yō suru.","german":"The company president often quotes the words of great figures in his speeches.","furigana":"しゃちょう|いじん|ことば|いんよう"}],"notes":"引用 is quoting or a quotation; as a transitive する-verb: 〜から…を引用する. Typical are 引用文, 引用元 \"quoted source\" and 出典を明記する \"to state the source\". In contrast to 転載 \"reprint, taking over in full\", with 引用 only a part is taken over and marked as someone else's. Neutral, in everyday life as well as in scholarship.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"剽窃は学術界における最も深刻な不正行為だ。","romaji":"Hyōsetsu wa gakujutsu-kai ni okeru mottomo shinkoku na fusei kōi da.","german":"Plagiarism is the most serious misconduct in academia.","furigana":"ひょうせつ|がくじゅつかい|もっと|しんこく|ふせいこうい"},{"japanese":"卒業論文の一部が、ネット記事からの剽窃だと判明した。","romaji":"Sotsugyō ronbun no ichibu ga, netto kiji kara no hyōsetsu da to hanmei shita.","german":"It turned out that part of the graduation thesis was plagiarized from an online article.","furigana":"そつぎょうろんぶん|いちぶ|きじ|ひょうせつ|はんめい"},{"japanese":"その作家は、他人のアイデアを剽窃したとして批判を浴びた。","romaji":"Sono sakka wa, tanin no aidea o hyōsetsu shita to shite hihan o abita.","german":"The writer was heavily criticized for plagiarizing other people's ideas.","furigana":"さっか|たにん|ひょうせつ|ひはん|あ"}],"notes":"剽窃 is plagiarism, i.e. taking over other people's texts or ideas as one's own; as a transitive する-verb: 論文を剽窃する. It is literary and legal-academic; for artworks and music one often says 盗作, colloquially パクリ or パクる. Typical: 剽窃が発覚する/判明する, 剽窃を疑われる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"西洋の詩では脚韻が重要な要素だ。","romaji":"Seiyō no shi de wa kyakuin ga jūyō na yōso da.","german":"In Western poetry, end rhyme is an important element.","furigana":"せいよう|し|きゃくいん|じゅうよう|ようそ"},{"japanese":"ラップの歌詞では、脚韻を踏むのが基本だ。","romaji":"Rappu no kashi de wa, kyakuin o fumu no ga kihon da.","german":"In rap lyrics, rhyming at the end of lines is basic.","furigana":"かし|きゃくいん|ふ|きほん"},{"japanese":"この二行は「ハート」と「スタート」で脚韻を踏んでいる。","romaji":"Kono nigyō wa \"hāto\" to \"sutāto\" de kyakuin o funde iru.","german":"These two lines rhyme with 「ハート」 and 「スタート」.","furigana":"にぎょう|きゃくいん|ふ"}],"notes":"脚韻 is end rhyme, i.e. the matching sound at the end of verses or lines; the verb for it is 踏む: 脚韻を踏む. The opposite term is 頭韻 \"alliteration\"; in general one says 韻を踏む \"to rhyme\". Classical Japanese poetry has hardly any rhyme; today 脚韻 is encountered mainly in rap, pop and translations of Western poetry.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"頭韻を踏んだフレーズは記憶に残りやすい。","romaji":"Tōin o funda furēzu wa kioku ni nokoriyasui.","german":"Alliterative phrases are easy to remember.","furigana":"とういん|ふ|きおく|のこ"},{"japanese":"「コカ・コーラ」は、頭韻を踏んだ商品名の一例だ。","romaji":"\"Koka kōra\" wa, tōin o funda shōhinmei no ichirei da.","german":"\"Coca-Cola\" is an example of an alliterative product name.","furigana":"とういん|ふ|しょうひんめい|いちれい"},{"japanese":"古いゲルマンの詩では、脚韻より頭韻が重視された。","romaji":"Furui Geruman no shi de wa, kyakuin yori tōin ga jūshi sareta.","german":"In old Germanic poetry, alliteration was valued more than end rhyme.","furigana":"ふる|し|きゃくいん|とういん|じゅうし"}],"notes":"頭韻 is the matching sound at the beginning of words, i.e. alliteration; as with end rhyme one says 頭韻を踏む. The opposite term is 脚韻 \"end rhyme\". Typical fields of application are advertising slogans, brand names and old Germanic poetry. Technical term from literary studies and rhetoric.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"縁語は和歌の中で関連する語を散りばめる技法だ。","romaji":"Engo wa waka no naka de kanren suru go o chiribameru gihō da.","german":"Engo is a technique of scattering related words through a waka poem.","furigana":"えんご|わか|なか|かんれん|ご|ち|ぎほう"},{"japanese":"縁語は、掛詞と組み合わせて使われることが多い。","romaji":"Engo wa, kakekotoba to kumiawasete tsukawareru koto ga ōi.","german":"Engo is often used in combination with kakekotoba.","furigana":"えんご|かけことば|く|あ|つか|おお"},{"japanese":"この歌では、「衣」の縁語として「着る」や「張る」が詠み込まれている。","romaji":"Kono uta de wa, \"koromo\" no engo to shite \"kiru\" ya \"haru\" ga yomikomarete iru.","german":"In this poem, 「着る」 and 「張る」 are worked in as engo of 「衣」.","furigana":"うた|ころも|えんご|き|は|よ|こ"}],"notes":"縁語 is a stylistic device of classical Japanese poetry, above all in 和歌: around a key word, words related in meaning are scattered that form an image field, e.g. for 衣 \"garment\" the words 着る, 張る and 褄. It is often combined with 掛詞 \"pun with double meaning\". Typical: 〜の縁語. Technical term from literature teaching.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"本歌取りは先行する和歌を踏まえた創作技法だ。","romaji":"Honkadori wa senkō suru waka o fumaeta sōsaku gihō da.","german":"Honkadori is a creative technique that builds on an earlier waka.","furigana":"ほんかど|せんこう|わか|ふ|そうさくぎほう"},{"japanese":"本歌取りは、新古今和歌集の時代に盛んに行われた。","romaji":"Honkadori wa, Shinkokin Wakashū no jidai ni sakan ni okonawareta.","german":"Honkadori was practiced vigorously in the age of the Shinkokinshū.","furigana":"ほんかど|しんこきんわかしゅう|じだい|さか|おこな"},{"japanese":"この歌詞は、有名な和歌の本歌取りになっている。","romaji":"Kono kashi wa, yūmei na waka no honkadori ni natte iru.","german":"These lyrics are a honkadori of a famous waka.","furigana":"かし|ゆうめい|わか|ほんかど"}],"notes":"本歌取り is a technique of waka poetry in which words or phrases of a famous older poem (本歌) are deliberately taken over to carry its mood into a new poem. Its heyday was the period of the 新古今和歌集, for instance with 藤原定家. Today the term is also used figuratively for a homage in song lyrics or films.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"歌枕として知られる名所を巡る旅に出た。","romaji":"Utamakura toshite shirareru meisho o meguru tabi ni deta.","german":"I set out on a trip to visit scenic spots known as utamakura.","furigana":"うたまくら|し|めいしょ|めぐ|たび|で"},{"japanese":"芭蕉は『おくのほそ道』の旅で、多くの歌枕を訪ねた。","romaji":"Bashō wa \"Oku no Hosomichi\" no tabi de, ōku no utamakura o tazuneta.","german":"On his journey for 『おくのほそ道』, Bashō visited many utamakura.","furigana":"|みち|たび|おお|うたまくら|たず"},{"japanese":"竜田川は、紅葉の歌枕として有名だ。","romaji":"Tatsutagawa wa, momiji no utamakura to shite yūmei da.","german":"The Tatsuta River is famous as an utamakura for autumn leaves.","furigana":"たつたがわ|もみじ|うたまくら|ゆうめい"}],"notes":"歌枕 is a place that is sung about again and again in waka poetry and carries fixed associations, such as 竜田川 with autumn leaves or 吉野 with cherry blossoms. Originally the word also denoted collections of such poetic terms. 芭蕉 deliberately visited many 歌枕 on his journey of the 奥の細道. Technical term from literature and cultural history.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「や」「かな」「けり」は代表的な切れ字だ。","romaji":"\"Ya\" \"kana\" \"keri\" wa daihyō-teki na kireji da.","german":"「や」, 「かな」 and 「けり」 are typical kireji.","furigana":"だいひょうてき|き|じ"},{"japanese":"「古池や」の「や」は切れ字で、句に間と余韻を生む。","romaji":"\"Furuike ya\" no \"ya\" wa kireji de, ku ni ma to yoin o umu.","german":"The 「や」 in 「古池や」 is a kireji, creating a pause and lingering resonance in the verse.","furigana":"ふるいけ|き|じ|く|ま|よいん|う"},{"japanese":"一句に切れ字を二つ使うのは避けたほうがいい。","romaji":"Ikku ni kireji o futatsu tsukau no wa saketa hō ga ii.","german":"It is better to avoid using two kireji in one verse.","furigana":"いっく|き|じ|ふた|つか|さ"}],"notes":"切れ字 are \"cutting words\" in haiku and renga that set a caesura and create lingering resonance (余韻), above all や, かな and けり, as in Bashō's 古池や. Traditionally one speaks of eighteen 切れ字. A basic rule is not to use two 切れ字 in one verse. The word belongs to the technical language of poetry and literature teaching.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"連歌は複数の詠み手が交互に句を詠む詩形だ。","romaji":"Renga wa fukusū no yomite ga kōgo ni ku o yomu shikei da.","german":"Renga is a poetic form in which several poets compose verses in turn.","furigana":"れんが|ふくすう|よ|て|こうご|く|よ|しけい"},{"japanese":"室町時代には、武士や僧の間で連歌が大流行した。","romaji":"Muromachi jidai ni wa, bushi ya sō no aida de renga ga dai-ryūkō shita.","german":"In the Muromachi period, renga was enormously popular among samurai and monks.","furigana":"むろまちじだい|ぶし|そう|あいだ|れんが|だいりゅうこう"},{"japanese":"俳句は、連歌の最初の句である発句が独立したものだ。","romaji":"Haiku wa, renga no saisho no ku de aru hokku ga dokuritsu shita mono da.","german":"Haiku arose when the opening verse of renga, the hokku, became independent.","furigana":"はいく|れんが|さいしょ|く|ほっく|どくりつ"}],"notes":"連歌 is a form of linked poetry in which several poets string together verses in turn in the patterns 5-7-5 and 7-7. It flourished in the Middle Ages, especially in the Muromachi period with masters such as 宗祇. From the more humorous 俳諧連歌 the 俳句 later developed, whose origin is the opening verse 発句. Typical: 連歌を詠む, 連歌会, 連歌師.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"狂歌は和歌の形式を借りた滑稽な詩だ。","romaji":"Kyōka wa waka no keishiki o karita kokkei na shi da.","german":"Kyōka is a comic poem that borrows the form of waka.","furigana":"きょうか|わか|けいしき|か|こっけい|し"},{"japanese":"江戸時代の中ごろ、狂歌は町人の間で大流行した。","romaji":"Edo jidai no nakagoro, kyōka wa chōnin no aida de dai-ryūkō shita.","german":"In the middle of the Edo period, kyōka became hugely popular among townspeople.","furigana":"えどじだい|なか|きょうか|ちょうにん|あいだ|だいりゅうこう"},{"japanese":"狂歌は和歌の形で、川柳は俳句の形で世の中を笑う。","romaji":"Kyōka wa waka no katachi de, senryū wa haiku no katachi de yo no naka o warau.","german":"Kyōka laughs at the world in waka form, senryū in haiku form.","furigana":"きょうか|わか|かたち|せんりゅう|はいく|かたち|よ|なか|わら"}],"notes":"狂歌 is a comic or satirical poem in the waka form 5-7-5-7-7 that mocks society and politics with wordplay and parody. Its heyday was the middle Edo period, with figures such as 大田南畝. The counterpart in the haiku form 5-7-5 is called 川柳. Typical: 狂歌を詠む; technical term from literary history.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"平家物語は代表的な軍記物語だ。","romaji":"Heike Monogatari wa daihyō-teki na gunki monogatari da.","german":"The Tale of the Heike is a representative war tale.","furigana":"へいけものがたり|だいひょうてき|ぐんきものがたり"},{"japanese":"『太平記』は、南北朝時代の争乱を描いた軍記物語だ。","romaji":"\"Taiheiki\" wa, Nanbokuchō jidai no sōran o egaita gunki monogatari da.","german":"The 『太平記』 is a war tale depicting the turmoil of the Northern and Southern Courts period.","furigana":"たいへいき|なんぼくちょうじだい|そうらん|えが|ぐんきものがたり"},{"japanese":"軍記物語の多くは、琵琶法師によって語り伝えられた。","romaji":"Gunki monogatari no ōku wa, biwa hōshi ni yotte kataritsutaerareta.","german":"Many war tales were handed down orally by biwa hōshi.","furigana":"ぐんきものがたり|おお|びわほうし|かた|つた"}],"notes":"軍記物語 is a genre of the Japanese Middle Ages that tells of the wars and battles of the samurai, often with the Buddhist idea of impermanence. Well-known works are 平家物語, 保元物語 and 太平記. More briefly one also says 軍記物 or 軍記. The 平家物語 was recited by blind lute players (琵琶法師). Technical term from literary history.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今昔物語集は説話文学の傑作だ。","romaji":"Konjaku Monogatari-shū wa setsuwa bungaku no kessaku da.","german":"The Konjaku Monogatari-shū is a masterpiece of setsuwa literature.","furigana":"こんじゃくものがたりしゅう|せつわぶんがく|けっさく"},{"japanese":"芥川龍之介の『羅生門』は、古い説話をもとに書かれた。","romaji":"Akutagawa Ryūnosuke no \"Rashōmon\" wa, furui setsuwa o moto ni kakareta.","german":"Akutagawa Ryūnosuke's 『羅生門』 was written based on an old setsuwa.","furigana":"|らしょうもん|ふる|せつわ|か"},{"japanese":"仏教の教えを分かりやすく伝えるために、多くの説話が作られた。","romaji":"Bukkyō no oshie o wakariyasuku tsutaeru tame ni, ōku no setsuwa ga tsukurareta.","german":"Many setsuwa were created to convey Buddhist teachings in an easy-to-understand way.","furigana":"ぶっきょう|おし|わ|つた|おお|せつわ|つく"}],"notes":"説話 denotes handed-down narratives such as Buddhist teaching tales, legends and popular anecdotes, especially the genre 説話文学 with collections such as 今昔物語集 or 宇治拾遺物語. In everyday life, for folktales one rather says 昔話, for stories of gods 神話, and for place-bound legends 伝説. Technical term, above all in the history of literature and religion.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"雅語を使うことで文章に格調が生まれる。","romaji":"Gago o tsukau koto de bunshō ni kakuchō ga umareru.","german":"Using elegant words gives the writing dignity.","furigana":"がご|つか|ぶんしょう|かくちょう|う"},{"japanese":"「いにしえ」は「昔」の雅語だ。","romaji":"\"Inishie\" wa \"mukashi\" no gago da.","german":"「いにしえ」 is the elegant word for 「昔」.","furigana":"むかし|がご"},{"japanese":"この作家は、雅語と俗語を巧みに混ぜた文体で知られる。","romaji":"Kono sakka wa, gago to zokugo o takumi ni mazeta buntai de shirareru.","german":"This author is known for a style that skillfully mixes elegant words and colloquial words.","furigana":"さっか|がご|ぞくご|たく|ま|ぶんたい|し"}],"notes":"雅語 are elevated, poetic words, especially the classical vocabulary of the Heian period that was used in 和歌, such as いにしえ instead of 昔 or 月影 instead of 月の光. The opposite term is 俗語 \"colloquial language, slang\". Almost synonymous is 雅言, which rather means the refined language as a whole. Technical term from linguistics and literature.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"雅言を用いた和歌は気品に満ちている。","romaji":"Gagen o mochiita waka wa kihin ni michite iru.","german":"Waka poems in elegant language are full of grace.","furigana":"がげん|もち|わか|きひん|み"},{"japanese":"江戸時代の国学者は、平安時代の言葉を雅言として重んじた。","romaji":"Edo jidai no kokugakusha wa, Heian jidai no kotoba o gagen to shite omonjita.","german":"The kokugaku scholars of the Edo period valued the language of the Heian period as gagen.","furigana":"えどじだい|こくがくしゃ|へいあんじだい|ことば|がげん|おも"},{"japanese":"雅言と俗語の違いを、例を挙げて説明しなさい。","romaji":"Gagen to zokugo no chigai o, rei o agete setsumei shinasai.","german":"Explain the difference between gagen and zokugo, giving examples.","furigana":"がげん|ぞくご|ちが|れい|あ|せつめい"}],"notes":"雅言 is the elegant, refined language, especially the classical court language of the Heian period, which scholars of the Edo period (国学) regarded as an ideal. The opposite terms are 俗語 or 俗言 \"vernacular\" and 俚言 \"dialect word\". In content almost the same as 雅語, which refers more to individual words. A rarer, scholarly technical term.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"国語学は日本語の歴史的変遷を研究する。","romaji":"Kokugogaku wa Nihongo no rekishi-teki hensen o kenkyū suru.","german":"Kokugogaku studies the historical changes of the Japanese language.","furigana":"こくごがく|にほんご|れきしてきへんせん|けんきゅう"},{"japanese":"大学では国語学を専攻し、方言の研究をしている。","romaji":"Daigaku de wa kokugogaku o senkō shi, hōgen no kenkyū o shite iru.","german":"At university I major in kokugogaku and research dialects.","furigana":"だいがく|こくごがく|せんこう|ほうげん|けんきゅう"},{"japanese":"最近は国語学ではなく日本語学という名前を使う大学が増えている。","romaji":"Saikin wa kokugogaku de wa naku Nihongogaku to iu namae o tsukau daigaku ga fuete iru.","german":"Recently, more and more universities use the name Nihongogaku rather than kokugogaku.","furigana":"さいきん|こくごがく|にほんごがく|なまえ|つか|だいがく|ふ"}],"notes":"国語学 is the traditional name for the scholarly study of the Japanese language – phonology, grammar, vocabulary, writing and above all their historical development. 国語 here means \"the national language\", i.e. Japanese from a Japanese insider's point of view. Today many universities and academic societies call the field, more neutrally, 日本語学. Not to be confused with 国文学, the study of Japanese literature; typical are 国語学を専攻する and 国語学者.","levelNote":"Compound: 国語 (N3) + 学 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"上代語の研究は万葉集の解読に不可欠だ。","romaji":"Jōdaigo no kenkyū wa Man'yōshū no kaidoku ni fukaketsu da.","german":"Research on Old Japanese is indispensable for deciphering the Man'yōshū.","furigana":"じょうだいご|けんきゅう|まんようしゅう|かいどく|ふかけつ"},{"japanese":"上代語には母音が八つあったという説がある。","romaji":"Jōdaigo ni wa boin ga yattsu atta to iu setsu ga aru.","german":"There is a theory that Old Japanese had eight vowels.","furigana":"じょうだいご|ぼいん|やっ|せつ"},{"japanese":"古事記の歌は上代語を知るための貴重な資料だ。","romaji":"Kojiki no uta wa jōdaigo o shiru tame no kichō na shiryō da.","german":"The songs in the Kojiki are valuable materials for learning about Old Japanese.","furigana":"こじき|うた|じょうだいご|し|きちょう|しりょう"}],"notes":"上代語 is a technical term of language history for the Japanese of the earliest written period, roughly up to the end of the Nara period (the early 8th century and before). The main sources are the 古事記, 日本書紀 and 万葉集, which are written with Chinese characters as phonetic signs (万葉仮名). In the usual division, 中古語 (Heian), 中世語 and 近世語 (Edo) follow. In a more general sense 上代語 can also simply mean \"old word, old language\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"源氏物語は中古語で書かれた作品だ。","romaji":"Genji Monogatari wa chūkogo de kakareta sakuhin da.","german":"The Tale of Genji is a work written in Early Middle Japanese.","furigana":"げんじものがたり|ちゅうこご|か|さくひん"},{"japanese":"枕草子を読むには、中古語の文法を知っておく必要がある。","romaji":"Makura no Sōshi o yomu ni wa, chūkogo no bunpō o shitte oku hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"To read The Pillow Book, you need to know the grammar of Early Middle Japanese.","furigana":"|よ|ちゅうこご|ぶんぽう|し|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"中古語の「いと」は、今の「とても」に当たる。","romaji":"Chūkogo no 'ito' wa, ima no 'totemo' ni ataru.","german":"The Early Middle Japanese 「いと」 corresponds to today's 「とても」.","furigana":"ちゅうこご|いま|あ"}],"notes":"中古語 denotes in Japanese language history the Japanese of the Heian period (approx. 9th-12th century), the language of 源氏物語 and 枕草子; in Western terminology \"Early Middle Japanese\". Classical school grammar (古典文法) is largely based on this stage of the language. The order of the period terms is 上代語 → 中古語 → 中世語 → 近世語. Note: 中古 alone means \"used, second-hand\" in everyday life (中古車), but with 語 it is always the period term.","levelNote":"Compound: 中古 (N3) + 語 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"近世語の研究は江戸文学の理解に役立つ。","romaji":"Kinseigo no kenkyū wa Edo bungaku no rikai ni yakudatsu.","german":"Research on Early Modern Japanese helps in understanding Edo literature.","furigana":"きんせいご|けんきゅう|えどぶんがく|りかい|やくだ"},{"japanese":"落語には近世語の言い回しが今も多く残っている。","romaji":"Rakugo ni wa kinseigo no iimawashi ga ima mo ōku nokotte iru.","german":"Many expressions of Early Modern Japanese still remain in rakugo today.","furigana":"らくご|きんせいご|い|まわ|いま|おお|のこ"},{"japanese":"近世語は上方語と江戸語に分けて論じられることが多い。","romaji":"Kinseigo wa kamigatago to Edogo ni wakete ronjirareru koto ga ōi.","german":"Early Modern Japanese is often discussed divided into Kamigata and Edo speech.","furigana":"きんせいご|かみがたご|えどご|わ|ろん|おお"}],"notes":"近世語 is the Japanese of the early modern period, i.e. the Edo period (1603-1868). It is often divided into 上方語 (Kyōto/Ōsaka, mainly the early Edo period) and 江戸語 (the language of Edo, which later dominates). Sources are e.g. 浮世草子, 歌舞伎 and 落語. Compared with 中古語, 近世語 is already quite close to present-day Japanese, but contains many words and expressions that are obsolete today.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"係り結びは古典文法特有の構文だ。","romaji":"Kakari musubi wa koten bunpō tokuyū no kōbun da.","german":"Kakari-musubi is a sentence structure peculiar to classical grammar.","furigana":"かか|むす|こてんぶんぽうとくゆう|こうぶん"},{"japanese":"「こそ」による係り結びでは、文末が已然形になる。","romaji":"'Koso' ni yoru kakari musubi de wa, bunmatsu ga izenkei ni naru.","german":"In kakari-musubi with 「こそ」, the sentence ends in the realis form.","furigana":"かか|むす|ぶんまつ|いぜんけい"},{"japanese":"高校の古文の授業で、係り結びの法則を習った。","romaji":"Kōkō no kobun no jugyō de, kakari musubi no hōsoku o naratta.","german":"In high-school classical Japanese class, I learned the rules of kakari-musubi.","furigana":"こうこう|こぶん|じゅぎょう|かか|むす|ほうそく|なら"}],"notes":"係り結び is a rule of classical Japanese: if the sentence contains an emphasizing particle (係助詞) such as ぞ, なむ, や or か, the sentence does not end in the terminal form (終止形) but in the attributive form (連体形); after こそ the realis form (已然形) is used. 係り is the triggering particle, 結び the required ending form. One speaks of the 係り結びの法則; it is standard material in the high-school subject 古文 and is no longer alive in modern Japanese.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"テニヲハを間違えると意味が通じなくなる。","romaji":"Teniwoha o machigaeru to imi ga tsūjinaku naru.","german":"If you get the particles wrong, the meaning becomes unintelligible.","furigana":"まちが|いみ|つう"},{"japanese":"作文のテニヲハを先生に直してもらった。","romaji":"Sakubun no teniwoha o sensei ni naoshite moratta.","german":"I had my teacher correct the particles in my composition.","furigana":"さくぶん|せんせい|なお"},{"japanese":"彼の話はテニヲハが合わなくて、何が言いたいのか分からない。","romaji":"Kare no hanashi wa teniwoha ga awanakute, nani ga iitai no ka wakaranai.","german":"His speech has mismatched particles, so I can't tell what he wants to say.","furigana":"かれ|はなし|あ|なに|い|わ"}],"notes":"テニヲハ (usually written てにをは) stands for the Japanese particles and function words as a whole; the name comes from the reading marks of the old kanbun reading, whose corner points stood for て, に, を and は. In everyday life it is almost always about correct usage: テニヲハがおかしい \"the particles are off\", テニヲハを直す \"to correct particle mistakes\". Figuratively, テニヲハが合わない means that a speech is not coherent in itself.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「花火」の「び」は連濁の例だ。","romaji":"\"Hanabi\" no \"bi\" wa rendaku no rei da.","german":"The 「び」 in 「花火」 is an example of rendaku.","furigana":"はなび|れんだく|れい"},{"japanese":"「本棚」では、「たな」が連濁して「だな」になる。","romaji":"\"Hondana\" de wa, \"tana\" ga rendaku shite \"dana\" ni naru.","german":"In 「本棚」, 「たな」 undergoes rendaku and becomes 「だな」.","furigana":"ほんだな|れんだく"},{"japanese":"どんな時に連濁が起こるかは、外国人にとって難しい問題だ。","romaji":"Donna toki ni rendaku ga okoru ka wa, gaikokujin ni totte muzukashii mondai da.","german":"When rendaku occurs is a difficult question for foreigners.","furigana":"とき|れんだく|お|がいこくじん|むずか|もんだい"}],"notes":"連濁 denotes the voicing of the initial of the second member of a compound: 花＋火 → はなび, 本＋棚 → ほんだな, 人＋人 → ひとびと. It can also stand as an intransitive する-verb: 「たな」が連濁して「だな」になる. 連濁 is not predictable, but there are tendencies, e.g. it mostly fails to occur when the second member already contains a voiced consonant (Lyman's law). Technical word of phonetics and grammar.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"促音は小さい「っ」で表記される。","romaji":"Sokuon wa chiisai \"tsu\" de hyōki sareru.","german":"The sokuon is written with a small 「っ」."},{"japanese":"「きて」と「きって」は、促音があるかないかで意味が変わる。","romaji":"\"Kite\" to \"kitte\" wa, sokuon ga aru ka nai ka de imi ga kawaru.","german":"「きて」 and 「きって」 differ in meaning depending on whether there is a sokuon or not.","furigana":"そくおん|いみ|か"},{"japanese":"促音も一拍と数えるので、「きって」は三拍になる。","romaji":"Sokuon mo ippaku to kazoeru node, \"kitte\" wa sanpaku ni naru.","german":"The sokuon also counts as one mora, so 「きって」 has three morae.","furigana":"そくおん|いっぱく|かぞ|さんぱく"}],"notes":"促音 is the \"stopped\" sound written with a small っ／ッ and pronounced as a doubling of the following consonant: きって, ベッド. It counts as a mora (拍) of its own. Whether a 促音 is present distinguishes words (きて \"come\" / きって \"stamp\"). Related terms are 撥音 (ん), 長音 and 拗音; the sound change in 待って or 買って is called 促音便.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"撥音「ん」の発音は後続の音によって変わる。","romaji":"Hatsuon \"n\" no hatsuon wa kōzoku no oto ni yotte kawaru.","german":"The pronunciation of the moraic nasal 「ん」 changes depending on the following sound.","furigana":"はつおん|はつおん|こうぞく|おと|か"},{"japanese":"「さんぽ」の撥音は、後ろの「ぽ」の影響で「m」に近い音になる。","romaji":"\"Sanpo\" no hatsuon wa, ushiro no \"po\" no eikyō de \"m\" ni chikai oto ni naru.","german":"The moraic nasal in 「さんぽ」 becomes a sound close to \"m\" under the influence of the following 「ぽ」.","furigana":"はつおん|うし|えいきょう|ちか|おと"},{"japanese":"日本語の単語は、基本的に撥音で始まることがない。","romaji":"Nihongo no tango wa, kihon-teki ni hatsuon de hajimaru koto ga nai.","german":"Japanese words basically never begin with the moraic nasal.","furigana":"にほんご|たんご|きほんてき|はつおん|はじ"}],"notes":"撥音 is the syllabic nasal ん／ン. It counts as a mora of its own and is pronounced, depending on the following sound, as [m], [n], [ŋ] or a nasalized vowel (さんぽ, あんない, りんご). Caution: 撥音 sounds exactly like 発音 \"pronunciation\", hence sentences like 撥音の発音. The sound change in 読んで or 死んで is called 撥音便. Related terms: 促音, 長音, 拗音.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"長音の有無で意味が変わる語がある。","romaji":"Chōon no umu de imi ga kawaru go ga aru.","german":"There are words whose meaning changes depending on whether there is a long vowel.","furigana":"ちょうおん|うむ|いみ|か|ご"},{"japanese":"外来語の長音は「ー」で書き表す。","romaji":"Gairaigo no chōon wa 'bō' de kakiarawasu.","german":"Long vowels in loanwords are written with 「ー」."},{"japanese":"日本語の長音は一拍として数える。","romaji":"Nihongo no chōon wa ippaku to shite kazoeru.","german":"A long vowel in Japanese is counted as one mora.","furigana":"にほんご|ちょうおん|いっぱく|かぞ"}],"notes":"長音 is the long vowel or vowel lengthening, e.g. in おばあさん or こうこう. In Japanese the lengthened part counts as a mora (拍) of its own, as do 促音 (the small っ) and 撥音 (ん). In katakana the 長音 is written with the dash \"ー\" (長音符), in hiragana with a vowel letter such as う or い. Because 長音 distinguishes meaning, it is a typical source of errors for learners.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"拗音は「きゃ」「しゅ」「ちょ」などの音だ。","romaji":"Yōon wa \"kya\" \"shu\" \"cho\" nado no oto da.","german":"Yōon are sounds such as 「きゃ」, 「しゅ」 and 「ちょ」.","furigana":"ようおん|おと"},{"japanese":"拗音は、小さい「ゃ」「ゅ」「ょ」を使って書く。","romaji":"Yōon wa, chiisai \"ya\" \"yu\" \"yo\" o tsukatte kaku.","german":"Yōon are written using the small 「ゃ」, 「ゅ」 and 「ょ」."},{"japanese":"「びょういん」と「びよういん」は、拗音かどうかで別の言葉になる。","romaji":"\"Byōin\" to \"biyōin\" wa, yōon ka dō ka de betsu no kotoba ni naru.","german":"「びょういん」 and 「びよういん」 are different words depending on whether there is a yōon.","furigana":"ようおん|べつ|ことば"}],"notes":"拗音 are the palatalized syllables written with an i-kana plus a small ゃ・ゅ・ょ: きゃ, しゅ, ちょ, りょ. Although they consist of two characters, they count as one mora. The opposite term is 直音 (syllables with a normal-size kana). There is a risk of confusion when reading with syllable sequences like びよういん \"beauty salon\" versus びょういん \"hospital\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"清音と濁音の区別は日本語の基本だ。","romaji":"Seion to dakuon no kubetsu wa Nihongo no kihon da.","german":"Distinguishing seion and dakuon is a basic point of Japanese.","furigana":"せいおん|だくおん|くべつ|にほんご|きほん"},{"japanese":"「か」に濁点を付けると、清音が濁音の「が」になる。","romaji":"'Ka' ni dakuten o tsukeru to, seion ga dakuon no 'ga' ni naru.","german":"If you add a dakuten to 「か」, the seion becomes the dakuon 「が」.","furigana":"だくてん|つ|せいおん|だくおん"},{"japanese":"連濁では、後ろの語の最初の清音が濁音に変わる。","romaji":"Rendaku de wa, ushiro no go no saisho no seion ga dakuon ni kawaru.","german":"In rendaku, the first seion of the second word changes into a dakuon.","furigana":"れんだく|うし|ご|さいしょ|せいおん|だくおん|か"}],"notes":"清音 are, in Japanese kana teaching, all syllables without dakuten (゛) or handakuten (゜), e.g. か, さ, た, は, but also あ, な, ま etc. It is therefore a writing term and not exactly the same as phonetic \"voiceless\": な and ま are voiced and are nevertheless 清音. The opposite terms are 濁音 (が, ざ, だ, ば) and 半濁音 (ぱ). In 連濁 the first 清音 of the second word becomes a 濁音 (ほん + たな → ほんだな).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"濁音は濁点を付けて表記する。","romaji":"Dakuon wa dakuten o tsukete hyōki suru.","german":"Dakuon are written by adding a dakuten.","furigana":"だくおん|だくてん|つ|ひょうき"},{"japanese":"怪獣の名前には、ゴジラのように濁音で始まるものが多い。","romaji":"Kaijū no namae ni wa, Gojira no yō ni dakuon de hajimaru mono ga ōi.","german":"Many monster names, like Godzilla, begin with a dakuon.","furigana":"かいじゅう|なまえ|だくおん|はじ|おお"},{"japanese":"和語では、語の最初に濁音が来ることはあまりない。","romaji":"Wago de wa, go no saisho ni dakuon ga kuru koto wa amari nai.","german":"In native Japanese words, a dakuon rarely comes at the beginning of a word.","furigana":"わご|ご|さいしょ|だくおん|く"}],"notes":"濁音 are the syllables with dakuten (゛, colloquially てんてん): が, ざ, だ, ば and their series; phonetically they have a voiced consonant. The opposite terms are 清音 (か, さ, た, は) and 半濁音 (ぱ). The character is called 濁点, the sound 濁音. Genuine Japanese words (和語) rarely begin with a 濁音; many Japanese perceive 濁音 as \"heavy, rough\", which is why e.g. monster names often start with them.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"半濁音は「ぱ」行の音を指す。","romaji":"Handakuon wa 'pa'-gyō no oto o sasu.","german":"Handakuon refers to the sounds of the 「ぱ」 row.","furigana":"はんだくおん|ぎょう|おと|さ"},{"japanese":"「は」に丸を付けると、半濁音の「ぱ」になる。","romaji":"'Ha' ni maru o tsukeru to, handakuon no 'pa' ni naru.","german":"If you add a circle to 「は」, it becomes the handakuon 「ぱ」.","furigana":"まる|つ|はんだくおん"},{"japanese":"外来語を除くと、半濁音で始まる言葉は擬音語が多い。","romaji":"Gairaigo o nozoku to, handakuon de hajimaru kotoba wa giongo ga ōi.","german":"Excluding loanwords, many words beginning with a handakuon are onomatopoeia.","furigana":"がいらいご|のぞ|はんだくおん|はじ|ことば|ぎおんご|おお"}],"notes":"半濁音 are only the syllables of the ぱ-row (ぱ, ぴ, ぷ, ぺ, ぽ), written with the small circle 半濁点 (゜, colloquially まる). The name \"half-voiced\" is traditional; phonetically, p is actually voiceless. Originally Japanese words with a 半濁音 at the beginning are almost only onomatopoeia such as ぱらぱら or ぴかぴか; otherwise they occur mainly in loanwords and after っ or ん (いっぱい, さんぽ).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"隣接する音の影響で同化が起きることがある。","romaji":"Rinsetsu suru oto no eikyō de dōka ga okiru koto ga aru.","german":"Assimilation can occur under the influence of adjacent sounds.","furigana":"りんせつ|おと|えいきょう|どうか|お"},{"japanese":"「ん」は後ろの音に同化して、「さんま」では[m]と発音される。","romaji":"'N' wa ushiro no oto ni dōka shite, 'sanma' de wa [m] to hatsuon sareru.","german":"The 「ん」 assimilates to the following sound and is pronounced [m] in 「さんま」.","furigana":"うし|おと|どうか|はつおん"},{"japanese":"移民が現地の社会に同化するには時間がかかる。","romaji":"Imin ga genchi no shakai ni dōka suru ni wa jikan ga kakaru.","german":"It takes time for immigrants to assimilate into local society.","furigana":"いみん|げんち|しゃかい|どうか|じかん"}],"notes":"同化 generally means \"adjustment, assimilation\" and, as a する-verb, is mostly intransitive: 社会に同化する \"to assimilate into society\", plus 同化政策 \"assimilation policy\". In phonetics it denotes the adjustment of a sound to its neighboring sounds, for instance when ん before m/b/p is pronounced as [m]. In biology 同化 stands for anabolism; the opposite term is in each case 異化.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"異化は同じ音が連続するのを避ける現象だ。","romaji":"Ika wa onaji oto ga renzoku suru no o sakeru genshō da.","german":"Dissimilation is a phenomenon in which the succession of the same sound is avoided.","furigana":"いか|おな|おと|れんぞく|さ|げんしょう"},{"japanese":"体の中では同化と異化が絶えず行われている。","romaji":"Karada no naka de wa dōka to ika ga taezu okonawarete iru.","german":"Inside the body, assimilation and catabolism are constantly taking place.","furigana":"からだ|なか|どうか|いか|た|おこな"},{"japanese":"ブレヒトの演劇では異化効果が重視される。","romaji":"Burehito no engeki de wa ika kōka ga jūshi sareru.","german":"In Brecht's theater, the alienation effect is emphasized.","furigana":"えんげき|いかこうか|じゅうし"}],"notes":"異化 is the opposite term to 同化. In phonetics it denotes dissimilation, i.e. the becoming unlike of identical or similar sounds. In biology 異化 means catabolism (breakdown); 同化 and 異化 together make up metabolism. In art and literary theory 異化 stands for \"defamiliarization\", known from 異化効果 (Brecht's alienation effect). The word is technical throughout.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"時代小説には綿密な考証が必要だ。","romaji":"Jidai shōsetsu ni wa menmitsu na kōshō ga hitsuyō da.","german":"Historical novels require meticulous historical research.","furigana":"じだいしょうせつ|めんみつ|こうしょう|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"このドラマの時代考証は大学の先生が担当している。","romaji":"Kono dorama no jidai kōshō wa daigaku no sensei ga tantō shite iru.","german":"The period research for this drama is handled by a university professor.","furigana":"じだいこうしょう|だいがく|せんせい|たんとう"},{"japanese":"古い絵巻をもとに、当時の衣装を考証した。","romaji":"Furui emaki o moto ni, tōji no ishō o kōshō shita.","german":"Based on an old picture scroll, I researched the clothing of the time.","furigana":"ふる|えまき|とうじ|いしょう|こうしょう"}],"notes":"考証 is the investigation of historical matters on the basis of evidence such as old documents, pictures or objects; as a する-verb, transitive: 衣装を考証する. Very common is 時代考証, the historical consultation for films, series and historical novels. In contrast to 検証 (\"verification\" in general), 考証 almost always relates to history and sources. Register: written, technical.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"諸本を比較して最も正確な本文を確定する。","romaji":"Shohon o hikaku shite mottomo seikaku na honbun o kakutei suru.","german":"Compare the various texts to establish the most accurate text.","furigana":"しょほん|ひかく|もっと|せいかく|ほんぶん|かくてい"},{"japanese":"平家物語には多くの諸本が伝わっている。","romaji":"Heike Monogatari ni wa ōku no shohon ga tsutawatte iru.","german":"Many different texts of the Heike Monogatari have been handed down.","furigana":"へいけものがたり|おお|しょほん|つた"},{"japanese":"諸本の系統を整理するのに十年かかった。","romaji":"Shohon no keitō o seiri suru no ni jūnen kakatta.","german":"It took ten years to sort out the lineage of the text versions.","furigana":"しょほん|けいとう|せいり|じゅうねん"}],"notes":"諸本 is a word of textual philology: all the transmitted manuscripts, prints and editions of a particular work taken together, e.g. 平家物語の諸本. 諸 means \"various, all\", 本 here \"copy, version\". Typical combinations are 諸本を比較する, 諸本を校合する and 諸本の系統 (lines of transmission). A single deviating version is called 異本, a manuscript 写本.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"異なる写本を校合して異同を調べた。","romaji":"Kotonaru shahon o kyōgō shite idō o shirabeta.","german":"I collated different manuscripts and examined the differences.","furigana":"こと|しゃほん|きょうごう|いどう|しら"},{"japanese":"原本と校合した結果、三か所の誤りが見つかった。","romaji":"Genpon to kyōgō shita kekka, sankasho no ayamari ga mitsukatta.","german":"Collating it with the original turned up three errors.","furigana":"げんぽん|きょうごう|けっか|さん|しょ|あやま|み"},{"japanese":"翻刻する前に、必ず複数の本と校合することにしている。","romaji":"Honkoku suru mae ni, kanarazu fukusū no hon to kyōgō suru koto ni shite iru.","german":"Before transcribing a text, I make it a rule to collate it with several copies.","furigana":"ほんこく|まえ|かなら|ふくすう|ほん|きょうごう"}],"notes":"校合 (きょうごう, more rarely こうごう) is the comparison of a text with another version or the original in order to determine differences (異同) and errors; as a する-verb, transitive: 写本を校合する. It is a word of philology and publishing. Related are 校正 (\"proofreading\" of printer's proofs) and 校訂 (producing a critically cleaned text on the basis of the comparison).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"奥付には出版社や発行日が記されている。","romaji":"Okuzuke ni wa shuppansha ya hakkōbi ga shirusarete iru.","german":"The publisher and the publication date are noted in the colophon.","furigana":"おくづけ|しゅっぱんしゃ|はっこうび|しる"},{"japanese":"引用するときは、奥付で発行年を確認してください。","romaji":"In'yō suru toki wa, okuzuke de hakkōnen o kakunin shite kudasai.","german":"When citing, please check the year of publication on the colophon.","furigana":"いんよう|おくづけ|はっこうねん|かくにん"},{"japanese":"奥付を見ると、この本はもう第十刷だった。","romaji":"Okuzuke o miru to, kono hon wa mō dai-jissatsu datta.","german":"Looking at the colophon, this book was already in its tenth printing.","furigana":"おくづけ|み|ほん|だいじっさつ"}],"notes":"奥付 is the page at the end of a Japanese book with the bibliographic details: title, author, publisher, publication date, and edition and printing (e.g. 初版第三刷). It roughly corresponds to the imprint page, but it comes at the back (奥 means \"far back, inside\"). For references, you look up the publication year in the 奥付. Typical phrases: 奥付を見る, 奥付に記載されている.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"美しい装丁の本は手に取る喜びがある。","romaji":"Utsukushii sōtei no hon wa te ni toru yorokobi ga aru.","german":"A beautifully designed book is a joy to hold.","furigana":"うつく|そうてい|ほん|て|と|よろこ"},{"japanese":"この本の装丁は有名なデザイナーが手がけた。","romaji":"Kono hon no sōtei wa yūmei na dezainā ga tegaketa.","german":"The design of this book was done by a famous designer.","furigana":"ほん|そうてい|ゆうめい|て"},{"japanese":"文庫化にあたって、装丁が新しくなった。","romaji":"Bunkoka ni atatte, sōtei ga atarashiku natta.","german":"The book got a new design when it was reissued as a paperback.","furigana":"ぶんこか|そうてい|あたら"}],"notes":"装丁 is the outer design of a book: binding, cover, dust jacket, typography and materials. It is also written 装幀 and 装釘; the designer is called 装丁家. As a transitive する-verb: 本を装丁する. It concerns the book as an object, not its content; for pictures within the text one says 挿絵, and for cover pictures 表紙絵 or カバーイラスト.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"挿絵が物語の世界観を豊かにしている。","romaji":"Sashie ga monogatari no sekaikan o yutaka ni shite iru.","german":"The illustrations enrich the world of the story.","furigana":"さしえ|ものがたり|せかいかん|ゆた"},{"japanese":"子どもの頃は、挿絵の多い本ばかり読んでいた。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro wa, sashie no ōi hon bakari yonde ita.","german":"As a child, I only read books with lots of illustrations.","furigana":"こ|ころ|さしえ|おお|ほん|よ"},{"japanese":"彼は新聞の連載小説に挿絵を描いている。","romaji":"Kare wa shinbun no rensai shōsetsu ni sashie o kaite iru.","german":"He draws the illustrations for a serialized novel in the newspaper.","furigana":"かれ|しんぶん|れんさいしょうせつ|さしえ|か"}],"notes":"挿絵 are pictures inserted into a text, in books, newspaper serials or textbooks; 挿 means \"to insert\". Typical phrases: 挿絵を描く, 挿絵入りの本, 挿絵画家. The more everyday イラスト today is broader and means any illustration; 挿絵 sounds somewhat more traditional and stresses the connection to the text. A picture at the front of a book, on the other hand, is called 口絵.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"口絵に著者の肖像画が掲載されていた。","romaji":"Kuchie ni chosha no shōzōga ga keisai sarete ita.","german":"A portrait of the author was printed in the frontispiece.","furigana":"くちえ|ちょしゃ|しょうぞうが|けいさい"},{"japanese":"初版本には色鮮やかな口絵が付いていた。","romaji":"Shohanbon ni wa iroazayaka na kuchie ga tsuite ita.","german":"The first edition came with a brightly colored frontispiece.","furigana":"しょはんぼん|いろあざ|くちえ|つ"},{"japanese":"このライトノベルは、カラーの口絵で登場人物を紹介している。","romaji":"Kono raito noberu wa, karā no kuchie de tōjō jinbutsu o shōkai shite iru.","german":"This light novel introduces its characters on color pages at the start of the book.","furigana":"くちえ|とうじょうじんぶつ|しょうかい"}],"notes":"口絵 is the picture or the picture pages at the very front of a book or magazine, often printed in color on better paper; the frontispiece. 口 here stands for \"beginning, entrance\". By contrast, 挿絵 are the illustrations within the text. Typical are カラー口絵 and 口絵に載る; in light novels, color 口絵 showing the characters are common.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"旧字体は正字とも呼ばれる。","romaji":"Kyū-jitai wa seiji to mo yobareru.","german":"The old character forms are also called 正字.","furigana":"きゅうじたい|せいじ|よ"},{"japanese":"テストでは略字ではなく正字で書きなさい。","romaji":"Tesuto de wa ryakuji de wa naku seiji de kakinasai.","german":"In the test, write the correct characters, not abbreviated forms.","furigana":"りゃくじ|せいじ|か"},{"japanese":"この辞書は、見出しの漢字に正字を併記している。","romaji":"Kono jisho wa, midashi no kanji ni seiji o heiki shite iru.","german":"This dictionary also gives the traditional form for the kanji in its headwords.","furigana":"じしょ|みだ|かんじ|せいじ|へいき"}],"notes":"正字 is the \"correct, standard character\", in contrast to 誤字 (wrong character), 俗字 (popular form) and 略字 (abbreviated form). In Japanese usage, 正字 often means the traditional, unsimplified forms based on the Kangxi dictionary, i.e. in practice the 旧字体 such as 國 or 學. It follows that 正字 specifically does not refer to the standard form in common use today (新字体).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"略字は手書きの際に便利だ。","romaji":"Ryakuji wa tegaki no sai ni benri da.","german":"Abbreviated characters are handy when writing by hand.","furigana":"りゃくじ|てが|さい|べんり"},{"japanese":"正式な書類には略字を使わないでください。","romaji":"Seishiki na shorui ni wa ryakuji o tsukawanaide kudasai.","german":"Please do not use abbreviated forms in official documents.","furigana":"せいしき|しょるい|りゃくじ|つか"},{"japanese":"祖父のメモは略字だらけで、とても読みにくい。","romaji":"Sofu no memo wa ryakuji darake de, totemo yominikui.","german":"Grandfather's notes are full of abbreviated characters and very hard to read.","furigana":"そふ|りゃくじ|よ"}],"notes":"略字 is a simplified, abbreviated form of a kanji, as it appears mainly in handwriting (e.g. the handwritten 门 for 門). Many of today's standard forms (新字体) developed from such 略字. In official documents and exams, non-standard 略字 are not considered correct; the opposite term is 正字. Rarely, 略字 also means \"abbreviation, acronym\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"戦前の書籍は旧字体で印刷されている。","romaji":"Senzen no shoseki wa kyū jitai de insatsu sarete iru.","german":"Prewar books are printed in the old character forms.","furigana":"せんぜん|しょせき|きゅうじたい|いんさつ"},{"japanese":"「国」の旧字体は「國」だ。","romaji":"'Kuni' no kyū jitai wa 'kuni' da.","german":"The old form of 「国」 is 「國」.","furigana":"くに|きゅうじたい|"},{"japanese":"斎藤さんの中には、名字に旧字体の「齋」を使う人もいる。","romaji":"Saitō-san no naka ni wa, myōji ni kyū jitai no 'sai' o tsukau hito mo iru.","german":"Some people named Saitō use the old form 「齋」 in their family name.","furigana":"さいとう|なか|みょうじ|きゅうじたい||つか|ひと"}],"notes":"旧字体 are the kanji forms that were standard in Japan until the script reform after World War II, e.g. 國 (now 国), 學 (学) or 體 (体). The opposite term is 新字体. You encounter them in prewar prints, on old signs and in family names (齋藤, 髙橋) that many people deliberately keep. They are not the same as traditional Chinese characters, but overlap with them a great deal.","levelNote":"Compound: 旧 (N3) + 字体 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"戦後の国語改革で新字体が導入された。","romaji":"Sengo no kokugo kaikaku de shin jitai ga dōnyū sareta.","german":"The new character forms were introduced by the postwar language reform.","furigana":"せんご|こくごかいかく|しんじたい|どうにゅう"},{"japanese":"「學」は新字体では「学」と書く。","romaji":"'Gaku' wa shin jitai de wa 'gaku' to kaku.","german":"The character 「學」 is written 「学」 in the new form.","furigana":"|しんじたい|がく|か"},{"japanese":"中国の簡体字と日本の新字体は、形が違うことが多い。","romaji":"Chūgoku no kantaiji to Nihon no shin jitai wa, katachi ga chigau koto ga ōi.","german":"Chinese simplified characters and Japanese shinjitai often look different.","furigana":"ちゅうごく|かんたいじ|にほん|しんじたい|かたち|ちが|おお"}],"notes":"新字体 are the simplified kanji forms introduced after the war with the 当用漢字 list and its table of character shapes (1949), and standard in Japan today, e.g. 学 instead of 學. The opposite term is 旧字体. Do not confuse them with Chinese simplified characters (簡体字): the two reforms often simplified differently, e.g. Japanese 広 versus Chinese 广.","levelNote":"Compound: 新 (N1) + 字体 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"文献学は古典テキストの正確な解読を目指す。","romaji":"Bunkengaku wa koten tekisuto no seikaku na kaidoku o mezasu.","german":"Die Philologie strebt die genaue Entzifferung klassischer Texte an.","furigana":"ぶんけんがく|こてん|せいかく|かいどく|めざ"},{"japanese":"彼女はドイツで古典文献学を学んだ。","romaji":"Kanojo wa Doitsu de koten bunkengaku o mananda.","german":"Sie hat in Deutschland Klassische Philologie studiert.","furigana":"かのじょ|こてんぶんけんがく|まな"},{"japanese":"文献学の方法で、写本の成立年代を推定した。","romaji":"Bunkengaku no hōhō de, shahon no seiritsu nendai o suitei shita.","german":"Mit philologischen Methoden wurde die Entstehungszeit der Handschrift bestimmt.","furigana":"ぶんけんがく|ほうほう|しゃほん|せいりつねんだい|すいてい"}],"notes":"文献学 ist die Philologie im Sinne der Textwissenschaft: das Sammeln, Vergleichen, Datieren und kritische Herausgeben schriftlicher Quellen, um den ursprünglichen Text und seine Bedeutung zu sichern. Typische Verbindungen sind 古典文献学 (klassische Philologie) und 文献学的な研究. Im Unterschied zu 言語学 („Linguistik“) stehen nicht Sprachsysteme, sondern Texte und ihre Überlieferung im Mittelpunkt.","levelNote":"Compound: 文献 (N2) + 学 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"国文学を専攻して平安文学を研究した。","romaji":"Kokubungaku o senkō shite Heian bungaku o kenkyū shita.","german":"I majored in Japanese literature and studied Heian literature.","furigana":"こくぶんがく|せんこう|へいあんぶんがく|けんきゅう"},{"japanese":"祖父は大学で国文学を教えていた。","romaji":"Sofu wa daigaku de kokubungaku o oshiete ita.","german":"My grandfather taught Japanese literature at university.","furigana":"そふ|だいがく|こくぶんがく|おし"},{"japanese":"最近は国文学の代わりに日本文学という名称がよく使われる。","romaji":"Saikin wa kokubungaku no kawari ni Nihon bungaku to iu meishō ga yoku tsukawareru.","german":"Recently, the name 日本文学 is often used instead of 国文学.","furigana":"さいきん|こくぶんがく|か|にほんぶんがく|めいしょう|つか"}],"notes":"国文学 means both \"Japanese literature\" as a subject matter and the field of study \"Japanese literary studies\" (国文学科, 国文学を専攻する). As in 国語, 国 means \"one's own country\" from the Japanese point of view. Counterparts are 外国文学 and, for the language, 国語学. As with 日本語学, some universities now replace the name with 日本文学.","levelNote":"Compound: 国 (N5) + 文学 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"比較文学の観点から日本とドイツの文学を分析した。","romaji":"Hikaku bungaku no kanten kara Nihon to Doitsu no bungaku o bunseki shita.","german":"Aus der Perspektive der vergleichenden Literaturwissenschaft analysierte ich japanische und deutsche Literatur.","furigana":"ひかくぶんがく|かんてん|にほん|ぶんがく|ぶんせき"},{"japanese":"大学院で比較文学を専攻している。","romaji":"Daigakuin de hikaku bungaku o senkō shite iru.","german":"Ich studiere im Master vergleichende Literaturwissenschaft.","furigana":"だいがくいん|ひかくぶんがく|せんこう"},{"japanese":"比較文学の授業で、夏目漱石と英文学の関係を学んだ。","romaji":"Hikaku bungaku no jugyō de, Natsume Sōseki to eibungaku no kankei o mananda.","german":"In einem Seminar zur Komparatistik ging es um Natsume Sōseki und die englische Literatur.","furigana":"ひかくぶんがく|じゅぎょう|なつめそうせき|えいぶんがく|かんけい|まな"}],"notes":"比較文学 ist die „vergleichende Literaturwissenschaft“: Sie untersucht Literatur über Sprach- und Ländergrenzen hinweg, etwa Einflüsse, Übersetzungen und Motivwanderungen. Typisch sind 比較文学を専攻する, 比較文学の観点から und 比較文学者. Abzugrenzen ist es von 国文学 (japanische Literatur) und 外国文学 (Literatur eines einzelnen fremden Landes). Register: akademisch.","levelNote":"Compound: 比較 (N3) + 文学 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"言語学概論の授業で基礎を学んだ。","romaji":"Gengogaku gairon no jugyō de kiso o mananda.","german":"I learned the basics in the introduction to linguistics course.","furigana":"げんごがくがいろん|じゅぎょう|きそ|まな"},{"japanese":"この本は心理学の概論として、とても分かりやすい。","romaji":"Kono hon wa shinrigaku no gairon to shite, totemo wakariyasui.","german":"As an overview of psychology, this book is very easy to understand.","furigana":"ほん|しんりがく|がいろん|わ"},{"japanese":"まず問題の概論を述べてから、具体例を挙げます。","romaji":"Mazu mondai no gairon o nobete kara, gutairei o agemasu.","german":"I will first give an overview of the problem and then give specific examples.","furigana":"もんだい|がいろん|の|ぐたいれい|あ"}],"notes":"概論 is an introductory overall survey of a subject and very often appears as a suffix in course and book titles: 言語学概論, 経済学概論. Unlike 入門 (\"a start for beginners\"), 概論 stresses the systematic overview; a 序論, by contrast, is the introduction to a single text. In curricula, the 概論 is often followed by the 各論. Use as a する-verb is rare.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"概論を終えてから各論に進む。","romaji":"Gairon o oete kara kakuron ni susumu.","german":"After finishing the overview, we move on to the specific topics.","furigana":"がいろん|お|かくろん|すす"},{"japanese":"改革案には総論賛成、各論反対という人が多い。","romaji":"Kaikakuan ni wa sōron sansei, kakuron hantai to iu hito ga ōi.","german":"Many people are in favor of reforms in general but against each concrete measure.","furigana":"かいかくあん|そうろんさんせい|かくろんはんたい|ひと|おお"},{"japanese":"医学の教科書は総論と各論に分かれている。","romaji":"Igaku no kyōkasho wa sōron to kakuron ni wakarete iru.","german":"Medical textbooks are divided into a general part and a specific part.","furigana":"いがく|きょうかしょ|そうろん|かくろん|わ"}],"notes":"各論 is the individual treatment or the \"special part\" of a topic, point by point; the opposite term is 総論 (\"general part\"). Textbooks (e.g. law, medicine) are often divided into 総論 and 各論. A very well-known phrase is 総論賛成、各論反対: to be in favor in principle but against every concrete implementation. Typical are 各論に入る and 各論で議論する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"総論では研究の全体像を示す。","romaji":"Sōron de wa kenkyū no zentai-zō o shimesu.","german":"The general part presents the overall picture of the research.","furigana":"そうろん|けんきゅう|ぜんたいぞう|しめ"},{"japanese":"総論としては賛成だが、細かい点には問題がある。","romaji":"Sōron to shite wa sansei da ga, komakai ten ni wa mondai ga aru.","german":"I agree in general, but there are problems in the details.","furigana":"そうろん|さんせい|こま|てん|もんだい"},{"japanese":"このレポートは総論ばかりで、具体的な提案がない。","romaji":"Kono repōto wa sōron bakari de, gutaiteki na teian ga nai.","german":"This report stays at the general level and contains no concrete proposals.","furigana":"そうろん|ぐたいてき|ていあん"}],"notes":"総論 is the \"general part\" or overall treatment of a topic, as opposed to 各論, the individual questions. In specialist books the opening chapter is often called 総論 (民法総論, 総論部分). In discussions, 総論 is also used somewhat critically for statements that stay general, especially in 総論賛成、各論反対. Register: formal, administration, scholarship.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"序論で研究の目的と方法を述べる。","romaji":"Joron de kenkyū no mokuteki to hōhō o noberu.","german":"The aim and method of the research are stated in the introduction.","furigana":"じょろん|けんきゅう|もくてき|ほうほう|の"},{"japanese":"論文は序論、本論、結論の三つの部分で構成する。","romaji":"Ronbun wa joron, honron, ketsuron no mittsu no bubun de kōsei suru.","german":"A thesis is structured in three parts: introduction, main body and conclusion.","furigana":"ろんぶん|じょろん|ほんろん|けつろん|みっ|ぶぶん|こうせい"},{"japanese":"序論が長すぎて、なかなか本題に入らない。","romaji":"Joron ga nagasugite, nakanaka hondai ni hairanai.","german":"The introduction is so long that it hardly gets to the point.","furigana":"じょろん|なが|ほんだい|はい"}],"notes":"序論 is the introduction of a scholarly work or treatise, in which the research question, aim and method are set out; the classic structure is 序論・本論・結論. An author's foreword is more often called 序文 or まえがき, and in term papers はじめに is also common. 概論, on the other hand, is an overview of a whole field. Register: academic, written.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"論文の抄録は三百字以内にまとめる。","romaji":"Ronbun no shōroku wa sanbyaku-ji inai ni matomeru.","german":"The abstract of the paper is to be summarized in no more than 300 characters.","furigana":"ろんぶん|しょうろく|さんびゃくじいない"},{"japanese":"学会の抄録集に、発表の要旨が載っている。","romaji":"Gakkai no shōrokushū ni, happyō no yōshi ga notte iru.","german":"The conference's book of abstracts contains the summaries of the presentations.","furigana":"がっかい|しょうろくしゅう|はっぴょう|ようし|の"},{"japanese":"作家の日記の一部を抄録して雑誌に載せた。","romaji":"Sakka no nikki no ichibu o shōroku shite zasshi ni noseta.","german":"Parts of the writer's diary were excerpted and published in a magazine.","furigana":"さっか|にっき|いちぶ|しょうろく|ざっし|の"}],"notes":"抄録 has two uses: the short version of an essay or talk (abstract), e.g. 論文の抄録 or 学会の抄録集, and copying or reproducing a text in excerpts, as a transitive する-verb: 日記を抄録する. In everyday speech one more often says 要約 or 要旨, and in the natural and medical sciences also アブストラクト. Register: technical, written.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"論文末尾に参考文献を一覧にして載せる。","romaji":"Ronbun matsubi ni sankō bunken o ichiran ni shite noseru.","german":"A list of references is placed at the end of the paper.","furigana":"ろんぶんまつび|さんこうぶんけん|いちらん|の"},{"japanese":"レポートには、参考文献を必ず明記すること。","romaji":"Repōto ni wa, sankō bunken o kanarazu meiki suru koto.","german":"Be sure to state your references in the report.","furigana":"さんこうぶんけん|かなら|めいき"},{"japanese":"参考文献の書き方は分野によって少しずつ違う。","romaji":"Sankō bunken no kakikata wa bun'ya ni yotte sukoshi zutsu chigau.","german":"How to format references differs slightly depending on the field.","furigana":"さんこうぶんけん|か|かた|ぶんや|すこ|ちが"}],"notes":"参考文献 are the sources one has consulted for a piece of work, and also the list of them at the end (bibliography). Typical: 参考文献を挙げる, 参考文献一覧, 参考文献リスト. In the narrower sense, 引用文献 are only the works quoted directly, while 参考文献 also includes literature that was merely consulted. Register: academic, but also common in school presentations.","levelNote":"Compound: 参考 (N3) + 文献 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"学際的な研究が新しい知見を生むことがある。","romaji":"Gakusai-teki na kenkyū ga atarashii chiken o umu koto ga aru.","german":"Interdisciplinary research can sometimes produce new insights.","furigana":"がくさいてき|けんきゅう|あたら|ちけん|う"},{"japanese":"環境問題には学際的に取り組む必要がある。","romaji":"Kankyō mondai ni wa gakusai-teki ni torikumu hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"Environmental problems need to be tackled in an interdisciplinary way.","furigana":"かんきょうもんだい|がくさいてき|と|く|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"来年、大学に学際的な新しい学部ができる。","romaji":"Rainen, daigaku ni gakusai-teki na atarashii gakubu ga dekiru.","german":"Next year, the university will get a new interdisciplinary faculty.","furigana":"らいねん|だいがく|がくさいてき|あたら|がくぶ"}],"notes":"学際的 is a な-adjective: 学際的な研究, adverbially 学際的に取り組む. 学際 literally means \"between the sciences\" (際 = border, as in 国際). Similar in meaning but more colloquial are 分野横断的 or 分野を越えた; 総合的, by contrast, means more \"comprehensive\". Typical of university and research policy, e.g. 学際的なプロジェクト.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"博覧強記の彼はどんな質問にも答えられる。","romaji":"Hakuran kyōki no kare wa donna shitsumon ni mo kotaerareru.","german":"With his encyclopedic knowledge, he can answer any question.","furigana":"はくらんきょうき|かれ|しつもん|こた"},{"japanese":"博覧強記で知られる教授の講義は、話題が次々に広がる。","romaji":"Hakuran kyōki de shirareru kyōju no kōgi wa, wadai ga tsugitsugi ni hirogaru.","german":"In the lectures of the professor, who is known for his vast learning, the topics keep branching out one after another.","furigana":"はくらんきょうき|し|きょうじゅ|こうぎ|わだい|つぎつぎ|ひろ"},{"japanese":"祖父は博覧強記で、歴史の年号を何でも覚えていた。","romaji":"Sofu wa hakuran kyōki de, rekishi no nengō o nan demo oboete ita.","german":"My grandfather was extremely well read and remembered every date in history.","furigana":"そふ|はくらんきょうき|れきし|ねんごう|なん|おぼ"}],"notes":"博覧強記 is made up of 博覧 \"having read a lot, widely read\" and 強記 \"a strong memory\"; it means someone who has read a great deal and remembers everything, that is, enormous, encyclopedic knowledge. It is a laudatory, rather literary yojijukugo, typically used as 博覧強記の人, 博覧強記で知られる or 博覧強記を誇る, mostly about scholars and writers.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"読み書きは全ての学びの土台である。","romaji":"Yomikaki wa subete no manabi no dodai de aru.","german":"Lesen und Schreiben sind das Fundament allen Lernens.","furigana":"よ|か|すべ|まな|どだい"},{"japanese":"昔は読み書きそろばんが教育の基本とされた。","romaji":"Mukashi wa yomikaki soroban ga kyōiku no kihon to sareta.","german":"Früher galten Lesen, Schreiben und Rechnen als Grundlage der Bildung.","furigana":"むかし|よ|か|きょういく|きほん"},{"japanese":"日本語の会話はできるが、漢字の読み書きはまだ苦手だ。","romaji":"Nihongo no kaiwa wa dekiru ga, kanji no yomikaki wa mada nigate da.","german":"Ich kann mich auf Japanisch unterhalten, aber Kanji lesen und schreiben fällt mir noch schwer.","furigana":"にほんご|かいわ|かんじ|よ|か|にがて"}],"notes":"読み書き heißt „Lesen und Schreiben“ als Grundfähigkeit: 読み書きができる, 読み書きが苦手, 漢字の読み書き. Die feste Wendung 読み書きそろばん (wörtlich „Lesen, Schreiben, Abakus“) entspricht dem deutschen „Lesen, Schreiben, Rechnen“. Das fachliche Wort für Alphabetisierung bzw. Lese- und Schreibkompetenz ist 識字 (識字率). Register: neutral.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"教え子が立派に成長した姿を見て感動した。","romaji":"Oshiego ga rippa ni seichō shita sugata o mite kandō shita.","german":"I was moved to see how splendidly my former student had grown up.","furigana":"おし|ご|りっぱ|せいちょう|すがた|み|かんどう"},{"japanese":"先生の退職祝いに、教え子たちが全国から集まった。","romaji":"Sensei no taishoku iwai ni, oshiego-tachi ga zenkoku kara atsumatta.","german":"For the teacher's retirement celebration, his former students gathered from all over the country.","furigana":"せんせい|たいしょくいわ|おし|ご|ぜんこく|あつ"},{"japanese":"彼は私の大学時代の教え子です。","romaji":"Kare wa watashi no daigaku jidai no oshiego desu.","german":"He was a student of mine in my university days.","furigana":"かれ|わたし|だいがくじだい|おし|ご"}],"notes":"教え子 is a pupil or student seen from the teacher's point of view, especially a former one: 私の教え子, 昔の教え子. Unlike 生徒 or 学生, it expresses the personal relationship with one's own teacher; 弟子, by contrast, stresses training under a master (art, craft, martial arts). The word always refers to a particular teacher: a teacher says 私の教え子, and about oneself one says 田中先生の教え子です.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"恩師の学恩に報いるために研究を続けている。","romaji":"Onshi no gakuon ni mukuiru tame ni kenkyū o tsuzukete iru.","german":"I continue my research to repay the debt I owe my mentor.","furigana":"おんし|がくおん|むく|けんきゅう|つづ"},{"japanese":"大学院時代の指導教員から受けた学恩は計り知れない。","romaji":"Daigakuin jidai no shidō kyōin kara uketa gakuon wa hakarishirenai.","german":"What I owe to my supervisor from my graduate school days is immeasurable.","furigana":"だいがくいんじだい|しどうきょういん|う|がくおん|はか|し"},{"japanese":"本書を、多大な学恩を受けた故田中教授に捧げる。","romaji":"Honsho o, tadai na gakuon o uketa ko-Tanaka kyōju ni sasageru.","german":"This book is dedicated to the late Professor Tanaka, to whom I owe an immense scholarly debt.","furigana":"ほんしょ|ただい|がくおん|う|こたなかきょうじゅ|ささ"}],"notes":"学恩 is the debt of gratitude owed to a teacher or scholar for the instruction and scholarly support received, that is, the kindness shown, not the feeling of gratitude itself. It is an elevated word from the academic world, typical in forewords and obituaries: 学恩を受ける, 学恩に報いる, 学恩に感謝する. Related are 恩師 (a revered teacher) and 師恩.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"校閲を経てようやく出版の運びとなった。","romaji":"Kōetsu o hete yōyaku shuppan no hakobi to natta.","german":"The book was finally published after going through editorial review.","furigana":"こうえつ|へ|しゅっぱん|はこ"},{"japanese":"彼女は新聞社の校閲部で、記事の事実確認をしている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa shinbunsha no kōetsubu de, kiji no jijitsu kakunin o shite iru.","german":"She works in the checking department of a newspaper, verifying the facts in articles.","furigana":"かのじょ|しんぶんしゃ|こうえつぶ|きじ|じじつかくにん"},{"japanese":"専門家に訳文を校閲してもらい、用語を直した。","romaji":"Senmonka ni yakubun o kōetsu shite morai, yōgo o naoshita.","german":"I had a specialist review the translation and corrected the terminology.","furigana":"せんもんか|やくぶん|こうえつ|ようご|なお"}],"notes":"校閲 is the review of a manuscript's content before publication: facts, names, figures, contradictions and wording. It is distinguished from 校正, proofreading of the printer's proofs for typos and typesetting errors. Newspapers and publishers have their own 校閲部; as a transitive する-verb: 原稿を校閲する. If a professional authority stands behind a work and is responsible for its content, one more often speaks of 監修.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この本の初版は既に絶版になっている。","romaji":"Kono hon no shohan wa sude ni zeppan ni natte iru.","german":"The first edition of this book is already out of print.","furigana":"ほん|しょはん|すで|ぜっぱん"},{"japanese":"古本屋で漱石の初版本を見つけた。","romaji":"Furuhon'ya de Sōseki no shohanbon o mitsuketa.","german":"I found a first edition of Sōseki at a secondhand bookshop.","furigana":"ふるほんや|そうせき|しょはんぼん|み"},{"japanese":"初版の誤りは第二版で訂正された。","romaji":"Shohan no ayamari wa dai-niban de teisei sareta.","german":"The errors of the first edition were corrected in the second edition.","furigana":"しょはん|あやま|だいにばん|ていせい"}],"notes":"初版 is the first edition of a book. In Japan one distinguishes 版 (edition; with changes to the content: 第二版, 改訂版) and 刷 (printing without changes): 初版第一刷 is the very first printing. 初版本 is a copy of the first edition, often a collector's item. Counterparts are 重版 or 増刷 (reprint) and 再版 (new edition).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"発売後すぐに重版が決まった。","romaji":"Hatsubai-go sugu ni jūhan ga kimatta.","german":"A reprint was decided right after it went on sale.","furigana":"はつばいご|じゅうはん|き"},{"japanese":"テレビで紹介されて、何度も重版がかかった。","romaji":"Terebi de shōkai sarete, nando mo jūhan ga kakatta.","german":"After it was featured on TV, it went back to press many times.","furigana":"しょうかい|なんど|じゅうはん"},{"japanese":"重版の際に、いくつかの誤植を直した。","romaji":"Jūhan no sai ni, ikutsu ka no goshoku o naoshita.","german":"Several misprints were corrected at the time of the reprint.","furigana":"じゅうはん|さい|ごしょく|なお"}],"notes":"重版 is a further printing of a book with (almost) unchanged content, that is, a reprint; a synonym is 増刷. A 重版 is a sign of good sales, typically: 重版が決まる, 重版がかかる, 重版出来 (じゅうはんしゅったい, \"reprint has come out\"). As a transitive する-verb: 本を重版する. If the content is revised, one speaks of 改訂版, not 重版.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"増補改訂版が新たに出版された。","romaji":"Zōho kaitei-ban ga arata ni shuppan sareta.","german":"A supplemented and revised edition has been newly published.","furigana":"ぞうほかいていばん|あら|しゅっぱん"},{"japanese":"新しい資料を加えて、十年前の本を増補した。","romaji":"Atarashii shiryō o kuwaete, jūnen mae no hon o zōho shita.","german":"New material was added to expand the ten-year-old book.","furigana":"あたら|しりょう|くわ|じゅうねんまえ|ほん|ぞうほ"},{"japanese":"増補版には、二つの章が書き加えられている。","romaji":"Zōhoban ni wa, futatsu no shō ga kakikuwaerarete iru.","german":"Two chapters have been added to the enlarged edition.","furigana":"ぞうほばん|ふた|しょう|か|くわ"}],"notes":"増補 means adding content to an existing work, supplementing and expanding it; as a transitive する-verb: 旧著を増補する. It is most often found in book titles such as 増補版 and 増補改訂版 (\"enlarged and revised edition\"). 改訂 means revising what exists, 増補 means adding to it; a mere reprint is 重版.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"教科書の改訂作業が進められている。","romaji":"Kyōkasho no kaitei sagyō ga susumerarete iru.","german":"Work on revising the textbook is under way.","furigana":"きょうかしょ|かいていさぎょう|すす"},{"japanese":"その辞書の改訂版が十年ぶりに出た。","romaji":"Sono jisho no kaiteiban ga jūnen-buri ni deta.","german":"A revised edition of that dictionary has come out for the first time in ten years.","furigana":"じしょ|かいていばん|じゅうねん|で"},{"japanese":"古いデータを改訂して、マニュアルを新しくした。","romaji":"Furui dēta o kaitei shite, manyuaru o atarashiku shita.","german":"We revised the outdated data and renewed the manual.","furigana":"ふる|かいてい|あたら"}],"notes":"改訂 is the revision of a text, such as a book, textbook or dictionary: 改訂版, 改訂する (transitive), 教科書の改訂. Careful, homophone: 改定 (same reading) means changing rules, prices or contracts (料金の改定, 規則の改定). Rule of thumb: texts are 改訂, stipulations are 改定. 増補 refers specifically to adding content.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"辞書の語釈を参考にして正確な意味を調べた。","romaji":"Jisho no goshaku o sankō ni shite seikaku na imi o shirabeta.","german":"I looked up the exact meaning by consulting the definition in the dictionary.","furigana":"じしょ|ごしゃく|さんこう|せいかく|いみ|しら"},{"japanese":"この国語辞典は、語釈が分かりやすいと評判だ。","romaji":"Kono kokugo jiten wa, goshaku ga wakariyasui to hyōban da.","german":"This Japanese dictionary has a reputation for easy-to-understand definitions.","furigana":"こくごじてん|ごしゃく|わ|ひょうばん"},{"japanese":"古文の授業では、まず難しい語の語釈を確認する。","romaji":"Kobun no jugyō de wa, mazu muzukashii go no goshaku o kakunin suru.","german":"In classical Japanese class, we first go over the explanations of the difficult words.","furigana":"こぶん|じゅぎょう|むずか|ご|ごしゃく|かくにん"}],"notes":"語釈 is the explanation of a word's meaning, above all the definition text in a dictionary or the word explanation in an annotated edition of classical texts. Typical: 辞書の語釈, 語釈が分かりやすい, 語釈を付ける. 語義 is the word's meaning itself, 語釈 is its explanation; 注釈 is more general, any note on a text.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"時代とともに語義が変化することがある。","romaji":"Jidai to tomo ni gogi ga henka suru koto ga aru.","german":"The meaning of a word can change over time.","furigana":"じだい|ごぎ|へんか"},{"japanese":"この辞書では、語義ごとに例文が挙げてある。","romaji":"Kono jisho de wa, gogi goto ni reibun ga agete aru.","german":"In this dictionary, example sentences are given for each sense.","furigana":"じしょ|ごぎ|れいぶん|あ"},{"japanese":"「確信犯」は本来の語義とは違う意味で使われることが多い。","romaji":"'Kakushinhan' wa honrai no gogi to wa chigau imi de tsukawareru koto ga ōi.","german":"「確信犯」 is often used in a sense different from its original meaning.","furigana":"かくしんはん|ほんらい|ごぎ|ちが|いみ|つか|おお"}],"notes":"語義 is the meaning of a word as an object of language study, for instance in dictionaries or semantics: 語義の変化, 語義ごとに, 本来の語義. In everyday speech one simply says 意味; 語義 sounds technical. 語釈 is the explanatory definition, 語義 the meaning itself. A word with many 語義 is called 多義語.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"多義語は文脈によって意味が決まる。","romaji":"Tagigo wa bunmyaku ni yotte imi ga kimaru.","german":"The meaning of a polysemous word is determined by the context.","furigana":"たぎご|ぶんみゃく|いみ|き"},{"japanese":"「かける」は日本語の代表的な多義語の一つだ。","romaji":"'Kakeru' wa Nihongo no daihyōteki na tagigo no hitotsu da.","german":"「かける」 is one of the most typical polysemous words in Japanese.","furigana":"にほんご|だいひょうてき|たぎご|ひと"},{"japanese":"多義語と同音異義語の違いを説明できますか。","romaji":"Tagigo to dōon igigo no chigai o setsumei dekimasu ka.","german":"Can you explain the difference between a polysemous word and a homonym?","furigana":"たぎご|どうおんいぎご|ちが|せつめい"}],"notes":"多義語 is a word with several related meanings (polysemy), e.g. かける or とる. To be distinguished from it is 同音異義語: different words with the same reading but unrelated meanings (橋 and 箸). With a 多義語, the context (文脈) decides the meaning; in dictionaries the 語義 are numbered.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"語感の微妙な違いを感じ取れるようになりたい。","romaji":"Gokan no bimyō na chigai o kanjitoreru yō ni naritai.","german":"I want to become able to pick up the subtle differences in nuance between words.","furigana":"ごかん|びみょう|ちが|かん|と"},{"japanese":"彼は語感が鋭く、言葉選びがとても上手だ。","romaji":"Kare wa gokan ga surudoku, kotoba erabi ga totemo jōzu da.","german":"He has a keen feel for language and is very good at choosing his words.","furigana":"かれ|ごかん|するど|ことばえら|じょうず"},{"japanese":"「死ぬ」より「亡くなる」のほうが柔らかい語感がある。","romaji":"'Shinu' yori 'nakunaru' no hō ga yawarakai gokan ga aru.","german":"「亡くなる」 has a softer nuance than 「死ぬ」.","furigana":"し|な|やわ|ごかん"}],"notes":"語感 has two meanings: a person's feel for language (語感が鋭い \"to have a fine sense of language\") and the nuance or connotation a word has (柔らかい語感, 語感が悪い). It does not mean the objective meaning (語義) but the impression a word makes when heard or read. Both uses are neutral to slightly elevated.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この語法は書き言葉に特有のものだ。","romaji":"Kono gohō wa kakikotoba ni tokuyū no mono da.","german":"This usage is peculiar to written language.","furigana":"ごほう|か|ことば|とくゆう"},{"japanese":"その言い方は語法として誤りだと指摘された。","romaji":"Sono iikata wa gohō to shite ayamari da to shiteki sareta.","german":"I was told that this way of putting it is incorrect as usage.","furigana":"い|かた|ごほう|あやま|してき"},{"japanese":"英語の語法を詳しく解説した参考書を買った。","romaji":"Eigo no gohō o kuwashiku kaisetsu shita sankōsho o katta.","german":"I bought a study book that explains English usage in detail.","furigana":"えいご|ごほう|くわ|かいせつ|さんこうしょ|か"}],"notes":"語法 refers to the rules for how words are actually used and combined, that is, word usage and grammar in detail. Typical are 語法の誤り, 語法上正しい and 英語の語法 (a popular topic of study books). 文法 is the whole grammatical system, 用法 the way a particular word is used; 語法 lies in between and stresses concrete usage.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"語誌を辿ると言葉の意味変遷がわかる。","romaji":"Goshi o tadoru to kotoba no imi hensen ga wakaru.","german":"If you trace the history of a word, you can see how its meaning changed.","furigana":"ごし|たど|ことば|いみへんせん"},{"japanese":"国語辞典の中には、語誌の欄を設けているものもある。","romaji":"Kokugo jiten no naka ni wa, goshi no ran o mōkete iru mono mo aru.","german":"Some Japanese dictionaries have a special section on word history.","furigana":"こくごじてん|なか|ごし|らん|もう"},{"japanese":"「やばい」の語誌を調べると、江戸時代までさかのぼる。","romaji":"'Yabai' no goshi o shiraberu to, Edo jidai made sakanoboru.","german":"If you look into the history of 「やばい」, it goes back to the Edo period.","furigana":"ごし|しら|えどじだい"}],"notes":"語誌 is the history of an individual word: its first attestations, its changes in form and meaning, and its spread. Large dictionaries such as the 日本国語大辞典 have their own 語誌 sections. Unlike 語源 (\"etymology, origin\"), 語誌 follows the whole further development. The rare meaning \"glossary\" plays hardly any role in present-day usage.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"品詞の分類は言語によって異なる。","romaji":"Hinshi no bunrui wa gengo ni yotte kotonaru.","german":"The classification of parts of speech differs from language to language.","furigana":"ひんし|ぶんるい|げんご|こと"},{"japanese":"辞書では、見出し語の後に品詞が書いてある。","romaji":"Jisho de wa, midashigo no ato ni hinshi ga kaite aru.","german":"In a dictionary, the part of speech is written after the headword.","furigana":"じしょ|みだ|ご|あと|ひんし|か"},{"japanese":"日本の学校文法では、品詞を十種類に分ける。","romaji":"Nihon no gakkō bunpō de wa, hinshi o jusshurui ni wakeru.","german":"Japanese school grammar divides words into ten parts of speech.","furigana":"にほん|がっこうぶんぽう|ひんし|じゅっしゅるい|わ"}],"notes":"品詞 is the part of speech. Japanese school grammar (学校文法) distinguishes ten 品詞: 名詞, 動詞, 形容詞, 形容動詞, 副詞, 連体詞, 接続詞, 感動詞, 助動詞 and 助詞. Typical phrases are 品詞を判別する, 品詞分解 (breaking a sentence down into its parts of speech, especially in 古文 classes) and 品詞の分類. Register: technical, but generally known from school.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"用言は活用する自立語を指す。","romaji":"Yōgen wa katsuyō suru jiritsugo o sasu.","german":"Yōgen refers to independent words that inflect.","furigana":"ようげん|かつよう|じりつご|さ"},{"japanese":"形容詞と形容動詞も用言に含まれる。","romaji":"Keiyōshi to keiyōdōshi mo yōgen ni fukumareru.","german":"Adjectives of both kinds are also included among yōgen.","furigana":"けいようし|けいようどうし|ようげん|ふく"},{"japanese":"体言と用言の区別は、国文法の基本だ。","romaji":"Taigen to yōgen no kubetsu wa, kokubunpō no kihon da.","german":"The distinction between taigen and yōgen is basic to Japanese grammar.","furigana":"たいげん|ようげん|くべつ|こくぶんぽう|きほん"}],"notes":"用言 is a term of Japanese grammar for independent words that inflect and can form a predicate on their own: 動詞, 形容詞 (い-adjectives) and 形容動詞 (な-adjectives). The opposite term is 体言, the non-inflecting words such as nouns and pronouns. The pairing 体言・用言 matters for rules such as 用言の連用形 or 体言止め.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"体言は活用しない自立語である。","romaji":"Taigen wa katsuyō shinai jiritsugo de aru.","german":"Taigen are independent words that do not inflect.","furigana":"たいげん|かつよう|じりつご"},{"japanese":"国文法では、名詞と代名詞をまとめて体言と呼ぶ。","romaji":"Kokubunpō de wa, meishi to daimeishi o matomete taigen to yobu.","german":"In Japanese grammar, nouns and pronouns are grouped together as taigen.","furigana":"こくぶんぽう|めいし|だいめいし|たいげん|よ"},{"japanese":"この句は体言止めで終わっている。","romaji":"Kono ku wa taigen-dome de owatte iru.","german":"This verse ends with a noun, that is, with the stylistic device taigen-dome.","furigana":"く|たいげんど|お"}],"notes":"体言 is a technical term of Japanese school grammar (国文法) for independent, non-inflecting words that can be a subject, in practice nouns including pronouns and numerals. The counterpart is 用言 (verbs and adjectives, which inflect). The term is well known from 連体形 (\"the form before a 体言\") and 体言止め, the stylistic device of ending a sentence or verse with a noun. The word hardly appears in everyday life.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"助詞と助動詞は付属語に分類される。","romaji":"Joshi to jodōshi wa fuzokugo ni bunrui sareru.","german":"Particles and auxiliary verbs are classified as dependent words.","furigana":"じょし|じょどうし|ふぞくご|ぶんるい"},{"japanese":"付属語は単独では文節を作ることができない。","romaji":"Fuzokugo wa tandoku de wa bunsetsu o tsukuru koto ga dekinai.","german":"Dependent words cannot form a phrase on their own.","furigana":"ふぞくご|たんどく|ぶんせつ|つく"},{"japanese":"付属語のうち、活用するのは助動詞だけだ。","romaji":"Fuzokugo no uchi, katsuyō suru no wa jodōshi dake da.","german":"Of the dependent words, only auxiliary verbs inflect.","furigana":"ふぞくご|かつよう|じょどうし"}],"notes":"付属語 are, in Japanese school grammar, the words that cannot form a 文節 (phrase) alone and are always attached to a 自立語: 助詞 (particles) and 助動詞 (auxiliary verbs). The opposite term is 自立語. Of the 付属語, only the 助動詞 inflect. Do not confuse with suffixes such as 〜さ or 〜的, which count as 接尾語 in word formation. Older spelling: 附属語.","levelNote":"Compound: 付属 (N2) + 語 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"名詞や動詞は自立語の例だ。","romaji":"Meishi ya dōshi wa jiritsugo no rei da.","german":"Nouns and verbs are examples of independent words.","furigana":"めいし|どうし|じりつご|れい"},{"japanese":"文節には必ず一つの自立語が含まれる。","romaji":"Bunsetsu ni wa kanarazu hitotsu no jiritsugo ga fukumareru.","german":"Every phrase contains exactly one independent word.","furigana":"ぶんせつ|かなら|ひと|じりつご|ふく"},{"japanese":"自立語のうち、活用するものを用言という。","romaji":"Jiritsugo no uchi, katsuyō suru mono o yōgen to iu.","german":"Of the independent words, those that inflect are called yōgen.","furigana":"じりつご|かつよう|ようげん"}],"notes":"自立語 are, in Japanese school grammar, the words with a meaning of their own that can form a 文節 (phrase) alone: nouns, verbs, adjectives, adverbs, 連体詞, conjunctions and interjections. Every 文節 begins with exactly one 自立語, optionally followed by 付属語 (particles, auxiliary verbs). Inflecting 自立語 are called 用言, and non-inflecting ones that can be a subject 体言. A purely technical term from grammar class and linguistics.","levelNote":"Compound: 自立 (N1) + 語 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"口承文学は文字を持たない文化において重要だ。","romaji":"Kōshō bungaku wa moji o motanai bunka ni oite jūyō da.","german":"Oral literature is important in cultures without writing.","furigana":"こうしょうぶんがく|もじ|も|ぶんか|じゅうよう"},{"japanese":"ユーカラはアイヌの口承文学を代表する叙事詩だ。","romaji":"Yūkara wa Ainu no kōshō bungaku o daihyō suru jojishi da.","german":"The Yukar is the epic poem that represents Ainu oral literature.","furigana":"こうしょうぶんがく|だいひょう|じょじし"},{"japanese":"昔話や民謡も口承文学の一つとして研究されている。","romaji":"Mukashibanashi ya min'yō mo kōshō bungaku no hitotsu to shite kenkyū sarete iru.","german":"Folktales and folk songs are also studied as a form of oral literature.","furigana":"むかしばなし|みんよう|こうしょうぶんがく|ひと|けんきゅう"}],"notes":"口承文学 is literature that is passed on orally (口承 \"handed down from mouth to mouth\") rather than in writing: myths, epics, tales, legends, folk songs. The opposite term is literature fixed in writing (記載文学). Synonymous are 口承文芸 and, more generally, 口頭伝承. A scholarly term from folklore studies, ethnology and literary studies; 口承 alone appears in, for example, 口承で伝わる \"to be handed down orally\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"文学理論を学ぶことで作品の新たな読み方が見えてくる。","romaji":"Bungaku riron o manabu koto de sakuhin no arata na yomikata ga miete kuru.","german":"Studying literary theory opens up new ways of reading works.","furigana":"ぶんがくりろん|まな|さくひん|あら|よ|かた|み"},{"japanese":"大学院で文学理論を専攻している。","romaji":"Daigakuin de bungaku riron o senkō shite iru.","german":"I am specializing in literary theory in graduate school.","furigana":"だいがくいん|ぶんがくりろん|せんこう"},{"japanese":"その評論はフェミニズムの文学理論を踏まえて書かれている。","romaji":"Sono hyōron wa feminizumu no bungaku riron o fumaete kakarete iru.","german":"The essay is written drawing on feminist literary theory.","furigana":"ひょうろん|ぶんがくりろん|ふ|か"}],"notes":"文学理論 is literary theory as an academic field: structuralism, reception aesthetics, deconstruction, feminist theory, etc. Typical are 文学理論を学ぶ/専攻する and 〜の文学理論を踏まえる. Closely related is 批評理論 (\"critical theory, theory of literary criticism\"); 文学論, by contrast, refers more to an individual treatise on literature, such as 夏目漱石の『文学論』. Elevated, academic vocabulary.","levelNote":"Compound: 文学 (N5) + 理論 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"文芸誌に短編が掲載された。","romaji":"Bungei-shi ni tanpen ga keisai sareta.","german":"A short story was published in the literary magazine.","furigana":"ぶんげいし|たんぺん|けいさい"},{"japanese":"高校では文芸部に入って小説を書いていた。","romaji":"Kōkō de wa bungei-bu ni haitte shōsetsu o kaite ita.","german":"In high school I joined the literature club and wrote novels.","furigana":"こうこう|ぶんげいぶ|はい|しょうせつ|か"},{"japanese":"この出版社は文芸書よりも実用書に強い。","romaji":"Kono shuppansha wa bungeisho yori mo jitsuyōsho ni tsuyoi.","german":"This publisher is stronger in practical books than in literary works.","furigana":"しゅっぱんしゃ|ぶんげいしょ|じつようしょ|つよ"}],"notes":"Like 文学, 文芸 means \"literature\", but it stresses the literary as an art and as creative work, and it appears mainly in fixed compounds: 文芸誌 (literary magazine), 文芸部 (literature club at schools), 文芸書 (\"belles-lettres\"), 文芸作品, 文芸評論. 文学, by contrast, is the more general word, also used for the field of study (文学部, 日本文学). In the older, broader sense, 文芸 covers literature and the arts in general.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"古代の碑文から当時の言語が推定できる。","romaji":"Kodai no hibun kara tōji no gengo ga suitei dekiru.","german":"The language of the time can be inferred from ancient inscriptions.","furigana":"こだい|ひぶん|とうじ|げんご|すいてい"},{"japanese":"公園の石碑の碑文は、長年の風雨で読みにくくなっていた。","romaji":"Kōen no sekihi no hibun wa, naganen no fūu de yominikuku natte ita.","german":"The inscription on the stone monument in the park had become hard to read after years of wind and rain.","furigana":"こうえん|せきひ|ひぶん|ながねん|ふうう|よ"},{"japanese":"記念碑の碑文には、震災で亡くなった人々の名前が刻まれている。","romaji":"Kinenhi no hibun ni wa, shinsai de nakunatta hitobito no namae ga kizamarete iru.","german":"The inscription on the memorial has the names of the people who died in the earthquake carved into it.","furigana":"きねんひ|ひぶん|しんさい|な|ひとびと|なまえ|きざ"}],"notes":"碑文 is the text on a 碑, that is, a memorial stone, monument or gravestone. Typical combinations: 碑文を刻む \"to carve an inscription\", 碑文を読む/解読する, 碑文に記される. For inscriptions on metal (bells, swords, mirrors) one more often says 銘文; the epitaph in the narrower sense is called 墓碑銘. Written language, frequent in archaeology, history and museum texts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"刀剣に刻まれた銘文を解読した。","romaji":"Tōken ni kizamareta meibun o kaidoku shita.","german":"I deciphered the inscription engraved on the sword.","furigana":"とうけん|きざ|めいぶん|かいどく"},{"japanese":"この鐘の銘文には、鋳造された年が記されている。","romaji":"Kono kane no meibun ni wa, chūzō sareta toshi ga shirusarete iru.","german":"The inscription on this bell records the year in which it was cast.","furigana":"かね|めいぶん|ちゅうぞう|とし|しる"},{"japanese":"古墳から出土した鉄剣の銘文が、古代史研究に大きな手がかりを与えた。","romaji":"Kofun kara shutsudo shita tekken no meibun ga, kodaishi kenkyū ni ōkina tegakari o ataeta.","german":"The inscription on an iron sword unearthed from an ancient burial mound gave an important clue to the study of ancient history.","furigana":"こふん|しゅつど|てっけん|めいぶん|こだいしけんきゅう|おお|て|あた"}],"notes":"銘文 is an engraved or cast inscription, above all on metal objects such as bells, swords, bronze mirrors or vessels; 碑文, by contrast, is for inscriptions on stone monuments. The single word 銘 also appears as a swordsmith's signature (刀の銘) and in 座右の銘 \"personal motto\". Typical: 銘文を刻む, 銘文を解読する. Technical vocabulary from archaeology and art history.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"爽やかな読後感を残す作品だった。","romaji":"Sawayaka na dokugokan o nokosu sakuhin datta.","german":"It was a work that left a refreshing impression after reading.","furigana":"さわ|どくごかん|のこ|さくひん"},{"japanese":"この小説は結末が重くて、読後感があまりよくない。","romaji":"Kono shōsetsu wa ketsumatsu ga omokute, dokugokan ga amari yokunai.","german":"The ending of this novel is so heavy that it doesn't leave a very good feeling afterwards.","furigana":"しょうせつ|けつまつ|おも|どくごかん"},{"japanese":"書評では、ストーリーだけでなく読後感についても書いた。","romaji":"Shohyō de wa, sutōrī dake de naku dokugokan ni tsuite mo kaita.","german":"In the book review, I wrote not only about the story but also about the impression the book leaves.","furigana":"しょひょう|どくごかん|か"}],"notes":"読後感 is the feeling that remains after reading a book, so more a mood than a fully formed opinion (for that one says 感想). Typical combinations: 読後感がいい／悪い, 爽やかな読後感, 読後感を残す. The word comes from book criticism and reviews, but is also used in everyday talk about novels and manga.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コンテストで佳作に選ばれた。","romaji":"Kontesuto de kasaku ni erabareta.","german":"I was chosen for an honorable mention in the contest.","furigana":"かさく|えら"},{"japanese":"派手さはないが、なかなかの佳作だ。","romaji":"Hadesa wa nai ga, nakanaka no kasaku da.","german":"It isn't flashy, but it is a quite good piece of work.","furigana":"はで|かさく"},{"japanese":"この短編集は佳作ぞろいだ。","romaji":"Kono tanpenshū wa kasaku-zoroi da.","german":"This short story collection is full of fine pieces.","furigana":"たんぺんしゅう|かさく"}],"notes":"佳作 has two uses: in general it is a successful, good work, a compliment but one step below 傑作 or 名作, typically in なかなかの佳作 or 佳作ぞろい. In competitions, 佳作 denotes the award below the actual prizes, roughly \"honorable mention\": 佳作に選ばれる, 佳作に入る, 選外佳作. Rather written language, but commonplace in competition results and reviews.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"批評家から駄作と酷評された。","romaji":"Hihyō-ka kara dasaku to kokuhyō sareta.","german":"It was savaged by critics as a poor piece of work.","furigana":"ひひょうか|ださく|こくひょう"},{"japanese":"シリーズ最終作は、正直言って駄作だった。","romaji":"Shirīzu saishūsaku wa, shōjiki itte dasaku datta.","german":"Honestly, the final installment of the series was a dud.","furigana":"さいしゅうさく|しょうじきい|ださく"},{"japanese":"駄作を恐れずに、とにかくたくさん書きなさい。","romaji":"Dasaku o osorezu ni, tonikaku takusan kakinasai.","german":"Don't be afraid of writing duds; just write as much as you can.","furigana":"ださく|おそ|か"}],"notes":"駄作 is a failed, worthless work; 駄 means \"inferior\" as in 駄菓子 or 駄洒落. The word is clearly derogatory, but quite common when talking about films, books or games: 駄作だった, 駄作と酷評される. The opposites are 名作, 傑作 and 佳作; more neutral for \"mediocre\" is 凡作. One also uses it self-deprecatingly about one's own work.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"時代を超えて読み継がれる名作がある。","romaji":"Jidai o koete yomitsugareru meisaku ga aru.","german":"There are masterpieces that continue to be read across the ages.","furigana":"じだい|こ|よ|つ|めいさく"},{"japanese":"夏休みに世界の名作を何冊か読んだ。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi ni sekai no meisaku o nansatsu ka yonda.","german":"During summer vacation I read several masterpieces of world literature.","furigana":"なつやす|せかい|めいさく|なんさつ|よ"},{"japanese":"この映画は何度見ても泣ける名作だ。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa nando mite mo nakeru meisaku da.","german":"This film is a masterpiece that makes me cry every time I watch it.","furigana":"えいが|なんどみ|な|めいさく"}],"notes":"名作 is a famous, widely recognized work - a classic in literature, film, music or games: 不朽の名作, 世界の名作, 名作映画. 傑作, by contrast, stresses outstanding quality, often in relation to an artist's overall output (最高傑作). For books, including nonfiction, there is 名著; for pieces of music 名曲; for paintings 名画. Neutral and very common.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"未完の遺作が没後に出版された。","romaji":"Mikan no isaku ga botsugo ni shuppan sareta.","german":"The unfinished posthumous work was published after his death.","furigana":"みかん|いさく|ぼつご|しゅっぱん"},{"japanese":"この絵が画家の遺作となった。","romaji":"Kono e ga gaka no isaku to natta.","german":"This painting became the painter's last work.","furigana":"え|がか|いさく"},{"japanese":"亡くなった監督の遺作が来月公開される。","romaji":"Nakunatta kantoku no isaku ga raigetsu kōkai sareru.","german":"The late director's last film will be released next month.","furigana":"な|かんとく|いさく|らいげつこうかい"}],"notes":"遺作 is the work a deceased person left behind, usually their last, often unfinished or published only after death. Typical frame: 〜が遺作となる \"became his/her last work\", 遺作展 \"exhibition of late works\". Related are 遺稿 (a manuscript left behind) and 絶筆 (the last thing written or painted). Neutral to written register, frequent in obituaries and cultural news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女の処女作はベストセラーとなった。","romaji":"Kanojo no shojosaku wa besutoserā to natta.","german":"Her first work became a bestseller.","furigana":"かのじょ|しょじょさく"},{"japanese":"処女作にはその作家のすべてが詰まっていると言われる。","romaji":"Shojosaku ni wa sono sakka no subete ga tsumatte iru to iwareru.","german":"It is said that everything that makes an author is already in their first work.","furigana":"しょじょさく|さっか|つ|い"},{"japanese":"彼の処女作は、学生時代に自費出版した詩集だった。","romaji":"Kare no shojosaku wa, gakusei jidai ni jihi shuppan shita shishū datta.","german":"His first work was a poetry collection he self-published as a student.","furigana":"かれ|しょじょさく|がくせいじだい|じひしゅっぱん|ししゅう"}],"notes":"処女作 is the first published work of an author or artist; 処女 here means \"first-time, untouched\" as in 処女航海 (maiden voyage). Because of the secondary meaning \"virgin\", some people avoid it in favor of デビュー作 (more casual, also used for film and music) or 第一作. Typical: 〜の処女作, 処女作で賞を取る. Neutral, common in literary criticism and biographies.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「坊っちゃん」は夏目漱石の代表作の一つだ。","romaji":"\"Botchan\" wa Natsume Sōseki no daihyōsaku no hitotsu da.","german":"「坊っちゃん」 is one of Natsume Sōseki's best-known works.","furigana":"ぼ|なつめそうせき|だいひょうさく|ひと"},{"japanese":"この映画は、監督の代表作と言われている。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa, kantoku no daihyōsaku to iwarete iru.","german":"This film is said to be the director's signature work.","furigana":"えいが|かんとく|だいひょうさく|い"},{"japanese":"面接で、自分の代表作を三つ見せてくださいと言われた。","romaji":"Mensetsu de, jibun no daihyōsaku o mittsu misete kudasai to iwareta.","german":"In the job interview, I was told to show three of my representative works.","furigana":"めんせつ|じぶん|だいひょうさく|みっ|み|い"}],"notes":"代表作 is the work that is most typical of an author, artist, director or musician and for which they are known: 〜の代表作, 代表作に挙げる, 代表作となる. It stresses fame and representativeness, not necessarily the highest quality; for that one says 傑作 or 名作. It applies to novels, films, paintings, songs and even a company's products.","levelNote":"Compound: 代表 (N3) + 作 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"原作を改作して舞台版に仕立てた。","romaji":"Gensaku o kaisaku shite butai-ban ni shitateta.","german":"The original was reworked into a stage version.","furigana":"げんさく|かいさく|ぶたいばん|した"},{"japanese":"作者は晩年、若い頃の作品を何度も改作した。","romaji":"Sakusha wa bannen, wakai koro no sakuhin o nando mo kaisaku shita.","german":"In his later years, the author revised his youthful works again and again.","furigana":"さくしゃ|ばんねん|わか|ころ|さくひん|なんど|かいさく"},{"japanese":"原作者の許可なく改作することは、著作権の侵害になりうる。","romaji":"Gensakusha no kyoka naku kaisaku suru koto wa, chosakuken no shingai ni nariuru.","german":"Reworking a work without the permission of its original author can be a copyright infringement.","furigana":"げんさくしゃ|きょか|かいさく|ちょさくけん|しんがい"}],"notes":"改作 means reworking an existing work, one's own or someone else's; as a transitive する-verb: 作品を改作する. Distinctions: 翻案 transfers material to another era, culture or genre, 脚色 adapts it for stage or film, and 改訂 corrects and updates a text (e.g. a textbook). Written language, mainly in literary and theater contexts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"シェイクスピアの戯曲を翻案した日本語劇だ。","romaji":"Sheikusupia no gikyoku o hon'an shita Nihongo-geki da.","german":"It is a Japanese-language play freely adapted from Shakespeare's drama.","furigana":"ぎきょく|ほんあん|にほんごげき"},{"japanese":"海外のミステリー小説を、舞台を日本に移して翻案した。","romaji":"Kaigai no misuterī shōsetsu o, butai o Nihon ni utsushite hon'an shita.","german":"A foreign mystery novel was adapted, with the setting moved to Japan.","furigana":"かいがい|しょうせつ|ぶたい|にほん|うつ|ほんあん"},{"japanese":"落語の『死神』は、グリム童話を翻案したものだと言われている。","romaji":"Rakugo no \"Shinigami\" wa, Gurimu dōwa o hon'an shita mono da to iwarete iru.","german":"The rakugo story 「死神」 is said to be an adaptation of a Grimm fairy tale.","furigana":"らくご|しにがみ|どうわ|ほんあん|い"}],"notes":"翻案 means taking over the plot and material of a (often foreign) work and adapting it to another time, country or genre; as a transitive する-verb: 〜を翻案する. Unlike 翻訳 (translation), the content is freely reshaped; 改作 is the more general \"rework\". 翻案小説 were widespread in the Meiji period; in copyright law there is the term 翻案権. Written language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"教育学の理論を実践に活かすことが重要だ。","romaji":"Kyōikugaku no riron o jissen ni ikasu koto ga jūyō da.","german":"It is important to put the theory of pedagogy into practice.","furigana":"きょういくがく|りろん|じっせん|い|じゅうよう"},{"japanese":"大学で教育学を学び、小学校の教師になった。","romaji":"Daigaku de kyōikugaku o manabi, shōgakkō no kyōshi ni natta.","german":"I studied education at university and became an elementary school teacher.","furigana":"だいがく|きょういくがく|まな|しょうがっこう|きょうし"},{"japanese":"教育学の観点から見ると、この宿題の出し方には問題がある。","romaji":"Kyōikugaku no kanten kara miru to, kono shukudai no dashikata ni wa mondai ga aru.","german":"From a pedagogical point of view, this way of assigning homework is problematic.","furigana":"きょういくがく|かんてん|み|しゅくだい|だ|かた|もんだい"}],"notes":"教育学 is the science of education and upbringing, that is, pedagogy or education studies as a field of study: 教育学を学ぶ/専攻する, 教育学の観点から. Subfields include 教育心理学 and 教育社会学. Careful: 教育学部 \"Faculty of Education\" is 教育＋学部, not 教育学＋部. The word is academic; for education itself one simply says 教育.","levelNote":"Compound: 教育 (N4) + 学 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"学校の教育理念に共感して入学を決めた。","romaji":"Gakkō no kyōiku rinen ni kyōkan shite nyūgaku o kimeta.","german":"I decided to enroll because I sympathized with the school's educational philosophy.","furigana":"がっこう|きょういくりねん|きょうかん|にゅうがく|き"},{"japanese":"創立者の教育理念は、今も校訓として受け継がれている。","romaji":"Sōritsusha no kyōiku rinen wa, ima mo kōkun to shite uketsugarete iru.","german":"The founder's educational ideals live on to this day in the school motto.","furigana":"そうりつしゃ|きょういくりねん|いま|こうくん|う|つ"},{"japanese":"面接では、本校の教育理念をどう理解しているか聞かれた。","romaji":"Mensetsu de wa, honkō no kyōiku rinen o dō rikai shite iru ka kikareta.","german":"In the interview, I was asked how I understand the school's educational philosophy.","furigana":"めんせつ|ほんこう|きょういくりねん|りかい|き"}],"notes":"教育理念 is the fundamental educational ideal or guiding principle of a school, university or company (理念 \"guiding idea, basic principle\"). It typically appears on websites and in brochures: 本校の教育理念, 教育理念に共感する, 教育理念を掲げる. More concrete are 教育方針 (\"educational policy\") and 校訓 (school motto); private schools also speak of 建学の精神. Formal and written language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"学位授与式は三月に行われる。","romaji":"Gakui juyo-shiki wa san-gatsu ni okonawareru.","german":"The degree conferral ceremony takes place in March.","furigana":"がくいじゅよしき|さんがつ|おこな"},{"japanese":"博士論文の審査に合格し、学位授与が決定した。","romaji":"Hakase ronbun no shinsa ni gōkaku shi, gakui juyo ga kettei shita.","german":"The dissertation passed review, and the conferral of the degree has been decided.","furigana":"はかせろんぶん|しんさ|ごうかく|がくいじゅよ|けってい"},{"japanese":"この大学は独自の学位授与方針を公表している。","romaji":"Kono daigaku wa dokuji no gakui juyo hōshin o kōhyō shite iru.","german":"This university has published its own policy on awarding degrees.","furigana":"だいがく|どくじ|がくいじゅよほうしん|こうひょう"}],"notes":"学位授与 is the formal conferral of an academic degree (授与 \"to confer ceremonially\", also used for prizes and decorations). Verbally: 学位を授与する or, passive, 学位が授与される; from the students' point of view one says 学位を取得する \"to obtain a degree\". Fixed combinations: 学位授与式 (graduation ceremony, in everyday speech often simply 卒業式) and 学位授与の方針 (a university's \"diploma policy\"). Official, formal style.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"教職に就くためには免許が必要だ。","romaji":"Kyōshoku ni tsuku tame ni wa menkyo ga hitsuyō da.","german":"You need a license to take up a teaching position.","furigana":"きょうしょく|つ|めんきょ|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"父は定年まで四十年近く教職を務めた。","romaji":"Chichi wa teinen made yonjūnen chikaku kyōshoku o tsutometa.","german":"My father worked as a teacher for nearly forty years until retirement.","furigana":"ちち|ていねん|よんじゅうねんちか|きょうしょく|つと"},{"japanese":"大学で教職課程を履修している。","romaji":"Daigaku de kyōshoku katei o rishū shite iru.","german":"I am taking teacher-training courses at university.","furigana":"だいがく|きょうしょくかてい|りしゅう"}],"notes":"教職 is the teaching profession as an occupation or office and sounds more formal than 教師 or 先生. Typical combinations: 教職に就く \"to become a teacher\", 教職を務める, 教職を離れる/去る. 教職課程 are the teacher-training modules taken at Japanese universities alongside the main course of study; 教職員 means teachers and other school staff together. The meaning \"instruction of laypeople\" (religious) is rare.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"指導教官の助言に従って論文を修正した。","romaji":"Shidō kyōkan no jogen ni shitagatte ronbun o shūsei shita.","german":"I revised the paper following my supervisor's advice.","furigana":"しどうきょうかん|じょげん|したが|ろんぶん|しゅうせい"},{"japanese":"困ったときは、専門家に助言を求めるといい。","romaji":"Komatta toki wa, senmonka ni jogen o motomeru to ii.","german":"When you are in trouble, it is good to ask an expert for advice.","furigana":"こま|せんもんか|じょげん|もと"},{"japanese":"先輩が転職について助言してくれた。","romaji":"Senpai ga tenshoku ni tsuite jogen shite kureta.","german":"A senior colleague gave me advice about changing jobs.","furigana":"せんぱい|てんしょく|じょげん"}],"notes":"助言 is factual, rather formal advice; in conversation アドバイス is more common. Typical combinations: 助言を求める/受ける/与える, 〜の助言に従う, and as a する-verb 人に助言する. Distinctions: 忠告 is warning, admonishing advice, and 勧告 is an official recommendation, for example from an authority. The word fits well in work, study and counseling situations.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"叙述トリックを用いた推理小説が人気だ。","romaji":"Jojutsu torikku o mochiita suiri shōsetsu ga ninki da.","german":"Mystery novels that use narrative tricks are popular.","furigana":"じょじゅつ|もち|すいりしょうせつ|にんき"},{"japanese":"最後の一行で叙述トリックに気づき、最初から読み直した。","romaji":"Saigo no ichigyō de jojutsu torikku ni kizuki, saisho kara yominaoshita.","german":"I realized the narrative trick in the very last line and reread everything from the beginning.","furigana":"さいご|いちぎょう|じょじゅつ|き|さいしょ|よ|なお"},{"japanese":"叙述トリックが使われているため、この小説は映像化が難しい。","romaji":"Jojutsu torikku ga tsukawarete iru tame, kono shōsetsu wa eizōka ga muzukashii.","german":"Because it uses a narrative trick, this novel is difficult to adapt for film.","furigana":"じょじゅつ|つか|しょうせつ|えいぞうか|むずか"}],"notes":"叙述トリック is a term from the Japanese mystery scene: it is not the characters but the reader who is deceived, through the way the story is told, for example a concealed gender, a hidden time level or the narrator's true identity. The counterpart is the 物理トリック at the crime scene. Typical: 叙述トリックを使う, 叙述トリックに騙される. Common fan jargon in reviews.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"信頼できない語り手という手法が注目されている。","romaji":"Shinrai dekinai katarite to iu shuhō ga chūmoku sarete iru.","german":"The technique of the unreliable narrator is attracting attention.","furigana":"しんらい|かた|て|しゅほう|ちゅうもく"},{"japanese":"この小説の語り手は、一匹の猫だ。","romaji":"Kono shōsetsu no katarite wa, ippiki no neko da.","german":"The narrator of this novel is a cat.","furigana":"しょうせつ|かた|て|いっぴき|ねこ"},{"japanese":"祖母は村の昔話の語り手として知られていた。","romaji":"Sobo wa mura no mukashibanashi no katarite to shite shirarete ita.","german":"My grandmother was known in the village as a teller of old tales.","furigana":"そぼ|むら|むかしばなし|かた|て|し"}],"notes":"語り手 (from 語る \"to narrate\" + 手 \"person\") is, on the one hand, the narrating voice of a literary text, which is not identical with the author (作者): 一人称の語り手, 信頼できない語り手. On the other hand, it denotes a person who recites stories orally. Distinctions: 話し手 is the speaker in a conversation (counterpart 聞き手), and ナレーター is the voice-over speaker in film or television.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"視点人物が変わることで物語に多層性が生まれる。","romaji":"Shiten jinbutsu ga kawaru koto de monogatari ni tasō-sei ga umareru.","german":"Changing the viewpoint character gives the story many layers.","furigana":"してんじんぶつ|か|ものがたり|たそうせい|う"},{"japanese":"この章では視点人物が犯人に切り替わる。","romaji":"Kono shō de wa shiten jinbutsu ga hannin ni kirikawaru.","german":"In this chapter, the viewpoint switches to the culprit.","furigana":"しょう|してんじんぶつ|はんにん|き|か"},{"japanese":"視点人物の知らないことは、読者にも知らされない。","romaji":"Shiten jinbutsu no shiranai koto wa, dokusha ni mo shirasarenai.","german":"What the viewpoint character doesn't know is not revealed to the reader either.","furigana":"してんじんぶつ|し|どくしゃ|し"}],"notes":"視点人物 is the character from whose perspective a scene is narrated, especially in third-person limited narration. It is not necessarily the 語り手 (narrator) or the 主人公 (protagonist). Typical: 視点人物が変わる/切り替わる, 視点人物を固定する. A technical term from literary studies and writing guides; in fan circles also 視点キャラ.","levelNote":"Compound: 視点 (N3) + 人物 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"多様な登場人物が織りなす群像劇だ。","romaji":"Tayō na tōjō jinbutsu ga orinasu gunzō-geki da.","german":"It is an ensemble drama woven together by a diverse cast of characters.","furigana":"たよう|とうじょうじんぶつ|お|ぐんぞうげき"},{"japanese":"この映画は、同じ日に空港に居合わせた人々を描いた群像劇だ。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa, onaji hi ni kūkō ni iawaseta hitobito o egaita gunzō-geki da.","german":"This film is an ensemble piece depicting people who happened to be at the airport on the same day.","furigana":"えいが|おな|ひ|くうこう|いあ|ひとびと|えが|ぐんぞうげき"},{"japanese":"主人公がいない群像劇は、登場人物を覚えるのが大変だ。","romaji":"Shujinkō ga inai gunzō-geki wa, tōjō jinbutsu o oboeru no ga taihen da.","german":"With ensemble stories that have no protagonist, it is hard to remember all the characters.","furigana":"しゅじんこう|ぐんぞうげき|とうじょうじんぶつ|おぼ|たいへん"}],"notes":"群像劇 is a film, play or novel in which many characters are equally at the center and several storylines run in parallel, without a single protagonist; an \"ensemble piece\". 群像 alone means a group of figures, for example in sculpture or in 青春群像. Typical: 〜を描いた群像劇, 群像劇として描く. Neutral, frequent in film and book reviews.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"西洋詩の韻脚には弱強格などがある。","romaji":"Seiyō-shi no inkyaku ni wa jakukyō-kaku nado ga aru.","german":"Western poetry's metrical feet include the iamb, among others.","furigana":"せいようし|いんきゃく|じゃくきょうかく"},{"japanese":"弱強五歩格では、一行が五つの韻脚から成る。","romaji":"Jakukyō gohokaku de wa, ichigyō ga itsutsu no inkyaku kara naru.","german":"In iambic pentameter, a line consists of five metrical feet.","furigana":"じゃくきょうごほかく|いちぎょう|いつ|いんきゃく|な"},{"japanese":"この漢詩は、第二句と第四句の末字が韻脚になっている。","romaji":"Kono kanshi wa, dai-ni-ku to dai-yon-ku no matsuji ga inkyaku ni natte iru.","german":"In this Chinese poem, the final characters of the second and fourth lines serve as the rhyme.","furigana":"かんし|だいにく|だいよんく|まつじ|いんきゃく"}],"notes":"韻脚 has two meanings: in Western metrics it is the metrical foot, a fixed sequence of stressed and unstressed syllables such as 弱強格 (iamb) or 強弱格 (trochee); 詩脚 is synonymous. In Chinese poetry (漢詩), it denotes the rhyming character at the end of a line. A purely technical term of prosody; \"to rhyme\" is generally 韻を踏む.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"写本間の異文を詳細に記録した。","romaji":"Shahon-kan no ibun o shōsai ni kiroku shita.","german":"The textual variants between the manuscripts were recorded in detail.","furigana":"しゃほんかん|いぶん|しょうさい|きろく"},{"japanese":"この和歌は、写本によって異文が伝わっている。","romaji":"Kono waka wa, shahon ni yotte ibun ga tsutawatte iru.","german":"Different versions of this waka have been handed down depending on the manuscript.","furigana":"わか|しゃほん|いぶん|つた"},{"japanese":"校訂本では、本文の下に主な異文が注記されている。","romaji":"Kōteibon de wa, honbun no shita ni omo na ibun ga chūki sarete iru.","german":"In the critical edition, the main variants are noted below the main text.","furigana":"こうていぼん|ほんぶん|した|おも|いぶん|ちゅうき"}],"notes":"異文 is a term of textual criticism: a passage whose wording in one manuscript or edition differs from other transmissions. Typical: 異文がある/伝わる, 異文を記録する/注記する, 〜による異文. To be distinguished from it is 異本 (a divergent manuscript or edition as a whole). Do not confuse with 異文化 \"foreign culture\", which consists of 異＋文化. Technical language of philology.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ひらがなの字母は漢字の草書体に由来する。","romaji":"Hiragana no jibo wa kanji no sōsho-tai ni yurai suru.","german":"The source characters of hiragana derive from the cursive forms of kanji.","furigana":"じぼ|かんじ|そうしょたい|ゆらい"},{"japanese":"「あ」の字母は「安」という漢字だ。","romaji":"'A' no jibo wa 'an' to iu kanji da.","german":"The source character of 「あ」 is the kanji 「安」.","furigana":"じぼ|あん|かんじ"},{"japanese":"ロシア語の字母は三十三文字ある。","romaji":"Roshiago no jibo wa sanjūsan moji aru.","german":"The Russian alphabet has 33 letters.","furigana":"ご|じぼ|さんじゅうさんもじ"}],"notes":"字母 has two main uses in Japanese: in the history of writing it is the kanji from which a kana originated (「あ」の字母は「安」). In general it denotes the letters of an alphabet, e.g. ロシア語の字母. In printing, it also meant the casting matrix for type. A technical word from the history of writing and linguistics; for \"letter\" in everyday speech one says 文字 or アルファベット.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本には言霊の思想が古くから存在する。","romaji":"Nihon ni wa kotodama no shisō ga furuku kara sonzai suru.","german":"The idea of kotodama has existed in Japan since ancient times.","furigana":"にほん|ことだま|しそう|ふる|そんざい"},{"japanese":"『万葉集』には、日本を「言霊の幸わう国」と詠んだ歌がある。","romaji":"Man'yōshū ni wa, Nihon o \"kotodama no sakiwau kuni\" to yonda uta ga aru.","german":"The Man'yōshū has a poem that sings of Japan as a 「言霊の幸わう国」 (land where the power of words brings blessing).","furigana":"まんようしゅう|にほん|ことだま||くに|よ|うた"},{"japanese":"受験生の前で「落ちる」と言わないのは、言霊を気にするからだ。","romaji":"Jukensei no mae de \"ochiru\" to iwanai no wa, kotodama o ki ni suru kara da.","german":"People avoid saying 「落ちる」 in front of exam candidates because they care about kotodama.","furigana":"じゅけんせい|まえ|お|い|ことだま|き"}],"notes":"言霊 is the old Japanese belief that words have a power of their own to take effect: what one utters can become reality. The word often appears in 言霊信仰 or 言霊の思想. To this day the idea lives on in 忌み言葉, words one avoids at weddings (切れる, 別れる) or before exams (落ちる, 滑る). A cultural-religious term, neutral to elevated.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"相手の言質を取ってから交渉を進めた。","romaji":"Aite no genchi o totte kara kōshō o susumeta.","german":"I moved the negotiation forward after getting the other party's commitment.","furigana":"あいて|げんち|と|こうしょう|すす"},{"japanese":"記者会見で大臣は、言質を与えないよう慎重に答えた。","romaji":"Kisha kaiken de daijin wa, genchi o ataenai yō shinchō ni kotaeta.","german":"At the press conference, the minister answered carefully so as not to commit himself.","furigana":"きしゃかいけん|だいじん|げんち|あた|しんちょう|こた"},{"japanese":"うっかり「やります」と言って、上司に言質を取られてしまった。","romaji":"Ukkari \"yarimasu\" to itte, jōshi ni genchi o torarete shimatta.","german":"I carelessly said 「やります」, and now my boss has pinned me down on it.","furigana":"い|じょうし|げんち|と"}],"notes":"言質 is a statement one can later hold someone to, a binding commitment. It almost always appears in fixed combinations: 言質を取る \"to take someone at their word, to extract a commitment\", 言質を与える \"to commit oneself\", 言質を取られる \"to be pinned down on something\". The correct reading is げんち; げんしつ is often heard, but is traditionally regarded as a misreading. Typical of politics, negotiations and business.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"報告書ではその問題について言及されていない。","romaji":"Hōkokusho de wa sono mondai ni tsuite genkyū sarete inai.","german":"The report does not mention that problem.","furigana":"ほうこくしょ|もんだい|げんきゅう"},{"japanese":"首相は増税について言及を避けた。","romaji":"Shushō wa zōzei ni tsuite genkyū o saketa.","german":"The prime minister avoided referring to the tax increase.","furigana":"しゅしょう|ぞうぜい|げんきゅう|さ"},{"japanese":"論文の中で、先行研究にも言及しておくべきだ。","romaji":"Ronbun no naka de, senkō kenkyū ni mo genkyū shite oku beki da.","german":"In your paper, you should also refer to previous research.","furigana":"ろんぶん|なか|せんこうけんきゅう|げんきゅう"}],"notes":"言及 means to come to speak of something or to refer to it. As an intransitive する-verb it takes に or について: 〜に言及する, 〜について言及する; also common is 言及を避ける/控える \"to not bring something up\". It is formal and typical of reports, news and academic texts; in conversation one more often says 触れる (話に触れる). Compared with 指摘 (\"to point out a matter\"), 言及 is more neutral.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"発話の意図を正確に理解することが大切だ。","romaji":"Hatsuwa no ito o seikaku ni rikai suru koto ga taisetsu da.","german":"It is important to understand the intention behind an utterance accurately.","furigana":"はつわ|いと|せいかく|りかい|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"子どもの最初の発話は、一歳前後に現れることが多い。","romaji":"Kodomo no saisho no hatsuwa wa, issai zengo ni arawareru koto ga ōi.","german":"A child's first utterances most often appear around the age of one.","furigana":"こ|さいしょ|はつわ|いっさいぜんご|あらわ|おお"},{"japanese":"音声認識システムが利用者の発話を文字に変換する。","romaji":"Onsei ninshiki shisutemu ga riyōsha no hatsuwa o moji ni henkan suru.","german":"The speech recognition system converts the user's speech into text.","furigana":"おんせいにんしき|りようしゃ|はつわ|もじ|へんかん"}],"notes":"発話 is a technical term of linguistics for a concrete linguistic utterance or for speaking itself: 発話の意図, 子どもの発話, 発話する. The speech act in the sense of pragmatics is more precisely called 発話行為. In everyday speech one doesn't say 発話 but 話す or 言う; 発言 is a contribution to a discussion, for example in a meeting. Also common in speech recognition and speech therapy.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"談話分析は言語学の重要な研究領域だ。","romaji":"Danwa bunseki wa gengogaku no jūyō na kenkyū ryōiki da.","german":"Discourse analysis is an important research area of linguistics.","furigana":"だんわぶんせき|げんごがく|じゅうよう|けんきゅうりょういき"},{"japanese":"戦後五十年に際し、首相談話が発表された。","romaji":"Sengo gojūnen ni saishi, shushō danwa ga happyō sareta.","german":"On the 50th anniversary of the end of the war, a statement by the prime minister was released.","furigana":"せんごごじゅうねん|さい|しゅしょうだんわ|はっぴょう"},{"japanese":"病院の談話室で、患者たちが楽しそうに話していた。","romaji":"Byōin no danwashitsu de, kanja-tachi ga tanoshisō ni hanashite ita.","german":"In the hospital lounge, the patients were chatting happily.","furigana":"びょういん|だんわしつ|かんじゃ|たの|はな"}],"notes":"談話 has three uses: a formal word for \"conversation, talk\" (談話する, 談話室 \"lounge\"), an official statement that has not been formally resolved (首相談話, 官房長官談話, such as the well-known 村山談話), and in linguistics \"discourse\" (談話分析). For an ordinary conversation, one says 会話 or 話 in everyday speech; 談話 sounds elevated or official.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"文字論では文字体系の構造と機能を研究する。","romaji":"Mojiron de wa moji taikei no kōzō to kinō o kenkyū suru.","german":"Graphemics studies the structure and function of writing systems.","furigana":"もじろん|もじたいけい|こうぞう|きのう|けんきゅう"},{"japanese":"日本語の文字論では、漢字と仮名の使い分けがよく議論される。","romaji":"Nihongo no mojiron de wa, kanji to kana no tsukaiwake ga yoku giron sareru.","german":"In studies of Japanese writing, the choice between kanji and kana is often discussed.","furigana":"にほんご|もじろん|かんじ|かな|つか|わ|ぎろん"},{"japanese":"文字論の講義で、表音文字と表語文字の違いを学んだ。","romaji":"Mojiron no kōgi de, hyōon moji to hyōgo moji no chigai o mananda.","german":"In the lecture on writing theory, we learned the difference between phonographic and logographic scripts.","furigana":"もじろん|こうぎ|ひょうおんもじ|ひょうごもじ|ちが|まな"}],"notes":"文字論 is the subfield of linguistics that studies writing and writing systems, that is, graphemics or theory of writing. In Japanese this often concerns the interplay of kanji, hiragana and katakana, and 表語文字 (logographic script) and 表音文字 (phonographic script). Related is 文字学, which more traditionally studies the origin and forms of individual characters, especially kanji. A purely academic term.","levelNote":"Compound: 文字 (N3) + 論 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"対照言語学は二つの言語を比較対照する。","romaji":"Taishō gengogaku wa futatsu no gengo o hikaku taishō suru.","german":"Contrastive linguistics compares and contrasts two languages.","furigana":"たいしょうげんごがく|ふた|げんご|ひかくたいしょう"},{"japanese":"対照言語学は、比較言語学と違って言語の系統を問わない。","romaji":"Taishō gengogaku wa, hikaku gengogaku to chigatte gengo no keitō o towanai.","german":"Unlike comparative linguistics, contrastive linguistics does not ask about the genealogy of languages.","furigana":"たいしょうげんごがく|ひかくげんごがく|ちが|げんご|けいとう|と"},{"japanese":"対照言語学の知見は、外国語教育にも役立つ。","romaji":"Taishō gengogaku no chiken wa, gaikokugo kyōiku ni mo yakudatsu.","german":"Findings of contrastive linguistics are also useful for foreign language teaching.","furigana":"たいしょうげんごがく|ちけん|がいこくごきょういく|やくだ"}],"notes":"対照言語学 compares two or more languages synchronically, that is, in their present state, to work out similarities and differences, often with foreign language teaching in mind. To be distinguished from it is 比較言語学, historical-comparative linguistics, which asks about language relationships and common proto-languages. Adjectivally: 対照言語学的な研究. An academic technical term.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ビジネスメールでは適切な敬意表現を使う必要がある。","romaji":"Bijinesu mēru de wa tekisetsu na keii hyōgen o tsukau hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"In business emails, you need to use appropriate respectful expressions.","furigana":"てきせつ|けいいひょうげん|つか|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"「恐れ入りますが」のような前置きも、敬意表現の一つだ。","romaji":"\"Osoreirimasu ga\" no yō na maeoki mo, keii hyōgen no hitotsu da.","german":"Introductory phrases such as 「恐れ入りますが」 are also a kind of respectful expression.","furigana":"おそ|い|まえお|けいいひょうげん|ひと"},{"japanese":"敬語を使わなくても、言葉の選び方で敬意表現はできる。","romaji":"Keigo o tsukawanakute mo, kotoba no erabikata de keii hyōgen wa dekiru.","german":"Even without using keigo, you can express respect through your choice of words.","furigana":"けいご|つか|ことば|えら|かた|けいいひょうげん"}],"notes":"敬意表現 is any linguistic form with which one expresses respect and consideration toward the other person. The term is broader than 敬語 (the grammatical honorific forms 尊敬語, 謙譲語, 丁寧語): it also includes introductory phrases such as 恐れ入りますが, indirect requests and considerate word choice. Still more general is 待遇表現, which also includes derogatory expressions. A technical term from language policy and Japanese language teaching.","levelNote":"Compound: 敬意 (N3) + 表現 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「書いて」の「い」は音便による変化だ。","romaji":"\"Kaite\" no \"i\" wa onbin ni yoru henka da.","german":"The 「い」 in 「書いて」 is a change due to onbin.","furigana":"か|おんびん|へんか"},{"japanese":"「読んで」は撥音便、「待って」は促音便の例だ。","romaji":"\"Yonde\" wa hatsuonbin, \"matte\" wa sokuonbin no rei da.","german":"「読んで」 is an example of the nasal onbin, and 「待って」 of the geminate onbin.","furigana":"よ|はつおんびん|ま|そくおんびん|れい"},{"japanese":"「ありがとう」も、「ありがたく」がウ音便で変化した形だ。","romaji":"\"Arigatō\" mo, \"arigataku\" ga u-onbin de henka shita katachi da.","german":"「ありがとう」 is also a form that changed from 「ありがたく」 through the u-onbin.","furigana":"おんびん|へんか|かたち"}],"notes":"音便 is a historical sound change for easier pronunciation, especially in the て／た-form of verbs: イ音便 (書いて), 促音便 (待って), 撥音便 (読んで) and ウ音便 (ありがたく → ありがとう, おはやく → おはよう). It explains why the て-form of godan verbs looks so irregular. The word is a grammatical technical term; one says 音便が起こる or 〜は音便による変化だ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"歴史的仮名遣いと現代仮名遣いの違いは転呼に関わる。","romaji":"Rekishi-teki kanazukai to gendai kanazukai no chigai wa tenko ni kakawaru.","german":"The difference between historical and modern kana orthography is related to tenko.","furigana":"れきしてきかなづか|げんだいかなづか|ちが|てんこ|かか"},{"japanese":"「かは」を「かわ」と読むのは、ハ行転呼の結果だ。","romaji":"\"Kaha\" o \"kawa\" to yomu no wa, ha-gyō tenko no kekka da.","german":"Reading 「かは」 as \"kawa\" is a result of the ha-row sound change.","furigana":"よ|ぎょうてんこ|けっか"},{"japanese":"助詞の「は」を「わ」と発音するのも、転呼の名残だ。","romaji":"Joshi no \"ha\" o \"wa\" to hatsuon suru no mo, tenko no nagori da.","german":"Pronouncing the particle 「は」 as \"wa\" is also a remnant of tenko.","furigana":"じょし|はつおん|てんこ|なごり"}],"notes":"転呼 (short for 転呼音) is a sound change by which pronunciation moves away from the traditional kana spelling. The most important example is the ハ行転呼: inside a word, は・ひ・ふ・へ・ほ became わ・い・う・え・お, hence かは → かわ, おもふ → おもう. The pronunciation of the particles は and へ as \"wa\" and \"e\" also goes back to it. A technical term of historical linguistics.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"上代日本語には母音調和の痕跡が見られる。","romaji":"Jōdai Nihongo ni wa boin chōwa no konseki ga mirareru.","german":"Traces of vowel harmony can be seen in Old Japanese.","furigana":"じょうだいにほんご|ぼいんちょうわ|こんせき|み"},{"japanese":"トルコ語やフィンランド語には母音調和がある。","romaji":"Torukogo ya Finrandogo ni wa boin chōwa ga aru.","german":"Turkish and Finnish have vowel harmony.","furigana":"ご|ご|ぼいんちょうわ"},{"japanese":"母音調和がある言語では、接尾辞の母音が語幹の母音に合わせて変わる。","romaji":"Boin chōwa ga aru gengo de wa, setsubiji no boin ga gokan no boin ni awasete kawaru.","german":"In languages with vowel harmony, the vowel of a suffix changes to match the vowels of the stem.","furigana":"ぼいんちょうわ|げんご|せつびじ|ぼいん|ごかん|ぼいん|あ|か"}],"notes":"母音調和 is vowel harmony: in a word only vowels from the same group (for example front or back vowels) may occur, and suffixes adapt their vowel to the stem. Well-known examples are Turkish, Finnish, Hungarian and Mongolian. For Old Japanese (上代日本語), a remnant of vowel harmony is discussed. A purely linguistic technical term.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「因縁」を「いんねん」と読むのは連声の例だ。","romaji":"\"Innen\" o \"innen\" to yomu no wa renjō no rei da.","german":"Reading 「因縁」 as 「いんねん」 is an example of renjō.","furigana":"いんねん|よ|れんじょう|れい"},{"japanese":"「天皇」を「てんのう」と読むのも、連声によるものだ。","romaji":"\"Tennō\" o \"tennō\" to yomu no mo, renjō ni yoru mono da.","german":"Reading 「天皇」 as 「てんのう」 is also due to renjō.","furigana":"てんのう|よ|れんじょう"},{"japanese":"日本語史の授業で、連声という現象を初めて知った。","romaji":"Nihongo-shi no jugyō de, renjō to iu genshō o hajimete shitta.","german":"In a class on the history of the Japanese language, I learned about the phenomenon called renjō for the first time.","furigana":"にほんごし|じゅぎょう|れんじょう|げんしょう|はじ|し"}],"notes":"連声 is generally \"sandhi\"; in Japanese it specifically means a historical sound change in kanji compounds: if the first element ends in n (or formerly m, t) and the second begins with a vowel, y or w, this becomes na-, ni-, no- etc.: 因縁 いん＋えん → いんねん, 観音 かん＋おん → かんのん, 天皇 てん＋おう → てんのう. Today no new cases arise. The word is purely technical (phonology, language history).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"教学の質を向上させるための施策が講じられた。","romaji":"Kyōgaku no shitsu o kōjō saseru tame no shisaku ga kōjirareta.","german":"Measures were taken to improve the quality of teaching and study.","furigana":"きょうがく|しつ|こうじょう|しさく|こう"},{"japanese":"大学の運営は、経営と教学の両面から考える必要がある。","romaji":"Daigaku no un'ei wa, keiei to kyōgaku no ryōmen kara kangaeru hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"In running a university, you have to consider both management and academic affairs.","furigana":"だいがく|うんえい|けいえい|きょうがく|りょうめん|かんが|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"教学担当の副学長が、カリキュラム改革を進めている。","romaji":"Kyōgaku tantō no fukugakuchō ga, karikyuramu kaikaku o susumete iru.","german":"The vice president in charge of academic affairs is pushing ahead with curriculum reform.","furigana":"きょうがくたんとう|ふくがくちょう|かいかく|すす"}],"notes":"教学 means \"teaching and study\" and today appears mainly in university administration: 教学部門 or 教学担当 stand for the academic side, as opposed to 経営 (finance, administration); 教学改革 is a study reform. In Buddhist contexts, 教学 denotes the doctrine or dogmatics of a school (天台教学). A formal, official word; in everyday speech one says 教育.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は国際的な名声を博している。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kokusaiteki na meisei o haku shite iru.","german":"She enjoys an international reputation.","furigana":"かのじょ|こくさいてき|めいせい|はく"},{"japanese":"その小説で、彼は一躍作家としての名声を得た。","romaji":"Sono shōsetsu de, kare wa ichiyaku sakka to shite no meisei o eta.","german":"With that novel, he suddenly gained fame as a writer.","furigana":"しょうせつ|かれ|いちやくさっか|めいせい|え"},{"japanese":"一度のスキャンダルで、長年築いた名声が地に落ちた。","romaji":"Ichido no sukyandaru de, naganen kizuita meisei ga chi ni ochita.","german":"A single scandal destroyed the reputation he had built up over many years.","furigana":"いちど|ながねんきず|めいせい|ち|お"}],"notes":"名声 is the good reputation, fame and standing that someone enjoys in public, always positive. Typical combinations: 名声を博する/得る/高める, 名声が高まる, 名声が地に落ちる \"the reputation is ruined\". 評判 is more everyday and can also be bad (評判が悪い); 名誉 is \"honor\", 有名 simply \"well-known\". Elevated style.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その決定は政府の威信に関わる問題だ。","romaji":"Sono kettei wa seifu no ishin ni kakawaru mondai da.","german":"That decision is a matter that affects the government's prestige.","furigana":"けってい|せいふ|いしん|かか|もんだい"},{"japanese":"両国は威信をかけて宇宙開発競争を繰り広げた。","romaji":"Ryōkoku wa ishin o kakete uchū kaihatsu kyōsō o kurihirogeta.","german":"The two countries staged a space race with their prestige at stake.","furigana":"りょうこく|いしん|うちゅうかいはつきょうそう|く|ひろ"},{"japanese":"相次ぐ不祥事で、警察の威信は大きく揺らいだ。","romaji":"Aitsugu fushōji de, keisatsu no ishin wa ōkiku yuraida.","german":"A string of scandals has badly shaken the police's standing.","furigana":"あいつ|ふしょうじ|けいさつ|いしん|おお|ゆ"}],"notes":"威信 is the prestige and dignity that an institution, a state or a leader projects outward: 国家の威信, 政府の威信. Typical: 威信をかける \"to stake one's whole prestige\", 威信が揺らぐ/失墜する, 威信を保つ/回復する. Unlike 名声 (personal fame), it concerns authority and respect; 権威 stresses authority based on power or expertise. Formal, frequent in the news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"貴族階級は多くの特権を享受していた。","romaji":"Kizoku kaikyū wa ōku no tokken o kyōju shite ita.","german":"The aristocratic class enjoyed many privileges.","furigana":"きぞくかいきゅう|おお|とっけん|きょうじゅ"},{"japanese":"失敗できるのは若者の特権だ。","romaji":"Shippai dekiru no wa wakamono no tokken da.","german":"Being allowed to fail is the privilege of the young.","furigana":"しっぱい|わかもの|とっけん"},{"japanese":"その外交官は、外交特権によって逮捕を免れた。","romaji":"Sono gaikōkan wa, gaikō tokken ni yotte taiho o manugareta.","german":"The diplomat escaped arrest thanks to diplomatic immunity.","furigana":"がいこうかん|がいこうとっけん|たいほ|"}],"notes":"特権 is a prerogative that belongs only to certain persons or groups: 特権を持つ/与える/享受する/乱用する, 特権階級, 外交特権 \"diplomatic immunity\". Casually it can also mean \"privilege\" in a figurative sense: 若者の特権. 権利 is a general right that can belong to everyone; 特典 is a perk, for example for customers or members.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"指導者としての責務を全うする覚悟がある。","romaji":"Shidōsha toshite no sekimu o mattō suru kakugo ga aru.","german":"I am prepared to fully carry out my duty as a leader.","furigana":"しどうしゃ|せきむ||かくご"},{"japanese":"親には子どもを教育する責務がある。","romaji":"Oya ni wa kodomo o kyōiku suru sekimu ga aru.","german":"Parents have a duty to educate their children.","furigana":"おや|こ|きょういく|せきむ"},{"japanese":"企業は環境保護に対する社会的責務を果たさなければならない。","romaji":"Kigyō wa kankyō hogo ni taisuru shakaiteki sekimu o hatasanakereba naranai.","german":"Companies must fulfill their social responsibility for environmental protection.","furigana":"きぎょう|かんきょうほご|たい|しゃかいてきせきむ|は"}],"notes":"責務 is the duty and responsibility that falls to one because of a role or position. Typical: 責務を果たす/全うする/負う, 〜としての責務, 社会的責務. 責任 is more general and also includes liability and blame (責任を取る); 義務 is more the (legal) obligation, and 任務 a concrete assignment. 責務 sounds formal and solemn, as in speeches and official texts.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"謙虚さは日本文化における美徳とされている。","romaji":"Kenkyo-sa wa Nihon bunka ni okeru bitoku to sarete iru.","german":"Modesty is regarded as a virtue in Japanese culture.","furigana":"けんきょ|にほんぶんか|びとく"},{"japanese":"我慢を美徳とする考え方が、過労の一因になっている。","romaji":"Gaman o bitoku to suru kangaekata ga, karō no ichiin ni natte iru.","german":"The attitude of regarding endurance as a virtue is one cause of overwork.","furigana":"がまん|びとく|かんが|かた|かろう|いちいん"},{"japanese":"時間を守ることは、どの国でも美徳だろう。","romaji":"Jikan o mamoru koto wa, dono kuni de mo bitoku darō.","german":"Punctuality is probably regarded as a virtue in every country.","furigana":"じかん|まも|くに|びとく"}],"notes":"美徳 is a virtue or a trait regarded as good: 〜を美徳とする, 〜は美徳とされる, 日本人の美徳. The opposite is 悪徳. The term is also often used critically, for example when endurance or restraint is exalted as a virtue (我慢を美徳とする風潮). 徳 alone means more moral integrity (徳が高い), and 長所 simply a strength. Neutral to elevated.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"悪徳商法に騙される消費者が後を絶たない。","romaji":"Akutoku shōhō ni damasareru shōhisha ga ato o tatanai.","german":"There is no end to consumers falling for fraudulent sales practices.","furigana":"あくとくしょうほう|だま|しょうひしゃ|あと|た"},{"japanese":"悪徳業者に高額な修理代を請求された。","romaji":"Akutoku gyōsha ni kōgaku na shūridai o seikyū sareta.","german":"An unscrupulous company charged me an exorbitant repair bill.","furigana":"あくとくぎょうしゃ|こうがく|しゅうりだい|せいきゅう"},{"japanese":"怠惰は古くから悪徳の一つに数えられてきた。","romaji":"Taida wa furuku kara akutoku no hitotsu ni kazoerarete kita.","german":"Sloth has been counted among the vices since ancient times.","furigana":"たいだ|ふる|あくとく|ひと|かぞ"}],"notes":"悪徳 as a noun means \"vice, immorality\" and is the opposite of 美徳; used this way it sounds rather literary. Today it is much more often found as a modifier before a noun in the sense of \"unscrupulous, fraudulent\": 悪徳商法 (fraudulent sales methods), 悪徳業者, 悪徳弁護士. For individual bad habits one more often says 悪癖 or 悪い癖.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"因習にとらわれず新しい発想を取り入れるべきだ。","romaji":"Inshū ni torawarezu atarashii hassō o toriireru beki da.","german":"We should not be bound by outdated customs but take in new ideas.","furigana":"いんしゅう|あたら|はっそう|と|い"},{"japanese":"古い因習を打破しようとした若者たちは、村で孤立した。","romaji":"Furui inshū o daha shiyō to shita wakamono-tachi wa, mura de koritsu shita.","german":"The young people who tried to break the old customs were isolated in the village.","furigana":"ふる|いんしゅう|だは|わかもの|むら|こりつ"},{"japanese":"その小説は、因習に縛られた山奥の村を舞台にしている。","romaji":"Sono shōsetsu wa, inshū ni shibarareta yamaoku no mura o butai ni shite iru.","german":"The novel is set in a remote mountain village bound by old customs.","furigana":"しょうせつ|いんしゅう|しば|やまおく|むら|ぶたい"}],"notes":"因習 is a time-honored custom that is felt to be outdated and constraining; the word is clearly negative. Typical: 因習にとらわれる/縛られる, 因習を打破する, 古い因習, 因習的な村. An older spelling is 因襲. Neutral, by contrast, are 習慣 (habit) and 慣習 (custom, also in law), and positive is 伝統 (tradition). Written language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"外国人の排斥は社会の分断を招く。","romaji":"Gaikokujin no haiseki wa shakai no bundan o maneku.","german":"The exclusion of foreigners leads to the division of society.","furigana":"がいこくじん|はいせき|しゃかい|ぶんだん|まね"},{"japanese":"異なる意見を持つ人を排斥するような組織は長続きしない。","romaji":"Kotonaru iken o motsu hito o haiseki suru yō na soshiki wa nagatsuzuki shinai.","german":"An organization that shuts out people with different opinions will not last long.","furigana":"こと|いけん|も|ひと|はいせき|そしき|ながつづ"},{"japanese":"二十世紀初頭、アメリカでは日本人移民の排斥運動が起きた。","romaji":"Nijusseiki shotō, Amerika de wa Nihonjin imin no haiseki undō ga okita.","german":"In the early 20th century, a movement to exclude Japanese immigrants arose in the United States.","furigana":"にじゅっせいきしょとう|にほんじんいみん|はいせきうんどう|お"}],"notes":"排斥 means actively excluding, pushing out or boycotting people, groups or goods; as a transitive する-verb: 〜を排斥する, passive 排斥される. Fixed combination: 排斥運動 \"boycott or exclusion movement\". 排除 is more general \"to eliminate, remove\" (also obstacles), 差別 is discrimination, and 疎外 is more the state of being left out or alienated. Formal, frequent in history and politics.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"都市化の進行は地域社会からの疎外感を生む。","romaji":"Toshika no shinkō wa chiiki shakai kara no sogaikan o umu.","german":"Advancing urbanization produces a sense of alienation from the local community.","furigana":"としか|しんこう|ちいきしゃかい|そがいかん|う"},{"japanese":"転校したばかりの頃は、クラスで疎外感を味わった。","romaji":"Tenkō shita bakari no koro wa, kurasu de sogaikan o ajiwatta.","german":"Right after changing schools, I felt left out in the class.","furigana":"てんこう|ころ|そがいかん|あじ"},{"japanese":"マルクスは、労働者が自らの労働から疎外されると論じた。","romaji":"Marukusu wa, rōdōsha ga mizukara no rōdō kara sogai sareru to ronjita.","german":"Marx argued that workers are alienated from their own labor.","furigana":"ろうどうしゃ|みずか|ろうどう|そがい|ろん"}],"notes":"疎外 means to exclude someone or to become alien to something; a transitive する-verb, but mostly used in the passive: 疎外される \"to be excluded\". Most common is 疎外感 \"the feeling of being left out\" (疎外感を覚える/味わう). In philosophy and sociology it is \"alienation\" (人間疎外, 労働の疎外). Colloquially, for being excluded from a group one says 仲間外れ; actively driving someone out is more 排斥.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"民族間の融和を促進する政策が実施された。","romaji":"Minzoku kan no yūwa o sokushin suru seisaku ga jisshi sareta.","german":"Policies to promote harmony between ethnic groups were implemented.","furigana":"みんぞくかん|ゆうわ|そくしん|せいさく|じっし"},{"japanese":"選挙後、新大統領は国民の融和を訴えた。","romaji":"Senkyo-go, shin-daitōryō wa kokumin no yūwa o uttaeta.","german":"After the election, the new president appealed for national reconciliation.","furigana":"せんきょご|しんだいとうりょう|こくみん|ゆうわ|うった"},{"japanese":"合併した二社の社員の融和を図るため、合同研修が行われた。","romaji":"Gappei shita nisha no shain no yūwa o hakaru tame, gōdō kenshū ga okonawareta.","german":"A joint training session was held to bring together the employees of the two merged companies.","furigana":"がっぺい|にしゃ|しゃいん|ゆうわ|はか|ごうどうけんしゅう|おこな"}],"notes":"融和 is the growing together and reconciliation of groups that were previously separate or hostile: 融和を図る/促進する/訴える, 民族の融和, 融和ムード. As a する-verb it is intransitive (両国が融和する). 和解 stresses the settlement of a concrete dispute, 調和 the harmony of elements (色の調和). Careful: the identically read 宥和 means \"appeasement\" (宥和政策 = appeasement policy). Formal, typical of politics and news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"党内の分裂が政権運営に支障をきたしている。","romaji":"Tōnai no bunretsu ga seiken un'ei ni shishō o kitashite iru.","german":"The split within the party is hampering the running of the government.","furigana":"とうない|ぶんれつ|せいけんうんえい|ししょう"},{"japanese":"細胞は分裂を繰り返して増えていく。","romaji":"Saibō wa bunretsu o kurikaeshite fuete iku.","german":"Cells multiply through repeated division.","furigana":"さいぼう|ぶんれつ|く|かえ|ふ"},{"japanese":"意見の対立から、バンドは二つに分裂した。","romaji":"Iken no tairitsu kara, bando wa futatsu ni bunretsu shita.","german":"Because of differences of opinion, the band split into two.","furigana":"いけん|たいりつ|ふた|ぶんれつ"}],"notes":"分裂 means that a whole breaks up into parts from within; as a する-verb it is intransitive: 党が分裂する, 〜に分裂する. It is used for organizations (党内分裂) but also in natural science: 細胞分裂, 核分裂. 分割, by contrast, is deliberate, transitive dividing (〜を分割する), and 分断 is cutting apart or separating, often from outside (社会の分断). Neutral to formal.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"労働者の搾取に対する抗議運動が広がった。","romaji":"Rōdōsha no sakushu ni taisuru kōgi undō ga hirogatta.","german":"Protest movements against the exploitation of workers spread.","furigana":"ろうどうしゃ|さくしゅ|たい|こうぎうんどう|ひろ"},{"japanese":"低賃金で長時間働かせるのは、明らかな搾取だ。","romaji":"Teichingin de chōjikan hatarakaseru no wa, akiraka na sakushu da.","german":"Making people work long hours for low pay is clearly exploitation.","furigana":"ていちんぎん|ちょうじかんはたら|あき|さくしゅ"},{"japanese":"「好きな仕事だから」と無給で働かせるのは、やりがい搾取と呼ばれる。","romaji":"\"Suki na shigoto da kara\" to mukyū de hatarakaseru no wa, yarigai sakushu to yobareru.","german":"Making people work unpaid on the grounds that \"you love your job\" is called passion exploitation.","furigana":"す|しごと|むきゅう|はたら|さくしゅ|よ"}],"notes":"搾取 is the exploitation of people, especially of their labor; as a transitive する-verb: 労働者を搾取する, passive 搾取される. Fixed expressions: 中間搾取 (exploitation by middlemen), 搾取工場 (\"sweatshop\") and, newer and colloquial, やりがい搾取, exploitation by appealing to one's passion for the work. The literal meaning \"to squeeze out, to milk\" is rare. 酷使 means \"to overwork\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"言論の自由に対する抑圧は民主主義の根幹を揺るがす。","romaji":"Genron no jiyū ni taisuru yokuatsu wa minshushugi no konkan o yurugasu.","german":"The suppression of freedom of speech shakes the foundations of democracy.","furigana":"げんろん|じゆう|たい|よくあつ|みんしゅしゅぎ|こんかん|ゆ"},{"japanese":"少数民族は長年、政府に抑圧されてきた。","romaji":"Shōsū minzoku wa naganen, seifu ni yokuatsu sarete kita.","german":"Ethnic minorities have been oppressed by the government for years.","furigana":"しょうすうみんぞく|ながねん|せいふ|よくあつ"},{"japanese":"怒りを抑圧し続けると、心の健康に悪影響が出る。","romaji":"Ikari o yokuatsu shitsuzukeru to, kokoro no kenkō ni akueikyō ga deru.","german":"If you keep suppressing your anger, it harms your mental health.","furigana":"いか|よくあつ|つづ|こころ|けんこう|あくえいきょう|で"}],"notes":"抑圧 refers to the forcible or systematic holding down of people, freedoms or feelings; as a transitive する-verb: 自由を抑圧する, passive 抑圧される. Typical are 抑圧された人々 and 〜に対する抑圧. Unlike 弾圧 (\"violent suppression\" by the state or police, very concrete), 抑圧 is more general and also used psychologically (感情を抑圧する \"to repress feelings\"). 抑制, by contrast, just means \"to curb, restrain\" neutrally (costs, inflation). Written language.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"政府による反体制派への弾圧が国際的に非難された。","romaji":"Seifu ni yoru hantaiseiha e no dan'atsu ga kokusaiteki ni hinan sareta.","german":"The government's repression of dissidents was condemned internationally.","furigana":"せいふ|はんたいせいは|だんあつ|こくさいてき|ひなん"},{"japanese":"江戸時代、キリスト教は厳しい弾圧を受けた。","romaji":"Edo jidai, Kirisutokyō wa kibishii dan'atsu o uketa.","german":"In the Edo period, Christianity suffered harsh persecution.","furigana":"えどじだい|きょう|きび|だんあつ|う"},{"japanese":"軍は平和的なデモを武力で弾圧した。","romaji":"Gun wa heiwa-teki na demo o buryoku de dan'atsu shita.","german":"The military put down the peaceful demonstration by force.","furigana":"ぐん|へいわてき|ぶりょく|だんあつ"}],"notes":"弾圧 is the violent suppression of opponents, religious groups or free speech by a power, usually the state: 〜を弾圧する, 弾圧を受ける, 弾圧を加える, 言論弾圧, 宗教弾圧. 抑圧 is more general (also feelings or social constraints), and 鎮圧 means putting down a concrete uprising. Typical of news, history and politics.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"宗教的迫害から逃れるために多くの人が亡命した。","romaji":"Shūkyōteki hakugai kara nogareru tame ni ōku no hito ga bōmei shita.","german":"Many people went into exile to escape religious persecution.","furigana":"しゅうきょうてきはくがい|のが|おお|ひと|ぼうめい"},{"japanese":"彼は政治的な意見のせいで迫害を受けた。","romaji":"Kare wa seijiteki na iken no sei de hakugai o uketa.","german":"He was persecuted because of his political opinions.","furigana":"かれ|せいじてき|いけん|はくがい|う"},{"japanese":"この資料館では、ナチスによるユダヤ人迫害の歴史を学べる。","romaji":"Kono shiryōkan de wa, Nachisu ni yoru Yudayajin hakugai no rekishi o manaberu.","german":"At this museum, you can learn about the history of the persecution of the Jews by the Nazis.","furigana":"しりょうかん|じんはくがい|れきし|まな"}],"notes":"迫害 is the targeted persecution and harassment of people because of religion, origin or conviction. Mostly passive: 迫害を受ける or 迫害される \"to be persecuted\"; active 〜を迫害する. Typical combinations: 宗教的迫害, 迫害から逃れる, ユダヤ人迫害. Unlike 抑圧 (\"suppression\" of freedoms), 迫害 stresses the oppression and suffering of concrete groups; in asylum law 迫害 is the technical term for \"persecution\". Written language.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"政治難民に庇護を与えることは国際法上の義務だ。","romaji":"Seiji nanmin ni higo o ataeru koto wa kokusaihōjō no gimu da.","german":"Granting protection to political refugees is an obligation under international law.","furigana":"せいじなんみん|ひご|あた|こくさいほうじょう|ぎむ"},{"japanese":"彼女は両親の庇護のもとで何不自由なく育った。","romaji":"Kanojo wa ryōshin no higo no moto de nani fujiyū naku sodatta.","german":"She grew up under her parents' protection without wanting for anything.","furigana":"かのじょ|りょうしん|ひご|なにふじゆう|そだ"},{"japanese":"その画家は有力な商人の庇護を受けて活動を続けた。","romaji":"Sono gaka wa yūryoku na shōnin no higo o ukete katsudō o tsuzuketa.","german":"The painter was able to continue his work with the patronage of an influential merchant.","furigana":"がか|ゆうりょく|しょうにん|ひご|う|かつどう|つづ"}],"notes":"庇護 is the protection that a stronger party grants a weaker one, often with the image of a \"protecting wing\". Typical: 〜の庇護のもとで（育つ）, 庇護を受ける, 庇護を与える, 庇護する. In asylum law it stands for \"protection/asylum\" (庇護申請 \"asylum application\"). In everyday speech one more often says 保護 or 守る; 庇護 sounds elevated and stresses dependence on the protector (parents, patron, state). Since 庇 is not a jōyō kanji, newspapers often add a reading aid.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仲裁委員会の裁定に双方が従うことになった。","romaji":"Chūsai iinkai no saitei ni sōhō ga shitagau koto ni natta.","german":"Both sides agreed to follow the ruling of the arbitration committee.","furigana":"ちゅうさいいいんかい|さいてい|そうほう|したが"},{"japanese":"労働委員会は会社側の主張を認めない裁定を下した。","romaji":"Rōdō iinkai wa kaishasha no shuchō o mitomenai saitei o kudashita.","german":"The labor commission issued a ruling rejecting the company's position.","furigana":"ろうどういいんかい||しゅちょう|みと|さいてい|くだ"},{"japanese":"協会はその試合の結果を無効と裁定した。","romaji":"Kyōkai wa sono shiai no kekka o mukō to saitei shita.","german":"The association ruled the result of that match invalid.","furigana":"きょうかい|しあい|けっか|むこう|さいてい"}],"notes":"裁定 is the binding decision of a competent body in a dispute: an arbitration panel, an authority, a judging panel. Typical: 裁定を下す \"to issue a ruling\", 裁定に従う, 〜と裁定する. Unlike 判決 (the judgment of an ordinary court) and 判定 (an assessment, e.g. by score judges, ビデオ判定), 裁定 refers more to arbitration and official decisions, for example in labor law or a sports federation. Written language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地方自治の強化は民主主義の発展に不可欠だ。","romaji":"Chihō jichi no kyōka wa minshushugi no hatten ni fukaketsu da.","german":"Strengthening local self-government is essential for the development of democracy.","furigana":"ちほうじち|きょうか|みんしゅしゅぎ|はってん|ふかけつ"},{"japanese":"マンションの自治会で、ごみ出しのルールを話し合った。","romaji":"Manshon no jichikai de, gomidashi no rūru o hanashiatta.","german":"At our apartment building's residents' association, we discussed the rules for taking out the garbage.","furigana":"じちかい|だ|はな|あ"},{"japanese":"その地域の住民は、より広い自治を求めている。","romaji":"Sono chiiki no jūmin wa, yori hiroi jichi o motomete iru.","german":"The residents of that region are demanding broader autonomy.","furigana":"ちいき|じゅうみん|ひろ|じち|もと"}],"notes":"自治 means \"self-government, autonomy\": a group manages its own affairs. Very common in compounds: 地方自治 \"local self-government\", 自治体 \"municipality, local public body\", 自治会 \"neighborhood or residents' association\", 学生自治 / 自治区 \"autonomous region\". On its own it mostly appears with を認める / を求める. Unlike 独立 (\"independence\"), with 自治 one remains part of a larger whole. Neutral to official.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人権意識の啓発活動に力を入れている。","romaji":"Jinken ishiki no keihatsu katsudō ni chikara o irete iru.","german":"We are putting great effort into awareness-raising activities on human rights.","furigana":"じんけんいしき|けいはつかつどう|ちから|い"},{"japanese":"彼は通勤電車でいつも自己啓発の本を読んでいる。","romaji":"Kare wa tsūkin densha de itsumo jiko keihatsu no hon o yonde iru.","german":"He always reads self-improvement books on the commuter train.","furigana":"かれ|つうきんでんしゃ|じこけいはつ|ほん|よ"},{"japanese":"先輩の働き方に大いに啓発された。","romaji":"Senpai no hatarakikata ni ōi ni keihatsu sareta.","german":"I was greatly inspired by my senior colleague's way of working.","furigana":"せんぱい|はたら|かた|おお|けいはつ"}],"notes":"啓発 means to awaken someone's awareness or knowledge: \"enlightenment, raising awareness, inspiration\". Very common: 啓発活動 \"awareness-raising work\", 意識を啓発する, 啓発ポスター, and 自己啓発 \"self-improvement, personal development\" (自己啓発本 = self-help literature). The passive 啓発される means \"to be stimulated, inspired\". The historical \"Enlightenment\" of philosophy, by contrast, is 啓蒙. Neutral to official.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"煽情的な報道は社会不安を助長する恐れがある。","romaji":"Senjōteki na hōdō wa shakai fuan o jochō suru osore ga aru.","german":"Sensationalist reporting may foster social unrest.","furigana":"せんじょうてき|ほうどう|しゃかいふあん|じょちょう|おそ"},{"japanese":"週刊誌は事件を煽情的な見出しで取り上げた。","romaji":"Shūkanshi wa jiken o senjōteki na midashi de toriageta.","german":"The weekly magazine covered the case with lurid headlines.","furigana":"しゅうかんし|じけん|せんじょうてき|みだ|と|あ"},{"japanese":"その広告は煽情的すぎるとして批判を浴びた。","romaji":"Sono kōkoku wa senjōteki sugiru to shite hihan o abita.","german":"The advertisement was criticized as too provocative.","furigana":"こうこく|せんじょうてき|ひはん|あ"}],"notes":"煽情 is the deliberate stirring up of emotions, especially in the media. Almost only as 煽情的（な）: 煽情的な報道 \"sensationalist reporting\", 煽情的な見出し. It can also mean sexually provocative (煽情的な服装, 煽情的なポーズ). Today it is mostly written 扇情 or 扇情的, since 煽 is not a jōyō kanji. Related is 煽る \"to stir up, incite\". Written language, critical and judgmental.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"国民に対する欺瞞行為は断じて許されない。","romaji":"Kokumin ni taisuru giman kōi wa danjite yurusarenai.","german":"Deceit toward the public must never be tolerated.","furigana":"こくみん|たい|ぎまんこうい|だん|ゆる"},{"japanese":"「環境に優しい」という宣伝は欺瞞に満ちている。","romaji":"\"Kankyō ni yasashii\" to iu senden wa giman ni michite iru.","german":"The \"eco-friendly\" advertising is full of deception.","furigana":"かんきょう|やさ|せんでん|ぎまん|み"},{"japanese":"「自分は悪くない」と思い込むのは自己欺瞞だ。","romaji":"\"Jibun wa warukunai\" to omoikomu no wa jiko giman da.","german":"Convincing yourself that \"it's not my fault\" is self-deception.","furigana":"じぶん|わる|おも|こ|じこぎまん"}],"notes":"欺瞞 is deliberate deception, the pretense of false facts, often in a moral or political context. Typical: 欺瞞に満ちた, 欺瞞的な, 欺瞞行為, 自己欺瞞 \"self-deception\". Unlike 詐欺 (fraud as a crime to obtain money) and だます (colloquial \"to trick\"), 欺瞞 sounds elevated and criticizes hypocrisy: 〜は欺瞞だ \"... is eyewash\". As a verb, 欺瞞する is hardly used.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"卑劣な手段を用いて政敵を陥れようとした。","romaji":"Hiretsu na shudan o mochiite seiteki o otoshiireyō to shita.","german":"He tried to bring down his political rival by despicable means.","furigana":"ひれつ|しゅだん|もち|せいてき|おとしい"},{"japanese":"お年寄りをだましてお金を奪うのは卑劣な犯罪だ。","romaji":"Otoshiyori o damashite okane o ubau no wa hiretsu na hanzai da.","german":"Deceiving elderly people and taking their money is a despicable crime.","furigana":"としよ|かね|うば|ひれつ|はんざい"},{"japanese":"匿名で人を攻撃するなんて卑劣だ。","romaji":"Tokumei de hito o kōgeki suru nante hiretsu da.","german":"Attacking people anonymously is cowardly and low.","furigana":"とくめい|ひと|こうげき|ひれつ"}],"notes":"卑劣 describes morally contemptible, cowardly behavior, especially underhanded action against the defenseless. As a な-adjective: 卑劣な手段, 卑劣な犯行, 卑劣な男; predicative 卑劣だ. Frequent in news about fraud or attacks (卑劣な犯罪). Stronger and more moral than ずるい (\"unfair, mean\" in everyday speech) and more formal than 汚い (\"dirty, unfair\"). The card type \"adjective\" here means a な-adjective.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"狡猾な手口で巨額の資金を横領した。","romaji":"Kōkatsu na teguchi de kyogaku no shikin o ōryō shita.","german":"He embezzled a huge sum of money by cunning methods.","furigana":"こうかつ|てぐち|きょがく|しきん|おうりょう"},{"japanese":"昔話では、キツネはよく狡猾な動物として描かれる。","romaji":"Mukashibanashi de wa, kitsune wa yoku kōkatsu na dōbutsu to shite egakareru.","german":"In folktales, the fox is often portrayed as a sly animal.","furigana":"むかしばなし|こうかつ|どうぶつ|えが"},{"japanese":"彼は狡猾に立ち回って、責任を部下に押し付けた。","romaji":"Kare wa kōkatsu ni tachimawatte, sekinin o buka ni oshitsuketa.","german":"He maneuvered craftily and shifted the responsibility onto his subordinates.","furigana":"かれ|こうかつ|た|まわ|せきにん|ぶか|お|つ"}],"notes":"狡猾 means \"sly, crafty\": clever, but in an underhanded, self-serving way. Always negative, unlike 賢い (\"clever\") or 抜け目ない (\"shrewd, savvy\", rather neutral). Typical: 狡猾な手口, 狡猾な男, 狡猾に立ち回る. In everyday speech one more often says ずる賢い. Written language; neither kanji is a jōyō kanji, so a reading aid is often added.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"権力は人を堕落させる傾向がある。","romaji":"Kenryoku wa hito o daraku saseru keikō ga aru.","german":"Power tends to corrupt people.","furigana":"けんりょく|ひと|だらく|けいこう"},{"japanese":"大学に入ってから、彼は遊んでばかりの堕落した生活を送っている。","romaji":"Daigaku ni haitte kara, kare wa asonde bakari no daraku shita seikatsu o okutte iru.","german":"Since entering university, he has been leading a dissolute life, doing nothing but playing around.","furigana":"だいがく|はい|かれ|あそ|だらく|せいかつ|おく"},{"japanese":"相次ぐ汚職事件は政治の堕落を示している。","romaji":"Aitsugu oshoku jiken wa seiji no daraku o shimeshite iru.","german":"The successive corruption cases show the moral decay of politics.","furigana":"あいつ|おしょくじけん|せいじ|だらく|しめ"}],"notes":"堕落 is the moral descent of a person or institution: \"depravity, corruption\". As an intransitive する-verb: 人が堕落する, 堕落した生活; causative 〜を堕落させる \"to corrupt\". Also common: 政治の堕落, 堕落した政治家. In the religious sense it is the \"Fall\" or falling away from the faith. In contrast to 衰退 (\"decline\" of power or economy), 堕落 is always about morals and way of life.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"社会の退廃を憂える声が高まっている。","romaji":"Shakai no taihai o ureeru koe ga takamatte iru.","german":"Voices lamenting the decay of society are growing louder.","furigana":"しゃかい|たいはい|うれ|こえ|たか"},{"japanese":"この映画は、世紀末の退廃的な雰囲気をうまく描いている。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa, seikimatsu no taihai-teki na fun'iki o umaku egaite iru.","german":"This film captures well the decadent atmosphere of the fin de siècle.","furigana":"えいが|せいきまつ|たいはいてき|ふんいき|えが"},{"japanese":"戦後の混乱の中で、都市の風紀は大きく退廃した。","romaji":"Sengo no konran no naka de, toshi no fūki wa ōkiku taihai shita.","german":"In the chaos of the postwar period, public morals in the cities declined greatly.","furigana":"せんご|こんらん|なか|とし|ふうき|おお|たいはい"}],"notes":"退廃 is the moral or cultural decay of a society, era or artistic movement; the older spelling is 頽廃. Very common is the adjective 退廃的 \"decadent\" (退廃的な雰囲気, 退廃的な美). As an intransitive する-verb: 風紀が退廃する. For purely material decay of buildings, one more often says 荒廃 or 老朽化. The word is written language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"公務員には廉潔な姿勢が求められる。","romaji":"Kōmuin ni wa renketsu na shisei ga motomerareru.","german":"Civil servants are expected to have an upright attitude.","furigana":"こうむいん|れんけつ|しせい|もと"},{"japanese":"その知事は廉潔な人柄で、賄賂を一切受け取らなかった。","romaji":"Sono chiji wa renketsu na hitogara de, wairo o issai uketoranakatta.","german":"The governor was a man of integrity and never accepted bribes.","furigana":"ちじ|れんけつ|ひとがら|わいろ|いっさいう|と"},{"japanese":"組織のトップには何よりも廉潔さが必要だ。","romaji":"Soshiki no toppu ni wa nani yori mo renketsusa ga hitsuyō da.","german":"Above all, integrity is needed at the top of an organization.","furigana":"そしき|なに|れんけつ|ひつよう"}],"notes":"廉潔 means \"honest and incorruptible\", especially in dealing with money and power. It is used as a な-adjective (廉潔な人物, 廉潔な姿勢) or as a noun (廉潔さ, 廉潔を旨とする). The word is rare and very elevated; more common are 清廉 and the four-character phrase 清廉潔白. Typical context: the ethics of civil servants and politicians.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"清廉潔白な人物こそ指導者にふさわしい。","romaji":"Seiren keppaku na jinbutsu koso shidōsha ni fusawashii.","german":"Only a person of unblemished integrity is fit to be a leader.","furigana":"せいれんけっぱく|じんぶつ|しどうしゃ"},{"japanese":"疑惑を受けた大臣は、記者会見で清廉潔白を主張した。","romaji":"Giwaku o uketa daijin wa, kisha kaiken de seiren keppaku o shuchō shita.","german":"The minister, who had come under suspicion, insisted on his innocence at a press conference.","furigana":"ぎわく|う|だいじん|きしゃかいけん|せいれんけっぱく|しゅちょう"},{"japanese":"祖父は清廉な人柄で、町の人から信頼されていた。","romaji":"Sofu wa seiren na hitogara de, machi no hito kara shinrai sarete ita.","german":"My grandfather was a man of integrity and was trusted by the people of the town.","furigana":"そふ|せいれん|ひとがら|まち|ひと|しんらい"}],"notes":"清廉 means \"pure, honest\", especially free from greed and corruptibility. Mostly in the phrase 清廉潔白 \"spotless, with a clean slate\" (清廉潔白な人, 清廉潔白を主張する \"to protest one's innocence\"). Alone as a な-adjective: 清廉な人柄, 清廉な政治家. Equal in meaning but rarer is 廉潔. Elevated; in everyday speech one more often says 正直 or まじめ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"社会福祉への献身的な活動が評価された。","romaji":"Shakai fukushi e no kenshinteki na katsudō ga hyōka sareta.","german":"His devoted work for social welfare was recognized.","furigana":"しゃかいふくし|けんしんてき|かつどう|ひょうか"},{"japanese":"母は病気の祖母を献身的に看病した。","romaji":"Haha wa byōki no sobo o kenshinteki ni kanbyō shita.","german":"My mother nursed my sick grandmother devotedly.","furigana":"はは|びょうき|そぼ|けんしんてき|かんびょう"},{"japanese":"彼女は生涯を難民の支援に献身した。","romaji":"Kanojo wa shōgai o nanmin no shien ni kenshin shita.","german":"She devoted her whole life to supporting refugees.","furigana":"かのじょ|しょうがい|なんみん|しえん|けんしん"}],"notes":"献身 is selfless devotion to a person or cause. Very common adverbially or adjectivally: 献身的に看病する, 献身的な努力 / 支え. As an intransitive する-verb with に: 〜に献身する \"to devote oneself to ...\". Unlike 犠牲 (\"sacrifice\"), 献身 stresses active, voluntary devotion; 貢献, by contrast, only means \"to make a contribution\". Neutral to elevated, strongly positive.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"博愛精神に基づいた国際支援が求められている。","romaji":"Hakuai seishin ni motozuita kokusai shien ga motomerarete iru.","german":"International aid based on a spirit of philanthropy is called for.","furigana":"はくあいせいしん|もと|こくさいしえん|もと"},{"japanese":"フランスの標語は「自由・平等・博愛」だ。","romaji":"Furansu no hyōgo wa \"jiyū, byōdō, hakuai\" da.","german":"The motto of France is 「自由・平等・博愛」 (\"liberty, equality, fraternity\").","furigana":"ひょうご|じゆう|びょうどう|はくあい"},{"japanese":"赤十字は博愛の理念のもとに設立された。","romaji":"Sekijūji wa hakuai no rinen no moto ni setsuritsu sareta.","german":"The Red Cross was founded on the principle of love for humanity.","furigana":"せきじゅうじ|はくあい|りねん|せつりつ"}],"notes":"博愛 is love for all people without distinction: \"love of humanity, brotherhood\". Typical: 博愛主義 \"philanthropy\", 博愛精神, 博愛の心. Known from the motto of the French Revolution 自由・平等・博愛 \"liberty, equality, fraternity\". Unlike 慈善 (\"charity\" as concrete help), 博愛 denotes the attitude. Elevated, hardly used in everyday conversation.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"互恵的な経済関係の構築が両国の利益になる。","romaji":"Gokeiteki na keizai kankei no kōchiku ga ryōkoku no rieki ni naru.","german":"Building economically reciprocal relations benefits both countries.","furigana":"ごけいてき|けいざいかんけい|こうちく|りょうこく|りえき"},{"japanese":"両首脳は戦略的互恵関係を推進することで一致した。","romaji":"Ryō shunō wa senryakuteki gokei kankei o suishin suru koto de itchi shita.","german":"The two leaders agreed to promote a mutually beneficial strategic relationship.","furigana":"りょうしゅのう|せんりゃくてきごけいかんけい|すいしん|いっち"},{"japanese":"この大学間交流は互恵の原則に基づいている。","romaji":"Kono daigakukan kōryū wa gokei no gensoku ni motozuite iru.","german":"This exchange between universities is based on the principle of reciprocity.","furigana":"だいがくかんこうりゅう|ごけい|げんそく|もと"}],"notes":"互恵 means \"reciprocity, mutual benefit\", especially in diplomacy and trade. It appears almost only in compounds or with 的: 互恵関係, 互恵的な協力, 互恵主義, 互恵の精神. Well known is 戦略的互恵関係 \"strategic relationship of mutual benefit\" (Japan-China relations). In everyday speech one says お互いのためになる or ウィンウィン. Very formal.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"戦後の窮乏生活を体験した世代は少なくなっている。","romaji":"Sengo no kyūbō seikatsu o taiken shita sedai wa sukunaku natte iru.","german":"Fewer and fewer people belong to the generation that experienced the destitution of the postwar years.","furigana":"せんご|きゅうぼうせいかつ|たいけん|せだい|すく"},{"japanese":"度重なる戦争で藩の財政は窮乏した。","romaji":"Tabikasanaru sensō de han no zaisei wa kyūbō shita.","german":"Due to repeated wars, the domain's finances fell into dire straits.","furigana":"たびかさ|せんそう|はん|ざいせい|きゅうぼう"},{"japanese":"失業が長引き、一家は窮乏に耐えながら暮らした。","romaji":"Shitsugyō ga nagabiki, ikka wa kyūbō ni taenagara kurashita.","german":"Unemployment dragged on, and the family lived enduring great hardship.","furigana":"しつぎょう|ながび|いっか|きゅうぼう|た|く"}],"notes":"窮乏 denotes a state of bitter poverty and deprivation in which even the necessities are lacking. Typical: 窮乏生活, 財政の窮乏 \"financial distress (of public budgets)\", 窮乏に耐える; as an intransitive する-verb 生活が窮乏する. Stronger and more literary than 貧乏 (\"poor, poverty\" in everyday speech) and more formal than 困窮 (similar, \"hardship\", frequent in official language: 生活困窮者). Mainly in historical or economic texts.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"歴史は帝国の興亡を繰り返してきた。","romaji":"Rekishi wa teikoku no kōbō o kurikaeshite kita.","german":"History has seen the rise and fall of empires again and again.","furigana":"れきし|ていこく|こうぼう|く|かえ"},{"japanese":"この小説は戦国大名の興亡を描いている。","romaji":"Kono shōsetsu wa sengoku daimyō no kōbō o egaite iru.","german":"This novel depicts the rise and fall of the warlords of the Sengoku period.","furigana":"しょうせつ|せんごくだいみょう|こうぼう|えが"},{"japanese":"IT業界では企業の興亡が激しい。","romaji":"Aitī gyōkai de wa kigyō no kōbō ga hageshii.","german":"In the IT industry, companies rise and fall at a furious pace.","furigana":"ぎょうかい|きぎょう|こうぼう|はげ"}],"notes":"興亡 is the \"rise and fall\" of states, dynasties, companies or cultures; 興 \"to flourish\", 亡 \"to perish\". Typical: 国家の興亡, 興亡の歴史, 〜の興亡を描く, and the four-character phrases 興亡盛衰 and 栄枯盛衰 (\"prosperity and decline\"). Unlike 盛衰 (\"ups and downs\", also for customs or fashions), 興亡 stresses the emergence and disappearance of whole powers. Written language, especially in history texts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"現在は旧制度から新制度への過渡期にある。","romaji":"Genzai wa kyū seido kara shin seido e no katoki ni aru.","german":"We are currently in a transitional period from the old system to the new one.","furigana":"げんざい|きゅうせいど|しんせいど|かとき"},{"japanese":"思春期は子どもから大人への過渡期だと言われる。","romaji":"Shishunki wa kodomo kara otona e no katoki da to iwareru.","german":"Adolescence is said to be the transitional period from child to adult.","furigana":"ししゅんき|こ|おとな|かとき|い"},{"japanese":"これはあくまで過渡的な措置で、来年には見直される。","romaji":"Kore wa akumade katoteki na sochi de, rainen ni wa minaosareru.","german":"This is merely a transitional measure and will be reviewed next year.","furigana":"かとてき|そち|らいねん|みなお"}],"notes":"過渡 means \"transition from the old to the new\" and occurs practically only in compounds: 過渡期 \"transitional period\", 過渡的（な） \"provisional, transitional\", 過渡的措置 \"transitional measure\", and in engineering 過渡現象 \"transient phenomenon\". 過渡 hardly stands alone. The old meaning \"ferry, crossing\" is obsolete. In everyday speech, for individual processes, one says 移行 or 切り替え. Neutral to written.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"組織の刷新を図るため新たな人材を登用した。","romaji":"Soshiki no sasshin o hakaru tame arata na jinzai o tōyō shita.","german":"New talent was brought in to pursue the renewal of the organization.","furigana":"そしき|さっしん|はか|あら|じんざい|とうよう"},{"japanese":"不祥事を受けて、会社は経営陣を刷新した。","romaji":"Fushōji o ukete, kaisha wa keieijin o sasshin shita.","german":"Following the scandal, the company replaced its entire management.","furigana":"ふしょうじ|う|かいしゃ|けいえいじん|さっしん"},{"japanese":"アプリのデザインが全面的に刷新された。","romaji":"Apuri no dezain ga zenmenteki ni sasshin sareta.","german":"The design of the app was completely overhauled.","furigana":"ぜんめんてき|さっしん"}],"notes":"刷新 is a fundamental renewal in which the old and its abuses are eliminated. As a transitive する-verb: 組織を刷新する, 人事を刷新する, デザインを刷新する; passive 刷新される. Typical after scandals: 人心の刷新 \"a fresh start to regain trust\". Stronger than 改善 (\"improvement\") and more concrete than 改革 (\"reform\" of systems); 更新, by contrast, only means \"to update, renew (a contract)\". Neutral to formal, often in business and politics.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新興国の台頭が国際秩序を変えつつある。","romaji":"Shinkōkoku no taitō ga kokusai chitsujo o kaetsutsu aru.","german":"The rise of emerging nations is changing the international order.","furigana":"しんこうこく|たいとう|こくさいちつじょ|か"},{"japanese":"多くの国で極右政党が台頭している。","romaji":"Ōku no kuni de kyokuu seitō ga taitō shite iru.","german":"Far-right parties are on the rise in many countries.","furigana":"おお|くに|きょくうせいとう|たいとう"},{"japanese":"今シーズンは若手選手の台頭が目立った。","romaji":"Kon shīzun wa wakate senshu no taitō ga medatta.","german":"This season, the emergence of young players stood out.","furigana":"こん|わかてせんしゅ|たいとう|めだ"}],"notes":"台頭 is the emergence and strengthening of a new force that was previously in the background: \"rise, gaining strength\". As an intransitive する-verb: 新興国が台頭する, 〜の台頭. Frequent for states, movements, ideologies and young talents (若手の台頭). Unlike 上昇 (\"rise\" of values) and 出世 (\"career\" of a person), 台頭 describes emerging in the balance of power, often with a slightly threatening undertone (ナショナリズムの台頭). Also written 擡頭 (outdated).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"内部対立が激化し組織は瓦解した。","romaji":"Naibu tairitsu ga gekika shi soshiki wa gakai shita.","german":"Internal conflicts intensified and the organization fell apart.","furigana":"ないぶたいりつ|げきか|そしき|がかい"},{"japanese":"江戸幕府の瓦解とともに、武士の時代は終わった。","romaji":"Edo bakufu no gakai to tomo ni, bushi no jidai wa owatta.","german":"With the collapse of the Edo shogunate, the age of the samurai ended.","furigana":"えどばくふ|がかい|ぶし|じだい|お"},{"japanese":"主要メンバーが抜けて、計画は一気に瓦解した。","romaji":"Shuyō menbā ga nukete, keikaku wa ikki ni gakai shita.","german":"When the key members left, the plan collapsed all at once.","furigana":"しゅよう|ぬ|けいかく|いっき|がかい"}],"notes":"瓦解 literally means \"to fall apart like roof tiles\": the sudden, complete collapse of an organization, regime or plan as soon as one part gives way. As an intransitive する-verb: 組織が瓦解する, 政権の瓦解. Unlike 崩壊 (generally \"collapse, caving in\", also of buildings), 瓦解 is used only figuratively. Written language, typical in history and political reports.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その企業は倒産に瀕している。","romaji":"Sono kigyō wa tōsan ni hin shite iru.","german":"That company is on the verge of bankruptcy.","furigana":"きぎょう|とうさん|ひん"},{"japanese":"この鳥は絶滅の危機に瀕している。","romaji":"Kono tori wa zetsumetsu no kiki ni hin shite iru.","german":"This bird is in danger of extinction.","furigana":"とり|ぜつめつ|きき|ひん"},{"japanese":"財政破綻に瀕した町は、大胆な改革に乗り出した。","romaji":"Zaisei hatan ni hin shita machi wa, daitan na kaikaku ni noridashita.","german":"The town, facing a financial collapse, embarked on bold reforms.","furigana":"ざいせいはたん|ひん|まち|だいたん|かいかく|の|だ"}],"notes":"瀕する means \"to stand directly before something bad\" and always takes に: 危機に瀕する, 絶滅の危機に瀕した動物, 倒産に瀕する, 死に瀕する. Almost always in the form 瀕している or attributively 〜に瀕した. It is more elevated than 〜しそうだ or 寸前だ and occurs mainly in news and factual texts. The noun 瀕死 \"near death\" (瀕死の重傷) belongs with it.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"政府の怠慢を糾弾する声明が発表された。","romaji":"Seifu no taiman o kyūdan suru seimei ga happyō sareta.","german":"A statement denouncing the government's negligence was released.","furigana":"せいふ|たいまん|きゅうだん|せいめい|はっぴょう"},{"japanese":"記者は会社の不正を記事で厳しく糾弾した。","romaji":"Kisha wa kaisha no fusei o kiji de kibishiku kyūdan shita.","german":"The reporter sharply denounced the company's wrongdoing in his article.","furigana":"きしゃ|かいしゃ|ふせい|きじ|きび|きゅうだん"},{"japanese":"差別的な発言をした議員は、各方面から糾弾された。","romaji":"Sabetsuteki na hatsugen o shita giin wa, kakuhōmen kara kyūdan sareta.","german":"The legislator who made discriminatory remarks was denounced from all quarters.","furigana":"さべつてき|はつげん|ぎいん|かくほうめん|きゅうだん"}],"notes":"糾弾 is the public, sharp denunciation of misconduct or responsibility: \"to denounce, to pillory\". As a transitive する-verb: 不正を糾弾する, 責任を糾弾する; passive 糾弾される, or 糾弾を浴びる / 受ける. Stronger than 批判 (\"criticism\") and 非難 (\"reproach, blame\"); 糾弾 presupposes that someone is to be held accountable. Written language, frequent in politics and the media.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人権擁護のための国際的な枠組みが整備されつつある。","romaji":"Jinken yōgo no tame no kokusaiteki na wakugumi ga seibi saretsutsu aru.","german":"An international framework for the protection of human rights is gradually being put in place.","furigana":"じんけんようご|こくさいてき|わくぐ|せいび"},{"japanese":"批判が集まる中、彼だけが同僚を擁護した。","romaji":"Hihan ga atsumaru naka, kare dake ga dōryō o yōgo shita.","german":"As criticism mounted, he alone stood up for his colleague.","furigana":"ひはん|あつ|なか|かれ|どうりょう|ようご"},{"japanese":"その団体は表現の自由の擁護を訴えている。","romaji":"Sono dantai wa hyōgen no jiyū no yōgo o uttaete iru.","german":"The organization is calling for the defense of freedom of expression.","furigana":"だんたい|ひょうげん|じゆう|ようご|うった"}],"notes":"擁護 means to protect something valuable against attack or to stand up for someone: \"to defend, protect, advocate\". Typical: 人権擁護, 憲法擁護, 〜を擁護する発言. Unlike 防衛 (military defense) and 保護 (\"protection\" of the weak, of nature), 擁護 is often about principles or defending a person against criticism. Neutral to formal.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は社会改革の必要性を声高に唱えている。","romaji":"Kare wa shakai kaikaku no hitsuyōsei o kowadaka ni tonaete iru.","german":"He is loudly advocating the need for social reform.","furigana":"かれ|しゃかいかいかく|ひつようせい||とな"},{"japanese":"お坊さんが静かにお経を唱えていた。","romaji":"Obōsan ga shizuka ni okyō o tonaete ita.","german":"The monk was quietly reciting a sutra.","furigana":"ぼう|しず|きょう|とな"},{"japanese":"会議で一人だけ、その計画に異議を唱えた。","romaji":"Kaigi de hitori dake, sono keikaku ni igi o tonaeta.","german":"In the meeting, only one person raised an objection to the plan.","furigana":"かいぎ|ひとり|けいかく|いぎ|とな"}],"notes":"唱える is transitive (〜を唱える) and has three uses: reciting formulas aloud (お経を唱える \"to recite sutras\", 呪文を唱える \"to speak a spell\"), calling something out loud (万歳を唱える \"to cheer banzai\") and publicly advocating an opinion (新説を唱える, 異議を唱える \"to raise an objection\"). In the last sense it is more elevated than 主張する and stresses putting forward a new or dissenting position. Do not confuse with 歌う (\"to sing\") or 述べる (\"to state\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"薬物乱用の蔓延を食い止める対策が急務だ。","romaji":"Yakubutsu ran'yō no man'en o kuitomeru taisaku ga kyūmu da.","german":"Measures to stop the spread of drug abuse are urgently needed.","furigana":"やくぶつらんよう|まんえん|く|と|たいさく|きゅうむ"},{"japanese":"冬になると、インフルエンザが学校で蔓延しやすい。","romaji":"Fuyu ni naru to, infuruenza ga gakkō de man'en shiyasui.","german":"In winter, influenza spreads easily in schools.","furigana":"ふゆ|がっこう|まんえん"},{"japanese":"その会社には「どうせ変わらない」というあきらめが蔓延していた。","romaji":"Sono kaisha ni wa \"dōse kawaranai\" to iu akirame ga man'en shite ita.","german":"A resigned attitude of \"nothing will change anyway\" had spread throughout the company.","furigana":"かいしゃ|か|まんえん"}],"notes":"蔓延 describes the uncontrolled spread of something undesirable, like vines overgrowing everything: diseases, drugs, corruption, moods. As an intransitive する-verb: 感染症が蔓延する, 〜の蔓延を防ぐ. Since 蔓 is not a jōyō kanji, government agencies write まん延 (e.g. まん延防止等重点措置 during the Covid period). Unlike neutral 普及 (\"spread\" of technology) and 広がる, 蔓延 is always negative. Written language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"貧困の根絶は国際社会の共通目標だ。","romaji":"Hinkon no konzetsu wa kokusai shakai no kyōtsū mokuhyō da.","german":"The eradication of poverty is a common goal of the international community.","furigana":"ひんこん|こんぜつ|こくさいしゃかい|きょうつうもくひょう"},{"japanese":"天然痘はワクチンによって根絶された。","romaji":"Tennentō wa wakuchin ni yotte konzetsu sareta.","german":"Smallpox was eradicated through vaccines.","furigana":"てんねんとう|こんぜつ"},{"japanese":"学校はいじめを根絶するために新しいルールを作った。","romaji":"Gakkō wa ijime o konzetsu suru tame ni atarashii rūru o tsukutta.","german":"The school created new rules to eradicate bullying completely.","furigana":"がっこう|こんぜつ|あたら|つく"}],"notes":"根絶 literally means \"to cut at the root\": to eliminate something evil completely and finally. As a transitive する-verb: 暴力を根絶する, passive 根絶される; common: 〜の根絶を目指す. Similar is 撲滅, which stresses the active fight (campaigns, slogans), while 根絶 stresses the complete result (天然痘は根絶された). 絶滅, on the other hand, is the extinction of species. Written language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"犯罪撲滅キャンペーンが全国的に展開された。","romaji":"Hanzai bokumetsu kyanpēn ga zenkokuteki ni tenkai sareta.","german":"A nationwide campaign to stamp out crime was launched.","furigana":"はんざいぼくめつ|ぜんこくてき|てんかい"},{"japanese":"駅前に「飲酒運転撲滅」のポスターが貼られている。","romaji":"Ekimae ni \"inshu unten bokumetsu\" no posutā ga hararete iru.","german":"A poster saying 「飲酒運転撲滅」 is put up in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|いんしゅうんてんぼくめつ|は"},{"japanese":"農家は協力して害虫を撲滅しようとしている。","romaji":"Nōka wa kyōryoku shite gaichū o bokumetsu shiyō to shite iru.","german":"The farmers are working together to exterminate the pests.","furigana":"のうか|きょうりょく|がいちゅう|ぼくめつ"}],"notes":"撲滅 means \"to wipe out by determined fighting\" and stands above all for campaigns against crime, diseases or pests. Very common as a compound with the target in front: 犯罪撲滅, 飲酒運転撲滅, がん撲滅, 害虫撲滅; as a verb 〜を撲滅する. Compared with 根絶 (\"to pull out by the root\", result), 撲滅 stresses the fight itself and has a poster-like feel, typical for slogans and posters. Written language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人間の尊厳を守ることは法の根本原理だ。","romaji":"Ningen no songen o mamoru koto wa hō no konpon genri da.","german":"Protecting human dignity is the fundamental principle of law.","furigana":"にんげん|そんげん|まも|ほう|こんぽんげんり"},{"japanese":"介護では、高齢者の尊厳を傷つけない言葉遣いが大切だ。","romaji":"Kaigo de wa, kōreisha no songen o kizutsukenai kotobazukai ga taisetsu da.","german":"In nursing care, it is important to use language that does not hurt the dignity of the elderly.","furigana":"かいご|こうれいしゃ|そんげん|きず|ことばづか|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"尊厳死をめぐる議論は今も続いている。","romaji":"Songenshi o meguru giron wa ima mo tsuzuite iru.","german":"The debate over death with dignity continues to this day.","furigana":"そんげんし|ぎろん|いま|つづ"}],"notes":"尊厳 is the inviolable dignity of a human being or of something sublime. Typical: 人間の尊厳, 個人の尊厳 (a constitutional term), 尊厳を守る / 傷つける / 保つ, 尊厳死 \"dying with dignity (passive euthanasia)\". Unlike 威厳 (\"dignified bearing, authority\" of a person) and プライド / 誇り (\"pride\"), 尊厳 denotes a value that belongs to everyone. Elevated, frequent in law, medicine and ethics.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"遵法精神を持つ市民の育成が社会の基盤を支える。","romaji":"Junpō seishin o motsu shimin no ikusei ga shakai no kiban o sasaeru.","german":"Raising citizens with respect for the law supports the foundation of society.","furigana":"じゅんぽうせいしん|も|しみん|いくせい|しゃかい|きばん|ささ"},{"japanese":"不祥事が続き、社員の遵法意識が問われている。","romaji":"Fushōji ga tsuzuki, shain no junpō ishiki ga towarete iru.","german":"After a series of scandals, employees' awareness of the law is being questioned.","furigana":"ふしょうじ|つづ|しゃいん|じゅんぽういしき|と"},{"japanese":"組合は賃上げを求めて遵法闘争に入った。","romaji":"Kumiai wa chin'age o motomete junpō tōsō ni haitta.","german":"The union began a work-to-rule action to demand wage increases.","furigana":"くみあい|ちんあ|もと|じゅんぽうとうそう|はい"}],"notes":"遵法 means \"to obey the laws, law-abidingness\". It appears almost only in compounds: 遵法精神, 遵法意識, 遵法闘争 \"work-to-rule\" (a form of labor action in which regulations are followed overly strictly). Also written 順法. For \"compliance\" in companies, one says 法令遵守 or コンプライアンス; the verb \"to keep rules\" is 守る or 遵守する. Formal, official.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"国民の総意に基づいて憲法は改正されるべきだ。","romaji":"Kokumin no sōi ni motozuite kenpō wa kaisei sareru beki da.","german":"The constitution should be amended on the basis of the general will of the people.","furigana":"こくみん|そうい|もと|けんぽう|かいせい"},{"japanese":"これは部員全員の総意として決めたことです。","romaji":"Kore wa buin zen'in no sōi to shite kimeta koto desu.","german":"This was decided as the collective will of all the club members.","furigana":"ぶいんぜんいん|そうい|き"},{"japanese":"住民の総意をまとめるのは簡単ではなかった。","romaji":"Jūmin no sōi o matomeru no wa kantan de wa nakatta.","german":"It was not easy to bring together a common will among the residents.","furigana":"じゅうみん|そうい|かんたん"}],"notes":"総意 is the common will or consensus of all members of a group. Typical: 国民の総意, 全員の総意, 〜の総意として, 総意に基づく. Known from Article 1 of the Japanese Constitution (天皇の地位は…日本国民の総意に基く). Unlike 合意 (\"agreement\" between parties after negotiation) and 多数意見 (\"majority opinion\"), 総意 denotes the will of the whole. Formal.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"帰属意識の希薄化が地域コミュニティの衰退を招いている。","romaji":"Kizoku ishiki no kihakuka ga chiiki komyuniti no suitai o maneite iru.","german":"The weakening of the sense of belonging is leading to the decline of local communities.","furigana":"きぞくいしき|きはくか|ちいき|すいたい|まね"},{"japanese":"島の帰属をめぐって、両国は長年対立している。","romaji":"Shima no kizoku o megutte, ryōkoku wa naganen tairitsu shite iru.","german":"The two countries have been in conflict for years over who the island belongs to.","furigana":"しま|きぞく|りょうこく|ながねんたいりつ"},{"japanese":"この作品の著作権は会社に帰属する。","romaji":"Kono sakuhin no chosakuken wa kaisha ni kizoku suru.","german":"The copyright of this work belongs to the company.","furigana":"さくひん|ちょさくけん|かいしゃ|きぞく"}],"notes":"帰属 means that something belongs to, or is attributed to, a group, a state or a person: 〜に帰属する. Common are 帰属意識 \"sense of belonging\", 領土の帰属 \"sovereignty over a territory\" and, legally, 権利は〜に帰属する. For everyday membership in a company, club or department, one says 所属. The word is formal and appears in law, politics and sociology.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"排他的な態度は国際協力の妨げとなる。","romaji":"Haitateki na taido wa kokusai kyōryoku no samatage to naru.","german":"Eine abschottende Haltung behindert die internationale Zusammenarbeit.","furigana":"はいたてき|たいど|こくさいきょうりょく|さまた"},{"japanese":"その村は排他的で、よそ者がなかなか受け入れられない。","romaji":"Sono mura wa haitateki de, yosomono ga nakanaka ukeirerarenai.","german":"Das Dorf ist sehr verschlossen, Fremde werden kaum aufgenommen.","furigana":"むら|はいたてき|もの|う|い"},{"japanese":"漁船が他国の排他的経済水域で拿捕された。","romaji":"Gyosen ga takoku no haitateki keizai suiiki de daho sareta.","german":"Ein Fischerboot wurde in der ausschließlichen Wirtschaftszone eines anderen Staates aufgebracht.","furigana":"ぎょせん|たこく|はいたてきけいざいすいいき|"}],"notes":"排他 bedeutet „andere ausschließen“ und kommt praktisch nur in Komposita vor, vor allem 排他的（な） „ausgrenzend, abschottend“: 排他的な態度, 排他的な村社会. Fachbegriffe sind 排他的経済水域 „ausschließliche Wirtschaftszone“ und in der IT 排他制御 „gegenseitiger Ausschluss (Locking)“. Achtung: Das deutsche „exklusiv“ im Sinne von „edel“ heißt nicht 排他的; das wäre 高級 oder 限定. Formell, in der Gesellschaftskritik negativ wertend.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"社会的包摂を推進する政策が注目を集めている。","romaji":"Shakaiteki hōsetsu o suishin suru seisaku ga chūmoku o atsumete iru.","german":"Policies promoting social inclusion are attracting attention.","furigana":"しゃかいてきほうせつ|すいしん|せいさく|ちゅうもく|あつ"},{"japanese":"多様な価値観を包摂できる組織こそが強い。","romaji":"Tayō na kachikan o hōsetsu dekiru soshiki koso ga tsuyoi.","german":"Organizations that can embrace diverse values are the strong ones.","furigana":"たよう|かちかん|ほうせつ|そしき|つよ"},{"japanese":"論理学では「犬」という概念は「動物」に包摂される。","romaji":"Ronrigaku de wa \"inu\" to iu gainen wa \"dōbutsu\" ni hōsetsu sareru.","german":"In logic, the concept \"dog\" is subsumed under the concept \"animal\".","furigana":"ろんりがく|いぬ|がいねん|どうぶつ|ほうせつ"}],"notes":"包摂 means \"to include, to encompass\". In social policy, 社会的包摂 is the technical term for \"social inclusion\" (opposite 社会的排除), and also 包摂的な社会. In logic and philosophy, 包摂 means \"subsumption\": a concept is placed under a more general one (AはBに包摂される). As a verb it is transitive: 〜を包摂する. In everyday speech one more often says 含む or 受け入れる; for inclusion in schools also インクルージョン. Technical and written language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地域間の経済的不均衡が社会問題を引き起こしている。","romaji":"Chiikikan no keizaiteki fukinkō ga shakai mondai o hikiokoshite iru.","german":"Economic imbalance between regions is causing social problems.","furigana":"ちいきかん|けいざいてきふきんこう|しゃかいもんだい|ひ|お"},{"japanese":"両国の間では長年、貿易の不均衡が続いている。","romaji":"Ryōkoku no aida de wa naganen, bōeki no fukinkō ga tsuzuite iru.","german":"A trade imbalance has persisted between the two countries for years.","furigana":"りょうこく|あいだ|ながねん|ぼうえき|ふきんこう|つづ"},{"japanese":"この学部は男女比が不均衡だ。","romaji":"Kono gakubu wa danjohi ga fukinkō da.","german":"The gender ratio in this faculty is unbalanced.","furigana":"がくぶ|だんじょひ|ふきんこう"}],"notes":"不均衡 is an imbalance between quantities that should actually be in balance: trade, income, regions, nutrition. Used as a noun (貿易不均衡, 需給の不均衡) and as a な-adjective (不均衡な配分). Opposite: 均衡 \"balance\". More neutral and technical than 格差 (\"gap, inequality\" between groups) and more formal than アンバランス or バランスが悪い in everyday speech. Frequent in economics and statistics.","levelNote":"Compound: 不 (N3) + 均衡 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"環境保護と経済発展の相克は現代社会の重要課題だ。","romaji":"Kankyō hogo to keizai hatten no sōkoku wa gendai shakai no jūyō kadai da.","german":"The conflict between environmental protection and economic development is an important issue of modern society.","furigana":"かんきょうほご|けいざいはってん|そうこく|げんだいしゃかい|じゅうようかだい"},{"japanese":"この小説は、義務と愛情の相克に苦しむ主人公を描いている。","romaji":"Kono shōsetsu wa, gimu to aijō no sōkoku ni kurushimu shujinkō o egaite iru.","german":"The novel portrays a protagonist suffering from the conflict between duty and affection.","furigana":"しょうせつ|ぎむ|あいじょう|そうこく|くる|しゅじんこう|えが"},{"japanese":"父と息子の相克は、三代にわたる家族の物語の中心にある。","romaji":"Chichi to musuko no sōkoku wa, sandai ni wataru kazoku no monogatari no chūshin ni aru.","german":"The antagonism between father and son is at the center of this family saga spanning three generations.","furigana":"ちち|むすこ|そうこく|さんだい|かぞく|ものがたり|ちゅうしん"}],"notes":"相克 is the conflict of two forces that exclude each other and cannot be reconciled: 理性と感情の相克, 〜と〜の相克. Also written 相剋. It originally comes from the theory of the five elements (相克 \"mutual overcoming\", opposite 相生). Compared with 対立 (\"opposition, confrontation\") and 葛藤 (\"inner conflict\"), 相克 sounds literary and stresses a fateful incompatibility. Written language, often in essays and literary criticism.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"利己的な行動が共同体の結束を弱めている。","romaji":"Rikoteki na kōdō ga kyōdōtai no kessoku o yowamete iru.","german":"Selfish behavior is weakening the cohesion of the community.","furigana":"りこてき|こうどう|きょうどうたい|けっそく|よわ"},{"japanese":"彼の決断は利己主義から来たものではなかった。","romaji":"Kare no ketsudan wa rikoshugi kara kita mono de wa nakatta.","german":"His decision did not come from egoism.","furigana":"かれ|けつだん|りこしゅぎ|き"},{"japanese":"自分の利益だけを考えるのは利己的すぎる。","romaji":"Jibun no rieki dake o kangaeru no wa rikoteki sugiru.","german":"Thinking only of your own interests is too selfish.","furigana":"じぶん|りえき|かんが|りこてき"}],"notes":"利己 means \"self-interest\" and appears almost only in 利己的（な） \"selfish\" and 利己主義 \"egoism (as an attitude)\". 利己的な遺伝子 is the Japanese title of Dawkins' \"The Selfish Gene\". Opposite: 利他. In everyday speech one more often says 自分勝手, わがまま or 自己中（心的）; 利己的 sounds more objective and analytical. Formal.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"利他的な精神が社会福祉の基盤を形成している。","romaji":"Ritateki na seishin ga shakai fukushi no kiban o keisei shite iru.","german":"The altruistic spirit forms the foundation of social welfare.","furigana":"りたてき|せいしん|しゃかいふくし|きばん|けいせい"},{"japanese":"一部の動物にも利他的な行動が見られる。","romaji":"Ichibu no dōbutsu ni mo ritateki na kōdō ga mirareru.","german":"Altruistic behavior is also observed in some animals.","furigana":"いちぶ|どうぶつ|りたてき|こうどう|み"},{"japanese":"その経営者は「利他の心」を経営の柱にしている。","romaji":"Sono keieisha wa \"rita no kokoro\" o keiei no hashira ni shite iru.","german":"That business owner has made the \"spirit of altruism\" a pillar of his management.","furigana":"けいえいしゃ|りた|こころ|けいえい|はしら"}],"notes":"利他 means \"unselfishness, acting for the benefit of others\" and mostly appears in 利他的（な） \"altruistic\" and 利他主義 \"altruism\". Opposite: 利己. In Buddhism, 利他 is a central value (自利利他 \"benefiting oneself and others\"). In everyday speech one more often says 人のために or 思いやりがある; 利他的 sounds objective, e.g. in psychology, biology or corporate philosophy.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"伝統工芸の技術を次世代に継承することが急務だ。","romaji":"Dentō kōgei no gijutsu o jisedai ni keishō suru koto ga kyūmu da.","german":"Passing on the techniques of traditional crafts to the next generation is an urgent task.","furigana":"でんとうこうげい|ぎじゅつ|じせだい|けいしょう|きゅうむ"},{"japanese":"金沢のお土産に、漆塗りの工芸品を買った。","romaji":"Kanazawa no omiyage ni, urushinuri no kōgeihin o katta.","german":"As a souvenir from Kanazawa, I bought a lacquerware craft piece.","furigana":"かなざわ|みやげ|うるしぬ|こうげいひん|か"},{"japanese":"彼女は大学で染織工芸を学んでいる。","romaji":"Kanojo wa daigaku de senshoku kōgei o manande iru.","german":"She is studying textile arts (dyeing and weaving) at university.","furigana":"かのじょ|だいがく|せんしょくこうげい|まな"}],"notes":"工芸 is craft art: objects that are both practical and beautiful (ceramics, lacquerware, textiles, woodwork). Typical: 伝統工芸, 工芸品, 工芸家, 美術工芸, 民芸 (folk craft). Unlike 美術 (\"fine art\"), 工芸 stresses use and craft technique; 手芸, by contrast, is handicraft such as knitting or embroidery as a hobby. Neutral.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"能楽はユネスコの無形文化遺産に登録されている。","romaji":"Nōgaku wa Yunesuko no mukei bunka isan ni tōroku sarete iru.","german":"Noh theater is registered as an Intangible Cultural Heritage of UNESCO.","furigana":"のうがく|むけいぶんかいさん|とうろく"},{"japanese":"週末、初めて能楽堂で公演を見た。","romaji":"Shūmatsu, hajimete nōgakudō de kōen o mita.","german":"At the weekend, I saw a performance at a Noh theater for the first time.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|はじ|のうがくどう|こうえん|み"},{"japanese":"彼の家は代々続く能楽の家系だ。","romaji":"Kare no ie wa daidai tsuzuku nōgaku no kakei da.","german":"His family is a Noh family going back generations.","furigana":"かれ|いえ|だいだいつづ|のうがく|かけい"}],"notes":"能楽 is the umbrella term for the classical stage art made up of 能 (serious masked drama with song and dance) and 狂言 (comic interludes). Typical: 能楽堂 \"Noh theater\", 能楽師 \"Noh performer\". In conversation one usually just says 能 (能を見に行く); 能楽 sounds more formal and appears in programs, specialist texts and names of institutions.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"邦楽の演奏には独特の間の取り方がある。","romaji":"Hōgaku no ensō ni wa dokutoku no ma no torikata ga aru.","german":"Performances of traditional Japanese music have a unique way of handling pauses.","furigana":"ほうがく|えんそう|どくとく|ま|と|かた"},{"japanese":"洋楽より邦楽のほうが歌詞がわかるから好きだ。","romaji":"Yōgaku yori hōgaku no hō ga kashi ga wakaru kara suki da.","german":"I like Japanese music better than Western music because I can understand the lyrics.","furigana":"ようがく|ほうがく|かし|す"},{"japanese":"娘は高校の邦楽部で琴を弾いている。","romaji":"Musume wa kōkō no hōgakubu de koto o hiite iru.","german":"My daughter plays the koto in her high school's traditional Japanese music club.","furigana":"むすめ|こうこう|ほうがくぶ|こと|ひ"}],"notes":"邦楽 has two meanings: in a cultural context, traditional Japanese music (koto, shamisen, shakuhachi, 邦楽器 \"Japanese instruments\"); in everyday speech and in record shops, however, simply \"Japanese music\" as opposed to 洋楽, including J-pop and Japanese rock (邦楽コーナー, 邦楽ロック). Which meaning is intended depends on the context. For traditional music in the narrower sense, one also says 和楽 or 伝統音楽.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"明治以降に洋楽が日本に本格的に導入された。","romaji":"Meiji ikō ni yōgaku ga Nihon ni honkakuteki ni dōnyū sareta.","german":"Western music was introduced to Japan in earnest after the Meiji period.","furigana":"めいじいこう|ようがく|にほん|ほんかくてき|どうにゅう"},{"japanese":"中学生のころから洋楽ばかり聴いている。","romaji":"Chūgakusei no koro kara yōgaku bakari kiite iru.","german":"Since junior high school I've listened to almost nothing but Western music.","furigana":"ちゅうがくせい|ようがく|き"},{"japanese":"洋楽の歌詞を訳しながら英語を勉強している。","romaji":"Yōgaku no kashi o yakushinagara eigo o benkyō shite iru.","german":"I study English by translating the lyrics of Western songs.","furigana":"ようがく|かし|やく|えいご|べんきょう"}],"notes":"洋楽 is \"Western music\". Historically it means the European art music introduced since the Meiji period; today in everyday speech it mainly means Western, mostly English-language, pop music (洋楽を聴く, 洋楽ファン, 洋楽ロック). The opposite term is 邦楽. For classical music, one nowadays more often says クラシック（音楽）. Neutral.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"各地の民謡にはその土地の風土や生活が反映されている。","romaji":"Kakuchi no min'yō ni wa sono tochi no fūdo ya seikatsu ga han'ei sarete iru.","german":"The folk songs of each region reflect the climate and way of life of that place.","furigana":"かくち|みんよう|とち|ふうど|せいかつ|はんえい"},{"japanese":"盆踊りでは、地元の民謡に合わせてみんなで踊る。","romaji":"Bon'odori de wa, jimoto no min'yō ni awasete minna de odoru.","german":"At the Bon dance, everyone dances to the local folk songs.","furigana":"ぼんおど|じもと|みんよう|あ|おど"},{"japanese":"祖母は若いころ、民謡の大会で優勝したそうだ。","romaji":"Sobo wa wakai koro, min'yō no taikai de yūshō shita sō da.","german":"My grandmother reportedly won a folk song contest when she was young.","furigana":"そぼ|わか|みんよう|たいかい|ゆうしょう"}],"notes":"民謡 are traditional folk songs of a region; in Japan they are often work songs or festival songs with a characteristic singing technique and shamisen accompaniment (津軽民謡, 民謡歌手). They are sung and danced to at 盆踊り. Unlike 童謡 (\"children's song\") and 演歌 (sentimental ballad), 民謡 are handed down anonymously. Western folk songs are also called 民謡 (ドイツ民謡). Neutral.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本舞踊は所作の美しさを極めた芸能だ。","romaji":"Nihon buyō wa shosa no utsukushisa o kiwameta geinō da.","german":"Japanese dance is a performing art that has perfected the beauty of movement.","furigana":"にほんぶよう|しょさ|うつく|きわ|げいのう"},{"japanese":"姉は子どものころから日本舞踊を習っている。","romaji":"Ane wa kodomo no koro kara Nihon buyō o naratte iru.","german":"My older sister has been learning classical Japanese dance since she was a child.","furigana":"あね|こ|にほんぶよう|なら"},{"japanese":"文化祭で各国の民族舞踊が披露された。","romaji":"Bunkasai de kakkoku no minzoku buyō ga hirō sareta.","german":"Folk dances from various countries were performed at the school festival.","furigana":"ぶんかさい|かっこく|みんぞくぶよう|ひろう"}],"notes":"舞踊 is dance as a performing art. Most often in 日本舞踊 \"classical Japanese dance\", and also 民族舞踊 \"folk dance\", 舞踊家, 舞踊団. In everyday speech, for dancing in general one says ダンス or 踊り / 踊る; 舞踊 sounds art-related and formal. 舞 stresses calm, circling movements, 踊 rhythmic jumping. Do not confuse with 舞踏 (Western ballroom dancing, Butō).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"暗黒舞踏は日本発の前衛的な身体表現だ。","romaji":"Ankoku butō wa Nihon hatsu no zen'eiteki na shintai hyōgen da.","german":"Ankoku butoh is an avant-garde form of physical expression that originated in Japan.","furigana":"あんこくぶとう|にほんはつ|ぜんえいてき|しんたいひょうげん"},{"japanese":"シンデレラは王子の舞踏会に出かけた。","romaji":"Shinderera wa ōji no butōkai ni dekaketa.","german":"Cinderella went to the prince's ball.","furigana":"おうじ|ぶとうかい|で"},{"japanese":"明治時代、鹿鳴館では西洋式の舞踏会が開かれた。","romaji":"Meiji jidai, Rokumeikan de wa seiyōshiki no butōkai ga hirakareta.","german":"In the Meiji era, Western-style balls were held at the Rokumeikan.","furigana":"めいじじだい|ろくめいかん|せいようしき|ぶとうかい|ひら"}],"notes":"舞踏 first denotes dance, especially Western ballroom dancing: 舞踏会 \"ball\" (シンデレラの舞踏会), 舞踏曲 \"dance music\". It is also the name of the Japanese avant-garde dance form Butō that emerged in the 1950s (暗黒舞踏, often also in katakana ブトー). Unlike 舞踊 (dance as a stage art, especially 日本舞踊), 舞踏 now sounds slightly old-fashioned or technical. Formal.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"俳句には自然を詠む風雅の精神が宿っている。","romaji":"Haiku ni wa shizen o yomu fūga no seishin ga yadotte iru.","german":"The spirit of elegance that sings of nature dwells in haiku.","furigana":"はいく|しぜん|よ|ふうが|せいしん|やど"},{"japanese":"苔むした庭に竹の筧があり、実に風雅な趣だ。","romaji":"Koke mushita niwa ni take no kakehi ga ari, jitsu ni fūga na omomuki da.","german":"There is a bamboo water pipe in the moss-covered garden, a truly refined atmosphere.","furigana":"こけ|にわ|たけ||じつ|ふうが|おもむき"},{"japanese":"祖父は茶道や書道をたしなむ風雅な人だった。","romaji":"Sofu wa sadō ya shodō o tashinamu fūga na hito datta.","german":"My grandfather was a cultured man who practiced tea ceremony and calligraphy.","furigana":"そふ|さどう|しょどう|ふうが|ひと"}],"notes":"風雅 is refined, cultivated elegance in the sense of traditional aesthetics, and also the pursuit of poetry, tea ceremony and calligraphy itself (風雅の道). As a な-adjective: 風雅な庭, 風雅な暮らし; also 風雅を解する \"to have a sense for the refined\". Compared with 優雅 (\"graceful, elegant\", also in a modern lifestyle), 風雅 is strongly literary and tied to nature and poetry; 風流 is similar but more common. Elevated.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"秋の紅葉には格別の情趣がある。","romaji":"Aki no kōyō ni wa kakubetsu no jōshu ga aru.","german":"The autumn foliage has an exceptional charm.","furigana":"あき|こうよう|かくべつ|じょうしゅ"},{"japanese":"石畳の路地が続く、情趣あふれる温泉街を歩いた。","romaji":"Ishidatami no roji ga tsuzuku, jōshu afureru onsengai o aruita.","german":"We walked through an atmospheric hot spring town with cobbled alleys.","furigana":"いしだたみ|ろじ|つづ|じょうしゅ|おんせんがい|ある"},{"japanese":"新しいビルばかりの街並みは便利だが情趣に欠ける。","romaji":"Atarashii biru bakari no machinami wa benri da ga jōshu ni kakeru.","german":"A cityscape of nothing but new buildings is convenient but lacks charm.","furigana":"あたら|まちな|べんり|じょうしゅ|か"}],"notes":"情趣 is the atmospheric, emotionally charged air that something radiates and that touches the heart: 情趣がある, 情趣あふれる町並み, 情趣に欠ける. Close in meaning are 情緒 (more common, also 下町情緒) and 趣 (\"charm, flair\"), which is the most common in everyday speech. 情趣 is written language and is used mainly for landscapes, seasons and classical literature.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"能の世界には幽玄の美が漂っている。","romaji":"Nō no sekai ni wa yūgen no bi ga tadayotte iru.","german":"The beauty of yūgen pervades the world of Noh.","furigana":"のう|せかい|ゆうげん|び|ただよ"},{"japanese":"霧に包まれた山寺は幽玄な雰囲気に満ちていた。","romaji":"Kiri ni tsutsumareta yamadera wa yūgen na fun'iki ni michite ita.","german":"The mountain temple, wrapped in mist, was filled with a mysterious atmosphere.","furigana":"きり|つつ|やまでら|ゆうげん|ふんいき|み"},{"japanese":"尺八の幽玄な音色に、観客は静かに聴き入った。","romaji":"Shakuhachi no yūgen na neiro ni, kankyaku wa shizuka ni kikiitta.","german":"The audience listened quietly to the profound tone of the shakuhachi.","furigana":"しゃくはち|ゆうげん|ねいろ|かんきゃく|しず|き|い"}],"notes":"幽玄 is a fundamental concept of Japanese aesthetics: a deep, mysterious beauty that suggests more than it shows. It particularly shapes 能 and classical poetry (幽玄の美, 幽玄の世界). As a な-adjective it now also describes mist-shrouded landscapes, dimly lit temples or music: 幽玄な雰囲気. Related are わび・さび (simple, transient beauty); 幽玄, by contrast, stresses the unfathomable. Elevated.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本独自の審美眼を養うには長年の修練が必要だ。","romaji":"Nihon dokuji no shinbigan o yashinau ni wa naganen no shūren ga hitsuyō da.","german":"Developing a uniquely Japanese aesthetic eye requires years of training.","furigana":"にほんどくじ|しんびがん|やしな|ながねん|しゅうれん|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"歯並びを整えるために審美歯科に通っている。","romaji":"Hanarabi o totonoeru tame ni shinbi shika ni kayotte iru.","german":"I go to a cosmetic dentist to have my teeth straightened.","furigana":"はなら|ととの|しんびしか|かよ"},{"japanese":"この建物は機能性だけでなく審美的な面でも高く評価されている。","romaji":"Kono tatemono wa kinōsei dake de naku shinbiteki na men de mo takaku hyōka sarete iru.","german":"This building is highly regarded not only for its functionality but also aesthetically.","furigana":"たてもの|きのうせい|しんびてき|めん|たか|ひょうか"}],"notes":"審美 is the judging of beauty and appears almost only in compounds: 審美眼 \"an eye for beauty, connoisseur's eye\" (審美眼がある / を養う), 審美的（な） \"aesthetic\", 審美歯科 \"cosmetic dentistry\". The philosophy of aesthetics is called 美学 (formerly also 審美学). 審美 hardly stands alone; in everyday speech one says センス or 美的感覚. Written or technical language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この彫刻家の造形感覚は独特で革新的だ。","romaji":"Kono chōkokuka no zōkei kankaku wa dokutoku de kakushinteki da.","german":"This sculptor's sense of form is unique and innovative.","furigana":"ちょうこくか|ぞうけいかんかく|どくとく|かくしんてき"},{"japanese":"この映画は怪獣の造形がとてもリアルだ。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa kaijū no zōkei ga totemo riaru da.","german":"In this film, the design of the monsters is very realistic.","furigana":"えいが|かいじゅう|ぞうけい"},{"japanese":"古代の土器には素朴な造形美がある。","romaji":"Kodai no doki ni wa soboku na zōkeibi ga aru.","german":"Ancient pottery has a simple formal beauty.","furigana":"こだい|どき|そぼく|ぞうけいび"}],"notes":"造形 is the shaping of forms, especially in the visual arts: sculpture, plastic art, design, figures. Typical: 造形美術 \"visual arts\", 造形美 \"formal beauty\", 造形作家, キャラクターの造形 \"character design\". As a する-verb it is hardly used; one more often says 形を作る or 造形を手がける. Also written 造型. Unlike デザイン (design in general), 造形 stresses three-dimensional, physical form. Neutral to technical.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"建築様式の変遷はその時代の美意識を反映している。","romaji":"Kenchiku yōshiki no hensen wa sono jidai no biishiki o han'ei shite iru.","german":"The changes in architectural styles reflect the aesthetic sense of each era.","furigana":"けんちくようしき|へんせん|じだい|びいしき|はんえい"},{"japanese":"申請書は所定の様式で提出してください。","romaji":"Shinseisho wa shotei no yōshiki de teishutsu shite kudasai.","german":"Please submit the application on the prescribed form.","furigana":"しんせいしょ|しょてい|ようしき|ていしゅつ"},{"japanese":"感染症の流行で、人々の生活様式は大きく変わった。","romaji":"Kansenshō no ryūkō de, hitobito no seikatsu yōshiki wa ōkiku kawatta.","german":"Because of the epidemic, people's way of life has changed greatly.","furigana":"かんせんしょう|りゅうこう|ひとびと|せいかつようしき|おお|か"}],"notes":"様式 denotes a fixed form or style, especially in compounds: 建築様式 \"architectural style\", 生活様式 \"way of life\", 行動様式 \"behavior pattern\". In government offices and companies, 様式 also means the prescribed form template, such as 所定の様式 \"the prescribed form\". Compared with スタイル, 様式 is more objective and more written; for an artist's personal style one more often says 作風 or スタイル.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"過剰な装飾を排した簡素な美が日本文化の特徴だ。","romaji":"Kajō na sōshoku o haishita kanso na bi ga Nihon bunka no tokuchō da.","german":"Simple beauty free of excessive ornament is a characteristic of Japanese culture.","furigana":"かじょう|そうしょく|はい|かんそ|び|にほんぶんか|とくちょう"},{"japanese":"結婚式の会場は白い花で装飾されていた。","romaji":"Kekkonshiki no kaijō wa shiroi hana de sōshoku sarete ita.","german":"The wedding venue was decorated with white flowers.","furigana":"けっこんしき|かいじょう|しろ|はな|そうしょく"},{"japanese":"この時計は装飾が少ない分、文字盤が見やすい。","romaji":"Kono tokei wa sōshoku ga sukunai bun, mojiban ga miyasui.","german":"Since this watch has little decoration, the dial is easy to read.","furigana":"とけい|そうしょく|すく|ぶん|もじばん|み"}],"notes":"装飾 is the decorative design of rooms, buildings or objects; as a transitive する-verb: 会場を花で装飾する, passive 装飾される. Common compounds are 室内装飾 \"interior decoration\" and 装飾品 \"ornament, accessory\"; 装飾的 means \"decorative\", and for texts also pejoratively \"overloaded\". In everyday speech one mostly says 飾り or 飾る or デコレーション; 装飾 sounds more objective and relates to art, architecture or design.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"金属象嵌の技術は古代から受け継がれている。","romaji":"Kinzoku zōgan no gijutsu wa kodai kara uketsugarete iru.","german":"The technique of metal inlay has been handed down since ancient times.","furigana":"きんぞくぞうがん|ぎじゅつ|こだい|う|つ"},{"japanese":"この刀の鍔には金で象嵌が施されている。","romaji":"Kono katana no tsuba ni wa kin de zōgan ga hodokosarete iru.","german":"The sword guard of this sword is decorated with gold inlay.","furigana":"かたな||きん|ぞうがん|ほどこ"},{"japanese":"職人は黒檀の箱に細い銀の線を象嵌した。","romaji":"Shokunin wa kokutan no hako ni hosoi gin no sen o zōgan shita.","german":"The craftsman inlaid thin silver lines into the ebony box.","furigana":"しょくにん||はこ|ほそ|ぎん|せん|ぞうがん"}],"notes":"象嵌 is a craft technique in which gold, silver, mother-of-pearl or the like is set into a cut surface of metal, wood or ceramic. Typical phrases are 象嵌を施す \"to apply inlay work\", 金象嵌 \"gold inlay\" and 象嵌細工 \"inlay work\"; as a transitive する-verb: 銀を象嵌する. For mother-of-pearl inlay in lacquer there is the separate term 螺鈿. The word is a technical term from art and craft and is also written 象眼.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"有田焼は日本を代表する陶磁器の一つだ。","romaji":"Arita-yaki wa Nihon o daihyō suru tōjiki no hitotsu da.","german":"Arita ware is one of Japan's representative ceramics.","furigana":"|にほん|だいひょう|とうじき|ひと"},{"japanese":"陶磁器は割れやすいので、丁寧に梱包してください。","romaji":"Tōjiki wa wareyasui node, teinei ni konpō shite kudasai.","german":"Ceramics break easily, so please pack them carefully.","furigana":"とうじき|わ|ていねい|こんぽう"},{"japanese":"美術館で中国の古い陶磁器の展示を見た。","romaji":"Bijutsukan de Chūgoku no furui tōjiki no tenji o mita.","german":"At the museum, I saw an exhibition of old Chinese ceramics.","furigana":"びじゅつかん|ちゅうごく|ふる|とうじき|てんじ|み"}],"notes":"陶磁器 is the collective term for 陶器 \"earthenware, pottery\" (made of clay, rather thick and porous) and 磁器 \"porcelain\" (thin, hard and light). One speaks of 陶磁器の産地 \"ceramics region\" or 陶磁器を焼く \"to fire ceramics\"; regional wares carry the ending 〜焼, as in 有田焼 or 備前焼. In everyday speech, for tableware one mostly says 焼き物 or 器; 陶磁器 sounds objective, as in museums, trade or shipping notices.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この窯元は三百年以上の歴史を持つ名窯だ。","romaji":"Kono kamamoto wa sanbyakunen ijō no rekishi o motsu meiyō da.","german":"This pottery is a renowned kiln with a history of over three hundred years.","furigana":"かまもと|さんびゃくねんいじょう|れきし|も|めいよう"},{"japanese":"旅行中に窯元を訪ねて、ろくろを体験した。","romaji":"Ryokōchū ni kamamoto o tazunete, rokuro o taiken shita.","german":"On a trip, I visited a pottery and tried the potter's wheel.","furigana":"りょこうちゅう|かまもと|たず|たいけん"},{"japanese":"窯元から直接買うと、お店より安いことが多い。","romaji":"Kamamoto kara chokusetsu kau to, omise yori yasui koto ga ōi.","german":"Buying directly from the pottery is often cheaper than from a shop.","furigana":"かまもと|ちょくせつか|みせ|やす|おお"}],"notes":"窯元 is the pottery or ceramics workshop that operates its own kiln, and also its owner or master potter. The kiln itself is only called 窯; 窯元 always means the business or the person behind it. Typical are 窯元を訪ねる, 窯元めぐり \"tour of potteries\" and 窯元直売 \"direct sales from the pottery\". 蔵元, for sake and soy sauce breweries, is formed similarly.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"匠の技が世界から注目を集めている。","romaji":"Takumi no waza ga sekai kara chūmoku o atsumete iru.","german":"The skill of master craftsmen is attracting attention from around the world.","furigana":"たくみ|わざ|せかい|ちゅうもく|あつ"},{"japanese":"寺の修復には、宮大工の匠たちが集められた。","romaji":"Tera no shūfuku ni wa, miyadaiku no takumitachi ga atsumerareta.","german":"Master temple carpenters were gathered for the restoration of the temple.","furigana":"てら|しゅうふく|みやだいく|たくみ|あつ"},{"japanese":"この包丁は、この道五十年の匠が一本ずつ手作りしている。","romaji":"Kono hōchō wa, kono michi gojūnen no takumi ga ippon zutsu tezukuri shite iru.","german":"These kitchen knives are handmade one by one by a master with fifty years in the trade.","furigana":"ほうちょう|みちごじゅうねん|たくみ|いっぽん|てづく"}],"notes":"匠 is a masterly craftsman whose skill has matured over many years; the word sounds appreciative and somewhat elevated. Fixed is 匠の技 \"masterly craftsmanship\", especially in advertising and media about traditional crafts. Neutrally one says 職人 \"craftsman\"; 名匠 and 巨匠 stress fame even more, 巨匠 above all for artists. As a component, 匠 is also in 意匠 \"design, ornamental design\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"名匠の手による茶碗は美術品としての価値も高い。","romaji":"Meishō no te ni yoru chawan wa bijutsuhin toshite no kachi mo takai.","german":"Tea bowls made by great masters have high value as works of art as well.","furigana":"めいしょう|て|ちゃわん|びじゅつひん|かち|たか"},{"japanese":"この仏像は鎌倉時代の名匠の作と伝えられている。","romaji":"Kono butsuzō wa Kamakura jidai no meishō no saku to tsutaerarete iru.","german":"This Buddha statue is said to be the work of a famous master of the Kamakura period.","furigana":"ぶつぞう|かまくらじだい|めいしょう|さく|つた"},{"japanese":"名匠と呼ばれた刀鍛冶の刀が、オークションで高値で落札された。","romaji":"Meishō to yobareta katanakaji no katana ga, ōkushon de takane de rakusatsu sareta.","german":"A sword by a swordsmith known as a master was sold at auction for a high price.","furigana":"めいしょう|よ|かたなかじ|かたな|たかね|らくさつ"}],"notes":"名匠 is a famous, outstanding master, especially in the crafts (swordsmiths, potters, sculptors), occasionally also for directors or chefs. Typical are 名匠の作 \"work of a famous master\" and 名匠の手による \"made by a master's hand\". Compared with 匠, 名匠 highlights fame; 巨匠 is more used for great artists in painting, music and film. Do not confuse with the similar-sounding 名称 \"name, designation\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"映画界の巨匠の新作が国際映画祭で上映された。","romaji":"Eigakai no kyoshō no shinsaku ga kokusai eigasai de jōei sareta.","german":"The new film by a master of the film world was screened at an international film festival.","furigana":"えいがかい|きょしょう|しんさく|こくさいえいがさい|じょうえい"},{"japanese":"この展覧会には、ルネサンスの巨匠たちの名画が集められている。","romaji":"Kono tenrankai ni wa, Runesansu no kyoshōtachi no meiga ga atsumerarete iru.","german":"Famous paintings by the great masters of the Renaissance are gathered in this exhibition.","furigana":"てんらんかい|きょしょう|めいが|あつ"},{"japanese":"若い頃は無名だった彼も、今では巨匠と呼ばれている。","romaji":"Wakai koro wa mumei datta kare mo, ima de wa kyoshō to yobarete iru.","german":"He too, unknown in his youth, is now called a great master.","furigana":"わか|ころ|むめい|かれ|いま|きょしょう|よ"}],"notes":"巨匠 is a \"grand master\" with an outstanding reputation in art, music, literature or film, e.g. 映画界の巨匠, ルネサンスの巨匠 or 巨匠と呼ばれる指揮者. It is a respectful, often journalistic term for recognized, usually older figures. For craftsmen, 名匠 or 匠 fit better, and for masters in games such as shōgi or Go, 名人.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"多くの門弟を育てた師匠の功績は計り知れない。","romaji":"Ōku no montei o sodateta shishō no kōseki wa hakarishirenai.","german":"The achievements of the master who trained many disciples are immeasurable.","furigana":"おお|もんてい|そだ|ししょう|こうせき|はか|し"},{"japanese":"彼は十五歳で名人の門弟となった。","romaji":"Kare wa jūgosai de meijin no montei to natta.","german":"He became a disciple of a great master at fifteen.","furigana":"かれ|じゅうごさい|めいじん|もんてい"},{"japanese":"孔子には三千人の門弟がいたと伝えられている。","romaji":"Kōshi ni wa sanzennin no montei ga ita to tsutaerarete iru.","german":"Confucius is said to have had three thousand disciples.","furigana":"こうし|さんぜんにん|もんてい|つた"}],"notes":"門弟 is a pupil who has been accepted into a master's school, for example in martial arts, tea ceremony, painting or classical scholarship. Typical are 門弟となる \"to become a disciple\", 門弟を育てる and the classic image of Confucius's three thousand disciples. The word is elevated and somewhat old-fashioned; 門人 is synonymous, and the normal everyday term is 弟子. For pupils of an ordinary school one says 生徒 or 学生, never 門弟.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"武道の奥義を極めるには生涯にわたる修行が必要だ。","romaji":"Budō no ōgi o kiwameru ni wa shōgai ni wataru shugyō ga hitsuyō da.","german":"Mastering the deepest secrets of the martial arts requires lifelong training.","furigana":"ぶどう|おうぎ|きわ|しょうがい|しゅぎょう|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"師匠は亡くなる前に、ただ一人の弟子に奥義を授けた。","romaji":"Shishō wa nakunaru mae ni, tada hitori no deshi ni ōgi o sazuketa.","german":"Before his death, the master passed on the secret teachings to his only disciple.","furigana":"ししょう|な|まえ|ひとり|でし|おうぎ|さず"},{"japanese":"ゲームの主人公は、最後のボス戦で奥義を繰り出した。","romaji":"Gēmu no shujinkō wa, saigo no bosusen de ōgi o kuridashita.","german":"The game's protagonist unleashed his ultimate technique in the final boss fight.","furigana":"しゅじんこう|さいご|せん|おうぎ|く|だ"}],"notes":"奥義 are the deepest, often secretly transmitted teachings or techniques of an art, which a master passes on only to advanced pupils. Typical verbs are 奥義を極める \"to master completely\", 奥義を伝授する or 奥義を授ける \"to pass on\" and 奥義を会得する \"to acquire\". Besides おうぎ, the reading おくぎ is also possible; related are 秘伝 \"secret teaching\" and 極意 \"core, innermost secret\". In manga and video games, 奥義 also denotes a character's strongest special technique.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この作品には日本文化の精髄が凝縮されている。","romaji":"Kono sakuhin ni wa Nihon bunka no seizui ga gyōshuku sarete iru.","german":"The quintessence of Japanese culture is concentrated in this work.","furigana":"さくひん|にほんぶんか|せいずい|ぎょうしゅく"},{"japanese":"茶道の精髄は、客をもてなす心にあると言われる。","romaji":"Sadō no seizui wa, kyaku o motenasu kokoro ni aru to iwareru.","german":"It is said that the essence of the tea ceremony lies in the spirit of welcoming guests.","furigana":"さどう|せいずい|きゃく|こころ|い"},{"japanese":"この本は、著者の四十年に及ぶ研究の精髄をまとめたものだ。","romaji":"Kono hon wa, chosha no yonjūnen ni oyobu kenkyū no seizui o matometa mono da.","german":"This book sums up the essence of the author's forty years of research.","furigana":"ほん|ちょしゃ|よんじゅうねん|およ|けんきゅう|せいずい"}],"notes":"精髄 is the most essential thing, the innermost core of a culture, teaching or art. It almost always appears with の: 日本文化の精髄, 思想の精髄; typical verbs are 精髄に触れる, 精髄を伝える and 精髄が凝縮される. More common and synonymous is 真髄 (also written 神髄); エッセンス sounds more colloquial. The word is written language and elevated.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"料理の極意は素材の味を最大限に引き出すことだ。","romaji":"Ryōri no gokui wa sozai no aji o saidaigen ni hikidasu koto da.","german":"The secret of cooking is to bring out the flavor of the ingredients to the fullest.","furigana":"りょうり|ごくい|そざい|あじ|さいだいげん|ひ|だ"},{"japanese":"弟子は長い修行の末、師匠から剣の極意を授けられた。","romaji":"Deshi wa nagai shugyō no sue, shishō kara ken no gokui o sazukerareta.","german":"After long training, the disciple was entrusted with the innermost secret of swordsmanship by his master.","furigana":"でし|なが|しゅぎょう|すえ|ししょう|けん|ごくい|さず"},{"japanese":"十年働いて、ようやく接客の極意がつかめてきた。","romaji":"Jūnen hataraite, yōyaku sekkyaku no gokui ga tsukamete kita.","german":"After ten years of work, I have finally grasped the essence of customer service.","furigana":"じゅうねんはたら|せっきゃく|ごくい"}],"notes":"極意 is the innermost secret or real essence of an art or skill, originally from martial arts and traditional arts: 剣の極意, 極意を授ける／伝授する, 極意をつかむ. Today it also appears casually in book titles such as 仕事の極意 or 会話の極意. コツ is an everyday \"knack\", 秘訣 a \"secret recipe\"; 極意 sounds the deepest and most elevated.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"能の所作には無駄のない洗練された美がある。","romaji":"Nō no shosa ni wa muda no nai senren sareta bi ga aru.","german":"The movements of Noh have a refined beauty with no waste.","furigana":"のう|しょさ|むだ|せんれん|び"},{"japanese":"彼女の所作はとても上品で、育ちの良さが感じられる。","romaji":"Kanojo no shosa wa totemo jōhin de, sodachi no yosa ga kanjirareru.","german":"Her bearing is very graceful, and you can sense her good upbringing.","furigana":"かのじょ|しょさ|じょうひん|そだ|よ|かん"},{"japanese":"着物を着たときの所作を、祖母から教わった。","romaji":"Kimono o kita toki no shosa o, sobo kara osowatta.","german":"I learned from my grandmother how to move when wearing a kimono.","furigana":"きもの|き|しょさ|そぼ|おそ"}],"notes":"所作 denotes the way one moves and behaves, especially the formalized, beautiful movements in tea ceremony, Noh, Kabuki or martial arts. One says 所作が美しい, 所作を身につける or 美しい所作. Unlike neutral 動作 \"movement, action\", 所作 stresses posture and grace; 仕草 are rather small, involuntary gestures, and 立ち居振る舞い is behavior as a whole. In Kabuki, 所作事 is a dance piece.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本の伝統芸能では型の習得が基本とされている。","romaji":"Nihon no dentō geinō de wa kata no shūtoku ga kihon to sarete iru.","german":"In traditional Japanese arts, mastering the forms is considered fundamental.","furigana":"にほん|でんとうげいのう|かた|しゅうとく|きほん"},{"japanese":"空手の大会で、彼女は型の部で優勝した。","romaji":"Karate no taikai de, kanojo wa kata no bu de yūshō shita.","german":"At the karate tournament, she won the kata division.","furigana":"からて|たいかい|かのじょ|かた|ぶ|ゆうしょう"},{"japanese":"型にはまった考え方では、新しいアイデアは生まれない。","romaji":"Kata ni hamatta kangaekata de wa, atarashii aidea wa umarenai.","german":"With formulaic thinking, no new ideas are born.","furigana":"かた|かんが|かた|あたら|う"}],"notes":"型 refers to a fixed form or pattern: in martial arts and traditional arts the set sequence of movements (kata), but also a mold, a model or a convention. Important phrases are 型を覚える, 型にはまる \"to be formulaic\" as well as 型にはまらない and 型破り \"unconventional\". As a suffix it is read がた: 血液型, 新型, 大型. Distinction: 形 is the outer shape, 型 the prescribed pattern according to which something is made.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"華道にはさまざまな流派が存在する。","romaji":"Kadō ni wa samazama na ryūha ga sonzai suru.","german":"Various schools exist in the art of flower arrangement.","furigana":"かどう|りゅうは|そんざい"},{"japanese":"同じ空手でも、流派によって型や構えが違う。","romaji":"Onaji karate demo, ryūha ni yotte kata ya kamae ga chigau.","german":"Even in karate, the kata and stances differ depending on the style.","furigana":"おな|からて|りゅうは|かた|かま|ちが"},{"japanese":"彼は流派を超えて、多くの書道家と交流してきた。","romaji":"Kare wa ryūha o koete, ōku no shodōka to kōryū shite kita.","german":"He has been in contact with many calligraphers across school lines.","furigana":"かれ|りゅうは|こ|おお|しょどうか|こうりゅう"}],"notes":"流派 is a school or style in an art or martial art that hands down its own teaching and technique, for example in ikebana, tea ceremony or karate. One says 流派に属する, 流派を開く \"to found a school\" and 流派を超えて \"across schools\". The names of such schools often end in 〜流, and at their head there is often an 家元. For scholarly or philosophical currents one more often says 学派.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"家元制度は日本の伝統文化の継承に重要な役割を果たしている。","romaji":"Iemoto seido wa Nihon no dentō bunka no keishō ni jūyō na yakuwari o hatashite iru.","german":"The iemoto system plays an important role in passing on traditional Japanese culture.","furigana":"いえもとせいど|にほん|でんとうぶんか|けいしょう|じゅうよう|やくわり|は"},{"japanese":"彼は二十代の若さで、華道の家元を継いだ。","romaji":"Kare wa nijūdai no wakasa de, kadō no iemoto o tsuida.","german":"In his twenties, he succeeded as head of an ikebana school.","furigana":"かれ|にじゅうだい|わか|かどう|いえもと|つ"},{"japanese":"家元の指導を直接受けられるのは、限られた弟子だけだ。","romaji":"Iemoto no shidō o chokusetsu ukerareru no wa, kagirareta deshi dake da.","german":"Only a limited number of disciples can receive instruction directly from the head of the school.","furigana":"いえもと|しどう|ちょくせつう|かぎ|でし"}],"notes":"家元 is the usually hereditary head of a school in traditional arts such as tea ceremony, ikebana, Noh, dance or music, and often also the family that runs the school. The 家元制度 regulates teaching authority and artist names; the title is usually passed on within the family: 家元を継ぐ. Respectfully one also says お家元; the designated successor is generally called 次期家元.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"無形文化財の継承者不足が深刻な問題だ。","romaji":"Mukei bunkazai no keishōsha busoku ga shinkoku na mondai da.","german":"The shortage of successors for intangible cultural properties is a serious problem.","furigana":"むけいぶんかざい|けいしょうしゃぶそく|しんこく|もんだい"},{"japanese":"地域の祭りを次の世代に継承していくことが大切だ。","romaji":"Chiiki no matsuri o tsugi no sedai ni keishō shite iku koto ga taisetsu da.","german":"It is important to pass local festivals on to the next generation.","furigana":"ちいき|まつ|つぎ|せだい|けいしょう|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"国王の死後、長男が王位を継承した。","romaji":"Kokuō no shigo, chōnan ga ōi o keishō shita.","german":"After the king's death, his eldest son succeeded to the throne.","furigana":"こくおう|しご|ちょうなん|おうい|けいしょう"}],"notes":"継承 is taking over and carrying on something handed down: rights, offices, traditions or techniques; as a transitive する-verb: 伝統を継承する, 王位を継承する. Common are 継承者 \"successor, heir\", 王位継承 \"succession to the throne\" and, in computing, \"inheritance\" (クラスの継承). For inheriting property, one says 相続 instead; in everyday speech 継承する is often replaced by 受け継ぐ or 引き継ぐ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"芸術の学習は模倣から始まり独創に至る。","romaji":"Geijutsu no gakushū wa mohō kara hajimari dokusō ni itaru.","german":"Learning art begins with imitation and leads to originality.","furigana":"げいじゅつ|がくしゅう|もほう|はじ|どくそう|いた"},{"japanese":"有名ブランドの模倣品がネットで売られている。","romaji":"Yūmei burando no mohōhin ga netto de urarete iru.","german":"Imitations of famous brands are sold online.","furigana":"ゆうめい|もほうひん|う"},{"japanese":"幼い子どもは、周りの大人の言葉や行動を模倣して覚える。","romaji":"Osanai kodomo wa, mawari no otona no kotoba ya kōdō o mohō shite oboeru.","german":"Small children learn by imitating the speech and behavior of the adults around them.","furigana":"おさな|こ|まわ|おとな|ことば|こうどう|もほう|おぼ"}],"notes":"模倣 is imitating or copying a model; as a transitive する-verb: 手本を模倣する. It is objective to written language and is often the opposite of 独創 or 創造; in everyday speech one says 真似 or 真似する. Common compounds are 模倣品 \"imitation product, counterfeit\" and 模倣犯 \"copycat criminal\". Depending on the context it sounds neutral (learning by imitation) or derogatory (plagiarism).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"独創的な発想がこの芸術家の作品を際立たせている。","romaji":"Dokusōteki na hassō ga kono geijutsuka no sakuhin o kiwadatasete iru.","german":"Original ideas make this artist's works stand out.","furigana":"どくそうてき|はっそう|げいじゅつか|さくひん|きわだ"},{"japanese":"この論文は独創性が高く評価された。","romaji":"Kono ronbun wa dokusōsei ga takaku hyōka sareta.","german":"The originality of this paper was highly rated.","furigana":"ろんぶん|どくそうせい|たか|ひょうか"},{"japanese":"彼の料理は伝統を踏まえながらも独創に富んでいる。","romaji":"Kare no ryōri wa dentō o fumaenagara mo dokusō ni tonde iru.","german":"His cooking builds on tradition yet is full of originality.","furigana":"かれ|りょうり|でんとう|ふ|どくそう|と"}],"notes":"独創 is producing something entirely one's own, not imitated. It seldom stands alone; it mostly appears as 独創的 \"original\", 独創性 \"originality\" or in phrases such as 独創に富む \"rich in originality\". The opposite term is 模倣 \"imitation\"; similar are 独自 \"one's own, independent\" and 創造 \"creation\", while オリジナリティ sounds more colloquial.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"平家物語は日本を代表する叙事文学の傑作だ。","romaji":"Heike Monogatari wa Nihon o daihyō suru joji bungaku no kessaku da.","german":"The Tale of the Heike is a masterpiece of Japan's epic literature.","furigana":"へいけものがたり|にほん|だいひょう|じょじぶんがく|けっさく"},{"japanese":"『イリアス』はトロイア戦争を描いた古代ギリシャの叙事詩だ。","romaji":"Iriasu wa Toroia sensō o egaita kodai Girisha no jojishi da.","german":"The Iliad is an ancient Greek epic poem about the Trojan War.","furigana":"せんそう|えが|こだい|じょじし"},{"japanese":"彼の小説は、感情を抑えた叙事的な文体で書かれている。","romaji":"Kare no shōsetsu wa, kanjō o osaeta jojiteki na buntai de kakarete iru.","german":"His novels are written in a narrative style that holds back emotion.","furigana":"かれ|しょうせつ|かんじょう|おさ|じょじてき|ぶんたい|か"}],"notes":"叙事 is the factual presentation of events and facts, without putting one's own feelings in the foreground. It hardly occurs on its own; the usual forms are 叙事詩 \"epic, epic poetry\", 叙事文学 and 叙事的 \"epic, narrative\". The opposite is 叙情 (also 抒情) \"lyric poetry, expression of feeling\", e.g. 叙情詩 \"lyric poem\". The word belongs to the technical language of literary studies.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"花鳥風月を詠むことは和歌の基本だ。","romaji":"Kachōfūgetsu o yomu koto wa waka no kihon da.","german":"Composing poems about the beauties of nature (flowers, birds, wind and moon) is the foundation of waka.","furigana":"かちょうふうげつ|よ|わか|きほん"},{"japanese":"旅先の美しい景色を見て、一句詠んでみた。","romaji":"Tabisaki no utsukushii keshiki o mite, ikku yonde mita.","german":"Seeing the beautiful scenery on my trip, I tried composing a haiku.","furigana":"たびさき|うつく|けしき|み|いっくよ"},{"japanese":"富士山は古くから多くの和歌に詠まれてきた。","romaji":"Fujisan wa furuku kara ōku no waka ni yomarete kita.","german":"Mt. Fuji has been celebrated in many waka poems since ancient times.","furigana":"ふじさん|ふる|おお|わか|よ"}],"notes":"詠む means \"to compose a poem (waka, tanka, haiku)\" and is used only for poetry: 歌を詠む, 一句詠む for a haiku, 一首詠む for a tanka. In the form 〜に詠まれる it means \"to be celebrated in poems\", e.g. 富士山は多くの歌に詠まれてきた. Do not confuse it with the identically pronounced 読む \"to read\"; for prose one says 書く, and for reciting rather 吟じる or 朗読する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"漢詩を朗々と吟じる姿が印象的だった。","romaji":"Kanshi o rōrō to ginjiru sugata ga inshōteki datta.","german":"The sight of him reciting Chinese poems in a resonant voice was impressive.","furigana":"かんし|ろうろう|ぎん|すがた|いんしょうてき"},{"japanese":"祖父は酒が入ると、決まって古い詩を吟じた。","romaji":"Sofu wa sake ga hairu to, kimatte furui shi o ginjita.","german":"Whenever my grandfather had had some drink, he would invariably recite old poems.","furigana":"そふ|さけ|はい|き|ふる|し|ぎん"},{"japanese":"詩吟の発表会で、生徒たちが一人ずつ漢詩を吟じた。","romaji":"Shigin no happyōkai de, seitotachi ga hitori zutsu kanshi o ginjita.","german":"At the shigin recital, the students each recited a Chinese poem in turn.","furigana":"しぎん|はっぴょうかい|せいと|ひとり|かんし|ぎん"}],"notes":"吟じる means to recite a poem in a solemn, melodic voice, above all Chinese poems (漢詩) or waka in the style of 詩吟. 吟ずる is synonymous and somewhat more classical; less often it also means \"to compose a poem\". Compared with the neutral 朗読する \"to read aloud\", 吟じる stresses the sung, drawn-out delivery; typical is 朗々と吟じる \"to recite in a resonant voice\". The word is elevated and literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この作品は独特の雰囲気を醸し出している。","romaji":"Kono sakuhin wa dokutoku no fun'iki o kamoshidashite iru.","german":"This work creates a unique atmosphere.","furigana":"さくひん|どくとく|ふんいき|かも|だ"},{"japanese":"首相の不用意な発言が物議を醸している。","romaji":"Shushō no fuyōi na hatsugen ga butsugi o kamoshite iru.","german":"The prime minister's careless remark is stirring up controversy.","furigana":"しゅしょう|ふようい|はつげん|ぶつぎ|かも"},{"japanese":"この蔵では、昔ながらの製法で酒を醸している。","romaji":"Kono kura de wa, mukashinagara no seihō de sake o kamoshite iru.","german":"This brewery makes sake using traditional methods.","furigana":"くら|むかし|せいほう|さけ|かも"}],"notes":"醸す originally means \"to brew sake or soy sauce by fermentation\"; today it is used mainly in a figurative sense. Very common are 物議を醸す \"to stir up controversy\" and the compound 醸し出す \"to give off, create an atmosphere\", e.g. 雰囲気を醸し出す. The word is literary; in everyday speech one says 造る (酒を造る) for brewing and rather 雰囲気がある for the mood.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"茶道を嗜む人が若い世代にも増えている。","romaji":"Sadō o tashinamu hito ga wakai sedai ni mo fuete iru.","german":"The number of people who practice the tea ceremony is growing even among the younger generation.","furigana":"さどう|たしな|ひと|わか|せだい|ふ"},{"japanese":"お酒はたしなむ程度で、たくさんは飲みません。","romaji":"Osake wa tashinamu teido de, takusan wa nomimasen.","german":"I only drink alcohol occasionally and in moderation, never much.","furigana":"さけ|ていど|の"},{"japanese":"祖父は若い頃から俳句を嗜んできた。","romaji":"Sofu wa wakai koro kara haiku o tashinande kita.","german":"My grandfather has been writing haiku since his youth.","furigana":"そふ|わか|ころ|はいく|たしな"}],"notes":"嗜む means to pursue an art, a sport or a luxury item with interest and in moderation: 茶道を嗜む, 俳句を嗜む, お酒を少々嗜む. It sounds cultured and modest and therefore suits self-introductions well; the more neutral alternative is 趣味にしている. It is mostly written in kana (たしなむ). The noun 嗜み means \"knowledge, skill\" and also \"decorum, good manners\" (大人の嗜み).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"物語を紡ぐ作家の想像力に脱帽する。","romaji":"Monogatari o tsumugu sakka no sōzōryoku ni datsubō suru.","german":"I take my hat off to the imagination of the author who spins stories.","furigana":"ものがたり|つむ|さっか|そうぞうりょく|だつぼう"},{"japanese":"祖母は糸車を回して、羊の毛から糸を紡いでいた。","romaji":"Sobo wa itoguruma o mawashite, hitsuji no ke kara ito o tsumuide ita.","german":"My grandmother turned the spinning wheel and spun thread from sheep's wool.","furigana":"そぼ|いとぐるま|まわ|ひつじ|け|いと|つむ"},{"japanese":"彼女は涙をこらえながら、一つ一つ言葉を紡いだ。","romaji":"Kanojo wa namida o koraenagara, hitotsu hitotsu kotoba o tsumuida.","german":"Fighting back tears, she put her words together one by one.","furigana":"かのじょ|なみだ|ひと|ひと|ことば|つむ"}],"notes":"紡ぐ literally means \"to spin fibers such as cotton, wool or silk into thread\": 糸を紡ぐ, 綿を紡ぐ. Figuratively and in literary use it means to piece together words, stories or memories with care: 言葉を紡ぐ, 物語を紡ぐ, 歴史を紡ぐ. This figurative use is now more common and sounds poetic. \"To weave\" in the literal sense is 織る instead.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"美術鑑賞は心を豊かにする。","romaji":"Bijutsu kanshō wa kokoro o yutaka ni suru.","german":"Appreciating art enriches the heart.","furigana":"びじゅつかんしょう|こころ|ゆた"},{"japanese":"趣味は映画鑑賞です。","romaji":"Shumi wa eiga kanshō desu.","german":"My hobby is watching movies.","furigana":"しゅみ|えいがかんしょう"},{"japanese":"美術館では、静かに作品を鑑賞してください。","romaji":"Bijutsukan de wa, shizuka ni sakuhin o kanshō shite kudasai.","german":"Please view the works quietly in the museum.","furigana":"びじゅつかん|しず|さくひん|かんしょう"}],"notes":"鑑賞 is the enjoying, understanding viewing or listening to art: 美術鑑賞, 音楽鑑賞, 映画鑑賞; as a transitive する-verb: 絵画を鑑賞する. 映画鑑賞 and 音楽鑑賞 are standard answers to the question about one's hobby. Not to be confused with the similar-sounding words 観賞 \"viewing nature, flowers or ornamental fish\" (観賞魚), 感傷 \"sentimentality\" and 干渉 \"interference\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は音楽の殿堂入りを果たした。","romaji":"Kare wa ongaku no dendōiri o hatashita.","german":"He was inducted into the music hall of fame.","furigana":"かれ|おんがく|でんどうい|は"},{"japanese":"大学はしばしば学問の殿堂と呼ばれる。","romaji":"Daigaku wa shibashiba gakumon no dendō to yobareru.","german":"Universities are often called temples of learning.","furigana":"だいがく|がくもん|でんどう|よ"},{"japanese":"この劇場は長年、クラシック音楽の殿堂として親しまれてきた。","romaji":"Kono gekijō wa naganen, kurashikku ongaku no dendō toshite shitashimarete kita.","german":"This theater has long been loved as a temple of classical music.","furigana":"げきじょう|ながねん|おんがく|でんどう|した"}],"notes":"殿堂 is originally a magnificent hall or temple building; today it is mostly used figuratively for an institution regarded as the supreme place of a field: 学問の殿堂 \"temple of learning\", 芸術の殿堂. Very common is 殿堂入り \"induction into the hall of fame\", also in sports (野球殿堂) and in rankings. The word is elevated; do not confuse it with the homophones 伝道 \"missionary work\" and 電動 \"electric\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"松尾芭蕉の句碑が全国各地に建てられている。","romaji":"Matsuo Bashō no kuhi ga zenkoku kakuchi ni taterarete iru.","german":"Stone monuments inscribed with verses by Matsuo Basho stand all over the country.","furigana":"|くひ|ぜんこくかくち|た"},{"japanese":"公園には戦争の犠牲者を悼む碑が建っている。","romaji":"Kōen ni wa sensō no giseisha o itamu hi ga tatte iru.","german":"A monument mourning the victims of the war stands in the park.","furigana":"こうえん|せんそう|ぎせいしゃ|いた|ひ|た"},{"japanese":"碑に刻まれた文字は風化して、ほとんど読めない。","romaji":"Hi ni kizamareta moji wa fūka shite, hotondo yomenai.","german":"The characters carved into the monument have weathered and are almost unreadable.","furigana":"ひ|きざ|もじ|ふうか|よ"}],"notes":"碑 is an inscribed stone commemorating a person, an event or a poem; read on its own it is ひ, poetically also いしぶみ. It mostly appears in compounds: 記念碑 \"monument\", 慰霊碑 \"memorial for the dead\", 石碑, 句碑 and 歌碑 for engraved haiku and waka respectively. Typical verbs are 碑を建てる and 碑に刻む. A gravestone, by contrast, is 墓石, and a statue 銅像.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"法隆寺は日本最古の国宝建造物を有する。","romaji":"Hōryūji wa Nihon saiko no kokuhō kenzōbutsu o yū suru.","german":"Horyu-ji has the oldest buildings in Japan designated as national treasures.","furigana":"ほうりゅうじ|にほんさいこ|こくほうけんぞうぶつ|ゆう"},{"japanese":"姫路城の天守は国宝に指定されている。","romaji":"Himeji-jō no tenshu wa kokuhō ni shitei sarete iru.","german":"The main keep of Himeji Castle is designated a national treasure.","furigana":"ひめじじょう|てんしゅ|こくほう|してい"},{"japanese":"祖父は人間国宝に認定された陶芸家だ。","romaji":"Sofu wa ningen kokuhō ni nintei sareta tōgeika da.","german":"My grandfather is a ceramic artist who was certified as a Living National Treasure.","furigana":"そふ|にんげんこくほう|にんてい|とうげいか"}],"notes":"国宝 is a cultural property officially designated a national treasure, the highest rank above 重要文化財; one says 国宝に指定する or 国宝に指定されている. 人間国宝 is the popular term for holders of outstanding traditional skills (\"Living National Treasure\"). In colloquial speech 国宝級 praises something as extraordinary, e.g. 国宝級の美しさ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この仏像は重要文化財に指定されている。","romaji":"Kono butsuzō wa jūyō bunkazai ni shitei sarete iru.","german":"This Buddha statue is designated an important cultural property.","furigana":"ぶつぞう|じゅうようぶんかざい|してい"},{"japanese":"重要文化財の建物の中は撮影禁止になっている。","romaji":"Jūyō bunkazai no tatemono no naka wa satsuei kinshi ni natte iru.","german":"Photography is prohibited inside the building, which is an important cultural property.","furigana":"じゅうようぶんかざい|たてもの|なか|さつえいきんし"},{"japanese":"火事で重要文化財の本堂が焼けてしまった。","romaji":"Kaji de jūyō bunkazai no hondō ga yakete shimatta.","german":"The main hall, an important cultural property, burned down in the fire.","furigana":"かじ|じゅうようぶんかざい|ほんどう|や"}],"notes":"重要文化財 is a statutory protection category for buildings, works of art and documents of special value; the most outstanding of them are additionally declared 国宝. One says 重要文化財に指定する or 指定されている; in the media and guidebooks the abbreviation 重文 is common. The word is official and matter-of-fact and is found mainly on information signs, in travel guides and in the news.","levelNote":"Compound: 重要 (N3) + 文化財 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は自らの信条を貫き通す強い意志を持っている。","romaji":"Kare wa mizukara no shinjō o tsuranukitōsu tsuyoi ishi o motte iru.","german":"He has the strong will to stand by his convictions.","furigana":"かれ|みずか|しんじょう|つらぬ|とお|つよ|いし|も"},{"japanese":"「約束は必ず守る」が私の信条です。","romaji":"\"Yakusoku wa kanarazu mamoru\" ga watashi no shinjō desu.","german":"\"Always keep your promises\" is my principle.","furigana":"やくそく|かなら|まも|わたし|しんじょう"},{"japanese":"憲法は信条による差別を禁止している。","romaji":"Kenpō wa shinjō ni yoru sabetsu o kinshi shite iru.","german":"The constitution prohibits discrimination on the basis of creed.","furigana":"けんぽう|しんじょう|さべつ|きんし"}],"notes":"信条 is a firm principle by which one lives or acts, and in the religious sense a creed. Typical are 〜を信条とする \"to make something one's principle\", 信条を貫く and 私の信条は〜だ. In legal texts 信条 stands for ideological conviction, for example in the constitution's ban on discrimination. Compared with 信念 \"firm conviction\", 信条 stresses more a rule of life that can be put into words.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"各宗派の教義の違いを理解することが宗教学の基本だ。","romaji":"Kaku shūha no kyōgi no chigai o rikai suru koto ga shūkyōgaku no kihon da.","german":"Understanding the differences between the doctrines of the various denominations is the foundation of religious studies.","furigana":"かくしゅうは|きょうぎ|ちが|りかい|しゅうきょうがく|きほん"},{"japanese":"カトリックの教義では、離婚は認められていない。","romaji":"Katorikku no kyōgi de wa, rikon wa mitomerarete inai.","german":"According to Catholic doctrine, divorce is not permitted.","furigana":"きょうぎ|りこん|みと"},{"japanese":"その教団は独自の教義を掲げて信者を集めた。","romaji":"Sono kyōdan wa dokuji no kyōgi o kakagete shinja o atsumeta.","german":"That religious group attracted followers by putting forward its own doctrine.","furigana":"きょうだん|どくじ|きょうぎ|かか|しんじゃ|あつ"}],"notes":"教義 is the binding teaching of a religion or faith community, its articles of faith. Typical phrases are 教義に従う, 教義に反する, 教義を説く and 教義を掲げる. Figuratively it can also mean the rigid doctrine of an ideology; \"dogmatic\", however, is 教条的. Distinctions: 教え is the general, softer word for \"teaching\", 経典 the scripture in which the teaching is written.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"僧侶は厳しい戒律を守って修行に励む。","romaji":"Sōryo wa kibishii kairitsu o mamotte shugyō ni hagemu.","german":"Monks keep strict precepts and devote themselves to training.","furigana":"そうりょ|きび|かいりつ|まも|しゅぎょう|はげ"},{"japanese":"イスラム教の戒律では、豚肉を食べることが禁じられている。","romaji":"Isuramukyō no kairitsu de wa, butaniku o taberu koto ga kinjirarete iru.","german":"Under Islamic religious law, eating pork is forbidden.","furigana":"きょう|かいりつ|ぶたにく|た|きん"},{"japanese":"戒律を破った僧は、寺を追われた。","romaji":"Kairitsu o yabutta sō wa, tera o owareta.","german":"The monk who broke the precepts was driven out of the temple.","furigana":"かいりつ|やぶ|そう|てら|お"}],"notes":"戒律 are the religious commandments and rules of conduct that believers, monks or nuns must observe, e.g. dietary and abstinence rules. Typical verbs are 戒律を守る, 戒律を破る and 戒律に従う; 厳しい戒律 is a common combination. Figuratively, the strict rules of a group can also be called this. Distinctions: 規則 are secular regulations, 教義 the content of the faith, 戒律 the rules for living.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"苦行を通じて精神の浄化を目指す修行者もいる。","romaji":"Kugyō o tsūjite seishin no jōka o mezasu shugyōsha mo iru.","german":"Some practitioners aim at purifying the mind through asceticism.","furigana":"くぎょう|つう|せいしん|じょうか|めざ|しゅぎょうしゃ"},{"japanese":"釈迦は六年間の苦行の末、それが悟りへの道ではないと気づいた。","romaji":"Shaka wa rokunenkan no kugyō no sue, sore ga satori e no michi de wa nai to kizuita.","german":"After six years of asceticism, Buddha realized that it was not the path to enlightenment.","furigana":"|ろくねんかん|くぎょう|すえ|さと|みち|き"},{"japanese":"満員電車で一時間通勤するのは、まさに苦行だ。","romaji":"Man'in densha de ichijikan tsūkin suru no wa, masa ni kugyō da.","german":"Commuting an hour on a packed train is truly an ordeal.","furigana":"まんいんでんしゃ|いちじかんつうきん|くぎょう"}],"notes":"苦行 is religious asceticism in which one seeks to purify mind and body through fasting, cold or pain; as an intransitive する-verb it is rare (苦行する), the usual forms being 苦行を積む and 苦行に耐える. Colloquially, and often with exaggeration, it denotes any agonizingly tedious activity: 〜はまさに苦行だ. Distinction: 修行 is religious or artistic training in general, while 苦行 stresses deliberate suffering.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"禅の修行において悟りは究極の目標とされる。","romaji":"Zen no shugyō ni oite satori wa kyūkyoku no mokuhyō to sareru.","german":"In Zen practice, enlightenment is regarded as the ultimate goal.","furigana":"ぜん|しゅぎょう|さと|きゅうきょく|もくひょう"},{"japanese":"釈迦は菩提樹の下で悟りを開いたと伝えられている。","romaji":"Shaka wa bodaiju no shita de satori o hiraita to tsutaerarete iru.","german":"Tradition has it that Buddha attained enlightenment under the Bodhi tree.","furigana":"||した|さと|ひら|つた"},{"japanese":"彼は悟りが早いので、説明は一度で十分だ。","romaji":"Kare wa satori ga hayai node, setsumei wa ichido de jūbun da.","german":"He is quick on the uptake, so explaining it once is enough.","furigana":"かれ|さと|はや|せつめい|いちど|じゅうぶん"}],"notes":"悟り is, in Buddhism, enlightenment, the awakening to the truth; fixed is 悟りを開く \"to attain enlightenment\". It also means in general \"understanding, grasp\", for example 悟りが早い \"quick to understand\". It is the noun of the verb 悟る \"to realize, comprehend\". Colloquially, さとり世代 denotes a sober young generation without strong desires.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仏教における解脱とは煩悩からの解放を意味する。","romaji":"Bukkyō ni okeru gedatsu to wa bonnō kara no kaihō o imi suru.","german":"In Buddhism, liberation means release from worldly desires.","furigana":"ぶっきょう|げだつ|ぼんのう|かいほう|いみ"},{"japanese":"仏教では、輪廻から解脱することが最終的な目標とされる。","romaji":"Bukkyō de wa, rinne kara gedatsu suru koto ga saishūteki na mokuhyō to sareru.","german":"In Buddhism, release from the cycle of rebirth is considered the final goal.","furigana":"ぶっきょう|りんね|げだつ|さいしゅうてき|もくひょう"},{"japanese":"受験が終わって、勉強漬けの日々からようやく解脱した気分だ。","romaji":"Juken ga owatte, benkyōzuke no hibi kara yōyaku gedatsu shita kibun da.","german":"The entrance exams are over, and I finally feel freed from the days of nonstop studying.","furigana":"じゅけん|お|べんきょうづ|ひび|げだつ|きぶん"}],"notes":"解脱 is, in Buddhism, liberation from desires and from the cycle of rebirth; as an intransitive する-verb: 輪廻から解脱する, 煩悩から解脱する. It is a religious technical term; closely related are 悟り \"enlightenment\" and 涅槃 \"nirvana\". It is also said jokingly when one feels freed from a burdensome duty. For worldly liberation one normally uses 解放.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"輪廻転生の思想はインド哲学に起源を持つ。","romaji":"Rinne tenshō no shisō wa Indo tetsugaku ni kigen o motsu.","german":"The idea of samsara and reincarnation originates in Indian philosophy.","furigana":"りんねてんしょう|しそう|てつがく|きげん|も"},{"japanese":"仏教では、生き物は死んでも輪廻を繰り返すと説かれる。","romaji":"Bukkyō de wa, ikimono wa shinde mo rinne o kurikaesu to tokareru.","german":"Buddhism teaches that living beings go through the cycle of rebirth again and again even after death.","furigana":"ぶっきょう|い|もの|し|りんね|く|かえ|と"},{"japanese":"輪廻を信じる人にとって、死は終わりではない。","romaji":"Rinne o shinjiru hito ni totte, shi wa owari de wa nai.","german":"For people who believe in reincarnation, death is not the end.","furigana":"りんね|しん|ひと|し|お"}],"notes":"輪廻 is the Buddhist-Hindu cycle of death and rebirth; mostly in the fixed combination 輪廻転生. Typical are 輪廻を繰り返す, 輪廻から抜け出す and 輪廻を信じる; more rarely as an intransitive する-verb \"to be reborn\". Liberation from it is called 解脱. The word is religious-philosophical; in everyday speech one says rather 生まれ変わり \"rebirth\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"前世の業が現世の運命を左右するとされている。","romaji":"Zense no gō ga gense no unmei o sayū suru to sarete iru.","german":"It is said that the karma of past lives determines one's fate in this life.","furigana":"ぜんせ|ごう|げんせ|うんめい|さゆう"},{"japanese":"欲を捨てられないのは、人間の業というものだろう。","romaji":"Yoku o suterarenai no wa, ningen no gō to iu mono darō.","german":"Not being able to cast off desire is probably what is called human karma.","furigana":"よく|す|にんげん|ごう"},{"japanese":"返事が来ないことに業を煮やした部長は、自分で取引先に電話した。","romaji":"Henji ga konai koto ni gō o nayashita buchō wa, jibun de torihikisaki ni denwa shita.","german":"The department head, losing patience at the lack of a reply, phoned the client himself.","furigana":"へんじ|こ|ごう||ぶちょう|じぶん|とりひきさき|でんわ"}],"notes":"業 (ごう) is the Buddhist term \"karma\": deeds and their consequences, which work beyond lifetimes, also in the sense of fate. Fixed phrases are 業が深い \"sinful, heavily burdened\", 人間の業 \"the inescapable nature of humans\" and 業を煮やす \"to boil with impatience\". Do not confuse it with the reading ぎょう (職業, 卒業) or わざ \"technique\". Related: 自業自得.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"除夜の鐘は百八つの煩悩を払うために撞かれる。","romaji":"Joya no kane wa hyakuyattsu no bonnō o harau tame ni tsukareru.","german":"The New Year's Eve bell is rung to drive away the 108 worldly desires.","furigana":"じょや|かね|ひゃくやっ|ぼんのう|はら|"},{"japanese":"座禅を組んでも、なかなか煩悩は消えない。","romaji":"Zazen o kunde mo, nakanaka bonnō wa kienai.","german":"Even when I sit in zazen, my worldly desires don't easily go away.","furigana":"ざぜん|く|ぼんのう|き"},{"japanese":"ダイエット中なのに、ケーキを見ると煩悩が湧いてくる。","romaji":"Daietto-chū na noni, kēki o miru to bonnō ga waite kuru.","german":"I'm on a diet, but when I see cake, temptation stirs.","furigana":"ちゅう|み|ぼんのう|わ"}],"notes":"煩悩 are, in Buddhism, the mental defilements such as greed, hatred and delusion that cause suffering; traditionally one speaks of 百八つの煩悩. Typical phrases are 煩悩を払う, 煩悩を断ち切る and 煩悩にとらわれる. In everyday speech it is often used jokingly for small cravings, such as a longing for sweets or laziness. 子煩悩, on the other hand, is praise: \"doting on one's own children\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仏の慈悲はすべての衆生に等しく注がれる。","romaji":"Hotoke no jihi wa subete no shujō ni hitoshiku sosogareru.","german":"Buddha's compassion is poured out equally on all sentient beings.","furigana":"ほとけ|じひ|しゅじょう|ひと|そそ"},{"japanese":"王は敗れた敵にも慈悲をかけた。","romaji":"Ō wa yabureta teki ni mo jihi o kaketa.","german":"The king showed mercy even to the defeated enemy.","furigana":"おう|やぶ|てき|じひ"},{"japanese":"「どうかお慈悲を」と男は裁判官にすがった。","romaji":"\"Dōka o-jihi o\" to otoko wa saibankan ni sugatta.","german":"\"Please, have mercy,\" the man begged the judge.","furigana":"じひ|おとこ|さいばんかん"}],"notes":"慈悲 is originally the Buddhist virtue of kindness and compassion for all beings; in general \"mercy, grace\". Typical phrases are 慈悲深い \"merciful\", 慈悲をかける \"to show mercy\" and the pleading request どうかお慈悲を. The opposite is 無慈悲 \"merciless\". Compared with 同情 \"sympathy\" and 思いやり \"consideration\", 慈悲 sounds exalted and religiously colored.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"観音菩薩は衆生の苦しみを救済する存在として信仰されている。","romaji":"Kannon Bosatsu wa shujō no kurushimi o kyūsai suru sonzai toshite shinkō sarete iru.","german":"The bodhisattva Kannon is worshipped as a being who saves sentient beings from suffering.","furigana":"|しゅじょう|くる|きゅうさい|そんざい|しんこう"},{"japanese":"道端のお地蔵さんは、地蔵菩薩のことだ。","romaji":"Michibata no o-Jizō-san wa, Jizō Bosatsu no koto da.","german":"The Jizo statues by the roadside are the bodhisattva Jizo.","furigana":"みちばた|じぞう|じぞうぼさつ"},{"japanese":"祖母は菩薩のように優しい人だった。","romaji":"Sobo wa bosatsu no yō ni yasashii hito datta.","german":"My grandmother was as kind as a bodhisattva.","furigana":"そぼ|ぼさつ|やさ|ひと"}],"notes":"菩薩 is, in Mahayana Buddhism, a being who strives for enlightenment but vows to save all other beings first. It often appears as a title after the name: 観音菩薩, 地蔵菩薩, 弥勒菩薩. In rank, 菩薩 stands below 如来, the perfected Buddha. Figuratively, 菩薩のような人 denotes an exceptionally kind person.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"阿弥陀如来は浄土宗の本尊として崇められている。","romaji":"Amida Nyorai wa Jōdoshū no honzon toshite agamerarete iru.","german":"Amida Nyorai is revered as the principal object of worship of the Jodo school.","furigana":"あみだにょらい|じょうどしゅう|ほんぞん|あが"},{"japanese":"薬師如来は病気を治す仏として信仰されてきた。","romaji":"Yakushi Nyorai wa byōki o naosu hotoke toshite shinkō sarete kita.","german":"Yakushi Nyorai has long been worshipped as the Buddha who heals illness.","furigana":"やくしにょらい|びょうき|なお|ほとけ|しんこう"},{"japanese":"本堂に入ると、金色の如来像が静かに座っていた。","romaji":"Hondō ni hairu to, kin'iro no nyoraizō ga shizuka ni suwatte ita.","german":"When we entered the main hall, a golden statue of a Buddha sat quietly there.","furigana":"ほんどう|はい|きんいろ|にょらいぞう|しず|すわ"}],"notes":"如来 is an honorific title for a perfected Buddha who has attained enlightenment; it follows the name: 阿弥陀如来, 薬師如来, 大日如来. In the ranking of Buddhist figures, 如来 stands above 菩薩, 明王 and 天. On temple visits the word appears mainly in names of statues (如来像) and main halls.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仏陀は悟りを開いた後に弟子たちに教えを説いた。","romaji":"Budda wa satori o hiraita ato ni deshitachi ni oshie o toita.","german":"After attaining enlightenment, the Buddha taught his disciples.","furigana":"ぶっだ|さと|ひら|あと|でし|おし|と"},{"japanese":"仏陀の教えは、やがてアジア各地に広まった。","romaji":"Budda no oshie wa, yagate Ajia kakuchi ni hiromatta.","german":"The Buddha's teachings eventually spread throughout Asia.","furigana":"ぶっだ|おし|かくち|ひろ"},{"japanese":"仏陀が生まれた場所は、現在のネパールにある。","romaji":"Budda ga umareta basho wa, genzai no Nepāru ni aru.","german":"The place where the Buddha was born is in present-day Nepal.","furigana":"ぶっだ|う|ばしょ|げんざい"}],"notes":"仏陀 is the kanji spelling of \"Buddha\", today mostly written in katakana as ブッダ. It refers above all to the historical Buddha, who in everyday speech is rather called お釈迦様 or 釈迦. ほとけ／仏, by contrast, denotes buddhas in general and also the deceased. Typical: 仏陀の教え, 仏陀が悟りを開く.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"般若心経は最も広く読まれている仏教経典の一つだ。","romaji":"Hannya Shingyō wa mottomo hiroku yomarete iru Bukkyō kyōten no hitotsu da.","german":"The Heart Sutra is one of the most widely read Buddhist sutras.","furigana":"はんにゃしんぎょう|もっと|ひろ|よ|ぶっきょうきょうてん|ひと"},{"japanese":"多くの経典が、シルクロードを通ってインドから中国に伝わった。","romaji":"Ōku no kyōten ga, Shiruku Rōdo o tōtte Indo kara Chūgoku ni tsutawatta.","german":"Many sutras reached China from India via the Silk Road.","furigana":"おお|きょうてん|とお|ちゅうごく|つた"},{"japanese":"彼は古い経典を現代語に翻訳している。","romaji":"Kare wa furui kyōten o gendaigo ni hon'yaku shite iru.","german":"He is translating old sutras into modern language.","furigana":"かれ|ふる|きょうてん|げんだいご|ほんやく"}],"notes":"経典 (きょうてん) are above all the Buddhist sutras, and in general also the holy scriptures of other religions such as the Bible or the Koran. Typical verbs are 経典を読む, 経典を唱える and 経典を翻訳する. Read けいてん, the same spelling denotes the Confucian classics. Distinctions: お経 is the everyday word for the sutras that are recited, 聖典 stresses the sacred character of a text, 教典 the doctrinal text.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"各宗教にはそれぞれの聖典が存在する。","romaji":"Kaku shūkyō ni wa sorezore no seiten ga sonzai suru.","german":"Every religion has its own sacred scriptures.","furigana":"かくしゅうきょう|せいてん|そんざい"},{"japanese":"コーランはイスラム教の聖典だ。","romaji":"Kōran wa Isuramukyō no seiten da.","german":"The Koran is the holy book of Islam.","furigana":"きょう|せいてん"},{"japanese":"この本は、登山愛好家の間で聖典とされている。","romaji":"Kono hon wa, tozan aikōka no aida de seiten to sarete iru.","german":"This book is regarded as the bible among mountaineering enthusiasts.","furigana":"ほん|とざんあいこうか|あいだ|せいてん"}],"notes":"聖典 is the holy scripture of a religion, such as the Bible, the Koran or the Vedas; the word stresses the sacred, authoritative character. Figuratively, a standard work that is revered almost cultishly in a group is called 〜の聖典 (\"the bible of ...\"). Distinctions: 経典 refers mainly to Buddhist sutras, 聖書 specifically to the Christian Bible.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"教典の解釈をめぐって学派間で論争が起きた。","romaji":"Kyōten no kaishaku o megutte gakuha kan de ronsō ga okita.","german":"A dispute arose between the schools over the interpretation of the doctrinal texts.","furigana":"きょうてん|かいしゃく|がくはかん|ろんそう|お"},{"japanese":"その教団は、教祖の言葉をまとめた本を教典としている。","romaji":"Sono kyōdan wa, kyōso no kotoba o matometa hon o kyōten to shite iru.","german":"That religious group takes a book compiling its founder's words as its doctrinal text.","furigana":"きょうだん|きょうそ|ことば|ほん|きょうてん"},{"japanese":"信者たちは毎朝、教典の一節を声に出して読む。","romaji":"Shinjatachi wa maiasa, kyōten no issetsu o koe ni dashite yomu.","german":"Every morning the believers read a passage from the doctrinal text aloud.","furigana":"しんじゃ|まいあさ|きょうてん|いっせつ|こえ|だ|よ"}],"notes":"教典 is a text in which the teaching of a religion is set down, especially in newer faith communities and schools; also an authoritative textbook. It is read the same as 経典, which mainly means Buddhist sutras; in conversation, context or the paraphrase 教えの本 therefore often helps. Typical are 教典を読む, 教典の解釈 and 〜を教典とする.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本の仏教にはさまざまな宗派が存在する。","romaji":"Nihon no Bukkyō ni wa samazama na shūha ga sonzai suru.","german":"Im japanischen Buddhismus gibt es verschiedene Schulen.","furigana":"にほん|ぶっきょう|しゅうは|そんざい"},{"japanese":"うちの宗派は浄土真宗なので、お経もそれに合わせる。","romaji":"Uchi no shūha wa Jōdo Shinshū na node, okyō mo sore ni awaseru.","german":"Unsere Familie gehört zur Jōdo-Shinshū-Schule, daher richten sich auch die Sutren danach.","furigana":"しゅうは|じょうどしんしゅう|きょう|あ"},{"japanese":"この霊園は宗派を問わず利用できる。","romaji":"Kono reien wa shūha o towazu riyō dekiru.","german":"Dieser Friedhof steht Angehörigen aller Konfessionen offen.","furigana":"れいえん|しゅうは|と|りよう"}],"notes":"宗派 ist eine Richtung oder Schule innerhalb einer Religion, etwa 浄土真宗 oder 曹洞宗 im Buddhismus bzw. Konfessionen im Christentum. Häufig ist 宗派を問わず „unabhängig von der Konfession“, z. B. auf Friedhöfen oder bei Trauerfeiern; うちの宗派 meint die Glaubensrichtung der eigenen Familie. Anders als das deutsche „Sekte“ ist 宗派 völlig wertneutral; für Kunstschulen sagt man 流派, für religiöse Gruppen allgemein 宗教団体.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"江戸時代には宗門改めによって住民の宗教が管理された。","romaji":"Edo jidai ni wa shūmon aratame ni yotte jūmin no shūkyō ga kanri sareta.","german":"In the Edo period, the religion of the population was controlled through official religious inspections.","furigana":"えどじだい|しゅうもんあらた|じゅうみん|しゅうきょう|かんり"},{"japanese":"宗門の教えに背いた僧侶は、厳しい処分を受けた。","romaji":"Shūmon no oshie ni somuita sōryo wa, kibishii shobun o uketa.","german":"The monk who had gone against the teachings of his sect was severely punished.","furigana":"しゅうもん|おし|そむ|そうりょ|きび|しょぶん|う"}],"notes":"宗門 is an older, formal word for a religious denomination or community, today mainly in history and in the usage of the temple organizations themselves. Well known is 宗門改め, the religious inspection of the Edo period, with which the shogunate tracked down Christians and assigned every household to a temple. In everyday speech today one says 宗派 instead.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"海外での布教活動を通じて仏教は世界に広まった。","romaji":"Kaigai de no fukyō katsudō o tsūjite Bukkyō wa sekai ni hiromatta.","german":"Buddhism spread across the world through missionary activity abroad.","furigana":"かいがい|ふきょうかつどう|つう|ぶっきょう|せかい|ひろ"},{"japanese":"十六世紀、宣教師たちが日本でキリスト教の布教を始めた。","romaji":"Jūroku seiki, senkyōshitachi ga Nihon de Kirisutokyō no fukyō o hajimeta.","german":"In the 16th century, missionaries began propagating Christianity in Japan.","furigana":"じゅうろくせいき|せんきょうし|にほん|きょう|ふきょう|はじ"},{"japanese":"友達に好きなアニメを布教したら、すっかりはまってくれた。","romaji":"Tomodachi ni suki na anime o fukyō shitara, sukkari hamatte kureta.","german":"I got my friend into my favorite anime, and now he is completely hooked.","furigana":"ともだち|す|ふきょう"}],"notes":"布教 is spreading a religion and recruiting new believers, regardless of the religion; as a transitive する-verb: 教えを布教する, but more often 布教活動 \"missionary activity\". For Christian missions 伝道 and 宣教 are also usual. Colloquially and jokingly, one speaks of 布教 when someone wants to get friends excited about their favorite hobby, series or band.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"フランシスコ・ザビエルは日本での伝道活動の先駆者だ。","romaji":"Furanshisuko Zabieru wa Nihon de no dendō katsudō no senkusha da.","german":"Francis Xavier was a pioneer of missionary work in Japan.","furigana":"にほん|でんどうかつどう|せんくしゃ"},{"japanese":"彼は伝道師としてアフリカに渡った。","romaji":"Kare wa dendōshi toshite Afurika ni watatta.","german":"He went to Africa as a missionary.","furigana":"かれ|でんどうし|わた"},{"japanese":"日曜日には、信者たちが駅前で伝道をしている。","romaji":"Nichiyōbi ni wa, shinjatachi ga ekimae de dendō o shite iru.","german":"On Sundays, believers preach in front of the station.","furigana":"にちようび|しんじゃ|えきまえ|でんどう"}],"notes":"伝道 is the proclamation of a religion, above all Christian mission and evangelism; as a する-verb: 伝道する. Common are 伝道活動, 伝道師 \"preacher, missionary\" and 伝道の旅. Close in meaning are 布教 (religion-neutral) and 宣教. Do not confuse it with the homophones 伝導 \"conduction (of heat, electricity)\", 殿堂 \"hall\" and 電動 \"electrically powered\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"強制的な改宗は信教の自由を侵害する行為だ。","romaji":"Kyōseiteki na kaishū wa shinkyō no jiyū o shingai suru kōi da.","german":"Forced conversion is an act that violates freedom of religion.","furigana":"きょうせいてき|かいしゅう|しんきょう|じゆう|しんがい|こうい"},{"japanese":"彼は結婚を機にイスラム教に改宗した。","romaji":"Kare wa kekkon o ki ni Isuramukyō ni kaishū shita.","german":"He converted to Islam when he got married.","furigana":"かれ|けっこん|き|きょう|かいしゅう"},{"japanese":"戦国時代には、キリスト教に改宗する大名もいた。","romaji":"Sengoku jidai ni wa, Kirisutokyō ni kaishū suru daimyō mo ita.","german":"In the Sengoku period, some daimyo converted to Christianity.","furigana":"せんごくじだい|きょう|かいしゅう|だいみょう"}],"notes":"改宗 is conversion to another religion or denomination; as an intransitive する-verb with に: キリスト教に改宗する, also 〜から〜に改宗する. Forced conversion is called 強制改宗. Jokingly it is also used for a change of preference, for example when switching to a different smartphone. Distinction: 帰依 stresses inner devotion, 改宗 the change itself.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"三宝に帰依することが仏教徒の出発点だ。","romaji":"Sanbō ni kie suru koto ga Bukkyōto no shuppatsuten da.","german":"Taking refuge in the Three Jewels is the starting point of a Buddhist.","furigana":"さんぼう|きえ|ぶっきょうと|しゅっぱつてん"},{"japanese":"晩年、彼は仏教に深く帰依した。","romaji":"Bannen, kare wa Bukkyō ni fukaku kie shita.","german":"In his later years, he devoted himself deeply to Buddhism.","furigana":"ばんねん|かれ|ぶっきょう|ふか|きえ"},{"japanese":"鎌倉時代には、多くの武士が禅に帰依した。","romaji":"Kamakura jidai ni wa, ōku no bushi ga Zen ni kie shita.","german":"In the Kamakura period, many samurai embraced Zen.","furigana":"かまくらじだい|おお|ぶし|ぜん|きえ"}],"notes":"帰依 means to entrust oneself entirely to a faith, a Buddha or a revered teacher and to take refuge in it; as an intransitive する-verb with に: 仏教に帰依する, 三宝に帰依する. It stresses inner devotion, whereas 改宗 only denotes the change of religion. The word is religious and literary and often occurs in biographies and historical accounts; 深く帰依する is a common combination.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"神社で五穀豊穣の祈祷が行われた。","romaji":"Jinja de gokoku hōjō no kitō ga okonawareta.","german":"A prayer for a bountiful harvest was held at the shrine.","furigana":"じんじゃ||きとう|おこな"},{"japanese":"厄年なので、神社で厄除けの祈祷を受けた。","romaji":"Yakudoshi na node, jinja de yakuyoke no kitō o uketa.","german":"Since it is my unlucky year, I had a prayer for warding off misfortune performed at the shrine.","furigana":"やくどし|じんじゃ|やくよ|きとう|う"},{"japanese":"母の病気が治るよう、お寺で祈祷してもらった。","romaji":"Haha no byōki ga naoru yō, otera de kitō shite moratta.","german":"We had prayers said at the temple for my mother's recovery.","furigana":"はは|びょうき|なお|てら|きとう"}],"notes":"祈祷 is a formal, ritual prayer, usually performed by priests or monks on someone's behalf, for example for health, harvest or protection from misfortune; as a する-verb: 祈祷する, often 祈祷を受ける, 祈祷してもらう. Related are 祈祷師 \"prayer healer\" and 厄除け祈祷 \"prayer to ward off misfortune\". The general word for \"prayer\" is 祈り or お祈り; 祈願 stresses the concrete wish. Traditionally it is also written 祈禱.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"合格祈願のために神社を訪れる受験生が多い。","romaji":"Gōkaku kigan no tame ni jinja o otozureru jukensei ga ōi.","german":"Many exam candidates visit shrines to pray for passing their exams.","furigana":"ごうかくきがん|じんじゃ|おとず|じゅけんせい|おお"},{"japanese":"新年に家族で神社へ行き、一年の健康を祈願した。","romaji":"Shinnen ni kazoku de jinja e iki, ichinen no kenkō o kigan shita.","german":"At New Year we went to the shrine as a family and prayed for good health for the year.","furigana":"しんねん|かぞく|じんじゃ|い|いちねん|けんこう|きがん"},{"japanese":"チームは開幕前に必勝祈願を行った。","romaji":"Chīmu wa kaimaku mae ni hisshō kigan o okonatta.","german":"Before the season opened, the team prayed for victory.","furigana":"かいまくまえ|ひっしょうきがん|おこな"}],"notes":"祈願 is the formal request for divine help for a specific goal, usually at a shrine or temple. As a transitive する-verb: 〜を祈願する (安全を祈願する). Very common in compounds such as 合格祈願, 必勝祈願 or 家内安全, which one reads on amulets and votive plaques. More general is 祈り \"prayer\"; 祈祷 stresses the ritual performed by a priest.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"禅寺での瞑想体験が観光客の間で人気を集めている。","romaji":"Zendera de no meisō taiken ga kankōkyaku no aida de ninki o atsumete iru.","german":"Meditation experiences at Zen temples are popular among tourists.","furigana":"ぜんでら|めいそうたいけん|かんこうきゃく|あいだ|にんき|あつ"},{"japanese":"毎朝十分間、目を閉じて瞑想するようにしている。","romaji":"Maiasa juppunkan, me o tojite meisō suru yō ni shite iru.","german":"I make a point of meditating with my eyes closed for ten minutes every morning.","furigana":"まいあさじゅっぷんかん|め|と|めいそう"},{"japanese":"彼は窓辺に座り、静かに瞑想にふけっていた。","romaji":"Kare wa madobe ni suwari, shizuka ni meisō ni fukette ita.","german":"He sat by the window, quietly lost in meditation.","furigana":"かれ|まどべ|すわ|しず|めいそう"}],"notes":"瞑想 is \"meditation\" in the general sense, both religious and secular for relaxation. Intransitive する-verb: 目を閉じて瞑想する; to become completely absorbed in thought is 瞑想にふける. The spelling 冥想 also occurs. 座禅, by contrast, is specifically the seated meditation of Zen; modern mindfulness practice is often called マインドフルネス.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"座禅を組むことで心の静寂を得ることができる。","romaji":"Zazen o kumu koto de kokoro no seijaku o eru koto ga dekiru.","german":"By practicing zazen one can attain inner stillness.","furigana":"ざぜん|く|こころ|せいじゃく|え"},{"japanese":"週末にお寺の座禅会に参加してみた。","romaji":"Shūmatsu ni otera no zazenkai ni sanka shite mita.","german":"On the weekend I tried a zazen session at a temple.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|てら|ざぜんかい|さんか"},{"japanese":"座禅中に足がしびれて、立てなくなった。","romaji":"Zazen-chū ni ashi ga shibirete, tatenaku natta.","german":"My legs went numb during zazen, and I couldn't stand up.","furigana":"ざぜんちゅう|あし|た"}],"notes":"座禅 is the seated meditation of Zen Buddhism, usually in the lotus or half-lotus position. The fixed combination is 座禅を組む \"to sit down for zazen, to practice zazen\"; also 座禅をする. The original spelling 坐禅 is used mainly by Zen temples. Anyone who nods off gets a blow on the shoulder with the wooden stick 警策.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"僧侶による読経の声が堂内に響き渡った。","romaji":"Sōryo ni yoru dokyō no koe ga dōnai ni hibikiwatatta.","german":"The voice of the monk reciting sutras echoed through the hall.","furigana":"そうりょ|どきょう|こえ|どうない|ひび|わた"},{"japanese":"葬儀では、僧侶の読経の間に参列者が焼香をする。","romaji":"Sōgi de wa, sōryo no dokyō no aida ni sanretsusha ga shōkō o suru.","german":"At the funeral, the mourners offer incense while the monk recites the sutras.","furigana":"そうぎ|そうりょ|どきょう|あいだ|さんれつしゃ|しょうこう"},{"japanese":"祖父の命日に、お坊さんが家に来て読経してくれた。","romaji":"Sofu no meinichi ni, obōsan ga ie ni kite dokyō shite kureta.","german":"On my grandfather's death anniversary, a monk came to our house and recited sutras.","furigana":"そふ|めいにち|ぼう|いえ|き|どきょう"}],"notes":"読経 is the loud, rhythmic recitation of Buddhist sutras by monks, for example at funerals and memorial services. Besides どきょう, the reading どっきょう is also in use. As an intransitive する-verb: 僧侶が読経する; common is 読経の声. In everyday speech one usually says more simply お経を読む or お経をあげる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"故人の一周忌法要が営まれた。","romaji":"Kojin no isshūki hōyō ga itonamareta.","german":"A memorial service for the first anniversary of the deceased's death was held.","furigana":"こじん|いっしゅうきほうよう|いとな"},{"japanese":"四十九日の法要が終わり、納骨を済ませた。","romaji":"Shijūkunichi no hōyō ga owari, nōkotsu o sumaseta.","german":"After the 49th-day memorial service, the interment of the ashes was completed.","furigana":"しじゅうくにち|ほうよう|お|のうこつ|す"},{"japanese":"震災の犠牲者を追悼する法要が寺で営まれた。","romaji":"Shinsai no giseisha o tsuitō suru hōyō ga tera de itonamareta.","german":"A memorial service to mourn the victims of the earthquake was held at the temple.","furigana":"しんさい|ぎせいしゃ|ついとう|ほうよう|てら|いとな"}],"notes":"法要 is a Buddhist ceremony, above all the memorial service for the deceased on fixed dates (四十九日, 一周忌, 三回忌). One says formally 法要を営む or neutrally 法要を行う. The everyday counterpart is 法事; 法要 sounds more formal and often appears in invitations and newspaper reports.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先祖供養は日本の仏教文化の重要な側面だ。","romaji":"Senzo kuyō wa Nihon no Bukkyō bunka no jūyō na sokumen da.","german":"Ancestor memorial rites are an important aspect of Japan's Buddhist culture.","furigana":"せんぞくよう|にほん|ぶっきょうぶんか|じゅうよう|そくめん"},{"japanese":"お盆には実家に帰って先祖を供養する。","romaji":"Obon ni wa jikka ni kaette senzo o kuyō suru.","german":"At Obon I go back to my parents' home to honor the ancestors.","furigana":"ぼん|じっか|かえ|せんぞ|くよう"},{"japanese":"古い人形は捨てずに、お寺で供養してもらった。","romaji":"Furui ningyō wa sutezu ni, otera de kuyō shite moratta.","german":"I didn't throw away the old dolls but had them ritually laid to rest at a temple.","furigana":"ふる|にんぎょう|す|てら|くよう"}],"notes":"供養 means to offer the deceased or Buddhas offerings, prayers and sutra recitations in order to comfort the souls. As a transitive する-verb: 先祖を供養する, 霊を供養する. In Japan one also bids things farewell ritually: at a 人形供養 or 針供養, worn-out dolls or sewing needles are gratefully \"buried\". 法要 is the individual ceremony, 供養 rather the act and intention of remembrance.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"功徳を故人に回向する法会が行われた。","romaji":"Kudoku o kojin ni ekō suru hōe ga okonawareta.","german":"A service was held to dedicate merit to the deceased.","furigana":"くどく|こじん|えこう|ほうえ|おこな"},{"japanese":"住職は戦没者の霊に回向した。","romaji":"Jūshoku wa senbotsusha no rei ni ekō shita.","german":"The head priest prayed for the souls of the war dead.","furigana":"じゅうしょく|せんぼつしゃ|れい|えこう"},{"japanese":"読経の最後に回向文が唱えられる。","romaji":"Dokyō no saigo ni ekōmon ga tonaerareru.","german":"At the end of the sutra recitation, a dedication text is chanted.","furigana":"どきょう|さいご|えこうもん|とな"}],"notes":"回向 is a Buddhist technical term: the \"turning toward\" others, especially the deceased, of the merit (功徳) earned through sutra recitation or good deeds, in order to further their spiritual welfare. From this comes the meaning \"prayer for the repose of the soul\". Patterns: 功徳を故人に回向する, 霊に回向する. The word is highly technical; in everyday speech one says 供養.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"密教における灌頂の儀式は秘密裡に行われる。","romaji":"Mikkyō ni okeru kanjō no gishiki wa himitsuri ni okonawareru.","german":"The initiation ritual in esoteric Buddhism is carried out in secret.","furigana":"みっきょう|かんじょう|ぎしき|ひみつり|おこな"},{"japanese":"空海は高雄山寺で最澄に灌頂を授けた。","romaji":"Kūkai wa Takaosanji de Saichō ni kanjō o sazuketa.","german":"Kukai conferred the kanjo initiation on Saicho at Takaosanji temple.","furigana":"くうかい|たかおさんじ|さいちょう|かんじょう|さず"},{"japanese":"高野山では、一般の人も結縁灌頂を受けることができる。","romaji":"Kōyasan de wa, ippan no hito mo kechien kanjō o ukeru koto ga dekiru.","german":"On Mt. Koya, ordinary people too can receive a kechien kanjo initiation.","furigana":"こうやさん|いっぱん|ひと||う"}],"notes":"灌頂 is, in esoteric Buddhism (密教, esp. Shingon and Tendai), the initiation in which water is poured over the disciple's head in order to transmit teachings or the rank of master to him. Typical are 灌頂を受ける / 灌頂を授ける as well as 結縁灌頂 (initiation for laypeople) and 伝法灌頂 (master's initiation). The reading かんちょう also occurs. Not an everyday word; it has only a distant connection with anointing in the Christian sense.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"得度式を経て正式に僧侶となった。","romaji":"Tokudoshiki o hete seishiki ni sōryo to natta.","german":"After the ordination ceremony, he formally became a monk.","furigana":"とくどしき|へ|せいしき|そうりょ"},{"japanese":"彼女は五十歳で得度し、尼僧として新しい人生を始めた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa gojussai de tokudo shi, nisō to shite atarashii jinsei o hajimeta.","german":"She was ordained at fifty and began a new life as a nun.","furigana":"かのじょ|ごじゅっさい|とくど|にそう|あたら|じんせい|はじ"},{"japanese":"寺の跡継ぎとして、彼は十歳で得度した。","romaji":"Tera no atotsugi to shite, kare wa jussai de tokudo shita.","german":"As heir to the temple, he was ordained at the age of ten.","furigana":"てら|あとつ|かれ|じゅっさい|とくど"}],"notes":"得度 is ordination: one takes the vows, receives a Buddhist name and enters the state of monk or nun, usually with a shaved head. Intransitive する-verb: 得度する; the ceremony is called 得度式. Similar in meaning is 出家 \"to leave home, become a monk\", which sounds more general; 得度 denotes the formal rite.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"涅槃とは一切の苦しみから解放された究極の境地だ。","romaji":"Nehan to wa issai no kurushimi kara kaihō sareta kyūkyoku no kyōchi da.","german":"Nirvana is the ultimate state of liberation from all suffering.","furigana":"ねはん|いっさい|くる|かいほう|きゅうきょく|きょうち"},{"japanese":"釈迦は八十歳で涅槃に入ったと伝えられる。","romaji":"Shaka wa hachijussai de nehan ni haitta to tsutaerareru.","german":"Tradition has it that the Buddha entered nirvana at the age of eighty.","furigana":"|はちじゅっさい|ねはん|はい|つた"},{"japanese":"タイの寺院で巨大な涅槃像を見た。","romaji":"Tai no jiin de kyodai na nehanzō o mita.","german":"I saw a huge reclining Buddha statue at a temple in Thailand.","furigana":"じいん|きょだい|ねはんぞう|み"}],"notes":"涅槃 is the state of complete liberation from desire and suffering, the final goal of Buddhist practice. At the same time it denotes the death of the Buddha: 涅槃に入る \"to enter nirvana\". Well-known compounds are 涅槃像 \"reclining Buddha\" and 涅槃会, the memorial service for the Buddha's death on February 15. The katakana form ニルヴァーナ is rare.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"此岸から彼岸へ渡ることが仏教修行の目的だ。","romaji":"Shigan kara higan e wataru koto ga Bukkyō shugyō no mokuteki da.","german":"The goal of Buddhist practice is to cross from this shore to the other shore.","furigana":"しがん|ひがん|わた|ぶっきょうしゅぎょう|もくてき"},{"japanese":"三途の川は此岸と彼岸の境だと言われる。","romaji":"Sanzu no kawa wa shigan to higan no sakai da to iwareru.","german":"The Sanzu River is said to be the boundary between this shore and the other shore.","furigana":"|かわ|しがん|ひがん|さかい|い"},{"japanese":"その小説は、此岸に残された者の悲しみを描いている。","romaji":"Sono shōsetsu wa, shigan ni nokosareta mono no kanashimi o egaite iru.","german":"The novel depicts the grief of those left behind on this shore.","furigana":"しょうせつ|しがん|のこ|もの|かな|えが"}],"notes":"此岸 is \"this shore\": the world of the living, full of suffering and desire, as opposed to 彼岸 \"that shore\", the beyond or enlightenment. The word almost always appears paired with 彼岸 (此岸と彼岸, 此岸から彼岸へ) and is Buddhist-literary. 此 is an old spelling for この. While お彼岸, the memorial week around the equinox, is known to everyone, 此岸 hardly occurs in everyday speech.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"浄土思想は平安末期に広く庶民に受け入れられた。","romaji":"Jōdo shisō wa Heian makki ni hiroku shomin ni ukeirerareta.","german":"The Pure Land teachings were widely accepted by the common people in the late Heian period.","furigana":"じょうどしそう|へいあんまっき|ひろ|しょみん|う|い"},{"japanese":"平等院鳳凰堂は極楽浄土を地上に再現しようとして建てられた。","romaji":"Byōdōin Hōōdō wa gokuraku jōdo o chijō ni saigen shiyō to shite taterareta.","german":"The Phoenix Hall of Byodo-in was built to recreate the Pure Land paradise on earth.","furigana":"びょうどういんほうおうどう|ごくらくじょうど|ちじょう|さいげん|た"},{"japanese":"うちの宗派は浄土真宗です。","romaji":"Uchi no shūha wa Jōdo Shinshū desu.","german":"Our family's denomination is Jodo Shinshu.","furigana":"しゅうは|じょうどしんしゅう"}],"notes":"浄土 is the \"Pure Land\", a world without suffering created by a Buddha; usually Amida's western paradise is meant (極楽浄土, 西方浄土). From it derive the schools 浄土宗 and 浄土真宗, whose believers hope to be reborn in the Pure Land by reciting the 念仏 (浄土に往生する). Figuratively it can denote a paradisiacally peaceful place; in everyday speech one rather says 楽園 for this.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"念仏を唱えれば極楽往生できると説かれている。","romaji":"Nenbutsu o tonaereba gokuraku ōjō dekiru to tokarete iru.","german":"It is taught that by reciting the nenbutsu one can be reborn in paradise.","furigana":"ねんぶつ|とな|ごくらくおうじょう|と"},{"japanese":"露天風呂につかって「ああ、極楽極楽」とつぶやいた。","romaji":"Rotenburo ni tsukatte \"Ā, gokuraku gokuraku\" to tsubuyaita.","german":"Soaking in the open-air bath, I murmured, \"Ahh, this is heaven.\"","furigana":"ろてんぶろ|ごくらくごくらく"},{"japanese":"求人広告では楽な仕事に見えたが、聞いて極楽見て地獄だった。","romaji":"Kyūjin kōkoku de wa raku na shigoto ni mieta ga, kiite gokuraku mite jigoku datta.","german":"The job ad made it look easy, but it was heaven to hear about and hell to see.","furigana":"きゅうじんこうこく|らく|しごと|み|き|ごくらくみ|じごく"}],"notes":"極楽 is originally Amida's paradise (short for 極楽浄土) and the opposite of 地獄 \"hell\". In everyday speech it is often used jokingly for supreme comfort, for example in a hot bath: 「ああ、極楽極楽」. Fixed phrases are 極楽往生 \"to die peacefully and enter paradise\" and 聞いて極楽見て地獄 \"sounds heavenly, is in reality hell\". 天国 is the word for \"heaven\" with a Christian coloring.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"輪廻転生の概念は東洋思想の根幹をなしている。","romaji":"Rinne tenshō no gainen wa tōyō shisō no konkan o nashite iru.","german":"The concept of reincarnation forms the core of Eastern thought.","furigana":"りんねてんしょう|がいねん|とうようしそう|こんかん"},{"japanese":"あなたは輪廻転生を信じますか。","romaji":"Anata wa rinne tenshō o shinjimasu ka.","german":"Do you believe in reincarnation?","furigana":"りんねてんしょう|しん"},{"japanese":"仏教では、悟りを開くことで輪廻転生の苦しみから解脱できるとされる。","romaji":"Bukkyō de wa, satori o hiraku koto de rinne tenshō no kurushimi kara gedatsu dekiru to sareru.","german":"In Buddhism, it is said that through enlightenment one can be freed from the suffering of the cycle of rebirth.","furigana":"ぶっきょう|さと|ひら|りんねてんしょう|くる|げだつ"}],"notes":"輪廻転生 is a yojijukugo made of 輪廻 \"cycle of birth and death\" and 転生 \"rebirth in another form\": the doctrine that all beings are reborn again and again after death. Besides りんねてんしょう, りんねてんせい is also common. Typical: 輪廻転生を信じる, 輪廻転生から解脱する; often one says just 輪廻. In pop culture, 転生 on its own is very popular, for example in novels about rebirth in another world.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"臨終に際して安らかな往生を遂げた。","romaji":"Rinjū ni saishite yasuraka na ōjō o togeta.","german":"At the moment of death, he passed away peacefully.","furigana":"りんじゅう|さい|やす|おうじょう|と"},{"japanese":"帰り道で急に大雨に降られて、すっかり往生したよ。","romaji":"Kaerimichi de kyū ni ōame ni furarete, sukkari ōjō shita yo.","german":"On the way home I got caught in a sudden downpour, and I was totally stuck.","furigana":"かえ|みち|きゅう|おおあめ|ふ|おうじょう"},{"japanese":"大雪で車が立ち往生し、一晩を車内で過ごした。","romaji":"Ōyuki de kuruma ga tachiōjō shi, hitoban o shanai de sugoshita.","german":"Because of heavy snow, the car got stuck, and we spent the night in the vehicle.","furigana":"おおゆき|くるま|た|おうじょう|ひとばん|しゃない|す"}],"notes":"往生 means in Buddhism \"to go over to the Pure Land and be reborn there\" and is therefore a euphemism for a peaceful death: 往生を遂げる, especially 大往生 \"death in old age after a fulfilled life\". Colloquially and somewhat old-fashioned, 往生する also means \"to not know what to do, to be in a fix\". Derived from it is 立ち往生 \"to get stuck, be stranded\" (train, car, speaker). Intransitive する-verb.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"すべての衆生は成仏できると大乗仏教は説く。","romaji":"Subete no shujō wa jōbutsu dekiru to Daijō Bukkyō wa toku.","german":"Mahayana Buddhism teaches that all sentient beings can attain buddhahood.","furigana":"しゅじょう|じょうぶつ|だいじょうぶっきょう|と"},{"japanese":"この世に未練が残っていると成仏できないと言われる。","romaji":"Kono yo ni miren ga nokotte iru to jōbutsu dekinai to iwareru.","german":"It is said that if you still cling to this world, you cannot rest in peace.","furigana":"よ|みれん|のこ|じょうぶつ|い"},{"japanese":"祖母の墓前で「どうか安らかに成仏してください」と手を合わせた。","romaji":"Sobo no bozen de \"Dōka yasuraka ni jōbutsu shite kudasai\" to te o awaseta.","german":"At my grandmother's grave, I put my hands together: \"Please rest in peace.\"","furigana":"そぼ|ぼぜん|やす|じょうぶつ|て|あ"}],"notes":"成仏 literally means \"to become a Buddha\", that is, to attain enlightenment. In everyday speech it mainly means that a deceased person finds peace and does not wander as a ghost: 安らかに成仏してください, and in the negative 成仏できない for a restless soul. In contrast to 往生 (rebirth in the Pure Land), 成仏 stresses buddhahood or the repose of the soul. Intransitive する-verb.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"善行を積むことで功徳を得られると信じられている。","romaji":"Zenkō o tsumu koto de kudoku o erareru to shinjirarete iru.","german":"It is believed that one can gain merit by accumulating good deeds.","furigana":"ぜんこう|つ|くどく|え|しん"},{"japanese":"四国遍路をして功徳を積みたいと考える人は多い。","romaji":"Shikoku henro o shite kudoku o tsumitai to kangaeru hito wa ōi.","german":"Many people want to accumulate merit by doing the Shikoku pilgrimage.","furigana":"しこくへんろ|くどく|つ|かんが|ひと|おお"},{"japanese":"写経には大きな功徳があるとされている。","romaji":"Shakyō ni wa ōki na kudoku ga aru to sarete iru.","german":"Copying sutras is said to bring great merit.","furigana":"しゃきょう|おお|くどく"}],"notes":"功徳 is the religious merit one earns through good deeds, donations or sutra recitation, and at the same time the blessing that arises from it. The fixed combination is 功徳を積む \"to accumulate merit\"; also 功徳がある \"to be beneficial\". Note: the reading is くどく, not こうとく. Close in meaning is ご利益 \"blessing, benefit (from gods or Buddhas)\", which stresses rather the concrete advantage for the believer.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"罪業を懺悔して新たな人生を歩み始めた。","romaji":"Zaigō o zange shite arata na jinsei o ayumi hajimeta.","german":"He repented of his sins and began a new life.","furigana":"ざいごう|ざんげ|あら|じんせい|あゆ|はじ"},{"japanese":"人は誰しも何らかの罪業を背負って生きている。","romaji":"Hito wa dare shimo nanraka no zaigō o seotte ikite iru.","german":"Everyone lives carrying some kind of guilt.","furigana":"ひと|だれ|なん|ざいごう|せお|い"},{"japanese":"主人公は自らの罪業の深さに苦しみ続けた。","romaji":"Shujinkō wa mizukara no zaigō no fukasa ni kurushimi tsuzuketa.","german":"The protagonist kept suffering over the depth of his sins.","furigana":"しゅじんこう|みずか|ざいごう|ふか|くる|つづ"}],"notes":"罪業 is a Buddhist-flavored term for sin in the sense of bad karma: misdeeds whose consequences one must bear in this or a later life. Typical are 罪業を背負う, 罪業が深い and 罪業を懺悔する. More general and everyday is 罪 \"guilt, sin, offense\"; 罪業 sounds heavy, literary and religious.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仏前で手を合わせ、これまでの過ちを懺悔した。","romaji":"Butsuzen de te o awase, kore made no ayamachi o zange shita.","german":"Pressing his hands together before the Buddha, he repented of his past errors.","furigana":"ぶつぜん|て|あ|あやま|ざんげ"},{"japanese":"修行僧たちは、毎朝自らの行いを懺悔する。","romaji":"Shugyōsō-tachi wa, maiasa mizukara no okonai o zange suru.","german":"The trainee monks repent of their conduct every morning.","furigana":"しゅぎょうそう|まいあさみずか|おこな|ざんげ"},{"japanese":"晩年の彼は、若い頃の過ちを懺悔するような気持ちで自伝を書いた。","romaji":"Bannen no kare wa, wakai koro no ayamachi o zange suru yō na kimochi de jiden o kaita.","german":"In his later years, he wrote his autobiography in the spirit of a confession of the mistakes of his youth.","furigana":"ばんねん|かれ|わか|ころ|あやま|ざんげ|きも|じでん|か"}],"notes":"懺悔 is the remorseful confession of one's own transgressions. Today it is generally read ざんげ, also in the Christian context (懺悔室 \"confessional\"); in Buddhism the reading さんげ is also traditionally used. As a transitive する-verb: 罪を懺悔する. In everyday speech it is often used half-jokingly for a confession; 後悔 by contrast is mere regret without confession.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"贖罪の意識が彼の人生を大きく変えた。","romaji":"Shokuzai no ishiki ga kare no jinsei o ōkiku kaeta.","german":"A sense of atonement changed his life greatly.","furigana":"しょくざい|いしき|かれ|じんせい|おお|か"},{"japanese":"キリスト教では、イエスの十字架の死は人類の贖罪のためだったとされる。","romaji":"Kirisutokyō de wa, Iesu no jūjika no shi wa jinrui no shokuzai no tame datta to sareru.","german":"In Christianity, Jesus' death on the cross is regarded as atonement for humankind.","furigana":"きょう|じゅうじか|し|じんるい|しょくざい"},{"japanese":"被告人は判決の前に贖罪寄付を行った。","romaji":"Hikokunin wa hanketsu no mae ni shokuzai kifu o okonatta.","german":"The defendant made an atonement donation before the verdict.","furigana":"ひこくにん|はんけつ|まえ|しょくざいきふ|おこな"}],"notes":"贖罪 is \"atonement\": to answer for guilt one has incurred through deeds or sacrifice. In Christian theology it denotes the redemption of humankind through the death of Jesus. In a secular sense it appears in 贖罪の意識 \"the need to atone for guilt\" and in criminal law as 贖罪寄付, a donation by the offender as a sign of remorse. The more everyday word for making amends is 償い (償う).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"修道院では禁欲的な生活が営まれている。","romaji":"Shūdōin de wa kin'yokuteki na seikatsu ga itonamarete iru.","german":"An ascetic life is led in the monastery.","furigana":"しゅうどういん|きんよくてき|せいかつ|いとな"},{"japanese":"大会前の一か月間、甘いものもお酒も断って禁欲生活を送った。","romaji":"Taikai mae no ikkagetsukan, amai mono mo osake mo tatte kin'yoku seikatsu o okutta.","german":"For the month before the tournament, I gave up sweets and alcohol and lived like an ascetic.","furigana":"たいかいまえ|いっ|げつかん|あま|さけ|た|きんよくせいかつ|おく"},{"japanese":"禁欲しすぎると、かえってストレスがたまる。","romaji":"Kin'yoku shisugiru to, kaette sutoresu ga tamaru.","german":"If you deny yourself too much, you actually build up stress.","furigana":"きんよく"}],"notes":"禁欲 is the deliberate renunciation of desires and pleasures such as sex, alcohol or luxury, whether religiously motivated as in monasticism or for personal reasons. Common in 禁欲的な生活 \"ascetic life\", 禁欲生活を送る and, as an intransitive する-verb, 禁欲する. Milder terms are 節制 \"moderation\" and 我慢 \"holding oneself back\"; 禁欲 means strict renunciation.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"清浄な心を保つことが修行の根本だ。","romaji":"Shōjō na kokoro o tamotsu koto ga shugyō no konpon da.","german":"Keeping a pure heart is the basis of religious practice.","furigana":"しょうじょう|こころ|たも|しゅぎょう|こんぽん"},{"japanese":"白装束の登山者たちが「六根清浄」と唱えながら山を登っていく。","romaji":"Shiroshōzoku no tozansha-tachi ga \"Rokkon shōjō\" to tonaenagara yama o nobotte iku.","german":"Climbers in white garb ascend the mountain chanting \"rokkon shojo\".","furigana":"しろしょうぞく|とざんしゃ|ろっこんしょうじょう|とな|やま|のぼ"},{"japanese":"仏教では、煩悩を離れた清浄な境地を目指す。","romaji":"Bukkyō de wa, bonnō o hanareta shōjō na kyōchi o mezasu.","german":"Buddhism aims at a pure state free of worldly desires.","furigana":"ぶっきょう|ぼんのう|はな|しょうじょう|きょうち|めざ"}],"notes":"清浄 means \"pure, free of dirt and defilement\". In religious contexts (Buddhism, Shinto) it is read しょうじょう, for example in 六根清浄, the chant of mountain pilgrims, or in 清浄な心. In the technical and everyday sense, by contrast, it is read せいじょう: 清浄な空気, 空気清浄機 \"air purifier\". Grammatically it is a noun and な-adjective (清浄な, 清浄に保つ).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"神道では穢れを祓うための儀式が重視される。","romaji":"Shintō de wa kegare o harau tame no gishiki ga jūshi sareru.","german":"In Shinto, rituals for purging impurity are emphasized.","furigana":"しんとう|けが|はら|ぎしき|じゅうし"},{"japanese":"葬儀の後に塩をまくのは、死の穢れを清めるためだと言われる。","romaji":"Sōgi no ato ni shio o maku no wa, shi no kegare o kiyomeru tame da to iwareru.","german":"It is said that salt is scattered after a funeral to purify the impurity of death.","furigana":"そうぎ|あと|しお|し|けが|きよ|い"},{"japanese":"子どもの穢れのない笑顔に心が洗われた。","romaji":"Kodomo no kegare no nai egao ni kokoro ga arawareta.","german":"The child's unblemished smile refreshed my heart.","furigana":"こ|けが|えがお|こころ|あら"}],"notes":"穢れ is a key concept of Shinto: ritual impurity that arises mainly through contact with death, blood or illness and is removed by 祓い or 禊 (穢れを祓う, 穢れを清める). Figuratively it also stands for a moral stain: 穢れのない心. The verb is intransitive 穢れる, transitive 穢す. It is often also written 汚れ; do not confuse it with よごれ \"physical dirt\" without ritual meaning.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"年末の大祓で一年の穢れを清める。","romaji":"Nenmatsu no ōharae de ichinen no kegare o kiyomeru.","german":"At the great purification at the end of the year, the impurities of the year are cleansed.","furigana":"ねんまつ||いちねん|けが|きよ"},{"japanese":"新しい車を買ったので、神社で交通安全のお祓いを受けた。","romaji":"Atarashii kuruma o katta node, jinja de kōtsū anzen no oharai o uketa.","german":"Since I bought a new car, I had a traffic-safety purification rite done at a shrine.","furigana":"あたら|くるま|か|じんじゃ|こうつうあんぜん|はら|う"},{"japanese":"今年は厄年なので、神社でお祓いをしてもらった。","romaji":"Kotoshi wa yakudoshi na node, jinja de oharai o shite moratta.","german":"Since this is my unlucky year, I had a purification rite performed at the shrine.","furigana":"ことし|やくどし|じんじゃ|はら"}],"notes":"祓い is the Shinto purification ceremony in which a priest drives away impurity (穢れ) and misfortune. In everyday speech one almost always says お祓い: お祓いを受ける, お祓いしてもらう, for example for a new car, in an unlucky year (厄祓い) or before building a house. Do not confuse it with the identically pronounced 払い \"payment\". The great purification ritual on June 30 and December 31 is called 大祓 (おおはらえ).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"滝に打たれる禊の行が今も一部で行われている。","romaji":"Taki ni utareru misogi no gyō ga ima mo ichibu de okonawarete iru.","german":"The ascetic practice of misogi under a waterfall is still carried out in some places today.","furigana":"たき|う|みそぎ|ぎょう|いま|いちぶ|おこな"},{"japanese":"古事記では、イザナギが川で禊をして穢れを落とした。","romaji":"Kojiki de wa, Izanagi ga kawa de misogi o shite kegare o otoshita.","german":"In the Kojiki, Izanagi performs misogi in a river and washes away his impurity.","furigana":"こじき|かわ|みそぎ|けが|お"},{"japanese":"不祥事を起こした議員は、選挙に勝って「禊は済んだ」と語った。","romaji":"Fushōji o okoshita giin wa, senkyo ni katte \"Misogi wa sunda\" to katatta.","german":"The lawmaker who had caused a scandal won the election and said, \"The misogi is done.\"","furigana":"ふしょうじ|お|ぎいん|せんきょ|か|みそぎ|す|かた"}],"notes":"禊 is the ritual purification of the body with water, in a river, in the sea or under a waterfall, to wash off impurity (穢れ); myth traces it back to the god イザナギ. One says 禊をする or 禊を行う; the spelling 禊ぎ also occurs. In politics and the media it is used figuratively: a politician who is reelected after a scandal declares 禊は済んだ \"the matter has been atoned for\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この神社の御神体は山そのものだ。","romaji":"Kono jinja no goshintai wa yama sono mono da.","german":"The sacred object of this shrine is the mountain itself.","furigana":"じんじゃ|ごしんたい|やま"},{"japanese":"那智の滝は飛瀧神社の御神体として祀られている。","romaji":"Nachi no taki wa Hirō Jinja no goshintai to shite matsurarete iru.","german":"The Nachi Falls are enshrined as the goshintai of Hiryu Shrine.","furigana":"なち|たき|ひろうじんじゃ|ごしんたい|まつ"},{"japanese":"御神体は本殿の奥に納められ、一般の人は見ることができない。","romaji":"Goshintai wa honden no oku ni osamerare, ippan no hito wa miru koto ga dekinai.","german":"The goshintai is kept deep inside the main hall and cannot be seen by the general public.","furigana":"ごしんたい|ほんでん|おく|おさ|いっぱん|ひと|み"}],"notes":"御神体 is the object in which, according to Shinto belief, the deity of a shrine dwells, for example a mirror, a sword, a rock, a waterfall or a whole mountain. It is usually kept hidden in the main sanctuary (本殿) and not shown publicly. Today it is often written ご神体; the prefix 御 expresses reverence, and the neutral form 神体 is rarer. Do not confuse it with 御本尊, the main object of worship of a Buddhist temple.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"古代では神託に基づいて重要な決定が下された。","romaji":"Kodai de wa shintaku ni motozuite jūyō na kettei ga kudasareta.","german":"Im Altertum wurden wichtige Entscheidungen auf der Grundlage von Orakeln getroffen.","furigana":"こだい|しんたく|もと|じゅうよう|けってい|くだ"},{"japanese":"デルフォイの神託は、ソクラテスより賢い者はいないと告げた。","romaji":"Derufoi no shintaku wa, Sokuratesu yori kashikoi mono wa inai to tsugeta.","german":"Das Orakel von Delphi verkündete, niemand sei weiser als Sokrates.","furigana":"しんたく|かしこ|もの|つ"},{"japanese":"彼は占い師の言葉を神託のように信じている。","romaji":"Kare wa uranaishi no kotoba o shintaku no yō ni shinjite iru.","german":"Er glaubt den Worten der Wahrsagerin wie einer göttlichen Offenbarung.","furigana":"かれ|うらな|し|ことば|しんたく|しん"}],"notes":"神託 ist eine Botschaft, die eine Gottheit über ein Medium wie eine Priesterin, einen Traum oder ein Los verkündet – im Deutschen „Orakel“ im Sinne des Orakelspruchs. Typische Verbindungen: 神託を受ける, 神託が下る, 神託を告げる. Umgangssprachlicher ist お告げ „Weisung, Eingebung“ (神のお告げ, 夢のお告げ). Übertragen heißt 神託のように „als wäre es ein Gotteswort“, für Aussagen, die man unkritisch befolgt.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"神道は日本固有の多神教的な信仰体系だ。","romaji":"Shintō wa Nihon koyū no tashinkyōteki na shinkō taikei da.","german":"Shinto is a polytheistic belief system native to Japan.","furigana":"しんとう|にほんこゆう|たしんきょうてき|しんこうたいけい"},{"japanese":"日本では、結婚式は神道、葬式は仏教で行う人が多い。","romaji":"Nihon de wa, kekkonshiki wa Shintō, sōshiki wa Bukkyō de okonau hito ga ōi.","german":"In Japan, many people hold weddings in the Shinto style and funerals in the Buddhist style.","furigana":"にほん|けっこんしき|しんとう|そうしき|ぶっきょう|おこな|ひと|おお"},{"japanese":"戦前は国家神道が国民の教育に組み込まれていた。","romaji":"Senzen wa kokka Shintō ga kokumin no kyōiku ni kumikomarete ita.","german":"Before the war, State Shinto was built into the education of the people.","furigana":"せんぜん|こっかしんとう|こくみん|きょういく|く|こ"}],"notes":"神道 is Japan's native religion, with the worship of countless deities (八百万の神) in nature, ancestors and places. Note the reading: しんとう, not しんどう. Typical contrast: 神道 and 仏教, shrine 神社 and temple 寺. Compounds: 国家神道 \"State Shinto\" (until 1945), 神道式 \"in the Shinto style\" (for a wedding also 神前式).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"境内には樹齢千年を超える御神木がそびえている。","romaji":"Keidai ni wa jurei sennen o koeru goshinboku ga sobiete iru.","german":"On the shrine grounds towers a sacred tree more than a thousand years old.","furigana":"けいだい|じゅれいせんねん|こ|ごしんぼく"},{"japanese":"夏祭りの日には、境内にたくさんの屋台が並ぶ。","romaji":"Natsumatsuri no hi ni wa, keidai ni takusan no yatai ga narabu.","german":"On the day of the summer festival, many food stalls line up on the shrine grounds.","furigana":"なつまつ|ひ|けいだい|やたい|なら"},{"japanese":"境内での喫煙はご遠慮ください。","romaji":"Keidai de no kitsuen wa goenryo kudasai.","german":"Please refrain from smoking on the temple grounds.","furigana":"けいだい|きつえん|えんりょ"}],"notes":"境内 is the enclosed precinct of a shrine or temple, usually counted from the torii or the temple gate. Typical: 境内に入る, 境内を散歩する, 境内で (as the site of festivals and markets), 境内の桜. On signs one often reads 境内での〜はご遠慮ください. For secular properties of schools, companies or houses, one says 敷地 instead.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"僧侶が托鉢をしながら経を唱えて町を巡った。","romaji":"Sōryo ga takuhatsu o shinagara kyō o tonaete machi o megutta.","german":"The monk walked through the town collecting alms and chanting sutras.","furigana":"そうりょ|たくはつ|きょう|とな|まち|めぐ"},{"japanese":"祖父の葬儀では、僧侶にお経をあげてもらった。","romaji":"Sofu no sōgi de wa, sōryo ni okyō o agete moratta.","german":"At my grandfather's funeral, we had a priest recite the sutras.","furigana":"そふ|そうぎ|そうりょ|きょう"},{"japanese":"彼は大学を出た後、僧侶になる道を選んだ。","romaji":"Kare wa daigaku o deta ato, sōryo ni naru michi o eranda.","german":"After finishing university, he chose the path of becoming a monk.","furigana":"かれ|だいがく|で|あと|そうりょ|みち|えら"}],"notes":"僧侶 is the neutral, somewhat formal word for Buddhist monks and priests. In everyday speech one says お坊さん, and for the head of a temple 住職 or 和尚. For Christian clergy one uses 牧師 or 神父, for Shinto priests 神主 or 神職. Typical: 僧侶になる, 僧侶にお経をあげてもらう.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この寺の住職は地域の文化活動にも熱心だ。","romaji":"Kono tera no jūshoku wa chiiki no bunka katsudō ni mo nesshin da.","german":"The abbot of this temple is also keen on the region's cultural activities.","furigana":"てら|じゅうしょく|ちいき|ぶんかかつどう|ねっしん"},{"japanese":"法事の日程について、ご住職に相談した。","romaji":"Hōji no nittei ni tsuite, gojūshoku ni sōdan shita.","german":"I consulted the head priest about the date of the memorial service.","furigana":"ほうじ|にってい|じゅうしょく|そうだん"},{"japanese":"父が引退し、兄が寺の住職を継いだ。","romaji":"Chichi ga intai shi, ani ga tera no jūshoku o tsuida.","german":"My father retired, and my older brother succeeded him as head priest of the temple.","furigana":"ちち|いんたい|あに|てら|じゅうしょく|つ"}],"notes":"住職 is the head of a Buddhist temple, who lives there and manages it; the post is often passed from father to son (住職を継ぐ). Respectful address: ご住職 or, depending on the school, 和尚さん. More general are 僧侶 and colloquially お坊さん \"monk\". The Shinto counterpart at a shrine is 宮司.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"宮司が祝詞を奏上して祭典が始まった。","romaji":"Gūji ga norito o sōjō shite saiten ga hajimatta.","german":"The festival began when the chief priest recited the ritual prayer.","furigana":"ぐうじ|のりと|そうじょう|さいてん|はじ"},{"japanese":"この神社の宮司は代々同じ家が務めている。","romaji":"Kono jinja no gūji wa daidai onaji ie ga tsutomete iru.","german":"The post of chief priest of this shrine has been held by the same family for generations.","furigana":"じんじゃ|ぐうじ|だいだいおな|いえ|つと"},{"japanese":"地鎮祭では宮司が土地を清め、工事の安全を祈った。","romaji":"Jichinsai de wa gūji ga tochi o kiyome, kōji no anzen o inotta.","german":"At the groundbreaking ceremony, the chief priest purified the land and prayed for safety in the construction.","furigana":"じちんさい|ぐうじ|とち|きよ|こうじ|あんぜん|いの"}],"notes":"宮司 is the highest priest of a Shinto shrine. Below him are ranks such as 禰宜 and 権禰宜; Shinto priests in general are called 神職 or, colloquially, 神主さん. Note the reading: ぐうじ, not きゅうじ. The Buddhist counterpart at a temple is 住職.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"巫女が神楽を舞う姿は神秘的な美しさがある。","romaji":"Miko ga kagura o mau sugata wa shinpiteki na utsukushisa ga aru.","german":"The sight of a shrine maiden performing kagura has a mystical beauty.","furigana":"みこ|かぐら|ま|すがた|しんぴてき|うつく"},{"japanese":"正月の三が日だけ、神社で巫女のアルバイトをした。","romaji":"Shōgatsu no sanganichi dake, jinja de miko no arubaito o shita.","german":"For just the first three days of the New Year, I worked a part-time job as a shrine maiden at a shrine.","furigana":"しょうがつ|さん|にち|じんじゃ|みこ"},{"japanese":"古代の巫女は神がかりになって神の言葉を伝えたとされる。","romaji":"Kodai no miko wa kamigakari ni natte kami no kotoba o tsutaeta to sareru.","german":"The shamanesses of ancient times are said to have conveyed the words of the gods in a trance.","furigana":"こだい|みこ|かみ|かみ|ことば|つた"}],"notes":"巫女 are young women in a white top and red hakama who help with rituals at the shrine, perform the sacred dance 神楽 and hand out amulets; at New Year many female students are hired as temporary help (巫女のアルバイト). Historically 巫女 also denotes a shamaness who proclaims the words of the gods in a trance. The actual priests are 神職 or 神主; 巫女 is not a consecrated priestly office.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"祝詞は古来の言葉で神への祈りを述べたものだ。","romaji":"Norito wa korai no kotoba de kami e no inori o nobeta mono da.","german":"A norito is a prayer to the gods set down in ancient language.","furigana":"のりと|こらい|ことば|かみ|いの|の"},{"japanese":"神主が祝詞をあげている間、参列者は頭を下げていた。","romaji":"Kannushi ga norito o agete iru aida, sanretsusha wa atama o sagete ita.","german":"While the priest was reciting the norito, the attendees kept their heads bowed.","furigana":"かんぬし|のりと|あいだ|さんれつしゃ|あたま|さ"},{"japanese":"結婚式で、宮司が二人の門出を祝う祝詞を奏上した。","romaji":"Kekkonshiki de, gūji ga futari no kadode o iwau norito o sōjō shita.","german":"At the wedding, the chief priest recited a norito celebrating the couple's new start.","furigana":"けっこんしき|ぐうじ|ふたり|かどで|いわ|のりと|そうじょう"}],"notes":"祝詞 is the prayer composed in Old Japanese that a Shinto priest recites before the gods at ceremonies. Fixed combinations: formally 祝詞を奏上する, otherwise 祝詞をあげる. The reading のりと is irregular; read しゅくし, the same characters mean \"words of congratulation\" (usually written 祝辞). What is meant is always the text or its recitation, not the whole ceremony.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新年に神社で御札を受けて神棚に祀る。","romaji":"Shinnen ni jinja de ofuda o ukete kamidana ni matsuru.","german":"At New Year, one receives an ofuda at the shrine and enshrines it on the household altar.","furigana":"しんねん|じんじゃ|おふだ|う|かみだな|まつ"},{"japanese":"台所の柱に火の用心の御札が貼ってある。","romaji":"Daidokoro no hashira ni hi no yōjin no ofuda ga hatte aru.","german":"A fire-prevention ofuda is stuck on the pillar in the kitchen.","furigana":"だいどころ|はしら|ひ|ようじん|おふだ|は"},{"japanese":"古い御札は、年末に神社へ返納する。","romaji":"Furui ofuda wa, nenmatsu ni jinja e hennō suru.","german":"Old ofuda are returned to the shrine at the end of the year.","furigana":"ふる|おふだ|ねんまつ|じんじゃ|へんのう"}],"notes":"御札 (usually written お札) is an amulet of paper or wood bearing the name of the deity and the seal of the shrine or temple, which one sets up at home on the 神棚 or attaches to a wall or door. One says 御札を受ける or いただく, not \"to buy\". Smaller and portable is お守り. Note: お札 read おさつ is a \"banknote\"; only おふだ is the amulet.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"五穀豊穣を祈って神前に奉納品が供えられた。","romaji":"Gokoku hōjō o inotte shinzen ni hōnōhin ga sonaerareta.","german":"Offerings were presented before the shrine with prayers for a bountiful harvest.","furigana":"|いの|しんぜん|ほうのうひん|そな"},{"japanese":"秋祭りでは、地元の子どもたちが神楽を奉納する。","romaji":"Akimatsuri de wa, jimoto no kodomotachi ga kagura o hōnō suru.","german":"At the autumn festival, local children dedicate kagura dances to the gods.","furigana":"あきまつ|じもと|こ|かぐら|ほうのう"},{"japanese":"酒造会社が神社に酒樽を奉納した。","romaji":"Shuzō gaisha ga jinja ni sakadaru o hōnō shita.","german":"A sake brewing company donated sake barrels to the shrine.","furigana":"しゅぞうがいしゃ|じんじゃ||ほうのう"}],"notes":"奉納 is the solemn offering of gifts or performances to gods and Buddhas: sake barrels, votive plaques, lanterns, but also dances, sumo or music. Transitive する-verb: 〜を神社に奉納する; as a modifier in 奉納相撲, 奉納演奏, 奉納品. In contrast to 供える \"to set out, offer (food etc.)\", 奉納 stresses the formal act of dedication; the donors are often named on plaques or stone lanterns.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"絵馬に願い事を書いて神社に奉納する。","romaji":"Ema ni negaigoto o kaite jinja ni hōnō suru.","german":"You write a wish on an ema and dedicate it at the shrine.","furigana":"えま|ねが|ごと|か|じんじゃ|ほうのう"},{"japanese":"天満宮には合格を願う受験生の絵馬がずらりと並んでいた。","romaji":"Tenmangū ni wa gōkaku o negau jukensei no ema ga zurari to narande ita.","german":"At the Tenmangu shrine, the ema of exam candidates praying to pass hung in rows.","furigana":"てんまんぐう|ごうかく|ねが|じゅけんせい|えま|なら"},{"japanese":"絵馬には、その年の干支が描かれていることが多い。","romaji":"Ema ni wa, sono toshi no eto ga egakarete iru koto ga ōi.","german":"Ema often depict the zodiac animal of that year.","furigana":"えま|とし||えが|おお"}],"notes":"絵馬 is a small wooden plaque on which one writes a wish and one's name and hangs it at a shrine or temple (絵馬を掛ける, 絵馬を奉納する). The name recalls that in the past living horses were donated and later replaced by pictures of horses; today many 絵馬 show the zodiac animal of the year. Especially well known are exam wishes at shrines of the god of scholars, Tenjin.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"厄年には厄払いの祈祷を受ける人が多い。","romaji":"Yakudoshi ni wa yakubarai no kitō o ukeru hito ga ōi.","german":"In an unlucky year, many people have a ritual to ward off misfortune performed.","furigana":"やくどし|やくばら|きとう|う|ひと|おお"},{"japanese":"四十二歳の厄年に、家族と神社へ厄払いに行った。","romaji":"Yonjūni-sai no yakudoshi ni, kazoku to jinja e yakubarai ni itta.","german":"In my unlucky year at forty-two, I went to the shrine with my family to have misfortune warded off.","furigana":"よんじゅうにさい|やくどし|かぞく|じんじゃ|やくばら|い"},{"japanese":"散々な一年だったから、厄払いにぱっと飲みに行こう。","romaji":"Sanzan na ichinen datta kara, yakubarai ni patto nomi ni ikō.","german":"It was a terrible year, so let's go out for a good drink to shake off the bad luck.","furigana":"さんざん|いちねん|やくばら|の|い"}],"notes":"厄払い is a ritual for warding off misfortune, especially in the 厄年, the years of life considered dangerous (around 42 for men, 33 for women). It is usually performed at a shrine: 厄払いをしてもらう, 厄払いに行く. Variants are the spelling 厄祓い and 厄除け, which is more usual at temples and for amulets. Colloquially it is also used figuratively: after a run of bad luck, 厄払いに飲みに行く.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"神仏習合は日本の宗教史における独特の現象だ。","romaji":"Shinbutsu shūgō wa Nihon no shūkyōshi ni okeru dokutoku no genshō da.","german":"The syncretism of Shinto and Buddhism is a unique phenomenon in Japanese religious history.","furigana":"しんぶつしゅうごう|にほん|しゅうきょうし|どくとく|げんしょう"},{"japanese":"寺の境内に神社があるのは、神仏習合の名残だ。","romaji":"Tera no keidai ni jinja ga aru no wa, shinbutsu shūgō no nagori da.","german":"The fact that there is a shrine on temple grounds is a remnant of the fusion of Shinto and Buddhism.","furigana":"てら|けいだい|じんじゃ|しんぶつしゅうごう|なごり"},{"japanese":"明治政府は神仏分離令を出し、神仏習合を否定した。","romaji":"Meiji seifu wa shinbutsu bunrirei o dashi, shinbutsu shūgō o hitei shita.","german":"The Meiji government issued an edict of separation and rejected the blending of Shinto and Buddhism.","furigana":"めいじせいふ|しんぶつぶんりれい|だ|しんぶつしゅうごう|ひてい"}],"notes":"神仏習合 is a yojijukugo made of 神 \"Shinto gods\", 仏 \"Buddhas\" and 習合 \"fusion\": the blending of Shinto and Buddhism that grew over more than a thousand years, in which Shinto gods were interpreted, for example, as manifestations of Buddhas (本地垂迹). The Meiji government officially ended it in 1868 with the 神仏分離令; the opposite term is therefore 神仏分離. A technical term of religious history, but common in travel guides.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"明治初期の廃仏毀釈により多くの寺院が破壊された。","romaji":"Meiji shoki no haibutsu kishaku ni yori ōku no jiin ga hakai sareta.","german":"Many temples were destroyed in the anti-Buddhist movement of the early Meiji period.","furigana":"めいじしょき|はいぶつきしゃく|おお|じいん|はかい"},{"japanese":"廃仏毀釈の嵐の中で、多くの仏像が売られたり焼かれたりした。","romaji":"Haibutsu kishaku no arashi no naka de, ōku no butsuzō ga uraretari yakaretari shita.","german":"In the storm of haibutsu kishaku, many Buddha statues were sold or burned.","furigana":"はいぶつきしゃく|あらし|なか|おお|ぶつぞう|う|や"},{"japanese":"興福寺の五重塔も、廃仏毀釈の時代には売りに出されたと伝えられる。","romaji":"Kōfukuji no gojū no tō mo, haibutsu kishaku no jidai ni wa uri ni dasareta to tsutaerareru.","german":"It is said that even the five-story pagoda of Kofuku-ji was put up for sale in the era of haibutsu kishaku.","furigana":"こうふくじ|ごじゅうのとう|はいぶつきしゃく|じだい|う|だ|つた"}],"notes":"廃仏毀釈 literally means \"to abolish Buddhism (廃仏) and destroy Shakyamuni (毀釈)\", where 釈 stands for 釈迦. It denotes the movement at the beginning of the Meiji period that, after the separation of Shinto and Buddhism in 1868, led to the destruction of countless temples, statues and scriptures. Typical: 廃仏毀釈の嵐, 廃仏毀釈で失われた. A historical technical term, rare outside historical writing.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"万物は無常であるという教えは仏教の根本思想だ。","romaji":"Banbutsu wa mujō de aru to iu oshie wa Bukkyō no konpon shisō da.","german":"The teaching that all things are impermanent is a fundamental idea of Buddhism.","furigana":"ばんぶつ|むじょう|おし|ぶっきょう|こんぽんしそう"},{"japanese":"桜がすぐに散るのを見ると、世の無常を感じる。","romaji":"Sakura ga sugu ni chiru no o miru to, yo no mujō o kanjiru.","german":"Seeing the cherry blossoms fall so quickly, I feel the impermanence of the world.","furigana":"さくら|ち|み|よ|むじょう|かん"},{"japanese":"方丈記には、鴨長明の無常観が色濃く表れている。","romaji":"Hōjōki ni wa, Kamo no Chōmei no mujōkan ga irokoku arawarete iru.","german":"The Hojoki strongly expresses Kamo no Chomei's view of impermanence.","furigana":"ほうじょうき||むじょうかん|いろこ|あらわ"}],"notes":"無常 is the fundamental Buddhist insight that nothing lasts and everything changes. As a noun and な/の-adjective: 人生の無常, 世の無常を感じる. Very common is 無常観 \"view of impermanence\", a central motif of classical literature such as 平家物語 and 方丈記. Do not confuse it with the identically pronounced 無情 \"heartless, unfeeling\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"無我の境地に至ることが仏教修行の核心だ。","romaji":"Muga no kyōchi ni itaru koto ga Bukkyō shugyō no kakushin da.","german":"Reaching the state of non-self is the core of Buddhist practice.","furigana":"むが|きょうち|いた|ぶっきょうしゅぎょう|かくしん"},{"japanese":"火事の中、彼は無我夢中で子どもを助け出した。","romaji":"Kaji no naka, kare wa muga muchū de kodomo o tasukedashita.","german":"In the fire, he rescued the child without a thought for himself.","furigana":"かじ|なか|かれ|むがむちゅう|こ|たす|だ"},{"japanese":"演奏に集中するうちに、無我の境地に入っていた。","romaji":"Ensō ni shūchū suru uchi ni, muga no kyōchi ni haitte ita.","german":"As I concentrated on playing, I had entered a state of complete self-forgetfulness.","furigana":"えんそう|しゅうちゅう|むが|きょうち|はい"}],"notes":"無我 has two levels: in Buddhism the doctrine of non-self, according to which there is no permanent ego; in everyday speech the state of complete self-forgetfulness. The latter occurs above all in 無我夢中 \"totally absorbed, without thinking\" (無我夢中で走った) and 無我の境地 \"state of self-forgetfulness\", for example in athletes or musicians. It coincides only partly with \"selflessness\" in the sense of unselfishness (無私, 利他).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"般若心経は色即是空の教えを説いている。","romaji":"Hannya Shingyō wa shiki soku ze kū no oshie o toite iru.","german":"The Heart Sutra teaches that form is emptiness.","furigana":"はんにゃしんぎょう|しきそくぜくう|おし|と"},{"japanese":"仏教の「空」は、何もないという意味ではない。","romaji":"Bukkyō no \"kū\" wa, nani mo nai to iu imi de wa nai.","german":"The \"emptiness\" of Buddhism does not mean that nothing exists at all.","furigana":"ぶっきょう|くう|なに|いみ"},{"japanese":"彼のパンチは空を切った。","romaji":"Kare no panchi wa kū o kitta.","german":"His punch cut through thin air.","furigana":"かれ|くう|き"}],"notes":"Read くう, 空 denotes in Buddhism \"emptiness\": all things have no independent, fixed essence, as known from 色即是空 in the Heart Sutra. Outside religion, くう stands for \"nothingness, into the void\": 空を切る \"to strike at thin air\", 空に帰する \"to come to nothing\". Do not confuse it with そら \"sky\" and から \"empty (container)\", which are written with the same character.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"諸行無常の響きありとは平家物語の冒頭だ。","romaji":"Shogyō mujō no hibiki ari to wa Heike Monogatari no bōtō da.","german":"\"... there is a sound of the impermanence of all things\" is the opening of the Tale of the Heike.","furigana":"しょぎょうむじょう|ひび|へいけものがたり|ぼうとう"},{"japanese":"栄華を誇った名店が閉店するとは、まさに諸行無常だ。","romaji":"Eiga o hokotta meiten ga heiten suru to wa, masa ni shogyō mujō da.","german":"For a once-glorious, famous restaurant to close, truly, nothing lasts.","furigana":"えいが|ほこ|めいてん|へいてん|しょぎょうむじょう"},{"japanese":"子どもの頃に遊んだ公園がなくなり、諸行無常を感じた。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro ni asonda kōen ga nakunari, shogyō mujō o kanjita.","german":"The park where I played as a child was gone, and I felt the impermanence of all things.","furigana":"こ|ころ|あそ|こうえん|しょぎょうむじょう|かん"}],"notes":"諸行無常 is a yojijukugo: 諸行 \"all conditioned things\" and 無常 \"without permanence\": nothing stays as it is. It is a principle of Buddhism and, through the famous opening of the 平家物語 (祇園精舎の鐘の声、諸行無常の響きあり), familiar to all Japanese. In everyday speech it is said with a sigh when something beautiful passes or the mighty fall: まさに諸行無常だ, 諸行無常を感じる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"色即是空は般若心経の核心的な教えだ。","romaji":"Shiki soku ze kū wa Hannya Shingyō no kakushinteki na oshie da.","german":"\"Form is emptiness\" is the central teaching of the Heart Sutra.","furigana":"しきそくぜくう|はんにゃしんぎょう|かくしんてき|おし"},{"japanese":"色即是空、空即是色と唱えながら写経をした。","romaji":"Shiki soku ze kū, kū soku ze shiki to tonaenagara shakyō o shita.","german":"I copied out the sutra while chanting, \"Form is emptiness, emptiness is form.\"","furigana":"しきそくぜくう|くうそくぜしき|とな|しゃきょう"},{"japanese":"色即是空の考え方に触れて、物への執着が少し薄れた。","romaji":"Shiki soku ze kū no kangaekata ni furete, mono e no shūchaku ga sukoshi usureta.","german":"Since encountering the idea of \"form is emptiness\", my attachment to things has faded a little.","furigana":"しきそくぜくう|かんが|かた|ふ|もの|しゅうちゃく|すこ|うす"}],"notes":"色即是空 comes from the Heart Sutra (般若心経): 色 \"form, everything perceptible to the senses\", 即是 \"is precisely\", 空 \"emptiness\": all phenomena have no fixed inherent nature. Immediately following is 空即是色 \"emptiness is form\"; the two are often quoted together. Note: 色 does not mean \"color\" here and is read しき. Outside religious or philosophical conversation it appears almost only as a quotation.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"浄土真宗では他力本願の教えが中心に据えられている。","romaji":"Jōdo Shinshū de wa tariki hongan no oshie ga chūshin ni suerarete iru.","german":"In Jodo Shinshu, the teaching of relying on Amida's power is at the center.","furigana":"じょうどしんしゅう|たりきほんがん|おし|ちゅうしん|す"},{"japanese":"他のチームの結果次第で優勝が決まるという他力本願の状況だ。","romaji":"Hoka no chīmu no kekka shidai de yūshō ga kimaru to iu tariki hongan no jōkyō da.","german":"The championship depends on the other teams' results, so it's out of our hands.","furigana":"ほか|けっかしだい|ゆうしょう|き|たりきほんがん|じょうきょう"},{"japanese":"宿題を友達に頼りきりの他力本願な態度は改めなさい。","romaji":"Shukudai o tomodachi ni tayorikiri no tariki hongan na taido wa aratamenasai.","german":"Stop depending entirely on your friends for your homework.","furigana":"しゅくだい|ともだち|たよ|たりきほんがん|たいど|あらた"}],"notes":"他力本願 is made up of 他力 \"another's power\", meaning the power of Amida, and 本願 \"original vow\", Amida's promise to save all beings. In 浄土真宗 it means relying entirely on this vow instead of on one's own effort (自力). Colloquially it is mostly used disparagingly for \"relying on others, doing nothing oneself\" (他力本願な態度), a usage that Buddhist circles criticize as a misunderstanding. In sports it is used soberly: one depends on the results of others.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"禅宗は自力による悟りを重視する。","romaji":"Zenshū wa jiriki ni yoru satori o jūshi suru.","german":"The Zen school emphasizes enlightenment through one's own effort.","furigana":"ぜんしゅう|じりき|さと|じゅうし"},{"japanese":"骨折から三か月で、ようやく自力で歩けるようになった。","romaji":"Kossetsu kara sankagetsu de, yōyaku jiriki de arukeru yō ni natta.","german":"Three months after the fracture, I was finally able to walk on my own again.","furigana":"こっせつ|さん|げつ|じりき|ある"},{"japanese":"遭難者は自力で山を下りてきた。","romaji":"Sōnansha wa jiriki de yama o orite kita.","german":"The stranded climber came down the mountain under his own power.","furigana":"そうなんしゃ|じりき|やま|お"}],"notes":"自力 means \"one's own strength\": managing something without outside help. In everyday speech it is very common with で: 自力で歩く, 自力で解決する, 自力で脱出する; in sports 自力優勝 \"winning the championship under one's own power\". In Buddhism it denotes the path to enlightenment through one's own practice, as in Zen; the opposite is 他力. Do not confuse it with 実力 \"actual ability\" and 自立 \"independence\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"浄土宗は他力による救済を説く。","romaji":"Jōdoshū wa tariki ni yoru kyūsai o toku.","german":"The Jodo school teaches salvation through the power of Buddha.","furigana":"じょうどしゅう|たりき|きゅうさい|と"},{"japanese":"自力か他力かという問題は、日本仏教の大きなテーマだった。","romaji":"Jiriki ka tariki ka to iu mondai wa, Nihon Bukkyō no ōkina tēma datta.","german":"The question of self-power or other-power was a major theme in Japanese Buddhism.","furigana":"じりき|たりき|もんだい|にほんぶっきょう|おお"},{"japanese":"親鸞は、自分の力を捨てて他力にすべてを任せよと説いた。","romaji":"Shinran wa, jibun no chikara o sutete tariki ni subete o makaseyo to toita.","german":"Shinran taught that one should abandon one's own power and entrust everything to other-power.","furigana":"|じぶん|ちから|す|たりき|まか|と"}],"notes":"他力 is \"another's power, help from outside\". In Buddhism, above all in 浄土宗 and 浄土真宗, it denotes the saving power of Amida Buddha, to whom the believer entrusts himself, as opposed to 自力, one's own practice. In everyday speech it appears almost only in contrast to 自力 or in 他力本願; for \"help from others\" one otherwise says 人の助け.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"南無阿弥陀仏と念仏を唱えることで極楽に生まれ変われると信じられている。","romaji":"Namu Amida Butsu to nenbutsu o tonaeru koto de gokuraku ni umarekawareru to shinjirarete iru.","german":"It is believed that by reciting the nenbutsu \"Namu Amida Butsu\" one can be reborn in paradise.","furigana":"なむあみだぶつ|ねんぶつ|とな|ごくらく|う|か|しん"},{"japanese":"祖母は毎朝、仏壇の前で念仏を唱えている。","romaji":"Sobo wa maiasa, butsudan no mae de nenbutsu o tonaete iru.","german":"Every morning my grandmother recites the nenbutsu in front of the household altar.","furigana":"そぼ|まいあさ|ぶつだん|まえ|ねんぶつ|とな"},{"japanese":"いくら注意しても、彼には馬の耳に念仏だ。","romaji":"Ikura chūi shite mo, kare ni wa uma no mimi ni nenbutsu da.","german":"No matter how much you warn him, it's like preaching to a horse's ear.","furigana":"ちゅうい|かれ|うま|みみ|ねんぶつ"}],"notes":"念仏 is, in Pure Land Buddhism, the invocation 南無阿弥陀仏; one says 念仏を唱える, more rarely 念仏する. Very common is the proverb 馬の耳に念仏 \"casting pearls before swine, preaching to deaf ears\". Also found is 念仏のように繰り返す \"to repeat like a prayer wheel\". For reciting sutras in general one says お経を読む／あげる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"真言宗では真言を唱えることで仏と一体になれるとされる。","romaji":"Shingonshū de wa shingon o tonaeru koto de hotoke to ittai ni nareru to sareru.","german":"In the Shingon school it is taught that by reciting mantras one can become one with the Buddha.","furigana":"しんごんしゅう|しんごん|とな|ほとけ|いったい"},{"japanese":"光明真言を唱えると、亡くなった人の罪が消えると信じられてきた。","romaji":"Kōmyō shingon o tonaeru to, nakunatta hito no tsumi ga kieru to shinjirarete kita.","german":"It has been believed that reciting the Komyo Shingon erases the sins of the dead.","furigana":"こうみょうしんごん|とな|な|ひと|つみ|き|しん"},{"japanese":"空海は唐で学んだ密教を、真言宗として日本に広めた。","romaji":"Kūkai wa Tō de mananda mikkyō o, Shingonshū to shite Nihon ni hirometa.","german":"Kukai spread the esoteric Buddhism he had learned in Tang China to Japan as the Shingon school.","furigana":"くうかい|とう|まな|みっきょう|しんごんしゅう|にほん|ひろ"}],"notes":"真言 is the \"true word\", the translation of mantra: short sacred formulas in Sino-Japanese rendering of the Sanskrit sounds, which are recited (真言を唱える). At the same time it is the short form for the school 真言宗 founded by 空海, centered on 高野山. The loanword マントラ is used rather in the context of yoga or Hinduism; related is 陀羅尼, a longer incantation.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"曼荼羅は仏教の宇宙観を視覚的に表現したものだ。","romaji":"Mandara wa Bukkyō no uchūkan o shikakuteki ni hyōgen shita mono da.","german":"A mandala is a visual representation of the Buddhist view of the cosmos.","furigana":"まんだら|ぶっきょう|うちゅうかん|しかくてき|ひょうげん"},{"japanese":"寺の本堂には、色鮮やかな曼荼羅が掛けられていた。","romaji":"Tera no hondō ni wa, iroazayaka na mandara ga kakerarete ita.","german":"A brightly colored mandala was hanging in the main hall of the temple.","furigana":"てら|ほんどう|いろあざ|まんだら|か"},{"japanese":"最近は、大人の塗り絵として曼荼羅アートが人気だ。","romaji":"Saikin wa, otona no nurie to shite mandara āto ga ninki da.","german":"Recently, mandala art has become popular as a coloring book for adults.","furigana":"さいきん|おとな|ぬ|え|まんだら|にんき"}],"notes":"曼荼羅 is a mandala: a mostly circular-square diagram that shows Buddhas and deities in a fixed arrangement and represents the cosmos or enlightenment. In Shingon, the 胎蔵界曼荼羅 and the 金剛界曼荼羅, together the 両界曼荼羅, are central. It is often also written in kana (まんだら, マンダラ) or as 曼陀羅. Figuratively it stands for a colorful, many-layered overall picture.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新年には神棚の御札を新しいものに取り替える。","romaji":"Shinnen ni wa kamidana no ofuda o atarashii mono ni torikaeru.","german":"At New Year, the talisman on the household altar is replaced with a new one.","furigana":"しんねん|かみだな|おふだ|あたら|と|か"},{"japanese":"祖父は毎朝、神棚に水とご飯を供えて手を合わせる。","romaji":"Sofu wa maiasa, kamidana ni mizu to gohan o sonaete te o awaseru.","german":"My grandfather puts water and rice on the household altar every morning and presses his hands together.","furigana":"そふ|まいあさ|かみだな|みず|はん|そな|て|あ"},{"japanese":"柔道の道場では、稽古の前に神棚に礼をする。","romaji":"Jūdō no dōjō de wa, keiko no mae ni kamidana ni rei o suru.","german":"In a judo dojo, people bow to the Shinto altar before practice.","furigana":"じゅうどう|どうじょう|けいこ|まえ|かみだな|れい"}],"notes":"神棚 is a small Shinto household altar in the form of a shelf, usually mounted high on a wall, on which one sets up お札 from shrines and offers rice, salt, water and 榊 branches. Its counterpart is the Buddhist 仏壇 for the ancestors; many traditional households have both. Typical: 神棚を祀る, 神棚に手を合わせる, 神棚にお供えする. One also finds a 神棚 in shops, companies and dojos.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"読経の際に木魚を叩いてリズムを刻む。","romaji":"Dokyō no sai ni mokugyo o tataite rizumu o kizamu.","german":"When reciting sutras, one strikes the wooden fish to keep the rhythm.","furigana":"どきょう|さい|もくぎょ|たた|きざ"},{"japanese":"ポクポクという木魚の音を聞くと、なぜか眠くなる。","romaji":"Pokupoku to iu mokugyo no oto o kiku to, nazeka nemuku naru.","german":"For some reason, I get sleepy when I hear the poku-poku of the wooden fish.","furigana":"もくぎょ|おと|き|ねむ"},{"japanese":"木魚の形が魚なのは、魚が昼も夜も目を閉じないからだと言われる。","romaji":"Mokugyo no katachi ga sakana na no wa, sakana ga hiru mo yoru mo me o tojinai kara da to iwareru.","german":"It is said that the mokugyo is shaped like a fish because fish never close their eyes, day or night.","furigana":"もくぎょ|かたち|さかな|さかな|ひる|よる|め|と|い"}],"notes":"木魚 is a hollow, round wooden instrument with carved fish scales that is struck with a padded mallet during sutra recitation to set the beat (木魚を叩く). The fish shape is meant to admonish vigilance, since according to old belief fish never close their eyes. It is common above all in Zen and in 浄土宗, but not in 浄土真宗. The tapping sound is rendered onomatopoeically as ポクポク.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大晦日に鐘楼で除夜の鐘が撞かれる。","romaji":"Ōmisoka ni shōrō de joya no kane ga tsukareru.","german":"On New Year's Eve, the year-end bell is rung in the bell tower.","furigana":"おおみそか|しょうろう|じょや|かね|"},{"japanese":"台風で古い鐘楼の屋根が壊れてしまった。","romaji":"Taifū de furui shōrō no yane ga kowarete shimatta.","german":"The roof of the old bell tower was damaged by the typhoon.","furigana":"たいふう|ふる|しょうろう|やね|こわ"},{"japanese":"ヨーロッパの町では、教会の鐘楼が遠くからでも見える。","romaji":"Yōroppa no machi de wa, kyōkai no shōrō ga tōku kara demo mieru.","german":"In European towns, you can see the church bell tower from far away.","furigana":"まち|きょうかい|しょうろう|とお|み"}],"notes":"鐘楼 is the bell tower or bell house of a temple, in which the large bronze bell hangs; in Japan usually an open pavilion with a roof. The bell is struck with a beam: 鐘を撞く（つく）. More colloquial is 鐘つき堂; 鐘楼 sounds technical and written and is also used for church towers (教会の鐘楼). Do not confuse it with the character 鍾.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この寺の山門は室町時代に建立されたものだ。","romaji":"Kono tera no sanmon wa Muromachi jidai ni konryū sareta mono da.","german":"The main gate of this temple was built in the Muromachi period.","furigana":"てら|さんもん|むろまちじだい|こんりゅう"},{"japanese":"山門をくぐると、静かな境内が広がっていた。","romaji":"Sanmon o kuguru to, shizuka na keidai ga hirogatte ita.","german":"Passing through the temple gate, a quiet precinct spread out before me.","furigana":"さんもん|しず|けいだい|ひろ"},{"japanese":"山門の両側には大きな仁王像が立っている。","romaji":"Sanmon no ryōgawa ni wa ōkina Niō-zō ga tatte iru.","german":"Large guardian statues stand on both sides of the temple gate.","furigana":"さんもん|りょうがわ|おお|におうぞう|た"}],"notes":"山門 is the main gate of a Buddhist temple. The name comes from the fact that temples were formerly located in the mountains and to this day bear a \"mountain name\" (山号). Typical are 山門をくぐる \"to pass through the gate\" and 山門の仁王像. At Zen temples the main gate is often written 三門; for Shinto shrines one says 鳥居 or 楼門, not 山門.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"壮大な伽藍配置はこの寺院の特徴だ。","romaji":"Sōdai na garan haichi wa kono jiin no tokuchō da.","german":"The magnificent layout of the temple complex is a feature of this temple.","furigana":"そうだい|がらんはいち|じいん|とくちょう"},{"japanese":"奈良には千年以上前の伽藍が今も残っている。","romaji":"Nara ni wa sennen ijō mae no garan ga ima mo nokotte iru.","german":"In Nara, temple complexes more than a thousand years old still stand today.","furigana":"なら|せんねんいじょうまえ|がらん|いま|のこ"},{"japanese":"火災で伽藍の大半が焼失した。","romaji":"Kasai de garan no taihan ga shōshitsu shita.","german":"Most of the temple complex was destroyed in the fire.","furigana":"かさい|がらん|たいはん|しょうしつ"}],"notes":"伽藍 is a short form of the Sanskrit word for \"monastery complex\" and denotes a large Buddhist temple with its halls. Fixed phrases are 伽藍配置 \"arrangement of the temple buildings\" and 七堂伽藍 \"complete complex with seven halls\". The word is art-historical and written; in everyday speech one simply says お寺. The word がらんどう \"completely empty, hollow\" is traced back to 伽藍堂.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"回廊を巡りながら壁画を鑑賞することができる。","romaji":"Kairō o meguri nagara hekiga o kanshō suru koto ga dekiru.","german":"Walking through the cloister, you can admire the wall paintings.","furigana":"かいろう|めぐ|へきが|かんしょう"},{"japanese":"厳島神社の回廊は海の上に延びている。","romaji":"Itsukushima Jinja no kairō wa umi no ue ni nobite iru.","german":"The corridors of Itsukushima Shrine extend out over the sea.","furigana":"|かいろう|うみ|うえ|の"},{"japanese":"雨の日でも回廊を通れば濡れずに本堂へ行ける。","romaji":"Ame no hi demo kairō o tōreba nurezu ni hondō e ikeru.","german":"Even on rainy days, if you take the covered corridor, you can get to the main hall without getting wet.","furigana":"あめ|ひ|かいろう|とお|ぬ|ほんどう|い"}],"notes":"回廊 is a roofed passage that runs around a building or a courtyard, at temples, shrines, palaces and monasteries (\"cloister\"). One goes 回廊を歩く／巡る. The ordinary hallway in a house or school is 廊下, a connecting passage between two buildings 渡り廊下. In politics and business 回廊 also stands for a \"corridor\", e.g. 経済回廊.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"境内には数多くの堂宇が建ち並んでいる。","romaji":"Keidai ni wa kazuōku no dōu ga tachinarande iru.","german":"Numerous buildings stand in a row on the temple grounds.","furigana":"けいだい|かずおお|どうう|た|なら"},{"japanese":"現在の堂宇は江戸時代に再建されたものだ。","romaji":"Genzai no dōu wa Edo jidai ni saiken sareta mono da.","german":"The present temple buildings were rebuilt in the Edo period.","furigana":"げんざい|どうう|えどじだい|さいけん"},{"japanese":"大地震で多くの堂宇が倒壊した。","romaji":"Ōjishin de ōku no dōu ga tōkai shita.","german":"Many temple halls collapsed in the great earthquake.","furigana":"おおじしん|おお|どうう|とうかい"}],"notes":"堂宇 is a literary word for temple buildings or halls; 堂 is the hall, 宇 the roof. It appears almost only in descriptions of temple complexes, in travel guides and in historical texts, often in the plural: 数多くの堂宇, 堂宇が建ち並ぶ. In speech one rather says お堂 or simply 建物.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仏像の光背は仏の智慧の光を象徴している。","romaji":"Butsuzō no kōhai wa hotoke no chie no hikari o shōchō shite iru.","german":"The halo of a Buddha statue symbolizes the light of the Buddha's wisdom.","furigana":"ぶつぞう|こうはい|ほとけ|ちえ|ひかり|しょうちょう"},{"japanese":"この仏像の光背には炎の模様が細かく彫られている。","romaji":"Kono butsuzō no kōhai ni wa honoo no moyō ga komakaku horarete iru.","german":"A flame pattern is finely carved into the halo of this Buddha statue.","furigana":"ぶつぞう|こうはい|ほのお|もよう|こま|ほ"},{"japanese":"修理のため、光背だけが本体から外された。","romaji":"Shūri no tame, kōhai dake ga hontai kara hazusareta.","german":"For the repair, only the halo was removed from the statue itself.","furigana":"しゅうり|こうはい|ほんたい|はず"}],"notes":"光背 is the sculpted aureole or mandorla behind a Buddha figure, a technical word of art history that refers to the physical component of the statue. For the glow of light in a figurative sense, one says 後光, for example 後光が差す \"to be surrounded by a halo\". For Christian images of saints one rather speaks of 光輪.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"蓮華は泥中から清らかに咲く花として仏教の象徴とされる。","romaji":"Renge wa deichū kara kiyoraka ni saku hana toshite Bukkyō no shōchō to sareru.","german":"The lotus is regarded as a symbol of Buddhism because it blooms pure out of the mud.","furigana":"れんげ|でいちゅう|きよ|さ|はな|ぶっきょう|しょうちょう"},{"japanese":"阿弥陀如来は蓮華座の上に座っている。","romaji":"Amida Nyorai wa rengeza no ue ni suwatte iru.","german":"Amida Buddha sits on a lotus pedestal.","furigana":"あみだにょらい|れんげざ|うえ|すわ"},{"japanese":"ラーメンのスープをれんげですくって飲んだ。","romaji":"Rāmen no sūpu o renge de sukutte nonda.","german":"I scooped up the ramen broth with the ceramic spoon and drank it.","furigana":"の"}],"notes":"蓮華 is the lotus blossom as a Buddhist symbol; the plant itself is called 蓮（はす）in everyday speech. Buddha figures sit on a lotus pedestal, 蓮華座. In daily life one meets the word mostly in kana: れんげ is the ceramic spoon for soup and ramen (short for 散り蓮華), and れんげ草 is the Chinese milk vetch, a spring flower in rice fields.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"僧侶は袈裟をまとって法要を執り行った。","romaji":"Sōryo wa kesa o matotte hōyō o toriokonatta.","german":"The monk put on a kesa and conducted the memorial service.","furigana":"そうりょ|けさ|ほうよう|と|おこな"},{"japanese":"バッグを袈裟懸けにして自転車に乗った。","romaji":"Baggu o kesagake ni shite jitensha ni notta.","german":"I slung my bag diagonally across my shoulder and rode my bicycle.","furigana":"けさが|じてんしゃ|の"},{"japanese":"坊主憎けりゃ袈裟まで憎いで、彼はその会社の製品を全部嫌がる。","romaji":"Bōzu nikukerya kesa made nikui de, kare wa sono kaisha no seihin o zenbu iyagaru.","german":"As the saying goes, if you hate the monk you hate even his robe; he dislikes every product of that company.","furigana":"ぼうずにく|けさ|にく|かれ|かいしゃ|せいひん|ぜんぶいや"}],"notes":"袈裟 is the outer garment of Buddhist monks, draped over the left shoulder; one says 袈裟をまとう／掛ける. Derived from it is 袈裟懸け \"diagonally over one shoulder\", e.g. for a shoulder bag. Well known is the proverb 坊主憎けりゃ袈裟まで憎い: if you hate someone, you hate everything connected with them.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"早朝の托鉢は禅寺の修行の一環だ。","romaji":"Sōchō no takuhatsu wa zendera no shugyō no ikkan da.","german":"The early-morning alms round is part of the training at a Zen temple.","furigana":"そうちょう|たくはつ|ぜんでら|しゅぎょう|いっかん"},{"japanese":"駅前で修行僧が托鉢をしていた。","romaji":"Ekimae de shugyōsō ga takuhatsu o shite ita.","german":"A trainee monk was collecting alms in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|しゅぎょうそう|たくはつ"},{"japanese":"タイでは毎朝、僧侶たちが托鉢に回る。","romaji":"Tai de wa maiasa, sōryotachi ga takuhatsu ni mawaru.","german":"In Thailand, monks go out on their alms round every morning.","furigana":"まいあさ|そうりょ|たくはつ|まわ"}],"notes":"托鉢 is the alms round of Buddhist monks, who receive food or money in their bowl (鉢); as a する-verb: 托鉢する, also 托鉢に出る／回る and 托鉢僧. In Japan one sees mainly Zen monks with large straw hats. The offering of laypeople is called 布施 or お布施; 托鉢 is the monk's side, 布施 that of the giver.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"精進料理は仏教の戒律に基づいた菜食料理だ。","romaji":"Shōjin ryōri wa Bukkyō no kairitsu ni motozuita saishoku ryōri da.","german":"Shojin cuisine is a vegetarian cuisine based on Buddhist precepts.","furigana":"しょうじんりょうり|ぶっきょう|かいりつ|もと|さいしょくりょうり"},{"japanese":"横綱の名に恥じぬよう、一層精進いたします。","romaji":"Yokozuna no na ni hajinu yō, issō shōjin itashimasu.","german":"I will strive even harder so as not to bring shame on the title of yokozuna.","furigana":"よこづな|な|は|いっそうしょうじん"},{"japanese":"彼は毎日の練習に精進し、ついに優勝を果たした。","romaji":"Kare wa mainichi no renshū ni shōjin shi, tsui ni yūshō o hatashita.","german":"He devoted himself to daily practice and finally won the championship.","furigana":"かれ|まいにち|れんしゅう|しょうじん|ゆうしょう|は"}],"notes":"精進 has two uses. Religiously, it is ascetic practice, above all abstaining from meat: 精進料理 \"Buddhist vegetarian cuisine\", 精進落とし \"the first normal meal after the mourning period\". Far more common today is 精進する \"to devote oneself wholeheartedly to something\", mostly with に: 芸に精進する. Typical of solemn resolutions is 一層精進いたします, for example in acceptance speeches of sumo wrestlers; 努力する is the neutral equivalent.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"注連縄は神聖な結界を示すものだ。","romaji":"Shimenawa wa shinsei na kekkai o shimesu mono da.","german":"A shimenawa rope marks a sacred boundary.","furigana":"しめなわ|しんせい|けっかい|しめ"},{"japanese":"この山はかつて女人結界とされ、女性は入れなかった。","romaji":"Kono yama wa katsute nyonin kekkai to sare, josei wa hairenakatta.","german":"This mountain was once a women-excluding sacred zone, and women could not enter.","furigana":"やま|にょにんけっかい|じょせい|はい"},{"japanese":"主人公は結界を張って妖怪の侵入を防いだ。","romaji":"Shujinkō wa kekkai o hatte yōkai no shinnyū o fuseida.","german":"The hero set up a barrier and kept the yokai from getting in.","furigana":"しゅじんこう|けっかい|は|ようかい|しんにゅう|ふせ"}],"notes":"結界 is originally a Buddhist term for a delimited sacred area into which disturbances may not intrude; Shinto ropes such as 注連縄 also mark such a boundary. Historically important is 女人結界, the ban on women entering sacred mountains. In manga, anime and games, 結界 is the magical protective barrier: 結界を張る \"to put up a barrier\", 結界を破る \"to break through it\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"四国八十八箇所の霊場巡りは古くからの巡礼の道だ。","romaji":"Shikoku hachijūhakkasho no reijō meguri wa furuku kara no junrei no michi da.","german":"The pilgrimage to the 88 sacred sites of Shikoku is an ancient pilgrim route.","furigana":"しこくはちじゅうはっかしょ|れいじょうめぐ|ふる|じゅんれい|みち"},{"japanese":"恐山は日本有数の霊場として知られている。","romaji":"Osorezan wa Nihon yūsū no reijō toshite shirarete iru.","german":"Osorezan is known as one of Japan's foremost sacred sites.","furigana":"|にほんゆうすう|れいじょう|し"},{"japanese":"母は西国三十三所の霊場を少しずつ巡っている。","romaji":"Haha wa Saigoku sanjūsansho no reijō o sukoshi zutsu megutte iru.","german":"My mother is visiting the sacred sites of the 33-temple Saigoku route little by little.","furigana":"はは|さいごくさんじゅうさんしょ|れいじょう|すこ|めぐ"}],"notes":"霊場 is a sacred site, above all a Buddhist temple or holy mountain as a pilgrimage destination. Such sites are often linked into numbered circuits, such as 四国八十八箇所 or 西国三十三所; visiting them is called 霊場巡り. The more general word 聖地 also covers sites of other religions and, jokingly, filming locations of anime; 霊場, by contrast, stays religious.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"巡礼者は白装束を身にまとって聖地を巡る。","romaji":"Junreisha wa shiroshōzoku o mi ni matotte seichi o meguru.","german":"Pilgrims dress in white garments and visit the holy sites.","furigana":"じゅんれいしゃ|しろしょうぞく|み|せいち|めぐ"},{"japanese":"定年後、父はスペインの巡礼の道を歩いた。","romaji":"Teinen-go, chichi wa Supein no junrei no michi o aruita.","german":"After retiring, my father walked the Camino de Santiago in Spain.","furigana":"ていねんご|ちち|じゅんれい|みち|ある"},{"japanese":"アニメの舞台になった町を巡礼するファンが増えている。","romaji":"Anime no butai ni natta machi o junrei suru fan ga fuete iru.","german":"More and more fans are making pilgrimages to towns that served as settings for anime.","furigana":"ぶたい|まち|じゅんれい|ふ"}],"notes":"巡礼 is a pilgrimage to several holy places, and also the pilgrim himself (usually 巡礼者). As a する-verb it takes を for the places visited: 聖地を巡礼する. Fixed phrases are 巡礼の旅 and 聖地巡礼, the latter today also used jokingly for fan trips to anime locations. 参拝, by contrast, is the single visit to a shrine or temple, 遍路 specifically the Shikoku pilgrimage.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お遍路さんをもてなす接待の文化が四国には残っている。","romaji":"Ohenro-san o motenasu settai no bunka ga Shikoku ni wa nokotte iru.","german":"The culture of offering hospitality to pilgrims survives on Shikoku.","furigana":"へんろ|せったい|ぶんか|しこく|のこ"},{"japanese":"母は一人で四国のお遍路に出た。","romaji":"Haha wa hitori de Shikoku no ohenro ni deta.","german":"My mother set out alone on the Shikoku pilgrimage.","furigana":"はは|ひとり|しこく|へんろ|で"},{"japanese":"遍路道の途中に小さな休憩所がある。","romaji":"Henro-michi no tochū ni chiisana kyūkeisho ga aru.","german":"There is a small rest area along the pilgrim trail.","furigana":"へんろみち|とちゅう|ちい|きゅうけいしょ"}],"notes":"遍路 denotes the pilgrimage to the 88 temples of Shikoku and also the pilgrim; in everyday speech almost always politely お遍路 or お遍路さん. One says お遍路に出る／お遍路を回る, and the pilgrim path is called 遍路道. The hospitality offered to pilgrims by locals is called お接待. For pilgrimages in general one uses 巡礼.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"宗教体験の神秘的な側面は科学では解明しきれない。","romaji":"Shūkyō taiken no shinpiteki na sokumen wa kagaku de wa kaimei shikirenai.","german":"The mystical aspects of religious experience cannot be fully explained by science.","furigana":"しゅうきょうたいけん|しんぴてき|そくめん|かがく|かいめい"},{"japanese":"宇宙の神秘に引かれて、天文学者になった。","romaji":"Uchū no shinpi ni hikarete, tenmongakusha ni natta.","german":"Drawn to the mysteries of the universe, I became an astronomer.","furigana":"うちゅう|しんぴ|ひ|てんもんがくしゃ"},{"japanese":"霧に包まれた湖は、神秘的な美しさだった。","romaji":"Kiri ni tsutsumareta mizuumi wa, shinpiteki na utsukushisa datta.","german":"The lake wrapped in mist had a mysterious beauty.","furigana":"きり|つつ|みずうみ|しんぴてき|うつく"}],"notes":"神秘 is the enigmatic, mysterious thing that exceeds human understanding. Common in 生命の神秘, 宇宙の神秘 and 神秘に包まれる; as an adjective one mostly uses 神秘的な \"mysterious, mystical\". 秘密, by contrast, is a secret that people deliberately conceal, 謎 an unsolved riddle. 神秘主義 is \"mysticism\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"聖人の奇跡が信仰を広める契機となった。","romaji":"Seijin no kiseki ga shinkō o hiromeru keiki to natta.","german":"The saint's miracles became an occasion for the spread of the faith.","furigana":"せいじん|きせき|しんこう|ひろ|けいき"},{"japanese":"車は大破したが、運転手は奇跡的に無傷だった。","romaji":"Kuruma wa taiha shita ga, untenshu wa kisekiteki ni mukizu datta.","german":"The car was totaled, but the driver miraculously escaped unharmed.","furigana":"くるま|たいは|うんてんしゅ|きせきてき|むきず"},{"japanese":"最後まで諦めなければ、奇跡が起きるかもしれない。","romaji":"Saigo made akiramenakereba, kiseki ga okiru kamo shirenai.","german":"If we don't give up until the end, a miracle might happen.","furigana":"さいご|あきら|きせき|お"}],"notes":"奇跡 is a miracle, in the religious as well as the everyday sense. Typical: 奇跡が起きる／起こる, 奇跡的に助かる \"to be saved as if by a miracle\", 奇跡の生還 \"miraculous survival\". In religious texts the spelling 奇蹟 is also found. 不思議 is only \"strange, mysterious\", without the sense of a supernatural event.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"預言者は神の啓示を人々に伝える使命を担った。","romaji":"Yogensha wa kami no keiji o hitobito ni tsutaeru shimei o ninatta.","german":"The prophet bore the mission of conveying God's revelation to the people.","furigana":"よげんしゃ|かみ|けいじ|ひとびと|つた|しめい|にな"},{"japanese":"彼女はその夢を神の啓示だと信じた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa sono yume o kami no keiji da to shinjita.","german":"She believed that dream to be a revelation from God.","furigana":"かのじょ|ゆめ|かみ|けいじ|しん"},{"japanese":"迷っていた私にとって、先生の一言はまるで啓示のようだった。","romaji":"Mayotte ita watashi ni totte, sensei no hitokoto wa marude keiji no yō datta.","german":"For me, who was undecided, my teacher's single remark was like a revelation.","furigana":"まよ|わたし|せんせい|ひとこと|けいじ"}],"notes":"啓示 is divine revelation, mostly in 神の啓示 and 啓示を受ける. Figuratively it stands for a sudden, decisive inspiration, often in a comparison: 天の啓示のように. For ordinary ideas one says ひらめき. Beware of homophones: 掲示 \"notice posted\" and 刑事 \"detective\" are also read けいじ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"衆生の救済は大乗仏教の根本理念だ。","romaji":"Shujō no kyūsai wa Daijō Bukkyō no konpon rinen da.","german":"The salvation of all sentient beings is the fundamental ideal of Mahayana Buddhism.","furigana":"しゅじょう|きゅうさい|だいじょうぶっきょう|こんぽんりねん"},{"japanese":"政府は被災者を救済するための特別措置を決めた。","romaji":"Seifu wa hisaisha o kyūsai suru tame no tokubetsu sochi o kimeta.","german":"The government decided on special measures to relieve the disaster victims.","furigana":"せいふ|ひさいしゃ|きゅうさい|とくべつそち|き"},{"japanese":"経営難の銀行を救済するため、公的資金が投入された。","romaji":"Keieinan no ginkō o kyūsai suru tame, kōteki shikin ga tōnyū sareta.","german":"Public funds were injected to bail out the troubled bank.","furigana":"けいえいなん|ぎんこう|きゅうさい|こうてきしきん|とうにゅう"}],"notes":"救済 means \"salvation\" religiously, but is just as often used in a secular sense for help that frees people or companies from distress: 被災者の救済, 救済措置, 救済策, 企業を救済する. 救助, by contrast, is the immediate rescue from danger (e.g. from rubble), 救援 the relief supplies from outside. As a transitive する-verb: 〜を救済する.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"救世主の到来を待ち望む思想は多くの宗教に共通する。","romaji":"Kyūseishu no tōrai o machinozomu shisō wa ōku no shūkyō ni kyōtsū suru.","german":"The expectation of the coming of a savior is common to many religions.","furigana":"きゅうせいしゅ|とうらい|ま|のぞ|しそう|おお|しゅうきょう|きょうつう"},{"japanese":"新しく入った選手は、チームの救世主となった。","romaji":"Atarashiku haitta senshu wa, chīmu no kyūseishu to natta.","german":"The new player became the savior of the team.","furigana":"あたら|はい|せんしゅ|きゅうせいしゅ"},{"japanese":"駅前で救世軍の人たちが募金を呼びかけていた。","romaji":"Ekimae de Kyūseigun no hitotachi ga bokin o yobikakete ita.","german":"People from the Salvation Army were soliciting donations in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|きゅうせいぐん|ひと|ぼきん|よ"}],"notes":"救世 \"saving the world\" hardly occurs on its own, but almost only in compounds: 救世主 \"savior, messiah\", figuratively also \"rescuer in need\", for example for a player who saves a team, and 救世軍 \"Salvation Army\". In Buddhist names it is read くせ, e.g. 救世観音.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"来世での幸福を願って善行を積む。","romaji":"Raise de no kōfuku o negatte zenkō o tsumu.","german":"I accumulate good deeds in the hope of happiness in the next life.","furigana":"らいせ|こうふく|ねが|ぜんこう|つ"},{"japanese":"来世でもまたあなたと出会いたい。","romaji":"Raise demo mata anata to deaitai.","german":"I want to meet you again in the next life too.","furigana":"らいせ|であ"},{"japanese":"仏教では、現世の行いが来世を決めると考えられている。","romaji":"Bukkyō de wa, gense no okonai ga raise o kimeru to kangaerarete iru.","german":"In Buddhism, it is believed that one's deeds in this life determine the next life.","furigana":"ぶっきょう|げんせ|おこな|らいせ|き|かんが"}],"notes":"来世 is, in the Buddhist worldview, the next life after rebirth, in the series 前世・現世・来世. あの世, by contrast, rather means the afterlife as the place of the dead. In everyday speech 来世 also appears romantically or jokingly: 来世でまた会おう, 来世に期待する \"maybe it'll work out in the next life\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"現世利益を求める信仰は日本的な宗教観の特徴だ。","romaji":"Genze riyaku o motomeru shinkō wa Nihonteki na shūkyōkan no tokuchō da.","german":"A faith that seeks this-worldly benefits is characteristic of the Japanese view of religion.","furigana":"げんぜりやく|もと|しんこう|にほんてき|しゅうきょうかん|とくちょう"},{"japanese":"現世での苦しみは来世で報われると信じる人もいる。","romaji":"Gense de no kurushimi wa raise de mukuwareru to shinjiru hito mo iru.","german":"Some people believe that suffering in this life will be rewarded in the next.","furigana":"げんせ|くる|らいせ|むく|しん|ひと"},{"japanese":"この絵巻は、現世と死後の世界の境を描いている。","romaji":"Kono emaki wa, gense to shigo no sekai no sakai o egaite iru.","german":"This picture scroll depicts the boundary between this world and the world after death.","furigana":"えまき|げんせ|しご|せかい|さかい|えが"}],"notes":"現世 is \"this world, this life\" as opposed to 前世 and 来世. The reading varies: on its own mostly げんせ, in Buddhist phrases げんぜ, especially in 現世利益（げんぜりやく） \"this-worldly benefit\", that is, prayers for health, success or wealth. In ordinary speech one more commonly says この世.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"前世の因縁が現世に影響を及ぼすと信じられている。","romaji":"Zense no innen ga gense ni eikyō o oyobosu to shinjirarete iru.","german":"It is believed that karmic connections from past lives influence the present life.","furigana":"ぜんせ|いんねん|げんせ|えいきょう|およ|しん"},{"japanese":"あの二人は、前世からの縁で結ばれているのかもしれない。","romaji":"Ano futari wa, zense kara no en de musubarete iru no kamo shirenai.","german":"Maybe those two are bound together by a tie from a past life.","furigana":"ふたり|ぜんせ|えん|むす"},{"japanese":"こんなに運が悪いなんて、前世で何か悪いことでもしたのかな。","romaji":"Konna ni un ga warui nante, zense de nanika warui koto demo shita no ka na.","german":"To have such bad luck... did I do something terrible in a past life?","furigana":"うん|わる|ぜんせ|なに|わる"}],"notes":"前世（ぜんせ） is the earlier life before the present existence, part of the series 前世・現世・来世. Typical: 前世の記憶, 前世からの縁, 前世で何か悪いことをしたのかな (jokingly when having bad luck). The reading ぜんせい, by contrast, means \"former ages\". The word is common in everyday conversations about fate and spirituality.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"怨霊信仰は日本の民間信仰の一つだ。","romaji":"Onryō shinkō wa Nihon no minkan shinkō no hitotsu da.","german":"Belief in vengeful spirits is part of Japanese folk religion.","furigana":"おんりょうしんこう|にほん|みんかんしんこう|ひと"},{"japanese":"菅原道真の怨霊を鎮めるために天満宮が建てられた。","romaji":"Sugawara no Michizane no onryō o shizumeru tame ni Tenmangū ga taterareta.","german":"The Tenmangu shrine was built to appease the vengeful spirit of Sugawara no Michizane.","furigana":"|おんりょう|しず|てんまんぐう|た"},{"japanese":"その屋敷には怨霊が出るという噂がある。","romaji":"Sono yashiki ni wa onryō ga deru to iu uwasa ga aru.","german":"Rumor has it that a vengeful spirit haunts that mansion.","furigana":"やしき|おんりょう|で|うわさ"}],"notes":"怨霊 is the ghost of a person who died with a grudge and takes revenge: 怨（うら）み \"grudge\" + 霊. Typical are 怨霊のたたり \"curse of a vengeful spirit\" and 怨霊を鎮める. Famous are, for example, the spirits of 菅原道真 and 平将門. 幽霊 is \"ghost\" in general, 悪霊 an evil spirit that does not necessarily come from a dead person.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"御霊信仰は非業の死を遂げた人の霊を鎮めるものだ。","romaji":"Goryō shinkō wa higō no shi o togeta hito no rei o shizumeru mono da.","german":"The Goryo cult serves to pacify the spirits of those who met an unjust death.","furigana":"ごりょうしんこう|ひごう|し|と|ひと|れい|しず"},{"japanese":"祇園祭は、もともと疫病を鎮める御霊会として始まった。","romaji":"Gion Matsuri wa, motomoto ekibyō o shizumeru goryōe toshite hajimatta.","german":"The Gion Festival began originally as a goryo-e ceremony to quell epidemics.","furigana":"|えきびょう|しず|ごりょうえ|はじ"},{"japanese":"京都には御霊神社と呼ばれる神社がいくつかある。","romaji":"Kyōto ni wa Goryō Jinja to yobareru jinja ga ikutsuka aru.","german":"There are several shrines in Kyoto called Goryo Shrine.","furigana":"きょうと|ごりょうじんじゃ|よ|じんじゃ"}],"notes":"御霊（ごりょう） is above all a term of religious history: the spirit of a person who died a violent or unjust death and must be pacified through worship, hence 御霊信仰 and 御霊会. The same characters are read みたま when speaking reverently of the soul of a deceased person. On condolence envelopes one finds 御霊前, read ごれいぜん.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"戦没者の鎮魂のための式典が毎年行われる。","romaji":"Senbotsusha no chinkon no tame no shikiten ga maitoshi okonawareru.","german":"A ceremony to repose the souls of the war dead is held every year.","furigana":"せんぼつしゃ|ちんこん|しきてん|まいとしおこな"},{"japanese":"式典の最後に、モーツァルトの鎮魂歌が演奏された。","romaji":"Shikiten no saigo ni, Mōtsaruto no chinkonka ga ensō sareta.","german":"At the end of the ceremony, Mozart's Requiem was performed.","furigana":"しきてん|さいご|ちんこんか|えんそう"},{"japanese":"人々は海に向かって鎮魂の祈りを捧げた。","romaji":"Hitobito wa umi ni mukatte chinkon no inori o sasageta.","german":"Facing the sea, people offered a prayer for the repose of the souls of the dead.","furigana":"ひとびと|うみ|む|ちんこん|いの|ささ"}],"notes":"鎮魂 is the pacification of the souls of the deceased, especially of war and disaster victims: 鎮魂の祈り, 鎮魂の碑. 鎮魂歌 is the \"requiem\". For memorial services one usually says 慰霊祭, for Buddhist memorial masses 供養; 鎮魂 sounds more solemn and literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"火による浄化の儀式は多くの文化に見られる。","romaji":"Hi ni yoru jōka no gishiki wa ōku no bunka ni mirareru.","german":"Rituals of purification by fire are found in many cultures.","furigana":"ひ|じょうか|ぎしき|おお|ぶんか|み"},{"japanese":"この装置は川の水を浄化して、飲めるようにする。","romaji":"Kono sōchi wa kawa no mizu o jōka shite, nomeru yō ni suru.","german":"This device purifies river water and makes it drinkable.","furigana":"そうち|かわ|みず|じょうか|の"},{"japanese":"美しい歌声を聴いて、心が浄化されたような気がした。","romaji":"Utsukushii utagoe o kiite, kokoro ga jōka sareta yō na ki ga shita.","german":"Listening to the beautiful singing voice, I felt as though my heart had been cleansed.","furigana":"うつく|うたごえ|き|こころ|じょうか|き"}],"notes":"浄化 is \"purification, cleansing\", religious but above all technical and figurative: 水を浄化する, 浄化槽 \"septic tank\", 政界の浄化 \"cleansing of politics\". In everyday speech one says 心が浄化される when music or a sight \"washes the soul clean\". 掃除 is mere cleaning, 洗浄 washing with water; ritual purification in Shinto is called お清め or お祓い.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"即身成仏の教えは真言密教の核心だ。","romaji":"Sokushin jōbutsu no oshie wa Shingon mikkyō no kakushin da.","german":"The teaching of attaining buddhahood in this very body is the core of Shingon esoteric Buddhism.","furigana":"そくしんじょうぶつ|おし|しんごんみっきょう|かくしん"},{"japanese":"空海は即身成仏の思想を体系的に説いた。","romaji":"Kūkai wa sokushin jōbutsu no shisō o taikeiteki ni toita.","german":"Kukai systematically expounded the idea of sokushin jobutsu.","furigana":"くうかい|そくしんじょうぶつ|しそう|たいけいてき|と"},{"japanese":"即身成仏と、ミイラになった即身仏とは別のものだ。","romaji":"Sokushin jōbutsu to, miira ni natta sokushinbutsu to wa betsu no mono da.","german":"Sokushin jobutsu is different from sokushinbutsu, the mummified monks.","furigana":"そくしんじょうぶつ|そくしんぶつ|べつ"}],"notes":"即身成仏 is a four-character term from Buddhist doctrine: 即身 \"in this very body\" + 成仏 \"to become a Buddha\", that is, attaining enlightenment already in this life instead of only after countless rebirths. It is the core teaching of the Shingon Buddhism founded by 空海. Do not confuse it with 即身仏, the self-mummified monks found, for example, in Yamagata. 成仏 on its own also means \"to rest in peace\" in everyday speech.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"キリシタンの殉教の歴史は長崎に深く刻まれている。","romaji":"Kirishitan no junkyō no rekishi wa Nagasaki ni fukaku kizamarete iru.","german":"The history of the martyrdom of the Christians is deeply engraved in Nagasaki.","furigana":"じゅんきょう|れきし|ながさき|ふか|きざ"},{"japanese":"多くの信者が信仰を捨てずに殉教した。","romaji":"Ōku no shinja ga shinkō o sutezu ni junkyō shita.","german":"Many believers died as martyrs rather than renounce their faith.","furigana":"おお|しんじゃ|しんこう|す|じゅんきょう"},{"japanese":"彼は殉教者として今も人々に敬われている。","romaji":"Kare wa junkyōsha toshite ima mo hitobito ni uyamawarete iru.","german":"He is still revered by people as a martyr.","furigana":"かれ|じゅんきょうしゃ|いま|ひとびと|うやま"}],"notes":"殉教 means to die for one's faith; as an intransitive する-verb: 殉教する, and the martyr is called 殉教者. In Japan one thinks above all of the persecuted Christians of the Edo period. Similarly built are 殉職 \"to die in the line of duty\" (police, fire department) and 殉死 \"to follow one's lord in death\"; only 殉教 is religious.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"正統派から異端と見なされた教派が弾圧された。","romaji":"Seitōha kara itan to minasareta kyōha ga dan'atsu sareta.","german":"Sects regarded as heretical by the orthodox were suppressed.","furigana":"せいとうは|いたん|み|きょうは|だんあつ"},{"japanese":"彼の理論は、当時の学界では異端とされた。","romaji":"Kare no riron wa, tōji no gakkai de wa itan to sareta.","german":"His theory was regarded as heterodox in the academic world of the time.","furigana":"かれ|りろん|とうじ|がっかい|いたん"},{"japanese":"彼はゲーム業界の異端児と呼ばれている。","romaji":"Kare wa gēmu gyōkai no itanji to yobarete iru.","german":"He is called the maverick of the gaming industry.","furigana":"かれ|ぎょうかい|いたんじ|よ"}],"notes":"異端 is a doctrine or person that deviates from the recognized doctrinal opinion (正統): 異端の説, 異端者, 異端視する, 異端審問 \"Inquisition\". The word is also used in a secular sense: 異端とされる理論 in science, 異端児 for an outsider or maverick in an industry. More neutral for \"unusual\" are 型破り or 変わり者.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"正統派の教義解釈をめぐって論争が続いている。","romaji":"Seitōha no kyōgi kaishaku o megutte ronsō ga tsuzuite iru.","german":"The dispute over the orthodox interpretation of the doctrine continues.","furigana":"せいとうは|きょうぎかいしゃく|ろんそう|つづ"},{"japanese":"彼こそが王家の正統な後継者だ。","romaji":"Kare koso ga ōke no seitō na kōkeisha da.","german":"He alone is the legitimate heir of the royal house.","furigana":"かれ|おうけ|せいとう|こうけいしゃ"},{"japanese":"この店では正統派のナポリピザが食べられる。","romaji":"Kono mise de wa seitōha no Napori piza ga taberareru.","german":"At this restaurant you can eat classic Neapolitan pizza.","furigana":"みせ|せいとうは|た"}],"notes":"正統 denotes the rightful, handed-down line: religiously orthodoxy (opposite 異端), politically the legitimate succession: 正統な後継者, 正統性. 正統派 also means \"classic, in the traditional style\" (正統派のピザ, 正統派の美人). Beware of the homophone 正当 \"justified, lawful\" in 正当な理由 or 正当防衛, which has nothing to do with tradition.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"世俗化の進行は宗教離れの一因とされている。","romaji":"Sezokuka no shinkō wa shūkyō banare no ichiin to sarete iru.","german":"The advance of secularization is considered one cause of the turn away from religion.","furigana":"せぞくか|しんこう|しゅうきょうばな|いちいん"},{"japanese":"名誉や金銭といった世俗的な欲から自由になりたい。","romaji":"Meiyo ya kinsen to itta sezokuteki na yoku kara jiyū ni naritai.","german":"I want to be free from worldly desires such as fame and money.","furigana":"めいよ|きんせん|せぞくてき|よく|じゆう"},{"japanese":"トルコは世俗主義を掲げる国家として建国された。","romaji":"Toruko wa sezoku shugi o kakageru kokka toshite kenkoku sareta.","german":"Turkey was founded as a state upholding secularism.","furigana":"せぞくしゅぎ|かか|こっか|けんこく"}],"notes":"世俗 is the \"world\" as opposed to the religious or spiritual: worldly things, ordinary customs and desires. Common are 世俗的な \"worldly, profane\", 世俗化 \"secularization\" and 世俗主義 \"secularism, laicism\". 俗世 similarly means the worldly life that one leaves as a monk; for society in everyday life one says 世間.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"聖職に就く者には高い倫理観が求められる。","romaji":"Seishoku ni tsuku mono ni wa takai rinrikan ga motomerareru.","german":"A high standard of ethics is demanded of those who hold clerical office.","furigana":"せいしょく|つ|もの|たか|りんりかん|もと"},{"japanese":"かつて教師は聖職と呼ばれていた。","romaji":"Katsute kyōshi wa seishoku to yobarete ita.","german":"Teaching used to be called a sacred calling.","furigana":"きょうし|せいしょく|よ"},{"japanese":"彼女の兄は教会の聖職者だ。","romaji":"Kanojo no ani wa kyōkai no seishokusha da.","german":"Her older brother is a clergyman at a church.","furigana":"かのじょ|あに|きょうかい|せいしょくしゃ"}],"notes":"聖職 is the clerical office; clergy are called 聖職者, and taking up an office is 聖職に就く. In addition, 聖職 denotes a profession regarded as a special, almost sacred calling, above all that of the teacher: 教師は聖職だ is a well-known formula, today often discussed critically. For the pastor himself one says concretely 牧師, 神父 or 僧侶.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"聖域に立ち入ることは固く禁じられている。","romaji":"Seiiki ni tachiiru koto wa kataku kinjirarete iru.","german":"Entering the sacred precinct is strictly prohibited.","furigana":"せいいき|た|い|かた|きん"},{"japanese":"財政再建のためには、どの予算にも聖域はない。","romaji":"Zaisei saiken no tame ni wa, dono yosan ni mo seiiki wa nai.","german":"For fiscal rehabilitation, no budget item is sacrosanct.","furigana":"ざいせいさいけん|よさん|せいいき"},{"japanese":"書斎は父にとっての聖域で、子どもは入れなかった。","romaji":"Shosai wa chichi ni totte no seiiki de, kodomo wa hairenakatta.","german":"The study was my father's sanctuary, and we children could not enter.","furigana":"しょさい|ちち|せいいき|こ|はい"}],"notes":"聖域 is first of all a holy precinct that one may not simply enter. Figuratively it denotes an area exempt from criticism or cuts; famous is the slogan 聖域なき構造改革 \"structural reform without sacred cows\"; one says 〜に聖域はない. Casually it can also mean a private retreat: 父の書斎は聖域だ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"俗世を離れて山中で修行する僧侶もいる。","romaji":"Zokuse o hanarete sanchū de shugyō suru sōryo mo iru.","german":"Some monks leave the secular world and train in the mountains.","furigana":"ぞくせ|はな|さんちゅう|しゅぎょう|そうりょ"},{"japanese":"山奥の温泉宿にいると、俗世の悩みを忘れられる。","romaji":"Yamaoku no onsen'yado ni iru to, zokuse no nayami o wasurerareru.","german":"Staying at an onsen inn deep in the mountains, you can forget the worries of the secular world.","furigana":"やまおく|おんせんやど|ぞくせ|なや|わす"},{"japanese":"出家とは、俗世との縁を断つことだ。","romaji":"Shukke to wa, zokuse to no en o tatsu koto da.","german":"Leaving home to become a monk means cutting one's ties with the secular world.","furigana":"しゅっけ|ぞくせ|えん|た"}],"notes":"俗世 is the ordinary world shaped by desires and worries, which one leaves behind as a monk. Typical: 俗世を離れる／捨てる, 俗世の煩わしさ. More common and somewhat more colloquial is the longer form 俗世間. 世俗 stresses the worldly as a quality (世俗的), 俗世 the place from which one withdraws.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"若くして出家し仏門に入った。","romaji":"Wakaku shite shukke shi butsumon ni haitta.","german":"He became a monk and entered the Buddhist priesthood while still young.","furigana":"わか|しゅっけ|ぶつもん|はい"},{"japanese":"夫を亡くした後、彼女は出家して尼になった。","romaji":"Otto o nakushita ato, kanojo wa shukke shite ama ni natta.","german":"After losing her husband, she renounced the world and became a nun.","furigana":"おっと|な|あと|かのじょ|しゅっけ|あま"},{"japanese":"出家した僧は、家族と離れて寺で暮らす。","romaji":"Shukke shita sō wa, kazoku to hanarete tera de kurasu.","german":"A monk who has renounced the world lives in a temple, apart from his family.","furigana":"しゅっけ|そう|かぞく|はな|てら|く"}],"notes":"出家 means to leave family life and become a monk or nun; as an intransitive する-verb: 出家する, often with 仏門に入る or 尼になる. It also denotes the monk himself. The opposite is 在家 \"layperson\"; returning to secular life is called 還俗.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"在家信者も寺院の運営に積極的に関わっている。","romaji":"Zaike shinja mo jiin no un'ei ni sekkyokuteki ni kakawatte iru.","german":"Lay followers too are actively involved in running the temple.","furigana":"ざいけしんじゃ|じいん|うんえい|せっきょくてき|かか"},{"japanese":"出家せず、在家のまま仏道を学ぶ人も多い。","romaji":"Shukke sezu, zaike no mama butsudō o manabu hito mo ōi.","german":"Many people study the Buddhist path as laypeople without becoming monks.","furigana":"しゅっけ|ざいけ|ぶつどう|まな|ひと|おお"},{"japanese":"浄土真宗は在家の信者を中心に広まった。","romaji":"Jōdo Shinshū wa zaike no shinja o chūshin ni hiromatta.","german":"Jodo Shinshu spread mainly among lay believers.","furigana":"じょうどしんしゅう|ざいけ|しんじゃ|ちゅうしん|ひろ"}],"notes":"在家 is the Buddhist layperson who leads a normal family life, as opposed to the 出家. It mostly appears attributively: 在家信者, 在家仏教, 在家のまま. The reading is ざいけ; the rare meaning \"farmhouse in the country\" plays hardly any role in present-day Japanese.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仏壇に位牌を安置して故人を偲ぶ。","romaji":"Butsudan ni ihai o anchi shite kojin o shinobu.","german":"The tablet is enshrined on the household altar in remembrance of the deceased.","furigana":"ぶつだん|いはい|あんち|こじん|しの"},{"japanese":"火事のとき、祖母は真っ先に位牌を持って逃げた。","romaji":"Kaji no toki, sobo wa massaki ni ihai o motte nigeta.","german":"When the fire broke out, my grandmother first of all fled with the ancestral tablets.","furigana":"かじ|そぼ|ま|さき|いはい|も|に"},{"japanese":"位牌には故人の戒名が書かれている。","romaji":"Ihai ni wa kojin no kaimyō ga kakarete iru.","german":"The deceased's posthumous Buddhist name is written on the memorial tablet.","furigana":"いはい|こじん|かいみょう|か"}],"notes":"位牌 is the lacquered wooden tablet bearing the Buddhist posthumous name (戒名) and the date of death, which stands in the household altar (仏壇) and is venerated on behalf of the deceased. After the funeral one first uses a plain wooden tablet (白木の位牌), and after 49 days a permanent one. Typical: 位牌を安置する, 位牌に手を合わせる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"墓地に立てられた卒塔婆は故人の追善供養のためだ。","romaji":"Bochi ni taterareta sotoba wa kojin no tsuizen kuyō no tame da.","german":"The sotoba set up in the graveyard are for the memorial rites of the deceased.","furigana":"ぼち|た|そとば|こじん|ついぜんくよう"},{"japanese":"七回忌の法要のため、お寺に卒塔婆を一本お願いした。","romaji":"Nanakaiki no hōyō no tame, otera ni sotoba o ippon onegai shita.","german":"For the memorial service on the sixth anniversary of the death, I asked the temple for one sotoba.","furigana":"ななかいき|ほうよう|てら|そとば|いっぽん|ねが"},{"japanese":"風が吹くと、墓の後ろの卒塔婆がカタカタと鳴った。","romaji":"Kaze ga fuku to, haka no ushiro no sotoba ga katakata to natta.","german":"When the wind blew, the sotoba behind the grave rattled.","furigana":"かぜ|ふ|はか|うし|そとば|な"}],"notes":"卒塔婆 goes back to the Sanskrit word for \"stupa\". In present-day Japan it almost always means the narrow wooden slats with Sanskrit characters and the posthumous name that are set up behind the grave at memorial services or at Obon: 卒塔婆を立てる. Short forms include 塔婆; the spelling 卒都婆 also occurs.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"般若の智慧をもって煩悩を打ち破る。","romaji":"Hannya no chie o motte bonnō o uchiyaburu.","german":"One overcomes worldly desires with the wisdom of prajna.","furigana":"はんにゃ|ちえ|ぼんのう|う|やぶ"},{"japanese":"祖父は毎朝、仏壇の前で般若心経を唱えている。","romaji":"Sofu wa maiasa, butsudan no mae de Hannya Shingyō o tonaete iru.","german":"Every morning my grandfather recites the Heart Sutra in front of the household altar.","furigana":"そふ|まいあさ|ぶつだん|まえ|はんにゃしんぎょう|とな"},{"japanese":"約束を忘れて帰ったら、妻が般若のような顔をしていた。","romaji":"Yakusoku o wasurete kaettara, tsuma ga hannya no yō na kao o shite ita.","german":"When I came home after forgetting our appointment, my wife had a face like a hannya.","furigana":"やくそく|わす|かえ|つま|はんにゃ|かお"}],"notes":"般若 is the Buddhist wisdom that leads to enlightenment; best known is the Heart Sutra 般若心経. In everyday life, however, one often thinks of the 般若 mask of Noh theater: a horned demoness embodying female jealousy and rage. Hence one says jokingly 般若のような顔 for a frighteningly angry face.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"座禅により三昧の境地に入ることを目指す。","romaji":"Zazen ni yori sanmai no kyōchi ni hairu koto o mezasu.","german":"Through zazen one aims to enter the state of samadhi.","furigana":"ざぜん|さんまい|きょうち|はい|めざ"},{"japanese":"休みの日は朝から晩まで読書三昧だ。","romaji":"Yasumi no hi wa asa kara ban made dokusho zanmai da.","german":"On days off I spend from morning till night absorbed in reading.","furigana":"やす|ひ|あさ|ばん|どくしょざんまい"},{"japanese":"宝くじに当たった彼は、贅沢三昧の生活を送っている。","romaji":"Takarakuji ni atatta kare wa, zeitaku zanmai no seikatsu o okutte iru.","german":"Since winning the lottery, he has been living a life of pure luxury.","furigana":"たから|あ|かれ|ぜいたくざんまい|せいかつ|おく"}],"notes":"三昧 is originally meditative absorption (samadhi): 三昧の境地. Much more common today is its use as a suffix with the voiced initial ざんまい: 読書三昧, ゲーム三昧, 贅沢三昧, \"to give oneself entirely to something, to one's heart's content\". Often there is an undertone of enjoyment or mild criticism of excess.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"住職の法話には日常に役立つ教えが含まれている。","romaji":"Jūshoku no hōwa ni wa nichijō ni yakudatsu oshie ga fukumarete iru.","german":"The abbot's sermons contain teachings useful in daily life.","furigana":"じゅうしょく|ほうわ|にちじょう|やくだ|おし|ふく"},{"japanese":"法事のあとで、お坊さんが短い法話をしてくださった。","romaji":"Hōji no ato de, obōsan ga mijikai hōwa o shite kudasatta.","german":"After the memorial service, the monk gave a short sermon.","furigana":"ほうじ|ぼう|みじか|ほうわ"},{"japanese":"その寺では月に一度、誰でも参加できる法話会が開かれる。","romaji":"Sono tera de wa tsuki ni ichido, dare demo sanka dekiru hōwakai ga hirakareru.","german":"At that temple, a sermon gathering that anyone can attend is held once a month.","furigana":"てら|つき|いちど|だれ|さんか|ほうわかい|ひら"}],"notes":"法話 is a Buddhist sermon, usually an easy-to-understand talk by a temple priest after a ceremony or at a lecture evening (法話会). One says 法話を聞く／する. The old word 説教 also meant \"sermon\" but in everyday speech mainly means \"a lecture, a telling-off\"; for religious addresses 法話 is therefore the unambiguous choice.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"禅問答は弟子の悟りの深さを試すものだ。","romaji":"Zen mondō wa deshi no satori no fukasa o tamesu mono da.","german":"Zen dialogue is meant to test the depth of a disciple's enlightenment.","furigana":"ぜんもんどう|でし|さと|ふか|ため"},{"japanese":"受付で、入れる入れないの押し問答になった。","romaji":"Uketsuke de, haireru hairenai no oshimondō ni natta.","german":"At the reception desk, it turned into a back-and-forth over whether we could go in or not.","furigana":"うけつけ|はい|はい|お|もんどう"},{"japanese":"問答無用で警備員に追い出された。","romaji":"Mondō muyō de keibiin ni oidasareta.","german":"The guard threw me out without any discussion.","furigana":"もんどうむよう|けいびいん|お|だ"}],"notes":"問答 is an exchange of questions and answers, especially the teaching dialogue in Zen (禅問答). Figuratively, 禅問答 also means \"an incomprehensible conversation in which people talk past each other\". Very common are 押し問答 \"stubborn back-and-forth\" and 問答無用 \"no discussion!\"; 一問一答 is a question-and-answer format.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"公案を通じて論理を超えた悟りの体験を促す。","romaji":"Kōan o tsūjite ronri o koeta satori no taiken o unagasu.","german":"Koans encourage an experience of enlightenment beyond logic.","furigana":"こうあん|つう|ろんり|こ|さと|たいけん|うなが"},{"japanese":"「隻手の声」は最もよく知られた公案の一つだ。","romaji":"\"Sekishu no koe\" wa mottomo yoku shirareta kōan no hitotsu da.","german":"\"The sound of one hand\" is one of the best-known koans.","furigana":"せきしゅ|こえ|もっと|し|こうあん|ひと"},{"japanese":"修行僧は師から与えられた公案について何日も考え続けた。","romaji":"Shugyōsō wa shi kara ataerareta kōan ni tsuite nannichi mo kangaetsuzuketa.","german":"The trainee monk kept pondering the koan his master gave him for days.","furigana":"しゅぎょうそう|し|あた|こうあん|なんにち|かんが|つづ"}],"notes":"公案 is a paradoxical task that the Zen master gives the student to meditate on, such as 隻手の声 \"the sound of one hand\". It is meant to break through logical thinking; one says 公案を与える, 公案に取り組む. The Rinzai school in particular works with koans, while the Soto school relies more on pure sitting (只管打坐).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"禅の教えは言葉を超えた直接的な体験を重視する。","romaji":"Zen no oshie wa kotoba o koeta chokusetsuteki na taiken o jūshi suru.","german":"The teaching of Zen values direct experience beyond words.","furigana":"ぜん|おし|ことば|こ|ちょくせつてき|たいけん|じゅうし"},{"japanese":"京都の禅寺で座禅を体験した。","romaji":"Kyōto no zendera de zazen o taiken shita.","german":"I tried seated meditation at a Zen temple in Kyoto.","furigana":"きょうと|ぜんでら|ざぜん|たいけん"},{"japanese":"スティーブ・ジョブズは禅に深い関心を持っていた。","romaji":"Sutību Jobuzu wa Zen ni fukai kanshin o motte ita.","german":"Steve Jobs was deeply interested in Zen.","furigana":"ぜん|ふか|かんしん|も"}],"notes":"禅 is both meditation (dhyana) and the Zen school of Buddhism. On its own it mostly stands in 禅の教え／思想／精神; more common are compounds: 禅宗 \"Zen school\", 禅寺 \"Zen temple\", 禅僧, 座禅 \"seated meditation\", 禅問答. Via the West, the word is now also returning as a lifestyle term for calm and simplicity.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"密教の修法は秘密の伝授によって師から弟子へ受け継がれる。","romaji":"Mikkyō no shuhō wa himitsu no denju ni yotte shi kara deshi e uketsugareru.","german":"The practices of esoteric Buddhism are passed from master to disciple through secret transmission.","furigana":"みっきょう|しゅほう|ひみつ|でんじゅ|し|でし|う|つ"},{"japanese":"高野山は真言密教の聖地として知られる。","romaji":"Kōyasan wa Shingon mikkyō no seichi toshite shirareru.","german":"Mt. Koya is known as the sacred site of Shingon esoteric Buddhism.","furigana":"こうやさん|しんごんみっきょう|せいち|し"},{"japanese":"密教の寺では、護摩を焚いて祈りを捧げる。","romaji":"Mikkyō no tera de wa, goma o taite inori o sasageru.","german":"At esoteric temples, people burn a goma fire and offer prayers.","furigana":"みっきょう|てら|ごま|た|いの|ささ"}],"notes":"密教 is esoteric Buddhism, whose teachings and rituals are passed on only through direct initiation from master to student. In Japan these are above all Shingon Buddhism (東密) and parts of the Tendai school (台密); typical are mandalas, mantras and the fire ritual 護摩. The counterpart is 顕教, the publicly accessible teaching.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"顕教は誰にでも開かれた教えを特徴とする。","romaji":"Kenkyō wa dare ni demo hirakareta oshie o tokuchō to suru.","german":"Exoteric Buddhism is characterized by teachings open to everyone.","furigana":"けんきょう|だれ|ひら|おし|とくちょう"},{"japanese":"空海は仏教を顕教と密教に分けて論じた。","romaji":"Kūkai wa Bukkyō o kenkyō to mikkyō ni wakete ronjita.","german":"Kukai discussed Buddhism by dividing it into exoteric and esoteric.","furigana":"くうかい|ぶっきょう|けんきょう|みっきょう|わ|ろん"},{"japanese":"中世の大寺院では、顕教と密教の両方が学ばれていた。","romaji":"Chūsei no daijiin de wa, kenkyō to mikkyō no ryōhō ga manabarete ita.","german":"At the great temples of the Middle Ages, both exoteric and esoteric teachings were studied.","furigana":"ちゅうせい|だいじいん|けんきょう|みっきょう|りょうほう|まな"}],"notes":"顕教 denotes the \"revealed\" teaching of Buddhism, accessible to all in texts and sermons. The word exists almost only as the counterpart of 密教, coined by 空海 to stress the superiority of the esoteric teaching; hence often in pairs: 顕教と密教, 顕密. A technical term of religious history, practically unused in everyday speech.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"末法思想の広まりが浄土信仰を盛んにした。","romaji":"Mappō shisō no hiromari ga Jōdo shinkō o sakan ni shita.","german":"The spread of mappo thought made Pure Land faith flourish.","furigana":"まっぽうしそう|ひろ|じょうどしんこう|さか"},{"japanese":"平安時代の人々は、1052年に末法の世が始まると信じていた。","romaji":"Heian jidai no hitobito wa, 1052-nen ni mappō no yo ga hajimaru to shinjite ita.","german":"People of the Heian period believed that the age of decline began in 1052.","furigana":"へいあんじだい|ひとびと|ねん|まっぽう|よ|はじ|しん"},{"japanese":"相次ぐ事件に、まるで末法の世だと嘆く人もいる。","romaji":"Aitsugu jiken ni, marude mappō no yo da to nageku hito mo iru.","german":"With one incident after another, some lament that we are living in a degenerate age.","furigana":"あいつ|じけん|まっぽう|よ|なげ|ひと"}],"notes":"末法 is the last of the three ages after the Buddha's death (正法・像法・末法), in which the teaching still exists but no one attains enlightenment anymore. In Japan it was believed to have begun in 1052; the 末法思想 promoted the Amida faith in the Pure Land. Figuratively 末法の世 means \"degenerate times\", like the everyday lament 世も末だ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仏教では方便を用いて人々を真理へ導くことが許される。","romaji":"Bukkyō de wa hōben o mochiite hitobito o shinri e michibiku koto ga yurusareru.","german":"In Buddhism, it is permitted to use skillful means to lead people to the truth.","furigana":"ぶっきょう|ほうべん|もち|ひとびと|しんり|みちび|ゆる"},{"japanese":"嘘も方便と言うし、病気の祖母には本当のことを言わなかった。","romaji":"Uso mo hōben to iu shi, byōki no sobo ni wa hontō no koto o iwanakatta.","german":"They say a lie can be an expedient, so I didn't tell my sick grandmother the truth.","furigana":"うそ|ほうべん|い|びょうき|そぼ|ほんとう|い"},{"japanese":"あの約束は、その場をしのぐための方便にすぎなかった。","romaji":"Ano yakusoku wa, sono ba o shinogu tame no hōben ni suginakatta.","german":"That promise was nothing more than an expedient to get through the moment.","furigana":"やくそく|ば|ほうべん"}],"notes":"方便 is in Buddhism the \"skillful means\" by which the Buddha adapts the teaching to the understanding of his listeners. In everyday speech it means \"a means to an end, a stopgap\", often with a slightly apologetic tone: 一時の方便, 〜するための方便にすぎない. Very well known is the proverb 嘘も方便 \"even a lie can be useful\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"宣教師たちは福音を伝えるために遥か東方まで来た。","romaji":"Senkyōshitachi wa fukuin o tsutaeru tame ni haruka tōhō made kita.","german":"The missionaries came all the way to the distant East to spread the gospel.","furigana":"せんきょうし|ふくいん|つた|はる|とうほう|き"},{"japanese":"この新薬は、難病に苦しむ患者にとって大きな福音となった。","romaji":"Kono shin'yaku wa, nanbyō ni kurushimu kanja ni totte ōkina fukuin to natta.","german":"This new drug was a great boon for patients suffering from intractable diseases.","furigana":"しんやく|なんびょう|くる|かんじゃ|おお|ふくいん"},{"japanese":"福音書には、イエスの生涯と教えが記されている。","romaji":"Fukuinsho ni wa, Iesu no shōgai to oshie ga shirusarete iru.","german":"The Gospels record the life and teachings of Jesus.","furigana":"ふくいんしょ|しょうがい|おし|しる"}],"notes":"福音 is the Christian gospel (福音を伝える／宣べ伝える); the four Gospels are called 福音書. In a secular sense it stands for \"good news\", especially an innovation that is a blessing for a particular group: 〜にとって福音となる, for example a new drug for patients. Somewhat elevated; in everyday speech one otherwise says いい知らせ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"洗礼を受けてキリスト教徒となった。","romaji":"Senrei o ukete Kirisutokyōto to natta.","german":"Er empfing die Taufe und wurde Christ.","furigana":"せんれい|う|きょうと"},{"japanese":"牧師が生まれたばかりの赤ちゃんに洗礼を授けた。","romaji":"Bokushi ga umareta bakari no akachan ni senrei o sazuketa.","german":"Der Pfarrer taufte das neugeborene Baby.","furigana":"ぼくし|う|あか|せんれい|さず"},{"japanese":"新人投手はデビュー戦でプロの洗礼を受けた。","romaji":"Shinjin tōshu wa debyūsen de puro no senrei o uketa.","german":"Der junge Pitcher bekam in seinem Debütspiel zu spüren, wie hart es bei den Profis zugeht.","furigana":"しんじんとうしゅ|せん|せんれい|う"}],"notes":"洗礼 ist die christliche Taufe: 洗礼を受ける „getauft werden“, 洗礼を授ける „taufen“, 洗礼名 „Taufname“. Sehr häufig ist die übertragene Bedeutung „erste (oft harte) Erfahrung mit etwas“: プロの洗礼を受ける, 都会の洗礼 – ähnlich dem deutschen „Feuertaufe“.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"聖餐式はキリスト教の重要な儀式の一つだ。","romaji":"Seisanshiki wa Kirisutokyō no jūyō na gishiki no hitotsu da.","german":"Holy Communion is one of the most important ceremonies of Christianity.","furigana":"せいさんしき|きょう|じゅうよう|ぎしき|ひと"},{"japanese":"日曜日の礼拝で聖餐にあずかった。","romaji":"Nichiyōbi no reihai de seisan ni azukatta.","german":"I received Communion at the Sunday service.","furigana":"にちようび|れいはい|せいさん"},{"japanese":"聖餐では、パンとぶどう酒がキリストの体と血を表す。","romaji":"Seisan de wa, pan to budōshu ga Kirisuto no karada to chi o arawasu.","german":"In Communion, the bread and wine represent the body and blood of Christ.","furigana":"せいさん|しゅ|からだ|ち|あらわ"}],"notes":"聖餐 is the Christian Lord's Supper with bread and wine; the celebration is called 聖餐式, and taking part is 聖餐にあずかる. The term is mainly Protestant; in the Catholic Church one speaks rather of 聖体拝領 or the ミサ. The historical \"Last Supper\" is 最後の晩餐. Do not confuse it with the homophones 清算 \"settlement of accounts\" and 精算 \"fare adjustment\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自然崇拝は世界各地の原始宗教に共通する要素だ。","romaji":"Shizen sūhai wa sekai kakuchi no genshi shūkyō ni kyōtsū suru yōso da.","german":"Nature worship is an element common to primitive religions around the world.","furigana":"しぜんすうはい|せかいかくち|げんししゅうきょう|きょうつう|ようそ"},{"japanese":"イスラム教では偶像崇拝が禁じられている。","romaji":"Isuramu-kyō de wa gūzō sūhai ga kinjirarete iru.","german":"Idol worship is forbidden in Islam.","furigana":"きょう|ぐうぞうすうはい|きん"},{"japanese":"彼はあの作家を神のように崇拝している。","romaji":"Kare wa ano sakka o kami no yō ni sūhai shite iru.","german":"He worships that writer like a god.","furigana":"かれ|さっか|かみ|すうはい"}],"notes":"崇拝 is deep veneration up to adoration, religious as well as secular; as a transitive する-verb: 〜を崇拝する. Fixed compounds are 偶像崇拝 \"idolatry\", 祖先崇拝 \"ancestor worship\", 自然崇拝, 英雄崇拝. Applied to persons it is much stronger than 尊敬 \"respect\", almost deification.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"偶像崇拝を禁じる宗教もある。","romaji":"Gūzō sūhai o kinjiru shūkyō mo aru.","german":"Some religions forbid the worship of idols.","furigana":"ぐうぞうすうはい|きん|しゅうきょう"},{"japanese":"戦後、その政治家は国民の偶像として祭り上げられた。","romaji":"Sengo, sono seijika wa kokumin no gūzō toshite matsuriagerareta.","german":"After the war, that politician was set up as an idol of the nation.","furigana":"せんご|せいじか|こくみん|ぐうぞう|まつ|あ"},{"japanese":"博物館には古代の木製の偶像が展示されている。","romaji":"Hakubutsukan ni wa kodai no mokusei no gūzō ga tenji sarete iru.","german":"Ancient wooden idols are on display at the museum.","furigana":"はくぶつかん|こだい|もくせい|ぐうぞう|てんじ"}],"notes":"偶像 denotes an image or statue venerated as a deity and appears above all in 偶像崇拝 \"idolatry, image worship\". Figuratively an 偶像 is a person who is blindly revered; 偶像化する means \"to turn into an idol, glorify\". For pop idols one now almost always says アイドル; 偶像 sounds stiff and literary for that.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本の宗教文化は多神教的な性格を持っている。","romaji":"Nihon no shūkyō bunka wa tashinkyōteki na seikaku o motte iru.","german":"Japan's religious culture has a polytheistic character.","furigana":"にほん|しゅうきょうぶんか|たしんきょうてき|せいかく|も"},{"japanese":"古代ギリシャは多神教の世界で、神々にはそれぞれ役割があった。","romaji":"Kodai Girisha wa tashinkyō no sekai de, kamigami ni wa sorezore yakuwari ga atta.","german":"Ancient Greece was a polytheistic world in which each god had its own role.","furigana":"こだい|たしんきょう|せかい|かみがみ|やくわり"},{"japanese":"授業で一神教と多神教の違いについて議論した。","romaji":"Jugyō de isshinkyō to tashinkyō no chigai ni tsuite giron shita.","german":"In class we discussed the difference between monotheism and polytheism.","furigana":"じゅぎょう|いっしんきょう|たしんきょう|ちが|ぎろん"}],"notes":"多神教 is the technical term for \"polytheism\"; the opposite is 一神教. With 的 it becomes adjectival: 多神教的な世界観 \"polytheistic worldview\". In connection with Japan the catchphrase 八百万の神 \"the eight million gods\" of Shinto often comes up, regarded as a typical example of 多神教. The word belongs to religious studies, history and factual texts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一神教の伝統はユダヤ教に始まる。","romaji":"Isshinkyō no dentō wa Yudayakyō ni hajimaru.","german":"The tradition of monotheism begins with Judaism.","furigana":"いっしんきょう|でんとう|きょう|はじ"},{"japanese":"キリスト教もイスラム教も一神教に分類される。","romaji":"Kirisutokyō mo Isuramukyō mo isshinkyō ni bunrui sareru.","german":"Both Christianity and Islam are classified as monotheistic.","furigana":"きょう|きょう|いっしんきょう|ぶんるい"},{"japanese":"一神教の文化圏では、神は唯一絶対の存在とされる。","romaji":"Isshinkyō no bunkaken de wa, kami wa yuiitsu zettai no sonzai to sareru.","german":"In monotheistic cultural spheres, God is regarded as the one absolute being.","furigana":"いっしんきょう|ぶんかけん|かみ|ゆいいつぜったい|そんざい"}],"notes":"一神教 \"monotheism\" denotes the belief in a single God; the opposite is 多神教. Typical 一神教 named are ユダヤ教, キリスト教 and イスラム教. Adjectivally: 一神教的な価値観 \"monotheistic values\". Note the reading いっしんきょう with small っ, not いちしんきょう.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本の自然観には汎神論的な要素が見られる。","romaji":"Nihon no shizenkan ni wa hanshinronteki na yōso ga mirareru.","german":"Pantheistic elements can be seen in the Japanese view of nature.","furigana":"にほん|しぜんかん|はんしんろんてき|ようそ|み"},{"japanese":"スピノザは「神即自然」という汎神論を唱えた。","romaji":"Supinoza wa \"Kami soku shizen\" to iu hanshinron o tonaeta.","german":"Spinoza advocated a pantheism summed up as \"God is nature\".","furigana":"かみそくしぜん|はんしんろん|とな"},{"japanese":"ゲーテの作品には汎神論の影響が見られる。","romaji":"Gēte no sakuhin ni wa hanshinron no eikyō ga mirareru.","german":"The influence of pantheism can be seen in Goethe's works.","furigana":"さくひん|はんしんろん|えいきょう|み"}],"notes":"汎神論 \"pantheism\" is the philosophical doctrine that God and nature, or the universe, are one. 汎 means \"all-encompassing\" (cf. 汎用 \"general-purpose\"), 論 \"doctrine, theory\". It is associated above all with Spinoza (スピノザの汎神論); adjectivally 汎神論的. Do not confuse it with 多神教: 汎神論 is a worldview, not a religion with many gods.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"克己心を養うことがスポーツ教育の目的の一つだ。","romaji":"Kokkishin o yashinau koto ga supōtsu kyōiku no mokuteki no hitotsu da.","german":"Cultivating self-discipline is one of the aims of sports education.","furigana":"こっきしん|やしな|きょういく|もくてき|ひと"},{"japanese":"禅の修行では克己が何よりも重んじられる。","romaji":"Zen no shugyō de wa kokki ga nani yori mo omonjirareru.","german":"In Zen training, self-conquest is valued above all else.","furigana":"ぜん|しゅぎょう|こっき|なに|おも"},{"japanese":"誘惑に打ち勝つには克己の精神が必要だ。","romaji":"Yūwaku ni uchikatsu ni wa kokki no seishin ga hitsuyō da.","german":"To overcome temptation, a spirit of self-restraint is needed.","furigana":"ゆうわく|う|か|こっき|せいしん|ひつよう"}],"notes":"克己 \"self-conquest\" (literally \"to defeat oneself\") is a literary, somewhat old-fashioned moral word. It mostly appears in combinations such as 克己心 \"self-control\" or 克己の精神 and in the Confucian phrase 克己復礼. As a verb, 克己する is rare; in everyday speech one rather says 自分に勝つ or 我慢する. Note the reading こっき with small っ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自律的な市民が民主社会の基盤を支えている。","romaji":"Jiritsuteki na shimin ga minshu shakai no kiban o sasaete iru.","german":"Autonomous citizens support the foundation of a democratic society.","furigana":"じりつてき|しみん|みんしゅしゃかい|きばん|ささ"},{"japanese":"ストレスで自律神経が乱れることがある。","romaji":"Sutoresu de jiritsu shinkei ga midareru koto ga aru.","german":"Stress can disrupt the autonomic nervous system.","furigana":"じりつしんけい|みだ"},{"japanese":"子どもの自律を育てるには、自分で決めさせることが大切だ。","romaji":"Kodomo no jiritsu o sodateru ni wa, jibun de kimesaseru koto ga taisetsu da.","german":"To foster autonomy in children, it is important to let them decide for themselves.","furigana":"こ|じりつ|そだ|じぶん|き|たいせつ"}],"notes":"自律 means \"self-determination, autonomy\": governing oneself by one's own rules, without external compulsion. Beware of the identically read 自立 \"independence, standing on one's own feet\" (経済的に自立する): 自律 concerns self-control, 自立 independence from others. Common are 自律的, 自律神経 \"autonomic nervous system\" and, in technology, 自律型ロボット \"autonomous robot\". As a する-verb it is rare.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"啓蒙思想家たちは、民衆の蒙を啓くことを使命とした。","romaji":"Keimō shisōkatachi wa, minshū no mō o hiraku koto o shimei to shita.","german":"The Enlightenment thinkers regarded it as their mission to free the people from ignorance.","furigana":"けいもうしそうか|みんしゅう|もう|ひら|しめい"},{"japanese":"先生の講義は、私の蒙を啓いてくれた。","romaji":"Sensei no kōgi wa, watashi no mō o hiraite kureta.","german":"My professor's lecture opened my eyes.","furigana":"せんせい|こうぎ|わたし|もう|ひら"}],"notes":"啓く is a rare, elevated spelling of ひらく in the sense of \"to enlighten, open someone's eyes\". It is alive almost only in the fixed phrase 蒙を啓く \"to free someone from ignorance\", from which 啓蒙 \"Enlightenment\" also derives. For \"to open a new era\" or \"to open up land\" one writes 開く or 拓く (新しい時代を拓く), not 啓く. JMdict marks the word as obsolete; one should recognize it in texts but hardly needs it actively.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このスキャンダルは政権の基盤を揺るがした。","romaji":"Kono sukyandaru wa seiken no kiban o yurugashita.","german":"This scandal shook the foundations of the government.","furigana":"せいけん|きばん|ゆ"},{"japanese":"世界を揺るがす大発見が、小さな研究室から生まれた。","romaji":"Sekai o yurugasu daihakken ga, chiisana kenkyūshitsu kara umareta.","german":"A world-shaking discovery was born in a small laboratory.","furigana":"せかい|ゆ|だいはっけん|ちい|けんきゅうしつ|う"},{"japanese":"友人の裏切りは、私の人間への信頼を根底から揺るがした。","romaji":"Yūjin no uragiri wa, watashi no ningen e no shinrai o kontei kara yurugashita.","german":"My friend's betrayal shook my trust in people to the core.","furigana":"ゆうじん|うらぎ|わたし|にんげん|しんらい|こんてい|ゆ"}],"notes":"揺るがす \"to shake, throw into disarray\" is transitive (Xを揺るがす) and mostly used figuratively: 政権を揺るがす, 世界を揺るがす大事件, 信頼を揺るがす. For the literal shaking of an object one rather says 揺らす or 揺さぶる. The intransitive counterpart 揺るぐ occurs almost only in the negative: 揺るがない決意, 揺るぎない自信 \"unshakable\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"腐敗が社会の根幹を蝕んでいる。","romaji":"Fuhai ga shakai no konkan o mushibande iru.","german":"Corruption is eating away at the foundations of society.","furigana":"ふはい|しゃかい|こんかん|むしば"},{"japanese":"長年の過労が彼の体を蝕んでいった。","romaji":"Naganen no karō ga kare no karada o mushibande itta.","german":"Years of overwork gradually ruined his body.","furigana":"ながねん|かろう|かれ|からだ|むしば"},{"japanese":"いじめは子どもの心を深く蝕む。","romaji":"Ijime wa kodomo no kokoro o fukaku mushibamu.","german":"Bullying deeply gnaws at a child's heart.","furigana":"こ|こころ|ふか|むしば"}],"notes":"蝕む originally means \"to be eaten away by worms\" (cf. 虫歯), but today it is used almost only figuratively: illness, stress or corruption slowly \"eat away at, undermine\" something from within. Typical are 病が体を蝕む, 心を蝕む, 社会を蝕む, often in the progressive 蝕んでいる or the passive 蝕まれる. It sounds literary and is often written in kana むしばむ. In contrast to 損なう, it stresses the creeping, unnoticed process.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"歴史は我々に同じ過ちを繰り返さないよう戒めている。","romaji":"Rekishi wa wareware ni onaji ayamachi o kurikaesanai yō imashimete iru.","german":"History warns us not to repeat the same mistakes.","furigana":"れきし|われわれ|おな|あやま|く|かえ|いまし"},{"japanese":"一度の成功で慢心しないよう、自らを戒めている。","romaji":"Ichido no seikō de manshin shinai yō, mizukara o imashimete iru.","german":"I remind myself not to become complacent after a single success.","furigana":"いちど|せいこう|まんしん|みずか|いまし"},{"japanese":"父は嘘をついた息子を厳しく戒めた。","romaji":"Chichi wa uso o tsuita musuko o kibishiku imashimeta.","german":"The father sternly admonished his son, who had lied.","furigana":"ちち|うそ|むすこ|きび|いまし"}],"notes":"戒める means \"to admonish, warn (against something)\"; patterns: 人を戒める or 〜ないよう戒める. Very common is the reflexive use 自らを戒める \"to call oneself to order\". It sounds more serious and more written than 注意する; while 叱る is mere scolding, 戒める stresses the lesson for the future. The noun 戒め means \"admonition, lesson\", e.g. 〜を戒めとする.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"公の場では言動を慎むべきだ。","romaji":"Ōyake no ba de wa gendō o tsutsushimu beki da.","german":"In public places, one should be careful about one's words and actions.","furigana":"おおやけ|ば|げんどう|つつし"},{"japanese":"医者に言われて、しばらくお酒を慎むことにした。","romaji":"Isha ni iwarete, shibaraku o-sake o tsutsushimu koto ni shita.","german":"On my doctor's advice, I decided to refrain from alcohol for a while.","furigana":"いしゃ|い|さけ|つつし"},{"japanese":"試験中の私語は慎んでください。","romaji":"Shiken-chū no shigo wa tsutsushinde kudasai.","german":"Please refrain from private conversation during the exam.","furigana":"しけんちゅう|しご|つつし"}],"notes":"慎む means \"to restrain oneself, hold back, exercise caution\"; the object is what one reins in or refrains from: 言葉を慎む, 酒を慎む, 外出を慎む. In rules and announcements it is often 〜は慎んでください. Do not confuse it with 謹む (also つつしむ) \"to be respectful\", which occurs mainly in 謹んで \"respectfully\". The noun 慎み stands for \"restraint, decorum\" (慎みのある人).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"宗教は罪を悔い改めた者を赦す道を示す。","romaji":"Shūkyō wa tsumi o kuiaratameta mono o yurusu michi o shimesu.","german":"Religion shows a path of forgiveness for those who repent of their sins.","furigana":"しゅうきょう|つみ|く|あらた|もの|ゆる|みち|しめ"},{"japanese":"主よ、私たちの罪をお赦しください。","romaji":"Shu yo, watashitachi no tsumi o o-yurushi kudasai.","german":"Lord, please forgive us our sins.","furigana":"しゅ|わたし|つみ|ゆる"},{"japanese":"新国王の即位を祝して、多くの囚人が罪を赦された。","romaji":"Shin kokuō no sokui o shukushite, ōku no shūjin ga tsumi o yurusareta.","german":"To celebrate the accession of the new king, many prisoners were pardoned.","furigana":"しんこくおう|そくい|しゅく|おお|しゅうじん|つみ|ゆる"}],"notes":"赦す is a special spelling of ゆるす for forgiving guilt and sin or pardoning offenders (cf. 恩赦 \"amnesty\", 赦免 \"pardon\"). In everyday writing - also for \"to permit\" - one writes 許す; 赦す seems solemn, religious or literary. Typical are 罪を赦す, 人を赦す and the passive 赦される.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"桜を愛でる花見の文化は日本の春の風物詩だ。","romaji":"Sakura o mederu hanami no bunka wa Nihon no haru no fūbutsushi da.","german":"The hanami culture of admiring cherry blossoms is a hallmark of the Japanese spring.","furigana":"さくら|め|はなみ|ぶんか|にほん|はる|ふうぶつし"},{"japanese":"中秋の名月を愛でながら、月見団子を食べた。","romaji":"Chūshū no meigetsu o medenagara, tsukimi dango o tabeta.","german":"We ate moon-viewing dumplings while admiring the harvest moon.","furigana":"ちゅうしゅう|めいげつ|め|つきみだんご|た"},{"japanese":"祖母は庭の草花を愛でるのが何よりの楽しみだ。","romaji":"Sobo wa niwa no kusabana o mederu no ga nani yori no tanoshimi da.","german":"For my grandmother, nothing is a greater pleasure than enjoying the flowers in the garden.","furigana":"そぼ|にわ|くさばな|め|なに|たの"}],"notes":"愛でる is an elevated, poetic word for \"to take pleasure in something beautiful, to gaze on lovingly\". Typical objects are nature and the seasons: 花を愛でる, 月を愛でる, 紅葉を愛でる; more rarely also children or animals in the sense of \"to caress\". In everyday speech one says 楽しむ, 眺める or かわいがる instead.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"平安貴族は和歌の贈答に興じた。","romaji":"Heian kizoku wa waka no zōtō ni kyōjita.","german":"The Heian aristocrats amused themselves exchanging waka poems.","furigana":"へいあんきぞく|わか|ぞうとう|きょう"},{"japanese":"休み時間、生徒たちはおしゃべりに興じていた。","romaji":"Yasumijikan, seitotachi wa oshaberi ni kyōjite ita.","german":"During the break, the students were absorbed in chatting.","furigana":"やす|じかん|せいと|きょう"},{"japanese":"宴会の後、同僚たちは夜遅くまでカラオケに興じた。","romaji":"Enkai no ato, dōryōtachi wa yoru osoku made karaoke ni kyōjita.","german":"After the banquet, my colleagues amused themselves with karaoke until late at night.","furigana":"えんかい|あと|どうりょう|よるおそ|きょう"}],"notes":"興じる \"to amuse oneself, be wholly absorbed in a pastime\" is intransitive and takes に: 遊びに興じる, 雑談に興じる, トランプに興じる. It sounds literary and mostly describes from the outside how people give themselves over to an entertainment. The variant 興ずる is synonymous and even more elevated. In everyday speech one says 楽しむ (transitive with を) or 夢中になる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"司法権を司る裁判所の独立性は保障されなければならない。","romaji":"Shihōken o tsukasadoru saibansho no dokuritsusei wa hoshō sarenakereba naranai.","german":"The independence of the courts, which exercise the judicial power, must be guaranteed.","furigana":"しほうけん|つかさど|さいばんしょ|どくりつせい|ほしょう"},{"japanese":"海馬は記憶を司る脳の部位だ。","romaji":"Kaiba wa kioku o tsukasadoru nō no bui da.","german":"The hippocampus is the part of the brain that governs memory.","furigana":"かいば|きおく|つかさど|のう|ぶい"},{"japanese":"ギリシャ神話では、ポセイドンが海を司る神とされる。","romaji":"Girisha shinwa de wa, Poseidon ga umi o tsukasadoru kami to sareru.","german":"In Greek mythology, Poseidon is regarded as the god who rules the sea.","furigana":"しんわ|うみ|つかさど|かみ"}],"notes":"司る means \"to be in charge of something, to direct or govern something\", often with institutions, organs or gods as the subject: 司法を司る, 記憶を司る脳の部位, 海を司る神. It is transitive (〜を司る) and has no intransitive partner. The word is literary; in everyday speech one says 担当する or 管理する. It is often written in kana つかさどる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不祥事が組織の信頼を大きく損なった。","romaji":"Fushōji ga soshiki no shinrai o ōkiku sokonatta.","german":"The scandal greatly damaged trust in the organization.","furigana":"ふしょうじ|そしき|しんらい|おお|そこ"},{"japanese":"無理なダイエットは健康を損なうおそれがある。","romaji":"Muri na daietto wa kenkō o sokonau osore ga aru.","german":"An extreme diet may harm your health.","furigana":"むり|けんこう|そこ"},{"japanese":"寝坊して、いつものバスに乗り損なった。","romaji":"Nebō shite, itsumo no basu ni norisokonatta.","german":"I overslept and missed my usual bus.","furigana":"ねぼう|の|そこ"}],"notes":"損なう \"to damage, impair\" is transitive and mostly takes abstract or health-related objects: 健康を損なう, 信頼を損なう, 機嫌を損なう, 美観を損なう. The ichidan variant 損ねる is synonymous. As a suffix on the ます-stem, 〜損なう means \"to miss, fail to do\": 見損なう, 言い損なう, 乗り損なう. Unlike 壊す (to destroy physically), 損なう describes rather a loss of value.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"国際的な協力体制の醸成が急務となっている。","romaji":"Kokusaiteki na kyōryoku taisei no jōsei ga kyūmu to natte iru.","german":"Building a framework for international cooperation has become an urgent task.","furigana":"こくさいてき|きょうりょくたいせい|じょうせい|きゅうむ"},{"japanese":"社員同士の信頼関係を醸成するため、定期的に交流会を開いている。","romaji":"Shain dōshi no shinrai kankei o jōsei suru tame, teikiteki ni kōryūkai o hiraite iru.","german":"To foster trust among employees, we hold regular social gatherings.","furigana":"しゃいんどうし|しんらいかんけい|じょうせい|ていきてき|こうりゅうかい|ひら"},{"japanese":"地域全体で、子育てを支える機運を醸成していきたい。","romaji":"Chiiki zentai de, kosodate o sasaeru kiun o jōsei shite ikitai.","german":"We want to build momentum across the whole region to support child-rearing.","furigana":"ちいきぜんたい|こそだ|ささ|きうん|じょうせい"}],"notes":"醸成 literally means \"brewing, fermenting\" (醸 as in 醸造), but is used almost only figuratively: to let a mood or relationship gradually \"come into being, mature\". Typical objects are 雰囲気を醸成する, 信頼関係を醸成する, 機運を醸成する \"to create a favorable climate\". It is a word of politics, business and newspapers. Related is the more common 醸し出す \"to give off (an atmosphere)\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"経済危機に陥った国への国際的な支援が求められている。","romaji":"Keizai kiki ni ochiitta kuni e no kokusaiteki na shien ga motomerarete iru.","german":"International aid is being called for to help countries that have fallen into an economic crisis.","furigana":"けいざいきき|おちい|くに|こくさいてき|しえん|もと"},{"japanese":"突然の停電で、会場はパニックに陥った。","romaji":"Totsuzen no teiden de, kaijō wa panikku ni ochiitta.","german":"The sudden power outage threw the hall into panic.","furigana":"とつぜん|ていでん|かいじょう|おちい"},{"japanese":"試験に落ちて、彼はひどい自己嫌悪に陥った。","romaji":"Shiken ni ochite, kare wa hidoi jiko ken'o ni ochiitta.","german":"After failing the exam, he fell into a deep state of self-loathing.","furigana":"しけん|お|かれ|じこけんお|おちい"}],"notes":"陥る means \"to fall into a bad state\" and takes に: 危機に陥る, パニックに陥る, 自己嫌悪に陥る, ジレンマに陥る, 罠に陥る. It is intransitive; its transitive partner 陥れる means \"to plunge someone into, to entrap\" (人を罠に陥れる). Compared with the everyday 落ちる or 〜になる, 陥る sounds literary and stresses that one gets into the state unintentionally.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は生涯を社会福祉に尽くした。","romaji":"Kanojo wa shōgai o shakai fukushi ni tsukushita.","german":"She devoted her whole life to social welfare.","furigana":"かのじょ|しょうがい|しゃかいふくし|つ"},{"japanese":"医師たちは最善を尽くしたが、患者は助からなかった。","romaji":"Ishitachi wa saizen o tsukushita ga, kanja wa tasukaranakatta.","german":"The doctors did their best, but the patient could not be saved.","furigana":"いし|さいぜん|つ|かんじゃ|たす"},{"japanese":"彼はこの町の路地を知り尽くしている。","romaji":"Kare wa kono machi no roji o shiritsukushite iru.","german":"He knows every alley of this town inside out.","furigana":"かれ|まち|ろじ|し|つ"}],"notes":"尽くす has two main uses: transitive \"to exhaust, to bring to bear fully\" (全力を尽くす, ベストを尽くす, 手を尽くす \"to try every means\") and, with に, \"to devote oneself (to someone or something), to serve\" (社会に尽くす, 家族に尽くす). As a suffix on the ます-stem, 〜尽くす means \"to do completely, thoroughly\": 食べ尽くす, 知り尽くす. The intransitive counterpart is 尽きる \"to run out\" (力が尽きる).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"金箔が秋の陽光に映えて荘厳な美しさを放つ。","romaji":"Kinpaku ga aki no yōkō ni haete sōgon na utsukushisa o hanatsu.","german":"The gold leaf, set off by the autumn sunlight, radiates solemn beauty.","furigana":"きんぱく|あき|ようこう|は|そうごん|うつく|はな"},{"japanese":"赤い鳥居が雪景色によく映える。","romaji":"Akai torii ga yukigeshiki ni yoku haeru.","german":"The red torii stands out beautifully against the snowy landscape.","furigana":"あか|とりい||は"},{"japanese":"このネクタイは紺のスーツに映えますね。","romaji":"Kono nekutai wa kon no sūtsu ni haemasu ne.","german":"This tie looks great with a navy suit, doesn't it?","furigana":"こん|は"}],"notes":"映える \"to shine, to look good against a background\" is intransitive; the background or light takes に: 夕日に映える, 青空に映える, 雪に映える. You can also simply say 〜がよく映える \"looks great\". As a suffix it becomes 〜映え (ばえ): 見映え \"appearance\", インスタ映え \"Instagram-worthy\". Do not confuse it with the identically read 生える \"to grow (hair, grass)\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"修復を経て古い壁画が鮮やかに甦った。","romaji":"Shūfuku o hete furui hekiga ga azayaka ni yomigaetta.","german":"After restoration, the old mural was revived in vivid colors.","furigana":"しゅうふく|へ|ふる|へきが|あざ|よみがえ"},{"japanese":"古い写真を見て、子どもの頃の記憶が甦った。","romaji":"Furui shashin o mite, kodomo no koro no kioku ga yomigaetta.","german":"Looking at old photographs, memories of my childhood came back.","furigana":"ふる|しゃしん|み|こ|ころ|きおく|よみがえ"},{"japanese":"震災から十年、町は見事に甦った。","romaji":"Shinsai kara jūnen, machi wa migoto ni yomigaetta.","german":"Ten years after the earthquake, the town has made a remarkable recovery.","furigana":"しんさい|じゅうねん|まち|みごと|よみがえ"}],"notes":"甦る is an alternative spelling of よみがえる; today 蘇る is more common, and the kana spelling is also usual. It is intransitive: something \"comes back to life, is revived\" (死者が甦る, 街が甦る), and very often it is about memories: 記憶が甦る \"the memory comes back\". \"To revive\" as an action is expressed with the causative 甦らせる; 復活する is the more matter-of-fact Sino-Japanese word.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"崇高な芸術は人間の精神を高みへと導く。","romaji":"Sūkō na geijutsu wa ningen no seishin o takami e to michibiku.","german":"Sublime art leads the human spirit to greater heights.","furigana":"すうこう|げいじゅつ|にんげん|せいしん|たか|みちび"},{"japanese":"医療に携わる者として、命を救うという崇高な使命を忘れてはならない。","romaji":"Iryō ni tazusawaru mono toshite, inochi o sukuu to iu sūkō na shimei o wasurete wa naranai.","german":"As someone working in medicine, you must never forget the noble mission of saving lives.","furigana":"いりょう|たずさ|もの|いのち|すく|すうこう|しめい|わす"},{"japanese":"夜明けの富士山の姿は、崇高としか言いようがない。","romaji":"Yoake no Fujisan no sugata wa, sūkō to shika iiyō ga nai.","german":"The sight of Mt. Fuji at dawn can only be called sublime.","furigana":"よあ|ふじさん|すがた|すうこう|い"}],"notes":"崇高 \"sublime, noble\" is a literary な-adjective for ideals and feelings that rise above the everyday: 崇高な理想, 崇高な使命, 崇高な精神. In aesthetics, 崇高 is also the technical term for \"the sublime\". It does not fit colloquial \"great, awesome\"; for that, use 素晴らしい or 立派. Do not confuse it with 高尚 \"refined, cultured (taste)\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"厳粛な面持ちで祈りを捧げた。","romaji":"Genshuku na omomochi de inori o sasageta.","german":"He offered a prayer with a solemn expression.","furigana":"げんしゅく|おもも|いの|ささ"},{"japanese":"卒業式は厳粛な雰囲気の中で行われた。","romaji":"Sotsugyōshiki wa genshuku na fun'iki no naka de okonawareta.","german":"The graduation ceremony was held in a solemn atmosphere.","furigana":"そつぎょうしき|げんしゅく|ふんいき|なか|おこな"},{"japanese":"政府は選挙の結果を厳粛に受け止めると述べた。","romaji":"Seifu wa senkyo no kekka o genshuku ni uketomeru to nobeta.","german":"The government stated that it takes the election result very seriously.","furigana":"せいふ|せんきょ|けっか|げんしゅく|う|と|の"}],"notes":"厳粛 is a な-adjective meaning \"solemnly serious, dignified\": 厳粛な雰囲気, 厳粛な儀式, 式は厳粛に執り行われた. It can also mean \"undeniable, to be taken seriously\": 厳粛な事実, 結果を厳粛に受け止める. Unlike 真剣 \"earnest (in an activity)\", 厳粛 describes a dignified mood or attitude; 厳格, by contrast, means \"strict (about rules)\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"敬虔な信者たちが毎朝礼拝に訪れる。","romaji":"Keiken na shinjatachi ga maiasa raihai ni otozureru.","german":"Devout believers come to worship every morning.","furigana":"けいけん|しんじゃ|まいあさらいはい|おとず"},{"japanese":"祖母は敬虔な仏教徒で、朝晩欠かさず仏壇に手を合わせている。","romaji":"Sobo wa keiken na bukkyōto de, asaban kakasazu butsudan ni te o awasete iru.","german":"My grandmother is a devout Buddhist and never fails to put her hands together before the family altar morning and evening.","furigana":"そぼ|けいけん|ぶっきょうと|あさばんか|ぶつだん|て|あ"},{"japanese":"大聖堂に入ると、自然と敬虔な気持ちになった。","romaji":"Daiseidō ni hairu to, shizen to keiken na kimochi ni natta.","german":"When I entered the cathedral, I naturally felt reverent.","furigana":"だいせいどう|はい|しぜん|けいけん|きも"}],"notes":"敬虔 \"devout, reverent\" is a な-adjective that almost always stands before nouns for people or attitudes: 敬虔な信者, 敬虔なクリスチャン, 敬虔な祈り, 敬虔な気持ち. It is literary; in conversation one says 信心深い. Note: 敬虔 (けいけん) sounds exactly like 経験 \"experience\"; only context tells them apart.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"古代の人々は太陽を神として崇めていた。","romaji":"Kodai no hitobito wa taiyō o kami toshite agamete ita.","german":"People in ancient times revered the sun as a god.","furigana":"こだい|ひとびと|たいよう|かみ|あが"},{"japanese":"彼はその画家を生涯の師と崇めていた。","romaji":"Kare wa sono gaka o shōgai no shi to agamete ita.","german":"He revered that painter as his lifelong teacher.","furigana":"かれ|がか|しょうがい|し|あが"},{"japanese":"村人たちは山の上の大木を御神木として崇めている。","romaji":"Murabitotachi wa yama no ue no taiboku o goshinboku toshite agamete iru.","german":"The villagers revere the large tree on the mountain as a sacred tree.","furigana":"むらびと|やま|うえ|たいぼく|ごしんぼく|あが"}],"notes":"崇める \"to revere, to worship\" is transitive and often takes として or と: 神として崇める, 師と崇める \"to revere as a master\". It is used for gods, but also for people one holds in exceptionally high regard; the passive 崇められる is common. The Sino-Japanese 崇拝する is related. Compared with 尊敬する \"to respect\", 崇める sounds almost religiously exalted.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"すべての生きものを慈しむことが仏教の教えだ。","romaji":"Subete no ikimono o itsukushimu koto ga Bukkyō no oshie da.","german":"Cherishing all living things is a teaching of Buddhism.","furigana":"い|いつく|ぶっきょう|おし"},{"japanese":"母親は生まれたばかりの赤ん坊を慈しむように抱いた。","romaji":"Hahaoya wa umareta bakari no akanbō o itsukushimu yō ni daita.","german":"The mother held her newborn baby tenderly.","furigana":"ははおや|う|あか|ぼう|いつく|だ"},{"japanese":"祖父は庭の木々を我が子のように慈しんで育てた。","romaji":"Sofu wa niwa no kigi o wagako no yō ni itsukushinde sodateta.","german":"My grandfather raised the trees in the garden lovingly, as if they were his own children.","furigana":"そふ|にわ|きぎ|わ|こ|いつく|そだ"}],"notes":"慈しむ \"to care for lovingly, to love tenderly\" is transitive and elevated; it describes a gentle, caring love, usually toward the small or weak: 子を慈しむ, 生き物を慈しむ, 自然を慈しむ. The noun 慈しみ \"kindness, compassion\" is common, e.g. in 慈しみの心. In everyday speech one says かわいがる or 大切にする; 慈悲 is the Buddhist term for \"mercy\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自然の力を畏れる心が信仰の原点だ。","romaji":"Shizen no chikara o osoreru kokoro ga shinkō no genten da.","german":"A heart that reveres the power of nature is the origin of faith.","furigana":"しぜん|ちから|おそ|こころ|しんこう|げんてん"},{"japanese":"昔の人々は雷を神の怒りとして畏れた。","romaji":"Mukashi no hitobito wa kaminari o kami no ikari toshite osoreta.","german":"People in the past feared thunder as the wrath of the gods.","furigana":"むかし|ひとびと|かみなり|かみ|いか|おそ"},{"japanese":"神をも畏れぬ振る舞いに、村の長老たちは眉をひそめた。","romaji":"Kami o mo osorenu furumai ni, mura no chōrōtachi wa mayu o hisometa.","german":"The village elders frowned at the behavior that showed no fear even of the gods.","furigana":"かみ|おそ|ふ|ま|むら|ちょうろう|まゆ"}],"notes":"畏れる is the spelling of おそれる for reverent awe before something overpowering or sacred: 神を畏れる, 自然を畏れる. For ordinary fear, one writes 恐れる (失敗を恐れる). Related are 畏れ多い \"too great an honor\" and 畏敬 \"reverence\" (畏敬の念). It is transitive with を and sounds elevated.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"罪を贖うために苦行に身を捧げた。","romaji":"Tsumi o aganau tame ni kugyō ni mi o sasageta.","german":"He devoted himself to ascetic practice to atone for his sins.","furigana":"つみ|あがな|くぎょう|み|ささ"},{"japanese":"彼は残りの人生をかけて、若い頃の過ちを贖おうとした。","romaji":"Kare wa nokori no jinsei o kakete, wakai koro no ayamachi o aganaō to shita.","german":"He tried to spend the rest of his life atoning for the mistakes of his youth.","furigana":"かれ|のこ|じんせい|わか|ころ|あやま|あがな"},{"japanese":"キリスト教では、イエスが人類の罪を贖ったと教えられる。","romaji":"Kirisutokyō de wa, Iesu ga jinrui no tsumi o aganatta to oshierareru.","german":"In Christianity, it is taught that Jesus atoned for the sins of humankind.","furigana":"きょう|じんるい|つみ|あがな|おし"}],"notes":"贖う \"to atone for, to make amends for\" is transitive: 罪を贖う, 過ちを贖う. The means of atonement takes で or をもって: 命をもって罪を贖う. The word is elevated and often has a religious ring; the related 贖罪 means \"atonement, (Christian) redemption\". In everyday speech 償う is more usual; in older texts あがなう also means \"to buy\" and is then written 購う.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"虐げられた人々の声に耳を傾けるべきだ。","romaji":"Shiitagerareta hitobito no koe ni mimi o katamukeru beki da.","german":"We should listen to the voices of the oppressed.","furigana":"しいた|ひとびと|こえ|みみ|かたむ"},{"japanese":"その国では長い間、少数民族が虐げられてきた。","romaji":"Sono kuni de wa nagai aida, shōsū minzoku ga shiitagerarete kita.","german":"In that country, ethnic minorities have been oppressed for a long time.","furigana":"くに|なが|あいだ|しょうすうみんぞく|しいた"},{"japanese":"物語の主人公は、継母に虐げられながらも優しさを失わなかった。","romaji":"Monogatari no shujinkō wa, mamahaha ni shiitagerarenagara mo yasashisa o ushinawanakatta.","german":"Although she was mistreated by her stepmother, the heroine of the story never lost her kindness.","furigana":"ものがたり|しゅじんこう|ままはは|しいた|やさ|うしな"}],"notes":"虐げる \"to oppress, to treat cruelly\" is transitive and is especially often used in the passive: 虐げられた人々 \"the oppressed\", 虐げられた民族. It describes lasting oppression of the weaker by the more powerful. For everyday bullying one says いじめる, for abuse in the legal sense 虐待する; 虐げる is literary and emotionally colored.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"基本的人権を蔑ろにすることは許されない。","romaji":"Kihonteki jinken o naigashiro ni suru koto wa yurusarenai.","german":"Disregarding basic human rights is unacceptable.","furigana":"きほんてきじんけん|ないがし|ゆる"},{"japanese":"仕事ばかりで家族を蔑ろにしてはいけない。","romaji":"Shigoto bakari de kazoku o naigashiro ni shite wa ikenai.","german":"You must not neglect your family because of work alone.","furigana":"しごと|かぞく|ないがし"},{"japanese":"利益を優先して安全を蔑ろにした結果、事故が起きた。","romaji":"Rieki o yūsen shite anzen o naigashiro ni shita kekka, jiko ga okita.","german":"An accident occurred as a result of putting profit first and neglecting safety.","furigana":"りえき|ゆうせん|あんぜん|ないがし|けっか|じこ|お"}],"notes":"蔑ろ \"disrespect, disregard\" occurs practically only in the phrase 〜を蔑ろにする \"to disregard, to neglect, to trample on something\": 規則を蔑ろにする, 家族を蔑ろにする, 安全を蔑ろにする. It is mostly written in kana, ないがしろ, because the kanji 蔑 (cf. 軽蔑 \"contempt\") is difficult. Unlike 無視する, it stresses treating something that is actually important as unimportant.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自己の利益ばかりで他人を顧みない社会は健全ではない。","romaji":"Jiko no rieki bakari de tanin o kaeriminai shakai wa kenzen de wa nai.","german":"A society that cares only about its own interests and pays no heed to others is not healthy.","furigana":"じこ|りえき|たにん|かえり|しゃかい|けんぜん"},{"japanese":"彼は危険を顧みず、川に飛び込んで子どもを助けた。","romaji":"Kare wa kiken o kaerimizu, kawa ni tobikonde kodomo o tasuketa.","german":"Disregarding the danger, he jumped into the river and saved the child.","furigana":"かれ|きけん|かえり|かわ|と|こ|こ|たす"},{"japanese":"この十年を顧みると、多くの人に支えられてきたと感じる。","romaji":"Kono jūnen o kaerimiru to, ōku no hito ni sasaerarete kita to kanjiru.","german":"Looking back on these ten years, I feel I have been supported by many people.","furigana":"じゅうねん|かえり|おお|ひと|ささ|かん"}],"notes":"顧みる has two main meanings: \"to look back (on the past)\" (過去を顧みる) and \"to heed, to take into consideration\", the latter mostly negated: 家庭を顧みない \"to neglect one's family\", 危険を顧みず \"regardless of the danger\". Do not confuse it with 省みる \"to reflect on oneself, to self-examine\" (自らを省みる). The word is literary; in conversation one says 振り返る or 気にする respectively.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"沈黙は同意と見做される場合がある。","romaji":"Chinmoku wa dōi to minasareru baai ga aru.","german":"Silence may be regarded as consent in some cases.","furigana":"ちんもく|どうい|みな|ばあい"},{"japanese":"期限までに提出しない場合は、棄権したものと見做します。","romaji":"Kigen made ni teishutsu shinai baai wa, kiken shita mono to minashimasu.","german":"If you do not submit by the deadline, you will be considered to have withdrawn.","furigana":"きげん|ていしゅつ|ばあい|きけん|みな"},{"japanese":"彼はこの分野の第一人者と見做されている。","romaji":"Kare wa kono bun'ya no daiichininsha to minasarete iru.","german":"He is regarded as the leading authority in this field.","furigana":"かれ|ぶんや|だいいちにんしゃ|みな"}],"notes":"見做す \"to regard as, to consider as, (legally) to deem\" appears in the pattern XをYと見做す, very often in the passive: Yと見做される. It is typical of rules, contracts and legal texts, e.g. 期限内に連絡がない場合は辞退と見做す. It is mostly written in kana, みなす. Unlike 考える or 思う, it expresses a (often formal) classification, not a personal opinion.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"源氏物語の文体は典雅で美しい。","romaji":"Genji Monogatari no buntai wa tenga de utsukushii.","german":"The style of The Tale of Genji is elegant and beautiful.","furigana":"げんじものがたり|ぶんたい|てんが|うつく"},{"japanese":"雅楽の典雅な調べが神社の境内に流れていた。","romaji":"Gagaku no tenga na shirabe ga jinja no keidai ni nagarete ita.","german":"The refined melodies of gagaku drifted through the shrine grounds.","furigana":"ががく|てんが|しら|じんじゃ|けいだい|なが"},{"japanese":"宮廷の舞は、ゆるやかで典雅な動きが特徴だ。","romaji":"Kyūtei no mai wa, yuruyaka de tenga na ugoki ga tokuchō da.","german":"The court dance is characterized by slow, elegant movements.","furigana":"きゅうてい|まい|てんが|うご|とくちょう"}],"notes":"典雅 \"classically elegant, refined and dignified\" is a rare, literary な-adjective. 典 points to the classical and exemplary, 雅 to courtly elegance (cf. 雅やか, 優雅). It describes mainly style, music and court art: 典雅な文体, 典雅な舞, 典雅な調べ. In everyday use 優雅 is far more common.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"芳醇な味わいの日本酒が全国各地で醸されている。","romaji":"Hōjun na ajiwai no nihonshu ga zenkoku kakuchi de kamosarete iru.","german":"Sake with a mellow, aromatic flavor is brewed all over the country.","furigana":"ほうじゅん|あじ|にほんしゅ|ぜんこくかくち|かも"},{"japanese":"グラスを回すと、ワインの芳醇な香りが広がった。","romaji":"Gurasu o mawasu to, wain no hōjun na kaori ga hirogatta.","german":"When I swirled the glass, the wine's rich aroma spread.","furigana":"まわ|ほうじゅん|かお|ひろ"},{"japanese":"深煎りの豆を使ったこのコーヒーは、芳醇なコクが楽しめる。","romaji":"Fukairi no mame o tsukatta kono kōhī wa, hōjun na koku ga tanoshimeru.","german":"This coffee, made from dark-roasted beans, offers a rich, full-bodied taste.","furigana":"ふかい|まめ|つか|ほうじゅん|たの"}],"notes":"芳醇 describes a full, mature aroma or flavor, especially in alcoholic drinks: 芳醇な香り, 芳醇な味わいの日本酒 or ワイン. Coffee, cheese or fruit can also be 芳醇. It is a word of gourmet and advertising language; in conversation one says 香りがいい or コクがある instead. The identically read 豊潤 \"rich, abundant\" is sometimes used synonymously, but it relates less to aroma.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"精緻を極めた蒔絵の技法に目を見張る。","romaji":"Seichi o kiwameta makie no gihō ni me o miharu.","german":"One marvels at the maki-e technique, refined to the utmost.","furigana":"せいち|きわ|まきえ|ぎほう|め|みは"},{"japanese":"この論文は、データの精緻な分析に基づいている。","romaji":"Kono ronbun wa, dēta no seichi na bunseki ni motozuite iru.","german":"This paper is based on a meticulous analysis of the data.","furigana":"ろんぶん|せいち|ぶんせき|もと"},{"japanese":"機械式時計の内部には、精緻な部品がぎっしり詰まっている。","romaji":"Kikaishiki tokei no naibu ni wa, seichi na buhin ga gisshiri tsumatte iru.","german":"The inside of a mechanical watch is densely packed with exquisite parts.","furigana":"きかいしきとけい|ないぶ|せいち|ぶひん|つ"}],"notes":"精緻 \"extremely fine, precise, worked out down to the detail\" is a な-adjective and noun of the written language. It describes craftsmanship (精緻な細工), but equally intellectual constructs: 精緻な分析, 精緻な理論. The phrase 精緻を極める \"to be of the highest delicacy\" is common. Compared with 細かい or 精密, 精緻 stresses artful, well-considered fineness.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"壮麗な城郭建築は当時の権力を象徴している。","romaji":"Sōrei na jōkaku kenchiku wa tōji no kenryoku o shōchō shite iru.","german":"The magnificent castle architecture symbolizes the power of its time.","furigana":"そうれい|じょうかくけんちく|とうじ|けんりょく|しょうちょう"},{"japanese":"ベルサイユ宮殿の壮麗な鏡の間に、観光客たちは息をのんだ。","romaji":"Berusaiyu kyūden no sōrei na kagami no ma ni, kankōkyakutachi wa iki o nonda.","german":"Tourists were breathless at the magnificent Hall of Mirrors at Versailles.","furigana":"きゅうでん|そうれい|かがみ|ま|かんこうきゃく|いき"},{"japanese":"山頂から見た日の出は、言葉にできないほど壮麗だった。","romaji":"Sanchō kara mita hinode wa, kotoba ni dekinai hodo sōrei datta.","german":"The sunrise seen from the summit was magnificent beyond words.","furigana":"さんちょう|み|ひ|で|ことば|そうれい"}],"notes":"壮麗 \"magnificent, grand and majestic\" is a な-adjective for large, impressive buildings and scenes: 壮麗な宮殿, 壮麗な大聖堂, 壮麗な景色. 壮 stands for greatness and strength, 麗 for beauty. Unlike 豪華 (luxurious, expensively furnished), 壮麗 stresses grandeur and dignity; 壮大 relates more to vast scale or plans.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"茶室の簡素な佇まいに日本の美が凝縮されている。","romaji":"Chashitsu no kanso na tatazumai ni Nihon no bi ga gyōshuku sarete iru.","german":"The essence of Japanese beauty is condensed in the simple appearance of a tea room.","furigana":"ちゃしつ|かんそ|たたず|にほん|び|ぎょうしゅく"},{"japanese":"結婚式は家族だけで簡素に行った。","romaji":"Kekkonshiki wa kazoku dake de kanso ni okonatta.","german":"We held the wedding simply, with family only.","furigana":"けっこんしき|かぞく|かんそ|おこな"},{"japanese":"市は行政手続きの簡素化を進めている。","romaji":"Shi wa gyōsei tetsuzuki no kansoka o susumete iru.","german":"The city is pushing ahead with the simplification of administrative procedures.","furigana":"し|ぎょうせいてつづ|かんそか|すす"}],"notes":"簡素 \"plain, simple, without anything superfluous\" is a な-adjective with a positive ring: 簡素な暮らし, 簡素な式, 簡素なデザイン. 簡素化 \"simplification\" is very common, especially for administrative procedures (手続きの簡素化). Unlike 質素 (\"frugal, thrifty\" in lifestyle), 簡素 stresses reduction to the essentials; 簡単, by contrast, means \"easy, uncomplicated\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本舞踊の優美な動きに心を奪われた。","romaji":"Nihon buyō no yūbi na ugoki ni kokoro o ubawareta.","german":"I was captivated by the graceful movements of Japanese dance.","furigana":"にほんぶよう|ゆうび|うご|こころ|うば"},{"japanese":"この花瓶は、首の部分の優美な曲線が美しい。","romaji":"Kono kabin wa, kubi no bubun no yūbi na kyokusen ga utsukushii.","german":"The graceful curve of the neck makes this vase beautiful.","furigana":"かびん|くび|ぶぶん|ゆうび|きょくせん|うつく"},{"japanese":"白鳥が湖面を優美に泳いでいく。","romaji":"Hakuchō ga komen o yūbi ni oyoide iku.","german":"A swan glides gracefully across the surface of the lake.","furigana":"はくちょう|こめん|ゆうび|およ"}],"notes":"優美 \"graceful, elegant\" is a な-adjective for the delicate, soft beauty of movements, shapes and lines: 優美な動き, 優美な曲線, 優美な姿. It is literary; the similar 優雅 relates more to a cultivated, relaxed way of life (優雅な暮らし). For people, everyday speech prefers 上品 or きれい.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"桃山文化は豪華絢爛な装飾で知られている。","romaji":"Momoyama bunka wa gōka kenran na sōshoku de shirarete iru.","german":"Momoyama culture is known for its gorgeous, resplendent decoration.","furigana":"ももやまぶんか|ごうかけんらん|そうしょく|し"},{"japanese":"記念日に、ホテルで豪華なディナーを楽しんだ。","romaji":"Kinenbi ni, hoteru de gōka na dinā o tanoshinda.","german":"On our anniversary, we enjoyed a luxurious dinner at a hotel.","furigana":"きねんび|ごうか|たの"},{"japanese":"今回の映画は豪華キャストで話題になっている。","romaji":"Konkai no eiga wa gōka kyasuto de wadai ni natte iru.","german":"This film is the talk of the town for its star-studded cast.","furigana":"こんかい|えいが|ごうか|わだい"}],"notes":"豪華 \"luxurious, gorgeous, lavish\" is a な-adjective for anything that shows a lot of money and effort: 豪華な料理, 豪華なホテル, 豪華客船. It often stands directly before nouns: 豪華賞品, 豪華キャスト \"all-star cast\". The intensified form 豪華絢爛 means \"magnificent and dazzling\". Unlike 贅沢, 豪華 usually sounds admiring, not disapproving.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"閑寂な山寺に身を置くと俗世を忘れる。","romaji":"Kanjaku na yamadera ni mi o oku to zokuse o wasureru.","german":"Staying in a tranquil mountain temple, one forgets the secular world.","furigana":"かんじゃく|やまでら|み|お|ぞくせ|わす"},{"japanese":"芭蕉の句には、閑寂な境地が詠まれている。","romaji":"Bashō no ku ni wa, kanjaku na kyōchi ga yomarete iru.","german":"Bashō's haiku express a state of tranquil solitude.","furigana":"|く|かんじゃく|きょうち|よ"},{"japanese":"苔むした閑寂な庭を、私は一人で眺めていた。","romaji":"Kokemushita kanjaku na niwa o, watashi wa hitori de nagamete ita.","german":"I gazed alone at the quiet, moss-covered garden.","furigana":"こけ|かんじゃく|にわ|わたし|ひとり|なが"}],"notes":"閑寂 \"quiet, secluded and peaceful\" is a rare, literary な-adjective for a calm, slightly lonely mood far from the bustle of the world: 閑寂な山寺, 閑寂な庭. In aesthetics it is often used to explain わび・さび. In ordinary usage one says 静か or 閑静 (閑静な住宅街 \"quiet residential area\"); 静寂 is the more common word for \"silence\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"荘重な鐘の音が境内に響き渡った。","romaji":"Sōchō na kane no oto ga keidai ni hibikiwatatta.","german":"The solemn sound of the bell echoed across the temple grounds.","furigana":"そうちょう|かね|おと|けいだい|ひび|わた"},{"japanese":"式典はオーケストラの荘重な演奏で幕を開けた。","romaji":"Shikiten wa ōkesutora no sōchō na ensō de maku o aketa.","german":"The ceremony opened with a stately performance by the orchestra.","furigana":"しきてん|そうちょう|えんそう|まく|あ"},{"japanese":"校長は荘重な口調で式辞を読み上げた。","romaji":"Kōchō wa sōchō na kuchō de shikiji o yomiageta.","german":"The principal read the address in a solemn tone.","furigana":"こうちょう|そうちょう|くちょう|しきじ|よ|あ"}],"notes":"荘重 \"solemn, dignified and weighty\" is a な-adjective for sounds, ceremonies and speech: 荘重な音楽, 荘重な儀式, 荘重な口調. It resembles 厳粛 (a serious, solemn mood) and 荘厳 (そうごん, sublime and splendid, often for temples and churches); 荘重 stresses the weighty, measured quality. A literary word.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"清楚な白い花が茶席に飾られていた。","romaji":"Seiso na shiroi hana ga chaseki ni kazararete ita.","german":"A simple, pure white flower was displayed in the tea room.","furigana":"せいそ|しろ|はな|ちゃせき|かざ"},{"japanese":"面接には清楚な服装で行くのが無難だ。","romaji":"Mensetsu ni wa seiso na fukusō de iku no ga bunan da.","german":"For an interview, it is safest to go in neat, simple clothing.","furigana":"めんせつ|せいそ|ふくそう|い|ぶなん"},{"japanese":"彼女は清楚な雰囲気で、誰からも好かれている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa seiso na fun'iki de, dare kara mo sukarete iru.","german":"She has a neat, unaffected air and is liked by everyone.","furigana":"かのじょ|せいそ|ふんいき|だれ|す"}],"notes":"清楚 \"simple, pure and neat\" describes a well-groomed, unobtrusive beauty without ostentation. It most often refers to women, their clothing and their aura: 清楚な女性, 清楚な服装, 清楚系 (a fashion style). Flowers such as white lilies are also considered 清楚. Opposites are 派手 \"flashy\" or 華やか; 上品 \"elegant\" is more general.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"絢爛たる平安絵巻の世界に魅了された。","romaji":"Kenran taru Heian emaki no sekai ni miryō sareta.","german":"I was fascinated by the gorgeous world of the Heian picture scrolls.","furigana":"けんらん|へいあんえまき|せかい|みりょう"},{"japanese":"能舞台では、絢爛たる衣装をまとった役者が静かに舞う。","romaji":"Nō butai de wa, kenran taru ishō o matotta yakusha ga shizuka ni mau.","german":"On the Noh stage, an actor in a gorgeous costume dances quietly.","furigana":"のうぶたい|けんらん|いしょう|やくしゃ|しず|ま"},{"japanese":"宮殿の大広間は豪華絢爛なシャンデリアで彩られていた。","romaji":"Kyūden no ōhiroma wa gōka kenran na shanderia de irodorarete ita.","german":"The great hall of the palace was adorned with sumptuous chandeliers.","furigana":"きゅうでん|おおひろま|ごうかけんらん|いろど"}],"notes":"絢爛 \"gorgeous, splendid, dazzling\" is a たる-adjective of the written language: attributive 絢爛たる, adverbial 絢爛と. It appears most often in the four-character compound 豪華絢爛 \"sumptuous and splendid\", which can also take な (豪華絢爛な衣装). It describes art, costumes and festivals, but also a flowery style (絢爛たる文体). In everyday speech one says 華やか.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"侘しい山里の風景が俳句の題材となっている。","romaji":"Wabishii yamazato no fūkei ga haiku no daizai to natte iru.","german":"The lonely mountain village scenery is the subject of haiku.","furigana":"わび|やまざと|ふうけい|はいく|だいざい"},{"japanese":"給料日前は、カップ麺だけの侘しい夕食になる。","romaji":"Kyūryōbi mae wa, kappumen dake no wabishii yūshoku ni naru.","german":"Before payday, dinner is a dreary affair of nothing but cup noodles.","furigana":"きゅうりょうびまえ|めん|わび|ゆうしょく"},{"japanese":"単身赴任先での侘しい一人暮らしにも、ようやく慣れてきた。","romaji":"Tanshin funin saki de no wabishii hitorigurashi ni mo, yōyaku narete kita.","german":"I have finally gotten used to the lonely life of living alone at my post away from my family.","furigana":"たんしんふにんさき|わび|ひとりぐ|な"}],"notes":"侘しい \"dreary, lonely, shabby\" is an い-adjective combining loneliness and bleakness: わびしい一人暮らし, わびしい食事, わびしい冬の景色. It is mostly written in kana. Unlike 寂しい (loneliness, longing for others), 侘しい also stresses the meager, joyless nature of the situation. Related is the aesthetic ideal 侘び (わび), which interprets plain austerity positively.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"揺るぎない信念を持って社会改革に取り組んだ。","romaji":"Yuruginai shinnen o motte shakai kaikaku ni torikunda.","german":"He took on social reform with unshakable conviction.","furigana":"ゆ|しんねん|も|しゃかいかいかく|と|く"},{"japanese":"彼女は実力でトップ選手としての揺るぎない地位を築いた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa jitsuryoku de toppu senshu toshite no yuruginai chii o kizuita.","german":"She built an unshakable position as a top athlete through her ability.","furigana":"かのじょ|じつりょく|せんしゅ|ゆ|ちい|きず"},{"japanese":"長年の取引で、両社の間には揺るぎない信頼関係がある。","romaji":"Naganen no torihiki de, ryōsha no aida ni wa yuruginai shinrai kankei ga aru.","german":"Years of dealings have created an unshakable relationship of trust between the two companies.","furigana":"ながねん|とりひき|りょうしゃ|あいだ|ゆ|しんらいかんけい"}],"notes":"揺るぎない \"unshakable, rock-solid\" is an い-adjective made of 揺るぎ \"wavering\" + ない. It stands before abstract nouns: 揺るぎない信念, 揺るぎない自信, 揺るぎない地位. 揺るぎのない and the verbal 揺るがない mean the same. It sounds elevated and positive; in everyday speech one also says 強い or しっかりした.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"根深い偏見を払拭するには地道な啓発活動が必要だ。","romaji":"Nebukai henken o fusshoku suru ni wa jimichi na keihatsu katsudō ga hitsuyō da.","german":"Persistent awareness-raising activities are needed to wipe out deep-rooted prejudice.","furigana":"ねぶか|へんけん|ふっしょく|じみち|けいはつかつどう|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"両国の対立は歴史的に根深い。","romaji":"Ryōkoku no tairitsu wa rekishiteki ni nebukai.","german":"The conflict between the two countries is historically deep-rooted.","furigana":"りょうこく|たいりつ|れきしてき|ねぶか"},{"japanese":"上司への根深い不信感から、彼は転職を決めた。","romaji":"Jōshi e no nebukai fushinkan kara, kare wa tenshoku o kimeta.","german":"Out of a deep-seated distrust of his boss, he decided to change jobs.","furigana":"じょうし|ねぶか|ふしんかん|かれ|てんしょく|き"}],"notes":"根深い \"deep-rooted, deep-seated\" is an い-adjective for problems, feelings and attitudes that have settled in for a long time and are hard to eliminate: 根深い問題, 根深い対立, 根深い不信感, 根深い差別. It is almost always negative in tone. For positive \"firmly established\" things such as popularity or traditions, one says 根強い (根強い人気) or 根付いている instead.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"すべての命は等しく尊いものだ。","romaji":"Subete no inochi wa hitoshiku tattoi mono da.","german":"All life is equally precious.","furigana":"いのち|ひと|たっと"},{"japanese":"事故で多くの尊い命が失われた。","romaji":"Jiko de ooku no tattoi inochi ga ushinawareta.","german":"Many precious lives were lost in the accident.","furigana":"じこ|おお|たっと|いのち|うしな"},{"japanese":"お寺で尊い教えを聞いて、心が洗われるようだった。","romaji":"Otera de tattoi oshie o kiite, kokoro ga arawareru you datta.","german":"Hearing the sacred teachings at the temple, I felt cleansed.","furigana":"てら|たっと|おし|き|こころ|あら"}],"notes":"尊い has two readings: たっとい and the now more usual とうとい; たっとい sounds older and more elevated. It means \"precious, irreplaceable\" (尊い命, 尊い犠牲) and \"venerable, sacred\" (尊い教え). Traditionally 尊い is written for \"worthy of reverence\" and 貴い for \"valuable\", but in modern usage the two are mixed. Compared with 大切 or 貴重, 尊い is more solemn and has a moral-religious coloring; the related verb 尊ぶ means \"to revere, to esteem highly\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大自然に対する畏敬の念が宗教の原点だ。","romaji":"Daishizen ni taisuru ikei no nen ga shūkyō no genten da.","german":"Awe toward great nature is the origin of religion.","furigana":"だいしぜん|たい|いけい|ねん|しゅうきょう|げんてん"},{"japanese":"弟子たちは師に対して深い畏敬の念を抱いていた。","romaji":"Deshitachi wa shi ni taishite fukai ikei no nen o idaite ita.","german":"The disciples held deep reverence for their master.","furigana":"でし|し|たい|ふか|いけい|ねん|いだ"},{"japanese":"満天の星を見上げると、宇宙への畏敬の念を覚える。","romaji":"Manten no hoshi o miageru to, uchū e no ikei no nen o oboeru.","german":"When I look up at the star-filled sky, I feel awe toward the universe.","furigana":"まんてん|ほし|みあ|うちゅう|いけい|ねん|おぼ"}],"notes":"畏敬 \"awe\" combines dread (畏) and esteem (敬). It almost always appears in the phrase 畏敬の念 \"a feeling of awe\": 〜に畏敬の念を抱く or 覚える. As a verb, 畏敬する is rarer. The object is nature, deities or outstanding people; compared with 尊敬 \"respect\", it carries a sense of dread before something overpowering.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この山は古来より神聖な場所として崇められてきた。","romaji":"Kono yama wa korai yori shinsei na basho toshite agamerarete kita.","german":"This mountain has been revered as a sacred place since ancient times.","furigana":"やま|こらい|しんせい|ばしょ|あが"},{"japanese":"インドでは牛は神聖な動物とされている。","romaji":"Indo de wa ushi wa shinsei na dōbutsu to sarete iru.","german":"In India, the cow is regarded as a sacred animal.","furigana":"うし|しんせい|どうぶつ"},{"japanese":"選挙での一票は国民の神聖な権利だ。","romaji":"Senkyo de no ippyō wa kokumin no shinsei na kenri da.","german":"A vote in an election is a sacred right of the citizens.","furigana":"せんきょ|いっぴょう|こくみん|しんせい|けんり"}],"notes":"神聖 \"holy, inviolable\" is a な-adjective and noun: 神聖な場所, 神聖な儀式, 神聖な義務. Figuratively, things that must not be violated, such as a right or a duty, are also called 神聖. 神聖視する means \"to regard as sacred\", 神聖さ \"sacredness\". Unlike the literary 聖なる (attributive only: 聖なる夜), 神聖 is the neutral standard word.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"職業に貴賤はないという考えが広まっている。","romaji":"Shokugyō ni kisen wa nai to iu kangae ga hiromatte iru.","german":"The view that there is no high or low among occupations is spreading.","furigana":"しょくぎょう|きせん|かんが|ひろ"},{"japanese":"寺には貴賤を問わず、多くの人々が参拝に訪れた。","romaji":"Tera ni wa kisen o towazu, ōku no hitobito ga sanpai ni otozureta.","german":"Many people visited the temple to worship, regardless of rank.","furigana":"てら|きせん|と|おお|ひとびと|さんぱい|おとず"},{"japanese":"江戸時代の身分制度では、生まれによって貴賤が決まっていた。","romaji":"Edo jidai no mibun seido de wa, umare ni yotte kisen ga kimatte ita.","german":"Under the Edo-period class system, whether one was of high or low status was determined by birth.","furigana":"えどじだい|みぶんせいど|う|きせん|き"}],"notes":"貴賤 \"high and low\" denotes the contrast between noble and humble social rank. It is alive mainly in set phrases: 職業に貴賤なし \"no occupation is inferior\" and 貴賤を問わず \"regardless of rank\". It is literary and sounds somewhat archaic; otherwise today one speaks rather of 身分 or 地位.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"栄枯盛衰は世の習いだ。","romaji":"Eiko seisui wa yo no narai da.","german":"Rise and fall is the way of the world.","furigana":"えいこせいすい|よ|なら"},{"japanese":"この港町は、貿易とともに栄枯盛衰を繰り返してきた。","romaji":"Kono minatomachi wa, bōeki to tomo ni eiko seisui o kurikaeshite kita.","german":"This port town has repeatedly gone through rise and fall along with trade.","furigana":"みなとまち|ぼうえき|えいこせいすい|く|かえ"},{"japanese":"古い屋敷の荒れた庭が、一族の栄枯を物語っている。","romaji":"Furui yashiki no areta niwa ga, ichizoku no eiko o monogatatte iru.","german":"The overgrown garden of the old mansion tells of the rise and fall of the family.","furigana":"ふる|やしき|あ|にわ|いちぞく|えいこ|ものがた"}],"notes":"栄枯 \"flourishing and withering\" stands figuratively for the rise and fall of families, cities or empires. On its own it is rare; it almost always appears in the four-character compound 栄枯盛衰 \"rise and fall, the ups and downs of fortune\". Typical frames are 栄枯盛衰は世の習い and 栄枯盛衰を繰り返す. The word is literary and has a reflective, historical tone.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"社会の分断化が進むことを深く憂慮している。","romaji":"Shakai no bundanka ga susumu koto o fukaku yūryo shite iru.","german":"I am deeply concerned that social division is advancing.","furigana":"しゃかい|ぶんだんか|すす|ふか|ゆうりょ"},{"japanese":"政府は感染の急拡大を憂慮すべき事態と受け止めている。","romaji":"Seifu wa kansen no kyūkakudai o yūryo subeki jitai to uketomete iru.","german":"The government regards the rapid spread of infections as a worrying situation.","furigana":"せいふ|かんせん|きゅうかくだい|ゆうりょ|じたい|う|と"},{"japanese":"子どもの読書離れを憂慮する声が教育関係者から上がっている。","romaji":"Kodomo no dokushobanare o yūryo suru koe ga kyōiku kankeisha kara agatte iru.","german":"Voices of concern are being raised by educators about children reading less.","furigana":"こ|どくしょばな|ゆうりょ|こえ|きょういくかんけいしゃ|あ"}],"notes":"憂慮 \"serious concern\" is a literary noun and する-verb (transitive: 事態を憂慮する). It is typical of politics, diplomacy and news: 深く憂慮している, 憂慮すべき事態 \"a worrying situation\". In everyday speech one says 心配する; 懸念 is similarly formal, but relates more to a concrete fear.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"伝統文化の衰退に対する危惧が高まっている。","romaji":"Dentō bunka no suitai ni taisuru kigu ga takamatte iru.","german":"Concern about the decline of traditional culture is growing.","furigana":"でんとうぶんか|すいたい|たい|きぐ|たか"},{"japanese":"専門家は景気の悪化を危惧している。","romaji":"Senmonka wa keiki no akka o kigu shite iru.","german":"Experts fear a worsening of the economy.","furigana":"せんもんか|けいき|あっか|きぐ"},{"japanese":"この鳥は絶滅危惧種に指定されている。","romaji":"Kono tori wa zetsumetsu kigushu ni shitei sarete iru.","german":"This bird is designated as an endangered species.","furigana":"とり|ぜつめつきぐしゅ|してい"}],"notes":"危惧 is a serious, well-founded \"apprehension\" and belongs to written and news language. Typical are 〜に対する危惧, 危惧を抱く and 危惧が高まる; as a transitive する-verb it is 〜を危惧する or 〜ではないかと危惧する. Well known is 絶滅危惧種 \"endangered species\". In everyday speech one says 心配 or 不安 instead.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの時の心配は杞憂に終わった。","romaji":"Ano toki no shinpai wa kiyū ni owatta.","german":"The worry I had back then turned out to be unfounded.","furigana":"とき|しんぱい|きゆう|お"},{"japanese":"君の心配は杞憂にすぎないよ。","romaji":"Kimi no shinpai wa kiyū ni suginai yo.","german":"Your worry is nothing but needless anxiety.","furigana":"きみ|しんぱい|きゆう"},{"japanese":"雨で中止になるかと思ったが、杞憂だった。","romaji":"Ame de chūshi ni naru ka to omotta ga, kiyū datta.","german":"I thought it might be canceled because of rain, but the worry was for nothing.","furigana":"あめ|ちゅうし|おも|きゆう"}],"notes":"杞憂 refers to a worry that turns out in retrospect to be \"unfounded\"; the word goes back to a Chinese story about a man from the state of 杞 who feared that the sky might fall. The set phrase is 杞憂に終わる \"to prove unfounded\", also 〜は杞憂だった and 杞憂にすぎない. It sounds educated; in everyday speech one says 心配しすぎ or 取り越し苦労 instead.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"旺盛な探究心が社会の発展を支えている。","romaji":"Ōsei na tankyūshin ga shakai no hatten o sasaete iru.","german":"A vigorous spirit of inquiry supports the development of society.","furigana":"おうせい|たんきゅうしん|しゃかい|はってん|ささ"},{"japanese":"うちの息子は食欲が旺盛で、ご飯を三杯も食べる。","romaji":"Uchi no musuko wa shokuyoku ga ōsei de, gohan o sanbai mo taberu.","german":"My son has a hearty appetite and eats three bowls of rice.","furigana":"むすこ|しょくよく|おうせい|はん|さんばい|た"},{"japanese":"好奇心旺盛な子どもは何でも触りたがる。","romaji":"Kōkishin ōsei na kodomo wa nan demo sawaritagaru.","german":"Curious children want to touch everything.","furigana":"こうきしんおうせい|こ|なん|さわ"}],"notes":"旺盛 describes a drive or power that is \"bursting with energy\": appetite, curiosity, drive to act, demand. Very often it is used as a suffix without な: 食欲旺盛, 好奇心旺盛, 元気旺盛; predicative 食欲が旺盛だ, attributive 旺盛な需要. It always needs such a related word: 好奇心旺盛な人 is natural, a bare 旺盛な人 is not. Neutral to literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"瑞々しい感性が若い芸術家の作品に溢れている。","romaji":"Mizumizushii kansei ga wakai geijutsuka no sakuhin ni afurete iru.","german":"Fresh sensibility overflows in the works of young artists.","furigana":"みずみず|かんせい|わか|げいじゅつか|さくひん|あふ"},{"japanese":"朝採れのトマトはみずみずしくて甘い。","romaji":"Asadore no tomato wa mizumizushikute amai.","german":"Tomatoes picked in the morning are juicy and sweet.","furigana":"あさど|あま"},{"japanese":"雨上がりの若葉がみずみずしく輝いている。","romaji":"Ameagari no wakaba ga mizumizushiku kagayaite iru.","german":"After the rain, the young leaves shine fresh and dewy.","furigana":"あめあ|わかば|かがや"}],"notes":"みずみずしい means \"fresh, juicy, full of life\" and is mostly written in kana; 瑞々しい is the kanji spelling. Concretely it stands for fruit, vegetables, skin or leaves that look plump and moist (みずみずしいトマト, みずみずしい肌); figuratively for the youthful freshness of feeling and expression (瑞々しい感性, 瑞々しい文体). Unlike 新鮮, it stresses less that something is new or freshly harvested than the juicy, lively impression.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"巧みな話術で聴衆を魅了した。","romaji":"Takumi na wajutsu de chōshū o miryō shita.","german":"He captivated the audience with his skillful speaking.","furigana":"たく|わじゅつ|ちょうしゅう|みりょう"},{"japanese":"彼は言葉巧みに老人をだまして金を奪った。","romaji":"Kare wa kotoba takumi ni rōjin o damashite kane o ubatta.","german":"Using smooth words, he deceived an old man and took his money.","furigana":"かれ|ことばたく|ろうじん|かね|うば"},{"japanese":"職人は巧みな手つきで竹を編んでいく。","romaji":"Shokunin wa takumi na tetsuki de take o ande iku.","german":"The craftsman weaves the bamboo with deft hands.","furigana":"しょくにん|たく|て|たけ|あ"}],"notes":"巧み is a な-adjective for \"skillful, adroit\" and stresses artful technique: 巧みな話術, 巧みな手つき, adverbial 巧みに操る or 巧みに利用する. Compared with 上手, it is more literary and can also mean \"cunning, crafty\", as in 巧みな嘘 or 言葉巧みに騙す. As a noun (\"craftsmanship\") it is rare today.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"拙いながらも心のこもった演奏に感動した。","romaji":"Tsutanai nagara mo kokoro no komotta ensō ni kandō shita.","german":"I was moved by the clumsy but heartfelt performance.","furigana":"つたな|こころ|えんそう|かんどう"},{"japanese":"拙い文章ですが、最後まで読んでいただければ幸いです。","romaji":"Tsutanai bunshō desu ga, saigo made yonde itadakereba saiwai desu.","german":"It is a poor piece of writing, but I would be glad if you would read it to the end.","furigana":"つたな|ぶんしょう|さいご|よ|さいわ"},{"japanese":"拙い日本語で一生懸命説明した。","romaji":"Tsutanai nihongo de isshōkenmei setsumei shita.","german":"I explained as hard as I could in my clumsy Japanese.","furigana":"つたな|にほんご|いっしょうけんめいせつめい"}],"notes":"つたない means \"unskilled, clumsy\" and sounds more elevated than 下手. Typical is the humble use for one's own performance: 拙い文章ですが, 拙い日本語で失礼します. The same kanji 拙い is also read まずい (\"unfavorable, bad\"), so つたない is often written in kana. Related is 稚拙 \"childishly clumsy\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"粗野な振る舞いは社交の場では許されない。","romaji":"Soya na furumai wa shakō no ba de wa yurusarenai.","german":"Coarse behavior is not tolerated in social settings.","furigana":"そや|ふ|ま|しゃこう|ば|ゆる"},{"japanese":"彼は見た目は粗野だが、実はとても優しい人だ。","romaji":"Kare wa mitame wa soya da ga, jitsu wa totemo yasashii hito da.","german":"He looks rough, but is actually a very kind person.","furigana":"かれ|み|め|そや|じつ|やさ|ひと"},{"japanese":"その小説の主人公は粗野な言葉遣いで読者を驚かせる。","romaji":"Sono shōsetsu no shujinkō wa soya na kotobazukai de dokusha o odorokaseru.","german":"The protagonist of the novel shocks readers with his crude language.","furigana":"しょうせつ|しゅじんこう|そや|ことばづか|どくしゃ|おどろ"}],"notes":"粗野 is a な-adjective and noun meaning \"uncouth, coarse\" in behavior, manner or speech: 粗野な振る舞い, 粗野な言葉遣い, 粗野な男. It criticizes a lack of refinement; unlike 乱暴, it is not about violence or recklessness. Antonyms are 上品 or 洗練された. Rather literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"戦争の野蛮さを後世に伝えなければならない。","romaji":"Sensō no yabansa o kōsei ni tsutaenakereba naranai.","german":"The barbarity of war must be passed on to future generations.","furigana":"せんそう|やばん|こうせい|つた"},{"japanese":"暴力で問題を解決しようとするのは野蛮なやり方だ。","romaji":"Bōryoku de mondai o kaiketsu shiyō to suru no wa yaban na yarikata da.","german":"Trying to solve problems with violence is a barbaric way of doing things.","furigana":"ぼうりょく|もんだい|かいけつ|やばん|かた"},{"japanese":"昔の人々は、自分と違う文化を野蛮だと見なすことが多かった。","romaji":"Mukashi no hitobito wa, jibun to chigau bunka o yaban da to minasu koto ga ōkatta.","german":"People in the past often regarded cultures different from their own as barbaric.","furigana":"むかし|ひとびと|じぶん|ちが|ぶんか|やばん|み|おお"}],"notes":"野蛮 is a な-adjective and noun meaning \"uncivilized, barbaric\": 野蛮な行為, 野蛮な考え, 野蛮人; the noun 野蛮さ denotes barbarity itself. It judges something as crude and uncultured. 乱暴 refers more to rough, violent behavior in an individual case, 残酷 to \"cruel\". Note: calling people or cultures 野蛮 sounds derogatory.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"未曾有の危機に直面して国際社会は団結した。","romaji":"Mizou no kiki ni chokumen shite kokusai shakai wa danketsu shita.","german":"Faced with an unprecedented crisis, the international community united.","furigana":"みぞう|きき|ちょくめん|こくさいしゃかい|だんけつ"},{"japanese":"その地震は、未曾有の被害をもたらした。","romaji":"Sono jishin wa, mizou no higai o motarashita.","german":"The earthquake caused unprecedented damage.","furigana":"じしん|みぞう|ひがい"},{"japanese":"首相が「未曾有」を「みぞうゆう」と読み間違えて話題になった。","romaji":"Shushou ga \"mizou\" o \"mizouyuu\" to yomimachigaete wadai ni natta.","german":"It became a topic of conversation when the prime minister misread 「未曾有」 as 「みぞうゆう」.","furigana":"しゅしょう|みぞう|よ|まちが|わだい"}],"notes":"未曾有 literally means \"never having been before\" (未だ曾て有らず) and almost always stands attributively with の: 未曾有の危機, 未曾有の大災害, 未曾有の事態. It sounds much weightier than 前例のない or 初めての and is typical of news and speeches about disasters. The reading みぞう is a well-known trap; the incorrect reading みぞうゆう once made headlines.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"戦争による国土の荒廃は目を覆うばかりだった。","romaji":"Sensō ni yoru kokudo no kōhai wa me o ōu bakari datta.","german":"The devastation of the land by the war was too terrible to look at.","furigana":"せんそう|こくど|こうはい|め|おお"},{"japanese":"住む人がいなくなり、村は荒廃してしまった。","romaji":"Sumu hito ga inaku nari, mura wa kōhai shite shimatta.","german":"With no one living there anymore, the village fell into ruin.","furigana":"す|ひと|むら|こうはい"},{"japanese":"長引く不況で人々の心が荒廃していった。","romaji":"Nagabiku fukyō de hitobito no kokoro ga kōhai shite itta.","german":"The prolonged recession caused people's hearts to become hardened.","furigana":"ながび|ふきょう|ひとびと|こころ|こうはい"}],"notes":"荒廃 denotes the state in which land, buildings or towns are \"devastated, fallen into ruin\"; as an intransitive する-verb: 町が荒廃する, often attributive 荒廃した土地. Figuratively one speaks of 人心の荒廃 or 教育の荒廃, moral decay. Literary; in everyday speech one says 荒れる (荒れた庭) instead.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"衰退した伝統芸能の再興を目指す若者が増えている。","romaji":"Suitai shita dentō geinō no saikō o mezasu wakamono ga fuete iru.","german":"The number of young people aiming to revive declining traditional performing arts is increasing.","furigana":"すいたい|でんとうげいのう|さいこう|めざ|わかもの|ふ"},{"japanese":"彼は没落した家の再興に生涯をささげた。","romaji":"Kare wa botsuraku shita ie no saikō ni shōgai o sasageta.","german":"He devoted his life to restoring his fallen family.","furigana":"かれ|ぼつらく|いえ|さいこう|しょうがい"},{"japanese":"戦後、その寺は地元の人々の寄付によって再興された。","romaji":"Sengo, sono tera wa jimoto no hitobito no kifu ni yotte saikō sareta.","german":"After the war, the temple was rebuilt through donations from local people.","furigana":"せんご|てら|じもと|ひとびと|きふ|さいこう"}],"notes":"再興 means \"to revive\" something that has declined, or its revival: a family, a country, an art or a temple. It can be used both transitively (家を再興する) and intransitively (国が再興する). Unlike 復興 (reconstruction after war or disaster, 震災復興), 再興 stresses the revival of institutions and traditions; 復活 is more general and everyday. Literary, often historical (お家再興).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"焼失した城の復元工事が完了した。","romaji":"Shōshitsu shita shiro no fukugen kōji ga kanryō shita.","german":"The reconstruction work on the burned-down castle has been completed.","furigana":"しょうしつ|しろ|ふくげんこうじ|かんりょう"},{"japanese":"削除したファイルを復元する方法を教えてください。","romaji":"Sakujo shita fairu o fukugen suru hōhō o oshiete kudasai.","german":"Please tell me how to restore deleted files.","furigana":"さくじょ|ふくげん|ほうほう|おし"},{"japanese":"発掘された骨から恐竜の姿が復元された。","romaji":"Hakkutsu sareta hone kara kyōryū no sugata ga fukugen sareta.","german":"The appearance of the dinosaur was reconstructed from the excavated bones.","furigana":"はっくつ|ほね|きょうりゅう|すがた|ふくげん"}],"notes":"復元 means to return something to its \"original state\": buildings, fossils, paintings, but also data. It is mostly transitive (城を復元する, passive 復元される); the result is called e.g. 復元図 or 復元模型. In IT it means \"to restore\" (ファイルを復元する). Distinctions: 復旧 is putting infrastructure back into operation (電車が復旧する), 修復 is the repair of damaged things.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"古い壁画の模写を通じて当時の技法を学ぶ。","romaji":"Furui hekiga no mosha o tsūjite tōji no gihō o manabu.","german":"One learns the techniques of the time by copying old murals.","furigana":"ふる|へきが|もしゃ|つう|とうじ|ぎほう|まな"},{"japanese":"美術学生が美術館でモネの絵を模写している。","romaji":"Bijutsu gakusei ga bijutsukan de Mone no e o mosha shite iru.","german":"An art student is copying a Monet painting at the museum.","furigana":"びじゅつがくせい|びじゅつかん|え|もしゃ"},{"japanese":"彼は有名人の声帯模写が得意だ。","romaji":"Kare wa yūmeijin no seitai mosha ga tokui da.","german":"He is good at impersonating the voices of celebrities.","furigana":"かれ|ゆうめいじん|せいたいもしゃ|とくい"}],"notes":"模写 is the exact copying of an original by hand, above all with paintings and calligraphy: 名画を模写する, 模写を通じて学ぶ. In addition, 声帯模写 and 形態模写 denote the imitation of voices or gestures as an entertainment art. Unlike コピー (mechanical), 模写 is handwork; 複製 is reproduction in general, 模倣 imitation of style or ideas.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大義名分のない戦争は国際社会の支持を得られない。","romaji":"Taigi meibun no nai sensō wa kokusai shakai no shiji o erarenai.","german":"A war without a just cause cannot win the support of the international community.","furigana":"たいぎめいぶん|せんそう|こくさいしゃかい|しじ|え"},{"japanese":"彼らは民族独立という大義のために命をかけた。","romaji":"Karera wa minzoku dokuritsu to iu taigi no tame ni inochi o kaketa.","german":"They risked their lives for the great cause of national independence.","furigana":"かれ|みんぞくどくりつ|たいぎ|いのち"},{"japanese":"増税するなら、それなりの大義が必要だ。","romaji":"Zōzei suru nara, sore nari no taigi ga hitsuyō da.","german":"If taxes are to be raised, a reasonable justification is needed.","furigana":"ぞうぜい|たいぎ|ひつよう"}],"notes":"大義 is the \"great, morally justified purpose\" one serves, originally the duty toward one's lord and state. Typical: 大義のために戦う, 大義を掲げる. Very common is 大義名分 \"official justification\", often critical in the sense of \"pretext\": 大義名分が立たない. Do not confuse it with the identically read 大儀 \"tiresome, burdensome\". Literary and elevated.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"良識ある市民の行動が社会を健全に保つ。","romaji":"Ryōshiki aru shimin no kōdō ga shakai o kenzen ni tamotsu.","german":"The conduct of sensible citizens keeps society healthy.","furigana":"りょうしき|しみん|こうどう|しゃかい|けんぜん|たも"},{"japanese":"あんな発言をするなんて、彼の良識を疑う。","romaji":"Anna hatsugen o suru nante, kare no ryōshiki o utagau.","german":"To say something like that, I doubt his good sense.","furigana":"はつげん|かれ|りょうしき|うたが"},{"japanese":"最後は読者の良識に判断を委ねたい。","romaji":"Saigo wa dokusha no ryōshiki ni handan o yudanetai.","german":"In the end, I would like to leave the judgment to the good sense of the readers.","furigana":"さいご|どくしゃ|りょうしき|はんだん|ゆだ"}],"notes":"良識 is the \"sound judgment\" of a decent, reasonable person: not mere knowledge, but a morally balanced judgment. Typical: 良識ある市民, 良識ある大人, 良識に訴える, 良識に欠ける and 良識を疑う \"to doubt someone's good sense\". Related is 常識 (\"social norms, common knowledge\"); 良識 is more elevated and stresses the ethical. The upper house is traditionally called 良識の府.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"困難に立ち向かう気概を持った若者が必要だ。","romaji":"Konnan ni tachimukau kigai o motta wakamono ga hitsuyō da.","german":"We need young people with the spirit to face difficulties.","furigana":"こんなん|た|む|きがい|も|わかもの|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"自分の力で会社を立て直すという気概で、彼は社長に就任した。","romaji":"Jibun no chikara de kaisha o tatenaosu to iu kigai de, kare wa shachō ni shūnin shita.","german":"With the resolve to rebuild the company by his own efforts, he took up the post of president.","furigana":"じぶん|ちから|かいしゃ|た|なお|きがい|かれ|しゃちょう|しゅうにん"},{"japanese":"最近の政治家には国を背負う気概が感じられない。","romaji":"Saikin no seijika ni wa kuni o seou kigai ga kanjirarenai.","german":"One does not sense in recent politicians the spirit to shoulder the nation.","furigana":"さいきん|せいじか|くに|せお|きがい|かん"}],"notes":"気概 is the \"strength of will\" not to be beaten by difficulties: backbone and fighting spirit. Typical: 気概を持つ, 気概がある/ない, 〜する気概, 〜という気概で. Compared with 意欲 (\"motivation\"), 気概 stresses steadfastness against resistance; 根性 is more colloquial. Elevated, often used in speeches and editorials.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"職人としての矜持を持って仕事に臨んでいる。","romaji":"Shokunin toshite no kyōji o motte shigoto ni nozonde iru.","german":"He approaches his work with the pride of a craftsman.","furigana":"しょくにん|きょうじ|も|しごと|のぞ"},{"japanese":"プロとしての矜持が、手を抜くことを許さなかった。","romaji":"Puro toshite no kyōji ga, te o nuku koto o yurusanakatta.","german":"His pride as a professional did not allow him to cut corners.","furigana":"きょうじ|て|ぬ|ゆる"},{"japanese":"敗れはしたが、彼らは王者としての矜持を最後まで保った。","romaji":"Yabure wa shita ga, karera wa ōja toshite no kyōji o saigo made tamotta.","german":"Though they lost, they kept their dignity as champions to the end.","furigana":"やぶ|かれ|おうじゃ|きょうじ|さいご|たも"}],"notes":"矜持 is the \"pride and self-respect\" that grow out of skill or position and that one does not want to betray: 職人としての矜持, 矜持を持つ, 矜持を保つ, 矜持が許さない. The original reading is きんじ; today きょうじ is generally used. Compared with プライド or 誇り, it is clearly literary and has nothing arrogant about it (unlike 高慢).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"預言者は天啓を受けて人々に神の言葉を伝えた。","romaji":"Yogensha wa tenkei o ukete hitobito ni kami no kotoba o tsutaeta.","german":"The prophet received a divine revelation and conveyed the word of God to the people.","furigana":"よげんしゃ|てんけい|う|ひとびと|かみ|ことば|つた"},{"japanese":"そのアイデアは天啓のように突然ひらめいた。","romaji":"Sono aidea wa tenkei no yō ni totsuzen hirameita.","german":"The idea struck me suddenly like an inspiration from above.","furigana":"てんけい|とつぜん"},{"japanese":"作曲家はその旋律を天啓だと感じたという。","romaji":"Sakkyokuka wa sono senritsu o tenkei da to kanjita to iu.","german":"The composer is said to have felt that melody was a divine inspiration.","furigana":"さっきょくか|せんりつ|てんけい|かん"}],"notes":"天啓 is a \"revelation\" from heaven or God in the religious sense: 天啓を受ける, 天啓が下る. Figuratively it denotes a sudden inspiration that seems to come from above: 天啓のようにひらめく. For an ordinary good idea, one says ひらめき or 思いつき; 啓示 is the more matter-of-fact word for religious revelation (神の啓示). Literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"護摩を焚いて諸願成就を祈る。","romaji":"Goma o taite shogan jōju o inoru.","german":"They light a goma fire and pray for the fulfillment of all wishes.","furigana":"ごま|た|しょがんじょうじゅ|いの"},{"japanese":"参拝者は護摩木に願い事を書いて奉納した。","romaji":"Sanpaisha wa gomagi ni negaigoto o kaite hōnō shita.","german":"The worshippers wrote their wishes on goma sticks and offered them.","furigana":"さんぱいしゃ|ごまぎ|ねが|ごと|か|ほうのう"},{"japanese":"成田山では毎日護摩祈祷が行われている。","romaji":"Naritasan de wa mainichi goma kitō ga okonawarete iru.","german":"Goma fire ceremonies are held every day at Naritasan.","furigana":"なりたさん|まいにちごまきとう|おこな"}],"notes":"護摩 is the fire ritual of esoteric Buddhism (above all Shingon and Tendai), in which wooden sticks (護摩木) bearing wishes are burned in the fire, usually before 不動明王. The accompanying verb is 焚く: 護摩を焚く; the ritual is also called 護摩祈祷. Do not confuse it with the identically read 胡麻 \"sesame\" - the idiom ごまをする \"to flatter\" refers to sesame.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"伊勢神宮の式年遷宮は二十年に一度行われる。","romaji":"Ise Jingū no shikinen sengū wa nijūnen ni ichido okonawareru.","german":"The ritual rebuilding of Ise Shrine is carried out once every twenty years.","furigana":"いせじんぐう|しきねんせんぐう|にじゅうねん|いちどおこな"},{"japanese":"遷宮を終えた新しい社殿に多くの参拝客が訪れた。","romaji":"Sengū o oeta atarashii shaden ni ōku no sanpaikyaku ga otozureta.","german":"Many worshippers visited the new shrine buildings after the relocation of the deity.","furigana":"せんぐう|お|あたら|しゃでん|おお|さんぱいきゃく|おとず"},{"japanese":"出雲大社でも、およそ六十年ごとに遷宮が行われている。","romaji":"Izumo Taisha de mo, oyoso rokujūnen goto ni sengū ga okonawarete iru.","german":"At Izumo Taisha, too, a sengu is held about every sixty years.","furigana":"|ろくじゅうねん|せんぐう|おこな"}],"notes":"遷宮 denotes the transfer of the deity of a Shinto shrine to a newly built or renewed sanctuary. Best known is the 式年遷宮 of Ise Shrine, in which the shrine buildings are rebuilt every twenty years; Izumo Taisha also holds a 遷宮. A technical term from Shinto, mostly used as a noun (遷宮が行われる, 遷宮祭), rarely as an intransitive する-verb.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"祭りで神輿を担いで町を練り歩く。","romaji":"Matsuri de mikoshi o katsuide machi o neriaruku.","german":"At the festival, they carry the portable shrine and parade through the town.","furigana":"まつ|みこし|かつ|まち|ね|ある"},{"japanese":"子どもたちが小さなお神輿を担いで、「わっしょい」と声を上げた。","romaji":"Kodomotachi ga chiisana omikoshi o katsuide, wasshoi to koe o ageta.","german":"The children carried a small mikoshi and shouted \"Wasshoi!\".","furigana":"こ|ちい|みこし|かつ|こえ|あ"},{"japanese":"彼は派閥の神輿として担がれ、総裁選に出馬した。","romaji":"Kare wa habatsu no mikoshi toshite katsugare, sōsaisen ni shutsuba shita.","german":"He was hoisted up as his faction's figurehead and ran in the party leadership election.","furigana":"かれ|はばつ|みこし|かつ|そうさいせん|しゅつば"}],"notes":"神輿 is the \"portable shrine\" in which the deity is carried through the neighborhood at a festival; one says 神輿を担ぐ, 神輿が出る, 神輿が町を練り歩く. In everyday speech the polite form お神輿 is very common, and it is often written in kana as みこし. Figuratively, 神輿を担ぐ means to put someone at the top as a figurehead and support them.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"江戸時代には伊勢講や富士講などの庶民の信仰組織があった。","romaji":"Edo jidai ni wa Isekō ya Fujikō nado no shomin no shinkō soshiki ga atta.","german":"In the Edo period there were popular religious organizations such as the Ise-kō and Fuji-kō.","furigana":"えどじだい|いせこう|ふじこう|しょみん|しんこうそしき"},{"japanese":"村の人々は講を組んで、毎月少しずつお金を積み立てた。","romaji":"Mura no hitobito wa kō o kunde, maitsuki sukoshi zutsu okane o tsumitateta.","german":"The villagers formed a kō and put aside a little money every month.","furigana":"むら|ひとびと|こう|く|まいつきすこ|かね|つ|た"},{"japanese":"この地域では今も念仏講の集まりが続いている。","romaji":"Kono chiiki de wa ima mo nenbutsukō no atsumari ga tsuzuite iru.","german":"In this area, meetings of the nenbutsu-kō continue to this day.","furigana":"ちいき|いま|ねんぶつこう|あつ|つづ"}],"notes":"講 historically denotes an association of believers who, for example, saved money together to finance pilgrimages; as a suffix it follows the pilgrimage destination or the venerated deity: 伊勢講, 富士講, 念仏講. Originally it was a Buddhist teaching assembly (the kanji as in 講義, 講話); later also a mutual savings and credit association such as 頼母子講. One says 講を組む. Today it is used mainly in history and folklore studies.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"天皇は国家の祭祀を司る存在として位置づけられている。","romaji":"Tennō wa kokka no saishi o tsukasadoru sonzai toshite ichizukerarete iru.","german":"The Emperor is positioned as the one who presides over the state rituals.","furigana":"てんのう|こっか|さいし|つかさど|そんざい|いち"},{"japanese":"この遺跡からは古代の祭祀に使われた土器が出土した。","romaji":"Kono iseki kara wa kodai no saishi ni tsukawareta doki ga shutsudo shita.","german":"Earthenware used in ancient rituals was excavated from this site.","furigana":"いせき|こだい|さいし|つか|どき|しゅつど"},{"japanese":"長男が先祖の祭祀を受け継ぐのが慣わしだった。","romaji":"Chōnan ga senzo no saishi o uketsugu no ga narawashi datta.","german":"It was the custom for the eldest son to take over the ancestral rites.","furigana":"ちょうなん|せんぞ|さいし|う|つ|なら"}],"notes":"祭祀 is the formal word for \"cult, ritual worship\" of gods and ancestors: 祭祀を行う, 祭祀を司る, 宮中祭祀 (rites at the imperial court). Unlike 祭り (\"festival\"), it denotes the religious act itself, not the folk festival. It is a technical term in religious studies, archaeology (祭祀遺跡) and law (祭祀財産, e.g. the grave and household altar in inheritance law).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その議論はまるで禅問答のように堂々巡りだった。","romaji":"Sono giron wa marude zen mondō no yō ni dōdōmeguri datta.","german":"The discussion went around in circles like a Zen dialogue.","furigana":"ぎろん|ぜんもんどう|どうどうめぐ"},{"japanese":"修行僧は師との禅問答を通して悟りを目指した。","romaji":"Shugyōsō wa shi to no zen mondō o tōshite satori o mezashita.","german":"The trainee monk aimed for enlightenment through Zen dialogues with his master.","furigana":"しゅぎょうそう|し|ぜんもんどう|とお|さと|めざ"},{"japanese":"役所に問い合わせたが、禅問答のような答えしか返ってこなかった。","romaji":"Yakusho ni toiawaseta ga, zen mondō no yō na kotae shika kaette konakatta.","german":"I inquired at the government office, but only got cryptic, noncommittal answers.","furigana":"やくしょ|と|あ|ぜんもんどう|こた|かえ"}],"notes":"禅問答 is originally the question-and-answer exchange between a Zen master and a disciple intended to lead to enlightenment (cf. 公案). In everyday speech it is mostly used pejoratively for a conversation in which questions and answers talk past each other or remain incomprehensible: まるで禅問答だ, 禅問答のようなやり取り. The figurative meaning is much more common today than the religious one.","levelNote":"Compound: 禅 (N1) + 問答 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一切衆生を救うことが菩薩の誓願だ。","romaji":"Issai shujō o sukuu koto ga bosatsu no seigan da.","german":"Saving all sentient beings is the vow of a bodhisattva.","furigana":"いっさいしゅじょう|すく|ぼさつ|せいがん"},{"japanese":"仏はすべての衆生に等しく慈悲を注ぐと説かれる。","romaji":"Hotoke wa subete no shujō ni hitoshiku jihi o sosogu to tokareru.","german":"It is taught that the Buddha pours out compassion equally on all living beings.","furigana":"ほとけ|しゅじょう|ひと|じひ|そそ|と"},{"japanese":"住職は法話で、衆生の苦しみについて語った。","romaji":"Jūshoku wa hōwa de, shujō no kurushimi ni tsuite katatta.","german":"In his sermon, the head priest spoke about the suffering of sentient beings.","furigana":"じゅうしょく|ほうわ|しゅじょう|くる|かた"}],"notes":"衆生 is a Buddhist term for \"all living beings\" who suffer in the cycle of rebirth and are to be saved. Set phrases are 一切衆生, 衆生を救う (衆生済度) and 衆生の苦しみ. Note that the reading is しゅじょう. Unlike 大衆 (\"the broad masses\"), it is a religious term that also includes animals, and it occurs almost only in Buddhist texts and sermons.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この作品には権力者への諷刺が巧みに織り込まれている。","romaji":"Kono sakuhin ni wa kenryokusha e no fūshi ga takumi ni orikomarete iru.","german":"This work skillfully weaves in satire of those in power.","furigana":"さくひん|けんりょくしゃ|ふうし|たく|お|こ"},{"japanese":"この漫画は政治家の汚職を鋭く諷刺している。","romaji":"Kono manga wa seijika no oshoku o surudoku fūshi shite iru.","german":"This manga sharply satirizes the corruption of politicians.","furigana":"まんが|せいじか|おしょく|するど|ふうし"},{"japanese":"江戸時代の川柳には世相への諷刺がよく見られる。","romaji":"Edo jidai no senryū ni wa sesō e no fūshi ga yoku mirareru.","german":"Satire of the state of society is often seen in the senryū of the Edo period.","furigana":"えどじだい|せんりゅう|せそう|ふうし|み"}],"notes":"諷刺 is \"satire\": abuses or the powerful are criticized in a witty, indirect way. Today 風刺 is the usual spelling; 諷刺 is the older one. Typical: 社会を諷刺する, 〜への諷刺, 諷刺画 (caricature), 諷刺が効いている. Unlike 皮肉 (\"irony, sarcasm\", also in personal conversation), 諷刺 is usually aimed at society or politics and is an artistic form.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"狂言の滑稽な演技が観客の笑いを誘った。","romaji":"Kyōgen no kokkei na engi ga kankyaku no warai o sasotta.","german":"The comical acting in kyōgen drew laughter from the audience.","furigana":"きょうげん|こっけい|えんぎ|かんきゃく|わら|さそ"},{"japanese":"必死に言い訳する彼の姿は、むしろ滑稽だった。","romaji":"Hisshi ni iiwake suru kare no sugata wa, mushiro kokkei datta.","german":"His desperate attempt to make excuses was rather ridiculous.","furigana":"ひっし|い|わけ|かれ|すがた|こっけい"},{"japanese":"ピエロの滑稽なしぐさに子どもたちは大笑いした。","romaji":"Piero no kokkei na shigusa ni kodomotachi wa ōwarai shita.","german":"The children laughed loudly at the clown's comical gestures.","furigana":"こっけい|こ|おおわら"}],"notes":"滑稽 is a な-adjective meaning \"comical, droll\" (滑稽な仕草, 滑稽な話), but also \"ridiculous\" in a derogatory sense: 必死に言い訳する姿は滑稽だった. Unlike おもしろい, it does not mean \"interesting\" but only \"provoking laughter\", and like ばかばかしい it can express mockery. The genre 滑稽本 (comic prose fiction) comes from the Edo period. Neutral to literary.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"食物連鎖の頂点に立つ捕食者が減少している。","romaji":"Shokumotsu rensa no chōten ni tatsu hoshokusha ga genshō shite iru.","german":"The predators at the top of the food chain are declining.","furigana":"しょくもつれんさ|ちょうてん|た|ほしょくしゃ|げんしょう"},{"japanese":"海の食物連鎖はプランクトンから始まる。","romaji":"Umi no shokumotsu rensa wa purankuton kara hajimaru.","german":"The marine food chain begins with plankton.","furigana":"うみ|しょくもつれんさ|はじ"},{"japanese":"有害物質は食物連鎖を通じて人間の体にも蓄積される。","romaji":"Yūgai busshitsu wa shokumotsu rensa o tsūjite ningen no karada ni mo chikuseki sareru.","german":"Harmful substances accumulate in the human body through the food chain, too.","furigana":"ゆうがいぶっしつ|しょくもつれんさ|つう|にんげん|からだ|ちくせき"}],"notes":"食物連鎖 is the \"food chain\" in ecology; here 食物 is read しょくもつ, not たべもの. Typical are 食物連鎖の頂点 \"top of the food chain\", 食物連鎖の上位/下位 and 食物連鎖を通じて, for example when pollutants accumulate (生物濃縮). A matter-of-fact technical term from biology and environmental reports.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"目の色は遺伝によって決まる。","romaji":"Me no iro wa iden ni yotte kimaru.","german":"Eye color is determined by heredity.","furigana":"め|いろ|いでん|き"},{"japanese":"父の背の高さが息子に遺伝したようだ。","romaji":"Chichi no se no takasa ga musuko ni iden shita yō da.","german":"It seems the father's height has been passed on to the son.","furigana":"ちち|せ|たか|むすこ|いでん"},{"japanese":"この病気は遺伝することがあるので、家族も検査を受けた。","romaji":"Kono byōki wa iden suru koto ga aru node, kazoku mo kensa o uketa.","german":"Since this disease can be hereditary, the family also had themselves tested.","furigana":"びょうき|いでん|かぞく|けんさ|う"}],"notes":"遺伝 is the biological \"inheritance\" of traits. As an intransitive する-verb, the trait takes が and the recipient takes に: 父の体質が子に遺伝する. Common derivatives are 遺伝子 \"gene\", 遺伝的 \"genetic\" and 隔世遺伝 \"skipping a generation (atavism)\". For inheriting property, one says 相続 instead, and for taking over in general 受け継ぐ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"細胞の核にはDNAが含まれている。","romaji":"Saibō no kaku ni wa DNA ga fukumarete iru.","german":"The cell nucleus contains DNA.","furigana":"さいぼう|かく|ふく"},{"japanese":"彼はチームの核となる選手だ。","romaji":"Kare wa chīmu no kaku to naru senshu da.","german":"He is the player who forms the core of the team.","furigana":"かれ|かく|せんしゅ"},{"japanese":"核兵器の廃絶を訴える集会が開かれた。","romaji":"Kakuheiki no haizetsu o uttaeru shūkai ga hirakareta.","german":"A rally calling for the abolition of nuclear weapons was held.","furigana":"かくへいき|はいぜつ|うった|しゅうかい|ひら"}],"notes":"核 is generally the \"core\": in biology the cell nucleus (細胞の核, 細胞核), in physics the atomic nucleus (原子核). Very often it stands as short for nuclear weapons: 核兵器, 核実験, 核の脅威, 核なき世界; a nuclear power plant, by contrast, is 原子力発電所. Figuratively it denotes the central core of a group or matter, as in チームの核となる選手. For the core of fruit, everyday speech prefers 種 or 芯.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"蛋白質は体の構成要素として不可欠である。","romaji":"Tanpakushitsu wa karada no kōsei yōso toshite fukaketsu de aru.","german":"Proteins are indispensable as building blocks of the body.","furigana":"たんぱくしつ|からだ|こうせいようそ|ふかけつ"},{"japanese":"運動の後は、たんぱく質を多めに摂るといい。","romaji":"Undō no ato wa, tanpakushitsu o ōme ni toru to ii.","german":"After exercise, it is good to take in a bit more protein.","furigana":"うんどう|あと|しつ|おお|と"},{"japanese":"大豆は植物性たんぱく質が豊富だ。","romaji":"Daizu wa shokubutsusei tanpakushitsu ga hōfu da.","german":"Soybeans are rich in plant protein.","furigana":"だいず|しょくぶつせい|しつ|ほうふ"}],"notes":"蛋白質 is \"protein\". The kanji 蛋白 have become rare; today it is mostly written たんぱく質 or タンパク質. Typical are たんぱく質を摂る, 良質なたんぱく質 and 動物性/植物性たんぱく質. Colloquially and in the fitness world, one also says プロテイン, but this often means the powder.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"炭水化物はエネルギー源として重要である。","romaji":"Tansuikabutsu wa enerugī gen toshite jūyō de aru.","german":"Carbohydrates are important as a source of energy.","furigana":"たんすいかぶつ|げん|じゅうよう"},{"japanese":"ダイエット中なので、夜は炭水化物を控えている。","romaji":"Daietto chū na node, yoru wa tansuikabutsu o hikaete iru.","german":"Since I am on a diet, I cut back on carbohydrates in the evening.","furigana":"ちゅう|よる|たんすいかぶつ|ひか"},{"japanese":"ラーメンとチャーハンのセットは炭水化物ばかりだ。","romaji":"Rāmen to chāhan no setto wa tansuikabutsu bakari da.","german":"The ramen and fried rice set is nothing but carbohydrates.","furigana":"たんすいかぶつ"}],"notes":"炭水化物 is the nutrient \"carbohydrate\" (literally \"carbon-water compound\"). In everyday speech it also simply refers to rice, bread and noodles: 炭水化物を控える, 炭水化物抜きダイエット. In diet contexts, 糖質 (\"digestible carbohydrates\") is at least as widespread, e.g. 糖質制限. Matter-of-fact, known from nutrition science and daily life alike.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"脂質の過剰摂取は健康に悪影響を及ぼす。","romaji":"Shishitsu no kajō sesshu wa kenkō ni akueikyō o oyobosu.","german":"Excessive intake of fat has a negative effect on health.","furigana":"ししつ|かじょうせっしゅ|けんこう|あくえいきょう|およ"},{"japanese":"この商品は脂質が少なく、カロリーも控えめだ。","romaji":"Kono shōhin wa shishitsu ga sukunaku, karorī mo hikaeme da.","german":"This product is low in fat and also modest in calories.","furigana":"しょうひん|ししつ|すく|ひか"},{"japanese":"健康診断で脂質異常症と言われた。","romaji":"Kenkō shindan de shishitsu ijōshō to iwareta.","german":"At my health checkup, I was told I have dyslipidemia.","furigana":"けんこうしんだん|ししついじょうしょう|い"}],"notes":"脂質 is the technical term \"lipid, fat\" from nutrition science and biochemistry and appears, for example, in nutrition labels alongside たんぱく質 and 炭水化物. In everyday speech one says 脂肪 (fat in food and body fat), 油 (oil) or 脂 (animal fat) instead. Common in medicine is 脂質異常症 \"dyslipidemia (lipid metabolism disorder)\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"真菌は分解者として生態系に貢献している。","romaji":"Shinkin wa bunkaisha toshite seitaikei ni kōken shite iru.","german":"Fungi contribute to the ecosystem as decomposers.","furigana":"しんきん|ぶんかいしゃ|せいたいけい|こうけん"},{"japanese":"水虫は白癬菌という真菌が原因で起こる。","romaji":"Mizumushi wa hakusenkin to iu shinkin ga gen'in de okoru.","german":"Athlete's foot is caused by a fungus called Trichophyton.","furigana":"みずむし||しんきん|げんいん|お"},{"japanese":"抗生物質は細菌には効くが、真菌には効かない。","romaji":"Kōsei busshitsu wa saikin ni wa kiku ga, shinkin ni wa kikanai.","german":"Antibiotics work against bacteria, but not against fungi.","furigana":"こうせいぶっしつ|さいきん|き|しんきん|き"}],"notes":"真菌 is the biological and medical technical term for \"fungi\" in the sense of the kingdom of organisms, including yeasts and molds. In everyday speech one says きのこ for edible mushrooms and カビ for mold. Important is the distinction from 細菌 \"bacteria\"; 菌 alone usually means germs in general. Medically: 真菌感染症, 抗真菌薬.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地衣類は大気汚染の指標として利用される。","romaji":"Chiirui wa taiki osen no shihyō toshite riyō sareru.","german":"Lichens are used as indicators of air pollution.","furigana":"ちいるい|たいきおせん|しひょう|りよう"},{"japanese":"地衣類は菌類と藻類が共生してできた生物だ。","romaji":"Chiirui wa kinrui to sōrui ga kyōsei shite dekita seibutsu da.","german":"Lichens are organisms formed by a symbiosis of fungi and algae.","furigana":"ちいるい|きんるい|そうるい|きょうせい|せいぶつ"},{"japanese":"岩の表面に地衣類がびっしりと張り付いている。","romaji":"Iwa no hyōmen ni chiirui ga bisshiri to haritsuite iru.","german":"Lichens are clinging densely to the surface of the rock.","furigana":"いわ|ひょうめん|ちいるい|は|つ"}],"notes":"地衣類 are \"lichens\", that is, communities of fungi and algae or cyanobacteria; 類 denotes the group of organisms. In everyday speech lichens are often simply called コケ, although biologically they are not mosses (コケ植物). A technical term from biology and environmental science.","levelNote":"Compound: 地 (N3) + 衣類 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"針葉樹は寒冷地に多く分布している。","romaji":"Shin'yōju wa kanreichi ni ōku bunpu shite iru.","german":"Conifers are distributed mainly in cold regions.","furigana":"しんようじゅ|かんれいち|おお|ぶんぷ"},{"japanese":"スギやヒノキなどの針葉樹は建築材として使われる。","romaji":"Sugi ya hinoki nado no shin'yōju wa kenchikuzai toshite tsukawareru.","german":"Conifers such as sugi and hinoki are used as building material.","furigana":"しんようじゅ|けんちくざい|つか"},{"japanese":"北海道には広大な針葉樹林が広がっている。","romaji":"Hokkaidō ni wa kōdai na shin'yōjurin ga hirogatte iru.","german":"Vast coniferous forests spread across Hokkaidō.","furigana":"ほっかいどう|こうだい|しんようじゅりん|ひろ"}],"notes":"針葉樹 literally means \"needle-leaf tree\", that is, a conifer such as 杉, 檜 or 松; the forest is called 針葉樹林. The counterpart is 広葉樹 \"broadleaf tree\". The classification by leaf shape is independent of 常緑樹/落葉樹: most conifers are evergreen, but the larch (カラマツ) sheds its needles. Matter-of-fact, from biology and forestry.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"広葉樹の森は生物多様性が高い。","romaji":"Kōyōju no mori wa seibutsu tayōsei ga takai.","german":"Laubwälder haben eine hohe Biodiversität.","furigana":"こうようじゅ|もり|せいぶつたようせい|たか"},{"japanese":"家具には、ナラやケヤキといった硬い広葉樹がよく使われる。","romaji":"Kagu ni wa, nara ya keyaki to itta katai kōyōju ga yoku tsukawareru.","german":"Für Möbel verwendet man häufig harte Laubhölzer wie Eiche und Zelkove.","furigana":"かぐ|かた|こうようじゅ|つか"},{"japanese":"この山は秋になると広葉樹が色づいて美しい。","romaji":"Kono yama wa aki ni naru to kōyōju ga irozuite utsukushii.","german":"Im Herbst färben sich die Laubbäume auf diesem Berg wunderschön.","furigana":"やま|あき|こうようじゅ|いろ|うつく"}],"notes":"広葉樹 heißt wörtlich „Breit-Blatt-Baum“, also „Laubbaum“ mit flachen, breiten Blättern; Gegenstück ist 針葉樹. Anders als das deutsche „Laubbaum“ umfasst es auch immergrüne Arten: 常緑広葉樹 (z. B. クスノキ, ツバキ) neben 落葉広葉樹 (z. B. ブナ, ケヤキ). Als Holz heißen sie auch 広葉樹材 „Laubholz“. Sachlich, aus Biologie und Forstwirtschaft.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"落葉樹は秋になると葉を落とす。","romaji":"Rakuyōju wa aki ni naru to ha o otosu.","german":"Deciduous trees shed their leaves in autumn.","furigana":"らくようじゅ|あき|は|お"},{"japanese":"落葉樹を庭に植えると、夏は日陰になり冬は日が当たる。","romaji":"Rakuyōju o niwa ni ueru to, natsu wa hikage ni nari fuyu wa hi ga ataru.","german":"If you plant a deciduous tree in the garden, it provides shade in summer and lets the sun through in winter.","furigana":"らくようじゅ|にわ|う|なつ|ひかげ|ふゆ|ひ|あ"},{"japanese":"カラマツは針葉樹だが、珍しく落葉樹でもある。","romaji":"Karamatsu wa shin'yōju da ga, mezurashiku rakuyōju demo aru.","german":"The larch is a conifer, but unusually it is also deciduous.","furigana":"しんようじゅ|めずら|らくようじゅ"}],"notes":"落葉樹 is a \"deciduous tree\"; here 落葉 is read らくよう, not おちば. The counterpart is 常緑樹 \"evergreen tree\". This classification concerns leaf shedding and is independent of 針葉樹/広葉樹 - hence expressions like 落葉広葉樹林 \"deciduous broadleaf forest\". Matter-of-fact, from biology and horticulture.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"常緑樹は一年中緑の葉を保つ。","romaji":"Jōryokuju wa ichinenjū midori no ha o tamotsu.","german":"Evergreen trees keep green leaves all year round.","furigana":"じょうりょくじゅ|いちねんじゅうみどり|は|たも"},{"japanese":"生け垣には一年中目隠しになる常緑樹が向いている。","romaji":"Ikegaki ni wa ichinenjū mekakushi ni naru jōryokuju ga muite iru.","german":"Evergreen trees, which provide privacy all year round, are suitable for hedges.","furigana":"い|がき|いちねんじゅうめかく|じょうりょくじゅ|む"},{"japanese":"神社の森には、クスノキなどの常緑樹が多い。","romaji":"Jinja no mori ni wa, kusunoki nado no jōryokuju ga ōi.","german":"There are many evergreen trees, such as camphor trees, in shrine forests.","furigana":"じんじゃ|もり|じょうりょくじゅ|おお"}],"notes":"常緑樹 is an \"evergreen tree\" (常 \"always\", 緑 \"green\") that bears leaves all year, such as 松, 杉, クスノキ or ツバキ. The counterpart is 落葉樹. There are evergreen conifers and broadleaf trees (常緑広葉樹). In horticulture it is often recommended for hedges and privacy screens. Matter-of-fact.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"灌木が生い茂る荒れ地を開墾した。","romaji":"Kanboku ga oishigeru arechi o kaikon shita.","german":"They cleared wasteland overgrown with shrubs for cultivation.","furigana":"かんぼく||しげ|あ|ち|かいこん"},{"japanese":"道の両側には背の低い灌木が続いていた。","romaji":"Michi no ryōgawa ni wa se no hikui kanboku ga tsuzuite ita.","german":"Low shrubs continued on both sides of the road.","furigana":"みち|りょうがわ|せ|ひく|かんぼく|つづ"},{"japanese":"ウサギは灌木の茂みに素早く身を隠した。","romaji":"Usagi wa kanboku no shigemi ni subayaku mi o kakushita.","german":"The rabbit quickly hid in a thicket of shrubs.","furigana":"かんぼく|しげ|すばや|み|かく"}],"notes":"灌木 is a \"shrub, bush\", a low woody plant without a distinct main trunk. Since 灌 is not a jōyō kanji, it is also written かん木; in modern botany the usual word is 低木 (opposite 高木). Typical are 灌木林 \"scrubland\" and 灌木の茂み \"thicket\". Rather literary; in everyday speech one says 茂み or 低い木.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"草本植物は木本植物と異なり木質化しない。","romaji":"Sōhon shokubutsu wa mokuhon shokubutsu to kotonari mokushitsuka shinai.","german":"Herbaceous plants, unlike woody plants, do not become woody.","furigana":"そうほんしょくぶつ|もくほんしょくぶつ|こと|もくしつか"},{"japanese":"この湿原には多様な草本が生育している。","romaji":"Kono shitsugen ni wa tayō na sōhon ga seiiku shite iru.","german":"A variety of herbaceous plants grow in this marsh.","furigana":"しつげん|たよう|そうほん|せいいく"},{"japanese":"竹は木のように見えるが、草本に分類されることもある。","romaji":"Take wa ki no yō ni mieru ga, sōhon ni bunrui sareru koto mo aru.","german":"Bamboo looks like a tree, but is sometimes classified as herbaceous.","furigana":"たけ|き|み|そうほん|ぶんるい"}],"notes":"草本 is the botanical term for \"herbaceous plants\" whose stems do not become woody; the opposite is 木本 \"woody plant\". It mostly appears in 草本植物 or in 多年生草本 \"perennial herb\". In everyday speech one simply says 草. A purely technical term from botany and plant science.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"チューリップの球根を秋に植えた。","romaji":"Chūrippu no kyūkon o aki ni ueta.","german":"I planted tulip bulbs in autumn.","furigana":"きゅうこん|あき|う"},{"japanese":"花が終わったら、球根を掘り上げて乾燥させておく。","romaji":"Hana ga owattara, kyūkon o horiagete kansō sasete oku.","german":"After the flowers are finished, dig up the bulbs and let them dry.","furigana":"はな|お|きゅうこん|ほ|あ|かんそう"},{"japanese":"水栽培でヒヤシンスの球根を育てている。","romaji":"Mizusaibai de hiyashinsu no kyūkon o sodatete iru.","german":"I am growing a hyacinth bulb in water culture.","furigana":"みずさいばい|きゅうこん|そだ"}],"notes":"球根 is the \"flower bulb\" or \"tuber\" as a storage organ of plants such as tulip, hyacinth or dahlia; one says 球根を植える, 球根を掘り上げる, 球根植物. Note: the kitchen onion is 玉ねぎ, not 球根. Neutral, used above all in horticulture.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"種子の発芽には水分と適切な温度が必要だ。","romaji":"Shushi no hatsuga ni wa suibun to tekisetsu na ondo ga hitsuyō da.","german":"Moisture and a suitable temperature are necessary for seeds to germinate.","furigana":"しゅし|はつが|すいぶん|てきせつ|おんど|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"タンポポの種子は風に乗って遠くまで運ばれる。","romaji":"Tanpopo no shushi wa kaze ni notte tōku made hakobareru.","german":"Dandelion seeds are carried far by the wind.","furigana":"しゅし|かぜ|の|とお|はこ"},{"japanese":"この施設では世界中の作物の種子が保存されている。","romaji":"Kono shisetsu de wa sekaijū no sakumotsu no shushi ga hozon sarete iru.","german":"Seeds of crops from all over the world are preserved at this facility.","furigana":"しせつ|せかいじゅう|さくもつ|しゅし|ほぞん"}],"notes":"種子 is the technical word for \"seed\"; in everyday speech one says 種 (たね). It appears in biology, agriculture and specialist texts: 種子の発芽, 種子を散布する, 種子植物 \"seed plant\". Note: in place names such as 種子島 it is read たね.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"春になると花粉症に悩まされる人が多い。","romaji":"Haru ni naru to kafunshō ni nayamasareru hito ga ōi.","german":"Many people suffer from hay fever in spring.","furigana":"はる|かふんしょう|なや|ひと|おお"},{"japanese":"今日は花粉が多く飛んでいるので、マスクをしたほうがいい。","romaji":"Kyō wa kafun ga ōku tonde iru node, masuku o shita hō ga ii.","german":"There is a lot of pollen in the air today, so you should wear a mask.","furigana":"きょう|かふん|おお|と"},{"japanese":"ミツバチは花から花へ花粉を運ぶ。","romaji":"Mitsubachi wa hana kara hana e kafun o hakobu.","german":"Honeybees carry pollen from flower to flower.","furigana":"はな|はな|かふん|はこ"}],"notes":"花粉 is \"pollen\". In everyday life it appears mainly in 花粉症 \"hay fever\", which is very widespread in Japan because of cedar pollen (スギ花粉). Typical phrases: 花粉が飛ぶ, 花粉の飛散量, 花粉を運ぶ (of insects). Neutral.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"蜜蜂は受粉において重要な役割を担っている。","romaji":"Mitsubachi wa jufun ni oite jūyō na yakuwari o ninatte iru.","german":"Honeybees play an important role in pollination.","furigana":"みつばち|じゅふん|じゅうよう|やくわり|にな"},{"japanese":"ハウス栽培のイチゴは、人の手で受粉させることもある。","romaji":"Hausu saibai no ichigo wa, hito no te de jufun saseru koto mo aru.","german":"Greenhouse strawberries are sometimes pollinated by hand.","furigana":"さいばい|ひと|て|じゅふん"},{"japanese":"受粉しなかった花は実をつけずに落ちてしまう。","romaji":"Jufun shinakatta hana wa mi o tsukezu ni ochite shimau.","german":"Flowers that are not pollinated drop off without bearing fruit.","furigana":"じゅふん|はな|み|お"}],"notes":"受粉 is \"pollination\"; as an intransitive する-verb it is 花が受粉する. Someone who pollinates actively uses the causative 受粉させる, for example in 人工受粉 \"artificial pollination\"; there is also 自家受粉 \"self-pollination\". Do not confuse it with 受精 \"fertilization\", which occurs only after pollination. Matter-of-fact.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"種を蒔いてから一週間で発芽が確認された。","romaji":"Tane o maite kara isshūkan de hatsuga ga kakunin sareta.","german":"Germination was confirmed one week after the seeds were sown.","furigana":"たね|ま|いっしゅうかん|はつが|かくにん"},{"japanese":"この種は気温が二十度以上にならないと発芽しない。","romaji":"Kono tane wa kion ga nijūdo ijō ni naranai to hatsuga shinai.","german":"These seeds do not germinate unless the temperature reaches twenty degrees or more.","furigana":"たね|きおん|にじゅうどいじょう|はつが"},{"japanese":"発芽玄米は普通の玄米より消化がいいと言われる。","romaji":"Hatsuga genmai wa futsū no genmai yori shōka ga ii to iwareru.","german":"Germinated brown rice is said to be easier to digest than ordinary brown rice.","furigana":"はつがげんまい|ふつう|げんまい|しょうか|い"}],"notes":"発芽 is \"germination\"; as an intransitive する-verb: 種が発芽する. Typical are 発芽率 \"germination rate\", 発芽条件 and 発芽玄米 \"germinated brown rice\". In everyday speech one mostly says 芽が出る; 発芽 sounds factual and scientific.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"桜の開花時期は年々早まっている。","romaji":"Sakura no kaika jiki wa nennen hayamatte iru.","german":"The flowering time of cherry blossoms is getting earlier every year.","furigana":"さくら|かいかじき|ねんねんはや"},{"japanese":"気象台が東京の桜の開花を発表した。","romaji":"Kishōdai ga Tōkyō no sakura no kaika o happyō shita.","german":"The Meteorological Agency announced the blooming of the cherry blossoms in Tokyo.","furigana":"きしょうだい|とうきょう|さくら|かいか|はっぴょう"},{"japanese":"彼女の才能は留学をきっかけに一気に開花した。","romaji":"Kanojo no sainō wa ryūgaku o kikkake ni ikki ni kaika shita.","german":"Her talent blossomed all at once after she studied abroad.","furigana":"かのじょ|さいのう|りゅうがく|いっき|かいか"}],"notes":"開花 is the \"blooming\" of flowers; as an intransitive する-verb: 桜が開花する. Very well known are 開花宣言 (the official announcement of the start of cherry blossom season) and 開花予想; 満開, by contrast, denotes full bloom. Figuratively it means \"to come into full development\": 才能が開花する, 文化が開花する. In everyday speech, for flowers one prefers 咲く.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"受粉が成功すると結実に至る。","romaji":"Jufun ga seikō suru to ketsujitsu ni itaru.","german":"When pollination is successful, fruit sets.","furigana":"じゅふん|せいこう|けつじつ|いた"},{"japanese":"長年の研究がついに新薬の開発として結実した。","romaji":"Naganen no kenkyū ga tsui ni shin'yaku no kaihatsu toshite ketsujitsu shita.","german":"Years of research finally bore fruit in the development of a new drug.","furigana":"ながねん|けんきゅう|しんやく|かいはつ|けつじつ"},{"japanese":"選手たちの努力が優勝という形で結実した。","romaji":"Senshutachi no doryoku ga yūshō to iu katachi de ketsujitsu shita.","german":"The players' efforts bore fruit in the form of a championship.","furigana":"せんしゅ|どりょく|ゆうしょう|かたち|けつじつ"}],"notes":"結実 means \"to set fruit\" in botany; as an intransitive する-verb: 〜が結実する. At least as common is the figurative meaning \"to bear fruit, to pay off\": 努力が結実する, 長年の研究が〜として結実する. In conversation one says 実を結ぶ instead, which uses the same image. Literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"秋の落葉は美しい風景を作り出す。","romaji":"Aki no rakuyō wa utsukushii fūkei o tsukuridasu.","german":"The fall of leaves in autumn creates beautiful scenery.","furigana":"あき|らくよう|うつく|ふうけい|つく|だ"},{"japanese":"イチョウは秋に黄葉した後、落葉する。","romaji":"Ichō wa aki ni kōyō shita ato, rakuyō suru.","german":"The ginkgo turns yellow in autumn and then sheds its leaves.","furigana":"あき|こうよう|あと|らくよう"},{"japanese":"日照りが続くと、夏でも落葉する木がある。","romaji":"Hideri ga tsuzuku to, natsu demo rakuyō suru ki ga aru.","german":"If drought continues, some trees shed their leaves even in summer.","furigana":"ひで|つづ|なつ|らくよう|き"}],"notes":"落葉 in the reading らくよう denotes \"leaf fall, shedding of leaves\" as a process and as an intransitive する-verb: 木が落葉する. For the fallen leaves themselves, everyday speech mostly uses 落ち葉 (おちば); らくよう sounds factual to technical. Attributively it appears in 落葉樹 \"deciduous tree\" and 落葉広葉樹.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"干ばつにより多くの樹木が枯死した。","romaji":"Kanbatsu ni yori ōku no jumoku ga koshi shita.","german":"Many trees died because of the drought.","furigana":"かん|おお|じゅもく|こし"},{"japanese":"害虫の被害で、山の松が次々と枯死している。","romaji":"Gaichū no higai de, yama no matsu ga tsugitsugi to koshi shite iru.","german":"The pines on the mountain are dying off one after another because of pest damage.","furigana":"がいちゅう|ひがい|やま|まつ|つぎつぎ|こし"},{"japanese":"根腐れを起こした植木は、そのまま枯死してしまった。","romaji":"Negusare o okoshita ueki wa, sono mama koshi shite shimatta.","german":"The potted plant with root rot died just like that.","furigana":"|お|うえき|こし"}],"notes":"枯死 is the matter-of-fact word for the \"dying off\" of plants, especially trees; as an intransitive する-verb: 木が枯死する. It appears in reports on drought, pests or environmental damage, e.g. 松枯れによる枯死. In everyday speech one simply says 枯れる; 枯死 is not used for people and animals. Literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この地域には貴重な植物群落が残っている。","romaji":"Kono chiiki ni wa kichō na shokubutsu gunraku ga nokotte iru.","german":"Valuable plant communities remain in this region.","furigana":"ちいき|きちょう|しょくぶつぐんらく|のこ"},{"japanese":"湿原にはミズバショウの大群落が広がっている。","romaji":"Shitsugen ni wa mizubashō no daigunraku ga hirogatte iru.","german":"A large colony of skunk cabbage spreads across the marsh.","furigana":"しつげん|だいぐんらく|ひろ"},{"japanese":"高山植物の群落を守るため、登山道の外への立ち入りは禁止されている。","romaji":"Kōzan shokubutsu no gunraku o mamoru tame, tozandō no soto e no tachiiri wa kinshi sarete iru.","german":"To protect the alpine plant communities, entering off the hiking trails is prohibited.","furigana":"こうざんしょくぶつ|ぐんらく|まも|とざんどう|そと|た|い|きんし"}],"notes":"群落 is, in botany and ecology, a \"plant community\" or a dense \"stand\" of one species at a site: 植物群落, ミズバショウの群落, 高山植物の群落. Related is 群生 \"to grow in groups\" (群生する). The older meaning \"a group of villages\" is rare today. Technical, frequent in nature conservation texts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"標高によって植生が大きく変化する。","romaji":"Hyōkō ni yotte shokusei ga ōkiku henka suru.","german":"Vegetation changes greatly depending on altitude.","furigana":"ひょうこう|しょくせい|おお|へんか"},{"japanese":"山火事の後、植生が回復するまでには何十年もかかる。","romaji":"Yamakaji no ato, shokusei ga kaifuku suru made ni wa nanjūnen mo kakaru.","german":"After a forest fire, it takes decades for the vegetation to recover.","furigana":"やまかじ|あと|しょくせい|かいふく|なんじゅうねん"},{"japanese":"この島は独特の植生で知られている。","romaji":"Kono shima wa dokutoku no shokusei de shirarete iru.","german":"This island is known for its unique vegetation.","furigana":"しま|どくとく|しょくせい|し"}],"notes":"植生 is the \"vegetation\" of an area as a whole, that is, which plants grow there and how they are distributed. Typical: 植生が豊か, 植生が変化する, 植生の回復, 植生図 \"vegetation map\". Unlike 植物 (individual plants or plants in general), 植生 describes the plant cover of a landscape. Technical, in geography and ecology.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"海岸沿いの樹林が防風林として機能している。","romaji":"Kaigan zoi no jurin ga bōfūrin toshite kinō shite iru.","german":"The stand of trees along the coast functions as a windbreak forest.","furigana":"かいがんぞ|じゅりん|ぼうふうりん|きのう"},{"japanese":"樹林帯を抜けると、急に視界が開けた。","romaji":"Jurintai o nukeru to, kyū ni shikai ga hiraketa.","german":"After passing through the belt of woods, the view suddenly opened up.","furigana":"じゅりんたい|ぬ|きゅう|しかい|ひら"},{"japanese":"この公園には広い樹林があり、野鳥観察に適している。","romaji":"Kono kōen ni wa hiroi jurin ga ari, yachō kansatsu ni tekishite iru.","german":"This park has a large woodland and is well suited to bird-watching.","furigana":"こうえん|ひろ|じゅりん|やちょうかんさつ|てき"}],"notes":"樹林 is a matter-of-fact, rather technical term for a stand of trees or a piece of woodland, as found in ecology, urban planning or hiking guides; in everyday speech one simply says 林 or 森. It is very common in compounds such as 針葉樹林 \"coniferous forest\", 広葉樹林 \"broadleaf forest\" or 樹林帯 \"forest belt\" (in mountain descriptions). The related 森林 denotes forest rather as an area or a resource (森林破壊 \"deforestation\"), while 樹林 emphasizes the stock of trees as a vegetation type.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この鳥は春に繁殖期を迎える。","romaji":"Kono tori wa haru ni hanshokuki o mukaeru.","german":"This bird enters its breeding season in spring.","furigana":"とり|はる|はんしょくき|むか"},{"japanese":"梅雨の時期は湿気が多く、カビが繁殖しやすい。","romaji":"Tsuyu no jiki wa shikke ga ōku, kabi ga hanshoku shiyasui.","german":"The rainy season is very humid, so mold spreads easily.","furigana":"つゆ|じき|しっけ|おお|はんしょく"},{"japanese":"動物園ではパンダの繁殖に成功した。","romaji":"Dōbutsuen de wa panda no hanshoku ni seikō shita.","german":"The zoo succeeded in breeding pandas.","furigana":"どうぶつえん|はんしょく|せいこう"}],"notes":"繁殖 is the multiplication or reproduction of animals, plants and microorganisms; as an intransitive する-verb: カビが繁殖する \"mold spreads\", 細菌が繁殖する. Typical compounds are 繁殖期 \"breeding season, mating season\", 繁殖力 \"reproductive capacity\" and 人工繁殖 \"captive breeding\". It is not used for humans. Distinguish 増殖, which describes rather the multiplication of cells or viruses (細胞が増殖する), and 生殖, reproduction as a biological function (生殖器).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"フクロウは夜間に小動物を捕食する。","romaji":"Fukurō wa yakan ni shōdōbutsu o hoshoku suru.","german":"Owls prey on small animals at night.","furigana":"やかん|しょうどうぶつ|ほしょく"},{"japanese":"生態系では、捕食者と被食者のバランスが保たれている。","romaji":"Seitaikei de wa, hoshokusha to hishokusha no baransu ga tamotarete iru.","german":"In an ecosystem, the balance between predators and prey is maintained.","furigana":"せいたいけい|ほしょくしゃ|ひしょくしゃ|たも"},{"japanese":"カマキリは自分より大きな虫を捕食することもある。","romaji":"Kamakiri wa jibun yori ōki na mushi o hoshoku suru koto mo aru.","german":"Praying mantises sometimes prey even on insects larger than themselves.","furigana":"じぶん|おお|むし|ほしょく"}],"notes":"捕食 means that an animal catches and eats another as prey; as a transitive する-verb: 獲物を捕食する. The word belongs to biology and nature documentaries; in everyday speech one says 食べる, 襲う or 狩る instead. Important derivatives are 捕食者 \"predator\" and its counterpart 被食者 \"prey animal\". Do not confuse it with 補食 \"supplementary meal, snack\", which is read the same.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"寄生虫は宿主から栄養を奪う。","romaji":"Kiseichū wa shukushu kara eiyō o ubau.","german":"Parasites take nutrients from their host.","furigana":"きせいちゅう|しゅくしゅ|えいよう|うば"},{"japanese":"この菌は他の植物の根に寄生して育つ。","romaji":"Kono kin wa hoka no shokubutsu no ne ni kisei shite sodatsu.","german":"This fungus grows by parasitizing the roots of other plants.","furigana":"きん|ほか|しょくぶつ|ね|きせい|そだ"},{"japanese":"彼は働かずに親に寄生している。","romaji":"Kare wa hatarakazu ni oya ni kisei shite iru.","german":"He doesn't work and lives off his parents.","furigana":"かれ|はたら|おや|きせい"}],"notes":"寄生 denotes the life of one organism at the expense of another; as an intransitive する-verb, the host takes に: 腸に寄生する \"to parasitize the intestine\". Common derivatives are 寄生虫 \"parasite\" and 寄生植物. Figuratively and pejoratively, 寄生する also describes people who live off others (親に寄生する \"to sponge off one's parents\"); the host is called 宿主.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"昆虫の擬態は進化の驚くべき産物である。","romaji":"Konchū no gitai wa shinka no odoroku beki sanbutsu de aru.","german":"Insect mimicry is an astonishing product of evolution.","furigana":"こんちゅう|ぎたい|しんか|おどろ|さんぶつ"},{"japanese":"ナナフシは木の枝に擬態して敵から身を守る。","romaji":"Nanafushi wa ki no eda ni gitai shite teki kara mi o mamoru.","german":"Stick insects mimic twigs to protect themselves from enemies.","furigana":"き|えだ|ぎたい|てき|み|まも"},{"japanese":"「キラキラ」や「ドキドキ」は擬態語と呼ばれる。","romaji":"\"Kirakira\" ya \"dokidoki\" wa gitaigo to yobareru.","german":"Words like 「キラキラ」 and 「ドキドキ」 are called mimetic words (gitaigo).","furigana":"ぎたいご|よ"}],"notes":"擬態 is the mimicry or camouflage of living things that imitate something else; as an intransitive する-verb, the model takes に: 木の枝に擬態する \"to mimic a twig\". The word is biological and technical; in everyday speech one says 隠れる or 化ける. Also well known is 擬態語, the mimetic words describing states, such as キラキラ, in contrast to 擬音語 or 擬声語, which imitate actual sounds.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"熊は冬眠中にほとんどエネルギーを消費しない。","romaji":"Kuma wa tōmin chū ni hotondo enerugī o shōhi shinai.","german":"Bears use almost no energy during hibernation.","furigana":"くま|とうみんちゅう|しょうひ"},{"japanese":"春になると、クマが冬眠から目覚める。","romaji":"Haru ni naru to, kuma ga tōmin kara mezameru.","german":"In spring, the bears wake up from hibernation.","furigana":"はる|とうみん|めざ"},{"japanese":"その計画は予算不足で冬眠状態になっている。","romaji":"Sono keikaku wa yosan busoku de tōmin jōtai ni natte iru.","german":"The plan is in a dormant state due to a lack of budget.","furigana":"けいかく|よさんぶそく|とうみんじょうたい"}],"notes":"冬眠 is the hibernation of animals; as an intransitive する-verb: クマが冬眠する. Typical combinations are 冬眠中, 冬眠に入る and 冬眠から覚める or 目覚める. Figuratively, one speaks of 冬眠状態 when a project or company is temporarily idle. Distinguish 休眠 \"dormancy\" (seeds, 休眠口座 \"dormant account\") and the more everyday 冬ごもり \"staying holed up in winter\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"渡り鳥は季節に応じて長距離を移動する。","romaji":"Wataridori wa kisetsu ni ōjite chōkyori o idō suru.","german":"Migratory birds travel long distances according to the season.","furigana":"わた|どり|きせつ|おう|ちょうきょり|いどう"},{"japanese":"ツバメは秋になると南へ渡りをする。","romaji":"Tsubame wa aki ni naru to minami e watari o suru.","german":"Swallows migrate south in autumn.","furigana":"あき|みなみ|わた"},{"japanese":"雨が降り出したところに友人の車が通りかかり、まさに渡りに船だった。","romaji":"Ame ga furidashita tokoro ni yūjin no kuruma ga tōrikakari, masa ni watari ni fune datta.","german":"Just as it started to rain, a friend's car happened by - it was exactly what I needed.","furigana":"あめ|ふ|だ|ゆうじん|くるま|とお|わた|ふね"}],"notes":"渡り is the nominalization of 渡る \"to cross over\"; in nature it denotes above all bird migration: 渡りをする, 渡りの季節, 渡り鳥 \"migratory bird\" (figuratively also someone who constantly changes jobs). It also stands for \"crossing, connection\", as in 渡り廊下 \"covered connecting corridor\". Set phrases are 渡りに船 \"exactly what one needs\" and 渡りをつける \"to establish contact\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"都市開発により野生動物の生息地が失われている。","romaji":"Toshi kaihatsu ni yori yasei dōbutsu no seisokuchi ga ushinawarete iru.","german":"Habitats of wild animals are being lost due to urban development.","furigana":"としかいはつ|やせいどうぶつ|せいそくち|うしな"},{"japanese":"この湿地はタンチョウの生息地として知られている。","romaji":"Kono shitchi wa tanchō no seisokuchi toshite shirarete iru.","german":"This wetland is known as a habitat of the red-crowned crane.","furigana":"しっち|せいそくち|し"},{"japanese":"パンダの生息地は中国の山岳地帯に限られている。","romaji":"Panda no seisokuchi wa Chūgoku no sangaku chitai ni kagirarete iru.","german":"The habitat of pandas is limited to mountainous areas of China.","furigana":"せいそくち|ちゅうごく|さんがくちたい|かぎ"}],"notes":"生息地 is the habitat of an animal species; the underlying 生息する \"to live (in the wild), to occur\" is used only for animals and plants (この川にはサケが生息している). Typical combinations are 生息地を守る, 生息地が失われる and 生息地の破壊. For humans one says 居住地, and for the origin of plants 原産地. The variant 棲息地 with the character 棲 is rarer.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"生物多様性の保全は地球規模の課題である。","romaji":"Seibutsu tayōsei no hozen wa chikyū kibo no kadai de aru.","german":"Conserving biodiversity is a global challenge.","furigana":"せいぶつたようせい|ほぜん|ちきゅうきぼ|かだい"},{"japanese":"外来種の増加は地域の生物多様性を脅かしている。","romaji":"Gairaishu no zōka wa chiiki no seibutsu tayōsei o obiyakashite iru.","german":"The increase of invasive species is threatening local biodiversity.","furigana":"がいらいしゅ|ぞうか|ちいき|せいぶつたようせい|おびや"},{"japanese":"サンゴ礁は生物多様性が非常に豊かな場所だ。","romaji":"Sangoshō wa seibutsu tayōsei ga hijō ni yutaka na basho da.","german":"Coral reefs are places with extremely rich biodiversity.","furigana":"しょう|せいぶつたようせい|ひじょう|ゆた|ばしょ"}],"notes":"生物多様性 is the technical term for biodiversity and appears mainly in environmental policy, science and the news. Typical combinations are 生物多様性の保全 \"conservation of biodiversity\", 生物多様性が失われる and 生物多様性が豊か or 高い. Also well known is the 生物多様性条約 \"Convention on Biological Diversity\". It can also be shortened to 多様性 when the context is clear.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新陳代謝が活発な生物ほど寿命が短い傾向がある。","romaji":"Shinchin taisha ga kappatsu na seibutsu hodo jumyō ga mijikai keikō ga aru.","german":"Organisms with an active metabolism tend to have shorter lifespans.","furigana":"しんちんたいしゃ|かっぱつ|せいぶつ|じゅみょう|みじか|けいこう"},{"japanese":"運動をすると新陳代謝が良くなり、肌の調子も整う。","romaji":"Undō o suru to shinchin taisha ga yoku nari, hada no chōshi mo totonou.","german":"Exercise boosts your metabolism and also improves the condition of your skin.","furigana":"うんどう|しんちんたいしゃ|よ|はだ|ちょうし|ととの"},{"japanese":"若手を積極的に登用し、組織の新陳代謝を図る。","romaji":"Wakate o sekkyokuteki ni tōyō shi, soshiki no shinchin taisha o hakaru.","german":"By actively promoting young people, we aim to renew the organization.","furigana":"わかて|せっきょくてき|とうよう|そしき|しんちんたいしゃ|はか"}],"notes":"新陳代謝 literally means \"replacing the old with the new\" and in everyday speech stands mainly for the body's metabolism: 新陳代謝がいい・活発だ・悪い, 新陳代謝を高める. In technical texts one usually says just 代謝 (基礎代謝 \"basal metabolic rate\"). As a four-character compound it also denotes the renewal of organizations, for example 組織の新陳代謝を図る \"to bring fresh blood into the company\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"樹皮の模様は樹種の識別に役立つ。","romaji":"Juhi no moyō wa jushu no shikibetsu ni yakudatsu.","german":"The pattern of the bark helps in identifying the tree species.","furigana":"じゅひ|もよう|じゅしゅ|しきべつ|やくだ"},{"japanese":"シナモンはある種の木の樹皮から作られる香辛料だ。","romaji":"Shinamon wa aru shu no ki no juhi kara tsukurareru kōshinryō da.","german":"Cinnamon is a spice made from the bark of a certain kind of tree.","furigana":"しゅ|き|じゅひ|つく|こうしんりょう"},{"japanese":"シカが冬に樹皮を食べてしまい、木が枯れる被害が出ている。","romaji":"Shika ga fuyu ni juhi o tabete shimai, ki ga kareru higai ga dete iru.","german":"Deer eat the bark in winter, and trees are dying as a result.","furigana":"ふゆ|じゅひ|た|き|か|ひがい|で"}],"notes":"樹皮 is tree bark in a technical or written sense (botany, forestry, spices, medicinal remedies); in everyday speech one says 木の皮. Typical combinations are 樹皮を剥ぐ \"to peel off the bark\" and 樹皮から作られる. Do not confuse it with 樹液 \"tree sap\" or 樹冠 \"tree crown\", which contain the same character 樹.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"年輪を数えることで樹木の樹齢が分かる。","romaji":"Nenrin o kazoeru koto de jumoku no jurei ga wakaru.","german":"By counting the annual rings, one can tell the age of a tree.","furigana":"ねんりん|かぞ|じゅもく|じゅれい|わ"},{"japanese":"祖父の顔には長い人生の年輪が刻まれている。","romaji":"Sofu no kao ni wa nagai jinsei no nenrin ga kizamarete iru.","german":"The rings of a long life are carved into my grandfather's face.","furigana":"そふ|かお|なが|じんせい|ねんりん|きざ"},{"japanese":"年輪を重ねたベテラン俳優ならではの深い演技だった。","romaji":"Nenrin o kasaneta beteran haiyū nara de wa no fukai engi datta.","german":"It was a profound performance, of a kind only a veteran actor with years behind him can give.","furigana":"ねんりん|かさ|はいゆう|ふか|えんぎ"}],"notes":"年輪 are the annual rings of a tree: 年輪を数える, 年輪が刻まれる. Figuratively it stands for life experience gathered over the years, especially in 年輪を重ねる \"to mature with years and experience\" or 年輪を感じさせる \"to bear witness to long experience\". This figurative use is appreciative and elevated in style.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"竹は根茎で地下に広がって繁殖する。","romaji":"Take wa konkei de chika ni hirogatte hanshoku suru.","german":"Bamboo spreads and multiplies underground by rhizomes.","furigana":"たけ|こんけい|ちか|ひろ|はんしょく"},{"japanese":"ショウガの食用部分は根ではなく根茎である。","romaji":"Shōga no shokuyō bubun wa ne de wa naku konkei de aru.","german":"The edible part of ginger is not a root but a rhizome.","furigana":"しょくようぶぶん|ね|こんけい"},{"japanese":"雑草は根茎が残っていると、何度抜いてもまた生えてくる。","romaji":"Zassō wa konkei ga nokotte iru to, nando nuite mo mata haete kuru.","german":"If the rhizomes remain, weeds keep growing back no matter how many times you pull them out.","furigana":"ざっそう|こんけい|のこ|なんどぬ|は"}],"notes":"根茎 is the botanical technical term for a rhizome, that is, a shoot growing underground that looks like a root. In more general usage one mostly says 地下茎 \"underground stem\". Well-known examples are ginger, bamboo and the lotus root レンコン, which botanically is not a true root. In herbal medicine, 根茎 often turns up among medicinal drugs.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"カブトムシは樹液に集まる習性がある。","romaji":"Kabutomushi wa jueki ni atsumaru shūsei ga aru.","german":"Rhinoceros beetles have a habit of gathering at tree sap.","furigana":"じゅえき|あつ|しゅうせい"},{"japanese":"メープルシロップはカエデの樹液を煮詰めて作る。","romaji":"Mēpuru shiroppu wa kaede no jueki o nitsumete tsukuru.","german":"Maple syrup is made by boiling down the sap of maple trees.","furigana":"じゅえき|につ|つく"},{"japanese":"天然ゴムはゴムの木の樹液から作られる。","romaji":"Tennen gomu wa gomu no ki no jueki kara tsukurareru.","german":"Natural rubber is made from the sap of the rubber tree.","furigana":"てんねん|き|じゅえき|つく"}],"notes":"樹液 is the sap that oozes out of or flows within trees. Typical are 樹液が出る・にじむ, 樹液を吸う (of insects) and 樹液に集まる; in Japan it immediately brings to mind beetles gathering in summer at oozing oaks. Maple syrup and natural rubber are also obtained from 樹液. The juice of fruit, by contrast, is 果汁.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"葉緑素は光のエネルギーを吸収する色素である。","romaji":"Yōryokuso wa hikari no enerugī o kyūshū suru shikiso de aru.","german":"Chlorophyll is a pigment that absorbs light energy.","furigana":"ようりょくそ|ひかり|きゅうしゅう|しきそ"},{"japanese":"秋になると葉緑素が分解され、葉が赤や黄色に変わる。","romaji":"Aki ni naru to yōryokuso ga bunkai sare, ha ga aka ya kiiro ni kawaru.","german":"In autumn, chlorophyll breaks down and the leaves turn red and yellow.","furigana":"あき|ようりょくそ|ぶんかい|は|あか|きいろ|か"},{"japanese":"植物の葉が緑に見えるのは葉緑素のためだ。","romaji":"Shokubutsu no ha ga midori ni mieru no wa yōryokuso no tame da.","german":"Plant leaves look green because of chlorophyll.","furigana":"しょくぶつ|は|みどり|み|ようりょくそ"}],"notes":"葉緑素 is the green leaf pigment chlorophyll and the usual word in school lessons; in technical texts one also reads クロロフィル. Do not confuse it with 葉緑体 \"chloroplast\", the cell organelle in which 葉緑素 is located. Typical contexts are 光合成 \"photosynthesis\" and autumn coloring, in which 葉緑素 is broken down.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"シダ植物は胞子によって繁殖する。","romaji":"Shida shokubutsu wa hōshi ni yotte hanshoku suru.","german":"Fern plants reproduce by spores.","furigana":"しょくぶつ|ほうし|はんしょく"},{"japanese":"カビの胞子は空気中を漂っている。","romaji":"Kabi no hōshi wa kūki chū o tadayotte iru.","german":"Mold spores float in the air.","furigana":"ほうし|くうきちゅう|ただよ"},{"japanese":"キノコのかさの裏から胞子が放出される。","romaji":"Kinoko no kasa no ura kara hōshi ga hōshutsu sareru.","german":"Spores are released from the underside of a mushroom cap.","furigana":"うら|ほうし|ほうしゅつ"}],"notes":"胞子 are the spores of ferns, mosses, fungi and mold. Typical combinations are 胞子で増える \"to multiply by spores\", 胞子を飛ばす and 胞子が放出される. In contrast, flowering plants reproduce by 種子 or 種 \"seeds\". In the household the word is met mainly with mold: カビの胞子.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"花粉が雌蕊に付着することで受粉が行われる。","romaji":"Kafun ga meshibe ni fuchaku suru koto de jufun ga okonawareru.","german":"Pollination takes place when pollen adheres to the pistil.","furigana":"かふん|めしべ|ふちゃく|じゅふん|おこな"},{"japanese":"理科の授業で、花のめしべとおしべを観察した。","romaji":"Rika no jugyō de, hana no meshibe to oshibe o kansatsu shita.","german":"In science class, we observed the pistil and stamens of a flower.","furigana":"りか|じゅぎょう|はな|かんさつ"},{"japanese":"この品種はめしべが退化していて、実がならない。","romaji":"Kono hinshu wa meshibe ga taika shite ite, mi ga naranai.","german":"In this variety the pistil has degenerated, so it bears no fruit.","furigana":"ひんしゅ|たいか|み"}],"notes":"雌蕊 is the pistil of a flower, the female organ on whose stigma 柱頭 the pollen lands. The character 蕊 is rare, so it is usually written めしべ or 雌しべ; in technical texts the Sino-Japanese reading しずい also occurs. The counterpart is 雄蕊 or おしべ \"stamen\"; both words are learned together in biology class.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"雄蕊から放出された花粉が風に運ばれる。","romaji":"Oshibe kara hōshutsu sareta kafun ga kaze ni hakobareru.","german":"Pollen released from the stamens is carried by the wind.","furigana":"おしべ|ほうしゅつ|かふん|かぜ|はこ"},{"japanese":"アサガオの花には五本のおしべがある。","romaji":"Asagao no hana ni wa gohon no oshibe ga aru.","german":"A morning glory flower has five stamens.","furigana":"はな|ごほん"},{"japanese":"ユリのおしべの花粉が服につくと、なかなか落ちない。","romaji":"Yuri no oshibe no kafun ga fuku ni tsuku to, nakanaka ochinai.","german":"If pollen from a lily's stamens gets on your clothes, it hardly comes off.","furigana":"かふん|ふく|お"}],"notes":"雄蕊 is the stamen, the male organ of the flower, at whose tip pollen is produced in the anther 葯. Since 蕊 is rare, in everyday writing it is おしべ or 雄しべ; in technical language there is also the reading ゆうずい. The counterpart is 雌蕊 or めしべ \"pistil\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"萼は花弁を保護する役割を持つ。","romaji":"Gaku wa kaben o hogo suru yakuwari o motsu.","german":"The calyx has the role of protecting the petals.","furigana":"がく|かべん|ほご|やくわり|も"},{"japanese":"イチゴのへたの部分は、実は萼である。","romaji":"Ichigo no heta no bubun wa, jitsu wa gaku de aru.","german":"The green cap of a strawberry is actually the calyx.","furigana":"ぶぶん|じつ|がく"},{"japanese":"桜の花が散った後も、萼は枝に残っている。","romaji":"Sakura no hana ga chitta ato mo, gaku wa eda ni nokotte iru.","german":"Even after the cherry blossoms have fallen, the calyxes remain on the branches.","furigana":"さくら|はな|ち|あと|がく|えだ|のこ"}],"notes":"萼 is the calyx, that is, the mostly green leaves that enclose the bud and sit beneath the petals. The character is rare, so it is often written in kana as がく. The individual sepal is more precisely called 萼片. A well-known everyday example is the green \"cap\" of the strawberry, called へた in daily speech. Do not confuse it with the identically sounding 額 \"frame, amount\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"花弁の色は昆虫を引き寄せるためにある。","romaji":"Kaben no iro wa konchū o hikiyoseru tame ni aru.","german":"The color of petals exists to attract insects.","furigana":"かべん|いろ|こんちゅう|ひ|よ"},{"japanese":"桜の花弁は五枚ある。","romaji":"Sakura no kaben wa gomai aru.","german":"Cherry blossoms have five petals.","furigana":"さくら|かべん|ごまい"},{"japanese":"図鑑では、花弁の数や形で植物を見分ける。","romaji":"Zukan de wa, kaben no kazu ya katachi de shokubutsu o miwakeru.","german":"In field guides, plants are distinguished by the number and shape of their petals.","furigana":"ずかん|かべん|かず|かたち|しょくぶつ|みわ"}],"notes":"花弁 is the botanical or literary term for petal; in everyday speech one almost always says 花びら. Counting uses 枚: 花弁が五枚ある. In botany one distinguishes 合弁花 (fused petals) and 離弁花 (free petals). The reading はなびら for 花弁 also occurs in texts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"果実の中に種子が含まれている。","romaji":"Kajitsu no naka ni shushi ga fukumarete iru.","german":"Seeds are contained within the fruit.","furigana":"かじつ|なか|しゅし|ふく"},{"japanese":"梅の果実を使って果実酒を作った。","romaji":"Ume no kajitsu o tsukatte kajitsushu o tsukutta.","german":"I made fruit liqueur using ume fruits.","furigana":"うめ|かじつ|つか|かじつしゅ|つく"},{"japanese":"改革の果実を国民全体に行き渡らせる必要がある。","romaji":"Kaikaku no kajitsu o kokumin zentai ni ikiwataraseru hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"The fruits of the reform need to reach the entire population.","furigana":"かいかく|かじつ|こくみんぜんたい|い|わた|ひつよう"}],"notes":"果実 is fruit in the botanical or formal sense; when one means fruit as food, one says 果物, and the fruit on the plant is called 実 in everyday speech (実がなる). 果実 often appears in compounds such as 果実酒 \"fruit liqueur\" and on labels. In elevated language it also stands figuratively for \"fruits, yield\": 改革の果実 \"the fruits of the reform\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"熱帯雨林の樹冠は太陽光を遮る。","romaji":"Nettai urin no jukan wa taiyōkō o saegiru.","german":"The canopy of the tropical rainforest blocks sunlight.","furigana":"ねったいうりん|じゅかん|たいようこう|さえぎ"},{"japanese":"この木は樹冠が大きく広がり、夏には涼しい木陰を作る。","romaji":"Kono ki wa jukan ga ōkiku hirogari, natsu ni wa suzushii kokage o tsukuru.","german":"This tree has a widely spreading crown and creates cool shade in summer.","furigana":"き|じゅかん|おお|ひろ|なつ|すず|こかげ|つく"},{"japanese":"街路樹の樹冠を剪定して、電線に触れないようにした。","romaji":"Gairoju no jukan o sentei shite, densen ni furenai yō ni shita.","german":"The crowns of the street trees were pruned so that they would not touch the power lines.","furigana":"がいろじゅ|じゅかん||でんせん|ふ"}],"notes":"樹冠 is the crown of a tree, that is, the entire area of branches and leaves; the word belongs to forestry, ecology and landscape care. For the closed canopy of a whole forest, one also says 林冠 in technical language. In everyday speech one speaks rather of 木のてっぺん or 枝葉. Typical combinations: 樹冠が広がる, 樹冠を剪定する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"森林の下層植生はわずかな光で生育する。","romaji":"Shinrin no kasō shokusei wa wazuka na hikari de seiiku suru.","german":"The understory of the forest grows on little light.","furigana":"しんりん|かそうしょくせい|ひかり|せいいく"},{"japanese":"シカが増えすぎて、森の下層植生がほとんど食べ尽くされた。","romaji":"Shika ga fuesugite, mori no kasō shokusei ga hotondo tabetsukusareta.","german":"Because there are too many deer, the understory of the forest has been almost entirely eaten away.","furigana":"ふ|もり|かそうしょくせい|た|つ"},{"japanese":"下層植生が失われると、雨で土壌が流出しやすくなる。","romaji":"Kasō shokusei ga ushinawareru to, ame de dojō ga ryūshutsu shiyasuku naru.","german":"When the understory is lost, soil is easily washed away by rain.","furigana":"かそうしょくせい|うしな|あめ|どじょう|りゅうしゅつ"}],"notes":"下層植生 is a technical term from forestry and ecology for the plants that grow beneath the forest canopy: shrubs, grasses and young trees. It is composed of 下層 \"lower layer\" and 植生 \"vegetation\". In everyday speech one says 下草 instead. In Japan the word is often used in connection with wildlife damage and erosion: 下層植生が衰退する, 下層植生が失われる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"腐葉土は植物に豊富な栄養を供給する。","romaji":"Fuyōdo wa shokubutsu ni hōfu na eiyō o kyōkyū suru.","german":"Leaf mold supplies plants with abundant nutrients.","furigana":"ふようど|しょくぶつ|ほうふ|えいよう|きょうきゅう"},{"japanese":"鉢植えの土に腐葉土を混ぜると、水はけが良くなる。","romaji":"Hachiue no tsuchi ni fuyōdo o mazeru to, mizuhake ga yoku naru.","german":"Mixing leaf mold into potting soil improves drainage.","furigana":"はちう|つち|ふようど|ま|みず|よ"},{"japanese":"森の地面には落ち葉が積もり、長い年月をかけて腐葉土になる。","romaji":"Mori no jimen ni wa ochiba ga tsumori, nagai nengetsu o kakete fuyōdo ni naru.","german":"Fallen leaves pile up on the forest floor and become leaf mold over many years.","furigana":"もり|じめん|お|ば|つ|なが|ねんげつ|ふようど"}],"notes":"腐葉土 is leaf mold or leaf humus made from rotted leaves and is used mainly in gardening: 腐葉土を混ぜる, 腐葉土を敷く. In Japan you buy it by the bag at a home improvement store. More general is 堆肥 \"compost\", which can also consist of manure or kitchen waste; the soil-science term for humus is 腐植.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"菌糸のネットワークが樹木同士を結んでいる。","romaji":"Kinshi no nettowāku ga jumoku dōshi o musunde iru.","german":"A network of fungal hyphae connects the trees with one another.","furigana":"きんし|じゅもくどうし|むす"},{"japanese":"キノコの本体は、地中に広がる菌糸である。","romaji":"Kinoko no hontai wa, chichū ni hirogaru kinshi de aru.","german":"The real body of a mushroom is the hyphae spreading underground.","furigana":"ほんたい|ちちゅう|ひろ|きんし"},{"japanese":"パンに生えたカビの白い菌糸が見えた。","romaji":"Pan ni haeta kabi no shiroi kinshi ga mieta.","german":"I could see the white hyphae of the mold growing on the bread.","furigana":"は|しろ|きんし|み"}],"notes":"菌糸 is the individual fungal hypha, a fine thread of which fungi and mold consist; the whole mesh is more precisely called 菌糸体 \"mycelium\". The visible mushroom is only the fruiting body 子実体, the actual organism is the underground 菌糸. Typical combinations are 菌糸が伸びる and 菌糸が広がる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"蝶の幼虫は葉を食べて成長する。","romaji":"Chō no yōchū wa ha o tabete seichō suru.","german":"Butterfly larvae grow by eating leaves.","furigana":"ちょう|ようちゅう|は|た|せいちょう"},{"japanese":"カブトムシの幼虫は土の中で一年近く過ごす。","romaji":"Kabutomushi no yōchū wa tsuchi no naka de ichinen chikaku sugosu.","german":"Rhinoceros beetle larvae spend nearly a year in the soil.","furigana":"ようちゅう|つち|なか|いちねんちか|す"},{"japanese":"夏の夜、セミの幼虫が木に登って羽化を始めた。","romaji":"Natsu no yoru, semi no yōchū ga ki ni nobotte uka o hajimeta.","german":"On a summer night, a cicada nymph climbed a tree and began to molt into an adult.","furigana":"なつ|よる|ようちゅう|き|のぼ|うか|はじ"}],"notes":"幼虫 is the larva of insects, that is, the stage after hatching from the egg; the counterpart is 成虫 \"adult insect\", and in many species 蛹 \"pupa\" lies in between. In everyday speech certain larvae are also called イモムシ \"caterpillar\", 毛虫 \"hairy caterpillar\" or ウジ \"maggot\". For frogs or fish, one speaks technically rather of 幼生 or 稚魚.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"蛹の中で幼虫が成虫へと変態する。","romaji":"Sanagi no naka de yōchū ga seichū e to hentai suru.","german":"Inside the pupa, the larva metamorphoses into an adult.","furigana":"さなぎ|なか|ようちゅう|せいちゅう|へんたい"},{"japanese":"幼虫は葉の裏で蛹になった。","romaji":"Yōchū wa ha no ura de sanagi ni natta.","german":"The larva pupated on the underside of a leaf.","furigana":"ようちゅう|は|うら|さなぎ"},{"japanese":"蛹から出てきたばかりの蝶は、しばらく羽を乾かしている。","romaji":"Sanagi kara dete kita bakari no chō wa, shibaraku hane o kawakashite iru.","german":"A butterfly that has just emerged from the pupa dries its wings for a while.","furigana":"さなぎ|で|ちょう|はね|かわ"}],"notes":"蛹 is the pupa of an insect, the resting stage between 幼虫 and 成虫. Since the character is rare, it is mostly written さなぎ or サナギ. Typical combinations are 蛹になる \"to pupate\" and 蛹から羽化する \"to emerge from the pupa\". Figuratively, さなぎから蝶へ stands for the transformation of a person, for example from an unremarkable girl to a self-confident woman.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"カエルは幼生から成体への変態を遂げる。","romaji":"Kaeru wa yōsei kara seitai e no hentai o togeru.","german":"Frogs undergo metamorphosis from larva to adult.","furigana":"ようせい|せいたい|へんたい|と"},{"japanese":"チョウは卵、幼虫、蛹、成虫と完全変態をする。","romaji":"Chō wa tamago, yōchū, sanagi, seichū to kanzen hentai o suru.","german":"Butterflies undergo complete metamorphosis: egg, larva, pupa, adult.","furigana":"たまご|ようちゅう|さなぎ|せいちゅう|かんぜんへんたい"},{"japanese":"「変態！」と言われて、彼は顔を真っ赤にした。","romaji":"\"Hentai!\" to iwarete, kare wa kao o makka ni shita.","german":"Being called a 「変態！」 (\"pervert!\") made him turn bright red.","furigana":"へんたい|い|かれ|かお|ま|か"}],"notes":"変態 denotes metamorphosis in biology, for example from tadpole to frog; as an intransitive する-verb: 変態する. One distinguishes 完全変態 (with a pupal stage, e.g. butterfly) and 不完全変態 (without a pupa, e.g. grasshopper). Note: in everyday speech 変態 mostly means \"pervert, perverted\" (short for 変態性欲), so one should use it carefully outside biological contexts. For \"transformation\" in the general sense, one prefers 変身 or 変化.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"蛇は成長に伴い定期的に脱皮する。","romaji":"Hebi wa seichō ni tomonai teikiteki ni dappi suru.","german":"Snakes shed their skin regularly as they grow.","furigana":"へび|せいちょう|ともな|ていきてき|だっぴ"},{"japanese":"カニは脱皮を繰り返して大きくなる。","romaji":"Kani wa dappi o kurikaeshite ōkiku naru.","german":"Crabs grow by molting repeatedly.","furigana":"だっぴ|く|かえ|おお"},{"japanese":"その会社は町工場から世界的な企業へと脱皮を遂げた。","romaji":"Sono kaisha wa machikōba kara sekaiteki na kigyō e to dappi o togeta.","german":"The company transformed itself from a small town factory into a world-class enterprise.","furigana":"かいしゃ|まちこうば|せかいてき|きぎょう|だっぴ|と"}],"notes":"脱皮 is the molting of snakes, insects or crustaceans; as an intransitive する-verb: ヘビが脱皮する. The shed skin is called 抜け殻. Figuratively, 脱皮 means \"to break away from the old, to reach a new stage\", often in 〜から脱皮する or 〜への脱皮を遂げる, for example when a company or an artist renews itself fundamentally. This figurative use is common in newspapers and speeches.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"昆虫は触角で匂いを感知する。","romaji":"Konchū wa shokkaku de nioi o kanchi suru.","german":"Insects detect smells with their antennae.","furigana":"こんちゅう|しょっかく|にお|かんち"},{"japanese":"アリは触角を触れ合わせて仲間と情報をやり取りする。","romaji":"Ari wa shokkaku o fureawasete nakama to jōhō o yaritori suru.","german":"Ants exchange information with their companions by touching antennae.","furigana":"しょっかく|ふ|あ|なかま|じょうほう|と"},{"japanese":"カタツムリの長い触角の先には目がある。","romaji":"Katatsumuri no nagai shokkaku no saki ni wa me ga aru.","german":"There are eyes at the tips of a snail's long tentacles.","furigana":"なが|しょっかく|さき|め"}],"notes":"触角 are the antennae of insects, crustaceans or snails, with which they feel and smell: 触角を動かす, 触角で感じる. Do not confuse it with 触手 \"tentacle\" (jellyfish, octopuses) or with アンテナ, the technical term for an antenna, which is also used figuratively (アンテナを張る \"to keep one's antennae out\"). 触角 itself is hardly used figuratively.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"甲殻類は海洋生態系の重要な構成要素である。","romaji":"Kōkakurui wa kaiyō seitaikei no jūyō na kōsei yōso de aru.","german":"Crustaceans are an important component of marine ecosystems.","furigana":"こうかくるい|かいようせいたいけい|じゅうよう|こうせいようそ"},{"japanese":"甲殻類アレルギーのため、エビやカニは食べられません。","romaji":"Kōkakurui arerugī no tame, ebi ya kani wa taberaremasen.","german":"Because of a crustacean allergy, I cannot eat shrimp or crab.","furigana":"こうかくるい|た"},{"japanese":"このスープには甲殻類が含まれています。","romaji":"Kono sūpu ni wa kōkakurui ga fukumarete imasu.","german":"This soup contains crustaceans.","furigana":"こうかくるい|ふく"}],"notes":"甲殻類 is the animal group of crustaceans, that is, shrimp, crabs, lobsters and woodlice; literally \"shell-armor class\". In everyday life the word is met mainly on menus and food labels, especially in 甲殻類アレルギー \"crustacean allergy\". Clams and squid do not belong to it; they are 軟体動物.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"貝やイカは軟体動物に分類される。","romaji":"Kai ya ika wa nantai dōbutsu ni bunrui sareru.","german":"Shellfish and squid are classified as mollusks.","furigana":"かい|なんたいどうぶつ|ぶんるい"},{"japanese":"タコは軟体動物の中でも特に知能が高いと言われる。","romaji":"Tako wa nantai dōbutsu no naka demo toku ni chinō ga takai to iwareru.","german":"Octopuses are said to be particularly intelligent among mollusks.","furigana":"なんたいどうぶつ|なか|とく|ちのう|たか|い"},{"japanese":"軟体動物には骨がないが、多くは殻で体を守っている。","romaji":"Nantai dōbutsu ni wa hone ga nai ga, ōku wa kara de karada o mamotte iru.","german":"Mollusks have no bones, but many protect their bodies with a shell.","furigana":"なんたいどうぶつ|ほね|おお|から|からだ|まも"}],"notes":"軟体動物 is the zoological term for mollusks such as clams, snails, squid and octopuses; literally \"soft-bodied animals\". In everyday speech the individual animals are simply called 貝, イカ or タコ; the collective term appears mainly in school textbooks, aquariums and nonfiction books. Contrasts are 節足動物 \"arthropods\" and 脊椎動物 \"vertebrates\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"魚類、両生類、爬虫類は全て脊椎動物である。","romaji":"Gyorui, ryōseirui, hachūrui wa subete sekitsui dōbutsu de aru.","german":"Fish, amphibians and reptiles are all vertebrates.","furigana":"ぎょるい|りょうせいるい|はちゅうるい|すべ|せきついどうぶつ"},{"japanese":"地球上の動物の大部分は無脊椎動物で、脊椎動物はごく一部にすぎない。","romaji":"Chikyūjō no dōbutsu no daibubun wa musekitsui dōbutsu de, sekitsui dōbutsu wa goku ichibu ni suginai.","german":"Most animals on Earth are invertebrates; vertebrates are only a tiny fraction.","furigana":"ちきゅうじょう|どうぶつ|だいぶぶん|むせきついどうぶつ|せきついどうぶつ|いちぶ"},{"japanese":"生物の授業で、脊椎動物を五つのグループに分けて学んだ。","romaji":"Seibutsu no jugyō de, sekitsui dōbutsu o itsutsu no gurūpu ni wakete mananda.","german":"In biology class, we learned about vertebrates divided into five groups.","furigana":"せいぶつ|じゅぎょう|せきついどうぶつ|いつ|わ|まな"}],"notes":"脊椎動物 are vertebrates, that is, animals with a backbone; 脊椎 is the technical word for the spinal column, while everyday speech says 背骨. The counterpart is 無脊椎動物 \"invertebrates\". In school lessons, 脊椎動物 are divided into five classes: 魚類, 両生類, 爬虫類, 鳥類 and 哺乳類.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鯨は海に生息する哺乳類である。","romaji":"Kujira wa umi ni seisoku suru honyūrui de aru.","german":"Whales are mammals that live in the sea.","furigana":"くじら|うみ|せいそく|ほにゅうるい"},{"japanese":"カモノハシは卵を産む珍しい哺乳類だ。","romaji":"Kamonohashi wa tamago o umu mezurashii honyūrui da.","german":"The platypus is a rare mammal that lays eggs.","furigana":"たまご|う|めずら|ほにゅうるい"},{"japanese":"コウモリは空を飛ぶことができる唯一の哺乳類である。","romaji":"Kōmori wa sora o tobu koto ga dekiru yuiitsu no honyūrui de aru.","german":"Bats are the only mammals that can fly.","furigana":"そら|と|ゆいいつ|ほにゅうるい"}],"notes":"哺乳類 is the class of mammals, literally \"the class that nourishes with milk\" (哺乳 \"to nurse\"). An individual mammal is also called 哺乳動物. In textbooks and newspapers the spelling ほ乳類 is often seen, because 哺 was long not among the standard characters. Typical are statements like 〜は哺乳類である or 〜は哺乳類の仲間だ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"爬虫類は体温を外部環境に依存する変温動物である。","romaji":"Hachūrui wa taion o gaibu kankyō ni izon suru hen'on dōbutsu de aru.","german":"Reptiles are cold-blooded animals whose body temperature depends on the external environment.","furigana":"はちゅうるい|たいおん|がいぶかんきょう|いぞん|へんおんどうぶつ"},{"japanese":"爬虫類をペットとして飼う人が増えている。","romaji":"Hachūrui o petto toshite kau hito ga fuete iru.","german":"More and more people keep reptiles as pets.","furigana":"はちゅうるい|か|ひと|ふ"},{"japanese":"恐竜は爬虫類の仲間だと考えられてきた。","romaji":"Kyōryū wa hachūrui no nakama da to kangaerarete kita.","german":"Dinosaurs have long been considered relatives of reptiles.","furigana":"きょうりゅう|はちゅうるい|なかま|かんが"}],"notes":"爬虫類 is the class of reptiles, that is, snakes, lizards, turtles and crocodiles; 爬 means \"to crawl\". In textbooks one often reads は虫類, because 爬 is not a standard character. In everyday life the word is met, for example, with pets (爬虫類を飼う) or in zoos. Do not confuse it with 両生類 \"amphibians\", which include frogs and newts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"両生類は水中と陸上の両方で生活できる。","romaji":"Ryōseirui wa suichū to rikujō no ryōhō de seikatsu dekiru.","german":"Amphibians can live both in water and on land.","furigana":"りょうせいるい|すいちゅう|りくじょう|りょうほう|せいかつ"},{"japanese":"両生類の皮膚は薄く、乾燥に弱い。","romaji":"Ryōseirui no hifu wa usuku, kansō ni yowai.","german":"The skin of amphibians is thin and sensitive to dryness.","furigana":"りょうせいるい|ひふ|うす|かんそう|よわ"},{"japanese":"環境汚染の影響で、世界中で両生類が減少している。","romaji":"Kankyō osen no eikyō de, sekaijū de ryōseirui ga genshō shite iru.","german":"Because of environmental pollution, amphibians are declining all over the world.","furigana":"かんきょうおせん|えいきょう|せかいじゅう|りょうせいるい|げんしょう"}],"notes":"両生類 are amphibians such as frogs, toads, newts and salamanders; literally \"animals that live on both sides\", that is, in water and on land. The larvae mostly live in water, the adult animals often on land. The older spelling is 両棲類. Do not confuse it with 爬虫類 \"reptiles\"; amphibians have moist, scaleless skin.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"昆虫やクモは節足動物に属する。","romaji":"Konchū ya kumo wa sessoku dōbutsu ni zoku suru.","german":"Insects and spiders belong to the arthropods.","furigana":"こんちゅう|せっそくどうぶつ|ぞく"},{"japanese":"エビやカニも、昆虫と同じ節足動物の仲間だ。","romaji":"Ebi ya kani mo, konchū to onaji sessoku dōbutsu no nakama da.","german":"Shrimp and crabs are also arthropods, like insects.","furigana":"こんちゅう|おな|せっそくどうぶつ|なかま"},{"japanese":"節足動物は、動物の種の大半を占めている。","romaji":"Sessoku dōbutsu wa, dōbutsu no shu no taihan o shimete iru.","german":"Arthropods make up the majority of animal species.","furigana":"せっそくどうぶつ|どうぶつ|しゅ|たいはん|し"}],"notes":"節足動物 are arthropods, literally \"animals with jointed legs\" (節 \"joint, segment\", 足 \"leg\"). They include insects, spiders, centipedes and crustaceans (甲殻類). The word is zoological and technical and appears mainly in school textbooks and nonfiction; typical frames are 〜は節足動物に属する and 節足動物の仲間.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ウニやヒトデは棘皮動物の代表例である。","romaji":"Uni ya hitode wa kyokuhi dōbutsu no daihyōrei de aru.","german":"Sea urchins and starfish are typical examples of echinoderms.","furigana":"きょくひどうぶつ|だいひょうれい"},{"japanese":"棘皮動物の体は、多くが星のように五方向に広がっている。","romaji":"Kyokuhi dōbutsu no karada wa, ōku ga hoshi no yō ni gohōkō ni hirogatte iru.","german":"The body of most echinoderms spreads out in five directions like a star.","furigana":"きょくひどうぶつ|からだ|おお|ほし|ごほうこう|ひろ"},{"japanese":"ナマコも、見た目は違うが棘皮動物の一種である。","romaji":"Namako mo, mitame wa chigau ga kyokuhi dōbutsu no isshu de aru.","german":"The sea cucumber, too, though it looks different, is a kind of echinoderm.","furigana":"み|め|ちが|きょくひどうぶつ|いっしゅ"}],"notes":"棘皮動物 are echinoderms such as sea urchins, starfish, sea cucumbers and sea lilies; 棘 means \"thorn, spine\", 皮 \"skin\". The word is purely zoological and appears almost only in textbooks, aquariums and nonfiction. In everyday speech the animals are simply called ウニ, ヒトデ or ナマコ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"オオカミの生態について長年の調査が行われた。","romaji":"Ōkami no seitai ni tsuite naganen no chōsa ga okonawareta.","german":"Über die Lebensweise der Wölfe wurde eine langjährige Untersuchung durchgeführt.","furigana":"せいたい|ながねん|ちょうさ|おこな"},{"japanese":"深海魚の生態には、まだ分からないことが多い。","romaji":"Shinkaigyo no seitai ni wa, mada wakaranai koto ga ōi.","german":"Über die Lebensweise von Tiefseefischen ist vieles noch unbekannt.","furigana":"しんかいぎょ|せいたい|わ|おお"},{"japanese":"この番組は、現代のサラリーマンの生態をユーモラスに描いている。","romaji":"Kono bangumi wa, gendai no sararīman no seitai o yūmorasu ni egaite iru.","german":"Diese Sendung zeigt auf humorvolle Weise, wie heutige Büroangestellte leben.","furigana":"ばんぐみ|げんだい|せいたい|えが"}],"notes":"生態 ist die Lebensweise eines Lebewesens, also wie es lebt, sich ernährt und fortpflanzt; typisch ist 〜の生態を調べる bzw. 〜の生態について. Scherzhaft wird es auch für Menschengruppen verwendet: 若者の生態 „wie junge Leute so ticken“. Abzugrenzen sind 生態系 „Ökosystem“ und 生態学 „Ökologie als Wissenschaft“; die deutsche Glosse „Ökologie“ meint hier also eher die Lebensweise.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"生態学の知見は環境保全に不可欠である。","romaji":"Seitaigaku no chiken wa kankyō hozen ni fukaketsu de aru.","german":"Ecological findings are indispensable for environmental conservation.","furigana":"せいたいがく|ちけん|かんきょうほぜん|ふかけつ"},{"japanese":"大学で生態学を専攻している。","romaji":"Daigaku de seitaigaku o senkō shite iru.","german":"I am majoring in ecology at university.","furigana":"だいがく|せいたいがく|せんこう"},{"japanese":"生態学者たちは、この湖の魚の減少を長年調べてきた。","romaji":"Seitaigakushatachi wa, kono mizuumi no sakana no genshō o naganen shirabete kita.","german":"Ecologists have been investigating the decline of fish in this lake for years.","furigana":"せいたいがくしゃ|みずうみ|さかな|げんしょう|ながねんしら"}],"notes":"生態学 is ecology as a science, that is, the study of the relationships between living things and their environment; the researcher is called 生態学者. The loanword エコロジー can mean the same, but often stands for environmental awareness in general (エコ). 生態学, by contrast, denotes only the discipline: 生態学を専攻する, 生態学的な視点.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"外来種の侵入が在来種の存続を脅かしている。","romaji":"Gairaishu no shinnyū ga zairaishu no sonzoku o obiyakashite iru.","german":"The invasion of non-native species threatens the survival of native species.","furigana":"がいらいしゅ|しんにゅう|ざいらいしゅ|そんぞく|おびや"},{"japanese":"アメリカザリガニは日本に持ち込まれた外来種だ。","romaji":"Amerika zarigani wa Nihon ni mochikomareta gairaishu da.","german":"The American crayfish is a non-native species brought to Japan.","furigana":"にほん|も|こ|がいらいしゅ"},{"japanese":"飼えなくなったペットを川に放すと、外来種が広がる原因になる。","romaji":"Kaenaku natta petto o kawa ni hanasu to, gairaishu ga hirogaru gen'in ni naru.","german":"Releasing pets you can no longer keep into a river causes non-native species to spread.","furigana":"か|かわ|はな|がいらいしゅ|ひろ|げんいん"}],"notes":"外来種 is a non-native species introduced by humans, the opposite of 在来種 \"native species\". Not every 外来種 is harmful; for the problematic ones one speaks more precisely of 侵略的外来種 \"invasive species\", and in Japan there are legally designated 特定外来生物. 外来生物 is also often used as an umbrella term. Typical are 外来種が増える・広がる and 外来種を駆除する.","levelNote":"Compound: 外来 (N2) + 種 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"在来種を保護するための対策が急務である。","romaji":"Zairaishu o hogo suru tame no taisaku ga kyūmu de aru.","german":"Measures to protect native species are urgently needed.","furigana":"ざいらいしゅ|ほご|たいさく|きゅうむ"},{"japanese":"外来種のブラックバスによって、湖の在来種が減ってしまった。","romaji":"Gairaishu no burakkubasu ni yotte, mizuumi no zairaishu ga hette shimatta.","german":"Native species in the lake have declined because of the introduced black bass.","furigana":"がいらいしゅ|みずうみ|ざいらいしゅ|へ"},{"japanese":"この地域では、昔ながらの在来種の大豆が今も栽培されている。","romaji":"Kono chiiki de wa, mukashinagara no zairaishu no daizu ga ima mo saibai sarete iru.","german":"In this region, traditional native varieties of soybeans are still cultivated today.","furigana":"ちいき|むかし|ざいらいしゅ|だいず|いま|さいばい"}],"notes":"在来種 is a native species, one originally occurring in an area, the opposite of 外来種. In agriculture it also denotes old, regional varieties or breeds (在来種の大豆, 在来種の馬), in contrast to modern cultivars. Distinguish 固有種: a 在来種 may also occur elsewhere, whereas a 固有種 lives only in this area.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ガラパゴス諸島には多くの固有種が生息している。","romaji":"Garapagosu shotō ni wa ōku no koyūshu ga seisoku shite iru.","german":"Many endemic species live on the Galápagos Islands.","furigana":"しょとう|おお|こゆうしゅ|せいそく"},{"japanese":"アマミノクロウサギは奄美大島と徳之島だけに住む固有種だ。","romaji":"Amami no kurousagi wa Amami Ōshima to Tokunoshima dake ni sumu koyūshu da.","german":"The Amami rabbit is an endemic species that lives only on Amami-Ōshima and Tokunoshima.","furigana":"|とくのしま|す|こゆうしゅ"},{"japanese":"島は外から隔てられているため、固有種が多く見られる。","romaji":"Shima wa soto kara hedaterarete iru tame, koyūshu ga ōku mirareru.","german":"Because islands are cut off from the outside, many endemic species are found there.","furigana":"しま|そと|へだ|こゆうしゅ|おお|み"}],"notes":"固有種 is an endemic species that occurs only in a particular area, for example on an island: 日本の固有種, 〜にしかいない固有種. Isolated islands such as the Galápagos, Okinawa or the Ogasawara Islands are known for many 固有種. Distinguish 在来種, which means only \"native\" and may also occur elsewhere.","levelNote":"Compound: 固有 (N1) + 種 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自然選択は進化の主要なメカニズムである。","romaji":"Shizen sentaku wa shinka no shuyō na mekanizumu de aru.","german":"Natural selection is the main mechanism of evolution.","furigana":"しぜんせんたく|しんか|しゅよう"},{"japanese":"ダーウィンは自然選択によって生物が進化すると考えた。","romaji":"Dāwin wa shizen sentaku ni yotte seibutsu ga shinka suru to kangaeta.","german":"Darwin held that living things evolve through natural selection.","furigana":"しぜんせんたく|せいぶつ|しんか|かんが"},{"japanese":"抗生物質の使いすぎは、耐性菌の自然選択を促してしまう。","romaji":"Kōsei busshitsu no tsukaisugi wa, taiseikin no shizen sentaku o unagashite shimau.","german":"Overuse of antibiotics promotes the natural selection of resistant bacteria.","furigana":"こうせいぶっしつ|つか|たいせいきん|しぜんせんたく|うなが"}],"notes":"自然選択 is natural selection in Darwin's sense, the technical term in biology and textbooks. Synonymous and more common in everyday speech is 自然淘汰, which is also used figuratively for companies or products that disappear from the market. The counterpart is 人為選択 \"artificial selection\" through breeding. Typical: 自然選択によって進化する, 自然選択が働く.","levelNote":"Compound: 自然 (N4) + 選択 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"食物網は食物連鎖よりも複雑な関係を示す。","romaji":"Shokumotsu mō wa shokumotsu rensa yori mo fukuzatsu na kankei o shimesu.","german":"A food web shows more complex relationships than a food chain.","furigana":"しょくもつもう|しょくもつれんさ|ふくざつ|かんけい|しめ"},{"japanese":"一つの種が絶滅すると、食物網全体に影響が及ぶ。","romaji":"Hitotsu no shu ga zetsumetsu suru to, shokumotsu mō zentai ni eikyō ga oyobu.","german":"If one species goes extinct, the whole food web is affected.","furigana":"ひと|しゅ|ぜつめつ|しょくもつもうぜんたい|えいきょう|およ"},{"japanese":"海の食物網では、プランクトンが重要な土台になっている。","romaji":"Umi no shokumotsu mō de wa, purankuton ga jūyō na dodai ni natte iru.","german":"In the marine food web, plankton forms an important foundation.","furigana":"うみ|しょくもつもう|じゅうよう|どだい"}],"notes":"食物網 is the food web, that is, the network of all feeding relationships in an ecosystem; 網 means \"net\". It is usually explained in contrast to the better-known 食物連鎖 \"food chain\", which shows only a linear sequence. The word is technical and appears in biology textbooks and environmental texts.","levelNote":"Compound: 食物 (N3) + 網 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"分解者は有機物を無機物に分解する。","romaji":"Bunkaisha wa yūkibutsu o mukibutsu ni bunkai suru.","german":"Decomposers break organic matter down into inorganic matter.","furigana":"ぶんかいしゃ|ゆうきぶつ|むきぶつ|ぶんかい"},{"japanese":"落ち葉が土に還るのは、菌類や細菌などの分解者のおかげだ。","romaji":"Ochiba ga tsuchi ni kaeru no wa, kinrui ya saikin nado no bunkaisha no okage da.","german":"Fallen leaves return to the soil thanks to decomposers such as fungi and bacteria.","furigana":"お|ば|つち|かえ|きんるい|さいきん|ぶんかいしゃ"},{"japanese":"ミミズは土の中で重要な分解者の役割を果たしている。","romaji":"Mimizu wa tsuchi no naka de jūyō na bunkaisha no yakuwari o hatashite iru.","german":"Earthworms play an important role as decomposers in the soil.","furigana":"つち|なか|じゅうよう|ぶんかいしゃ|やくわり|は"}],"notes":"分解者 is, in ecology, the decomposer, that is, organisms such as fungi, bacteria or earthworms that break down dead organic material. Together with 生産者 \"producer\" and 消費者 \"consumer\" it forms the basic concepts of the ecosystem in school lessons. Outside biology, 分解者 is hardly used; the related verb is 分解する \"to decompose, to break down\".","levelNote":"Compound: 分解 (N2) + 者 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"植物は生態系における主要な生産者である。","romaji":"Shokubutsu wa seitaikei ni okeru shuyō na seisansha de aru.","german":"Plants are the main producers in an ecosystem.","furigana":"しょくぶつ|せいたいけい|しゅよう|せいさんしゃ"},{"japanese":"スーパーで、生産者の名前と写真が付いた野菜を買った。","romaji":"Sūpā de, seisansha no namae to shashin ga tsuita yasai o katta.","german":"At the supermarket, I bought vegetables with the producer's name and photo attached.","furigana":"せいさんしゃ|なまえ|しゃしん|つ|やさい|か"},{"japanese":"海では、植物プランクトンが主な生産者だ。","romaji":"Umi de wa, shokubutsu purankuton ga omo na seisansha da.","german":"In the sea, phytoplankton is the main producer.","furigana":"うみ|しょくぶつ|おも|せいさんしゃ"}],"notes":"生産者 means in everyday speech \"producer, maker\", especially farmers and fishers: 生産者の顔が見える野菜 \"vegetables whose producer you know\". In ecology it denotes the producers, that is, plants and algae that make organic substances through photosynthesis; 消費者 and 分解者 belong with it. For industrial manufacturers one usually says メーカー or 製造業者.","levelNote":"Compound: 生産 (N4) + 者 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"草食動物は一次消費者に分類される。","romaji":"Sōshoku dōbutsu wa ichiji shōhisha ni bunrui sareru.","german":"Herbivores are classified as primary consumers.","furigana":"そうしょくどうぶつ|いちじしょうひしゃ|ぶんるい"},{"japanese":"肉食動物は、草食動物を食べる二次消費者にあたる。","romaji":"Nikushoku dōbutsu wa, sōshoku dōbutsu o taberu niji shōhisha ni ataru.","german":"Carnivores that eat herbivores are secondary consumers.","furigana":"にくしょくどうぶつ|そうしょくどうぶつ|た|にじしょうひしゃ"},{"japanese":"値上げが続き、消費者の財布のひもは固くなっている。","romaji":"Neage ga tsuzuki, shōhisha no saifu no himo wa kataku natte iru.","german":"With price increases continuing, consumers are keeping a tight grip on their wallets.","furigana":"ねあ|つづ|しょうひしゃ|さいふ|かた"}],"notes":"消費者 in everyday speech is above all the \"consumer\": 消費者物価 \"consumer prices\", 消費者庁 \"Consumer Affairs Agency\", 消費者の声. In ecology it denotes animals that feed on other living things; herbivores are 一次消費者, carnivores 二次消費者, and so on. The counterpart is 生産者, to which 分解者 is added.","levelNote":"Compound: 消費 (N3) + 者 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"マメ科植物は根粒菌による窒素固定を行う。","romaji":"Mameka shokubutsu wa konryūkin ni yoru chisso kotei o okonau.","german":"Legumes carry out nitrogen fixation with the help of root nodule bacteria.","furigana":"かしょくぶつ|こんりゅうきん|ちっそこてい|おこな"},{"japanese":"空気中の窒素は、そのままでは植物が利用できないため、窒素固定が必要になる。","romaji":"Kūkichū no chisso wa, sono mama de wa shokubutsu ga riyō dekinai tame, chisso kotei ga hitsuyō ni naru.","german":"Plants cannot use the nitrogen in the air as it is, so nitrogen fixation is necessary.","furigana":"くうきちゅう|ちっそ|しょくぶつ|りよう|ちっそこてい|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"田んぼにレンゲを植えるのは、窒素固定で土を肥やすためだ。","romaji":"Tanbo ni renge o ueru no wa, chisso kotei de tsuchi o koyasu tame da.","german":"Milk vetch is planted in rice paddies to enrich the soil through nitrogen fixation.","furigana":"た|う|ちっそこてい|つち|こ"}],"notes":"窒素固定 is nitrogen fixation, that is, the conversion of atmospheric nitrogen into a form usable by plants. Typical frames: 〜による窒素固定 or 窒素固定を行う. It is well known in legumes (マメ科) and their root nodule bacteria 根粒菌, which is why clover or vetch is planted to improve the soil. The word is technical (biology, agriculture, chemistry).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"光周性により植物の開花時期が制御される。","romaji":"Kōshūsei ni yori shokubutsu no kaika jiki ga seigyo sareru.","german":"Photoperiodism controls the flowering time of plants.","furigana":"こうしゅうせい|しょくぶつ|かいかじき|せいぎょ"},{"japanese":"電照菊は、光周性を利用して夜も明かりを当て、開花を遅らせる。","romaji":"Denshōgiku wa, kōshūsei o riyō shite yoru mo akari o ate, kaika o okuraseru.","german":"With electric-light chrysanthemums, photoperiodism is exploited: they are lit at night to delay flowering.","furigana":"でんしょうぎく|こうしゅうせい|りよう|よる|あ|あ|かいか|おく"},{"japanese":"渡り鳥の移動の時期にも、光周性が関わっていると考えられている。","romaji":"Wataridori no idō no jiki ni mo, kōshūsei ga kakawatte iru to kangaerarete iru.","german":"It is thought that photoperiodism is also involved in the timing of bird migration.","furigana":"わた|どり|いどう|じき|こうしゅうせい|かか|かんが"}],"notes":"光周性 is photoperiodism, that is, the response of plants and animals to day length (光 \"light\", 周 \"period\"). In botany one distinguishes 短日植物 \"short-day plants\" such as chrysanthemums and 長日植物 \"long-day plants\". The word is purely technical; typical are 光周性を示す and 光周性を利用する, for example in horticulture.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"植物は葉の気孔から水分を蒸散させる。","romaji":"Shokubutsu wa ha no kikō kara suibun wo jōsan saseru.","german":"Plants transpire water through the stomata of their leaves.","furigana":"しょくぶつ|は|きこう|すいぶん|じょうさん"},{"japanese":"夏の暑い日には、葉からの蒸散量が多くなる。","romaji":"Natsu no atsui hi ni wa, ha kara no jōsanryō ga ōku naru.","german":"On hot summer days, the amount of transpiration from the leaves increases.","furigana":"なつ|あつ|ひ|は|じょうさんりょう|おお"},{"japanese":"鉢植えにビニール袋をかぶせると、蒸散した水が袋の内側に付く。","romaji":"Hachiue ni binīru-bukuro o kabuseru to, jōsan shita mizu ga fukuro no uchigawa ni tsuku.","german":"If you cover a potted plant with a plastic bag, the transpired water collects on the inside of the bag.","furigana":"はちう|ぶくろ|じょうさん|みず|ふくろ|うちがわ|つ"}],"notes":"蒸散 is a technical term of botany for the release of water vapor through the plant surface, above all through the 気孔. As an intransitive する-verb, the water takes が: 水分が蒸散する; with the plant as the agent, the causative form 水分を蒸散させる is used. It differs from the general 蒸発 \"evaporation\" (puddles, laundry) in that it is used practically only for plants. Typical compounds: 蒸散作用, 蒸散量.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"気孔の開閉はガス交換を調節する。","romaji":"Kikō no kaihei wa gasu kōkan wo chōsetsu suru.","german":"The opening and closing of stomata regulates gas exchange.","furigana":"きこう|かいへい|こうかん|ちょうせつ"},{"japanese":"乾燥すると、植物は気孔を閉じて水分の損失を防ぐ。","romaji":"Kansō suru to, shokubutsu wa kikō o tojite suibun no sonshitsu o fusegu.","german":"When it is dry, plants close their stomata to prevent water loss.","furigana":"かんそう|しょくぶつ|きこう|と|すいぶん|そんしつ|ふせ"},{"japanese":"顕微鏡で葉の裏を見ると、たくさんの気孔が観察できる。","romaji":"Kenbikyō de ha no ura o miru to, takusan no kikō ga kansatsu dekiru.","german":"Looking at the underside of a leaf under a microscope, you can observe many stomata.","furigana":"けんびきょう|は|うら|み|きこう|かんさつ"}],"notes":"気孔 denotes in botany the stomata of leaves, through which gas exchange and 蒸散 take place; one says 気孔が開く／閉じる and 気孔の開閉. In materials engineering it also stands for gas pores in metal or ceramics. Do not confuse it with the homophone 気候 \"climate\" - in conversation the context usually settles it. The skin pores of humans, by contrast, are 毛穴.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"維管束は植物体内で水や養分を運搬する。","romaji":"Ikansoku wa shokubutsu tainai de mizu ya yōbun wo unpan suru.","german":"Vascular bundles transport water and nutrients within the plant body.","furigana":"いかんそく|しょくぶつたいない|みず|ようぶん|うんぱん"},{"japanese":"コケ植物には維管束がない。","romaji":"Koke shokubutsu ni wa ikansoku ga nai.","german":"Mosses have no vascular bundles.","furigana":"しょくぶつ|いかんそく"},{"japanese":"茎の断面を染色すると、維管束が輪のように並んでいるのが分かる。","romaji":"Kuki no danmen o senshoku suru to, ikansoku ga wa no yō ni narande iru no ga wakaru.","german":"If you stain a cross-section of a stem, you can see that the vascular bundles are arranged in a ring.","furigana":"くき|だんめん|せんしょく|いかんそく|わ|なら|わ"}],"notes":"維管束 is the vascular bundle of vascular plants; it consists of the water-conducting 道管（導管） and the nutrient-conducting 師管. The word occurs almost only in biology class and technical texts, for example in 維管束植物 \"vascular plants\" as opposed to mosses, which have no vascular bundles. By the arrangement of the vascular bundles in the stem, one distinguishes 単子葉類 (scattered) and 双子葉類 (in a ring).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"師管は光合成産物を植物全体に輸送する。","romaji":"Shikan wa kōgōsei sanbutsu o shokubutsu zentai ni yusō suru.","german":"Sieve tubes transport the products of photosynthesis throughout the plant.","furigana":"しかん|こうごうせいさんぶつ|しょくぶつぜんたい|ゆそう"},{"japanese":"道管は水を運び、師管は養分を運ぶ。","romaji":"Dōkan wa mizu o hakobi, shikan wa yōbun o hakobu.","german":"Vessels carry water, and sieve tubes carry nutrients.","furigana":"どうかん|みず|はこ|しかん|ようぶん|はこ"},{"japanese":"アブラムシは師管に口を差し込んで汁を吸う。","romaji":"Aburamushi wa shikan ni kuchi o sashikonde shiru o suu.","german":"Aphids insert their mouthparts into the sieve tubes and suck the sap.","furigana":"しかん|くち|さ|こ|しる|す"}],"notes":"師管 is the sieve tube, that is, the conducting tissue that transports the sugars formed in photosynthesis (養分) from the leaves to roots, fruits and other plant parts. The counterpart is the 道管（導管）, which conducts water from bottom to top; together the two form the 維管束. The whole phloem is strictly called 師部, but in school contexts it is often equated with 師管. Older texts write 篩管 (篩 = \"sieve\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"導管は根から吸収した水を上方へ運ぶ。","romaji":"Dōkan wa ne kara kyūshū shita mizu o jōhō e hakobu.","german":"Vessels carry water absorbed from the roots upward.","furigana":"どうかん|ね|きゅうしゅう|みず|じょうほう|はこ"},{"japanese":"都市ガスは地下の導管を通じて各家庭に届けられる。","romaji":"Toshi gasu wa chika no dōkan o tsūjite kaku katei ni todokerareru.","german":"City gas is delivered to each household through underground pipes.","furigana":"とし|ちか|どうかん|つう|かくかてい|とど"},{"japanese":"唾液は導管を通って口の中に分泌される。","romaji":"Daeki wa dōkan o tōtte kuchi no naka ni bunpitsu sareru.","german":"Saliva is secreted into the mouth through ducts.","furigana":"だえき|どうかん|とお|くち|なか|ぶんぴつ"}],"notes":"導管 generally means \"conduit, pipe\" for liquids or gases (ガス導管) and, in anatomy, the excretory duct of a gland. In botany it is the water-conducting vessel in the wood, which transports water and minerals from the roots upward. Today's Japanese school textbooks mostly write 道管 for this (same reading); 導管 is found in older and more general texts. The counterpart is the 師管, which conducts nutrients.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"草食動物は植物を主食とする。","romaji":"Sōshoku dōbutsu wa shokubutsu wo shushoku to suru.","german":"Herbivores feed mainly on plants.","furigana":"そうしょくどうぶつ|しょくぶつ|しゅしょく"},{"japanese":"キリンは草食で、高い木の葉を食べる。","romaji":"Kirin wa sōshoku de, takai ki no ha o taberu.","german":"Giraffes are herbivores and eat the leaves of tall trees.","furigana":"そうしょく|たか|き|は|た"},{"japanese":"最近は恋愛に消極的な草食系男子が増えたと言われる。","romaji":"Saikin wa ren'ai ni shōkyokuteki na sōshokukei danshi ga fueta to iwareru.","german":"It is said that there are now more young men who are rather reserved when it comes to romance.","furigana":"さいきん|れんあい|しょうきょくてき|そうしょくけいだんし|ふ|い"}],"notes":"草食 means \"to feed on plants\" and usually stands as a modifier before a noun: 草食動物 \"herbivore\", 草食恐竜, or with 性: 草食性の魚. As a する-verb (草食する) it is rare. Colloquially, 草食系（男子） became popular for reserved men who show little initiative in romance; the opposite is 肉食系. The biological counterparts are 肉食 and 雑食.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"肉食動物は鋭い牙と爪を持つ。","romaji":"Nikushoku dōbutsu wa surudoi kiba to tsume wo motsu.","german":"Carnivores have sharp fangs and claws.","furigana":"にくしょくどうぶつ|するど|きば|つめ|も"},{"japanese":"明治時代になって、日本でも肉食が広まった。","romaji":"Meiji jidai ni natte, Nihon de mo nikushoku ga hiromatta.","german":"It was only in the Meiji era that meat eating spread in Japan, too.","furigana":"めいじじだい|にほん|にくしょく|ひろ"},{"japanese":"サメの多くは肉食で、魚やアザラシを襲う。","romaji":"Same no ōku wa nikushoku de, sakana ya azarashi o osou.","german":"Most sharks are carnivores and attack fish and seals.","furigana":"おお|にくしょく|さかな|おそ"}],"notes":"肉食 has two uses: for humans, \"eating meat, a meat diet\" (肉食を控える, 肉食文化); for animals, \"carnivorous\", mostly as a modifier: 肉食動物, 肉食獣, 肉食性. Opposites are 草食 and, for human diet, 菜食 \"vegetarian diet\". Colloquially, 肉食系 refers to people who are active and bold in romance. The reading にくじき occurs only in the Buddhist expression 肉食妻帯.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"熊は雑食性で果実も魚も食べる。","romaji":"Kuma wa zasshokusei de kajitsu mo sakana mo taberu.","german":"Bears are omnivorous and eat both fruit and fish.","furigana":"くま|ざっしょくせい|かじつ|さかな|た"},{"japanese":"人間は雑食なので、肉も野菜も消化できる。","romaji":"Ningen wa zasshoku na node, niku mo yasai mo shōka dekiru.","german":"Humans are omnivores and can digest both meat and vegetables.","furigana":"にんげん|ざっしょく|にく|やさい|しょうか"},{"japanese":"音楽の好みは雑食で、演歌からジャズまで何でも聴きます。","romaji":"Ongaku no konomi wa zasshoku de, enka kara jazu made nan demo kikimasu.","german":"My taste in music is omnivorous; I listen to everything from enka to jazz.","furigana":"おんがく|この|ざっしょく|えんか|なん|き"}],"notes":"雑食 means \"to feed on both plants and animals, to eat everything\"; typical are 雑食性, 雑食動物 and 〜は雑食だ. Opposites are 草食 and 肉食. It is also said loosely of people with broad tastes, for example in reading or music: 読書は雑食です \"I read widely\". Distinguish 悪食, eating unusual or disgusting things.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"雄のライオンは広大な縄張りを持つ。","romaji":"Osu no raion wa kōdai na nawabari wo motsu.","german":"Male lions have a vast territory.","furigana":"おす|こうだい|なわば|も"},{"japanese":"猫は自分の縄張りに入ってきた猫を追い払う。","romaji":"Neko wa jibun no nawabari ni haitte kita neko o oiharau.","german":"Cats chase away cats that enter their territory.","furigana":"ねこ|じぶん|なわば|はい|ねこ|お|はら"},{"japanese":"部署間の縄張り意識が強く、協力がなかなか進まない。","romaji":"Busho-kan no nawabari ishiki ga tsuyoku, kyōryoku ga nakanaka susumanai.","german":"There is a strong sense of turf between departments, so cooperation makes little progress.","furigana":"ぶしょかん|なわば|いしき|つよ|きょうりょく|すす"}],"notes":"縄張り originally means marking off an area with a rope; today it mainly means the \"territory\" of an animal: 縄張りを持つ, 縄張りを守る, 縄張り争い. Figuratively it denotes the sphere of responsibility or influence of people, often pejoratively for turf-mindedness in government offices and companies, or a gang's area: 縄張り意識が強い. It is also written 縄張 or, in games and magazines, ナワバリ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鮭は海を回遊したあと、生まれた川に戻って産卵する。","romaji":"Sake wa umi o kaiyū shita ato, umareta kawa ni modotte sanran suru.","german":"Salmon travel around the sea and then return to the river where they were born to spawn.","furigana":"さけ|うみ|かいゆう|う|かわ|もど|さんらん"},{"japanese":"マグロは広い海を回遊する魚だ。","romaji":"Maguro wa hiroi umi o kaiyū suru sakana da.","german":"Tuna are fish that migrate across the wide ocean.","furigana":"ひろ|うみ|かいゆう|さかな"},{"japanese":"この庭園は池の周りを歩いて楽しむ回遊式庭園です。","romaji":"Kono teien wa ike no mawari o aruite tanoshimu kaiyūshiki teien desu.","german":"This garden is a strolling garden that you enjoy by walking around the pond.","furigana":"ていえん|いけ|まわ|ある|たの|かいゆうしきていえん"}],"notes":"回遊 denotes above all the regular migrations of fish and marine animals for spawning or foraging: 海を回遊する, 回遊魚 (e.g. tuna, bonito). For salmon swimming up the river, one says 遡上, and for migratory birds 渡り. The original meaning \"tour, round trip\" lives on in 回遊式庭園 (strolling garden with a circuit path) and in urban planning (回遊性 \"inviting to stroll around\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ウミガメは砂浜に上陸して産卵する。","romaji":"Umigame wa sunahama ni jōriku shite sanran suru.","german":"Sea turtles come ashore on the sandy beach to lay eggs.","furigana":"すなはま|じょうりく|さんらん"},{"japanese":"この川では秋になると鮭の産卵が見られる。","romaji":"Kono kawa de wa aki ni naru to sake no sanran ga mirareru.","german":"At this river, you can see salmon spawning in autumn.","furigana":"かわ|あき|さけ|さんらん|み"},{"japanese":"産卵期の魚は釣らないようにしましょう。","romaji":"Sanranki no sakana wa tsuranai yō ni shimashō.","german":"Let's avoid fishing for fish in their spawning season.","furigana":"さんらんき|さかな|つ"}],"notes":"産卵 is the technical word for \"laying eggs\" or \"spawning\" in fish, birds, insects and reptiles: 産卵する, 産卵期, 産卵場所. In everyday speech, for example with chickens, one rather says 卵を産む; 産卵 sounds factual and is used in nature documentaries and the news. For mammals, one says 出産 \"giving birth\" instead.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"卵が孵化するまで約三週間かかる。","romaji":"Tamago ga fuka suru made yaku sanshūkan kakaru.","german":"It takes about three weeks for the eggs to hatch.","furigana":"たまご|ふか|やくさんしゅうかん"},{"japanese":"サケの稚魚は人工孵化で育てられ、春に川へ放流される。","romaji":"Sake no chigyo wa jinkō fuka de sodaterare, haru ni kawa e hōryū sareru.","german":"Salmon fry are raised by artificial hatching and released into the river in spring.","furigana":"ちぎょ|じんこうふか|そだ|はる|かわ|ほうりゅう"},{"japanese":"孵化したばかりのヒナが親鳥に餌をねだっている。","romaji":"Fuka shita bakari no hina ga oyadori ni esa o nedatte iru.","german":"The newly hatched chicks are begging their parent birds for food.","furigana":"ふか|おやどり|えさ"}],"notes":"孵化 means \"to hatch\" (intransitive: 卵が孵化する, ヒナが孵化する) or \"to incubate, to hatch out\" (especially artificially: 卵を孵化させる, 人工孵化). Since 孵 is not a jōyō kanji, newspapers and textbooks mostly write ふ化. In everyday speech one often says more simply 卵がかえる. Distinguish 羽化, the emergence of an insect from the pupa.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"テントウムシはアブラムシの天敵である。","romaji":"Tentōmushi wa aburamushi no tenteki de aru.","german":"Ladybugs are natural enemies of aphids.","furigana":"てんてき"},{"japanese":"この島には天敵がいないため、外来種のネズミが急増した。","romaji":"Kono shima ni wa tenteki ga inai tame, gairaishu no nezumi ga kyūzō shita.","german":"Because there are no natural enemies on this island, the introduced rats have multiplied rapidly.","furigana":"しま|てんてき|がいらいしゅ|きゅうぞう"},{"japanese":"湿気は髪のくせが強い私にとって天敵だ。","romaji":"Shikke wa kami no kuse ga tsuyoi watashi ni totte tenteki da.","german":"With my stubbornly wavy hair, humidity is my arch-enemy.","furigana":"しっけ|かみ|つよ|わたし|てんてき"}],"notes":"天敵 is the \"natural enemy\" of a species, one that eats or parasitizes it: AはBの天敵だ, 天敵がいない. In agriculture, one speaks of 天敵を利用した害虫駆除 (biological pest control). Colloquially and jokingly, it also denotes a person or thing one simply cannot get along with: 彼は私の天敵だ \"He is my arch-enemy\". The general word for \"enemy\" is 敵.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"被食者は捕食者から逃れるための防御機構を持つ。","romaji":"Hishokusha wa hoshokusha kara nogareru tame no bōgyo kikō wo motsu.","german":"Prey animals have defense mechanisms to escape from predators.","furigana":"ひしょくしゃ|ほしょくしゃ|のが|ぼうぎょきこう|も"},{"japanese":"捕食者と被食者の数は、互いに影響し合いながら増減する。","romaji":"Hoshokusha to hishokusha no kazu wa, tagai ni eikyō shiai nagara zōgen suru.","german":"The numbers of predators and prey rise and fall, influencing each other.","furigana":"ほしょくしゃ|ひしょくしゃ|かず|たが|えいきょう|あ|ぞうげん"},{"japanese":"シマウマは群れで行動することで、被食者としての危険を減らしている。","romaji":"Shimauma wa mure de kōdō suru koto de, hishokusha to shite no kiken o herashite iru.","german":"Zebras reduce their danger as prey animals by living in herds.","furigana":"む|こうどう|ひしょくしゃ|きけん|へ"}],"notes":"被食者 is the ecological technical term for the animal that is eaten (被 = \"to suffer\", 食 = \"to eat\") and forms a pair with 捕食者 \"predator\"; the relationship is called 捕食・被食の関係 or 食う・食われるの関係. The word occurs almost only in biology textbooks and technical texts. In everyday speech one says 獲物 \"prey\" (from the hunter's viewpoint) or paraphrases with 食べられる側.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"捕食者の存在が生態系のバランスを保つ。","romaji":"Hoshokusha no sonzai ga seitaikei no baransu wo tamotsu.","german":"The presence of predators maintains the balance of the ecosystem.","furigana":"ほしょくしゃ|そんざい|せいたいけい|たも"},{"japanese":"オオカミが絶滅したことで、シカには捕食者がいなくなった。","romaji":"Ōkami ga zetsumetsu shita koto de, shika ni wa hoshokusha ga inaku natta.","german":"Since wolves went extinct, deer no longer have any predators.","furigana":"ぜつめつ|ほしょくしゃ"},{"japanese":"シャチは海の頂点捕食者として知られている。","romaji":"Shachi wa umi no chōten hoshokusha to shite shirarete iru.","german":"Orcas are known as apex predators of the sea.","furigana":"うみ|ちょうてんほしょくしゃ|し"}],"notes":"捕食者 is the \"predator\" in ecology, that is, an animal that catches and eats other animals; the related verb is 捕食する. The counterpart is 被食者. The word is technical; in everyday speech one rather says 肉食動物 or names the animal specifically. A set combination is 頂点捕食者 \"apex predator\" for animals at the top of the food chain.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"河口での堆積作用が三角州を形成する。","romaji":"Kakō de no taiseki sayō ga sankakusu wo keisei suru.","german":"Deposition at the river mouth forms a delta.","furigana":"かこう|たいせきさよう|さんかくす|けいせい"},{"japanese":"長い年月をかけて堆積した地層が、崖にはっきり見える。","romaji":"Nagai nengetsu o kakete taiseki shita chisō ga, gake ni hakkiri mieru.","german":"Strata deposited over a long period are clearly visible on the cliff.","furigana":"なが|ねんげつ|たいせき|ちそう|がけ|み"},{"japanese":"ダムの底に土砂が堆積して、貯水量が減っている。","romaji":"Damu no soko ni dosha ga taiseki shite, chosuiryō ga hette iru.","german":"Sediment is accumulating at the bottom of the dam, and the water storage capacity is decreasing.","furigana":"そこ|どしゃ|たいせき|ちょすいりょう|へ"}],"notes":"堆積 means that something piles up or is deposited; in geology it is \"sedimentation\": 土砂が堆積する, 堆積物, 堆積岩, 堆積作用. It is mostly intransitive with が; the counterpart in the geological cycle is 浸食 \"erosion\". For snow, dust or garbage it sounds literary (ごみが堆積する); in everyday speech one says 積もる or たまる. Do not confuse it with the homophone 体積 \"volume\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"波による海岸の浸食が進んでいる。","romaji":"Nami ni yoru kaigan no shinshoku ga susunde iru.","german":"Erosion of the coast by waves is progressing.","furigana":"なみ|かいがん|しんしょく|すす"},{"japanese":"川の水が長い時間をかけて岩を浸食し、深い谷ができた。","romaji":"Kawa no mizu ga nagai jikan o kakete iwa o shinshoku shi, fukai tani ga dekita.","german":"River water eroded the rock over a long time, creating a deep valley.","furigana":"かわ|みず|なが|じかん|いわ|しんしょく|ふか|たに"},{"japanese":"砂浜の浸食を防ぐため、沖に消波ブロックが置かれた。","romaji":"Sunahama no shinshoku o fusegu tame, oki ni shōha burokku ga okareta.","german":"To prevent erosion of the sandy beach, wave-dissipating blocks were placed offshore.","furigana":"すなはま|しんしょく|ふせ|おき|しょうは|お"}],"notes":"浸食 is \"erosion\" or \"wearing away\" by water, waves or wind: 波が海岸を浸食する, 浸食が進む, 浸食作用. It is used as a noun and as a transitive する-verb; the affected rock takes the passive: 岩が浸食される. Equally common is the spelling 侵食, which also stands for the figurative \"encroachment, spreading\" (市場を侵食する \"to eat into market share\"). The counterpart in the geological cycle is 堆積.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"岩石は長い年月をかけて風化する。","romaji":"Ganseki wa nagai nengetsu wo kakete fūka suru.","german":"Rocks weather over a long period of time.","furigana":"がんせき|なが|ねんげつ|ふうか"},{"japanese":"震災の記憶を風化させないよう、毎年追悼式が行われている。","romaji":"Shinsai no kioku o fūka sasenai yō, maitoshi tsuitōshiki ga okonawarete iru.","german":"A memorial service is held every year so that the memory of the earthquake disaster does not fade.","furigana":"しんさい|きおく|ふうか|まいとしついとうしき|おこな"},{"japanese":"古い石像は風化が進み、顔の表情がほとんど分からない。","romaji":"Furui sekizō wa fūka ga susumi, kao no hyōjō ga hotondo wakaranai.","german":"The old stone statue is so weathered that the expression on its face can hardly be made out.","furigana":"ふる|せきぞう|ふうか|すす|かお|ひょうじょう|わ"}],"notes":"風化 is first of all the \"weathering\" of rock by wind, rain and temperature changes: 岩石が風化する, 風化が進む. Very common in news and speeches is the figurative meaning: memories or lessons from disasters and wars \"fade\" with time, e.g. 震災の記憶を風化させてはならない. The verb is intransitive; \"to let something fall into oblivion\" is 風化させる. In chemistry it also denotes the efflorescence of crystals.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"土壌の質は農作物の生育に大きく影響する。","romaji":"Dojō no shitsu wa nōsakumotsu no seiiku ni ōkiku eikyō suru.","german":"Soil quality greatly affects the growth of crops.","furigana":"どじょう|しつ|のうさくもつ|せいいく|おお|えいきょう"},{"japanese":"工場跡地から土壌汚染が見つかった。","romaji":"Kōjō atochi kara dojō osen ga mitsukatta.","german":"Soil contamination was found at the former factory site.","furigana":"こうじょうあとち|どじょうおせん|み"},{"japanese":"上下関係が厳しい職場は、パワハラを生む土壌になりやすい。","romaji":"Jōge kankei ga kibishii shokuba wa, pawahara o umu dojō ni nariyasui.","german":"Workplaces with strict hierarchy easily become breeding grounds for power harassment.","furigana":"じょうげかんけい|きび|しょくば|う|どじょう"}],"notes":"土壌 is the technical term for \"soil, earth\" as a material and habitat, for example in agriculture and environmental protection: 土壌汚染, 土壌改良, 肥沃な土壌. In everyday speech one simply says 土 \"earth\"; 地面, by contrast, is the ground surface one stands on. Figuratively, 土壌 means \"breeding ground\" for a development: 不正を生む土壌, 才能が育つ土壌.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地殻は地球の最も外側の層である。","romaji":"Chikaku wa chikyū no mottomo sotogawa no sō de aru.","german":"The Earth's crust is the outermost layer of the planet.","furigana":"ちかく|ちきゅう|もっと|そとがわ|そう"},{"japanese":"この地域では地殻変動によって山が少しずつ隆起している。","romaji":"Kono chiiki de wa chikaku hendō ni yotte yama ga sukoshi zutsu ryūki shite iru.","german":"In this region, the mountains are gradually being uplifted by crustal movement.","furigana":"ちいき|ちかくへんどう|やま|すこ|りゅうき"},{"japanese":"選挙の結果、政界に地殻変動が起きた。","romaji":"Senkyo no kekka, seikai ni chikaku hendō ga okita.","german":"As a result of the election, a seismic shift occurred in the political world.","furigana":"せんきょ|けっか|せいかい|ちかくへんどう|お"}],"notes":"地殻 is the \"Earth's crust\", the outermost solid layer of the Earth above the マントル. Typical compounds are 地殻変動 \"crustal movement, tectonic change\" as well as 大陸地殻 and 海洋地殻. The word is technical (geology, earthquake reports). Figuratively, 地殻変動 is also used for fundamental upheavals in politics or the economy. Beware of the homophones 知覚 \"perception\" and 近く \"vicinity\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"マントルの対流がプレートの移動を引き起こす。","romaji":"Mantoru no tairyū ga purēto no idō wo hikiokosu.","german":"Convection in the mantle causes the movement of the plates.","furigana":"たいりゅう|いどう|ひ|お"},{"japanese":"地球の内部は、地殻、マントル、核の三つの層に分けられる。","romaji":"Chikyū no naibu wa, chikaku, mantoru, kaku no mittsu no sō ni wakerareru.","german":"The Earth's interior is divided into three layers: crust, mantle and core.","furigana":"ちきゅう|ないぶ|ちかく|かく|みっ|そう|わ"},{"japanese":"マグマはマントルの一部が溶けてできる。","romaji":"Maguma wa mantoru no ichibu ga tokete dekiru.","german":"Magma is formed when part of the mantle melts.","furigana":"いちぶ|と"}],"notes":"マントル is, in geology, the \"Earth's mantle\" between the 地殻 and the 核 \"core\"; typical compounds are マントル対流 \"mantle convection\" and 上部マントル／下部マントル. This loanword from English is written only in katakana. It does not refer to a \"mantle\" as a garment; for that one says コート, and for a cloak マント. Rarely it also stands for the mantle of a gas lamp (ガスマントル).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この地域の岩石は多様な鉱物を含んでいる。","romaji":"Kono chiiki no ganseki wa tayō na kōbutsu wo fukunde iru.","german":"The rocks of this region contain a variety of minerals.","furigana":"ちいき|がんせき|たよう|こうぶつ|ふく"},{"japanese":"水晶は石英という鉱物の結晶だ。","romaji":"Suishō wa sekiei to iu kōbutsu no kesshō da.","german":"Rock crystal is a crystal of the mineral quartz.","furigana":"すいしょう|せきえい|こうぶつ|けっしょう"},{"japanese":"その国は鉱物資源が豊富で、輸出の大半を占めている。","romaji":"Sono kuni wa kōbutsu shigen ga hōfu de, yushutsu no taihan o shimete iru.","german":"The country is rich in mineral resources, which make up most of its exports.","furigana":"くに|こうぶつしげん|ほうふ|ゆしゅつ|たいはん|し"}],"notes":"鉱物 is a \"mineral\" in the geological sense, that is, a naturally occurring crystalline substance such as quartz or feldspar: 鉱物を含む, 鉱物資源, 鉱物標本. To be distinguished from ミネラル, which in Japanese stands almost only for mineral nutrients in food and water (ミネラルウォーター). 鉱石, by contrast, is \"ore\", that is, rock from which metal is extracted, and 岩石 is rock, which consists of minerals.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"家庭の生ゴミから堆肥を作ることができる。","romaji":"Katei no namagomi kara taihi wo tsukuru koto ga dekiru.","german":"Compost can be made from household food waste.","furigana":"かてい|なま|たいひ|つく"},{"japanese":"落ち葉を集めて堆肥にすると、良い土ができる。","romaji":"Ochiba o atsumete taihi ni suru to, yoi tsuchi ga dekiru.","german":"If you collect fallen leaves and turn them into compost, you get good soil.","furigana":"お|ば|あつ|たいひ|よ|つち"},{"japanese":"春の植え付けの前に、畑に堆肥をすき込んだ。","romaji":"Haru no uetsuke no mae ni, hatake ni taihi o sukikonda.","german":"Before spring planting, I dug compost into the field.","furigana":"はる|う|つ|まえ|はたけ|たいひ|こ"}],"notes":"堆肥 is \"compost\" made from rotted plant remains, kitchen waste or manure: 堆肥を作る, 堆肥にする, 堆肥を畑にすき込む. It is one of the organic fertilizers; the general term for fertilizer is 肥料, and artificial fertilizer is called 化学肥料. In everyday speech one also hears the loanword コンポスト, which can also refer to the composter itself.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"雑草が繁茂して庭が荒れてしまった。","romaji":"Zassō ga hanmo shite niwa ga arete shimatta.","german":"Weeds grew rampant and the garden became overgrown.","furigana":"ざっそう|はんも|にわ|あ"},{"japanese":"熱帯雨林では、多種多様な植物が繁茂している。","romaji":"Nettai urin de wa, tashu tayō na shokubutsu ga hanmo shite iru.","german":"In the tropical rainforest, a great variety of plants grow luxuriantly.","furigana":"ねったいうりん|たしゅたよう|しょくぶつ|はんも"},{"japanese":"水温が上がると、湖に藻が繁茂して水質が悪化する。","romaji":"Suion ga agaru to, mizuumi ni mo ga hanmo shite suishitsu ga akka suru.","german":"When the water temperature rises, algae proliferate in the lake and the water quality deteriorates.","furigana":"すいおん|あ|みずうみ|も|はんも|すいしつ|あっか"}],"notes":"繁茂 describes lush, dense plant growth: 草木が繁茂する, 藻が繁茂する, 雑草の繁茂. It is an intransitive する-verb and sounds literary; in conversation one tends to say 生い茂る or 草がぼうぼうに生える. It often has a negative connotation (weeds, algae), but can also neutrally describe dense vegetation. For the multiplication of animals, on the other hand, one says 繁殖, and for thriving businesses 繁盛.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この花は日本の山岳地帯に自生している。","romaji":"Kono hana wa Nihon no sangaku chitai ni jisei shite iru.","german":"This flower grows wild in the mountainous regions of Japan.","furigana":"はな|にほん|さんがくちたい|じせい"},{"japanese":"この地域はミズバショウの自生地として知られている。","romaji":"Kono chiiki wa mizubashō no jiseichi to shite shirarete iru.","german":"This area is known as a natural habitat of the Asian skunk cabbage.","furigana":"ちいき|じせいち|し"},{"japanese":"庭に自生していたミョウガを収穫した。","romaji":"Niwa ni jisei shite ita myōga o shūkaku shita.","german":"I harvested the myoga ginger that had grown on its own in the garden.","furigana":"にわ|じせい|しゅうかく"}],"notes":"自生 means that a plant grows in a place by itself, that is, wild and without cultivation by humans: 〜に自生する, 自生地, 自生種. It mostly appears in the form 自生している or attributively as 自生の植物. The opposite is 栽培 \"cultivation\". It differs from 野生 in that 自生 is used only for plants, whereas 野生 is also used for animals (野生動物).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ラベンダーが群生する丘は観光名所になっている。","romaji":"Rabendā ga gunsei suru oka wa kankō meisho ni natte iru.","german":"The hill where lavender grows in masses has become a tourist attraction.","furigana":"ぐんせい|おか|かんこうめいしょ"},{"japanese":"この山の斜面はカタクリの群生地として有名だ。","romaji":"Kono yama no shamen wa katakuri no gunseichi to shite yūmei da.","german":"This mountain slope is famous as a growing site of dogtooth violets.","furigana":"やま|しゃめん|ぐんせいち|ゆうめい"},{"japanese":"繁殖期になると、この岩場にはウミネコが群生する。","romaji":"Hanshokuki ni naru to, kono iwaba ni wa umineko ga gunsei suru.","german":"In the breeding season, black-tailed gulls gather in colonies on these rocks.","furigana":"はんしょくき|いわば|ぐんせい"}],"notes":"群生 means that plants of one species grow close together in large numbers: 〜が群生する, 〜の群生地. It is also used for animals that live in herds or colonies; in that case the spelling 群棲 is also common. In contrast to 繁茂 \"to grow luxuriantly\", 群生 emphasizes the gathering of many specimens in one place, not the vigor of growth. 群生地 often appears on information signs in nature reserves.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"系統樹の枝分かれは種の分化を示す。","romaji":"Keitōju no edawakare wa shu no bunka wo shimesu.","german":"The branching of the phylogenetic tree shows the differentiation of species.","furigana":"けいとうじゅ|えだわ|しゅ|ぶんか|しめ"},{"japanese":"この先で道が二つに枝分かれしている。","romaji":"Kono saki de michi ga futatsu ni edawakare shite iru.","german":"The road forks in two just ahead.","furigana":"さき|みち|ふた|えだわ"},{"japanese":"梅の枝は細かく枝分かれして、花をたくさん付ける。","romaji":"Ume no eda wa komakaku edawakare shite, hana o takusan tsukeru.","german":"The branches of the plum tree branch out finely and bear many flowers.","furigana":"うめ|えだ|こま|えだわ|はな|つ"}],"notes":"枝分かれ literally means \"to split into branches\" and is used for trees, rivers, roads, family trees or veins: 道が枝分かれする, 川が二つに枝分かれする. It is an intransitive する-verb; more formal and technical is 分岐（する）, and the fork point is called 分岐点. Figuratively, 枝分かれ also describes how languages, species or fields of study develop from a common origin.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"台風の強風で果樹園に落果が相次いだ。","romaji":"Taifū no kyōfū de kajuen ni rakka ga aitsuida.","german":"Strong typhoon winds caused fruit to fall one after another in the orchards.","furigana":"たいふう|きょうふう|かじゅえん|らっか|あいつ"},{"japanese":"夏の乾燥が続くと、リンゴの落果が増える。","romaji":"Natsu no kansō ga tsuzuku to, ringo no rakka ga fueru.","german":"If the summer drought continues, more apples drop prematurely.","furigana":"なつ|かんそう|つづ|らっか|ふ"},{"japanese":"落果したミカンはジュースの原料として出荷される。","romaji":"Rakka shita mikan wa jūsu no genryō to shite shukka sareru.","german":"Fallen mandarin oranges are shipped as raw material for juice.","furigana":"らっか|げんりょう|しゅっか"}],"notes":"落果 is a technical term from fruit growing and agriculture for the premature falling of fruit from the tree (due to storms, drought or natural thinning), and also for the fallen fruit itself: 落果する, 落果が相次ぐ, 落果防止. It is very common in damage reports after typhoons. In everyday speech one says 実が落ちる. Do not confuse it with the homophones 落下 \"falling down\" and 落花 \"falling blossoms\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"着生植物は他の樹木の上で生育する。","romaji":"Chakusei shokubutsu wa hoka no jumoku no ue de seiiku suru.","german":"Epiphytes grow on other trees.","furigana":"ちゃくせいしょくぶつ|ほか|じゅもく|うえ|せいいく"},{"japanese":"ランの多くは木の幹に根を張る着生植物だ。","romaji":"Ran no ōku wa ki no miki ni ne o haru chakusei shokubutsu da.","german":"Many orchids are epiphytes that anchor their roots on tree trunks.","furigana":"おお|き|みき|ね|は|ちゃくせいしょくぶつ"},{"japanese":"着生植物は寄生植物と違い、宿主から栄養を奪わない。","romaji":"Chakusei shokubutsu wa kisei shokubutsu to chigai, shukushu kara eiyō o ubawanai.","german":"Unlike parasitic plants, epiphytes do not take nutrients from their host.","furigana":"ちゃくせいしょくぶつ|きせいしょくぶつ|ちが|しゅくしゅ|えいよう|うば"}],"notes":"着生植物 are \"epiphytes\", that is, plants that grow on other plants (mostly tree trunks and branches) or on rocks without taking nutrients from them; many orchids, ferns and bromeliads belong here. 着生 means \"to attach oneself and grow\" (〜に着生する). The difference from 寄生植物 \"parasitic plant\", which takes nutrients from its host, is important. The word is technical, but also appears in connection with houseplants and horticulture.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"多肉植物は葉に水分を蓄える。","romaji":"Taniku shokubutsu wa ha ni suibun wo takuwaeru.","german":"Succulents store water in their leaves.","furigana":"たにくしょくぶつ|は|すいぶん|たくわ"},{"japanese":"多肉植物は水やりが少なくて済むので、忙しい人にも育てやすい。","romaji":"Taniku shokubutsu wa mizuyari ga sukunakute sumu node, isogashii hito ni mo sodateyasui.","german":"Succulents need little watering, so they are easy to grow even for busy people.","furigana":"たにくしょくぶつ|みず|すく|す|いそが|ひと|そだ"},{"japanese":"多肉植物は水のやりすぎで根腐れすることが多い。","romaji":"Taniku shokubutsu wa mizu no yarisugi de negusare suru koto ga ōi.","german":"Succulents often suffer root rot from too much watering.","furigana":"たにくしょくぶつ|みず||おお"}],"notes":"多肉植物 are \"succulents\", that is, plants with thick, fleshy leaves, stems or roots in which they store water (多肉 = \"much flesh\"). Cacti (サボテン) belong to them botanically, but in Japanese they are often named separately: サボテンと多肉植物. The word is well known in everyday life because succulents are popular houseplants; among enthusiasts one says simply 多肉.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"食虫植物は昆虫を捕らえて栄養を得る。","romaji":"Shokuchū shokubutsu wa konchū wo toraete eiyō wo eru.","german":"Carnivorous plants catch insects to obtain nutrients.","furigana":"しょくちゅうしょくぶつ|こんちゅう|と|えいよう|え"},{"japanese":"ハエトリグサは葉を素早く閉じて虫を捕まえる食虫植物だ。","romaji":"Haetorigusa wa ha o subayaku tojite mushi o tsukamaeru shokuchū shokubutsu da.","german":"The Venus flytrap is a carnivorous plant that catches insects by snapping its leaves shut at lightning speed.","furigana":"は|すばや|と|むし|つか|しょくちゅうしょくぶつ"},{"japanese":"食虫植物の多くは、栄養の少ない湿地に生えている。","romaji":"Shokuchū shokubutsu no ōku wa, eiyō no sukunai shitchi ni haete iru.","german":"Many carnivorous plants grow in nutrient-poor wetlands.","furigana":"しょくちゅうしょくぶつ|おお|えいよう|すく|しっち|は"}],"notes":"食虫植物 are \"carnivorous plants\" (literally \"insect-eating plants\") that catch and digest insects and other small animals to obtain nitrogen, e.g. ハエトリグサ (Venus flytrap), ウツボカズラ (pitcher plant) or モウセンゴケ (sundew). Typical verbs are 虫を捕らえる／捕まえる and 消化する. The word is common both in textbooks and in everyday life (garden centers, children's nonfiction books).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この杉は樹齢三千年を超えると推定されている。","romaji":"Kono sugi wa jurei sanzennen wo koeru to suitei sarete iru.","german":"This cedar is estimated to be over three thousand years old.","furigana":"すぎ|じゅれいさんぜんねん|こ|すいてい"},{"japanese":"年輪を数えると、この木の樹齢は約百二十年だと分かった。","romaji":"Nenrin o kazoeru to, kono ki no jurei wa yaku hyakunijūnen da to wakatta.","german":"Counting the annual rings showed that this tree is about 120 years old.","furigana":"ねんりん|かぞ|き|じゅれい|やくひゃくにじゅうねん|わ"},{"japanese":"神社の境内には樹齢数百年のクスノキが立っている。","romaji":"Jinja no keidai ni wa jurei sūhyakunen no kusunoki ga tatte iru.","german":"A camphor tree several hundred years old stands on the shrine grounds.","furigana":"じんじゃ|けいだい|じゅれいすうひゃくねん|た"}],"notes":"樹齢 is the \"age of a tree\" and usually comes directly before a statement of age: 樹齢千年の杉, 樹齢は約三百年, 樹齢百年を超える. It is determined by the 年輪 \"annual rings\" (年輪を数える). For people and animals one uses 年齢, for buildings 築年数. Particularly old trees at shrines are often described with their 樹齢 on a sign.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"強風による落枝に注意が必要だ。","romaji":"Kyōfū ni yoru rakushi ni chūi ga hitsuyō da.","german":"One must beware of branches falling in strong winds.","furigana":"きょうふう|らくし|ちゅうい|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"公園の古い桜は落枝の恐れがあるため、周りが立ち入り禁止になった。","romaji":"Kōen no furui sakura wa rakushi no osore ga aru tame, mawari ga tachiiri kinshi ni natta.","german":"Because branches might fall from the old cherry trees in the park, the area around them was closed off.","furigana":"こうえん|ふる|さくら|らくし|おそ|まわ|た|い|きんし"},{"japanese":"森の地面には落葉や落枝が厚く積もっている。","romaji":"Mori no jimen ni wa rakuyō ya rakushi ga atsuku tsumotte iru.","german":"A thick layer of fallen leaves and branches lies on the forest floor.","furigana":"もり|じめん|らくよう|らくし|あつ|つ"}],"notes":"落枝 is a rare technical term from forestry, ecology and tree care for branches that break off or fall from a tree, or for the fallen branches themselves. It is found in warning notices from parks and road authorities (落枝に注意) and in ecological texts together with 落葉 as part of the forest litter. In everyday speech one says 枝が落ちる, 落ちた枝 or 枯れ枝. 落石 \"rockfall\" is formed analogously.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"倒木が登山道を塞いでいた。","romaji":"Tōboku ga tozandō wo fusaide ita.","german":"A fallen tree was blocking the hiking trail.","furigana":"とうぼく|とざんどう|ふさ"},{"japanese":"台風の影響で倒木が電線にかかり、停電が発生した。","romaji":"Taifū no eikyō de tōboku ga densen ni kakari, teiden ga hassei shita.","german":"Because of the typhoon, a fallen tree landed on a power line and caused a power outage.","furigana":"たいふう|えいきょう|とうぼく|でんせん|ていでん|はっせい"},{"japanese":"苔むした倒木の上から、若い木が育っている。","romaji":"Kokemushita tōboku no ue kara, wakai ki ga sodatte iru.","german":"Young trees are growing from a moss-covered fallen log.","furigana":"こけ|とうぼく|うえ|わか|き|そだ"}],"notes":"倒木 is a tree that has fallen over, for example after a storm, under snow load or from old age: 倒木が道を塞ぐ, 倒木の撤去, 倒木の被害. It is a noun; the falling over itself is described with 木が倒れる. It is very common in typhoon news and in notices for hikers. In ecology, 倒木更新 is used when young trees germinate on a decaying trunk.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"林床には多種多様な菌類が生息している。","romaji":"Rinshō ni wa tashu tayō na kinrui ga seisoku shite iru.","german":"A wide variety of fungi live on the forest floor.","furigana":"りんしょう|たしゅたよう|きんるい|せいそく"},{"japanese":"木を間引くと林床に光が届き、草花が育つようになる。","romaji":"Ki o mabiku to rinshō ni hikari ga todoki, kusabana ga sodatsu yō ni naru.","german":"When trees are thinned out, light reaches the forest floor and flowers can grow.","furigana":"き|まび|りんしょう|ひかり|とど|くさばな|そだ"},{"japanese":"春先の林床には、木の葉が茂る前に咲く花が多い。","romaji":"Harusaki no rinshō ni wa, ki no ha ga shigeru mae ni saku hana ga ōi.","german":"In early spring, many flowers bloom on the forest floor before the leaves come out on the trees.","furigana":"はるさき|りんしょう|き|は|しげ|まえ|さ|はな|おお"}],"notes":"林床 is a technical term of forestry and vegetation science for the \"forest floor\" as a habitat, that is, the ground layer beneath the tree crowns with leaf litter, mosses, herbs and fungi: 林床植物, 林床に光が差し込む. It occurs mainly in ecological texts and nature guides. In everyday speech one says 森の地面 or 林の中の地面. The low vegetation on the forest floor is called 下草.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"珊瑚礁は海洋生物の重要な生息地である。","romaji":"Sangoshō wa kaiyō seibutsu no jūyō na seisokuchi de aru.","german":"Coral reefs are important habitats for marine life.","furigana":"さんごしょう|かいようせいぶつ|じゅうよう|せいそくち"},{"japanese":"海水温の上昇で、沖縄の珊瑚礁の白化が進んでいる。","romaji":"Kaisuion no jōshō de, Okinawa no sangoshō no hakka ga susunde iru.","german":"Because of rising sea temperatures, coral bleaching is progressing on Okinawa's reefs.","furigana":"かいすいおん|じょうしょう|おきなわ|さんごしょう|はっか|すす"},{"japanese":"珊瑚礁の海でシュノーケリングを楽しんだ。","romaji":"Sangoshō no umi de shunōkeringu o tanoshinda.","german":"I enjoyed snorkeling in the sea over the coral reef.","furigana":"さんごしょう|うみ|たの"}],"notes":"珊瑚礁 is a \"coral reef\"; 礁 means \"reef, underwater rock\". Since 珊瑚 is not a jōyō kanji, newspapers and school textbooks usually write サンゴ礁. Typical expressions are 珊瑚礁が広がる, 珊瑚礁の白化 \"coral bleaching\" and 珊瑚礁を保護する. The individual coral or the material is called 珊瑚／サンゴ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"潮汐は月と太陽の引力によって引き起こされる。","romaji":"Chōseki wa tsuki to taiyō no inryoku ni yotte hikiokosareru.","german":"The tides are caused by the gravitational pull of the moon and the sun.","furigana":"ちょうせき|つき|たいよう|いんりょく|ひ|お"},{"japanese":"潮汐を利用した発電の研究が進んでいる。","romaji":"Chōseki o riyō shita hatsuden no kenkyū ga susunde iru.","german":"Research on power generation using tides is progressing.","furigana":"ちょうせき|りよう|はつでん|けんきゅう|すす"},{"japanese":"釣りに行く前に、潮汐表で満潮の時刻を確かめた。","romaji":"Tsuri ni iku mae ni, chōsekihyō de manchō no jikoku o tashikameta.","german":"Before going fishing, I checked the tide table for the time of high tide.","furigana":"つ|い|まえ|ちょうせきひょう|まんちょう|じこく|たし"}],"notes":"潮汐 is the scientific term for the tides, that is, ebb and flow as a whole. It appears mainly in technical texts and compounds: 潮汐力, 潮汐発電, 潮汐表. In everyday speech one says 潮の満ち引き, 干満 or simply 潮; high tide is called 満潮, low tide 干潮.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"満潮時には砂浜が海水に覆われる。","romaji":"Manchō ji ni wa sunahama ga kaisui ni ōwareru.","german":"At high tide, the sandy beach is covered by seawater.","furigana":"まんちょうじ|すなはま|かいすい|おお"},{"japanese":"満潮になると、この島へ渡る道は海に沈んでしまう。","romaji":"Manchō ni naru to, kono shima e wataru michi wa umi ni shizunde shimau.","german":"When the tide comes in, the road to this island sinks into the sea.","furigana":"まんちょう|しま|わた|みち|うみ|しず"},{"japanese":"今日の満潮は午後三時ごろだ。","romaji":"Kyō no manchō wa gogo sanji goro da.","german":"Today's high tide is around three in the afternoon.","furigana":"きょう|まんちょう|ごごさんじ"}],"notes":"満潮 is the \"high tide\" or high-water level of the sea; its counterpart is 干潮 \"low tide\". Typical expressions are 満潮時, 満潮になる, 満潮を迎える; tide tables call it 満潮時刻. A synonym is 満ち潮, which emphasizes more the rising of the water. Do not confuse it with 高潮 \"storm surge\", a dangerous rise in sea level caused by typhoons.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"干潮時に潮干狩りを楽しむ人が多い。","romaji":"Kanchō ji ni shiohigari wo tanoshimu hito ga ōi.","german":"Many people enjoy shellfish gathering at low tide.","furigana":"かんちょうじ|しおひが|たの|ひと|おお"},{"japanese":"干潮になると、岩場に取り残された小さな魚やカニが見られる。","romaji":"Kanchō ni naru to, iwaba ni torinokosareta chiisana sakana ya kani ga mirareru.","german":"At low tide, you can see small fish and crabs left behind among the rocks.","furigana":"かんちょう|いわば|と|のこ|ちい|さかな|み"},{"japanese":"干潮で水位が下がり、船が港から出られなくなった。","romaji":"Kanchō de suii ga sagari, fune ga minato kara derarenaku natta.","german":"The water level dropped due to the low tide, and the boat could no longer leave the harbor.","furigana":"かんちょう|すいい|さ|ふね|みなと|で"}],"notes":"干潮 is the \"low tide\" or low-water level; its counterpart is 満潮. Typical are 干潮時, 干潮になる and 干潮の時刻. Tidal flats exposed at low tide are called 干潟, and the shellfish gathering at low tide that is popular in Japan is 潮干狩り. A synonym is 引き潮, which emphasizes more the receding of the water.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"暖かい海流が沿岸の気候を穏やかにする。","romaji":"Atatakai kairyū ga engan no kikō wo odayaka ni suru.","german":"Warm ocean currents make the coastal climate milder.","furigana":"あたた|かいりゅう|えんがん|きこう|おだ"},{"japanese":"日本の近海では、暖流の黒潮と寒流の親潮がぶつかっている。","romaji":"Nihon no kinkai de wa, danryū no Kuroshio to kanryū no Oyashio ga butsukatte iru.","german":"In the waters off Japan, the warm Kuroshio and the cold Oyashio meet.","furigana":"にほん|きんかい|だんりゅう|くろしお|かんりゅう|おやしお"},{"japanese":"瓶に入れた手紙が海流に乗って、遠い国の海岸に流れ着いた。","romaji":"Bin ni ireta tegami ga kairyū ni notte, tōi kuni no kaigan ni nagaretsuita.","german":"A letter in a bottle rode the ocean current and drifted ashore in a distant country.","furigana":"びん|い|てがみ|かいりゅう|の|とお|くに|かいがん|なが|つ"}],"notes":"海流 is a large-scale, steady \"ocean current\", e.g. the warm 黒潮 (日本海流) and the cold 親潮 (千島海流). A distinction is made between 暖流 and 寒流; typical are 海流に乗る and 海流に流される. Small-scale currents along the coast, on the other hand, are called 潮流 (tidal current) or, in everyday speech, 潮の流れ; the dangerous rip current at a swimming beach is 離岸流.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"深海にはまだ未知の生物が多く生息している。","romaji":"Shinkai ni wa mada michi no seibutsu ga ōku seisoku shite iru.","german":"Many unknown organisms still live in the deep sea.","furigana":"しんかい|みち|せいぶつ|おお|せいそく"},{"japanese":"深海魚の中には、自ら光を放つものもいる。","romaji":"Shinkaigyo no naka ni wa, mizukara hikari o hanatsu mono mo iru.","german":"Some deep-sea fish emit light by themselves.","furigana":"しんかいぎょ|なか|みずか|ひかり|はな"},{"japanese":"無人探査機が水深六千メートルの深海を調査した。","romaji":"Mujin tansaki ga suishin rokusen mētoru no shinkai o chōsa shita.","german":"An unmanned probe surveyed the deep sea at a depth of 6,000 meters.","furigana":"むじんたんさき|すいしんろくせん|しんかい|ちょうさ"}],"notes":"深海 is the \"deep sea\", usually from about 200 meters down, where hardly any sunlight reaches. It is often used as a modifier: 深海魚, 深海生物, 深海探査, 深海の水圧. The opposite is 浅海, or in everyday speech 浅い海. The word is neutral and, thanks to aquariums and nature documentaries, also familiar in everyday life.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"山麓の湧水は清らかで飲料に適している。","romaji":"Sanroku no yūsui wa kiyoraka de inryō ni tekishite iru.","german":"The spring water at the foot of the mountain is pure and suitable for drinking.","furigana":"さんろく|ゆうすい|きよ|いんりょう|てき"},{"japanese":"この町では、湧水を使った豆腐作りが盛んだ。","romaji":"Kono machi de wa, yūsui o tsukatta tōfu-zukuri ga sakan da.","german":"In this town, tofu making using spring water thrives.","furigana":"まち|ゆうすい|つか|とうふづく|さか"},{"japanese":"トンネル工事中に大量の湧水が発生し、作業が中断された。","romaji":"Tonneru kōjichū ni tairyō no yūsui ga hassei shi, sagyō ga chūdan sareta.","german":"A large amount of groundwater gushed in during tunnel construction, and work was interrupted.","furigana":"こうじちゅう|たいりょう|ゆうすい|はっせい|さぎょう|ちゅうだん"}],"notes":"湧水 is water that wells up out of the ground by itself, that is, \"spring water\" or a spring: 湧水地, 湧水群, 湧水を汲む. It sounds literary and often appears on signs and in travel guides; in everyday speech one says 湧き水. More generally, \"spring\" is 泉, and a hot spring is 温泉. In construction, 湧水 also refers to groundwater seeping in, for example during tunnel work.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"川の水が砂利の下に潜って伏流水となり、下流で再び湧き出している。","romaji":"Kawa no mizu ga jari no shita ni mogutte fukuryūsui to nari, karyū de futatabi wakidashite iru.","german":"River water sinks under the gravel, flows on as subsurface water, and wells up again downstream.","furigana":"かわ|みず|じゃり|した|もぐ|ふくりゅうすい|かりゅう|ふたた|わ|だ"},{"japanese":"この酒蔵では、富士山の伏流水を仕込み水に使っている。","romaji":"Kono sakagura de wa, Fujisan no fukuryūsui o shikomimizu ni tsukatte iru.","german":"This sake brewery uses the subsurface flow from Mt. Fuji as brewing water.","furigana":"さかぐら|ふじさん|ふくりゅうすい|しこ|みず|つか"},{"japanese":"市の水道は、川の伏流水を井戸でくみ上げて利用している。","romaji":"Shi no suidō wa, kawa no fukuryūsui o ido de kumiagete riyō shite iru.","german":"The city's water supply pumps up the river's subsurface flow through wells and uses it.","furigana":"し|すいどう|かわ|ふくりゅうすい|いど|あ|りよう"}],"notes":"伏流水 is water that flows through gravel and sand beneath a riverbed or alluvial fan, that is, a river that in places \"goes underground\" (伏 = \"to hide\"). The related verb is 伏流する, and typical is 伏流水が湧き出る. It is popular as drinking water or in sake breweries and is emphasized in advertising (富士山の伏流水). General groundwater is called 地下水; 伏流水 is a special case of it that is closely tied to a river.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地下の水脈を探し当てて井戸を掘った。","romaji":"Chika no suimyaku wo sagashiatete ido wo hotta.","german":"I found the underground water vein and dug a well.","furigana":"ちか|すいみゃく|さが|あ|いど|ほ"},{"japanese":"トンネル工事で水脈を断ち切ってしまい、周辺の井戸が枯れた。","romaji":"Tonneru kōji de suimyaku o tachikitte shimai, shūhen no ido ga kareta.","german":"The tunnel construction cut off a water vein, and the wells in the surrounding area dried up.","furigana":"こうじ|すいみゃく|た|き|しゅうへん|いど|か"},{"japanese":"ダウジングで水脈を探す方法は、科学的には証明されていない。","romaji":"Daujingu de suimyaku o sagasu hōhō wa, kagakuteki ni wa shōmei sarete inai.","german":"It has not been scientifically proven that dowsing can locate water veins.","furigana":"すいみゃく|さが|ほうほう|かがくてき|しょうめい"}],"notes":"水脈 is an underground \"water vein\", that is, a watercourse in the ground that one looks for when building a well: 水脈を探す, 水脈に当たる, 水脈を掘り当てる. The second meaning \"navigable channel, waterway\" is rare. More general is 地下水; a well is called 井戸. In literary language, 水脈 occasionally also stands for a hidden, continuing current or tradition.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地下水の汲み上げ過ぎが地盤沈下を引き起こす。","romaji":"Chikasui no kumiagesugi ga jiban chinka o hikiokosu.","german":"Excessive pumping of groundwater causes land subsidence.","furigana":"ちかすい|く|あ|す|じばんちんか|ひ|お"},{"japanese":"この町の水道水は地下水を使っているので、とてもおいしい。","romaji":"Kono machi no suidōsui wa chikasui o tsukatte iru node, totemo oishii.","german":"The tap water in this town comes from groundwater, so it tastes very good.","furigana":"まち|すいどうすい|ちかすい|つか"},{"japanese":"工場の排水で、周辺の地下水が汚染された。","romaji":"Kōjō no haisui de, shūhen no chikasui ga osen sareta.","german":"The groundwater in the surrounding area was contaminated by the factory's wastewater.","furigana":"こうじょう|はいすい|しゅうへん|ちかすい|おせん"}],"notes":"地下水 is water in the soil beneath the earth's surface. Typical combinations: 地下水を汲み上げる \"to extract groundwater\", 地下水が湧く, 地下水汚染, 地下水位. Different from these are 湧き水 (spring water emerging at the surface) and 井戸水 (well water). The word is neutral and appears in environmental topics, the news and everyday life.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"永久凍土の融解がメタンガスを放出させる。","romaji":"Eikyū tōdo no yūkai ga metan gasu wo hōshutsu saseru.","german":"The thawing of permafrost releases methane gas.","furigana":"えいきゅうとうど|ゆうかい|ほうしゅつ"},{"japanese":"シベリアの永久凍土からマンモスの死骸が見つかった。","romaji":"Shiberia no eikyū tōdo kara manmosu no shigai ga mitsukatta.","german":"The carcass of a mammoth was found in the Siberian permafrost.","furigana":"えいきゅうとうど|しがい|み"},{"japanese":"永久凍土の上に建てられた家が、地盤の融解で傾いている。","romaji":"Eikyū tōdo no ue ni taterareta ie ga, jiban no yūkai de katamuite iru.","german":"Houses built on permafrost are tilting because the ground is thawing.","furigana":"えいきゅうとうど|うえ|た|いえ|じばん|ゆうかい|かたむ"}],"notes":"永久凍土 is \"permafrost\", that is, ground that stays frozen continuously for at least two years, for example in Siberia, Alaska or Canada. The word is composed of 永久 \"permanent\" and 凍土 \"frozen ground\". In news about climate change, 永久凍土が溶ける／融解する in particular is common, because methane and carbon dioxide are released in the process. Further compounds are 永久凍土層 and 永久凍土地帯.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"堆積岩は長い年月をかけて形成される。","romaji":"Taisekigan wa nagai nengetsu wo kakete keisei sareru.","german":"Sedimentary rocks are formed over long periods of time.","furigana":"たいせきがん|なが|ねんげつ|けいせい"},{"japanese":"堆積岩の中からは、しばしば化石が見つかる。","romaji":"Taisekigan no naka kara wa, shibashiba kaseki ga mitsukaru.","german":"Fossils are often found in sedimentary rock.","furigana":"たいせきがん|なか|かせき|み"},{"japanese":"石灰岩は、サンゴや貝殻が積もってできた堆積岩だ。","romaji":"Sekkaigan wa, sango ya kaigara ga tsumotte dekita taisekigan da.","german":"Limestone is a sedimentary rock formed from accumulated corals and shells.","furigana":"せっかいがん|かいがら|つ|たいせきがん"}],"notes":"堆積岩 is \"sedimentary rock\", which forms when sand, mud, gravel or the remains of living things settle (堆積) and become consolidated. Examples are 砂岩 (sandstone), 泥岩 (mudstone), 礫岩 (conglomerate) and 石灰岩 (limestone). Together with 火成岩 and 変成岩, it makes up the three main groups of rocks. Typical of 堆積岩 are stratification (地層) and the occurrence of 化石.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"花崗岩は代表的な火成岩である。","romaji":"Kakōgan wa daihyōteki na kaseigan de aru.","german":"Granite is a typical igneous rock.","furigana":"|だいひょうてき|かせいがん"},{"japanese":"火成岩は、マグマが冷えて固まることでできる。","romaji":"Kaseigan wa, maguma ga hiete katamaru koto de dekiru.","german":"Igneous rocks are formed when magma cools and solidifies.","furigana":"かせいがん|ひ|かた"},{"japanese":"火成岩は、冷え方の違いによって火山岩と深成岩に分けられる。","romaji":"Kaseigan wa, hiekata no chigai ni yotte kazangan to shinseigan ni wakerareru.","german":"Igneous rocks are divided into volcanic rocks and plutonic rocks according to how they cooled.","furigana":"かせいがん|ひ|かた|ちが|かざんがん|しんせいがん|わ"}],"notes":"火成岩 is \"igneous rock\", that is, rock formed from solidified magma. A distinction is made between 火山岩 (solidified quickly at the surface, e.g. 玄武岩 \"basalt\", 安山岩 \"andesite\") and 深成岩 (solidified slowly at depth, e.g. 花崗岩 \"granite\"). Together with 堆積岩 and 変成岩, it makes up the three main groups of rocks. The word belongs to geography and geology classes.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大理石は石灰岩が変化した変成岩である。","romaji":"Dairiseki wa sekkaigan ga henka shita henseigan de aru.","german":"Marble is a metamorphic rock that changed from limestone.","furigana":"だいりせき|せっかいがん|へんか|へんせいがん"},{"japanese":"変成岩は、高い温度や圧力によって岩石が変化してできる。","romaji":"Henseigan wa, takai ondo ya atsuryoku ni yotte ganseki ga henka shite dekiru.","german":"Metamorphic rocks are formed when rock is transformed by high temperature and pressure.","furigana":"へんせいがん|たか|おんど|あつりょく|がんせき|へんか"},{"japanese":"この地域には、プレートの沈み込みによってできた変成岩が広く分布している。","romaji":"Kono chiiki ni wa, purēto no shizumikomi ni yotte dekita henseigan ga hiroku bunpu shite iru.","german":"Metamorphic rocks formed by plate subduction are widely distributed in this region.","furigana":"ちいき|しず|こ|へんせいがん|ひろ|ぶんぷ"}],"notes":"変成岩 is \"metamorphic rock\", that is, rock that has been transformed by high pressure and high temperature inside the Earth without melting; the process is called 変成作用. Well-known examples are 大理石 (marble from limestone) and 片麻岩 (gneiss). Be careful with the spelling: 変成 \"transformation\" is not to be confused with the identically read 編成 \"organization, formation\" and 変性 \"denaturation\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大雪の後は雪崩の危険性が高まる。","romaji":"Ōyuki no ato wa nadare no kikensei ga takamaru.","german":"After heavy snowfall, the risk of avalanches rises.","furigana":"おおゆき|あと|なだれ|きけんせい|たか"},{"japanese":"スキー場の外で雪崩に巻き込まれ、二人が行方不明になった。","romaji":"Sukījō no soto de nadare ni makikomare, futari ga yukue fumei ni natta.","german":"Two people were caught in an avalanche outside the ski area and are missing.","furigana":"じょう|そと|なだれ|ま|こ|ふたり|ゆくえふめい"},{"japanese":"セールの開始と同時に、客が雪崩を打って店内に入ってきた。","romaji":"Sēru no kaishi to dōji ni, kyaku ga nadare o utte tennai ni haitte kita.","german":"The moment the sale began, customers poured into the store in a rush.","furigana":"かいし|どうじ|きゃく|なだれ|う|てんない|はい"}],"notes":"雪崩 is an \"avalanche\"; typical expressions are 雪崩が起きる／発生する, 雪崩に巻き込まれる, 雪崩注意報 as well as 表層雪崩 and 全層雪崩. The reading なだれ is a special reading (熟字訓) and must be learned as a whole. Figuratively, 雪崩 stands for a mass of people or things suddenly rushing in: 雪崩を打って \"in droves, all at once\", 雪崩現象 \"avalanche effect\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"山頂から町全体を鳥瞰することができた。","romaji":"Sanchō kara machi zentai o chōkan suru koto ga dekita.","german":"From the summit, I could take in the whole town at a glance.","furigana":"さんちょう|まちぜんたい|ちょうかん"},{"japanese":"この鳥瞰図を見れば、城下町の構造がよく分かる。","romaji":"Kono chōkanzu o mireba, jōkamachi no kōzō ga yoku wakaru.","german":"If you look at this bird's-eye view map, you can clearly see the layout of the castle town.","furigana":"ちょうかんず|み|じょうかまち|こうぞう|わ"},{"japanese":"本書は日本の近代史を鳥瞰的にまとめている。","romaji":"Honsho wa Nihon no kindaishi o chōkanteki ni matomete iru.","german":"This book gives a broad overview of modern Japanese history.","furigana":"ほんしょ|にほん|きんだいし|ちょうかんてき"}],"notes":"鳥瞰 means \"to look over from above from a bird's-eye perspective\" (literally \"to look down like a bird\") and is a transitive する-verb: 町を鳥瞰する. It is common in compounds: 鳥瞰図 \"bird's-eye view picture, panoramic map\", 鳥瞰的な視点. Figuratively, it means to gain a general overview of a topic, for example in book titles such as 〜史を鳥瞰する. The word is literary; a synonym that is more common today is 俯瞰.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"気候変動は人類が直面する最大の課題の一つである。","romaji":"Kikō hendō wa jinrui ga chokumen suru saidai no kadai no hitotsu de aru.","german":"Climate change is one of the greatest challenges facing humanity.","furigana":"きこうへんどう|じんるい|ちょくめん|さいだい|かだい|ひと"},{"japanese":"気候変動の影響で、各地で豪雨や猛暑が増えている。","romaji":"Kikō hendō no eikyō de, kakuchi de gōu ya mōsho ga fuete iru.","german":"Because of climate change, heavy rain and extreme heat are increasing in many places.","furigana":"きこうへんどう|えいきょう|かくち|ごうう|もうしょ|ふ"},{"japanese":"政府は気候変動対策として、再生可能エネルギーの導入を進めている。","romaji":"Seifu wa kikō hendō taisaku to shite, saisei kanō enerugī no dōnyū o susumete iru.","german":"As a measure against climate change, the government is promoting the introduction of renewable energy.","furigana":"せいふ|きこうへんどうたいさく|さいせいかのう|どうにゅう|すす"}],"notes":"気候変動 is \"climate change\" and the standard word in politics, science and the media: 気候変動対策, 気候変動の影響, 気候変動に関する政府間パネル (IPCC). Alongside it, 地球温暖化 \"global warming\" is common, which refers more narrowly to the temperature rise only; 気候危機 \"climate crisis\" emphasizes urgency. The word is a noun; as a verb one says 気候が変動する.","levelNote":"Compound: 気候 (N3) + 変動 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二酸化炭素の増加が温室効果を強めている。","romaji":"Nisanka tanso no zōka ga onshitsu kōka o tsuyomete iru.","german":"The increase in carbon dioxide is strengthening the greenhouse effect.","furigana":"にさんかたんそ|ぞうか|おんしつこうか|つよ"},{"japanese":"政府は温室効果ガスの排出を半分に減らす目標を発表した。","romaji":"Seifu wa onshitsu kōka gasu no haishutsu o hanbun ni herasu mokuhyō o happyō shita.","german":"The government announced a target of cutting greenhouse gas emissions in half.","furigana":"せいふ|おんしつこうか|はいしゅつ|はんぶん|へ|もくひょう|はっぴょう"},{"japanese":"金星は強い温室効果のせいで表面がとても熱い。","romaji":"Kinsei wa tsuyoi onshitsu kōka no sei de hyōmen ga totemo atsui.","german":"Because of a strong greenhouse effect, the surface of Venus is extremely hot.","furigana":"きんせい|つよ|おんしつこうか|ひょうめん|あつ"}],"notes":"温室効果 is the technical term for the \"greenhouse effect\" and is usually followed by の and another noun: 温室効果の仕組み. It is most often encountered in the compound 温室効果ガス \"greenhouse gas\", as in 温室効果ガスを削減する \"to reduce greenhouse gases\". The consequence, global warming, is called 地球温暖化; the two terms are often confused, but denote cause and effect. Register: news, school, technical texts.","levelNote":"Compound: 温室 (N2) + 効果 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"有害物質の排出基準が厳格化された。","romaji":"Yūgai busshitsu no haishutsu kijun ga genkakuka sareta.","german":"Emission standards for harmful substances were tightened.","furigana":"ゆうがいぶっしつ|はいしゅつきじゅん|げんかくか"},{"japanese":"輸入されたおもちゃから有害物質が検出された。","romaji":"Yunyū sareta omocha kara yūgai busshitsu ga kenshutsu sareta.","german":"Harmful substances were detected in imported toys.","furigana":"ゆにゅう|ゆうがいぶっしつ|けんしゅつ"},{"japanese":"たばこの煙には多くの有害物質が含まれている。","romaji":"Tabako no kemuri ni wa ōku no yūgai busshitsu ga fukumarete iru.","german":"Tobacco smoke contains many harmful substances.","furigana":"けむり|おお|ゆうがいぶっしつ|ふく"}],"notes":"有害物質 refers generally to \"harmful substances\", that is, substances that damage health or the environment. Typical are 有害物質を含む \"to contain harmful substances\", 有害物質が検出される \"harmful substances are detected\" and 有害物質を排出する. 有害 means \"harmful\" in the broad sense, whereas 有毒 means \"poisonous\" more narrowly; 有毒物質 is therefore a stronger expression. Typical of official language, news and product warnings.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"放射性廃棄物の最終処分場の選定が難航している。","romaji":"Hōshasei haikibutsu no saishū shobunjō no sentei ga nankō shite iru.","german":"Selecting a final disposal site for radioactive waste is proving difficult.","furigana":"ほうしゃせいはいきぶつ|さいしゅうしょぶんじょう|せんてい|なんこう"},{"japanese":"高レベル放射性廃棄物は地下深くに埋める計画だ。","romaji":"Kō reberu hōshasei haikibutsu wa chika fukaku ni umeru keikaku da.","german":"The plan is to bury high-level radioactive waste deep underground.","furigana":"こう|ほうしゃせいはいきぶつ|ちかふか|う|けいかく"},{"japanese":"病院から出る放射性廃棄物も厳しく管理されている。","romaji":"Byōin kara deru hōshasei haikibutsu mo kibishiku kanri sarete iru.","german":"Radioactive waste from hospitals is also strictly controlled.","furigana":"びょういん|で|ほうしゃせいはいきぶつ|きび|かんり"}],"notes":"放射性廃棄物 is the official expression for \"radioactive waste\" from nuclear power plants, medicine and research. A distinction is made between 高レベル and 低レベル放射性廃棄物; typical expressions are 放射性廃棄物を処分する \"to dispose of\", 地下に埋める and 最終処分場 \"final repository\". In the media and in everyday speech one also hears the colloquial 核のごみ \"nuclear garbage\". Register: news, politics, technical texts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"太陽光発電のコストが年々低下している。","romaji":"Taiyōkō hatsuden no kosuto ga nennen teika shite iru.","german":"The cost of solar power generation is falling every year.","furigana":"たいようこうはつでん|ねんねんていか"},{"japanese":"家を建てるときに屋根に太陽光発電を導入した。","romaji":"Ie o tateru toki ni yane ni taiyōkō hatsuden o dōnyū shita.","german":"When we built our house, we installed solar power generation on the roof.","furigana":"いえ|た|やね|たいようこうはつでん|どうにゅう"},{"japanese":"曇りの日は太陽光発電の発電量が大きく減る。","romaji":"Kumori no hi wa taiyōkō hatsuden no hatsudenryō ga ōkiku heru.","german":"On cloudy days, the output of solar power generation drops sharply.","furigana":"くも|ひ|たいようこうはつでん|はつでんりょう|おお|へ"}],"notes":"太陽光発電 is \"power generation using sunlight\", that is, photovoltaics. Typical are 太陽光発電を導入する \"to install photovoltaics\" and 太陽光発電のパネル; the modules themselves are usually called ソーラーパネル or 太陽光パネル. To be distinguished from it is 太陽熱, which uses the sun's heat, for example for hot water. It is used equally in the news and in everyday life.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"海上風力発電が新たなエネルギー源として注目されている。","romaji":"Kaijō fūryoku hatsuden ga arata na enerugī gen toshite chūmoku sarete iru.","german":"Offshore wind power is attracting attention as a new energy source.","furigana":"かいじょうふうりょくはつでん|あら|げん|ちゅうもく"},{"japanese":"この海岸は一年中風が強いので、風力発電に向いている。","romaji":"Kono kaigan wa ichinenjū kaze ga tsuyoi node, fūryoku hatsuden ni muite iru.","german":"Because the wind is strong all year round on this coast, it is well suited to wind power generation.","furigana":"かいがん|いちねんじゅうかぜ|つよ|ふうりょくはつでん|む"},{"japanese":"風力発電の風車の音がうるさいと住民から苦情が出た。","romaji":"Fūryoku hatsuden no fūsha no oto ga urusai to jūmin kara kujō ga deta.","german":"Residents complained that the noise from the wind power turbines was loud.","furigana":"ふうりょくはつでん|ふうしゃ|おと|じゅうみん|くじょう|で"}],"notes":"風力発電 refers to \"power generation from wind power\", not the individual installation. A single wind turbine is called 風車 or 風力発電機, and a wind farm 風力発電所 or ウィンドファーム. Offshore wind power is 洋上風力発電 or 海上風力発電, and on land one speaks of 陸上風力発電. Typical expressions: 風力発電を導入する, 風力発電に適した地域.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本では水力発電が古くから利用されてきた。","romaji":"Nihon de wa suiryoku hatsuden ga furuku kara riyō sarete kita.","german":"Hydroelectric power has been used in Japan since long ago.","furigana":"にほん|すいりょくはつでん|ふる|りよう"},{"japanese":"この国は電力のほとんどを水力発電でまかなっている。","romaji":"Kono kuni wa denryoku no hotondo o suiryoku hatsuden de makanatte iru.","german":"This country meets almost all of its electricity needs with hydroelectric power.","furigana":"くに|でんりょく|すいりょくはつでん"},{"japanese":"雨が少ない年は、水力発電の発電量が落ちる。","romaji":"Ame ga sukunai toshi wa, suiryoku hatsuden no hatsudenryō ga ochiru.","german":"In years with little rain, the output of hydroelectric power falls.","furigana":"あめ|すく|とし|すいりょくはつでん|はつでんりょう|お"}],"notes":"水力発電 is \"power generation from water power\", usually with dams; the power plant is called 水力発電所. It forms a series with 火力発電 \"thermal power\", 原子力発電 \"nuclear power\", 太陽光発電 and 風力発電. Small installations on streams or canals are called 小水力発電. Typical expressions: ダムを使った水力発電, 水力発電に頼る.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"化石燃料からの脱却が国際的な課題となっている。","romaji":"Kaseki nenryō kara no dakkyaku ga kokusaiteki na kadai to natte iru.","german":"Moving away from fossil fuels has become an international challenge.","furigana":"かせきねんりょう|だっきゃく|こくさいてき|かだい"},{"japanese":"日本はエネルギーの多くを輸入した化石燃料に頼っている。","romaji":"Nihon wa enerugī no ōku o yunyū shita kaseki nenryō ni tayotte iru.","german":"Japan relies on imported fossil fuels for much of its energy.","furigana":"にほん|おお|ゆにゅう|かせきねんりょう|たよ"},{"japanese":"化石燃料を燃やすと、二酸化炭素が発生する。","romaji":"Kaseki nenryō o moyasu to, nisanka tanso ga hassei suru.","german":"Burning fossil fuels produces carbon dioxide.","furigana":"かせきねんりょう|も|にさんかたんそ|はっせい"}],"notes":"化石燃料 is the general term for \"fossil fuels\" such as 石油, 石炭 and 天然ガス. Typical are 化石燃料に頼る/依存する \"to be dependent on fossil fuels\", 化石燃料を燃やす and 化石燃料からの脱却 \"moving away from fossil fuels\". Its counterpart in debates is 再生可能エネルギー \"renewable energy\". Register: news, politics, textbooks.","levelNote":"Compound: 化石 (N1) + 燃料 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"省エネルギー技術の開発が進められている。","romaji":"Shō enerugī gijutsu no kaihatsu ga susumerarete iru.","german":"The development of energy-saving technology is being pushed forward.","furigana":"しょう|ぎじゅつ|かいはつ|すす"},{"japanese":"古いエアコンを省エネ型に買い替えたら、電気代が下がった。","romaji":"Furui eakon o shōene-gata ni kaikaetara, denkidai ga sagatta.","german":"After I replaced my old air conditioner with an energy-saving model, my electricity bill went down.","furigana":"ふる|しょう|がた|か|か|でんきだい|さ"},{"japanese":"この建物は省エネルギーの基準を満たしていない。","romaji":"Kono tatemono wa shō enerugī no kijun o mitashite inai.","german":"This building does not meet the energy conservation standards.","furigana":"たてもの|しょう|きじゅん|み"}],"notes":"省エネルギー means \"saving energy\"; in everyday speech the short form 省エネ is almost always used, while the long form sounds official or technical. 省エネ often stands before nouns (省エネ家電 \"energy-saving appliances\", 省エネ性能) or as an adjective with 型: 省エネ型の冷蔵庫. As a verb one says 省エネする or, descriptively, エネルギーを節約する; 節電 refers more narrowly to electricity.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"循環型社会の実現に向けてリサイクルが推進されている。","romaji":"Junkan gata shakai no jitsugen ni mukete risaikuru ga suishin sarete iru.","german":"Recycling is being promoted to realize a sound material-cycle society.","furigana":"じゅんかんがたしゃかい|じつげん|む|すいしん"},{"japanese":"市は循環型社会を目指して、生ごみの堆肥化を始めた。","romaji":"Shi wa junkan gata shakai o mezashite, namagomi no taihika o hajimeta.","german":"Aiming for a sound material-cycle society, the city has started composting kitchen waste.","furigana":"し|じゅんかんがたしゃかい|めざ|なま|たいひか|はじ"},{"japanese":"授業で循環型社会について調べ、発表した。","romaji":"Jugyō de junkan gata shakai ni tsuite shirabe, happyō shita.","german":"In class, I researched the sound material-cycle society and gave a presentation.","furigana":"じゅぎょう|じゅんかんがたしゃかい|しら|はっぴょう"}],"notes":"循環型社会 is a guiding concept of policy: a \"recycling-oriented society\" that avoids, reuses and recycles waste. It appears almost always in fixed expressions such as 循環型社会の実現, 循環型社会を目指す or 循環型社会を築く. The principles behind it are the 3 Rs: リデュース, リユース, リサイクル. Today one also hears サーキュラーエコノミー \"circular economy\" alongside it. Typical of government agencies, news and textbooks.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"生態系保全のための国際条約が締結された。","romaji":"Seitaikei hozen no tame no kokusai jōyaku ga teiketsu sareta.","german":"An international treaty for ecosystem conservation was concluded.","furigana":"せいたいけいほぜん|こくさいじょうやく|ていけつ"},{"japanese":"地元のNPOが干潟の生態系保全に取り組んでいる。","romaji":"Jimoto no enupīō ga higata no seitaikei hozen ni torikunde iru.","german":"A local NPO is working on the conservation of the tidal flat ecosystem.","furigana":"じもと|ひがた|せいたいけいほぜん|と|く"},{"japanese":"ダム建設は生態系保全の観点から見直された。","romaji":"Damu kensetsu wa seitaikei hozen no kanten kara minaosareta.","german":"The dam construction was reviewed from the standpoint of ecosystem conservation.","furigana":"けんせつ|せいたいけいほぜん|かんてん|みなお"}],"notes":"生態系保全 is composed of 生態系 \"ecosystem\" and 保全 \"preservation, protection\" and means the protection of ecosystems as a whole. 保全 emphasizes maintaining a condition; 保護 sounds more like protecting individual animals or areas (動物保護, 自然保護). Typical expressions are 生態系保全のための, 生態系保全に取り組む and 生態系保全活動. Used almost exclusively in technical, official and news language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"トキは日本を代表する絶滅危惧種である。","romaji":"Toki wa Nihon o daihyō suru zetsumetsu kigushu de aru.","german":"The crested ibis is a representative endangered species of Japan.","furigana":"にほん|だいひょう|ぜつめつきぐしゅ"},{"japanese":"ニホンウナギは数年前に絶滅危惧種に指定された。","romaji":"Nihon unagi wa sūnen mae ni zetsumetsu kigushu ni shitei sareta.","german":"The Japanese eel was designated an endangered species a few years ago.","furigana":"すうねんまえ|ぜつめつきぐしゅ|してい"},{"japanese":"公衆電話は今や絶滅危惧種だね。","romaji":"Kōshū denwa wa ima ya zetsumetsu kigushu da ne.","german":"Public phones are an endangered species by now, aren't they.","furigana":"こうしゅうでんわ|いま|ぜつめつきぐしゅ"}],"notes":"絶滅危惧種 is the technical term for an \"endangered species\", officially listed in the Red List (レッドリスト). Typical are 絶滅危惧種に指定される \"to be designated as endangered\" and 絶滅危惧種を守る. Already extinct is 絶滅した, \"extinct in the wild\" is 野生絶滅. Colloquially, the word is also used jokingly for dying-out things, such as 絶滅危惧種のガラケー.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自然保護区での開発行為は厳しく制限されている。","romaji":"Shizen hogoku de no kaihatsu kōi wa kibishiku seigen sarete iru.","german":"Development activities in nature reserves are strictly restricted.","furigana":"しぜんほごく|かいはつこうい|きび|せいげん"},{"japanese":"この湿地は渡り鳥のために自然保護区に指定された。","romaji":"Kono shitchi wa wataridori no tame ni shizen hogoku ni shitei sareta.","german":"This wetland was designated a nature reserve for migratory birds.","furigana":"しっち|わた|どり|しぜんほごく|してい"},{"japanese":"自然保護区の中では植物を採ってはいけません。","romaji":"Shizen hogoku no naka de wa shokubutsu o totte wa ikemasen.","german":"Plants must not be picked inside the nature reserve.","furigana":"しぜんほごく|なか|しょくぶつ|と"}],"notes":"自然保護区 is a \"nature reserve\" in which interventions are restricted by law. Typical expressions: 自然保護区に指定する, 自然保護区内では…が禁止されている. In Japan, many areas carry more specific names such as 自然環境保全地域 or 鳥獣保護区 \"bird and wildlife sanctuary\"; 国立公園, by contrast, is a \"national park\" that is used for tourism. Register: official and news language, travel guides.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大気汚染が呼吸器疾患の原因となっている。","romaji":"Taiki osen ga kokyūki shikkan no gen'in to natte iru.","german":"Air pollution is a cause of respiratory diseases.","furigana":"たいきおせん|こきゅうきしっかん|げんいん"},{"japanese":"大気汚染がひどくて、町が一日中かすんで見えた。","romaji":"Taiki osen ga hidokute, machi ga ichinichijū kasunde mieta.","german":"The air pollution was so bad that the town looked hazy all day.","furigana":"たいきおせん|まち|いちにちじゅう|み"},{"japanese":"車の数を減らして大気汚染を防ごうとしている。","romaji":"Kuruma no kazu o herashite taiki osen o fusegō to shite iru.","german":"They are trying to prevent air pollution by reducing the number of cars.","furigana":"くるま|かず|へ|たいきおせん|ふせ"}],"notes":"大気汚染 is \"air pollution\" caused by exhaust gases, industry or fine particulate matter. Typical are 大気汚染が深刻だ, 大気汚染が進む and 大気汚染を防ぐ. It belongs to the series of environmental problems 水質汚濁, 土壌汚染 and 騒音. In everyday speech one often simply says 空気が汚い; 大気汚染 is the objective term from the news and reports.","levelNote":"Compound: 大気 (N3) + 汚染 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"水質汚濁防止法により排水基準が定められている。","romaji":"Suishitsu odaku bōshi hō ni yori haisui kijun ga sadamerarete iru.","german":"Effluent standards are set by the Water Pollution Control Act.","furigana":"すいしつおだくぼうしほう|はいすいきじゅん|さだ"},{"japanese":"家庭からの生活排水も水質汚濁の大きな原因だ。","romaji":"Katei kara no seikatsu haisui mo suishitsu odaku no ōki na gen'in da.","german":"Domestic wastewater from households is also a major cause of water pollution.","furigana":"かてい|せいかつはいすい|すいしつおだく|おお|げんいん"},{"japanese":"湖の水質汚濁が進み、魚が大量に死んだ。","romaji":"Mizuumi no suishitsu odaku ga susumi, sakana ga tairyō ni shinda.","german":"Pollution of the lake advanced, and large numbers of fish died.","furigana":"みずうみ|すいしつおだく|すす|さかな|たいりょう|し"}],"notes":"水質汚濁 is the official term for \"water pollution\"; 汚濁 literally means \"contamination and turbidity\". It appears mainly in laws and reports, e.g. in the 水質汚濁防止法 \"Water Pollution Control Act\". In general usage, 水質汚染 is more common and has the same meaning. Typical expressions: 水質汚濁が進む, 水質汚濁の原因.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"工場跡地の土壌汚染が問題視されている。","romaji":"Kōjō atochi no dojō osen ga mondaishi sarete iru.","german":"Soil contamination at former factory sites is regarded as a problem.","furigana":"こうじょうあとち|どじょうおせん|もんだいし"},{"japanese":"建設予定地で土壌汚染が見つかり、工事が延期された。","romaji":"Kensetsu yoteichi de dojō osen ga mitsukari, kōji ga enki sareta.","german":"Soil contamination was found at the planned construction site, and the work was postponed.","furigana":"けんせつよていち|どじょうおせん|み|こうじ|えんき"},{"japanese":"土地を買う前に土壌汚染の調査を依頼した。","romaji":"Tochi o kau mae ni dojō osen no chōsa o irai shita.","german":"Before buying the land, I commissioned a soil contamination survey.","furigana":"とち|か|まえ|どじょうおせん|ちょうさ|いらい"}],"notes":"土壌汚染 is \"soil pollution\" or \"soil contamination\" by chemicals, heavy metals or oil. Typical expressions are 土壌汚染が見つかる/判明する, 土壌汚染を調査する and 土壌汚染対策. The word became known in Japan through, among other things, land issues when building markets and housing complexes on former industrial sites. 土壌 is the technical word for \"soil\"; in everyday speech one says 土.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"空港周辺の騒音公害対策が強化された。","romaji":"Kūkō shūhen no sōon kōgai taisaku ga kyōka sareta.","german":"Measures against noise pollution around the airport were strengthened.","furigana":"くうこうしゅうへん|そうおんこうがいたいさく|きょうか"},{"japanese":"高速道路沿いの住民は長年騒音公害に悩まされてきた。","romaji":"Kōsoku dōro zoi no jūmin wa naganen sōon kōgai ni nayamasarete kita.","german":"Residents along the expressway have suffered from noise pollution for many years.","furigana":"こうそくどうろぞ|じゅうみん|ながねんそうおんこうがい|なや"},{"japanese":"住民たちは騒音公害を理由に工場を訴えた。","romaji":"Jūmintachi wa sōon kōgai o riyū ni kōjō o uttaeta.","german":"The residents sued the factory on the grounds of noise pollution.","furigana":"じゅうみん|そうおんこうがい|りゆう|こうじょう|うった"}],"notes":"騒音公害 refers to noise as an \"environmental burden\" affecting many people, for example from airports, expressways or factories; 公害 is the general term for such public environmental damage. For everyday noise, such as from a neighbor, one simply says 騒音 or 近所の騒音. Typical expressions: 騒音公害に悩まされる, 騒音公害対策, 騒音公害で訴える. Register: news, official language.","levelNote":"Compound: 騒音 (N4) + 公害 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"都市部の光害により星が見えにくくなっている。","romaji":"Toshibu no kōgai ni yori hoshi ga mienikuku natte iru.","german":"Because of light pollution in urban areas, stars have become hard to see.","furigana":"としぶ|こうがい|ほし|み"},{"japanese":"天体観測には光害の少ない山の上がいい。","romaji":"Tentai kansoku ni wa kōgai no sukunai yama no ue ga ii.","german":"For astronomical observation, a mountaintop with little light pollution is best.","furigana":"てんたいかんそく|こうがい|すく|やま|うえ"},{"japanese":"町は光害対策として、街灯を下向きのものに替えた。","romaji":"Machi wa kōgai taisaku toshite, gaitō o shitamuki no mono ni kaeta.","german":"As a measure against light pollution, the town replaced its street lamps with downward-facing ones.","furigana":"まち|こうがいたいさく|がいとう|したむ|か"}],"notes":"光害 is \"light pollution\" caused by excessive artificial light, which, for example, hides the starry sky or disturbs animals. It is read こうがい and is thus homophonous with 公害 \"environmental pollution\"; to avoid confusion, people often say ひかりがい in speech. Typical expressions: 光害が少ない場所, 光害対策. A still fairly new technical term from astronomy and environmental protection.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"酸性雨が森林や湖沼に深刻な被害を与えている。","romaji":"Sansei u ga shinrin ya koshō ni shinkoku na higai o ataete iru.","german":"Acid rain is causing serious damage to forests and lakes.","furigana":"さんせいう|しんりん|こしょう|しんこく|ひがい|あた"},{"japanese":"酸性雨で古い石像の表面が溶けてしまった。","romaji":"Sansei u de furui sekizō no hyōmen ga tokete shimatta.","german":"Acid rain has eroded the surface of the old stone statue.","furigana":"さんせいう|ふる|せきぞう|ひょうめん|と"},{"japanese":"理科の授業で酸性雨ができる仕組みを学んだ。","romaji":"Rika no jugyō de sansei u ga dekiru shikumi o mananda.","german":"In science class, we learned how acid rain forms.","furigana":"りか|じゅぎょう|さんせいう|しく|まな"}],"notes":"酸性雨 is \"acid rain\", which is caused by sulfur and nitrogen oxides from exhaust gases. Typical expressions are 酸性雨が降る, 酸性雨の被害 and 酸性雨で森が枯れる. The topic was very prominent in the 1980s and today appears mainly in textbooks and environmental reports. 酸性 means \"acidic\" in the chemical sense; for taste one says 酸っぱい.","levelNote":"Compound: 酸性 (N2) + 雨 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"フロンガスによるオゾン層の破壊が問題となった。","romaji":"Furon gasu ni yoru ozon sō no hakai ga mondai to natta.","german":"The destruction of the ozone layer by chlorofluorocarbons became a problem.","furigana":"そう|はかい|もんだい"},{"japanese":"オゾン層は太陽からの有害な紫外線を吸収している。","romaji":"Ozon sō wa taiyō kara no yūgai na shigaisen o kyūshū shite iru.","german":"The ozone layer absorbs harmful ultraviolet rays from the sun.","furigana":"そう|たいよう|ゆうがい|しがいせん|きゅうしゅう"},{"japanese":"国際的な規制のおかげで、オゾン層は少しずつ回復している。","romaji":"Kokusaiteki na kisei no okage de, ozon sō wa sukoshi zutsu kaifuku shite iru.","german":"Thanks to international regulations, the ozone layer is gradually recovering.","furigana":"こくさいてき|きせい|そう|すこ|かいふく"}],"notes":"オゾン層 is the \"ozone layer\" in the stratosphere, which protects against UV radiation. Typical expressions are オゾン層の破壊 \"depletion of the ozone layer\", オゾン層を守る and オゾンホール \"ozone hole\". The main culprit, CFCs, is called フロン or フロンガス in Japanese. 層 means \"layer\" and also appears in 地層 or 富裕層.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"オゾン層の破壊により紫外線の量が増加している。","romaji":"Ozon sō no hakai ni yori shigaisen no ryō ga zōka shite iru.","german":"Due to the destruction of the ozone layer, the amount of ultraviolet radiation is increasing.","furigana":"そう|はかい|しがいせん|りょう|ぞうか"},{"japanese":"夏は紫外線が強いから、帽子をかぶって出かけよう。","romaji":"Natsu wa shigaisen ga tsuyoi kara, bōshi o kabutte dekakeyō.","german":"UV rays are strong in summer, so let's wear a hat when we go out.","furigana":"なつ|しがいせん|つよ|ぼうし|で"},{"japanese":"この窓ガラスは紫外線を九割カットする。","romaji":"Kono madogarasu wa shigaisen o kyūwari katto suru.","german":"This window glass blocks ninety percent of UV rays.","furigana":"まど|しがいせん|きゅうわり"}],"notes":"紫外線 is \"UV radiation\", literally \"rays beyond violet\"; its counterpart is 赤外線 \"infrared\". It is very common in everyday life, above all in connection with skin protection: 紫外線が強い, 紫外線を浴びる, 紫外線対策, 紫外線カット. UV or UVカット are used with the same meaning. Sunburn itself is called 日焼け.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"メタンは二酸化炭素よりも強力な温室効果ガスである。","romaji":"Metan wa nisanka tanso yori mo kyōryoku na onshitsu kōka gasu de aru.","german":"Methane is a more potent greenhouse gas than carbon dioxide.","furigana":"にさんかたんそ|きょうりょく|おんしつこうか"},{"japanese":"牛のげっぷには大量のメタンが含まれている。","romaji":"Ushi no geppu ni wa tairyō no metan ga fukumarete iru.","german":"Cow burps contain large amounts of methane.","furigana":"うし|たいりょう|ふく"},{"japanese":"ごみ処分場から出るメタンを集めて発電に利用している。","romaji":"Gomi shobunjō kara deru metan o atsumete hatsuden ni riyō shite iru.","german":"Methane from the landfill is collected and used to generate electricity.","furigana":"しょぶんじょう|で|あつ|はつでん|りよう"}],"notes":"メタン is the gas \"methane\" and is always written in katakana; one often also hears メタンガス. In an environmental context it appears mainly as a 温室効果ガス; sources include 牛のげっぷ \"cow burps\", 水田 \"paddy fields\" and landfills. Typical expressions: メタンを排出する, メタンが発生する. The fuel ice on the seabed is called メタンハイドレート.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自動車の排気ガス規制が強化されている。","romaji":"Jidōsha no haiki gasu kisei ga kyōka sarete iru.","german":"Exhaust gas regulations for motor vehicles are being tightened.","furigana":"じどうしゃ|はいき|きせい|きょうか"},{"japanese":"大通りを歩くと排気ガスのにおいがひどい。","romaji":"Ōdōri o aruku to haiki gasu no nioi ga hidoi.","german":"Walking along the main street, the smell of exhaust fumes is terrible.","furigana":"おおどお|ある|はいき"},{"japanese":"電気自動車は走行中に排気ガスを出さない。","romaji":"Denki jidōsha wa sōkōchū ni haiki gasu o dasanai.","german":"Electric vehicles do not emit exhaust gas while driving.","furigana":"でんきじどうしゃ|そうこうちゅう|はいき|だ"}],"notes":"排気ガス is \"exhaust gas\", especially from cars, motorcycles and machines; 排気 means \"exhaust, venting\". Typical are 排気ガスを出す, 排気ガス規制 and 排気ガスを吸い込む. In everyday speech the short form 排ガス is often used, especially in the news (排ガス規制). For smoke from factory chimneys one tends to say 排煙.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"工事現場からの粉塵が周辺住民の健康を脅かしている。","romaji":"Kōji genba kara no funjin ga shūhen jūmin no kenkō o obiyakashite iru.","german":"Dust from the construction site is threatening the health of nearby residents.","furigana":"こうじげんば|ふんじん|しゅうへんじゅうみん|けんこう|おびや"},{"japanese":"作業中は粉塵を吸い込まないようにマスクを着けてください。","romaji":"Sagyōchū wa funjin o suikomanai yō ni masuku o tsukete kudasai.","german":"Please wear a mask while working so that you do not inhale dust.","furigana":"さぎょうちゅう|ふんじん|す|こ|つ"},{"japanese":"小麦粉の粉塵に火がつくと、爆発が起きることがある。","romaji":"Komugiko no funjin ni hi ga tsuku to, bakuhatsu ga okiru koto ga aru.","german":"If flour dust catches fire, an explosion can occur.","furigana":"こむぎこ|ふんじん|ひ|ばくはつ|お"}],"notes":"粉塵 refers to fine dust that gets into the air during construction work, in mining or in factories. Because 塵 is not one of the standard kanji, 粉じん is often written in official documents and on signs. Typical expressions: 粉塵が舞う, 粉塵を吸い込む, 粉塵対策 and 粉塵爆発 \"dust explosion\". For household dust one says ほこり; fine particulate matter in the sense of PM2.5 is 微小粒子状物質.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"汚水処理施設の整備が遅れている地域がある。","romaji":"Osui shori shisetsu no seibi ga okurete iru chiiki ga aru.","german":"There are areas where the development of sewage treatment facilities is lagging behind.","furigana":"おすいしょりしせつ|せいび|おく|ちいき"},{"japanese":"その工場は汚水を川に垂れ流していた。","romaji":"Sono kōjō wa osui o kawa ni tarenagashite ita.","german":"The factory had been discharging its wastewater untreated into the river.","furigana":"こうじょう|おすい|かわ|た|なが"},{"japanese":"大雨で下水があふれ、道路に汚水が流れ出た。","romaji":"Ōame de gesui ga afure, dōro ni osui ga nagaredeta.","german":"Heavy rain made the sewers overflow, and sewage flowed out onto the road.","furigana":"おおあめ|げすい|どうろ|おすい|なが|で"}],"notes":"汚水 is \"dirty water\" or \"sewage\", especially from households, toilets and factories. 下水 refers to the wastewater and sewer system (下水道), and 排水 to drained water in general, without emphasis on pollution. Typical expressions: 汚水を処理する, 汚水を垂れ流す \"to discharge untreated\", 汚水処理場. Register: rather written and technical.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"脱炭素社会の実現に向けた政策が推進されている。","romaji":"Datsu tanso shakai no jitsugen ni muketa seisaku ga suishin sarete iru.","german":"Policies aimed at realizing a decarbonized society are being promoted.","furigana":"だつたんそしゃかい|じつげん|む|せいさく|すいしん"},{"japanese":"多くの企業が脱炭素に向けて再生可能エネルギーへの切り替えを進めている。","romaji":"Ōku no kigyō ga datsu tanso ni mukete saisei kanō enerugī e no kirikae o susumete iru.","german":"Many companies are switching to renewable energy in order to decarbonize.","furigana":"おお|きぎょう|だつたんそ|む|さいせいかのう|き|か|すす"},{"japanese":"脱炭素の流れで、石炭火力発電所が次々と閉鎖されている。","romaji":"Datsu tanso no nagare de, sekitan karyoku hatsudensho ga tsugitsugi to heisa sarete iru.","german":"In the trend toward decarbonization, coal-fired power plants are being closed one after another.","furigana":"だつたんそ|なが|せきたんかりょくはつでんしょ|つぎつぎ|へいさ"}],"notes":"脱炭素 means \"decarbonization\", that is, giving up CO₂ emissions; 脱 means \"away from, exit from\" as in 脱原発. It mostly stands before nouns: 脱炭素社会, 脱炭素化, 脱炭素経営. Closely related is カーボンニュートラル \"carbon neutrality\", the goal of balancing emissions and absorption. A very current buzzword in politics and business.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"多くの国が炭素中立を目標に掲げている。","romaji":"Ōku no kuni ga tanso chūritsu o mokuhyō ni kakagete iru.","german":"Many countries have set carbon neutrality as a goal.","furigana":"おお|くに|たんそちゅうりつ|もくひょう|かか"},{"japanese":"政府は二〇五〇年までに炭素中立を達成すると宣言した。","romaji":"Seifu wa nisen gojū nen made ni tanso chūritsu o tassei suru to sengen shita.","german":"The government declared that it will achieve carbon neutrality by 2050.","furigana":"せいふ||たんそちゅうりつ|たっせい|せんげん"},{"japanese":"その会社は植林によって炭素中立を実現したと主張している。","romaji":"Sono kaisha wa shokurin ni yotte tanso chūritsu o jitsugen shita to shuchō shite iru.","german":"The company claims to have achieved carbon neutrality through tree planting.","furigana":"かいしゃ|しょくりん|たんそちゅうりつ|じつげん|しゅちょう"}],"notes":"炭素中立 is the literal translation of \"carbon neutrality\": emissions and absorption of CO₂ balance each other out. In Japanese practice, however, the loanword カーボンニュートラル is far more common; 炭素中立 is read more in technical texts or translations. Typical expressions: 炭素中立を目指す, 炭素中立を達成する, 炭素中立を目標に掲げる. Related is 脱炭素 \"decarbonization\", the path toward it.","levelNote":"Compound: 炭素 (N1) + 中立 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"排出権取引は温室効果ガス削減の経済的手法である。","romaji":"Haishutsuken torihiki wa onshitsu kōka gasu sakugen no keizaiteki shuhō de aru.","german":"Emissions trading is an economic method of reducing greenhouse gases.","furigana":"はいしゅつけんとりひき|おんしつこうか|さくげん|けいざいてきしゅほう"},{"japanese":"EUは早くから排出権取引の制度を導入している。","romaji":"Īyū wa hayaku kara haishutsuken torihiki no seido o dōnyū shite iru.","german":"The EU introduced an emissions trading system early on.","furigana":"はや|はいしゅつけんとりひき|せいど|どうにゅう"},{"japanese":"目標を達成できなかった企業は、排出権取引の市場で権利を買うことになる。","romaji":"Mokuhyō o tassei dekinakatta kigyō wa, haishutsuken torihiki no shijō de kenri o kau koto ni naru.","german":"Companies that fail to meet their targets have to buy allowances on the emissions trading market.","furigana":"もくひょう|たっせい|きぎょう|はいしゅつけんとりひき|しじょう|けんり|か"}],"notes":"排出権取引 is \"emissions trading\": companies or states buy and sell rights to emit a certain amount of CO₂. 排出権 is the \"emission right\", 取引 the \"trading\". In official Japanese texts the system is nowadays usually called 排出量取引 (制度); 排出権取引, however, remains common in the media. Typical expressions: 排出権取引制度, 排出権取引市場, 排出権を購入する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"環境基準を超える汚染物質が検出された。","romaji":"Kankyō kijun o koeru osen busshitsu ga kenshutsu sareta.","german":"Pollutants exceeding the environmental standards were detected.","furigana":"かんきょうきじゅん|こ|おせんぶっしつ|けんしゅつ"},{"japanese":"この地域の川は、ようやく水質の環境基準を満たすようになった。","romaji":"Kono chiiki no kawa wa, yōyaku suishitsu no kankyō kijun o mitasu yō ni natta.","german":"The rivers in this region have finally come to meet the environmental standards for water quality.","furigana":"ちいき|かわ|すいしつ|かんきょうきじゅん|み"},{"japanese":"国は騒音に関する環境基準を見直すことにした。","romaji":"Kuni wa sōon ni kansuru kankyō kijun o minaosu koto ni shita.","german":"The state decided to review the environmental standards for noise.","furigana":"くに|そうおん|かん|かんきょうきじゅん|みなお"}],"notes":"環境基準 are the legally set \"environmental standards\" or \"environmental quality standards\" for air, water, soil and noise. Typical expressions are 環境基準を超える \"to exceed the limits\", 環境基準を満たす/達成する \"to meet\" and 環境基準値. To be distinguished from it is 排出基準, which sets how much a single facility may emit. Register: official and news language.","levelNote":"Compound: 環境 (N4) + 基準 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"埋立地の残余容量が逼迫している。","romaji":"Umetatechi no zan'yo yōryō ga hippaku shite iru.","german":"The remaining capacity of landfills is running short.","furigana":"うめたてち|ざんよようりょう|ひっぱく"},{"japanese":"関西国際空港は、海を埋め立てた埋立地の上に建っている。","romaji":"Kansai Kokusai Kūkō wa, umi o umetateta umetatechi no ue ni tatte iru.","german":"Kansai International Airport stands on reclaimed land built by filling in the sea.","furigana":"かんさいこくさいくうこう|うみ|う|た|うめたてち|うえ|た"},{"japanese":"埋立地は、地震のときに液状化が起こりやすい。","romaji":"Umetatechi wa, jishin no toki ni ekijōka ga okoriyasui.","german":"Reclaimed land is prone to liquefaction during earthquakes.","furigana":"うめたてち|じしん|えきじょうか|お"}],"notes":"埋立地 (also 埋め立て地) is land created by filling in, especially in the sea or in bays: parts of Tokyo Bay, お台場 and Kansai International Airport are on 埋立地. In an environmental context it also refers to a landfill where waste is buried (ごみの埋立地, technically 最終処分場). The related verb is 埋め立てる. In earthquakes, 埋立地 is considered prone to soil liquefaction (液状化).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ゴミの焼却処分はダイオキシンの発生源となりうる。","romaji":"Gomi no shōkyaku shobun wa daiokishin no hasseigen to nariuru.","german":"Waste incineration can be a source of dioxins.","furigana":"しょうきゃくしょぶん|はっせいげん"},{"japanese":"個人情報が書かれた書類は、すべて焼却してください。","romaji":"Kojin jōhō ga kakareta shorui wa, subete shōkyaku shite kudasai.","german":"Please incinerate all documents containing personal information.","furigana":"こじんじょうほう|か|しょるい|しょうきゃく"},{"japanese":"市の焼却施設で出た熱を温水プールに利用している。","romaji":"Shi no shōkyaku shisetsu de deta netsu o onsui pūru ni riyō shite iru.","german":"The heat produced at the city's incineration plant is used for a heated swimming pool.","furigana":"し|しょうきゃくしせつ|で|ねつ|おんすい|りよう"}],"notes":"焼却 means \"incineration, destruction by fire\", especially of garbage and documents; as a transitive する-verb: ごみを焼却する. Typical expressions are 焼却処分 \"disposal by incineration\", 焼却炉 \"incinerator\" and ごみ焼却場. 燃やす is the everyday word, while 焼却 sounds official and technical. For cremation, on the other hand, one says 火葬.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本では分別収集が徹底されている。","romaji":"Nihon de wa bunbetsu shūshū ga tettei sarete iru.","german":"Separate waste collection is thoroughly implemented in Japan.","furigana":"にほん|ぶんべつしゅうしゅう|てってい"},{"japanese":"来月から、プラスチックの分別収集が始まります。","romaji":"Raigetsu kara, purasuchikku no bunbetsu shūshū ga hajimarimasu.","german":"From next month, plastic will be collected separately.","furigana":"らいげつ|ぶんべつしゅうしゅう|はじ"},{"japanese":"分別収集のルールは市町村によって違うので、引っ越したら確認しよう。","romaji":"Bunbetsu shūshū no rūru wa shichōson ni yotte chigau node, hikkoshitara kakunin shiyō.","german":"The rules for separate waste collection differ by municipality, so check after you move.","furigana":"ぶんべつしゅうしゅう|しちょうそん|ちが|ひ|こ|かくにん"}],"notes":"分別収集 is \"separate waste collection\": the municipality collects waste sorted by type, e.g. 燃えるごみ, 燃えないごみ, 資源ごみ. 分別 is read ぶんべつ here; ふんべつ, on the other hand, means \"good sense, discretion\". Citizens themselves 分別する or ごみを分別して出す \"sort their waste\"; 分別収集 emphasizes the municipal collection system. Typical of municipal notices and the news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"使用済み製品の再資源化率を高めることが求められている。","romaji":"Shiyōzumi seihin no saishigenka ritsu o takameru koto ga motomerarete iru.","german":"There is a demand to raise the recycling rate of used products.","furigana":"しようず|せいひん|さいしげんかりつ|たか|もと"},{"japanese":"回収した古いスマホから金や銅を再資源化している。","romaji":"Kaishū shita furui sumaho kara kin ya dō o saishigenka shite iru.","german":"Gold and copper are being recovered from collected old smartphones.","furigana":"かいしゅう|ふる|きん|どう|さいしげんか"},{"japanese":"建設現場で出たコンクリートの多くは再資源化される。","romaji":"Kensetsu genba de deta konkurīto no ōku wa saishigenka sareru.","german":"Much of the concrete from construction sites is recycled into resources.","furigana":"けんせつげんば|で|おお|さいしげんか"}],"notes":"再資源化 literally means \"making into resources again\" and refers to the material recycling of waste and old equipment. As a transitive する-verb: 廃家電を再資源化する. Typical expressions: 再資源化率 \"recycling rate\", 再資源化を進める. Compared with the everyday リサイクル, 再資源化 sounds official and technical; it often appears in laws and company reports.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"環境負荷の低い製品が消費者に支持されている。","romaji":"Kankyō fuka no hikui seihin ga shōhisha ni shiji sarete iru.","german":"Products with a low environmental impact are favored by consumers.","furigana":"かんきょうふか|ひく|せいひん|しょうひしゃ|しじ"},{"japanese":"物流を鉄道に切り替えて、環境負荷を減らした。","romaji":"Butsuryū o tetsudō ni kirikaete, kankyō fuka o herashita.","german":"By switching freight to rail, we reduced the environmental impact.","furigana":"ぶつりゅう|てつどう|き|か|かんきょうふか|へ"},{"japanese":"大量の使い捨て容器は環境に大きな負荷をかける。","romaji":"Tairyō no tsukaisute yōki wa kankyō ni ōki na fuka o kakeru.","german":"Large quantities of disposable containers put a heavy burden on the environment.","furigana":"たいりょう|つか|す|ようき|かんきょう|おお|ふか"}],"notes":"環境負荷 is the \"environmental burden\" or \"environmental impact\" caused by a product, an activity or a company. Typical expressions are 環境負荷が低い/小さい, 環境負荷を減らす/低減する and 環境に負荷をかける. 負荷 actually means \"load, burden\" as in engineering. The word is typical of company reports, advertising and technical texts; in everyday speech one tends to say 環境に悪い／優しい.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"熱帯地域での森林伐採が深刻な環境問題となっている。","romaji":"Nettai chiiki de no shinrin bassai ga shinkoku na kankyō mondai to natte iru.","german":"Deforestation in tropical regions has become a serious environmental problem.","furigana":"ねったいちいき|しんりんばっさい|しんこく|かんきょうもんだい"},{"japanese":"違法な森林伐採を取り締まるため、衛星写真が使われている。","romaji":"Ihō na shinrin bassai o torishimaru tame, eisei shashin ga tsukawarete iru.","german":"Satellite images are used to crack down on illegal logging.","furigana":"いほう|しんりんばっさい|と|し|えいせいしゃしん|つか"},{"japanese":"森林伐採のせいで、大雨のたびに土砂崩れが起きるようになった。","romaji":"Shinrin bassai no sei de, ōame no tabi ni dosha kuzure ga okiru yō ni natta.","german":"Because of deforestation, landslides now occur every time there is heavy rain.","furigana":"しんりんばっさい|おおあめ|どしゃくず|お"}],"notes":"森林伐採 refers to the \"clearing of forests\", often as a large-scale environmental problem (\"deforestation\"). 伐採 is the felling of trees; as a verb one says 木を伐採する or 森林を伐採する. Typical expressions: 違法な森林伐採, 森林伐採が進む. The opposite is 植林 \"afforestation\". Register: news, textbooks, technical texts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"過度の放牧が砂漠化を加速させている。","romaji":"Kado no hōboku ga sabakuka o kasoku sasete iru.","german":"Overgrazing is accelerating desertification.","furigana":"かど|ほうぼく|さばくか|かそく"},{"japanese":"雨が減り、かつての農地が砂漠化してしまった。","romaji":"Ame ga heri, katsute no nōchi ga sabakuka shite shimatta.","german":"Rainfall decreased, and what was once farmland has turned into desert.","furigana":"あめ|へ|のうち|さばくか"},{"japanese":"砂漠化を防ぐため、乾燥に強い木を植えている。","romaji":"Sabakuka o fusegu tame, kansō ni tsuyoi ki o uete iru.","german":"Drought-resistant trees are being planted to prevent desertification.","furigana":"さばくか|ふせ|かんそう|つよ|き|う"}],"notes":"砂漠化 is \"desertification\", that is, the emergence of desert-like landscapes through climate or overuse. As an intransitive する-verb: 土地が砂漠化する; also common are 砂漠化が進む \"desertification advances\" and 砂漠化を防ぐ. The suffix 化 \"-ization, becoming\" is also known from 温暖化 or 都市化. In a figurative sense it is sometimes used loosely, e.g. 頭皮の砂漠化 for a dry scalp.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"湖沼の富栄養化により藻類が大量発生している。","romaji":"Koshō no fueiyōka ni yori sōrui ga tairyō hassei shite iru.","german":"Eutrophication of lakes is causing mass outbreaks of algae.","furigana":"こしょう|ふえいようか|そうるい|たいりょうはっせい"},{"japanese":"畑の肥料が川に流れ込み、湾の富栄養化が進んだ。","romaji":"Hatake no hiryō ga kawa ni nagarekomi, wan no fueiyōka ga susunda.","german":"Fertilizer from the fields flowed into the river, and the eutrophication of the bay progressed.","furigana":"はたけ|ひりょう|かわ|なが|こ|わん|ふえいようか|すす"},{"japanese":"洗剤に含まれるリンが原因で、池が富栄養化した。","romaji":"Senzai ni fukumareru rin ga gen'in de, ike ga fueiyōka shita.","german":"The pond became eutrophic because of phosphorus contained in detergents.","furigana":"せんざい|ふく|げんいん|いけ|ふえいようか"}],"notes":"富栄養化 is \"eutrophication\": bodies of water become over-enriched with nutrients such as nitrogen and phosphorus from wastewater and fertilizer. As an intransitive する-verb: 湖が富栄養化する; 富栄養化が進む is also common. Consequences are algal blooms, アオコ in fresh water and 赤潮 in the sea. A technical word from biology and environmental reports.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"赤潮の発生により養殖魚が大量に死んだ。","romaji":"Akashio no hassei ni yori yōshoku gyo ga tairyō ni shinda.","german":"The outbreak of a red tide killed large numbers of farmed fish.","furigana":"あかしお|はっせい|ようしょくぎょ|たいりょう|し"},{"japanese":"今年の夏は水温が高く、湾内で赤潮が発生した。","romaji":"Kotoshi no natsu wa suion ga takaku, wannai de akashio ga hassei shita.","german":"This summer the water was warm, and a red tide occurred in the bay.","furigana":"ことし|なつ|すいおん|たか|わんない|あかしお|はっせい"},{"japanese":"赤潮のため、しばらく貝の出荷が止められた。","romaji":"Akashio no tame, shibaraku kai no shukka ga tomerareta.","german":"Because of the red tide, shipments of shellfish were stopped for a while.","furigana":"あかしお|かい|しゅっか|と"}],"notes":"赤潮 is the \"red tide\": a mass proliferation of plankton in the sea that colors the water reddish and kills fish. Typical expressions are 赤潮が発生する, 赤潮の被害 and 赤潮で魚が死ぬ. A related phenomenon is 青潮, oxygen-poor water that appears bluish-white; in fresh water one speaks of アオコ. Frequent in news from fishing and coastal regions.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地下水の過剰汲み上げが地盤沈下を引き起こしている。","romaji":"Chikasui no kajō kumiage ga jiban chinka o hikiokoshite iru.","german":"Excessive pumping of groundwater is causing land subsidence.","furigana":"ちかすい|かじょうく|あ|じばんちんか|ひ|お"},{"japanese":"かつての工業都市も、今では経済の地盤沈下が進んでいる。","romaji":"Katsute no kōgyō toshi mo, ima de wa keizai no jiban chinka ga susunde iru.","german":"Even the once-industrial city is now suffering an economic decline.","furigana":"こうぎょうとし|いま|けいざい|じばんちんか|すす"},{"japanese":"選挙で大敗し、党の地盤沈下が明らかになった。","romaji":"Senkyo de taihai shi, tō no jiban chinka ga akiraka ni natta.","german":"The crushing election defeat made clear the erosion of the party's base.","furigana":"せんきょ|たいはい|とう|じばんちんか|あき"}],"notes":"地盤沈下 is the sinking of the ground, for example through excessive groundwater extraction; as an intransitive する-verb: 地盤が沈下する or 地盤沈下する. Very common is the figurative use for the gradual loss of importance or power of a party, industry or region: 地方経済の地盤沈下. The word is formal and typical of news and economic reports.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"環境保全と経済発展の両立が求められている。","romaji":"Kankyō hozen to keizai hatten no ryōritsu ga motomerarete iru.","german":"Reconciling environmental conservation with economic development is required.","furigana":"かんきょうほぜん|けいざいはってん|りょうりつ|もと"},{"japanese":"当社は工場周辺の環境保全に積極的に取り組んでいます。","romaji":"Tōsha wa kōjō shūhen no kankyō hozen ni sekkyokuteki ni torikunde imasu.","german":"Our company is actively working on environmental conservation around our factories.","furigana":"とうしゃ|こうじょうしゅうへん|かんきょうほぜん|せっきょくてき|と|く"},{"japanese":"学生たちが川辺の環境保全活動に参加した。","romaji":"Gakuseitachi ga kawabe no kankyō hozen katsudō ni sanka shita.","german":"Students took part in an environmental conservation activity on the riverbank.","furigana":"がくせい|かわべ|かんきょうほぜんかつどう|さんか"}],"notes":"環境保全 means \"environmental protection\" in the sense of \"preserving the environment\" in good condition. 環境保護 is largely synonymous, but emphasizes protecting from damage; 保全 sounds somewhat more official and is popular in laws and corporate mission statements. Typical expressions: 環境保全に取り組む, 環境保全活動, 環境保全と経済の両立. Register: formal, government agencies and companies.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"温室効果ガスの主な発生源は化石燃料の燃焼である。","romaji":"Onshitsu kōka gasu no omo na hasseigen wa kaseki nenryō no nenshō de aru.","german":"The main source of greenhouse gases is the burning of fossil fuels.","furigana":"おんしつこうか|おも|はっせいげん|かせきねんりょう|ねんしょう"},{"japanese":"この会社は温室効果ガスの排出量を毎年公表している。","romaji":"Kono kaisha wa onshitsu kōka gasu no haishutsuryō o maitoshi kōhyō shite iru.","german":"This company publishes its greenhouse gas emissions every year.","furigana":"かいしゃ|おんしつこうか|はいしゅつりょう|まいとしこうひょう"},{"japanese":"飛行機に乗る代わりに電車を使って、温室効果ガスを減らそう。","romaji":"Hikōki ni noru kawari ni densha o tsukatte, onshitsu kōka gasu o herasō.","german":"Let's use the train instead of flying and cut greenhouse gases.","furigana":"ひこうき|の|か|でんしゃ|つか|おんしつこうか|へ"}],"notes":"温室効果ガス are \"greenhouse gases\" such as 二酸化炭素, メタン or 一酸化二窒素. Typical expressions are 温室効果ガスを排出する, 温室効果ガスの排出量 and 温室効果ガスを削減する. In the news it is often shortened to \"CO₂\", although that actually means only a single gas. ガス is always written in katakana.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"火山国である日本は地熱発電の潜在力が高い。","romaji":"Kazan koku de aru Nihon wa chinetsu hatsuden no senzairyoku ga takai.","german":"Japan, a volcanic country, has high potential for geothermal power generation.","furigana":"かざんこく|にほん|ちねつはつでん|せんざいりょく|たか"},{"japanese":"温泉の経営者たちは、地熱発電の開発に反対している。","romaji":"Onsen no keieishatachi wa, chinetsu hatsuden no kaihatsu ni hantai shite iru.","german":"The owners of hot springs oppose the development of geothermal power generation.","furigana":"おんせん|けいえいしゃ|ちねつはつでん|かいはつ|はんたい"},{"japanese":"地熱発電は天候に左右されないのが強みだ。","romaji":"Chinetsu hatsuden wa tenkō ni sayū sarenai no ga tsuyomi da.","german":"The strength of geothermal power is that it is not affected by the weather.","furigana":"ちねつはつでん|てんこう|さゆう|つよ"}],"notes":"地熱発電 is \"power generation from geothermal heat\"; 地熱 (ちねつ, less often じねつ) is the heat of the Earth. As a volcanic country, Japan has large reserves, but development often stalls because many sources lie within national parks or because operators of thermal baths (温泉) fear for their water. Typical expressions: 地熱発電所, 地熱発電の潜在力, 地熱発電を開発する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"バイオマス発電は再生可能エネルギーの一つである。","romaji":"Baiomasu hatsuden wa saisei kanō enerugī no hitotsu de aru.","german":"Biomass power generation is one form of renewable energy.","furigana":"はつでん|さいせいかのう|ひと"},{"japanese":"この町では間伐材をバイオマス燃料として使っている。","romaji":"Kono machi de wa kanbatsuzai o baiomasu nenryō toshite tsukatte iru.","german":"In this town, thinned timber is used as biomass fuel.","furigana":"まち|かんばつざい|ねんりょう|つか"},{"japanese":"コンビニのレジ袋にバイオマス素材が混ぜられている。","romaji":"Konbini no rejibukuro ni baiomasu sozai ga mazerarete iru.","german":"Biomass material is mixed into the plastic bags at convenience stores.","furigana":"ぶくろ|そざい|ま"}],"notes":"バイオマス refers to \"biomass\", that is, organic material such as wood residue, straw, manure or food waste that is used as an energy source or raw material. It mostly stands before other nouns: バイオマス発電 \"biomass power plant/power\", バイオマス燃料, バイオマスプラスチック. Biofuels are called バイオ燃料. Register: technical and news language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"省資源型の製造プロセスが求められている。","romaji":"Shō shigen gata no seizō purosesu ga motomerarete iru.","german":"Resource-saving manufacturing processes are in demand.","furigana":"しょうしげんがた|せいぞう|もと"},{"japanese":"包装を簡単にすることで、省資源につなげている。","romaji":"Hōsō o kantan ni suru koto de, shō shigen ni tsunagete iru.","german":"Simplifying the packaging contributes to resource conservation.","furigana":"ほうそう|かんたん|しょうしげん"},{"japanese":"この会社は省資源と省エネルギーを経営目標に掲げている。","romaji":"Kono kaisha wa shō shigen to shō enerugī o keiei mokuhyō ni kakagete iru.","german":"This company has set resource conservation and energy conservation as its management goals.","furigana":"かいしゃ|しょうしげん|しょう|けいえいもくひょう|かか"}],"notes":"省資源 means \"resource saving, resource conservation\" and is the counterpart of 省エネ for raw materials and materials. It almost always stands before nouns or with 型/化: 省資源型の製品, 省資源化を進める, 省資源・省エネルギー. In everyday speech one would rather say 資源を節約する or 無駄を減らす; 省資源 belongs in company, official and technical texts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"廃水処理技術の向上が水環境の改善に寄与している。","romaji":"Haisui shori gijutsu no kōjō ga mizu kankyō no kaizen ni kiyo shite iru.","german":"Improvements in wastewater treatment technology are contributing to better water environments.","furigana":"はいすいしょりぎじゅつ|こうじょう|みずかんきょう|かいぜん|きよ"},{"japanese":"この工場は廃水処理の設備が古く、基準を守れていなかった。","romaji":"Kono kōjō wa haisui shori no setsubi ga furuku, kijun o mamorete inakatta.","german":"This factory's wastewater treatment equipment was old, and it had not been meeting the standards.","furigana":"こうじょう|はいすいしょり|せつび|ふる|きじゅん|まも"},{"japanese":"廃水処理の過程で出た汚泥は肥料にも使われる。","romaji":"Haisui shori no katei de deta odei wa hiryō ni mo tsukawareru.","german":"Sludge produced in the wastewater treatment process is also used as fertilizer.","furigana":"はいすいしょり|かてい|で|おでい|ひりょう|つか"}],"notes":"廃水処理 is \"wastewater treatment\", especially of industrial and factory wastewater. 廃水 is \"used, unusable water\"; the homophone 排水 means more generally \"drainage, discharge\", and 排水処理 is often used with the same meaning. Household wastewater usually falls under 下水処理. Typical expressions: 廃水処理施設, 廃水処理技術, 廃水を処理する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"汚泥の有効利用として肥料への転換が研究されている。","romaji":"Odei no yūkō riyō toshite hiryō e no tenkan ga kenkyū sarete iru.","german":"Converting sludge into fertilizer is being researched as an effective use of it.","furigana":"おでい|ゆうこうりよう|ひりょう|てんかん|けんきゅう"},{"japanese":"下水処理場から出る汚泥を燃料に変える取り組みが始まった。","romaji":"Gesui shorijō kara deru odei o nenryō ni kaeru torikumi ga hajimatta.","german":"Efforts have begun to turn the sludge from sewage treatment plants into fuel.","furigana":"げすいしょりじょう|で|おでい|ねんりょう|か|と|く|はじ"},{"japanese":"工場の汚泥から重金属が検出された。","romaji":"Kōjō no odei kara jūkinzoku ga kenshutsu sareta.","german":"Heavy metals were detected in the factory's sludge.","furigana":"こうじょう|おでい|じゅうきんぞく|けんしゅつ"}],"notes":"汚泥 is \"sludge\", especially the \"sewage sludge\" (下水汚泥) or industrial sludge produced during wastewater treatment. Typical expressions: 汚泥を処理する, 汚泥処理, 汚泥を肥料にする. For the stinking mud at the bottom of rivers and harbors, one colloquially says ヘドロ, and for ordinary mud 泥. In elevated language, 汚泥 rarely also stands figuratively for a \"hopeless situation\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"埋立地からの浸出水が地下水を汚染する恐れがある。","romaji":"Umetatechi kara no shinshutsusui ga chikasui o osen suru osore ga aru.","german":"There is a risk that leachate from landfills will contaminate groundwater.","furigana":"うめたてち|しんしゅつすい|ちかすい|おせん|おそ"},{"japanese":"処分場では、浸出水を処理してから川に流している。","romaji":"Shobunjō de wa, shinshutsusui o shori shite kara kawa ni nagashite iru.","german":"At the disposal site, the leachate is treated before being released into the river.","furigana":"しょぶんじょう|しんしゅつすい|しょり|かわ|なが"},{"japanese":"浸出水の水質は、毎月検査されている。","romaji":"Shinshutsusui no suishitsu wa, maitsuki kensa sarete iru.","german":"The quality of the leachate is tested every month.","furigana":"しんしゅつすい|すいしつ|まいつきけんさ"}],"notes":"浸出水 is a technical term of waste management: the leachate that runs through a landfill, picks up pollutants and emerges at the bottom. Typical are 浸出水処理（施設） and 浸出水が漏れる. It belongs to 浸出（しんしゅつ）\"leaching, seeping out\". The word hardly occurs in everyday life, but does in environmental reports and news about landfills.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"原発事故後の除染作業が長期にわたって続いている。","romaji":"Genpatsu jiko go no josen sagyō ga chōki ni watatte tsuzuite iru.","german":"Decontamination work after the nuclear plant accident has continued over a long period.","furigana":"げんぱつじこご|じょせんさぎょう|ちょうき|つづ"},{"japanese":"子どもたちのために、まず学校の校庭を除染した。","romaji":"Kodomotachi no tame ni, mazu gakkō no kōtei o josen shita.","german":"For the sake of the children, the school playground was decontaminated first.","furigana":"こ|がっこう|こうてい|じょせん"},{"japanese":"除染で出た土の保管場所が決まらない。","romaji":"Josen de deta tsuchi no hokan basho ga kimaranai.","german":"A storage site for the soil removed during decontamination has not been decided.","furigana":"じょせん|で|つち|ほかんばしょ|き"}],"notes":"除染 means \"decontamination\", the removal of radioactive or chemical contamination from soil, buildings or people. As a transitive する-verb: 校庭を除染する. Since the Fukushima nuclear accident in 2011, the word has been generally known; typical expressions are 除染作業, 除染土 \"contaminated soil from decontamination\" and 除染が進む. Register: news, government agencies.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"作業員の被曝線量を厳格に管理する必要がある。","romaji":"Sagyōin no hibaku senryō o genkaku ni kanri suru hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"The radiation doses of workers must be strictly controlled.","furigana":"さぎょういん|ひばくせんりょう|げんかく|かんり|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"レントゲン検査による被曝はごくわずかだ。","romaji":"Rentogen kensa ni yoru hibaku wa goku wazuka da.","german":"The radiation exposure from an X-ray examination is extremely small.","furigana":"けんさ|ひばく"},{"japanese":"事故の際、数名の作業員が被曝した。","romaji":"Jiko no sai, sūmei no sagyōin ga hibaku shita.","german":"In the accident, several workers were exposed to radiation.","furigana":"じこ|さい|すうめい|さぎょういん|ひばく"}],"notes":"被曝 means \"radiation exposure\", that is, being exposed to radioactive radiation; as an intransitive する-verb: 作業員が被曝した. Typical are 被曝線量 \"radiation dose\", 内部被曝 and 外部被曝. Not to be confused with the identically read 被爆 \"to be hit by an (atomic) bomb\", as in 被爆者 \"atomic bomb victim\". Since 曝 is not a standard kanji, government agencies often write 被ばく.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"プルトニウム239の半減期は約二万四千年である。","romaji":"Purutoniumu nihyaku sanjūkyū no hangenki wa yaku niman yonsen nen de aru.","german":"The half-life of plutonium-239 is about 24,000 years.","furigana":"はんげんき|やくにまんよんせんねん"},{"japanese":"セシウム137は半減期が約三十年と長い。","romaji":"Seshiumu hyaku sanjūnana wa hangenki ga yaku sanjū nen to nagai.","german":"Cesium-137 has a long half-life of about 30 years.","furigana":"はんげんき|やくさんじゅうねん|なが"},{"japanese":"この薬は半減期が短いので、一日に三回飲む必要がある。","romaji":"Kono kusuri wa hangenki ga mijikai node, ichinichi ni sankai nomu hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"This medicine has a short half-life, so it has to be taken three times a day.","furigana":"くすり|はんげんき|みじか|いちにち|さんかいの|ひつよう"}],"notes":"半減期 is the \"half-life\", the time in which the amount of a radioactive substance (or active ingredient) is halved. Typical patterns: Xの半減期は…年だ, 半減期が長い/短い. The word is also used in pharmacology for medicines (薬の半減期) and occasionally figuratively, e.g. for quickly fading interest. Register: scientific, but familiar from the news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"生態系サービスの経済的価値を評価する研究が進んでいる。","romaji":"Seitaikei sābisu no keizaiteki kachi o hyōka suru kenkyū ga susunde iru.","german":"Research evaluating the economic value of ecosystem services is advancing.","furigana":"せいたいけい|けいざいてきかち|ひょうか|けんきゅう|すす"},{"japanese":"ハチによる受粉も、大切な生態系サービスの一つだ。","romaji":"Hachi ni yoru jufun mo, taisetsu na seitaikei sābisu no hitotsu da.","german":"Pollination by bees is also one important ecosystem service.","furigana":"じゅふん|たいせつ|せいたいけい|ひと"},{"japanese":"森林が失われると、水をきれいにする生態系サービスも失われる。","romaji":"Shinrin ga ushinawareru to, mizu o kirei ni suru seitaikei sābisu mo ushinawareru.","german":"When forests are lost, the ecosystem service of cleaning water is lost as well.","furigana":"しんりん|うしな|みず|せいたいけい|うしな"}],"notes":"生態系サービス is the technical term for \"ecosystem services\": the benefits people derive from nature, such as food, clean water, pollination or recreation. Typical expressions are 生態系サービスを評価する, 生態系サービスの価値 and 生態系サービスを受ける. サービス here does not mean a service in the economic sense, but a \"provision\" of nature. Used almost only in science and environmental policy.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"有害物質が食物連鎖を通じて生物濃縮される。","romaji":"Yūgai busshitsu ga shokumotsu rensa o tsūjite seibutsu nōshuku sareru.","german":"Harmful substances become biologically concentrated through the food chain.","furigana":"ゆうがいぶっしつ|しょくもつれんさ|つう|せいぶつのうしゅく"},{"japanese":"水俣病は、水銀の生物濃縮によって起きた公害病だ。","romaji":"Minamatabyō wa, suigin no seibutsu nōshuku ni yotte okita kōgaibyō da.","german":"Minamata disease is a pollution disease caused by the bioconcentration of mercury.","furigana":"|すいぎん|せいぶつのうしゅく|お|こうがいびょう"},{"japanese":"マグロのような大型の魚ほど、生物濃縮の影響を受けやすい。","romaji":"Maguro no yō na ōgata no sakana hodo, seibutsu nōshuku no eikyō o ukeyasui.","german":"Large fish such as tuna are more susceptible to the effects of bioconcentration.","furigana":"おおがた|さかな|せいぶつのうしゅく|えいきょう|う"}],"notes":"生物濃縮 is \"bioaccumulation\" or \"biomagnification\": pollutants such as mercury or PCBs accumulate in organisms through the food chain. It mostly appears in the passive or as a subject: 生物濃縮される, 生物濃縮が起こる; typical is 食物連鎖を通じて \"through the food chain\". In Japan the concept is closely tied to Minamata disease (水俣病). A technical word from biology and environmental science.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"環境ホルモンが野生動物の生殖機能に影響を与えている。","romaji":"Kankyō horumon ga yasei dōbutsu no seishoku kinō ni eikyō o ataete iru.","german":"Environmental hormones are affecting the reproductive function of wild animals.","furigana":"かんきょう|やせいどうぶつ|せいしょくきのう|えいきょう|あた"},{"japanese":"プラスチック容器を電子レンジで温めると、環境ホルモンが溶け出すと心配する人もいる。","romaji":"Purasuchikku yōki o denshi renji de atatameru to, kankyō horumon ga tokedasu to shinpai suru hito mo iru.","german":"Some people worry that environmental hormones leach out when plastic containers are heated in a microwave.","furigana":"ようき|でんし|あたた|かんきょう|と|だ|しんぱい|ひと"},{"japanese":"環境ホルモンという言葉は九十年代に大きな話題になった。","romaji":"Kankyō horumon to iu kotoba wa kyūjū nendai ni ōki na wadai ni natta.","german":"The term \"environmental hormones\" became a big topic in the 1990s.","furigana":"かんきょう|ことば|きゅうじゅうねんだい|おお|わだい"}],"notes":"環境ホルモン is the popular term coined in Japan for \"endocrine disruptors\", that is, chemicals that act like hormones and disturb the hormone system. The technical term is 内分泌かく乱物質. The word was a major media topic in the late 1990s (e.g. bisphenol A in plastic tableware). Typical expressions: 環境ホルモンの影響, 環境ホルモンが溶け出す.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"都市部のヒートアイランド現象が熱中症の増加に繋がっている。","romaji":"Toshibu no hīto airando genshō ga netchūshō no zōka ni tsunagatte iru.","german":"The urban heat island phenomenon is leading to an increase in heatstroke.","furigana":"としぶ|げんしょう|ねっちゅうしょう|ぞうか|つな"},{"japanese":"ヒートアイランド対策として、ビルの屋上に緑を増やしている。","romaji":"Hīto airando taisaku toshite, biru no okujō ni midori o fuyashite iru.","german":"As a measure against the heat island effect, greenery is being added to building rooftops.","furigana":"たいさく|おくじょう|みどり|ふ"},{"japanese":"ヒートアイランドのせいで、東京は夜になっても気温が下がらない。","romaji":"Hīto airando no sei de, Tōkyō wa yoru ni natte mo kion ga sagaranai.","german":"Because of the heat island effect, the temperature in Tokyo does not drop even at night.","furigana":"とうきょう|よる|きおん|さ"}],"notes":"ヒートアイランド refers to the \"urban heat island\": cities are noticeably warmer than their surroundings because of asphalt, concrete and waste heat. It mostly appears in the combination ヒートアイランド現象 \"heat island effect\" or ヒートアイランド対策. Countermeasures include 屋上緑化 \"rooftop greening\" and 打ち水 \"sprinkling water\". Frequent in weather reports and urban planning.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"屋上緑化がヒートアイランド対策として注目されている。","romaji":"Okujō ryokuka ga hīto airando taisaku to shite chūmoku sarete iru.","german":"Rooftop greening is attracting attention as a measure against the heat island effect.","furigana":"おくじょうりょくか|たいさく|ちゅうもく"},{"japanese":"市は駅前の緑化を進めている。","romaji":"Shi wa ekimae no ryokuka o susumete iru.","german":"The city is promoting greening in front of the station.","furigana":"し|えきまえ|りょくか|すす"},{"japanese":"砂漠を緑化するプロジェクトに、ボランティアとして参加した。","romaji":"Sabaku o ryokuka suru purojekuto ni, borantia to shite sanka shita.","german":"I took part as a volunteer in a project to green the desert.","furigana":"さばく|りょくか|さんか"}],"notes":"緑化 means greening areas with plants and trees, as a transitive する-verb: 町を緑化する. Common compounds are 屋上緑化 \"rooftop greening\", 壁面緑化 \"wall greening\", 緑化運動 and 砂漠の緑化. Besides りょくか, the reading りょっか is also in use. The word belongs to urban planning and environmental policy.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"荒廃した山地に植林活動が行われている。","romaji":"Kōhai shita sanchi ni shokurin katsudō ga okonawarete iru.","german":"Tree planting activities are being carried out on devastated mountain land.","furigana":"こうはい|さんち|しょくりんかつどう|おこな"},{"japanese":"祖父は戦後、この山に杉を植林した。","romaji":"Sofu wa sengo, kono yama ni sugi o shokurin shita.","german":"After the war, my grandfather planted cedars on this mountain.","furigana":"そふ|せんご|やま|すぎ|しょくりん"},{"japanese":"会社の社会貢献として、社員が植林ボランティアに参加した。","romaji":"Kaisha no shakai kōken to shite, shain ga shokurin borantia ni sanka shita.","german":"As part of the company's social contribution, employees took part in a tree-planting volunteer event.","furigana":"かいしゃ|しゃかいこうけん|しゃいん|しょくりん|さんか"}],"notes":"植林 means deliberately planting trees on bare or logged-over land so that forest (re)grows. As a する-verb: 山に杉を植林する; also 植林地 \"afforested land\" and 植林活動. To be distinguished from 植樹 \"planting individual trees\" (often ceremonial, 植樹祭); the opposite is 伐採 \"logging\". Neutral, common in environmental and forestry contexts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"適切な間伐は森林の健全な成長を促す。","romaji":"Tekisetsu na kanbatsu wa shinrin no kenzen na seichō o unagasu.","german":"Proper thinning promotes the healthy growth of the forest.","furigana":"てきせつ|かんばつ|しんりん|けんぜん|せいちょう|うなが"},{"japanese":"間伐が遅れた人工林は、地面に日が当たらず荒れてしまう。","romaji":"Kanbatsu ga okureta jinkōrin wa, jimen ni hi ga atarazu arete shimau.","german":"Plantation forests that are not thinned in time become neglected because sunlight does not reach the ground.","furigana":"かんばつ|おく|じんこうりん|じめん|ひ|あ|あ"},{"japanese":"この割り箸は間伐材から作られています。","romaji":"Kono waribashi wa kanbatsuzai kara tsukurarete imasu.","german":"These disposable chopsticks are made from thinned timber.","furigana":"わ|ばし|かんばつざい|つく"}],"notes":"間伐 is the planned removal of individual trees from a stand that is too dense, so that the remaining ones receive light and grow strongly. As a する-verb: 森を間伐する; the wood produced in the process is called 間伐材 (e.g. for chopsticks). To be distinguished from 伐採 \"felling trees\" in general and 皆伐 \"clear-cutting\". Note: the identically pronounced 干ばつ/旱魃 means \"drought\". Technical, from forestry.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"遊休農地の活用が地域再生の鍵となっている。","romaji":"Yūkyū nōchi no katsuyō ga chiiki saisei no kagi to natte iru.","german":"Making use of idle farmland is the key to regional revival.","furigana":"ゆうきゅうのうち|かつよう|ちいきさいせい|かぎ"},{"japanese":"祖父母が亡くなってから、実家の畑は遊休農地になっている。","romaji":"Sofubo ga nakunatte kara, jikka no hatake wa yūkyū nōchi ni natte iru.","german":"Since my grandparents died, the field at my parents' home has become idle farmland.","furigana":"そふぼ|な|じっか|はたけ|ゆうきゅうのうち"},{"japanese":"町は遊休農地を若い新規就農者に貸し出している。","romaji":"Machi wa yūkyū nōchi o wakai shinki shūnōsha ni kashidashite iru.","german":"The town leases idle farmland to young new entrants to farming.","furigana":"まち|ゆうきゅうのうち|わか|しんきしゅうのうしゃ|か|だ"}],"notes":"遊休農地 is farmland that could be used for agriculture but is currently not being cultivated. 遊休 means \"unused, lying idle\" (cf. 遊休地, 遊休資産). Typical: 遊休農地を活用する/解消する/貸し出す. Closely related is 耕作放棄地 \"abandoned farmland\", which emphasizes permanent abandonment. Language of government agencies and newspapers; in everyday speech one tends to say 使っていない畑.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"環境配慮型の製品設計が企業に求められている。","romaji":"Kankyō hairyo gata no seihin sekkei ga kigyō ni motomerarete iru.","german":"Companies are required to design products with environmental considerations in mind.","furigana":"かんきょうはいりょがた|せいひんせっけい|きぎょう|もと"},{"japanese":"この商品は、環境配慮の観点からパッケージを紙に変えた。","romaji":"Kono shōhin wa, kankyō hairyo no kanten kara pakkēji o kami ni kaeta.","german":"For this product, the packaging was changed to paper from the standpoint of environmental consideration.","furigana":"しょうひん|かんきょうはいりょ|かんてん|かみ|か"},{"japanese":"新しい市庁舎は、太陽光発電などの環境配慮が評価された。","romaji":"Atarashii shichōsha wa, taiyōkō hatsuden nado no kankyō hairyo ga hyōka sareta.","german":"The new city hall was praised for its environmental considerations, such as solar power generation.","furigana":"あたら|しちょうしゃ|たいようこうはつでん|かんきょうはいりょ|ひょうか"}],"notes":"環境配慮 is a compound of 環境 \"environment\" and 配慮 \"consideration\" and refers to taking environmental concerns into account in products, buildings or corporate decisions. Typical are 環境配慮型 \"eco-friendly\" (環境配慮型製品) and 環境配慮の観点から. In running text, the verbal form 環境に配慮した〜 is even more common. Business and administrative language.","levelNote":"Compound: 環境 (N4) + 配慮 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"湿地の自然再生事業が各地で進められている。","romaji":"Shitchi no shizen saisei jigyō ga kakuchi de susumerarete iru.","german":"Wetland restoration projects are being carried out in various places.","furigana":"しっち|しぜんさいせいじぎょう|かくち|すす"},{"japanese":"コンクリートで固めた川を元の姿に戻す自然再生の試みが始まった。","romaji":"Konkurīto de katameta kawa o moto no sugata ni modosu shizen saisei no kokoromi ga hajimatta.","german":"An attempt at nature restoration has begun to return a concrete-lined river to its original form.","furigana":"かた|かわ|もと|すがた|もど|しぜんさいせい|こころ|はじ"},{"japanese":"自然再生には何十年もかかるので、地元住民の協力が欠かせない。","romaji":"Shizen saisei ni wa nanjūnen mo kakaru node, jimoto jūmin no kyōryoku ga kakasenai.","german":"Nature restoration takes decades, so the cooperation of local residents is indispensable.","furigana":"しぜんさいせい|なんじゅうねん|じもとじゅうみん|きょうりょく|か"}],"notes":"自然再生 refers to the restoration of damaged ecosystems such as wetlands, tidal flats or straightened rivers, that is, \"renaturation\". Typical: 自然再生事業, 自然再生に取り組む; the legal basis is the 自然再生推進法. In contrast to 自然保護 (protecting existing nature), this is about bringing back lost nature. 再生 alone is also known as \"playback\" for videos. Technical and administrative language.","levelNote":"Compound: 自然 (N4) + 再生 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ウミガメの保護活動にボランティアが参加している。","romaji":"Umigame no hogo katsudō ni borantia ga sanka shite iru.","german":"Volunteers are taking part in conservation activities for sea turtles.","furigana":"ほごかつどう|さんか"},{"japanese":"地元の高校生が干潟の野鳥の保護活動を続けている。","romaji":"Jimoto no kōkōsei ga higata no yachō no hogo katsudō o tsuzukete iru.","german":"Local high school students continue conservation activities for wild birds on the tidal flats.","furigana":"じもと|こうこうせい|ひがた|やちょう|ほごかつどう|つづ"},{"japanese":"保護活動には資金が必要なので、寄付を呼びかけています。","romaji":"Hogo katsudō ni wa shikin ga hitsuyō na node, kifu o yobikakete imasu.","german":"Conservation activities require funds, so we are appealing for donations.","furigana":"ほごかつどう|しきん|ひつよう|きふ|よ"}],"notes":"保護活動 are organized efforts to protect animals, nature or cultural properties: ウミガメの保護活動, 文化財の保護活動. Common verbs are 保護活動に参加する/取り組む and 保護活動を行う/続ける. 保護 emphasizes protecting something endangered; for the lasting preservation of whole ecosystems one tends to say 保全 (環境保全). Neutral, frequent in the news and among associations.","levelNote":"Compound: 保護 (N2) + 活動 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"保護活動の成果でトキの生息数が回復してきた。","romaji":"Hogo katsudō no seika de toki no seisokusū ga kaifuku shite kita.","german":"Thanks to conservation activities, the crested ibis population has recovered.","furigana":"ほごかつどう|せいか|せいそくすう|かいふく"},{"japanese":"研究チームは毎年、湖の白鳥の生息数を調べている。","romaji":"Kenkyū chīmu wa maitoshi, mizuumi no hakuchō no seisokusū o shirabete iru.","german":"The research team surveys the number of swans at the lake every year.","furigana":"けんきゅう|まいとし|みずうみ|はくちょう|せいそくすう|しら"},{"japanese":"シカの生息数が増えすぎて、農作物の被害が広がっている。","romaji":"Shika no seisokusū ga fuesugite, nōsakumotsu no higai ga hirogatte iru.","german":"The deer population has grown too large, and damage to crops is spreading.","furigana":"せいそくすう|ふ|のうさくもつ|ひがい|ひろ"}],"notes":"生息数 is the population count of a wild animal species in an area; 生息 means \"to live, inhabit (as an animal)\". Typical: 生息数が増える/減る/回復する, 生息数を調査する. For humans one says 人口, and in biology also 個体数 \"number of individuals\". Objective, in reports and news about nature conservation and wildlife damage.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"マグロの乱獲が国際的な問題となっている。","romaji":"Maguro no rankaku ga kokusaiteki na mondai to natte iru.","german":"Overfishing of tuna has become an international problem.","furigana":"らんかく|こくさいてき|もんだい"},{"japanese":"かつてクジラは世界中で乱獲され、数が激減した。","romaji":"Katsute kujira wa sekaijū de rankaku sare, kazu ga gekigen shita.","german":"Whales were once overhunted around the world, and their numbers plummeted.","furigana":"せかいじゅう|らんかく|かず|げきげん"},{"japanese":"ウナギの乱獲を防ぐため、稚魚の漁獲量が制限された。","romaji":"Unagi no rankaku o fusegu tame, chigyo no gyokakuryō ga seigen sareta.","german":"To prevent overfishing of eels, the catch of glass eels was restricted.","furigana":"らんかく|ふせ|ちぎょ|ぎょかくりょう|せいげん"}],"notes":"乱獲 is the indiscriminate, excessive catching or hunting of fish and wild animals, so that the stock is endangered; 乱 \"disorderly\", 獲 \"to capture\". As a する-verb, mostly in the passive: クジラが乱獲される. Unlike 密猟, 乱獲 need not be illegal, only excessive. For forests, the counterpart is 乱伐. Occasionally written 濫獲; objective, in environmental reports.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"象牙目的の密猟がアフリカゾウを絶滅の危機に追いやっている。","romaji":"Zōge mokuteki no mitsuryō ga Afurika zō o zetsumetsu no kiki ni oiyatte iru.","german":"Poaching for ivory is driving African elephants to the brink of extinction.","furigana":"ぞうげもくてき|みつりょう|ぜつめつ|きき|お"},{"japanese":"国立公園では密猟を防ぐため、レンジャーが夜も巡回している。","romaji":"Kokuritsu kōen de wa mitsuryō o fusegu tame, renjā ga yoru mo junkai shite iru.","german":"In the national park, rangers patrol even at night to prevent poaching.","furigana":"こくりつこうえん|みつりょう|ふせ|よる|じゅんかい"},{"japanese":"サイを密猟した男たちが逮捕された。","romaji":"Sai o mitsuryō shita otokotachi ga taiho sareta.","german":"The men who poached rhinoceroses were arrested.","furigana":"みつりょう|おとこ|たいほ"}],"notes":"密猟 is the prohibited, secret hunting of wild animals: 密 \"secret\", 猟 \"hunting\". As a する-verb: サイを密猟する; the offender is called 密猟者. Not to be confused with the identically pronounced 密漁 \"illegal fishing\" (character 漁 with the water radical). In contrast to 乱獲, 密猟 emphasizes illegality. Objective, in the news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"違法伐採が熱帯雨林の減少を加速させている。","romaji":"Ihō bassai ga nettai urin no genshō o kasoku sasete iru.","german":"Illegal logging is accelerating the decline of tropical rainforests.","furigana":"いほうばっさい|ねったいうりん|げんしょう|かそく"},{"japanese":"違法伐採された木材を輸入しないよう、業者に確認が求められている。","romaji":"Ihō bassai sareta mokuzai o yunyū shinai yō, gyōsha ni kakunin ga motomerarete iru.","german":"Dealers are required to confirm that they are not importing illegally logged timber.","furigana":"いほうばっさい|もくざい|ゆにゅう|ぎょうしゃ|かくにん|もと"},{"japanese":"衛星写真で違法伐採の現場が見つかった。","romaji":"Eisei shashin de ihō bassai no genba ga mitsukatta.","german":"A site of illegal logging was found on satellite images.","furigana":"えいせいしゃしん|いほうばっさい|げんば|み"}],"notes":"違法伐採 is the felling of trees without a permit or in violation of forestry laws, especially in tropical forests. Typical: 違法伐採された木材 \"illegally logged timber\", 違法伐採対策, 違法伐採を取り締まる. 伐採 is the neutral term for logging; 乱伐 means excessive, but not necessarily illegal, felling. News and government language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"海洋中のマイクロプラスチックが生態系に深刻な影響を与えている。","romaji":"Kaiyō chū no maikuro purasuchikku ga seitaikei ni shinkoku na eikyō o ataete iru.","german":"Microplastics in the oceans are having a serious impact on ecosystems.","furigana":"かいようちゅう|せいたいけい|しんこく|えいきょう|あた"},{"japanese":"洗顔料に含まれるマイクロプラスチックが規制されるようになった。","romaji":"Senganryō ni fukumareru maikuro purasuchikku ga kisei sareru yō ni natta.","german":"Microplastics contained in facial cleansers have come to be regulated.","furigana":"せんがんりょう|ふく|きせい"},{"japanese":"調査で、魚の胃からマイクロプラスチックが見つかった。","romaji":"Chōsa de, sakana no i kara maikuro purasuchikku ga mitsukatta.","german":"A survey found microplastics in the stomachs of fish.","furigana":"ちょうさ|さかな|い|み"}],"notes":"マイクロプラスチック are plastic particles smaller than about 5 mm, which come, for example, from disintegrating plastic waste or from cosmetics. Typical: マイクロプラスチックが海に流れ出る, 魚の体内から検出される, マイクロプラスチック汚染. A katakana loanword, treated as a mass noun (no counter needed). Neutral, very common in the news and in science.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"石油流出事故による海洋汚染が深刻化している。","romaji":"Sekiyu ryūshutsu jiko ni yoru kaiyō osen ga shinkokuka shite iru.","german":"Marine pollution from oil spill accidents is becoming more serious.","furigana":"せきゆりゅうしゅつじこ|かいようおせん|しんこくか"},{"japanese":"プラスチックごみによる海洋汚染は、私たちの生活と深く関わっている。","romaji":"Purasuchikku gomi ni yoru kaiyō osen wa, watashitachi no seikatsu to fukaku kakawatte iru.","german":"Marine pollution from plastic waste is closely tied to our daily lives.","furigana":"かいようおせん|わたし|せいかつ|ふか|かか"},{"japanese":"授業で海洋汚染について調べ、発表した。","romaji":"Jugyō de kaiyō osen ni tsuite shirabe, happyō shita.","german":"In class, I researched marine pollution and gave a presentation.","furigana":"じゅぎょう|かいようおせん|しら|はっぴょう"}],"notes":"海洋汚染 is the pollution of the seas by oil, plastic waste, chemicals or wastewater. 海洋 is the formal word for \"ocean, sea\"; in conversation one tends to say 海の汚れ or 海が汚れる. Typical: 海洋汚染が深刻化する, 海洋汚染を防ぐ, 〜による海洋汚染. It joins the series 大気汚染, 水質汚染, 土壌汚染. Objective, in the news and at school.","levelNote":"Compound: 海洋 (N2) + 汚染 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"発電所からの温排水が周辺海域の生態系に影響を与えている。","romaji":"Hatsudensho kara no on haisui ga shūhen kaiiki no seitaikei ni eikyō o ataete iru.","german":"Heated effluent from the power plant is affecting the ecosystem of the surrounding waters.","furigana":"はつでんしょ|おんはいすい|しゅうへんかいいき|せいたいけい|えいきょう|あた"},{"japanese":"原子力発電所は大量の温排水を海に放出する。","romaji":"Genshiryoku hatsudensho wa tairyō no on haisui o umi ni hōshutsu suru.","german":"Nuclear power plants discharge large amounts of heated cooling water into the sea.","furigana":"げんしりょくはつでんしょ|たいりょう|おんはいすい|うみ|ほうしゅつ"},{"japanese":"温排水の影響で、この湾では南の海の魚が見られるようになった。","romaji":"On haisui no eikyō de, kono wan de wa minami no umi no sakana ga mirareru yō ni natta.","german":"Because of the thermal discharge, fish from southern seas can now be seen in this bay.","furigana":"おんはいすい|えいきょう|わん|みなみ|うみ|さかな|み"}],"notes":"温排水 is the heated cooling water that thermal and nuclear power plants or factories return to the sea or rivers; it is usually several degrees warmer than the surrounding water. Typical: 温排水を放出する/海に流す, 温排水の影響. It is thus about the water itself, not household wastewater (生活排水). Technical language from energy and environmental engineering.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"森林は水源涵養機能を有している。","romaji":"Shinrin wa suigen kan'yō kinō o yū shite iru.","german":"Forests have the function of recharging water sources.","furigana":"しんりん|すいげんかんようきのう|ゆう"},{"japanese":"水源涵養のため、上流の森では伐採が制限されている。","romaji":"Suigen kan'yō no tame, jōryū no mori de wa bassai ga seigen sarete iru.","german":"For the sake of water source conservation, logging is restricted in the upstream forests.","furigana":"すいげんかんよう|じょうりゅう|もり|ばっさい|せいげん"},{"japanese":"市は水源涵養林の手入れに補助金を出している。","romaji":"Shi wa suigen kan'yōrin no teire ni hojokin o dashite iru.","german":"The city provides subsidies for the upkeep of watershed protection forests.","furigana":"し|すいげんかんようりん|てい|ほじょきん|だ"}],"notes":"水源涵養 refers to the ability of forests and soils to store rain, even out the runoff into rivers and purify water; 涵養 means \"to nourish gradually, to build up\". It occurs almost only in fixed expressions: 水源涵養機能, 水源涵養林. Since 涵 is not a jōyō kanji, government agencies often write 水源かん養. Very formal, forestry and administrative language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"海岸沿いの防災林が津波の被害を軽減した。","romaji":"Kaigan zoi no bōsairin ga tsunami no higai o keigen shita.","german":"The disaster-prevention forest along the coast reduced tsunami damage.","furigana":"かいがんぞ|ぼうさいりん|つなみ|ひがい|けいげん"},{"japanese":"震災後、海岸に新しい防災林を作るため、市民が松の苗を植えた。","romaji":"Shinsai go, kaigan ni atarashii bōsairin o tsukuru tame, shimin ga matsu no nae o ueta.","german":"After the earthquake, citizens planted pine seedlings to create a new disaster-prevention forest on the coast.","furigana":"しんさいご|かいがん|あたら|ぼうさいりん|つく|しみん|まつ|なえ|う"},{"japanese":"山の斜面の防災林は、大雨のときに土砂崩れを防ぐ役割がある。","romaji":"Yama no shamen no bōsairin wa, ōame no toki ni dosha kuzure o fusegu yakuwari ga aru.","german":"The disaster-prevention forest on the mountain slope plays a role in preventing landslides during heavy rain.","furigana":"やま|しゃめん|ぼうさいりん|おおあめ|どしゃくず|ふせ|やくわり"}],"notes":"防災林 is a forest that is deliberately created or maintained to protect land, roads or settlements from natural disasters. Depending on the hazard there are special terms: 防風林 \"windbreak forest\", 防潮林 or 海岸防災林 against storm surges and tsunamis, 防砂林 against drifting sand. Typical: 防災林を整備する/植える. Objective, in administration and the news, especially since 2011.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"環境税の導入により二酸化炭素排出の抑制が期待される。","romaji":"Kankyōzei no dōnyū ni yori nisanka tanso haishutsu no yokusei ga kitai sareru.","german":"The introduction of an environmental tax is expected to curb carbon dioxide emissions.","furigana":"かんきょうぜい|どうにゅう|にさんかたんそはいしゅつ|よくせい|きたい"},{"japanese":"環境税が導入されれば、ガソリン代が上がるかもしれない。","romaji":"Kankyōzei ga dōnyū sarereba, gasorin dai ga agaru ka mo shirenai.","german":"If an environmental tax is introduced, gasoline might get more expensive.","furigana":"かんきょうぜい|どうにゅう|だい|あ"},{"japanese":"環境税の税収は、再生可能エネルギーの普及に使われる。","romaji":"Kankyōzei no zeishū wa, saisei kanō enerugī no fukyū ni tsukawareru.","german":"Revenue from the environmental tax is used to promote the spread of renewable energy.","furigana":"かんきょうぜい|ぜいしゅう|さいせいかのう|ふきゅう|つか"}],"notes":"環境税 is a tax that makes environmentally harmful behavior (above all CO2 emissions) more expensive or finances environmental protection. In Japan, concrete examples are the 地球温暖化対策税 and the 森林環境税; closely related is 炭素税 \"carbon tax\". Typical: 環境税を導入する/課す, 環境税の税収. Objective, language of politics and business.","levelNote":"Compound: 環境 (N4) + 税 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"環境監査を通じて企業の環境管理体制が評価される。","romaji":"Kankyō kansa o tsūjite kigyō no kankyō kanri taisei ga hyōka sareru.","german":"Through environmental audits, companies' environmental management systems are evaluated.","furigana":"かんきょうかんさ|つう|きぎょう|かんきょうかんりたいせい|ひょうか"},{"japanese":"来月、外部の専門家による環境監査を受ける予定だ。","romaji":"Raigetsu, gaibu no senmonka ni yoru kankyō kansa o ukeru yotei da.","german":"Next month, we are scheduled to undergo an environmental audit by outside experts.","furigana":"らいげつ|がいぶ|せんもんか|かんきょうかんさ|う|よてい"},{"japanese":"環境監査で、廃棄物の記録が不十分だと指摘された。","romaji":"Kankyō kansa de, haikibutsu no kiroku ga fujūbun da to shiteki sareta.","german":"The environmental audit pointed out that the waste records were insufficient.","furigana":"かんきょうかんさ|はいきぶつ|きろく|ふじゅうぶん|してき"}],"notes":"環境監査 is the systematic examination of whether a company complies with environmental regulations and its own environmental management (e.g. ISO 14001). Typical: 環境監査を受ける/実施する, 内部環境監査. 監査 means an examination of processes and documents (like 会計監査); 検査 is more a technical inspection, and 審査 an assessment, for example for certification. Business and technical language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"排水基準を超えた工場に罰則が科される。","romaji":"Haisui kijun o koeta kōjō ni bassoku ga kasareru.","german":"Penalties are imposed on factories that exceed the effluent standards.","furigana":"はいすいきじゅん|こ|こうじょう|ばっそく|か"},{"japanese":"この工場の排水は、国の排水基準を十分に満たしている。","romaji":"Kono kōjō no haisui wa, kuni no haisui kijun o jūbun ni mitashite iru.","german":"This factory's wastewater fully meets the national effluent standards.","furigana":"こうじょう|はいすい|くに|はいすいきじゅん|じゅうぶん|み"},{"japanese":"県は国の排水基準より厳しい独自の基準を定めた。","romaji":"Ken wa kuni no haisui kijun yori kibishii dokuji no kijun o sadameta.","german":"The prefecture set its own standards that are stricter than the national effluent standards.","furigana":"けん|くに|はいすいきじゅん|きび|どくじ|きじゅん|さだ"}],"notes":"排水基準 are the legally set maximum values for pollutants in the wastewater of factories and businesses (in Japan under the 水質汚濁防止法). Typical: 排水基準を守る/満たす/超える, 排水基準に違反する. Not to be confused with 環境基準, which describes quality targets for rivers or air and does not apply directly to individual businesses. Language of government agencies and technical texts.","levelNote":"Compound: 排水 (N1) + 基準 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"山岳地帯では気温が低く気候が厳しい。","romaji":"Sangaku chitai de wa kion ga hikuku kikō ga kibishii.","german":"In mountainous regions, temperatures are low and the climate is harsh.","furigana":"さんがくちたい|きおん|ひく|きこう|きび"},{"japanese":"大学では山岳部に入って、毎週末山に登っていた。","romaji":"Daigaku de wa sangakubu ni haitte, maishūmatsu yama ni nobotte ita.","german":"At university I joined the mountaineering club and climbed mountains every weekend.","furigana":"だいがく|さんがくぶ|はい|まいしゅうまつやま|のぼ"},{"japanese":"冬山で遭難した登山者を山岳救助隊が救出した。","romaji":"Fuyuyama de sōnan shita tozansha o sangaku kyūjotai ga kyūshutsu shita.","german":"The mountain rescue team rescued the climbers who got into trouble in the winter mountains.","furigana":"ふゆやま|そうなん|とざんしゃ|さんがくきゅうじょたい|きゅうしゅつ"}],"notes":"山岳 is the literary word for \"mountains, mountain range\" and rarely stands alone, mostly appearing as a modifier: 山岳地帯 \"mountainous region\", 山岳部 \"mountaineering club\", 山岳救助隊 \"mountain rescue team\", 山岳信仰 \"mountain worship\". In everyday speech one simply says 山. 山脈, by contrast, refers to a specific, named mountain chain (アルプス山脈).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"台地の上は水はけが良く畑作に適している。","romaji":"Daichi no ue wa mizuhake ga yoku hatasaku ni tekishite iru.","german":"The top of the plateau is well drained and suitable for farming.","furigana":"だいち|うえ|みず|よ|はたさく|てき"},{"japanese":"昔の人々は、洪水を避けるために台地の上に村を作った。","romaji":"Mukashi no hitobito wa, kōzui o sakeru tame ni daichi no ue ni mura o tsukutta.","german":"In the old days, people built villages on top of plateaus to avoid floods.","furigana":"むかし|ひとびと|こうずい|さ|だいち|うえ|むら|つく"},{"japanese":"駅から台地の上の住宅地まで、急な坂を上らなければならない。","romaji":"Eki kara daichi no ue no jūtakuchi made, kyū na saka o noboranakereba naranai.","german":"From the station you have to climb a steep slope to the residential area on top of the plateau.","furigana":"えき|だいち|うえ|じゅうたくち|きゅう|さか|のぼ"}],"notes":"台地 refers to a raised piece of land, largely flat on top, with steep edges, such as the 武蔵野台地 near Tokyo; many fields and suburbs lie on such terraces. Unlike 高原 \"highland\", which lies considerably higher and is often a holiday region, a 台地 only needs to lie a few dozen meters above the plain; the counterpart is 低地 \"lowland\". Not to be confused with the identically read 大地 \"earth, ground\". A neutral term from geography and local history, mostly in 台地の上.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"なだらかな丘陵地帯にブドウ畑が広がっている。","romaji":"Nadaraka na kyūryō chitai ni budō batake ga hirogatte iru.","german":"Vineyards spread across the gently rolling hill country.","furigana":"きゅうりょうちたい|ばたけ|ひろ"},{"japanese":"多摩丘陵には、大規模な住宅地が開発された。","romaji":"Tama kyūryō ni wa, daikibo na jūtakuchi ga kaihatsu sareta.","german":"Large residential areas were developed in the Tama Hills.","furigana":"たまきゅうりょう|だいきぼ|じゅうたくち|かいはつ"},{"japanese":"丘陵地を歩くハイキングコースは、初心者にも人気がある。","romaji":"Kyūryōchi o aruku haikingu kōsu wa, shoshinsha ni mo ninki ga aru.","german":"Hiking courses through the hill country are popular even with beginners.","furigana":"きゅうりょうち|ある|しょしんしゃ|にんき"}],"notes":"丘陵 is extensive, gently undulating hill country, lower than real mountains. Typical: 丘陵地, 丘陵地帯 and in proper names such as 多摩丘陵. The everyday 丘 refers to a single hill (丘の上の家), whereas 丘陵 refers more to the landform; it is literary and geographical. Often with なだらかな \"gentle\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"東京湾の沿岸には大きな工業地帯が広がっている。","romaji":"Tōkyō wan no engan ni wa ōkina kōgyō chitai ga hirogatte iru.","german":"A large industrial zone stretches along the coast of Tokyo Bay.","furigana":"とうきょうわん|えんがん|おお|こうぎょうちたい|ひろ"},{"japanese":"湾内は波が穏やかなので、カキの養殖に向いている。","romaji":"Wannai wa nami ga odayaka na node, kaki no yōshoku ni muite iru.","german":"The waves inside the bay are calm, so it is well suited to oyster farming.","furigana":"わんない|なみ|おだ|ようしょく|む"},{"japanese":"湾に面したホテルからは、きれいな夕日が見える。","romaji":"Wan ni men shita hoteru kara wa, kirei na yūhi ga mieru.","german":"From the hotel facing the bay, you can see a beautiful sunset.","furigana":"わん|めん|ゆうひ|み"}],"notes":"湾 means \"bay, gulf\" and mostly appears as a suffix after names: 東京湾, 大阪湾, ペルシャ湾 \"Persian Gulf\", メキシコ湾. On its own it appears in 湾内 \"inside the bay\" or 湾に面した〜. Smaller, narrow inlets are rather called 入江. Related is 湾岸 \"bay coast\" (湾岸戦争 \"Gulf War\"). Not to be confused with 椀/碗 (わん) \"bowl\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"穏やかな入江に漁船が停泊していた。","romaji":"Odayaka na irie ni gyosen ga teihaku shite ita.","german":"Fishing boats were moored in the calm inlet.","furigana":"おだ|いりえ|ぎょせん|ていはく"},{"japanese":"小さな入江に、波の静かな砂浜が隠れていた。","romaji":"Chiisa na irie ni, nami no shizuka na sunahama ga kakurete ita.","german":"A sandy beach with quiet waves lay hidden in a small cove.","furigana":"ちい|いりえ|なみ|しず|すなはま|かく"},{"japanese":"昔、この入江は海賊の隠れ家だったと言われている。","romaji":"Mukashi, kono irie wa kaizoku no kakurega datta to iwarete iru.","german":"It is said that this inlet was once a pirate hideout.","furigana":"むかし|いりえ|かいぞく|かく|が|い"}],"notes":"入江 is a small bay or arm of the sea reaching deep into the land, also on lakes. It sounds scenic and somewhat literary; it is found in travelogues and novels (静かな入江, 入江に船を泊める). For large, named bays, 湾 is used (東京湾). 入江 also occurs as a family name.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"津軽海峡は本州と北海道を隔てている。","romaji":"Tsugaru kaikyō wa Honshū to Hokkaidō o hedatete iru.","german":"The Tsugaru Strait separates Honshu from Hokkaido.","furigana":"つがるかいきょう|ほんしゅう|ほっかいどう|へだ"},{"japanese":"関門海峡は潮の流れが速く、船の難所として知られる。","romaji":"Kanmon kaikyō wa shio no nagare ga hayaku, fune no nansho to shite shirareru.","german":"The Kanmon Strait has fast tidal currents and is known as a difficult passage for ships.","furigana":"かんもんかいきょう|しお|なが|はや|ふね|なんしょ|し"},{"japanese":"フェリーで海峡を渡って、九州から本州に戻った。","romaji":"Ferī de kaikyō o watatte, Kyūshū kara Honshū ni modotta.","german":"I crossed the strait by ferry and returned from Kyushu to Honshu.","furigana":"かいきょう|わた|きゅうしゅう|ほんしゅう|もど"}],"notes":"海峡 is a strait between two landmasses, mostly with a name in front: 津軽海峡, 関門海峡, ドーバー海峡, マラッカ海峡. Typical: 海峡を渡る, 海峡を隔てる, 海峡トンネル. Narrower sea passages are often called 水道 (豊後水道) or 瀬戸 (鳴門の瀬戸). Neutral, geographical.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本列島は環太平洋火山帯に位置している。","romaji":"Nihon rettō wa kan Taiheiyō kazantai ni ichi shite iru.","german":"The Japanese archipelago is located on the Pacific Ring of Fire.","furigana":"にほんれっとう|かんたいへいようかざんたい|いち"},{"japanese":"今週末は強い寒気が日本列島を覆うでしょう。","romaji":"Konshūmatsu wa tsuyoi kanki ga Nihon rettō o ōu deshō.","german":"This weekend, strong cold air will cover the whole Japanese archipelago.","furigana":"こんしゅうまつ|つよ|かんき|にほんれっとう|おお"},{"japanese":"千島列島は北海道から北東へ長く延びている。","romaji":"Chishima rettō wa Hokkaidō kara hokutō e nagaku nobite iru.","german":"The Kuril Islands stretch in a long line northeast from Hokkaido.","furigana":"ちしまれっとう|ほっかいどう|ほくとう|なが|の"}],"notes":"列島 is a long, elongated chain of islands lying in a row, above all in 日本列島 and 千島列島. In news and weather reports, 日本列島 often simply stands for \"all of Japan\": 寒波が日本列島を襲う, 列島各地で. To be distinguished from 諸島 \"island group\" (not necessarily in a line) and 群島 (closely clustered). Neutral to literary.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"小笠原諸島は世界自然遺産に登録されている。","romaji":"Ogasawara shotō wa sekai shizen isan ni tōroku sarete iru.","german":"The Ogasawara Islands are registered as a World Natural Heritage site.","furigana":"|せかいしぜんいさん|とうろく"},{"japanese":"ハワイ諸島は火山活動によってできた。","romaji":"Hawai shotō wa kazan katsudō ni yotte dekita.","german":"The Hawaiian Islands were formed by volcanic activity.","furigana":"しょとう|かざんかつどう"},{"japanese":"台風が奄美諸島に接近しているため、船の欠航が相次いでいる。","romaji":"Taifū ga Amami shotō ni sekkin shite iru tame, fune no kekkō ga aitsuide iru.","german":"Because a typhoon is approaching the Amami Islands, ship sailings are being canceled one after another.","furigana":"たいふう||せっきん|ふね|けっこう|あいつ"}],"notes":"諸島 refers to a group of islands that belong together and almost always stands after a name: 小笠原諸島, 奄美諸島, ハワイ諸島, ガラパゴス諸島. 諸 means \"various, several\". Unlike 列島, the islands do not need to lie in a line. In everyday speech one says 島々 \"the islands\". Neutral, geographical.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"利根川の流域面積は日本最大である。","romaji":"Tonegawa no ryūiki menseki wa Nihon saidai de aru.","german":"The Tone River's drainage basin is the largest in Japan.","furigana":"|りゅういきめんせき|にほんさいだい"},{"japanese":"大雨で、川の流域の住民に避難指示が出された。","romaji":"Ōame de, kawa no ryūiki no jūmin ni hinan shiji ga dasareta.","german":"Because of the heavy rain, evacuation orders were issued to residents of the river basin.","furigana":"おおあめ|かわ|りゅういき|じゅうみん|ひなんしじ|だ"},{"japanese":"黄河の流域では、古代から文明が栄えた。","romaji":"Kōga no ryūiki de wa, kodai kara bunmei ga sakaeta.","german":"In the Yellow River basin, civilization has flourished since ancient times.","furigana":"こうが|りゅういき|こだい|ぶんめい|さか"}],"notes":"流域 is the entire area drained by a river with its tributaries, and in a broader sense also the landscape along the river. Typical: 〜川流域, 流域面積 \"area of the drainage basin\", 流域の住民, 上流域/下流域. In everyday speech, one more often says 川沿い \"along the river\"; 流域 is factual and geographic, and is common in news about floods.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"川の上流には手付かずの自然が残っている。","romaji":"Kawa no jōryū ni wa tetsukazu no shizen ga nokotte iru.","german":"Unspoiled nature remains in the upper reaches of the river.","furigana":"かわ|じょうりゅう|てつ|しぜん|のこ"},{"japanese":"サケは卵を産むために川の上流へ上っていく。","romaji":"Sake wa tamago o umu tame ni kawa no jōryū e nobotte iku.","german":"Salmon swim upstream to lay their eggs.","furigana":"たまご|う|かわ|じょうりゅう|のぼ"},{"japanese":"彼女は上流階級の家庭に生まれた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa jōryū kaikyū no katei ni umareta.","german":"She was born into an upper-class family.","furigana":"かのじょ|じょうりゅうかいきゅう|かてい|う"}],"notes":"上流 is the upper course of a river or the direction \"upstream\": 川の上流, 上流へ向かう; counterparts are 中流 and 下流. In conversation one also hears 川上 (かわかみ). Figuratively, 上流 stands for the social upper class (上流階級, 上流社会); in business, 上流工程 refers to the early phases of a project.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"川の下流域には広大な平野が広がっている。","romaji":"Kawa no karyūiki ni wa kōdai na heiya ga hirogatte iru.","german":"A vast plain spreads across the lower reaches of the river.","furigana":"かわ|かりゅういき|こうだい|へいや|ひろ"},{"japanese":"ダムの放流で下流の水位が急に上がることがある。","romaji":"Damu no hōryū de karyū no suii ga kyū ni agaru koto ga aru.","german":"When a dam releases water, the water level downstream can rise suddenly.","furigana":"ほうりゅう|かりゅう|すいい|きゅう|あ"},{"japanese":"川に落ちた帽子は、あっという間に下流へ流されていった。","romaji":"Kawa ni ochita bōshi wa, atto iu ma ni karyū e nagasarete itta.","german":"The hat that fell into the river was swept downstream in no time.","furigana":"かわ|お|ぼうし|ま|かりゅう|なが"}],"notes":"下流 (かりゅう) is the lower course of a river or \"downstream\": 下流へ流される, 下流域. The counterpart is 上流; colloquially also 川下 (かわしも). Figuratively, 下流 means \"lower social class\" (下流社会); this sounds derogatory and should be used with care. Do not read it as したながれ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"多くの支流が本流に合流している。","romaji":"Ōku no shiryū ga honryū ni gōryū shite iru.","german":"Many tributaries join the main stream.","furigana":"おお|しりゅう|ほんりゅう|ごうりゅう"},{"japanese":"この川は信濃川の支流の一つだ。","romaji":"Kono kawa wa Shinanogawa no shiryū no hitotsu da.","german":"This river is one of the tributaries of the Shinano River.","furigana":"かわ||しりゅう|ひと"},{"japanese":"大雨で支流があふれ、周辺の田畑が水につかった。","romaji":"Ōame de shiryū ga afure, shūhen no tahata ga mizu ni tsukatta.","german":"Heavy rain made a tributary overflow, and the surrounding fields were flooded.","furigana":"おおあめ|しりゅう|しゅうへん|たはた|みず"}],"notes":"支流 is a tributary that flows into a larger river; the counterpart is 本流 \"main stream\". Typical: 〜川の支流, 支流が本流に合流する. Figuratively, 支流 can also refer to a side branch of a school or movement, e.g. in art or martial arts. Objective, geographical.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二つの川の合流点に街が発展した。","romaji":"Futatsu no kawa no gōryūten ni machi ga hatten shita.","german":"A town developed at the confluence of two rivers.","furigana":"ふた|かわ|ごうりゅうてん|まち|はってん"},{"japanese":"仕事が終わったら、私も後で合流します。","romaji":"Shigoto ga owattara, watashi mo ato de gōryū shimasu.","german":"I'll join you later, after I finish work.","furigana":"しごと|お|わたし|あと|ごうりゅう"},{"japanese":"高速道路の合流地点では、スピードを合わせて入ることが大切だ。","romaji":"Kōsoku dōro no gōryū chiten de wa, supīdo o awasete hairu koto ga taisetsu da.","german":"When merging onto an expressway, it is important to match your speed.","furigana":"こうそくどうろ|ごうりゅうちてん|あ|はい|たいせつ"}],"notes":"合流 first of all means the flowing together of rivers (合流点 \"confluence\"), but in everyday speech it is very often used for people: 後で合流する \"to join later\", 駅で合流する. Intransitive する-verb: AがBに合流する or AとBが合流する. In traffic it means merging (合流車線), in politics the merger of parties. To be distinguished from 集合 \"to gather at a meeting point\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ナイル川の三角州は世界的に有名である。","romaji":"Nairu gawa no sankakusu wa sekaiteki ni yūmei de aru.","german":"The Nile Delta is world famous.","furigana":"がわ|さんかくす|せかいてき|ゆうめい"},{"japanese":"広島市は太田川の三角州の上に発展した町だ。","romaji":"Hiroshima-shi wa Ōtagawa no sankakusu no ue ni hatten shita machi da.","german":"Hiroshima is a city that developed on the delta of the Ōta River.","furigana":"ひろしまし||さんかくす|うえ|はってん|まち"},{"japanese":"三角州は土地が低いため、高潮の被害を受けやすい。","romaji":"Sankakusu wa tochi ga hikui tame, takashio no higai o ukeyasui.","german":"Because deltas are low-lying, they are prone to storm surge damage.","furigana":"さんかくす|とち|ひく|たかしお|ひがい|う"}],"notes":"三角州 is a river delta, that is, the usually triangular alluvial area at a river mouth where the river deposits sand and mud. 州 means \"sandbar\" here and is read す (cf. 中州, 砂州), not しゅう as in カリフォルニア州. The loanword デルタ (デルタ地帯) is synonymous. School geography, objective.","levelNote":"Compound: 三角 (N3) + 州 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"扇状地は果樹栽培に適した地形である。","romaji":"Senjōchi wa kaju saibai ni tekishita chikei de aru.","german":"Alluvial fans are a landform suited to fruit growing.","furigana":"せんじょうち|かじゅさいばい|てき|ちけい"},{"japanese":"扇状地の中央部は水はけがよく、川の水が地下にしみ込んでしまう。","romaji":"Senjōchi no chūōbu wa mizuhake ga yoku, kawa no mizu ga chika ni shimikonde shimau.","german":"The middle part of an alluvial fan is well drained, and the river water seeps underground.","furigana":"せんじょうち|ちゅうおうぶ|みず|かわ|みず|ちか|こ"},{"japanese":"地理の授業で、扇状地のでき方を図にかいて説明した。","romaji":"Chiri no jugyō de, senjōchi no dekikata o zu ni kaite setsumei shita.","german":"In geography class, we explained how an alluvial fan forms using a diagram.","furigana":"ちり|じゅぎょう|せんじょうち|かた|ず|せつめい"}],"notes":"扇状地 is an alluvial fan: where a river emerges from the mountains onto the plain, it deposits gravel and sand in a fan shape (扇状 \"fan-shaped\"). In geography class, one distinguishes 扇頂, 扇央 and 扇端. The middle part is very permeable (水はけがよい) and suited to fruit growing; springs often emerge at the edge. A technical term of geography.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"氾濫原は肥沃な土壌を持ち農業に利用される。","romaji":"Hanrangen wa hiyoku na dojō o mochi nōgyō ni riyō sareru.","german":"Floodplains have fertile soil and are used for agriculture.","furigana":"はんらんげん|ひよく|どじょう|も|のうぎょう|りよう"},{"japanese":"氾濫原に建てられた住宅地は、洪水のリスクが高い。","romaji":"Hanrangen ni taterareta jūtakuchi wa, kōzui no risuku ga takai.","german":"Residential areas built on floodplains have a high flood risk.","furigana":"はんらんげん|た|じゅうたくち|こうずい|たか"},{"japanese":"氾濫原には、自然堤防や後背湿地といった地形が見られる。","romaji":"Hanrangen ni wa, shizen teibō ya kōhai shitchi to itta chikei ga mirareru.","german":"Landforms such as natural levees and back marshes can be seen on floodplains.","furigana":"はんらんげん|しぜんていぼう|こうはいしっち|ちけい|み"}],"notes":"氾濫原 is the flat valley floor next to a river that is regularly flooded at high water; 氾濫 means \"flood, overflowing the banks\", 原 \"plain\". Typical landforms within it are 自然堤防 \"natural levee\" and 後背湿地 \"back marsh\". A technical term from geography and flood control; in everyday speech one tends to say 浸水しやすい土地.","levelNote":"Compound: 氾濫 (N1) + 原 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"褶曲山脈は地殻の圧縮によって形成される。","romaji":"Shūkyoku sanmyaku wa chikaku no asshuku ni yotte keisei sareru.","german":"Fold mountains are formed by compression of the Earth's crust.","furigana":"しゅうきょくさんみゃく|ちかく|あっしゅく|けいせい"},{"japanese":"道路沿いの崖で、地層が大きく褶曲しているのが見える。","romaji":"Dōro zoi no gake de, chisō ga ōkiku shūkyoku shite iru no ga mieru.","german":"In the cliff along the road, you can see the strata folded greatly.","furigana":"どうろぞ|がけ|ちそう|おお|しゅうきょく|み"},{"japanese":"褶曲と断層の違いを、粘土を使って実験した。","romaji":"Shūkyoku to dansō no chigai o, nendo o tsukatte jikken shita.","german":"We experimented with clay to show the difference between folding and faulting.","furigana":"しゅうきょく|だんそう|ちが|ねんど|つか|じっけん"}],"notes":"褶曲 is the geological folding of rock layers by lateral pressure. As an intransitive する-verb: 地層が褶曲する; also 褶曲山脈 \"fold mountains\". To be distinguished from 断層 \"fault\", where the layers break instead of bending. 褶 is not a jōyō kanji, so it is also written しゅう曲. A purely technical term.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本は四つのプレートが交わる場所に位置している。","romaji":"Nihon wa yottsu no purēto ga majiwaru basho ni ichi shite iru.","german":"Japan is located where four plates meet.","furigana":"にほん|よっ|まじ|ばしょ|いち"},{"japanese":"太平洋プレートが北米プレートの下に沈み込んでいる。","romaji":"Taiheiyō purēto ga Hokubei purēto no shita ni shizumikonde iru.","german":"The Pacific Plate is subducting beneath the North American Plate.","furigana":"たいへいよう|ほくべい|した|しず|こ"},{"japanese":"プレートの境界では、大きな地震が起こりやすい。","romaji":"Purēto no kyōkai de wa, ōkina jishin ga okoriyasui.","german":"Large earthquakes tend to occur at plate boundaries.","furigana":"きょうかい|おお|じしん|お"}],"notes":"プレート has many meanings (name plate, license plate ナンバープレート, plate/dish, the home plate in baseball); in geography it is the tectonic plate. Mostly with names: 太平洋プレート, フィリピン海プレート, ユーラシアプレート, 北米プレート. Typical: プレートが沈み込む, プレートの境界, プレートテクトニクス. Neutral, familiar from textbooks and earthquake reports.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ヒマラヤ山脈は造山運動によって隆起した。","romaji":"Himaraya sanmyaku wa zōzan undō ni yotte ryūki shita.","german":"The Himalayas were uplifted by orogeny.","furigana":"さんみゃく|ぞうざんうんどう|りゅうき"},{"japanese":"アルプス山脈は、今も続く造山運動によって少しずつ高くなっている。","romaji":"Arupusu sanmyaku wa, ima mo tsuzuku zōzan undō ni yotte sukoshi zutsu takaku natte iru.","german":"The Alps are gradually getting higher through orogeny that continues even now.","furigana":"さんみゃく|いま|つづ|ぞうざんうんどう|すこ|たか"},{"japanese":"山頂付近で貝の化石が見つかるのは、造山運動で海底が持ち上げられたからだ。","romaji":"Sanchō fukin de kai no kaseki ga mitsukaru no wa, zōzan undō de kaitei ga mochiagerareta kara da.","german":"Shell fossils are found near the summit because the seabed was lifted up by orogeny.","furigana":"さんちょうふきん|かい|かせき|み|ぞうざんうんどう|かいてい|も|あ"}],"notes":"造山運動 is mountain building (orogeny) through movements of the Earth's crust; 運動 means \"movement\" here, not \"sport\". Typical: 造山運動によって隆起する/形成される. The zones formed in the process are called 造山帯, e.g. 環太平洋造山帯 or アルプス・ヒマラヤ造山帯. A technical term of geology and school geography.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地殻の隆起により海底が陸地となった。","romaji":"Chikaku no ryūki ni yori kaitei ga rikuchi to natta.","german":"The uplift of the Earth's crust turned the seabed into dry land.","furigana":"ちかく|りゅうき|かいてい|りくち"},{"japanese":"大地震で海岸が二メートルほど隆起した。","romaji":"Daijishin de kaigan ga ni mētoru hodo ryūki shita.","german":"The coast rose by about two meters in the big earthquake.","furigana":"だいじしん|かいがん|に|りゅうき"},{"japanese":"皮膚に小さな隆起ができたので、皮膚科で診てもらった。","romaji":"Hifu ni chiisa na ryūki ga dekita node, hifuka de mite moratta.","german":"A small bump formed on my skin, so I had it checked at a dermatologist.","furigana":"ひふ|ちい|りゅうき|ひふか|み"}],"notes":"隆起 is rising or bulging up, above all of land: 土地が隆起する, 地殻の隆起. The opposite is 沈降 \"subsidence\". As an intransitive する-verb it has no transitive counterpart; for \"to lift up\" one says 持ち上げる. Also used for bulges on the body (皮膚の隆起, 筋肉の隆起). Literary, in geology and medicine.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地盤の沈降により海面下の土地が広がった。","romaji":"Jiban no chinkō ni yori kaimenka no tochi ga hirogatta.","german":"Subsidence of the ground enlarged the area of land below sea level.","furigana":"じばん|ちんこう|かいめんか|とち|ひろ"},{"japanese":"瀬戸内海は、土地の沈降によって生まれた海だと言われている。","romaji":"Setonaikai wa, tochi no chinkō ni yotte umareta umi da to iwarete iru.","german":"The Seto Inland Sea is said to have been created by the sinking of land.","furigana":"せとないかい|とち|ちんこう|う|うみ|い"},{"japanese":"血液検査で赤血球の沈降速度を調べた。","romaji":"Ketsueki kensa de sekkekkyū no chinkō sokudo o shirabeta.","german":"In the blood test, the erythrocyte sedimentation rate was checked.","furigana":"けつえきけんさ|せっけっきゅう|ちんこうそくど|しら"}],"notes":"沈降 has two technical meanings: the sinking of land (the opposite of 隆起) and the settling of particles in a liquid, for example 赤血球沈降速度 \"erythrocyte sedimentation rate\". Intransitive する-verb: 土地が沈降する. For the everyday sinking of the ground due to groundwater extraction, one usually says 地盤沈下. Literary and technical.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"河川による堆積平野は農業に適している。","romaji":"Kasen ni yoru taiseki heiya wa nōgyō ni tekishite iru.","german":"Depositional plains formed by rivers are suited to agriculture.","furigana":"かせん|たいせきへいや|のうぎょう|てき"},{"japanese":"関東平野は、日本で最も大きな堆積平野だ。","romaji":"Kantō heiya wa, Nihon de mottomo ōkina taiseki heiya da.","german":"The Kanto Plain is the largest depositional plain in Japan.","furigana":"かんとうへいや|にほん|もっと|おお|たいせきへいや"},{"japanese":"地理の教科書では、平野を堆積平野と侵食平野に分けている。","romaji":"Chiri no kyōkasho de wa, heiya o taiseki heiya to shinshoku heiya ni wakete iru.","german":"In geography textbooks, plains are divided into depositional plains and erosional plains.","furigana":"ちり|きょうかしょ|へいや|たいせきへいや|しんしょくへいや|わ"}],"notes":"堆積平野 is a plain formed by sediments deposited by rivers or the sea; 堆積 means \"deposition\". The opposite is 侵食平野 \"erosional plain\". Depositional plains include 沖積平野 and 海岸平野; most of Japan's large plains are of this type. A technical term of school geography.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"石灰岩が溶食されてカルスト地形が形成された。","romaji":"Sekkaigan ga yōshoku sarete karusuto chikei ga keisei sareta.","german":"Limestone was dissolved, and karst topography was formed.","furigana":"せっかいがん|ようしょく|ちけい|けいせい"},{"japanese":"山口県の秋吉台は、日本最大級のカルスト地形として知られている。","romaji":"Yamaguchi-ken no Akiyoshidai wa, Nihon saidaikyū no karusuto chikei to shite shirarete iru.","german":"Akiyoshidai in Yamaguchi Prefecture is known as one of the largest karst landscapes in Japan.","furigana":"やまぐちけん|あきよしだい|にほんさいだいきゅう|ちけい|し"},{"japanese":"カルスト地形の地下には、鍾乳洞が広がっていることが多い。","romaji":"Karusuto chikei no chika ni wa, shōnyūdō ga hirogatte iru koto ga ōi.","german":"Beneath karst topography, limestone caves often extend.","furigana":"ちけい|ちか|しょうにゅうどう|ひろ|おお"}],"notes":"カルスト地形 is a karst landscape, which forms when rainwater dissolves limestone (石灰岩). Typical forms are sinkholes (ドリーネ) and limestone caves (鍾乳洞). The best-known example in Japan is the plateau 秋吉台. Briefly, one also says just カルスト. A technical term of geography, but also familiar from travel guides.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"河岸段丘の上に集落が形成されている。","romaji":"Kagan dankyū no ue ni shūraku ga keisei sarete iru.","german":"A settlement has formed on the river terrace.","furigana":"かがんだんきゅう|うえ|しゅうらく|けいせい"},{"japanese":"川沿いを歩くと、階段のような河岸段丘がはっきり分かる。","romaji":"Kawazoi o aruku to, kaidan no yō na kagan dankyū ga hakkiri wakaru.","german":"Walking along the river, you can clearly see the stair-like river terraces.","furigana":"かわぞ|ある|かいだん|かがんだんきゅう|わ"},{"japanese":"河岸段丘は、土地の隆起と川の侵食がくり返されてできる。","romaji":"Kagan dankyū wa, tochi no ryūki to kawa no shinshoku ga kurikaesarete dekiru.","german":"River terraces form through repeated land uplift and river erosion.","furigana":"かがんだんきゅう|とち|りゅうき|かわ|しんしょく|かえ"}],"notes":"河岸段丘 are stair-like river terraces along a valley; they form when land rises and the river cuts down deeper again and again. The flat steps (段丘面) often carry settlements and fields; the steep edges are called 段丘崖. 河岸 is read かがん here; かし is the riverbank with a fish market (魚河岸). A technical term of geography.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"海底火山の噴火によって新しい島が誕生した。","romaji":"Kaitei kazan no funka ni yotte atarashii shima ga tanjō shita.","german":"A new island was born from the eruption of an undersea volcano.","furigana":"かいていかざん|ふんか|あたら|しま|たんじょう"},{"japanese":"沈没船は今も深い海底に眠っている。","romaji":"Chinbotsusen wa ima mo fukai kaitei ni nemutte iru.","german":"The sunken ship still lies asleep on the deep seabed.","furigana":"ちんぼつせん|いま|ふか|かいてい|ねむ"},{"japanese":"青函トンネルは、海底を通る鉄道トンネルだ。","romaji":"Seikan tonneru wa, kaitei o tōru tetsudō tonneru da.","german":"The Seikan Tunnel is a railway tunnel that runs under the seabed.","furigana":"|かいてい|とお|てつどう"}],"notes":"海底 is the seabed or sea floor: 海底に沈む, 深い海底. It very often stands before other nouns in the sense of \"undersea\": 海底トンネル, 海底ケーブル, 海底火山, 海底地震. To be distinguished from 海中 \"in the sea, underwater\" (the water column) and the more general 水底 \"bottom of a body of water\". Neutral.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本海溝は太平洋プレートの沈み込みによって形成された。","romaji":"Nihon kaikō wa Taiheiyō purēto no shizumikomi ni yotte keisei sareta.","german":"The Japan Trench was formed by the subduction of the Pacific Plate.","furigana":"にほんかいこう|たいへいよう|しず|こ|けいせい"},{"japanese":"マリアナ海溝は世界で最も深い海溝だ。","romaji":"Mariana kaikō wa sekai de mottomo fukai kaikō da.","german":"The Mariana Trench is the deepest ocean trench in the world.","furigana":"かいこう|せかい|もっと|ふか|かいこう"},{"japanese":"東日本大震災を引き起こした地震は、日本海溝沿いで発生した。","romaji":"Higashi Nihon daishinsai o hikiokoshita jishin wa, Nihon kaikō zoi de hassei shita.","german":"The earthquake that caused the Great East Japan Earthquake disaster occurred along the Japan Trench.","furigana":"ひがしにほんだいしんさい|ひ|お|じしん|にほんかいこうぞ|はっせい"}],"notes":"海溝 is an ocean trench, a long, very deep groove in the seabed where one oceanic plate dives under another. Mostly with names: マリアナ海溝, 日本海溝, 千島海溝. Less deep grooves of this kind are called トラフ (南海トラフ). 溝 means \"ditch, groove\" (cf. 溝 みぞ). A technical term, often in earthquake reports.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大陸棚は豊かな漁場として知られている。","romaji":"Tairikudana wa yutaka na gyojō to shite shirarete iru.","german":"The continental shelf is known as a rich fishing ground.","furigana":"たいりくだな|ゆた|ぎょじょう|し"},{"japanese":"大陸棚の海底には石油や天然ガスが眠っている可能性がある。","romaji":"Tairikudana no kaitei ni wa sekiyu ya tennen gasu ga nemutte iru kanōsei ga aru.","german":"Oil and natural gas may lie beneath the seabed of the continental shelf.","furigana":"たいりくだな|かいてい|せきゆ|てんねん|ねむ|かのうせい"},{"japanese":"両国は大陸棚の境界をめぐって交渉を続けている。","romaji":"Ryōkoku wa tairikudana no kyōkai o megutte kōshō o tsuzukete iru.","german":"The two countries continue to negotiate over the boundary of the continental shelf.","furigana":"りょうこく|たいりくだな|きょうかい|こうしょう|つづ"}],"notes":"大陸棚 is the shallow seabed that extends from the mainland to a water depth of about 200 m, before the steep 大陸斜面 \"continental slope\" begins. The word is encountered in geography, fishing and above all in maritime law: because of oil and gas deposits, the 大陸棚 often appears in the news alongside the 排他的経済水域 \"exclusive economic zone\". 棚 is read だな here with rendaku, as in 本棚. A purely technical term, not an everyday word.","levelNote":"Compound: 大陸 (N3) + 棚 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"モルディブは環礁からなる島国である。","romaji":"Morudibu wa kanshō kara naru shimaguni de aru.","german":"The Maldives is an island nation made up of atolls.","furigana":"かんしょう|しまぐに"},{"japanese":"環礁の内側には、波の静かなラグーンが広がっている。","romaji":"Kanshō no uchigawa ni wa, nami no shizuka na ragūn ga hirogatte iru.","german":"Inside the atoll lies a calm lagoon.","furigana":"かんしょう|うちがわ|なみ|しず|ひろ"},{"japanese":"海面が上昇すると、低い環礁の島々は水没するおそれがある。","romaji":"Kaimen ga jōshō suru to, hikui kanshō no shimajima wa suibotsu suru osore ga aru.","german":"If sea levels rise, the islands of low-lying atolls are at risk of being submerged.","furigana":"かいめん|じょうしょう|ひく|かんしょう|しまじま|すいぼつ"}],"notes":"環礁 is an atoll, a ring-shaped coral reef that encloses a lagoon (礁湖, ラグーン). Coral reefs in general are called サンゴ礁; 環 \"ring\" makes the shape clear. Beware of many homophones: 鑑賞 \"appreciation (of art)\", 干渉 \"interference\", 感傷 \"sentimentality\". Objective, geographical.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"船が霧の中で暗礁に乗り上げた。","romaji":"Fune ga kiri no naka de anshō ni noriageta.","german":"The ship ran onto a hidden reef in the fog.","furigana":"ふね|きり|なか|あんしょう|の|あ"},{"japanese":"両国の交渉は、領土問題で暗礁に乗り上げた。","romaji":"Ryōkoku no kōshō wa, ryōdo mondai de anshō ni noriageta.","german":"The negotiations between the two countries ran aground over the territorial issue.","furigana":"りょうこく|こうしょう|りょうどもんだい|あんしょう|の|あ"},{"japanese":"資金が集まらず、駅前の再開発計画は暗礁に乗り上げている。","romaji":"Shikin ga atsumarazu, ekimae no saikaihatsu keikaku wa anshō ni noriagete iru.","german":"Funds are not coming in, and the redevelopment plan in front of the station has hit a snag.","furigana":"しきん|あつ|えきまえ|さいかいはつけいかく|あんしょう|の|あ"}],"notes":"暗礁 is a reef or rock hidden just below the water surface that is dangerous for ships. It is most often encountered in the idiom 暗礁に乗り上げる, literally \"to run onto a reef\", figuratively \"to come to a standstill, to get stuck\", for instance in negotiations or projects. The running aground of a ship itself is called 座礁. Literary, very familiar from the news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"天橋立は砂州によって形成された景勝地である。","romaji":"Amanohashidate wa sasu ni yotte keisei sareta keishōchi de aru.","german":"Amanohashidate is a scenic spot formed by a sandbar.","furigana":"|さす|けいせい|けいしょうち"},{"japanese":"砂州によって湾がふさがれ、湖ができた。","romaji":"Sasu ni yotte wan ga fusagare, mizuumi ga dekita.","german":"A sandbar closed off the bay, and a lake was formed.","furigana":"さす|わん|みずうみ"},{"japanese":"砂州の上の細い道を、松並木に沿って歩いた。","romaji":"Sasu no ue no hosoi michi o, matsu namiki ni sotte aruita.","german":"I walked along the narrow path on the sandbar, beside a row of pine trees.","furigana":"さす|うえ|ほそ|みち|まつなみき|そ|ある"}],"notes":"砂州 is a sandbar piled up by currents, which often grows from the coast into a bay and almost closes it off (spit); the most famous example is 天橋立. Read さす, not さしゅう; also written 砂洲. A sand island in the middle of a river is called 中州. A technical term of geography, but also found in travel guides.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"沖積平野は河川の堆積作用によって形成される。","romaji":"Chūseki heiya wa kasen no taiseki sayō ni yotte keisei sareru.","german":"Alluvial plains are formed by the depositional action of rivers.","furigana":"ちゅうせきへいや|かせん|たいせきさよう|けいせい"},{"japanese":"日本の大都市の多くは、沖積平野の上に作られている。","romaji":"Nihon no daitoshi no ōku wa, chūseki heiya no ue ni tsukurarete iru.","german":"Many of Japan's big cities are built on alluvial plains.","furigana":"にほん|だいとし|おお|ちゅうせきへいや|うえ|つく"},{"japanese":"沖積平野の地盤はやわらかく、地震のときに液状化が起きやすい。","romaji":"Chūseki heiya no jiban wa yawarakaku, jishin no toki ni ekijōka ga okiyasui.","german":"The ground of alluvial plains is soft, so liquefaction easily occurs during earthquakes.","furigana":"ちゅうせきへいや|じばん|じしん|えきじょうか|お"}],"notes":"沖積平野 is a young alluvial plain that rivers have built up in the most recent geological history (Holocene, formerly 沖積世). It includes 扇状地, 氾濫原 and 三角州. Its counterpart is the older, higher-lying 洪積台地. Since many of Japan's cities lie on it, it is vulnerable to flooding and soil liquefaction because of its soft ground. A technical term.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"武蔵野台地は洪積台地の代表例である。","romaji":"Musashino daichi wa kōseki daichi no daihyōrei de aru.","german":"The Musashino Plateau is a typical example of a diluvial upland.","furigana":"|こうせきだいち|だいひょうれい"},{"japanese":"洪積台地は地盤がしっかりしているため、地震に比較的強い。","romaji":"Kōseki daichi wa jiban ga shikkari shite iru tame, jishin ni hikakuteki tsuyoi.","german":"Diluvial uplands have firm ground, so they are relatively resistant to earthquakes.","furigana":"こうせきだいち|じばん|じしん|ひかくてきつよ"},{"japanese":"洪積台地の上は水が得にくく、昔は畑や雑木林が多かった。","romaji":"Kōseki daichi no ue wa mizu ga enikuku, mukashi wa hatake ya zōkibayashi ga ōkatta.","german":"Water was hard to obtain on diluvial uplands, so in the past there were mostly fields and coppice woodland.","furigana":"こうせきだいち|うえ|みず|え|むかし|はたけ|ぞうきばやし|おお"}],"notes":"洪積台地 is an upland made of deposits from the Pleistocene (formerly 洪積世) that lies somewhat higher than the surrounding 沖積平野. The ground is firm and well drained, but water used to be scarce there. A well-known example: 武蔵野台地. The term 洪積 is considered outdated in technical language (today 更新世, 段丘), but is still common in textbooks and place descriptions.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"三陸海岸はリアス式海岸の典型例である。","romaji":"Sanriku kaigan wa riasu shiki kaigan no tenkeirei de aru.","german":"The Sanriku Coast is a typical example of a ria coast.","furigana":"さんりくかいがん|しきかいがん|てんけいれい"},{"japanese":"リアス式海岸は湾が深く入り組んでいるため、津波が高くなりやすい。","romaji":"Riasu shiki kaigan wa wan ga fukaku irikunde iru tame, tsunami ga takaku nariyasui.","german":"Because the bays of a ria coast are deep and intricate, tsunamis tend to become higher there.","furigana":"しきかいがん|わん|ふか|い|く|つなみ|たか"},{"japanese":"波の静かなリアス式海岸の湾では、カキやワカメの養殖が盛んだ。","romaji":"Nami no shizuka na riasu shiki kaigan no wan de wa, kaki ya wakame no yōshoku ga sakan da.","german":"In the calm bays of the ria coast, farming of oysters and wakame thrives.","furigana":"なみ|しず|しきかいがん|わん|ようしょく|さか"}],"notes":"リアス式海岸 is a ria coast: river valleys have been drowned by the sea, creating a heavily indented, saw-toothed coast with deep bays. Well-known examples in Japan: 三陸海岸, 志摩半島, 若狭湾. The calm bays are suited to harbors and aquaculture, but amplify tsunamis. More recent textbooks also write リアス海岸.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ノルウェーのフィヨルドは氷河の浸食によって形成された。","romaji":"Noruwē no fiyorudo wa hyōga no shinshoku ni yotte keisei sareta.","german":"The fjords of Norway were formed by glacial erosion.","furigana":"ひょうが|しんしょく|けいせい"},{"japanese":"夏休みにフィヨルドを巡るクルーズに参加した。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi ni fiyorudo o meguru kurūzu ni sanka shita.","german":"During summer vacation, I took a cruise around the fjords.","furigana":"なつやす|めぐ|さんか"},{"japanese":"フィヨルドは氷河が削った谷に、リアス式海岸は川が削った谷に海水が入ってできた。","romaji":"Fiyorudo wa hyōga ga kezutta tani ni, riasu shiki kaigan wa kawa ga kezutta tani ni kaisui ga haitte dekita.","german":"Fjords were formed when seawater entered valleys carved by glaciers, whereas ria coasts formed when it entered valleys cut by rivers.","furigana":"ひょうが|けず|たに|しきかいがん|かわ|けず|たに|かいすい|はい"}],"notes":"フィヨルド is a fjord, a valley carved out by glaciers and later flooded by the sea, with steep walls. Known from Norway, New Zealand and Chile; there are none in Japan. In class it is often contrasted with the リアス式海岸, which arises from river valleys. A loanword from Norwegian, neutral.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"阿蘇山のカルデラは世界最大級の大きさを誇る。","romaji":"Asosan no karudera wa sekai saidaikyū no ōkisa o hokoru.","german":"The caldera of Mt. Aso boasts a size among the largest in the world.","furigana":"|せかいさいだいきゅう|おお|ほこ"},{"japanese":"洞爺湖は、火山の噴火でできたカルデラ湖だ。","romaji":"Tōyako wa, kazan no funka de dekita karudera ko da.","german":"Lake Toya is a caldera lake formed by a volcanic eruption.","furigana":"|かざん|ふんか|こ"},{"japanese":"カルデラの中に町があり、多くの人が暮らしている。","romaji":"Karudera no naka ni machi ga ari, ōku no hito ga kurashite iru.","german":"There is a town inside the caldera, and many people live there.","furigana":"なか|まち|おお|ひと|く"}],"notes":"カルデラ is a technical word from Spanish or Portuguese (\"cauldron\") and refers to the large, usually round depression that forms when a volcano collapses in on itself after a gigantic eruption. It is much larger than an ordinary crater 火口; the surrounding ring wall is called 外輪山. If the depression fills with water, a カルデラ湖 forms, such as 洞爺湖 or 十和田湖. The word is written only in katakana.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"火口湖の水は火山性ガスで酸性を帯びている。","romaji":"Kakōko no mizu wa kazansei gasu de sansei o obite iru.","german":"The water of the crater lake is acidic from volcanic gases.","furigana":"かこうこ|みず|かざんせい|さんせい|お"},{"japanese":"登山者たちは山頂から火口をのぞき込んだ。","romaji":"Tozansha-tachi wa sanchō kara kakō o nozokikonda.","german":"The climbers peered into the crater from the summit.","furigana":"とざんしゃ|さんちょう|かこう|こ"},{"japanese":"噴火警戒レベルが上がり、火口周辺への立ち入りが規制された。","romaji":"Funka keikai reberu ga agari, kakō shūhen e no tachiiri ga kisei sareta.","german":"After the volcanic alert level was raised, entry to the area around the crater was restricted.","furigana":"ふんかけいかい|あ|かこうしゅうへん|た|い|きせい"}],"notes":"火口 is the opening of a volcano from which lava, gas and ash emerge, that is, the \"crater\"; synonymous but more technical is 噴火口. Typical combinations are 火口湖 \"crater lake\", 火口付近 and 火口周辺への立ち入り規制 in warning notices. A much larger collapse depression is called カルデラ. Beware of homophones: 河口 \"river mouth\" and 加工 \"processing\" are also read かこう; in the reading ひぐち, by contrast, 火口 means the point where a fire starts.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"溶岩台地は、流れ出た溶岩が何層にも積み重なってできた地形だ。","romaji":"Yōgan daichi wa, nagaredeta yōgan ga nansō ni mo tsumikasanatte dekita chikei da.","german":"A lava plateau is a landform made of many layers of flowed-out lava piled on top of each other.","furigana":"ようがんだいち|なが|で|ようがん|なんそう|つ|かさ|ちけい"},{"japanese":"インドのデカン高原は、世界有数の溶岩台地として知られている。","romaji":"Indo no Dekan kōgen wa, sekai yūsū no yōgan daichi to shite shirarete iru.","german":"The Deccan Plateau in India is known as one of the world's leading lava plateaus.","furigana":"こうげん|せかいゆうすう|ようがんだいち|し"},{"japanese":"アイスランドでは、草もまばらな溶岩台地がどこまでも続いていた。","romaji":"Aisurando de wa, kusa mo mabara na yōgan daichi ga doko made mo tsuzuite ita.","german":"In Iceland, a sparsely grassed lava plateau stretched on endlessly.","furigana":"くさ|ようがんだいち|つづ"}],"notes":"溶岩台地 is composed of 溶岩 \"lava\" and 台地 \"plateau, tableland\": a wide, flat upland that forms when fluid basalt lava flows out in large quantities and layers up one on top of another. It is a technical term of geography and geology. It is to be distinguished from 火山灰台地 such as the シラス台地 in Kyushu, which consists of ash instead of lava; 高原 is generally a \"highland\" with no statement about its origin.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"豪雨の後に大規模な地滑りが発生した。","romaji":"Gōu no ato ni daikibo na jisuberi ga hassei shita.","german":"Nach dem Starkregen ereignete sich ein großer Erdrutsch.","furigana":"ごうう|あと|だいきぼ|じすべ|はっせい"},{"japanese":"この斜面は地滑りの危険があるため、立ち入り禁止になっている。","romaji":"Kono shamen wa jisuberi no kiken ga aru tame, tachiiri kinshi ni natte iru.","german":"Dieser Hang ist wegen Erdrutschgefahr gesperrt.","furigana":"しゃめん|じすべ|きけん|た|い|きんし"},{"japanese":"野党は選挙で地滑り的な勝利を収めた。","romaji":"Yatō wa senkyo de jisuberiteki na shōri o osameta.","german":"Die Opposition errang bei der Wahl einen Erdrutschsieg.","furigana":"やとう|せんきょ|じすべ|てき|しょうり|おさ"}],"notes":"地滑り (in Behörden- und Fachtexten meist 地すべり geschrieben) ist ein „Erdrutsch“, bei dem eine Bodenmasse entlang einer Gleitfläche hangabwärts rutscht – oft langsam und über größere Flächen. Ein plötzlicher Abbruch eines steilen Hangs heißt eher 山崩れ oder がけ崩れ; in Nachrichten ist 土砂崩れ der allgemeine Oberbegriff, 土石流 ist eine Mure. Typisch: 地滑りが起きる／発生する, 地滑りの危険. Übertragen gibt es wie im Deutschen den „Erdrutschsieg“: 地滑り的勝利.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"土石流が集落を直撃し甚大な被害が出た。","romaji":"Dosekiryū ga shūraku o chokugeki shi jindai na higai ga deta.","german":"A debris flow hit the settlement directly and caused enormous damage.","furigana":"どせきりゅう|しゅうらく|ちょくげき|じんだい|ひがい|で"},{"japanese":"大雨の際は、谷沿いに住む人は土石流に警戒してください。","romaji":"Ōame no sai wa, tanizoi ni sumu hito wa dosekiryū ni keikai shite kudasai.","german":"In heavy rain, people living along valleys should be on guard against debris flows.","furigana":"おおあめ|さい|たにぞ|す|ひと|どせきりゅう|けいかい"},{"japanese":"噴火で積もった火山灰が雨で流され、土石流となって村を襲った。","romaji":"Funka de tsumotta kazanbai ga ame de nagasare, dosekiryū to natte mura o osotta.","german":"Volcanic ash deposited by the eruption was washed away by rain and struck the village as a debris flow.","furigana":"ふんか|つ|かざんばい|あめ|なが|どせきりゅう|むら|おそ"}],"notes":"土石流 is a \"debris flow\": a water-saturated stream of earth, rubble and boulders that rushes through valleys at high speed during heavy rain or after volcanic eruptions. An older, popular term is 山津波. Unlike 地滑り, where a slope slides as a whole, in a 土石流 the material flows like a flood. Typical: 土石流が発生する, 土石流に巻き込まれる／のみ込まれる, 土石流に警戒する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"河川の侵食作用がV字谷を形成する。","romaji":"Kasen no shinshoku sayō ga buijikoku o keisei suru.","german":"The erosive action of rivers forms V-shaped valleys.","furigana":"かせん|しんしょくさよう|じこく|けいせい"},{"japanese":"川の流れには、侵食作用・運搬作用・堆積作用の三つの働きがある。","romaji":"Kawa no nagare ni wa, shinshoku sayō, unpan sayō, taiseki sayō no mittsu no hataraki ga aru.","german":"The flow of a river has three actions: erosion, transportation and deposition.","furigana":"かわ|なが|しんしょくさよう|うんぱんさよう|たいせきさよう|みっ|はたら"},{"japanese":"海岸の崖は、波の侵食作用によって少しずつ後退している。","romaji":"Kaigan no gake wa, nami no shinshoku sayō ni yotte sukoshi zutsu kōtai shite iru.","german":"The coastal cliff is gradually receding due to the erosive action of waves.","furigana":"かいがん|がけ|なみ|しんしょくさよう|すこ|こうたい"}],"notes":"侵食作用 is the textbook term for the \"eroding action\" of flowing water, waves, wind or ice; 作用 means \"action, process\" here. In geography class it almost always appears in the triad 侵食作用・運搬作用・堆積作用 \"erosion, transportation, deposition\". Besides 侵食, the spelling 浸食 is also common. 侵食 alone can also mean \"encroachment, intrusion\" figuratively (市場を侵食する), whereas 侵食作用 refers only to the natural process.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"堆積作用により河口に広大な平野が形成される。","romaji":"Taiseki sayō ni yori kakō ni kōdai na heiya ga keisei sareru.","german":"Deposition forms a vast plain at the river mouth.","furigana":"たいせきさよう|かこう|こうだい|へいや|けいせい"},{"japanese":"扇状地は、山から出た川の堆積作用によってできる地形だ。","romaji":"Senjōchi wa, yama kara deta kawa no taiseki sayō ni yotte dekiru chikei da.","german":"An alluvial fan is a landform created by the depositional action of a river emerging from the mountains.","furigana":"せんじょうち|やま|で|かわ|たいせきさよう|ちけい"},{"japanese":"ダムの上流では堆積作用が進み、貯水量が減ってしまう。","romaji":"Damu no jōryū de wa taiseki sayō ga susumi, chosuiryō ga hette shimau.","german":"Upstream of the dam, deposition advances and the storage capacity decreases.","furigana":"じょうりゅう|たいせきさよう|すす|ちょすいりょう|へ"}],"notes":"堆積作用 is the \"deposition\" of sand, gravel and mud when water, wind or ice lose strength; it is the counterpart of 侵食作用 and follows transportation, 運搬作用. This process creates forms such as 扇状地 \"alluvial fan\" and 三角州 \"delta\". As a verb one says, intransitively, 土砂が堆積する; the deposited material is called 堆積物, and the rock formed from it 堆積岩. A technical expression from geography and geology.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"砂漠地帯では風食による地形変化が著しい。","romaji":"Sabaku chitai de wa fūshoku ni yoru chikei henka ga ichijirushii.","german":"In desert regions, landform change due to wind erosion is remarkable.","furigana":"さばくちたい|ふうしょく|ちけいへんか|いちじる"},{"japanese":"風食を受けた岩が、キノコのような形になっている。","romaji":"Fūshoku o uketa iwa ga, kinoko no yō na katachi ni natte iru.","german":"The rock shaped by wind erosion has taken on a mushroom-like form.","furigana":"ふうしょく|う|いわ|かたち"},{"japanese":"畑の表土が風食で失われないよう、防風林が植えられた。","romaji":"Hatake no hyōdo ga fūshoku de ushinawarenai yō, bōfūrin ga uerareta.","german":"Windbreak forests were planted so that the topsoil of fields would not be lost to wind erosion.","furigana":"はたけ|ひょうど|ふうしょく|うしな|ぼうふうりん|う"}],"notes":"風食 (older spelling 風蝕) is \"wind erosion\": wind carries away loose soil and grinds rock with the sand it carries, creating, for example, mushroom-shaped rocks. It forms a series with 水食 and 氷食. Not to be confused with 風化 \"weathering\", the disintegration of rock in place (figuratively also the \"fading\" of memories). According to dictionaries it is also possible as a する-verb, but in practice it is almost only a noun: 風食による, 風食を受ける.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"氷食によってU字谷が形成された。","romaji":"Hyōshoku ni yotte yūjikoku ga keisei sareta.","german":"A U-shaped valley was formed by glacial erosion.","furigana":"ひょうしょく|じこく|けいせい"},{"japanese":"フィヨルドは、氷食でできた谷に海水が入り込んだ地形だ。","romaji":"Fiyorudo wa, hyōshoku de dekita tani ni kaisui ga hairikonda chikei da.","german":"A fjord is a landform where seawater has entered a valley formed by glacial erosion.","furigana":"ひょうしょく|たに|かいすい|はい|こ|ちけい"},{"japanese":"日本アルプスにも、かつての氷食の跡が残っている。","romaji":"Nihon Arupusu ni mo, katsute no hyōshoku no ato ga nokotte iru.","german":"Traces of past glacial erosion remain in the Japanese Alps as well.","furigana":"にほん|ひょうしょく|あと|のこ"}],"notes":"氷食 (also 氷蝕) is \"glacial erosion\", that is, the grinding and scooping out of the ground by a flowing glacier 氷河. Typical forms created by 氷食 are U字谷 \"U-shaped valley\", 圏谷 or カール \"cirque\", fjords フィヨルド and pointed peaks ホルン \"horns\". It belongs to the series 風食・水食・氷食 and is a purely technical term; it mostly appears with によって／による or as 氷食地形 \"glacially shaped landscape\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"モレーンは氷河が運搬した堆積物である。","romaji":"Morēn wa hyōga ga unpan shita taisekibutsu de aru.","german":"Moraines are deposits transported by glaciers.","furigana":"ひょうが|うんぱん|たいせきぶつ"},{"japanese":"氷河が後退した後には、大きな岩を含むモレーンが残された。","romaji":"Hyōga ga kōtai shita ato ni wa, ōkina iwa o fukumu morēn ga nokosareta.","german":"After the glacier retreated, a moraine containing large boulders was left behind.","furigana":"ひょうが|こうたい|あと|おお|いわ|ふく|のこ"},{"japanese":"この湖は、モレーンが川をせき止めてできたものだ。","romaji":"Kono mizuumi wa, morēn ga kawa o sekitomete dekita mono da.","german":"This lake was formed when a moraine dammed a river.","furigana":"みずうみ|かわ|と"}],"notes":"モレーン is the loanword for \"moraine\", the debris of rock fragments, gravel and sand that a glacier carries along and deposits. The Japanese technical word for it is 堆石 or 氷堆石 – not to be confused with the identically sounding 堆積 \"deposition\". A distinction is made, for example, between lateral moraines (側堆石) and terminal moraines (末端モレーン). Moraines can block valleys, so that a glacial lake 氷河湖 forms behind them.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"圏谷は氷河の侵食によって山腹にできたくぼみである。","romaji":"Kenkoku wa hyōga no shinshoku ni yotte sanpuku ni dekita kubomi de aru.","german":"Ein Kar ist eine Vertiefung am Berghang, die durch Gletschererosion entstanden ist.","furigana":"けんこく|ひょうが|しんしょく|さんぷく"},{"japanese":"圏谷は、ドイツ語に由来する「カール」とも呼ばれる。","romaji":"Kenkoku wa, Doitsugo ni yurai suru \"kāru\" to mo yobareru.","german":"Ein 圏谷 wird auch „Kāru“ genannt, nach dem deutschen Wort „Kar“.","furigana":"けんこく|ご|ゆらい|よ"},{"japanese":"木曽駒ヶ岳の千畳敷カールは、日本を代表する圏谷の一つだ。","romaji":"Kiso Komagatake no Senjōjiki kāru wa, Nihon o daihyō suru kenkoku no hitotsu da.","german":"Das Senjōjiki-Kar am Kiso-Komagatake ist eines der bekanntesten Kare Japans.","furigana":"||にほん|だいひょう|けんこく|ひと"}],"notes":"圏谷 ist das sinojapanische Fachwort für das „Kar“, eine sesselförmige Mulde am oberen Berghang, die ein Gletscher ausgeschürft hat. Im Alltag, bei Wanderern und auf Schildern ist das aus dem Deutschen entlehnte カール viel gebräuchlicher, z. B. 千畳敷カール; 圏谷 begegnet vor allem in Lehrbüchern. Liegt in der Mulde ein kleiner See, spricht man von 圏谷湖 bzw. カール湖. In Japan finden sich Kare vor allem in den Japanischen Alpen und in Hokkaidō.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"氷河湖の水は、細かい岩の粉のために青白く濁って見えることが多い。","romaji":"Hyōgako no mizu wa, komakai iwa no kona no tame ni aojiroku nigotte mieru koto ga ōi.","german":"The water of glacial lakes often looks bluish-white and cloudy because of fine rock powder.","furigana":"ひょうがこ|みず|こま|いわ|こな|あおじろ|にご|み|おお"},{"japanese":"温暖化で氷河湖が決壊し、下流の村が洪水に襲われる危険がある。","romaji":"Ondanka de hyōgako ga kekkai shi, karyū no mura ga kōzui ni osowareru kiken ga aru.","german":"Due to warming, there is a danger that a glacial lake will burst and the villages downstream will be hit by flooding.","furigana":"おんだんか|ひょうがこ|けっかい|かりゅう|むら|こうずい|おそ|きけん"},{"japanese":"北アメリカの五大湖も、氷河の作用でできた氷河湖である。","romaji":"Kita Amerika no Godaiko mo, hyōga no sayō de dekita hyōgako de aru.","german":"North America's Great Lakes are also glacial lakes formed by the action of glaciers.","furigana":"きた|ごだいこ|ひょうが|さよう|ひょうがこ"}],"notes":"氷河湖 is a \"glacial lake\": a lake created by the work of a glacier – for example in a scooped-out basin, in a cirque or behind a blocking moraine. The suffix 湖 is read こ here, as in カルデラ湖 or 火口湖. Because of fine rock powder, the water is often milky turquoise. In the news the word often appears in connection with climate change: 氷河湖の決壊 \"outburst of a glacial lake\" threatens villages in the Himalayas.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地滑りによって堰止湖が形成された。","romaji":"Jisuberi ni yotte sekitomeko ga keisei sareta.","german":"A landslide formed a barrier lake.","furigana":"じすべ|せきとめこ|けいせい"},{"japanese":"中禅寺湖は、男体山の溶岩が川をせき止めてできた堰止湖だ。","romaji":"Chūzenjiko wa, Nantaisan no yōgan ga kawa o sekitomete dekita sekitomeko da.","german":"Lake Chuzenji is a barrier lake formed when lava from Mt. Nantai dammed a river.","furigana":"ちゅうぜんじこ|なんたいさん|ようがん|かわ|と|せきとめこ"},{"japanese":"地震でできた堰止湖が決壊するおそれがあり、住民に避難指示が出た。","romaji":"Jishin de dekita sekitomeko ga kekkai suru osore ga ari, jūmin ni hinan shiji ga deta.","german":"Because the barrier lake created by the earthquake risked bursting, evacuation orders were issued to residents.","furigana":"じしん|せきとめこ|けっかい|じゅうみん|ひなんしじ|で"}],"notes":"堰止湖 (also written せき止め湖 or 堰き止め湖) comes from 堰き止める \"to dam up\" and refers to a naturally dammed lake: a landslide, lava flow or moraine blocks a river valley, and water collects behind it. Well-known examples are 中禅寺湖 and the Fuji Five Lakes. When such a lake forms freshly after an earthquake or heavy rain, the news often speaks of 天然ダム or 土砂ダム and warns of a dam break 決壊.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"海嶺ではマグマが噴出し新しい地殻が形成される。","romaji":"Kairei de wa maguma ga funshutsu shi atarashii chikaku ga keisei sareru.","german":"At a mid-ocean ridge, magma erupts and new crust is formed.","furigana":"かいれい|ふんしゅつ|あたら|ちかく|けいせい"},{"japanese":"大西洋の中央には、南北に長い海嶺が走っている。","romaji":"Taiseiyō no chūō ni wa, nanboku ni nagai kairei ga hashitte iru.","german":"A long ridge runs north to south through the middle of the Atlantic.","furigana":"たいせいよう|ちゅうおう|なんぼく|なが|かいれい|はし"},{"japanese":"アイスランドは、海嶺が海面上に現れた珍しい場所だ。","romaji":"Aisurando wa, kairei ga kaimenjō ni arawareta mezurashii basho da.","german":"Iceland is a rare place where an oceanic ridge appears above sea level.","furigana":"かいれい|かいめんじょう|あらわ|めずら|ばしょ"}],"notes":"海嶺 is generally an elongated \"oceanic ridge\", an undersea mountain range. In plate tectonics it mostly means the 中央海嶺 \"mid-ocean ridge\", where plates drift apart, magma rises and new oceanic crust forms. Its counterpart is the 海溝 \"ocean trench\", where a plate dives down again; individual undersea mountains are called 海山. The word belongs to the technical language of geology and oceanography.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"フォッサマグナは日本列島を横断する大地溝帯である。","romaji":"Fossa Maguna wa Nihon rettō o ōdan suru dai chikōtai de aru.","german":"The Fossa Magna is a large rift zone that crosses the Japanese archipelago.","furigana":"にほんれっとう|おうだん|だいちこうたい"},{"japanese":"東アフリカの大地溝帯は、人類誕生の地としても知られる。","romaji":"Higashi Afurika no dai chikōtai wa, jinrui tanjō no chi to shite mo shirareru.","german":"The East African Rift is also known as the cradle of humankind.","furigana":"ひがし|だいちこうたい|じんるいたんじょう|ち|し"},{"japanese":"断層に挟まれた土地が落ち込むと、地溝ができる。","romaji":"Dansō ni hasamareta tochi ga ochikomu to, chikō ga dekiru.","german":"When a strip of land between faults sinks, a graben is formed.","furigana":"だんそう|はさ|とち|お|こ|ちこう"}],"notes":"地溝 is a \"graben\" or rift: an elongated strip of land that has sunk between two parallel faults 断層. The opposite, a block raised between faults, is called 地塁 \"horst\". The word is most often encountered in 地溝帯 \"rift zone\", for example 東アフリカ大地溝帯 \"East African Rift\"; lakes formed in a rift are called 地溝湖. The フォッサマグナ in central Japan is often described as a large rift.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"パナマ地峡に運河が建設された。","romaji":"Panama chikyō ni unga ga kensetsu sareta.","german":"A canal was built across the Isthmus of Panama.","furigana":"ちきょう|うんが|けんせつ"},{"japanese":"海峡が二つの海を結ぶ水路なのに対し、地峡は二つの陸地を結ぶ細い陸地だ。","romaji":"Kaikyō ga futatsu no umi o musubu suiro na no ni taishi, chikyō wa futatsu no rikuchi o musubu hosoi rikuchi da.","german":"Whereas a strait is a waterway connecting two seas, an isthmus is a narrow strip of land connecting two landmasses.","furigana":"かいきょう|ふた|うみ|むす|すいろ|たい|ちきょう|ふた|りくち|むす|ほそ|りくち"},{"japanese":"古代ギリシャでは、コリントス地峡で船を陸上で運んでいた。","romaji":"Kodai Girisha de wa, Korintosu chikyō de fune o rikujō de hakonde ita.","german":"In ancient Greece, ships were transported overland across the Isthmus of Corinth.","furigana":"こだい|ちきょう|ふね|りくじょう|はこ"}],"notes":"地峡 is an \"isthmus\", a narrow strip of land that connects two larger landmasses and is bordered by sea on both sides. It is easily confused with 海峡 \"strait\" – exactly the reverse, a narrow waterway between two landmasses. 地峡 mostly stands after a proper name: パナマ地峡, スエズ地峡, コリントス地峡. Because a canal can be dug through an isthmus, it often appears together with 運河.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"奄美群島は鹿児島県に属し、独自の文化を持っている。","romaji":"Amami guntō wa Kagoshima-ken ni zokushi, dokuji no bunka o motte iru.","german":"The Amami Islands belong to Kagoshima Prefecture and have a distinctive culture.","furigana":"|かごしまけん|ぞく|どくじ|ぶんか|も"},{"japanese":"インドネシアは世界最大の群島国家だ。","romaji":"Indoneshia wa sekai saidai no guntō kokka da.","german":"Indonesia is the world's largest archipelagic state.","furigana":"せかいさいだい|ぐんとうこっか"},{"japanese":"船の上から、大小さまざまな島からなる群島が見えた。","romaji":"Fune no ue kara, daishō samazama na shima kara naru guntō ga mieta.","german":"From the ship, we saw an archipelago made up of islands of all sizes.","furigana":"ふね|うえ|だいしょう|しま|ぐんとう|み"}],"notes":"群島 is an \"island group\", a cluster of islands lying close together. In proper names it competes with 諸島, the most common word for a group of islands (小笠原諸島), and 列島, which refers more to a long chain of islands (日本列島, 千島列島). A fixed Japanese name with 群島 is 奄美群島. In international law, an \"archipelagic state\" is called 群島国家, e.g. Indonesia or the Philippines.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"環太平洋火山帯は地震と火山活動が活発な地域である。","romaji":"Kan Taiheiyō kazantai wa jishin to kazan katsudō ga kappatsu na chiiki de aru.","german":"The Pacific Ring of Fire is an area of active earthquake and volcanic activity.","furigana":"かんたいへいようかざんたい|じしん|かざんかつどう|かっぱつ|ちいき"},{"japanese":"環太平洋パートナーシップ協定は、TPPと略される。","romaji":"Kan Taiheiyō pātonāshippu kyōtei wa, TPP to ryakusareru.","german":"The Trans-Pacific Partnership Agreement is abbreviated as TPP.","furigana":"かんたいへいよう|きょうてい|りゃく"},{"japanese":"環太平洋の国々が集まり、貿易について話し合った。","romaji":"Kan Taiheiyō no kuniguni ga atsumari, bōeki ni tsuite hanashiatta.","german":"Countries around the Pacific Rim gathered and discussed trade.","furigana":"かんたいへいよう|くにぐに|あつ|ぼうえき|はな|あ"}],"notes":"環太平洋 means \"around the Pacific\" (環 \"ring, to surround\") and corresponds to the English \"Pacific Rim\". It almost never stands alone, but as a modifier: 環太平洋地域 \"Pacific Rim region\", 環太平洋の国々. The \"Pacific Ring of Fire\" is only the compound 環太平洋火山帯 (or 環太平洋造山帯 for the mountain-building belt). Known from economic policy is 環太平洋パートナーシップ協定, the TPP free trade agreement.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"沈み込み帯では巨大地震が発生しやすい。","romaji":"Shizumikomi tai de wa kyodai jishin ga hassei shiyasui.","german":"Large earthquakes tend to occur in subduction zones.","furigana":"しず|こ|たい|きょだいじしん|はっせい"},{"japanese":"日本の太平洋側の沖には、プレートの沈み込み帯が延びている。","romaji":"Nihon no Taiheiyō-gawa no oki ni wa, purēto no shizumikomi tai ga nobite iru.","german":"A subduction zone of plates extends off Japan's Pacific side.","furigana":"にほん|たいへいようがわ|おき|しず|こ|たい|の"},{"japanese":"沈み込み帯に沿って、火山が列をなして並んでいる。","romaji":"Shizumikomi tai ni sotte, kazan ga retsu o nashite narande iru.","german":"Volcanoes line up in a row along the subduction zone.","furigana":"しず|こ|たい|そ|かざん|れつ|なら"}],"notes":"沈み込み帯 is the \"subduction zone\": the area where an oceanic plate dives beneath another plate. The word is formed from 沈み込む \"to sink down, dive\" and 帯 \"zone, belt\"; less often one also says サブダクション帯. At the surface, an ocean trench 海溝 marks the zone, and volcanoes line up above it. Japan lies above several such zones, which is why large earthquakes of the 海溝型地震 type occur there.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"背斜構造は石油の貯留に適している。","romaji":"Haisha kōzō wa sekiyu no choryū ni tekishite iru.","german":"Anticlinal structures are suitable for trapping oil.","furigana":"はいしゃこうぞう|せきゆ|ちょりゅう|てき"},{"japanese":"地層が上に向かって山のように曲がった部分を背斜という。","romaji":"Chisō ga ue ni mukatte yama no yō ni magatta bubun o haisha to iu.","german":"The part where rock layers are bent upward like a mountain is called an anticline.","furigana":"ちそう|うえ|む|やま|ま|ぶぶん|はいしゃ"},{"japanese":"崖の断面には、背斜と向斜が交互に現れていた。","romaji":"Gake no danmen ni wa, haisha to kōsha ga kōgo ni arawarete ita.","german":"In the cross-section of the cliff, anticlines and synclines appeared alternately.","furigana":"がけ|だんめん|はいしゃ|こうしゃ|こうご|あらわ"}],"notes":"背斜 is a geological \"anticline\": a fold 褶曲 in which the rock layers are arched upward like a hump. Its counterpart, the downward-bent trough, is called 向斜 \"syncline\". It often appears in 背斜構造 \"anticlinal structure\" or 背斜軸 \"anticlinal axis\"; oil and natural gas often collect at the crest of such anticlines. Beware of homophones: 歯医者 \"dentist\" and 敗者 \"loser\" are also read はいしゃ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"向斜の底部には地下水が溜まりやすい。","romaji":"Kōsha no teibu ni wa chikasui ga tamariyasui.","german":"Groundwater tends to collect at the bottom of a syncline.","furigana":"こうしゃ|ていぶ|ちかすい|た"},{"japanese":"地層が下に向かって谷のように曲がった部分を向斜という。","romaji":"Chisō ga shita ni mukatte tani no yō ni magatta bubun o kōsha to iu.","german":"The part of a stratum that bends downward like a valley is called a syncline.","furigana":"ちそう|した|む|たに|ま|ぶぶん|こうしゃ"},{"japanese":"盆地の地下には、大きな向斜構造が隠れていることがある。","romaji":"Bonchi no chika ni wa, ōkina kōsha kōzō ga kakurete iru koto ga aru.","german":"A large synclinal structure is sometimes hidden beneath a basin.","furigana":"ぼんち|ちか|おお|こうしゃこうぞう|かく"}],"notes":"向斜 is a geological \"trough\" (syncline): a fold in which the rock layers sag downward like a bowl. Its counterpart is the anticline 背斜; together they form a fold structure 褶曲. Common combinations are 向斜構造 and 向斜軸. Because water flows together in troughs, they often serve as groundwater reservoirs. Homophones such as 後者 \"the latter\" and 校舎 \"school building\" are also read こうしゃ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"高層ビルの基礎は、固い岩盤の上に築かれる。","romaji":"Kōsō biru no kiso wa, katai ganban no ue ni kizukareru.","german":"The foundations of a skyscraper are built on hard bedrock.","furigana":"こうそう|きそ|かた|がんばん|うえ|きず"},{"japanese":"首相は「岩盤規制」に穴をあけると強調した。","romaji":"Shushō wa \"ganban kisei\" ni ana o akeru to kyōchō shita.","german":"The prime minister stressed that he would break through the \"bedrock regulations\".","furigana":"しゅしょう|がんばんきせい|あな|きょうちょう"},{"japanese":"週末に岩盤浴に行って、たっぷり汗をかいた。","romaji":"Shūmatsu ni ganban'yoku ni itte, tappuri ase o kaita.","german":"I went to a bedrock bath on the weekend and had a really good sweat.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|がんばんよく|い|あせ"}],"notes":"岩盤 is the solid \"bedrock\" beneath soil and rubble, on which, for example, skyscrapers, bridges or nuclear power plants are founded: 岩盤の上に建てる, 固い岩盤. Figuratively, 岩盤 is common in politics: 岩盤規制 refers to \"entrenched regulations that are hardly ever broken open\", and 岩盤支持層 to an \"unshakable core base of voters\". In everyday life it is also known from 岩盤浴, a wellness bath on heated stone slabs.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"関東ローム層は火山灰を起源とする風成層である。","romaji":"Kantō rōmu sō wa kazanbai o kigen to suru fūseisō de aru.","german":"The Kanto loam layer is an aeolian deposit of volcanic origin.","furigana":"かんとう|そう|かざんばい|きげん|ふうせいそう"},{"japanese":"中国の黄土高原は、風で運ばれた砂やちりが積もった厚い風成層でできている。","romaji":"Chūgoku no Kōdo kōgen wa, kaze de hakobareta suna ya chiri ga tsumotta atsui fūseisō de dekite iru.","german":"China's Loess Plateau consists of thick aeolian deposits of sand and dust carried by the wind.","furigana":"ちゅうごく|こうどこうげん|かぜ|はこ|すな|つ|あつ|ふうせいそう"},{"japanese":"風成層は、水中でできた地層に比べて粒の大きさがそろっていることが多い。","romaji":"Fūseisō wa, suichū de dekita chisō ni kurabete tsubu no ōkisa ga sorotte iru koto ga ōi.","german":"Aeolian deposits often have more uniform grain sizes than strata formed in water.","furigana":"ふうせいそう|すいちゅう|ちそう|くら|つぶ|おお|おお"}],"notes":"風成層 is an \"aeolian deposit\", that is, a layer of rock or soil formed from material carried by the wind: 風成 \"formed by wind\" + 層 \"layer\". It includes loess レス, dune sand and wind-blown volcanic ash such as the 関東ローム層. Its counterpart is the water-laid strata 水成層. A rare technical term from geology and soil science.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"河口付近では淡水と海水が混じり合う。","romaji":"Kakō fukin de wa tansui to kaisui ga majiriau.","german":"Fresh water and seawater mix near the river mouth.","furigana":"かこうふきん|たんすい|かいすい|ま|あ"},{"japanese":"河口に近づくにつれて、川幅はどんどん広くなる。","romaji":"Kakō ni chikazuku ni tsurete, kawahaba wa dondon hiroku naru.","german":"The closer you get to the river mouth, the wider the river becomes.","furigana":"かこう|ちか|かわはば|ひろ"},{"japanese":"毎年冬になると、河口の干潟に多くの渡り鳥が集まる。","romaji":"Maitoshi fuyu ni naru to, kakō no higata ni ōku no wataridori ga atsumaru.","german":"Every winter, many migratory birds gather on the tidal flats at the river mouth.","furigana":"まいとしふゆ|かこう|ひがた|おお|わた|どり|あつ"}],"notes":"河口 is the \"river mouth\", the place where a river flows into the sea or a lake. Common combinations are 河口付近, 河口域 and 河口堰 \"estuary barrage\". Careful: 火口 \"crater\" sounds exactly the same, and Lake Kawaguchi near Mount Fuji, 河口湖, is read not かこうこ but かわぐちこ. In everyday speech people often say more simply 川の河口 or 川が海に注ぐところ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"汽水域は独特の生態系を有している。","romaji":"Kisuiiki wa dokutoku no seitaikei o yūshite iru.","german":"The brackish zone has a unique ecosystem.","furigana":"きすいいき|どくとく|せいたいけい|ゆう"},{"japanese":"シジミは、川の水と海の水が混じり合う汽水域でよくとれる。","romaji":"Shijimi wa, kawa no mizu to umi no mizu ga majiriau kisuiiki de yoku toreru.","german":"Basket clams are mostly found in brackish water, where river water and seawater mix.","furigana":"かわ|みず|うみ|みず|ま|あ|きすいいき"},{"japanese":"ウナギは汽水域を通って川をさかのぼる。","romaji":"Unagi wa kisuiiki o tōtte kawa o sakanoboru.","german":"Eels travel upriver through the brackish zone.","furigana":"きすいいき|とお|かわ"}],"notes":"汽水域 is the \"brackish zone\" where fresh water 淡水 and seawater 海水 mix, typically in river mouths and lagoons. 汽水 means \"brackish water\" and 域 \"area, zone\"; a brackish lake is a 汽水湖 such as 宍道湖 or 浜名湖. The word appears especially in ecology and fisheries, because typical species such as shijimi clams, eels and mullet live there. Compared with 河口, 汽水域 emphasizes the salinity rather than the place.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"瀬戸内海は多くの島々が点在する穏やかな海域である。","romaji":"Setonaikai wa ōku no shimajima ga tenzai suru odayaka na kaiiki de aru.","german":"The Seto Inland Sea is a calm body of water dotted with many islands.","furigana":"せとないかい|おお|しまじま|てんざい|おだ|かいいき"},{"japanese":"音戸の瀬戸は、平清盛が切り開いたと伝えられる狭い海峡だ。","romaji":"Ondo no seto wa, Taira no Kiyomori ga kirihiraita to tsutaerareru semai kaikyō da.","german":"The Ondo Strait is a narrow strait said to have been cut open by Taira no Kiyomori.","furigana":"おんど|せと||き|ひら|つた|せま|かいきょう"},{"japanese":"島と島の間の狭い瀬戸を、潮が速く流れている。","romaji":"Shima to shima no aida no semai seto o, shio ga hayaku nagarete iru.","german":"The tide flows fast through the narrow strait between the islands.","furigana":"しま|しま|あいだ|せま|せと|しお|はや|なが"}],"notes":"瀬戸 denotes a narrow \"strait\", often with strong currents, between two coasts or islands. As a general word people now usually say 海峡; 瀬戸 survives mainly in names, such as 瀬戸内海 \"Seto Inland Sea\" or 音戸の瀬戸. Derived words are 瀬戸際 \"the critical moment, the brink\" (瀬戸際に立たされる) and 瀬戸物 \"porcelain, earthenware\", named after the pottery town 瀬戸 in Aichi.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"海食崖は波の侵食によって形成される。","romaji":"Kaishokugai wa nami no shinshoku ni yotte keisei sareru.","german":"Sea cliffs are formed by the erosion of waves.","furigana":"かいしょくがい|なみ|しんしょく|けいせい"},{"japanese":"半島の先端には、高さ百メートルを超える海食崖が続いている。","romaji":"Hantō no sentan ni wa, takasa hyaku mētoru o koeru kaishokugai ga tsuzuite iru.","german":"At the tip of the peninsula, sea cliffs more than a hundred meters high continue on.","furigana":"はんとう|せんたん|たか|ひゃく|こ|かいしょくがい|つづ"},{"japanese":"遊覧船から海食崖を眺めるツアーが人気だ。","romaji":"Yūransen kara kaishokugai o nagameru tsuā ga ninki da.","german":"Tours viewing the sea cliffs from a sightseeing boat are popular.","furigana":"ゆうらんせん|かいしょくがい|なが|にんき"}],"notes":"海食崖 is a steep coast washed out by the surf, that is, a cliff by the sea; literally \"sea\" + \"eat away\" + \"cliff\". The older spelling 海蝕崖 is also found, since 食 here stands for 蝕 \"to eat away\" (cf. 侵食/侵蝕). In everyday speech people simply say 崖 or 断崖; 海食崖 belongs to geology, geography class and travel guides, often together with 海食洞 \"sea cave\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"波の浸食により海食洞が形成された。","romaji":"Nami no shinshoku ni yori kaishokudō ga keisei sareta.","german":"A sea cave was formed by wave erosion.","furigana":"なみ|しんしょく|かいしょくどう|けいせい"},{"japanese":"イタリアの「青の洞窟」は、海食洞の代表的な例だ。","romaji":"Itaria no \"Ao no Dōkutsu\" wa, kaishokudō no daihyōteki na rei da.","german":"Italy's \"Blue Grotto\" is a typical example of a sea cave.","furigana":"あお|どうくつ|かいしょくどう|だいひょうてき|れい"},{"japanese":"干潮の時だけ、歩いて海食洞の奥まで入ることができる。","romaji":"Kanchō no toki dake, aruite kaishokudō no oku made hairu koto ga dekiru.","german":"You can walk into the depths of the sea cave only at low tide.","furigana":"かんちょう|とき|ある|かいしょくどう|おく|はい"}],"notes":"海食洞 (also 海蝕洞) is a \"sea cave\": a cave in a cliff coast that waves have carved out by erosion 海食. It belongs to a series of technical terms for coastal landforms such as 海食崖 \"sea cliff\" and 海食台 \"wave-cut platform\". In everyday speech people simply say 洞窟 or 洞くつ; a famous example is the \"Blue Grotto\" on Capri, and in Japan, for instance, the caves of 江の島岩屋.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"砂嘴は沿岸流によって砂が堆積して形成される。","romaji":"Sashi wa enganryū ni yotte suna ga taiseki shite keisei sareru.","german":"A sand spit is formed by sand deposited by coastal currents.","furigana":"さし|えんがんりゅう|すな|たいせき|けいせい"},{"japanese":"北海道の野付半島は、日本最大級の砂嘴として知られる。","romaji":"Hokkaidō no Notsuke hantō wa, Nihon saidaikyū no sashi to shite shirareru.","german":"The Notsuke Peninsula in Hokkaido is known as one of the largest sand spits in Japan.","furigana":"ほっかいどう||にほんさいだいきゅう|さし|し"},{"japanese":"砂嘴がさらに伸びて湾をふさぐと、砂州になる。","romaji":"Sashi ga sara ni nobite wan o fusagu to, sasu ni naru.","german":"When a sand spit extends further and closes off a bay, it becomes a sandbar.","furigana":"さし|の|わん|さす"}],"notes":"砂嘴 is a \"sand spit\": a narrow tongue of sand that a coastal current builds out into the sea from the shore; 嘴 means \"beak\", after its curved shape. If the spit keeps growing until it closes off a bay or reaches an island, it is called a 砂州 \"sandbar\", and a 潟湖 can form behind it. Well-known examples are the 野付半島 in Hokkaido and the peninsula of 三保の松原.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"砂州の内側に潟湖が形成されている。","romaji":"Sasu no uchigawa ni sekiko ga keisei sarete iru.","german":"A lagoon has formed on the inner side of the sandbar.","furigana":"さす|うちがわ|せきこ|けいせい"},{"japanese":"北海道のサロマ湖は、日本最大の潟湖だ。","romaji":"Hokkaidō no Saroma-ko wa, Nihon saidai no sekiko da.","german":"Lake Saroma in Hokkaido is Japan's largest lagoon.","furigana":"ほっかいどう|こ|にほんさいだい|せきこ"},{"japanese":"潟湖の多くは、川から流れ込む土砂で次第に浅くなっていく。","romaji":"Sekiko no ōku wa, kawa kara nagarekomu dosha de shidai ni asaku natte iku.","german":"Many lagoons gradually become shallower from the sediment that flows in from rivers.","furigana":"せきこ|おお|かわ|なが|こ|どしゃ|しだい|あさ"}],"notes":"潟湖 is a \"lagoon\": a shallow body of water separated from the open sea by a sandbar 砂州 or a sand spit 砂嘴, often with brackish water. 潟 (かた／がた) on its own means tidal flat or lagoon and appears in names like 新潟 or 八郎潟. In everyday speech and travel guides the loanword ラグーン is more common; 潟湖 is the technical term. Japan's largest lagoon is the サロマ湖 in Hokkaido.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"有明海の干拓地は広大な農地として利用されている。","romaji":"Ariakekai no kantakuchi wa kōdai na nōchi toshite riyō sarete iru.","german":"The reclaimed land of the Ariake Sea is used as vast farmland.","furigana":"|かんたくち|こうだい|のうち|りよう"},{"japanese":"八郎潟は干拓され、今では広い干拓地で米が作られている。","romaji":"Hachirōgata wa kantaku sare, ima de wa hiroi kantakuchi de kome ga tsukurarete iru.","german":"Hachirogata was drained, and rice is now grown on the broad reclaimed land.","furigana":"はちろうがた|かんたく|いま|ひろ|かんたくち|こめ|つく"},{"japanese":"オランダでは、国土のかなりの部分が干拓地だ。","romaji":"Oranda de wa, kokudo no kanari no bubun ga kantakuchi da.","german":"In the Netherlands, a considerable part of the country is polder.","furigana":"こくど|ぶぶん|かんたくち"}],"notes":"干拓地 is a \"polder\": land gained by diking a tidal flat, lagoon or shallow lake and pumping out the water (干拓する \"to drain, reclaim\"). It should be distinguished from 埋立地, new land created by filling in the sea with earth or refuse. Well-known Japanese examples are the former lake 八郎潟 in Akita and the polders on the 有明海; worldwide one thinks of the Netherlands. 干拓地 is mostly used as farmland, especially for rice.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"東京湾の埋立地に新しい都市が建設された。","romaji":"Tōkyō-wan no umetatechi ni atarashii toshi ga kensetsu sareta.","german":"A new city was built on the reclaimed land in Tokyo Bay.","furigana":"とうきょうわん|うめたてち|あたら|とし|けんせつ"},{"japanese":"燃えないごみは処分場で埋立処分される。","romaji":"Moenai gomi wa shobunjō de umetate shobun sareru.","german":"Non-burnable waste is disposed of as landfill at the disposal site.","furigana":"も|しょぶんじょう|うめたてしょぶん"},{"japanese":"干潟の埋立に反対する住民運動が起きた。","romaji":"Higata no umetate ni hantai suru jūmin undō ga okita.","german":"A residents' movement arose against the reclamation of the tidal flats.","furigana":"ひがた|うめたて|はんたい|じゅうみんうんどう|お"}],"notes":"埋立 \"land reclamation, landfill\" is the noun form of the transitive verb 埋め立てる (to fill in the sea, a swamp or pits with earth). Written out in full it is usually 埋め立て; the short form 埋立 without め is found mainly in compounds and official language: 埋立地 \"reclaimed land\", 埋立工事, 埋立処分 \"landfill disposal\". Besides land reclamation in the sea, it thus also means burying waste in landfills. Distinguish it from 干拓, land reclamation by diking and draining.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"入り組んだ海岸線が天然港を形成している。","romaji":"Irikunda kaigansen ga tennenkō o keisei shite iru.","german":"The indented coastline forms a natural harbor.","furigana":"い|く|かいがんせん|てんねんこう|けいせい"},{"japanese":"長崎は、深く入り込んだ湾を持つ天然港として発展した。","romaji":"Nagasaki wa, fukaku irikonda wan o motsu tennenkō to shite hatten shita.","german":"Nagasaki developed as a natural harbor with a deeply recessed bay.","furigana":"ながさき|ふか|い|こ|わん|も|てんねんこう|はってん"},{"japanese":"防波堤がなくても、この天然港は台風の時に船を守ってくれる。","romaji":"Bōhatei ga nakute mo, kono tennenkō wa taifū no toki ni fune o mamotte kureru.","german":"Even without a breakwater, this natural harbor protects ships during typhoons.","furigana":"ぼうはてい|てんねんこう|たいふう|とき|ふね|まも"}],"notes":"天然港 is a \"natural harbor\", that is, a harbor that bays, peninsulas or offshore islands protect from wind and waves by nature, in contrast to the artificially built 人工港. In ordinary usage the phrase 天然の良港 \"a good natural harbor\" is heard even more often. Many such harbors lie on rugged ria coasts リアス海岸; Nagasaki and Sydney are classic examples.","levelNote":"Compound: 天然 (N3) + 港 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"分水嶺を境に水は太平洋側と日本海側に分かれて流れる。","romaji":"Bunsuirei o sakai ni mizu wa Taiheiyō gawa to Nihonkai gawa ni wakarete nagareru.","german":"An der Wasserscheide teilt sich das Wasser und fließt zur Pazifik- und zur Japanischen-Meer-Seite.","furigana":"ぶんすいれい|さかい|みず|たいへいようがわ|にほんかいがわ|わ|なが"},{"japanese":"峠の上に「中央分水嶺」と書かれた標識が立っていた。","romaji":"Tōge no ue ni \"Chūō Bunsuirei\" to kakareta hyōshiki ga tatte ita.","german":"Oben auf dem Pass stand ein Schild mit der Aufschrift „Hauptwasserscheide“.","furigana":"とうげ|うえ|ちゅうおうぶんすいれい|か|ひょうしき|た"},{"japanese":"今回の選挙は、政権交代の分水嶺になるだろう。","romaji":"Konkai no senkyo wa, seiken kōtai no bunsuirei ni naru darō.","german":"Diese Wahl wird wohl zum Wendepunkt für einen Regierungswechsel werden.","furigana":"こんかい|せんきょ|せいけんこうたい|ぶんすいれい"}],"notes":"分水嶺 ist wörtlich der „Bergkamm, der das Wasser teilt“, also die „Wasserscheide“ zwischen zwei Flussgebieten; in Japan verläuft der 中央分水嶺 zwischen Pazifik- und Japanmeerseite. Mindestens ebenso häufig wird es übertragen gebraucht wie das deutsche „Wendepunkt“: 〜が勝敗の分水嶺となった, 人生の分水嶺. Der nüchterne Fachbegriff für die Grenzlinie ist 分水界. Gehobenes, eher schriftsprachliches Wort.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"利根川水系は関東地方の主要な水源である。","romaji":"Tonegawa suikei wa Kantō chihō no shuyō na suigen de aru.","german":"The Tone River system is the main water source of the Kanto region.","furigana":"|かんとうちほう|しゅよう|すいげん"},{"japanese":"日本の大きな川は、国が管理する一級水系に指定されている。","romaji":"Nihon no ōkina kawa wa, kuni ga kanri suru ikkyū suikei ni shitei sarete iru.","german":"Japan's large rivers are designated as Class A river systems managed by the state.","furigana":"にほん|おお|かわ|くに|かんり|いっきゅうすいけい|してい"},{"japanese":"本流と支流をまとめて、一つの水系と呼ぶ。","romaji":"Honryū to shiryū o matomete, hitotsu no suikei to yobu.","german":"The main stream and its tributaries together are called one river system.","furigana":"ほんりゅう|しりゅう|ひと|すいけい|よ"}],"notes":"水系 is the \"river system\": a main stream 本流 with all its tributaries 支流, lakes and the associated catchment area. It typically follows a river name: 利根川水系, 淀川水系. In administrative language Japan divides its rivers into 一級水系 (managed by the state) and 二級水系 (managed by the prefectures). In addition, in medicine 水系 means \"via water\": 水系感染 \"infection via drinking water\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"豪雨により低地の住宅が浸水した。","romaji":"Gōu ni yori teichi no jūtaku ga shinsui shita.","german":"Houses in the lowlands were flooded by the torrential rain.","furigana":"ごうう|ていち|じゅうたく|しんすい"},{"japanese":"大雨で、駅前の商店街の多くが床上浸水の被害を受けた。","romaji":"Ōame de, ekimae no shōtengai no ōku ga yukaue shinsui no higai o uketa.","german":"The heavy rain caused above-floor flooding in many shops in the shopping street in front of the station.","furigana":"おおあめ|えきまえ|しょうてんがい|おお|ゆかうえしんすい|ひがい|う"},{"japanese":"船体に穴が開き、船が浸水し始めた。","romaji":"Sentai ni ana ga aki, fune ga shinsui shihajimeta.","german":"A hole opened in the hull, and the ship began to take on water.","furigana":"せんたい|あな|あ|ふね|しんすい|はじ"}],"notes":"浸水 means that water enters houses, land or a ship; as an intransitive する-verb: 家が浸水する, 船が浸水する. In storm news, 床上浸水 \"water above the floor\" and 床下浸水 \"water only under the floor\" are fixed terms, as are 浸水被害 and 浸水想定区域 on hazard maps. Distinguish 冠水 (roads or fields are under water), 洪水 \"flood\" as an event and 氾濫 \"overflowing of a river\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"遊水地は河川の氾濫を防ぐ治水施設である。","romaji":"Yūsuichi wa kasen no hanran o fusegu chisui shisetsu de aru.","german":"A retarding basin is a flood-control facility that prevents river flooding.","furigana":"ゆうすいち|かせん|はんらん|ふせ|ちすいしせつ"},{"japanese":"渡良瀬遊水地は、日本最大の遊水地として知られている。","romaji":"Watarase yūsuichi wa, Nihon saidai no yūsuichi to shite shirarete iru.","german":"The Watarase Retarding Basin is known as the largest in Japan.","furigana":"|にほんさいだい|ゆうすいち|し"},{"japanese":"普段は公園として使われている遊水地に、台風で川の水が流れ込んだ。","romaji":"Fudan wa kōen to shite tsukawarete iru yūsuichi ni, taifū de kawa no mizu ga nagarekonda.","german":"During the typhoon, river water flowed into the retarding basin, which is normally used as a park.","furigana":"ふだん|こうえん|つか|ゆうすいち|たいふう|かわ|みず|なが|こ"}],"notes":"遊水地 (also 遊水池) is a \"retarding basin\": an area next to a river that is deliberately flooded during high water to store water temporarily and relieve the lower reaches. Outside flood periods it is often used as fields, a park, a sports ground or a wetland. The largest in Japan is the 渡良瀬遊水地. Related are 調整池 \"detention pond\" and ダム; the word belongs to the word field 治水 \"river control, flood protection\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地図学の発展により正確な地形把握が可能になった。","romaji":"Chizugaku no hatten ni yori seikaku na chikei haaku ga kanō ni natta.","german":"The development of cartography made accurate understanding of terrain possible.","furigana":"ちずがく|はってん|せいかく|ちけいはあく|かのう"},{"japanese":"大学で地図学を学び、今は地図の製作会社で働いている。","romaji":"Daigaku de chizugaku o manabi, ima wa chizu no seisaku gaisha de hataraite iru.","german":"I studied cartography at university and now work at a map-making company.","furigana":"だいがく|ちずがく|まな|いま|ちず|せいさくがいしゃ|はたら"},{"japanese":"地図学では、丸い地球を平面にどう描くかが大きな課題になる。","romaji":"Chizugaku de wa, marui chikyū o heimen ni dō egaku ka ga ōkina kadai ni naru.","german":"In cartography, a major challenge is how to depict the round Earth on a flat surface.","furigana":"ちずがく|まる|ちきゅう|へいめん|えが|おお|かだい"}],"notes":"地図学 is \"cartography\" as a science: the study of how to measure the Earth's surface, represent it and translate it into maps, for example with various map projections 図法. For the practical activity people rather say 地図作成 or 地図作り. Closely related are 測量 \"surveying\" and, today, GIS 地理情報システム. The word is academic and appears mainly in university and specialist texts.","levelNote":"Compound: 地図 (N5) + 学 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"等高線の間隔が狭いほど傾斜が急であることを示す。","romaji":"Tōkōsen no kankaku ga semai hodo keisha ga kyū de aru koto o shimesu.","german":"The narrower the spacing of the contour lines, the steeper the slope.","furigana":"とうこうせん|かんかく|せま|けいしゃ|きゅう|しめ"},{"japanese":"登山の前に、地図の等高線を見てルートの険しさを確かめた。","romaji":"Tozan no mae ni, chizu no tōkōsen o mite rūto no kewashisa o tashikameta.","german":"Before the climb, I checked the contour lines on the map to see how rugged the route was.","furigana":"とざん|まえ|ちず|とうこうせん|み|けわ|たし"},{"japanese":"地形図には、10メートルごとに等高線が引かれている。","romaji":"Chikeizu ni wa, jū mētoru goto ni tōkōsen ga hikarete iru.","german":"On the topographic map, a contour line is drawn every 10 meters.","furigana":"ちけいず|とうこうせん|ひ"}],"notes":"等高線 is the \"contour line\" on a map, connecting points of equal elevation. People speak of 等高線を読む \"reading contour lines\" and 等高線の間隔 \"contour spacing\": the denser they are, the steeper the terrain. Formed on the same pattern 等〜線 \"line of equal ...\" are 等圧線 \"isobar\" on weather maps and 等温線 \"isotherm\". On the official 1:25,000 maps 地形図, they are spaced 10 meters apart.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本の標準時は、東経135度の子午線を基準としている。","romaji":"Nihon no hyōjunji wa, tōkei hyaku sanjūgo do no shigosen o kijun to shite iru.","german":"Japan Standard Time is based on the meridian at 135 degrees east longitude.","furigana":"にほん|ひょうじゅんじ|とうけい|ど|しごせん|きじゅん"},{"japanese":"グリニッジ天文台を通る子午線は、本初子午線と呼ばれる。","romaji":"Gurinijji tenmondai o tōru shigosen wa, honsho shigosen to yobareru.","german":"The meridian passing through the Greenwich Observatory is called the prime meridian.","furigana":"てんもんだい|とお|しごせん|ほんしょしごせん|よ"},{"japanese":"明石市立天文科学館の時計塔は、ちょうど子午線の上に建っている。","romaji":"Akashi shiritsu tenmon kagakukan no tokeitō wa, chōdo shigosen no ue ni tatte iru.","german":"The clock tower of the Akashi Municipal Planetarium stands exactly on the meridian.","furigana":"|とけいとう|しごせん|うえ|た"}],"notes":"子午線 is the \"meridian\", a north-south line across the poles. The name comes from the old way of indicating direction with zodiac signs: 子 (rat) stands for north, 午 (horse) for south. In school geography and everyday speech the synonymous 経線 \"line of longitude\" is more common; 子午線 sounds more elevated and is usual in astronomy and timekeeping, for example 本初子午線 \"prime meridian\". The city of Akashi calls itself the \"city of the meridian\" because the meridian for Japan Standard Time runs through it.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"北極圏では夏に白夜が観測される。","romaji":"Hokkyokuken de wa natsu ni byakuya ga kansoku sareru.","german":"In the Arctic, the midnight sun can be observed in summer.","furigana":"ほっきょくけん|なつ|びゃくや|かんそく"},{"japanese":"北極圏の氷が、温暖化で急速に溶けている。","romaji":"Hokkyokuken no kōri ga, ondanka de kyūsoku ni tokete iru.","german":"The ice of the Arctic is melting rapidly due to global warming.","furigana":"ほっきょくけん|こおり|おんだんか|きゅうそく|と"},{"japanese":"フィンランドのロヴァニエミは、北極圏の入り口の町として知られる。","romaji":"Finrando no Rovaniemi wa, hokkyokuken no iriguchi no machi to shite shirareru.","german":"Rovaniemi in Finland is known as the town at the gateway to the Arctic.","furigana":"ほっきょくけん|い|ぐち|まち|し"}],"notes":"北極圏 denotes both the \"Arctic Circle\" as a line (about 66.5° N) and, more often, the whole region north of it, that is, the \"Arctic\". 圏 means \"sphere, realm\", as in 首都圏 or 大気圏. Typical are 北極圏に入る, 北極圏の国々 and topics like 白夜, polar lights オーロラ and melting ice. Its counterpart is 南極圏; 北極 alone is the North Pole.","levelNote":"Compound: 北極 (N2) + 圏 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"偏西風が日本の天候に大きな影響を与えている。","romaji":"Henseifū ga Nihon no tenkō ni ōkina eikyō o ataete iru.","german":"The westerlies have a great influence on Japan's weather.","furigana":"へんせいふう|にほん|てんこう|おお|えいきょう|あた"},{"japanese":"偏西風に乗って、中国大陸から黄砂が飛んでくる。","romaji":"Henseifū ni notte, Chūgoku tairiku kara kōsa ga tonde kuru.","german":"Carried by the westerlies, yellow dust flies in from the Chinese mainland.","furigana":"へんせいふう|の|ちゅうごくたいりく|こうさ|と"},{"japanese":"偏西風が大きく蛇行したため、この夏は記録的な猛暑となった。","romaji":"Henseifū ga ōkiku dakō shita tame, kono natsu wa kirokuteki na mōsho to natta.","german":"Because the westerlies meandered greatly, this summer saw record-breaking heat.","furigana":"へんせいふう|おお|だこう|なつ|きろくてき|もうしょ"}],"notes":"偏西風 are the \"westerlies\" of the middle latitudes, which blow steadily from west to east; that is why weather systems in Japan mostly approach from the west. Their strongest core at high altitude is the jet stream ジェット気流. In the news you often read 偏西風の蛇行 \"meandering of the westerlies\" as a cause of heat waves or cold snaps, and 偏西風に乗って when yellow dust 黄砂 or fine particulates are carried over from China.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"貿易風は赤道付近の低緯度地域で吹く恒常風である。","romaji":"Bōekifū wa sekidō fukin no teiido chiiki de fuku kōjōfū de aru.","german":"Trade winds are constant winds that blow in the low latitudes near the equator.","furigana":"ぼうえきふう|せきどうふきん|ていいどちいき|ふ|こうじょうふう"},{"japanese":"帆船の時代、貿易風は大西洋を渡る船の重要な動力だった。","romaji":"Hansen no jidai, bōekifū wa Taiseiyō o wataru fune no jūyō na dōryoku datta.","german":"In the age of sailing ships, the trade winds were an important source of power for ships crossing the Atlantic.","furigana":"はんせん|じだい|ぼうえきふう|たいせいよう|わた|ふね|じゅうよう|どうりょく"},{"japanese":"貿易風が弱まると、エルニーニョ現象が起きやすくなる。","romaji":"Bōekifū ga yowamaru to, Eru Nīnyo genshō ga okiyasuku naru.","german":"When the trade winds weaken, El Nino events are more likely to occur.","furigana":"ぼうえきふう|よわ|げんしょう|お"}],"notes":"貿易風 is the \"trade wind\": a steady wind that blows from the subtropical high-pressure belts toward the equator, from the northeast in the Northern Hemisphere (北東貿易風) and from the southeast in the Southern Hemisphere (南東貿易風). The name is a calque of English \"trade wind\", because merchant sailing ships used it. Together with 偏西風, it belongs to the 恒常風 \"permanent winds\"; when it weakens, an El Nino event エルニーニョ現象 can arise.","levelNote":"Compound: 貿易 (N4) + 風 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"アジアの季節風は農業に大きな影響を与える。","romaji":"Ajia no kisetsufū wa nōgyō ni ōkina eikyō o ataeru.","german":"The Asian monsoon has a great influence on agriculture.","furigana":"きせつふう|のうぎょう|おお|えいきょう|あた"},{"japanese":"冬になると、北西の季節風が日本海側に大雪を降らせる。","romaji":"Fuyu ni naru to, hokusei no kisetsufū ga Nihonkai-gawa ni ōyuki o furaseru.","german":"In winter, the northwest monsoon brings heavy snow to the Sea of Japan side.","furigana":"ふゆ|ほくせい|きせつふう|にほんかいがわ|おおゆき|ふ"},{"japanese":"季節風の影響で、インドでは夏に雨季が訪れる。","romaji":"Kisetsufū no eikyō de, Indo de wa natsu ni uki ga otozureru.","german":"Because of the monsoon, the rainy season arrives in India in summer.","furigana":"きせつふう|えいきょう|なつ|うき|おとず"}],"notes":"季節風 is a wind that changes direction with the season, that is, the \"monsoon\" (loanword モンスーン). For Japan, the winter monsoon is central: the 北西の季節風 from Siberia brings heavy snowfall to the Sea of Japan side and dry, clear weather to the Pacific side; in summer it blows humid from the southeast. Typical combinations are 季節風が吹く／強まる and 冬の季節風. In South and Southeast Asia it determines the rainy and dry seasons.","levelNote":"Compound: 季節 (N5) + 風 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"寒冷な気団と温暖な気団がぶつかると前線が形成される。","romaji":"Kanrei na kidan to ondan na kidan ga butsukaru to zensen ga keisei sareru.","german":"When a cold air mass and a warm air mass collide, a front forms.","furigana":"かんれい|きだん|おんだん|きだん|ぜんせん|けいせい"},{"japanese":"梅雨前線は、オホーツク海気団と小笠原気団の間にできる。","romaji":"Baiu zensen wa, Ohōtsukukai kidan to Ogasawara kidan no aida ni dekiru.","german":"The rainy-season front forms between the Okhotsk air mass and the Ogasawara air mass.","furigana":"ばいうぜんせん|かいきだん||あいだ"},{"japanese":"冬の日本は、冷たく乾いたシベリア気団に覆われる。","romaji":"Fuyu no Nihon wa, tsumetaku kawaita Shiberia kidan ni ōwareru.","german":"In winter, Japan is covered by a cold, dry Siberian air mass.","furigana":"ふゆ|にほん|つめ|かわ|きだん|おお"}],"notes":"気団 is an \"air mass\": a large body of air with uniform temperature and humidity, named after its region of origin. For Japan's weather, school teaches mainly the シベリア気団 (winter, cold and dry), the 小笠原気団 (summer, hot and humid) and the オホーツク海気団. Where two air masses meet, a front 前線 forms, e.g. the rainy-season front 梅雨前線. The word is technical (meteorology).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"太平洋高気圧が勢力を強めると猛暑になる。","romaji":"Taiheiyō kōkiatsu ga seiryoku o tsuyomeru to mōsho ni naru.","german":"When the Pacific high gains strength, it gets extremely hot.","furigana":"たいへいようこうきあつ|せいりょく|つよ|もうしょ"},{"japanese":"明日は高気圧に覆われて、全国的に晴れるでしょう。","romaji":"Ashita wa kōkiatsu ni ōwarete, zenkokuteki ni hareru deshō.","german":"Tomorrow, a high-pressure system will cover the country, and it will probably be sunny nationwide.","furigana":"あした|こうきあつ|おお|ぜんこくてき|は"},{"japanese":"春は移動性高気圧と低気圧が交互にやってくる。","romaji":"Haru wa idōsei kōkiatsu to teikiatsu ga kōgo ni yatte kuru.","german":"In spring, migratory highs and lows come alternately.","furigana":"はる|いどうせいこうきあつ|ていきあつ|こうご"}],"notes":"高気圧 is a \"high-pressure area\" that usually brings fine, dry weather. The most important phrase in the weather report is 高気圧に覆われる \"to be under the influence of high pressure\"; also 高気圧が張り出す \"to extend\" and 勢力を強める. Typical names are 太平洋高気圧 for the hot summer and 移動性高気圧 \"migratory highs\" in spring and autumn. Its counterpart is 低気圧.","levelNote":"Compound: 高 (N2) + 気圧 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"発達した低気圧が暴風雨をもたらした。","romaji":"Hattatsu shita teikiatsu ga bōfūu o motarashita.","german":"A well-developed low-pressure system brought a rainstorm.","furigana":"はったつ|ていきあつ|ぼうふうう"},{"japanese":"台風は北上して、温帯低気圧に変わった。","romaji":"Taifū wa hokujō shite, ontai teikiatsu ni kawatta.","german":"The typhoon moved north and changed into an extratropical cyclone.","furigana":"たいふう|ほくじょう|おんたいていきあつ|か"},{"japanese":"低気圧が近づくと、いつも頭が痛くなる。","romaji":"Teikiatsu ga chikazuku to, itsumo atama ga itaku naru.","german":"Whenever a low approaches, I always get a headache.","furigana":"ていきあつ|ちか|あたま|いた"}],"notes":"低気圧 is a \"low-pressure area\" that brings clouds, rain and wind. Common are 低気圧が発達する \"to intensify\", 爆弾低気圧 \"bomb cyclone\" and 温帯低気圧 \"extratropical cyclone\", into which a typhoon turns on its way north. In everyday speech you often hear 低気圧で頭が痛い, because many people are sensitive to the weather. Colloquially and jokingly, 低気圧 also means \"bad mood\": 今日は部長が低気圧だ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"冬型の気圧配置が日本海側に大雪をもたらす。","romaji":"Fuyugata no kiatsu haichi ga Nihonkaigawa ni ōyuki o motarasu.","german":"A winter-type pressure pattern brings heavy snow to the Sea of Japan side.","furigana":"ふゆがた|きあつはいち|にほんかいがわ|おおゆき"},{"japanese":"夏は南高北低の気圧配置になり、蒸し暑い日が続く。","romaji":"Natsu wa nankō hokutei no kiatsu haichi ni nari, mushiatsui hi ga tsuzuku.","german":"In summer, the pressure pattern is high in the south and low in the north, and muggy days continue.","furigana":"なつ|なんこうほくてい|きあつはいち|む|あつ|ひ|つづ"},{"japanese":"天気図を見れば、その日の気圧配置がすぐにわかる。","romaji":"Tenkizu o mireba, sono hi no kiatsu haichi ga sugu ni wakaru.","german":"If you look at the weather map, you can immediately tell the day's pressure pattern.","furigana":"てんきず|み|ひ|きあつはいち"}],"notes":"気圧配置 is the \"air pressure distribution\" or \"weather pattern\" as seen on the weather map 天気図. The best-known formula is 西高東低の冬型の気圧配置 (the winter-type pattern with high pressure in the west and low in the east), which brings Japan cold and, on the Sea of Japan side, snow; in summer, by contrast, 南高北低 prevails. In the weather report you often hear 冬型の気圧配置が強まる／緩む. A technical word, but generally known through weather forecasts.","levelNote":"Compound: 気圧 (N2) + 配置 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今年の降水量は例年を大幅に上回った。","romaji":"Kotoshi no kōsuiryō wa reinen o ōhaba ni uwamawatta.","german":"This year's precipitation far exceeded the normal level.","furigana":"ことし|こうすいりょう|れいねん|おおはば|うわまわ"},{"japanese":"屋久島は、日本でも有数の降水量の多い島だ。","romaji":"Yakushima wa, Nihon de mo yūsū no kōsuiryō no ōi shima da.","german":"Yakushima is one of the islands with the highest precipitation in Japan.","furigana":"やくしま|にほん|ゆうすう|こうすいりょう|おお|しま"},{"japanese":"1時間の降水量が100ミリを超える猛烈な雨が降った。","romaji":"Ichijikan no kōsuiryō ga hyaku miri o koeru mōretsu na ame ga futta.","german":"Torrential rain fell, with precipitation of over 100 millimeters in one hour.","furigana":"じかん|こうすいりょう|こ|もうれつ|あめ|ふ"}],"notes":"降水量 is the \"amount of precipitation\", that is, rain, snow and hail together, given in millimeters (ミリ). Typical are 年間降水量, 降水量が多い／少ない and 例年を上回る／下回る. Do not confuse it with 降水確率 \"probability of precipitation\" from the weather report. In everyday speech, for rain people also simply say 雨量; 降水量 is the official and scientific term.","levelNote":"Compound: 降水 (N1) + 量 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"乾燥帯では年間降水量が極めて少ない。","romaji":"Kansōtai de wa nenkan kōsuiryō ga kiwamete sukunai.","german":"In the arid zone, annual precipitation is extremely low.","furigana":"かんそうたい|ねんかんこうすいりょう|きわ|すく"},{"japanese":"ケッペンの気候区分では、乾燥帯は砂漠気候とステップ気候に分けられる。","romaji":"Keppen no kikō kubun de wa, kansōtai wa sabaku kikō to suteppu kikō ni wakerareru.","german":"In the Koppen climate classification, the arid zone is divided into desert climate and steppe climate.","furigana":"きこうくぶん|かんそうたい|さばくきこう|きこう|わ"},{"japanese":"乾燥帯の人々は、オアシスの周りで農業を営んできた。","romaji":"Kansōtai no hitobito wa, oashisu no mawari de nōgyō o itonande kita.","german":"People in the arid zone have long practiced farming around oases.","furigana":"かんそうたい|ひとびと|まわ|のうぎょう|いとな"}],"notes":"乾燥帯 is the \"arid zone\" in the Koppen climate classification (ケッペンの気候区分), alongside 熱帯, 温帯, 冷帯（亜寒帯） and 寒帯. Here more water evaporates than falls as precipitation; it is subdivided into 砂漠気候 \"desert climate\" and ステップ気候 \"steppe climate\". The more general word for \"dry region\" is 乾燥地帯; 乾燥帯 is mainly a textbook term.","levelNote":"Compound: 乾燥 (N2) + 帯 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本の大部分は温帯に属している。","romaji":"Nihon no daibubun wa ontai ni zoku shite iru.","german":"Most of Japan belongs to the temperate zone.","furigana":"にほん|だいぶぶん|おんたい|ぞく"},{"japanese":"温帯は四季の変化がはっきりしているのが特徴だ。","romaji":"Ontai wa shiki no henka ga hakkiri shite iru no ga tokuchō da.","german":"The temperate zone is characterized by clearly distinct seasons.","furigana":"おんたい|しき|へんか|とくちょう"},{"japanese":"温帯の森では、秋になると多くの木が葉を落とす。","romaji":"Ontai no mori de wa, aki ni naru to ōku no ki ga ha o otosu.","german":"In temperate-zone forests, many trees shed their leaves in autumn.","furigana":"おんたい|もり|あき|おお|き|は|お"}],"notes":"温帯 is the \"temperate zone\" between the tropics and the polar regions, with mild temperatures and distinct seasons. It appears in 温帯気候, 温帯林 and 温帯湿潤気候, the climate of most of Japan. Weather reports also speak of 温帯低気圧 when a typhoon turns into an \"extratropical low\". The sequence of climate zones is 熱帯・亜熱帯・温帯・冷帯（亜寒帯）・寒帯.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"沖縄は亜熱帯気候に分類される。","romaji":"Okinawa wa anettai kikō ni bunrui sareru.","german":"Okinawa is classified as having a subtropical climate.","furigana":"おきなわ|あねったいきこう|ぶんるい"},{"japanese":"亜熱帯の島々では、マングローブの林が見られる。","romaji":"Anettai no shimajima de wa, mangurōbu no hayashi ga mirareru.","german":"Mangrove forests can be seen on the subtropical islands.","furigana":"あねったい|しまじま|はやし|み"},{"japanese":"温暖化の影響で、東京の気候が亜熱帯に近づいているとも言われる。","romaji":"Ondanka no eikyō de, Tōkyō no kikō ga anettai ni chikazuite iru to mo iwareru.","german":"It is also said that the climate of Tokyo is approaching the subtropics due to the effects of global warming.","furigana":"おんだんか|えいきょう|とうきょう|きこう|あねったい|ちか|い"}],"notes":"亜熱帯 is the \"subtropics\" between the tropics and the temperate zone. The prefix 亜 means \"sub-, next to, almost\" and is also found in 亜寒帯 \"subarctic zone\" or 亜種 \"subspecies\". Common combinations are 亜熱帯気候, 亜熱帯植物 and 亜熱帯高気圧. In Japan, mainly Okinawa and the Ogasawara Islands count as subtropical; with climate change one also reads that Tokyo is becoming \"more subtropical\" 亜熱帯化.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"熱帯地域では一年中高温多湿である。","romaji":"Nettai chiiki de wa ichinenjū kōon tashitsu de aru.","german":"In the tropics it is hot and humid all year round.","furigana":"ねったいちいき|いちねんじゅうこうおんたしつ"},{"japanese":"今夜も熱帯夜で、なかなか眠れない。","romaji":"Kon'ya mo nettaiya de, nakanaka nemurenai.","german":"Tonight is another tropical night, and I can hardly sleep.","furigana":"こんや|ねったいや|ねむ"},{"japanese":"子どもの頃、家で熱帯魚を飼っていた。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, ie de nettaigyo o katte ita.","german":"When I was a child, I kept tropical fish at home.","furigana":"こ|ころ|いえ|ねったいぎょ|か"}],"notes":"熱帯 refers to the tropical climate zone around the equator. Very common in compounds: 熱帯雨林 \"tropical rainforest\", 熱帯魚 \"tropical aquarium fish\" and 熱帯夜 \"tropical night\" (a night when the temperature does not fall below 25 °C - a standard term in Japanese summer weather reports). Attributively it takes の: 熱帯の植物. The series of climate zones is 熱帯・亜熱帯・温帯・亜寒帯・寒帯; Okinawa counts as 亜熱帯 \"subtropics\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"寒帯には樹木がほとんど育たない地域が広がっている。","romaji":"Kantai ni wa jumoku ga hotondo sodatanai chiiki ga hirogatte iru.","german":"The polar zone includes vast areas where almost no trees grow.","furigana":"かんたい|じゅもく|そだ|ちいき|ひろ"},{"japanese":"寒帯の動物は厚い脂肪や毛皮で寒さから身を守っている。","romaji":"Kantai no dōbutsu wa atsui shibō ya kegawa de samusa kara mi o mamotte iru.","german":"Animals of the polar zone protect themselves from the cold with thick fat and fur.","furigana":"かんたい|どうぶつ|あつ|しぼう|けがわ|さむ|み|まも"},{"japanese":"地理の授業で熱帯・温帯・寒帯の違いを習った。","romaji":"Chiri no jugyō de nettai, ontai, kantai no chigai o naratta.","german":"In geography class, we learned the differences between the tropical, temperate and polar zones.","furigana":"ちり|じゅぎょう|ねったい|おんたい|かんたい|ちが|なら"}],"notes":"寒帯 is the cold climate zone of the polar regions, a technical term from geography and climatology (寒帯気候, 寒帯に属する). It is the counterpart of 熱帯; between them lie 温帯 and 亜寒帯 (e.g. Hokkaidō). In everyday speech one more often says 北極・南極 or 寒い地方. Not to be confused with the identically read 艦隊 \"fleet\" and 歓待 \"warm hospitality\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ツンドラ地帯の地下には永久凍土が広がっている。","romaji":"Tsundora chitai no chika ni wa eikyū tōdo ga hirogatte iru.","german":"Permafrost extends beneath the tundra zone.","furigana":"ちたい|ちか|えいきゅうとうど|ひろ"},{"japanese":"短い夏の間、ツンドラには小さな花が一面に咲く。","romaji":"Mijikai natsu no aida, tsundora ni wa chiisana hana ga ichimen ni saku.","german":"During the short summer, small flowers bloom all over the tundra.","furigana":"みじか|なつ|あいだ|ちい|はな|いちめん|さ"},{"japanese":"トナカイはツンドラでコケを食べて生きている。","romaji":"Tonakai wa tsundora de koke o tabete ikite iru.","german":"Reindeer live on the tundra, eating moss.","furigana":"た|い"}],"notes":"ツンドラ \"tundra\" is a loanword (via Russian) and is always written in katakana. It means the treeless cold steppe with mosses, lichens and dwarf shrubs above permafrost (永久凍土). Typical combinations: ツンドラ地帯, ツンドラ気候. To the south it is followed by the タイガ \"coniferous forest\". In internet slang there is also ツンドラ as a pun on ツンデレ for a person who is merely cold and snippy - that is slang and not meant here.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"シベリアのタイガは世界最大の森林地帯である。","romaji":"Shiberia no taiga wa sekai saidai no shinrin chitai de aru.","german":"The Siberian taiga is the largest forest region in the world.","furigana":"せかいさいだい|しんりんちたい"},{"japanese":"タイガにはモミやトウヒなどの針葉樹が多い。","romaji":"Taiga ni wa momi ya tōhi nado no shin'yōju ga ōi.","german":"The taiga has many conifers such as fir and spruce.","furigana":"しんようじゅ|おお"},{"japanese":"タイガの森林火災が年々大規模になっている。","romaji":"Taiga no shinrin kasai ga nennen daikibo ni natte iru.","german":"Forest fires in the taiga are getting bigger every year.","furigana":"しんりんかさい|ねんねんだいきぼ"}],"notes":"タイガ (from Russian) is the boreal coniferous forest belt south of the tundra, mainly in Siberia, Scandinavia and Canada. It is a geography term; synonymous are 針葉樹林帯 or 北方林. Typical: シベリアのタイガ, タイガ地帯. Watch the long vowel: タイガー with ー is the \"tiger\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"モンゴルのステップでは遊牧民が暮らしている。","romaji":"Mongoru no suteppu de wa yūbokumin ga kurashite iru.","german":"Nomads live on the Mongolian steppe.","furigana":"ゆうぼくみん|く"},{"japanese":"ステップ気候の地域は雨が少なく、木がほとんど育たない。","romaji":"Suteppu kikō no chiiki wa ame ga sukunaku, ki ga hotondo sodatanai.","german":"In regions with a steppe climate, there is little rain and hardly any trees grow.","furigana":"きこう|ちいき|あめ|すく|き|そだ"},{"japanese":"列車の窓から、果てしなく続くステップを眺めた。","romaji":"Ressha no mado kara, hateshinaku tsuzuku suteppu o nagameta.","german":"From the train window, I gazed at the endless steppe.","furigana":"れっしゃ|まど|は|つづ|なが"}],"notes":"ステップ in the sense of \"steppe\" (from Russian степь) is a treeless grassland in a dry climate, for example in Mongolia or Kazakhstan. Careful: in everyday speech the homonym ステップ from English \"step\" is much more common (step, dance step, 次のステップ \"the next step\"). For clarity people therefore often say ステップ気候 or simply 草原. Distinction: サバンナ is tropical grassland with scattered trees.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"アフリカのサバンナには多種多様な野生動物が生息している。","romaji":"Afurika no sabanna ni wa tashu tayō na yasei dōbutsu ga seisoku shite iru.","german":"A wide variety of wild animals live in the African savanna.","furigana":"たしゅたよう|やせいどうぶつ|せいそく"},{"japanese":"サバンナでは乾季になると、動物たちが水を求めて移動する。","romaji":"Sabanna de wa kanki ni naru to, dōbutsutachi ga mizu o motomete idō suru.","german":"In the savanna, animals move around in search of water during the dry season.","furigana":"かんき|どうぶつ|みず|もと|いどう"},{"japanese":"いつかサバンナでライオンの群れを見てみたい。","romaji":"Itsuka sabanna de raion no mure o mite mitai.","german":"Someday I would like to see a pride of lions in the savanna.","furigana":"む|み"}],"notes":"サバンナ \"savanna\" is tropical grassland with scattered trees and a clear alternation of rainy and dry seasons, mainly in Africa. In geography books the spelling サバナ (サバナ気候) is often used; in everyday speech and animal programs people say サバンナ. Typical: サバンナの動物, サバンナに暮らす. In contrast to the ステップ, the savanna lies in the tropics and is not entirely treeless.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"山麓に広がる牧草地で牛が放牧されている。","romaji":"Sanroku ni hirogaru bokusōchi de ushi ga hōboku sarete iru.","german":"Cows are grazing on the pasture that spreads out at the foot of the mountain.","furigana":"さんろく|ひろ|ぼくそうち|うし|ほうぼく"},{"japanese":"ロープウェイの山麓駅で切符を買った。","romaji":"Rōpuwei no sanroku eki de kippu o katta.","german":"I bought a ticket at the base station of the ropeway.","furigana":"さんろくえき|きっぷ|か"},{"japanese":"富士山麓には湖や洞窟などの観光地が多い。","romaji":"Fujisanroku ni wa mizuumi ya dōkutsu nado no kankōchi ga ōi.","german":"There are many tourist spots such as lakes and caves at the foot of Mt. Fuji.","furigana":"ふじさんろく|みずうみ|どうくつ|かんこうち|おお"}],"notes":"山麓 is the written-language word for the foot of a mountain and appears in descriptions of places, tourism texts and the news (山麓の町, 富士山麓). In speech people rather say 山のふもと. 裾野, by contrast, emphasizes the widely spreading area around the mountain. For cable cars, the valley station is called 山麓駅 and the summit station 山頂駅.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"山頂からの眺望は壮大であった。","romaji":"Sanchō kara no chōbō wa sōdai de atta.","german":"The view from the summit was magnificent.","furigana":"さんちょう|ちょうぼう|そうだい"},{"japanese":"やっと山頂に着いて、みんなでお弁当を食べた。","romaji":"Yatto sanchō ni tsuite, minna de obentō o tabeta.","german":"We finally reached the summit and all ate our lunch boxes together.","furigana":"さんちょう|つ|べんとう|た"},{"japanese":"天気が崩れそうなので、山頂を目指すのはあきらめた。","romaji":"Tenki ga kuzuresō na node, sanchō o mezasu no wa akirameta.","german":"The weather looked likely to turn, so we gave up on aiming for the summit.","furigana":"てんき|くず|さんちょう|めざ"}],"notes":"山頂 is the neutral standard word for the summit of a mountain. Typical combinations: 山頂に着く／到達する, 山頂を目指す, 山頂からの眺め. In speech you often also hear 頂上, which can additionally be used figuratively (e.g. 頂上会談 \"summit meeting\"); 山頂, by contrast, stays literally with the mountain. 頂 (いただき) is literary. The opposite is 山麓.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"尾根伝いに縦走する登山ルートが人気だ。","romaji":"One zutai ni jūsō suru tozan rūto ga ninki da.","german":"Climbing routes that traverse along the ridge are popular.","furigana":"おねづた|じゅうそう|とざん|にんき"},{"japanese":"尾根に出ると、急に風が強くなった。","romaji":"One ni deru to, kyū ni kaze ga tsuyoku natta.","german":"When we came out onto the ridge, the wind suddenly got stronger.","furigana":"おね|で|きゅう|かぜ|つよ"},{"japanese":"この尾根道は眺めがよく、初心者にも歩きやすい。","romaji":"Kono onemichi wa nagame ga yoku, shoshinsha ni mo arukiyasui.","german":"This ridge trail has a nice view and is easy to walk even for beginners.","furigana":"おねみち|なが|しょしんしゃ|ある"}],"notes":"尾根 is the elongated mountain ridge that separates two valleys; its counterpart is 谷 or 沢. Typical in hiking: 尾根を歩く, 尾根に出る, 尾根伝いに \"along the ridge\", 尾根道 \"ridge trail\". 稜線 means almost the same but emphasizes the visible line of the crest against the sky and is stylistically more elevated.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"谷底を清流が流れている。","romaji":"Tanizoko o seiryū ga nagarete iru.","german":"A clear stream flows along the valley floor.","furigana":"たにぞこ|せいりゅう|なが"},{"japanese":"車がガードレールを越えて谷底に転落した。","romaji":"Kuruma ga gādorēru o koete tanizoko ni tenraku shita.","german":"A car broke through the guardrail and plunged to the bottom of the valley.","furigana":"くるま|こ|たにぞこ|てんらく"},{"japanese":"つり橋の上から谷底を見下ろすと、足がすくんだ。","romaji":"Tsuribashi no ue kara tanizoko o miorosu to, ashi ga sukunda.","german":"When I looked down at the valley floor from the suspension bridge, my legs froze.","furigana":"ばし|うえ|たにぞこ|みお|あし"}],"notes":"谷底 (with voicing: たにぞこ) is the deepest part of a valley or gorge. Common in accident reports: 谷底に転落する \"to fall into the gorge\"; also 谷底を見下ろす. For a figurative low point (poverty, despair) people rather say どん底; in economics people speak of 景気の谷.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この峡谷は数百万年の侵食によって形成された。","romaji":"Kono kyōkoku wa sūhyakuman nen no shinshoku ni yotte keisei sareta.","german":"This canyon was formed by millions of years of erosion.","furigana":"きょうこく|すうひゃくまんねん|しんしょく|けいせい"},{"japanese":"峡谷に架かるつり橋を渡った。","romaji":"Kyōkoku ni kakaru tsuribashi o watatta.","german":"We crossed the suspension bridge spanning the gorge.","furigana":"きょうこく|か|ばし|わた"},{"japanese":"秋になると、この峡谷は紅葉を見に来る観光客でにぎわう。","romaji":"Aki ni naru to, kono kyōkoku wa kōyō o mi ni kuru kankōkyaku de nigiwau.","german":"In autumn, this gorge is crowded with tourists who come to see the autumn leaves.","furigana":"あき|きょうこく|こうよう|み|く|かんこうきゃく"}],"notes":"峡谷 is a deep, narrow valley with steep rock walls, usually carved out by a river (\"gorge, canyon\"). It sounds rather written and appears in nature and travel descriptions and in proper names (黒部峡谷); the Grand Canyon is called グランドキャニオン or 大峡谷. The very common 渓谷 rather emphasizes a mountain valley with a clear stream; the boundary is fluid. 谷 is the general umbrella term.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"稜線を歩く登山者のシルエットが美しい。","romaji":"Ryōsen o aruku tozansha no shiruetto ga utsukushii.","german":"The silhouettes of climbers walking along the ridgeline are beautiful.","furigana":"りょうせん|ある|とざんしゃ|うつく"},{"japanese":"夕日が西の稜線に沈んでいった。","romaji":"Yūhi ga nishi no ryōsen ni shizunde itta.","german":"The evening sun sank behind the western ridgeline.","furigana":"ゆうひ|にし|りょうせん|しず"},{"japanese":"稜線に出ると、遠くに富士山が見えた。","romaji":"Ryōsen ni deru to, tōku ni Fujisan ga mieta.","german":"When we came out onto the ridgeline, we could see Mt. Fuji in the distance.","furigana":"りょうせん|で|とお|ふじさん|み"}],"notes":"稜線 is the line that a mountain ridge draws against the sky (\"ridgeline, crest line\"). The word is elevated and popular in mountain reports and landscape descriptions: 稜線を歩く, 稜線に出る, 夕日が稜線に沈む. Compared with 尾根, the terrain ridge itself, 稜線 emphasizes the line or silhouette. The kanji 稜 means \"edge\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二つの峰の間の鞍部にテントを張った。","romaji":"Futatsu no mine no aida no anbu ni tento o hatta.","german":"We pitched our tent in the saddle between two peaks.","furigana":"ふた|みね|あいだ|あんぶ|は"},{"japanese":"登山道は鞍部まで下ってから、再び急な登りになる。","romaji":"Tozandō wa anbu made kudatte kara, futatabi kyū na nobori ni naru.","german":"The trail descends to the saddle and then climbs steeply again.","furigana":"とざんどう|あんぶ|くだ|ふたた|きゅう|のぼ"},{"japanese":"鞍部は風の通り道になりやすく、天気が変わりやすい。","romaji":"Anbu wa kaze no tōrimichi ni nariyasuku, tenki ga kawariyasui.","german":"Saddles tend to be wind corridors, and the weather changes easily there.","furigana":"あんぶ|かぜ|とお|みち|てんき|か"}],"notes":"鞍部 is the lowest point of a ridge between two peaks, literally \"saddle part\" (鞍 = saddle) - that is, \"mountain saddle, col\". It is a technical word from geography and mountaineering. Climbers often say コル; in place names 〜乗越 (のっこし) appears; a pass over which a path or road leads is called 峠.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"富士山の裾野には広大な森林が広がっている。","romaji":"Fujisan no susono ni wa kōdai na shinrin ga hirogatte iru.","german":"A vast forest spreads out at the foot of Mt. Fuji.","furigana":"ふじさん|すその|こうだい|しんりん|ひろ"},{"japanese":"自動車産業は裾野が広く、多くの中小企業を支えている。","romaji":"Jidōsha sangyō wa susono ga hiroku, ōku no chūshō kigyō o sasaete iru.","german":"The automobile industry has a broad base and supports many small and medium-sized companies.","furigana":"じどうしゃさんぎょう|すその|ひろ|おお|ちゅうしょうきぎょう|ささ"},{"japanese":"子ども向けの教室を開いて、競技の裾野を広げたい。","romaji":"Kodomo muke no kyōshitsu o hiraite, kyōgi no susono o hirogetai.","german":"I want to widen the base of the sport by opening classes for children.","furigana":"こ|む|きょうしつ|ひら|きょうぎ|すその|ひろ"}],"notes":"裾野 literally means \"hem field\" (裾 = hem of a garment): the gently sloping plain at the foot of a mountain, classically 富士山の裾野. In contrast to 山麓 or ふもと, it emphasizes the wide expanse. Figuratively it means the broad base of something: 裾野が広い \"to have a broad base\" (for example an industry with many suppliers), 競技の裾野を広げる \"to promote a sport at the grassroots\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"河床が隆起して水の流れが変わった。","romaji":"Kashō ga ryūki shite mizu no nagare ga kawatta.","german":"The riverbed rose, and the flow of water changed.","furigana":"かしょう|りゅうき|みず|なが|か"},{"japanese":"大雨で土砂がたまり、河床が上がっている。","romaji":"Ōame de dosha ga tamari, kashō ga agatte iru.","german":"Sediment has accumulated after heavy rain, and the riverbed is rising.","furigana":"おおあめ|どしゃ|かしょう|あ"},{"japanese":"日照りが続き、川の水が干上がって河床が露出した。","romaji":"Hideri ga tsuzuki, kawa no mizu ga hiagatte kashō ga roshutsu shita.","german":"The drought continued, the river dried up, and the riverbed was exposed.","furigana":"ひで|つづ|かわ|みず||かしょう|ろしゅつ"}],"notes":"河床 \"riverbed\" is a technical word from geography, geology and hydraulic engineering (河床が上がる／下がる, 河床の掘削). In everyday speech people say 川底. The kanji 川床 or 河床 read かわどこ／かわゆか, by contrast, denote the summer dining terraces over rivers, for example in Kyoto. Note the reading: 床 here is しょう.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"河川の上流では砂礫が多く堆積している。","romaji":"Kasen no jōryū de wa sareki ga ōku taiseki shite iru.","german":"A lot of gravel has accumulated in the upper reaches of the river.","furigana":"かせん|じょうりゅう|されき|おお|たいせき"},{"japanese":"この地域の地下には厚い砂礫層がある。","romaji":"Kono chiiki no chika ni wa atsui sarekisō ga aru.","german":"There is a thick gravel layer beneath this area.","furigana":"ちいき|ちか|あつ|されきそう"},{"japanese":"火山の斜面は砂礫に覆われ、植物がほとんど育たない。","romaji":"Kazan no shamen wa sareki ni ōware, shokubutsu ga hotondo sodatanai.","german":"The slopes of the volcano are covered with gravel, and hardly any plants grow.","furigana":"かざん|しゃめん|されき|おお|しょくぶつ|そだ"}],"notes":"砂礫 denotes a mixture of sand (砂) and small stones (礫) - \"gravel, shingle, rubble\". It is a technical word from geology, construction and geography: 砂礫層 \"gravel layer\", 砂礫地, 砂礫が堆積する. In everyday speech people say 砂利 (e.g. for gravel paths) or 小石. The kanji 礫 otherwise occurs almost only in technical words such as 礫岩 \"conglomerate\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"沖合に巨大な海底山脈が存在する。","romaji":"Okiai ni kyodai na kaitei sanmyaku ga sonzai suru.","german":"A huge undersea mountain range exists offshore.","furigana":"おきあい|きょだい|かいていさんみゃく|そんざい"},{"japanese":"千葉県の沖合で地震が発生しました。","romaji":"Chiba-ken no okiai de jishin ga hassei shimashita.","german":"An earthquake occurred off the coast of Chiba Prefecture.","furigana":"ちばけん|おきあい|じしん|はっせい"},{"japanese":"漁船は沖合十キロの地点で網を下ろした。","romaji":"Gyosen wa okiai jukkiro no chiten de ami o oroshita.","german":"The fishing boat dropped its nets at a point ten kilometers offshore.","furigana":"ぎょせん|おきあいじゅっ|ちてん|あみ|お"}],"notes":"沖合 (also 沖合い) denotes the sea at some distance from the coast, \"offshore, at sea\". It is typical of news and of earthquake and weather reports: 〜の沖合で地震が発生, 沖合10キロ. 沖 alone is synonymous and more everyday; 沖合 emphasizes the area. It does not mean the high seas: for that there are 外洋 or 遠洋 (遠洋漁業), while 沖合漁業 is fishing between the coast and the high seas.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"内湾は波が穏やかで養殖に適している。","romaji":"Naiwan wa nami ga odayaka de yōshoku ni tekishite iru.","german":"The waves are calm in an inner bay, making it suitable for aquaculture.","furigana":"ないわん|なみ|おだ|ようしょく|てき"},{"japanese":"内湾は海水が入れ替わりにくく、赤潮が発生しやすい。","romaji":"Naiwan wa kaisui ga irekawarinikuku, akashio ga hassei shiyasui.","german":"In an inner bay, seawater is slow to be exchanged, so red tides occur easily.","furigana":"ないわん|かいすい|い|か|あかしお|はっせい"},{"japanese":"この港は内湾にあるので、台風のときも船が避難できる。","romaji":"Kono minato wa naiwan ni aru node, taifū no toki mo fune ga hinan dekiru.","german":"This harbor is in an inner bay, so ships can take shelter there even during typhoons.","furigana":"みなと|ないわん|たいふう|ふね|ひなん"}],"notes":"内湾 is a bay cut deep into the land and almost closed off from the outside, with calm water (\"inner bay\"). The word is technical (geography, fisheries, environmental reports: 内湾の水質, 内湾漁業). Its opposites are 外洋 and 外海. Do not confuse it with 内海 (うちうみ／ないかい) \"inland sea\" as in the 瀬戸内海.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"外洋では潮の流れが非常に速い。","romaji":"Gaiyō de wa shio no nagare ga hijō ni hayai.","german":"In the open sea, the tidal current is extremely fast.","furigana":"がいよう|しお|なが|ひじょう|はや"},{"japanese":"小さなヨットで外洋に出るのは危険だ。","romaji":"Chiisana yotto de gaiyō ni deru no wa kiken da.","german":"It is dangerous to go out into the open sea in a small yacht.","furigana":"ちい|がいよう|で|きけん"},{"japanese":"湾を抜けると、外洋のうねりで船が大きく揺れ始めた。","romaji":"Wan o nukeru to, gaiyō no uneri de fune ga ōkiku yure hajimeta.","german":"As soon as we left the bay, the ship began to roll heavily in the swell of the open sea.","furigana":"わん|ぬ|がいよう|ふね|おお|ゆ|はじ"}],"notes":"外洋 is the open sea beyond bays and coastal waters, with high waves (\"open sea, high seas\"). Typical: 外洋に出る, 外洋航海, 外洋のうねり. Its opposites are 内湾 and 沿岸. 遠洋 emphasizes the great distance from home (遠洋漁業), and 公海 is the international-law term for \"international waters\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"陸棚の海底にはガス田が存在することがある。","romaji":"Rikudana no kaitei ni wa gasuden ga sonzai suru koto ga aru.","german":"Gas fields can exist on the seabed of the continental shelf.","furigana":"りくだな|かいてい|でん|そんざい"},{"japanese":"陸棚は日光が届くため、魚の種類が多い。","romaji":"Rikudana wa nikkō ga todoku tame, sakana no shurui ga ōi.","german":"Because sunlight reaches the continental shelf, there are many kinds of fish.","furigana":"りくだな|にっこう|とど|さかな|しゅるい|おお"},{"japanese":"東シナ海の大部分は浅い陸棚である。","romaji":"Higashi Shinakai no daibubun wa asai rikudana de aru.","german":"Most of the East China Sea is a shallow continental shelf.","furigana":"ひがし|かい|だいぶぶん|あさ|りくだな"}],"notes":"陸棚 (with voicing: りくだな) is the shallow sea floor down to a depth of about 200 m along the continents, \"shelf, continental shelf\". It is the technical short form of 大陸棚, which is much more usual in everyday speech and in international law (大陸棚の境界). Seaward follow the 大陸斜面 and finally the 大洋底.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大洋底には平坦な深海平原が広がっている。","romaji":"Taiyōtei ni wa heitan na shinkai heigen ga hirogatte iru.","german":"Wide, flat abyssal plains stretch across the ocean floor.","furigana":"たいようてい||しんかいへいげん|ひろ"},{"japanese":"大洋底の岩石は大陸の岩石よりずっと新しい。","romaji":"Taiyōtei no ganseki wa tairiku no ganseki yori zutto atarashii.","german":"The rocks of the ocean floor are much younger than those of the continents.","furigana":"たいようてい|がんせき|たいりく|がんせき|あたら"},{"japanese":"大洋底は中央海嶺で生まれ、少しずつ移動している。","romaji":"Taiyōtei wa chūō kairei de umare, sukoshi zutsu idō shite iru.","german":"The ocean floor is born at mid-ocean ridges and slowly moves.","furigana":"たいようてい|ちゅうおうかいれい|う|すこ|いどう"}],"notes":"大洋底 is composed of 大洋 \"ocean\" and 底 \"bottom\" and in oceanography denotes the floor of the great oceans beyond the continental margin, mostly at a depth of 3,000 to 6,000 m. The division seen from land: 陸棚 → 大陸斜面 → 大洋底 (with 深海平原, 海山 and 海溝). In everyday speech people simply say 海底 or 深海の底.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"海山の周辺は豊かな漁場となっている。","romaji":"Kaizan no shūhen wa yutaka na gyojō to natte iru.","german":"The area around a seamount makes a rich fishing ground.","furigana":"かいざん|しゅうへん|ゆた|ぎょじょう"},{"japanese":"調査船が水深二千メートルにある海山を詳しく調べた。","romaji":"Chōsasen ga suishin nisen mētoru ni aru kaizan o kuwashiku shirabeta.","german":"A research vessel examined in detail a seamount at a depth of 2,000 meters.","furigana":"ちょうさせん|すいしんにせん|かいざん|くわ|しら"},{"japanese":"ハワイ諸島の北西には、古い火山が沈んでできた海山が連なっている。","romaji":"Hawai shotō no hokusei ni wa, furui kazan ga shizunde dekita kaizan ga tsuranatte iru.","german":"Northwest of the Hawaiian Islands, a chain of seamounts formed by sunken old volcanoes extends.","furigana":"しょとう|ほくせい|ふる|かざん|しず|かいざん|つら"}],"notes":"海山 read かいざん is a mostly volcanic mountain on the sea floor that does not reach the surface (\"seamount\"); with a flattened summit it is called 平頂海山. It is an oceanography term. Careful: the same kanji read うみやま mean \"sea and mountains\" and, figuratively, \"immeasurable\", for example in 海山の恩 \"a deep debt of gratitude\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"生態回廊の設置が動物の移動を可能にする。","romaji":"Seitai kairō no setchi ga dōbutsu no idō o kanō ni suru.","german":"Installing ecological corridors makes animal movement possible.","furigana":"せいたいかいろう|せっち|どうぶつ|いどう|かのう"},{"japanese":"道路の下にトンネルを作り、生態回廊として動物が通れるようにした。","romaji":"Dōro no shita ni tonneru o tsukuri, seitai kairō to shite dōbutsu ga tōreru yō ni shita.","german":"A tunnel was built under the road to serve as an ecological corridor so animals can pass.","furigana":"どうろ|した|つく|せいたいかいろう|どうぶつ|とお"},{"japanese":"森が分断されると、生態回廊がなければ動物は孤立してしまう。","romaji":"Mori ga bundan sareru to, seitai kairō ga nakereba dōbutsu wa koritsu shite shimau.","german":"When a forest is fragmented, animals become isolated without ecological corridors.","furigana":"もり|ぶんだん|せいたいかいろう|どうぶつ|こりつ"}],"notes":"生態回廊 is a strip of habitat (forest, hedgerow, river bank, green bridge) that connects separated protected areas so that animals can migrate and populations can exchange - \"ecological corridor, habitat network\". A technical term from nature conservation; synonymous are エコロジカル・コリドー and, for forest corridors of the Japanese forestry agency, 緑の回廊. Typical: 生態回廊を整備する／設ける. 回廊 alone is a roofed colonnade, for example in temples.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"汚染された土地の環境修復に長い年月を要する。","romaji":"Osen sareta tochi no kankyō shūfuku ni nagai nengetsu o yō suru.","german":"The environmental remediation of contaminated land takes many years.","furigana":"おせん|とち|かんきょうしゅうふく|なが|ねんげつ|よう"},{"japanese":"鉱山の跡地では、植林による環境修復が進められている。","romaji":"Kōzan no atochi de wa, shokurin ni yoru kankyō shūfuku ga susumerarete iru.","german":"At the former mine site, environmental restoration through tree planting is under way.","furigana":"こうざん|あとち|しょくりん|かんきょうしゅうふく|すす"},{"japanese":"その企業は環境修復の費用として多額の賠償金を支払った。","romaji":"Sono kigyō wa kankyō shūfuku no hiyō to shite tagaku no baishōkin o shiharatta.","german":"The company paid a large sum in compensation for the cost of environmental remediation.","furigana":"きぎょう|かんきょうしゅうふく|ひよう|たがく|ばいしょうきん|しはら"}],"notes":"環境修復 denotes the restoration of polluted or destroyed environment such as soil, waters and ecosystems (\"environmental remediation\"). It is a technical word in official and environmental texts: 環境修復に取り組む, 環境修復事業. Related are 環境再生, 土壌浄化 \"soil remediation\" and バイオレメディエーション (remediation with microorganisms). 修復 alone means \"repair, restoration\" (文化財の修復, 関係修復).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"帰化植物が在来種の生育を圧迫している。","romaji":"Kika shokubutsu ga zairaishu no seiiku o appaku shite iru.","german":"Naturalized plants are crowding out the growth of native species.","furigana":"きかしょくぶつ|ざいらいしゅ|せいいく|あっぱく"},{"japanese":"道端に咲いているこのタンポポは、実は帰化植物だ。","romaji":"Michibata ni saite iru kono tanpopo wa, jitsu wa kika shokubutsu da.","german":"This dandelion blooming by the roadside is actually a naturalized plant.","furigana":"みちばた|さ|じつ|きかしょくぶつ"},{"japanese":"河川敷の植物を調べると、半分近くが帰化植物だった。","romaji":"Kasenshiki no shokubutsu o shiraberu to, hanbun chikaku ga kika shokubutsu datta.","german":"When we surveyed the plants on the riverbank, nearly half were naturalized plants.","furigana":"|しょくぶつ|しら|はんぶんちか|きかしょくぶつ"}],"notes":"帰化植物 is a foreign plant species introduced by humans that reproduces independently in the wild (\"naturalized plant, neophyte\"). 帰化 actually means \"naturalization\" and is also used for people (日本に帰化する). The opposite term is 在来種 or 在来植物. Well-known examples are セイヨウタンポポ and セイタカアワダチソウ. For problematic species as a whole people say 外来種, and legally 特定外来生物.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"落葉広葉樹林は四季の変化が美しい。","romaji":"Rakuyō kōyōju rin wa shiki no henka ga utsukushii.","german":"Deciduous broadleaf forests are beautiful for their changes through the four seasons.","furigana":"らくようこうようじゅりん|しき|へんか|うつく"},{"japanese":"白神山地には世界最大級のブナの落葉広葉樹林が残っている。","romaji":"Shirakami sanchi ni wa sekai saidaikyū no buna no rakuyō kōyōju rin ga nokotte iru.","german":"One of the world's largest beech deciduous forests remains in the Shirakami Mountains.","furigana":"|せかいさいだいきゅう|らくようこうようじゅりん|のこ"},{"japanese":"冬の落葉広葉樹林は葉が落ちて、林の中まで日が差し込む。","romaji":"Fuyu no rakuyō kōyōju rin wa ha ga ochite, hayashi no naka made hi ga sashikomu.","german":"In winter, the deciduous forest loses its leaves, and the sun shines in even to the interior of the woods.","furigana":"ふゆ|らくようこうようじゅりん|は|お|はやし|なか|ひ|さ|こ"}],"notes":"落葉広葉樹林 is composed of 落葉 \"shedding leaves\", 広葉樹 \"broadleaf tree\" and 林 \"forest\": a deciduous broadleaf forest that is bare in winter. In Japan it characterizes the cooler climate of Tohoku and Hokkaido, typically with ブナ \"beech\" and ナラ \"oak\". Its opposites are 照葉樹林 (evergreen, southwestern Japan) and 針葉樹林 \"coniferous forest\". It is a technical word from biology and geography; in everyday speech people simply say 林 or 森.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"九州南部から沖縄にかけて照葉樹林が分布している。","romaji":"Kyūshū nanbu kara Okinawa ni kakete shōyōju rin ga bunpu shite iru.","german":"Vom Süden Kyushus bis Okinawa erstrecken sich immergrüne Lorbeerwälder.","furigana":"きゅうしゅうなんぶ|おきなわ|しょうようじゅりん|ぶんぷ"},{"japanese":"照葉樹林は冬でも葉が茂り、森の中は一年中薄暗い。","romaji":"Shōyōju rin wa fuyu demo ha ga shigeri, mori no naka wa ichinenjū usugurai.","german":"Im Lorbeerwald ist das Laub auch im Winter dicht, sodass es im Inneren das ganze Jahr dämmrig ist.","furigana":"しょうようじゅりん|ふゆ|は|しげ|もり|なか|いちねんじゅううすぐら"},{"japanese":"奈良の春日山には、古くから守られてきた照葉樹林がある。","romaji":"Nara no Kasugayama ni wa, furuku kara mamorarete kita shōyōju rin ga aru.","german":"Am Kasuga-Berg in Nara gibt es einen seit alters geschützten Lorbeerwald.","furigana":"なら||ふる|まも|しょうようじゅりん"}],"notes":"照葉樹林 ist ein immergrüner Laubwald aus Bäumen mit glänzenden, ledrigen Blättern (照 „glänzen“, 葉 „Blatt“) wie シイ, カシ, クスノキ oder ツバキ; auf Deutsch spricht man vom „Lorbeerwald“. Er ist typisch für das warm-feuchte Klima Südwestjapans bis Südchina. Der Begriff 照葉樹林文化 bezeichnet eine Theorie gemeinsamer ostasiatischer Kulturelemente wie Mochi, Nattō und Tee. Gegenbegriff ist 落葉広葉樹林.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"標高の中間帯には針広混交林が見られる。","romaji":"Hyōkō no chūkan tai ni wa shinkō konkō rin ga mirareru.","german":"Mixed coniferous-broadleaf forests can be seen in the middle altitude zone.","furigana":"ひょうこう|ちゅうかんたい|しんこうこんこうりん|み"},{"japanese":"北海道の森林の多くは針広混交林である。","romaji":"Hokkaidō no shinrin no ōku wa shinkō konkō rin de aru.","german":"Many of Hokkaido's forests are mixed coniferous-broadleaf forests.","furigana":"ほっかいどう|しんりん|おお|しんこうこんこうりん"},{"japanese":"スギの人工林に広葉樹を植え、針広混交林に変えていく計画がある。","romaji":"Sugi no jinkōrin ni kōyōju o ue, shinkō konkō rin ni kaete iku keikaku ga aru.","german":"There is a plan to plant broadleaf trees in artificial cedar plantations and convert them into mixed forests.","furigana":"じんこうりん|こうようじゅ|う|しんこうこんこうりん|か|けいかく"}],"notes":"針広混交林 is a short form made of 針葉樹 \"conifer\", 広葉樹 \"broadleaf tree\" and 混交林 \"mixed forest\": a forest in which conifers and broadleaf trees grow together. It is typical of Hokkaido and of middle altitudes in the transition between deciduous and coniferous forest. The word comes from forestry and vegetation science; in forestry policy there is often talk of converting uniform cedar plantations into 針広混交林.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"高山帯では森林限界を超えて高山植物が生育する。","romaji":"Kōzantai de wa shinrin genkai o koete kōzan shokubutsu ga seiiku suru.","german":"In the alpine zone, alpine plants grow beyond the tree line.","furigana":"こうざんたい|しんりんげんかい|こ|こうざんしょくぶつ|せいいく"},{"japanese":"高山帯にはハイマツなど、厳しい環境に強い植物が生える。","romaji":"Kōzantai ni wa haimatsu nado, kibishii kankyō ni tsuyoi shokubutsu ga haeru.","german":"Plants that withstand harsh conditions, such as Siberian dwarf pine, grow in the alpine zone.","furigana":"こうざんたい|きび|かんきょう|つよ|しょくぶつ|は"},{"japanese":"温暖化の影響で、高山帯に住むライチョウが減っている。","romaji":"Ondanka no eikyō de, kōzantai ni sumu raichō ga hette iru.","german":"Because of the effects of global warming, the rock ptarmigan living in the alpine zone are declining.","furigana":"おんだんか|えいきょう|こうざんたい|す|へ"}],"notes":"高山帯 is the altitude zone above the tree line (森林限界) with krummholz such as ハイマツ, meadows and 高山植物 \"alpine plants\"; in central Japan it begins at about 2,500 m. The Japanese sequence of altitude zones is 丘陵帯・山地帯・亜高山帯・高山帯. It is a technical word from biology and geography; in everyday speech people rather say 高い山 or 高山.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"森林限界は緯度と標高によって変化する。","romaji":"Shinrin genkai wa ido to hyōkō ni yotte henka suru.","german":"The tree line varies with latitude and altitude.","furigana":"しんりんげんかい|いど|ひょうこう|へんか"},{"japanese":"森林限界を超えると、急に視界が開けた。","romaji":"Shinrin genkai o koeru to, kyū ni shikai ga hiraketa.","german":"Once we passed the tree line, the view suddenly opened up.","furigana":"しんりんげんかい|こ|きゅう|しかい|ひら"},{"japanese":"森林限界より上では雷を避ける場所がほとんどない。","romaji":"Shinrin genkai yori ue de wa kaminari o sakeru basho ga hotondo nai.","german":"Above the tree line, there are almost no places to take shelter from lightning.","furigana":"しんりんげんかい|うえ|かみなり|さ|ばしょ"}],"notes":"森林限界 is the limit in altitude (or latitude) above which no closed forests grow - the \"tree line\". Typical: 森林限界を超える, 森林限界より上. In the Japanese Northern Alps it lies at about 2,500 m, and in Hokkaido considerably lower. It matters in mountain hiking because above it you are exposed to wind, sun and thunderstorms without protection.","levelNote":"Compound: 森林 (N2) + 限界 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"植物の垂直分布は気温と降水量に影響される。","romaji":"Shokubutsu no suichoku bunpu wa kion to kōsuiryō ni eikyō sareru.","german":"The vertical distribution of plants is influenced by temperature and precipitation.","furigana":"しょくぶつ|すいちょくぶんぷ|きおん|こうすいりょう|えいきょう"},{"japanese":"富士山に登ると、植物の垂直分布がよくわかる。","romaji":"Fujisan ni noboru to, shokubutsu no suichoku bunpu ga yoku wakaru.","german":"If you climb Mt. Fuji, you can clearly see the vertical distribution of plants.","furigana":"ふじさん|のぼ|しょくぶつ|すいちょくぶんぷ"},{"japanese":"この調査では、深海魚の垂直分布を水深ごとに記録した。","romaji":"Kono chōsa de wa, shinkaigyo no suichoku bunpu o suishin goto ni kiroku shita.","german":"In this survey, the vertical distribution of deep-sea fish was recorded by water depth.","furigana":"ちょうさ|しんかいぎょ|すいちょくぶんぷ|すいしん|きろく"}],"notes":"垂直分布 is the distribution of plants or animals by height above sea level (or by water depth) - \"vertical distribution, altitudinal zonation\". Its counterpart is 水平分布, the distribution by latitude. It is a technical word and a classic topic in Japanese biology classes (丘陵帯・山地帯・亜高山帯・高山帯).","levelNote":"Compound: 垂直 (N2) + 分布 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"植生の水平分布は緯度と大陸の位置に左右される。","romaji":"Shokusei no suihei bunpu wa ido to tairiku no ichi ni sayū sareru.","german":"The horizontal distribution of vegetation depends on latitude and the position on the continent.","furigana":"しょくせい|すいへいぶんぷ|いど|たいりく|いち|さゆう"},{"japanese":"日本列島は南北に長いため、植物の水平分布の違いがはっきりしている。","romaji":"Nihon rettō wa nanboku ni nagai tame, shokubutsu no suihei bunpu no chigai ga hakkiri shite iru.","german":"Because the Japanese archipelago is long from north to south, differences in the horizontal distribution of plants are clear.","furigana":"にほんれっとう|なんぼく|なが|しょくぶつ|すいへいぶんぷ|ちが"},{"japanese":"テストで、垂直分布と水平分布の違いを説明する問題が出た。","romaji":"Tesuto de, suichoku bunpu to suihei bunpu no chigai o setsumei suru mondai ga deta.","german":"A question on the test asked us to explain the difference between vertical and horizontal distribution.","furigana":"すいちょくぶんぷ|すいへいぶんぷ|ちが|せつめい|もんだい|で"}],"notes":"水平分布 is the distribution of organisms or vegetation by latitude, that is, from south to north - \"horizontal distribution\". In Japan it extends roughly from the subtropical forest of Okinawa to the coniferous forest of Hokkaido; temperature and precipitation are the main determinants. It is the counterpart to 垂直分布 and appears almost only in specialist texts and in teaching.","levelNote":"Compound: 水平 (N2) + 分布 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この瀑布は落差百メートルを超える。","romaji":"Kono bakufu wa rakusa hyaku mētoru o koeru.","german":"This waterfall has a drop of more than a hundred meters.","furigana":"ばくふ|らくさひゃく|こ"},{"japanese":"ナイアガラ瀑布を見に、カナダ側の展望台へ行った。","romaji":"Naiagara bakufu o mi ni, Kanada-gawa no tenbōdai e itta.","german":"To see Niagara Falls, I went to the observation deck on the Canadian side.","furigana":"ばくふ|み|がわ|てんぼうだい|い"},{"japanese":"雪解けの季節には、瀑布が轟音を立てて流れ落ちる。","romaji":"Yukidoke no kisetsu ni wa, bakufu ga gōon o tatete nagareochiru.","german":"In the snowmelt season, the waterfall pours down with a thunderous roar.","furigana":"ゆきど|きせつ|ばくふ||た|なが|お"}],"notes":"瀑布 is the Sino-Japanese, elevated word for \"waterfall\"; in everyday speech people say 滝. It appears in written descriptions and in some names, above all ナイアガラ瀑布 \"Niagara Falls\". The height of the fall is called 落差. The kanji 瀑 is rare and otherwise occurs in, for example, 瀑声 \"roar of a waterfall\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"渓谷の急流は川下りの名所として知られている。","romaji":"Keikoku no kyūryū wa kawakudari no meisho toshite shirarete iru.","german":"The rapids in the gorge are known as a famous spot for river rafting.","furigana":"けいこく|きゅうりゅう|かわくだ|めいしょ|し"},{"japanese":"子どもが急流に流されたが、無事に救助された。","romaji":"Kodomo ga kyūryū ni nagasareta ga, buji ni kyūjo sareta.","german":"A child was swept away by the rapids but was rescued safely.","furigana":"こ|きゅうりゅう|なが|ぶじ|きゅうじょ"},{"japanese":"日本の川は短くて急流が多いと言われる。","romaji":"Nihon no kawa wa mijikakute kyūryū ga ōi to iwareru.","german":"Japan's rivers are said to be short and full of rapids.","furigana":"にほん|かわ|みじか|きゅうりゅう|おお|い"}],"notes":"急流 denotes fast, violent flowing water or a rushing stretch of river (\"rapids, swift current\"). Typical: 急流に流される \"to be swept away by the current\", 急流を下る, 急流下り. Japanese rivers are considered especially 急流 because of their shortness and gradient. 激流 is even more violent; 早瀬 more poetically denotes a shallow, fast-flowing spot.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"川の早瀬を鮎が遡上する。","romaji":"Kawa no hayase o ayu ga sojō suru.","german":"Ayu fish swim up the rapids of the river.","furigana":"かわ|はやせ||そじょう"},{"japanese":"早瀬の水音が谷に響いていた。","romaji":"Hayase no mizuoto ga tani ni hibiite ita.","german":"The sound of the rapids echoed through the valley.","furigana":"はやせ|みずおと|たに|ひび"},{"japanese":"早瀬では足を取られやすいので、川を渡るときは注意しよう。","romaji":"Hayase de wa ashi o torareyasui node, kawa o wataru toki wa chūi shiyō.","german":"It is easy to lose your footing in rapids, so be careful when crossing the river.","furigana":"はやせ|あし|と|かわ|わた|ちゅうい"}],"notes":"早瀬 is a shallow river spot with a fast current (瀬 = shallow place in a river). Its counterpart is 淵, the deep, calm spot; rivers alternate between 瀬 and 淵. The word is elevated to literary and appears in nature and angling texts; more soberly, people say 急流 or 流れの速い所. Related is 浅瀬 \"shoal\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"川の淵には大きな岩魚が潜んでいる。","romaji":"Kawa no fuchi ni wa ōkina iwana ga hisonde iru.","german":"Large char lurk in the deep pools of the river.","furigana":"かわ|ふち|おお||ひそ"},{"japanese":"彼は事故で生死の淵をさまよった。","romaji":"Kare wa jiko de seishi no fuchi o samayotta.","german":"He wandered on the brink between life and death after the accident.","furigana":"かれ|じこ|せいし|ふち"},{"japanese":"会社が倒産し、彼は絶望の淵に立たされた。","romaji":"Kaisha ga tōsan shi, kare wa zetsubō no fuchi ni tatasareta.","german":"When his company went bankrupt, he was left standing on the brink of despair.","furigana":"かいしゃ|とうさん|かれ|ぜつぼう|ふち|た"}],"notes":"淵 is a deep, calm spot in a river or lake, often looking dark (\"deep pool\"); its counterpart is 瀬, the shallow, fast-flowing spot. Figuratively it stands for an abyss: 絶望の淵 \"abyss of despair\", 生死の淵をさまよう \"to hover between life and death\". Do not confuse it with the identically read 縁 \"rim\" (眼鏡の縁).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"平野部を流れる河川は蛇行を繰り返す。","romaji":"Heiyabu o nagareru kasen wa dakō o kurikaesu.","german":"Rivers flowing through plains meander repeatedly.","furigana":"へいやぶ|なが|かせん|だこう|く|かえ"},{"japanese":"酒に酔った男が蛇行運転をして逮捕された。","romaji":"Sake ni yotta otoko ga dakō unten o shite taiho sareta.","german":"A drunken man was arrested for driving in a zigzag.","furigana":"さけ|よ|おとこ|だこううんてん|たいほ"},{"japanese":"偏西風が大きく蛇行しているため、今年の夏は異常に暑い。","romaji":"Henseifū ga ōkiku dakō shite iru tame, kotoshi no natsu wa ijō ni atsui.","german":"Because the westerlies are meandering greatly, this summer is unusually hot.","furigana":"へんせいふう|おお|だこう|ことし|なつ|いじょう|あつ"}],"notes":"蛇行 literally means \"snake walk\" and denotes a snake-like back and forth, for rivers \"meandering\". As an intransitive する-verb: 川が蛇行する, 道が蛇行している. In the news you often see 蛇行運転 \"weaving in a zigzag\" (e.g. when drunk) and in the weather report 偏西風の蛇行 \"meandering of the jet stream\". When a river loop is cut off, an 三日月湖 \"oxbow lake\" is formed.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"河川の蛇行が切断されて三日月湖が形成された。","romaji":"Kasen no dakō ga setsudan sarete mikazukiko ga keisei sareta.","german":"Durch das Abschneiden eines Flussmäanders entstand ein Altwassersee.","furigana":"かせん|だこう|せつだん|みかづきこ|けいせい"},{"japanese":"石狩川の流域には、たくさんの三日月湖が残っている。","romaji":"Ishikarigawa no ryūiki ni wa, takusan no mikazukiko ga nokotte iru.","german":"Im Einzugsgebiet des Ishikari gibt es viele Altwasserseen.","furigana":"いしかりがわ|りゅういき|みかづきこ|のこ"},{"japanese":"上空から見ると、三日月湖の形がはっきりわかる。","romaji":"Jōkū kara miru to, mikazukiko no katachi ga hakkiri wakaru.","german":"Aus der Luft erkennt man die Sichelform des Altarms deutlich.","furigana":"じょうくう|み|みかづきこ|かたち"}],"notes":"三日月湖 heißt wörtlich „Mondsichelsee“: Er entsteht, wenn ein mäandrierender Fluss (蛇行) eine Schleife abschneidet und der alte Bogen als sichelförmiger See zurückbleibt – auf Deutsch „Altarm“ oder „Altwassersee“. In Japan ist das Gebiet des 石狩川 in Hokkaidō dafür bekannt. Es ist ein Fachwort der Erdkunde; der Oberbegriff lautet 河跡湖.","levelNote":"Compound: 三日月 (N3) + 湖 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"河川沿いに自然堤防が形成され集落が立地した。","romaji":"Kasen zoi ni shizen teibō ga keisei sare shūraku ga ritchi shita.","german":"Natural levees formed along the river, and settlements were established there.","furigana":"かせんぞ|しぜんていぼう|けいせい|しゅうらく|りっち"},{"japanese":"自然堤防は周りより少し高いので、畑として利用されることが多い。","romaji":"Shizen teibō wa mawari yori sukoshi takai node, hatake to shite riyō sareru koto ga ōi.","german":"Natural levees are a little higher than their surroundings, so they are often used as fields.","furigana":"しぜんていぼう|まわ|すこ|たか|はたけ|りよう|おお"},{"japanese":"ハザードマップを見ると、この地区は自然堤防の上にあって浸水しにくい。","romaji":"Hazādo mappu o miru to, kono chiku wa shizen teibō no ue ni atte shinsui shinikui.","german":"According to the hazard map, this district is on a natural levee and is unlikely to be flooded.","furigana":"み|ちく|しぜんていぼう|うえ|しんすい"}],"notes":"自然堤防 is a low ridge along a river that forms when the river deposits sand on its banks during floods - a \"natural levee\". Because it lies somewhat higher and drier than its surroundings, settlements, roads and fields were traditionally laid out on it; behind it lies the lower 後背湿地. Its counterpart is the artificially built 堤防 \"embankment\". A technical word from geography and disaster prevention.","levelNote":"Compound: 自然 (N4) + 堤防 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自然堤防の背後には後背湿地が広がっている。","romaji":"Shizen teibō no haigo ni wa kōhai shitchi ga hirogatte iru.","german":"A back marsh spreads out behind the natural levee.","furigana":"しぜんていぼう|はいご|こうはいしっち|ひろ"},{"japanese":"後背湿地は水はけが悪いため、昔から水田に利用されてきた。","romaji":"Kōhai shitchi wa mizuhake ga warui tame, mukashi kara suiden ni riyō sarete kita.","german":"Because back marshes drain poorly, they have long been used as rice paddies.","furigana":"こうはいしっち|みず|わる|むかし|すいでん|りよう"},{"japanese":"後背湿地を埋め立てた住宅地は、大雨のときに浸水しやすい。","romaji":"Kōhai shitchi o umetateta jūtakuchi wa, ōame no toki ni shinsui shiyasui.","german":"Residential areas built on filled-in back marshes are prone to flooding during heavy rain.","furigana":"こうはいしっち|う|た|じゅうたくち|おおあめ|しんすい"}],"notes":"後背湿地 is the low-lying, clayey wet lowland behind the natural levee (自然堤防): floodwater that overflows the levee remains standing here. In Japan it was traditionally used as rice paddy (水田); today built-up 後背湿地 are considered especially at risk of flooding and liquefaction (液状化). It is a technical word from geography and disaster prevention.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"乾燥地域では天水に頼る農業が行われている。","romaji":"Kansō chiiki de wa tensui ni tayoru nōgyō ga okonawarete iru.","german":"In dry regions, agriculture that depends on rainwater is practiced.","furigana":"かんそうちいき|てんすい|たよ|のうぎょう|おこな"},{"japanese":"この棚田には用水路がなく、天水だけで稲を育てている。","romaji":"Kono tanada ni wa yōsuiro ga naku, tensui dake de ine o sodatete iru.","german":"These terraced rice fields have no irrigation canal and grow rice with rainwater alone.","furigana":"たなだ|ようすいろ|てんすい|いね|そだ"},{"japanese":"寺の門の前には、火事に備えた天水桶が置かれている。","romaji":"Tera no mon no mae ni wa, kaji ni sonaeta tensuioke ga okarete iru.","german":"In front of the temple gate stands a rainwater barrel prepared for fires.","furigana":"てら|もん|まえ|かじ|そな|てんすいおけ|お"}],"notes":"天水 literally means \"heaven water\" and means rainwater, especially as a water source. It is a technical word mainly in agriculture: 天水農業 \"rain-fed agriculture\", 天水田 \"rice field irrigated only by rain\". In everyday speech people say 雨水 (あまみず). A 天水桶 is a rain barrel that used to stand in front of houses and temples as a firefighting water supply.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"水文学は水循環の仕組みを解明する学問である。","romaji":"Suimongaku wa mizu junkan no shikumi o kaimei suru gakumon de aru.","german":"Hydrology is the science that clarifies the mechanism of the water cycle.","furigana":"すいもんがく|みずじゅんかん|しく|かいめい|がくもん"},{"japanese":"大学院で水文学を専攻し、洪水の予測を研究している。","romaji":"Daigakuin de suimongaku o senkō shi, kōzui no yosoku o kenkyū shite iru.","german":"I majored in hydrology in graduate school and am researching flood prediction.","furigana":"だいがくいん|すいもんがく|せんこう|こうずい|よそく|けんきゅう"},{"japanese":"水文学の観点から見ると、森林は天然のダムの役割を果たしている。","romaji":"Suimongaku no kanten kara miru to, shinrin wa tennen no damu no yakuwari o hatashite iru.","german":"From a hydrological point of view, forests play the role of natural dams.","furigana":"すいもんがく|かんてん|み|しんりん|てんねん|やくわり|は"}],"notes":"水文学 \"hydrology\" is the science of the water cycle: precipitation, runoff, evaporation and groundwater. Note the reading: 文 is read もん here (as in 天文学), not ぶん. Derived are 水文観測 and 水文データ. Not to be confused with 水理学 \"hydraulics\", the study of the flow of water in engineering.","levelNote":"Compound: 水 (N5) + 文学 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"治水事業により洪水の被害が大幅に減少した。","romaji":"Chisui jigyō ni yori kōzui no higai ga ōhaba ni genshō shita.","german":"Flood control projects greatly reduced flood damage.","furigana":"ちすいじぎょう|こうずい|ひがい|おおはば|げんしょう"},{"japanese":"武田信玄は治水に力を入れ、堤防を築いた。","romaji":"Takeda Shingen wa chisui ni chikara o ire, teibō o kizuita.","german":"Takeda Shingen put effort into flood control and had embankments built.","furigana":"たけだしんげん|ちすい|ちから|い|ていぼう|きず"},{"japanese":"温暖化で豪雨が増え、これまでの治水対策では足りなくなっている。","romaji":"Ondanka de gōu ga fue, kore made no chisui taisaku de wa tarinaku natte iru.","german":"Torrential rains are increasing because of global warming, and conventional flood control measures are no longer sufficient.","furigana":"おんだんか|ごうう|ふ|ちすいたいさく|た"}],"notes":"治水 literally means \"to tame the water\" (治 = to govern) and covers flood protection and river regulation. Typical: 治水事業, 治水対策, 治水ダム. Often in the pair 治水と利水 (flood control and water use) or as 治山治水 (protection of mountain forests and rivers). Historically 治水 was regarded as a core task of rulers - famous is the embankment 信玄堤 of Takeda Shingen.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ダムは治水と利水の両方の機能を持つ。","romaji":"Damu wa chisui to risui no ryōhō no kinō o motsu.","german":"Dams have both flood control and water utilization functions.","furigana":"ちすい|りすい|りょうほう|きのう|も"},{"japanese":"渇水で、農業用水などの利水に影響が出始めている。","romaji":"Kassui de, nōgyō yōsui nado no risui ni eikyō ga dehajimete iru.","german":"Because of the drought, water use such as agricultural water has begun to be affected.","furigana":"かっすい|のうぎょうようすい|りすい|えいきょう|ではじ"},{"japanese":"利水ダムでも、大雨の前には事前に水位を下げる運用が行われている。","romaji":"Risui damu demo, ōame no mae ni wa jizen ni suii o sageru un'yō ga okonawarete iru.","german":"Even at water-supply dams, the water level is lowered in advance before heavy rain.","furigana":"りすい|おおあめ|まえ|じぜん|すいい|さ|うんよう|おこな"}],"notes":"利水 is the use of river or reservoir water for drinking water, irrigation, industry and power generation (\"water utilization\"). It usually appears in a pair with 治水: multipurpose dams serve 治水・利水・発電. Typical are 利水ダム and 利水者 \"water user\". Not to be confused with 水利 (water supply, 水利権 \"water right\") - the same kanji in reverse order.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この地域は豊富な地下資源に恵まれている。","romaji":"Kono chiiki wa hōfu na chika shigen ni megumarete iru.","german":"This region is blessed with abundant mineral resources.","furigana":"ちいき|ほうふ|ちかしげん|めぐ"},{"japanese":"日本は地下資源に乏しく、石油のほとんどを輸入に頼っている。","romaji":"Nihon wa chika shigen ni toboshiku, sekiyu no hotondo o yunyū ni tayotte iru.","german":"Japan is poor in mineral resources and relies on imports for almost all of its oil.","furigana":"にほん|ちかしげん|とぼ|せきゆ|ゆにゅう|たよ"},{"japanese":"地下資源をめぐって、周辺国との対立が深まった。","romaji":"Chika shigen o megutte, shūhenkoku to no tairitsu ga fukamatta.","german":"Conflict with neighboring countries deepened over mineral resources.","furigana":"ちかしげん|しゅうへんこく|たいりつ|ふか"}],"notes":"地下資源 are raw materials from the subsurface such as ores, coal, oil and natural gas (\"mineral resources\"). Typical: 地下資源に恵まれる／乏しい, 地下資源を開発する. Japan is considered a 地下資源に乏しい国. 鉱物資源 means only mineral raw materials; 天然資源 is broader and includes forests, water and fish stocks.","levelNote":"Compound: 地下 (N5) + 資源 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新たな鉱床の発見が地域経済を活性化させた。","romaji":"Arata na kōshō no hakken ga chiiki keizai o kasseika saseta.","german":"The discovery of a new ore deposit revitalized the regional economy.","furigana":"あら|こうしょう|はっけん|ちいきけいざい|かっせいか"},{"japanese":"沖縄近海の海底で、新しい熱水鉱床が見つかった。","romaji":"Okinawa kinkai no kaitei de, atarashii nessui kōshō ga mitsukatta.","german":"A new hydrothermal deposit was found on the seabed near Okinawa.","furigana":"おきなわきんかい|かいてい|あたら|ねっすいこうしょう|み"},{"japanese":"この鉱床は金の含有量が高く、今も採掘が続けられている。","romaji":"Kono kōshō wa kin no gan'yūryō ga takaku, ima mo saikutsu ga tsuzukerarete iru.","german":"This ore deposit has a high gold content and is still being mined today.","furigana":"こうしょう|きん|がんゆうりょう|たか|いま|さいくつ|つづ"}],"notes":"鉱床 is a natural accumulation of useful minerals in rock that is worth mining (\"ore deposit\"). A technical word from geology and mining: 鉱床を発見する, 金の鉱床, 海底熱水鉱床 \"hydrothermal deposit on the seabed\". Related: 鉱山 \"mine\", 鉱脈 \"ore vein\". Beware of homophones such as 交渉 \"negotiation\" or 高尚 \"refined\" - context decides.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"川岸の露頭から地層の構造を観察できる。","romaji":"Kawagishi no rotō kara chisō no kōzō o kansatsu dekiru.","german":"You can observe the structure of the strata from the outcrop on the riverbank.","furigana":"かわぎし|ろとう|ちそう|こうぞう|かんさつ"},{"japanese":"この露頭では、地震で動いた断層をはっきり見ることができる。","romaji":"Kono rotō de wa, jishin de ugoita dansō o hakkiri miru koto ga dekiru.","german":"At this outcrop, you can clearly see the fault that moved in the earthquake.","furigana":"ろとう|じしん|うご|だんそう|み"},{"japanese":"道路工事で新しい露頭ができ、化石が見つかった。","romaji":"Dōro kōji de atarashii rotō ga deki, kaseki ga mitsukatta.","german":"Road construction exposed a new outcrop, and fossils were found.","furigana":"どうろこうじ|あたら|ろとう|かせき|み"}],"notes":"露頭 is a place where rock or rock layers are exposed uncovered at the surface (露 = to be exposed) - geologically an \"outcrop\". Typical: 露頭を観察する, 地層の露頭, 断層の露頭. Not to be confused with the identically read 路頭 in the phrase 路頭に迷う \"to be left standing in the street, to become destitute\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"柱状節理は溶岩の冷却によって形成される。","romaji":"Chūjō setsuri wa yōgan no reikyaku ni yotte keisei sareru.","german":"Columnar jointing is formed by the cooling of lava.","furigana":"ちゅうじょうせつり|ようがん|れいきゃく|けいせい"},{"japanese":"東尋坊の断崖では、見事な柱状節理を見ることができる。","romaji":"Tōjinbō no dangai de wa, migoto na chūjō setsuri o miru koto ga dekiru.","german":"At the cliffs of Tojinbo, you can see magnificent columnar jointing.","furigana":"とうじんぼう|だんがい|みごと|ちゅうじょうせつり|み"},{"japanese":"岩盤に節理が多いと、崖崩れが起こりやすい。","romaji":"Ganban ni setsuri ga ōi to, gakekuzure ga okoriyasui.","german":"If the bedrock has many joints, landslides are likely to occur.","furigana":"がんばん|せつり|おお|がけくず|お"}],"notes":"節理 denotes in geology regular cracks in rock along which the blocks have not shifted against each other (\"joint, jointing\"); when there is displacement, it is called 断層 \"fault\". Well known are 柱状節理 \"columnar jointing\" (e.g. at 東尋坊 or in the 玄武洞) and 板状節理. The rare meaning \"reason, logic\" (JMdict Sense 2) is not meant here.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"片麻岩は高温高圧の変成作用で形成された岩石である。","romaji":"Henmagan wa kōon kōatsu no hensei sayō de keisei sareta ganseki de aru.","german":"Gneiss is a rock formed by metamorphism under high temperature and high pressure.","furigana":"へんまがん|こうおんこうあつ|へんせいさよう|けいせい|がんせき"},{"japanese":"片麻岩は白と黒の縞模様が特徴だ。","romaji":"Henmagan wa shiro to kuro no shimamoyō ga tokuchō da.","german":"Gneiss is characterized by its white and black striped pattern.","furigana":"へんまがん|しろ|くろ|しまもよう|とくちょう"},{"japanese":"飛騨地方には、日本で最も古い岩石の一つである片麻岩が分布している。","romaji":"Hida chihō ni wa, Nihon de mottomo furui ganseki no hitotsu de aru henmagan ga bunpu shite iru.","german":"Gneiss, one of the oldest rocks in Japan, is distributed in the Hida region.","furigana":"|にほん|もっと|ふる|がんせき|ひと|へんまがん|ぶんぷ"}],"notes":"片麻岩 \"gneiss\" is a metamorphic rock with striped banding of light and dark minerals. It forms under high pressure and high temperature, often from granite (花崗岩) or sedimentary rock. Related technical words: 変成岩 \"metamorphic rock\", 片岩 \"schist\" (e.g. mica schist). In Japan the rock is rare; well known is the 飛騨片麻岩 in central Japan.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地政学的な観点から国際関係を分析する。","romaji":"Chiseigakuteki na kanten kara kokusai kankei o bunseki suru.","german":"We analyze international relations from a geopolitical perspective.","furigana":"ちせいがくてき|かんてん|こくさいかんけい|ぶんせき"},{"japanese":"地政学的リスクの高まりで、原油価格が急騰した。","romaji":"Chiseigakuteki risuku no takamari de, gen'yu kakaku ga kyūtō shita.","german":"Oil prices soared as geopolitical risks heightened.","furigana":"ちせいがくてき|たか|げんゆかかく|きゅうとう"},{"japanese":"大学で地政学の講義を取っている。","romaji":"Daigaku de chiseigaku no kōgi o totte iru.","german":"I am taking a lecture on geopolitics at university.","furigana":"だいがく|ちせいがく|こうぎ|と"}],"notes":"地政学 \"geopolitics\" studies how location and geography shape the politics and power of states. The derived 地政学的 is very common: 地政学的リスク \"geopolitical risks\" (a standard expression in business news), 地政学的な観点, 地政学上の要衝 \"strategically important place\". The word is matter-of-fact and elevated and appears mainly in the media, business and scholarship.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この地誌は地域の自然と文化を詳細に記録している。","romaji":"Kono chishi wa chiiki no shizen to bunka wo shōsai ni kiroku shite iru.","german":"This regional gazetteer records the nature and culture of the region in detail.","furigana":"ちし|ちいき|しぜん|ぶんか|しょうさい|きろく"},{"japanese":"高校の地理の授業で、アフリカの地誌を学んだ。","romaji":"Kōkō no chiri no jugyō de, Afurika no chishi o mananda.","german":"In high school geography class, we studied the regional geography of Africa.","furigana":"こうこう|ちり|じゅぎょう|ちし|まな"},{"japanese":"江戸時代には、各地の藩が地誌を編纂した。","romaji":"Edo jidai ni wa, kakuchi no han ga chishi o hensan shita.","german":"In the Edo period, domains in various places compiled regional gazetteers.","furigana":"えどじだい|かくち|はん|ちし|へんさん"}],"notes":"地誌 is the description of an area by nature, climate, population, economy and history, that is, \"regional geography\" (as a subject 地誌学, in contrast to systematic geography 系統地理). The word has nothing to do with the physical landform (地形); \"topography\" is therefore correct only in the old sense of \"description of places\". Historically, the regional descriptions compiled by domains and offices are also called 地誌; typical are 地誌を編纂する and 〜の地誌. Technical and written language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この島の植物相は独自の進化を遂げてきた。","romaji":"Kono shima no shokubutsusō wa dokuji no shinka wo togete kita.","german":"The flora of this island has undergone its own evolution.","furigana":"しま|しょくぶつそう|どくじ|しんか|と"},{"japanese":"高山の植物相を調べるため、毎年夏に調査を行っている。","romaji":"Kōzan no shokubutsusō o shiraberu tame, maitoshi natsu ni chōsa o okonatte iru.","german":"To investigate the flora of the high mountains, we conduct a survey every summer.","furigana":"こうざん|しょくぶつそう|しら|まいとしなつ|ちょうさ|おこな"},{"japanese":"外来種の侵入によって、この地域の植物相は大きく変わった。","romaji":"Gairaishu no shinnyū ni yotte, kono chiiki no shokubutsusō wa ōkiku kawatta.","german":"The flora of this region has changed greatly due to the invasion of alien species.","furigana":"がいらいしゅ|しんにゅう|ちいき|しょくぶつそう|おお|か"}],"notes":"植物相 is the totality of plant species in an area, that is, the \"flora\" in the sense of a species list: 〜の植物相を調べる, 植物相が豊か. To be distinguished from 植生 \"vegetation\", which describes the actual plant cover and its structure. The counterpart for animals is 動物相, and both together are called 生物相. For gut flora people do not say 植物相 but 腸内細菌叢 or 腸内フローラ. Technical language of biology.","levelNote":"Compound: 植物 (N3) + 相 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"オーストラリアの動物相は他の大陸と大きく異なる。","romaji":"Ōsutoraria no dōbutsusō wa hoka no tairiku to ōkiku kotonaru.","german":"The fauna of Australia differs greatly from that of other continents.","furigana":"どうぶつそう|ほか|たいりく|おお|こと"},{"japanese":"洞窟の動物相は、目が退化した生き物が多いのが特徴だ。","romaji":"Dōkutsu no dōbutsusō wa, me ga taika shita ikimono ga ōi no ga tokuchō da.","german":"The fauna of caves is characterized by many creatures with degenerated eyes.","furigana":"どうくつ|どうぶつそう|め|たいか|い|もの|おお|とくちょう"},{"japanese":"研究者たちは化石から当時の動物相を復元した。","romaji":"Kenkyūsha-tachi wa kaseki kara tōji no dōbutsusō o fukugen shita.","german":"The researchers reconstructed the fauna of that time from fossils.","furigana":"けんきゅうしゃ|かせき|とうじ|どうぶつそう|ふくげん"}],"notes":"動物相 denotes the totality of animal species of an area or era, that is, the \"fauna\": 〜の動物相, 動物相が豊富だ, 当時の動物相を復元する. The counterpart is 植物相 \"flora\"; together they are called 生物相. The word is technical language of biology and paleontology; in everyday speech people simply say そこに住む動物 or 生き物.","levelNote":"Compound: 動物 (N5) + 相 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"極相林はその地域の気候条件下で最も安定した植生である。","romaji":"Kyokusōrin wa sono chiiki no kikō jōken ka de mottomo antei shita shokusei de aru.","german":"A climax forest is the most stable vegetation under the climatic conditions of the region.","furigana":"きょくそうりん|ちいき|きこうじょうけんか|もっと|あんてい|しょくせい"},{"japanese":"遷移が進むと、森林はやがて極相に達する。","romaji":"Sen'i ga susumu to, shinrin wa yagate kyokusō ni tassuru.","german":"As succession progresses, the forest eventually reaches climax.","furigana":"せんい|すす|しんりん|きょくそう|たっ"},{"japanese":"日本の暖かい地方では、シイやカシの森が極相となる。","romaji":"Nihon no atatakai chihō de wa, shii ya kashi no mori ga kyokusō to naru.","german":"In the warm regions of Japan, forests of shii and kashi (evergreen oaks) form the climax.","furigana":"にほん|あたた|ちほう|もり|きょくそう"}],"notes":"極相 is in ecology the stable end stage of a succession, the \"climax\": 極相に達する, 極相林 \"climax forest\", 極相種. In Japan, evergreen forests of シイ and カシ form the 極相 in warm areas, and beech forests (ブナ) in cooler ones. For the high point of a story people never say 極相, but クライマックス or 山場. A purely technical term.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"溶岩台地での一次遷移は非常にゆっくりと進む。","romaji":"Yōgan daichi de no ichiji sen'i wa hijō ni yukkuri to susumu.","german":"Primary succession on lava plateaus proceeds very slowly.","furigana":"ようがんだいち|いちじせんい|ひじょう|すす"},{"japanese":"一次遷移は、地衣類やコケ類が岩の上に定着するところから始まる。","romaji":"Ichiji sen'i wa, chiirui ya kokerui ga iwa no ue ni teichaku suru tokoro kara hajimaru.","german":"Primary succession begins when lichens and mosses settle on the rock.","furigana":"いちじせんい|ちいるい|るい|いわ|うえ|ていちゃく|はじ"},{"japanese":"氷河が後退した跡地でも、一次遷移が観察されている。","romaji":"Hyōga ga kōtai shita atochi de mo, ichiji sen'i ga kansatsu sarete iru.","german":"Primary succession has also been observed on land left behind by a retreating glacier.","furigana":"ひょうが|こうたい|あとち|いちじせんい|かんさつ"}],"notes":"一次遷移 is primary succession: plants colonize a substrate without soil and without a seed bank, such as fresh lava or rock exposed by a glacier. It usually begins with lichens (地衣類) and mosses and takes very long. The counterpart is 二次遷移 on areas where soil has remained. 遷移 alone means \"succession\" in ecology; typical verbs are 一次遷移が進む／始まる／起こる. School biology and technical language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"伐採された森林での二次遷移は比較的速い。","romaji":"Bassai sareta shinrin de no niji sen'i wa hikakuteki hayai.","german":"Secondary succession in logged forests is relatively fast.","furigana":"ばっさい|しんりん|にじせんい|ひかくてきはや"},{"japanese":"山火事の跡地では、土壌が残っているのですぐに二次遷移が始まる。","romaji":"Yamakaji no atochi de wa, dojō ga nokotte iru node sugu ni niji sen'i ga hajimaru.","german":"On land burned by a wildfire, secondary succession begins right away because the soil remains.","furigana":"やまかじ|あとち|どじょう|のこ|にじせんい|はじ"},{"japanese":"耕作をやめた畑は、二次遷移によって草地から林へと変わっていく。","romaji":"Kōsaku o yameta hatake wa, niji sen'i ni yotte kusachi kara hayashi e to kawatte iku.","german":"Fields that are no longer cultivated change from grassland to woodland through secondary succession.","furigana":"こうさく|はたけ|にじせんい|くさち|はやし|か"}],"notes":"二次遷移 is secondary succession: after a forest fire, clear-cutting or on abandoned fields, soil, seeds and roots remain, so recolonization proceeds much faster than in 一次遷移. Typical sequence: grasses, then shrubs, sun-loving trees (陽樹) and finally shade-tolerant trees (陰樹). Common combinations are 二次遷移が始まる／進む and 二次遷移によって〜になる. Technical language of ecology.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先駆種は荒れ地に最初に定着する植物である。","romaji":"Senkushu wa arechi ni saisho ni teichaku suru shokubutsu de aru.","german":"Pioneer species are the plants that first settle on wasteland.","furigana":"せんくしゅ|あ|ち|さいしょ|ていちゃく|しょくぶつ"},{"japanese":"噴火のあと、イタドリなどの先駆種がいち早く生えてきた。","romaji":"Funka no ato, itadori nado no senkushu ga ichihayaku haete kita.","german":"After the eruption, pioneer species such as Japanese knotweed were the first to sprout.","furigana":"ふんか|せんくしゅ|はや|は"},{"japanese":"先駆種は成長が速いが、寿命が短いものが多い。","romaji":"Senkushu wa seichō ga hayai ga, jumyō ga mijikai mono ga ōi.","german":"Pioneer species grow quickly, but many of them have short lifespans.","furigana":"せんくしゅ|せいちょう|はや|じゅみょう|みじか|おお"}],"notes":"先駆種 are pioneer species that are the first to colonize open or disturbed areas, e.g. ススキ, イタドリ or アカマツ. They grow fast, need a lot of light and are often short-lived, so they are displaced over the course of succession. Plants are mostly meant; in addition, パイオニア種 and パイオニア植物 are in use. Typical: 先駆種が侵入する／定着する. Technical language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"陽樹は十分な日光を必要とする樹種である。","romaji":"Yōju wa jūbun na nikkō wo hitsuyō to suru jushu de aru.","german":"Sun trees are tree species that need plenty of sunlight.","furigana":"ようじゅ|じゅうぶん|にっこう|ひつよう|じゅしゅ"},{"japanese":"アカマツやシラカバは、代表的な陽樹だ。","romaji":"Akamatsu ya shirakaba wa, daihyōteki na yōju da.","german":"Japanese red pine and white birch are typical sun trees.","furigana":"だいひょうてき|ようじゅ"},{"japanese":"森が育って林床が暗くなると、陽樹の芽生えは育たなくなる。","romaji":"Mori ga sodatte rinshō ga kuraku naru to, yōju no mebae wa sodatanaku naru.","german":"When the forest matures and the forest floor becomes dark, the seedlings of sun trees no longer grow.","furigana":"もり|そだ|りんしょう|くら|ようじゅ|めば|そだ"}],"notes":"陽樹 are shade-intolerant (light-demanding) tree species: their seedlings need full sunlight and hardly grow in shade, but they grow fast on open ground. Typical examples in Japan are アカマツ, シラカバ and コナラ. Over the course of succession they are replaced by 陰樹 \"shade-tolerant trees\", whose seedlings survive in the dark forest. A term of school biology and forestry.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"陰樹は日陰でも生育できる耐陰性の高い樹木である。","romaji":"Inju wa hikage de mo seiiku dekiru taiinsei no takai jumoku de aru.","german":"Shade-tolerant trees are trees with high shade tolerance that can grow even in shade.","furigana":"いんじゅ|ひかげ|せいいく|たいいんせい|たか|じゅもく"},{"japanese":"遷移の後半になると、陽樹に代わって陰樹が森の主役になる。","romaji":"Sen'i no kōhan ni naru to, yōju ni kawatte inju ga mori no shuyaku ni naru.","german":"In the later stage of succession, shade-tolerant trees take over from sun trees as the main players of the forest.","furigana":"せんい|こうはん|ようじゅ|か|いんじゅ|もり|しゅやく"},{"japanese":"ブナやシイは、日本の代表的な陰樹である。","romaji":"Buna ya shii wa, Nihon no daihyōteki na inju de aru.","german":"Beech and shii are typical shade-tolerant trees of Japan.","furigana":"にほん|だいひょうてき|いんじゅ"}],"notes":"陰樹 are shade-tolerant tree species whose seedlings can grow even in the partial shade under the canopy, e.g. ブナ, シイ, カシ. For this reason they replace the 陽樹 over the course of succession and form the climax forest (極相林). Careful: the English \"shade tree\" in the sense of \"a tree that provides shade\" is not meant; that would be 緑陰樹 or simply 日陰を作る木. A technical term from biology and forestry.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"熱帯雨林の林冠は多様な生物の生息場所となっている。","romaji":"Nettai urin no rinkan wa tayō na seibutsu no seisoku basho to natte iru.","german":"The canopy of the tropical rainforest is a habitat for diverse organisms.","furigana":"ねったいうりん|りんかん|たよう|せいぶつ|せいそくばしょ"},{"japanese":"大木が倒れて林冠にすき間ができると、林床まで光が届く。","romaji":"Taiboku ga taorete rinkan ni sukima ga dekiru to, rinshō made hikari ga todoku.","german":"When a large tree falls and a gap opens in the canopy, light reaches the forest floor.","furigana":"たいぼく|たお|りんかん|ま|りんしょう|ひかり|とど"},{"japanese":"研究者たちは、クレーンを使って林冠の調査を行った。","romaji":"Kenkyūsha-tachi wa, kurēn o tsukatte rinkan no chōsa o okonatta.","german":"The researchers surveyed the canopy using a crane.","furigana":"けんきゅうしゃ|つか|りんかん|ちょうさ|おこな"}],"notes":"林冠 is the \"canopy\" of a forest, that is, the closed layer of tree crowns. The opposite term is 林床 \"forest floor\". Typical combinations: 林冠が閉じる \"the canopy closes\", 林冠ギャップ for a gap after a tree fall, 林冠を形成する. Technical language of ecology and forestry.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"林縁部は日光が差し込み多様な植物が生育する。","romaji":"Rin'enbu wa nikkō ga sashikomi tayō na shokubutsu ga seiiku suru.","german":"Sunlight enters the forest edge, and diverse plants grow there.","furigana":"りんえんぶ|にっこう|さ|こ|たよう|しょくぶつ|せいいく"},{"japanese":"林縁には、ノウサギやキツネなど多くの動物が姿を見せる。","romaji":"Rin'en ni wa, nōsagi ya kitsune nado ōku no dōbutsu ga sugata o miseru.","german":"Many animals such as hares and foxes show themselves at the forest edge.","furigana":"りんえん|おお|どうぶつ|すがた|み"},{"japanese":"道路の建設で新たな林縁ができ、森の内部まで乾燥が進んだ。","romaji":"Dōro no kensetsu de arata na rin'en ga deki, mori no naibu made kansō ga susunda.","german":"A new forest edge was created by road construction, and drying progressed even into the interior of the forest.","furigana":"どうろ|けんせつ|あら|りんえん|もり|ないぶ|かんそう|すす"}],"notes":"林縁 is the forest edge as an ecological transition zone between forest and open land; often 林縁部. It is brighter and richer in species there than in the forest interior, and typical belts of shrubs and climbers form (マント群落). The word is technical; in everyday speech people say 森のはずれ or 林の端.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"乾燥地域では疎林が広がっている。","romaji":"Kansō chiiki de wa sorin ga hirogatte iru.","german":"Sparse forests spread across dry regions.","furigana":"かんそうちいき|そりん|ひろ"},{"japanese":"サバンナでは、草原の中に木がまばらに生える疎林も見られる。","romaji":"Sabanna de wa, sōgen no naka ni ki ga mabara ni haeru sorin mo mirareru.","german":"In the savanna, you can also see open woodland where trees grow sparsely in the grassland.","furigana":"そうげん|なか|き|は|そりん|み"},{"japanese":"山火事のあと、その斜面は疎林になってしまった。","romaji":"Yamakaji no ato, sono shamen wa sorin ni natte shimatta.","german":"After the wildfire, that slope was reduced to sparse woodland.","furigana":"やまかじ|しゃめん|そりん"}],"notes":"疎林 is a light forest in which the trees stand far apart so that a lot of light falls on the ground; the opposite is 密林. It is found in dry regions, savannas or after fires and logging. The word is literary and technical; colloquially people say 木がまばらな林.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"アマゾンの密林には未知の生物が数多く生息している。","romaji":"Amazon no mitsurin ni wa michi no seibutsu ga kazuōku seisoku shite iru.","german":"Numerous unknown creatures live in the Amazon jungle.","furigana":"みつりん|みち|せいぶつ|かずおお|せいそく"},{"japanese":"探検隊は、密林の中を何日も歩き続けた。","romaji":"Tankentai wa, mitsurin no naka o nannichi mo arukitsuzuketa.","german":"The expedition kept walking through the jungle for days.","furigana":"たんけんたい|みつりん|なか|なんにち|ある|つづ"},{"japanese":"ネットでは、通販サイトのアマゾンを冗談で「密林」と呼ぶことがある。","romaji":"Netto de wa, tsūhan saito no Amazon o jōdan de \"mitsurin\" to yobu koto ga aru.","german":"On the internet, the online shopping site Amazon is sometimes jokingly called \"mitsurin\".","furigana":"つうはん|じょうだん|みつりん|よ"}],"notes":"密林 is a dense, hardly penetrable forest, especially the tropical jungle: アマゾンの密林, 密林に分け入る. The opposite is 疎林. In everyday speech ジャングル is more common; 密林 sounds more literary and adventurous. In internet slang, 密林 is also a joking name for the online retailer Amazon.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"北海道には広大な原野が広がっている。","romaji":"Hokkaidō ni wa kōdai na gen'ya ga hirogatte iru.","german":"A vast wilderness spreads across Hokkaido.","furigana":"ほっかいどう|こうだい|げんや|ひろ"},{"japanese":"開拓者たちは原野を切り開いて畑を作った。","romaji":"Kaitakusha-tachi wa gen'ya o kirihiraite hatake o tsukutta.","german":"The settlers cleared the wilderness and made fields.","furigana":"かいたくしゃ|げんや|き|ひら|はたけ|つく"},{"japanese":"価値のない土地を高く売りつける「原野商法」に注意してください。","romaji":"Kachi no nai tochi o takaku uritsukeru \"gen'ya shōhō\" ni chūi shite kudasai.","german":"Beware of the \"wasteland scam\" in which worthless land is sold at a high price.","furigana":"かち|とち|たか|う|げんやしょうほう|ちゅうい"}],"notes":"原野 is wide, undeveloped and uncultivated land, mostly grassland, heath or moor, not dense forest: 広大な原野, 原野を切り開く \"to clear wilderness\". 荒野, by contrast, emphasizes desolation and dryness, and 荒れ地 fallow land. Also well known is 原野商法, the fraudulent sale of worthless plots in the wilderness. Literary, but common.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"泥炭地は二酸化炭素の巨大な貯蔵庫である。","romaji":"Deitanchi wa nisanka tanso no kyodai na chozōko de aru.","german":"Peatlands are huge reservoirs of carbon dioxide.","furigana":"でいたんち|にさんかたんそ|きょだい|ちょぞうこ"},{"japanese":"スコッチウイスキーの製造では、泥炭を燃やして麦芽を乾燥させる。","romaji":"Sukocchi uisukī no seizō de wa, deitan o moyashite bakuga o kansō saseru.","german":"In the production of Scotch whisky, peat is burned to dry the malt.","furigana":"せいぞう|でいたん|も|ばくが|かんそう"},{"japanese":"昔は泥炭を掘り出して、燃料として使っていた。","romaji":"Mukashi wa deitan o horidashite, nenryō to shite tsukatte ita.","german":"In the past, peat was dug out and used as fuel.","furigana":"むかし|でいたん|ほ|だ|ねんりょう|つか"}],"notes":"泥炭 is \"peat\", incompletely decomposed plant material from bogs; the peat bog is called 泥炭地. In connection with whisky and garden soil, the loanword ピート is usual (ピート香, ピートモス). The word element 炭 recalls that peat is a precursor of coal and served as fuel. Technical and neutral.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"マングローブ林は沿岸の生態系を保護する役割がある。","romaji":"Mangurōbu rin wa engan no seitaikei wo hogo suru yakuwari ga aru.","german":"Mangrove forests play a role in protecting coastal ecosystems.","furigana":"りん|えんがん|せいたいけい|ほご|やくわり"},{"japanese":"西表島では、カヌーでマングローブの中を進むツアーが人気だ。","romaji":"Iriomotejima de wa, kanū de mangurōbu no naka o susumu tsuā ga ninki da.","german":"On Iriomote Island, tours paddling a canoe through the mangroves are popular.","furigana":"|なか|すす|にんき"},{"japanese":"エビの養殖池を作るために、多くのマングローブが伐採された。","romaji":"Ebi no yōshokuike o tsukuru tame ni, ōku no mangurōbu ga bassai sareta.","german":"Many mangroves were cut down to make shrimp farming ponds.","furigana":"ようしょくいけ|つく|おお|ばっさい"}],"notes":"マングローブ is not a single tree species but the collective name for salt-tolerant woody plants in the tidal zone of tropical and subtropical coasts; the forest is called マングローブ林. In Japan it grows in Okinawa and Amami, where the trees are called ヒルギ. It protects coasts from waves and serves fish as a nursery. Common: マングローブを植林する／伐採する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"藻場は海洋生物の産卵場所として重要である。","romaji":"Moba wa kaiyō seibutsu no sanran basho toshite jūyō de aru.","german":"Seaweed beds are important as spawning grounds for marine life.","furigana":"もば|かいようせいぶつ|さんらんばしょ|じゅうよう"},{"japanese":"藻場を再生するため、漁師たちが海藻を植え付ける活動を続けている。","romaji":"Moba o saisei suru tame, ryōshi-tachi ga kaisō o uetsukeru katsudō o tsuzukete iru.","german":"Fishermen have been continuing to plant seaweed to restore the seaweed beds.","furigana":"もば|さいせい|りょうし|かいそう|う|つ|かつどう|つづ"},{"japanese":"埋め立てによって、湾内の藻場の多くが失われた。","romaji":"Umetate ni yotte, wannai no moba no ōku ga ushinawareta.","german":"Reclamation has destroyed many of the seaweed beds in the bay.","furigana":"う|た|わんない|もば|おお|うしな"}],"notes":"藻場 are stands of seaweed or seagrass in shallow coastal water, such as アマモ場 (eelgrass meadow) or kelp forests of ホンダワラ, アラメ and カジメ. They are regarded as the \"nursery of the sea\" (海のゆりかご), because fish spawn and young fish grow up there. Their disappearance is called 磯焼け. Typical: 藻場を再生する／保全する, 藻場が失われる. Technical language of fisheries and marine ecology.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"海水温の上昇が磯焼けの原因とされている。","romaji":"Kaisuion no jōshō ga isoyake no gen'in to sarete iru.","german":"Rising seawater temperature is thought to be the cause of the loss of seaweed beds.","furigana":"かいすいおん|じょうしょう|いそや|げんいん"},{"japanese":"ウニが海藻を食べ尽くし、磯焼けが広がっている。","romaji":"Uni ga kaisō o tabetsukushi, isoyake ga hirogatte iru.","german":"Sea urchins have eaten up the seaweed, and the barren coast is spreading.","furigana":"かいそう|た|つ|いそや|ひろ"},{"japanese":"磯焼けのせいで、アワビやサザエがほとんど獲れなくなった。","romaji":"Isoyake no sei de, awabi ya sazae ga hotondo torenaku natta.","german":"Because of the loss of seaweed, hardly any abalone or turban shells can be caught anymore.","furigana":"いそや|"}],"notes":"磯焼け literally means \"burning of the rocky shore\": the kelp forests (藻場) on rocky coasts disappear, leaving behind bare rock with a whitish crust. Causes are rising water temperatures and grazing by sea urchins (ウニ) or fish such as アイゴ. For coastal fisheries this is a serious problem, because abalone and turban shells lack food. Typical: 磯焼けが起きる／広がる／進む. Technical language, but common in the news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"サンゴの白化現象が世界各地で報告されている。","romaji":"Sango no hakka genshō ga sekai kakuchi de hōkoku sarete iru.","german":"Coral bleaching has been reported in various parts of the world.","furigana":"はっかげんしょう|せかいかくち|ほうこく"},{"japanese":"高い海水温が続くと、サンゴの白化現象が起きやすい。","romaji":"Takai kaisuion ga tsuzuku to, sango no hakka genshō ga okiyasui.","german":"When high seawater temperatures persist, coral bleaching is likely to occur.","furigana":"たか|かいすいおん|つづ|はっかげんしょう|お"},{"japanese":"潜ってみると、白化現象で真っ白になったサンゴが広がっていた。","romaji":"Mogutte miru to, hakka genshō de masshiro ni natta sango ga hirogatte ita.","german":"When I dived, vast stretches of corals turned pure white by bleaching spread out before me.","furigana":"もぐ|はっかげんしょう|ま|しろ|ひろ"}],"notes":"白化現象 is a general term for turning white when pigment is lost, but it nearly always refers to coral bleaching, as in サンゴの白化現象. When the water temperature rises, corals expel their symbiotic algae and turn white; if this continues, they die. As a verb, people say サンゴが白化する. Less often it stands for albinism in animals or chlorosis in plants. Technical language, common in environmental news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"過放牧による草地の荒廃が進んでいる。","romaji":"Kahōboku ni yoru sōchi no kōhai ga susunde iru.","german":"Degradation of grassland due to overgrazing is progressing.","furigana":"かほうぼく|そうち|こうはい|すす"},{"japanese":"モンゴルでは過放牧が原因で砂漠化が広がっている。","romaji":"Mongoru de wa kahōboku ga gen'in de sabakuka ga hirogatte iru.","german":"In Mongolia, desertification is spreading because of overgrazing.","furigana":"かほうぼく|げんいん|さばくか|ひろ"},{"japanese":"過放牧を防ぐため、家畜の数を制限している地域もある。","romaji":"Kahōboku o fusegu tame, kachiku no kazu o seigen shite iru chiiki mo aru.","german":"Some regions limit the number of livestock to prevent overgrazing.","furigana":"かほうぼく|ふせ|かちく|かず|せいげん|ちいき"}],"notes":"過放牧 is composed of 過 \"too much\" and 放牧 \"grazing of livestock\": more animals graze than the vegetation can bear. It is typical in environmental texts on desertification, e.g. 過放牧による砂漠化, often in a series with 過耕作 \"overuse of farmland\" and 森林伐採 \"deforestation\". Grammatically it almost always stands as a noun with による or が原因で; the する-form is rare. Technical language of ecology and geography.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"灌漑による塩害が農地の荒廃を引き起こしている。","romaji":"Kangai ni yoru engai ga nōchi no kōhai o hikiokoshite iru.","german":"Salt damage from irrigation is causing farmland to deteriorate.","furigana":"かんがい|えんがい|のうち|こうはい|ひ|お"},{"japanese":"台風の後、海沿いの田んぼが塩害を受けた。","romaji":"Taifū no ato, umizoi no tanbo ga engai o uketa.","german":"After the typhoon, the rice fields along the coast suffered salt damage.","furigana":"たいふう|あと|うみぞ|た|えんがい|う"},{"japanese":"海の近くに住むなら、車の塩害対策が欠かせない。","romaji":"Umi no chikaku ni sumu nara, kuruma no engai taisaku ga kakasenai.","german":"If you live near the sea, protecting your car against salt damage is essential.","furigana":"うみ|ちか|す|くるま|えんがいたいさく|か"}],"notes":"塩害 is damage caused by salt: either by seawater and salty sea air, for example to crops, power lines or cars after a typhoon, or by soil salinization through improper irrigation. Typical combinations are 塩害を受ける \"to suffer salt damage\", 塩害対策 \"protection against salt damage\" and 塩害による停電. Formed like 水害 \"flood damage\" and 冷害 \"cold damage\". Neutral, from agriculture, engineering and the news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"同じ作物を続けて栽培すると連作障害が発生する。","romaji":"Onaji sakumotsu wo tsuzukete saibai suru to rensaku shōgai ga hassei suru.","german":"Continuous-cropping injury occurs when the same crop is grown repeatedly.","furigana":"おな|さくもつ|つづ|さいばい|れんさくしょうがい|はっせい"},{"japanese":"トマトやナスは連作障害が出やすいので、毎年植える場所を変えている。","romaji":"Tomato ya nasu wa rensaku shōgai ga deyasui node, maitoshi ueru basho o kaete iru.","german":"Tomatoes and eggplants are prone to continuous-cropping injury, so I change where I plant them every year.","furigana":"れんさくしょうがい|で|まいとしう|ばしょ|か"},{"japanese":"輪作は、連作障害を防ぐための基本的な方法だ。","romaji":"Rinsaku wa, rensaku shōgai o fusegu tame no kihonteki na hōhō da.","german":"Crop rotation is the basic method for preventing continuous-cropping injury.","furigana":"りんさく|れんさくしょうがい|ふせ|きほんてき|ほうほう"}],"notes":"連作障害 are the yield losses and diseases that occur when the same crop is grown year after year on the same plot (連作); causes are one-sided nutrient depletion and soil pests. The remedy is 輪作 \"crop rotation\". Especially susceptible are nightshades such as トマト, ナス and ジャガイモ. Typical: 連作障害が出る／起きる, 連作障害を防ぐ／避ける. Also common among hobby gardeners.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"水質の評価に生物指標が用いられる。","romaji":"Suishitsu no hyōka ni seibutsu shihyō ga mochiirareru.","german":"Bioindicators are used to evaluate water quality.","furigana":"すいしつ|ひょうか|せいぶつしひょう|もち"},{"japanese":"カワゲラの幼虫は、きれいな川の生物指標として知られている。","romaji":"Kawagera no yōchū wa, kirei na kawa no seibutsu shihyō to shite shirarete iru.","german":"Stonefly larvae are known as a bioindicator of clean rivers.","furigana":"ようちゅう|かわ|せいぶつしひょう|し"},{"japanese":"地衣類を生物指標にして、都市の大気汚染を調べた。","romaji":"Chiirui o seibutsu shihyō ni shite, toshi no taiki osen o shirabeta.","german":"Using lichens as a bioindicator, we investigated air pollution in the city.","furigana":"ちいるい|せいぶつしひょう|とし|たいきおせん|しら"}],"notes":"生物指標 denotes organisms whose presence or condition allows conclusions about the environment, for example insect larvae for water quality or lichens for air purity. Synonymous and at least as common is 指標生物; typical frames are 〜を生物指標とする and 〜の生物指標として用いる. The second element 指標 \"indicator, index\" also occurs in 経済指標 \"economic indicator\". Purely technical and written language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大規模開発には環境アセスメントの実施が求められる。","romaji":"Daikibo kaihatsu ni wa kankyō asesumento no jisshi ga motomerareru.","german":"An environmental impact assessment is required for large-scale development.","furigana":"だいきぼかいはつ|かんきょう|じっし|もと"},{"japanese":"風力発電所の建設計画について、環境アセスメントの手続きが始まった。","romaji":"Fūryoku hatsudensho no kensetsu keikaku ni tsuite, kankyō asesumento no tetsuzuki ga hajimatta.","german":"Environmental assessment procedures have begun for the planned construction of the wind power plant.","furigana":"ふうりょくはつでんしょ|けんせつけいかく|かんきょう|てつづ|はじ"},{"japanese":"住民は、環境アセスメントの内容が不十分だと反発している。","romaji":"Jūmin wa, kankyō asesumento no naiyō ga fujūbun da to hanpatsu shite iru.","german":"Residents are protesting that the content of the environmental impact assessment is inadequate.","furigana":"じゅうみん|かんきょう|ないよう|ふじゅうぶん|はんぱつ"}],"notes":"環境アセスメント is the environmental impact assessment before large projects such as power plants, roads or airports. The official term is 環境影響評価 (the law: 環境影響評価法); the media also abbreviate it to 環境アセス. Typical combinations: 環境アセスメントを実施する／行う, 環境アセスメントの手続き, 環境アセスメントの対象になる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"排出規制の強化が産業界に大きな影響を与えている。","romaji":"Haishutsu kisei no kyōka ga sangyōkai ni ōkina eikyō wo ataete iru.","german":"Tighter emission regulations are having a big impact on industry.","furigana":"はいしゅつきせい|きょうか|さんぎょうかい|おお|えいきょう|あた"},{"japanese":"自動車の排出規制が厳しくなり、ディーゼル車の販売が減った。","romaji":"Jidōsha no haishutsu kisei ga kibishiku nari, dīzerusha no hanbai ga hetta.","german":"Emission regulations for automobiles became stricter, and sales of diesel vehicles fell.","furigana":"じどうしゃ|はいしゅつきせい|きび|しゃ|はんばい|へ"},{"japanese":"政府は、工場に対する排出規制を緩和する方針を示した。","romaji":"Seifu wa, kōjō ni taisuru haishutsu kisei o kanwa suru hōshin o shimeshita.","german":"The government indicated a policy of easing emission regulations for factories.","furigana":"せいふ|こうじょう|たい|はいしゅつきせい|かんわ|ほうしん|しめ"}],"notes":"排出規制 are legal limits on the output of exhaust gases, pollutants or greenhouse gases. Typical: 排出規制を強化する／緩和する, 排出規制が厳しくなる, 排出規制をクリアする. Concrete limit values are called 排出基準; for car exhaust, 排ガス規制 is common in colloquial speech. Language of administration, business and the news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"荒廃した湿地の環境再生プロジェクトが進行中である。","romaji":"Kōhai shita shitchi no kankyō saisei purojekuto ga shinkōchū de aru.","german":"An environmental restoration project for the degraded wetland is under way.","furigana":"こうはい|しっち|かんきょうさいせい|しんこうちゅう"},{"japanese":"水俣市は、公害の経験を生かして環境再生のまちづくりを進めてきた。","romaji":"Minamata-shi wa, kōgai no keiken o ikashite kankyō saisei no machizukuri o susumete kita.","german":"Minamata City has drawn on its experience of pollution to pursue community building centered on environmental regeneration.","furigana":"|こうがい|けいけん|い|かんきょうさいせい|すす"},{"japanese":"市民ボランティアが、川の環境再生に取り組んでいる。","romaji":"Shimin borantia ga, kawa no kankyō saisei ni torikunde iru.","german":"Citizen volunteers are working on restoring the environment of the river.","furigana":"しみん|かわ|かんきょうさいせい|と|く"}],"notes":"環境再生 means the restoration of a damaged environment, for example polluted rivers, destroyed wetlands or former industrial areas. Typical: 環境再生に取り組む, 環境再生事業, 環境再生プロジェクト. Close terms are 自然再生 (more about ecosystems) and 環境保全 \"environmental protection\", which preserves what exists instead of restoring what was destroyed. Language of officialdom and the media.","levelNote":"Compound: 環境 (N4) + 再生 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"航空会社がカーボンオフセットの仕組みを導入した。","romaji":"Kōkū gaisha ga kābon ofusetto no shikumi wo dōnyū shita.","german":"The airline introduced a carbon offset scheme.","furigana":"こうくうがいしゃ|しく|どうにゅう"},{"japanese":"植林への寄付で、出張で出たCO2をカーボンオフセットする企業が増えている。","romaji":"Shokurin e no kifu de, shutchō de deta CO2 o kābon ofusetto suru kigyō ga fuete iru.","german":"More and more companies are carbon-offsetting the CO2 from business trips through donations to tree planting.","furigana":"しょくりん|きふ|しゅっちょう|で|きぎょう|ふ"},{"japanese":"カーボンオフセットに頼る前に、まず排出を減らすべきだという意見もある。","romaji":"Kābon ofusetto ni tayoru mae ni, mazu haishutsu o herasu beki da to iu iken mo aru.","german":"Some argue that we should first reduce emissions before relying on carbon offsets.","furigana":"たよ|まえ|はいしゅつ|へ|いけん"}],"notes":"カーボンオフセット means compensating for unavoidable CO2 emissions through climate protection projects such as reforestation or the purchase of certificates (カーボンクレジット). Related is カーボンニュートラル \"carbon neutral\", the goal of the offsetting. People say カーボンオフセットを導入する, occasionally also as a する-verb: 排出量をカーボンオフセットする. Language of business and the media.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"都市計画にグリーンインフラの概念が取り入れられている。","romaji":"Toshi keikaku ni gurīn infura no gainen ga toriirerarete iru.","german":"The concept of green infrastructure is being incorporated into urban planning.","furigana":"としけいかく|がいねん|と|い"},{"japanese":"雨水を地面にしみ込ませる公園は、洪水対策のグリーンインフラとして注目されている。","romaji":"Usui o jimen ni shimikomaseru kōen wa, kōzui taisaku no gurīn infura to shite chūmoku sarete iru.","german":"Parks that let rainwater soak into the ground are attracting attention as green infrastructure for flood control.","furigana":"うすい|じめん|こ|こうえん|こうずいたいさく|ちゅうもく"},{"japanese":"コンクリートの堤防だけでなく、湿地を生かしたグリーンインフラも組み合わせるべきだ。","romaji":"Konkurīto no teibō dake de naku, shitchi o ikashita gurīn infura mo kumiawaseru beki da.","german":"We should combine not only concrete embankments but also green infrastructure that makes use of wetlands.","furigana":"ていぼう|しっち|い|く|あ"}],"notes":"グリーンインフラ is the short form of グリーンインフラストラクチャー: nature and green spaces are deliberately used as infrastructure, e.g. parks and wetlands as rainwater retention and street trees against heat. The opposite term is グレーインフラ, classic concrete infrastructure such as embankments and canals. Typical: グリーンインフラを導入する／活用する. Language of administration and planning.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"森林の生態系機能には水源涵養や土砂災害防止が含まれる。","romaji":"Shinrin no seitaikei kinō ni wa suigen kan'yō ya dosha saigai bōshi ga fukumareru.","german":"The ecosystem functions of forests include water source conservation and prevention of landslide disasters.","furigana":"しんりん|せいたいけいきのう|すいげんかんよう|どしゃさいがいぼうし|ふく"},{"japanese":"外来種の侵入は、在来の生態系機能を損なうおそれがある。","romaji":"Gairaishu no shinnyū wa, zairai no seitaikei kinō o sokonau osore ga aru.","german":"The invasion of alien species may impair native ecosystem functions.","furigana":"がいらいしゅ|しんにゅう|ざいらい|せいたいけいきのう|そこ"},{"japanese":"研究チームは湿地の生態系機能を評価し、その経済的価値を試算した。","romaji":"Kenkyū chīmu wa shitchi no seitaikei kinō o hyōka shi, sono keizaiteki kachi o shisan shita.","german":"The research team evaluated the ecosystem functions of the wetland and estimated their economic value.","furigana":"けんきゅう|しっち|せいたいけいきのう|ひょうか|けいざいてきかち|しさん"}],"notes":"生態系機能 are the processes that an ecosystem performs itself, such as nutrient cycles, water retention, soil formation or pollination. Closely related is 生態系サービス \"ecosystem services\", which emphasizes the benefit of these functions to humans. Typical: 生態系機能を維持する／損なう／評価する. Technical language of ecology and environmental policy.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"底生生物の調査が水質評価に利用される。","romaji":"Teisei seibutsu no chōsa ga suishitsu hyōka ni riyō sareru.","german":"Surveys of benthic organisms are used for water quality assessment.","furigana":"ていせいせいぶつ|ちょうさ|すいしつひょうか|りよう"},{"japanese":"干潟の泥の中には、ゴカイや貝などの底生生物がたくさんすんでいる。","romaji":"Higata no doro no naka ni wa, gokai ya kai nado no teisei seibutsu ga takusan sunde iru.","german":"Many benthic organisms such as polychaete worms and shellfish live in the mud of the tidal flats.","furigana":"ひがた|どろ|なか|かい|ていせいせいぶつ"},{"japanese":"底引き網漁は、海底の底生生物に大きなダメージを与える。","romaji":"Sokobikiami ryō wa, kaitei no teisei seibutsu ni ōkina damēji o ataeru.","german":"Bottom trawling does great damage to the benthic organisms on the seabed.","furigana":"そこび|あみりょう|かいてい|ていせいせいぶつ|おお|あた"}],"notes":"底生生物 are organisms that live on or in the bottom of seas, lakes and rivers, that is, the \"benthos\": shellfish, worms (ゴカイ), crustaceans or aquatic insect larvae. The loanword ベントス is a synonym. It is one of the three ecological groups of aquatic organisms, alongside 浮遊生物 \"plankton\" and 遊泳生物 \"nekton\". Because they are sedentary, they serve as indicators of water quality. Technical language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"浮遊生物は海洋食物連鎖の基盤となっている。","romaji":"Fuyū seibutsu wa kaiyō shokumotsu rensa no kiban to natte iru.","german":"Plankton forms the basis of the marine food chain.","furigana":"ふゆうせいぶつ|かいようしょくもつれんさ|きばん"},{"japanese":"浮遊生物は、水の流れに逆らって泳ぐことができない。","romaji":"Fuyū seibutsu wa, mizu no nagare ni sakaratte oyogu koto ga dekinai.","german":"Plankton cannot swim against the flow of water.","furigana":"ふゆうせいぶつ|みず|なが|さか|およ"},{"japanese":"網で海水をすくい、顕微鏡で浮遊生物を観察した。","romaji":"Ami de kaisui o sukui, kenbikyō de fuyū seibutsu o kansatsu shita.","german":"We scooped up seawater with a net and observed the plankton under a microscope.","furigana":"あみ|かいすい|けんびきょう|ふゆうせいぶつ|かんさつ"}],"notes":"浮遊生物 is the Sino-Japanese technical term for \"plankton\": organisms that drift in the water and cannot swim against the current. In everyday speech, and even in many specialist texts, プランクトン is much more common (植物プランクトン, 動物プランクトン). The opposites are 遊泳生物 \"nekton\" and 底生生物 \"benthos\". 浮遊 alone means \"to float, to drift\", as in 浮遊物.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"魚やイルカは遊泳生物に分類される。","romaji":"Sakana ya iruka wa yūei seibutsu ni bunrui sareru.","german":"Fish and dolphins are classified as nekton.","furigana":"さかな|ゆうえいせいぶつ|ぶんるい"},{"japanese":"遊泳生物は、自分の力で海流に逆らって移動できる。","romaji":"Yūei seibutsu wa, jibun no chikara de kairyū ni sakaratte idō dekiru.","german":"Nekton can move against ocean currents under their own power.","furigana":"ゆうえいせいぶつ|じぶん|ちから|かいりゅう|さか|いどう"},{"japanese":"クラゲは泳ぐが、流れに逆らえないため遊泳生物ではなく浮遊生物に入る。","romaji":"Kurage wa oyogu ga, nagare ni sakaraenai tame yūei seibutsu de wa naku fuyū seibutsu ni hairu.","german":"Jellyfish swim, but they cannot go against the current, so they are classed as plankton rather than nekton.","furigana":"およ|なが|さか|ゆうえいせいぶつ|ふゆうせいぶつ|はい"}],"notes":"遊泳生物 are aquatic animals that can swim against currents under their own power, that is, the \"nekton\": fish, squid, whales, seals. ネクトン is a synonym. What matters is swimming strength, not swimming as such: jellyfish do swim but count as 浮遊生物. The third group is 底生生物 \"benthos\". Technical language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"海洋への栄養塩の流入が赤潮を引き起こすことがある。","romaji":"Kaiyō e no eiyōen no ryūnyū ga akashio wo hikiokosu koto ga aru.","german":"The inflow of nutrient salts into the ocean can cause red tides.","furigana":"かいよう|えいようえん|りゅうにゅう|あかしお|ひ|お"},{"japanese":"冬の海では上下の水がよく混ざり、表層に栄養塩が供給される。","romaji":"Fuyu no umi de wa jōge no mizu ga yoku mazari, hyōsō ni eiyōen ga kyōkyū sareru.","german":"In the winter sea, the upper and lower water mix well, and nutrient salts are supplied to the surface layer.","furigana":"ふゆ|うみ|じょうげ|みず|ま|ひょうそう|えいようえん|きょうきゅう"},{"japanese":"下水処理が進んで栄養塩が減りすぎ、ノリの色落ちが問題になっている。","romaji":"Gesui shori ga susunde eiyōen ga herisugi, nori no iroochi ga mondai ni natte iru.","german":"Sewage treatment has advanced so far that nutrient salts have decreased too much, and the fading of the color of nori seaweed has become a problem.","furigana":"げすいしょり|すす|えいようえん|へ|いろお|もんだい"}],"notes":"栄養塩 (also 栄養塩類) are the nutrients dissolved in water for algae and plankton, above all nitrogen, phosphorus and silicate compounds. Too much leads to 富栄養化 \"eutrophication\" and 赤潮 \"algal bloom\"; too little leads to nutrient deficiency in the sea. Typical: 栄養塩が供給される／不足する, 栄養塩濃度. Technical language of oceanography and fisheries.","levelNote":"Compound: 栄養 (N3) + 塩 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"土壌中の有機物は植物の成長に不可欠である。","romaji":"Dojōchū no yūkibutsu wa shokubutsu no seichō ni fukaketsu de aru.","german":"Organic matter in the soil is essential for plant growth.","furigana":"どじょうちゅう|ゆうきぶつ|しょくぶつ|せいちょう|ふかけつ"},{"japanese":"生ごみなどの有機物は、微生物によって分解される。","romaji":"Namagomi nado no yūkibutsu wa, biseibutsu ni yotte bunkai sareru.","german":"Organic matter such as food waste is decomposed by microorganisms.","furigana":"なま|ゆうきぶつ|びせいぶつ|ぶんかい"},{"japanese":"炭素を含む化合物の多くは、有機物に分類される。","romaji":"Tanso o fukumu kagōbutsu no ōku wa, yūkibutsu ni bunrui sareru.","german":"Many compounds containing carbon are classified as organic matter.","furigana":"たんそ|ふく|かごうぶつ|おお|ゆうきぶつ|ぶんるい"}],"notes":"有機物 is \"organic matter\", that is, carbon-containing material from living things such as fallen leaves, food scraps or compost; in chemistry also \"organic compound\" (有機化合物). The opposite term is 無機物. Typical: 有機物を分解する, 有機物を多く含む土. Not to be confused with 有機 in 有機野菜 or 有機栽培, where it means \"organic, ecologically grown\". School and technical language.","levelNote":"Compound: 有機 (N3) + 物 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"分解者は有機物を無機物に変換する。","romaji":"Bunkaisha wa yūkibutsu wo mukibutsu ni henkan suru.","german":"Decomposers convert organic matter into inorganic matter.","furigana":"ぶんかいしゃ|ゆうきぶつ|むきぶつ|へんかん"},{"japanese":"水や食塩、金属などは無機物である。","romaji":"Mizu ya shokuen, kinzoku nado wa mukibutsu de aru.","german":"Water, table salt and metals are inorganic substances.","furigana":"みず|しょくえん|きんぞく|むきぶつ"},{"japanese":"有機物と違って、無機物は燃やしても二酸化炭素が出ない。","romaji":"Yūkibutsu to chigatte, mukibutsu wa moyashite mo nisanka tanso ga denai.","german":"Unlike organic matter, inorganic matter does not give off carbon dioxide when burned.","furigana":"ゆうきぶつ|ちが|むきぶつ|も|にさんかたんそ|で"}],"notes":"無機物 is \"inorganic matter\": water, salts, metals, rocks and gases such as carbon dioxide. The opposite term is 有機物; decomposers convert 有機物 back into 無機物. In nutrition, minerals are by contrast called 無機質 or ミネラル. The adjective 無機質な is used figuratively for \"cold, soulless\" (無機質な部屋), 無機物 itself is not. School and technical language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"生態系における物質循環は地球環境を維持する。","romaji":"Seitaikei ni okeru busshitsu junkan wa chikyū kankyō wo iji suru.","german":"Material cycling in ecosystems maintains the Earth's environment.","furigana":"せいたいけい|ぶっしつじゅんかん|ちきゅうかんきょう|いじ"},{"japanese":"分解者がいなければ、生態系の物質循環は止まってしまう。","romaji":"Bunkaisha ga inakereba, seitaikei no busshitsu junkan wa tomatte shimau.","german":"Without decomposers, the material cycle of the ecosystem would come to a halt.","furigana":"ぶんかいしゃ|せいたいけい|ぶっしつじゅんかん|と"},{"japanese":"廃棄物を資源として再利用し、社会の中で物質循環を進める必要がある。","romaji":"Haikibutsu o shigen to shite sairiyō shi, shakai no naka de busshitsu junkan o susumeru hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"We need to reuse waste as resources and promote material cycling within society.","furigana":"はいきぶつ|しげん|さいりよう|しゃかい|なか|ぶっしつじゅんかん|すす|ひつよう"}],"notes":"物質循環 is the \"material cycle\": elements such as carbon, nitrogen or phosphorus move between organisms, soil, water and air. Typical: 生態系における物質循環, 物質循環を担う. In environmental policy the word also stands for the circulation of raw materials and waste in society, related to 循環型社会 \"recycling-oriented society\". For the flow of energy, by contrast, people say エネルギーの流れ, because energy does not return.","levelNote":"Compound: 物質 (N3) + 循環 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"森林は炭素循環において重要な役割を果たす。","romaji":"Shinrin wa tanso junkan ni oite jūyō na yakuwari wo hatasu.","german":"Forests play an important role in the carbon cycle.","furigana":"しんりん|たんそじゅんかん|じゅうよう|やくわり|は"},{"japanese":"化石燃料の大量消費が、地球の炭素循環のバランスを崩している。","romaji":"Kaseki nenryō no tairyō shōhi ga, chikyū no tanso junkan no baransu o kuzushite iru.","german":"The massive consumption of fossil fuels is upsetting the balance of the Earth's carbon cycle.","furigana":"かせきねんりょう|たいりょうしょうひ|ちきゅう|たんそじゅんかん|くず"},{"japanese":"光合成と呼吸は、炭素循環を支える二つの重要な過程だ。","romaji":"Kōgōsei to kokyū wa, tanso junkan o sasaeru futatsu no jūyō na katei da.","german":"Photosynthesis and respiration are two important processes that support the carbon cycle.","furigana":"こうごうせい|こきゅう|たんそじゅんかん|ささ|ふた|じゅうよう|かてい"}],"notes":"炭素循環 is the carbon cycle between atmosphere, organisms, ocean and rock. Important steps are 光合成 (uptake of CO2) and 呼吸 or 分解 (release). Typical: 炭素循環において, 炭素循環のバランスが崩れる, 地球規模の炭素循環. Technical language, common in climate reports.","levelNote":"Compound: 炭素 (N1) + 循環 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"窒素循環の乱れが環境問題を引き起こしている。","romaji":"Chisso junkan no midare ga kankyō mondai o hikiokoshite iru.","german":"Disruption of the nitrogen cycle is causing environmental problems.","furigana":"ちっそじゅんかん|みだ|かんきょうもんだい|ひ|お"},{"japanese":"土の中の微生物は、窒素循環に重要な役割を果たしている。","romaji":"Tsuchi no naka no biseibutsu wa, chisso junkan ni jūyō na yakuwari o hatashite iru.","german":"Microorganisms in the soil play an important role in the nitrogen cycle.","furigana":"つち|なか|びせいぶつ|ちっそじゅんかん|じゅうよう|やくわり|は"},{"japanese":"水槽の窒素循環が安定するまで、魚を入れないでください。","romaji":"Suisō no chisso junkan ga antei suru made, sakana o irenaide kudasai.","german":"Please do not add fish until the nitrogen cycle in the tank has stabilized.","furigana":"すいそう|ちっそじゅんかん|あんてい|さかな|い"}],"notes":"窒素循環 is the cycle of nitrogen between air, soil, organisms and water: bacteria fix it, plants take it up, and it is released again during decomposition. Formed like 炭素循環 \"carbon cycle\" and 水循環 \"water cycle\"; typical are 窒素循環が乱れる and 窒素循環を担う微生物. The word also appears in aquarium keeping, when filter bacteria break down ammonia. Technical language of biology and environmental science.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"水循環は蒸発、降水、流出の過程から成る。","romaji":"Mizu junkan wa jōhatsu, kōsui, ryūshutsu no katei kara naru.","german":"The water cycle consists of the processes of evaporation, precipitation and runoff.","furigana":"みずじゅんかん|じょうはつ|こうすい|りゅうしゅつ|かてい|な"},{"japanese":"森林は雨水をたくわえ、水循環を安定させる働きがある。","romaji":"Shinrin wa usui o takuwae, mizu junkan o antei saseru hataraki ga aru.","german":"Forests store rainwater and have the function of stabilizing the water cycle.","furigana":"しんりん|うすい|みずじゅんかん|あんてい|はたら"},{"japanese":"都市化で地面が舗装され、水循環のバランスが崩れている。","romaji":"Toshika de jimen ga hosō sare, mizu junkan no baransu ga kuzurete iru.","german":"Urbanization has paved the ground, and the balance of the water cycle is being upset.","furigana":"としか|じめん|ほそう|みずじゅんかん|くず"}],"notes":"水循環 is read みずじゅんかん, not すいじゅんかん. It means the water cycle made up of 蒸発 \"evaporation\", 降水 \"precipitation\", 浸透 \"infiltration\" and 流出 \"runoff\". In Japan the 水循環基本法 regulates the protection of this cycle, so the word also appears in official texts. Typical: 健全な水循環, 水循環を保つ.","levelNote":"Compound: 水 (N5) + 循環 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"リン循環は、炭素や窒素の循環と違って、大気を経由しない。","romaji":"Rin junkan wa, tanso ya chisso no junkan to chigatte, taiki o keiyu shinai.","german":"Unlike the carbon and nitrogen cycles, the phosphorus cycle does not pass through the atmosphere.","furigana":"じゅんかん|たんそ|ちっそ|じゅんかん|ちが|たいき|けいゆ"},{"japanese":"リン鉱石の採掘と肥料の大量使用が、リン循環を大きく変えている。","romaji":"Rin kōseki no saikutsu to hiryō no tairyō shiyō ga, rin junkan o ōkiku kaete iru.","german":"The mining of phosphate rock and the heavy use of fertilizer are greatly changing the phosphorus cycle.","furigana":"こうせき|さいくつ|ひりょう|たいりょうしよう|じゅんかん|おお|か"},{"japanese":"海鳥のふんは海から陸へリンを運び、リン循環の一部を担っている。","romaji":"Umidori no fun wa umi kara riku e rin o hakobi, rin junkan no ichibu o ninatte iru.","german":"Seabird droppings carry phosphorus from the sea to the land and form part of the phosphorus cycle.","furigana":"うみどり|うみ|りく|はこ|じゅんかん|いちぶ|にな"}],"notes":"リン循環 is the phosphorus cycle. Phosphorus is almost always written in katakana in Japan (リン, rarely 燐). Unlike the carbon and nitrogen cycles, there is no significant route through the atmosphere; phosphorus enters soil and waters through weathering of rock and ends up in marine sediment. Humans intervene strongly through リン鉱石 \"phosphate rock\" and fertilizers. Technical language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"湧昇流は深海の栄養塩を表層に運び豊かな漁場を形成する。","romaji":"Yūshōryū wa shinkai no eiyōen wo hyōsō ni hakobi yutaka na gyojō wo keisei suru.","german":"Upwelling currents carry nutrient salts from the deep sea to the surface and form rich fishing grounds.","furigana":"ゆうしょうりゅう|しんかい|えいようえん|ひょうそう|はこ|ゆた|ぎょじょう|けいせい"},{"japanese":"ペルー沖では湧昇が起こるため、カタクチイワシの好漁場となっている。","romaji":"Perū oki de wa yūshō ga okoru tame, katakuchiiwashi no kō gyojō to natte iru.","german":"Upwelling occurs off Peru, so it is a good fishing ground for anchovies.","furigana":"おき|ゆうしょう|お|こうぎょじょう"},{"japanese":"エルニーニョ現象が起きると湧昇が弱まり、漁獲量が減る。","romaji":"Erunīnyo genshō ga okiru to yūshō ga yowamari, gyokakuryō ga heru.","german":"When El Nino occurs, upwelling weakens and catches decline.","furigana":"げんしょう|お|ゆうしょう|よわ|ぎょかくりょう|へ"}],"notes":"湧昇 is oceanic \"upwelling\": cold, nutrient-rich deep water rises to the surface, often off west coasts such as Peru or California. Most often you hear 湧昇流 \"upwelling current\" or 湧昇域 \"upwelling area\"; 湧 is the same character as in 湧く \"to well up\". Careful, homophone: ゆうしょう is in everyday speech almost always 優勝 \"championship\". Technical language of oceanography.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"沿岸流が砂を運搬し海岸地形を変化させる。","romaji":"Enganryū ga suna wo unpan shi kaigan chikei wo henka saseru.","german":"Longshore currents transport sand and change coastal landforms.","furigana":"えんがんりゅう|すな|うんぱん|かいがんちけい|へんか"},{"japanese":"海水浴では、沖へ向かう離岸流のほか、岸に沿って流れる沿岸流にも注意が必要だ。","romaji":"Kaisuiyoku de wa, oki e mukau rigan-ryū no hoka, kishi ni sotte nagareru enganryū ni mo chūi ga hitsuyō da.","german":"When swimming in the sea, besides rip currents heading offshore, you also need to watch out for longshore currents flowing along the shore.","furigana":"かいすいよく|おき|む|りがんりゅう|きし|そ|なが|えんがんりゅう|ちゅうい|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"防波堤の建設で沿岸流が変わり、隣の海岸の砂浜がやせてしまった。","romaji":"Bōhatei no kensetsu de enganryū ga kawari, tonari no kaigan no sunahama ga yasete shimatta.","german":"The construction of a breakwater changed the longshore current, and the sandy beach next door shrank.","furigana":"ぼうはてい|けんせつ|えんがんりゅう|か|となり|かいがん|すなはま"}],"notes":"沿岸流 is a current that runs parallel to the coast, on a large scale as a coastal current of the ocean, on a small scale as a longshore current in the surf zone. It transports sand and shapes beaches and sand spits (砂州, 砂嘴). Not to be confused with 離岸流, the dangerous rip current that pulls away from the shore into the open sea. Technical language, also generally known from swimming warnings.","levelNote":"Compound: 沿岸 (N1) + 流 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"深層水は栄養塩を豊富に含んでいる。","romaji":"Shinsōsui wa eiyōen wo hōfu ni fukunde iru.","german":"Deep water is rich in nutrient salts.","furigana":"しんそうすい|えいようえん|ほうふ|ふく"},{"japanese":"高知県の室戸では、海洋深層水を使った飲料水や塩が作られている。","romaji":"Kōchi-ken no Muroto de wa, kaiyō shinsōsui o tsukatta inryōsui ya shio ga tsukurarete iru.","german":"In Muroto, Kochi Prefecture, drinking water and salt are made using deep ocean water.","furigana":"こうちけん|むろと|かいようしんそうすい|つか|いんりょうすい|しお|つく"},{"japanese":"深層水は水温が低く、細菌などがほとんど含まれていない。","romaji":"Shinsōsui wa suion ga hikuku, saikin nado ga hotondo fukumarete inai.","german":"Deep water is cold and contains almost no bacteria.","furigana":"しんそうすい|すいおん|ひく|さいきん|ふく"}],"notes":"深層水 is cold deep water below the layer reached by light; the opposite term is 表層水. In Japan, 海洋深層水 is best known; it is pumped, for example, in 室戸 (Kochi) or Toyama and marketed as drinking water, salt or cosmetics. It is regarded as nutrient-rich, clean and low in germs. Neutral, common in advertising.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"黒潮は日本列島沿いを流れる暖流である。","romaji":"Kuroshio wa Nihon rettō zoi wo nagareru danryū de aru.","german":"The Kuroshio is a warm current that flows along the Japanese archipelago.","furigana":"くろしお|にほんれっとうぞ|なが|だんりゅう"},{"japanese":"暖流の影響で、この地方は冬でも比較的暖かい。","romaji":"Danryū no eikyō de, kono chihō wa fuyu de mo hikakuteki atatakai.","german":"Because of the warm current, this region is relatively warm even in winter.","furigana":"だんりゅう|えいきょう|ちほう|ふゆ|ひかくてきあたた"},{"japanese":"対馬海流は日本海を北上する暖流で、冬の大雪とも関係している。","romaji":"Tsushima kairyū wa Nihonkai o hokujō suru danryū de, fuyu no ōyuki to mo kankei shite iru.","german":"The Tsushima Current is a warm current flowing north through the Sea of Japan and is also related to the heavy snowfall in winter.","furigana":"|にほんかい|ほくじょう|だんりゅう|ふゆ|おおゆき|かんけい"}],"notes":"暖流 is a warm ocean current that usually flows poleward from low latitudes; the opposite term is 寒流. The well-known 暖流 around Japan are the 黒潮 (日本海流) on the Pacific side and the 対馬海流 in the Sea of Japan. Typical: 暖流が流れる, 暖流の影響で暖かい. School geography vocabulary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"親潮は北から南下する寒流で豊かな漁場を形成する。","romaji":"Oyashio wa kita kara nanka suru kanryū de yutaka na gyojō wo keisei suru.","german":"The Oyashio is a cold current flowing south from the north and forms rich fishing grounds.","furigana":"おやしお|きた|なんか|かんりゅう|ゆた|ぎょじょう|けいせい"},{"japanese":"寒流の上では空気が冷やされ、霧が発生しやすい。","romaji":"Kanryū no ue de wa kūki ga hiyasare, kiri ga hassei shiyasui.","german":"Over a cold current, the air is cooled and fog forms easily.","furigana":"かんりゅう|うえ|くうき|ひ|きり|はっせい"},{"japanese":"ペルー海流は、赤道近くまで北上する寒流だ。","romaji":"Perū kairyū wa, sekidō chikaku made hokujō suru kanryū da.","german":"The Peru Current is a cold current that flows north to near the equator.","furigana":"かいりゅう|せきどうちか|ほくじょう|かんりゅう"}],"notes":"寒流 is a cold ocean current that usually flows from high latitudes toward the equator; the opposite term is 暖流. Around Japan, 親潮 (千島海流) and the リマン海流 are the most important 寒流. Where 寒流 and 暖流 meet (潮目), rich fishing grounds arise; sea fog often forms over cold currents. School geography vocabulary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"暖流と寒流がぶつかる潮目は好漁場となる。","romaji":"Danryū to kanryū ga butsukaru shiome wa kō gyojō to naru.","german":"The current boundary where warm and cold currents collide becomes a good fishing ground.","furigana":"だんりゅう|かんりゅう|しおめ|こうぎょじょう"},{"japanese":"討論会のあと選挙戦の潮目が変わり、野党の候補が優勢になった。","romaji":"Tōronkai no ato senkyosen no shiome ga kawari, yatō no kōho ga yūsei ni natta.","german":"After the debate, the tide of the election campaign turned, and the opposition candidate took the lead.","furigana":"とうろんかい|せんきょせん|しおめ|か|やとう|こうほ|ゆうせい"},{"japanese":"ベテランの漁師は、海の色の違いで潮目を見分ける。","romaji":"Beteran no ryōshi wa, umi no iro no chigai de shiome o miwakeru.","german":"Veteran fishermen can tell the current boundary by the difference in the color of the sea.","furigana":"りょうし|うみ|いろ|ちが|しおめ|みわ"}],"notes":"潮目 is the visible boundary line where two ocean currents or water masses meet; plankton and fish gather there, hence good fishing grounds. Very common is the figurative use for a turning point in politics, business or competition: 潮目が変わる \"the tide turns\", 潮目を読む. Kun reading しおめ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"縄文時代には海進により関東平野の一部が海中にあった。","romaji":"Jōmon jidai ni wa kaishin ni yori Kantō heiya no ichibu ga kaichū ni atta.","german":"During the Jomon period, part of the Kanto Plain was under the sea because of marine transgression.","furigana":"じょうもんじだい|かいしん|かんとうへいや|いちぶ|かいちゅう"},{"japanese":"貝塚が内陸で見つかるのは、縄文海進で海岸線が今より奥にあったからだ。","romaji":"Kaizuka ga nairiku de mitsukaru no wa, Jōmon kaishin de kaigansen ga ima yori oku ni atta kara da.","german":"Shell mounds are found inland because, owing to the Jomon transgression, the coastline lay farther inland than it does now.","furigana":"かいづか|ないりく|み|じょうもんかいしん|かいがんせん|いま|おく"},{"japanese":"地層を調べると、この地域で海進と海退が何度も繰り返されたことがわかる。","romaji":"Chisō o shiraberu to, kono chiiki de kaishin to kaitai ga nando mo kurikaesareta koto ga wakaru.","german":"Examining the strata shows that marine transgression and regression were repeated many times in this region.","furigana":"ちそう|しら|ちいき|かいしん|かいたい|なんど|く|かえ"}],"notes":"海進 is geological \"transgression\": the sea level rises or the land subsides, and the sea advances inland. The opposite term is 海退. Famous is the 縄文海進 about 6,000 years ago, when the sea reached far into what is now the Kanto region; that is why many shell mounds (貝塚) lie inland today. For the current rise in sea level people rather say 海面上昇. Technical language of geology and history.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"氷河期には海退により大陸間を徒歩で渡ることができた。","romaji":"Hyōgaki ni wa kaitai ni yori tairikukan wo toho de wataru koto ga dekita.","german":"During the Ice Age, marine regression made it possible to travel between continents on foot.","furigana":"ひょうがき|かいたい|たいりくかん|とほ|わた"},{"japanese":"海退によって、かつての海底が陸地になった。","romaji":"Kaitai ni yotte, katsute no kaitei ga rikuchi ni natta.","german":"Through marine regression, the former seabed became dry land.","furigana":"かいたい|かいてい|りくち"},{"japanese":"海岸段丘は、土地の隆起や海退によってできる地形だ。","romaji":"Kaigan dankyū wa, tochi no ryūki ya kaitai ni yotte dekiru chikei da.","german":"Coastal terraces are landforms created by uplift of the land or marine regression.","furigana":"かいがんだんきゅう|とち|りゅうき|かいたい|ちけい"}],"notes":"海退 is geological \"regression\": the sea retreats because sea level falls (e.g. in ice ages) or the land rises; former sea floor becomes land. The opposite term is 海進. In the ice ages, 海退 created land bridges, for example between Japan and the continent. Technical language of geology and Earth history.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"痩せ地でも育つ植物が品種改良されている。","romaji":"Yasechi de mo sodatsu shokubutsu ga hinshu kairyō sarete iru.","german":"Plants that can grow even in poor soil are being bred.","furigana":"や|ち|そだ|しょくぶつ|ひんしゅかいりょう"},{"japanese":"祖父は、痩せ地を何十年もかけて豊かな畑に変えた。","romaji":"Sofu wa, yasechi o nanjūnen mo kakete yutaka na hatake ni kaeta.","german":"My grandfather spent decades turning barren land into rich fields.","furigana":"そふ|や|ち|なんじゅうねん|ゆた|はたけ|か"},{"japanese":"痩せ地にはマメ科の植物を植えて、土を肥やすとよい。","romaji":"Yasechi ni wa mameka no shokubutsu o uete, tsuchi o koyasu to yoi.","german":"It is good to plant legumes in poor soil to enrich it.","furigana":"や|ち|か|しょくぶつ|う|つち|こ"}],"notes":"痩せ地 (often also written やせ地) is nutrient-poor, infertile soil. In everyday speech people mostly say 痩せた土地; for soil to \"become depleted\" it is 土地が痩せる. The opposite is 肥沃な土地; to improve soil is 土を肥やす. Typical: 痩せ地でも育つ, 痩せ地を耕す. Neutral to literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"火山灰が堆積した土壌は肥沃である。","romaji":"Kazanbai ga taiseki shita dojō wa hiyoku de aru.","german":"Soil on which volcanic ash has accumulated is fertile.","furigana":"かざんばい|たいせき|どじょう|ひよく"},{"japanese":"ナイル川の氾濫が、流域に肥沃な土地をもたらした。","romaji":"Nairu-gawa no hanran ga, ryūiki ni hiyoku na tochi o motarashita.","german":"The flooding of the Nile brought fertile land to the river basin.","furigana":"がわ|はんらん|りゅういき|ひよく|とち"},{"japanese":"堆肥を入れ続けたおかげで、畑の土が肥沃になった。","romaji":"Taihi o iretsuzuketa okage de, hatake no tsuchi ga hiyoku ni natta.","german":"Thanks to continuously adding compost, the soil of the field has become fertile.","furigana":"たいひ|い|つづ|はたけ|つち|ひよく"}],"notes":"肥沃 is a な-adjective (also a noun) for fertile, nutrient-rich soil: 肥沃な土地／土壌／平野, 土地が肥沃になる. It is used almost only for land and soil, not for people or ideas. Opposites are 不毛 and 痩せた. For fertile animals or people, one does not say 肥沃 but 繁殖力が強い or 子宝に恵まれる. Literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"長年の過耕作により土地が不毛になった。","romaji":"Naganen no ka kōsaku ni yori tochi ga fumō ni natta.","german":"The land became barren through years of overcultivation.","furigana":"ながねん|かこうさく|とち|ふもう"},{"japanese":"砂漠の不毛の地にも、わずかな植物が生えている。","romaji":"Sabaku no fumō no chi ni mo, wazuka na shokubutsu ga haete iru.","german":"Even in the barren land of the desert, a few plants grow.","furigana":"さばく|ふもう|ち|しょくぶつ|は"},{"japanese":"そんな不毛な議論はもうやめよう。","romaji":"Sonna fumō na giron wa mō yameyō.","german":"Let's stop this fruitless argument already.","furigana":"ふもう|ぎろん"}],"notes":"不毛 literally means \"without growth\": 不毛の地／不毛な土地 is infertile, barren land. Very common is the figurative use for something fruitless without result: 不毛な議論, 不毛な争い. It stands as a な-adjective or with の before nouns. The opposite in the literal sense is 肥沃; in the figurative sense 有意義 or 実りある fit. Literary, but common.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"森林の劣化が生態系サービスの低下を招いている。","romaji":"Shinrin no rekka ga seitaikei sābisu no teika wo maneite iru.","german":"The degradation of forests is leading to a decline in ecosystem services.","furigana":"しんりん|れっか|せいたいけい|ていか|まね"},{"japanese":"スマホのバッテリーが劣化して、すぐに充電が切れる。","romaji":"Sumaho no batterī ga rekka shite, sugu ni jūden ga kireru.","german":"My smartphone's battery has deteriorated, and the charge runs out quickly.","furigana":"れっか|じゅうでん|き"},{"japanese":"紫外線でプラスチックが劣化し、ひび割れが起きた。","romaji":"Shigaisen de purasuchikku ga rekka shi, hibiware ga okita.","german":"UV rays degraded the plastic, and it cracked.","furigana":"しがいせん|れっか|わ|お"}],"notes":"劣化 is the deterioration of quality or performance through age, use or environmental influences; as an intransitive する-verb: バッテリーが劣化する, 経年劣化 \"age-related wear\". For situations or health people say 悪化, and for aging buildings and facilities 老朽化. Colloquially, 劣化 is used disparagingly of celebrities who \"look worse\"; that is impolite. Transitive: 劣化させる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新規化学物質の生態毒性を評価する試験が義務付けられている。","romaji":"Shinki kagaku busshitsu no seitai dokusei wo hyōka suru shiken ga gimuzukerarete iru.","german":"Tests to evaluate the ecotoxicity of new chemical substances are mandatory.","furigana":"しんきかがくぶっしつ|せいたいどくせい|ひょうか|しけん|ぎむづ"},{"japanese":"この農薬は人への毒性は低いが、魚類に対する生態毒性が高い。","romaji":"Kono nōyaku wa hito e no dokusei wa hikui ga, gyorui ni taisuru seitai dokusei ga takai.","german":"This pesticide has low toxicity to humans but high ecotoxicity to fish.","furigana":"のうやく|ひと|どくせい|ひく|ぎょるい|たい|せいたいどくせい|たか"},{"japanese":"研究室では、ミジンコを使って排水の生態毒性を調べている。","romaji":"Kenkyūshitsu de wa, mijinko o tsukatte haisui no seitai dokusei o shirabete iru.","german":"In the laboratory, they use water fleas to examine the ecotoxicity of wastewater.","furigana":"けんきゅうしつ|つか|はいすい|せいたいどくせい|しら"}],"notes":"生態毒性 is ecotoxicity, that is, the harmful effect of a substance on organisms in the environment such as fish, water fleas or algae, as opposed to toxicity to humans (ヒトへの毒性). Typical: 生態毒性を評価する, 生態毒性試験, 生態毒性が高い／低い. Technical language of chemicals law and environmental toxicology.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この森林には希少種の蝶が生息している。","romaji":"Kono shinrin ni wa kishōshu no chō ga seisoku shite iru.","german":"A rare species of butterfly lives in this forest.","furigana":"しんりん|きしょうしゅ|ちょう|せいそく"},{"japanese":"希少種の密猟や違法な取引が後を絶たない。","romaji":"Kishōshu no mitsuryō ya ihō na torihiki ga ato o tatanai.","german":"Poaching and illegal trade in rare species show no sign of ending.","furigana":"きしょうしゅ|みつりょう|いほう|とりひき|あと|た"},{"japanese":"開発予定地で希少種が見つかり、工事が一時中断された。","romaji":"Kaihatsu yoteichi de kishōshu ga mitsukari, kōji ga ichiji chūdan sareta.","german":"A rare species was found at the planned development site, and construction was temporarily suspended.","furigana":"かいはつよていち|きしょうしゅ|み|こうじ|いちじちゅうだん"}],"notes":"希少種 (also 稀少種) is a rare animal or plant species with a small population or range. It overlaps with 絶滅危惧種 \"endangered species\" but is broader: a species can be rare without being acutely threatened. Legally protected species are called 希少野生動植物種. Typical: 希少種を保護する, 希少種が生息する. Technical and media language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この魚は準絶滅危惧種に指定されている。","romaji":"Kono sakana wa jun zetsumetsu kigushu ni shitei sarete iru.","german":"This fish is designated as a near-threatened species.","furigana":"さかな|じゅんぜつめつきぐしゅ|してい"},{"japanese":"生息地の減少が続けば、準絶滅危惧種から絶滅危惧種に引き上げられるおそれがある。","romaji":"Seisokuchi no genshō ga tsuzukeba, jun zetsumetsu kigushu kara zetsumetsu kigushu ni hikiagerareru osore ga aru.","german":"If habitat loss continues, it may be upgraded from near-threatened to threatened.","furigana":"せいそくち|げんしょう|つづ|じゅんぜつめつきぐしゅ|ぜつめつきぐしゅ|ひ|あ"},{"japanese":"庭で見かけたこのチョウが準絶滅危惧種だと知って驚いた。","romaji":"Niwa de mikaketa kono chō ga jun zetsumetsu kigushu da to shitte odoroita.","german":"I was surprised to learn that this butterfly I saw in my garden is a near-threatened species.","furigana":"にわ|み|じゅんぜつめつきぐしゅ|し|おどろ"}],"notes":"準絶滅危惧種 is the Red List category \"near threatened\" (NT): the species is not yet acutely endangered but could move up into a threat category if conditions change. 準 means \"quasi, preliminary stage\"; the actual threat levels are called 絶滅危惧Ⅰ類 and 絶滅危惧Ⅱ類. Typical combinations are 〜に指定される, 〜に選定される and レッドリストで〜に分類される. In official lists often only 準絶滅危惧 appears without 種; the expression belongs to the language of nature conservation, officialdom and the news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"保全活動により個体数が徐々に回復している。","romaji":"Hozen katsudō ni yori kotaisū ga jojo ni kaifuku shite iru.","german":"Thanks to conservation efforts, the population is gradually recovering.","furigana":"ほぜんかつどう|こたいすう|じょじょ|かいふく"},{"japanese":"この湖では、外来魚の個体数を減らすために駆除が行われている。","romaji":"Kono mizuumi de wa, gairaigyo no kotaisū o herasu tame ni kujo ga okonawarete iru.","german":"In this lake, culling is carried out to reduce the population of invasive fish.","furigana":"みずうみ|がいらいぎょ|こたいすう|へ|くじょ|おこな"},{"japanese":"調査チームはドローンを使ってシカの個体数を推定した。","romaji":"Chōsa chīmu wa dorōn o tsukatte shika no kotaisū o suitei shita.","german":"The survey team estimated the number of deer using drones.","furigana":"ちょうさ|つか|こたいすう|すいてい"}],"notes":"個体数 is in biology and ecology the number of individual animals or plants in a population, that is, the \"population size\". Typical are 個体数が増える・減る・回復する and 個体数を調査する・推定する・管理する. For humans people say 人口; 生息数 is a close synonym for wild animals in an area, and 頭数 counts mainly large animals and livestock. 個体 alone denotes the single organism as a specimen.","levelNote":"Compound: 個体 (N2) + 数 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"種子は生育に適さない条件下では休眠状態に入る。","romaji":"Shushi wa seiiku ni tekisanai jōken ka de wa kyūmin jōtai ni hairu.","german":"Seeds enter a dormant state under conditions unsuitable for growth.","furigana":"しゅし|せいいく|てき|じょうけんか|きゅうみんじょうたい|はい"},{"japanese":"十年以上使っていない口座は、休眠口座として扱われることがある。","romaji":"Jūnen ijō tsukatte inai kōza wa, kyūmin kōza to shite atsukawareru koto ga aru.","german":"Accounts that have not been used for more than ten years may be treated as dormant accounts.","furigana":"じゅうねんいじょうつか|こうざ|きゅうみんこうざ|あつか"},{"japanese":"このサークルは部員不足で、しばらく休眠している。","romaji":"Kono sākuru wa buin busoku de, shibaraku kyūmin shite iru.","german":"This club has been dormant for a while because of a lack of members.","furigana":"ぶいんぶそく|きゅうみん"}],"notes":"休眠 is the resting phase of seeds, buds, insects (diapause) or spores in which metabolism almost stands still; as an intransitive する-verb: 種子が休眠する. Fixed phrases are 休眠状態に入る, 休眠から覚める and 休眠打破 \"breaking of dormancy\". Figuratively it means \"dormant, inactive\": 休眠口座 \"dormant account\", 休眠会社 \"dormant company\", 休眠中のプロジェクト. The hibernation of animals, by contrast, is 冬眠, and ordinary sleep 睡眠.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日長の変化が植物の開花を制御する。","romaji":"Nitchō no henka ga shokubutsu no kaika o seigyo suru.","german":"Changes in day length control the flowering of plants.","furigana":"にっちょう|へんか|しょくぶつ|かいか|せいぎょ"},{"japanese":"秋になって日長が短くなると、鳥は渡りの準備を始める。","romaji":"Aki ni natte nitchō ga mijikaku naru to, tori wa watari no junbi o hajimeru.","german":"When the day length shortens in autumn, birds begin preparing for migration.","furigana":"あき|にっちょう|みじか|とり|わた|じゅんび|はじ"},{"japanese":"菊の農家は電灯で日長を調節して、出荷の時期をずらしている。","romaji":"Kiku no nōka wa dentō de nitchō o chōsetsu shite, shukka no jiki o zurashite iru.","german":"Chrysanthemum farmers adjust the day length with electric lights to shift the shipping season.","furigana":"きく|のうか|でんとう|にっちょう|ちょうせつ|しゅっか|じき"}],"notes":"日長 in the reading にっちょう is a technical word from biology and agriculture for the length of the light part of the day, to which plants and animals respond. Typical compounds are 日長反応 \"photoperiodism\" as well as 短日植物 and 長日植物 \"short-day and long-day plants\". In everyday speech people rather say 昼の長さ; 日照時間, by contrast, means the actual hours of sunshine. Not to be confused with ひなが (usually written 日永), a poetic word for the long days of spring.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ヒマワリは向日性を示す代表的な植物である。","romaji":"Himawari wa kōjitsusei o shimesu daihyōteki na shokubutsu de aru.","german":"The sunflower is a typical plant that shows heliotropism.","furigana":"こうじつせい|しめ|だいひょうてき|しょくぶつ"},{"japanese":"窓辺の鉢植えが光の方へ傾くのは、植物の向日性によるものだ。","romaji":"Madobe no hachiue ga hikari no hō e katamuku no wa, shokubutsu no kōjitsusei ni yoru mono da.","german":"That potted plants on the windowsill lean toward the light is due to the heliotropism of plants.","furigana":"まどべ|はちう|ひかり|ほう|かたむ|しょくぶつ|こうじつせい"},{"japanese":"彼女の向日性の明るさに、周りの人はいつも励まされている。","romaji":"Kanojo no kōjitsusei no akarusa ni, mawari no hito wa itsumo hagemasarete iru.","german":"The people around her are always encouraged by her sunny cheerfulness.","furigana":"かのじょ|こうじつせい|あか|まわ|ひと|はげ"}],"notes":"向日性 literally means \"the property of turning toward the sun\" and is the traditional botanical term for heliotropism; the opposite is 背日性. In modern biology people usually speak more generally of 光屈性 (phototropism). Typical is 向日性を示す or 向日性がある. Occasionally the word is transferred to people in a literary way: 向日性の人 is someone with a \"sunny\", life-affirming nature.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"植物の根は重力屈性によって下方へ伸びる。","romaji":"Shokubutsu no ne wa jūryoku kussei ni yotte kahō e nobiru.","german":"Plant roots grow downward by gravitropism.","furigana":"しょくぶつ|ね|じゅうりょくくっせい|かほう|の"},{"japanese":"茎は光の来る方向へ曲がり、正の光屈性を示す。","romaji":"Kuki wa hikari no kuru hōkō e magari, sei no kōkussei o shimesu.","german":"The stem bends toward the direction of the light, showing positive phototropism.","furigana":"くき|ひかり|く|ほうこう|ま|せい|こうくっせい|しめ"},{"japanese":"屈性と傾性の違いは、刺激の方向に関係するかどうかだ。","romaji":"Kussei to keisei no chigai wa, shigeki no hōkō ni kankei suru ka dō ka da.","german":"The difference between tropism and nastic movement lies in whether it relates to the direction of the stimulus.","furigana":"くっせい|けいせい|ちが|しげき|ほうこう|かんけい"}],"notes":"屈性 is the technical term for tropism, a directed growth movement of plants toward or away from a stimulus. It mostly appears in compounds: 光屈性 (phototropism), 重力屈性 (gravitropism, older 屈地性), 接触屈性, 水分屈性. Toward the stimulus is 正の屈性, away from the stimulus 負の屈性. To be distinguished from 傾性 (nasty), in which the direction of movement does not depend on the direction of the stimulus, such as tulips opening in warmth.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"塩性湿地は海岸域の独特な生態系を形成する。","romaji":"Ensei shitchi wa kaigan'iki no dokutoku na seitaikei o keisei suru.","german":"Salt marshes form a unique ecosystem of the coastal zone.","furigana":"えんせいしっち|かいがんいき|どくとく|せいたいけい|けいせい"},{"japanese":"東京湾の埋め立てで、かつての塩性湿地の多くが失われた。","romaji":"Tōkyō-wan no umetate de, katsute no ensei shitchi no ōku ga ushinawareta.","german":"Land reclamation in Tokyo Bay destroyed many of the former salt marshes.","furigana":"とうきょうわん|う|た|えんせいしっち|おお|うしな"},{"japanese":"満潮になると、この塩性湿地は海水に浸かる。","romaji":"Manchō ni naru to, kono ensei shitchi wa kaisui ni tsukaru.","german":"At high tide, this salt marsh is submerged in seawater.","furigana":"まんちょう|えんせいしっち|かいすい|つ"}],"notes":"塩性湿地 (\"salty wetland\") is the salt marsh: a flat coastal wetland that is flooded by seawater at high tide and covered with salt-tolerant plants (塩生植物) such as アッケシソウ or ヨシ. To be distinguished are 干潟 (tidal flat, mostly unvegetated), 湿原 (moor, fresh water) and 汽水域 (brackish zone). The word belongs to the technical language of ecology and nature conservation; typical combinations are 塩性湿地が広がる, 塩性湿地を保全する, 塩性湿地が失われる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"河畔林は洪水時に水の勢いを弱める働きがある。","romaji":"Kahanrin wa kōzui-ji ni mizu no ikioi o yowameru hataraki ga aru.","german":"Riparian forests have the function of weakening the force of the water during floods.","furigana":"かはんりん|こうずいじ|みず|いきお|よわ|はたら"},{"japanese":"河畔林の木陰が川の水温の上昇を抑え、魚のすみかになっている。","romaji":"Kahanrin no kokage ga kawa no suion no jōshō o osae, sakana no sumika ni natte iru.","german":"The shade of the riparian forest keeps the river's water temperature from rising and serves as a home for fish.","furigana":"かはんりん|こかげ|かわ|すいおん|じょうしょう|おさ|さかな"},{"japanese":"河川改修の際、河畔林をどこまで伐採するかが議論になった。","romaji":"Kasen kaishū no sai, kahanrin o doko made bassai suru ka ga giron ni natta.","german":"During river improvement work, there was debate over how much of the riparian forest should be cut down.","furigana":"かせんかいしゅう|さい|かはんりん|ばっさい|ぎろん"}],"notes":"河畔林 is the forest that grows on river banks and in floodplains, typically with willows (ヤナギ) and alders (ハンノキ). 河畔 is the written-language word for \"river bank\"; in everyday speech people say 川沿い or 川辺; forest along mountain streams is called 渓畔林. The word appears mainly in ecology, hydraulic engineering and nature conservation: 河畔林を保全する, 河畔林を伐採する, 河畔林が魚のすみかになる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"水辺の生態系は多くの生物種を支えている。","romaji":"Mizube no seitaikei wa ōku no seibutsu shu o sasaete iru.","german":"Waterside ecosystems support many species.","furigana":"みずべ|せいたいけい|おお|せいぶつしゅ|ささ"},{"japanese":"夏休みは水辺での事故が増えるので、注意してください。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi wa mizube de no jiko ga fueru node, chūi shite kudasai.","german":"Accidents near the water increase during summer vacation, so please be careful.","furigana":"なつやす|みずべ|じこ|ふ|ちゅうい"},{"japanese":"週末は子どもと水辺の公園でザリガニを捕まえた。","romaji":"Shūmatsu wa kodomo to mizube no kōen de zarigani o tsukamaeta.","german":"On the weekend, I caught crayfish with my kids at a park by the water.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|こ|みずべ|こうえん|つか"}],"notes":"水辺 is quite generally \"at the water\", that is, the area at the edge of rivers, lakes, ponds or the sea. It is neutral and appears both in everyday speech and in texts: 水辺で遊ぶ, 水辺の生き物, 水辺の公園, 水辺の事故. More specific are 川辺 (river bank), 海辺 (seashore), 浜辺 (beach) and 岸 (the shore as a line). In warnings about swimming accidents, 水辺 is the usual word.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"紅樹林は沿岸域の生態系保全に不可欠である。","romaji":"Kōjurin wa engan'iki no seitaikei hozen ni fukaketsu de aru.","german":"Mangrove forests are indispensable for conserving coastal ecosystems.","furigana":"こうじゅりん|えんがんいき|せいたいけいほぜん|ふかけつ"},{"japanese":"西表島の河口には、マングローブとも呼ばれる紅樹林が広がっている。","romaji":"Iriomote-jima no kakō ni wa, mangurōbu to mo yobareru kōjurin ga hirogatte iru.","german":"Mangrove forests, also called mangroves, spread out at the river mouths of Iriomote Island.","furigana":"|かこう|よ|こうじゅりん|ひろ"},{"japanese":"エビの養殖池をつくるために、多くの紅樹林が伐採された。","romaji":"Ebi no yōshokuike o tsukuru tame ni, ōku no kōjurin ga bassai sareta.","german":"Many mangrove forests were cut down to make shrimp farming ponds.","furigana":"ようしょくいけ|おお|こうじゅりん|ばっさい"}],"notes":"紅樹林 is a rare, scholarly-sounding word for the mangrove forest; 紅樹 (\"red tree\") denotes the mangrove after its reddish wood or bark. In present-day Japanese people almost always say マングローブ林 or simply マングローブ; 紅樹林 appears rather in technical texts, older writings or Chinese contexts. In Japan, mangroves grow mainly in Okinawa, Iriomote and Amami.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"山頂から見下ろす雲海は幻想的な光景だった。","romaji":"Sanchō kara miorosu unkai wa gensōteki na kōkei datta.","german":"The sea of clouds we looked down on from the summit was a fantastic sight.","furigana":"さんちょう|みお|うんかい|げんそうてき|こうけい"},{"japanese":"竹田城跡は、秋の早朝に雲海に浮かぶように見えることで有名だ。","romaji":"Takeda-jō ato wa, aki no sōchō ni unkai ni ukabu yō ni mieru koto de yūmei da.","german":"Takeda Castle ruins are famous for appearing to float on a sea of clouds on early autumn mornings.","furigana":"たけだじょうあと|あき|そうちょう|うんかい|う|み|ゆうめい"},{"japanese":"飛行機の窓の外には、一面の雲海が広がっていた。","romaji":"Hikōki no mado no soto ni wa, ichimen no unkai ga hirogatte ita.","german":"Outside the airplane window, a sea of clouds spread as far as the eye could see.","furigana":"ひこうき|まど|そと|いちめん|うんかい|ひろ"}],"notes":"雲海 is the \"sea of clouds\" seen from above, for example from a mountain summit or from an airplane: the cloud cover lies like a sea beneath you. Typical are 雲海が広がる, 雲海を見下ろす and 雲海に浮かぶ (e.g. 雲海に浮かぶ城, \"castle above the clouds\"). It often forms on cool autumn mornings in valley basins. Someone standing in the mist themselves speaks of 霧, not 雲海.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"厳冬期の山岳地帯では霧氷が木々を白く覆う。","romaji":"Gentōki no sangaku chitai de wa muhyō ga kigi o shiroku ōu.","german":"In the mountains in the depth of winter, rime ice covers the trees in white.","furigana":"げんとうき|さんがくちたい|むひょう|きぎ|しろ|おお"},{"japanese":"朝の冷え込みで、高原の木々に霧氷が咲いた。","romaji":"Asa no hiekomi de, kōgen no kigi ni muhyō ga saita.","german":"With the morning chill, rime ice bloomed on the trees of the highlands.","furigana":"あさ|ひ|こ|こうげん|きぎ|むひょう|さ"},{"japanese":"霧氷を見るなら、晴れて風の弱い早朝がいちばんだ。","romaji":"Muhyō o miru nara, harete kaze no yowai sōchō ga ichiban da.","german":"If you want to see rime ice, the best time is an early morning when it is clear and the wind is light.","furigana":"むひょう|み|は|かぜ|よわ|そうちょう"}],"notes":"霧氷 is the umbrella term for ice deposits that form on trees and objects in frost from fog or cloud droplets or water vapor; this includes 樹氷 (soft rime), 粗氷 (hard rime) and 樹霜 (hoar frost on twigs). People say 霧氷がつく, 霧氷ができる and, poetically, 霧氷が咲く. To be distinguished are 霜, frost on the ground and on surfaces, and 霜柱, ice needles in the soil.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"蔵王の樹氷は冬の名物として観光客を集めている。","romaji":"Zaō no juhyō wa fuyu no meibutsu toshite kankōkyaku o atsumete iru.","german":"The rime-covered trees of Zao are a winter specialty that draws tourists.","furigana":"|じゅひょう|ふゆ|めいぶつ|かんこうきゃく|あつ"},{"japanese":"ロープウェイを降りると、目の前に巨大な樹氷が立ち並んでいた。","romaji":"Rōpuwei o oriru to, me no mae ni kyodai na juhyō ga tachinarande ita.","german":"When we got off the ropeway, giant rime-covered trees stood in rows right before our eyes.","furigana":"お|め|まえ|きょだい|じゅひょう|た|なら"},{"japanese":"暖冬の影響で、今年は樹氷の成長が遅れている。","romaji":"Dantō no eikyō de, kotoshi wa juhyō no seichō ga okurete iru.","german":"Because of the warm winter, the growth of the rime-covered trees is delayed this year.","furigana":"だんとう|えいきょう|ことし|じゅひょう|せいちょう|おく"}],"notes":"樹氷 is rime ice that freezes onto trees when supercooled fog droplets are carried by the wind against branches; it is a form of 霧氷. Famous are the completely iced-over firs of Zao, Hakkoda or Moriyoshi, which are also called スノーモンスター (\"snow monsters\"). Typical are 樹氷原, 樹氷ツアー, 樹氷のライトアップ and 樹氷が成長する. As a tourism word, it is much more common than 霧氷.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"融雪水が河川に流れ込み春の増水を引き起こす。","romaji":"Yūsetsusui ga kasen ni nagarekomi haru no zōsui o hikiokosu.","german":"Snowmelt water flows into rivers and causes spring flooding.","furigana":"ゆうせつすい|かせん|なが|こ|はる|ぞうすい|ひ|お"},{"japanese":"冬の朝は、道路に融雪剤がまかれていることが多い。","romaji":"Fuyu no asa wa, dōro ni yūsetsuzai ga makarete iru koto ga ōi.","german":"On winter mornings, snow-melting agent is often spread on the roads.","furigana":"ふゆ|あさ|どうろ|ゆうせつざい|おお"},{"japanese":"雪国の町では、道路の融雪装置から水が出ている。","romaji":"Yukiguni no machi de wa, dōro no yūsetsu sōchi kara mizu ga dete iru.","german":"In towns in snow country, water comes out of the snow-melting devices on the roads.","furigana":"ゆきぐに|まち|どうろ|ゆうせつそうち|みず|で"}],"notes":"融雪 is the melting of snow or the melted snow; the everyday word for it is 雪解け. 融雪 sounds technical and appears mainly in the news, weather reports and compounds: 融雪水 \"meltwater\", 融雪剤 \"de-icing salt, snow-melting agent\", 融雪装置 \"snow-melting system\" (such as water nozzles in roads in snowy regions) and 融雪洪水 \"snowmelt flood\". As a verb people do not say 融雪する but 雪が解ける.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"冬の朝、畑の土に霜柱が立っていた。","romaji":"Fuyu no asa, hatake no tsuchi ni shimobashira ga tatte ita.","german":"On a winter morning, frost columns stood in the soil of the field.","furigana":"ふゆ|あさ|はたけ|つち|しもばしら|た"},{"japanese":"子どもたちは登校中、霜柱をザクザク踏んで楽しんでいる。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa tōkōchū, shimobashira o zakuzaku funde tanoshinde iru.","german":"On the way to school, the children enjoy crunching through the needle ice.","furigana":"こ|とうこうちゅう|しもばしら|ふ|たの"},{"japanese":"霜柱で苗が持ち上げられないよう、株元をわらで覆った。","romaji":"Shimobashira de nae ga mochiagerarenai yō, kabumoto o wara de ōtta.","german":"So that the seedlings would not be pushed up by frost columns, I covered the base of the plants with straw.","furigana":"しもばしら|なえ|も|あ|かぶもと|おお"}],"notes":"霜柱 are the fine ice needles that grow from damp soil on cold nights and lift the topmost layer of earth. The fixed phrase is 霜柱が立つ; if you step on them, you hear a crunch (ザクザク). They are dreaded in gardening and farming because 霜柱 pushes seedlings up. Not to be confused with 霜 (frost on surfaces) and つらら (icicles); in haiku, 霜柱 is a winter seasonal word.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"電線への着氷が停電の原因となった。","romaji":"Densen e no chakuhyō ga teiden no gen'in to natta.","german":"Icing on the power lines caused the blackout.","furigana":"でんせん|ちゃくひょう|ていでん|げんいん"},{"japanese":"主翼への着氷を防ぐため、出発前に防氷剤を散布した。","romaji":"Shuyoku e no chakuhyō o fusegu tame, shuppatsu mae ni bōhyōzai o sanpu shita.","german":"To prevent icing on the main wings, de-icing fluid was sprayed before departure.","furigana":"しゅよく|ちゃくひょう|ふせ|しゅっぱつまえ|ぼうひょうざい|さんぷ"},{"japanese":"4回転ジャンプの着氷が乱れ、転倒してしまった。","romaji":"Yon-kaiten janpu no chakuhyō ga midare, tentō shite shimatta.","german":"The landing of the quadruple jump broke down, and the skater fell.","furigana":"かいてん|ちゃくひょう|みだ|てんとう"}],"notes":"着氷 is the freezing of ice onto objects, above all on aircraft wings, ships and power lines; as a する-verb: 翼に着氷する. The weather service issues an icing advisory 着氷注意報 when there is danger. In sports reporting, 着氷 also means the landing of a jump in figure skating: 着氷が乱れる, ジャンプを着氷する. Not to be confused with 結氷 (\"freezing over\" of bodies of water) and 凍結 (\"freezing\", also of roads).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"厳しい寒さで湖が結氷した。","romaji":"Kibishii samusa de mizuumi ga keppyō shita.","german":"The lake froze over in the severe cold.","furigana":"きび|さむ|みずうみ|けっぴょう"},{"japanese":"湖が全面結氷すると、ワカサギ釣りが始まる。","romaji":"Mizuumi ga zenmen keppyō suru to, wakasagi-tsuri ga hajimaru.","german":"When the lake freezes over completely, smelt fishing begins.","furigana":"みずうみ|ぜんめんけっぴょう|つ|はじ"},{"japanese":"この港は冬の間結氷するため、船が出られない。","romaji":"Kono minato wa fuyu no aida keppyō suru tame, fune ga derarenai.","german":"This harbor freezes over during winter, so ships cannot leave.","furigana":"みなと|ふゆ|あいだけっぴょう|ふね|で"}],"notes":"結氷 means that an ice cover forms on a body of water: 湖が結氷する \"the lake freezes over\". It is an intransitive する-verb with the body of water as subject and sounds matter-of-fact and written, for example in weather reports; in conversation people say 凍る or 氷が張る. Common combinations are 全面結氷 \"completely frozen over\" and 結氷期 \"freezing period\". The opposite, the breaking up of the ice, is 解氷.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"春の気温上昇とともに河川の解氷が始まる。","romaji":"Haru no kion jōshō to tomo ni kasen no kaihyō ga hajimaru.","german":"The ice breakup of rivers begins with the rise in temperature in spring.","furigana":"はる|きおんじょうしょう|かせん|かいひょう|はじ"},{"japanese":"湖が解氷すると、ワカサギ釣りのシーズンも終わる。","romaji":"Mizuumi ga kaihyō suru to, wakasagi-tsuri no shīzun mo owaru.","german":"When the ice on the lake melts, the smelt fishing season ends too.","furigana":"みずうみ|かいひょう|つ|お"},{"japanese":"北極海の解氷が進み、新たな航路が注目されている。","romaji":"Hokkyokukai no kaihyō ga susumi, aratana kōro ga chūmoku sarete iru.","german":"As the ice thaws in the Arctic Ocean, new shipping routes are attracting attention.","furigana":"ほっきょくかい|かいひょう|すす|あら|こうろ|ちゅうもく"}],"notes":"解氷 is the thawing or breaking up of the ice cover of rivers, lakes and seas in spring; as an intransitive する-verb: 湖が解氷する. The counterpart is 結氷 (\"freezing over\"); the season is called 解氷期. For the thawing of frozen food people say 解凍, for physical melting 融解, and for melting snow 雪解け. The word is rather literary and appears in weather and environmental reports.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"オホーツク海の流氷は北海道の冬の風物詩である。","romaji":"Ohōtsuku kai no ryūhyō wa Hokkaidō no fuyu no fūbutsushi de aru.","german":"The drift ice of the Sea of Okhotsk is a signature sight of winter in Hokkaido.","furigana":"かい|りゅうひょう|ほっかいどう|ふゆ|ふうぶつし"},{"japanese":"網走では砕氷船に乗って、流氷を間近に見ることができる。","romaji":"Abashiri de wa saihyōsen ni notte, ryūhyō o majika ni miru koto ga dekiru.","german":"In Abashiri, you can ride an icebreaker and see the drift ice up close.","furigana":"|さいひょうせん|の|りゅうひょう|まじか|み"},{"japanese":"今年は流氷の接岸が例年より一週間遅かった。","romaji":"Kotoshi wa ryūhyō no setsugan ga reinen yori isshūkan osokatta.","german":"This year the drift ice reached the shore a week later than usual.","furigana":"ことし|りゅうひょう|せつがん|れいねん|いっしゅうかんおそ"}],"notes":"流氷 is ice drifting on the sea, that is, ice floes and pack ice. In Japan, one thinks immediately of the Sea of Okhotsk off Hokkaido (Abashiri, Monbetsu, Shiretoko), where the ice reaches the coast every winter: 流氷が接岸する, 流氷初日, 流氷観光砕氷船. An iceberg that breaks off a glacier, by contrast, is called 氷山; 海氷 is the general technical term for sea ice.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"圧雪された道路は滑りやすく危険である。","romaji":"Assetsu sareta dōro wa suberiyasuku kiken de aru.","german":"Roads with packed snow are slippery and dangerous.","furigana":"あっせつ|どうろ|すべ|きけん"},{"japanese":"朝一番に圧雪されたゲレンデを滑るのは気持ちがいい。","romaji":"Asa ichiban ni assetsu sareta gerende o suberu no wa kimochi ga ii.","german":"It feels great to ski down the groomed slope first thing in the morning.","furigana":"あさいちばん|あっせつ|すべ|きも"},{"japanese":"峠道は圧雪路面のため、冬用タイヤが必要です。","romaji":"Tōge-michi wa assetsu romen no tame, fuyuyō taiya ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"The mountain pass road has a packed-snow surface, so winter tires are required.","furigana":"とうげみち|あっせつろめん|ふゆよう|ひつよう"}],"notes":"圧雪 is compressed, packed snow as well as the compacting of snow; as a transitive する-verb: コースを圧雪する. In traffic reports, 圧雪路面 stands for a snow-covered, packed road surface; at ski resorts 圧雪バーン stands for a groomed slope (opposite 非圧雪, powder snow); the snow groomer is called 圧雪車. To be distinguished are 新雪 (\"fresh snow\") and 根雪 (snow cover that stays on the ground).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"嗜好品の消費が年々増加している。","romaji":"Shikōhin no shōhi ga nennen zōka shite iru.","german":"Consumption of luxury goods like coffee and tobacco is rising year by year.","furigana":"しこうひん|しょうひ|ねんねんぞうか"},{"japanese":"アンケートで、回答者の食の嗜好を詳しく尋ねた。","romaji":"Ankēto de, kaitōsha no shoku no shikō o kuwashiku tazuneta.","german":"In the survey, respondents were asked in detail about their food preferences.","furigana":"かいとうしゃ|しょく|しこう|くわ|たず"},{"japanese":"このワインは日本人の嗜好に合うよう、甘めに仕上げてある。","romaji":"Kono wain wa Nihonjin no shikō ni au yō, amame ni shiagete aru.","german":"This wine is made on the sweet side to suit Japanese tastes.","furigana":"にほんじん|しこう|あ|あま|しあ"}],"notes":"嗜好 is a preference (often in taste), especially for food, drink and luxury items such as coffee, tobacco and alcohol; the word is literary and sounds objective. Typical are 嗜好品 (\"luxury foods and drinks\" such as coffee, tobacco, alcohol), 消費者の嗜好, 嗜好に合う and 嗜好が変化する. In everyday speech people say 好み. Beware of the homophones 志向 (\"orientation\", e.g. 健康志向), 思考 (\"thinking\") and 試行 (\"trial\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この地域には娯楽施設がほとんどない。","romaji":"Kono chiiki ni wa goraku shisetsu ga hotondo nai.","german":"There are almost no entertainment facilities in this area.","furigana":"ちいき|ごらくしせつ"},{"japanese":"昔はラジオが家庭の数少ない娯楽だった。","romaji":"Mukashi wa rajio ga katei no kazusukunai goraku datta.","german":"In the old days, the radio was one of the few forms of entertainment in households.","furigana":"むかし|かてい|かずすく|ごらく"},{"japanese":"この映画は難しいことを考えずに楽しめる娯楽作品だ。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa muzukashii koto o kangaezu ni tanoshimeru goraku sakuhin da.","german":"This movie is pure entertainment that you can enjoy without thinking about anything difficult.","furigana":"えいが|むずか|かんが|たの|ごらくさくひん"}],"notes":"娯楽 is entertainment or amusement as a category, that is, what one does to pass the time for relaxation. Typical are 娯楽施設, 娯楽番組, 娯楽映画・娯楽作品, 娯楽が少ない and 〜を娯楽として楽しむ. The word sounds somewhat factual and general; a personal hobby is 趣味, outdoor leisure activities are more often レジャー, and for show business one now often says エンターテインメント.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"余暇を有意義に過ごすことが大切だ。","romaji":"Yoka o yūigi ni sugosu koto ga taisetsu da.","german":"It is important to spend your leisure time meaningfully.","furigana":"よか|ゆういぎ|す|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"定年後の余暇を利用して、陶芸教室に通っている。","romaji":"Teinen-go no yoka o riyō shite, tōgei kyōshitsu ni kayotte iru.","german":"He uses his free time in retirement to attend a pottery class.","furigana":"ていねんご|よか|りよう|とうげいきょうしつ|かよ"},{"japanese":"労働時間の短縮で、国民の余暇が増えた。","romaji":"Rōdō jikan no tanshuku de, kokumin no yoka ga fueta.","german":"Shorter working hours have given people more leisure time.","furigana":"ろうどうじかん|たんしゅく|こくみん|よか|ふ"}],"notes":"余暇 is the free time left over after work and duties; the word is literary and often appears in statistics, advice books and official documents. Typical are 余暇を過ごす・楽しむ・活用する and 余暇活動. In everyday speech people tend to say 暇, 自由な時間 or 休みの日; 暇 can also have the negative sense \"having nothing to do\", which 余暇 does not.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"暇つぶしに古本屋を覗いてみた。","romaji":"Himatsubushi ni furuhon'ya o nozoite mita.","german":"To kill time, I looked into a secondhand bookshop.","furigana":"ひま|ふるほんや|のぞ"},{"japanese":"このパズルは長い移動中のいい暇つぶしになる。","romaji":"Kono pazuru wa nagai idōchū no ii himatsubushi ni naru.","german":"This puzzle is a good way to pass the time on long trips.","furigana":"なが|いどうちゅう|ひま"},{"japanese":"会議が始まるまで、ロビーで新聞を読んで暇つぶしをした。","romaji":"Kaigi ga hajimaru made, robī de shinbun o yonde himatsubushi o shita.","german":"Until the meeting started, I killed time reading the newspaper in the lobby.","furigana":"かいぎ|はじ|しんぶん|よ|ひま"}],"notes":"暇つぶし is a pastime that bridges waiting time or boredom; the word is colloquial and very common. Typical frames are 暇つぶしに〜する, 暇つぶしをする and 〜はいい暇つぶしになる; as a verb phrase one says 暇をつぶす. Pejoratively it can also mean \"waste of time\": ただの暇つぶしにすぎない. Compared with the neutral 気晴らし (\"diversion, recreation\"), 暇つぶし stresses that one actually has nothing better to do.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"休日に旧市街を散策するのが好きだ。","romaji":"Kyūjitsu ni kyūshigai o sansaku suru no ga suki da.","german":"I like strolling through the old town on days off.","furigana":"きゅうじつ|きゅうしがい|さんさく|す"},{"japanese":"ホテルの周りには、湖畔を一周できる散策路が整備されている。","romaji":"Hoteru no mawari ni wa, kohan o isshū dekiru sansakuro ga seibi sarete iru.","german":"Around the hotel, a promenade has been laid out that goes all the way around the lakeshore.","furigana":"まわ|こはん|いっしゅう|さんさくろ|せいび"},{"japanese":"雨が上がったので、近くの森を少し散策してみた。","romaji":"Ame ga agatta node, chikaku no mori o sukoshi sansaku shite mita.","german":"The rain had stopped, so I took a short stroll through the nearby forest.","furigana":"あめ|あ|ちか|もり|すこ|さんさく"}],"notes":"散策 is a leisurely walk in which one enjoys the surroundings, for example in old towns, parks or by lakes; as a する-verb with を for the area walked through: 旧市街を散策する. It sounds more refined than 散歩 and is typical of travel guides and tourism texts (散策路, 散策コース, 町並み散策). For the daily walk or walking the dog, one says 散歩; even more literary is 逍遥.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"庭園を逍遥しながら詩を詠んだ。","romaji":"Teien o shōyō shinagara shi o yonda.","german":"He composed poems while strolling through the garden.","furigana":"ていえん|しょうよう|し|よ"},{"japanese":"哲学者は思索にふけりながら、川沿いの小道を逍遥した。","romaji":"Tetsugakusha wa shisaku ni fukerinagara, kawazoi no komichi o shōyō shita.","german":"The philosopher walked along the path by the river, lost in thought.","furigana":"てつがくしゃ|しさく|かわぞ|こみち|しょうよう"},{"japanese":"アリストテレスの一派は、歩きながら講義したことから逍遥学派と呼ばれる。","romaji":"Arisutoteresu no ippa wa, arukinagara kōgi shita koto kara shōyō gakuha to yobareru.","german":"Aristotle's school is called the Peripatetic school because lectures were given while walking.","furigana":"いっぱ|ある|こうぎ|しょうようがくは|よ"}],"notes":"逍遥 is a literary word for aimless, unhurried wandering, often lost in thought; as a する-verb with を for the place: 庭園を逍遥する. It is hardly heard in spoken Japanese, where people say 散歩 or, more formally, 散策. It is known from 逍遥学派, the \"Peripatetic school\" of Aristotle, and from the pen name of the writer 坪内逍遥.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"陶器の箸置きをお土産に買った。","romaji":"Tōki no hashioki o omiyage ni katta.","german":"I bought a ceramic chopstick rest as a souvenir.","furigana":"とうき|はしお|みやげ|か"},{"japanese":"食事の途中で箸を置くときは、器の上ではなく箸置きに置きましょう。","romaji":"Shokuji no tochū de hashi o oku toki wa, utsuwa no ue de wa naku hashioki ni okimashō.","german":"When you put your chopsticks down during a meal, place them on the chopstick rest, not across the bowl.","furigana":"しょくじ|とちゅう|はし|お|うつわ|うえ|はしお|お"},{"japanese":"箸置きがなかったので、箸袋を折って代わりにした。","romaji":"Hashioki ga nakatta node, hashibukuro o otte kawari ni shita.","german":"There was no chopstick rest, so I folded the chopstick wrapper to use instead.","furigana":"はしお|はしぶくろ|お|か"}],"notes":"箸置き is the small rest on which you lay the tips of your chopsticks so that they do not touch the table; one says 箸を箸置きに置く. They come in ceramic, glass or wood, often with seasonal motifs, and are a popular souvenir. Distinguish 箸立て (a container for chopsticks standing upright) and 箸袋 (the paper wrapper), from which one folds a makeshift 箸置き in a restaurant if necessary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"客間に座布団を並べてお客様を迎えた。","romaji":"Kyakuma ni zabuton o narabete okyakusama o mukaeta.","german":"I laid out floor cushions in the guest room and welcomed the guests.","furigana":"きゃくま|ざぶとん|なら|きゃくさま|むか"},{"japanese":"どうぞ座布団をお使いください。","romaji":"Dōzo zabuton o otsukai kudasai.","german":"Please, use a floor cushion.","furigana":"ざぶとん|つか"},{"japanese":"横綱が負けると、観客席から座布団が舞った。","romaji":"Yokozuna ga makeru to, kankyakuseki kara zabuton ga matta.","german":"When the yokozuna lost, floor cushions flew from the spectator seats.","furigana":"よこづな|ま|かんきゃくせき|ざぶとん|ま"}],"notes":"座布団 is the flat, usually rectangular floor cushion on which one kneels or sits on tatami; it is counted with 枚. Typical are 座布団を敷く・出す・すすめる and 座布団に座る; a guest is first offered a 座布団, and it is considered rude to step on one. Well known are the flying 座布団 at sumo when a yokozuna loses. A Western cushion for a sofa or chair is called クッション.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"引越しの荷造りに三日もかかった。","romaji":"Hikkoshi no nizukuri ni mikka mo kakatta.","german":"Packing for the move took as many as three days.","furigana":"ひっこ|にづく|みっか"},{"japanese":"来月、会社の近くに引越しすることになった。","romaji":"Raigetsu, kaisha no chikaku ni hikkoshi suru koto ni natta.","german":"Next month I will be moving close to my company.","furigana":"らいげつ|かいしゃ|ちか|ひっこ"},{"japanese":"春は引越しシーズンなので、業者の予約が取りにくい。","romaji":"Haru wa hikkoshi shīzun na node, gyōsha no yoyaku ga torinikui.","german":"Spring is moving season, so it is hard to book a moving company.","furigana":"はる|ひっこ|ぎょうしゃ|よやく|と"}],"notes":"引越し is the move of a home or office; as a する-verb: 引越しする, and as a verb also 引っ越す (東京に引っ越す, 〜から〜へ引っ越す). The most common spelling is 引っ越し; 引越し without the small っ is seen mainly in company names and advertising (引越し業者, 引越センター). Typical are 引越しの準備, 引越し祝い and 引越しそば. Officially, a change of residence is called 転居, and the relocation of a company or office 移転.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"年末に家族総出で大掃除をする。","romaji":"Nenmatsu ni kazoku sōde de ōsōji o suru.","german":"Am Jahresende machen wir mit der ganzen Familie einen Großputz.","furigana":"ねんまつ|かぞくそうで|おおそうじ"},{"japanese":"大掃除で、押し入れの奥から昔のアルバムが出てきた。","romaji":"Ōsōji de, oshiire no oku kara mukashi no arubamu ga dete kita.","german":"Beim Großputz kam hinten im Wandschrank ein altes Fotoalbum zum Vorschein.","furigana":"おおそうじ|お|い|おく|むかし|で"},{"japanese":"新しい社長は、組織の大掃除に乗り出した。","romaji":"Atarashii shachō wa, soshiki no ōsōji ni noridashita.","german":"Der neue Firmenchef machte sich daran, im Unternehmen gründlich aufzuräumen.","furigana":"あたら|しゃちょう|そしき|おおそうじ|の|だ"}],"notes":"大掃除 ist ein gründlicher Großputz, bei dem man auch Ecken und Schränke reinigt, die sonst liegen bleiben. In Japan ist vor allem 年末の大掃除 Tradition: Vor Neujahr wird Haus, Schule oder Büro gründlich geputzt, ähnlich dem deutschen Frühjahrsputz. Man sagt 大掃除をする. Übertragen bedeutet es auch „gründlich aufräumen, säubern“ in Organisationen: 組織の大掃除.","levelNote":"Compound: 大 (N3) + 掃除 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"六月になると衣替えの季節だ。","romaji":"Rokugatsu ni naru to koromogae no kisetsu da.","german":"June is the season for changing into summer clothes.","furigana":"ろくがつ|ころもが|きせつ"},{"japanese":"週末に衣替えをして、冬物をクローゼットにしまった。","romaji":"Shūmatsu ni koromogae o shite, fuyumono o kurōzetto ni shimatta.","german":"At the weekend I switched over my wardrobe and put the winter clothes away in the closet.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|ころもが|ふゆもの"},{"japanese":"駅前の書店が、カフェを併設した店舗に衣替えした。","romaji":"Ekimae no shoten ga, kafe o heisetsu shita tenpo ni koromogae shita.","german":"The bookstore in front of the station has been remodeled into a shop with a café attached.","furigana":"えきまえ|しょてん|へいせつ|てんぽ|ころもが"}],"notes":"衣替え is the seasonal change of clothing: one puts away the summer or winter clothes and takes out the others. Traditionally, school and work uniforms switch to summer wear on June 1 and to winter wear on October 1. One says 衣替えをする. Figuratively, 衣替え also means \"makeover, new look\", for example when a shop is refurbished: 〜に衣替えする. Older spelling: 更衣.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"家事を分担することで夫婦円満になる。","romaji":"Kaji o buntan suru koto de fūfu enman ni naru.","german":"Sharing the housework keeps a marriage harmonious.","furigana":"かじ|ぶんたん|ふうふえんまん"},{"japanese":"共働きなので、週末にまとめて家事をしている。","romaji":"Tomobataraki na node, shūmatsu ni matomete kaji o shite iru.","german":"Since we both work, we do the housework all at once on the weekend.","furigana":"ともばたら|しゅうまつ|かじ"},{"japanese":"家事の都合により、本日は休ませていただきます。","romaji":"Kaji no tsugō ni yori, honjitsu wa yasumasete itadakimasu.","german":"Due to family circumstances, I will take the day off today.","furigana":"かじ|つごう|ほんじつ|やす"}],"notes":"家事 is housework, that is, cooking, cleaning, laundry and the like: 家事をする, 家事を分担する, 家事と育児, 家事代行 (\"housekeeping service\"). In formal excuses, 家事 also means \"family matters\": 家事の都合により. Individual tasks have their own words, such as 炊事 (cooking) and 洗濯 (laundry). Not to be confused with the identically pronounced 火事 (\"fire\").","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 22 (source: N4; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"炊事場の設備が老朽化している。","romaji":"Suijiba no setsubi ga rōkyūka shite iru.","german":"The equipment in the kitchen area is aging.","furigana":"すいじば|せつび|ろうきゅうか"},{"japanese":"キャンプでは、炊事当番が朝早く起きてご飯を炊いた。","romaji":"Kyanpu de wa, suiji tōban ga asa hayaku okite gohan o taita.","german":"At camp, the cooking-duty person got up early and cooked the rice.","furigana":"すいじとうばん|あさはや|お|はん|た"},{"japanese":"母は炊事、洗濯、掃除を一人でこなしていた。","romaji":"Haha wa suiji, sentaku, sōji o hitori de konashite ita.","german":"My mother handled cooking, laundry and cleaning all by herself.","furigana":"はは|すいじ|せんたく|そうじ|ひとり"}],"notes":"炊事 is cooking and kitchen work as a household duty, often in the list 炊事・洗濯・掃除. Typical are 炊事場 (cooking area at campsites or in communal lodgings), 炊事当番 (\"kitchen duty\") and 炊事をする. The word sounds somewhat old-fashioned or factual; for cooking as an activity or a dish one says 料理, and for cooking for oneself 自炊.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"祖母は裁縫が得意で何でも縫ってくれた。","romaji":"Sobo wa saihō ga tokui de nan demo nutte kureta.","german":"My grandmother was good at sewing and made anything for us.","furigana":"そぼ|さいほう|とくい|なん|ぬ"},{"japanese":"家庭科の授業で、裁縫道具を使ってエプロンを作った。","romaji":"Kateika no jugyō de, saihō dōgu o tsukatte epuron o tsukutta.","german":"In home economics class, we made an apron using sewing tools.","furigana":"かていか|じゅぎょう|さいほうどうぐ|つか|つく"},{"japanese":"裁縫は苦手で、ボタン付けもうまくできない。","romaji":"Saihō wa nigate de, botan-tsuke mo umaku dekinai.","german":"I'm bad at sewing - I can't even sew on a button properly.","furigana":"さいほう|にがて|つ"}],"notes":"裁縫 is sewing as a skill, by hand or by machine: 裁縫が得意・苦手, 裁縫道具, 裁縫箱. At school one learns it in the subject 家庭科. The verb itself is 縫う (\"to sew\"); 手芸 is the umbrella term for handicrafts such as knitting and embroidery, and 洋裁 and 和裁 are the tailoring of Western and Japanese clothing respectively.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"靴下の繕いをする人は最近少ない。","romaji":"Kutsushita no tsukuroi o suru hito wa saikin sukunai.","german":"Few people these days darn socks.","furigana":"くつした|つくろ|ひと|さいきんすく"},{"japanese":"夜になると、母はよく繕い物をしていた。","romaji":"Yoru ni naru to, haha wa yoku tsukuroimono o shite ita.","german":"In the evening my mother often did mending.","furigana":"よる|はは|つくろ|もの"},{"japanese":"漁師たちは港で網の繕いに追われていた。","romaji":"Ryōshitachi wa minato de ami no tsukuroi ni owarete ita.","german":"The fishermen were busy mending their nets at the harbor.","furigana":"りょうし|みなと|あみ|つくろ|お"}],"notes":"繕い is mending and patching, above all of clothing, socks or nets; it is the noun of the transitive godan verb 繕う (穴を繕う). Common is 繕い物 (\"mending, things to be mended\"). The word sounds somewhat old-fashioned; today people mostly say 直す, 修理 or, for clothes, お直し. 繕う also has the figurative meaning \"to keep up appearances\": 体裁を繕う.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"週末に常備菜を作り置きしておくと平日が楽だ。","romaji":"Shūmatsu ni jōbisai o tsukurioki shite oku to heijitsu ga raku da.","german":"If you prepare side dishes in advance on the weekend, weekdays are easier.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|じょうびさい|つく|お|へいじつ|らく"},{"japanese":"きんぴらごぼうは日持ちするので、常備菜にぴったりだ。","romaji":"Kinpira gobō wa himochi suru node, jōbisai ni pittari da.","german":"Kinpira burdock keeps well, so it is perfect as a make-ahead side dish.","furigana":"ひも|じょうびさい"},{"japanese":"常備菜は清潔な容器に入れて、三、四日で食べきるようにしている。","romaji":"Jōbisai wa seiketsu na yōki ni irete, san, yokka de tabekiru yō ni shite iru.","german":"I put make-ahead side dishes in clean containers and try to finish them within three or four days.","furigana":"じょうびさい|せいけつ|ようき|い|さん|よっか|た"}],"notes":"常備菜 (\"side dishes always kept in stock\") are side dishes cooked in larger quantities and kept in the refrigerator for several days, such as きんぴら, ひじきの煮物, 煮豆 or pickles. One says 常備菜を作る or 常備菜を作り置きする. 作り置き is the more colloquial word and stresses cooking in advance, whereas 常備菜 stresses the long-keeping side dish itself. The word is common in cookbooks and household advice books.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"災害に備えて保存食を備蓄している。","romaji":"Saigai ni sonaete hozonshoku o bichiku shite iru.","german":"I keep a stock of non-perishable food in case of disasters.","furigana":"さいがい|そな|ほぞんしょく|びちく"},{"japanese":"梅干しや漬物は、昔ながらの保存食だ。","romaji":"Umeboshi ya tsukemono wa, mukashinagara no hozonshoku da.","german":"Umeboshi and pickles are traditional preserved foods.","furigana":"うめぼ|つけもの|むかし|ほぞんしょく"},{"japanese":"冬が長い地方では、秋のうちに保存食を作っておく習慣がある。","romaji":"Fuyu ga nagai chihō de wa, aki no uchi ni hozonshoku o tsukutte oku shūkan ga aru.","german":"In regions with long winters, it is customary to prepare preserved food in autumn.","furigana":"ふゆ|なが|ちほう|あき|ほぞんしょく|つく|しゅうかん"}],"notes":"保存食 is food that keeps for a long time through drying, pickling, smoking or canning, such as 梅干し, 漬物, 干物 or 缶詰. Typical are 保存食を作る, 保存食を備蓄する and 昔ながらの保存食. Supplies meant specifically for disasters are called 非常食; 保存食 is broader and also covers traditional food storage.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"父は毎晩の晩酌を楽しみにしている。","romaji":"Chichi wa maiban no banshaku o tanoshimi ni shite iru.","german":"My father looks forward to his evening drink every night.","furigana":"ちち|まいばん|ばんしゃく|たの"},{"japanese":"健康診断の結果を見て、晩酌をビール一本までに減らした。","romaji":"Kenkō shindan no kekka o mite, banshaku o bīru ippon made ni herashita.","german":"After seeing my health checkup results, I cut my evening drink down to one bottle of beer.","furigana":"けんこうしんだん|けっか|み|ばんしゃく|いっぽん|へ"},{"japanese":"今夜の晩酌のお供は、冷ややっこと枝豆だ。","romaji":"Kon'ya no banshaku no otomo wa, hiyayakko to edamame da.","german":"Tonight's accompaniments for the evening drink are chilled tofu and edamame.","furigana":"こんや|ばんしゃく|とも|ひ|えだまめ"}],"notes":"晩酌 is the little glass of beer or sake one drinks at home with dinner; as a する-verb: 晩酌する. Typical are 晩酌を楽しむ, 毎晩の晩酌 and 晩酌のお供 (snacks to go with it). Distinguish drinking out (飲みに行く) and 寝酒, the nightcap before going to bed. The modern, more casual word for drinking at home is 家飲み.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"枝豆は酒の肴にぴったりだ。","romaji":"Edamame wa sake no sakana ni pittari da.","german":"Edamame is the perfect accompaniment to sake.","furigana":"えだまめ|さけ|さかな"},{"japanese":"昔話を肴に、旧友と夜遅くまで飲んだ。","romaji":"Mukashibanashi o sakana ni, kyūyū to yoru osoku made nonda.","german":"Using old stories as our topic, I drank with an old friend until late at night.","furigana":"むかしばなし|さかな|きゅうゆう|よるおそ|の"},{"japanese":"この店は日本酒に合う肴がそろっている。","romaji":"Kono mise wa nihonshu ni au sakana ga sorotte iru.","german":"This place has a good selection of small dishes that go well with sake.","furigana":"みせ|にほんしゅ|あ|さかな"}],"notes":"肴 is anything one eats with alcohol, such as nibbles or small dishes; the set phrase is 酒の肴. The word arose from 酒 + 菜 (\"side dish\") and is homophonous with 魚 (\"fish\"), which often leads to confusion. Figuratively, 肴 is also a topic of conversation or a performance that livens up a drinking party: 〜を肴に飲む. Colloquially, for snacks to go with drinks, people tend to say つまみ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"茶托に湯呑みを載せてお客様に出す。","romaji":"Chataku ni yunomi o nosete okyakusama ni dasu.","german":"I put the teacup on its saucer and serve it to the guest.","furigana":"ちゃたく|ゆの|の|きゃくさま|だ"},{"japanese":"お茶を飲むときは、茶托ごと持ち上げず、湯呑みだけを持つ。","romaji":"Ocha o nomu toki wa, chataku-goto mochiagezu, yunomi dake o motsu.","german":"When drinking tea, don't lift the saucer along with it; hold only the teacup.","furigana":"ちゃ|の|ちゃたく|も|あ|ゆの|も"},{"japanese":"祖母は来客用に、漆塗りの茶托を大切にしまっている。","romaji":"Sobo wa raikyakuyō ni, urushinuri no chataku o taisetsu ni shimatte iru.","german":"My grandmother carefully keeps lacquered tea saucers for guests.","furigana":"そぼ|らいきゃくよう|うるしぬ|ちゃたく|たいせつ"}],"notes":"茶托 is the small stand of wood, lacquer or metal on which a Japanese teacup (湯呑み) is served. When serving guests, including in the office, 茶托に載せて出す is considered polite; but one drinks only from the cup and does not lift the 茶托 along with it. For coffee cups one says ソーサー, for glass coasters コースター.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"土瓶で沸かしたお湯はまろやかだ。","romaji":"Dobin de wakashita oyu wa maroyaka da.","german":"Water heated in a clay kettle tastes mellow.","furigana":"どびん|わ|ゆ"},{"japanese":"秋の料亭で、松茸の土瓶蒸しを味わった。","romaji":"Aki no ryōtei de, matsutake no dobinmushi o ajiwatta.","german":"In autumn, at a fine restaurant, I enjoyed matsutake mushroom soup steamed in a small clay teapot.","furigana":"あき|りょうてい|まつたけ|どびんむ|あじ"},{"japanese":"祖母は土瓶でほうじ茶をたっぷり入れてくれた。","romaji":"Sobo wa dobin de hōjicha o tappuri irete kureta.","german":"My grandmother brewed plenty of hōjicha for me in a clay teapot.","furigana":"そぼ|どびん|ちゃ|い"}],"notes":"土瓶 is an earthenware pot with a bail handle (つる) over the lid, in which one brews tea such as ほうじ茶 or 番茶 or, in the past, decocted medicinal herbs. Well known is the dish 土瓶蒸し, a soup steamed in a small pot, usually with matsutake. Distinguish 急須 (a small teapot with a side handle for green tea) and やかん (a kettle for boiling water).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"夏の夜は蚊取り線香が欠かせない。","romaji":"Natsu no yoru wa katorisenkō ga kakasenai.","german":"A mosquito coil is indispensable on summer nights.","furigana":"なつ|よる|かと|せんこう|か"},{"japanese":"縁側で蚊取り線香をたいて、夕涼みをした。","romaji":"Engawa de katorisenkō o taite, yūsuzumi o shita.","german":"We lit a mosquito coil on the veranda and enjoyed the evening cool.","furigana":"えんがわ|かと|せんこう|ゆうすず"},{"japanese":"蚊取り線香の匂いをかぐと、子どもの頃の夏休みを思い出す。","romaji":"Katorisenkō no nioi o kagu to, kodomo no koro no natsuyasumi o omoidasu.","german":"When I smell mosquito coils, I remember the summer vacations of my childhood.","furigana":"かと|せんこう|にお|こ|ころ|なつやす|おも|だ"}],"notes":"蚊取り線香 is the green incense coil against mosquitoes that one lights in summer: 蚊取り線香をつける or 蚊取り線香をたく. In Japan its smell is regarded as the typical scent of summer; the coil often sits in a pig-shaped holder (蚊遣り豚). Electric versions are called 電子蚊取り. Literally: \"mosquito-catching incense stick\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"旅館の部屋に、一人ずつお膳が運ばれてきた。","romaji":"Ryokan no heya ni, hitori zutsu ozen ga hakobarete kita.","german":"In the ryokan room, a tray meal was brought to each person.","furigana":"りょかん|へや|ひとり|ぜん|はこ"},{"japanese":"ご飯をもう一膳いかがですか。","romaji":"Gohan o mō ichizen ikaga desu ka.","german":"Would you like another bowl of rice?","furigana":"はん|いちぜん"},{"japanese":"食事が済んだら、膳を台所に下げてください。","romaji":"Shokuji ga sundara, zen o daidokoro ni sagete kudasai.","german":"When you have finished eating, please take the trays back to the kitchen.","furigana":"しょくじ|す|ぜん|だいどころ|さ"}],"notes":"膳 is the small individual table or tray, often with legs, on which each guest is served at traditional meals; usually one says politely お膳: お膳を運ぶ, お膳を下げる. As a counter, 膳 counts bowls of rice (ご飯一膳) and pairs of chopsticks (箸一膳). Related are 食膳 (\"dining table, meal\") and the expression 据え膳 (\"a meal set out and ready\", figuratively: an opportunity offered on a plate).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"桐の箪笥は着物の保管に最適だ。","romaji":"Kiri no tansu wa kimono no hokan ni saiteki da.","german":"A paulownia chest is ideal for storing kimono.","furigana":"きり|たんす|きもの|ほかん|さいてき"},{"japanese":"冬物のセーターを箪笥の引き出しにしまった。","romaji":"Fuyumono no seetaa o tansu no hikidashi ni shimatta.","german":"I put my winter sweaters away in the drawer of the chest.","furigana":"ふゆもの|たんす|ひ|だ"},{"japanese":"祖母は銀行を信用せず、現金をたんすの奥に隠していた。","romaji":"Sobo wa ginkou o shin'you sezu, genkin o tansu no oku ni kakushite ita.","german":"My grandmother didn't trust banks and hid her cash at the back of the chest of drawers.","furigana":"そぼ|ぎんこう|しんよう|げんきん|おく|かく"}],"notes":"箪笥 is the traditional piece of furniture with drawers for clothing, so more a \"chest of drawers\"; it is almost always written たんす or タンス. In compounds it becomes だんす: 洋服だんす \"wardrobe\", 茶だんす \"dish cabinet\". Clothes are put away with 箪笥にしまう; also well known is たんす預金 \"cash hoarded at home\". A built-in closet, by contrast, is called 押し入れ or クローゼット.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 44 (source: N4; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"若くして所帯を持つのは大変だ。","romaji":"Wakaku shite shotai o motsu no wa taihen da.","german":"It is hard to set up a household of your own at a young age.","furigana":"わか|しょたい|も|たいへん"},{"japanese":"結婚してから、彼女は急に所帯じみてきた。","romaji":"Kekkon shite kara, kanojo wa kyū ni shotaijimite kita.","german":"Since getting married, she has suddenly started to look domesticated.","furigana":"けっこん|かのじょ|きゅう|しょたい"},{"japanese":"うちの部は部員が百人を超える大所帯だ。","romaji":"Uchi no bu wa buin ga hyakunin o koeru ōjotai da.","german":"Our club is a big group, with over a hundred members.","furigana":"ぶ|ぶいん|ひゃくにん|こ|おおじょたい"}],"notes":"所帯 is the household as a unit of living and economy and sounds down-to-earth. Important phrases: 所帯を持つ (\"to set up a household of one's own\", usually through marriage), 所帯持ち (\"married, with a family\"), 所帯じみる (\"to look worn down by domestic life\") and 大所帯 (\"large group, large operation\"). In statistics and official language, 世帯 is used instead.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"風邪を引いたので養生に努めている。","romaji":"Kaze o hiita node yōjō ni tsutomete iru.","german":"I have a cold, so I am trying to take care of myself.","furigana":"かぜ|ひ|ようじょう|つと"},{"japanese":"引越しの前に、業者が廊下の壁を養生してくれた。","romaji":"Hikkoshi no mae ni, gyōsha ga rōka no kabe o yōjō shite kureta.","german":"Before the move, the movers covered the corridor walls with protective material.","furigana":"ひっこ|まえ|ぎょうしゃ|ろうか|かべ|ようじょう"},{"japanese":"医者の不養生とはよく言ったもので、先生自身が過労で倒れた。","romaji":"Isha no fuyōjō to wa yoku itta mono de, sensei jishin ga karō de taoreta.","german":"As the saying goes, \"a doctor doesn't look after his own health\": the doctor himself collapsed from overwork.","furigana":"いしゃ|ふようじょう|い|せんせいじしん|かろう|たお"}],"notes":"養生 first means taking care of one's health and resting after an illness: 養生する, 養生に努める, and in letters ご養生ください. The opposite is 不養生, known from the proverb 医者の不養生 (\"doctors take the least care of themselves\"). On construction sites and when moving, 養生 means covering with protective material: 床を養生する, 養生テープ, 養生シート; for concrete, letting it cure. For recovery under medical supervision, people tend to say 療養.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"温泉地で療養生活を送っている。","romaji":"Onsenchi de ryōyō seikatsu o okutte iru.","german":"He is convalescing at a hot spring resort.","furigana":"おんせんち|りょうようせいかつ|おく"},{"japanese":"医師の指示で、二週間の自宅療養をすることになった。","romaji":"Ishi no shiji de, ni-shūkan no jitaku ryōyō o suru koto ni natta.","german":"On the doctor's orders, I have to recuperate at home for two weeks.","furigana":"いし|しじ|にしゅうかん|じたくりょうよう"},{"japanese":"部長は病気療養のため、しばらく休職するそうだ。","romaji":"Buchō wa byōki ryōyō no tame, shibaraku kyūshoku suru sō da.","german":"The department head is reportedly taking leave for a while to recover from an illness.","furigana":"ぶちょう|びょうきりょうよう|きゅうしょく"}],"notes":"療養 is recovery, usually lengthy, under medical treatment; as an intransitive する-verb: 療養する. Typical are 療養中, 自宅療養, 病気療養のため, 療養生活 and 療養所 (\"sanatorium\"). Distinguish 治療 (the treatment itself), 静養 (recovery through rest, without illness) and 養生 (general health care, more everyday). 療養 sounds objective and is often used in official announcements.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"通夜に参列して故人を偲んだ。","romaji":"Tsuya ni sanretsu shite kojin o shinonda.","german":"I attended the wake and remembered the deceased.","furigana":"つや|さんれつ|こじん|しの"},{"japanese":"仕事の都合で告別式には出られないので、お通夜に伺うことにした。","romaji":"Shigoto no tsugō de kokubetsushiki ni wa derarenai node, otsuya ni ukagau koto ni shita.","german":"I can't attend the funeral service because of work, so I decided to go to the wake.","furigana":"しごと|つごう|こくべつしき|で|つや|うかが"},{"japanese":"試合に負けた後のロッカールームは、まるでお通夜のようだった。","romaji":"Shiai ni maketa ato no rokkārūmu wa, marude otsuya no yō datta.","german":"After losing the game, the locker room felt just like a wake.","furigana":"しあい|ま|あと|つや"}],"notes":"通夜 is the wake held on the evening before the funeral; usually one says お通夜: お通夜に行く・参列する・伺う. Originally people kept watch over the deceased all night; today the ceremony usually lasts one to two hours in the evening, and afterwards there is often 通夜振る舞い, a meal for the mourners. On the following day come the 葬儀 and the 告別式. Figuratively, お通夜のよう describes a gloomy, silent mood.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"告別式は近親者のみで執り行われた。","romaji":"Kokubetsushiki wa kinshinsha nomi de toriokonawareta.","german":"The funeral service was held for close relatives only.","furigana":"こくべつしき|きんしんしゃ|と|おこな"},{"japanese":"告別式の後、出棺を見送った。","romaji":"Kokubetsushiki no ato, shukkan o miokutta.","german":"After the funeral service, we saw the coffin off.","furigana":"こくべつしき|あと|しゅっかん|みおく"},{"japanese":"明日の告別式には、黒いネクタイを締めて行かなければならない。","romaji":"Ashita no kokubetsushiki ni wa, kuroi nekutai o shimete ikanakereba naranai.","german":"I have to wear a black tie to tomorrow's funeral service.","furigana":"あした|こくべつしき|くろ|し|い"}],"notes":"告別式 is the farewell ceremony at which relatives and acquaintances take leave of the deceased; it usually takes place on the day after the 通夜 and is often combined with the religious part as 葬儀・告別式. Typical are 告別式に参列する, 告別式が執り行われる and 近親者のみで. In everyday speech people say 葬式, formally 葬儀; a later memorial gathering is called お別れの会 or 偲ぶ会.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"初七日の法要を営んだ。","romaji":"Shonanoka no hōyō o itonanda.","german":"We held the memorial service for the seventh day after the death.","furigana":"しょなのか|ほうよう|いとな"},{"japanese":"遠方の親戚に配慮して、初七日は葬儀と同じ日に行った。","romaji":"Enpō no shinseki ni hairyo shite, shonanoka wa sōgi to onaji hi ni okonatta.","german":"Out of consideration for relatives living far away, we held the seventh-day service on the same day as the funeral.","furigana":"えんぽう|しんせき|はいりょ|しょなのか|そうぎ|おな|ひ|おこな"},{"japanese":"祖母の初七日が過ぎて、家族はようやく少し落ち着いた。","romaji":"Sobo no shonanoka ga sugite, kazoku wa yōyaku sukoshi ochitsuita.","german":"Once the seventh day after my grandmother's death had passed, the family finally calmed down a little.","furigana":"そぼ|しょなのか|す|かぞく|すこ|お|つ"}],"notes":"初七日 is, in Buddhist mourning practice, the seventh day after death (the day of death counts) and the service held on that day; the standard reading is しょなのか, but one also hears しょなぬか. One says 初七日の法要を営む or 行う. Today the service is often held on the day of the burial itself (繰り上げ初七日), so that relatives do not have to travel again. Further memorial days follow up to the 四十九日; the word belongs to ceremonial vocabulary.","levelNote":"Compound: 初 (N1) + 七日 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"年始の挨拶回りに近所を訪ねた。","romaji":"Nenshi no aisatsumawari ni kinjo o tazuneta.","german":"I visited the neighbors for New Year courtesy calls.","furigana":"ねんし|あいさつまわ|きんじょ|たず"},{"japanese":"引っ越してきたら、両隣と上下の部屋に挨拶回りをするのが一般的だ。","romaji":"Hikkoshite kitara, ryōdonari to jōge no heya ni aisatsumawari o suru no ga ippanteki da.","german":"After moving in, it is common to make courtesy calls on the neighbors on both sides and the units above and below.","furigana":"ひ|こ|りょうどなり|じょうげ|へや|あいさつまわ|いっぱんてき"},{"japanese":"新しい担当者として、取引先へ挨拶回りに行った。","romaji":"Atarashii tantōsha to shite, torihikisaki e aisatsumawari ni itta.","german":"As the new person in charge, I made courtesy calls on our business partners.","furigana":"あたら|たんとうしゃ|とりひきさき|あいさつまわ|い"}],"notes":"挨拶回り is going around to several people or places to introduce oneself or say goodbye. Typical occasions: after a move, to the neighbors (often with a small gift); at the start of the year, to business partners (年始の挨拶回り); on taking up a post or being transferred. One says 挨拶回りをする or 挨拶回りに行く; often あいさつ回り is written half in kana.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"防災の心得を日頃から確認しておく。","romaji":"Bōsai no kokoroe o higoro kara kakunin shite oku.","german":"I always check the basic rules of disaster preparedness.","furigana":"ぼうさい|こころえ|ひごろ|かくにん"},{"japanese":"彼女は柔道の心得があるので、いざというときも落ち着いていた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa jūdō no kokoroe ga aru node, iza to iu toki mo ochitsuite ita.","german":"She has some training in judo, so she stayed calm even in an emergency.","furigana":"かのじょ|じゅうどう|こころえ|お|つ"},{"japanese":"入社初日に、新入社員の心得を記した冊子を渡された。","romaji":"Nyūsha shonichi ni, shinnyū shain no kokoroe o shirushita sasshi o watasareta.","german":"On my first day at the company, I was handed a booklet setting out guidelines for new employees.","furigana":"にゅうしゃしょにち|しんにゅうしゃいん|こころえ|しる|さっし|わた"}],"notes":"心得 has several meanings: \"knowledge, mastery\" of a skill (茶道の心得がある, \"to know one's way around the tea ceremony\"), \"basic rules, rules of conduct\" (防災の心得, 新入社員の心得) and \"inner attitude\". Placed after a title, 〜心得 denotes an acting rank, e.g. 課長心得. The underlying verb is the ichidan verb 心得る (\"to understand, to know\"), known from 心得ました.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"茶道は大人の嗜みとして人気がある。","romaji":"Sadō wa otona no tashinami to shite ninki ga aru.","german":"The tea ceremony is popular as a cultured pursuit for adults.","furigana":"さどう|おとな|たしな|にんき"},{"japanese":"人前で大声で話さないのは、社会人としての嗜みだ。","romaji":"Hitomae de ōgoe de hanasanai no wa, shakaijin to shite no tashinami da.","german":"Not talking loudly in public is part of good manners for a working adult.","furigana":"ひとまえ|おおごえ|はな|しゃかいじん|たしな"},{"japanese":"彼は書道の嗜みがあり、年賀状もすべて筆で書く。","romaji":"Kare wa shodō no tashinami ga ari, nengajō mo subete fude de kaku.","german":"He is skilled in calligraphy and writes even all his New Year cards with a brush.","furigana":"かれ|しょどう|たしな|ねんがじょう|ふで|か"}],"notes":"嗜み is usually written in kana as たしなみ and denotes, on the one hand, cultivated skill in the arts (茶道・書道のたしなみがある), and on the other, good manners, restraint and propriety (大人のたしなみ, 社会人としてのたしなみ). Related is 身だしなみ (\"neat appearance\"). The verb 嗜む (godan, transitive) means \"to pursue something with connoisseurship, to enjoy in moderation\": お酒を嗜む, 俳句を嗜む.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"古い町並みの佇まいに心が和む。","romaji":"Furui machinami no tatazumai ni kokoro ga nagomu.","german":"The atmosphere of the old townscape soothes the heart.","furigana":"ふる|まちな|たたず|こころ|なご"},{"japanese":"その旅館は、創業当時の佇まいを今も残している。","romaji":"Sono ryokan wa, sōgyō tōji no tatazumai o ima mo nokoshite iru.","german":"The inn still preserves the look it had when it was founded.","furigana":"りょかん|そうぎょうとうじ|たたず|いま|のこ"},{"japanese":"着物姿の彼女の凛とした佇まいに、皆が見とれた。","romaji":"Kimono sugata no kanojo no rin to shita tatazumai ni, mina ga mitoreta.","german":"Everyone was spellbound by her dignified bearing in a kimono.","furigana":"きものすがた|かのじょ|りん|たたず|みな|み"}],"notes":"佇まい is the calm overall appearance or aura of a place, building or person. Typical are 町の佇まい, 静かな佇まい, 落ち着いた佇まい and, for people, 凛とした佇まい. It derives from 佇む (\"to stand still\") and sounds elevated and literary; often it is written in kana as たたずまい. More neutral are 雰囲気 (\"atmosphere\") and 外観 (\"outward appearance\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"花火大会は夏の風物詩だ。","romaji":"Hanabi taikai wa natsu no fūbutsushi da.","german":"Fireworks festivals are a hallmark of summer.","furigana":"はなびたいかい|なつ|ふうぶつし"},{"japanese":"焼き芋屋の呼び声は、冬の風物詩として親しまれている。","romaji":"Yakiimo-ya no yobigoe wa, fuyu no fūbutsushi to shite shitashimarete iru.","german":"The calls of the roasted sweet potato vendors are a familiar feature of winter.","furigana":"や|いもや|よ|ごえ|ふゆ|ふうぶつし|した"},{"japanese":"帰省ラッシュは、年末の風物詩としてニュースで毎年伝えられる。","romaji":"Kisei rasshu wa, nenmatsu no fūbutsushi to shite nyūsu de maitoshi tsutaerareru.","german":"The holiday homecoming rush is reported in the news every year as a fixture of the year's end.","furigana":"きせい|ねんまつ|ふうぶつし|まいとしつた"}],"notes":"風物詩 is something typical of a particular season that makes it palpable: a sight, sound, event or food. It almost always appears in the frame 〜は夏の風物詩だ or 冬の風物詩として親しまれている. Literally it is a \"poem about scenery and the seasons\" (meaning 2, rare today). Distinguish 名物 (\"local specialty\") and 季語 (the seasonal word in haiku).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"年中行事を通じて季節を感じる。","romaji":"Nenchū gyōji o tsūjite kisetsu o kanjiru.","german":"One senses the seasons through annual events.","furigana":"ねんちゅうぎょうじ|つう|きせつ|かん"},{"japanese":"節分やひな祭りなど、日本の年中行事を子どもに教えている。","romaji":"Setsubun ya hinamatsuri nado, Nihon no nenchū gyōji o kodomo ni oshiete iru.","german":"I teach my children Japan's annual events such as Setsubun and Hinamatsuri.","furigana":"せつぶん|まつ|にほん|ねんちゅうぎょうじ|こ|おし"},{"japanese":"年末の大掃除は我が家の年中行事になっている。","romaji":"Nenmatsu no ōsōji wa wagaya no nenchū gyōji ni natte iru.","german":"The big year-end cleaning has become an annual ritual in our home.","furigana":"ねんまつ|おおそうじ|わ|や|ねんちゅうぎょうじ"}],"notes":"年中行事 are the festivals and customs that recur every year at the same time, such as 正月, 節分, ひな祭り or お盆; one speaks of 日本の年中行事 or 宮中の年中行事. Figuratively it also denotes something that reliably repeats every year, often slightly ironically: 〜はもう年中行事になっている. Mind the reading: ねんちゅう, not ねんじゅう as in 年中 \"all year round\".","levelNote":"Compound: 年中 (N3) + 行事 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地域の仕来りに従って祭りを行う。","romaji":"Chiiki no shikitari ni shitagatte matsuri o okonau.","german":"The festival is held in accordance with local customs.","furigana":"ちいき|しきた|したが|まつ|おこな"},{"japanese":"嫁いだ先の家のしきたりに慣れるまで時間がかかった。","romaji":"Totsuida saki no ie no shikitari ni nareru made jikan ga kakatta.","german":"It took time until I got used to the customs of the family I married into.","furigana":"とつ|さき|いえ|な|じかん"},{"japanese":"古いしきたりにとらわれず、新しいやり方を試すべきだ。","romaji":"Furui shikitari ni torawarezu, atarashii yarikata o tamesu beki da.","german":"We should try new ways instead of being bound by old conventions.","furigana":"ふる|あたら|かた|ため"}],"notes":"しきたり are handed-down rules and practices of a family, place or industry that one is expected to observe: しきたりに従う／しきたりを守る／しきたりを破る, 古いしきたり, 家のしきたり. Compared with 習慣 \"habit\" (also personal) and 風習 \"folk custom\", しきたり stresses the binding character, often with an overtone of narrowness. The word is usually written in kana; 仕来り is rare.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この村には古くからの慣わしが残っている。","romaji":"Kono mura ni wa furuku kara no narawashi ga nokotte iru.","german":"Old customs remain in this village.","furigana":"むら|ふる|なら|のこ"},{"japanese":"正月には家族そろって初詣に行くのが我が家の慣わしだ。","romaji":"Shōgatsu ni wa kazoku sorotte hatsumōde ni iku no ga wagaya no narawashi da.","german":"In our family, it is the custom to go together for the first shrine visit of the year at New Year.","furigana":"しょうがつ|かぞく|はつもうで|い|わ|や|なら"},{"japanese":"新入社員が最初の朝礼で挨拶するのが、この会社の慣わしになっている。","romaji":"Shinnyū shain ga saisho no chōrei de aisatsu suru no ga, kono kaisha no narawashi ni natte iru.","german":"In this company, it is customary for new employees to introduce themselves at their first morning meeting.","furigana":"しんにゅうしゃいん|さいしょ|ちょうれい|あいさつ|かいしゃ|なら"}],"notes":"慣わし (also 習わし) is a custom that has long been practiced. Typical is the frame 〜のが慣わしだ or 〜のが慣わしになっている \"it is the custom that ...\", and also 古くからの慣わし. Compared with しきたり, 慣わし sounds less binding and more descriptive; compared with 習慣, more refined and slightly literary. The set phrase 世の習い means \"the way of the world\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"店仕舞いの時間が近づいてきた。","romaji":"Misejimai no jikan ga chikazuite kita.","german":"The shop's closing time is approaching.","furigana":"みせじま|じかん|ちか"},{"japanese":"今日の授業はこれでおしまいです。","romaji":"Kyō no jugyō wa kore de oshimai desu.","german":"That's all for today's class.","furigana":"きょう|じゅぎょう"},{"japanese":"彼は最初は黙っていたが、しまいには怒り出した。","romaji":"Kare wa saisho wa damatte ita ga, shimai ni wa okoridashita.","german":"He was silent at first, but in the end he got angry.","furigana":"かれ|さいしょ|だま|おこ|だ"}],"notes":"しまい means \"end, finish\" and belongs to the verb 仕舞う \"to finish; to put away\". In daily life one hears mainly おしまい \"that's it, it's over\" (これでおしまい) and しまいには \"in the end, finally\", mostly for an unpleasant escalation. In compounds it becomes じまい: 店じまい \"closing time; closing down a business\", 〜ずじまい \"ended up not doing\". It is usually written in kana.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"後始末をきちんとしてから帰る。","romaji":"Atoshimatsu o kichinto shite kara kaeru.","german":"I tidy up properly before going home.","furigana":"あとしまつ|かえ"},{"japanese":"彼は遅刻ばかりか、ついには無断欠勤する始末だ。","romaji":"Kare wa chikoku bakari ka, tsui ni wa mudan kekkin suru shimatsu da.","german":"Not only is he always late, it has now come to the point where he stays away from work without notice.","furigana":"かれ|ちこく|むだんけっきん|しまつ"},{"japanese":"この子はいたずらばかりして、本当に始末に負えない。","romaji":"Kono ko wa itazura bakari shite, hontō ni shimatsu ni oenai.","german":"This child does nothing but play pranks; he is really unmanageable.","furigana":"こ|ほんとう|しまつ|お"}],"notes":"始末 has several important uses. As a transitive する-verb it means \"to deal with, get rid of, dispose of\": ゴミを始末する, 後始末 \"cleaning up afterwards\". 〜始末だ describes a bad final outcome: 〜する始末だ \"and in the end it came to the point where ...\"; 始末に負えない and 始末が悪い mean \"unmanageable, hard to handle\". 始末書 is a written letter of apology at the workplace; the meaning \"thrift\" (始末する, 始末屋) is rather old-fashioned.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"少ない収入で遣り繰りするのは大変だ。","romaji":"Sukunai shūnyū de yarikuri suru no wa taihen da.","german":"It is hard to make ends meet on a small income.","furigana":"すく|しゅうにゅう|や|く|たいへん"},{"japanese":"仕事と育児の合間に時間をやりくりして、資格の勉強をしている。","romaji":"Shigoto to ikuji no aima ni jikan o yarikuri shite, shikaku no benkyō o shite iru.","german":"I squeeze out time between work and childcare to study for a qualification.","furigana":"しごと|いくじ|あいま|じかん|しかく|べんきょう"},{"japanese":"母は家計のやりくりが上手で、毎月少しずつ貯金していた。","romaji":"Haha wa kakei no yarikuri ga jōzu de, maitsuki sukoshi zutsu chokin shite ita.","german":"My mother was good at managing the household budget and put a little aside every month.","furigana":"はは|かけい|じょうず|まいつきすこ|ちょきん"}],"notes":"やりくり is skillful management of scarce resources in order to somehow get by. Typical are 家計のやりくり, 少ない収入でやりくりする and, figuratively, 時間をやりくりする \"to squeeze time out of a busy schedule\"; someone who is good at it is やりくり上手. Compared with 節約 \"saving\", やりくり stresses juggling limited resources, not the saving itself. It is almost always written in kana.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"倹約家の妻のおかげで貯金が増えた。","romaji":"Ken'yakuka no tsuma no okage de chokin ga fueta.","german":"Thanks to my thrifty wife, our savings have grown.","furigana":"けんやくか|つま|ちょきん|ふ"},{"japanese":"旅行の費用をためるため、外食を控えて倹約している。","romaji":"Ryokō no hiyō o tameru tame, gaishoku o hikaete ken'yaku shite iru.","german":"To save money for the trip, I am cutting back on eating out and living frugally.","furigana":"りょこう|ひよう|がいしょく|ひか|けんやく"},{"japanese":"倹約も度が過ぎると、ただのけちだと思われる。","romaji":"Ken'yaku mo do ga sugiru to, tada no kechi da to omowareru.","german":"If you take frugality too far, people will think you are just stingy.","furigana":"けんやく|ど|す|おも"}],"notes":"倹約 is deliberate thrift in one's own spending; as a する-verb: 食費を倹約する or simply 倹約して暮らす. A thrifty person is a 倹約家. The more everyday 節約 can also be applied to time, electricity or water, whereas 倹約 is used almost only for money and way of life and sounds somewhat more refined. Excessive thrift is pejoratively called けち.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"質素な暮らしの中にも幸せはある。","romaji":"Shisso na kurashi no naka ni mo shiawase wa aru.","german":"There is happiness even in a simple life.","furigana":"しっそ|く|なか|しあわ"},{"japanese":"祖父は社長になっても、質素な服装を好んだ。","romaji":"Sofu wa shachō ni natte mo, shisso na fukusō o kononda.","german":"Even after becoming company president, my grandfather preferred plain clothing.","furigana":"そふ|しゃちょう|しっそ|ふくそう|この"},{"japanese":"結婚式は身内だけで質素に行った。","romaji":"Kekkonshiki wa miuchi dake de shisso ni okonatta.","german":"We held the wedding simply, with family only.","furigana":"けっこんしき|みうち|しっそ|おこな"}],"notes":"質素 is a な-adjective for a deliberately simple, modest way of life without luxury: 質素な暮らし／食事／服装, 質素に暮らす. It usually has a positive overtone of frugality and contentment; the opposite is 贅沢. 簡素, by contrast, stresses leaving out the superfluous in forms and procedures (簡素な手続き), and 地味 the unobtrusive in appearance.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"無精者の彼は部屋がいつも散らかっている。","romaji":"Bushōmono no kare wa heya ga itsumo chirakatte iru.","german":"He is a lazy person, and his room is always messy.","furigana":"ぶしょうもの|かれ|へや|ち"},{"japanese":"連休中は無精ひげを伸ばしたまま過ごした。","romaji":"Renkyūchū wa bushō hige o nobashita mama sugoshita.","german":"During the long holiday weekend I spent my time letting my stubble grow.","furigana":"れんきゅうちゅう|ぶしょう|の|す"},{"japanese":"筆無精なので、年賀状の返事もつい遅れてしまう。","romaji":"Fudebushō na node, nengajō no henji mo tsui okurete shimau.","german":"I am bad at writing letters, so I often end up replying late even to New Year cards.","furigana":"ふでぶしょう|ねんがじょう|へんじ|おく"}],"notes":"無精 (also 不精) is the unwillingness to bother with everyday matters; it is used as a noun and as a な-adjective. Compounds are very common: 無精ひげ \"stubble (from not bothering to shave)\", 筆無精 \"bad at writing letters\", 出不精 \"homebody\", 無精者 \"lazybones\". Compared with the formal 怠惰 \"idleness\", 無精 is everyday and refers more to small lapses of care than to fundamental laziness.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"祖父の代から旅館を営んでいる。","romaji":"Sofu no dai kara ryokan o itonande iru.","german":"We have been running a ryokan since my grandfather's generation.","furigana":"そふ|だい|りょかん|いとな"},{"japanese":"人は一人では社会生活を営むことができない。","romaji":"Hito wa hitori de wa shakai seikatsu o itonamu koto ga dekinai.","german":"A person cannot lead a social life alone.","furigana":"ひと|ひとり|しゃかいせいかつ|いとな"},{"japanese":"父の三回忌の法要を寺で営んだ。","romaji":"Chichi no sankaiki no hōyō o tera de itonanda.","german":"We held the memorial service for the second anniversary of my father's death at the temple.","furigana":"ちち|さんかいき|ほうよう|てら|いとな"}],"notes":"営む is a transitive, slightly elevated verb with three uses: to run a business (店・旅館・農業を営む), to lead a life (生活を営む, 社会生活を営む) and to hold a religious ceremony (法要を営む). Compared with 経営する, 営む stresses less the management than the day-to-day running, often of a family business; in conversation one simply says 店をやっている. The related noun is 営み \"activity, doings\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"母が心を込めて弁当を拵えてくれた。","romaji":"Haha ga kokoro o komete bentō o koshiraete kureta.","german":"My mother lovingly made a lunch box for me.","furigana":"はは|こころ|こ|べんとう|こしら"},{"japanese":"旅行の費用をこしらえるために、毎月少しずつ貯金している。","romaji":"Ryokou no hiyou o koshiraeru tame ni, maitsuki sukoshi zutsu chokin shite iru.","german":"To raise the money for the trip, I save a little every month.","furigana":"りょこう|ひよう|まいつきすこ|ちょきん"},{"japanese":"彼は遅刻のたびに、もっともらしい言い訳をこしらえる。","romaji":"Kare wa chikoku no tabi ni, mottomorashii iiwake o koshiraeru.","german":"Every time he is late, he comes up with a plausible excuse.","furigana":"かれ|ちこく|い|わけ"}],"notes":"こしらえる is a somewhat old-fashioned, colloquial word for \"to make, to put together\" and is mostly written in kana; the kanji 拵える is rare. Compared with the neutral 作る, it sounds like handiwork and effort: 弁当をこしらえる, 料理をこしらえる. By extension it also means \"to raise money\" (金をこしらえる) or \"to think something up\" (言い訳をこしらえる). Transitive with を, with no intransitive counterpart.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"猫の寝顔を見ると心が和む。","romaji":"Neko no negao o miru to kokoro ga nagomu.","german":"When I see a cat sleeping, my heart softens.","furigana":"ねこ|ねがお|み|こころ|なご"},{"japanese":"彼の冗談で会議の雰囲気が少し和んだ。","romaji":"Kare no jōdan de kaigi no fun'iki ga sukoshi nagonda.","german":"His joke lightened the mood of the meeting a little.","furigana":"かれ|じょうだん|かいぎ|ふんいき|すこ|なご"},{"japanese":"窓辺の花が、病室の空気を和ませてくれる。","romaji":"Madobe no hana ga, byōshitsu no kūki o nagomasete kureru.","german":"The flowers by the window brighten the atmosphere of the hospital room.","furigana":"まどべ|はな|びょうしつ|くうき|なご"}],"notes":"和む is intransitive: the heart, mood or atmosphere becomes gentle and relaxed, usually because of something lovable. Typical are 心が和む, 気持ちが和む and 場が和む. The transitive counterpart is the causative form 和ませる: 冗談で場を和ませる \"to lighten the mood with a joke\". Related are 和やか \"harmonious, friendly\" and 和らぐ, which is used more for easing pain or cold.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一日頑張った自分をねぎらって、晩酌を楽しむ。","romaji":"Ichinichi ganbatta jibun o negiratte, banshaku o tanoshimu.","german":"I reward myself for a hard day's work by enjoying an evening drink.","furigana":"いちにちがんば|じぶん|ばんしゃく|たの"},{"japanese":"社長は社員一人一人の労をねぎらった。","romaji":"Shachō wa shain hitori hitori no rō o negiratta.","german":"The president thanked each employee for their efforts.","furigana":"しゃちょう|しゃいんひとりひとり|ろう"},{"japanese":"大会が終わった後、監督は選手たちを温かい言葉でねぎらった。","romaji":"Taikai ga owatta ato, kantoku wa senshutachi o atatakai kotoba de negiratta.","german":"After the tournament, the coach acknowledged the players with warm words.","furigana":"たいかい|お|あと|かんとく|せんしゅ|あたた|ことば"}],"notes":"ねぎらう means to thank someone for their trouble and acknowledge it; the set phrase is 〜の労をねぎらう. It comes from equals or superiors (boss to employee, coach to player); toward superiors it sounds presumptuous, and one says お礼を言う or 感謝する instead. Newer but very common is 自分をねぎらう \"to treat oneself\". Mostly written in kana.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"寒さを忍んで早朝の散歩に出かけた。","romaji":"Samusa o shinonde sōchō no sanpo ni dekaketa.","german":"Putting up with the cold, I went out for an early-morning walk.","furigana":"さむ|しの|そうちょう|さんぽ|で"},{"japanese":"恥を忍んでお願いしますが、お金を少し貸していただけませんか。","romaji":"Haji o shinonde onegai shimasu ga, okane o sukoshi kashite itadakemasen ka.","german":"I am embarrassed to ask, but could you lend me a little money?","furigana":"はじ|しの|ねが|かね|すこ|か"},{"japanese":"二人は人目を忍んで会っていた。","romaji":"Futari wa hitome o shinonde atte ita.","german":"The two met in secret, avoiding other people's eyes.","furigana":"ふたり|ひとめ|しの|あ"}],"notes":"忍ぶ has two meanings. \"To endure, to bear\": 寒さを忍ぶ, 恥を忍んで \"swallowing one's shame\", 見るに忍びない \"cannot bear to look\". \"To hide oneself, to do secretly\": 人目を忍ぶ \"to hide from others' eyes\", 忍び込む \"to sneak in\". Do not confuse it with the identically read 偲ぶ \"to recall with longing, to remember fondly\". The word is rather elevated and occurs mainly in set phrases.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"手際よく料理を仕上げる。","romaji":"Tegiwa yoku ryōri o shiageru.","german":"She finishes the cooking efficiently.","furigana":"てぎわ|りょうり|しあ"},{"japanese":"新人なのに、彼女は手際よく受付の仕事をこなしている。","romaji":"Shinjin na no ni, kanojo wa tegiwa yoku uketsuke no shigoto o konashite iru.","german":"Although she is new, she handles the reception work efficiently.","furigana":"しんじん|かのじょ|てぎわ|うけつけ|しごと"},{"japanese":"救急隊員の手際よい対応で、けが人はすぐに病院へ運ばれた。","romaji":"Kyūkyū taiin no tegiwa yoi taiō de, keganin wa sugu ni byōin e hakobareta.","german":"Thanks to the paramedics' efficient response, the injured were taken to the hospital right away.","furigana":"きゅうきゅうたいいん|てぎわ|たいおう|にん|びょういん|はこ"}],"notes":"手際よい describes someone carrying out a task quickly, in an orderly way and without unnecessary steps; the adverb 手際よく (手際よく片付ける) is the most common form. Alongside it are the separated forms 手際がよい／手際のよい and the opposite 手際が悪い. 器用 refers more to manual dexterity in general, 要領がいい to a knack for organizing work cleverly (sometimes conveniently). Also written 手際良い.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"閑静な住宅街に住んでいる。","romaji":"Kansei na jūtakugai ni sunde iru.","german":"I live in a quiet residential area.","furigana":"かんせい|じゅうたくがい|す"},{"japanese":"このホテルは駅から近いのに、周囲は意外と閑静だ。","romaji":"Kono hoteru wa eki kara chikai no ni, shūi wa igai to kansei da.","german":"Although this hotel is close to the station, the surroundings are surprisingly quiet.","furigana":"えき|ちか|しゅうい|いがい|かんせい"},{"japanese":"定年後は郊外の閑静な土地に家を建てたい。","romaji":"Teinengo wa kōgai no kansei na tochi ni ie o tatetai.","german":"After retirement, I want to build a house in a quiet place on the outskirts.","furigana":"ていねんご|こうがい|かんせい|とち|いえ|た"}],"notes":"閑静 is a な-adjective for a pleasantly quiet area secluded from noise. It is used almost only for residential areas and places, especially in 閑静な住宅街, a standard formula in real estate ads. For people or sounds one says 静か; 静寂 is the stillness itself, のどか peaceful rural calm. The word sounds somewhat elevated and literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"責任を取って辞職を申し出た。","romaji":"Sekinin o totte jishoku o mōshideta.","german":"He offered to resign to take responsibility.","furigana":"せきにん|と|じしょく|もう|で"},{"japanese":"不祥事が発覚し、市長は辞職に追い込まれた。","romaji":"Fushōji ga hakkaku shi, shichō wa jishoku ni oikomareta.","german":"After the scandal came to light, the mayor was forced to resign.","furigana":"ふしょうじ|はっかく|しちょう|じしょく|お|こ"},{"japanese":"内閣が総辞職し、新しい首相が選ばれた。","romaji":"Naikaku ga sōjishoku shi, atarashii shushō ga erabareta.","german":"The cabinet resigned en masse, and a new prime minister was chosen.","furigana":"ないかく|そうじしょく|あたら|しゅしょう|えら"}],"notes":"辞職 is resignation from an office or position, above all for civil servants, politicians and executives; as an intransitive する-verb: 辞職する. Typical are 辞職を申し出る, 辞職に追い込まれる, 引責辞職 \"resignation to take responsibility\" and 内閣総辞職. 辞任 refers to a particular post (会長を辞任する), 退職 to leaving employment in general, including retirement; in everyday speech people say 辞める.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"病気療養のため半年間休職した。","romaji":"Byōki ryōyō no tame hantoshikan kyūshoku shita.","german":"I took half a year off work to recover from an illness.","furigana":"びょうきりょうよう|はんとしかんきゅうしょく"},{"japanese":"休職中の社員が来月から職場に復帰する。","romaji":"Kyūshokuchū no shain ga raigetsu kara shokuba ni fukki suru.","german":"The employee on leave will return to the workplace next month.","furigana":"きゅうしょくちゅう|しゃいん|らいげつ|しょくば|ふっき"},{"japanese":"親の介護のために休職するか、退職するか迷っている。","romaji":"Oya no kaigo no tame ni kyūshoku suru ka, taishoku suru ka mayotte iru.","german":"I can't decide whether to take leave to care for my parent or to resign.","furigana":"おや|かいご|きゅうしょく|たいしょく|まよ"}],"notes":"休職 is a temporary leave in which the employment relationship continues, for example because of illness, caregiving or study; as an intransitive する-verb: 休職する. Common are 休職中, 休職届 and the return, 復職. Do not confuse it with 休暇 \"vacation\", 休業 \"business closure\" or the legally regulated 育児休業 \"childcare leave\", nor with 退職, the final departure.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"係長から課長に昇格した。","romaji":"Kakarichō kara kachō ni shōkaku shita.","german":"I was promoted from section chief to department head.","furigana":"かかりちょう|かちょう|しょうかく"},{"japanese":"そのチームは今季優勝し、一部リーグへの昇格を決めた。","romaji":"Sono chīmu wa konki yūshō shi, ichibu rīgu e no shōkaku o kimeta.","german":"The team won the championship this season and secured promotion to the top league.","furigana":"こんきゆうしょう|いちぶ|しょうかく|き"},{"japanese":"業績が認められ、営業所が支店に昇格した。","romaji":"Gyōseki ga mitomerare, eigyōsho ga shiten ni shōkaku shita.","german":"In recognition of its results, the sales office was upgraded to a branch.","furigana":"ぎょうせき|みと|えいぎょうしょ|してん|しょうかく"}],"notes":"昇格 is advancement to a higher level or status; as an intransitive する-verb: 課長に昇格する, causative 昇格させる. It applies not only to people but also to teams (一部リーグに昇格) and organizations (支店に昇格). 昇進, by contrast, refers only to a person's advancement to a higher post. The opposite is 降格.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"本社への栄転が決まりお祝いの席を設けた。","romaji":"Honsha e no eiten ga kimari oiwai no seki o mōketa.","german":"The transfer with promotion to headquarters was decided, so we held a celebration.","furigana":"ほんしゃ|えいてん|き|いわ|せき|もう"},{"japanese":"部長、このたびはご栄転おめでとうございます。","romaji":"Buchō, kono tabi wa go-eiten omedetō gozaimasu.","german":"Department head, congratulations on your promotion and transfer.","furigana":"ぶちょう|えいてん"},{"japanese":"地方への異動は左遷かと思ったが、実は栄転だった。","romaji":"Chihō e no idō wa sasen ka to omotta ga, jitsu wa eiten datta.","german":"I thought the transfer to the provinces was a demotion, but it was actually a promotion.","furigana":"ちほう|いどう|させん|おも|じつ|えいてん"}],"notes":"栄転 is a transfer that is at the same time a promotion; as an intransitive する-verb: 本社に栄転する. The opposite is 左遷 \"demotion by transfer\". Because it expresses recognition, it is used mainly politely about others: ご栄転おめでとうございます. About oneself one speaks more modestly of 異動 or 転勤.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい校長が着任の挨拶をした。","romaji":"Atarashii kōchō ga chakunin no aisatsu o shita.","german":"The new principal gave an address on taking office.","furigana":"あたら|こうちょう|ちゃくにん|あいさつ"},{"japanese":"彼は四月に大阪支社の支社長として着任した。","romaji":"Kare wa shigatsu ni Ōsaka shisha no shishachō to shite chakunin shita.","german":"He took up his post as head of the Osaka branch in April.","furigana":"かれ|しがつ|おおさかししゃ|ししゃちょう|ちゃくにん"},{"japanese":"新しい大使は着任早々、首相を表敬訪問した。","romaji":"Atarashii taishi wa chakunin sōsō, shushō o hyōkei hōmon shita.","german":"Right after taking up his post, the new ambassador paid a courtesy call on the prime minister.","furigana":"あたら|たいし|ちゃくにんそうそう|しゅしょう|ひょうけいほうもん"}],"notes":"着任 is arriving at the new place of duty and taking up a new post, typical of teachers, civil servants, diplomats and branch managers; as an intransitive する-verb: 〜に着任する, 〜として着任する. Common are 着任の挨拶 and 着任早々 \"right after taking up one's post\"; the counterpart is 離任. 赴任 stresses the journey or move to the place of duty, 就任 the assumption of an office, as in 社長に就任する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"専門家に調査を委嘱した。","romaji":"Senmonka ni chōsa o ishoku shita.","german":"We commissioned an expert to carry out the survey.","furigana":"せんもんか|ちょうさ|いしょく"},{"japanese":"大学教授が市の審議会委員に委嘱された。","romaji":"Daigaku kyōju ga shi no shingikai iin ni ishoku sareta.","german":"A university professor was appointed as a member of the city council.","furigana":"だいがくきょうじゅ|し|しんぎかいいいん|いしょく"},{"japanese":"委嘱状を受け取り、正式に相談員としての活動を始めた。","romaji":"Ishokujō o uketori, seishiki ni sōdan'in to shite no katsudō o hajimeta.","german":"After receiving the letter of appointment, I formally began my activities as a counselor.","furigana":"いしょくじょう|う|と|せいしき|そうだんいん|かつどう|はじ"}],"notes":"委嘱 is a formal word for entrusting a task or an office to someone outside one's own organization: XにYを委嘱する, passive 〜に委嘱される. Typical are 委員を委嘱する, 委嘱状 \"letter of appointment\" and 委嘱を受ける. 委託 refers more to outsourcing business to companies, 依頼 to a general request, 任命 to an appointment within a hierarchy.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"建設工事を請負で受注した。","romaji":"Kensetsu kōji o ukeoi de juchū shita.","german":"We won the construction work on a contract basis.","furigana":"けんせつこうじ|うけおい|じゅちゅう"},{"japanese":"工務店と請負契約を結び、家の建築が始まった。","romaji":"Kōmuten to ukeoi keiyaku o musubi, ie no kenchiku ga hajimatta.","german":"We signed a contract with the builder, and the construction of the house began.","furigana":"こうむてん|うけおいけいやく|むす|いえ|けんちく|はじ"},{"japanese":"彼は会社員ではなく、請負でシステム開発の仕事をしている。","romaji":"Kare wa kaishain de wa naku, ukeoi de shisutemu kaihatsu no shigoto o shite iru.","german":"He is not a company employee but does system development work on a contract basis.","furigana":"かれ|かいしゃいん|うけおい|かいはつ|しごと"}],"notes":"請負 is undertaking work for payment against an agreed result, that is, a contract for work from the contractor's point of view. Typical are 請負契約, 請負業者, 請負工事 and 請負で働く; the related verb is 請け負う. The spelling 請け負い also occurs. Unlike 派遣 \"temporary agency work\", under 請負 one does not work under the instructions of the client.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"下請企業への支払いが遅れている。","romaji":"Shitauke kigyō e no shiharai ga okurete iru.","german":"Payments to subcontractors are delayed.","furigana":"したうけきぎょう|しはら|おく"},{"japanese":"大手メーカーは部品の製造を中小企業に下請けに出している。","romaji":"Ōte mēkā wa buhin no seizō o chūshō kigyō ni shitauke ni dashite iru.","german":"Large manufacturers outsource the production of parts to small and medium-sized firms.","furigana":"おおて|ぶひん|せいぞう|ちゅうしょうきぎょう|したう|だ"},{"japanese":"うちの会社は長年、大手建設会社の下請けとして仕事をしてきた。","romaji":"Uchi no kaisha wa naganen, ōte kensetsu gaisha no shitauke to shite shigoto o shite kita.","german":"Our company has worked for years as a subcontractor for a major construction company.","furigana":"かいしゃ|ながねん|おおてけんせつがいしゃ|したう|しごと"}],"notes":"下請 (usually written 下請け) is a subcontract, or the subcontractor who takes over part of the work from the main contractor 元請. Typical are 下請けに出す \"to subcontract out\", 下請け企業／下請け業者 and 下請けとして働く; one level further down is 孫請け. The word often carries an overtone of dependence, for example in 下請けいじめ \"bullying of suppliers\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"元請企業が工事全体の管理を行う。","romaji":"Motouke kigyō ga kōji zentai no kanri o okonau.","german":"The main contractor manages the construction as a whole.","furigana":"もとうけきぎょう|こうじぜんたい|かんり|おこな"},{"japanese":"元請から下請けへの代金の支払いが遅れ、問題になった。","romaji":"Motouke kara shitauke e no daikin no shiharai ga okure, mondai ni natta.","german":"Payment from the main contractor to the subcontractors was delayed, which became a problem.","furigana":"もとうけ|したう|だいきん|しはら|おく|もんだい"},{"japanese":"今回はうちが元請として、市から直接工事を受注した。","romaji":"Konkai wa uchi ga motouke to shite, shi kara chokusetsu kōji o juchū shita.","german":"This time, as prime contractor, we received the construction order directly from the city.","furigana":"こんかい|もとうけ|し|ちょくせつこうじ|じゅちゅう"}],"notes":"元請 (also 元請け) is the main contractor who receives the order directly from the client (発注者) and passes parts of it on to 下請け. The word comes mainly from the construction and IT industries: 元請業者, 元請会社, 元請として受注する. The counterpart is 下請け, and one level below that is 孫請け.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新工場が来月から稼働する予定だ。","romaji":"Shin kōjō ga raigetsu kara kadō suru yotei da.","german":"The new factory is scheduled to start operating next month.","furigana":"しんこうじょう|らいげつ|かどう|よてい"},{"japanese":"夏場は電力需要が増えるため、発電所をフル稼働させている。","romaji":"Natsuba wa denryoku juyō ga fueru tame, hatsudensho o furu kadō sasete iru.","german":"Because demand for electricity rises in summer, the power plants are running at full capacity.","furigana":"なつば|でんりょくじゅよう|ふ|はつでんしょ|かどう"},{"japanese":"新しい会計システムは十月から本番稼働する。","romaji":"Atarashii kaikei shisutemu wa jūgatsu kara honban kadō suru.","german":"The new accounting system goes live in October.","furigana":"あたら|かいけい|じゅうがつ|ほんばんかどう"}],"notes":"稼働 (also 稼動) means that machines, plants or systems are in operation; mostly intransitive: 工場が稼働する, 稼働中, 24時間稼働. For putting something into operation one usually uses 稼働させる; フル稼働 means \"running at full capacity\", 稼働率 \"utilization rate\", and in IT 本番稼働 \"going live\". 運転 refers to operating a machine, 作動 to the functioning of a mechanism; 操業 means the operation of a factory as a whole.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"老朽化した設備で操業を続けるのは危険だ。","romaji":"Rōkyūka shita setsubi de sōgyō o tsuzukeru no wa kiken da.","german":"It is dangerous to continue operating with aging equipment.","furigana":"ろうきゅうか|せつび|そうぎょう|つづ|きけん"},{"japanese":"台風の影響で、工場は二日間操業を停止した。","romaji":"Taifū no eikyō de, kōjō wa futsukakan sōgyō o teishi shita.","german":"Because of the typhoon, the factory suspended operations for two days.","furigana":"たいふう|えいきょう|こうじょう|ふつかかんそうぎょう|ていし"},{"japanese":"受注の減少により、操業短縮を余儀なくされた。","romaji":"Juchū no genshō ni yori, sōgyō tanshuku o yoginaku sareta.","german":"Because of the decline in orders, we were forced to reduce operating hours.","furigana":"じゅちゅう|げんしょう|そうぎょうたんしゅく|よぎ"}],"notes":"操業 is the ongoing operation of a factory, mine or fishing business as an economic activity; as an intransitive する-verb: 工場が操業する. Typical are 操業を開始する／停止する／再開する, 操業中 and 操業短縮 \"short-time work, production cutback\". 稼働 refers more to individual machines and plants. Note: the identically read 創業 means \"founding\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"歩合制の給与体系で働いている。","romaji":"Buaisei no kyūyo taikei de hataraite iru.","german":"I work under a commission-based pay system.","furigana":"ぶあいせい|きゅうよたいけい|はたら"},{"japanese":"不動産の営業は、売り上げに応じて歩合が支払われる。","romaji":"Fudōsan no eigyō wa, uriage ni ōjite buai ga shiharawareru.","german":"In real estate sales, commission is paid according to sales.","furigana":"ふどうさん|えいぎょう|う|あ|おう|ぶあい|しはら"},{"japanese":"日本銀行はかつて公定歩合を引き下げて景気を刺激した。","romaji":"Nippon Ginkō wa katsute kōtei buai o hikisagete keiki o shigeki shita.","german":"The Bank of Japan once lowered the official discount rate to stimulate the economy.","furigana":"にっぽんぎんこう|こうていぶあい|ひ|さ|けいき|しげき"}],"notes":"歩合 has two meanings. In the working world it is the sales-based commission: 歩合制 \"commission system\", 歩合給, 完全歩合制 \"pure commission basis\". In general it denotes a ratio or percentage, today mainly in set terms such as 公定歩合 \"discount rate (of the central bank)\". For fees one says 手数料, for everyday ratios 割合.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今年の賞与は業績好調で増額された。","romaji":"Kotoshi no shōyo wa gyōseki kōchō de zōgaku sareta.","german":"This year's bonus was increased thanks to strong results.","furigana":"ことし|しょうよ|ぎょうせきこうちょう|ぞうがく"},{"japanese":"当社では賞与を年二回、六月と十二月に支給しています。","romaji":"Tōsha de wa shōyo o nen nikai, rokugatsu to jūnigatsu ni shikyū shite imasu.","german":"Our company pays a bonus twice a year, in June and December.","furigana":"とうしゃ|しょうよ|ねんにかい|ろくがつ|じゅうにがつ|しきゅう"},{"japanese":"業績悪化のため、冬の賞与は見送られることになった。","romaji":"Gyōseki akka no tame, fuyu no shōyo wa miokurareru koto ni natta.","german":"Because of worsening results, the winter bonus will be skipped.","furigana":"ぎょうせきあっか|ふゆ|しょうよ|みおく"}],"notes":"賞与 is the formal word for the bonus payment that in Japan is usually paid twice a year (summer and winter) in addition to the monthly salary. It appears in employment contracts, job ads and on pay slips: 賞与を支給する, 賞与年2回, 冬季賞与. In conversation people say ボーナス. The monthly salary is called 給与, allowances 手当.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"福利厚生が充実した企業は人気が高い。","romaji":"Fukuri kōsei ga jūjitsu shita kigyō wa ninki ga takai.","german":"Companies with excellent employee benefits are very popular.","furigana":"ふくりこうせい|じゅうじつ|きぎょう|にんき|たか"},{"japanese":"この会社は住宅手当や社員食堂など、福利厚生が手厚い。","romaji":"Kono kaisha wa jūtaku teate ya shain shokudō nado, fukuri kōsei ga teatsui.","german":"This company has generous benefits, such as housing allowance and an employee cafeteria.","furigana":"かいしゃ|じゅうたくてあて|しゃいんしょくどう|ふくりこうせい|てあつ"},{"japanese":"転職先を選ぶときは、給料だけでなく福利厚生も確認したほうがいい。","romaji":"Tenshokusaki o erabu toki wa, kyūryō dake de naku fukuri kōsei mo kakunin shita hō ga ii.","german":"When choosing where to change jobs, it is better to check the benefits as well as the salary.","furigana":"てんしょくさき|えら|きゅうりょう|ふくりこうせい|かくにん"}],"notes":"福利厚生 covers all benefits an employer provides beyond salary, such as housing allowance, a cafeteria, recreation facilities, or subsidies for leisure and further education. Typical are 福利厚生が充実している／手厚い, 福利厚生費 and 福利厚生施設. In job hunting it is an important criterion alongside 給与. The word belongs to the language of personnel departments and job ads.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"就業規則の改定案が労使間で協議されている。","romaji":"Shūgyō kisoku no kaitei an ga rōshikan de kyōgi sarete iru.","german":"A draft revision of the work rules is being discussed between labor and management.","furigana":"しゅうぎょうきそく|かいていあん|ろうしかん|きょうぎ"},{"japanese":"副業が認められているかどうか、就業規則で確認した。","romaji":"Fukugyō ga mitomerarete iru ka dō ka, shūgyō kisoku de kakunin shita.","german":"I checked the work rules to see whether side jobs are permitted.","furigana":"ふくぎょう|みと|しゅうぎょうきそく|かくにん"},{"japanese":"就業規則に違反したとして、社員が懲戒処分を受けた。","romaji":"Shūgyō kisoku ni ihan shita to shite, shain ga chōkai shobun o uketa.","german":"An employee received disciplinary action for violating the work rules.","furigana":"しゅうぎょうきそく|いはん|しゃいん|ちょうかいしょぶん|う"}],"notes":"就業規則 are the work rules issued by the employer, setting out working hours, leave, pay and discipline; in Japan they are mandatory from ten employees under the 労働基準法. Typical phrases are 就業規則に定める, 就業規則に違反する, 就業規則を変更する. 社内規定 is the more general umbrella term for internal regulations. Technical language of personnel management and labor law.","levelNote":"Compound: 就業 (N1) + 規則 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"労使交渉が難航している。","romaji":"Rōshi kōshō ga nankō shite iru.","german":"Labor-management negotiations are proving difficult.","furigana":"ろうしこうしょう|なんこう"},{"japanese":"労使双方が歩み寄り、賃上げで合意した。","romaji":"Rōshi sōhō ga ayumiyori, chin'age de gōi shita.","german":"Both labor and management compromised and agreed on a pay raise.","furigana":"ろうしそうほう|あゆ|よ|ちんあ|ごうい"},{"japanese":"良好な労使関係を保つことが、会社の安定につながる。","romaji":"Ryōkō na rōshi kankei o tamotsu koto ga, kaisha no antei ni tsunagaru.","german":"Maintaining good labor-management relations contributes to the stability of the company.","furigana":"りょうこう|ろうしかんけい|たも|かいしゃ|あんてい"}],"notes":"労使 combines employees (労働者) and employers (使用者) as the two parties to labor relations. It appears almost always in compounds: 労使交渉 \"collective bargaining\", 労使関係, 労使協定, 労使紛争, 労使双方, 労使間. The word is typical of news and labor law; in conversation people tend to say 会社と組合 or the like.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"待遇改善を求めて罷業に踏み切った。","romaji":"Taigū kaizen o motomete higyō ni fumikitta.","german":"They resolved to strike, demanding better working conditions.","furigana":"たいぐうかいぜん|もと|ひぎょう|ふ|き"},{"japanese":"法律上、ストライキは「同盟罷業」と呼ばれる。","romaji":"Hōritsujō, sutoraiki wa \"dōmei higyō\" to yobareru.","german":"Legally, a strike is called \"dōmei higyō\".","furigana":"ほうりつじょう|どうめいひぎょう|よ"},{"japanese":"戦前の日本でも、労働者による罷業がたびたび起きた。","romaji":"Senzen no Nihon de mo, rōdōsha ni yoru higyō ga tabitabi okita.","german":"Even in prewar Japan, strikes by workers occurred repeatedly.","furigana":"せんぜん|にほん|ろうどうしゃ|ひぎょう|お"}],"notes":"罷業 is the formal, legal word for \"strike\"; in law it is called 同盟罷業. Typical are 罷業に踏み切る \"to go on strike\" and 罷業権 \"right to strike\". In everyday life and in the news today, people almost always say ストライキ or, shortened, スト; 罷業 is met mainly in legal texts and historical accounts. 怠業, by contrast, denotes deliberate slowdown.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"プロジェクト全体を統括する立場にある。","romaji":"Purojekuto zentai o tōkatsu suru tachiba ni aru.","german":"I am in a position to oversee the whole project.","furigana":"ぜんたい|とうかつ|たちば"},{"japanese":"彼女はアジア地域の営業を統括している。","romaji":"Kanojo wa Ajia chiiki no eigyō o tōkatsu shite iru.","german":"She oversees sales for the whole Asia region.","furigana":"かのじょ|ちいき|えいぎょう|とうかつ"},{"japanese":"各部署の意見を統括して、最終的な方針を決める。","romaji":"Kakubusho no iken o tōkatsu shite, saishūteki na hōshin o kimeru.","german":"We consolidate the views of each department and decide on the final policy.","furigana":"かくぶしょ|いけん|とうかつ|さいしゅうてき|ほうしん|き"}],"notes":"統括 means to bring several parts or departments together and manage them as a whole; as a transitive する-verb: 〜を統括する. Common are 統括責任者, 統括部長 and 統括本部. Do not confuse it with 総括 \"to take stock, to evaluate as a whole\" (一年を総括する). 管理 is more general, \"management, administration\", and 監督 \"supervision\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"委員会が改革案を提言した。","romaji":"Iinkai ga kaikaku an o teigen shita.","german":"The committee put forward a reform proposal.","furigana":"いいんかい|かいかくあん|ていげん"},{"japanese":"専門家グループは、少子化対策について政府への提言をまとめた。","romaji":"Senmonka gurūpu wa, shōshika taisaku ni tsuite seifu e no teigen o matometa.","german":"The expert group compiled recommendations to the government on countering the declining birthrate.","furigana":"せんもんか|しょうしかたいさく|せいふ|ていげん"},{"japanese":"若手社員の提言がきっかけで、在宅勤務制度が導入された。","romaji":"Wakate shain no teigen ga kikkake de, zaitaku kinmu seido ga dōnyū sareta.","german":"A proposal by younger employees led to the introduction of a work-from-home system.","furigana":"わかてしゃいん|ていげん|ざいたくきんむせいど|どうにゅう"}],"notes":"提言 is a well-founded proposal, usually made by experts or bodies to politicians, authorities or the public; as a transitive する-verb: 〜を提言する, 政府に提言する. Common are 提言をまとめる, 政策提言 and 〜への提言. 提案 is the general \"proposal\" in any situation, 助言 advice to a person, 進言 advice to a superior. 提言 sounds formal and public.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"労働者が怠業戦術で抗議した。","romaji":"Rōdōsha ga taigyō senjutsu de kōgi shita.","german":"The workers protested with slowdown tactics.","furigana":"ろうどうしゃ|たいぎょうせんじゅつ|こうぎ"},{"japanese":"ストライキとは違い、怠業では作業を続けながら能率をわざと落とす。","romaji":"Sutoraiki to wa chigai, taigyō de wa sagyō o tsuzukenagara nōritsu o wazato otosu.","german":"Unlike a strike, in a slowdown work continues, but efficiency is deliberately lowered.","furigana":"ちが|たいぎょう|さぎょう|つづ|のうりつ|お"},{"japanese":"「サボる」という言葉は、怠業を意味するフランス語の「サボタージュ」から来ている。","romaji":"\"Saboru\" to iu kotoba wa, taigyō o imi suru Furansugo no \"sabotāju\" kara kite iru.","german":"The word \"saboru\" (to skip work) comes from the French \"sabotage\", which means a slowdown.","furigana":"ことば|たいぎょう|いみ|ご|き"}],"notes":"怠業 is an industrial action in which employees keep working but deliberately slowly or carelessly, that is, a go-slow; the loanword サボタージュ fits here, from which the colloquial サボる \"to skip work, to slack off\" is derived. Typical are 怠業戦術 and 怠業する. In a 罷業 or ストライキ, by contrast, work is stopped entirely. The word is formal and legal.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"部長職と課長職を兼務している。","romaji":"Buchōshoku to kachōshoku o kenmu shite iru.","german":"He holds the posts of department head and section chief at the same time.","furigana":"ぶちょうしょく|かちょうしょく|けんむ"},{"japanese":"人手不足のため、店長が経理も兼務している。","romaji":"Hitode busoku no tame, tenchō ga keiri mo kenmu shite iru.","german":"Due to a labor shortage, the store manager also handles accounting.","furigana":"ひとでぶそく|てんちょう|けいり|けんむ"},{"japanese":"兼務が多すぎて、どの仕事も中途半端になってしまう。","romaji":"Kenmu ga ōsugite, dono shigoto mo chūto hanpa ni natte shimau.","german":"With so many concurrent duties, every job ends up half done.","furigana":"けんむ|おお|しごと|ちゅうとはんぱ"}],"notes":"兼務 means to perform another function at the same time in addition to one's own post; as a transitive する-verb: AとBを兼務する, 〜も兼務する. Nearly synonymous is 兼任, which is somewhat more common for official posts. The opposite is 専任 \"exclusively in charge\"; 兼業, by contrast, means a side occupation outside the company.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"専任の担当者を配置する必要がある。","romaji":"Sennin no tantōsha o haichi suru hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"It is necessary to assign a dedicated person in charge.","furigana":"せんにん|たんとうしゃ|はいち|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"彼女は大学の専任講師として、毎日授業を受け持っている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa daigaku no sennin kōshi to shite, mainichi jugyō o ukemotte iru.","german":"As a full-time lecturer at the university, she teaches classes every day.","furigana":"かのじょ|だいがく|せんにんこうし|まいにちじゅぎょう|う|も"},{"japanese":"兼任ではなく専任で、品質管理を担当することになった。","romaji":"Kennin de wa naku sennin de, hinshitsu kanri o tantō suru koto ni natta.","german":"I will be in charge of quality control exclusively rather than as a side duty.","furigana":"けんにん|せんにん|ひんしつかんり|たんとう"}],"notes":"専任 means being exclusively or full-time responsible for a particular task; mostly attributive with の: 専任の担当者, 専任スタッフ. At universities, 専任講師 is a permanent lecturer post, as opposed to 非常勤 \"part-time lecturer\". The opposite is 兼任 or 兼務; in job ads, 専任 often stands for a full-time position without side duties.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"辣腕の経営者が会社を再建した。","romaji":"Ratsuwan no keieisha ga kaisha o saiken shita.","german":"A forceful executive turned the company around.","furigana":"らつわん|けいえいしゃ|かいしゃ|さいけん"},{"japanese":"新社長は就任直後から辣腕を振るい、不採算部門を次々と整理した。","romaji":"Shin shachō wa shūnin chokugo kara ratsuwan o furui, fusaisan bumon o tsugitsugi to seiri shita.","german":"Right after taking office, the new president took decisive action and disposed of unprofitable divisions one after another.","furigana":"しんしゃちょう|しゅうにんちょくご|らつわん|ふ|ふさいさんぶもん|つぎつぎ|せいり"},{"japanese":"彼は辣腕弁護士として業界で知られている。","romaji":"Kare wa ratsuwan bengoshi to shite gyōkai de shirarete iru.","german":"He is known in the industry as a formidable lawyer.","furigana":"かれ|らつわんべんごし|ぎょうかい|し"}],"notes":"辣腕 is exceptional drive and assertiveness, especially in business, politics and law. Fixed is 辣腕を振るう \"to act with a firm hand\", plus 辣腕家 and, attributively, 辣腕の経営者／辣腕弁護士. Through 辣 \"sharp, biting\" it suggests toughness and can easily shade into ruthlessness. 敏腕 is more positive, \"capable, able\", and 手腕 is neutral, \"ability\". Literary and journalistic.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"交渉の手腕が高く評価されている。","romaji":"Kōshō no shuwan ga takaku hyōka sarete iru.","german":"His negotiating skill is highly valued.","furigana":"こうしょう|しゅわん|たか|ひょうか"},{"japanese":"新しい監督の手腕が問われるシーズンになる。","romaji":"Atarashii kantoku no shuwan ga towareru shīzun ni naru.","german":"It will be a season in which the new manager's ability is put to the test.","furigana":"あたら|かんとく|しゅわん|と"},{"japanese":"彼女は経営手腕を発揮して、赤字の店を立て直した。","romaji":"Kanojo wa keiei shuwan o hakki shite, akaji no mise o tatenaoshita.","german":"She showed her management skill and turned around a loss-making shop.","furigana":"かのじょ|けいえいしゅわん|はっき|あかじ|みせ|た|なお"}],"notes":"手腕 is ability proven in practice, especially in leading, negotiating and managing. Typical are 手腕を発揮する, 手腕を振るう, 手腕が問われる \"to have to prove one's abilities\", as well as 経営手腕 and 政治手腕. For manual skill, people tend to say 腕前 or 器用; 辣腕 and 敏腕 stress particular sharpness or efficiency. The word is literary; in conversation 腕 is enough.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"上司の叱咤激励を受けて奮起した。","romaji":"Jōshi no shitta gekirei o ukete funki shita.","german":"Spurred on by my superior's stern encouragement, I pulled myself together.","furigana":"じょうし|しったげきれい|う|ふんき"},{"japanese":"コーチは試合中、ベンチから選手たちを大声で叱咤した。","romaji":"Kōchi wa shiaichū, benchi kara senshutachi o ōgoe de shitta shita.","german":"During the game, the coach shouted at the players from the bench to drive them on.","furigana":"しあいちゅう|せんしゅ|おおごえ|しった"},{"japanese":"恩師の叱咤がなければ、私は途中で研究をあきらめていただろう。","romaji":"Onshi no shitta ga nakereba, watashi wa tochū de kenkyū o akiramete ita darō.","german":"Without my mentor's stern urging, I would probably have given up my research partway.","furigana":"おんし|しった|わたし|とちゅう|けんきゅう"}],"notes":"叱咤 means to shout at someone loudly, but usually with the aim of driving them on. The most common form is the four-character phrase 叱咤激励 \"to spur on with stern words\"; as a transitive する-verb: 選手を叱咤する. Compared with 叱責 \"formal reprimand\", 叱咤 carries a note of encouragement, and compared with 激励 \"encouragement\", a note of sternness. Literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ミスを犯して上司から叱責された。","romaji":"Misu o okashite jōshi kara shisseki sareta.","german":"I made a mistake and was reprimanded by my boss.","furigana":"おか|じょうし|しっせき"},{"japanese":"不適切な発言をした大臣は、首相から厳しく叱責された。","romaji":"Futekisetsu na hatsugen o shita daijin wa, shushō kara kibishiku shisseki sareta.","german":"The minister was sharply rebuked by the prime minister for his inappropriate remark.","furigana":"ふてきせつ|はつげん|だいじん|しゅしょう|きび|しっせき"},{"japanese":"部下を人前で叱責するのは逆効果だ。","romaji":"Buka o hitomae de shisseki suru no wa gyakukōka da.","german":"Scolding subordinates in front of others is counterproductive.","furigana":"ぶか|ひとまえ|しっせき|ぎゃくこうか"}],"notes":"叱責 is a serious, formal reprimand by superiors or an authority; as a transitive する-verb often in the passive: 上司から叱責される, 叱責を受ける, 厳しく叱責する. In everyday speech people say 叱る or, colloquially, 怒られる. Unlike 叱咤, 叱責 contains no encouragement; 譴責 is a formal disciplinary punishment.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"半期ごとに人事考課が行われる。","romaji":"Hanki goto ni jinji kōka ga okonawareru.","german":"A personnel appraisal is carried out every half year.","furigana":"はんき|じんじこうか|おこな"},{"japanese":"人事考課の結果は、昇給や賞与に反映される。","romaji":"Jinji kōka no kekka wa, shōkyū ya shōyo ni han'ei sareru.","german":"The results of the personnel appraisal are reflected in raises and bonuses.","furigana":"じんじこうか|けっか|しょうきゅう|しょうよ|はんえい"},{"japanese":"上司と面談しながら、自己評価を考課表に記入した。","romaji":"Jōshi to mendan shinagara, jiko hyōka o kōkahyō ni kinyū shita.","german":"In discussion with my superior, I filled in my self-assessment on the appraisal form.","furigana":"じょうし|めんだん|じこひょうか|こうかひょう|きにゅう"}],"notes":"考課 is the evaluation of employees' work performance and occurs almost only in 人事考課 \"personnel appraisal\" and 考課表 \"appraisal form\". The result feeds into promotion, 昇給 and 賞与. 評価 is the general word for \"evaluation\", 査定 the rating (also of prices). Beware of identically read words such as 効果 \"effect\" or 硬貨 \"coin\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"取引先との折衝に時間がかかっている。","romaji":"Torihikisaki to no sesshō ni jikan ga kakatte iru.","german":"Negotiations with the business partner are taking time.","furigana":"とりひきさき|せっしょう|じかん"},{"japanese":"予算をめぐって、財務省との折衝が続いている。","romaji":"Yosan o megutte, Zaimushō to no sesshō ga tsuzuite iru.","german":"Negotiations with the Ministry of Finance over the budget are continuing.","furigana":"よさん|ざいむしょう|せっしょう|つづ"},{"japanese":"何度も折衝を重ねた末、ようやく条件がまとまった。","romaji":"Nando mo sesshō o kasaneta sue, yōyaku jōken ga matomatta.","german":"After repeated rounds of negotiation, the terms were finally settled.","furigana":"なんど|せっしょう|かさ|すえ|じょうけん"}],"notes":"折衝 is negotiation in which interests are weighed and compromises are fought for, especially between organizations, authorities or states; as an intransitive する-verb: 〜と折衝する. Typical are 折衝を重ねる, 予算折衝 and 事務レベルの折衝. 交渉 is the general word for \"negotiation\"; 折衝 sounds more formal and stresses the tough back and forth.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"関係各所に照会して情報を収集した。","romaji":"Kankei kakusho ni shōkai shite jōhō o shūshū shita.","german":"We made inquiries with the relevant offices and gathered information.","furigana":"かんけいかくしょ|しょうかい|じょうほう|しゅうしゅう"},{"japanese":"ATMで口座の残高を照会した。","romaji":"ATM de kōza no zandaka o shōkai shita.","german":"I checked my account balance at the ATM.","furigana":"こうざ|ざんだか|しょうかい"},{"japanese":"応募者の経歴について、前の勤務先に照会した。","romaji":"Ōbosha no keireki ni tsuite, mae no kinmusaki ni shōkai shita.","german":"We contacted the applicant's previous employer to ask about his work history.","furigana":"おうぼしゃ|けいれき|まえ|きんむさき|しょうかい"}],"notes":"照会 is an official inquiry to verify facts with a responsible body; as a transitive する-verb: 〜に…を照会する. Typical are 残高照会 \"balance inquiry\", 在庫照会, 身元照会 and, in IT, a database query. In everyday speech people say 問い合わせ. Do not confuse it with the identically read 紹介 \"introduction, recommendation\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"研究開発の受託業務を行っている。","romaji":"Kenkyū kaihatsu no jutaku gyōmu o okonatte iru.","german":"We carry out contract work in research and development.","furigana":"けんきゅうかいはつ|じゅたくぎょうむ|おこな"},{"japanese":"当社は市から図書館の運営を受託している。","romaji":"Tōsha wa shi kara toshokan no un'ei o jutaku shite iru.","german":"Our company has been commissioned by the city to run the library.","furigana":"とうしゃ|し|としょかん|うんえい|じゅたく"},{"japanese":"元市長が受託収賄の疑いで逮捕された。","romaji":"Moto shichō ga jutaku shūwai no utagai de taiho sareta.","german":"The former mayor was arrested on suspicion of accepting a bribe in return for a requested favor.","furigana":"もとしちょう|じゅたくしゅうわい|うたが|たいほ"}],"notes":"受託 is the acceptance of a commission or custody by the commissioned side; the counterpart on the client's side is 委託. As a transitive する-verb: 業務を受託する. Common are 受託業務, 受託開発 \"contract development\", 受託者 and, in law, 受託収賄 \"bribery (accepting a bribe on request)\". Formal business and legal language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"納品物の検収が完了した。","romaji":"Nōhinbutsu no kenshū ga kanryō shita.","german":"The acceptance inspection of the delivered goods has been completed.","furigana":"のうひんぶつ|けんしゅう|かんりょう"},{"japanese":"支払いは検収後、翌月末となります。","romaji":"Shiharai wa kenshūgo, yokugetsumatsu to narimasu.","german":"Payment will be made at the end of the following month after acceptance inspection.","furigana":"しはら|けんしゅうご|よくげつまつ"},{"japanese":"届いた部品を検収したところ、数が注文と合わなかった。","romaji":"Todoita buhin o kenshū shita tokoro, kazu ga chūmon to awanakatta.","german":"When I inspected the parts that arrived, the quantity did not match the order.","furigana":"とど|ぶひん|けんしゅう|かず|ちゅうもん|あ"}],"notes":"検収 is the inspection and formal acceptance of delivered goods or services by the buyer; payment usually follows only after that. As a transitive する-verb: 納品物を検収する; typical are 検収書, 検収日 and 検収後に支払う. 検品 means only the inspection of goods, 納品 the delivery itself. A technical word from purchasing, accounting and IT projects.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"顧客台帳を電子化して管理する。","romaji":"Kokyaku daichō o denshika shite kanri suru.","german":"We digitize and manage the customer register.","furigana":"こきゃくだいちょう|でんしか|かんり"},{"japanese":"市役所では住民基本台帳をもとに住民票を発行する。","romaji":"Shiyakusho de wa jūmin kihon daichō o moto ni jūminhyō o hakkō suru.","german":"City hall issues residence certificates based on the basic resident register.","furigana":"しやくしょ|じゅうみんきほんだいちょう|じゅうみんひょう|はっこう"},{"japanese":"新しく購入したパソコンを備品台帳に登録した。","romaji":"Atarashiku kōnyū shita pasokon o bihin daichō ni tōroku shita.","german":"I registered the newly purchased computer in the equipment ledger.","furigana":"あたら|こうにゅう|びひんだいちょう|とうろく"}],"notes":"台帳 is an official or company master record in which data are entered continuously: 顧客台帳, 固定資産台帳, 土地台帳, 住民基本台帳 \"resident register\". Typical verbs are 台帳に記入する／登録する and 台帳で管理する. 帳簿 are the account books of bookkeeping, 名簿 a list of names. The secondary meaning \"theater script\" is rare.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"中期経営計画の策定に着手した。","romaji":"Chūki keiei keikaku no sakutei ni chakushu shita.","german":"We started drawing up the medium-term business plan.","furigana":"ちゅうきけいえいけいかく|さくてい|ちゃくしゅ"},{"japanese":"政府は新たな防災計画を策定した。","romaji":"Seifu wa arata na bōsai keikaku o sakutei shita.","german":"The government has formulated a new disaster prevention plan.","furigana":"せいふ|あら|ぼうさいけいかく|さくてい"},{"japanese":"社内でAI利用のガイドラインを策定中だ。","romaji":"Shanai de AI riyō no gaidorain o sakuteichū da.","german":"The company is currently drawing up guidelines for the use of AI.","furigana":"しゃない|りよう|さくていちゅう"}],"notes":"策定 is the drawing up and binding establishment of plans, guidelines or strategies by authorities and organizations; as a transitive する-verb: 計画を策定する. Typical are 方針・指針・基準の策定, 策定に着手する and 策定中. 立案 is the earlier phase of drafting, 作成 the mere creation of documents, 制定 the enactment of laws. Formal official and business language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新規プロジェクトの立案を任された。","romaji":"Shinki purojekuto no ritsuan o makasareta.","german":"I was entrusted with planning the new project.","furigana":"しんき|りつあん|まか"},{"japanese":"若手職員が中心となって、観光振興策を立案した。","romaji":"Wakate shokuin ga chūshin to natte, kankō shinkōsaku o ritsuan shita.","german":"Young staff members took the lead in drawing up a tourism promotion plan.","furigana":"わかてしょくいん|ちゅうしん|かんこうしんこうさく|りつあん"},{"japanese":"政策の立案には、現場の声を反映させることが欠かせない。","romaji":"Seisaku no ritsuan ni wa, genba no koe o han'ei saseru koto ga kakasenai.","german":"In drafting policy, it is essential to reflect voices from the field.","furigana":"せいさく|りつあん|げんば|こえ|はんえい|か"}],"notes":"立案 is the drafting of a plan or concept; as a transitive する-verb: 計画を立案する. Common are 企画立案, 政策立案, 立案者 and 法案を立案する \"to draft a bill\". 立案 stands for the conceptual phase, 策定 for working out up to the binding decision; in everyday speech people simply say 計画を立てる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新制度の起案者として名前が挙がった。","romaji":"Shinseido no kiansha to shite namae ga agatta.","german":"His name came up as the originator of the new system.","furigana":"しんせいど|きあんしゃ|なまえ|あ"},{"japanese":"課長に起案書を提出して、決裁を待っている。","romaji":"Kachō ni kiansho o teishutsu shite, kessai o matte iru.","german":"I submitted the draft proposal to the section chief and am waiting for approval.","furigana":"かちょう|きあんしょ|ていしゅつ|けっさい|ま"},{"japanese":"この条例案は若手職員が起案したものだ。","romaji":"Kono jōreian wa wakate shokuin ga kian shita mono da.","german":"This draft ordinance was drawn up by young staff members.","furigana":"じょうれいあん|わかてしょくいん|きあん"}],"notes":"起案 denotes the drafting of a document, usually official or in-house, which is then passed on for approval (決裁); transitive: 計画を起案する. Typical in government and company jargon are 起案書 \"approval request document\" and 起案者 \"drafter, originator\". 起草 is used more for drafting legal and contract texts or speeches, while 立案 stresses the planning itself. In everyday speech people simply say 案を作る or 企画書を書く.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"来月から本格的に工事に着手する。","romaji":"Raigetsu kara honkakuteki ni kōji ni chakushu suru.","german":"From next month we will start the construction work in earnest.","furigana":"らいげつ|ほんかくてき|こうじ|ちゃくしゅ"},{"japanese":"新しい研究にはまだ着手していない。","romaji":"Atarashii kenkyū ni wa mada chakushu shite inai.","german":"I haven't started the new research yet.","furigana":"あたら|けんきゅう|ちゃくしゅ"},{"japanese":"警察は汚職事件の捜査に着手した。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa oshoku jiken no sōsa ni chakushu shita.","german":"The police have begun an investigation into the corruption case.","furigana":"けいさつ|おしょくじけん|そうさ|ちゃくしゅ"}],"notes":"着手 means \"to take something in hand, to begin something\"; the undertaking takes に: 工事に着手する, 改革に着手する. It sounds more formal than 取りかかる or 始める and is typical of construction projects, reforms, investigations and larger projects. The negative 〜にはまだ着手していない \"have not yet started\" is common. In lawyers' jargon there is 着手金 \"retainer paid at the start of a case\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"資金不足でプロジェクトが頓挫した。","romaji":"Shikin busoku de purojekuto ga tonza shita.","german":"The project collapsed due to lack of funds.","furigana":"しきんぶそく|とんざ"},{"japanese":"反対運動によって再開発計画は頓挫した。","romaji":"Hantai undō ni yotte saikaihatsu keikaku wa tonza shita.","german":"The redevelopment plan was derailed by the opposition movement.","furigana":"はんたいうんどう|さいかいはつけいかく|とんざ"},{"japanese":"交渉が途中で頓挫し、合意には至らなかった。","romaji":"Kōshō ga tochū de tonza shi, gōi ni wa itaranakatta.","german":"The negotiations broke down partway and no agreement was reached.","furigana":"こうしょう|とちゅう|とんざ|ごうい|いた"}],"notes":"頓挫 describes an ongoing undertaking suddenly getting stuck or failing because of an obstacle; intransitive: 計画が頓挫する, the cause takes で or によって. Subjects are plans, projects and negotiations, not people. For a person who fails at something and gives up, people tend to say 挫折する; 中断 is a neutral \"interruption\". The word is literary and frequent in the news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の温かい人柄に惹かれる人は多い。","romaji":"Kare no atatakai hitogara ni hikareru hito wa ōi.","german":"Many people are drawn to his warm personality.","furigana":"かれ|あたた|ひとがら||ひと|おお"},{"japanese":"面接では能力だけでなく人柄も見られる。","romaji":"Mensetsu de wa nōryoku dake de naku hitogara mo mirareru.","german":"In interviews, not only ability but also personality is assessed.","furigana":"めんせつ|のうりょく|ひとがら|み"},{"japanese":"手紙の文面から彼女の誠実な人柄がうかがえる。","romaji":"Tegami no bunmen kara kanojo no seijitsu na hitogara ga ukagaeru.","german":"Her sincere character can be sensed from the wording of her letter.","furigana":"てがみ|ぶんめん|かのじょ|せいじつ|ひとがら"}],"notes":"人柄 is a person's character as it comes across to others in dealings, and it is used almost always positively: 人柄がいい, 温かい人柄, 誠実な人柄. 性格 describes inner qualities neutrally, including negative ones, and 人格 the moral personality as a whole. About third parties one politely says お人柄. Typical are 人柄が出る or 人柄がにじみ出る \"the character shows\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"芸術家気質の彼は自由奔放に生きている。","romaji":"Geijutsuka kishitsu no kare wa jiyū honpō ni ikite iru.","german":"With his artistic temperament, he lives freely and without restraint.","furigana":"げいじゅつかきしつ|かれ|じゆうほんぽう|い"},{"japanese":"兄は穏やかな気質で、めったに怒らない。","romaji":"Ani wa odayaka na kishitsu de, metta ni okoranai.","german":"My older brother has a mild temperament and rarely gets angry.","furigana":"あに|おだ|きしつ|おこ"},{"japanese":"気質の違いは、子どもの頃から表れるという。","romaji":"Kishitsu no chigai wa, kodomo no koro kara arawareru to iu.","german":"Differences in temperament are said to show from childhood.","furigana":"きしつ|ちが|こ|ころ|あらわ"}],"notes":"気質 denotes a person's or group's innate temperament or typical nature: 穏やかな気質, 日本人の気質, 県民気質. After occupational or period terms it is often read かたぎ (職人気質, 昔気質) and then means the typical \"type\" of such people; for 芸術家気質 both readings are common. 性格 is the general word for \"character\", and 気性 stresses temperament in the sense of hot-tempered or gentle (気性が荒い).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"指導者としての資質が問われている。","romaji":"Shidōsha to shite no shishitsu ga towarete iru.","german":"His qualities as a leader are being called into question.","furigana":"しどうしゃ|ししつ|と"},{"japanese":"リーダーに必要な資質とは何だと思いますか。","romaji":"Rīdā ni hitsuyō na shishitsu to wa nan da to omoimasu ka.","german":"What qualities do you think a leader needs?","furigana":"ひつよう|ししつ|なん|おも"},{"japanese":"彼女は研究者としての資質に恵まれている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kenkyūsha to shite no shishitsu ni megumarete iru.","german":"She is blessed with the qualities of a researcher.","furigana":"かのじょ|けんきゅうしゃ|ししつ|めぐ"}],"notes":"資質 are the innate or developed qualities that make someone suited to a role; typical is 〜としての資質 (指導者としての資質) as well as 資質がある, 資質に恵まれる and 資質が問われる \"one's suitability is questioned\". 素質 means more the innate talent for a particular skill (音楽の素質), 才能 \"talent\" itself. 資質 sounds objective to formal and is often found in connection with leadership, occupation and responsibility.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"器量の大きい人間になりたい。","romaji":"Kiryō no ōkii ningen ni naritai.","german":"I want to become a person of great caliber.","furigana":"きりょう|おお|にんげん"},{"japanese":"彼には社長になるだけの器量がない。","romaji":"Kare ni wa shachō ni naru dake no kiryō ga nai.","german":"He lacks the caliber to become company president.","furigana":"かれ|しゃちょう|きりょう"},{"japanese":"祖母は若い頃、村一番の器量よしだったそうだ。","romaji":"Sobo wa wakai koro, mura ichiban no kiryōyoshi datta sō da.","german":"My grandmother is said to have been the most beautiful girl in the village in her youth.","furigana":"そぼ|わか|ころ|むらいちばん|きりょう"}],"notes":"器量 has two main meanings: appearance, especially a woman's face (器量よし \"pretty\", 器量がいい), which is old-fashioned today and slightly delicate; and a person's \"caliber\", that is, ability and stature to fill a role (器量が大きい, 〜だけの器量がない). In the second meaning, 器 \"caliber\" is closely related (社長の器ではない). 度量, by contrast, means specifically generosity and tolerance toward others.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"度量の広い上司に恵まれた。","romaji":"Doryō no hiroi jōshi ni megumareta.","german":"I was fortunate to have a broad-minded boss.","furigana":"どりょう|ひろ|じょうし|めぐ"},{"japanese":"人の失敗を笑って許せるのは、度量が大きい証拠だ。","romaji":"Hito no shippai o waratte yuruseru no wa, doryō ga ōkii shōko da.","german":"Being able to laugh off and forgive other people's mistakes is proof of a generous nature.","furigana":"ひと|しっぱい|わら|ゆる|どりょう|おお|しょうこ"},{"japanese":"批判を受け入れる度量がなければ、組織は成長しない。","romaji":"Hihan o ukeireru doryō ga nakereba, soshiki wa seichō shinai.","german":"Without the largeness of mind to accept criticism, an organization cannot grow.","furigana":"ひはん|う|い|どりょう|そしき|せいちょう"}],"notes":"度量 is the inner breadth to accept other opinions and mistakes: 度量が広い or 度量が大きい \"big-hearted\", 度量が狭い \"petty\" (cf. 狭量). A relative clause often precedes it: 批判を受け入れる度量. 器量 means more ability and caliber, while 寛容 is the more abstract \"tolerance\" as an attitude. The meaning \"measures of length and volume\" is met practically only in 度量衡 \"weights and measures\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"愚かな過ちを二度と繰り返さない。","romaji":"Oroka na ayamachi wo nido to kurikaesanai.","german":"I will never repeat that foolish mistake.","furigana":"おろ|あやま|にど|く|かえ"},{"japanese":"戦争ほど愚かなことはない。","romaji":"Sensō hodo oroka na koto wa nai.","german":"Nothing is as foolish as war.","furigana":"せんそう|おろ"},{"japanese":"あんな噂を信じるなんて、愚かだった。","romaji":"Anna uwasa o shinjiru nante, oroka datta.","german":"It was foolish of me to believe such a rumor.","furigana":"うわさ|しん|おろ"}],"notes":"愚か is a な-adjective: 愚かな行為, 愚かだった. It sounds more serious and literary than the colloquial バカ and evaluates actions and decisions rather than intelligence; a \"fool\" is an 愚か者. The same word gives rise to the pattern 〜はおろか \"let alone\", which is usually written in kana (漢字はおろかひらがなも読めない).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"賢明な判断を下すことが求められる。","romaji":"Kenmei na handan wo kudasu koto ga motomerareru.","german":"Wise judgment is required.","furigana":"けんめい|はんだん|くだ|もと"},{"japanese":"今は無理をせず、休むのが賢明だ。","romaji":"Ima wa muri o sezu, yasumu no ga kenmei da.","german":"For now, it is wise not to push yourself and to rest.","furigana":"いま|むり|やす|けんめい"},{"japanese":"台風が近づいていたので、旅行を延期したのは賢明な選択だった。","romaji":"Taifū ga chikazuite ita node, ryokō o enki shita no wa kenmei na sentaku datta.","german":"A typhoon was approaching, so postponing the trip was a wise choice.","furigana":"たいふう|ちか|りょこう|えんき|けんめい|せんたく"}],"notes":"賢明 evaluates a decision or course of action as sensible and wise: 賢明な判断, 賢明な選択, 〜のが賢明だ \"it is advisable to ...\". So it describes actions rather than a person's intelligence (for which 聡明 or 頭がいい). Do not confuse it with the identically read 懸命 \"earnest, with all one's might\" (懸命に働く, 一生懸命).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"聡明な彼女は常に的確な意見を述べる。","romaji":"Sōmei na kanojo wa tsuneni tekikaku na iken wo noberu.","german":"She is intelligent and always gives apt opinions.","furigana":"そうめい|かのじょ|つね|てきかく|いけん|の"},{"japanese":"祖母は八十を過ぎても聡明で、記憶力も衰えていない。","romaji":"Sobo wa hachijū o sugite mo sōmei de, kiokuryoku mo otoroete inai.","german":"My grandmother is still sharp in her eighties, and her memory has not declined.","furigana":"そぼ|はちじゅう|す|そうめい|きおくりょく|おとろ"},{"japanese":"聡明な読者なら、この矛盾にすぐ気づくだろう。","romaji":"Sōmei na dokusha nara, kono mujun ni sugu kizuku darō.","german":"A discerning reader will notice this contradiction right away.","furigana":"そうめい|どくしゃ|むじゅん|き"}],"notes":"聡明 praises a person as clever, sharp and capable of judgment, a mixture of intelligence and wisdom. It is literary and respectful and is often found in descriptions of people: 聡明な女性, 聡明な顔立ち. 賢明, by contrast, evaluates an action (賢明な判断), and 利発 the alertness of a child; colloquially people say 頭がいい or 賢い.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"利発な子供で何を教えてもすぐに覚える。","romaji":"Rihatsu na kodomo de nani wo oshiete mo sugu ni oboeru.","german":"He is a bright child who learns anything right away.","furigana":"りはつ|こども|なに|おし|おぼ"},{"japanese":"利発そうな目をした少年が質問してきた。","romaji":"Rihatsu sō na me o shita shōnen ga shitsumon shite kita.","german":"A boy with bright-looking eyes asked me a question.","furigana":"りはつ|め|しょうねん|しつもん"},{"japanese":"娘さんは本当に利発ですね。","romaji":"Musume-san wa hontō ni rihatsu desu ne.","german":"Your daughter is really bright, isn't she?","furigana":"むすめ|ほんとう|りはつ"}],"notes":"利発 describes almost exclusively children or young people who grasp things quickly and answer cleverly: 利発な子, 利発そうな顔. It is a somewhat elevated, slightly old-fashioned compliment; in everyday speech people tend to say 賢い or 頭がいい. For adults, 聡明 or 有能 is used instead.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は人の気持ちに鈍感なところがある。","romaji":"Kare wa hito no kimochi ni donkan na tokoro ga aru.","german":"He can be insensitive to other people's feelings.","furigana":"かれ|ひと|きも|どんかん"},{"japanese":"鈍感な彼は、彼女の好意にまったく気づいていない。","romaji":"Donkan na kare wa, kanojo no kōi ni mattaku kizuite inai.","german":"He is so dense that he has no idea she likes him.","furigana":"どんかん|かれ|かのじょ|こうい|き"},{"japanese":"年をとると暑さに鈍感になるので、熱中症に注意が必要だ。","romaji":"Toshi o toru to atsusa ni donkan ni naru node, netchūshō ni chūi ga hitsuyō da.","german":"With age you become less sensitive to heat, so you need to watch out for heatstroke.","furigana":"とし|あつ|どんかん|ねっちゅうしょう|ちゅうい|ひつよう"}],"notes":"鈍感 means that someone barely perceives stimuli, moods or other people's feelings; the object takes に: 人の気持ちに鈍感だ, 痛みに鈍感. The opposite is 敏感. It is usually a reproach, but as a buzzword, 鈍感力 \"thick-skinned strength\", it is also positive. Colloquially people also say 鈍い (彼は鈍いね).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は周囲の変化に敏感に反応する。","romaji":"Kanojo wa shūi no henka ni binkan ni hannō suru.","german":"She reacts sensitively to changes in her surroundings.","furigana":"かのじょ|しゅうい|へんか|びんかん|はんのう"},{"japanese":"私は敏感肌なので、化粧品選びに気を使う。","romaji":"Watashi wa binkanhada na node, keshōhin erabi ni ki o tsukau.","german":"I have sensitive skin, so I am careful in choosing cosmetics.","furigana":"わたし|びんかんはだ|けしょうひんえら|き|つか"},{"japanese":"若者は流行に敏感だ。","romaji":"Wakamono wa ryūkō ni binkan da.","german":"Young people are quick to pick up on trends.","furigana":"わかもの|りゅうこう|びんかん"}],"notes":"敏感 means reacting quickly and strongly to stimuli or changes; the object takes に: 音に敏感, 流行に敏感, adverbially 敏感に反応する. It is usually neutral to positive (\"perceptive, attentive\"); when the sensitivity is excessive or pathological, people say 過敏 (神経過敏). Opposite: 鈍感. Set expression: 敏感肌 \"sensitive skin\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"狭量な考え方では人はついてこない。","romaji":"Kyōryō na kangaekata de wa hito wa tsuite konai.","german":"People won't follow you if you think in a narrow-minded way.","furigana":"きょうりょう|かんが|かた|ひと"},{"japanese":"部下の小さなミスも許せないとは、狭量な上司だ。","romaji":"Buka no chiisana misu mo yurusenai to wa, kyōryō na jōshi da.","german":"A boss who can't forgive even small mistakes by his subordinates is narrow-minded.","furigana":"ぶか|ちい|ゆる|きょうりょう|じょうし"},{"japanese":"異なる意見を排除するのは狭量だと批判された。","romaji":"Kotonaru iken o haijo suru no wa kyōryō da to hihan sareta.","german":"He was criticized as narrow-minded for excluding differing opinions.","furigana":"こと|いけん|はいじょ|きょうりょう|ひはん"}],"notes":"狭量 describes someone who gives others little leeway and does not tolerate differing opinions or mistakes: 狭量な考え方, 狭量な上司. It is literary; the opposite is 度量が広い or 寛大. In conversation people tend to say 心が狭い or 器が小さい.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"温厚な彼が怒るのは余程のことだ。","romaji":"Onkō na kare ga okoru no wa yohodo no koto da.","german":"When a mild-mannered man like him gets angry, it must be something serious.","furigana":"おんこう|かれ|おこ|よほど"},{"japanese":"新しい担任は温厚な人柄で、生徒に人気がある。","romaji":"Atarashii tannin wa onkō na hitogara de, seito ni ninki ga aru.","german":"The new homeroom teacher has a gentle personality and is popular with the students.","furigana":"あたら|たんにん|おんこう|ひとがら|せいと|にんき"},{"japanese":"温厚で知られる議員が、珍しく声を荒らげた。","romaji":"Onkō de shirareru giin ga, mezurashiku koe o arageta.","german":"The lawmaker, known for his mildness, raised his voice, which was unusual.","furigana":"おんこう|し|ぎいん|めずら|こえ|あ"}],"notes":"温厚 describes a calm, friendly nature that does not easily fly into anger: 温厚な性格, 温厚な人柄, 温厚篤実 \"gentle and sincere\". It is used of adults and sounds appreciative and somewhat elevated. 穏やか is broader (also weather, sea), and 優しい stresses kindness toward others.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女の冷淡な態度に傷ついた。","romaji":"Kanojo no reitan na taido ni kizutsuita.","german":"I was hurt by her cold attitude.","furigana":"かのじょ|れいたん|たいど|きず"},{"japanese":"政府は被災者に対して冷淡だと批判された。","romaji":"Seifu wa hisaisha ni taishite reitan da to hihan sareta.","german":"The government was criticized for being indifferent to the disaster victims.","furigana":"せいふ|ひさいしゃ|たい|れいたん|ひはん"},{"japanese":"彼は政治には冷淡で、選挙にも行かない。","romaji":"Kare wa seiji ni wa reitan de, senkyo ni mo ikanai.","german":"He is indifferent to politics and doesn't even go to vote.","furigana":"かれ|せいじ|れいたん|せんきょ|い"}],"notes":"冷淡 means \"cool, indifferent\" toward people or things; the object takes に or に対して: 弱者に冷淡だ. It ranges from mere lack of interest (政治に冷淡) to heartlessness (冷淡な態度). Colloquially people say 冷たい; 冷酷 is stronger and means cruel harshness, 無関心 only lack of interest.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不誠実な態度は相手を傷つける。","romaji":"Fuseijitsu na taido wa aite wo kizutsukeru.","german":"An insincere attitude hurts the other person.","furigana":"ふせいじつ|たいど|あいて|きず"},{"japanese":"謝罪もせずに言い訳ばかりするのは不誠実だ。","romaji":"Shazai mo sezu ni iiwake bakari suru no wa fuseijitsu da.","german":"Making only excuses without apologizing is insincere.","furigana":"しゃざい|い|わけ|ふせいじつ"},{"japanese":"顧客への不誠実な対応が、会社の評判を落とした。","romaji":"Kokyaku e no fuseijitsu na taiō ga, kaisha no hyōban o otoshita.","german":"The company's reputation suffered from its insincere handling of customers.","furigana":"こきゃく|ふせいじつ|たいおう|かいしゃ|ひょうばん|お"}],"notes":"不誠実 is the negation of 誠実 \"sincere, conscientious\" and criticizes behavior that does not take trust or obligations seriously: 不誠実な態度, 不誠実な対応, 〜のは不誠実だ. It is common in complaints about companies, in the media and in relationships (also \"unfaithful\"). 嘘つき stresses outright lying, 不誠実 rather a lack of seriousness and responsibility.","levelNote":"Compound: 不 (N3) + 誠実 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"横柄な物言いに腹が立った。","romaji":"Ōhei na monoii ni hara ga tatta.","german":"His arrogant way of speaking made me angry.","furigana":"おうへい|ものい|はら|た"},{"japanese":"店員の横柄な態度に、客が怒って帰ってしまった。","romaji":"Ten'in no ōhei na taido ni, kyaku ga okotte kaette shimatta.","german":"The customer got angry at the clerk's haughty attitude and left.","furigana":"てんいん|おうへい|たいど|きゃく|おこ|かえ"},{"japanese":"彼は出世してから横柄になった。","romaji":"Kare wa shusse shite kara ōhei ni natta.","german":"He has become arrogant since being promoted.","furigana":"かれ|しゅっせ|おうへい"}],"notes":"横柄 describes a condescending, impolite manner, above all in tone and bearing: 横柄な態度, 横柄な口のきき方, 横柄に振る舞う. 傲慢 denotes rather the inner arrogance, and 偉そう is the colloquial equivalent. It is typical in criticism of superiors, officials, staff or customers.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"卑屈にならずに堂々と意見を述べるべきだ。","romaji":"Hikutsu ni narazu ni dōdō to iken wo noberu beki da.","german":"You should state your opinion confidently without being servile.","furigana":"ひくつ|どうどう|いけん|の"},{"japanese":"何を言われても卑屈な笑いを浮かべるだけだった。","romaji":"Nani o iwarete mo hikutsu na warai o ukaberu dake datta.","german":"No matter what was said to him, he just wore a servile smile.","furigana":"なに|い|ひくつ|わら|う"},{"japanese":"失敗が続いて、すっかり卑屈になってしまった。","romaji":"Shippai ga tsuzuite, sukkari hikutsu ni natte shimatta.","german":"After a string of failures, he has become completely self-abasing.","furigana":"しっぱい|つづ|ひくつ"}],"notes":"卑屈 describes someone who humbles himself needlessly and behaves obsequiously toward others: 卑屈になる, 卑屈な態度, 卑屈な笑い. Unlike the positive 謙虚 \"humble\", 卑屈 carries an overtone of contempt or lack of self-esteem. The admonition 卑屈になるな \"don't belittle yourself\" is common.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"高潔な人格で周囲から尊敬されている。","romaji":"Kōketsu na jinkaku de shūi kara sonkei sarete iru.","german":"He is respected by those around him for his noble character.","furigana":"こうけつ|じんかく|しゅうい|そんけい"},{"japanese":"彼は一度も賄賂を受け取らなかった高潔な政治家だ。","romaji":"Kare wa ichido mo wairo o uketoranakatta kōketsu na seijika da.","german":"He is an upright politician who never once accepted a bribe.","furigana":"かれ|いちど|わいろ|う|と|こうけつ|せいじか"},{"japanese":"物語の主人公は高潔で、決して嘘をつかない騎士だ。","romaji":"Monogatari no shujinkō wa kōketsu de, kesshite uso o tsukanai kishi da.","german":"The hero of the story is a noble knight who never lies.","furigana":"ものがたり|しゅじんこう|こうけつ|けっ|うそ|きし"}],"notes":"高潔 praises a person as morally spotless and noble-minded, free of self-interest: 高潔な人格, 高潔な人物, 高潔な精神. It is literary and elevated, typical of obituaries, biographies and literature. More everyday are 立派 or 誠実; the four-character phrase 清廉潔白 stresses specifically incorruptibility.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"実直な人柄が商売の信用につながる。","romaji":"Jitchoku na hitogara ga shōbai no shin'yō ni tsunagaru.","german":"An honest character leads to trust in business.","furigana":"じっちょく|ひとがら|しょうばい|しんよう"},{"japanese":"父は四十年間、同じ会社で実直に働いた。","romaji":"Chichi wa yonjū-nenkan, onaji kaisha de jitchoku ni hataraita.","german":"My father worked diligently at the same company for forty years.","furigana":"ちち|よんじゅうねんかん|おな|かいしゃ|じっちょく|はたら"},{"japanese":"実直そうな青年が道を教えてくれた。","romaji":"Jitchoku sō na seinen ga michi o oshiete kureta.","german":"An honest-looking young man showed me the way.","furigana":"じっちょく|せいねん|みち|おし"}],"notes":"実直 describes an honest, conscientious person without show or tricks who does his work steadily: 実直な人柄, 実直に働く. It sounds appreciative but somewhat old-fashioned and appears in the four-character phrase 謹厳実直 \"strict and upright\". 誠実 stresses sincerity toward others, and 真面目 is the everyday word for \"serious, diligent\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"律儀な彼は必ず約束の時間に来る。","romaji":"Richigi na kare wa kanarazu yakusoku no jikan ni kuru.","german":"He is so dutiful that he always comes at the appointed time.","furigana":"りちぎ|かれ|かなら|やくそく|じかん|く"},{"japanese":"彼女は律儀に毎年年賀状を送ってくれる。","romaji":"Kanojo wa richigi ni maitoshi nengajō o okutte kureru.","german":"She dutifully sends me a New Year card every year.","furigana":"かのじょ|りちぎ|まいとしねんがじょう|おく"},{"japanese":"借りた傘を律儀に返しに来るなんて、真面目な人だね。","romaji":"Karita kasa o richigi ni kaeshi ni kuru nante, majime na hito da ne.","german":"Coming back to return the umbrella he borrowed; what a conscientious person.","furigana":"か|かさ|りちぎ|かえ|く|まじめ|ひと"}],"notes":"律儀 describes someone who keeps promises, duties and manners precisely, even in small matters: 律儀な人, 律儀に約束を守る, 律儀にお礼を言う. It is also written 律義 and is usually appreciative, but can easily sound slightly ironic, like \"overly punctilious\". 几帳面, by contrast, stresses order and precision in work style, and 義理堅い a sense of obligation for favors received.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"粗暴な振る舞いは周囲を不快にさせる。","romaji":"Sobō na furumai wa shūi wo fukai ni saseru.","german":"Rough behavior makes the people around you uncomfortable.","furigana":"そぼう|ふ|ま|しゅうい|ふかい"},{"japanese":"酒を飲むと粗暴になる人もいる。","romaji":"Sake o nomu to sobō ni naru hito mo iru.","german":"Some people become violent when they drink.","furigana":"さけ|の|そぼう|ひと"},{"japanese":"容疑者は以前から粗暴な言動が目立っていたという。","romaji":"Yōgisha wa izen kara sobō na gendō ga medatte ita to iu.","german":"The suspect reportedly had been noted for rough words and behavior before.","furigana":"ようぎしゃ|いぜん|そぼう|げんどう|めだ"}],"notes":"粗暴 describes coarse, inconsiderate to violent behavior of people: 粗暴な振る舞い, 粗暴な言動, 粗暴になる. It is literary and frequent in news and police reports (粗暴犯 \"violent offense, violent offender\"). The everyday word is 乱暴, which can also refer to the handling of things (乱暴な運転, 乱暴に扱う).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"寡黙な父だが、その背中から多くを学んだ。","romaji":"Kamoku na chichi da ga, sono senaka kara ōku wo mananda.","german":"My father was a man of few words, but I learned a lot from watching him.","furigana":"かもく|ちち|せなか|おお|まな"},{"japanese":"寡黙な職人は、作品で語る。","romaji":"Kamoku na shokunin wa, sakuhin de kataru.","german":"A taciturn craftsman speaks through his work.","furigana":"かもく|しょくにん|さくひん|かた"},{"japanese":"彼は会議ではいつも寡黙だが、発言すると鋭い。","romaji":"Kare wa kaigi de wa itsumo kamoku da ga, hatsugen suru to surudoi.","german":"He is always quiet in meetings, but when he does speak, he is sharp.","furigana":"かれ|かいぎ|かもく|はつげん|するど"}],"notes":"寡黙 describes a person who speaks little, often with a positive undertone of seriousness and inner calm: 寡黙な父, 寡黙な職人. It is more literary than 無口, which sounds neutral to slightly negative; colloquially people also say 口数が少ない. The character 寡 means \"few\", as in 寡占 \"oligopoly\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"雄弁な演説で聴衆の心を掴んだ。","romaji":"Yūben na enzetsu de chōshū no kokoro wo tsukanda.","german":"He captured the audience's hearts with an eloquent speech.","furigana":"ゆうべん|えんぜつ|ちょうしゅう|こころ|つか"},{"japanese":"彼は普段は無口だが、好きな映画の話になると雄弁になる。","romaji":"Kare wa fudan wa mukuchi da ga, suki na eiga no hanashi ni naru to yūben ni naru.","german":"He is usually quiet, but when the talk turns to his favorite movies, he becomes eloquent.","furigana":"かれ|ふだん|むくち|す|えいが|はなし|ゆうべん"},{"japanese":"この写真が、事故の悲惨さを雄弁に物語っている。","romaji":"Kono shashin ga, jiko no hisansa o yūben ni monogatatte iru.","german":"This photograph eloquently shows how terrible the accident was.","furigana":"しゃしん|じこ|ひさん|ゆうべん|ものがた"}],"notes":"雄弁 is persuasive, stirring oratory: 雄弁な演説, 雄弁に語る, 雄弁家 \"good speaker\". Very common is the figurative use 〜が…を雄弁に物語る \"something speaks volumes\", also of photos, figures or traces. Well known is the proverb 雄弁は銀、沈黙は金. 多弁, by contrast, means merely \"talking a lot\", and 口がうまい is rather pejorative, \"smooth at talking people into things\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"多弁な人は時に信用を失うことがある。","romaji":"Taben na hito wa toki ni shin'yō wo ushinau koto ga aru.","german":"Talkative people sometimes lose people's trust.","furigana":"たべん|ひと|とき|しんよう|うしな"},{"japanese":"緊張すると、つい多弁になってしまう。","romaji":"Kinchō suru to, tsui taben ni natte shimau.","german":"When I get nervous, I end up talking too much.","furigana":"きんちょう|たべん"},{"japanese":"普段は物静かな彼が、その夜は珍しく多弁だった。","romaji":"Fudan wa monoshizuka na kare ga, sono yoru wa mezurashiku taben datta.","german":"The usually quiet man was unusually talkative that evening.","furigana":"ふだん|ものしず|かれ|よる|めずら|たべん"}],"notes":"多弁 simply means \"talking a lot\" and is literary; depending on context it sounds neutral, \"talkative\", or slightly pejorative, \"chatty\": 多弁な人, 多弁になる. The opposite is 寡黙. 雄弁, by contrast, praises the persuasive power of a speech, and in everyday speech people simply say おしゃべり for \"talkative\". The botanical meanings (\"many-petaled\") play no role in everyday life.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"思慮深い彼は軽率な行動をしない。","romaji":"Shiryobukai kare wa keisotsu na kōdō wo shinai.","german":"He is thoughtful and doesn't act rashly.","furigana":"しりょぶか|かれ|けいそつ|こうどう"},{"japanese":"思慮深い人ほど、すぐに結論を出さない。","romaji":"Shiryobukai hito hodo, sugu ni ketsuron o dasanai.","german":"The more thoughtful a person is, the less quickly they draw conclusions.","furigana":"しりょぶか|ひと|けつろん|だ"},{"japanese":"彼女の思慮深い助言のおかげで、トラブルを避けられた。","romaji":"Kanojo no shiryobukai jogen no okage de, toraburu o sakerareta.","german":"Thanks to her thoughtful advice, we were able to avoid trouble.","furigana":"かのじょ|しりょぶか|じょげん|さ"}],"notes":"思慮深い (from 思慮 \"consideration\" and 深い) describes someone who considers the consequences and shows regard for others before acting or speaking; as an い-adjective also adverbial: 思慮深く行動する. The opposite is 思慮が浅い or 軽率. 慎重 stresses caution in acting, 思慮深い the depth of reflection. The word is elevated and sounds appreciative.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"短気を起こさず冷静に対処する。","romaji":"Tanki wo okosazu reisei ni taisho suru.","german":"Deal with it calmly without losing your temper.","furigana":"たんき|お|れいせい|たいしょ"},{"japanese":"父は短気で、すぐに大声を出す。","romaji":"Chichi wa tanki de, sugu ni ōgoe o dasu.","german":"My father is short-tempered and quickly raises his voice.","furigana":"ちち|たんき|おおごえ|だ"},{"japanese":"短気は損気と言うから、落ち着いて話し合おう。","romaji":"Tanki wa sonki to iu kara, ochitsuite hanashiaou.","german":"They say impatience does no good, so let's calm down and talk it over.","furigana":"たんき|そんき|い|お|つ|はな|あ"}],"notes":"短気 means that someone quickly loses patience and flares up: 短気な人, 父は短気だ. Set expressions are 短気を起こす \"to lose one's temper\" and the proverb 短気は損気 \"impatience brings only harm\". The opposite is 気が長い; colloquially people also say 怒りっぽい or すぐキレる. Do not confuse it with the identically read 短期 \"short-term\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"気丈に振る舞う彼女の姿が印象的だった。","romaji":"Kijō ni furumau kanojo no sugata ga inshōteki datta.","german":"Her brave bearing was impressive.","furigana":"きじょう|ふ|ま|かのじょ|すがた|いんしょうてき"},{"japanese":"夫を亡くしたばかりなのに、彼女は葬儀の間ずっと気丈だった。","romaji":"Otto o nakushita bakari na noni, kanojo wa sōgi no aida zutto kijō datta.","german":"Although she had just lost her husband, she remained composed throughout the funeral.","furigana":"おっと|な|かのじょ|そうぎ|あいだ|きじょう"},{"japanese":"入院中の母は「大丈夫よ」と気丈に笑ってみせた。","romaji":"Nyūin-chū no haha wa \"daijōbu yo\" to kijō ni waratte miseta.","german":"My mother, in the hospital, smiled bravely and said, \"I'm fine.\"","furigana":"にゅういんちゅう|はは|だいじょうぶ|きじょう|わら"}],"notes":"気丈 describes someone who keeps their composure in pain, grief or crisis and does not let themselves be beaten down; typical are 気丈に振る舞う and 気丈な母. It is often used of women, older people or the bereaved and implies that the person is suffering inwardly. 勇敢 means courage in danger or battle, 気が強い rather assertiveness, which can also come across negatively.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"意固地にならず柔軟に対応すべきだ。","romaji":"Ikoji ni narazu jūnan ni taiō subeki da.","german":"You shouldn't be obstinate; you should respond flexibly.","furigana":"いこじ|じゅうなん|たいおう"},{"japanese":"一度反対したら、彼は意固地になって譲らない。","romaji":"Ichido hantai shitara, kare wa ikoji ni natte yuzuranai.","german":"Once he has objected, he digs in and won't give way.","furigana":"いちどはんたい|かれ|いこじ|ゆず"},{"japanese":"謝るタイミングを逃して、お互いに意固地になっていた。","romaji":"Ayamaru taimingu o nogashite, otagai ni ikoji ni natte ita.","german":"We missed the moment to apologize, and both of us stubbornly held our ground.","furigana":"あやま|のが|たが|いこじ"}],"notes":"意固地 means defiant stubbornness, often arising from wounded pride; it almost always appears as 意固地になる \"to dig in one's heels, to become obstinate\". It is also written 依怙地. 頑固 is the general \"stubborn\" as a character trait, 強情 stresses insisting on one's own will; a related phrase is 意地を張る.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"強情を張らずに素直に従いなさい。","romaji":"Gōjō wo harazu ni sunao ni shitagainasai.","german":"Don't be stubborn; do as you're told.","furigana":"ごうじょう|は|すなお|したが"},{"japanese":"この子は強情で、一度言い出したら聞かない。","romaji":"Kono ko wa gōjō de, ichido iidashitara kikanai.","german":"This child is headstrong; once he says something, he won't listen.","furigana":"こ|ごうじょう|いちどい|だ|き"},{"japanese":"彼は最後まで自分の非を認めず、強情を張り通した。","romaji":"Kare wa saigo made jibun no hi o mitomezu, gōjō o haritōshita.","german":"He never admitted his fault to the end and kept on being stubborn.","furigana":"かれ|さいご|じぶん|ひ|みと|ごうじょう|は|とお"}],"notes":"強情 describes someone who persistently sticks to their opinion or will even when in the wrong; set phrase: 強情を張る \"to be stubborn\". It is typical of children or of quarrels. 頑固 is the more general \"stubborn\" and can also praise loyalty to principle (頑固な職人), 意固地 stresses defiance born of hurt feelings.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"多様な価値観に対して寛容であるべきだ。","romaji":"Tayō na kachikan ni taishite kan'yō de aru beki da.","german":"We should be tolerant of diverse values.","furigana":"たよう|かちかん|たい|かんよう"},{"japanese":"うちの親は門限に寛容だった。","romaji":"Uchi no oya wa mongen ni kan'yō datta.","german":"My parents were lenient about curfew.","furigana":"おや|もんげん|かんよう"},{"japanese":"寛容の精神を持って、相手の話を最後まで聞こう。","romaji":"Kan'yō no seishin o motte, aite no hanashi o saigo made kikō.","german":"Let's be tolerant and listen to the other person to the end.","furigana":"かんよう|せいしん|も|あいて|はなし|さいご|き"}],"notes":"寛容 means tolerating other views, mistakes or ways of life instead of condemning them strictly; the object takes に or に対して: 多様性に寛容な社会. It often appears in abstract contexts (society, religion, politics) as 寛容な, 寛容さ, 寛容の精神, with the opposite 不寛容. 寛大 stresses generosity in forgiving or punishing (寛大な処置), 度量 personal big-heartedness.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"偏屈な老人と思われがちだが実は優しい。","romaji":"Henkutsu na rōjin to omowaregachi da ga jitsu wa yasashii.","german":"He is often taken for a cantankerous old man, but he is actually kind.","furigana":"へんくつ|ろうじん|おも|じつ|やさ"},{"japanese":"偏屈な店主だが、作るラーメンは絶品だ。","romaji":"Henkutsu na tenshu da ga, tsukuru rāmen wa zeppin da.","german":"The owner is a cranky character, but the ramen he makes is superb.","furigana":"へんくつ|てんしゅ|つく|ぜっぴん"},{"japanese":"彼は偏屈で、自分のやり方以外は一切認めない。","romaji":"Kare wa henkutsu de, jibun no yarikata igai wa issai mitomenai.","german":"He is pigheaded and accepts nothing other than his own way.","furigana":"かれ|へんくつ|じぶん|かたいがい|いっさいみと"}],"notes":"偏屈 describes an obstinate, eccentric person who does not adapt and has trouble getting along with others: 偏屈な老人, 偏屈者. Unlike 頑固, which means stubbornness about a matter, 偏屈 stresses the loner-like, twisted nature as a whole; similar are へそ曲がり and ひねくれ者. The phrase 偏屈だが根は優しい \"cranky but kind at heart\" is common.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"物腰の柔らかい人で誰からも好かれる。","romaji":"Monogoshi no yawarakai hito de dare kara mo sukareru.","german":"He has a gentle manner and is liked by everyone.","furigana":"ものごし|やわ|ひと|だれ|す"},{"japanese":"彼女は物腰が上品で、育ちの良さを感じさせる。","romaji":"Kanojo wa monogoshi ga jōhin de, sodachi no yosa o kanjisaseru.","german":"Her manner is refined and suggests a good upbringing.","furigana":"かのじょ|ものごし|じょうひん|そだ|よ|かん"},{"japanese":"受付の男性は丁寧な物腰で案内してくれた。","romaji":"Uketsuke no dansei wa teinei na monogoshi de annai shite kureta.","german":"The man at the reception desk guided me with a polite manner.","furigana":"うけつけ|だんせい|ていねい|ものごし|あんない"}],"notes":"物腰 is the way someone speaks and moves, that is, their manner in dealing with others; it almost always appears with an adjective: 物腰が柔らかい, 物腰の丁寧な人, 上品な物腰. 態度 is more neutral and can also be negative (態度が悪い), whereas 物腰 is mostly used in praise. For purely physical agility, people say 身のこなし.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"威厳のある風貌の紳士が入ってきた。","romaji":"Igen no aru fūbō no shinshi ga haitte kita.","german":"A gentleman of dignified appearance came in.","furigana":"いげん|ふうぼう|しんし|はい"},{"japanese":"彼はいかにも芸術家らしい風貌をしている。","romaji":"Kare wa ikanimo geijutsuka rashii fūbō o shite iru.","german":"He looks every bit the artist.","furigana":"かれ|げいじゅつか|ふうぼう"},{"japanese":"久しぶりに会った恩師は、すっかり白髪になり風貌が変わっていた。","romaji":"Hisashiburi ni atta onshi wa, sukkari shiraga ni nari fūbō ga kawatte ita.","german":"My old teacher, whom I met after a long time, had gone completely gray and looked changed.","furigana":"ひさ|あ|おんし|しらが|ふうぼう|か"}],"notes":"風貌 denotes a person's whole outward appearance (face, bearing, aura), especially when it reveals something about their nature: 〜らしい風貌, 威厳のある風貌, 〜な風貌をしている. The word is literary. 容姿 refers to figure and beauty, 顔つき to the face, and 外見 is the neutral \"outward appearance\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"母の面影を娘に見ることがある。","romaji":"Haha no omokage wo musume ni miru koto ga aru.","german":"Sometimes I see my mother's features in my daughter.","furigana":"はは|おもかげ|むすめ|み"},{"japanese":"この町には、まだ昔の面影が残っている。","romaji":"Kono machi ni wa, mada mukashi no omokage ga nokotte iru.","german":"Traces of the old days still remain in this town.","furigana":"まち|むかし|おもかげ|のこ"},{"japanese":"同窓会で会った彼女には、少女の頃の面影がなかった。","romaji":"Dōsōkai de atta kanojo ni wa, shōjo no koro no omokage ga nakatta.","german":"When I met her at the class reunion, nothing remained of the girl she used to be.","furigana":"どうそうかい|あ|かのじょ|しょうじょ|ころ|おもかげ"}],"notes":"面影 is the image of a face or place as one remembers it, and the traces of it still visible today: 〜の面影がある, 面影が残る, 面影をしのぶ. For people it is often about resemblance (父の面影がある \"has something of one's father\"), for places 昔の面影. 名残 is similar, but denotes rather remnants of a past era or event, not facial features.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"厳しい面持ちで会議に臨んだ。","romaji":"Kibishii omomochi de kaigi ni nozonda.","german":"He went into the meeting with a stern look.","furigana":"きび|おもも|かいぎ|のぞ"},{"japanese":"受験生たちは緊張した面持ちで試験開始を待っていた。","romaji":"Jukensei-tachi wa kinchō shita omomochi de shiken kaishi o matte ita.","german":"The exam candidates waited for the exam to start with tense expressions.","furigana":"じゅけんせい|きんちょう|おもも|しけんかいし|ま"},{"japanese":"医師は深刻な面持ちで検査結果を告げた。","romaji":"Ishi wa shinkoku na omomochi de kensa kekka o tsugeta.","german":"The doctor announced the test results with a grave expression.","furigana":"いし|しんこく|おもも|けんさけっか|つ"}],"notes":"面持ち is the facial expression as a mirror of one's emotional state and almost always appears in the form 〜な面持ちで + action: 緊張した面持ちで, 不安そうな面持ちで, 神妙な面持ちで. The word is literary and frequent in news and narratives. In everyday speech people say 表情 or simply 顔.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"年齢を重ねて風格が備わってきた。","romaji":"Nenrei wo kasanete fūkaku ga sonawatte kita.","german":"With age, he has acquired dignity.","furigana":"ねんれい|かさ|ふうかく|そな"},{"japanese":"老舗旅館には、長い歴史が生んだ風格がある。","romaji":"Shinise ryokan ni wa, nagai rekishi ga unda fūkaku ga aru.","german":"The long-established inn has a dignity born of its long history.","furigana":"しにせりょかん|なが|れきし|う|ふうかく"},{"japanese":"新人ながら、彼の投球にはすでにエースの風格が漂っている。","romaji":"Shinjin nagara, kare no tōkyū ni wa sude ni ēsu no fūkaku ga tadayotte iru.","german":"Though a rookie, his pitching already has the air of an ace.","furigana":"しんじん|かれ|とうきゅう|ふうかく|ただよ"}],"notes":"風格 is a dignified, assured aura that arises from experience, skill or age: in people, but also in buildings, cities or handwriting: 風格がある, 風格が備わる, 風格が漂う, 王者の風格. 品格, by contrast, stresses moral decency and refinement, and 威厳 an authority that commands respect.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"品格のある振る舞いを心がけている。","romaji":"Hinkaku no aru furumai wo kokorogakete iru.","german":"I try to behave with dignity.","furigana":"ひんかく|ふ|ま|こころ"},{"japanese":"政治家としての品格が問われる発言だ。","romaji":"Seijika to shite no hinkaku ga towareru hatsugen da.","german":"This remark calls into question his dignity as a politician.","furigana":"せいじか|ひんかく|と|はつげん"},{"japanese":"この茶碗には落ち着いた品格がある。","romaji":"Kono chawan ni wa ochitsuita hinkaku ga aru.","german":"This tea bowl has a quiet elegance.","furigana":"ちゃわん|お|つ|ひんかく"}],"notes":"品格 is dignity and refinement that come from decency and character: 品格がある, 品格に欠ける, 〜としての品格が問われる. Things such as works of art or language can also have 品格; in sumo, the 品格 of a yokozuna is regularly debated. The everyday counterpart is 品 (品がある, 上品), and 風格 stresses more aura and assuredness.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"威厳を保ちながらも親しみやすい教授だ。","romaji":"Igen wo tamochi nagara mo shitashimiyasui kyōju da.","german":"He is a professor who maintains his dignity yet is approachable.","furigana":"いげん|たも|した|きょうじゅ"},{"japanese":"父親としての威厳を保とうと、彼は無理に厳しく振る舞った。","romaji":"Chichioya to shite no igen o tamotō to, kare wa muri ni kibishiku furumatta.","german":"To keep up his dignity as a father, he forced himself to act strict.","furigana":"ちちおや|いげん|たも|かれ|むり|きび|ふ|ま"},{"japanese":"王の肖像画は威厳に満ちていた。","romaji":"Ō no shōzōga wa igen ni michite ita.","german":"The portrait of the king was full of majesty.","furigana":"おう|しょうぞうが|いげん|み"}],"notes":"威厳 is a dignified bearing that inspires respect or even awe: 威厳がある, 威厳を保つ, 威厳に満ちた, 威厳を損なう. It is typical of rulers, judges, fathers or teachers. 風格 means assured class through experience, 品格 moral refinement; 権威, by contrast, is \"authority\" in the sense of power or professional standing (その分野の権威).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"体裁を気にして本心を言えない人が多い。","romaji":"Teisai wo ki ni shite honshin wo ienai hito ga ōi.","german":"Many people can't say what they really feel because they worry about appearances.","furigana":"ていさい|き|ほんしん|い|ひと|おお"},{"japanese":"レポートの体裁を整えてから提出してください。","romaji":"Repōto no teisai o totonoete kara teishutsu shite kudasai.","german":"Please put your report into proper form before submitting it.","furigana":"ていさい|ととの|ていしゅつ"},{"japanese":"体裁のいいことばかり言って、何もしない政治家にはうんざりだ。","romaji":"Teisai no ii koto bakari itte, nani mo shinai seijika ni wa unzari da.","german":"I'm fed up with politicians who only say nice-sounding things and do nothing.","furigana":"ていさい|い|なに|せいじか"}],"notes":"体裁 has several meanings: the impression one makes outwardly (体裁を気にする, 体裁が悪い \"embarrassing\", 体裁を繕う \"to keep up appearances\"), the formal layout of a document (体裁を整える) and pleasant-sounding, empty words (体裁のいいことを言う). 世間体 stresses specifically one's reputation in society, 見た目 is the plain \"looks\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"虚栄心の強い人は他人の評価を気にする。","romaji":"Kyoeishin no tsuyoi hito wa tanin no hyōka wo ki ni suru.","german":"Vain people worry about how others judge them.","furigana":"きょえいしん|つよ|ひと|たにん|ひょうか|き"},{"japanese":"彼がブランド品ばかり買うのは、虚栄心からだろう。","romaji":"Kare ga burando-hin bakari kau no wa, kyoeishin kara darō.","german":"The reason he buys only brand-name goods is probably vanity.","furigana":"かれ|ひん|か|きょえいしん"},{"japanese":"虚栄心を満たすために、収入を偽る人もいる。","romaji":"Kyoeishin o mitasu tame ni, shūnyū o itsuwaru hito mo iru.","german":"Some people lie about their income to satisfy their vanity.","furigana":"きょえいしん|み|しゅうにゅう|いつわ|ひと"}],"notes":"虚栄心 (from 虚栄 \"vain glory\" and 心) is the need to appear better than one is in front of others: 虚栄心が強い, 虚栄心を満たす, 〜は虚栄心からだ. The word is always negative. 自尊心, by contrast, is one's own self-respect, which can be quite justified; the colloquial 見栄 (見栄を張る) denotes the showing off itself.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自尊心を傷つけるような言葉は避けるべきだ。","romaji":"Jisonshin wo kizutsukeru yō na kotoba wa sakeru beki da.","german":"We should avoid words that hurt someone's self-esteem.","furigana":"じそんしん|きず|ことば|さ"},{"japanese":"自尊心が強い彼は、人に助けを求めるのが苦手だ。","romaji":"Jisonshin ga tsuyoi kare wa, hito ni tasuke o motomeru no ga nigate da.","german":"He is very proud and finds it hard to ask others for help.","furigana":"じそんしん|つよ|かれ|ひと|たす|もと|にがて"},{"japanese":"子どもの自尊心を育てるには、小さな成功を褒めることが大切だ。","romaji":"Kodomo no jisonshin o sodateru ni wa, chiisana seikō o homeru koto ga taisetsu da.","german":"To build children's self-esteem, it is important to praise small successes.","furigana":"こ|じそんしん|そだ|ちい|せいこう|ほ|たいせつ"}],"notes":"自尊心 is the sense of one's own worth and dignity, \"self-respect\" or \"pride\" depending on context: 自尊心が強い, 自尊心を傷つける, 自尊心をくすぐる \"to flatter someone\". Colloquially people usually say プライド (プライドが高い). In psychology, \"self-esteem\" is also called 自己肯定感 or 自尊感情; 虚栄心, by contrast, is vanity before others.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の心意気に感動した。","romaji":"Kare no kokoroiki ni kandō shita.","german":"I was moved by his spirit.","furigana":"かれ|こころいき|かんどう"},{"japanese":"赤字でも地元の店を守ろうという店主の心意気に打たれた。","romaji":"Akaji demo jimoto no mise o mamorō to iu tenshu no kokoroiki ni utareta.","german":"I was struck by the shop owner's determination to protect the local store even at a loss.","furigana":"あかじ|じもと|みせ|まも|てんしゅ|こころいき|う"},{"japanese":"最後まで諦めない、その心意気が大事だ。","romaji":"Saigo made akiramenai, sono kokoroiki ga daiji da.","german":"What matters is the spirit of never giving up until the end.","furigana":"さいご|あきら|こころいき|だいじ"}],"notes":"心意気 is the spirited, often selfless attitude with which someone takes on a matter: 〜という心意気, 心意気に感じる, 心意気に打たれる, 職人の心意気. It is appreciative, emotional and somewhat old-fashioned (江戸っ子の心意気). 意気込み is the eagerness before a concrete task, 決意 the sober resolution.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"情けは人の為ならずという諺がある。","romaji":"Nasake wa hito no tame narazu to iu kotowaza ga aru.","german":"There is a proverb: kindness to others comes back to you in the end.","furigana":"なさ|ひと|ため|ことわざ"},{"japanese":"敵に情けをかけるな。","romaji":"Teki ni nasake o kakeru na.","german":"Show no mercy to the enemy.","furigana":"てき|なさ"},{"japanese":"旅先で困っていたとき、見知らぬ人の情けが身にしみた。","romaji":"Tabisaki de komatte ita toki, mishiranu hito no nasake ga mi ni shimita.","german":"When I was in trouble while traveling, a stranger's kindness touched me deeply.","furigana":"たびさき|こま|みし|ひと|なさ|み"}],"notes":"情け is compassion and kindness toward others: 情けをかける \"to show mercy\", 人の情け, 情け容赦なく \"mercilessly\". The proverb 情けは人の為ならず is often wrongly understood as \"pity spoils the other person\"; it means \"kindness comes back to oneself\". Do not confuse it with the adjective 情けない \"pitiful, shameful\". 同情 is more matter-of-fact \"sympathy\", 哀れみ more condescending \"pity\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"哀れみの目で見られるのは辛い。","romaji":"Awaremi no me de mirareru no wa tsurai.","german":"It is painful to be looked at with pitying eyes.","furigana":"あわ|め|み|つら"},{"japanese":"彼は哀れみを乞うような目で私を見た。","romaji":"Kare wa awaremi o kou yō na me de watashi o mita.","german":"He looked at me with eyes that seemed to beg for pity.","furigana":"かれ|あわ|こ|め|わたし|み"},{"japanese":"捨てられた子犬に哀れみを感じて、家に連れて帰った。","romaji":"Suterareta koinu ni awaremi o kanjite, ie ni tsurete kaetta.","german":"Feeling pity for the abandoned puppy, I took it home.","furigana":"す|こいぬ|あわ|かん|いえ|つ|かえ"}],"notes":"哀れみ (also 憐れみ) is pity for someone in a pitiable situation; it is the noun of the verb 哀れむ. Typical are 哀れみを感じる, 哀れみを乞う and 哀れみの目. Because it carries a gap between the one who pities and the one pitied, those affected often find it insulting; 同情 is more neutral, 思いやり warmer.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"侮蔑的な言葉を投げかけるのは許されない。","romaji":"Bubetsuteki na kotoba wo nagekakeru no wa yurusarenai.","german":"It is unacceptable to hurl contemptuous words at someone.","furigana":"ぶべつてき|ことば|な|ゆる"},{"japanese":"彼は侮蔑のまなざしで相手を見下ろした。","romaji":"Kare wa bubetsu no manazashi de aite o mioroshita.","german":"He looked down at the other person with scorn.","furigana":"かれ|ぶべつ|あいて|みお"},{"japanese":"移民を侮蔑する発言が大きな批判を浴びた。","romaji":"Imin o bubetsu suru hatsugen ga ōkina hihan o abita.","german":"Remarks that disparaged immigrants drew heavy criticism.","furigana":"いみん|ぶべつ|はつげん|おお|ひはん|あ"}],"notes":"侮蔑 is open contempt with which one belittles someone; as a transitive する-verb 〜を侮蔑する, but more often in 侮蔑的な, 侮蔑の目, 侮蔑のまなざし, 侮蔑を込めて. It is literary and strong. 軽蔑 is the more general word for \"contempt\" and is also common in everyday speech; 侮辱 denotes the concrete \"insult\" through words or deeds.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不正に対して激しく憤慨した。","romaji":"Fusei ni taishite hageshiku fungai shita.","german":"He was deeply indignant at the injustice.","furigana":"ふせい|たい|はげ|ふんがい"},{"japanese":"住民たちは市の一方的な決定に憤慨している。","romaji":"Jūmin-tachi wa shi no ippōteki na kettei ni fungai shite iru.","german":"The residents are outraged at the city's unilateral decision.","furigana":"じゅうみん|し|いっぽうてき|けってい|ふんがい"},{"japanese":"憤慨した客が、店長を呼べと怒鳴った。","romaji":"Fungai shita kyaku ga, tenchō o yobe to donatta.","german":"An angry customer shouted for the manager to be called.","furigana":"ふんがい|きゃく|てんちょう|よ|どな"}],"notes":"憤慨 is indignation at something one considers unjust or outrageous; as a する-verb, the cause takes に or に対して: 不正に憤慨する. It denotes a morally grounded, visible agitation, not mere annoyance (腹が立つ, 怒る). 憤り is the more elevated noun for the feeling itself.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"社会の不公平に対する憤りを感じる。","romaji":"Shakai no fukōhei ni taisuru ikidōri wo kanjiru.","german":"I feel indignation at the unfairness of society.","furigana":"しゃかい|ふこうへい|たい|いきどお|かん"},{"japanese":"被害者の家族は、判決に強い憤りをあらわにした。","romaji":"Higaisha no kazoku wa, hanketsu ni tsuyoi ikidōri o arawa ni shita.","german":"The victim's family openly showed their outrage at the verdict.","furigana":"ひがいしゃ|かぞく|はんけつ|つよ|いきどお"},{"japanese":"彼は憤りを抑えきれず、机をたたいた。","romaji":"Kare wa ikidōri o osaekirezu, tsukue o tataita.","german":"He could not contain his anger and banged on the desk.","furigana":"かれ|いきどお|おさ|つくえ"}],"notes":"憤り is anger or indignation at injustice; it is the noun of the verb 憤る. Typical are 〜に対する憤りを感じる, 憤りを覚える, 憤りをあらわにする and 憤りを抑える. The word is elevated and frequent in news and statements. 怒り is the general word for \"anger\", 憤慨 the state of being indignant as a する-verb.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"長年の取引で懇意にしている業者がある。","romaji":"Naganen no torihiki de kon'i ni shite iru gyōsha ga aru.","german":"There is a supplier with whom we have a close relationship from years of trading.","furigana":"ながねん|とりひき|こんい|ぎょうしゃ"},{"japanese":"社長とは昔から懇意にさせていただいております。","romaji":"Shachō to wa mukashi kara kon'i ni sasete itadaite orimasu.","german":"I have been on friendly terms with the company president for a long time.","furigana":"しゃちょう|むかし|こんい"},{"japanese":"懇意にしている店の主人が、特別に席を用意してくれた。","romaji":"Kon'i ni shite iru mise no shujin ga, tokubetsu ni seki o yōi shite kureta.","german":"The owner of a shop I frequent specially arranged a seat for us.","furigana":"こんい|みせ|しゅじん|とくべつ|せき|ようい"}],"notes":"懇意 denotes a close, friendly relationship, often in a business setting too. It almost always appears in set phrases: 懇意にする \"to cultivate good relations\", 懇意にしている店 \"a place where one is well known\", 懇意な間柄 \"close relationship\". It sounds more elevated and somewhat more distant than 親しい or 仲がいい. Do not confuse it with 好意 \"affection\" or 厚意 \"kindness, favor\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"引っ越してから旧友と疎遠になった。","romaji":"Hikkoshite kara kyūyū to soen ni natta.","german":"Since moving, I have drifted apart from old friends.","furigana":"ひ|こ|きゅうゆう|そえん"},{"japanese":"仕事が忙しくて、実家とすっかり疎遠になってしまった。","romaji":"Shigoto ga isogashikute, jikka to sukkari soen ni natte shimatta.","german":"I've been so busy with work that I've completely lost touch with my parents' home.","furigana":"しごと|いそが|じっか|そえん"},{"japanese":"疎遠だった親戚と、祖父の葬儀で久しぶりに再会した。","romaji":"Soen datta shinseki to, sofu no sōgi de hisashiburi ni saikai shita.","german":"At my grandfather's funeral, I met again after a long time the relatives I had been distant from.","furigana":"そえん|しんせき|そふ|そうぎ|ひさ|さいかい"}],"notes":"疎遠 describes contact and closeness fading over time, usually without a quarrel, simply through distance or changed circumstances. Typical: (〜と)疎遠になる \"to lose touch\", 疎遠な関係, 疎遠だった親戚. The opposite is 親密 or 懇意. Unlike 不仲 or 確執, it carries no hostility.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"兄弟間の確執が長年続いている。","romaji":"Kyōdaikan no kakushitsu ga naganen tsuzuite iru.","german":"The feud between the brothers has lasted for many years.","furigana":"きょうだいかん|かくしつ|ながねんつづ"},{"japanese":"監督と主力選手の確執が報じられた。","romaji":"Kantoku to shuryoku senshu no kakushitsu ga hōjirareta.","german":"A rift between the manager and a key player was reported.","furigana":"かんとく|しゅりょくせんしゅ|かくしつ|ほう"},{"japanese":"父との確執を乗り越え、彼は家業を継ぐことにした。","romaji":"Chichi to no kakushitsu o norikoe, kare wa kagyō o tsugu koto ni shita.","german":"Overcoming his conflict with his father, he decided to take over the family business.","furigana":"ちち|かくしつ|の|こ|かれ|かぎょう|つ"}],"notes":"確執 is a deep-seated, long-lasting conflict between persons or groups in which both sides stubbornly stick to their position. Typical: 〜との確執, 〜の間に確執がある/生じる, 確執が深まる, 確執を乗り越える. The word is literary and popular in press reports about families, politicians or sports clubs; in everyday speech people tend to say 仲が悪い or もめている. Unlike 喧嘩, it does not mean a single quarrel but a lasting rift.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"些細なことから反目し合うようになった。","romaji":"Sasai na koto kara hanmoku shiau yō ni natta.","german":"They came to be at odds over a trivial matter.","furigana":"ささい|はんもく|あ"},{"japanese":"両国は長年反目してきたが、ついに和解した。","romaji":"Ryōkoku wa naganen hanmoku shite kita ga, tsui ni wakai shita.","german":"The two countries were hostile for many years but finally reconciled.","furigana":"りょうこく|ながねんはんもく|わかい"},{"japanese":"社内の二つの派閥が反目し、会議が全く進まない。","romaji":"Shanai no futatsu no habatsu ga hanmoku shi, kaigi ga mattaku susumanai.","german":"Two factions within the company are at odds, and meetings get nowhere.","furigana":"しゃない|ふた|はばつ|はんもく|かいぎ|まった|すす"}],"notes":"反目 means that two sides stand against each other with hostility and cannot stand one another. As an intransitive する-verb: AとBが反目する, 〜と反目する, very often 反目し合う \"to feud with each other\"; as a noun: 両派の反目. It is literary and stresses mutual aversion, whereas 確執 denotes the entrenched conflict and 対立 neutrally the opposing positions.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"恩義のある人には報いたいと思う。","romaji":"Ongi no aru hito ni wa mukuitai to omou.","german":"I want to repay those to whom I am indebted.","furigana":"おんぎ|ひと|むく|おも"},{"japanese":"彼には若い頃に助けてもらった恩義がある。","romaji":"Kare ni wa wakai koro ni tasukete moratta ongi ga aru.","german":"I owe him a debt of gratitude for helping me when I was young.","furigana":"かれ|わか|ころ|たす|おんぎ"},{"japanese":"会社への恩義を感じて、転職の誘いを断った。","romaji":"Kaisha e no ongi o kanjite, tenshoku no sasoi o kotowatta.","german":"Feeling indebted to my company, I turned down the job offer.","furigana":"かいしゃ|おんぎ|かん|てんしょく|さそ|ことわ"}],"notes":"恩義 is the moral obligation that arises from kindness received. Typical: (〜に)恩義がある \"to be indebted to someone\", 恩義を感じる, 恩義に報いる \"to repay a debt of gratitude\", 恩義を忘れる. 恩 alone is the benefit itself (恩を返す, 恩返し), while 恩義 stresses more the resulting duty. The word is serious and somewhat old-fashioned; one who disregards it is called 恩知らず \"ungrateful\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"師への報恩の気持ちを忘れない。","romaji":"Shi e no hōon no kimochi wo wasurenai.","german":"I will never forget my feeling of gratitude toward my teacher.","furigana":"し|ほうおん|きも|わす"},{"japanese":"地域への報恩として、彼は財産の一部を学校に寄付した。","romaji":"Chiiki e no hōon to shite, kare wa zaisan no ichibu o gakkō ni kifu shita.","german":"To repay the community's kindness, he donated part of his fortune to a school.","furigana":"ちいき|ほうおん|かれ|ざいさん|いちぶ|がっこう|きふ"},{"japanese":"報恩の念を胸に、彼女は恩師の研究を引き継いだ。","romaji":"Hōon no nen o mune ni, kanojo wa onshi no kenkyū o hikitsuida.","german":"With gratitude in her heart, she carried on her mentor's research.","furigana":"ほうおん|ねん|むね|かのじょ|おんし|けんきゅう|ひ|つ"}],"notes":"報恩 means repaying kindness received (報いる + 恩). It is literary and often has a moral or religious coloring, for example in 報恩感謝 or the Buddhist 報恩講. In everyday speech people say 恩返し(をする) or 恩に報いる instead. The opposite is 忘恩 \"ingratitude\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二人の出会いには不思議な因縁がある。","romaji":"Futari no deai ni wa fushigi na innen ga aru.","german":"There is a strange destiny behind the two people's meeting.","furigana":"ふたり|であ|ふしぎ|いんねん"},{"japanese":"因縁の対決は延長戦の末に決着した。","romaji":"Innen no taiketsu wa enchōsen no sue ni ketchaku shita.","german":"The long-awaited showdown between old rivals was decided after extra time.","furigana":"いんねん|たいけつ|えんちょうせん|すえ|けっちゃく"},{"japanese":"駅で酔っ払いに因縁をつけられて困った。","romaji":"Eki de yopparai ni innen o tsukerarete komatta.","german":"A drunk picked a fight with me at the station for no reason, which was quite unpleasant.","furigana":"えき|よ|ぱら|いんねん|こま"}],"notes":"因縁 comes from Buddhism (因 \"cause\" and 縁 \"condition\") and denotes a fateful connection: 不思議な因縁, 浅からぬ因縁 \"a deep connection\", 因縁の対決 \"showdown between old rivals\". Also very common is 因縁をつける \"to pick a fight under a pretext, to accost someone\". The word sounds heavier and often gloomier than 縁 (ご縁がある), which is friendly and everyday.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"縁故採用に対する批判が高まっている。","romaji":"Enko saiyō ni taisuru hihan ga takamatte iru.","german":"Criticism of hiring through connections is growing.","furigana":"えんこさいよう|たい|ひはん|たか"},{"japanese":"彼は縁故を頼って東京で仕事を見つけた。","romaji":"Kare wa enko o tayotte Tōkyō de shigoto o mitsuketa.","german":"He found a job in Tokyo by relying on his connections.","furigana":"かれ|えんこ|たよ|とうきょう|しごと|み"},{"japanese":"この地域には縁故のある人が一人もいない。","romaji":"Kono chiiki ni wa enko no aru hito ga hitori mo inai.","german":"I have no connections whatsoever in this area.","furigana":"ちいき|えんこ|ひと|ひとり"}],"notes":"縁故 denotes personal connections through kinship, marriage or acquaintance, especially when used for advantage. Typical: 縁故採用 \"hiring through connections\", 縁故を頼る \"to rely on one's connections\", 縁故のある土地. In everyday speech コネ is more common and more casual; 縁故 is neutral-formal, but in 縁故採用 it is usually meant critically.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"末永いご交誼をお願い申し上げます。","romaji":"Suenagai go kōgi wo onegai mōshiagemasu.","german":"I humbly ask for your continued friendship.","furigana":"すえなが|こうぎ|ねが|もう|あ"},{"japanese":"平素は格別のご交誼を賜り、厚く御礼申し上げます。","romaji":"Heiso wa kakubetsu no go kōgi o tamawari, atsuku onrei mōshiagemasu.","german":"We sincerely thank you for your especially kind goodwill as always.","furigana":"へいそ|かくべつ|こうぎ|たまわ|あつ|おんれいもう|あ"},{"japanese":"両家は三代にわたって交誼を結んできた。","romaji":"Ryōke wa sandai ni watatte kōgi o musunde kita.","german":"The two families have maintained friendly relations for three generations.","furigana":"りょうけ|さんだい|こうぎ|むす"}],"notes":"交誼 is a very formal word for friendly relations and occurs almost only in letters, greeting cards and speeches, usually with the prefix ご: 平素のご交誼に感謝申し上げます, 今後とも変わらぬご交誼のほどお願い申し上げます. There is also 交誼を結ぶ \"to form friendly relations\". In conversation people say お付き合い or 親交 instead; the identically read 厚誼 \"cordial friendship\" appears in the same formulas.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼とは学生時代からの知己だ。","romaji":"Kare to wa gakusei jidai kara no chiki da.","german":"He and I have known each other since our student days.","furigana":"かれ|がくせいじだい|ちき"},{"japanese":"留学先で多くの知己を得たことが、私の財産だ。","romaji":"Ryūgakusaki de ōku no chiki o eta koto ga, watashi no zaisan da.","german":"The many acquaintances I made while studying abroad are my treasure.","furigana":"りゅうがくさき|おお|ちき|え|わたし|ざいさん"},{"japanese":"初対面なのに、まるで百年の知己のように話が弾んだ。","romaji":"Shotaimen na no ni, marude hyakunen no chiki no yō ni hanashi ga hazunda.","german":"Although we had only just met, we hit it off as if we had been friends for a hundred years.","furigana":"しょたいめん|ひゃくねん|ちき|はなし|はず"}],"notes":"知己 is an elevated word, originally \"one who truly knows (知) me (己)\", that is, a confidant who understands one. It also simply means \"acquaintance\": 知己を得る \"to make someone's acquaintance\". Well known is 百年の知己 \"as if one had known each other for a hundred years\", for instant familiarity. In everyday speech people say 知り合い or 親友; 知己 sounds literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"信頼できる朋友に恵まれた人生だ。","romaji":"Shinrai dekiru hōyū ni megumareta jinsei da.","german":"It is a life blessed with trustworthy friends.","furigana":"しんらい|ほうゆう|めぐ|じんせい"},{"japanese":"卒業式で、校長は朋友との絆を大切にするよう語った。","romaji":"Sotsugyōshiki de, kōchō wa hōyū to no kizuna o taisetsu ni suru yō katatta.","german":"At the graduation ceremony, the principal told the students to cherish their bonds with their companions.","furigana":"そつぎょうしき|こうちょう|ほうゆう|きずな|たいせつ|かた"},{"japanese":"彼は朋友の死を悼んで一編の詩を書いた。","romaji":"Kare wa hōyū no shi o itande ippen no shi o kaita.","german":"Mourning the death of his friend, he wrote a poem.","furigana":"かれ|ほうゆう|し|いた|いっぺん|し|か"}],"notes":"朋友 is a literary, somewhat old-fashioned word for \"friend, companion\" found almost only in written texts, speeches or poems. Both characters mean \"friend\"; 朋 originally meant fellow students under the same teacher. In everyday speech people use 友人, 友達 or 仲間.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"海外で暮らす同胞を支援する。","romaji":"Kaigai de kurasu dōhō wo shien suru.","german":"We support compatriots living overseas.","furigana":"かいがい|く|どうほう|しえん"},{"japanese":"災害の知らせを受け、世界中の同胞から寄付が集まった。","romaji":"Saigai no shirase o uke, sekaijū no dōhō kara kifu ga atsumatta.","german":"After the news of the disaster, donations were collected from compatriots around the world.","furigana":"さいがい|し|う|せかいじゅう|どうほう|きふ|あつ"},{"japanese":"演説で大統領は「同胞の皆さん」と国民に呼びかけた。","romaji":"Enzetsu de daitōryō wa 'dōhō no minasan' to kokumin ni yobikaketa.","german":"In his speech, the president addressed the people as \"fellow countrymen\".","furigana":"えんぜつ|だいとうりょう|どうほう|みな|こくみん|よ"}],"notes":"同胞 denotes people of the same nation or country, that is, \"compatriots\", often with an emotional undertone of solidarity: 在外同胞 \"compatriots abroad\", 同胞を救う. Originally it means \"siblings from the same womb\"; in this meaning and poetically it is also read はらから. More neutral are 同国人 or 同郷の人 \"people from the same home region\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先達の功績に敬意を表する。","romaji":"Sendatsu no kōseki ni keii wo hyō suru.","german":"We pay tribute to the achievements of our predecessors.","furigana":"せんだつ|こうせき|けいい|ひょう"},{"japanese":"この分野の先達として、彼女は若手研究者を導いてきた。","romaji":"Kono bun'ya no sendatsu to shite, kanojo wa wakate kenkyūsha o michibiite kita.","german":"As a pioneer in this field, she has guided young researchers.","furigana":"ぶんや|せんだつ|かのじょ|わかてけんきゅうしゃ|みちび"},{"japanese":"山では経験豊かな先達に従うのが一番安全だ。","romaji":"Yama de wa keiken yutaka na sendatsu ni shitagau no ga ichiban anzen da.","german":"In the mountains, it is safest to follow an experienced guide.","furigana":"やま|けいけんゆた|せんだつ|したが|いちばんあんぜん"}],"notes":"先達 is someone who walked a path first and leads others on it: a pioneer or experienced predecessor in a field. Typical: 先達の教え/知恵/功績, 〜の先達として. It is elevated and respectful; in everyday speech people say 先輩 or 先人. Originally it denotes the leader of pilgrim groups in Shugendō; besides せんだつ, the reading せんだち is also common.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"肉親との別れほど辛いものはない。","romaji":"Nikushin to no wakare hodo tsurai mono wa nai.","german":"Nothing is as painful as parting from a blood relative.","furigana":"にくしん|わか|つら"},{"japanese":"戦争で生き別れた肉親を、彼は六十年探し続けた。","romaji":"Sensō de ikiwakareta nikushin o, kare wa rokujūnen sagashitsuzuketa.","german":"For sixty years he kept searching for the relatives from whom he had been separated by the war.","furigana":"せんそう|い|わか|にくしん|かれ|ろくじゅうねんさが|つづ"},{"japanese":"祖母は私にとって唯一の肉親です。","romaji":"Sobo wa watashi ni totte yuiitsu no nikushin desu.","german":"My grandmother is my only blood relative.","furigana":"そぼ|わたし|ゆいいつ|にくしん"}],"notes":"肉親 denotes the closest blood relatives (parents, children, siblings) and stresses the physical and emotional bond: 肉親の情, 肉親を亡くす, 唯一の肉親. It is narrower than 親戚 or 親類 (relatives in general) and warmer than the legal 親族. It is often met in serious contexts such as death, illness or separation.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"係累のない身軽さで世界を旅している。","romaji":"Keirui no nai migarusa de sekai wo tabi shite iru.","german":"Free of family ties, he travels the world unencumbered.","furigana":"けいるい|みがる|せかい|たび"},{"japanese":"係累が多く、彼は簡単には転職できなかった。","romaji":"Keirui ga ōku, kare wa kantan ni wa tenshoku dekinakatta.","german":"He had many dependents, so he could not easily change jobs.","furigana":"けいるい|おお|かれ|かんたん|てんしょく"},{"japanese":"係累を持たない僧侶として、彼は山寺で静かに暮らした。","romaji":"Keirui o motanai sōryo to shite, kare wa yamadera de shizuka ni kurashita.","german":"As a monk without family ties, he lived quietly in a mountain temple.","furigana":"けいるい|も|そうりょ|かれ|やまでら|しず|く"}],"notes":"係累 are dependents one has to provide for, and in a wider sense everything that binds and restricts one. It mostly appears negated or as a burden: 係累がない \"unattached, without family\", 係累が多い, 係累に縛られる. It is literary; in everyday speech people say 家族 or 扶養家族, and for social obligations rather しがらみ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"親睦を深めるために懇親会を開いた。","romaji":"Shinboku wo fukameru tame ni konshinkai wo hiraita.","german":"We held a social gathering to deepen friendship.","furigana":"しんぼく|ふか|こんしんかい|ひら"},{"japanese":"新入社員の親睦を図るため、バーベキュー大会が企画された。","romaji":"Shinnyū shain no shinboku o hakaru tame, bābekyū taikai ga kikaku sareta.","german":"A barbecue event was planned to help the new employees get to know each other.","furigana":"しんにゅうしゃいん|しんぼく|はか|たいかい|きかく"},{"japanese":"町内会の親睦旅行で温泉に行った。","romaji":"Chōnaikai no shinboku ryokō de onsen ni itta.","german":"We went to a hot spring on the neighborhood association's social trip.","furigana":"ちょうないかい|しんぼくりょこう|おんせん|い"}],"notes":"親睦 is friendly togetherness in a group, such as among colleagues, neighbors or club members. It almost always appears in set combinations: 親睦を深める/図る, 親睦会 \"social gathering\", 親睦旅行, 親睦団体. It sounds more formal than 仲良くなる and refers to the group atmosphere, not to a single close friendship like 親交.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"交遊の広い人物として知られている。","romaji":"Kōyū no hiroi jinbutsu to shite shirarete iru.","german":"He is known as a person with a wide circle of acquaintances.","furigana":"こうゆう|ひろ|じんぶつ|し"},{"japanese":"作家の交遊録には、多くの著名人が登場する。","romaji":"Sakka no kōyūroku ni wa, ōku no chomeijin ga tōjō suru.","german":"Many famous people appear in the writer's memoir of his associates.","furigana":"さっか|こうゆうろく|おお|ちょめいじん|とうじょう"},{"japanese":"学生時代、彼は留学生たちと盛んに交遊した。","romaji":"Gakusei jidai, kare wa ryūgakuseitachi to sakan ni kōyū shita.","german":"As a student, he socialized actively with international students.","furigana":"がくせいじだい|かれ|りゅうがくせい|さか|こうゆう"}],"notes":"交遊 denotes sociable contact with friends and acquaintances: going out, meeting and doing things together. Typical: 〜と交遊がある, 交遊範囲が広い, 交遊録 \"memoir of associates\". The identically read 交友 means rather the friendship or the friends themselves (交友関係 is the more common spelling); 交遊 stresses doing things together. Both are literary; in conversation people say 付き合い.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女の細やかな気遣いに感謝している。","romaji":"Kanojo no komayaka na kizukai ni kansha shite iru.","german":"I am grateful for her attentive thoughtfulness.","furigana":"かのじょ|こま|きづか|かんしゃ"},{"japanese":"「どうぞお気遣いなく」と言って、彼はお茶を断った。","romaji":"'Dōzo okizukai naku' to itte, kare wa ocha o kotowatta.","german":"Saying \"please don't trouble yourself\", he declined the tea.","furigana":"きづか|い|かれ|ちゃ|ことわ"},{"japanese":"彼は気遣いができるので、職場で頼りにされている。","romaji":"Kare wa kizukai ga dekiru node, shokuba de tayori ni sarete iru.","german":"He is thoughtful, so people rely on him at work.","furigana":"かれ|きづか|しょくば|たよ"}],"notes":"気遣い is attentive consideration, thinking ahead about the needs of others, a highly valued trait in Japanese. Typical: 細やかな気遣い, 気遣いができる人, 〜への気遣い. Very common are the polite formulas お気遣いありがとうございます and お気遣いなく \"please don't go to any trouble\". The verb is 気遣う \"to worry about someone\"; 配慮 is more formal and matter-of-fact, 心配 stresses the worry itself.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"病人への労りの気持ちを忘れてはならない。","romaji":"Byōnin e no itawari no kimochi wo wasurete wa naranai.","german":"We must not forget compassion for the sick.","furigana":"びょうにん|いたわ|きも|わす"},{"japanese":"疲れて帰った夫に、妻はいたわりの言葉をかけた。","romaji":"Tsukarete kaetta otto ni, tsuma wa itawari no kotoba o kaketa.","german":"When her husband came home tired, his wife spoke kind words to him.","furigana":"つか|かえ|おっと|つま|ことば"},{"japanese":"お年寄りへのいたわりが感じられる町づくりを目指す。","romaji":"Otoshiyori e no itawari ga kanjirareru machizukuri o mezasu.","german":"We aim to build a town where consideration for the elderly can be felt.","furigana":"としよ|かん|まち|めざ"}],"notes":"労り (mostly いたわり in kana) is compassionate care toward the weak, sick, elderly or exhausted: one shows consideration and spares them. Typical: いたわりの気持ち/言葉, 〜へのいたわり. The verb is 労る/いたわる \"to go easy on, to look after\" (体をいたわる). Unlike 気遣い, which means general attentiveness, いたわり puts the foreground on sympathy for someone in a difficult situation.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"敬愛する先生に感謝の手紙を書いた。","romaji":"Keiai suru sensei ni kansha no tegami wo kaita.","german":"I wrote a thank-you letter to my beloved teacher.","furigana":"けいあい|せんせい|かんしゃ|てがみ|か"},{"japanese":"故人は地域の人々から敬愛されていた。","romaji":"Kojin wa chiiki no hitobito kara keiai sarete ita.","german":"The deceased was revered and loved by the people of the area.","furigana":"こじん|ちいき|ひとびと|けいあい"},{"japanese":"部下たちは社長に深い敬愛の念を抱いていた。","romaji":"Bukatachi wa shachō ni fukai keiai no nen o idaite ita.","german":"The employees held deep respect and affection for the president.","furigana":"ぶか|しゃちょう|ふか|けいあい|ねん|いだ"}],"notes":"敬愛 combines respect (敬) and affection (愛): one looks up to someone and is fond of them at the same time. Typical as an attributive する-verb: 敬愛する先生/作家, and also 〜を敬愛する, 〜から敬愛される, 敬愛の念を抱く. The word is elevated; 尊敬 stresses only esteem, 親愛 only affection (親愛なる〜 \"Dear ...\" in letters).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"年長者を敬うことは大切な礼儀だ。","romaji":"Nenchōsha wo uyamau koto wa taisetsu na reigi da.","german":"Respecting one's elders is an important form of courtesy.","furigana":"ねんちょうしゃ|うやま|たいせつ|れいぎ"},{"japanese":"敬老の日は、お年寄りを敬い、長寿を祝う日だ。","romaji":"Keirō no hi wa, otoshiyori o uyamai, chōju o iwau hi da.","german":"Respect for the Aged Day is a day to honor older people and celebrate longevity.","furigana":"けいろう|ひ|としよ|うやま|ちょうじゅ|いわ|ひ"},{"japanese":"この村の人々は昔から山の神を敬ってきた。","romaji":"Kono mura no hitobito wa mukashi kara yama no kami o uyamatte kita.","german":"The people of this village have revered the mountain god since long ago.","furigana":"むら|ひとびと|むかし|やま|かみ|うやま"}],"notes":"敬う means to esteem and honor someone, above all elders, parents, teachers, gods or ancestors: 年長者を敬う, 神仏を敬う. It describes a basic attitude or duty rather than a personal feeling; 尊敬する is more everyday and expresses personal admiration (尊敬する人 \"role model\"). As a noun one usually uses 敬意 \"respect\" (敬意を払う/表する).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子供に優しく諭すように話した。","romaji":"Kodomo ni yasashiku satosu yō ni hanashita.","german":"I spoke to the child gently, as if to reason with him.","furigana":"こども|やさ|さと|はな"},{"japanese":"先生は、嘘をついた生徒に正直であることの大切さを諭した。","romaji":"Sensei wa, uso o tsuita seito ni shōjiki de aru koto no taisetsusa o satoshita.","german":"The teacher counseled the student who had lied on the importance of being honest.","furigana":"せんせい|うそ|せいと|しょうじき|たいせつ|さと"},{"japanese":"無理をするなと母に諭され、私は退院を一日延ばした。","romaji":"Muri o suru na to haha ni satosare, watashi wa taiin o ichinichi nobashita.","german":"My mother told me not to overdo it, so I extended my hospital stay by a day.","furigana":"むり|はは|さと|わたし|たいいん|いちにちの"}],"notes":"諭す means calmly and reasonably making someone understand what they are doing wrong or what is right, typically from parents, teachers or elders toward younger people. Construction: 人を諭す, 人に〜と諭す or 〜ように諭す. Unlike 叱る \"to scold\", the tone is gentle; 説得する \"to persuade\" aims at a decision, 諭す at insight. Intensified: 教え諭す.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"無礼な態度を窘められた。","romaji":"Burei na taido wo tashinamerareta.","german":"I was reprimanded for my rude attitude.","furigana":"ぶれい|たいど|たしな"},{"japanese":"母は、食事中にスマホを見る弟をたしなめた。","romaji":"Haha wa, shokujichū ni sumaho o miru otōto o tashinameta.","german":"My mother scolded my younger brother for looking at his phone during the meal.","furigana":"はは|しょくじちゅう|み|おとうと"},{"japanese":"議長は感情的になった委員を穏やかにたしなめた。","romaji":"Gichō wa kanjōteki ni natta iin o odayaka ni tashinameta.","german":"The chairman calmly rebuked the committee member who had become emotional.","furigana":"ぎちょう|かんじょうてき|いいん|おだ"}],"notes":"窘める (mostly たしなめる in kana) means to reprimand someone calmly but clearly for inappropriate behavior or words, more a brief rebuke than a lecture. Typical: 〜を(やんわりと)たしなめる, 軽率な発言をたしなめる. It is milder than 叱る and also occurs among equals or toward elders. Do not confuse it with the similar-sounding 嗜む \"to devote oneself to something, to enjoy in moderation\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"怒っている彼女を宥めるのに苦労した。","romaji":"Okotte iru kanojo wo nadameru no ni kurō shita.","german":"It was hard to calm her down when she was angry.","furigana":"おこ|かのじょ|なだ|くろう"},{"japanese":"雷を怖がる子供を優しく宥めた。","romaji":"Kaminari o kowagaru kodomo o yasashiku nadameta.","german":"I gently soothed the child, who was afraid of the thunder.","furigana":"かみなり|こわ|こども|やさ|なだ"}],"notes":"人を宥める means to calm or soothe someone, for example when they are angry or frightened. This transitive ichidan verb forms 宥めます・宥めて・宥めない. The kana spelling なだめる is common. Unlike 宥す（ゆるす, to forgive）, it is about the person's feelings, not about forgiving a fault.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"弱い者を庇うのは当然のことだ。","romaji":"Yowai mono wo kabau no wa tōzen no koto da.","german":"It is only natural to protect the weak.","furigana":"よわ|もの|かば|とうぜん"},{"japanese":"怪我をした右足をかばいながら、彼はゴールまで走った。","romaji":"Kega o shita migiashi o kabainagara, kare wa gōru made hashitta.","german":"Favoring his injured right foot, he ran all the way to the finish.","furigana":"けが|みぎあし|かれ|はし"},{"japanese":"彼女は友達をかばって、先生に嘘をついた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa tomodachi o kabatte, sensei ni uso o tsuita.","german":"She covered for her friend and lied to the teacher.","furigana":"かのじょ|ともだち|せんせい|うそ"}],"notes":"庇う (mostly かばう in kana) means to protect someone from attacks, criticism or punishment by standing in front of them, also in the negative sense of \"covering for\": 部下をかばう, 犯人をかばう. It is also used for body parts: 痛めた足をかばって歩く \"to walk favoring the injured leg\". Unlike 守る, which generally means \"to protect, to keep\" (約束を守る), かばう stresses partisan advocacy for someone.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"友人の過ちを諫める勇気が必要だ。","romaji":"Yūjin no ayamachi wo isameru yūki ga hitsuyō da.","german":"It takes courage to admonish a friend for his mistakes.","furigana":"ゆうじん|あやま|いさ|ゆうき|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"家臣は命がけで主君の無謀な計画を諫めた。","romaji":"Kashin wa inochigake de shukun no mubō na keikaku o isameta.","german":"The retainer, at the risk of his life, remonstrated with his lord about his reckless plan.","furigana":"かしん|いのち|しゅくん|むぼう|けいかく|いさ"},{"japanese":"社長の独断を諫められる役員は一人もいなかった。","romaji":"Shachō no dokudan o isamerareru yakuin wa hitori mo inakatta.","german":"Not a single executive was able to remonstrate with the president about his arbitrary decisions.","furigana":"しゃちょう|どくだん|いさ|やくいん|ひとり"}],"notes":"諫める means to advise against a mistake and admonish someone, above all a superior, traditionally the lord, today for example the boss or parents: 主君を諫める, 社長の独断を諫める. This is the difference from 諭す and たしなめる, which mostly go from top to bottom. The word is elevated; there is also the spelling 諌める and the noun 諫言 \"admonition to a superior\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"恩に報いるために精一杯努力する。","romaji":"On ni mukuiru tame ni seiippai doryoku suru.","german":"I will do my very best to repay the kindness I was shown.","furigana":"おん|むく|せいいっぱいどりょく"},{"japanese":"社員の努力に報いて、会社は特別賞与を出した。","romaji":"Shain no doryoku ni mukuite, kaisha wa tokubetsu shōyo o dashita.","german":"In recognition of the employees' efforts, the company paid a special bonus.","furigana":"しゃいん|どりょく|むく|かいしゃ|とくべつしょうよ|だ"},{"japanese":"最終回に一矢を報いたが、試合には負けた。","romaji":"Saishūkai ni isshi o mukuita ga, shiai ni wa maketa.","german":"We got one back in the final inning, but we still lost the game.","furigana":"さいしゅうかい|いっし|むく|しあい|ま"}],"notes":"報いる means to respond appropriately to kindness, expectations or effort that one has received. The recipient takes に: 恩に報いる, 期待に報いる, 努力に報いる. The passive form 報われる \"to pay off, to be rewarded\" is very common (努力が報われた). In the sense of \"to strike back\" it appears almost only in 一矢を報いる \"to land a counterblow\"; the noun 報い often means \"deserved punishment\" (当然の報い).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子供がすぐに先生に懐いた。","romaji":"Kodomo ga sugu ni sensei ni natsuita.","german":"The child quickly became attached to the teacher.","furigana":"こども|せんせい|なつ"},{"japanese":"うちの猫は人見知りで、なかなか客になつかない。","romaji":"Uchi no neko wa hitomishiri de, nakanaka kyaku ni natsukanai.","german":"Our cat is shy around strangers and doesn't warm up to guests easily.","furigana":"ねこ|ひとみし|きゃく"},{"japanese":"保護した犬が、やっと私に懐いてくれた。","romaji":"Hogo shita inu ga, yatto watashi ni natsuite kureta.","german":"The dog I rescued has finally taken to me.","furigana":"ほご|いぬ|わたし|なつ"}],"notes":"懐く (usually written なつく in kana) means that a child or animal gains trust and becomes affectionate: 〜に懐く, 犬が懐く. The subject is almost always the child or animal that turns to someone. The transitive counterpart is 懐ける \"to tame, to win over\"; related is 人懐っこい \"friendly, sociable\". Do not confuse it with 懐かしい \"nostalgic\", which uses the same kanji.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"贔屓の引き倒しにならないよう気をつける。","romaji":"Hiiki no hikitaoshi ni naranai yō ki wo tsukeru.","german":"I'll be careful that my support for him doesn't end up doing more harm than good.","furigana":"ひいき|ひ|たお|き"},{"japanese":"先生はあの生徒ばかりひいきしている。","romaji":"Sensei wa ano seito bakari hiiki shite iru.","german":"The teacher always favors that one student.","furigana":"せんせい|せいと"},{"japanese":"ひいきの寿司屋に友人を連れて行った。","romaji":"Hiiki no sushiya ni yūjin o tsurete itta.","german":"I took a friend to my favorite sushi restaurant.","furigana":"すしや|ゆうじん|つ|い"}],"notes":"贔屓 (often written ひいき in kana) means to favor or support someone. As a する-verb: 〜をひいきする; pejorative えこひいき \"unfair favoritism\". Positive uses: ひいきの店/チーム \"regular haunt, favorite team\", ご贔屓に \"thank you for your continued patronage\" (shopkeeper to customers), ひいき目に見ても \"even looking at it favorably\". 判官びいき is sympathy for the underdog; 贔屓の引き倒し means harming someone by supporting them too much.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"身内だけで静かに祝った。","romaji":"Miuchi dake de shizuka ni iwatta.","german":"We celebrated quietly, with family only.","furigana":"みうち|しず|いわ"},{"japanese":"身内に不幸があり、明日は休ませていただきます。","romaji":"Miuchi ni fukō ga ari, ashita wa yasumasete itadakimasu.","german":"There has been a death in my family, so I will take tomorrow off.","furigana":"みうち|ふこう|あした|やす"},{"japanese":"身内の不祥事を隠そうとした会社が批判された。","romaji":"Miuchi no fushōji o kakusō to shita kaisha ga hihan sareta.","german":"The company was criticized for trying to cover up a scandal among its own people.","furigana":"みうち|ふしょうじ|かく|かいしゃ|ひはん"}],"notes":"身内 are one's own relatives, and in a wider sense members of one's own company or group, \"our people\" as opposed to outsiders. Typical: 身内だけで (式を挙げる/葬儀を行う), 身内の恥, 身内びいき \"nepotism\". As a polite explanation, 身内に不幸がありまして \"there has been a death in the family\" is a set phrase. Unlike 親戚 \"relatives\", 身内 stresses belonging to the close circle.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"世間体を気にして本音を言えない。","romaji":"Sekentei wo ki ni shite honne wo ienai.","german":"I worry about appearances and can't say what I really think.","furigana":"せけんてい|き|ほんね|い"},{"japanese":"失業したことを、世間体が悪いと親に隠していた。","romaji":"Shitsugyō shita koto o, sekentei ga warui to oya ni kakushite ita.","german":"I hid from my parents that I had lost my job, because it looked bad.","furigana":"しつぎょう|せけんてい|わる|おや|かく"},{"japanese":"世間体より、自分の幸せを大切にしたい。","romaji":"Sekentei yori, jibun no shiawase o taisetsu ni shitai.","german":"I want to value my own happiness more than what people think.","furigana":"せけんてい|じぶん|しあわ|たいせつ"}],"notes":"世間体 is the impression one makes in the eyes of society (世間), that is, what neighbors, relatives or colleagues might think. Typical: 世間体を気にする, 世間体が悪い \"it looks bad\", 世間体を繕う. Unlike 評判 \"reputation\" or 体面 \"dignity, prestige\", it is about social conformity and fear of gossip, so the word often sounds critical.","levelNote":"Compound: 世間 (N3) + 体 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"体面を保つために無理をしている。","romaji":"Taimen wo tamotsu tame ni muri wo shite iru.","german":"He is overextending himself to keep up appearances.","furigana":"たいめん|たも|むり"},{"japanese":"国の体面にかかわる問題なので、政府は慎重に対応した。","romaji":"Kuni no taimen ni kakawaru mondai na node, seifu wa shinchō ni taiō shita.","german":"Because it concerned the honor of the nation, the government responded cautiously.","furigana":"くに|たいめん|もんだい|せいふ|しんちょう|たいおう"},{"japanese":"家の体面を重んじる祖父は、離婚に強く反対した。","romaji":"Ie no taimen o omonjiru sofu wa, rikon ni tsuyoku hantai shita.","german":"My grandfather, who values the family's honor, strongly opposed the divorce.","furigana":"いえ|たいめん|おも|そふ|りこん|つよ|はんたい"}],"notes":"体面 is the outward dignity and prestige that a person, family or institution wants to maintain in front of others: 体面を保つ/重んじる, 体面を傷つける, 体面にかかわる. It is more formal than メンツ \"face\" and stresses one's own dignity, whereas 世間体 refers to the opinion of other people. Do not confuse it with the identically read 対面 \"meeting, face-to-face encounter\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"面目を失って立場がなくなった。","romaji":"Menboku wo ushinatte tachiba ga nakunatta.","german":"He lost face and was left without standing.","furigana":"めんぼく|うしな|たちば"},{"japanese":"約束を守れず、本当に面目ない。","romaji":"Yakusoku o mamorezu, hontō ni menboku nai.","german":"I couldn't keep my promise; I am truly ashamed.","furigana":"やくそく|まも|ほんとう|めんぼく"},{"japanese":"彼が決勝点を挙げて、エースとしての面目を保った。","romaji":"Kare ga kesshōten o agete, ēsu to shite no menboku o tamotta.","german":"By scoring the winning goal, he upheld his honor as the team's ace.","furigana":"かれ|けっしょうてん|あ|めんぼく|たも"}],"notes":"面目 is \"face\" in the sense of honor and standing in front of others. Typical: 面目を失う/潰す \"to lose face / to embarrass someone\", 面目が立つ, 面目を保つ, 面目丸つぶれ. Very common is 面目ない \"I am ashamed, I am terribly sorry\" as an apology. The reading めんもく is also used, especially in 面目躍如 \"living up to one's reputation, in one's element\" and 面目一新.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"廉恥心のない行動は許されない。","romaji":"Renchishin no nai kōdō wa yurusarenai.","german":"Conduct without any sense of shame is unacceptable.","furigana":"れんちしん|こうどう|ゆる"},{"japanese":"政治家には、何よりも廉恥の心が求められる。","romaji":"Seijika ni wa, nani yori mo renchi no kokoro ga motomerareru.","german":"Above all else, politicians are required to have a sense of integrity.","furigana":"せいじか|なに|れんち|こころ|もと"},{"japanese":"破廉恥な行為で、議員は辞職に追い込まれた。","romaji":"Harenchi na kōi de, giin wa jishoku ni oikomareta.","german":"The lawmaker was forced to resign over his shameless behavior.","furigana":"はれんち|こうい|ぎいん|じしょく|お|こ"}],"notes":"廉恥 is the sense of decency and honor, that is, the ability to feel ashamed of wrongdoing (廉 \"honest, upright\" + 恥 \"shame\"). On its own it is very rare; it appears almost only in 廉恥心 and above all in its opposite 破廉恥 \"shameless, scandalous\" (破廉恥な行為). It is literary and morally weighty; in everyday speech one says 恥を知る \"to have a sense of shame\" or 恥知らず \"shameless\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"羞恥心から顔が真っ赤になった。","romaji":"Shūchishin kara kao ga makka ni natta.","german":"My face turned bright red from embarrassment.","furigana":"しゅうちしん|かお|ま|か"},{"japanese":"人前で歌うには、羞恥心を捨てる必要がある。","romaji":"Hitomae de utau ni wa, shūchishin o suteru hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"To sing in front of people, you have to throw away your self-consciousness.","furigana":"ひとまえ|うた|しゅうちしん|す|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"電車の中で化粧をする人は、羞恥心がないのかと批判されることがある。","romaji":"Densha no naka de keshō o suru hito wa, shūchishin ga nai no ka to hihan sareru koto ga aru.","german":"People who put on makeup on the train are sometimes criticized for having no sense of shame.","furigana":"でんしゃ|なか|けしょう|ひと|しゅうちしん|ひはん"}],"notes":"羞恥心 is a sense of shame in the sense of embarrassment and self-consciousness, for example when one feels exposed: 羞恥心がある/ない, 羞恥心を感じる, 羞恥心を捨てる. Unlike 廉恥心, the moral shame over wrongdoing, this is about awkwardness and shyness. In everyday speech one usually says 恥ずかしい; 羞恥心 is a factual, descriptive word.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"往生際が悪いと見苦しい。","romaji":"Ōjōgiwa ga warui to migurushii.","german":"It looks pathetic when someone can't accept the inevitable.","furigana":"おうじょうぎわ|わる|みぐる"},{"japanese":"証拠を突きつけられても認めないなんて、往生際が悪いね。","romaji":"Shōko o tsukitsukerarete mo mitomenai nante, ōjōgiwa ga warui ne.","german":"He won't admit it even when confronted with the evidence; he just can't accept defeat.","furigana":"しょうこ|つ|みと|おうじょうぎわ|わる"},{"japanese":"潔く負けを認めた彼の往生際の良さに、観客は拍手を送った。","romaji":"Isagiyoku make o mitometa kare no ōjōgiwa no yosa ni, kankyaku wa hakushu o okutta.","german":"The audience applauded him for admitting defeat gracefully and without hesitation.","furigana":"いさぎよ|ま|みと|かれ|おうじょうぎわ|よ|かんきゃく|はくしゅ|おく"}],"notes":"往生際 is literally \"the moment of dying\" (往生 \"rebirth in paradise; death\" + 際 \"moment\"). Today it is used almost only in 往生際が悪い \"unable to give up, refusing to admit defeat\", for example when someone makes excuses despite a clear loss. Less often in a positive sense: 往生際がいい \"to yield gracefully\". The expression is colloquial and reproachful, and is often directed at someone: 往生際が悪いぞ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"未練を断ち切って新しい生活を始める。","romaji":"Miren wo tachikitte atarashii seikatsu wo hajimeru.","german":"I'll cut off my lingering attachments and start a new life.","furigana":"みれん|た|き|あたら|せいかつ|はじ"},{"japanese":"別れた恋人にまだ未練がある。","romaji":"Wakareta koibito ni mada miren ga aru.","german":"I still haven't gotten over the lover I broke up with.","furigana":"わか|こいびと|みれん"},{"japanese":"この街に未練はないので、転勤も平気だ。","romaji":"Kono machi ni miren wa nai node, tenkin mo heiki da.","german":"I have no attachment to this town, so a transfer doesn't bother me.","furigana":"まち|みれん|てんきん|へいき"}],"notes":"未練 is the inability to let go: one still clings to a person, thing or possibility even though it is over. Typical: 〜に未練がある/ない, 未練が残る, 未練を断ち切る; as an adjective 未練がましい \"pitifully clinging\" and colloquially 未練たらたら. Especially common after breakups. 後悔 is regret over one's own actions, whereas 未練 is wistful clinging.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"将来への不安に懊悩する日々だ。","romaji":"Shōrai e no fuan ni ōnō suru hibi da.","german":"These are days of anguish over anxiety about the future.","furigana":"しょうらい|ふあん|おうのう|ひび"},{"japanese":"主人公の懊悩が丁寧に描かれた小説だ。","romaji":"Shujinkō no ōnō ga teinei ni egakareta shōsetsu da.","german":"It is a novel that carefully portrays the protagonist's anguish.","furigana":"しゅじんこう|おうのう|ていねい|えが|しょうせつ"},{"japanese":"長い懊悩の末に、彼は医師の道を捨てた。","romaji":"Nagai ōnō no sue ni, kare wa ishi no michi o suteta.","german":"After long inner torment, he gave up the path of medicine.","furigana":"なが|おうのう|すえ|かれ|いし|みち|す"}],"notes":"懊悩 is a strongly literary word for tormenting inner brooding and mental anguish. As an intransitive する-verb: 〜に懊悩する; as a noun: 懊悩の日々, 懊悩の末に. It is much stronger and more literary than 悩む or 苦悩 and is found mainly in novels, biographies and essays.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"逡巡することなく即座に決断した。","romaji":"Shunjun suru koto naku sokuza ni ketsudan shita.","german":"He decided immediately, without hesitation.","furigana":"しゅんじゅん|そくざ|けつだん"},{"japanese":"彼女はしばらく逡巡した末に、本当のことを話した。","romaji":"Kanojo wa shibaraku shunjun shita sue ni, hontō no koto o hanashita.","german":"After hesitating for a while, she told the truth.","furigana":"かのじょ|しゅんじゅん|すえ|ほんとう|はな"},{"japanese":"転職するかどうか、彼は一年近く逡巡していた。","romaji":"Tenshoku suru ka dō ka, kare wa ichinen chikaku shunjun shite ita.","german":"He wavered for nearly a year over whether to change jobs.","furigana":"てんしょく|かれ|いちねんちか|しゅんじゅん"}],"notes":"逡巡 is the literary word for \"hesitation, wavering\", an indecisive back and forth before acting. As an intransitive する-verb it typically appears in しばらく逡巡した末に \"after hesitating for a while\" and 逡巡することなく \"without hesitation\". In everyday speech one says ためらう or 迷う; 躊躇 is a similarly formal synonym, but 逡巡 stresses more the irresolute shrinking back. Common in novels, speeches and commentary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"躊躇せずに助けを求めるべきだ。","romaji":"Chūcho sezu ni tasuke wo motomeru beki da.","german":"You should ask for help without hesitating.","furigana":"ちゅうちょ|たす|もと"},{"japanese":"彼は一瞬の躊躇もなく川に飛び込んだ。","romaji":"Kare wa isshun no chūcho mo naku kawa ni tobikonda.","german":"Without a moment's hesitation, he jumped into the river.","furigana":"かれ|いっしゅん|ちゅうちょ|かわ|と|こ"},{"japanese":"値段を見て、買うのを躊躇した。","romaji":"Nedan o mite, kau no o chūcho shita.","german":"When I saw the price, I hesitated to buy it.","furigana":"ねだん|み|か|ちゅうちょ"}],"notes":"躊躇 is hesitation before an action because one is unsure or has misgivings. Typical: 〜するのを躊躇する, 躊躇なく/躊躇せずに \"without hesitating\", 一瞬の躊躇もなく. The same kanji also write the verb 躊躇う (ためらう) \"to hesitate\"; in conversation ためらう is more common, while ちゅうちょ sounds more formal. 迷う stresses wavering between options, 躊躇 stresses shrinking back from acting.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"猜疑心が強すぎると人間関係が壊れる。","romaji":"Saigishin ga tsuyosugiru to ningen kankei ga kowareru.","german":"If one is too suspicious, human relationships break down.","furigana":"さいぎしん|つよ|にんげんかんけい|こわ"},{"japanese":"猜疑心に駆られて、彼は妻の携帯をこっそり見た。","romaji":"Saigishin ni kararete, kare wa tsuma no keitai o kossori mita.","german":"Driven by suspicion, he secretly looked at his wife's phone.","furigana":"さいぎしん|か|かれ|つま|けいたい|み"},{"japanese":"独裁者は猜疑心から側近を次々と粛清した。","romaji":"Dokusaisha wa saigishin kara sokkin o tsugitsugi to shukusei shita.","german":"Out of suspicion, the dictator purged his close associates one after another.","furigana":"どくさいしゃ|さいぎしん|そっきん|つぎつぎ|しゅくせい"}],"notes":"猜疑心 is deep-seated distrust of people: one assumes bad intentions on their part. Typical: 猜疑心が強い, 猜疑心に駆られる \"to be driven by suspicion\", 猜疑心を抱く. 疑い is neutral doubt about a matter; 猜疑心 is a trait of character or a feeling toward others and often involves envy or jealousy. The word is literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"互いに猜疑の目で見合っている。","romaji":"Tagai ni saigi no me de miatte iru.","german":"They eye each other with suspicion.","furigana":"たが|さいぎ|め|みあ"},{"japanese":"彼は猜疑心が強く、親しい友人さえ信用しない。","romaji":"Kare wa saigishin ga tsuyoku, shitashii yūjin sae shin'yō shinai.","german":"He is very distrustful and doesn't trust even close friends.","furigana":"かれ|さいぎしん|つよ|した|ゆうじん|しんよう"},{"japanese":"王は猜疑に駆られ、忠実な家臣まで遠ざけた。","romaji":"Ō wa saigi ni karare, chūjitsu na kashin made tōzaketa.","german":"Driven by suspicion, the king even kept his loyal retainers at a distance.","furigana":"おう|さいぎ|か|ちゅうじつ|かしん|とお"}],"notes":"猜疑 is suspicious distrust: one attributes bad intentions to others without real grounds, often out of jealousy or envy. On its own it is rare, and it almost always occurs in fixed combinations such as 猜疑心 \"distrust\" (猜疑心が強い \"very distrustful\"), 猜疑の目で見る \"to view with suspicion\" or 猜疑に駆られる \"to be driven by suspicion\". More neutral are 疑い and 疑念 \"doubt\"; 猜疑 always has a negative, almost pathological nuance. Literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"憐憫の情を催す光景だった。","romaji":"Renbin no jō wo moyōsu kōkei datta.","german":"It was a scene that stirred pity.","furigana":"れんびん|じょう|もよお|こうけい"},{"japanese":"彼は憐憫の目で見られることを何よりも嫌った。","romaji":"Kare wa renbin no me de mirareru koto o nani yori mo kiratta.","german":"More than anything, he hated being looked at with pity.","furigana":"かれ|れんびん|め|み|なに|きら"},{"japanese":"自己憐憫に浸っていても、何も変わらない。","romaji":"Jiko renbin ni hitatte ite mo, nani mo kawaranai.","german":"Wallowing in self-pity won't change anything.","furigana":"じこれんびん|ひた|なに|か"}],"notes":"憐憫 is pity or compassion for someone who is doing badly, often from a position of superiority. Typical: 憐憫の情 (を催す/を抱く), 憐憫の目で見る, 自己憐憫 \"self-pity\". It is literary; in everyday speech one says 同情 or かわいそうに思う. The person being pitied often finds 憐憫 condescending, much like 哀れみ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"敵意のない態度で接することが大切だ。","romaji":"Tekii no nai taido de sessuru koto ga taisetsu da.","german":"It is important to approach people without hostility.","furigana":"てきい|たいど|せっ|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"彼はライバルに敵意をむき出しにした。","romaji":"Kare wa raibaru ni tekii o mukidashi ni shita.","german":"He openly showed his hostility toward his rival.","furigana":"かれ|てきい|だ"},{"japanese":"野良猫は敵意のこもった目でこちらをにらんだ。","romaji":"Noraneko wa tekii no komotta me de kochira o niranda.","german":"The stray cat glared at us with hostile eyes.","furigana":"|てきい|め"}],"notes":"敵意 is a hostile attitude toward someone. Typical: 敵意を抱く/持つ, 敵意をむき出しにする \"to be openly hostile\", 敵意のこもった目, 〜に敵意を向ける. The word is neutral and common; 悪意 means ill intent (even without an adversary), and 反感 is more of an aversion or resentment.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"組織の体質を根本から変える必要がある。","romaji":"Soshiki no taishitsu wo konpon kara kaeru hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"The culture of the organization needs to be changed from the ground up.","furigana":"そしき|たいしつ|こんぽん|か|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"私はお酒を飲めない体質です。","romaji":"Watashi wa osake o nomenai taishitsu desu.","german":"I am the type who can't drink alcohol.","furigana":"わたし|さけ|の|たいしつ"},{"japanese":"この薬は体質に合わず、湿疹が出た。","romaji":"Kono kusuri wa taishitsu ni awazu, shisshin ga deta.","german":"This medicine didn't agree with me, and I broke out in a rash.","furigana":"くすり|たいしつ|あ|しっしん|で"}],"notes":"体質 is first of all one's physical constitution: アレルギー体質, 太りやすい体質, 体質に合う/合わない \"to agree / not agree with one's body\", 体質改善. Figuratively it denotes the deeply rooted character of an organization or industry, usually critically: 古い体質, 隠蔽体質 \"culture of cover-ups\". Unlike 性格 \"personality\" or 体格 \"build\", it concerns innate or ingrained tendencies.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"気性の激しい馬を乗りこなす。","romaji":"Kishō no hageshii uma wo norikonasu.","german":"He rides a spirited horse with skill.","furigana":"きしょう|はげ|うま|の"},{"japanese":"祖母は気性のさっぱりした人で、誰からも好かれた。","romaji":"Sobo wa kishō no sappari shita hito de, dare kara mo sukareta.","german":"My grandmother had a frank, easygoing nature and was liked by everyone.","furigana":"そぼ|きしょう|ひと|だれ|す"},{"japanese":"彼は気性が荒く、すぐに怒鳴る。","romaji":"Kare wa kishō ga araku, sugu ni donaru.","german":"He has a rough temper and shouts right away.","furigana":"かれ|きしょう|あら|どな"}],"notes":"気性 is the innate temperament of a person or animal, above all how easily it gets excited: 気性が激しい/荒い \"fiery, hot-tempered\", 気性がさっぱりしている \"easygoing, straightforward\", 負けず嫌いの気性. Unlike 性格, the whole personality, 気性 stresses the basic emotional nature. 気質 (きしつ) is similar but sounds more objective.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"気骨のある人物として尊敬されている。","romaji":"Kikotsu no aru jinbutsu to shite sonkei sarete iru.","german":"He is respected as a man of backbone.","furigana":"きこつ|じんぶつ|そんけい"},{"japanese":"気骨のある記者は、圧力に屈せず真実を報道した。","romaji":"Kikotsu no aru kisha wa, atsuryoku ni kussezu shinjitsu o hōdō shita.","german":"The plucky reporter did not bow to pressure and reported the truth.","furigana":"きこつ|きしゃ|あつりょく|くっ|しんじつ|ほうどう"},{"japanese":"最近の若手には気骨が足りないと、部長は嘆いた。","romaji":"Saikin no wakate ni wa kikotsu ga tarinai to, buchō wa nageita.","german":"The department head lamented that young people these days lack backbone.","furigana":"さいきん|わかて|きこつ|た|ぶちょう|なげ"}],"notes":"気骨 (きこつ) is inner firmness: the courage to hold to one's convictions and not bow to the powerful or to pressure. Typical: 気骨のある人物/政治家, 気骨を示す. It is appreciative and somewhat elevated. Note: read きぼね, the same spelling means \"mental strain\", as in 気骨が折れる \"to be nerve-racking\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鷹揚な態度で部下の失敗を許した。","romaji":"Ōyō na taido de buka no shippai wo yurushita.","german":"With a magnanimous attitude, he forgave his subordinate's mistake.","furigana":"おうよう|たいど|ぶか|しっぱい|ゆる"},{"japanese":"社長は鷹揚に構えて、誰の意見にも耳を傾けた。","romaji":"Shachō wa ōyō ni kamaete, dare no iken ni mo mimi o katamuketa.","german":"The president stayed calm and listened to everyone's opinion.","furigana":"しゃちょう|おうよう|かま|だれ|いけん|みみ|かたむ"},{"japanese":"祖父は孫たちのいたずらにも鷹揚に笑っていた。","romaji":"Sofu wa magotachi no itazura ni mo ōyō ni waratte ita.","german":"Grandfather just laughed good-naturedly even at his grandchildren's pranks.","furigana":"そふ|まご|おうよう|わら"}],"notes":"鷹揚 describes a generous, composed attitude that does not get worked up over trifles; originally the proud, calm circling of a hawk (鷹). As a な-adjective: 鷹揚な態度/人柄, 鷹揚に構える \"to stay calm and unbothered\", 鷹揚にうなずく. It sounds elevated and is often used of older people or superiors. Similar are 寛大 \"magnanimous\" and おおらか \"easygoing, big-hearted\"; do not confuse it with the identically read 応用 \"application\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"厚顔無恥な振る舞いに呆れた。","romaji":"Kōgan muchi na furumai ni akireta.","german":"I was appalled by his shameless behavior.","furigana":"こうがんむち|ふ|ま|あき"},{"japanese":"彼は厚顔にも、借金を返さないまま再び金を借りに来た。","romaji":"Kare wa kōgan ni mo, shakkin o kaesanai mama futatabi kane o kari ni kita.","german":"He had the nerve to come to borrow money again without having repaid his debt.","furigana":"かれ|こうがん|しゃっきん|かえ|ふたた|かね|か|き"},{"japanese":"あれほどの失敗をしておいて、よくそんな厚顔な態度がとれるものだ。","romaji":"Are hodo no shippai o shite oite, yoku sonna kōgan na taido ga toreru mono da.","german":"After such a blunder, how can he carry himself with such brazenness?","furigana":"しっぱい|こうがん|たいど"}],"notes":"厚顔 literally means \"thick face\" and denotes brazenness: someone feels no shame even though they have every reason to. Most common in the four-character compound 厚顔無恥 \"shameless and brazen\"; also 厚顔にも \"to have the nerve to ...\" and 厚顔な態度. It is literary and sharply reproachful; in everyday speech one says 図々しい or 厚かましい.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"殊勝な心がけで仕事に取り組んでいる。","romaji":"Shushō na kokorogake de shigoto ni torikunde iru.","german":"He applies himself to his work with a commendable attitude.","furigana":"しゅしょう|こころ|しごと|と|く"},{"japanese":"息子が殊勝にも、自分から皿洗いを申し出た。","romaji":"Musuko ga shushō ni mo, jibun kara saraarai o mōshideta.","german":"My son, commendably, offered on his own to wash the dishes.","furigana":"むすこ|しゅしょう|じぶん|さらあら|もう|で"},{"japanese":"叱られた彼は殊勝な顔をしていたが、反省しているかは怪しい。","romaji":"Shikarareta kare wa shushō na kao o shite ita ga, hansei shite iru ka wa ayashii.","german":"Having been scolded, he put on a contrite face, but whether he truly regrets it is doubtful.","furigana":"しか|かれ|しゅしょう|かお|はんせい|あや"}],"notes":"殊勝 praises an attitude as admirable and well-behaved, especially in younger people or in someone from whom one wouldn't have expected it: 殊勝な心がけ, 殊勝にも \"commendably\". Because it easily praises from above, it is not used for superiors. Irony often resonates: 殊勝な顔をする \"to put on a contrite look\", which may not be genuine. More neutral are 感心な or 立派な.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"図太い神経の持ち主でないとこの仕事は務まらない。","romaji":"Zubutoi shinkei no mochinushi de nai to kono shigoto wa tsutomaranai.","german":"You can't do this job unless you have thick nerves.","furigana":"ずぶと|しんけい|も|ぬし|しごと|つと"},{"japanese":"あれだけ叱られても平気で遅刻するなんて、図太いやつだ。","romaji":"Are dake shikararete mo heiki de chikoku suru nante, zubutoi yatsu da.","german":"Even after being scolded that much, he calmly shows up late again. What a nerve.","furigana":"しか|へいき|ちこく|ずぶと"},{"japanese":"大地震の後でも、彼女は図太く店を開け続けた。","romaji":"Ōjishin no ato demo, kanojo wa zubutoku mise o aketsuzuketa.","german":"Even after the great earthquake, she brazenly kept her shop open.","furigana":"おおじしん|あと|かのじょ|ずぶと|みせ|あ|つづ"}],"notes":"図太い describes someone who cannot be thrown off balance by anything and does not shrink even from embarrassing situations, \"strong-nerved\" or \"brazen\" depending on context. Typical: 図太い神経, 神経が図太い, 図太く生きる. It sounds positive when it refers to resilience and negative when it refers to impudence; 図々しい, by contrast, is clearly negative, \"pushy, cheeky\". The opposite is 繊細 \"sensitive\" or 気が小さい \"timid\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"生真面目な性格が災いして融通が利かない。","romaji":"Kimajime na seikaku ga wazawai shite yūzū ga kikanai.","german":"His overly earnest nature works against him, and he is inflexible.","furigana":"きまじめ|せいかく|わざわ|ゆうずう|き"},{"japanese":"冗談を生真面目に受け取られて、困ってしまった。","romaji":"Jōdan o kimajime ni uketorarete, komatte shimatta.","german":"My joke was taken completely seriously, and I was at a loss.","furigana":"じょうだん|きまじめ|う|と|こま"},{"japanese":"生真面目な彼は、締め切りを一度も破ったことがない。","romaji":"Kimajime na kare wa, shimekiri o ichido mo yabutta koto ga nai.","german":"Being so conscientious, he has never missed a deadline.","furigana":"きまじめ|かれ|し|き|いちど|やぶ"}],"notes":"生真面目 means \"through and through serious and conscientious\"; 生 stresses \"unadulterated, pure\" here. It can be meant appreciatively (生真面目な人柄), but often carries the nuance \"too serious, humorless, inflexible\": 生真面目すぎる, 生真面目に受け取る \"to take something far too seriously\". 真面目 alone is neutral-positive \"diligent, serious\"; 生真面目 intensifies it to the point of excess.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"厭世的な考え方に陥ってはいけない。","romaji":"Enseiteki na kangaekata ni ochiitte wa ikenai.","german":"You must not fall into a pessimistic way of thinking.","furigana":"えんせいてき|かんが|かた|おちい"},{"japanese":"失恋してから、彼はすっかり厭世的になってしまった。","romaji":"Shitsuren shite kara, kare wa sukkari enseiteki ni natte shimatta.","german":"Since his heartbreak, he has become thoroughly world-weary.","furigana":"しつれん|かれ|えんせいてき"},{"japanese":"その詩人の作品には、厭世的な雰囲気が漂っている。","romaji":"Sono shijin no sakuhin ni wa, enseiteki na fun'iki ga tadayotte iru.","german":"A world-weary atmosphere hangs over that poet's works.","furigana":"しじん|さくひん|えんせいてき|ふんいき|ただよ"}],"notes":"厭世的 means \"world-weary, tired of life\": one is weary of the world and of living (厭 \"to loathe\" + 世 \"world\"). Typical: 厭世的な考え/気分/作家, 厭世的になる. It is stronger and more existential than 悲観的 \"pessimistic\" about an outcome, and sounds literary; related are 厭世観 \"pessimistic view of the world, Weltschmerz\" and 厭世主義.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"楽天的な性格のおかげで困難を乗り越えた。","romaji":"Rakutenteki na seikaku no okage de konnan wo norikoeta.","german":"Thanks to his optimistic nature, he overcame the difficulties.","furigana":"らくてんてき|せいかく|こんなん|の|こ"},{"japanese":"「まあ、何とかなるよ」と、彼はいつも楽天的だ。","romaji":"\"Maa, nan to ka naru yo\" to, kare wa itsumo rakutenteki da.","german":"\"Oh, it'll work out somehow,\" he says; he is always optimistic.","furigana":"なん|かれ|らくてんてき"},{"japanese":"物事を楽天的に考えすぎると、準備が甘くなる。","romaji":"Monogoto o rakutenteki ni kangaesugiru to, junbi ga amaku naru.","german":"If you think about things too optimistically, your preparation gets sloppy.","furigana":"ものごと|らくてんてき|かんが|じゅんび|あま"}],"notes":"楽天的 is a な-adjective that describes above all a temperament: someone who worries little and trusts that everything will work out (楽天的な性格, 楽天的に考える). The close synonym 楽観的 refers more to the assessment of a concrete situation (楽観的な見通し \"optimistic outlook\"). Such a person is a 楽天家; the opposite is 悲観的 \"pessimistic\". Depending on context, 楽天的 can also sound slightly critical in the sense of \"carefree\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"内向的な性格で人前に出るのが苦手だ。","romaji":"Naikōteki na seikaku de hitomae ni deru no ga nigate da.","german":"I'm introverted and bad at appearing in front of people.","furigana":"ないこうてき|せいかく|ひとまえ|で|にがて"},{"japanese":"息子は内向的で、休みの日は一人で本を読んでいることが多い。","romaji":"Musuko wa naikōteki de, yasumi no hi wa hitori de hon o yonde iru koto ga ōi.","german":"My son is introverted and often reads alone on his days off.","furigana":"むすこ|ないこうてき|やす|ひ|ひとり|ほん|よ|おお"},{"japanese":"内向的な人は、大勢の飲み会より少人数の集まりを好む傾向がある。","romaji":"Naikōteki na hito wa, ōzei no nomikai yori shōninzū no atsumari o konomu keikō ga aru.","german":"Introverted people tend to prefer small gatherings to big drinking parties.","furigana":"ないこうてき|ひと|おおぜい|の|かい|しょうにんずう|あつ|この|けいこう"}],"notes":"内向的 is a な-adjective for an inward-turned personality: 内向的な性格, 内向的な人. The opposite is 外向的 \"extroverted\". In everyday speech, for \"shy\" one tends to say 内気 or 人見知り; 内向的 sounds somewhat more objective, almost psychological, and is not necessarily negative. Figuratively it can also describe an \"inward-looking\" policy or attitude (内向的な政策).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"社交的な彼女はすぐに友達を作る。","romaji":"Shakōteki na kanojo wa sugu ni tomodachi wo tsukuru.","german":"She is sociable and makes friends quickly.","furigana":"しゃこうてき|かのじょ|ともだち|つく"},{"japanese":"面接では、社交的な性格をアピールした。","romaji":"Mensetsu de wa, shakōteki na seikaku o apīru shita.","german":"In the interview, I emphasized my outgoing personality.","furigana":"めんせつ|しゃこうてき|せいかく"},{"japanese":"兄は社交的だが、弟は人見知りだ。","romaji":"Ani wa shakōteki da ga, otōto wa hitomishiri da.","german":"The older brother is sociable, but the younger one is shy around strangers.","furigana":"あに|しゃこうてき|おとうと|ひとみし"}],"notes":"社交的 is a な-adjective for someone who likes being among people and makes contacts easily: 社交的な人, 社交的な性格. Close in meaning is 外向的 \"extroverted\"; the everyday opposite is often 人見知り \"shy around strangers\". Do not confuse it with 社交辞令, which means \"polite formula, empty compliment\". In job applications and self-descriptions, 社交的 is a common positive trait.","levelNote":"Compound: 社交 (N1) + 的 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"刹那的な快楽に溺れてはいけない。","romaji":"Setsunateki na kairaku ni oborete wa ikenai.","german":"You must not drown in fleeting pleasures.","furigana":"せつなてき|かいらく|おぼ"},{"japanese":"将来を考えず刹那的に生きる若者を描いた小説だ。","romaji":"Shōrai o kangaezu setsunateki ni ikiru wakamono o egaita shōsetsu da.","german":"It is a novel about a young man who lives for the moment without thinking about the future.","furigana":"しょうらい|かんが|せつなてき|い|わかもの|えが|しょうせつ"},{"japanese":"彼の刹那的な恋愛は、いつも長続きしない。","romaji":"Kare no setsunateki na ren'ai wa, itsumo nagatsuzuki shinai.","german":"His fleeting romances never last long.","furigana":"かれ|せつなてき|れんあい|ながつづ"}],"notes":"刹那 is a Buddhist term for \"the shortest instant\". 刹那的 describes an attitude aimed only at the moment with no thought for the future, or things that last only briefly: 刹那的な快楽 \"fleeting pleasures\", 刹那的に生きる \"to live for the moment\". The word is rather literary and usually has a critical or melancholy coloring. More neutral for \"transient\" are 一時的 or はかない.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"献身的な介護に頭が下がる。","romaji":"Kenshinteki na kaigo ni atama ga sagaru.","german":"I take my hat off to such devoted care.","furigana":"けんしんてき|かいご|あたま|さ"},{"japanese":"彼女は病気の夫を献身的に支え続けた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa byōki no otto o kenshinteki ni sasae tsuzuketa.","german":"She continued to support her sick husband devotedly.","furigana":"かのじょ|びょうき|おっと|けんしんてき|ささ|つづ"},{"japanese":"地域の子どもたちのために献身的に働くボランティアがいる。","romaji":"Chiiki no kodomotachi no tame ni kenshinteki ni hataraku borantia ga iru.","german":"There are volunteers who work selflessly for the children of the community.","furigana":"ちいき|こ|けんしんてき|はたら"}],"notes":"献身的 (\"self-giving\", from 献身 \"devotion, self-sacrifice\") describes selfless commitment to other people or a cause. Typically adverbial: 献身的に支える, 献身的に尽くす, 献身的に看病する; attributive 献身的な介護, 献身的な努力. Unlike 一生懸命, which only expresses great effort, 献身的 stresses sacrifice for the benefit of others. The tone is appreciative and rather elevated.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"利己的な行動は周囲の信頼を失う。","romaji":"Rikoteki na kōdō wa shūi no shinrai wo ushinau.","german":"Selfish behavior loses the trust of those around you.","furigana":"りこてき|こうどう|しゅうい|しんらい|うしな"},{"japanese":"自分の利益しか考えない利己的な態度に、皆があきれた。","romaji":"Jibun no rieki shika kangaenai rikoteki na taido ni, mina ga akireta.","german":"Everyone was appalled at his selfish attitude of thinking only of his own profit.","furigana":"じぶん|りえき|かんが|りこてき|たいど|みな"},{"japanese":"『利己的な遺伝子』は世界的なベストセラーになった。","romaji":"\"Rikoteki na Idenshi\" wa sekaiteki na besutoserā ni natta.","german":"\"The Selfish Gene\" became a worldwide bestseller.","furigana":"りこてき|いでんし|せかいてき"}],"notes":"利己的 (\"intent on one's own benefit\") is the objective, literary word for egoistic behavior: 利己的な考え, 利己的な行動, 利己主義 \"egoism\". Colloquially one rather says わがまま (willful, capricious) or 自己中 (short for 自己中心的 \"self-centered\"). The opposite is 利他的 \"altruistic\". Well known is the book title 利己的な遺伝子 (\"The Selfish Gene\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"利他的な精神でボランティア活動に参加する。","romaji":"Ritateki na seishin de borantia katsudō ni sanka suru.","german":"I take part in volunteer activities in an altruistic spirit.","furigana":"りたてき|せいしん|かつどう|さんか"},{"japanese":"動物にも利他的な行動が見られることがある。","romaji":"Dōbutsu ni mo ritateki na kōdō ga mirareru koto ga aru.","german":"Altruistic behavior can sometimes be seen in animals too.","furigana":"どうぶつ|りたてき|こうどう|み"},{"japanese":"自分の得より人のためを考える利他的な経営者として知られている。","romaji":"Jibun no toku yori hito no tame o kangaeru ritateki na keieisha to shite shirarete iru.","german":"He is known as an altruistic business owner who thinks of others more than his own gain.","furigana":"じぶん|とく|ひと|かんが|りたてき|けいえいしゃ|し"}],"notes":"利他的 (\"benefiting others\") is the counterpart of 利己的 and means \"altruistic, selfless\": 利他的な行動, 利他的な精神, 利他主義 \"altruism\". It is a rather literary word from ethics, biology and economic philosophy. In everyday speech, selfless people are rather described with 思いやりがある or 人のために尽くす.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"打算的な付き合い方は長続きしない。","romaji":"Dasanteki na tsukiaikata wa nagatsuzuki shinai.","german":"A calculating way of dealing with people doesn't last.","furigana":"ださんてき|つ|あ|かた|ながつづ"},{"japanese":"彼女が彼と結婚したのは打算的な理由からだと噂されている。","romaji":"Kanojo ga kare to kekkon shita no wa dasanteki na riyū kara da to uwasa sarete iru.","german":"It is rumored that she married him for calculated reasons.","furigana":"かのじょ|かれ|けっこん|ださんてき|りゆう|うわさ"},{"japanese":"打算的に人に近づく人は、すぐに見抜かれる。","romaji":"Dasanteki ni hito ni chikazuku hito wa, sugu ni minukareru.","german":"People who approach others out of calculation are quickly seen through.","furigana":"ださんてき|ひと|ちか|ひと|みぬ"}],"notes":"打算 is weighing advantages and disadvantages for oneself; 打算的 therefore means \"calculating\" and is clearly pejorative: 打算的な人, 打算的な結婚, 打算的に動く. Colloquially 計算高い means the same. The base word also appears in expressions like 打算が働く \"calculation is at work\" or 打算抜きで \"without ulterior motives\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"潔癖症の彼は外食を嫌がる。","romaji":"Keppekishō no kare wa gaishoku wo iyagaru.","german":"He has a cleanliness obsession and avoids eating out.","furigana":"けっぺきしょう|かれ|がいしょく|いや"},{"japanese":"彼は金銭に関して潔癖で、一円の誤差も許さない。","romaji":"Kare wa kinsen ni kanshite keppeki de, ichi-en no gosa mo yurusanai.","german":"He is scrupulous about money and doesn't tolerate even a one-yen discrepancy.","furigana":"かれ|きんせん|かん|けっぺき|いちえん|ごさ|ゆる"},{"japanese":"潔癖な母は、私たちが帰宅するとすぐに手を洗わせる。","romaji":"Keppeki na haha wa, watashitachi ga kitaku suru to sugu ni te o arawaseru.","german":"Our germophobic mother makes us wash our hands as soon as we get home.","furigana":"けっぺき|はは|わたし|きたく|て|あら"}],"notes":"潔癖 is a noun and な-adjective with two meanings: excessive cleanliness (潔癖な人, very commonly 潔癖症 \"obsessive cleanliness\", colloquially also used for \"cleaning mania\") and moral integrity that tolerates no dishonesty (金銭に潔癖だ). In the second sense it is praising, in the first often slightly mocking. Close in meaning to the second sense are 清廉 and 生真面目.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"吝嗇な人は友人を失いやすい。","romaji":"Rinshoku na hito wa yūjin wo ushinaiyasui.","german":"Stingy people easily lose friends.","furigana":"りんしょく|ひと|ゆうじん|うしな"},{"japanese":"祖父は吝嗇家として有名で、紙一枚も無駄にしなかった。","romaji":"Sofu wa rinshokuka to shite yūmei de, kami ichimai mo muda ni shinakatta.","german":"My grandfather was famous as a miser and never wasted even a sheet of paper.","furigana":"そふ|りんしょくか|ゆうめい|かみいちまい|むだ"},{"japanese":"金持ちほど吝嗇だと言われることがある。","romaji":"Kanemochi hodo rinshoku da to iwareru koto ga aru.","german":"It is sometimes said that the richer people are, the stingier they are.","furigana":"かねも|りんしょく|い"}],"notes":"吝嗇 is a very literary word for stinginess, as a noun and な-adjective (吝嗇な人); the miser is called 吝嗇家. In everyday speech one almost always says けち (けちな人, けちる) instead. 吝嗇 is found mainly in literature, essays and character descriptions, and sounds detached and judgmental compared with けち. The kanji are rare and often given furigana.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"些細なことで悶着を起こす人がいる。","romaji":"Sasai na koto de monchaku wo okosu hito ga iru.","german":"There are people who start trouble over trivial matters.","furigana":"ささい|もんちゃく|お|ひと"},{"japanese":"遺産の分け方をめぐって、兄弟の間で一悶着あった。","romaji":"Isan no wakekata o megutte, kyōdai no aida de hitomonchaku atta.","german":"There was a row among the siblings over how to divide the inheritance.","furigana":"いさん|わ|かた|きょうだい|あいだ|ひともんちゃく"},{"japanese":"会議の席順のことで、ちょっとした悶着が起きた。","romaji":"Kaigi no sekijun no koto de, chotto shita monchaku ga okita.","german":"A minor squabble arose over the seating order at the meeting.","furigana":"かいぎ|せきじゅん|もんちゃく|お"}],"notes":"悶着 is a quarrel or hassle, usually over rather small but unpleasant matters: 悶着を起こす \"to cause trouble\", 悶着が起きる. Very common is the form 一悶着 (ひともんちゃく): 一悶着ある/起きる \"there is a to-do, a row\". Close in meaning are もめごと and the colloquial トラブル; 喧嘩 is more direct and can also be physical.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"部署間の軋轢が業務に支障をきたしている。","romaji":"Bushokan no atsureki ga gyōmu ni shishō wo kitashite iru.","german":"Friction between departments is hampering operations.","furigana":"ぶしょかん|あつれき|ぎょうむ|ししょう"},{"japanese":"新しい制度の導入は、ベテラン社員との間に軋轢を生んだ。","romaji":"Atarashii seido no dōnyū wa, beteran shain to no aida ni atsureki o unda.","german":"The introduction of the new system created friction with the veteran employees.","furigana":"あたら|せいど|どうにゅう|しゃいん|あいだ|あつれき|う"},{"japanese":"嫁と姑の軋轢に悩む家庭は少なくない。","romaji":"Yome to shūtome no atsureki ni nayamu katei wa sukunaku nai.","german":"Quite a few households suffer from friction between daughter-in-law and mother-in-law.","furigana":"よめ|しゅうとめ|あつれき|なや|かてい|すく"}],"notes":"軋轢 (literally the \"grinding\" of wheels, cf. 軋む) denotes friction and tension between people or groups: 軋轢が生じる, 軋轢を生む, 軋轢を避ける, 〜との軋轢. It is a literary word from news and specialist texts. 摩擦 can be used similarly (貿易摩擦), but is more general and also physical; 対立 is open opposition, whereas 軋轢 is more smoldering discord.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の自慢話に辟易している。","romaji":"Kare no jimanbanashi ni hekieki shite iru.","german":"I am fed up with his bragging.","furigana":"かれ|じまんばなし|へきえき"},{"japanese":"毎日同じ説教を聞かされて、さすがに辟易した。","romaji":"Mainichi onaji sekkyō o kikasarete, sasuga ni hekieki shita.","german":"Having to listen to the same lecture every day, I really got sick of it.","furigana":"まいにちおな|せっきょう|き|へきえき"},{"japanese":"夏の蒸し暑さには毎年辟易する。","romaji":"Natsu no mushiatsusa ni wa maitoshi hekieki suru.","german":"I'm worn down by the muggy summer heat every year.","furigana":"なつ|む|あつ|まいとしへきえき"}],"notes":"辟易 is almost always used as a する-verb: 〜に辟易する \"to be sick of something, to be unable to bear something any longer\", often in the state 辟易している. Typical triggers are long speeches, bragging, heat or smells. It is more literary and stronger than うんざりする, which is more usual in conversation. The older sense \"to recoil, to shrink back\" is rare today.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"試験に落ちて落胆している。","romaji":"Shiken ni ochite rakutan shite iru.","german":"He is dejected because he failed the exam.","furigana":"しけん|お|らくたん"},{"japanese":"結果を聞いた選手たちは、落胆の色を隠せなかった。","romaji":"Kekka o kiita senshutachi wa, rakutan no iro o kakusenakatta.","german":"The players couldn't hide their disappointment when they heard the result.","furigana":"けっか|き|せんしゅ|らくたん|いろ|かく"},{"japanese":"一度の失敗でそんなに落胆することはないよ。","romaji":"Ichido no shippai de sonna ni rakutan suru koto wa nai yo.","german":"There's no need to be so discouraged over a single failure.","furigana":"いちど|しっぱい|らくたん"}],"notes":"落胆 (literally \"the gallbladder falls\") is deep disappointment and dejection after a failure; as an intransitive する-verb: 落胆する, 結果に落胆する. Set phrase: 落胆の色を隠せない \"cannot hide one's disappointment\". In conversation one usually says がっかりする; 落胆 is more literary and sounds heavier. 失望 stresses more that expectations of someone were disappointed.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"失意のどん底から立ち直った。","romaji":"Shitsui no donzoko kara tachinaotta.","german":"He recovered from the depths of despondency.","furigana":"しつい|ぞこ|た|なお"},{"japanese":"彼は事業に失敗し、失意のうちに故郷へ帰った。","romaji":"Kare wa jigyō ni shippai shi, shitsui no uchi ni kokyō e kaetta.","german":"After his business failed, he returned to his hometown in despair.","furigana":"かれ|じぎょう|しっぱい|しつい|こきょう|かえ"},{"japanese":"失意の日々を支えてくれたのは、友人たちの励ましだった。","romaji":"Shitsui no hibi o sasaete kureta no wa, yūjintachi no hagemashi datta.","german":"What supported me through those dark days was my friends' encouragement.","furigana":"しつい|ひび|ささ|ゆうじん|はげ"}],"notes":"失意 is the state of deep disappointment when hopes have failed; it is a literary noun, not a する-verb. Typical phrases: 失意のどん底 \"the depths of despair\", 失意のうちに \"in deep disappointment\", 失意の日々. The opposite concept is 得意 in the sense of \"success, elation\" (得意の絶頂). Compared with 落胆, 失意 describes more a longer-lasting phase of life.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"職人の技に感嘆の声を上げた。","romaji":"Shokunin no waza ni kantan no koe wo ageta.","german":"I let out a cry of admiration at the craftsman's skill.","furigana":"しょくにん|わざ|かんたん|こえ|あ"},{"japanese":"見事な夕日に、観光客たちは思わず感嘆した。","romaji":"Migoto na yūhi ni, kankōkyakutachi wa omowazu kantan shita.","german":"The tourists involuntarily marveled at the magnificent sunset.","furigana":"みごと|ゆうひ|かんこうきゃく|おも|かんたん"},{"japanese":"文末に感嘆符を多用すると、文章が幼く見える。","romaji":"Bunmatsu ni kantanfu o tayō suru to, bunshō ga osanaku mieru.","german":"If you use too many exclamation marks at the end of sentences, your writing looks childish.","furigana":"ぶんまつ|かんたんふ|たよう|ぶんしょう|おさな|み"}],"notes":"感嘆 is astonished admiration that is often voiced aloud: 感嘆の声を上げる/漏らす, 〜に感嘆する. It is more elevated than 感心する (to be impressed, to appreciate) and more spontaneous than 感動 (inner emotion). Grammatically important is 感嘆符 \"exclamation mark\"; 感嘆文 is an exclamatory sentence.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"名残惜しいがここでお別れだ。","romaji":"Nagorioshii ga koko de owakare da.","german":"I'm reluctant to part, but this is where we say goodbye.","furigana":"なごりお|わか"},{"japanese":"この町には、江戸時代の名残が今も見られる。","romaji":"Kono machi ni wa, Edo jidai no nagori ga ima mo mirareru.","german":"In this town, traces of the Edo period can still be seen today.","furigana":"まち|えどじだい|なごり|いま|み"},{"japanese":"卒業式の後も、生徒たちは名残を惜しんでなかなか帰らなかった。","romaji":"Sotsugyōshiki no ato mo, seitotachi wa nagori o oshinde nakanaka kaeranakatta.","german":"Even after the graduation ceremony, the students were reluctant to part and didn't go home for a long time.","furigana":"そつぎょうしき|あと|せいと|なごり|お|かえ"}],"notes":"名残 has two main meanings: traces or remnants of something past (昔の名残, 江戸時代の名残, 夏の名残) and the wistful feeling at parting (名残を惜しむ \"to be reluctant to part\"). The adjective 名残惜しい \"hard to part\" is very common. For purely material remains one rather says 跡 or 痕跡; 名残 always has a nostalgic undertone.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の思想に心酔している若者が多い。","romaji":"Kare no shisō ni shinsui shite iru wakamono ga ōi.","german":"Many young people are devoted to his ideas.","furigana":"かれ|しそう|しんすい|わかもの|おお"},{"japanese":"若い頃、私はある哲学者に心酔していた。","romaji":"Wakai koro, watashi wa aru tetsugakusha ni shinsui shite ita.","german":"In my youth, I was completely enthralled by a certain philosopher.","furigana":"わか|ころ|わたし|てつがくしゃ|しんすい"},{"japanese":"彼は師匠に心酔するあまり、話し方まで似てきた。","romaji":"Kare wa shishō ni shinsui suru amari, hanashikata made nite kita.","german":"He admires his master so much that he has even come to talk like him.","furigana":"かれ|ししょう|しんすい|はな|かた|に"}],"notes":"心酔 means being completely captivated by a person or their ideas: 〜に心酔する, often as a state 心酔している. Typical objects are teachers, thinkers, artists or ideologies. 夢中になる is more everyday and also used for hobbies; 傾倒 stresses more intellectual devotion, 崇拝 worship. 心酔 can point slightly critically to a lack of distance.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"使わない荷物を納戸に入れておく。","romaji":"Tsukawanai nimotsu wo nando ni irete oku.","german":"I keep luggage I don't use in the storeroom.","furigana":"つか|にもつ|なんど|い"},{"japanese":"祖母の家の納戸から、古いアルバムが出てきた。","romaji":"Sobo no ie no nando kara, furui arubamu ga dete kita.","german":"Old photo albums turned up from the storeroom at my grandmother's house.","furigana":"そぼ|いえ|なんど|ふる|で"},{"japanese":"この部屋は窓がないので、間取り図では納戸と表示されている。","romaji":"Kono heya wa mado ga nai node, madorizu de wa nando to hyōji sarete iru.","german":"This room has no window, so it is labeled as a storeroom on the floor plan.","furigana":"へや|まど|まど|ず|なんど|ひょうじ"}],"notes":"納戸 is a windowless storage room in a Japanese house, traditionally for clothing, household goods and dishes. On present-day floor plans, 納戸 (or \"S\" for サービスルーム) stands for a room that does not count as living space because it lacks windows. To be distinguished from 押し入れ (a wall closet for futons) and 物置 (a shed, storage place). As a color name (納戸色) it denotes a muted gray-blue.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"布巾を清潔に保つことが衛生管理の基本だ。","romaji":"Fukin wo seiketsu ni tamotsu koto ga eisei kanri no kihon da.","german":"Keeping dishcloths clean is the basis of hygiene management.","furigana":"ふきん|せいけつ|たも|えいせいかんり|きほん"},{"japanese":"洗った皿を布巾で拭いて、棚に戻した。","romaji":"Aratta sara o fukin de fuite, tana ni modoshita.","german":"I dried the washed dishes with a dishcloth and put them back on the shelf.","furigana":"あら|さら|ふきん|ふ|たな|もど"},{"japanese":"台布巾でテーブルを拭いてくれる？","romaji":"Daifukin de tēburu o fuite kureru?","german":"Could you wipe the table with the table cloth?","furigana":"だいふきん|ふ"}],"notes":"布巾 is a clean cloth for the kitchen and table: for drying dishes or wiping the table (台布巾). Typical: 布巾で拭く, 布巾を絞る, 布巾を煮沸する. For dirty jobs such as floors, one uses 雑巾 instead; mixing the two up would be a real faux pas in Japan. タオル is a towel for the body.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"雑巾がけをして床を磨く。","romaji":"Zōkingake wo shite yuka wo migaku.","german":"I wipe and polish the floor with a rag.","furigana":"ぞうきん|ゆか|みが"},{"japanese":"雑巾をよく絞ってから窓を拭いてください。","romaji":"Zōkin o yoku shibotte kara mado o fuite kudasai.","german":"Please wring out the rag well before wiping the windows.","furigana":"ぞうきん|しぼ|まど|ふ"},{"japanese":"小学校では、新学期に雑巾を一枚持っていく習慣がある。","romaji":"Shōgakkō de wa, shingakki ni zōkin o ichimai motte iku shūkan ga aru.","german":"In elementary school, it is customary to bring one rag at the start of the new term.","furigana":"しょうがっこう|しんがっき|ぞうきん|いちまいも|しゅうかん"}],"notes":"雑巾 is the cleaning rag for floors, windows and furniture, often sewn from old towels. Typical: 雑巾で拭く, 雑巾を絞る, 雑巾がけ \"wiping the floor on your knees with a rag\" - in Japanese schools a daily cleaning task for the pupils. For dishes and dining tables one never uses a 雑巾, but a 布巾.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"箒と塵取りで玄関を掃く。","romaji":"Hōki to chiritori de genkan wo haku.","german":"I sweep the entrance with a broom and dustpan.","furigana":"ほうき|ちりと|げんかん|は"},{"japanese":"ちりとりに集めたごみを、ごみ袋に入れる。","romaji":"Chiritori ni atsumeta gomi o, gomibukuro ni ireru.","german":"I put the trash collected in the dustpan into the garbage bag.","furigana":"あつ|ぶくろ|い"},{"japanese":"割れたガラスは、ほうきで塵取りに集めて捨てた。","romaji":"Wareta garasu wa, hōki de chiritori ni atsumete suteta.","german":"I swept the broken glass into the dustpan with the broom and threw it away.","furigana":"わ|ちりと|あつ|す"}],"notes":"塵取り (\"dust-taker\") is the dustpan; in everyday life it is almost always written in kana: ちりとり. It usually appears together with the broom: ほうきとちりとり. Typical: ちりとりに集める, ちりとりで取る. 塵 means \"dust\" and also appears in 塵も積もれば山となる \"many a little makes a mickle\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"竹箒で落ち葉を掃く。","romaji":"Takebōki de ochiba wo haku.","german":"I sweep fallen leaves with a bamboo broom.","furigana":"|お|ば|は"},{"japanese":"魔女がほうきに乗って空を飛ぶ。","romaji":"Majo ga hōki ni notte sora o tobu.","german":"A witch flies through the sky on a broom.","furigana":"まじょ|の|そら|と"},{"japanese":"教室の床をほうきで掃いてから、机を並べた。","romaji":"Kyōshitsu no yuka o hōki de haite kara, tsukue o narabeta.","german":"We swept the classroom floor with a broom and then arranged the desks.","furigana":"きょうしつ|ゆか|は|つくえ|なら"}],"notes":"箒 is the broom; the kanji is rare, and in everyday writing it is almost always written ほうき. The related verb is 掃く \"to sweep\": ほうきで掃く. In compounds the initial sound is voiced: 竹箒 (たけぼうき) \"bamboo broom\". A vacuum cleaner is called 掃除機; ほうき and ちりとり are often mentioned as a pair. A well-known image: the witch flying on a broom.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"盥に水を張って洗濯する。","romaji":"Tarai ni mizu wo hatte sentaku suru.","german":"I fill a washtub with water and do the laundry.","furigana":"たらい|みず|は|せんたく"},{"japanese":"電話で問い合わせたら、部署から部署へたらい回しにされた。","romaji":"Denwa de toiawasetara, busho kara busho e taraimawashi ni sareta.","german":"When I called to inquire, I got passed from one department to another.","furigana":"でんわ|と|あ|ぶしょ|ぶしょ|まわ"},{"japanese":"夏の庭で、子どもたちがたらいの水で遊んでいる。","romaji":"Natsu no niwa de, kodomotachi ga tarai no mizu de asonde iru.","german":"In the summer garden, the children are playing in the water in a tub.","furigana":"なつ|にわ|こ|みず|あそ"}],"notes":"盥 is a shallow tub or basin, formerly used for washing clothes and bathing small children; today it is usually written たらい. Typical: たらいに水を張る. The idiom たらい回し（にする/される） is important: passing a person or a matter from office to office without anyone feeling responsible, for example at government offices or hospitals.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"設備の老朽化により、部品の一部に欠損が生じた。","romaji":"Setsubi no rōkyūka ni yori, buhin no ichibu ni kesson ga shōjita.","german":"Due to the aging of the equipment, some parts have been damaged.","furigana":"せつび|ろうきゅうか|ぶひん|いちぶ|けっそん|しょう"},{"japanese":"今期は三千万円の欠損を出した。","romaji":"Konki wa sanzen man'en no kesson o dashita.","german":"This term, we posted a loss of 30 million yen.","furigana":"こんき|さんぜんまんえん|けっそん|だ"},{"japanese":"遺伝子の一部が欠損していると、この病気になりやすい。","romaji":"Idenshi no ichibu ga kesson shite iru to, kono byōki ni nariyasui.","german":"If part of the gene is missing, one is more prone to this disease.","furigana":"いでんし|いちぶ|けっそん|びょうき"}],"notes":"欠損 covers two areas: in accounting, a deficit or loss (欠損を出す, 欠損金, 欠損を補う), and otherwise the partial absence or damage of something, e.g. 歯の欠損, 遺伝子の欠損, 部品の欠損. As an intransitive する-verb: 一部が欠損している. It is a technical word; in general business one usually says 赤字 or 損失 for losses.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"休日は専ら読書に費やしている。","romaji":"Kyūjitsu wa moppara dokusho ni tsuiyashite iru.","german":"I spend my days off almost entirely reading.","furigana":"きゅうじつ|もっぱ|どくしょ|つい"},{"japanese":"最近は自炊をやめて、もっぱらコンビニ弁当だ。","romaji":"Saikin wa jisui o yamete, moppara konbini bentō da.","german":"Lately I've stopped cooking for myself and it's convenience-store bento all the time.","furigana":"さいきん|じすい|べんとう"},{"japanese":"彼が次の社長になるというのが、もっぱらの噂だ。","romaji":"Kare ga tsugi no shachō ni naru to iu no ga, moppara no uwasa da.","german":"The rumor going around is that he will be the next president.","furigana":"かれ|つぎ|しゃちょう|うわさ"}],"notes":"専ら is mostly written in kana: もっぱら. It means that one does almost exclusively or predominantly one thing: 休日はもっぱら読書, 最近はもっぱら電車通勤だ. Compared with 主に \"mainly\", もっぱら sounds stronger and somewhat elevated. Set phrase: もっぱらの噂 \"what everyone is talking about\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"成功は偏に皆様のおかげです。","romaji":"Seikō wa hitoe ni minasama no okage desu.","german":"The success is entirely thanks to all of you.","furigana":"せいこう|ひとえ|みなさま"},{"japanese":"今回の事故は、ひとえに私どもの確認不足によるものです。","romaji":"Konkai no jiko wa, hitoe ni watakushidomo no kakunin busoku ni yoru mono desu.","german":"This accident was due solely to our lack of checking.","furigana":"こんかい|じこ|わたくし|かくにんぶそく"},{"japanese":"今後ともご支援のほど、ひとえにお願い申し上げます。","romaji":"Kongo tomo goshien no hodo, hitoe ni onegai mōshiagemasu.","german":"We sincerely ask for your continued support.","furigana":"こんご|しえん|ねが|もう|あ"}],"notes":"偏に (usually ひとえに) is an elevated adverb meaning \"solely, wholly\". Typical in thank-you speeches and apologies: ひとえに皆様のおかげです, ひとえに私の不徳の致すところです. In formal requests it strengthens sincerity: ひとえにお願い申し上げます. In everyday speech one says simply ただ or 全く. Do not confuse it with 一重 \"single, single-layered\", which is read the same.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"殊更問題を大きくする必要はない。","romaji":"Kotosara mondai wo ōkiku suru hitsuyō wa nai.","german":"There is no need to deliberately make the problem bigger.","furigana":"ことさらもんだい|おお|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"今年の冬はことさら寒さが厳しい。","romaji":"Kotoshi no fuyu wa kotosara samusa ga kibishii.","german":"This winter the cold is especially severe.","furigana":"ことし|ふゆ|さむ|きび"},{"japanese":"彼は自分の功績をことさらに強調した。","romaji":"Kare wa jibun no kōseki o kotosara ni kyōchō shita.","german":"He made a point of emphasizing his own achievements.","furigana":"かれ|じぶん|こうせき|きょうちょう"}],"notes":"殊更 (usually ことさら, also ことさらに) has two meanings: \"deliberately, ostentatiously\", often critical when something is artificially emphasized (ことさら強調する, ことさら騒ぎ立てる), and \"especially, particularly\" (今年の夏はことさら暑い). In the first sense, わざと is the everyday word, in the second 特に; ことさら sounds more elevated in each case.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"矢張り直接会って話すべきだった。","romaji":"Yahari chokusetsu atte hanasu beki datta.","german":"I should have met in person and talked after all.","furigana":"やは|ちょくせつあ|はな"},{"japanese":"天気予報どおり、やはり午後から雨が降り出した。","romaji":"Tenki yohō dōri, yahari gogo kara ame ga furidashita.","german":"Just as the forecast said, it did start raining in the afternoon.","furigana":"てんきよほう|ごご|あめ|ふ|だ"},{"japanese":"色々な店で食べたけど、やっぱり母の料理が一番だ。","romaji":"Iroiro na mise de tabeta kedo, yappari haha no ryōri ga ichiban da.","german":"I've eaten at many restaurants, but my mother's cooking is still the best.","furigana":"いろいろ|みせ|た|はは|りょうり|いちばん"}],"notes":"矢張り is practically never written with kanji, but as やはり, colloquially やっぱり (very casually やっぱ). It expresses that an expectation is confirmed (\"as expected\"), that after reflection one comes to a conclusion (\"after all, in the end\"), or that something stays the same (\"as before, likewise\"). Typical: やはりそうだったか, やっぱりやめた \"I'll skip it after all\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4 (source: N4; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"漸く長い冬が終わりを告げた。","romaji":"Yōyaku nagai fuyu ga owari wo tsugeta.","german":"The long winter has finally come to an end.","furigana":"ようや|なが|ふゆ|お|つ"},{"japanese":"三回目の挑戦で、ようやく試験に合格した。","romaji":"Sankaime no chōsen de, yōyaku shiken ni gōkaku shita.","german":"I finally passed the exam on my third attempt.","furigana":"さんかいめ|ちょうせん|しけん|ごうかく"},{"japanese":"薬が効いてきて、熱もようやく下がり始めた。","romaji":"Kusuri ga kiite kite, netsu mo yōyaku sagari hajimeta.","german":"The medicine is taking effect, and the fever has finally started to come down.","furigana":"くすり|き|ねつ|さ|はじ"}],"notes":"漸く (usually ようやく) means \"finally\" after a long wait or effort, and can also mean \"gradually\" or \"barely\". やっと is more emotional and colloquial and stresses relief; ようやく sounds more factual and fits well in news and reports. ついに stresses the culmination or final result, even with negative things.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"品質が良く、しかも値段が手頃だ。","romaji":"Hinshitsu ga yoku, shikamo nedan ga tegoro da.","german":"The quality is good, and what's more, the price is reasonable.","furigana":"ひんしつ|よ|ねだん|てごろ"},{"japanese":"雨が降ってきた。しかも、傘を持っていない。","romaji":"Ame ga futte kita. Shikamo, kasa o motte inai.","german":"It started to rain. And on top of that, I don't have an umbrella.","furigana":"あめ|ふ|かさ|も"},{"japanese":"彼は何度も注意されて、しかも同じミスを繰り返した。","romaji":"Kare wa nando mo chuui sarete, shikamo onaji misu o kurikaeshita.","german":"He was warned many times, and yet he repeated the same mistake.","furigana":"かれ|なんど|ちゅうい|おな|く|かえ"}],"notes":"しかも adds a second, often intensifying piece of information: \"and what's more, on top of that\" - in positive as well as negative contexts. Similar are それに (more colloquial, often when listing reasons) and その上 (somewhat more formal). More rarely it means \"and yet\" when the second contradicts the first. It is almost always written in kana; the kanji spelling 然も is obsolete and can also be read さも.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本料理の中で取り分け寿司が好きだ。","romaji":"Nihon ryōri no naka de toriwake sushi ga suki da.","german":"Among Japanese dishes, I like sushi in particular.","furigana":"にほんりょうり|なか|と|わ|すし|す"},{"japanese":"彼女は語学が得意で、とりわけ英語が上手だ。","romaji":"Kanojo wa gogaku ga tokui de, toriwake eigo ga jōzu da.","german":"She is good at languages, and her English in particular is excellent.","furigana":"かのじょ|ごがく|とくい|えいご|じょうず"},{"japanese":"この地域は、とりわけ秋の紅葉が美しいことで知られている。","romaji":"Kono chiiki wa, toriwake aki no kōyō ga utsukushii koto de shirarete iru.","german":"This region is known especially for its beautiful autumn leaves.","furigana":"ちいき|あき|こうよう|うつく|し"}],"notes":"取り分け as an adverb is mostly written in kana: とりわけ \"especially, above all\". It singles out one element from a group and is somewhat more elevated than 特に; 中でも is similar. There is also the noun 取り分け \"portioning\" from the verb 取り分ける \"to divide food onto plates\"; in that meaning it is usually written with kanji.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は徐に口を開いて語り始めた。","romaji":"Kare wa omomuro ni kuchi wo hiraite katari hajimeta.","german":"He slowly opened his mouth and began to speak.","furigana":"かれ|おもむろ|くち|ひら|かた|はじ"},{"japanese":"社長はおもむろに立ち上がり、窓の外を眺めた。","romaji":"Shachō wa omomuro ni tachiagari, mado no soto o nagameta.","german":"The president rose unhurriedly and looked out of the window.","furigana":"しゃちょう|た|あ|まど|そと|なが"},{"japanese":"彼はおもむろにポケットから手紙を取り出した。","romaji":"Kare wa omomuro ni poketto kara tegami o toridashita.","german":"He slowly took a letter out of his pocket.","furigana":"かれ|てがみ|と|だ"}],"notes":"徐に (usually おもむろに) originally means \"slowly, in a measured way, without haste\" and often introduces a significant action: おもむろに立ち上がる, おもむろに口を開く. Many Japanese people today wrongly use it in the sense of \"suddenly\"; this counts as a mistake in exams and careful writing. Opposites: いきなり, 突然.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仮令失敗しても諦めない覚悟だ。","romaji":"Tatoe shippai shite mo akiramenai kakugo da.","german":"I am determined not to give up even if I fail.","furigana":"たとえしっぱい|あきら|かくご"},{"japanese":"たとえ雨でも、試合は予定どおり行われる。","romaji":"Tatoe ame demo, shiai wa yotei dōri okonawareru.","german":"Even if it rains, the match will take place as scheduled.","furigana":"あめ|しあい|よてい|おこな"},{"japanese":"たとえ冗談でも、そんなことを言ってはいけない。","romaji":"Tatoe jōdan demo, sonna koto o itte wa ikenai.","german":"Even as a joke, you must not say such a thing.","furigana":"じょうだん|い"}],"notes":"仮令 is nowadays almost only written in kana: たとえ. It introduces a concessive clause and almost always occurs with 〜ても/〜でも: たとえ失敗しても \"even if I fail\", たとえ冗談でも \"even if it's only a joke\". The sentence remains grammatical without たとえ, but たとえ strengthens the \"even if\". Do not confuse it with the noun 例え \"comparison, simile\" (verb 例える).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"碌に食事もとらずに働いている。","romaji":"Roku ni shokuji mo torazu ni hataraite iru.","german":"He works without even eating properly.","furigana":"ろく|しょくじ|はたら"},{"japanese":"昨夜は忙しくて、ろくに寝ていない。","romaji":"Yūbe wa isogashikute, roku ni nete inai.","german":"I was so busy last night that I hardly slept.","furigana":"ゆうべ|いそが|ね"},{"japanese":"ろくに調べもしないで、文句を言うな。","romaji":"Roku ni shirabe mo shinai de, monku o iu na.","german":"Don't complain when you haven't even looked into it properly.","furigana":"しら|もんく|い"}],"notes":"碌に (usually ろくに) always occurs with a negative and means \"not properly, hardly decently\": ろくに寝ていない, ろくに知らない. The tone is colloquial and often reproachful (ろくに調べもしないで). Related are ろくな + negative (ろくなことがない \"nothing good\") and ろくでもない \"good-for-nothing, lousy\". More neutral is あまり〜ない or 十分に〜ない.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"漫然と日々を過ごしてはいけない。","romaji":"Manzen to hibi wo sugoshite wa ikenai.","german":"You must not spend your days aimlessly.","furigana":"まんぜん|ひび|す"},{"japanese":"漫然と運転していると、事故を起こしやすい。","romaji":"Manzen to unten shite iru to, jiko o okoshiyasui.","german":"If you drive without paying attention, you easily cause an accident.","furigana":"まんぜん|うんてん|じこ|お"},{"japanese":"目標もなく漫然と勉強しても、なかなか身につかない。","romaji":"Mokuhyō mo naku manzen to benkyō shite mo, nakanaka mi ni tsukanai.","german":"Studying without a goal, just going through the motions, you hardly retain anything.","furigana":"もくひょう|まんぜん|べんきょう|み"}],"notes":"漫然と describes doing something without goal, concentration or intention: 漫然と過ごす, 漫然と眺める, 漫然と運転する. Attributively one says 漫然とした (漫然とした日々). It is literary and almost always critical, for example in admonitions or accident reports. Similar but more colloquial is ぼんやり; なんとなく stresses more the lack of a clear reason.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"粛々と手続きを進めている。","romaji":"Shukushuku to tetsuzuki wo susumete iru.","german":"We are proceeding with the procedure quietly and steadily.","furigana":"しゅくしゅく|てつづ|すす"},{"japanese":"反対の声があっても、計画は粛々と進められた。","romaji":"Hantai no koe ga atte mo, keikaku wa shukushuku to susumerareta.","german":"Despite voices of opposition, the plan was carried forward undeterred.","furigana":"はんたい|こえ|けいかく|しゅくしゅく|すす"},{"japanese":"式は遺族が見守る中、粛々と執り行われた。","romaji":"Shiki wa izoku ga mimamoru naka, shukushuku to toriokonawareta.","german":"The ceremony was held solemnly, with the bereaved family looking on.","furigana":"しき|いぞく|みまも|なか|しゅくしゅく|と|おこな"}],"notes":"粛々と originally means \"quietly and solemnly\" (式が粛々と行われる). Today it is heard mainly in politics and administration: 粛々と進める \"to continue calmly and according to plan, without fuss\". This formula is sometimes criticized because it suggests that opposition is simply being ignored. Attributively: 粛々とした雰囲気. Similar but more neutral is 淡々と.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は淡々と事実を述べた。","romaji":"Kare wa tantan to jijitsu wo nobeta.","german":"He stated the facts impassively.","furigana":"かれ|たんたん|じじつ|の"},{"japanese":"彼女は表情を変えず、淡々と仕事をこなしている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa hyōjō o kaezu, tantan to shigoto o konashite iru.","german":"Without changing her expression, she calmly gets her work done.","furigana":"かのじょ|ひょうじょう|か|たんたん|しごと"},{"japanese":"映画は、ある家族の日常を淡々と描いている。","romaji":"Eiga wa, aru kazoku no nichijō o tantan to egaite iru.","german":"The film depicts a family's daily life in a plain, understated way.","furigana":"えいが|かぞく|にちじょう|たんたん|えが"}],"notes":"淡々と describes composed, matter-of-fact behavior without visible emotion: 淡々と語る, 淡々と仕事をこなす. Attributively: 淡々とした口調, 淡々とした文章. Depending on context, it sounds appreciative (calm, objective) or somewhat cold. Originally 淡々 also means \"plain, light\", for example of taste (淡々とした味). Do not confuse it with 堂々と \"confidently, with dignity\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"毅然とした態度で不正に立ち向かう。","romaji":"Kizen to shita taido de fusei ni tachimukau.","german":"He confronts injustice with a resolute attitude.","furigana":"きぜん|たいど|ふせい|た|む"},{"japanese":"理不尽な要求には、毅然と断るべきだ。","romaji":"Rifujin na yōkyū ni wa, kizen to kotowaru beki da.","german":"One should firmly refuse unreasonable demands.","furigana":"りふじん|ようきゅう|きぜん|ことわ"},{"japanese":"記者の厳しい質問にも、大臣は毅然として答えた。","romaji":"Kisha no kibishii shitsumon ni mo, daijin wa kizen to shite kotaeta.","german":"The minister answered firmly even the reporters' tough questions.","furigana":"きしゃ|きび|しつもん|だいじん|きぜん|こた"}],"notes":"毅然と describes a firm, unyielding attitude, especially in the face of pressure or injustice. Most common is the form 毅然とした態度 \"a resolute attitude\"; adverbially 毅然と断る, 毅然として答える. The word sounds elevated and appreciative and is typical of politics, schools and corporate ethics. Similar but more neutral is きっぱり（と）, which mainly stresses clear words.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一度下した決定を翻すわけにはいかない。","romaji":"Ichido kudashita kettei wo hirugaesu wake ni wa ikanai.","german":"I can't go back on a decision once it has been made.","furigana":"いちどくだ|けってい|ひるがえ"},{"japanese":"彼は裁判で、それまでの証言を翻した。","romaji":"Kare wa saiban de, sore made no shōgen o hirugaeshita.","german":"In court, he retracted his earlier testimony.","furigana":"かれ|さいばん|しょうげん|ひるがえ"},{"japanese":"彼女はスカートを翻して、階段を駆け上がった。","romaji":"Kanojo wa sukāto o hirugaeshite, kaidan o kakeagatta.","german":"With her skirt flying, she ran up the stairs.","furigana":"かのじょ|ひるがえ|かいだん|か|あ"}],"notes":"翻す is transitive (〜を翻す); the intransitive partner is 翻る \"to flutter, to turn over\". Mainly used figuratively: to reverse an opinion, statement or decision – 前言を翻す, 証言を翻す, 決定を翻す; 反旗を翻す means \"to rise up, to rebel\". Concretely: 身を翻す \"to whirl around in a flash\", to make a skirt or flag flutter (旗を翻す).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"何度夢を挫かれても、彼は立ち上がった。","romaji":"Nando yume o kujikarete mo, kare wa tachiagatta.","german":"No matter how many times his dreams were crushed, he got back up.","furigana":"なんどゆめ|くじ|かれ|た|あ"},{"japanese":"階段で足首を挫いてしまった。","romaji":"Kaidan de ashikubi o kujiite shimatta.","german":"I sprained my ankle on the stairs.","furigana":"かいだん|あしくび|くじ"},{"japanese":"相手チームの出鼻を挫く先制ゴールだった。","romaji":"Aite chīmu no debana o kujiku sensei gōru datta.","german":"It was an opening goal that took the wind out of the opposing team's sails.","furigana":"あいて|でばな|くじ|せんせい"}],"notes":"挫く (often くじく) is transitive and has two meanings: to sprain a joint (足首を挫く) and to break another's courage or momentum (意欲を挫く, 出鼻を挫く \"to stop someone right at the start\"). The intransitive partner is 挫ける \"to lose heart\": 何度失敗しても挫けない. The noun 挫折 \"failure, setback\" contains the same kanji.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"他人を揶揄するような発言は慎むべきだ。","romaji":"Tanin wo yayu suru yō na hatsugen wa tsutsushimu beki da.","german":"One should refrain from remarks that make fun of others.","furigana":"たにん|やゆ|はつげん|つつし"},{"japanese":"若者の新しい言葉遣いは、しばしばメディアで揶揄の対象になる。","romaji":"Wakamono no atarashii kotobazukai wa, shibashiba media de yayu no taishō ni naru.","german":"Young people's new way of speaking often becomes a target of ridicule in the media.","furigana":"わかもの|あたら|ことばづか|やゆ|たいしょう"},{"japanese":"その政策は「ばらまき」と揶揄された。","romaji":"Sono seisaku wa \"baramaki\" to yayu sareta.","german":"The policy was derided as \"handing out money indiscriminately\".","furigana":"せいさく|やゆ"}],"notes":"揶揄 is the literary word for mockery or mocking criticism, as a transitive する-verb: 〜を揶揄する. Very typical is the passive form with a quotation: 「〜」と揶揄される \"to be derisively called ...\". In everyday speech one says からかう, which is sometimes even written with the same kanji, 揶揄う. 揶揄 often targets politics, society or public figures.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この体たらくでは信用を失うのも当然だ。","romaji":"Kono teitaraku de wa shin'yō wo ushinau no mo tōzen da.","german":"With this sorry state of affairs, it is only natural to lose trust.","furigana":"てい|しんよう|うしな|とうぜん"},{"japanese":"優勝候補と言われながら、初戦で敗退するとは何という体たらくだ。","romaji":"Yūshō kōho to iwarenagara, shosen de haitai suru to wa nan to iu teitaraku da.","german":"Billed as a title favorite, yet knocked out in the first round – what a disgrace!","furigana":"ゆうしょうこうほ|い|しょせん|はいたい|なん|てい"},{"japanese":"部屋は散らかり放題で、我ながらひどい体たらくだ。","romaji":"Heya wa chirakarihōdai de, ware nagara hidoi teitaraku da.","german":"My room is a total mess, and even I find it a sorry state.","furigana":"へや|ち|ほうだい|われ|てい"}],"notes":"体たらく denotes a shameful, pitiful state and is always pejorative. Typical are exclamatory frames such as この体たらく, 何という体たらくだ or 〜とはひどい体たらくだ, as a reproach to others or as self-criticism. Neutral words for \"state\" are 状態 or 有様; 有様 can also be negative but is less harsh.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不甲斐ない結果に自分自身に腹が立つ。","romaji":"Fugainai kekka ni jibun jishin ni hara ga tatsu.","german":"I'm angry at myself over this disappointing result.","furigana":"ふがい|けっか|じぶんじしん|はら|た"},{"japanese":"後輩に助けてもらってばかりで、先輩として不甲斐ない。","romaji":"Kōhai ni tasukete moratte bakari de, senpai to shite fugainai.","german":"I always rely on my junior to help me, and as a senior I feel pathetic.","furigana":"こうはい|たす|せんぱい|ふがい"},{"japanese":"ファンは、チームの不甲斐ない戦いぶりに怒りの声を上げた。","romaji":"Fan wa, chīmu no fugainai tatakaiburi ni ikari no koe o ageta.","german":"The fans raised angry voices over the team's feeble performance.","furigana":"ふがい|たたか|いか|こえ|あ"}],"notes":"不甲斐ない (also ふがいない) is an い-adjective for someone who falls short of expectations and shows themselves to be pitiful and spineless. Often as self-reproach: 自分が不甲斐ない, 不甲斐ない思いをする; or as criticism: 不甲斐ない結果, 不甲斐ない戦いぶり. It contains 甲斐 \"worth, reward\", so literally \"without worth\". Similar is 情けない, which means \"pathetic, shameful\" more generally.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"甲斐甲斐しく働く姿に感心した。","romaji":"Kaigaishiku hataraku sugata ni kanshin shita.","german":"I admired the sight of her working diligently.","furigana":"かいがい|はたら|すがた|かんしん"},{"japanese":"彼女は入院中の母をかいがいしく世話した。","romaji":"Kanojo wa nyūinchū no haha o kaigaishiku sewa shita.","german":"She looked after her hospitalized mother devotedly.","furigana":"かのじょ|にゅういんちゅう|はは|せわ"},{"japanese":"エプロン姿の夫が、かいがいしく台所で動き回っている。","romaji":"Epuron sugata no otto ga, kaigaishiku daidokoro de ugokimawatte iru.","german":"My husband, in an apron, is busily moving around the kitchen.","furigana":"すがた|おっと|だいどころ|うご|まわ"}],"notes":"甲斐甲斐しい (usually かいがいしい) describes someone who works or cares for others nimbly, eagerly and devotedly. Most often adverbial: かいがいしく世話をする, かいがいしく働く. The word has a warm, appreciative tone and is often used for nursing, housework or hosts. Do not confuse it with 不甲斐ない, which, despite the shared 甲斐, expresses the opposite.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"健気に頑張る子供の姿に涙が出た。","romaji":"Kenage ni ganbaru kodomo no sugata ni namida ga deta.","german":"I was moved to tears by the sight of the child doing their best so bravely.","furigana":"けなげ|がんば|こども|すがた|なみだ|で"},{"japanese":"母を助けようと家事をこなす少女の健気さに胸を打たれた。","romaji":"Haha o tasukeyō to kaji o konasu shōjo no kenagesa ni mune o utareta.","german":"I was deeply touched by the girl's plucky effort doing the housework to help her mother.","furigana":"はは|たす|かじ|しょうじょ|けなげ|むね|う"},{"japanese":"アスファルトの隙間から健気に咲く花を見つけた。","romaji":"Asufaruto no sukima kara kenage ni saku hana o mitsuketa.","german":"I found a flower blooming bravely in a crack in the asphalt.","furigana":"すきま|けなげ|さ|はな|み"}],"notes":"健気 is a な-adjective for the brave, dutiful behavior of someone weaker despite difficult circumstances, typically of children, young people or even animals and plants: 健気な子, 健気に頑張る, 健気に咲く花. It stirs emotion and sympathy in the observer. For brave adults in danger, one rather says 勇敢 or 勇ましい.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あどけない笑顔に心が癒される。","romaji":"Adokenai egao ni kokoro ga iyasareru.","german":"An innocent smile soothes the heart.","furigana":"えがお|こころ|いや"},{"japanese":"子どものあどけない寝顔を見ていると、疲れも忘れる。","romaji":"Kodomo no adokenai negao o mite iru to, tsukare mo wasureru.","german":"Looking at my child's innocent sleeping face, I forget my fatigue.","furigana":"こ|ねがお|み|つか|わす"},{"japanese":"高校生になっても、彼女の顔にはまだあどけなさが残っている。","romaji":"Kōkōsei ni natte mo, kanojo no kao ni wa mada adokenasa ga nokotte iru.","german":"Even though she is in high school, her face still retains a childlike innocence.","furigana":"こうこうせい|かのじょ|かお|のこ"}],"notes":"あどけない is an い-adjective for the innocent, guileless aura of a small child, especially in the face and expression: あどけない笑顔, あどけない寝顔. For teenagers or adults, one often says あどけなさが残る \"still looks childlike\". Unlike 無邪気, which also describes childlike behavior, or 幼い, which just means \"young, immature\", あどけない is always affectionate and never pejorative in the sense of \"naive\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"忌々しい失敗をしてしまった。","romaji":"Imaimashii shippai wo shite shimatta.","german":"I made an infuriating mistake.","furigana":"いまいま|しっぱい"},{"japanese":"忌々しいことに、出かけようとした途端に雨が降り出した。","romaji":"Imaimashii koto ni, dekakeyō to shita totan ni ame ga furidashita.","german":"Damn it, it started raining just as I was about to leave.","furigana":"いまいま|で|とたん|あめ|ふ|だ"},{"japanese":"彼はライバルの成功を忌々しそうに見ていた。","romaji":"Kare wa raibaru no seikō o imaimashisō ni mite ita.","german":"He watched his rival's success with bitter annoyance.","furigana":"かれ|せいこう|いまいま|み"}],"notes":"忌々しい (often いまいましい) expresses fierce annoyance at something one cannot change, almost like a curse: 忌々しい雨だ \"this damned rain\". Also typical are 忌々しいことに \"annoyingly\" and 忌々しそうに \"with an irritated look\". The kanji 忌 (\"to avoid, taboo\") recalls the older sense \"ominous\". Compared with 腹立たしい, 忌々しい is more emotional and bitter.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"空々しい言い訳を並べている。","romaji":"Sorazorashii iiwake wo narabete iru.","german":"He lines up hollow excuses.","furigana":"そらぞら|い|わけ|なら"},{"japanese":"心のこもっていない謝罪は、かえって空々しく聞こえる。","romaji":"Kokoro no komotte inai shazai wa, kaette sorazorashiku kikoeru.","german":"An apology without real feeling sounds hollow instead.","furigana":"こころ|しゃざい|そらぞら|き"},{"japanese":"彼の空々しいお世辞には、もううんざりだ。","romaji":"Kare no sorazorashii oseji ni wa, mō unzari da.","german":"I'm thoroughly fed up with his empty flattery.","furigana":"かれ|そらぞら|せじ"}],"notes":"空々しい describes words or gestures that sound hollow and insincere because there is no real feeling behind them: 空々しいお世辞, 空々しい言い訳, 空々しく聞こえる. Related is 白々しい, which stresses more the brazenness with which someone obviously lies or feigns ignorance. 空々しい stresses emptiness; similar is 上辺だけの \"merely superficial\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"白々しい嘘をつくのはやめてほしい。","romaji":"Shirajirashii uso wo tsuku no wa yamete hoshii.","german":"I wish you would stop telling such obvious lies.","furigana":"しらじら|うそ"},{"japanese":"全部見ていたのに、彼は白々しく「何のこと？」と聞き返した。","romaji":"Zenbu mite ita noni, kare wa shirajirashiku \"nan no koto?\" to kikikaeshita.","german":"Although he had seen everything, he asked back with feigned innocence, \"What do you mean?\"","furigana":"ぜんぶみ|かれ|しらじら|なん|き|かえ"},{"japanese":"今さら「応援している」なんて、白々しいことを言わないでほしい。","romaji":"Imasara \"ōen shite iru\" nante, shirajirashii koto o iwanaide hoshii.","german":"Please don't say something so hypocritical as \"I'm rooting for you\" now, of all times.","furigana":"いま|おうえん|しらじら|い"}],"notes":"白々しい describes brazen, transparent insincerity: 白々しい嘘 \"a bald-faced lie\", 白々しいお世辞, or pretending not to know: 白々しく「知らない」と言う. Compared with 空々しい (\"hollow, empty\"), 白々しい is sharper and stresses the brazenness. Do not confuse it with the expression 白々と明ける (しらじらと) \"dawn breaks, the sky grows light\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"物々しい警備体制が敷かれている。","romaji":"Monomonoshii keibi taisei ga shikarete iru.","german":"A heavy security presence has been put in place.","furigana":"ものもの|けいびたいせい|し"},{"japanese":"物々しい雰囲気の中、裁判が始まった。","romaji":"Monomonoshii fun'iki no naka, saiban ga hajimatta.","german":"The trial began in a tense atmosphere.","furigana":"ものもの|ふんいき|なか|さいばん|はじ"},{"japanese":"ちょっとした検査なのに、そんな物々しい言い方をしないでよ。","romaji":"Chotto shita kensa na noni, sonna monomonoshii iikata o shinaide yo.","german":"It's just a minor examination, so don't put it in such grand terms.","furigana":"けんさ|ものもの|い|かた"}],"notes":"物々しい describes something that looks intimidating or exaggerated because of its scale or severity. Most common in the news: 物々しい警備 \"heavy security\", 物々しい雰囲気 \"tense, martial atmosphere\". In everyday life it also means \"exaggerated, blown up\": 物々しい言い方, 物々しい格好. For merely \"magnificent, impressive\", 堂々とした or 立派 is more usual.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"外国人の子供には何もかもが物珍しい。","romaji":"Gaikokujin no kodomo ni wa nani mo ka mo ga monomezurashii.","german":"Everything is novel to a foreign child.","furigana":"がいこくじん|こども|なに|ものめずら"},{"japanese":"村の子供たちは物珍しそうに外国人観光客を見ていた。","romaji":"Mura no kodomotachi wa monomezurashisō ni gaikokujin kankōkyaku o mite ita.","german":"The village children looked at the foreign tourists with curiosity.","furigana":"むら|こども|ものめずら|がいこくじんかんこうきゃく|み"},{"japanese":"初めて来た客は、物珍しげに店内を見回した。","romaji":"Hajimete kita kyaku wa, monomezurashige ni tennai o mimawashita.","german":"The first-time customer looked around the shop with curiosity.","furigana":"はじ|き|きゃく|ものめずら|てんない|みまわ"}],"notes":"物珍しい describes something that is new and unfamiliar to the observer and therefore arouses curiosity. Unlike 珍しい (\"rare, unusual\"), which states an objective rarity, 物珍しい stresses the subjective appeal of the unfamiliar. Very common are the adverbial forms 物珍しそうに / 物珍しげに (\"(to look) with curiosity, with wonder\") and the noun 物珍しさ, e.g. 物珍しさから \"out of sheer curiosity\". Slightly elevated, rather written tone.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仰々しい言い方をしなくてもいいだろう。","romaji":"Gyōgyōshii iikata o shinakute mo ii darō.","german":"There's no need to put it in such grandiose words.","furigana":"ぎょうぎょう|い|かた"},{"japanese":"仰々しい警備の中、首脳会談が行われた。","romaji":"Gyōgyōshii keibi no naka, shunō kaidan ga okonawareta.","german":"The summit was held under heavy, showy security.","furigana":"ぎょうぎょう|けいび|なか|しゅのうかいだん|おこな"},{"japanese":"彼はちょっとしたことでも仰々しく騒ぎ立てる。","romaji":"Kare wa chotto shita koto demo gyōgyōshiku sawagitateru.","german":"He makes a huge fuss even over trivial matters.","furigana":"かれ|ぎょうぎょう|さわ|た"}],"notes":"仰々しい means that something is presented in a more elaborate, solemn or dramatic way than the matter deserves: words, titles, packaging, a security presence. In everyday speech one usually says 大げさ (\"exaggerated\"); 仰々しい stresses more the pompous, blown-up quality and is pejorative. Adverbial: 仰々しく騒ぎ立てる \"to make a big fuss\". Also written 仰仰しい; \"boastful\" in the sense of showing off would rather be 自慢げ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"雨の日は外出するのが億劫だ。","romaji":"Ame no hi wa gaishutsu suru no ga okkū da.","german":"I can't be bothered to go out on rainy days.","furigana":"あめ|ひ|がいしゅつ|おっくう"},{"japanese":"年を取ると、新しいことを始めるのが億劫になる。","romaji":"Toshi o toru to, atarashii koto o hajimeru no ga okkū ni naru.","german":"As you get older, it becomes a chore to start something new.","furigana":"とし|と|あたら|はじ|おっくう"},{"japanese":"返事を書くのが億劫で、メールを一週間も放っておいた。","romaji":"Henji o kaku no ga okkū de, mēru o isshūkan mo hōtte oita.","german":"I couldn't be bothered to write a reply and left the email for a whole week.","furigana":"へんじ|か|おっくう|いっしゅうかん|ほう"}],"notes":"億劫 is a な-adjective that describes inner sluggishness: something seems so tedious that one cannot bring oneself to do it. Typical is the frame 〜のが億劫だ / 億劫になる, plus the verb 億劫がる. Whereas 面倒 (\"troublesome, bothersome\") judges the matter itself as laborious, 億劫 stresses one's own reluctance. Often written in kana as おっくう; the word comes from the Buddhist 億劫 (\"an immeasurably long period of time\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"書類の手続きが面倒臭くて仕方がない。","romaji":"Shorui no tetsuzuki ga mendōkusakute shikata ga nai.","german":"The paperwork procedures are a terrible hassle.","furigana":"しょるい|てつづ|めんどうくさ|しかた"},{"japanese":"雨の日は出かけるのが面倒くさい。","romaji":"Ame no hi wa dekakeru no ga mendōkusai.","german":"On rainy days, going out is a pain.","furigana":"あめ|ひ|で|めんどう"},{"japanese":"彼は細かいことにうるさくて、ちょっと面倒くさい人だ。","romaji":"Kare wa komakai koto ni urusakute, chotto mendōkusai hito da.","german":"He is fussy about small details and is a bit of a pain to deal with.","furigana":"かれ|こま|めんどう|ひと"}],"notes":"面倒臭い expresses that one finds something burdensome out of reluctance: 手続きが面倒臭い, 〜するのが面倒臭い. It is usually written 面倒くさい or めんどくさい; in speech the う is often dropped, and informally one also says めんどい. Compared with 面倒な \"complicated, laborious\", the word stresses one's own reluctance more and is more colloquial. Said of people, it means \"tiring, difficult to deal with\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"機械に疎いので使い方がわからない。","romaji":"Kikai ni utoi node tsukaikata ga wakaranai.","german":"I'm not familiar with machines, so I don't know how to use it.","furigana":"きかい|うと|つか|かた"},{"japanese":"私は流行に疎いので、若い人の話についていけない。","romaji":"Watashi wa ryūkō ni utoi node, wakai hito no hanashi ni tsuite ikenai.","german":"I'm out of touch with trends, so I can't keep up with what young people talk about.","furigana":"わたし|りゅうこう|うと|わか|ひと|はなし"},{"japanese":"卒業してから、昔の仲間とは付き合いが疎くなった。","romaji":"Sotsugyō shite kara, mukashi no nakama to wa tsukiai ga utoku natta.","german":"Since graduating, my contact with my old friends has become sparse.","furigana":"そつぎょう|むかし|なかま|つ|あ|うと"}],"notes":"疎い usually takes に and means \"to be unfamiliar with something, to know little about it\": 機械に疎い, 流行に疎い, 世事に疎い. The older meaning \"estranged, distant\" lives on in 付き合いが疎くなる and in the proverb 去る者は日々に疎し (\"out of sight, out of mind\"); in everyday speech one rather says 疎遠になる for this. Compared with 詳しくない, 疎い sounds somewhat more elevated and is often self-deprecating.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"逞しく成長した息子を誇りに思う。","romaji":"Takumashiku seichō shita musuko o hokori ni omou.","german":"I am proud of my son, who has grown up strong.","furigana":"たくま|せいちょう|むすこ|ほこ|おも"},{"japanese":"彼は日焼けした逞しい腕で重い荷物を持ち上げた。","romaji":"Kare wa hiyake shita takumashii ude de omoi nimotsu o mochiageta.","german":"He lifted the heavy luggage with his tanned, sturdy arms.","furigana":"かれ|ひや|たくま|うで|おも|にもつ|も|あ"},{"japanese":"被災地の人々は、困難の中でも逞しく生きている。","romaji":"Hisaichi no hitobito wa, konnan no naka demo takumashiku ikite iru.","german":"The people in the disaster area live resiliently even amid hardship.","furigana":"ひさいち|ひとびと|こんなん|なか|たくま|い"}],"notes":"逞しい first describes physical strength (逞しい腕 \"strong arms\"), then also inner strength and resilience: 逞しく生きる \"to live resiliently\". For children, 逞しく育つ praises that they have become robust and independent. Set phrase: 想像を逞しくする \"to let one's imagination run wild\". Mostly written in kana, たくましい; unlike 強い, there is a note of appreciation for staying power.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"疾しいことは何もしていない。","romaji":"Yamashii koto wa nani mo shite inai.","german":"I haven't done anything I have to feel guilty about.","furigana":"やま|なに"},{"japanese":"何かやましいことがあるから、目をそらすんだろう。","romaji":"Nanika yamashii koto ga aru kara, me o sorasu n darō.","german":"You're looking away because you have something to hide, aren't you?","furigana":"なに|め"},{"japanese":"嘘をついたことに、少しやましさを感じている。","romaji":"Uso o tsuita koto ni, sukoshi yamashisa o kanjite iru.","german":"I feel a little guilty about having lied.","furigana":"うそ|すこ|かん"}],"notes":"やましい (mostly in kana; the kanji is rather 疚しい than 疾しい) denotes a guilty conscience because one has something to hide. Most common is the negation やましいことは何もない / していない \"I have nothing to reproach myself for\". Noun: やましさ. Close in meaning are 後ろめたい (\"to feel guilty, to have a bad conscience\") and 気がとがめる; \"ashamed\" in the sense of embarrassed would instead be 恥ずかしい.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"遣る瀬無い気持ちで一杯だった。","romaji":"Yarusenai kimochi de ippai datta.","german":"I was filled with inconsolable sorrow.","furigana":"や|せな|きも|いっぱい"},{"japanese":"努力が報われず、やるせない思いがした。","romaji":"Doryoku ga mukuwarezu, yarusenai omoi ga shita.","german":"My efforts went unrewarded, and I felt a dreary helplessness.","furigana":"どりょく|むく|おも"},{"japanese":"事故で若い命が失われたというニュースは、本当にやるせない。","romaji":"Jiko de wakai inochi ga ushinawareta to iu nyūsu wa, hontō ni yarusenai.","german":"The news that young lives were lost in the accident is truly heartbreaking.","furigana":"じこ|わか|いのち|うしな|ほんとう"}],"notes":"やるせない (mostly in kana) describes a grief one cannot get rid of and is powerless against: sadness, frustration and helplessness at once. Literally about \"there is no ford (瀬) to which one could send it\". Typical are やるせない気持ち / 思い, often in the face of injustice, senseless loss or fruitless effort. Similar is 切ない, which sounds more yearning and wistful; やるせない stresses the lack of a way out.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"目まぐるしく変化する社会に対応する。","romaji":"Memagurushiku henka suru shakai ni taiō suru.","german":"We adapt to a rapidly changing society.","furigana":"め|へんか|しゃかい|たいおう"},{"japanese":"今週は会議と出張が続いて、目まぐるしい一週間だった。","romaji":"Konshū wa kaigi to shutchō ga tsuzuite, memagurushii isshūkan datta.","german":"With meetings and business trips back to back, this week was a whirlwind.","furigana":"こんしゅう|かいぎ|しゅっちょう|つづ|め|いっしゅうかん"},{"japanese":"試合は目まぐるしく展開し、最後まで目が離せなかった。","romaji":"Shiai wa memagurushiku tenkai shi, saigo made me ga hanasenakatta.","german":"The game unfolded at a dizzying pace, and I couldn't take my eyes off it until the end.","furigana":"しあい|め|てんかい|さいご|め|はな"}],"notes":"目まぐるしい literally means \"so fast that it makes your eyes spin\" and describes rapid, hard-to-follow changes or hectic goings-on. Very common adverbially: 目まぐるしく変わる / 変化する / 展開する. Attributively: 目まぐるしい一日 \"a hectic day\". Unlike 忙しい (\"busy, having a lot to do\"), 目まぐるしい stresses the pace and the constant change.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"適切に取り計らいますのでご安心ください。","romaji":"Tekisetsu ni torihakaraimasu node go anshin kudasai.","german":"We will take care of it appropriately, so please rest assured.","furigana":"てきせつ|と|はか|あんしん"},{"japanese":"先生が、私が試験を受け直せるように取り計らってくださった。","romaji":"Sensei ga, watashi ga shiken o ukenaoseru yō ni torihakaratte kudasatta.","german":"The teacher arranged things so that I could retake the exam.","furigana":"せんせい|わたし|しけん|う|なお|と|はか"},{"japanese":"特別なお取り計らいをいただき、ありがとうございました。","romaji":"Tokubetsu na o-torihakarai o itadaki, arigatō gozaimashita.","german":"Thank you for your special consideration.","furigana":"とくべつ|と|はか"}],"notes":"取り計らう means to handle a matter in the interest of those involved, or to arrange something so that it works out; typical is 〜ように取り計らう. The word sounds formal and occurs mainly in business and official language, often with keigo: 取り計らってくださる. The noun お取り計らい (\"consideration, arrangement\") is very common in thank-you formulas. More neutral are 手配する (\"to arrange\") or 処理する (\"to deal with\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"台風で甚大な被害を被った。","romaji":"Taifū de jindai na higai o kōmutta.","german":"We suffered enormous damage from the typhoon.","furigana":"たいふう|じんだい|ひがい|こうむ"},{"japanese":"取引先の倒産で、我が社も大きな損失を被った。","romaji":"Torihikisaki no tōsan de, wagasha mo ōkina sonshitsu o kōmutta.","german":"Our company also incurred a large loss due to a business partner's bankruptcy.","furigana":"とりひきさき|とうさん|わ|しゃ|おお|そんしつ|こうむ"},{"japanese":"そんな危ない仕事は御免被るよ。","romaji":"Sonna abunai shigoto wa gomen kōmuru yo.","german":"I'd rather be excused from such a dangerous job.","furigana":"あぶ|しごと|ごめんこうむ"}],"notes":"被る (こうむる) means \"to suffer or incur something (usually negative)\": 被害 / 損害 / 損失 / 迷惑を被る. More rarely and in elevated language also something positive: 恩恵を被る \"to receive benefits\". Note: the same kanji is read かぶる (\"to put on, to pull over\", 帽子をかぶる); in combination with 被害 or 損害 it is always こうむる. Set phrase: 御免被る \"I'd rather be excused, no thank you\". Literary; in conversation one rather says 受ける.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"支払いが滞ると信用問題に発展する。","romaji":"Shiharai ga todokōru to shin'yō mondai ni hatten suru.","german":"If payments fall behind, it develops into a matter of trust.","furigana":"しはら|とどこお|しんようもんだい|はってん"},{"japanese":"大雪で物流が滞り、店の棚が空になった。","romaji":"Ōyuki de butsuryū ga todokōri, mise no tana ga kara ni natta.","german":"Heavy snow held up distribution, and the shelves in the stores went empty.","furigana":"おおゆき|ぶつりゅう|とどこお|みせ|たな|から"},{"japanese":"家賃が三か月分滞っている。","romaji":"Yachin ga sankagetsu-bun todokōtte iru.","german":"The rent is three months in arrears.","furigana":"やちん|さん|げつぶんとどこお"}],"notes":"滞る is intransitive and means that something that should flow or progress comes to a standstill: 仕事 / 物流 / 交通が滞る. For payments it means \"to be in arrears\": 家賃 / 支払いが滞る. The noun 滞り belongs with it; 滞りなく (\"smoothly, without incident\") is a set formula, e.g. 式は滞りなく終わった. Related is 停滞 (\"stagnation\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"次世代を担う人材を育成する。","romaji":"Jisedai o ninau jinzai o ikusei suru.","german":"We train talent who will shoulder the next generation.","furigana":"じせだい|にな|じんざい|いくせい"},{"japanese":"彼女はプロジェクトの中心的な役割を担っている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa purojekuto no chūshinteki na yakuwari o ninatte iru.","german":"She plays a central role in the project.","furigana":"かのじょ|ちゅうしんてき|やくわり|にな"},{"japanese":"観光業はこの地域の経済を担う重要な産業だ。","romaji":"Kankōgyō wa kono chiiki no keizai o ninau jūyō na sangyō da.","german":"Tourism is an important industry that supports the economy of this region.","furigana":"かんこうぎょう|ちいき|けいざい|にな|じゅうよう|さんぎょう"}],"notes":"担う today means almost only figuratively \"to bear responsibility, a role or a task\": 役割 / 責任 / 未来を担う. Very common is 次世代を担う / 未来を担う (\"who will carry the future\") as a modifier before 人材 or 若者. The literal meaning \"to carry on the shoulder\" is rare; for that one says 担ぐ (かつぐ), which has the same kanji. The word sounds elevated and suits speeches, newspapers and corporate texts.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"細部に拘る職人気質の持ち主だ。","romaji":"Saibu ni kodawaru shokunin kishitsu no mochinushi da.","german":"He has a craftsman's temperament and is particular about details.","furigana":"さいぶ|こだわ|しょくにんきしつ|も|ぬし"},{"japanese":"過去の失敗にいつまでもこだわっていても仕方がない。","romaji":"Kako no shippai ni itsu made mo kodawatte ite mo shikata ga nai.","german":"There's no point in dwelling on past mistakes forever.","furigana":"かこ|しっぱい|しかた"},{"japanese":"この店は素材にこだわったラーメンで人気だ。","romaji":"Kono mise wa sozai ni kodawatta rāmen de ninki da.","german":"This shop is popular for its ramen, made with great attention to the ingredients.","furigana":"みせ|そざい|にんき"}],"notes":"こだわる (mostly in kana) takes に and carries two evaluations: negative \"to get hung up, to cling to something\" (過去にこだわる, 細かいことにこだわる), positive \"to place special value on, to be uncompromising\" (素材にこだわる). The positive reading is very common in advertising, also as the noun こだわり: こだわりの一品 \"a product made with devotion\". Unlike 気にする (\"to worry\"), it is about persistent adherence.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人目を憚って密かに会った。","romaji":"Hitome o habakatte hisoka ni atta.","german":"Wary of other people's eyes, they met in secret.","furigana":"ひとめ|はばか|ひそ|あ"},{"japanese":"人前では口にするのも憚られる話だ。","romaji":"Hitomae de wa kuchi ni suru no mo habakarareru hanashi da.","german":"It is a story that one hardly dares to say in public.","furigana":"ひとまえ|くち|はばか|はなし"},{"japanese":"「憎まれっ子世に憚る」と言うが、あの人はますます偉くなった。","romaji":"\"Nikumarekko yo ni habakaru\" to iu ga, ano hito wa masumasu eraku natta.","german":"They say \"the hated child gets ahead in the world\", and that person has become more and more powerful.","furigana":"にく|こよ|はばか|い|ひと|えら"}],"notes":"憚る means to avoid something out of consideration for others or out of reserve: 人目を憚る \"to shun other people's eyes\". Very common is the passive form: 口にするのも憚られる \"one hardly dares to say it\", as well as 〜を憚らず / 誰に憚ることなく \"without any reserve\". The second meaning \"to throw one's weight around\" is known almost only from the proverb 憎まれっ子世に憚る (\"ill weeds grow apace\"). Elevated, written tone; 遠慮する is the everyday equivalent.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子供たちは、夏休みが来るのを待ち侘びている。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa, natsuyasumi ga kuru no o machiwabite iru.","german":"The children are eagerly waiting for summer vacation to come.","furigana":"こども|なつやす|く|ま|わ"},{"japanese":"都会の暮らしに住み侘びて、彼は故郷へ帰った。","romaji":"Tokai no kurashi ni sumiwabite, kare wa kokyō e kaetta.","german":"Weary of city life, he returned to his hometown.","furigana":"とかい|く|す|わ|かれ|こきょう|かえ"},{"japanese":"山奥の庵で、老僧は独り侘びて暮らしていた。","romaji":"Yamaoku no iori de, rōsō wa hitori wabite kurashite ita.","german":"In a hermitage deep in the mountains, the old monk lived alone in solitude.","furigana":"やまおく||ろうそう|ひと|わ|く"}],"notes":"侘びる is a literary, today almost archaic verb: \"to pine, to be lonely and miserable, to live a sad life (in a place)\". It is alive mainly as the second element after a verb stem: 待ち侘びる \"to wait longingly, impatiently for something\" (〜を待ち侘びる) and 住み侘びる \"to become weary of living in a place\". Related are the adjective 侘しい (\"dreary, lonely, shabby\") and the aesthetic ideal 侘び寂び. Note: \"to apologize\" is written 詫びる (same reading わびる, お詫び).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"上司に諂うだけでは出世できない。","romaji":"Jōshi ni hetsurau dake de wa shusse dekinai.","german":"You can't get promoted just by flattering your boss.","furigana":"じょうし|へつら|しゅっせ"},{"japanese":"彼は権力者に諂うことを何とも思わない。","romaji":"Kare wa kenryokusha ni hetsurau koto o nan to mo omowanai.","german":"He thinks nothing of fawning on the powerful.","furigana":"かれ|けんりょくしゃ|へつら|なん|おも"},{"japanese":"客にこびへつらうような接客は好きではない。","romaji":"Kyaku ni kobihetsurau yō na sekkyaku wa suki de wa nai.","german":"I don't like service that grovels to customers.","furigana":"きゃく|せっきゃく|す"}],"notes":"へつらう (mostly in kana) means to ingratiate oneself with those more powerful through flattery and servility; it takes に: 上司に諂う, 権力に諂う. The intensified こびへつらう (\"to kowtow, to ingratiate oneself\") is very common. The word is clearly pejorative and rather written; neutral praise would be 褒める, and colloquially one says ごまをする.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"想定以上に費用が嵩んでしまった。","romaji":"Sōtei ijō ni hiyō ga kasande shimatta.","german":"The costs have mounted beyond what was expected.","furigana":"そうていいじょう|ひよう|かさ"},{"japanese":"入院が長引き、治療費が嵩んでいる。","romaji":"Nyūin ga nagabiki, chiryōhi ga kasande iru.","german":"The hospital stay is dragging on, and the treatment costs are piling up.","furigana":"にゅういん|ながび|ちりょうひ|かさ"},{"japanese":"お土産を買いすぎて、荷物がかさんでしまった。","romaji":"Omiyage o kaisugite, nimotsu ga kasande shimatta.","german":"I bought too many souvenirs, and my luggage has grown bulky.","furigana":"みやげ|か|にもつ"}],"notes":"嵩む is intransitive and means that something increases in volume or amount, especially costs and debts: 費用 / 出費 / 借金がかさむ. It usually suggests an undesired increase. From the base word 嵩 (かさ, \"volume\"), it also concerns bulky quantity: 荷物がかさむ. Mostly written in kana, かさむ; more neutral are 増える or 膨らむ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"個人情報に関わるためコメントを差し控える。","romaji":"Kojin jōhō ni kakawaru tame komento o sashihikaeru.","german":"Since it concerns personal information, I will refrain from commenting.","furigana":"こじんじょうほう|かか|さ|ひか"},{"japanese":"医者に言われて、しばらく飲酒を差し控えている。","romaji":"Isha ni iwarete, shibaraku inshu o sashihikaete iru.","german":"On the doctor's advice, I am abstaining from drinking for the time being.","furigana":"いしゃ|い|いんしゅ|さ|ひか"},{"japanese":"不要不急の外出はお差し控えください。","romaji":"Fuyō fukyū no gaishutsu wa o-sashihikae kudasai.","german":"Please refrain from non-essential, non-urgent outings.","furigana":"ふようふきゅう|がいしゅつ|さ|ひか"}],"notes":"差し控える is the formal variant of 控える and means to deliberately do without or hold back on something: コメント / 発言 / 外出を差し控える. Typical in press statements (コメントは差し控えさせていただきます \"I would prefer not to comment on that\") and announcements: お差し控えください \"please refrain\". In conversation, 控える or やめる is enough.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"諸事情を勘案した上で決定する。","romaji":"Shojijō o kan'an shita ue de kettei suru.","german":"We will decide after taking the various circumstances into consideration.","furigana":"しょじじょう|かんあん|うえ|けってい"},{"japanese":"給与は経験や能力を勘案して決定します。","romaji":"Kyūyo wa keiken ya nōryoku o kan'an shite kettei shimasu.","german":"Salary is determined taking experience and ability into account.","furigana":"きゅうよ|けいけん|のうりょく|かんあん|けってい"},{"japanese":"物価の動向も勘案すると、今回の値上げはやむを得ない。","romaji":"Bukka no dōkō mo kan'an suru to, konkai no neage wa yamu o enai.","german":"Considering the trend in prices as well, this price increase is unavoidable.","furigana":"ぶっか|どうこう|かんあん|こんかい|ねあ|え"}],"notes":"勘案 means weighing several factors against one another and including them in a decision; mostly as 〜を勘案する / 勘案して / 勘案した上で. It is a typical word of official, legal and business language. 考慮 (\"consideration\") is more general and also common in speech; 勘案 stresses adding up various circumstances. In everyday speech one says 考えに入れる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"余剰資金を設備投資に充当する。","romaji":"Yojō shikin o setsubi tōshi ni jūtō suru.","german":"Surplus funds will be allocated to capital investment.","furigana":"よじょうしきん|せつびとうし|じゅうとう"},{"japanese":"ボーナスの一部をローンの返済に充当した。","romaji":"Bōnasu no ichibu o rōn no hensai ni jūtō shita.","german":"I applied part of my bonus to repaying the loan.","furigana":"いちぶ|へんさい|じゅうとう"},{"japanese":"欠員が出たため、他部署から人員を充当した。","romaji":"Ketsuin ga deta tame, ta-busho kara jin'in o jūtō shita.","german":"Because a vacancy arose, staff were assigned from another department.","furigana":"けついん|で|たぶしょ|じんいん|じゅうとう"}],"notes":"充当 means assigning or using money, resources or personnel for a specific purpose; frame: AをBに充当する. It is a word of financial and administrative language, e.g. 予算を人件費に充当する; in tax law also \"to offset\" (還付金を未納分に充当する). Close in meaning is 充てる (あてる), which is more common in normal usage.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"融通の利かない規則に不満がある。","romaji":"Yūzū no kikanai kisoku ni fuman ga aru.","german":"I am dissatisfied with the inflexible rules.","furigana":"ゆうずう|き|きそく|ふまん"},{"japanese":"友人に十万円を融通してもらった。","romaji":"Yūjin ni jūman-en o yūzū shite moratta.","german":"A friend lent me 100,000 yen.","furigana":"ゆうじん|じゅうまんえん|ゆうずう"},{"japanese":"締め切りについては、多少融通がきく。","romaji":"Shimekiri ni tsuite wa, tashō yūzū ga kiku.","german":"There is some leeway on the deadline.","furigana":"し|き|たしょうゆうずう"}],"notes":"融通 (ゆうずう, not ゆうつう) has two main meanings: \"to lend or procure money or goods\" as a する-verb (資金を融通する), and \"flexibility, leeway\", especially in 融通が利く / 融通が利かない (\"to be flexible / stubborn, inflexible\"). Said of people, 融通が利かない is a frequent reproach. Related: 融資 (\"a bank loan\"), 臨機応変 (\"flexible according to the situation\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼らは反乱を企てた。","romaji":"Karera wa hanran o kuwadateta.","german":"They plotted a rebellion.","furigana":"かれ|はんらん|くわだ"},{"japanese":"何者かが会社の乗っ取りを企てているらしい。","romaji":"Nanimono ka ga kaisha no nottori o kuwadatete iru rashii.","german":"Someone seems to be plotting a takeover of the company.","furigana":"なにもの|かいしゃ|の|と|くわだ"},{"japanese":"彼は新しい事業を企てたが、資金が集まらなかった。","romaji":"Kare wa atarashii jigyō o kuwadateta ga, shikin ga atsumaranakatta.","german":"He planned a new business, but couldn't raise the funds.","furigana":"かれ|あたら|じぎょう|くわだ|しきん|あつ"}],"notes":"企てる means to intend to carry something out according to plan, and often has a negative undertone: 反乱 / 陰謀 / 暗殺を企てる, 自殺を企てる \"to attempt suicide\". It is used neutrally for ventures: 新事業を企てる. The noun 企て means \"plan, attempt\". 企む (たくらむ) is always negative (\"to hatch a scheme\"), whereas 計画する is completely neutral.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の失敗を嘲る者がいた。","romaji":"Kare no shippai o azakeru mono ga ita.","german":"There were people who mocked his failure.","furigana":"かれ|しっぱい|あざけ|もの"},{"japanese":"貧しい身なりを嘲られても、彼女は堂々としていた。","romaji":"Mazushii minari o azakeraretemo, kanojo wa dōdō to shite ita.","german":"Even when mocked for her poor clothes, she remained dignified.","furigana":"まず|み|あざけ|かのじょ|どうどう"},{"japanese":"彼は「そんなこともできないのか」と嘲るように笑った。","romaji":"Kare wa \"Sonna koto mo dekinai no ka\" to azakeru yō ni waratta.","german":"\"You can't even do that?\" he said, laughing mockingly.","furigana":"かれ|あざけ|わら"}],"notes":"嘲る means to scornfully ridicule someone or something, often because of weakness or failure: 人の失敗を嘲る. Common are 嘲るように笑う \"to laugh scornfully\" and the passive 嘲られる. The noun is 嘲り, the Sino-Japanese word 嘲笑 (ちょうしょう). In everyday speech one rather says あざ笑う or バカにする; からかう, by contrast, is harmless, often friendly teasing.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は研究に没頭している。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kenkyū ni bottō shite iru.","german":"She is absorbed in her research.","furigana":"かのじょ|けんきゅう|ぼっとう"},{"japanese":"読書に没頭して、降りる駅を通り過ぎた。","romaji":"Dokusho ni bottō shite, oriru eki o tōrisugita.","german":"I got so absorbed in reading that I passed my stop.","furigana":"どくしょ|ぼっとう|お|えき|とお|す"},{"japanese":"子供はゲームに没頭すると、呼んでも返事をしない。","romaji":"Kodomo wa gēmu ni bottō suru to, yonde mo henji o shinai.","german":"When the child gets absorbed in a game, they don't answer even when called.","furigana":"こども|ぼっとう|よ|へんじ"}],"notes":"没頭する takes に and means to be so absorbed in an activity that one forgets everything around: 研究 / 仕事 / 読書に没頭する. Unlike 熱中する (to be enthusiastically involved), 没頭 stresses complete immersion; 専念する, by contrast, means to consciously devote oneself exclusively to one task. 夢中になる is the more colloquial equivalent.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"目標に向かって邁進する。","romaji":"Mokuhyō ni mukatte maishin suru.","german":"I will press on toward my goal.","furigana":"もくひょう|む|まいしん"},{"japanese":"優勝を目指して、チーム一丸となって邁進してまいります。","romaji":"Yūshō o mezashite, chīmu ichigan to natte maishin shite mairimasu.","german":"Aiming for the championship, we will keep pushing forward as one team.","furigana":"ゆうしょう|めざ|いちがん|まいしん"},{"japanese":"彼は脇目も振らず、研究の道を邁進してきた。","romaji":"Kare wa wakime mo furazu, kenkyū no michi o maishin shite kita.","german":"Without looking aside, he has pressed on along the path of research.","furigana":"かれ|わきめ|ふ|けんきゅう|みち|まいしん"}],"notes":"邁進する means to work steadfastly and with full force toward a goal. Typical frames are 〜に向かって邁進する, 〜の道を邁進する and the formula 一路邁進. The word is very formal and sounds like a speech, a New Year's address or a company statement, often with the humble form 邁進してまいります. In conversation one rather says 頑張る or 突き進む; 前進する, by contrast, is neutral \"to move forward\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"礼儀を弁えた行動をしなさい。","romaji":"Reigi o wakimaeta kōdō o shinasai.","german":"Behave with proper manners.","furigana":"れいぎ|わきま|こうどう"},{"japanese":"自分の立場をわきまえて発言しなさい。","romaji":"Jibun no tachiba o wakimaete hatsugen shinasai.","german":"Speak with an awareness of your position.","furigana":"じぶん|たちば|はつげん"},{"japanese":"小さな子供でも、善悪はちゃんとわきまえている。","romaji":"Chiisana kodomo demo, zen'aku wa chanto wakimaete iru.","german":"Even small children know right from wrong.","furigana":"ちい|こども|ぜんあく"}],"notes":"わきまえる (mostly in kana) means to know the difference between right and wrong and to act accordingly: 善悪をわきまえる. Even more common is the meaning \"to know what is proper\": 礼儀 / 立場 / 場所をわきまえる, in modern usage also TPOをわきまえる. The negation 立場をわきまえない is a reproach that someone forgets their place. 区別する, by contrast, is mere distinguishing without a moral undertone.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"痛みを堪えて走り続けた。","romaji":"Itami o koraete hashiri tsuzuketa.","german":"I kept running, bearing the pain.","furigana":"いた|こら|はし|つづ"},{"japanese":"彼女は涙をこらえて、最後まで挨拶を続けた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa namida o koraete, saigo made aisatsu o tsuzuketa.","german":"She held back her tears and continued her speech to the end.","furigana":"かのじょ|なみだ|さいご|あいさつ|つづ"},{"japanese":"会議中に部長の寝癖を見て、笑いをこらえるのに必死だった。","romaji":"Kaigichuu ni buchou no neguse o mite, warai o koraeru no ni hisshi datta.","german":"When I saw the department head's bed hair during the meeting, I desperately tried to hold back my laughter.","furigana":"かいぎちゅう|ぶちょう|ねぐせ|み|わら|ひっし"}],"notes":"こらえる means to deliberately hold back an emotional or physical reaction: 涙をこらえる, 笑いをこらえる, 痛みをこらえる, 怒りをこらえる. Mostly written in kana, because 堪える is also read たえる \"to endure, to withstand\" and こたえる \"to take a toll\". Compared with 我慢する, こらえる is somewhat more literary and stresses suppression in the moment; 耐える describes more the longer endurance of hardship or pressure.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の実力は師匠を凌ぐ。","romaji":"Kare no jitsuryoku wa shishō o shinogu.","german":"His ability surpasses that of his master.","furigana":"かれ|じつりょく|ししょう|しの"},{"japanese":"木陰で暑さを凌いだ。","romaji":"Kokage de atsusa o shinoida.","german":"I escaped the heat in the shade of a tree.","furigana":"こかげ|あつ|しの"},{"japanese":"貯金を取り崩して、なんとか急場を凌いだ。","romaji":"Chokin o torikuzushite, nan to ka kyūba o shinoida.","german":"I dipped into my savings and somehow got through the emergency.","furigana":"ちょきん|と|くず|きゅうば|しの"}],"notes":"凌ぐ has two groups of meaning: \"to surpass\" (師匠を凌ぐ, 前年を凌ぐ売り上げ, 〜を凌ぐ人気) and \"to ward off or get through something unpleasant\": 暑さ / 寒さ / 雨露を凌ぐ, 飢えを凌ぐ, 急場を凌ぐ \"to get through an emergency\". The expression しのぎを削る (\"to fight a hard contest\") does not belong here etymologically (鎬). Often in kana; in everyday speech one says 超える or 耐える.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の夢は潰えた。","romaji":"Kare no yume wa tsuieta.","german":"His dream was shattered.","furigana":"かれ|ゆめ|つい"},{"japanese":"決勝で敗れ、優勝の望みは潰えた。","romaji":"Kesshō de yabure, yūshō no nozomi wa tsuieta.","german":"He lost in the final, and the hope of winning the title was dashed.","furigana":"けっしょう|やぶ|ゆうしょう|のぞ|つい"},{"japanese":"資金不足で、新空港の建設計画は潰えた。","romaji":"Shikin busoku de, shinkūkō no kensetsu keikaku wa tsuieta.","german":"Due to a lack of funds, the plan to build the new airport fell through.","furigana":"しきんぶそく|しんくうこう|けんせつけいかく|つい"}],"notes":"潰える is a literary, intransitive verb: hopes, dreams and plans \"burst, come to nothing\"; originally also an army \"being utterly defeated\". Typical: 夢 / 望み / 野望 / 計画が潰える. The same kanji is used in つぶれる / つぶす (\"to be crushed / to crush\"), which are common in everyday speech. Neutrally one says 駄目になる or 失敗に終わる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"困難に怯まず前に進む。","romaji":"Konnan ni hirumazu mae ni susumu.","german":"I press forward without flinching at difficulties.","furigana":"こんなん|ひる|まえ|すす"},{"japanese":"相手の大きな声に一瞬怯んだが、すぐに言い返した。","romaji":"Aite no ōkina koe ni isshun hirunda ga, sugu ni iikaeshita.","german":"I flinched for a moment at the other person's loud voice, but immediately talked back.","furigana":"あいて|おお|こえ|いっしゅんひる|い|かえ"},{"japanese":"彼女は批判にも怯むことなく、自分の意見を述べた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa hihan ni mo hirumu koto naku, jibun no iken o nobeta.","german":"She stated her opinion without flinching even at criticism.","furigana":"かのじょ|ひはん|ひる|じぶん|いけん|の"}],"notes":"ひるむ (mostly in kana) describes a brief recoil or being intimidated in the face of danger, pressure or a strong opponent; the trigger takes に: 相手の勢いに怯む. Very often negated: 怯まず / 怯むことなく \"undaunted\". Unlike ためらう (\"to hesitate\", out of indecision), ひるむ is a reaction out of fear; 臆する is more elevated and similar.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"何度失敗しても挫けない。","romaji":"Nando shippai shite mo kujikenai.","german":"No matter how many times I fail, I don't lose heart.","furigana":"なんどしっぱい|くじ"},{"japanese":"長いリハビリで、何度も心が挫けそうになった。","romaji":"Nagai rihabiri de, nando mo kokoro ga kujikesō ni natta.","german":"During the long rehabilitation, my spirit nearly gave way many times.","furigana":"なが|なんど|こころ|くじ"},{"japanese":"「挫けるな！」と監督が選手たちに声をかけた。","romaji":"\"Kujikeru na!\" to kantoku ga senshutachi ni koe o kaketa.","german":"\"Don't lose heart!\" the coach called out to the players.","furigana":"くじ|かんとく|せんしゅ|こえ"}],"notes":"くじける (mostly in kana) means to lose courage after setbacks and buckle inwardly; the subject is the person or 心 / 気持ち. Very often negated (挫けない, 挫けずに) or as an exhortation 挫けるな. The transitive counterpart is 挫く: 足首を挫く (\"to sprain one's ankle\"), 出鼻を挫く (\"to stop someone right at the start\"). Unlike 諦める (\"to give up\", a decision), くじける describes a mental collapse.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の顔には自信が漲っている。","romaji":"Kare no kao ni wa jishin ga minagitte iru.","german":"His face brims with confidence.","furigana":"かれ|かお|じしん|みなぎ"},{"japanese":"決勝を前に、会場には緊張感が漲っていた。","romaji":"Kesshō o mae ni, kaijō ni wa kinchōkan ga minagitte ita.","german":"Ahead of the final, tension filled the venue.","furigana":"けっしょう|まえ|かいじょう|きんちょうかん|みなぎ"},{"japanese":"雪解けの季節になると、川に水が漲る。","romaji":"Yukidoke no kisetsu ni naru to, kawa ni mizu ga minagiru.","german":"In the snowmelt season, the river swells with water.","furigana":"ゆきど|きせつ|かわ|みず|みなぎ"}],"notes":"みなぎる (often in kana) means that something fills a space or body to the brim: originally water swelling (川に水が漲る), today mostly strength, energy or mood: 体に力が漲る, 会場に緊張感が漲る. The force is the subject with が, and the place takes に; \"he brims with ...\" is thus 彼には自信が漲っている, not 自信に漲る. Similar: 満ちる, あふれる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"良いアイデアが閃いた。","romaji":"Yoi aidea ga hirameita.","german":"A good idea flashed into my mind.","furigana":"よ|ひらめ"},{"japanese":"散歩中に、問題を解く方法が閃いた。","romaji":"Sanpochū ni, mondai o toku hōhō ga hirameita.","german":"During a walk, a way to solve the problem occurred to me.","furigana":"さんぽちゅう|もんだい|と|ほうほう|ひらめ"},{"japanese":"夜空に稲妻が閃いた。","romaji":"Yozora ni inazuma ga hirameita.","german":"Lightning flashed in the night sky.","furigana":"よぞら|いなずま|ひらめ"}],"notes":"ひらめく (mostly in kana) first means \"to flash briefly, to flare up\" (稲妻が閃く) or \"to flutter\" (旗が閃く); but most often that an idea suddenly comes to one: アイデアが閃く, with the idea as subject. The noun 閃き means \"flash of inspiration\". Unlike 思いつく (\"to come up with\", also quite everyday), 閃く stresses the sudden, inspired quality.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その事件は社会を揺さぶった。","romaji":"Sono jiken wa shakai o yusabutta.","german":"That incident shook society.","furigana":"じけん|しゃかい|ゆ"},{"japanese":"子供が枝を揺さぶると、葉が落ちた。","romaji":"Kodomo ga eda o yusaburu to, ha ga ochita.","german":"When the child shook the branch, leaves fell.","furigana":"こども|えだ|ゆ|は|お"},{"japanese":"彼女の歌声は、聴く人の心を揺さぶった。","romaji":"Kanojo no utagoe wa, kiku hito no kokoro o yusabutta.","german":"Her singing voice moved the hearts of those who listened.","furigana":"かのじょ|うたごえ|き|ひと|こころ|ゆ"}],"notes":"揺さぶる means to shake something vigorously back and forth (木を揺さぶる, 肩を揺さぶって起こす) and, figuratively, to shake people or systems: 心を揺さぶる (\"to move deeply\"), 社会 / 政界を揺さぶる. In sports and negotiations also \"to unsettle the opponent\". 揺する is similar but weaker; 揺らす is gentle rocking; the intransitive is 揺れる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の演説は聴衆の感情を掻き立てた。","romaji":"Kare no enzetsu wa chōshū no kanjō o kakitateta.","german":"His speech stirred up the audience's emotions.","furigana":"かれ|えんぜつ|ちょうしゅう|かんじょう|か|た"},{"japanese":"暖炉の火を掻き立てて、部屋を暖めた。","romaji":"Danro no hi o kakitatete, heya o atatameta.","german":"I stoked the fire in the fireplace to warm the room.","furigana":"だんろ|ひ|か|た|へや|あたた"},{"japanese":"その小説の冒頭は、読者の好奇心を掻き立てる。","romaji":"Sono shōsetsu no bōtō wa, dokusha no kōkishin o kakitateru.","german":"The opening of that novel arouses the readers' curiosity.","furigana":"しょうせつ|ぼうとう|どくしゃ|こうきしん|か|た"}],"notes":"掻き立てる literally means \"to stir up, to poke\" (火を掻き立てる, to stoke a fire), and figuratively to awaken or fuel feelings and imagination: 好奇心 / 想像力 / 不安 / 食欲を掻き立てる. The object takes を; the word is neutral to positive (\"to stimulate, to inspire\"), whereas 煽る (\"to incite, to egg on\") is rather negative. Also written in kana, かきたてる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人の気持ちを蔑ろにしてはいけない。","romaji":"Hito no kimochi o naigashiro ni shite wa ikenai.","german":"You must not disregard other people's feelings.","furigana":"ひと|きも|ないがし"},{"japanese":"仕事ばかりで、家族をないがしろにしてきた。","romaji":"Shigoto bakari de, kazoku o naigashiro ni shite kita.","german":"I've been so caught up in work that I've neglected my family.","furigana":"しごと|かぞく"},{"japanese":"安全対策をないがしろにした結果、大事故が起きた。","romaji":"Anzen taisaku o naigashiro ni shita kekka, daijiko ga okita.","german":"As a result of neglecting safety measures, a major accident occurred.","furigana":"あんぜんたいさく|けっか|だいじこ|お"}],"notes":"ないがしろにする (mostly in kana) means to belittle and neglect something or someone who deserves attention: 家族 / 基本 / 安全 / 人の意見をないがしろにする. The expression is clearly reproachful. 無視する (\"to ignore\") is more direct, 軽視する (\"to underestimate, to belittle\") more literary; おろそかにする stresses negligence in duties.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"相手の立場を慮る。","romaji":"Aite no tachiba o omonpakaru.","german":"I take the other person's position into consideration.","furigana":"あいて|たちば|おもんぱか"},{"japanese":"被災者の気持ちを慮って、祝賀行事は中止された。","romaji":"Hisaisha no kimochi o omonpakatte, shukuga gyōji wa chūshi sareta.","german":"Out of consideration for the feelings of the disaster victims, the celebration was canceled.","furigana":"ひさいしゃ|きも|おもんぱか|しゅくがぎょうじ|ちゅうし"},{"japanese":"将来を慮ると、今のうちに対策を取るべきだ。","romaji":"Shōrai o omonpakaru to, ima no uchi ni taisaku o toru beki da.","german":"Considering the future, we should take measures now.","furigana":"しょうらい|おもんぱか|いま|たいさく|と"}],"notes":"慮る (おもんぱかる, also おもんばかる) means to carefully consider other people's situation or feelings, or future consequences: 相手の立場 / 気持ちを慮る, 将来を慮る. It is an elevated, literary word found mainly in newspapers and speeches; in everyday speech one says 気遣う, 配慮する or 考える. The て-form is common as a reason: 〜を慮って中止する \"to cancel out of consideration for ...\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は機会を覗っている。","romaji":"Kare wa kikai o ukagatte iru.","german":"He is watching for an opportunity.","furigana":"かれ|きかい|うかが"},{"japanese":"ドアの隙間から部屋の様子を覗った。","romaji":"Doa no sukima kara heya no yōsu o ukagatta.","german":"I peeked at the room through a gap in the door.","furigana":"すきま|へや|ようす|うかが"},{"japanese":"子供は親の顔色をうかがいながら、おずおずと話し始めた。","romaji":"Kodomo wa oya no kaoiro o ukagai nagara, ozuozu to hanashihajimeta.","german":"Watching their parents' expressions, the child timidly began to speak.","furigana":"こども|おや|かおいろ|はな|はじ"}],"notes":"うかがう in the spelling 覗う / 窺う (窺う is more usual, often also kana) means \"to peek secretly, to observe\" and \"to lie in wait for an opportunity\": 様子をうかがう, 機会 / チャンスをうかがう, 顔色をうかがう (\"to read someone's expression, to defer to them\"). Do not confuse it with the identically sounding humble 伺う (\"to ask, to visit\"). 覗く (のぞく) has the same kanji and means \"to look in\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"師の指導を仰ぐ。","romaji":"Shi no shidō o aogu.","german":"I ask my teacher for guidance.","furigana":"し|しどう|あお"},{"japanese":"顔を上げて、夜空を仰いだ。","romaji":"Kao o agete, yozora o aoida.","german":"I lifted my face and looked up at the night sky.","furigana":"かお|あ|よぞら|あお"},{"japanese":"彼女は田中先生を生涯の師と仰いでいる。","romaji":"Kanojo wa Tanaka-sensei o shōgai no shi to aoide iru.","german":"She looks up to Professor Tanaka as her teacher for life.","furigana":"かのじょ|たなかせんせい|しょうがい|し|あお"}],"notes":"仰ぐ literally means \"to look upward\" (空 / 天を仰ぐ, often as a gesture of despair or wonder). Figuratively: to revere someone (師と仰ぐ \"to revere as a teacher\") and to ask a higher authority for something: 指導 / 判断 / 指示 / 協力を仰ぐ, typical in business language. Do not confuse it with the homophone 扇ぐ (\"to fan\"). The word is elevated; in everyday speech one says 見上げる or お願いする.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その寺の歴史は千年前に遡る。","romaji":"Sono tera no rekishi wa sennen mae ni sakanoboru.","german":"The history of that temple goes back a thousand years.","furigana":"てら|れきし|せんねんまえ|さかのぼ"},{"japanese":"川を遡って、滝の近くまで歩いた。","romaji":"Kawa o sakanobotte, taki no chikaku made aruita.","german":"I walked upstream along the river to near the waterfall.","furigana":"かわ|さかのぼ|たき|ちか|ある"},{"japanese":"この規則は四月一日に遡って適用される。","romaji":"Kono kisoku wa shigatsu tsuitachi ni sakanobotte tekiyō sareru.","german":"This rule applies retroactively to April 1.","furigana":"きそく|しがつついたち|さかのぼ|てきよう"}],"notes":"さかのぼる (often in kana) means, spatially, \"to go upstream\" (川を遡る, with を for the route) and, temporally, \"to go back, to date back\": 〜に遡る / 〜まで遡る, e.g. その歴史は千年前に遡る. In administration and law, 〜に遡って適用する means \"to apply retroactively\". Although it takes を, it is intransitive; similar is 戻る, which however does not express tracing back.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"手を翳して日差しを遮った。","romaji":"Te o kazashite hizashi o saegitta.","german":"He held up his hand to shield the sunlight.","furigana":"て|かざ|ひざ|さえぎ"},{"japanese":"寒かったので、ストーブに手を翳して温めた。","romaji":"Samukatta node, sutōbu ni te o kazashite atatameta.","german":"Because it was cold, I warmed my hands by holding them over the stove.","furigana":"さむ|て|かざ|あたた"},{"japanese":"改札でICカードをかざして通る。","romaji":"Kaisatsu de aishī kādo o kazashite tōru.","german":"At the ticket gate, you hold your IC card over the reader and pass through.","furigana":"かいさつ|とお"}],"notes":"かざす (almost always in kana) means to hold something up, or to hold it over or in front of something: 手をかざして日差しを遮る (\"to hold a hand protectively over one's eyes\"), 火に手をかざす (\"to warm one's hands at the fire\"). Very everyday today: カードやスマホを読み取り機にかざす. The direction takes に, the held object を. Unlike 持ち上げる (\"to lift\"), it is about holding something out in a direction.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"道を塞いで通れなくした。","romaji":"Michi o fusaide tōrenaku shita.","german":"They blocked the road so that it was impassable.","furigana":"みち|ふさ|とお"},{"japanese":"工事の音が大きくて、思わず耳を塞いだ。","romaji":"Kōji no oto ga ōkikute, omowazu mimi o fusaida.","german":"The construction noise was so loud that I covered my ears without thinking.","furigana":"こうじ|おと|おお|おも|みみ|ふさ"},{"japanese":"雨の日が続くと、気が塞ぐ。","romaji":"Ame no hi ga tsuzuku to, ki ga fusagu.","german":"When rainy days go on, I feel gloomy.","furigana":"あめ|ひ|つづ|き|ふさ"}],"notes":"塞ぐ is transitive and means \"to close off, to block, to cover\": 穴 / 道 / 耳 / 目を塞ぐ. The intransitive counterpart is 塞がる: 道が塞がる, 手が塞がっている (\"to have no hand free\"). There is also the intransitive 気が塞ぐ (\"to feel depressed\"). Unlike 閉める (\"to close\", a door or window), the point is sealing or blocking an opening.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"判決が覆った。","romaji":"Hanketsu ga kutsugaetta.","german":"The ruling was overturned.","furigana":"はんけつ|くつがえ"},{"japanese":"新しい証拠で、これまでの常識が覆った。","romaji":"Atarashii shōko de, kore made no jōshiki ga kutsugaetta.","german":"New evidence overturned the conventional wisdom.","furigana":"あたら|しょうこ|じょうしき|くつがえ"},{"japanese":"突風で小さな船が覆った。","romaji":"Toppū de chiisana fune ga kutsugaetta.","german":"A gust of wind capsized the small boat.","furigana":"とっぷう|ちい|ふね|くつがえ"}],"notes":"覆る is intransitive: something is knocked over or capsizes (船が覆る), but above all verdicts, decisions or previous certainties are overturned: 判決 / 定説 / 常識 / 結果が覆る. The transitive counterpart is 覆す (判決を覆す). Do not confuse it with 覆う (おおう, \"to cover\"), which has the same kanji. In everyday speech, for boats one rather says ひっくり返る or 転覆する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"株で大金を儲けた。","romaji":"Kabu de taikin o mōketa.","german":"He made a lot of money on stocks.","furigana":"かぶ|たいきん|もう"},{"japanese":"楽して儲けようとすると、詐欺に引っかかるよ。","romaji":"Raku shite mōkeyō to suru to, sagi ni hikkakaru yo.","german":"If you try to make money without effort, you'll get caught in a scam.","furigana":"らく|もう|さぎ|ひ"},{"japanese":"二人は結婚して三人の子供を儲けた。","romaji":"Futari wa kekkon shite sannin no kodomo o mōketa.","german":"The two got married and had three children.","furigana":"ふたり|けっこん|さんにん|こども|もう"}],"notes":"儲ける means \"to make a profit, to earn (money)\", usually with an undertone of business or speculation: 株 / 商売で儲ける. For wage labor one more often says 稼ぐ. The intransitive counterpart is 儲かる (\"to pay off, to yield a profit\": この商売は儲かる), and the noun is 儲け (\"profit\"). In elevated usage, 子供を儲ける means \"to have children\". Not to be confused with 設ける (\"to set up, to establish\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"多くの時間を研究に費やした。","romaji":"Ōku no jikan o kenkyū ni tsuiyashita.","german":"I spent a lot of time on research.","furigana":"おお|じかん|けんきゅう|つい"},{"japanese":"休日を丸一日、部屋の片付けに費やした。","romaji":"Kyūjitsu o maru ichinichi, heya no katazuke ni tsuiyashita.","german":"I spent a whole day off tidying my room.","furigana":"きゅうじつ|まるいちにち|へや|かたづ|つい"},{"japanese":"無駄な会議に何時間も費やすのはもったいない。","romaji":"Muda na kaigi ni nanjikan mo tsuiyasu no wa mottainai.","german":"It is a waste to spend hours on pointless meetings.","furigana":"むだ|かいぎ|なんじかん|つい"}],"notes":"費やす means to expend time, money, effort or words on something: AをBに費やす. It is neutral in itself, but in context can also express waste (無駄な会議に時間を費やす). 浪費する, by contrast, explicitly means \"to waste\", and 使う is the everyday \"to use\". Common phrases: 多くの年月を費やす, 言葉を費やす (\"to use many words\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"権力を恣にする。","romaji":"Kenryoku o hoshiimama ni suru.","german":"He wields power at will.","furigana":"けんりょく|ほしいまま"},{"japanese":"彼は若くして天才の名をほしいままにした。","romaji":"Kare wa wakaku shite tensai no na o hoshiimama ni shita.","german":"He enjoyed the fame of a genius from a young age.","furigana":"かれ|わか|てんさい|な"},{"japanese":"展望台からは、富士山の絶景をほしいままにできる。","romaji":"Tenbōdai kara wa, Fujisan no zekkei o hoshiimama ni dekiru.","german":"From the observation deck, you can take in the superb view of Mt. Fuji to your heart's content.","furigana":"てんぼうだい|ふじさん|ぜっけい"}],"notes":"ほしいままにする (mostly in kana, also 擅にする) means to possess or use something without restriction, as one pleases. Negative for power: 権力をほしいままにする (\"to abuse power, to rule at will\"); positive for fame or views: 名声をほしいままにする (\"to enjoy undisputed fame\"), 絶景をほしいままにする. Adverbial: ほしいままに振る舞う \"to do as one pleases\". Literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の表情が翳った。","romaji":"Kare no hyōjō ga kagetta.","german":"His expression clouded over.","furigana":"かれ|ひょうじょう|かげ"},{"japanese":"急に日が翳って、風が冷たくなった。","romaji":"Kyū ni hi ga kagette, kaze ga tsumetaku natta.","german":"The sun suddenly went behind clouds, and the wind turned cold.","furigana":"きゅう|ひ|かげ|かぜ|つめ"},{"japanese":"かつてのスターの人気も、最近は翳ってきた。","romaji":"Katsute no sutā no ninki mo, saikin wa kagette kita.","german":"The popularity of the former star has also been fading lately.","furigana":"にんき|さいきん|かげ"}],"notes":"かげる (also written 陰る, which is more common today) means that light fades: 日が翳る (\"the sun disappears behind clouds\"). Figuratively, a face darkens (表情が翳る) or something loses its luster: 人気 / 景気が翳る. Very common is the noun 翳り / 陰り: 景気に陰りが見える \"the economy is showing signs of clouding over\". Intransitive; similar is 曇る, which concretely means \"to become cloudy\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"病気で窶れた顔をしていた。","romaji":"Byōki de yatsureta kao o shite ita.","german":"He had a face worn thin by illness.","furigana":"びょうき|やつ|かお"},{"japanese":"看病疲れで、母はすっかりやつれてしまった。","romaji":"Kanbyō zukare de, haha wa sukkari yatsurete shimatta.","german":"Exhausted from nursing, my mother has become completely haggard.","furigana":"かんびょうづか|はは"},{"japanese":"久しぶりに会った友人は、少しやつれて見えた。","romaji":"Hisashiburi ni atta yūjin wa, sukoshi yatsurete mieta.","german":"The friend I met after a long time looked a little gaunt.","furigana":"ひさ|あ|ゆうじん|すこ|み"}],"notes":"やつれる (mostly in kana) describes someone who becomes thin and haggard-looking through illness, worry or exhaustion; often 顔がやつれる, やつれた顔, with the cause 〜でやつれる. Unlike 痩せる (\"to lose weight, to become slim\", neutral to positive), やつれる is always a sign of suffering. Similar: 憔悴する (literary), げっそりする (colloquial).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人の感情を弄んではいけない。","romaji":"Hito no kanjō o moteasonde wa ikenai.","german":"You must not play with other people's feelings.","furigana":"ひと|かんじょう|もてあそ"},{"japanese":"彼女は緊張すると、髪の毛を弄ぶ癖がある。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kinchō suru to, kaminoke o moteasobu kuse ga aru.","german":"When she is nervous, she has a habit of fiddling with her hair.","furigana":"かのじょ|きんちょう|かみ|け|もてあそ|くせ"},{"japanese":"彼は運命に弄ばれ、故郷を追われた。","romaji":"Kare wa unmei ni moteasobare, kokyō o owareta.","german":"Toyed with by fate, he was driven from his hometown.","furigana":"かれ|うんめい|もてあそ|こきょう|お"}],"notes":"弄ぶ literally means to play around with something in the hand (髪 / ペンを弄ぶ) and, figuratively, to treat people or feelings ruthlessly: 人の気持ちを弄ぶ. In the passive, 運命に弄ばれる (\"to be buffeted by fate\") is common in narratives. Unlike 遊ぶ (\"to play\", intransitive), 弄ぶ is transitive and mostly pejorative; colloquially one says いじる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"規則から逸脱した行為だ。","romaji":"Kisoku kara itsudatsu shita kōi da.","german":"It is an act that deviates from the rules.","furigana":"きそく|いつだつ|こうい"},{"japanese":"話が本題から逸脱してしまったので、元に戻しましょう。","romaji":"Hanashi ga hondai kara itsudatsu shite shimatta node, moto ni modoshimashō.","german":"The conversation has strayed from the main topic, so let's get back to it.","furigana":"はなし|ほんだい|いつだつ|もと|もど"},{"japanese":"職務の範囲を逸脱した行為で、彼は処分を受けた。","romaji":"Shokumu no han'i o itsudatsu shita kōi de, kare wa shobun o uketa.","german":"He was disciplined for acting beyond the scope of his duties.","furigana":"しょくむ|はんい|いつだつ|こうい|かれ|しょぶん|う"}],"notes":"逸脱する means to deviate from a norm, rule or given framework: 〜から逸脱する (規則 / 本題 / 軌道から) or with を: 権限 / 職務の範囲を逸脱する (\"to exceed one's authority\"). The word is literary and sounds like law, administration or science. In conversation one says 外れる; for topics, colloquially 脱線する (\"to digress\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の言葉に奮い立った。","romaji":"Kare no kotoba ni furuitatta.","german":"His words fired me up.","furigana":"かれ|ことば|ふる|た"},{"japanese":"観客の大きな声援に、選手たちは奮い立った。","romaji":"Kankyaku no ōkina seien ni, senshutachi wa furuitatta.","german":"The players were fired up by the audience's loud cheers.","furigana":"かんきゃく|おお|せいえん|せんしゅ|ふる|た"},{"japanese":"失敗が続いても、自分を奮い立たせて試験勉強を続けた。","romaji":"Shippai ga tsuzuite mo, jibun o furuitatasete shiken benkyō o tsuzuketa.","german":"Even as failures continued, I pulled myself together and kept studying for the exam.","furigana":"しっぱい|つづ|じぶん|ふる|た|しけんべんきょう|つづ"}],"notes":"奮い立つ describes someone inwardly summoning new courage and fighting spirit, \"to feel roused, to catch fire\". The trigger takes に: 言葉に奮い立つ, 声援に奮い立つ. The transitive counterpart is the causative 奮い立たせる, especially 自分を奮い立たせる \"to rouse oneself\". Rather elevated and narrative; in everyday speech one often says やる気が出る or 元気が出る.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"秘密を友人に打ち明けた。","romaji":"Himitsu o yūjin ni uchiaketa.","german":"I confided the secret to a friend.","furigana":"ひみつ|ゆうじん|う|あ"},{"japanese":"一人で悩まず、家族に不安を打ち明けた。","romaji":"Hitori de nayamazu, kazoku ni fuan o uchiaketa.","german":"Instead of worrying alone, I confided my anxieties to my family.","furigana":"ひとり|なや|かぞく|ふあん|う|あ"},{"japanese":"実は会社を辞めようと思っていると、上司に打ち明けた。","romaji":"Jitsu wa kaisha o yameyō to omotte iru to, jōshi ni uchiaketa.","german":"I confided to my boss that, in fact, I was thinking of quitting the company.","furigana":"じつ|かいしゃ|や|おも|じょうし|う|あ"}],"notes":"打ち明ける means to openly tell a trusted person something previously kept secret: 人に秘密・悩み・本心を打ち明ける. The person takes に, the content を or a quotation (〜と打ち明ける). Unlike 告白する (mainly a declaration of love or a confession of guilt) or 明かす (to reveal, also matter-of-fact: 正体を明かす), 打ち明ける stresses personal trust. Related is the noun 打ち明け話 \"confidential disclosure\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"感染の拡大を食い止める。","romaji":"Kansen no kakudai o kuitomeru.","german":"We will stop the spread of the infection.","furigana":"かんせん|かくだい|く|と"},{"japanese":"早い修理で、水漏れの被害を食い止めた。","romaji":"Hayai shūri de, mizumore no higai o kuitometa.","german":"A quick repair limited the damage from the water leak.","furigana":"はや|しゅうり|みずも|ひがい|く|と"},{"japanese":"町は若者の流出を食い止めようと、家賃の補助を始めた。","romaji":"Machi wa wakamono no ryūshutsu o kuitomeyō to, yachin no hojo o hajimeta.","german":"To stem the exodus of young people, the town began subsidizing rent.","furigana":"まち|わかもの|りゅうしゅつ|く|と|やちん|ほじょ|はじ"}],"notes":"食い止める means to halt a negative development that is already under way and spreading: 被害・感染拡大・人口流出・値下がりを食い止める. Often with a limit: 被害を最小限に食い止める \"to keep the damage to a minimum\". 防ぐ is more general and often means preventing before something happens; 食い止める presupposes that the problem has already begun. There is no intransitive partner; potential and passive forms are common (食い止められない).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大きな障害が立ち塞がった。","romaji":"Ōkina shōgai ga tachifusagatta.","german":"A major obstacle stood in the way.","furigana":"おお|しょうがい|た|ふさ"},{"japanese":"男が両手を広げて、出口の前に立ち塞がった。","romaji":"Otoko ga ryōte o hirogete, deguchi no mae ni tachifusagatta.","german":"A man spread his arms and blocked the exit.","furigana":"おとこ|りょうて|ひろ|でぐち|まえ|た|ふさ"},{"japanese":"決勝では、去年の王者が私たちの前に立ち塞がる。","romaji":"Kesshō de wa, kyonen no ōja ga watashitachi no mae ni tachifusagaru.","german":"In the final, last year's champion will stand in our way.","furigana":"けっしょう|きょねん|おうじゃ|わたし|まえ|た|ふさ"}],"notes":"立ち塞がる literally means \"to stand and block the way\". Concretely, a person stands in someone's way (〜の前に立ち塞がる, 行く手に立ち塞がる); figuratively, obstacles, problems or opponents stand in the way (困難が立ち塞がる). The verb is intransitive, and the obstacle takes が. By comparison, 道を塞ぐ \"to block the road\" is transitive. Often also written 立ちふさがる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"将来を見据えた計画を立てる。","romaji":"Shōrai o misueta keikaku o tateru.","german":"I make plans with an eye on the future.","furigana":"しょうらい|みす|けいかく|た"},{"japanese":"相手の目を見据えて、はっきり答えた。","romaji":"Aite no me o misuete, hakkiri kotaeta.","german":"I looked the other person straight in the eye and answered clearly.","furigana":"あいて|め|みす|こた"},{"japanese":"夢を語る前に、まず現実を見据えるべきだ。","romaji":"Yume o kataru mae ni, mazu genjitsu o misueru beki da.","german":"Before talking about dreams, one should first face reality.","furigana":"ゆめ|かた|まえ|げんじつ|みす"}],"notes":"見据える has two uses: literally \"to look at fixedly, steadily\" (相手の目を見据える, 一点を見据える) and figuratively \"to keep something firmly in view and factor it in\" (将来・現実・次の選挙を見据える). Very common as 〜を見据えて \"with a view to ...\" or attributively 将来を見据えた計画. Compared with 見つめる (to gaze for a long time, often tenderly), it seems resolute and sober; the figurative meaning is typical newspaper and business language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"問題を突き詰めて考える。","romaji":"Mondai o tsukitsumete kangaeru.","german":"I think the problem through thoroughly.","furigana":"もんだい|つ|つ|かんが"},{"japanese":"原因を突き詰めると、情報共有の不足に行き着いた。","romaji":"Gen'in o tsukitsumeru to, jōhō kyōyū no fusoku ni ikitsuita.","german":"When we probed the cause to the bottom, we ended up at a lack of information sharing.","furigana":"げんいん|つ|つ|じょうほうきょうゆう|ふそく|い|つ"},{"japanese":"突き詰めて言えば、問題はお金ではなく時間だ。","romaji":"Tsukitsumete ieba, mondai wa okane de wa naku jikan da.","german":"Ultimately, the problem is not money but time.","furigana":"つ|つ|い|もんだい|かね|じかん"}],"notes":"突き詰める means to think through or investigate a matter to the last point: 問題・原因・理論を突き詰める, 突き詰めて考える. Set phrases are 突き詰めれば and 突き詰めて言えば \"in the end, at its core\". 突き止める, by contrast, stresses finding out a concrete result (原因・犯人を突き止める), whereas 突き詰める is more the thorough thought process. For \"to brood obsessively on one thought\", 思い詰める is more usual.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は協力を申し出た。","romaji":"Kare wa kyōryoku o mōshideta.","german":"He offered his cooperation.","furigana":"かれ|きょうりょく|もう|で"},{"japanese":"道に迷った旅行者に、案内を申し出た。","romaji":"Michi ni mayotta ryokōsha ni, annai o mōshideta.","german":"I offered to guide a traveler who had lost his way.","furigana":"みち|まよ|りょこうしゃ|あんない|もう|で"},{"japanese":"落とし物を拾ったら、駅員に申し出てください。","romaji":"Otoshimono o hirottara, ekiin ni mōshidete kudasai.","german":"If you find a lost item, please report it to a station attendant.","furigana":"お|もの|ひろ|えきいん|もう|で"}],"notes":"申し出る means to come forward on one's own with an offer, a request or a report to a person or the responsible office: 協力・寄付・辞退を申し出る, 係員に申し出る. The addressee takes に, the content を or a quotation (〜たいと申し出る). Very common in notices: お気軽にお申し出ください \"please feel free to contact us\". Unlike 申し込む (to sign up, to apply for participation), the focus is on putting forward an intention. Noun: 申し出 \"offer, request\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"議論をまとめて上司に報告する。","romaji":"Giron o matomete jōshi ni hōkoku suru.","german":"I sum up the discussion and report to my boss.","furigana":"ぎろん|じょうし|ほうこく"},{"japanese":"引っ越しの前に、荷物を一つの箱にまとめた。","romaji":"Hikkoshi no mae ni, nimotsu o hitotsu no hako ni matometa.","german":"Before the move, I packed my belongings together into one box.","furigana":"ひ|こ|まえ|にもつ|ひと|はこ"},{"japanese":"部長が両社の話をまとめて、契約が成立した。","romaji":"Buchō ga ryōsha no hanashi o matomete, keiyaku ga seiritsu shita.","german":"The department head brought the talks between the two companies to a conclusion, and the contract was concluded.","furigana":"ぶちょう|りょうしゃ|はなし|けいやく|せいりつ"}],"notes":"まとめる (the kanji 纏める are rare; it is almost always written in kana) means to bring scattered things into a whole: to bundle things (荷物をまとめる), to summarize content (意見・要点をまとめる), to bring negotiations to a conclusion (話・交渉をまとめる) and to hold a group together (チームをまとめる). The intransitive partner is まとまる (意見がまとまる \"people come to an agreement\"). まとめて also means \"all at once, together\" (まとめて払う); the noun まとめ means \"summary\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼がイベントを仕切っている。","romaji":"Kare ga ibento o shikitte iru.","german":"He is running the event.","furigana":"かれ|しき"},{"japanese":"棚で部屋を二つに仕切った。","romaji":"Tana de heya o futatsu ni shikitta.","german":"I divided the room into two with a shelf.","furigana":"たな|へや|ふた|しき"},{"japanese":"彼はすぐに何でも仕切りたがる。","romaji":"Kare wa sugu ni nan demo shikiritagaru.","german":"He immediately wants to take charge of everything.","furigana":"かれ|なん|しき"}],"notes":"仕切る has two main meanings: spatially \"to partition, to divide off\" (部屋をカーテンで仕切る) and \"to run, to organize, to be in charge\" (会議・宴会・店を仕切る). The second is very common in colloquial speech; 仕切りたがる describes someone who always wants to be in charge of everything. More formal and stronger: 取り仕切る \"to manage with full responsibility\". Nouns: 仕切り \"partition\", 仕切り直し \"fresh start\" (originally from sumo).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"台風が大きな被害をもたらした。","romaji":"Taifū ga ōkina higai o motarashita.","german":"The typhoon caused great damage.","furigana":"たいふう|おお|ひがい"},{"japanese":"新しい技術は私たちの生活に大きな変化をもたらした。","romaji":"Atarashii gijutsu wa watashitachi no seikatsu ni ōkina henka o motarashita.","german":"New technology has brought great changes to our lives.","furigana":"あたら|ぎじゅつ|わたし|せいかつ|おお|へんか"},{"japanese":"ポルトガル人が日本に鉄砲をもたらした。","romaji":"Porutogarujin ga Nihon ni teppō o motarashita.","german":"The Portuguese brought firearms to Japan.","furigana":"じん|にほん|てっぽう"}],"notes":"もたらす (the kanji 齎す are hardly used) means \"to bring with it, to bring about\": 利益・変化・被害・幸運をもたらす; the target takes に (社会に影響をもたらす). It also means bringing news, techniques or cultural goods to a place. Literary-neutral and typical of news and factual texts; in everyday speech one rather says 〜につながる or 〜を起こす. Unlike 引き起こす (usually triggering something negative), もたらす can refer to the positive as well as the negative.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は巧みに相手を丸め込んだ。","romaji":"Kare wa takumi ni aite o marumekonda.","german":"He skillfully talked the other person around.","furigana":"かれ|たく|あいて|まる|こ"},{"japanese":"子どもにうまく丸め込まれて、おもちゃを買ってしまった。","romaji":"Kodomo ni umaku marumekomarete, omocha o katte shimatta.","german":"My child cleverly talked me round, and I ended up buying the toy.","furigana":"こ|まる|こ|か"},{"japanese":"店員に丸め込まれないよう、契約書をよく読んだ。","romaji":"Ten'in ni marumekomarenai yō, keiyakusho o yoku yonda.","german":"I read the contract carefully so as not to be talked round by the salesperson.","furigana":"てんいん|まる|こ|けいやくしょ|よ"}],"notes":"丸め込む means to get someone to do something with clever words, often in a slightly unfair way: \"to cajole, to talk round\". Pattern: 人を丸め込む, very often in the passive 〜に丸め込まれる \"to be talked into something\". Colloquial and pejorative; neutral would be 説得する \"to persuade\", and very similar is 言いくるめる (stresses twisting of words). The literal meaning \"to roll up and stuff in\" is rare.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"会議を締め括る挨拶をした。","romaji":"Kaigi o shimekukuru aisatsu o shita.","german":"I gave a short speech to wrap up the meeting.","furigana":"かいぎ|し|くく|あいさつ"},{"japanese":"彼はスピーチを感謝の言葉で締め括った。","romaji":"Kare wa supīchi o kansha no kotoba de shimekukutta.","german":"He closed his speech with words of thanks.","furigana":"かれ|かんしゃ|ことば|し|くく"},{"japanese":"一年を締め括るにふさわしい試合だった。","romaji":"Ichinen o shimekukuru ni fusawashii shiai datta.","german":"It was a game fitting to round off the year.","furigana":"いちねん|し|くく|しあい"}],"notes":"締め括る means to bring something to a proper, well-rounded end: 会議・スピーチ・一年を締め括る; the means takes で (感謝の言葉で締め括る). The noun 締め括り \"conclusion\" is common (締め括りの言葉 \"closing words\"). Unlike 終える (simply to finish), it stresses a summarizing, dignified ending. Very often also written 締めくくる; colloquially it is shortened to 締める (会を締める).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"伝統に根差した文化がある。","romaji":"Dentō ni nezashita bunka ga aru.","german":"There is a culture rooted in tradition.","furigana":"でんとう|ねざ|ぶんか"},{"japanese":"この祭りは地域の暮らしに深く根差している。","romaji":"Kono matsuri wa chiiki no kurashi ni fukaku nezashite iru.","german":"This festival is deeply rooted in the life of the region.","furigana":"まつ|ちいき|く|ふか|ねざ"},{"japanese":"差別は無知に根差していることが多い。","romaji":"Sabetsu wa muchi ni nezashite iru koto ga ōi.","german":"Discrimination is often rooted in ignorance.","furigana":"さべつ|むち|ねざ|おお"}],"notes":"根差す means \"to have one's roots in something, to be based on something\": 〜に根差す, mostly as 根差している or attributively (地域に根差した店 \"a business rooted in the community\"). Typical partners are 文化・伝統・生活・地域・経験, but also negatives like prejudice. Intransitive. 基づく \"to be based on\" (data, rules) is more factual; 根差す stresses grown, deep rootedness. Also written 根ざす.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"法律に則って行動する。","romaji":"Hōritsu ni nottotte kōdō suru.","german":"I act in accordance with the law.","furigana":"ほうりつ|のっと|こうどう"},{"japanese":"試合はルールに則って公正に行われた。","romaji":"Shiai wa rūru ni nottotte kōsei ni okonawareta.","german":"The match was conducted fairly and according to the rules.","furigana":"しあい|のっと|こうせい|おこな"},{"japanese":"式は古い伝統に則った形で行われた。","romaji":"Shiki wa furui dentō ni nottotta katachi de okonawareta.","german":"The ceremony was held in accordance with old tradition.","furigana":"しき|ふる|でんとう|のっと|かたち|おこな"}],"notes":"則る means \"to follow a rule or model\" and appears almost only as 〜に則って \"in accordance with\" or attributively 〜に則った \"in accordance with ...\". Typical partners: 法律・規則・伝統・先例・作法. Very formal; in everyday speech one says 〜に従って, 〜に沿って or 〜に基づいて. Do not confuse it with the identically read 乗っ取る \"to hijack, to take over\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"運命に逆らうことはできない。","romaji":"Unmei ni sakarau koto wa dekinai.","german":"You cannot go against fate.","furigana":"うんめい|さか"},{"japanese":"子どもの頃は、親に逆らってばかりいた。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro wa, oya ni sakaratte bakari ita.","german":"When I was a child, I was always defying my parents.","furigana":"こ|ころ|おや|さか"},{"japanese":"風に逆らって自転車をこぐのは大変だ。","romaji":"Kaze ni sakaratte jitensha o kogu no wa taihen da.","german":"Cycling against the wind is hard.","furigana":"かぜ|さか|じてんしゃ|たいへん"}],"notes":"逆らう means \"to set oneself against something\"; the person, order or current takes に: 親・命令・流れ・時代に逆らう. It also works purely physically: 風・流れに逆らって進む \"to move against the wind, against the current\". Neutral and everyday; the opposite is 従う \"to follow, to obey\". 反抗する is stronger (open rebellion, e.g. by teenagers), 抵抗する emphasizes active resistance against pressure.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は現状に甘んじている。","romaji":"Kare wa genjō ni amanjite iru.","german":"He is resigned to the status quo.","furigana":"かれ|げんじょう|あま"},{"japanese":"決勝で敗れ、準優勝に甘んじた。","romaji":"Kesshō de yabure, jun'yūshō ni amanjita.","german":"He lost in the final and had to settle for second place.","furigana":"けっしょう|やぶ|じゅんゆうしょう|あま"},{"japanese":"彼は批判を甘んじて受けると言った。","romaji":"Kare wa hihan o amanjite ukeru to itta.","german":"He said he would accept the criticism without complaint.","furigana":"かれ|ひはん|あま|う|い"}],"notes":"甘んじる means to settle for something that is usually less than hoped: 〜に甘んじる (現状・二位・低い給料に甘んじる). Set phrase: 〜を甘んじて受ける \"to accept something without complaint\" (批判・罰を甘んじて受ける). Literary; variant 甘んずる. Do not confuse it with 甘える \"to be indulged, to depend on others\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ポケットに手紙を忍ばせた。","romaji":"Poketto ni tegami o shinobaseta.","german":"I tucked the letter secretly into my pocket.","furigana":"てがみ|しの"},{"japanese":"彼は足音を忍ばせて部屋に入った。","romaji":"Kare wa ashioto o shinobasete heya ni haitta.","german":"He entered the room with muffled footsteps.","furigana":"かれ|あしおと|しの|へや|はい"},{"japanese":"彼女はいつもかばんにお守りを忍ばせている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa itsumo kaban ni omamori o shinobasete iru.","german":"She always keeps a charm hidden in her bag.","furigana":"かのじょ|まも|しの"}],"notes":"忍ばせる means to hide something discreetly on one's person: 〜に〜を忍ばせる (ポケットにナイフを忍ばせる). Set phrases are 足音を忍ばせる \"to walk on tiptoe, softly\" and 声を忍ばせる \"to speak in a hushed voice\". It is the causative form of 忍ぶ \"to hide oneself\". Rather literary; in everyday speech one says こっそり入れる or 隠す.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"老いに抗うことはできない。","romaji":"Oi ni aragau koto wa dekinai.","german":"One cannot resist aging.","furigana":"お|あらが"},{"japanese":"主人公は最後まで権力に抗い続けた。","romaji":"Shujinkō wa saigo made kenryoku ni aragai tsuzuketa.","german":"The protagonist kept resisting power until the end.","furigana":"しゅじんこう|さいご|けんりょく|あらが|つづ"},{"japanese":"強い眠気に抗えず、授業中に寝てしまった。","romaji":"Tsuyoi nemuke ni aragaezu, jugyōchū ni nete shimatta.","german":"I couldn't resist the strong drowsiness and fell asleep in class.","furigana":"つよ|ねむけ|あらが|じゅぎょうちゅう|ね"}],"notes":"抗う means \"to brace against an overwhelming force, to resist\": 〜に抗う; typical partners are 運命・時代の流れ・権力・老い・眠気・誘惑. Literary and expressive; in everyday speech one says 逆らう or 抵抗する. Often negated in the potential: 抗えない \"irresistible, impossible to fend off\". Also written in kana, あらがう.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この城にまつわる伝説は数多い。","romaji":"Kono shiro ni matsuwaru densetsu wa kazu ōi.","german":"There are many legends associated with this castle.","furigana":"しろ|でんせつ|かずおお"},{"japanese":"お金にまつわるトラブルは、家族の間でも起こる。","romaji":"Okane ni matsuwaru toraburu wa, kazoku no aida de mo okoru.","german":"Money-related trouble occurs even among family.","furigana":"かね|かぞく|あいだ|お"},{"japanese":"祖父から、戦争にまつわる話を聞いた。","romaji":"Sofu kara, sensō ni matsuwaru hanashi o kiita.","german":"I heard stories about the war from my grandfather.","furigana":"そふ|せんそう|はなし|き"}],"notes":"まつわる (the kanji 纏わる are rare) is used almost only attributively as 〜にまつわる + noun: \"connected with ..., surrounding ...\", e.g. 城にまつわる伝説, お金にまつわる話. Compared with 〜に関する, it is more elevated and has the nuance of stories and memories that gather around something. The concrete meaning \"to wind around something\" is rare; for that one says まとわりつく.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"決断を躊躇ってはいけない。","romaji":"Ketsudan o tameratte wa ikenai.","german":"You must not hesitate over a decision.","furigana":"けつだん|ためら"},{"japanese":"彼は本当のことを言うのをためらっているようだった。","romaji":"Kare wa hontou no koto o iu no o tameratte iru you datta.","german":"He seemed to hesitate to tell the truth.","furigana":"かれ|ほんとう|い"},{"japanese":"困ったときは、ためらわずに相談してください。","romaji":"Komatta toki wa, tamerawazu ni soudan shite kudasai.","german":"If you have a problem, please consult us without hesitation.","furigana":"こま|そうだん"}],"notes":"ためらう means to hesitate over an action out of uncertainty or inhibition, and it is almost always written in kana. Typical are 〜するのをためらう, 返事をためらう, 決断をためらう and, in the negative, ためらわずに \"without hesitating\". The Sino-Japanese word 躊躇(ちゅうちょ)する has the same meaning but is more literary. Compared with 迷う \"to waver, to be unable to decide\", ためらう stresses the inner shrinking back from taking the step.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"感染症が蔓延している。","romaji":"Kansenshō ga man'en shite iru.","german":"The infectious disease is spreading.","furigana":"かんせんしょう|まんえん"},{"japanese":"社会に無力感が蔓延している。","romaji":"Shakai ni muryokukan ga man'en shite iru.","german":"A sense of powerlessness has spread through society.","furigana":"しゃかい|むりょくかん|まんえん"},{"japanese":"不正が蔓延する組織を変えるのは難しい。","romaji":"Fusei ga man'en suru soshiki o kaeru no wa muzukashii.","german":"It is difficult to change an organization in which misconduct is rampant.","furigana":"ふせい|まんえん|そしき|か|むずか"}],"notes":"蔓延する (literally \"to spread like vines\") describes the uncontrolled spread of something harmful: diseases and pests, but also corruption, drugs or moods (不信感が蔓延する). Intransitive: 〜が蔓延する, the space takes に (社会に蔓延する). Exclusively negative; neutral spreading is 広がる or 普及する. In official language and media also written まん延 (まん延防止等重点措置).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不安を払拭するために説明した。","romaji":"Fuan o fusshoku suru tame ni setsumei shita.","german":"I explained in order to dispel the anxiety.","furigana":"ふあん|ふっしょく|せつめい"},{"japanese":"実演を見て、操作が難しいという不安を払拭できた。","romaji":"Jitsuen o mite, sōsa ga muzukashii to iu fuan o fusshoku dekita.","german":"Seeing the demonstration dispelled my worry that it would be hard to operate.","furigana":"じつえん|み|そうさ|むずか|ふあん|ふっしょく"},{"japanese":"新社長は会社の古いイメージを払拭しようとしている。","romaji":"Shin-shachō wa kaisha no furui imēji o fusshoku shiyō to shite iru.","german":"The new president is trying to shake off the company's old image.","furigana":"しんしゃちょう|かいしゃ|ふる|ふっしょく"}],"notes":"払拭する literally means \"to wipe away\" and is used figuratively for completely removing negative notions: 不安・疑念・不信感・悪いイメージを払拭する. Transitive with を; passive 不安が払拭される. Formal, typical of media, politics and business language; in everyday speech one says 不安をなくす or 消す. It is not used for physically wiping off dirt.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"敵の防衛線を粉砕した。","romaji":"Teki no bōeisen o funsai shita.","german":"They smashed the enemy's defensive line.","furigana":"てき|ぼうえいせん|ふんさい"},{"japanese":"工場では機械で石を細かく粉砕している。","romaji":"Kōjō de wa kikai de ishi o komakaku funsai shite iru.","german":"In the factory, stones are crushed fine by machine.","furigana":"こうじょう|きかい|いし|こま|ふんさい"},{"japanese":"日本代表は強豪チームを三対〇で粉砕した。","romaji":"Nihon daihyō wa kyōgō chīmu o san tai zero de funsai shita.","german":"The Japan national team crushed the powerhouse team 3-0.","furigana":"にほんだいひょう|きょうごう||ふんさい"}],"notes":"粉砕する literally means \"to smash into powder\". Technically it stands for grinding and crushing (石・氷・ごみを粉砕する, 粉砕機 \"crusher, mill\"). Figuratively it means to defeat an opponent or resistance utterly (敵・野望・反対運動を粉砕する), forceful and often used in sports and combat rhetoric. Transitive; passive 粉砕される. More everyday is 砕く \"to break, to crush\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"長年の経験が凝縮された作品だ。","romaji":"Naganen no keiken ga gyōshuku sareta sakuhin da.","german":"It is a work in which many years of experience are condensed.","furigana":"ながねん|けいけん|ぎょうしゅく|さくひん"},{"japanese":"短い挨拶に、長年の感謝が凝縮されていた。","romaji":"Mijikai aisatsu ni, naganen no kansha ga gyōshuku sarete ita.","german":"Years of gratitude were condensed into the short greeting.","furigana":"みじか|あいさつ|ながねん|かんしゃ|ぎょうしゅく"},{"japanese":"冷たいグラスの表面で、空気中の水蒸気が凝縮する。","romaji":"Tsumetai gurasu no hyōmen de, kūkichū no suijōki ga gyōshuku suru.","german":"On the surface of a cold glass, water vapor in the air condenses.","furigana":"つめ|ひょうめん|くうきちゅう|すいじょうき|ぎょうしゅく"}],"notes":"凝縮する means \"to condense, to compress\". Physically, gas turns into liquid (水蒸気が凝縮する). Figuratively very often in the passive: experiences, feelings or content are condensed into a small space (〜に〜が凝縮されている). It can be used both transitively (内容を凝縮する) and intransitively. Do not confuse it with 濃縮 \"to concentrate, to thicken\" (濃縮ジュース).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は決断を前に逡巡した。","romaji":"Kare wa ketsudan o mae ni shunjun shita.","german":"He hesitated before making the decision.","furigana":"かれ|けつだん|まえ|しゅんじゅん"},{"japanese":"一瞬逡巡したが、彼女は手を挙げて質問した。","romaji":"Isshun shunjun shita ga, kanojo wa te o agete shitsumon shita.","german":"She hesitated for a moment, but then raised her hand and asked a question.","furigana":"いっしゅんしゅんじゅん|かのじょ|て|あ|しつもん"},{"japanese":"転職すべきか、彼は何か月も逡巡していた。","romaji":"Tenshoku subeki ka, kare wa nankagetsu mo shunjun shite ita.","german":"For months he wavered over whether he should change jobs.","furigana":"てんしょく|かれ|なん|げつ|しゅんじゅん"}],"notes":"逡巡する means \"to hesitate, to waver indecisively\" and is literary to bookish. Mostly without an object: 決断を前に逡巡する, 〜か逡巡する; as a noun in 逡巡の末 \"after long hesitation\". Everyday equivalents are ためらう and 迷う; 躊躇する means the same but is much more common.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"政治家の汚職を糾弾する。","romaji":"Seijika no oshoku o kyūdan suru.","german":"They denounce the politicians' corruption.","furigana":"せいじか|おしょく|きゅうだん"},{"japanese":"市民団体は、企業の差別的な対応を厳しく糾弾した。","romaji":"Shimin dantai wa, kigyō no sabetsuteki na taiō o kibishiku kyūdan shita.","german":"The citizens' group sharply denounced the company's discriminatory treatment.","furigana":"しみんだんたい|きぎょう|さべつてき|たいおう|きび|きゅうだん"},{"japanese":"会議で、彼は責任者として糾弾された。","romaji":"Kaigi de, kare wa sekininsha to shite kyūdan sareta.","german":"At the meeting, he was called to account as the person responsible.","furigana":"かいぎ|かれ|せきにんしゃ|きゅうだん"}],"notes":"糾弾する means to publicly and sharply denounce misconduct or those responsible: 不正・汚職・差別を糾弾する, 責任者を糾弾する. Transitive, often with 厳しく; passive 糾弾される \"to be pilloried\". Stronger and more public than 非難する \"to criticize, to blame\"; 追及する stresses persistently pressing for responsibility. Formal, typical of the press and politics.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"容疑者を拘束した。","romaji":"Yōgisha o kōsoku shita.","german":"The suspect was detained.","furigana":"ようぎしゃ|こうそく"},{"japanese":"この契約は双方を三年間拘束する。","romaji":"Kono keiyaku wa sōhō o sannenkan kōsoku suru.","german":"This contract binds both parties for three years.","furigana":"けいやく|そうほう|さんねんかんこうそく"},{"japanese":"会議が長引いて、一日中拘束された。","romaji":"Kaigi ga nagabiite, ichinichijū kōsoku sareta.","german":"The meeting dragged on, and I was tied up all day.","furigana":"かいぎ|ながび|いちにちじゅうこうそく"}],"notes":"拘束する means to hold someone or restrict their freedom. In police use: 容疑者・身柄を拘束する \"to take into custody\" (the formal arrest is 逮捕する). Figuratively \"to bind, to take up\": contracts, rules or work tie someone down (時間を拘束される; 拘束時間 \"total time on duty\"). Transitive, often in the passive. 束縛する, by contrast, stresses more personal, emotional restriction (恋人を束縛する).","levelNote":"Derived from 拘束 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は助けを懇願した。","romaji":"Kanojo wa tasuke o kongan shita.","german":"She begged for help.","furigana":"かのじょ|たす|こんがん"},{"japanese":"彼は涙ながらに、もう一度チャンスをくださいと懇願した。","romaji":"Kare wa namida nagara ni, mō ichido chansu o kudasai to kongan shita.","german":"With tears in his eyes, he begged for another chance.","furigana":"かれ|なみだ|いちど|こんがん"},{"japanese":"住民たちは市に工事の中止を懇願した。","romaji":"Jūmintachi wa shi ni kōji no chūshi o kongan shita.","german":"The residents implored the city to halt the construction.","furigana":"じゅうみん|し|こうじ|ちゅうし|こんがん"}],"notes":"懇願する means \"to ask earnestly and humbly, to implore\". Pattern: 人に〜を懇願する or with a quotation 〜てくださいと懇願する. Much stronger than 頼む or お願いする. Similar are 嘆願する (a formal request, often written and from groups to authorities) and 哀願する (to plead piteously). Formal, literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この辞書はすべての語彙を網羅している。","romaji":"Kono jisho wa subete no goi o mōra shite iru.","german":"This dictionary covers the entire vocabulary.","furigana":"じしょ|ごい|もうら"},{"japanese":"この案内は、申請に必要な手続きを網羅している。","romaji":"Kono annai wa, shinsei ni hitsuyō na tetsuzuki o mōra shite iru.","german":"This guide covers all the procedures necessary for the application.","furigana":"あんない|しんせい|ひつよう|てつづ|もうら"},{"japanese":"試験範囲を網羅するのは無理なので、重要な所だけ勉強した。","romaji":"Shiken han'i o mōra suru no wa muri na node, jūyō na tokoro dake benkyō shita.","german":"It was impossible to cover the entire exam range, so I studied only the important parts.","furigana":"しけんはんい|もうら|むり|じゅうよう|ところ|べんきょう"}],"notes":"網羅する means \"to cover without gaps, to include completely\": 全分野・情報・必要事項を網羅する; adverbially 網羅的に \"comprehensively, exhaustively\". Typical of reference works, lists, manuals and exam material. Compared with 含む \"to contain\", it stresses completeness; someone who collects only a few examples is 網羅していない. Formal, common in factual and promotional texts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自分の立場を堅持する。","romaji":"Jibun no tachiba o kenji suru.","german":"I hold fast to my position.","furigana":"じぶん|たちば|けんじ"},{"japanese":"政府は従来の方針を堅持すると述べた。","romaji":"Seifu wa jūrai no hōshin o kenji suru to nobeta.","german":"The government stated that it would adhere to its existing policy.","furigana":"せいふ|じゅうらい|ほうしん|けんじ|の"},{"japanese":"創業以来、この店は手作りの伝統を堅持している。","romaji":"Sōgyō irai, kono mise wa tezukuri no dentō o kenji shite iru.","german":"Since its founding, this shop has kept to its tradition of handmade products.","furigana":"そうぎょういらい|みせ|てづく|でんとう|けんじ"}],"notes":"堅持する means \"to hold steadfastly to something\": 方針・原則・立場・伝統を堅持する. Transitive. Very formal, typical of government and corporate statements. 維持する \"to maintain\" is more neutral and also usual for states; 固執する \"to stick stubbornly\" sounds obstinate and rather negative, whereas 堅持 sounds positive and principled.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"問題を傍観してはいけない。","romaji":"Mondai o bōkan shite wa ikenai.","german":"You must not just stand by and watch the problem.","furigana":"もんだい|ぼうかん"},{"japanese":"いじめを傍観していた生徒も責任を感じていた。","romaji":"Ijime o bōkan shite ita seito mo sekinin o kanjite ita.","german":"The students who had looked on at the bullying also felt responsible.","furigana":"ぼうかん|せいと|せきにん|かん"},{"japanese":"彼は議論に加わらず、ただ傍観していた。","romaji":"Kare wa giron ni kuwawarazu, tada bōkan shite ita.","german":"He didn't join the discussion and merely looked on.","furigana":"かれ|ぎろん|くわ|ぼうかん"}],"notes":"傍観する means to merely watch something without intervening, usually with a critical undertone (\"doing nothing\"). Pattern: 〜を傍観する, often ただ傍観する or 傍観している場合ではない \"this is no time to stand by\". Related are 傍観者 \"uninvolved bystander\" and 傍観者効果 \"bystander effect\". Unlike 見物する (to watch out of interest) or 見守る (to watch over benevolently), 傍観 stresses a lack of engagement.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"裏で暗躍する人物がいる。","romaji":"Ura de an'yaku suru jinbutsu ga iru.","german":"There is a person operating behind the scenes.","furigana":"うら|あんやく|じんぶつ"},{"japanese":"事件の背後では、外国のスパイが暗躍していたらしい。","romaji":"Jiken no haigo de wa, gaikoku no supai ga an'yaku shite ita rashii.","german":"Foreign spies seem to have been working in the shadows behind the incident.","furigana":"じけん|はいご|がいこく|あんやく"},{"japanese":"ネット上では、詐欺グループが暗躍している。","romaji":"Netto-jō de wa, sagi gurūpu ga an'yaku shite iru.","german":"On the internet, fraud groups are active behind the scenes.","furigana":"じょう|さぎ|あんやく"}],"notes":"暗躍する means to be active in secret, usually with dishonest aims: \"to pull strings in the background, to work in the shadows\". Intransitive: 〜が暗躍する, often with 裏で, 背後で or 陰で. Typical subjects are スパイ, 黒幕 or 詐欺グループ. Clearly negative and literary (press, crime fiction). The counterpart is 活躍する \"to be successfully active, to distinguish oneself\" (positive and public).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい勢力が台頭してきた。","romaji":"Atarashii seiryoku ga taitō shite kita.","german":"A new power has emerged.","furigana":"あたら|せいりょく|たいとう"},{"japanese":"若手の台頭で、ベテラン選手の出番が減った。","romaji":"Wakate no taitō de, beteran senshu no deban ga hetta.","german":"With the rise of young players, the veterans get fewer appearances.","furigana":"わかて|たいとう|せんしゅ|でばん|へ"},{"japanese":"各地でポピュリズムが台頭している。","romaji":"Kakuchi de popyurizumu ga taitō shite iru.","german":"Populism is on the rise in many places.","furigana":"かくち|たいとう"}],"notes":"台頭する means that a new force gains importance and comes to the fore: 新興国・新勢力・若手・ナショナリズムが台頭する. Intransitive, often as 台頭してくる (increasingly); as a noun very common: 〜の台頭 \"the rise of ...\". Typical of business, political and sports reporting, neutral to slightly cautionary. Similar: 躍進する \"to make great strides\", 浮上する \"to surface, to come into play\". Older spelling: 擡頭.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"帝国の凋落が始まった。","romaji":"Teikoku no chōraku ga hajimatta.","german":"The empire's decline began.","furigana":"ていこく|ちょうらく|はじ"},{"japanese":"かつての名門チームは、ここ数年で大きく凋落した。","romaji":"Katsute no meimon chīmu wa, koko sūnen de ōkiku chōraku shita.","german":"The once-prestigious team has declined greatly in the past few years.","furigana":"めいもん|すうねん|おお|ちょうらく"},{"japanese":"スマホの普及で、その会社は急速に凋落していった。","romaji":"Sumaho no fukyū de, sono kaisha wa kyūsoku ni chōraku shite itta.","german":"With the spread of smartphones, the company declined rapidly.","furigana":"ふきゅう|かいしゃ|きゅうそく|ちょうらく"}],"notes":"凋落する originally means \"to wither\" (leaves, flowers) and today mainly the decline of power, reputation or success: 帝国・名門・企業・人気が凋落する. Intransitive; very common as a noun 〜の凋落 or 凋落ぶり. Very formal and literary. Similar: 没落する (the fall of a family or class), 衰退する (gradual decline, e.g. of an industry or region).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"財政が逼迫している。","romaji":"Zaisei ga hippaku shite iru.","german":"The finances are tight.","furigana":"ざいせい|ひっぱく"},{"japanese":"患者が急増し、病床が逼迫している。","romaji":"Kanja ga kyūzō shi, byōshō ga hippaku shite iru.","german":"The number of patients has risen sharply, and hospital beds are running short.","furigana":"かんじゃ|きゅうぞう|びょうしょう|ひっぱく"},{"japanese":"電力が逼迫するおそれがあるため、節電が呼びかけられた。","romaji":"Denryoku ga hippaku suru osore ga aru tame, setsuden ga yobikakerareta.","german":"Because there was a risk that the power supply would be strained, people were urged to save electricity.","furigana":"でんりょく|ひっぱく|せつでん|よ"}],"notes":"逼迫する describes a tight, strained situation with no room to spare: 財政・家計・医療・電力需給・病床が逼迫する. Intransitive, usually used as 逼迫している; the noun is 〜の逼迫. It is also used for situations that are coming to a head: 事態が逼迫する. Formal and typical of news reporting; often written ひっ迫. Similar is 切迫する (pressing in terms of time, imminent); in everyday speech people more often say 苦しい or 足りない.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"組織の問題点が露呈した。","romaji":"Soshiki no mondaiten ga rotei shita.","german":"The organization's problems came to light.","furigana":"そしき|もんだいてん|ろてい"},{"japanese":"今回の災害で、町の防災体制の弱さが露呈した。","romaji":"Konkai no saigai de, machi no bōsai taisei no yowasa ga rotei shita.","german":"The recent disaster exposed the weakness of the town's disaster-prevention system.","furigana":"こんかい|さいがい|まち|ぼうさいたいせい|よわ|ろてい"},{"japanese":"彼は記者の質問に答えられず、知識不足を露呈した。","romaji":"Kare wa kisha no shitsumon ni kotaerarezu, chishiki busoku o rotei shita.","german":"He could not answer the reporters' questions and so revealed his lack of knowledge.","furigana":"かれ|きしゃ|しつもん|こた|ちしきぶそく|ろてい"}],"notes":"露呈する means that something hidden, usually negative, comes out into the open: 弱点・問題点・矛盾・準備不足が露呈する (intransitive). Used transitively, 〜を露呈する means \"to reveal unintentionally\" (知識不足を露呈する). Unlike 公開する or 明らかにする (to make public deliberately), it happens against one's will. Formal, typical of commentaries and reports; similar is 露見する \"to be found out\" (a lie, a crime).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"計画が頓挫した。","romaji":"Keikaku ga tonza shita.","german":"The plan stalled.","furigana":"けいかく|とんざ"},{"japanese":"交渉は条件が合わず、途中で頓挫した。","romaji":"Kōshō wa jōken ga awazu, tochū de tonza shita.","german":"The negotiations broke down halfway because the terms could not be agreed on.","furigana":"こうしょう|じょうけん|あ|とちゅう|とんざ"},{"japanese":"怪我で、彼の選手生活は一時頓挫した。","romaji":"Kega de, kare no senshu seikatsu wa ichiji tonza shita.","german":"Because of an injury, his athletic career was temporarily brought to a standstill.","furigana":"けが|かれ|せんしゅせいかつ|いちじとんざ"}],"notes":"頓挫する means that an ongoing project suddenly stalls or fails: 計画・交渉・事業・改革が頓挫する. Intransitive; the cause is marked with で (資金不足で頓挫する). It emphasizes a breakdown in the middle of the process (途中で頓挫する). Formal, typical of news. 挫折する, by contrast, refers more to personal failure and giving up a goal.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"政権が瓦解した。","romaji":"Seiken ga gakai shita.","german":"The government collapsed.","furigana":"せいけん|がかい"},{"japanese":"一つのうそから、二人の信頼関係は瓦解した。","romaji":"Hitotsu no uso kara, futari no shinrai kankei wa gakai shita.","german":"Because of a single lie, the trust between the two of them fell apart.","furigana":"ひと|ふたり|しんらいかんけい|がかい"},{"japanese":"主力選手が抜け、チームの守備は瓦解した。","romaji":"Shuryoku senshu ga nuke, chīmu no shubi wa gakai shita.","german":"After the key players left, the team's defense collapsed.","furigana":"しゅりょくせんしゅ|ぬ|しゅび|がかい"}],"notes":"瓦解する (literally \"like roof tiles sliding down\") means that a structure as a whole collapses as soon as one part gives way: 政権・組織・体制・信頼関係が瓦解する. Intransitive; used almost only for abstract structures, not for buildings (for those, use 崩れる or 倒壊する). 崩壊する is similar but more general and can also be used for concrete things. Formal, written language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼のしつこさに辟易した。","romaji":"Kare no shitsukosa ni hekieki shita.","german":"I was fed up with his persistence.","furigana":"かれ|へきえき"},{"japanese":"毎日同じ話を聞かされて、さすがに辟易している。","romaji":"Mainichi onaji hanashi o kikasarete, sasuga ni hekieki shite iru.","german":"Being made to listen to the same story every day, I really am fed up by now.","furigana":"まいにちおな|はなし|き|へきえき"},{"japanese":"観光地の人の多さに辟易して、早めにホテルに戻った。","romaji":"Kankōchi no hito no ōsa ni hekieki shite, hayame ni hoteru ni modotta.","german":"Put off by the crowds at the tourist spot, I went back to the hotel early.","furigana":"かんこうち|ひと|おお|へきえき|はや|もど"}],"notes":"辟易する means to have had enough of something and to shrink back from it inwardly: \"to be annoyed, to be sick of it\". Pattern: 〜に辟易する (しつこさ・長話・暑さに辟易する). Intransitive. A more elevated expression for うんざりする, which is more common in everyday speech; 閉口する is similar (\"not knowing what to say anymore\"). Occasionally written in katakana ヘキエキ for a lighter tone.","levelNote":"Derived from 辟易 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"運命に翻弄された人生だった。","romaji":"Unmei ni honrō sareta jinsei datta.","german":"It was a life tossed about by fate.","furigana":"うんめい|ほんろう|じんせい"},{"japanese":"小さな船は嵐の波に翻弄された。","romaji":"Chiisana fune wa arashi no nami ni honrō sareta.","german":"The little boat was tossed about by the stormy waves.","furigana":"ちい|ふね|あらし|なみ|ほんろう"},{"japanese":"若いボクサーは素早い動きで王者を翻弄した。","romaji":"Wakai bokusā wa subayai ugoki de ōja o honrō shita.","german":"The young boxer made a fool of the champion with his quick movements.","furigana":"わか|すばや|うご|おうじゃ|ほんろう"}],"notes":"翻弄する means to toss someone or something about at will, to play with them. Very common in the passive: 運命・時代・波・噂に翻弄される \"to become the plaything of ...\". Active: 相手を翻弄する \"to make a fool of the opponent, to run rings around them\" (sports, negotiations); the means is marked with で. Transitive, literary and figurative. Similar is もてあそぶ \"to play with something\", often with feelings.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"犯罪が跋扈する地域がある。","romaji":"Hanzai ga bakko suru chiiki ga aru.","german":"There are areas where crime runs rampant.","furigana":"はんざい|ばっこ|ちいき"},{"japanese":"悪質な業者が跋扈して、消費者の被害が増えている。","romaji":"Akushitsu na gyōsha ga bakko shite, shōhisha no higai ga fuete iru.","german":"Unscrupulous companies are running rampant, and harm to consumers is increasing.","furigana":"あくしつ|ぎょうしゃ|ばっこ|しょうひしゃ|ひがい|ふ"},{"japanese":"戦国時代には、各地に盗賊が跋扈していた。","romaji":"Sengoku jidai ni wa, kakuchi ni tōzoku ga bakko shite ita.","german":"In the Sengoku period, bandits ran rampant in every region.","furigana":"せんごくじだい|かくち|とうぞく|ばっこ"}],"notes":"跋扈する means that undesirable people or forces act unchecked and without consideration: \"to run rampant, to take over\". Intransitive, usually 〜が跋扈する; typical subjects are 盗賊・悪徳業者・偽物・犯罪・フェイクニュース. Often used in the intensified form 跳梁跋扈. Very much written language and pejorative. Difference: 蔓延する describes spreading (disease, mood), whereas 跋扈する describes the brazen activity of actors.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自由を渇望する人々がいた。","romaji":"Jiyū o katsubō suru hitobito ga ita.","german":"There were people who longed for freedom.","furigana":"じゆう|かつぼう|ひとびと"},{"japanese":"そのチームは長年、優勝を渇望してきた。","romaji":"Sono chīmu wa naganen, yūshō o katsubō shite kita.","german":"The team has craved a championship for years.","furigana":"ながねん|ゆうしょう|かつぼう"},{"japanese":"砂漠を歩き続け、彼らは一滴の水を渇望した。","romaji":"Sabaku o arukitsuzuke, karera wa itteki no mizu o katsubō shita.","german":"Walking on through the desert, they craved a single drop of water.","furigana":"さばく|ある|つづ|かれ|いってき|みず|かつぼう"}],"notes":"渇望する literally means \"to desire thirstily\" and describes a burning, long-unsatisfied longing: 自由・平和・愛情・勝利を渇望する. Transitive with を; also used as a noun, 〜への渇望. Stronger and more literary than 望む or 欲しがる. Similar are 切望する (to wish urgently, more matter-of-fact and formal) and 熱望する (to wish ardently).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"両者の勢力が拮抗している。","romaji":"Ryōsha no seiryoku ga kikkō shite iru.","german":"The strength of the two sides is evenly matched.","furigana":"りょうしゃ|せいりょく|きっこう"},{"japanese":"試合は最後まで実力が拮抗していた。","romaji":"Shiai wa saigo made jitsuryoku ga kikkō shite ita.","german":"The two sides were evenly matched in ability right to the end of the game.","furigana":"しあい|さいご|じつりょく|きっこう"},{"japanese":"賛成と反対の意見が拮抗し、結論が出なかった。","romaji":"Sansei to hantai no iken ga kikkō shi, ketsuron ga denakatta.","german":"Opinions for and against were evenly balanced, so no conclusion was reached.","furigana":"さんせい|はんたい|いけん|きっこう|けつろん|で"}],"notes":"拮抗する means that two sides are roughly equal in strength and balance each other: 実力・勢力・賛否が拮抗する; with a partner, AとBが拮抗する or AがBと拮抗する. Intransitive. Typical of sports, elections and polls (拮抗した試合 \"a close, balanced match\"); in medicine, 拮抗薬 \"antagonist\". Formal; in everyday speech people more often say 互角 or いい勝負.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"市場を壟断する企業がある。","romaji":"Shijō o rōdan suru kigyō ga aru.","german":"There are companies that monopolize the market.","furigana":"しじょう|ろうだん|きぎょう"},{"japanese":"一部の貴族が政治を壟断していた。","romaji":"Ichibu no kizoku ga seiji o rōdan shite ita.","german":"A small group of nobles had monopolized politics.","furigana":"いちぶ|きぞく|せいじ|ろうだん"},{"japanese":"情報を壟断する者が権力を握る。","romaji":"Jōhō o rōdan suru mono ga kenryoku o nigiru.","german":"Whoever monopolizes information holds power.","furigana":"じょうほう|ろうだん|もの|けんりょく|にぎ"}],"notes":"壟断する goes back to an anecdote in Mencius: a merchant climbed a mound (壟断), surveyed the market and raked in all the profits. Hence \"to seize profits or power for oneself, to monopolize\": 利益・市場・政権を壟断する. Transitive and pejorative. Very rare and elevated written language; today people almost always say 独占する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の言葉は何かを暗示している。","romaji":"Kare no kotoba wa nanika o anji shite iru.","german":"His words hint at something.","furigana":"かれ|ことば|なに|あんじ"},{"japanese":"映画の最初の場面が、結末を暗示している。","romaji":"Eiga no saisho no bamen ga, ketsumatsu o anji shite iru.","german":"The opening scene of the film already hints at the ending.","furigana":"えいが|さいしょ|ばめん|けつまつ|あんじ"},{"japanese":"自分は大丈夫だと、自分に暗示をかけた。","romaji":"Jibun wa daijōbu da to, jibun ni anji o kaketa.","german":"I told myself that I would be all right.","furigana":"じぶん|だいじょうぶ|じぶん|あんじ"}],"notes":"暗示する means \"to hint indirectly, to point to something\": 結末・危険・将来を暗示する; things are often the subject (タイトルが内容を暗示している). The opposite is 明示する \"to state explicitly\". 示唆する is similar but more matter-of-fact (data suggest something), whereas 暗示 is more figurative and literary. In psychology: 暗示をかける \"to suggest, to talk into\", 自己暗示 \"autosuggestion\".","levelNote":"Derived from 暗示 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"調査結果は問題の存在を示唆している。","romaji":"Chōsa kekka wa mondai no sonzai o shisa shite iru.","german":"The survey results suggest that a problem exists.","furigana":"ちょうさけっか|もんだい|そんざい|しさ"},{"japanese":"売り上げの変化は、客の好みの変化を示唆している。","romaji":"Uriage no henka wa, kyaku no konomi no henka o shisa shite iru.","german":"The change in sales indicates a change in customers' tastes.","furigana":"う|あ|へんか|きゃく|この|へんか|しさ"},{"japanese":"先生は、別の解き方があることを示唆してくれた。","romaji":"Sensei wa, betsu no tokikata ga aru koto o shisa shite kureta.","german":"The teacher hinted that there is another way to solve it.","furigana":"せんせい|べつ|と|かた|しさ"}],"notes":"示唆する means \"to point to, to suggest, to hint\"; the subject is often data, results or experts: 可能性・存在・必要性を示唆する. It is a pointer, not proof; 証明する would be \"to prove\". Set phrase: 示唆に富む \"insightful, thought-provoking\". More matter-of-fact than 暗示する and typical of science, reports and news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の提案は黙殺された。","romaji":"Kare no teian wa mokusatsu sareta.","german":"His proposal was ignored.","furigana":"かれ|ていあん|もくさつ"},{"japanese":"会社は社員の訴えを黙殺し続けた。","romaji":"Kaisha wa shain no uttae o mokusatsu shi tsuzuketa.","german":"The company kept ignoring the employees' appeals.","furigana":"かいしゃ|しゃいん|うった|もくさつ|つづ"},{"japanese":"大臣は記者の質問を黙殺して、会場を後にした。","romaji":"Daijin wa kisha no shitsumon o mokusatsu shite, kaijō o ato ni shita.","german":"The minister ignored the reporters' questions and left the hall.","furigana":"だいじん|きしゃ|しつもん|もくさつ|かいじょう|あと"}],"notes":"黙殺する means to deliberately say nothing and not react to something: 意見・提案・抗議・批判を黙殺する; very often in the passive, 黙殺される. Stronger than 無視する: it emphasizes deliberate disregard, often by an authority or institution. Formal, typical of politics and the press; historically known through the Japanese government's reaction to the Potsdam Declaration in 1945.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"相手の要求を一蹴した。","romaji":"Aite no yōkyū o isshū shita.","german":"He rejected the other side's demand out of hand.","furigana":"あいて|ようきゅう|いっしゅう"},{"japanese":"社長は私の企画を非現実的だと一蹴した。","romaji":"Shachō wa watashi no kikaku o higenjitsuteki da to isshū shita.","german":"The president dismissed my plan as unrealistic.","furigana":"しゃちょう|わたし|きかく|ひげんじつてき|いっしゅう"},{"japanese":"チャンピオンは挑戦者をあっさり一蹴した。","romaji":"Chanpion wa chōsensha o assari isshū shita.","german":"The champion easily brushed aside the challenger.","furigana":"ちょうせんしゃ|いっしゅう"}],"notes":"一蹴する literally means \"to kick away with one kick\". Main meaning: to dismiss proposals, demands or criticism out of hand (要求・提案・批判を一蹴する), often with the reason as a quotation (〜と一蹴する). In sports also \"to beat an opponent with ease\". Transitive. Blunter and more dismissive than 断る or 拒否する; journalistic and formal. Do not confuse with 一周 \"one lap\" (same reading).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"男は大声で店員を恫喝した。","romaji":"Otoko wa ōgoe de ten'in o dōkatsu shita.","german":"The man bullied the shop clerk by shouting at her.","furigana":"おとこ|おおごえ|てんいん|どうかつ"},{"japanese":"上司が部下を恫喝するのはパワハラだ。","romaji":"Jōshi ga buka o dōkatsu suru no wa pawahara da.","german":"It is power harassment when a boss yells at and threatens his subordinates.","furigana":"じょうし|ぶか|どうかつ"},{"japanese":"大国が軍事力で小国を恫喝している。","romaji":"Taikoku ga gunjiryoku de shōkoku o dōkatsu shite iru.","german":"A major power is intimidating a small country with its military might.","furigana":"たいこく|ぐんじりょく|しょうこく|どうかつ"}],"notes":"恫喝する means to intimidate someone with loud threats or a show of power: 部下・店員・相手国を恫喝する; the means is marked with で (大声で, 武力で). Transitive. Unlike 脅迫する (legal: threatening a concrete harm, blackmail), 恫喝 emphasizes shouting at someone and pressure from a position of superiority. Written language; in everyday speech people say 脅す.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"異端者を排斥する風潮がある。","romaji":"Itansha o haiseki suru fūchō ga aru.","german":"There is a tendency to exclude heretics.","furigana":"いたんしゃ|はいせき|ふうちょう"},{"japanese":"外国製品を排斥する運動が起きた。","romaji":"Gaikoku seihin o haiseki suru undō ga okita.","german":"A movement to boycott foreign products arose.","furigana":"がいこくせいひん|はいせき|うんどう|お"},{"japanese":"彼は派閥争いに敗れ、党から排斥された。","romaji":"Kare wa habatsu arasoi ni yabure, tō kara haiseki sareta.","german":"He lost the factional struggle and was pushed out of the party.","furigana":"かれ|はばつあらそ|やぶ|とう|はいせき"}],"notes":"排斥する means to deliberately shut out and keep away people, groups or things: 移民・異分子・外国製品を排斥する; the origin is marked with から (党から排斥される). Transitive, often in the passive. Similar are 排除する (more matter-of-fact \"to remove, to exclude\") and 追放する (\"to banish, to expel\"). Typical of political and historical contexts, e.g. 排斥運動 \"boycott or exclusion movement\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"時代とともに価値観は変遷する。","romaji":"Jidai to tomo ni kachikan wa hensen suru.","german":"Values change with the times.","furigana":"じだい|かちかん|へんせん"},{"japanese":"日本語の敬語は時代によって大きく変遷してきた。","romaji":"Nihongo no keigo wa jidai ni yotte ōkiku hensen shite kita.","german":"Japanese honorific language has changed greatly from era to era.","furigana":"にほんご|けいご|じだい|おお|へんせん"},{"japanese":"この展示では、町の姿の変遷をたどることができる。","romaji":"Kono tenji de wa, machi no sugata no hensen o tadoru koto ga dekiru.","german":"In this exhibition you can trace how the town's appearance changed over time.","furigana":"てんじ|まち|すがた|へんせん"}],"notes":"変遷 refers to gradual change over a long period, usually of eras, systems, language or values. It is written language and often appears as a noun: 〜の変遷をたどる \"to trace the development of ...\"; as an intransitive する-verb it is used as 時代とともに変遷する. Unlike 変化 \"change\" (neutral, also short-term), 変遷 emphasizes a succession of stages in history. In everyday speech people more often say 移り変わる.","levelNote":"Derived from 変遷 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"議論が紛糾した。","romaji":"Giron ga funkyū shita.","german":"The discussion got completely out of hand.","furigana":"ぎろん|ふんきゅう"},{"japanese":"予算案をめぐって会議が紛糾し、結論は次回に持ち越された。","romaji":"Yosan'an o megutte kaigi ga funkyū shi, ketsuron wa jikai ni mochikosareta.","german":"The meeting over the budget proposal descended into wrangling, and the conclusion was postponed to the next time.","furigana":"よさんあん|かいぎ|ふんきゅう|けつろん|じかい|も|こ"},{"japanese":"住民説明会は工事の騒音問題で紛糾した。","romaji":"Jūmin setsumeikai wa kōji no sōon mondai de funkyū shita.","german":"The residents' briefing became heated over the problem of construction noise.","furigana":"じゅうみんせつめいかい|こうじ|そうおんもんだい|ふんきゅう"}],"notes":"紛糾 describes a meeting, debate or negotiation getting out of control and bogged down through quarrels and conflicting positions. Typical are 会議が紛糾する, 議論が紛糾する, 審議が紛糾した; intransitive with が, the point of dispute is marked with をめぐって or で. Unlike 混乱 \"confusion\" (general, also for traffic or systems), 紛糾 is always about a dispute of opinion. Written language, frequent in news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"利害関係が錯綜している。","romaji":"Rigai kankei ga sakusō shite iru.","german":"Interests are intricately intertwined.","furigana":"りがいかんけい|さくそう"},{"japanese":"事故直後は情報が錯綜していた。","romaji":"Jiko chokugo wa jōhō ga sakusō shite ita.","german":"Right after the accident, information was confused and conflicting.","furigana":"じこちょくご|じょうほう|さくそう"},{"japanese":"この小説は複数の時間軸が錯綜していて、少し読みにくい。","romaji":"Kono shōsetsu wa fukusū no jikanjiku ga sakusō shite ite, sukoshi yominikui.","german":"This novel interweaves several timelines, so it is a little hard to read.","furigana":"しょうせつ|ふくすう|じかんじく|さくそう|すこ|よ"}],"notes":"錯綜 means that many threads, such as information, interests and relationships, run into one another and can hardly be untangled. Almost always in the state 錯綜している or attributively: 錯綜した情報 \"conflicting, jumbled reports\"; typical after accidents and disasters is 情報が錯綜する. 複雑 is the general \"complicated\"; 錯綜 emphasizes the tangle of many crossing elements. Written language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"交渉は三か月前から膠着している。","romaji":"Kōshō wa sankagetsu mae kara kōchaku shite iru.","german":"The negotiations have been deadlocked for three months.","furigana":"こうしょう|さん|げつまえ|こうちゃく"},{"japanese":"戦線は膠着し、どちらの軍も前進できなかった。","romaji":"Sensen wa kōchaku shi, dochira no gun mo zenshin dekinakatta.","german":"The front line stalled, and neither army could advance.","furigana":"せんせん|こうちゃく|ぐん|ぜんしん"},{"japanese":"試合は後半に入っても膠着したままだった。","romaji":"Shiai wa kōhan ni haitte mo kōchaku shita mama datta.","german":"Even after the second half began, the game remained deadlocked.","furigana":"しあい|こうはん|はい|こうちゃく"}],"notes":"膠着する means that a situation is stuck and no longer moving: in negotiations, war fronts, scores or debates. 膠 means \"glue\", so literally \"to stick fast\"; this basic meaning now appears only in technical texts, as does 膠着語 \"agglutinative language\". Intransitive, mostly in the form 膠着している; 膠着状態に陥る \"to fall into a stalemate\" is also very common. News-language, formal; in conversation people more often say 行き詰まる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"公私を峻別する必要がある。","romaji":"Kōshi o shunbetsu suru hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"It is necessary to draw a strict line between public and private.","furigana":"こうし|しゅんべつ|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"報道では事実と意見を峻別しなければならない。","romaji":"Hōdō de wa jijitsu to iken o shunbetsu shinakereba naranai.","german":"In reporting, facts and opinions must be strictly separated.","furigana":"ほうどう|じじつ|いけん|しゅんべつ"},{"japanese":"裁判所は法的責任と道義的責任を峻別した。","romaji":"Saibansho wa hōteki sekinin to dōgiteki sekinin o shunbetsu shita.","german":"The court strictly distinguished between legal responsibility and moral responsibility.","furigana":"さいばんしょ|ほうてきせきにん|どうぎてきせきにん|しゅんべつ"}],"notes":"峻別 means to keep two things sharply and uncompromisingly apart. Typical patterns are AとBを峻別する and the fixed combination 公私を峻別する \"to strictly separate work and private life\". Written language, frequent in legal, scholarly and ethical texts. More neutral are 区別する \"to distinguish\" and 分ける; 峻別 emphasizes that no mixing whatsoever is tolerated.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"弱い者を恤む心が大切だ。","romaji":"Yowai mono o awaremu kokoro ga taisetsu da.","german":"A heart that feels compassion for the weak is important.","furigana":"よわ|もの|あわれ|こころ|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"王は飢えに苦しむ民を恤み、倉の米を配った。","romaji":"Ō wa ue ni kurushimu tami o awaremi, kura no kome o kubatta.","german":"The king took pity on the people suffering from hunger and distributed the rice in the storehouses.","furigana":"おう|う|くる|たみ|あわれ|くら|こめ|くば"},{"japanese":"人に恤まれることを、彼の誇りが許さなかった。","romaji":"Hito ni awaremareru koto o, kare no hokori ga yurusanakatta.","german":"His pride would not allow him to be pitied by others.","furigana":"ひと|あわれ|かれ|ほこ|ゆる"}],"notes":"恤む (あわれむ) means \"to pity, to have mercy\", usually toward the weak or those in need: 弱者を恤む. The spelling with 恤 (cf. 救恤 \"relief for the needy\") is very rare and outside the Jōyō list; 哀れむ, 憐れむ or kana are usual. Transitive godan verb with を; the passive 恤まれる \"to be pitied\" is often felt to be insulting. Written language and somewhat old-fashioned; in everyday speech people say 同情する or かわいそうに思う.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自分の力を誇示する。","romaji":"Jibun no chikara o koji suru.","german":"He flaunts his own power.","furigana":"じぶん|ちから|こじ"},{"japanese":"その国は大規模な軍事パレードで軍事力を誇示した。","romaji":"Sono kuni wa daikibo na gunji parēdo de gunjiryoku o koji shita.","german":"The country displayed its military strength with a large military parade.","furigana":"くに|だいきぼ|ぐんじ|ぐんじりょく|こじ"},{"japanese":"彼は高級時計を誇示するように腕をまくった。","romaji":"Kare wa kōkyū tokei o koji suru yō ni ude o makutta.","german":"He rolled up his sleeve as if to show off his expensive watch.","furigana":"かれ|こうきゅうとけい|こじ|うで"}],"notes":"誇示 means to demonstratively show off something, such as power, wealth or skill, so that others see it. Typical are 力を誇示する, 軍事力を誇示する, 存在感を誇示する. The word is written language and usually sounds critical. Unlike 自慢する \"to boast, to brag\" (mainly in words, everyday), 誇示 emphasizes display through deeds or objects.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の嘘を看破した。","romaji":"Kare no uso o kanpa shita.","german":"He saw through his lie.","furigana":"かれ|うそ|かんぱ"},{"japanese":"名探偵は犯人のトリックを一目で看破した。","romaji":"Meitantei wa hannin no torikku o hitome de kanpa shita.","german":"The master detective saw through the culprit's trick at a glance.","furigana":"めいたんてい|はんにん|ひとめ|かんぱ"},{"japanese":"彼女は相手の本当の狙いを看破していた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa aite no hontō no nerai o kanpa shite ita.","german":"She had seen through the other party's true aim.","furigana":"かのじょ|あいて|ほんとう|ねら|かんぱ"}],"notes":"看破 means to see through a hidden truth, such as a lie, a trick or an intention, with a sharp eye. Typical objects are 嘘を看破する, 正体を看破する, 意図を看破する, トリックを看破する. Written language and somewhat literary; in everyday speech people say 見破る or 見抜く. 看破 emphasizes the mental sharpness of the one who sees through.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"注意を喚起する必要がある。","romaji":"Chūi o kanki suru hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"It is necessary to call people's attention to it.","furigana":"ちゅうい|かんき|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"気象庁は大雨への警戒を喚起した。","romaji":"Kishōchō wa ōame e no keikai o kanki shita.","german":"The Meteorological Agency called for vigilance against heavy rain.","furigana":"きしょうちょう|おおあめ|けいかい|かんき"},{"japanese":"その写真は幼い頃の記憶を喚起した。","romaji":"Sono shashin wa osanai koro no kioku o kanki shita.","german":"That photo evoked memories of my childhood.","furigana":"しゃしん|おさな|ころ|きおく|かんき"}],"notes":"喚起 means to call something up in others: attention, interest, memories, public opinion. Fixed phrases are 注意を喚起する \"to warn, to urge caution\" (as a noun 注意喚起 \"warning notice\"), 関心を喚起する, 世論を喚起する, 記憶を喚起する. Written language, typical of government agencies and news. Beware when listening: homophone 換気 \"ventilation\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"実権を掌握した。","romaji":"Jikken o shōaku shita.","german":"He seized real power.","furigana":"じっけん|しょうあく"},{"japanese":"新しい部長はすぐに部下を掌握した。","romaji":"Atarashii buchō wa sugu ni buka o shōaku shita.","german":"The new department head quickly got his subordinates under control.","furigana":"あたら|ぶちょう|ぶか|しょうあく"},{"japanese":"軍が首都を掌握したと報じられた。","romaji":"Gun ga shuto o shōaku shita to hōjirareta.","german":"It was reported that the military had taken control of the capital.","furigana":"ぐん|しゅと|しょうあく|ほう"}],"notes":"掌握 literally means \"to hold in the palm of the hand\": to have power, an organization or a situation firmly in one's grip. Typical are 実権を掌握する, 政権を掌握する, 部下を掌握する \"to have one's people under control\" and 状況を掌握する \"to have the situation under control\". Written language; more everyday is 握る (権力を握る). Do not confuse with 把握, which only means \"to grasp, to understand\", not to control.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は批判を受けても恬としている。","romaji":"Kare wa hihan o ukete mo ten to shite iru.","german":"Even when criticized, he remains completely unmoved.","furigana":"かれ|ひはん|う|てん"},{"japanese":"彼は約束を破っても恬として恥じない。","romaji":"Kare wa yakusoku o yabutte mo ten to shite hajinai.","german":"Even when he breaks a promise, he feels not the slightest shame.","furigana":"かれ|やくそく|やぶ|てん|は"},{"japanese":"公金を私的に使いながら恬として恥じない政治家もいる。","romaji":"Kōkin o shiteki ni tsukainagara ten to shite hajinai seijika mo iru.","german":"Some politicians use public funds privately and feel not the slightest shame about it.","furigana":"こうきん|してき|つか|てん|は|せいじか"}],"notes":"恬として describes someone remaining completely unmoved where one would actually expect shame or pangs of conscience. It almost always appears in the phrase 恬として恥じない \"not to be in the least ashamed, to be shameless\", less often 恬として顧みない. It is written language and always sounds critical; for positive composure one says 平然と or 落ち着いて. The rare character 恬 belongs purely to reading vocabulary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は出世に躍起になっている。","romaji":"Kare wa shusse ni yakki ni natte iru.","german":"He is desperately intent on getting ahead.","furigana":"かれ|しゅっせ|やっき"},{"japanese":"政府は円安を止めようと躍起になっている。","romaji":"Seifu wa en'yasu o tomeyō to yakki ni natte iru.","german":"The government is desperately trying to stop the fall of the yen.","furigana":"せいふ|えんやす|と|やっき"},{"japanese":"彼はうわさを躍起になって否定した。","romaji":"Kare wa uwasa o yakki ni natte hitei shita.","german":"He vehemently denied the rumor.","furigana":"かれ|やっき|ひてい"}],"notes":"躍起になる means to go all out for something feverishly and doggedly, often under pressure or out of annoyance. Typical patterns are 〜に躍起になる, 〜しようと躍起になる and adverbial 躍起になって否定する \"to deny vehemently\". Usually slightly critical: the zeal seems exaggerated or desperate. More neutral is 熱心に; similar, but more petulant and childish, is むきになる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"組織を刷新する必要がある。","romaji":"Soshiki o sasshin suru hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"It is necessary to overhaul the organization.","furigana":"そしき|さっしん|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"新社長は経営陣を大幅に刷新した。","romaji":"Shinshachō wa keieijin o ōhaba ni sasshin shita.","german":"The new president largely replaced the management team.","furigana":"しんしゃちょう|けいえいじん|おおはば|さっしん"},{"japanese":"アプリのデザインが刷新されて、使いやすくなった。","romaji":"Apuri no dezain ga sasshin sarete, tsukaiyasuku natta.","german":"The app's design was renewed and has become easier to use.","furigana":"さっしん|つか"}],"notes":"刷新 means to fundamentally renew a system, an organization or an appearance and to get rid of what has become stale. Typical objects are 組織を刷新する, 人事を刷新する, イメージを刷新する; as a noun 政治の刷新. Written language, frequent in business and politics. Unlike 改善 \"to improve\" (gradual) or 更新 \"to update, to renew\", 刷新 stands for a clear fresh start; a close synonym is 一新.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不安な空気が社会に瀰漫している。","romaji":"Fuan na kūki ga shakai ni biman shite iru.","german":"An atmosphere of unease pervades society.","furigana":"ふあん|くうき|しゃかい|びまん"},{"japanese":"戦後、国民の間に虚無感が瀰漫していた。","romaji":"Sengo, kokumin no aida ni kyomukan ga biman shite ita.","german":"After the war, a sense of emptiness had spread among the people.","furigana":"せんご|こくみん|あいだ|きょむかん|びまん"},{"japanese":"組織全体に事なかれ主義が瀰漫している。","romaji":"Soshiki zentai ni kotonakare shugi ga biman shite iru.","german":"A policy of avoiding any trouble has spread throughout the whole organization.","furigana":"そしきぜんたい|こと|しゅぎ|びまん"}],"notes":"瀰漫 means that a mood or attitude spreads everywhere and permeates everything, almost always something negative such as unease, distrust or resignation. Pattern: 〜が社会に瀰漫する, mostly in the state 瀰漫している. Very much written and literary language; JMdict also lists the spelling 弥漫. In everyday speech people say 広がる, and for diseases and evils 蔓延する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"青春を謳歌する。","romaji":"Seishun o ōka suru.","german":"Enjoy your youth to the fullest.","furigana":"せいしゅん|おうか"},{"japanese":"戦争が終わり、人々は自由を謳歌した。","romaji":"Sensō ga owari, hitobito wa jiyū o ōka shita.","german":"The war was over, and people reveled in their freedom.","furigana":"せんそう|お|ひとびと|じゆう|おうか"},{"japanese":"定年後、父は趣味三昧の生活を謳歌している。","romaji":"Teinen-go, chichi wa shumi-zanmai no seikatsu o ōka shite iru.","german":"Since retiring, my father has been enjoying a life devoted to his hobbies.","furigana":"ていねんご|ちち|しゅみざんまい|せいかつ|おうか"}],"notes":"謳歌 literally means \"to sing praises\": to enjoy something beautiful to the fullest and, as it were, celebrate it. Typical objects are 青春を謳歌する, 自由を謳歌する, 人生を謳歌する, 平和を謳歌する. Elevated, but also common in newspapers and advertising. Unlike 楽しむ \"to enjoy\" (everyday, also for small things), 謳歌 stands for a comprehensive, happy state of life; 享受, by contrast, soberly emphasizes receiving benefits.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大衆に迎合する政治家が多い。","romaji":"Taishū ni geigō suru seijika ga ōi.","german":"Many politicians pander to the masses.","furigana":"たいしゅう|げいごう|せいじか|おお"},{"japanese":"彼は上司に迎合してばかりで、自分の意見を言わない。","romaji":"Kare wa jōshi ni geigō shite bakari de, jibun no iken o iwanai.","german":"He just keeps currying favor with his boss and never states his own opinion.","furigana":"かれ|じょうし|げいごう|じぶん|いけん|い"},{"japanese":"流行に迎合しない作品を作りたい。","romaji":"Ryūkō ni geigō shinai sakuhin o tsukuritai.","german":"I want to make works that do not pander to trends.","furigana":"りゅうこう|げいごう|さくひん|つく"}],"notes":"迎合 means to adapt to the opinions or tastes of others in order to please them, and is clearly pejorative. The target is marked with に: 大衆に迎合する, 世論に迎合する, 権力に迎合する, 時流に迎合する. Written language. Related are こびる \"to ingratiate oneself\" (more personal) and ご機嫌を取る; 迎合 emphasizes giving up one's own position.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"相手の気持ちを汲み取る。","romaji":"Aite no kimochi o kumitoru.","german":"To sense the other person's feelings.","furigana":"あいて|きも|く|と"},{"japanese":"言葉にならない気持ちを、表情から汲み取った。","romaji":"Kotoba ni naranai kimochi o, hyōjō kara kumitotta.","german":"I picked up on her unspoken feelings from her facial expression.","furigana":"ことば|きも|ひょうじょう|く|と"},{"japanese":"井戸から水を汲み取って、畑にまいた。","romaji":"Ido kara mizu o kumitotte, hatake ni maita.","german":"I scooped water out of the well and sprinkled it on the field.","furigana":"いど|みず|く|と|はたけ"}],"notes":"汲み取る has two meanings: literally \"to scoop or pump out a liquid\" (水を汲み取る; hence 汲み取り式トイレ \"pit toilet\") and figuratively \"to sensitively pick up feelings or intentions\": 気持ちを汲み取る, 意図を汲み取る, 真意を汲み取る. The figurative meaning is the more common one today and suits a style of communication in which much remains unspoken. Close are 察する \"to sense\" and 汲む (気持ちを汲む); the spelling くみ取る is also widespread.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"裁判官が判決を言い渡した。","romaji":"Saibankan ga hanketsu o iiwatashita.","german":"The judge handed down the verdict.","furigana":"さいばんかん|はんけつ|い|わた"},{"japanese":"彼は突然、会社から解雇を言い渡された。","romaji":"Kare wa totsuzen, kaisha kara kaiko o iiwatasareta.","german":"He was suddenly given notice of dismissal by the company.","furigana":"かれ|とつぜん|かいしゃ|かいこ|い|わた"},{"japanese":"試合中の暴言で、選手に退場が言い渡された。","romaji":"Shiai-chū no bōgen de, senshu ni taijō ga iiwatasareta.","german":"For abusive language during the match, the player was sent off.","furigana":"しあいちゅう|ぼうげん|せんしゅ|たいじょう|い|わた"}],"notes":"言い渡す means to communicate a decision officially and from above, so that the recipient has to accept it. Typical are 判決を言い渡す \"to pronounce a verdict\", 解雇を言い渡す, 別れを言い渡す; the recipient is marked with に: 部下に異動を言い渡す. Often in the passive 〜を言い渡される. Unlike 伝える \"to tell\" (neutral) or 発表する \"to announce\" (public), 言い渡す emphasizes the speaker's authority.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"原因を突き止めた。","romaji":"Gen'in o tsukitometa.","german":"I pinned down the cause.","furigana":"げんいん|つ|と"},{"japanese":"記録を調べて、荷物が届かなかった理由を突き止めた。","romaji":"Kiroku o shirabete, nimotsu ga todokanakatta riyū o tsukitometa.","german":"By going through the records, I found out why the package had not arrived.","furigana":"きろく|しら|にもつ|とど|りゆう|つ|と"},{"japanese":"警察は防犯カメラの映像から犯人の居場所を突き止めた。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa bōhan kamera no eizō kara hannin no ibasho o tsukitometa.","german":"From security camera footage, the police tracked down the culprit's whereabouts.","furigana":"けいさつ|ぼうはん|えいぞう|はんにん|いばしょ|つ|と"}],"notes":"突き止める means to establish something hidden with certainty by persistent investigation. Typical objects are 原因を突き止める, 犯人を突き止める, 居場所や所在を突き止める, 正体を突き止める. It emphasizes the result of the search; the process itself is 調べる or 探る. Neutral, common in everyday speech as well as in the news; transitive with を.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"局面を打開する策を考える。","romaji":"Kyokumen o dakai suru saku o kangaeru.","german":"I am thinking of a measure to break the deadlock.","furigana":"きょくめん|だかい|さく|かんが"},{"japanese":"新しい提案が、話し合いの行き詰まりを打開した。","romaji":"Atarashii teian ga, hanashiai no ikizumari o dakai shita.","german":"A new proposal broke the impasse in the talks.","furigana":"あたら|ていあん|はな|あ|い|づ|だかい"},{"japanese":"赤字続きの現状を打開するため、会社は新事業に乗り出した。","romaji":"Akaji tsuzuki no genjō o dakai suru tame, kaisha wa shinjigyō ni noridashita.","german":"To break out of the current state of continuing losses, the company moved into a new business.","furigana":"あかじつづ|げんじょう|だかい|かいしゃ|しんじぎょう|の|だ"}],"notes":"打開 means to break open a stuck, hopeless situation through a new step. Typical objects are 局面を打開する, 現状を打開する, 難局を打開する, 行き詰まりを打開する; very common as a noun in 打開策 \"way out, approach to a solution\". Written language, especially in politics and business. Unlike 解決する \"to solve\", 打開 means overcoming the blockage, not necessarily the final solution.","levelNote":"Derived from 打開 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しいリーダーを擁立する。","romaji":"Atarashii rīdā o yōritsu suru.","german":"To back a new leader.","furigana":"あたら|ようりつ"},{"japanese":"野党は知事選に独自の候補を擁立した。","romaji":"Yatō wa chijisen ni dokuji no kōho o yōritsu shita.","german":"The opposition fielded its own candidate in the gubernatorial election.","furigana":"やとう|ちじせん|どくじ|こうほ|ようりつ"},{"japanese":"家臣たちは幼い王子を擁立して権力を握った。","romaji":"Kashin-tachi wa osanai ōji o yōritsu shite kenryoku o nigitta.","german":"The retainers installed the young prince as ruler and seized power.","furigana":"かしん|おさな|おうじ|ようりつ|けんりょく|にぎ"}],"notes":"擁立 means to put someone forward as a candidate or leader while actively supporting him. Today mainly in politics: 候補者を擁立する \"to field a candidate\", 独自候補を擁立する. Historically it also means to raise a young heir to the throne. Written language; unlike 推薦する \"to recommend\", here the group putting the person forward carries the candidacy itself.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"相手側と折衝を重ねた。","romaji":"Aite-gawa to sesshō o kasaneta.","german":"We held repeated negotiations with the other side.","furigana":"あいてがわ|せっしょう|かさ"},{"japanese":"担当者が取引先と価格について折衝している。","romaji":"Tantōsha ga torihikisaki to kakaku ni tsuite sesshō shite iru.","german":"The person in charge is negotiating the price with the business partner.","furigana":"たんとうしゃ|とりひきさき|かかく|せっしょう"},{"japanese":"年末の予算折衝で、各省庁と財務省の調整が続いた。","romaji":"Nenmatsu no yosan sesshō de, kakushōchō to Zaimushō no chōsei ga tsuzuita.","german":"In the year-end budget negotiations, coordination between the ministries and the Ministry of Finance continued.","furigana":"ねんまつ|よさんせっしょう|かくしょうちょう|ざいむしょう|ちょうせい|つづ"}],"notes":"折衝 refers to hard-fought business, official or diplomatic negotiations in which interests are weighed against each other. Typical are 〜と折衝する, 折衝を重ねる, 予算折衝, 外交折衝. Written language; in everyday speech people say 交渉する. 折衝 emphasizes the back and forth until a compromise, often at working level between agencies or companies.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自由の恩恵を享受する。","romaji":"Jiyū no onkei o kyōju suru.","german":"To enjoy the benefits of freedom.","furigana":"じゆう|おんけい|きょうじゅ"},{"japanese":"私たちは科学技術の発展による便利さを享受している。","romaji":"Watashitachi wa kagaku gijutsu no hatten ni yoru benrisa o kyōju shite iru.","german":"We enjoy the convenience brought by the development of science and technology.","furigana":"わたし|かがくぎじゅつ|はってん|べんり|きょうじゅ"},{"japanese":"全ての国民が等しく教育を受ける権利を享受すべきだ。","romaji":"Subete no kokumin ga hitoshiku kyōiku o ukeru kenri o kyōju subeki da.","german":"All citizens should equally enjoy the right to receive an education.","furigana":"すべ|こくみん|ひと|きょういく|う|けんり|きょうじゅ"}],"notes":"享受 means to receive and enjoy benefits, rights or comforts. Typical objects are 恩恵を享受する, 自由を享受する, 権利を享受する, 豊かさを享受する. Written language, frequent in essays, law and politics. Unlike 謳歌 (exuberant celebration), 享受 is sober: one comes to enjoy something that falls to one. Do not confuse with 甘受 \"to accept something unpleasant\".","levelNote":"Derived from 享受 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"結果を甘受するしかない。","romaji":"Kekka o kanju suru shika nai.","german":"There is nothing to do but accept the result.","furigana":"けっか|かんじゅ"},{"japanese":"責任者として、どんな批判も甘受するつもりだ。","romaji":"Sekininsha to shite, donna hihan mo kanju suru tsumori da.","german":"As the person responsible, I intend to accept any criticism.","furigana":"せきにんしゃ|ひはん|かんじゅ"},{"japanese":"低い給料を甘受してきた労働者たちが、ついに声を上げた。","romaji":"Hikui kyūryō o kanju shite kita rōdōshatachi ga, tsui ni koe o ageta.","german":"The workers who had long put up with low wages finally raised their voices.","furigana":"ひく|きゅうりょう|かんじゅ|ろうどうしゃ|こえ|あ"}],"notes":"甘受 means to accept something unpleasant, such as criticism, punishment or an unfavorable result, without protest, because one cannot change it or considers it justified. Typical are 批判を甘受する, 処分を甘受する, 運命を甘受する, often with 〜するしかない or 〜するつもりだ. Written language. Despite the character 甘 \"sweet\", nothing pleasant is meant; do not confuse with 享受, which receives something positive. More everyday are 受け入れる and 我慢する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"過去の経験に鑑みて判断する。","romaji":"Kako no keiken ni kangamite handan suru.","german":"To judge in light of past experience.","furigana":"かこ|けいけん|かんが|はんだん"},{"japanese":"事態の深刻さに鑑み、大会は中止となりました。","romaji":"Jitai no shinkokusa ni kangami, taikai wa chūshi to narimashita.","german":"In view of the seriousness of the situation, the tournament has been canceled.","furigana":"じたい|しんこく|かんが|たいかい|ちゅうし"},{"japanese":"昨年の反省に鑑みて、今年は早めに準備を始めた。","romaji":"Sakunen no hansei ni kangamite, kotoshi wa hayame ni junbi o hajimeta.","german":"Taking last year's reflections into account, we started preparing earlier this year.","furigana":"さくねん|はんせい|かんが|ことし|はや|じゅんび|はじ"}],"notes":"鑑みる means to take something as a standard or lesson and judge accordingly. It appears almost only as 〜に鑑みて or, in writing, 〜に鑑み: 現状に鑑み, 事の重大さに鑑みて \"in view of the seriousness of the matter\". Very formal, typical of official letters, press releases and speeches; in everyday speech people say 〜を考えて or 〜を考慮して. The variant 〜を鑑みて is widespread but considered non-standard.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"風に髪が靡く。","romaji":"Kaze ni kami ga nabiku.","german":"Her hair flutters in the wind.","furigana":"かぜ|かみ|なび"},{"japanese":"彼はお金や地位には決して靡かない人だ。","romaji":"Kare wa okane ya chii ni wa kesshite nabikanai hito da.","german":"He is a man who never yields to money or status.","furigana":"かれ|かね|ちい|けっ|なび|ひと"},{"japanese":"多くの議員が、勢いのある新党になびいた。","romaji":"Ōku no giin ga, ikioi no aru shintō ni nabiita.","german":"Many lawmakers went over to the up-and-coming new party.","furigana":"おお|ぎいん|いきお|しんとう"}],"notes":"靡く has two meanings: literally \"to wave in the wind, to flutter, to sway\" (旗が風に靡く, 髪が靡く) and figuratively \"to bow, to yield, to turn toward someone\" (権力に靡く, 金に靡かない, 誘いに靡く). The trigger is always marked with に; the verb is intransitive. It is mostly written in kana, なびく. Figuratively it often sounds pejorative (opportunistic conformity) or romantic (giving in to someone's courtship).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"時間を稼ぐ必要がある。","romaji":"Jikan o kasegu hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"We need to buy time.","furigana":"じかん|かせ|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"彼女はアルバイトで学費を稼いでいる。","romaji":"Kanojo wa arubaito de gakuhi o kaseide iru.","german":"She earns her tuition with a part-time job.","furigana":"かのじょ|がくひ|かせ"},{"japanese":"このステージでボーナスポイントを稼いでおこう。","romaji":"Kono sutēji de bōnasu pointo o kaseide okō.","german":"Let's pick up a few bonus points in this stage.","furigana":"かせ"}],"notes":"稼ぐ primarily means \"to earn money\": 生活費を稼ぐ, アルバイトで稼ぐ, 稼いだお金. Figuratively it means \"to get something for oneself\": 時間を稼ぐ \"to gain time\", 点数を稼ぐ \"to score points\" (also in the sense of \"to curry favor\"). Transitive with を, neutral and everyday. In contrast to 儲ける \"to make a profit\" (more from business deals), 稼ぐ emphasizes one's own work; the noun is 稼ぎ \"earnings\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"市場が飽和状態に達した。","romaji":"Shijō ga hōwa jōtai ni tasshita.","german":"The market has reached saturation.","furigana":"しじょう|ほうわじょうたい|たっ"},{"japanese":"水に塩を溶かし続けると、やがて飽和して溶けなくなる。","romaji":"Mizu ni shio o tokashi tsuzukeru to, yagate hōwa shite tokenaku naru.","german":"If you keep dissolving salt in water, it eventually becomes saturated and stops dissolving.","furigana":"みず|しお|と|つづ|ほうわ|と"},{"japanese":"都心の駐車場は平日の昼にはほぼ飽和している。","romaji":"Toshin no chūshajō wa heijitsu no hiru ni wa hobo hōwa shite iru.","german":"Parking lots in the city center are almost completely full at midday on weekdays.","furigana":"としん|ちゅうしゃじょう|へいじつ|ひる|ほうわ"}],"notes":"飽和 means that something has reached its capacity and can absorb nothing more. Originally a technical term of chemistry (飽和溶液 \"saturated solution\"), also used figuratively for markets, cities or amounts of information. Very common as a noun: 飽和状態, 飽和点; as a verb mostly in the state 飽和している. The opposite is 不飽和 (e.g. 不飽和脂肪酸 \"unsaturated fatty acid\"); in everyday speech people simply say いっぱいになる.","levelNote":"Derived from 飽和 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この技術は広く汎用されている。","romaji":"Kono gijutsu wa hiroku han'yō sarete iru.","german":"This technology is widely used.","furigana":"ぎじゅつ|ひろ|はんよう"},{"japanese":"この部品は汎用品なので、どの店でも手に入る。","romaji":"Kono buhin wa han'yōhin na node, dono mise de mo te ni hairu.","german":"This is a standard part, so you can get it at any shop.","furigana":"ぶひん|はんようひん|みせ|て|はい"},{"japanese":"このソフトは汎用性が高く、さまざまな業界で使われている。","romaji":"Kono sofuto wa han'yōsei ga takaku, samazama na gyōkai de tsukawarete iru.","german":"This software is highly versatile and is used in a wide range of industries.","furigana":"はんようせい|たか|ぎょうかい|つか"}],"notes":"汎用 means \"usable for many purposes, universal\". It lives almost exclusively as a noun or attribute: 汎用の部品, 汎用品 \"standard article\", 汎用機, 汎用性が高い \"highly versatile\", 汎用的な. As a verb, 汎用する appears only rarely, mostly in the passive in technical texts (汎用されている). The counterpart is 専用 \"intended for one purpose only\"; in everyday speech people say 幅広く使う instead of 汎用する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"情状を酌量して刑を軽くした。","romaji":"Jōjō o shakuryō shite kei o karuku shita.","german":"The sentence was reduced in consideration of the circumstances.","furigana":"じょうじょう|しゃくりょう|けい|かる"},{"japanese":"被告の境遇には、情状酌量の余地がある。","romaji":"Hikoku no kyōgū ni wa, jōjō shakuryō no yochi ga aru.","german":"The defendant's circumstances leave room for mitigating circumstances.","furigana":"ひこく|きょうぐう|じょうじょうしゃくりょう|よち"},{"japanese":"先生は遅刻の事情を酌量して、今回は注意だけにした。","romaji":"Sensei wa chikoku no jijō o shakuryō shite, konkai wa chūi dake ni shita.","german":"The teacher took the reason for the lateness into account and let it go with a warning this time.","furigana":"せんせい|ちこく|じじょう|しゃくりょう|こんかい|ちゅうい"}],"notes":"酌量 means to take special circumstances into account with goodwill, especially in determining a sentence. It is almost always found in the fixed compound 情状酌量 \"consideration of mitigating circumstances\": 情状を酌量する, 情状酌量の余地がある／ない. The character 酌 is the same as in 酌む \"to empathize with (others' feelings)\". Legal and written language; in everyday speech people say 考慮する or 事情を汲む.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"支払いを督促する。","romaji":"Shiharai o tokusoku suru.","german":"To press for payment.","furigana":"しはら|とくそく"},{"japanese":"図書館から本の返却を督促するメールが届いた。","romaji":"Toshokan kara hon no henkyaku o tokusoku suru mēru ga todoita.","german":"I received an email from the library demanding the return of a book.","furigana":"としょかん|ほん|へんきゃく|とくそく|とど"},{"japanese":"税金を滞納していたら、市役所から督促状が来た。","romaji":"Zeikin o tainō shite itara, shiyakusho kara tokusokujō ga kita.","german":"When I was behind on my taxes, a demand notice came from the city hall.","furigana":"ぜいきん|たいのう|しやくしょ|とくそくじょう|き"}],"notes":"督促 means to formally and emphatically remind someone of a due obligation, especially payments or returns. Typical are 支払いを督促する, 返済を督促する and the noun 督促状 \"reminder letter\". Official language of agencies, banks and companies. More everyday is 催促する \"to press, to follow up\", which also fits among friends; 督促 sounds official and strict.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"素材を吟味して選ぶ。","romaji":"Sozai o ginmi shite erabu.","german":"To choose materials after careful selection.","furigana":"そざい|ぎんみ|えら"},{"japanese":"複数の説明を吟味してから、結論を出した。","romaji":"Fukusū no setsumei o ginmi shite kara, ketsuron o dashita.","german":"I weighed several explanations carefully before reaching a conclusion.","furigana":"ふくすう|せつめい|ぎんみ|けつろん|だ"},{"japanese":"彼はスピーチの言葉を一つ一つ吟味した。","romaji":"Kare wa supīchi no kotoba o hitotsu hitotsu ginmi shita.","german":"He carefully weighed each and every word of his speech.","furigana":"かれ|ことば|ひと|ひと|ぎんみ"}],"notes":"吟味 means to carefully examine the quality, content or suitability of something before choosing or deciding. Typical are 素材を吟味する, 内容を吟味する, 言葉を吟味する and 吟味された食材 \"carefully selected ingredients\" (popular in gastronomy and advertising). Elevated, but common. Unlike 検討する \"to consider options\" or 確認する \"to check\", 吟味 emphasizes weighing quality through examination.","levelNote":"Derived from 吟味 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は法律に精通している。","romaji":"Kare wa hōritsu ni seitsū shite iru.","german":"He is well versed in law.","furigana":"かれ|ほうりつ|せいつう"},{"japanese":"地域の事情に精通した人に相談した。","romaji":"Chiiki no jijō ni seitsū shita hito ni sōdan shita.","german":"I consulted someone who knows the local situation well.","furigana":"ちいき|じじょう|せいつう|ひと|そうだん"},{"japanese":"三か国語に精通した通訳を探している。","romaji":"Sankakokugo ni seitsū shita tsūyaku o sagashite iru.","german":"We are looking for an interpreter fluent in three languages.","furigana":"さん|こくご|せいつう|つうやく|さが"}],"notes":"精通 means to know a field or certain circumstances thoroughly and in detail. The field is marked with に: 法律に精通する, 業界の事情に精通している, 〜に精通した人物. Mostly in the state 精通している or attributively 精通した; written language, frequent in résumés and personnel descriptions. Unlike 詳しい \"to be knowledgeable\" (everyday), 精通 sounds like real expertise; merely having heard of something (聞いたことがある) is not enough.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"信頼関係を醸成する。","romaji":"Shinrai kankei o jōsei suru.","german":"To build a relationship of trust.","furigana":"しんらいかんけい|じょうせい"},{"japanese":"地域全体で子育てを支える機運を醸成したい。","romaji":"Chiiki zentai de kosodate o sasaeru kiun o jōsei shitai.","german":"We want to build momentum for supporting child-rearing across the whole community.","furigana":"ちいきぜんたい|こそだ|ささ|きうん|じょうせい"},{"japanese":"共同作業を通じて、チームの一体感が醸成された。","romaji":"Kyōdō sagyō o tsūjite, chīmu no ittaikan ga jōsei sareta.","german":"Through working together, a sense of unity was fostered in the team.","furigana":"きょうどうさぎょう|つう|いったいかん|じょうせい"}],"notes":"醸成 originally means \"to let ferment, to brew\" and today is used mainly figuratively: \"to let a mood, trust or awareness arise gradually\". Typical are 信頼関係を醸成する, 機運を醸成する, 雰囲気を醸成する, 一体感を醸成する. Written language, popular in politics and corporate texts; for actual beer or sake brewing, people say 醸造. The image of slow fermentation distinguishes it from 作る or 生み出す.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不正を一掃する。","romaji":"Fusei o issō suru.","german":"To eradicate wrongdoing.","furigana":"ふせい|いっそう"},{"japanese":"在庫一掃セールで、冬物が半額になっている。","romaji":"Zaiko issō sēru de, fuyumono ga hangaku ni natte iru.","german":"In the stock clearance sale, winter wear is half price.","furigana":"ざいこいっそう|ふゆもの|はんがく"},{"japanese":"監督の一言が、チームの不安を一掃した。","romaji":"Kantoku no hitokoto ga, chīmu no fuan o issō shita.","german":"A single word from the manager swept away the team's anxiety.","furigana":"かんとく|ひとこと|ふあん|いっそう"}],"notes":"一掃 means to remove something undesirable completely in one stroke. Typical objects are 不正を一掃する, 暴力団を一掃する, 不安を一掃する; in retail 在庫一掃セール \"clearance sale\". Written language, but also common in advertising. Unlike 取り除く, 一掃 emphasizes thoroughness and completeness. Do not confuse with the identically read adverb 一層 \"even more\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人生を回顧する時間が必要だ。","romaji":"Jinsei o kaiko suru jikan ga hitsuyō da.","german":"We need time to look back on our lives.","furigana":"じんせい|かいこ|じかん|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"元首相は回顧録で、当時の決断を回顧している。","romaji":"Moto-shushō wa kaikoroku de, tōji no ketsudan o kaiko shite iru.","german":"In his memoirs, the former prime minister looks back on the decisions he made at the time.","furigana":"もとしゅしょう|かいころく|とうじ|けつだん|かいこ"},{"japanese":"美術館で、その画家の回顧展が開かれている。","romaji":"Bijutsukan de, sono gaka no kaikoten ga hirakarete iru.","german":"A retrospective of that painter is being held at the art museum.","furigana":"びじゅつかん|がか|かいこてん|ひら"}],"notes":"回顧 means to look back on past times or events and consciously call them to mind. Typical are 人生を回顧する, 当時を回顧する as well as the nouns 回顧録 \"memoirs\" and 回顧展 \"retrospective\". Written language. Unlike 思い出す \"to remember\" (spontaneous, everyday), 回顧 is an orderly retrospective; 懐古, by contrast, emphasizes nostalgic longing.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"苦痛に悶える。","romaji":"Kutsū ni modaeru.","german":"To writhe in pain.","furigana":"くつう|もだ"},{"japanese":"彼は叶わぬ恋に一人で悶えていた。","romaji":"Kare wa kanawanu koi ni hitori de modaete ita.","german":"He was tormented alone by an unrequited love.","furigana":"かれ|かな|こい|ひとり|もだ"},{"japanese":"子猫の寝顔がかわいすぎて、思わず悶えてしまった。","romaji":"Koneko no negao ga kawaisugite, omowazu modaete shimatta.","german":"The sleeping kitten's face was so cute that I couldn't help swooning.","furigana":"こねこ|ねがお|おも|もだ"}],"notes":"悶える means to writhe in physical pain or to suffer mental torment. The trigger is marked with に: 苦痛に悶える, 恋に悶える; intensified, 悶え苦しむ \"to writhe in agony\". Intransitive, rather literary. In colloquial and internet language it is also used jokingly for \"to swoon with delight\" (かわいすぎて悶える). More everyday are 苦しむ and 悩む.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"運命を諦観する。","romaji":"Unmei o teikan suru.","german":"To accept one's fate with equanimity.","furigana":"うんめい|ていかん"},{"japanese":"晩年の彼は、すべてを諦観したような穏やかな表情をしていた。","romaji":"Bannen no kare wa, subete o teikan shita yō na odayaka na hyōjō o shite ita.","german":"In his later years, he had a calm expression, as if he had come to terms with everything.","furigana":"ばんねん|かれ|ていかん|おだ|ひょうじょう"},{"japanese":"この作品には、人生に対する作者の諦観がにじみ出ている。","romaji":"Kono sakuhin ni wa, jinsei ni taisuru sakusha no teikan ga nijimidete iru.","german":"This work shows the author's serene, resigned outlook on life.","furigana":"さくひん|じんせい|たい|さくしゃ|ていかん|で"}],"notes":"諦観 has two sides: originally (Buddhist) \"to see clearly through the nature of things\", today mostly \"to accept one's fate calmly, to be serenely resigned\". Typical are 運命を諦観する, 人生を諦観する as well as the noun forms 諦観の境地 \"a state of serene resignation\" and 諦観した態度. Very much written and literary language. Unlike 諦める \"to give up\" (everyday, often disappointed), 諦観 carries a note of serene insight.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"返答に窮した。","romaji":"Hentō ni kyū shita.","german":"I was at a loss for an answer.","furigana":"へんとう|きゅう"},{"japanese":"失業して生活に窮する人が増えている。","romaji":"Shitsugyō shite seikatsu ni kyū suru hito ga fuete iru.","german":"More and more people are falling into hardship due to unemployment.","furigana":"しつぎょう|せいかつ|きゅう|ひと|ふ"},{"japanese":"窮すれば通ずで、追い詰められて初めていいアイデアが浮かんだ。","romaji":"Kyū sureba tsūzu de, oitsumerarete hajimete ii aidea ga ukanda.","german":"Necessity is the mother of invention: only when I was cornered did a good idea come to me.","furigana":"きゅう|つう|お|つ|はじ|う"}],"notes":"窮する means to get into a situation from which one sees no way out. The area is marked with に: 返答に窮する \"to be at a loss for an answer\", 説明に窮する, 生活に窮する \"to fall into material hardship\". Well known is the proverb 窮すれば通ず \"necessity is the mother of invention\". Written language; in everyday speech people say 困る.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の言動を揶揄する記事が出た。","romaji":"Kare no gendō o yayu suru kiji ga deta.","german":"An article appeared mocking his words and actions.","furigana":"かれ|げんどう|やゆ|きじ|で"},{"japanese":"首相の対応は、野党から「後手後手だ」と揶揄された。","romaji":"Shushō no taiō wa, yatō kara gotegote da to yayu sareta.","german":"The prime minister's response was mocked by the opposition as \"always a step behind\".","furigana":"しゅしょう|たいおう|やとう|ごてごて|やゆ"},{"japanese":"その漫画家は、社会の矛盾を揶揄する作品で人気を集めた。","romaji":"Sono mangaka wa, shakai no mujun o yayu suru sakuhin de ninki o atsumeta.","german":"That manga artist gained popularity with works that satirize the contradictions of society.","furigana":"まんがか|しゃかい|むじゅん|やゆ|さくひん|にんき|あつ"}],"notes":"揶揄 means to make someone or something look ridiculous with mocking, ironic remarks. Typical in the media are 〜を揶揄する記事 and the passive 〜と揶揄される \"to be mocked as ...\". Written language; because of the difficult characters it is often written やゆ. More everyday are からかう \"to tease\" (mostly harmless) and 皮肉る \"to jibe\"; 揶揄 is often aimed at public figures or social conditions.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"好機を逸してはならない。","romaji":"Kōki o isshite wa naranai.","german":"You must not miss a good opportunity.","furigana":"こうき|いっ"},{"japanese":"彼の行動は常軌を逸している。","romaji":"Kare no kōdō wa jōki o isshite iru.","german":"His behavior is beyond the bounds of common sense.","furigana":"かれ|こうどう|じょうき|いっ"},{"japanese":"チームは終盤のミスで優勝を逸した。","romaji":"Chīmu wa shūban no misu de yūshō o isshita.","german":"Because of a mistake in the closing stages, the team missed out on the championship.","furigana":"しゅうばん|ゆうしょう|いっ"}],"notes":"逸する has three meanings: \"to miss an opportunity\" (好機を逸する, 機会を逸する, 優勝を逸する), \"to overlook, to omit\" and \"to deviate\", above all in the fixed phrase 常軌を逸する \"to go beyond all normal bounds\". Transitive with を, written language. It conjugates as a する-verb of the special class: 逸した, 逸して. In everyday speech people say チャンスを逃す.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"議論は同じ結論に帰結した。","romaji":"Giron wa onaji ketsuron ni kiketsu shita.","german":"The discussion came down to the same conclusion.","furigana":"ぎろん|おな|けつろん|きけつ"},{"japanese":"無理な計画が失敗に終わったのは当然の帰結だ。","romaji":"Muri na keikaku ga shippai ni owatta no wa tōzen no kiketsu da.","german":"That the unreasonable plan ended in failure was the natural consequence.","furigana":"むり|けいかく|しっぱい|お|とうぜん|きけつ"},{"japanese":"少子化の問題は、最終的に働き方の問題に帰結する。","romaji":"Shōshika no mondai wa, saishūteki ni hatarakikata no mondai ni kiketsu suru.","german":"The problem of the falling birthrate ultimately comes down to the question of how we work.","furigana":"しょうしか|もんだい|さいしゅうてき|はたら|かた|もんだい|きけつ"}],"notes":"帰結 refers to the result that a train of thought or a development leads to logically. As a verb it is 〜に帰結する \"to come down to ...\"; very common is the noun in 当然の帰結 \"the logical consequence\" and 論理的帰結. Written language, typical of arguments and technical texts. Unlike 結果 \"result\", 帰結 emphasizes the inevitable logical follow-through; 結論 is the conclusion explicitly drawn.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"事実を歪めて伝える。","romaji":"Jijitsu o yugamete tsutaeru.","german":"To report facts in a distorted way.","furigana":"じじつ|ゆが|つた"},{"japanese":"彼は痛みに顔を歪めた。","romaji":"Kare wa itami ni kao o yugameta.","german":"He twisted his face in pain.","furigana":"かれ|いた|かお|ゆが"},{"japanese":"厳しすぎる家庭環境が彼の性格を歪めてしまった。","romaji":"Kibishisugiru katei kankyō ga kare no seikaku o yugamete shimatta.","german":"An overly strict home environment warped his character.","furigana":"きび|かていかんきょう|かれ|せいかく|ゆが"}],"notes":"歪める is the transitive counterpart of 歪む \"to warp, to bend\": to bring something out of its proper shape. Concretely: 顔を歪める \"to contort one's face\" (from pain or disgust), 口元を歪める. Figuratively: 事実を歪める, 真実を歪める, 歴史を歪める \"to falsify\", also 性格を歪める. Neutral to written language; related noun 歪み \"distortion\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"周囲から疎まれている。","romaji":"Shūi kara utomarete iru.","german":"He is shunned by those around him.","furigana":"しゅうい|うと"},{"japanese":"彼は正論ばかり言うので、上司に疎まれていた。","romaji":"Kare wa seiron bakari iu node, jōshi ni utomarete ita.","german":"Because he said nothing but correct things, he was disliked by his boss.","furigana":"かれ|せいろん|い|じょうし|うと"},{"japanese":"才能のある新人を疎む先輩もいる。","romaji":"Sainō no aru shinjin o utomu senpai mo iru.","german":"Some senior colleagues shun talented newcomers.","furigana":"さいのう|しんじん|うと|せんぱい"}],"notes":"疎む means to find someone a nuisance and therefore avoid them or keep them at a distance. It is almost always used in the passive: 周囲から疎まれる, 上司に疎まれる \"to be unpopular with the boss\". Written language; more common is the adjective 疎ましい \"annoying, unpleasant\". Similar are 嫌う \"to dislike\" (more general) and 避ける \"to avoid\"; 疎む emphasizes cool distance. Related: 疎い \"to be unfamiliar with\", 疎遠 \"estranged\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人権を蹂躙する行為は許されない。","romaji":"Jinken o jūrin suru kōi wa yurusarenai.","german":"Acts that trample on human rights are not permitted.","furigana":"じんけん|じゅうりん|こうい|ゆる"},{"japanese":"敵の軍隊に国土を蹂躙された。","romaji":"Teki no guntai ni kokudo o jūrin sareta.","german":"The country was overrun by enemy troops.","furigana":"てき|ぐんたい|こくど|じゅうりん"},{"japanese":"強豪チームは格下の相手を一方的に蹂躙した。","romaji":"Kyōgō chīmu wa kakushita no aite o ippōteki ni jūrin shita.","german":"The powerhouse team ran roughshod over its lower-ranked opponent.","furigana":"きょうごう|かくした|あいて|いっぽうてき|じゅうりん"}],"notes":"蹂躙 literally means \"to trample underfoot\" and refers to violently disregarding rights or overrunning a country. Typical are 人権を蹂躙する, 主権を蹂躙する, 国土が蹂躙される. Strongly written and emotional language, frequent in news and historical accounts; in sports also used hyperbolically for a crushing rout. More neutral are 侵害する \"to infringe\" (for rights) and 踏みにじる (same image, somewhat more everyday).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ネット上で詐欺が横行している。","romaji":"Netto-jō de sagi ga ōkō shite iru.","german":"Fraud is rampant on the Internet.","furigana":"じょう|さぎ|おうこう"},{"japanese":"この地域では違法駐車が横行している。","romaji":"Kono chiiki de wa ihō chūsha ga ōkō shite iru.","german":"Illegal parking is rampant in this area.","furigana":"ちいき|いほうちゅうしゃ|おうこう"},{"japanese":"当時は役人の汚職が横行していた。","romaji":"Tōji wa yakunin no oshoku ga ōkō shite ita.","german":"At that time, corruption among officials was rampant.","furigana":"とうじ|やくにん|おしょく|おうこう"}],"notes":"横行 means that something bad, such as fraud, corruption or violence, spreads unchecked. Pattern: 〜が横行する, mostly in the state 横行している: 詐欺が横行する, 不正が横行する, 違法駐車が横行する. Written language, typical of news and always negative. Unlike 流行する \"to be in fashion\" (neutral) or 蔓延する (disease, evil), 横行 emphasizes the brazenness with which something happens with impunity.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この概念は多くの意味を包含している。","romaji":"Kono gainen wa ōku no imi o hōgan shite iru.","german":"This concept contains many meanings.","furigana":"がいねん|おお|いみ|ほうがん"},{"japanese":"この計画は多くのリスクを包含している。","romaji":"Kono keikaku wa ōku no risuku o hōgan shite iru.","german":"This plan involves many risks.","furigana":"けいかく|おお|ほうがん"},{"japanese":"新しい法律は、オンライン取引も規制の対象として包含する。","romaji":"Atarashii hōritsu wa, onrain torihiki mo kisei no taishō to shite hōgan suru.","german":"The new law also brings online transactions within the scope of regulation.","furigana":"あたら|ほうりつ|とりひき|きせい|たいしょう|ほうがん"}],"notes":"包含 means that something includes other things as a component. Pattern: AはBを包含する, 〜を包含した概念. Very much written language, typical of philosophy, law and science (in mathematics 包含関係 \"inclusion relation\"). In everyday speech people say 含む; 包括, by contrast, means \"to cover comprehensively\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"古い制度を廃する。","romaji":"Furui seido o hai suru.","german":"To abolish the old system.","furigana":"ふる|せいど|はい"},{"japanese":"年賀状などの虚礼を廃する会社が増えている。","romaji":"Nengajō nado no kyorei o hai suru kaisha ga fuete iru.","german":"More and more companies are doing away with empty formalities such as New Year's cards.","furigana":"ねんがじょう|きょれい|はい|かいしゃ|ふ"},{"japanese":"将軍は家臣たちによって廃された。","romaji":"Shōgun wa kashintachi ni yotte haisareta.","german":"The shogun was deposed by his retainers.","furigana":"しょうぐん|かしん|はい"}],"notes":"廃する means to abolish an existing institution, rule or custom: 制度を廃する, 虚礼を廃する \"to abolish empty formalities\". Historically it also means \"to depose a ruler\". Written language; more usual are 廃止する (laws, institutions) and やめる. Do not confuse with the identically read 排する \"to exclude, to push aside\" (私情を排する).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"相手の動きを牽制する。","romaji":"Aite no ugoki o kensei suru.","german":"To keep the opponent's moves in check.","furigana":"あいて|うご|けんせい"},{"japanese":"ピッチャーは一塁ランナーを何度も牽制した。","romaji":"Pitchā wa ichirui rannā o nando mo kensei shita.","german":"The pitcher made several pickoff throws to the runner on first base.","furigana":"いちるい|なんど|けんせい"},{"japanese":"首相の発言は、増税論を牽制する狙いがあるとみられる。","romaji":"Shushō no hatsugen wa, zōzeiron o kensei suru nerai ga aru to mirareru.","german":"The prime minister's remark is seen as aimed at checking calls for a tax increase.","furigana":"しゅしょう|はつげん|ぞうぜいろん|けんせい|ねら"}],"notes":"牽制 means to keep an opponent from acting freely through threatening gestures, statements or maneuvers. Typical are 相手を牽制する, 〜の動きを牽制する, 互いに牽制し合う; in baseball, 牽制球 is the throw to a base that holds the runner. Frequent in political, business and sports reports; newspapers often write けん制. Unlike 阻止する \"to prevent\", 牽制 only aims to keep the other side in check.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"国の将来を憂える。","romaji":"Kuni no shōrai o ureeru.","german":"To be concerned about the country's future.","furigana":"くに|しょうらい|うれ"},{"japanese":"多くの専門家が、若者の読書離れを憂えている。","romaji":"Ōku no senmonka ga, wakamono no dokusho-banare o ureete iru.","german":"Many experts are concerned that young people are reading less.","furigana":"おお|せんもんか|わかもの|どくしょばな|うれ"},{"japanese":"戦争で荒れ果てた故郷を憂える詩が残っている。","romaji":"Sensō de arehateta kokyō o ureeru shi ga nokotte iru.","german":"A poem survives that laments a hometown ravaged by war.","furigana":"せんそう|あ|は|こきょう|うれ|し|のこ"}],"notes":"憂える means \"to worry about, to be distressed over\" and mostly concerns large, impersonal matters: 国の将来を憂える, 環境破壊を憂える, 少子化を憂える. It is transitive with を and is considerably more serious and literary than 心配する; unlike 悩む it is hardly about one's own problems but about the state of society. There is also the synonymous older form 憂う, for example in 憂うべき事態 \"a worrying situation\". The noun is 憂い or 憂え \"worry\", cf. 備えあれば憂いなし.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"犯罪を抑止する対策が必要だ。","romaji":"Hanzai o yokushi suru taisaku ga hitsuyō da.","german":"Measures to deter crime are needed.","furigana":"はんざい|よくし|たいさく|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"核兵器による抑止力の是非が議論されている。","romaji":"Kakuheiki ni yoru yokushiryoku no zehi ga giron sarete iru.","german":"The merits of deterrence by nuclear weapons are being debated.","furigana":"かくへいき|よくしりょく|ぜひ|ぎろん"},{"japanese":"罰則の強化で、飲酒運転を抑止できると考えられている。","romaji":"Bassoku no kyōka de, inshu unten o yokushi dekiru to kangaerarete iru.","german":"It is thought that stricter penalties can deter drunk driving.","furigana":"ばっそく|きょうか|いんしゅうんてん|よくし|かんが"}],"notes":"抑止 means to prevent something undesirable, such as crime, war or abuse, in advance through deterrence or countermeasures. Typical are 犯罪を抑止する, 戦争を抑止する and, very commonly, the noun 抑止力 \"deterrent effect\" (核抑止 \"nuclear deterrence\"). Written language, in politics and security debates. Unlike 抑制 \"to curb, to hold down\" (costs, appetite), 抑止 emphasizes prevention through deterrence.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"細胞が急速に増殖する。","romaji":"Saibō ga kyūsoku ni zōshoku suru.","german":"The cells multiply rapidly.","furigana":"さいぼう|きゅうそく|ぞうしょく"},{"japanese":"夏場は食べ物の中で細菌が増殖しやすい。","romaji":"Natsuba wa tabemono no naka de saikin ga zōshoku shiyasui.","german":"In summer, bacteria multiply easily in food.","furigana":"なつば|た|もの|なか|さいきん|ぞうしょく"},{"japanese":"駅前にコンビニが増殖して、今では五軒もある。","romaji":"Ekimae ni konbini ga zōshoku shite, ima de wa go-ken mo aru.","german":"Convenience stores have sprung up like mushrooms in front of the station; there are now five of them.","furigana":"えきまえ|ぞうしょく|いま|ごけん"}],"notes":"増殖 means that cells or organisms multiply by division or reproduction: 細胞が増殖する, 細菌が増殖する, ウイルスが増殖する. Figuratively, and often ironically, also for things that spread uncontrollably (空き家が増殖する). Intransitive; technical in biology and medicine, otherwise written language. Unlike 増える \"to increase\", 増殖 emphasizes self-multiplication; 繁殖 refers more to the reproduction of animals and plants.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"権限を付与する。","romaji":"Kengen o fuyo suru.","german":"To grant authority.","furigana":"けんげん|ふよ"},{"japanese":"新しい会員には100ポイントが付与されます。","romaji":"Atarashii kaiin ni wa hyaku pointo ga fuyo saremasu.","german":"New members will be granted 100 points.","furigana":"あたら|かいいん|ふよ"},{"japanese":"各商品に番号を付与して管理する。","romaji":"Kaku shōhin ni bangō o fuyo shite kanri suru.","german":"Each product is assigned a number for management.","furigana":"かくしょうひん|ばんごう|ふよ|かんり"}],"notes":"付与する is a formal administrative and technical word for \"to allot, to confer (rights, status, points)\". Typical: 権限を付与する, 資格を付与する, 番号を付与する; the recipient is marked with に (ユーザーに権限を付与する), very often in the passive: ポイントが付与される. In everyday speech people simply say 与える or あげる; the ceremonial presentation of titles or prizes is 授与する, the issuing of documents 交付する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"前任者の方針を踏襲する。","romaji":"Zen'ninsha no hōshin o tōshū suru.","german":"To follow the policy of one's predecessor.","furigana":"ぜんにんしゃ|ほうしん|とうしゅう"},{"japanese":"新モデルは旧モデルのデザインをほぼ踏襲している。","romaji":"Shin moderu wa kyū moderu no dezain o hobo tōshū shite iru.","german":"The new model largely follows the design of the old model.","furigana":"しん|きゅう|とうしゅう"},{"japanese":"今年の文化祭も、例年のやり方を踏襲することになった。","romaji":"Kotoshi no bunkasai mo, reinen no yarikata o tōshū suru koto ni natta.","german":"This year's school festival will also follow the usual way of previous years.","furigana":"ことし|ぶんかさい|れいねん|かた|とうしゅう"}],"notes":"踏襲する means to take over and continue an existing course, precedent or style unchanged: 前例を踏襲する, 方針・路線を踏襲する, デザインを踏襲する. It is a written and news word; more neutral is 受け継ぐ \"to inherit, to carry on\", and for traditions or offices 継承する. Beware of a notorious reading mistake: the correct reading is とうしゅう, not ふしゅう.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"事件の真相を糾明する。","romaji":"Jiken no shinsō o kyūmei suru.","german":"To get to the truth of the incident.","furigana":"じけん|しんそう|きゅうめい"},{"japanese":"野党は大臣の責任を厳しく糾明した。","romaji":"Yatō wa daijin no sekinin o kibishiku kyūmei shita.","german":"The opposition strictly called the minister to account for his responsibility.","furigana":"やとう|だいじん|せきにん|きび|きゅうめい"},{"japanese":"市民団体は、補助金の不正使用を徹底的に糾明するよう求めた。","romaji":"Shimin dantai wa, hojokin no fusei shiyō o tetteiteki ni kyūmei suru yō motometa.","german":"The citizens' group demanded a thorough investigation into the misuse of subsidies.","furigana":"しみんだんたい|ほじょきん|ふせいしよう|てっていてき|きゅうめい|もと"}],"notes":"糾明する means to investigate wrongdoing or guilt thoroughly and bring it to light: 責任を糾明する, 不正を糾明する, 真相を糾明する. It sounds accusatory; one calls someone to account. Do not confuse with the identically read 究明する, which means neutral research into causes (事故の原因を究明する); 究明 is used for accidents and scientific questions, 糾明 for scandals and questions of guilt. Written language, typical of news, parliament and the judiciary; older spelling 糺明.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"戒厳令を布告する。","romaji":"Kaigenrei o fukoku suru.","german":"To proclaim martial law.","furigana":"かいげんれい|ふこく"},{"japanese":"1941年、日本はアメリカに宣戦を布告した。","romaji":"Sen kyūhyaku yonjūichi nen, Nihon wa Amerika ni sensen o fukoku shita.","german":"In 1941, Japan declared war on the United States.","furigana":"ねん|にほん|せんせん|ふこく"},{"japanese":"新しい社長は、無駄な会議に宣戦布告した。","romaji":"Atarashii shachō wa, muda na kaigi ni sensen fukoku shita.","german":"The new president declared war on pointless meetings.","furigana":"あたら|しゃちょう|むだ|かいぎ|せんせんふこく"}],"notes":"布告する is the official \"proclaiming, declaring\" by a state or authority: 戒厳令を布告する. Today the word lives mainly in 宣戦布告 \"declaration of war\" (〜に宣戦を布告する), which is also used jokingly in a figurative sense (\"to declare war on someone\"). The promulgation of laws is 公布する, a general declaration 宣言する, an announcement 発表する.","levelNote":"Derived from 布告 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"反乱を制圧した。","romaji":"Hanran o seiatsu shita.","german":"The rebellion was suppressed.","furigana":"はんらん|せいあつ"},{"japanese":"警察官が刃物を持った男を素早く制圧した。","romaji":"Keisatsukan ga hamono o motta otoko o subayaku seiatsu shita.","german":"The police officer quickly overpowered the man holding a knife.","furigana":"けいさつかん|はもの|も|おとこ|すばや|せいあつ"},{"japanese":"前半はホームチームが中盤を完全に制圧していた。","romaji":"Zenhan wa hōmu chīmu ga chūban o kanzen ni seiatsu shite ita.","german":"In the first half, the home team completely dominated midfield.","furigana":"ぜんはん|ちゅうばん|かんぜん|せいあつ"}],"notes":"制圧する means to bring something completely under control by force or superiority: 反乱・暴動を制圧する, 首都を制圧する; the police 犯人を制圧する \"overpower\" a culprit; in sports also \"to dominate\" (中盤を制圧する). Putting down unrest is also specifically called 鎮圧する; 抑圧する is the political \"oppressing\" of people and sounds negative; 征服する \"to conquer\". Passive: 制圧される.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不正を暴露した。","romaji":"Fusei o bakuro shita.","german":"He exposed the wrongdoing.","furigana":"ふせい|ばくろ"},{"japanese":"元社員が週刊誌で会社の内情を暴露した。","romaji":"Moto shain ga shūkanshi de kaisha no naijō o bakuro shita.","german":"A former employee revealed the company's inside story in a weekly magazine.","furigana":"もとしゃいん|しゅうかんし|かいしゃ|ないじょう|ばくろ"},{"japanese":"友達に恥ずかしい昔の話を暴露されてしまった。","romaji":"Tomodachi ni hazukashii mukashi no hanashi o bakuro sarete shimatta.","german":"A friend exposed an embarrassing old story about me.","furigana":"ともだち|は|むかし|はなし|ばくろ"}],"notes":"暴露する means to \"reveal, spill\" something hidden against the will of others: 秘密・不正・正体を暴露する; an exposé book is a 暴露本. Often in the passive (〜に暴露される). Neutral \"to disclose\" is 明かす, the formal reporting of abuses is 告発する, and colloquially people say ばらす. In technical texts 曝露 stands for \"exposure\" to harmful substances.","levelNote":"Derived from 暴露 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"他人の私生活を詮索するべきではない。","romaji":"Tanin no shiseikatsu o sensaku suru beki dewa nai.","german":"You should not pry into other people's private lives.","furigana":"たにん|しせいかつ|せんさく"},{"japanese":"理由は詮索しないでおくよ。","romaji":"Riyū wa sensaku shinaide oku yo.","german":"I'll refrain from asking about the reason.","furigana":"りゆう|せんさく"},{"japanese":"隣の人は詮索好きで、来客のたびに誰なのか聞いてくる。","romaji":"Tonari no hito wa sensakuzuki de, raikyaku no tabi ni dare na no ka kiite kuru.","german":"The neighbor is nosy and asks who it is every time a visitor comes.","furigana":"となり|ひと|せんさくず|らいきゃく|だれ|き"}],"notes":"詮索する means to poke around and dig nosily into other people's affairs; the word is almost always pejorative: 他人の私生活を詮索する, あれこれ詮索する, 余計な詮索はしない. A nosy person is 詮索好き. Neutral investigating is 調べる or 調査する, covert spying out is 探る.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"形式に拘泥するべきではない。","romaji":"Keishiki ni kōdei suru beki dewa nai.","german":"You should not be hung up on form.","furigana":"けいしき|こうでい"},{"japanese":"過去の失敗に拘泥していては前に進めない。","romaji":"Kako no shippai ni kōdei shite ite wa mae ni susumenai.","german":"You can't move forward if you dwell on past failures.","furigana":"かこ|しっぱい|こうでい|まえ|すす"},{"japanese":"細部に拘泥せず、全体の流れを見てください。","romaji":"Saibu ni kōdei sezu, zentai no nagare o mite kudasai.","german":"Don't get caught up in the details; please look at the overall flow.","furigana":"さいぶ|こうでい|ぜんたい|なが|み"}],"notes":"拘泥する is a written word for \"to (excessively) fixate on something, to stick to something\" and takes に: 形式に拘泥する, 細部に拘泥する, 過去に拘泥する. It is always negative and often appears negated (〜に拘泥せず). The everyday word is こだわる, which, however, can also mean positively \"to be particular about\" (素材にこだわる), which 拘泥する never does.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自分の行いを猛省する。","romaji":"Jibun no okonai o mōsei suru.","german":"To deeply reflect on one's own conduct.","furigana":"じぶん|おこな|もうせい"},{"japanese":"今回の不祥事について、会社として猛省しております。","romaji":"Konkai no fushōji ni tsuite, kaisha to shite mōsei shite orimasu.","german":"As a company, we are reflecting deeply on this scandal.","furigana":"こんかい|ふしょうじ|かいしゃ|もうせい"},{"japanese":"監督は選手たちに猛省を促した。","romaji":"Kantoku wa senshutachi ni mōsei o unagashita.","german":"The coach urged the players to reflect seriously.","furigana":"かんとく|せんしゅ|もうせい|うなが"}],"notes":"猛省する is an intensified 反省する: \"to take a hard look at oneself, to rethink one's conduct in a sharply self-critical way\". Typical in official apologies (深く猛省しております) and in the phrase 猛省を促す \"to urge someone to reflect seriously on their conduct\". 後悔する, by contrast, is the mere feeling of regret, without the aspect of self-examination.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は会長就任を固辞した。","romaji":"Kare wa kaichō shūnin o koji shita.","german":"He firmly declined to take the chairmanship.","furigana":"かれ|かいちょうしゅうにん|こじ"},{"japanese":"彼女は何度頼まれても謝礼を固辞した。","romaji":"Kanojo wa nando tanomarete mo sharei o koji shita.","german":"However many times she was asked, she firmly refused any honorarium.","furigana":"かのじょ|なんどたの|しゃれい|こじ"},{"japanese":"国民栄誉賞を固辞した選手もいる。","romaji":"Kokumin eiyoshō o koji shita senshu mo iru.","german":"There are also athletes who declined the People's Honor Award.","furigana":"こくみんえいよしょう|こじ|せんしゅ"}],"notes":"固辞する means to \"firmly decline\" an offer or an honor despite repeated urging: 就任・受賞・謝礼を固辞する. It sounds politely modest, not hostile. Neutral declining is 辞退する, in everyday speech 断る; 拒否する is a blunt, confrontational \"refusal\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"社会のために献身する。","romaji":"Shakai no tame ni kenshin suru.","german":"To devote oneself to society.","furigana":"しゃかい|けんしん"},{"japanese":"彼女は長年、地域医療に献身してきた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa naganen, chiiki iryō ni kenshin shite kita.","german":"She has devoted herself to community medicine for many years.","furigana":"かのじょ|ながねん|ちいきいりょう|けんしん"},{"japanese":"母の献身的な看護のおかげで、父は元気になった。","romaji":"Haha no kenshinteki na kango no okage de, chichi wa genki ni natta.","german":"Thanks to my mother's devoted nursing care, my father got well.","furigana":"はは|けんしんてき|かんご|ちち|げんき"}],"notes":"献身する means to selflessly \"devote, sacrifice oneself\" to a cause or to people; it is intransitive: 〜に献身する or 〜のために献身する. Even more common than the verb is the adjective 献身的 (献身的な看護, 献身的に支える). 専念する only means \"to concentrate entirely on something\"; 尽くす is the more everyday \"to do one's best for someone\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"夥しい数の星が見えた。","romaji":"Obitadashii kazu no hoshi ga mieta.","german":"A countless number of stars could be seen.","furigana":"おびただ|かず|ほし|み"},{"japanese":"事故現場には夥しい量の血が残っていた。","romaji":"Jiko genba ni wa obitadashii ryō no chi ga nokotte ita.","german":"An enormous amount of blood was left at the scene of the accident.","furigana":"じこげんば|おびただ|りょう|ち|のこ"},{"japanese":"台風のあと、海岸に夥しいごみが打ち上げられた。","romaji":"Taifū no ato, kaigan ni obitadashii gomi ga uchiagerareta.","german":"After the typhoon, vast amounts of trash were washed up on the shore.","furigana":"たいふう|かいがん|おびただ|う|あ"}],"notes":"夥しい describes an enormous number or quantity, often of something alarming: 夥しい数の〜, 夥しい量の血, 夥しい人出. It is a written word and is mostly written in kana, おびただしい. In a literary-ironic use it follows 〜こと: 不便なこと夥しい \"extremely inconvenient\". More sober are 無数の \"countless\" and 膨大な \"huge (amounts of data, costs)\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"忌まわしい記憶を消したい。","romaji":"Imawashii kioku o keshitai.","german":"I want to erase the abominable memory.","furigana":"い|きおく|け"},{"japanese":"あの忌まわしい事件から十年が経った。","romaji":"Ano imawashii jiken kara jūnen ga tatta.","german":"Ten years have passed since that dreadful incident.","furigana":"い|じけん|じゅうねん|た"},{"japanese":"村には古くから忌まわしい言い伝えが残っている。","romaji":"Mura ni wa furuku kara imawashii iitsutae ga nokotte iru.","german":"An ominous legend has remained in the village since ancient times.","furigana":"むら|ふる|い|い|つた|のこ"}],"notes":"忌まわしい expresses strong abhorrence of something one would most like to forget or avoid, often with an undertone of ill omen: 忌まわしい事件, 忌まわしい記憶, 忌まわしい過去. The word is written language and weighty; in everyday speech people say 嫌な. For \"ominous\" alone, 不吉な is more usual.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"勇ましい掛け声が響いた。","romaji":"Isamashii kakegoe ga hibiita.","german":"Spirited shouts rang out.","furigana":"いさ|か|ごえ|ひび"},{"japanese":"消防士たちは勇ましく炎の中へ入っていった。","romaji":"Shōbōshitachi wa isamashiku honō no naka e haitte itta.","german":"The firefighters went bravely into the flames.","furigana":"しょうぼうし|いさ|ほのお|なか|はい"},{"japanese":"口では勇ましいことを言うが、実際は何もしない。","romaji":"Kuchi de wa isamashii koto o iu ga, jissai wa nani mo shinai.","german":"He talks big, but in reality he does nothing.","furigana":"くち|いさ|い|じっさい|なに"}],"notes":"勇ましい means \"brave, valiant\", but it often also describes sound and bearing: \"spirited, vigorous, stirring\" (勇ましい行進曲, 勇ましい掛け声). Adverbially: 勇ましく戦う. Slightly ironic is 勇ましいことを言う \"to talk big\". 勇敢な, by contrast, soberly stresses the brave deed and is more formal.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"厳かな雰囲気の中で式が行われた。","romaji":"Ogosoka na fun'iki no naka de shiki ga okonawareta.","german":"The ceremony was held in a solemn atmosphere.","furigana":"おごそ|ふんいき|なか|しき|おこな"},{"japanese":"神父は厳かな声で祈りを捧げた。","romaji":"Shinpu wa ogosoka na koe de inori o sasageta.","german":"The priest offered a prayer in a solemn voice.","furigana":"しんぷ|おごそ|こえ|いの|ささ"},{"japanese":"夜明けの山頂は、厳かな静けさに包まれていた。","romaji":"Yoake no sanchō wa, ogosoka na shizukesa ni tsutsumarete ita.","german":"The mountaintop at dawn was wrapped in a solemn stillness.","furigana":"よあ|さんちょう|おごそ|しず|つつ"}],"notes":"厳か is a な-adjective for a \"solemn, dignified\" mood at ceremonies and rituals: 厳かな雰囲気, 厳かな声, 厳かに執り行う. Do not confuse with the same kanji in 厳しい \"strict\". 荘厳な \"sublime, magnificent\" describes buildings or music rather.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は朗らかな性格だ。","romaji":"Kanojo wa hogaraka na seikaku da.","german":"She has a cheerful personality.","furigana":"かのじょ|ほが|せいかく"},{"japanese":"祖母はいつも朗らかに笑っている。","romaji":"Sobo wa itsumo hogaraka ni waratte iru.","german":"My grandmother is always laughing cheerfully.","furigana":"そぼ|ほが|わら"},{"japanese":"教室に子どもたちの朗らかな歌声が響いた。","romaji":"Kyōshitsu ni kodomotachi no hogaraka na utagoe ga hibiita.","german":"The cheerful singing voices of the children rang through the classroom.","furigana":"きょうしつ|こ|ほが|うたごえ|ひび"}],"notes":"朗らか describes a \"cheerful, sunny\" nature, laugh, or a clear, cheerful voice: 朗らかな人, 朗らかな性格, 朗らかに笑う. It sounds a bit more elevated than 明るい; 陽気な is more \"exuberant, merry\". Rarely, and in literary use, it also describes clear, beautiful weather.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"密やかな会合が開かれた。","romaji":"Hisoyaka na kaigō ga hirakareta.","german":"A secret meeting was held.","furigana":"ひそ|かいごう|ひら"},{"japanese":"夜の読書が私の密やかな楽しみだ。","romaji":"Yoru no dokusho ga watashi no hisoyaka na tanoshimi da.","german":"Reading at night is my quiet pleasure.","furigana":"よる|どくしょ|わたし|ひそ|たの"},{"japanese":"二人は町外れで密やかに暮らしていた。","romaji":"Futari wa machihazure de hisoyaka ni kurashite ita.","german":"The two lived quietly on the outskirts of town.","furigana":"ふたり|まちはず|ひそ|く"}],"notes":"密やか is a literary な-adjective for \"quiet, secret, inconspicuous\": 密やかな楽しみ, 密やかな足音, 密やかに暮らす. It is often written in kana, ひそやか. Much more common in everyday speech is the related 密か (ひそかに \"secretly\"), and colloquially 内緒で.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"艶やかな黒髪が美しい。","romaji":"Tsuyayaka na kurokami ga utsukushii.","german":"The glossy black hair is beautiful.","furigana":"つや|くろかみ|うつく"},{"japanese":"艶やかに光るさくらんぼが店先に並んでいる。","romaji":"Tsuyayaka ni hikaru sakuranbo ga misesaki ni narande iru.","german":"Shiny cherries are lined up in front of the shop.","furigana":"つや|ひか|みせさき|なら"},{"japanese":"このクリームを使うと、肌が艶やかになる。","romaji":"Kono kurīmu o tsukau to, hada ga tsuyayaka ni naru.","german":"If you use this cream, your skin becomes glowing.","furigana":"つか|はだ|つや"}],"notes":"艶やか (つややか) describes a beautiful, healthy \"gloss\" of hair, skin, fruit or lacquer: 艶やかな黒髪, 肌が艶やかになる. Note: the same spelling read あでやか means \"enchanting, glamorous\" (of women or kimono); therefore it is often written in kana as つややか. More colloquial are つやつや and ぴかぴか.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"卑しい行為に及んだ。","romaji":"Iyashii kōi ni oyonda.","german":"He committed a despicable act.","furigana":"いや|こうい|およ"},{"japanese":"人の弱みにつけ込むなんて、卑しいやり方だ。","romaji":"Hito no yowami ni tsukekomu nante, iyashii yarikata da.","german":"Taking advantage of other people's weaknesses is a despicable way to act.","furigana":"ひと|よわ|こ|いや|かた"},{"japanese":"食べ物のことになると、弟は卑しくなる。","romaji":"Tabemono no koto ni naru to, otōto wa iyashiku naru.","german":"When it comes to food, my younger brother gets greedy.","furigana":"た|もの|おとうと|いや"}],"notes":"卑しい is a sharp value judgment with several meanings: \"base, mean\" (卑しい行為, 卑しいやり方), \"greedy\" especially about food (食べ物に卑しい) and, obsolete, \"of low social standing\". 下品な refers more to vulgar manners, 卑怯な to cowardly, unfair behavior.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"紛らわしい表現を避ける。","romaji":"Magirawashii hyōgen o sakeru.","german":"To avoid confusing expressions.","furigana":"まぎ|ひょうげん|さ"},{"japanese":"二つの駅の名前が紛らわしくて、電車を乗り間違えた。","romaji":"Futatsu no eki no namae ga magirawashikute, densha o norimachigaeta.","german":"The names of the two stations are confusingly similar, and I took the wrong train.","furigana":"ふた|えき|なまえ|まぎ|でんしゃ|の|まちが"},{"japanese":"紛らわしい言い方をしないで、はっきり言ってよ。","romaji":"Magirawashii iikata o shinaide, hakkiri itte yo.","german":"Don't put it in a confusing way; say it clearly.","furigana":"まぎ|い|かた|い"}],"notes":"紛らわしい means \"easily confused, misleading\" because things resemble each other or something is ambiguous: 紛らわしい名前, 紛らわしい表現, 紛らわしい言い方. In conversation also as an annoyed exclamation: 紛らわしい！. ややこしい, by contrast, means \"complicated, tangled\", and 曖昧な \"vague, unclear\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"過酷な労働条件に耐える。","romaji":"Kakoku na rōdō jōken ni taeru.","german":"To endure harsh working conditions.","furigana":"かこく|ろうどうじょうけん|た"},{"japanese":"砂漠の過酷な環境でも生きられる植物がある。","romaji":"Sabaku no kakoku na kankyō demo ikirareru shokubutsu ga aru.","german":"There are plants that can live even in the harsh environment of the desert.","furigana":"さばく|かこく|かんきょう|い|しょくぶつ"},{"japanese":"このマラソンは坂が多く、とても過酷なレースだ。","romaji":"Kono marason wa saka ga ōku, totemo kakoku na rēsu da.","german":"This marathon has many hills and is a very grueling race.","furigana":"さか|おお|かこく"}],"notes":"過酷な describes conditions that are \"harsh, merciless\" beyond what is bearable: 過酷な労働, 過酷な環境, 過酷なレース, 過酷な試練. The variant 苛酷 emphasizes cruel treatment by people. 厳しい is the general, weaker \"strict, severe\"; 残酷な means morally \"cruel\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"殊勝な態度で謝罪した。","romaji":"Shushō na taido de shazai shita.","german":"He apologized with a contrite attitude.","furigana":"しゅしょう|たいど|しゃざい"},{"japanese":"毎朝早起きして勉強するとは、殊勝な心がけだね。","romaji":"Maiasa hayaoki shite benkyō suru to wa, shushō na kokorogake da ne.","german":"Getting up early every morning to study, what a commendable attitude!","furigana":"まいあさはやお|べんきょう|しゅしょう|こころ"},{"japanese":"いつも文句ばかりの弟が、珍しく殊勝なことを言った。","romaji":"Itsumo monku bakari no otōto ga, mezurashiku shushō na koto o itta.","german":"My brother, who is always complaining, said something admirable for once.","furigana":"もんく|おとうと|めずら|しゅしょう|い"}],"notes":"殊勝な praises behavior as \"laudable, good, contrite\", often about someone from whom one would not have expected it: 殊勝な心がけ, 殊勝な態度, 殊勝なことを言う. Because a judgment from above resonates in it, one does not say it about superiors, and it can sound ironic. 立派な and 感心な are more neutral words of praise.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人間関係が希薄になっている。","romaji":"Ningen kankei ga kihaku ni natte iru.","german":"Human relationships are growing thin.","furigana":"にんげんかんけい|きはく"},{"japanese":"高い山では空気が希薄なので、息が苦しくなる。","romaji":"Takai yama de wa kūki ga kihaku na node, iki ga kurushiku naru.","german":"The air is thin on high mountains, so breathing becomes difficult.","furigana":"たか|やま|くうき|きはく|いき|くる"},{"japanese":"社員の危機感が希薄なことが問題だ。","romaji":"Shain no kikikan ga kihaku na koto ga mondai da.","german":"The problem is that employees have little sense of crisis.","furigana":"しゃいん|ききかん|きはく|もんだい"}],"notes":"希薄な literally means \"thin, diluted\" (空気が希薄だ, 酸素が希薄) and figuratively \"weakly developed, lacking\": 人間関係が希薄だ, 危機感が希薄, 当事者意識が希薄. Mostly predicative with が ... 希薄だ or 希薄になる. The everyday words are 薄い and 乏しい \"scanty\"; the variant 稀薄 is rare.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"稀有な才能の持ち主だ。","romaji":"Keu na sainō no mochinushi da.","german":"He is the possessor of a rare talent.","furigana":"けう|さいのう|も|ぬし"},{"japanese":"夫婦がそろって百歳を超えるのは稀有な例だ。","romaji":"Fūfu ga sorotte hyakusai o koeru no wa keu na rei da.","german":"It is a rare case for both husband and wife to live past one hundred.","furigana":"ふうふ|ひゃくさい|こ|けう|れい"},{"japanese":"宇宙から地球を見るという稀有な体験をした。","romaji":"Uchū kara chikyū o miru to iu keu na taiken o shita.","german":"He had the rare experience of seeing the Earth from space.","furigana":"うちゅう|ちきゅう|み|けう|たいけん"}],"notes":"稀有な is a written word for \"extremely rare, extraordinary\", mostly positive or neutral: 稀有な才能, 稀有な例, 稀有な存在, 稀有な体験. It is also written 希有 and read けう (きゆう is rare). In everyday speech people say 珍しい; 稀な (まれな) is also elevated, but more common.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"多大な貢献をした。","romaji":"Tadai na kōken o shita.","german":"He made a considerable contribution.","furigana":"ただい|こうけん"},{"japanese":"このたびは多大なご迷惑をおかけし、申し訳ございません。","romaji":"Kono tabi wa tadai na gomeiwaku o okakeshi, mōshiwake gozaimasen.","german":"We sincerely apologize for the great inconvenience this time.","furigana":"ただい|めいわく|もう|わけ"},{"japanese":"彼の小説は若い作家たちに多大な影響を与えた。","romaji":"Kare no shōsetsu wa wakai sakkatachi ni tadai na eikyō o ataeta.","german":"His novels had a great influence on young writers.","furigana":"かれ|しょうせつ|わか|さっか|ただい|えいきょう|あた"}],"notes":"多大な denotes a \"considerable, great\" amount of something abstract: 多大な貢献, 多大な影響, 多大な損害; very common in formal thank-you and apology formulas (多大なご支援, 多大なご迷惑をおかけしました). It is not used for concrete size (buildings, etc.). 莫大な emphasizes huge sums (莫大な費用), 甚大な serious damage.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"崇高な理念を掲げる。","romaji":"Sūkō na rinen o kakageru.","german":"To uphold lofty ideals.","furigana":"すうこう|りねん|かか"},{"japanese":"命をかけて人を救うのは崇高な行為だ。","romaji":"Inochi o kakete hito o sukuu no wa sūkō na kōi da.","german":"Saving people at the risk of one's life is a noble act.","furigana":"いのち|ひと|すく|すうこう|こうい"},{"japanese":"山頂から見た景色には崇高な美しさがあった。","romaji":"Sanchō kara mita keshiki ni wa sūkō na utsukushisa ga atta.","german":"The view from the summit had a sublime beauty.","furigana":"さんちょう|み|けしき|すうこう|うつく"}],"notes":"崇高な means \"lofty, noble\" and is used for ideals, goals and deeds: 崇高な理念, 崇高な使命, 崇高な精神, 崇高な行為. In aesthetics 崇高 is \"the sublime\". The word is elevated; 高尚な means rather \"refined, cultured\" (taste, hobbies) and can sound aloof, while 気高い is the native \"noble, dignified\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"甚大な被害が出た。","romaji":"Jindai na higai ga deta.","german":"Enormous damage occurred.","furigana":"じんだい|ひがい|で"},{"japanese":"台風による農作物への被害は甚大だった。","romaji":"Taifū ni yoru nōsakumotsu e no higai wa jindai datta.","german":"The damage to crops from the typhoon was enormous.","furigana":"たいふう|のうさくもつ|ひがい|じんだい"},{"japanese":"今回の不祥事は会社の信用に甚大な影響を及ぼした。","romaji":"Konkai no fushōji wa kaisha no shin'yō ni jindai na eikyō o oyoboshita.","german":"The scandal had a grave impact on the company's credibility.","furigana":"こんかい|ふしょうじ|かいしゃ|しんよう|じんだい|えいきょう|およ"}],"notes":"甚大な denotes an \"enormous, serious\" extent, almost always of damage and negative consequences: 甚大な被害, 甚大な損害, 甚大な影響; also predicative, 被害は甚大だ. Typical of news about disasters. 多大な can also describe positive things (多大な貢献), 莫大な emphasizes huge sums of money.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"精緻な模型を作った。","romaji":"Seichi na mokei o tsukutta.","german":"I made a finely detailed model.","furigana":"せいち|もけい|つく"},{"japanese":"この寺の天井には精緻な彫刻が施されている。","romaji":"Kono tera no tenjō ni wa seichi na chōkoku ga hodokosarete iru.","german":"The ceiling of this temple is adorned with elaborate carvings.","furigana":"てら|てんじょう|せいち|ちょうこく|ほどこ"},{"japanese":"彼の論文は精緻な分析で高く評価された。","romaji":"Kare no ronbun wa seichi na bunseki de takaku hyōka sareta.","german":"His paper was highly rated for its meticulous analysis.","furigana":"かれ|ろんぶん|せいち|ぶんせき|たか|ひょうか"}],"notes":"精緻な means \"worked out down to the finest detail, precise\" and praises both manual and intellectual work: 精緻な細工, 精緻な模型, 精緻な分析, 精緻な理論. It is a written word. 精密な emphasizes technical accuracy (精密機械), 緻密な careful, gapless planning (緻密な計画); in everyday speech people say 細かい.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"簡潔な文章を心がける。","romaji":"Kanketsu na bunshō o kokorogakeru.","german":"To strive for concise writing.","furigana":"かんけつ|ぶんしょう|こころ"},{"japanese":"要点を簡潔に説明してください。","romaji":"Yōten o kanketsu ni setsumei shite kudasai.","german":"Please explain the main points concisely.","furigana":"ようてん|かんけつ|せつめい"},{"japanese":"彼のスピーチは簡潔で分かりやすかった。","romaji":"Kare no supīchi wa kanketsu de wakariyasukatta.","german":"His speech was concise and easy to understand.","furigana":"かれ|かんけつ|わ"}],"notes":"簡潔な means \"brief and to the point\" and refers to language: 簡潔な文章, 簡潔な説明; very often adverbial: 簡潔に説明する, 簡潔にまとめる. Do not confuse with 簡単 \"simple, easy\" and 簡素 \"plain, unadorned\". In speech people also say 手短に \"briefly\"; the opposite is 冗漫.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"強靭な精神力を持っている。","romaji":"Kyōjin na seishinryoku o motte iru.","german":"He has tremendous mental toughness.","furigana":"きょうじん|せいしんりょく|も"},{"japanese":"クモの糸は細いが、非常に強靭だ。","romaji":"Kumo no ito wa hosoi ga, hijō ni kyōjin da.","german":"Spider silk is thin but extremely tough.","furigana":"いと|ほそ|ひじょう|きょうじん"},{"japanese":"災害に備えて、強靭なインフラを整備する。","romaji":"Saigai ni sonaete, kyōjin na infura o seibi suru.","german":"In preparation for disasters, we are building resilient infrastructure.","furigana":"さいがい|そな|きょうじん|せいび"}],"notes":"強靭な means \"tough, resilient, durable\" and applies to materials (強靭な繊維), the body (強靭な肉体) and the mind (強靭な精神力); in politics 国土強靭化 is \"strengthening disaster resilience of the land\". Spelling variants: 強靱, 強じん. 頑丈な \"sturdy\" describes objects rather, and 丈夫な is the everyday word.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"獰猛な動物に注意する。","romaji":"Dōmō na dōbutsu ni chūi suru.","german":"Beware of ferocious animals.","furigana":"どうもう|どうぶつ|ちゅうい"},{"japanese":"この犬は見た目は獰猛だが、性格はおとなしい。","romaji":"Kono inu wa mitame wa dōmō da ga, seikaku wa otonashii.","german":"This dog looks fierce but is gentle by nature.","furigana":"いぬ|み|め|どうもう|せいかく"},{"japanese":"サメの中には獰猛な種類もいる。","romaji":"Same no naka ni wa dōmō na shurui mo iru.","german":"Some kinds of sharks are ferocious.","furigana":"なか|どうもう|しゅるい"}],"notes":"獰猛な describes mainly animals as \"aggressive, dangerously wild\": 獰猛な犬, 獰猛な猛獣, 獰猛なサメ; figuratively also a fierce appearance (獰猛な顔つき). Because of the difficult kanji it is often written どう猛. Do not confuse with 野生の \"wild (not domesticated)\"; 凶暴な \"violent\" is also used for people.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"悲惨な事故が起きた。","romaji":"Hisan na jiko ga okita.","german":"A tragic accident occurred.","furigana":"ひさん|じこ|お"},{"japanese":"戦争の悲惨さを次の世代に伝えたい。","romaji":"Sensō no hisansa o tsugi no sedai ni tsutaetai.","german":"I want to convey the horrors of war to the next generation.","furigana":"せんそう|ひさん|つぎ|せだい|つた"},{"japanese":"昨日のテストの結果は悲惨だった。","romaji":"Kinō no tesuto no kekka wa hisan datta.","german":"Yesterday's test results were dismal.","furigana":"きのう|けっか|ひさん"}],"notes":"悲惨な means \"tragic, shocking, wretched\" for accidents and conditions: 悲惨な事故, 悲惨な状況, 戦争の悲惨さ. Colloquially and hyperbolically also \"disastrous\" (テストの結果は悲惨だった). 惨めな emphasizes personal humiliation (\"pitiful\"), 気の毒な sympathy (\"pitiable\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"露骨な差別発言をした。","romaji":"Rokotsu na sabetsu hatsugen o shita.","german":"He made a blatantly discriminatory remark.","furigana":"ろこつ|さべつはつげん"},{"japanese":"頼みごとをしたら、彼は露骨に嫌な顔をした。","romaji":"Tanomigoto o shitara, kare wa rokotsu ni iya na kao o shita.","german":"When I asked him for a favor, he openly made a sour face.","furigana":"たの|かれ|ろこつ|いや|かお"},{"japanese":"この映画には露骨な描写があるので、子どもには向かない。","romaji":"Kono eiga ni wa rokotsu na byōsha ga aru node, kodomo ni wa mukanai.","german":"This film contains explicit depictions, so it is not suitable for children.","furigana":"えいが|ろこつ|びょうしゃ|こ|む"}],"notes":"露骨な means \"unconcealed, blunt\" and describes feelings or intentions that one tactlessly shows openly: 露骨な差別, 露骨に嫌な顔をする; for depictions also \"explicit, suggestive\" (露骨な描写). Almost always negative, very often adverbial 露骨に. あからさまな is similar, whereas 率直な \"frank, honest\" is positive.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"旺盛な好奇心を持つ。","romaji":"Ōsei na kōkishin o motsu.","german":"To have a vigorous curiosity.","furigana":"おうせい|こうきしん|も"},{"japanese":"息子は食欲旺盛で、毎食ご飯を三杯食べる。","romaji":"Musuko wa shokuyoku ōsei de, maishoku gohan o sanbai taberu.","german":"My son has a hearty appetite and eats three bowls of rice at every meal.","furigana":"むすこ|しょくよくおうせい|まいしょく|はん|さんばいた"},{"japanese":"旺盛な需要に支えられて、売り上げが伸びている。","romaji":"Ōsei na juyō ni sasaerarete, uriage ga nobite iru.","german":"Supported by strong demand, sales are growing.","furigana":"おうせい|じゅよう|ささ|う|あ|の"}],"notes":"旺盛な means \"vigorous, strong, full of ...\" and is used with drives and energies: 旺盛な好奇心, 旺盛な食欲, 旺盛な需要. Very often placed after the noun in fixed combinations: 食欲旺盛, 好奇心旺盛, 元気旺盛 (好奇心旺盛な子ども). 盛んな describes brisk activities (スポーツが盛んだ), 活発な lively behavior.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"チームワークは不可欠な要素だ。","romaji":"Chīmuwāku wa fukaketsu na yōso da.","german":"Teamwork is an indispensable element.","furigana":"ふかけつ|ようそ"},{"japanese":"水は生命にとって不可欠だ。","romaji":"Mizu wa seimei ni totte fukaketsu da.","german":"Water is indispensable for life.","furigana":"みず|せいめい|ふかけつ"},{"japanese":"この仕事には英語力が必要不可欠です。","romaji":"Kono shigoto ni wa eigoryoku ga hitsuyō fukaketsu desu.","german":"English ability is absolutely essential for this job.","furigana":"しごと|えいごりょく|ひつようふかけつ"}],"notes":"不可欠な means \"indispensable\" and typically goes with 〜に(とって): 生命にとって不可欠だ, 成功に不可欠な条件. The intensified fixed phrase 必要不可欠 is very common. The word is formal; in conversation people say 欠かせない, and the weaker 必要な means \"necessary\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"変化は不可避なものだ。","romaji":"Henka wa fukahi na mono da.","german":"Change is unavoidable.","furigana":"へんか|ふかひ"},{"japanese":"このままでは値上げは不可避だ。","romaji":"Kono mama de wa neage wa fukahi da.","german":"If this continues, a price increase is unavoidable.","furigana":"ねあ|ふかひ"},{"japanese":"両国の対立は、もはや不可避と見られている。","romaji":"Ryōkoku no tairitsu wa, mohaya fukahi to mirarete iru.","german":"A conflict between the two countries is now seen as inevitable.","furigana":"りょうこく|たいりつ|ふかひ|み"}],"notes":"不可避な means \"unavoidable\" and is a written word from politics, business and science; mostly predicative (〜は不可避だ / 不可避である) or in 不可避と見られる. In conversation people say 避けられない; やむを得ない means \"cannot be helped, out of necessity\", and 必然的 \"inevitable (logically necessary)\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不遜な態度を取る。","romaji":"Fuson na taido o toru.","german":"To take an arrogant attitude.","furigana":"ふそん|たいど|と"},{"japanese":"記者会見での不遜な発言が批判を浴びた。","romaji":"Kisha kaiken de no fuson na hatsugen ga hihan o abita.","german":"His arrogant remark at the press conference drew criticism.","furigana":"きしゃかいけん|ふそん|はつげん|ひはん|あ"},{"japanese":"神をも恐れぬ不遜な行為だ。","romaji":"Kami o mo osorenu fuson na kōi da.","german":"It is a presumptuous act that does not even fear the gods.","furigana":"かみ|おそ|ふそん|こうい"}],"notes":"不遜な means \"arrogant, presumptuous\", especially toward superiors or what deserves respect: 不遜な態度, 不遜な発言; as a four-character compound 傲岸不遜. It is written language. 生意気な \"cheeky, impertinent\" is colloquial and mostly refers to younger people, 横柄な describes a condescending manner.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"冗漫な説明は避けるべきだ。","romaji":"Jōman na setsumei wa sakeru beki da.","german":"Wordy explanations should be avoided.","furigana":"じょうまん|せつめい|さ"},{"japanese":"この小説は中盤が冗漫で、途中で読むのをやめた。","romaji":"Kono shōsetsu wa chūban ga jōman de, tochū de yomu no o yameta.","german":"The middle part of this novel is so long-winded that I stopped reading partway.","furigana":"しょうせつ|ちゅうばん|じょうまん|とちゅう|よ"},{"japanese":"冗漫な部分を削って、記事を半分の長さにした。","romaji":"Jōman na bubun o kezutte, kiji o hanbun no nagasa ni shita.","german":"I cut the wordy parts and made the article half as long.","furigana":"じょうまん|ぶぶん|けず|きじ|はんぶん|なが"}],"notes":"冗漫な means \"verbose, long-winded\" and criticizes texts, speeches or films that are not tightened: 冗漫な文章, 冗漫な説明. It is a written word and the opposite of 簡潔な. 冗長な is very similar (in technology also \"redundant\"), and くどい is the colloquial \"long-winded, repetitive\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"杜撰な管理が事故を招いた。","romaji":"Zusan na kanri ga jiko o maneita.","german":"Sloppy management led to the accident.","furigana":"ずさん|かんり|じこ|まね"},{"japanese":"杜撰な計画のせいで、旅行は散々だった。","romaji":"Zusan na keikaku no sei de, ryokō wa sanzan datta.","german":"Because of the sloppy planning, the trip was a disaster.","furigana":"ずさん|けいかく|りょこう|さんざん"},{"japanese":"警察の杜撰な捜査が批判されている。","romaji":"Keisatsu no zusan na sōsa ga hihan sarete iru.","german":"The police's sloppy investigation is being criticized.","furigana":"けいさつ|ずさん|そうさ|ひはん"}],"notes":"杜撰な means \"sloppy, faulty\" and criticizes above all organization and procedures: 杜撰な管理, 杜撰な計画, 杜撰な捜査, 杜撰な工事. It is mostly written in kana, ずさん, and is typical of news criticism. In everyday speech people say いい加減な or 雑な; 不注意 is an individual lapse of attention.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"データが如実に現状を示している。","romaji":"Dēta ga nyojitsu ni genjō o shimeshite iru.","german":"The data clearly show the current situation.","furigana":"にょじつ|げんじょう|しめ"},{"japanese":"彼の表情が、試合の結果を如実に物語っていた。","romaji":"Kare no hyōjō ga, shiai no kekka o nyojitsu ni monogatatte ita.","german":"His expression vividly told how the match had gone.","furigana":"かれ|ひょうじょう|しあい|けっか|にょじつ|ものがた"},{"japanese":"疲れが如実に顔に出ている。","romaji":"Tsukare ga nyojitsu ni kao ni dete iru.","german":"His fatigue is clearly visible on his face.","furigana":"つか|にょじつ|かお|で"}],"notes":"如実 means \"in accordance with reality\" and is practically used only adverbially as 如実に: 如実に示す, 如実に表す, 如実に物語る, 如実に現れる \"to show clearly, to reflect without embellishment\". The form 如実な is rare. It is a written word; in everyday speech people say はっきり or ありのままに.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"深刻な環境問題に直面している。","romaji":"Shinkoku na kankyō mondai ni chokumen shite iru.","german":"We are facing a serious environmental problem.","furigana":"しんこく|かんきょうもんだい|ちょくめん"},{"japanese":"そんなに深刻な顔をしないで。大したことじゃないよ。","romaji":"Sonna ni shinkoku na kao o shinaide. Taishita koto ja nai yo.","german":"Don't look so serious. It's not a big deal.","furigana":"しんこく|かお|たい"},{"japanese":"地方では医師不足が深刻になっている。","romaji":"Chihō de wa ishi busoku ga shinkoku ni natte iru.","german":"In rural areas, the shortage of doctors is becoming more serious.","furigana":"ちほう|いしぶそく|しんこく"}],"notes":"深刻な means \"serious, grave\" and describes problems and situations: 深刻な問題, 深刻な影響, 深刻な状況; 深刻化する \"to worsen\". For people, 深刻な顔 means \"a serious, worried expression\". Common mistake: a serious attitude (\"to study seriously\") is 真剣, not 深刻. 重大な emphasizes the significance.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"切実な願いを訴えた。","romaji":"Setsujitsu na negai o uttaeta.","german":"He made an earnest plea.","furigana":"せつじつ|ねが|うった"},{"japanese":"子育て世代にとって、保育園不足は切実な問題だ。","romaji":"Kosodate sedai ni totte, hoikuen busoku wa setsujitsu na mondai da.","german":"For the child-rearing generation, the shortage of nurseries is a pressing problem.","furigana":"こそだ|せだい|ほいくえんぶそく|せつじつ|もんだい"},{"japanese":"一人暮らしを始めて、家族のありがたさを切実に感じた。","romaji":"Hitorigurashi o hajimete, kazoku no arigatasa o setsujitsu ni kanjita.","german":"Since I started living alone, I keenly felt how much my family means to me.","furigana":"ひとりぐ|はじ|かぞく|せつじつ|かん"}],"notes":"切実な describes something that concerns one personally and directly: \"pressing, earnest, painfully felt\". Typical: 切実な願い, 切実な問題, 切実な訴え; adverbial 切実に感じる \"to feel keenly firsthand\". 深刻な rather assesses severity objectively, 緊急の urgency in terms of time.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"粗雑な仕事は許されない。","romaji":"Sozatsu na shigoto wa yurusarenai.","german":"Careless work is not acceptable.","furigana":"そざつ|しごと|ゆる"},{"japanese":"荷物を粗雑に扱わないでください。","romaji":"Nimotsu o sozatsu ni atsukawanaide kudasai.","german":"Please do not handle the luggage roughly.","furigana":"にもつ|そざつ|あつか"},{"japanese":"その主張は根拠が粗雑で、説得力に欠ける。","romaji":"Sono shuchō wa konkyo ga sozatsu de, settokuryoku ni kakeru.","german":"That argument is crudely grounded and lacks persuasiveness.","furigana":"しゅちょう|こんきょ|そざつ|せっとくりょく|か"}],"notes":"粗雑な means \"rough, careless, unrefined\" and refers to work, handling of things and arguments: 粗雑な仕事, 粗雑な作り, 粗雑に扱う, 粗雑な議論. In everyday speech people say 雑な. 杜撰な criticizes rather sloppy organization, and 粗末な means \"poor, inferior\" (粗末にする \"to waste, to treat with disrespect\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"陰湿ないじめが問題になっている。","romaji":"Inshitsu na ijime ga mondai ni natte iru.","german":"Insidious bullying has become a problem.","furigana":"いんしつ|もんだい"},{"japanese":"ネット上の陰湿な嫌がらせに悩まされている。","romaji":"Netto-jō no inshitsu na iyagarase ni nayamasarete iru.","german":"I am troubled by malicious harassment on the Internet.","furigana":"じょう|いんしつ|いや|なや"},{"japanese":"日の当たらない陰湿な路地裏を通り抜けた。","romaji":"Hi no ataranai inshitsu na rojiura o tōrinuketa.","german":"We passed through a sunless, damp back alley.","furigana":"ひ|あ|いんしつ|ろじうら|とお|ぬ"}],"notes":"陰湿な describes malice that proceeds in a hidden, wearing way: \"insidious, underhanded\": 陰湿ないじめ, 陰湿な嫌がらせ, 陰湿なやり方. Literally it also means \"shady and damp\" (陰湿な場所). 陰険な characterizes rather a sly person, and 意地悪な is the everyday word for \"mean\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"計画は悉く失敗した。","romaji":"Keikaku wa kotogotoku shippai shita.","german":"The plans all failed.","furigana":"けいかく|ことごと|しっぱい"},{"japanese":"私の提案は会議で悉く却下された。","romaji":"Watashi no teian wa kaigi de kotogotoku kyakka sareta.","german":"My proposals were all rejected at the meeting.","furigana":"わたし|ていあん|かいぎ|ことごと|きゃっか"},{"japanese":"天気予報が悉く外れた一週間だった。","romaji":"Tenki yohō ga kotogotoku hazureta isshūkan datta.","german":"It was a week in which every single weather forecast was wrong.","furigana":"てんきよほう|ことごと|はず|いっしゅうかん"}],"notes":"悉く means \"every single one, completely\" and is often used with negative outcomes: 悉く失敗する, 悉く却下される, 予想が悉く外れる. It is written language and mostly written in kana, ことごとく. Neutral are すべて and 全部; 軒並み emphasizes \"across the board, on a broad front\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"企業の業績が軒並み悪化した。","romaji":"Kigyō no gyōseki ga nokinami akka shita.","german":"Corporate earnings worsened across the board.","furigana":"きぎょう|ぎょうせき|のきな|あっか"},{"japanese":"台風の影響で、新幹線は軒並み運休となった。","romaji":"Taifū no eikyō de, shinkansen wa nokinami unkyū to natta.","german":"Because of the typhoon, Shinkansen trains were canceled one after another.","furigana":"たいふう|えいきょう|しんかんせん|のきな|うんきゅう"},{"japanese":"4月から食品が軒並み値上がりした。","romaji":"Shigatsu kara shokuhin ga nokinami neagari shita.","german":"Food prices have gone up across the board since April.","furigana":"がつ|しょくひん|のきな|ねあ"}],"notes":"軒並み literally means \"a row of houses\" (軒 = eaves), but as an adverb it means \"across the board, one after another, extensively\": 軒並み値上がりする, 軒並み運休する, 業績が軒並み悪化する. Typical of business and transport news. 一斉に emphasizes simultaneity, 悉く exclusion of no exception.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"経過を逐一報告する。","romaji":"Keika o chikuichi hōkoku suru.","german":"To report the progress point by point.","furigana":"けいか|ちくいちほうこく"},{"japanese":"母は私の行動を逐一チェックしたがる。","romaji":"Haha wa watashi no kōdō o chikuichi chekku shitagaru.","german":"My mother wants to check my every move.","furigana":"はは|わたし|こうどう|ちくいち"},{"japanese":"新しい情報が入り次第、逐一お知らせします。","romaji":"Atarashii jōhō ga hairi shidai, chikuichi oshirase shimasu.","german":"We will keep you informed one by one as new information comes in.","furigana":"あたら|じょうほう|はい|しだい|ちくいち|し"}],"notes":"逐一 means \"one by one, point by point, without gaps\" and stands before verbs of reporting and checking: 逐一報告する, 逐一説明する, 逐一チェックする, 逐一お知らせする. It is rather formal and matter-of-fact. The colloquial いちいち means the same, but often sounds annoyed (\"every time, over every little thing\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"失敗したら尚更努力すべきだ。","romaji":"Shippai shitara naosara doryoku subeki da.","german":"If you fail, you should make all the more effort.","furigana":"しっぱい|なおさらどりょく"},{"japanese":"雨が降るなら、尚更早く出かけたほうがいい。","romaji":"Ame ga furu nara, naosara hayaku dekaketa hō ga ii.","german":"If it is going to rain, you had better leave all the earlier.","furigana":"あめ|ふ|なおさらはや|で"},{"japanese":"止められると、尚更やりたくなる。","romaji":"Tomerareru to, naosara yaritaku naru.","german":"When I'm told to stop, I want to do it all the more.","furigana":"と|なおさら"}],"notes":"尚更 means \"all the more, even more so\" and intensifies an inference: 〜なら尚更だ, それなら尚更, 止められると尚更やりたくなる. With a negative it means \"even less\". Mostly written in kana, なおさら. ましてや \"let alone\" introduces a more extreme case, and 余計に is colloquial and often negatively colored.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"矢継ぎ早に質問が飛んだ。","romaji":"Yatsugibaya ni shitsumon ga tonda.","german":"Questions flew in rapid succession.","furigana":"やつ|ばや|しつもん|と"},{"japanese":"面接官から矢継ぎ早に質問されて、頭が真っ白になった。","romaji":"Mensetsukan kara yatsugibaya ni shitsumon sarete, atama ga masshiro ni natta.","german":"Bombarded with questions by the interviewer, my mind went blank.","furigana":"めんせつかん|やつ|ばや|しつもん|あたま|ま|しろ"},{"japanese":"その会社は矢継ぎ早に新製品を発表している。","romaji":"Sono kaisha wa yatsugibaya ni shinseihin o happyō shite iru.","german":"That company is releasing new products one after another.","furigana":"かいしゃ|やつ|ばや|しんせいひん|はっぴょう"}],"notes":"矢継ぎ早 comes from quickly nocking one arrow after another and means \"in rapid succession, thick and fast\". Most often with questions: 矢継ぎ早に質問する, 矢継ぎ早の質問; but also 矢継ぎ早に新製品を出す, 対策を打ち出す. More neutral are 次々に \"one after another\" and 立て続けに \"in a row\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"辛辣に批判された。","romaji":"Shinratsu ni hihan sareta.","german":"He was sharply criticized.","furigana":"しんらつ|ひはん"},{"japanese":"彼女は映画評論家として、辛辣な批評で知られている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa eiga hyōronka to shite, shinratsu na hihyō de shirarete iru.","german":"As a film critic, she is known for her scathing reviews.","furigana":"かのじょ|えいがひょうろんか|しんらつ|ひひょう|し"},{"japanese":"祖父は優しいが、政治については辛辣に語る。","romaji":"Sofu wa yasashii ga, seiji ni tsuite wa shinratsu ni kataru.","german":"My grandfather is kind, but he speaks bitingly about politics.","furigana":"そふ|やさ|せいじ|しんらつ|かた"}],"notes":"辛辣 means \"biting, sharp-tongued\" and refers to criticism and words: 辛辣に批判する, 辛辣な批評, 辛辣な皮肉, 辛辣な口調. A person who likes to talk like that has a 毒舌 \"sharp tongue\". 厳しく is more general \"strictly\"; 痛烈に emphasizes the force, 手厳しい the unforgiving nature.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"状況は一向に改善しない。","romaji":"Jōkyō wa ikkō ni kaizen shinai.","german":"The situation is not improving at all.","furigana":"じょうきょう|いっこう|かいぜん"},{"japanese":"薬を飲んでいるのに、熱が一向に下がらない。","romaji":"Kusuri o nonde iru noni, netsu ga ikkō ni sagaranai.","german":"Even though I'm taking medicine, my fever won't go down at all.","furigana":"くすり|の|ねつ|いっこう|さ"},{"japanese":"私は一向に構いませんよ。","romaji":"Watashi wa ikkō ni kamaimasen yo.","german":"I don't mind at all.","furigana":"わたし|いっこう|かま"}],"notes":"一向に is almost always used with a negative and means \"not at all, not in the least\", often with the undertone that one is waiting in vain for a change: 一向に良くならない, 一向に連絡が来ない. The fixed phrase 一向に構わない means \"I don't mind at all\". Compared with 全然 and 少しも, 一向に sounds more like written language and emphasizes the absence despite expectation. The positive meaning \"entirely\" is obsolete.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"体調は頗る良好だ。","romaji":"Taichō wa sukoburu ryōkō da.","german":"My physical condition is extremely good.","furigana":"たいちょう|すこぶ|りょうこう"},{"japanese":"今日の部長はすこぶる機嫌がいい。","romaji":"Kyō no buchō wa sukoburu kigen ga ii.","german":"The department head is in an especially good mood today.","furigana":"きょう|ぶちょう|きげん"},{"japanese":"新しいパン屋の評判は頗るいい。","romaji":"Atarashii pan'ya no hyōban wa sukoburu ii.","german":"The new bakery has an excellent reputation.","furigana":"あたら|や|ひょうばん|すこぶ"}],"notes":"頗る is an intensifying adverb meaning \"extremely, decidedly\" and stands before adjectives or expressions of state: 頗る元気, 頗る機嫌がいい, 頗る評判がいい. It sounds somewhat old-fashioned to elevated and is often used in a jokingly formal way; in everyday speech people more often say とても or すごく. It is mostly written in kana (すこぶる).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"批判するより寧ろ協力すべきだ。","romaji":"Hihan suru yori mushiro kyōryoku subeki da.","german":"Rather than criticizing, we should cooperate.","furigana":"ひはん|むし|きょうりょく"},{"japanese":"今日は寒いというより、寧ろ涼しいくらいだ。","romaji":"Kyō wa samui to iu yori, mushiro suzushii kurai da.","german":"Today is not so much cold as rather cool.","furigana":"きょう|さむ|むし|すず"},{"japanese":"あの失敗は、寧ろ良い経験になった。","romaji":"Ano shippai wa, mushiro yoi keiken ni natta.","german":"That failure turned out to be, if anything, a good experience.","furigana":"しっぱい|むし|よ|けいけん"}],"notes":"寧ろ means \"rather, instead\" and, in a comparison, picks the more fitting or better of two possibilities; typical frames are AよりむしろB and AというよりむしろB. It often corrects an expectation: the failure was むしろ a good experience. Unlike かえって (\"on the contrary, all the more\", an unexpected opposite effect), むしろ expresses a weighing of options. Usually written in kana.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"助けようとして却って迷惑をかけた。","romaji":"Tasukeyō to shite kaette meiwaku o kaketa.","german":"I tried to help but ended up causing trouble instead.","furigana":"たす|かえ|めいわく"},{"japanese":"タクシーで行ったら、渋滞で却って時間がかかった。","romaji":"Takushī de ittara, jūtai de kaette jikan ga kakatta.","german":"Going by taxi actually took longer because of traffic.","furigana":"い|じゅうたい|かえ|じかん"},{"japanese":"薬を飲みすぎると、却って体に悪い。","romaji":"Kusuri o nomisugiru to, kaette karada ni warui.","german":"Taking too much medicine is actually bad for the body.","furigana":"くすり|の|かえ|からだ|わる"}],"notes":"却って describes a result that runs counter to what was intended or expected: you wanted to help and ended up doing harm, かえって. Typical after clauses with て, のに or たら. Unlike むしろ (a deliberate weighing, \"rather\"), かえって stresses the unexpected opposite effect. Usually written in kana; not to be confused with the verb 帰って.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仕事は粗方終わった。","romaji":"Shigoto wa arakata owatta.","german":"The work is mostly finished.","furigana":"しごと|あらかたお"},{"japanese":"引っ越しの荷物は粗方片付いた。","romaji":"Hikkoshi no nimotsu wa arakata katazuita.","german":"The moving boxes have mostly been put away.","furigana":"ひ|こ|にもつ|あらかたかたづ"},{"japanese":"説明を聞いて、話の内容はあらかた理解できた。","romaji":"Setsumei o kiite, hanashi no naiyō wa arakata rikai dekita.","german":"After listening to the explanation, I was able to understand most of what was said.","furigana":"せつめい|き|はなし|ないよう|りかい"}],"notes":"粗方 means \"for the most part, nearly\" and describes that something is almost completely done or understood: あらかた終わった, あらかた片付いた. It is somewhat more colloquial than 大体 or ほとんど and is mostly written in kana. As a noun it denotes \"the greater part\": 客のあらかたが帰った.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"何分初めてのことなのでご容赦ください。","romaji":"Nanibun hajimete no koto na node go-yōsha kudasai.","german":"Since this is my first time, please be lenient with me.","furigana":"なにぶんはじ|ようしゃ"},{"japanese":"何分古い建物なので、修理に費用がかかります。","romaji":"Nanibun furui tatemono na node, shūri ni hiyō ga kakarimasu.","german":"Since it is an old building, repairs cost money.","furigana":"なにぶんふる|たてもの|しゅうり|ひよう"},{"japanese":"不慣れな点もございますが、何分よろしくお願いいたします。","romaji":"Funare na ten mo gozaimasu ga, nanibun yoroshiku onegai itashimasu.","german":"I may still be inexperienced in some respects, but I sincerely ask for your support.","furigana":"ふな|てん|なにぶん|ねが"}],"notes":"何分 (なにぶん) is used mainly in polite speech as a reason and an apology: \"since, after all\", typically with ので: 何分初めてなので…. Second, it reinforces requests: 何分よろしくお願いいたします \"I sincerely ask this of you\". Not to be confused with 何分 (なんぷん) \"how many minutes\". Also intensified as 何分にも.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"飽くまで自分の意見を貫く。","romaji":"Aku made jibun no iken o tsuranuku.","german":"I stick to my own opinion to the end.","furigana":"あ|じぶん|いけん|つらぬ"},{"japanese":"これはあくまで私個人の意見です。","romaji":"Kore wa aku made watashi kojin no iken desu.","german":"This is only my personal opinion.","furigana":"わたしこじん|いけん"},{"japanese":"表示の時間は飽くまで目安です。","romaji":"Hyōji no jikan wa aku made meyasu desu.","german":"The time shown is only a rough guide.","furigana":"ひょうじ|じかん|あ|めやす"}],"notes":"飽くまで has two uses. First, \"to the very end, persistently\": あくまで反対する, あくまで戦う. Second, very common in modern everyday speech, as a limiting \"only, merely\": あくまで個人の意見です, あくまで目安です - one stresses that something is no more than that. Usually written in kana; also intensified as あくまでも.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"殆どの人が賛成した。","romaji":"Hotondo no hito ga sansei shita.","german":"Almost everyone agreed.","furigana":"ほとん|ひと|さんせい"},{"japanese":"宿題はほとんど終わった。","romaji":"Shukudai wa hotondo owatta.","german":"The homework is almost done.","furigana":"しゅくだい|お"},{"japanese":"昨夜は心配で殆ど眠れなかった。","romaji":"Sakuya wa shinpai de hotondo nemurenakatta.","german":"I could hardly sleep last night because of worry.","furigana":"さくや|しんぱい|ほとん|ねむ"}],"notes":"殆ど means \"almost, nearly\": ほとんど終わった. With a negative it means \"hardly\": ほとんど寝ていない. As a noun with の it stands for \"the majority, almost all\": ほとんどの人. In everyday writing it is almost always written in kana; the kanji spelling is a typical reading exercise at N1 level.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4 (source: N4; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"勿論お手伝いします。","romaji":"Mochiron otetsudai shimasu.","german":"Of course I'll help you.","furigana":"もちろん|てつだ"},{"japanese":"「一緒に行く？」「もちろん！」","romaji":"\"Issho ni iku?\" \"Mochiron!\"","german":"\"Are you coming along?\" \"Of course!\"","furigana":"いっしょ|い"},{"japanese":"彼は英語は勿論、中国語も話せる。","romaji":"Kare wa eigo wa mochiron, chūgokugo mo hanaseru.","german":"He can speak not only English but also Chinese, of course.","furigana":"かれ|えいご|もちろん|ちゅうごくご|はな"}],"notes":"勿論 means \"of course, naturally\" and stands alone as an answer (もちろん！) or before a statement. The pattern AはもちろんB(も) means \"not only A, but of course B as well\". In everyday writing it is almost always in kana; the kanji 勿論 are seen in older or formal texts. More polite in replies is もちろんです.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4 (source: N4; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は頻りに時計を見ていた。","romaji":"Kare wa shikiri ni tokei o mite ita.","german":"He kept looking at his watch.","furigana":"かれ|しき|とけい|み"},{"japanese":"夜中に犬が頻りに吠えている。","romaji":"Yonaka ni inu ga shikiri ni hoete iru.","german":"A dog is barking incessantly in the middle of the night.","furigana":"よなか|いぬ|しき|ほ"},{"japanese":"母は私にしきりに留学を勧めた。","romaji":"Haha wa watashi ni shikiri ni ryūgaku o susumeta.","german":"My mother earnestly urged me to study abroad.","furigana":"はは|わたし|りゅうがく|すす"}],"notes":"頻りに describes an action that repeats conspicuously often (\"constantly, incessantly\"): しきりに時計を見る, 犬がしきりに吠える. With verbs such as 勧める, 頼む, 感心する it means \"earnestly, insistently\". It sounds somewhat more literary than 何度も or ずっと and is usually written in kana.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"結果は思いの外良かった。","romaji":"Kekka wa omoi no hoka yokatta.","german":"The result was unexpectedly good.","furigana":"けっか|おも|ほかよ"},{"japanese":"駅までは思いの外遠かった。","romaji":"Eki made wa omoi no hoka tōkatta.","german":"It was farther to the station than I expected.","furigana":"えき|おも|ほかとお"},{"japanese":"片付けに思いの外時間がかかってしまった。","romaji":"Katazuke ni omoi no hoka jikan ga kakatte shimatta.","german":"The cleanup took surprisingly long.","furigana":"かたづ|おも|ほかじかん"}],"notes":"思いの外 means \"contrary to expectation, surprisingly\" and expresses that something turns out differently (usually stronger or better) than thought: 思いの外簡単だった, 思いの外時間がかかった. In content it corresponds to 意外に or 予想以上に, but sounds somewhat more elevated. Literally \"outside of what was thought\"; with の it can also be used attributively: 思いの外の結果.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"辛くも難を逃れた。","romaji":"Karakumo nan o nogareta.","german":"He narrowly escaped disaster.","furigana":"から|なん|のが"},{"japanese":"決勝戦は一対〇で辛くも勝利した。","romaji":"Kesshōsen wa ittai zero de karakumo shōri shita.","german":"They narrowly won the final 1-0.","furigana":"けっしょうせん||から|しょうり"},{"japanese":"彼は前回の選挙で辛くも当選した。","romaji":"Kare wa zenkai no senkyo de karakumo tōsen shita.","german":"He narrowly won election in the last election.","furigana":"かれ|ぜんかい|せんきょ|から|とうせん"}],"notes":"辛くも means \"narrowly, only just\" and stands before verbs of succeeding or escaping: 辛くも勝つ, 辛くも逃れる, 辛くも当選する. It is written language and typical of news and sports reports; in conversation people more often say ぎりぎり or かろうじて. Do not confuse with 辛い (からい \"spicy\" / つらい \"hard, painful\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"噂は忽ち広まった。","romaji":"Uwasa wa tachimachi hiromatta.","german":"The rumor spread instantly.","furigana":"うわさ|たちま|ひろ"},{"japanese":"その新商品は発売されると、忽ち売り切れた。","romaji":"Sono shinshōhin wa hatsubai sareru to, tachimachi urikireta.","german":"As soon as that new product went on sale, it sold out.","furigana":"しんしょうひん|はつばい|たちま|う|き"},{"japanese":"空が暗くなり、たちまち大雨になった。","romaji":"Sora ga kuraku nari, tachimachi ōame ni natta.","german":"The sky grew dark, and in no time it was pouring.","furigana":"そら|くら|おおあめ"}],"notes":"忽ち means \"in a flash, immediately\" and describes a change that occurs very quickly and often without anyone doing anything: たちまち売り切れる, たちまち広まる. Unlike すぐに (also used for deliberate actions: すぐに行きます), たちまち describes processes rather than actions and sounds narrative. Usually written in kana.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"畢竟、努力が物を言う。","romaji":"Hikkyō, doryoku ga mono o iu.","german":"In the end, effort is what counts.","furigana":"ひっきょう|どりょく|もの|い"},{"japanese":"畢竟、人間は一人では生きられないのだ。","romaji":"Hikkyō, ningen wa hitori de wa ikirarenai no da.","german":"In the final analysis, human beings cannot live alone.","furigana":"ひっきょう|にんげん|ひとり|い"},{"japanese":"彼の言い訳は、畢竟責任逃れにすぎない。","romaji":"Kare no iiwake wa, hikkyō sekinin nogare ni suginai.","german":"His excuses are, in the end, nothing but an attempt to dodge responsibility.","furigana":"かれ|い|わけ|ひっきょうせきにんのが"}],"notes":"畢竟 is an elevated, written adverb meaning \"in the end, fundamentally\" and introduces a summarizing conclusion, often at the start of a sentence followed by a comma. In content it corresponds to 結局 or つまり, but seems literary to archaic. Frequently combined with 〜にすぎない or 〜のだ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"情報を逐次更新する。","romaji":"Jōhō o chikuji kōshin suru.","german":"To update information continuously.","furigana":"じょうほう|ちくじこうしん"},{"japanese":"進捗状況は逐次報告してください。","romaji":"Shinchoku jōkyō wa chikuji hōkoku shite kudasai.","german":"Please report on progress as it develops.","furigana":"しんちょくじょうきょう|ちくじほうこく"},{"japanese":"会議では逐次通訳が付きます。","romaji":"Kaigi de wa chikuji tsūyaku ga tsukimasu.","german":"Consecutive interpreting will be provided at the conference.","furigana":"かいぎ|ちくじつうやく|つ"}],"notes":"逐次 means \"one after another, continuously, step by step\" and describes that something happens continuously in individual steps: 逐次報告する, 逐次更新する. It is a formal word from administration, business and technology; in everyday language it corresponds to 順次 or その都度. Well-known compounds are 逐次通訳 \"consecutive interpreting\" and 逐次処理.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その知らせは遍く広まった。","romaji":"Sono shirase wa amaneku hiromatta.","german":"The news spread everywhere.","furigana":"し|あまね|ひろ"},{"japanese":"彼の名は遍く世に知られている。","romaji":"Kare no na wa amaneku yo ni shirarete iru.","german":"His name is known all over the world.","furigana":"かれ|な|あまね|よ|し"},{"japanese":"政府は支援が遍く国民に行き渡るよう努めている。","romaji":"Seifu wa shien ga amaneku kokumin ni ikiwataru yō tsutomete iru.","german":"The government is striving to make sure the support reaches all citizens.","furigana":"せいふ|しえん|あまね|こくみん|い|わた|つと"}],"notes":"遍く is a written adverb meaning \"everywhere, generally, without exception\" and emphasizes that something reaches every part of an area: 遍く知られる, 遍く行き渡る. In everyday speech people more often say 広く or みんなに. Typical are verbs of spreading and being known; mostly written in kana.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"由々しい事態に発展した。","romaji":"Yuyushii jitai ni hatten shita.","german":"It developed into a serious situation.","furigana":"ゆゆ|じたい|はってん"},{"japanese":"これは会社の信用に関わる由々しい問題だ。","romaji":"Kore wa kaisha no shin'yō ni kakawaru yuyushii mondai da.","german":"This is a serious problem that affects the company's credibility.","furigana":"かいしゃ|しんよう|かか|ゆゆ|もんだい"},{"japanese":"少子化は国にとって由々しき事態だ。","romaji":"Shōshika wa kuni ni totte yuyushiki jitai da.","german":"The falling birthrate is an alarming situation for the country.","furigana":"しょうしか|くに|ゆゆ|じたい"}],"notes":"由々しい means \"serious, grave, alarming\" and describes situations that can have serious consequences: 由々しい問題, 由々しい事態. Very common is the classical attributive form 由々しき: 由々しき事態. The word is formal and typical of news, speeches and editorials; more neutral are 深刻な or 重大な.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"凡そ三時間かかる。","romaji":"Oyoso sanjikan kakaru.","german":"It takes about three hours.","furigana":"およ|さんじかん"},{"japanese":"そんな乱暴な言い方は、およそ彼らしくない。","romaji":"Sonna ranbō na iikata wa, oyoso kare rashiku nai.","german":"Such a rough way of speaking is not at all like him.","furigana":"らんぼう|い|かた|かれ"},{"japanese":"話の凡そは分かりました。","romaji":"Hanashi no oyoso wa wakarimashita.","german":"I understood the gist of the story.","furigana":"はなし|およ|わ"}],"notes":"凡そ mostly means \"about, approximately\" before numbers: およそ三時間. With a negative or negatively valued expressions it means \"(not) at all\": およそ彼らしくない. As a noun it stands for \"the essentials, the rough outline\": 話のおよそは分かった. Compared with 約 and 大体, およそ sounds somewhat more elevated; usually written in kana.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"兎に角やってみよう。","romaji":"Tonikaku yatte miyō.","german":"Let's just try it anyway.","furigana":"と|かく"},{"japanese":"とにかく、無事でよかった。","romaji":"Tonikaku, buji de yokatta.","german":"Anyway, I'm glad you're safe.","furigana":"ぶじ"},{"japanese":"あの店は兎に角安い。","romaji":"Ano mise wa tonikaku yasui.","german":"That shop is just really cheap.","furigana":"みせ|と|かくやす"}],"notes":"兎に角 means \"anyway, in any case\" and sets aside secondary matters in order to get to the essential point or to action: とにかくやってみよう. Colloquially it also intensifies, \"simply, really\": とにかく安い. Close synonyms are ともかく (more common in Nはともかく \"setting N aside\") and いずれにしても. The kanji spelling 兎に角 is ateji and it is almost only written in kana.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"態々来ていただきありがとうございます。","romaji":"Wazawaza kite itadaki arigatō gozaimasu.","german":"Thank you for coming all this way.","furigana":"わざわざき"},{"japanese":"わざわざ買いに行ったのに、売り切れだった。","romaji":"Wazawaza kai ni itta noni, urikire datta.","german":"I went out of my way to buy it, but it was sold out.","furigana":"か|い|う|き"},{"japanese":"彼は態々聞こえるように悪口を言った。","romaji":"Kare wa wazawaza kikoeru yō ni waruguchi o itta.","german":"He deliberately badmouthed me so that I could hear it.","furigana":"かれ|わざわざき|わるぐち|い"}],"notes":"態々 means \"specially, expressly, going to the trouble\" and stands before an action that would not actually have been necessary. As thanks it is very common: わざわざありがとうございます. In a negative context it means \"deliberately, needlessly\": わざわざ聞こえるように言う. Distinction: わざと emphasizes only the intention (often malicious), わざわざ the unnecessary effort. Almost always written in kana.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"こうなっては致し方ない。","romaji":"Kō natte wa itashikata nai.","german":"Now that it has come to this, it can't be helped.","furigana":"いた|かた"},{"japanese":"悪天候のため、中止も致し方ありません。","romaji":"Akutenkō no tame, chūshi mo itashikata arimasen.","german":"Because of the bad weather, cancellation is unavoidable.","furigana":"あくてんこう|ちゅうし|いた|かた"},{"japanese":"約束を三回も破られたら、彼女が怒るのも致し方ない。","romaji":"Yakusoku o sankai mo yaburaretara, kanojo ga okoru no mo itashikata nai.","german":"If a promise were broken three times, it is understandable that she would be angry.","furigana":"やくそく|さんかい|やぶ|かのじょ|おこ|いた|かた"}],"notes":"致し方ない is the polite, formal variant of 仕方がない: \"it can't be helped, there is no other choice\". 致す is the humble form of する. It is heard in business language and speeches, often as 致し方ありません or 致し方ないことです. For a justified reaction of others: 怒るのも致し方ない \"it is understandable that ...\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"健康が大切なのは言うまでもない。","romaji":"Kenkō ga taisetsu na no wa iu made mo nai.","german":"It goes without saying that health is important.","furigana":"けんこう|たいせつ|い"},{"japanese":"言うまでもなく、試験中の私語は禁止です。","romaji":"Iu made mo naku, shiken-chū no shigo wa kinshi desu.","german":"Needless to say, talking during the exam is prohibited.","furigana":"い|しけんちゅう|しご|きんし"},{"japanese":"彼は英語は言うまでもなく、フランス語も話せる。","romaji":"Kare wa eigo wa iu made mo naku, furansugo mo hanaseru.","german":"Not to mention English, he can also speak French.","furigana":"かれ|えいご|い|ご|はな"}],"notes":"言うまでもない means \"it goes without saying, of course\". At the end of a sentence: 〜のは言うまでもない. Adverbially at the start of a sentence: 言うまでもなく, \"needless to say\". The pattern Aは言うまでもなく、Bも means \"not only A, but B all the more\", similar to Aはもちろん. Neutral to formal; in conversation people also say もちろん.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"成功したのは努力の結果に他ならない。","romaji":"Seikō shita no wa doryoku no kekka ni hoka naranai.","german":"The success is nothing other than the result of effort.","furigana":"せいこう|どりょく|けっか|ほか"},{"japanese":"彼女が選ばれたのは、実力が認められたからに他ならない。","romaji":"Kanojo ga erabareta no wa, jitsuryoku ga mitomerareta kara ni hoka naranai.","german":"She was chosen for no other reason than that her ability was recognized.","furigana":"かのじょ|えら|じつりょく|みと|ほか"},{"japanese":"他ならぬあなたの頼みなら、断れません。","romaji":"Hoka naranu anata no tanomi nara, kotowaremasen.","german":"If it's a request from you of all people, I can't refuse.","furigana":"ほか|たの|ことわ"}],"notes":"他ならない (mostly as Nに他ならない or 〜からに他ならない) emphatically asserts \"is nothing other than, is solely\". It is written language and typical of essays and speeches. The attributive form 他ならぬ means \"none other than\" or \"precisely (this person)\": 他ならぬあなたの頼みなら. Close in content: 〜そのものだ, 〜以外の何物でもない. Often in kana: ほかならない.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"見るに堪えない光景だった。","romaji":"Miru ni taenai kōkei datta.","german":"It was a sight too awful to look at.","furigana":"み|た|こうけい"},{"japanese":"彼の演説は聞くに堪えない内容だった。","romaji":"Kare no enzetsu wa kiku ni taenai naiyō datta.","german":"The content of his speech was hardly bearable to listen to.","furigana":"かれ|えんぜつ|き|た|ないよう"},{"japanese":"皆様のご支援には感謝に堪えません。","romaji":"Minasama no go-shien ni wa kansha ni taemasen.","german":"I cannot thank you enough for your support.","furigana":"みなさま|しえん|かんしゃ|た"}],"notes":"After a verb of perception (見る, 聞く, 読む), に堪えない means \"unbearable to, not worth\": 見るに堪えない光景. After emotion nouns such as 感謝, 遺憾, 同情, by contrast, it means \"extremely, cannot help but\": 感謝に堪えません \"I am deeply grateful\". Both uses are written language and formal. Do not confuse with 絶えない \"incessant\" (笑顔が絶えない).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"計画の変更を余儀なくされた。","romaji":"Keikaku no henkō o yogi naku sareta.","german":"We were forced to change the plan.","furigana":"けいかく|へんこう|よぎ"},{"japanese":"台風の影響で、多くの便が欠航を余儀なくされた。","romaji":"Taifū no eikyō de, ōku no bin ga kekkō o yogi naku sareta.","german":"Because of the typhoon, many flights were forced to be canceled.","furigana":"たいふう|えいきょう|おお|びん|けっこう|よぎ"},{"japanese":"彼は怪我のため、引退を余儀なくされた。","romaji":"Kare wa kega no tame, intai o yogi naku sareta.","german":"Because of an injury, he was forced to retire.","furigana":"かれ|けが|いんたい|よぎ"}],"notes":"Nを余儀なくされる means \"to be forced to N\" by external circumstances, against one's will. N is usually a Sino-Japanese action noun: 中止, 変更, 撤退, 引退, 欠航. The pattern is very common in news. The active form Xが Yに Nを余儀なくさせる \"X forces Y to N\" is rarer. As an adverb: 余儀なく〜する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"現実に即した対策を講じる。","romaji":"Genjitsu ni sokushita taisaku o kōjiru.","german":"To take measures that suit reality.","furigana":"げんじつ|そく|たいさく|こう"},{"japanese":"事実に即して報告してください。","romaji":"Jijitsu ni sokushite hōkoku shite kudasai.","german":"Please report in line with the facts.","furigana":"じじつ|そく|ほうこく"},{"japanese":"具体例に即して説明すると、分かりやすくなる。","romaji":"Gutairei ni sokushite setsumei suru to, wakariyasuku naru.","german":"Explaining it using concrete examples makes it easier to understand.","furigana":"ぐたいれい|そく|せつめい|わ"}],"notes":"Nに即して means \"in accordance with, in conformity with\" and requires that one orient oneself by concrete facts or circumstances: 事実に即して, 実情に即して, 現実に即して. Before a noun the form is Nに即した: 現実に即した対策. It is formal and typical of reports and administrative language. For rules and laws, 則して (法律に則して) is written more often, or people say に従って.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"調査結果を踏まえて判断する。","romaji":"Chōsa kekka o fumaete handan suru.","german":"To judge based on the survey results.","furigana":"ちょうさけっか|ふ|はんだん"},{"japanese":"前回の反省を踏まえて、今回は早めに準備した。","romaji":"Zenkai no hansei o fumaete, konkai wa hayame ni junbi shita.","german":"Learning from the previous time, we prepared early this time.","furigana":"ぜんかい|はんせい|ふ|こんかい|はや|じゅんび"},{"japanese":"お客様の声を踏まえた新商品を開発しました。","romaji":"Okyakusama no koe o fumaeta shinshōhin o kaihatsu shimashita.","german":"We have developed a new product that reflects customers' feedback.","furigana":"きゃくさま|こえ|ふ|しんしょうひん|かいはつ"}],"notes":"Nを踏まえて means \"taking N into account, on the basis of N\": results, experiences or opinions are taken as the starting point for a decision. Typical N are 結果, 経験, 反省, 意見, 現状. Before a noun: Nを踏まえた提案. Unlike に基づいて (\"strictly relying on\", e.g. data, laws), を踏まえて emphasizes incorporating N into one's own thinking. Formal, very common in business language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"会議に先立って資料を配布した。","romaji":"Kaigi ni sakidatte shiryō o haifu shita.","german":"Materials were distributed ahead of the meeting.","furigana":"かいぎ|さきだ|しりょう|はいふ"},{"japanese":"映画の公開に先立って、試写会が開かれた。","romaji":"Eiga no kōkai ni sakidatte, shishakai ga hirakareta.","german":"A preview screening was held prior to the film's release.","furigana":"えいが|こうかい|さきだ|ししゃかい|ひら"},{"japanese":"試合に先立ち、黙祷が捧げられた。","romaji":"Shiai ni sakidachi, mokutō ga sasagerareta.","german":"A moment of silence was offered before the match.","furigana":"しあい|さきだ||ささ"}],"notes":"Nに先立って means \"before, in the run-up to\" and indicates that something is done as preparation ahead of an important event: 開会に先立って, 発売に先立って. In writing also に先立ち, and before a noun に先立つ. It is more formal and ceremonial than 前に. The verb 先立つ alone can also mean \"to die before someone\" (親に先立つ).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"危険を顧みず人を助けた。","romaji":"Kiken o kaerimizu hito o tasuketa.","german":"He helped someone regardless of the danger.","furigana":"きけん|かえり|ひと|たす"},{"japanese":"父は家庭を顧みず、仕事ばかりしていた。","romaji":"Chichi wa katei o kaerimizu, shigoto bakari shite ita.","german":"My father neglected his family and did nothing but work.","furigana":"ちち|かてい|かえり|しごと"},{"japanese":"周囲の迷惑を顧みず、夜中に大声で騒ぐ人がいる。","romaji":"Shūi no meiwaku o kaerimizu, yonaka ni ōgoe de sawagu hito ga iru.","german":"Some people make loud noise in the middle of the night, with no regard for those around them.","furigana":"しゅうい|めいわく|かえり|よなか|おおごえ|さわ|ひと"}],"notes":"Nを顧みず means \"without regard for N\"; ず is the classical negation (= 顧みないで). Typical N are 危険, 身の危険, 家庭, 周囲の迷惑. Depending on context it sounds heroic (危険を顧みず助ける) or reproachful (家庭を顧みず仕事ばかり). Do not confuse with 省みる \"to reflect on oneself\" (自らを省みる). Written language and formal.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"成績は悪く、数学に至っては赤点だった。","romaji":"Seiseki wa waruku, sūgaku ni itatte wa akaten datta.","german":"His grades were poor, and in math he even got a failing mark.","furigana":"せいせき|わる|すうがく|いた|あかてん"},{"japanese":"家族はみんな朝が弱いが、父に至っては昼まで寝ている。","romaji":"Kazoku wa minna asa ga yowai ga, chichi ni itatte wa hiru made nete iru.","german":"Everyone in my family is bad at mornings, and my father even sleeps until noon.","furigana":"かぞく|あさ|よわ|ちち|いた|ひる|ね"},{"japanese":"参加者は少なく、若者に至っては一人もいなかった。","romaji":"Sankasha wa sukunaku, wakamono ni itatte wa hitori mo inakatta.","german":"Few participants came, and as for young people, there was not a single one.","furigana":"さんかしゃ|すく|わかもの|いた|ひとり"}],"notes":"Nに至っては picks out the most extreme case from a series of mostly negative examples: \"and as for N, even ...\". The preceding sentence often states the general situation, then the extreme case follows. Unlike neutral については or に関しては, に至っては carries a critical or surprised judgment. Do not confuse with に至るまで \"all the way to\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"連休が好天と相まって、観光地は大勢の人でにぎわった。","romaji":"Renkyū ga kōten to aimatte, kankōchi wa ōzei no hito de nigiwatta.","german":"Combined with the good weather, the holiday weekend left the tourist spots bustling with crowds.","furigana":"れんきゅう|こうてん|あい|かんこうち|おおぜい|ひと"},{"japanese":"彼の才能は努力と相まって、大きく開花した。","romaji":"Kare no sainō wa doryoku to aimatte, ōkiku kaika shita.","german":"His talent, coupled with effort, blossomed greatly.","furigana":"かれ|さいのう|どりょく|あい|おお|かいか"},{"japanese":"美しい夜景と相まって、料理が一層おいしく感じられた。","romaji":"Utsukushii yakei to aimatte, ryōri ga issō oishiku kanjirareta.","german":"Together with the beautiful night view, the food tasted even better.","furigana":"うつく|やけい|あい|りょうり|いっそう|かん"}],"notes":"AがBと相まって means \"A works together with B\": two factors reinforce each other and jointly lead to a result. AとBが相まって is also possible. The result is usually an amplified effect, positive or negative. Formal and written language; often in kana, あいまって. Do not confuse with the verb 合う.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"東京を皮切りに全国ツアーを行う。","romaji":"Tōkyō o kawakiri ni zenkoku tsuā o okonau.","german":"A nationwide tour will be held, starting with Tokyo.","furigana":"とうきょう|かわき|ぜんこく|おこな"},{"japanese":"一人の発言を皮切りに、次々と反対意見が出た。","romaji":"Hitori no hatsugen o kawakiri ni, tsugitsugi to hantai iken ga deta.","german":"Starting with one person's remark, objections came out one after another.","furigana":"ひとり|はつげん|かわき|つぎつぎ|はんたいいけん|で"},{"japanese":"今回の提携を皮切りに、海外展開を進める予定だ。","romaji":"Konkai no teikei o kawakiri ni, kaigai tenkai o susumeru yotei da.","german":"Starting with this partnership, we plan to advance our overseas expansion.","furigana":"こんかい|ていけい|かわき|かいがいてんかい|すす|よてい"}],"notes":"Nを皮切りに means \"starting with N\": N is the starting point of a whole series of similar events that follow and expand. Typical for tours, event series and business expansions. Also を皮切りとして, を皮切りにして. Unlike をはじめ (names a representative example), を皮切りに emphasizes the chronological sequence. Originally 皮切り refers to the first moxibustion treatment.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"円安が物価上昇に拍車をかけた。","romaji":"En'yasu ga bukka jōshō ni hakusha o kaketa.","german":"The weak yen accelerated the rise in prices.","furigana":"えんやす|ぶっかじょうしょう|はくしゃ"},{"japanese":"SNSの普及が若者の活字離れに拍車をかけている。","romaji":"Esu enu esu no fukyū ga wakamono no katsuji banare ni hakusha o kakete iru.","german":"The spread of social media is accelerating young people's drift away from print.","furigana":"ふきゅう|わかもの|かつじばな|はくしゃ"},{"japanese":"締め切りが近づき、作業に拍車がかかった。","romaji":"Shimekiri ga chikazuki, sagyō ni hakusha ga kakatta.","german":"As the deadline approached, work picked up speed.","furigana":"し|き|ちか|さぎょう|はくしゃ"}],"notes":"拍車 is the spur on a riding boot; Nに拍車をかける literally means \"to give N the spurs\" and means that a factor speeds up a development that is already underway. Often for negative trends (物価上昇, 過疎化, 少子化), but also neutral. Intransitive: Nに拍車がかかる \"N speeds up\". Formal, typical of news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"周囲の反対を余所に計画を進めた。","romaji":"Shūi no hantai o yoso ni keikaku o susumeta.","german":"He pushed ahead with the plan regardless of opposition from those around him.","furigana":"しゅうい|はんたい|よそ|けいかく|すす"},{"japanese":"親の心配をよそに、息子は毎晩遊び歩いている。","romaji":"Oya no shinpai o yoso ni, musuko wa maiban asobiaruite iru.","german":"Heedless of his parents' worry, the son goes out partying every night.","furigana":"おや|しんぱい|むすこ|まいばんあそ|ある"},{"japanese":"不況をよそに、その店には連日行列ができている。","romaji":"Fukyō o yoso ni, sono mise ni wa renjitsu gyōretsu ga dekite iru.","german":"In spite of the recession, long lines form at that shop every day.","furigana":"ふきょう|みせ|れんじつぎょうれつ"}],"notes":"Nを余所に means \"regardless of N, without caring about N\": the acting person ignores the worries, expectations or opposition of others, or a development runs counter to the general situation. Typical: 親の心配をよそに, 周囲の期待をよそに, 不況をよそに. Mostly written in kana. Unlike にもかかわらず, をよそに emphasizes disinterest or indifference toward N.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"失礼極まりない態度だ。","romaji":"Shitsurei kiwamarinai taido da.","german":"That is utterly rude behavior.","furigana":"しつれいきわ|たいど"},{"japanese":"雨の夜に無灯火で走るのは危険極まりない。","romaji":"Ame no yoru ni mutōka de hashiru no wa kiken kiwamarinai.","german":"Riding at night in the rain without a light is extremely dangerous.","furigana":"あめ|よる|むとうか|はし|きけんきわ"},{"japanese":"役所の手続きは複雑極まりない。","romaji":"Yakusho no tetsuzuki wa fukuzatsu kiwamarinai.","german":"Procedures at government offices are extremely complicated.","furigana":"やくしょ|てつづ|ふくざつきわ"}],"notes":"極まりない is attached to a noun or the stem of a な-adjective and means \"extremely, beyond all measure\": 失礼極まりない, 危険極まりない, 不愉快極まりない. It almost always intensifies negative evaluations and sounds formal and indignant. Synonymous is 極まる (失礼極まる). As an independent adjective it also means \"boundless\" (極まりない宇宙).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"信頼に足る人物だ。","romaji":"Shinrai ni taru jinbutsu da.","german":"He is a person worthy of trust.","furigana":"しんらい|た|じんぶつ"},{"japanese":"これは十分検討するに足る提案だ。","romaji":"Kore wa jūbun kentō suru ni taru teian da.","german":"This is a proposal well worth considering.","furigana":"じゅうぶんけんとう|た|ていあん"},{"japanese":"そんな取るに足らないことで悩む必要はない。","romaji":"Sonna toru ni taranai koto de nayamu hitsuyō wa nai.","german":"There is no need to worry over such a trivial matter.","furigana":"と|た|なや|ひつよう"}],"notes":"V-るに足る or Nに足る means \"to be worth, to be sufficient for\": 信頼に足る人物, 検討するに足る提案. It is written language; in everyday speech people more often say 〜できる, 〜する価値がある. Very common is the fixed negation 取るに足らない \"insignificant, trivial\". 足る is an older form of 足りる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"批判をものともせず突き進んだ。","romaji":"Hihan o mono tomo sezu tsukisusunda.","german":"He pushed ahead undeterred by the criticism.","furigana":"ひはん|つ|すす"},{"japanese":"選手たちは大雨をものともせず、最後まで走り抜いた。","romaji":"Senshutachi wa ōame o mono tomo sezu, saigo made hashirinuita.","german":"The runners ran through to the end, undaunted by the heavy rain.","furigana":"せんしゅ|おおあめ|さいご|はし|ぬ"},{"japanese":"彼女は病気をものともせず、舞台に立ち続けた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa byōki o mono tomo sezu, butai ni tachitsuzuketa.","german":"Defying her illness, she continued to stand on stage.","furigana":"かのじょ|びょうき|ぶたい|た|つづ"}],"notes":"Nをものともせず means \"unfazed by N, in spite of N\": one does not let difficulties such as rain, injury or criticism stop one. It expresses admiration and usually describes others, not oneself. Distinction: をよそに emphasizes indifference, を顧みず disregard of a risk. Written language; almost always in kana.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"驚きを禁じ得ない。","romaji":"Odoroki o kinji enai.","german":"I cannot hide my astonishment.","furigana":"おどろ|きん|え"},{"japanese":"被害者の話を聞き、涙を禁じ得なかった。","romaji":"Higaisha no hanashi o kiki, namida o kinji enakatta.","german":"Hearing the victim's story, I could not hold back my tears.","furigana":"ひがいしゃ|はなし|き|なみだ|きん|え"},{"japanese":"今回の判決には怒りを禁じ得ない。","romaji":"Konkai no hanketsu ni wa ikari o kinji enai.","german":"I cannot help feeling anger at this ruling.","furigana":"こんかい|はんけつ|いか|きん|え"}],"notes":"Nを禁じ得ない means \"cannot suppress N\", that is, \"cannot help feeling N\". N is an emotion noun: 涙, 同情, 怒り, 驚き, 失望. It is very formal and written language and describes the speaker's own feelings. In everyday speech people say 〜ずにはいられない or simply 思わず泣いた. Also written 禁じえない.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一秒たりとも無駄にできない。","romaji":"Ichibyō tari tomo muda ni dekinai.","german":"I can't waste even a second.","furigana":"いちびょう|むだ"},{"japanese":"そんな請求には一円たりとも払うつもりはない。","romaji":"Sonna seikyū ni wa ichien tari tomo harau tsumori wa nai.","german":"I have no intention of paying even a single yen for such a bill.","furigana":"せいきゅう|いちえん|はら"},{"japanese":"何人たりとも、ここへの立ち入りを禁ずる。","romaji":"Nanpito tari tomo, koko e no tachiiri o kinzuru.","german":"No one, whoever they may be, is permitted to enter here.","furigana":"なんぴと|た|い|きん"}],"notes":"A counter with 一 + たりとも + negation means \"not even a single ...\": 一秒たりとも, 一円たりとも, 一日たりとも. It is more emphatic and formal than 一〜も. The classical phrase 何人たりとも (なんぴとたりとも) means \"no one, whoever it may be\". Almost always followed by a prohibition, an impossibility or a firm resolution.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ただでさえ忙しいのに仕事が増えた。","romaji":"Tada de sae isogashii noni shigoto ga fueta.","german":"I'm already busy as it is, and now there's more work.","furigana":"いそが|しごと|ふ"},{"japanese":"ただでさえ狭い部屋に、大きな本棚を置いてしまった。","romaji":"Tada de sae semai heya ni, ōki na hondana o oite shimatta.","german":"I went and put a big bookshelf in a room that was already small.","furigana":"せま|へや|おお|ほんだな|お"},{"japanese":"ただでさえ混む駅が、連休で一層混雑している。","romaji":"Tada de sae komu eki ga, renkyū de issō konzatsu shite iru.","german":"The station, which is crowded to begin with, is even more packed because of the holiday.","furigana":"こ|えき|れんきゅう|いっそうこんざつ"}],"notes":"ただでさえ means \"already, even under normal circumstances\". One names a situation that is already unfavorable and made worse by an additional factor; typical is ただでさえ〜のに: ただでさえ忙しいのに. A reproach or complaint often follows. Neutral, common in both speech and writing.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"京都ならではの風景が広がる。","romaji":"Kyōto nara dewa no fūkei ga hirogaru.","german":"A landscape unique to Kyoto spreads out before you.","furigana":"きょうと|ふうけい|ひろ"},{"japanese":"このセーターには手編みならではの温かみがある。","romaji":"Kono sētā ni wa teami nara dewa no atatakami ga aru.","german":"This sweater has the warmth that only hand-knitting has.","furigana":"てあ|あたた"},{"japanese":"一瞬で魚をさばくとは、さすがプロならではの技だ。","romaji":"Isshun de sakana o sabaku to wa, sasuga puro nara dewa no waza da.","german":"Filleting a fish in an instant, that is truly a skill only a professional has.","furigana":"いっしゅん|さかな|わざ"}],"notes":"Nならでは(の) means \"typical of N, possible only with N\" and highlights a special, positive quality: 京都ならではの風景, プロならではの技. Before a noun it is ならではの, at the end of a sentence ならではだ. It is used only in praise, never for disadvantages. The older meaning \"except, without\" (Nならでは〜ない) is literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"休日とあって公園は賑わっていた。","romaji":"Kyūjitsu to atte kōen wa nigiwatte ita.","german":"Since it was a holiday, the park was bustling.","furigana":"きゅうじつ|こうえん|にぎ"},{"japanese":"人気歌手が来るとあって、会場には朝から長い列ができていた。","romaji":"Ninki kashu ga kuru to atte, kaijō ni wa asa kara nagai retsu ga dekite ita.","german":"Because a popular singer was coming, a long line had formed at the venue since morning.","furigana":"にんきかしゅ|く|かいじょう|あさ|なが|れつ"},{"japanese":"初めての海外旅行とあって、彼女はとても興奮していた。","romaji":"Hajimete no kaigai ryokō to atte, kanojo wa totemo kōfun shite ita.","german":"As it was her first trip abroad, she was very excited.","furigana":"はじ|かいがいりょこう|かのじょ|こうふん"}],"notes":"Nとあって or sentence+とあって means \"since it is a case of ...\": one names a special circumstance and a consequence that follows naturally from it, from an observer's viewpoint. Typical: 休日とあって, 人気歌手が来るとあって, 初めてとあって. It sounds like news or reportage style; the consequence is not a request or intention of the speaker. Related: とあれば \"if it is a matter of ...\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"本日を以て閉店いたします。","romaji":"Honjitsu o motte heiten itashimasu.","german":"We will close down as of today.","furigana":"ほんじつ|もっ|へいてん"},{"japanese":"結果は後日、書面を以てお知らせします。","romaji":"Kekka wa gojitsu, shomen o motte oshirase shimasu.","german":"We will notify you of the result in writing at a later date.","furigana":"けっか|ごじつ|しょめん|もっ|し"},{"japanese":"彼は失敗の怖さを身を以て知った。","romaji":"Kare wa shippai no kowasa o mi o motte shitta.","german":"He learned the fear of failure through personal experience.","furigana":"かれ|しっぱい|こわ|み|もっ|し"}],"notes":"をもって is a formal expression that appears in kana more often than in the spelling を以て. It marks above all the means (\"with, by\": 書面をもって通知する, 身をもって知る \"to learn from personal experience\") and a point in time or an end (\"with, as of\": 本日をもって閉店, 以上をもって終了します); in elevated usage also a reason. In announcements, letters and notices it replaces the neutral で; in conversation it sounds stiff. Even more polite is をもちまして, typical of announcements.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"教師にあるまじき行為だ。","romaji":"Kyōshi ni aru majiki kōi da.","german":"That is conduct unbecoming of a teacher.","furigana":"きょうし|こうい"},{"japanese":"彼の発言は政治家にあるまじきものとして批判された。","romaji":"Kare no hatsugen wa seijika ni aru majiki mono to shite hihan sareta.","german":"His remark was criticized as unworthy of a politician.","furigana":"かれ|はつげん|せいじか|ひはん"},{"japanese":"弱い者をいじめるのは、許すまじき行為だ。","romaji":"Yowai mono o ijimeru no wa, yurusu majiki kōi da.","german":"Bullying the weak is an unforgivable act.","furigana":"よわ|もの|ゆる|こうい"}],"notes":"まじき is the attributive form of the classical auxiliary まじ \"should not, must not\". Almost always in Nにあるまじき N: \"unworthy of N, unforgivable for N\", e.g. 教師にあるまじき行為, 政治家にあるまじき発言. It expresses sharp moral condemnation and is very formal. Also with other verbs: 許すまじき行為 \"an unforgivable act\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"始末に負えない問題になった。","romaji":"Shimatsu ni oenai mondai ni natta.","german":"It became a problem that could not be handled.","furigana":"しまつ|お|もんだい"},{"japanese":"彼は酔っ払うと始末に負えない。","romaji":"Kare wa yopparau to shimatsu ni oenai.","german":"He is impossible to deal with when he is drunk.","furigana":"かれ|よ|ぱら|しまつ|お"},{"japanese":"いたずら盛りの子犬で、まったく始末に負えない。","romaji":"Itazura zakari no koinu de, mattaku shimatsu ni oenai.","german":"The puppy is at its mischievous stage and is completely unmanageable.","furigana":"|こいぬ|しまつ|お"}],"notes":"始末 means \"settlement, taking care of\" and 負えない \"cannot bear\"; 始末に負えない thus means \"cannot be brought under control, hopeless\". It often describes people or animals with troublesome behavior (酔うと始末に負えない) or hopelessly tangled problems. Very similar is 手に負えない; 始末に負えない sounds more exasperated. The spelling 始末に終えない is a common mistake.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"目を見張るような成長を遂げた。","romaji":"Me o miharu yō na seichō o togeta.","german":"He achieved astonishing growth.","furigana":"め|みは|せいちょう|と"},{"japanese":"子供たちは初めて見る海に目を見張った。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa hajimete miru umi ni me o mihatta.","german":"The children stared wide-eyed at the sea they were seeing for the first time.","furigana":"こども|はじ|み|うみ|め|みは"},{"japanese":"その職人の技術には誰もが目を見張る。","romaji":"Sono shokunin no gijutsu ni wa daremo ga me o miharu.","german":"Everyone marvels at that craftsman's skill.","furigana":"しょくにん|ぎじゅつ|だれ|め|みは"}],"notes":"目を見張る literally means \"to open one's eyes wide\" and describes astonishment or admiration. As a verb it takes に or を: 美しさに目を見張る. It is very common attributively: 目を見張るような〜 / 目を見張るほどの〜 \"astonishing, breathtaking\", e.g. 目を見張る成長. Neutral to formal; not to be confused with 見張る on its own, \"to keep watch\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の説明は腑に落ちない。","romaji":"Kare no setsumei wa fu ni ochinai.","german":"His explanation doesn't add up.","furigana":"かれ|せつめい|ふ|お"},{"japanese":"どうも腑に落ちない点がいくつかある。","romaji":"Dōmo fu ni ochinai ten ga ikutsu ka aru.","german":"There are a few points that just don't make sense to me.","furigana":"ふ|お|てん"},{"japanese":"何度も読み返して、やっと腑に落ちた。","romaji":"Nando mo yomikaeshite, yatto fu ni ochita.","german":"After reading it over many times, it finally clicked.","furigana":"なんど|よ|かえ|ふ|お"}],"notes":"腑 means the innards, formerly regarded as the seat of understanding; 腑に落ちない means \"doesn't quite make sense to me, isn't entirely clear to me\". You have heard an explanation, but a remnant of doubt remains: どうも腑に落ちない. The positive form 腑に落ちる \"to make sense, to click\" is now common as well. Related: 納得できない, 釈然としない (more formal).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女のスピーチは胸を打った。","romaji":"Kanojo no supīchi wa mune o utta.","german":"Her speech touched people's hearts.","furigana":"かのじょ|むね|う"},{"japanese":"被災者の言葉が、多くの人の胸を打った。","romaji":"Hisaisha no kotoba ga, ōku no hito no mune o utta.","german":"The words of the disaster victims moved many people.","furigana":"ひさいしゃ|ことば|おお|ひと|むね|う"},{"japanese":"これは家族の絆を描いた、胸を打つ物語だ。","romaji":"Kore wa kazoku no kizuna o egaita, mune o utsu monogatari da.","german":"This is a moving story about family bonds.","furigana":"かぞく|きずな|えが|むね|う|ものがたり"}],"notes":"胸を打つ means \"to move deeply, to touch the heart\". The subject is the thing that moves (speech, story, words); the object, if any, is the person: 人々の胸を打つ. Passive: 胸を打たれる \"to be deeply moved\". Very common attributively: 胸を打つ物語. 心を打つ means the same; 感動させる sounds more matter-of-fact.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あっさり解決して拍子抜けした。","romaji":"Assari kaiketsu shite hyōshinuke shita.","german":"It was resolved so easily that I felt rather let down.","furigana":"かいけつ|ひょうしぬ"},{"japanese":"厳しい面接だと聞いていたのに、拍子抜けするほど簡単だった。","romaji":"Kibishii mensetsu da to kiite ita noni, hyōshinuke suru hodo kantan datta.","german":"I had been told the interview would be tough, but it was so easy it was an anticlimax.","furigana":"きび|めんせつ|き|ひょうしぬ|かんたん"},{"japanese":"張り切って準備したのに、イベントが中止になって拍子抜けした。","romaji":"Harikitte junbi shita noni, ibento ga chūshi ni natte hyōshinuke shita.","german":"I had prepared with great enthusiasm, but the event was cancelled and I felt deflated.","furigana":"は|き|じゅんび|ちゅうし|ひょうしぬ"}],"notes":"拍子抜け describes the feeling when you have braced yourself for something difficult or exciting and it turns out far easier or more harmless: \"anticlimax, to be let down\". Literally \"to fall out of the beat\". As a する-verb: 拍子抜けした; attributively 拍子抜けするほど簡単. Unlike がっかり (disappointment at something bad), 拍子抜け leaves the feeling that your own tension was for nothing.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は私のために骨を折ってくれた。","romaji":"Kare wa watashi no tame ni hone o otte kureta.","german":"He went to great lengths for me.","furigana":"かれ|わたし|ほね|お"},{"japanese":"新しい取引先を見つけるのに、ずいぶん骨を折った。","romaji":"Atarashii torihikisaki o mitsukeru no ni, zuibun hone o otta.","german":"It took a great deal of effort to find a new business partner.","furigana":"あたら|とりひきさき|み|ほね|お"},{"japanese":"このたびはいろいろとお骨折りいただき、ありがとうございました。","romaji":"Kono tabi wa iroiro to ohoneori itadaki, arigatō gozaimashita.","german":"Thank you very much for all the trouble you have taken on this occasion.","furigana":"ほねお"}],"notes":"骨を折る literally means \"to break a bone\" (but for that one usually says 骨折する), and figuratively \"to take great pains for something or someone\". Often with てくれる for others' efforts: 私のために骨を折ってくれた. The noun 骨折り appears in the polite thank-you formula お骨折りいただき. Neutral to polite.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の指摘は的を射ている。","romaji":"Kare no shiteki wa mato o ite iru.","german":"His remark hits the mark.","furigana":"かれ|してき|まと|い"},{"japanese":"「的を射た質問ですね」と講師がほめた。","romaji":"\"Mato o ita shitsumon desu ne\" to kōshi ga hometa.","german":"\"That's a very pertinent question,\" the lecturer praised.","furigana":"まと|い|しつもん|こうし"},{"japanese":"会議での彼の説明は的を射ていなかった。","romaji":"Kaigi de no kare no setsumei wa mato o ite inakatta.","german":"His explanation at the meeting missed the point.","furigana":"かいぎ|かれ|せつめい|まと|い"}],"notes":"的を射る literally means \"to hit the target\" (射る \"to shoot with a bow\") and means \"to be to the point, accurate\". It almost always evaluates statements: 的を射た意見・質問・指摘 or predicatively 〜は的を射ている. The common variant 的を得る is traditionally considered an error (a mix-up with 当を得る) and should be avoided in formal texts. The opposite is 的外れ \"off the mark, beside the point\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"医者も匙を投げた。","romaji":"Isha mo saji o nageta.","german":"Even the doctor gave up.","furigana":"いしゃ|さじ|な"},{"japanese":"何度教えても覚えないので、先生もとうとう匙を投げた。","romaji":"Nando oshiete mo oboenai node, sensei mo tōtō saji o nageta.","german":"No matter how many times he was taught, he never remembered, so even the teacher finally gave up on him.","furigana":"なんどおし|おぼ|せんせい|さじ|な"},{"japanese":"このパソコンの不具合には修理業者も匙を投げた。","romaji":"Kono pasokon no fuguai ni wa shūri gyōsha mo saji o nageta.","german":"Even the repair company threw in the towel over this computer fault.","furigana":"ふぐあい|しゅうりぎょうしゃ|さじ|な"}],"notes":"匙を投げる literally means \"to throw away the spoon\": originally it refers to a doctor who puts down his medicine spoon because he considers the patient incurable. Today it generally means \"to give up on something or someone as hopeless\", typically 医者も・先生も匙を投げた or 〜には匙を投げる. Unlike the neutral あきらめる, it stresses that someone capitulates after efforts of their own. Often written in kana as さじを投げる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"褒められて図に乗るな。","romaji":"Homerarete zu ni noru na.","german":"Don't get carried away just because you were praised.","furigana":"ほ|ず|の"},{"japanese":"一度許したら、彼はすっかり図に乗ってしまった。","romaji":"Ichido yurushitara, kare wa sukkari zu ni notte shimatta.","german":"The moment I let him off once, he got completely cocky.","furigana":"いちどゆる|かれ|ず|の"},{"japanese":"少し売れたからといって図に乗っていると、すぐに失敗するよ。","romaji":"Sukoshi ureta kara to itte zu ni notte iru to, sugu ni shippai suru yo.","german":"If you get cocky just because you've sold a little, you'll fail soon.","furigana":"すこ|う|ず|の|しっぱい"}],"notes":"図に乗る means to become overconfident and brazen through success, praise or other people's indulgence. According to the common explanation, 図 originally referred to a tonal change point in Buddhist chanting; whoever hits it well becomes bold. The expression is clearly reproachful and often appears in the prohibition 図に乗るな or as 図に乗って…. The similar 調子に乗る is broader and can also mean \"to get into the swing of things\" neutrally; 図に乗る is always negative.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"急いでいることがばれて、足元を見られ、高い値段をつけられた。","romaji":"Isoide iru koto ga barete, ashimoto o mirare, takai nedan o tsukerareta.","german":"They noticed I was in a hurry, took advantage of it, and charged me a high price.","furigana":"いそ|あしもと|み|たか|ねだん"},{"japanese":"相手はこちらが断れないと足元を見て、無理な条件を出してきた。","romaji":"Aite wa kochira ga kotowarenai to ashimoto o mite, muri na jōken o dashite kita.","german":"The other side knew we could not refuse and demanded unreasonable terms.","furigana":"あいて|ことわ|あしもと|み|むり|じょうけん|だ"},{"japanese":"客の足元を見るような商売は長続きしない。","romaji":"Kyaku no ashimoto o miru yō na shōbai wa nagatsuzuki shinai.","german":"A business that exploits its customers' predicament does not last long.","furigana":"きゃく|あしもと|み|しょうばい|ながつづ"}],"notes":"足元を見る literally means \"to look at the feet\": in the past, palanquin bearers judged by a traveler's feet how tired he was and then demanded more money. Figuratively it means to exploit another person's predicament or weakness, especially over the price. Often in the passive: 足元を見られる \"to be taken advantage of\". The literal meaning (足元に気を付ける \"watch your step\") is to be distinguished from this. Neutral.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の行為は目に余る。","romaji":"Kare no kōi wa me ni amaru.","german":"His behavior is intolerable.","furigana":"かれ|こうい|め|あま"},{"japanese":"最近の彼の遅刻は目に余るものがある。","romaji":"Saikin no kare no chikoku wa me ni amaru mono ga aru.","german":"His lateness lately is hardly acceptable any more.","furigana":"さいきん|かれ|ちこく|め|あま"},{"japanese":"観光客の目に余るマナー違反が問題になっている。","romaji":"Kankōkyaku no me ni amaru manā ihan ga mondai ni natte iru.","german":"The appalling manners of tourists have become a problem.","furigana":"かんこうきゃく|め|あま|いはん|もんだい"}],"notes":"目に余る literally means \"to exceed the eye\": something is so bad that you can no longer overlook it, hence \"intolerable, no longer acceptable\". It almost always judges other people's behavior: 目に余る行為・態度・振る舞い or predicatively 〜は目に余るものがある. Unlike 我慢できない (personal feeling), it sounds like an objective, rather formal judgment from outside. The meaning \"too much to see it all\" is rare.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この問題は私の手に負えない。","romaji":"Kono mondai wa watashi no te ni oenai.","german":"This problem is beyond my ability.","furigana":"もんだい|わたし|て|お"},{"japanese":"あの子はいたずらばかりで、もう親の手に負えない。","romaji":"Ano ko wa itazura bakari de, mō oya no te ni oenai.","german":"That child does nothing but mischief, and the parents can no longer handle him.","furigana":"こ|おや|て|お"},{"japanese":"火が大きくなって、もう手に負えなくなった。","romaji":"Hi ga ōkiku natte, mō te ni oenaku natta.","german":"The fire grew so big that it was no longer under control.","furigana":"ひ|おお|て|お"}],"notes":"負う means \"to carry, to take on\": 手に負えない means \"one cannot handle it with one's own hands\" - too difficult, out of control, no longer tameable. The person who is overwhelmed is marked with の: 私の手に負えない, 親の手に負えない. Also used of people and things: 手に負えない子供, 火が手に負えなくなる. Polite: 手に負えません. Neutral, more expressive than a bare 無理.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"結果は言わずもがなだ。","romaji":"Kekka wa iwazu mogana da.","german":"The result goes without saying.","furigana":"けっか|い"},{"japanese":"彼は英語は言わずもがな、フランス語も中国語も話せる。","romaji":"Kare wa eigo wa iwazu mogana, furansugo mo chūgokugo mo hanaseru.","german":"He speaks English, needless to say, and also French and Chinese.","furigana":"かれ|えいご|い|ご|ちゅうごくご|はな"},{"japanese":"つい言わずもがなのことを言って、彼女を怒らせてしまった。","romaji":"Tsui iwazu mogana no koto o itte, kanojo o okorasete shimatta.","german":"I carelessly said something that should have been left unsaid and made her angry.","furigana":"い|い|かのじょ|おこ"}],"notes":"言わずもがな is a classical form of \"better not to say\" and has two uses. Mostly it means \"of course, not to mention\", especially in the pattern AはBは言わずもがな、Cも… \"A of course, but also C\". With の before a noun, however, it means \"what would better be left unsaid\": 言わずもがなのことを言う \"to say something superfluous or tactless\". The expression is elevated and literary; in everyday speech people usually say もちろん or 言うまでもない.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"如何なる困難にも負けない。","romaji":"Ikanaru konnan ni mo makenai.","german":"I won't give in to any difficulty.","furigana":"いか|こんなん|ま"},{"japanese":"いかなる理由があっても、暴力は許されない。","romaji":"Ikanaru riyū ga atte mo, bōryoku wa yurusarenai.","german":"Violence is not permitted for any reason whatsoever.","furigana":"りゆう|ぼうりょく|ゆる"},{"japanese":"彼がいかなる人物なのか、誰も知らない。","romaji":"Kare ga ikanaru jinbutsu na no ka, dare mo shiranai.","german":"Nobody knows what kind of person he actually is.","furigana":"かれ|じんぶつ|だれ|し"}],"notes":"いかなる is the elevated, literary counterpart of どんな and always stands directly before a noun. The most common use is the generalizing one with も・でも・にも: いかなる理由があっても \"for whatever reason\", いかなる困難にも \"in the face of any difficulty\". There is also the question use \"what kind of …?\" (いかなる人物か). The kanji spelling 如何なる is rare; kana is usual.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今年の学生は押し並べて優秀だ。","romaji":"Kotoshi no gakusei wa oshinabete yūshū da.","german":"This year's students are excellent across the board.","furigana":"ことし|がくせい|お|な|ゆうしゅう"},{"japanese":"この地方の冬はおしなべて雪が多い。","romaji":"Kono chihō no fuyu wa oshinabete yuki ga ōi.","german":"Winters in this region generally have a lot of snow.","furigana":"ちほう|ふゆ|ゆき|おお"},{"japanese":"新製品の評判はおしなべて良好だ。","romaji":"Shinseihin no hyōban wa oshinabete ryōkō da.","german":"Reception of the new product is good across the board.","furigana":"しんせいひん|ひょうばん|りょうこう"}],"notes":"おしなべて sums up a group: \"on the whole, across the board\". It stands before evaluative statements such as 高い, 良好だ, 優秀だ and refers to many individual cases at once. Equivalent in meaning but somewhat more common are 概して and 総じて; the neutral 一般に sounds less like a summing-up. Almost always written in kana; the kanji 押し並べて are rare.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"否応なく参加させられた。","romaji":"Iya ō naku sanka saserareta.","german":"I was made to take part whether I liked it or not.","furigana":"いやおう|さんか"},{"japanese":"年を取れば、否応なく体力は落ちていく。","romaji":"Toshi o toreba, iya ō naku tairyoku wa ochite iku.","german":"As you get older, your physical strength inevitably declines.","furigana":"とし|と|いやおう|たいりょく|お"},{"japanese":"転勤が決まり、家族は否応なく引っ越すことになった。","romaji":"Tenkin ga kimari, kazoku wa iya ō naku hikkosu koto ni natta.","german":"The transfer was decided, and the family had no choice but to move.","furigana":"てんきん|き|かぞく|いやおう|ひ|こ"}],"notes":"否応 means \"yes or no\"; 否応なく thus means \"without being able to say yes or no\", that is, \"whether one wants to or not, inevitably\". It often appears with the passive, the causative-passive or 〜ことになる: 否応なく参加させられる. 否応なしに means the same, and before nouns 否応なしの. Unlike 無理やり (someone forces actively), it stresses the inescapability of circumstances.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"結果的に正しい判断だった。","romaji":"Kekkateki ni tadashii handan datta.","german":"In the end it was the right decision.","furigana":"けっかてき|ただ|はんだん"},{"japanese":"善意でしたことが、結果的に彼を傷つけてしまった。","romaji":"Zen'i de shita koto ga, kekkateki ni kare o kizutsukete shimatta.","german":"What I did with good intentions ended up hurting him.","furigana":"ぜんい|けっかてき|かれ|きず"},{"japanese":"電車が遅れたおかげで、結果的に忘れ物に気づけた。","romaji":"Densha ga okureta okage de, kekkateki ni wasuremono ni kizuketa.","german":"Thanks to the train being late, I ended up noticing that I had forgotten something.","furigana":"でんしゃ|おく|けっかてき|わす|もの|き"}],"notes":"結果的に describes how something \"turned out in the end\", often differently from what was intended or without having been planned. It differs from その結果 \"as a result of that\", which links a logical consequence, and from the more colloquial 結局 \"after all, in the end\". It is typical in retrospectives: 結果的に良かった, 結果的に〜してしまった.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"端的に言えば失敗だった。","romaji":"Tanteki ni ieba shippai datta.","german":"To put it bluntly, it was a failure.","furigana":"たんてき|い|しっぱい"},{"japanese":"この数字が、問題の深刻さを端的に示している。","romaji":"Kono sūji ga, mondai no shinkokusa o tanteki ni shimeshite iru.","german":"This figure shows plainly how serious the problem is.","furigana":"すうじ|もんだい|しんこく|たんてき|しめ"},{"japanese":"時間がないので、要点だけ端的に説明してください。","romaji":"Jikan ga nai node, yōten dake tanteki ni setsumei shite kudasai.","german":"We have little time, so please explain just the essentials briefly.","furigana":"じかん|ようてん|たんてき|せつめい"}],"notes":"端的に is the adverbial form of 端的 and means \"to the point, frankly\". Very common is 端的に言えば・言うと \"in short\", similar to 要するに but more matter-of-fact. With verbs such as 示す・表す it means \"clearly, unmistakably\": the matter shows itself directly and without detour. Unlike 簡単に \"simply\", it is not about ease but about directness.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"悪習を根こそぎ取り除く。","romaji":"Akushū o nekosogi torinozoku.","german":"We will eliminate the bad habits root and branch.","furigana":"あくしゅう|ね|と|のぞ"},{"japanese":"空き巣に入られ、金目の物を根こそぎ持っていかれた。","romaji":"Akisu ni hairare, kaneme no mono o nekosogi motte ikareta.","german":"We were burgled, and everything of value was taken.","furigana":"あ|す|はい|かねめ|もの|ね|も"},{"japanese":"台風で庭の木が根こそぎ倒れた。","romaji":"Taifū de niwa no ki ga nekosogi taoreta.","german":"The typhoon uprooted the tree in the garden.","furigana":"たいふう|にわ|き|ね|たお"}],"notes":"根こそぎ literally means \"pulled out by the roots\" and is used both concretely (trees, plants) and figuratively \"completely, root and branch\". Typical combinations are 根こそぎ奪う・盗まれる・持っていかれる, often in connection with theft or destruction. As a noun it appears in 根こそぎにする \"to eradicate\". Compared with すべて or 全部, it stresses thoroughness and usually a loss.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"渋々ながら承諾した。","romaji":"Shibushibu nagara shōdaku shita.","german":"He consented, though reluctantly.","furigana":"しぶしぶ|しょうだく"},{"japanese":"子どもは渋々宿題を始めた。","romaji":"Kodomo wa shibushibu shukudai o hajimeta.","german":"The child reluctantly started his homework.","furigana":"こ|しぶしぶしゅくだい|はじ"},{"japanese":"上司に頼まれ、彼は渋々残業を引き受けた。","romaji":"Jōshi ni tanomare, kare wa shibushibu zangyō o hikiuketa.","german":"Asked by his boss, he reluctantly took on the overtime.","furigana":"じょうし|たの|かれ|しぶしぶざんぎょう|ひ|う"}],"notes":"渋々 (from 渋い \"astringent\") describes doing something \"reluctantly, out of necessity\", usually after being asked or pressed to do so. Typical with verbs of consenting: 渋々承知する・従う・引き受ける, strengthened also as 渋々ながら. Compared with いやいや the aversion is weaker; one yields more in resignation. Often written in kana as しぶしぶ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は悠然と構えている。","romaji":"Kare wa yūzen to kamaete iru.","german":"He remains perfectly composed.","furigana":"かれ|ゆうぜん|かま"},{"japanese":"大きな鷲が空を悠然と舞っていた。","romaji":"Ōkina washi ga sora o yūzen to matte ita.","german":"A large eagle soared leisurely in the sky.","furigana":"おお|わし|そら|ゆうぜん|ま"},{"japanese":"試合に遅れそうなのに、彼は悠然とコーヒーを飲んでいた。","romaji":"Shiai ni okuresō na no ni, kare wa yūzen to kōhī o nonde ita.","german":"Although he was about to be late for the game, he was calmly drinking coffee.","furigana":"しあい|おく|かれ|ゆうぜん|の"}],"notes":"悠然と describes a calm, unshakable composure, often precisely when others get flustered: \"unhurriedly, leisurely\". Typical are 悠然と構える \"to wait calmly\", 悠然と歩く and 悠然と空を舞う. Before nouns the literary form 悠然たる態度 is used. It sounds more elevated than 落ち着いて and, unlike のんびり, has no connotation of sluggishness.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"暴力を嫌悪する。","romaji":"Bōryoku o ken'o suru.","german":"He detests violence.","furigana":"ぼうりょく|けんお"},{"japanese":"彼は不正を何よりも嫌悪していた。","romaji":"Kare wa fusei o nani yori mo ken'o shite ita.","german":"He detested dishonesty more than anything else.","furigana":"かれ|ふせい|なに|けんお"},{"japanese":"自分の弱さを嫌悪して、眠れない夜を過ごした。","romaji":"Jibun no yowasa o ken'o shite, nemurenai yoru o sugoshita.","german":"Disgusted with my own weakness, I spent a sleepless night.","furigana":"じぶん|よわ|けんお|ねむ|よる|す"}],"notes":"嫌悪する means \"to detest, to reject deeply\" and is clearly stronger and more formal than 嫌う or 嫌がる. It is transitive: 暴力を嫌悪する. More common than the verb is the noun in fixed combinations: 嫌悪感を抱く・覚える \"to feel revulsion\", 自己嫌悪 \"self-loathing, dissatisfaction with oneself\". The reading is けんお, not けんあく.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"資金集めに奔走する。","romaji":"Shikin atsume ni honsō suru.","german":"He is working hard to raise funds.","furigana":"しきんあつ|ほんそう"},{"japanese":"彼は友人の就職のために奔走した。","romaji":"Kare wa yūjin no shūshoku no tame ni honsō shita.","german":"He ran around trying to get a job for his friend.","furigana":"かれ|ゆうじん|しゅうしょく|ほんそう"},{"japanese":"災害の後、市の職員は避難所の運営に奔走している。","romaji":"Saigai no ato, shi no shokuin wa hinanjo no un'ei ni honsō shite iru.","german":"Since the disaster, city employees have been busy running the evacuation shelters.","furigana":"さいがい|あと|し|しょくいん|ひなんじょ|うんえい|ほんそう"}],"notes":"奔走する literally means \"to run about\" and means \"to work tirelessly for something, to pull out all the stops\". The goal takes に: 資金集めに奔走する, 問題の解決に奔走する, or 〜のために. It is literary and sounds appreciative, similar to 尽力する; the everyday word 走り回る, by contrast, tends to be meant literally.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"政府が市場に介入した。","romaji":"Seifu ga shijō ni kainyū shita.","german":"The government intervened in the market.","furigana":"せいふ|しじょう|かいにゅう"},{"japanese":"親が子どものけんかに介入しすぎるのはよくない。","romaji":"Oya ga kodomo no kenka ni kainyū shisugiru no wa yoku nai.","german":"It is not good for parents to intervene too much in their children's quarrels.","furigana":"おや|こ|かいにゅう"},{"japanese":"国連が紛争に介入することになった。","romaji":"Kokuren ga funsō ni kainyū suru koto ni natta.","german":"The United Nations has decided to intervene in the conflict.","furigana":"こくれん|ふんそう|かいにゅう"}],"notes":"介入する means \"to intervene\" and is intransitive: what is intervened in takes に (市場に介入する, 紛争に介入する). Typical compounds are 軍事介入 and 為替介入 \"currency market intervention\". Compared with 干渉する \"to interfere\", which is clearly negative, 介入 sounds more matter-of-fact; colloquially one says 口を出す.","levelNote":"Derived from 介入 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"普遍的な価値観を尊重する。","romaji":"Fuhenteki na kachikan o sonchō suru.","german":"We respect universal values.","furigana":"ふへんてき|かちかん|そんちょう"},{"japanese":"愛は時代を超えた普遍的なテーマだ。","romaji":"Ai wa jidai o koeta fuhenteki na tēma da.","german":"Love is a timeless, universal theme.","furigana":"あい|じだい|こ|ふへんてき"},{"japanese":"この法則は普遍的に成り立つ。","romaji":"Kono hōsoku wa fuhenteki ni naritatsu.","german":"This law holds universally.","furigana":"ほうそく|ふへんてき|な|た"}],"notes":"普遍的な means \"universal\", that is, valid for all people, times and cases. Typical are 普遍的な真理・価値・テーマ; the opposite is 特殊な or 個別的な. Unlike 一般的な \"common, widespread\", it is not about frequency but about exceptionless validity. Adverbially: 普遍的に成り立つ; the related noun is 普遍性.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"恒常的な人手不足に悩む。","romaji":"Kōjōteki na hitode-busoku ni nayamu.","german":"We are plagued by a chronic labor shortage.","furigana":"こうじょうてき|ひとでぶそく|なや"},{"japanese":"この会社は恒常的に残業が多い。","romaji":"Kono kaisha wa kōjōteki ni zangyō ga ōi.","german":"This company constantly has a lot of overtime.","furigana":"かいしゃ|こうじょうてき|ざんぎょう|おお"},{"japanese":"一時的な対策ではなく、恒常的な仕組みが必要だ。","romaji":"Ichijiteki na taisaku de wa naku, kōjōteki na shikumi ga hitsuyō da.","german":"We need a permanent system, not a temporary measure.","furigana":"いちじてき|たいさく|こうじょうてき|しく|ひつよう"}],"notes":"恒常的な means \"constant, permanent\" and describes a state that does not pass; the opposite is 一時的な. It is often used with problems: 恒常的な人手不足・赤字, adverbially 恒常的に残業が多い. Similar is 慢性的な \"chronic\", which sounds even more negative; 恒久的な \"designed to last\" instead stresses a deliberately lasting arrangement (恒久的な平和).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"端的な表現を心がける。","romaji":"Tanteki na hyōgen o kokorogakeru.","german":"I make an effort to express myself directly.","furigana":"たんてき|ひょうげん|こころ"},{"japanese":"彼の質問に、彼女は一言で端的な答えを返した。","romaji":"Kare no shitsumon ni, kanojo wa hitokoto de tanteki na kotae o kaeshita.","german":"To his question, she gave a clear answer in a single word.","furigana":"かれ|しつもん|かのじょ|ひとこと|たんてき|こた|かえ"},{"japanese":"この事件は、制度の欠陥を示す端的な例だ。","romaji":"Kono jiken wa, seido no kekkan o shimesu tanteki na rei da.","german":"This incident is a telling example of the flaws in the system.","furigana":"じけん|せいど|けっかん|しめ|たんてき|れい"}],"notes":"端的な is the attributive form of 端的 and means \"direct, clear and to the point\". Typical are 端的な表現・答え・説明 and 端的な例 \"a clear, typical example\". More common than the な-form, however, is the adverb 端的に (端的に言えば). It praises brevity and clarity; rude directness is more likely described with 率直な or ぶっきらぼうな.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"厄介な問題を抱えている。","romaji":"Yakkai na mondai o kakaete iru.","german":"I have a troublesome problem.","furigana":"やっかい|もんだい|かか"},{"japanese":"厄介なことに、鍵をなくしたのは日曜の夜だった。","romaji":"Yakkai na koto ni, kagi o nakushita no wa nichiyō no yoru datta.","german":"To make matters worse, I lost the key on Sunday night.","furigana":"やっかい|かぎ|にちよう|よる"},{"japanese":"大学時代、叔父の家に二年間厄介になった。","romaji":"Daigaku jidai, oji no ie ni ninenkan yakkai ni natta.","german":"During my university days I stayed at my uncle's house for two years.","furigana":"だいがくじだい|おじ|いえ|にねんかんやっかい"}],"notes":"厄介な means \"troublesome, knotty, burdensome\" for things or people that cause you trouble: 厄介な問題・客・病気. Compared with 面倒な \"cumbersome\", it stresses that the matter is hard to resolve and becomes a burden. Also common are 厄介なことに \"unfortunately, to make matters worse\" and the noun in 〜に厄介になる \"to stay with someone, to be a burden to someone\" (e.g. to live with relatives).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"脆弱なインフラが課題だ。","romaji":"Zeijaku na infura ga kadai da.","german":"Vulnerable infrastructure is a challenge.","furigana":"ぜいじゃく|かだい"},{"japanese":"このシステムはセキュリティが脆弱だ。","romaji":"Kono shisutemu wa sekyuritī ga zeijaku da.","german":"This system has weak security."},{"japanese":"経営基盤が脆弱な中小企業は、不況の影響を受けやすい。","romaji":"Keiei kiban ga zeijaku na chūshō kigyō wa, fukyō no eikyō o ukeyasui.","german":"Small and medium-sized companies with a weak management base are easily affected by recessions.","furigana":"けいえいきばん|ぜいじゃく|ちゅうしょうきぎょう|ふきょう|えいきょう|う"}],"notes":"脆弱な means \"weak, susceptible, vulnerable\" and mostly describes structures and systems: 脆弱な地盤・インフラ・経営基盤. In IT the noun 脆弱性 \"vulnerability\" is very common. It sounds technical and formal; in everyday speech people say 弱い, and もろい for physically fragile things. The reading is ぜいじゃく; in newspapers it is also written ぜい弱.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"諸手を挙げて賛成する。","romaji":"Morote o agete sansei suru.","german":"I agree wholeheartedly.","furigana":"もろて|あ|さんせい"},{"japanese":"地元の人々は新しい駅の建設を諸手を挙げて歓迎した。","romaji":"Jimoto no hitobito wa atarashii eki no kensetsu o morote o agete kangei shita.","german":"The locals welcomed the construction of the new station with open arms.","furigana":"じもと|ひとびと|あたら|えき|けんせつ|もろて|あ|かんげい"},{"japanese":"その案には、諸手を挙げて賛成というわけにはいかない。","romaji":"Sono an ni wa, morote o agete sansei to iu wake ni wa ikanai.","german":"I can't give that proposal my wholehearted support.","furigana":"あん|もろて|あ|さんせい"}],"notes":"諸手を挙げて literally means \"with both hands raised\" and means \"unreservedly, without restriction\". It almost always stands before 賛成する or 歓迎する. The negation is very common: 諸手を挙げて賛成はできない \"cannot agree without reservation\", hinting at concerns. Also written もろ手を挙げて.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は歯に衣着せぬ物言いで知られている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa ha ni kinu kisenu monoii de shirarete iru.","german":"She is known for her blunt way of speaking.","furigana":"かのじょ|は|きぬき|ものい|し"},{"japanese":"歯に衣着せずに言うと、この企画は面白くない。","romaji":"Ha ni kinu kisezu ni iu to, kono kikaku wa omoshirokunai.","german":"To put it bluntly, this project is not interesting.","furigana":"は|きぬき|い|きかく|おもしろ"},{"japanese":"祖母は歯に衣着せぬ人で、私の服装にもはっきり文句を言う。","romaji":"Sobo wa ha ni kinu kisenu hito de, watashi no fukusō ni mo hakkiri monku o iu.","german":"My grandmother speaks her mind, and she plainly complains even about my clothes.","furigana":"そぼ|は|きぬき|ひと|わたし|ふくそう|もんく|い"}],"notes":"歯に衣着せぬ literally means \"to put no clothes on one's teeth\" (衣 is read きぬ here, not ころも); 着せぬ is a classical negation. It means to give one's opinion openly without sugarcoating. It mostly stands attributively: 歯に衣着せぬ発言／物言い／批評／人; adverbially it is 歯に衣着せずに言う. Depending on context it sounds appreciative (honest) or slightly critical (tactless).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"調査が問題を浮き彫りにした。","romaji":"Chōsa ga mondai o ukibori ni shita.","german":"The survey highlighted the problem.","furigana":"ちょうさ|もんだい|う|ぼ"},{"japanese":"この小説は、戦後の若者の孤独を浮き彫りにしている。","romaji":"Kono shōsetsu wa, sengo no wakamono no kodoku o ukibori ni shite iru.","german":"This novel brings the loneliness of postwar youth into sharp relief.","furigana":"しょうせつ|せんご|わかもの|こどく|う|ぼ"},{"japanese":"今回の災害で、高齢者支援の課題が浮き彫りになった。","romaji":"Konkai no saigai de, kōreisha shien no kadai ga ukibori ni natta.","german":"The recent disaster highlighted the challenges in supporting the elderly.","furigana":"こんかい|さいがい|こうれいしゃしえん|かだい|う|ぼ"}],"notes":"浮き彫り is actually a \"relief\" (sculpture); 浮き彫りにする means figuratively \"to make stand out clearly, to expose\", above all for problems and hidden conditions: 問題・課題・実態を浮き彫りにする. The intransitive counterpart is 浮き彫りになる \"to come clearly to light\". Compared with 明らかにする, it sounds more vivid and is often used in reports and newspapers.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"情報は玉石混淆である。","romaji":"Jōhō wa gyokuseki konkō de aru.","german":"The information is a mix of good and bad.","furigana":"じょうほう|ぎょくせきこんこう"},{"japanese":"ネット上のレビューは玉石混淆なので、うのみにしないほうがいい。","romaji":"Netto-jō no rebyū wa gyokuseki konkō na node, unomi ni shinai hō ga ii.","german":"Online reviews are a mix of good and bad, so you shouldn't swallow them whole.","furigana":"じょう|ぎょくせきこんこう"},{"japanese":"応募作品は玉石混淆で、審査に時間がかかった。","romaji":"Ōbo sakuhin wa gyokuseki konkō de, shinsa ni jikan ga kakatta.","german":"The submitted works were of very mixed quality, and the judging took a long time.","furigana":"おうぼさくひん|ぎょくせきこんこう|しんさ|じかん"}],"notes":"玉石混淆 is made up of 玉 \"gem\", 石 \"ordinary stone\" and 混淆 \"mixture\": valuable and worthless things lie jumbled together. It describes sets of information, works or applicants whose quality varies widely: 〜は玉石混淆だ, 玉石混淆の情報. Grammatically it is a noun (with だ, の, で). Often also written 玉石混交.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"犯人の手がかりがなく、五里霧中だ。","romaji":"Hannin no tegakari ga naku, gori muchū da.","german":"There are no clues to the culprit, and we are groping in the dark.","furigana":"はんにん|て|ごりむちゅう"},{"japanese":"新しい仕事は分からないことばかりで、まだ五里霧中の状態です。","romaji":"Atarashii shigoto wa wakaranai koto bakari de, mada gori muchū no jōtai desu.","german":"The new job is full of things I don't understand, and I'm still completely at sea.","furigana":"あたら|しごと|わ|ごりむちゅう|じょうたい"},{"japanese":"交渉は五里霧中のまま、三か月が過ぎた。","romaji":"Kōshō wa gori muchū no mama, sankagetsu ga sugita.","german":"Negotiations remained in the dark for three months.","furigana":"こうしょう|ごりむちゅう|さん|げつ|す"}],"notes":"五里霧中 means \"in the middle of a fog five ri (an old unit of length) wide\": you have no orientation at all and don't know how things will continue. Typical are 五里霧中だ, 五里霧中の状態, 五里霧中のまま. A common writing mistake: 夢中 instead of 霧中. Neutral to literary, e.g. for investigations, negotiations or a new job.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"傍目には幸せそうに見える。","romaji":"Hatame ni wa shiawase sō ni mieru.","german":"To an onlooker, he seems happy.","furigana":"はため|しあわ|み"},{"japanese":"傍目には楽な仕事に見えるが、実際はかなり大変だ。","romaji":"Hatame ni wa raku na shigoto ni mieru ga, jissai wa kanari taihen da.","german":"It looks like an easy job from the outside, but it is actually quite hard.","furigana":"はため|らく|しごと|み|じっさい|たいへん"},{"japanese":"二人は傍目にもわかるほど仲が悪かった。","romaji":"Futari wa hatame ni mo wakaru hodo naka ga warukatta.","german":"The two got along so badly that even bystanders could tell.","furigana":"ふたり|はため|なか|わる"}],"notes":"傍目 (はため, often also written はた目) is the view of an outsider; 傍目に, usually 傍目には, means \"from the outside, to outsiders\" and almost always introduces a contrast with reality: 傍目には幸せそうに見えるが…. Similar are 外から見ると and はたから見ると. With the reading おかめ, 傍目 appears in the proverb 傍目八目 \"onlookers see more than the players\". Neutral to slightly literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"逆境を跳ね返す力がある。","romaji":"Gyakkyō o hanekaesu chikara ga aru.","german":"He has the strength to bounce back from adversity.","furigana":"ぎゃっきょう|は|かえ|ちから"},{"japanese":"キーパーがシュートを跳ね返した。","romaji":"Kīpā ga shūto o hanekaeshita.","german":"The goalkeeper deflected the shot.","furigana":"は|かえ"},{"japanese":"白い壁は日光を跳ね返すので、部屋が明るい。","romaji":"Shiroi kabe wa nikkō o hanekaesu node, heya ga akarui.","german":"A white wall reflects sunlight, so the room is bright.","furigana":"しろ|かべ|にっこう|は|かえ|へや|あか"}],"notes":"跳ね返す is transitive and concretely means \"to make bounce back, to ward off, to reflect\" (ボール・光を跳ね返す), figuratively \"to overcome, to not let get one down\": 逆境・圧力・プレッシャーを跳ね返す. The intransitive counterpart is 跳ね返る \"to rebound, to spring back\" (ボールが壁に跳ね返る). In sports it is the typical verb for a save or a rebound.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"知性と美貌を兼ね備えている。","romaji":"Chisei to bibō o kanesonaete iru.","german":"She has both intelligence and beauty.","furigana":"ちせい|びぼう|か|そな"},{"japanese":"この車は燃費の良さと走りの楽しさを兼ね備えている。","romaji":"Kono kuruma wa nenpi no yosa to hashiri no tanoshisa o kanesonaete iru.","german":"This car combines good fuel economy with fun driving.","furigana":"くるま|ねんぴ|よ|はし|たの|か|そな"},{"japanese":"リーダーには決断力と思いやりを兼ね備えた人が求められる。","romaji":"Rīdā ni wa ketsudanryoku to omoiyari o kanesonaeta hito ga motomerareru.","german":"Leaders are expected to be people who combine decisiveness and compassion.","furigana":"けつだんりょく|おも|か|そな|ひと|もと"}],"notes":"兼ね備える means \"to possess at the same time, to combine in oneself\", usually two positive qualities: AとBを兼ね備える, often as 兼ね備えている or before nouns 〜を兼ね備えた人物. It is praising and rather literary. Not to be confused with 兼ねる, which expresses a double function (趣味と実益を兼ねる \"to combine hobby and benefit\", 会議室を兼ねる).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新たな事実が浮かび上がった。","romaji":"Arata na jijitsu ga ukabiagatta.","german":"New facts have emerged.","furigana":"あら|じじつ|う|あ"},{"japanese":"潜水艦がゆっくりと海面に浮かび上がった。","romaji":"Sensuikan ga yukkuri to kaimen ni ukabiagatta.","german":"The submarine slowly rose to the surface of the sea.","furigana":"せんすいかん|かいめん|う|あ"},{"japanese":"ライトアップされた城が、夜空にくっきりと浮かび上がっている。","romaji":"Raitoappu sareta shiro ga, yozora ni kukkiri to ukabiagatte iru.","german":"The illuminated castle stands out sharply against the night sky.","furigana":"しろ|よぞら|う|あ"}],"notes":"浮かび上がる is intransitive and literally means \"to rise to the surface\" (水面に浮かび上がる). Figuratively it means \"to come to light, to emerge\" (新たな事実・容疑者が浮かび上がる) or \"to stand out clearly\" against a background (夜空に浮かび上がる). The simple 浮かぶ, by contrast, means \"to float, to drift\" or \"to come to mind\". The transitive form is 浮かび上がらせる; close in meaning is 浮き彫りになる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"交渉が妥結した。","romaji":"Kōshō ga daketsu shita.","german":"The negotiations were settled.","furigana":"こうしょう|だけつ"},{"japanese":"賃上げ交渉は3パーセントで妥結した。","romaji":"Chin'age kōshō wa san-pāsento de daketsu shita.","german":"The wage negotiations were settled at 3 percent.","furigana":"ちんあ|こうしょう|だけつ"},{"japanese":"両国の貿易協議は、年内の妥結を目指している。","romaji":"Ryōkoku no bōeki kyōgi wa, nennai no daketsu o mezashite iru.","german":"The two countries' trade talks aim for a settlement within the year.","furigana":"りょうこく|ぼうえききょうぎ|ねんない|だけつ|めざ"}],"notes":"妥結する means \"to reach an agreement (after negotiations), to come to a conclusion\" and is intransitive: the subject is the negotiations themselves (交渉が妥結する). The result takes で: 3パーセントで妥結する. It is a word from news, business and politics, often also as a noun (妥結を目指す). Unlike 妥協する \"to make concessions\", it describes the successful conclusion, not the giving way.","levelNote":"Derived from 妥結 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の思想に傾倒する。","romaji":"Kare no shisō ni keitō suru.","german":"I am devoted to his ideas.","furigana":"かれ|しそう|けいとう"},{"japanese":"学生時代、私はドストエフスキーに傾倒していた。","romaji":"Gakusei jidai, watashi wa Dosutoefusukī ni keitō shite ita.","german":"In my student days I was an ardent admirer of Dostoevsky.","furigana":"がくせいじだい|わたし|けいとう"},{"japanese":"彼女は晩年、仏教に深く傾倒した。","romaji":"Kanojo wa bannen, bukkyō ni fukaku keitō shita.","german":"In her later years she became deeply devoted to Buddhism.","furigana":"かのじょ|ばんねん|ぶっきょう|ふか|けいとう"}],"notes":"傾倒する literally means \"to lean\" and means \"to devote oneself enthusiastically to (a person, doctrine or art), to be an ardent follower\". The target takes に: 思想・作家・宗教に傾倒する, often reinforced by 深く. Unlike 熱中する \"to be absorbed in an activity\", it is about intellectual veneration; 心酔する is even more rapturous. Literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"理想と現実が大きく隔たっている。","romaji":"Risō to genjitsu ga ōkiku hedatatte iru.","german":"The ideal and reality are far apart.","furigana":"りそう|げんじつ|おお|へだ"},{"japanese":"二人の意見は大きく隔たっていて、話し合いは平行線だった。","romaji":"Futari no iken wa ōkiku hedatatte ite, hanashiai wa heikōsen datta.","german":"The two people's opinions were far apart, and the discussion went nowhere.","furigana":"ふたり|いけん|おお|へだ|はな|あ|へいこうせん"},{"japanese":"故郷から遠く隔たった町で暮らしている。","romaji":"Kokyō kara tōku hedatatta machi de kurashite iru.","german":"I live in a town far away from my hometown.","furigana":"こきょう|とお|へだ|まち|く"}],"notes":"隔たる is intransitive and means \"to be distant\", spatially, temporally or figuratively with opinions and ideas: 意見が大きく隔たる, 理想と現実が隔たる. It mostly appears in the form 隔たっている or attributively 隔たった. The transitive counterpart is 隔てる \"to separate\" (川を隔てて). Very common is the noun 隔たり \"distance, gap\" (意見の隔たり).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は辞任を仄めかした。","romaji":"Kare wa jinin o honomekashita.","german":"He hinted at his resignation.","furigana":"かれ|じにん|ほの"},{"japanese":"彼女は転職を考えていることをそれとなくほのめかした。","romaji":"Kanojo wa tenshoku o kangaete iru koto o soretonaku honomekashita.","german":"She subtly hinted that she is thinking of changing jobs.","furigana":"かのじょ|てんしょく|かんが"},{"japanese":"犯人は手紙の中で次の犯行をほのめかしていた。","romaji":"Hannin wa tegami no naka de tsugi no hankō o honomekashite ita.","german":"In the letter, the culprit had hinted at the next crime.","furigana":"はんにん|てがみ|なか|つぎ|はんこう"}],"notes":"ほのめかす is transitive and means \"to hint, to let on\" without saying it openly: 辞任・引退・関与をほのめかす, also 〜ことをほのめかす. それとなく often stands before it. It is more neutral than the colloquial 匂わせる and more everyday than the abstract 暗示する. Mostly written in kana; the kanji 仄めかす are rare.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"努力が空回りしている。","romaji":"Doryoku ga karamawari shite iru.","german":"My efforts are spinning their wheels.","furigana":"どりょく|からまわ"},{"japanese":"雪道でタイヤが空回りして、車が動かなくなった。","romaji":"Yukimichi de taiya ga karamawari shite, kuruma ga ugokanaku natta.","german":"The tires spun on the snowy road and the car stopped moving.","furigana":"ゆきみち|からまわ|くるま|うご"},{"japanese":"初日で張り切りすぎて、気持ちばかりが空回りした。","romaji":"Shonichi de harikirisugite, kimochi bakari ga karamawari shita.","german":"I was too keen on the first day, and my feelings just went to waste.","furigana":"しょにち|は|き|きも|からまわ"}],"notes":"空回りする literally means \"to turn idly\" (engine, wheels: タイヤが空回りする) and figuratively \"to run in vain, to achieve nothing\". Typical are 努力・議論が空回りする and 気持ちが空回りする, when someone is overeager but has no effect. It is intransitive and rather colloquial-neutral. Compared with 無駄になる, it stresses the frantic effort without result.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"最初の段階で躓いた。","romaji":"Saisho no dankai de tsumazuita.","german":"I stumbled at the very first stage.","furigana":"さいしょ|だんかい|つまず"},{"japanese":"段差につまずいて転びそうになった。","romaji":"Dansa ni tsumazuite korobisō ni natta.","german":"I tripped on a step and almost fell.","furigana":"だんさ|ころ"},{"japanese":"数学は分数でつまずく子どもが多い。","romaji":"Sūgaku wa bunsū de tsumazuku kodomo ga ōi.","german":"Many children get stuck on fractions in math.","furigana":"すうがく|ぶんすう|こ|おお"}],"notes":"つまずく is intransitive and concretely means \"to trip\": the obstacle is marked with に (石・段差につまずく). Figuratively it means \"to fail, to get stuck\"; then the point is marked with で (最初の段階で・分数でつまずく). Unlike 転ぶ \"to fall down\", it refers only to catching one's foot. Usually written in kana; the kanji 躓く are difficult and rare.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"議論が噛み合わない。","romaji":"Giron ga kamiawanai.","german":"The discussion isn't meshing.","furigana":"ぎろん|か|あ"},{"japanese":"歯車がうまく噛み合わないと、機械は動かない。","romaji":"Haguruma ga umaku kamiawanai to, kikai wa ugokanai.","german":"If the gears don't mesh well, the machine won't run.","furigana":"はぐるま|か|あ|きかい|うご"},{"japanese":"今日は攻撃と守備がうまく噛み合って、快勝した。","romaji":"Kyō wa kōgeki to shubi ga umaku kamiatte, kaishō shita.","german":"Today the offense and defense meshed well, and we won easily.","furigana":"きょう|こうげき|しゅび|か|あ|かいしょう"}],"notes":"噛み合う concretely means \"to mesh\" (gears, teeth) and is intransitive: 歯車が噛み合う. Figuratively it says that conversations or parts \"fit together\"; especially common is the negation 議論・話が噛み合わない \"to talk past each other\". In sports: 攻守が噛み合う \"offense and defense work in harmony\". The transitive form is 噛み合わせる; also written かみ合う.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"陳腐な表現は避けるべきだ。","romaji":"Chinpu na hyōgen wa sakeru beki da.","german":"Clichéd expressions should be avoided.","furigana":"ちんぷ|ひょうげん|さ"},{"japanese":"そんな陳腐な言い訳はもう聞き飽きた。","romaji":"Sonna chinpu na iiwake wa mō kikiakita.","german":"I'm tired of hearing such stale excuses.","furigana":"ちんぷ|い|わけ|き|あ"},{"japanese":"技術の進歩が速く、製品がすぐに陳腐化してしまう。","romaji":"Gijutsu no shinpo ga hayaku, seihin ga sugu ni chinpuka shite shimau.","german":"Technology advances so fast that products quickly become obsolete.","furigana":"ぎじゅつ|しんぽ|はや|せいひん|ちんぷか"}],"notes":"陳腐な means \"hackneyed, banal, outdated\" and criticizes expressions, ideas or stories you have heard too often: 陳腐な表現・言い訳・セリフ. It is sharper than ありきたりな \"ordinary\"; 古臭い stresses the old-fashioned more. In business, 陳腐化 means the \"becoming obsolete, obsolescence\" of products and technology.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"手を尽くしたが助からなかった。","romaji":"Te o tsukushita ga tasukaranakatta.","german":"We did everything we could, but he couldn't be saved.","furigana":"て|つ|たす"},{"japanese":"八方手を尽くして探したが、猫は見つからなかった。","romaji":"Happō te o tsukushite sagashita ga, neko wa mitsukaranakatta.","german":"I searched by every possible means, but the cat was not found.","furigana":"はっぽうて|つ|さが|ねこ|み"},{"japanese":"会社はあらゆる手を尽くして、倒産を回避した。","romaji":"Kaisha wa arayuru te o tsukushite, tōsan o kaihi shita.","german":"The company tried every possible measure and avoided bankruptcy.","furigana":"かいしゃ|て|つ|とうさん|かいひ"}],"notes":"手を尽くす means \"to exhaust all means, to leave nothing untried\", literally \"to use up one's hands (means)\". It is reinforced by あらゆる手を尽くす or 八方手を尽くす \"to try everything in every direction\". Very often a disappointing result follows with が or けれど. Similar are 全力を尽くす \"to give it everything\" and 最善を尽くす \"to do one's best\", which however stress effort rather than means.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"毅然とした態度で臨む。","romaji":"Kizen to shita taido de nozomu.","german":"I face it with a resolute attitude.","furigana":"きぜん|たいど|のぞ"},{"japanese":"理不尽な要求には毅然とした対応が必要だ。","romaji":"Rifujin na yōkyū ni wa kizen to shita taiō ga hitsuyō da.","german":"Unreasonable demands require a firm response.","furigana":"りふじん|ようきゅう|きぜん|たいおう|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"彼女は記者の質問にも毅然とした表情で答えた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kisha no shitsumon ni mo kizen to shita hyōjō de kotaeta.","german":"She answered the reporters' questions, too, with a resolute expression.","furigana":"かのじょ|きしゃ|しつもん|きぜん|ひょうじょう|こた"}],"notes":"毅然とした is the attributive form of 毅然 and describes a \"resolute, unbending\" attitude, especially toward pressure or unjust demands: 毅然とした態度・対応・表情. Adverbially it is 毅然と(して)断る; literary 毅然たる. Similar is 断固とした, which stresses the firm decision more, whereas 毅然 emphasizes dignified, calm steadfastness.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"荘厳な雰囲気に包まれた。","romaji":"Sōgon na fun'iki ni tsutsumareta.","german":"We were enveloped in a solemn atmosphere.","furigana":"そうごん|ふんいき|つつ"},{"japanese":"大聖堂にパイプオルガンの荘厳な音色が響いた。","romaji":"Daiseidō ni paipu orugan no sōgon na neiro ga hibiita.","german":"The majestic sound of the pipe organ echoed in the cathedral.","furigana":"だいせいどう|そうごん|ねいろ|ひび"},{"japanese":"式典は荘厳に執り行われた。","romaji":"Shikiten wa sōgon ni toriokonawareta.","german":"The ceremony was conducted with solemnity.","furigana":"しきてん|そうごん|と|おこな"}],"notes":"荘厳な (そうごん) means \"sublime, solemn, majestic\" and describes buildings, music, ceremonies or an atmosphere that inspires awe: 荘厳な寺院・音楽・儀式. Close in meaning is 厳か (おごそか), which stresses solemn stillness more; 壮大な refers to sheer size. The reading しょうごん denotes the adorning of temples in Buddhism and is a different word.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"教育が社会の根幹を成す。","romaji":"Kyōiku ga shakai no konkan o nasu.","german":"Education forms the foundation of society.","furigana":"きょういく|しゃかい|こんかん|な"},{"japanese":"信頼関係こそが、このチームの根幹を成している。","romaji":"Shinrai kankei koso ga, kono chīmu no konkan o nashite iru.","german":"Mutual trust is exactly what forms the core of this team.","furigana":"しんらいかんけい|こんかん|な"},{"japanese":"国民主権は、憲法の根幹を成す原則の一つだ。","romaji":"Kokumin shuken wa, kenpō no konkan o nasu gensoku no hitotsu da.","german":"Popular sovereignty is one of the principles that form the core of the constitution.","furigana":"こくみんしゅけん|けんぽう|こんかん|な|げんそく|ひと"}],"notes":"根幹 literally means \"root and trunk\", hence \"core, foundation\"; 根幹を成す (often also 根幹をなす) means \"to form the foundation of something\". What it is the foundation of comes before it with の: 社会・憲法・計画の根幹を成す. Related expressions are 根幹にかかわる \"to concern the fundamentals\" and 根幹を揺るがす \"to shake to the foundations\". Compared with 基礎 or 土台, it sounds more elevated and stresses the essential.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不正を白日の下に晒した。","romaji":"Fusei o hakujitsu no moto ni sarashita.","german":"He exposed the wrongdoing to the light of day.","furigana":"ふせい|はくじつ|もと|さら"},{"japanese":"記者の調査によって、政治家の汚職が白日の下に晒された。","romaji":"Kisha no chōsa ni yotte, seijika no oshoku ga hakujitsu no moto ni sarasareta.","german":"The reporter's investigation brought the politician's corruption to light.","furigana":"きしゃ|ちょうさ|せいじか|おしょく|はくじつ|もと|さら"},{"japanese":"内部告発者は、会社の隠蔽工作を白日の下に晒す覚悟を決めた。","romaji":"Naibu kokuhatsusha wa, kaisha no inpei kōsaku o hakujitsu no moto ni sarasu kakugo o kimeta.","german":"The whistleblower resolved to expose the company's cover-up.","furigana":"ないぶこくはつしゃ|かいしゃ|いんぺいこうさく|はくじつ|もと|さら|かくご|き"}],"notes":"白日 is \"bright daylight\", 下(もと) \"under\"; 白日の下に晒す thus means \"to drag into broad daylight, to expose publicly\". It almost always concerns hidden wrongdoing: 不正・汚職・隠蔽を白日の下に晒す. Very common is the passive 白日の下に晒される \"to come to light\". The verb is often written in kana as さらす; the expression is literary and typical of the press and factual texts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"翻って日本の状況を見てみよう。","romaji":"Hirugaette Nihon no jōkyō o mite miyō.","german":"Let us now look at the situation in Japan.","furigana":"ひるがえ|にほん|じょうきょう|み"},{"japanese":"他人の欠点はよく見える。翻って自分はどうだろうか。","romaji":"Tanin no ketten wa yoku mieru. Hirugaette jibun wa dō darō ka.","german":"Other people's faults are easy to see. What about my own?","furigana":"たにん|けってん|み|ひるがえ|じぶん"},{"japanese":"若い頃は親の忠告がうるさかった。翻って考えると、あれは愛情だったのだ。","romaji":"Wakai koro wa oya no chūkoku ga urusakatta. Hirugaette kangaeru to, are wa aijō datta no da.","german":"When I was young, my parents' advice was annoying. Looking back, that was affection.","furigana":"わか|ころ|おや|ちゅうこく|ひるがえ|かんが|あいじょう"}],"notes":"翻って is the て-form of 翻る \"to turn around\" and introduces a change of perspective: \"if one turns the other way, on the other hand\". It mostly stands at the beginning of a sentence after a statement about others, and you turn your gaze to yourself or your own country: 翻って日本は・自分は…, or 翻って考えると. It is literary, typical of essays and speeches, and does not simply mean \"on the contrary\" (それに反して).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"重篤な症状が出ている。","romaji":"Jūtoku na shōjō ga dete iru.","german":"Serious symptoms have appeared.","furigana":"じゅうとく|しょうじょう|で"},{"japanese":"患者は事故後、重篤な状態が続いている。","romaji":"Kanja wa jiko go, jūtoku na jōtai ga tsuzuite iru.","german":"The patient's critical condition has continued since the accident.","furigana":"かんじゃ|じこご|じゅうとく|じょうたい|つづ"},{"japanese":"この薬には、まれに重篤な副作用がある。","romaji":"Kono kusuri ni wa, mare ni jūtoku na fukusayō ga aru.","german":"This drug has rare but serious side effects.","furigana":"くすり|じゅうとく|ふくさよう"}],"notes":"重篤な is a medical technical term for \"severe, life-threatening\": 重篤な症状・副作用・患者, 重篤な状態 \"critical condition\". It is stronger and more formal than 重い or 重症の and appears above all in news, drug information leaflets and hospital reports. For serious errors or crimes one does not say 重篤 but 重大な. The related verb is 重篤化する \"to worsen to a life-threatening state\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"奇抜なアイデアを提案した。","romaji":"Kibatsu na aidea o teian shita.","german":"He proposed an unconventional idea.","furigana":"きばつ|ていあん"},{"japanese":"彼はいつも奇抜な服装で現れる。","romaji":"Kare wa itsumo kibatsu na fukusō de arawareru.","german":"He always shows up in outlandish clothes.","furigana":"かれ|きばつ|ふくそう|あらわ"},{"japanese":"その建物は奇抜なデザインで、街の名所になっている。","romaji":"Sono tatemono wa kibatsu na dezain de, machi no meisho ni natte iru.","german":"The building has an eccentric design and has become a landmark of the city.","furigana":"たてもの|きばつ|まち|めいしょ"}],"notes":"奇抜な means \"unconventional, original, strikingly unusual\": 奇抜なアイデア・デザイン・服装・発想. Depending on context it is praise (\"original\") or mild criticism (\"flashy\"). 斬新な \"innovative\" is clearly positive, 突飛な \"wild, far-fetched\" rather negative, and 奇妙な simply means \"strange\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"理論と実践が乖離している。","romaji":"Riron to jissen ga kairi shite iru.","german":"Theory and practice have diverged.","furigana":"りろん|じっせん|かいり"},{"japanese":"世論調査の結果は、実際の選挙結果と大きく乖離していた。","romaji":"Yoron chōsa no kekka wa, jissai no senkyo kekka to ōkiku kairi shite ita.","german":"The poll results diverged greatly from the actual election results.","furigana":"よろんちょうさ|けっか|じっさい|せんきょけっか|おお|かいり"},{"japanese":"政治が国民の感覚から乖離してはならない。","romaji":"Seiji ga kokumin no kankaku kara kairi shite wa naranai.","german":"Politics must not become detached from the sentiments of the people.","furigana":"せいじ|こくみん|かんかく|かいり"}],"notes":"乖離する means \"to diverge, to deviate\" and is intransitive: AとBが乖離する or AがBから乖離する. Even more common than the verb is the noun: 理想と現実の乖離, 〜との乖離が大きい. It is a formal word from business, politics and science; in everyday speech people say ずれる or かけ離れる. Also written かい離.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"権利には義務が付随する。","romaji":"Kenri ni wa gimu ga fuzui suru.","german":"Rights come with duties.","furigana":"けんり|ぎむ|ふずい"},{"japanese":"引っ越しに付随する手続きが多くて大変だ。","romaji":"Hikkoshi ni fuzui suru tetsuzuki ga ōkute taihen da.","german":"There are many procedures that go with moving, which is hard work.","furigana":"ひ|こ|ふずい|てつづ|おお|たいへん"},{"japanese":"地震に付随して、各地で停電が発生した。","romaji":"Jishin ni fuzui shite, kakuchi de teiden ga hassei shita.","german":"Along with the earthquake, power outages occurred in various places.","furigana":"じしん|ふずい|かくち|ていでん|はっせい"}],"notes":"付随する means \"to go along with something, to be connected with it\" and is intransitive: AにBが付随する \"B goes along with A\". Typical are 〜に付随する問題・業務・手続き and 〜に付随して起こる. Close in meaning and more everyday is 伴う (〜に伴って); 付随 sounds more formal and stresses that B is a side phenomenon of A.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"相手を挑発する発言をした。","romaji":"Aite o chōhatsu suru hatsugen o shita.","german":"He made a remark that provoked the other party.","furigana":"あいて|ちょうはつ|はつげん"},{"japanese":"安い挑発に乗ってはいけない。","romaji":"Yasui chōhatsu ni notte wa ikenai.","german":"You must not rise to cheap provocations.","furigana":"やす|ちょうはつ|の"},{"japanese":"隣国はミサイル発射で周辺国を挑発した。","romaji":"Ringoku wa misairu hassha de shūhenkoku o chōhatsu shita.","german":"The neighboring country provoked surrounding countries with a missile launch.","furigana":"りんごく|はっしゃ|しゅうへんこく|ちょうはつ"}],"notes":"挑発する means \"to provoke, to challenge\" and is transitive: 相手を挑発する. Very common are the noun in 挑発に乗る \"to take the bait\" and the adjective 挑発的な \"provocative\" (also for revealing clothes). In politics it denotes military saber-rattling. Unlike 挑戦する \"to take on a challenge\", with 挑発 you want to trigger a reaction in the other person.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"記念誌を関係者に頒布する。","romaji":"Kinenshi o kankeisha ni hanpu suru.","german":"We will distribute the commemorative volume to those concerned.","furigana":"きねんし|かんけいしゃ|はんぷ"},{"japanese":"同人誌即売会で、自作の漫画を頒布した。","romaji":"Dōjinshi sokubaikai de, jisaku no manga o hanpu shita.","german":"I distributed my own manga at a doujinshi fair.","furigana":"どうじんしそくばいかい|じさく|まんが|はんぷ"},{"japanese":"この神社では、お守りやお札を頒布している。","romaji":"Kono jinja de wa, omamori ya ofuda o hanpu shite iru.","german":"This shrine distributes amulets and talismans.","furigana":"じんじゃ|まも|ふだ|はんぷ"}],"notes":"頒布する means \"to distribute, to hand out (to many people)\", free of charge or for a fee, and is transitive. It is used above all for printed matter and special items: 記念誌を頒布する; shrines hand out amulets (お守り) this way, and at doujinshi fairs people deliberately say 頒布 instead of 販売. For working documents in everyday life 配布する or 配る is usual; 頒布 sounds official and formal.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"影響が他の産業にも波及した。","romaji":"Eikyō ga hoka no sangyō ni mo hakyū shita.","german":"The impact spread to other industries.","furigana":"えいきょう|ほか|さんぎょう|はきゅう"},{"japanese":"一つのクラスで始まった運動が、学校全体に波及した。","romaji":"Hitotsu no kurasu de hajimatta undō ga, gakkō zentai ni hakyū shita.","german":"A movement that started in one class spread to the whole school.","furigana":"ひと|はじ|うんどう|がっこうぜんたい|はきゅう"},{"japanese":"オリンピックの開催は、地域経済に大きな波及効果をもたらす。","romaji":"Orinpikku no kaisai wa, chiiki keizai ni ōkina hakyū kōka o motarasu.","german":"Hosting the Olympics brings a large ripple effect to the regional economy.","furigana":"かいさい|ちいきけいざい|おお|はきゅうこうか"}],"notes":"波及する literally means \"to spread like waves\" and means \"to spill over, to have an effect on\". It is intransitive; the target takes に: 影響が他の業界に波及する. Very common is 波及効果 \"ripple effect, multiplier effect\". Compared with the general 広がる, it stresses the chain reaction from a starting point; typical of business and news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"信念を貫く。","romaji":"Shinnen o tsuranuku.","german":"I stand by my convictions.","furigana":"しんねん|つらぬ"},{"japanese":"弾丸が窓ガラスを貫いた。","romaji":"Dangan ga madogarasu o tsuranuita.","german":"The bullet pierced the window glass.","furigana":"だんがん|まど|つらぬ"},{"japanese":"町の中心を貫いて大きな川が流れている。","romaji":"Machi no chūshin o tsuranuite ōkina kawa ga nagarete iru.","german":"A large river runs through the center of the town.","furigana":"まち|ちゅうしん|つらぬ|おお|かわ|なが"}],"notes":"貫く is transitive and has three uses: \"to pierce, to penetrate\" (弾丸が壁を貫く), \"to run through\" (町を貫いて流れる川) and figuratively \"to stick to something to the end\" (信念・意志・独身を貫く). In the figurative sense it is stronger than 通す and stresses steadfastness against resistance. The compound 貫き通す \"to persevere unwaveringly\" is also common.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仕事が捗っている。","romaji":"Shigoto ga hakadotte iru.","german":"The work is progressing well.","furigana":"しごと|はかど"},{"japanese":"静かな図書館だと勉強がはかどる。","romaji":"Shizuka na toshokan da to benkyō ga hakadoru.","german":"Studying goes well in a quiet library.","furigana":"しず|としょかん|べんきょう"},{"japanese":"暑さのせいで、作業がなかなかはかどらない。","romaji":"Atsusa no sei de, sagyō ga nakanaka hakadoranai.","german":"Because of the heat, the work isn't progressing at all.","furigana":"あつ|さぎょう"}],"notes":"はかどる is intransitive and means \"to progress well\"; the subject is the work itself: 仕事・勉強・作業がはかどる. The negation はかどらない \"isn't making progress\" is very common. Compared with 進む, it stresses swift, efficient progress; the noun 進捗 is the formal word for \"progress\". Mostly written in kana; the kanji 捗る are rare.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"事実を覆い隠すことはできない。","romaji":"Jijitsu o ōikakusu koto wa dekinai.","german":"The facts cannot be covered up.","furigana":"じじつ|おお|かく"},{"japanese":"厚い雲が山頂を覆い隠していた。","romaji":"Atsui kumo ga sanchō o ōikakushite ita.","german":"Thick clouds were hiding the summit.","furigana":"あつ|くも|さんちょう|おお|かく"},{"japanese":"彼女は笑顔で不安を覆い隠した。","romaji":"Kanojo wa egao de fuan o ōikakushita.","german":"She masked her anxiety with a smile.","furigana":"かのじょ|えがお|ふあん|おお|かく"}],"notes":"覆い隠す is transitive and combines 覆う \"to cover\" with 隠す \"to hide\": \"to conceal completely, to shroud\". Concretely, clouds or cloths hide something (雲が山頂を覆い隠す); figuratively one conceals facts or feelings (事実・不安を覆い隠す). It is stronger and more vivid than the simple 隠す and sounds rather literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自分の無力さを思い知った。","romaji":"Jibun no muryokusa o omoishitta.","german":"I painfully realized my own powerlessness.","furigana":"じぶん|むりょく|おも|し"},{"japanese":"留学して、言葉の壁の厚さを思い知らされた。","romaji":"Ryūgaku shite, kotoba no kabe no atsusa o omoishirasareta.","german":"Studying abroad made me painfully aware of how thick the language barrier is.","furigana":"りゅうがく|ことば|かべ|あつ|おも|し"},{"japanese":"今度こそ思い知らせてやる！","romaji":"Kondo koso omoishirasete yaru!","german":"This time I'll teach you a lesson!","furigana":"こんど|おも|し"}],"notes":"思い知る means to painfully grasp something, usually unpleasant, through your own experience; the object takes を: 無力さを思い知る, 〜ことを思い知る. Very common is the passive 思い知らされる \"to be painfully made to realize\". The causative 思い知らせてやる is a threat: \"I'll show you!\". Neutral noticing is 気づく; 痛感する is a more formal synonym.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい考え方が社会に浸透した。","romaji":"Atarashii kangaekata ga shakai ni shintō shita.","german":"The new way of thinking has permeated society.","furigana":"あたら|かんが|かた|しゃかい|しんとう"},{"japanese":"雨水が地面に浸透する。","romaji":"Amamizu ga jimen ni shintō suru.","german":"Rainwater seeps into the ground.","furigana":"あまみず|じめん|しんとう"},{"japanese":"新しいルールが社内にまだ浸透していない。","romaji":"Atarashii rūru ga shanai ni mada shintō shite inai.","german":"The new rules have not yet taken hold within the company.","furigana":"あたら|しゃない|しんとう"}],"notes":"浸透する is intransitive and describes how ideas, rules, products or also liquids gradually penetrate something: 社会に浸透する, 水が土に浸透する. The destination takes に, the thing penetrating takes が. For the spread of devices or technology one says 普及する rather, in everyday speech simply 広まる. Often negated: まだ浸透していない \"has not yet caught on\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"問題が顕在化してきた。","romaji":"Mondai ga kenzaika shite kita.","german":"The problem has started to surface.","furigana":"もんだい|けんざいか"},{"japanese":"少子化の影響が地方で顕在化している。","romaji":"Shōshika no eikyō ga chihō de kenzaika shite iru.","german":"The effects of the declining birthrate are becoming apparent in the regions.","furigana":"しょうしか|えいきょう|ちほう|けんざいか"},{"japanese":"調査によって、隠れていたニーズを顕在化させることができた。","romaji":"Chōsa ni yotte, kakurete ita nīzu o kenzaika saseru koto ga dekita.","german":"The survey allowed us to bring hidden needs to the surface.","furigana":"ちょうさ|かく|けんざいか"}],"notes":"顕在化する is a literary word for problems, risks or needs that were hidden until now and become visible: 問題が顕在化する. The opposite is 潜在化 or 潜在的 \"latent\". Transitively the causative 〜を顕在化させる \"to make visible\" is used. Typical of news, reports and business texts; in everyday speech people say 表に出る or はっきりしてくる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"議論が一つの結論に収斂した。","romaji":"Giron ga hitotsu no ketsuron ni shūren shita.","german":"The discussion converged on one conclusion.","furigana":"ぎろん|ひと|けつろん|しゅうれん"},{"japanese":"光がレンズで一点に収斂する。","romaji":"Hikari ga renzu de itten ni shūren suru.","german":"Light converges to a single point at the lens.","furigana":"ひかり|いってん|しゅうれん"},{"japanese":"業界の技術は次第に同じ方向に収斂しつつある。","romaji":"Gyōkai no gijutsu wa shidai ni onaji hōkō ni shūren shitsutsu aru.","german":"The industry's technologies are gradually converging in the same direction.","furigana":"ぎょうかい|ぎじゅつ|しだい|おな|ほうこう|しゅうれん"}],"notes":"収斂する is an elevated, often technical word for \"to converge, to be bundled\": opinions or developments 〜に収斂する, light is focused at one point. In biology there is 収斂進化 \"convergent evolution\", in cosmetics 収れん化粧水 \"astringent toner\". Often also written 収れん. In everyday speech people say まとまる; for mathematical convergence 収束 is usual today.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"痛烈な批判を受けた。","romaji":"Tsūretsu na hihan o uketa.","german":"He received scathing criticism.","furigana":"つうれつ|ひはん|う"},{"japanese":"彼女は上司に痛烈な皮肉を言った。","romaji":"Kanojo wa jōshi ni tsūretsu na hiniku o itta.","german":"She made a cutting remark to her boss.","furigana":"かのじょ|じょうし|つうれつ|ひにく|い"},{"japanese":"その敗北は、チームにとって痛烈な一撃となった。","romaji":"Sono haiboku wa, chīmu ni totte tsūretsu na ichigeki to natta.","german":"That defeat became a severe blow for the team.","furigana":"はいぼく|つうれつ|いちげき"}],"notes":"痛烈な describes criticism, mockery or blows that hit especially hard: 痛烈な批判, 痛烈な皮肉, 痛烈な一撃. Adverbial: 痛烈に批判する \"to criticize sharply\". 厳しい is more general \"strict, harsh\"; 辛辣 stresses more the biting, spiteful tongue of a person. Rather literary and typical of news and reviews.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"尤もらしい理由を並べた。","romaji":"Mottomorashii riyū o narabeta.","german":"He listed plausible-sounding reasons.","furigana":"もっと|りゆう|なら"},{"japanese":"彼はもっともらしい顔で嘘をつく。","romaji":"Kare wa mottomorashii kao de uso o tsuku.","german":"He lies with a straight face.","furigana":"かれ|かお|うそ"},{"japanese":"ネットにはもっともらしいデマがあふれている。","romaji":"Netto ni wa mottomorashii dema ga afurete iru.","german":"The internet is flooded with plausible-sounding false rumors."}],"notes":"もっともらしい is almost always written in kana; 尤もらしい is rare. It means \"plausible-sounding\" and often has a suspicious undertone: something sounds reasonable but perhaps is not (もっともらしい理由, もっともらしい嘘). To be distinguished from もっともな \"truly justified\" (もっともな意見). もっともらしい顔 means \"a serious, weighty expression\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"円滑なコミュニケーションを図る。","romaji":"Enkatsu na komyunikēshon o hakaru.","german":"We aim for smooth communication.","furigana":"えんかつ|はか"},{"japanese":"会議は円滑に進んだ。","romaji":"Kaigi wa enkatsu ni susunda.","german":"The meeting went smoothly.","furigana":"かいぎ|えんかつ|すす"},{"japanese":"引き継ぎを円滑に行うため、マニュアルを作った。","romaji":"Hikitsugi o enkatsu ni okonau tame, manyuaru o tsukutta.","german":"We made a manual to ensure a smooth handover.","furigana":"ひ|つ|えんかつ|おこな|つく"}],"notes":"円滑な means \"smooth\" for processes, relationships and communication: 円滑な人間関係, 円滑な取引. Very often adverbial: 円滑に進む, 円滑に行う, 円滑に運営する. It is more formal than the loanword スムーズ, which is mostly used in everyday speech. Typical of business language and official texts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"効果が顕著に表れた。","romaji":"Kōka ga kencho ni arawareta.","german":"The effect was noticeably apparent.","furigana":"こうか|けんちょ|あらわ"},{"japanese":"薬を飲み始めてから、症状が顕著に改善した。","romaji":"Kusuri o nomihajimete kara, shōjō ga kencho ni kaizen shita.","german":"Since I started taking the medicine, the symptoms have improved markedly.","furigana":"くすり|の|はじ|しょうじょう|けんちょ|かいぜん"},{"japanese":"若者のテレビ離れが顕著に進んでいる。","romaji":"Wakamono no terebi banare ga kencho ni susunde iru.","german":"Young people turning away from television is progressing markedly.","furigana":"わかもの|ばな|けんちょ|すす"}],"notes":"顕著に is the adverbial form of 顕著な \"conspicuous, marked\": 顕著に表れる, 顕著に改善する, 顕著な差. It is literary and typical of reports, statistics and science. A close synonym is 著しく, which stresses the extent more; in everyday speech people say はっきり or 目立って.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"朧げに記憶している。","romaji":"Oboroge ni kioku shite iru.","german":"I remember it vaguely.","furigana":"おぼろ|きおく"},{"japanese":"霧の向こうに山の形が朧げに見えた。","romaji":"Kiri no mukō ni yama no katachi ga oboroge ni mieta.","german":"The shape of the mountain was faintly visible beyond the fog.","furigana":"きり|む|やま|かたち|おぼろ|み"},{"japanese":"計画の全体像が朧げに見えてきた。","romaji":"Keikaku no zentaizō ga oboroge ni miete kita.","german":"The overall picture of the plan has started to become dimly visible.","furigana":"けいかく|ぜんたいぞう|おぼろ|み"}],"notes":"朧げに (often おぼろげに in kana) describes that something is perceived or remembered only vaguely: おぼろげに覚えている, 朧げに見える. It comes from 朧 \"haze, veil\", as in 朧月 \"hazy moon\". More everyday and broader is ぼんやり; うっすら stresses \"very faintly, ever so slightly\". Sounds somewhat literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は虚勢を張って平気なふりをした。","romaji":"Kare wa kyosei o hatte heiki na furi o shita.","german":"He put on a brave front and pretended to be fine.","furigana":"かれ|きょせい|は|へいき"},{"japanese":"弱い犬ほど虚勢を張ってよく吠える。","romaji":"Yowai inu hodo kyosei o hatte yoku hoeru.","german":"The weaker the dog, the more it bluffs and barks.","furigana":"よわ|いぬ|きょせい|は|ほ"},{"japanese":"虚勢を張らずに、困ったら素直に助けを求めなさい。","romaji":"Kyosei o harazu ni, komattara sunao ni tasuke o motomenasai.","german":"Don't put on a brave front; if you're in trouble, honestly ask for help.","furigana":"きょせい|は|こま|すなお|たす|もと"}],"notes":"虚勢 is \"empty strength, bluff\", 張る \"to stretch, to put on display\": 虚勢を張る thus means to feign strength or courage although you are actually weak or afraid. It is not boasting about achievements (that would be 自慢する or 大口をたたく). Similar is 見栄を張る, which however concerns vanity and status symbols more; colloquially one says 強がる. The verb 張る conjugates normally (張って, 張らずに).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"改革の機運が高まっている。","romaji":"Kaikaku no kiun ga takamatte iru.","german":"The momentum for reform is growing.","furigana":"かいかく|きうん|たか"},{"japanese":"地域で環境保護への機運が高まりつつある。","romaji":"Chiiki de kankyō hogo e no kiun ga takamaritsutsu aru.","german":"Momentum for environmental protection is growing in the region.","furigana":"ちいき|かんきょうほご|きうん|たか"},{"japanese":"選挙を前に、政権交代の機運が一気に高まった。","romaji":"Senkyo o mae ni, seiken kōtai no kiun ga ikki ni takamatta.","german":"Ahead of the election, momentum for a change of government suddenly surged.","furigana":"せんきょ|まえ|せいけんこうたい|きうん|いっき|たか"}],"notes":"機運 is the favorable mood or momentum for a change; 〜の機運が高まる means \"the readiness for … grows, … gains momentum\". Typical are 改革の機運, 政権交代の機運, 〜への機運. Transitively one says 機運を高める \"to foster the mood for something\". It is a word of news and written language; the variant 気運 also occurs.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"途方に暮れて立ち尽くした。","romaji":"Tohō ni kurete tachitsukushita.","german":"I stood there at a loss.","furigana":"とほう|く|た|つ"},{"japanese":"財布をなくして、知らない町で途方に暮れた。","romaji":"Saifu o nakushite, shiranai machi de tohō ni kureta.","german":"I lost my wallet and was at a loss in an unfamiliar town.","furigana":"さいふ|し|まち|とほう|く"},{"japanese":"突然仕事を失い、彼は途方に暮れている。","romaji":"Totsuzen shigoto o ushinai, kare wa tohō ni kurete iru.","german":"He suddenly lost his job and is at his wits' end.","furigana":"とつぜんしごと|うしな|かれ|とほう|く"}],"notes":"途方 means \"way, means\", 暮れる \"to come to an end\" like the day: you no longer know a way and stand completely at a loss. Often with the cause in て-form: 財布をなくして途方に暮れる, or with どうしていいか分からず. 困る is more general, 戸惑う more a brief confusion. Not to be confused with 途方もない \"tremendous, absurd\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"時間を持て余している。","romaji":"Jikan o moteamashite iru.","german":"I have time on my hands.","furigana":"じかん|も|あま"},{"japanese":"元気な子どもたちを持て余して、母親は疲れ切っていた。","romaji":"Genki na kodomotachi o moteamashite, hahaoya wa tsukarekitte ita.","german":"The mother was exhausted, unable to cope with her energetic children.","furigana":"げんき|こ|も|あま|ははおや|つか|き"},{"japanese":"彼女は行き場のない怒りを持て余していた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa ikiba no nai ikari o moteamashite ita.","german":"She didn't know what to do with her pent-up anger.","furigana":"かのじょ|い|ば|いか|も|あま"}],"notes":"持て余す is transitive: you have something you can't cope with or don't know what to do with. Typical are 暇・時間を持て余す \"to have time on one's hands, not to know what to do out of boredom\", 子どもを持て余す \"to be overwhelmed by one's child\" and 感情・怒りを持て余す. Similar is 手に負えない \"unmanageable\", which however takes no object with を.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"本物と見紛うほどの精巧さだ。","romaji":"Honmono to mimagau hodo no seikōsa da.","german":"It is so exquisite that it could be mistaken for the real thing.","furigana":"ほんもの|みまが|せいこう"},{"japanese":"雪と見紛うばかりの桜吹雪だった。","romaji":"Yuki to mimagau bakari no sakura fubuki datta.","german":"It was a cherry blossom flurry that looked just like snow.","furigana":"ゆき|みまが|さくらふぶき"},{"japanese":"あれは見紛うことなき彼の筆跡だ。","romaji":"Are wa mimagau koto naki kare no hisseki da.","german":"That is unmistakably his handwriting.","furigana":"みまが|かれ|ひっせき"}],"notes":"見紛う is literary and means \"to mistake A for B, to confuse\". Typical are 〜と見紛うほど／ばかり \"so much that one could take it for …\" and the fixed expression 見紛うことなき／見紛うはずもない \"unmistakable\". In everyday speech people say 見間違える. Besides みまがう there is the older reading みまごう.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"スキャンダルを揉み消そうとした。","romaji":"Sukyandaru o momikesō to shita.","german":"He tried to cover up the scandal.","furigana":"も|け"},{"japanese":"彼はたばこを灰皿で揉み消した。","romaji":"Kare wa tabako o haizara de momikeshita.","german":"He stubbed out his cigarette in the ashtray.","furigana":"かれ|はいざら|も|け"},{"japanese":"上司のミスを揉み消すよう頼まれたが、断った。","romaji":"Jōshi no misu o momikesu yō tanomareta ga, kotowatta.","german":"I was asked to cover up my boss's mistake, but I refused.","furigana":"じょうし|も|け|たの|ことわ"}],"notes":"揉み消す (often もみ消す) has two meanings: literally to put out a cigarette or embers by rubbing (たばこを揉み消す), figuratively to cover up a scandal, mistake or evidence (事件を揉み消す). The figurative meaning is more common and sounds like abuse of power. Similar is 隠蔽する, more formal and legalistic; putting something out with the foot, by contrast, is 踏み消す.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"平和を切に願う。","romaji":"Heiwa o setsu ni negau.","german":"I earnestly wish for peace.","furigana":"へいわ|せつ|ねが"},{"japanese":"皆様のご協力を切にお願い申し上げます。","romaji":"Minasama no go-kyōryoku o setsu ni onegai mōshiagemasu.","german":"We sincerely ask for everyone's cooperation.","furigana":"みなさま|きょうりょく|せつ|ねが|もう|あ"},{"japanese":"一日も早いご回復を切にお祈りしております。","romaji":"Ichinichi mo hayai go-kaifuku o setsu ni oinori shite orimasu.","german":"We earnestly pray for your recovery as soon as possible.","furigana":"いちにち|はや|かいふく|せつ|いの"}],"notes":"切に is a formal adverb that almost only appears with verbs of wishing and requesting: 切に願う, 切に望む, 切に祈る, 切にお願い申し上げます. It expresses deep, sincere urgency and suits speeches, letters and official appeals. In everyday speech people say 心から or 本当に.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ようやく解決の見通しが立った。","romaji":"Yōyaku kaiketsu no mitōshi ga tatta.","german":"A prospect of resolution has finally emerged.","furigana":"かいけつ|みとお|た"},{"japanese":"電車の運転再開の見通しが立っていない。","romaji":"Densha no unten saikai no mitōshi ga tatte inai.","german":"There is no prospect of when train service will resume.","furigana":"でんしゃ|うんてんさいかい|みとお|た"},{"japanese":"返済の見通しが立たないまま、借金だけが増えていった。","romaji":"Hensai no mitōshi ga tatanai mama, shakkin dake ga fuete itta.","german":"With no prospect of repayment, only the debt kept growing.","furigana":"へんさい|みとお|た|しゃっきん|ふ"}],"notes":"見通し is \"outlook, forecast\"; 〜の見通しが立つ means that it can be foreseen when or how something will succeed. Very common in the negative: 見通しが立たない \"not yet foreseeable\", for example with train cancellations or disasters. Transitively one says 見通しを立てる \"to make a forecast\". A close synonym is めどが立つ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"事態は収拾がつかなくなった。","romaji":"Jitai wa shūshū ga tsukanaku natta.","german":"The situation got out of hand.","furigana":"じたい|しゅうしゅう"},{"japanese":"子どもたちが騒ぎ出して、教室は収拾がつかなくなった。","romaji":"Kodomotachi ga sawagidashite, kyōshitsu wa shūshū ga tsukanaku natta.","german":"The children started making a racket, and the classroom got out of control.","furigana":"こ|さわ|だ|きょうしつ|しゅうしゅう"},{"japanese":"意見が多すぎて、会議の収拾がつかない。","romaji":"Iken ga ōsugite, kaigi no shūshū ga tsukanai.","german":"There are too many opinions, and the meeting can't be brought under control.","furigana":"いけん|おお|かいぎ|しゅうしゅう"}],"notes":"収拾 is \"getting a situation back under control\"; 収拾がつかない means that chaos can no longer be put in order. Very common is 収拾がつかなくなる \"to get out of control\". Transitively one says 事態を収拾する or 収拾をつける. Not to be confused with the identically read 収集 \"collecting\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の謝罪で溜飲を下げた。","romaji":"Kare no shazai de ryūin o sageta.","german":"His apology satisfied me.","furigana":"かれ|しゃざい|りゅういん|さ"},{"japanese":"生意気なライバルが負けて、ファンは溜飲を下げた。","romaji":"Namaiki na raibaru ga makete, fan wa ryūin o sageta.","german":"The fans felt vindicated when the cocky rival lost.","furigana":"なまいき|ま|りゅういん|さ"},{"japanese":"言いたいことを全部言って、少し溜飲を下げた。","romaji":"Iitai koto o zenbu itte, sukoshi ryūin o sageta.","german":"I said everything I wanted to say and felt a bit better.","furigana":"い|ぜんぶい|すこ|りゅういん|さ"}],"notes":"溜飲 is actually sour reflux; when it \"goes down\", the pressure is gone. 溜飲を下げる therefore means that pent-up anger or frustration dissipates and you feel satisfaction, often because someone gets their just punishment or you are proven right. It is not actively venting steam (that would be 鬱憤を晴らす). Intransitive also 溜飲が下がる; the mixed form 溜飲を晴らす is considered wrong.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しいトレンドが世界を席巻している。","romaji":"Atarashii torendo ga sekai o sekken shite iru.","german":"A new trend is sweeping the world.","furigana":"あたら|せかい|せっけん"},{"japanese":"その日本車は一時、アメリカ市場を席巻した。","romaji":"Sono Nihon-sha wa ichiji, Amerika shijō o sekken shita.","german":"That Japanese car once swept the American market.","furigana":"にほんしゃ|いちじ|しじょう|せっけん"},{"japanese":"騎馬軍団がたちまちヨーロッパを席巻した。","romaji":"Kiba gundan ga tachimachi Yōroppa o sekken shita.","german":"The mounted army swept over Europe in no time.","furigana":"きばぐんだん|せっけん"}],"notes":"席巻する originally means \"to roll up a mat\" and means to conquer a territory or market by storm: 世界を席巻する, 市場を席巻する. It is transitive with を and literary; the original spelling is 席捲. More neutral are 支配する or, for fashions, 流行する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"概して成功と言える。","romaji":"Gaishite seikō to ieru.","german":"On the whole, it can be called a success.","furigana":"がい|せいこう|い"},{"japanese":"今年の新入社員は概してまじめだ。","romaji":"Kotoshi no shinnyū shain wa gaishite majime da.","german":"This year's new employees are generally serious.","furigana":"ことし|しんにゅうしゃいん|がい"},{"japanese":"この地方の冬は概して温暖である。","romaji":"Kono chihō no fuyu wa gaishite ondan de aru.","german":"Winters in this region are generally mild.","furigana":"ちほう|ふゆ|がい|おんだん"}],"notes":"概して is a literary adverb for \"by and large, as a rule\" and introduces a generalization: 概して言えば, 概して〜傾向がある. In conversation people rather say 全体的に, 一般的に or だいたい. It sounds matter-of-fact and judging, and suits reports, evaluations and essays.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地域に根ざした活動を行う。","romaji":"Chiiki ni nezashita katsudō o okonau.","german":"We carry out activities rooted in the community.","furigana":"ちいき|ね|かつどう|おこな"},{"japanese":"この祭りは農耕文化に根ざしている。","romaji":"Kono matsuri wa nōkō bunka ni nezashite iru.","german":"This festival is rooted in agricultural culture.","furigana":"まつ|のうこうぶんか|ね"},{"japanese":"彼の偏見は子どもの頃の体験に根ざしたものだ。","romaji":"Kare no henken wa kodomo no koro no taiken ni nezashita mono da.","german":"His prejudice is rooted in experiences from his childhood.","furigana":"かれ|へんけん|こ|ころ|たいけん|ね"}],"notes":"根ざす (also 根差す) is intransitive and means \"to be rooted in something\": 〜に根ざす. Very common attributively: 地域に根ざした活動 \"regionally anchored activities\", 生活に根ざした文化. 基づく stresses more a factual basis (データに基づく), 根付く the taking root of something new.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"闇の中で何かが蠢いている。","romaji":"Yami no naka de nanika ga ugomeite iru.","german":"Something is stirring in the darkness.","furigana":"やみ|なか|なに|うごめ"},{"japanese":"石をどけると、たくさんの虫が蠢いていた。","romaji":"Ishi o dokeru to, takusan no mushi ga ugomeite ita.","german":"When I moved the stone, many insects were squirming about.","furigana":"いし|むし|うごめ"},{"japanese":"政界の裏では、さまざまな思惑が蠢いている。","romaji":"Seikai no ura de wa, samazama na omowaku ga ugomeite iru.","german":"Behind the scenes of politics, various schemes are stirring.","furigana":"せいかい|うら|おもわく|うごめ"}],"notes":"蠢く describes a slow, crawling writhing of small creatures such as worms, insects or larvae, usually with an eerie or disgusting undertone. Figuratively one speaks of people or forces acting in secret: 裏で蠢く勢力 \"forces at work in the background\". Intransitive, mostly in the form 蠢いている; the difficult kanji is often replaced by kana うごめく. Compared with 動く \"to move\" and うようよする \"to swarm\", 蠢く stresses the sluggish, uncanny stirring; rather literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"心配は杞憂に終わった。","romaji":"Shinpai wa kiyū ni owatta.","german":"The worry turned out to be unfounded.","furigana":"しんぱい|きゆう|お"},{"japanese":"雨が心配だったが、杞憂に終わり、当日は快晴だった。","romaji":"Ame ga shinpai datta ga, kiyū ni owari, tōjitsu wa kaisei datta.","german":"I was worried about rain, but it was needless, and the day was brilliantly clear.","furigana":"あめ|しんぱい|きゆう|お|とうじつ|かいせい"},{"japanese":"私の不安が杞憂に終わればいいのだが。","romaji":"Watashi no fuan ga kiyū ni owareba ii no da ga.","german":"I hope my anxiety turns out to be unfounded.","furigana":"わたし|ふあん|きゆう|お"}],"notes":"杞憂 goes back to a man from the ancient state of 杞 who feared the sky might fall: a completely unnecessary worry. 杞憂に終わる means that a fear turns out in hindsight to be unfounded, that is, a relieved, positive outcome. Typical: 心配は杞憂に終わった; as a hope 杞憂に終わればいいが. Elevated style.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"努力した甲斐があった。","romaji":"Doryoku shita kai ga atta.","german":"My effort was worth it.","furigana":"どりょく|かい"},{"japanese":"待った甲斐があって、素晴らしい日の出が見られた。","romaji":"Matta kai ga atte, subarashii hinode ga mirareta.","german":"Waiting paid off, and we got to see a wonderful sunrise.","furigana":"ま|かい|すば|ひ|で|み"},{"japanese":"何度言っても聞かないなら、注意する甲斐がない。","romaji":"Nando itte mo kikanai nara, chūi suru kai ga nai.","german":"If he doesn't listen no matter how often I say it, there's no point in warning him.","furigana":"なんどい|き|ちゅうい|かい"}],"notes":"甲斐 is the \"reward, effect\" of an effort; typical is Vた甲斐がある \"it was worth doing …\", negated 甲斐がない \"it's no use\". Often written in kana as かいがある. As a suffix it becomes -がい: 生きがい \"purpose in life\", やりがい \"fulfillment in work\". More elevated and emphatic is 〜甲斐あって without が.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"心の拠り所を見つける。","romaji":"Kokoro no yoridokoro o mitsukeru.","german":"I find an emotional anchor.","furigana":"こころ|よ|どころ|み"},{"japanese":"この判断は何を拠り所にしているのですか。","romaji":"Kono handan wa nani o yoridokoro ni shite iru no desu ka.","german":"What is this judgment based on?","furigana":"はんだん|なに|よ|どころ"},{"japanese":"故郷を離れた移民にとって、教会は大切な拠り所だった。","romaji":"Kokyō o hanareta imin ni totte, kyōkai wa taisetsu na yoridokoro datta.","german":"For immigrants who had left their homeland, the church was an important mainstay.","furigana":"こきょう|はな|いみん|きょうかい|たいせつ|よ|どころ"}],"notes":"拠り所 (also よりどころ) is that on which one relies: emotionally as 心の拠り所 \"support, comfort\", factually as the basis of a judgment or theory (判断の拠り所). Often in 〜を拠り所とする／にする. 根拠 refers more to evidence and proof, 支え to concrete support from people.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その批判はお門違いだ。","romaji":"Sono hihan wa okadochigai da.","german":"That criticism is misdirected.","furigana":"ひはん|かどちが"},{"japanese":"私に文句を言うのはお門違いですよ。","romaji":"Watashi ni monku o iu no wa okadochigai desu yo.","german":"It's misplaced to complain to me.","furigana":"わたし|もんく|い|かどちが"},{"japanese":"彼にお金の相談をするなんて、お門違いもいいところだ。","romaji":"Kare ni okane no sōdan o suru nante, okadochigai mo ii tokoro da.","german":"Asking him for financial advice is totally barking up the wrong tree.","furigana":"かれ|かね|そうだん|かどちが"}],"notes":"門 here is the gate of a house: お門違い literally means \"ringing the wrong house's bell\". It is used when criticism, resentment or a request is directed at the wrong person: 〜に文句を言うのはお門違いだ, お門違いの批判. Similar are 見当違い \"off the mark\" and 筋違い \"out of place, not responsible\". Mostly predicative with だ／です or attributive with の.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"批判を逆手に取って反撃した。","romaji":"Hihan o sakate ni totte hangeki shita.","german":"Man nutzte die Kritik zu seinem Vorteil und schlug zurück.","furigana":"ひはん|さかて|と|はんげき"},{"japanese":"雨の多い気候を逆手に取って、傘の専門店を開いた。","romaji":"Ame no ōi kikō o sakate ni totte, kasa no senmonten o hiraita.","german":"Er machte aus dem regenreichen Klima ein Geschäft und eröffnete einen Schirmladen.","furigana":"あめ|おお|きこう|さかて|と|かさ|せんもんてん|ひら"},{"japanese":"背が低いことを逆手に取って、彼は素早い動きで相手を翻弄した。","romaji":"Se ga hikui koto o sakate ni totte, kare wa subayai ugoki de aite o honrō shita.","german":"Er machte seine geringe Größe zum Vorteil und brachte den Gegner mit schnellen Bewegungen durcheinander.","furigana":"せ|ひく|さかて|と|かれ|すばや|うご|あいて|ほんろう"}],"notes":"逆手 ist ein umgekehrter Griff; 逆手に取る heißt, die Waffe, das Argument oder einen Nachteil des Gegners umzudrehen und zum eigenen Vorteil zu nutzen. Typisch: 批判を逆手に取る, 弱点を逆手に取る, 〜ことを逆手に取って. Neben さかて ist auch die Lesung ぎゃくて verbreitet. Im Deutschen entspricht es oft „den Spieß umdrehen“.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"完璧に見えた計画が綻び始めた。","romaji":"Kanpeki ni mieta keikaku ga hokorobi hajimeta.","german":"The plan that had looked perfect began to unravel.","furigana":"かんぺき|み|けいかく|ほころ|はじ"},{"japanese":"シャツの袖口がほころびてしまった。","romaji":"Shatsu no sodeguchi ga hokorobite shimatta.","german":"The cuff of the shirt has come unstitched.","furigana":"そでぐち"},{"japanese":"桜のつぼみがほころび始めた。","romaji":"Sakura no tsubomi ga hokorobi hajimeta.","german":"The cherry buds have begun to open.","furigana":"さくら|はじ"}],"notes":"綻びる (mostly in kana ほころびる) is intransitive and has three images: a seam comes apart (袖口がほころびる), buds open (つぼみがほころびる), and figuratively a plan or system develops cracks (計画が綻びる). The face \"opening\" into a smile is usually 顔がほころぶ, with the equivalent godan form 綻ぶ. The noun 綻び means \"a seam that has come apart, a weak point\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"やっとプロジェクト完了の目処が立った。","romaji":"Yatto purojekuto kanryō no medo ga tatta.","german":"At last there is a prospect of finishing the project.","furigana":"かんりょう|めど|た"},{"japanese":"大雨の影響で、電車の運転再開の目処が立っていない。","romaji":"Ōame no eikyō de, densha no unten saikai no medo ga tatte inai.","german":"Because of the heavy rain, there is no prospect yet of when trains will resume.","furigana":"おおあめ|えいきょう|でんしゃ|うんてんさいかい|めど|た"},{"japanese":"ようやく借金を返す目処が立って、ほっとした。","romaji":"Yōyaku shakkin o kaesu medo ga tatte, hotto shita.","german":"I was relieved that I could finally see a way to pay back my debt.","furigana":"しゃっきん|かえ|めど|た"}],"notes":"めど means \"goal, prospect\"; 目処 is an ateji spelling, and it is often written in hiragana (also 目途). 目処が立つ means that an end or a solution becomes foreseeable: 完成の目処が立つ, 復旧の目処が立たない (very common in the news). The transitive counterpart is 目処をつける \"to create a prospect, to roughly get something under way\". Neutral, very common in business and the media.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"無闇に批判すべきではない。","romaji":"Muyami ni hihan subeki dewa nai.","german":"One should not criticize indiscriminately.","furigana":"むやみ|ひはん"},{"japanese":"知らない人にむやみに個人情報を教えないでください。","romaji":"Shiranai hito ni muyami ni kojin jōhō o oshienaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't give out personal information to strangers carelessly.","furigana":"し|ひと|こじんじょうほう|おし"},{"japanese":"薬をむやみに飲むのは体によくない。","romaji":"Kusuri o muyami ni nomu no wa karada ni yoku nai.","german":"Taking medicine indiscriminately is bad for your body.","furigana":"くすり|の|からだ"}],"notes":"無闇に is mostly written in kana as むやみに and means \"thoughtlessly, indiscriminately, without good reason\", often in warnings: むやみに〜しない, むやみに触らないでください. It also means \"excessively\". Intensified: むやみやたらに. Similar is やたらに, which is more colloquial and stresses the excess more.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"成功の一端を担った。","romaji":"Seikō no ittan o ninatta.","german":"I played a part in the success.","furigana":"せいこう|いったん|にな"},{"japanese":"私たちの会社も、町の観光振興の一端を担っている。","romaji":"Watashitachi no kaisha mo, machi no kankō shinkō no ittan o ninatte iru.","german":"Our company, too, plays a part in promoting tourism in the town.","furigana":"わたし|かいしゃ|まち|かんこうしんこう|いったん|にな"},{"japanese":"若い世代にも、伝統文化の継承の一端を担ってほしい。","romaji":"Wakai sedai ni mo, dentō bunka no keishō no ittan o ninatte hoshii.","german":"I want the younger generation, too, to take on a part of passing down traditional culture.","furigana":"わか|せだい|でんとうぶんか|けいしょう|いったん|にな"}],"notes":"一端 (いったん) is \"one part, one end\" and not to be confused with the identically read 一旦 \"once, for the time being\". 〜の一端を担う means to share part of the responsibility or work for something bigger: 成功の一端を担う, 地域医療の一端を担う. It sounds modest and formal and is typical of speeches, company profiles and applications. More general is 役割を果たす.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日は私が奢るよ。","romaji":"Kyō wa watashi ga ogoru yo.","german":"It's on me today.","furigana":"きょう|わたし|おご"},{"japanese":"先輩に昼ご飯を奢ってもらった。","romaji":"Senpai ni hirugohan o ogotte moratta.","german":"My senior treated me to lunch.","furigana":"せんぱい|ひる|はん|おご"},{"japanese":"高級料理ばかり食べて、すっかり口が奢ってしまった。","romaji":"Kōkyū ryōri bakari tabete, sukkari kuchi ga ogotte shimatta.","german":"I've eaten nothing but fine cuisine and have become thoroughly spoiled.","furigana":"こうきゅうりょうり|た|くち|おご"}],"notes":"奢る (mostly in kana おごる) today mainly means \"to treat someone, to pick up the bill\": 今日は私がおごる, 先輩におごってもらう. It also means \"to live in luxury, to be pampered\", for example 口が奢る \"to have a pampered palate\". \"To be arrogant\", by contrast, is written 驕る (驕る平家は久しからず). The counterpart when being treated is ごちそうになる or ごちそうさま.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一つのミスが全ての努力を帳消しにした。","romaji":"Hitotsu no misu ga subete no doryoku o chōkeshi ni shita.","german":"One mistake wiped out all the effort.","furigana":"ひと|すべ|どりょく|ちょうけ"},{"japanese":"友人が借金を帳消しにしてくれた。","romaji":"Yūjin ga shakkin o chōkeshi ni shite kureta.","german":"My friend wrote off my debt.","furigana":"ゆうじん|しゃっきん|ちょうけ"},{"japanese":"後半のゴールで前半のミスを帳消しにした。","romaji":"Kōhan no gōru de zenhan no misu o chōkeshi ni shita.","german":"The goal in the second half made up for the mistake in the first half.","furigana":"こうはん|ぜんはん|ちょうけ"}],"notes":"帳消し is originally the striking of a debt from the account book. 〜を帳消しにする therefore means \"to cancel a debt\" and figuratively \"to nullify, to offset\": a mistake can undo all the effort, but a success can also make up for an earlier mistake. Intransitively one says 帳消しになる. Neutral and common in sports and business reports.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"精鋭部隊が派遣された。","romaji":"Seiei butai ga haken sareta.","german":"Eine Elitetruppe wurde entsandt.","furigana":"せいえいぶたい|はけん"},{"japanese":"少数精鋭のチームでプロジェクトを進める。","romaji":"Shōsū seiei no chīmu de purojekuto o susumeru.","german":"Wir treiben das Projekt mit einem kleinen Eliteteam voran.","furigana":"しょうすうせいえい|すす"},{"japanese":"各国から選ばれた精鋭が大会に集まった。","romaji":"Kakkoku kara erabareta seiei ga taikai ni atsumatta.","german":"Die Besten aus allen Ländern versammelten sich zum Turnier.","furigana":"かっこく|えら|せいえい|たいかい|あつ"}],"notes":"精鋭 bedeutet „die Besten, Elite“ und wird fast immer als Nomen gebraucht: 精鋭部隊, 選りすぐりの精鋭, 少数精鋭 „kleines, aber schlagkräftiges Team“. Die Form 精鋭な steht zwar im Wörterbuch, ist aber ungebräuchlich; natürlich sind 精鋭の〜 oder Komposita. Es hat keinen abwertenden Beiklang wie das deutsche „elitär“.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"忸怩たる思いを抱えている。","romaji":"Jikuji taru omoi o kakaete iru.","german":"I am filled with deep shame.","furigana":"じくじ|おも|かか"},{"japanese":"大臣は国民の信頼を裏切ったことについて忸怩たる思いを語った。","romaji":"Daijin wa kokumin no shinrai o uragitta koto ni tsuite jikuji taru omoi o katatta.","german":"The minister spoke of his deep regret at having betrayed the public's trust.","furigana":"だいじん|こくみん|しんらい|うらぎ|じくじ|おも|かた"},{"japanese":"準備不足で試合に負け、監督として忸怩たる思いだ。","romaji":"Junbi busoku de shiai ni make, kantoku to shite jikuji taru omoi da.","german":"We lost the match through poor preparation, and as manager I feel deeply ashamed.","furigana":"じゅんびぶそく|しあい|ま|かんとく|じくじ|おも"}],"notes":"忸怩たる is an elevated たる-form and appears almost only in 忸怩たる思い（がある／だ）: deep shame over one's own failure. Typical of politicians, managers and coaches who admit responsibility. It is often wrongly used in the sense of \"annoying, frustrating\" (悔しい); correct is shame about oneself. More everyday are 恥ずかしい or 情けない.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"環境破壊に歯止めをかける。","romaji":"Kankyō hakai ni hadome o kakeru.","german":"We will curb environmental destruction.","furigana":"かんきょうはかい|はど"},{"japanese":"政府は円安に歯止めをかけるため、市場に介入した。","romaji":"Seifu wa en'yasu ni hadome o kakeru tame, shijō ni kainyū shita.","german":"The government intervened in the market to halt the weakening yen.","furigana":"せいふ|えんやす|はど|しじょう|かいにゅう"},{"japanese":"甘い物を食べすぎないよう、自分で歯止めをかけている。","romaji":"Amai mono o tabesuginai yō, jibun de hadome o kakete iru.","german":"I put a brake on myself so as not to eat too many sweets.","furigana":"あま|もの|た|じぶん|はど"}],"notes":"歯止め is a brake block; 〜に歯止めをかける means to halt a negative development: 少子化・物価高・円安に歯止めをかける. Intransitively one says 歯止めがかかる, negated 歯止めがかからない／きかない \"cannot be stopped\". Typical of politics and business, but also of self-discipline.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"見事な成績で面目を施した。","romaji":"Migoto na seiseki de menmoku o hodokoshita.","german":"I did myself credit with excellent results.","furigana":"みごと|せいせき|めんもく|ほどこ"},{"japanese":"初めての大舞台で好演し、彼は大いに面目を施した。","romaji":"Hajimete no ōbutai de kōen shi, kare wa ōi ni menmoku o hodokoshita.","german":"He performed well on a big stage for the first time and greatly enhanced his reputation.","furigana":"はじ|おおぶたい|こうえん|かれ|おお|めんもく|ほどこ"},{"japanese":"交渉をまとめ、担当者として面目を施すことができた。","romaji":"Kōshō o matome, tantōsha to shite menmoku o hodokosu koto ga dekita.","german":"I closed the negotiations and was able to do myself honor as the person in charge.","furigana":"こうしょう|たんとうしゃ|めんもく|ほどこ"}],"notes":"面目 is \"honor, reputation, face\"; 面目を施す means to gain honor and raise your reputation through an achievement. To be distinguished from this are 面目を保つ \"to save face\", 面目を失う／面目丸つぶれ \"to lose face\" and 面目ない \"to be ashamed\". For this expression the reading めんぼく is more traditional, but めんもく is also widespread. Elevated style.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不正に対して憤慨した。","romaji":"Fusei ni taishite fungai shita.","german":"I was indignant at the injustice.","furigana":"ふせい|たい|ふんがい"},{"japanese":"住民たちは市の一方的な決定に憤慨している。","romaji":"Jūmintachi wa shi no ippōteki na kettei ni fungai shite iru.","german":"The residents are outraged at the city's unilateral decision.","furigana":"じゅうみん|し|いっぽうてき|けってい|ふんがい"},{"japanese":"失礼な客の態度に、店員は内心憤慨していた。","romaji":"Shitsurei na kyaku no taido ni, ten'in wa naishin fungai shite ita.","german":"The clerk was inwardly furious at the rude customer's attitude.","furigana":"しつれい|きゃく|たいど|てんいん|ないしんふんがい"}],"notes":"憤慨する denotes indignant outrage, usually at injustice or insolent behavior: 〜に憤慨する, 〜に対して憤慨する. It is more formal than 怒る and stresses moral indignation; 激怒する is violent rage, 腹を立てる everyday annoyance. Common in reports on the reactions of citizens or those affected.","levelNote":"Derived from 憤慨 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"社長の逆鱗に触れてしまった。","romaji":"Shachō no gekirin ni furete shimatta.","german":"I have incurred the president's wrath.","furigana":"しゃちょう|げきりん|ふ"},{"japanese":"不用意な発言が監督の逆鱗に触れ、彼は試合から外された。","romaji":"Fuyōi na hatsugen ga kantoku no gekirin ni fure, kare wa shiai kara hazusareta.","german":"A careless remark provoked the manager's fury, and he was dropped from the game.","furigana":"ふようい|はつげん|かんとく|げきりん|ふ|かれ|しあい|はず"},{"japanese":"父の逆鱗に触れないよう、門限は必ず守っている。","romaji":"Chichi no gekirin ni furenai yō, mongen wa kanarazu mamotte iru.","german":"I always keep to my curfew so as not to provoke my father's wrath.","furigana":"ちち|げきりん|ふ|もんげん|かなら|まも"}],"notes":"逆鱗 is the scale growing in the wrong direction on a dragon's throat; whoever touches it is killed. 〜の逆鱗に触れる thus means to throw a superior or powerful person into violent rage: 社長の逆鱗に触れる, 父の逆鱗に触れる. It is not used for one's own anger and, strictly speaking, not toward equals or subordinates. Elevated expression.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仲間と切磋琢磨して成長する。","romaji":"Nakama to sessa takuma shite seichō suru.","german":"I grow by pushing each other to improve with my peers.","furigana":"なかま|せっさたくま|せいちょう"},{"japanese":"二人はライバルとして互いに切磋琢磨してきた。","romaji":"Futari wa raibaru to shite tagai ni sessa takuma shite kita.","german":"The two have spurred each other on as rivals.","furigana":"ふたり|たが|せっさたくま"},{"japanese":"研究室では、学生同士が切磋琢磨できる環境を大切にしている。","romaji":"Kenkyūshitsu de wa, gakusei dōshi ga sessa takuma dekiru kankyō o taisetsu ni shite iru.","german":"In our laboratory we value an environment in which students can improve each other.","furigana":"けんきゅうしつ|がくせいどうし|せっさたくま|かんきょう|たいせつ"}],"notes":"切磋琢磨 comes from the image of cutting and polishing bone, ivory and jade: you grind each other into shape. 切磋琢磨する means to spur each other on and improve in friendly rivalry, typically 〜と切磋琢磨する, 互いに切磋琢磨する. It is positive and popular in school, sports and corporate mission statements; pure competition would be 競い合う.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"恋をすると痘痕も靨だ。","romaji":"Koi o suru to abata mo ekubo da.","german":"When you are in love, even pockmarks look like dimples.","furigana":"こい|あばた|えくぼ"},{"japanese":"彼女の短気なところさえかわいいなんて、まさにあばたもえくぼだね。","romaji":"Kanojo no tanki na tokoro sae kawaii nante, masa ni abata mo ekubo da ne.","german":"Finding even her short temper cute is truly a case of love is blind.","furigana":"かのじょ|たんき"},{"japanese":"結婚して三年、あばたもえくぼの時期はとっくに過ぎた。","romaji":"Kekkon shite sannen, abata mo ekubo no jiki wa tokku ni sugita.","german":"After three years of marriage, the time when love was blind is long gone.","furigana":"けっこん|さんねん|じき|す"}],"notes":"痘痕 are pockmarks, 靨 dimples: to those in love even pockmarks look like dimples, hence \"love is blind\". The proverb is almost always written in kana as あばたもえくぼ and used with だ or の (あばたもえくぼの時期). It is colloquial and jocular and mostly comments on other people's infatuation.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"状況は漸次改善されている。","romaji":"Jōkyō wa zenji kaizen sarete iru.","german":"The situation is gradually improving.","furigana":"じょうきょう|ぜんじかいぜん"},{"japanese":"新制度は来年度から漸次導入される予定だ。","romaji":"Shinseido wa rainendo kara zenji dōnyū sareru yotei da.","german":"The new system is scheduled to be introduced step by step from next fiscal year.","furigana":"しんせいど|らいねんど|ぜんじどうにゅう|よてい"},{"japanese":"気温は午後にかけて漸次上昇する見込みです。","romaji":"Kion wa gogo ni kakete zenji jōshō suru mikomi desu.","german":"Temperatures are expected to rise gradually toward the afternoon.","furigana":"きおん|ごご|ぜんじじょうしょう|みこ"}],"notes":"漸次 is a literary adverb for \"step by step, little by little\", typical of official language, weather forecasts and technical texts: 漸次導入する, 漸次改善される. In everyday speech people say 徐々に, 次第に or だんだん. Not to be confused with 暫時 \"for a short while\". As an adjective: 漸次的な変化.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は経営者として辣腕を振るった。","romaji":"Kare wa keieisha to shite ratsuwan o furutta.","german":"He exercised his formidable abilities as an executive.","furigana":"かれ|けいえいしゃ|らつわん|ふ"},{"japanese":"検事として辣腕を振るった彼は、のちに政治家になった。","romaji":"Kenji to shite ratsuwan o furutta kare wa, nochi ni seijika ni natta.","german":"He, who had wielded great skill as a prosecutor, later became a politician.","furigana":"けんじ|らつわん|ふ|かれ|せいじか"},{"japanese":"大臣は辣腕を振るって、無駄な予算を大胆に削減した。","romaji":"Daijin wa ratsuwan o furutte, muda na yosan o daitan ni sakugen shita.","german":"The minister took decisive action and boldly cut wasteful budgets.","furigana":"だいじん|らつわん|ふ|むだ|よさん|だいたん|さくげん"}],"notes":"辣腕 is \"sharp, assertive ability\", 振るう \"to wield, to use\": 辣腕を振るう means to take energetic and sometimes ruthless action with great skill. Typical of managers, politicians, lawyers or prosecutors; also 辣腕の弁護士, 辣腕家. More neutral is 手腕を発揮する; 辣腕 sounds of toughness and sharpness.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"観客は固唾を飲んで見守った。","romaji":"Kankyaku wa katazu o nonde mimamotta.","german":"The audience watched with bated breath.","furigana":"かんきゃく|かたず|の|みまも"},{"japanese":"家族は固唾を飲んで合格発表を待った。","romaji":"Kazoku wa katazu o nonde gōkaku happyō o matta.","german":"The family waited with bated breath for the announcement of the exam results.","furigana":"かぞく|かたず|の|ごうかくはっぴょう|ま"},{"japanese":"会場は固唾を飲むような静けさに包まれた。","romaji":"Kaijō wa katazu o nomu yō na shizukesa ni tsutsumareta.","german":"The venue was wrapped in a hushed silence.","furigana":"かいじょう|かたず|の|しず|つつ"}],"notes":"固唾 (かたず, not かたつば) is the saliva that gathers in the mouth from tension; 固唾を飲む means to swallow it, that is, to wait tensely with bated breath. Almost always 固唾を飲んで見守る／待つ; also written 固唾をのむ. It is about tense expectation, not fright (that would be 息をのむ).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"将来を嘱望されている若者だ。","romaji":"Shōrai o shokubō sarete iru wakamono da.","german":"He is a young man with a promising future.","furigana":"しょうらい|しょくぼう|わかもの"},{"japanese":"会社は彼女を次期部長として嘱望している。","romaji":"Kaisha wa kanojo o jiki buchō to shite shokubō shite iru.","german":"The company has high hopes for her as the next department head.","furigana":"かいしゃ|かのじょ|じきぶちょう|しょくぼう"},{"japanese":"将来を嘱望されながら、彼はけがで引退を余儀なくされた。","romaji":"Shōrai o shokubō sarenagara, kare wa kega de intai o yoginaku sareta.","german":"Although he had been tipped for a bright future, he was forced to retire due to injury.","furigana":"しょうらい|しょくぼう|かれ|いんたい|よぎ"}],"notes":"嘱望する means to place great hopes in a person's future. Almost always in the passive: 将来を嘱望される, 前途を嘱望された若手 \"promising\". It is formal and typical of reports on talents in sports, art and science. More general and everyday is 期待する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"メディアが大々的に喧伝した。","romaji":"Media ga daidaiteki ni kenden shita.","german":"The media publicized it on a large scale.","furigana":"だいだいてき|けんでん"},{"japanese":"その薬は奇跡の新薬だと喧伝されたが、効果は限定的だった。","romaji":"Sono kusuri wa kiseki no shin'yaku da to kenden sareta ga, kōka wa genteiteki datta.","german":"The drug was touted as a miracle new medicine, but its effect was limited.","furigana":"くすり|きせき|しんやく|けんでん|こうか|げんていてき"},{"japanese":"政府は経済政策の成果を盛んに喧伝している。","romaji":"Seifu wa keizai seisaku no seika o sakan ni kenden shite iru.","german":"The government is eagerly trumpeting the results of its economic policy.","furigana":"せいふ|けいざいせいさく|せいか|さか|けんでん"}],"notes":"喧伝する means to make something known loudly and everywhere; often with a critical undertone that there is more noise than substance behind it. Typical: 大々的に喧伝する, 〜だと喧伝される \"to be touted as …\". 宣伝する is neutral advertising, 吹聴する stresses boastful spreading of the word. Literary, common in commentaries and criticism.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"性能が格段に向上した。","romaji":"Seinō ga kakudan ni kōjō shita.","german":"Performance has improved dramatically.","furigana":"せいのう|かくだん|こうじょう"},{"japanese":"新しいスマホは前の機種より格段に使いやすい。","romaji":"Atarashii sumaho wa mae no kishu yori kakudan ni tsukaiyasui.","german":"The new smartphone is far easier to use than the previous model.","furigana":"あたら|まえ|きしゅ|かくだん|つか"},{"japanese":"留学してから、彼の日本語は格段に上達した。","romaji":"Ryūgaku shite kara, kare no Nihongo wa kakudan ni jōtatsu shita.","german":"Since studying abroad, his Japanese has improved remarkably.","furigana":"りゅうがく|かれ|にほんご|かくだん|じょうたつ"}],"notes":"格段に stresses a clear qualitative leap in a comparison, usually for the better: 格段に向上する, 格段に使いやすい, 格段に上達する. As a noun: 格段の差, 格段の進歩. 大幅に fits numbers and quantities better, はるかに is more general; in everyday speech people say ずっと.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二つの立場が截然と分かれている。","romaji":"Futatsu no tachiba ga setsuzen to wakarete iru.","german":"The two positions are sharply divided.","furigana":"ふた|たちば|せつぜん|わ"},{"japanese":"公と私を截然と区別する。","romaji":"Kō to shi o setsuzen to kubetsu suru.","german":"I draw a clear distinction between public and private.","furigana":"こう|し|せつぜん|くべつ"},{"japanese":"川を境に、町の景色は截然と異なる。","romaji":"Kawa o sakai ni, machi no keshiki wa setsuzen to kotonaru.","german":"With the river as the boundary, the scenery of the town differs sharply.","furigana":"かわ|さかい|まち|けしき|せつぜん|こと"}],"notes":"截然と is a very elevated adverb for a sharp, unambiguous separation: 截然と分かれる, 截然と区別する, 截然と異なる. 截 means \"to cut off\", so the image is a clean cut. The reading さいぜん is also rare. In everyday speech people say はっきり or 明確に.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"参加者は一様に驚いた。","romaji":"Sankasha wa ichiyō ni odoroita.","german":"The participants were all equally surprised.","furigana":"さんかしゃ|いちよう|おどろ"},{"japanese":"全国一様に同じ制度を適用するのは難しい。","romaji":"Zenkoku ichiyō ni onaji seido o tekiyō suru no wa muzukashii.","german":"It is difficult to apply the same system uniformly nationwide.","furigana":"ぜんこくいちよう|おな|せいど|てきよう|むずか"},{"japanese":"被害の程度は地域によって一様ではない。","romaji":"Higai no teido wa chiiki ni yotte ichiyō de wa nai.","german":"The extent of the damage is not uniform and varies by region.","furigana":"ひがい|ていど|ちいき|いちよう"}],"notes":"一様に means \"all equally, without difference\" and mostly describes that many people react the same way or that something is uniform: 一様に驚く, 一様に〜を適用する. The negation 一様ではない \"is different, not uniform\" is very common. 一律に stresses a uniform rule (一律千円), 同様に \"likewise\" a comparison.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"称賛を惜しみなく送った。","romaji":"Shōsan o oshiminaku okutta.","german":"He lavished praise.","furigana":"しょうさん|お|おく"},{"japanese":"祖父は孫たちのために惜しみなくお金を使う。","romaji":"Sofu wa magotachi no tame ni oshiminaku okane o tsukau.","german":"The grandfather spends money unstintingly on his grandchildren.","furigana":"そふ|まご|お|かね|つか"},{"japanese":"先輩は自分の知識を惜しみなく後輩に教えてくれた。","romaji":"Senpai wa jibun no chishiki o oshiminaku kōhai ni oshiete kureta.","german":"My senior generously shared his knowledge with the juniors.","furigana":"せんぱい|じぶん|ちしき|お|こうはい|おし"}],"notes":"惜しみなく comes from 惜しむ \"to be stingy, to regret\" + なく: \"without stinting\". It is used with giving, praising and supporting: 惜しみなく与える, 惜しみなく協力する, 称賛を惜しみなく送る. Attributively it is 惜しみない: 惜しみない拍手. Positive and slightly elevated.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"見るに忍びない光景だった。","romaji":"Miru ni shinobinai kōkei datta.","german":"It was a sight too painful to watch.","furigana":"み|しの|こうけい"},{"japanese":"長年使ってきた机を捨てるのは忍びない。","romaji":"Naganen tsukatte kita tsukue o suteru no wa shinobinai.","german":"I can't bear to throw away the desk I've used for years.","furigana":"ながねんつか|つくえ|す|しの"},{"japanese":"断るのは忍びないのですが、今回はお引き受けできません。","romaji":"Kotowaru no wa shinobinai no desu ga, konkai wa ohikiuke dekimasen.","german":"I hate to refuse, but I can't take it on this time.","furigana":"ことわ|しの|こんかい|ひ|う"}],"notes":"忍びない (from 忍ぶ \"to endure\") expresses that you \"cannot bring yourself\" to do something out of compassion or regret. Typical patterns are dictionary-form verb + に忍びない (見るに忍びない \"cannot bear to watch\", 聞くに忍びない) and 〜のは忍びない (捨てるのは忍びない). It sounds elevated; in everyday speech people say 見ていられない or 〜のはかわいそう. 耐えられない, by contrast, means general inability to endure, e.g. pain or heat.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自然に対して畏敬の念を抱く。","romaji":"Shizen ni taishite ikei no nen o idaku.","german":"I feel awe toward nature.","furigana":"しぜん|たい|いけい|ねん|いだ"},{"japanese":"初めて富士山を間近で見て、畏敬の念を抱かずにはいられなかった。","romaji":"Hajimete Fujisan o majika de mite, ikei no nen o idakazu ni wa irarenakatta.","german":"Seeing Mt. Fuji up close for the first time, I couldn't help feeling awe.","furigana":"はじ|ふじさん|まじか|み|いけい|ねん|いだ"},{"japanese":"弟子たちは皆、厳しい師匠に畏敬の念を抱いていた。","romaji":"Deshi-tachi wa mina, kibishii shishō ni ikei no nen o idaite ita.","german":"All the disciples held their strict master in awe.","furigana":"でし|みな|きび|ししょう|いけい|ねん|いだ"}],"notes":"畏敬 combines 畏 \"reverent fear\" and 敬 \"respect\"; 畏敬の念 is the \"feeling of awe\", and 抱く (read いだく here) means \"to harbor\". The object takes に or に対して: 自然に畏敬の念を抱く. Instead of 抱く one also says 畏敬の念を覚える or 持つ. Unlike 尊敬 (\"respect for a person\"), 畏敬 contains a reverence before something sublime such as nature, deities or great masters; 畏怖 stresses fear more. Elevated written language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"居丈高な態度で話す。","romaji":"Itakedaka na taido de hanasu.","german":"He speaks in an overbearing manner.","furigana":"いたけだか|たいど|はな"},{"japanese":"その客は店員に居丈高に文句を言い続けた。","romaji":"Sono kyaku wa ten'in ni itakedaka ni monku o iitsuzuketa.","german":"The customer kept complaining to the clerk in a domineering way.","furigana":"きゃく|てんいん|いたけだか|もんく|い|つづ"},{"japanese":"立場が上になると、急に居丈高になる人がいる。","romaji":"Tachiba ga ue ni naru to, kyū ni itakedaka ni naru hito ga iru.","german":"Some people suddenly become overbearing when they gain a higher position.","furigana":"たちば|うえ|きゅう|いたけだか|ひと"}],"notes":"居丈高 describes a domineering, intimidating manner of treating others \"from above\"; literally, sitting up tall (居丈 \"sitting height\") to look down at the other person. Typical are 居丈高な態度, 居丈高に言う／命じる and 居丈高になる. It is more aggressive than 偉そう and close to 高圧的 and 横柄; 傲慢 denotes more the character trait, 居丈高 the concrete behavior. Also written 威丈高.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"戦争の帰趨が決まった。","romaji":"Sensō no kisū ga kimatta.","german":"The outcome of the war was decided.","furigana":"せんそう|きすう|き"},{"japanese":"今回の選挙の帰趨を左右するのは無党派層だ。","romaji":"Konkai no senkyo no kisū o sayū suru no wa mutōhasō da.","german":"Independent voters will decide the outcome of this election.","furigana":"こんかい|せんきょ|きすう|さゆう|むとうはそう"},{"japanese":"交渉の帰趨は依然として不透明だ。","romaji":"Kōshō no kisū wa izen to shite futōmei da.","german":"The outcome of the negotiations remains unclear.","furigana":"こうしょう|きすう|いぜん|ふとうめい"}],"notes":"帰趨 is the \"outcome\" of a matter or the direction it is heading, above all with wars, elections, negotiations or trials. Fixed combinations are 勝敗の帰趨, 帰趨を決する (\"to decide the outcome\"), 帰趨を見守る and 帰趨が注目される. The word belongs to written and news language; in everyday speech people say 結果 or 行方. Unlike 結果, 帰趨 usually stresses a still open course whose end one awaits with suspense.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その風景は故郷を彷彿とさせる。","romaji":"Sono fūkei wa kokyō o hōfutsu to saseru.","german":"That scenery is reminiscent of my hometown.","furigana":"ふうけい|こきょう|ほうふつ"},{"japanese":"彼女の歌声は往年の名歌手を彷彿とさせる。","romaji":"Kanojo no utagoe wa ōnen no meikashu o hōfutsu to saseru.","german":"Her singing voice evokes a great singer of bygone days.","furigana":"かのじょ|うたごえ|おうねん|めいかしゅ|ほうふつ"},{"japanese":"息子の笑い方は、亡くなった父親を彷彿とさせた。","romaji":"Musuko no waraikata wa, nakunatta chichioya o hōfutsu to saseta.","german":"My son's way of laughing recalled my late father.","furigana":"むすこ|わら|かた|な|ちちおや|ほうふつ"}],"notes":"彷彿 means \"standing vividly before the eyes, strikingly similar\"; 彷彿とさせる means \"to vividly remind of, to make one think of involuntarily\". The pattern is A が B を彷彿とさせる, where A and B are strikingly similar. The short form 彷彿させる is equally common; without the causative one says 〜が彷彿とする or 目に彷彿と浮かぶ. Compared with 思い出させる it is literary and stresses strong similarity; very common in reviews and travel reports.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"交渉の端緒を開いた。","romaji":"Kōshō no tancho o hiraita.","german":"He opened the way for negotiations.","furigana":"こうしょう|たんちょ|ひら"},{"japanese":"この発見が新薬開発の端緒を開いた。","romaji":"Kono hakken ga shin'yaku kaihatsu no tancho o hiraita.","german":"This discovery opened the door to the development of a new drug.","furigana":"はっけん|しんやくかいはつ|たんちょ|ひら"},{"japanese":"彼の一言が、長年の対立を解消する端緒を開いた。","romaji":"Kare no hitokoto ga, naganen no tairitsu o kaishō suru tancho o hiraita.","german":"His one remark opened the way to resolving the long-standing conflict.","furigana":"かれ|ひとこと|ながねん|たいりつ|かいしょう|たんちょ|ひら"}],"notes":"端緒 is the \"first approach, starting point\" (originally read たんしょ, today mostly たんちょ); 端緒を開く means \"to take the first step, to pave the way for something\". The pattern is 〜の端緒を開く, e.g. 解決の端緒を開く or 交渉の端緒を開く. Related are 端緒をつかむ (\"to find a first clue\", e.g. in investigations) and 〜の端緒となる. Literary; in everyday speech people say きっかけを作る or 道を開く.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"寂然とした空間が広がる。","romaji":"Sekizen to shita kūkan ga hirogaru.","german":"A desolate, silent space stretches out.","furigana":"せきぜん|くうかん|ひろ"},{"japanese":"夜の境内は寂然と静まり返っていた。","romaji":"Yoru no keidai wa sekizen to shizumarikaette ita.","german":"The temple grounds were utterly silent and lonely at night.","furigana":"よる|けいだい|せきぜん|しず|かえ"},{"japanese":"住む人のいなくなった村に、古い家々が寂然と立ち並んでいる。","romaji":"Sumu hito no inaku natta mura ni, furui ieie ga sekizen to tachinarande iru.","german":"In the village where no one lives anymore, old houses stand in a row, forlorn and silent.","furigana":"す|ひと|むら|ふる|いえいえ|せきぜん|た|なら"}],"notes":"寂然 describes a stillness that seems at once lonely and abandoned, for example in a deserted temple or an abandoned village. As an adverb, 寂然と mostly stands with verbs such as 静まり返る, 佇む or 立ち並ぶ; attributively one says 寂然とした + noun, elevated also 寂然たる. The word is purely literary; in everyday speech people say ひっそり(と) or しんと. The Buddhist reading じゃくねん appears above all in religious texts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不躾なお願いで恐縮ですが、推薦状を書いていただけないでしょうか。","romaji":"Bushitsuke na onegai de kyōshuku desu ga, suisenjō o kaite itadakenai deshō ka.","german":"I apologize for the impertinent request, but could I ask you to write me a letter of recommendation?","furigana":"ぶしつけ|ねが|きょうしゅく|すいせんじょう|か"},{"japanese":"初対面の人に年収を聞くのは不躾だ。","romaji":"Shotaimen no hito ni nenshū o kiku no wa bushitsuke da.","german":"Asking someone you've just met about their annual income is rude.","furigana":"しょたいめん|ひと|ねんしゅう|き|ぶしつけ"},{"japanese":"その男は私の顔を不躾にじろじろと見た。","romaji":"Sono otoko wa watashi no kao o bushitsuke ni jirojiro to mita.","german":"The man stared at my face rudely.","furigana":"おとこ|わたし|かお|ぶしつけ|み"}],"notes":"不躾 literally means \"without 躾 (upbringing, good manners)\", hence \"tactless, boorish, impertinent\". Typical are 不躾な質問／お願い and 不躾にじろじろ見る. Very common is the modest cushioning phrase 不躾なお願いで恐縮ですが or 不躾ながら, with which one introduces a direct request or question and apologizes in advance for the imposition. Compared with 失礼, 不躾 aims more at a lack of upbringing; 無礼 is sharper.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は危機的状況でも泰然自若としていた。","romaji":"Kare wa kikiteki jōkyō demo taizen jijaku to shite ita.","german":"He remained unruffled even in a critical situation.","furigana":"かれ|ききてきじょうきょう|たいぜんじじゃく"},{"japanese":"大きな地震が来ても、祖母は泰然自若としてお茶を飲んでいた。","romaji":"Ōkina jishin ga kite mo, sobo wa taizen jijaku to shite ocha o nonde ita.","german":"Even when a big earthquake struck, my grandmother was calmly drinking tea.","furigana":"おお|じしん|き|そぼ|たいぜんじじゃく|ちゃ|の"},{"japanese":"記者の厳しい質問にも、首相は泰然自若たる態度で答えた。","romaji":"Kisha no kibishii shitsumon ni mo, shushō wa taizen jijaku taru taido de kotaeta.","german":"The prime minister answered the reporters' tough questions with composure.","furigana":"きしゃ|きび|しつもん|しゅしょう|たいぜんじじゃく|たいど|こた"}],"notes":"泰然 means \"calm, serene\", 自若 \"to remain oneself, unchanged\". 泰然自若 describes someone who stays perfectly composed even in danger or under pressure. It is a たる-adjective: adverbially 泰然自若として（いる）, attributively 泰然自若たる態度; a な-use is uncommon. Related are 冷静 and 落ち着いている; 泰然自若 is more elevated and stresses inner imperturbability.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は準備に余念がない。","romaji":"Kare wa junbi ni yonen ga nai.","german":"He is meticulous in his preparations.","furigana":"かれ|じゅんび|よねん"},{"japanese":"受験を控えた生徒たちは、最後の追い込みに余念がない。","romaji":"Juken o hikaeta seito-tachi wa, saigo no oikomi ni yonen ga nai.","german":"Students facing exams are working hard on their final push.","furigana":"じゅけん|ひか|せいと|さいご|お|こ|よねん"},{"japanese":"開店を前に、店主は料理の仕込みに余念がなかった。","romaji":"Kaiten o mae ni, tenshu wa ryōri no shikomi ni yonen ga nakatta.","german":"Ahead of opening, the shop owner was fully occupied with preparing the food.","furigana":"かいてん|まえ|てんしゅ|りょうり|しこ|よねん"}],"notes":"余念 means \"other thoughts\"; 余念がない therefore means \"to think of nothing else but …\", \"to be completely absorbed in something\". The target always takes に: 準備に余念がない, 練習に余念がない. It mostly describes others (athletes, merchants, examinees) in an appreciative tone; about oneself it sounds unusual. Similar are 〜に専念する and 〜に没頭する; 余念がない is, however, a fixed adjectival predicate and typical of news and reports.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"堰を切ったように話し始めた。","romaji":"Seki o kitta yō ni hanashi hajimeta.","german":"She began to speak as if a dam had broken.","furigana":"せき|き|はな|はじ"},{"japanese":"母の顔を見た途端、彼女は堰を切ったように泣き出した。","romaji":"Haha no kao o mita totan, kanojo wa seki o kitta yō ni nakidashita.","german":"The moment she saw her mother's face, she burst into tears.","furigana":"はは|かお|み|とたん|かのじょ|せき|き|な|だ"},{"japanese":"規制が緩和されると、堰を切ったように新しい企業が参入してきた。","romaji":"Kisei ga kanwa sareru to, seki o kitta yō ni atarashii kigyō ga sannyū shite kita.","german":"When the regulations were relaxed, new companies poured in as if a dam had burst.","furigana":"きせい|かんわ|せき|き|あたら|きぎょう|さんにゅう"}],"notes":"堰 is a weir or dam; 堰を切る literally means \"to open the weir\", so that the dammed-up water shoots out. It almost always appears as 堰を切ったように (\"as if a dam had broken\") before verbs such as 泣き出す, 話し出す or 押し寄せる: something held back for a long time (tears, words, people, inquiries) suddenly bursts forth. Intransitively one also says 堰が切れる. Despite its -iru ending, 切る is a godan verb (切った).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の慧眼には感服する。","romaji":"Kare no keigan ni wa kanpuku suru.","german":"I admire his keen insight.","furigana":"かれ|けいがん|かんぷく"},{"japanese":"この技術の将来性を十年前に見抜いていたとは、さすがの慧眼だ。","romaji":"Kono gijutsu no shōraisei o jūnen mae ni minuite ita to wa, sasuga no keigan da.","german":"To have seen through the future potential of this technology ten years ago is truly his sharp eye.","furigana":"ぎじゅつ|しょうらいせい|じゅうねんまえ|みぬ|けいがん"},{"japanese":"社長は人材を見抜く慧眼の持ち主として知られている。","romaji":"Shachō wa jinzai o minuku keigan no mochinushi to shite shirarete iru.","german":"The president is known as a man with a keen eye for talent.","furigana":"しゃちょう|じんざい|みぬ|けいがん|も|ぬし|し"}],"notes":"慧眼 is a \"sharp, penetrating eye\" for the essence of things, especially the ability to judge people or developments correctly at an early stage. Typical are 慧眼の持ち主, 慧眼の士, 慧眼に感服する and さすがの慧眼だ. It is a praising word of written language and is said almost only about others. Similar are 炯眼 (same reading, \"piercing gaze\"), 洞察力 (\"insight\") and 先見の明 (\"foresight\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"憫然たる状況に置かれている。","romaji":"Binzen taru jōkyō ni okarete iru.","german":"He is placed in a pitiable situation.","furigana":"びんぜん|じょうきょう|お"},{"japanese":"戦火を逃れた難民たちの憫然たる姿が報じられた。","romaji":"Senka o nogareta nanmin-tachi no binzen taru sugata ga hōjirareta.","german":"The pitiable state of the refugees who escaped the flames of war was reported.","furigana":"せんか|のが|なんみん|びんぜん|すがた|ほう"},{"japanese":"かつての権力者は、憫然たる最期を遂げた。","romaji":"Katsute no kenryokusha wa, binzen taru saigo o togeta.","german":"The once powerful man met a miserable end.","furigana":"けんりょくしゃ|びんぜん|さいご|と"}],"notes":"憫然 means \"pitiable, wretched\": a condition that arouses pity (憫 = \"pity\"). The たる-form stands attributively before nouns such as 姿, 有様, 状況 or 最期; there are also 憫然な and adverbial 憫然と. The word is very literary; in everyday speech people say 哀れな, 気の毒な or 不憫な, the latter above all for children and animals.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"技術の進歩は諸刃の剣だ。","romaji":"Gijutsu no shinpo wa moroha no tsurugi da.","german":"Der technologische Fortschritt ist ein zweischneidiges Schwert.","furigana":"ぎじゅつ|しんぽ|もろは|つるぎ"},{"japanese":"SNSは便利だが、使い方次第では諸刃の剣になる。","romaji":"Esu-enu-esu wa benri da ga, tsukaikata shidai de wa moroha no tsurugi ni naru.","german":"Soziale Medien sind praktisch, können aber je nach Nutzung zum zweischneidigen Schwert werden.","furigana":"べんり|つか|かたしだい|もろは|つるぎ"},{"japanese":"強い薬はよく効く反面、副作用もあって諸刃の剣だ。","romaji":"Tsuyoi kusuri wa yoku kiku hanmen, fukusayō mo atte moroha no tsurugi da.","german":"Starke Medikamente wirken gut, haben aber auch Nebenwirkungen – ein zweischneidiges Schwert.","furigana":"つよ|くすり|き|はんめん|ふくさよう|もろは|つるぎ"}],"notes":"諸刃 ist eine beidseitig geschliffene Klinge (諸 = „beide“), 剣 (hier つるぎ gelesen) ein Schwert: Wer damit zuschlägt, kann sich selbst verletzen. Bildlich ist es wie im Deutschen das „zweischneidige Schwert“ – etwas, das großen Nutzen bringt, aber ebenso schaden kann. Typisch sind 〜は諸刃の剣だ und 諸刃の剣となる. Auch 両刃の剣 geschrieben. Neutral und in Kommentaren, Reden und Essays häufig.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"手前味噌ですが、うちの製品は品質がいい。","romaji":"Temae miso desu ga, uchi no seihin wa hinshitsu ga ii.","german":"It may sound like boasting, but our products are of high quality.","furigana":"てまえみそ|せいひん|ひんしつ"},{"japanese":"手前味噌になりますが、この企画はよくできていると思います。","romaji":"Temae miso ni narimasu ga, kono kikaku wa yoku dekite iru to omoimasu.","german":"I don't mean to praise myself, but I think this plan turned out well.","furigana":"てまえみそ|きかく|おも"},{"japanese":"部長の話はいつも手前味噌ばかりで、聞いていて疲れる。","romaji":"Buchō no hanashi wa itsumo temae miso bakari de, kiite ite tsukareru.","german":"The department head's talk is always just self-praise, and listening to it is tiring.","furigana":"ぶちょう|はなし|てまえみそ|き|つか"}],"notes":"手前 \"I myself, one's own\" and 味噌 \"miso\": one praises one's own homemade miso, that is, one's own things or achievements. Mostly used as a modest preface before praising something of one's own: 手前味噌ですが…／手前味噌になりますが… \"it may sound like self-praise, but...\". As criticism of others: 手前味噌ばかり. Polite-colloquial, common in working life.","levelNote":"Compound: 手前 (N2) + 味噌 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の発言が議論に一石を投じた。","romaji":"Kare no hatsugen ga giron ni isseki o tōjita.","german":"Seine Äußerung löste eine Debatte aus.","furigana":"かれ|はつげん|ぎろん|いっせき|とう"},{"japanese":"その映画は、学校教育のあり方に一石を投じた。","romaji":"Sono eiga wa, gakkō kyōiku no arikata ni isseki o tōjita.","german":"Der Film gab einen Denkanstoß zur Frage, wie Schule aussehen sollte.","furigana":"えいが|がっこうきょういく|かた|いっせき|とう"},{"japanese":"若手議員が党の方針に一石を投じる質問をした。","romaji":"Wakate giin ga tō no hōshin ni isseki o tōjiru shitsumon o shita.","german":"Ein junger Abgeordneter stellte eine Frage, die die Parteilinie zur Diskussion stellte.","furigana":"わかてぎいん|とう|ほうしん|いっせき|とう|しつもん"}],"notes":"Wörtlich „einen Stein (ins Wasser) werfen“, sodass sich Wellen ausbreiten: Man wirft eine Frage oder einen Vorschlag auf, der eine Diskussion auslöst oder bestehende Ansichten in Frage stellt. Das Muster ist 〜に一石を投じる (議論に, 業界に, 社会に). Die Wendung ist neutral bis anerkennend – anders als das deutsche „Stein des Anstoßes“, das ein Ärgernis meint. Gehoben auch 一石を投ずる; verwandt ist 波紋を呼ぶ („Wellen schlagen“).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この研究が新分野の嚆矢となった。","romaji":"Kono kenkyū ga shinbun'ya no kōshi to natta.","german":"This research marked the beginning of a new field.","furigana":"けんきゅう|しんぶんや|こうし"},{"japanese":"この小説は日本の推理小説の嚆矢となった作品だ。","romaji":"Kono shōsetsu wa Nihon no suiri shōsetsu no kōshi to natta sakuhin da.","german":"This novel is the work that started Japanese detective fiction.","furigana":"しょうせつ|にほん|すいりしょうせつ|こうし|さくひん"},{"japanese":"彼が開いた小さなカフェが、商店街再生の嚆矢となった。","romaji":"Kare ga hiraita chiisana kafe ga, shōtengai saisei no kōshi to natta.","german":"The small cafe he opened became the first step in the revival of the shopping street.","furigana":"かれ|ひら|ちい|しょうてんがいさいせい|こうし"}],"notes":"嚆矢 was originally a \"whistling arrow\" shot in ancient China as a signal for the start of a battle; from this came \"beginning, first case\" of a development. 〜の嚆矢となる means \"to form the beginning of …, to be the first …\". Equally common is 〜を嚆矢とする (\"to begin with …\"), e.g. in historical accounts. Very literary and typical of specialist and historical texts; in everyday speech people say 始まり or 先駆け.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"全体を俯瞰する視点が必要だ。","romaji":"Zentai o fukan suru shiten ga hitsuyō da.","german":"A perspective that surveys the whole is needed.","furigana":"ぜんたい|ふかん|してん|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"展望台からは町全体を俯瞰することができる。","romaji":"Tenbōdai kara wa machi zentai o fukan suru koto ga dekiru.","german":"From the observation deck you can overlook the whole town.","furigana":"てんぼうだい|まちぜんたい|ふかん"},{"japanese":"一歩引いて状況を俯瞰すると、問題の原因が見えてきた。","romaji":"Ippo hiite jōkyō o fukan suru to, mondai no gen'in ga miete kita.","german":"When I stepped back and viewed the situation from above, the cause of the problem became visible.","furigana":"いっぽひ|じょうきょう|ふかん|もんだい|げんいん|み"}],"notes":"俯瞰 means \"to look down from above, to view from a bird's-eye perspective\" (俯 \"to bend down\", 瞰 \"to look down\"). Literally: 山頂から町を俯瞰する; today even more common figuratively: 全体を俯瞰する, 状況を俯瞰的に見る (\"to view the big picture with distance\"). A transitive する-verb with を. Compared with 見下ろす (\"to look down\", also \"to look down on\"), it is more matter-of-fact and elevated; compared with 見渡す, it stresses the overview from above. Related: 俯瞰図 (\"bird's-eye view diagram\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いかなる時も矜持を保つ。","romaji":"Ikanaru toki mo kyōji o tamotsu.","german":"I keep my dignity at all times.","furigana":"とき|きょうじ|たも"},{"japanese":"経営が苦しくても、その店は老舗としての矜持を保ち続けた。","romaji":"Keiei ga kurushikute mo, sono mise wa shinise to shite no kyōji o tamochitsuzuketa.","german":"Even when business was hard, the shop kept its pride as a long-established store.","furigana":"けいえい|くる|みせ|しにせ|きょうじ|たも|つづ"},{"japanese":"地位を失っても、彼は最後まで矜持を保っていた。","romaji":"Chii o ushinatte mo, kare wa saigo made kyōji o tamotte ita.","german":"Even after losing his position, he maintained his dignity to the end.","furigana":"ちい|うしな|かれ|さいご|きょうじ|たも"}],"notes":"矜持 is \"pride, self-respect\" in the dignified sense: the awareness of one's own worth that keeps you from degrading yourself. 矜持を保つ means \"to preserve one's dignity\"; related are 矜持を持つ, 〜としての矜持 (職人としての矜持) and 矜持が許さない. The original reading is きんじ, today きょうじ is usual. Unlike プライド or 自尊心, which can also mean vanity or touchiness, 矜持 is positive and elevated.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"晦渋な文章で読みにくい。","romaji":"Kaijū na bunshō de yominikui.","german":"The prose is obscure and hard to read.","furigana":"かいじゅう|ぶんしょう|よ"},{"japanese":"彼の論文は晦渋で、専門家でさえ理解に苦しむ。","romaji":"Kare no ronbun wa kaijū de, senmonka de sae rikai ni kurushimu.","german":"His paper is so obscure that even specialists struggle to understand it.","furigana":"かれ|ろんぶん|かいじゅう|せんもんか|りかい|くる"},{"japanese":"晦渋な表現は避けて、誰にでも分かる言葉で書いてください。","romaji":"Kaijū na hyōgen wa sakete, dare ni demo wakaru kotoba de kaite kudasai.","german":"Please avoid obscure expressions and write in words anyone can understand.","furigana":"かいじゅう|ひょうげん|さ|だれ|わ|ことば|か"}],"notes":"晦渋 describes texts, ways of expression or lines of thought that are \"dark, convoluted and hard to access\" (晦 \"dark\", 渋 \"halting\"). Typical combinations are 晦渋な文章, 晦渋な表現, 晦渋な文体 and 晦渋で読みにくい. Compared with the more common 難解 (\"hard to understand\"), 晦渋 stresses the unclear, twisting presentation and sounds more critical and literary. The opposite is 平易 (\"easy to understand\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"旧友と思いがけず邂逅した。","romaji":"Kyūyū to omoigakezu kaikō shita.","german":"I unexpectedly ran into an old friend.","furigana":"きゅうゆう|おも|かいこう"},{"japanese":"旅先のパリで、二十年ぶりに恩師と邂逅した。","romaji":"Tabisaki no Pari de, nijūnen-buri ni onshi to kaikō shita.","german":"On a trip in Paris, I met my former teacher by chance for the first time in twenty years.","furigana":"たびさき|にじゅうねん|おんし|かいこう"},{"japanese":"一冊の本との邂逅が、彼の人生を大きく変えた。","romaji":"Issatsu no hon to no kaikō ga, kare no jinsei o ōkiku kaeta.","german":"An encounter with a single book changed his life greatly.","furigana":"いっさつ|ほん|かいこう|かれ|じんせい|おお|か"}],"notes":"邂逅 is a \"chance, often fateful encounter\", especially an unexpected reunion after a long time. The other party takes と: 旧友と邂逅する; the nominal use 〜との邂逅 or 運命的な邂逅 is also common. The word is literary and elevated; in everyday speech people say 偶然会う, 出会う or 巡り合う. A formative encounter with a book, an idea or an artwork can also be a 邂逅.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その理論を敷衍して説明した。","romaji":"Sono riron o fuen shite setsumei shita.","german":"I explained by elaborating on that theory.","furigana":"りろん|ふえん|せつめい"},{"japanese":"先生の言葉を敷衍すれば、努力は才能に勝るということだ。","romaji":"Sensei no kotoba o fuen sureba, doryoku wa sainō ni masaru to iu koto da.","german":"Expanding on the teacher's words, effort beats talent.","furigana":"せんせい|ことば|ふえん|どりょく|さいのう|まさ"},{"japanese":"この調査結果は、他の地域にも敷衍できるだろう。","romaji":"Kono chōsa kekka wa, hoka no chiiki ni mo fuen dekiru darō.","german":"These survey results can probably be extended to other regions as well.","furigana":"ちょうさけっか|ほか|ちいき|ふえん"}],"notes":"敷衍 means to \"elaborate\" a thought or \"think it further\": to explain a brief statement with examples or to transfer a principle to other cases. Typical are 〜を敷衍して説明する, 敷衍して言えば (\"taken further, this means …\") and 〜を〜に敷衍する (\"to extend to …\"). Compared with 説明する it is academic and literary; 詳述する stresses detail, 敷衍する the widening of the thought. Also written 敷延.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"そんな行為は言語道断だ。","romaji":"Sonna kōi wa gongo dōdan da.","german":"Such conduct is outrageous.","furigana":"こうい|ごんごどうだん"},{"japanese":"飲酒運転なんて言語道断です。","romaji":"Inshu unten nante gongo dōdan desu.","german":"Drunk driving is out of the question.","furigana":"いんしゅうんてん|ごんごどうだん"},{"japanese":"顧客の個人情報を売るとは、言語道断の不祥事だ。","romaji":"Kokyaku no kojin jōhō o uru to wa, gongo dōdan no fushōji da.","german":"Selling customers' personal information is an outrageous scandal.","furigana":"こきゃく|こじんじょうほう|う|ごんごどうだん|ふしょうじ"}],"notes":"Originally a Buddhist term: the deepest truth lies beyond language (言語 \"words\", 道断 \"the way is cut off\"). Today 言語道断 means \"so outrageous that words fail\". Note the reading: 言語 is read ごんご here, not げんご. Typical frames are 〜とは言語道断だ, 〜なんて言語道断だ and 言語道断な／の行為; the expression is a sharp moral condemnation, stronger than とんでもない or ひどい.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の胸に怒りが滾った。","romaji":"Kare no mune ni ikari ga tagitta.","german":"Anger seethed in his chest.","furigana":"かれ|むね|いか|たぎ"},{"japanese":"鍋の湯がぐらぐらと滾っている。","romaji":"Nabe no yu ga guragura to tagitte iru.","german":"The water in the pot is boiling vigorously.","furigana":"なべ|ゆ|たぎ"},{"japanese":"決勝戦を前に、選手たちの血が滾っていた。","romaji":"Kesshōsen o mae ni, senshu-tachi no chi ga tagitte ita.","german":"Ahead of the final, the players' blood was boiling.","furigana":"けっしょうせん|まえ|せんしゅ|ち|たぎ"}],"notes":"滾る first describes water that boils bubbling or foams as a torrent (湯が滾る, 滾る激流). Figuratively it stands for surging emotions: 血が滾る (\"the blood is stirred\"), 闘志が滾る, 怒りが滾る. It is intransitive; \"to bring to a boil, to stoke\" is 滾らせる (闘志を滾らせる). Compared with 沸く／沸騰する it is more vivid and literary; mostly written in kana (たぎる).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"店に入る前に傘を窄めた。","romaji":"Mise ni hairu mae ni kasa o subometa.","german":"I closed my umbrella before entering the shop.","furigana":"みせ|はい|まえ|かさ|すぼ"},{"japanese":"冷たい風に肩を窄めながら、駅まで歩いた。","romaji":"Tsumetai kaze ni kata o subomenagara, eki made aruita.","german":"I walked to the station, hunching my shoulders against the cold wind.","furigana":"つめ|かぜ|かた|すぼ|えき|ある"},{"japanese":"子供はすっぱい梅干しを食べて、口を窄めた。","romaji":"Kodomo wa suppai umeboshi o tabete, kuchi o subometa.","german":"The child ate a sour pickled plum and puckered his mouth.","furigana":"こども|うめぼ|た|くち|すぼ"}],"notes":"窄める means \"to make narrower, to draw together\" and occurs almost only in fixed combinations: 傘を窄める (\"to close the umbrella\"), 肩を窄める (\"to hunch the shoulders, to make oneself small\", from cold or embarrassment) and 口を窄める (\"to purse the lips, to pucker the mouth\"). It is transitive with を; intransitive counterparts are 窄まる and 窄む (\"to become narrower\"). Mostly written in kana (すぼめる). Shrugging the shoulders, by contrast, is 肩をすくめる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は「俺に勝てる奴はいない」と嘯いている。","romaji":"Kare wa \"Ore ni kateru yatsu wa inai\" to usobuite iru.","german":"He brags, \"Nobody can beat me.\"","furigana":"かれ|おれ|か|やつ|うそぶ"},{"japanese":"証拠を突きつけられても、彼は「何のことか分からない」と嘯いた。","romaji":"Shōko o tsukitsukerarete mo, kare wa \"Nan no koto ka wakaranai\" to usobuita.","german":"Even when confronted with the evidence, he brazenly said, \"I don't know what you mean.\"","furigana":"しょうこ|つ|かれ|なん|わ|うそぶ"},{"japanese":"「金なんかに興味はない」と嘯いていた男が、実は大金を隠していた。","romaji":"\"Kane nanka ni kyōmi wa nai\" to usobuite ita otoko ga, jitsu wa taikin o kakushite ita.","german":"The man who had claimed \"I'm not interested in money\" was actually hiding a large sum.","furigana":"かね|きょうみ|うそぶ|おとこ|じつ|たいきん|かく"}],"notes":"嘯く today almost always appears with a quotation: 「…」と嘯く. It means to announce something brazenly or boastfully, often with feigned indifference (\"to make a show of saying\"), or to brazenly pretend ignorance (「知らない」と嘯く). The tone is always critical of the speaker. Unlike 自慢する (\"to boast about something\", with を), 嘯く takes no direct object such as 実力を. Literary and mostly written in kana; the old meanings \"to recite to oneself\" and \"to howl (of animals)\" are rare.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"出発を前にして気が逸る。","romaji":"Shuppatsu o mae ni shite ki ga hayaru.","german":"I feel impatient ahead of departure.","furigana":"しゅっぱつ|まえ|き|はや"},{"japanese":"逸る気持ちを抑えて、まずは深呼吸した。","romaji":"Hayaru kimochi o osaete, mazu wa shinkokyū shita.","german":"Holding back my eager feelings, I first took a deep breath.","furigana":"はや|きも|おさ|しんこきゅう"},{"japanese":"血気に逸った若者たちが、無謀な計画を立てた。","romaji":"Kekki ni hayatta wakamono-tachi ga, mubō na keikaku o tateta.","german":"Young men carried away by hot-bloodedness made a reckless plan.","furigana":"けっき|はや|わかもの|むぼう|けいかく|た"}],"notes":"逸る describes a feeling that runs ahead: you are impatient, excited or hasty. Typical are 気が逸る, 心が逸る, 逸る気持ちを抑える and 血気に逸る (\"to be carried away by youthful impetuosity\", rather negative). It is intransitive and mostly written in kana; not to be confused with the identically sounding 流行る (\"to be in fashion, to be rampant\"). 焦る, by contrast, stresses nervousness and time pressure more.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子供を拐かす事件が報道された。","romaji":"Kodomo o kadowakasu jiken ga hōdō sareta.","german":"A case of child abduction was reported.","furigana":"こども|かどわ|じけん|ほうどう"},{"japanese":"「暗くなる前に帰らないと、人さらいに拐かされるよ」と祖母はよく言っていた。","romaji":"\"Kuraku naru mae ni kaeranai to, hitosarai ni kadowakasareru yo\" to sobo wa yoku itte ita.","german":"\"If you don't come home before dark, a kidnapper will take you,\" my grandmother often said.","furigana":"くら|まえ|かえ|ひと|かどわ|そぼ|い"},{"japanese":"物語の中で、姫は盗賊に拐かされてしまう。","romaji":"Monogatari no naka de, hime wa tōzoku ni kadowakasarete shimau.","german":"In the story, the princess is abducted by bandits.","furigana":"ものがたり|なか|ひめ|とうぞく|かどわ"}],"notes":"拐かす means \"to abduct or carry off someone by force or trickery\", traditionally above all children or young women. It sounds archaic and appears in tales, historical novels and fairy tales, often in the passive (人さらいに拐かされる). In news and everyday speech people say 誘拐する or さらう. Transitive with を; mostly written in kana (かどわかす).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"他人を謗る行為は慎むべきだ。","romaji":"Tanin o soshiru kōi wa tsutsushimu beki da.","german":"One should refrain from slandering others.","furigana":"たにん|そし|こうい|つつし"},{"japanese":"彼は世間から裏切り者と謗られた。","romaji":"Kare wa seken kara uragirimono to soshirareta.","german":"He was denounced by the world as a traitor.","furigana":"かれ|せけん|うらぎ|もの|そし"},{"japanese":"このような判断は、無責任の謗りを免れないだろう。","romaji":"Kono yō na handan wa, musekinin no soshiri o manukarenai darō.","german":"Such a decision will not escape the charge of irresponsibility.","furigana":"はんだん|むせきにん|そし|まぬか"}],"notes":"謗る means \"to revile, to disparage, to run someone down\" and is transitive with を: 人を謗る, 陰で謗る. It is literary and mostly written in kana; in everyday speech people say 悪口を言う or 非難する, and 中傷する for targeted defamation. Very common is the noun 謗り in the fixed phrase 〜の謗りを免れない (\"cannot escape the charge of …\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"パン生地をよく捏ねると美味しくなる。","romaji":"Pan kiji o yoku koneru to oishiku naru.","german":"Kneading bread dough well makes it tasty.","furigana":"きじ|こ|おい"},{"japanese":"子供がおもちゃ売り場で駄々を捏ねている。","romaji":"Kodomo ga omocha uriba de dada o konete iru.","german":"A child is throwing a tantrum in the toy section.","furigana":"こども|う|ば|だだ|こ"},{"japanese":"彼はいつも屁理屈を捏ねて、面倒な仕事を断る。","romaji":"Kare wa itsumo herikutsu o konete, mendō na shigoto o kotowaru.","german":"He always quibbles to refuse troublesome jobs.","furigana":"かれ|へりくつ|こ|めんどう|しごと|ことわ"}],"notes":"捏ねる first means \"to knead\" (パン生地を捏ねる, 粘土を捏ねる). Figuratively it appears in fixed phrases for persistent, useless arguing: 駄々を捏ねる (\"to whine, to throw a tantrum\", especially of children) and 理屈／屁理屈を捏ねる (\"to split hairs\"). Transitive with を, mostly written in kana (こねる). The similar 練る also means \"to knead\", but also \"to work out (a plan)\" -- one does not say 計画を捏ねる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"雨垂れ石を穿つという諺がある。","romaji":"Amadare ishi o ugatsu to iu kotowaza ga aru.","german":"There is a proverb that dripping water wears away stone.","furigana":"|いし|うが|ことわざ"},{"japanese":"作業員たちは固い岩盤を穿って、トンネルを掘り進めた。","romaji":"Sagyōin-tachi wa katai ganban o ugatte, tonneru o horisusumeta.","german":"The workers drilled through the hard bedrock and pushed the tunnel forward.","furigana":"さぎょういん|かた|がんばん|うが|ほ|すす"},{"japanese":"彼の批評は、人間の本質を穿っている。","romaji":"Kare no hihyō wa, ningen no honshitsu o ugatte iru.","german":"His criticism hits upon the essence of human nature.","furigana":"かれ|ひひょう|にんげん|ほんしつ|うが"}],"notes":"穿つ literally means \"to bore a hole, to pierce\" (岩を穿つ), known from the proverb 雨垂れ石を穿つ (\"constant dripping wears away the stone\"). Figuratively it means \"to hit the core, to grasp shrewdly\": 穿った見方 is actually a \"pertinent, penetrating view\". In present-day usage, however, 穿った見方をする is often used in the sense of \"to look at with suspicion, in a biased way\", which is considered a misuse. Literary; transitive with を.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"馬が牧場で草を食んでいる。","romaji":"Uma ga bokujō de kusa o hande iru.","german":"A horse is grazing on the pasture.","furigana":"うま|ぼくじょう|くさ|は"},{"japanese":"小鳥が庭の柿の実を食んでいる。","romaji":"Kotori ga niwa no kaki no mi o hande iru.","german":"A little bird is pecking at the persimmons in the garden.","furigana":"ことり|にわ|かき|み|は"},{"japanese":"彼は三十年間、公務員として国の禄を食んできた。","romaji":"Kare wa sanjūnen-kan, kōmuin to shite kuni no roku o hande kita.","german":"For thirty years he drew a state salary as a civil servant.","furigana":"かれ|さんじゅうねんかん|こうむいん|くに|ろく|は"}],"notes":"食む is a literary word for the eating of animals, especially grazing and pecking (草を食む, 木の実を食む); for people one says 食べる. In the fixed phrase 禄を食む it means \"to be on the payroll, to draw a salary\" (originally from the feudal lord), today for example for civil servants. Transitive with を; mostly written in kana (はむ). Not to be confused with 蝕む (むしばむ, \"to eat away, to undermine\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"古い床板が歩くたびに軋む。","romaji":"Furui yukaita ga aruku tabi ni kishimu.","german":"The old floorboards creak with every step.","furigana":"ふる|ゆかいた|ある|きし"},{"japanese":"夜中にドアの軋む音で目が覚めた。","romaji":"Yonaka ni doa no kishimu oto de me ga sameta.","german":"I woke in the middle of the night to the sound of a creaking door.","furigana":"よなか|きし|おと|め|さ"},{"japanese":"貿易問題をめぐって、両国の関係が軋み始めている。","romaji":"Bōeki mondai o megutte, ryōkoku no kankei ga kishimi hajimete iru.","german":"Over trade issues, relations between the two countries are starting to strain.","furigana":"ぼうえきもんだい|りょうこく|かんけい|きし|はじ"}],"notes":"軋む describes the creaking, squeaking or grating of wood, doors, wheels or joints (床が軋む, ドアが軋む); the onomatopoeia ぎしぎし fits it. Figuratively one says 関係が軋む for relationships in which things \"grate\"; the noun 軋み and the related 軋轢 (\"friction, tension\") are used in this sense. Intransitive; the transitive counterpart is 軋ませる. Mostly written in kana (きしむ).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不満が社員の間で燻っている。","romaji":"Fuman ga shain no aida de kusubutte iru.","german":"Discontent is smoldering among the employees.","furigana":"ふまん|しゃいん|あいだ|くすぶ"},{"japanese":"消したはずの焚き火が、まだ燻っていた。","romaji":"Keshita hazu no takibi ga, mada kusubutte ita.","german":"The campfire I thought I had put out was still smoldering.","furigana":"け|た|び|くすぶ"},{"japanese":"才能があるのに、彼はいつまでも田舎で燻っている。","romaji":"Sainō ga aru no ni, kare wa itsu made mo inaka de kusubutte iru.","german":"Although he has talent, he keeps languishing in the countryside.","furigana":"さいのう|かれ|いなか|くすぶ"}],"notes":"燻る first means \"to smolder, to smoke without really burning\" (焚き火が燻る). Figuratively it stands for conflicts or discontent that keep smoldering beneath the surface (不満が燻る, 火種が燻る), and for people who despite abilities make no progress and \"languish\" (地方で燻っている). Intransitive, mostly in kana (くすぶる). With the same kanji there are いぶる (\"to smoke\") and the transitive 燻す (いぶす, \"to smoke-cure\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は逃亡者を自宅に匿った。","romaji":"Kare wa tōbōsha o jitaku ni kakumatta.","german":"He hid the fugitive in his home.","furigana":"かれ|とうぼうしゃ|じたく|かくま"},{"japanese":"息子は拾ってきた子猫を、親に内緒で部屋に匿っていた。","romaji":"Musuko wa hirotte kita koneko o, oya ni naisho de heya ni kakumatte ita.","german":"My son was secretly keeping the kitten he had picked up in his room, without telling his parents.","furigana":"むすこ|ひろ|こねこ|おや|ないしょ|へや|かくま"},{"japanese":"犯人を匿った疑いで、知人の男が逮捕された。","romaji":"Hannin o kakumatta utagai de, chijin no otoko ga taiho sareta.","german":"An acquaintance was arrested on suspicion of harboring the culprit.","furigana":"はんにん|かくま|うたが|ちじん|おとこ|たいほ"}],"notes":"匿う means \"to hide and protect someone (secretly), to give shelter\", mostly to the persecuted, fugitives or animals. The object takes を, the place に: 逃亡者を自宅に匿う. Unlike 隠す, which is also used for things, 匿う refers almost only to people or animals one takes under protection. Legally, aiding an offender is 犯人蔵匿. Mostly written in kana (かくまう).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"写真の背景を暈す技法を使った。","romaji":"Shashin no haikei o bokasu gihō o tsukatta.","german":"I used a technique that blurs the background of the photo.","furigana":"しゃしん|はいけい|ぼか|ぎほう|つか"},{"japanese":"アイシャドウは指で暈すと自然に仕上がる。","romaji":"Aishadō wa yubi de bokasu to shizen ni shiagaru.","german":"Blending eyeshadow with your finger gives a natural finish.","furigana":"ゆび|ぼか|しぜん|しあ"},{"japanese":"記者の質問に、大臣は肝心な点を暈して答えた。","romaji":"Kisha no shitsumon ni, daijin wa kanjin na ten o bokashite kotaeta.","german":"To the reporters' questions, the minister answered while keeping the crucial points vague.","furigana":"きしゃ|しつもん|だいじん|かんじん|てん|ぼか|こた"}],"notes":"暈す means \"to blur, to let contours fade softly\", for example in painting, photography or make-up (背景を暈す, アイシャドウを暈す). Figuratively it means \"to deliberately keep something vague, to beat around the bush\": 話を暈す, 答えを暈す, 肝心な点を暈す. Transitive with を; the intransitive counterpart is 暈ける (ぼける, \"to become blurred\"). Almost always written in kana (ぼかす).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新入社員に仕事を宛てがった。","romaji":"Shinnyū shain ni shigoto o ategatta.","german":"I assigned work to the new employee.","furigana":"しんにゅうしゃいん|しごと|あ"},{"japanese":"私たちに宛てがわれたのは、窓のない狭い部屋だった。","romaji":"Watashi-tachi ni ategawareta no wa, mado no nai semai heya datta.","german":"What was allotted to us was a small room without windows.","furigana":"わたし|あ|まど|せま|へや"},{"japanese":"ぶつけた膝に、冷たいタオルを宛てがった。","romaji":"Butsuketa hiza ni, tsumetai taoru o ategatta.","german":"I held a cold towel against my bruised knee.","furigana":"ひざ|つめ|あ"}],"notes":"宛てがう has three uses: \"to assign, to allot\" (仕事を宛てがう), \"to give (someone something), to provide\" and \"to apply to something, to hold against\" (傷口にタオルを宛てがう). With allotting there is often an implication that the recipient has no say, especially in the passive 宛てがわれた部屋 (\"the room assigned to one\"). Transitive; mostly written in kana (あてがう). More neutral are 割り当てる and 与える.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"度重なる失敗が彼の意欲を削いだ。","romaji":"Tabikasanaru shippai ga kare no iyoku o soida.","german":"Repeated failures dampened his motivation.","furigana":"たびかさ|しっぱい|かれ|いよく|そ"},{"japanese":"竹を斜めに削いで、花を生ける器を作った。","romaji":"Take o naname ni soide, hana o ikeru utsuwa o tsukutta.","german":"I sliced bamboo at an angle to make a vessel for arranging flowers.","furigana":"たけ|なな|そ|はな|い|うつわ|つく"},{"japanese":"彼の余計な一言が、せっかくの宴の興を削いだ。","romaji":"Kare no yokei na hitokoto ga, sekkaku no utage no kyō o soida.","german":"His needless remark spoiled the fun of the otherwise good banquet.","furigana":"かれ|よけい|ひとこと|うたげ|きょう|そ"}],"notes":"削ぐ concretely means \"to cut off at a slant, to shave, to peel thinly\" (竹を削ぐ, 鰹節を削ぐ). More common is the figurative use \"to diminish, to dampen\": 意欲を削ぐ, 興を削ぐ (\"to spoil the mood\"), 勢力を削ぐ (\"to curtail power\"). Transitive with を. Compared with 削る (けずる, \"to plane, to cut down\", also 予算を削る), 削ぐ stresses the slanting cut or the weakening of strength and motivation. Also written 殺ぐ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"腹痛で道端に蹲った。","romaji":"Fukutsū de michibata ni uzukumatta.","german":"I crouched by the roadside with a stomachache.","furigana":"ふくつう|みちばた|うずくま"},{"japanese":"叱られた少年は、部屋の隅に蹲って泣いていた。","romaji":"Shikarareta shōnen wa, heya no sumi ni uzukumatte naite ita.","german":"The scolded boy was crouching in the corner of the room, crying.","furigana":"しか|しょうねん|へや|すみ|うずくま|な"},{"japanese":"雪の中で、一匹の猫が身を丸めて蹲っていた。","romaji":"Yuki no naka de, ippiki no neko ga mi o marumete uzukumatte ita.","german":"In the snow, a cat was crouching with its body curled up.","furigana":"ゆき|なか|いっぴき|ねこ|み|まる|うずくま"}],"notes":"蹲る means \"to crouch, to squat hunched over\", mostly from pain, fear, cold or exhaustion. The place takes に: 道端に蹲る, 部屋の隅に蹲る; animals such as cats also \"crouch\" this way. Unlike しゃがむ (\"to squat down\", neutral), 蹲る stresses making oneself small and curling up. Intransitive; mostly written in kana (うずくまる).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"長い入院生活で、すっかり足が萎えてしまった。","romaji":"Nagai nyūin seikatsu de, sukkari ashi ga naete shimatta.","german":"After a long hospital stay, my legs have become completely weak.","furigana":"なが|にゅういんせいかつ|あし|な"},{"japanese":"何度も不採用の通知が届き、気力が萎えかけた。","romaji":"Nando mo fusaiyō no tsūchi ga todoki, kiryoku ga naekaketa.","german":"Rejection notices arrived again and again, and my spirit almost flagged.","furigana":"なんど|ふさいよう|つうち|とど|きりょく|な"},{"japanese":"日曜日に仕事のメールが来て、一気に萎えた。","romaji":"Nichiyōbi ni shigoto no mēru ga kite, ikki ni naeta.","german":"A work email arriving on Sunday killed my mood at once.","furigana":"にちようび|しごと|き|いっき|な"}],"notes":"萎える means that strength or tension slackens: limbs become weak (足が萎える, e.g. after long bed rest), courage and willpower flag (気力が萎える). For wilting plants one more often says 萎れる (しおれる). Colloquially 萎える is very common today in the sense of \"to lose the desire, to be turned off\" (それは萎える). Intransitive. Legs that have fallen asleep after sitting seiza, by contrast, are 足がしびれる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"弾丸が頬を掠めた。","romaji":"Dangan ga hō o kasumeta.","german":"The bullet grazed his cheek.","furigana":"だんがん|ほお|かす"},{"japanese":"ふと、嫌な予感が頭を掠めた。","romaji":"Futo, iya na yokan ga atama o kasumeta.","german":"Suddenly an ominous feeling flashed through my mind.","furigana":"いや|よかん|あたま|かす"},{"japanese":"男は店員の目を掠めて、商品をポケットに入れた。","romaji":"Otoko wa ten'in no me o kasumete, shōhin o poketto ni ireta.","german":"The man slipped past the clerk's eyes and put the goods in his pocket.","furigana":"おとこ|てんいん|め|かす|しょうひん|い"}],"notes":"掠める means at its core \"to touch lightly in passing, to graze\" (弾が頬を掠める). From this develop two common uses: a thought or feeling \"flits past\" (不安が頭を掠める, 笑みが口元を掠める), and \"to take something unnoticed\", especially in 〜の目を掠めて (\"unnoticed by …\") and 掠め取る. Transitive with を; mostly written in kana (かすめる). Not to be confused with 掠れる (かすれる, \"to become hoarse, to fade\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"坂道を走って喘いでいる。","romaji":"Sakamichi o hashitte aeide iru.","german":"I am panting after running up the slope.","furigana":"さかみち|はし|あえ"},{"japanese":"長引く不況に喘ぐ中小企業が多い。","romaji":"Nagabiku fukyō ni aegu chūshō kigyō ga ōi.","german":"Many small and medium-sized companies are struggling under the prolonged recession.","furigana":"ながび|ふきょう|あえ|ちゅうしょうきぎょう|おお"},{"japanese":"熱を出した子どもが、苦しそうに喘いでいた。","romaji":"Netsu o dashita kodomo ga, kurushisō ni aeide ita.","german":"The child who had a fever was gasping painfully.","furigana":"ねつ|だ|こ|くる|あえ"}],"notes":"喘ぐ first means \"to gasp, to struggle for breath\", for example after exertion or in illness. At N1 level the figurative meaning \"to suffer under something, to groan\" is particularly important, with に for the burden: 不況に喘ぐ \"to groan under the recession\", 重税に喘ぐ, 貧困に喘ぐ. For plain breathlessness people usually say 息が切れる in everyday speech; 喘ぐ is more vivid and literary. Intransitive godan verb; news likes to use it for companies and regions in distress.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は権力を貪り続けた。","romaji":"Kare wa kenryoku o musabori tsuzuketa.","german":"He kept greedily pursuing power.","furigana":"かれ|けんりょく|むさぼ|つづ"},{"japanese":"お腹を空かせた子供たちは、パンを貪るように食べた。","romaji":"Onaka o sukaseta kodomo-tachi wa, pan o musaboru yō ni tabeta.","german":"The hungry children ate the bread as if devouring it.","furigana":"なか|す|こども|むさぼ|た"},{"japanese":"休日だったので、昼過ぎまで惰眠を貪ってしまった。","romaji":"Kyūjitsu datta node, hirusugi made damin o musabotte shimatta.","german":"It was a holiday, so I ended up idly sleeping until past noon.","furigana":"きゅうじつ|ひるす|だみん|むさぼ"}],"notes":"貪る means \"to crave greedily\" and is transitive with を: 権力を貪る, 利益を貪る. In fixed combinations it also means \"to indulge oneself extensively in something\": 惰眠を貪る (\"to sleep lazily\"), 本を貪るように読む (\"to devour books\"). Concretely it can mean \"to devour greedily\" with food, mostly as 貪るように食べる. Literary and mostly written in kana; the related adjective is 貪欲 (\"greedy\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"春になると梅の蕾が綻ぶ。","romaji":"Haru ni naru to ume no tsubomi ga hokorobu.","german":"When spring comes, the plum buds open.","furigana":"はる|うめ|つぼみ|ほころ"},{"japanese":"孫の顔を見て、祖父の顔が思わず綻んだ。","romaji":"Mago no kao o mite, sofu no kao ga omowazu hokoronda.","german":"Seeing his grandchild's face, the grandfather's face broke into a smile.","furigana":"まご|かお|み|そふ|かお|おも|ほころ"},{"japanese":"着古したシャツの袖口が綻んでいる。","romaji":"Kifurushita shatsu no sodeguchi ga hokoronde iru.","german":"The cuff of the worn-out shirt has come unstitched.","furigana":"きふる|そでぐち|ほころ"}],"notes":"綻ぶ describes how something closed opens slightly: buds \"open\" (蕾が綻ぶ, a typical spring image), a face \"brightens into a smile\" (顔が綻ぶ, 口元が綻ぶ) and seams \"come undone\" (袖口が綻ぶ). Intransitive; transitively one says 顔を綻ばせる. There also exists the equivalent ichidan form 綻びる; the noun 綻び also stands for weak points in plans or systems. Mostly written in kana (ほころぶ).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"幸運にも彼は災難を免れた。","romaji":"Kōun ni mo kare wa sainan o manukareta.","german":"Luckily, he escaped the disaster.","furigana":"こううん|かれ|さいなん|まぬか"},{"japanese":"銀行の支援により、会社はかろうじて倒産を免れた。","romaji":"Ginkō no shien ni yori, kaisha wa karōjite tōsan o manukareta.","german":"With the bank's support, the company narrowly avoided bankruptcy.","furigana":"ぎんこう|しえん|かいしゃ|とうさん|まぬか"},{"japanese":"今回の判断ミスについて、監督の責任は免れないだろう。","romaji":"Konkai no handan misu ni tsuite, kantoku no sekinin wa manukarenai darō.","german":"The manager will probably not escape responsibility for this misjudgment.","furigana":"こんかい|はんだん|かんとく|せきにん|まぬか"}],"notes":"免れる means \"to escape a danger or burden\": 災難を免れる, 倒産を免れる, 責任を免れる. Very common is the negated form 〜を免れない (\"… is unavoidable\"): 批判は免れない, 〜の謗りを免れない. The traditional reading is まぬかれる; in present-day Japanese まぬがれる is at least as widespread. The object takes を. Literary; in everyday speech people say 助かる or 避けられる.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"身分を偽って入場した。","romaji":"Mibun o itsuwatte nyūjō shita.","german":"He entered by falsifying his identity.","furigana":"みぶん|いつわ|にゅうじょう"},{"japanese":"自分を偽って生きるのは、もうやめたい。","romaji":"Jibun o itsuwatte ikiru no wa, mō yametai.","german":"I want to stop living by deceiving myself.","furigana":"じぶん|いつわ|い"},{"japanese":"彼女は偽らざる気持ちを手紙に綴った。","romaji":"Kanojo wa itsuwarazaru kimochi o tegami ni tsuzutta.","german":"She put her honest feelings into a letter.","furigana":"かのじょ|いつわ|きも|てがみ|"}],"notes":"偽る means \"to give out something false, to falsify\" and is transitive with を: 身分を偽る, 年齢を偽る, 経歴を偽る, 自分を偽る (\"to put on a pretense\"). The fixed phrase 偽らざる + noun (偽らざる気持ち, 偽らざる本音) means \"sincere, unvarnished\". Compared with 嘘をつく (\"to lie\", general), 偽る stresses the falsifying of a specific fact; 騙す is \"to deceive someone\", 偽造する \"to forge documents\". Neutral to literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は不幸な人々を憐れんだ。","romaji":"Kanojo wa fukō na hitobito o awarenda.","german":"She pitied the unfortunate people.","furigana":"かのじょ|ふこう|ひとびと|あわ"},{"japanese":"人に憐れまれるくらいなら、一人で頑張りたい。","romaji":"Hito ni awaremareru kurai nara, hitori de ganbaritai.","german":"If people are going to pity me, I'd rather struggle on alone.","furigana":"ひと|あわ|ひとり|がんば"},{"japanese":"彼は自分の境遇を憐れむばかりで、何もしようとしない。","romaji":"Kare wa jibun no kyōgū o awaremu bakari de, nani mo shiyō to shinai.","german":"He just feels sorry for his own circumstances and does nothing.","furigana":"かれ|じぶん|きょうぐう|あわ|なに"}],"notes":"憐れむ means \"to pity, to take pity\" and is transitive with を: 不幸な人を憐れむ, 自分を憐れむ (\"to feel sorry for oneself\"). Often a gradient is implied -- one looks down on someone weaker -- which is why 憐れむような目で見られる (\"being looked at with pitying eyes\") is felt as insulting. More neutral is 同情する (\"to sympathize\"). Also written 哀れむ; the noun is 憐れみ (\"pity, mercy\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"部下が上司の暴走を諌めた。","romaji":"Buka ga jōshi no bōsō o isameta.","german":"The subordinate remonstrated with the boss over his reckless behavior.","furigana":"ぶか|じょうし|ぼうそう|いさ"},{"japanese":"家臣は命がけで主君を諌めた。","romaji":"Kashin wa inochigake de shukun o isameta.","german":"The retainer remonstrated with his lord at the risk of his life.","furigana":"かしん|いのち|しゅくん|いさ"},{"japanese":"父の飲み過ぎを、娘がやんわりと諌めた。","romaji":"Chichi no nomisugi o, musume ga yanwari to isameta.","german":"The daughter gently admonished her father for drinking too much.","furigana":"ちち|の|す|むすめ|いさ"}],"notes":"諌める means to point out someone's faults and urge them to turn back -- traditionally to a superior such as a lord, boss or parents: 主君を諌める, 上司の暴走を諌める. The person takes を, and the misbehavior also takes を (〜の〜を諌める). Compared with 注意する or たしなめる (\"to reprimand\", more toward equals or inferiors), 諌める stresses the respectful, often courageous intervention from below; 戒める means generally \"to warn, to admonish\", also oneself. The correct spelling is actually 諫める.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"失礼な発言を深く詫びた。","romaji":"Shitsurei na hatsugen o fukaku wabita.","german":"I deeply apologized for my rude remark.","furigana":"しつれい|はつげん|ふか|わ"},{"japanese":"約束の時間に遅れたことを、電話で先方に詫びた。","romaji":"Yakusoku no jikan ni okureta koto o, denwa de senpō ni wabita.","german":"I apologized by phone to the other party for being late for the appointment.","furigana":"やくそく|じかん|おく|でんわ|せんぽう|わ"},{"japanese":"彼は迷惑をかけた近所の人々に、一軒ずつ詫びて回った。","romaji":"Kare wa meiwaku o kaketa kinjo no hitobito ni, ikken-zutsu wabite mawatta.","german":"He went around apologizing, house by house, to the neighbors he had troubled.","furigana":"かれ|めいわく|きんじょ|ひとびと|いっけん|わ|まわ"}],"notes":"詫びる means \"to apologize (formally)\". The reason is marked with を (遅刻を詫びる, 非礼を詫びる), the person with に (先方に詫びる). It is more formal and more literary than 謝る; especially common is the noun in お詫びする, お詫び申し上げます and 詫びを入れる (\"to formally ask for forgiveness\"). Typical of business letters, official statements and novels.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"若い頃の過ちを今でも悔いている。","romaji":"Wakai koro no ayamachi o ima demo kuite iru.","german":"I still regret the mistakes of my youth.","furigana":"わか|ころ|あやま|いま|く"},{"japanese":"被告は法廷で、自らの罪を深く悔いていると述べた。","romaji":"Hikoku wa hōtei de, mizukara no tsumi o fukaku kuite iru to nobeta.","german":"The defendant stated in court that he deeply regretted his crime.","furigana":"ひこく|ほうてい|みずか|つみ|ふか|く|の"},{"japanese":"罪を悔い改める者は救われる、と牧師は語った。","romaji":"Tsumi o kuiaratameru mono wa sukuwareru, to bokushi wa katatta.","german":"\"Those who repent of their sins will be saved,\" the pastor said.","furigana":"つみ|く|あらた|もの|すく|ぼくし|かた"}],"notes":"悔いる means \"to regret\" in a serious, moral sense: 過ちを悔いる, 罪を悔いる. It is more literary and weightier than 後悔する, which also fits everyday trifles. Common related words are the noun 悔い (悔いが残る \"regret remains\", 悔いのない人生 \"a life without regret\") and the compound 悔い改める (\"to repent and mend one's ways\", also in a religious sense). Transitive with を.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不摂生が祟って病気になった。","romaji":"Fusessei ga tatatte byōki ni natta.","german":"His unhealthy lifestyle took its toll and he fell ill.","furigana":"ふせっせい|たた|びょうき"},{"japanese":"この祠を壊すと祟られると、村では信じられている。","romaji":"Kono hokora o kowasu to tatarareru to, mura de wa shinjirarete iru.","german":"In the village it is believed that anyone who destroys this shrine will be cursed.","furigana":"|こわ|たた|むら|しん"},{"japanese":"序盤のミスが祟って、チームは試合に敗れた。","romaji":"Joban no misu ga tatatte, chīmu wa shiai ni yabureta.","german":"The early mistakes came back to haunt them, and the team lost the match.","furigana":"じょばん|たた|しあい|やぶ"}],"notes":"祟る originally means that gods, spirits or the dead bring misfortune upon people (祟り \"curse, punishment of the spirits\"; passive 祟られる). In everyday speech the figurative use is much more common: something done earlier \"comes back to bite\" and leads to bad consequences, in the pattern 〜が祟って (無理が祟って, 寝不足が祟って, 不摂生が祟って). Intransitive. Similar but more neutral is 〜のせいで.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"酔っ払いが通りで喚いている。","romaji":"Yopparai ga tōri de wameite iru.","german":"A drunk is shouting in the street.","furigana":"よ|ぱら|とお|わめ"},{"japanese":"子供が床に寝転がって泣き喚いている。","romaji":"Kodomo ga yuka ni nekorogatte nakiwameite iru.","german":"The child is lying on the floor, crying and wailing.","furigana":"こども|ゆか|ねころ|な|わめ"},{"japanese":"彼は思い通りにならないと、すぐに喚き散らす。","romaji":"Kare wa omoidōri ni naranai to, sugu ni wamekichirasu.","german":"When things do not go his way, he immediately starts yelling wildly.","furigana":"かれ|おも|どお|わめ|ち"}],"notes":"喚く means \"to scream or shout loudly and without restraint\", often in a way others find annoying. Typical forms are 大声で喚く, 泣き喚く (\"to cry and wail\") and 喚き散らす (\"to rant and rave\"). Compared with 叫ぶ (\"to cry out, shout\", neutral) and 怒鳴る (\"to shout at someone\"), 喚く stresses chaotic, uncontrolled noise. Intransitive, pejorative, usually written in kana (わめく).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼を辞職するように仕向けた。","romaji":"Kare o jishoku suru yō ni shimuketa.","german":"I maneuvered him into resigning.","furigana":"かれ|じしょく|しむ"},{"japanese":"子供が自分から勉強するように仕向けるのは難しい。","romaji":"Kodomo ga jibun kara benkyō suru yō ni shimukeru no wa muzukashii.","german":"It is hard to get children to study of their own accord.","furigana":"こども|じぶん|べんきょう|しむ|むずか"},{"japanese":"犯人は、警察が別の人物を疑うように仕向けた。","romaji":"Hannin wa, keisatsu ga betsu no jinbutsu o utagau yō ni shimuketa.","german":"The culprit steered things so that the police suspected someone else.","furigana":"はんにん|けいさつ|べつ|じんぶつ|うたが|しむ"}],"notes":"仕向ける means \"to (cleverly) get someone to do something\", mostly in the pattern 〜を／〜に…ように仕向ける. It often carries a sense of steering or manipulation: you do not give a direct order but create circumstances in which the other person seems to act on their own. Compared with the direct causative (させる) and 促す (\"to prompt, urge\"), it is more indirect and can sound negative. In trade, 仕向け also means \"shipment\" (仕向け地 \"destination\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"周囲が彼の怒りを焚きつけた。","romaji":"Shūi ga kare no ikari o takitsuketa.","german":"Those around him fanned his anger.","furigana":"しゅうい|かれ|いか|た"},{"japanese":"丸めた新聞紙で薪ストーブを焚きつけた。","romaji":"Marumeta shinbunshi de makisutōbu o takitsuketa.","german":"I lit the wood stove with rolled-up newspaper.","furigana":"まる|しんぶんし|まき|た"},{"japanese":"彼は友人に焚きつけられて、社長に直談判しに行った。","romaji":"Kare wa yūjin ni takitsukerarete, shachō ni jikidanpan shi ni itta.","german":"Egged on by a friend, he went to confront the company president directly.","furigana":"かれ|ゆうじん|た|しゃちょう|じきだんぱん|い"}],"notes":"焚きつける literally means \"to light or kindle a fire\" (ストーブを焚きつける; the noun 焚きつけ is \"kindling\"). Figuratively it means \"to egg someone on, to incite\" so that they do something: 〜を焚きつけて…させる, often in the passive 〜に焚きつけられる. Similar are 煽る (\"to fan, stir up\", also for moods and media) and けしかける (\"to set on someone\"). Also written 焚き付ける; neutral to slightly colloquial.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"敵が先に戦いを仕掛けてきた。","romaji":"Teki ga saki ni tatakai o shikakete kita.","german":"The enemy started the fight first.","furigana":"てき|さき|たたか|しか"},{"japanese":"台所にネズミ捕りを仕掛けた。","romaji":"Daidokoro ni nezumitori o shikaketa.","german":"I set a mousetrap in the kitchen.","furigana":"だいどころ|と|しか"},{"japanese":"駅に爆弾が仕掛けられているという通報があった。","romaji":"Eki ni bakudan ga shikakerarete iru to iu tsūhō ga atta.","german":"There was a report that a bomb had been planted at the station.","furigana":"えき|ばくだん|しか|つうほう"}],"notes":"仕掛ける is transitive and has two main uses: to \"start\" a conflict (戦い・喧嘩・攻撃を仕掛ける), often with 〜てくる when the opponent attacks on their own initiative, and to secretly \"set up, plant\" something (罠・爆弾・隠しカメラを仕掛ける). Unlike neutral 始める, it always implies intent or cunning. The noun 仕掛け means \"mechanism, trick, device\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は親の期待に背いた。","romaji":"Kare wa oya no kitai ni somuita.","german":"He went against his parents' expectations.","furigana":"かれ|おや|きたい|そむ"},{"japanese":"兵士は上官の命令に背いて、仲間を助けに戻った。","romaji":"Heishi wa jōkan no meirei ni somuite, nakama o tasuke ni modotta.","german":"The soldier disobeyed his superior's order and went back to help his comrades.","furigana":"へいし|じょうかん|めいれい|そむ|なかま|たす|もど"},{"japanese":"法に背く行為は、どんな理由があっても許されない。","romaji":"Hō ni somuku kōi wa, donna riyū ga atte mo yurusarenai.","german":"Acts that violate the law cannot be forgiven, whatever the reason.","furigana":"ほう|そむ|こうい|りゆう|ゆる"}],"notes":"背く is intransitive and takes に: 命令に背く \"to disobey an order\", 法に背く \"to violate the law\", 期待に背く \"to disappoint expectations\". Compared with 逆らう (\"to defy\", more toward people and forces), 背く stresses the breaking of a duty or loyalty and sounds more literary; 反する is the plain \"to be contrary to\". The transitive counterpart 背ける means \"to turn away\" (顔を背ける).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"経費を誤魔化して私的に使った。","romaji":"Keihi o gomakashite shiteki ni tsukatta.","german":"He fudged the expense records and used the money privately.","furigana":"けいひ|ごまか|してき|つか"},{"japanese":"都合の悪い質問をされると、彼はいつも笑ってごまかす。","romaji":"Tsugō no warui shitsumon o sareru to, kare wa itsumo waratte gomakasu.","german":"When asked an awkward question, he always laughs it off.","furigana":"つごう|わる|しつもん|かれ|わら"},{"japanese":"年齢をごまかしてアルバイトに応募した。","romaji":"Nenrei o gomakashite arubaito ni ōbo shita.","german":"I lied about my age and applied for a part-time job.","furigana":"ねんれい|おうぼ"}],"notes":"ごまかす is almost always written in kana; 誤魔化す is an ateji spelling. It means \"to fudge, cheat\" with figures and information (年齢・数字・経費をごまかす) and \"to gloss over, wriggle out\" with questions or mistakes (話をごまかす, 笑ってごまかす). Unlike 騙す, which tricks a person directly, ごまかす is mostly about obscuring facts. The noun ごまかし means \"swindle, evasion\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"失敗を取り繕おうとしたが無駄だった。","romaji":"Shippai o toritsukuroō to shita ga muda datta.","german":"I tried to cover up the failure, but it was useless.","furigana":"しっぱい|と|つくろ|むだ"},{"japanese":"彼女は慌てて笑顔を作り、その場を取り繕った。","romaji":"Kanojo wa awatete egao o tsukuri, sono ba o toritsukurotta.","german":"She hurriedly put on a smile and smoothed over the moment.","furigana":"かのじょ|あわ|えがお|つく|ば|と|つくろ"},{"japanese":"あの会社は体裁を取り繕うことばかり考えている。","romaji":"Ano kaisha wa teisai o toritsukurou koto bakari kangaete iru.","german":"That company thinks only of keeping up appearances.","furigana":"かいしゃ|ていさい|と|つくろ|かんが"}],"notes":"取り繕う means to cover up a mistake or an embarrassing situation in a makeshift way so that everything seems fine on the outside. Typical combinations are その場を取り繕う \"to somehow smooth over the situation\", 体裁を取り繕う \"to keep up appearances\" and 失敗を取り繕う. The original meaning \"to mend, patch\" is rare today. Transitive with を; unlike 言い繕う, the covering up is done not only with words but also through behavior.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"危機一髪で災害から逃れた。","romaji":"Kiki ippatsu de saigai kara nogareta.","german":"I narrowly escaped the disaster.","furigana":"ききいっぱつ|さいがい|のが"},{"japanese":"彼は責任を逃れようと、言い訳ばかりしている。","romaji":"Kare wa sekinin o nogareyō to, iiwake bakari shite iru.","german":"He keeps making excuses in an attempt to evade responsibility.","furigana":"かれ|せきにん|のが|い|わけ"},{"japanese":"都会の喧騒を逃れて、山あいの村に移り住んだ。","romaji":"Tokai no kensō o nogarete, yamaai no mura ni utsurisunda.","german":"Escaping the bustle of the city, I moved to a village in the mountains.","furigana":"とかい||のが|やま|むら|うつ|す"}],"notes":"逃れる is intransitive and takes から or を: 危険から逃れる, 責任を逃れる. It is more formal and abstract than 逃げる, which describes physical flight; typical objects are responsibility, punishment, pursuit or fate. Related are 言い逃れ \"excuse\" and 免れる (\"to be spared\"). Transitive relatives are 逃す/逃がす \"to miss; to let escape\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この問題を見過ごすわけにはいかない。","romaji":"Kono mondai o misugosu wake ni wa ikanai.","german":"We cannot just let this problem pass.","furigana":"もんだい|みす"},{"japanese":"校正のとき、小さな誤字を見過ごしてしまった。","romaji":"Kōsei no toki, chiisana goji o misugoshite shimatta.","german":"While proofreading, I overlooked a small typo.","furigana":"こうせい|ちい|ごじ|みす"},{"japanese":"目の前のチャンスをみすみす見過ごすなんてもったいない。","romaji":"Me no mae no chansu o misumisu misugosu nante mottainai.","german":"It would be a waste to let the chance in front of you slip by like that.","furigana":"め|まえ|みす"}],"notes":"見過ごす is transitive and has two nuances: to \"overlook\" something unintentionally (like 見落とす) and to knowingly \"let it pass, stand by and do nothing\" (like 見逃す). The second nuance often appears in the negative: 見過ごすわけにはいかない \"we cannot just look the other way\". With みすみす you stress that one lets an opportunity slip by with open eyes.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"詐欺師が老人を誑かして金を奪った。","romaji":"Sagishi ga rōjin o taburakashite kane o ubatta.","german":"A swindler deceived an old man and took his money.","furigana":"さぎし|ろうじん|たぶら|かね|うば"},{"japanese":"昔話では、狐がよく人を誑かす。","romaji":"Mukashibanashi de wa, kitsune ga yoku hito o taburakasu.","german":"In folktales, foxes often bewitch people.","furigana":"むかしばなし|きつね|ひと|たぶら"},{"japanese":"甘い言葉に誑かされて、彼女は貯金を全部渡してしまった。","romaji":"Amai kotoba ni taburakasarete, kanojo wa chokin o zenbu watashite shimatta.","german":"Taken in by sweet words, she handed over all her savings.","furigana":"あま|ことば|たぶら|かのじょ|ちょきん|ぜんぶわた"}],"notes":"たぶらかす is mostly written in kana and sounds archaic to literary. It means \"to bewitch, lead astray\", often with fine words or seduction, and is typical of foxes and tanuki in folktales (狐に誑かされる). Neutral 騙す is the everyday word for \"to deceive\". It often appears in the passive: 甘い言葉に誑かされる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"犬を嗾けて不審者を追い払った。","romaji":"Inu o keshikakete fushinsha o oiharatta.","german":"I set the dog on the suspicious person and drove him away.","furigana":"いぬ|けしか|ふしんしゃ|お|はら"},{"japanese":"友達に嗾けられて、思い切って彼女に告白した。","romaji":"Tomodachi ni keshikakerarete, omoikitte kanojo ni kokuhaku shita.","german":"Egged on by my friends, I plucked up the courage to confess to her.","furigana":"ともだち|けしか|おも|き|かのじょ|こくはく"},{"japanese":"彼は裏で仲間を嗾けて、ライバルの悪い噂を流させた。","romaji":"Kare wa ura de nakama o keshikakete, raibaru no warui uwasa o nagasaseta.","german":"Behind the scenes he incited his associates to spread bad rumors about his rival.","furigana":"かれ|うら|なかま|けしか|わる|うわさ|なが"}],"notes":"けしかける is mostly written in kana. It means to \"set\" a dog on someone (犬を嗾ける) and, figuratively, to \"egg someone on\" to do something: 人を嗾けて〜させる. The passive is very common: 友達に嗾けられて. Related are 唆す (\"to tempt into something bad\", rather covert) and 煽る (\"to fan\", often a crowd or mood).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"馬が大きな声で嘶いた。","romaji":"Uma ga ōkina koe de inanaita.","german":"The horse neighed loudly.","furigana":"うま|おお|こえ|いなな"},{"japanese":"遠くの牧場から、馬の嘶く声が聞こえてきた。","romaji":"Tōku no bokujō kara, uma no inanaku koe ga kikoete kita.","german":"From a distant ranch came the sound of a horse neighing.","furigana":"とお|ぼくじょう|うま|いなな|こえ|き"},{"japanese":"騎手が手綱を強く引くと、馬は高く嘶いて前脚を上げた。","romaji":"Kishu ga tazuna o tsuyoku hiku to, uma wa takaku inanaite maeashi o ageta.","german":"When the jockey pulled hard on the reins, the horse neighed high and reared up.","furigana":"きしゅ||つよ|ひ|うま|たか|いなな|まえあし|あ"}],"notes":"嘶く is intransitive and used practically only for horses (occasionally donkeys); the subject takes が or は. The noun is 嘶き \"neigh\", the onomatopoeia is ヒヒーン. Other animal sounds have their own verbs: dogs 吠える, birds and insects generally 鳴く. The word belongs to written, narrative language; the kanji is rare, so it is also found in kana as いななく.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"チョコレートが口の中で蕩ける。","romaji":"Chokorēto ga kuchi no naka de torokeru.","german":"The chocolate melts in your mouth.","furigana":"くち|なか|とろ"},{"japanese":"オーブンで焼いたら、チーズがいい具合に蕩けた。","romaji":"Ōbun de yaitara, chīzu ga ii guai ni toroketa.","german":"When I baked it in the oven, the cheese melted nicely.","furigana":"や|ぐあい|とろ"},{"japanese":"甘い声でささやかれて、心が蕩けそうになった。","romaji":"Amai koe de sasayakarete, kokoro ga torokesō ni natta.","german":"Whispered to in a sweet voice, her heart almost melted.","furigana":"あま|こえ|こころ|とろ"}],"notes":"とろける is mostly written in kana. Unlike the general 溶ける (\"to melt, dissolve\"), とろける describes a soft, creamy melting and sounds pleasant, which is why it is popular in advertising: 口の中でとろける, とろけるチーズ. Figuratively it means \"to melt, be enraptured\": 心がとろける, とろけるような笑顔. The transitive counterpart 蕩かす is rare.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"耳を劈くような悲鳴が響いた。","romaji":"Mimi o tsunzaku yō na himei ga hibiita.","german":"An ear-piercing scream rang out.","furigana":"みみ|つんざ|ひめい|ひび"},{"japanese":"突然、雷鳴が夜の静けさを劈いた。","romaji":"Totsuzen, raimei ga yoru no shizukesa o tsunzaita.","german":"Suddenly a thunderclap tore through the stillness of the night.","furigana":"とつぜん|らいめい|よる|しず|つんざ"},{"japanese":"選手たちが登場すると、耳を劈くような歓声が上がった。","romaji":"Senshutachi ga tōjō suru to, mimi o tsunzaku yō na kansei ga agatta.","german":"When the players appeared, deafening cheers rose up.","furigana":"せんしゅ|とうじょう|みみ|つんざ|かんせい|あ"}],"notes":"つんざく is mostly written in kana and is a literary, transitive verb. It appears almost always in the set phrase 耳をつんざく(ような) \"deafening\", for screams, explosions or cheers. Besides that, a sound or flash \"tears through\" silence, air or darkness: 静寂・空気・闇を劈く. In everyday speech one says すごい音 or 耳が痛いほどの音 instead.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の言葉が心を抉った。","romaji":"Kare no kotoba ga kokoro o egutta.","german":"His words cut deep into my heart.","furigana":"かれ|ことば|こころ|えぐ"},{"japanese":"ナイフでジャガイモの芽を抉り取った。","romaji":"Naifu de jagaimo no me o eguritotta.","german":"I gouged the sprouts out of the potato with a knife.","furigana":"め|えぐ|と"},{"japanese":"この映画は現代社会の闇を鋭く抉っている。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa gendai shakai no yami o surudoku egutte iru.","german":"This film sharply exposes the darkness of modern society.","furigana":"えいが|げんだいしゃかい|やみ|するど|えぐ"}],"notes":"えぐる is mostly written in kana and is transitive. Literally it means \"to gouge, hollow out\" (potato eyes, a hole in the ground), often as 抉り取る. Figuratively it can \"wound deeply\" emotionally (心を抉る) or, in journalism and criticism, \"ruthlessly expose\" a problem (社会の闇・問題の核心を抉る). Not to be confused with the colloquial adjective えぐい.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"濡れたタオルを捩って水を絞った。","romaji":"Nureta taoru o nejitte mizu o shibotta.","german":"I wrung out the wet towel.","furigana":"ぬ|ねじ|みず|しぼ"},{"japanese":"固い瓶の蓋を力いっぱい捩って開けた。","romaji":"Katai bin no futa o chikara ippai nejitte aketa.","german":"I twisted off the stuck jar lid with all my strength.","furigana":"かた|びん|ふた|ちから|ねじ|あ"},{"japanese":"ストレッチで、上半身をゆっくり左右に捩る。","romaji":"Sutoretchi de, jōhanshin o yukkuri sayū ni nejiru.","german":"When stretching, you slowly twist your upper body to the left and right.","furigana":"じょうはんしん|さゆう|ねじ"}],"notes":"ねじる is mostly written in kana or as 捻じる; the kanji 捩る can also be read よじる (\"to writhe\") or もじる (\"to parody\"). It is transitive and means to \"turn, twist\" something around its own axis: 蓋をねじる, 体をねじる, タオルをねじって絞る. Similar is ひねる, which means more a short, small twist (蛇口をひねる). The intransitive counterpart is ねじれる \"to get twisted\"; related is ねじ \"screw\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"壁に凭れて休んだ。","romaji":"Kabe ni motarete yasunda.","german":"He leaned against the wall and rested.","furigana":"かべ|もた|やす"},{"japanese":"ゆうべ揚げ物を食べすぎて、胃がもたれている。","romaji":"Yuube agemono o tabesugite, i ga motarete iru.","german":"I ate too much fried food last night, and now my stomach feels heavy.","furigana":"あ|もの|た|い"},{"japanese":"電車で隣の人が眠ってしまい、私の肩にもたれてきた。","romaji":"Densha de tonari no hito ga nemutte shimai, watashi no kata ni motarete kita.","german":"On the train, the person next to me fell asleep and leaned on my shoulder.","furigana":"でんしゃ|となり|ひと|ねむ|わたし|かた"}],"notes":"もたれる has two meanings: to lean one's body against something (壁にもたれる, いすの背にもたれる) and, relating to the stomach, \"to sit heavily in the stomach\" (胃がもたれる, whence the noun 胃もたれ). The kanji 凭れる is rare; it is almost always written もたれる. Close in meaning is 寄りかかる, which stresses putting one's weight on something more and can also mean \"to depend on someone\" figuratively. Intransitive; the support is marked with に.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"上司に阿るような態度は好まれない。","romaji":"Jōshi ni omoneru yō na taido wa konomarenai.","german":"An attitude of sucking up to one's boss is not liked.","furigana":"じょうし|おもね|たいど|この"},{"japanese":"権力に阿る新聞は信用できない。","romaji":"Kenryoku ni omoneru shinbun wa shin'yō dekinai.","german":"A newspaper that fawns on those in power cannot be trusted.","furigana":"けんりょく|おもね|しんぶん|しんよう"},{"japanese":"彼は流行に阿ることなく、自分の音楽を貫いた。","romaji":"Kare wa ryūkō ni omoneru koto naku, jibun no ongaku o tsuranuita.","german":"He stayed true to his own music without pandering to trends.","furigana":"かれ|りゅうこう|おもね|じぶん|おんがく|つらぬ"}],"notes":"おもねる is intransitive, takes に and belongs to written, critical language: 権力に阿る \"to toady to those in power\", 大衆に阿る \"to tell the public what it wants to hear\". In everyday speech one says 媚びる, ご機嫌を取る or へつらう instead. It often appears negated as praise: 流行に阿らない \"not to pander, to stay unwavering\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"恐怖で足が竦んで動けなかった。","romaji":"Kyōfu de ashi ga sukunde ugokenakatta.","german":"My legs froze with fear and I could not move.","furigana":"きょうふ|あし|すく|うご"},{"japanese":"突然の大きな音に、彼女はその場に立ち竦んだ。","romaji":"Totsuzen no ōkina oto ni, kanojo wa sono ba ni tachisukunda.","german":"At the sudden loud noise, she stood rooted to the spot.","furigana":"とつぜん|おお|おと|かのじょ|ば|た|すく"},{"japanese":"面接官に鋭い目で見つめられ、身の竦む思いがした。","romaji":"Mensetsukan ni surudoi me de mitsumerare, mi no sukumu omoi ga shita.","german":"Stared at sharply by the interviewer, I felt myself shrink.","furigana":"めんせつかん|するど|め|み|み|すく|おも"}],"notes":"すくむ is intransitive and describes freezing up from fear, shock or awe. Typical phrases are 足が竦む (\"to get weak knees, be unable to go on\"), 身が竦む (\"to be as if paralyzed\") and the compound 立ち竦む \"to stand rooted to the spot\". The transitive counterpart 竦める appears mainly in 首・肩を竦める \"to pull in one's head, shrug one's shoulders\". Usually written in kana.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お客様の前で畏まって座った。","romaji":"Okyakusama no mae de kashikomatte suwatta.","german":"I sat formally in front of the customer.","furigana":"きゃくさま|まえ|かしこ|すわ"},{"japanese":"「会議室を予約しておいてください。」「かしこまりました。」","romaji":"\"Kaigishitsu o yoyaku shite oite kudasai.\" \"Kashikomarimashita.\"","german":"\"Please reserve the meeting room.\" \"Certainly.\"","furigana":"かいぎしつ|よやく"},{"japanese":"友達同士なんだから、そんなに畏まらなくていいよ。","romaji":"Tomodachi dōshi nan da kara, sonna ni kashikomaranakute ii yo.","german":"We are friends, so you do not need to be so formal.","furigana":"ともだちどうし|かしこ"}],"notes":"かしこまる is mostly written in kana. It is most often met as かしこまりました, the humble polite formula \"certainly, understood\" in customer service and toward superiors (more polite than 分かりました). Otherwise it describes stiff, formal-respectful behavior or sitting: 畏まって座る, 畏まった席 \"a formal occasion\", そんなに畏まらないで \"don't be so formal\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"恐ろしい光景に身が慄いた。","romaji":"Osoroshii kōkei ni mi ga ononoita.","german":"I shuddered at the horrifying sight.","furigana":"おそ|こうけい|み|おのの"},{"japanese":"犯人からの脅迫電話に、家族は恐怖に慄いた。","romaji":"Hannin kara no kyōhaku denwa ni, kazoku wa kyōfu ni ononoita.","german":"The family trembled with fear at the threatening phone call from the culprit.","furigana":"はんにん|きょうはくでんわ|かぞく|きょうふ|おのの"},{"japanese":"合格発表の前夜、彼は不安に慄いて眠れなかった。","romaji":"Gōkaku happyō no zen'ya, kare wa fuan ni ononoite nemurenakatta.","german":"The night before the exam results, he trembled with anxiety and could not sleep.","furigana":"ごうかくはっぴょう|ぜんや|かれ|ふあん|おのの|ねむ"}],"notes":"おののく is intransitive and literary; it means \"to tremble, quake\" with fear, excitement or cold and typically appears as 恐怖に慄く or 不安に慄く. The kanji 慄く and 戦く are equivalent spellings of the same word, but it is often written in kana. The 慄 also appears in 戦慄 \"shudder, horror\". Neutral 震える is the everyday word; おののく stresses inner shock.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子供たちが公園で燥いでいる。","romaji":"Kodomotachi ga kōen de hashaide iru.","german":"The children are romping in the park.","furigana":"こども|こうえん|はしゃ"},{"japanese":"初めての海外旅行に、母は子供のように燥いでいた。","romaji":"Hajimete no kaigai ryokō ni, haha wa kodomo no yō ni hashaide ita.","german":"On her first trip abroad, my mother was as excited as a child.","furigana":"はじ|かいがいりょこう|はは|こども|はしゃ"},{"japanese":"昼間に燥ぎすぎて、帰りの車ではみんな寝てしまった。","romaji":"Hiruma ni hashagisugite, kaeri no kuruma de wa minna nete shimatta.","german":"They played around too much during the day, and everyone fell asleep in the car on the way back.","furigana":"ひるま|はしゃ|かえ|くるま|ね"}],"notes":"はしゃぐ is almost always written in kana; 燥ぐ is rare. It is intransitive and describes exuberant, loud joy, typically in children; for adults often with 子供のように. Common is はしゃぎすぎる \"to overdo the frolicking\". 騒ぐ stresses the noise, 浮かれる the elevated, slightly frivolous mood.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"寒さと恐怖で全身が戦いた。","romaji":"Samusa to kyōfu de zenshin ga ononoita.","german":"My whole body trembled from the cold and fear.","furigana":"さむ|きょうふ|ぜんしん|おのの"},{"japanese":"敵の大軍を目の前にして、兵士たちは戦いた。","romaji":"Teki no taigun o me no mae ni shite, heishitachi wa ononoita.","german":"Facing the enemy's huge army, the soldiers trembled.","furigana":"てき|たいぐん|め|まえ|へいし|おのの"},{"japanese":"激しい揺れに、人々は恐れ戦いた。","romaji":"Hageshii yure ni, hitobito wa osore ononoita.","german":"People trembled in fear at the violent shaking.","furigana":"はげ|ゆ|ひとびと|おそ|おのの"}],"notes":"戦く is the same word as 慄く: intransitive, literary, \"to tremble, quake\" with fear or excitement. The compound 恐れ戦く \"to tremble in fear\" is very common. Because 戦 is otherwise read たたかう or いくさ (戦いた could also be misread as たたかった), the word is often written in kana. The everyday word is 震える.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新人が出過ぎた発言をして先輩に注意された。","romaji":"Shinjin ga desugita hatsugen o shite senpai ni chūi sareta.","german":"The newcomer made an overstepping remark and was warned by a senior colleague.","furigana":"しんじん|です|はつげん|せんぱい|ちゅうい"},{"japanese":"出過ぎた真似をして、申し訳ありませんでした。","romaji":"Desugita mane o shite, mōshiwake arimasen deshita.","german":"I apologize for overstepping.","furigana":"です|まね|もう|わけ"},{"japanese":"お茶を淹れたまま放っておいたら、出過ぎて苦くなった。","romaji":"Ocha o ireta mama hōtte oitara, desugite nigaku natta.","german":"I left the tea steeping and it brewed too long and became bitter.","furigana":"ちゃ||ほう|です|にが"}],"notes":"出過ぎる is intransitive. Figuratively it means \"to take too much upon oneself, be forward\", above all in the set phrases 出過ぎた真似をする and 出過ぎたことを言う, often in apologies to superiors. More colloquial is 出しゃばる. Literally it means \"to stick out too far\" or, with tea, \"to steep too long, get too strong\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"健康のために飲酒を控えている。","romaji":"Kenkō no tame ni inshu o hikaete iru.","german":"I am cutting back on alcohol for my health.","furigana":"けんこう|いんしゅ|ひか"},{"japanese":"念のため、予約番号を控えておいてください。","romaji":"Nen no tame, yoyaku bangō o hikaete oite kudasai.","german":"Just in case, please make a note of your reservation number.","furigana":"ねん|よやくばんごう|ひか"},{"japanese":"大事な試験を明日に控えて、彼は朝から落ち着かない。","romaji":"Daiji na shiken o ashita ni hikaete, kare wa asa kara ochitsukanai.","german":"With an important exam tomorrow, he has been restless since morning.","furigana":"だいじ|しけん|あした|ひか|かれ|あさ|お|つ"}],"notes":"控える is mostly transitive: 〜を控える means \"to refrain from, abstain from something\" (飲酒・外出・発言を控える); as a polite prohibition you often read 撮影はお控えください. Second, it means \"to note down\" (番号を控える). Third, 〜を…に控える expresses an \"upcoming event\": 試験を明日に控える \"to have the exam tomorrow ahead of one\". Related are 控えめ \"restrained, modest\" and 控え室 \"waiting room\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"家臣が主君に叛いた。","romaji":"Kashin ga shukun ni somuita.","german":"The vassal rebelled against his lord.","furigana":"かしん|しゅくん|そむ"},{"japanese":"軍の一部が政府に叛き、首都を占拠した。","romaji":"Gun no ichibu ga seifu ni somuki, shuto o senkyo shita.","german":"Part of the army rose against the government and occupied the capital.","furigana":"ぐん|いちぶ|せいふ|そむ|しゅと|せんきょ"},{"japanese":"彼は祖国に叛いた裏切り者と呼ばれた。","romaji":"Kare wa sokoku ni somuita uragirimono to yobareta.","german":"He was called a traitor who had turned against his homeland.","furigana":"かれ|そこく|そむ|うらぎ|もの|よ"}],"notes":"叛く is a special spelling of 背く with the same meaning and grammar (〜に叛く). The kanji 叛 (\"rebellion\", as in 謀叛 and 叛乱) stresses open revolt against a ruler, a government or the fatherland. In modern texts 背く is the standard spelling; 叛く appears mainly in historical and literary texts.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"無礼な態度を取る後輩を嗜めた。","romaji":"Burei na taido o toru kōhai o tashinameta.","german":"I admonished a junior colleague who was behaving rudely.","furigana":"ぶれい|たいど|と|こうはい|たしな"},{"japanese":"電車で騒ぐ子供を、母親が小声で嗜めた。","romaji":"Densha de sawagu kodomo o, hahaoya ga kogoe de tashinameta.","german":"A mother quietly scolded her child for making noise on the train.","furigana":"でんしゃ|さわ|こども|ははおや|こごえ|たしな"},{"japanese":"「それは言い過ぎだよ」と友人に嗜められた。","romaji":"\"Sore wa iisugi da yo\" to yūjin ni tashinamerareta.","german":"\"That's going too far,\" a friend chided me.","furigana":"い|す|ゆうじん|たしな"}],"notes":"たしなめる is mostly written in kana and is transitive (人を嗜める). It denotes a calm, often gentle \"admonishing, rebuking\", typically by elders or superiors. 叱る is stronger and angrier, 注意する more neutral. Not to be confused with 嗜む (\"to be versed in something, to enjoy in moderation\"), which has the same kanji.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は友人の過ちを宥した。","romaji":"Kare wa yūjin no ayamachi o yurushita.","german":"He forgave his friend's mistake.","furigana":"かれ|ゆうじん|あやま|ゆる"},{"japanese":"王は罪人を宥し、自由の身にした。","romaji":"Ō wa zainin o yurushi, jiyū no mi ni shita.","german":"The king pardoned the offender and set him free.","furigana":"おう|ざいにん|ゆる|じゆう|み"}],"notes":"宥す means to treat a mistake or guilt with leniency. The rare spelling stresses the forgiving; in everyday writing 許す is usual, and for a remission of punishment also 赦す. Not to be confused with 宥める (なだめる, \"to soothe\"). Typical are 人を宥す and 罪を宥す. Transitive godan verb in す: 宥します・宥して・宥さない.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"旗が風に翻っている。","romaji":"Hata ga kaze ni hirugaette iru.","german":"The flag is fluttering in the wind.","furigana":"はた|かぜ|ひるがえ"},{"japanese":"何度説得しても、彼の決心は翻らなかった。","romaji":"Nando settoku shite mo, kare no kesshin wa hirugaeranakatta.","german":"No matter how many times they tried to persuade him, his resolve did not change.","furigana":"なんどせっとく|かれ|けっしん|ひるがえ"},{"japanese":"翻って日本の現状を見ると、課題はまだ多い。","romaji":"Hirugaette Nihon no genjō o miru to, kadai wa mada ōi.","german":"Looking at it the other way round, at the current state of Japan, there are still many challenges.","furigana":"ひるがえ|にほん|げんじょう|み|かだい|おお"}],"notes":"翻る is intransitive: flags, skirts or coats \"flutter, wave\" (旗が風に翻る), things \"turn over\", and opinions or decisions \"suddenly change\". The te-form 翻って serves in texts as a transition: \"looking at it the other way round, on the other hand ...\". The transitive counterpart 翻す appears in 前言を翻す \"to take back one's words\" and 反旗を翻す \"to raise the banner of revolt\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"泥に塗れて帰ってきた。","romaji":"Doro ni mamirete kaette kita.","german":"He came home covered in mud.","furigana":"どろ|まみ|かえ"},{"japanese":"一日中畑で働いて、全身汗に塗れていた。","romaji":"Ichinichijū hatake de hataraite, zenshin ase ni mamirete ita.","german":"After working in the field all day, he was soaked in sweat from head to toe.","furigana":"いちにちじゅうはたけ|はたら|ぜんしんあせ|まみ"},{"japanese":"彼は借金まみれの生活からようやく抜け出した。","romaji":"Kare wa shakkin mamire no seikatsu kara yōyaku nukedashita.","german":"He finally escaped from a life buried in debt.","furigana":"かれ|しゃっきん|せいかつ|ぬ|だ"}],"notes":"まみれる is intransitive and takes に: 泥に塗れる, 汗に塗れる \"to be covered all over\". Much more common is the suffix form 〜まみれ in kana: 泥まみれ, 血まみれ, 借金まみれ. Unlike 〜だらけ (\"full of ...\", also for many individual things such as 間違いだらけ), 〜まみれ stresses being smeared on the surface or, figuratively, being completely sunk in something. Set phrase: 一敗地に塗れる \"to suffer a crushing defeat\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"相手の攻撃を巧みに躱した。","romaji":"Aite no kōgeki o takumi ni kawashita.","german":"He skillfully dodged his opponent's attack.","furigana":"あいて|こうげき|たく|かわ"},{"japanese":"記者の厳しい質問を、大臣は笑顔で躱した。","romaji":"Kisha no kibishii shitsumon o, daijin wa egao de kawashita.","german":"The minister parried the reporter's tough question with a smile.","furigana":"きしゃ|きび|しつもん|だいじん|えがお|かわ"},{"japanese":"自転車が急に飛び出してきたが、とっさに身を躱した。","romaji":"Jitensha ga kyū ni tobidashite kita ga, tossa ni mi o kawashita.","german":"A bicycle suddenly shot out, but I dodged it instantly.","furigana":"じてんしゃ|きゅう|と|だ|み|かわ"}],"notes":"かわす is mostly written in kana and is transitive: to physically \"dodge\" an attack or obstacle (攻撃を躱す, 身を躱す) and figuratively to \"deftly fend off\" questions or criticism (質問・追及を躱す). Similar is 避ける, which means \"to avoid\" more generally. Not to be confused with the homophone 交わす \"to exchange\" (言葉・約束を交わす).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は言い繕おうとしたが見破られた。","romaji":"Kare wa iitsukuroō to shita ga miyaburareta.","german":"He tried to talk his way out of it but was seen through.","furigana":"かれ|い|つくろ|みやぶ"},{"japanese":"遅刻の理由をいくら言い繕っても、上司には通じなかった。","romaji":"Chikoku no riyū o ikura iitsukurotte mo, jōshi ni wa tsūjinakatta.","german":"No matter how much he glossed over the reason for being late, it did not work on his boss.","furigana":"ちこく|りゆう|い|つくろ|じょうし|つう"},{"japanese":"大臣は失言を言い繕ったが、かえって批判を浴びた。","romaji":"Daijin wa shitsugen o iitsukurotta ga, kaette hihan o abita.","german":"The minister tried to smooth over his gaffe but only drew more criticism.","furigana":"だいじん|しつげん|い|つくろ|ひはん|あ"}],"notes":"言い繕う means to gloss over a mistake or an omission with words, that is, with excuses or clever explanations. Typical are 失敗をうまく言い繕う and その場を言い繕う. It is the verbal variant of 取り繕う; similar, but aimed more at talking one's way out or deceiving, are 言い逃れる \"to talk one's way out\" and ごまかす. Transitive godan verb, rather literary and slightly pejorative.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"困難を前にして怖じけてはいけない。","romaji":"Konnan o mae ni shite ojikete wa ikenai.","german":"You must not be daunted in the face of difficulties.","furigana":"こんなん|まえ|お"},{"japanese":"大観衆に怖じけることなく、彼女は堂々と歌った。","romaji":"Daikanshū ni ojikeru koto naku, kanojo wa dōdō to utatta.","german":"Not intimidated by the huge audience, she sang with composure.","furigana":"だいかんしゅう|お|かのじょ|どうどう|うた"},{"japanese":"相手が大男だと分かると、彼はすっかり怖じけてしまった。","romaji":"Aite ga ōotoko da to wakaru to, kare wa sukkari ojikete shimatta.","german":"When he realized his opponent was a big man, he completely lost his nerve.","furigana":"あいて|おおおとこ|わ|かれ|お"}],"notes":"怖じける is intransitive and literary; it means \"to be intimidated, lose courage\" in the face of a person or situation (〜に怖じける, 〜を前にして怖じける). More often heard is 怖気づく with the same meaning. Related are 怖気 \"fear\" and 物怖じ \"timidity\" (物怖じしない \"fearless\"). It often appears negated: 〜に怖じけることなく.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"偽情報がネット上に蔓延っている。","romaji":"Nise jōhō ga netto jō ni habikotte iru.","german":"False information is rampant on the internet.","furigana":"にせじょうほう|じょう|はびこ"},{"japanese":"留守の間に、庭じゅうに雑草が蔓延ってしまった。","romaji":"Rusu no aida ni, niwajū ni zassō ga habikotte shimatta.","german":"While we were away, weeds overran the whole garden.","furigana":"るす|あいだ|にわ|ざっそう|はびこ"},{"japanese":"役所には古い慣習と不正が蔓延っていた。","romaji":"Yakusho ni wa furui kanshū to fusei ga habikotte ita.","german":"Old customs and corruption were rife in the government office.","furigana":"やくしょ|ふる|かんしゅう|ふせい|はびこ"}],"notes":"はびこる is mostly written in kana and is intransitive (〜に蔓延る). Literally weeds \"run riot\"; figuratively unwanted things such as corruption, violence, prejudice or false information \"are rampant, spread\". The word always has a negative coloring, unlike neutral 広がる. Read with the same kanji, 蔓延 (まんえん) means \"spread\", above all of diseases.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は密かに復讐を目論んでいた。","romaji":"Kare wa hisoka ni fukushū o mokuronde ita.","german":"He was secretly plotting revenge.","furigana":"かれ|ひそ||もくろ"},{"japanese":"その会社はアジア市場への進出を目論んでいる。","romaji":"Sono kaisha wa Ajia shijō e no shinshutsu o mokuronde iru.","german":"That company is aiming to expand into the Asian market.","furigana":"かいしゃ|しじょう|しんしゅつ|もくろ"},{"japanese":"一儲けしようと目論んだが、見事に失敗した。","romaji":"Hitomōke shiyō to mokuronda ga, migoto ni shippai shita.","german":"I schemed to make a quick profit but failed miserably.","furigana":"ひともう|もくろ|みごと|しっぱい"}],"notes":"目論む is transitive and means \"to plan, have in mind\", usually with an air of ambition or calculation: 復讐・進出・一儲けを目論む, or 〜ようと目論む. Clearly more negative is 企む \"to hatch a plot\"; 計画する is neutral. The noun 目論見 is very common: 目論見通り \"as planned\", 目論見が外れる \"the plan does not work out\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"他人の不幸を嗤うのは恥ずべき行為だ。","romaji":"Tanin no fukō o warau no wa hazubeki kōi da.","german":"Laughing scornfully at other people's misfortune is a shameful act.","furigana":"たにん|ふこう|わら|は|こうい"},{"japanese":"彼の夢を、周りの人々は無謀だと嗤った。","romaji":"Kare no yume o, mawari no hitobito wa mubō da to waratta.","german":"The people around him ridiculed his dream as reckless.","furigana":"かれ|ゆめ|まわ|ひとびと|むぼう|わら"},{"japanese":"失敗した私を、彼は鼻で嗤った。","romaji":"Shippai shita watashi o, kare wa hana de waratta.","german":"When I failed, he just snorted at me.","furigana":"しっぱい|わたし|かれ|はな|わら"}],"notes":"嗤う is the same verb as 笑う; the spelling with 嗤 fixes it to \"to deride, to ridicule\" and is literary. In this meaning it is transitive (人・夢・失敗を嗤う). Typical is 鼻で嗤う \"to laugh contemptuously\". In everyday texts one usually writes 笑う and recognizes the mockery from the context; an unambiguous word is 嘲笑う \"to jeer at\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4 (source: N4; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"石に蹴躓いて転んだ。","romaji":"Ishi ni ketsumazuite koronda.","german":"I tripped on a stone and fell.","furigana":"いし|けつまず|ころ"},{"japanese":"暗い廊下で段差に蹴躓いて、膝を擦りむいた。","romaji":"Kurai rōka de dansa ni ketsumazuite, hiza o surimuita.","german":"I tripped on a step in the dark corridor and scraped my knee.","furigana":"くら|ろうか|だんさ|けつまず|ひざ|す"},{"japanese":"新事業は最初の一年で蹴躓いてしまった。","romaji":"Shin jigyō wa saisho no ichinen de ketsumazuite shimatta.","german":"The new business stumbled in its first year.","furigana":"しんじぎょう|さいしょ|いちねん|けつまず"}],"notes":"けつまずく is mostly written in kana and is a colloquially intensified form of つまずく; the 蹴 (\"to kick\") stresses that one catches one's foot on something. It is intransitive and takes に: 石に蹴躓く. Figuratively it can mean \"to falter, fail\" like つまずく, though for failures 躓く is more usual.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"罪悪感に苛まれている。","romaji":"Zaiakukan ni sainamarete iru.","german":"I am tormented by guilt.","furigana":"ざいあくかん|さいな"},{"japanese":"彼女は長年、原因不明の頭痛に苛まれてきた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa naganen, gen'in fumei no zutsū ni sainamarete kita.","german":"She has been plagued for years by headaches of unknown cause.","furigana":"かのじょ|ながねん|げんいんふめい|ずつう|さいな"},{"japanese":"夜になると、将来への不安が彼の心を苛んだ。","romaji":"Yoru ni naru to, shōrai e no fuan ga kare no kokoro o sainanda.","german":"At night, anxiety about the future tormented him.","furigana":"よる|しょうらい|ふあん|かれ|こころ|さいな"}],"notes":"苛む is transitive but appears almost always in the passive: 罪悪感・不安・後悔・痛みに苛まれる \"to be tormented by ...\". It is literary and describes lasting mental or physical torment; intensified also 責め苛む. More neutral is 苦しめる; 悩む, by contrast, is intransitive and refers to one's own brooding.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"スーツを誂えるために仕立て屋を訪ねた。","romaji":"Sūtsu o atsuraeru tame ni shitateya o tazuneta.","german":"I visited a tailor to have a suit made.","furigana":"あつら|した|や|たず"},{"japanese":"結婚式のために、着物を誂えた。","romaji":"Kekkonshiki no tame ni, kimono o atsuraeta.","german":"I had a kimono made for the wedding.","furigana":"けっこんしき|きもの|あつら"},{"japanese":"今日はピクニックにお誂え向きの天気だ。","romaji":"Kyō wa pikunikku ni oatsuraemuki no tenki da.","german":"Today's weather is just right for a picnic.","furigana":"きょう|あつら|む|てんき"}],"notes":"あつらえる is transitive and means to \"have something made\" to one's own wishes, above all clothing, kimono, shoes or furniture (スーツを誂える). General ordering is 注文する; nowadays one also says オーダーメイドで作る. The phrase お誂え向き \"tailor-made for, ideal for\" is common: ピクニックにお誂え向きの天気.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"質問されても惚けて答えなかった。","romaji":"Shitsumon sarete mo tobokete kotaenakatta.","german":"Even when questioned, he played dumb and did not answer.","furigana":"しつもん|とぼ|こた"},{"japanese":"「つまみ食いしたでしょう？」と聞くと、弟は「何のこと？」と惚けた。","romaji":"\"Tsumamigui shita deshō?\" to kiku to, otōto wa \"Nan no koto?\" to toboketa.","german":"When asked \"You snuck a bite, didn't you?\", my younger brother played innocent: \"What do you mean?\"","furigana":"ぐ|き|おとうと|なん|とぼ"},{"japanese":"あの漫才師のとぼけた味がたまらない。","romaji":"Ano manzaishi no toboketa aji ga tamaranai.","german":"I love the deadpan charm of that comedian.","furigana":"まんざいし|あじ"}],"notes":"とぼける is mostly written in kana and is intransitive. Its main meaning is \"to play dumb, act clueless\", for example when caught; a synonym is しらばくれる. As とぼけた + noun it also describes a dry, quirky humor (とぼけた味, とぼけた顔). Note: the same kanji are also read ぼける (\"to go senile\") and ほうける.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"煙で噎せて咳が止まらなかった。","romaji":"Kemuri de musete seki ga tomaranakatta.","german":"The smoke made me choke and I could not stop coughing.","furigana":"けむり|む|せき|と"},{"japanese":"慌ててご飯を食べたら、噎せてしまった。","romaji":"Awatete gohan o tabetara, musete shimatta.","german":"I ate my rice in a hurry and ended up choking.","furigana":"あわ|はん|た|む"},{"japanese":"部屋に入ると、噎せ返るような香水の匂いがした。","romaji":"Heya ni hairu to, musekaeru yō na kōsui no nioi ga shita.","german":"When I entered the room, there was a stifling smell of perfume.","furigana":"へや|はい|む|かえ|こうすい|にお"}],"notes":"むせる is mostly written in kana and is intransitive. One chokes on food or drink, or gets an urge to cough from smoke, dust or a strong smell: 煙に噎せる, 煙で噎せる. The intensified form 噎せ返る is also used figuratively: 噎せ返るような暑さ・香り \"stifling, overpowering\". 咳き込む stresses the coughing fit itself.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"冬物の服は嵩張るので収納に困る。","romaji":"Fuyumono no fuku wa kasabaru node shūnō ni komaru.","german":"Winter clothes are bulky, so storing them is a problem.","furigana":"ふゆもの|ふく|かさば|しゅうのう|こま"},{"japanese":"お土産は嵩張らないものにしよう。","romaji":"Omiyage wa kasabaranai mono ni shiyō.","german":"Let's pick souvenirs that are not bulky.","furigana":"みやげ|かさば"},{"japanese":"紙の辞書は嵩張るので、旅行には電子辞書を持っていく。","romaji":"Kami no jisho wa kasabaru node, ryokō ni wa denshi jisho o motte iku.","german":"Paper dictionaries are bulky, so I take an electronic dictionary when traveling.","furigana":"かみ|じしょ|かさば|りょこう|でんしじしょ|も"}],"notes":"かさばる is mostly written in kana and is intransitive: luggage, clothes or books \"are bulky, take up a lot of room\". It is about volume (嵩), not necessarily weight; for weight one says 重い. Not to be confused with 嵩む \"to mount up\", which applies above all to costs: 費用が嵩む.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"試合前に気持ちが昂ぶって眠れなかった。","romaji":"Shiai mae ni kimochi ga takabutte nemurenakatta.","german":"I was so keyed up before the match that I could not sleep.","furigana":"しあいまえ|きも|たか|ねむ"},{"japanese":"感情が昂ぶって、思わず声を荒らげてしまった。","romaji":"Kanjō ga takabutte, omowazu koe o aragete shimatta.","german":"My emotions ran high and I involuntarily raised my voice.","furigana":"かんじょう|たか|おも|こえ|あ"},{"japanese":"優勝が決まった瞬間、選手たちの感情は一気に昂ぶった。","romaji":"Yūshō ga kimatta shunkan, senshutachi no kanjō wa ikki ni takabutta.","german":"The moment the title was decided, the players' emotions surged all at once.","furigana":"ゆうしょう|き|しゅんかん|せんしゅ|かんじょう|いっき|たか"}],"notes":"昂ぶる is intransitive and is used with 気持ち・感情・神経が as the subject: feelings \"rise, come to a boil\". The spelling 高ぶる is more common and also carries the second meaning \"to be haughty\" (驕り高ぶる). More neutral is 興奮する; 昂ぶる sounds somewhat more literary and stresses inner tension.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は機会を窺っている。","romaji":"Kare wa kikai o ukagatte iru.","german":"He is waiting for his chance.","furigana":"かれ|きかい|うかが"},{"japanese":"上司の顔色を窺いながら話を切り出した。","romaji":"Jōshi no kaoiro o ukagainagara hanashi o kiridashita.","german":"Watching my boss's expression, I broached the subject.","furigana":"じょうし|かおいろ|うかが|はなし|き|だ"},{"japanese":"彼の文章からは、深い苦悩が窺える。","romaji":"Kare no bunshō kara wa, fukai kunō ga ukagaeru.","german":"Deep anguish can be glimpsed in his writing.","furigana":"かれ|ぶんしょう|ふか|くのう|うかが"}],"notes":"窺う is mostly written in kana and is transitive. It means \"to watch secretly\" (中の様子を窺う), \"to lie in wait\" (機会・チャンスを窺う), appears in the set phrase 顔色を窺う \"to gauge someone's mood\", and as 〜が窺える \"can be discerned\". Not to be confused with the homophone humble 伺う \"to ask; to visit\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"命に拘わる重大な問題だ。","romaji":"Inochi ni kakawaru jūdai na mondai da.","german":"It is a serious problem that involves life and death.","furigana":"いのち|かか|じゅうだい|もんだい"},{"japanese":"あんな連中とは拘わらないほうがいい。","romaji":"Anna renchū to wa kakawaranai hō ga ii.","german":"You had better not get involved with people like that.","furigana":"れんちゅう|かか"},{"japanese":"彼は長年、この研究に拘わってきた。","romaji":"Kare wa naganen, kono kenkyū ni kakawatte kita.","german":"He has been involved in this research for many years.","furigana":"かれ|ながねん|けんきゅう|かか"}],"notes":"拘わる is a rare spelling of 関わる, which is standard today; the kanji 拘 is otherwise mostly read こだわる (拘る) or in にも拘らず. The verb is intransitive: 〜に関わる \"to concern, to have to do with, to be involved in\" (命に関わる \"life-threatening\", 事件に関わる), and 〜と関わる \"to get involved with someone\". The related noun is 関わり \"relationship, connection\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"微々たる金額では何も変わらない。","romaji":"Bibitaru kingaku de wa nani mo kawaranai.","german":"A trifling sum will not change anything.","furigana":"びび|きんがく|なに|か"},{"japanese":"私の貢献など微々たるものです。","romaji":"Watashi no kōken nado bibitaru mono desu.","german":"My contribution is negligible.","furigana":"わたし|こうけん|びび"},{"japanese":"両者の差は微々たるもので、ほとんど気にならない。","romaji":"Ryōsha no sa wa bibitaru mono de, hotondo ki ni naranai.","german":"The difference between the two is slight and hardly noticeable.","furigana":"りょうしゃ|さ|びび|き"}],"notes":"微々たる is the attributive form of the たる-adjective 微々 and stands only before nouns: 微々たる金額・差・量・変化. As a predicate one says 微々たるものだ. Compared with neutral わずか it sounds more literary and stresses that something hardly counts; it is often used modestly for one's own contribution (私の力など微々たるものです).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"憎々しい態度に腹が立った。","romaji":"Nikunikushii taido ni hara ga tatta.","german":"His hateful attitude made me angry.","furigana":"にくにく|たいど|はら|た"},{"japanese":"被告は法廷で憎々しげに検事を睨みつけた。","romaji":"Hikoku wa hōtei de nikunikushige ni kenji o niramitsuketa.","german":"In court, the defendant glared hatefully at the prosecutor.","furigana":"ひこく|ほうてい|にくにく|けんじ|にら"},{"japanese":"ドラマの悪役を憎々しく演じきった俳優が話題になった。","romaji":"Dorama no akuyaku o nikunikushiku enjikitta haiyū ga wadai ni natta.","german":"The actor who played the drama's villain with convincing odiousness became a talking point.","furigana":"あくやく|にくにく|えん|はいゆう|わだい"}],"notes":"憎々しい is an i-adjective describing people, faces, tone of voice or behavior that \"arouse hatred, seem repulsive\": 憎々しい態度・口調・顔. It is stronger and more serious than 憎らしい, which can also be playful (憎らしいほど上手). The form 憎々しげに, by contrast, describes someone looking or speaking spitefully themselves.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"朝日に照らされた山が神々しく見えた。","romaji":"Asahi ni terasareta yama ga kōgōshiku mieta.","german":"The mountain lit by the morning sun looked divine.","furigana":"あさひ|て|やま|こうごう|み"},{"japanese":"薄暗い本堂の中で、仏像が神々しく輝いていた。","romaji":"Usugurai hondō no naka de, butsuzō ga kōgōshiku kagayaite ita.","german":"In the dim main hall, the Buddha statue shone with a sacred glow.","furigana":"うすぐら|ほんどう|なか|ぶつぞう|こうごう|かがや"},{"japanese":"白いドレス姿の花嫁は、神々しいほど美しかった。","romaji":"Shiroi doresu sugata no hanayome wa, kōgōshii hodo utsukushikatta.","german":"The bride in her white dress was so beautiful she seemed divine.","furigana":"しろ|すがた|はなよめ|こうごう|うつく"}],"notes":"神々しい is an i-adjective for a sublime, almost divine radiance that inspires awe: the rising sun, sacred mountains, shrines, Buddha statues or an otherworldly beautiful appearance. Typical are 神々しく輝く and 神々しいほど美しい. Related is 厳か \"solemn\", which rather describes a ceremony or atmosphere.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"禍々しい雰囲気が辺りを包んでいた。","romaji":"Magamagashii fun'iki ga atari o tsutsunde ita.","german":"An ominous atmosphere enveloped the area.","furigana":"まがまが|ふんいき|あた|つつ"},{"japanese":"空が禍々しい赤色に染まっていた。","romaji":"Sora ga magamagashii akairo ni somatte ita.","german":"The sky was stained an ominous red.","furigana":"そら|まがまが|あかいろ|そ"},{"japanese":"その古い屋敷には、禍々しい言い伝えが残っている。","romaji":"Sono furui yashiki ni wa, magamagashii iitsutae ga nokotte iru.","german":"Sinister legends remain about that old mansion.","furigana":"ふる|やしき|まがまが|い|つた|のこ"}],"notes":"禍々しい is an i-adjective from 禍 \"calamity\" and means \"ominous, threatening, foreboding misfortune\". It is literary and popular in novels, horror and fantasy: 禍々しい雰囲気・気配・色・言い伝え. 不吉 is the more matter-of-fact \"inauspicious\", 不気味 stresses the eerie more.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仄かな花の香りが漂ってきた。","romaji":"Honoka na hana no kaori ga tadayotte kita.","german":"A faint scent of flowers drifted over.","furigana":"ほの|はな|かお|ただよ"},{"japanese":"このお茶には仄かな甘みがある。","romaji":"Kono ocha ni wa honoka na amami ga aru.","german":"This tea has a subtle sweetness.","furigana":"ちゃ|ほの|あま"},{"japanese":"当時、私は彼女に仄かな恋心を抱いていた。","romaji":"Tōji, watashi wa kanojo ni honoka na koigokoro o idaite ita.","german":"At the time, I harbored a faint affection for her.","furigana":"とうじ|わたし|かのじょ|ほの|こいごころ|いだ"}],"notes":"ほのか is a na-adjective and is mostly written in kana. It describes delicate, barely perceptible but pleasant impressions: ほのかな香り・甘み・光, ほのかな恋心 \"a faint affection\"; adverbial ほのかに. Compared with かすか (\"barely perceptible\", neutral, also for sounds and memories), ほのか has a soft, positive ring.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"遠くから微かな音が聞こえた。","romaji":"Tōku kara kasuka na oto ga kikoeta.","german":"I heard a faint sound from far away.","furigana":"とお|かす|おと|き"},{"japanese":"子供の頃のことは微かに覚えている。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro no koto wa kasuka ni oboete iru.","german":"I faintly remember my childhood.","furigana":"こども|ころ|かす|おぼ"},{"japanese":"生存者がいるという微かな望みを捨てなかった。","romaji":"Seizonsha ga iru to iu kasuka na nozomi o sutenakatta.","german":"He did not give up the slim hope that there were survivors.","furigana":"せいぞんしゃ|かす|のぞ|す"}],"notes":"かすか is a na-adjective and is mostly written in kana. It means \"faint, barely perceptible\" and suits sounds, light, smells, memories and hopes: かすかな音, かすかに覚えている, かすかな望み. Unlike ほのか, which sounds delicate and pleasant, かすか is neutral and stresses the edge of perceptibility.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"長閑な田園風景が広がっている。","romaji":"Nodoka na den'en fūkei ga hirogatte iru.","german":"A tranquil pastoral landscape stretches out.","furigana":"のどか|でんえんふうけい|ひろ"},{"japanese":"春の長閑な日差しの中で、猫が昼寝をしている。","romaji":"Haru no nodoka na hizashi no naka de, neko ga hirune o shite iru.","german":"In the peaceful spring sunshine, a cat is napping.","furigana":"はる|のどか|ひざ|なか|ねこ|ひるね"},{"japanese":"都会と違って、この島では時間が長閑に流れる。","romaji":"Tokai to chigatte, kono shima de wa jikan ga nodoka ni nagareru.","german":"Unlike in the city, time flows leisurely on this island.","furigana":"とかい|ちが|しま|じかん|のどか|なが"}],"notes":"のどか is a na-adjective and is mostly written in kana. It describes a peaceful, leisurely mood, typical of rural landscapes and mild spring weather: のどかな田園風景, のどかな春の日. Adverbial のどかに means \"leisurely\". 静か means only \"quiet, calm\", 穏やか \"gentle, even-tempered\" and also suits people and the sea.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"制服姿が凛々しい。","romaji":"Seifuku sugata ga ririshii.","german":"He looks dashing in his uniform.","furigana":"せいふくすがた|りり"},{"japanese":"彼は凛々しい眉の青年だった。","romaji":"Kare wa ririshii mayu no seinen datta.","german":"He was a young man with finely set eyebrows.","furigana":"かれ|りり|まゆ|せいねん"},{"japanese":"袴姿で弓を引く彼女の横顔は凛々しかった。","romaji":"Hakama sugata de yumi o hiku kanojo no yokogao wa ririshikatta.","german":"Her profile as she drew the bow in hakama was gallant.","furigana":"はかますがた|ゆみ|ひ|かのじょ|よこがお|りり"}],"notes":"凛々しい is an i-adjective that praises a resolute, dignified and energetic appearance, often of young men but also of women: 凛々しい顔立ち・眉・姿, 制服姿が凛々しい. Related is 凛とした \"taut, dignified\". Colloquially one says かっこいい; 勇ましい stresses courage and fighting spirit.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新入社員の初々しい姿が微笑ましい。","romaji":"Shinnyū shain no uiuishii sugata ga hohoemashii.","german":"The fresh look of the new employees is heartwarming.","furigana":"しんにゅうしゃいん|ういうい|すがた|ほほえ"},{"japanese":"新婚の二人は、まだ初々しい様子だった。","romaji":"Shinkon no futari wa, mada uiuishii yōsu datta.","german":"The newlyweds still looked fresh and shy.","furigana":"しんこん|ふたり|ういうい|ようす"},{"japanese":"初めての舞台で緊張する彼女の姿は初々しかった。","romaji":"Hajimete no butai de kinchō suru kanojo no sugata wa uiuishikatta.","german":"She looked fresh and endearing, nervous on stage for the first time.","furigana":"はじ|ぶたい|きんちょう|かのじょ|すがた|ういうい"}],"notes":"初々しい is an i-adjective that affectionately describes the freshness and awkwardness of newcomers: new employees, first-year students, newlyweds or first love. It is meant positively, unlike 未熟 \"immature\". 若々しい, by contrast, means \"youthful, vital\" and is often used for older people who look young.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"急によそよそしい態度を取られた。","romaji":"Kyū ni yosoyososhii taido o torareta.","german":"I was suddenly treated in a cold, distant manner.","furigana":"きゅう|たいど|と"},{"japanese":"喧嘩して以来、二人の会話はどこかよそよそしい。","romaji":"Kenka shite irai, futari no kaiwa wa dokoka yosoyososhii.","german":"Ever since their quarrel, the conversation between the two has been somehow stiff.","furigana":"けんか|いらい|ふたり|かいわ"},{"japanese":"敬語なんて使わないでよ、よそよそしいなあ。","romaji":"Keigo nante tsukawanaide yo, yosoyososhii nā.","german":"Don't use polite language with me, it feels so distant.","furigana":"けいご|つか"}],"notes":"よそよそしい is an i-adjective from よそ \"strange, elsewhere\" and describes cool, distant behavior, mostly in someone who is actually close to you: よそよそしい態度・口調. Typical is the switch from familiarity to distance, for instance after a quarrel. 冷たい is harsher, 他人行儀 stresses exaggerated formality as among strangers.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"猛々しい叫び声が響き渡った。","romaji":"Takedakeshii sakebigoe ga hibikiwatatta.","german":"A fierce cry rang out.","furigana":"たけだけ|さけ|ごえ|ひび|わた"},{"japanese":"檻の中の虎が猛々しい目でこちらを睨んでいた。","romaji":"Ori no naka no tora ga takedakeshii me de kochira o nirande ita.","german":"The tiger in the cage was glaring at us with ferocious eyes.","furigana":"おり|なか|とら|たけだけ|め|にら"},{"japanese":"人の物を盗んでおいて文句を言うなんて、盗人猛々しい。","romaji":"Hito no mono o nusunde oite monku o iu nante, nusutto takedakeshii.","german":"To steal someone's property and then complain about it, what a nerve.","furigana":"ひと|もの|ぬす|もんく|い|"}],"notes":"猛々しい is an i-adjective and literary. It means \"wild, fierce, belligerent\" and suits predators, warriors, glances or cries. The second meaning \"brazen, impudent\" survives mainly in the saying 盗人猛々しい: the one in the wrong also acts boldly and demandingly. 勇ましい is the positive \"brave\", 荒々しい \"rough, violent\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"荒々しい波が岸壁に打ち付けた。","romaji":"Araarashii nami ga ganpeki ni uchitsuketa.","german":"Rough waves crashed against the quay.","furigana":"あらあら|なみ|がんぺき|う|つ"},{"japanese":"彼は怒って荒々しくドアを閉めた。","romaji":"Kare wa okotte araarashiku doa o shimeta.","german":"Angry, he slammed the door violently.","furigana":"かれ|おこ|あらあら|し"},{"japanese":"北海道の荒々しい自然に心を打たれた。","romaji":"Hokkaidō no araarashii shizen ni kokoro o utareta.","german":"I was moved by the rugged nature of Hokkaido.","furigana":"ほっかいどう|あらあら|しぜん|こころ|う"}],"notes":"荒々しい describes something raw, untamed and violent: forces of nature (荒々しい波, 荒々しい岩肌), but also behavior and speech (荒々しい口調, 荒々しくドアを閉める). Compared with the base word 荒い \"rough, fierce\", the doubling stresses the wild, unrestrained impression and sounds somewhat more literary. For landscapes or people it can also mean appreciatively \"untamed, powerful\" (荒々しい自然).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"麗しい友情の物語に感動した。","romaji":"Uruwashii yūjō no monogatari ni kandō shita.","german":"I was moved by the story of a beautiful friendship.","furigana":"うるわ|ゆうじょう|ものがたり|かんどう"},{"japanese":"舞台に麗しい姿の女優が現れた。","romaji":"Butai ni uruwashii sugata no joyū ga arawareta.","german":"An actress of graceful appearance appeared on stage.","furigana":"ぶたい|うるわ|すがた|じょゆう|あらわ"},{"japanese":"「ご機嫌麗しゅうございます」と執事が挨拶した。","romaji":"\"Gokigen uruwashū gozaimasu\" to shitsuji ga aisatsu shita.","german":"\"I trust you are in good spirits,\" the butler greeted.","furigana":"きげんうるわ|しつじ|あいさつ"}],"notes":"麗しい is an elevated, rather literary word for \"beautiful, graceful\". It describes on the one hand looks, voice or bearing (麗しい姿, 見目麗しい) and on the other heartwarming relationships (麗しい友情, 麗しい師弟愛). In everyday speech it sounds solemn or slightly ironic; neutrally one says 美しい or きれい. The set phrase ご機嫌麗しゅう is an old-fashioned polite greeting formula.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"我が子が愛おしくてたまらない。","romaji":"Waga ko ga itooshikute tamaranai.","german":"I love my child so much I can hardly bear it.","furigana":"わ|こ|いと"},{"japanese":"眠っている猫の寝顔が愛おしい。","romaji":"Nemutte iru neko no negao ga itooshii.","german":"The sleeping face of the cat is adorable.","furigana":"ねむ|ねこ|ねがお|いと"},{"japanese":"学生時代の何気ない日々が今では愛おしく思える。","romaji":"Gakusei jidai no nanigenai hibi ga ima de wa itooshiku omoeru.","german":"The ordinary days of my school years now seem precious to me.","furigana":"がくせいじだい|なにげ|ひび|いま|いと|おも"}],"notes":"愛おしい expresses a tender, almost painful feeling of affection: one wants to protect and hold on to someone or something. Typical for children, partners, pets or past times (愛おしい日々), intensified with 〜てたまらない: 愛おしくてたまらない. Compared with かわいい it is deeper and more emotional; unlike 愛くるしい \"cute to look at\", it describes one's own feeling, not the appearance. The older secondary meaning \"pitiable\" is rare today; いとおしい is often written in kana.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"愛くるしい子犬に心を奪われた。","romaji":"Aikurushii koinu ni kokoro o ubawareta.","german":"I was captivated by the adorable puppy.","furigana":"あい|こいぬ|こころ|うば"},{"japanese":"赤ちゃんの愛くるしい笑顔に家族みんなが癒やされた。","romaji":"Akachan no aikurushii egao ni kazoku minna ga iyasareta.","german":"The baby's charming smile soothed the whole family.","furigana":"あか|あい|えがお|かぞく|い"},{"japanese":"パンダの愛くるしいしぐさを見ようと、動物園に行列ができた。","romaji":"Panda no aikurushii shigusa o miyō to, dōbutsuen ni gyōretsu ga dekita.","german":"A line formed at the zoo to see the pandas' adorable gestures.","furigana":"あい|み|どうぶつえん|ぎょうれつ"}],"notes":"愛くるしい describes a disarming, childlike cuteness, above all in babies, small children and animals: 愛くるしい笑顔, 愛くるしい目, 愛くるしいしぐさ. It is somewhat more elevated and expressive than かわいい and sounds natural in narratives or advertising copy. Unlike 愛おしい, which expresses one's own tender affection, 愛くるしい describes outward appearance. For adults it fits only a childlike, sweet face or smile.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"毒々しい色のキノコを見つけた。","romaji":"Dokudokushii iro no kinoko o mitsuketa.","german":"I found a mushroom with a poisonous-looking color.","furigana":"どくどく|いろ|み"},{"japanese":"毒々しい色のネオンが夜の街を照らしていた。","romaji":"Dokudokushii iro no neon ga yoru no machi o terashite ita.","german":"Garish neon lights lit up the night town.","furigana":"どくどく|いろ|よる|まち|て"},{"japanese":"彼女は毒々しい口調で元同僚の悪口を言った。","romaji":"Kanojo wa dokudokushii kuchō de moto dōryō no warukuchi o itta.","german":"She spoke venomously ill of her former colleague.","furigana":"かのじょ|どくどく|くちょう|もとどうりょう|わるくち|い"}],"notes":"毒々しい first means \"poisonous-looking\", for example mushrooms or insects with warning colors, and from there extends to anything that looks garish, obtrusive or repulsive: 毒々しい色, 毒々しい化粧 \"overdone makeup\", 毒々しい看板. For words or attitude it means \"spiteful, vicious\" (毒々しい言葉). The word is always negative; for neutral bright colors one uses 鮮やか, and for \"garish, flashy\" also けばけばしい.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"おどろおどろしい雰囲気の廃墟に入った。","romaji":"Odoroodoroshii fun'iki no haikyo ni haitta.","german":"I entered a ruin with an eerie atmosphere.","furigana":"ふんいき|はいきょ|はい"},{"japanese":"映画のポスターには、おどろおどろしい文字でタイトルが書かれていた。","romaji":"Eiga no posutā ni wa, odoroodoroshii moji de taitoru ga kakarete ita.","german":"The title on the movie poster was written in creepy lettering.","furigana":"えいが|もじ|か"},{"japanese":"事件はおどろおどろしく報じられたが、実際は単なる事故だった。","romaji":"Jiken wa odoroodoroshiku hōjirareta ga, jissai wa tannaru jiko datta.","german":"The incident was reported in a lurid way, but in reality it was a mere accident.","furigana":"じけん|ほう|じっさい|たん|じこ"}],"notes":"おどろおどろしい describes an eerie, ghastly effect, as known from horror films and ghost stories: おどろおどろしい雰囲気, おどろおどろしい音楽, おどろおどろしいタイトル. It is almost always written in hiragana. It often implies that something is deliberately staged in an exaggeratedly gloomy way (hence also \"overblown, sensational\"). More neutral are 不気味 and 気味が悪い \"creepy\"; 怖い describes more one's own feeling of fear.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"そんな馬鹿馬鹿しい話は信じられない。","romaji":"Sonna bakabakashii hanashi wa shinjirarenai.","german":"I can't believe such a ridiculous story.","furigana":"ばかばか|はなし|しん"},{"japanese":"五分の会議のために一時間もかけて来るなんて馬鹿馬鹿しい。","romaji":"Gofun no kaigi no tame ni ichijikan mo kakete kuru nante bakabakashii.","german":"It's absurd to spend a whole hour getting here for a five-minute meeting.","furigana":"ごふん|かいぎ|いちじかん|く|ばかばか"},{"japanese":"答えは馬鹿馬鹿しいほど簡単だった。","romaji":"Kotae wa bakabakashii hodo kantan datta.","german":"The answer was ridiculously simple.","furigana":"こた|ばかばか|かんたん"}],"notes":"馬鹿馬鹿しい calls something \"ridiculous, absurd, foolish\" and mostly expresses annoyance or contempt: 馬鹿馬鹿しい話, 馬鹿馬鹿しくてやっていられない \"so silly I won't go along with it\". It judges a matter or situation, not a person; of a person one rather says 馬鹿だ. In everyday writing it is mostly in hiragana (ばかばかしい). With 〜ほど it also means \"ridiculously (easy/cheap)\": 馬鹿馬鹿しいほど簡単.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"水々しい野菜が市場に並んでいる。","romaji":"Mizumizushii yasai ga ichiba ni narande iru.","german":"Fresh vegetables are lined up at the market.","furigana":"みずみず|やさい|いちば|なら"},{"japanese":"朝露にぬれた葉がみずみずしく光っている。","romaji":"Asatsuyu ni nureta ha ga mizumizushiku hikatte iru.","german":"The leaves wet with morning dew glisten freshly.","furigana":"あさつゆ|は|ひか"},{"japanese":"十代の作者のみずみずしい感性が光る小説だ。","romaji":"Jūdai no sakusha no mizumizushii kansei ga hikaru shōsetsu da.","german":"It is a novel in which the fresh sensibility of its teenage author shines.","furigana":"じゅうだい|さくしゃ|かんせい|ひか|しょうせつ"}],"notes":"みずみずしい means \"fresh, juicy, full of life\". Concretely it stands for fruit, vegetables and leaves (みずみずしいトマト), figuratively for young skin, youthful radiance or a fresh, unspoiled sensibility (みずみずしい感性, みずみずしい文章). It is mostly written in hiragana; as kanji, 瑞々しい is more usual than 水々しい. Compared with 新鮮 \"fresh (not old)\", it stresses juiciness, elasticity and vitality.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"年齢の割に若々しい見た目だ。","romaji":"Nenrei no wari ni wakawakashii mitame da.","german":"He looks youthful for his age.","furigana":"ねんれい|わり|わかわか|み|め"},{"japanese":"祖母は七十歳を過ぎても若々しく、毎日ジムに通っている。","romaji":"Sobo wa nanajussai o sugite mo wakawakashiku, mainichi jimu ni kayotte iru.","german":"My grandmother is still youthful in her seventies and goes to the gym every day.","furigana":"そぼ|ななじゅっさい|す|わかわか|まいにち|かよ"},{"japanese":"いつまでも若々しくいられる秘訣は何ですか。","romaji":"Itsu made mo wakawakashiku irareru hiketsu wa nan desu ka.","german":"What is your secret to staying so young?","furigana":"わかわか||なん"}],"notes":"若々しい describes someone who looks youthful - usually someone who is actually no longer young. That is why it is a typical compliment for older people: いつまでも若々しい, 年齢の割に若々しい. It refers to appearance, voice, movements or attitude of mind. 若い, by contrast, merely states actual young age; with truly young people, 若々しい at most stresses freshness and vigor (若々しい声).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"事故現場の生々しい映像が流れた。","romaji":"Jiko genba no namanamashii eizō ga nagareta.","german":"Graphic footage of the accident scene was broadcast.","furigana":"じこげんば|なまなま|えいぞう|なが"},{"japanese":"戦争体験者の生々しい証言に、会場は静まり返った。","romaji":"Sensō taikensha no namanamashii shōgen ni, kaijō wa shizumarikaetta.","german":"The vivid testimony of a war survivor silenced the hall.","furigana":"せんそうたいけんしゃ|なまなま|しょうげん|かいじょう|しず|かえ"},{"japanese":"彼の腕には手術の生々しい傷跡があった。","romaji":"Kare no ude ni wa shujutsu no namanamashii kizuato ga atta.","german":"He had a fresh surgical scar on his arm.","furigana":"かれ|うで|しゅじゅつ|なまなま|きずあと"}],"notes":"生々しい means \"raw, unvarnished, as if experienced firsthand\". It describes depictions, memories and reports so graphic and immediate that they almost hurt: 生々しい映像, 生々しい証言, 記憶がまだ生々しい. Second, it means \"still fresh\" for wounds or traces: 生々しい傷跡. Unlike リアル or 鮮やか \"lively, vivid\", it usually has an unpleasant, shocking undertone.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"怪我をした子供の姿が痛々しかった。","romaji":"Kega o shita kodomo no sugata ga itaitashikatta.","german":"The sight of the injured child was heartbreaking.","furigana":"けが|こども|すがた|いたいた"},{"japanese":"腕に巻かれた包帯が痛々しい。","romaji":"Ude ni makareta hōtai ga itaitashii.","german":"The bandage wrapped around his arm is painful to look at.","furigana":"うで|ま|ほうたい|いたいた"},{"japanese":"無理に明るく振る舞う彼女の姿は、見ていて痛々しかった。","romaji":"Muri ni akaruku furumau kanojo no sugata wa, mite ite itaitashikatta.","german":"Watching her force herself to act cheerful was painful.","furigana":"むり|あか|ふ|ま|かのじょ|すがた|み|いたいた"}],"notes":"痛々しい is said when a sight is so pitiable that it hurts oneself: 痛々しい姿, 包帯が痛々しい, 見ていて痛々しい. It describes the observer's impression, not one's own pain (for that, 痛い). Unlike 痛ましい, which judges tragic events such as accidents, it concerns the visible suffering or condition of a person. Colloquially it is also used for desperately trying behavior that is cringeworthy (cf. the slang イタい).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"重々しい口調で宣言を読み上げた。","romaji":"Omoomoshii kuchō de sengen o yomiageta.","german":"He read out the declaration in a solemn tone.","furigana":"おもおも|くちょう|せんげん|よ|あ"},{"japanese":"会議室には重々しい空気が漂っていた。","romaji":"Kaigishitsu ni wa omoomoshii kūki ga tadayotte ita.","german":"A heavy atmosphere hung over the meeting room.","furigana":"かいぎしつ|おもおも|くうき|ただよ"},{"japanese":"医者は重々しくうなずき、検査結果を説明し始めた。","romaji":"Isha wa omoomoshiku unazuki, kensa kekka o setsumei shihajimeta.","german":"The doctor nodded gravely and began explaining the test results.","furigana":"いしゃ|おもおも|けんさけっか|せつめい|はじ"}],"notes":"重々しい describes a heavy, weighty effect of voice, bearing or atmosphere: 重々しい口調, 重々しい雰囲気, 重々しくうなずく. Depending on context it sounds \"dignified, solemn\" or \"oppressive, weighing\"; for objects also \"massive-looking\" (重々しい扉). Not to be confused with 重苦しい \"heavy, oppressive\", which is clearly negative. Its counterpart in behavior is 軽々しい \"frivolous\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"軽々しく約束すべきではない。","romaji":"Karugarushiku yakusoku subeki de wa nai.","german":"You should not make promises lightly.","furigana":"かるがる|やくそく"},{"japanese":"確証もないのに、軽々しく人を疑うものではない。","romaji":"Kakushō mo nai no ni, karugarushiku hito o utagau mono de wa nai.","german":"You should not suspect people rashly without proof.","furigana":"かくしょう|かるがる|ひと|うたが"},{"japanese":"大臣の軽々しい発言が国際問題に発展した。","romaji":"Daijin no karugarushii hatsugen ga kokusai mondai ni hatten shita.","german":"The minister's careless remark developed into an international issue.","furigana":"だいじん|かるがる|はつげん|こくさいもんだい|はってん"}],"notes":"軽々しい means \"frivolous, thoughtless\" and criticizes words or actions without sufficient consideration. It most often stands adverbially in admonitions: 軽々しく口にする, 軽々しく約束する, 軽々しく判断すべきではない. Not to be confused with 軽々と (かるがると) \"effortlessly, with ease\", which is positive: 重い荷物を軽々と持ち上げる. The opposite in behavior is 慎重 \"cautious, careful\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"険しい山道を慎重に登った。","romaji":"Kewashii yamamichi o shinchō ni nobotta.","german":"I carefully climbed the steep mountain path.","furigana":"けわ|やまみち|しんちょう|のぼ"},{"japanese":"父は手紙を読むと急に険しい顔になった。","romaji":"Chichi wa tegami o yomu to kyū ni kewashii kao ni natta.","german":"When my father read the letter, his face suddenly turned stern.","furigana":"ちち|てがみ|よ|きゅう|けわ|かお"},{"japanese":"優勝までの道のりはまだ険しい。","romaji":"Yūshō made no michinori wa mada kewashii.","german":"The road to the championship is still rugged.","furigana":"ゆうしょう|みち|けわ"}],"notes":"険しい literally means \"steep, rugged, hard to reach\": 険しい山, 険しい坂道, 険しい崖. Figuratively it describes a dark, tense facial expression (険しい顔, 険しい目つき) as well as an arduous road or a serious situation (険しい道のり, 前途は険しい). Unlike 急 \"steep (slope)\", 険しい always carries a sense of danger and hardship. A 険しい顔 is a momentary expression of anger or worry, whereas 厳しい describes more a strict attitude or person.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"全員合格という喜ばしい結果だった。","romaji":"Zen'in gōkaku to iu yorokobashii kekka datta.","german":"It was a pleasing result: everyone passed.","furigana":"ぜんいんごうかく|よろこ|けっか"},{"japanese":"本日このように皆様をお迎えできることを大変喜ばしく思います。","romaji":"Honjitsu kono yō ni minasama o omukae dekiru koto o taihen yorokobashiku omoimasu.","german":"I am very pleased to be able to welcome you all here today.","furigana":"ほんじつ|みなさま|むか|たいへんよろこ|おも"},{"japanese":"地元出身の選手が金メダルを取ったという喜ばしい知らせが届いた。","romaji":"Jimoto shusshin no senshu ga kin medaru o totta to iu yorokobashii shirase ga todoita.","german":"The welcome news arrived that an athlete from the local area had won a gold medal.","furigana":"じもとしゅっしん|せんしゅ|きん|と|よろこ|し|とど"}],"notes":"喜ばしい judges an event or a piece of news as \"pleasing, welcome\": 喜ばしい知らせ, 喜ばしい結果, 〜は誠に喜ばしい. It is rather formal and typical of speeches, addresses and newspaper style. For one's own joy in daily life one says 嬉しい; 喜ばしい expresses a more general, objective assessment. The opposite is 嘆かわしい \"deplorable\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"若者のモラル低下は嘆かわしい。","romaji":"Wakamono no moraru teika wa nagekawashii.","german":"The decline in young people's morals is deplorable.","furigana":"わかもの|ていか|なげ"},{"japanese":"公の場で平気で嘘をつく政治家が多いのは嘆かわしい。","romaji":"Ōyake no ba de heiki de uso o tsuku seijika ga ōi no wa nagekawashii.","german":"It is deplorable that so many politicians lie casually in public.","furigana":"おおやけ|ば|へいき|うそ|せいじか|おお|なげ"},{"japanese":"文化財に落書きをするとは、実に嘆かわしい。","romaji":"Bunkazai ni rakugaki o suru to wa, jitsu ni nagekawashii.","german":"Scribbling graffiti on cultural properties is truly deplorable.","furigana":"ぶんかざい|らくが|じつ|なげ"}],"notes":"嘆かわしい (from 嘆く \"to lament, sigh\") expresses that a state of affairs is morally regrettable or outrageous: 嘆かわしい事態, 嘆かわしい風潮, 〜とは嘆かわしい. It is formal and critical and often sounds a bit preachy, typical of editorials, commentaries and older speakers lamenting social ills. It does not fit personal regret over one's own bad luck; for that one uses 残念 or 悔しい.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"卒業式は晴れがましい日だ。","romaji":"Sotsugyōshiki wa haregamashii hi da.","german":"The graduation ceremony is a day of glory.","furigana":"そつぎょうしき|は|ひ"},{"japanese":"晴れがましい表彰式の舞台に立ち、彼は少し緊張していた。","romaji":"Haregamashii hyōshōshiki no butai ni tachi, kare wa sukoshi kinchō shite ita.","german":"Standing on the stage of the glittering award ceremony, he was a little nervous.","furigana":"は|ひょうしょうしき|ぶたい|た|かれ|すこ|きんちょう"},{"japanese":"大勢の前で褒められて、晴れがましくて落ち着かなかった。","romaji":"Ōzei no mae de homerarete, haregamashikute ochitsukanakatta.","german":"Praised in front of so many people, I felt both proud and uneasy.","furigana":"おおぜい|まえ|ほ|は|お|つ"}],"notes":"晴れがましい describes the glory and festivity of a special occasion at which one is oneself the center of attention: 晴れがましい舞台, 晴れがましい日, 晴れがましい気持ち \"proud and elated\". Related is 晴れ \"festive\" as in 晴れ着 \"formal clothes\" and 晴れ舞台 \"big moment\". Depending on context it also expresses the embarrassed feeling of standing in the limelight (晴れがましくて落ち着かない). Slightly elevated; in daily life one rather says 誇らしい or 華やか.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は華々しい経歴の持ち主だ。","romaji":"Kare wa hanabanashii keireki no mochinushi da.","german":"He has a brilliant career.","furigana":"かれ|はなばな|けいれき|も|ぬし"},{"japanese":"その新人歌手は華々しいデビューを飾った。","romaji":"Sono shinjin kashu wa hanabanashii debyū o kazatta.","german":"The new singer made a dazzling debut.","furigana":"しんじんかしゅ|はなばな|かざ"},{"japanese":"彼は日本代表として華々しく活躍した。","romaji":"Kare wa Nihon daihyō to shite hanabanashiku katsuyaku shita.","german":"He played a brilliant role as a member of the Japan national team.","furigana":"かれ|にほんだいひょう|はなばな|かつやく"}],"notes":"華々しい means \"glorious, spectacular, sensational\" and describes successes, appearances and careers that attract much attention: 華々しい活躍, 華々しいデビュー, 華々しい経歴. It often stands adverbially: 華々しく登場する. Compared with 華やか \"gorgeous, colorful\", which describes more the outward appearance, 華々しい stresses achievement and effect. In 華々しく散る \"to fall gloriously\" it stands for a glorious end.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"清らかな泉の水を飲んだ。","romaji":"Kiyoraka na izumi no mizu o nonda.","german":"I drank water from a clear spring.","furigana":"きよ|いずみ|みず|の"},{"japanese":"少女の清らかな歌声が教会に響いた。","romaji":"Shōjo no kiyoraka na utagoe ga kyōkai ni hibiita.","german":"The girl's pure singing voice echoed through the church.","furigana":"しょうじょ|きよ|うたごえ|きょうかい|ひび"},{"japanese":"彼は清らかな心の持ち主で、人を疑うことを知らない。","romaji":"Kare wa kiyoraka na kokoro no mochinushi de, hito o utagau koto o shiranai.","german":"He has a pure heart and does not know what it is to doubt people.","furigana":"かれ|きよ|こころ|も|ぬし|ひと|うたが|し"}],"notes":"清らか is a na-adjective for \"pure, clear, unsullied\". Concretely it describes clear water and clean air (清らかな流れ, 清らかな空気), figuratively a pure, innocent soul or love (清らかな心, 清らかな愛). It sounds poetic and elevated; in daily speech one says きれい or 澄んだ for water. Compared with 清潔 \"clean, hygienic\", it is not about hygiene but about purity in an aesthetic or moral sense.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"痛ましい事故の報道に胸が痛んだ。","romaji":"Itamashii jiko no hōdō ni mune ga itanda.","german":"The report of the tragic accident pained my heart.","furigana":"いた|じこ|ほうどう|むね|いた"},{"japanese":"このような痛ましい事故が二度と起きないよう、安全対策を徹底します。","romaji":"Kono yō na itamashii jiko ga nido to okinai yō, anzen taisaku o tettei shimasu.","german":"We will thoroughly implement safety measures so that such a tragic accident never happens again.","furigana":"いた|じこ|にど|お|あんぜんたいさく|てってい"},{"japanese":"幼い命が奪われた痛ましい事件に、町中が悲しみに包まれた。","romaji":"Osanai inochi ga ubawareta itamashii jiken ni, machijū ga kanashimi ni tsutsumareta.","german":"The whole town was wrapped in sorrow over the heartbreaking incident in which a young life was taken.","furigana":"おさな|いのち|うば|いた|じけん|まちじゅう|かな|つつ"}],"notes":"痛ましい judges an event as \"tragic, heartrending\", especially accidents, crimes and disasters with victims: 痛ましい事故, 痛ましい事件, 痛ましい犠牲. It is typical of news and official statements (このような痛ましい事故が二度と起きないよう…). Unlike 痛々しい, which describes the pitiable sight of a person, it concerns the event as a whole. Similar but more drastic is 悲惨 \"dreadful, disastrous\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"目紛しく変わる世の中についていけない。","romaji":"Memagurushiku kawaru yononaka ni tsuite ikenai.","german":"I cannot keep up with the rapidly changing world.","furigana":"めまぐる|か|よ|なか"},{"japanese":"引っ越しと転職が重なり、めまぐるしい一か月だった。","romaji":"Hikkoshi to tenshoku ga kasanari, memagurushii ikkagetsu datta.","german":"With a move and a job change at the same time, it was a hectic month.","furigana":"ひ|こ|てんしょく|かさ|いっ|げつ"},{"japanese":"試合は点の取り合いになり、リードがめまぐるしく入れ替わった。","romaji":"Shiai wa ten no toriai ni nari, rīdo ga memagurushiku irekawatta.","german":"The game turned into a scoring duel, and the lead changed hands dizzyingly.","furigana":"しあい|てん|と|あ|い|か"}],"notes":"目紛しい (literally something like \"so that everything spins before one's eyes\") describes changes or sequences that follow each other so fast that one can hardly keep up: 目紛しい変化, 目紛しく変わる, 目紛しい一日. It is almost always written in hiragana (めまぐるしい); the kanji spelling is rare. Unlike 忙しい \"busy\", it describes not one's own workload but the rapid pace of events; 急速 \"rapid\" is more matter-of-fact and less emotional.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"居丈高な態度で部下に命令した。","romaji":"Itakedaka na taido de buka ni meirei shita.","german":"He gave orders to his subordinates in a domineering manner.","furigana":"いたけだか|たいど|ぶか|めいれい"},{"japanese":"客が店員に居丈高に文句を言っていた。","romaji":"Kyaku ga ten'in ni itakedaka ni monku o itte ita.","german":"A customer was complaining to the shop assistant in an overbearing way.","furigana":"きゃく|てんいん|いたけだか|もんく|い"},{"japanese":"相手が居丈高に出てきても、冷静に対応しよう。","romaji":"Aite ga itakedaka ni dete kite mo, reisei ni taiō shiyō.","german":"Even if the other side comes on overbearing, let's respond calmly.","furigana":"あいて|いたけだか|で|れいせい|たいおう"}],"notes":"居丈高 describes an overbearing, intimidating manner in which someone barks at others from above: 居丈高な態度, 居丈高に命令する, 居丈高に出る. The image is of sitting up tall to look down on others; it is also written 威丈高. It is a rather literary, clearly critical word; in daily speech one says 偉そう or 上から目線. Closely related is 高飛車, which particularly stresses the imperious tone that tolerates no contradiction.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"高飛車な物言いに皆が反感を持った。","romaji":"Takabisha na monoii ni mina ga hankan o motta.","german":"Everyone resented his high-handed way of talking.","furigana":"たかびしゃ|ものい|みな|はんかん|も"},{"japanese":"取引先に高飛車に出たら、交渉が決裂してしまった。","romaji":"Torihikisaki ni takabisha ni detara, kōshō ga ketsuretsu shite shimatta.","german":"When I came on strong with the business partner, the negotiations broke down.","furigana":"とりひきさき|たかびしゃ|で|こうしょう|けつれつ"},{"japanese":"彼女は美人だが、高飛車なところがあって近寄りがたい。","romaji":"Kanojo wa bijin da ga, takabisha na tokoro ga atte chikayorigatai.","german":"She is beautiful, but she has a haughty side that makes her hard to approach.","furigana":"かのじょ|びじん|たかびしゃ|ちかよ"}],"notes":"高飛車 comes from 将棋 (Japanese chess): the rook (飛車) is moved far forward to put pressure on offensively. From this arises the meaning \"imperious, from above\": 高飛車な態度, 高飛車な物言い, 高飛車に出る \"to come on strong, to use pressure\". It stresses that someone forces their will on others; 居丈高 stresses more the intimidating, bombastic manner. Colloquially it is now also used for snooty, arrogant people.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"差し出がましいようですが一言申し上げます。","romaji":"Sashidegamashii yō desu ga hitokoto mōshiagemasu.","german":"This may be presumptuous of me, but let me say a word.","furigana":"さ|で|ひとこともう|あ"},{"japanese":"差し出がましいとは存じますが、この資料の数字をもう一度ご確認いただけますでしょうか。","romaji":"Sashidegamashii to wa zonjimasu ga, kono shiryō no sūji o mō ichido go-kakunin itadakemasu deshō ka.","german":"I realize it is presumptuous, but could you please check the figures in this document once more?","furigana":"さ|で|ぞん|しりょう|すうじ|いちど|かくにん"},{"japanese":"新人のくせに差し出がましい口をきくな、と怒られた。","romaji":"Shinjin no kuse ni sashidegamashii kuchi o kiku na, to okorareta.","german":"I was scolded for speaking out of turn, being only a newcomer.","furigana":"しんじん|さ|で|くち|おこ"}],"notes":"差し出がましい describes behavior in which one meddles unasked in other people's affairs or oversteps one's role: 差し出がましい口をきく, 差し出がましいことを言う. It is very often used as a humble introduction before advice or an objection to a superior: 差し出がましいようですが…, 差し出がましいとは存じますが… \"forgive me for interfering\". Related is お節介 \"intrusive helpfulness\", which is more colloquial; 差し出がましい sounds more formal.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"戦争の悲惨な現実を伝える写真だ。","romaji":"Sensō no hisan na genjitsu o tsutaeru shashin da.","german":"This is a photograph that conveys the tragic reality of war.","furigana":"せんそう|ひさん|げんじつ|つた|しゃしん"},{"japanese":"大雨で村は悲惨な状況に陥った。","romaji":"Ōame de mura wa hisan na jōkyō ni ochiitta.","german":"The village fell into a terrible situation because of the heavy rain.","furigana":"おおあめ|むら|ひさん|じょうきょう|おちい"},{"japanese":"昨日の数学のテスト、結果は悲惨だったよ。","romaji":"Kinō no sūgaku no tesuto, kekka wa hisan datta yo.","german":"The result of yesterday's math test was dismal.","furigana":"きのう|すうがく|けっか|ひさん"}],"notes":"悲惨 (な) describes conditions or events so bad that they arouse pity and horror: 悲惨な事故, 悲惨な現実, 悲惨な状況, 戦争の悲惨さ. In everyday speech it is also used in exaggeration for personal mishaps: テストの結果が悲惨だった \"disastrous\". Unlike 悲しい \"sad\", it does not denote a feeling but the objective severity of the situation; 凄惨 and 無惨 stress the gruesome sight even more.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"凄惨な事件現場に警察が駆けつけた。","romaji":"Seisan na jiken genba ni keisatsu ga kaketsuketa.","german":"The police rushed to the gruesome crime scene.","furigana":"せいさん|じけんげんば|けいさつ|か"},{"japanese":"生存者は当時の凄惨な体験を静かに語った。","romaji":"Seizonsha wa tōji no seisan na taiken o shizuka ni katatta.","german":"The survivor quietly recounted the horrific experience of that time.","furigana":"せいぞんしゃ|とうじ|せいさん|たいけん|しず|かた"},{"japanese":"戦場は凄惨を極めていた。","romaji":"Senjō wa seisan o kiwamete ita.","german":"The battlefield was horrific in the extreme.","furigana":"せんじょう|せいさん|きわ"}],"notes":"凄惨 (な) means \"gruesome, ghastly\" and describes scenes full of blood and violence: 凄惨な事件, 凄惨な現場, 凄惨な光景, 凄惨を極める. It is a literary word, typical of news, court reports and war accounts. Compared with 悲惨 \"tragic, wretched\", the emphasis is on the dreadful, often bloody sight; it is not used for everyday mishaps.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"苛烈な競争を勝ち抜いた。","romaji":"Karetsu na kyōsō o kachinuita.","german":"He came through the fierce competition.","furigana":"かれつ|きょうそう|か|ぬ"},{"japanese":"両軍の戦闘は日に日に苛烈さを増していった。","romaji":"Ryōgun no sentō wa hi ni hi ni karetsusa o mashite itta.","german":"The fighting between the two armies grew more ferocious day by day.","furigana":"りょうぐん|せんとう|ひ|ひ|かれつ|ま"},{"japanese":"南極の苛烈な環境の中で、隊員たちは一年を過ごした。","romaji":"Nankyoku no karetsu na kankyō no naka de, taiintachi wa ichinen o sugoshita.","german":"The crew spent a year in the harsh environment of Antarctica.","furigana":"なんきょく|かれつ|かんきょう|なか|たいいん|いちねん|す"}],"notes":"苛烈 (な) means \"mercilessly harsh, extremely intense\" and stands for competition, fighting or conditions that know no mercy: 苛烈な競争, 苛烈な戦い, 苛烈な環境, 苛烈を極める. It is literary and occurs in business and sports reports or historical texts. Similar is 熾烈 \"fierce\", used above all for competitions; 苛烈 additionally stresses harshness and mercilessness.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"壮絶な戦いの末に勝利した。","romaji":"Sōzetsu na tatakai no sue ni shōri shita.","german":"We won after a fierce battle.","furigana":"そうぜつ|たたか|すえ|しょうり"},{"japanese":"彼女は病気と闘った壮絶な日々を本にまとめた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa byōki to tatakatta sōzetsu na hibi o hon ni matometa.","german":"She compiled her harrowing days of fighting illness into a book.","furigana":"かのじょ|びょうき|たたか|そうぜつ|ひび|ほん"},{"japanese":"子どもの頃、兄とは毎日壮絶なけんかをしていた。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, ani to wa mainichi sōzetsu na kenka o shite ita.","german":"As children, my brother and I had epic fights every day.","furigana":"こ|ころ|あに|まいにちそうぜつ"}],"notes":"壮絶 (な) describes something that exceeds all measures in intensity and drama: 壮絶な戦い, 壮絶な最期, 壮絶な人生, 壮絶な体験. Originally it carries admiration for courage and greatness (\"heroic\"); today it mainly stresses the extreme, shattering scale, for instance in battles, illness or blows of fate. Colloquially also in exaggeration: 壮絶な兄弟げんか. Compared with 凄絶, which highlights the gruesome, 壮絶 sounds more like heroic grandeur.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"醜悪な権力争いが繰り広げられた。","romaji":"Shūaku na kenryoku arasoi ga kurihirogerareta.","german":"A hideous power struggle unfolded.","furigana":"しゅうあく|けんりょくあらそ|く|ひろ"},{"japanese":"酒に酔った彼は人間の醜悪な本性をさらけ出した。","romaji":"Sake ni yotta kare wa ningen no shūaku na honshō o sarakedashita.","german":"Drunk, he exposed the ugly true nature of humanity.","furigana":"さけ|よ|かれ|にんげん|しゅうあく|ほんしょう|だ"},{"japanese":"駅前に建った醜悪なビルが景観を台無しにしている。","romaji":"Ekimae ni tatta shūaku na biru ga keikan o dainashi ni shite iru.","german":"The hideous building erected in front of the station ruins the scenery.","furigana":"えきまえ|た|しゅうあく|けいかん|だいな"}],"notes":"醜悪 (な) means \"repulsively ugly\", outwardly but above all morally: 醜悪な争い, 醜悪な本性, 醜悪な姿をさらす. It is a literary, sharply judgmental word expressing contempt. For merely unattractive looks one rather says 醜い or 見苦しい; 醜悪 denotes something downright revolting, such as greed, intrigue or hypocrisy.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"陰鬱な空模様が気分を沈ませた。","romaji":"In'utsu na soramoyō ga kibun o shizumaseta.","german":"The gloomy sky dampened my mood.","furigana":"いんうつ|そらもよう|きぶん|しず"},{"japanese":"彼は会議の間ずっと陰鬱な表情を浮かべていた。","romaji":"Kare wa kaigi no aida zutto in'utsu na hyōjō o ukabete ita.","german":"He wore a gloomy expression throughout the meeting.","furigana":"かれ|かいぎ|あいだ|いんうつ|ひょうじょう|う"},{"japanese":"その小説は陰鬱な港町を舞台にしている。","romaji":"Sono shōsetsu wa in'utsu na minatomachi o butai ni shite iru.","german":"The novel is set in a gloomy port town.","furigana":"しょうせつ|いんうつ|みなとまち|ぶたい"}],"notes":"陰鬱 (な) describes a gloomy, oppressive mood that weighs heavily on the mind: 陰鬱な天気, 陰鬱な表情, 陰鬱な雰囲気. It is rather literary; in everyday speech one says 暗い, どんよりした or 憂うつ. Compared with 憂鬱 \"depressed\", which mainly denotes one's own mood, 陰鬱 rather describes the atmosphere of weather, places or faces.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"朧月夜に桜が浮かび上がった。","romaji":"Oborozukiyo ni sakura ga ukabiagatta.","german":"Cherry blossoms stood out on a hazy moonlit night.","furigana":"おぼろづきよ|さくら|う|あ"},{"japanese":"霧の向こうに山の輪郭がおぼろに見えた。","romaji":"Kiri no mukō ni yama no rinkaku ga oboro ni mieta.","german":"The outline of the mountain could be seen dimly beyond the fog.","furigana":"きり|む|やま|りんかく|み"},{"japanese":"幼い頃の祖父の顔は、もうおぼろにしか思い出せない。","romaji":"Osanai koro no sofu no kao wa, mō oboro ni shika omoidasenai.","german":"I can now only vaguely recall my grandfather's face from my childhood.","furigana":"おさな|ころ|そふ|かお|おも|だ"}],"notes":"朧 (おぼろ) describes something blurred and shadowy, especially light in the haze of spring: 朧月 \"hazy moon\", 朧月夜 \"spring night with a misty moon\", a classic motif of poetry. As a na-adjective it stands for indistinct outlines or memories (おぼろに見える), mostly in hiragana. In everyday speech the derivative おぼろげ (おぼろげな記憶, おぼろげに覚えている) is much more common. As a noun, おぼろ in cooking also denotes seasoned, finely shredded flakes of fish or meat.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は名誉に恬淡とした人物だ。","romaji":"Kare wa meiyo ni tentan to shita jinbutsu da.","german":"He is a person indifferent to honors.","furigana":"かれ|めいよ|てんたん|じんぶつ"},{"japanese":"勝ち負けに恬淡とした態度が、かえって周りの信頼を集めた。","romaji":"Kachimake ni tentan to shita taido ga, kaette mawari no shinrai o atsumeta.","german":"His detached attitude toward winning and losing instead earned the trust of those around him.","furigana":"か|ま|てんたん|たいど|まわ|しんらい|あつ"},{"japanese":"晩年の祖父は無欲恬淡として、静かに暮らしていた。","romaji":"Bannen no sofu wa muyoku tentan to shite, shizuka ni kurashite ita.","german":"In his later years my grandfather lived quietly, free of desire and ambition.","furigana":"ばんねん|そふ|むよくてんたん|しず|く"}],"notes":"恬淡 describes a person who does not cling to fame, money or success and is not thrown off balance by them. Typical are 〜に恬淡としている, 名利に恬淡 \"indifferent to fame and profit\" and the phrase 無欲恬淡 \"selfless and content\". It is mostly used with と/たる (恬淡とした人, 恬淡と生きる) and is an elevated, literary compliment. Similar but closer to daily speech is 淡白 \"easygoing, not intense\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"斑模様の猫が日向ぼっこをしている。","romaji":"Madara moyō no neko ga hinatabokko o shite iru.","german":"A spotted cat is sunning itself.","furigana":"まだらもよう|ねこ|ひなた"},{"japanese":"春先の山には、まだ雪がまだらに残っている。","romaji":"Harusaki no yama ni wa, mada yuki ga madara ni nokotte iru.","german":"In early spring, snow still remains in patches on the mountains.","furigana":"はるさき|やま|ゆき|のこ"},{"japanese":"日焼け止めを塗り忘れて、肌がまだらに焼けてしまった。","romaji":"Hiyakedome o nuriwasurete, hada ga madara ni yakete shimatta.","german":"I forgot to apply sunscreen and my skin got tanned unevenly.","furigana":"ひや|ど|ぬ|わす|はだ|や"}],"notes":"まだら means \"spotted, mottled, uneven\" and is mostly written in hiragana: まだら模様, まだらの犬, まだらに日焼けする. It describes natural patterns in animals and plants as well as unwanted unevenness, for instance in color or snow (雪がまだらに残る). The kanji 斑 has other readings: ぶち \"piebald (animal coat)\" and むら \"unevenness\" (色むら); which one is meant depends on context.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"無惨な光景が目の前に広がった。","romaji":"Muzan na kōkei ga me no mae ni hirogatta.","german":"A gruesome sight spread out before me.","furigana":"むざん|こうけい|め|まえ|ひろ"},{"japanese":"台風のあと、自慢の庭は見るも無惨な姿になっていた。","romaji":"Taifū no ato, jiman no niwa wa miru mo muzan na sugata ni natte ita.","german":"After the typhoon, his proud garden was in a pitiful state.","furigana":"たいふう|じまん|にわ|み|むざん|すがた"},{"japanese":"優勝候補のチームが無惨にも初戦で敗れた。","romaji":"Yūshō kōho no chīmu ga muzan ni mo shosen de yabureta.","german":"The favored team was miserably defeated in the first match.","furigana":"ゆうしょうこうほ|むざん|しょせん|やぶ"}],"notes":"無惨 (な) has two sides: \"cruel, merciless\" with regard to a deed (無惨に殺される), but above all \"gruesome, pitifully mangled\" for the result of destruction: 無惨な姿, 無惨な光景, 見るも無惨 \"a wretched sight\". Figuratively also for defeats: 無惨な敗北, 無惨にも敗れる. It is also written 無残. Compared with 悲惨 \"tragic, wretched (situation)\", 無惨 highlights the shocking state of what has been destroyed.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"試験の結果は惨憺たるものだった。","romaji":"Shiken no kekka wa santan taru mono datta.","german":"The exam result was dismal.","furigana":"しけん|けっか|さんたん"},{"japanese":"地震の後、町は惨憺たる有様だった。","romaji":"Jishin no ato, machi wa santan taru arisama datta.","german":"After the earthquake, the town was in a dreadful state.","furigana":"じしん|あと|まち|さんたん|ありさま"},{"japanese":"苦心惨憺の末、ようやく新製品が完成した。","romaji":"Kushin santan no sue, yōyaku shinseihin ga kansei shita.","german":"After great pains, the new product was finally completed.","furigana":"くしんさんたん|すえ|しんせいひん|かんせい"}],"notes":"惨憺 is a たる-adjective and appears almost only in two set patterns: 惨憺たる \"disastrous, wretched\" (惨憺たる結果, 惨憺たる有様, 惨憺たるものだった) and 苦心惨憺 \"with the greatest effort\". In the first sense it judges results or conditions as completely failed or devastating; in the second it describes laborious effort. It is literary and in conversation sounds elevated or humorously exaggerated.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"峻厳な裁きが下された。","romaji":"Shungen na sabaki ga kudasareta.","german":"A severe judgment was handed down.","furigana":"しゅんげん|さば|くだ"},{"japanese":"祖父は自分にも他人にも峻厳な人だった。","romaji":"Sofu wa jibun ni mo tanin ni mo shungen na hito datta.","german":"My grandfather was strict with himself and with others.","furigana":"そふ|じぶん|たにん|しゅんげん|ひと"},{"japanese":"その修道院では峻厳な戒律が守られている。","romaji":"Sono shūdōin de wa shungen na kairitsu ga mamorarete iru.","german":"Strict precepts are observed in that monastery.","furigana":"しゅうどういん|しゅんげん|かいりつ|まも"}],"notes":"峻厳 (な) means \"relentlessly strict, tolerating no leniency\" and refers to attitudes, judgments, rules or personalities: 峻厳な態度, 峻厳な裁き, 峻厳な規律. The first kanji 峻 \"steep, rugged\" gives the word a lofty, unapproachable hardness. It is very literary; in daily speech one says 厳しい or 厳格. Compared with 峻烈, which rather stresses the sharpness of criticism or attacks, 峻厳 denotes a lasting, principled strictness.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"峻烈な批判を浴びた。","romaji":"Shunretsu na hihan o abita.","german":"He came under severe criticism.","furigana":"しゅんれつ|ひはん|あ"},{"japanese":"評論家はその映画を峻烈な言葉で酷評した。","romaji":"Hyōronka wa sono eiga o shunretsu na kotoba de kokuhyō shita.","german":"The critic panned the film in scathing words.","furigana":"ひょうろんか|えいが|しゅんれつ|ことば|こくひょう"},{"japanese":"野党は政府の対応を峻烈に追及した。","romaji":"Yatō wa seifu no taiō o shunretsu ni tsuikyū shita.","german":"The opposition parties pursued the government's response relentlessly.","furigana":"やとう|せいふ|たいおう|しゅんれつ|ついきゅう"}],"notes":"峻烈 (な) means \"sharp, cutting, relentless\" and is used above all for criticism, attacks or commentary: 峻烈な批判, 峻烈な論調, 峻烈を極める. It is a literary word from the press, scholarship and literature. Compared with the more common 痛烈 \"biting, scathing\", 峻烈 sounds more elevated and stresses uncompromising severity; 峻厳 denotes a strict attitude rather than an attack.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"凄絶な最期を遂げた武将の話だ。","romaji":"Seizetsu na saigo o togeta bushō no hanashi da.","german":"It is the story of a warlord who met a harrowing end.","furigana":"せいぜつ|さいご|と|ぶしょう|はなし"},{"japanese":"二人のボクサーは凄絶な打ち合いを演じた。","romaji":"Futari no bokusā wa seizetsu na uchiai o enjita.","german":"The two boxers staged a fierce slugfest.","furigana":"ふたり|せいぜつ|う|あ|えん"},{"japanese":"被災者の凄絶な体験談に、言葉を失った。","romaji":"Hisaisha no seizetsu na taikendan ni, kotoba o ushinatta.","german":"I was speechless at the disaster victim's harrowing account.","furigana":"ひさいしゃ|せいぜつ|たいけんだん|ことば|うしな"}],"notes":"凄絶 (な) means \"terribly intense, ghastly-extreme\" and describes fights, death scenes or experiences whose harshness takes your breath away: 凄絶な戦い, 凄絶な最期, 凄絶な体験. It is literary and occurs mainly in narratives, historical accounts and reports. Compared with the similar-sounding 壮絶, which stresses heroic grandeur, 凄絶 highlights the gruesome, terrible; 凄惨 relates more to a bloody sight.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"僭越ながら私の意見を述べさせていただきます。","romaji":"Sen'etsu nagara watashi no iken o nobesasete itadakimasu.","german":"If I may be so presumptuous, let me state my opinion.","furigana":"せんえつ|わたし|いけん|の"},{"japanese":"僭越ながら、乾杯の音頭を取らせていただきます。","romaji":"Sen'etsu nagara, kanpai no ondo o torasete itadakimasu.","german":"If I may be so bold, I will lead the toast.","furigana":"せんえつ|かんぱい||と"},{"japanese":"新人が社長に意見するなど僭越だと思われるかもしれない。","romaji":"Shinjin ga shachō ni iken suru nado sen'etsu da to omowareru kamo shirenai.","german":"It may be considered presumptuous for a newcomer to give his opinion to the company president.","furigana":"しんじん|しゃちょう|いけん|せんえつ|おも"}],"notes":"僭越 means \"presumptuous, taking something upon oneself that is not one's place\". It almost always appears in the humble introductory formula 僭越ながら \"if I may take the liberty\", with which one downplays one's role in speeches, toasts or remarks; similar are 僭越ですが or 僭越至極. As criticism of others (僭越な振る舞い) it is rarer and very formal. Related is 差し出がましい, which refers more to interfering in other people's affairs.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"老婆心ながら一つ忠告しておく。","romaji":"Rōbashin nagara hitotsu chūkoku shite oku.","german":"Though it may be overly fussy of me, let me give you one piece of advice.","furigana":"ろうばしん|ひと|ちゅうこく"},{"japanese":"老婆心から申し上げますが、契約書は必ず隅々まで読んでください。","romaji":"Rōbashin kara mōshiagemasu ga, keiyakusho wa kanarazu sumizumi made yonde kudasai.","german":"I say this out of concern, but please be sure to read the contract thoroughly.","furigana":"ろうばしん|もう|あ|けいやくしょ|かなら|すみずみ|よ"},{"japanese":"先輩の言葉は老婆心だとわかっていても、少しうるさく感じた。","romaji":"Senpai no kotoba wa rōbashin da to wakatte ite mo, sukoshi urusaku kanjita.","german":"Even though I knew my senior's words came from concern, they felt a little nagging.","furigana":"せんぱい|ことば|ろうばしん|すこ|かん"}],"notes":"老婆心 (literally \"the heart of an old woman\") denotes a perhaps exaggerated but well-meaning solicitude. It almost always appears in the humble introduction 老婆心ながら \"even if I may be worrying too much\" before unsolicited advice, mostly to younger people; likewise 老婆心から言うと. It is a noun and is used regardless of the speaker's gender. Related is お節介, which sounds more negative and criticizes the interference itself.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"出しゃばりな性格のせいで、彼は周りから嫌われている。","romaji":"Deshabari na seikaku no sei de, kare wa mawari kara kirawarete iru.","german":"Because of his pushy personality, he is disliked by those around him.","furigana":"で|せいかく|かれ|まわ|きら"},{"japanese":"出しゃばりだと思われたくないので、会議では黙っていた。","romaji":"Deshabari da to omowaretaku nai node, kaigi de wa damatte ita.","german":"I did not want to be thought pushy, so I stayed quiet in the meeting.","furigana":"で|おも|かいぎ|だま"},{"japanese":"「余計なことするな、この出しゃばり！」と姉に怒られた。","romaji":"\"Yokei na koto suru na, kono deshabari!\" to ane ni okorareta.","german":"\"Stop butting in, you busybody!\" my older sister scolded me.","furigana":"よけい|で|あね|おこ"}],"notes":"出しゃばり is the noun for the verb 出しゃばる \"to push oneself forward, to meddle unasked\" and denotes both this behavior and the person: 出しゃばりな人, あの出しゃばり. It is colloquial and clearly pejorative; often in hiragana (でしゃばり). Typical are 出しゃばりだと思われる and 出しゃばったまねをする. Compared with お節介, which means too much solicitude, 出しゃばり stresses pushing oneself into the foreground; 差し出がましい is the formal equivalent.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"徒然なるままに日記を書いた。","romaji":"Tsurezure naru mama ni nikki o kaita.","german":"In idle hours, I wrote in my diary.","furigana":"つれづれ|にっき|か"},{"japanese":"雨の日のつれづれに、昔のアルバムを眺めた。","romaji":"Ame no hi no tsurezure ni, mukashi no arubamu o nagameta.","german":"On a rainy day, to pass the time, I looked through old albums.","furigana":"あめ|ひ|むかし|なが"},{"japanese":"入院中のつれづれを慰めようと、友人が本を持ってきてくれた。","romaji":"Nyūinchū no tsurezure o nagusameyō to, yūjin ga hon o motte kite kureta.","german":"To ease the boredom of my hospital stay, a friend brought me books.","furigana":"にゅういんちゅう|なぐさ|ゆうじん|ほん|も"}],"notes":"徒然 (usually つれづれ in kana) denotes the state of having nothing to do: leisure, boredom, time passing quietly. The opening of the classic essay 徒然草 is famous: つれづれなるままに \"in idle hours, as things come to mind\". Today the word sounds literary and slightly nostalgic: 雨の日のつれづれに, 老後のつれづれを慰める, つれづれ日記. In everyday speech one says 暇 or 退屈; つれづれ has a more poetic, pleasantly melancholy ring.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"参加者は夫々の意見を述べた。","romaji":"Sankasha wa sorezore no iken o nobeta.","german":"The participants each stated their opinions.","furigana":"さんかしゃ|それぞれ|いけん|の"},{"japanese":"兄弟でも、性格はそれぞれ違う。","romaji":"Kyōdai demo, seikaku wa sorezore chigau.","german":"Even siblings each have different personalities.","furigana":"きょうだい|せいかく|ちが"},{"japanese":"二人はそれぞれ東京と大阪に住んでいる。","romaji":"Futari wa sorezore Tōkyō to Ōsaka ni sunde iru.","german":"The two live in Tokyo and Osaka respectively.","furigana":"ふたり|とうきょう|おおさか|す"}],"notes":"夫々 is a rare kanji spelling of それぞれ \"each, individually\"; today it is almost always written in kana, occasionally 其々. As a noun it takes の before nouns (それぞれの意見), as an adverb it stands directly before the predicate (それぞれ違う), also in the sense of \"respectively\". Compared with 各自 \"each individual\" (people only), それぞれ can also refer to things; 各々 is more formal. 夫 stands here for the demonstrative それ, a spelling from kanbun; on its own it is read おっと \"husband\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"各々が自分の役割を果たすべきだ。","romaji":"Onoono ga jibun no yakuwari o hatasu beki da.","german":"Each person should fulfill his or her own role.","furigana":"おのおの|じぶん|やくわり|は"},{"japanese":"昼食は各々でご用意ください。","romaji":"Chūshoku wa onoono de go-yōi kudasai.","german":"Please prepare your own lunch individually.","furigana":"ちゅうしょく|おのおの|ようい"},{"japanese":"参加者は各々の意見を自由に述べた。","romaji":"Sankasha wa onoono no iken o jiyū ni nobeta.","german":"The participants freely expressed their respective opinions.","furigana":"さんかしゃ|おのおの|いけん|じゆう|の"}],"notes":"各々 (often おのおの in kana) means \"each individual, respectively\" and is a more elevated, somewhat more formal equivalent of それぞれ. It appears as a noun (各々が, 各々の意見) or adverbially (各々準備する). It is typical in announcements and instructions to a group: 昼食は各々でご用意ください. In everyday speech それぞれ is much more common; the archaic meaning \"you\" (addressing several people) is known almost only from samurai dramas.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"計画は往々にして予定通りにいかない。","romaji":"Keikaku wa ōō ni shite yotei dōri ni ikanai.","german":"Plans often do not go as scheduled.","furigana":"けいかく|おうおう|よていどお"},{"japanese":"第一印象は往々にして当てにならない。","romaji":"Daiichi inshō wa ōō ni shite ate ni naranai.","german":"First impressions are often unreliable.","furigana":"だいいちいんしょう|おうおう|あ"},{"japanese":"大きな事故は、往々にして小さなミスの積み重ねから起こる。","romaji":"Ōki na jiko wa, ōō ni shite chiisa na misu no tsumikasane kara okoru.","german":"Major accidents often arise from an accumulation of small mistakes.","furigana":"おお|じこ|おうおう|ちい|つ|かさ|お"}],"notes":"往々にして means \"often, not infrequently\" and describes a general tendency, usually an undesirable one: 〜は往々にして〜するものだ, 往々にしてある. It is literary and sounds like experience or analysis. Unlike よく or しばしば, it does not refer to concrete repeated events but to what experience shows often happens; similar is とかく \"to tend to\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人は兎角噂話が好きなものだ。","romaji":"Hito wa tokaku uwasabanashi ga suki na mono da.","german":"People tend to love gossip.","furigana":"ひと|とかくうわさばなし|す"},{"japanese":"忙しいと、とかく食事がおろそかになりがちだ。","romaji":"Isogashii to, tokaku shokuji ga orosoka ni narigachi da.","german":"When busy, meals tend to get neglected.","furigana":"いそが|しょくじ"},{"japanese":"あの政治家には、とかくの噂がつきまとう。","romaji":"Ano seijika ni wa, tokaku no uwasa ga tsukimatou.","german":"All sorts of rumors surround that politician.","furigana":"せいじか|うわさ"}],"notes":"とかく (兎角, usually in kana) has several uses. The most common is \"to tend to, easily\": 人はとかく〜しがちだ, often combined with 〜がち or 〜やすい. Second, \"all sorts of (negative things)\": とかくの噂がある \"there are all sorts of rumors\", and \"this and that\" in とかくするうちに. Rarely also \"anyway\" (like とにかく). The kanji 兎角 are an ateji spelling (literally \"hare's horn\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"如何にして問題を解決するかが重要だ。","romaji":"Ika ni shite mondai o kaiketsu suru ka ga jūyō da.","german":"How to solve the problem is what matters.","furigana":"いか|もんだい|かいけつ|じゅうよう"},{"japanese":"病気になって、健康がいかに大切かを思い知った。","romaji":"Byōki ni natte, kenkō ga ika ni taisetsu ka o omoishitta.","german":"Falling ill made me realize how important health is.","furigana":"びょうき|けんこう|たいせつ|おも|し"},{"japanese":"いかに優秀な人でも、一人でできることには限りがある。","romaji":"Ika ni yūshū na hito demo, hitori de dekiru koto ni wa kagiri ga aru.","german":"However talented a person is, there is a limit to what one can do alone.","furigana":"ゆうしゅう|ひと|ひとり|かぎ"}],"notes":"いかに (如何に, usually in kana) is the elevated counterpart of どのように and どれほど. It has three main meanings: \"how, in what way\" (いかにして解決するか), \"how much\" (いかに大切かがわかる) and, with 〜ても, \"however much\": いかに努力しても. It is typical of speeches, essays and nonfiction. Not to be confused with いかにも \"really, quite typical\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"甚だ遺憾ながら計画は中止となった。","romaji":"Hanahada ikan nagara keikaku wa chūshi to natta.","german":"It is extremely regrettable, but the plan has been cancelled.","furigana":"はなは|いかん|けいかく|ちゅうし"},{"japanese":"夜中の騒音は甚だ迷惑だ。","romaji":"Yonaka no sōon wa hanahada meiwaku da.","german":"Noise in the middle of the night is a great nuisance.","furigana":"よなか|そうおん|はなは|めいわく"},{"japanese":"甚だ簡単ではございますが、これをもちましてご挨拶とさせていただきます。","romaji":"Hanahada kantan de wa gozaimasu ga, kore o mochimashite go-aisatsu to sasete itadakimasu.","german":"Though very brief, with this I will conclude my greeting.","furigana":"はなは|かんたん|あいさつ"}],"notes":"甚だ is a formal degree adverb for \"extremely, highly\" and almost always stands before negatively evaluative words: 甚だ遺憾, 甚だ迷惑, 甚だ疑問だ, 甚だ残念. In speeches and letters it also serves as a humble formula: 甚だ簡単ではございますが \"though only very briefly\". In everyday speech one says とても or 非常に. Derived from it is the adjective 甚だしい \"excessive, glaring\" (勘違いも甚だしい).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"学生は須く勉学に励むべし。","romaji":"Gakusei wa subekaraku bengaku ni hagemu beshi.","german":"Students ought by all means to devote themselves to their studies.","furigana":"がくせい|すべから|べんがく|はげ"},{"japanese":"政治家たる者、須く国民の声に耳を傾けるべきだ。","romaji":"Seijika taru mono, subekaraku kokumin no koe ni mimi o katamukeru beki da.","german":"Those who are politicians should by all means listen to the voice of the people.","furigana":"せいじか|もの|すべから|こくみん|こえ|みみ|かたむ"},{"japanese":"研究者は須く、データを誠実に扱うべきである。","romaji":"Kenkyūsha wa subekaraku, dēta o seijitsu ni atsukau beki de aru.","german":"Researchers should, without exception, handle data honestly.","furigana":"けんきゅうしゃ|すべから|せいじつ|あつか"}],"notes":"須く (すべからく) comes from kanbun and is linked with べし or べきだ: 〜はすべからく〜べきだ \"should by all means, naturally has to ...\". It is an elevated, didactic word in speeches, editorials and essays; usually written in kana. Common, but wrong by the traditional view, is its use in the sense of すべて \"all, without exception\" (若者はすべからくスマホ中毒だ); JMdict lists this meaning as a secondary one, but learners should avoid it.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"恰も夢を見ているかのような光景だった。","romaji":"Atakamo yume o mite iru ka no yō na kōkei datta.","german":"It was a scene as if I were dreaming.","furigana":"あたか|ゆめ|み|こうけい"},{"japanese":"彼はあたかも自分が考えたかのように、他人のアイデアを発表した。","romaji":"Kare wa atakamo jibun ga kangaeta ka no yō ni, tanin no aidea o happyō shita.","german":"He presented someone else's idea as if he had thought of it himself.","furigana":"かれ|じぶん|かんが|たにん|はっぴょう"},{"japanese":"記事は、噂をあたかも事実であるかのように報じていた。","romaji":"Kiji wa, uwasa o atakamo jijitsu de aru ka no yō ni hōjite ita.","german":"The article reported the rumor as if it were fact.","furigana":"きじ|うわさ|じじつ|ほう"}],"notes":"あたかも (恰も, usually in kana) introduces a comparison and means \"just as if\". It is almost always combined with 〜ように, 〜かのように or 〜ごとく: あたかも〜かのように振る舞う \"to act as if\". It is more literary than まるで, which is usual in conversation. A rare second use is \"right at that time\" (時あたかも…), almost only in literary texts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"個人の努力が延いては社会全体の発展に繋がる。","romaji":"Kojin no doryoku ga hiite wa shakai zentai no hatten ni tsunagaru.","german":"Individual effort leads to the development of society as a whole.","furigana":"こじん|どりょく|ひ|しゃかいぜんたい|はってん|つな"},{"japanese":"社員の健康を守ることは、ひいては会社の利益にもなる。","romaji":"Shain no kenkō o mamoru koto wa, hiite wa kaisha no rieki ni mo naru.","german":"Protecting employees' health ultimately benefits the company as well.","furigana":"しゃいん|けんこう|まも|かいしゃ|りえき"},{"japanese":"この一件は、彼の信用、ひいては会社全体の評判を傷つけた。","romaji":"Kono ikken wa, kare no shin'yō, hiite wa kaisha zentai no hyōban o kizutsuketa.","german":"This incident damaged his credibility and, by extension, the reputation of the whole company.","furigana":"いっけん|かれ|しんよう|かいしゃぜんたい|ひょうばん|きず"}],"notes":"ひいては (延いては, usually in kana) means \"and thus ultimately also, by extension\" and links a closer effect with a larger, further-reaching one: AがB、ひいてはCにつながる. Typical is the escalation from small to large: 個人、ひいては社会 \"the individual and thus also society\". It is literary and occurs in speeches, essays and reports. Unlike さらに \"moreover\", it does not simply add something but shows a chain of consequences.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"暫し黙想にふけった。","romaji":"Shibashi mokusō ni fuketta.","german":"I was lost in meditation for a while.","furigana":"しば|もくそう"},{"japanese":"「しばしお待ちください」とアナウンスが流れた。","romaji":"\"Shibashi omachi kudasai\" to anaunsu ga nagareta.","german":"\"Please wait a moment,\" an announcement played.","furigana":"ま|なが"},{"japanese":"美しい夕日にしばし見とれた。","romaji":"Utsukushii yūhi ni shibashi mitoreta.","german":"I was entranced by the beautiful sunset for a while.","furigana":"うつく|ゆうひ|み"}],"notes":"しばし is an elevated, slightly literary word for \"for a while, for a moment\"; in daily speech one mostly says しばらく. It is almost always written in kana; the kanji form 暫し is rare. Typical are set phrases such as しばしお待ちください (polite announcement), しばしの間 \"for a short time\", しばしの別れ \"a temporary parting\" and しばし沈黙する. Unlike しばらく, it never means \"not for a long time\" (しばらくぶり).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"愈々明日から新学期が始まる。","romaji":"Iyoiyo ashita kara shin gakki ga hajimaru.","german":"The new school term finally starts tomorrow.","furigana":"いよいよあした|しんがっき|はじ"},{"japanese":"夜になって、雨はいよいよ激しくなってきた。","romaji":"Yoru ni natte, ame wa iyoiyo hageshiku natte kita.","german":"As night fell, the rain grew heavier and heavier.","furigana":"よる|あめ|はげ"},{"japanese":"いよいよとなったら、親に相談するつもりだ。","romaji":"Iyoiyo to nattara, oya ni sōdan suru tsumori da.","german":"If it comes down to it, I intend to consult my parents.","furigana":"おや|そうだん"}],"notes":"いよいよ (mostly in kana, rarely 愈々) has two main meanings. 1. \"Finally, the time has come\" - for an event one has been eagerly awaiting that is now imminent: いよいよ明日だ. Unlike とうとう and ついに, which more often describe a result that has already occurred after a long time, いよいよ stresses tense anticipation. 2. \"More and more\" for intensification: いよいよ激しくなる (≈ ますます). The phrase いよいよとなったら means \"when it gets serious\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この問題は啻に経済だけの問題ではない。","romaji":"Kono mondai wa tada ni keizai dake no mondai de wa nai.","german":"This problem is not only an economic one.","furigana":"もんだい|ただ|けいざい|もんだい"},{"japanese":"環境破壊はただに一国の問題ではなく、人類全体の課題である。","romaji":"Kankyō hakai wa tada ni ikkoku no mondai de wa naku, jinrui zentai no kadai de aru.","german":"Environmental destruction is not just one country's problem but a challenge for all humankind.","furigana":"かんきょうはかい|いっこく|もんだい|じんるいぜんたい|かだい"},{"japanese":"彼の功績はただに学界のみならず、社会全体に及んだ。","romaji":"Kare no kōseki wa tada ni gakkai nominarazu, shakai zentai ni oyonda.","german":"His achievements extended not only to the academic world but to society as a whole.","furigana":"かれ|こうせき|がっかい|しゃかいぜんたい|およ"}],"notes":"ただに is a literary, almost archaic adverb and stands practically only in negating or extending frames: ただに〜のみならず \"not only ... but also\" or ただに〜だけではない. It reinforces the statement that something goes beyond the named domain. It is mostly written in kana; 啻に is very rare. In normal speech it corresponds to 単に〜だけでなく or ただ〜だけでなく.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"偶々通りかかって旧友に出会った。","romaji":"Tamatama tōrikakatte kyūyū ni deatta.","german":"I happened to pass by and ran into an old friend.","furigana":"たまたまとお|きゅうゆう|であ"},{"japanese":"昨日はたまたま家にいたので、荷物を受け取れた。","romaji":"Kinō wa tamatama ie ni ita node, nimotsu o uketoreta.","german":"I happened to be at home yesterday, so I was able to receive the package.","furigana":"きのう|いえ|にもつ|う|と"},{"japanese":"今回勝てたのは、たまたま運がよかっただけだ。","romaji":"Konkai kateta no wa, tamatama un ga yokatta dake da.","german":"Winning this time was just a matter of luck.","furigana":"こんかいか|うん"}],"notes":"たまたま (almost always in kana) means \"by chance, as luck would have it\" and stresses that something happened without intention or plan. The frame たまたま〜だけだ \"it was merely a coincidence\" is common, often to downplay success. 偶然 means something similar, but can also be a noun (偶然の一致); たまたま is only an adverb and sounds somewhat more colloquial. The meaning \"occasionally\" is rare; for that one normally says たまに.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の計画は尽く失敗した。","romaji":"Kare no keikaku wa kotogotoku shippai shita.","german":"All of his plans failed.","furigana":"かれ|けいかく|ことごと|しっぱい"},{"japanese":"私の提案はことごとく却下された。","romaji":"Watashi no teian wa kotogotoku kyakka sareta.","german":"Every one of my proposals was rejected.","furigana":"わたし|ていあん|きゃっか"},{"japanese":"台風で畑の作物がことごとくだめになった。","romaji":"Taifū de hatake no sakumotsu ga kotogotoku dame ni natta.","german":"The typhoon ruined all the crops in the fields.","furigana":"たいふう|はたけ|さくもつ"}],"notes":"ことごとく (usually in kana, also 悉く) means \"all, without exception\" and is more literary than すべて or 全部. It typically stands directly before the verb and is especially often used with negative results: ことごとく失敗する, ことごとく却下される, ことごとく外れる. This makes it sound emphatic, often frustrated. As a noun with の it is rare.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"然るべき処置を取るべきだ。","romaji":"Shikarubeki shochi o toru beki da.","german":"Appropriate measures should be taken.","furigana":"しか|しょち|と"},{"japanese":"この件はしかるべき機関に相談したほうがいい。","romaji":"Kono ken wa shikarubeki kikan ni sōdan shita hō ga ii.","german":"It would be better to consult the appropriate agency about this matter.","furigana":"けん|きかん|そうだん"},{"japanese":"会社側から謝罪があってしかるべきだ。","romaji":"Kaisha-gawa kara shazai ga atte shikarubeki da.","german":"The company ought to apologize.","furigana":"かいしゃがわ|しゃざい"}],"notes":"しかるべき (usually in kana) is not an adverb but an attributive word standing before nouns (連体詞): \"appropriate, due\" or \"competent\". Typical are しかるべき処置 \"appropriate measures\", しかるべき機関 \"the competent authority\" and しかるべき人 \"a suitable person\". Predicatively it stands in the pattern 〜てしかるべきだ \"it would only be right and fair that ...\". The adverbial form is しかるべく \"in an appropriate manner\". Register: formal, administrative and business language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"行方は杳として知れない。","romaji":"Yukue wa yō to shite shirenai.","german":"His whereabouts remain completely unknown.","furigana":"ゆくえ|よう|し"},{"japanese":"墜落した機体の消息は、今も杳としてつかめていない。","romaji":"Tsuiraku shita kitai no shōsoku wa, ima mo yō to shite tsukamete inai.","german":"The fate of the crashed aircraft is still unknown.","furigana":"ついらく|きたい|しょうそく|いま|よう"},{"japanese":"古代文明が滅んだ理由は杳として分からない。","romaji":"Kodai bunmei ga horonda riyū wa yō to shite wakaranai.","german":"The reason the ancient civilization perished remains entirely unknown.","furigana":"こだいぶんめい|ほろ|りゆう|よう|わ"}],"notes":"杳として is a literary expression from 杳 \"dark, distant, unfathomable\" and stands practically only before negated verbs of knowing: 杳として知れない, 杳として分からない, 杳としてつかめない. A typical subject is the unknown whereabouts of people, ships or aircraft (行方は杳として知れない) or an unsolved mystery. It is found in news and novels, not in conversation, where one says まったく分からない.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不正に対して毅然とした態度を取った。","romaji":"Fusei ni taishite kizen to shita taido o totta.","german":"He took a firm stand against the wrongdoing.","furigana":"ふせい|たい|きぜん|たいど|と"},{"japanese":"彼女は理不尽な要求を毅然と断った。","romaji":"Kanojo wa rifujin na yōkyū o kizen to kotowatta.","german":"She firmly refused the unreasonable demand.","furigana":"かのじょ|りふじん|ようきゅう|きぜん|ことわ"},{"japanese":"首相は記者の厳しい質問にも毅然として答えた。","romaji":"Shushō wa kisha no kibishii shitsumon ni mo kizen to shite kotaeta.","german":"The prime minister answered resolutely even the reporter's tough questions.","furigana":"しゅしょう|きしゃ|きび|しつもん|きぜん|こた"}],"notes":"毅然 describes a firm, unyielding attitude, especially toward pressure, injustice or outrageous demands. It is used like a たる-adjective: attributive 毅然とした態度 (the most common combination) or 毅然たる, adverbial 毅然と(して) 断る / 対応する. Similar is 断固, which stresses the determination of an action; colloquially one says きっぱり. Register: elevated, common in politics and news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"危機的状況でも泰然と構えていた。","romaji":"Kikiteki jōkyō demo taizen to kamaete ita.","german":"He remained composed even in a critical situation.","furigana":"ききてきじょうきょう|たいぜん|かま"},{"japanese":"祖父は何が起きても泰然としている人だった。","romaji":"Sofu wa nani ga okite mo taizen to shite iru hito datta.","german":"My grandfather was a man who stayed calm whatever happened.","furigana":"そふ|なに|お|たいぜん|ひと"},{"japanese":"周りが騒いでも、監督は泰然とした様子でベンチに座っていた。","romaji":"Mawari ga sawaide mo, kantoku wa taizen to shita yōsu de benchi ni suwatte ita.","german":"Even as those around him fussed, the manager sat on the bench looking unruffled.","furigana":"まわ|さわ|かんとく|たいぜん|ようす|すわ"}],"notes":"泰然 means \"calm, not to be rattled by anything\" and praises a masterful inner calm, especially in crises. Typical are 泰然と構える \"to wait calmly\", 泰然としている and 泰然とした態度; intensified in the four-character word 泰然自若. Compared with 冷静 (level-headed, cool) and 落ち着いた (calm), 泰然 sounds dignified and elevated. Grammatically a たる-adjective with adverbial 泰然と.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は世俗から超然として暮らしている。","romaji":"Kare wa sezoku kara chōzen to shite kurashite iru.","german":"He lives detached from worldly affairs.","furigana":"かれ|せぞく|ちょうぜん|く"},{"japanese":"派閥争いにも加わらず、彼は超然とした立場を貫いた。","romaji":"Habatsu arasoi ni mo kuwawarazu, kare wa chōzen to shita tachiba o tsuranuita.","german":"Not taking part in factional struggles, he maintained an aloof position.","furigana":"はばつあらそ|くわ|かれ|ちょうぜん|たちば|つらぬ"},{"japanese":"周りが流行を追う中、彼女だけは超然としていた。","romaji":"Mawari ga ryūkō o ou naka, kanojo dake wa chōzen to shite ita.","german":"While everyone around her chased trends, she alone stayed above it all.","furigana":"まわ|りゅうこう|お|なか|かのじょ|ちょうぜん"}],"notes":"超然 describes someone who \"stands above things\" and is not touched by worldly worries, strife or fashions. Typical are 〜から超然としている \"to keep aloof from ..., to stand above ...\" and 超然とした態度. Depending on context it sounds positive (masterful, unswayed) or negative (aloof, indifferent). Not to be confused with 超越する \"to transcend\", which is a true verb with を.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"会場が粛然と静まり返った。","romaji":"Kaijō ga shukuzen to shizumarikaetta.","german":"The hall fell solemnly silent.","furigana":"かいじょう|しゅくぜん|しず|かえ"},{"japanese":"慰霊碑の前に立つと、誰もが粛然とした気持ちになった。","romaji":"Ireihi no mae ni tatsu to, daremo ga shukuzen to shita kimochi ni natta.","german":"Standing before the memorial, everyone felt a reverent mood.","furigana":"いれいひ|まえ|た|だれ|しゅくぜん|きも"},{"japanese":"先人の苦労を知り、粛然と襟を正した。","romaji":"Senjin no kurō o shiri, shukuzen to eri o tadashita.","german":"Learning of the hardships of those who came before, I solemnly straightened myself.","furigana":"せんじん|くろう|し|しゅくぜん|えり|ただ"}],"notes":"粛然 describes a solemn, serious silence or reverence that arises in the face of something dignified or tragic. Typical are 粛然とする, 粛然と静まり返る \"to fall solemnly silent\" and the phrase 粛然と襟を正す \"to adopt a reverent posture, collect oneself inwardly\". Related is the more common 厳粛 \"solemn, grave\" (厳粛な式典). 粛然 is literary and occurs mainly in reports and literature.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"予想外の展開に唖然とした。","romaji":"Yosōgai no tenkai ni azen to shita.","german":"I was stunned by the unexpected turn of events.","furigana":"よそうがい|てんかい|あぜん"},{"japanese":"彼のあまりに非常識な発言に、一同唖然とした。","romaji":"Kare no amari ni hijōshiki na hatsugen ni, ichidō azen to shita.","german":"Everyone was dumbfounded by his utterly outrageous remark.","furigana":"かれ|ひじょうしき|はつげん|いちどうあぜん"},{"japanese":"請求書の金額を見て、唖然として言葉も出なかった。","romaji":"Seikyūsho no kingaku o mite, azen to shite kotoba mo denakatta.","german":"Seeing the amount on the invoice, I was too stunned to speak.","furigana":"せいきゅうしょ|きんがく|み|あぜん|ことば|で"}],"notes":"唖然 means \"speechless, flabbergasted\" from astonishment or disbelief, usually at something unbelievable or outrageous. It almost always stands as 唖然とする or 唖然として言葉も出ない. Since 唖 is not a jōyō kanji, the spelling あ然 is often seen. To distinguish: 呆然 is \"stunned, absent-minded\", 愕然 \"shocked by a bitter realization\", あきれる \"aghast, disgusted\" (more colloquial).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"事実を知って愕然とした。","romaji":"Jijitsu o shitte gakuzen to shita.","german":"I was appalled to learn the facts.","furigana":"じじつ|し|がくぜん"},{"japanese":"健康診断の結果を見て愕然とした。","romaji":"Kenkō shindan no kekka o mite gakuzen to shita.","german":"I was shocked when I saw the results of my health checkup.","furigana":"けんこうしんだん|けっか|み|がくぜん"},{"japanese":"久しぶりに故郷を訪れ、その変わりように愕然とした。","romaji":"Hisashiburi ni kokyō o otozure, sono kawariyō ni gakuzen to shita.","german":"Visiting my hometown for the first time in a long while, I was appalled at how much it had changed.","furigana":"ひさ|こきょう|おとず|か|がくぜん"}],"notes":"愕然 describes the shock when one suddenly recognizes something alarming or bitter, such as a truth, a bad diagnosis or great decay. It almost always stands as 愕然とする, often with the trigger in te-form or with に: 〜を知って愕然とする, その変わりように愕然とする. Unlike 唖然 (speechless with astonishment), 愕然 puts the accent on shock and consternation. Elevated written language, but also understood in conversation.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その恐ろしい話に慄然とした。","romaji":"Sono osoroshii hanashi ni ritsuzen to shita.","german":"I shuddered at the terrible story.","furigana":"おそ|はなし|りつぜん"},{"japanese":"一歩間違えば大事故になっていたと知り、慄然とした。","romaji":"Ippo machigaeba daijiko ni natte ita to shiri, ritsuzen to shita.","german":"I shuddered to learn that one wrong step would have caused a major accident.","furigana":"いっぽまちが|だいじこ|し|りつぜん"},{"japanese":"戦場の記録写真を前に、慄然たる思いがした。","romaji":"Senjō no kiroku shashin o mae ni, ritsuzen taru omoi ga shita.","german":"Before the documentary photographs of the battlefield, I felt a shudder of horror.","furigana":"せんじょう|きろくしゃしん|まえ|りつぜん|おも"}],"notes":"慄然 means \"shuddering, frozen in horror\" and is a literary expression. Typical are 慄然とする and attributive 慄然たる思い \"a feeling of horror\". It is often triggered by the belated realization of a danger or by cruel facts. In conversation one says ぞっとする or 背筋が寒くなる instead.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不合格の通知を受けて悄然と帰宅した。","romaji":"Fugōkaku no tsūchi o ukete shōzen to kitaku shita.","german":"Having received the notice of failure, he dejectedly went home.","furigana":"ふごうかく|つうち|う|しょうぜん|きたく"},{"japanese":"試合に負けた選手たちは悄然とうなだれていた。","romaji":"Shiai ni maketa senshu-tachi wa shōzen to unadarete ita.","german":"The players who lost the match hung their heads in dejection.","furigana":"しあい|ま|せんしゅ|しょうぜん"},{"japanese":"叱られた弟は、悄然とした様子で部屋に戻った。","romaji":"Shikarareta otōto wa, shōzen to shita yōsu de heya ni modotta.","german":"The scolded younger brother returned to his room looking dejected.","furigana":"しか|おとうと|しょうぜん|ようす|へや|もど"}],"notes":"悄然 describes visible dejection: drooping shoulders, bowed head, quietly slinking away. Typical are 悄然と帰る, 悄然とうなだれる, 悄然と肩を落とす and 悄然とした様子. It is the elevated, literary counterpart of しょんぼり. Not to be confused with 騒然 \"tumultuous\" or 粛然 \"solemnly silent\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の傍若無人な態度に、周囲は呆れた。","romaji":"Kare no bōjaku bujin na taido ni, shūi wa akireta.","german":"Those around him were appalled at his inconsiderate attitude.","furigana":"かれ|ぼうじゃくぶじん|たいど|しゅうい|あき"},{"japanese":"電車の中で傍若無人に騒ぐ若者たちがいた。","romaji":"Densha no naka de bōjaku bujin ni sawagu wakamonotachi ga ita.","german":"There were young people making a racket on the train without regard for others.","furigana":"でんしゃ|なか|ぼうじゃくぶじん|さわ|わかもの"},{"japanese":"あの客の傍若無人ぶりには、店員も手を焼いている。","romaji":"Ano kyaku no bōjaku bujin-buri ni wa, ten'in mo te o yaite iru.","german":"Even the staff are struggling with that customer's utter disregard for others.","furigana":"きゃく|ぼうじゃくぶじん|てんいん|て|や"}],"notes":"Literally \"beside one, as if there were no one\" (傍らに人無きが若し): 傍若無人 describes ruthless, brazen behavior as if there were no other people. Typical: 傍若無人な態度／振る舞い, 傍若無人に振る舞う, 傍若無人ぶり. It is strongly reproachful and sharper than 失礼 or わがまま; similar is 厚かましい, which stresses brazenness in making demands.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの厚顔無恥な態度には呆れる。","romaji":"Ano kōgan muchi na taido ni wa akireru.","german":"That shameless attitude is appalling.","furigana":"こうがんむち|たいど|あき"},{"japanese":"厚顔無恥にも謝らずに帰った。","romaji":"Kōgan muchi ni mo ayamarazu ni kaetta.","german":"Shamelessly, he went home without apologizing.","furigana":"こうがんむち|あやま|かえ"},{"japanese":"嘘がばれても平気な顔をしているなんて、厚顔無恥にもほどがある。","romaji":"Uso ga barete mo heiki na kao o shite iru nante, kōgan muchi ni mo hodo ga aru.","german":"Acting unfazed even after the lie was exposed - that is the height of shamelessness.","furigana":"うそ|へいき|かお|こうがんむち"}],"notes":"厚顔無恥 literally means \"thick face, no shame\": impudent and shameless; someone acts brazenly without feeling ashamed. It is a な-adjective (厚顔無恥な態度, 厚顔無恥な人) and also appears in the phrase 厚顔無恥にも \"shamelessly\" and 厚顔無恥にもほどがある \"this is the height of shamelessness\". It is a strong, condemnatory expression, often directed at politicians or companies. Milder and more everyday are 図々しい and 厚かましい.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"瞠目すべき技術の進歩があった。","romaji":"Dōmoku subeki gijutsu no shinpo ga atta.","german":"There has been remarkable technological progress.","furigana":"どうもく|ぎじゅつ|しんぽ"},{"japanese":"彼の上達ぶりには誰もが瞠目した。","romaji":"Kare no jōtatsuburi ni wa daremo ga dōmoku shita.","german":"Everyone marveled at how much he had improved.","furigana":"かれ|じょうたつ|だれ|どうもく"},{"japanese":"その新人は、瞠目に値する成績を残した。","romaji":"Sono shinjin wa, dōmoku ni atai suru seiseki o nokoshita.","german":"The newcomer achieved results worthy of admiration.","furigana":"しんじん|どうもく|あたい|せいせき|のこ"}],"notes":"瞠目 literally means \"to open one's eyes wide\" and denotes looking on in astonishment. It is a noun or intransitive する-verb: 〜に瞠目する \"to marvel at something\". It most often stands in the set phrases 瞠目すべき \"remarkable, astonishing\" and 瞠目に値する \"deserves admiration\", for achievements, progress or results. Very literary; in daily speech one says 目を見張る.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"答えの出ない問題に煩悶した。","romaji":"Kotae no denai mondai ni hanmon shita.","german":"I agonized over a problem with no answer.","furigana":"こた|で|もんだい|はんもん"},{"japanese":"進学か就職かで煩悶する日々が続いた。","romaji":"Shingaku ka shūshoku ka de hanmon suru hibi ga tsuzuita.","german":"Days of agonizing over whether to go on to higher education or get a job continued.","furigana":"しんがく|しゅうしょく|はんもん|ひび|つづ"},{"japanese":"主人公の青年の煩悶が、この小説の中心テーマだ。","romaji":"Shujinkō no seinen no hanmon ga, kono shōsetsu no chūshin tēma da.","german":"The young protagonist's anguish is the central theme of this novel.","furigana":"しゅじんこう|せいねん|はんもん|しょうせつ|ちゅうしん"}],"notes":"煩悶 is a literary word for tormenting inner struggle when one finds no way out, for example over life decisions, matters of conscience or lovesickness. As an intransitive する-verb: 〜に煩悶する or 〜で煩悶する; as a noun, for example 煩悶の日々 or 青年の煩悶. Compared with the everyday 悩む/悩み it sounds literary and more intense, like 苦悩. It has nothing to do with \"anxiety\" in the sense of fear.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"卒業式で深い感慨に浸った。","romaji":"Sotsugyōshiki de fukai kangai ni hitatta.","german":"At the graduation ceremony I was immersed in deep emotion.","furigana":"そつぎょうしき|ふか|かんがい|ひた"},{"japanese":"二十年ぶりに母校を訪れ、感慨深いものがあった。","romaji":"Nijūnen-buri ni bokō o otozure, kangaibukai mono ga atta.","german":"Visiting my alma mater for the first time in twenty years, I was filled with deep feeling.","furigana":"にじゅうねん|ぼこう|おとず|かんがいぶか"},{"japanese":"引退会見で、彼は「感慨無量です」と語った。","romaji":"Intai kaiken de, kare wa \"kangai muryō desu\" to katatta.","german":"At his retirement press conference, he said, \"I am overcome with emotion.\"","furigana":"いんたいかいけん|かれ|かんがいむりょう|かた"}],"notes":"感慨 is a deep, reflective emotion, mostly when looking back on the past: farewells, reunions, the end of a phase of life. Common combinations are 感慨にふける / 感慨に浸る \"to sink into memories and feelings\", 感慨深い \"moving\" and the four-character word 感慨無量 \"overwhelmed with emotion\". Unlike 感動, which means spontaneous being moved by something beautiful, 感慨 always involves reflection on times gone by.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"戦争の悲哀を語り継ぐべきだ。","romaji":"Sensō no hiai o kataritsugu beki da.","german":"We should keep passing down the sorrow of war.","furigana":"せんそう|ひあい|かた|つ"},{"japanese":"中年サラリーマンの悲哀を描いたドラマが話題になった。","romaji":"Chūnen sarariiman no hiai o egaita dorama ga wadai ni natta.","german":"A drama depicting the pathos of middle-aged salarymen became a talking point.","furigana":"ちゅうねん|ひあい|えが|わだい"},{"japanese":"彼の歌声には、どこか悲哀が漂っている。","romaji":"Kare no utagoe ni wa, doko ka hiai ga tadayotte iru.","german":"There is somehow a note of sorrow in his singing voice.","furigana":"かれ|うたごえ|ひあい|ただよ"}],"notes":"悲哀 is a literary word for deep sadness and the suffering that belongs to a particular situation. It mostly stands in the pattern 〜の悲哀: 人生の悲哀, 戦争の悲哀, and, often slightly ironically, 中間管理職の悲哀 \"the woes of middle management\". Other combinations are 悲哀を感じる and 悲哀が漂う. In daily speech one says 悲しみ; 悲哀 sounds literary and more like a general mood than a concrete loss.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"突然の質問に困惑の表情を浮かべた。","romaji":"Totsuzen no shitsumon ni konwaku no hyōjō o ukabeta.","german":"He put on a bewildered expression at the sudden question.","furigana":"とつぜん|しつもん|こんわく|ひょうじょう|う"},{"japanese":"急な方針変更に、社員たちは困惑している。","romaji":"Kyū na hōshin henkō ni, shain-tachi wa konwaku shite iru.","german":"Employees are baffled by the sudden policy change.","furigana":"きゅう|ほうしんへんこう|しゃいん|こんわく"},{"japanese":"容疑者の矛盾した説明に、警察も困惑を隠せなかった。","romaji":"Yōgisha no mujun shita setsumei ni, keisatsu mo konwaku o kakusenakatta.","german":"The police, too, could not hide their bewilderment at the suspect's contradictory explanation.","furigana":"ようぎしゃ|むじゅん|せつめい|けいさつ|こんわく|かく"}],"notes":"困惑 denotes perplexity and embarrassment when one does not know how to react. As an intransitive する-verb: 〜に困惑する; as a noun often in 困惑の表情, 困惑を隠せない, 困惑した様子. It is more formal than 困る and describes the inner state more; 戸惑う stresses hesitation at something unfamiliar. Typical of news and reports on the reactions of those affected.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地震が起きて住民は狼狽した。","romaji":"Jishin ga okite jūmin wa rōbai shita.","german":"Residents panicked when the earthquake struck.","furigana":"じしん|お|じゅうみん|ろうばい"},{"japanese":"嘘を指摘されると、彼は明らかに狼狽した。","romaji":"Uso o shiteki sareru to, kare wa akiraka ni rōbai shita.","german":"When his lie was pointed out, he was visibly flustered.","furigana":"うそ|してき|かれ|あき|ろうばい"},{"japanese":"株価の急落を受けて、市場には狼狽売りが広がった。","romaji":"Kabuka no kyūraku o ukete, shijō ni wa rōbaiuri ga hirogatta.","german":"Following the sharp drop in stock prices, panic selling spread through the market.","furigana":"かぶか|きゅうらく|う|しじょう|ろうばいう|ひろ"}],"notes":"狼狽 denotes suddenly losing one's composure: one does not know what to do and appears frantic or caught out. As an intransitive する-verb: 狼狽する, often with the trigger marked by に; intensified in the four-character word 周章狼狽. The same kanji are also used for the verb うろたえる (狼狽える), which is more everyday. In stock-market language there is 狼狽売り \"panic selling\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"怨念が込められた呪いの人形が見つかった。","romaji":"Onnen ga komerareta noroi no ningyō ga mitsukatta.","german":"A cursed doll imbued with a vengeful grudge was found.","furigana":"おんねん|こ|のろ|にんぎょう|み"},{"japanese":"長年の怨念を晴らすため、彼は復讐を誓った。","romaji":"Naganen no onnen o harasu tame, kare wa fukushū o chikatta.","german":"To settle a long-held grudge, he swore revenge.","furigana":"ながねん|おんねん|は|かれ||ちか"},{"japanese":"この屋敷には、殺された武士の怨念が残っていると言われている。","romaji":"Kono yashiki ni wa, korosareta bushi no onnen ga nokotte iru to iwarete iru.","german":"It is said that the grudge of a murdered samurai remains in this mansion.","furigana":"やしき|ころ|ぶし|おんねん|のこ|い"}],"notes":"怨念 is a deep-seated, long-nursed grudge, often with a desire for retribution. Typical are 怨念を抱く, 怨念がこもる/込められる and 怨念を晴らす. It appears especially often in connection with ghosts and curses: the grudge of a dead person that clings to a place. In daily speech one says 恨み; 怨念 sounds darker, heavier and more literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女の瞳には深い愁いが宿っていた。","romaji":"Kanojo no hitomi ni wa fukai urei ga yadotte ita.","german":"Deep melancholy dwelt in her eyes.","furigana":"かのじょ|ひとみ|ふか|うれ|やど"},{"japanese":"秋の夕暮れは、どこか愁いを帯びている。","romaji":"Aki no yūgure wa, doko ka urei o obite iru.","german":"An autumn dusk has a somehow melancholic touch.","furigana":"あき|ゆうぐ|うれ|お"},{"japanese":"彼は愁いを含んだ表情で窓の外を見ていた。","romaji":"Kare wa urei o fukunda hyōjō de mado no soto o mite ita.","german":"He was gazing out the window with a pensive expression.","furigana":"かれ|うれ|ふく|ひょうじょう|まど|そと|み"}],"notes":"愁い is a quiet, melancholic sadness or wistfulness. Typical are 愁いを帯びる \"to have a melancholic cast\", 愁いを含んだ表情 and 愁いに沈む. The meaning \"worry, apprehension\" is usually written 憂い, for example in 後顧の憂い or 備えあれば憂いなし. The word is literary; in daily speech one says 悲しみ or 心配.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"遺族の慟哭が式場に響いた。","romaji":"Izoku no dōkoku ga shikijō ni hibiita.","german":"The bereaved family's wailing echoed through the hall.","furigana":"いぞく|どうこく|しきじょう|ひび"},{"japanese":"息子の訃報を聞いた母親は、その場で慟哭した。","romaji":"Musuko no fuhō o kiita hahaoya wa, sono ba de dōkoku shita.","german":"On hearing the news of her son's death, the mother broke down sobbing on the spot.","furigana":"むすこ|ふほう|き|ははおや|ば|どうこく"},{"japanese":"小説の終盤、主人公が慟哭する場面に胸を打たれた。","romaji":"Shōsetsu no shūban, shujinkō ga dōkoku suru bamen ni mune o utareta.","german":"I was moved by the scene near the end of the novel in which the protagonist wails in grief.","furigana":"しょうせつ|しゅうばん|しゅじんこう|どうこく|ばめん|むね|う"}],"notes":"慟哭 is loud, despairing weeping out of the deepest pain, typically at a death or immeasurable loss. As an intransitive する-verb: 慟哭する; as a noun, for example 遺族の慟哭. It is much stronger than 泣く or 号泣 (\"to weep loudly\") and belongs to elevated written language, such as reports or literature.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は侮辱されて激昂した。","romaji":"Kare wa bujoku sarete gekikō shita.","german":"He flew into a rage when insulted.","furigana":"かれ|ぶじょく|げきこう"},{"japanese":"審判の判定に激昂したファンが、グラウンドに物を投げ込んだ。","romaji":"Shinpan no hantei ni gekikō shita fan ga, guraundo ni mono o nagekonda.","german":"Fans enraged by the referee's call threw objects onto the field.","furigana":"しんぱん|はんてい|げきこう|もの|な|こ"},{"japanese":"彼は激昂しやすい性格で、すぐに声を荒らげる。","romaji":"Kare wa gekikō shiyasui seikaku de, sugu ni koe o araraeru.","german":"He has a hot-tempered nature and immediately raises his voice."}],"notes":"激昂 denotes violent agitation, especially flaring anger. As an intransitive する-verb: 〜に激昂する \"to fly into a rage over something\", 激昂した群衆. Besides げきこう, the reading げっこう is also common. Compared with 激怒 (to be very angry), 激昂 stresses the visible welling up of emotions; 逆上 is going completely berserk. Register: literary, common in news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"美しい音楽に恍惚とした。","romaji":"Utsukushii ongaku ni kōkotsu to shita.","german":"I was entranced by the beautiful music.","furigana":"うつく|おんがく|こうこつ"},{"japanese":"ゴールの瞬間、選手は恍惚とした表情で天を仰いだ。","romaji":"Gōru no shunkan, senshu wa kōkotsu to shita hyōjō de ten o aoida.","german":"At the moment of the goal, the player looked up at the sky with a rapturous expression.","furigana":"しゅんかん|せんしゅ|こうこつ|ひょうじょう|てん|あお"},{"japanese":"有吉佐和子の『恍惚の人』は、認知症の老人の介護を描いた小説だ。","romaji":"Ariyoshi Sawako no \"Kōkotsu no hito\" wa, ninchishō no rōjin no kaigo o egaita shōsetsu da.","german":"Sawako Ariyoshi's \"Kokotsu no Hito\" is a novel depicting the care of an elderly person with dementia.","furigana":"ありよしさわこ|こうこつ|ひと|にんちしょう|ろうじん|かいご|えが|しょうせつ"}],"notes":"恍惚 is a state of rapture in which one is completely captivated by something beautiful or overwhelming and forgets everything else. Typical are 恍惚とする, 恍惚とした表情, 恍惚の表情 and 恍惚感. Related is 陶酔, which stresses more the \"intoxication\" with something. The second meaning \"senile confusion\" is obsolete today and known mainly through Sawako Ariyoshi's novel 『恍惚の人』; today one says 認知症. Register: literary, elevated.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自分の成功に陶酔してはいけない。","romaji":"Jibun no seikō ni tōsui shite wa ikenai.","german":"You must not become intoxicated with your own success.","furigana":"じぶん|せいこう|とうすい"},{"japanese":"観客はピアニストの演奏に陶酔していた。","romaji":"Kankyaku wa pianisuto no ensō ni tōsui shite ita.","german":"The audience was enraptured by the pianist's performance.","furigana":"かんきゃく|えんそう|とうすい"},{"japanese":"彼のスピーチは自己陶酔が過ぎて、聞いていられなかった。","romaji":"Kare no supiichi wa jiko tōsui ga sugite, kiite irarenakatta.","german":"His speech was so self-indulgent that I could not bear to listen.","furigana":"かれ|じことうすい|す|き"}],"notes":"陶酔 means \"to be intoxicated, completely carried away\", by music, art, a mood or even by oneself. As an intransitive する-verb: 〜に陶酔する. Very common is 自己陶酔 \"narcissism, self-congratulation\", which sounds negative; also 陶酔感 and 陶酔しきった表情. Unlike 心酔 (admiration for a person or doctrine), 陶酔 is about a pleasurable state of intoxication. The literal sense \"drunk on alcohol\" is rare in modern Japanese.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"周囲の嘲笑にも屈しなかった。","romaji":"Shūi no chōshō ni mo kusshinakatta.","german":"He did not yield even to the derision of those around him.","furigana":"しゅうい|ちょうしょう|くっ"},{"japanese":"彼の発明は、当初は世間の嘲笑を浴びた。","romaji":"Kare no hatsumei wa, tōsho wa seken no chōshō o abita.","german":"His invention was at first met with public ridicule.","furigana":"かれ|はつめい|とうしょ|せけん|ちょうしょう|あ"},{"japanese":"人の失敗を嘲笑するような態度は許されない。","romaji":"Hito no shippai o chōshō suru yō na taido wa yurusarenai.","german":"An attitude of mocking other people's failures is not acceptable.","furigana":"ひと|しっぱい|ちょうしょう|たいど|ゆる"}],"notes":"嘲笑 is contemptuous laughing at someone, scorn and ridicule. As a transitive する-verb: 人を嘲笑する; as a noun often in 嘲笑を浴びる \"to be laughed at\", 嘲笑の的になる \"to become the target of ridicule\" and 周囲の嘲笑. The same kanji also stand for the native verb あざ笑う. To distinguish are 冷笑 (a cold, condescending smile) and 苦笑 (a wry smile); in daily speech one rather says ばかにする or 笑い者にする.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"苦悶の表情を浮かべながら倒れた。","romaji":"Kumon no hyōjō o ukabe nagara taoreta.","german":"Er brach mit einem qualvollen Gesichtsausdruck zusammen.","furigana":"くもん|ひょうじょう|う|たお"},{"japanese":"患者は激しい痛みに苦悶していた。","romaji":"Kanja wa hageshii itami ni kumon shite ita.","german":"Der Patient wand sich vor heftigen Schmerzen.","furigana":"かんじゃ|はげ|いた|くもん"},{"japanese":"責任と良心の間で苦悶する主人公の姿が描かれている。","romaji":"Sekinin to ryōshin no aida de kumon suru shujinkō no sugata ga egakarete iru.","german":"Der Roman zeigt einen Helden, der qualvoll zwischen Pflicht und Gewissen hin- und hergerissen ist.","furigana":"せきにん|りょうしん|あいだ|くもん|しゅじんこう|すがた|えが"}],"notes":"苦悶 bezeichnet sichtbare, quälende Pein – körperlich durch Schmerzen oder seelisch durch einen inneren Konflikt. Typisch sind 苦悶の表情, 苦悶の声 und 痛みに苦悶する (intransitives する-Verb). Anders als 煩悶, das rein gedankliches Grübeln beschreibt, zeigt sich 苦悶 oft im Gesicht oder am Körper. Achtung: Das deutsche „Agonie/Todeskampf“ trifft es nicht; 苦悶 heißt einfach „Qual“. Register: schriftsprachlich.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不正に対する忿怒の声が上がった。","romaji":"Fusei ni taisuru funnu no koe ga agatta.","german":"Voices of wrath rose against the injustice.","furigana":"ふせい|たい|ふんぬ|こえ|あ"},{"japanese":"不動明王は忿怒の形相で悪を打ち払うとされる。","romaji":"Fudō Myōō wa funnu no gyōsō de aku o uchiharau to sareru.","german":"Fudo Myoo is said to drive out evil with a wrathful countenance.","furigana":"ふどうみょうおう|ふんぬ|ぎょうそう|あく|う|はら"},{"japanese":"裏切りを知った王は、忿怒に燃えた。","romaji":"Uragiri o shitta ō wa, funnu ni moeta.","german":"On learning of the betrayal, the king burned with fury.","furigana":"うらぎ|し|おう|ふんぬ|も"}],"notes":"忿怒 (ふんぬ) is violent anger, very literary. The usual modern spelling is 憤怒 (ふんぬ or ふんど); 忿怒 appears mainly in a Buddhist context, for example in the 忿怒の形相 \"wrathful countenance\" of guardian deities such as 不動明王. Typical are 忿怒に燃える and 忿怒の声. In daily speech one says 怒り or 激怒.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"感動のあまり嗚咽を漏らした。","romaji":"Kandō no amari oetsu o morashita.","german":"I let out a sob, overcome with emotion.","furigana":"かんどう|おえつ|も"},{"japanese":"電話口で、母は嗚咽をこらえながら話した。","romaji":"Denwaguchi de, haha wa oetsu o koraenagara hanashita.","german":"On the phone, my mother spoke while holding back sobs.","furigana":"でんわぐち|はは|おえつ|はな"},{"japanese":"葬儀の間、会場のあちこちから嗚咽が聞こえた。","romaji":"Sōgi no aida, kaijō no achikochi kara oetsu ga kikoeta.","german":"During the funeral, sobbing could be heard from all around the hall.","furigana":"そうぎ|あいだ|かいじょう|おえつ|き"}],"notes":"嗚咽 is choked, suppressed sobbing in which one can barely hold back the tears. Typical are 嗚咽を漏らす \"a sob escapes someone\", 嗚咽をこらえる and 嗚咽する (intransitive). Unlike 慟哭 (loud, despairing wailing), 嗚咽 is rather quiet; in daily speech it corresponds to すすり泣き. Register: literary, in reports and novels.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"都会の喧噪から逃れて田舎に移住した。","romaji":"Tokai no kensō kara nogarete inaka ni ijū shita.","german":"I escaped the clamor of the city and moved to the countryside.","furigana":"とかい|けんそう|のが|いなか|いじゅう"},{"japanese":"祭りの夜、街は喧噪に包まれていた。","romaji":"Matsuri no yoru, machi wa kensō ni tsutsumarete ita.","german":"On the night of the festival, the town was wrapped in bustle.","furigana":"まつ|よる|まち|けんそう|つつ"},{"japanese":"昼間の喧噪が嘘のように、深夜の駅前は静まり返っていた。","romaji":"Hiruma no kensō ga uso no yō ni, shin'ya no ekimae wa shizumarikaette ita.","german":"As if the daytime hubbub had never been, the area in front of the station was hushed late at night.","furigana":"ひるま|けんそう|うそ|しんや|えきまえ|しず|かえ"}],"notes":"喧噪 denotes loud bustle, noise and commotion, typically of a big city or a crowd. The more common spelling today is 喧騒. Typical are 都会の喧噪, 喧噪を離れる/逃れる and 喧噪に包まれる. Unlike 騒音 (disturbing noise, for example from machines), 喧噪 means the lively, restless soundscape of a place. Register: literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"秋の夕暮れに寂寥感を覚えた。","romaji":"Aki no yūgure ni sekiryōkan o oboeta.","german":"I felt a sense of desolation in the autumn dusk.","furigana":"あき|ゆうぐ|せきりょうかん|おぼ"},{"japanese":"人影のない冬の海辺は、寂寥とした風景だった。","romaji":"Hitokage no nai fuyu no umibe wa, sekiryō to shita fūkei datta.","german":"The deserted winter seashore was a desolate landscape.","furigana":"ひとかげ|ふゆ|うみべ|せきりょう|ふうけい"},{"japanese":"友人たちが帰った後、部屋には寂寥感だけが残った。","romaji":"Yūjin-tachi ga kaetta ato, heya ni wa sekiryōkan dake ga nokotta.","german":"After my friends left, only a feeling of emptiness remained in the room.","furigana":"ゆうじん|かえ|あと|へや|せきりょうかん|のこ"}],"notes":"寂寥 is a deep, quiet loneliness and desolation, often of a landscape or mood. Most common is 寂寥感 \"a feeling of emptiness and loneliness\"; as a たる-adjective also 寂寥とした風景 / 寂寥たる. The word is purely literary; in daily speech one says 寂しさ. Unlike 孤独 (being alone, having no ties), 寂寥 rather describes the bleak atmosphere.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"交渉が膠着状態に陥った。","romaji":"Kōshō ga kōchaku jōtai ni ochiitta.","german":"The negotiations fell into a deadlock.","furigana":"こうしょう|こうちゃくじょうたい|おちい"},{"japanese":"膠着状態を打開するため、両国の首脳が直接会談した。","romaji":"Kōchaku jōtai o dakai suru tame, ryōkoku no shunō ga chokusetsu kaidan shita.","german":"To break the deadlock, the leaders of the two countries met directly.","furigana":"こうちゃくじょうたい|だかい|りょうこく|しゅのう|ちょくせつかいだん"},{"japanese":"日本語は膠着語に分類される。","romaji":"Nihongo wa kōchakugo ni bunrui sareru.","german":"Japanese is classified as an agglutinative language.","furigana":"にほんご|こうちゃくご|ぶんるい"}],"notes":"膠着 denotes a standstill in which neither side makes progress; most often in the set expression 膠着状態: 膠着状態に陥る \"to get stuck\", 膠着状態が続く, 膠着状態を打開する \"to break the deadlock\". 膠 means \"glue\", and the basic meaning \"sticking\" lives on in technical terms like 膠着語 \"agglutinative language\". The verb 膠着する is intransitive. Compared with 停滞 \"stagnation\" (growth, progress), 膠着 stresses the stalemate between opponents; news language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"長い会議で倦怠感が漂った。","romaji":"Nagai kaigi de kentaikan ga tadayotta.","german":"A sense of weariness hung over the long meeting.","furigana":"なが|かいぎ|けんたいかん|ただよ"},{"japanese":"発熱や全身の倦怠感が続く場合は、医師に相談してください。","romaji":"Hatsunetsu ya zenshin no kentaikan ga tsuzuku baai wa, ishi ni sōdan shite kudasai.","german":"If fever or general fatigue persists, please consult a doctor.","furigana":"はつねつ|ぜんしん|けんたいかん|つづ|ばあい|いし|そうだん"},{"japanese":"結婚して五年、二人は倦怠期を迎えていた。","romaji":"Kekkon shite gonen, futari wa kentaiki o mukaete ita.","german":"Five years into their marriage, the two had reached a stale period.","furigana":"けっこん|ごねん|ふたり|けんたいき|むか"}],"notes":"倦怠 means languor and weariness. It most often appears in two compounds: 倦怠感 \"listlessness\", a typical symptom in medical texts (全身の倦怠感), and 倦怠期 \"a phase of routine and boredom\" in a relationship. Standing alone or as a する-verb, 倦怠 is rare. In daily speech one says だるい or 疲れ for tiredness.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"専門家としての自負がある。","romaji":"Senmonka to shite no jifu ga aru.","german":"I have pride as a professional.","furigana":"せんもんか|じふ"},{"japanese":"彼は業界一の技術を持っていると自負している。","romaji":"Kare wa gyōkai ichi no gijutsu o motte iru to jifu shite iru.","german":"He is confident that he has the best skills in the industry.","furigana":"かれ|ぎょうかいいち|ぎじゅつ|も|じふ"},{"japanese":"この町の発展を支えてきたという自負が、職人たちにはある。","romaji":"Kono machi no hatten o sasaete kita to iu jifu ga, shokunin-tachi ni wa aru.","german":"The craftsmen take pride in having supported this town's development.","furigana":"まち|はってん|ささ|じふ|しょくにん"}],"notes":"自負 is justified pride in one's own ability or achievements, that is, healthy self-confidence. Typical are 〜としての自負がある, 自負心 and the pattern 〜と自負する \"to be convinced that ...\". Unlike うぬぼれ or 慢心, 自負 is not inherently pejorative; it sounds like professional honor. Related is プライド, which is more common in daily speech.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"成功に慢心して努力を怠った。","romaji":"Seikō ni manshin shite doryoku o okotatta.","german":"Becoming complacent about his success, he neglected his effort.","furigana":"せいこう|まんしん|どりょく|おこた"},{"japanese":"優勝候補と言われても、慢心せずに練習を続けた。","romaji":"Yūshō kōho to iwarete mo, manshin sezu ni renshū o tsuzuketa.","german":"Though called a title favorite, they kept practicing without getting cocky.","furigana":"ゆうしょうこうほ|い|まんしん|れんしゅう|つづ"},{"japanese":"長年の独占で、その企業には慢心が生まれていた。","romaji":"Naganen no dokusen de, sono kigyō ni wa manshin ga umarete ita.","german":"Years of monopoly had bred complacency in that company.","furigana":"ながねん|どくせん|きぎょう|まんしん|う"}],"notes":"慢心 is arrogance and complacency, especially after a success, which leads one to become careless. As an intransitive する-verb: 〜に慢心する, 慢心せずに; as a noun 慢心が生まれる, 慢心を戒める. In contrast to 自負 (justified pride), 慢心 is always negative; similar are おごり and 油断 (carelessness).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"過度な卑下は逆効果になることもある。","romaji":"Kado na hige wa gyaku kōka ni naru koto mo aru.","german":"Excessive self-deprecation can sometimes backfire.","furigana":"かど|ひげ|ぎゃくこうか"},{"japanese":"そんなに自分を卑下することはないよ。","romaji":"Sonna ni jibun o hige suru koto wa nai yo.","german":"There is no need to put yourself down so much.","furigana":"じぶん|ひげ"},{"japanese":"謙遜と卑下は似ているようで違う。","romaji":"Kenson to hige wa nite iru yō de chigau.","german":"Modesty and self-abasement seem similar but differ.","furigana":"けんそん|ひげ|に|ちが"}],"notes":"卑下 means to belittle and diminish oneself. As a transitive する-verb: 自分を卑下する; often with criticism: 卑下しすぎる, 卑下することはない \"you don't have to make yourself so small\". The difference from 謙遜 is important: 謙遜 is appropriate, polite modesty, whereas 卑下 is exaggerated, often uncomfortable self-abasement. Not to be confused with the identically pronounced ひげ \"beard\" (髭).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"思慮深い判断が必要だ。","romaji":"Shiryo bukai handan ga hitsuyō da.","german":"Judgment that is well considered is needed.","furigana":"しりょぶか|はんだん|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"彼の思慮に欠ける発言が、多くの人を傷つけた。","romaji":"Kare no shiryo ni kakeru hatsugen ga, ōku no hito o kizutsuketa.","german":"His thoughtless remark hurt many people.","furigana":"かれ|しりょ|か|はつげん|おお|ひと|きず"},{"japanese":"彼女は若いのに思慮分別のある人だ。","romaji":"Kanojo wa wakai no ni shiryo funbetsu no aru hito da.","german":"Though young, she is a person of discretion and good sense.","furigana":"かのじょ|わか|しりょふんべつ|ひと"}],"notes":"思慮 is careful, circumspect thinking before one acts or speaks. It appears almost only in set combinations: 思慮深い \"prudent\", 思慮が浅い \"rash\", 思慮に欠ける \"thoughtless\" and 思慮分別 \"prudence and discernment\". As a verb 思慮する it is rare. To distinguish are 考慮 \"to take into consideration\" (を考慮する) and 配慮 \"consideration for others\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大人としての分別を持つべきだ。","romaji":"Otona to shite no funbetsu o motsu beki da.","german":"You should have the good sense expected of an adult.","furigana":"おとな|ふんべつ|も"},{"japanese":"酔っていたとはいえ、分別のない行動だった。","romaji":"Yotte ita to wa ie, funbetsu no nai kōdō datta.","german":"Even though he was drunk, it was senseless behavior.","furigana":"よ|ふんべつ|こうどう"},{"japanese":"子どもに善悪の分別がつくのは何歳ごろだろう。","romaji":"Kodomo ni zen'aku no funbetsu ga tsuku no wa nansai goro darō.","german":"At about what age do children come to tell right from wrong?","furigana":"こ|ぜんあく|ふんべつ|なんさい"}],"notes":"分別 (ふんべつ) is reason and judgment, especially the ability of an adult to tell right from wrong. Typical are 分別がある, 分別のある大人, 分別がつく \"to become sensible\", 善悪の分別 and the opposite 無分別. Note: with the reading ぶんべつ, the same kanji means \"separating, sorting\", for example ゴミの分別 \"waste separation\". Related is 思慮分別.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人の心の機微を察する力が必要だ。","romaji":"Hito no kokoro no kibi o sassuru chikara ga hitsuyō da.","german":"The ability to sense the subtleties of people's hearts is needed.","furigana":"ひと|こころ|きび|さっ|ちから|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"落語には人情の機微が巧みに描かれている。","romaji":"Rakugo ni wa ninjō no kibi ga takumi ni egakarete iru.","german":"Rakugo skillfully depicts the subtleties of human feeling.","furigana":"らくご|にんじょう|きび|たく|えが"},{"japanese":"彼は政治の機微に通じたベテラン議員だ。","romaji":"Kare wa seiji no kibi ni tsūjita beteran giin da.","german":"He is a veteran legislator well versed in the subtleties of politics.","furigana":"かれ|せいじ|きび|つう|ぎいん"}],"notes":"機微 denotes the fine, hard-to-grasp stirrings and connections that one perceives only with intuition. Typical are 人情の機微 \"the subtle stirrings of the human heart\", 心の機微, 機微に触れる and 機微に通じる \"to be versed in the subtleties\". It is mostly about feelings or interpersonal and political relations, not technical details (for those, 細部 or ニュアンス). Register: elevated, literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"機転を利かせて危機を乗り越えた。","romaji":"Kiten o kikasete kiki o norikoeta.","german":"Using quick wit, he overcame the crisis.","furigana":"きてん|き|きき|の|こ"},{"japanese":"店員が機転を利かせて、傘を貸してくれた。","romaji":"Ten'in ga kiten o kikasete, kasa o kashite kureta.","german":"A shop assistant thoughtfully lent me an umbrella.","furigana":"てんいん|きてん|き|かさ|か"},{"japanese":"彼女は機転が利くので、接客に向いている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kiten ga kiku node, sekkyaku ni muite iru.","german":"She is quick-witted, so she is suited to customer service.","furigana":"かのじょ|きてん|き|せっきゃく|む"}],"notes":"機転 is the ability to do the right thing in a situation in a flash. It stands almost always in two expressions: 機転が利く \"to be quick-witted, resourceful\" and 機転を利かせる \"to show presence of mind\". Similar is 気が利く, which refers more to attentiveness to others' needs, as well as 臨機応変 \"flexible according to the situation\". The spelling 気転 is rarer.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仕事の要領を掴むのが早い人だ。","romaji":"Shigoto no yōryō o tsukamu no ga hayai hito da.","german":"He is someone who quickly gets the hang of a job.","furigana":"しごと|ようりょう|つか|はや|ひと"},{"japanese":"彼の説明は要領を得なくて、何が言いたいのか分からなかった。","romaji":"Kare no setsumei wa yōryō o enakute, nani ga iitai no ka wakaranakatta.","german":"His explanation was so muddled that I couldn't tell what he wanted to say.","furigana":"かれ|せつめい|ようりょう|え|なに|い|わ"},{"japanese":"弟は要領がいいから、叱られるのはいつも私だ。","romaji":"Otōto wa yōryō ga ii kara, shikarareru no wa itsumo watashi da.","german":"My little brother is shrewd, so it is always me who gets scolded.","furigana":"おとうと|ようりょう|しか|わたし"}],"notes":"要領 has two meanings. 1. \"The essentials, the gist\": 要領を得ない \"muddled, not to the point\", 要領よく説明する; also \"guidelines\" in 募集要領 or 学習指導要領. 2. \"Knack, the trick of it\": 要領をつかむ \"to get the hang of it\". 要領がいい means \"skillful, efficient\", but can also mean, pejoratively, \"cunning, shrewd about one's own advantage\"; the opposite is 要領が悪い \"clumsy, inefficient\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"商売の才覚に長けた経営者だ。","romaji":"Shōbai no saikaku ni taketa keieisha da.","german":"She is a manager with great business acumen.","furigana":"しょうばい|さいかく||けいえいしゃ"},{"japanese":"彼女は自分の才覚一つで、小さな店を大企業に育て上げた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa jibun no saikaku hitotsu de, chiisana mise o daikigyō ni sodateageta.","german":"By her own resourcefulness alone, she grew a small shop into a large company.","furigana":"かのじょ|じぶん|さいかくひと|ちい|みせ|だいきぎょう|そだ|あ"},{"japanese":"資金がなくても、才覚次第で道は開ける。","romaji":"Shikin ga nakute mo, saikaku shidai de michi wa hirakeru.","german":"Even without funds, a way opens up depending on one's resourcefulness.","furigana":"しきん|さいかくしだい|みち|ひら"}],"notes":"才覚 is practical skill and resourcefulness, especially in achieving something with few means or doing business. Typical are 才覚がある, 商売の才覚, 自分の才覚一つで \"purely by one's own ability and resourcefulness\" and 才覚次第. Unlike 才能 (talent in general) and 才知 (intelligence), 才覚 stresses practical, often commercial instinct. The meaning \"to raise money\" (金を才覚する) is obsolete.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女の才知に感嘆した。","romaji":"Kanojo no saichi ni kantan shita.","german":"I marveled at her wit.","furigana":"かのじょ|さいち|かんたん"},{"japanese":"彼は才知に富んだ若者として、早くから注目されていた。","romaji":"Kare wa saichi ni tonda wakamono to shite, hayaku kara chūmoku sarete ita.","german":"He had drawn attention early on as a young man full of talent and intelligence.","furigana":"かれ|さいち|と|わかもの|はや|ちゅうもく"},{"japanese":"才知だけでなく、人柄も優れた指導者だった。","romaji":"Saichi dake de naku, hitogara mo sugureta shidōsha datta.","german":"He was a leader who excelled not only in intellect but also in character.","furigana":"さいち|ひとがら|すぐ|しどうしゃ"}],"notes":"才知 (also 才智) denotes talent and intelligence together, that is, a bright, agile mind. Typical are 才知に富む \"witty and clever\", 才知にたける and 才知あふれる. The word is elevated and rather literary; in daily speech one says 頭がいい or 賢い. Unlike 才覚 (practical resourcefulness), 才知 stresses intellectual brilliance.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"度重なる心労で体を壊した。","romaji":"Tabikasanaru shinrō de karada o kowashita.","german":"He ruined his health from repeated worries.","furigana":"たびかさ|しんろう|からだ|こわ"},{"japanese":"母は父の介護による心労で、すっかりやつれてしまった。","romaji":"Haha wa chichi no kaigo ni yoru shinrō de, sukkari yatsurete shimatta.","german":"My mother became quite haggard from the strain of caring for my father.","furigana":"はは|ちち|かいご|しんろう"},{"japanese":"ご心労をおかけして、申し訳ありません。","romaji":"Goshinrō o okake shite, mōshiwake arimasen.","german":"I apologize for causing you concern.","furigana":"しんろう|もう|わけ"}],"notes":"心労 is mental strain from continuing worries, which can wear one down physically. Typical are 心労が重なる, 心労がたたる \"the worries take their toll\", 心労で倒れる and, politely, ご心労をおかけする \"to cause someone worry\". In daily speech one says ストレス or 気苦労; 心労 sounds more serious and formal. Similar is 心痛, which rather means grief and heartache.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"長時間労働で社員が疲弊している。","romaji":"Chōjikan rōdō de shain ga hihei shite iru.","german":"Employees are worn out by long working hours.","furigana":"ちょうじかんろうどう|しゃいん|ひへい"},{"japanese":"長引く不況で、地方の経済は疲弊しきっている。","romaji":"Nagabiku fukyō de, chihō no keizai wa hihei shikitte iru.","german":"Rural economies are thoroughly exhausted by the prolonged recession.","furigana":"ながび|ふきょう|ちほう|けいざい|ひへい"},{"japanese":"戦争で疲弊した国を立て直すには、長い時間がかかる。","romaji":"Sensō de hihei shita kuni o tatenaosu ni wa, nagai jikan ga kakaru.","german":"It will take a long time to rebuild a country exhausted by war.","furigana":"せんそう|ひへい|くに|た|なお|なが|じかん"}],"notes":"疲弊 denotes deep, wearing exhaustion from long strain, in people as well as in organizations, regions or national economies (地方経済の疲弊, 財政の疲弊). As an intransitive する-verb: 〜で疲弊する, 疲弊しきる. Compared with 疲労 (physical tiredness), 疲弊 stresses the drained state after long overload. Register: literary, common in news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"精神的な消耗が激しい仕事だ。","romaji":"Seishinteki na shōmō ga hageshii shigoto da.","german":"It is a job that is mentally very draining.","furigana":"せいしんてき|しょうもう|はげ|しごと"},{"japanese":"暑さで体力をかなり消耗した。","romaji":"Atsusa de tairyoku o kanari shōmō shita.","german":"The heat sapped a lot of my strength.","furigana":"あつ|たいりょく|しょうもう"},{"japanese":"コピー用紙などの消耗品は総務課に請求してください。","romaji":"Kopii yōshi nado no shōmōhin wa sōmuka ni seikyū shite kudasai.","german":"Please request consumables such as copy paper from the general affairs section.","furigana":"ようし|しょうもうひん|そうむか|せいきゅう"}],"notes":"消耗 is the using up and wearing out of strength, material or resources. It can be used transitively (体力を消耗する \"to use up one's strength\") and intransitively (体力が消耗する). Common compounds are 消耗品 \"consumables\" and 消耗戦 \"war of attrition\". The traditional reading is しょうこう; today しょうもう is standard. Compared with 消費 (consumption, using up in general), 消耗 stresses wearing out and being drained.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地下水が枯渇する恐れがある。","romaji":"Chikasui ga kokatsu suru osore ga aru.","german":"There is a danger that groundwater will dry up.","furigana":"ちかすい|こかつ|おそ"},{"japanese":"石油などの資源は、いずれ枯渇すると言われている。","romaji":"Sekiyu nado no shigen wa, izure kokatsu suru to iwarete iru.","german":"It is said that resources such as oil will eventually be exhausted.","furigana":"せきゆ|しげん|こかつ|い"},{"japanese":"締め切りに追われ続けて、アイデアが枯渇してしまった。","romaji":"Shimekiri ni owaretsuzukete, aidea ga kokatsu shite shimatta.","german":"Constantly chased by deadlines, my ideas have run dry.","furigana":"し|き|お|つづ|こかつ"}],"notes":"枯渇 means \"to dry up\" (springs, groundwater) and figuratively \"to run out, be exhausted\" for resources, funds or even ideas and talent. As an intransitive する-verb: 〜が枯渇する; as a noun, for example 資源の枯渇 and 財源の枯渇. In daily speech one says 尽きる or なくなる; 枯渇 is typical of news and specialist texts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"タイヤの摩耗が進んでいる。","romaji":"Taiya no mamō ga susunde iru.","german":"The wear on the tires is progressing.","furigana":"まもう|すす"},{"japanese":"長年使ったので、歯車がすっかり摩耗している。","romaji":"Naganen tsukatta node, haguruma ga sukkari mamō shite iru.","german":"After years of use, the gears are completely worn.","furigana":"ながねんつか|はぐるま|まもう"},{"japanese":"過酷な現場で働くうちに、心が摩耗していくのを感じた。","romaji":"Kakoku na genba de hataraku uchi ni, kokoro ga mamō shite iku no o kanjita.","german":"Working at a harsh site, I felt my spirit being worn down.","furigana":"かこく|げんば|はたら|こころ|まもう|かん"}],"notes":"摩耗 (also 磨耗) is wear caused by friction, a typical term in engineering and the automotive field: タイヤの摩耗, ブレーキパッドの摩耗, 部品が摩耗する (intransitive する-verb). Figuratively one also speaks of 心が摩耗する \"to be worn down inside\". The native counterpart is すり減る; 劣化, by contrast, means general aging and loss of quality.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"経営の破綻により会社が倒産した。","romaji":"Keiei no hatan ni yori kaisha ga tōsan shita.","german":"The company went bankrupt due to the collapse of its management.","furigana":"けいえい|はたん|かいしゃ|とうさん"},{"japanese":"二人の結婚生活は、わずか三年で破綻した。","romaji":"Futari no kekkon seikatsu wa, wazuka sannen de hatan shita.","german":"The couple's marriage fell apart in just three years.","furigana":"ふたり|けっこんせいかつ|さんねん|はたん"},{"japanese":"君の理論は、前提の段階で既に破綻している。","romaji":"Kimi no riron wa, zentei no dankai de sude ni hatan shite iru.","german":"Your theory has already broken down at the stage of its premises.","furigana":"きみ|りろん|ぜんてい|だんかい|すで|はたん"}],"notes":"破綻 denotes the collapse of something that previously worked: companies and finances (経営破綻, 財政破綻), relationships (結婚生活が破綻する) or arguments (論理が破綻している \"the logic falls apart\"). As an intransitive する-verb: 〜が破綻する. Unlike 倒産, which means only the insolvency of a company, 破綻 is much broader in use. Register: literary, common in business news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"景気の低迷が長引いている。","romaji":"Keiki no teimei ga nagabiite iru.","german":"The economic slump is dragging on.","furigana":"けいき|ていめい|ながび"},{"japanese":"内閣の支持率が三割前後で低迷している。","romaji":"Naikaku no shijiritsu ga sanwari zengo de teimei shite iru.","german":"The cabinet's approval rating is stuck at around thirty percent.","furigana":"ないかく|しじりつ|さんわりぜんご|ていめい"},{"japanese":"そのチームは長年リーグの下位に低迷している。","romaji":"Sono chīmu wa naganen rīgu no kai ni teimei shite iru.","german":"That team has been languishing in the lower ranks of the league for years.","furigana":"ながねん|かい|ていめい"}],"notes":"低迷 describes economy, stock prices, sales or performance staying at a low level for a long time without recovering. Typical are 景気の低迷, 低迷が続く/長引く and, as an intransitive する-verb, 株価が低迷している or 支持率が低迷する; in sports, 下位に低迷する means \"to be stuck in the bottom of the table\". While 不振 mainly stresses bad results or performance, 低迷 underlines the sluggish persistence. The original meaning \"hanging low (of clouds)\" is literary. News and written language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"売り上げ不振の原因を分析した。","romaji":"Uriage fushin no gen'in o bunseki shita.","german":"We analyzed the cause of the sales slump.","furigana":"う|あ|ふしん|げんいん|ぶんせき"},{"japanese":"食欲不振が続いたので、病院で診てもらった。","romaji":"Shokuyoku fushin ga tsuzuita node, byōin de mite moratta.","german":"My loss of appetite continued, so I had it checked at the hospital.","furigana":"しょくよくふしん|つづ|びょういん|み"},{"japanese":"エースが不振に陥り、チームは連敗した。","romaji":"Ēsu ga fushin ni ochiiri, chīmu wa renpai shita.","german":"The ace fell into a slump, and the team lost consecutive games.","furigana":"ふしん|おちい|れんぱい"}],"notes":"不振 means that something is going badly or not delivering the expected performance. It mostly stands in compounds: 業績不振 \"poor business results\", 売り上げ不振, 食欲不振 \"loss of appetite\", 打撃不振 \"batting slump (baseball)\". Set combinations are 不振に陥る \"to fall into a crisis/slump\", 不振が続く and 不振から抜け出す/脱する; there is no する-verb. Unlike 低迷 (lasting persistence at a low level), 不振 stresses the weak showing. Not to be confused with the identically read 不審 \"suspicious\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"観光業が活況を呈している。","romaji":"Kankōgyō ga kakkyō o teishite iru.","german":"The tourism industry is booming.","furigana":"かんこうぎょう|かっきょう|てい"},{"japanese":"新製品の発表を受けて、株式市場は活況に沸いた。","romaji":"Shin seihin no happyō o ukete, kabushiki shijō wa kakkyō ni waita.","german":"After the announcement of the new product, the stock market was buoyant.","furigana":"しんせいひん|はっぴょう|う|かぶしきしじょう|かっきょう|わ"},{"japanese":"再開発のおかげで、駅前は以前の活況を取り戻した。","romaji":"Saikaihatsu no okage de, ekimae wa izen no kakkyō o torimodoshita.","german":"Thanks to the redevelopment, the area in front of the station has regained its former liveliness.","furigana":"さいかいはつ|えきまえ|いぜん|かっきょう|と|もど"}],"notes":"活況 refers to lively business and brisk demand in a market, an industry or a district. The fixed, written-style phrase is 活況を呈する \"to show lively activity, to boom\"; also 活況に沸く, 活況を取り戻す and attributively 活況な市場. Its opposites are 不振, 低迷 and 閑散. 好況 means a good economy in general, while 活況 is more the visibly busy bustle. It is not used for people.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"年末は繁忙期で残業が増える。","romaji":"Nenmatsu wa hanbōki de zangyō ga fueru.","german":"The end of the year is the busy season, and overtime increases.","furigana":"ねんまつ|はんぼうき|ざんぎょう|ふ"},{"japanese":"引っ越し業者にとって、三月は最も繁忙な時期だ。","romaji":"Hikkoshi gyōsha ni totte, sangatsu wa mottomo hanbō na jiki da.","german":"For moving companies, March is the busiest time of the year.","furigana":"ひ|こ|ぎょうしゃ|さんがつ|もっと|はんぼう|じき"},{"japanese":"業務が繁忙を極め、休日出勤が続いた。","romaji":"Gyōmu ga hanbō o kiwame, kyūjitsu shukkin ga tsuzuita.","german":"The workload became extremely heavy, and I had to keep working on days off.","furigana":"ぎょうむ|はんぼう|きわ|きゅうじつしゅっきん|つづ"}],"notes":"繁忙 means \"swamped with work, very busy\" and belongs to business and administrative language; in everyday speech people simply say 忙しい. The most common form is the compound 繁忙期 \"peak season, rush period\", with its counterpart 閑散期. There are also 繁忙な時期/業務 and, in written style, 繁忙を極める \"to be extremely busy\". Do not confuse it with 繁盛 \"to do well, to flourish (of a business)\": 繁忙 describes the workload, not the success.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"平日の商店街は閑散としている。","romaji":"Heijitsu no shōtengai wa kansan to shite iru.","german":"The shopping street is empty and quiet on weekdays.","furigana":"へいじつ|しょうてんがい|かんさん"},{"japanese":"閑散期には航空券が安くなる。","romaji":"Kansanki ni wa kōkūken ga yasuku naru.","german":"Airline tickets get cheaper in the off season.","furigana":"かんさんき|こうくうけん|やす"},{"japanese":"試合が終わると、スタジアムの周りは急に閑散とした。","romaji":"Shiai ga owaru to, sutajiamu no mawari wa kyū ni kansan to shita.","german":"When the game ended, the area around the stadium suddenly became deserted.","furigana":"しあい|お|まわ|きゅう|かんさん"}],"notes":"閑散 describes places or businesses where there should be activity but hardly anyone is around: \"deserted, dead quiet, slack\". It mostly appears with と: 閑散としている, 閑散とした街. In a business context, 閑散期 \"off season\" is the counterpart to 繁忙期, and 閑散な取引 means \"sluggish trading\". Unlike 静か (simply quiet), 閑散 carries a sense of emptiness and desertion. Rather literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"財政が逼迫して予算削減を余儀なくされた。","romaji":"Zaisei ga hippaku shite yosan sakugen o yogi naku sareta.","german":"Finances became so tight that budget cuts were unavoidable.","furigana":"ざいせい|ひっぱく|よさんさくげん|よぎ"},{"japanese":"猛暑で電力の需給が逼迫している。","romaji":"Mōsho de denryoku no jukyū ga hippaku shite iru.","german":"Because of the intense heat, electricity supply and demand are extremely tight.","furigana":"もうしょ|でんりょく|じゅきゅう|ひっぱく"},{"japanese":"感染者の急増で病床が逼迫した。","romaji":"Kansensha no kyūzō de byōshō ga hippaku shita.","german":"With the rapid rise in infections, hospital beds became scarce.","furigana":"かんせんしゃ|きゅうぞう|びょうしょう|ひっぱく"}],"notes":"逼迫 means that something is reaching its limit and is under heavy pressure, especially finances and supply: 財政が逼迫する, 電力需給が逼迫する, 医療の逼迫 \"overload of the healthcare system\". It is an intransitive する-verb used with が; attributively 逼迫した状況/情勢 \"tense situation\". Since 逼 is not a Jōyō kanji, newspapers often write ひっ迫. 不足 means a mere lack, while 逼迫 means a threatening scarcity; 切迫 emphasizes urgency in terms of time.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ビタミン欠乏が健康に影響を与える。","romaji":"Bitamin ketsubō ga kenkō ni eikyō o ataeru.","german":"Vitamin deficiency affects health.","furigana":"けつぼう|けんこう|えいきょう|あた"},{"japanese":"高い山では酸素が欠乏しやすい。","romaji":"Takai yama de wa sanso ga ketsubō shiyasui.","german":"At high altitudes, oxygen is easily lacking.","furigana":"たか|やま|さんそ|けつぼう"},{"japanese":"戦争中は物資が欠乏し、人々は配給に頼った。","romaji":"Sensōchū wa busshi ga ketsubō shi, hitobito wa haikyū ni tayotta.","german":"During the war, supplies ran short and people depended on rationing.","furigana":"せんそうちゅう|ぶっし|けつぼう|ひとびと|はいきゅう|たよ"}],"notes":"欠乏 refers to a serious lack of something vital such as nutrients, oxygen, food or goods: ビタミン欠乏, 欠乏症 \"deficiency disease\", 酸素欠乏 (short: 酸欠). As an intransitive する-verb: 物資が欠乏する. 不足 is the neutral everyday word (人手不足, 睡眠不足); 欠乏 sounds stronger and more technical; 欠如 denotes the absence of abstract qualities (常識の欠如).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"災害時に食料が払底した。","romaji":"Saigaiji ni shokuryō ga futtei shita.","german":"Food ran out at the time of the disaster.","furigana":"さいがいじ|しょくりょう|ふってい"},{"japanese":"人気商品の在庫が払底し、入荷は来月になる。","romaji":"Ninki shōhin no zaiko ga futtei shi, nyūka wa raigetsu ni naru.","german":"The popular item is completely sold out; new stock arrives next month.","furigana":"にんきしょうひん|ざいこ|ふってい|にゅうか|らいげつ"},{"japanese":"この業界では若い技術者が払底している。","romaji":"Kono gyōkai de wa wakai gijutsusha ga futtei shite iru.","german":"Young engineers are practically nonexistent in this industry.","furigana":"ぎょうかい|わか|ぎじゅつしゃ|ふってい"}],"notes":"払底 literally means \"to sweep (払) out down to the bottom (底)\": a supply is completely used up. It is an intransitive する-verb: 在庫が払底する; figuratively also 人材が払底している \"there are hardly any capable people left\". The word is literary and somewhat old-fashioned; in everyday speech people say 底をつく, 品切れになる or 切らす. It is much stronger than 不足.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"海岸線が波に侵食されている。","romaji":"Kaigansen ga nami ni shinshoku sarete iru.","german":"The coastline is being eroded by waves.","furigana":"かいがんせん|なみ|しんしょく"},{"japanese":"格安スマホが大手のシェアを侵食している。","romaji":"Kakuyasu sumaho ga ōte no shea o shinshoku shite iru.","german":"Cheap smartphone providers are eating into the market share of the big carriers.","furigana":"かくやす|おおて|しんしょく"},{"japanese":"在宅勤務で、仕事が私生活を侵食し始めた。","romaji":"Zaitaku kinmu de, shigoto ga shiseikatsu o shinshoku shihajimeta.","german":"With working from home, work began to encroach on my private life.","furigana":"ざいたくきんむ|しごと|しせいかつ|しんしょく|はじ"}],"notes":"侵食 refers to gradual intrusion and eating away. In geography it is erosion by water or wind (波の侵食, 川が谷を侵食する); for water erosion the spelling 浸食 is also found. Figuratively: 市場シェアを侵食する \"to take away market share\" or 仕事が私生活を侵食する. It is a transitive する-verb (AがBを侵食する) and often appears in the passive: 〜に侵食される. 侵略, by contrast, is an active, violent invasion.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大手企業の傘下に入った中小企業が増えている。","romaji":"Ōte kigyō no sanka ni haitta chūshō kigyō ga fuete iru.","german":"More and more small and medium-sized companies are coming under the umbrella of large corporations.","furigana":"おおてきぎょう|さんか|はい|ちゅうしょうきぎょう|ふ"},{"japanese":"その組合は全国組織の傘下にある。","romaji":"Sono kumiai wa zenkoku soshiki no sanka ni aru.","german":"The union is affiliated with a nationwide organization.","furigana":"くみあい|ぜんこくそしき|さんか"},{"japanese":"同社は買収によって三つのホテルチェーンを傘下に収めた。","romaji":"Dōsha wa baishū ni yotte mittsu no hoteru chēn o sanka ni osameta.","german":"Through acquisitions, the company brought three hotel chains under its umbrella.","furigana":"どうしゃ|ばいしゅう|みっ|さんか|おさ"}],"notes":"傘下 literally means \"under the umbrella\" and refers to belonging to a higher-level organization such as a corporate group or an umbrella association. Typical patterns are Aの傘下に入る \"to come under A\", 傘下に収める \"to bring under one's wing, to acquire\", 傘下にある, as well as 傘下の企業/団体 and 傘下企業 \"group subsidiary\". It has nothing to do with \"patronage\" in the sense of sponsorship; that would be 後援 or 主催. Business and news language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"会計処理に誤りがないか確認した。","romaji":"Kaikei shori ni ayamari ga nai ka kakunin shita.","german":"I checked whether there were any errors in the accounting.","furigana":"かいけいしょり|あやま|かくにん"},{"japanese":"すみません、お会計をお願いします。","romaji":"Sumimasen, okaikei o onegai shimasu.","german":"Excuse me, could we have the check, please?","furigana":"かいけい|ねが"},{"japanese":"町内会で会計を担当することになった。","romaji":"Chōnaikai de kaikei o tantō suru koto ni natta.","german":"I have been put in charge of the treasury for the neighborhood association.","furigana":"ちょうないかい|かいけい|たんとう"}],"notes":"会計 has two main uses. Technically it is accounting: 会計処理, 会計年度, 公認会計士 \"certified public accountant\". In daily life it is paying at a restaurant or shop: お会計お願いします \"the check, please\", お会計は二千円です. In clubs and associations, 会計 also refers to the treasurer. 経理 is accounting as a department in companies, お勘定 an older alternative for the bill, レジ the register as a place.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"経理部で日々の仕訳を行っている。","romaji":"Keiribu de hibi no shiwake o okonatte iru.","german":"I do the daily journal entries in the accounting department.","furigana":"けいりぶ|ひび|しわけ|おこな"},{"japanese":"この取引をどう仕訳すればいいか分からない。","romaji":"Kono torihiki o dō shiwake sureba ii ka wakaranai.","german":"I do not know how to record this transaction.","furigana":"とりひき|しわけ|わ"},{"japanese":"仕訳帳に記入してから、総勘定元帳に転記する。","romaji":"Shiwakechō ni kinyū shite kara, sōkanjō motochō ni tenki suru.","german":"You first enter it in the journal and then post it to the general ledger.","furigana":"しわけちょう|きにゅう|そうかんじょうもとちょう|てんき"}],"notes":"仕訳 is the journal entry of double-entry bookkeeping: each transaction is split into 借方 \"debit\" and 貸方 \"credit\". One says 仕訳する or, in jargon, 仕訳を切る \"to make a journal entry\"; the journal is called 仕訳帳. For general sorting (parcels, garbage) one usually writes 仕分け; the spelling 仕訳 is standard for bookkeeping. Transitive する-verb.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"事件は所轄の警察署で捜査されている。","romaji":"Jiken wa shokatsu no keisatsusho de sōsa sarete iru.","german":"The case is being investigated by the police station with jurisdiction.","furigana":"じけん|しょかつ|けいさつしょ|そうさ"},{"japanese":"確定申告の書類は所轄の税務署に提出してください。","romaji":"Kakutei shinkoku no shorui wa shokatsu no zeimusho ni teishutsu shite kudasai.","german":"Please submit your tax return documents to the tax office with jurisdiction.","furigana":"かくていしんこく|しょるい|しょかつ|ぜいむしょ|ていしゅつ"},{"japanese":"刑事ドラマでは、本庁と所轄の対立がよく描かれる。","romaji":"Keiji dorama de wa, honchō to shokatsu no tairitsu ga yoku egakareru.","german":"Crime dramas often depict conflict between headquarters and the local station.","furigana":"けいじ|ほんちょう|しょかつ|たいりつ|えが"}],"notes":"所轄 refers to the jurisdiction of an authority over a certain area or subject matter, and above all to the specific office in charge: 所轄の警察署, 所轄の税務署, 所轄官庁. In police and crime-drama language, 所轄 on its own means the local station as opposed to the 本庁 (prefectural police headquarters). 管轄 is the more general word for area of jurisdiction (管轄区域); 所轄 stresses who is actually responsible. Administrative language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"機知に富んだ返答で場を和ませた。","romaji":"Kichi ni tonda hentō de ba o nagomaseta.","german":"He lightened the mood with a witty reply.","furigana":"きち|と|へんとう|ば|なご"},{"japanese":"彼は機知を働かせて、記者の意地悪な質問をうまくかわした。","romaji":"Kare wa kichi o hatarakasete, kisha no ijiwaru na shitsumon o umaku kawashita.","german":"Using his quick wit, he skillfully deflected the reporter's nasty question.","furigana":"かれ|きち|はたら|きしゃ|いじわる|しつもん"},{"japanese":"この小説は、機知に富んだ会話が魅力だ。","romaji":"Kono shōsetsu wa, kichi ni tonda kaiwa ga miryoku da.","german":"The charm of this novel lies in its witty dialogue.","furigana":"しょうせつ|きち|と|かいわ|みりょく"}],"notes":"機知 is quick, clever wit: the ability to make a smart, often humorous remark at just the right moment. It almost always appears in 機知に富む \"witty\" (機知に富んだ会話) or 機知を働かせる. Compared with ユーモア \"humor\" and 冗談 \"joke\", 機知 emphasizes intelligence and spontaneity; the loanword ウィット is synonymous. For practical presence of mind in action, 機転 is more usual (機転が利く). Elevated, mostly complimentary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"心配していたが結局取り越し苦労だった。","romaji":"Shinpai shite ita ga kekkyoku torikoshi gurō datta.","german":"I had been worried, but in the end it was needless worry.","furigana":"しんぱい|けっきょくと|こ|ぐろう"},{"japanese":"母はいつも取り越し苦労ばかりしている。","romaji":"Haha wa itsumo torikoshi gurō bakari shite iru.","german":"My mother is always worrying needlessly.","furigana":"はは|と|こ|ぐろう"},{"japanese":"雨の心配は取り越し苦労に終わり、運動会は晴天だった。","romaji":"Ame no shinpai wa torikoshi gurō ni owari, undōkai wa seiten datta.","german":"The worry about rain turned out to be groundless, and the sports day was held under clear skies.","furigana":"あめ|しんぱい|と|こ|ぐろう|お|うんどうかい|せいてん"}],"notes":"取り越し苦労 literally means \"advance trouble\" (取り越す \"to anticipate\"): one worries in advance about things that then never happen. Typical is the retrospective 取り越し苦労だった or 取り越し苦労に終わった \"the worry was unfounded\", as well as 取り越し苦労をする. Neutral and suitable for everyday use; the literary synonym is 杞憂, and colloquially one also says 心配しすぎ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"コンサートの余韻に浸っている。","romaji":"Konsāto no yoin ni hitatte iru.","german":"He is still basking in the afterglow of the concert.","furigana":"よいん|ひた"},{"japanese":"鐘の余韻がいつまでも響いていた。","romaji":"Kane no yoin ga itsumade mo hibiite ita.","german":"The resonance of the bell kept ringing for a long time.","furigana":"かね|よいん|ひび"},{"japanese":"結末をはっきり書かない、余韻の残る小説だった。","romaji":"Ketsumatsu o hakkiri kakanai, yoin no nokoru shōsetsu datta.","german":"It was a novel with an open ending that stays with you.","furigana":"けつまつ|か|よいん|のこ|しょうせつ"}],"notes":"余韻 is first of all the lingering resonance of a sound (鐘の余韻), and then the pleasant afterglow of an experience: 余韻に浸る \"to bask in the afterglow\", 余韻が残る, 余韻を楽しむ. In literary criticism, 余韻のある文章 means a suggestive text that lingers. The connotation is positive; for negative aftereffects one uses 余波 or 後遺症. Slightly elevated.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"金融危機の余波が世界中に広がった。","romaji":"Kin'yū kiki no yoha ga sekaijū ni hirogatta.","german":"The repercussions of the financial crisis spread around the world.","furigana":"きんゆうきき|よは|せかいじゅう|ひろ"},{"japanese":"台風の余波で、海はまだ荒れている。","romaji":"Taifū no yoha de, umi wa mada arete iru.","german":"Because of the aftereffects of the typhoon, the sea is still rough.","furigana":"たいふう|よは|うみ|あ"},{"japanese":"円安の余波を受けて、輸入品の値段が上がった。","romaji":"En'yasu no yoha o ukete, yunyūhin no nedan ga agatta.","german":"As a result of the weak yen, the prices of imported goods have risen.","furigana":"えんやす|よは|う|ゆにゅうひん|ねだん|あ"}],"notes":"余波 literally means the waves that remain after a storm, and figuratively the (mostly negative) aftereffects of an event. Typical: 〜の余波を受ける \"to be hit by the consequences of\", 余波が及ぶ/広がる, 〜の余波で \"as a result of\". With weather, 台風の余波 is still almost literal. 影響 is neutral and general, 余韻 the positive afterglow of an experience. News language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"急激な改革の反動が起きた。","romaji":"Kyūgeki na kaikaku no handō ga okita.","german":"There was a backlash against the radical reforms.","furigana":"きゅうげき|かいかく|はんどう|お"},{"japanese":"ダイエットの反動で、つい食べ過ぎてしまった。","romaji":"Daietto no handō de, tsui tabesugite shimatta.","german":"As a reaction to my diet, I ended up overeating.","furigana":"はんどう|た|す"},{"japanese":"銃を撃つと、反動で肩に強い衝撃が来る。","romaji":"Jū o utsu to, handō de kata ni tsuyoi shōgeki ga kuru.","german":"When you fire a gun, you feel a strong shock in your shoulder from the recoil.","furigana":"じゅう|う|はんどう|かた|つよ|しょうげき|く"}],"notes":"反動 is the counter-movement that follows a strong movement: physically the recoil (銃の反動), in everyday life the swing of a pendulum back, often as 〜の反動で \"as a reaction to\": ダイエットの反動で食べすぎる. In politics, 反動 or 反動的 means \"reactionary\" (反動勢力). Unlike 反発 (active rejection, resistance), 反動 is an almost inevitable counter-reaction; 反応 is the neutral response to a stimulus.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新政策に対する国民の反発が強い。","romaji":"Shin seisaku ni taisuru kokumin no hanpatsu ga tsuyoi.","german":"Public resistance to the new policy is strong.","furigana":"しんせいさく|たい|こくみん|はんぱつ|つよ"},{"japanese":"同じ極どうしの磁石は反発し合う。","romaji":"Onaji kyoku dōshi no jishaku wa hanpatsu shiau.","german":"Magnets with the same poles repel each other.","furigana":"おな|きょく|じしゃく|はんぱつ|あ"},{"japanese":"前日の大幅安から一転、株価は大きく反発した。","romaji":"Zenjitsu no ōhabayasu kara itten, kabuka wa ōkiku hanpatsu shita.","german":"Reversing the previous day's sharp drop, stock prices rebounded strongly.","furigana":"ぜんじつ|おおはばやす|いってん|かぶか|おお|はんぱつ"}],"notes":"反発 is above all resistance or rejection against something: 〜に反発する, 〜への反発, 反発を招く/買う \"to provoke resentment\", 反発を強める. Physically it means \"to repel each other\" (磁石が反発し合う), and on the stock market \"to rebound after a decline\" (株価が反発する). Intransitive する-verb. 反抗 is rebellion against authority (反抗期), 反対 is a matter-of-fact opposing position, and 反動 is the inevitable counter-reaction.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"リスクの忌避が投資を妨げている。","romaji":"Risuku no kihi ga tōshi o samatagete iru.","german":"Risk aversion is hindering investment.","furigana":"きひ|とうし|さまた"},{"japanese":"被告側は裁判官の忌避を申し立てた。","romaji":"Hikokugawa wa saibankan no kihi o mōshitateta.","german":"The defense filed a motion to recuse the judge.","furigana":"ひこくがわ|さいばんかん|きひ|もう|た"},{"japanese":"戦時中、徴兵を忌避して国外に逃れた若者もいた。","romaji":"Senjichū, chōhei o kihi shite kokugai ni nogareta wakamono mo ita.","german":"During the war, some young men fled abroad to evade conscription.","furigana":"せんじちゅう|ちょうへい|きひ|こくがい|のが|わかもの"}],"notes":"忌避 means to deliberately avoid something or evade it out of aversion or fear: 徴兵忌避 \"draft evasion\", リスク忌避 \"risk aversion\", 責任を忌避する. In law, 忌避 is the challenge of a judge for bias (裁判官の忌避を申し立てる). There is also 忌避剤 \"repellent\". 回避 is the neutral \"to avoid, to sidestep\" (危険を回避する); 忌避 sounds more like inner revulsion. Very literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"現実逃避をしても問題は解決しない。","romaji":"Genjitsu tōhi o shite mo mondai wa kaiketsu shinai.","german":"Escaping from reality will not solve the problem.","furigana":"げんじつとうひ|もんだい|かいけつ"},{"japanese":"試験前になると、つい部屋の掃除に逃避してしまう。","romaji":"Shiken mae ni naru to, tsui heya no sōji ni tōhi shite shimau.","german":"Before exams, I always end up escaping into cleaning my room.","furigana":"しけんまえ|へや|そうじ|とうひ"},{"japanese":"二人は家族の反対を押し切って逃避行に出た。","romaji":"Futari wa kazoku no hantai o oshikitte tōhikō ni deta.","german":"Defying their families' opposition, the two ran away together.","furigana":"ふたり|かぞく|はんたい|お|き|とうひこう|で"}],"notes":"逃避 is fleeing from something unpleasant, mostly in a psychological sense: 現実逃避 \"escape from reality\", 〜から逃避する, 〜に逃避する \"to take refuge in something\". 逃避行 is flight into the unknown, typically by a pair of lovers. In economics there is 資本逃避 \"capital flight\". For the physical flight of a culprit one says 逃走, for escaping from danger 脱出. Intransitive する-verb.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"恩師への敬慕の念は、今も少しも変わらない。","romaji":"Onshi e no keibo no nen wa, ima mo sukoshi mo kawaranai.","german":"My reverence for my former teacher has not changed in the slightest, even now.","furigana":"おんし|けいぼ|ねん|いま|すこ|か"},{"japanese":"弟子たちは師匠を心から敬慕していた。","romaji":"Deshitachi wa shishō o kokoro kara keibo shite ita.","german":"The disciples revered their master from the bottom of their hearts.","furigana":"でし|ししょう|こころ|けいぼ"},{"japanese":"地元では今も、その医師を敬慕する人が多い。","romaji":"Jimoto de wa ima mo, sono ishi o keibo suru hito ga ōi.","german":"In the area, many people still revere that doctor today.","furigana":"じもと|いま|いし|けいぼ|ひと|おお"}],"notes":"敬慕 combines respect (敬) with heartfelt affection (慕): one reveres someone and feels inwardly attached to them. Typical are 敬慕の念を抱く and 〜を敬慕する; objects are teachers, masters, parents or great figures. The word is very literary. 尊敬 is the normal everyday expression for respect, 崇拝 a stronger, idealizing worship, and 思慕 yearning affection.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"パリへの憧憬を抱いて留学した。","romaji":"Pari e no dōkei o idaite ryūgaku shita.","german":"He went abroad to study with a longing for Paris.","furigana":"どうけい|いだ|りゅうがく"},{"japanese":"少年時代の彼には、都会への強い憧憬があった。","romaji":"Shōnen jidai no kare ni wa, tokai e no tsuyoi dōkei ga atta.","german":"As a boy, he had a strong yearning for the big city.","furigana":"しょうねんじだい|かれ|とかい|つよ|どうけい"},{"japanese":"西洋文化への憧憬は、明治の作家たちに共通していた。","romaji":"Seiyō bunka e no dōkei wa, Meiji no sakkatachi ni kyōtsū shite ita.","german":"A yearning for Western culture was common to the writers of the Meiji era.","furigana":"せいようぶんか|どうけい|めいじ|さっか|きょうつう"}],"notes":"憧憬 is the literary counterpart of 憧れ: longing and infatuation for something idealized, often distant or unattainable. Typical are 〜への憧憬, 憧憬を抱く and 憧憬の念. The usual reading is どうけい; the original reading しょうけい is also considered correct. In everyday speech one says 憧れ/憧れる. 渇望, by contrast, is a burning desire for something one lacks.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自由を渇望する人々が立ち上がった。","romaji":"Jiyū o katsubō suru hitobito ga tachiagatta.","german":"People who longed for freedom rose up.","furigana":"じゆう|かつぼう|ひとびと|た|あ"},{"japanese":"彼の演技には、認められたいという渇望がにじみ出ていた。","romaji":"Kare no engi ni wa, mitomeraretai to iu katsubō ga nijimidete ita.","german":"His acting oozed a craving to be recognized.","furigana":"かれ|えんぎ|みと|かつぼう|で"},{"japanese":"勝利への渇望が、彼女を厳しい練習に向かわせた。","romaji":"Shōri e no katsubō ga, kanojo o kibishii renshū ni mukawaseta.","german":"Her desire for victory drove her to hard training.","furigana":"しょうり|かつぼう|かのじょ|きび|れんしゅう|む"}],"notes":"渇望 literally means \"to thirst for\" (渇 = thirst) and refers to a burning desire for something one lacks: freedom, peace, love, recognition, victory. It is a transitive する-verb (〜を渇望する); as a noun it appears with 〜への渇望 or 渇望感. It is stronger than 切望 and 熱望 and sounds literary. 憧憬 is more of an infatuated longing for an ideal.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"優勝は長年の悲願だった。","romaji":"Yūshō wa naganen no higan datta.","german":"Winning the championship had been a long-cherished wish.","furigana":"ゆうしょう|ながねん|ひがん"},{"japanese":"地元の人々の悲願だった橋が、ついに完成した。","romaji":"Jimoto no hitobito no higan datta hashi ga, tsui ni kansei shita.","german":"The bridge that the locals had longed for was finally completed.","furigana":"じもと|ひとびと|ひがん|はし|かんせい"},{"japanese":"被爆者たちは、核兵器の廃絶を悲願として訴え続けている。","romaji":"Hibakushatachi wa, kaku heiki no haizetsu o higan to shite uttae tsuzukete iru.","german":"The atomic bomb survivors continue to appeal for the abolition of nuclear weapons as their dearest wish.","furigana":"ひばくしゃ|かくへいき|はいぜつ|ひがん|うった|つづ"}],"notes":"悲願 is a long-cherished, fervent wish that often comes true only after many setbacks; originally it denotes the Buddhist vow to save all beings. Typical are 長年の悲願, 悲願の初優勝, 悲願を達成する/果たす, 悲願がかなう. It carries emotional weight and often concerns a community (team, region, nation). 念願 is lighter and more personal (念願のマイホーム). Common in sports and news language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"念願のマイホームを手に入れた。","romaji":"Nengan no mai hōmu o te ni ireta.","german":"He realized his long-cherished wish of owning a home.","furigana":"ねんがん|て|い"},{"japanese":"念願かなって、来月からパリで働くことになった。","romaji":"Nengan kanatte, raigetsu kara Pari de hataraku koto ni natta.","german":"My dream is coming true: from next month I will be working in Paris.","furigana":"ねんがん|らいげつ|はたら"},{"japanese":"定年後、念願だった四国のお遍路に出かけた。","romaji":"Teinengo, nengan datta Shikoku no ohenro ni dekaketa.","german":"After retirement, he finally set out on the long-awaited pilgrimage around Shikoku.","furigana":"ていねんご|ねんがん|しこく|へんろ|で"}],"notes":"念願 is a heartfelt wish held for a long time. It most often appears attributively as 念願の + noun (念願のマイホーム, 念願の一人暮らし) or in 念願がかなう / 念願かなって \"the wish came true\" and 念願を果たす. As a する-verb it is rare. Compared with 悲願, 念願 is lighter, more personal and suited to everyday use; 夢 is the simplest everyday word.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"両家の間に遺恨が残っている。","romaji":"Ryōke no aida ni ikon ga nokotte iru.","german":"A grudge remains between the two families.","furigana":"りょうけ|あいだ|いこん|のこ"},{"japanese":"前回の乱闘騒ぎのせいで、両チームの試合は遺恨試合と呼ばれている。","romaji":"Zenkai no rantō sawagi no sei de, ryō chīmu no shiai wa ikon jiai to yobarete iru.","german":"Because of the brawl last time, the match between the two teams is called a grudge match.","furigana":"ぜんかい|らんとうさわ|りょう|しあい|いこんじあい|よ"},{"japanese":"強引な合併は社員の間に遺恨を残した。","romaji":"Gōin na gappei wa shain no aida ni ikon o nokoshita.","german":"The forced merger left lasting resentment among the employees.","furigana":"ごういん|がっぺい|しゃいん|あいだ|いこん|のこ"}],"notes":"遺恨 is a grudge that remains for a long time after an incident (遺 \"to leave behind\", 恨 \"grudge\"). Typical are 遺恨が残る, 遺恨を残す, 〜に遺恨を抱く and 遺恨を晴らす \"to take revenge for an old grudge\". In sports, 遺恨試合 is a match with a bad history. The everyday word is 恨み; わだかまり is a quieter, unspoken ill feeling. Literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの日の判断を、悔恨の念とともに振り返った。","romaji":"Ano hi no handan o, kaikon no nen to tomo ni furikaetta.","german":"He looked back on the decision of that day with deep remorse.","furigana":"ひ|はんだん|かいこん|ねん|ふ|かえ"},{"japanese":"彼は晩年、若い頃の過ちへの悔恨にさいなまれた。","romaji":"Kare wa bannen, wakai koro no ayamachi e no kaikon ni sainamareta.","german":"In his later years he was tormented by remorse over the mistakes of his youth.","furigana":"かれ|ばんねん|わか|ころ|あやま|かいこん"},{"japanese":"被告は法廷で悔恨の涙を流した。","romaji":"Hikoku wa hōtei de kaikon no namida o nagashita.","german":"The defendant shed tears of remorse in court.","furigana":"ひこく|ほうてい|かいこん|なみだ|なが"}],"notes":"悔恨 is deep, tormenting remorse over one's own actions. It is very literary and appears mainly in fixed combinations: 悔恨の念, 悔恨の涙, 悔恨にさいなまれる \"to be tormented by remorse\". In everyday speech one says 後悔. 痛恨 refers more to the bitter pain over a single mistake (痛恨のミス), 遺憾 to formal regret over a matter.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"痛恨のミスで試合に負けた。","romaji":"Tsūkon no misu de shiai ni maketa.","german":"He lost the game because of a regrettable error.","furigana":"つうこん|しあい|ま"},{"japanese":"今回の不祥事は痛恨の極みであり、深くお詫び申し上げます。","romaji":"Konkai no fushōji wa tsūkon no kiwami de ari, fukaku owabi mōshiagemasu.","german":"This scandal is deeply regrettable, and we sincerely apologize.","furigana":"こんかい|ふしょうじ|つうこん|きわ|ふか|わ|もう|あ"},{"japanese":"締め切りを一日間違えたのは痛恨だった。","romaji":"Shimekiri o ichinichi machigaeta no wa tsūkon datta.","german":"It was painful that I had mistaken the deadline by one day.","furigana":"し|き|いちにちまちが|つうこん"}],"notes":"痛恨 refers to the bitter pain over something one deeply regrets, usually one's own mistake or a missed chance. It is most often used attributively: 痛恨のミス/エラー/一打 \"a costly, bitter error\" (sports reports). In official apologies, 痛恨の極み \"utterly regrettable\" is used. Unlike 悔恨 (lasting inner remorse), 痛恨 refers to a concrete, painful event.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今回の事態は誠に遺憾である。","romaji":"Konkai no jitai wa makoto ni ikan de aru.","german":"The present situation is truly regrettable.","furigana":"こんかい|じたい|まこと|いかん"},{"japanese":"政府は隣国の行動に対し、遺憾の意を表明した。","romaji":"Seifu wa ringoku no kōdō ni taishi, ikan no i o hyōmei shita.","german":"The government expressed regret over the neighboring country's actions.","furigana":"せいふ|りんごく|こうどう|たい|いかん|い|ひょうめい"},{"japanese":"彼女は決勝で実力を遺憾なく発揮した。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kesshō de jitsuryoku o ikan naku hakki shita.","german":"In the final, she showed her full ability.","furigana":"かのじょ|けっしょう|じつりょく|いかん|はっき"}],"notes":"遺憾 formally expresses that something is regrettable or unsatisfactory; it is the language of government offices, companies and diplomacy: 誠に遺憾である, 遺憾に思う, 遺憾の意を表する \"to express regret\", 遺憾ながら \"regrettably\". Important: 遺憾 is not an apology but only regret, which is why it is often criticized as an evasive formula. The fixed phrase 遺憾なく, however, means \"fully\": 実力を遺憾なく発揮する. In everyday speech one says 残念.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"怒りを感じたが自制した。","romaji":"Ikari o kanjita ga jisei shita.","german":"He felt anger, but restrained himself.","furigana":"いか|かん|じせい"},{"japanese":"子どもに自制心を身につけさせるのは難しい。","romaji":"Kodomo ni jiseishin o mi ni tsukesaseru no wa muzukashii.","german":"It is hard to teach children self-control.","furigana":"こ|じせいしん|み|むずか"},{"japanese":"国連は両国に最大限の自制を求めた。","romaji":"Kokuren wa ryōkoku ni saidaigen no jisei o motometa.","german":"The UN called on both countries to exercise the utmost restraint.","furigana":"こくれん|りょうこく|さいだいげん|じせい|もと"}],"notes":"自制 is the ability to keep one's own feelings and desires in check. As a する-verb it often stands without an object (怒りを感じたが自制した); 自制心 \"self-control\" and 自制が利かない \"cannot control oneself\" are also common. In diplomacy, 自制を求める/促す means \"to call for restraint\". 自粛, by contrast, is voluntary abstention from certain activities, and 我慢 is enduring or putting up with something.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"感染拡大を防ぐため外出を自粛した。","romaji":"Kansen kakudai o fusegu tame gaishutsu o jishuku shita.","german":"To prevent the spread of infection, people refrained from going out.","furigana":"かんせんかくだい|ふせ|がいしゅつ|じしゅく"},{"japanese":"不祥事を受けて、その俳優はしばらく芸能活動を自粛することになった。","romaji":"Fushōji o ukete, sono haiyū wa shibaraku geinō katsudō o jishuku suru koto ni natta.","german":"In response to the scandal, the actor decided to voluntarily suspend his entertainment activities for a while.","furigana":"ふしょうじ|う|はいゆう|げいのうかつどう|じしゅく"},{"japanese":"震災の直後だったので、祭りの花火は自粛された。","romaji":"Shinsai no chokugo datta node, matsuri no hanabi wa jishuku sareta.","german":"Because it was right after the earthquake, the fireworks at the festival were called off.","furigana":"しんさい|ちょくご|まつ|はなび|じしゅく"}],"notes":"自粛 means voluntarily refraining from or limiting something, often out of consideration or under social pressure. Typical are 外出自粛, 営業自粛, 自粛要請 \"request for self-restraint\" (Covid era), 活動を自粛する after a scandal, and cancelling festive events after disasters. Transitive する-verb: 〜を自粛する, often in the passive. 自制 is inner self-control, and 遠慮 is polite reserve in dealing with others.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自嘲気味に笑いながら話した。","romaji":"Jichō gimi ni warai nagara hanashita.","german":"Er sprach mit einem selbstironischen Lächeln.","furigana":"じちょうぎみ|わら|はな"},{"japanese":"「どうせ僕なんて」と彼は自嘲した。","romaji":"\"Dōse boku nante\" to kare wa jichō shita.","german":"„Ich bin doch sowieso ein Niemand“, spottete er über sich selbst.","furigana":"ぼく|かれ|じちょう"},{"japanese":"万年平社員だと、自嘲的な口調で語った。","romaji":"Mannen hirashain da to, jichōteki na kuchō de katatta.","german":"In bitter-ironischem Ton erzählte er, er sei ein ewiger einfacher Angestellter.","furigana":"まんねんひらしゃいん|じちょうてき|くちょう|かた"}],"notes":"自嘲 heißt, sich über sich selbst lustig zu machen, meist mit einem bitteren oder resignierten Unterton, weniger spielerisch als das deutsche „Selbstironie“. Typische Formen sind 自嘲気味に „leicht selbstspöttisch“, 自嘲的な笑み/口調 und 自嘲する. 自虐 ist die stärkere Selbstherabsetzung, heute oft in 自虐ネタ „Witze auf eigene Kosten“. Schriftsprachlich.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"失敗が続いて自暴自棄になった。","romaji":"Shippai ga tsuzuite jibō jiki ni natta.","german":"After repeated failures, he fell into despair.","furigana":"しっぱい|つづ|じぼうじき"},{"japanese":"自暴自棄にならず、前を向こう。","romaji":"Jibō jiki ni narazu, mae o mukō.","german":"Don't give up on yourself; look ahead.","furigana":"じぼうじき|まえ|む"},{"japanese":"仕事を失った彼は、自暴自棄な生活を送っていた。","romaji":"Shigoto o ushinatta kare wa, jibō jiki na seikatsu o okutte ita.","german":"After losing his job, he lived a reckless, self-destructive life.","furigana":"しごと|うしな|かれ|じぼうじき|せいかつ|おく"}],"notes":"Literally: 自暴 \"to do violence to oneself\" and 自棄 \"to throw oneself away\". It means giving up all hope out of despair and letting oneself go. Typical are 自暴自棄になる and 自暴自棄な／の生活; in advice it is often negated: 自暴自棄にならないで. Colloquially, やけになる corresponds to it.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"戦争で街が廃墟と化した。","romaji":"Sensō de machi ga haikyo to kashita.","german":"The city was reduced to ruins by the war.","furigana":"せんそう|まち|はいきょ|か"},{"japanese":"山奥に廃墟となったホテルがある。","romaji":"Yamaoku ni haikyo to natta hoteru ga aru.","german":"Deep in the mountains there is a hotel that has become a ruin.","furigana":"やまおく|はいきょ"},{"japanese":"週末に廃墟の写真を撮りに行くのが趣味だ。","romaji":"Shūmatsu ni haikyo no shashin o tori ni iku no ga shumi da.","german":"My hobby is going out on weekends to take photos of ruins.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|はいきょ|しゃしん|と|い|しゅみ"}],"notes":"廃墟 are abandoned, decayed buildings or entire places: 廃墟と化す \"to turn into ruins\", 廃墟になる, 廃墟となった工場. Today there are also hobbies such as 廃墟探索 \"urban exploration\". 遺跡, by contrast, denotes archaeological or historical sites, 残骸 the debris and wreckage of something destroyed, and 跡 the spot where something used to stand.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"墜落した飛行機の残骸が発見された。","romaji":"Tsuiraku shita hikōki no zangai ga hakken sareta.","german":"The wreckage of the crashed airplane was found.","furigana":"ついらく|ひこうき|ざんがい|はっけん"},{"japanese":"事故現場には車の残骸が散らばっていた。","romaji":"Jiko genba ni wa kuruma no zangai ga chirabatte ita.","german":"Pieces of wrecked cars were scattered at the accident site.","furigana":"じこげんば|くるま|ざんがい|ち"},{"japanese":"パーティーの翌朝、部屋にはお菓子の袋や空き缶の残骸が残っていた。","romaji":"Pātī no yokuasa, heya ni wa okashi no fukuro ya akikan no zangai ga nokotte ita.","german":"The morning after the party, the remains of snack bags and empty cans were left in the room.","furigana":"よくあさ|へや|かし|ふくろ|あ|かん|ざんがい|のこ"}],"notes":"残骸 are the destroyed remains of something that was once whole: wreckage of airplanes and cars, rubble of a building, and figuratively also 夢の残骸 (literary). Typical: 機体の残骸, 残骸が散乱する, 残骸を回収する. 廃墟 is an abandoned building that is still standing, 瓦礫 is the rubble after a collapse, and 破片 means individual splinters. Also used jokingly for the leftovers of a party.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"封建時代の残滓がまだ社会に残っている。","romaji":"Hōken jidai no zanshi ga mada shakai ni nokotte iru.","german":"Relics of the feudal era still remain in society.","furigana":"ほうけんじだい|ざんし|しゃかい|のこ"},{"japanese":"フィルターには燃えかすなどの残滓がたまっていた。","romaji":"Firutā ni wa moekasu nado no zanshi ga tamatte ita.","german":"Residues such as burnt remains had collected in the filter.","furigana":"も|ざんし"},{"japanese":"彼の言葉には、かつての情熱の残滓すら感じられなかった。","romaji":"Kare no kotoba ni wa, katsute no jōnetsu no zanshi sura kanjirarenakatta.","german":"In his words, one could not feel even a remnant of his former passion.","furigana":"かれ|ことば|じょうねつ|ざんし|かん"}],"notes":"残滓 originally means \"sediment, residue\" (滓 = かす) and is used mainly figuratively for leftovers of an outdated era or way of thinking: 封建制の残滓, 植民地主義の残滓. The word is very literary; ざんし is the established reading, and the original reading is さんし. In engineering and chemistry it denotes concrete residues. 名残 sounds wistful and positive, while 痕跡 means neutral traces.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"犯人の痕跡が現場に残されていた。","romaji":"Hannin no konseki ga genba ni nokosarete ita.","german":"Traces of the culprit were left at the scene.","furigana":"はんにん|こんせき|げんば|のこ"},{"japanese":"火星にはかつて水が流れていた痕跡がある。","romaji":"Kasei ni wa katsute mizu ga nagarete ita konseki ga aru.","german":"There are traces on Mars showing that water once flowed there.","furigana":"かせい|みず|なが|こんせき"},{"japanese":"古い町並みには、宿場町だった頃の痕跡がとどめられている。","romaji":"Furui machinami ni wa, shukubamachi datta koro no konseki ga todomerarete iru.","german":"The old streetscape preserves traces of the time when it was a post town.","furigana":"ふる|まちな|しゅくばまち|ころ|こんせき"}],"notes":"痕跡 are traces proving that something existed or happened. Typical are 痕跡を残す/消す/とどめる, 痕跡がある and 痕跡もなく \"without a trace\". It is used in criminology, archaeology and science (痕跡器官 \"vestigial organ\"). 跡 is the general everyday word (足跡, 傷跡), while 形跡 emphasizes signs that someone has done something (侵入した形跡).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"斜陽産業から新たな成長分野への転換が必要だ。","romaji":"Shayō sangyō kara arata na seichō bun'ya e no tenkan ga hitsuyō da.","german":"A shift from declining industries to new growth fields is necessary.","furigana":"しゃようさんぎょう|あら|せいちょうぶんや|てんかん|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"かつて栄えた炭鉱の町は、エネルギー政策の転換とともに斜陽化した。","romaji":"Katsute sakaeta tankō no machi wa, enerugī seisaku no tenkan to tomo ni shayōka shita.","german":"The once-thriving coal-mining town went into decline with the change in energy policy.","furigana":"さか|たんこう|まち|せいさく|てんかん|しゃようか"},{"japanese":"窓から差し込む斜陽が、部屋を赤く染めていた。","romaji":"Mado kara sashikomu shayō ga, heya o akaku somete ita.","german":"The setting sun streaming in through the window dyed the room red.","furigana":"まど|さ|こ|しゃよう|へや|あか|そ"}],"notes":"斜陽 literally means the evening sun shining in at a slant, but in current usage it mostly means decline: 斜陽産業 \"declining industry\", 斜陽化する \"to go into decline\". The figurative meaning became popular through Dazai Osamu's novel 『斜陽』 (1947) about an impoverished noble family, hence also 斜陽族. The literal meaning is literary; in everyday speech one says 夕日.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"歴史は栄枯盛衰の繰り返しだ。","romaji":"Rekishi wa eiko seisui no kurikaeshi da.","german":"History is a repetition of rise and fall.","furigana":"れきし|えいこせいすい|く|かえ"},{"japanese":"企業の栄枯盛衰は世の常である。","romaji":"Kigyō no eiko seisui wa yo no tsune de aru.","german":"The rise and fall of companies is the way of the world.","furigana":"きぎょう|えいこせいすい|よ|つね"},{"japanese":"さびれた炭鉱町を歩くと、栄枯盛衰を感じずにはいられない。","romaji":"Sabireta tankō-machi o aruku to, eiko seisui o kanjizu ni wa irarenai.","german":"Walking through the deserted mining town, one cannot help feeling the transience of glory.","furigana":"たんこうまち|ある|えいこせいすい|かん"}],"notes":"栄枯 is \"flourishing and withering\", 盛衰 \"prosperity and decline\"; together 栄枯盛衰 describes the inevitable alternation of rise and fall in empires, families, companies or cities. The classic phrase is 栄枯盛衰は世の習い／世の常. Other patterns: 栄枯盛衰を見る／感じる, 〜の栄枯盛衰. The tone is reflective and slightly melancholy, comparable to the Buddhist 諸行無常.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"犯罪組織が跋扈する地域では、治安が急速に悪化した。","romaji":"Hanzai soshiki ga bakko suru chiiki de wa, chian ga kyūsoku ni akka shita.","german":"In the area where criminal organizations run rampant, public safety deteriorated rapidly.","furigana":"はんざいそしき|ばっこ|ちいき|ちあん|きゅうそく|あっか"},{"japanese":"ネット上には悪質な詐欺業者が跋扈している。","romaji":"Nettojō ni wa akushitsu na sagi gyōsha ga bakko shite iru.","german":"Malicious scam operators are rampant on the internet.","furigana":"じょう|あくしつ|さぎぎょうしゃ|ばっこ"},{"japanese":"戦国時代には、各地で群雄が跋扈していた。","romaji":"Sengoku jidai ni wa, kakuchi de gun'yū ga bakko shite ita.","german":"In the Warring States period, rival warlords ran rampant in every region.","furigana":"せんごくじだい|かくち|ぐんゆう|ばっこ"}],"notes":"跋扈 means that bad actors run rampant unhindered and spread themselves around. The subject is mostly people or groups: 悪徳業者/犯罪組織/詐欺師が跋扈する; intensified in the yojijukugo 跳梁跋扈. It is an intransitive する-verb, strongly negative and very literary; because of the difficult kanji it is often written ばっこ. For abuses themselves, 横行 fits better (不正が横行する), and for diseases or vices 蔓延.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"心の蟠りが解けないまま別れた。","romaji":"Kokoro no wadakamari ga tokenai mama wakareta.","german":"They parted without the resentment in their hearts being resolved.","furigana":"こころ|わだかま|と|わか"},{"japanese":"話し合ったことで、二人の間のわだかまりが消えた。","romaji":"Hanashiatta koto de, futari no aida no wadakamari ga kieta.","german":"Talking it over made the bad feeling between the two disappear.","furigana":"はな|あ|ふたり|あいだ|き"},{"japanese":"昔のことには、もう何のわだかまりもない。","romaji":"Mukashi no koto ni wa, mō nan no wadakamari mo nai.","german":"I no longer hold any grudge about what happened back then.","furigana":"むかし|なん"}],"notes":"わだかまり is an unspoken ill feeling or unresolved unease that remains in the heart or between people. It is almost always written in kana; 蟠り is rare. Typical: わだかまりが残る/ある, わだかまりが解ける/消える, わだかまりを捨てる and わだかまりなく \"without reservation, without resentment\". It is quieter than 遺恨 or 恨み: more of a thorn than open enmity. The related verb 蟠る means \"to lodge itself, to be stuck\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日頃の不満が鬱積して爆発した。","romaji":"Higoro no fuman ga usseki shite bakuhatsu shita.","german":"Everyday dissatisfaction built up and exploded.","furigana":"ひごろ|ふまん|うっせき|ばくはつ"},{"japanese":"長年鬱積していた住民の怒りが、ついに抗議運動となった。","romaji":"Naganen usseki shite ita jūmin no ikari ga, tsui ni kōgi undō to natta.","german":"The residents' anger, which had been building up for years, finally turned into a protest movement.","furigana":"ながねんうっせき|じゅうみん|いか|こうぎうんどう"},{"japanese":"鬱積したストレスを、週末のジョギングで発散している。","romaji":"Usseki shita sutoresu o, shūmatsu no jogingu de hassan shite iru.","german":"I let off the pent-up stress by jogging on weekends.","furigana":"うっせき|しゅうまつ|はっさん"}],"notes":"鬱積 describes how negative feelings such as dissatisfaction, anger or stress accumulate over a long time, often until they burst out: 不満が鬱積する, 鬱積した怒り, 鬱積を晴らす. Intransitive する-verb with が; in newspapers also written うっ積. 鬱屈 emphasizes the depressed, inhibited state of mind, while 蓄積 is neutral accumulation (疲労の蓄積, データの蓄積). Literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"前回の敗北の雪辱を果たした。","romaji":"Zenkai no haiboku no setsujoku o hatashita.","german":"He got his revenge for the previous defeat.","furigana":"ぜんかい|はいぼく|せつじょく|は"},{"japanese":"去年の決勝で負けた相手に、今年は雪辱を期して挑む。","romaji":"Kyonen no kesshō de maketa aite ni, kotoshi wa setsujoku o kishite idomu.","german":"This year we take on the opponent who beat us in last year's final, determined to get revenge.","furigana":"きょねん|けっしょう|ま|あいて|ことし|せつじょく|き|いど"},{"japanese":"一度落ちた試験に再挑戦し、見事に雪辱した。","romaji":"Ichido ochita shiken ni sai chōsen shi, migoto ni setsujoku shita.","german":"I retook the exam I had failed once and brilliantly redeemed myself.","furigana":"いちどお|しけん|さいちょうせん|みごと|せつじょく"}],"notes":"雪辱 literally means \"to wash away (雪ぐ) a disgrace (辱)\": one makes up for an earlier defeat with a later success. Fixed phrases are 雪辱を果たす \"to take revenge\", 雪辱を期す \"to be firmly determined to get revenge\" and 雪辱戦 \"rematch\"; 雪辱を晴らす is considered a mistake (confusion with 屈辱を晴らす). It concerns sports, competitions or exams, not revenge on persons; for that there are 報復 or 復讐.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"報復の連鎖を断ち切ることが重要だ。","romaji":"Hōfuku no rensa o tachikiru koto ga jūyō da.","german":"It is important to break the chain of retaliation.","furigana":"ほうふく|れんさ|た|き|じゅうよう"},{"japanese":"米国の関税引き上げに対し、相手国は報復関税を発動した。","romaji":"Beikoku no kanzei hikiage ni taishi, aitekoku wa hōfuku kanzei o hatsudō shita.","german":"In response to the US tariff increase, the other country imposed retaliatory tariffs.","furigana":"べいこく|かんぜいひ|あ|たい|あいてこく|ほうふくかんぜい|はつどう"},{"japanese":"内部告発をした社員が、会社から報復人事を受けた。","romaji":"Naibu kokuhatsu o shita shain ga, kaisha kara hōfuku jinji o uketa.","german":"The employee who had blown the whistle was punished by the company with a retaliatory transfer.","furigana":"ないぶこくはつ|しゃいん|かいしゃ|ほうふくじんじ|う"}],"notes":"報復 is retaliation for harm suffered, especially between states and organizations or in conflicts: 報復攻撃, 報復措置, 報復関税 \"retaliatory tariffs\", 〜に報復する, 報復の連鎖. In companies, 報復人事 refers to a punitive transfer as an act of revenge. Intransitive する-verb with に. 復讐 is personal, emotional revenge, and 仕返し is the colloquial \"to get back at\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人をだましてきた彼が、今度は自分がだまされた。まさに因果応報だ。","romaji":"Hito o damashite kita kare ga, kondo wa jibun ga damasareta. Masa ni inga ōhō da.","german":"He had always deceived others, and now he himself was deceived. It is truly karma.","furigana":"ひと|かれ|こんど|じぶん|いんがおうほう"},{"japanese":"因果応報というから、人には親切にしておきなさい。","romaji":"Inga ōhō to iu kara, hito ni wa shinsetsu ni shite okinasai.","german":"They say what goes around comes around, so be kind to people.","furigana":"いんがおうほう|ひと|しんせつ"},{"japanese":"いじめをした生徒が後で孤立したのは、因果応報と言えるだろう。","romaji":"Ijime o shita seito ga ato de koritsu shita no wa, inga ōhō to ieru darō.","german":"That the student who bullied others was later isolated himself can probably be called karma.","furigana":"せいと|あと|こりつ|いんがおうほう|い"}],"notes":"因果応報 is composed of 因果 (\"cause and effect\") and 応報 (\"retribution\") and comes from Buddhist teaching: good deeds bring good results, bad deeds bring bad ones. Today it is mostly used in a negative sense, when the consequences of one's own wrongdoing catch up with someone: まさに因果応報だ, 因果応報というものだ. Closer to \"having only oneself to blame\" is 自業自得. The tone is rather literary to elevated-colloquial.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"原因がわからず、暗中模索の状態が続いた。","romaji":"Gen'in ga wakarazu, anchū mosaku no jōtai ga tsuzuita.","german":"Since the cause was unknown, we continued to grope in the dark.","furigana":"げんいん|あんちゅうもさく|じょうたい|つづ"},{"japanese":"新しい事業は、まだ暗中模索の段階だ。","romaji":"Atarashii jigyō wa, mada anchū mosaku no dankai da.","german":"The new business is still at the stage of groping for a way.","furigana":"あたら|じぎょう|あんちゅうもさく|だんかい"},{"japanese":"開発チームは暗中模索しながら、新しい薬を作り上げた。","romaji":"Kaihatsu chīmu wa anchū mosaku shinagara, atarashii kusuri o tsukuriageta.","german":"The development team felt its way forward and created a new drug.","furigana":"かいはつ|あんちゅうもさく|あたら|くすり|つく|あ"}],"notes":"暗中模索 means \"to grope (模索) in the dark (暗中)\": to search for a solution or a way without any clues. It is used as a noun (暗中模索の状態, 暗中模索が続く) or as a する-verb (暗中模索する), often for new projects, research or investigations. Synonymous but more everyday is 手探り（で）. The expression is not necessarily negative, but often describes the difficult initial phase.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しいソフトの使い方に悪戦苦闘した。","romaji":"Atarashii sofuto no tsukaikata ni akusen kutō shita.","german":"I struggled with how to use the new software.","furigana":"あたら|つか|かた|あくせんくとう"},{"japanese":"彼は難問に悪戦苦闘している。","romaji":"Kare wa nanmon ni akusen kutō shite iru.","german":"He is wrestling hard with a difficult problem.","furigana":"かれ|なんもん|あくせんくとう"},{"japanese":"締め切りに間に合わせようと、チーム全員で悪戦苦闘した。","romaji":"Shimekiri ni ma ni awaseyō to, chīmu zen'in de akusen kutō shita.","german":"To meet the deadline, the whole team struggled desperately.","furigana":"し|き|ま|あ|ぜんいん|あくせんくとう"}],"notes":"Literally: 悪戦 \"bad (difficult) fight\" and 苦闘 \"agonizing struggle\". It means to struggle doggedly with something under adverse circumstances. Typical is 〜に悪戦苦闘する, for example with new technology, difficult tasks or tight deadlines. Neutral; unlike 四苦八苦, it stresses the stubborn fight more than the distress.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"改革は前途多難だが諦めない。","romaji":"Kaikaku wa zento tanan da ga akiramenai.","german":"The reform faces many difficulties ahead, but he will not give up.","furigana":"かいかく|ぜんとたなん|あきら"},{"japanese":"新政権は少数与党で、前途多難な船出となった。","romaji":"Shin seiken wa shōsū yotō de, zento tanan na funade to natta.","german":"The new government is a minority government and got off to a difficult start.","furigana":"しんせいけん|しょうすうよとう|ぜんとたなん|ふなで"},{"japanese":"開店初日から大雨で客が来ず、前途多難を思わせるスタートだった。","romaji":"Kaiten shonichi kara ōame de kyaku ga kozu, zento tanan o omowaseru sutāto datta.","german":"On the opening day, no customers came because of heavy rain, a start that hinted at hardships to come.","furigana":"かいてんしょにち|おおあめ|きゃく|こ|ぜんとたなん|おも"}],"notes":"前途多難 is composed of 前途 \"the road ahead, the future\" and 多難 \"many difficulties\": an undertaking still faces many obstacles. Typical patterns are 前途多難だ, 前途多難な船出 \"difficult start\" and 前途多難が予想される. The opposite is 前途洋々 \"bright prospects\". Frequent in news and speeches; in everyday speech also slightly jokingly about one's own plans.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"連敗でチーム全体が意気消沈した。","romaji":"Renpai de chīmu zentai ga iki shōchin shita.","german":"The whole team was demoralized by the losing streak.","furigana":"れんぱい|ぜんたい|いきしょうちん"},{"japanese":"第一志望に落ちて意気消沈している息子を、父は黙って見守った。","romaji":"Daiichi shibō ni ochite iki shōchin shite iru musuko o, chichi wa damatte mimamotta.","german":"The father silently watched over his son, who was dejected after failing his first-choice school.","furigana":"だいいちしぼう|お|いきしょうちん|むすこ|ちち|だま|みまも"},{"japanese":"プレゼンで厳しい指摘を受け、彼は意気消沈して会議室を出た。","romaji":"Purezen de kibishii shiteki o uke, kare wa iki shōchin shite kaigishitsu o deta.","german":"After receiving harsh criticism in the presentation, he left the meeting room dejected.","furigana":"きび|してき|う|かれ|いきしょうちん|かいぎしつ|で"}],"notes":"意気消沈 combines 意気 \"spirit, fighting spirit\" with 消沈 \"to die out, to sink\": one has lost all courage after a failure. It is an intransitive する-verb: 意気消沈する, 意気消沈した様子. Its opposites are 意気揚々 and 意気軒昂. In everyday speech one more often says 落ち込む or しょんぼりする; 意気消沈 sounds somewhat more elevated and is often used in reports.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"満身創痍でもプロジェクトを完遂した。","romaji":"Manshin sōi demo purojekuto o kansui shita.","german":"Even though he was battered all over, he completed the project.","furigana":"まんしんそうい|かんすい"},{"japanese":"激しい戦闘の末、兵士たちは満身創痍で帰還した。","romaji":"Hageshii sentō no sue, heishitachi wa manshin sōi de kikan shita.","german":"After fierce fighting, the soldiers returned covered in wounds.","furigana":"はげ|せんとう|すえ|へいし|まんしんそうい|きかん"},{"japanese":"不祥事が続き、内閣は批判を浴びて満身創痍だ。","romaji":"Fushōji ga tsuzuki, naikaku wa hihan o abite manshin sōi da.","german":"After a series of scandals, the cabinet is badly battered by criticism.","furigana":"ふしょうじ|つづ|ないかく|ひはん|あ|まんしんそうい"}],"notes":"満身創痍 is a yojijukugo made of 満身 \"the whole body\" and 創痍 \"wounds\", literally \"wounded all over the body\". Figuratively it describes someone badly battered by criticism, setbacks or overwork, such as a cabinet after scandals or an athlete with many injuries. It is used predicatively (満身創痍だ), adverbially (満身創痍で戦う) and attributively (満身創痍の状態). Elevated, popular in news and sports reports.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"難しい話題を敬遠する傾向がある。","romaji":"Muzukashii wadai o keien suru keikō ga aru.","german":"There is a tendency to avoid difficult topics.","furigana":"むずか|わだい|けいえん|けいこう"},{"japanese":"きつい・汚い・危険な仕事は若者に敬遠されがちだ。","romaji":"Kitsui, kitanai, kiken na shigoto wa wakamono ni keien saregachi da.","german":"Tough, dirty and dangerous jobs tend to be shunned by young people.","furigana":"きたな|きけん|しごと|わかもの|けいえん"},{"japanese":"監督は四番打者を敬遠するよう指示した。","romaji":"Kantoku wa yoban dasha o keien suru yō shiji shita.","german":"The manager instructed the pitcher to intentionally walk the cleanup hitter.","furigana":"かんとく|よばんだしゃ|けいえん|しじ"}],"notes":"敬遠 literally means \"to respect and keep at a distance\": one politely keeps one's distance from someone without really liking them. Today it mostly simply means \"to avoid something unpleasant, to shy away from something\": 〜を敬遠する, 若者に敬遠される職業, 敬遠されがち. In baseball, 敬遠 is the intentional walk, in which a strong batter is sent to base. So politeness is not the core of the meaning; 避ける is the neutral everyday word.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"多忙を極めて休む暇もない。","romaji":"Tabō o kiwamete yasumu hima mo nai.","german":"He is extremely busy and has no time to rest.","furigana":"たぼう|きわ|やす|ひま"},{"japanese":"ご多忙のところ恐れ入りますが、ご確認をお願いいたします。","romaji":"Gotabō no tokoro osoreirimasu ga, gokakunin o onegai itashimasu.","german":"I apologize for troubling you when you are so busy, but I ask you to please check this.","furigana":"たぼう|おそ|い|かくにん|ねが"},{"japanese":"社長は多忙のため、今回の式典を欠席した。","romaji":"Shachō wa tabō no tame, konkai no shikiten o kesseki shita.","german":"The company president was absent from the ceremony because of a busy schedule.","furigana":"しゃちょう|たぼう|こんかい|しきてん|けっせき"}],"notes":"多忙 is the formal equivalent of 忙しい. It is very common in business letters and e-mails: ご多忙のところ/ご多忙中恐れ入りますが \"I am sorry to trouble you when you are so busy\". Other patterns are 多忙を極める \"to be extremely busy\", 多忙な毎日 and 多忙のため欠席する. 繁忙 refers more to periods and workload (繁忙期), 多忙 to people.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"傍目には幸せそうに見える。","romaji":"Hatame ni wa shiawase sō ni mieru.","german":"From the outside, they look happy.","furigana":"はため|しあわ|み"},{"japanese":"彼が落ち込んでいるのは、傍目にも明らかだった。","romaji":"Kare ga ochikonde iru no wa, hatame ni mo akiraka datta.","german":"It was obvious even to an onlooker that he was depressed.","furigana":"かれ|お|こ|はため|あき"},{"japanese":"傍目を気にせず、好きな服を着ればいい。","romaji":"Hatame o ki ni sezu, suki na fuku o kireba ii.","german":"Wear the clothes you like without worrying about what others think.","furigana":"はため|き|す|ふく|き"}],"notes":"傍目 is the view of an outsider, that is, how something looks to others. Typical are 傍目には/傍目から見ると \"seen from the outside\", 傍目にも分かる \"obvious even to outsiders\" and 傍目を気にする \"to worry about what others think\". Often also written はた目. Note: in the proverb 傍目八目 \"onlookers see more than the players\", 傍目 is read おかめ. 人目, by contrast, emphasizes being seen in public.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"経済の沈滞が長引いている。","romaji":"Keizai no chintai ga nagabiite iru.","german":"The economic stagnation is dragging on.","furigana":"けいざい|ちんたい|ながび"},{"japanese":"連敗が続き、チームには沈滞ムードが漂っている。","romaji":"Renpai ga tsuzuki, chīmu ni wa chintai mūdo ga tadayotte iru.","german":"After the losing streak, a mood of stagnation hangs over the team.","furigana":"れんぱい|つづ|ちんたい|ただよ"},{"japanese":"新しい部長の就任で、沈滞していた職場に活気が戻った。","romaji":"Atarashii buchō no shūnin de, chintai shite ita shokuba ni kakki ga modotta.","german":"With the arrival of the new department head, vitality returned to the sluggish workplace.","furigana":"あたら|ぶちょう|しゅうにん|ちんたい|しょくば|かっき|もど"}],"notes":"沈滞 refers to a state without momentum or movement: the economy, a market, an organization or a mood is as if paralyzed. Typical are 沈滞ムード, 沈滞した空気/雰囲気 and 沈滞する (intransitive). 停滞 emphasizes the stalling of progress (景気の停滞, 前線が停滞する), and 低迷 staying at a low level; 沈滞 highlights the lack of drive. Opposite: 活気. Literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"かつて殷賑を極めた港町も、今はひっそりとしている。","romaji":"Katsute inshin o kiwameta minatomachi mo, ima wa hissori to shite iru.","german":"Even the port town that once flourished is quiet now.","furigana":"いんしん|きわ|みなとまち|いま"},{"japanese":"江戸時代の日本橋は、全国から商人が集まり殷賑を極めた。","romaji":"Edo jidai no Nihonbashi wa, zenkoku kara shōnin ga atsumari inshin o kiwameta.","german":"In the Edo period, Nihonbashi was at its most prosperous, as merchants gathered there from all over the country.","furigana":"えどじだい|にほんばし|ぜんこく|しょうにん|あつ|いんしん|きわ"},{"japanese":"小説の冒頭では、戦前の上海の殷賑ぶりが描かれている。","romaji":"Shōsetsu no bōtō de wa, senzen no Shanhai no inshinburi ga egakarete iru.","german":"The novel opens with a description of the bustling prosperity of prewar Shanghai.","furigana":"しょうせつ|ぼうとう|せんぜん||いんしん|えが"}],"notes":"殷賑 is a very elevated, almost archaic word for the lively, prosperous bustle of a city or trading place. It occurs almost only in the fixed phrase 殷賑を極める \"to be at the height of prosperity\" and in forms such as 殷賑ぶり. It is found in historical accounts and literature; in present-day usage one says にぎわい, 繁栄 or 活況.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"経済的窮迫に陥った家庭を支援する。","romaji":"Keizaiteki kyūhaku ni ochiitta katei o shien suru.","german":"We support families who have fallen into financial distress.","furigana":"けいざいてききゅうはく|おちい|かてい|しえん"},{"japanese":"戦後、国の財政は極度に窮迫していた。","romaji":"Sengo, kuni no zaisei wa kyokudo ni kyūhaku shite ita.","german":"After the war, the nation's finances were in extreme straits.","furigana":"せんご|くに|ざいせい|きょくど|きゅうはく"},{"japanese":"生活の窮迫を理由に、学費の免除を申請した。","romaji":"Seikatsu no kyūhaku o riyū ni, gakuhi no menjo o shinsei shita.","german":"Citing the hardship of his livelihood, he applied for an exemption from tuition fees.","furigana":"せいかつ|きゅうはく|りゆう|がくひ|めんじょ|しんせい"}],"notes":"窮迫 is an extremely hard-pressed situation, especially financially: 財政が窮迫する \"the finances are at their end\", 経済的窮迫, 生活の窮迫. It serves as a noun or an intransitive する-verb and is very formal, typical of official documents, history books and newspapers. Similar is 困窮 \"hardship, poverty\" (more the living circumstances of people); 逼迫 denotes a shortage and is also used for resources (医療の逼迫). In everyday speech one says お金に困る.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鬱屈した感情が芸術作品に表れている。","romaji":"Ukkutsu shita kanjō ga geijutsu sakuhin ni arawarete iru.","german":"Repressed feelings show in the work of art.","furigana":"うっくつ|かんじょう|げいじゅつさくひん|あらわ"},{"japanese":"受験に失敗した後、彼は鬱屈した日々を送っていた。","romaji":"Juken ni shippai shita ato, kare wa ukkutsu shita hibi o okutte ita.","german":"After failing the entrance exam, he spent his days in gloom.","furigana":"じゅけん|しっぱい|あと|かれ|うっくつ|ひび|おく"},{"japanese":"言いたいことを言えず、心の中で思いが鬱屈していった。","romaji":"Iitai koto o iezu, kokoro no naka de omoi ga ukkutsu shite itta.","german":"Unable to say what he wanted to say, his feelings grew more pent up inside.","furigana":"い|い|こころ|なか|おも|うっくつ"}],"notes":"鬱屈 describes a depressed state of mind in which feelings find no outlet and one feels inwardly inhibited and closed off. Typical are 鬱屈した感情/思い/日々 and 鬱屈する (intransitive). 鬱積 emphasizes the building up until an outburst, while 鬱屈 is more the depressed, closed-off mood itself. Literary; common in novels and reviews about characters or artists.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"同じ過ちを繰り返さないよう自戒している。","romaji":"Onaji ayamachi o kurikaesanai yō jikai shite iru.","german":"He warns himself not to repeat the same mistake.","furigana":"おな|あやま|く|かえ|じかい"},{"japanese":"自戒を込めて言うが、締め切り直前に慌てるのはよくない。","romaji":"Jikai o komete iu ga, shimekiri chokuzen ni awateru no wa yoku nai.","german":"I say this as a warning to myself as well: getting into a panic right before the deadline is not a good idea.","furigana":"じかい|こ|い|し|き|ちょくぜん|あわ"},{"japanese":"今回の失敗を自戒の念として、手帳に書き留めた。","romaji":"Konkai no shippai o jikai no nen to shite, techō ni kakitometa.","german":"I wrote this failure down in my planner as a reminder to myself.","furigana":"こんかい|しっぱい|じかい|ねん|てちょう|か|と"}],"notes":"自戒 is \"self-admonition\", that is, a conscious warning to oneself not to repeat a mistake. As an intransitive する-verb: 自戒する; very common are 自戒を込めて \"also as a reminder to myself\" (typical in columns and blogs) and 自戒の念. The word is literary; in everyday speech one more often says 反省する \"to reflect on oneself, to regret\" or 自分を戒める. 自制, by contrast, means \"self-control\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"長年の研究が実り本懐を遂げた。","romaji":"Naganen no kenkyū ga minori honkai o togeta.","german":"His years of research bore fruit, and he fulfilled his long-cherished wish.","furigana":"ながねん|けんきゅう|みの|ほんかい|と"},{"japanese":"日本代表に選ばれ、彼はついに本懐を遂げた。","romaji":"Nihon daihyō ni erabare, kare wa tsui ni honkai o togeta.","german":"With his selection for the national team, his heart's desire was finally fulfilled.","furigana":"にほんだいひょう|えら|かれ|ほんかい|と"},{"japanese":"教え子たちが立派に育ってくれたことこそ、教師としての本懐だ。","romaji":"Oshiego-tachi ga rippa ni sodatte kureta koto koso, kyōshi to shite no honkai da.","german":"That my students have turned out well is, for me as a teacher, the true fulfillment.","furigana":"おし|ご|りっぱ|そだ|きょうし|ほんかい"}],"notes":"本懐 is the \"long-cherished wish\", what one has always hoped for from the bottom of one's heart. It almost always appears in the fixed phrase 本懐を遂げる \"to fulfill one's heart's desire, to achieve one's goal\" or predicatively: 〜こそ本懐だ \"that is exactly my true fulfillment\" (also 男子の本懐). The word sounds elevated and somewhat archaic; more neutral are 念願, 宿願, 悲願 (a fervent wish, often unfulfilled for a long time) or 本望.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「直系血族」とは、祖父母、父母、子、孫のように、上下にまっすぐつながる血族のことだ。","romaji":"Chokkei ketsuzoku to wa, sofubo, fubo, ko, mago no yō ni, jōge ni massugu tsunagaru ketsuzoku no koto da.","german":"\"Direct blood relatives\" are blood relatives in a straight line, such as grandparents, parents, children and grandchildren.","furigana":"ちょっけいけつぞく|そふぼ|ふぼ|こ|まご|じょうげ|けつぞく"},{"japanese":"日本では、いとこ同士は結婚できるが、おじと姪のような三親等内の血族は結婚できない。","romaji":"Nihon de wa, itoko dōshi wa kekkon dekiru ga, oji to mei no yō na sanshintō-nai no ketsuzoku wa kekkon dekinai.","german":"In Japan, cousins may marry, but blood relatives within the third degree, such as an uncle and a niece, may not.","furigana":"にほん|どうし|けっこん|めい|さんしんとうない|けつぞく|けっこん"},{"japanese":"相続の手続きのために、亡くなった父の血族を戸籍で調べた。","romaji":"Sōzoku no tetsuzuki no tame ni, nakunatta chichi no ketsuzoku o koseki de shirabeta.","german":"For the inheritance procedure, I looked up my late father's blood relatives in the family register.","furigana":"そうぞく|てつづ|な|ちち|けつぞく|こせき|しら"}],"notes":"血族 is a technical term from law and ethnology for \"blood relatives, blood relationship\". In family law it is contrasted with 姻族 \"relatives by marriage\"; together they form the 親族 \"kinship\". A distinction is made between 直系血族 (direct line: parents, children, grandchildren) and 傍系血族 (collateral line: siblings, cousins). In everyday speech one more often says 血のつながった家族, 血縁 or 身内.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"内因性ぜんそくだと診断されました。","romaji":"Naiinsei zensoku da to shindan saremashita.","german":"I was diagnosed with intrinsic asthma.","furigana":"ないいんせい|しんだん"},{"japanese":"敗因を内因と外因に分けて考えてみよう。","romaji":"Haiin o naiin to gaiin ni wakete kangaete miyō.","german":"Let us divide the reasons for the defeat into internal and external causes.","furigana":"はいいん|ないいん|がいいん|わ|かんが"},{"japanese":"業績悪化の内因は、経営陣の判断ミスにあった。","romaji":"Gyōseki akka no naiin wa, keieijin no handan misu ni atta.","german":"The real internal cause of the worsening business results was the management's poor decisions.","furigana":"ぎょうせきあっか|ないいん|けいえいじん|はんだん"}],"notes":"内因 is the \"internal cause\" of a process; its counterpart is 外因 \"external cause\", and the two often appear as a pair: 内因と外因. In medicine it forms 内因性 \"endogenous, intrinsic\" (内因性うつ病, 内因性ぜんそく). The word is technical or analytical; in everyday speech 原因 or 内部の原因 is enough. Do not confuse it with 要因 \"factor\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は、漢方の薬方を研究しています。","romaji":"Watashi wa, kanpō no yakuhō o kenkyū shite imasu.","german":"I research Kampo medicinal formulas.","furigana":"わたし|かんぽう|やくほう|けんきゅう"},{"japanese":"この店には、江戸時代から伝わる家伝の薬方があるそうだ。","romaji":"Kono mise ni wa, Edo jidai kara tsutawaru kaden no yakuhō ga aru sō da.","german":"This shop is said to have a family formula handed down since the Edo period.","furigana":"みせ|えどじだい|つた|かでん|やくほう"},{"japanese":"古文書に記された薬方をもとに、昔の薬を再現した。","romaji":"Komonjo ni shirusareta yakuhō o moto ni, mukashi no kusuri o saigen shita.","german":"Based on a formula recorded in old documents, they reproduced a historical medicine.","furigana":"こもんじょ|しる|やくほう|むかし|くすり|さいげん"}],"notes":"薬方 is the \"medicinal formula\", that is, the composition and preparation instructions of a remedy. The word is found mainly in connection with Kampo (漢方) and historical remedies: 家伝の薬方 \"handed-down family formula\", 古い薬方. For a doctor's prescription today one says 処方, and for the prescription form 処方箋; 方 here, as in 処方, means \"prescription, recipe\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"会社を清算して残余財産があれば、株主に分配されます。","romaji":"Kaisha o seisan shite zan'yo zaisan ga areba, kabunushi ni bunpai saremasu.","german":"If residual assets remain when the company is liquidated, they are distributed to the shareholders.","furigana":"かいしゃ|せいさん|ざんよざいさん|かぶぬし|ぶんぱい"},{"japanese":"契約の残余期間については、料金が日割りで返金されます。","romaji":"Keiyaku no zan'yo kikan ni tsuite wa, ryōkin ga hiwari de henkin saremasu.","german":"For the remaining term of the contract, the fee is refunded on a daily pro rata basis.","furigana":"けいやく|ざんよきかん|りょうきん|ひわ|へんきん"},{"japanese":"予算の残余は来年度に繰り越すことになった。","romaji":"Yosan no zan'yo wa rainendo ni kurikosu koto ni natta.","german":"It was decided that the remainder of the budget would be carried over to the next fiscal year.","furigana":"よさん|ざんよ|らいねんど|く|こ"}],"notes":"残余 means \"remainder, remnant\" and belongs to official, legal and financial language. It mostly stands before another noun or with の: 残余財産 \"residual assets (after liquidation)\", 残余期間 \"remaining term\", 予算の残余. In everyday speech one says 残り or 余り; the balance of an account, however, is called 残高.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"下記に、関連法規を全て列挙しています。","romaji":"Kaki ni, kanren hōki o subete rekkyo shite imasu.","german":"Below, we have listed all the relevant laws and regulations.","furigana":"かき|かんれんほうき|すべ|れっきょ"},{"japanese":"交通法規を守って、安全運転を心がけましょう。","romaji":"Kōtsū hōki o mamotte, anzen unten o kokorogakemashō.","german":"Obey the traffic regulations and drive safely.","furigana":"こうつうほうき|まも|あんぜんうんてん|こころ"},{"japanese":"その工場は環境法規に違反したとして、営業停止処分を受けた。","romaji":"Sono kōjō wa kankyō hōki ni ihan shita to shite, eigyō teishi shobun o uketa.","german":"The factory was ordered to suspend operations for violating environmental regulations.","furigana":"こうじょう|かんきょうほうき|いはん|えいぎょうていししょぶん|う"}],"notes":"法規 is a collective term for \"laws and ordinances\", that is, all legally binding rules in a field: 交通法規, 関連法規, 環境法規. Typical are 法規を守る and 法規に違反する. 法律, by contrast, means a single law passed by parliament, 規則 means rather rules and house rules; 法令 is an almost synonymous official word.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ここの従業員たちは、大阪支店の従業員たちより楽観的なようだ。","romaji":"Koko no jūgyōintachi wa, Ōsaka shiten no jūgyōintachi yori rakkanteki na yō da.","german":"The employees here seem more optimistic than those at the Osaka branch.","furigana":"じゅうぎょういん|おおさかしてん|じゅうぎょういん|らっかんてき"},{"japanese":"この会社の従業員数は約五百人です。","romaji":"Kono kaisha no jūgyōin-sū wa yaku gohyaku-nin desu.","german":"This company has about 500 employees.","furigana":"かいしゃ|じゅうぎょういんすう|やくごひゃくにん"},{"japanese":"当工場の従業者は、作業中ヘルメットを着用すること。","romaji":"Tō kōjō no jūgyōsha wa, sagyō-chū herumetto o chakuyō suru koto.","german":"All workers at this factory must wear a helmet while working.","furigana":"とうこうじょう|じゅうぎょうしゃ|さぎょうちゅう|ちゃくよう"}],"notes":"従業 means \"employment, working in a business\", but appears almost only in compounds: 従業員 \"employees, staff\", 従業者 \"persons employed\" (official language), 従業員数. The verb 従業する exists but is rare; one says 働く, 勤務する or 〜に従事する \"to be engaged in a field\" instead. Watch the pronunciation: 従業 じゅうぎょう has a long う, whereas 授業 じゅぎょう \"class, lesson\" does not.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"そんなの、ただの脅しだよ。気にしないで。","romaji":"Sonna no, tada no odoshi da yo. Ki ni shinaide.","german":"That is just an empty threat. Don't worry about it.","furigana":"おど|き"},{"japanese":"会社は脅しに屈することなく、警察に通報した。","romaji":"Kaisha wa odoshi ni kussuru koto naku, keisatsu ni tsūhō shita.","german":"The company did not yield to the threat and notified the police.","furigana":"かいしゃ|おど|くっ|けいさつ|つうほう"},{"japanese":"取引先に「契約を打ち切るぞ」と脅しをかけられた。","romaji":"Torihikisaki ni 'Keiyaku o uchikiru zo' to odoshi o kakerareta.","german":"A business partner threatened us, saying they would terminate the contract.","furigana":"とりひきさき|けいやく|う|き|おど"}],"notes":"脅し is the noun of 脅す \"to threaten, to intimidate\" and means \"threat, intimidation\". Typical combinations: 脅しをかける \"to apply pressure, to threaten\", 脅しに屈する \"to yield to a threat\", ただの脅し \"an empty threat\", 脅し文句. The legal and formal word is 脅迫 (脅迫状 \"threatening letter\"), while 威嚇 means a threatening gesture or a warning shot. Rarely, a scarecrow is also meant (鳥脅し); おどし is often written in kana.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この音楽はとても律動的ですね。","romaji":"Kono ongaku wa totemo ritsudōteki desu ne.","german":"This music is very rhythmic.","furigana":"おんがく|りつどうてき"},{"japanese":"波が律動的に岸に打ち寄せている。","romaji":"Nami ga ritsudōteki ni kishi ni uchiyosete iru.","german":"The waves are breaking on the shore in a steady rhythm.","furigana":"なみ|りつどうてき|きし|う|よ"},{"japanese":"ダンサーたちの律動的な動きに観客は魅了された。","romaji":"Dansā-tachi no ritsudōteki na ugoki ni kankyaku wa miryō sareta.","german":"The audience was captivated by the dancers' rhythmic movements.","furigana":"りつどうてき|うご|かんきゃく|みりょう"}],"notes":"律動的 is a な-adjective meaning \"rhythmic, in a regular beat\", formed from 律動 \"rhythm, rhythmic movement\". It is used for music, movements, waves or the heartbeat: 律動的な動き, 律動的に響く. It sounds literary and somewhat elevated; in conversation one usually says リズミカル(な) or リズムがいい.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"世界の偉人たちの名言を集めました。","romaji":"Sekai no ijintachi no meigen o atsumemashita.","german":"I have collected famous sayings of great figures of the world.","furigana":"せかい|いじん|めいげん|あつ"},{"japanese":"子どもの頃、偉人伝を読むのが好きだった。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, ijinden o yomu no ga suki datta.","german":"As a child, I liked reading biographies of great figures.","furigana":"こ|ころ|いじんでん|よ|す"},{"japanese":"野口英世は、福島県が誇る郷土の偉人だ。","romaji":"Noguchi Hideyo wa, Fukushima-ken ga hokoru kyōdo no ijin da.","german":"Hideyo Noguchi is a great local figure that Fukushima Prefecture takes pride in.","furigana":"|ふくしまけん|ほこ|きょうど|いじん"}],"notes":"偉人 refers to a \"great person\", usually a historical figure who deserves admiration for achievements or character: 歴史上の偉人, 郷土の偉人, 偉人伝 \"biographies of great figures\" (a classic children's book genre). Do not confuse it with 偉い人, which colloquially simply means \"big shots, superiors\", often slightly ironically. 有名人 is a \"celebrity\", 英雄 a \"hero\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人類史上最も偉大な発明は何ですか。","romaji":"Jinrui shijō mottomo idai na hatsumei wa nani desu ka.","german":"What is the greatest invention in the whole of human history?","furigana":"じんるいしじょうもっと|いだい|はつめい|なに"},{"japanese":"母の偉大さは、自分が親になって初めて分かった。","romaji":"Haha no idaisa wa, jibun ga oya ni natte hajimete wakatta.","german":"I only understood my mother's greatness once I became a parent myself.","furigana":"はは|いだい|じぶん|おや|はじ|わ"},{"japanese":"彼は偉大な先輩たちの記録を次々と塗り替えた。","romaji":"Kare wa idai na senpai-tachi no kiroku o tsugitsugi to nurikaeta.","german":"He broke the records of his great predecessors one after another.","furigana":"かれ|いだい|せんぱい|きろく|つぎつぎ|ぬ|か"}],"notes":"偉大 is a な-adjective: \"great, grand, eminent\" in terms of rank and achievement, never of physical size (for that, 大きい). Typical: 偉大な人物, 偉大な業績, 偉大な発明; the noun \"greatness\" is 偉大さ (母の偉大さ). It sounds solemn and admiring; 偉い is more everyday and can also mean \"of high standing\", and 立派 means \"admirable, fine\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"社長がそんなに偉いんですか？","romaji":"Shachō ga sonnani erai n desu ka?","german":"Is the company president really that important?","furigana":"しゃちょう|えら"},{"japanese":"一人で着替えられたの？偉いね！","romaji":"Hitori de kigaerareta no? Erai ne!","german":"You changed clothes all by yourself? Good for you!","furigana":"ひとり|きが|えら"},{"japanese":"大雪で電車が止まって、えらいことになった。","romaji":"Ōyuki de densha ga tomatte, erai koto ni natta.","german":"Trains stopped because of heavy snow, and it turned into a huge mess.","furigana":"おおゆき|でんしゃ|と"}],"notes":"偉い has several faces. As praise it means \"great, commendable\", especially toward children or juniors (偉いね！); toward superiors it sounds condescending. Second, it means \"of high standing, important\": 偉い人 \"big shots\", 偉そうにする \"to act important\". Colloquially and mostly in kana, えらい also means \"terrible, severe\" (えらいことになった) and in the Kansai dialect \"exhausting\" (= しんどい) or adverbially \"very\" (えらい暑い).","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 28 (source: N4; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お勘定をお願いします。","romaji":"O kanjō o onegai shimasu.","german":"Could I have the check, please?","furigana":"かんじょう|ねが"},{"japanese":"レジのお金を勘定したら、千円足りなかった。","romaji":"Reji no okane o kanjō shitara, sen en tarinakatta.","german":"When I counted the money in the register, it was a thousand yen short.","furigana":"かね|かんじょう|せんえんた"},{"japanese":"渋滞の時間も勘定に入れて、早めに出発した。","romaji":"Jūtai no jikan mo kanjō ni irete, hayame ni shuppatsu shita.","german":"I also factored in the traffic jam time and left early.","furigana":"じゅうたい|じかん|かんじょう|い|はや|しゅっぱつ"}],"notes":"勘定 means \"bill, payment\" and also \"calculation, counting\". In a restaurant one says お勘定をお願いします (today just as often お会計); 勘定する means \"to count (money), to calculate\". Important phrases: 勘定に入れる \"to factor in, to take into account\", 損得勘定 \"cost-benefit thinking\", 勘定が合う \"the accounts add up\", and in bookkeeping 勘定科目 \"account\". 勘定高い describes a \"calculating\" person.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あなたのお姉さまは神に召されたのですよ。","romaji":"Anata no onēsama wa kami ni mesareta no desu yo.","german":"Your older sister has been called to God.","furigana":"ねえ|かみ|め"},{"japanese":"この着物はお気に召しましたか。","romaji":"Kono kimono wa o-ki ni meshimashita ka.","german":"Is this kimono to your liking?","furigana":"きもの|き|め"},{"japanese":"お年を召した方には席をお譲りください。","romaji":"O-toshi o meshita kata ni wa seki o o-yuzuri kudasai.","german":"Please give up your seat to elderly people.","furigana":"とし|め|かた|せき|ゆず"}],"notes":"召す is a respectful verb (尊敬語) and never refers to oneself. Today it lives mainly in fixed phrases: お気に召す \"to be to one's liking\" (お気に召しましたか), お年を召した方 \"elderly people\", 着物をお召しになる \"to wear\", 風邪を召す \"to catch a cold\" and 神に召される, a Christian euphemism for \"to die\". The common 召し上がる \"to eat, to drink\" is derived from it. The basic meaning \"to call, to summon to oneself\" is rare today.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの政治家は、世論を喚起するのが巧い。","romaji":"Ano seijika wa, seron o kanki suru no ga umai.","german":"That politician is skilled at stirring up public opinion.","furigana":"せいじか|せろん|かんき|うま"},{"japanese":"気象庁は大雨への注意を喚起している。","romaji":"Kishōchō wa ōame e no chūi o kanki shite iru.","german":"The Japan Meteorological Agency is calling attention to heavy rain.","furigana":"きしょうちょう|おおあめ|ちゅうい|かんき"},{"japanese":"この広告は、消費者の購買意欲を喚起するよう工夫されている。","romaji":"Kono kōkoku wa, shōhisha no kōbai iyoku o kanki suru yō kufū sarete iru.","german":"This advertisement is designed to arouse consumers' desire to buy.","furigana":"こうこく|しょうひしゃ|こうばいいよく|かんき|くふう"}],"notes":"喚起する means to awaken attention, awareness or feelings in others. Fixed combinations are 注意を喚起する \"to urge caution\" (in official notices often shortened to 注意喚起), 世論を喚起する, 関心を喚起する and 記憶を喚起する. Transitive with を; the object is always something mental, never a person. Do not confuse it with the identically read 換気 \"ventilation\". Formal, typical of news, administration and advertising.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は愛媛県の出身です。","romaji":"Watashi wa Ehimeken no shusshin desu.","german":"I am from Ehime Prefecture.","furigana":"わたし|えひめけん|しゅっしん"},{"japanese":"愛媛県はみかんの産地として有名だ。","romaji":"Ehime-ken wa mikan no sanchi to shite yūmei da.","german":"Ehime Prefecture is famous as a mandarin-growing region.","furigana":"えひめけん|さんち|ゆうめい"},{"japanese":"夏休みに愛媛県の道後温泉に行ってきました。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi ni Ehime-ken no Dōgo Onsen ni itte kimashita.","german":"During the summer vacation, I went to Dogo Onsen in Ehime Prefecture.","furigana":"なつやす|えひめけん|どうごおんせん|い"}],"notes":"愛媛県 is a prefecture in the northwest of the island of Shikoku, with 松山市 as its capital. It is known for mandarin oranges (みかん, いよかん), the hot spring resort 道後温泉 and the towels from 今治. The reading is irregular: 愛 is read え here. In conversation people often simply say 愛媛.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は慈愛に満ちた微笑を僕に向けた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa jiai ni michita bishō o boku ni muketa.","german":"She turned a smile full of loving kindness toward me.","furigana":"かのじょ|じあい|み|びしょう|ぼく|む"},{"japanese":"聖母マリアの像は、穏やかな慈愛の表情を浮かべている。","romaji":"Seibo Maria no zō wa, odayaka na jiai no hyōjō o ukabete iru.","german":"The statue of the Virgin Mary bears an expression of gentle loving kindness.","furigana":"せいぼ|ぞう|おだ|じあい|ひょうじょう|う"},{"japanese":"院長は、身寄りのない子どもたちを慈愛深く育てた。","romaji":"Inchō wa, miyori no nai kodomo-tachi o jiai bukaku sodateta.","german":"The director raised the orphaned children with great tenderness.","furigana":"いんちょう|みよ|こ|じあいぶか|そだ"}],"notes":"慈愛 is \"kind, caring love\", as parents show to their child or a deity to humankind, that is, affection from above rather than romantic love. Typical is 慈愛に満ちた (目・まなざし・笑顔), plus 慈愛深い and 慈愛の心. The word is literary; in everyday speech one says 愛情 or 優しさ. The Buddhist term 慈悲, by contrast, emphasizes \"compassion\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どうして野菜を水に浸しているの？","romaji":"Dōshite yasai o mizu ni hitashite iru no?","german":"Why are you soaking the vegetables in water?","furigana":"やさい|みず|ひた"},{"japanese":"タオルを冷たい水に浸して、額に当てた。","romaji":"Taoru o tsumetai mizu ni hitashite, hitai ni ateta.","german":"I dipped a towel in cold water and put it on my forehead.","furigana":"つめ|みず|ひた|ひたい|あ"},{"japanese":"疲れた足をお湯に浸すと、気持ちがいい。","romaji":"Tsukareta ashi o oyu ni hitasu to, kimochi ga ii.","german":"It feels good to soak tired feet in hot water.","furigana":"つか|あし|ゆ|ひた|きも"}],"notes":"浸す is transitive: \"to immerse, to soak, to drench\", with the pattern XをYに浸す (タオルを水に浸す). The intransitive partner is 浸る \"to be immersed\", often figurative: 思い出に浸る \"to indulge in memories\". For comparison: つける means a brief dipping (しょうゆにつける), 漬ける a longer soaking or pickling (漬物).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は離婚騒動で渦中の人だよ。","romaji":"Kare wa rikon sōdō de kachū no hito da yo.","german":"He is in the thick of a divorce scandal.","furigana":"かれ|りこんそうどう|かちゅう|ひと"},{"japanese":"渦中の大臣が、ようやく記者会見を開いた。","romaji":"Kachū no daijin ga, yōyaku kisha kaiken o hiraita.","german":"The minister at the center of the controversy finally held a press conference.","furigana":"かちゅう|だいじん|きしゃかいけん|ひら"},{"japanese":"彼女は知らないうちに、社内の派閥争いの渦中に巻き込まれていた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa shiranai uchi ni, shanai no habatsu arasoi no kachū ni makikomarete ita.","german":"Without realizing it, she had been drawn into the middle of a factional struggle within the company.","furigana":"かのじょ|し|しゃない|はばつあらそ|かちゅう|ま|こ"}],"notes":"渦中 literally means \"in the whirlpool\", but is used almost only figuratively: \"in the middle of a scandal, dispute or turmoil\". Very typical for news is 渦中の人 \"the person at the center of the scandal\", plus 騒動・論争の渦中にいる and 渦中に巻き込まれる \"to get dragged in\". Neutral \"in the middle of something\" without a scandal connotation is 最中 or 真っただ中.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この化粧水が、あなたのお肌の潤いを保ちます。","romaji":"Kono keshōsui ga, anata no o hada no uruoi o tamochimasu.","german":"This lotion keeps your skin moisturized.","furigana":"けしょうすい|はだ|うるお|たも"},{"japanese":"庭に花を植えたら、毎日の生活に潤いが生まれた。","romaji":"Niwa ni hana o uetara, mainichi no seikatsu ni uruoi ga umareta.","german":"Since I planted flowers in the garden, my daily life has become richer.","furigana":"にわ|はな|う|まいにち|せいかつ|うるお|う"},{"japanese":"雨のおかげで、乾いた大地に潤いが戻った。","romaji":"Ame no okage de, kawaita daichi ni uruoi ga modotta.","german":"Thanks to the rain, moisture returned to the dry earth.","furigana":"あめ|かわ|だいち|うるお|もど"}],"notes":"潤い is the noun of 潤う \"to become moist; to profit\" and literally means \"moisture\", especially of skin, hair and soil: 肌の潤い, 潤いを保つ, 潤いを与える (typical of cosmetics advertising). Figuratively it stands for \"enrichment, joy in life\": 生活に潤いを与える, 潤いのある暮らし. Unpleasant humidity in the air, by contrast, is 湿気; the transitive partner of the verb is 潤す.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"洗濯粉がきれちゃった。","romaji":"Sentakuko ga kirechatta.","german":"We have run out of laundry detergent.","furigana":"せんたくこ"},{"japanese":"洗濯粉を入れすぎると、すすぎ残しの原因になる。","romaji":"Sentakuko o iresugiru to, susugi-nokoshi no gen'in ni naru.","german":"Using too much laundry powder causes residue to be left in the clothes.","furigana":"せんたくこ|い|のこ|げんいん"},{"japanese":"昔は、洗濯粉を溶かしたたらいで服を手洗いしていた。","romaji":"Mukashi wa, sentakuko o tokashita tarai de fuku o tearai shite ita.","german":"In the old days, people washed clothes by hand in a tub with dissolved laundry powder.","furigana":"むかし|せんたくこ|と|ふく|てあら"}],"notes":"洗濯粉 is \"laundry powder\", i.e. powdered detergent. The word sounds somewhat old-fashioned; today one usually says 洗剤 or 洗濯洗剤 and distinguishes 粉末洗剤 \"powder\" from 液体洗剤 \"liquid detergent\". Typical: 洗濯粉を入れる, 洗濯粉が切れる \"to run out\".","levelNote":"Compound: 洗濯 (N5) + 粉 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"洗濯屋にワイシャツを取りに行ってきます。","romaji":"Sentakuya ni waishatsu o tori ni itte kimasu.","german":"I am going to pick up my dress shirt from the cleaner's.","furigana":"せんたくや|と|い"},{"japanese":"昔、この商店街には洗濯屋が三軒もあった。","romaji":"Mukashi, kono shōtengai ni wa sentakuya ga sangen mo atta.","german":"In the past, this shopping street had as many as three laundries.","furigana":"むかし|しょうてんがい|せんたくや|さんげん"},{"japanese":"スーツは自分で洗わずに、洗濯屋に出したほうがいい。","romaji":"Sūtsu wa jibun de arawazu ni, sentakuya ni dashita hō ga ii.","german":"You should not wash suits yourself but take them to the cleaner's.","furigana":"じぶん|あら|せんたくや|だ"}],"notes":"洗濯屋 is the \"laundry, dry cleaner's\", a shop that washes clothes for payment; politely also 洗濯屋さん. The word sounds somewhat old-fashioned: today one usually says クリーニング屋 or クリーニング店, and a self-service laundromat is called コインランドリー. Typical: 洗濯屋に出す \"to take to the cleaner's\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"乾燥対策でマスクをつけています。","romaji":"Kansō taisaku de masuku o tsukete imasu.","german":"I wear a mask to deal with the dryness.","furigana":"かんそうたいさく"},{"japanese":"冬は空気が乾燥しているので、火の元に注意してください。","romaji":"Fuyu wa kūki ga kansō shite iru node, hi no moto ni chūi shite kudasai.","german":"The air is dry in winter, so please be careful with open flames.","furigana":"ふゆ|くうき|かんそう|ひ|もと|ちゅうい"},{"japanese":"洗濯物を乾燥機で乾燥させたら、セーターが縮んでしまった。","romaji":"Sentakumono o kansōki de kansō sasetara, sētā ga chijinde shimatta.","german":"When I dried the laundry in the dryer, my sweater shrank.","furigana":"せんたくもの|かんそうき|かんそう|ちぢ"}],"notes":"乾燥 means \"dryness\" and, as a する-verb, \"to be/become dry\" or \"to dry\". Intransitive: 空気が乾燥している, 肌が乾燥する; the transitive meaning is usually expressed with 乾燥させる (木材を乾燥させる). Common compounds: 乾燥肌, 乾燥機 \"dryer\", 乾燥剤, 乾燥注意報. In everyday speech, for laundry one more often says 乾く/乾かす; 無味乾燥 means \"dry, boring\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"空き瓶はこちらに捨ててください。","romaji":"Akibin wa kochira ni sutete kudasai.","german":"Please throw empty bottles away here.","furigana":"あ|びん|す"},{"japanese":"空き瓶に花を挿して、テーブルに飾った。","romaji":"Akibin ni hana o sashite, tēburu ni kazatta.","german":"I put flowers in an empty bottle and decorated the table with it.","furigana":"あ|びん|はな|さ|かざ"},{"japanese":"ジャムの空き瓶を洗って、調味料入れにしている。","romaji":"Jamu no akibin o aratte, chōmiryō-ire ni shite iru.","german":"I wash empty jam jars and use them as seasoning containers.","furigana":"あ|びん|あら|ちょうみりょうい"}],"notes":"空き瓶 is an \"empty bottle\" or \"empty jar\" (e.g. a jam jar). In waste sorting it belongs to the 資源ごみ (recyclables), alongside 空き缶 \"empty cans\" and ペットボトル. It is also often written 空きびん or 空きビン. The prefix 空き \"empty, vacant\" is also known from 空き家 and 空き地.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"男は火炎瓶を投げつけた。","romaji":"Otoko wa kaenbin o nagetsuketa.","german":"The man threw a Molotov cocktail.","furigana":"おとこ|かえんびん|な"},{"japanese":"デモ隊の一部が警察に向かって火炎瓶を投げ込んだ。","romaji":"Demotai no ichibu ga keisatsu ni mukatte kaenbin o nagekonda.","german":"Some of the demonstrators hurled Molotov cocktails at the police.","furigana":"たい|いちぶ|けいさつ|む|かえんびん|な|こ"},{"japanese":"1970年前後の学生運動では、火炎瓶が使われることもあった。","romaji":"Sen kyūhyaku nanajū nen zengo no gakusei undō de wa, kaenbin ga tsukawareru koto mo atta.","german":"In the student movement around 1970, Molotov cocktails were sometimes used.","furigana":"ねんぜんご|がくせいうんどう|かえんびん|つか"}],"notes":"火炎瓶 is a \"Molotov cocktail, incendiary device\", a bottle filled with gasoline with a wick. The word appears mainly in news about riots and attacks as well as in reports on the student movement of the 1960s/70s. Typical: 火炎瓶を投げる・投げ込む・投げつける. Occasionally it is also written 火焔瓶.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"メープルシロップを瓶詰しています。","romaji":"Mēpurushiroppu o binzume shite imasu.","german":"I bottle maple syrup.","furigana":"びんづめ"},{"japanese":"祖母が作った瓶詰めのジャムをもらった。","romaji":"Sobo ga tsukutta binzume no jamu o moratta.","german":"I received jam in a jar that my grandmother made.","furigana":"そぼ|つく|びんづ"},{"japanese":"非常用に瓶詰めや缶詰を買っておいた。","romaji":"Hijouyou ni binzume ya kanzume o katte oita.","german":"I bought bottled and canned food for emergencies.","furigana":"ひじょうよう|びんづ|かんづめ|か"}],"notes":"瓶詰 (usually written 瓶詰め) refers to filling bottles or jars and, above all, the result, that is, food in a jar: ジャムの瓶詰め, 瓶詰めのピクルス. As a する-verb it is transitive: 〜を瓶詰めにする or 瓶詰めする. For metal cans one says 缶詰, and for drinks in plastic bottles ペットボトル入り. In everyday speech the noun is much more common than the verb form.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この辺りには耕地が広がっています。","romaji":"Kono atari ni wa kōchi ga hirogatte imasu.","german":"Farmland stretches across this area.","furigana":"あた|こうち|ひろ"},{"japanese":"日本の耕地面積は年々減少している。","romaji":"Nihon no kōchi menseki wa nennen genshō shite iru.","german":"Japan's cultivated land area is shrinking year by year.","furigana":"にほん|こうちめんせき|ねんねんげんしょう"},{"japanese":"人々は森を切り開いて、新たな耕地を作った。","romaji":"Hitobito wa mori o kirihiraite, arata na kōchi o tsukutta.","german":"People cleared the forest and created new farmland.","furigana":"ひとびと|もり|き|ひら|あら|こうち|つく"}],"notes":"耕地 is \"arable land, cultivated area\" and is used mainly in statistics, geography and administration: 耕地面積 \"cultivated area\", 耕地を広げる, 耕地整理. More general for agricultural land (also in the legal sense) is 農地; in everyday speech one says 田畑 or simply 畑 or 田んぼ. Fields no longer being farmed are called 耕作放棄地.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この土地を耕作するには許可がいります。","romaji":"Kono tochi o kōsaku suru ni wa kyoka ga irimasu.","german":"A permit is required to cultivate this land.","furigana":"とち|こうさく|きょか"},{"japanese":"高齢化で、耕作されなくなった田んぼが増えている。","romaji":"Kōreika de, kōsaku sarenaku natta tanbo ga fuete iru.","german":"Because of the aging population, the number of rice paddies that are no longer cultivated is increasing.","furigana":"こうれいか|こうさく|た|ふ"},{"japanese":"彼は会社を辞めて、借りた畑を一人で耕作している。","romaji":"Kare wa kaisha o yamete, karita hatake o hitori de kōsaku shite iru.","german":"He quit his company and now farms a rented field by himself.","furigana":"かれ|かいしゃ|や|か|はたけ|ひとり|こうさく"}],"notes":"耕作 means \"tilling the soil, farming\"; as a transitive する-verb, the land is its object: 土地・畑を耕作する \"to till, to farm land\". Common are 耕作地, 耕作面積 and 耕作放棄地 \"abandoned farmland\". The everyday verb for \"to plow, to dig up\" is 耕す; for growing specific plants one says 栽培する (トマトを栽培する), not 耕作する.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"昔はこの村でも、牛を使って農耕をしていた。","romaji":"Mukashi wa kono mura de mo, ushi o tsukatte nōkō o shite ita.","german":"In the past, even in this village, farming was done with the help of oxen.","furigana":"むかし|むら|うし|つか|のうこう"},{"japanese":"弥生時代に、稲作を中心とする農耕が広まった。","romaji":"Yayoi jidai ni, inasaku o chūshin to suru nōkō ga hiromatta.","german":"In the Yayoi period, farming centered on rice cultivation spread.","furigana":"やよいじだい|いなさく|ちゅうしん|のうこう|ひろ"},{"japanese":"農耕民族と狩猟民族では、考え方に違いがあると言われる。","romaji":"Nōkō minzoku to shuryō minzoku de wa, kangaekata ni chigai ga aru to iwareru.","german":"It is said that farming peoples and hunting peoples differ in their way of thinking.","furigana":"のうこうみんぞく|しゅりょうみんぞく|かんが|かた|ちが|い"}],"notes":"農耕 refers to \"farming\" as a human way of life and culture, often in a historical or ethnological context: 農耕が始まる, 農耕社会, 農耕文化, 農耕民族 (in contrast to 狩猟民族 \"hunting peoples\" and 遊牧民族 \"nomads\"), 農耕馬. For today's branch of the economy or the occupation one says 農業; \"He is a farmer\" is therefore 農業をしている, not 農耕をしている.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この子は本当に聡い子だよ。","romaji":"Kono ko wa hontōni satoi ko da yo.","german":"This is a really clever child.","furigana":"こ|ほんとう|さと|こ"},{"japanese":"彼は利に聡い商人として知られていた。","romaji":"Kare wa ri ni satoi shōnin to shite shirarete ita.","german":"He was known as a merchant with a keen nose for profit.","furigana":"かれ|り|さと|しょうにん|し"},{"japanese":"祖母は年をとっても耳が聡く、小さな物音にもすぐ気づく。","romaji":"Sobo wa toshi o totte mo mimi ga satoku, chiisa na monooto ni mo sugu kizuku.","german":"Even in old age, my grandmother has sharp hearing and immediately notices even small sounds.","furigana":"そぼ|とし|みみ|さと|ちい|ものおと|き"}],"notes":"聡い means \"clever, quick-witted\" and \"sharp, sensitive (of the senses)\"; it sounds literary and is often written in kana as さとい. Fixed combinations: 利に聡い \"mindful of one's advantage, business-savvy\", 耳聡い \"to have sharp ears, to catch everything\" and the related 目ざとい \"sharp-eyed\". In everyday speech one says 賢い or 頭がいい; more formal is the な-adjective 聡明.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どうして肯定も否定もしないんですか。","romaji":"Dōshite kōtei mo hitei mo shinai n desu ka.","german":"Why won't you either confirm or deny it?","furigana":"こうてい|ひてい"},{"japanese":"次の肯定文を否定文に書き換えなさい。","romaji":"Tsugi no kōteibun o hiteibun ni kakikaenasai.","german":"Rewrite the following affirmative sentence as a negative sentence.","furigana":"つぎ|こうていぶん|ひていぶん|か|か"},{"japanese":"子どもの自己肯定感を育てるには、まず褒めることが大切だ。","romaji":"Kodomo no jiko kōteikan o sodateru ni wa, mazu homeru koto ga taisetsu da.","german":"To nurture children's self-esteem, praising them is the first important step.","furigana":"こ|じここうていかん|そだ|ほ|たいせつ"}],"notes":"肯定 is \"affirmation, agreement\" and the opposite of 否定 \"negation\". As a transitive する-verb: 事実を肯定する \"to confirm a fact\"; fixed is 肯定も否定もしない \"neither to confirm nor to deny\". Important derivatives are 肯定的(な) \"positive, affirmative\", 肯定文 \"affirmative sentence\" in grammar and 自己肯定感 \"self-esteem\". The word is rather formal; in conversation one often says 認める or そうだと言う.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"太ももの脂肪を落としたいんです。","romaji":"Futomomo no shibō o otoshitai n desu.","german":"I want to get rid of the fat on my thighs.","furigana":"ふと|しぼう|お"},{"japanese":"健康診断で、内臓脂肪が多いと言われた。","romaji":"Kenkō shindan de, naizō shibō ga ōi to iwareta.","german":"At my health checkup, I was told I have a lot of visceral fat.","furigana":"けんこうしんだん|ないぞうしぼう|おお|い"},{"japanese":"健康のために、低脂肪の牛乳を選んでいる。","romaji":"Kenkō no tame ni, tei-shibō no gyūnyū o erande iru.","german":"For my health, I choose low-fat milk.","furigana":"けんこう|ていしぼう|ぎゅうにゅう|えら"}],"notes":"脂肪 is \"fat\" as a substance in the body and in food, rather factual or medical: 体脂肪, 内臓脂肪 \"visceral fat\", 皮下脂肪, 脂肪がつく \"to put on fat\", 脂肪を落とす \"to lose fat\", 低脂肪 \"low-fat\". In everyday cooking one speaks of あぶら (油 for liquid oil, 脂 for solid fat), and for meat the fatty part is 脂身.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これを透明の樹脂で固めました。","romaji":"Kore o tōmei no jushi de katamemashita.","german":"I encased this in transparent resin.","furigana":"とうめい|じゅし|かた"},{"japanese":"このスプーンは樹脂製なので、落としても割れません。","romaji":"Kono supūn wa jushi-sei na node, otoshite mo waremasen.","german":"This spoon is made of resin, so it will not break even if dropped.","furigana":"じゅしせい|お|わ"},{"japanese":"松の木の幹から、樹脂がにじみ出ている。","romaji":"Matsu no ki no miki kara, jushi ga nijimidete iru.","german":"Resin is oozing out of the pine trunk.","furigana":"まつ|き|みき|じゅし|で"}],"notes":"樹脂 is \"resin\": natural tree resin (天然樹脂, e.g. 松やに) and above all synthetic resin or plastic (合成樹脂). Very common in product descriptions: 樹脂製 \"made of plastic\", アクリル樹脂, エポキシ樹脂. In everyday speech, plastic objects are usually called プラスチック; 樹脂 sounds more technical. Used with の or as a first element: 樹脂の部品, 樹脂加工.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その苗字、初めて聞きました。","romaji":"Sono myōji, hajimete kikimashita.","german":"I've never heard that surname before.","furigana":"みょうじ|はじ|き"},{"japanese":"結婚して苗字が変わりました。","romaji":"Kekkon shite myōji ga kawarimashita.","german":"My surname changed when I got married.","furigana":"けっこん|みょうじ|か"},{"japanese":"日本では、名前を書くときは苗字を先に書きます。","romaji":"Nihon de wa, namae o kaku toki wa myōji o saki ni kakimasu.","german":"In Japan, when writing a name, the surname is written first.","furigana":"にほん|なまえ|か|みょうじ|さき|か"}],"notes":"苗字 is the \"family name, surname\"; there is also the spelling 名字, which is more usual in school textbooks and government offices. In Japanese, the 苗字 comes before the given name (下の名前). Typical: 苗字が変わる (e.g. through marriage), 珍しい苗字, 苗字で呼ぶ. On forms, the more formal 姓 (姓・名) or 氏名 for the full name appears; 名前 can mean the full name or only the given name.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"蓮の花の花言葉は何ですか。","romaji":"Hasu no hana no hanakotoba wa nani desu ka.","german":"What is the meaning of the lotus flower in the language of flowers?","furigana":"はす|はな|はなことば|なに"},{"japanese":"夏の朝、お寺の池に蓮の花が咲いていた。","romaji":"Natsu no asa, otera no ike ni hasu no hana ga saite ita.","german":"On a summer morning, lotus flowers were blooming in the temple pond.","furigana":"なつ|あさ|てら|いけ|はす|はな|さ"},{"japanese":"蓮根は、蓮の地下茎を食べる野菜だ。","romaji":"Renkon wa, hasu no chikakei o taberu yasai da.","german":"Renkon is a vegetable in which you eat the underground stem of the lotus.","furigana":"れんこん|はす|ちかけい|た|やさい"}],"notes":"蓮 (はす, often also written in kana はす or ハス) is the \"lotus\". Its edible root is called 蓮根 (れんこん). In Buddhism the lotus is a symbol of purity, because it grows from the mud and yet blooms clean; one speaks of 蓮の花, 蓮の葉 and 蓮池. Do not confuse it with 睡蓮 \"water lily\", whose leaves lie flat on the water, whereas lotus leaves rise above it.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仏像は蓮花の形をした台座の上に座っている。","romaji":"Butsuzō wa renge no katachi o shita daiza no ue ni suwatte iru.","german":"The Buddha statue sits on a pedestal in the shape of a lotus flower.","furigana":"ぶつぞう|れんげ|かたち|だいざ|うえ|すわ"},{"japanese":"ラーメンのスープをれんげですくって飲んだ。","romaji":"Rāmen no sūpu o renge de sukutte nonda.","german":"I scooped up the ramen broth with a ceramic spoon and drank it.","furigana":"の"},{"japanese":"春になると、田んぼ一面にれんげが咲く。","romaji":"Haru ni naru to, tanbo ichimen ni renge ga saku.","german":"In spring, Chinese milk vetch blooms all over the rice paddies.","furigana":"はる|た|いちめん|さ"}],"notes":"れんげ (usually written 蓮華, more rarely 蓮花) originally means \"lotus flower\"; in this meaning it is mainly Buddhist-literary (蓮華座 \"lotus seat\" of a Buddha statue). The plant in the pond is called 蓮 or 蓮の花 in everyday speech. Today れんげ is mostly encountered in kana in two shortened meanings: as a ceramic spoon for ramen and soups (short for 散り蓮華) and as れんげ草, the Chinese milk vetch that colors rice paddies pink in spring.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は最後まで貞節を守ったんだ。","romaji":"Kanojo wa saigo made teisetsu o mamotta n da.","german":"She remained faithful to the very end.","furigana":"かのじょ|さいご|ていせつ|まも"},{"japanese":"昔の小説には、貞節な妻が理想の女性として描かれることが多い。","romaji":"Mukashi no shōsetsu ni wa, teisetsu na tsuma ga risō no josei to shite egakareru koto ga ōi.","german":"In old novels, the faithful wife is often depicted as the ideal woman.","furigana":"むかし|しょうせつ|ていせつ|つま|りそう|じょせい|えが|おお"},{"japanese":"「貞節」という言葉は、今ではあまり使われなくなった。","romaji":"'Teisetsu' to iu kotoba wa, ima de wa amari tsukawarenaku natta.","german":"The word \"teisetsu\" is hardly used anymore today.","furigana":"ていせつ|ことば|いま|つか"}],"notes":"貞節 means \"marital fidelity, chastity\", traditionally a woman's fidelity to her husband. Typical: 貞節を守る・貫く and, as a な-adjective, 貞節な妻. The word belongs to old moral vocabulary and is found today mainly in literature and history. 貞操 emphasizes specifically sexual chastity, and the literary 操 (みさお) is almost synonymous; for couples today one more often speaks of 浮気しない or 誠実.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの男が彼女の貞操を奪ったのよ。","romaji":"Ano otoko ga kanojo no teisō o ubatta no yo.","german":"That man took her virginity.","furigana":"おとこ|かのじょ|ていそう|うば"},{"japanese":"最近の若者は貞操観念が薄いと嘆く人もいる。","romaji":"Saikin no wakamono wa teisō kannen ga usui to nageku hito mo iru.","german":"Some lament that young people today have lax moral standards about chastity.","furigana":"さいきん|わかもの|ていそうかんねん|うす|なげ|ひと"},{"japanese":"博物館で、中世ヨーロッパの貞操帯が展示されていた。","romaji":"Hakubutsukan de, chūsei Yōroppa no teisōtai ga tenji sarete ita.","german":"A chastity belt from medieval Europe was on display at the museum.","furigana":"はくぶつかん|ちゅうせい|ていそうたい|てんじ"}],"notes":"貞操 refers to \"chastity, sexual fidelity\". Typical combinations: 貞操を守る, 貞操を奪う (a euphemism for \"to deflower, to rape\"), 貞操観念 \"moral attitudes in sexual matters\" (貞操観念が低い・ない) and 貞操帯 \"chastity belt\". The word sounds old-fashioned; only 貞操観念 is still common in conversation today. 貞節 is broader and means fidelity in marriage as a whole.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"妻が不貞をはたらいたので、離婚しました。","romaji":"Tsuma ga futei o hataraita node, rikon shimashita.","german":"My wife was unfaithful, so we got divorced.","furigana":"つま|ふてい|りこん"},{"japanese":"相手の不貞行為を理由に、慰謝料を請求した。","romaji":"Aite no futei kōi o riyū ni, isharyō o seikyū shita.","german":"She demanded compensation on the grounds of her partner's infidelity.","furigana":"あいて|ふていこうい|りゆう|いしゃりょう|せいきゅう"},{"japanese":"不貞の疑いをかけられ、彼は必死に否定した。","romaji":"Futei no utagai o kakerare, kare wa hisshi ni hitei shita.","german":"Suspected of infidelity, he desperately denied it.","furigana":"ふてい|うたが|かれ|ひっし|ひてい"}],"notes":"不貞 means \"infidelity, adultery\". Typical: 不貞を働く \"to commit adultery\" and above all the legal term 不貞行為, which counts as a ground for divorce in divorce law; adjectivally 不貞な妻・夫. In everyday speech one says 浮気 or (for married people) 不倫. Caution: the same characters appear in 不貞腐れる (ふてくされる) \"to sulk\" and 不貞寝 (ふてね), which have nothing to do with infidelity.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は自宅軟禁下に置かれました。","romaji":"Kanojo wa jitaku nankinka ni okaremashita.","german":"She was placed under house arrest.","furigana":"かのじょ|じたくなんきんか|お"},{"japanese":"クーデターの後、大統領は軍に軟禁された。","romaji":"Kūdetā no ato, daitōryō wa gun ni nankin sareta.","german":"After the coup, the president was placed under house arrest by the military.","furigana":"あと|だいとうりょう|ぐん|なんきん"},{"japanese":"その作家は、締め切りまでホテルに軟禁状態だった。","romaji":"Sono sakka wa, shimekiri made hoteru ni nankin jōtai datta.","german":"The writer was practically confined to a hotel until the deadline.","furigana":"さっか|し|き|なんきんじょうたい"}],"notes":"軟禁 is \"house arrest\": a person is not locked up but may not leave a place (usually their own residence) and has no free contact. Typical: 自宅軟禁, 軟禁される, 軟禁状態. To be distinguished are 監禁 \"unlawful confinement\" (usually by criminals) and 拘束・拘禁 \"detention\". The word appears mainly in news about political prisoners, and jokingly also in a figurative sense.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この豪華な建物は、大統領の私邸です。","romaji":"Kono gōka na tatemono wa, daitōryō no shitei desu.","german":"This magnificent building is the president's private residence.","furigana":"ごうか|たてもの|だいとうりょう|してい"},{"japanese":"首相は週末を都内の私邸で過ごした。","romaji":"Shushō wa shūmatsu o tonai no shitei de sugoshita.","german":"The prime minister spent the weekend at his private residence in Tokyo.","furigana":"しゅしょう|しゅうまつ|とない|してい|す"},{"japanese":"大臣の私邸前には、朝から記者が詰めかけていた。","romaji":"Daijin no shitei mae ni wa, asa kara kisha ga tsumekakete ita.","german":"Reporters had been crowding in front of the minister's private residence since morning.","furigana":"だいじん|していまえ|あさ|きしゃ|つ"}],"notes":"私邸 is the \"private residence\" of a public figure (politician, head of state, nobility), as opposed to the official 官邸 or 公邸. The word is typical of news: 首相が私邸に戻る, 私邸で静養する. For ordinary people one says 自宅 or 家.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"首相官邸ツアーに参加しました。","romaji":"Shushō kantei tsuā ni sanka shimashita.","german":"I took part in a tour of the prime minister's official residence.","furigana":"しゅしょうかんてい|さんか"},{"japanese":"官邸で緊急の閣僚会議が開かれた。","romaji":"Kantei de kinkyū no kakuryō kaigi ga hirakareta.","german":"An emergency cabinet meeting was held at the prime minister's office.","furigana":"かんてい|きんきゅう|かくりょうかいぎ|ひら"},{"japanese":"この政策は、官邸主導で進められた。","romaji":"Kono seisaku wa, kantei shudō de susumerareta.","german":"This policy was driven by the prime minister's office.","furigana":"せいさく|かんていしゅどう|すす"}],"notes":"官邸 is the \"official seat\" of a high official, above all 首相官邸, the building where the prime minister works. In political language, 官邸 often stands for the prime minister and his staff: 官邸主導 \"led by the prime minister's office\". Distinguish: 官邸 = official and working seat, 公邸 = official residence, 私邸 = private residence.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大統領公邸が何者かに襲撃されました。","romaji":"Daitouryou koutei ga nanimono ka ni shuugeki saremashita.","german":"The president's official residence was attacked by unknown persons.","furigana":"だいとうりょうこうてい|なにもの|しゅうげき"},{"japanese":"首相は官邸での仕事を終え、隣の公邸に戻った。","romaji":"Shushō wa kantei de no shigoto o oe, tonari no kōtei ni modotta.","german":"After finishing work at the official office, the prime minister returned to the adjacent official residence.","furigana":"しゅしょう|かんてい|しごと|お|となり|こうてい|もど"},{"japanese":"大使公邸で、各国の来賓を招いたパーティーが開かれた。","romaji":"Taishi kōtei de, kakkoku no raihin o maneita pātī ga hirakareta.","german":"A party for guests from many countries was held at the ambassador's residence.","furigana":"たいしこうてい|かっこく|らいひん|まね|ひら"}],"notes":"公邸 is the \"official residence\" that an office-holder lives in at the state's expense: 首相公邸, 知事公邸, 大使公邸 (an ambassador's residence, often used for receptions). In Japan a distinction is made between 首相官邸 (workplace) and the neighboring 首相公邸 (living quarters); one's own private home is called 私邸.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この邸宅は、幽霊が出ることで有名です。","romaji":"Kono teitaku wa, yūrei ga deru koto de yūmei desu.","german":"This mansion is famous for being haunted.","furigana":"ていたく|ゆうれい|で|ゆうめい"},{"japanese":"彼は事業に成功して、高台に大きな邸宅を構えた。","romaji":"Kare wa jigyō ni seikō shite, takadai ni ōki na teitaku o kamaeta.","german":"After his business success, he built a large mansion on a hill.","furigana":"かれ|じぎょう|せいこう|たかだい|おお|ていたく|かま"},{"japanese":"明治時代の実業家の邸宅が、今は美術館として公開されている。","romaji":"Meiji jidai no jitsugyōka no teitaku ga, ima wa bijutsukan to shite kōkai sarete iru.","german":"The mansion of a Meiji-era businessman is now open to the public as an art museum.","furigana":"めいじじだい|じつぎょうか|ていたく|いま|びじゅつかん|こうかい"}],"notes":"邸宅 is a \"large, stately residence, mansion\". Typical are 豪華な邸宅, 大邸宅 and 邸宅を構える \"to set up an estate\"; the word sounds elevated and is found in reports, real estate ads and travel guides. 豪邸 is more colloquial (\"palatial villa\"), 屋敷 emphasizes the traditional estate with grounds, and 住宅 and 家 are neutral.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これは、邸内の写真です。","romaji":"Kore wa, teinai no shashin desu.","german":"This is a photo from inside the estate.","furigana":"ていない|しゃしん"},{"japanese":"何者かが大臣の邸内に侵入し、警察が捜査している。","romaji":"Nanimono ka ga daijin no teinai ni shinnyū shi, keisatsu ga sōsa shite iru.","german":"Someone broke into the minister's residence, and the police are investigating.","furigana":"なにもの|だいじん|ていない|しんにゅう|けいさつ|そうさ"},{"japanese":"邸内には日本庭園があり、季節ごとに違う花が楽しめる。","romaji":"Teinai ni wa Nihon teien ga ari, kisetsu goto ni chigau hana ga tanoshimeru.","german":"The grounds have a Japanese garden where you can enjoy different flowers in every season.","furigana":"ていない|にほんていえん|きせつ|ちが|はな|たの"}],"notes":"邸内 means \"within an estate\", that is, the buildings and grounds of a stately residence (邸). Typical are 邸内に侵入する, 邸内を案内する and 邸内の庭園. The word is literary; for an ordinary house one says 家の中, and for premises in general 敷地内.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は東京近郊に住んでいます。","romaji":"Watashi wa Tōkyō kinkō ni sunde imasu.","german":"I live in the suburbs of Tokyo.","furigana":"わたし|とうきょうきんこう|す"},{"japanese":"週末は、都市近郊の温泉に日帰りで出かけることが多い。","romaji":"Shūmatsu wa, toshi kinkō no onsen ni higaeri de dekakeru koto ga ōi.","german":"On weekends, I often make a day trip to a hot spring in the area around the city.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|としきんこう|おんせん|ひがえ|で|おお"},{"japanese":"大阪近郊の農家は、新鮮な野菜を都市部へ出荷している。","romaji":"Ōsaka kinkō no nōka wa, shinsen na yasai o toshibu e shukka shite iru.","german":"Farms in the environs of Osaka ship fresh vegetables to the urban area.","furigana":"おおさかきんこう|のうか|しんせん|やさい|としぶ|しゅっか"}],"notes":"近郊 is the \"nearby surroundings, environs\" of a city, usually after a place name: 東京近郊, 大阪近郊, or generally 都市近郊, 近郊農業. The word emphasizes proximity to the big city. 郊外, by contrast, means the \"city edge, suburb\" as opposed to the city center (郊外に住む); 近所 is the immediate neighborhood.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"現在、郡は地名に使われることが多い。","romaji":"Genzai, gun wa chimei ni tsukawareru koto ga ooi.","german":"Today, the county (gun) is mostly used only as part of place names.","furigana":"げんざい|ぐん|ちめい|つか|おお"},{"japanese":"祖父の家の住所は、長野県北佐久郡軽井沢町です。","romaji":"Sofu no ie no jūsho wa, Nagano-ken Kitasaku-gun Karuizawa-machi desu.","german":"My grandfather's address is Karuizawa Town, Kitasaku County, Nagano Prefecture.","furigana":"そふ|いえ|じゅうしょ|ながのけんきたさくぐんかるいざわまち"},{"japanese":"アメリカの郡は、州の下にある行政単位だ。","romaji":"Amerika no gun wa, shū no shita ni aru gyōsei tan'i da.","german":"A county in the United States is an administrative unit below the state.","furigana":"ぐん|しゅう|した|ぎょうせいたんい"}],"notes":"郡 is a \"county, district\"; in Japan it is a unit within a prefecture that groups several small towns (町) and villages (村). As an administrative body, the 郡 was abolished as early as the 1920s and today lives on almost only in addresses: 〇〇県△△郡□□町. As a suffix it comes directly after the name (北佐久郡). It is also used as a translation for territorial units of other countries, e.g. the American county.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これについて、正式に釈明をする必要がある。","romaji":"Kore nitsuite, seishiki ni shakumei o suru hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"You need to give an official explanation of this.","furigana":"せいしき|しゃくめい|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"不祥事を受けて、社長が釈明会見を開いた。","romaji":"Fushōji o ukete, shachō ga shakumei kaiken o hiraita.","german":"After the scandal, the company president faced questions at a press conference.","furigana":"ふしょうじ|う|しゃちょう|しゃくめいかいけん|ひら"},{"japanese":"野党は政府に対して、十分な釈明を求めた。","romaji":"Yatō wa seifu ni taishite, jūbun na shakumei o motometa.","german":"The opposition demanded a sufficient explanation from the government.","furigana":"やとう|せいふ|たい|じゅうぶん|しゃくめい|もと"}],"notes":"釈明 is a \"justification, clarifying statement\" with which one explains one's position after criticism or a misunderstanding. Typical: 釈明する, 釈明を求める, 釈明に追われる, 釈明会見 \"press conference to explain oneself\"; it is a word of news and official language. 説明 is a neutral \"explanation\", 弁解 and 言い訳 sound like \"excuse\", and 弁明 is a formal synonym (e.g. in court).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その場合は、注釈を付けてください。","romaji":"Sono baai wa, chūshaku o tsukete kudasai.","german":"In that case, please add annotations.","furigana":"ばあい|ちゅうしゃく|つ"},{"japanese":"古典の授業では、注釈を参考にしながら源氏物語を読んだ。","romaji":"Koten no jugyō de wa, chūshaku o sankō ni shinagara Genji Monogatari o yonda.","german":"In classics class, we read The Tale of Genji with the help of the annotations.","furigana":"こてん|じゅぎょう|ちゅうしゃく|さんこう|げんじものがたり|よ"},{"japanese":"グラフのデータの出典は、下の注釈に記載しました。","romaji":"Gurafu no dēta no shutten wa, shita no chūshaku ni kisai shimashita.","german":"I stated the source of the data in the graph in the note below.","furigana":"しゅってん|した|ちゅうしゃく|きさい"}],"notes":"注釈 is a \"note, explanation\" on a text, a table or a work: 注釈を付ける・加える, 注釈付きの本, 注釈書 \"commentary\" on classics. Short footnotes are called 注 or 脚注, a supplement 補足; a personal remark in conversation is more of a コメント. As a verb 注釈する is rare; one usually says 注釈を付ける.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"会釈をしたら会釈を返すのが礼儀でしょう。","romaji":"Eshaku o shitara eshaku o kaesu no ga reigi deshō.","german":"When someone bows to you, it is polite to bow back.","furigana":"えしゃく|えしゃく|かえ|れいぎ"},{"japanese":"廊下で部長とすれ違ったので、軽く会釈した。","romaji":"Rōka de buchō to surechigatta node, karuku eshaku shita.","german":"I passed the department head in the hallway and gave him a slight bow.","furigana":"ろうか|ぶちょう|ちが|かる|えしゃく"},{"japanese":"彼は遠慮会釈もなく、人の家の冷蔵庫を開けた。","romaji":"Kare wa enryo eshaku mo naku, hito no ie no reizōko o aketa.","german":"He opened someone else's refrigerator without any hesitation.","furigana":"かれ|えんりょえしゃく|ひと|いえ|れいぞうこ|あ"}],"notes":"会釈 is a \"slight bow, a greeting nod\" of about 15 degrees, for example when passing each other or in an elevator; deeper ones are 敬礼 and 最敬礼. Typical: 軽く会釈する, 会釈を返す, 会釈を交わす; the verb is intransitive (〜に会釈する). The second meaning \"consideration\" survives only in 遠慮会釈もなく \"without any consideration, unabashedly\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"そのことが心の片隅にずっと引っかかっていたんです。","romaji":"Sono koto ga kokoro no katasumi ni zutto hikkakatte ita n desu.","german":"That had always nagged at me in a corner of my heart.","furigana":"こころ|かたすみ|ひ"},{"japanese":"この話、頭の片隅に置いておいてください。","romaji":"Kono hanashi, atama no katasumi ni oite oite kudasai.","german":"Please keep this in the back of your mind.","furigana":"はなし|あたま|かたすみ|お"},{"japanese":"二人は大都会の片隅で、ひっそりと暮らしていた。","romaji":"Futari wa daitokai no katasumi de, hissori to kurashite ita.","german":"The two lived quietly in an obscure corner of the big city.","furigana":"ふたり|だいとかい|かたすみ|く"}],"notes":"片隅 is a \"corner, nook\", usually an inconspicuous, out-of-the-way place: 部屋の片隅, 都会の片隅で暮らす. Figuratively very common: 心の片隅に \"in a corner of the heart\" and 頭の片隅に置いておく \"to keep in the back of one's mind\". 隅 is the neutral (inside) \"corner\", whereas 角 (かど) is the protruding corner of a street or table.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"写真の四隅を暗く加工するにはどうすればいいのか教えてください。","romaji":"Shashin no yosumi o kuraku kakō suru ni wa dō sure ba ii no ka oshiete kudasai.","german":"Please tell me how to darken the four corners of a photo.","furigana":"しゃしん|よすみ|くら|かこう|おし"},{"japanese":"風で飛ばないように、シートの四隅に石を置いた。","romaji":"Kaze de tobanai you ni, shiito no yosumi ni ishi o oita.","german":"I put stones on the four corners of the sheet so that it would not blow away.","furigana":"かぜ|と|よすみ|いし|お"},{"japanese":"書類の四隅をきちんとそろえてから、クリップで留めてください。","romaji":"Shorui no yosumi o kichinto soroete kara, kurippu de tomete kudasai.","german":"Please line up the four corners of the documents neatly and then fasten them with a clip.","furigana":"しょるい|よすみ|と"}],"notes":"四隅 refers to the four corners of a rectangular surface: 部屋の四隅, 紙の四隅, 写真の四隅. Typical phrases are 四隅をそろえる \"to line the corners up flush\" and 四隅を押さえる \"to hold down all four corners\". For a single inside corner one says 隅, for a protruding outer edge 角. Rarely (more in literary writing) 四隅 also stands for the four intercardinal directions.","levelNote":"Compound: 隅 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日は部屋を隅々まで掃除しました。","romaji":"Kyō wa heya o sumizumi made sōji shimashita.","german":"Today I cleaned every corner of my room.","furigana":"きょう|へや|すみずみ|そうじ"},{"japanese":"契約書は隅々まで目を通してからサインしてください。","romaji":"Keiyakusho wa sumizumi made me o tooshite kara sain shite kudasai.","german":"Please read through the contract in every detail before you sign.","furigana":"けいやくしょ|すみずみ|め|とお"},{"japanese":"彼はこの町の隅々まで知り尽くしている。","romaji":"Kare wa kono machi no sumizumi made shiritsukushite iru.","german":"He knows every nook and cranny of this town.","furigana":"かれ|まち|すみずみ|し|つ"}],"notes":"隅々 means \"every corner, down to the last nook\" and almost always appears as 隅々まで before a verb: 隅々まで掃除する, 隅々まで探す, 隅々まで目を通す \"to read everything carefully\". It can be used spatially or figuratively (町の隅々まで知っている, 全国の隅々に行き渡る). A synonym, somewhat more colloquial, is 隅から隅まで. The repetition mark 々 is read like 隅 here, with rendaku: すみずみ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"セーターをお湯で洗濯したら縮んでしまった。","romaji":"Sētā o oyu de sentaku shitara chijinde shimatta.","german":"I washed my sweater in hot water and it shrank.","furigana":"ゆ|せんたく|ちぢ"},{"japanese":"週末にまとめて洗濯するので、平日は洗濯物がたまってしまう。","romaji":"Shuumatsu ni matomete sentaku suru node, heijitsu wa sentakumono ga tamatte shimau.","german":"Because I do the laundry all at once on the weekend, laundry piles up during the week.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|せんたく|へいじつ|せんたくもの"},{"japanese":"久しぶりに温泉に行って、命の洗濯をしてきた。","romaji":"Hisashiburi ni onsen ni itte, inochi no sentaku o shite kita.","german":"I went to a hot spring for the first time in a while and really recharged.","furigana":"ひさ|おんせん|い|いのち|せんたく"}],"notes":"洗濯する means \"to do laundry\" and is used only for clothing and textiles: 服を洗濯する or simply 洗濯をする. For hands, dishes or vegetables one says 洗う, for the car 洗車する. Important words are 洗濯物 \"the laundry\" (洗濯物を干す / たたむ) and 洗濯機. Figuratively, 命の洗濯 means \"to really relax, to recharge one's soul\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 8 (source: N5; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の祖父は世界恐慌が起きた年に生まれました。","romaji":"Watashi no sofu wa sekai kyōkō ga okita toshi ni umaremashita.","german":"My grandfather was born in the year the Great Depression broke out.","furigana":"わたし|そふ|せかいきょうこう|お|とし|う"},{"japanese":"1929年にニューヨークの株価が暴落し、世界恐慌が始まった。","romaji":"Sen kyuuhyaku nijuukyuu nen ni Nyuuyooku no kabuka ga bouraku shi, sekai kyoukou ga hajimatta.","german":"In 1929, stock prices in New York collapsed, and the Great Depression began.","furigana":"ねん|かぶか|ぼうらく|せかいきょうこう|はじ"},{"japanese":"世界恐慌の影響で、日本の農村も深刻な不況に陥った。","romaji":"Sekai kyoukou no eikyou de, Nihon no nouson mo shinkoku na fukyou ni ochiitta.","german":"As a result of the Great Depression, Japan's farming villages also fell into serious depression.","furigana":"せかいきょうこう|えいきょう|にほん|のうそん|しんこく|ふきょう|おちい"}],"notes":"世界恐慌 usually means the Great Depression beginning in 1929 (1929年の世界恐慌), but can also refer in general to a worldwide depression. The usual verbs are 世界恐慌が起こる / 起きる / 始まる; 世界恐慌の影響で \"as a result of the Great Depression\". 恐慌 alone is a severe economic crisis with panic, whereas 不況 is merely a recession or slump. The 2008 financial crisis, by contrast, is usually called リーマン・ショック or 金融危機.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どうやってコーヒー豆を煎るんですか？","romaji":"Dō yatte kōhī mame o iru n desu ka?","german":"How do you roast coffee beans?","furigana":"まめ|い"},{"japanese":"ごまをフライパンで軽く煎ると、香りがよくなる。","romaji":"Goma o furaipan de karuku iru to, kaori ga yoku naru.","german":"If you lightly roast sesame seeds in a frying pan, they smell better.","furigana":"かる|い|かお"},{"japanese":"節分の豆まきには、煎った大豆を使います。","romaji":"Setsubun no mamemaki ni wa, itta daizu o tsukaimasu.","german":"For the bean-throwing at Setsubun, roasted soybeans are used.","furigana":"せつぶん|まめ|い|だいず|つか"}],"notes":"煎る (also written 炒る) means \"to roast in a pan without oil\", for example coffee beans, sesame, soybeans or tea (ほうじ茶). For coffee and tea 煎る is usual, for sesame often 炒る. Not to be confused with 炒める \"to stir-fry in oil\" and 焼く \"to bake, to grill\". Note: 煎る is a godan verb (煎って, 煎らない) and sounds like いる \"to be\" or 要る \"to need\" - context decides.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どうやら妻が不倫をしているようなんです。","romaji":"Dōyara tsuma ga furin o shite iru yō na n desu.","german":"It seems that my wife is having an affair.","furigana":"つま|ふりん"},{"japanese":"その俳優は不倫が報じられて、番組を降板した。","romaji":"Sono haiyuu wa furin ga houjirarete, bangumi o kouban shita.","german":"After his affair was reported, the actor left the show.","furigana":"はいゆう|ふりん|ほう|ばんぐみ|こうばん"},{"japanese":"彼が上司と不倫していたことが、会社中に知れ渡った。","romaji":"Kare ga joushi to furin shite ita koto ga, kaishajuu ni shirewatatta.","german":"The fact that he had been having an affair with his boss became known throughout the company.","furigana":"かれ|じょうし|ふりん|かいしゃじゅう|し|わた"}],"notes":"不倫 is an extramarital affair, that is, adultery: 不倫する, 不倫をする, 〜と不倫関係にある, 不倫相手 \"affair partner\". For celebrities it is a perennial topic of the gossip press (不倫報道, 不倫騒動). 浮気 is broader and more colloquial, also covering unfaithfulness in a relationship without marriage; 姦通 is an obsolete legal term. A nominal adjective 不倫な is possible but rare.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"固定観念にとらわれていると、ものの見方が偏狭になる。","romaji":"Kotei kannen ni torawarete iru to, mono no mikata ga henkyou ni naru.","german":"If you are bound by fixed ideas, your way of looking at things becomes narrow-minded.","furigana":"こていかんねん|みかた|へんきょう"},{"japanese":"彼は偏狭な人で、自分と違う意見を絶対に認めない。","romaji":"Kare wa henkyou na hito de, jibun to chigau iken o zettai ni mitomenai.","german":"He is narrow-minded and never accepts opinions different from his own.","furigana":"かれ|へんきょう|ひと|じぶん|ちが|いけん|ぜったい|みと"},{"japanese":"偏狭なナショナリズムが、国同士の対立をあおっている。","romaji":"Henkyou na nashonarizumu ga, kuni doushi no tairitsu o aotte iru.","german":"Narrow-minded nationalism is fueling conflict between countries.","furigana":"へんきょう|くにどうし|たいりつ"}],"notes":"偏狭 is a な-adjective meaning \"narrow-minded, intolerant\": 偏狭な考え, 偏狭な人, 偏狭なナショナリズム. It is literary and sounds clearly more critical than the everyday 心が狭い; closely related is 狭量. 頑固, by contrast, means \"stubborn\", not necessarily intolerant. Do not confuse it with the identically pronounced 辺境 \"border region, remote area\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"メッシが、移籍を示唆するコメントをしたようだ。","romaji":"Messhi ga, iseki o shisa suru komento o shita yō da.","german":"Messi seems to have made a comment hinting at a transfer.","furigana":"いせき|しさ"},{"japanese":"この調査結果は、睡眠不足が肥満の原因になりうることを示唆している。","romaji":"Kono chousa kekka wa, suimin busoku ga himan no gen'in ni nariuru koto o shisa shite iru.","german":"The results of this survey suggest that lack of sleep can cause obesity.","furigana":"ちょうさけっか|すいみんぶそく|ひまん|げんいん|しさ"},{"japanese":"先生の講演は示唆に富んでいて、とても勉強になった。","romaji":"Sensei no kouen wa shisa ni tonde ite, totemo benkyou ni natta.","german":"The professor's lecture was full of insights, and I learned a lot.","furigana":"せんせい|こうえん|しさ|と|べんきょう"}],"notes":"示唆する means \"to hint at, to suggest, to point to something\" and is typical of news and science: 〜を示唆する, 〜ことを示唆している. Fixed phrases are 示唆に富む \"insightful, thought-provoking\" and 示唆を与える / 得る. ほのめかす is more of a deliberate, veiled hint by a person, whereas 指摘する is an explicit pointing out. In everyday speech one says more simply 〜を感じさせる or ヒントになる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"噴煙を上げる桜島の写真を撮りました。","romaji":"Fun'en o ageru Sakurajima no shashin o torimashita.","german":"I took a photo of Sakurajima sending up a plume of smoke.","furigana":"ふんえん|あ|さくらじま|しゃしん|と"},{"japanese":"火口から噴煙が上がり、高さは三千メートルに達した。","romaji":"Kakou kara fun'en ga agari, takasa wa sanzen meetoru ni tasshita.","german":"An ash plume rose from the crater and reached a height of 3,000 meters.","furigana":"かこう|ふんえん|あ|たか|さんぜん|たっ"},{"japanese":"噴煙の影響で、飛行機が何便も欠航した。","romaji":"Fun'en no eikyou de, hikouki ga nanbin mo kekkou shita.","german":"Because of the ash plume, many flights were canceled.","furigana":"ふんえん|えいきょう|ひこうき|なんびん|けっこう"}],"notes":"噴煙 is the cloud of smoke and ash that a volcano emits. Fixed combinations are 噴煙を上げる (with the volcano as subject), 噴煙が上がる and 噴煙が〜メートルに達する; typical of news and weather reports. Ordinary smoke is simply 煙, and the ash that falls down is called 火山灰. For the eruption itself one says 噴火.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"催涙スプレーの噴射方法を教えてください。","romaji":"Sairui supurē no funsha hōhō o oshiete kudasai.","german":"Please show me how to spray tear gas.","furigana":"さいるい|ふんしゃほうほう|おし"},{"japanese":"ロケットはエンジンを噴射して、宇宙へ飛び立った。","romaji":"Roketto wa enjin o funsha shite, uchuu e tobitatta.","german":"The rocket fired its engines and flew off into space.","furigana":"ふんしゃ|うちゅう|と|た"},{"japanese":"この車は、燃料噴射装置に不具合が見つかった。","romaji":"Kono kuruma wa, nenryou funsha souchi ni fuguai ga mitsukatta.","german":"A defect was found in this car's fuel injection system.","furigana":"くるま|ねんりょうふんしゃそうち|ふぐあい|み"}],"notes":"噴射 is the targeted ejection of gas, liquid or fuel under pressure; as a する-verb it is transitive: ガスを噴射する, スプレーを噴射する. Technical combinations are 燃料噴射 \"fuel injection\", ジェット噴射 \"jet stream\" and 逆噴射 \"thrust reversal\". 噴出, by contrast, describes more an uncontrolled gushing out (mostly intransitive), and 発射 the firing of a rocket or bullet.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あそこの噴水で待ち合わせをしましょう。","romaji":"Asoko no funsui de machiawase o shimashō.","german":"Let's meet at the fountain over there.","furigana":"ふんすい|ま|あ"},{"japanese":"夏になると、子どもたちが公園の噴水で水遊びをしている。","romaji":"Natsu ni naru to, kodomotachi ga kouen no funsui de mizuasobi o shite iru.","german":"In summer, children play in the water in the park's fountain.","furigana":"なつ|こ|こうえん|ふんすい|みずあそ"},{"japanese":"夜になると、噴水が音楽に合わせてライトアップされる。","romaji":"Yoru ni naru to, funsui ga ongaku ni awasete raitoappu sareru.","german":"At night, the fountain is lit up in time with the music.","furigana":"よる|ふんすい|おんがく|あ"}],"notes":"噴水 is a fountain or water jet, for example in a park or on a plaza. Typical are 噴水がある, 噴水の前で待ち合わせる and 噴水が上がる \"the fountain shoots up\". A natural spring, by contrast, is called 泉 or 湧き水, and a drinking fountain 水飲み場. The expression is neutral and everyday.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ブラックホールからガスが噴出しているって本当ですか？","romaji":"Burakkuhōru kara gasu ga funshutsu shite iru tte hontō desu ka?","german":"Is it true that gas is gushing out of black holes?","furigana":"ふんしゅつ|ほんとう"},{"japanese":"地震の後、道路の割れ目から水が噴出した。","romaji":"Jishin no ato, douro no wareme kara mizu ga funshutsu shita.","german":"After the earthquake, water gushed out of cracks in the road.","furigana":"じしん|あと|どうろ|わ|め|みず|ふんしゅつ"},{"japanese":"新しい制度への不満が、社員の間から一気に噴出した。","romaji":"Atarashii seido e no fuman ga, shain no aida kara ikki ni funshutsu shita.","german":"Dissatisfaction with the new system burst out all at once among the employees.","furigana":"あたら|せいど|ふまん|しゃいん|あいだ|いっき|ふんしゅつ"}],"notes":"噴出する means \"to gush out, to spout\" of gas, water, lava or oil and is mostly intransitive: 〜から〜が噴出する. Figuratively one often says 不満が噴出する or 問題が噴出する \"to burst forth in large numbers\". The transitive use (火山が溶岩を噴出する) is rarer. The word is literary; in everyday speech one says 噴き出す, which can also mean \"to burst out laughing\". 噴射, by contrast, emphasizes targeted technical ejection.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"妻と相談して、孤児を引き取ることにしました。","romaji":"Tsuma to sōdan shite, koji o hikitoru koto ni shimashita.","german":"After talking it over with my wife, we decided to take in an orphan.","furigana":"つま|そうだん|こじ|ひ|と"},{"japanese":"戦争で多くの子どもが孤児になった。","romaji":"Sensou de ooku no kodomo ga koji ni natta.","german":"Many children became orphans because of the war.","furigana":"せんそう|おお|こ|こじ"},{"japanese":"彼は幼いころに両親を亡くし、孤児として施設で育った。","romaji":"Kare wa osanai koro ni ryoushin o nakushi, koji toshite shisetsu de sodatta.","german":"He lost his parents at a young age and grew up as an orphan in an institution.","furigana":"かれ|おさな|りょうしん|な|こじ|しせつ|そだ"}],"notes":"孤児 is an orphan, that is, a child without parents; the word is rather formal and often appears in compounds such as 戦争孤児 or 震災孤児. The older 孤児院 \"orphanage\" is now officially called 児童養護施設. In everyday speech people prefer to paraphrase: 親のいない子; みなしご is outdated. Figuratively 孤児 can also refer to an outsider without support.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今は孤島を舞台とした小説を書いています。","romaji":"Ima wa kotō o butai to shita shōsetsu o kaite imasu.","german":"I am currently writing a novel set on a remote island.","furigana":"いま|ことう|ぶたい|しょうせつ|か"},{"japanese":"大雪で道路が通れなくなり、村は陸の孤島となった。","romaji":"Ooyuki de douro ga toorenaku nari, mura wa riku no kotou to natta.","german":"Heavy snow made the roads impassable, and the village became cut off from the outside world.","furigana":"おおゆき|どうろ|とお|むら|りく|ことう"},{"japanese":"絶海の孤島には、珍しい鳥が数多く生息している。","romaji":"Zekkai no kotou ni wa, mezurashii tori ga kazuooku seisoku shite iru.","german":"Many rare birds live on the remote islands far out at sea.","furigana":"ぜっかい|ことう|めずら|とり|かずおお|せいそく"}],"notes":"孤島 is a remote, solitary island; the fixed phrase 絶海の孤島 means an island far out in the open sea. Very common is the image 陸の孤島 \"island on the mainland\" for a place cut off from the outside world, such as a village after severe weather or a district without a rail connection. 無人島, by contrast, emphasizes that nobody lives there. The word is rather literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ブラックホールが、恒星をばらばらに引き裂くことがあるらしい。","romaji":"Burakkuhouru ga, kousei o barabara ni hikisaku koto ga aru rashii.","german":"Offenbar gibt es Fälle, in denen schwarze Löcher Sterne in Stücke reißen.","furigana":"こうせい|ひ|さ"},{"japanese":"太陽は、自ら光を放つ恒星の一つだ。","romaji":"Taiyou wa, mizukara hikari o hanatsu kousei no hitotsu da.","german":"Die Sonne ist einer der Sterne, die selbst Licht ausstrahlen.","furigana":"たいよう|みずか|ひかり|はな|こうせい|ひと"},{"japanese":"惑星は恒星の周りを回り、衛星は惑星の周りを回っている。","romaji":"Wakusei wa kousei no mawari o mawari, eisei wa wakusei no mawari o mawatte iru.","german":"Planeten kreisen um Sterne, und Monde kreisen um Planeten.","furigana":"わくせい|こうせい|まわ|まわ|えいせい|わくせい|まわ|まわ"}],"notes":"恒星 ist ein Stern, der selbst leuchtet, wie die Sonne – im Deutschen „Fixstern“. Das Wort gehört zur Fachsprache der Astronomie und bildet ein Begriffsfeld mit 惑星 „Planet“, 衛星 „Mond, Satellit“ und 彗星 „Komet“. Im Alltag sagt man einfach 星. Weitere Begriffe: 恒星系 „Sternsystem“, 恒星の周りを回る „um einen Stern kreisen“.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"うちの会社では、毎年恒例のクリスマス会があるんだよ。","romaji":"Uchi no kaisha de wa, maitoshi kōrei no kurisumasukai ga aru n da yo.","german":"At our company, there is a Christmas party every year as a tradition.","furigana":"かいしゃ|まいとしこうれい|かい"},{"japanese":"夏祭りの花火大会は、この町の恒例行事です。","romaji":"Natsumatsuri no hanabi taikai wa, kono machi no kourei gyouji desu.","german":"The fireworks display at the summer festival is a regular event in this town.","furigana":"なつまつ|はなびたいかい|まち|こうれいぎょうじ"},{"japanese":"年末に家族で旅行するのが、我が家の恒例になっている。","romaji":"Nenmatsu ni kazoku de ryokou suru no ga, wagaya no kourei ni natte iru.","german":"Traveling with the family at the end of the year has become a tradition in our home.","furigana":"ねんまつ|かぞく|りょこう|わ|や|こうれい"}],"notes":"恒例 refers to something that takes place regularly, usually every year, and has become a fixed custom: 毎年恒例の〜, 恒例行事, 〜が恒例となっている. It emphasizes regular recurrence, not age like 伝統 \"tradition\". 慣例 is a (often formal) custom, 習慣 a personal habit. Frequent in announcements: 新春恒例, 夏の恒例イベント.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"恒久の平和を祈っています。","romaji":"Kōkyū no heiwa o inotte imasu.","german":"I pray for lasting peace.","furigana":"こうきゅう|へいわ|いの"},{"japanese":"応急処置ではなく、恒久的な対策が必要だ。","romaji":"Oukyuu shochi de wa naku, koukyuuteki na taisaku ga hitsuyou da.","german":"We need a permanent measure, not an emergency fix.","furigana":"おうきゅうしょち|こうきゅうてき|たいさく|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"日本国憲法の前文には、「恒久の平和を念願し」という言葉がある。","romaji":"Nihonkoku kenpou no zenbun ni wa, “koukyuu no heiwa o nengan shi” to iu kotoba ga aru.","german":"The preamble of the Constitution of Japan contains the words \"desiring lasting peace\".","furigana":"にほんこくけんぽう|ぜんぶん|こうきゅう|へいわ|ねんがん|ことば"}],"notes":"恒久 means \"permanence, durability\" and is used adjectivally as 恒久の or, more often, 恒久的な: 恒久の平和, 恒久的な対策 \"a permanent solution\". The opposite is 一時的 or 暫定的. 永久 and 永遠 mean more \"eternal\", whereas 恒久 means \"designed to last\"; it is literary and typical of politics and administration. Well known is the phrase 恒久の平和を念願し from the preamble of the Japanese Constitution.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"僕は、勉強への意欲は無く、ただ惰性で大学へ通っていました。","romaji":"Boku wa, benkyou e no iyoku wa naku, tada dasei de daigaku e kayotte imashita.","german":"I had no desire to study and was just going to university out of habit.","furigana":"ぼく|べんきょう|いよく|な|だせい|だいがく|かよ"},{"japanese":"惰性で続けているだけのサブスクを、いくつか解約した。","romaji":"Dasei de tsuzukete iru dake no sabusuku o, ikutsu ka kaiyaku shita.","german":"I canceled several subscriptions that I was only continuing out of inertia.","furigana":"だせい|つづ|かいやく"},{"japanese":"坂道ではペダルをこがずに、惰性で進んだ。","romaji":"Sakamichi de wa pedaru o kogazu ni, dasei de susunda.","german":"On the downhill slope, I did not pedal and just coasted.","furigana":"さかみち|だせい|すす"}],"notes":"惰性 is above all \"the force of habit, the rut\": 惰性で〜する means to simply keep doing something without real motivation (惰性で続ける, 惰性で通う). It usually sounds negative. Literally it also means physical inertia, for example when a vehicle coasts without power (惰性で走る); in physics, though, one rather says 慣性. 怠惰, by contrast, is laziness as a character trait.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自分の不倫を得意気に自慢するとか、ありえないんだけど。","romaji":"Jibun no furin o tokuige ni jiman suru toka, arienai n da kedo.","german":"Proudly bragging about your own affair - that's unbelievable.","furigana":"じぶん|ふりん|とくいげ|じまん"},{"japanese":"当店自慢のラーメンを、ぜひ召し上がってください。","romaji":"Touten jiman no raamen o, zehi meshiagatte kudasai.","german":"Please be sure to try our ramen, the specialty of the house.","furigana":"とうてんじまん|め|あ"},{"japanese":"彼はいつも息子の成績を自慢している。","romaji":"Kare wa itsumo musuko no seiseki o jiman shite iru.","german":"He is always bragging about his son's grades.","furigana":"かれ|むすこ|せいせき|じまん"}],"notes":"自慢する means \"to boast, to brag\" about something: 〜を自慢する, 自慢話 \"bragging\", 自慢げに \"boastfully\". Positive is 自慢の〜 \"my pride and joy\": 自慢の息子, 当店自慢のラーメン. 自慢じゃないけど introduces a bit of self-presentation in a modest-ironic way. 誇る is more formal and neutral-positive (\"to be proud of\"), 威張る means \"to throw one's weight around\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あのウエイトレスは動きが緩慢なので首にしたよ。","romaji":"Ano ueitoresu wa ugoki ga kanman na node kubi ni shita yo.","german":"I fired that waitress because her movements were so sluggish.","furigana":"うご|かんまん|くび"},{"japanese":"政府の緩慢な対応に、批判が集まっている。","romaji":"Seifu no kanman na taiou ni, hihan ga atsumatte iru.","german":"The government's sluggish response is drawing criticism.","furigana":"せいふ|かんまん|たいおう|ひはん|あつ"},{"japanese":"薬の副作用で、動作が緩慢になることがあります。","romaji":"Kusuri no fukusayou de, dousa ga kanman ni naru koto ga arimasu.","german":"As a side effect of the medicine, movements may become slow.","furigana":"くすり|ふくさよう|どうさ|かんまん"}],"notes":"緩慢 is a な-adjective for \"slow, sluggish, ponderous\", above all for movements and reactions: 動作が緩慢だ, 緩慢な動き. Second, it means \"lax, hesitant\" in the actions of government offices: 緩慢な対応. It is literary and also common in medicine; colloquially one says のろい, and neutrally ゆっくり (without negative connotation). Do not confuse it with 怠慢 \"negligence, neglect of duty\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その映画の、主役の二人が甘い抱擁を交わすシーンがとても美しかったよ。","romaji":"Sono eiga no, shuyaku no futari ga amai houyou o kawasu shiin ga totemo utsukushikatta yo.","german":"The scene in that movie where the two leads exchange a sweet embrace was very beautiful.","furigana":"えいが|しゅやく|ふたり|あま|ほうよう|か|うつく"},{"japanese":"空港で再会した親子は、固く抱擁した。","romaji":"Kuukou de saikai shita oyako wa, kataku houyou shita.","german":"The parent and child who met again at the airport embraced tightly.","furigana":"くうこう|さいかい|おやこ|かた|ほうよう"},{"japanese":"優勝が決まると、監督と選手たちは抱擁して喜び合った。","romaji":"Yuushou ga kimaru to, kantoku to senshutachi wa houyou shite yorokobiatta.","german":"When the title was decided, the manager and players hugged and rejoiced together.","furigana":"ゆうしょう|き|かんとく|せんしゅ|ほうよう|よろこ|あ"}],"notes":"抱擁 is an embrace and belongs to written and literary language: 抱擁する, 抱擁を交わす \"to embrace each other\", 熱い抱擁. In everyday speech one says 抱きしめる, 抱き合う or ハグする. The する-verb is transitive (子どもを抱擁する) but is often used without an object for mutual embracing. Typical of novels, film descriptions and reports on reunions.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"野党は、次の選挙で統一候補を擁立する方針だ。","romaji":"Yatou wa, tsugi no senkyo de touitsu kouho o youritsu suru houshin da.","german":"The opposition plans to field a unified candidate in the next election.","furigana":"やとう|つぎ|せんきょ|とういつこうほ|ようりつ|ほうしん"},{"japanese":"党は、知名度の高いタレントを候補者に擁立した。","romaji":"Tou wa, chimeido no takai tarento o kouhosha ni youritsu shita.","german":"The party fielded a well-known TV personality as its candidate.","furigana":"とう|ちめいど|たか|こうほしゃ|ようりつ"},{"japanese":"家臣たちは幼い王子を擁立して、権力を握ろうとした。","romaji":"Kashintachi wa osanai ouji o youritsu shite, kenryoku o nigirou to shita.","german":"The retainers installed the young prince as ruler in order to seize power themselves.","furigana":"かしん|おさな|おうじ|ようりつ|けんりょく|にぎ"}],"notes":"擁立する means \"to put someone forward (for office), to install someone in a post\", above all in politics: 候補者を擁立する, 〜を党首に擁立する, 独自候補の擁立. Historically it means to install someone as ruler (幼い王子を擁立する). So it is more than mere 支持 \"support\": the group actively puts the person forward. 推薦する means \"to recommend\", 立候補する \"to run for office oneself\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"神奈川県は千葉県よりも多い人口を擁しています。","romaji":"Kanagawaken wa Chibaken yori mo ōi jinkō o yōshite imasu.","german":"Kanagawa Prefecture has a larger population than Chiba Prefecture.","furigana":"かながわけん|ちばけん|おお|じんこう|よう"},{"japanese":"このチームは、代表クラスの選手を何人も擁している。","romaji":"Kono chiimu wa, daihyou kurasu no senshu o nannin mo youshite iru.","german":"This team has several players of national-team caliber.","furigana":"だいひょう|せんしゅ|なんにん|よう"},{"japanese":"東京は、約千四百万人の人口を擁する大都市だ。","romaji":"Toukyou wa, yaku sen'yonhyakuman nin no jinkou o yousuru daitoshi da.","german":"Tokyo is a major city with a population of about 14 million.","furigana":"とうきょう|やくせんよんひゃくまんにん|じんこう|よう|だいとし"}],"notes":"擁する is literary and today mostly means \"to have, to possess\", especially with residents, members or talent: 人口百万を擁する都市, 優秀な選手を擁するチーム. The original meaning \"to embrace\" is rare (for that, 抱擁する), and \"to lead, to support\" survives in 擁立. In everyday speech one says 〜がいる / 〜がある or 抱える instead. It conjugates like a する-verb: 擁して, 擁した, 擁している.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"没後50年を記念した展覧会を予定しています。","romaji":"Botsugo gojuu nen o kinen shita tenrankai o yotei shite imasu.","german":"We are planning an exhibition commemorating the 50th anniversary of his death.","furigana":"ぼつご|ねん|きねん|てんらんかい|よてい"},{"japanese":"ゴッホの作品の多くは、没後に高く評価されるようになった。","romaji":"Gohho no sakuhin no ooku wa, botsugo ni takaku hyouka sareru you ni natta.","german":"Many of Van Gogh's works came to be highly regarded only after his death.","furigana":"さくひん|おお|ぼつご|たか|ひょうか"},{"japanese":"作家の没後、未発表の原稿が書斎から見つかった。","romaji":"Sakka no botsugo, mihappyou no genkou ga shosai kara mitsukatta.","german":"After the author's death, unpublished manuscripts were found in his study.","furigana":"さっか|ぼつご|みはっぴょう|げんこう|しょさい|み"}],"notes":"没後 means \"after death\" and is used for well-known figures such as artists, writers or rulers, especially at anniversaries: 没後百年, 没後〜年記念展. The counterpart is 生誕〜年 \"...years since birth\". The general word, also used in daily life, is 死後; 没後 sounds literary and respectful. As an adverb: 没後に評価される \"to be recognized posthumously\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ここには、多くの戦没者が眠っています。","romaji":"Koko ni wa, ooku no senbotsusha ga nemutte imasu.","german":"Many war dead rest here.","furigana":"おお|せんぼつしゃ|ねむ"},{"japanese":"毎年八月十五日に、全国戦没者追悼式が行われる。","romaji":"Maitoshi hachigatsu juugonichi ni, zenkoku senbotsusha tsuitoushiki ga okonawareru.","german":"Every year on August 15, the National Memorial Service for the War Dead is held.","furigana":"まいとしはちがつじゅうごにち|ぜんこくせんぼつしゃついとうしき|おこな"},{"japanese":"祖父の兄は、南方の島で戦没したそうだ。","romaji":"Sofu no ani wa, nanpou no shima de senbotsu shita sou da.","german":"My grandfather's older brother is said to have died in the war on an island in the South Pacific.","furigana":"そふ|あに|なんぽう|しま|せんぼつ"}],"notes":"戦没 means \"to fall in war, to perish in war\" and occurs almost only in compounds: 戦没者 \"war dead, fallen\", 戦没者慰霊碑 \"war memorial\", 全国戦没者追悼式 (the memorial service on August 15). The verb 戦没する is rare and solemn; 戦死する is more usual. 戦没 also covers civilians and soldiers who died of illness or hunger during the war, whereas 戦死 covers only death in combat.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"タイタニック号の沈没を予言していた人がいるんです。","romaji":"Taitanikkugō no chinbotsu o yogen shite ita hito ga iru n desu.","german":"There was someone who predicted the sinking of the Titanic.","furigana":"ごう|ちんぼつ|よげん|ひと"},{"japanese":"嵐で漁船が沈没したが、乗組員は全員救助された。","romaji":"Arashi de gyosen ga chinbotsu shita ga, norikumiin wa zen'in kyuujo sareta.","german":"A fishing boat sank in the storm, but the entire crew was rescued.","furigana":"あらし|ぎょせん|ちんぼつ|のりくみいん|ぜんいんきゅうじょ"},{"japanese":"昨日の飲み会で、彼は早々に沈没してしまった。","romaji":"Kinou no nomikai de, kare wa hayabaya ni chinbotsu shite shimatta.","german":"At yesterday's drinking party, he passed out early.","furigana":"きのう|の|かい|かれ|はやばや|ちんぼつ"}],"notes":"沈没する means \"to sink, to go under\" and is intransitive: 船が沈没する. It is used for ships; sinking in general (also of the sun) is 沈む, actively sinking a ship is 撃沈する, and running aground is 座礁する. Colloquially 沈没 also means \"to pass out dead drunk\" (飲み会で沈没する) or, among backpackers, \"to get stuck somewhere\". Well known is the novel title 日本沈没.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今出発すれば、日没までには着けると思います。","romaji":"Ima shuppatsu sure ba, nichibotsu made ni wa tsukeru to omoimasu.","german":"If I leave now, I think I can arrive before sunset.","furigana":"いましゅっぱつ|にちぼつ|つ|おも"},{"japanese":"山では日没後に気温が急に下がるので、上着を持っていこう。","romaji":"Yama de wa nichibotsu go ni kion ga kyuu ni sagaru node, uwagi o motte ikou.","german":"In the mountains the temperature drops sharply after sunset, so let's take a jacket.","furigana":"やま|にちぼつご|きおん|きゅう|さ|うわぎ|も"},{"japanese":"今日の日没は、午後五時十二分です。","romaji":"Kyou no nichibotsu wa, gogo goji juuni fun desu.","german":"Today's sunset is at 5:12 p.m.","furigana":"きょう|にちぼつ|ごごごじじゅうにふん"}],"notes":"日没 is sunset as a point in time and sounds somewhat formal or technical: 日没前, 日没後, 日没時刻, 日没までに. In everyday speech one also says 日の入り (opposite: 日の出) or 日暮れ; 夕日 is the setting sun itself or its light. 日没 is typical in weather forecasts, hiking guides and regulations (日没後は立ち入り禁止).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"本当に日本は没落していると思いますか？","romaji":"Hontou ni Nippon wa botsuraku shite iru to omoimasu ka?","german":"Do you really think Japan is in decline?","furigana":"ほんとう|にっぽん|ぼつらく|おも"},{"japanese":"戦後の農地改革で、多くの地主が没落した。","romaji":"Sengo no nouchi kaikaku de, ooku no jinushi ga botsuraku shita.","german":"Because of the postwar land reform, many landlords were ruined.","furigana":"せんご|のうちかいかく|おお|じぬし|ぼつらく"},{"japanese":"『斜陽』は、没落していく貴族の一家を描いた小説だ。","romaji":"“Shayou” wa, botsuraku shite iku kizoku no ikka o egaita shousetsu da.","german":"『斜陽』 is a novel depicting a noble family in decline.","furigana":"しゃよう|ぼつらく|きぞく|いっか|えが|しょうせつ"}],"notes":"没落する means \"to fall, to go to ruin, to lose one's rank\", mostly of families, social classes, empires or companies: 没落した貴族, 名家が没落する, 帝国の没落. It is intransitive and literary. 衰退 is a gradual decline (e.g. of an industry), 破産 financial bankruptcy; 没落 emphasizes the fall from a formerly high position.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"整形手術で二重の埋没手術をしました。","romaji":"Seikei shujutsu de nijū no maibotsu shujutsu o shimashita.","german":"I had double-eyelid suture surgery done through plastic surgery.","furigana":"せいけいしゅじゅつ|にじゅう|まいぼつしゅじゅつ"},{"japanese":"土砂崩れで、家が何軒も土砂に埋没した。","romaji":"Dosha kuzure de, ie ga nanken mo dosha ni maibotsu shita.","german":"Several houses were buried by the landslide.","furigana":"どしゃくず|いえ|なんけん|どしゃ|まいぼつ"},{"japanese":"大きな組織の中では、個人の才能が埋没してしまうこともある。","romaji":"Ookina soshiki no naka de wa, kojin no sainou ga maibotsu shite shimau koto mo aru.","german":"In large organizations, an individual's talent can easily get lost.","furigana":"おお|そしき|なか|こじん|さいのう|まいぼつ"}],"notes":"埋没する literally means \"to be buried, to be covered\" (土砂に埋没する) and figuratively \"to sink into obscurity, to go unnoticed\": 才能が埋没する, 個性が埋没する. More rarely it means \"to immerse oneself in something\" (研究に埋没する), for which 没頭する is usually used. In medicine there is 埋没法 or 埋没手術 for eyelid fold surgery with sutures, and 埋没歯 \"impacted tooth\". It is literary and intransitive.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本各地でクマが大量出没している。","romaji":"Nippon kakuchi de kuma ga tairyō shutsubotsu shite iru.","german":"Bears are appearing in large numbers all over Japan.","furigana":"にっぽんかくち|たいりょうしゅつぼつ"},{"japanese":"この辺りは、夜になるとタヌキが出没する。","romaji":"Kono atari wa, yoru ni naru to tanuki ga shutsubotsu suru.","german":"In this area, raccoon dogs show up at night.","furigana":"あた|よる|しゅつぼつ"},{"japanese":"駅の周辺に不審者が出没しているので、気をつけてください。","romaji":"Eki no shuuhen ni fushinsha ga shutsubotsu shite iru node, ki o tsukete kudasai.","german":"A suspicious person has been hanging around the station area, so please be careful.","furigana":"えき|しゅうへん|ふしんしゃ|しゅつぼつ|き"}],"notes":"出没する means \"to turn up (again and again), to be seen prowling\", literally \"to appear and disappear\". It is used for animals or unwanted persons: クマが出没する, 不審者が出没する; the place is marked with に. Very well known are warning signs such as クマ出没注意 or 痴漢出没注意. For a one-time appearance one says 現れる; 出没 emphasizes repeated, unpredictable appearances.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の叔父は猟師です。","romaji":"Watashi no oji wa ryōshi desu.","german":"My uncle is a hunter.","furigana":"わたし|おじ|りょうし"},{"japanese":"この店では、地元の猟師が仕留めたシカの肉を出している。","romaji":"Kono mise de wa, jimoto no ryoushi ga shitometa shika no niku o dashite iru.","german":"This restaurant serves venison shot by a local hunter.","furigana":"みせ|じもと|りょうし|しと|にく|だ"},{"japanese":"冬になると、猟師たちは山へ猟に出かける。","romaji":"Fuyu ni naru to, ryoushitachi wa yama e ryou ni dekakeru.","german":"In winter, the hunters head into the mountains to hunt.","furigana":"ふゆ|りょうし|やま|りょう|で"}],"notes":"猟師 is a hunter, especially someone who hunts professionally or traditionally: 猟に出る, 猟銃, 猟師が仕留める \"to bring down\". Caution: 漁師 \"fisherman\" is also read りょうし - only the kanji differs (猟 with the animal radical, 漁 with the water radical). In official usage it is 狩猟者, colloquially also ハンター. The traditional mountain hunters of northern Japan are called マタギ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この鳥の狩猟は禁じられています。","romaji":"Kono tori no shuryou wa kinjirarete imasu.","german":"Hunting this bird is prohibited.","furigana":"とり|しゅりょう|きん"},{"japanese":"狩猟をするには、免許が必要です。","romaji":"Shuryou o suru ni wa, menkyo ga hitsuyou desu.","german":"You need a license to hunt.","furigana":"しゅりょう|めんきょ|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"人類は長い間、狩猟と採集で暮らしてきた。","romaji":"Jinrui wa nagai aida, shuryou to saishuu de kurashite kita.","german":"Humans lived by hunting and gathering for a long time.","furigana":"じんるい|なが|あいだ|しゅりょう|さいしゅう|く"}],"notes":"狩猟 is hunting as an activity, often in a legal or scientific context: 狩猟免許 \"hunting license\", 狩猟期間 \"hunting season\", 狩猟禁止, 狩猟採集 \"hunting and gathering\". The verb 狩猟する is rather formal; in everyday speech one says 狩りをする or 猟をする. For the person one says 猟師 or 狩猟者.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一般的に猟犬はよく吠えると言われています。","romaji":"Ippanteki ni ryouken wa yoku hoeru to iwarete imasu.","german":"It is generally said that hunting dogs bark a lot.","furigana":"いっぱんてき|りょうけん|ほ|い"},{"japanese":"祖父は猟犬を連れて、毎朝山に入る。","romaji":"Sofu wa ryouken o tsurete, maiasa yama ni hairu.","german":"My grandfather goes into the mountains every morning with his hunting dog.","furigana":"そふ|りょうけん|つ|まいあさやま|はい"},{"japanese":"ビーグルは、もともとウサギ狩りの猟犬として改良された犬種だ。","romaji":"Biiguru wa, motomoto usagigari no ryouken toshite kairyou sareta kenshu da.","german":"The beagle is a breed originally developed as a hunting dog for rabbit hunting.","furigana":"が|りょうけん|かいりょう|けんしゅ"}],"notes":"猟犬 is a hunting dog, that is, a dog bred or trained for hunting: 猟犬を連れる, 猟犬として飼う. Many well-known breeds such as the beagle or retriever were originally 猟犬. Figuratively 猟犬 can also refer to a tenacious pursuer, such as an investigator (権力の猟犬). The general 犬 or 飼い犬 does not have this function; a police sniffer dog is called 警察犬.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"デコポンは日本発祥のみかんの品種です。","romaji":"Dekopon wa Nippon hasshō no mikan no hinshu desu.","german":"Dekopon is a mandarin variety that originated in Japan.","furigana":"にっぽんはっしょう|ひんしゅ"},{"japanese":"柔道は日本で発祥し、今では世界中で行われている。","romaji":"Juudou wa Nihon de hasshou shi, ima de wa sekaijuu de okonawarete iru.","german":"Judo originated in Japan and is now practiced all over the world.","furigana":"じゅうどう|にほん|はっしょう|いま|せかいじゅう|おこな"},{"japanese":"黄河の流域は、中国文明発祥の地とされている。","romaji":"Kouga no ryuuiki wa, Chuugoku bunmei hasshou no chi to sarete iru.","german":"The Yellow River basin is regarded as the cradle of Chinese civilization.","furigana":"こうが|りゅういき|ちゅうごくぶんめいはっしょう|ち"}],"notes":"発祥 means \"origin, emergence\" and is mostly used as a noun: 〜発祥の地 \"birthplace of ...\", 日本発祥の〜 \"originating in Japan\". As a する-verb it is intransitive: 〜で発祥した. 起源 is more general and scientific (生命の起源), 由来 explains the origin of a name, and 原産 the native region of plants and animals. 発祥 fits especially culture, sports, foods and civilizations.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"警察は自分たちの不祥事を隠そうとしているんだ。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa jibuntachi no fushōji o kakusō to shite iru n da.","german":"The police are trying to cover up their own scandal.","furigana":"けいさつ|じぶん|ふしょうじ|かく"},{"japanese":"社員の不祥事の責任を取って、社長が辞任した。","romaji":"Shain no fushōji no sekinin o totte, shachō ga jinin shita.","german":"The company president resigned, taking responsibility for an employee's misconduct.","furigana":"しゃいん|ふしょうじ|せきにん|と|しゃちょう|じにん"},{"japanese":"あの球団では最近、選手の不祥事が相次いでいる。","romaji":"Ano kyūdan de wa saikin, senshu no fushōji ga aitsuide iru.","german":"Scandals involving players have been occurring one after another at that baseball club recently.","furigana":"きゅうだん|さいきん|せんしゅ|ふしょうじ|あいつ"}],"notes":"不祥事 refers to embarrassing, reputation-damaging misconduct within an organization, such as embezzlement, data falsification or harassment. Typical are 不祥事を起こす, 不祥事が相次ぐ, 不祥事を隠蔽する and 不祥事の責任を取って辞任する. Unlike スキャンダル, which often concerns the private lives of celebrities, 不祥事 is mostly used for companies, government offices, the police and sports clubs. Neutral to formal, a typical news word.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"食糧の備蓄は十分にあります。","romaji":"Shokuryō no bichiku wa jūbun ni arimasu.","german":"We have ample food reserves.","furigana":"しょくりょう|びちく|じゅうぶん"},{"japanese":"世界では今も、多くの人が食糧不足に苦しんでいる。","romaji":"Sekai de wa ima mo, ooku no hito ga shokuryou busoku ni kurushinde iru.","german":"Many people in the world are still suffering from food shortages.","furigana":"せかい|いま|おお|ひと|しょくりょうぶそく|くる"},{"japanese":"被災地に、ヘリコプターで食糧と水が届けられた。","romaji":"Hisaichi ni, herikoputaa de shokuryou to mizu ga todokerareta.","german":"Food and water were delivered to the disaster area by helicopter.","furigana":"ひさいち|しょくりょう|みず|とど"}],"notes":"食糧 refers to food as a supply good, especially staples such as rice and wheat: 食糧不足, 食糧危機, 食糧援助, 食糧を備蓄する. 食料 is more general and covers all foodstuffs including meat and vegetables (食料品店). In politics and disaster management one often reads 食糧; the difference is fluid, however, and authorities today mostly write 食料 (食料自給率).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"食べ過ぎてお腹がパンパンに膨れています。","romaji":"Tabe sugite onaka ga panpan ni fukurete imasu.","german":"I ate too much and my stomach is swollen tight.","furigana":"た|す|なか|ふく"},{"japanese":"お餅を焼くと、ぷうっと膨れてくる。","romaji":"Omochi o yaku to, puutto fukurete kuru.","german":"When you grill rice cake, it puffs up.","furigana":"もち|や|ふく"},{"japanese":"注意されると、娘はすぐにふくれてしまう。","romaji":"Chuui sareru to, musume wa sugu ni fukurete shimau.","german":"When she is scolded, my daughter immediately starts to sulk.","furigana":"ちゅうい|むすめ"}],"notes":"膨れる is intransitive and means \"to swell, to puff up\": お腹が膨れる, 餅が膨れる, 費用が膨れる (intensified: 膨れ上がる). The transitive counterpart is 膨らます / 膨らませる. 膨らむ is very similar but sounds more neutral and is also used positively (夢が膨らむ, つぼみが膨らむ). Second meaning: \"to sulk\", mostly in kana: ふくれる, ふくれっ面.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"宇宙は膨張しています。","romaji":"Uchū wa bōchō shite imasu.","german":"The universe is expanding.","furigana":"うちゅう|ぼうちょう"},{"japanese":"金属は熱を加えると膨張する。","romaji":"Kinzoku wa netsu o kuwaeru to bouchou suru.","german":"Metal expands when heat is applied.","furigana":"きんぞく|ねつ|くわ|ぼうちょう"},{"japanese":"防衛費の膨張が国の財政を圧迫している。","romaji":"Boueihi no bouchou ga kuni no zaisei o appaku shite iru.","german":"Ballooning defense spending is putting pressure on the nation's finances.","furigana":"ぼうえいひ|ぼうちょう|くに|ざいせい|あっぱく"}],"notes":"膨張 is the technical word for expanding and growing, mainly in physics (熱膨張, 気体が膨張する) and astronomy (宇宙の膨張), and figuratively also for budgets, cities or populations: 予算が膨張する \"the budget balloons\". As a する-verb it is intransitive; the opposite is 収縮. The older spelling 膨脹 is still found in specialist texts. In everyday speech, for balloons, bellies or dough one more often says 膨らむ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お風呂あがりには、アロマオイルの芳香を楽しみます。","romaji":"O furo agari ni wa, aromaoiru no hōkō o tanoshimimasu.","german":"After a bath, I enjoy the scent of aroma oil.","furigana":"ふろ|ほうこう|たの"},{"japanese":"トイレに新しい芳香剤を置いた。","romaji":"Toire ni atarashii houkouzai o oita.","german":"I put a new air freshener in the toilet.","furigana":"あたら|ほうこうざい|お"},{"japanese":"庭のバラが、甘い芳香を放っている。","romaji":"Niwa no bara ga, amai houkou o hanatte iru.","german":"The roses in the garden give off a sweet fragrance.","furigana":"にわ|あま|ほうこう|はな"}],"notes":"芳香 is a pleasant scent, a fragrance, and sounds literary: 芳香を放つ \"to give off a fragrance\", 甘い芳香. Very everyday, however, is the compound 芳香剤 \"room fragrance, air freshener\"; in chemistry 芳香族 means \"aromatic\". Otherwise in everyday speech one says 香り or いい匂い; 臭い is a bad smell. Do not confuse it with the identically read 方向 \"direction\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この季節は、梅の香りがとても芳しいですね。","romaji":"Kono kisetsu wa, ume no kaori ga totemo kanbashii desu ne.","german":"The scent of plum blossoms is wonderfully fragrant in this season, isn't it?","furigana":"きせつ|うめ|かお|かんば"},{"japanese":"売り上げが芳しくないので、販売戦略を見直すことにした。","romaji":"Uriage ga kanbashiku nai node, hanbai senryaku o minaosu koto ni shita.","german":"Since sales are not good, we decided to review our sales strategy.","furigana":"う|あ|かんば|はんばいせんりゃく|みなお"},{"japanese":"手術の後、父の体調はあまり芳しくない。","romaji":"Shujutsu no ato, chichi no taichou wa amari kanbashiku nai.","german":"Since the surgery, my father's health has not been very good.","furigana":"しゅじゅつ|あと|ちち|たいちょう|かんば"}],"notes":"芳しい has two meanings. Literally \"fragrant, sweet-smelling\" it is literary (芳しい花の香り); for the roasted aroma of coffee or grilled food, 香ばしい (こうばしい) is used instead. Much more common is the figurative meaning \"good, favorable\", almost always negated: 芳しくない \"not good, unsatisfactory\" for results, reputation or health (成績が芳しくない, 芳しくない評判).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"うちの息子は、若いのに覇気が無い。","romaji":"Uchi no musuko wa, wakai noni haki ga nai.","german":"My son lacks drive even though he is young.","furigana":"むすこ|わか|はき|な"},{"japanese":"新しいキャプテンは覇気があって、チームをしっかり引っ張っている。","romaji":"Atarashii kyaputen wa haki ga atte, chiimu o shikkari hippatte iru.","german":"The new captain has spirit and is leading the team firmly.","furigana":"あたら|はき|ひ|ぱ"},{"japanese":"面接官に「もっと覇気を見せてほしい」と言われた。","romaji":"Mensetsukan ni “motto haki o misete hoshii” to iwareta.","german":"The interviewer said, \"I would like you to show more drive.\"","furigana":"めんせつかん|はき|み|い"}],"notes":"覇気 is drive, vigor and ambition with which someone wants to achieve something. It almost always appears in 覇気がある / 覇気がない or 覇気のない顔 / 声. やる気 is more everyday and means motivation for a concrete task, 野心 is ambition with an often negative connotation, and 意欲 is neutral drive. Manga fans know the word from One Piece as \"Haki\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"わが校の野球部が、秋のリーグ戦を制覇しました。","romaji":"Wagakō no yakyūbu ga, aki no rīgusen o seiha shimashita.","german":"Our school's baseball team won the autumn league.","furigana":"こう|やきゅうぶ|あき|せん|せいは"},{"japanese":"このチームの目標は、全国制覇だ。","romaji":"Kono chiimu no mokuhyou wa, zenkoku seiha da.","german":"This team's goal is to win the national championship.","furigana":"もくひょう|ぜんこくせいは"},{"japanese":"駅前のラーメン屋は、もう全店制覇した。","romaji":"Ekimae no raamen'ya wa, mou zenten seiha shita.","german":"I have already conquered every ramen shop in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|や|ぜんてんせいは"}],"notes":"制覇する means \"to conquer, to dominate\" and, in sports, \"to take the title, to win a tournament\": 〜を制覇する, 全国制覇, 世界制覇. Unlike 優勝する, 制覇 emphasizes complete dominance and sounds more forceful. Used casually it also means \"to tick everything off, to try them all\": 駅前のラーメン屋を全店制覇した. Winning titles in succession is 連覇.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あいつらが前年度のこの大会の覇者です。","romaji":"Aitsura ga zennendo no kono taikai no hasha desu.","german":"Those guys are last year's champions of this tournament.","furigana":"ぜんねんど|たいかい|はしゃ"},{"japanese":"戦国時代の最終的な覇者となったのは、徳川家康だった。","romaji":"Sengoku jidai no saishuuteki na hasha to natta no wa, Tokugawa Ieyasu datta.","german":"The one who ultimately came out on top in the Warring States period was Tokugawa Ieyasu.","furigana":"せんごくじだい|さいしゅうてき|はしゃ|"},{"japanese":"今年のマラソン大会の覇者は、無名の大学生だった。","romaji":"Kotoshi no marason taikai no hasha wa, mumei no daigakusei datta.","german":"This year's marathon winner was an unknown university student.","furigana":"ことし|たいかい|はしゃ|むめい|だいがくせい"}],"notes":"覇者 was originally a ruler who rules by power and force, as opposed to the virtuous 王者. Today it mostly means \"winner, title holder\" of a competition: 大会の覇者, 前年度の覇者 \"defending champion\", 世界の覇者. It sounds somewhat elevated and is often used in sports reports; more neutral are 優勝者 and 王者 or チャンピオン.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今までに甲子園を三連覇した高校はありますか？","romaji":"Ima made ni Kōshien o san renpa shita kōkō wa arimasu ka?","german":"Has there ever been a high school that won Koshien three times in a row?","furigana":"いま|こうしえん|さんれんぱ|こうこう"},{"japanese":"彼女はこの大会で、ついに五連覇を達成した。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kono taikai de, tsui ni go renpa o tassei shita.","german":"She finally achieved her fifth consecutive title at this tournament.","furigana":"かのじょ|たいかい|ごれんぱ|たっせい"},{"japanese":"王者チームは決勝で敗れ、連覇を逃した。","romaji":"Ouja chiimu wa kesshou de yabure, renpa o nogashita.","german":"The champion team lost in the final and missed out on a repeat title.","furigana":"おうじゃ|けっしょう|やぶ|れんぱ|のが"}],"notes":"連覇 means \"consecutive title wins\" and often appears with a number: 二連覇, 三連覇, 大会五連覇. Typical combinations are 連覇を達成する, 連覇を狙う and 連覇を逃す \"to miss out on defending the title\". As a する-verb, the competition can take を (甲子園を三連覇する). It is a term from sports and competitions; a single win is 優勝 or 制覇.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"最高級のトロを一貫だけ注文しました。","romaji":"Sai kōkyū no toro o ikkan dake chūmon shimashita.","german":"I ordered just one piece of the finest fatty tuna.","furigana":"さいこうきゅう|いっかん|ちゅうもん"},{"japanese":"彼は最初から一貫して、計画に反対していた。","romaji":"Kare wa saisho kara ikkan shite, keikaku ni hantai shite ita.","german":"He has consistently opposed the plan from the very beginning.","furigana":"かれ|さいしょ|いっかん|けいかく|はんたい"},{"japanese":"娘は、中高一貫校に通っています。","romaji":"Musume wa, chuukou ikkankou ni kayotte imasu.","german":"My daughter attends an integrated middle and high school.","furigana":"むすめ|ちゅうこういっかんこう|かよ"}],"notes":"一貫 has several meanings. The most important is \"consistency, coherence\": 一貫して \"throughout, consistently\", 一貫した態度, 一貫性 \"consistency\", 中高一貫校 \"school with integrated middle and high school\". In a sushi restaurant, 貫 is the counter for nigiri pieces: 一貫, 二貫. The old unit of weight 貫 (about 3.75 kg) now appears only in set expressions.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ブラジャーのワイヤーが弾丸の貫通を防いでくれた。","romaji":"Burajā no waiyā ga dangan no kantsū o fuseide kureta.","german":"The wire in my bra protected me from the bullet penetrating.","furigana":"だんがん|かんつう|ふせ"},{"japanese":"長年の工事の末、ついにトンネルが貫通した。","romaji":"Naganen no kouji no sue, tsui ni tonneru ga kantsuu shita.","german":"After years of construction work, the tunnel was finally broken through.","furigana":"ながねん|こうじ|すえ|かんつう"},{"japanese":"釘が板を貫通して、裏側に突き出ている。","romaji":"Kugi ga ita o kantsuu shite, uragawa ni tsukidete iru.","german":"The nail went through the board and sticks out on the back.","furigana":"くぎ|いた|かんつう|うらがわ|つ|で"}],"notes":"貫通する means \"to penetrate, to pierce, to go all the way through\" and can be intransitive (弾丸が貫通する) or transitive (弾丸が壁を貫通する). Typical subjects are bullets, nails and tunnels: トンネルが貫通する refers to the breakthrough in tunnel construction. The native verb 貫く is similar but is also used figuratively (信念を貫く); 貫通, by contrast, stays concrete and sounds technical.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大変だったけど、みんなで決めた計画を貫徹することができた。","romaji":"Taihen datta kedo, minna de kimeta keikaku o kantetsu suru koto ga dekita.","german":"It was hard, but we were able to carry out the plan we all decided on.","furigana":"たいへん|き|けいかく|かんてつ"},{"japanese":"初志貫徹で、最後まであきらめずに頑張ります。","romaji":"Shoshi kantetsu de, saigo made akiramezu ni ganbarimasu.","german":"I will stay true to my original resolve and keep going without giving up until the end.","furigana":"しょしかんてつ|さいご|がんば"},{"japanese":"労働組合は、賃上げの要求を最後まで貫徹した。","romaji":"Roudou kumiai wa, chin'age no youkyuu o saigo made kantetsu shita.","german":"The labor union pushed its wage demand through to the end.","furigana":"ろうどうくみあい|ちんあ|ようきゅう|さいご|かんてつ"}],"notes":"貫徹する means \"to carry through to the end\" a plan, a demand or a will: 計画を貫徹する, 要求を貫徹する. Very well known is the four-character phrase 初志貫徹 \"to stay true to one's original resolve\". It sounds formal and determined; more everyday are やり遂げる and 貫く. Do not confuse it with the identically read slang word 完徹 \"to pull an all-nighter\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"突貫工事で犬小屋を建てました。","romaji":"Tokkan kouji de inugoya o tatemashita.","german":"We built the doghouse in a rush job.","furigana":"とっかんこうじ|いぬごや|た"},{"japanese":"開会式に間に合わせるため、会場は突貫工事で仕上げられた。","romaji":"Kaikaishiki ni maniawaseru tame, kaijou wa tokkan kouji de shiagerareta.","german":"To make it in time for the opening ceremony, the venue was finished at breakneck speed.","furigana":"かいかいしき|ま|あ|かいじょう|とっかんこうじ|しあ"},{"japanese":"試験の前の晩に突貫で勉強したが、ほとんど覚えていない。","romaji":"Shiken no mae no ban ni tokkan de benkyou shita ga, hotondo oboete inai.","german":"I crammed the night before the exam, but I hardly remember anything.","furigana":"しけん|まえ|ばん|とっかん|べんきょう|おぼ"}],"notes":"突貫 was originally a charge with battle cries (obsolete, military). Today it appears almost only in 突貫工事 \"construction work at breakneck speed\" and, figuratively, for any rushed effort under time pressure: 突貫で仕上げる, 突貫作業. It often implies that quality may suffer. As a する-verb (突貫する) it is rare.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不正を行った社員の処遇を考えなくてはいけません。","romaji":"Fusei o okonatta shain no shogū o kangaenakute wa ikemasen.","german":"We have to think about how to deal with the employee who committed misconduct.","furigana":"ふせい|おこな|しゃいん|しょぐう|かんが"},{"japanese":"介護職員の処遇改善が、強く求められている。","romaji":"Kaigo shokuin no shoguu kaizen ga, tsuyoku motomerarete iru.","german":"Improvement of care workers' pay and working conditions is strongly demanded.","furigana":"かいごしょくいん|しょぐうかいぜん|つよ|もと"},{"japanese":"能力に見合った処遇を受けられず、彼は転職を決めた。","romaji":"Nouryoku ni miatta shoguu o ukerarezu, kare wa tenshoku o kimeta.","german":"Not receiving treatment commensurate with his abilities, he decided to change jobs.","furigana":"のうりょく|みあ|しょぐう|う|かれ|てんしょく|き"}],"notes":"処遇 is the treatment of a person by an organization - above all status, pay and working conditions, but also the decision on how to deal with someone (e.g. after misconduct). Common are 処遇改善 \"improvement of pay and conditions\" and 処遇を決める / 検討する. 待遇 is very similar but more everyday (待遇がいい会社); 処分 is disciplinary punishment. As a verb 処遇する is rare.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"悲惨な交通事故に遭遇しました。","romaji":"Hisan na kōtsū jiko ni sōgū shimashita.","german":"I witnessed a terrible traffic accident.","furigana":"ひさん|こうつうじこ|そうぐう"},{"japanese":"山道で、野生のクマに遭遇した。","romaji":"Yamamichi de, yasei no kuma ni souguu shita.","german":"I encountered a wild bear on a mountain path.","furigana":"やまみち|やせい|そうぐう"},{"japanese":"海外旅行中に、思わぬトラブルに遭遇した。","romaji":"Kaigai ryokouchuu ni, omowanu toraburu ni souguu shita.","german":"I ran into unexpected trouble while traveling abroad.","furigana":"かいがいりょこうちゅう|おも|そうぐう"}],"notes":"遭遇する means \"to encounter (unexpectedly), to come across, to run into\" and takes に: 事故に遭遇する, トラブルに遭遇する, クマに遭遇する. It is mostly about unplanned, often unpleasant or unusual encounters; also well known is 未知との遭遇 (\"Close Encounters\"). It sounds more formal than 出会う or 出くわす; for misfortune alone one also says 遭う. 遭遇 does not fit arranged meetings.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このカードのお陰で、VIP待遇を味わうことができました。","romaji":"Kono kādo no okage de, VIP taigū o ajiwau koto ga dekimashita.","german":"Thanks to this card, I was able to enjoy VIP treatment.","furigana":"かげ|たいぐう|あじ"},{"japanese":"この会社は給料がよくて休みも多いので、待遇に満足しています。","romaji":"Kono kaisha wa kyuuryou ga yokute yasumi mo ooi node, taiguu ni manzoku shite imasu.","german":"This company pays well and gives a lot of time off, so I am satisfied with the conditions.","furigana":"かいしゃ|きゅうりょう|やす|おお|たいぐう|まんぞく"},{"japanese":"パートの人たちが待遇の改善を求めています。","romaji":"Paato no hitotachi ga taiguu no kaizen o motomete imasu.","german":"The part-time workers are demanding improved working conditions.","furigana":"ひと|たいぐう|かいぜん|もと"}],"notes":"待遇 has two main meanings: the way one treats or receives someone (VIP待遇, 特別待遇, 客の待遇), and the working conditions including pay at a company (待遇がいい会社, 待遇改善を求める). In job ads, 待遇 stands for salary and benefits. As a verb 待遇する is rare; one rather says 扱う or もてなす. Counterparts are 冷遇 \"poor treatment\" and 厚遇 \"very good treatment\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"高齢者を優遇しすぎじゃないでしょうか。","romaji":"Kōreisha o yūgū shisugi ja nai deshō ka.","german":"Aren't the elderly being favored a bit too much?","furigana":"こうれいしゃ|ゆうぐう"},{"japanese":"この会社は、英語ができる人を優遇している。","romaji":"Kono kaisha wa, eigo ga dekiru hito o yūgū shite iru.","german":"This company gives preference to people who can speak English.","furigana":"かいしゃ|えいご|ひと|ゆうぐう"},{"japanese":"新しい制度では、子育て世帯が税制上優遇される。","romaji":"Atarashii seido de wa, kosodate setai ga zeiseijō yūgū sareru.","german":"Under the new system, households raising children are given preferential tax treatment.","furigana":"あたら|せいど|こそだ|せたい|ぜいせいじょうゆうぐう"}],"notes":"優遇する means \"to give preferential, especially good treatment\": 高齢者を優遇する, 経験者を優遇する. It is very common in job ads (経験者優遇 \"experienced applicants preferred\") and in tax and official language: 税制上の優遇措置 \"tax incentives\", 優遇金利 \"preferential interest rate\". The opposite is 冷遇 \"to treat poorly\"; 厚遇 is very similar but emphasizes generous pay or hospitality. Transitive with を, often in the passive 優遇される.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は実力があるのにどうしてこんなに冷遇されているんですか。","romaji":"Kare wa jitsuryoku ga aru noni doushite konna ni reiguu sarete iru n desu ka.","german":"He is capable, so why is he being treated so poorly?","furigana":"かれ|じつりょく|れいぐう"},{"japanese":"上司に逆らった彼は、地方の支店で冷遇された。","romaji":"Joushi ni sakaratta kare wa, chihou no shiten de reiguu sareta.","german":"Because he had defied his boss, he was sidelined at a provincial branch.","furigana":"じょうし|さか|かれ|ちほう|してん|れいぐう"},{"japanese":"その画家は生前は冷遇されたが、死後に高く評価された。","romaji":"Sono gaka wa seizen wa reiguu sareta ga, shigo ni takaku hyouka sareta.","german":"The painter was neglected during his lifetime but highly appreciated after his death.","furigana":"がか|せいぜん|れいぐう|しご|たか|ひょうか"}],"notes":"冷遇 means to treat someone coolly, dismissively or below their worth - for example an employee who is passed over or sidelined despite performance. It most often appears in the passive: 会社で冷遇される \"to be treated poorly at the company\". The opposites are 厚遇 and 優遇 \"preferential treatment\". The word is rather literary; in conversation one also says 冷たくされる or 冷たい扱いを受ける.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自分の不幸を境遇のせいにするんじゃない。","romaji":"Jibun no fukō o kyōgū no sei ni suru n ja nai.","german":"Don't blame your misfortune on your circumstances.","furigana":"じぶん|ふこう|きょうぐう"},{"japanese":"彼女は恵まれない境遇で育ちながら、医者になった。","romaji":"Kanojo wa megumarenai kyouguu de sodachinagara, isha ni natta.","german":"She grew up in disadvantaged circumstances, yet became a doctor.","furigana":"かのじょ|めぐ|きょうぐう|そだ|いしゃ"},{"japanese":"同じ境遇の人と話すと、気持ちが楽になります。","romaji":"Onaji kyouguu no hito to hanasu to, kimochi ga raku ni narimasu.","german":"Talking with people in the same situation makes me feel better.","furigana":"おな|きょうぐう|ひと|はな|きも|らく"}],"notes":"境遇 refers to the circumstances of life into which one is born or has fallen - family, background, economic situation. Typical are 恵まれた境遇 \"privileged circumstances\", 恵まれない境遇, 苦しい境遇 and 同じ境遇の人 \"people in the same situation\". Unlike 環境 \"surroundings, environment\", 境遇 refers to one's personal lot; 状況, by contrast, is a momentary situation. Rather elevated, but also common in conversation.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"露骨な人種差別に遭ったことはありますか。","romaji":"Rokotsu na jinshu sabetsu ni atta koto wa arimasu ka.","german":"Have you ever experienced blatant racial discrimination?","furigana":"ろこつ|じんしゅさべつ|あ"},{"japanese":"帰り道で急に雨に遭って、びしょ濡れになった。","romaji":"Kaerimichi de kyuu ni ame ni atte, bishonure ni natta.","german":"On the way home I was suddenly caught in the rain and got soaked.","furigana":"かえ|みち|きゅう|あめ|あ|ぬ"},{"japanese":"祖母が電話で詐欺に遭いそうになりました。","romaji":"Sobo ga denwa de sagi ni aisou ni narimashita.","german":"My grandmother almost fell victim to a scam over the phone.","furigana":"そぼ|でんわ|さぎ|あ"}],"notes":"遭う means \"to experience (something unpleasant), to get caught in something\" and takes に: 事故に遭う, 雨に遭う, 詐欺に遭う, ひどい目に遭う \"to go through something terrible\". It is a special spelling of 会う: for meeting people one writes 会う, for accidents, bad weather and crimes 遭う. In everyday writing it is often simply written in hiragana あう. The verb is intransitive and has no transitive counterpart.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"危うく遭難するところでした。","romaji":"Ayauku sōnan suru tokoro deshita.","german":"We nearly got into serious trouble.","furigana":"あや|そうなん"},{"japanese":"冬山で遭難した登山者が、三日ぶりに救助された。","romaji":"Fuyuyama de sounan shita tozansha ga, mikka buri ni kyuujo sareta.","german":"The mountain climbers who got into distress in the winter mountains were rescued after three days.","furigana":"ふゆやま|そうなん|とざんしゃ|みっか|きゅうじょ"},{"japanese":"嵐で漁船が遭難し、海上保安庁が捜索している。","romaji":"Arashi de gyosen ga sounan shi, kaijou hoanchou ga sousaku shite iru.","german":"A fishing boat got into distress in the storm, and the Japan Coast Guard is searching for it.","furigana":"あらし|ぎょせん|そうなん|かいじょうほあんちょう|そうさく"}],"notes":"遭難 means to get into mortal danger or have an accident in the mountains or at sea - through bad weather, avalanches, shipwreck, or because one has lost one's way. Typical: 山で遭難する, 冬山で遭難した登山者, 遭難者 \"victim, person in distress\", 遭難信号 \"distress signal\". For traffic accidents one says 事故; 遭難 emphasizes that one is stranded and must be rescued. Intransitive する-verb, with the subject marked by が.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本が鎖国していたのはいつですか。","romaji":"Nihon ga sakoku shite ita no wa itsu desu ka.","german":"When was Japan in isolation?","furigana":"にほん|さこく"},{"japanese":"ペリーの来航をきっかけに、日本は鎖国をやめて開国した。","romaji":"Perii no raikou o kikkake ni, Nihon wa sakoku o yamete kaikoku shita.","german":"Triggered by Perry's arrival, Japan ended its isolation and opened the country.","furigana":"らいこう|にほん|さこく|かいこく"},{"japanese":"外国人を一人も採用しないなんて、この会社はまるで鎖国状態だ。","romaji":"Gaikokujin o hitori mo saiyou shinai nante, kono kaisha wa marude sakoku joutai da.","german":"Not hiring a single foreigner - this company is practically in a state of isolation.","furigana":"がいこくじん|ひとり|さいよう|かいしゃ|さこくじょうたい"}],"notes":"鎖国 refers to a country's closure to the outside, above all Japan's isolation policy under the Tokugawa shogunate (c. 1639-1854). Typical phrases: 鎖国政策, 鎖国を解く, 鎖国していた時代. The opposite is 開国 \"opening of the country\". Figuratively it is also used to criticize present-day insularity, e.g. 鎖国状態 or 鎖国的な考え方.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"銀行が潰れて、預金が封鎖されてしまったんです。","romaji":"Ginkou ga tsuburete, yokin ga fuusa sarete shimatta n desu.","german":"The bank collapsed, and my deposits were frozen.","furigana":"ぎんこう|つぶ|よきん|ふうさ"},{"japanese":"事故の影響で、高速道路の一部が封鎖されています。","romaji":"Jiko no eikyou de, kousoku douro no ichibu ga fuusa sarete imasu.","german":"Part of the expressway is closed because of an accident.","furigana":"じこ|えいきょう|こうそくどうろ|いちぶ|ふうさ"},{"japanese":"警察は犯人を捕まえるため、駅の出口をすべて封鎖した。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa hannin o tsukamaeru tame, eki no deguchi o subete fuusa shita.","german":"To catch the culprit, the police sealed off all the station exits.","furigana":"けいさつ|はんにん|つか|えき|でぐち|ふうさ"}],"notes":"封鎖 means to completely block an access, an area or assets: 道路を封鎖する, 港を封鎖する \"to blockade a port\", 現場が封鎖される, 預金封鎖 \"freezing of bank deposits\". Unlike 閉鎖 \"closure (of a facility)\", 封鎖 emphasizes the sealing off, often by police, military or authorities. Also common are 都市封鎖 \"lockdown\" (often ロックダウン) and 経済封鎖 \"economic blockade\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このプールは今月で閉鎖されます。","romaji":"Kono puuru wa kongetsu de heisa saremasu.","german":"This pool will be closed at the end of this month.","furigana":"こんげつ|へいさ"},{"japanese":"インフルエンザが流行して、息子のクラスが学級閉鎖になった。","romaji":"Infuruenza ga ryuukou shite, musuko no kurasu ga gakkyuu heisa ni natta.","german":"With the flu spreading, my son's class was closed.","furigana":"りゅうこう|むすこ|がっきゅうへいさ"},{"japanese":"あの村は閉鎖的で、よそ者はなかなか受け入れられない。","romaji":"Ano mura wa heisateki de, yosomono wa nakanaka ukeirerarenai.","german":"That village is closed-minded, and outsiders are hardly accepted.","furigana":"むら|へいさてき|もの|う|い"}],"notes":"閉鎖 is the usually longer-term or final closure of a facility or an access: 工場を閉鎖する, 店舗の閉鎖, 学級閉鎖 \"closure of a school class (e.g. because of the flu)\". For the daily closing of a shop one says 閉める or 閉店; 閉鎖 sounds official. The adjective 閉鎖的 means \"closed, insular\" (閉鎖的な村). Compare 封鎖 \"sealing off, blockade\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"まずはこの負の連鎖を断ち切る必要があります。","romaji":"Mazu wa kono fu no rensa o tachikiru hitsuyou ga arimasu.","german":"First of all, we need to break this negative chain.","furigana":"ふ|れんさ|た|き|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"食物連鎖の頂点にいるのは人間だと言われる。","romaji":"Shokumotsu rensa no chouten ni iru no wa ningen da to iwareru.","german":"It is said that humans are at the top of the food chain.","furigana":"しょくもつれんさ|ちょうてん|にんげん|い"},{"japanese":"一社の倒産が連鎖して、取引先も次々とつぶれた。","romaji":"Issha no tousan ga rensa shite, torihikisaki mo tsugitsugi to tsubureta.","german":"One company's bankruptcy set off a chain reaction, and its business partners went under one after another.","furigana":"いっしゃ|とうさん|れんさ|とりひきさき|つぎつぎ"}],"notes":"連鎖 is a chain of connected things or events in which one triggers the next. Frequent in compounds: 連鎖反応 \"chain reaction\", 食物連鎖 \"food chain\", 連鎖倒産 \"chain bankruptcies\", 負の連鎖 \"vicious cycle\". As a verb 連鎖する is intransitive. For a physical metal chain one says 鎖 or チェーン, not 連鎖.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地震であそこの道路が陥没したそうです。","romaji":"Jishin de asoko no dōro ga kanbotsu shita sō desu.","german":"I hear the road over there caved in because of the earthquake.","furigana":"じしん|どうろ|かんぼつ"},{"japanese":"水道管の破裂で道路が陥没し、車が穴に落ちた。","romaji":"Suidoukan no haretsu de douro ga kanbotsu shi, kuruma ga ana ni ochita.","german":"A water main burst and the road collapsed, and a car fell into the hole.","furigana":"すいどうかん|はれつ|どうろ|かんぼつ|くるま|あな|お"},{"japanese":"転んで頭を強く打ち、頭の骨が陥没していた。","romaji":"Koronde atama o tsuyoku uchi, atama no hone ga kanbotsu shite ita.","german":"He fell and hit his head hard, and his skull was depressed.","furigana":"ころ|あたま|つよ|う|あたま|ほね|かんぼつ"}],"notes":"陥没 refers to a surface sinking or caving in downward: 道路が陥没する, 地面の陥没, 陥没骨折 \"depressed fracture\". It is an intransitive する-verb with the ground or road as the subject (with が). Unlike 崩壊 or 倒壊 \"collapse of a building\", 陥没 is about sagging into the depths. Typical of news and technical texts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"トヨタが世界一から陥落したのは何故だと思いますか。","romaji":"Toyota ga sekaiichi kara kanraku shita no wa naze da to omoimasu ka.","german":"Why do you think Toyota fell from the number one spot in the world?","furigana":"せかいいち|かんらく|なぜ|おも"},{"japanese":"激しい攻撃の末、城はついに陥落した。","romaji":"Hageshii kougeki no sue, shiro wa tsui ni kanraku shita.","german":"After fierce attacks, the castle finally fell.","furigana":"はげ|こうげき|すえ|しろ|かんらく"},{"japanese":"何度も頼まれて、ついに父も陥落した。","romaji":"Nando mo tanomarete, tsui ni chichi mo kanraku shita.","german":"After being asked many times, my father finally gave in too.","furigana":"なんど|たの|ちち|かんらく"}],"notes":"陥落 has several meanings: the fall of a fortress or city (首都が陥落する, 城が陥落した), the drop from a top position (首位から陥落する) and, more rarely, the sinking of the ground. Colloquially it also means \"to let oneself be persuaded, to give in\". It is intransitive; transitively one says 攻め落とす \"to capture\". Rather news, history and sports language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"消費者からこの商品の安全性の欠陥が指摘されています。","romaji":"Shouhisha kara kono shouhin no anzensei no kekkan ga shiteki sarete imasu.","german":"Consumers have pointed out safety defects in this product.","furigana":"しょうひしゃ|しょうひん|あんぜんせい|けっかん|してき"},{"japanese":"ブレーキに欠陥が見つかり、車が回収されることになった。","romaji":"Bureeki ni kekkan ga mitsukari, kuruma ga kaishuu sareru koto ni natta.","german":"A defect was found in the brakes, so the cars are going to be recalled.","furigana":"けっかん|み|くるま|かいしゅう"},{"japanese":"新築なのに雨漏りがするなんて、欠陥住宅だ。","romaji":"Shinchiku na noni amamori ga suru nante, kekkan juutaku da.","german":"Rain leaking into a brand-new house - that's a defective house.","furigana":"しんちく|あまも|けっかんじゅうたく"}],"notes":"欠陥 is a fundamental, often dangerous flaw in a product, building, system or plan: 欠陥商品, 欠陥住宅, 制度の欠陥, 欠陥が見つかる. It is stronger than 欠点 \"weakness, drawback\" and more technical than ミス. Applied to people, 欠陥 sounds very disparaging (人格的な欠陥). Related: 不具合 \"malfunction, glitch\", which sounds milder.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"デフォルトでは、名前と生年月日、メールアドレスの入力が必須になっています。","romaji":"Deforuto de wa, namae to seinengappi, mēru adoresu no nyūryoku ga hissu ni natte imasu.","german":"By default, entering your name, date of birth and e-mail address is required.","furigana":"なまえ|せいねんがっぴ|にゅうりょく|ひっす"},{"japanese":"この仕事には運転免許が必須です。","romaji":"Kono shigoto ni wa unten menkyo ga hissu desu.","german":"A driver's license is required for this job.","furigana":"しごと|うんてんめんきょ|ひっす"},{"japanese":"冬山に登るなら、防寒具は必須だ。","romaji":"Fuyuyama ni noboru nara, bōkangu wa hissu da.","german":"If you climb in the mountains in winter, cold-weather gear is a must.","furigana":"ふゆやま|のぼ|ぼうかんぐ|ひっす"}],"notes":"必須 means \"absolutely necessary, mandatory\". It is most often used predicatively (〜が必須だ) or with の before a noun: 必須の条件, 必須科目 \"required subject\", 必須項目 \"required field\" in forms; the form 必須な is rarer. It is stronger than 必要 \"necessary\": one cannot do without it. Reading ひっす, not ひつす; the opposite is 任意 \"optional\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"茜は颯と涙を拭った。","romaji":"Akane wa satto namida o nugutta.","german":"Akane quickly wiped away her tears.","furigana":"あかね|さっ|なみだ|ぬぐ"},{"japanese":"ほうれん草はお湯でさっとゆでてください。","romaji":"Hourensou wa oyu de satto yudete kudasai.","german":"Please blanch the spinach briefly in hot water.","furigana":"そう|ゆ"},{"japanese":"窓を開けると、冷たい風がさっと入ってきた。","romaji":"Mado o akeru to, tsumetai kaze ga satto haitte kita.","german":"When I opened the window, a cold breeze suddenly blew in.","furigana":"まど|あ|つめ|かぜ|はい"}],"notes":"さっと describes a quick, deft action (さっと拭く, さっと片付ける, さっと茹でる \"to blanch briefly\") or a wind or rain that suddenly sets in (風がさっと吹く). The kanji spelling 颯と is rare and literary; さっと is normally written in hiragana. Similar is すっと \"quietly, smoothly\"; さっと stresses speed and lightness. In recipes, さっと means \"just briefly\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子供の時、綾取りをしていたのを覚えています。","romaji":"Kodomo no toki, ayatori o shite ita no o oboete imasu.","german":"I remember playing cat's cradle as a child.","furigana":"こども|とき|あやと|おぼ"},{"japanese":"おばあちゃんにあやとりで東京タワーの作り方を教わった。","romaji":"Obaachan ni ayatori de Toukyou Tawaa no tsukurikata o osowatta.","german":"My grandma taught me how to make the Tokyo Tower with string.","furigana":"とうきょう|つく|かた|おそ"},{"japanese":"雨の日は、教室で友達とあやとりをして遊んだ。","romaji":"Ame no hi wa, kyoushitsu de tomodachi to ayatori o shite asonda.","german":"On rainy days, we played cat's cradle with friends in the classroom.","furigana":"あめ|ひ|きょうしつ|ともだち|あそ"}],"notes":"あやとり is the string game in which a loop of knotted string is used to form figures between the fingers, played alone or by two people taking turns. You say あやとりをする or あやとりで遊ぶ; well-known figures include 東京タワー and ほうき \"broom\". The word is usually written in hiragana; 綾取り is the rarer kanji spelling. 綾 means \"pattern\", 取り means \"taking\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私、紫蘇は苦手なんです。","romaji":"Watashi, shiso wa nigate na n desu.","german":"I'm not really a fan of shiso.","furigana":"わたし|しそ|にがて"},{"japanese":"刺身の下に青じそが敷いてあります。","romaji":"Sashimi no shita ni aojiso ga shiite arimasu.","german":"Green shiso leaves are laid under the sashimi.","furigana":"さしみ|した|あお|し"},{"japanese":"梅干しを赤く染めるのに、赤じそを使います。","romaji":"Umeboshi o akaku someru no ni, akajiso o tsukaimasu.","german":"Red shiso is used to dye umeboshi red.","furigana":"うめぼ|あか|そ|あか|つか"}],"notes":"しそ is the herb perilla (shiso), whose aromatic leaves are common in Japanese cooking: green 青じそ with sashimi, tempura and noodles, and red 赤じそ for coloring umeboshi and for drinks. しそ is usually written in hiragana; 紫蘇 is seen more on labels. In supermarkets the green leaves are often called 大葉. The flavor is strong, so not everyone likes it.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"息子は皮膚病を患っています。","romaji":"Musuko wa hifubyō o wazuratte imasu.","german":"My son is suffering from a skin disease.","furigana":"むすこ|ひふびょう|わずら"},{"japanese":"犬が体をかいてばかりいるので、皮膚病かもしれません。","romaji":"Inu ga karada o kaite bakari iru node, hifubyou kamo shiremasen.","german":"The dog keeps scratching itself, so it may have a skin disease.","furigana":"いぬ|からだ|ひふびょう"},{"japanese":"この皮膚病は人にうつりませんから、安心してください。","romaji":"Kono hifubyou wa hito ni utsurimasen kara, anshin shite kudasai.","german":"This skin disease isn't contagious, so please don't worry.","furigana":"ひふびょう|ひと|あんしん"}],"notes":"皮膚病 is the general term for skin diseases such as eczema, fungal infections or atopic dermatitis. Typical are 皮膚病にかかる, the more formal 皮膚病を患う, and 皮膚病が治る. In conversation people often say 皮膚の病気 too; the medical specialty is 皮膚科 \"dermatology\". The word is also used for pets: 犬の皮膚病.","levelNote":"Compound: 皮膚 (N3) + 病 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"襟元にファンデーションがついていますよ。","romaji":"Erimoto ni fuandeeshon ga tsuite imasu yo.","german":"There's foundation on your collar.","furigana":"えりもと"},{"japanese":"風が冷たいので、マフラーで襟元を温めた。","romaji":"Kaze ga tsumetai node, mafuraa de erimoto o atatameta.","german":"The wind was cold, so I kept my neck warm with a scarf.","furigana":"かぜ|つめ|えりもと|あたた"},{"japanese":"面接の前に、鏡で襟元を整えた。","romaji":"Mensetsu no mae ni, kagami de erimoto o totonoeta.","german":"Before the interview, I straightened my collar in the mirror.","furigana":"めんせつ|まえ|かがみ|えりもと|ととの"}],"notes":"襟元 is the area around the collar, i.e. the front of the neck, meaning both the clothing there and the skin beneath it. Typical: 襟元を正す \"to straighten one's collar\" (also figuratively \"to pull oneself together\"), 襟元が寒い, 襟元が汚れる. The collar alone as a part of a garment is called 襟 or カラー; 襟元 emphasizes the spot on the body. Neutral to slightly formal.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ピンクの襟巻きを巻いているのが私の妹です。","romaji":"Pinku no erimaki o maite iru no ga watashi no imōto desu.","german":"The girl wearing the pink scarf is my little sister.","furigana":"えりま|ま|わたし|いもうと"},{"japanese":"祖母は冬になると、毛糸の襟巻きを編んでくれた。","romaji":"Sobo wa fuyu ni naru to, keito no erimaki o ande kureta.","german":"Every winter, my grandmother knitted me a woolen scarf.","furigana":"そぼ|ふゆ|けいと|えりま|あ"},{"japanese":"昔の写真では、母が狐の襟巻きをしている。","romaji":"Mukashi no shashin de wa, haha ga kitsune no erimaki o shite iru.","german":"In old photos, my mother is wearing a fox-fur stole.","furigana":"むかし|しゃしん|はは|きつね|えりま"}],"notes":"襟巻き is a scarf wrapped around the neck to keep warm. The word sounds a little old-fashioned today; in everyday speech people mostly say マフラー, and スカーフ or ストール for thin fashion scarves. Typical: 襟巻きをする, 襟巻きを巻く. A fur collar can also be called this, e.g. 狐の襟巻き \"fox-fur stole\". The spelling 襟巻 without き also occurs.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お婆ちゃんが丹誠込めて育てたトマトだよ。","romaji":"Obaachan ga tansei komete sodateta tomato da yo.","german":"These are tomatoes that Grandma grew with devoted care.","furigana":"ばあ|たんせいこ|そだ"},{"japanese":"職人が丹誠を込めて作った茶碗です。","romaji":"Shokunin ga tansei o komete tsukutta chawan desu.","german":"This tea bowl was made by a craftsman with great devotion.","furigana":"しょくにん|たんせい|こ|つく|ちゃわん"},{"japanese":"丹誠込めて育てたバラが、台風で全部倒れてしまった。","romaji":"Tansei komete sodateta bara ga, taifuu de zenbu taorete shimatta.","german":"The roses I had raised with such care were all knocked down by the typhoon.","furigana":"たんせいこ|そだ|たいふう|ぜんぶたお"}],"notes":"丹誠 means sincere, tireless effort and care. It almost always appears in the fixed phrase 丹誠を込める or 丹誠込めて \"with one's whole heart\", e.g. when raising vegetables and flowers or in craftwork. Today the spelling 丹精 is much more common; 丹誠 seems somewhat old-fashioned. Do not confuse it with 丹念 \"careful, thorough\", which describes the precise way of working.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"熟練の染師が、一つ一つ丹念に染め上げました。","romaji":"Jukuren no someshi ga, hitotsu hitotsu tannen ni someagemashita.","german":"The skilled dyer dyed each piece one by one with great care.","furigana":"じゅくれん||ひと|ひと|たんねん|そ|あ"},{"japanese":"刑事は現場を丹念に調べて、小さな証拠を見つけた。","romaji":"Keiji wa genba o tannen ni shirabete, chiisana shouko o mitsuketa.","german":"The detective examined the scene thoroughly and found a tiny piece of evidence.","furigana":"けいじ|げんば|たんねん|しら|ちい|しょうこ|み"},{"japanese":"彼女の論文は、資料を丹念に集めて書かれている。","romaji":"Kanojo no ronbun wa, shiryou o tannen ni atsumete kakarete iru.","german":"Her paper is written on the basis of carefully collected materials.","furigana":"かのじょ|ろんぶん|しりょう|たんねん|あつ|か"}],"notes":"丹念 is a な-adjective for a very careful, thorough way of working in which nothing is left out. It mostly appears adverbially: 丹念に調べる, 丹念に磨く, 丹念に読む. Similar are 念入り \"thorough\" and 丁寧 \"careful, polite\"; 丹念 sounds somewhat more elevated and stresses patient attention to detail. The related noun is 丹誠 \"devoted effort\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"過剰包装は紙の無駄です。","romaji":"Kajō hōsō wa kami no muda desu.","german":"Excessive packaging is a waste of paper.","furigana":"かじょうほうそう|かみ|むだ"},{"japanese":"ちょっと注意されただけで怒るなんて、過剰反応だよ。","romaji":"Chotto chuui sareta dake de okoru nante, kajou hannou da yo.","german":"Getting angry just because you were told off a little - that is an overreaction.","furigana":"ちゅうい|おこ|かじょうはんのう"},{"japanese":"親の過剰な期待が、子どもの負担になることもある。","romaji":"Oya no kajou na kitai ga, kodomo no futan ni naru koto mo aru.","german":"Parents' excessive expectations can become a burden for children.","furigana":"おや|かじょう|きたい|こ|ふたん"}],"notes":"過剰 means \"too much, beyond the necessary measure\" and is used as a noun, as a な-adjective and above all in compounds: 過剰包装, 過剰反応 \"overreaction\", 自信過剰 \"overconfidence\", 人員過剰. Before nouns it appears as 過剰な: 過剰な期待. The opposite is 不足 \"shortage\". Unlike 余剰 \"surplus, remainder\", 過剰 is clearly negative in tone.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"余剰資金で株を買いました。","romaji":"Yojō shikin de kabu o kaimashita.","german":"I bought stocks with surplus funds.","furigana":"よじょうしきん|かぶ|か"},{"japanese":"会社は余剰人員を減らすため、早期退職を募った。","romaji":"Kaisha wa yojou jin'in o herasu tame, souki taishoku o tsunotta.","german":"To reduce surplus staff, the company called for early retirement.","furigana":"かいしゃ|よじょうじんいん|へ|そうきたいしょく|つの"},{"japanese":"太陽光発電の余剰電力は、電力会社に売ることができる。","romaji":"Taiyoukou hatsuden no yojou denryoku wa, denryoku gaisha ni uru koto ga dekiru.","german":"Surplus electricity from solar power can be sold to the power company.","furigana":"たいようこうはつでん|よじょうでんりょく|でんりょくがいしゃ|う"}],"notes":"余剰 is a surplus, i.e. what is left over after needs are met. It almost always stands before another noun: 余剰資金, 余剰人員 \"surplus staff\", 余剰電力, 余剰生産物, or with の (余剰の米). There is no な-form. Unlike 過剰 \"too much\", 余剰 is factual and neutral and typical of economics and administration; in everyday speech people simply say 余り or 余分.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"平壌で英語を教えていたことがあります。","romaji":"Pyon'yan de eigo o oshiete ita koto ga arimasu.","german":"I once taught English in Pyongyang.","furigana":"ぴょんやん|えいご|おし"},{"japanese":"ニュースで平壌の街の様子が映っていた。","romaji":"Nyuusu de Pyon'yan no machi no yousu ga utsutte ita.","german":"The news showed scenes of the streets of Pyongyang.","furigana":"ぴょんやん|まち|ようす|うつ"},{"japanese":"日清戦争では、平壌で大きな戦いがあった。","romaji":"Nisshin sensou de wa, Heijou de ookina tatakai ga atta.","german":"In the Sino-Japanese War, there was a major battle at Pyongyang.","furigana":"|へいじょう|おお|たたか"}],"notes":"平壌 is the capital of North Korea. Today it is read ピョンヤン; in the news this reading often appears as furigana, or the katakana spelling is used directly. The Sino-Japanese reading へいじょう appears only in historical contexts, such as the 平壌の戦い (へいじょうのたたかい) in the First Sino-Japanese War. Typical are 平壌市内 and 平壌からの報道.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これが、あの有名な最高司令官のマッカーサー元帥です。","romaji":"Kore ga, ano yuumei na saikou shireikan no Makkaasaa gensui desu.","german":"This is the famous Supreme Commander, Marshal MacArthur.","furigana":"ゆうめい|さいこうしれいかん|げんすい"},{"japanese":"東郷平八郎は、のちに元帥の称号を与えられた。","romaji":"Tougou Heihachirou wa, nochi ni gensui no shougou o ataerareta.","german":"Tōgō Heihachirō was later given the title of marshal.","furigana":"とうごうへいはちろう|げんすい|しょうごう|あた"},{"japanese":"ナポレオンは多くの部下を元帥に任命した。","romaji":"Naporeon wa ooku no buka o gensui ni ninmei shita.","german":"Napoleon appointed many of his subordinates marshals.","furigana":"おお|ぶか|げんすい|にんめい"}],"notes":"元帥 is the highest military rank, e.g. \"field marshal\" in the army and \"fleet admiral\" in the navy (陸軍元帥, 海軍元帥). It is used like a title after the name: マッカーサー元帥, 東郷元帥. This rank no longer exists in Japan today, so the word appears mainly in historical texts and for foreign armies. Do not confuse it with 将軍 \"general, shogun\" or 総帥 \"head of an organization\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"それは、グループの総帥じきじきの指示だったのです。","romaji":"Sore wa, guruupu no sousui jikijiki no shiji datta no desu.","german":"That was an instruction directly from the head of the whole group.","furigana":"そうすい|しじ"},{"japanese":"彼は三十代の若さで、巨大企業グループの総帥となった。","romaji":"Kare wa sanjuudai no wakasa de, kyodai kigyou guruupu no sousui to natta.","german":"In his thirties, he became the head of a huge corporate group.","furigana":"かれ|さんじゅうだい|わか|きょだいきぎょう|そうすい"},{"japanese":"敵軍の総帥が降伏を申し出た。","romaji":"Tekigun no sousui ga koufuku o moushideta.","german":"The supreme commander of the enemy army offered to surrender.","furigana":"てきぐん|そうすい|こうふく|もう|で"}],"notes":"総帥 is the supreme head of a large organization who directs everything: originally the commander-in-chief of an army, today mainly the head of a conglomerate or corporate group (グループの総帥, 財閥の総帥). It sounds weighty and is typical of business journalism. For an ordinary company head, people say 社長; 元帥, on the other hand, is a military rank. Hitler's title \"Führer\" is 総統 in Japanese, not 総帥.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日は学校で、天皇の統帥権について学びました。","romaji":"Kyou wa gakkou de, tennou no tousuiken ni tsuite manabimashita.","german":"Today at school, I learned about the Emperor's supreme command authority.","furigana":"きょう|がっこう|てんのう|とうすいけん|まな"},{"japanese":"当時、陸軍と海軍の統帥は天皇が行うと定められていた。","romaji":"Touji, rikugun to kaigun no tousui wa tennou ga okonau to sadamerarete ita.","german":"At that time, it was stipulated that the Emperor exercised supreme command over the army and navy.","furigana":"とうじ|りくぐん|かいぐん|とうすい|てんのう|おこな|さだ"},{"japanese":"大統領は軍の統帥権を持っている。","romaji":"Daitouryou wa gun no tousuiken o motte iru.","german":"The president holds supreme command of the military.","furigana":"だいとうりょう|ぐん|とうすいけん|も"}],"notes":"統帥 is military supreme command, i.e. the highest authority to command the armed forces. It is known mainly from history: under the Meiji Constitution, the 統帥権 \"right of supreme command\" lay with the Emperor, and the dispute over its independence from the government strengthened the military's influence. The word is formal and today appears almost only in historical or political texts. Compare 総帥 \"head\": that is the person, whereas 統帥 is the authority to command.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"住宅地や学校の近くでは、徐行してください。","romaji":"Juutakuchi ya gakkou no chikaku de wa, jokou shite kudasai.","german":"Please drive slowly near residential areas and schools.","furigana":"じゅうたくち|がっこう|ちか|じょこう"},{"japanese":"大雨のため、電車は徐行運転をしています。","romaji":"Ooame no tame, densha wa jokou unten o shite imasu.","german":"Because of the heavy rain, trains are running at reduced speed.","furigana":"おおあめ|でんしゃ|じょこううんてん"},{"japanese":"徐行の標識があったら、すぐ止まれる速さで走らなければならない。","romaji":"Jokou no hyoushiki ga attara, sugu tomareru hayasa de hashiranakereba naranai.","german":"When there is a slow-down sign, you must drive at a speed at which you can stop immediately.","furigana":"じょこう|ひょうしき|と|はや|はし"}],"notes":"徐行 means moving so slowly that you can stop immediately at any time. It is mainly traffic language: signs show 徐行, announcements and driving instructors say 徐行してください. Trains, too, run 徐行運転 during construction work or heavy rain. For pedestrians one does not say 徐行, but ゆっくり歩く. An intransitive する-verb.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 33 (source: N4; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二学期に入って、徐々に成績が上がり始めました。","romaji":"Ni-gakki ni haitte, jojo ni seiseki ga agari hajimemashita.","german":"After the second term began, my grades gradually started to rise.","furigana":"にがっき|はい|じょじょ|せいせき|あ|はじ"},{"japanese":"新しい仕事にも徐々に慣れてきました。","romaji":"Atarashii shigoto ni mo jojo ni narete kimashita.","german":"I've gradually gotten used to the new job.","furigana":"あたら|しごと|じょじょ|な"},{"japanese":"手術の後、父の体力は徐々に回復している。","romaji":"Shujutsu no ato, chichi no tairyoku wa jojo ni kaifuku shite iru.","german":"After the operation, my father's strength is gradually recovering.","furigana":"しゅじゅつ|あと|ちち|たいりょく|じょじょ|かいふく"}],"notes":"徐々 almost always appears as the adverb 徐々に \"gradually, little by little\": 徐々に慣れる, 徐々に回復する, 徐々に増える. It sounds somewhat more literary than だんだん and stresses a steady, slow change; 次第に is similar but even more formal. In conversation, だんだん or 少しずつ are more common. The forms 徐々と and 徐々たる are obsolete.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"惨事を招くことは避けたい。","romaji":"Sanji o maneku koto wa saketai.","german":"I want to avoid bringing about a disaster.","furigana":"さんじ|まね|さ"},{"japanese":"運転手の居眠りが、大惨事を引き起こした。","romaji":"Untenshu no inemuri ga, daisanji o hikiokoshita.","german":"The driver falling asleep caused a terrible disaster.","furigana":"うんてんしゅ|いねむ|だいさんじ|ひ|お"},{"japanese":"火災報知器のおかげで、惨事を防ぐことができた。","romaji":"Kasai houchiki no okage de, sanji o fusegu koto ga dekita.","german":"Thanks to the smoke detector, a tragedy could be prevented.","furigana":"かさいほうちき|さんじ|ふせ"}],"notes":"惨事 is a terrible event with many victims or great destruction, such as a serious accident, fire or attack. The intensified form 大惨事 is very common: 大惨事になる, 大惨事を招く. Typical verbs are 起こる, 招く \"to bring about\" and 防ぐ \"to prevent\". Unlike 災害 \"(natural) disaster\", 惨事 stresses the gruesome occurrence; 惨状, on the other hand, is the terrible sight afterwards.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本はワールドカップで惨敗してしまった。","romaji":"Nippon wa wārudokappu de zanpai shite shimatta.","german":"Japan was crushed at the World Cup.","furigana":"にっぽん|ざんぱい"},{"japanese":"与党は今回の選挙で惨敗し、首相が辞任した。","romaji":"Yotou wa konkai no senkyo de zanpai shi, shushou ga jinin shita.","german":"The ruling party suffered a crushing defeat in this election, and the prime minister resigned.","furigana":"よとう|こんかい|せんきょ|ざんぱい|しゅしょう|じにん"},{"japanese":"ゲームで弟に挑んだが、0対10で惨敗した。","romaji":"Geemu de otouto ni idonda ga, zero tai juu de zanpai shita.","german":"I challenged my little brother at a game but lost 0 to 10.","furigana":"おとうと|いど|たい|ざんぱい"}],"notes":"惨敗 is a crushing, humiliating defeat, in sports, elections or exams. Typical: 〜に惨敗する \"to lose by a landslide to ...\", 選挙で惨敗する, and the formal 惨敗を喫する. The usual reading is ざんぱい; さんぱい also occurs. The opposite is 圧勝 \"overwhelming victory\"; an ordinary loss is 負ける or 敗北.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"東日本大震災の惨状を忘れることができません。","romaji":"Higashi Nihon daishinsai no sanjou o wasureru koto ga dekimasen.","german":"I cannot forget the devastation of the Great East Japan Earthquake.","furigana":"ひがしにほんだいしんさい|さんじょう|わす"},{"japanese":"記者は戦地の惨状を世界に伝えた。","romaji":"Kisha wa senchi no sanjou o sekai ni tsutaeta.","german":"The reporter told the world about the appalling conditions in the war zone.","furigana":"きしゃ|せんち|さんじょう|せかい|つた"},{"japanese":"台風の翌朝、畑の惨状を見て農家の人たちは言葉を失った。","romaji":"Taifuu no yokuasa, hatake no sanjou o mite nouka no hitotachi wa kotoba o ushinatta.","german":"The morning after the typhoon, the farmers were left speechless at the sight of their devastated fields.","furigana":"たいふう|よくあさ|はたけ|さんじょう|み|のうか|ひと|ことば|うしな"}],"notes":"惨状 is the horrific condition or sight after a disaster, war or accident: 被災地の惨状, 惨状を目の当たりにする \"to see the misery with one's own eyes\", 惨状を訴える. While 惨事 is the terrible event itself, 惨状 describes the situation one finds afterwards. The word is literary and typical of reports and news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"誕生日会で、手品を披露しました。","romaji":"Tanjōbikai de, tejina o hirō shimashita.","german":"I performed magic tricks at the birthday party.","furigana":"たんじょうびかい|てじな|ひろう"},{"japanese":"新郎の友人が、披露宴でギターの演奏を披露した。","romaji":"Shinrou no yuujin ga, hirouen de gitaa no ensou o hirou shita.","german":"The groom's friend played the guitar at the wedding reception.","furigana":"しんろう|ゆうじん|ひろうえん|えんそう|ひろう"},{"japanese":"その歌手は、コンサートで新曲を初めて披露した。","romaji":"Sono kashu wa, konsaato de shinkyoku o hajimete hirou shita.","german":"The singer performed her new song for the first time at the concert.","furigana":"かしゅ|しんきょく|はじ|ひろう"}],"notes":"披露 means to show or perform something in front of others, or to announce it officially: 手品を披露する, 歌を披露する, 新作を披露する, 腕前を披露する \"to show one's skill\". Well known is 結婚披露宴 \"wedding reception\", where the couple is introduced to the guests. Unlike 発表 \"announcement, presentation\", 披露 often carries a sense of proudly showing off. Transitive する-verb with を.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"アスパラガスが歯に挟まっています。","romaji":"Asuparagasu ga ha ni hasamatte imasu.","german":"You have asparagus stuck in your teeth.","furigana":"は|はさ"},{"japanese":"電車のドアにかばんが挟まってしまった。","romaji":"Densha no doa ni kaban ga hasamatte shimatta.","german":"My bag got caught in the train door.","furigana":"でんしゃ|はさ"},{"japanese":"母と妻の間に挟まって、毎日気が休まらない。","romaji":"Haha to tsuma no aida ni hasamatte, mainichi ki ga yasumaranai.","german":"Caught between my mother and my wife, I get no peace of mind any day.","furigana":"はは|つま|あいだ|はさ|まいにちき|やす"}],"notes":"挟まる means \"to get between things, to be caught\": 歯に何かが挟まる, ドアに指が挟まる. It is intransitive, with が for the thing caught and に for the place; the transitive partner is 挟む \"to pinch, to put in between\" (パンにハムを挟む). Figuratively, one can also be stuck between two parties: 母と妻の間に挟まる, often in the sense of an unpleasant mediator role (cf. 板挟み). A neutral everyday word.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今度、この機械を工場に据えようと思っているんだ。","romaji":"Kondo, kono kikai o kōjō ni sueyō to omotte iru n da.","german":"I'm thinking of installing this machine in the factory soon.","furigana":"こんど|きかい|こうじょう|す|おも"},{"japanese":"創業者は、息子を次の社長に据えた。","romaji":"Sougyousha wa, musuko o tsugi no shachou ni sueta.","german":"The founder installed his son as the next president.","furigana":"そうぎょうしゃ|むすこ|つぎ|しゃちょう|す"},{"japanese":"腰を据えて、日本語の勉強に取り組むつもりです。","romaji":"Koshi o suete, nihongo no benkyou ni torikumu tsumori desu.","german":"I intend to settle down and seriously commit to studying Japanese.","furigana":"こし|す|にほんご|べんきょう|と|く"}],"notes":"据える means to place something heavy or important firmly in a certain spot: 機械を据える, 墓石を据える. Figuratively, it means to install someone in a position (社長に据える \"to make someone president\") or to fix one's gaze firmly on something. Common phrases: 腰を据える \"to settle down and devote oneself seriously and lastingly to something\" and 腹を据える \"to make up one's mind firmly\". The intransitive counterpart is 据わる (目が据わる \"to have a fixed stare\").","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"モーターショーでは、各メーカーが最新鋭のエンジンを搭載した車を発表した。","romaji":"Mootaa shoo de wa, kaku meekaa ga saishin'ei no enjin o tousai shita kuruma o happyou shita.","german":"At the motor show, each manufacturer unveiled cars equipped with the latest engines.","furigana":"かく|さいしんえい|とうさい|くるま|はっぴょう"},{"japanese":"このスマホには高性能のカメラが搭載されている。","romaji":"Kono sumaho ni wa kouseinou no kamera ga tousai sarete iru.","german":"This smartphone is equipped with a high-performance camera.","furigana":"こうせいのう|とうさい"},{"japanese":"ロケットに観測衛星が搭載され、打ち上げられた。","romaji":"Roketto ni kansoku eisei ga tousai sare, uchiagerareta.","german":"An observation satellite was loaded onto the rocket and launched.","furigana":"かんそくえいせい|とうさい|う|あ"}],"notes":"搭載 originally means to load cargo onto a ship or aircraft; today it mainly means to equip a device or vehicle with a function or component: カメラ機能を搭載する, ミサイルを搭載した艦. Typical patterns are X に Y を搭載する and Y 搭載の X \"X equipped with Y\" (AI搭載のスマホ). It sounds technical and is very common in advertising and news; in everyday speech people also say 付いている.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"枯れ木に花を咲かせましょう。","romaji":"Kareki ni hana o sakasemashō.","german":"Let's make flowers bloom on the dead tree.","furigana":"か|き|はな|さ"},{"japanese":"台風で倒れそうな枯れ木を、市が切り倒した。","romaji":"Taifuu de taoresou na kareki o, shi ga kiritaoshita.","german":"The city cut down a dead tree that looked about to fall in the typhoon.","furigana":"たいふう|たお|か|き|し|き|たお"},{"japanese":"冬の公園には、葉を落とした枯れ木が並んでいた。","romaji":"Fuyu no kouen ni wa, ha o otoshita kareki ga narande ita.","german":"In the winter park, bare trees that had shed their leaves stood in a row.","furigana":"ふゆ|こうえん|は|お|か|き|なら"}],"notes":"枯れ木 is a dead tree or a bare tree without leaves. Well known is the phrase 枯れ木に花を咲かせる from the fairy tale 花咲かじいさん, in which an old man makes dead trees bloom; figuratively it means \"to revive something lost\". The proverb 枯れ木も山の賑わい means \"even something insignificant is better than nothing\". The related verb is 枯れる \"to wither, die\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"水をやりすぎて、サボテンを枯らしてしまいました。","romaji":"Mizu o yari sugite, saboten o karashite shimaimashita.","german":"I watered the cactus too much and it died.","furigana":"みず|か"},{"japanese":"除草剤をまいて、庭の雑草を枯らした。","romaji":"Josouzai o maite, niwa no zassou o karashita.","german":"I sprayed weed killer and killed the weeds in the garden.","furigana":"じょそうざい|にわ|ざっそう|か"},{"japanese":"応援団は声を枯らして選手を励ました。","romaji":"Ouendan wa koe o karashite senshu o hagemashita.","german":"The cheering squad encouraged the players until their voices were hoarse.","furigana":"おうえんだん|こえ|か|せんしゅ|はげ"}],"notes":"枯らす is the transitive counterpart of 枯れる: you let plants die, usually by accident and therefore often in the form 枯らしてしまう. Typical: 植物を枯らす, 水やりを忘れて枯らす, 除草剤で雑草を枯らす. Rarely it also means \"to let wood season\". Figuratively, people say 声を枯らす \"to shout oneself hoarse\" (also written 嗄らす).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"スープが沸騰してグツグツいってますよ。","romaji":"Sūpu ga futtō shite gutsugutsu ittemasu yo.","german":"The soup is boiling and bubbling away.","furigana":"ふっとう"},{"japanese":"水が沸騰したら、パスタを入れてください。","romaji":"Mizu ga futtou shitara, pasuta o irete kudasai.","german":"Once the water boils, please add the pasta.","furigana":"みず|ふっとう|い"},{"japanese":"その発言をきっかけに、ネット上で議論が沸騰した。","romaji":"Sono hatsugen o kikkake ni, netto-jou de giron ga futtou shita.","german":"That remark set off a heated debate online.","furigana":"はつげん|じょう|ぎろん|ふっとう"}],"notes":"沸騰 means \"to boil, to come to a boil\" and is the somewhat more formal word for 沸く: お湯が沸騰する, 沸騰したお湯. Figuratively it describes a heated mood (議論が沸騰する, 人気が沸騰する) or sharply rising prices (地価の沸騰). It is intransitive; transitively, say お湯を沸かす or 沸騰させる. Common in recipes: 沸騰したら火を弱める.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この事件が業界の自浄を促すだろう。","romaji":"Kono jiken ga gyoukai no jijou o unagasu darou.","german":"This incident will probably prompt the industry to clean up its own act.","furigana":"じけん|ぎょうかい|じじょう|うなが"},{"japanese":"川には自浄作用があるが、汚染が多すぎると追いつかない。","romaji":"Kawa ni wa jijou sayou ga aru ga, osen ga oosugiru to oitsukanai.","german":"Rivers have a self-purifying capacity, but it can't keep up if pollution is too heavy.","furigana":"かわ|じじょうさよう|おせん|おお|お"},{"japanese":"不祥事が続き、組織の自浄能力が問われている。","romaji":"Fushouji ga tsuzuki, soshiki no jijou nouryoku ga towarete iru.","german":"After a string of scandals, the organization's capacity for self-purification is being questioned.","furigana":"ふしょうじ|つづ|そしき|じじょうのうりょく|と"}],"notes":"自浄 is the ability to cleanse oneself by one's own power. It almost always appears in the compounds 自浄作用 \"self-purifying action\" (of rivers, the sea or the body) and 自浄能力: 川の自浄作用, 組織の自浄能力が問われる. Figuratively it concerns organizations that are supposed to remove abuses themselves. As a verb, 自浄する is rare; the word is formal and typical of news and commentary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"インドでは、左手を不浄の手として、右手でご飯を食べる。","romaji":"Indo de wa, hidarite o fujō no te toshite, migite de gohan o taberu.","german":"In India, the left hand is regarded as unclean, and people eat with the right hand.","furigana":"ひだりて|ふじょう|て|みぎて|はん|た"},{"japanese":"神社に入る前に、手と口を清めて不浄を払う。","romaji":"Jinja ni hairu mae ni, te to kuchi o kiyomete fujou o harau.","german":"Before entering a shrine, you cleanse your hands and mouth to wash away impurity.","furigana":"じんじゃ|はい|まえ|て|くち|きよ|ふじょう|はら"},{"japanese":"祖母はトイレのことを「ご不浄」と呼んでいた。","romaji":"Sobo wa toire no koto o gofujou to yonde ita.","german":"My grandmother always called the toilet \"gofujō\".","furigana":"そぼ|ふじょう|よ"}],"notes":"不浄 means \"unclean\" in a religious or ritual sense and is used as a noun or with の/な: 不浄の手, 不浄なもの, 不浄を清める. In Shintō and Buddhism, the contrast 清浄 \"pure\" - 不浄 plays a large role. The polite, old-fashioned term ご不浄 means \"toilet\". For everyday dirt, say 汚い, not 不浄.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"三十万円の浄水器を売りつけられそうになりました。","romaji":"Sanjuuman-en no jousuiki o uritsukeraresou ni narimashita.","german":"I was almost talked into buying a 300,000 yen water purifier.","furigana":"さんじゅうまんえん|じょうすいき|う"},{"japanese":"この町の水道水は、川の水を浄水場できれいにしたものです。","romaji":"Kono machi no suidousui wa, kawa no mizu o jousuijou de kirei ni shita mono desu.","german":"The tap water in this town is river water that has been cleaned at a water purification plant.","furigana":"まち|すいどうすい|かわ|みず|じょうすいじょう"},{"japanese":"お茶をいれるときは、水道水ではなく浄水を使っています。","romaji":"Ocha o ireru toki wa, suidousui de wa naku jousui o tsukatte imasu.","german":"When making tea, I use purified water instead of tap water.","furigana":"ちゃ|すいどうすい|じょうすい|つか"}],"notes":"浄水 is purified, clean water, mostly in the context of water treatment. It is common in compounds: 浄水器 \"water filter\", 浄水場 \"water purification plant\", 浄水処理 \"water treatment\". In everyday speech, for drinking water people tend to say きれいな水 or 水道水; 浄水 sounds technical. The opposite is 汚水 \"sewage\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"電気ポットはどうやって洗浄してますか？","romaji":"Denki potto wa dō yatte senjō shitemasu ka?","german":"How do you clean your electric kettle?","furigana":"でんき|せんじょう"},{"japanese":"転んでできた傷は、まず水でよく洗浄してください。","romaji":"Koronde dekita kizu wa, mazu mizu de yoku senjou shite kudasai.","german":"For a wound from a fall, first rinse it well with water.","furigana":"ころ|きず|みず|せんじょう"},{"japanese":"犯罪で得たお金を洗浄した疑いで、男が逮捕された。","romaji":"Hanzai de eta okane o senjou shita utagai de, otoko ga taiho sareta.","german":"A man was arrested on suspicion of laundering money obtained through crime.","furigana":"はんざい|え|かね|せんじょう|うたが|おとこ|たいほ"}],"notes":"洗浄 is thorough cleaning with water or a cleaning agent, especially of devices, wounds and organs: 傷口を洗浄する, 胃洗浄 \"stomach pumping\", 洗浄剤 \"cleaning agent\", 温水洗浄便座 \"bidet toilet seat\". It sounds more technical than 洗う, which usually suffices in everyday speech. Figuratively there is 資金洗浄 \"money laundering\" (often マネーロンダリング). Transitive する-verb with を.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"蚊取り線香はどうして渦巻き状になっているんですか。","romaji":"Katorisenkō wa dōshite uzumakijō ni natte iru n desu ka.","german":"Why are mosquito coils spiral-shaped?","furigana":"かと|せんこう|うずま|じょう"},{"japanese":"川の真ん中に大きな渦巻きができていて危ない。","romaji":"Kawa no mannaka ni ookina uzumaki ga dekite ite abunai.","german":"A large whirlpool has formed in the middle of the river, and it's dangerous.","furigana":"かわ|ま|なか|おお|うずま|あぶ"},{"japanese":"娘に渦巻き模様のキャンディーを買ってあげた。","romaji":"Musume ni uzumaki moyou no kyandii o katte ageta.","german":"I bought my daughter a candy with a swirl pattern.","furigana":"むすめ|うずま|もよう|か"}],"notes":"渦巻き refers to a whirlpool or eddy of water and, in an extended sense, to any spiral shape: 渦巻き模様, 渦巻き状, 渦巻きパン. For whirlpools in the sea, people often simply say 渦 (鳴門の渦潮). The verb 渦巻く means \"to swirl\" and is also used figuratively: 不満が渦巻く \"discontent is seething\". The spelling 渦巻 also occurs alongside 渦巻き.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この店の料理は玄人好みの味だ。","romaji":"Kono mise no ryouri wa kurouto gonomi no aji da.","german":"The food at this restaurant is a taste for connoisseurs.","furigana":"みせ|りょうり|くろうとごの|あじ"},{"japanese":"彼の絵は、素人とは思えない玄人はだしの腕前だ。","romaji":"Kare no e wa, shirouto to wa omoenai kurouto hadashi no udemae da.","german":"His paintings are so skilled that you'd hardly think he's an amateur.","furigana":"かれ|え|しろうと|おも|くろうと|うでまえ"},{"japanese":"これは玄人でも難しい作業です。","romaji":"Kore wa kurouto demo muzukashii sagyou desu.","german":"This is difficult work even for professionals.","furigana":"くろうと|むずか|さぎょう"}],"notes":"玄人 is someone who masters a field professionally and with great skill, a pro or connoisseur. The counterpart is 素人 \"layperson, amateur\". Typical: 玄人はだし \"(an amateur) so good that even professionals are astonished\", 玄人好み \"something for connoisseurs\", 玄人向け. In the past, 玄人 also referred to women of the pleasure trade. In everyday speech, プロ is more common today.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"原稿にコーヒーをこぼしてしまいました。","romaji":"Genkou ni koohii o koboshite shimaimashita.","german":"I spilled coffee on my manuscript.","furigana":"げんこう"},{"japanese":"締め切りまでに原稿が間に合いそうにない。","romaji":"Shimekiri made ni genkou ga maniai sou ni nai.","german":"It doesn't look like my manuscript will be ready by the deadline.","furigana":"し|き|げんこう|ま|あ"},{"japanese":"スピーチの原稿を見ないで話せるように練習した。","romaji":"Supiichi no genkou o minai de hanaseru you ni renshuu shita.","german":"I practiced so that I could give my speech without looking at the script.","furigana":"げんこう|み|はな|れんしゅう"}],"notes":"原稿 is a manuscript or text that is to be published, read aloud or printed: 小説の原稿, 原稿を書く, 原稿の締め切り \"deadline\". The script of a speech or presentation is also called 原稿 (スピーチの原稿, 原稿を読み上げる). The classic Japanese writing paper with squares is called 原稿用紙. The fee for it is called 原稿料 or, in short, 稿料.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"フェイスブックに犬の写真を投稿しました。","romaji":"Feisubukku ni inu no shashin o tōkō shimashita.","german":"I posted photos of my dog on Facebook.","furigana":"いぬ|しゃしん|とうこう"},{"japanese":"不適切な投稿は、管理者によって削除されます。","romaji":"Futekisetsu na tōkō wa, kanrisha ni yotte sakujo saremasu.","german":"Inappropriate posts will be deleted by the administrators.","furigana":"ふてきせつ|とうこう|かんりしゃ|さくじょ"},{"japanese":"祖父は昔、新聞に俳句を投稿していた。","romaji":"Sofu wa mukashi, shinbun ni haiku o tōkō shite ita.","german":"My grandfather used to send haiku to the newspaper.","furigana":"そふ|むかし|しんぶん|はいく|とうこう"}],"notes":"投稿 was originally the sending of a contribution to a newspaper, magazine or radio program (新聞に投稿する, 投稿欄 \"readers' letters column\"); today it mainly means posting on social networks: SNSに写真を投稿する. As a する-verb it is transitive with を, and the destination is marked with に. The noun also refers to the post itself (投稿を削除する \"to delete a post\"). Colloquially, people also say アップする; 投稿 is the neutral standard word, also on buttons in apps.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"わずかですが、稿料をもらいました。","romaji":"Wazuka desu ga, kōryō o moraimashita.","german":"It wasn't much, but I received a fee for my manuscript.","furigana":"こうりょう"},{"japanese":"雑誌に書いたエッセイの稿料が振り込まれた。","romaji":"Zasshi ni kaita essei no kouryou ga furikomareta.","german":"The fee for the essay I wrote for the magazine has been transferred.","furigana":"ざっし|か|こうりょう|ふ|こ"},{"japanese":"若い頃は、稿料だけではとても生活できなかった。","romaji":"Wakai koro wa, kouryou dake de wa totemo seikatsu dekinakatta.","german":"In my younger days, I couldn't live on writing fees alone.","furigana":"わか|ころ|こうりょう|せいかつ"}],"notes":"稿料 is the fee for a published text, such as an article, a column or a novel. The longer form 原稿料 is more common; 稿料 sounds terser and somewhat technical (publishing industry). Payment is often per manuscript page or per character: 一枚あたりの稿料. For royalties from book sales, on the other hand, people say 印税.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"理論上は、南緯45度が南極点から赤道までの距離の中間地点であるとされる。","romaji":"Rironjou wa, nan'i 45-do ga Nankyokuten kara sekidou made no kyori no chuukan chiten de aru to sareru.","german":"In theory, 45 degrees south latitude is said to be the midpoint between the South Pole and the equator.","furigana":"りろんじょう|なんい|ど|なんきょくてん|せきどう|きょり|ちゅうかんちてん"},{"japanese":"シドニーはおよそ南緯34度に位置している。","romaji":"Shidonii wa oyoso nan'i 34-do ni ichi shite iru.","german":"Sydney is located at about 34 degrees south latitude.","furigana":"なんい|ど|いち"},{"japanese":"南緯と北緯を間違えると、地図上で全く違う場所になる。","romaji":"Nan'i to hokui o machigaeru to, chizujou de mattaku chigau basho ni naru.","german":"If you mix up south and north latitude, you end up in a completely different place on the map.","furigana":"なんい|ほくい|まちが|ちずじょう|まった|ちが|ばしょ"}],"notes":"南緯 is south latitude, i.e. how far a place lies south of the equator. Degrees are given with 度: 南緯30度, 南緯35度線. The counterpart is 北緯 \"north latitude\"; lines of longitude are called 東経 and 西経. The general term is 緯度 \"latitude\". Typical of geography, seafaring and the news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"江ノ島とギリシャのクレタ島の北緯は同じです。","romaji":"Enoshima to Girisha no Kuretatō no hokui wa onaji desu.","german":"Enoshima and the Greek island of Crete are at the same north latitude.","furigana":"え|しま|とう|ほくい|おな"},{"japanese":"朝鮮半島は北緯38度線を境に南北に分かれている。","romaji":"Chousen hantou wa hokui 38-do sen o sakai ni nanboku ni wakarete iru.","german":"The Korean Peninsula is divided into north and south along the 38th parallel.","furigana":"ちょうせんはんとう|ほくい|どせん|さかい|なんぼく|わ"},{"japanese":"東京は北緯35度、東経139度あたりにある。","romaji":"Toukyou wa hokui 35-do, toukei 139-do atari ni aru.","german":"Tokyo is located at around 35 degrees north latitude and 139 degrees east longitude.","furigana":"とうきょう|ほくい|ど|とうけい|ど"}],"notes":"北緯 is north latitude, i.e. how far a place lies north of the equator: 北緯35度, 北緯38度線 \"the 38th parallel\" (the dividing line between North and South Korea). The counterpart is 南緯; together with 東経 \"east longitude\" it is used to give positions: 北緯35度、東経139度. Japan lies roughly between 20 and 45 degrees north latitude.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"緯度0度の緯線のことを、赤道と呼びます。","romaji":"Ido zero-do no isen no koto o, sekidou to yobimasu.","german":"The parallel of latitude at 0 degrees is called the equator.","furigana":"いど|ど|いせん|せきどう|よ"},{"japanese":"地図の横の線が緯線で、縦の線が経線です。","romaji":"Chizu no yoko no sen ga isen de, tate no sen ga keisen desu.","german":"The horizontal lines on a map are parallels of latitude, and the vertical lines are meridians.","furigana":"ちず|よこ|せん|いせん|たて|せん|けいせん"},{"japanese":"緯線はすべて赤道と平行に引かれている。","romaji":"Isen wa subete sekidou to heikou ni hikarete iru.","german":"All parallels of latitude are drawn parallel to the equator.","furigana":"いせん|せきどう|へいこう|ひ"}],"notes":"緯線 are the parallels of latitude, i.e. the imaginary lines parallel to the equator on globes and maps. The counterpart is 経線 \"meridians, lines of longitude\"; together they are called 経緯線. Latitude as a numerical value is 緯度. The word belongs to geography and cartography; in everyday speech people more often say 〜度線, e.g. 38度線.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"掃除には、化繊箒を使っています。","romaji":"Sōji ni wa, kasen hōki o tsukatte imasu.","german":"I use a synthetic-fiber broom for sweeping.","furigana":"そうじ|かせんほうき|つか"},{"japanese":"化繊の服は、冬になると静電気が起きやすい。","romaji":"Kasen no fuku wa, fuyu ni naru to seidenki ga okiyasui.","german":"Clothes made of synthetic fiber easily build up static electricity in winter.","furigana":"かせん|ふく|ふゆ|せいでんき|お"},{"japanese":"このシャツは化繊なので、洗ってもすぐ乾きます。","romaji":"Kono shatsu wa kasen na node, aratte mo sugu kawakimasu.","german":"This shirt is synthetic, so it dries right away after washing.","furigana":"かせん|あら|かわ"}],"notes":"化繊 is the short form of 化学繊維 \"chemical fiber\" and covers both fully synthetic fibers (合成繊維, short 合繊, e.g. polyester, nylon) and regenerated fibers such as viscose (レーヨン). The opposite is 天然繊維 such as 綿, 麻, ウール or 絹. In daily life it appears mainly on labels and in expressions like 化繊のシャツ, 化繊混 \"with a synthetic fiber blend\"; typical properties people talk about are 静電気 and quick drying.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"原油安やナフサ安を受けて、合繊の価格も下落しています。","romaji":"Gen'yuyasu ya nafusayasu o ukete, gōsen no kakaku mo geraku shite imasu.","german":"With crude oil and naphtha prices down, the price of synthetic fibers is falling as well.","furigana":"げんゆやす|やす|う|ごうせん|かかく|げらく"},{"japanese":"ポリエステルやナイロンは、代表的な合繊です。","romaji":"Poriesuteru ya nairon wa, daihyouteki na gousen desu.","german":"Polyester and nylon are typical synthetic fibers.","furigana":"だいひょうてき|ごうせん"},{"japanese":"この工場では、衣料用の合繊の糸を生産している。","romaji":"Kono koujou de wa, iryouyou no gousen no ito o seisan shite iru.","german":"This factory produces synthetic yarn for clothing.","furigana":"こうじょう|いりょうよう|ごうせん|いと|せいさん"}],"notes":"合繊 is the short form of 合成繊維 \"synthetic fiber\", i.e. fully synthetic fibers made from petroleum such as ポリエステル, ナイロン or アクリル. It is narrower than 化繊, which also includes regenerated fibers such as viscose. The word belongs more to business news and the technical language of the textile industry (合繊メーカー, 合繊の糸); in everyday speech people mostly say 化繊 or name the material directly.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"母は話を聞きながら、ウンウンと肯きました。","romaji":"Haha wa hanashi o kiki nagara, un'un to unazukimashita.","german":"My mother kept nodding \"uh-huh, uh-huh\" as she listened.","furigana":"はは|はなし|き|うなず"},{"japanese":"先生の説明に、学生たちは大きくうなずいた。","romaji":"Sensei no setsumei ni, gakuseitachi wa ookiku unazuita.","german":"The students nodded emphatically at the teacher's explanation.","furigana":"せんせい|せつめい|がくせい|おお"},{"japanese":"「一緒に行く?」と聞くと、娘は黙ってうなずいた。","romaji":"\"Issho ni iku?\" to kiku to, musume wa damatte unazuita.","german":"When I asked, \"Are you coming along?\", my daughter nodded silently.","furigana":"いっしょ|い|き|むすめ|だま"}],"notes":"うなずく means \"to nod\", usually as a sign of agreement or that one is listening: 黙ってうなずく, 大きくうなずく. The usual kanji spelling is 頷く; 肯く is a variant form, and in everyday writing people mostly use うなずく. The verb is intransitive; what one nods to is marked with に: 彼の意見にうなずく. Shaking the head to say no is 首を横に振る.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"こんなところで胡座をかいてないで、さっさと仕事にとりかかりなさい。","romaji":"Konna tokoro de agura o kaitenaide, sassato shigoto ni torikakari nasai.","german":"Don't sit around cross-legged in a place like this; get started on your work quickly.","furigana":"あぐら|しごと"},{"japanese":"正座がつらかったら、あぐらでもいいですよ。","romaji":"Seiza ga tsurakattara, agura demo ii desu yo.","german":"If sitting formally on your knees is hard, cross-legged is fine too.","furigana":"せいざ"},{"japanese":"人気にあぐらをかいていると、客はすぐに離れていく。","romaji":"Ninki ni agura o kaite iru to, kyaku wa sugu ni hanarete iku.","german":"If you rest on your popularity, customers will quickly drift away.","furigana":"にんき|きゃく|はな"}],"notes":"胡座 is almost always written in kana, あぐら; the kanji are rarely seen. The accompanying verb is かく: あぐらをかく \"to sit cross-legged\". The counterpart is the formal kneeling position 正座; on casual occasions the host often says どうぞ、あぐらで. Figuratively, 〜にあぐらをかく means \"to rest on one's laurels\", e.g. on success or position, without making further effort.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お肉の脂身って苦手なんですよね。","romaji":"O niku no aburami tte nigate na n desu yo ne.","german":"I'm just not good with the fat on meat.","furigana":"にく|あぶらみ|にがて"},{"japanese":"このステーキは脂身が少なくて、さっぱりしている。","romaji":"Kono suteeki wa aburami ga sukunakute, sappari shite iru.","german":"This steak has little fat and tastes light.","furigana":"あぶらみ|すく"},{"japanese":"豚肉の脂身を取り除いてから、炒めてください。","romaji":"Butaniku no aburami o torinozoite kara, itamete kudasai.","german":"Please trim the fat from the pork before stir-frying it.","furigana":"ぶたにく|あぶらみ|と|のぞ|いた"}],"notes":"脂身 is the fatty part of meat, i.e. the fat edge and layers of fat, as opposed to 赤身 \"lean meat\". People say 脂身が多い・少ない, 脂身を取る・取り除く, 脂身が苦手. The kanji 脂 stands for animal or solid fat, 油 for liquid oil; hence also 脂がのった魚 \"fatty, rich fish\". With fish, however, people tend to say トロ or 脂, not 脂身.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この道路はまだ舗装されていない。","romaji":"Kono douro wa mada hosou sarete inai.","german":"This road hasn't been paved yet.","furigana":"どうろ|ほそう"},{"japanese":"来月から、駅前の道路を舗装し直す工事が始まる。","romaji":"Raigetsu kara, ekimae no douro o hosou shinaosu kouji ga hajimaru.","german":"Starting next month, work to repave the road in front of the station will begin.","furigana":"らいげつ|えきまえ|どうろ|ほそう|なお|こうじ|はじ"},{"japanese":"村を出ると、未舗装の山道が続いていた。","romaji":"Mura o deru to, mihosou no yamamichi ga tsuzuite ita.","german":"Beyond the village, an unpaved mountain road continued.","furigana":"むら|で|みほそう|やまみち|つづ"}],"notes":"舗装 refers to surfacing a road or path with asphalt, concrete or paving stones, mostly asphalt. As a transitive する-verb (道路を舗装する), it very often appears in the passive or attributively: 舗装された道, 舗装道路; unpaved roads are called 未舗装の道 or 砂利道. Do not confuse it with the identically sounding 包装 \"packaging\" (ほうそう).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ベビー用品は、いつも赤ちゃん本舗で買います。","romaji":"Bebī yōhin wa, itsumo akachanhonpo de kaimasu.","german":"I always buy baby supplies at Akachan Honpo.","furigana":"ようひん|あか|ほんぽ|か"},{"japanese":"店の看板には「元祖たこ焼き本舗」と大きく書いてある。","romaji":"Mise no kanban ni wa \"Ganso takoyaki honpo\" to ookiku kaite aru.","german":"The shop sign says in large letters \"Original Takoyaki Honpo\".","furigana":"みせ|かんばん|がんそ|や|ほんぽ|おお|か"},{"japanese":"この和菓子は、京都にある本舗でしか買えないそうだ。","romaji":"Kono wagashi wa, Kyouto ni aru honpo de shika kaenai sou da.","german":"I hear this Japanese sweet can only be bought at the main shop in Kyoto.","furigana":"わがし|きょうと|ほんぽ|か"}],"notes":"本舗 is the original shop of a manufacturer or retailer selling a particular product, and appears almost only as part of company and shop names: ○○本舗. It sounds traditional and advertises authenticity, similar to 元祖 \"original\"; for this reason modern chains also like to use the word. In ordinary speech, the main store of a chain is 本店, and a long-established shop is 老舗.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"海軍の艦艇の乗組員が不足しています。","romaji":"Kaigun no kantei no norikumiin ga fusoku shite imasu.","german":"The navy's ships are short of crew.","furigana":"かいぐん|かんてい|のりくみいん|ふそく"},{"japanese":"海上自衛隊の艦艇が、補給のために港に入った。","romaji":"Kaijou jieitai no kantei ga, hokyuu no tame ni minato ni haitta.","german":"A Maritime Self-Defense Force vessel entered port for resupply.","furigana":"かいじょうじえいたい|かんてい|ほきゅう|みなと|はい"},{"japanese":"合同演習には、各国から二十隻以上の艦艇が参加した。","romaji":"Goudou enshuu ni wa, kakkoku kara nijusseki ijou no kantei ga sanka shita.","german":"More than twenty naval vessels from various countries took part in the joint exercise.","furigana":"ごうどうえんしゅう|かっこく|にじゅっせきいじょう|かんてい|さんか"}],"notes":"艦艇 is a collective term for all ships of a navy, from large warships (艦) to small boats (艇), and belongs to the language of news and the military. It is counted with 隻. More specific are 軍艦 \"warship\", 戦艦 \"battleship\", 護衛艦 \"destroyer\" (of the Japanese Self-Defense Forces) and 潜水艦 \"submarine\". Beware of homophones: 首相官邸 \"official residence\" and 鑑定 \"expert appraisal\" are also read かんてい.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"競艇で大儲けしました。","romaji":"Kyōtei de ōmōke shimashita.","german":"I made a big win at the boat races.","furigana":"きょうてい|おおもう"},{"japanese":"週末に競艇場へ行くのが、父の唯一の楽しみだ。","romaji":"Shuumatsu ni kyouteijou e iku no ga, chichi no yuiitsu no tanoshimi da.","german":"Going to the boat racing track on weekends is my father's only pleasure.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|きょうていじょう|い|ちち|ゆいいつ|たの"},{"japanese":"競艇の売り上げの一部は、自治体の財源になっている。","romaji":"Kyoutei no uriage no ichibu wa, jichitai no zaigen ni natte iru.","german":"Part of the revenue from boat racing becomes funding for local governments.","furigana":"きょうてい|う|あ|いちぶ|じちたい|ざいげん"}],"notes":"競艇 is Japanese motorboat racing, on which one may bet under state license; like 競馬 (horse racing), 競輪 (track cycling) and オートレース, it is one of the 公営競技. For some years the official name has been ボートレース, but in everyday speech people still say 競艇. Related are 競艇場 \"racecourse\", 競艇選手 and 舟券 \"betting slip\". People say 競艇をする in the sense of \"to bet on boat races\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この盆栽は苗木から育てたんです。","romaji":"Kono bonsai wa naegi kara sodateta n desu.","german":"I raised this bonsai from a sapling.","furigana":"ぼんさい|なえぎ|そだ"},{"japanese":"子どもが生まれた記念に、庭に桜の苗木を植えた。","romaji":"Kodomo ga umareta kinen ni, niwa ni sakura no naegi o ueta.","german":"To commemorate our child's birth, we planted a cherry sapling in the garden.","furigana":"こ|う|きねん|にわ|さくら|なえぎ|う"},{"japanese":"植樹祭では、参加者が一人一本ずつ苗木を植えました。","romaji":"Shokujusai de wa, sankasha ga hitori ippon zutsu naegi o uemashita.","german":"At the tree-planting festival, each participant planted one sapling.","furigana":"しょくじゅさい|さんかしゃ|ひとりいっぽん|なえぎ|う"}],"notes":"苗木 is a young tree or shrub for planting out, i.e. a \"sapling, young plant\" for woody plants. For vegetables, flowers and rice, people simply say 苗 (トマトの苗, 稲の苗). Typical verbs are 苗木を植える and 苗木を育てる; in spring there are 苗木市 \"sapling markets\" in many places. Someone who grows from seed says 種から育てる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日は夫と苺の苗床を作りました。","romaji":"Kyō wa otto to ichigo no naedoko o tsukurimashita.","german":"Today I made a strawberry seedbed with my husband.","furigana":"きょう|おっと|いちご|なえどこ|つく"},{"japanese":"苗床で育てたトマトの苗を、畑に植え替えた。","romaji":"Naedoko de sodateta tomato no nae o, hatake ni uekaeta.","german":"I transplanted the tomato seedlings grown in the seedbed to the field.","furigana":"なえどこ|そだ|なえ|はたけ|う|か"},{"japanese":"苗床の土が乾かないように、毎朝水をやっている。","romaji":"Naedoko no tsuchi ga kawakanai you ni, maiasa mizu o yatte iru.","german":"I water every morning so the soil in the seedbed doesn't dry out.","furigana":"なえどこ|つち|かわ|まいあさみず"}],"notes":"苗床 is a seedbed in which seedlings are raised before being transplanted (植え替える) to the field or garden bed. So it is not the final vegetable bed, which is called 畑 or 花壇. Figuratively, 苗床 can also mean \"breeding ground, hotbed\"; for negative things such as crime, however, 温床 is the usual expression.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"徳川氏の家紋といえば、「三つ葉葵」が一番有名だ。","romaji":"Tokugawa-shi no kamon to ieba, \"Mitsuba aoi\" ga ichiban yuumei da.","german":"When it comes to the Tokugawa family crest, the \"three-leaf hollyhock\" is the most famous.","furigana":"とくがわし|かもん|み|ばあおい|いちばんゆうめい"},{"japanese":"京都の葵祭では、行列の人々が葵の葉を身につけている。","romaji":"Kyouto no Aoi Matsuri de wa, gyouretsu no hitobito ga aoi no ha o mi ni tsukete iru.","german":"At Kyoto's Aoi Festival, the people in the procession wear hollyhock leaves.","furigana":"きょうと|あおいまつり|ぎょうれつ|ひとびと|あおい|は|み"},{"japanese":"梅雨の頃になると、道端に立葵の花が咲き始める。","romaji":"Tsuyu no koro ni naru to, michibata ni tachiaoi no hana ga sakihajimeru.","german":"Around the rainy season, hollyhocks begin to bloom by the roadside.","furigana":"つゆ|ころ|みちばた|たちあおい|はな|さ|はじ"}],"notes":"葵 refers to plants of the mallow family, in daily life mainly the hollyhock 立葵; in culture, however, the wild ginger フタバアオイ is usually meant, whose heart-shaped leaves appear in the Tokugawa crest (三つ葉葵, 葵の御紋) and at Kyoto's 葵祭. As a single word, 葵 is seen today mainly in these fixed expressions and as a popular given name (あおい). Note: ひまわり \"sunflower\" is written 向日葵, but botanically it does not belong here.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この絵には、蒙古襲来の時の様子が描かれています。","romaji":"Kono e ni wa, Mōko shūrai no toki no yōsu ga egakarete imasu.","german":"This picture shows the scene at the time of the Mongol invasion.","furigana":"え|もうこしゅうらい|とき|ようす|えが"},{"japanese":"赤ちゃんのお尻の青いあざは蒙古斑と呼ばれる。","romaji":"Akachan no oshiri no aoi aza wa mōkohan to yobareru.","german":"The blue mark on a baby's bottom is called a Mongolian spot.","furigana":"あか|しり|あお|もうこはん|よ"},{"japanese":"蒙古の騎馬軍団は、ユーラシア大陸の広い範囲を支配した。","romaji":"Mōko no kiba gundan wa, Yūrashia tairiku no hiroi han'i o shihai shita.","german":"The Mongol cavalry armies ruled a wide area of the Eurasian continent.","furigana":"もうこ|きばぐんだん|たいりく|ひろ|はんい|しはい"}],"notes":"蒙古 is the older designation for Mongolia and the Mongols, adopted from Chinese; the present-day state is called モンゴル (国). The word is alive mainly in historical terms such as 蒙古襲来 \"the Mongol invasions of Japan (1274 and 1281)\", also called 元寇, and in 蒙古斑 \"Mongolian spot\" on babies. Since 蒙 actually means \"ignorant\", the spelling is sometimes perceived as derogatory and replaced by モンゴル in neutral texts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"南京大虐殺が捏造だったというなら、その証拠を説明してください。","romaji":"Nankin dai gyakusatsu ga netsuzō datta to iunara, sono shōko o setsumei shite kudasai.","german":"If you say the Nanjing Massacre was fabricated, please explain the evidence.","furigana":"|ねつぞう|しょうこ|せつめい"},{"japanese":"内戦で、多くの民間人が虐殺された。","romaji":"Naisen de, ooku no minkanjin ga gyakusatsu sareta.","german":"In the civil war, many civilians were massacred.","furigana":"ないせん|おお|みんかんじん|ぎゃくさつ"},{"japanese":"その映画は、ある村で起きた虐殺事件を描いている。","romaji":"Sono eiga wa, aru mura de okita gyakusatsu jiken o egaite iru.","german":"That film depicts a massacre that took place in a village.","furigana":"えいが|むら|お|ぎゃくさつじけん|えが"}],"notes":"虐殺 is the cruel, mostly mass killing of defenseless people, i.e. \"massacre, slaughter\". As a transitive する-verb, it is mostly used in the passive: 住民が虐殺された. Common combinations are 大虐殺, 大量虐殺 (also ジェノサイド) and 虐殺事件. More neutral for individual killings is 殺害, and more elevated and literary is 殺戮. The word belongs to history, politics and the news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"虐待を受けた子供は、自己評価が非常に低くなることが多い。","romaji":"Gyakutai o uketa kodomo wa, jiko hyōka ga hijō ni hikuku naru koto ga ōi.","german":"Children who have been abused often develop very low self-esteem.","furigana":"ぎゃくたい|う|こども|じこひょうか|ひじょう|ひく|おお"},{"japanese":"ペットを虐待するのは犯罪です。","romaji":"Petto o gyakutai suru no wa hanzai desu.","german":"Abusing pets is a crime.","furigana":"ぎゃくたい|はんざい"},{"japanese":"近所の人の通報で、児童虐待が発覚した。","romaji":"Kinjo no hito no tsuuhou de, jidou gyakutai ga hakkaku shita.","german":"A tip from a neighbor brought the child abuse to light.","furigana":"きんじょ|ひと|つうほう|じどうぎゃくたい|はっかく"}],"notes":"虐待 is the continued mistreatment of a weaker person who cannot defend themselves. Typical are 児童虐待, 動物虐待, 高齢者虐待; people say 虐待を受ける \"to be abused\" or, as a transitive する-verb, 子どもを虐待する. To be distinguished from it are いじめ \"bullying\" among equals, DV \"domestic violence\" between partners and パワハラ at the workplace.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これは非常に残虐な犯行です。","romaji":"Kore wa hijō ni zangyaku na hankō desu.","german":"This is an extremely brutal crime.","furigana":"ひじょう|ざんぎゃく|はんこう"},{"japanese":"この映画には残虐な場面が多いので、子どもには見せられない。","romaji":"Kono eiga ni wa zangyaku na bamen ga ooi node, kodomo ni wa miserarenai.","german":"This film has many cruel scenes, so it can't be shown to children.","furigana":"えいが|ざんぎゃく|ばめん|おお|こ|み"},{"japanese":"この資料館は、戦争の残虐さを後世に伝えるために作られた。","romaji":"Kono shiryoukan wa, sensou no zangyakusa o kousei ni tsutaeru tame ni tsukurareta.","german":"This museum was created to convey the brutality of war to later generations.","furigana":"しりょうかん|せんそう|ざんぎゃく|こうせい|つた|つく"}],"notes":"残虐 is a な-adjective for acts that are deliberately cruel and inhuman: 残虐な犯行, 残虐な行為, 残虐な事件; the related noun is 残虐さ or 残虐性. It is narrower and stronger than 残酷, which also applies to a cruel fate or harsh truths (残酷な運命). 残忍 describes more the cold-hearted nature of a person. Register: news and written language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あいつらは暴虐の限りを尽くしたんだ。","romaji":"Aitsura wa bōgyaku no kagiri o tsukushita n da.","german":"Those people committed every atrocity imaginable.","furigana":"ぼうぎゃく|かぎ|つ"},{"japanese":"人々は、王の暴虐に耐えかねて立ち上がった。","romaji":"Hitobito wa, ou no bougyaku ni taekanete tachiagatta.","german":"The people could no longer bear the king's tyranny and rose up.","furigana":"ひとびと|おう|ぼうぎゃく|た|た|あ"},{"japanese":"暴虐な独裁者のもとで、国民は自由を奪われていた。","romaji":"Bougyaku na dokusaisha no moto de, kokumin wa jiyuu o ubawarete ita.","german":"Under the brutal dictator, the people were deprived of freedom.","furigana":"ぼうぎゃく|どくさいしゃ|こくみん|じゆう|うば"}],"notes":"暴虐 refers to rough, cruel tyrannical rule, especially by rulers or occupiers, and can be used as a noun (王の暴虐) or as a な-adjective (暴虐な支配者). The fixed phrase 暴虐の限りを尽くす means \"to commit every conceivable cruelty\". The word is literary and historical; for a domineering boss in daily life, people say 横暴. Related is 残虐, which stresses the cruelty of individual acts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その教諭は、昔の教え子と結婚したらしい。","romaji":"Sono kyouyu wa, mukashi no oshiego to kekkon shita rashii.","german":"That teacher is said to have married a former student.","furigana":"きょうゆ|むかし|おし|ご|けっこん"},{"japanese":"彼女は県立高校で国語の教諭をしている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kenritsu koukou de kokugo no kyouyu o shite iru.","german":"She is a Japanese-language teacher at a prefectural high school.","furigana":"かのじょ|けんりつこうこう|こくご|きょうゆ"},{"japanese":"小学校教諭の免許を取るために、大学で勉強しています。","romaji":"Shougakkou kyouyu no menkyo o toru tame ni, daigaku de benkyou shite imasu.","german":"I'm studying at university to obtain a primary school teaching license.","furigana":"しょうがっこうきょうゆ|めんきょ|と|だいがく|べんきょう"}],"notes":"教諭 is the official job title for licensed teaching staff at schools: 小学校教諭, 高校教諭, 養護教諭 \"school nurse\". It is found in news (男性教諭), documents and résumés, never as a form of address; in conversation people say 先生. More general are 教師 (teacher as a profession) and 教員 (teaching staff, administrative language); at universities, teaching staff are called 教授 or 講師.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は事業で成功し、故郷に錦を飾った。","romaji":"Kare wa jigyō de seikō shi, kokyō ni nishiki o kazatta.","german":"He succeeded in business and returned to his hometown in glory.","furigana":"かれ|じぎょう|せいこう|こきょう|にしき|かざ"},{"japanese":"秋になると、山々が錦のような紅葉に彩られる。","romaji":"Aki ni naru to, yamayama ga nishiki no yō na kōyō ni irodorareru.","german":"In autumn, the mountains are adorned with brocade-like autumn leaves.","furigana":"あき|やまやま|にしき|こうよう|いろど"},{"japanese":"この帯には、金糸で織られた錦が使われている。","romaji":"Kono obi ni wa, kinshi de orareta nishiki ga tsukawarete iru.","german":"Brocade woven with gold thread is used in this obi.","furigana":"おび|きんし|お|にしき|つか"}],"notes":"錦 is a splendid silk fabric patterned with gold and colored threads, i.e. brocade. More common than the fabric itself is the figurative use for something colorfully beautiful: 紅葉の錦 \"brocade of autumn leaves\", 錦鯉 \"koi\", 錦絵 \"color woodblock print\". The fixed phrase 故郷に錦を飾る means \"to return home as a success\", and 錦の御旗 (the imperial brocade banner) stands for an unassailable justification. Elevated, literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"庭の池に錦鯉を放ちました。","romaji":"Niwa no ike ni nishikigoi o hanachimashita.","german":"I released koi into the pond in my garden.","furigana":"にわ|いけ|にしきごい|はな"},{"japanese":"新潟県は、錦鯉の産地として有名です。","romaji":"Niigata-ken wa, nishikigoi no sanchi to shite yuumei desu.","german":"Niigata Prefecture is famous as a koi-producing region.","furigana":"にいがたけん|にしきごい|さんち|ゆうめい"},{"japanese":"錦鯉の中には、一匹数千万円で取引されるものもある。","romaji":"Nishikigoi no naka ni wa, ippiki suusenman'en de torihiki sareru mono mo aru.","german":"Some koi are traded for tens of millions of yen per fish.","furigana":"にしきごい|なか|いっぴきすうせんまんえん|とりひき"}],"notes":"錦鯉 is the koi, the colorfully bred ornamental carp; 錦 means \"brocade\". In biology it is written ニシキゴイ, and colloquially also 色鯉. Well-known varieties are 紅白 and 大正三色; Niigata Prefecture is regarded as the region of origin. Plain 鯉, on the other hand, usually means the dark edible or wild carp (真鯉). They are kept in a pond (池で飼う) and released into it (池に放つ).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この寿司屋は、店内の随所に和風の飾りが置かれています。","romaji":"Kono sushiya wa, tennai no zuisho ni wafuu no kazari ga okarete imasu.","german":"This sushi restaurant has Japanese-style decorations placed throughout the interior.","furigana":"すしや|てんない|ずいしょ|わふう|かざ|お"},{"japanese":"この町には、江戸時代の建物が随所に残っている。","romaji":"Kono machi ni wa, Edo jidai no tatemono ga zuisho ni nokotte iru.","german":"Buildings from the Edo period remain in many places in this town.","furigana":"まち|えどじだい|たてもの|ずいしょ|のこ"},{"japanese":"彼の論文には、随所に独自の工夫が見られる。","romaji":"Kare no ronbun ni wa, zuisho ni dokuji no kufuu ga mirareru.","german":"In his paper, his own original touches can be seen throughout.","furigana":"かれ|ろんぶん|ずいしょ|どくじ|くふう|み"}],"notes":"随所 means \"in many places, scattered everywhere\" and is used almost only adverbially: 随所に・随所で, often with 見られる, ある or 感じられる, and in reference to a whole: 町の随所に, 作品の随所に. It is written language; in conversation people say あちこちに or いたるところに. It usually does not mean \"really everywhere\" but that something turns up at many points within a space or text.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"随分大胆なことをしたんですね。","romaji":"Zuibun daitan na koto o shita n desu ne.","german":"You did something quite bold, didn't you?","furigana":"ずいぶんだいたん"},{"japanese":"日本語がずいぶん上手になりましたね。","romaji":"Nihongo ga zuibun jouzu ni narimashita ne.","german":"Your Japanese has gotten really good!","furigana":"にほんご|じょうず"},{"japanese":"駅までずいぶん歩きました。","romaji":"Eki made zuibun arukimashita.","german":"We walked quite a distance to the station.","furigana":"えき|ある"}],"notes":"随分 expresses that something clearly exceeds what was expected, often with a touch of surprise: ずいぶん寒いですね \"it's pretty cold\", ずいぶん待ちました \"I waited a long time\". It stands before adjectives, verbs and time or quantity expressions; ずいぶんと is equivalent. Compared with とても, ずいぶん sounds more subjective, and said about a person it can be reproachful: ずいぶんですね \"that's rather harsh of you\". Usually written in kana as ずいぶん.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4 (source: N4; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この会社は、圧倒的な強さで、他社の追随を許しませんでした。","romaji":"Kono kaisha wa, attouteki na tsuyosa de, tasha no tsuizui o yurushimasen deshita.","german":"With overwhelming strength, this company let no other firm catch up.","furigana":"かいしゃ|あっとうてき|つよ|たしゃ|ついずい|ゆる"},{"japanese":"大手が値下げすると、中小のメーカーもすぐに追随した。","romaji":"Oote ga nesage suru to, chuushou no meekaa mo sugu ni tsuizui shita.","german":"When the big players cut prices, the small and medium-sized manufacturers immediately followed suit.","furigana":"おおて|ねさ|ちゅうしょう|ついずい"},{"japanese":"政府は、アメリカの方針に追随するだけだと批判されている。","romaji":"Seifu wa, Amerika no houshin ni tsuizui suru dake da to hihan sarete iru.","german":"The government is criticized for merely following America's policy.","furigana":"せいふ|ほうしん|ついずい|ひはん"}],"notes":"追随 means \"to follow someone, to do as they do\", often with the undertone of uncritical imitation: 〜に追随する, 対米追随 \"following the US lead\". Very common is the phrase 他の追随を許さない \"to be unrivaled, to allow no competition\" (in the sense of \"catching up, drawing level\"). It is an intransitive する-verb of written and business language. Similar, but with an undertone of flattery, is 追従.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どうして株価がこんなに高騰しているんだ。","romaji":"Dōshite kabuka ga konnani kōtō shite iru n da.","german":"Why are stock prices surging like this?","furigana":"かぶか|こうとう"},{"japanese":"原油価格の高騰で、ガソリン代が上がった。","romaji":"Gen'yu kakaku no koutou de, gasorindai ga agatta.","german":"Because of the surge in crude oil prices, gasoline costs have gone up.","furigana":"げんゆかかく|こうとう|だい|あ"},{"japanese":"野菜の値段が高騰して、家計が苦しい。","romaji":"Yasai no nedan ga koutou shite, kakei ga kurushii.","german":"Vegetable prices have soared, and the household budget is tight.","furigana":"やさい|ねだん|こうとう|かけい|くる"}],"notes":"高騰 is a sharp rise in prices and is used as an intransitive する-verb with prices, rates and costs as the subject: 物価・地価・原油価格が高騰する, 〜の高騰が続く. The word says nothing about speed; for a sudden jump use 急騰, and for an extreme rise that gets out of control use 暴騰. The opposite is 下落 or 暴落. Homophones are 口頭 \"oral\" and 高等 \"higher\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"住宅価格が暴騰している。","romaji":"Jūtaku kakaku ga bōtō shite iru.","german":"Housing prices are skyrocketing.","furigana":"じゅうたくかかく|ぼうとう"},{"japanese":"買収のうわさが流れ、その会社の株価は一日で暴騰した。","romaji":"Baishuu no uwasa ga nagare, sono kaisha no kabuka wa ichinichi de boutou shita.","german":"After rumors of a takeover spread, the company's stock price skyrocketed in a single day.","furigana":"ばいしゅう|なが|かいしゃ|かぶか|いちにち|ぼうとう"},{"japanese":"不作の年には、米の値段が暴騰したこともあった。","romaji":"Fusaku no toshi ni wa, kome no nedan ga boutou shita koto mo atta.","german":"In years of poor harvest, the price of rice has also skyrocketed.","furigana":"ふさく|とし|こめ|ねだん|ぼうとう"}],"notes":"暴騰 is the strongest of the words for price rises: an extreme, abrupt rise felt to be out of control. Like 高騰 and 急騰, it is an intransitive する-verb with prices as the subject (株価が暴騰する). It belongs mainly in stock market and business reports. The opposite 暴落 \"price crash\" is much more common than 暴騰 itself.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ドルが再び急騰しています。","romaji":"Doru ga futatabi kyūtō shite imasu.","german":"The dollar is surging again.","furigana":"ふたた|きゅうとう"},{"japanese":"新製品の発表を受けて、その会社の株価が急騰した。","romaji":"Shinseihin no happyou o ukete, sono kaisha no kabuka ga kyuutou shita.","german":"Following the announcement of a new product, the company's stock price surged.","furigana":"しんせいひん|はっぴょう|う|かいしゃ|かぶか|きゅうとう"},{"japanese":"地震のニュースが流れると、金の価格が急騰した。","romaji":"Jishin no nyuusu ga nagareru to, kin no kakaku ga kyuutou shita.","german":"When the news of the earthquake broke, the price of gold surged.","furigana":"じしん|なが|きん|かかく|きゅうとう"}],"notes":"急騰 is a sudden, steep rise in prices or rates within a short time; it is an intransitive する-verb (株価が急騰する) and the counterpart of 急落 \"sudden plunge\". Unlike 高騰, which refers to a high rise in general, 急騰 stresses the speed; 暴騰 is even more extreme. Typical of stock market and foreign exchange reports.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ニシキゴイは緋鯉をもとに改良されたものです。","romaji":"Nishikigoi wa higoi o moto ni kairyou sareta mono desu.","german":"The nishikigoi was bred from the red carp.","furigana":"ひごい|かいりょう"},{"japanese":"こいのぼりの歌では、大きい真鯉がお父さんで、小さい緋鯉が子どもたちだ。","romaji":"Koinobori no uta de wa, ookii magoi ga otousan de, chiisai higoi ga kodomotachi da.","german":"In the carp streamer song, the big black carp is the father and the small red carps are the children.","furigana":"うた|おお|まごい|とう|ちい|ひごい|こ"},{"japanese":"お寺の池で、緋鯉がゆっくり泳いでいた。","romaji":"Otera no ike de, higoi ga yukkuri oyoide ita.","german":"In the temple pond, red carp were swimming leisurely.","furigana":"てら|いけ|ひごい|およ"}],"notes":"緋鯉 is a red to golden-colored carp; 緋 means \"scarlet\" (as in 緋色), and 鯉 becomes ごい through rendaku. It is regarded as a forerunner of the colorful 錦鯉. It is best known from the children's song \"こいのぼり\": the big black 真鯉 is the father, and the small 緋鯉 are the children; on carp streamers, the red carp therefore hangs below the black one.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"真鯉の刺青を背中に彫りました。","romaji":"Magoi no shisei o senaka ni horimashita.","german":"I had a tattoo of a black carp done on my back.","furigana":"まごい|しせい|せなか|ほ"},{"japanese":"こいのぼりの一番上の大きな黒い鯉を、真鯉という。","romaji":"Koinobori no ichiban ue no ookina kuroi koi o, magoi to iu.","german":"The big black carp at the very top of the carp streamers is called magoi.","furigana":"いちばんうえ|おお|くろ|こい|まごい"},{"japanese":"川で釣れたのは、色の黒い真鯉だった。","romaji":"Kawa de tsureta no wa, iro no kuroi magoi datta.","german":"What I caught in the river was a dark-colored magoi.","furigana":"かわ|つ|いろ|くろ|まごい"}],"notes":"真鯉 is the ordinary, dark-colored carp, as opposed to the colored 緋鯉 and 錦鯉; 真 here means \"genuine, original\", and 鯉 becomes ごい through rendaku. The word is best known from the carp streamers for Children's Day: the topmost big black carp is the 真鯉 and stands for the father. It does not mean the Chinese \"black amur\", which is called アオウオ in Japanese.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"王は、自慢の駿馬に乗って姿を現した。","romaji":"Ō wa, jiman no shunme ni notte sugata o arawashita.","german":"The king appeared riding his prized steed.","furigana":"おう|じまん|しゅんめ|の|すがた|あらわ"},{"japanese":"草原を駆ける駿馬の姿は、本当に美しかった。","romaji":"Sougen o kakeru shunme no sugata wa, hontou ni utsukushikatta.","german":"The sight of the fine horse galloping across the grassland was truly beautiful.","furigana":"そうげん|か|しゅんめ|すがた|ほんとう|うつく"},{"japanese":"その武将は、一日に千里を走るという駿馬を手に入れた。","romaji":"Sono bushou wa, ichinichi ni senri o hashiru to iu shunme o te ni ireta.","german":"The warlord obtained a steed said to run a thousand ri a day.","furigana":"ぶしょう|いちにち|せんり|はし|しゅんめ|て|い"}],"notes":"駿馬 is a swift, noble horse and belongs to literary, historical or poetic language; besides しゅんめ, the reading しゅんば is also used. In daily life people say 速い馬 or 名馬 \"famous, excellent horse\". The opposite word is 駑馬 \"nag\". The character 駿 also appears in given names (駿) and, in the sense of \"outstanding\", in 俊才.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このボールは軟らかいから、室内でも使える。","romaji":"Kono bōru wa yawarakai kara, shitsunai demo tsukaeru.","german":"This ball is soft, so it can be used indoors too.","furigana":"やわ|しつない|つか"},{"japanese":"雨のあとで、グラウンドの土が軟らかくなっている。","romaji":"Ame no ato de, guraundo no tsuchi ga yawarakaku natte iru.","german":"After the rain, the soil on the playing field has become soft.","furigana":"あめ|つち|やわ"},{"japanese":"祖母のために、ご飯を少し軟らかめに炊いた。","romaji":"Sobo no tame ni, gohan o sukoshi yawarakame ni taita.","german":"For my grandmother, I cooked the rice a bit softer.","furigana":"そぼ|はん|すこ|やわ|た"}],"notes":"軟らかい is a spelling variant of やわらかい that, following the official recommendation, is chosen for the opposite of 硬い \"hard, firm\": 軟らかい土, 軟らかいご飯, 軟らかいボール. For softness in the sense of supple, gentle or flexible, one writes 柔らかい (柔らかい毛布, 柔らかい表情, 頭が柔らかい). In everyday life, 柔らかい is overall much more common; the character 軟 is known from 軟式, 軟水 and 柔軟.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 36 (source: N4; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"風呂場で滑ってお尻の骨を折りました。","romaji":"Furoba de subette oshiri no hone o orimashita.","german":"Ich bin im Badezimmer ausgerutscht und habe mir das Steißbein gebrochen.","furigana":"ふろば|すべ|しり|ほね|お"},{"japanese":"風呂場のカビを取るのに一時間もかかった。","romaji":"Furoba no kabi o toru no ni ichijikan mo kakatta.","german":"Ich habe eine ganze Stunde gebraucht, um den Schimmel im Bad zu entfernen.","furigana":"ふろば|と|いちじかん"},{"japanese":"冬は脱衣所と風呂場の温度差に気をつけてください。","romaji":"Fuyu wa datsuijo to furoba no ondosa ni ki o tsukete kudasai.","german":"Achten Sie im Winter auf den Temperaturunterschied zwischen Umkleide und Bad.","furigana":"ふゆ|だついじょ|ふろば|おんどさ|き"}],"notes":"風呂場 ist der Raum, in dem man badet, mit Wanne und Waschbereich davor; anders als im deutschen „Badezimmer“ ist die Toilette in japanischen Wohnungen meist ein eigener Raum (トイレ). Neutral-alltäglich; förmlicher bzw. in Immobilienanzeigen heißt es 浴室, umgangssprachlich oft einfach お風呂. Davor liegt meist der Umkleide- und Waschraum 脱衣所. Typisch: 風呂場で滑る, 風呂場を掃除する.","levelNote":"Compound: 風呂 (N5) + 場 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"風呂屋に来るのは初めてです。","romaji":"Furoya ni kuru no wa hajimete desu.","german":"It's my first time at a public bathhouse.","furigana":"ふろや|く|はじ"},{"japanese":"子どもの頃は家に風呂がなかったので、毎晩風呂屋に通っていた。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro wa ie ni furo ga nakatta node, maiban furoya ni kayotte ita.","german":"We had no bath at home when I was a kid, so I went to the bathhouse every evening.","furigana":"こ|ころ|いえ|ふろ|まいばんふろや|かよ"},{"japanese":"最近は、町の風呂屋がどんどん減っている。","romaji":"Saikin wa, machi no furoya ga dondon hette iru.","german":"Recently, the neighborhood bathhouses are disappearing one after another.","furigana":"さいきん|まち|ふろや|へ"}],"notes":"風呂屋 is the colloquial, somewhat old-fashioned term for a public bathhouse; the neutral word is 銭湯. People say 風呂屋に行く or 風呂屋に通う. It is set apart from 温泉 (hot spring) and modern スーパー銭湯 by its simple neighborhood atmosphere. JMdict also lists the meaning \"bathhouse operator\", which is hardly used anymore.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"医療倫理学の講座を受講しています。","romaji":"Iryou rinrigaku no kouza o jukou shite imasu.","german":"I'm taking a course on medical ethics.","furigana":"いりょうりんりがく|こうざ|じゅこう"},{"japanese":"大学では、哲学科で倫理学を専攻していました。","romaji":"Daigaku de wa, tetsugakuka de rinrigaku o senkou shite imashita.","german":"At university, I majored in ethics in the philosophy department.","furigana":"だいがく|てつがくか|りんりがく|せんこう"},{"japanese":"カントは、倫理学の歴史において非常に重要な哲学者だ。","romaji":"Kanto wa, rinrigaku no rekishi ni oite hijou ni juuyou na tetsugakusha da.","german":"Kant is an extremely important philosopher in the history of ethics.","furigana":"りんりがく|れきし|ひじょう|じゅうよう|てつがくしゃ"}],"notes":"倫理学 is ethics as an academic discipline, a branch of philosophy (哲学). To be distinguished from it are 倫理, morality or ethical norms themselves (企業倫理, 倫理観), and 道徳, everyday morality (also a school subject). Typical are 倫理学を専攻する, 倫理学の講義 and hyphenated ethics such as 生命倫理学 \"bioethics\" and 応用倫理学 \"applied ethics\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"うちの会社は、世界で最も倫理的な企業の一つに選ばれました。","romaji":"Uchi no kaisha wa, sekai de mottomo rinriteki na kigyou no hitotsu ni erabaremashita.","german":"Our company was chosen as one of the most ethical companies in the world.","furigana":"かいしゃ|せかい|もっと|りんりてき|きぎょう|ひと|えら"},{"japanese":"動物実験には、倫理的な問題があると考える人も多い。","romaji":"Doubutsu jikken ni wa, rinriteki na mondai ga aru to kangaeru hito mo ooi.","german":"Many people think animal testing raises ethical problems.","furigana":"どうぶつじっけん|りんりてき|もんだい|かんが|ひと|おお"},{"japanese":"法律的には問題なくても、倫理的には許されない行為だ。","romaji":"Houritsuteki ni wa mondai nakute mo, rinriteki ni wa yurusarenai koui da.","german":"Even if it's not a problem legally, it's an act that is ethically unacceptable.","furigana":"ほうりつてき|もんだい|りんりてき|ゆる|こうい"}],"notes":"倫理的 is a な-adjective meaning \"ethical, morally acceptable\": 倫理的な問題, 倫理的な判断, adverbially 倫理的に問題がある. It is often contrasted with the legal: 法的には問題ないが、倫理的には.... 道徳的 emphasizes personal morality in everyday life more; the opposite is 非倫理的 \"unethical\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あなたって、考え方にすごく偏りがあるよね。","romaji":"Anata tte, kangaekata ni sugoku katayori ga aru yo ne.","german":"You have a really biased way of thinking, you know.","furigana":"かんが|かた|かたよ"},{"japanese":"栄養の偏りが気になるので、野菜を多めに食べるようにしている。","romaji":"Eiyou no katayori ga ki ni naru node, yasai o oome ni taberu you ni shite iru.","german":"I'm worried about an unbalanced diet, so I try to eat extra vegetables.","furigana":"えいよう|かたよ|き|やさい|おお|た"},{"japanese":"回答者の年齢に偏りがあるため、この結果は参考程度にしてください。","romaji":"Kaitousha no nenrei ni katayori ga aru tame, kono kekka wa sankou teido ni shite kudasai.","german":"Because the respondents' ages are skewed, please treat this result as a rough guide only.","furigana":"かいとうしゃ|ねんれい|かたよ|けっか|さんこうていど"}],"notes":"偏り is the noun for the intransitive verb 偏る \"to be one-sided, to lean to one side\" and refers to any kind of skew or imbalance: 栄養の偏り, 考え方の偏り, データの偏り. Typical frames are 〜に偏りがある・偏りが見られる and 偏りなく \"evenly\". For prejudice against people, 偏見 is the usual word, and for one-sided reporting 偏向.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この冠を被れるのは王だけです。","romaji":"Kono kanmuri o kabureru no wa ō dake desu.","german":"Only the king may wear this crown.","furigana":"かんむり|かぶ|おう"},{"japanese":"「花」の上の部分は「草冠」と呼ばれます。","romaji":"\"Hana\" no ue no bubun wa \"kusakanmuri\" to yobaremasu.","german":"The top part of 「花」 is called the \"grass radical\".","furigana":"はな|うえ|ぶぶん|くさかんむり|よ"},{"japanese":"約束をすっぽかしたので、彼女はすっかりお冠だ。","romaji":"Yakusoku o suppokashita node, kanojo wa sukkari okanmuri da.","german":"Because I stood her up, she is thoroughly annoyed.","furigana":"やくそく|かのじょ|かんむり"}],"notes":"冠 is primarily the crown or the traditional headgear of court officials and Shintō priests; for a royal crown, 王冠 is the more common word. In addition, 冠 denotes the kanji component at the top: 草冠 (くさかんむり), うかんむり. Colloquially, お冠 means \"annoyed, in a bad mood\". In the media, 冠 also denotes the sponsor's name in the title of a program or tournament (冠番組, 冠大会).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は恋をして王冠を捨てたんだ。","romaji":"Kare wa koi o shite ōkan o suteta n da.","german":"He fell in love and gave up the crown.","furigana":"かれ|こい|おうかん|す"},{"japanese":"戴冠式で、新しい国王の頭に王冠が載せられた。","romaji":"Taikanshiki de, atarashii kokuou no atama ni oukan ga noserareta.","german":"At the coronation, the crown was placed on the new king's head.","furigana":"たいかんしき|あたら|こくおう|あたま|おうかん|の"},{"japanese":"子どもの頃、ジュースの瓶の王冠を集めていた。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, juusu no bin no oukan o atsumete ita.","german":"As a child, I collected the bottle caps from juice bottles.","furigana":"こ|ころ|びん|おうかん|あつ"}],"notes":"王冠 is the crown of a king or queen, also as a symbol of royal dignity: 王冠を戴く, 王冠を捨てる \"to renounce the throne\". A second, everyday meaning is the crown cap of a bottle (ビールの王冠). More general and also used for court headwear is 冠; for victory in competitions people tend to speak of タイトル or 三冠 \"Triple Crown\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"攻守ともに殊勲を立てました。","romaji":"Koushu tomo ni shukun o tatemashita.","german":"He distinguished himself in both offense and defense.","furigana":"こうしゅ|しゅくん|た"},{"japanese":"横綱を倒した力士に、殊勲賞が贈られた。","romaji":"Yokozuna o taoshita rikishi ni, shukunshou ga okurareta.","german":"The wrestler who defeated the yokozuna was awarded the Outstanding Performance Prize.","furigana":"よこづな|たお|りきし|しゅくんしょう|おく"},{"japanese":"九回に逆転の殊勲打を放ったのは、新人選手だった。","romaji":"Kyuukai ni gyakuten no shukunda o hanatta no wa, shinjin senshu datta.","german":"The rookie hit the game-turning decisive hit in the ninth inning.","furigana":"きゅうかい|ぎゃくてん|しゅくんだ|はな|しんじんせんしゅ"}],"notes":"殊勲 is an outstanding achievement or special merit, originally in war, today mainly in sports. Fixed combinations are 殊勲を立てる \"to distinguish oneself by special service\", 殊勲打 \"decisive hit\" in baseball, and 殊勲賞, the sumō prize for victories over yokozuna or ōzeki. More general is 功績 \"merits\", colloquially 手柄.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どうして勲章を辞退したんですか。","romaji":"Doushite kunshou o jitai shita n desu ka.","german":"Why did you decline the decoration?","furigana":"くんしょう|じたい"},{"japanese":"祖父は、長年の功績が認められて勲章を受けた。","romaji":"Sofu wa, naganen no kouseki ga mitomerarete kunshou o uketa.","german":"My grandfather received a decoration in recognition of his many years of service.","furigana":"そふ|ながねん|こうせき|みと|くんしょう|う"},{"japanese":"この傷は、試合で頑張った名誉の勲章だよ。","romaji":"Kono kizu wa, shiai de ganbatta meiyo no kunshou da yo.","german":"This scar is my badge of honor from the match where I gave it my all.","furigana":"きず|しあい|がんば|めいよ|くんしょう"}],"notes":"勲章 is a state decoration for merit, such as the 文化勲章 \"Order of Culture\". People say 勲章を授与する \"to confer\" and 勲章を受ける; receiving it is 受章 (not 受賞, which is for prizes), and the conferral as a whole is 叙勲. Figuratively, a scar or trace of hard work is jokingly called 名誉の勲章 \"badge of honor\". Sports medals, by contrast, are called メダル.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"うちにもその商品を卸してもらえませんか。","romaji":"Uchi ni mo sono shōhin o oroshite moraemasen ka.","german":"Could you also supply those goods to us wholesale?","furigana":"しょうひん|おろ"},{"japanese":"この農家は、地元のスーパーに野菜を卸している。","romaji":"Kono nouka wa, jimoto no suupaa ni yasai o oroshite iru.","german":"This farm supplies vegetables wholesale to the local supermarkets.","furigana":"のうか|じもと|やさい|おろ"},{"japanese":"魚を三枚に卸すのは、思ったより難しい。","romaji":"Sakana o sanmai ni orosu no wa, omotta yori muzukashii.","german":"Filleting a fish into three pieces is harder than I thought.","furigana":"さかな|さんまい|おろ|おも|むずか"}],"notes":"卸す means above all \"to sell wholesale to retailers\": 小売店に商品を卸す; related are 卸売り, 卸問屋 and 卸値. In the kitchen, おろす also means \"to grate\" (大根をおろす) and \"to fillet a fish\" (三枚におろす); for these one usually writes kana or 下ろす. Not to be confused with 下ろす \"to take down, to withdraw money\" and 降ろす \"to let off\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"それは卸値で購入することができたんです。","romaji":"Sore wa oroshine de kōnyū suru koto ga dekita n desu.","german":"I was able to buy it at the wholesale price.","furigana":"おろしね|こうにゅう"},{"japanese":"野菜の卸値が上がり、スーパーの店頭価格も上がった。","romaji":"Yasai no oroshine ga agari, suupaa no tentou kakaku mo agatta.","german":"Wholesale vegetable prices rose, and the prices in supermarkets went up too.","furigana":"やさい|おろしね|あ|てんとうかかく|あ"},{"japanese":"小売価格は、卸値の二倍近くになることもある。","romaji":"Kouri kakaku wa, oroshine no nibai chikaku ni naru koto mo aru.","german":"The retail price can be nearly double the wholesale price.","furigana":"こうりかかく|おろしね|にばいちか"}],"notes":"卸値 is the wholesale price, i.e. the price at which wholesalers sell to retailers. The contrasting terms are 小売価格 \"retail price\" and 定価 \"list price\"; from the retailer's point of view, the purchase price is 仕入れ値 and the manufacturing cost is 原価. Typical: 卸値で買う, 卸値が上がる・下がる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"電子書籍で自叙伝を販売しようと思っています。","romaji":"Denshi shoseki de jijoden o hanbai shiyō to omotte imasu.","german":"I'm thinking of selling my autobiography as an e-book.","furigana":"でんししょせき|じじょでん|はんばい|おも"},{"japanese":"引退した俳優が、自叙伝を出版した。","romaji":"Intai shita haiyuu ga, jijoden o shuppan shita.","german":"A retired actor published his autobiography.","furigana":"いんたい|はいゆう|じじょでん|しゅっぱん"},{"japanese":"彼の自叙伝を読むと、苦労の多い人生だったことが分かる。","romaji":"Kare no jijoden o yomu to, kurou no ooi jinsei datta koto ga wakaru.","german":"Reading his autobiography, you can tell his life was full of hardship.","furigana":"かれ|じじょでん|よ|くろう|おお|じんせい|わ"}],"notes":"自叙伝 is an autobiography, i.e. one's own life story; the shorter 自伝 means the same and is more common in daily life. If someone else's life is described, it is 伝記, and for memories of particular periods people say 回顧録 \"memoirs\". Typical: 自叙伝を書く・出版する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"学生の頃は、いつも阿呆なことばかりしてたな。","romaji":"Gakusei no koro wa, itsumo aho na koto bakari shiteta na.","german":"When I was a student, I was always doing stupid things.","furigana":"がくせい|ころ|あほ"},{"japanese":"そんなあほなこと、あるわけないやろ。","romaji":"Sonna aho na koto, aru wake nai yaro.","german":"There's no way something that silly could happen!"},{"japanese":"財布を家に忘れるなんて、ほんまにあほやなあ。","romaji":"Saifu o ie ni wasureru nante, honma ni aho ya naa.","german":"Forgetting your wallet at home, you really are a fool.","furigana":"さいふ|いえ|わす"}],"notes":"阿呆 \"fool, idiot; stupid\" is practically always written in kana, あほ or アホ; the full reading あほう sounds old-fashioned. It is typical of the Kansai region, where アホ comes across as teasing and friendly, while バカ sounds harsher there; in Tokyo it is the reverse. As a noun and な-adjective: アホなこと, アホみたい. Toward strangers it is rude and should be avoided.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの芸人は呆けるのが本当にうまい。","romaji":"Ano geinin wa bokeru no ga hontou ni umai.","german":"That comedian is really good at playing the fool.","furigana":"げいにん|ぼ|ほんとう"},{"japanese":"最近、祖父が少し呆けてきたようで心配だ。","romaji":"Saikin, sofu ga sukoshi bokete kita you de shinpai da.","german":"Recently my grandfather seems to be getting a bit forgetful, and I'm worried.","furigana":"さいきん|そふ|すこ|ぼ|しんぱい"},{"japanese":"寝不足で頭が呆けているのか、同じミスばかりしてしまう。","romaji":"Nebusoku de atama ga bokete iru no ka, onaji misu bakari shite shimau.","german":"Maybe my head is foggy from lack of sleep; I keep making the same mistake.","furigana":"ねぶそく|あたま|ぼ|おな"}],"notes":"呆ける is mostly written in kana, ぼける, and is intransitive: \"to become forgetful or senile with age\", \"to be mentally absent\" and, in a manzai comedy duo, \"to play the fool\"; this role is called ボケ, and its counterpart ツッコミ. For dementia, ぼける is now regarded as slangy and insensitive; the neutral phrase is 認知症になる. Note: in 遊び呆ける, 呆ける is read ほうける, and for blurry photos ぼける is written with 暈ける.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼が行っていた不正が、すべて露呈した。","romaji":"Kare ga okonatte ita fusei ga, subete rotei shita.","german":"All the wrongdoing he had been committing came to light.","furigana":"かれ|おこな|ふせい|ろてい"},{"japanese":"今回の災害で、町の防災体制の弱さが露呈した。","romaji":"Konkai no saigai de, machi no bousai taisei no yowasa ga rotei shita.","german":"This disaster exposed the weakness of the town's disaster-prevention system.","furigana":"こんかい|さいがい|まち|ぼうさいたいせい|よわ|ろてい"},{"japanese":"決勝戦で、チームは経験不足を露呈してしまった。","romaji":"Kesshousen de, chiimu wa keiken busoku o rotei shite shimatta.","german":"In the final, the team ended up exposing its lack of experience.","furigana":"けっしょうせん|けいけんぶそく|ろてい"}],"notes":"露呈 means that something hidden, mostly negative, comes out into the open: 弱点・問題点・矛盾・経験不足が露呈する. It is mainly intransitive, but can also be transitive with the party concerned as the subject: チームは弱さを露呈した \"the team revealed its weakness\". Unlike 暴露, where someone deliberately reveals something, 露呈 happens more by itself; 発覚 stresses the discovery of misdeeds. Colloquially people say バレる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"訪問者の多いブログには、Tポイントが進呈されます。","romaji":"Houmonsha no ooi burogu ni wa, T pointo ga shintei saremasu.","german":"T-Points will be presented to blogs with many visitors.","furigana":"ほうもんしゃ|おお|しんてい"},{"japanese":"アンケートにお答えいただいた方には、もれなく粗品を進呈いたします。","romaji":"Ankeeto ni okotae itadaita kata ni wa, morenaku soshina o shintei itashimasu.","german":"Everyone who answers the survey will receive a small gift.","furigana":"こた|かた|そしな|しんてい"},{"japanese":"出版した本を、お世話になった先生に一冊進呈した。","romaji":"Shuppan shita hon o, osewa ni natta sensei ni issatsu shintei shita.","german":"I presented one copy of my published book to the teacher who had helped me.","furigana":"しゅっぱん|ほん|せわ|せんせい|いっさつしんてい"}],"notes":"進呈 is the polite, slightly humble \"presenting\" of a gift from the giver's point of view. Today it appears mainly in advertising and campaigns: もれなく粗品を進呈, プレゼント進呈, ポイント進呈. More solemn and used for public ceremonies is 贈呈; in conversation people simply say 差し上げる or プレゼントする.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"主役を演じた友人に花束を贈呈しました。","romaji":"Shuyaku o enjita yūjin ni hanataba o zōtei shimashita.","german":"I presented a bouquet to my friend who played the lead role.","furigana":"しゅやく|えん|ゆうじん|はなたば|ぞうてい"},{"japanese":"優勝チームには、トロフィーと賞金が贈呈された。","romaji":"Yuushou chiimu ni wa, torofii to shoukin ga zoutei sareta.","german":"The winning team was presented with a trophy and prize money.","furigana":"ゆうしょう|しょうきん|ぞうてい"},{"japanese":"卒業式で、卒業生から学校に記念品が贈呈された。","romaji":"Sotsugyoushiki de, sotsugyousei kara gakkou ni kinenhin ga zoutei sareta.","german":"At the graduation ceremony, the graduates presented a commemorative gift to the school.","furigana":"そつぎょうしき|そつぎょうせい|がっこう|きねんひん|ぞうてい"}],"notes":"贈呈 is the formal presentation of a gift, prize or souvenir, typically at ceremonies: 花束贈呈, 記念品を贈呈する, 贈呈式. Compared with 進呈, it is more official and public. For donations to institutions people say 寄贈 (図書館に本を寄贈する), and for conferral by an authority 授与 (賞・学位を授与する).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"乳児哺育の方法は大きく変化しています。","romaji":"Nyūji hoiku no hōhō wa ōkiku henka shite imasu.","german":"Methods of infant feeding and care are changing greatly.","furigana":"にゅうじほいく|ほうほう|おお|へんか"},{"japanese":"パンダの母親は、生まれた子を一頭ずつ大切に哺育する。","romaji":"Panda no hahaoya wa, umareta ko o ittou zutsu taisetsu ni hoiku suru.","german":"A panda mother carefully raises her newborn cubs one at a time.","furigana":"ははおや|う|こ|いっとう|たいせつ|ほいく"},{"japanese":"母親が育児を放棄したため、子ゾウは飼育員の手で人工哺育された。","romaji":"Hahaoya ga ikuji o houki shita tame, kozou wa shiikuin no te de jinkou hoiku sareta.","german":"Because the mother abandoned its care, the baby elephant was hand-reared by the keepers.","furigana":"ははおや|いくじ|ほうき|こ|しいくいん|て|じんこうほいく"}],"notes":"哺育 is the suckling and rearing of young, especially in animals, and belongs to the language of biology, zoos and specialist literature: 子を哺育する, 人工哺育 \"bottle-rearing\". The identically sounding 保育 \"childcare\" (保育園) is a different word; human breastfeeding is 授乳, and child-rearing in general is 育児. 哺 is known from 哺乳類 \"mammals\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今までためていた怒りが一気に噴き出した。","romaji":"Ima made tamete ita ikari ga ikkini fukidashita.","german":"The anger I had been bottling up burst out all at once.","furigana":"いま|いか|いっき|ふ|だ"},{"japanese":"火口から、真っ赤な溶岩が噴き出している。","romaji":"Kakou kara, makka na yougan ga fukidashite iru.","german":"Bright red lava is spewing from the crater.","furigana":"かこう|ま|か|ようがん|ふ|だ"},{"japanese":"外に出たとたん、全身から汗が噴き出した。","romaji":"Soto ni deta totan, zenshin kara ase ga fukidashita.","german":"The moment I stepped outside, sweat poured out of my whole body.","furigana":"そと|で|ぜんしん|あせ|ふ|だ"}],"notes":"噴き出す is intransitive and describes liquid, gas or lava shooting out under pressure: 温泉・溶岩・汗が噴き出す; figuratively also pent-up feelings or problems: 不満が噴き出す. For \"to burst out laughing\" it is usually written 吹き出す, and 吹き出す is often used as a variant in other cases too. The base verb is 噴く \"to spew, to spray\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は孤児院で幼少期を過ごしました。","romaji":"Watashi wa kojiin de yōshōki o sugoshimashita.","german":"I spent my early childhood in an orphanage.","furigana":"わたし|こじいん|ようしょうき|す"},{"japanese":"戦後、多くの戦災孤児が孤児院に引き取られた。","romaji":"Sengo, ooku no sensai koji ga kojiin ni hikitorareta.","german":"After the war, many war orphans were taken in by orphanages.","furigana":"せんご|おお|せんさいこじ|こじいん|ひ|と"},{"japanese":"その小説の主人公は、孤児院で育った少女だ。","romaji":"Sono shousetsu no shujinkou wa, kojiin de sodatta shoujo da.","german":"The protagonist of the novel is a girl who grew up in an orphanage.","furigana":"しょうせつ|しゅじんこう|こじいん|そだ|しょうじょ"}],"notes":"孤児院 \"orphanage\" is an older term; in Japan, such facilities have officially been called 児童養護施設 since 1947 and also take in children whose parents are still alive. Today 孤児院 is used mainly in historical contexts, for facilities abroad or in novels. Related are 孤児 \"orphan\" and 戦災孤児 \"war orphans\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"昔々あるところに、水飴が大好きな和尚がおりました。","romaji":"Mukashimukashi aru tokoro ni, mizuame ga daisuki na oshō ga orimashita.","german":"Once upon a time, there was a priest who loved mizuame (malt syrup).","furigana":"むかしむかし|みずあめ|だいす|おしょう"},{"japanese":"法事では、お寺の和尚さんにお経をあげてもらった。","romaji":"Houji de wa, otera no oshou-san ni okyou o agete moratta.","german":"At the memorial service, the temple priest chanted the sutras for us.","furigana":"ほうじ|てら|おしょう|きょう"},{"japanese":"とんち話で有名な一休さんは、室町時代の和尚だ。","romaji":"Tonchibanashi de yuumei na Ikkyuu-san wa, Muromachi jidai no oshou da.","german":"Ikkyū-san, famous for his witty tales, was a priest of the Muromachi period.","furigana":"ばなし|ゆうめい|いっきゅう|むろまちじだい|おしょう"}],"notes":"和尚 is a Buddhist monk or priest, especially the head of a temple; in daily life people say respectfully 和尚さん or お坊さん. More official for the temple head is 住職, and for clergy in general 僧侶. Depending on the school, the word is also read わじょう (Shingon) or かしょう (Tendai). In folktales and anecdotes, the 和尚 is a stock character.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"高尚な趣味をお持ちなんですね。","romaji":"Kōshō na shumi o omochi nan desu ne.","german":"You have such refined hobbies.","furigana":"こうしょう|しゅみ|も"},{"japanese":"彼の話は高尚すぎて、私には全然分からなかった。","romaji":"Kare no hanashi wa kōshō sugite, watashi ni wa zenzen wakaranakatta.","german":"What he said was far too highbrow, and I didn't understand it at all.","furigana":"かれ|はなし|こうしょう|わたし|ぜんぜんわ"},{"japanese":"高尚な芸術だけでなく、漫画やアニメも立派な文化だ。","romaji":"Kōshō na geijutsu dake de naku, manga ya anime mo rippa na bunka da.","german":"Not only high art, but manga and anime too are legitimate culture.","furigana":"こうしょう|げいじゅつ|まんが|りっぱ|ぶんか"}],"notes":"高尚 describes interests, art, topics or conversations as intellectually elevated and cultured: 高尚な趣味, 高尚な話題, 高尚な文学. It is a な-adjective and sounds elevated; often there is an undertone of irony or distance, as in 高尚すぎて分からない \"too highbrow to understand\". The opposite is 低俗 \"vulgar, lowbrow\". For a noble character, 高潔 or 気高い is more usual.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"結論を出すのは時期尚早だ。","romaji":"Ketsuron o dasu no wa jiki shōsō da.","german":"It is too early to draw a conclusion.","furigana":"けつろん|だ|じきしょうそう"},{"japanese":"新しい店を出すのは、まだ時期尚早だと思います。","romaji":"Atarashii mise o dasu no wa, mada jiki shōsō da to omoimasu.","german":"I think it is still premature to open a new shop.","furigana":"あたら|みせ|だ|じきしょうそう|おも"},{"japanese":"その計画は時期尚早として見送られた。","romaji":"Sono keikaku wa jiki shōsō to shite miokurareta.","german":"The plan was shelved as premature.","furigana":"けいかく|じきしょうそう|みおく"}],"notes":"時期 \"time, timing\" and 尚早 \"still too early\": something is premature, the time is not yet ripe. Almost always predicative: 〜するのは時期尚早だ, or in 時期尚早として見送る \"to shelve as premature\". Formal and typical of politics, business and discussions; in daily life one rather says まだ早い.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私みたいな庶民には関係のない話です。","romaji":"Watashi mitai na shomin ni wa kankei no nai hanashi desu.","german":"That's a story that has nothing to do with ordinary people like me.","furigana":"わたし|しょみん|かんけい|はなし"},{"japanese":"物価が上がって、庶民の暮らしはますます苦しくなった。","romaji":"Bukka ga agatte, shomin no kurashi wa masumasu kurushiku natta.","german":"With prices rising, the lives of ordinary people have become even harder.","furigana":"ぶっか|あ|しょみん|く|くる"},{"japanese":"ここは庶民的な店で、値段も手ごろです。","romaji":"Koko wa shominteki na mise de, nedan mo tegoro desu.","german":"This is a down-to-earth restaurant, and the prices are reasonable too.","furigana":"しょみんてき|みせ|ねだん|て"}],"notes":"庶民 are the common people, as opposed to the rich, the nobility or the powerful: 庶民の暮らし, 庶民感覚, 庶民の味. It is often used self-ironically: 私たち庶民には.... The derivative 庶民的 means \"popular, down-to-earth, affordable\" (庶民的な店). Related are 大衆 \"the broad masses\" and 一般人; 市民, on the other hand, stresses the role of citizen.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"こんど、庶務課に配属されることになりました。","romaji":"Kondo, shomuka ni haizoku sareru koto ni narimashita.","german":"I'm going to be assigned to the General Affairs Section soon.","furigana":"しょむか|はいぞく"},{"japanese":"庶務の仕事には、備品の管理や郵便物の仕分けなどがある。","romaji":"Shomu no shigoto ni wa, bihin no kanri ya yuubinbutsu no shiwake nado ga aru.","german":"General affairs work includes managing supplies and sorting mail.","furigana":"しょむ|しごと|びひん|かんり|ゆうびんぶつ|しわ"},{"japanese":"会議室の予約は、庶務の担当者にお願いしてください。","romaji":"Kaigishitsu no yoyaku wa, shomu no tantousha ni onegai shite kudasai.","german":"Please ask the person in charge of general affairs to book the meeting room.","furigana":"かいぎしつ|よやく|しょむ|たんとうしゃ|ねが"}],"notes":"庶務 refers to general administrative tasks not assigned to a specialist department: office supplies, mail, room bookings, documents. Typical are 庶務課, 庶務係 and 庶務担当. 総務 is the broader, higher-level area of \"general affairs\" (総務部), and 事務 is office work in general. The word is company and government language.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"退職後は田舎で悠々と暮らしたいな。","romaji":"Taishokugo wa inaka de yūyū to kurashitai na.","german":"After retirement, I'd like to live a leisurely life in the countryside.","furigana":"たいしょくご|いなか|ゆうゆう|く"},{"japanese":"走らなくても、終電には悠々間に合うよ。","romaji":"Hashiranakute mo, shuuden ni wa yuuyuu maniau yo.","german":"Even without running, you'll easily make the last train.","furigana":"はし|しゅうでん|ゆうゆうま|あ"},{"japanese":"大きな鷹が空を悠々と飛んでいる。","romaji":"Ooki na taka ga sora o yuuyuu to tonde iru.","german":"A large hawk is soaring leisurely through the sky.","furigana":"おお|たか|そら|ゆうゆう|と"}],"notes":"悠々 (also 悠悠) describes calm, unhurried doing, and it mostly stands as 悠々と before the verb: 悠々と暮らす, 悠々と空を飛ぶ. Also very common is the meaning \"with ease, with plenty of room to spare\": 悠々間に合う \"to make it easily in time\", 悠々勝つ. Fixed phrase: 悠々自適 \"a carefree, self-directed life (in retirement)\". Unlike のんびり, 悠々 sounds more elevated and stresses composed calm.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"悠久の大自然の中で、人生についていろいろ考えてみました。","romaji":"Yuukyuu no daishizen no naka de, jinsei ni tsuite iroiro kangaete mimashita.","german":"Amid the timeless wonders of nature, I thought a great deal about life.","furigana":"ゆうきゅう|だいしぜん|なか|じんせい|かんが"},{"japanese":"中国には悠久の歴史がある。","romaji":"Chuugoku ni wa yuukyuu no rekishi ga aru.","german":"China has a history stretching back through the ages.","furigana":"ちゅうごく|ゆうきゅう|れきし"},{"japanese":"川は悠久の時を経て、この深い谷を刻んだ。","romaji":"Kawa wa yuukyuu no toki o hete, kono fukai tani o kizanda.","german":"Over countless ages, the river carved out this deep valley.","furigana":"かわ|ゆうきゅう|とき|へ|ふか|たに|きざ"}],"notes":"悠久 means \"eternal, continuing since time immemorial\" and is an elevated, literary word. It almost always appears attributively with の: 悠久の歴史, 悠久の時, 悠久の大自然; the form 悠久な is possible but much rarer. It is typical in travel prose, speeches and texts about history and nature. In daily life people tend to say 永遠 \"eternal\" or 長い歴史.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"そんな悠長なことを言ってる時間はないよ。","romaji":"Sonna yuuchou na koto o itteru jikan wa nai yo.","german":"We don't have time to talk so leisurely.","furigana":"ゆうちょう|い|じかん"},{"japanese":"締め切りは明日なのに、彼は悠長に構えている。","romaji":"Shimekiri wa ashita na no ni, kare wa yuuchou ni kamaete iru.","german":"The deadline is tomorrow, yet he is taking it easy.","furigana":"し|き|あした|かれ|ゆうちょう|かま"},{"japanese":"祖父は悠長な話し方をする人だ。","romaji":"Sofu wa yuuchou na hanashikata o suru hito da.","german":"My grandfather is a person who speaks in an unhurried way.","furigana":"そふ|ゆうちょう|はな|かた|ひと"}],"notes":"悠長 means \"leisurely, without hurry\", but mostly has a critical undertone: one takes too much time although the situation is pressing. Very typical: 悠長なことを言っている場合ではない \"this is no time to be talking so leisurely\" and 悠長に構える \"to wait calmly and composedly\". Neutrally, it can describe a deliberate way of speaking (悠長な話し方). のんびり, by contrast, is mostly positive or neutral.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先ほどは愚にもつかないことを申し上げてしまい、誠に申し訳ございませんでした。","romaji":"Sakihodo wa gu ni mo tsukanai koto o moushiagete shimai, makoto ni moushiwake gozaimasen deshita.","german":"I sincerely apologize for saying such nonsense earlier.","furigana":"さき|ぐ|もう|あ|まこと|もう|わけ"},{"japanese":"台風の日に海で泳ぐなんて愚の骨頂だ。","romaji":"Taifuu no hi ni umi de oyogu nante gu no kocchou da.","german":"Swimming in the sea on a typhoon day is the height of folly.","furigana":"たいふう|ひ|うみ|およ|ぐ|こっちょう"},{"japanese":"二度と同じ愚を繰り返してはならない。","romaji":"Nido to onaji gu o kurikaeshite wa naranai.","german":"We must never repeat the same folly again.","furigana":"にど|おな|ぐ|く|かえ"}],"notes":"愚 \"folly, stupidity\" is a written-language word that appears mainly in fixed expressions: 愚の骨頂 \"the height of folly\", 愚を犯す \"to commit a folly\", 同じ愚を繰り返す, 愚にもつかない \"nonsensical, silly\". The adjective \"foolish\" in present-day Japanese is normally 愚か(な); 愚な is rare and archaic. As a prefix, 愚 is used in humble language for one's own things: 愚見 \"my humble opinion\", 愚息 \"my (unworthy) son\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"社長の息子を誘拐するつもりだ。","romaji":"Shachō no musuko o yūkai suru tsumori da.","german":"I'm going to kidnap the president's son.","furigana":"しゃちょう|むすこ|ゆうかい"},{"japanese":"子どもが誘拐される事件が相次いでいる。","romaji":"Kodomo ga yuukai sareru jiken ga aitsuide iru.","german":"Cases of children being kidnapped are occurring one after another.","furigana":"こ|ゆうかい|じけん|あいつ"},{"japanese":"誘拐犯は身代金を要求してきた。","romaji":"Yuukaihan wa minoshirokin o youkyuu shite kita.","german":"The kidnapper demanded a ransom.","furigana":"ゆうかいはん||ようきゅう"}],"notes":"誘拐する is transitive: 人を誘拐する \"to kidnap someone\", very often in the passive: 子どもが誘拐される. Related are 誘拐犯 \"kidnapper\", 誘拐事件 and 身代金 \"ransom\". For political or state abductions, 拉致 is used more, and for hijackings of aircraft ハイジャック. The word is neutral to news language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"銀杏の実は「ぎんなん」と呼ばれ、食べ過ぎると中毒症状が出ます。","romaji":"Ichou no mi wa 'ginnan' to yobare, tabesugiru to chuudoku shoujou ga demasu.","german":"The fruit of the ginkgo is called ginnan, and eating too much of it causes symptoms of poisoning.","furigana":"いちょう|み|よ|た|す|ちゅうどくしょうじょう|で"},{"japanese":"秋になると、銀杏の葉が黄色く色づきます。","romaji":"Aki ni naru to, ichou no ha ga kiiroku irozukimasu.","german":"In autumn, ginkgo leaves turn yellow.","furigana":"あき|いちょう|は|きいろ|いろ"},{"japanese":"大学の正門前には銀杏並木が続いている。","romaji":"Daigaku no seimon mae ni wa ichou namiki ga tsuzuite iru.","german":"A row of ginkgo trees continues in front of the university's main gate.","furigana":"だいがく|せいもんまえ|いちょうなみき|つづ"}],"notes":"銀杏 read いちょう is the ginkgo tree; in everyday writing it is mostly written in kana (イチョウ, いちょう). The same characters read ぎんなん refer to the edible ginkgo nuts - a common source of confusion. Typical are 銀杏並木 \"ginkgo avenue\" and the yellow autumn foliage (銀杏の葉が黄色く色づく). The tree is, for example, a symbol of the city of Tokyo.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"庭でトマトを栽培しているんです。","romaji":"Niwa de tomato o saibai shite iru n desu.","german":"I'm growing tomatoes in my garden.","furigana":"にわ|さいばい"},{"japanese":"この地域ではお茶の栽培が盛んです。","romaji":"Kono chiiki de wa ocha no saibai ga sakan desu.","german":"Tea cultivation is thriving in this region.","furigana":"ちいき|ちゃ|さいばい|さか"},{"japanese":"有機栽培の野菜しか買わない。","romaji":"Yuuki saibai no yasai shika kawanai.","german":"I only buy organically grown vegetables.","furigana":"ゆうきさいばい|やさい|か"}],"notes":"栽培 refers to the deliberate growing of plants, as a transitive する-verb: 野菜を栽培する. Common in compounds: 有機栽培 \"organic farming\", 水耕栽培 \"hydroponics\", 栽培が盛んだ \"cultivation is widespread\". More general and everyday is 育てる; for fish farming people say 養殖, and for keeping animals 飼育.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"空欄に答えを書き込んでください。","romaji":"Kūran ni kotae o kakikonde kudasai.","german":"Please write your answer in the blank.","furigana":"くうらん|こた|か|こ"},{"japanese":"空欄のまま提出しないでください。","romaji":"Kuuran no mama teishutsu shinaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't submit it with the blanks left empty.","furigana":"くうらん|ていしゅつ"},{"japanese":"該当しない項目は空欄で結構です。","romaji":"Gaitou shinai koumoku wa kuuran de kekkou desu.","german":"Items that don't apply can be left blank.","furigana":"がいとう|こうもく|くうらん|けっこう"}],"notes":"空欄 is an empty field in a form, test or table. Typical: 空欄に記入する / 書き込む \"to fill in the blank field\", 空欄を埋める \"to fill in the gaps\", 空欄のまま \"left blank\". 空白 is more general (an empty spot, also figuratively: 記憶の空白), and 余白 is the free margin of a page.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"サインが欄外にはみ出てしまったんですが、大丈夫でしょうか。","romaji":"Sain ga rangai ni hamidete shimatta n desu ga, daijōbu deshō ka.","german":"My signature ran outside the box; is that okay?","furigana":"らんがい|で|だいじょうぶ"},{"japanese":"注意事項は欄外に小さく書かれている。","romaji":"Chuui jikou wa rangai ni chiisaku kakarete iru.","german":"The precautions are written in small print in the margin.","furigana":"ちゅういじこう|らんがい|ちい|か"},{"japanese":"欄外に「要確認」とメモしておいた。","romaji":"Rangai ni 'youkakunin' to memo shite oita.","german":"I jotted \"needs checking\" in the margin.","furigana":"らんがい|ようかくにん"}],"notes":"欄外 refers to the area outside a designated field or text block, i.e. the margin of a page or form. Typical: 欄外に書く / はみ出す, 欄外の注 \"marginal note, footnote\". Unlike 余白 \"free margin, blank space\", 欄外 stresses that something stands outside the actual field. It is a noun and is used with に or の.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その飛行機事故では、多くの犠牲者が出ました。","romaji":"Sono hikouki jiko de wa, ooku no giseisha ga demashita.","german":"There were many casualties in that plane crash.","furigana":"ひこうきじこ|おお|ぎせいしゃ|で"},{"japanese":"地震の犠牲者に黙祷を捧げた。","romaji":"Jishin no giseisha ni mokutou o sasageta.","german":"We observed a moment of silence for the earthquake victims.","furigana":"じしん|ぎせいしゃ||ささ"},{"japanese":"彼もまた、いじめの犠牲者だった。","romaji":"Kare mo mata, ijime no giseisha datta.","german":"He too was a victim of bullying.","furigana":"かれ|ぎせいしゃ"}],"notes":"犠牲者 are victims, especially fatalities of accidents, disasters or wars: 犠牲者が出る, 犠牲者を悼む. Figuratively also for people who suffer from something (いじめの犠牲者). For those harmed by crimes or damage, who have often survived, 被害者 is the usual word; 死傷者 covers dead and injured. Related: 犠牲になる \"to fall victim\".","levelNote":"Compound: 犠牲 (N1) + 者 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"哺乳瓶を熱湯消毒しています。","romaji":"Honyū bin o nettō shōdoku shite imasu.","german":"I'm sterilizing the baby bottle with boiling water.","furigana":"ほにゅうびん|ねっとうしょうどく"},{"japanese":"赤ちゃんが哺乳瓶でミルクを飲んでいる。","romaji":"Akachan ga honyuubin de miruku o nonde iru.","german":"The baby is drinking milk from a bottle.","furigana":"あか|ほにゅうびん|の"},{"japanese":"哺乳瓶を嫌がって、なかなか飲んでくれない。","romaji":"Honyuubin o iyagatte, nakanaka nonde kurenai.","german":"The baby dislikes the bottle and won't drink.","furigana":"ほにゅうびん|いや|の"}],"notes":"哺乳瓶 is the baby bottle; also often written ほ乳瓶 or ほ乳びん. One drinks from it with で: 哺乳瓶でミルクを飲む; ミルク here usually means infant formula (粉ミルク), as opposed to 母乳 \"breast milk\". Typical collocations are 哺乳瓶を消毒する and 哺乳瓶を嫌がる \"to refuse the bottle\". The 哺乳 is also found in 哺乳類 \"mammals\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"こんな空疎な論争を続けるのは時間の無駄だ。","romaji":"Konna kūso na ronsō o tsuzukeru no wa jikan no muda da.","german":"Continuing such a pointless debate is a waste of time.","furigana":"くうそ|ろんそう|つづ|じかん|むだ"},{"japanese":"空疎な言葉を並べるだけの演説だった。","romaji":"Kuuso na kotoba o naraberu dake no enzetsu datta.","german":"The speech was nothing but a string of empty words.","furigana":"くうそ|ことば|なら|えんぜつ"},{"japanese":"内容の空疎な報告書を何度も書き直させられた。","romaji":"Naiyou no kuuso na houkokusho o nando mo kakinaosaserareta.","german":"I was made to rewrite the report, which had little substance, over and over.","furigana":"ないよう|くうそ|ほうこくしょ|なんど|か|なお"}],"notes":"空疎 means \"hollow, empty of content\" and criticizes speeches, arguments or texts without substance: 空疎な議論, 空疎な言葉, 内容が空疎だ. It is written language and judgmental. Beware of confusion with 空虚: that describes more an inner emptiness or meaninglessness (空虚な気持ち), whereas 空疎 refers to lacking content. More neutral are 中身がない or 内容が薄い.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"空襲を避けるために田舎へ疎開していました。","romaji":"Kūshū o sakeru tame ni inaka e sokai shite imashita.","german":"We had been evacuated to the countryside to avoid air raids.","furigana":"くうしゅう|さ|いなか|そかい"},{"japanese":"戦争中、子どもたちは集団疎開で地方の寺に送られた。","romaji":"Sensouchuu, kodomotachi wa shuudan sokai de chihou no tera ni okurareta.","german":"During the war, children were sent to rural temples in a group evacuation.","furigana":"せんそうちゅう|こ|しゅうだんそかい|ちほう|てら|おく"},{"japanese":"祖母は疎開先で終戦を迎えた。","romaji":"Sobo wa sokaisaki de shuusen o mukaeta.","german":"My grandmother experienced the end of the war at her evacuation site.","furigana":"そぼ|そかいさき|しゅうせん|むか"}],"notes":"疎開 refers above all to the evacuation of city dwellers to the countryside during World War II: 田舎へ疎開する, 学童疎開 / 集団疎開 \"school-children's evacuation\", 疎開先 \"evacuation destination\". For fleeing acute danger such as earthquakes or tsunami, people today say 避難. As a する-verb it is mostly intransitive, with へ/に for the destination; the military sense \"to disperse troops\" is rare.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼とは、意思疎通が全然できないんです。","romaji":"Kare to wa, ishi sotsū ga zenzen dekinai n desu.","german":"I can't communicate with him at all.","furigana":"かれ|いしそつう|ぜんぜん"},{"japanese":"上司と部下の意思疎通を図るために、定例会議を設けた。","romaji":"Joushi to buka no ishi sotsuu o hakaru tame ni, teirei kaigi o mouketa.","german":"We set up regular meetings to improve communication between bosses and subordinates.","furigana":"じょうし|ぶか|いしそつう|はか|ていれいかいぎ|もう"},{"japanese":"言葉が通じなくても、身振りで何とか意思の疎通ができた。","romaji":"Kotoba ga tsuujinakute mo, miburi de nantoka ishi no sotsuu ga dekita.","german":"Even though we didn't share a language, we managed to communicate somehow with gestures.","furigana":"ことば|つう|みぶ|なん|いし|そつう"}],"notes":"疎通 occurs almost only in the compound 意思疎通 or 意思の疎通 \"mutual understanding\": 意思疎通ができる, 意思の疎通を図る \"to promote mutual understanding\", 意思疎通が取れない. It sounds more formal than コミュニケーション or 話が通じる and is typical of the working world, nursing care and newspapers. The sense \"unobstructed flow through\" is technical and rare.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"昼間は賑やかですが、夜になると人通りも疎らになります。","romaji":"Hiruma wa nigiyaka desu ga, yoru ni naru to hitodōri mo mabara ni narimasu.","german":"It's lively during the day, but at night foot traffic becomes sparse.","furigana":"ひるま|にぎ|よる|ひとどお|まば"},{"japanese":"平日の昼間なので、客はまばらだった。","romaji":"Heijitsu no hiruma na node, kyaku wa mabara datta.","german":"It was a weekday afternoon, so there were few customers.","furigana":"へいじつ|ひるま|きゃく"},{"japanese":"山の斜面に木がまばらに生えている。","romaji":"Yama no shamen ni ki ga mabara ni haete iru.","german":"Trees grow sparsely on the mountain slope.","furigana":"やま|しゃめん|き|は"}],"notes":"疎ら \"sparse, scattered\" is mostly written in kana: まばら. It describes things or people that are widely spread out and unevenly distributed: 人通りがまばら, 客がまばら, まばらな拍手 \"sparse applause\", 木がまばらに生えている. Unlike 少ない, まばら stresses the loose, gappy distribution. Opposite: 密集した \"densely packed\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の妹は、精神疾患を抱えています。","romaji":"Watashi no imōto wa, seishin shikkan o kakaete imasu.","german":"My younger sister has a mental illness.","furigana":"わたし|いもうと|せいしんしっかん|かか"},{"japanese":"心臓に疾患のある方は、このアトラクションには乗れません。","romaji":"Shinzou ni shikkan no aru kata wa, kono atorakushon ni wa noremasen.","german":"People with heart conditions cannot ride this attraction.","furigana":"しんぞう|しっかん|かた|の"},{"japanese":"冬になると、呼吸器疾患の患者が増える。","romaji":"Fuyu ni naru to, kokyuuki shikkan no kanja ga fueru.","german":"In winter, the number of patients with respiratory diseases increases.","furigana":"ふゆ|こきゅうきしっかん|かんじゃ|ふ"}],"notes":"疾患 is the medical technical term for a disease, usually tied to an organ or area: 心疾患, 精神疾患, 呼吸器疾患, 心臓に疾患がある. In daily life people say 病気; 疾病, by contrast, is the administrative and statistical term (insurance, laws). 疾患を抱える \"to suffer from a condition\" is a common phrase.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"少年は、駅の構内を全力疾走していた。","romaji":"Shōnen wa, eki no kōnai o zenryoku shissō shite ita.","german":"The boy was sprinting at full speed through the station concourse.","furigana":"しょうねん|えき|こうない|ぜんりょくしっそう"},{"japanese":"一台のバイクが夜の高速道路を疾走していった。","romaji":"Ichidai no baiku ga yoru no kousoku douro o shissou shite itta.","german":"A motorcycle raced off along the highway at night.","furigana":"いちだい|よる|こうそくどうろ|しっそう"},{"japanese":"馬が草原を疾走する姿は美しい。","romaji":"Uma ga sougen o shissou suru sugata wa utsukushii.","german":"A horse galloping across the grassland is a beautiful sight.","furigana":"うま|そうげん|しっそう|すがた|うつく"}],"notes":"疾走 is a written-language word for \"to race, to run or drive at full speed\". It is intransitive; the route is marked with を: 道を疾走する. Very common is 全力疾走 \"to sprint with all one's strength\". It suits people, animals and vehicles and feels more dynamic than 走る or 駆ける; colloquially people tend to say ダッシュする.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"真っ黒な犬が、疾風のように河原を駆け抜けていった。","romaji":"Makkuro na inu ga, shippū no yō ni kawara o kakenukete itta.","german":"Der schwarze Hund rannte wie ein Windstoß über das Flussbett.","furigana":"ま|くろ|いぬ|しっぷう||か|ぬ"},{"japanese":"18世紀のドイツ文学には「疾風怒濤」と呼ばれる時代がある。","romaji":"Juuhasseiki no Doitsu bungaku ni wa 'shippuu dotou' to yobareru jidai ga aru.","german":"In der deutschen Literatur des 18. Jahrhunderts gibt es eine Epoche, die „Sturm und Drang“ genannt wird.","furigana":"せいき|ぶんがく|しっぷうどとう|よ|じだい"},{"japanese":"疾風が吹き荒れ、船は大きく揺れた。","romaji":"Shippuu ga fukiare, fune wa ookiku yureta.","german":"Ein heftiger Sturmwind tobte, und das Schiff schwankte stark.","furigana":"しっぷう|ふ|あ|ふね|おお|ゆ"}],"notes":"疾風 ist ein heftiger, schneller Wind und gehört zur gehobenen Sprache; in der Meteorologie bezeichnet es Windstärke 5 nach Beaufort („frische Brise“). Häufig ist der Vergleich 疾風のように „wie der Wind, blitzschnell“. Die Literaturepoche „Sturm und Drang“ heißt auf Japanisch 疾風怒濤. Im Alltag sagt man eher 強風 oder 突風 „Windböe“.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"がん、急性心筋梗塞、脳卒中のことを三大疾病といいます。","romaji":"Gan, kyūsei shinkinkōsoku, nōsotchū no koto o san dai shippei to iimasu.","german":"Cancer, acute myocardial infarction and stroke are called the three major diseases.","furigana":"きゅうせいしんきんこうそく|のうそっちゅう|さんだいしっぺい"},{"japanese":"疾病の予防と早期発見に努めています。","romaji":"Shippei no yobou to souki hakken ni tsutomete imasu.","german":"We are working on disease prevention and early detection.","furigana":"しっぺい|よぼう|そうきはっけん|つと"},{"japanese":"世界保健機関は疾病の国際的な分類を定めている。","romaji":"Sekai hoken kikan wa shippei no kokusaiteki na bunrui o sadamete iru.","german":"The World Health Organization sets the international classification of diseases.","furigana":"せかいほけんきかん|しっぺい|こくさいてき|ぶんるい|さだ"}],"notes":"疾病 is the official or statistical umbrella term for diseases and appears in laws, insurance and statistics: 三大疾病, 疾病保険, 国際疾病分類 \"ICD\", 疾病の予防. In conversation people say 病気. 疾患, by contrast, is the clinical term for a specific organ disease (心疾患). The reading is しっぺい, not しつびょう.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"音痴なので、カラオケは苦手です。","romaji":"Onchi na node, karaoke wa nigate desu.","german":"I'm tone-deaf, so I'm bad at karaoke.","furigana":"おんち|にがて"},{"japanese":"私はひどい方向音痴で、すぐ道に迷います。","romaji":"Watashi wa hidoi houkou onchi de, sugu michi ni mayoimasu.","german":"I have a terrible sense of direction and get lost easily.","furigana":"わたし|ほうこうおんち|みち|まよ"},{"japanese":"機械音痴の父にスマホの使い方を教えた。","romaji":"Kikai onchi no chichi ni sumaho no tsukaikata o oshieta.","german":"I taught my father, who is hopeless with machines, how to use a smartphone.","furigana":"きかいおんち|ちち|つか|かた|おし"}],"notes":"音痴 first of all means \"tone-deaf, singing off-key\": 私は音痴だ, 音痴なので歌が苦手. As a suffix it denotes a lack of feel for a field: 方向音痴 \"having no sense of direction\", 機械音痴 \"technically inept\", 運動音痴 \"unathletic\". It is colloquial and often used self-ironically; said about others, it can be hurtful.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"祖母の痴呆が始まったんです。","romaji":"Sobo no chihō ga hajimatta n desu.","german":"My grandmother's dementia has started.","furigana":"そぼ|ちほう|はじ"},{"japanese":"かつて「痴呆症」と呼ばれていた病気は、現在では「認知症」と言います。","romaji":"Katsute 'chihoushou' to yobarete ita byouki wa, genzai de wa 'ninchishou' to iimasu.","german":"The illness once called \"chihō-shō\" is now called \"ninchi-shō\".","furigana":"ちほうしょう|よ|びょうき|げんざい|にんちしょう|い"},{"japanese":"古い医学書では「老人性痴呆」という言葉がよく使われている。","romaji":"Furui igakusho de wa 'roujinsei chihou' to iu kotoba ga yoku tsukawarete iru.","german":"In older medical books, the term \"senile dementia\" is often used.","furigana":"ふる|いがくしょ|ろうじんせいちほう|ことば|つか"}],"notes":"痴呆 used to be the usual term for dementia (痴呆症, 老人性痴呆). Because it sounds derogatory (\"idiocy\"), it was officially replaced by 認知症 in 2004; today, 認知症 should be used in conversation and in texts. 痴呆 is still encountered in older books, quotations and some technical terms. It is a noun.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今朝、痴漢を逮捕しました。","romaji":"Kesa, chikan o taiho shimashita.","german":"We arrested a groper this morning.","furigana":"けさ|ちかん|たいほ"},{"japanese":"電車で痴漢に遭ったら、大きな声を出してください。","romaji":"Densha de chikan ni attara, ookina koe o dashite kudasai.","german":"If you are groped on the train, please shout out loudly.","furigana":"でんしゃ|ちかん|あ|おお|こえ|だ"},{"japanese":"痴漢対策として、朝の電車には女性専用車両がある。","romaji":"Chikan taisaku to shite, asa no densha ni wa josei sen'you sharyou ga aru.","german":"As a measure against gropers, there are women-only cars on the morning trains.","furigana":"ちかんたいさく|あさ|でんしゃ|じょせいせんようしゃりょう"}],"notes":"痴漢 refers to a man who sexually harasses women, above all by groping them on crowded trains, and also to the act itself (痴漢行為). Typical: 痴漢に遭う \"to be groped\", 痴漢をする, 痴漢対策, 痴漢冤罪 \"false accusation of groping\". More general is セクハラ for harassment at the workplace; 変質者 means a pervert in general.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"戸棚の中には、何千本というマニキュアが無秩序に並べられていた。","romaji":"Todana no naka ni wa, nanzenbon to iu manikyua ga muchitsujo ni naraberarete ita.","german":"Thousands of bottles of nail polish were lined up in the cupboard in no order at all.","furigana":"とだな|なか|なんぜんぼん|むちつじょ|なら"},{"japanese":"郊外の無秩序な開発が問題になっている。","romaji":"Kōgai no muchitsujo na kaihatsu ga mondai ni natte iru.","german":"Unplanned development of the suburbs has become a problem.","furigana":"こうがい|むちつじょ|かいはつ|もんだい"},{"japanese":"政府が崩壊し、国は無秩序な状態に陥った。","romaji":"Seifu ga hōkai shi, kuni wa muchitsujo na jōtai ni ochiitta.","german":"After the government collapsed, the country fell into a state of disorder.","furigana":"せいふ|ほうかい|くに|むちつじょ|じょうたい|おちい"}],"notes":"無秩序 means \"without order, chaotic\" and works as a な-adjective or noun: 無秩序な開発 \"unplanned development\", 無秩序に並ぶ, 無秩序状態. It sounds factual and written, and often refers to society, urban planning or systems; for an untidy room people tend to say 散らかっている or ごちゃごちゃ. The opposite is 秩序正しい. Similar is 混乱 \"confusion\", which, however, describes more a process.","levelNote":"Compound: 無 (N3) + 秩序 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"静粛に願います。","romaji":"Seishuku ni negaimasu.","german":"Silence, please.","furigana":"せいしゅく|ねが"},{"japanese":"裁判長が「静粛に！」と注意した。","romaji":"Saibanchou ga 'Seishuku ni!' to chuui shita.","german":"The presiding judge called for order: \"Silence!\"","furigana":"さいばんちょう|せいしゅく|ちゅうい"},{"japanese":"式典の会場は静粛な雰囲気に包まれていた。","romaji":"Shikiten no kaijou wa seishuku na fun'iki ni tsutsumarete ita.","german":"The ceremony venue was wrapped in a solemn, hushed atmosphere.","furigana":"しきてん|かいじょう|せいしゅく|ふんいき|つつ"}],"notes":"静粛 means \"quiet and orderly\" and is used almost only in formal requests: 静粛に願います / 静粛にお願いします at ceremonies, exams or in court; the judge calls 静粛に！ \"Order in the court!\". In daily life people simply say 静かに. 静粛 stresses appropriate, dignified silence, not merely the absence of noise.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"兵糧攻めをするつもりなんだ。","romaji":"Hyōrōzeme o suru tsumori na n da.","german":"He intends to starve them out by siege.","furigana":"ひょうろうぜ"},{"japanese":"籠城が長引き、城内の兵糧が尽きかけていた。","romaji":"Roujou ga nagabiki, jounai no hyourou ga tsukikakete ita.","german":"The siege dragged on, and the provisions inside the castle were running out.","furigana":"ろうじょう|ながび|じょうない|ひょうろう|つ"},{"japanese":"受験勉強の兵糧として、母がおにぎりを作ってくれた。","romaji":"Juken benkyou no hyourou to shite, haha ga onigiri o tsukutte kureta.","german":"As fuel for my exam studying, my mother made me onigiri.","furigana":"じゅけんべんきょう|ひょうろう|はは|つく"}],"notes":"兵糧 is the provisions of an army, a historical word from the time of the samurai wars. Well known is 兵糧攻め \"siege by starvation\": the enemy's supply is cut off. Another expression: 兵糧が尽きる \"the provisions run out\". Today it is also used jokingly for food during strenuous work; neutrally, people say 食料.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ドアノブの指紋を綺麗に拭き取った。","romaji":"Doa nobu no shimon o kirei ni fukitotta.","german":"I wiped the fingerprints off the doorknob clean.","furigana":"しもん|きれい|ふ|と"},{"japanese":"現場から犯人の指紋が検出された。","romaji":"Genba kara hannin no shimon ga kenshutsu sareta.","german":"The culprit's fingerprints were detected at the scene.","furigana":"げんば|はんにん|しもん|けんしゅつ"},{"japanese":"スマホのロックは指紋認証で解除しています。","romaji":"Sumaho no rokku wa shimon ninshou de kaijo shite imasu.","german":"I unlock my smartphone with fingerprint authentication.","furigana":"しもんにんしょう|かいじょ"}],"notes":"指紋 is the fingerprint. Typical combinations: 指紋を採る \"to take fingerprints\", 指紋が検出される, 指紋を拭き取る, 指紋認証 \"fingerprint recognition\". A fingerprint as a smudge on glass or a screen is also called 指紋 (画面に指紋がつく). For footprints people say 足跡.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大臣の不用意な発言が波紋を呼んだ。","romaji":"Daijin no fuyōi na hatsugen ga hamon o yonda.","german":"The minister's careless remark caused a stir.","furigana":"だいじん|ふようい|はつげん|はもん|よ"},{"japanese":"池に石を投げると、水面に波紋が広がった。","romaji":"Ike ni ishi o nageru to, suimen ni hamon ga hirogatta.","german":"When I threw a stone into the pond, ripples spread over the water's surface.","furigana":"いけ|いし|な|すいめん|はもん|ひろ"},{"japanese":"その判決は業界に大きな波紋を広げた。","romaji":"Sono hanketsu wa gyoukai ni ookina hamon o hirogeta.","german":"The ruling sent big ripples through the industry.","furigana":"はんけつ|ぎょうかい|おお|はもん|ひろ"}],"notes":"波紋 are first of all the rings that spread across water (水面に波紋が広がる). Much more common is the figurative sense \"repercussions, unrest\", especially in the news: 発言が波紋を呼ぶ / 波紋を広げる \"to make waves\", 波紋が広がる. Unlike the neutral 影響 or the often positive 反響, 波紋 usually indicates controversy and agitation.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これは、王家の紋章です。","romaji":"Kore wa, ōke no monshō desu.","german":"This is the royal family's coat of arms.","furigana":"おうけ|もんしょう"},{"japanese":"菊の紋章は皇室の象徴だ。","romaji":"Kiku no monshou wa koushitsu no shouchou da.","german":"The chrysanthemum crest is the symbol of the Imperial Household.","furigana":"きく|もんしょう|こうしつ|しょうちょう"},{"japanese":"盾には獅子の紋章が描かれている。","romaji":"Tate ni wa shishi no monshou ga egakarete iru.","german":"A lion crest is depicted on the shield.","furigana":"たて|しし|もんしょう|えが"}],"notes":"紋章 is a coat of arms or official emblem, for example of a royal house, noble family, state or city: 王家の紋章, 菊の紋章 (the crest of the Japanese Imperial House). The traditional Japanese family crest is usually called 家紋 or simply 紋; a school badge is 校章. 紋章 sounds somewhat more formal and is often used for European heraldry.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"茨城県に実家があります。","romaji":"Ibarakiken ni jikka ga arimasu.","german":"My family home is in Ibaraki Prefecture.","furigana":"いばらきけん|じっか"},{"japanese":"茨城県の県庁所在地は水戸市です。","romaji":"Ibarakiken no kenchou shozaichi wa Mitoshi desu.","german":"The prefectural capital of Ibaraki is Mito.","furigana":"いばらきけん|けんちょうしょざいち|"},{"japanese":"茨城県は納豆の産地として有名だ。","romaji":"Ibarakiken wa nattou no sanchi to shite yuumei da.","german":"Ibaraki Prefecture is famous as a production area for natto.","furigana":"いばらきけん|なっとう|さんち|ゆうめい"}],"notes":"茨城県 is a prefecture in the Kantō region northeast of Tokyo, with the capital 水戸. The correct reading is いばらき - the widespread pronunciation いばらぎ is considered wrong and annoys many locals. The prefecture is known for 納豆, the research city 筑波 (つくば) and the garden 偕楽園. The character 茨 means \"thorn bush\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ある閉ざされた山荘で悲劇は起きた。","romaji":"Aru tozasareta sansou de higeki wa okita.","german":"A tragedy took place in a mountain lodge cut off from the outside world.","furigana":"と|さんそう|ひげき|お"},{"japanese":"夏は家族で山荘に滞在します。","romaji":"Natsu wa kazoku de sansou ni taizai shimasu.","german":"In summer, we stay at the mountain villa as a family.","furigana":"なつ|かぞく|さんそう|たいざい"},{"japanese":"大学の山荘でゼミの合宿をした。","romaji":"Daigaku no sansou de zemi no gasshuku o shita.","german":"We held our seminar's training camp at the university's mountain lodge.","furigana":"だいがく|さんそう|がっしゅく"}],"notes":"山荘 is a house or country residence in the mountains, often as a holiday home or as part of accommodation names (〜山荘). Unlike 山小屋, the simple hut for mountaineers, 山荘 sounds more comfortable and upscale. 別荘 is a holiday home in general, whether by the sea or in the mountains. In detective stories, 閉ざされた山荘 \"the mountain lodge cut off from the outside world\" is a classic motif.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この春に別荘を売ってしまうつもりです。","romaji":"Kono haru ni bessō o utte shimau tsumori desu.","german":"I'm planning to sell my holiday home this spring.","furigana":"はる|べっそう|う"},{"japanese":"彼は軽井沢に別荘を持っている。","romaji":"Kare wa Karuizawa ni bessou o motte iru.","german":"He owns a holiday home in Karuizawa.","furigana":"かれ|かるいざわ|べっそう|も"},{"japanese":"あいつは今、別荘暮らしだよ。刑務所のことだけどね。","romaji":"Aitsu wa ima, bessou gurashi da yo. Keimusho no koto da kedo ne.","german":"That guy is living in his \"villa\" now. I mean prison, of course.","furigana":"いま|べっそうぐ|けいむしょ"}],"notes":"別荘 is a holiday home or villa as a second residence, for example in resort areas such as 軽井沢: 別荘を持つ, 別荘を建てる, 別荘地 \"vacation home area\". Colloquially and jokingly, 別荘 also refers to prison (\"to be at the holiday home\" = to be locked up). A rented holiday home is often called 貸別荘; a house in the mountains can also be called 山荘.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 23 (source: N4; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日のカラオケは、昭和歌謡縛りにしよう。","romaji":"Kyō no karaoke wa, shōwa kayō shibari ni shiyō.","german":"Let's limit today's karaoke to Showa-era songs.","furigana":"きょう|しょうわかようしば"},{"japanese":"祖父は今でも昔の歌謡曲をよく口ずさむ。","romaji":"Sofu wa ima demo mukashi no kayoukyoku o yoku kuchizusamu.","german":"My grandfather still often hums old popular songs.","furigana":"そふ|いま|むかし|かようきょく|くち"},{"japanese":"『古事記』には多くの古代歌謡が収められている。","romaji":"'Kojiki' ni wa ooku no kodai kayou ga osamerarete iru.","german":"Many ancient songs are contained in the Kojiki.","furigana":"こじき|おお|こだいかよう|おさ"}],"notes":"歌謡 is an umbrella term for sung songs and mostly appears in compounds: 歌謡曲 \"Japanese pop song (esp. of the Shōwa era)\", 昭和歌謡, 歌謡番組 \"music program\", 古代歌謡 \"ancient Japanese songs\". On its own it sounds rather formal; in everyday speech one says 歌 or 曲. Cf. 民謡 \"folk song\" and 童謡 \"children's song\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"幼稚園で新しい童謡を習ってきました。","romaji":"Yōchien de atarashii dōyō o naratte kimashita.","german":"I learned a new children's song at kindergarten.","furigana":"ようちえん|あたら|どうよう|なら"},{"japanese":"「赤とんぼ」は日本を代表する童謡の一つです。","romaji":"'Akatonbo' wa Nihon o daihyou suru douyou no hitotsu desu.","german":"\"Akatonbo\" is one of Japan's most representative children's songs.","furigana":"あか|にほん|だいひょう|どうよう|ひと"},{"japanese":"母は寝る前によく童謡を歌ってくれた。","romaji":"Haha wa neru mae ni yoku douyou o utatte kureta.","german":"My mother often sang children's songs to me before bedtime.","furigana":"はは|ね|まえ|どうよう|うた"}],"notes":"童謡 are children's songs, especially the songs composed for children since the Taishō period, like 「赤とんぼ」 or 「ふるさと」: 童謡を歌う, 童謡を習う. Traditional play songs with no known composer are called わらべうた, and lullabies 子守歌. For songs at kindergarten, people in daily life also simply say 子どもの歌.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は最後まで自分の信念を貫き通しました。","romaji":"Kare wa saigo made jibun no shinnen o tsuranukitōshimashita.","german":"He stuck to his convictions to the very end.","furigana":"かれ|さいご|じぶん|しんねん|つらぬ|とお"},{"japanese":"叔母は生涯独身を貫き通した。","romaji":"Oba wa shougai dokushin o tsuranukitooshita.","german":"My aunt remained single all her life, by choice.","furigana":"おば|しょうがいどくしん|つらぬ|とお"},{"japanese":"放たれた矢が厚い板を貫き通した。","romaji":"Hanatareta ya ga atsui ita o tsuranukitooshita.","german":"The arrow that was shot pierced right through the thick board.","furigana":"はな|や|あつ|いた|つらぬ|とお"}],"notes":"貫き通す intensifies 貫く: to \"consistently carry something through to the end\". Typical objects with を: 信念 / 意志 / 主張 / 沈黙を貫き通す, 生涯独身を貫き通す. It stresses steadfastness against opposition and sounds somewhat elevated. The literal sense \"to pierce all the way through\" (矢が板を貫き通す) is rarer. There is no intransitive partner; 貫く is likewise transitive.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"賠償金をお支払い頂く可能性があります。","romaji":"Baishoukin o oshiharai itadaku kanousei ga arimasu.","german":"There is a possibility that you may be asked to pay compensation.","furigana":"ばいしょうきん|しはら|いただ|かのうせい"},{"japanese":"裁判所は会社に一億円の賠償金の支払いを命じた。","romaji":"Saibansho wa kaisha ni ichioku en no baishoukin no shiharai o meijita.","german":"The court ordered the company to pay 100 million yen in damages.","furigana":"さいばんしょ|かいしゃ|いちおくえん|ばいしょうきん|しはら|めい"},{"japanese":"敗戦国には多額の賠償金が課された。","romaji":"Haisenkoku ni wa tagaku no baishoukin ga kasareta.","german":"Heavy reparations were imposed on the defeated nations.","furigana":"はいせんこく|たがく|ばいしょうきん|か"}],"notes":"賠償金 is money paid as compensation for damages, as well as reparations between states: 賠償金を払う / 請求する / 受け取る, 損害賠償金. Related: 慰謝料 \"compensation for emotional distress\" and 補償金 \"compensation\" for losses caused lawfully (e.g. expropriation). 賠償 presupposes fault or unlawful damage.","levelNote":"Compound: 賠償 (N1) + 金 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"理論は実践に移してこそ意味があるのです。","romaji":"Riron wa jissen ni utsushite koso imi ga aru no desu.","german":"Theory only has meaning when it is put into practice.","furigana":"りろん|じっせん|うつ|いみ"},{"japanese":"本で学んだ節約術を毎日実践している。","romaji":"Hon de mananda setsuyakujutsu o mainichi jissen shite iru.","german":"I practice the money-saving techniques I learned from the book every day.","furigana":"ほん|まな|せつやくじゅつ|まいにちじっせん"},{"japanese":"この講座では実践的な英会話が学べます。","romaji":"Kono kouza de wa jissenteki na eikaiwa ga manabemasu.","german":"In this course, you can learn practical English conversation.","furigana":"こうざ|じっせんてき|えいかいわ|まな"}],"notes":"実践 means \"to put into practice\" and is also common as a transitive する-verb: 教えを実践する, 学んだことを実践する. Fixed pairs and phrases: 理論と実践, 実践に移す, 実践的な \"practical\". Beware the homophone: 実戦 (じっせん) is \"real combat, the real thing\". 実行 stresses carrying out a plan, 実践 living by principles or knowledge.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この黒毛和牛のステーキは、シェフのこだわりの逸品です。","romaji":"Kono kuroge wagyū no sutēki wa, shefu no kodawari no ippin desu.","german":"This Japanese black beef steak is a gem that the chef takes special pride in.","furigana":"くろげわぎゅう|いっぴん"},{"japanese":"この茶碗は江戸時代の逸品だ。","romaji":"Kono chawan wa Edo jidai no ippin da.","german":"This tea bowl is a superb piece from the Edo period.","furigana":"ちゃわん|えどじだい|いっぴん"},{"japanese":"お土産に、地元の職人が作った逸品を選びました。","romaji":"Omiyage ni, jimoto no shokunin ga tsukutta ippin o erabimashita.","german":"As a souvenir, I chose a fine piece made by a local craftsman.","furigana":"みやげ|じもと|しょくにん|つく|いっぴん|えら"}],"notes":"逸品 is an \"exquisite, outstanding piece\" - a work of art, a craft product or, in gastronomy, a special dish: シェフ自慢の逸品, 江戸時代の逸品. Beware the homophone: 一品 (いっぴん) means only \"one item, one dish\" (一品料理). 絶品 stresses the superlative (often for taste), 傑作 is the masterpiece of an artist.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この広告は非常に秀逸だと評判です。","romaji":"Kono kōkoku wa hijō ni shūitsu da to hyōban desu.","german":"This advertisement is said to be truly outstanding.","furigana":"こうこく|ひじょう|しゅういつ|ひょうばん"},{"japanese":"彼女の作品は応募作の中でもひときわ秀逸だった。","romaji":"Kanojo no sakuhin wa oubosaku no naka de mo hitokiwa shuuitsu datta.","german":"Her work stood out even among the submitted entries.","furigana":"かのじょ|さくひん|おうぼさく|なか|しゅういつ"},{"japanese":"秀逸なトリックに最後まで騙された。","romaji":"Shuuitsu na torikku ni saigo made damasareta.","german":"I was fooled by the brilliant trick until the very end.","furigana":"しゅういつ|さいご|だま"}],"notes":"秀逸 \"excellent, outstanding\" is an evaluative, rather written word for works, ideas, phrasings or achievements: 秀逸な作品, 秀逸なアイデア, 秀逸なキャッチコピー. For people and their abilities, 優秀 is more usual (優秀な学生). In daily life, 秀逸 sounds slightly appreciative and elevated; more neutral are 優れた or すばらしい.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女はさっと目を逸らした。","romaji":"Kanojo wa satto me o sorashita.","german":"She quickly looked away.","furigana":"かのじょ|め|そ"},{"japanese":"話を逸らさないで、ちゃんと答えてよ。","romaji":"Hanashi o sorasanaide, chanto kotaete yo.","german":"Don't change the subject; answer me properly.","furigana":"はなし|そ|こた"},{"japanese":"キャッチャーがボールを逸らして、ランナーが生還した。","romaji":"Kyacchaa ga booru o sorashite, rannaa ga seikan shita.","german":"The catcher let the ball get past, and the runner scored.","furigana":"そ|せいかん"}],"notes":"逸らす (mostly in kana: そらす) is transitive: 目 / 顔を逸らす \"to avert one's gaze\", 話を逸らす \"to change the subject\", 注意 / 気を逸らす \"to distract\". In sports also \"to let (the ball) through, to miss\". The intransitive partner is 逸れる \"to deviate\": 話が逸れる, 台風が逸れる. Do not confuse it with 反らす \"to bend backward\" (胸を反らす).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この住宅ローンには最優遇金利が適用されます。","romaji":"Kono juutaku roon ni wa saiyuuguu kinri ga tekiyou saremasu.","german":"The best preferential interest rate applies to this home loan.","furigana":"じゅうたく|さいゆうぐうきんり|てきよう"},{"japanese":"会員の方は、最優遇レートで外貨に両替できます。","romaji":"Kaiin no kata wa, saiyuuguu reeto de gaika ni ryougae dekimasu.","german":"Members can exchange foreign currency at the best preferential rate.","furigana":"かいいん|かた|さいゆうぐう|がいか|りょうがえ"},{"japanese":"長年のお取引に感謝し、最優遇の条件をご用意しました。","romaji":"Naganen no otorihiki ni kansha shi, saiyuuguu no jouken o goyoui shimashita.","german":"In gratitude for your many years of business with us, we have prepared our best terms for you.","furigana":"ながねん|とりひき|かんしゃ|さいゆうぐう|じょうけん|ようい"}],"notes":"最優遇 is the \"most favorable preferential treatment\" and occurs almost only in banking and business language: 最優遇金利 \"preferential interest rate, prime rate\", 最優遇レート \"best exchange rate\", 最優遇の条件. It is hardly used as a する-verb; \"to give someone preferential treatment\" is 優遇する. In trade between states, people say 最恵国待遇 \"most-favored-nation treatment\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"両チームのファンからやじの応酬が続いた。","romaji":"Ryō chīmu no fan kara yaji no ōshū ga tsuzuita.","german":"The fans of both teams kept up an exchange of jeers.","furigana":"りょう|おうしゅう|つづ"},{"japanese":"記者の厳しい質問に、彼は皮肉で応酬した。","romaji":"Kisha no kibishii shitsumon ni, kare wa hiniku de ōshū shita.","german":"He retorted to the reporter's harsh question with sarcasm.","furigana":"きしゃ|きび|しつもん|かれ|ひにく|おうしゅう"},{"japanese":"両国の間で報復関税の応酬が続き、貿易摩擦が深まった。","romaji":"Ryōkoku no aida de hōfuku kanzei no ōshū ga tsuzuki, bōeki masatsu ga fukamatta.","german":"Retaliatory tariffs went back and forth between the two countries, and trade friction deepened.","furigana":"りょうこく|あいだ|ほうふくかんぜい|おうしゅう|つづ|ぼうえきまさつ|ふか"}],"notes":"応酬 is a back and forth of words, blows or measures in which each side reacts to the other: 非難の応酬 \"mutual accusations\", やじの応酬, 報復関税の応酬. As a する-verb it means \"to retort, to respond\", usually with で for the means: 皮肉で応酬する. Typical in reports on politics, sports and diplomacy; formal. In conversation, people tend to say 言い合い or やり合う.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼らは、不当に過酷な労働を強いられています。","romaji":"Karera wa, futou ni kakoku na roudou o shiirarete imasu.","german":"They are being forced into unfairly harsh labor.","furigana":"かれ|ふとう|かこく|ろうどう|し"},{"japanese":"砂漠という過酷な環境で、彼は三日間生き延びた。","romaji":"Sabaku to iu kakoku na kankyou de, kare wa mikkakan ikinobita.","german":"In the harsh environment of the desert, he survived for three days.","furigana":"さばく|かこく|かんきょう|かれ|みっかかんい|の"},{"japanese":"過酷なスケジュールが続いて、体を壊してしまった。","romaji":"Kakoku na sukejuuru ga tsuzuite, karada o kowashite shimatta.","german":"The grueling schedule went on, and I ended up ruining my health.","furigana":"かこく|つづ|からだ|こわ"}],"notes":"過酷 describes conditions that are harsh beyond what is reasonable: 過酷な労働 / 環境 / 条件 / スケジュール, 過酷な運命. 苛酷 is read the same and stresses more the cruel treatment by someone; today both are often used interchangeably. 厳しい is the neutral everyday word, and 残酷 means \"cruel\" toward living beings.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"労働者を酷使するブラック企業をこのまま野放しにしていてもいいんですか。","romaji":"Rōdōsha o kokushi suru burakku kigyō o kono mama nobanashi ni shite ite mo ii n desu ka.","german":"Is it all right to let black companies that work their employees to the bone go unchecked like this?","furigana":"ろうどうしゃ|こくし|きぎょう|のばな"},{"japanese":"パソコンで目を酷使しているせいか、視力が落ちた。","romaji":"Pasokon de me o kokushi shite iru sei ka, shiryoku ga ochita.","german":"Maybe it's because I overuse my eyes at the computer, but my eyesight has gotten worse.","furigana":"め|こくし|しりょく|お"},{"japanese":"若い頃に膝を酷使したので、今は走れない。","romaji":"Wakai koro ni hiza o kokushi shita node, ima wa hashirenai.","german":"Because I overworked my knees when I was young, I can't run now.","furigana":"わか|ころ|ひざ|こくし|いま|はし"}],"notes":"酷使 means \"to use ruthlessly, to overtax\" and is transitive: 労働者を酷使する, 体 / 目 / 喉を酷使する, 機械を酷使する. It stresses overloading the object, not financial gain; economic exploitation in the narrow sense is 搾取. With body parts, it is completely everyday.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"三冊の本は、どれも内容が酷似している。","romaji":"Sansatsu no hon wa, dore mo naiyō ga kokuji shite iru.","german":"The contents of all three books are strikingly similar.","furigana":"さんさつ|ほん|ないよう|こくじ"},{"japanese":"防犯カメラに映った男は、指名手配犯に酷似していた。","romaji":"Bouhan kamera ni utsutta otoko wa, shimei tehaihan ni kokuji shite ita.","german":"The man captured on the security camera closely resembled the wanted suspect.","furigana":"ぼうはん|うつ|おとこ|しめいてはいはん|こくじ"},{"japanese":"新製品のデザインが他社の商品に酷似していると指摘された。","romaji":"Shinseihin no dezain ga tasha no shouhin ni kokuji shite iru to shiteki sareta.","german":"It was pointed out that the design of the new product closely resembles another company's product.","furigana":"しんせいひん|たしゃ|しょうひん|こくじ|してき"}],"notes":"酷似 means \"to be strikingly similar\" and is an intransitive する-verb, mostly in the form 〜に酷似している or AとBが酷似している. It is written language and typical of news about plagiarism, counterfeits or descriptions of suspects. In daily life people say よく似ている or そっくりだ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女の作った料理は、どれも酷い味だった。","romaji":"Kanojo no tsukutta ryōri wa, dore mo hidoi aji datta.","german":"Everything she cooked tasted terrible.","furigana":"かのじょ|つく|りょうり|ひど|あじ"},{"japanese":"そんなひどいことを言わないで。","romaji":"Sonna hidoi koto o iwanaide.","german":"Don't say such awful things.","furigana":"い"},{"japanese":"ひどい風邪をひいて、一週間学校を休みました。","romaji":"Hidoi kaze o hiite, isshūkan gakkō o yasumimashita.","german":"I caught a terrible cold and missed school for a week.","furigana":"かぜ|いっしゅうかんがっこう|やす"}],"notes":"ひどい is mostly written in kana and means \"terrible, bad\" (ひどい味, ひどい風邪, ひどい雨) as well as \"mean, cruel\" toward people: ひどいことを言う, ひどい人. As an exclamation, ひどい！ means \"How mean!\". The adverb form ひどく intensifies: ひどく疲れた. More generally \"bad\" is 悪い; ひどい is emotionally stronger.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今年の夏は、酷暑が続きました。","romaji":"Kotoshi no natsu wa, kokusho ga tsuzukimashita.","german":"This summer, the scorching heat continued.","furigana":"ことし|なつ|こくしょ|つづ"},{"japanese":"酷暑の中、屋外での作業はなるべく控えてください。","romaji":"Kokusho no naka, okugai de no sagyou wa narubeku hikaete kudasai.","german":"In this extreme heat, please refrain from outdoor work as much as possible.","furigana":"こくしょ|なか|おくがい|さぎょう|ひか"},{"japanese":"酷暑の候、いかがお過ごしでしょうか。","romaji":"Kokusho no kou, ikaga osugoshi deshou ka.","german":"In this season of scorching heat, how are you?","furigana":"こくしょ|こう|す"}],"notes":"酷暑 is \"extreme, oppressive summer heat\" and a written-language word. It appears in news, warnings and letter style: 酷暑の候 as a seasonal greeting in July/August. In weather reports, 猛暑 (猛暑日 from 35 °C) is even more common; in daily life people simply say すごく暑い. Opposite: 酷寒 \"bitter cold\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は、ひどく残酷な決断を迫られました。","romaji":"Watashi wa, hidoku zankoku na ketsudan o semararemashita.","german":"I was forced into an extremely cruel decision.","furigana":"わたし|ざんこく|けつだん|せま"},{"japanese":"動物を残酷に扱ってはいけない。","romaji":"Doubutsu o zankoku ni atsukatte wa ikenai.","german":"You must not treat animals cruelly.","furigana":"どうぶつ|ざんこく|あつか"},{"japanese":"この映画には残酷なシーンがあるので、子どもには見せられない。","romaji":"Kono eiga ni wa zankoku na shiin ga aru node, kodomo ni wa miserarenai.","german":"This film has brutal scenes, so I can't show it to children.","furigana":"えいが|ざんこく|こ|み"}],"notes":"残酷 means \"cruel, brutal\" and describes actions, depictions or circumstances that inflict suffering: 残酷な仕打ち, 残酷なシーン, 動物を残酷に扱う, 残酷な現実. 冷酷, by contrast, stresses the coldness of a person, and 残忍 deliberate, bestial cruelty. 過酷 describes harsh conditions, not cruelty.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あなたのこと、ずっと冷酷な人だと思っていました。","romaji":"Anata no koto, zutto reikoku na hito da to omotte imashita.","german":"I always thought you were a cold-hearted person.","furigana":"れいこく|ひと|おも"},{"japanese":"会社は冷酷にも、病気の社員を解雇した。","romaji":"Kaisha wa reikoku ni mo, byouki no shain o kaiko shita.","german":"The company callously fired a sick employee.","furigana":"かいしゃ|れいこく|びょうき|しゃいん|かいこ"},{"japanese":"彼は冷酷な表情で「もう来るな」と言った。","romaji":"Kare wa reikoku na hyoujou de 'mou kuru na' to itta.","german":"With a cold expression, he said, \"Don't come anymore.\"","furigana":"かれ|れいこく|ひょうじょう|く|い"}],"notes":"冷酷 means \"cold-hearted, merciless\" and stresses a lack of compassion: 冷酷な人, 冷酷な表情, 冷酷にも〜する \"to do something coldly\". Unlike 残酷, which describes the cruel act or situation, 冷酷 characterizes more the person and their attitude. Weaker and more everyday is 冷たい.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いちいち酷評を気にしてたら、何も書けないよ。","romaji":"Ichiichi kokuhyou o ki ni shitetara, nani mo kakenai yo.","german":"If I worried about every scathing review, I couldn't write anything.","furigana":"こくひょう|き|なに|か"},{"japanese":"新作映画は評論家たちに酷評された。","romaji":"Shinsaku eiga wa hyouronkatachi ni kokuhyou sareta.","german":"The new film was panned by the critics.","furigana":"しんさくえいが|ひょうろんか|こくひょう"},{"japanese":"上司に企画書を酷評されて、すっかり落ち込んだ。","romaji":"Joushi ni kikakusho o kokuhyou sarete, sukkari ochikonda.","german":"My boss tore my proposal apart, and I was completely crushed.","furigana":"じょうし|きかくしょ|こくひょう|お|こ"}],"notes":"酷評 is devastating criticism, a \"scathing review\"; as a transitive する-verb it is very often used in the passive: 評論家に酷評される. Typical of reviews of films, books, restaurants or work results. Opposite: 絶賛 \"effusive praise\". More neutral is 批判 \"criticism\", and weaker is 辛口の評価.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"手が滑って、鎌がすっぽ抜けただけです。","romaji":"Te ga subette, kama ga supponuketa dake desu.","german":"The sickle just slipped out of my hand.","furigana":"て|すべ|かま|ぬ"},{"japanese":"祖父は鎌で庭の草を刈っている。","romaji":"Sofu wa kama de niwa no kusa o katte iru.","german":"My grandfather is cutting the grass in the garden with a sickle.","furigana":"そふ|かま|にわ|くさ|か"},{"japanese":"鎌をかけたら、彼はあっさり本当のことを話した。","romaji":"Kama o kaketara, kare wa assari hontou no koto o hanashita.","german":"When I fished for information with a leading question, he readily told the truth.","furigana":"かま|かれ|ほんとう|はな"}],"notes":"鎌 is the sickle, used to cut grass or rice: 鎌で草を刈る, 草刈り鎌; a large scythe is called 大鎌. Important is the idiom 鎌をかける \"to ask someone a leading question, to fish for information\". Do not confuse it with the homophone 釜 \"kettle, pot\". The character is also found in the place name 鎌倉.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その陳列室には、いったい何が並べられているのですか。","romaji":"Sono chinretsushitsu ni wa, ittai nani ga naraberarete iru no desu ka.","german":"What on earth is on display in that exhibition room?","furigana":"ちんれつしつ|なに|なら"},{"japanese":"博物館の陳列室には古い刀がずらりと並んでいた。","romaji":"Hakubutsukan no chinretsushitsu ni wa furui katana ga zurari to narande ita.","german":"Old swords were lined up in rows in the museum's exhibition room.","furigana":"はくぶつかん|ちんれつしつ|ふる|かたな|なら"},{"japanese":"新しい商品は一階の陳列室でご覧いただけます。","romaji":"Atarashii shouhin wa ikkai no chinretsushitsu de goran itadakemasu.","german":"You can view the new products in the showroom on the first floor.","furigana":"あたら|しょうひん|いっかい|ちんれつしつ|らん"}],"notes":"陳列室 is a room in which goods or exhibits are displayed; 陳列 means \"to put on display\" (商品を陳列する). The word sounds a bit old-fashioned and formal: for car dealerships or furniture, people today mostly say ショールーム, and in museums more often 展示室. 陳列室 is still encountered in older buildings, museums and texts.","levelNote":"Compound: 陳列 (N1) + 室 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お肌の張りと艶は、モテる女の必須条件です。","romaji":"Ohada no hari to tsuya wa, moteru onna no hissu jouken desu.","german":"Taut, glowing skin is an essential condition for a woman to be popular.","furigana":"はだ|は|つや|おんな|ひっすじょうけん"},{"japanese":"この仕事では、運転免許が必須条件です。","romaji":"Kono shigoto de wa, unten menkyo ga hissu jouken desu.","german":"For this job, a driver's license is a mandatory requirement.","furigana":"しごと|うんてんめんきょ|ひっすじょうけん"},{"japanese":"平和は経済発展の必須条件だ。","romaji":"Heiwa wa keizai hatten no hissu jouken da.","german":"Peace is an essential condition for economic development.","furigana":"へいわ|けいざいはってん|ひっすじょうけん"}],"notes":"必須条件 is an \"indispensable prerequisite\": 〜が必須条件だ, 〜の必須条件. Common in job advertisements (応募の必須条件, as opposed to 歓迎条件 \"desirable\") and in arguments. 必須 (ひっす) means \"mandatory, indispensable\". In logic, people speak of 必要条件 \"necessary condition\" and 十分条件 \"sufficient condition\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どうして我々の陰謀が露顕したのだ。","romaji":"Dōshite wareware no inbō ga roken shita no da.","german":"How did our conspiracy come to light?","furigana":"われわれ|いんぼう|ろけん"},{"japanese":"不正が露顕して、社長は辞任に追い込まれた。","romaji":"Fusei ga roken shite, shachou wa jinin ni oikomareta.","german":"When the wrongdoing came to light, the company president was forced to resign.","furigana":"ふせい|ろけん|しゃちょう|じにん|お|こ"},{"japanese":"嘘はいずれ露顕するものだ。","romaji":"Uso wa izure roken suru mono da.","german":"Lies are bound to come out sooner or later.","furigana":"うそ|ろけん"}],"notes":"露顕 means \"to come to light, to be exposed\" - of conspiracies, lies or misdeeds that someone wanted to hide. It is intransitive: 悪事が露顕する. Today the spelling 露見 is much more common; in the news 発覚 is often used, and colloquially ばれる. The tone is written to dramatic.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この絵は、人間の顕在意識と潜在意識を象徴しています。","romaji":"Kono e wa, ningen no kenzai ishiki to senzai ishiki o shōchō shite imasu.","german":"This picture symbolizes the conscious and unconscious mind of human beings.","furigana":"え|にんげん|けんざいいしき|せんざいいしき|しょうちょう"},{"japanese":"少子化の影響が地方で顕在化している。","romaji":"Shoushika no eikyou ga chihou de kenzaika shite iru.","german":"The effects of the declining birthrate are becoming apparent in rural areas.","furigana":"しょうしか|えいきょう|ちほう|けんざいか"},{"japanese":"潜在的な需要を顕在化させるのがマーケティングの役割だ。","romaji":"Senzaiteki na juyou o kenzaika saseru no ga maaketingu no yakuwari da.","german":"The role of marketing is to bring latent demand to the surface.","furigana":"せんざいてき|じゅよう|けんざいか|やくわり"}],"notes":"顕在 refers to something that lies openly in view and is the opposite of 潜在 \"latent, hidden\". On its own it is rare; typical are compounds such as 顕在化する \"to come to the surface, to become visible\" (問題が顕在化する), 顕在的 \"manifest\" and 顕在意識 \"waking consciousness\". The word belongs to written and technical style (economics, sociology, psychology); in daily life people tend to say 表に出る or はっきりする.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"明日、市民会館で顕彰式が行われます。","romaji":"Ashita, shimin kaikan de kenshoushiki ga okonawaremasu.","german":"The commendation ceremony will be held at the civic hall tomorrow.","furigana":"あした|しみんかいかん|けんしょうしき|おこな"},{"japanese":"市は地元出身の偉人を顕彰する碑を建てた。","romaji":"Shi wa jimoto shusshin no ijin o kenshou suru hi o tateta.","german":"The city erected a monument honoring a great figure from the area.","furigana":"し|じもとしゅっしん|いじん|けんしょう|ひ|た"},{"japanese":"長年のボランティア活動が顕彰された。","romaji":"Naganen no borantia katsudou ga kenshou sareta.","german":"Her many years of volunteer work were publicly recognized.","furigana":"ながねん|かつどう|けんしょう"}],"notes":"顕彰 means to honor and make known someone's merits or good deeds publicly; transitive with を: 功績を顕彰する, in the passive 〜が顕彰される. Typical are 顕彰式, 顕彰碑 \"memorial stone\" and 顕彰制度. Unlike the more everyday 表彰 \"to commend with a certificate or prize\", 顕彰 stresses public recognition, often also of deceased or historical figures. Very formal, mainly in official and press language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は捕鯨には反対です。","romaji":"Watashi wa hogei ni wa hantai desu.","german":"I'm against whaling.","furigana":"わたし|ほげい|はんたい"},{"japanese":"日本は2019年に商業捕鯨を再開した。","romaji":"Nihon wa nisen juukyuu nen ni shougyou hogei o saikai shita.","german":"Japan resumed commercial whaling in 2019.","furigana":"にほん|ねん|しょうぎょうほげい|さいかい"},{"japanese":"この町はかつて捕鯨で栄えた。","romaji":"Kono machi wa katsute hogei de sakaeta.","german":"This town once flourished thanks to whaling.","furigana":"まち|ほげい|さか"}],"notes":"捕鯨 is whaling as an activity or industry. Common combinations: 商業捕鯨 \"commercial whaling\", 調査捕鯨 \"research whaling\", 捕鯨船, 捕鯨問題, 捕鯨に反対する／賛成する. It is normally not used as a する-verb; the actual catching is 鯨を捕る. The word is neutral but appears mainly in news and debates.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"美味しい鯨肉の店を知っているよ。","romaji":"Oishii geiniku no mise o shitte iru yo.","german":"I know a good whale meat restaurant.","furigana":"おい|げいにく|みせ|し"},{"japanese":"昭和の給食では鯨肉がよく出た。","romaji":"Shouwa no kyuushoku de wa geiniku ga yoku deta.","german":"Whale meat was often served in school lunches in the Showa era.","furigana":"しょうわ|きゅうしょく|げいにく|で"},{"japanese":"鯨肉の消費量は年々減っている。","romaji":"Geiniku no shouhiryou wa nennen hette iru.","german":"Whale meat consumption is falling year by year.","furigana":"げいにく|しょうひりょう|ねんねんへ"}],"notes":"鯨肉 is the Sino-Japanese reading for \"whale meat\" and sounds factual and written (statistics, labels, reports). In conversation people mostly say 鯨の肉 or くじら肉. Common are 鯨肉の消費, 鯨肉を食べる, 鯨肉の竜田揚げ. Older Japanese people still know whale meat from school lunches in the postwar period.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"空が夕焼けで茜色に染まっていて、とても綺麗でした。","romaji":"Sora ga yūyake de akaneiro ni somatte ite, totemo kirei deshita.","german":"The sky was dyed madder red by the sunset, and it was very beautiful.","furigana":"そら|ゆうや|あかねいろ|そ|きれい"},{"japanese":"彼女は茜色の着物を着ていた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa akaneiro no kimono o kite ita.","german":"She was wearing a madder-red kimono.","furigana":"かのじょ|あかねいろ|きもの|き"},{"japanese":"この布は茜の根で茜色に染めたものです。","romaji":"Kono nuno wa akane no ne de akaneiro ni someta mono desu.","german":"This cloth was dyed madder red with madder root.","furigana":"ぬの|あかね|ね|あかねいろ|そ"}],"notes":"茜色 is a deep, warm red named after the dye plant 茜 \"madder\". The word is literary and poetic and describes above all the evening sky: 夕焼けで空が茜色に染まる, 茜色の空; related is 茜空. Grammatically, it takes の before nouns and に with 染まる／染める. In daily life, people simply say 赤 or 真っ赤 for red.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日曜日に京都の錦市場に行きました。","romaji":"Nichiyoubi ni Kyouto no Nishiki Ichiba ni ikimashita.","german":"On Sunday, I went to Nishiki Market in Kyoto.","furigana":"にちようび|きょうと|にしきいちば|い"},{"japanese":"錦市場は「京都の台所」と呼ばれている。","romaji":"Nishiki Ichiba wa \"Kyouto no daidokoro\" to yobarete iru.","german":"Nishiki Market is called \"Kyoto's kitchen\".","furigana":"にしきいちば|きょうと|だいどころ|よ"},{"japanese":"錦市場で漬物や湯葉を食べ歩きした。","romaji":"Nishiki Ichiba de tsukemono ya yuba o tabearuki shita.","german":"At Nishiki Market, I snacked my way along on pickles and yuba.","furigana":"にしきいちば|つけもの|ゆば|た|ある"}],"notes":"錦市場 is a covered market street about 400 meters long in central Kyoto, known as \"Kyoto's kitchen\" (京の台所). There you find tsukemono, yuba, fish, sweets and kitchen knives; today it is also popular with tourists for 食べ歩き. Watch the reading: 市場 as a concrete marketplace is read いちば, while しじょう means the market in the economic sense (株式市場).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"悪因悪果って言うだろ？当然のむくいだよ。","romaji":"Akuin'akka tte iu daro? Touzen no mukui da yo.","german":"They say bad causes bring bad results, right? It's a well-deserved retribution.","furigana":"あくいんあっか|い|とうぜん"},{"japanese":"悪因悪果というように、人をだました者はいずれ自分もだまされる。","romaji":"Akuin'akka to iu you ni, hito o damashita mono wa izure jibun mo damasareru.","german":"As the saying goes, evil causes evil effects: whoever deceives others will eventually be deceived himself.","furigana":"あくいんあっか|ひと|もの|じぶん"},{"japanese":"仏教では悪因悪果、善因善果と説かれる。","romaji":"Bukkyou de wa akuin'akka, zen'in zenka to tokareru.","german":"Buddhism teaches that evil causes bring evil results and good causes bring good results.","furigana":"ぶっきょう|あくいんあっか|ぜんいんぜんか|と"}],"notes":"悪因悪果 is a Buddhist-influenced four-character expression: 悪因 \"bad cause\" and 悪果 \"bad fruit\" - evil deeds have evil consequences. The counterpart is 善因善果; more general and more common is 因果応報, and 自業自得 stresses more \"you brought it on yourself\". Typical frames: 悪因悪果というように..., まさに悪因悪果だ. Written to didactic; in conversation, rather ironic and quoting.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これは上から見ると不等辺三角形になるようにデザインされています。","romaji":"Kore wa ue kara miru to futouhen sankakkei ni naru you ni dezain sarete imasu.","german":"This is designed so that it forms a scalene triangle when seen from above.","furigana":"うえ|み|ふとうへんさんかっけい"},{"japanese":"次の不等式を解きなさい。","romaji":"Tsugi no futoushiki o tokinasai.","german":"Solve the following inequality.","furigana":"つぎ|ふとうしき|と"},{"japanese":"「<」や「>」の記号を不等号と言います。","romaji":"\"<\" ya \">\" no kigou o futougou to iimasu.","german":"The symbols \"<\" and \">\" are called inequality signs.","furigana":"きごう|ふとうごう|い"}],"notes":"不等 \"inequality\" occurs almost only in mathematical-technical compounds: 不等式 \"inequality\", 不等号 \"inequality sign\", 不等辺三角形 \"scalene triangle\". As an independent word it is rare. Do not confuse it with the identically read, much more common 不当 \"unjust, unlawful\" (不当な扱い, 不当解雇).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"小さい頃、継父にいつもおんぶをしてもらっていました。","romaji":"Chiisai koro, keifu ni itsumo onbu o shite moratte imashita.","german":"When I was little, my stepfather always gave me piggyback rides.","furigana":"ちい|ころ|けいふ"},{"japanese":"母の再婚相手である継父とは、最初はうまくいかなかった。","romaji":"Haha no saikon aite de aru keifu to wa, saisho wa umaku ikanakatta.","german":"I didn't get along with my stepfather, my mother's new husband, at first.","furigana":"はは|さいこんあいて|けいふ|さいしょ"},{"japanese":"書類の続柄の欄に「継父」と書いた。","romaji":"Shorui no zokugara no ran ni \"keifu\" to kaita.","german":"I wrote \"stepfather\" in the relationship field of the form.","furigana":"しょるい|ぞくがら|らん|けいふ|か"}],"notes":"継父 is the formal word for \"stepfather\", i.e. the mother's husband who is not the biological father; the counterpart is 継母 (けいぼ). It appears in documents, reports and literature. In conversation people tend to say 義理の父, 母の再婚相手 or simply お父さん; 義父 is ambiguous because it also means \"father-in-law\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"そいつにチャンピオンの面目躍如たるところを見せつけてやれ！","romaji":"Soitsu ni chanpion no menboku yakujo taru tokoro o misetsukete yare!","german":"Show that guy what a real champion is made of!","furigana":"めんぼくやくじょ|み"},{"japanese":"ベテラン選手が決勝ゴールを決め、面目躍如の活躍だった。","romaji":"Beteran senshu ga kesshou gooru o kime, menmoku yakujo no katsuyaku datta.","german":"The veteran player scored the winning goal and played up to his reputation.","furigana":"せんしゅ|けっしょう|き|めんもくやくじょ|かつやく"},{"japanese":"この小説には作者の人柄が躍如として表れている。","romaji":"Kono shousetsu ni wa sakusha no hitogara ga yakujo to shite arawarete iru.","german":"In this novel, the author's personality comes through vividly.","furigana":"しょうせつ|さくしゃ|ひとがら|やくじょ|あらわ"}],"notes":"躍如 \"vivid, standing out clearly\" occurs almost exclusively in the fixed phrase 面目躍如 \"to fully live up to one's reputation, to show oneself at one's best\" (面目 is also read めんぼく). Forms: 面目躍如たる活躍, 面目躍如だ, 面目躍如の一打; rarer is 躍如として \"vividly\". The word is written language and typical of sports reports and reviews.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"生理前はどうしても感情の起伏が激しくなってしまう。","romaji":"Seiri mae wa dōshitemo kanjō no kifuku ga hageshiku natte shimau.","german":"Before my period, my emotional swings inevitably become intense.","furigana":"せいりまえ|かんじょう|きふく|はげ"},{"japanese":"このコースは起伏が多くて、自転車ではきつい。","romaji":"Kono koosu wa kifuku ga ookute, jitensha de wa kitsui.","german":"This course has a lot of ups and downs and is tough by bicycle.","furigana":"きふく|おお|じてんしゃ"},{"japanese":"祖父は起伏に富んだ人生を送った。","romaji":"Sofu wa kifuku ni tonda jinsei o okutta.","german":"My grandfather lived a life full of ups and downs.","furigana":"そふ|きふく|と|じんせい|おく"}],"notes":"起伏 literally means \"rising and falling\": on the one hand, undulations in terrain (起伏の多い道, 起伏に富んだ地形), on the other the ups and downs of emotions or life stories (感情の起伏が激しい, 起伏の多い人生). It is mostly used as a noun; the intransitive 起伏する (起伏する丘陵) is written language. A similar, more casual word for life phases is 浮き沈み.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どうして弟が犯罪者であることを伏せていたんだ。","romaji":"Dōshite otōto ga hanzaisha dearu koto o fusete ita n da.","german":"Why did you keep it secret that your brother is a criminal?","furigana":"おとうと|はんざいしゃ|ふ"},{"japanese":"彼女は恥ずかしそうに目を伏せた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa hazukashisou ni me o fuseta.","german":"She lowered her eyes shyly.","furigana":"かのじょ|は|め|ふ"},{"japanese":"読みかけの本をテーブルに伏せて置いた。","romaji":"Yomikake no hon o teeburu ni fusete oita.","german":"I put the half-read book face down on the table.","furigana":"よ|ほん|ふ|お"}],"notes":"伏せる is a transitive Ichidan verb with several meanings: to lay something face down (本を伏せる, カードを伏せる), to lower one's gaze (目を伏せる), to lie flat (床に身を伏せる) and to keep something secret (名前を伏せる, 事実を伏せる). For a dog, 伏せ！ means \"Down!\". The literary counterpart 伏す is intransitive; \"to lie ill in bed\" is 病に臥せる (also written 伏せる).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"絶対にあいつらを降伏させてみせる。","romaji":"Zettai ni aitsura o kōfuku sasete miseru.","german":"I'll make them surrender, no matter what.","furigana":"ぜったい|こうふく"},{"japanese":"1945年8月、日本は連合国に降伏した。","romaji":"Sen kyūhyaku yonjūgo-nen hachi-gatsu, Nihon wa rengōkoku ni kōfuku shita.","german":"In August 1945, Japan surrendered to the Allied Powers.","furigana":"ねん|がつ|にほん|れんごうこく|こうふく"},{"japanese":"城は三か月の包囲に耐えたが、ついに降伏した。","romaji":"Shiro wa sankagetsu no hōi ni taeta ga, tsui ni kōfuku shita.","german":"The castle withstood a three-month siege but finally surrendered.","furigana":"しろ|さん|げつ|ほうい|た|こうふく"}],"notes":"降伏 means \"to capitulate, to surrender\", mainly in a military sense: 敵に降伏する (the opponent takes に), 無条件降伏 \"unconditional surrender\", 降伏を勧告する \"to call on someone to surrender\". It is an intransitive する-verb; to force someone to give up is 降伏させる. The synonym 降参 is also used jokingly in daily life (降参だ \"I give up\"), while 降伏 stays serious and formal. Do not confuse it with the identically read 幸福 \"happiness\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"警察は容疑者の潜伏先を突き止めた。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa yougisha no senpukusaki o tsukitometa.","german":"The police tracked down the suspect's hideout.","furigana":"けいさつ|ようぎしゃ|せんぷくさき|つ|と"},{"japanese":"このウイルスの潜伏期間は約1週間だ。","romaji":"Kono uirusu no senpuku kikan wa yaku isshuukan da.","german":"The incubation period of this virus is about one week.","furigana":"せんぷくきかん|やく|しゅうかん"},{"japanese":"江戸時代、キリシタンは潜伏して信仰を守り続けた。","romaji":"Edo jidai, kirishitan wa senpuku shite shinkou o mamoritsuzuketa.","german":"In the Edo period, Christians went into hiding and kept their faith.","furigana":"えどじだい|せんぷく|しんこう|まも|つづ"}],"notes":"潜伏 has two main meanings: to stay hidden or go underground (犯人が〜に潜伏する, 潜伏先 \"hideout\") and, in medicine, incubation or latency (潜伏期間 \"incubation period\", ウイルスが体内に潜伏する). It is an intransitive する-verb with に for the place. Undercover investigations, on the other hand, are called 潜入捜査 - 潜入 is \"to infiltrate, to sneak in\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"伏兵に襲われたんだ。","romaji":"Fukuhei ni osowareta n da.","german":"We were attacked from ambush.","furigana":"ふくへい|おそ"},{"japanese":"優勝候補は、思わぬ伏兵に初戦で敗れた。","romaji":"Yuushou kouho wa, omowanu fukuhei ni shosen de yabureta.","german":"The title favorite lost in the first round to an unexpected underdog.","furigana":"ゆうしょうこうほ|おも|ふくへい|しょせん|やぶ"},{"japanese":"計画の最大の伏兵は円安だった。","romaji":"Keikaku no saidai no fukuhei wa en'yasu datta.","german":"The biggest unforeseen obstacle to the plan was the weak yen.","furigana":"けいかく|さいだい|ふくへい|えんやす"}],"notes":"伏兵 literally means \"hidden soldiers\", i.e. an ambush (伏兵を置く, 伏兵に襲われる). Figuratively it refers to an unexpected opponent or unforeseen obstacle, especially in sports and elections: 思わぬ伏兵, 伏兵に足をすくわれる \"to be tripped up by an underdog\". The act of lying in wait itself is 待ち伏せ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私はイジメをただ傍観していました。","romaji":"Watashi wa ijime o tada bōkan shite imashita.","german":"I just stood by and watched the bullying.","furigana":"わたし|ぼうかん"},{"japanese":"もう黙って傍観している場合ではない。","romaji":"Mou damatte boukan shite iru baai de wa nai.","german":"This is no time to stay silent and look on any longer.","furigana":"だま|ぼうかん|ばあい"},{"japanese":"政府は事態を傍観するだけで、何の対策も取らなかった。","romaji":"Seifu wa jitai o boukan suru dake de, nan no taisaku mo toranakatta.","german":"The government merely looked on at the situation and took no measures at all.","furigana":"せいふ|じたい|ぼうかん|なん|たいさく|と"}],"notes":"傍観 means to merely watch something without intervening - almost always with a critical undertone. Transitive with を: 事態を傍観する, often reinforced by ただ／黙って. Common are 傍観者 \"onlooker, bystander\" and 傍観している場合ではない \"we must not stand idly by\". By contrast, 見守る is benevolent watching and 見物 is watching for pleasure.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"警察は、犯罪捜査のために、通信の傍受を行うことがあります。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa, hanzai sōsa no tame ni, tsūshin no bōju o okonau koto ga arimasu.","german":"The police may intercept communications for criminal investigations.","furigana":"けいさつ|はんざいそうさ|つうしん|ぼうじゅ|おこな"},{"japanese":"軍は敵の無線を傍受した。","romaji":"Gun wa teki no musen o bouju shita.","german":"The military intercepted the enemy's radio.","furigana":"ぐん|てき|むせん|ぼうじゅ"},{"japanese":"父の趣味は、アマチュア無線で船の交信を傍受することだ。","romaji":"Chichi no shumi wa, amachua musen de fune no koushin o bouju suru koto da.","german":"My father's hobby is listening in on ships' radio communications with his amateur radio.","furigana":"ちち|しゅみ|むせん|ふね|こうしん|ぼうじゅ"}],"notes":"傍受 refers to listening in on or intercepting radio and communication signals not addressed to oneself: 無線を傍受する, 通信傍受, 通信傍受法. It is a neutral, technical-official term and also includes legal interception by authorities. 盗聴, by contrast, is secret eavesdropping with bugs and sounds clearly negative or illegal.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"タイプの女性がいたので、思わず凝視してしまいました。","romaji":"Taipu no josei ga ita node, omowazu gyōshi shite shimaimashita.","german":"There was a woman who was my type, so I couldn't help staring at her.","furigana":"じょせい|おも|ぎょうし"},{"japanese":"彼は画面を凝視したまま動かなかった。","romaji":"Kare wa gamen o gyoushi shita mama ugokanakatta.","german":"He stared fixedly at the screen and didn't move.","furigana":"かれ|がめん|ぎょうし|うご"},{"japanese":"猫が窓の外の一点を凝視している。","romaji":"Neko ga mado no soto no itten o gyoushi shite iru.","german":"The cat is staring fixedly at a spot outside the window.","furigana":"ねこ|まど|そと|いってん|ぎょうし"}],"notes":"凝視 means to stare at something for a long time and with concentration; transitive with を: 画面を凝視する, 一点を凝視する. The word is written language and sounds intense. In conversation people mostly say じっと見つめる; 注視 stresses attentive observation (e.g. of a situation), and じろじろ見る rude scrutinizing.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"デスクワークのせいで肩や首が凝るのは仕方がない。","romaji":"Desuku wāku no sei de kata ya kubi ga koru no wa shikata ga nai.","german":"It can't be helped that your shoulders and neck get stiff from desk work.","furigana":"かた|くび|こ|しかた"},{"japanese":"最近、父はコーヒーに凝っている。","romaji":"Saikin, chichi wa koohii ni kotte iru.","german":"Recently, my father has been really into coffee.","furigana":"さいきん|ちち|こ"},{"japanese":"このケーキは見た目がとても凝っていますね。","romaji":"Kono keeki wa mitame ga totemo kotte imasu ne.","german":"This cake looks really elaborate.","furigana":"み|め|こ"}],"notes":"凝る is intransitive and has three main meanings: muscles get stiff (肩が凝る, noun 肩こり), one gets passionately absorbed in something (〜に凝る, 凝り性 \"perfectionist\"), and something is elaborately made (凝った料理, 凝ったデザイン). The transitive counterpart is 凝らす: 工夫を凝らす \"to devise something ingenious\", 目を凝らす \"to look closely\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は日本国民全員が神道を信奉すべきだと思います。","romaji":"Watashi wa Nippon kokumin zen'in ga shintō o shinpō subekida to omoimasu.","german":"I think all Japanese citizens should believe in Shinto.","furigana":"わたし|にっぽんこくみんぜんいん|しんとう|しんぽう|おも"},{"japanese":"彼は自由主義経済を信奉している。","romaji":"Kare wa jiyuu shugi keizai o shinpou shite iru.","german":"He is a firm believer in free-market economics.","furigana":"かれ|じゆうしゅぎけいざい|しんぽう"},{"japanese":"その思想家には今も多くの信奉者がいる。","romaji":"Sono shisouka ni wa ima mo ooku no shinpousha ga iru.","german":"That thinker still has many followers today.","furigana":"しそうか|いま|おお|しんぽうしゃ"}],"notes":"信奉 means to believe in a doctrine, ideology or person and follow it; transitive with を: 市場原理を信奉する, 〜主義を信奉する. 信奉者 \"follower\" belongs here. Unlike 信仰 (religious faith, 神を信仰する), 信奉 refers more to doctrines and schools of thought; 信じる is the general everyday word. Written language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お礼奉公とは、奨学金を出してくれた病院で、卒業後に一定期間働くことです。","romaji":"Orei boukou to wa, shougakukin o dashite kureta byouin de, sotsugyougo ni ittei kikan hataraku koto desu.","german":"\"Orei-bōkō\" means working for a set period after graduation at the hospital that paid your scholarship.","furigana":"れいぼうこう|しょうがくきん|だ|びょういん|そつぎょうご|いっていきかんはたら"},{"japanese":"江戸時代、多くの子どもが商家に奉公に出された。","romaji":"Edo jidai, ooku no kodomo ga shouka ni houkou ni dasareta.","german":"In the Edo period, many children were sent into service at merchant houses.","furigana":"えどじだい|おお|こ|しょうか|ほうこう|だ"},{"japanese":"滅私奉公の働き方はもう時代遅れだ。","romaji":"Messhi houkou no hatarakikata wa mou jidai okure da.","german":"The self-sacrificing way of working for one's company is already outdated.","furigana":"めっしほうこう|はたら|かた|じだいおく"}],"notes":"奉公 used to refer mainly to service as a servant or apprentice living in the master's house: 奉公に出る／出される, 丁稚奉公, 奉公人. The second meaning is service to one's lord or the public, for example in the Kamakura feudal relationship ご恩と奉公 or in 滅私奉公 \"selfless devotion\". Today it appears mostly in historical contexts or fixed expressions such as お礼奉公 (working in return for training or a scholarship). Intransitive する-verb.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"弥生時代には稲作が各地に広まった。","romaji":"Yayoi jidai ni wa inasaku ga kakuchi ni hirogatta.","german":"Rice cultivation spread across many regions in the Yayoi period."},{"japanese":"博物館で弥生土器を見た。","romaji":"Hakubutsukan de Yayoi doki o mita.","german":"I saw Yayoi pottery at the museum.","furigana":"はくぶつかん|やよいどき|み"},{"japanese":"「弥生」は旧暦三月の呼び名です。","romaji":"\"Yayoi\" wa kyuureki sangatsu no yobina desu.","german":"\"Yayoi\" is the old name for the third month of the lunar calendar.","furigana":"やよい|きゅうれきさんがつ|よ|な"}],"notes":"弥生 has two meanings: the Yayoi period (roughly 300 BC to 300 AD by the classical dating; recent research places the start earlier), when wet-rice farming and metal tools spread, named after the Tokyo district 弥生, where the first 弥生土器 were found in 1884. It is preceded by the 縄文時代 and followed by the 古墳時代. 弥生 is also the poetic name of the third month in the old calendar and a woman's given name. The reading やよい is an irregular reading.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"哀悼の意を表して、黙祷しました。","romaji":"Aitō no i o arawashite, mokutō shimashita.","german":"I expressed my condolences and observed a moment of silence.","furigana":"あいとう|い|あらわ|"},{"japanese":"謹んで哀悼の意を表します。","romaji":"Tsutsushinde aitou no i o hyou shimasu.","german":"We respectfully offer our deepest condolences.","furigana":"つつし|あいとう|い|ひょう"},{"japanese":"国中が英雄の死を哀悼した。","romaji":"Kunijuu ga eiyuu no shi o aitou shita.","german":"The whole country mourned the hero's death.","furigana":"くにじゅう|えいゆう|し|あいとう"}],"notes":"哀悼 is grief over a deceased person and sympathy with the bereaved. It almost always appears in the fixed phrase 哀悼の意を表する／示す, especially 謹んで哀悼の意を表します \"we offer our sincere condolences\". As a transitive する-verb (故人の死を哀悼する) it is rarer. 追悼 refers more to shared remembrance and memorial events; personally, one says お悔やみ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"みんなで奴の追悼コンサートを開かないか？","romaji":"Minna de yatsu no tsuitō konsāto o hirakanai ka?","german":"Why don't we all hold a memorial concert for him?","furigana":"やつ|ついとう|ひら"},{"japanese":"震災の犠牲者を追悼する式典が行われた。","romaji":"Shinsai no giseisha o tsuitou suru shikiten ga okonawareta.","german":"A ceremony was held to honor the victims of the earthquake.","furigana":"しんさい|ぎせいしゃ|ついとう|しきてん|おこな"},{"japanese":"テレビ局は亡くなった俳優の追悼番組を放送した。","romaji":"Terebikyoku wa nakunatta haiyuu no tsuitou bangumi o housou shita.","german":"The TV station broadcast a tribute program for the late actor.","furigana":"きょく|な|はいゆう|ついとうばんぐみ|ほうそう"}],"notes":"追悼 means to remember a deceased person and mourn him or her; transitive with を: 犠牲者を追悼する. Very common as a modifier: 追悼式 \"memorial service\", 追悼集会, 追悼番組, 追悼コンサート, 追悼文. Compared with 哀悼 (the feeling of grief, mostly in 哀悼の意), 追悼 stresses active, often public remembrance.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"わが校は、惜敗でベスト8入りを逃しました。","romaji":"Waga kou wa, sekihai de besuto eito iri o nogashimashita.","german":"Unsere Schule verpasste durch eine knappe Niederlage den Einzug in die Top Acht.","furigana":"こう|せきはい|い|のが"},{"japanese":"日本代表はドイツに1対2で惜敗した。","romaji":"Nihon daihyou wa Doitsu ni ittai ni de sekihai shita.","german":"Die japanische Nationalmannschaft verlor knapp mit 1:2 gegen Deutschland.","furigana":"にほんだいひょう|たい|せきはい"},{"japanese":"選挙で惜敗した候補が、比例代表で復活当選した。","romaji":"Senkyo de sekihai shita kouho ga, hirei daihyou de fukkatsu tousen shita.","german":"Der im Wahlkreis knapp unterlegene Kandidat zog über die Verhältniswahlliste doch noch ins Parlament ein.","furigana":"せんきょ|せきはい|こうほ|ひれいだいひょう|ふっかつとうせん"}],"notes":"惜敗 ist eine knappe, bedauerliche Niederlage nach gutem Kampf; als intransitives する-Verb mit に für den Gegner: 1点差で強豪に惜敗する. Im Kontrast dazu stehen 完敗 und 惨敗 „klare bzw. vernichtende Niederlage“; das Gegenstück auf der Siegerseite ist 辛勝 „knapper Sieg“. Bei Wahlen gibt es den Begriff 惜敗率. Typisch für Sport- und Wahlberichte.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このアニメは愉快なキャラクターがたくさん出てくる。","romaji":"Kono anime wa yukai na kyarakutaa ga takusan dete kuru.","german":"Many amusing characters appear in this anime.","furigana":"ゆかい|で"},{"japanese":"彼は本当に愉快な人だ。","romaji":"Kare wa hontou ni yukai na hito da.","german":"He is a truly fun person.","furigana":"かれ|ほんとう|ゆかい|ひと"},{"japanese":"昨夜は昔の仲間と愉快に飲んだ。","romaji":"Sakuya wa mukashi no nakama to yukai ni nonda.","german":"Last night I had a merry drink with old friends.","furigana":"さくや|むかし|なかま|ゆかい|の"}],"notes":"愉快 is a な-adjective meaning \"cheerful, pleasant, amusing\" and often describes people, conversations or moods: 愉快な人, 愉快な仲間, 愉快に過ごす. It sounds somewhat more elevated than 楽しい and emphasizes the cheering quality. Very common is the negative 不愉快 \"unpleasant, annoying\" (不愉快な思いをする). 痛快, by contrast, is \"wonderfully liberating, thrilling\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"憂国の若きサムライたちよ、今こそ立ち上がれ！","romaji":"Yūkoku no wakaki samuraitachi yo, ima koso tachiagare!","german":"Young patriotic samurai, rise up now!","furigana":"ゆうこく|わか|いま|た|あ"},{"japanese":"幕末には多くの憂国の志士が現れた。","romaji":"Bakumatsu ni wa ooku no yuukoku no shishi ga arawareta.","german":"Many patriots concerned for their country appeared at the end of the Edo period.","furigana":"ばくまつ|おお|ゆうこく|しし|あらわ"},{"japanese":"彼は憂国の情に駆られて、新聞に投書した。","romaji":"Kare wa yuukoku no jou ni kararete, shinbun ni tousho shita.","german":"Driven by concern for his country, he wrote a letter to the newspaper.","furigana":"かれ|ゆうこく|じょう|か|しんぶん|とうしょ"}],"notes":"憂国 is composed of 憂える \"to worry\" and 国 \"country\": deep concern for the future of one's own country. Typical are 憂国の士／憂国の志士 \"concerned patriot\" and 憂国の情. The word is literary, sounds pathetic and is often nationalistically tinged; Mishima's story 『憂国』 is well known. Unlike 愛国 \"love of country\", 憂国 stresses worry.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一抹の不安が胸をよぎった。","romaji":"Ichimatsu no fuan ga mune o yogitta.","german":"A trace of anxiety crossed my heart.","furigana":"いちまつ|ふあん|むね"},{"japanese":"卒業式の日、一抹の寂しさを感じた。","romaji":"Sotsugyoushiki no hi, ichimatsu no sabishisa o kanjita.","german":"On graduation day, I felt a touch of loneliness.","furigana":"そつぎょうしき|ひ|いちまつ|さび|かん"},{"japanese":"彼の説明には一抹の疑問が残った。","romaji":"Kare no setsumei ni wa ichimatsu no gimon ga nokotta.","german":"A slight doubt remained about his explanation.","furigana":"かれ|せつめい|いちまつ|ぎもん|のこ"}],"notes":"一抹 literally means \"one brush stroke\" and is used almost only as 一抹の + feeling, meaning \"a touch, a trace of\": 一抹の不安, 一抹の寂しさ, 一抹の疑問. These are mostly quiet, negative feelings that one 覚える／感じる or that 残る. The word is written language; in everyday speech one says ちょっと不安 or 少し寂しい.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"都合の悪い意見は会議で抹殺された。","romaji":"Tsugou no warui iken wa kaigi de massatsu sareta.","german":"Inconvenient opinions were suppressed at the meeting.","furigana":"つごう|わる|いけん|かいぎ|まっさつ"},{"japanese":"独裁者は反対派を次々と抹殺した。","romaji":"Dokusaisha wa hantaiha o tsugitsugi to massatsu shita.","german":"The dictator eliminated his opponents one after another.","furigana":"どくさいしゃ|はんたいは|つぎつぎ|まっさつ"},{"japanese":"スキャンダルで彼は芸能界から抹殺された。","romaji":"Sukyandaru de kare wa geinoukai kara massatsu sareta.","german":"The scandal wiped him out of show business.","furigana":"かれ|げいのうかい|まっさつ"}],"notes":"抹殺 is a strong transitive する-verb: to eliminate people or groups (反対派を抹殺する), to suppress or silence opinions or facts (意見を抹殺する) and, more rarely, to strike out something written. The passive 社会的に抹殺される \"to be socially destroyed\" is common. Unlike 抹消 \"to delete (an entry)\", 抹殺 means violent or complete eradication.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は怪我で一軍登録を抹消されたんだ。","romaji":"Kare wa kega de ichigun touroku o masshou sareta n da.","german":"He was removed from the first-team roster because of an injury.","furigana":"かれ|けが|いちぐんとうろく|まっしょう"},{"japanese":"車を廃車にするには、抹消登録の手続きが必要です。","romaji":"Kuruma o haisha ni suru ni wa, masshou touroku no tetsuzuki ga hitsuyou desu.","german":"To scrap a car, you need to go through the deregistration procedure.","furigana":"くるま|はいしゃ|まっしょうとうろく|てつづ|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"退会後、個人データはすべて抹消されます。","romaji":"Taikaigo, kojin deeta wa subete masshou saremasu.","german":"After you leave the membership, all personal data will be deleted.","furigana":"たいかいご|こじん|まっしょう"}],"notes":"抹消 means to officially and completely delete or strike out an entry or record: 登録を抹消する, 名簿から抹消する, データを抹消する. It is typical of official language, e.g. 抹消登録 \"deregistration of a vehicle\"; in baseball, 一軍登録を抹消される means \"to be dropped from the top-team roster\". More everyday are 消す and, for computer files, 削除.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ここに戸棚を据え付けるつもりだよ。","romaji":"Koko ni todana o suetsukeru tsumori da yo.","german":"I'm planning to install a cupboard here.","furigana":"とだな|す|つ"},{"japanese":"工場に新しい機械を据え付けた。","romaji":"Koujou ni atarashii kikai o suetsuketa.","german":"A new machine was installed at the factory.","furigana":"こうじょう|あたら|きかい|す|つ"},{"japanese":"エアコンの据え付け工事は午後に行います。","romaji":"Eakon no suetsuke kouji wa gogo ni okonaimasu.","german":"The air conditioner installation work will be done in the afternoon.","furigana":"す|つ|こうじ|ごご|おこな"}],"notes":"据え付ける is transitive and means to firmly install a heavy device or piece of furniture in its place: 機械を据え付ける, エアコンを据え付ける, 金庫を据え付ける. The noun 据え付け often appears in 据え付け工事. More general is 取り付ける \"to attach, fit\" (also small parts); 設置する is the formal word for facilities. The base verb 据える \"to set firmly\" is known from 腰を据える.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"元旦には家族で初詣に行きます。","romaji":"Gantan ni wa kazoku de hatsumōde ni ikimasu.","german":"On New Year's Day, I go to the first shrine visit of the year with my family.","furigana":"がんたん|かぞく|はつもうで|い"},{"japanese":"年賀状の日付は「元旦」と書くのが一般的です。","romaji":"Nengajou no hizuke wa \"gantan\" to kaku no ga ippanteki desu.","german":"It is common to write \"元旦\" as the date on New Year's cards.","furigana":"ねんがじょう|ひづけ|がんたん|か|いっぱんてき"},{"japanese":"元旦から仕事なんて、ついてないね。","romaji":"Gantan kara shigoto nante, tsuite nai ne.","german":"Having to work from New Year's Day on -- what bad luck.","furigana":"がんたん|しごと"}],"notes":"元旦 is New Year's Day; strictly speaking, 旦 means \"morning, sunrise\", so it refers to the morning of January 1, whereas 元日 covers the whole day. In everyday speech both are mostly used the same way. On New Year's cards (年賀状) you simply write 元旦 as the date; \"1月1日元旦\" or 元旦の朝 are considered redundant.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"商売が繁盛するように、神社でお祈りをしてきました。","romaji":"Shōbai ga hanjō suru yō ni, jinja de oinori o shite kimashita.","german":"I prayed at a shrine for my business to prosper.","furigana":"しょうばい|はんじょう|じんじゃ|いの"},{"japanese":"駅前のラーメン屋は、いつも客でいっぱいで繁盛している。","romaji":"Ekimae no rāmen-ya wa, itsumo kyaku de ippai de hanjō shite iru.","german":"The ramen shop in front of the station is always full of customers and doing great.","furigana":"えきまえ|や|きゃく|はんじょう"},{"japanese":"開店当初は苦労したが、口コミで店は次第に繁盛した。","romaji":"Kaiten tōsho wa kurō shita ga, kuchikomi de mise wa shidai ni hanjō shita.","german":"It struggled at first, but thanks to word of mouth the shop gradually flourished.","furigana":"かいてんとうしょ|くろう|くち|みせ|しだい|はんじょう"}],"notes":"繁盛 means that a business, restaurant or place has many customers and thrives: 店が繁盛する, 繁盛している店, 商売繁盛 \"good business\" as a wish found at shrines and on lucky charms. It is an intransitive する-verb with が; the spelling 繁昌 also exists. Compared with 繁栄 \"prosperity, flourishing\" (of a country, a city), 繁盛 is more concrete and relates to trade and customers. Neutral, everyday.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"栃木県の出身なんですか？","romaji":"Tochigiken no shusshin na n desu ka?","german":"Are you from Tochigi Prefecture?","furigana":"とちぎけん|しゅっしん"},{"japanese":"日光東照宮は栃木県にあります。","romaji":"Nikkou Toushouguu wa Tochigiken ni arimasu.","german":"Nikkō Tōshōgū Shrine is in Tochigi Prefecture.","furigana":"にっこうとうしょうぐう|とちぎけん"},{"japanese":"栃木県はいちごの生産量が日本一だ。","romaji":"Tochigiken wa ichigo no seisanryou ga Nihon'ichi da.","german":"Tochigi produces the most strawberries in all of Japan.","furigana":"とちぎけん|せいさんりょう|にほんいち"}],"notes":"栃木県 is a prefecture in the Kantō region north of Tokyo, with the capital 宇都宮, which is known for gyoza. Also well known are 日光 with the Tōshōgū Shrine, the highlands of 那須 and strawberries (とちおとめ). The character 栃 \"horse chestnut\" is a kanji created in Japan; in conversation people often say just 栃木.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"洗浄剤なら、シンクの下にあります。","romaji":"Senjōzai nara, shinku no shita ni arimasu.","german":"The cleaning agent is under the sink.","furigana":"せんじょうざい|した"},{"japanese":"塩素系の洗浄剤と酸性の洗浄剤を混ぜると危険です。","romaji":"Ensokei no senjouzai to sansei no senjouzai o mazeru to kiken desu.","german":"Mixing chlorine-based and acidic cleaners is dangerous.","furigana":"えんそけい|せんじょうざい|さんせい|せんじょうざい|ま|きけん"},{"japanese":"祖母は毎晩、入れ歯を洗浄剤につけている。","romaji":"Sobo wa maiban, ireba o senjouzai ni tsukete iru.","german":"My grandmother soaks her dentures in cleaning solution every night.","furigana":"そぼ|まいばん|い|ば|せんじょうざい"}],"notes":"洗浄剤 is a cleaning agent and sounds somewhat more technical than the everyday word 洗剤 (dish and laundry detergent). It is found mainly on product labels: トイレ用洗浄剤, パイプ洗浄剤, 入れ歯洗浄剤. 洗浄 is the formal word for \"cleaning, washing\" (洗浄する), and the suffix 剤 means \"agent\" as in 薬剤 or 漂白剤. The warning 混ぜるな危険 \"do not mix, dangerous\" on chlorine-based products is well known.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"瓜実顔で雰囲気のある女優を探しています。","romaji":"Urizanegao de fun'iki no aru joyū o sagashite imasu.","german":"I'm looking for an actress with an oval face and a distinctive presence.","furigana":"うりざねがお|ふんいき|じょゆう|さが"},{"japanese":"浮世絵の美人は瓜実顔に描かれていることが多い。","romaji":"Ukiyoe no bijin wa urizanegao ni egakarete iru koto ga ooi.","german":"Beautiful women in ukiyo-e are often depicted with oval faces.","furigana":"うきよえ|びじん|うりざねがお|えが|おお"},{"japanese":"祖母は若い頃、色白の瓜実顔で評判の美人だったそうだ。","romaji":"Sobo wa wakai koro, irojiro no urizanegao de hyouban no bijin datta sou da.","german":"They say my grandmother was a famous beauty in her youth, with a fair-skinned oval face.","furigana":"そぼ|わか|ころ|いろじろ|うりざねがお|ひょうばん|びじん"}],"notes":"瓜実顔 is an oval, slightly elongated face, named after the shape of a melon seed (瓜実); fair skin often goes with it. It was regarded as a beauty ideal for women, especially in the Edo period, and is known from ukiyo-e portraits of women. The word is literary to old-fashioned; today people tend to say 卵形の顔 or 面長, and the opposite is 丸顔.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"虫歯が自然治癒することはありません。","romaji":"Mushiba ga shizen chiyu suru koto wa arimasen.","german":"Cavities do not heal on their own.","furigana":"むしば|しぜんちゆ"},{"japanese":"傷が完全に治癒するまで、約3か月かかります。","romaji":"Kizu ga kanzen ni chiyu suru made, yaku sankagetsu kakarimasu.","german":"It takes about three months for the wound to heal completely.","furigana":"きず|かんぜん|ちゆ|やく|げつ"},{"japanese":"人間には本来、自然治癒力が備わっている。","romaji":"Ningen ni wa honrai, shizen chiyuryoku ga sonawatte iru.","german":"Human beings are naturally endowed with self-healing power.","furigana":"にんげん|ほんらい|しぜんちゆりょく|そな"}],"notes":"治癒 is healing as a result; intransitive: 病気／傷が治癒する. Common are 自然治癒 \"natural healing\", 治癒力 \"healing power\" and 完全に治癒する. The word is medical and formal; in everyday speech one says 治る. Not to be confused with 治療 \"treatment\" and the transitive 治す \"to cure (something)\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"粗悪なガソリンは、車の寿命を縮める。","romaji":"Soaku na gasorin wa, kuruma no jumyou o chijimeru.","german":"Poor-quality gasoline shortens the life of a car.","furigana":"そあく|くるま|じゅみょう|ちぢ"},{"japanese":"ネットで買った財布は粗悪品だった。","romaji":"Netto de katta saifu wa soakuhin datta.","german":"The wallet I bought online was shoddy goods.","furigana":"か|さいふ|そあくひん"},{"japanese":"粗悪なコピー商品が市場に出回っている。","romaji":"Soaku na kopii shouhin ga shijou ni demawatte iru.","german":"Inferior counterfeit goods are in circulation on the market.","furigana":"そあく|しょうひん|しじょう|でまわ"}],"notes":"粗悪 is a な-adjective for inferior, poorly made goods and materials: 粗悪な商品, 粗悪な材料, especially often 粗悪品 \"shoddy goods\". For poor living or working conditions, 劣悪 is used instead (劣悪な環境). The word is written language; in everyday speech one says 質が悪い or 安物.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このサイトでは、人気アプリの累計ダウンロードランキングを毎日更新しています。","romaji":"Kono saito de wa, ninki apuri no ruikei daunrōdo rankingu o mainichi kōshin shite imasu.","german":"This site updates the ranking of cumulative downloads of popular apps every day.","furigana":"にんき|るいけい|まいにちこうしん"},{"japanese":"このシリーズは累計発行部数が1億部を突破した。","romaji":"Kono shiriizu wa ruikei hakkou busuu ga ichioku bu o toppa shita.","german":"The series' cumulative circulation has surpassed 100 million copies.","furigana":"るいけいはっこうぶすう|おくぶ|とっぱ"},{"japanese":"今年の売上を累計すると、去年を上回った。","romaji":"Kotoshi no uriage o ruikei suru to, kyonen o uwamawatta.","german":"Adding up this year's sales, they exceeded last year's.","furigana":"ことし|うりあげ|るいけい|きょねん|うわまわ"}],"notes":"累計 is the total accumulated up to a point in time: 累計発行部数, 累計販売台数, 累計で100万人. It often stands directly before counters or with で; as a transitive する-verb (売上を累計する) it is rarer. 合計 is simply the sum of several items, while 累計 stresses adding up over time.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は若くして部長に累進した。","romaji":"Kare wa wakaku shite buchō ni ruishin shita.","german":"He was promoted step by step to department head at a young age.","furigana":"かれ|わか|ぶちょう|るいしん"},{"japanese":"日本の所得税は累進課税である。","romaji":"Nihon no shotokuzei wa ruishin kazei de aru.","german":"Japan's income tax is progressive.","furigana":"にほん|しょとくぜい|るいしんかぜい"},{"japanese":"電気料金は、使用量が増えるほど単価が上がる累進制になっている。","romaji":"Denki ryoukin wa, shiyouryou ga fueru hodo tanka ga agaru ruishinsei ni natte iru.","german":"Electricity charges are tiered: the more you use, the higher the unit price.","furigana":"でんきりょうきん|しようりょう|ふ|たんか|あ|るいしんせい"}],"notes":"累進 means \"to rise step by step\". Today it appears mainly in the second meaning \"to increase progressively\": 累進課税 \"progressive taxation\", 累進税率, 累進制. The meaning \"to be promoted step by step\" (部長に累進する) is written language and rather dated; normally one says 昇進する. Intransitive する-verb.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本政府が巨額の国債を累積していることについて、あなたはどのようにお考えですか。","romaji":"Nippon seifu ga kyogaku no kokusai o ruiseki shite iru koto nitsuite, anata wa dono yō ni o kangae desu ka.","german":"What do you think about the Japanese government accumulating a huge amount of government bonds?","furigana":"にっぽんせいふ|きょがく|こくさい|るいせき|かんが"},{"japanese":"会社の累積赤字は10億円に達した。","romaji":"Kaisha no ruiseki akaji wa juuoku en ni tasshita.","german":"The company's accumulated deficit reached one billion yen.","furigana":"かいしゃ|るいせきあかじ|おくえん|たっ"},{"japanese":"サッカーでは、イエローカードが累積すると出場停止になる。","romaji":"Sakkaa de wa, ierookaado ga ruiseki suru to shutsujou teishi ni naru.","german":"In soccer, you get suspended when yellow cards accumulate.","furigana":"るいせき|しゅつじょうていし"}],"notes":"累積 is accumulation, mostly of undesirable things: 赤字が累積する, 累積赤字, 累積債務, 疲労の累積. It can be used intransitively (〜が累積する) and transitively (〜を累積する) and often stands as a modifier before nouns. 蓄積 is more general and can also be positive (知識の蓄積); 累計, on the other hand, is the accumulated total as a number.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼らは、まさに老舗の意地を見せたんだ。","romaji":"Karera wa, masani shinise no iji o miseta n da.","german":"They really showed the pride of an old established firm.","furigana":"かれ|しにせ|いじ|み"},{"japanese":"創業三百年の老舗の和菓子屋で羊羹を買った。","romaji":"Sougyou sanbyaku nen no shinise no wagashiya de youkan o katta.","german":"I bought yōkan at a 300-year-old traditional Japanese confectionery shop.","furigana":"そうぎょうさんびゃくねん|しにせ|わがしや|ようかん|か"},{"japanese":"この旅館は京都でも有名な老舗だ。","romaji":"Kono ryokan wa Kyouto demo yuumei na shinise da.","german":"This inn is a famous long-established one even in Kyoto.","furigana":"りょかん|きょうと|ゆうめい|しにせ"}],"notes":"老舗 is a traditional shop or company that has existed for generations; the reading しにせ is an irregular reading (rarely also ろうほ). Typical are 老舗旅館, 老舗の和菓子屋, 創業百年の老舗; figuratively also 老舗メーカー or 老舗ブランド for long-established firms in any industry. With 老舗の意地／看板 one stresses pride and reputation.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大変だ。捕虜が脱走したぞ！","romaji":"Taihen da. Horyo ga dassō shita zo!","german":"This is terrible. A prisoner of war has escaped!","furigana":"たいへん|ほりょ|だっそう"},{"japanese":"祖父は戦争中、シベリアで捕虜になった。","romaji":"Sofu wa sensouchuu, Shiberia de horyo ni natta.","german":"My grandfather was taken prisoner in Siberia during the war.","furigana":"そふ|せんそうちゅう|ほりょ"},{"japanese":"両国は捕虜の交換に合意した。","romaji":"Ryoukoku wa horyo no koukan ni goui shita.","german":"The two countries agreed to a prisoner exchange.","furigana":"りょうこく|ほりょ|こうかん|ごうい"}],"notes":"捕虜 is a soldier or person captured in war: 捕虜になる \"to be taken prisoner\", 捕虜にする, 捕虜収容所, 捕虜交換. Also used figuratively in literary language: 恋の捕虜 \"captive of love\". To be distinguished are 人質 \"hostage\" and 囚人 \"prisoner (in jail)\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この商品も、キャンペーンの対象に該当しますか？","romaji":"Kono shōhin mo, kyanpēn no taishō ni gaitō shimasu ka?","german":"Does this product also fall under the campaign?","furigana":"しょうひん|たいしょう|がいとう"},{"japanese":"該当する項目に丸をつけてください。","romaji":"Gaitou suru koumoku ni maru o tsukete kudasai.","german":"Please circle the applicable items.","furigana":"がいとう|こうもく|まる"},{"japanese":"検索条件に該当する結果はありませんでした。","romaji":"Kensaku jouken ni gaitou suru kekka wa arimasen deshita.","german":"There were no results matching the search criteria.","furigana":"けんさくじょうけん|がいとう|けっか"}],"notes":"該当 means \"to apply to something, to fall under something\"; intransitive with に: 条件に該当する, 対象に該当する. Typical of forms and official language: 該当する項目, 該当者, 該当箇所, 該当なし. In everyday speech one says 当てはまる. Not to be confused with the identically read 街頭 \"street\" (街頭演説).","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"社長が外国の貴賓を接待する際に、通訳として同行しました。","romaji":"Shachō ga gaikoku no kihin o settai suru sai ni, tsūyaku toshite dōkō shimashita.","german":"When the company president entertained a foreign dignitary, I accompanied him as an interpreter.","furigana":"しゃちょう|がいこく|きひん|せったい|さい|つうやく|どうこう"},{"japanese":"空港の貴賓室で大臣を出迎えた。","romaji":"Kuukou no kihinshitsu de daijin o demukaeta.","german":"We welcomed the minister in the VIP room at the airport.","furigana":"くうこう|きひんしつ|だいじん|でむか"},{"japanese":"王族が貴賓席から試合を観戦した。","romaji":"Ouzoku ga kihinseki kara shiai o kansen shita.","german":"Members of the royal family watched the match from the VIP seats.","furigana":"おうぞく|きひんせき|しあい|かんせん"}],"notes":"貴賓 is a high-ranking, distinguished guest. On its own the word is rare; it mostly appears in 貴賓室 \"VIP room\" and 貴賓席 \"VIP box\". Related words: 来賓 \"invited guest at a ceremony\", 国賓 \"state guest\", 賓客 \"honored guest\". Very formal.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"アメリカ大統領が国賓として来日しました。","romaji":"Amerika daitōryō ga kokuhin toshite rainichi shimashita.","german":"The American president visited Japan as a state guest.","furigana":"だいとうりょう|こくひん|らいにち"},{"japanese":"国賓を迎えて宮中晩餐会が開かれた。","romaji":"Kokuhin o mukaete kyuuchuu bansankai ga hirakareta.","german":"A banquet was held at the Imperial Palace in honor of the state guest.","furigana":"こくひん|むか|きゅうちゅうばんさんかい|ひら"},{"japanese":"彼はまるで国賓のような待遇を受けた。","romaji":"Kare wa marude kokuhin no you na taiguu o uketa.","german":"He was treated like a state guest.","furigana":"かれ|こくひん|たいぐう|う"}],"notes":"国賓 is a state guest officially invited by the government, usually a head of state, who is received with the highest honors. Typical: 国賓として来日する, 国賓待遇, 国賓級; a 宮中晩餐会 is held for state guests. One step below is 公賓 \"official guest\". Figuratively, 国賓のような扱い is used for royal treatment.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"来賓の方々をはじめ、皆様にはお忙しい中、ご臨席を賜り誠にありがとうございます。","romaji":"Raihin no katagata o hajime, minasama ni wa o-isogashii naka, go-rinseki o tamawari makoto ni arigatou gozaimasu.","german":"Thank you very much to everyone, including our guests of honor, for attending despite your busy schedules.","furigana":"らいひん|かたがた|みなさま|いそが|なか|りんせき|たまわ|まこと"},{"japanese":"卒業式で来賓の市長が祝辞を述べた。","romaji":"Sotsugyoushiki de raihin no shichou ga shukuji o nobeta.","german":"At the graduation ceremony, the mayor, as a guest of honor, gave a congratulatory address.","furigana":"そつぎょうしき|らいひん|しちょう|しゅくじ|の"},{"japanese":"来賓席はステージの右側です。","romaji":"Raihinseki wa suteeji no migigawa desu.","german":"The guest-of-honor seats are on the right side of the stage.","furigana":"らいひんせき|みぎがわ"}],"notes":"来賓 are the specially invited guests at celebrations and ceremonies, such as school festivals, weddings or openings. Typical are ご来賓の皆様, 来賓席, 来賓祝辞 and 来賓のあいさつ. For customers or private visitors, one says お客様 or 客 instead. Formal, especially in speeches.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"海外の賓客を一流の和食でもてなしました。","romaji":"Kaigai no hinkyaku o ichiryū no washoku de motenashimashita.","german":"We entertained the foreign guest of honor with first-class Japanese cuisine.","furigana":"かいがい|ひんきゃく|いちりゅう|わしょく"},{"japanese":"国王は賓客として盛大に迎えられた。","romaji":"Kokuou wa hinkyaku toshite seidai ni mukaerareta.","german":"The king was welcomed with great pomp as a guest of honor.","furigana":"こくおう|ひんきゃく|せいだい|むか"},{"japanese":"この部屋は賓客をお迎えするための特別室です。","romaji":"Kono heya wa hinkyaku o omukae suru tame no tokubetsushitsu desu.","german":"This is a special room for receiving distinguished guests.","furigana":"へや|ひんきゃく|むか|とくべつしつ"}],"notes":"賓客 is an important guest received with honors (more rarely also read ひんかく). Typical: 賓客として迎える, 賓客をもてなす. The word is written language and elevated. By comparison: 国賓 is the official state guest, 来賓 the invited guest at a celebration, and 貴賓 occurs mainly in 貴賓室／貴賓席.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"旦那は出張で今週ずっと留守にしています。","romaji":"Danna wa shutchō de konshū zutto rusu ni shite imasu.","german":"My husband is away on a business trip all week.","furigana":"だんな|しゅっちょう|こんしゅう|るす"},{"japanese":"お隣の旦那さんは料理が上手らしい。","romaji":"Otonari no danna-san wa ryouri ga jouzu rashii.","german":"The husband next door is said to be a good cook.","furigana":"となり|だんな|りょうり|じょうず"},{"japanese":"「旦那、安くしておきますよ」と店の主人が声をかけた。","romaji":"\"Danna, yasuku shite okimasu yo\" to mise no shujin ga koe o kaketa.","german":"\"I'll give you a good price, sir,\" called the shop owner.","furigana":"だんな|やす|みせ|しゅじん|こえ"}],"notes":"旦那 today means mainly \"husband\" in familiar speech: うちの旦那 for one's own husband, 旦那さん／旦那様 for another person's husband. More formal are 夫 or 主人. Originally 旦那 was the master of the house or a patron (from the Sanskrit word for \"alms\"); merchants still jokingly address male customers as 旦那. It also refers to a geisha's patron.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"涙で輪郭が霞んで見えました。","romaji":"Namida de rinkaku ga kasunde miemashita.","german":"The outline looked blurred through my tears.","furigana":"なみだ|りんかく|かす|み"},{"japanese":"顔の輪郭に合った髪型を選びましょう。","romaji":"Kao no rinkaku ni atta kamigata o erabimashou.","german":"Let's choose a hairstyle that suits the shape of your face.","furigana":"かお|りんかく|あ|かみがた|えら"},{"japanese":"捜査が進み、事件の輪郭が見えてきた。","romaji":"Sousa ga susumi, jiken no rinkaku ga miete kita.","german":"As the investigation progressed, the outline of the case began to emerge.","furigana":"そうさ|すす|じけん|りんかく|み"}],"notes":"輪郭 is the outline or contour of a shape, especially 顔の輪郭 and 輪郭線 in drawing. Figuratively it refers to the basic features of a matter: 計画の輪郭, 事件の輪郭が見えてくる／はっきりする. For a written overview, one says 概要 instead; シルエット is the dark silhouette.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"警察が三日がかりでデモ隊を鎮圧しました。","romaji":"Keisatsu ga mikka-gakari de demotai o chin'atsu shimashita.","german":"The police took three days to put down the demonstrators.","furigana":"けいさつ|みっか|たい|ちんあつ"},{"japanese":"政府軍は反乱を武力で鎮圧した。","romaji":"Seifugun wa hanran o buryoku de chin'atsu shita.","german":"The government troops put down the rebellion by force.","furigana":"せいふぐん|はんらん|ぶりょく|ちんあつ"},{"japanese":"火災は約2時間後に鎮圧された。","romaji":"Kasai wa yaku nijikan go ni chin'atsu sareta.","german":"The fire was brought under control after about two hours.","furigana":"かさい|やく|じかんご|ちんあつ"}],"notes":"鎮圧 means to bring a rebellion, unrest or a fire under control by force or with determination; transitive with を: 反乱を鎮圧する, 暴動を武力で鎮圧する, passive 鎮圧される. For firefighters, 鎮圧 means \"under control\", before the final 鎮火 \"extinguished\". Unlike 弾圧 \"oppression (of people, opinions)\", 鎮圧 is directed against a specific event.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の会社は、業界の重鎮達を次々と役員に迎え入れた。","romaji":"Kare no kaisha wa, gyōkai no jūchintachi o tsugitsugi to yakuin ni mukaeireta.","german":"His company brought industry heavyweights onto its board one after another.","furigana":"かれ|かいしゃ|ぎょうかい|じゅうちんたち|つぎつぎ|やくいん|むか|い"},{"japanese":"彼女は料理界の重鎮として知られている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa ryourikai no juuchin toshite shirarete iru.","german":"She is known as a leading figure in the culinary world.","furigana":"かのじょ|りょうりかい|じゅうちん|し"},{"japanese":"党の重鎮たちが新しい総裁を支持した。","romaji":"Tou no juuchintachi ga atarashii sousai o shiji shita.","german":"The party's heavyweights supported the new president.","furigana":"とう|じゅうちん|あたら|そうさい|しじ"}],"notes":"重鎮 is an influential, respected leading figure in a field, usually an older, experienced person: 業界の重鎮, 政界の重鎮, 党の重鎮, 文壇の重鎮. The word is respectful and written language. More casual is 大物 \"big shot\"; 第一人者 stresses a top professional position.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"医者に鎮痛剤を注射してもらいました。","romaji":"Isha ni chintsūzai o chūsha shite moraimashita.","german":"I had the doctor give me a painkiller injection.","furigana":"いしゃ|ちんつうざい|ちゅうしゃ"},{"japanese":"頭痛がひどいので、鎮痛剤を飲みました。","romaji":"Zutsuu ga hidoi node, chintsuuzai o nomimashita.","german":"I had a terrible headache, so I took a painkiller.","furigana":"ずつう|ちんつうざい|の"},{"japanese":"鎮痛剤が切れてきたのか、また傷が痛み出した。","romaji":"Chintsuuzai ga kirete kita no ka, mata kizu ga itamidashita.","german":"The painkiller may be wearing off, as the wound has started to hurt again.","furigana":"ちんつうざい|き|きず|いた|だ"}],"notes":"鎮痛剤 is the technical word for \"painkiller\", as used on package inserts and by doctors; in everyday speech one mostly says 痛み止め. Tablets are taken with 飲む; injected, it is 注射する. Typical are 鎮痛剤が効く \"the painkiller works\" and 鎮痛剤が切れる \"the effect wears off\". To be distinguished: 解熱剤 \"fever reducer\" and 麻酔 \"anesthesia\" -- a 鎮痛剤 only relieves pain, it does not numb.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"プロの神髄を見せつけられました。","romaji":"Puro no shinzui o misetsukeraremashita.","german":"I was shown what being a professional truly means.","furigana":"しんずい|み"},{"japanese":"茶道の神髄は、もてなしの心にあると言われる。","romaji":"Sadou no shinzui wa, motenashi no kokoro ni aru to iwareru.","german":"It is said that the essence of the tea ceremony lies in the spirit of hospitality.","furigana":"さどう|しんずい|こころ|い"},{"japanese":"彼は一生をかけて武道の神髄を極めた。","romaji":"Kare wa isshou o kakete budou no shinzui o kiwameta.","german":"He devoted his whole life to mastering the essence of the martial arts.","furigana":"かれ|いっしょう|ぶどう|しんずい|きわ"}],"notes":"神髄 (also written 真髄) is the innermost core, the true \"essence\" of an art, teaching or skill -- an elevated, rather written word. Typical expressions: 〜の神髄を極める \"to master the essence of ... to the fullest\", 〜の神髄に触れる \"to come into contact with the essence of ...\", 神髄を見せる. Unlike the neutral 本質 \"essence, nature of a thing\", admiration resonates: it means the highest that a master attains.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"事故で脊髄に傷がついてしまったのです。","romaji":"Jiko de sekizui ni kizu ga tsuite shimatta no desu.","german":"My spinal cord was injured in the accident.","furigana":"じこ|せきずい|きず"},{"japanese":"熱いものに触って手を引っ込めるのは、脊髄反射だ。","romaji":"Atsui mono ni sawatte te o hikkomeru no wa, sekizui hansha da.","german":"Pulling your hand back when you touch something hot is a spinal reflex.","furigana":"あつ|さわ|て|ひ|こ|せきずいはんしゃ"},{"japanese":"記事をよく読まずに、脊髄反射で反論する人が多い。","romaji":"Kiji o yoku yomazu ni, sekizui hansha de hanron suru hito ga ooi.","german":"Many people rebut by reflex without reading the article properly.","furigana":"きじ|よ|せきずいはんしゃ|はんろん|ひと|おお"}],"notes":"脊髄 is the \"spinal cord\" in the vertebral canal -- not the spine itself, which is 背骨 or, technically, 脊椎 or 脊柱. Common compounds: 脊髄損傷 \"spinal cord injury\" and 脊髄反射 \"spinal reflex\"; the latter is also used colloquially for thoughtless, reflexive reactions, especially on the internet. Not to be confused with 骨髄 \"bone marrow\" (骨髄移植).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の大好物はフグの唐揚げです。","romaji":"Watashi no daikōbutsu wa fugu no karaage desu.","german":"My absolute favorite dish is deep-fried pufferfish.","furigana":"わたし|だいこうぶつ|からあ"},{"japanese":"お弁当に唐揚げを入れると、子どもが喜ぶ。","romaji":"Obentō ni karaage o ireru to, kodomo ga yorokobu.","german":"Kids are happy when I put karaage in their bento.","furigana":"べんとう|からあ|い|こ|よろこ"},{"japanese":"居酒屋で、とりあえずビールと唐揚げを注文した。","romaji":"Izakaya de, toriaezu bīru to karaage o chūmon shita.","german":"At the izakaya, we ordered beer and karaage first of all.","furigana":"いざかや|からあ|ちゅうもん"}],"notes":"唐揚げ is a deep-fried dish in which marinated pieces are lightly coated in flour or potato starch and fried; without further specification it almost always means chicken (鶏の唐揚げ), but there are also タコの唐揚げ and フグの唐揚げ. Spellings: 唐揚げ, から揚げ, 空揚げ. Unlike 天ぷら, it has no liquid batter, and unlike フライ, no breading. Popular in bento, at izakaya and as home cooking.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼らは敵に降伏するよりも、集団自決を選んだとされています。","romaji":"Karera wa teki ni kōfuku suru yori mo, shūdan jiketsu o eranda to sarete imasu.","german":"They are said to have chosen mass suicide rather than surrender to the enemy.","furigana":"かれ|てき|こうふく|しゅうだんじけつ|えら"},{"japanese":"第一次世界大戦後、民族自決の考え方が広まった。","romaji":"Dai-ichiji sekai taisen-go, minzoku jiketsu no kangaekata ga hiromatta.","german":"After World War I, the idea of national self-determination spread.","furigana":"だいいちじせかいたいせんご|みんぞくじけつ|かんが|かた|ひろ"},{"japanese":"敗戦を知った将校は、その夜自決した。","romaji":"Haisen o shitta shōkō wa, sono yoru jiketsu shita.","german":"The officer took his own life that very night when he learned of the defeat.","furigana":"はいせん|し|しょうこう|よるじけつ"}],"notes":"自決 has two meanings: politically \"self-determination\", especially in 民族自決 \"the right of peoples to self-determination\", and \"taking one's own life\", especially out of a sense of honor or to avoid capture (集団自決 in the Battle of Okinawa in 1945). The second use is a historical, formal expression; neutrally one speaks of 自殺. The verb 自決する almost always refers to suicide, while self-determination appears as a noun. Serious, written register.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"みんな、彼の非凡な才能を妬んでいたんだと思います。","romaji":"Minna, kare no hibon na sainō o netande ita n da to omoimasu.","german":"I think everyone was jealous of his extraordinary talent.","furigana":"かれ|ひぼん|さいのう|ねた|おも"},{"japanese":"彼女は幼い頃から、音楽に非凡な才能を示した。","romaji":"Kanojo wa osanai koro kara, ongaku ni hibon na sainou o shimeshita.","german":"Since she was little, she showed extraordinary talent for music.","furigana":"かのじょ|おさな|ころ|おんがく|ひぼん|さいのう|しめ"},{"japanese":"監督は、この新人選手の非凡さをすぐに見抜いた。","romaji":"Kantoku wa, kono shinjin senshu no hibonsa o sugu ni minuita.","german":"The manager immediately recognized this rookie player's exceptional ability.","furigana":"かんとく|しんじんせんしゅ|ひぼん|みぬ"}],"notes":"非凡 (\"not ordinary\") is the elevated counterpart of 平凡 and praises outstanding talent. It almost always stands attributively with な before words like 才能, 能力, センス: 非凡な才能 \"extraordinary talent\"; as a noun 非凡さ. Predicatively (〜は非凡だ) it is rarer. In conversation one says すごい or 並外れた rather; not to be confused with 非常 \"emergency; extremely\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"平凡な毎日に退屈していたんです。","romaji":"Heibon na mainichi ni taikutsu shite ita n desu.","german":"I was bored with my ordinary daily life.","furigana":"へいぼん|まいにち|たいくつ"},{"japanese":"私は平凡な家庭に生まれ育ちました。","romaji":"Watashi wa heibon na katei ni umaresodachimashita.","german":"I was born and raised in an ordinary family.","furigana":"わたし|へいぼん|かてい|う|そだ"},{"japanese":"そのアイデアは悪くないが、少し平凡すぎる。","romaji":"Sono aidea wa warukunai ga, sukoshi heibon sugiru.","german":"The idea is not bad, but it is a little too ordinary.","furigana":"わる|すこ|へいぼん"}],"notes":"平凡 means \"ordinary, everyday, with nothing special\": 平凡な人生, 平凡な毎日, 平凡なサラリーマン. Unlike the neutral 普通 \"normal, usual\", 平凡 stresses the lack of brilliance and can be slightly derogatory (\"unimaginative\"). Often, however, it is also meant positively, as quiet, modest happiness: 平凡が一番. Opposite: 非凡; much more derogatory is 凡庸.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は凡庸な男でしたが、愛に満ちあふれていました。","romaji":"Kare wa bon'you na otoko deshita ga, ai ni michiafurete imashita.","german":"He was a mediocre man, but he was full of love.","furigana":"かれ|ぼんよう|おとこ|あい|み"},{"japanese":"批評家は、その小説を「凡庸な作品」と切り捨てた。","romaji":"Hihyouka wa, sono shousetsu o \"bon'you na sakuhin\" to kirisuteta.","german":"The critic dismissed the novel as a \"mediocre work\".","furigana":"ひひょうか|しょうせつ|ぼんよう|さくひん|き|す"},{"japanese":"凡庸な指導者では、この危機は乗り越えられない。","romaji":"Bon'you na shidousha de wa, kono kiki wa norikoerarenai.","german":"With mediocre leadership, this crisis cannot be overcome.","furigana":"ぼんよう|しどうしゃ|きき|の|こ"}],"notes":"凡庸 \"mediocre, without special talent\" is elevated and clearly more derogatory than 平凡. While 平凡 rather describes a quiet, unremarkable life, 凡庸 judges abilities or achievements: 凡庸な作品, 凡庸な政治家, 凡庸な才能. Typical in reviews and commentary; in conversation ありきたり or 大したことない would be more usual. Opposite: 非凡, 卓越した.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は呉服屋の一人息子でした。","romaji":"Kare wa gofukuya no hitori musuko deshita.","german":"He was the only son of a kimono merchant.","furigana":"かれ|ごふくや|ひとりむすこ"},{"japanese":"この百貨店は、もともと江戸時代の呉服店だった。","romaji":"Kono hyakkaten wa, motomoto Edo jidai no gofukuten datta.","german":"This department store was originally a kimono shop from the Edo period.","furigana":"ひゃっかてん|えどじだい|ごふくてん"},{"japanese":"成人式の着物は、祖母が呉服屋で選んでくれた。","romaji":"Seijinshiki no kimono wa, sobo ga gofukuya de erande kureta.","german":"My grandmother chose the kimono for my coming-of-age ceremony at the kimono shop.","furigana":"せいじんしき|きもの|そぼ|ごふくや|えら"}],"notes":"呉服 refers to fabrics for Japanese clothing, especially kimono fabrics, and in a wider sense the kimono as merchandise. It is most often found in 呉服屋 or 呉服店 \"kimono shop\"; large department stores such as 三越 or 大丸 started out as 呉服店. Western clothing, by contrast, is 洋服, and fabric in general is 生地 or 布. The name recalls the ancient Chinese state 呉, from which the art of weaving came.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ライバル社同士が呉越同舟で共同プロジェクトに取り組んだ。","romaji":"Raibaru-sha dōshi ga goetsu dōshū de kyōdō purojekuto ni torikunda.","german":"Rivalisierende Firmen arbeiteten als Feinde im selben Boot an einem gemeinsamen Projekt.","furigana":"しゃどうし|ごえつどうしゅう|きょうどう|と|く"},{"japanese":"政治の世界では呉越同舟も珍しくない。","romaji":"Seiji no sekai de wa goetsu dōshū mo mezurashiku nai.","german":"In der Welt der Politik ist es nicht ungewöhnlich, dass Rivalen zusammenarbeiten.","furigana":"せいじ|せかい|ごえつどうしゅう|めずら"},{"japanese":"仲の悪い二人が同じ班になるなんて、まさに呉越同舟だね。","romaji":"Naka no warui futari ga onaji han ni naru nante, masa ni goetsu dōshū da ne.","german":"Dass ausgerechnet die beiden Streithähne in dieselbe Gruppe kommen – das sind wirklich ungleiche Bettgenossen.","furigana":"なか|わる|ふたり|おな|はん|ごえつどうしゅう"}],"notes":"Aus dem Sunzi: Die verfeindeten Staaten 呉 und 越 sitzen im selben Boot, und als ein Sturm aufkommt, müssen ihre Leute zusammenarbeiten. 呉越同舟 bezeichnet Rivalen oder Feinde, die zufällig zusammenkommen oder aus Not kooperieren. Gebräuchlich als Prädikat (まさに呉越同舟だ) oder adverbial mit で. Verwandt ist das deutsche „ungleiche Bettgenossen“; ohne Feindschaft spricht man einfach von 協力 oder 手を組む.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"本日は、皇太子殿下御生誕の嘉日なり。","romaji":"Honjitsu wa, kōtaishi denka go seitan no kajitsu nari.","german":"Today is the auspicious day of the birth of the Crown Prince.","furigana":"ほんじつ|こうたいしでんかごせいたん|かじつ"},{"japanese":"本日の嘉日を迎え、心よりお祝い申し上げます。","romaji":"Honjitsu no kajitsu o mukae, kokoro yori oiwai moushiagemasu.","german":"On this festive day, I offer my heartfelt congratulations.","furigana":"ほんじつ|かじつ|むか|こころ|いわ|もう|あ"},{"japanese":"祖父母は嘉日を選んで、新しい家に引っ越した。","romaji":"Sofubo wa kajitsu o erande, atarashii ie ni hikkoshita.","german":"My grandparents chose a lucky day to move into the new house.","furigana":"そふぼ|かじつ|えら|あたら|いえ|ひ|こ"}],"notes":"嘉日 \"auspicious, festive day\" is very elevated and archaic; it is found in celebratory speeches, congratulatory formulas and older texts. 嘉 means \"good, happy\". More common are 吉日 (きちじつ, e.g. in the letter date 〇月吉日) and the identically read 佳日 \"beautiful day\". Beware of the homophone 過日 \"the other day, a few days ago\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いつか自分の劇団を主宰するのが夢です。","romaji":"Itsuka jibun no gekidan o shusai suru no ga yume desu.","german":"My dream is to run my own theater company someday.","furigana":"じぶん|げきだん|しゅさい|ゆめ"},{"japanese":"彼女は二十年間、俳句の会を主宰してきた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa nijuunenkan, haiku no kai o shusai shite kita.","german":"She has led a haiku circle for twenty years.","furigana":"かのじょ|にじゅうねんかん|はいく|かい|しゅさい"},{"japanese":"この劇団の主宰は、脚本も演出も自分で手がけている。","romaji":"Kono gekidan no shusai wa, kyakuhon mo enshutsu mo jibun de tegakete iru.","german":"The head of this theater company writes the plays and directs them himself.","furigana":"げきだん|しゅさい|きゃくほん|えんしゅつ|じぶん|て"}],"notes":"主宰 means to lead a group as its founder and head -- typical of theater companies, haiku circles, study groups or art schools: 劇団を主宰する. The person himself or herself is also called 主宰 (劇団の主宰). Not to be confused with the identically read 主催 \"to host an event\" (イベントを主催する). For companies one says 経営する, for meetings 司会する or 議長を務める.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"英国宰相から直々に手紙を頂きました。","romaji":"Eikoku saishō kara jikijiki ni tegami o itadakimashita.","german":"Ich erhielt persönlich einen Brief vom britischen Premierminister.","furigana":"えいこくさいしょう|じきじき|てがみ|いただ"},{"japanese":"ビスマルクは「鉄血宰相」と呼ばれた。","romaji":"Bisumaruku wa \"tekketsu saishou\" to yobareta.","german":"Bismarck wurde der „Eiserne Kanzler“ genannt.","furigana":"てっけつさいしょう|よ"},{"japanese":"彼には一国の宰相としての器がない。","romaji":"Kare ni wa ikkoku no saishou to shite no utsuwa ga nai.","german":"Ihm fehlt das Format, ein Land als Regierungschef zu führen.","furigana":"かれ|いっこく|さいしょう|うつわ"}],"notes":"宰相 ist ein gehobenes, etwas altertümliches Wort für den Regierungschef. Der japanische Premierminister heißt heute offiziell 内閣総理大臣, kurz 首相; 宰相 findet man in Geschichtsdarstellungen, Biografien, Leitartikeln und für ausländische Regierungschefs, etwa den deutschen Kanzler (鉄血宰相ビスマルク). Das Wort klingt würdevoll und betont staatsmännisches Format: 一国の宰相.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"赤ん坊の泣き声が静寂を破った。","romaji":"Akanbō no nakigoe ga seijaku o yabutta.","german":"The baby's crying broke the silence.","furigana":"あか|ぼう|な|ごえ|せいじゃく|やぶ"},{"japanese":"雪の降る夜、町は静寂に包まれていた。","romaji":"Yuki no furu yoru, machi wa seijaku ni tsutsumarete ita.","german":"On the snowy night, the town was wrapped in silence.","furigana":"ゆき|ふ|よる|まち|せいじゃく|つつ"},{"japanese":"森の静寂の中で、鳥の声だけが聞こえた。","romaji":"Mori no seijaku no naka de, tori no koe dake ga kikoeta.","german":"In the stillness of the forest, only the birds could be heard.","furigana":"もり|せいじゃく|なか|とり|こえ|き"}],"notes":"静寂 is the deep, solemn \"stillness\" of a place -- elevated and literary. Fixed expressions: 静寂を破る \"to break the silence\", 静寂に包まれる \"to be wrapped in silence\", 夜の静寂. In poetry it is also read しじま. In everyday speech one says 静けさ or simply 静か; 沈黙, by contrast, means the \"silence\" of people.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大尉の死は決して無駄ではなかった。","romaji":"Taii no shi wa kesshite muda de wa nakatta.","german":"The captain's death was by no means in vain.","furigana":"たいい|し|けっ|むだ"},{"japanese":"彼は二十八歳で大尉に昇進した。","romaji":"Kare wa nijuuhassai de taii ni shoushin shita.","german":"He was promoted to captain at the age of twenty-eight.","furigana":"かれ|にじゅうはっさい|たいい|しょうしん"},{"japanese":"「了解しました、田中大尉！」","romaji":"\"Ryoukai shimashita, Tanaka taii!\"","german":"\"Understood, Captain Tanaka!\"","furigana":"りょうかい|たなかたいい"}],"notes":"大尉 is an officer rank: \"captain\" in the army, \"lieutenant\" in the navy. The company-grade ranks rise 少尉 < 中尉 < 大尉, followed by 少佐 and so on. The Japan Self-Defense Forces officially call this rank 一尉; 大尉 is therefore found mainly in historical texts, in foreign armies and in films. As a form of address it follows the name: 田中大尉.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の曽祖父は、旧日本陸軍少尉でした。","romaji":"Watashi no sōsofu wa, kyū Nippon rikugun shōi deshita.","german":"My great-grandfather was a second lieutenant in the former Imperial Japanese Army.","furigana":"わたし|そうそふ|きゅうにっぽんりくぐんしょうい"},{"japanese":"彼は士官学校を卒業して、少尉に任官した。","romaji":"Kare wa shikan gakkō o sotsugyō shite, shōi ni ninkan shita.","german":"After graduating from military academy, he was commissioned as a second lieutenant.","furigana":"かれ|しかんがっこう|そつぎょう|しょうい|にんかん"},{"japanese":"映画の主人公は、若い海軍少尉だった。","romaji":"Eiga no shujinkō wa, wakai kaigun shōi datta.","german":"The hero of the film was a young naval ensign.","furigana":"えいが|しゅじんこう|わか|かいぐんしょうい"}],"notes":"少尉 is the lowest officer rank, \"second lieutenant\" (in the navy \"ensign\"). Above it come 中尉 \"first lieutenant\" and 大尉 \"captain\", then 少佐, 中佐 and 大佐. The Japan Self-Defense Forces use different rank names (三尉), so 少尉 is found mainly in historical texts, in foreign armies, in films and in anime. As a form of address, the rank is attached to the name: 山田少尉.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"我が軍の中尉が敵軍に拉致され、処刑されました。","romaji":"Waga gun no chuui ga tekigun ni rachi sare, shokei saremashita.","german":"Our army's first lieutenant was abducted by the enemy and executed.","furigana":"わ|ぐん|ちゅうい|てきぐん|らち|しょけい"},{"japanese":"祖父は戦時中、海軍の中尉だった。","romaji":"Sofu wa senjichuu, kaigun no chuui datta.","german":"My grandfather was a first lieutenant in the navy during the war.","furigana":"そふ|せんじちゅう|かいぐん|ちゅうい"},{"japanese":"少尉から中尉に昇進するまで、二年かかった。","romaji":"Shoui kara chuui ni shoushin suru made, ninen kakatta.","german":"It took two years to be promoted from second lieutenant to first lieutenant.","furigana":"しょうい|ちゅうい|しょうしん|にねん"}],"notes":"中尉 is the \"first lieutenant\" (navy: \"lieutenant junior grade\"), the middle lieutenant rank between 少尉 \"second lieutenant\" and 大尉 \"captain\". In today's Self-Defense Forces the rank is called 二尉, so 中尉 belongs to historical and foreign military contexts. Note: read the same as 注意 \"caution, attention\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は、美しく、尚且つ頭も良いが、性格は悪い。","romaji":"Kanojo wa, utsukushiku, naokatsu atama mo yoi ga, seikaku wa warui.","german":"She is beautiful and also smart, but she has a bad personality.","furigana":"かのじょ|うつく|なおか|あたま|よ|せいかく|わる"},{"japanese":"安くて、なおかつ栄養のある料理を考えています。","romaji":"Yasukute, naokatsu eiyou no aru ryouri o kangaete imasu.","german":"I'm thinking up dishes that are cheap and at the same time nutritious.","furigana":"やす|えいよう|りょうり|かんが"},{"japanese":"何度失敗しても、それでもなおかつ挑戦し続けることが大切だ。","romaji":"Nando shippai shite mo, soredemo naokatsu chousen shitsuzukeru koto ga taisetsu da.","german":"No matter how many times you fail, it is important to keep challenging yourself all the same.","furigana":"なんどしっぱい|ちょうせん|つづ|たいせつ"}],"notes":"なおかつ connects two qualities or conditions that are met at the same time: \"and in addition, and at the same time\" -- 安くて、なおかつ美味しい. It also means \"and yet, all the same\", often strengthened as それでもなおかつ. Mostly written in kana; 尚且つ seems old-fashioned. It is somewhat more formal than そして or しかも, though しかも stresses an escalation, while なおかつ stresses that both points are met at once.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ここのうどんは、この庶民的な味がいいんだよ。","romaji":"Koko no udon wa, kono shominteki na aji ga ii n da yo.","german":"What's good about the udon here is this simple, unpretentious flavor.","furigana":"しょみんてき|あじ"},{"japanese":"下町には、庶民的な商店街が今も残っている。","romaji":"Shitamachi ni wa, shominteki na shoutengai ga ima mo nokotte iru.","german":"In the old downtown area, down-to-earth shopping streets still remain today.","furigana":"したまち|しょみんてき|しょうてんがい|いま|のこ"},{"japanese":"新しい大臣は、庶民的な人柄で人気がある。","romaji":"Atarashii daijin wa, shominteki na hitogara de ninki ga aru.","german":"The new minister is popular for his down-to-earth personality.","furigana":"あたら|だいじん|しょみんてき|ひとがら|にんき"}],"notes":"庶民的 means \"down-to-earth, unpretentious, affordable for ordinary people\" and is mostly positive: 庶民的な店, 庶民的な味, 庶民的な値段, 庶民的な雰囲気. For people it means \"approachable, without airs\": 庶民的な政治家. Opposite: 高級, 上品, 貴族的. 庶民 alone is \"the common people\"; with 的 it becomes a な-adjective.","levelNote":"Compound: 庶民 (N1) + 的 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"学生時代は弦楽部でチェロを弾いていました。","romaji":"Gakusei jidai wa gengakubu de chero o hiite imashita.","german":"In my school days, I played cello in the strings club.","furigana":"がくせいじだい|げんがくぶ|ひ"},{"japanese":"今夜は、モーツァルトの弦楽四重奏を聴きに行きます。","romaji":"Kon'ya wa, Moutsaruto no gengaku shijuusou o kiki ni ikimasu.","german":"Tonight I'm going to hear a Mozart string quartet.","furigana":"こんや|げんがくしじゅうそう|き|い"},{"japanese":"この映画の音楽は、弦楽の響きがとても美しい。","romaji":"Kono eiga no ongaku wa, gengaku no hibiki ga totemo utsukushii.","german":"The strings sound in this film's music is very beautiful.","furigana":"えいが|おんがく|げんがく|ひび|うつく"}],"notes":"弦楽 is music for stringed instruments (violin, viola, cello, double bass) and mostly appears in compounds: 弦楽四重奏 \"string quartet\", 弦楽合奏 \"string ensemble\", 弦楽部 \"strings club\" at schools. 弦楽器 means \"stringed instrument\" more generally and also includes guitar or koto. Counterpart: 管楽 \"wind music\". On its own as an object (弦楽をやる) it sounds unusual; one rather says バイオリンを習う.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"インフルエンザによる高熱で、体が怠いんです。","romaji":"Infuruenza ni yoru kōnetsu de, karada ga darui n desu.","german":"My body feels sluggish because of the high fever from the flu.","furigana":"こうねつ|からだ|だる"},{"japanese":"一日中立ち仕事をしたので、足がだるい。","romaji":"Ichinichijū tachishigoto o shita node, ashi ga darui.","german":"I worked on my feet all day, so my legs feel heavy.","furigana":"いちにちじゅうた|しごと|あし"},{"japanese":"月曜の朝は、会社に行くのがだるい。","romaji":"Getsuyō no asa wa, kaisha ni iku no ga darui.","german":"On Monday mornings, I really don't feel like going to work.","furigana":"げつよう|あさ|かいしゃ|い"}],"notes":"だるい describes a physical feeling of heaviness and lethargy, for example with fever, exhaustion or heat: 体がだるい, 足がだるい. The kanji spelling 怠い is rare; it is almost always written in kana (怠 is known from 怠ける \"to be lazy\"). Colloquially, especially among younger people, だるい also means \"tiresome, not in the mood\": 学校だるい. Unlike 疲れた, だるい stresses a diffuse malaise; the noun is だるさ.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は会社で一番の怠け者だが、一番成績を上げている。","romaji":"Kare wa kaisha de ichiban no namakemono da ga, ichiban seiseki o agete iru.","german":"He is the laziest person in the company, but he has the best results.","furigana":"かれ|かいしゃ|いちばん|なま|もの|いちばんせいせき|あ"},{"japanese":"怠け者だと思われたくないので、毎朝早く出社している。","romaji":"Namakemono da to omowaretakunai node, maiasa hayaku shussha shite iru.","german":"I don't want to be seen as lazy, so I come to the office early every morning.","furigana":"なま|もの|おも|まいあさはや|しゅっしゃ"},{"japanese":"「この怠け者！いつまで寝ているの？」","romaji":"\"Kono namakemono! Itsu made nete iru no?\"","german":"\"You lazybones! How long are you going to sleep?\"","furigana":"なま|もの|ね"}],"notes":"怠け者 is a \"lazybones\", someone who shirks work - clearly derogatory. It comes from the verb 怠ける \"to be idle, to shirk\". More colloquial are ぐうたら or 面倒くさがり. In katakana, ナマケモノ refers to the sloth. A well-known proverb: 怠け者の節句働き \"lazybones work on holidays of all days\".","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 19 (source: N4; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二人は神前で恭しく頭を下げた。","romaji":"Futari wa shinzen de uyauyashiku atama o sageta.","german":"The two bowed reverently before the altar.","furigana":"ふたり|しんぜん|うやうや|あたま|さ"},{"japanese":"執事は恭しく手紙を差し出した。","romaji":"Shitsuji wa uyauyashiku tegami o sashidashita.","german":"The butler presented the letter with a respectful gesture.","furigana":"しつじ|うやうや|てがみ|さ|だ"},{"japanese":"優勝者は、恭しい態度で賞状を受け取った。","romaji":"Yuushousha wa, uyauyashii taido de shoujou o uketotta.","german":"The winner received the certificate with a respectful attitude.","furigana":"ゆうしょうしゃ|うやうや|たいど|しょうじょう|う|と"}],"notes":"恭しい describes reverent, solemnly respectful behavior, especially toward gods, superiors or sacred things. It mostly appears adverbially: 恭しく頭を下げる, 恭しく差し出す, 恭しく受け取る. The word is written language and elevated; in everyday speech one says 丁寧に or 礼儀正しく. Unlike 丁寧 \"careful, polite\", 恭しい stresses reverence and an often ceremonial gesture.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ご満悦のようですね。","romaji":"Go man'etsu no yō desu ne.","german":"You seem quite delighted.","furigana":"まんえつ"},{"japanese":"孫にプレゼントをもらって、祖父はご満悦だった。","romaji":"Mago ni purezento o moratte, sofu wa go man'etsu datta.","german":"Grandfather was delighted to receive a present from his grandchild.","furigana":"まご|そふ|まんえつ"},{"japanese":"猫はひなたで寝そべって、ご満悦の表情だ。","romaji":"Neko wa hinata de nesobette, go man'etsu no hyoujou da.","german":"The cat is sprawled out in the sun with a thoroughly contented look.","furigana":"ねこ|ね|まんえつ|ひょうじょう"}],"notes":"満悦 \"great pleasure, satisfaction\" almost always appears as ご満悦: ご満悦だ, ご満悦の様子, ご満悦の表情. It is often said slightly ironically about someone visibly pleased with themselves and the world -- also about children or pets. The verb form 満悦する is rare and written language. Neutral alternatives would be 大満足 or とても喜んでいる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"すでに脈拍が止まっている。","romaji":"Sudeni myakuhaku ga tomatte iru.","german":"There is already no pulse.","furigana":"みゃくはく|と"},{"japanese":"看護師が血圧と脈拍を測ってくれた。","romaji":"Kangoshi ga ketsuatsu to myakuhaku o hakatte kureta.","german":"The nurse measured my blood pressure and pulse.","furigana":"かんごし|けつあつ|みゃくはく|はか"},{"japanese":"走った後、脈拍が一分間に百五十まで上がった。","romaji":"Hashitta ato, myakuhaku ga ippunkan ni hyakugojuu made agatta.","german":"After running, my pulse rose to 150 per minute.","furigana":"はし|あと|みゃくはく|いっぷんかん|ひゃくごじゅう|あ"}],"notes":"脈拍 is the \"pulse\" or pulse rate -- factual and medical, for example at the doctor's or on measuring devices. Typical: 脈拍を測る, 脈拍が速い, 脈拍が乱れる, 脈拍数. In everyday speech one usually says the shorter 脈: 脈をとる \"to take the pulse\". Similar is 心拍 \"heartbeat\" (心拍数 on fitness watches).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"レモンを唐揚げに搾ってもいいですか？","romaji":"Remon o karaage ni shibotte mo ii desu ka?","german":"May I squeeze lemon over the fried chicken?","furigana":"からあ|しぼ"},{"japanese":"牧場で牛の乳を搾る体験をした。","romaji":"Bokujō de ushi no chichi o shiboru taiken o shita.","german":"At the ranch, I had the experience of milking a cow.","furigana":"ぼくじょう|うし|ちち|しぼ|たいけん"},{"japanese":"昔の領主は、農民から重い年貢を搾り取った。","romaji":"Mukashi no ryōshu wa, nōmin kara omoi nengu o shiboritotta.","german":"In the past, feudal lords extorted heavy taxes from the peasants.","furigana":"むかし|りょうしゅ|のうみん|おも||しぼ|と"}],"notes":"搾る means to press liquid out by pressure: レモンを搾る, 牛の乳を搾る \"to milk\", 油を搾る \"to press oil\". Figuratively it means \"to exploit, to extort\", e.g. 税金を搾り取る (cf. 搾取). The identically read 絞る is used for wringing out (タオルを絞る), narrowing down (候補を絞る), turning down (音量を絞る) and 知恵を絞る; 搾る is used only when juice, milk, oil or money is extracted. Transitive godan verb; noun 搾り, as in 一番搾り.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"牧場で搾乳体験をしてきました。","romaji":"Bokujō de sakunyū taiken o shite kimashita.","german":"I tried milking at a ranch.","furigana":"ぼくじょう|さくにゅうたいけん"},{"japanese":"この牧場では、一日二回機械で搾乳している。","romaji":"Kono bokujou de wa, ichinichi nikai kikai de sakunyuu shite iru.","german":"On this ranch, cows are milked by machine twice a day.","furigana":"ぼくじょう|いちにちにかいきかい|さくにゅう"},{"japanese":"職場に復帰してからは、休憩時間に搾乳しています。","romaji":"Shokuba ni fukki shite kara wa, kyuukei jikan ni sakunyuu shite imasu.","german":"Since returning to work, I pump milk during my breaks.","furigana":"しょくば|ふっき|きゅうけいじかん|さくにゅう"}],"notes":"搾乳 is the \"milking\" of cows, goats and so on -- a technical agricultural word (搾乳機 \"milking machine\", 搾乳体験). It also refers to pumping breast milk (搾乳器 \"breast pump\"). Colloquially one says 乳を搾る or 乳搾り. As a verb it is used with or without an object: 牛を搾乳する, 一日二回搾乳する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日の最高気温は、摂氏二十五度です。","romaji":"Kyou no saikou kion wa, sesshi nijuugo do desu.","german":"Today's high temperature is twenty-five degrees Celsius.","furigana":"きょう|さいこうきおん|せっしにじゅうごど"},{"japanese":"水は摂氏百度で沸騰する。","romaji":"Mizu wa sesshi hyaku do de futtou suru.","german":"Water boils at one hundred degrees Celsius.","furigana":"みず|せっしひゃくど|ふっとう"},{"japanese":"アメリカでは、気温を摂氏ではなく華氏で表す。","romaji":"Amerika de wa, kion o sesshi de wa naku kashi de arawasu.","german":"In America, temperature is expressed in Fahrenheit, not Celsius.","furigana":"きおん|せっし|かし|あらわ"}],"notes":"摂氏 means \"Celsius\"; it goes back to the Chinese transcription of the name Celsius. It comes before the number: 摂氏二十五度, symbol ℃. In everyday speech it is usually omitted and one simply says 二十五度, because Celsius is the standard in Japan; 摂氏 serves mainly to distinguish from 華氏 \"Fahrenheit\" and in factual, scientific texts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"誰も自然の摂理には逆らえないよ。","romaji":"Dare mo shizen no setsuri ni wa sakaraenai yo.","german":"No one can defy the laws of nature.","furigana":"だれ|しぜん|せつり|さか"},{"japanese":"すべては神の摂理によるものだと、彼女は信じている。","romaji":"Subete wa kami no setsuri ni yoru mono da to, kanojo wa shinjite iru.","german":"She believes that everything happens by the providence of God.","furigana":"かみ|せつり|かのじょ|しん"},{"japanese":"親が子を守ろうとするのは、生命の摂理なのだろう。","romaji":"Oya ga ko o mamorou to suru no wa, seimei no setsuri na no darou.","german":"Parents wanting to protect their children is probably a law of life.","furigana":"おや|こ|まも|せいめい|せつり"}],"notes":"摂理 refers on the one hand to the ordering regularity of nature -- 自然の摂理 \"the course of nature, natural law\" -- and on the other, in a religious sense, to divine \"providence\" (神の摂理). The word is elevated; typical verbs are 逆らう and 従う. Unlike 法則, a scientifically formulated law, 摂理 means an inescapable, larger order, such as that of coming into being and passing away.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「智に働けば角が立つ」は、漱石の『草枕』の有名な一節だ。","romaji":"\"Chi ni hatarakeba kado ga tatsu\" wa, Souseki no \"Kusamakura\" no yuumei na issetsu da.","german":"\"If you go by reason alone, you will clash with others\" is a famous passage from Sōseki's \"Kusamakura\".","furigana":"ち|はたら|かど|た|そうせき|くさまくら|ゆうめい|いっせつ"},{"japanese":"智・仁・勇は、儒教で重んじられた三つの徳である。","romaji":"Chi, jin, yuu wa, jukyou de omonjirareta mittsu no toku de aru.","german":"Wisdom, benevolence and courage are the three virtues prized in Confucianism.","furigana":"ち|じん|ゆう|じゅきょう|おも|みっ|とく"},{"japanese":"人類の智を結集して、この難題に挑まなければならない。","romaji":"Jinrui no chi o kesshuu shite, kono nandai ni idomanakereba naranai.","german":"We must gather the wisdom of humankind and take on this difficult problem.","furigana":"じんるい|ち|けっしゅう|なんだい|いど"}],"notes":"智 (ち) \"wisdom, intelligence\" is elevated and rare as a single word; today 知 is mostly written instead. It is known from 智・仁・勇, the three Confucian virtues \"wisdom, benevolence, courage\", and from the opening of Natsume Sōseki's 草枕: 智に働けば角が立つ \"if you go by reason alone, you will clash with others\". The character appears more often in compounds such as 機智 \"wit, quick-wittedness\" and 叡智 \"profound wisdom\"; in Buddhism it refers to higher knowledge.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"おじいさんは、山で柴刈りをしていました。","romaji":"Ojiisan wa, yama de shibakari o shite imashita.","german":"The old man was gathering brushwood in the mountains.","furigana":"やま|しばか"},{"japanese":"昔は、山で集めた柴を燃やして風呂を沸かしていた。","romaji":"Mukashi wa, yama de atsumeta shiba o moyashite furo o wakashite ita.","german":"In the old days, people heated bathwater by burning brushwood collected in the mountains.","furigana":"むかし|やま|あつ|しば|も|ふろ|わ"},{"japanese":"うちの柴犬は散歩が大好きだ。","romaji":"Uchi no shibainu wa sanpo ga daisuki da.","german":"Our Shiba Inu loves walks.","furigana":"しばいぬ|さんぽ|だいす"}],"notes":"柴 means \"brushwood\", thin branches and undergrowth that people used to collect in the mountains as firewood. It is known from the beginning of the fairy tale 桃太郎: おじいさんは山へ柴刈りに -- 柴刈り is thus \"gathering brushwood\", not \"mowing the lawn\"; that would be 芝刈り with the identically read 芝 \"lawn\". Today the character appears mainly in 柴犬 \"Shiba Inu\" and in family names such as 柴田.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ニッカは柴犬ではなく珍島犬です。","romaji":"Nikka wa shibainu de wa naku Chindoken desu.","german":"Nikka is not a Shiba but a Jindo dog.","furigana":"しばいぬ|"},{"japanese":"柴犬は飼い主に忠実だが、頑固なところもある。","romaji":"Shibainu wa kainushi ni chūjitsu da ga, ganko na tokoro mo aru.","german":"Shibas are loyal to their owners but can also be quite stubborn.","furigana":"しばいぬ|か|ぬし|ちゅうじつ|がんこ"},{"japanese":"毎朝、近所の人が柴犬を散歩させている。","romaji":"Maiasa, kinjo no hito ga shibainu o sanpo sasete iru.","german":"Every morning, a neighbor walks his Shiba.","furigana":"まいあさ|きんじょ|ひと|しばいぬ|さんぽ"}],"notes":"柴犬 is a small Japanese dog breed with pricked ears and a curled tail, protected since 1936 as a 天然記念物 \"natural monument\". Besides しばいぬ, the reading しばけん is also heard; in conversation it is often shortened to 柴 or affectionately 柴ちゃん. Other Japanese breeds include 秋田犬 and 甲斐犬. Shibas are considered loyal but independent and stubborn; they became internationally known through the \"Doge\" meme. Neutral.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お前に三日間の猶予を与えてやろう。","romaji":"Omae ni sannichikan no yūyo o ataete yarō.","german":"I'll give you a grace period of three days.","furigana":"まえ|さんにちかん|ゆうよ|あた"},{"japanese":"事態は深刻で、一刻の猶予も許されない。","romaji":"Jitai wa shinkoku de, ikkoku no yuuyo mo yurusarenai.","german":"The situation is serious, and not a moment can be spared.","furigana":"じたい|しんこく|いっこく|ゆうよ|ゆる"},{"japanese":"被告には、懲役二年、執行猶予三年の判決が下された。","romaji":"Hikoku ni wa, choueki ninen, shikkou yuuyo sannen no hanketsu ga kudasareta.","german":"The defendant was sentenced to two years in prison, suspended for three years.","furigana":"ひこく|ちょうえきにねん|しっこうゆうよさんねん|はんけつ|くだ"}],"notes":"猶予 is a \"grace period, postponement\" granted to someone: 猶予を与える, 猶予をもらう, 一週間の猶予. Fixed expressions are 一刻の猶予もない \"there is not a second to spare\", 執行猶予 \"suspension of sentence on probation\" and 支払いを猶予する \"to defer payment\". The word is elevated to official; in everyday speech one rather says 待ってもらう or 延期.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"基本を疎かにしてはいけません。","romaji":"Kihon o orosoka ni shite wa ikemasen.","german":"You must not neglect the basics.","furigana":"きほん|おろそ"},{"japanese":"仕事に夢中で、家庭をおろそかにしていた。","romaji":"Shigoto ni muchuu de, katei o orosoka ni shite ita.","german":"I was so absorbed in work that I neglected my family.","furigana":"しごと|むちゅう|かてい"},{"japanese":"部活が忙しくても、勉強がおろそかになってはいけない。","romaji":"Bukatsu ga isogashikute mo, benkyou ga orosoka ni natte wa ikenai.","german":"Even if club activities are busy, studying must not fall by the wayside.","furigana":"ぶかつ|いそが|べんきょう"}],"notes":"おろそか \"negligent, neglected\" appears almost always in the phrase 〜をおろそかにする \"to neglect something\" or 〜がおろそかになる \"to fall by the wayside\", often as an admonition: 基本をおろそかにしてはいけない. Typical objects are 勉強, 仕事, 家庭, 健康. Mostly written in kana. Caution: the same kanji are also read おろか (〜はおろか \"let alone\"), and 愚か \"foolish\" is a different word. The matching verb is 怠る.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は方向音痴で、すぐ道に迷います。","romaji":"Watashi wa hōkō onchi de, sugu michi ni mayoimasu.","german":"I have no sense of direction and get lost right away.","furigana":"わたし|ほうこうおんち|みち|まよ"},{"japanese":"方向音痴なので、地図アプリがないと出かけられません。","romaji":"Houkou onchi na node, chizu apuri ga nai to dekakeraremasen.","german":"Because I have no sense of direction, I can't go out without a map app.","furigana":"ほうこうおんち|ちず|で"},{"japanese":"方向音痴の私が迷わないように、彼は駅まで迎えに来てくれた。","romaji":"Houkou onchi no watashi ga mayowanai you ni, kare wa eki made mukae ni kite kureta.","german":"So that I, with my poor sense of direction, wouldn't get lost, he came to meet me at the station.","furigana":"ほうこうおんち|わたし|まよ|かれ|えき|むか|き"}],"notes":"方向音痴 is colloquial and humorous -- literally something like \"direction-tone-deaf\", after 音痴 \"tone-deaf, singing off-key\". It refers to a lack of sense of direction and also to the person: 私は方向音痴だ \"I have zero sense of direction\". Following the same pattern there are 運動音痴 \"unathletic\" and 機械音痴 \"bad with machines\". The opposite is expressed with 方向感覚がいい.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"誰かが墓碑を倒したんです。","romaji":"Dare ka ga bohi o taoshita n desu.","german":"Someone knocked over the gravestone.","furigana":"だれ|ぼひ|たお"},{"japanese":"墓碑には、祖父の名前と没年が刻まれている。","romaji":"Bohi ni wa, sofu no namae to botsunen ga kizamarete iru.","german":"My grandfather's name and year of death are carved into the gravestone.","furigana":"ぼひ|そふ|なまえ|ぼつねん|きざ"},{"japanese":"古い墓碑銘を読むと、当時の人々の暮らしがわかる。","romaji":"Furui bohimei o yomu to, touji no hitobito no kurashi ga wakaru.","german":"Reading old epitaphs tells you about the lives of people of that time.","furigana":"ふる|ぼひめい|よ|とうじ|ひとびと|く"}],"notes":"墓碑 is the \"gravestone\" or grave marker with an inscription -- a written-language word. In everyday speech one says 墓石 or simply お墓. Common are 墓碑銘 \"epitaph, grave inscription\" and 墓碑に刻む \"to carve into the gravestone\". Related are 石碑 \"memorial stone\" and 記念碑 \"monument\", which, however, do not stand on graves.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これが石碑に彫られていた文字です。","romaji":"Kore ga sekihi ni horarete ita moji desu.","german":"These are the characters that were carved into the stone monument.","furigana":"せきひ|ほ|もじ"},{"japanese":"村の入り口に、昔の洪水を伝える石碑が建っている。","romaji":"Mura no iriguchi ni, mukashi no kouzui o tsutaeru sekihi ga tatte iru.","german":"At the village entrance stands a memorial stone telling of a past flood.","furigana":"むら|い|ぐち|むかし|こうずい|つた|せきひ|た"},{"japanese":"この石碑は、千年以上前に建てられたものだ。","romaji":"Kono sekihi wa, sennen ijou mae ni taterareta mono da.","german":"This memorial stone was erected over a thousand years ago.","furigana":"せきひ|せんねんいじょうまえ|た"}],"notes":"石碑 is a \"memorial stone, stone monument\" with a carved inscription, for example commemorating events, people or poems (句碑, 歌碑). Typical: 石碑を建てる, 石碑に刻む, 石碑に刻まれた文字. 記念碑 stresses the commemorative purpose and can also be made of metal; 墓碑 is specifically the gravestone.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これは何の記念碑ですか。","romaji":"Kore wa nani no kinenhi desu ka.","german":"What kind of monument is this?","furigana":"なに|きねんひ"},{"japanese":"公園の中央には、戦争で亡くなった人々の記念碑がある。","romaji":"Kouen no chuuou ni wa, sensou de nakunatta hitobito no kinenhi ga aru.","german":"In the center of the park, there is a memorial to those who died in the war.","furigana":"こうえん|ちゅうおう|せんそう|な|ひとびと|きねんひ"},{"japanese":"創立百周年を記念して、校庭に記念碑が建てられた。","romaji":"Souritsu hyakushuunen o kinen shite, koutei ni kinenhi ga taterareta.","german":"To commemorate the 100th anniversary of its founding, a monument was erected in the schoolyard.","furigana":"そうりつひゃくしゅうねん|きねん|こうてい|きねんひ|た"}],"notes":"記念碑 is a \"monument, memorial stone\" that commemorates an event or a person: 平和記念碑, 戦没者の記念碑, 記念碑を建てる. The word is factual and neutral. 石碑 stresses stone and inscription; 銅像 is a statue. Figuratively, 記念碑的 means \"groundbreaking, epoch-making\": 記念碑的な作品.","levelNote":"Compound: 記念 (N3) + 碑 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このスープには五種類の穀物が入っています。","romaji":"Kono sūpu ni wa go shurui no kokumotsu ga haitte imasu.","german":"This soup contains five kinds of grain.","furigana":"ごしゅるい|こくもつ|はい"},{"japanese":"日本は穀物の多くを輸入に頼っている。","romaji":"Nihon wa kokumotsu no ooku o yunyuu ni tayotte iru.","german":"Japan depends on imports for much of its grain.","furigana":"にほん|こくもつ|おお|ゆにゅう|たよ"},{"japanese":"干ばつの影響で、世界の穀物価格が上がった。","romaji":"Kanbatsu no eikyou de, sekai no kokumotsu kakaku ga agatta.","german":"Because of the drought, grain prices have risen worldwide.","furigana":"かん|えいきょう|せかい|こくもつかかく|あ"}],"notes":"穀物 is the umbrella term for \"grain\" such as rice, wheat, barley, corn and millet - matter-of-fact, used in economics, agriculture and nutrition: 穀物の輸入, 穀物価格, 穀物を栽培する. 穀類 is almost synonymous, but more technical (nutrition science, food groups). 五穀 are the \"five grains\" in set phrases such as 五穀豊穣 \"abundant harvest\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は穀類を毎食摂るようにしています。","romaji":"Watashi wa kokurui o maishoku toru yō ni shite imasu.","german":"I try to eat grains at every meal.","furigana":"わたし|こくるい|まいしょくと"},{"japanese":"穀類、豆類、野菜をバランスよく食べましょう。","romaji":"Kokurui, mamerui, yasai o baransu yoku tabemashou.","german":"Let's eat grains, legumes and vegetables in a balanced way.","furigana":"こくるい|まめるい|やさい|た"},{"japanese":"この地域では、米や麦などの穀類が主に栽培されている。","romaji":"Kono chiiki de wa, kome ya mugi nado no kokurui ga omo ni saibai sarete iru.","german":"In this region, grains such as rice and wheat are mainly cultivated.","furigana":"ちいき|こめ|むぎ|こくるい|おも|さいばい"}],"notes":"穀類 \"grains, cereals\" is a technical collective term, typical in nutrition science and food statistics, often alongside 豆類, 野菜類 or 魚介類. In content it largely coincides with 穀物; 類 stresses the group as a category. In everyday speech people tend to talk concretely about ご飯, パン or 麺.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"祖父は町で小さな米穀店を営んでいた。","romaji":"Sofu wa machi de chiisana beikokuten o itonande ita.","german":"My grandfather ran a small rice shop in town.","furigana":"そふ|まち|ちい|べいこくてん|いとな"},{"japanese":"戦後しばらくの間、米穀の流通は国が管理していた。","romaji":"Sengo shibaraku no aida, beikoku no ryuutsuu wa kuni ga kanri shite ita.","german":"For some time after the war, the state controlled the distribution of rice.","furigana":"せんご|あいだ|べいこく|りゅうつう|くに|かんり"},{"japanese":"我が社は、米穀の輸出入を主な事業としています。","romaji":"Waga sha wa, beikoku no yushutsunyuu o omo na jigyou to shite imasu.","german":"Our company's main business is the import and export of rice.","furigana":"わ|しゃ|べいこく|ゆしゅつにゅう|おも|じぎょう"}],"notes":"米穀 is an official and economic word for \"rice\" as a grain and trade commodity. It is mainly found in compounds such as 米穀店 \"rice shop\" or in expressions such as 米穀の流通 \"rice distribution\". In everyday speech one simply says 米 or お米. Beware of the homophone 米国 \"USA\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"死因は首を絞められたことによる窒息です。","romaji":"Shiin wa kubi o shimerareta koto ni yoru chissoku desu.","german":"The cause of death was suffocation due to strangulation of the neck.","furigana":"しいん|くび|し|ちっそく"},{"japanese":"正月には、餅をのどに詰まらせて窒息する事故が多い。","romaji":"Shougatsu ni wa, mochi o nodo ni tsumarasete chissoku suru jiko ga ooi.","german":"At New Year, there are many accidents in which people suffocate on rice cakes stuck in the throat.","furigana":"しょうがつ|もち|つ|ちっそく|じこ|おお"},{"japanese":"規則ばかりの職場で、窒息しそうだ。","romaji":"Kisoku bakari no shokuba de, chissoku shisou da.","german":"In this workplace full of rules, I feel like I'm suffocating.","furigana":"きそく|しょくば|ちっそく"}],"notes":"窒息 is \"suffocation, asphyxiation\" -- medical and factual: 窒息する, 窒息死 \"death by suffocation\", 餅をのどに詰まらせて窒息する. Figuratively it describes an oppressive atmosphere: 窒息しそうな職場. In everyday speech one says 息が詰まる (also figuratively); むせる, by contrast, only means \"to choke, cough\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"窮地に追い込まれた犯人は、ついに尻尾を出した。","romaji":"Kyuuchi ni oikomareta hannin wa, tsui ni shippo o dashita.","german":"Driven into a corner, the culprit finally gave himself away.","furigana":"きゅうち|お|こ|はんにん|しっぽ|だ"},{"japanese":"不祥事が続き、政府は窮地に立たされている。","romaji":"Fushouji ga tsuzuki, seifu wa kyuuchi ni tatasarete iru.","german":"After a string of scandals, the government is in a tight spot.","furigana":"ふしょうじ|つづ|せいふ|きゅうち|た"},{"japanese":"友人の助けで、何とか窮地を脱することができた。","romaji":"Yuujin no tasuke de, nantoka kyuuchi o dassuru koto ga dekita.","german":"With a friend's help, I somehow managed to get out of the predicament.","furigana":"ゆうじん|たす|なん|きゅうち|だっ"}],"notes":"窮地 is a \"dire situation, tight spot\". Fixed expressions: 窮地に陥る \"to get into a tight spot\", 窮地に追い込まれる or 窮地に立たされる \"to be driven into a corner\", 窮地を脱する \"to get out of a predicament\", 窮地を救う \"to rescue from distress\". Elevated, common in the news. 窮状, by contrast, means the wretched condition itself (poverty, distress), 窮地 the cornered position.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"窮屈な下着は体によくない。","romaji":"Kyūkutsu na shitagi wa karada ni yokunai.","german":"Tight underwear is not good for the body.","furigana":"きゅうくつ|したぎ|からだ"},{"japanese":"飛行機のエコノミー席は窮屈で、よく眠れなかった。","romaji":"Hikouki no ekonomii seki wa kyuukutsu de, yoku nemurenakatta.","german":"The economy seat on the plane was cramped, and I couldn't sleep well.","furigana":"ひこうき|せき|きゅうくつ|ねむ"},{"japanese":"校則が厳しくて、学校生活が窮屈に感じる。","romaji":"Kousoku ga kibishikute, gakkou seikatsu ga kyuukutsu ni kanjiru.","german":"The school rules are strict, so school life feels stifling.","furigana":"こうそく|きび|がっこうせいかつ|きゅうくつ|かん"}],"notes":"窮屈 first means \"tight, cramped\" in terms of space (窮屈な靴, 窮屈な席), and figuratively \"stiff, unfree, constricting\" when rules or surroundings leave no room: 窮屈な規則, 窮屈な思いをする \"to feel confined, uncomfortable\". Also financially: 予算が窮屈だ \"the budget is tight\". Unlike 狭い, which only describes size, 窮屈 stresses the unpleasant feeling of being confined.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"両親は生活に困窮していましたが、私にも彼らを助ける余裕はありませんでした。","romaji":"Ryoushin wa seikatsu ni konkyuu shite imashita ga, watashi ni mo karera o tasukeru yoyuu wa arimasen deshita.","german":"My parents were living in poverty, but I couldn't afford to help them either.","furigana":"りょうしん|せいかつ|こんきゅう|わたし|かれ|たす|よゆう"},{"japanese":"市は、生活困窮者への支援を強化すると発表した。","romaji":"Shi wa, seikatsu konkyuusha e no shien o kyouka suru to happyou shita.","german":"The city announced it would strengthen support for people in financial hardship.","furigana":"し|せいかつこんきゅうしゃ|しえん|きょうか|はっぴょう"},{"japanese":"突然の質問に、大臣は返答に困窮した様子だった。","romaji":"Totsuzen no shitsumon ni, daijin wa hentou ni konkyuu shita yousu datta.","german":"At the sudden question, the minister seemed at a loss for an answer.","furigana":"とつぜん|しつもん|だいじん|へんとう|こんきゅう|ようす"}],"notes":"困窮 refers to great material \"distress, poverty\" -- elevated, typical in the news and official language: 生活に困窮する \"to live in great hardship\", 生活困窮者 \"people in need\". It can also mean being greatly at a loss over something: 返答に困窮する. In everyday speech one rather says 生活が苦しい or お金に困っている; 貧困 is \"poverty\" as a social condition.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"役所に窮状を訴えたのですが、聞き入れてもらえませんでした。","romaji":"Yakusho ni kyuujou o uttaeta no desu ga, kikiirete moraemasen deshita.","german":"I appealed to the government office about my plight, but it wasn't heard.","furigana":"やくしょ|きゅうじょう|うった|き|い"},{"japanese":"被災地の窮状を見かねて、多くのボランティアが集まった。","romaji":"Hisaichi no kyuujou o mikanete, ooku no borantia ga atsumatta.","german":"Many volunteers gathered because they couldn't bear to watch the plight of the disaster area.","furigana":"ひさいち|きゅうじょう|み|おお|あつ"},{"japanese":"農家の窮状を伝えるニュースが続いている。","romaji":"Nouka no kyuujou o tsutaeru nyuusu ga tsuzuite iru.","german":"News reports on the plight of farmers continue.","furigana":"のうか|きゅうじょう|つた|つづ"}],"notes":"窮状 is a \"plight, predicament\" -- mostly economic or social hardship that one describes to others. Fixed combinations: 窮状を訴える \"to appeal about one's plight\", 窮状を見かねる \"to be unable to bear watching the plight\", 窮状を救う. Elevated, common in the media and petitions. 窮地 stresses the hopeless position, 窮状 the condition itself.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は、英国紳士の鏡のような人でした。","romaji":"Kare wa, Eikoku shinshi no kagami no yō na hito deshita.","german":"He was a paragon of the English gentleman.","furigana":"かれ|えいこくしんし|かがみ|ひと"},{"japanese":"紳士服売り場は三階です。","romaji":"Shinshifuku uriba wa sangai desu.","german":"The men's clothing department is on the third floor.","furigana":"しんしふくう|ば|さんがい"},{"japanese":"女性にドアを開けてあげるなんて、彼は本当の紳士だ。","romaji":"Josei ni doa o akete ageru nante, kare wa hontou no shinshi da.","german":"Opening the door for a woman -- he's a true gentleman.","furigana":"じょせい|あ|かれ|ほんとう|しんし"}],"notes":"紳士 is a \"gentleman\" -- a man with good manners and education: 紳士的な態度 \"gentlemanly\", 英国紳士, 紳士協定 \"gentlemen's agreement\". In department stores, 紳士 means \"men's\": 紳士服, 紳士靴, 紳士用; the counterpart is 婦人 \"women's\". The address 紳士淑女の皆様 \"ladies and gentlemen\" sounds ceremonial.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"縫製加工はこのミシンで行われました。","romaji":"Housei kakou wa kono mishin de okonawaremashita.","german":"The sewing was done with this sewing machine.","furigana":"ほうせいかこう|おこな"},{"japanese":"このシャツは縫製が丁寧で、長く着られる。","romaji":"Kono shatsu wa housei ga teinei de, nagaku kirareru.","german":"This shirt is carefully sewn and lasts a long time.","furigana":"ほうせい|ていねい|なが|き"},{"japanese":"縫製工場の多くは、人件費の安い国へ移った。","romaji":"Housei koujou no ooku wa, jinkenhi no yasui kuni e utsutta.","german":"Many sewing factories have moved to countries with low labor costs.","furigana":"ほうせいこうじょう|おお|じんけんひ|やす|くに|うつ"}],"notes":"縫製 is the commercial, mostly machine \"sewing, garment manufacturing\" of clothing -- a technical word of the textile industry: 縫製工場 \"sewing factory\", 縫製工程, 縫製が丁寧だ \"neatly made\". For sewing at home, one says 縫う or 裁縫. 縫製 often describes the construction quality of a finished garment.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"舶来のブランデーだと叔父はよく自慢していた。","romaji":"Hakurai no burandē da to oji wa yoku jiman shite ita.","german":"My uncle often boasted that it was imported brandy.","furigana":"はくらい|おじ|じまん"},{"japanese":"昔は、舶来品というだけでありがたがられたものだ。","romaji":"Mukashi wa, hakuraihin to iu dake de arigatagarareta mono da.","german":"In the past, things were prized simply for being imported.","furigana":"むかし|はくらいひん"},{"japanese":"祖父の書斎には、舶来の万年筆が大切にしまってある。","romaji":"Sofu no shosai ni wa, hakurai no mannenhitsu ga taisetsu ni shimatte aru.","german":"An imported fountain pen is carefully stored in my grandfather's study.","furigana":"そふ|しょさい|はくらい|まんねんひつ|たいせつ"}],"notes":"舶来 \"brought by ship\" means \"imported, from abroad\" and sounds old-fashioned today, with a touch of luxury and admiration for Western goods: 舶来品, 舶来の時計, 舶来のウイスキー. It almost always appears with の or as part of 舶来品. In neutral, modern speech one says 輸入品 or 外国製; the opposite is 国産.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"心に残る昭和の歌謡曲ベスト100のリストが公表されました。","romaji":"Kokoro ni nokoru Shouwa no kayoukyoku besuto hyaku no risuto ga kouhyou saremashita.","german":"A list of the 100 best unforgettable Showa-era kayōkyoku has been released.","furigana":"こころ|のこ|しょうわ|かようきょく|こうひょう"},{"japanese":"父は車の中で、いつも昔の歌謡曲を口ずさんでいる。","romaji":"Chichi wa kuruma no naka de, itsumo mukashi no kayoukyoku o kuchizusande iru.","german":"My father is always humming old kayōkyoku in the car.","furigana":"ちち|くるま|なか|むかし|かようきょく|くち"},{"japanese":"最近、若者の間で昭和の歌謡曲が人気を集めている。","romaji":"Saikin, wakamono no aida de Shouwa no kayoukyoku ga ninki o atsumete iru.","german":"Recently, Showa-era kayōkyoku have been gaining popularity among young people.","furigana":"さいきん|わかもの|あいだ|しょうわ|かようきょく|にんき|あつ"}],"notes":"歌謡曲 refers to the Japanese pop music of the Shōwa era -- catchy popular songs with orchestral accompaniment, sung by stars such as 美空ひばり. Today the word sounds nostalgic (昭和歌謡); current pop music is called J-POP, the sentimental ballad form 演歌. In general, 歌謡曲 can also simply mean \"popular song\".","levelNote":"Compound: 歌謡 (N2) + 曲 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"それじゃあ、ここも政府が直轄している機関なんですか。","romaji":"Sore jā, koko mo seifu ga chokkatsu shite iru kikan na n desu ka.","german":"So this is also an agency under the direct control of the government?","furigana":"せいふ|ちょっかつ|きかん"},{"japanese":"この新事業は、社長直轄のプロジェクトとして進められる。","romaji":"Kono shinjigyou wa, shachou chokkatsu no purojekuto to shite susumerareru.","german":"This new business will be pursued as a project directly under the president.","furigana":"しんじぎょう|しゃちょうちょっかつ|すす"},{"japanese":"江戸時代、長崎は幕府が直轄する土地だった。","romaji":"Edo jidai, Nagasaki wa bakufu ga chokkatsu suru tochi datta.","german":"In the Edo period, Nagasaki was land under the direct administration of the shogunate.","furigana":"えどじだい|ながさき|ばくふ|ちょっかつ|とち"}],"notes":"直轄 means to administer or place something \"directly under\" oneself without an intermediate authority: 国が直轄する, 政府直轄の機関, 直轄地 \"directly administered territory\". Often attributive with の: 社長直轄のプロジェクト \"directly under the president\", 国の直轄事業. Official and business language; the opposite is 委託 \"commissioning, outsourcing\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その鐘の音は、遥か彼方まで響き渡った。","romaji":"Sono kane no oto wa, haruka kanata made hibikiwatatta.","german":"The sound of the bell resounded far into the distance.","furigana":"かね|おと|はる|かなた|ひび|わた"},{"japanese":"恐竜は、はるか昔に絶滅した。","romaji":"Kyouryuu wa, haruka mukashi ni zetsumetsu shita.","german":"The dinosaurs went extinct very long ago.","furigana":"きょうりゅう|むかし|ぜつめつ"},{"japanese":"試験は、思っていたよりはるかに難しかった。","romaji":"Shiken wa, omotte ita yori haruka ni muzukashikatta.","german":"The exam was far harder than I had expected.","furigana":"しけん|おも|むずか"}],"notes":"はるか means \"far away\" in terms of space (はるか遠く, はるか彼方) and \"long ago, a long time back\" in terms of time (はるか昔). Very common is はるかに in comparisons: \"far more, by far\" -- 予想よりはるかに難しい. Mostly written in kana; the kanji 遥か or 遙か seem literary. In comparisons, ずっと is the more spoken alternative.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"無報酬でもかまいませんよ。","romaji":"Muhoushuu demo kamaimasen yo.","german":"I don't mind even if it's unpaid.","furigana":"むほうしゅう"},{"japanese":"彼は、地域の子どもたちに無報酬で勉強を教えている。","romaji":"Kare wa, chiiki no kodomotachi ni muhoushuu de benkyou o oshiete iru.","german":"He tutors the neighborhood children for free.","furigana":"かれ|ちいき|こ|むほうしゅう|べんきょう|おし"},{"japanese":"理事の仕事は無報酬だが、やりがいがある。","romaji":"Riji no shigoto wa muhoushuu da ga, yarigai ga aru.","german":"The work of a board member is unpaid, but it is rewarding.","furigana":"りじ|しごと|むほうしゅう"}],"notes":"無報酬 \"without payment, unpaid\" is factual and mostly appears with で or の: 無報酬で働く, 無報酬で引き受ける, 無報酬の仕事. Unlike 無料 \"free of charge\" (from the customer's point of view), 無報酬 means that someone receives no fee for their work. Colloquially and often with a complaint, one says ただ働き.","levelNote":"Compound: 無 (N3) + 報酬 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ちょっとそれは時代錯誤じゃないか。","romaji":"Chotto sore wa jidai sakugo ja nai ka.","german":"Isn't that a bit anachronistic?","furigana":"じだいさくご"},{"japanese":"試行錯誤を重ねて、ようやく新製品が完成した。","romaji":"Shikou sakugo o kasanete, youyaku shinseihin ga kansei shita.","german":"After repeated trial and error, the new product was finally completed.","furigana":"しこうさくご|かさ|しんせいひん|かんせい"},{"japanese":"重要な点に錯誤があった契約は、取り消すことができる。","romaji":"Juuyou na ten ni sakugo ga atta keiyaku wa, torikesu koto ga dekiru.","german":"A contract in which there was a mistake about an important point can be rescinded.","furigana":"じゅうよう|てん|さくご|けいやく|と|け"}],"notes":"錯誤 \"error, mistake, deviation\" is an elevated word that, on its own, appears mainly in legal and technical language (in civil law, the \"mistake\" in a declaration of intent). In everyday speech it is found almost only in fixed compounds: 試行錯誤 \"trial and error\" and 時代錯誤 \"anachronism, out of step with the times\". For ordinary mistakes, one says 間違い, ミス or 誤り.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"それがまるで自分のもののような錯覚に襲われたんです。","romaji":"Sore ga marude jibun no mono no you na sakkaku ni osowareta n desu.","german":"I was overcome by the illusion that it was somehow mine.","furigana":"じぶん|さっかく|おそ"},{"japanese":"二本の線は同じ長さなのに、目の錯覚で違って見える。","romaji":"Nihon no sen wa onaji nagasa na noni, me no sakkaku de chigatte mieru.","german":"The two lines are the same length, but they look different because of an optical illusion.","furigana":"にほん|せん|おな|なが|め|さっかく|ちが|み"},{"japanese":"隣の電車が動き出すと、自分の電車が動いたと錯覚することがある。","romaji":"Tonari no densha ga ugokidasu to, jibun no densha ga ugoita to sakkaku suru koto ga aru.","german":"When the train next to yours starts moving, you sometimes mistake it for your own train moving.","furigana":"となり|でんしゃ|うご|だ|じぶん|でんしゃ|うご|さっかく"}],"notes":"錯覚 is on the one hand a \"sensory illusion\" (目の錯覚 \"optical illusion\"), on the other a \"false impression\" in which one takes something to be true: 〜と錯覚する, 〜ような錯覚に陥る or 錯覚に襲われる \"to have the feeling as if ...\". Unlike 勘違い (an everyday misunderstanding) or 幻覚 \"hallucination\", 錯覚 means a misperception of something that is actually there.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"薬の影響で錯乱しているようでした。","romaji":"Kusuri no eikyou de sakuran shite iru you deshita.","german":"He seemed to be delirious from the effects of the medication.","furigana":"くすり|えいきょう|さくらん"},{"japanese":"事故の直後、彼女は錯乱状態に陥った。","romaji":"Jiko no chokugo, kanojo wa sakuran joutai ni ochiitta.","german":"Right after the accident, she fell into a state of delirium.","furigana":"じこ|ちょくご|かのじょ|さくらんじょうたい|おちい"},{"japanese":"高熱で錯乱した父が、意味の分からないことを叫んでいた。","romaji":"Kounetsu de sakuran shita chichi ga, imi no wakaranai koto o sakende ita.","german":"My father, delirious with a high fever, was shouting things that made no sense.","furigana":"こうねつ|さくらん|ちち|いみ|わ|さけ"}],"notes":"錯乱 refers to a severe state of mental confusion in which someone can no longer think or act clearly -- for instance through shock, high fever, drugs or medication. Typical are 錯乱状態に陥る \"to fall into a state of confusion\" and the compound 精神錯乱 \"mental derangement\"; as an intransitive する-verb: 錯乱する. Unlike 混乱, which can also apply to situations, traffic or thoughts in everyday life (\"to be in disarray\"), 錯乱 refers only to the state of mind and sounds much more dramatic.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"色々な感情が交錯し、結論を出せずにいました。","romaji":"Iroiro na kanjō ga kōsaku shi, ketsuron o dasezu ni imashita.","german":"Various emotions were intermingled and we couldn't reach a conclusion.","furigana":"いろいろ|かんじょう|こうさく|けつろん|だ"},{"japanese":"期待と不安が交錯する中、合格発表を待った。","romaji":"Kitai to fuan ga kousaku suru naka, goukaku happyou o matta.","german":"Torn between hope and anxiety, I waited for the exam results.","furigana":"きたい|ふあん|こうさく|なか|ごうかくはっぴょう|ま"},{"japanese":"地震の直後は情報が交錯し、被害の全容がつかめなかった。","romaji":"Jishin no chokugo wa jouhou ga kousaku shi, higai no zen'you ga tsukamenakatta.","german":"Right after the earthquake, information was conflicting and the full extent of the damage couldn't be grasped.","furigana":"じしん|ちょくご|じょうほう|こうさく|ひがい|ぜんよう"}],"notes":"交錯 means that several things cross or intertwine, creating a jumble. Very common are 感情が交錯する \"to have mixed feelings\", 期待と不安が交錯する and 情報が交錯する \"conflicting reports come in\"; concretely also for lines, rays of light or paths that cross. It is an intransitive する-verb (〜が交錯する) and rather written language. 混在 stresses only the coexistence, 混乱 the resulting confusion.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の小説には、倒錯した愛の形が描かれている。","romaji":"Kare no shousetsu ni wa, tousaku shita ai no katachi ga egakarete iru.","german":"His novels depict twisted forms of love.","furigana":"かれ|しょうせつ|とうさく|あい|かたち|えが"},{"japanese":"金もうけが目的になり、手段と目的が倒錯している。","romaji":"Kanemouke ga mokuteki ni nari, shudan to mokuteki ga tousaku shite iru.","german":"Making money has become the goal, and means and ends are inverted.","furigana":"かね|もくてき|しゅだん|もくてき|とうさく"},{"japanese":"性的倒錯は、精神医学の研究対象の一つだ。","romaji":"Seiteki tousaku wa, seishin igaku no kenkyuu taishou no hitotsu da.","german":"Sexual perversion is one of the subjects of psychiatric research.","furigana":"せいてきとうさく|せいしんいがく|けんきゅうたいしょう|ひと"}],"notes":"倒錯 literally means \"reversed and muddled\": something is turned upside down, especially values, feelings or sexual behavior. Most common are 性的倒錯 \"sexual perversion\" and the attributive form 倒錯した (倒錯した愛情, 倒錯した世界). In the sense of \"inversion\" one says, for example, 価値観が倒錯している or 手段と目的が倒錯している; in everyday speech 本末転倒 or 逆転 are more common for this. The word is written language and sounds technical or literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"靖国神社に参拝したことはありますか。","romaji":"Yasukunijinja ni sanpai shita koto wa arimasu ka.","german":"Have you ever visited Yasukuni Shrine?","furigana":"やすくにじんじゃ|さんぱい"},{"japanese":"首相の靖国神社参拝は、近隣諸国から強い批判を受けた。","romaji":"Shushou no Yasukuni Jinja sanpai wa, kinrin shokoku kara tsuyoi hihan o uketa.","german":"The prime minister's visit to Yasukuni Shrine drew strong criticism from neighboring countries.","furigana":"しゅしょう|やすくにじんじゃさんぱい|きんりんしょこく|つよ|ひはん|う"},{"japanese":"東京の桜の開花は、靖国神社にある標本木で判断されます。","romaji":"Toukyou no sakura no kaika wa, Yasukuni Jinja ni aru hyouhonboku de handan saremasu.","german":"Whether the cherry blossoms have bloomed in Tokyo is judged by the reference tree at Yasukuni Shrine.","furigana":"とうきょう|さくら|かいか|やすくにじんじゃ|ひょうほんぼく|はんだん"}],"notes":"靖国神社 is a Shinto shrine in Tokyo (Chiyoda ward, near 九段下) that enshrines the souls of those who died for Japan -- since 1978 also convicted Class A war criminals. Visits by politicians, especially the prime minister (首相の靖国参拝), are therefore sensitive both domestically and diplomatically; the debate is called 靖国問題 for short. For a shrine visit with prayer, one says に参拝する. The reference tree by which the start of the cherry blossom season in Tokyo is announced also stands there.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新しい恋人に飽きてきたところなの。","romaji":"Atarashii koibito ni akite kita tokoro na no.","german":"I'm just starting to get bored with my new partner.","furigana":"あたら|こいびと|あ"},{"japanese":"毎日同じお弁当だと、さすがに飽きる。","romaji":"Mainichi onaji obentou da to, sasuga ni akiru.","german":"The same lunch box every day - you really do get sick of it eventually.","furigana":"まいにちおな|べんとう|あ"},{"japanese":"その話はもう聞き飽きたよ。","romaji":"Sono hanashi wa mou kikiakita yo.","german":"I'm sick of hearing that story already.","furigana":"はなし|き|あ"}],"notes":"飽きる means to lose interest in something through repetition or too much of it; the object takes に: ゲームに飽きる. Attached to a verb stem, it forms compounds such as 見飽きる \"to have seen enough\", 聞き飽きる or 食べ飽きる. 退屈, by contrast, describes boredom because nothing is going on, whereas 飽きる presupposes that one has already had something (too) often. Related are 飽きっぽい \"easily bored, fickle\" and 飽きが来る \"to grow tired of something\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本は、戦後の食料不足から飽食の時代へ大きく変わりました。","romaji":"Nippon wa, sengo no shokuryō fusoku kara hōshoku no jidai e ōkiku kawarimashita.","german":"Japan changed greatly from the food shortages of the postwar period to an era of plenty.","furigana":"にっぽん|せんご|しょくりょうふそく|ほうしょく|じだい|おお|か"},{"japanese":"飽食の陰で、まだ食べられる食品が大量に捨てられている。","romaji":"Houshoku no kage de, mada taberareru shokuhin ga tairyou ni suterarete iru.","german":"Behind the abundance, huge amounts of still-edible food are being thrown away.","furigana":"ほうしょく|かげ|た|しょくひん|たいりょう|す"},{"japanese":"昔の人は飽食を戒め、腹八分目を勧めた。","romaji":"Mukashi no hito wa houshoku o imashime, harahachibunme o susumeta.","german":"People in the past warned against overeating and recommended eating until 80 percent full.","furigana":"むかし|ひと|ほうしょく|いまし|はらはちぶんめ|すす"}],"notes":"飽食 means to eat one's fill and, figuratively, a state in which food is available in abundance. It is most often found in 飽食の時代 \"the age of plenty\" as a socially critical catchphrase, often together with topics such as food waste (食品ロス). As a verb (飽食する) it is rare and written language; in everyday speech one says お腹いっぱい食べる. The counterpart is 飢餓 \"hunger, famine\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"窓から蝶々がひらりと入ってきた。","romaji":"Mado kara chōchō ga hirari to haitte kita.","german":"A butterfly fluttered in through the window.","furigana":"まど|ちょうちょう|はい"},{"japanese":"公園で子どもたちが蝶々を追いかけている。","romaji":"Kouen de kodomotachi ga chouchou o oikakete iru.","german":"Children are chasing butterflies in the park.","furigana":"こうえん|こ|ちょうちょう|お"},{"japanese":"靴ひもをしっかり蝶々結びにしましょう。","romaji":"Kutsuhimo o shikkari chouchou-musubi ni shimashou.","german":"Let's tie your shoelaces firmly in a bow.","furigana":"くつ|ちょうちょうむす"}],"notes":"蝶々 is a softer, more affectionate form of 蝶 \"butterfly\"; in speech it is often shortened to ちょうちょ, for example in the children's song 「ちょうちょう」. In factual texts and in biology, 蝶 or katakana チョウ is more common; 蝶々 is used in everyday speech and with children. A fixed combination is 蝶々結び \"bow\" (on shoelaces or gift ribbon). Moths, by contrast, are 蛾.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この度は弊社のサービスをご利用いただきまして誠にありがとうございます。","romaji":"Kono tabi wa heisha no sābisu o go riyō itadakimashite makotoni arigatō gozaimasu.","german":"Thank you very much for using our company's service this time.","furigana":"たび|へいしゃ|りよう|まこと"},{"japanese":"詳細につきましては、弊社の担当者から改めてご連絡いたします。","romaji":"Shousai ni tsukimashite wa, heisha no tantousha kara aratamete go renraku itashimasu.","german":"Regarding the details, our person in charge will contact you again.","furigana":"しょうさい|へいしゃ|たんとうしゃ|あらた|れんらく"},{"japanese":"弊社では現在、エンジニアを募集しております。","romaji":"Heisha de wa genzai, enjinia o boshuu shite orimasu.","german":"Our company is currently recruiting engineers.","furigana":"へいしゃ|げんざい|ぼしゅう"}],"notes":"弊社 is the humble (謙譲) term for one's own company when speaking to customers and business partners, in emails as well as in conversation. The other party's company is then 御社 (spoken) or 貴社 (written). 当社 is neutral and appears in press releases or terms and conditions, for example; among colleagues one simply says うちの会社. Typical is its combination with humble forms: 弊社の担当者からご連絡いたします.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日曜日は、熊野古道を歩いて、那智の滝を見に行く予定です。","romaji":"Nichiyōbi wa, Kumano Kodō o aruite, Nachi no Taki o mi ni iku yotei desu.","german":"On Sunday, I'm planning to walk the Kumano Kodo and go see Nachi Falls.","furigana":"にちようび|くまのこどう|ある|なち|たき|み|い|よてい"},{"japanese":"那智の滝は日本三名瀑の一つに数えられています。","romaji":"Nachi no Taki wa Nihon sanmeibaku no hitotsu ni kazoerarete imasu.","german":"Nachi Falls is counted as one of Japan's three most famous waterfalls.","furigana":"なち|たき||ひと|かぞ"},{"japanese":"那智の滝の前に立つと、水しぶきと轟音に圧倒されました。","romaji":"Nachi no Taki no mae ni tatsu to, mizushibuki to gōon ni attō saremashita.","german":"Standing in front of Nachi Falls, I was overwhelmed by the spray and the roar.","furigana":"なち|たき|まえ|た|みず||あっとう"}],"notes":"The 那智の滝 is in Wakayama Prefecture in the Kumano area and, at about 133 m, has the greatest drop of any single-tier waterfall in Japan. Together with 華厳の滝 and 袋田の滝, it is counted among the 日本三名瀑 (\"Japan's three most famous waterfalls\"). The waterfall itself is worshipped as a deity at the shrine 飛瀧神社 and, together with the pilgrimage route 熊野古道, is part of the UNESCO World Heritage site in the Kii Mountains. Typical: 那智の滝を見に行く, 那智の滝に参拝する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"学校では日本語でしか話してはいけないという不文律のようなものがあります。","romaji":"Gakkou de wa nihongo de shika hanashite wa ikenai to iu fubunritsu no you na mono ga arimasu.","german":"An der Schule gibt es so etwas wie ein ungeschriebenes Gesetz, dass man nur Japanisch sprechen darf.","furigana":"がっこう|にほんご|はな|ふぶんりつ"},{"japanese":"新人は先輩より先に帰らないというのが、この職場の不文律だ。","romaji":"Shinjin wa senpai yori saki ni kaeranai to iu no ga, kono shokuba no fubunritsu da.","german":"In diesem Betrieb gilt das ungeschriebene Gesetz, dass Neulinge nicht vor den Älteren nach Hause gehen.","furigana":"しんじん|せんぱい|さき|かえ|しょくば|ふぶんりつ"},{"japanese":"大差がついた試合では盗塁しないという不文律が、野球にはある。","romaji":"Taisa ga tsuita shiai de wa tourui shinai to iu fubunritsu ga, yakyuu ni wa aru.","german":"Im Baseball gibt es das ungeschriebene Gesetz, bei klarem Vorsprung keine Bases mehr zu stehlen.","furigana":"たいさ|しあい|とうるい|ふぶんりつ|やきゅう"}],"notes":"不文律 ist eine Regel, die nirgends schriftlich festgehalten ist, aber von allen beachtet wird – im Deutschen „ungeschriebenes Gesetz“. Meist geht es um Gepflogenheiten in Firmen, Schulen, im Sport oder in der Politik: 〜というのが不文律だ, 不文律を破る, 不文律になっている. Im juristischen Sinn „Gewohnheitsrecht“ sagt man fachsprachlich eher 不文法 oder 慣習法. Ähnlich, aber lockerer ist 暗黙のルール bzw. 暗黙の了解.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"我々の血脈を絶やしてはならない。","romaji":"Wareware no ketsumyaku o tayashite wa naranai.","german":"We must not let our bloodline die out.","furigana":"われわれ|けつみゃく|た"},{"japanese":"彼は名門武家の血脈を受け継いでいる。","romaji":"Kare wa meimon buke no ketsumyaku o uketsuide iru.","german":"He carries on the bloodline of a distinguished samurai family.","furigana":"かれ|めいもんぶけ|けつみゃく|う|つ"},{"japanese":"跡継ぎがいなかったため、その家の血脈は途絶えた。","romaji":"Atotsugi ga inakatta tame, sono ie no ketsumyaku wa todaeta.","german":"Because there was no heir, that family's bloodline came to an end.","furigana":"あとつ|いえ|けつみゃく|とだ"}],"notes":"血脈 refers to blood kinship or the line of descent of a family; it is written language and elevated. Typical are 血脈を受け継ぐ \"to carry on the bloodline\" and 血脈が絶える \"the line dies out\". In everyday speech one rather says 血筋 or 血統 (the latter also for breeding animals); the meaning \"blood vessel\" is obsolete, and today 血管 is used for it. In Buddhism the word is read けちみゃく and means the transmission of the teaching from master to disciple.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"熊之実はイソギンチャクと共生する魚だ。","romaji":"Kumanomi wa isoginchaku to kyōsei suru sakana da.","german":"Clownfish live in symbiosis with sea anemones.","furigana":"くまのみ|きょうせい|さかな"},{"japanese":"子どもはあの映画を見てから、クマノミが大好きになった。","romaji":"Kodomo wa ano eiga o mite kara, kumanomi ga daisuki ni natta.","german":"Ever since my child saw that movie, he has loved clownfish.","furigana":"こ|えいが|み|だいす"},{"japanese":"沖縄の海でシュノーケリングをしたら、クマノミを見つけた。","romaji":"Okinawa no umi de shunōkeringu o shitara, kumanomi o mitsuketa.","german":"When I went snorkeling in the sea off Okinawa, I found a clownfish.","furigana":"おきなわ|うみ|み"}],"notes":"熊之実 is the clownfish or anemonefish, which lives in symbiosis with sea anemones (イソギンチャク). The kanji spelling is a rare ateji; like most animal and fish names, the word is almost always written in katakana in everyday use: クマノミ. The fish is known mainly from aquariums and from \"Finding Nemo\" -- Nemo himself is a カクレクマノミ (\"false clownfish\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どちらも甲乙つけがたい出来栄えだ。","romaji":"Dochira mo kouotsu tsukegatai dekibae da.","german":"Both are so well done that it's hard to say which is better.","furigana":"こうおつ|できば"},{"japanese":"決勝に残った二人の実力に甲乙はない。","romaji":"Kesshou ni nokotta futari no jitsuryoku ni kouotsu wa nai.","german":"There is no difference in ability between the two who made it to the finals.","furigana":"けっしょう|のこ|ふたり|じつりょく|こうおつ"},{"japanese":"契約書の甲乙を逆に書いてしまった。","romaji":"Keiyakusho no kouotsu o gyaku ni kaite shimatta.","german":"I wrote 甲 and 乙 the wrong way around in the contract.","furigana":"けいやくしょ|こうおつ|ぎゃく|か"}],"notes":"甲 and 乙 are the first two of the ten heavenly stems and therefore stand for \"first and second\" or \"better and worse\". 甲乙 almost always appears in 甲乙つけがたい \"it is hard to say which is better\" (praise for two equally good things) and 甲乙ない / 甲乙なし \"equal in value\". In contracts, 甲 and 乙 denote the contracting parties (\"Party A/B\"); 甲乙 together then means both parties. The word is written language; in conversation one rather says どっちもいい.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"森林を伐採する際は、事前に届け出が必要です。","romaji":"Shinrin o bassai suru sai wa, jizen ni todokede ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"When felling a forest, prior notification is required.","furigana":"しんりん|ばっさい|さい|じぜん|とど|で|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"違法な森林伐採によって、熱帯雨林が減り続けている。","romaji":"Ihou na shinrin bassai ni yotte, nettai urin ga heri tsuzukete iru.","german":"Because of illegal deforestation, tropical rainforests keep shrinking.","furigana":"いほう|しんりんばっさい|ねったいうりん|へ|つづ"},{"japanese":"道路を広げる工事のため、街路樹が伐採された。","romaji":"Douro o hirogeru kouji no tame, gairoju ga bassai sareta.","german":"The roadside trees were cut down for road-widening work.","furigana":"どうろ|ひろ|こうじ|がいろじゅ|ばっさい"}],"notes":"伐採 is the technical or official word for felling trees and clearing forests: 木を伐採する, 森林伐採, 違法伐採 \"illegal logging\". It is transitive (を); in the passive the tree becomes the subject: 木が伐採された. In everyday speech one says more simply 木を切る or 切り倒す. To be distinguished are 間伐 \"thinning\" and 開墾 \"clearing land for cultivation\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"会社から、三割の減俸を言い渡されました。","romaji":"Kaisha kara, sanwari no genpou o iiwatasaremashita.","german":"The company told me my pay would be cut by 30 percent.","furigana":"かいしゃ|さんわり|げんぽう|い|わた"},{"japanese":"部下の不正を見逃した管理職が、減俸処分を受けた。","romaji":"Buka no fusei o minogashita kanrishoku ga, genpou shobun o uketa.","german":"The manager who overlooked his subordinate's misconduct received a pay-cut penalty.","furigana":"ぶか|ふせい|みのが|かんりしょく|げんぽうしょぶん|う"},{"japanese":"その投手は、成績不振で大幅な減俸となった。","romaji":"Sono toushu wa, seiseki fushin de oohaba na genpou to natta.","german":"The pitcher took a significant pay cut because of poor performance.","furigana":"とうしゅ|せいせきふしん|おおはば|げんぽう"}],"notes":"減俸 is a reduction in salary, usually for a limited period and as a disciplinary penalty, e.g. for civil servants or after a company scandal: 減俸処分, 減俸10%（3か月）. It often stands as a noun or in the passive: 減俸になる/となる, 減俸処分を受ける, 減俸される. In contract negotiations of professional baseball players, too, one often reads of 減俸. 減給 is the more general word for a pay cut; the opposite is 昇給 \"raise\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この選手は、破格の年俸をオファーされましたが、それを蹴ってチームに留まりました。","romaji":"Kono senshu wa, hakaku no nenpō o ofā saremashita ga, sore o kette chīmu ni todomarimashita.","german":"This player was offered an exceptional annual salary, but turned it down and stayed with his team.","furigana":"せんしゅ|はかく|ねんぽう|け|とど"},{"japanese":"うちの会社は年俸制なので、毎月の給料はほぼ同じです。","romaji":"Uchi no kaisha wa nenpōsei na node, maitsuki no kyūryō wa hobo onaji desu.","german":"Our company has an annual salary system, so the monthly pay is almost always the same.","furigana":"かいしゃ|ねんぽうせい|まいつき|きゅうりょう|おな"},{"japanese":"来季の年俸交渉で、彼は大幅なアップを勝ち取った。","romaji":"Raiki no nenpō kōshō de, kare wa ōhaba na appu o kachitotta.","german":"In the salary negotiations for next season, he won a significant raise.","furigana":"らいき|ねんぽうこうしょう|かれ|おおはば|か|と"}],"notes":"年俸 is the salary fixed by contract for a whole year, typical of professional athletes, executives and companies with a 年俸制 (\"annual salary system\"). To be distinguished from it are 年収, the actual annual income from all sources, and 月給, the monthly salary. Typical combinations: 年俸〇億円, 年俸交渉, 年俸がアップする/ダウンする, 年俸で契約する. Note the reading ねんぽう.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"我が社では、特殊な技術を用いて、樹脂凸版の直接製版を格安で行っています。","romaji":"Waga sha de wa, tokushu na gijutsu o mochiite, jushi toppan no chokusetsu seihan o kakuyasu de okonatte imasu.","german":"Our company uses a special technique to make resin relief printing plates directly at low cost.","furigana":"わ|しゃ|とくしゅ|ぎじゅつ|もち|じゅしとっぱん|ちょくせつせいはん|かくやす|おこな"},{"japanese":"版画の授業で、木版画は凸版の一種だと習った。","romaji":"Hanga no jugyou de, mokuhanga wa toppan no isshu da to naratta.","german":"In printmaking class, I learned that woodblock printing is a kind of relief printing.","furigana":"はんが|じゅぎょう|もくはんが|とっぱん|いっしゅ|なら"},{"japanese":"結婚式の招待状を、昔ながらの凸版で印刷してもらった。","romaji":"Kekkonshiki no shoutaijou o, mukashinagara no toppan de insatsu shite moratta.","german":"I had our wedding invitations printed by traditional letterpress.","furigana":"けっこんしき|しょうたいじょう|むかし|とっぱん|いんさつ"}],"notes":"凸版 is a printing form in which the printing areas are raised -- relief printing, as with woodblock prints, stamps or classic letterpress (活版印刷). The counterparts are 凹版 \"intaglio\", 平版 \"planographic printing (offset)\" and 孔版 \"stencil printing\". The word is technical and is found mainly in 凸版印刷 and in art classes. It is also known through the name of the large printing company 凸版印刷 (now TOPPAN).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"凹凸のはっきりした顔立ちですね。","romaji":"Ōtotsu no hakkiri shita kaodachi desu ne.","german":"You have distinct facial features.","furigana":"おうとつ|かおだ"},{"japanese":"道路に凹凸が多くて、自転車が走りにくい。","romaji":"Douro ni outotsu ga ookute, jitensha ga hashirinikui.","german":"The road has many bumps, so it's hard to ride a bicycle.","furigana":"どうろ|おうとつ|おお|じてんしゃ|はし"},{"japanese":"このファンデーションは、肌の凹凸を目立たなくする。","romaji":"Kono fandeeshon wa, hada no outotsu o medatanaku suru.","german":"This foundation makes unevenness in the skin less noticeable.","furigana":"はだ|おうとつ|めだ"}],"notes":"凹凸 refers to unevenness of a surface -- raised and sunken parts -- and is a noun, not an adjective: 凹凸がある, 凹凸のある道, 肌の凹凸. Figuratively it can also mean inequality or disparity, for example between regions. The synonymous でこぼこ (written 凸凹, with the characters in reverse order) is colloquial and more vivid, while 凹凸 is more written and technical. With faces, 凹凸のはっきりした顔 describes pronounced, sculpted features.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不倫をダシにヤクザに恐喝されています。","romaji":"Furin o dashi ni yakuza ni kyoukatsu sarete imasu.","german":"I'm being blackmailed by the yakuza over my affair.","furigana":"ふりん|きょうかつ"},{"japanese":"男は恐喝の容疑で逮捕された。","romaji":"Otoko wa kyoukatsu no yougi de taiho sareta.","german":"The man was arrested on suspicion of extortion.","furigana":"おとこ|きょうかつ|ようぎ|たいほ"},{"japanese":"彼は同級生を恐喝して、金を巻き上げていた。","romaji":"Kare wa doukyuusei o kyoukatsu shite, kane o makiagete ita.","german":"He extorted money from his classmates.","furigana":"かれ|どうきゅうせい|きょうかつ|かね|ま|あ"}],"notes":"恐喝 is extorting money or valuables through threats and also the legal term (恐喝罪 \"extortion\"). Transitive with を for the victim: 人を恐喝する; very common in the passive 恐喝される or in police reports: 恐喝の容疑で逮捕. 脅迫 is more general \"threat, intimidation\", without it having to involve money. Colloquially one says ゆする, and among teenagers カツアゲ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大岡忠相は、八代将軍吉宗の時代に江戸町奉行を務めた。","romaji":"Ooka Tadasuke wa, hachidai shougun Yoshimune no jidai ni Edo machibugyou o tsutometa.","german":"Ōoka Tadasuke served as Edo's town magistrate under the eighth shogun, Yoshimune.","furigana":"||じだい|えどまちぶぎょう|つと"},{"japanese":"時代劇では、お奉行様が悪人を裁く場面がよく出てくる。","romaji":"Jidaigeki de wa, obugyou-sama ga akunin o sabaku bamen ga yoku dete kuru.","german":"In period dramas, scenes of the magistrate judging villains often appear.","furigana":"じだいげき|ぶぎょうさま|あくにん|さば|ばめん|で"},{"japanese":"鍋料理になると、父はいつも鍋奉行になる。","romaji":"Nabe ryouri ni naru to, chichi wa itsumo nabebugyou ni naru.","german":"When it comes to hot pot, my father always becomes the pot magistrate.","furigana":"なべりょうり|ちち|なべぶぎょう"}],"notes":"奉行 was the title of high administrative officials from the Kamakura to the Edo period. Particularly well known are the three offices of the Edo period (三奉行): 寺社奉行 for temples and shrines, 町奉行 for administration, police and jurisdiction in Edo, and 勘定奉行 for finances. In period dramas (時代劇), お奉行様 is the stern judge. Jokingly, 鍋奉行 today is someone who takes charge when eating hot pot.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"弔意を表して花を贈りました。","romaji":"Chōi o arawashite hana o okurimashita.","german":"I sent flowers to express my condolences.","furigana":"ちょうい|あらわ|はな|おく"},{"japanese":"葬儀に参列できないため、弔電で弔意を伝えた。","romaji":"Sougi ni sanretsu dekinai tame, chouden de choui o tsutaeta.","german":"Since I couldn't attend the funeral, I conveyed my condolences by condolence telegram.","furigana":"そうぎ|さんれつ|ちょうでん|ちょうい|つた"},{"japanese":"各国の首脳から、相次いで弔意が寄せられた。","romaji":"Kakkoku no shunou kara, aitsuide choui ga yoserareta.","german":"Condolences poured in one after another from leaders around the world.","furigana":"かっこく|しゅのう|あいつ|ちょうい|よ"}],"notes":"弔意 is the formal word for sympathy at someone's death and appears almost only in fixed combinations: 弔意を表す \"to express condolences\", 弔意を伝える, 謹んで弔意を表します. It appears in the news, official letters and condolence telegrams (弔電). In conversation one says お悔やみ申し上げます instead; 哀悼の意 is similarly elevated.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"誰が弔辞を読むのですか。","romaji":"Dare ga chōji o yomu no desu ka.","german":"Who will read the eulogy?","furigana":"だれ|ちょうじ|よ"},{"japanese":"友人代表として、彼が弔辞を述べた。","romaji":"Yuujin daihyou to shite, kare ga chouji o nobeta.","german":"He gave the eulogy on behalf of the friends.","furigana":"ゆうじんだいひょう|かれ|ちょうじ|の"},{"japanese":"恩師の葬儀で読まれた弔辞に、多くの人が涙した。","romaji":"Onshi no sougi de yomareta chouji ni, ooku no hito ga namida shita.","german":"Many people shed tears at the eulogy read at our beloved teacher's funeral.","furigana":"おんし|そうぎ|よ|ちょうじ|おお|ひと|なみだ"}],"notes":"弔辞 is the address to the deceased given at the funeral or farewell ceremony (告別式), usually by a close friend or a representative of a company or association. One says 弔辞を読む (the speech is traditionally read from a sheet), 弔辞を述べる or 弔辞を捧げる. Not to be confused with 弔電 \"condolence telegram\" and 弔意 \"condolences\"; the word is formal.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"まずは、その悪循環を断ち切ることが大切です。","romaji":"Mazu wa, sono akujunkan o tachikiru koto ga taisetsu desu.","german":"First of all, it is important to break that vicious circle.","furigana":"あくじゅんかん|た|き|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"寝不足でミスが増え、残業してさらに寝不足になるという悪循環に陥った。","romaji":"Nebusoku de misu ga fue, zangyou shite sara ni nebusoku ni naru to iu akujunkan ni ochiitta.","german":"I fell into a vicious circle: lack of sleep led to more mistakes, and working overtime meant even less sleep.","furigana":"ねぶそく|ふ|ざんぎょう|ねぶそく|あくじゅんかん|おちい"},{"japanese":"物価が上がって消費が減り、景気がさらに悪くなる悪循環が続いている。","romaji":"Bukka ga agatte shouhi ga heri, keiki ga sara ni waruku naru akujunkan ga tsuzuite iru.","german":"The vicious circle of rising prices, falling consumption and a still weaker economy continues.","furigana":"ぶっか|あ|しょうひ|へ|けいき|わる|あくじゅんかん|つづ"}],"notes":"悪循環 is a vicious circle in which cause and effect worsen each other. Typical verbs are 悪循環に陥る \"to fall into a vicious circle\", 悪循環を断ち切る \"to break the vicious circle\" and 悪循環が続く. The content is often placed in front with 〜という悪循環. The opposite is 好循環 \"virtuous circle\".","levelNote":"Compound: 悪 (N2) + 循環 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"当院には、循環器専門医が五人います。","romaji":"Touin ni wa, junkanki senmon'i ga gonin imasu.","german":"Our clinic has five cardiovascular specialists.","furigana":"とういん|じゅんかんきせんもんい|ごにん"},{"japanese":"胸の痛みが続くので、循環器内科を受診した。","romaji":"Mune no itami ga tsuzuku node, junkanki naika o jushin shita.","german":"My chest pain wouldn't stop, so I went to see a cardiologist.","furigana":"むね|いた|つづ|じゅんかんきないか|じゅしん"},{"japanese":"高血圧は、循環器の病気の大きな原因になる。","romaji":"Kouketsuatsu wa, junkanki no byouki no ookina gen'in ni naru.","german":"High blood pressure is a major cause of cardiovascular disease.","furigana":"こうけつあつ|じゅんかんき|びょうき|おお|げんいん"}],"notes":"循環器 is the medical collective term for the organs of blood circulation, that is, the heart and vessels. In everyday life it appears mainly in medical specialties and names of diseases: 循環器内科 \"cardiology\", 循環器専門医, 循環器疾患 \"cardiovascular diseases\". Formed analogously are 消化器 \"digestive organs\" and 呼吸器 \"respiratory organs\". The underlying word is 循環 \"circulation\".","levelNote":"Compound: 循環 (N2) + 器 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"惜しい人を亡くしたものだ。","romaji":"Oshii hito o nakushita mono da.","german":"We have really lost a precious person.","furigana":"お|ひと|な"},{"japanese":"惜しい！あと1点で合格だったのに。","romaji":"Oshii! Ato itten de goukaku datta noni.","german":"So close! I would have passed with one more point.","furigana":"お|てん|ごうかく"},{"japanese":"寝る時間も惜しいほど、研究に夢中だ。","romaji":"Neru jikan mo oshii hodo, kenkyuu ni muchuu da.","german":"I'm so absorbed in my research that even the time to sleep feels precious.","furigana":"ね|じかん|お|けんきゅう|むちゅう"}],"notes":"惜しい expresses regret that something valuable is being lost or was narrowly missed. As an exclamation, 惜しい！ means \"so close!\"; 惜しい人を亡くす means \"to lose a valued person\". In the sense \"too precious to give up\" it takes が: 命が惜しい, 時間が惜しい, 捨てるのは惜しい. 残念 is general regret, whereas 惜しい presupposes that something good was almost achieved or is too good to waste - close to もったいない.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このビデオは、見ていて不愉快です。","romaji":"Kono bideo wa, mite ite fuyukai desu.","german":"This video is unpleasant to watch.","furigana":"み|ふゆかい"},{"japanese":"このたびは不愉快な思いをさせてしまい、申し訳ございませんでした。","romaji":"Kono tabi wa fuyukai na omoi o sasete shimai, moushiwake gozaimasen deshita.","german":"We sincerely apologize for causing you an unpleasant experience this time.","furigana":"ふゆかい|おも|もう|わけ"},{"japanese":"彼の失礼な態度に、みんな不愉快になった。","romaji":"Kare no shitsurei na taido ni, minna fuyukai ni natta.","german":"Everyone was upset by his rude behavior.","furigana":"かれ|しつれい|たいど|ふゆかい"}],"notes":"不愉快 describes an unpleasant, irritating feeling, usually triggered by the behavior of others -- more \"annoyed, put out\" than \"physically uncomfortable\". Typical are 不愉快な思いをする/させる, 不愉快に思う and 不愉快な態度; it is a な-adjective. 不快 is similar but also covers physical discomfort (不快感, 不快指数); colloquially one simply says 嫌な気分 or むかつく.","levelNote":"Compound: 不 (N3) + 愉快 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は、憂いを帯びた顔でこちらを見ていた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa, urei o obita kao de kochira o mite ita.","german":"She was looking at us with a melancholy expression.","furigana":"かのじょ|うれ|お|かお|み"},{"japanese":"備えあれば憂いなし。","romaji":"Sonae areba urei nashi.","german":"Being prepared means no worries.","furigana":"そな|うれ"},{"japanese":"彼は国の将来に深い憂いを抱いていた。","romaji":"Kare wa kuni no shourai ni fukai urei o idaite ita.","german":"He harbored deep concern for the future of the country.","furigana":"かれ|くに|しょうらい|ふか|うれ|いだ"}],"notes":"憂い is an elevated word for sorrow, melancholy and worry about the future; it is the noun for 憂う/憂える. Typical are 憂いを帯びた顔 \"a melancholy expression\", 後顧の憂い \"worry about what one leaves behind\" and the proverb 備えあれば憂いなし \"preparedness means no worries\". For rather sad moods, 愁い is also written. In everyday speech one says 心配 or 悩み.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は、世界各地で果敢な取材をしていた。","romaji":"Kare wa, sekai kakuchi de kakan na shuzai o shite ita.","german":"He was reporting fearlessly in all parts of the world.","furigana":"かれ|せかいかくち|かかん|しゅざい"},{"japanese":"若い選手たちが、強豪チームに果敢に挑んだ。","romaji":"Wakai senshutachi ga, kyougou chiimu ni kakan ni idonda.","german":"The young players boldly challenged the powerhouse team.","furigana":"わか|せんしゅ|きょうごう|かかん|いど"},{"japanese":"消防士は燃え盛る建物に果敢に飛び込んだ。","romaji":"Shouboushi wa moesakaru tatemono ni kakan ni tobikonda.","german":"The firefighter dashed boldly into the burning building.","furigana":"しょうぼうし|も|さか|たてもの|かかん|と|こ"}],"notes":"果敢 describes resolute, bold action despite risk or difficulty. It mostly appears adverbially with に (果敢に挑む, 果敢に攻める) or attributively (果敢な攻撃, 果敢な挑戦). Fixed combinations are 勇猛果敢 \"brave and fearless\" and 積極果敢. 勇敢 stresses bravery, 大胆 boldness (also in the sense of audacious); 果敢 highlights resolve in action and is rather written language, for example in sports reports.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"本当にこの作戦を敢行するつもりですか。","romaji":"Hontōni kono sakusen o kankō suru tsumori desu ka.","german":"Do you really intend to go through with this operation?","furigana":"ほんとう|さくせん|かんこう"},{"japanese":"嵐の中、救助隊は夜間の捜索を敢行した。","romaji":"Arashi no naka, kyuujotai wa yakan no sousaku o kankou shita.","german":"In the middle of the storm, the rescue team went ahead with a nighttime search.","furigana":"あらし|なか|きゅうじょたい|やかん|そうさく|かんこう"},{"japanese":"その会社は、業界の常識を破る大幅な値下げを敢行した。","romaji":"Sono kaisha wa, gyoukai no joushiki o yaburu oohaba na nesage o kankou shita.","german":"The company dared to make a drastic price cut that defied industry conventions.","furigana":"かいしゃ|ぎょうかい|じょうしき|やぶ|おおはば|ねさ|かんこう"}],"notes":"敢行 means to resolutely carry something out despite risk, resistance or poor conditions: 作戦を敢行する, 値下げを敢行する, 夜間の捜索を敢行する. It is transitive (を) and written language, typical of the news and reports. 実行 is neutral \"to carry out\", 決行 stresses sticking to the plan (雨天決行 \"held even in rain\"), 強行 pushing through against resistance, often with a negative connotation.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は、会社の不正に敢然と立ち向かった。","romaji":"Kanojo wa, kaisha no fusei ni kanzen to tachimukatta.","german":"She boldly stood up against her company's wrongdoing.","furigana":"かのじょ|かいしゃ|ふせい|かんぜん|た|む"},{"japanese":"小さな国が、大国の圧力に敢然と抵抗した。","romaji":"Chiisana kuni ga, taikoku no atsuryoku ni kanzen to teikou shita.","german":"The small country resolutely resisted the pressure of the great power.","furigana":"ちい|くに|たいこく|あつりょく|かんぜん|ていこう"},{"japanese":"会議で誰も反対しない中、彼は敢然と異を唱えた。","romaji":"Kaigi de dare mo hantai shinai naka, kare wa kanzen to i o tonaeta.","german":"While no one objected in the meeting, he fearlessly voiced his objection.","furigana":"かいぎ|だれ|はんたい|なか|かれ|かんぜん|い|とな"}],"notes":"敢然 describes a fearless, resolute approach against danger or resistance. It almost always appears adverbially as 敢然と, especially with 立ち向かう, 戦う, 挑む or 抗議する; the form 敢然たる (敢然たる態度) is rare and very much written language. 果敢 is a な-adjective with a similar meaning (果敢に挑む); 敢然と stresses more the fearless confrontation. Not to be confused with the identically read 完全 \"complete\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"優勝候補を相手に、よく敢闘したと思います。","romaji":"Yūshō kōho o aite ni, yoku kantō shita to omoimasu.","german":"I think they fought bravely against the title favorite.","furigana":"ゆうしょうこうほ|あいて|かんとう|おも"},{"japanese":"新入幕の力士が、敢闘賞を受賞した。","romaji":"Shinnyuumaku no rikishi ga, kantoushou o jushou shita.","german":"A wrestler who had just been promoted to the top division received the Fighting Spirit Prize.","furigana":"しんにゅうまく|りきし|かんとうしょう|じゅしょう"},{"japanese":"惜しくも敗れたが、選手たちの敢闘をたたえたい。","romaji":"Oshikumo yabureta ga, senshutachi no kantou o tataetai.","german":"We narrowly lost, but I want to praise the players' valiant effort.","furigana":"お|やぶ|せんしゅ|かんとう"}],"notes":"敢闘 means \"to fight bravely, to give a good account of oneself\" and is used mainly in sports, often as praise for a narrow defeat: よく敢闘した. Best known is 敢闘賞, the Fighting Spirit Prize in sumo, and also 敢闘精神 \"fighting spirit\". It is an intransitive する-verb and rather written language; in everyday speech one says よく頑張った. 奮闘 is similar but stresses the effort, 健闘 the good performance.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"駐車場で、バイクの八の字旋回を練習した。","romaji":"Chuushajou de, baiku no hachi no ji senkai o renshuu shita.","german":"In the parking lot, I practiced figure-eight turns on my motorcycle.","furigana":"ちゅうしゃじょう|はち|じせんかい|れんしゅう"},{"japanese":"ヘリコプターが、さっきから上空を旋回している。","romaji":"Herikoputaa ga, sakki kara joukuu o senkai shite iru.","german":"A helicopter has been circling overhead for a while.","furigana":"じょうくう|せんかい"},{"japanese":"トンビが空高く輪を描いて旋回していた。","romaji":"Tonbi ga sora takaku wa o egaite senkai shite ita.","german":"A black kite was circling high in the sky, drawing rings.","furigana":"そらたか|わ|えが|せんかい"}],"notes":"旋回 refers to circling or a turn, especially of airplanes, helicopters, birds and ships: 上空を旋回する (を marks the space of movement), 左旋回, 急旋回, 旋回半径. Machine parts that swivel around an axis also 旋回する. Figuratively, 大旋回 stands for a U-turn in politics. In everyday speech, for cars one rather says 曲がる or Uターンする; 回転, by contrast, means turning on one's own axis.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"海賊達の目の前で、旋風は嵐へと変わっていった。","romaji":"Kaizokutachi no me no mae de, senpū wa arashi e to kawatte itta.","german":"Right before the pirates' eyes, the whirlwind turned into a storm.","furigana":"かいぞくたち|め|まえ|せんぷう|あらし|か"},{"japanese":"新人歌手のデビュー曲が、音楽界に旋風を巻き起こした。","romaji":"Shinjin kashu no debyuu kyoku ga, ongakukai ni senpuu o makiokoshita.","german":"The new singer's debut song created a sensation in the music world.","furigana":"しんじんかしゅ|きょく|おんがくかい|せんぷう|ま|お"},{"japanese":"校庭で小さな旋風が起こり、砂ぼこりが舞い上がった。","romaji":"Koutei de chiisana senpuu ga okori, sunabokori ga maiagatta.","german":"A small whirlwind arose on the schoolyard and kicked up dust.","furigana":"こうてい|ちい|せんぷう|お|すな|ま|あ"}],"notes":"旋風 literally means a whirlwind; in everyday speech one rather says つむじ風 for it, and a tornado is 竜巻. Much more common is the figurative meaning \"sensation, stir\", almost always in 旋風を巻き起こす \"to create a sensation\", for example of newcomers in sports, music or politics. Compounds such as 〇〇旋風 (\"the XY craze\") are also popular in headlines. The word is rather written language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"楓の葉も色づいてきました。","romaji":"Kaede no ha mo irozuite kimashita.","german":"The maple leaves have started to change color, too.","furigana":"かえで|は|いろ"},{"japanese":"カナダの国旗には楓の葉が描かれています。","romaji":"Kanada no kokki ni wa kaede no ha ga egakarete imasu.","german":"A maple leaf is depicted on the Canadian flag.","furigana":"こっき|かえで|は|えが"},{"japanese":"祖父は庭に小さな楓の木を植えた。","romaji":"Sofu wa niwa ni chiisa na kaede no ki o ueta.","german":"My grandfather planted a small maple tree in the garden.","furigana":"そふ|にわ|ちい|かえで|き|う"}],"notes":"楓 is the maple tree. In everyday speech, for maple -- especially the Japanese maple with red autumn leaves -- one often says もみじ (紅葉); 紅葉 read こうよう, by contrast, means autumn leaf coloring in general. 楓 sounds somewhat more botanical or elevated and is also a popular girl's name. Typical: 楓の葉, 楓の木, 楓が色づく; maple syrup is メープルシロップ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"料理には砂糖の代わりに楓糖を使うことが多いです。","romaji":"Ryouri ni wa satou no kawari ni kaedetou o tsukau koto ga ooi desu.","german":"I often use maple sugar instead of sugar when cooking.","furigana":"りょうり|さとう|か|かえでとう|つか|おお"},{"japanese":"楓糖は、サトウカエデの樹液を煮詰めて作られる。","romaji":"Kaedetou wa, satoukaede no jueki o nitsumete tsukurareru.","german":"Maple sugar is made by boiling down the sap of the sugar maple.","furigana":"かえでとう|じゅえき|につ|つく"},{"japanese":"カナダ旅行のお土産に、楓糖のキャンディーを買った。","romaji":"Kanada ryokou no omiyage ni, kaedetou no kyandii o katta.","german":"As a souvenir from my trip to Canada, I bought maple sugar candies.","furigana":"りょこう|みやげ|かえでとう|か"}],"notes":"楓糖 is maple sugar, obtained by boiling down the sap of the sugar maple (サトウカエデ). The word is rare and written language; in everyday speech and on packaging it is almost always メープルシュガー, and the syrup is メープルシロップ. Its components are 楓 \"maple\" (かえで, also もみじ for the red autumn leaves) and 糖 \"sugar\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どのくらい頻繁に水槽の掃除をしますか。","romaji":"Dono kurai hinpan ni suisō no sōji o shimasu ka.","german":"How often do you clean the aquarium?","furigana":"ひんぱん|すいそう|そうじ"},{"japanese":"子どもが夏祭りですくった金魚を、水槽で飼っています。","romaji":"Kodomo ga natsumatsuri de sukutta kingyo o, suisou de katte imasu.","german":"We keep the goldfish that my child scooped up at the summer festival in an aquarium.","furigana":"こ|なつまつ|きんぎょ|すいそう|か"},{"japanese":"マンションの屋上にある水槽を点検した。","romaji":"Manshon no okujou ni aru suisou o tenken shita.","german":"I inspected the water tank on the roof of the apartment building.","furigana":"おくじょう|すいそう|てんけん"}],"notes":"水槽 is generally a water container or tank -- from an aquarium for fish to a water tank on the roof (受水槽, 高架水槽) or a fire-fighting water tank (防火水槽). Typical are 水槽で金魚を飼う, 水槽の水を替える, 熱帯魚の水槽. Note: 水族館 is the aquarium as an institution, 水槽 only the tank itself.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本が多くの国に円借款を提供しているのはご存知ですか。","romaji":"Nippon ga ōku no kuni ni en shakkan o teikyō shite iru no wa gozonji desu ka.","german":"Did you know that Japan provides yen loans to many countries?","furigana":"にっぽん|おお|くに|えんしゃっかん|ていきょう|ぞんじ"},{"japanese":"政府は、橋の建設のために借款を供与した。","romaji":"Seifu wa, hashi no kensetsu no tame ni shakkan o kyouyo shita.","german":"The government extended a loan for the construction of a bridge.","furigana":"せいふ|はし|けんせつ|しゃっかん|きょうよ"},{"japanese":"両国の首脳が、新たな借款協定に調印した。","romaji":"Ryoukoku no shunou ga, arata na shakkan kyoutei ni chouin shita.","german":"The leaders of the two countries signed a new loan agreement.","furigana":"りょうこく|しゅのう|あら|しゃっかんきょうてい|ちょういん"}],"notes":"借款 is a loan, especially between states or from one state to another country; private loans are called ローン, 融資 or 借金. Most common is 円借款 \"yen loan\", with which Japan finances infrastructure as part of development aid. Typical combinations: 借款を供与する \"to extend a loan\", 借款協定, 借款を返済する. The word belongs to economic and political language.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"烏龍茶を氷抜きでください。","romaji":"Ūroncha o kōri nuki de kudasai.","german":"An oolong tea without ice, please.","furigana":"|こおりぬ"},{"japanese":"車で来たので、私はウーロン茶にします。","romaji":"Kuruma de kita node, watashi wa ūroncha ni shimasu.","german":"I came by car, so I'll have an oolong tea.","furigana":"くるま|き|わたし|ちゃ"},{"japanese":"脂っこい料理には烏龍茶がよく合う。","romaji":"Aburakkoi ryōri ni wa ūroncha ga yoku au.","german":"Oolong tea goes well with greasy food.","furigana":"あぶら|りょうり||あ"}],"notes":"烏龍茶 is Chinese oolong tea, a semi-fermented tea. The kanji spelling can be seen on the menus of Chinese restaurants, for example; otherwise it is almost always written ウーロン茶. In Japan it is mostly drunk cold from bottles or cans, and in izakaya it is the standard drink for anyone who doesn't drink alcohol; mixed with shōchū it is called ウーロンハイ. Ordering: ウーロン茶をください, ウーロン茶を一つ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その家には、盲人の老人が一人で住んでいます。","romaji":"Sono ie ni wa, moujin no roujin ga hitori de sunde imasu.","german":"An elderly blind man lives alone in that house.","furigana":"いえ|もうじん|ろうじん|ひとり|す"},{"japanese":"江戸時代、多くの盲人が鍼やあんまを仕事にしていた。","romaji":"Edo jidai, ooku no moujin ga hari ya anma o shigoto ni shite ita.","german":"In the Edo period, many blind people made their living from acupuncture and massage.","furigana":"えどじだい|おお|もうじん||しごと"},{"japanese":"今日では、盲人より視覚障害者という言い方が一般的だ。","romaji":"Konnichi de wa, moujin yori shikaku shougaisha to iu iikata ga ippanteki da.","german":"Today, the expression \"visually impaired person\" is more common than 盲人.","furigana":"こんにち|もうじん|しかくしょうがいしゃ|い|かた|いっぱんてき"}],"notes":"盲人 means \"blind person\". The word is outdated today and felt to be insensitive; in official language and the media one says 視覚障害者, in everyday speech 目の不自由な人. It is still found in historical texts, in literature and in older designations. A common mistake is confusing it with 盲点 \"blind spot\" or 盲目 \"blindness\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"犯人は捜査の盲点を突いてきた。","romaji":"Hannin wa sōsa no mōten o tsuite kita.","german":"The criminal exploited a blind spot in the investigation.","furigana":"はんにん|そうさ|もうてん|つ"},{"japanese":"なるほど、それは盲点だった。","romaji":"Naruhodo, sore wa mouten datta.","german":"I see -- I hadn't thought of that at all.","furigana":"もうてん"},{"japanese":"人の目には盲点があるが、普段は脳がその部分を補っている。","romaji":"Hito no me ni wa mouten ga aru ga, fudan wa nou ga sono bubun o oginatte iru.","german":"The human eye has a blind spot, but normally the brain compensates for that area.","furigana":"ひと|め|もうてん|ふだん|のう|ぶぶん|おぎな"}],"notes":"盲点 is first of all the blind spot on the retina, but much more often, figuratively, a point that one overlooks or a gap in a system: 盲点を突く \"to exploit a weak point\", 盲点になる, 法の盲点 \"loophole in the law\". The exclamation それは盲点だった means \"I hadn't thought of that at all!\". Note: the blind spot while driving a car is called 死角, not 盲点.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私達は、盲目の犬を飼っているんです。","romaji":"Watashitachi wa, mōmoku no inu o katte iru n desu.","german":"We have a blind dog.","furigana":"わたしたち|もうもく|いぬ|か"},{"japanese":"恋は盲目と言うけれど、彼女には彼の欠点が全く見えていない。","romaji":"Koi wa moumoku to iu keredo, kanojo ni wa kare no ketten ga mattaku miete inai.","german":"They say love is blind, and she cannot see his flaws at all.","furigana":"こい|もうもく|い|かのじょ|かれ|けってん|まった|み"},{"japanese":"上司の指示に盲目的に従うのは危険だ。","romaji":"Joushi no shiji ni moumokuteki ni shitagau no wa kiken da.","german":"It is dangerous to follow the boss's instructions blindly.","furigana":"じょうし|しじ|もうもくてき|したが|きけん"}],"notes":"盲目 literally means \"blindness, blind\" and takes の: 盲目のピアニスト. More common is the figurative meaning \"blind, uncritical\", especially in the proverb 恋は盲目 \"love is blind\" and in the form 盲目的 (盲目的に信じる, 盲目的な愛). For physical blindness the word is considered somewhat sensitive; more neutral are 目が見えない or 視覚障害. Note: 盲目な as a な-adjective is rare; one says 盲目的な.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"色盲って治るんですか。","romaji":"Shikimō tte naoru n desu ka.","german":"Can color blindness be cured?","furigana":"しきもう|なお"},{"japanese":"父は軽い色盲で、赤と緑の区別がつきにくい。","romaji":"Chichi wa karui shikimou de, aka to midori no kubetsu ga tsukinikui.","german":"My father is slightly color-blind and has trouble telling red and green apart.","furigana":"ちち|かる|しきもう|あか|みどり|くべつ"},{"japanese":"最近は、色盲ではなく色覚異常と言うことが多い。","romaji":"Saikin wa, shikimou de wa naku shikikaku ijou to iu koto ga ooi.","german":"These days, people mostly say \"color vision deficiency\" rather than 色盲.","furigana":"さいきん|しきもう|しきかくいじょう|い|おお"}],"notes":"色盲 is the traditional term for color blindness or color vision deficiency. The word is considered outdated and discriminatory today; in medicine and in schools one says 色覚異常, and more recently also 色覚多様性. Typical: 赤緑色盲 \"red-green color blindness\". It is a noun, even though it is often used like a quality (父は色盲だ).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お風呂に粗塩を入れると体にいいらしいですよ。","romaji":"Ofuro ni arajio o ireru to karada ni ii rashii desu yo.","german":"Apparently, putting coarse salt in the bath is good for the body.","furigana":"ふろ|あらじお|い|からだ"},{"japanese":"焼く前に、魚に粗塩をふってしばらく置いておく。","romaji":"Yaku mae ni, sakana ni arajio o futte shibaraku oite oku.","german":"Before grilling, sprinkle the fish with coarse salt and let it sit for a while.","furigana":"や|まえ|さかな|あらじお|お"},{"japanese":"店の入り口に、粗塩で盛り塩をした。","romaji":"Mise no iriguchi ni, arajio de morijio o shita.","german":"We made a small mound of coarse salt at the shop entrance for good luck.","furigana":"みせ|い|ぐち|あらじお|も|じお"}],"notes":"粗塩 (also written 荒塩) is coarse, little-refined salt with residual moisture and minerals, often from seawater. It is used for seasoning and salting (魚に粗塩をふる), but also ritually: for 盛り塩 at the entrance of shops or for purification after a funeral (お清め). The counterpart is 精製塩 or 食卓塩 \"refined table salt\". Note the reading: あらじお with a voiced じ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"黒と濃紺のスーツが一着ずつあると便利ですよ。","romaji":"Kuro to nōkon no sūtsu ga ichichaku zutsu aru to benri desu yo.","german":"It's handy to have one black suit and one dark navy suit.","furigana":"くろ|のうこん|いちちゃく|べんり"},{"japanese":"その学校の制服は、濃紺のブレザーだ。","romaji":"Sono gakkou no seifuku wa, noukon no burezaa da.","german":"That school's uniform is a dark navy blazer.","furigana":"がっこう|せいふく|のうこん"},{"japanese":"夕暮れの空が、だんだん濃紺に染まっていった。","romaji":"Yuugure no sora ga, dandan noukon ni somatte itta.","german":"The evening sky gradually turned a deep dark blue.","furigana":"ゆうぐ|そら|のうこん|そ"}],"notes":"濃紺 is a very dark navy blue, darker than 紺 or 紺色. It mostly stands with の before clothing: 濃紺のスーツ, 濃紺の制服, 濃紺のブレザー -- the classic color for business suits and school uniforms. Related color words are 藍色 \"indigo\" and 群青 \"ultramarine\"; 紺 alone is more everyday.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このガイドブックは、市内のレストランをほぼ網羅している。","romaji":"Kono gaidobukku wa, shinai no resutoran o hobo moura shite iru.","german":"This guidebook covers almost all the restaurants in the city.","furigana":"しない|もうら"},{"japanese":"試験範囲を網羅した問題集を買った。","romaji":"Shiken han'i o moura shita mondaishuu o katta.","german":"I bought a workbook that covers the entire exam scope.","furigana":"しけんはんい|もうら|もんだいしゅう|か"},{"japanese":"過去の研究を網羅的に調べてから、論文を書き始めた。","romaji":"Kako no kenkyuu o mourateki ni shirabete kara, ronbun o kakihajimeta.","german":"I started writing my paper after comprehensively surveying past research.","furigana":"かこ|けんきゅう|もうらてき|しら|ろんぶん|か|はじ"}],"notes":"網羅 means to capture something completely and without gaps -- literally \"to catch with a net\". It is typical of reference works, lists and exam material: 〜を網羅する, 〜を網羅した辞書, 網羅的に調べる \"to investigate exhaustively\". It is transitive (を) and written language. For \"to master a subject\" one does not say 網羅 but 把握する or 覚える; 含む, on the other hand, is only \"to contain\", without a claim to completeness.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一見すると意味不明な文字の羅列のように見える。","romaji":"Ikken suru to imi fumei na moji no raretsu no yō ni mieru.","german":"At first glance, it looks like a meaningless string of characters.","furigana":"いっけん|いみふめい|もじ|られつ|み"},{"japanese":"レポートでは、データを羅列するだけでなく、自分の考察も書きなさい。","romaji":"Repooto de wa, deeta o raretsu suru dake de naku, jibun no kousatsu mo kakinasai.","german":"In the report, don't just list data, but also write your own analysis.","furigana":"られつ|じぶん|こうさつ|か"},{"japanese":"彼のスピーチは専門用語の羅列で、とても分かりにくかった。","romaji":"Kare no supiichi wa senmon yougo no raretsu de, totemo wakarinikukatta.","german":"His speech was a string of technical terms and was very hard to understand.","furigana":"かれ|せんもんようご|られつ|わ"}],"notes":"羅列 is a mere lining up of things, usually with a slightly derogatory tone: without order, connection or message. Typical are 文字の羅列, 数字の羅列, 専門用語の羅列 and 事実を羅列する \"to merely list facts\". Neutral are 列挙 \"to enumerate\" or 並べる; 羅列 is used more in criticism of texts, speeches or data.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は私にとって、人生の羅針盤だったのです。","romaji":"Kare wa watashi ni totte, jinsei no rashinban datta no desu.","german":"He was the compass of my life.","furigana":"かれ|わたし|じんせい|らしんばん"},{"japanese":"昔の船乗りは、羅針盤を頼りに大海原を航海した。","romaji":"Mukashi no funanori wa, rashinban o tayori ni oounabara o koukai shita.","german":"In the old days, sailors navigated the open oceans relying on the compass.","furigana":"むかし|ふなの|らしんばん|たよ||こうかい"},{"japanese":"羅針盤は、火薬・活版印刷と並ぶルネサンスの三大発明の一つだ。","romaji":"Rashinban wa, kayaku, kappan insatsu to narabu runesansu no sandai hatsumei no hitotsu da.","german":"The compass is one of the three great inventions of the Renaissance, alongside gunpowder and letterpress printing.","furigana":"らしんばん|かやく|かっぱんいんさつ|なら|さんだいはつめい|ひと"}],"notes":"羅針盤 is the (ship's) compass; in everyday speech one mostly says コンパス (also for the drawing compass) or 方位磁針/方位磁石. The word sounds historical or elevated and is often used figuratively: 人生の羅針盤 \"a compass/guiding principle for life\". Together with 火薬 and 活版印刷, the 羅針盤 is counted among the three great inventions of the Renaissance.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"エコーで胎児が足を伸ばしたり縮めたりするところが見られました。","romaji":"Ekoo de taiji ga ashi o nobashitari chijimetari suru tokoro ga miraremashita.","german":"On the ultrasound, I could see the fetus stretching and drawing in its legs.","furigana":"たいじ|あし|の|ちぢ|み"},{"japanese":"妊娠中の飲酒は、胎児に悪い影響を与える。","romaji":"Ninshinchuu no inshu wa, taiji ni warui eikyou o ataeru.","german":"Drinking alcohol during pregnancy has a bad effect on the fetus.","furigana":"にんしんちゅう|いんしゅ|たいじ|わる|えいきょう|あた"},{"japanese":"胎児の性別は、いつごろ分かりますか。","romaji":"Taiji no seibetsu wa, itsu goro wakarimasu ka.","german":"When can you tell the baby's sex?","furigana":"たいじ|せいべつ|わ"}],"notes":"胎児 is the child in the womb, that is, a fetus or embryo; the word is medical and factual. Typical are 胎児の成長, 胎児に影響する, 胎児の性別 and 胎児期. In conversation one says, more affectionately, お腹の赤ちゃん. After birth one speaks of 新生児 \"newborn\"; 胎児 is a noun.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女が堕胎を拒んでいるみたいよ。","romaji":"Kanojo ga datai o kobande iru mitai yo.","german":"It seems she is refusing to have an abortion.","furigana":"かのじょ|だたい|こば"},{"japanese":"日本の刑法には、今も堕胎罪の規定がある。","romaji":"Nihon no keihou ni wa, ima mo dataizai no kitei ga aru.","german":"Japan's Penal Code still contains a provision on the crime of abortion.","furigana":"にほん|けいほう|いま|だたいざい|きてい"},{"japanese":"かつては多くの国で、堕胎が法律で禁じられていた。","romaji":"Katsute wa ooku no kuni de, datai ga houritsu de kinjirarete ita.","german":"In the past, abortion was prohibited by law in many countries.","furigana":"おお|くに|だたい|ほうりつ|きん"}],"notes":"堕胎 is the traditional, legal and written-language word for abortion and has a negative, morally judgmental tone; the Japanese Penal Code still contains the offense 堕胎罪. In medicine and the media, people say neutrally 人工妊娠中絶 or, for short, 中絶, and colloquially 子どもをおろす. As a verb, 堕胎する is possible but rare.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その牝馬は、無事に受胎したことが確認された。","romaji":"Sono hinba wa, buji ni jutai shita koto ga kakunin sareta.","german":"It was confirmed that the mare had conceived successfully.","furigana":"ひんば|ぶじ|じゅたい|かくにん"},{"japanese":"美術館で、ダ・ヴィンチの受胎告知を見た。","romaji":"Bijutsukan de, Da Vinchi no Jutai Kokuchi o mita.","german":"At the museum, I saw da Vinci's \"Annunciation\".","furigana":"びじゅつかん|じゅたいこくち|み"},{"japanese":"排卵日の前後は受胎しやすいと言われる。","romaji":"Hairanbi no zengo wa jutai shiyasui to iwareru.","german":"Conception is said to be most likely around the day of ovulation.","furigana":"はいらんび|ぜんご|じゅたい|い"}],"notes":"受胎 is conception, that is, the beginning of a pregnancy; the word is medical or elevated and is also used for animals. Typical are 受胎する, 受胎しやすい時期, 受胎調節 \"contraception\" and the Christian motif 受胎告知 \"the Annunciation\". In everyday speech one says 妊娠する \"to become pregnant\"; 受精 is the biological fertilization of the egg cell.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"胃腸がそんなに丈夫な方ではありません。","romaji":"Ichō ga sonnani jōbu na hō de wa arimasen.","german":"Mein Magen ist nicht besonders stark.","furigana":"いちょう|じょうぶ|ほう"},{"japanese":"食べ過ぎたので、胃腸薬を飲んだ。","romaji":"Tabesugita node, ichouyaku o nonda.","german":"Weil ich zu viel gegessen hatte, nahm ich ein Mittel für Magen und Darm.","furigana":"た|す|いちょうやく|の"},{"japanese":"子どもが胃腸炎で学校を休んだ。","romaji":"Kodomo ga ichouen de gakkou o yasunda.","german":"Mein Kind ist wegen einer Magen-Darm-Grippe nicht zur Schule gegangen.","furigana":"こ|いちょうえん|がっこう|やす"}],"notes":"胃腸 fasst Magen und Darm zusammen, also den Verdauungstrakt. Typisch sind 胃腸が弱い/丈夫だ „einen empfindlichen/robusten Magen haben“, 胃腸の調子が悪い, 胃腸薬 „Magen-Darm-Mittel“ und 胃腸炎 „Magen-Darm-Entzündung“. Es ist ein Nomen; im Deutschen entspricht es oft einfach „Magen“ oder „Verdauung“.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"手術で盲腸を取ってしまいました。","romaji":"Shujutsu de mōchō o totte shimaimashita.","german":"I had my appendix removed in surgery.","furigana":"しゅじゅつ|もうちょう|と"},{"japanese":"子どものころ、盲腸で一週間入院したことがある。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, mouchou de isshuukan nyuuin shita koto ga aru.","german":"When I was a child, I was in the hospital for a week with appendicitis.","furigana":"こ|もうちょう|いっしゅうかんにゅういん"},{"japanese":"いわゆる盲腸は、医学的には虫垂炎と呼ばれる。","romaji":"Iwayuru mouchou wa, igakuteki ni wa chuusuien to yobareru.","german":"What is commonly called \"mōchō\" is medically called appendicitis.","furigana":"もうちょう|いがくてき|ちゅうすいえん|よ"}],"notes":"盲腸 is anatomically the cecum (caecum). Colloquially, however, it usually means the appendix and above all appendicitis: 盲腸になる \"to get appendicitis\", 盲腸の手術. Medically correct terms for these are 虫垂 and 虫垂炎. It is a noun.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大腸がんが見つかりました。","romaji":"Daichō gan ga mitsukarimashita.","german":"Colon cancer was found.","furigana":"だいちょう|み"},{"japanese":"井戸水から大腸菌が検出された。","romaji":"Idomizu kara daichoukin ga kenshutsu sareta.","german":"E. coli was detected in the well water.","furigana":"いどみず|だいちょうきん|けんしゅつ"},{"japanese":"来週、大腸の内視鏡検査を受ける予定だ。","romaji":"Raishuu, daichou no naishikyou kensa o ukeru yotei da.","german":"Next week I'm scheduled for a colonoscopy.","furigana":"らいしゅう|だいちょう|ないしきょうけんさ|う|よてい"}],"notes":"大腸 is the large intestine; its counterpart is 小腸 \"small intestine\", and 腸 alone means the intestines in general. Common compounds are 大腸がん \"colon cancer\", 大腸菌 \"E. coli\" and 大腸内視鏡検査 \"colonoscopy\". It is a medical, factual noun that nevertheless often occurs in everyday life (screening, hygiene).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あなたの役目は、二人の恋の萌芽を絶つことよ。","romaji":"Anata no yakume wa, futari no koi no houga o tatsu koto yo.","german":"Your job is to nip the budding love between the two of them.","furigana":"やくめ|ふたり|こい|ほうが|た"},{"japanese":"この時代の思想に、民主主義の萌芽が見られる。","romaji":"Kono jidai no shisou ni, minshushugi no houga ga mirareru.","german":"The first signs of democracy can be seen in the thought of this era.","furigana":"じだい|しそう|みんしゅしゅぎ|ほうが|み"},{"japanese":"春になると、切り株から新しい枝が萌芽する。","romaji":"Haru ni naru to, kirikabu kara atarashii eda ga houga suru.","german":"In spring, new branches sprout from the tree stump.","furigana":"はる|き|かぶ|あたら|えだ|ほうが"}],"notes":"萌芽 literally means the sprouting of buds and shoots, and figuratively the first beginnings of a development. The figurative meaning clearly predominates: 〜の萌芽 (近代科学の萌芽, 恋の萌芽), 萌芽が見られる, 萌芽期 \"initial phase\". It is written language; in everyday speech one says 芽生え or 芽生える. For germinating seeds 発芽 is more usual; 萌芽 is used in botany more for buds and stump sprouts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は突然体の平衡を失い、ひっくり返った。","romaji":"Kanojo wa totsuzen karada no heikō o ushinai, hikkurikaetta.","german":"She suddenly lost her balance and fell over.","furigana":"かのじょ|とつぜんからだ|へいこう|うしな|かえ"},{"japanese":"彼は平衡感覚が優れているので、綱渡りも得意だ。","romaji":"Kare wa heikou kankaku ga sugurete iru node, tsunawatari mo tokui da.","german":"He has an excellent sense of balance and is even good at tightrope walking.","furigana":"かれ|へいこうかんかく|すぐ|つなわた|とくい"},{"japanese":"化学反応は、やがて平衡状態に達する。","romaji":"Kagaku hannou wa, yagate heikou joutai ni tassuru.","german":"A chemical reaction eventually reaches a state of equilibrium.","furigana":"かがくはんのう|へいこうじょうたい|たっ"}],"notes":"平衡 is equilibrium in the physical, bodily and chemical sense: 平衡を保つ/失う, 平衡感覚 \"sense of balance\", 平衡状態, 化学平衡. In everyday speech one mostly says バランス; 均衡 refers more to a balance of forces or in economics and politics (需要と供給の均衡). Beware of the homophones 平行 \"parallel\", 並行 \"simultaneous\" and 閉口 \"to be speechless\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"航海の途中で海賊に襲われました。","romaji":"Koukai no tochuu de kaizoku ni osowaremashita.","german":"We were attacked by pirates in the middle of the voyage.","furigana":"こうかい|とちゅう|かいぞく|おそ"},{"japanese":"海賊版のDVDを買ってはいけません。","romaji":"Kaizokuban no DVD o katte wa ikemasen.","german":"You must not buy pirated DVDs.","furigana":"かいぞくばん|か"},{"japanese":"子どもの頃、海賊が出てくる冒険小説が大好きだった。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, kaizoku ga dete kuru bouken shousetsu ga daisuki datta.","german":"As a child, I loved adventure novels with pirates.","furigana":"こ|ころ|かいぞく|で|ぼうけんしょうせつ|だいす"}],"notes":"海賊 is a \"pirate, sea robber\". One is attacked by pirates: 海賊に襲われる. Common compounds are 海賊船 \"pirate ship\", 海賊行為 \"piracy\" and especially 海賊版 \"pirated copy\" (illegally copied DVDs, software, books). The counterpart on land is 山賊 \"mountain bandit\"; generally, robbers are 盗賊.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"昔、この峠には山賊が出たそうだ。","romaji":"Mukashi, kono touge ni wa sanzoku ga deta sou da.","german":"They say bandits used to appear on this mountain pass.","furigana":"むかし|とうげ|さんぞく|で"},{"japanese":"旅人は山賊に襲われて、持ち物をすべて奪われた。","romaji":"Tabibito wa sanzoku ni osowarete, mochimono o subete ubawareta.","german":"The traveler was attacked by bandits and robbed of all his belongings.","furigana":"たびびと|さんぞく|おそ|も|もの|うば"},{"japanese":"この店の山賊焼きは名物です。","romaji":"Kono mise no sanzokuyaki wa meibutsu desu.","german":"The sanzoku-yaki at this restaurant is a specialty.","furigana":"みせ|さんぞくや|めいぶつ"}],"notes":"山賊 are \"bandits, highwaymen\" who rob travelers in the mountains -- a word found mainly in history, fairy tales and adventure stories. Typical: 山賊が出る \"bandits are active\", 山賊に襲われる. The counterpart at sea is 海賊, and the more general word is 盗賊. 山賊焼き is a dish from Nagano (fried chicken) and shares only the name with robbers.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今度こそ盗賊の隠れ家を突き止めてやる。","romaji":"Kondo koso tōzoku no kakurega o tsukitomete yaru.","german":"This time I'll track down the robbers' hideout!","furigana":"こんど|とうぞく|かく|が|つ|と"},{"japanese":"『アリババと四十人の盗賊』は有名な物語です。","romaji":"\"Aribaba to yonjuunin no touzoku\" wa yuumei na monogatari desu.","german":"\"Ali Baba and the Forty Thieves\" is a famous story.","furigana":"よんじゅうにん|とうぞく|ゆうめい|ものがたり"},{"japanese":"その城は盗賊に襲われ、宝物を奪われた。","romaji":"Sono shiro wa touzoku ni osoware, takaramono o ubawareta.","german":"The castle was attacked by robbers, and its treasures were stolen.","furigana":"しろ|とうぞく|おそ|たからもの|うば"}],"notes":"盗賊 means \"robbers, thieves\", often as a gang -- a rather archaic, narrative word known from fairy tales, period dramas and titles such as 『アリババと四十人の盗賊』. For today's burglar or pickpocket, one says 泥棒, 空き巣 or スリ; in the news 窃盗犯 or 強盗. 盗賊団 is a \"gang of robbers\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私は、迎賓館に招待されました。","romaji":"Watashi wa, geihinkan ni shoutai saremashita.","german":"I was invited to the State Guest House.","furigana":"わたし|げいひんかん|しょうたい"},{"japanese":"赤坂の迎賓館は一般公開されることがあります。","romaji":"Akasaka no geihinkan wa ippan koukai sareru koto ga arimasu.","german":"The State Guest House in Akasaka is sometimes open to the public.","furigana":"あかさか|げいひんかん|いっぱんこうかい"},{"japanese":"首脳会談は京都迎賓館で行われた。","romaji":"Shunou kaidan wa Kyouto geihinkan de okonawareta.","german":"The summit meeting was held at the Kyoto State Guest House.","furigana":"しゅのうかいだん|きょうとげいひんかん|おこな"}],"notes":"迎賓館 is a \"guest house for state guests\" in which foreign heads of state are received and accommodated. Usually what is meant is the 迎賓館 in Akasaka (Tokyo) or the 京都迎賓館; both can be visited at certain times. For an ordinary guest house, one says ゲストハウス; for a reception hall in hotels, rather ロビー or 宴会場.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一遍だけでいいから、キャビアを食べてみたかったの。","romaji":"Ippen dake de ii kara, kyabia o tabete mitakatta no.","german":"Even just once, I wanted to try caviar.","furigana":"いっぺん|た"},{"japanese":"もう一遍言ってみろ！","romaji":"Mou ippen itte miro!","german":"Say that one more time, if you dare!","furigana":"いっぺんい"},{"japanese":"そんなに一遍に言われても覚えられないよ。","romaji":"Sonna ni ippen ni iwarete mo oboerarenai yo.","german":"Even if you tell me so much all at once, I can't remember it.","furigana":"いっぺん|い|おぼ"}],"notes":"一遍 is a colloquial variant of 一度 or 一回 \"once\" and is usually written in kana as いっぺん: もういっぺん \"once more\". いっぺんに means \"all at once, in one go\". As a suffix, 一遍 means \"only, exclusively\", as in 通り一遍 \"superficial, pro forma\". Not to be confused with the identically read 一変 \"complete change\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この映画は何遍みても泣けます。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa nanben mite mo nakemasu.","german":"No matter how many times I watch this movie, I cry.","furigana":"えいが|なんべん|な"},{"japanese":"何遍言ったらわかるの？","romaji":"Nanben ittara wakaru no?","german":"How many times do I have to tell you?","furigana":"なんべんい"},{"japanese":"京都にはもう何遍も行きました。","romaji":"Kyouto ni wa mou nanben mo ikimashita.","german":"I've been to Kyoto countless times already.","furigana":"きょうと|なんべん|い"}],"notes":"何遍 is the colloquial counterpart of 何度 or 何回 \"how often, how many times\". Typical are 何遍も \"many times, over and over\", 何遍でも \"as often as you like\" and reproachful questions such as 何遍言ったらわかるの？. With 〜ても it expresses \"no matter how often\". In written or polite texts, one prefers 何度.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"マイケルにはおもしろい恋愛遍歴がある。","romaji":"Maikeru ni wa omoshiroi ren'ai henreki ga aru.","german":"Michael has an interesting romantic history.","furigana":"れんあいへんれき"},{"japanese":"若い頃、彼は諸国を遍歴して修行を積んだ。","romaji":"Wakai koro, kare wa shokoku o henreki shite shugyou o tsunda.","german":"In his youth, he traveled through many lands and trained himself along the way.","furigana":"わか|ころ|かれ|しょこく|へんれき|しゅぎょう|つ"},{"japanese":"彼女の職業遍歴は実に多彩だ。","romaji":"Kanojo no shokugyou henreki wa jitsu ni tasai da.","german":"Her career history is truly varied.","furigana":"かのじょ|しょくぎょうへんれき|じつ|たさい"}],"notes":"遍歴 originally means \"wandering\" from place to place (諸国を遍歴する \"to travel through many lands\"); today, figuratively, it mostly means a sequence of varied experiences: 恋愛遍歴 \"love life, past relationships\", 職業遍歴 \"career path with many stations\". It stresses the many and the changeable; a straightforward life history is rather 経歴 or 履歴. Written and elevated.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人の話を遮るのは失礼です。","romaji":"Hito no hanashi o saegiru no wa shitsurei desu.","german":"It is rude to interrupt someone.","furigana":"ひと|はなし|さえぎ|しつれい"},{"japanese":"高い建物が日差しを遮っている。","romaji":"Takai tatemono ga hizashi o saegitte iru.","german":"A tall building blocks the sunlight.","furigana":"たか|たてもの|ひざ|さえぎ"},{"japanese":"倒れた木が行く手を遮った。","romaji":"Taoreta ki ga yukute o saegitta.","german":"A fallen tree blocked our way.","furigana":"たお|き|ゆ|て|さえぎ"}],"notes":"遮る means \"to interrupt, to block, to shield\" and is transitive with を: 話を遮る \"to cut someone off\", 視界を遮る \"to block the view\", 光や風を遮る \"to keep out light or wind\", 行く手を遮る \"to block the way\". There is no intransitive counterpart; the passive 遮られる is used. Despite its ending in -いる, it is a godan verb. For stopping a process, one rather says 中断する.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"サイトへのアクセスが遮断されました。","romaji":"Saito e no akusesu ga shadan saremashita.","german":"Access to the website was blocked.","furigana":"しゃだん"},{"japanese":"踏切の遮断機が下りてきた。","romaji":"Fumikiri no shadanki ga orite kita.","german":"The crossing gate at the railroad crossing came down.","furigana":"ふみきり|しゃだんき|お"},{"japanese":"このカーテンは外の光をほぼ完全に遮断します。","romaji":"Kono kaaten wa soto no hikari o hobo kanzen ni shadan shimasu.","german":"These curtains block almost all of the light from outside.","furigana":"そと|ひかり|かんぜん|しゃだん"}],"notes":"遮断 is the \"cutting off, shielding, interrupting\" of a flow or a connection, transitive as 遮断する or passive 遮断される: 交通を遮断する, 電源を遮断する, 光や音を遮断する, 外部との連絡を遮断する. It is technical and factual; for interrupting a conversation, one says 遮る, not 遮断. 遮断機 is the crossing gate at a railroad crossing.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自作酵母を使ってパンを作っています。","romaji":"Jisaku kōbo o tsukatte pan o tsukutte imasu.","german":"I make bread using my own homemade yeast.","furigana":"じさくこうぼ|つか|つく"},{"japanese":"ビールは酵母が糖をアルコールに変えることでできる。","romaji":"Biiru wa koubo ga tou o arukooru ni kaeru koto de dekiru.","german":"Beer is made by yeast converting sugar into alcohol.","furigana":"こうぼ|とう|か"},{"japanese":"このパン屋は天然酵母しか使っていない。","romaji":"Kono pan'ya wa tennen koubo shika tsukatte inai.","german":"This bakery uses only wild yeast.","furigana":"や|てんねんこうぼ|つか"}],"notes":"酵母 is \"yeast\" as a microorganism and technical term, for example in 酵母菌, 天然酵母 \"natural leaven/wild yeast\" and ビール酵母 \"brewer's yeast\". In everyday cooking, baker's yeast is mostly called イースト or ドライイースト. The process that yeast brings about is 発酵 \"fermentation\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ワインの甘みを残して発酵を止める方法を教えてください。","romaji":"Wain no amami o nokoshite hakkō o tomeru hōhō o oshiete kudasai.","german":"Please tell me how to stop fermentation while keeping the wine's sweetness.","furigana":"あま|のこ|はっこう|と|ほうほう|おし"},{"japanese":"納豆は大豆を発酵させて作ります。","romaji":"Nattou wa daizu o hakkou sasete tsukurimasu.","german":"Natto is made by fermenting soybeans.","furigana":"なっとう|だいず|はっこう|つく"},{"japanese":"パン生地が十分に発酵するまで一時間待ちます。","romaji":"Pan kiji ga juubun ni hakkou suru made ichijikan machimasu.","german":"Wait an hour until the bread dough has fermented sufficiently.","furigana":"きじ|じゅうぶん|はっこう|いちじかんま"}],"notes":"発酵 is \"fermentation\". As a する-verb it is intransitive (生地が発酵する \"the dough rises\"); for letting something ferment, one says 発酵させる. Important words are 発酵食品 \"fermented foods\" (味噌, 納豆, ヨーグルト) and 発酵茶. The older spelling 醗酵 still occurs. The opposite is spoilage: 腐敗 or 腐る.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ビールの醸造所を見学してきました。","romaji":"Biiru no jouzousho o kengaku shite kimashita.","german":"I toured a beer brewery.","furigana":"じょうぞうしょ|けんがく"},{"japanese":"この蔵では江戸時代から日本酒を醸造している。","romaji":"Kono kura de wa Edo jidai kara nihonshu o jouzou shite iru.","german":"Sake has been brewed at this brewery since the Edo period.","furigana":"くら|えどじだい|にほんしゅ|じょうぞう"},{"japanese":"醤油は大豆と小麦を原料に醸造される。","romaji":"Shouyu wa daizu to komugi o genryou ni jouzou sareru.","german":"Soy sauce is brewed from soybeans and wheat.","furigana":"しょうゆ|だいず|こむぎ|げんりょう|じょうぞう"}],"notes":"醸造 is \"brewing\" through fermentation, that is, the production of beer, sake, wine, soy sauce, miso or vinegar; transitive: 日本酒を醸造する. The brewery itself is 醸造所, and for sake also 酒蔵. Important is the contrast between 醸造酒 (beer, wine, sake) and 蒸留酒 (whisky, shochu): whisky is distilled in a 蒸留所, not brewed. The base verb 醸す is literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"とりあえず、今は父の怒りが鎮まるのを待ちましょう。","romaji":"Toriaezu, ima wa chichi no ikari ga shizumaru no o machimashō.","german":"For now, let's wait for Father's anger to subside.","furigana":"いま|ちち|いか|しず|ま"},{"japanese":"警察の出動で、ようやく暴動が鎮まった。","romaji":"Keisatsu no shutsudou de, youyaku boudou ga shizumatta.","german":"It was only when the police arrived that the riot subsided.","furigana":"けいさつ|しゅつどう|ぼうどう|しず"},{"japanese":"薬を飲んだら、痛みが少し鎮まった。","romaji":"Kusuri o nondara, itami ga sukoshi shizumatta.","german":"After I took the medicine, the pain eased a little.","furigana":"くすり|の|いた|すこ|しず"}],"notes":"鎮まる is intransitive: unrest, anger, pain or agitation \"subsides, calms down\", e.g. 怒りが鎮まる, 暴動が鎮まる, 痛みが鎮まる. The transitive counterpart is 鎮める \"to soothe, to suppress\". The identically read 静まる is used more for noise and sounds that fall silent (会場が静まる). In everyday speech one often says 収まる as well.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この国ではネット検閲が行われています。","romaji":"Kono kuni de wa netto ken'etsu ga okonawarete imasu.","german":"Internet censorship is practiced in this country.","furigana":"くに|けんえつ|おこな"},{"japanese":"戦時中は手紙も検閲された。","romaji":"Senjichuu wa tegami mo ken'etsu sareta.","german":"During the war, even letters were censored.","furigana":"せんじちゅう|てがみ|けんえつ"},{"japanese":"その映画は検閲で一部のシーンがカットされた。","romaji":"Sono eiga wa ken'etsu de ichibu no shiin ga katto sareta.","german":"Some scenes of that film were cut by the censors.","furigana":"えいが|けんえつ|いちぶ"}],"notes":"検閲 is above all state \"censorship\" of the press, mail, films or the internet; transitive: 手紙を検閲する, mostly passive 検閲される. Common are ネット検閲, 検閲制度 and 自己検閲 \"self-censorship\". The second meaning, \"inspection\" (military), is rare; for ordinary checks, one says 検査 or 点検. Voluntary restraint by the media is rather 自主規制.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの監督は、選手たちの士気を鼓舞するのがうまい。","romaji":"Ano kantoku wa, senshutachi no shiki o kobu suru no ga umai.","german":"That manager is good at boosting the players' morale.","furigana":"かんとく|せんしゅ|しき|こぶ"},{"japanese":"国王の演説が兵士たちを鼓舞した。","romaji":"Kokuou no enzetsu ga heishitachi o kobu shita.","german":"The king's speech fired up the soldiers.","furigana":"こくおう|えんぜつ|へいし|こぶ"},{"japanese":"自らを鼓舞して、最後まで走り切った。","romaji":"Mizukara o kobu shite, saigo made hashirikitta.","german":"I psyched myself up and ran all the way to the end.","furigana":"みずか|こぶ|さいご|はし|き"}],"notes":"鼓舞 means \"to fire up, to encourage, to rouse\", literally \"to drum and dance\". It is written language and typically occurs with 士気を鼓舞する \"to raise morale\" or 人々を鼓舞する; also 自らを鼓舞する \"to psych oneself up\". In everyday speech one says 励ます (to comfort/encourage an individual) or 盛り上げる; 激励 is the formal giving of encouragement.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"少女の顔が赤くなり、鼓動が高鳴りました。","romaji":"Shōjo no kao ga akaku nari, kodō ga takanarimashita.","german":"The girl's face turned red and her heart pounded.","furigana":"しょうじょ|かお|あか|こどう|たかな"},{"japanese":"聴診器で赤ちゃんの心臓の鼓動を聞いた。","romaji":"Choushinki de akachan no shinzou no kodou o kiita.","german":"With the stethoscope, I heard the baby's heartbeat.","furigana":"ちょうしんき|あか|しんぞう|こどう|き"},{"japanese":"この街には新しい時代の鼓動が感じられる。","romaji":"Kono machi ni wa atarashii jidai no kodou ga kanjirareru.","german":"You can feel the pulse of a new era in this city.","furigana":"まち|あたら|じだい|こどう|かん"}],"notes":"鼓動 is the \"heartbeat\" as a perceptible pounding, especially in excitement: 鼓動が高鳴る, 鼓動が速くなる. Medically and soberly, one speaks of 心拍 or 脈 \"pulse\"; onomatopoeically one says ドキドキする. Figuratively, 鼓動 can also mean the \"pulsing\" of an era or a city (新しい時代の鼓動). As a verb, 鼓動する is rare and literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"温泉に入って旅の疲れを癒した。","romaji":"Onsen ni haitte tabi no tsukare o iyashita.","german":"I soothed my travel fatigue by soaking in a hot spring.","furigana":"おんせん|はい|たび|つか|いや"},{"japanese":"冷たい水でのどの渇きを癒した。","romaji":"Tsumetai mizu de nodo no kawaki o iyashita.","german":"I quenched my thirst with cold water.","furigana":"つめ|みず|かわ|いや"},{"japanese":"時間が心の傷を癒してくれるはずだ。","romaji":"Jikan ga kokoro no kizu o iyashite kureru hazu da.","german":"Time should heal the wounds of the heart.","furigana":"じかん|こころ|きず|いや"}],"notes":"癒す (also 癒やす) means \"to heal, to soothe, to quench\" and is transitive: 疲れを癒す \"to recover\", 心の傷を癒す, のどの渇きを癒す \"to quench thirst\". It is mostly about exhaustion, emotional wounds or needs; the medical healing of an illness is rather 治す. The intransitive counterpart is 癒える \"to heal over\". The noun 癒し \"relaxation, comfort\" is very widespread (癒し系).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"遼太は怒りで顔面蒼白になった。","romaji":"Ryōta wa ikari de ganmen sōhaku ni natta.","german":"Ryota's face turned pale with anger.","furigana":"りょうた|いか|がんめんそうはく"},{"japanese":"事故の知らせを聞いて、母は顔面蒼白になった。","romaji":"Jiko no shirase o kiite, haha wa ganmen sōhaku ni natta.","german":"When my mother heard the news of the accident, she turned deathly pale.","furigana":"じこ|し|き|はは|がんめんそうはく"},{"japanese":"彼は高熱で、蒼白な顔をしてベッドに横たわっていた。","romaji":"Kare wa kōnetsu de, sōhaku na kao o shite beddo ni yokotawatte ita.","german":"He lay in bed with a high fever and a pallid face.","furigana":"かれ|こうねつ|そうはく|かお|よこ"}],"notes":"蒼白 describes a face color that is ashen and pale from fright, fear or illness, and is written language. It almost always refers to the face: 顔面蒼白になる, 顔が蒼白になる, 蒼白な顔. In conversation one rather says 顔が青白い or 青ざめる \"to turn pale\"; paleness is expressed in Japanese with 青. For light skin as a beauty feature, 蒼白 is not appropriate; for that one says 色白.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この男は、東方の国から来たらしい。","romaji":"Kono otoko wa, touhou no kuni kara kita rashii.","german":"This man seems to have come from a country in the East.","furigana":"おとこ|とうほう|くに|き"},{"japanese":"村の東方に小さな山がある。","romaji":"Mura no touhou ni chiisana yama ga aru.","german":"There is a small mountain to the east of the village.","furigana":"むら|とうほう|ちい|やま"},{"japanese":"クリスマス劇で、東方の三博士の役を演じた。","romaji":"Kurisumasu geki de, touhou no san hakase no yaku o enjita.","german":"In the Christmas play, I played one of the Magi.","furigana":"げき|とうほう|さんはかせ|やく|えん"}],"notes":"東方 is the written-language word for \"east, eastern direction\" (村の東方に \"east of the village\") and for the \"Orient\", for example in 東方の三博士 \"the Magi (Wise Men from the East)\" or 東方正教会 \"Eastern Church\". In everyday speech one simply says 東 or 東の方 (ひがしのほう). The counterpart is 西方.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本代表は延長戦の末、二対一で辛勝した。","romaji":"Nihon daihyou wa enchousen no sue, ni tai ichi de shinshou shita.","german":"The Japan national team won a narrow 2-1 victory after extra time.","furigana":"にほんだいひょう|えんちょうせん|すえ|にたいいち|しんしょう"},{"japanese":"現職の市長が選挙で新人に辛勝した。","romaji":"Genshoku no shichou ga senkyo de shinjin ni shinshou shita.","german":"The incumbent mayor narrowly beat the newcomer in the election.","furigana":"げんしょく|しちょう|せんきょ|しんじん|しんしょう"},{"japanese":"辛勝とはいえ、勝ちは勝ちだ。","romaji":"Shinshou to wa ie, kachi wa kachi da.","german":"Even if it was a narrow win, a win is a win.","furigana":"しんしょう|か|か"}],"notes":"辛勝 is a \"narrow victory\", literally \"to win with difficulty\"; as an intransitive する-verb the opponent takes に: 強豪に辛勝する. The word comes from sports and election reports. Opposites are 楽勝 \"easy win\", 圧勝 \"landslide victory\" and 惜敗 \"narrow defeat\". Colloquially one says ギリギリで勝つ or 辛くも勝つ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"我が社は円安の恩恵を大いに享受している。","romaji":"Waga sha wa en'yasu no onkei o ooi ni kyouju shite iru.","german":"Our company is enjoying great benefits from the weak yen.","furigana":"わ|しゃ|えんやす|おんけい|おお|きょうじゅ"},{"japanese":"すべての国民は、自由と平等を享受する権利がある。","romaji":"Subete no kokumin wa, jiyuu to byoudou o kyouju suru kenri ga aru.","german":"All citizens have the right to enjoy freedom and equality.","furigana":"こくみん|じゆう|びょうどう|きょうじゅ|けんり"},{"japanese":"退職後、父は田舎での静かな暮らしを享受している。","romaji":"Taishokugo, chichi wa inaka de no shizuka na kurashi o kyouju shite iru.","german":"Since retiring, my father has been enjoying a quiet life in the countryside.","furigana":"たいしょくご|ちち|いなか|しず|く|きょうじゅ"}],"notes":"享受 means \"to enjoy, to come to enjoy something\" and is a formal, transitive する-verb. Its objects are abstract things such as 恩恵, 権利, 自由, 豊かさ or 平和: 恩恵を享受する \"to benefit from something\". For personal enjoyment of food or leisure, use 楽しむ or 味わう. Do not confuse it with the identically read 教授 \"professor\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本史上、最強の豪傑は誰だと思いますか。","romaji":"Nihon shijou, saikyou no gouketsu wa dare da to omoimasu ka.","german":"Who do you think was the strongest hero in Japanese history?","furigana":"にほんしじょう|さいきょう|ごうけつ|だれ|おも"},{"japanese":"『三国志』には数多くの豪傑が登場する。","romaji":"\"Sangokushi\" ni wa kazuooku no gouketsu ga toujou suru.","german":"Numerous bold heroes appear in \"Romance of the Three Kingdoms\".","furigana":"さんごくし|かずおお|ごうけつ|とうじょう"},{"japanese":"祖父は細かいことを気にしない豪傑タイプの人だった。","romaji":"Sofu wa komakai koto o ki ni shinai gouketsu taipu no hito datta.","german":"My grandfather was a fearless type who didn't worry about small things.","furigana":"そふ|こま|き|ごうけつ|ひと"}],"notes":"豪傑 is a \"hero, bold warrior\": someone of great physical strength and fearlessness, especially in war stories such as 三国志. In contrast to 英雄 \"hero\" (great deeds, role model), 豪傑 emphasizes strength, daring and a generous, carefree nature. Today it is also used half-jokingly for people with a rough, carefree temperament (豪傑タイプ).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"解剖をしてみないと何とも言えません。","romaji":"Kaibō o shite minai to nantomo iemasen.","german":"We can't say anything until we have performed an autopsy.","furigana":"かいぼう|なん|い"},{"japanese":"理科の授業でカエルの解剖をした。","romaji":"Rika no jugyou de kaeru no kaibou o shita.","german":"In science class we dissected a frog.","furigana":"りか|じゅぎょう|かいぼう"},{"japanese":"その本は現代社会の問題を鋭く解剖している。","romaji":"Sono hon wa gendai shakai no mondai o surudoku kaibou shite iru.","german":"The book sharply dissects the problems of modern society.","furigana":"ほん|げんだいしゃかい|もんだい|するど|かいぼう"}],"notes":"解剖 means \"dissection, autopsy\" and is transitive: カエルを解剖する. Common are 司法解剖 \"forensic autopsy\", 病理解剖 and 解剖学 \"anatomy\". Figuratively it means a thorough \"dissection, analysis\" of a problem or work (社会を解剖する). For a doctor's examination of living patients, use 診察 or 検査, not 解剖.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この呆け！ちゃんと前見て歩け。","romaji":"Kono boke! Chanto mae mite aruke.","german":"You idiot! Watch where you're walking.","furigana":"ぼ|まえみ|ある"},{"japanese":"時差ぼけで昼間も眠くてたまらない。","romaji":"Jisaboke de hiruma mo nemukute tamaranai.","german":"Because of jet lag I'm unbearably sleepy even during the day.","furigana":"じさ|ひるま|ねむ"},{"japanese":"漫才では、ぼけとツッコミの掛け合いが大事だ。","romaji":"Manzai de wa, boke to tsukkomi no kakeai ga daiji da.","german":"In manzai, the interplay between the boke and the tsukkomi is important.","furigana":"まんざい|か|あ|だいじ"}],"notes":"ぼけ (rarely 呆け, often ボケ) is first of all a term of abuse, \"fool, idiot\", particularly strong in the Kansai dialect (このボケ！). It also colloquially refers to declining mental faculties in old age; today the more respectful 認知症 is used for that. As a suffix it means \"dazed from\": 時差ぼけ \"jet lag\", 寝ぼけ. In 漫才, the ボケ is the comedian who talks nonsense and the ツッコミ is the one who corrects him.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女のおかしな言動に、みんな呆気に取られていました。","romaji":"Kanojo no okashina gendou ni, minna akke ni torarete imashita.","german":"Everyone was dumbfounded by her strange behavior.","furigana":"かのじょ|げんどう|あっけ|と"},{"japanese":"あまりの値段の高さに、呆気に取られた。","romaji":"Amari no nedan no takasa ni, akke ni torareta.","german":"I was stunned by how high the price was.","furigana":"ねだん|たか|あっけ|と"},{"japanese":"楽しみにしていた試合は、呆気なく終わってしまった。","romaji":"Tanoshimi ni shite ita shiai wa, akkenaku owatte shimatta.","german":"The match I had been looking forward to ended disappointingly quickly.","furigana":"たの|しあい|あっけ|お"}],"notes":"呆気 occurs practically only in two expressions and is usually written in kana: 呆気に取られる \"to be dumbfounded, speechless\" (frozen with surprise) and the adjective 呆気ない \"disappointingly short, over all too quickly\". The cause is marked with に: 彼の言葉に呆気に取られる. Similar is ぽかんとする; stronger is 唖然とする.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"部屋があまりに汚かったので、呆れ返ってしまいました。","romaji":"Heya ga amarini kitanakatta node, akirekaette shimaimashita.","german":"I was utterly appalled because the room was so dirty.","furigana":"へや|きたな|あき|かえ"},{"japanese":"彼の無責任な態度には、ほとほと呆れ返った。","romaji":"Kare no musekinin na taido ni wa, hotohoto akirekaetta.","german":"I was truly aghast at his irresponsible attitude.","furigana":"かれ|むせきにん|たいど|あき|かえ"},{"japanese":"あまりの非常識さに、呆れ返って物も言えない。","romaji":"Amari no hijoushikisa ni, akirekaette mono mo ienai.","german":"I'm so appalled at such lack of common sense that I'm speechless.","furigana":"ひじょうしき|あき|かえ|もの|い"}],"notes":"呆れ返る is an intensification of 呆れる: \"to be completely stunned, appalled\", usually out of indignation or disgust at stupidity, impudence or sloppiness. The trigger is marked with に: 彼の態度に呆れ返る. Set phrase: 呆れ返って物も言えない \"so appalled that one is lost for words\". Intransitive; often written in kana as あきれ返る.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"うちの社長は資金繰りに奔走しています。","romaji":"Uchi no shachō wa shikinguri ni honsō shite imasu.","german":"Our company president is busy scrambling to arrange funding.","furigana":"しゃちょう|しきんぐ|ほんそう"},{"japanese":"彼は被災地の支援のために奔走した。","romaji":"Kare wa hisaichi no shien no tame ni honsou shita.","german":"He ran around tirelessly to support the disaster area.","furigana":"かれ|ひさいち|しえん|ほんそう"},{"japanese":"友人の奔走のおかげで、就職先が見つかった。","romaji":"Yuujin no honsou no okage de, shuushokusaki ga mitsukatta.","german":"Thanks to a friend's efforts, I found a job.","furigana":"ゆうじん|ほんそう|しゅうしょくさき|み"}],"notes":"奔走 literally means \"running around\" and refers to working tirelessly for a goal, running everywhere and negotiating with many people. The goal is marked with に or 〜のために: 資金集めに奔走する. As a noun it also means the \"mediation, efforts\" of others: 友人の奔走で. Related is the saying 東奔西走. Literary; in everyday speech, 走り回る or 駆け回る is used.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの自由奔放な性格に惹かれてしまったんだ。","romaji":"Ano jiyuu honpou na seikaku ni hikarete shimatta n da.","german":"I was drawn to that free-spirited personality.","furigana":"じゆうほんぽう|せいかく|"},{"japanese":"彼女は奔放な想像力で独特な絵を描く。","romaji":"Kanojo wa honpou na souzouryoku de dokutoku na e o kaku.","german":"She paints unique pictures with her unbridled imagination.","furigana":"かのじょ|ほんぽう|そうぞうりょく|どくとく|え|か"},{"japanese":"若い頃は、親の言うことも聞かずに奔放に生きていた。","romaji":"Wakai koro wa, oya no iu koto mo kikazu ni honpou ni ikite ita.","german":"In my youth I lived a wild life without listening to my parents.","furigana":"わか|ころ|おや|い|き|ほんぽう|い"}],"notes":"奔放 means \"unrestrained, free and unconstrained\": someone follows only their own impulses, without regard for conventions. It almost always appears in the combination 自由奔放 (な性格, に生きる). Depending on context it sounds positive (free-spirited, creative: 奔放な想像力) or negative (licentious, e.g. 恋愛に奔放). It is a な-adjective: 奔放な, adverbial 奔放に. わがまま, in contrast, emphasizes selfishness.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"紙媒体で仕入れた情報の方が何故か安心するんだよね。","romaji":"Kami baitai de shiireta jōhō no hō ga nazeka anshin suru n da yo ne.","german":"For some reason I feel more reassured by information I get from print media.","furigana":"かみばいたい|しい|じょうほう|ほう|なぜ|あんしん"},{"japanese":"広告をどの媒体に出すか検討している。","romaji":"Koukoku o dono baitai ni dasu ka kentou shite iru.","german":"We are considering which medium to place the advertisement in.","furigana":"こうこく|ばいたい|だ|けんとう"},{"japanese":"データは外部の記録媒体に保存してください。","romaji":"Deeta wa gaibu no kiroku baitai ni hozon shite kudasai.","german":"Please save the data on an external storage medium.","furigana":"がいぶ|きろくばいたい|ほぞん"}],"notes":"媒体 is a \"medium, carrier\": on the one hand communication channels such as 紙媒体 \"print media\", 広告媒体, 電子媒体; on the other hand technical carriers such as 記録媒体 \"storage medium\", and physically the substance that transmits sound or heat. For \"the media\" as the press, メディア or マスコミ is usually used. Do not confuse it with 媒介 \"mediation, transmission (e.g. of diseases)\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私どもの結婚式の媒酌の労をおとりいただき、本当にありがとうございました。","romaji":"Watashidomo no kekkonshiki no baishaku no rō o otori itadaki, hontō ni arigatō gozaimashita.","german":"Thank you so much for taking the trouble to act as go-between at our wedding.","furigana":"わたし|けっこんしき|ばいしゃく|ろう|ほんとう"},{"japanese":"二人は大学の恩師の媒酌で結婚した。","romaji":"Futari wa daigaku no onshi no baishaku de kekkon shita.","german":"The two married through the matchmaking of their former university professor.","furigana":"ふたり|だいがく|おんし|ばいしゃく|けっこん"},{"japanese":"最近は媒酌人を立てない結婚式が増えている。","romaji":"Saikin wa baishakunin o tatenai kekkonshiki ga fuete iru.","german":"Recently there are more and more weddings without a go-between.","furigana":"さいきん|ばいしゃくにん|た|けっこんしき|ふ"}],"notes":"媒酌 refers to the role of the official matchmaker or go-between (仲人) at a traditional Japanese wedding. The person is called 媒酌人, which is more formal than 仲人 and typical of wedding speeches and invitation cards. Set phrases are 〜の媒酌で結婚する and 媒酌の労をとる (\"to take on the role of go-between\"); as a verb, 媒酌する is rarely used. Today many couples do without a 媒酌人 altogether.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ほら、あそこにヨットが帆走しているよ。","romaji":"Hora, asoko ni yotto ga hansō shite iru yo.","german":"Look, a yacht is sailing over there.","furigana":"はんそう"},{"japanese":"この船はエンジンを使わず、風の力だけで帆走する。","romaji":"Kono fune wa enjin o tsukawazu, kaze no chikara dake de hansou suru.","german":"This ship sails with wind power alone, without an engine.","furigana":"ふね|つか|かぜ|ちから|はんそう"},{"japanese":"練習船は太平洋を帆走して横断した。","romaji":"Renshuusen wa Taiheiyou o hansou shite oudan shita.","german":"The training ship crossed the Pacific under sail.","furigana":"れんしゅうせん|たいへいよう|はんそう|おうだん"}],"notes":"帆走 is \"sailing\" in the sense of \"traveling by sail power\" and belongs to technical and written language (帆走訓練, 帆走中). Intransitive; the route traveled is marked with を: 太平洋を帆走する. In everyday speech, ヨットに乗る or セーリングする is more common. Related: 帆船 \"sailing ship\", 帆を張る \"to set the sails\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の叔父は、弦楽器の修理をしています。","romaji":"Watashi no oji wa, gengakki no shūri o shite imasu.","german":"My uncle repairs stringed instruments.","furigana":"わたし|おじ|げんがっき|しゅうり"},{"japanese":"バイオリンやチェロは弦楽器の仲間です。","romaji":"Baiorin ya chero wa gengakki no nakama desu.","german":"Violins and cellos are members of the string family.","furigana":"げんがっき|なかま"},{"japanese":"三味線や琴も弦楽器に含まれる。","romaji":"Shamisen ya koto mo gengakki ni fukumareru.","german":"The shamisen and koto are also counted as stringed instruments.","furigana":"しゃみせん|こと|げんがっき|ふく"}],"notes":"弦楽器 are all \"stringed instruments\", both bowed ones such as バイオリン and チェロ and plucked ones such as ギター, 琴 or 三味線. \"Bowed string instrument\" in the narrow sense is the technical term 擦弦楽器. The other groups are 管楽器 \"wind instruments\" and 打楽器 \"percussion instruments\". Related: 弦楽四重奏 \"string quartet\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は管弦楽のための組曲を作曲した。","romaji":"Kare wa kangengaku no tame no kumikyoku o sakkyoku shita.","german":"He composed a suite for orchestra.","furigana":"かれ|かんげんがく|くみきょく|さっきょく"},{"japanese":"週末に市民会館で管弦楽の演奏会がある。","romaji":"Shuumatsu ni shimin kaikan de kangengaku no ensoukai ga aru.","german":"There is an orchestral concert at the civic hall this weekend.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|しみんかいかん|かんげんがく|えんそうかい"},{"japanese":"大学の管弦楽団でチェロを弾いている。","romaji":"Daigaku no kangengakudan de chero o hiite iru.","german":"I play the cello in the university orchestra.","furigana":"だいがく|かんげんがくだん|ひ"}],"notes":"管弦楽 is \"orchestral music\", literally music for \"pipes (winds) and strings\". The word is formal and appears in titles of works (管弦楽のための〜) and concert programs. The orchestra itself is called 管弦楽団, but in everyday speech usually オーケストラ. Chamber music is 室内楽, a wind band is 吹奏楽.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"園児たちが音楽にあわせて遊戯をしています。","romaji":"Enjitachi ga ongaku ni awasete yūgi o shite imasu.","german":"The kindergarten children are doing action games to music.","furigana":"えんじ|おんがく|ゆうぎ"},{"japanese":"運動会で年長組がお遊戯を披露した。","romaji":"Undoukai de nenchougumi ga oyuugi o hirou shita.","german":"At the sports day, the oldest class performed a dance.","furigana":"うんどうかい|ねんちょうぐみ|ゆうぎ|ひろう"},{"japanese":"これは単なる言葉の遊戯にすぎない。","romaji":"Kore wa tannaru kotoba no yuugi ni suginai.","german":"This is nothing more than a play on words.","furigana":"たん|ことば|ゆうぎ"}],"notes":"遊戯 is a formal word for \"play, playing\" (遊戯施設 \"play facilities\", 遊戯室 \"playroom\"). In everyday life it appears mainly as お遊戯: singing and movement games that kindergarten children perform to music. Figuratively, 〜の遊戯 means a mere \"game, trifling\". For ordinary playing, use 遊び/遊ぶ. Do not confuse it with 遊技 (gaming machines, pachinko).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ほとんどの学生は、父母の扶助を受けて生活している。","romaji":"Hotondo no gakusei wa, fubo no fujo o ukete seikatsu shite iru.","german":"Most students live on support from their parents.","furigana":"がくせい|ふぼ|ふじょ|う|せいかつ"},{"japanese":"生活が苦しい家庭には、国から生活扶助が支給される。","romaji":"Seikatsu ga kurushii katei ni wa, kuni kara seikatsu fujo ga shikyuu sareru.","german":"Families in financial difficulty receive living assistance from the state.","furigana":"せいかつ|くる|かてい|くに|せいかつふじょ|しきゅう"},{"japanese":"夫婦は互いに扶助する義務を負う。","romaji":"Fuufu wa tagai ni fujo suru gimu o ou.","german":"Spouses are obliged to support each other.","furigana":"ふうふ|たが|ふじょ|ぎむ|お"}],"notes":"扶助 is formal or legal \"support, assistance\", especially material help for people who cannot provide for themselves: 生活扶助 \"living-expense assistance\" under welfare, 相互扶助 \"mutual aid\", 扶助を受ける. 扶養, in contrast, means the duty to support dependents; in everyday speech, 援助, 支援 or simply 助け is used.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これは不朽の名作です。","romaji":"Kore wa fukyū no meisaku desu.","german":"This is an immortal masterpiece.","furigana":"ふきゅう|めいさく"},{"japanese":"彼は物理学に不朽の功績を残した。","romaji":"Kare wa butsurigaku ni fukyuu no kouseki o nokoshita.","german":"He left enduring achievements in physics.","furigana":"かれ|ぶつりがく|ふきゅう|こうせき|のこ"},{"japanese":"この発見によって、彼女の名は不朽のものとなった。","romaji":"Kono hakken ni yotte, kanojo no na wa fukyuu no mono to natta.","german":"Through this discovery, her name became immortal.","furigana":"はっけん|かのじょ|な|ふきゅう"}],"notes":"不朽 means \"imperishable, immortal\" (literally \"not rotting\") and is used practically only attributively with の: 不朽の名作, 不朽の名曲, 不朽の功績, 不朽の名声. It is elevated and praising. Similar is 不滅 (不滅の記録 \"eternal record\"); the opposite, decay through age, is expressed by 老朽 or 朽ちる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その建物はかなり老朽化しているので、立ち入らない方がいい。","romaji":"Sono tatemono wa kanari roukyuuka shite iru node, tachiiranai hou ga ii.","german":"That building is quite dilapidated, so you'd better not go in.","furigana":"たてもの|ろうきゅうか|た|い|ほう"},{"japanese":"老朽化した水道管の交換が急がれている。","romaji":"Roukyuuka shita suidoukan no koukan ga isogarete iru.","german":"Replacing the aging water pipes is urgent.","furigana":"ろうきゅうか|すいどうかん|こうかん|いそ"},{"japanese":"老朽校舎の建て替えが決まった。","romaji":"Roukyuu kousha no tatekae ga kimatta.","german":"It was decided to rebuild the run-down school building.","furigana":"ろうきゅうこうしゃ|た|か|き"}],"notes":"老朽 refers to decay through age and today is used almost only for buildings, facilities and infrastructure, mostly as 老朽化 (する): 老朽化した橋, 老朽化が進む. As a prefix it appears in 老朽施設 or 老朽校舎. For people it is outdated. 劣化, in contrast, means general deterioration of material or quality.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"交通事故で脊椎を損傷し、下半身不随になったのです。","romaji":"Koutsuu jiko de sekitsui o sonshou shi, kahanshin fuzui ni natta no desu.","german":"I injured my spine in a traffic accident and was left paralyzed from the waist down.","furigana":"こうつうじこ|せきつい|そんしょう|かはんしんふずい"},{"japanese":"人間は脊椎動物の一種だ。","romaji":"Ningen wa sekitsui doubutsu no isshu da.","german":"Humans are a kind of vertebrate.","furigana":"にんげん|せきついどうぶつ|いっしゅ"},{"japanese":"来月、脊椎の手術を受けることになった。","romaji":"Raigetsu, sekitsui no shujutsu o ukeru koto ni natta.","german":"Next month I'm going to have surgery on my spine.","furigana":"らいげつ|せきつい|しゅじゅつ|う"}],"notes":"脊椎 is the medical term for the \"spine, vertebral column\" or a single \"vertebra\"; in everyday speech 背骨 is used. Important are 脊椎動物 \"vertebrates\" and 無脊椎動物 \"invertebrates\". Do not confuse it with 脊髄 \"spinal cord\", which runs inside the spine (脊髄損傷 \"spinal cord injury\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"カナエはとってもお淑やかな性格をしている。","romaji":"Kanae wa tottemo oshitoyaka na seikaku o shite iru.","german":"Kanae has a very gentle, ladylike personality.","furigana":"しと|せいかく"},{"japanese":"彼女は着物姿でしとやかに歩いた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kimono sugata de shitoyaka ni aruita.","german":"She walked gracefully in her kimono.","furigana":"かのじょ|きものすがた|ある"},{"japanese":"子どもの頃は、もっとおしとやかにしなさいとよく言われた。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro wa, motto oshitoyaka ni shinasai to yoku iwareta.","german":"As a child I was often told to behave more demurely.","furigana":"こ|ころ|い"}],"notes":"しとやか (rarely 淑やか) describes \"gentle, ladylike, gracefully reserved\" behavior and is used almost only for women, usually with お: おしとやかな人, おしとやかにする. It sounds somewhat old-fashioned and carries traditional gender roles. 上品 \"refined\" is more general; 大人しい \"quiet, well-behaved\" emphasizes restraint without elegance.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"紳士淑女のみなさん、こんばんは。","romaji":"Shinshi shukujo no minasan, konbanwa.","german":"Good evening, ladies and gentlemen.","furigana":"しんししゅくじょ"},{"japanese":"彼女は誰もが認める立派な淑女に成長した。","romaji":"Kanojo wa dare mo ga mitomeru rippa na shukujo ni seichou shita.","german":"She has grown into a fine lady whom everyone admires.","furigana":"かのじょ|だれ|みと|りっぱ|しゅくじょ|せいちょう"},{"japanese":"この小説には、上流社会の淑女たちの暮らしが描かれている。","romaji":"Kono shousetsu ni wa, jouryuu shakai no shukujotachi no kurashi ga egakarete iru.","german":"This novel depicts the lives of ladies of high society.","furigana":"しょうせつ|じょうりゅうしゃかい|しゅくじょ|く|えが"}],"notes":"淑女 is a \"lady\" in the sense of an educated, refined woman and is the counterpart of 紳士 \"gentleman\". It is most often heard in the form of address 紳士淑女の皆さん \"ladies and gentlemen\". Otherwise it sounds elevated to old-fashioned; in everyday speech, 女性 or レディー is used. In internet slang, 淑女 is used ironically for \"perverted\" women.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"湖の水質汚濁を防止するために規制が設けられた。","romaji":"Mizuumi no suishitsu odaku o boushi suru tame ni kisei ga moukerareta.","german":"Regulations were introduced to prevent water pollution of the lake.","furigana":"みずうみ|すいしつおだく|ぼうし|きせい|もう"},{"japanese":"工場の排水で川が汚濁した。","romaji":"Koujou no haisui de kawa ga odaku shita.","german":"The river was polluted by the factory's wastewater.","furigana":"こうじょう|はいすい|かわ|おだく"},{"japanese":"新しい首相は政界の汚濁を一掃すると約束した。","romaji":"Atarashii shushou wa seikai no odaku o issou suru to yakusoku shita.","german":"The new prime minister promised to sweep away the corruption in politics.","furigana":"あたら|しゅしょう|せいかい|おだく|いっそう|やくそく"}],"notes":"汚濁 is \"pollution, turbidity\", above all of water; the fixed term is 水質汚濁 \"water pollution\" (law: 水質汚濁防止法). As an intransitive する-verb: 川が汚濁する. Figuratively it means moral \"depravity, corruption\" (政界の汚濁). 汚染 is the more general word, also for air, soil or radioactivity.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"核心をついた質問に、社長は言葉を濁した。","romaji":"Kakushin o tsuita shitsumon ni, shachō wa kotoba o nigoshita.","german":"Faced with a question that hit the core of the matter, the president was evasive.","furigana":"かくしん|しつもん|しゃちょう|ことば|にご"},{"japanese":"子どもが池の水をかき回して濁してしまった。","romaji":"Kodomo ga ike no mizu o kakimawashite nigoshite shimatta.","german":"A child stirred the pond and made the water cloudy.","furigana":"こ|いけ|みず|まわ|にご"},{"japanese":"準備不足だったので、適当な話でお茶を濁した。","romaji":"Junbi busoku datta node, tekitou na hanashi de ocha o nigoshita.","german":"Since I was poorly prepared, I got by with some vague talk.","furigana":"じゅんびぶそく|てきとう|はなし|ちゃ|にご"}],"notes":"濁す is the transitive verb of 濁る \"to become cloudy\": literally \"to make cloudy, to muddy\" (水を濁す). Much more common is the figurative use: 言葉を濁す \"to answer evasively, not to commit oneself\" and お茶を濁す \"to muddle through with a makeshift solution, to talk around something\". The proverb 立つ鳥跡を濁さず urges one to leave a place clean.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"濁流に飲み込まれそうになったのを、彼が救ってくれたのです。","romaji":"Dakuryuu ni nomikomaresou ni natta no o, kare ga sukutte kureta no desu.","german":"I was nearly swallowed by the muddy torrent, but he saved me.","furigana":"だくりゅう|の|こ|かれ|すく"},{"japanese":"大雨で川が濁流となって流れている。","romaji":"Ooame de kawa ga dakuryuu to natte nagarete iru.","german":"Because of the heavy rain, the river is flowing as a muddy torrent.","furigana":"おおあめ|かわ|だくりゅう|なが"},{"japanese":"濁流が橋を押し流した。","romaji":"Dakuryuu ga hashi o oshinagashita.","german":"The muddy torrent swept the bridge away.","furigana":"だくりゅう|はし|お|なが"}],"notes":"濁流 is a \"muddy, raging flood\", as rivers carry after heavy rain or typhoons: 濁流に飲み込まれる \"to be swept away by the flood\", 川が濁流となる. The opposite is 清流 \"clear stream\". The word is used mainly in news and narratives; in everyday speech, 泥水 or 増水した川 is used.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"起訴猶予処分がくだされました。","romaji":"Kiso yūyo shobun ga kudasaremashita.","german":"A decision was made not to prosecute.","furigana":"きそゆうよしょぶん"},{"japanese":"初犯で深く反省していることから、起訴猶予となった。","romaji":"Shohan de fukaku hansei shite iru koto kara, kiso yuuyo to natta.","german":"Because it was his first offense and he was deeply remorseful, prosecution was suspended.","furigana":"しょはん|ふか|はんせい|きそゆうよ"},{"japanese":"起訴猶予は無罪判決とは違い、嫌疑があることを前提とした処分だ。","romaji":"Kiso yuuyo wa muzai hanketsu to wa chigai, kengi ga aru koto o zentei to shita shobun da.","german":"Unlike an acquittal, suspension of prosecution presupposes that there is suspicion of a crime.","furigana":"きそゆうよ|むざいはんけつ|ちが|けんぎ|ぜんてい|しょぶん"}],"notes":"起訴猶予 means that the public prosecutor \"refrains from indictment\" despite sufficient suspicion, for example because of triviality, a first offense or remorse. It is a form of 不起訴処分 and is expressed with 起訴猶予処分になる/とする. Do not confuse it with 執行猶予 \"suspended sentence on probation\" after a conviction or with 嫌疑不十分 \"for lack of evidence\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どこで硫酸を手に入れたんだ。","romaji":"Doko de ryūsan o te ni ireta n da.","german":"Where did you get sulfuric acid?","furigana":"りゅうさん|て|い"},{"japanese":"実験で硫酸を扱うときは、必ず保護メガネをかけること。","romaji":"Jikken de ryuusan o atsukau toki wa, kanarazu hogo megane o kakeru koto.","german":"When handling sulfuric acid in experiments, always wear safety goggles.","furigana":"じっけん|りゅうさん|あつか|かなら|ほご"},{"japanese":"濃硫酸を薄めるときは、水に少しずつ加える。","romaji":"Nouryuusan o usumeru toki wa, mizu ni sukoshi zutsu kuwaeru.","german":"When diluting concentrated sulfuric acid, add it to water little by little.","furigana":"のうりゅうさん|うす|みず|すこ|くわ"}],"notes":"硫酸 is \"sulfuric acid\"; 硫 stands for sulfur (硫黄), 酸 for acid. One distinguishes 濃硫酸 \"concentrated\" and 希硫酸 \"dilute sulfuric acid\"; salts are called 硫酸塩 (e.g. 硫酸銅). Other important acids are 塩酸 \"hydrochloric acid\" and 硝酸 \"nitric acid\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ストレス解消に窃盗を繰り返していたそうだ。","romaji":"Sutoresu kaishō ni settō o kurikaeshite ita sō da.","german":"Apparently he repeatedly stole to relieve stress.","furigana":"かいしょう|せっとう|く|かえ"},{"japanese":"男は窃盗の容疑で逮捕された。","romaji":"Otoko wa settou no yougi de taiho sareta.","german":"The man was arrested on suspicion of theft.","furigana":"おとこ|せっとう|ようぎ|たいほ"},{"japanese":"駅前で自転車窃盗の被害が増えている。","romaji":"Ekimae de jitensha settou no higai ga fuete iru.","german":"Bicycle thefts are increasing in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|じてんしゃせっとう|ひがい|ふ"}],"notes":"窃盗 is the legal and journalistic term for \"theft\" without violence (窃盗罪, 窃盗犯, 窃盗の容疑). With violence or threats it is 強盗 \"robbery\"; subtypes are 万引き \"shoplifting\", 空き巣 \"burglary of an unoccupied home\" or スリ \"pickpocketing\". In everyday speech, 盗む or 泥棒 is used; 窃盗する as a verb is rare.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あいつらとは紳士協定を結んでいるはずだ。","romaji":"Aitsura to wa shinshi kyōtei o musunde iru hazu da.","german":"We're supposed to have a gentlemen's agreement with those guys.","furigana":"しんしきょうてい|むす"},{"japanese":"両社の間には、互いの社員を引き抜かないという紳士協定がある。","romaji":"Ryousha no aida ni wa, tagai no shain o hikinukanai to iu shinshi kyoutei ga aru.","german":"The two companies have a gentlemen's agreement not to poach each other's employees.","furigana":"りょうしゃ|あいだ|たが|しゃいん|ひ|ぬ|しんしきょうてい"},{"japanese":"紳士協定には法的な拘束力がない。","romaji":"Shinshi kyoutei ni wa houteki na kousokuryoku ga nai.","german":"A gentlemen's agreement has no legal binding force.","furigana":"しんしきょうてい|ほうてき|こうそくりょく"}],"notes":"紳士協定 is a \"gentlemen's agreement\": an unwritten arrangement based on mutual trust that is not legally binding. Typical verbs: 紳士協定を結ぶ, 守る, 破る. It is found in business (no poaching, no price wars), sports and diplomacy. A formal contract is 契約 or 協定.","levelNote":"Compound: 紳士 (N1) + 協定 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"西陣の糸屋で絹糸を買ってきてちょうだい。","romaji":"Nishijin no itoya de kenshi o katte kite choudai.","german":"Please buy some silk thread at the thread shop in Nishijin.","furigana":"にしじん|いとや|けんし|か"},{"japanese":"絹糸は光沢があり、着物の刺繍によく使われる。","romaji":"Kenshi wa koutaku ga ari, kimono no shishuu ni yoku tsukawareru.","german":"Silk thread has a gloss and is often used for kimono embroidery.","furigana":"けんし|こうたく|きもの|ししゅう|つか"},{"japanese":"この地域はかつて絹糸の輸出で栄えた。","romaji":"Kono chiiki wa katsute kenshi no yushutsu de sakaeta.","german":"This region once prospered through the export of silk thread.","furigana":"ちいき|けんし|ゆしゅつ|さか"}],"notes":"絹糸 \"silk thread, silk yarn\" has two readings: in everyday speech usually きぬいと, in technical language and industry (textiles, trade) rather けんし. It is used for sewing, embroidering and weaving fine fabrics such as kimonos. Not the same is 生糸 \"raw silk\", the yarn reeled directly from the cocoon; cotton thread is 綿糸.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"金縛りにあったことはありますか。","romaji":"Kanashibari ni atta koto wa arimasu ka.","german":"Have you ever experienced sleep paralysis?","furigana":"かなしば"},{"japanese":"金縛りは疲れているときに起こりやすいと言われる。","romaji":"Kanashibari wa tsukarete iru toki ni okoriyasui to iwareru.","german":"Sleep paralysis is said to occur easily when you are tired.","furigana":"かなしば|つか|お|い"},{"japanese":"あまりの恐怖に、金縛りにあったように動けなくなった。","romaji":"Amari no kyoufu ni, kanashibari ni atta you ni ugokenaku natta.","german":"I was so frightened that I couldn't move, as if paralyzed.","furigana":"きょうふ|かなしば|うご"}],"notes":"金縛り is above all \"sleep paralysis\": you wake up but cannot move. One says 金縛りにあう (遭う); in folk belief it is often attributed to ghosts, medically it is called 睡眠麻痺. Figuratively, 金縛りにあったように describes freezing from fear or shock. Literally it means \"bound with metal fetters\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"西部劇では、悪党が縛り首にされる場面がよく出てくる。","romaji":"Seibugeki de wa, akutou ga shibarikubi ni sareru bamen ga yoku dete kuru.","german":"In Westerns, there are often scenes where villains are hanged.","furigana":"せいぶげき|あくとう|しば|くび|ばめん|で"},{"japanese":"中世のヨーロッパでは、泥棒が縛り首にされることもあった。","romaji":"Chuusei no Yooroppa de wa, dorobou ga shibarikubi ni sareru koto mo atta.","german":"In medieval Europe, thieves were sometimes hanged.","furigana":"ちゅうせい|どろぼう|しば|くび"},{"japanese":"そんなことがばれたら、縛り首ものだぞ。","romaji":"Sonna koto ga baretara, shibarikubi mono da zo.","german":"If that gets out, you'll hang for it!","furigana":"しば|くび"}],"notes":"縛り首 is \"death by the rope, hanging\" as a punishment; literally \"neck-binding\". Typical: 縛り首にする/される. The word sounds archaic and appears in Western films, historical novels or translations. The legal technical term, also for the present-day death penalty in Japan, is 絞首刑.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自分で縫ったので縫い目がガタガタなんです。","romaji":"Jibun de nutta node nuime ga gatagata na n desu.","german":"I sewed it myself, so the seams are crooked.","furigana":"じぶん|ぬ|ぬ|め"},{"japanese":"ズボンの縫い目がほつれてしまった。","romaji":"Zubon no nuime ga hotsurete shimatta.","german":"The seam of my trousers has come apart.","furigana":"ぬ|め"},{"japanese":"手術の縫い目がまだ少し痛む。","romaji":"Shujutsu no nuime ga mada sukoshi itamu.","german":"The surgical stitches still hurt a little.","furigana":"しゅじゅつ|ぬ|め|すこ|いた"}],"notes":"ぬいめ \"seam, stitch\" is normally written 縫い目; the spelling 縫目 without い is rare. It refers to the seam on clothing (縫い目がほつれる \"the seam comes undone\"), individual stitches (縫い目が粗い/細かい) and also the surgical suture after an operation. Related are 縫う \"to sew\" and 継ぎ目 \"joint, seam\" in components.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの画家に私の肖像を描かせてみよう。","romaji":"Ano gaka ni watashi no shouzou o egakasete miyou.","german":"Let me have that painter paint my portrait.","furigana":"がか|わたし|しょうぞう|えが"},{"japanese":"お札には歴史上の人物の肖像が印刷されている。","romaji":"Osatsu ni wa rekishijou no jinbutsu no shouzou ga insatsu sarete iru.","german":"Portraits of historical figures are printed on banknotes.","furigana":"さつ|れきしじょう|じんぶつ|しょうぞう|いんさつ"},{"japanese":"他人の写真を勝手にネットに載せると、肖像権の侵害になることがある。","romaji":"Tanin no shashin o katte ni netto ni noseru to, shouzouken no shingai ni naru koto ga aru.","german":"Posting other people's photos online without permission can violate their portrait rights.","furigana":"たにん|しゃしん|かって|の|しょうぞうけん|しんがい"}],"notes":"肖像 refers to the likeness of a specific person, usually as a painting, photo or sculpture. On its own it belongs more to elevated language (肖像を描く); in everyday life one almost always hears compounds such as 肖像画 \"portrait painting\" or 肖像写真 \"portrait photo\". An important legal term is 肖像権 \"right to one's own image\". For a quick photo of a face, 顔写真 is used rather than 肖像.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"藩主の墓に参ってきた。","romaji":"Hanshu no haka ni maitte kita.","german":"I went to visit the grave of the domain lord.","furigana":"はんしゅ|はか|まい"},{"japanese":"このお城には、代々の藩主が暮らしていました。","romaji":"Kono oshiro ni wa, daidai no hanshu ga kurashite imashita.","german":"Generations of domain lords lived in this castle.","furigana":"しろ|だいだい|はんしゅ|く"},{"japanese":"初代藩主は、川の治水工事に力を入れたと言われている。","romaji":"Shodai hanshu wa, kawa no chisui kouji ni chikara o ireta to iwarete iru.","german":"The first domain lord is said to have devoted himself to flood-control works on the river.","furigana":"しょだいはんしゅ|かわ|ちすいこうじ|ちから|い|い"}],"notes":"藩主 is the lord of a 藩, that is, a feudal domain of the Edo period; essentially a Daimyō in his role as ruler of a territory. The word is a historical technical term and appears in history books, museums and castle tours: 〇〇藩の藩主, 初代藩主, 藩主の居城. 大名 emphasizes the rank as a feudal lord relative to the Shōgun, while 藩主 emphasizes rule over a specific territory.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"棋譜の付け方を教えてください。","romaji":"Kifu no tsukekata o oshiete kudasai.","german":"Please teach me how to record a game.","furigana":"きふ|つ|かた|おし"},{"japanese":"毎晩、プロの棋譜を並べて勉強しています。","romaji":"Maiban, puro no kifu o narabete benkyou shite imasu.","german":"Every evening I study by replaying professional game records on the board.","furigana":"まいばん|きふ|なら|べんきょう"},{"japanese":"名人戦の棋譜がネットで公開された。","romaji":"Meijinsen no kifu ga netto de koukai sareta.","german":"The game record of the Meijin title match was published online.","furigana":"めいじんせん|きふ|こうかい"}],"notes":"棋譜 is the written record of a game of shōgi, go or chess, move by move. One \"keeps\" it with 棋譜を付ける or 棋譜を取る; replaying a game on the board is called 棋譜を並べる or 棋譜並べ and is a classic training method. The word belongs to the technical language of board games; for musical notation, 楽譜 is used instead.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この巻物には、一族の系譜が記されています。","romaji":"Kono makimono ni wa, ichizoku no keifu ga shirusarete imasu.","german":"This scroll records the family's genealogy.","furigana":"まきもの|いちぞく|けいふ|しる"},{"japanese":"彼の作品は、日本の自然主義文学の系譜を引いている。","romaji":"Kare no sakuhin wa, Nihon no shizenshugi bungaku no keifu o hiite iru.","german":"His works follow the lineage of Japanese naturalist literature.","furigana":"かれ|さくひん|にほん|しぜんしゅぎぶんがく|けいふ|ひ"},{"japanese":"この車は、名門スポーツカーの系譜に連なる最新モデルだ。","romaji":"Kono kuruma wa, meimon supootsukaa no keifu ni tsuranaru saishin moderu da.","german":"This car is the latest model in a lineage of prestigious sports cars.","furigana":"くるま|めいもん|けいふ|つら|さいしん"}],"notes":"系譜 is first of all the family tree or genealogy of a family (一族の系譜, 家の系譜). Very often it is used figuratively for a line of tradition or development: 〜の系譜を引く \"to stand in the tradition of ...\", 〜の系譜に連なる. It sounds literary; the everyday word for a drawn family tree is 家系図.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この作家の年譜を作ってくれないか。","romaji":"Kono sakka no nenpu o tsukutte kurenai ka.","german":"Could you make a chronology of this author's life?","furigana":"さっか|ねんぷ|つく"},{"japanese":"全集の最後の巻に、詳しい年譜が付いている。","romaji":"Zenshuu no saigo no kan ni, kuwashii nenpu ga tsuite iru.","german":"The last volume of the complete works includes a detailed chronology.","furigana":"ぜんしゅう|さいご|かん|くわ|ねんぷ|つ"},{"japanese":"展示室の壁には、画家の年譜が写真と一緒に並べられていた。","romaji":"Tenjishitsu no kabe ni wa, gaka no nenpu ga shashin to issho ni naraberarete ita.","german":"On the wall of the exhibition room, the painter's chronology was displayed along with photographs.","furigana":"てんじしつ|かべ|がか|ねんぷ|しゃしん|いっしょ|なら"}],"notes":"年譜 is a chronological overview of a person's life and work, typically as a table at the end of a biography, a volume of collected works or in an exhibition: 作家の年譜, 年譜を作る, 年譜をたどる. For general timelines of historical events, 年表 is more usual; 年譜 is tied to a person (or institution).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"譜面を無料でダウンロードできるサイトを探しています。","romaji":"Fumen o muryou de daunroodo dekiru saito o sagashite imasu.","german":"I'm looking for a site where I can download sheet music for free.","furigana":"ふめん|むりょう|さが"},{"japanese":"彼女は譜面を見ずに、その曲を最後まで弾いた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa fumen o mizu ni, sono kyoku o saigo made hiita.","german":"She played the piece to the end without looking at the score.","furigana":"かのじょ|ふめん|み|きょく|さいご|ひ"},{"japanese":"本番の前に、譜面台の高さを調節しておいてください。","romaji":"Honban no mae ni, fumendai no takasa o chousetsu shite oite kudasai.","german":"Please adjust the height of the music stand before the performance.","furigana":"ほんばん|まえ|ふめんだい|たか|ちょうせつ"}],"notes":"譜面 is the music as it lies in front of you in concrete form: the sheet of music on the stand (譜面台). 楽譜 is the more general, somewhat more formal word for sheet music or scores. Typical are 譜面を読む, 譜面を見ながら弾く and 譜面なしで弾く \"to play from memory\". In rhythm games, the sequence of notes of a song is also called 譜面.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は特別に罪の赦免が認められた。","romaji":"Kare wa tokubetsu ni tsumi no shamen ga mitomerareta.","german":"He was exceptionally granted a pardon for his crime.","furigana":"かれ|とくべつ|つみ|しゃめん|みと"},{"japanese":"島に流された僧は、十年後にようやく赦免された。","romaji":"Shima ni nagasareta sou wa, juunengo ni youyaku shamen sareta.","german":"The monk exiled to an island was finally pardoned after ten years.","furigana":"しま|なが|そう|じゅうねんご|しゃめん"},{"japanese":"王の結婚を祝って、多くの囚人が赦免された。","romaji":"Ou no kekkon o iwatte, ooku no shuujin ga shamen sareta.","german":"In celebration of the king's wedding, many prisoners were pardoned.","furigana":"おう|けっこん|いわ|おお|しゅうじん|しゃめん"}],"notes":"赦免 is the remission of a punishment or guilt by a higher authority, that is, a pardon. As a transitive する-verb: 罪人を赦免する, passive 赦免される; also 赦免状 \"letter of pardon\". The word sounds historical or legal-solemn; for present-day state amnesty, 恩赦 is mostly used, and for everyday forgiving, 許す.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"罪人は容赦なく罰せられなくてはならない。","romaji":"Zainin wa yousha naku basserarenakute wa naranai.","german":"Criminals must be punished without mercy.","furigana":"ざいにん|ようしゃ|ばっ"},{"japanese":"工事中はご迷惑をおかけしますが、何卒ご容赦ください。","romaji":"Koujichuu wa gomeiwaku o okake shimasu ga, nanitozo goyousha kudasai.","german":"We apologize for the inconvenience during construction and ask for your kind understanding.","furigana":"こうじちゅう|めいわく|なにとぞ|ようしゃ"},{"japanese":"真夏の太陽が容赦なく照りつけていた。","romaji":"Manatsu no taiyou ga yousha naku teritsukete ita.","german":"The midsummer sun beat down mercilessly.","furigana":"まなつ|たいよう|ようしゃ|て"}],"notes":"容赦 means leniency or sparing. Most often it appears negated: 容赦なく \"mercilessly, ruthlessly\", 容赦しない \"to show no mercy\", 情け容赦もない. In polite notices, one asks for understanding or pardon with ご容赦ください, for example for disruptions or mistakes. Positively and without negation it is rarely used outside this formula.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"久しぶりに会った友人と、夜遅くまで酒を酌み交わした。","romaji":"Hisashiburi ni atta yuujin to, yoru osoku made sake o kumikawashita.","german":"I drank sake late into the night with a friend I hadn't seen in a long time.","furigana":"ひさ|あ|ゆうじん|よるおそ|さけ|く|か"},{"japanese":"彼女の気持ちを酌んで、それ以上は何も聞かなかった。","romaji":"Kanojo no kimochi o kunde, sore ijou wa nani mo kikanakatta.","german":"Taking her feelings into account, I did not ask anything further.","furigana":"かのじょ|きも|く|いじょう|なに|き"},{"japanese":"部長はこちらの事情を酌んで、締め切りを延ばしてくれた。","romaji":"Buchou wa kochira no jijou o kunde, shimekiri o nobashite kureta.","german":"The department head took our circumstances into account and extended the deadline.","furigana":"ぶちょう|じじょう|く|し|き|の"}],"notes":"酌む originally means \"to pour sake\" or \"to drink sake together\", today mainly in 酒を酌み交わす \"to drink together\". The second, very common meaning is \"to take someone's feelings or situation into account\": 気持ちを酌む, 事情を酌む, 意を酌む. Not to be confused with the identically read 汲む \"to draw water\" - in the figurative meaning both spellings are used, but drawing water from a well is written only with 汲む. Pouring itself is usually called お酌をする or 注ぐ in everyday speech.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"犯人と間違われて手錠を掛けられたんだ。","romaji":"Hannin to machigawarete tejou o kakerareta n da.","german":"I was mistaken for the culprit and handcuffed.","furigana":"はんにん|まちが|てじょう|か"},{"japanese":"警察官は容疑者の両手に手錠をかけた。","romaji":"Keisatsukan wa yougisha no ryoute ni tejou o kaketa.","german":"The police officer put handcuffs on the suspect's wrists.","furigana":"けいさつかん|ようぎしゃ|りょうて|てじょう"},{"japanese":"取調室に入ると、ようやく手錠が外された。","romaji":"Torishirabeshitsu ni hairu to, youyaku tejou ga hazusareta.","german":"The handcuffs were finally removed once I entered the interrogation room.","furigana":"|はい|てじょう|はず"}],"notes":"手錠 means handcuffs; the standard verb is 手錠をかける \"to put handcuffs on\", passive 手錠をかけられる, and to remove them is 手錠を外す. Less often one hears 手錠をはめる. The word appears mainly in news, crime stories and police contexts. 錠 means \"lock\", hence also 南京錠 \"padlock\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"錠剤を飲み込むことができないので、砕いて飲んでいます。","romaji":"Jōzai o nomikomu koto ga dekinai node, kudaite nonde imasu.","german":"I can't swallow tablets, so I crush them before taking them.","furigana":"じょうざい|の|こ|くだ|の"},{"japanese":"この錠剤は一日三回、食後に一錠ずつ飲んでください。","romaji":"Kono jouzai wa ichinichi sankai, shokugo ni ichijou zutsu nonde kudasai.","german":"Take one of these tablets three times a day after meals.","furigana":"じょうざい|いちにちさんかい|しょくご|いちじょう|の"},{"japanese":"粉薬が苦手なら、錠剤に変えてもらえますよ。","romaji":"Konagusuri ga nigate nara, jouzai ni kaete moraemasu yo.","german":"If you're not good with powdered medicine, they can switch you to tablets.","furigana":"こなぐすり|にがて|じょうざい|か"}],"notes":"錠剤 is a tablet as a dosage form, in contrast to 粉薬 \"powder\", カプセル \"capsule\" or 水薬 \"liquid medicine\". One takes it with 飲む (錠剤を水で飲む); tablets are counted with 錠: 一回二錠. In everyday speech one often says simply 薬; 錠剤 emphasizes the form and often appears on package inserts and in the pharmacy.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私って、飽きっぽいのよね。","romaji":"Watashi tte, akippoi no yo ne.","german":"I just get bored of things easily.","furigana":"わたし|あ"},{"japanese":"このデザインはシンプルで、長く使っても飽きが来ない。","romaji":"Kono dezain wa shinpuru de, nagaku tsukatte mo aki ga konai.","german":"This design is simple, so you don't tire of it even after using it for a long time.","furigana":"なが|つか|あ|こ"},{"japanese":"毎日同じ弁当だと、さすがに飽きが来るね。","romaji":"Mainichi onaji bentou da to, sasuga ni aki ga kuru ne.","german":"Having the same bento every day, you do get sick of it eventually.","furigana":"まいにちおな|べんとう|あ|く"}],"notes":"飽き is the noun of 飽きる \"to get tired of something\" and refers to the weariness or boredom that sets in after a while. Typical are 飽きが来る \"to get sick of it\" and the praise 飽きが来ない \"you never tire of it\" (for design, taste, music). From the same root comes 飽きっぽい \"quickly bored, lacking perseverance\". For general boredom without prior excess, 退屈 is more usual.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"飽くまでも、これは個人的な見解です。","romaji":"Akumademo, kore wa kojinteki na kenkai desu.","german":"To be clear, this is only my personal opinion.","furigana":"あ|こじんてき|けんかい"},{"japanese":"彼は最後まであくまでも計画に反対した。","romaji":"Kare wa saigo made akumademo keikaku ni hantai shita.","german":"He persistently opposed the plan to the very end.","furigana":"かれ|さいご|けいかく|はんたい"},{"japanese":"この数字はあくまでも目安なので、多少変わることがあります。","romaji":"Kono suuji wa akumademo meyasu na node, tashou kawaru koto ga arimasu.","german":"These figures are only a rough guide, so they may change somewhat.","furigana":"すうじ|めやす|たしょうか"}],"notes":"飽くまでも is almost always written in kana (あくまでも, shorter あくまで). It has two meanings: \"persistently, to the bitter end\" (あくまでも反対する, あくまで戦う) and, very common in everyday speech, in a limiting sense \"only, merely, after all\", to qualify a statement: あくまでも個人的な意見ですが…, あくまで参考です. The translation \"quite\" fits neither meaning.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"できるだけ鶏肉のささみを食べるようにしています。","romaji":"Dekiru dake toriniku no sasami o taberu yō ni shite imasu.","german":"I try to eat chicken tenderloin as much as possible.","furigana":"とりにく|た"},{"japanese":"買い物のメモに「とり肉三百グラム」と書きました。","romaji":"Kaimono no memo ni \"toriniku sanbyaku guramu\" to kakimashita.","german":"I wrote \"300 grams of chicken\" on my shopping list.","furigana":"か|もの|にくさんびゃく|か"}],"notes":"鶏肉 is read とりにく here and refers to chicken meat. とり肉 is a mixed spelling with hiragana for 鶏, which one may encounter in a simplified text, for example. 鶏肉のささみ are the tenderloins of the chicken breast. 食べるようにしています describes a habit kept up deliberately; できるだけ means \"as much as possible\" here, not a fixed amount of meat. The also attested reading けいにく is not the reading practiced on this card.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5 (source: N5; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"鶏卵の価格が、この一年で大きく上がった。","romaji":"Keiran no kakaku ga, kono ichinen de ookiku agatta.","german":"The price of chicken eggs has risen sharply over the past year.","furigana":"けいらん|かかく|いちねん|おお|あ"},{"japanese":"この商品には原材料の一部に鶏卵が含まれています。","romaji":"Kono shouhin ni wa genzairyou no ichibu ni keiran ga fukumarete imasu.","german":"This product contains chicken egg as part of its ingredients.","furigana":"しょうひん|げんざいりょう|いちぶ|けいらん|ふく"},{"japanese":"息子は鶏卵アレルギーなので、外食のときは注意しています。","romaji":"Musuko wa keiran arerugii na node, gaishoku no toki wa chuui shite imasu.","german":"My son has an egg allergy, so we are careful when eating out.","furigana":"むすこ|けいらん|がいしょく|ちゅうい"}],"notes":"鶏卵 is the formal word for \"hen's egg\" and appears in statistics, economic news, food labeling and allergy information (鶏卵アレルギー). In everyday speech one says simply 卵 or, for cooked eggs and on menus, often 玉子. Someone who says 鶏卵 in a supermarket sounds like a technical article.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"養鶏経営の難しさを痛感しました。","romaji":"Yōkei keiei no muzukashisa o tsūkan shimashita.","german":"I keenly felt how difficult it is to run a poultry farm.","furigana":"ようけいけいえい|むずか|つうかん"},{"japanese":"鳥インフルエンザが出て、近くの養鶏場では大量の鶏が処分された。","romaji":"Tori infuruenza ga dete, chikaku no youkeijou de wa tairyou no niwatori ga shobun sareta.","german":"After an outbreak of bird flu, a large number of chickens were culled at a nearby poultry farm.","furigana":"とり|で|ちか|ようけいじょう|たいりょう|にわとり|しょぶん"},{"japanese":"祖父は田舎で小さな養鶏業を営んでいた。","romaji":"Sofu wa inaka de chiisana youkeigyou o itonande ita.","german":"My grandfather ran a small poultry farm in the countryside.","furigana":"そふ|いなか|ちい|ようけいぎょう|いとな"}],"notes":"養鶏 is the keeping of chickens or poultry as an industry. It appears almost always in compounds: 養鶏場 \"chicken farm\", 養鶏業 \"poultry industry\", 養鶏農家 \"poultry farmer\". The pattern 養〜 \"to raise\" is also known from 養豚 \"pig farming\" and 養殖 \"aquaculture\". For keeping a few chickens in the yard, 鶏を飼う is more usual.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"父は、様々な経験の後に諦観に至った。","romaji":"Chichi wa, samazama na keiken no nochi ni teikan ni itatta.","german":"After many experiences, my father arrived at a serene acceptance.","furigana":"ちち|さまざま|けいけん|のち|ていかん|いた"},{"japanese":"その老僧の言葉には、深い諦観がにじんでいた。","romaji":"Sono rousou no kotoba ni wa, fukai teikan ga nijinde ita.","german":"A deep, resigned serenity showed through the old monk's words.","furigana":"ろうそう|ことば|ふか|ていかん"},{"japanese":"彼女は病気を諦観をもって受け入れ、残りの日々を穏やかに過ごした。","romaji":"Kanojo wa byouki o teikan o motte ukeire, nokori no hibi o odayaka ni sugoshita.","german":"She accepted her illness with equanimity and spent her remaining days in peace.","furigana":"かのじょ|びょうき|ていかん|う|い|のこ|ひび|おだ|す"}],"notes":"諦観 is a literary, Buddhist-tinged word with two meanings: \"clear insight into the nature of things\" and, more common today, the calm, composed acceptance of fate. Typical are 諦観に至る, 諦観の境地 and 諦観をもって受け入れる. Unlike the everyday 諦め \"giving up\", 諦観 carries a sense of calm and inner maturity, not defeat.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"莉子は、瑠璃色のワンピースを着て諒一郎との待ち合わせ場所に現れた。","romaji":"Riko wa, ruriiro no wanpiisu o kite Ryouichirou to no machiawase basho ni arawareta.","german":"Riko appeared at the meeting place with Ryōichirō in a lapis-blue dress.","furigana":"りこ|るりいろ|き|りょういちろう|ま|あ|ばしょ|あらわ"},{"japanese":"展望台からは、瑠璃色に輝く海が見渡せた。","romaji":"Tenboudai kara wa, ruriiro ni kagayaku umi ga miwataseta.","german":"From the observation deck, we could see the sea shimmering deep blue.","furigana":"てんぼうだい|るりいろ|かがや|うみ|みわた"},{"japanese":"この瑠璃色のグラスは、職人が一つずつ手で作ったものです。","romaji":"Kono ruriiro no gurasu wa, shokunin ga hitotsu zutsu te de tsukutta mono desu.","german":"These deep-blue glasses were each made by hand by a craftsman.","furigana":"るりいろ|しょくにん|ひと|て|つく"}],"notes":"瑠璃色 is a deep, luminous blue with a slight violet tint, named after the semi-precious stone 瑠璃 \"lapis lazuli\". The word is poetic and is used for the sea, sky, glass, fabrics and bird plumage: 瑠璃色の海, 瑠璃色に輝く. In ordinary conversation one says simply 青 or 紺; 瑠璃色 sounds elevated and vivid.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"菅原っていう苗字、なんかかっこよくね？","romaji":"Sugahara tte iu myouji, nanka kakkoyokune?","german":"Sugahara is a cool surname, don't you think?","furigana":"すがはら|みょうじ"},{"japanese":"菅原さんは来月から大阪支社に異動になります。","romaji":"Sugahara-san wa raigetsu kara Oosaka shisha ni idou ni narimasu.","german":"Mr. Sugahara will be transferred to the Osaka branch from next month.","furigana":"すがはら|らいげつ|おおさかししゃ|いどう"},{"japanese":"「菅原」は「すがはら」と「すがわら」のどちらで読むんですか。","romaji":"\"Sugahara\" wa \"sugahara\" to \"sugawara\" no dochira de yomu n desu ka.","german":"Is \"菅原\" read Sugahara or Sugawara?","furigana":"すがはら|よ"}],"notes":"菅原 is a Japanese family name, not general vocabulary. The usual reading is すがわら (known from the scholar 菅原道真); the reading すがはら occurs as a rarer variant. With names, you can never be sure of the reading from the kanji, so one politely asks: お名前は何とお読みすればいいですか. The character 菅 means \"sedge, reed grass\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本の裁判には「情状酌量」とよばれるものがあります。","romaji":"Nihon no saiban ni wa \"joujou shakuryou\" to yobareru mono ga arimasu.","german":"In Japanese trials, there is something called \"extenuating circumstances\".","furigana":"にほん|さいばん|じょうじょうしゃくりょう"},{"japanese":"裁判所は被告の生い立ちを酌量して、刑を軽くした。","romaji":"Saibansho wa hikoku no oitachi o shakuryou shite, kei o karuku shita.","german":"The court took the defendant's upbringing into consideration and reduced the sentence.","furigana":"さいばんしょ|ひこく||た|しゃくりょう|けい|かる"},{"japanese":"計画的な犯行であり、酌量の余地はないと検察は主張した。","romaji":"Keikakuteki na hankou de ari, shakuryou no yochi wa nai to kensatsu wa shuchou shita.","german":"The prosecution argued that it was a premeditated crime and there was no room for leniency.","furigana":"けいかくてき|はんこう|しゃくりょう|よち|けんさつ|しゅちょう"}],"notes":"酌量 means to take circumstances into account in a mitigating way when judging something. It appears almost always in the legal expression 情状酌量 \"consideration of extenuating circumstances\" and in 酌量の余地がある／ない \"there is (no) room for leniency\". As a transitive する-verb: 事情を酌量する. The kanji 酌 is the same as in 気持ちを酌む \"to take feelings into account\"; outside law and news the word sounds very formal.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今年は祖父の三回忌です。","romaji":"Kotoshi wa sofu no sankaiki desu.","german":"This year is my grandfather's second death anniversary.","furigana":"ことし|そふ|さんかいき"},{"japanese":"母の七回忌の法要に、親戚が久しぶりに集まった。","romaji":"Haha no nanakaiki no houyou ni, shinseki ga hisashiburi ni atsumatta.","german":"Relatives gathered for the first time in a long while for the memorial service marking six years since my mother's death.","furigana":"はは|ななかいき|ほうよう|しんせき|ひさ|あつ"},{"japanese":"三十三回忌を最後に、法要を締めくくる家が多い。","romaji":"Sanjuusankaiki o saigo ni, houyou o shimekukuru ie ga ooi.","german":"Many families conclude the memorial services with the 33rd anniversary.","furigana":"さんじゅうさんかいき|さいご|ほうよう|し|いえ|おお"}],"notes":"回忌 is a counter for the Buddhist memorial anniversaries of a deceased person and follows numbers: 三回忌, 七回忌, 十三回忌. Note the counting: the day of death itself counts as the first, so 三回忌 is the second anniversary and 七回忌 the sixth. Only the first anniversary has its own name, 一周忌. On these dates one holds a 法要 or 法事.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"昔、この村ではその山に入ることが禁忌とされていた。","romaji":"Mukashi, kono mura de wa sono yama ni hairu koto ga kinki to sarete ita.","german":"In the past, entering that mountain was considered taboo in this village.","furigana":"むかし|むら|やま|はい|きんき"},{"japanese":"この薬は妊娠中の方には禁忌です。","romaji":"Kono kusuri wa ninshinchuu no kata ni wa kinki desu.","german":"This medicine is contraindicated for pregnant women.","furigana":"くすり|にんしんちゅう|かた|きんき"},{"japanese":"その作家は、社会の禁忌にあえて踏み込む作品で知られている。","romaji":"Sono sakka wa, shakai no kinki ni aete fumikomu sakuhin de shirarete iru.","german":"That author is known for works that deliberately venture into society's taboos.","furigana":"さっか|しゃかい|きんき|ふ|こ|さくひん|し"}],"notes":"禁忌 is a taboo, that is, something forbidden for religious, social or moral reasons: 禁忌を犯す \"to break a taboo\", 禁忌とされる. In medicine it means a contraindication, for example on package inserts (禁忌, 併用禁忌 \"must not be combined\"). In everyday speech タブー is more usual; 禁忌 sounds written and technical.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"七月には、妻の父の一周忌の法事があります。","romaji":"Shichigatsu ni wa, tsuma no chichi no isshūki no hōji ga arimasu.","german":"In July there is a memorial service for the first anniversary of my wife's father's death.","furigana":"しちがつ|つま|ちち|いっしゅうき|ほうじ"},{"japanese":"祖母が亡くなって、早いもので来月一周忌を迎えます。","romaji":"Sobo ga nakunatte, hayai mono de raigetsu isshuuki o mukaemasu.","german":"My grandmother passed away, and time flies: next month will be the first anniversary.","furigana":"そぼ|な|はや|らいげついっしゅうき|むか"},{"japanese":"一周忌の法要には、どんな服装で行けばいいでしょうか。","romaji":"Isshuuki no houyou ni wa, donna fukusou de ikeba ii deshou ka.","german":"What should one wear to a first-anniversary memorial service?","furigana":"いっしゅうき|ほうよう|ふくそう|い"}],"notes":"一周忌 is the first anniversary of a death and the accompanying Buddhist memorial service (一周忌の法要／法事). After that, counting continues with 回忌: the second anniversary is already 三回忌. Typical combinations are 一周忌を迎える and 一周忌を営む \"to hold the memorial service\". Until the 一周忌, one is considered in mourning (喪中) in Japan.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"昔は稲の苗を水田に植えつける女性のことを「早乙女」や「植女」と呼んでいました。","romaji":"Mukashi wa ine no nae o suiden ni uetsukeru josei no koto o \"saotome\" ya \"ueme\" to yonde imashita.","german":"In the past, women who planted rice seedlings in paddies were called \"saotome\" or \"ueme\".","furigana":"むかし|いね|なえ|すいでん|う|じょせい|さおとめ||よ"},{"japanese":"田植え祭りでは、着物姿の早乙女たちが歌に合わせて苗を植える。","romaji":"Taue matsuri de wa, kimono sugata no saotome-tachi ga uta ni awasete nae o ueru.","german":"At the rice-planting festival, women in kimono plant seedlings to the rhythm of songs.","furigana":"たう|まつ|きものすがた|さおとめ|うた|あ|なえ|う"},{"japanese":"地元の小学生が早乙女の衣装を着て、田植えを体験した。","romaji":"Jimoto no shōgakusei ga saotome no ishō o kite, taue o taiken shita.","german":"Local elementary school children put on saotome costumes and tried rice planting.","furigana":"じもと|しょうがくせい|さおとめ|いしょう|き|たう|たいけん"}],"notes":"早乙女 are the young women who set the seedlings in the field during traditional rice planting (田植え); さ (早) is the same prefix as in 早苗 (\"rice seedling\"), and 乙女 means \"maiden\". Today the word is alive mainly in folk customs and rice festivals, where 早乙女 plant in kimono and straw hats (菅笠). In older language it can mean \"young girl\" in general; 早乙女 is also a well-known family name.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"太平洋戦争のことを、大東亜戦争と呼ぶのは何故ですか。","romaji":"Taiheiyou sensou no koto o, Daitoua sensou to yobu no wa naze desu ka.","german":"Why is the Pacific War called the Greater East Asia War?","furigana":"たいへいようせんそう|だいとうあせんそう|よ|なぜ"},{"japanese":"戦前の新聞には、「東亜」という言葉がよく出てくる。","romaji":"Senzen no shinbun ni wa, \"Toua\" to iu kotoba ga yoku dete kuru.","german":"The word \"Tōa\" for East Asia often appears in prewar newspapers.","furigana":"せんぜん|しんぶん|とうあ|ことば|で"},{"japanese":"韓国の東亜日報は、1920年に創刊された新聞です。","romaji":"Kankoku no Toua Nippou wa, sen kyuuhyaku nijuu nen ni soukan sareta shinbun desu.","german":"Korea's Dong-A Ilbo is a newspaper founded in 1920.","furigana":"かんこく|とうあにっぽう|ねん|そうかん|しんぶん"}],"notes":"東亜 is an older, literary word for East Asia. Today 東アジア is normally used; 東亜 lives on mainly in the proper names of companies, universities and newspapers (e.g. the Korean newspaper 東亜日報) and in historical wartime terms such as 大東亜共栄圏 and 大東亜戦争. Because of this association, the word seems historical and partly politically loaded in modern texts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"こんな侮辱を受けて、黙っていろというのか。","romaji":"Konna bujoku o ukete, damatte iro to iu no ka.","german":"Are you telling me to stay quiet after receiving an insult like this?","furigana":"ぶじょく|う|だま"},{"japanese":"彼は会議で部下を侮辱するような発言をして、問題になった。","romaji":"Kare wa kaigi de buka o bujoku suru you na hatsugen o shite, mondai ni natta.","german":"He made a remark in the meeting that insulted his subordinates, which became a problem.","furigana":"かれ|かいぎ|ぶか|ぶじょく|はつげん|もんだい"},{"japanese":"ネットでの誹謗中傷が増え、侮辱罪が厳しくなった。","romaji":"Netto de no hibou chuushou ga fue, bujokuzai ga kibishiku natta.","german":"With online slander on the rise, the insult offense has been made stricter.","furigana":"|ふ|ぶじょくざい|きび"}],"notes":"侮辱 is an insult or humiliation that injures another person's honor. As a transitive する-verb: 人を侮辱する; from the victim's perspective 侮辱を受ける or 侮辱される; adjectivally 侮辱的な発言. Legally there is 侮辱罪 \"insult (as a criminal offense)\". In everyday speech, for milder mocking, 馬鹿にする is more usual; 侮辱 is more serious and more formal.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"床がちょっと凸凹しているのが気になるね。","romaji":"Yuka ga chotto dekoboko shite iru no ga ki ni naru ne.","german":"It bothers me that the floor is a little uneven.","furigana":"ゆか|でこぼこ|き"},{"japanese":"山道は凸凹で、自転車ではとても走りにくかった。","romaji":"Yamamichi wa dekoboko de, jitensha de wa totemo hashirinikukatta.","german":"The mountain path was bumpy and very hard to ride on by bicycle.","furigana":"やまみち|でこぼこ|じてんしゃ|はし"},{"japanese":"背の高い彼と小柄な彼女は、職場で有名なでこぼこコンビだ。","romaji":"Se no takai kare to kogara na kanojo wa, shokuba de yuumei na dekoboko konbi da.","german":"The tall man and the petite woman are a well-known mismatched pair at work.","furigana":"せ|たか|かれ|こがら|かのじょ|しょくば|ゆうめい"}],"notes":"凸凹 (usually written でこぼこ) describes an uneven, bumpy surface: でこぼこ道, 道がでこぼこしている, as an adjective でこぼこな／でこぼこの. Figuratively it denotes unevenness or imbalance, e.g. in performance or in 凸凹コンビ \"mismatched duo\". The same kanji are also read おうとつ in technical usage (\"projections and depressions\") - that is a different, written word.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"失恋で凹んでいます。","romaji":"Shitsuren de hekonde imasu.","german":"I'm feeling down because of a breakup.","furigana":"しつれん|へこ"},{"japanese":"駐車場でぶつけられて、車のドアが凹んでしまった。","romaji":"Chuushajou de butsukerarete, kuruma no doa ga hekonde shimatta.","german":"I was hit in the parking lot, and the car door got dented.","furigana":"ちゅうしゃじょう|くるま|へこ"},{"japanese":"そんなに凹むなよ。次はきっとうまくいくって。","romaji":"Sonna ni hekomu na yo. Tsugi wa kitto umaku iku tte.","german":"Don't be so down. Next time it will surely go well.","furigana":"へこ|つぎ"}],"notes":"凹む (usually written へこむ) first means \"to be pressed in, to get a dent\": 車のドアが凹んだ. Very common in colloquial speech is the figurative meaning \"to be dejected, to feel down\", often with で for the reason: 失恋で凹む, 上司に怒られて凹んでいる. It is intransitive; the transitive counterpart is 凹ませる \"to dent, to pull in\" (お腹を凹ませる). The opposite in the literal sense is 出っ張る \"to stick out\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"結婚相手はよく吟味してから決めたい。","romaji":"Kekkon aite wa yoku ginmi shite kara kimetai.","german":"I want to choose my spouse only after careful consideration.","furigana":"けっこんあいて|ぎんみ|き"},{"japanese":"当店では、産地を吟味した野菜だけを使っています。","romaji":"Touten de wa, sanchi o ginmi shita yasai dake o tsukatte imasu.","german":"Our shop uses only vegetables from carefully selected growing regions.","furigana":"とうてん|さんち|ぎんみ|やさい|つか"},{"japanese":"提案の内容をもう一度吟味する必要がある。","romaji":"Teian no naiyou o mou ichido ginmi suru hitsuyou ga aru.","german":"We need to scrutinize the content of the proposal once more.","furigana":"ていあん|ないよう|いちどぎんみ|ひつよう"}],"notes":"吟味 means to examine something carefully and select the best, with emphasis on quality: 材料を吟味する, 内容をよく吟味する. Typical in restaurant advertising (吟味した素材 \"carefully selected ingredients\") and for decisions one does not want to make hastily. In contrast to 検査 \"technical inspection\" or 審査 \"assessment by an authority\", 吟味 is about critical weighing. Transitive する-verb.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"婚姻届を役所に提出しました。","romaji":"Kon'intodoke o yakusho ni teishutsu shimashita.","german":"We submitted our marriage registration to the municipal office.","furigana":"こんいんとどけ|やくしょ|ていしゅつ"},{"japanese":"二人は長く一緒に暮らしているが、法律上の婚姻関係にはない。","romaji":"Futari wa nagaku issho ni kurashite iru ga, houritsujou no kon'in kankei ni wa nai.","german":"The two have lived together for a long time but are not in a legal marital relationship.","furigana":"ふたり|なが|いっしょ|く|ほうりつじょう|こんいんかんけい"},{"japanese":"同性同士の婚姻を認めるかどうか、議論が続いている。","romaji":"Douseidoushi no kon'in o mitomeru ka dou ka, giron ga tsuzuite iru.","german":"The debate continues over whether to recognize marriage between same-sex couples.","furigana":"どうせいどうし|こんいん|みと|ぎろん|つづ"}],"notes":"婚姻 is the legal term for marriage or the contracting of marriage and appears in laws, forms and news: 婚姻届 \"marriage registration (at the office)\", 婚姻関係 \"marital relationship\", 婚姻率 \"marriage rate\". In Japan one is legally married as soon as the 婚姻届 is accepted; a wedding ceremony is not necessary for this. In everyday speech one says 結婚; 婚姻 sounds official.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あそこには米軍が駐屯しているんだよ。","romaji":"Asoko ni wa Beigun ga chūton shite iru n da yo.","german":"US forces are stationed over there.","furigana":"べいぐん|ちゅうとん"},{"japanese":"自衛隊の駐屯地では、毎年夏祭りが開かれる。","romaji":"Jieitai no chuutonchi de wa, maitoshi natsumatsuri ga hirakareru.","german":"A summer festival is held every year at the Self-Defense Forces garrison.","furigana":"じえいたい|ちゅうとんち|まいとしなつまつ|ひら"},{"japanese":"その部隊は、国境近くの町に半年間駐屯した。","romaji":"Sono butai wa, kokkyou chikaku no machi ni hantoshikan chuuton shita.","german":"The unit was stationed for six months in a town near the border.","furigana":"ぶたい|こっきょうちか|まち|はんとしかんちゅうとん"}],"notes":"駐屯 means that troops are stationed at a place and take up quarters there. It is an intransitive する-verb: 部隊が〜に駐屯する, 〜に駐屯している. Very common is 駐屯地 \"garrison, barracks\", especially for sites of the Japan Ground Self-Defense Force (陸上自衛隊〇〇駐屯地). For the active \"to station troops\", 駐留させる or 配備する is more usual.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"親しい友人の慶事は自分のことの様にうれしい。","romaji":"Shitashii yūjin no keiji wa jibun no koto no yō ni ureshii.","german":"The happy events of close friends make me as happy as if they were my own.","furigana":"した|ゆうじん|けいじ|じぶん|よう"},{"japanese":"結婚式の招待状の返信には、慶事用の切手を貼るのがマナーです。","romaji":"Kekkonshiki no shoutaijou no henshin ni wa, keijiyou no kitte o haru no ga manaa desu.","german":"When replying to a wedding invitation, etiquette is to affix a stamp for celebratory occasions.","furigana":"けっこんしき|しょうたいじょう|へんしん|けいじよう|きって|は"},{"japanese":"皇室の慶事を祝って、各地で記念行事が行われた。","romaji":"Koushitsu no keiji o iwatte, kakuchi de kinen gyouji ga okonawareta.","german":"To celebrate the joyous event in the imperial family, commemorative events were held in various places.","furigana":"こうしつ|けいじ|いわ|かくち|きねんぎょうじ|おこな"}],"notes":"慶事 is a formal word for a joyful, festive event such as a wedding, birth or anniversary. Its counterpart is 弔事 \"bereavement\"; together they form 慶弔. It appears in questions of etiquette (慶事のマナー, 慶事用の切手), in news about the imperial family (皇室の慶事) and in letters. In conversation one says おめでたいこと or おめでた instead.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"弁慶と牛若丸は清水寺で戦いました。","romaji":"Benkei to Ushiwakamaru wa Kiyomizudera de tatakaimashita.","german":"Benkei and Ushiwakamaru fought at Kiyomizu Temple.","furigana":"べんけい|うしわかまる|きよみずでら|たたか"},{"japanese":"机の脚に弁慶の泣き所をぶつけて、思わずうずくまった。","romaji":"Tsukue no ashi ni benkei no nakidokoro o butsukete, omowazu uzukumatta.","german":"I banged my shin against the table leg and instinctively crouched down in pain.","furigana":"つくえ|あし|べんけい|な|どころ|おも"},{"japanese":"うちの息子は内弁慶で、家ではうるさいのに学校ではおとなしい。","romaji":"Uchi no musuko wa uchibenkei de, ie de wa urusai noni gakkō de wa otonashii.","german":"Our son is a lion at home but quiet at school.","furigana":"むすこ|うちべんけい|いえ|がっこう"}],"notes":"弁慶 is 武蔵坊弁慶, the legendary warrior monk of the 12th century who, according to legend, lost a duel to the young 牛若丸 (later 源義経) and then served him faithfully. By extension, 弁慶 stands for a \"strongman\" and appears in many expressions: 弁慶の泣き所 (\"shin; Achilles' heel\"), 内弁慶 (\"strong at home, timid outside\"), 弁慶の立ち往生 (\"to die standing; to be unable to go forward or back\"). 弁慶 also refers to a perforated bamboo stand for kitchen utensils and, as a short form of 弁慶縞, a checkered pattern.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"慶祝の意を表して、乾杯！","romaji":"Keishuku no i o arawashite, kanpai!","german":"To express our congratulations, cheers!","furigana":"けいしゅく|い|あらわ|かんぱい"},{"japanese":"新天皇の即位を慶祝する行事が、全国で開かれた。","romaji":"Shin tennou no sokui o keishuku suru gyouji ga, zenkoku de hirakareta.","german":"Events celebrating the new emperor's accession were held throughout the country.","furigana":"しんてんのう|そくい|けいしゅく|ぎょうじ|ぜんこく|ひら"},{"japanese":"創立百周年を迎え、町は慶祝ムードに包まれている。","romaji":"Souritsu hyakushuunen o mukae, machi wa keishuku muudo ni tsutsumarete iru.","german":"With the hundredth anniversary of its founding, the town is wrapped in a celebratory mood.","furigana":"そうりつひゃくしゅうねん|むか|まち|けいしゅく|つつ"}],"notes":"慶祝 means to celebrate a joyous event solemnly or to congratulate on it, mostly on a public or official level: 慶祝行事 \"celebration event\", 慶祝ムード, 〜を慶祝する. The word is very formal and belongs to speeches, commemorative volumes and news. For personal congratulations, お祝い or 祝う is used.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"慶弔用の礼服は持っているのか。","romaji":"Keichōyō no reifuku wa motte iru no ka.","german":"Do you have formal wear for weddings and funerals?","furigana":"けいちょうよう|れいふく|も"},{"japanese":"祖母の葬儀のため、会社に慶弔休暇を申請した。","romaji":"Sobo no sougi no tame, kaisha ni keichou kyuuka o shinsei shita.","german":"For my grandmother's funeral, I applied to the company for ceremonial leave.","furigana":"そぼ|そうぎ|かいしゃ|けいちょうきゅうか|しんせい"},{"japanese":"慶弔の際のお金の包み方は、地域によって少しずつ違う。","romaji":"Keichou no sai no okane no tsutsumikata wa, chiiki ni yotte sukoshi zutsu chigau.","german":"How to wrap money for celebrations and condolences differs slightly from region to region.","furigana":"けいちょう|さい|かね|つつ|かた|ちいき|すこ|ちが"}],"notes":"慶弔 combines joyful (慶) and sad (弔) occasions, that is, weddings and births as well as deaths. The word appears mainly in compounds from the world of companies and etiquette: 慶弔休暇 \"special leave for a wedding or bereavement\", 慶弔費 \"payments for celebrations and condolences\", 慶弔電報. On its own it is hardly used in conversation.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"失恋の憂き目に遭いました。","romaji":"Shitsuren no ukime ni aimashita.","german":"I suffered the pain of a broken heart.","furigana":"しつれん|う|め|あ"},{"japanese":"不況のあおりを受けて、老舗の旅館が倒産の憂き目を見た。","romaji":"Fukyou no aori o ukete, shinise no ryokan ga tousan no ukime o mita.","german":"Hit by the recession, a long-established inn went through the bitter experience of bankruptcy.","furigana":"ふきょう|う|しにせ|りょかん|とうさん|う|め|み"},{"japanese":"優勝候補だったチームが、まさかの予選敗退の憂き目に遭った。","romaji":"Yuushou kouho datta chiimu ga, masaka no yosen haitai no ukime ni atta.","german":"The team that was a title favorite suffered the unexpected misfortune of being eliminated in the qualifiers.","furigana":"ゆうしょうこうほ|よせんはいたい|う|め|あ"}],"notes":"憂き目 is a bitter, painful experience that befalls someone. It appears almost always in the set phrases 〜の憂き目に遭う or 〜の憂き目を見る, with the misfortune before it: 倒産の憂き目, 落選の憂き目, リストラの憂き目. The word is literary and common in news and sports reports; in conversation one says つらい目に遭う instead.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"稚拙な文章ですがもしよければ読んでください。","romaji":"Chisetsu na bunshou desu ga moshi yokereba yonde kudasai.","german":"It is a clumsy piece of writing, but please read it if you like.","furigana":"ちせつ|ぶんしょう|よ"},{"japanese":"そんな稚拙な言い訳では、誰も納得しないよ。","romaji":"Sonna chisetsu na iiwake de wa, dare mo nattoku shinai yo.","german":"No one will be convinced by such a childish excuse.","furigana":"ちせつ|い|わけ|だれ|なっとく"},{"japanese":"その画家の絵は一見稚拙だが、不思議な魅力がある。","romaji":"Sono gaka no e wa ikken chisetsu da ga, fushigi na miryoku ga aru.","german":"That painter's pictures look crude at first glance, but they have a strange charm.","furigana":"がか|え|いっけんちせつ|ふしぎ|みりょく"}],"notes":"稚拙 describes something as clumsy and childishly immature, especially texts, drawings, arguments or techniques: 稚拙な文章, 稚拙な言い訳. As a な-adjective it is rather disparaging, but it is often said modestly about one's own work (稚拙な文章ですが…). In contrast to 下手 \"bad, unskilled\", 稚拙 emphasizes immaturity; in art it can also mean positively \"naive, unaffected\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"模擬試験の結果はどうだったの？","romaji":"Mogi shiken no kekka wa dō datta no?","german":"How did the mock exam results turn out?","furigana":"もぎしけん|けっか"},{"japanese":"文化祭で、うちのクラスは焼きそばの模擬店を出します。","romaji":"Bunkasai de, uchi no kurasu wa yakisoba no mogiten o dashimasu.","german":"At the school festival, our class is running a yakisoba stall.","furigana":"ぶんかさい|や|もぎてん|だ"},{"japanese":"法学部の学生が、模擬裁判で弁護士の役を演じた。","romaji":"Hougakubu no gakusei ga, mogi saiban de bengoshi no yaku o enjita.","german":"A law student played the role of defense lawyer in a mock trial.","furigana":"ほうがくぶ|がくせい|もぎさいばん|べんごし|やく|えん"}],"notes":"模擬 means \"imitation, simulation\" and appears almost only as the first element of compounds denoting a practice or trial version: 模擬試験 (short 模試) \"mock exam\", 模擬店 \"food stall at a school festival\", 模擬裁判 \"mock trial\", 模擬授業 \"demonstration lesson\". On its own or as a する-verb it is rarely used; for \"to simulate\", シミュレーションする is more usual.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"戦車は木の枝で擬装されていて、遠くからは見えなかった。","romaji":"Sensha wa ki no eda de gisou sarete ite, tooku kara wa mienakatta.","german":"The tank was camouflaged with tree branches and could not be seen from a distance.","furigana":"せんしゃ|き|えだ|ぎそう|とお|み"},{"japanese":"敵の目を欺くため、その船は漁船に擬装していた。","romaji":"Teki no me o azamuku tame, sono fune wa gyosen ni gisou shite ita.","german":"To deceive the enemy, the ship was disguised as a fishing boat.","furigana":"てき|め|あざむ|ふね|ぎょせん|ぎそう"},{"japanese":"陣地全体が擬装網で覆われている。","romaji":"Jinchi zentai ga gisoumou de oowarete iru.","german":"The entire position is covered with camouflage netting.","furigana":"じんちぜんたい|ぎそうもう|おお"}],"notes":"擬装 means to disguise something so that it looks like something else, especially in the military sense: 車両を木の枝で擬装する, 擬装網 \"camouflage net\". The homophonous spelling 偽装 is much more common today for deception and fraud: 食品偽装 \"food fraud\", 偽装結婚 \"sham marriage\". Both are transitive する-verbs; 擬装 seems more technical-military, 偽装 more criminal.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"法曹を目指そうと思ったきっかけは何ですか。","romaji":"Housou o mezasou to omotta kikkake wa nan desu ka.","german":"What made you decide to aim for a legal career?","furigana":"ほうそう|めざ|おも|なん"},{"japanese":"彼は司法試験に合格し、法曹の道に進んだ。","romaji":"Kare wa shihou shiken ni goukaku shi, housou no michi ni susunda.","german":"He passed the bar exam and entered the legal profession.","furigana":"かれ|しほうしけん|ごうかく|ほうそう|みち|すす"},{"japanese":"この判決は、法曹界でも大きな議論を呼んだ。","romaji":"Kono hanketsu wa, housoukai de mo ooki na giron o yonda.","german":"This ruling sparked major debate even in legal circles.","furigana":"はんけつ|ほうそうかい|おお|ぎろん|よ"}],"notes":"法曹 refers to the professional group of legal practitioners, that is, judges, prosecutors and lawyers, together called 法曹三者. Typical are 法曹界 \"legal world\", 法曹資格 \"qualification to practice law\" and 法曹を目指す \"to aim for a legal career\". Not everyone with a law degree is 法曹; for that one must pass the 司法試験. The word is formal and appears mainly in education and judicial contexts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ご無沙汰しております。","romaji":"Gobusata shite orimasu.","german":"It has been such a long time since we last met.","furigana":"ぶさた"},{"japanese":"長い間ご無沙汰してしまい、大変失礼いたしました。","romaji":"Nagai aida gobusata shite shimai, taihen shitsurei itashimashita.","german":"I apologize very much for not having been in touch for such a long time.","furigana":"なが|あいだ|ぶさた|たいへんしつれい"},{"japanese":"忙しくて、最近はジムにすっかりご無沙汰している。","romaji":"Isogashikute, saikin wa jimu ni sukkari gobusata shite iru.","german":"I've been so busy that I haven't been to the gym at all lately.","furigana":"いそが|さいきん|ぶさた"}],"notes":"ご無沙汰 means not having contacted or visited someone for a long time; with it, one apologizes, so to speak, for one's own silence. The fixed polite formula is ご無沙汰しております - toward superiors, customers and in letters or emails; among friends one more often says 久しぶり. Also written 御無沙汰. Colloquially it is also used figuratively for places or activities: ジムにはご無沙汰だ \"I haven't been to the gym in ages\".","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"全てのロマが貧しい流浪の民というわけではない。","romaji":"Subete no Roma ga mazushii rurou no tami to iu wake de wa nai.","german":"Not all Roma are poor, wandering people.","furigana":"すべ|まず|るろう|たみ"},{"japanese":"戦争で故郷を失った人々は、何年も各地を流浪した。","romaji":"Sensou de kokyou o ushinatta hitobito wa, nannen mo kakuchi o rurou shita.","german":"People who had lost their homeland in the war roamed from place to place for years.","furigana":"せんそう|こきょう|うしな|ひとびと|なんねん|かくち|るろう"},{"japanese":"主君を失った侍は、流浪の旅に出た。","romaji":"Shukun o ushinatta samurai wa, rurou no tabi ni deta.","german":"The samurai who had lost his lord set out on a wandering journey.","furigana":"しゅくん|うしな|さむらい|るろう|たび|で"}],"notes":"流浪 is aimless wandering without a fixed residence, often involuntary, for example after being driven out: 国を追われて流浪する, 流浪の旅, 流浪の民 \"wandering people\". The word is literary and suggests homelessness and hardship. 放浪 is similar but emphasizes free, often self-chosen roaming; 流浪 has a heavier, fateful tone. Intransitive する-verb.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"当時は、逃げるように各地を転々と放浪していました。","romaji":"Tōji wa, nigeru yō ni kakuchi o tenten to hōrō shite imashita.","german":"At that time I drifted from place to place as if fleeing.","furigana":"とうじ|に|かくち|てんてん|ほうろう"},{"japanese":"彼は若いころ、バックパック一つでアジアを放浪した。","romaji":"Kare wa wakai koro, bakkupakku hitotsu de Ajia o hourou shita.","german":"In his youth, he wandered around Asia with just a backpack.","furigana":"かれ|わか|ひと|ほうろう"},{"japanese":"うちの猫は放浪癖があって、二、三日帰ってこないこともある。","romaji":"Uchi no neko wa hourouheki ga atte, ni, sannichi kaette konai koto mo aru.","german":"Our cat has a tendency to roam and sometimes doesn't come home for two or three days.","furigana":"ねこ|ほうろうへき|に|さんにちかえ"}],"notes":"放浪 is wandering without a fixed goal or residence: 各地を放浪する, 放浪の旅, 放浪生活. It can sound romantically free (artists, globetrotters); hence 放浪癖 \"wanderlust, tendency to roam\". Compared to 流浪 it is less fateful; for mere strolling around town, ぶらぶらする or 散策 is more usual. Intransitive する-verb; the area roamed is marked with を.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"終戦直後の駅には、多くの浮浪者が寝泊まりしていた。","romaji":"Shuusen chokugo no eki ni wa, ooku no furousha ga netomari shite ita.","german":"Right after the war ended, many vagrants slept at the stations.","furigana":"しゅうせんちょくご|えき|おお|ふろうしゃ|ねと"},{"japanese":"小説の主人公は、浮浪者に身をやつした元貴族だった。","romaji":"Shousetsu no shujinkou wa, furousha ni mi o yatsushita moto kizoku datta.","german":"The novel's protagonist was a former nobleman who had been reduced to living as a vagrant.","furigana":"しょうせつ|しゅじんこう|ふろうしゃ|み|もときぞく"},{"japanese":"今は「浮浪者」ではなく、「ホームレス」と言うことが多い。","romaji":"Ima wa \"furousha\" de wa naku, \"hoomuresu\" to iu koto ga ooi.","german":"Today people often say \"homeless\" rather than \"furōsha\".","furigana":"いま|ふろうしゃ|い|おお"}],"notes":"浮浪者 refers to a vagrant, that is, someone without a fixed residence or regular work. The word is considered derogatory today and is avoided in the media; neutral terms are ホームレス or, officially, 路上生活者. 浮浪者 is therefore found mainly in older texts, novels and historical accounts, for example about the postwar period.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"漆黒の闇に青い地球が浮かんでいた。","romaji":"Shikkoku no yami ni aoi chikyū ga ukande ita.","german":"The blue Earth floated in the pitch-black darkness.","furigana":"しっこく|やみ|あお|ちきゅう|う"},{"japanese":"彼女の漆黒の髪が、月の光に輝いていた。","romaji":"Kanojo no shikkoku no kami ga, tsuki no hikari ni kagayaite ita.","german":"Her jet-black hair shone in the moonlight.","furigana":"かのじょ|しっこく|かみ|つき|ひかり|かがや"},{"japanese":"この器は、漆黒の地に金で模様が描かれている。","romaji":"Kono utsuwa wa, shikkoku no ji ni kin de moyou ga egakarete iru.","german":"This vessel has a pattern painted in gold on a deep black ground.","furigana":"うつわ|しっこく|じ|きん|もよう|えが"}],"notes":"漆黒 is a deep, glossy black like lacquer (漆 \"urushi lacquer\"). It is used attributively with の: 漆黒の闇, 漆黒の髪, 漆黒の夜空. The word is literary and vivid; in everyday speech 真っ黒 \"completely black\" is used. Unlike 真っ黒, which can also mean \"dirty\" or \"sunburned\", 漆黒 always describes a beautiful, rich black.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"岩礁を埋め立て、人工島に造り替えたんです。","romaji":"Ganshou o umetate, jinkoutou ni tsukurikaeta n desu.","german":"The reef was reclaimed and turned into an artificial island.","furigana":"がんしょう|う|た|じんこうとう|つく|か"},{"japanese":"嵐の夜、貨物船が岩礁に乗り上げた。","romaji":"Arashi no yoru, kamotsusen ga ganshou ni noriageta.","german":"On a stormy night, a cargo ship ran aground on a reef.","furigana":"あらし|よる|かもつせん|がんしょう|の|あ"},{"japanese":"この岩礁の周りは、魚が多くて釣り人に人気だ。","romaji":"Kono ganshou no mawari wa, sakana ga ookute tsuribito ni ninki da.","german":"Around this reef there are many fish, so it is popular with anglers.","furigana":"がんしょう|まわ|さかな|おお|つ|びと|にんき"}],"notes":"岩礁 is a rocky reef, that is, rocks lying just below or above the water surface that are dangerous for ships. Typical is 岩礁に乗り上げる \"to run aground on a reef\". For coral reefs, サンゴ礁 is used; 暗礁 is a hidden reef under water and appears figuratively in 暗礁に乗り上げる \"to run into a deadlock\". 岩礁 is a plain, rather written term from seafaring and geography.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"できれば禍根を残すことは避けたい。","romaji":"Dekireba kakon o nokosu koto wa saketai.","german":"If possible, I would like to avoid leaving behind seeds of future trouble.","furigana":"かこん|のこ|さ"},{"japanese":"拙速な決定は、将来に禍根を残すことになりかねない。","romaji":"Sessoku na kettei wa, shourai ni kakon o nokosu koto ni narikanenai.","german":"A hasty decision could end up sowing seeds of trouble for the future.","furigana":"せっそく|けってい|しょうらい|かこん|のこ"},{"japanese":"事件の禍根を断つため、制度そのものを見直すべきだ。","romaji":"Jiken no kakon o tatsu tame, seido sono mono o minaosu beki da.","german":"To cut off the root of the incident, the system itself should be reviewed.","furigana":"じけん|かこん|た|せいど|みなお"}],"notes":"禍根 is the root of a future disaster, that is, a cause that will bring trouble later. It appears almost always in 禍根を残す \"to sow the seed of future trouble\" (often 将来に禍根を残す) and 禍根を断つ \"to strike the evil at its root\". The word is written-style and typical of politics, editorials and negotiations; in conversation one says 後で問題になる instead.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"戦禍を逃れて田舎に疎開した。","romaji":"Senka o nogarete inaka ni sokai shita.","german":"We escaped the ravages of the war and were evacuated to the countryside.","furigana":"せんか|のが|いなか|そかい"},{"japanese":"多くの市民が戦禍に巻き込まれ、家を失った。","romaji":"Ooku no shimin ga senka ni makikomare, ie o ushinatta.","german":"Many citizens were caught up in the war and lost their homes.","furigana":"おお|しみん|せんか|ま|こ|いえ|うしな"},{"japanese":"この寺は、奇跡的に戦禍を免れた。","romaji":"Kono tera wa, kisekiteki ni senka o manugareta.","german":"This temple miraculously escaped the destruction of war.","furigana":"てら|きせきてき|せんか|"}],"notes":"戦禍 refers to the devastation and suffering caused by a war. Typical combinations: 戦禍を逃れる \"to escape the turmoil of war\", 戦禍に巻き込まれる \"to be drawn into the war\", 戦禍を被る \"to suffer from the war\". It is a written word from news and historical accounts; 戦災 emphasizes more the concrete material damage (戦災孤児).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"父が危篤だという電話がありました。","romaji":"Chichi ga kitoku da to iu denwa ga arimashita.","german":"I got a phone call saying my father is in critical condition.","furigana":"ちち|きとく|でんわ"},{"japanese":"祖母が危篤状態に陥り、家族全員が病院に集まった。","romaji":"Sobo ga kitoku joutai ni ochiiri, kazoku zen'in ga byouin ni atsumatta.","german":"My grandmother fell into critical condition, and the whole family gathered at the hospital.","furigana":"そぼ|きとくじょうたい|おちい|かぞくぜんいん|びょういん|あつ"},{"japanese":"危篤の知らせを受けて、彼は急いで新幹線に飛び乗った。","romaji":"Kitoku no shirase o ukete, kare wa isoide shinkansen ni tobinotta.","german":"Upon receiving the news of his critical condition, he hurriedly jumped on the Shinkansen.","furigana":"きとく|し|う|かれ|いそ|しんかんせん|と|の"}],"notes":"危篤 refers to the critical condition of a sick person who could die at any moment. Typical: 危篤だ, 危篤状態に陥る, 危篤の知らせを受ける. It is a noun (with だ or の), not an adjective. In contrast to 重体 \"seriously injured / in serious condition\", which is often used after accidents, 危篤 signals that death is imminent - the family is then summoned immediately.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 33 (source: N4; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"牽切紡績とは、化学繊維特有の紡績方法で、綿からの紡績ではありません。","romaji":"Kensetsu bōseki to wa, kagaku sen'i tokuyū no bōseki hōhō de, men kara no bōseki de wa arimasen.","german":"Break spinning is a spinning method specific to synthetic fibers and is not spinning from cotton.","furigana":"|かがくせんいとくゆう|ぼうせきほうほう|めん|ぼうせき"},{"japanese":"明治時代、紡績業は日本の近代化を支えた。","romaji":"Meiji jidai, bōsekigyō wa Nihon no kindaika o sasaeta.","german":"In the Meiji era, the spinning industry supported Japan's modernization.","furigana":"めいじじだい|ぼうせきぎょう|にほん|きんだいか|ささ"},{"japanese":"祖母は若い頃、紡績工場で働いていた。","romaji":"Sobo wa wakai koro, bōseki kōjō de hataraite ita.","german":"My grandmother worked in a spinning mill when she was young.","furigana":"そぼ|わか|ころ|ぼうせきこうじょう|はたら"}],"notes":"紡績 is the industrial spinning of fibers such as cotton or wool into yarn; the corresponding verb is 紡ぐ (\"to spin\"). The word is encountered mainly in business and history: 紡績業, 紡績工場, 紡績会社, 紡績機. To be distinguished are 製糸 (producing raw silk from cocoons) and 織物 or 機織り (weaving). In the Meiji era, cotton spinning was an engine of industrialization; company names like 東洋紡 still carry the short form 紡.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"戦後の日本の繊維産業は、綿紡織と化学繊維を中心に復興した。","romaji":"Sengo no Nihon no sen'i sangyou wa, men boushoku to kagaku sen'i o chuushin ni fukkou shita.","german":"After the war, Japan's textile industry recovered mainly around cotton spinning and weaving and synthetic fibers.","furigana":"せんご|にほん|せんいさんぎょう|めんぼうしょく|かがくせんい|ちゅうしん|ふっこう"},{"japanese":"祖母は若いころ、紡織工場で働いていたそうだ。","romaji":"Sobo wa wakai koro, boushoku koujou de hataraite ita sou da.","german":"I hear my grandmother worked in a spinning and weaving mill when she was young.","furigana":"そぼ|わか|ぼうしょくこうじょう|はたら"},{"japanese":"明治時代、紡織業は日本の近代化を支えた。","romaji":"Meiji jidai, boushokugyou wa Nihon no kindaika o sasaeta.","german":"In the Meiji era, the textile industry supported Japan's modernization.","furigana":"めいじじだい|ぼうしょくぎょう|にほん|きんだいか|ささ"}],"notes":"紡織 combines spinning (紡) and weaving (織) and refers to textile manufacturing as a branch of industry. It is a technical word from business and history: 紡織業, 紡織工場, 綿紡織 \"cotton spinning and weaving\". 紡織 played a central role in Japan's modernization; in everyday speech one talks more of 繊維産業 or 織物.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あわよくばおこぼれにあずかりたいという魂胆が見え見えだよ。","romaji":"Awayokuba okobore ni azukaritai to iu kontan ga miemie da yo.","german":"It's obvious that you have an ulterior motive of getting some leftovers if possible.","furigana":"こんたん|み|み"},{"japanese":"急に優しくなるなんて、何か魂胆があるに違いない。","romaji":"Kyuu ni yasashiku naru nante, nanika kontan ga aru ni chigainai.","german":"Suddenly being nice like that, he must have some scheme.","furigana":"きゅう|やさ|なに|こんたん|ちが"},{"japanese":"安く買い叩こうという相手の魂胆は、最初から分かっていた。","romaji":"Yasuku kaitatakou to iu aite no kontan wa, saisho kara wakatte ita.","german":"I knew from the start the other side's scheme of trying to beat the price down.","furigana":"やす|か|たた|あいて|こんたん|さいしょ|わ"}],"notes":"魂胆 is a hidden, usually self-serving plan; an ulterior motive that one imputes to others. Typical and derogatory: 魂胆がある, 何か魂胆があるに違いない, 魂胆が見え見えだ／見え透いている. It is used almost only about others, not about oneself. More neutral are 下心 \"ulterior motive (often romantic)\" or 狙い \"aim, intention\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"胆嚢炎は、ほとんどの場合、胆石が原因です。","romaji":"Tannōen wa, hotondo no baai, tanseki ga gen'in desu.","german":"Gallbladder inflammation is in most cases caused by gallstones.","furigana":"たんのうえん|ばあい|たんせき|げんいん"},{"japanese":"父は胆石の手術を受けて、一週間入院した。","romaji":"Chichi wa tanseki no shujutsu o ukete, isshuukan nyuuin shita.","german":"My father had surgery for gallstones and was in the hospital for a week.","furigana":"ちち|たんせき|しゅじゅつ|う|いっしゅうかんにゅういん"},{"japanese":"脂っこい食事を続けると、胆石ができやすくなるそうです。","romaji":"Aburakkoi shokuji o tsuzukeru to, tanseki ga dekiyasuku naru sou desu.","german":"If you keep eating greasy food, you apparently develop gallstones more easily.","furigana":"あぶら|しょくじ|つづ|たんせき"}],"notes":"胆石 is a gallstone (胆 \"gall\", 石 \"stone\"). Typical are 胆石ができる \"gallstones form\", 胆石の手術 and 胆石症 \"gallstone disease\". Similarly formed are 尿路結石 and 腎臓結石 \"kidney stone\". The word is medical but also common in everyday speech when talking about illnesses.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大腸菌を美少女に擬人化したカードゲームのアプリがあります。","romaji":"Daichōkin o bishōjo ni gijinka shita kādo gēmu no apuri ga arimasu.","german":"There is a card game app in which E. coli is personified as a pretty girl.","furigana":"だいちょうきん|びしょうじょ|ぎじんか"},{"japanese":"井戸水から大腸菌が検出されました。","romaji":"Ido mizu kara daichōkin ga kenshutsu saremashita.","german":"E. coli was detected in the well water.","furigana":"いどみず|だいちょうきん|けんしゅつ"},{"japanese":"生肉を触った手には大腸菌が付いていることがある。","romaji":"Namaniku o sawatta te ni wa daichōkin ga tsuite iru koto ga aru.","german":"E. coli can be present on hands that have touched raw meat.","furigana":"なまにく|さわ|て|だいちょうきん|つ"}],"notes":"大腸菌 is the intestinal bacterium Escherichia coli, literally \"large-intestine bacterium\". It is typical in news about hygiene and food: 大腸菌が検出される \"E. coli is detected\", 大腸菌に汚染される; in water testing one speaks of 大腸菌群 \"coliforms\", and in food poisoning of 腸管出血性大腸菌 (e.g. O157). Bacteria in general are called 細菌 or colloquially 菌 or バクテリア.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このブログでは、萌え系を中心に、僕の好きなアニメや漫画を紹介していきます。","romaji":"Kono burogu de wa, moekei o chūshin ni, boku no suki na anime ya manga o shōkai shite ikimasu.","german":"On this blog, I'll introduce my favorite anime and manga, focusing on moe types.","furigana":"も|けい|ちゅうしん|ぼく|す|まんが|しょうかい"},{"japanese":"秋葉原には萌えグッズを売る店がたくさんある。","romaji":"Akihabara ni wa moe guzzu o uru mise ga takusan aru.","german":"In Akihabara there are many shops selling moe merchandise.","furigana":"あきはばら|も|う|みせ"},{"japanese":"彼は眼鏡をかけたキャラクターに萌えるタイプだ。","romaji":"Kare wa megane o kaketa kyarakutā ni moeru taipu da.","german":"He is the type who gets infatuated with characters who wear glasses.","furigana":"かれ|めがね|も"}],"notes":"萌え is otaku slang for tender enthusiasm for (mostly cute) anime and manga characters; as an exclamation 萌え～! \"how cute!\", as a verb 〜に萌える \"to be smitten with something\". Common in compounds such as 萌えキャラ, 萌え系, 萌えグッズ. Originally 萌え or 萌える literally means \"sprouting, budding\" (cf. 萌え出る); today the slang word seems somewhat out of fashion, and younger fans rather say 推し or 尊い.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"とても普遍的なテーマだと思います。","romaji":"Totemo fuhenteki na tēma da to omoimasu.","german":"I think it's a very universal theme.","furigana":"ふへんてき|おも"},{"japanese":"親が子を思う気持ちは、時代や国を問わず普遍的なものだ。","romaji":"Oya ga ko o omou kimochi wa, jidai ya kuni o towazu fuhenteki na mono da.","german":"A parent's love for their child is universal, regardless of era or country.","furigana":"おや|こ|おも|きも|じだい|くに|と|ふへんてき"},{"japanese":"科学の法則は普遍的に成り立つと考えられている。","romaji":"Kagaku no hōsoku wa fuhenteki ni naritatsu to kangaerarete iru.","german":"The laws of science are thought to hold universally.","furigana":"かがく|ほうそく|ふへんてき|な|た|かんが"}],"notes":"普遍的 is a な-adjective: 普遍的な価値 \"universal value\", 普遍的に成り立つ \"to hold universally\". It emphasizes that something applies to all times, places and people, often in discussions of philosophy, art and science; the opposite is 特殊 \"special\" or 個別的. Do not confuse it with 一般的 \"generally common, widespread\", which describes only frequency. The corresponding noun is 普遍性 \"universality\".","levelNote":"Compound: 普遍 (N1) + 的 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一遍にいろんなことを言われても、分からないわ。","romaji":"Ippen ni ironna koto o iwarete mo, wakaranai wa.","german":"Even if you tell me so many different things at once, I can't understand.","furigana":"いっぺん|い|わ"},{"japanese":"宿題を一遍に片付けようとしないで、毎日少しずつやりなさい。","romaji":"Shukudai o ippen ni katazukeyō to shinaide, mainichi sukoshi zutsu yarinasai.","german":"Don't try to finish all your homework at once; do a little every day.","furigana":"しゅくだい|いっぺん|かたづ|まいにちすこ"},{"japanese":"冷たい水で顔を洗ったら、眠気が一遍に吹き飛んだ。","romaji":"Tsumetai mizu de kao o arattara, nemuke ga ippen ni fukitonda.","german":"When I washed my face with cold water, my drowsiness vanished all at once.","furigana":"つめ|みず|かお|あら|ねむけ|いっぺん|ふ|と"}],"notes":"一遍に means \"everything at once, in one go\" and is usually written in kana as いっぺんに; it is somewhat more colloquial than 一度に or 同時に. It is typical with things that pile up: 一遍に片付ける, 一遍に言われても困る. It also means \"suddenly, instantly\": 眠気が一遍に吹き飛ぶ. Do not confuse it with 一遍 without に (\"once\", e.g. 一遍読んだ).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"遮断機が降りている時に、線路を渡ってはいけません。","romaji":"Shadanki ga orite iru toki ni, senro o watatte wa ikemasen.","german":"Do not cross the tracks while the crossing gate is down.","furigana":"しゃだんき|お|とき|せんろ|わた"},{"japanese":"電車が通過すると、遮断機が上がった。","romaji":"Densha ga tsūka suru to, shadanki ga agatta.","german":"After the train passed, the gate went up.","furigana":"でんしゃ|つうか|しゃだんき|あ"},{"japanese":"駐車場の出口では、料金を払わないと遮断機が上がりません。","romaji":"Chūshajō no deguchi de wa, ryōkin o harawanai to shadanki ga agarimasen.","german":"At the parking lot exit, the barrier won't go up unless you pay the fee.","furigana":"ちゅうしゃじょう|でぐち|りょうきん|はら|しゃだんき|あ"}],"notes":"遮断機 is the barrier at a railroad crossing (踏切), literally \"blocking machine\"; barriers at toll gates and parking lots are also called this. One says 遮断機が下りる (also written 降りる) \"the barrier comes down\" and 遮断機が上がる \"goes up\". The crossing itself is 踏切, and the warning device with bell and flashing light is 警報機. The verb 遮断する generally means \"to block off, to cut off\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"来年は、遷都1300年記念のイベントが開催されます。","romaji":"Rainen wa, sento 1300-nen kinen no ibento ga kaisai saremasu.","german":"Next year, an event commemorating the 1300th anniversary of the relocation of the capital will be held.","furigana":"らいねん|せんと|ねんきねん|かいさい"},{"japanese":"794年、桓武天皇は平安京に遷都した。","romaji":"794-nen, Kanmu tennō wa Heiankyō ni sento shita.","german":"In 794, Emperor Kanmu moved the capital to Heian-kyō.","furigana":"ねん|かんむてんのう|へいあんきょう|せんと"},{"japanese":"首都機能の移転が議論されたが、本格的な遷都には至らなかった。","romaji":"Shuto kinō no iten ga giron sareta ga, honkakuteki na sento ni wa itaranakatta.","german":"Relocating the capital's functions was discussed, but it never came to an actual move of the capital.","furigana":"しゅときのう|いてん|ぎろん|ほんかくてき|せんと|いた"}],"notes":"遷都 is the relocation of the capital, especially in Japanese history: 平城京に遷都する, 平安遷都 (794). As an intransitive する-verb, the destination is marked with に or へ. The word is written-style and historically tinged; for present-day plans to relocate government functions, 首都移転 or 首都機能移転 is more usual. 都 means \"capital\" here, 遷 \"to move\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ここは日本酪農発祥の地です。","romaji":"Koko wa Nippon rakunō hasshō no chi desu.","german":"This is the birthplace of Japanese dairy farming.","furigana":"にっぽんらくのうはっしょう|ち"},{"japanese":"北海道は酪農が盛んな地域です。","romaji":"Hokkaidō wa rakunō ga sakan na chiiki desu.","german":"Hokkaido is a region where dairy farming thrives.","furigana":"ほっかいどう|らくのう|さか|ちいき"},{"japanese":"祖父は牧場で酪農を営んでいる。","romaji":"Sofu wa bokujō de rakunō o itonande iru.","german":"My grandfather runs a dairy farm on a ranch.","furigana":"そふ|ぼくじょう|らくのう|いとな"}],"notes":"酪農 is dairy farming, that is, keeping cows to obtain milk, butter and cheese; 酪 stands for dairy products. Typical combinations: 酪農が盛んだ \"dairy farming is widespread\", 酪農を営む \"to run a dairy farm\", 酪農家 \"dairy farmer\". The umbrella term for livestock farming in general (including meat) is 畜産, and grazing livestock on pasture is 牧畜.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ヒマラヤスギは、樹齢30年を超えないと雌花を付けません。","romaji":"Himarayasugi wa, jurei 30nen o koenai to mebana o tsukemasen.","german":"Himalayan cedars do not bear female flowers until they are over 30 years old.","furigana":"じゅれい|ねん|こ|めばな|つ"},{"japanese":"キュウリの雌花には、小さな実のようなふくらみがある。","romaji":"Kyūri no mebana ni wa, chiisana mi no yō na fukurami ga aru.","german":"The female flower of a cucumber has a small swelling that looks like a tiny fruit.","furigana":"めばな|ちい|み"},{"japanese":"カボチャは雄花の花粉を雌花につけて受粉させます。","romaji":"Kabocha wa obana no kafun o mebana ni tsukete jufun sasemasu.","german":"For pumpkins, pollen from the male flower is applied to the female flower to pollinate it.","furigana":"おばな|かふん|めばな|じゅふん"}],"notes":"雌花 is a female (pistil-bearing only) flower; its counterpart is 雄花 \"male flower\". In compounds 雌 is read め (雌花, 雌しべ \"pistil\"), on its own めす. One says 雌花が咲く or 雌花をつける \"to bear female flowers\". The word appears mainly in vegetable growing (cucumbers, pumpkins) and in botany.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"みなさんは、誰がこの詠歌を作ったのか知っていますか？","romaji":"Minasan wa, dare ga kono eika o tsukutta no ka shitte imasu ka?","german":"Do you all know who composed this poem?","furigana":"だれ|えいか|つく|し"},{"japanese":"巡礼者たちが御詠歌を唱えながら山道を歩いていた。","romaji":"Junreisha-tachi ga go-eika o tonaenagara yamamichi o aruite ita.","german":"The pilgrims walked along the mountain path chanting their pilgrim hymns.","furigana":"じゅんれいしゃ|ごえいか|とな|やまみち|ある"},{"japanese":"歌会で、参加者それぞれの詠歌が披露された。","romaji":"Utakai de, sankasha sorezore no eika ga hirō sareta.","german":"At the poetry gathering, each participant's poem was presented.","furigana":"うたかい|さんかしゃ|えいか|ひろう"}],"notes":"詠歌 is an elevated, rather classical word for a (waka) poem, especially a tanka, or the composing itself; the corresponding verb is 詠む \"to compose poetry\". Today it is encountered mainly in 御詠歌 (ご詠歌), the Buddhist pilgrim songs sung with small bells on pilgrimages such as the 33 temples of Saigoku. In everyday speech, for poems one says 和歌, 短歌 or simply 歌.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その山の頂上から見た夕日の美しさに詠嘆した。","romaji":"Sono yama no chōjō kara mita yūhi no utsukushisa ni eitan shita.","german":"I was moved to admiration by the beauty of the sunset seen from the summit of that mountain.","furigana":"やま|ちょうじょう|み|ゆうひ|うつく|えいたん"},{"japanese":"古文の「けり」には、詠嘆の意味もある。","romaji":"Kobun no \"keri\" ni wa, eitan no imi mo aru.","german":"The classical \"keri\" can also carry an exclamatory meaning.","furigana":"こぶん|えいたん|いみ"},{"japanese":"彼は思わず「なんと美しい」と詠嘆の声を上げた。","romaji":"Kare wa omowazu \"nanto utsukushii\" to eitan no koe o ageta.","german":"He involuntarily cried out in admiration, \"How beautiful!\"","furigana":"かれ|おも|うつく|えいたん|こえ|あ"}],"notes":"詠嘆 is a deeply felt exclamation of admiration or emotion, such as \"Ah, how beautiful!\". As an intransitive する-verb, the trigger is marked with に: 美しさに詠嘆する; more common, however, are 詠嘆の声 and 詠嘆を込めて. The word is very often encountered in classical-language grammar: 詠嘆の助動詞「けり」, 詠嘆の終助詞「かな」. In everyday speech 感嘆 or 感動する is more usual.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"沙羅さんと会釈をして別れました。","romaji":"Sara-san to eshaku o shite wakaremashita.","german":"Sara and I exchanged a nod and parted.","furigana":"さら|えしゃく|わか"},{"japanese":"沙羅双樹の花の色、盛者必衰の理をあらはす。","romaji":"Sarasōju no hana no iro, jōsha hissui no kotowari o arawasu.","german":"The color of the sal flowers reveals the truth that the prosperous must decline.","furigana":"さらそうじゅ|はな|いろ|じょうしゃひっすい|"},{"japanese":"お寺の庭で沙羅の花が咲いていた。","romaji":"Otera no niwa de sara no hana ga saite ita.","german":"Stewartia flowers were blooming in the temple garden.","furigana":"てら|にわ|さら|はな|さ"}],"notes":"In the dictionary, 沙羅 refers to the Indian sal tree under which the Buddha is said to have died; 沙羅双樹 from the opening of the 平家物語 is famous. Since the sal tree does not grow in Japan, the Japanese stewartia (ナツツバキ) is called 沙羅 or 沙羅の木 there; it blooms in early summer in many temple gardens. 沙羅 (さら) is also a popular girl's name.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"申し申し、そこのお方、財布を落とされましたよ。","romaji":"Mōshi mōshi, soko no okata, saifu o otosaremashita yo.","german":"Excuse me, sir, you've dropped your wallet!","furigana":"もう|もう|かた|さいふ|お"},{"japanese":"時代劇で、町人が「申し申し、お侍様」と呼び止める場面があった。","romaji":"Jidaigeki de, chōnin ga \"mōshi mōshi, o-samurai-sama\" to yobitomeru bamen ga atta.","german":"In the period drama, there was a scene in which a townsman stopped a samurai by calling, \"Excuse me, honorable samurai!\"","furigana":"じだいげき|ちょうにん|もう|もう|さむらいさま|よ|と|ばめん"},{"japanese":"「もしもし」は「申し申し」が縮まった言葉だと言われている。","romaji":"\"Moshimoshi\" wa \"mōshi mōshi\" ga chijimatta kotoba da to iwarete iru.","german":"\"Moshimoshi\" is said to be a shortened form of \"mōshi mōshi\".","furigana":"もう|もう|ちぢ|ことば|い"}],"notes":"申し申し is an archaic call for addressing or stopping someone: \"Excuse me!, You there!\", literally the doubling of 申す \"to say\". Today it is heard only in samurai dramas (時代劇) or rakugo. The telephone もしもし developed from it; but nobody says 申し申し on the phone, and businesses answer with their name rather than with もしもし anyway.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"次の内閣の首班には誰が選ばれると思う？","romaji":"Tsugi no naikaku no shuhan ni wa dare ga erabareru to omou?","german":"Who do you think will be chosen as head of the next cabinet?","furigana":"つぎ|ないかく|しゅはん|だれ|えら|おも"},{"japanese":"国会で首班指名選挙が行われた。","romaji":"Kokkai de shuhan shimei senkyo ga okonawareta.","german":"The election to designate the prime minister was held in the Diet.","furigana":"こっかい|しゅはんしめいせんきょ|おこな"},{"japanese":"連立政権では、どの党の党首が首班となるかが焦点だ。","romaji":"Renritsu seiken de wa, dono tō no tōshu ga shuhan to naru ka ga shōten da.","german":"In a coalition government, the key question is which party's leader becomes prime minister.","furigana":"れんりつせいけん|とう|とうしゅ|しゅはん|しょうてん"}],"notes":"首班 today refers almost only to the head of government, that is, the prime minister as head of the cabinet. The most important combination is 首班指名 \"designation of the prime minister by the parliament\", also 内閣の首班 and 首班に選ばれる. The word is political and written-style; for a leader in general one says リーダー or 指導者, and for the top of the class 首席.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大学では解剖学を研究しています。","romaji":"Daigaku de wa kaibōgaku o kenkyū shite imasu.","german":"I do research in anatomy at university.","furigana":"だいがく|かいぼうがく|けんきゅう"},{"japanese":"医学部の学生は、解剖学の授業で人体の構造を学ぶ。","romaji":"Igakubu no gakusei wa, kaibōgaku no jugyō de jintai no kōzō o manabu.","german":"Medical students learn the structure of the human body in anatomy class.","furigana":"いがくぶ|がくせい|かいぼうがく|じゅぎょう|じんたい|こうぞう|まな"},{"japanese":"その画家は、人物を正確に描くために解剖学を勉強した。","romaji":"Sono gaka wa, jinbutsu o seikaku ni egaku tame ni kaibōgaku o benkyō shita.","german":"That painter studied anatomy in order to depict people accurately.","furigana":"がか|じんぶつ|せいかく|えが|かいぼうがく|べんきょう"}],"notes":"解剖学 is anatomy as the science of body structure; 解剖 on its own is the dissection itself (解剖する, 司法解剖 \"forensic autopsy\"). One says 解剖学を学ぶ／研究する, 人体解剖学, and as an adjective 解剖学的な. Do not confuse it with 生理学 \"physiology\", which studies the functions of the body.","levelNote":"Compound: 解剖 (N1) + 学 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は犯人を隠匿していたんだよ。","romaji":"Kanojo wa hannin o intoku shite ita n da yo.","german":"She was harboring the criminal.","furigana":"かのじょ|はんにん|いんとく"},{"japanese":"社長は会社の資産を隠匿した疑いで逮捕された。","romaji":"Shachō wa kaisha no shisan o intoku shita utagai de taiho sareta.","german":"The company president was arrested on suspicion of concealing company assets.","furigana":"しゃちょう|かいしゃ|しさん|いんとく|うたが|たいほ"},{"japanese":"戦後、軍の隠匿物資が闇市に流れた。","romaji":"Sengo, gun no intoku busshi ga yamiichi ni nagareta.","german":"After the war, hoarded military supplies flowed into the black markets.","furigana":"せんご|ぐん|いんとくぶっし|やみいち|なが"}],"notes":"隠匿する is a legal, written-style transitive verb (X を隠匿する) for hiding things or people, mostly in connection with wrongdoing: 財産を隠匿する, 隠匿物資 \"hoarded goods\". In everyday speech one simply says 隠す. To be distinguished is 隠蔽 \"to cover up\" (facts, scandals: 事実を隠蔽する); hiding a perpetrator is called 犯人蔵匿 in criminal law.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"匿名で投書をしました。","romaji":"Tokumei de tōsho o shimashita.","german":"I sent in a letter anonymously.","furigana":"とくめい|とうしょ"},{"japanese":"ネットの匿名の書き込みでも、責任は伴う。","romaji":"Netto no tokumei no kakikomi demo, sekinin wa tomonau.","german":"Even anonymous posts on the internet carry responsibility.","furigana":"とくめい|か|こ|せきにん|ともな"},{"japanese":"寄付をしてくださった方は、匿名を希望されています。","romaji":"Kifu o shite kudasatta kata wa, tokumei o kibō sarete imasu.","german":"The donor wishes to remain anonymous.","furigana":"きふ|かた|とくめい|きぼう"}],"notes":"匿名 means appearing without a (real) name: 匿名で \"anonymously\", 匿名の手紙 \"anonymous letter\", 匿名希望 \"wishes to remain anonymous\", 匿名性 \"anonymity (e.g. online)\". The opposite is 実名 \"real name\"; a self-chosen name is 仮名 (かめい) or ペンネーム. Do not confuse it with 無名 \"unknown, nameless\" (無名の画家).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"升目を数えると、全部で400ありました。","romaji":"Masume o kazoeru to, zenbu de 400 arimashita.","german":"When I counted the squares, there were 400 in total.","furigana":"ますめ|かぞ|ぜんぶ"},{"japanese":"原稿用紙の升目に一字ずつ丁寧に書いた。","romaji":"Genkō yōshi no masume ni ichiji zutsu teinei ni kaita.","german":"I carefully wrote one character in each square of the manuscript paper.","furigana":"げんこうようし|ますめ|いちじ|ていねい|か"},{"japanese":"申込書の升目に郵便番号を記入してください。","romaji":"Mōshikomisho no masume ni yūbin bangō o kinyū shite kudasai.","german":"Please write the postal code in the boxes on the application form.","furigana":"もうしこみしょ|ますめ|ゆうびんばんごう|きにゅう"}],"notes":"升目 is a single square in a grid, such as on graph paper, on Japanese manuscript paper (原稿用紙) or in form fields; today it is mostly written マス目, more rarely 枡目. Typical: 升目に書く／記入する, 升目を埋める. Originally 升 is a wooden measure for rice, hence also the old meaning \"measure, quantity\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私のおじさんは、唄うアコーディオン弾きです。","romaji":"Watashi no ojisan wa, utau akōdion hiki desu.","german":"My uncle is a singing accordion player.","furigana":"わたし|うた|ひ"},{"japanese":"祖母は台所で民謡を唄いながら料理をしていた。","romaji":"Sobo wa daidokoro de min'yō o utainagara ryōri o shite ita.","german":"My grandmother was cooking in the kitchen while singing folk songs.","furigana":"そぼ|だいどころ|みんよう|うた|りょうり"},{"japanese":"盆踊りでは、みんなで輪になって唄い踊った。","romaji":"Bon odori de wa, minna de wa ni natte utai odotta.","german":"At the Bon dance, everyone formed a circle, singing and dancing.","furigana":"ぼんおど|わ|うた|おど"}],"notes":"唄う is a spelling variant of 歌う \"to sing\". 唄 is preferred for traditional Japanese songs such as 民謡, 長唄 or 小唄 and when a folksy, heartfelt tone is meant; the standard in school and everyday life is 歌う. Not to be confused with 謳う (\"to extol; to stipulate explicitly\", e.g. 憲法に謳われた権利). Construction as with 歌う: 民謡を唄う, 〜を唄いながら.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5 (source: N5; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"長唄教室に通い始めました。","romaji":"Nagauta kyōshitsu ni kayoi hajimemashita.","german":"I've started going to a nagauta class.","furigana":"ながうたきょうしつ|かよ|はじ"},{"japanese":"歌舞伎の舞踊では、長唄が伴奏として演奏される。","romaji":"Kabuki no buyō de wa, nagauta ga bansō to shite ensō sareru.","german":"In kabuki dances, nagauta is played as accompaniment.","furigana":"かぶき|ぶよう|ながうた|ばんそう|えんそう"},{"japanese":"祖母は若いころから長唄と三味線を習っている。","romaji":"Sobo wa wakai koro kara nagauta to shamisen o naratte iru.","german":"My grandmother has been learning nagauta and shamisen since she was young.","furigana":"そぼ|わか|ながうた|しゃみせん|なら"}],"notes":"長唄 is a genre of shamisen vocal music that arose in Edo in the early 17th century as accompaniment for kabuki dance. One speaks of 長唄を習う, 長唄の師匠, 長唄三味線. The character 唄 instead of 歌 is used for traditional songs sung to the shamisen; shorter forms are called 小唄 and 端唄, folk songs 民謡.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地下20mのところに坑道が掘られています。","romaji":"Chika 20 mētoru no tokoro ni kōdō ga horarete imasu.","german":"A mine tunnel has been dug 20 meters underground."},{"japanese":"落盤事故で、作業員が坑道に閉じ込められた。","romaji":"Rakuban jiko de, sagyōin ga kōdō ni tojikomerareta.","german":"In a cave-in accident, workers were trapped in the tunnel.","furigana":"らくばんじこ|さぎょういん|こうどう|と|こ"},{"japanese":"閉山した鉱山の坑道を見学できるツアーがある。","romaji":"Heizan shita kōzan no kōdō o kengaku dekiru tsuā ga aru.","german":"There are tours where you can see the tunnels of a closed mine.","furigana":"へいざん|こうざん|こうどう|けんがく"}],"notes":"坑道 is an underground passage in mining, that is, a tunnel or shaft; 坑 means \"pit\" (cf. 炭坑 \"coal mine\"). Typical: 坑道を掘る, 坑道に閉じ込められる, 坑道の入口. For traffic tunnels, トンネル is used. Beware of the homophones 行動 \"action\" and 講堂 \"auditorium\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"花婿が結婚式場から逃げ出したらしいわよ。","romaji":"Hanamuko ga kekkonshikijō kara nigedashitarashii wa yo.","german":"I hear the groom ran away from the wedding hall!","furigana":"はなむこ|けっこんしきじょう|に|だ"},{"japanese":"花婿と花嫁が並んで写真を撮った。","romaji":"Hanamuko to hanayome ga narande shashin o totta.","german":"The bride and groom had their picture taken side by side.","furigana":"はなむこ|はなよめ|なら|しゃしん|と"},{"japanese":"花婿の父として、ひと言ご挨拶申し上げます。","romaji":"Hanamuko no chichi to shite, hitokoto go-aisatsu mōshiagemasu.","german":"As the groom's father, I would like to say a few words of greeting.","furigana":"はなむこ|ちち|こと|あいさつもう|あ"}],"notes":"花婿 is the bridegroom, paired with 花嫁 \"bride\". At weddings and in speeches, 新郎 (with 新婦) is the more formal choice: 新郎新婦のご入場. 婿 on its own means \"son-in-law\" or a man who marries into his wife's family (婿入り, 婿養子).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いずれは娘婿を社長にするつもりです。","romaji":"Izure wa musume muko o shachō ni suru tsumori desu.","german":"I intend to make my son-in-law president eventually.","furigana":"むすめむこ|しゃちょう"},{"japanese":"娘婿とは、休みの日によく一緒に釣りに行く。","romaji":"Musumemuko to wa, yasumi no hi ni yoku issho ni tsuri ni iku.","german":"On days off, I often go fishing with my son-in-law.","furigana":"むすめむこ|やす|ひ|いっしょ|つ|い"},{"japanese":"娘婿の実家は九州なので、なかなか会えません。","romaji":"Musumemuko no jikka wa Kyūshū na node, nakanaka aemasen.","german":"My son-in-law's family home is in Kyushu, so we can't see each other often.","furigana":"むすめむこ|じっか|きゅうしゅう|あ"}],"notes":"娘婿 is the husband of one's own daughter, that is, the son-in-law from the parents' point of view; more neutral but longer is 義理の息子. If he marries into the wife's family and takes her name, he is a 婿養子. The counterpart from the parents' point of view is the daughter-in-law 息子の嫁.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"寡婦控除について詳しく教えてください。","romaji":"Kafu kōjo nitsuite kuwashiku oshiete kudasai.","german":"Please explain the widow's deduction to me in detail.","furigana":"かふこうじょ|くわ|おし"},{"japanese":"戦争で夫を亡くした寡婦たちは、苦しい生活を強いられた。","romaji":"Sensō de otto o nakushita kafu-tachi wa, kurushii seikatsu o shiirareta.","german":"Widows who lost their husbands in the war were forced to lead hard lives.","furigana":"せんそう|おっと|な|かふ|くる|せいかつ|し"},{"japanese":"法律上の「寡婦」には、離婚して再婚していない女性も含まれる。","romaji":"Hōritsujō no \"kafu\" ni wa, rikon shite saikon shite inai josei mo fukumareru.","german":"Legally, \"widows\" also include divorced women who have not remarried.","furigana":"ほうりつじょう|かふ|りこん|さいこん|じょせい|ふく"}],"notes":"寡婦 is a written-style, official term for a widow, in law also for a divorced woman who has not remarried; the tax term 寡婦控除 is well known. In everyday speech one says 未亡人 (now sometimes felt to be inappropriate) or the circumlocution 夫を亡くした女性; 後家 and やもめ are outdated. A widower is called, with the same reading, 寡夫 (かふ).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"店内は帆船をイメージした内装となっております。","romaji":"Tennai wa hansen o imēji shita naisō to natte orimasu.","german":"The shop's interior is designed with the image of a sailing ship.","furigana":"てんない|はんせん|ないそう"},{"japanese":"港に大きな帆船が停泊していた。","romaji":"Minato ni ōkina hansen ga teihaku shite ita.","german":"A large sailing ship was moored in the harbor.","furigana":"みなと|おお|はんせん|ていはく"},{"japanese":"大航海時代、ヨーロッパの帆船は世界中の海を渡った。","romaji":"Daikōkai jidai, Yōroppa no hansen wa sekaijū no umi o watatta.","german":"In the Age of Discovery, European sailing ships crossed the seas of the whole world.","furigana":"だいこうかいじだい|はんせん|せかいじゅう|うみ|わた"}],"notes":"帆船 is a sailing ship; 帆 (ほ) is the sail, 帆を張る \"to set the sails\". It contrasts with 汽船 \"steamship\"; small recreational sailboats are usually called ヨット. Besides はんせん there is the rarer reading ほぶね. Typical are 帆船が停泊する and 帆船模型 \"sailing ship model\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この木は帆柱にちょうどいいぞ。","romaji":"Kono ki wa hobashira ni chōdo ii zo.","german":"This tree is just right for a mast.","furigana":"き|ほばしら"},{"japanese":"嵐で帆柱が折れてしまった。","romaji":"Arashi de hobashira ga orete shimatta.","german":"The mast broke in the storm.","furigana":"あらし|ほばしら|お"},{"japanese":"船員が帆柱に登って、遠くの島を探した。","romaji":"Sen'in ga hobashira ni nobotte, tōku no shima o sagashita.","german":"A sailor climbed the mast and searched for a distant island.","furigana":"せんいん|ほばしら|のぼ|とお|しま|さが"}],"notes":"帆柱 is the mast of a sailing ship, to which the sail (帆) is attached; the reading ほばしら follows 帆 ほ and 柱 はしら. Today the loanword マスト is more common; 帆柱 seems traditional and suits old ships or literature. Typical: 帆柱が折れる, 帆柱に登る, 帆柱を立てる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"廉価版のゲームのパッケージって安物感丸出しだよな。","romaji":"Renkaban no gēmu no pakkēji tte yasumonokan marudashi da yo na.","german":"The packaging of budget versions of games looks so cheap, doesn't it?","furigana":"れんかばん|やすものかんまるだ"},{"japanese":"この店では、ブランド品を廉価で販売している。","romaji":"Kono mise de wa, burando-hin o renka de hanbai shite iru.","german":"This store sells brand-name goods at low prices.","furigana":"みせ|ひん|れんか|はんばい"},{"japanese":"スマホの廉価モデルでも、普段使いには十分だ。","romaji":"Sumaho no renka moderu demo, fudan-zukai ni wa jūbun da.","german":"Even a budget smartphone model is enough for everyday use.","furigana":"れんか|ふだんづか|じゅうぶん"}],"notes":"廉価 is a written-style, commercial word for a \"low, inexpensive price\". It mostly appears as a noun: 廉価で販売する \"to sell cheaply\", 廉価版 \"budget edition\", 廉価モデル, 廉価品; as a な-adjective (廉価な商品) it is rarer. In everyday speech one says 安い, and in advertising and business also 安価 or 低価格. 廉 means \"cheap\" here (but cf. 清廉 \"honest, upright\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"小さな管弦楽団でバイオリンを演奏しています。","romaji":"Chiisana kangengakudan de baiorin o ensō shite imasu.","german":"I play the violin in a small orchestra.","furigana":"ちい|かんげんがくだん|えんそう"},{"japanese":"市の管弦楽団が、毎年クリスマスにコンサートを開いている。","romaji":"Shi no kangengakudan ga, maitoshi kurisumasu ni konsāto o hiraite iru.","german":"The city orchestra gives a concert every Christmas.","furigana":"し|かんげんがくだん|まいとし|ひら"},{"japanese":"ウィーン・フィルハーモニー管弦楽団の来日公演のチケットを手に入れた。","romaji":"Wīn Firuhāmonī Kangengakudan no rainichi kōen no chiketto o te ni ireta.","german":"I got tickets for the Vienna Philharmonic Orchestra's concert in Japan.","furigana":"かんげんがくだん|らいにちこうえん|て|い"}],"notes":"管弦楽団 is an orchestra, literally a \"wind-and-string music group\" (管 wind instruments, 弦 stringed instruments). In everyday speech one usually says オーケストラ; 管弦楽団 appears mainly in ensemble names such as 〜フィルハーモニー管弦楽団 or 〜室内管弦楽団, alongside 〜交響楽団 \"symphony orchestra\". A wind band without strings is called 吹奏楽団, and orchestral music in general 管弦楽.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"異国の地で、母への思慕が募るばかりです。","romaji":"Ikoku no chi de, haha e no shibo ga tsunoru bakari desu.","german":"In a foreign land, my longing for my mother only grows.","furigana":"いこく|ち|はは|しぼ|つの"},{"japanese":"彼女は、亡き師への思慕の念を手紙につづった。","romaji":"Kanojo wa, naki shi e no shibo no nen o tegami ni tsuzutta.","german":"She put her deep affection for her late teacher into a letter.","furigana":"かのじょ|な|し|しぼ|ねん|てがみ"},{"japanese":"少年は年上の女性をひそかに思慕していた。","romaji":"Shōnen wa toshiue no josei o hisoka ni shibo shite ita.","german":"The boy secretly yearned for an older woman.","furigana":"しょうねん|としうえ|じょせい|しぼ"}],"notes":"思慕 is a literary term for deep, yearning affection for someone who is distant, deceased or revered. Typical are 〜への思慕, 思慕の念を抱く, 思慕が募る; as a transitive する-verb: 母を思慕する. The character 慕 is also found in the more common verb 慕う \"to be attached to, to adore\"; romantic love is rather 恋慕, and in everyday speech one says 恋しい or 会いたい.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"卒業式で感慨無量の思いだった。","romaji":"Sotsugyōshiki de kangai muryō no omoi datta.","german":"I was filled with deep emotion at the graduation ceremony.","furigana":"そつぎょうしき|かんがいむりょう|おも"},{"japanese":"三十年ぶりに故郷に帰ってきて、感慨無量です。","romaji":"Sanjū-nen-buri ni kokyō ni kaette kite, kangai muryō desu.","german":"Returning to my hometown for the first time in thirty years, I am filled with deep emotion.","furigana":"さんじゅうねん|こきょう|かえ|かんがいむりょう"},{"japanese":"娘の花嫁姿を見て、父は感慨無量の面持ちだった。","romaji":"Musume no hanayome sugata o mite, chichi wa kangai muryō no omomochi datta.","german":"Seeing his daughter in her wedding dress, the father had a look of deep emotion.","furigana":"むすめ|はなよめすがた|み|ちち|かんがいむりょう|おもも"}],"notes":"感慨 is the deep feeling when looking back on something, and 無量 means \"immeasurable\": 感慨無量 describes the overwhelming emotion at graduations, reunions or anniversaries. Typical: 感慨無量だ／です, 感慨無量の思い／面持ち. The short form 感無量 is also common. It is more solemn than 感動する; while 感動 reacts to something beautiful, 感慨無量 carries a sense of looking back on a long road.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"初めて自分の書いたものが出版されるのは、やっぱり感慨深いものがあります。","romaji":"Hajimete jibun no kaita mono ga shuppan sareru no wa, yappari kangaibukai mono ga arimasu.","german":"Having something I wrote published for the first time is, of course, deeply moving.","furigana":"はじ|じぶん|か|しゅっぱん|かんがいぶか"},{"japanese":"二十年ぶりに母校を訪れて、感慨深かった。","romaji":"Nijū-nen-buri ni bokō o otozurete, kangaibukakatta.","german":"Visiting my old school for the first time in twenty years was deeply moving.","furigana":"にじゅうねん|ぼこう|おとず|かんがいぶか"},{"japanese":"この橋が完成するまでの十年を思うと、感慨深い。","romaji":"Kono hashi ga kansei suru made no jū-nen o omou to, kangaibukai.","german":"When I think of the ten years until this bridge was completed, I am deeply moved.","furigana":"はし|かんせい|じゅうねん|おも|かんがいぶか"}],"notes":"感慨深い is an い-adjective for the moved, reflective feeling when looking back on past time, hardships or memories. Very typical is the phrase 感慨深いものがある; related is 感慨にふける \"to sink into reminiscence\". Unlike 感動的 \"moving\" (a film, a speech), it usually describes one's own emotion at a milestone, for example a graduation, reunion or anniversary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今子供達の間で流行っている悪戯ですよ。","romaji":"Ima kodomotachi no aida de hayatte iru itazura desu yo.","german":"That's a prank that's popular among kids right now.","furigana":"いまこどもたち|あいだ|はや|いたずら"},{"japanese":"子どもの頃、よく友達にいたずらをして叱られた。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, yoku tomodachi ni itazura o shite shikarareta.","german":"When I was a child, I often played pranks on my friends and got scolded.","furigana":"こ|ころ|ともだち|しか"},{"japanese":"夜中にいたずら電話がかかってきた。","romaji":"Yonaka ni itazura denwa ga kakatte kita.","german":"A prank call came in the middle of the night.","furigana":"よなか|でんわ"}],"notes":"いたずら is a \"prank, mischief\", especially by children: いたずらをする, 〜にいたずらする \"to play a prank on someone\". As a na-adjective, いたずらな means \"naughty, mischievous\"; common compounds are いたずら電話 \"prank call\" and いたずら書き \"scribbling\". It can also mean unauthorized fiddling with objects (火でいたずらする), and in the news it is a euphemism for sexual assault. Almost always written in kana; 悪戯 is seen mainly in novels.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"戯れに文章を綴っただけなのに、賞を獲ってしまった。","romaji":"Tawamure ni bunshō o tsuzutta dake na noni, shō o totte shimatta.","german":"I only wrote it for fun, yet it won a prize.","furigana":"たわむ|ぶんしょう||しょう|"},{"japanese":"ほんの戯れのつもりで言ったことが、彼女を深く傷つけた。","romaji":"Honno tawamure no tsumori de itta koto ga, kanojo o fukaku kizutsuketa.","german":"What I said as a mere joke hurt her deeply.","furigana":"たわむ|い|かのじょ|ふか|きず"},{"japanese":"二人が再会したのは、まさに運命の戯れだった。","romaji":"Futari ga saikai shita no wa, masa ni unmei no tawamure datta.","german":"That the two met again was truly a trick of fate.","furigana":"ふたり|さいかい|うんめい|たわむ"}],"notes":"戯れ is the noun of 戯れる (\"to play, frolic; to dally\") and means \"play, jest, whim\". It is written-literary; in everyday speech one says 冗談 or 遊び. Common is the adverbial 戯れに (\"for fun, on a whim\"), as well as 戯れの恋 (\"a flirtation\") and 運命の戯れ (\"a trick of fate\"). Related, but derogatory, is 戯言 (たわごと) \"nonsense, silly talk\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"俺の姉は、マフィアから残虐な拷問を受けたんだ。","romaji":"Ore no ane wa, mafia kara zangyaku na goumon o uketa n da.","german":"My older sister was brutally tortured by the mafia.","furigana":"おれ|あね|ざんぎゃく|ごうもん|う"},{"japanese":"拷問によって得られた自白は、証拠として認められない。","romaji":"Gōmon ni yotte erareta jihaku wa, shōko to shite mitomerarenai.","german":"Confessions obtained through torture are not accepted as evidence.","furigana":"ごうもん|え|じはく|しょうこ|みと"},{"japanese":"この暑さの中で一時間も待たされるなんて、まるで拷問だ。","romaji":"Kono atsusa no naka de ichi-jikan mo matasareru nante, marude gōmon da.","german":"Being made to wait an hour in this heat is sheer torture.","furigana":"あつ|なか|いちじかん|ま|ごうもん"}],"notes":"拷問 is torture, especially to extort a confession or information. Typical combinations: 拷問を受ける \"to be tortured\", 拷問を加える, 拷問にかける. Colloquially it is used figuratively: まるで拷問だ \"it's pure torture\". Do not confuse it with 尋問 \"interrogation\" or 虐待 \"abuse (e.g. of children)\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"某私立高校に伝わる、奇妙な噂があるんだ。","romaji":"Bō shiritsu kōkō ni tsutawaru, kimyō na uwasa ga aru n da.","german":"There is a strange rumor going around about a certain private high school.","furigana":"ぼうしりつこうこう|つた|きみょう|うわさ"},{"japanese":"都内某所で、人気歌手のドラマ撮影が行われた。","romaji":"Tonai bōsho de, ninki kashu no dorama satsuei ga okonawareta.","german":"A popular singer's drama was filmed at an undisclosed location in Tokyo.","furigana":"とないぼうしょ|にんきかしゅ|さつえい|おこな"},{"japanese":"先日、某大手企業の社長と食事をする機会があった。","romaji":"Senjitsu, bō ōte kigyō no shachō to shokuji o suru kikai ga atta.","german":"The other day I had the chance to have a meal with the president of a certain major company.","furigana":"せんじつ|ぼうおおてきぎょう|しゃちょう|しょくじ|きかい"}],"notes":"某 (ぼう) is placed before a noun when one deliberately does not name something: 某所 \"a certain place\", 某氏 \"a certain gentleman\", 某社, 某大学, 某日. After a family name it means \"a certain ...\" (山田某). It is typical of news and written language, but is also used jokingly on blogs; more neutral is ある (ある日, ある会社). The kun reading なにがし (山田なにがし) is old-fashioned.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その刑事は、銃弾を浴びて殉職しました。","romaji":"Sono keiji wa, jūdan o abite junshoku shimashita.","german":"That detective was hit by bullets and died in the line of duty.","furigana":"けいじ|じゅうだん|あ|じゅんしょく"},{"japanese":"消火活動中に殉職した消防士を追悼する式典が開かれた。","romaji":"Shōka katsudōchū ni junshoku shita shōbōshi o tsuitō suru shikiten ga hirakareta.","german":"A ceremony was held to mourn the firefighter who died on duty while fighting a fire.","furigana":"しょうかかつどうちゅう|じゅんしょく|しょうぼうし|ついとう|しきてん|ひら"},{"japanese":"殉職者の家族には、国から補償が支払われる。","romaji":"Junshokusha no kazoku ni wa, kuni kara hoshō ga shiharawareru.","german":"The families of those who die in the line of duty receive compensation from the state.","furigana":"じゅんしょくしゃ|かぞく|くに|ほしょう|しはら"}],"notes":"殉職する is an intransitive する-verb: to lose one's life in the line of duty, especially of police officers, firefighters, soldiers or rescue workers. Typical: 職務中に殉職する, 殉職者, 殉職警察官. Related but different are 戦死 \"to fall in war\", 殉教 \"martyrdom (for one's faith)\" and 殉死 \"to follow one's lord in death\". The word has a respectful, honoring tone.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"吸い殻が二本たまったら、すぐに灰皿を交換してください。","romaji":"Suigara ga nihon tamattara, sugu ni haizara o kōkan shite kudasai.","german":"When two butts have accumulated, please replace the ashtray right away.","furigana":"す|がら|にほん|はいざら|こうかん"},{"japanese":"吸い殻のポイ捨ては条例で禁止されています。","romaji":"Suigara no poisute wa jōrei de kinshi sarete imasu.","german":"Throwing away cigarette butts is prohibited by ordinance.","furigana":"す|がら|す|じょうれい|きんし"},{"japanese":"公園の清掃で、たくさんの吸い殻を拾った。","romaji":"Kōen no seisō de, takusan no suigara o hirotta.","german":"While cleaning the park, we picked up lots of cigarette butts.","furigana":"こうえん|せいそう|す|がら|ひろ"}],"notes":"吸い殻 is a cigarette butt, from 吸う \"to smoke\" and 殻 \"shell, husk\"; it is counted with 本 and is also written 吸殻. Typical: タバコの吸い殻, 吸い殻を捨てる, 吸い殻入れ, and the prohibited ポイ捨て \"careless littering\". The ashtray is called 灰皿.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"胃液は1日1～2Lも分泌されています。","romaji":"Ieki wa ichinichi ichi kara ni rittoru mo bunpitsu sarete imasu.","german":"As much as one to two liters of gastric juice are secreted per day."},{"japanese":"緊張すると、アドレナリンが分泌される。","romaji":"Kinchō suru to, adorenarin ga bunpitsu sareru.","german":"When you are nervous, adrenaline is secreted.","furigana":"きんちょう|ぶんぴつ"},{"japanese":"汗を分泌する汗腺は、全身に分布している。","romaji":"Ase o bunpitsu suru kansen wa, zenshin ni bunpu shite iru.","german":"The sweat glands that secrete sweat are distributed over the whole body.","furigana":"あせ|ぶんぴつ|かんせん|ぜんしん|ぶんぷ"}],"notes":"分泌 means \"secretion\" by glands and organs; in medicine the reading ぶんぴ is also heard. It is both transitive and intransitive: 汗を分泌する, 胃液が分泌する, but most often in the passive 〜が分泌される. Compounds: 分泌物 \"secretion\", 内分泌 \"internal secretion, endocrine\". Note: in 泌尿器 the same character 泌 is read ひ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"泌尿器の病気で入院することになりました。","romaji":"Hinyōki no byōki de nyūin suru koto ni narimashita.","german":"I am going to be hospitalized for a disease of the urinary organs.","furigana":"ひにょうき|びょうき|にゅういん"},{"japanese":"念のため、泌尿器科で検査を受けた。","romaji":"Nen no tame, hinyōkika de kensa o uketa.","german":"Just to be safe, I had an examination in urology.","furigana":"ねん|ひにょうきか|けんさ|う"},{"japanese":"年を取ると、泌尿器のトラブルが増えやすい。","romaji":"Toshi o toru to, hinyōki no toraburu ga fueyasui.","german":"As you get older, urinary problems tend to increase.","furigana":"とし|と|ひにょうき|ふ"}],"notes":"泌尿器 are the organs that produce and drain urine: kidneys, ureters, bladder and urethra; 泌 \"to secrete\" is read ひ here (unlike in 分泌 ぶんぴつ), and 尿 is \"urine\". The word is most often encountered in 泌尿器科 \"urology, urology department\". Typical are 泌尿器の病気 and 泌尿器系.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ラフティングで渓流を下りました。","romaji":"Rafutingu de keiryuu o orimashita.","german":"We went down the mountain stream while rafting.","furigana":"けいりゅう|お"},{"japanese":"渓流釣りでイワナを三匹釣った。","romaji":"Keiryū-zuri de iwana o sanbiki tsutta.","german":"Stream fishing, I caught three char.","furigana":"けいりゅうづ|"},{"japanese":"渓流のせせらぎを聞きながら、キャンプを楽しんだ。","romaji":"Keiryū no seseragi o kikinagara, kyanpu o tanoshinda.","german":"We enjoyed camping while listening to the murmur of the mountain stream.","furigana":"けいりゅう|き|たの"}],"notes":"渓流 is a mountain stream flowing through a narrow valley (渓); one thinks of clear, fast water between rocks. Typical are 渓流釣り \"stream fishing (for char, trout)\", 渓流沿いの道 and 渓流のせせらぎ \"the murmur of the stream\". The valley itself is 渓谷; a brook in the lowlands is more likely 小川, and a clear river 清流.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼がポテトチップスを食べる音が煩くて苛々します。","romaji":"Kare ga potetochippusu o taberu oto ga urusakute iraira shimasu.","german":"The sound of him eating potato chips is noisy and irritating.","furigana":"かれ|た|おと|うるさ|いらいら"},{"japanese":"夜は煩くしないでください。","romaji":"Yoru wa urusaku shinaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't make noise at night.","furigana":"よる|うるさ"}],"notes":"煩い is read うるさい here and is mostly written in hiragana too. It describes disturbing noise and can also express that something gets on one's nerves. As an i-adjective it becomes うるさくて before て and うるさくしないで in a negative request. A bare うるさい shouted on its own can sound very harsh.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 32"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"煩雑な手続きに苛々します。","romaji":"Hanzatsu na tetsuzuki ni iraira shimasu.","german":"The cumbersome procedures irritate me.","furigana":"はんざつ|てつづ|いらいら"},{"japanese":"書類の管理が煩雑になってきたので、システムを導入した。","romaji":"Shorui no kanri ga hanzatsu ni natte kita node, shisutemu o dōnyū shita.","german":"Because managing the documents had become too cumbersome, we introduced a system.","furigana":"しょるい|かんり|はんざつ|どうにゅう"},{"japanese":"説明が煩雑にならないよう、要点だけをまとめた。","romaji":"Setsumei ga hanzatsu ni naranai yō, yōten dake o matometa.","german":"So that the explanation would not get too convoluted, I summarized only the main points.","furigana":"せつめい|はんざつ|ようてん"}],"notes":"煩雑 is a な-adjective for things that are cumbersome and troublesome because of many details: 煩雑な手続き, 煩雑な事務作業, 手続きが煩雑になる. It is also written 繁雑 with the same meaning. In contrast to 複雑 \"complicated (in structure)\", 煩雑 emphasizes the effort and annoyance; colloquially one says 面倒 or ややこしい.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日々の煩忙から逃れ、ハワイにやって来ました。","romaji":"Hibi no hanbō kara nogare, Hawai ni yattekimashita.","german":"I escaped my hectic daily routine and came to Hawaii.","furigana":"ひび|はんぼう|のが|き"},{"japanese":"年末の煩忙に紛れて、年賀状を書きそびれた。","romaji":"Nenmatsu no hanbō ni magirete, nengajō o kakisobireta.","german":"Lost in the bustle of the year-end, I failed to write my New Year's cards.","furigana":"ねんまつ|はんぼう|まぎ|ねんがじょう|か"},{"japanese":"煩忙な日々を送る中でも、家族との時間を大切にしている。","romaji":"Hanbō na hibi o okuru naka demo, kazoku to no jikan o taisetsu ni shite iru.","german":"Even while living busy days, I value time with my family.","furigana":"はんぼう|ひび|おく|なか|かぞく|じかん|たいせつ"}],"notes":"煩忙 means \"very busy, rushed\", as a noun or な-adjective: 日々の煩忙, 煩忙な日々. The word is rare and written-style; much more common is the homophone 繁忙 (繁忙期 \"peak season\", 繁忙を極める). In everyday speech one says 忙しい, and in business letters 多忙 (ご多忙のところ).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"夏は、静かな湖畔のロッジで読書をしています。","romaji":"Natsu wa, shizuka na kohan no rojji de dokusho o shite imasu.","german":"In summer, I read in a quiet lodge by the lakeside.","furigana":"なつ|しず|こはん|どくしょ"},{"japanese":"湖畔を散歩しながら、朝日を眺めた。","romaji":"Kohan o sanpo shinagara, asahi o nagameta.","german":"While strolling along the lakeshore, I watched the sunrise.","furigana":"こはん|さんぽ|あさひ|なが"},{"japanese":"湖畔のキャンプ場は、週末になると家族連れでにぎわう。","romaji":"Kohan no kyanpujō wa, shūmatsu ni naru to kazokuzure de nigiwau.","german":"The campground by the lake is lively with families on weekends.","furigana":"こはん|じょう|しゅうまつ|かぞくづ"}],"notes":"湖畔 is the lakeshore or the area right at the lake; 畔 means \"edge, bank\". Typical are 湖畔の宿／ホテル, 湖畔に建つ, 湖畔を散歩する. In parallel there is 河畔 \"riverbank\"; by the sea one says 海辺 or 海岸. Literary-soft is 湖のほとり.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"男女計5人から赤痢菌が検出されました。","romaji":"Danjo kei gonin kara sekirikin ga kenshutsu saremashita.","german":"Dysentery bacteria were detected in five people in total, men and women.","furigana":"だんじょけい|にん|せきりきん|けんしゅつ"},{"japanese":"旅行先で生水を飲んで、赤痢にかかってしまった。","romaji":"Ryokōsaki de namamizu o nonde, sekiri ni kakatte shimatta.","german":"I drank unboiled water while traveling and caught dysentery.","furigana":"りょこうさき|なまみず|の|せきり"},{"japanese":"赤痢は、昔の日本では命にかかわる感染症だった。","romaji":"Sekiri wa, mukashi no Nihon de wa inochi ni kakawaru kansenshō datta.","german":"In Japan in the past, dysentery was a life-threatening infectious disease.","furigana":"せきり|むかし|にほん|いのち|かんせんしょう"}],"notes":"赤痢 is dysentery, an intestinal infection with bloody diarrhea: 赤 \"red\" and 痢 \"diarrhea\" (cf. 下痢). One distinguishes 細菌性赤痢 and アメーバ赤痢; the pathogen is called 赤痢菌 (Shigella, named after the Japanese 志賀潔). Typical: 赤痢にかかる \"to contract dysentery\", 赤痢菌が検出される.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この海藻は食べられますか。","romaji":"Kono kaisou wa taberaremasu ka.","german":"Is this seaweed edible?","furigana":"かいそう|た"},{"japanese":"海藻にはミネラルや食物繊維が豊富に含まれている。","romaji":"Kaisō ni wa mineraru ya shokumotsu sen'i ga hōfu ni fukumarete iru.","german":"Seaweed is rich in minerals and dietary fiber.","furigana":"かいそう|しょくもつせんい|ほうふ|ふく"},{"japanese":"日本人は昔から、わかめや昆布などの海藻を食べてきた。","romaji":"Nihonjin wa mukashi kara, wakame ya konbu nado no kaisō o tabete kita.","german":"Japanese people have eaten seaweed such as wakame and kombu since long ago.","furigana":"にほんじん|むかし|こんぶ|かいそう|た"}],"notes":"海藻 is the general term for marine algae or seaweed, such as わかめ, 昆布, ひじき, もずく or 海苔. The identically pronounced 海草 actually refers to seagrass (flowering plants in the sea); in everyday speech the two are often confused, but for edible ones 海藻 is correct. Typical: 海藻サラダ, 海藻類, 海藻を食べる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"チキン南蛮の簡単でおいしいレシピがあれば教えてください。","romaji":"Chikin nanban no kantan de oishii reshipi ga areba oshiete kudasai.","german":"If you have an easy and delicious recipe for chicken nanban, please tell me.","furigana":"なんばん|かんたん|おし"},{"japanese":"16世紀、ポルトガル人との南蛮貿易が始まった。","romaji":"Jūroku-seiki, Porutogarujin to no nanban bōeki ga hajimatta.","german":"In the 16th century, nanban trade with the Portuguese began.","furigana":"せいき|じん|なんばんぼうえき|はじ"},{"japanese":"そば屋で鴨南蛮を注文した。","romaji":"Sobaya de kamo nanban o chūmon shita.","german":"At the soba restaurant, I ordered duck nanban.","furigana":"や||ちゅうもん"}],"notes":"南蛮 was originally a derogatory Chinese term for \"barbarians of the south\"; in Japan from the 16th century it referred to the Portuguese and Spanish who came via Southeast Asia: 南蛮人, 南蛮貿易, 南蛮文化. Today it is encountered mainly in cooking: dishes with chili and leek or in a spicy vinegar marinade are called 〜南蛮 (鴨南蛮, カレー南蛮, チキン南蛮, 南蛮漬け); regionally 南蛮 also simply means \"chili\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あいつらはそこで蛮行を繰り返しているんだ。","romaji":"Aitsura wa soko de bankō o kurikaeshite iru n da.","german":"Those guys keep committing atrocities there.","furigana":"ばんこう|く|かえ"},{"japanese":"文化財を破壊するという蛮行に、世界中から非難の声が上がった。","romaji":"Bunkazai o hakai suru to iu bankō ni, sekaijū kara hinan no koe ga agatta.","german":"The barbaric destruction of cultural property drew condemnation from around the world.","furigana":"ぶんかざい|はかい|ばんこう|せかいじゅう|ひなん|こえ|あ"},{"japanese":"酔った観光客の蛮行が、地元住民を悩ませている。","romaji":"Yotta kankōkyaku no bankō ga, jimoto jūmin o nayamasete iru.","german":"The rowdy behavior of drunken tourists is troubling local residents.","furigana":"よ|かんこうきゃく|ばんこう|じもとじゅうみん|なや"}],"notes":"蛮行 is a barbaric, brutal act; the word appears mainly in news and commentary: 蛮行を働く／繰り返す, 〜という蛮行に非難が集まる. The matching adjective is 野蛮 \"barbaric\". In contrast to 暴行 \"act of violence, assault\" (legal), 蛮行 emphasizes moral condemnation; serious atrocities are also called 残虐行為.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"年賀状には謹賀新年と書いてありました。","romaji":"Nengajō ni wa kinga shinnen to kaite arimashita.","german":"The New Year's card said \"Happy New Year\".","furigana":"ねんがじょう|きんがしんねん|か"},{"japanese":"謹賀新年。旧年中は大変お世話になりました。","romaji":"Kinga shinnen. Kyūnenchū wa taihen o-sewa ni narimashita.","german":"Happy New Year. Thank you very much for all your help over the past year.","furigana":"きんがしんねん|きゅうねんちゅう|たいへん|せわ"},{"japanese":"目上の人への年賀状には、「謹賀新年」のような四字の賀詞が適している。","romaji":"Meue no hito e no nengajō ni wa, \"kinga shinnen\" no yō na yoji no gashi ga tekishite iru.","german":"For New Year's cards to superiors, four-character greetings like 「謹賀新年」 are suitable.","furigana":"めうえ|ひと|ねんがじょう|きんがしんねん|よじ|がし|てき"}],"notes":"謹賀新年 is a written New Year formula for New Year's cards (年賀状) and notices, literally \"respectfully congratulate on the new year\"; it is not spoken, and verbally one says あけましておめでとうございます. As a four-character formula, it is considered polite even toward superiors, while short formulas like 賀正 or 迎春 are used more among equals. Doubling up, such as 謹賀新年 next to あけましておめでとう on the same card, is considered stylistically unattractive.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"父は弟を容赦なく殴りつけました。","romaji":"Chichi wa otōto o yōsha naku naguritsukemashita.","german":"Father beat my younger brother mercilessly.","furigana":"ちち|おとうと|ようしゃ|なぐ"},{"japanese":"あの先生は、生徒の間違いを容赦なく指摘する。","romaji":"Ano sensei wa, seito no machigai o yōsha naku shiteki suru.","german":"That teacher points out students' mistakes without mercy.","furigana":"せんせい|せいと|まちが|ようしゃ|してき"},{"japanese":"真夏の日差しが容赦なく照りつける。","romaji":"Manatsu no hizashi ga yōsha naku teritsukeru.","german":"The midsummer sun beats down mercilessly.","furigana":"まなつ|ひざ|ようしゃ|て"}],"notes":"容赦なく is the adverbial form of 容赦ない \"merciless, ruthless\"; 容赦 means \"leniency, pardon\" (cf. the polite ご容赦ください \"please be lenient\"). It describes people (容赦なく批判する) as well as forces of nature or time (日差しが容赦なく照りつける). Intensified: 情け容赦なく. Do not confuse it with 遠慮なく \"without reserve, freely\", which is meant positively.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"社長の逝去は本当に大きなショックです。","romaji":"Shachō no seikyo wa hontōni ōkina shokku desu.","german":"The president's passing is a really big shock.","furigana":"しゃちょう|せいきょ|ほんとう|おお"},{"japanese":"ご尊父様のご逝去を悼み、心からお悔やみ申し上げます。","romaji":"Go-sonpu-sama no go-seikyo o itami, kokoro kara o-kuyami mōshiagemasu.","german":"In mourning the passing of your esteemed father, I offer my heartfelt condolences.","furigana":"そんぷさま|せいきょ|いた|こころ|く|もう|あ"},{"japanese":"元首相が昨夜、八十九歳で逝去された。","romaji":"Moto shushō ga sakuya, hachijūkyū-sai de seikyo sareta.","german":"The former prime minister passed away last night at the age of eighty-nine.","furigana":"もとしゅしょう|さくや|はちじゅうきゅうさい|せいきょ"}],"notes":"逝去 is a respectful (尊敬語) expression for the death of another, usually esteemed person: ご逝去, 逝去される. It appears in letters of condolence and obituaries, never for members of one's own family or company when speaking to outsiders; for those one uses 死去 or 他界. Neutral in the news is 死去, and in everyday speech 亡くなる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あんな醜態を演じたのに、よく平気な顔で会社に行けるね。","romaji":"Anna shūtai o enjita noni, yoku heiki na kao de kaisha ni ikeru ne.","german":"After making such a spectacle of yourself, how can you go to work with such a calm face?","furigana":"しゅうたい|えん|へいき|かお|かいしゃ|い"},{"japanese":"酒に酔って、上司の前で醜態をさらしてしまった。","romaji":"Sake ni yotte, jōshi no mae de shūtai o sarashite shimatta.","german":"I got drunk and made a fool of myself in front of my boss.","furigana":"さけ|よ|じょうし|まえ|しゅうたい"},{"japanese":"試合終盤のミスの連続は、優勝候補とは思えない醜態だった。","romaji":"Shiai shūban no misu no renzoku wa, yūshō kōho to wa omoenai shūtai datta.","german":"The string of errors late in the match was a disgrace unworthy of a title favorite.","furigana":"しあいしゅうばん|れんぞく|ゆうしょうこうほ|おも|しゅうたい"}],"notes":"醜態 is shameful, embarrassing behavior that others witness: 醜態を演じる and 醜態をさらす \"to make a fool of oneself\". It is often about drunkenness, outbursts of anger or a disastrous failure in public. Similar but different is 失態 \"blunder, failure (at a task)\"; 醜態 emphasizes the undignified picture one presents.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"誰がこんな根も葉もない醜聞を流したんだ。","romaji":"Dare ga konna ne mo ha mo nai shūbun o nagashita n da.","german":"Who spread such a baseless scandal?","furigana":"だれ|ね|は|しゅうぶん|なが"},{"japanese":"大臣の醜聞が週刊誌に報じられた。","romaji":"Daijin no shūbun ga shūkanshi ni hōjirareta.","german":"A scandal involving the minister was reported in a weekly magazine.","furigana":"だいじん|しゅうぶん|しゅうかんし|ほう"},{"japanese":"醜聞が広まり、彼は会長を辞任せざるを得なかった。","romaji":"Shūbun ga hiromari, kare wa kaichō o jinin sezaru o enakatta.","german":"As the scandal spread, he had no choice but to resign as chairman.","furigana":"しゅうぶん|ひろ|かれ|かいちょう|じにん|え"}],"notes":"醜聞 is a written-style word for \"scandal, scandalous news\", literally \"ugly report\". In everyday speech and in the media one usually says スキャンダル; 醜聞 seems elevated or journalistic. Typical: 醜聞が広まる, 醜聞を流す (\"to spread\"), 政界の醜聞. For misconduct in companies or government offices, 不祥事 is the usual word.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"叩頭は、日本の土下座に似た中国の礼法です。","romaji":"Kōtō wa, Nihon no dogeza ni nita Chūgoku no reihō desu.","german":"The kowtow is a Chinese form of obeisance similar to Japan's dogeza.","furigana":"こうとう|にほん|どげざ|に|ちゅうごく|れいほう"},{"japanese":"使節は皇帝の前で叩頭した。","romaji":"Shisetsu wa kōtei no mae de kōtō shita.","german":"The envoy kowtowed before the emperor.","furigana":"しせつ|こうてい|まえ|こうとう"},{"japanese":"彼は叩頭して許しを請うた。","romaji":"Kare wa kōtō shite yurushi o kōta.","german":"He prostrated himself and begged for forgiveness.","furigana":"かれ|こうとう|ゆる|こ"}],"notes":"叩頭 is the kowtow: kneeling and bowing so low that the forehead touches the ground, originally a gesture of homage at the Chinese imperial court (三跪九叩頭). As an intransitive する-verb: 皇帝の前で叩頭する, 叩頭して許しを請う. The word is written-historical; the Japanese counterpart, bowing on the knees with the head to the floor, is called 土下座, and an ordinary bow お辞儀.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"領袖の言うことは絶対だ。","romaji":"Ryōshū no iu koto wa zettai da.","german":"What the leader says is absolute.","furigana":"りょうしゅう|い|ぜったい"},{"japanese":"党内の各派閥の領袖が集まり、次の総裁候補について話し合った。","romaji":"Tōnai no kaku habatsu no ryōshū ga atsumari, tsugi no sōsai kōho ni tsuite hanashiatta.","german":"The heads of the party's factions gathered and discussed the next candidate for party president.","furigana":"とうない|かくはばつ|りょうしゅう|あつ|つぎ|そうさいこうほ|はな|あ"},{"japanese":"彼は若くして財界の領袖と目されるようになった。","romaji":"Kare wa wakakushite zaikai no ryōshū to mokusareru yō ni natta.","german":"He came to be regarded as a leader of the business world at a young age.","furigana":"かれ|わか|ざいかい|りょうしゅう|もく"}],"notes":"領袖 refers to the leader of a group, today almost only in politics: 派閥の領袖 \"head of a party faction\", 党の領袖. Literally, 領 \"collar\" and 袖 \"sleeve\" are the most conspicuous parts of a garment, hence the meaning \"outstanding person\". The word is written-style; in general one says リーダー or 指導者, and the party chairman is called 党首.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"冥王星の軌道は細長い楕円形で、太陽を一周するのに約２５０年かかります。","romaji":"Meiōsei no kidō wa hosonagai daenkei de, taiyō o isshū suru no ni yaku nihyakugojūnen kakarimasu.","german":"Pluto's orbit is an elongated ellipse, and it takes about 250 years to go around the Sun once.","furigana":"めいおうせい|きどう|ほそなが|だえんけい|たいよう|いっしゅう|やく|ねん"},{"japanese":"冥王星は２００６年に惑星から準惑星に分類し直された。","romaji":"Meiōsei wa nisenrokunen ni wakusei kara junwakusei ni bunrui shinaosareta.","german":"In 2006, Pluto was reclassified from a planet to a dwarf planet.","furigana":"めいおうせい|ねん|わくせい|じゅんわくせい|ぶんるい|なお"},{"japanese":"探査機が初めて冥王星に接近し、その表面を撮影した。","romaji":"Tansaki ga hajimete meiōsei ni sekkin shi, sono hyōmen o satsuei shita.","german":"A space probe approached Pluto for the first time and photographed its surface.","furigana":"たんさき|はじ|めいおうせい|せっきん|ひょうめん|さつえい"}],"notes":"冥王星 is Pluto; the name literally means \"star of the king of the underworld\" (冥王), corresponding to the Roman god Pluto. Since 2006 it is no longer regarded as a planet (惑星) but as a dwarf planet (準惑星); one says, for example, 冥王星が惑星から外された. The planet names follow the same pattern with 星: 水星, 金星, 火星, 木星, 土星, 天王星, 海王星.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"冥界に入れるのは死者の魂だけだ。","romaji":"Meikai ni ireru no wa shisha no tamashii dake da.","german":"Only the souls of the dead can enter the underworld.","furigana":"めいかい|い|ししゃ|たましい"},{"japanese":"ギリシャ神話では、ハデスが冥界を支配している。","romaji":"Girisha shinwa de wa, Hadesu ga meikai o shihai shite iru.","german":"In Greek mythology, Hades rules the underworld.","furigana":"しんわ|めいかい|しはい"},{"japanese":"物語の主人公は、亡き妻を連れ戻すために冥界へ下った。","romaji":"Monogatari no shujinkō wa, naki tsuma o tsuremodosu tame ni meikai e kudatta.","german":"The hero of the story descended into the underworld to bring back his late wife.","furigana":"ものがたり|しゅじんこう|な|つま|つ|もど|めいかい|くだ"}],"notes":"冥界 is the world of the dead, the underworld; 冥 means \"dark, of the other world\". The word is written-style and appears in mythology, fantasy and religion: 冥界の王, 冥界へ下る. Colloquially one says あの世; in Japanese myth the underworld is called 黄泉; 地獄, in contrast, is hell as a place of punishment. The same character appears in ご冥福をお祈りします \"May he/she rest in peace\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どんなにお金を稼いでも、冥土には持っていけないんだよ。","romaji":"Donna ni okane o kaseide mo, meido ni wa motte ikenai n da yo.","german":"No matter how much money you earn, you can't take it with you to the next world.","furigana":"かね|かせ|めいど|も"},{"japanese":"孫の花嫁姿が見られて、いい冥土の土産ができた。","romaji":"Mago no hanayome sugata ga mirarete, ii meido no miyage ga dekita.","german":"Seeing my granddaughter in her wedding dress gave me a fine memory to take to the grave.","furigana":"まご|はなよめすがた|み|めいど|みやげ"},{"japanese":"昔の人は、死者は川を渡って冥土へ行くと信じていた。","romaji":"Mukashi no hito wa, shisha wa kawa o watatte meido e iku to shinjite ita.","german":"People in the past believed that the dead crossed a river and went to the netherworld.","furigana":"むかし|ひと|ししゃ|かわ|わた|めいど|い|しん"}],"notes":"冥土 (also written 冥途) is a Buddhist-tinged, elevated word for the \"realm of the dead\" to which the soul travels after death; one goes 冥土へ or arrives in 冥土. A very common phrase is 冥土の土産, a last fine experience that one \"takes to the grave\", often said jokingly by older people. In everyday speech one rather says あの世; 地獄 and 天国, in contrast, denote hell and heaven as places of punishment and reward respectively.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの日、ここで酒を酌み交わしたこと、覚えているか。","romaji":"Ano hi, koko de sake o kumikawashita koto, oboete iru ka.","german":"Do you remember drinking sake together here that day?","furigana":"ひ|さけ|く|か|おぼ"},{"japanese":"久しぶりに会った友人と酒を酌み交わしながら、昔話に花を咲かせた。","romaji":"Hisashiburi ni atta yuujin to sake o kumikawashinagara, mukashibanashi ni hana o sakaseta.","german":"Drinking sake with a friend I hadn't seen in a long time, we chatted happily about old times.","furigana":"ひさ|あ|ゆうじん|さけ|く|か|むかしばなし|はな|さ"},{"japanese":"交渉がまとまった後、両社の社長は杯を酌み交わした。","romaji":"Koushou ga matomatta ato, ryousha no shachou wa sakazuki o kumikawashita.","german":"After the negotiations were concluded, the presidents of the two companies exchanged cups.","furigana":"こうしょう|あと|りょうしゃ|しゃちょう|さかずき|く|か"}],"notes":"酌み交わす is composed of 酌む \"to pour\" and 交わす \"to exchange\": people pour for each other and drink together. Typical are 酒を酌み交わす and 杯を酌み交わす, usually with 〜と for the drinking partner. Unlike the neutral 一緒に飲む, the word emphasizes familiarity, friendship or reconciliation and sounds elevated to literary. The background is the custom of not pouring for oneself but for the other person (お酌をする).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これは、彼が恋人のために詠った愛の詩です。","romaji":"Kore wa, kare ga koibito no tame ni utatta ai no shi desu.","german":"This is a love poem he wrote for his girlfriend.","furigana":"かれ|こいびと|うた|あい|し"},{"japanese":"古くから多くの歌人が富士山の美しさを詠ってきた。","romaji":"Furuku kara ooku no kajin ga Fujisan no utsukushisa o utatte kita.","german":"Since ancient times, many poets have sung of the beauty of Mount Fuji.","furigana":"ふる|おお|かじん|ふじさん|うつく|うた"},{"japanese":"この詩は、故郷を離れた悲しみを詠っている。","romaji":"Kono shi wa, kokyou o hanareta kanashimi o utatte iru.","german":"This poem expresses the sorrow of having left one's hometown.","furigana":"し|こきょう|はな|かな|うた"}],"notes":"詠う is one of the spellings of うたう and is used for celebrating a theme in a poem: 恋を詠う, 自然の美しさを詠う. For ordinary singing one writes 歌う, for \"to extol, to stipulate explicitly\" (e.g. in a law) 謳う. Not to be confused with 詠む (よむ), which refers to composing a specific poem: 俳句を詠む, 短歌を詠む. 詠う is literary and appears mainly in texts about literature.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5 (source: N5; change limited to two levels)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これは、山田某の話である。","romaji":"Kore wa, Yamada nanigashi no hanashi de aru.","german":"This is the story of a certain Yamada.","furigana":"やまだなにがし|はなし"},{"japanese":"昨日、鈴木某という男から電話があった。","romaji":"Kinou, Suzuki nanigashi to iu otoko kara denwa ga atta.","german":"Yesterday I got a call from a man named Suzuki or something.","furigana":"きのう|すずきなにがし|おとこ|でんわ"},{"japanese":"手伝ってくれた学生には、なにがしかの謝礼を払うつもりだ。","romaji":"Tetsudatte kureta gakusei ni wa, nanigashika no sharei o harau tsumori da.","german":"I intend to pay the students who helped some token of thanks.","furigana":"てつだ|がくせい|しゃれい|はら"}],"notes":"某 read as なにがし follows a family name and means \"a certain ...\" when one does not know or does not want to give the full name: 山田某. The word sounds old-fashioned to literary. In なにがしか(の) \"a certain amount, some\" it is almost always written in kana: なにがしかのお礼. As a prefix, in contrast, 某 is read ぼう (某国, 某所, 某氏); in historical dramas, なにがし can also mean \"I\" humbly.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本に留学生を多く送りこんでいる某国で教えています。","romaji":"Nippon ni ryūgakusei o ōku okurikonde iru bōkoku de oshiete imasu.","german":"I teach in a certain country that sends many international students to Japan.","furigana":"にっぽん|りゅうがくせい|おお|おく|ぼうこく|おし"},{"japanese":"事件には某国の情報機関が関与した疑いがある。","romaji":"Jiken ni wa boukoku no jouhou kikan ga kan'yo shita utagai ga aru.","german":"There is a suspicion that the intelligence agency of a certain country was involved in the case.","furigana":"じけん|ぼうこく|じょうほうきかん|かんよ|うたが"},{"japanese":"番組では、彼は「某国の大統領」とだけ紹介された。","romaji":"Bangumi de wa, kare wa boukoku no daitouryou to dake shoukai sareta.","german":"On the program, he was introduced only as \"the president of a certain country\".","furigana":"ばんぐみ|かれ|ぼうこく|だいとうりょう|しょうかい"}],"notes":"某国 refers to a country whose name one deliberately does not mention, for example out of diplomatic consideration, to protect sources or because it is obvious to the reader anyway. The word is typical of news, reports and insinuations: 某国の情報機関, 某国の大統領. In fictional stories, where a country simply remains unspecified, the neutral ある国 is more usual. Formed in the same way are 某所, 某氏, 某社 and 某日.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"栃木県の某所で行われたイベントに行ってきました。","romaji":"Tochigiken no bousho de okonawareta ibento ni itte kimashita.","german":"I went to an event held at a certain place in Tochigi Prefecture.","furigana":"とちぎけん|ぼうしょ|おこな|い"},{"japanese":"犯人は都内某所に潜伏しているとみられる。","romaji":"Hannin wa tonai bousho ni senpuku shite iru to mirareru.","german":"The culprit is believed to be hiding somewhere in Tokyo.","furigana":"はんにん|とないぼうしょ|せんぷく"},{"japanese":"新作映画の撮影は某所で極秘に進められている。","romaji":"Shinsaku eiga no satsuei wa bousho de gokuhi ni susumerarete iru.","german":"Filming of the new movie is proceeding in strict secrecy at an undisclosed location.","furigana":"しんさくえいが|さつえい|ぼうしょ|ごくひ|すす"}],"notes":"某所 is a \"certain place\" whose exact location is kept secret. Typical are phrases like 都内某所 \"somewhere in Tokyo\" or 県内某所, often in news, secret film shoots or events. On social media, 某所 is also used with a wink when everyone guesses which place is meant. Without any intent to conceal, one simply says ある場所 or どこか.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"審査員の某氏に会いました。","romaji":"Shinsain no bōshi ni aimashita.","german":"I met one of the judges.","furigana":"しんさいん|ぼうし|あ"},{"japanese":"関係者の某氏によると、計画はすでに中止されたという。","romaji":"Kankeisha no boushi ni yoru to, keikaku wa sude ni chuushi sareta to iu.","german":"According to a person connected with the matter, the plan has already been cancelled.","furigana":"かんけいしゃ|ぼうし|けいかく|ちゅうし"},{"japanese":"記事では実名を避けて、「A社の某氏」と書かれていた。","romaji":"Kiji de wa jitsumei o sakete, A-sha no boushi to kakarete ita.","german":"The article avoided the real name and wrote only \"a person from Company A\".","furigana":"きじ|じつめい|さ|しゃ|ぼうし|か"}],"notes":"某氏 means \"Mr./Ms. X\": a specific person whose name one deliberately does not give. Because of 氏 it sounds polite and matter-of-fact; it is typical of newspaper articles, reports and anonymized sources: 関係者の某氏. If the person is truly unknown, such as a perpetrator, one says 何者か; neutral for \"someone\" is ある人. Pronounced the same but unrelated is 帽子 \"hat\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"一連 almost always appears as 一連の before a noun and groups things into a \"connected series\": 一連の事件, 一連の流れ, 一連の騒動, 一連の作業. In contrast to いくつかの, it emphasizes the internal connection or fixed sequence. It is especially common in news when several incidents are presented as belonging together.","examples":[{"japanese":"一連の問題について調査しています。","romaji":"Ichiren no mondai ni tsuite chōsa shite imasu.","german":"We are investigating a series of related problems.","furigana":"いちれん|もんだい|ちょうさ"},{"japanese":"一連の作業を自動化しました。","romaji":"Ichiren no sagyō o jidōka shimashita.","german":"We automated the whole sequence of work.","furigana":"いちれん|さぎょう|じどうか"},{"japanese":"一連の事件は同じ人物の犯行とみられている。","romaji":"Ichiren no jiken wa onaji jinbutsu no hankou to mirarete iru.","german":"The whole series of incidents is believed to be the work of the same person.","furigana":"いちれん|じけん|おな|じんぶつ|はんこう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"一律 means \"the same for everyone, across the board, without distinction\". It is used adverbially as 一律に (一律に値上げする), attributively as 一律の (一律の基準) or directly before amounts: 一律千円. Criticism is often implied when individual differences are ignored: 一律に禁止するのはおかしい. Related are 均一 (uniform price, e.g. 百円均一) and 一様 (uniform in appearance or condition).","examples":[{"japanese":"参加費は一律千円です。","romaji":"Sankahi wa ichiritsu sen en desu.","german":"The participation fee is a flat 1,000 yen.","furigana":"さんかひ|いちりつせんえん"},{"japanese":"すべての学校に一律の基準を適用しました。","romaji":"Subete no gakkō ni ichiritsu no kijun o tekiyō shimashita.","german":"We applied a uniform standard to all schools.","furigana":"がっこう|いちりつ|きじゅん|てきよう"},{"japanese":"会社は全社員の給料を一律に三パーセント引き上げた。","romaji":"Kaisha wa zen shain no kyuuryou o ichiritsu ni san paasento hikiageta.","german":"The company raised all employees' salaries uniformly by three percent.","furigana":"かいしゃ|ぜんしゃいん|きゅうりょう|いちりつ|さん|ひ|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"一切 has two uses: as an adverb with a negative it means \"not at all, none whatsoever\" and is stronger and more formal than 全然 - typical on signs and in statements: 一切お断り, 一切関係ない. As a noun it means \"everything, all\": 財産の一切, 一切を任せる; intensified also 一切合切. Do not confuse it with 一切れ (ひときれ) \"one piece\".","examples":[{"japanese":"追加料金は一切かかりません。","romaji":"Tsuika ryōkin wa issai kakarimasen.","german":"There are no additional charges whatsoever.","furigana":"ついかりょうきん|いっさい"},{"japanese":"その件には一切関わっていません。","romaji":"Sono ken ni wa issai kakawatte imasen.","german":"I have had nothing whatsoever to do with that matter.","furigana":"けん|いっさいかか"},{"japanese":"父は家のことの一切を母に任せていた。","romaji":"Chichi wa ie no koto no issai o haha ni makasete ita.","german":"My father left everything about the household to my mother.","furigana":"ちち|いえ|いっさい|はは|まか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"一部 (いちぶ) first of all means \"a part\": 計画の一部, 一部の人 \"some people\", 一部の地域 \"some areas\". As a counter for printed matter it means \"one copy\": 資料を一部, 二部 and so on. In contrast to 全部, it denotes only a portion; 一部分 is a somewhat more concrete \"section\". A well-known phrase: 一部始終 \"the whole course of events from beginning to end\".","examples":[{"japanese":"計画の一部を変更しました。","romaji":"Keikaku no ichibu o henkō shimashita.","german":"We changed part of the plan.","furigana":"けいかく|いちぶ|へんこう"},{"japanese":"資料を一部ください。","romaji":"Shiryō o ichibu kudasai.","german":"Please give me one copy of the materials.","furigana":"しりょう|いちぶ"},{"japanese":"台風の影響で、一部の地域では停電が続いている。","romaji":"Taifuu no eikyou de, ichibu no chiiki de wa teiden ga tsuzuite iru.","german":"Because of the typhoon, power outages continue in some areas.","furigana":"たいふう|えいきょう|いちぶ|ちいき|ていでん|つづ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"異論 is a \"dissenting opinion\" or an \"objection\". Typical combinations: 異論はない (no objections), 異論が出る, 異論を唱える (to object). It sounds matter-of-fact to formal and suits meetings and debates. 反論 is a concrete counterargument to a statement, and 異議 a formal objection (異議あり in meetings or in court).","examples":[{"japanese":"この提案に異論はありません。","romaji":"Kono teian ni iron wa arimasen.","german":"I have no objection to this proposal.","furigana":"ていあん|いろん"},{"japanese":"会議では異論にも耳を傾けました。","romaji":"Kaigi de wa iron ni mo mimi o katamukemashita.","german":"At the meeting, we also listened to dissenting opinions.","furigana":"かいぎ|いろん|みみ|かたむ"},{"japanese":"彼が優秀な選手であることには、誰も異論がないだろう。","romaji":"Kare ga yuushuu na senshu de aru koto ni wa, dare mo iron ga nai darou.","german":"No one is likely to dispute that he is an excellent player.","furigana":"かれ|ゆうしゅう|せんしゅ|だれ|いろん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"内訳 is the \"breakdown\" of a total sum or total number into its items: 費用の内訳, 売上の内訳, 内訳は次の通りです. It is read with the kun reading うちわけ. 明細, in contrast, is the detailed itemized list as a document (給与明細 \"payslip\"); 内訳 emphasizes more what the whole is made up of, and is also used for groups of people.","examples":[{"japanese":"費用の内訳を教えてください。","romaji":"Hiyō no uchiwake o oshiete kudasai.","german":"Please give me a breakdown of the costs.","furigana":"ひよう|うちわけ|おし"},{"japanese":"参加者の内訳を表にまとめました。","romaji":"Sankasha no uchiwake o hyō ni matomemashita.","german":"I summarized the breakdown of participants in a table.","furigana":"さんかしゃ|うちわけ|ひょう"},{"japanese":"請求書に内訳が書いていなかったので、問い合わせた。","romaji":"Seikyuusho ni uchiwake ga kaite inakatta node, toiawaseta.","german":"The invoice didn't have a breakdown written on it, so I inquired.","furigana":"せいきゅうしょ|うちわけ|か|と|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"後回し means \"to put off until later, to put at the back of the line\" and almost always appears as 後回しにする (active) or 後回しになる (it turns out that way). It concerns the order of tasks, not a new date. For the official postponement of an appointment one says 延期, and for putting off unpleasant problems, especially in politics, 先送り.","examples":[{"japanese":"重要な問題を後回しにしてはいけません。","romaji":"Jūyō na mondai o atomawashi ni shite wa ikemasen.","german":"You must not put off important problems.","furigana":"じゅうよう|もんだい|あとまわ"},{"japanese":"片付けは後回しにして、まず食事にしました。","romaji":"Katazuke wa atomawashi ni shite, mazu shokuji ni shimashita.","german":"We put off the tidying up and had a meal first.","furigana":"かたづ|あとまわ|しょくじ"},{"japanese":"忙しいと、つい自分の健康を後回しにしてしまう。","romaji":"Isogashii to, tsui jibun no kenkou o atomawashi ni shite shimau.","german":"When I'm busy, I easily end up putting my own health last.","furigana":"いそが|じぶん|けんこう|あとまわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"合間 is the short \"interval\" between two phases of an activity or event: 仕事の合間に, 雨の合間, CMの合間. Very idiomatic is 合間を縫って \"to squeeze in time in between\". In contrast to 休憩 (official break), 合間 emphasizes the gap that one uses on the side; for spatial gaps one mostly uses 隙間.","examples":[{"japanese":"仕事の合間に単語を復習します。","romaji":"Shigoto no aima ni tango o fukushū shimasu.","german":"I review vocabulary in between work.","furigana":"しごと|あいま|たんご|ふくしゅう"},{"japanese":"雨の合間を見て買い物に出ました。","romaji":"Ame no aima o mite kaimono ni demashita.","german":"I went out shopping during a break in the rain.","furigana":"あめ|あいま|み|か|もの|で"},{"japanese":"子育ての合間を縫って、資格の勉強を続けています。","romaji":"Kosodate no aima o nutte, shikaku no benkyou o tsuzukete imasu.","german":"Squeezing it in between childcare, I keep studying for a qualification.","furigana":"こそだ|あいま|ぬ|しかく|べんきょう|つづ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"過ち is a \"mistake\" with weight: a misstep with consequences or a moral dimension. Typical are 過ちを犯す, 過ちを認める, 過ちを繰り返す. For everyday slips one says 間違い or ミス, and for factual errors in texts and judgments 誤り. 過ち sounds serious and rather written-style, for example in confessions or historical retrospectives.","examples":[{"japanese":"過ちを認めて謝りました。","romaji":"Ayamachi o mitomete ayamarimashita.","german":"I admitted my mistake and apologized.","furigana":"あやま|みと|あやま"},{"japanese":"同じ過ちを繰り返さないようにします。","romaji":"Onaji ayamachi o kurikaesanai yō ni shimasu.","german":"I will make sure not to repeat the same mistake.","furigana":"おな|あやま|く|かえ"},{"japanese":"若い頃、私は大きな過ちを犯した。","romaji":"Wakai koro, watashi wa ookina ayamachi o okashita.","german":"In my youth, I made a grave mistake.","furigana":"わか|ころ|わたし|おお|あやま|おか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"案の定 means \"as expected, sure enough\" and confirms a supposition, mostly a fear that then actually comes true. It stands as an adverb at the beginning of a sentence or before the predicate, often with a comma. 予想通り is more neutral and can also introduce something positive; やっぱり is more colloquial and broader. Literally: 案 \"supposition\" + 定 \"certainty\".","examples":[{"japanese":"傘を忘れた日に、案の定雨が降りました。","romaji":"Kasa o wasureta hi ni, annojō ame ga furimashita.","german":"On the day I forgot my umbrella, it rained, sure enough.","furigana":"かさ|わす|ひ|あん|じょうあめ|ふ"},{"japanese":"人気の店は案の定満席でした。","romaji":"Ninki no mise wa annojō manseki deshita.","german":"The popular restaurant was full, as expected.","furigana":"にんき|みせ|あん|じょうまんせき"},{"japanese":"全然勉強しなかったら、案の定、試験に落ちた。","romaji":"Zenzen benkyou shinakattara, annojou, shiken ni ochita.","german":"I didn't study at all, and, sure enough, I failed the exam.","furigana":"ぜんぜんべんきょう|あん|じょう|しけん|お"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"育成 is the systematic \"bringing up, fostering\" of people or abilities and is a transitive する-verb: 人材を育成する, 後継者の育成. It is also used for raising plants and animals (稚魚の育成) and is known from 育成ゲーム. 養成 denotes targeted vocational training (教員養成), and 育てる is the everyday \"to raise\"; 育成 sounds official or like companies and administration.","examples":[{"japanese":"若い人材の育成に力を入れています。","romaji":"Wakai jinzai no ikusei ni chikara o irete imasu.","german":"We are putting effort into developing young talent.","furigana":"わか|じんざい|いくせい|ちから|い"},{"japanese":"地域全体で子供を育成する仕組みを作ります。","romaji":"Chiiki zentai de kodomo o ikusei suru shikumi o tsukurimasu.","german":"We will create a system in which the whole community nurtures children.","furigana":"ちいきぜんたい|こども|いくせい|しく|つく"},{"japanese":"この施設では、絶滅が心配される魚を育成している。","romaji":"Kono shisetsu de wa, zetsumetsu ga shinpai sareru sakana o ikusei shite iru.","german":"At this facility, fish that are feared to be going extinct are being raised.","furigana":"しせつ|ぜつめつ|しんぱい|さかな|いくせい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"受け止める literally means \"to catch something coming at you\" (ボールを受け止める) and figuratively \"to take in, to take seriously, to process\": 批判を真剣に受け止める, 現実を受け止める. The passive 受け止められる describes how something is received: 好意的に受け止められた. 受け入れる, in contrast, means \"to accept, to agree to\", and 受け取る \"to receive\" or \"to interpret\". There is no intransitive counterpart.","examples":[{"japanese":"客の批判を真剣に受け止めました。","romaji":"Kyaku no hihan o shinken ni uketomemashita.","german":"We took the customers' criticism seriously.","furigana":"きゃく|ひはん|しんけん|う|と"},{"japanese":"飛んできたボールを両手で受け止めました。","romaji":"Tonde kita bōru o ryōte de uketomemashita.","german":"I caught the incoming ball with both hands.","furigana":"と|りょうて|う|と"},{"japanese":"突然の別れを、まだ受け止められずにいる。","romaji":"Totsuzen no wakare o, mada uketomerarezu ni iru.","german":"I still can't come to terms with the sudden parting.","furigana":"とつぜん|わか|う|と"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"受け付ける means to officially \"accept\" applications, orders or inquiries: 申し込みを受け付ける, 予約は電話で受け付けております. The noun 受付 refers to the acceptance and the reception desk. In the negative it also means \"cannot tolerate, will not admit\": 体が食べ物を受け付けない, 人の意見を受け付けない. 受け取る, in contrast, is simply receiving an object.","examples":[{"japanese":"申し込みは月末まで受け付けます。","romaji":"Mōshikomi wa getsumatsu made uketsukemasu.","german":"We accept applications until the end of the month.","furigana":"もう|こ|げつまつ|う|つ"},{"japanese":"電話でも質問を受け付けています。","romaji":"Denwa de mo shitsumon o uketsukete imasu.","german":"We also accept questions by phone.","furigana":"でんわ|しつもん|う|つ"},{"japanese":"熱があって、体が食べ物を受け付けない。","romaji":"Netsu ga atte, karada ga tabemono o uketsukenai.","german":"I have a fever, and my body won't keep food down.","furigana":"ねつ|からだ|た|もの|う|つ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"打ち切る means to \"break off\" or \"discontinue\" something in progress, either early or for good: 議論を打ち切る, 交渉を打ち切る, 支援を打ち切る. The passive and the noun 打ち切り are very common, for example when a series is taken off the air: 番組が打ち切りになる. 中止 means more \"to call off\" (often before something begins), and 中断 means \"to interrupt\" with a possible resumption.","examples":[{"japanese":"時間切れで議論を打ち切りました。","romaji":"Jikangire de giron o uchikirimashita.","german":"We cut off the discussion because time ran out.","furigana":"じかんぎ|ぎろん|う|き"},{"japanese":"会社はその商品の生産を打ち切りました。","romaji":"Kaisha wa sono shōhin no seisan o uchikirimashita.","german":"The company discontinued production of that product.","furigana":"かいしゃ|しょうひん|せいさん|う|き"},{"japanese":"視聴率が低く、そのドラマは三か月で打ち切られた。","romaji":"Shichouritsu ga hikuku, sono dorama wa sankagetsu de uchikirareta.","german":"Because of low ratings, the drama was cancelled after three months.","furigana":"しちょうりつ|ひく|さん|げつ|う|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"会見 is an official meeting or a press conference. The most common form is 記者会見 (often shortened to 会見): 会見を開く, 会見に臨む. As an intransitive する-verb it refers to a formal meeting of high-ranking people: 首相と会見する. 面会 is a visit (hospital, prison), 面接 is a job interview, and インタビュー is an interview with individuals.","examples":[{"japanese":"市長は記者会見で方針を説明しました。","romaji":"Shichō wa kisha kaiken de hōshin o setsumei shimashita.","german":"The mayor explained his policy at a press conference.","furigana":"しちょう|きしゃかいけん|ほうしん|せつめい"},{"japanese":"会見の内容が新聞に載りました。","romaji":"Kaiken no naiyō ga shinbun ni norimashita.","german":"The content of the press conference appeared in the newspaper.","furigana":"かいけん|ないよう|しんぶん|の"},{"japanese":"首相は来日中のアメリカ大統領と会見した。","romaji":"Shushou wa rainichi-chuu no Amerika daitouryou to kaiken shita.","german":"The prime minister met with the US president, who was visiting Japan.","furigana":"しゅしょう|らいにちちゅう|だいとうりょう|かいけん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"開催 is the formal word for \"organizing, holding\" conferences, trade fairs, tournaments or festivals. As a transitive する-verb: 会議を開催する; the passive 開催される \"to take place\" is very common. Related words include 開催地 (venue), 開催国 and 開催中. In everyday speech people say the simpler 開く or 行う; 催す is literary.","examples":[{"japanese":"来月、国際会議を開催します。","romaji":"Raigetsu, kokusai kaigi o kaisai shimasu.","german":"Next month we will hold an international conference.","furigana":"らいげつ|こくさいかいぎ|かいさい"},{"japanese":"雨でも大会は開催されます。","romaji":"Ame de mo taikai wa kaisai saremasu.","german":"The tournament will be held even if it rains.","furigana":"あめ|たいかい|かいさい"},{"japanese":"次のオリンピックの開催地が発表された。","romaji":"Tsugi no Orinpikku no kaisaichi ga happyou sareta.","german":"The host city of the next Olympics was announced.","furigana":"つぎ|かいさいち|はっぴょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"確定 means \"to fix definitively\" or \"to be settled\" and, as a する-verb, is both transitive and intransitive: 日程を確定する / 日程が確定する. It is common in administration and online forms (確定ボタン, 注文確定) as well as in 確定申告 \"income tax return\". 決定 stresses the decision itself, 確定 stresses that nothing will change anymore; 確認 only means \"to check, to confirm\".","examples":[{"japanese":"旅行の日程が確定しました。","romaji":"Ryokō no nittei ga kakutei shimashita.","german":"The travel schedule has been finalized.","furigana":"りょこう|にってい|かくてい"},{"japanese":"金額が確定してから連絡します。","romaji":"Kingaku ga kakutei shite kara renraku shimasu.","german":"I will contact you once the amount is settled.","furigana":"きんがく|かくてい|れんらく"},{"japanese":"確定申告は毎年三月十五日までに行う必要がある。","romaji":"Kakutei shinkoku wa maitoshi sangatsu juugonichi made ni okonau hitsuyou ga aru.","german":"The tax return must be filed by March 15 every year.","furigana":"かくていしんこく|まいとしさんがつじゅうごにち|おこな|ひつよう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"家計 means the finances of a private household, that is, the family's income and expenses. Typical phrases: 家計が苦しい, 家計を支える, 家計を圧迫する, 家計を見直す; a household account book is 家計簿 (家計簿をつける). 生計 is \"livelihood\" (生計を立てる), and 予算 is a planned budget, also for companies or states.","examples":[{"japanese":"家計を見直して支出を減らしました。","romaji":"Kakei o minaoshite shishutsu o herashimashita.","german":"We reviewed our household budget and cut spending.","furigana":"かけい|みなお|ししゅつ|へ"},{"japanese":"物価の上昇が家計を圧迫しています。","romaji":"Bukka no jōshō ga kakei o appaku shite imasu.","german":"Rising prices are putting a strain on the household budget.","furigana":"ぶっか|じょうしょう|かけい|あっぱく"},{"japanese":"母は毎晩、家計簿をつけている。","romaji":"Haha wa maiban, kakeibo o tsukete iru.","german":"My mother keeps her household account book every evening.","furigana":"はは|まいばん|かけいぼ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"箇条書き is a presentation in individual points, that is, a bullet list instead of running text (文章). Typical: 箇条書きにする, 箇条書きで書く, 要点を箇条書きにまとめる. 箇条 means \"item, article\" - the same 条 as in legal articles (第一条). It is very common in office and school life, for example for minutes, presentation slides and application documents.","examples":[{"japanese":"要点を箇条書きにしてください。","romaji":"Yōten o kajōgaki ni shite kudasai.","german":"Please write the main points as a list.","furigana":"ようてん|かじょうが"},{"japanese":"箇条書きなら内容を確認しやすいです。","romaji":"Kajōgaki nara naiyō o kakunin shiyasui desu.","german":"A bullet-point list makes it easier to check the content.","furigana":"かじょうが|ないよう|かくにん"},{"japanese":"スライドの文章は、短い箇条書きにまとめましょう。","romaji":"Suraido no bunshou wa, mijikai kajougaki ni matomemashou.","german":"Let's summarize the text on the slides in short bullet points.","furigana":"ぶんしょう|みじか|かじょうが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"加入 is the formal word for \"joining, signing up for\" and an intransitive する-verb used with に: 保険に加入する, 組合に加入する, 年金に加入する, 条約に加入する. A person who has joined is a 加入者 (insured person, subscriber, member). For associations and clubs people prefer 入会 or 入部, for taking part in events 参加; states usually join organizations with 加盟 (国連に加盟する).","examples":[{"japanese":"旅行の前に保険に加入しました。","romaji":"Ryokō no mae ni hoken ni kanyū shimashita.","german":"I took out insurance before the trip.","furigana":"りょこう|まえ|ほけん|かにゅう"},{"japanese":"新しい会員が組合に加入しました。","romaji":"Atarashii kaiin ga kumiai ni kanyū shimashita.","german":"A new member joined the union.","furigana":"あたら|かいいん|くみあい|かにゅう"},{"japanese":"このサービスの加入者は百万人を超えた。","romaji":"Kono saabisu no kanyuusha wa hyakuman nin o koeta.","german":"This service now has over a million subscribers.","furigana":"かにゅうしゃ|ひゃくまんにん|こ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"肝心 refers to \"the crucial thing, what really matters\" - the core of a matter. It is used as a な-adjective (肝心なこと, 〜が肝心だ) and very often as 肝心の + noun: 肝心の本人がいない \"the main person, of all people, is missing\". Literally \"liver and heart\", i.e. vital organs; hence also the intensified form 肝心要. 大切 and 重要 are more general, while 肝心 often stresses that precisely the most important thing is missing or going wrong. Alternative spelling: 肝腎.","examples":[{"japanese":"肝心なことを聞き忘れました。","romaji":"Kanjin na koto o kikiwasuremashita.","german":"I forgot to ask about the most important thing.","furigana":"かんじん|き|わす"},{"japanese":"継続することが肝心です。","romaji":"Keizoku suru koto ga kanjin desu.","german":"What matters is to keep at it.","furigana":"けいぞく|かんじん"},{"japanese":"パーティーは始まったのに、肝心の主役がまだ来ていない。","romaji":"Paatii wa hajimatta noni, kanjin no shuyaku ga mada kite inai.","german":"The party has already started, but the guest of honor, of all people, hasn't arrived yet.","furigana":"はじ|かんじん|しゅやく|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"関与 is the factual \"involvement\" in a matter and an intransitive する-verb with に: 計画に関与する. It is often about entanglement in scandals, crimes or political decisions: 事件への関与を否定する. 参加 is active participation in an event, and 関わる is the everyday \"to have to do with something\"; 関与 sounds sober, legal or journalistic.","examples":[{"japanese":"多くの専門家が計画に関与しています。","romaji":"Ōku no senmonka ga keikaku ni kan'yo shite imasu.","german":"Many experts are involved in the plan.","furigana":"おお|せんもんか|けいかく|かんよ"},{"japanese":"彼はその決定には関与していません。","romaji":"Kare wa sono kettei ni wa kan'yo shite imasen.","german":"He was not involved in this decision.","furigana":"かれ|けってい|かんよ"},{"japanese":"警察は、元社員が事件に関与したとみて調べている。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa, moto shain ga jiken ni kan'yo shita to mite shirabete iru.","german":"The police believe a former employee was involved in the case and are investigating.","furigana":"けいさつ|もとしゃいん|じけん|かんよ|しら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"慣例 is a practice, often unwritten, that has arisen through repetition within an organization or at ceremonies. Typical: 慣例に従う, 慣例を破る, 慣例により, 〜のが慣例になっている. 習慣 is more a personal habit, 慣習 is a social custom, and 前例 is a precedent. 慣例 sounds formal and is common in administration, politics and corporate culture.","examples":[{"japanese":"慣例に従って式を進めました。","romaji":"Kanrei ni shitagatte shiki o susumemashita.","german":"We carried out the ceremony according to the usual customs.","furigana":"かんれい|したが|しき|すす"},{"japanese":"古い慣例を見直す必要があります。","romaji":"Furui kanrei o minaosu hitsuyō ga arimasu.","german":"Old conventions need to be reconsidered.","furigana":"ふる|かんれい|みなお|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"この会社では、新入社員が忘年会で挨拶をするのが慣例になっている。","romaji":"Kono kaisha de wa, shinnyuu shain ga bounenkai de aisatsu o suru no ga kanrei ni natte iru.","german":"At this company, it is customary for new employees to give a speech at the year-end party.","furigana":"かいしゃ|しんにゅうしゃいん|ぼうねんかい|あいさつ|かんれい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"合致 means \"to agree, to correspond\" and is an intransitive する-verb with と or に: 予想と合致する, 条件に合致する, 目的に合致する. It sounds more formal than the more common 一致 and often stresses that something meets criteria or requirements. In everyday speech people simply say 合う (条件に合う). The pronunciation has a geminate consonant: がっち.","examples":[{"japanese":"結果は予想と合致しました。","romaji":"Kekka wa yosou to gatchi shimashita.","german":"The result matched our expectations.","furigana":"けっか|よそう|がっち"},{"japanese":"両者の利益が合致しています。","romaji":"Ryousha no rieki ga gatchi shite imasu.","german":"The interests of both sides coincide.","furigana":"りょうしゃ|りえき|がっち"},{"japanese":"応募条件に合致する人は、一人もいなかった。","romaji":"Oubo jouken ni gatchi suru hito wa, hitori mo inakatta.","german":"No one met the application requirements.","furigana":"おうぼじょうけん|がっち|ひと|ひとり"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"聞き取り has two meanings: in language classes \"listening comprehension\" (聞き取りの練習, 聞き取り問題) and, in administration or research, \"oral questioning\" to gather information (聞き取り調査, 関係者から聞き取りを行う). The related verb is 聞き取る \"to catch, to make out\". Among learners リスニング is also common; a police interrogation, by contrast, is called 事情聴取.","examples":[{"japanese":"毎日、聞き取りの練習をしています。","romaji":"Mainichi, kikitori no renshū o shite imasu.","german":"I practice listening comprehension every day.","furigana":"まいにち|き|と|れんしゅう"},{"japanese":"住民への聞き取り調査を行いました。","romaji":"Jūmin e no kikitori chōsa o okonaimashita.","german":"We questioned the residents in person.","furigana":"じゅうみん|き|と|ちょうさ|おこな"},{"japanese":"試験の聞き取り問題は、二回ずつ流れます。","romaji":"Shiken no kikitori mondai wa, nikai zutsu nagaremasu.","german":"In the exam, each listening passage is played twice.","furigana":"しけん|き|と|もんだい|にかい|なが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"期日 is a fixed, agreed-upon day, especially a submission or payment deadline: 期日までに提出する, 期日を守る, 支払期日. Also well known is 期日前投票 \"early voting\". 期限 stresses the time limit as a span and its expiry (期限切れ), 締め切り is the cut-off for submissions or editorial deadlines, and 日付 is only the date on a document. 期日 sounds formal and official.","examples":[{"japanese":"期日までに書類を提出してください。","romaji":"Kijitsu made ni shorui o teishutsu shite kudasai.","german":"Please submit the documents by the deadline.","furigana":"きじつ|しょるい|ていしゅつ"},{"japanese":"支払いの期日が近づいています。","romaji":"Shiharai no kijitsu ga chikazuite imasu.","german":"The payment due date is approaching.","furigana":"しはら|きじつ|ちか"},{"japanese":"期日前投票を利用して、先週のうちに投票を済ませた。","romaji":"Kijitsuzen touhyou o riyou shite, senshuu no uchi ni touhyou o sumaseta.","german":"I voted early and cast my ballot last week.","furigana":"きじつぜんとうひょう|りよう|せんしゅう|とうひょう|す"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"規定 is a concretely established \"provision, regulation\" in laws, statutes or company rules: 規定に従う, 規定により, 規定の用紙 \"prescribed form\". As a transitive する-verb it means \"to stipulate, to prescribe\", often in the passive: 法律で規定されている. 規則 are general rules of conduct, 規約 are rules agreed between parties (e.g. terms of use), and 規制 is a restrictive regulation.","examples":[{"japanese":"会社の規定に従って申請します。","romaji":"Kaisha no kitei ni shitagatte shinsei shimasu.","german":"I am filing the application in accordance with company regulations.","furigana":"かいしゃ|きてい|したが|しんせい"},{"japanese":"利用時間は規定で決められています。","romaji":"Riyō jikan wa kitei de kimerarete imasu.","german":"The hours of use are set by a regulation.","furigana":"りようじかん|きてい|き"},{"japanese":"申し込みは、規定の用紙で行ってください。","romaji":"Moushikomi wa, kitei no youshi de okonatte kudasai.","german":"Please register only on the prescribed form.","furigana":"もう|こ|きてい|ようし|おこな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"規約 are written rules that members or users agree to - the bylaws of an association or the terms of use of a service (利用規約). Typical: 規約に同意する, 規約に違反する, 規約を改正する. 規定 is a single provision, 規則 is a general rule; treaties between states are called 条約.","examples":[{"japanese":"利用規約を読んでから登録してください。","romaji":"Riyō kiyaku o yonde kara tōroku shite kudasai.","german":"Please read the terms of use before registering.","furigana":"りようきやく|よ|とうろく"},{"japanese":"会員の同意を得て規約を変更しました。","romaji":"Kaiin no dōi o ete kiyaku o henkō shimashita.","german":"With the members' consent, we amended the rules.","furigana":"かいいん|どうい|え|きやく|へんこう"},{"japanese":"規約に違反したアカウントは削除されます。","romaji":"Kiyaku ni ihan shita akaunto wa sakujo saremasu.","german":"Accounts that violate the terms of use will be deleted.","furigana":"きやく|いはん|さくじょ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"許容 means \"to tolerate, to accept as still permissible\" and is a transitive する-verb: 誤差を許容する. It is very common in compounds such as 許容範囲 (tolerance range), 許容量 and 許容できない. It sounds objective to technical; in everyday speech people say 許す. 許可, by contrast, is official permission, and 容認 is the (often reluctant) acceptance of an attitude or action.","examples":[{"japanese":"この程度の誤差は許容できます。","romaji":"Kono teido no gosa wa kyoyō dekimasu.","german":"A deviation of this size is acceptable.","furigana":"ていど|ごさ|きょよう"},{"japanese":"騒音が許容範囲を超えています。","romaji":"Sōon ga kyoyō han'i o koete imasu.","german":"The noise exceeds the permissible range.","furigana":"そうおん|きょようはんい|こ"},{"japanese":"部下のミスをどこまで許容するかは、難しい問題だ。","romaji":"Buka no misu o doko made kyoyou suru ka wa, muzukashii mondai da.","german":"How many mistakes to tolerate from one's staff is a difficult question.","furigana":"ぶか|きょよう|むずか|もんだい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"議題 is the topic discussed at a meeting, that is, an \"agenda item\". Typical: 議題に移る, 議題に加える, 議題にする, 議題に上がる (to come up on the agenda). 話題, by contrast, is an everyday topic of conversation, and 議案 is a formal motion or bill that is voted on. The whole agenda is called 議事日程.","examples":[{"japanese":"次の議題に移りましょう。","romaji":"Tsugi no gidai ni utsurimashō.","german":"Let's move on to the next agenda item.","furigana":"つぎ|ぎだい|うつ"},{"japanese":"予算の見直しを議題に加えました。","romaji":"Yosan no minaoshi o gidai ni kuwaemashita.","german":"We put the budget review on the agenda.","furigana":"よさん|みなお|ぎだい|くわ"},{"japanese":"環境問題が国会で議題に上がった。","romaji":"Kankyou mondai ga kokkai de gidai ni agatta.","german":"Environmental issues came onto the agenda in parliament.","furigana":"かんきょうもんだい|こっかい|ぎだい|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"業者 is a commercial provider or specialist firm that you hire or buy from: 引っ越し業者, 清掃業者, 業者に頼む. The word sounds somewhat impersonal and can have a negative tone in combinations such as 悪質な業者 or 悪徳業者. From a company's point of view, 同業者 means \"competitor in the same industry\". More neutral words are 会社 or 専門店.","examples":[{"japanese":"修理は専門の業者に頼みました。","romaji":"Shūri wa senmon no gyōsha ni tanomimashita.","german":"I left the repair to a specialist firm.","furigana":"しゅうり|せんもん|ぎょうしゃ|たの"},{"japanese":"複数の業者から見積もりを取りました。","romaji":"Fukusū no gyōsha kara mitsumori o torimashita.","german":"I got quotes from several providers.","furigana":"ふくすう|ぎょうしゃ|みつ|と"},{"japanese":"悪質な業者による高額な請求に注意してください。","romaji":"Akushitsu na gyousha ni yoru kougaku na seikyuu ni chuui shite kudasai.","german":"Beware of inflated bills from unscrupulous companies.","furigana":"あくしつ|ぎょうしゃ|こうがく|せいきゅう|ちゅうい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"業務 means the work duties and ongoing operations of a company or government office. It is more businesslike than 仕事 and appears in many compounds: 業務内容, 通常業務, 業務時間, 業務提携; 業務用 means \"for commercial use\". On signs and in announcements you read, for example, 本日の業務は終了しました.","examples":[{"japanese":"担当する業務について説明を受けました。","romaji":"Tantō suru gyōmu ni tsuite setsumei o ukemashita.","german":"I was briefed on my duties.","furigana":"たんとう|ぎょうむ|せつめい|う"},{"japanese":"業務の効率を高める方法を考えています。","romaji":"Gyōmu no kōritsu o takameru hōhō o kangaete imasu.","german":"We are considering how to make our workflows more efficient.","furigana":"ぎょうむ|こうりつ|たか|ほうほう|かんが"},{"japanese":"本日の業務は終了いたしました。","romaji":"Honjitsu no gyoumu wa shuuryou itashimashita.","german":"We have closed for today.","furigana":"ほんじつ|ぎょうむ|しゅうりょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"食い違う is intransitive and means \"to not fit together, to diverge\" - originally of seams or gears, today mostly of statements, opinions and figures: 話が食い違う, 意見が食い違う, 証言が食い違う. The noun is 食い違い. 異なる only neutrally means \"to be different\", and 矛盾する means \"to contradict logically\"; 食い違う stresses that two versions that should agree do not.","examples":[{"japanese":"二人の説明が食い違っています。","romaji":"Futari no setsumei ga kuichigatte imasu.","german":"The two of them give conflicting accounts.","furigana":"ふたり|せつめい|く|ちが"},{"japanese":"待ち合わせの場所について話が食い違っていて、会えませんでした。","romaji":"Machiawase no basho ni tsuite hanashi ga kuichigatte ite, aemasen deshita.","german":"We had misunderstood each other about the meeting place and so missed each other.","furigana":"ま|あ|ばしょ|はなし|く|ちが|あ"},{"japanese":"今後の方針をめぐって、社長と副社長の意見が食い違っている。","romaji":"Kongo no houshin o megutte, shachou to fukushachou no iken ga kuichigatte iru.","german":"The president and his deputy disagree over the future course.","furigana":"こんご|ほうしん|しゃちょう|ふくしゃちょう|いけん|く|ちが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"経過 has two main meanings: the passing of time (一時間が経過する, 経過時間) and the course of a development (手術後の経過, 経過報告, 経過観察). As a する-verb it is mostly intransitive. In everyday speech people prefer 経つ for time; 過程 stresses the individual stages of a process, while 経過 stresses the progression over time. Typical for medicine, reports and announcements.","examples":[{"japanese":"手術後の経過は良好です。","romaji":"Shujutsugo no keika wa ryōkō desu.","german":"The progress after the operation is good.","furigana":"しゅじゅつご|けいか|りょうこう"},{"japanese":"開始から一時間が経過しました。","romaji":"Kaishi kara ichijikan ga keika shimashita.","german":"An hour has passed since the start.","furigana":"かいし|いちじかん|けいか"},{"japanese":"これまでの経過を簡単に報告します。","romaji":"Kore made no keika o kantan ni houkoku shimasu.","german":"I will briefly report on the situation so far.","furigana":"けいか|かんたん|ほうこく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"軽減 means \"to reduce, to alleviate, to relieve\", especially of burdens, pain, damage or costs: 負担を軽減する, 負担軽減, 痛みの軽減. As a する-verb it is mostly transitive. Well known is 軽減税率 \"reduced tax rate\". 削減 is cutting an amount (costs, staff), 緩和 is easing rules or tensions; 減らす is the everyday word.","examples":[{"japanese":"新しい制度で負担を軽減します。","romaji":"Atarashii seido de futan o keigen shimasu.","german":"The new rule reduces the burden.","furigana":"あたら|せいど|ふたん|けいげん"},{"japanese":"この道具は作業の負担軽減に役立ちます。","romaji":"Kono dōgu wa sagyō no futan keigen ni yakudachimasu.","german":"This tool makes the work easier.","furigana":"どうぐ|さぎょう|ふたんけいげん|やくだ"},{"japanese":"食料品には軽減税率が適用されている。","romaji":"Shokuryouhin ni wa keigen zeiritsu ga tekiyou sarete iru.","german":"A reduced tax rate applies to food.","furigana":"しょくりょうひん|けいげんぜいりつ|てきよう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"経歴 is a person's, especially professional, \"career history\" - education, positions held, experience: 経歴を確認する, 〜した経歴がある, 華やかな経歴. The document itself is called 履歴書; 学歴 is only the educational path, 職歴 the job history. Giving false information on a résumé is called 経歴を偽る or 経歴詐称.","examples":[{"japanese":"応募者の経歴を確認しました。","romaji":"Ōbosha no keireki o kakunin shimashita.","german":"We checked the applicants' backgrounds.","furigana":"おうぼしゃ|けいれき|かくにん"},{"japanese":"彼女は海外で働いた経歴があります。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kaigai de hataraita keireki ga arimasu.","german":"She has experience of working abroad.","furigana":"かのじょ|かいがい|はたら|けいれき"},{"japanese":"彼が経歴を偽って入社していたことが分かった。","romaji":"Kare ga keireki o itsuwatte nyuusha shite ita koto ga wakatta.","german":"It turned out that he had joined the company by falsifying his background.","furigana":"かれ|けいれき|いつわ|にゅうしゃ|わ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 49 (source: N4; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"見地 is a written-language word for \"standpoint, viewpoint\" and almost always appears as 〜の見地から or 〜的見地から: 専門的な見地から, 医学的見地から, 安全の見地から. In everyday speech people prefer 観点 or 視点; 立場, by contrast, is one's own role or position. 見地 sounds objective and official and suits expert opinions, statements and reports.","examples":[{"japanese":"専門的な見地から意見を述べました。","romaji":"Senmonteki na kenchi kara iken o nobemashita.","german":"I gave my opinion from a professional standpoint.","furigana":"せんもんてき|けんち|いけん|の"},{"japanese":"安全の見地から計画を見直します。","romaji":"Anzen no kenchi kara keikaku o minaoshimasu.","german":"We are reviewing the plan from a safety perspective.","furigana":"あんぜん|けんち|けいかく|みなお"},{"japanese":"医学的見地から言えば、その方法は勧められない。","romaji":"Igakuteki kenchi kara ieba, sono houhou wa susumerarenai.","german":"From a medical standpoint, this method is not recommended.","furigana":"いがくてきけんち|い|ほうほう|すす"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"原文 is the \"original text\" before a translation, summary or adaptation: 原文と比べる, 原文を確認する, 原文に忠実な翻訳. 原文のまま means \"word for word as in the original\", for example with quotations. 原作, by contrast, is the original work that gets adapted or filmed, 原稿 is a manuscript, and 本文 is the main text of a document.","examples":[{"japanese":"訳文を原文と比べました。","romaji":"Yakubun o genbun to kurabemashita.","german":"I compared the translation with the original.","furigana":"やくぶん|げんぶん|くら"},{"japanese":"意味が曖昧なので原文を確認します。","romaji":"Imi ga aimai na node genbun o kakunin shimasu.","german":"Since the meaning is unclear, I will check the original text.","furigana":"いみ|あいまい|げんぶん|かくにん"},{"japanese":"引用は原文のまま載せてください。","romaji":"In'you wa genbun no mama nosete kudasai.","german":"Please copy quotations from the original unchanged.","furigana":"いんよう|げんぶん|の"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"個別 means \"individual, separately for each\" and appears as 個別に (adverbial) or 個別の (attributive): 個別に対応する, 個別の事情. Common compounds are 個別指導 (one-on-one tutoring), 個別面談 and 個別包装. The opposite is 一括 or 全体; in everyday speech people often say the simpler 別々に. 個々 stresses \"each single one\", 個人 the individual person.","examples":[{"japanese":"質問には個別にお答えします。","romaji":"Shitsumon ni wa kobetsu ni okotae shimasu.","german":"We answer questions on an individual basis.","furigana":"しつもん|こべつ|こた"},{"japanese":"個別の事情を考慮する必要があります。","romaji":"Kobetsu no jijō o kōryo suru hitsuyō ga arimasu.","german":"The circumstances of each case must be taken into account.","furigana":"こべつ|じじょう|こうりょ|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"息子は塾で個別指導を受けている。","romaji":"Musuko wa juku de kobetsu shidou o ukete iru.","german":"My son gets one-on-one lessons at cram school.","furigana":"むすこ|じゅく|こべつしどう|う"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"根底 is the deepest \"foundation\" or the real origin of something abstract. Typical: 〜の根底にある \"to lie at the root of ...\" and 根底から \"from the ground up\" (根底から覆す, 根底から揺るがす). 根本 is similar but more common as the adjective 根本的; 基礎 and 土台 are rather concrete or technical foundations. 根底 belongs to written language.","examples":[{"japanese":"問題の根底には情報不足があります。","romaji":"Mondai no kontei ni wa jōhō busoku ga arimasu.","german":"Lack of information lies at the root of the problem.","furigana":"もんだい|こんてい|じょうほうぶそく"},{"japanese":"この発見は従来の考えを根底から変えました。","romaji":"Kono hakken wa jūrai no kangae o kontei kara kaemashita.","german":"This discovery fundamentally changed previous ways of thinking.","furigana":"はっけん|じゅうらい|かんが|こんてい|か"},{"japanese":"彼の作品の根底には、平和への強い願いがある。","romaji":"Kare no sakuhin no kontei ni wa, heiwa e no tsuyoi negai ga aru.","german":"A deep longing for peace underlies his works.","furigana":"かれ|さくひん|こんてい|へいわ|つよ|ねが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"混同 means failing to keep two different things apart or mixing them up; as a transitive する-verb: AとBを混同する, AをBと混同する. Fixed phrase: 公私混同 \"mixing of official and private matters\", for example the private use of company resources. 間違える is the everyday \"to mix up\", and 混乱 means \"confusion, disorder\" as a state.","examples":[{"japanese":"似た言葉を混同しないようにしましょう。","romaji":"Nita kotoba o kondō shinai yō ni shimashō.","german":"Let's be careful not to confuse similar words.","furigana":"に|ことば|こんどう"},{"japanese":"事実と意見が混同されています。","romaji":"Jijitsu to iken ga kondō sarete imasu.","german":"Facts and opinions are being mixed together.","furigana":"じじつ|いけん|こんどう"},{"japanese":"会社の車で家族旅行に行くのは公私混同だ。","romaji":"Kaisha no kuruma de kazoku ryokou ni iku no wa koushi kondou da.","german":"Taking the company car on a family vacation is mixing business with private matters.","furigana":"かいしゃ|くるま|かぞくりょこう|い|こうしこんどう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"最善 is \"the best possible\" in the sense of the best course of action: 最善を尽くす \"to do one's best\", 最善の方法, 最善策. The second-best solution is 次善の策. 最良 refers more to the best quality (最良の日), and 最高 means the highest or, colloquially, \"awesome\". 最善 sounds serious and suits promises, decisions and official statements.","examples":[{"japanese":"問題解決のために最善を尽くします。","romaji":"Mondai kaiketsu no tame ni saizen o tsukushimasu.","german":"I will do my best to solve the problem.","furigana":"もんだいかいけつ|さいぜん|つ"},{"japanese":"これが現時点で最善の方法です。","romaji":"Kore ga genjiten de saizen no hōhō desu.","german":"That is the best method at present.","furigana":"げんじてん|さいぜん|ほうほう"},{"japanese":"患者にとって何が最善かを、家族と一緒に考えた。","romaji":"Kanja ni totte nani ga saizen ka o, kazoku to issho ni kangaeta.","german":"Together with the family, we considered what would be best for the patient.","furigana":"かんじゃ|なに|さいぜん|かぞく|いっしょ|かんが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"定める means \"to establish, to determine\", especially rules, standards, goals and limits: 法律で定める, 〜と定められている (typical of legal texts), 目標を定める. The idiom 狙いを定める means \"to take aim at\". The intransitive counterpart is 定まる (方針が定まる). 決める is the everyday \"to decide\"; 定める sounds more formal and stresses a binding determination.","examples":[{"japanese":"法律で基準が定められています。","romaji":"Hōritsu de kijun ga sadamerarete imasu.","german":"The standards are set by law.","furigana":"ほうりつ|きじゅん|さだ"},{"japanese":"目標を定めて努力を続けました。","romaji":"Mokuhyō o sadamete doryoku o tsuzukemashita.","german":"I set a goal and kept working toward it.","furigana":"もくひょう|さだ|どりょく|つづ"},{"japanese":"猫は獲物に狙いを定めて、じっと動かなかった。","romaji":"Neko wa emono ni nerai o sadamete, jitto ugokanakatta.","german":"The cat fixed its eyes on its prey and froze.","furigana":"ねこ|えもの|ねら|さだ|うご"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"強いる means \"to force someone to do something, to impose something on someone\"; pattern: 人に〜を強いる. The passive 〜を強いられる \"to be forced to ...\" is very common, for example 苦戦を強いられる \"to be forced into a hard fight\" or 我慢を強いられる. Despite the ending -iru, it is an ichidan verb (強いて, 強いない); the form 強いて as an adverb means \"if one must\" (強いて言えば). Compared with 強制する, 強いる is somewhat more literary; in everyday speech people prefer 無理にさせる.","examples":[{"japanese":"他人に自分の考えを強いてはいけません。","romaji":"Tanin ni jibun no kangae o shiite wa ikemasen.","german":"You must not impose your views on others.","furigana":"たにん|じぶん|かんが|し"},{"japanese":"住民は長い避難生活を強いられました。","romaji":"Jūmin wa nagai hinan seikatsu o shiiraremashita.","german":"The residents were forced to live a long time in emergency shelters.","furigana":"じゅうみん|なが|ひなんせいかつ|し"},{"japanese":"部下に飲酒を強いるのはハラスメントです。","romaji":"Buka ni inshu o shiiru no wa harasumento desu.","german":"Pressuring subordinates to drink is harassment.","furigana":"ぶか|いんしゅ|し"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"視野 literally means the \"field of view\" of the eyes (視野が狭くなる), but it is used even more often figuratively for the mental \"horizon\": 視野を広げる \"to broaden one's horizons\", 視野が狭い \"to be narrow-minded\", 広い視野を持つ. The fixed phrase 〜を視野に入れる means \"to take something into consideration, to have something in view\" (e.g. 将来の留学を視野に入れる). For plain visibility in fog or rain, people prefer 視界.","examples":[{"japanese":"海外での経験が視野を広げました。","romaji":"Kaigai de no keiken ga shiya o hirogemashita.","german":"Experience abroad broadened my horizons.","furigana":"かいがい|けいけん|しや|ひろ"},{"japanese":"長期的な影響も視野に入れて考えます。","romaji":"Chōkiteki na eikyō mo shiya ni irete kangaemasu.","german":"We also take long-term effects into account.","furigana":"ちょうきてき|えいきょう|しや|い|かんが"},{"japanese":"スピードを出すと視野が狭くなります。","romaji":"Supiido o dasu to shiya ga semaku narimasu.","german":"At high speed, the field of vision narrows.","furigana":"だ|しや|せま"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"集計 means \"adding up, tabulating\" numbers, votes or answers; as a transitive する-verb: 票を集計する, アンケートを集計する. Typical compounds are 集計結果 \"tabulation result\" and 集計表 \"summary table\". Unlike 計算 (\"calculating\" in general), 集計 stresses gathering many individual values into a total or overview; 合計, by contrast, is only the grand total itself.","examples":[{"japanese":"アンケートの回答を集計しました。","romaji":"Ankēto no kaitō o shūkei shimashita.","german":"We tallied the survey responses.","furigana":"かいとう|しゅうけい"},{"japanese":"集計結果は明日発表します。","romaji":"Shūkei kekka wa ashita happyō shimasu.","german":"We will announce the tally results tomorrow.","furigana":"しゅうけいけっか|あしたはっぴょう"},{"japanese":"毎月の売上を集計して部長に報告します。","romaji":"Maitsuki no uriage o shuukei shite buchou ni houkoku shimasu.","german":"I add up the sales every month and report to the department manager.","furigana":"まいつき|うりあげ|しゅうけい|ぶちょう|ほうこく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"主体 refers to the \"bearer\" or \"acting subject\" of something as well as the \"main component\". Typical frames: 〜が主体となって \"with ... taking the lead\", 〜を主体とする \"consisting mainly of ...\". The derivative 主体的 \"independent, on one's own initiative\" (主体的に行動する) and 主体性 \"initiative\" are very common. Do not confuse it with 主催 \"organizer\": 主体 stresses who acts actively or forms the core.","examples":[{"japanese":"住民が主体となって活動しています。","romaji":"Jūmin ga shutai to natte katsudō shite imasu.","german":"The residents themselves carry out the activities.","furigana":"じゅうみん|しゅたい|かつどう"},{"japanese":"野菜を主体とした食事に変えました。","romaji":"Yasai o shutai to shita shokuji ni kaemashita.","german":"I switched to a diet that is mainly plant-based.","furigana":"やさい|しゅたい|しょくじ|か"},{"japanese":"子どもたちが主体的に学べる授業を目指しています。","romaji":"Kodomotachi ga shutaiteki ni manaberu jugyou o mezashite imasu.","german":"We aim for lessons in which children can learn independently.","furigana":"こ|しゅたいてき|まな|じゅぎょう|めざ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"所在 is \"the place where something or someone is\", the \"whereabouts\". It is a formal word: 所在が分からない, 所在不明 \"whereabouts unknown\", 所在地 \"location, address\" (e.g. of a company). Figuratively, 責任の所在 asks \"with whom the responsibility lies\". In everyday speech people say the simpler どこにあるか or 居場所 (for people). The adjective 所在ない, by contrast, means \"bored, idle\".","examples":[{"japanese":"書類の所在が分かりません。","romaji":"Shorui no shozai ga wakarimasen.","german":"I don't know where the documents are.","furigana":"しょるい|しょざい|わ"},{"japanese":"責任の所在を明らかにする必要があります。","romaji":"Sekinin no shozai o akiraka ni suru hitsuyō ga arimasu.","german":"It must be clarified who is responsible.","furigana":"せきにん|しょざい|あき|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"申込書に会社の所在地を記入してください。","romaji":"Moushikomisho ni kaisha no shozaichi o kinyuu shite kudasai.","german":"Please enter the address of the company's head office on the application form.","furigana":"もうしこみしょ|かいしゃ|しょざいち|きにゅう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"所定 means \"prescribed, designated for that purpose\" and is practically always used as 所定の before a noun: 所定の用紙 \"the designated form\", 所定の場所, 所定の手続き, 所定の時間. It is typical of instructions at government offices, companies and notices. Compared with 規定 (the rule itself), 所定 stresses that something has already been determined; in everyday speech people say the simpler 決められた.","examples":[{"japanese":"所定の用紙に記入してください。","romaji":"Shotei no yōshi ni kinyū shite kudasai.","german":"Please fill out the designated form.","furigana":"しょてい|ようし|きにゅう"},{"japanese":"所定の手続きを終えました。","romaji":"Shotei no tetsuzuki o oemashita.","german":"I completed the prescribed procedure.","furigana":"しょてい|てつづ|お"},{"japanese":"使い終わったら所定の場所に戻してください。","romaji":"Tsukaiowattara shotei no basho ni modoshite kudasai.","german":"Please return it to its designated place after use.","furigana":"つか|お|しょてい|ばしょ|もど"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"持続 means that a state or effect \"continues, lasts\". As an intransitive する-verb: 効果が持続する; maintaining something by someone is usually expressed with 持続させる. Very common is 持続可能 \"sustainable\" (持続可能な社会, SDGs). Unlike 継続 (deliberately continuing an activity), 持続 stresses that something endures over time; 維持 means \"to maintain a state\".","examples":[{"japanese":"効果が長く持続します。","romaji":"Kōka ga nagaku jizoku shimasu.","german":"The effect lasts a long time.","furigana":"こうか|なが|じぞく"},{"japanese":"集中力を持続させるには休憩も必要です。","romaji":"Shūchūryoku o jizoku saseru ni wa kyūkei mo hitsuyō desu.","german":"To stay focused, you also need breaks.","furigana":"しゅうちゅうりょく|じぞく|きゅうけい|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"持続可能な社会を作るために何ができるでしょうか。","romaji":"Jizoku kanou na shakai o tsukuru tame ni nani ga dekiru deshou ka.","german":"What can we do for a sustainable society?","furigana":"じぞくかのう|しゃかい|つく|なに"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"辞退 means to politely \"decline\" something offered to oneself or to \"waive\" it: 参加を辞退する, 受賞を辞退する, 内定を辞退する \"to turn down a job offer\". It is used with を and sounds formal and modest. Unlike 断る (\"to refuse\" in general, also other people's requests), 辞退 refers to honors, posts, participation or gifts; 遠慮 is rather a polite holding back.","examples":[{"japanese":"都合により参加を辞退しました。","romaji":"Tsugō ni yori sanka o jitai shimashita.","german":"I declined to take part for personal reasons.","furigana":"つごう|さんか|じたい"},{"japanese":"彼は受賞を辞退しました。","romaji":"Kare wa jushō o jitai shimashita.","german":"He turned down the award.","furigana":"かれ|じゅしょう|じたい"},{"japanese":"第一志望の会社に受かったので、ほかの内定を辞退しました。","romaji":"Dai-ichi shibou no kaisha ni ukatta node, hoka no naitei o jitai shimashita.","german":"Because I was accepted by my preferred company, I turned down the other offers.","furigana":"だいいちしぼう|かいしゃ|う|ないてい|じたい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"実質 is the \"actual content, the substance\" as opposed to outward form (形式) or name (名目). Often used as 実質的な \"actual, de facto\" and adverbially as 実質 or 実質的に \"practically, effectively\": 実質無料, 実質値上げ \"hidden price increase\". In economics it stands for \"real\" as opposed to \"nominal\": 実質賃金, 実質GDP.","examples":[{"japanese":"値上げで実質的な負担が増えました。","romaji":"Neage de jisshitsuteki na futan ga fuemashita.","german":"The price increase has raised the actual burden.","furigana":"ねあ|じっしつてき|ふたん|ふ"},{"japanese":"形式より実質を重視します。","romaji":"Keishiki yori jisshitsu o jūshi shimasu.","german":"We give more weight to content than to form.","furigana":"けいしき|じっしつ|じゅうし"},{"japanese":"このプランは実質無料で使えます。","romaji":"Kono puran wa jisshitsu muryou de tsukaemasu.","german":"This plan can be used practically for free.","furigana":"じっしつむりょう|つか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"地元 is the \"home area\" where one grew up or lives, and as 地元の also \"local, regional\" from the viewpoint of a particular place: 地元の人, 地元の食材, 地元企業, 地元の警察. 地元に帰る means \"to return to one's hometown\". Unlike 故郷 or ふるさと, which emotionally refer to one's birthplace, 地元 is matter-of-fact and everyday; 現地, by contrast, means \"on the spot\" in a foreign place.","examples":[{"japanese":"地元の食材を使っています。","romaji":"Jimoto no shokuzai o tsukatte imasu.","german":"We use local ingredients.","furigana":"じもと|しょくざい|つか"},{"japanese":"卒業後は地元に戻るつもりです。","romaji":"Sotsugyōgo wa jimoto ni modoru tsumori desu.","german":"After graduating, I want to return to my hometown.","furigana":"そつぎょうご|じもと|もど"},{"japanese":"地元の人しか知らない店に連れて行ってもらいました。","romaji":"Jimoto no hito shika shiranai mise ni tsurete itte moraimashita.","german":"I was taken to a restaurant that only the locals know.","furigana":"じもと|ひと|し|みせ|つ|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"従事 means \"to be professionally active in a field\"; the field is marked with に: 農業に従事する, 医療に従事する. It is a formal word from résumés, statistics and news (医療従事者 \"medical personnel\"). Close in meaning is 携わる (〜に携わる), which sounds a bit more personal; in everyday speech people simply say 〜の仕事をしている.","examples":[{"japanese":"長年、教育に従事してきました。","romaji":"Naganen, kyōiku ni jūji shite kimashita.","german":"I worked in the field of education for many years.","furigana":"ながねん|きょういく|じゅうじ"},{"japanese":"医療に従事する人々を支援します。","romaji":"Iryō ni jūji suru hitobito o shien shimasu.","german":"We support people working in medical professions.","furigana":"いりょう|じゅうじ|ひとびと|しえん"},{"japanese":"祖父は六十年間、農業に従事していました。","romaji":"Sofu wa rokujuunenkan, nougyou ni juuji shite imashita.","german":"My grandfather worked in agriculture for sixty years.","furigana":"そふ|ろくじゅうねんかん|のうぎょう|じゅうじ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"重複 means \"duplication, overlap\"; as an intransitive する-verb: 予定が重複する, データが重複している. The traditional reading is ちょうふく, but in everyday speech じゅうふく is heard at least as often; both are now considered correct. Often used adverbially as 重複して \"twice over\" (重複して申し込む) and in 重複を避ける. For overlapping appointments in time, 重なる is also very common.","examples":[{"japanese":"二つの予定が重複しています。","romaji":"Futatsu no yotei ga chōfuku shite imasu.","german":"Two appointments overlap.","furigana":"ふた|よてい|ちょうふく"},{"japanese":"重複した情報を整理しました。","romaji":"Chōfuku shita jōhō o seiri shimashita.","german":"I cleaned up duplicate information.","furigana":"ちょうふく|じょうほう|せいり"},{"japanese":"同じ講座に重複して申し込まないでください。","romaji":"Onaji kouza ni choufuku shite moushikomanaide kudasai.","german":"Please do not register twice for the same course.","furigana":"おな|こうざ|ちょうふく|もう|こ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"切実 describes something that \"affects one directly and deeply\": a \"pressing\" problem or an \"earnest, urgent\" wish. Typical are 切実な問題, 切実な願い, 切実な声 and adverbially 切実に感じる/訴える. Unlike 緊急 (\"urgent\" in the sense of hurried), 切実 stresses personal concern and seriousness, not haste.","examples":[{"japanese":"住民の切実な願いを聞きました。","romaji":"Jūmin no setsujitsu na negai o kikimashita.","german":"I listened to the residents' urgent requests.","furigana":"じゅうみん|せつじつ|ねが|き"},{"japanese":"人手不足は切実な問題です。","romaji":"Hitode busoku wa setsujitsu na mondai desu.","german":"The labor shortage is a pressing problem.","furigana":"ひとでぶそく|せつじつ|もんだい"},{"japanese":"一人暮らしを始めて、料理の大切さを切実に感じています。","romaji":"Hitorigurashi o hajimete, ryouri no taisetsusa o setsujitsu ni kanjite imasu.","german":"Since I started living alone, I keenly feel how important cooking is.","furigana":"ひとりぐ|はじ|りょうり|たいせつ|せつじつ|かん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"前例 is a \"precedent, previous example\". Typical are 前例がない/前例のない \"unprecedented\", 前例を作る \"to set a precedent\" and 前例になる; 前例に従う or 前例を参考にする means to follow earlier cases. 前例主義 criticizes rigid adherence to what has been done before. Since 前 already contains \"earlier\", 過去の前例 is considered redundant.","examples":[{"japanese":"前例がないからといって諦めません。","romaji":"Zenrei ga nai kara to itte akiramemasen.","german":"We won't give up just because there is no precedent.","furigana":"ぜんれい|あきら"},{"japanese":"前例を参考に判断しました。","romaji":"Zenrei o sankou ni handan shimashita.","german":"I based my decision on earlier cases.","furigana":"ぜんれい|さんこう|はんだん"},{"japanese":"一度例外を認めると、それが前例になってしまいます。","romaji":"Ichido reigai o mitomeru to, sore ga zenrei ni natte shimaimasu.","german":"Once you allow an exception, it becomes a precedent.","furigana":"いちどれいがい|みと|ぜんれい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"存続 is the \"continued existence\" of an institution, company, tradition or species. Typical: 〜の存続が危ぶまれる \"the continued existence is in danger\", 存続を求める, and for actively preserving 存続させる. As a する-verb it is mostly used intransitively (制度が存続する). Compared with 継続 (\"continuation of an activity\"), 存続 is about continuing to exist at all, often in the face of impending closure.","examples":[{"japanese":"学校の存続について話し合いました。","romaji":"Gakkō no sonzoku ni tsuite hanashiaimashita.","german":"We discussed the future of the school.","furigana":"がっこう|そんぞく|はな|あ"},{"japanese":"伝統を存続させる努力が続いています。","romaji":"Dentō o sonzoku saseru doryoku ga tsuzuite imasu.","german":"Efforts to preserve the tradition continue.","furigana":"でんとう|そんぞく|どりょく|つづ"},{"japanese":"赤字が続き、会社の存続が危うくなっています。","romaji":"Akaji ga tsuzuki, kaisha no sonzoku ga ayauku natte imasu.","german":"After continued losses, the company's survival is at stake.","furigana":"あかじ|つづ|かいしゃ|そんぞく|あや"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"単独 means \"alone, independent, without other participants\". Mostly as 単独で (単独で行動する) or as a prefix in compounds: 単独行動, 単独犯 \"lone offender\", 単独事故 \"single-vehicle accident\", 単独首位 \"sole lead\". It is more businesslike than 一人で and can also be applied to companies, states or parties (一社単独, 単独政権).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は単独で調査を行いました。","romaji":"Kare wa tandoku de chōsa o okonaimashita.","german":"He carried out the investigation alone.","furigana":"かれ|たんどく|ちょうさ|おこな"},{"japanese":"一社単独では実現できない計画です。","romaji":"Issha tandoku de wa jitsugen dekinai keikaku desu.","german":"One company alone cannot realize this plan.","furigana":"いっしゃたんどく|じつげん|けいかく"},{"japanese":"その選手は最終日に単独首位に立ちました。","romaji":"Sono senshu wa saishuubi ni tandoku shui ni tachimashita.","german":"On the final day, the player took sole possession of the lead.","furigana":"せんしゅ|さいしゅうび|たんどくしゅい|た"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"直面 means \"to face something directly\"; almost always in the pattern 〜に直面する with difficult things: 問題, 危機, 困難, 現実, 死. It is an intransitive する-verb, and the thing faced takes に, not を. Compared with 向き合う (\"to consciously confront something\"), 直面 describes more the situation one gets into; in everyday speech people also say 〜にぶつかる.","examples":[{"japanese":"大きな課題に直面しています。","romaji":"Ōkina kadai ni chokumen shite imasu.","german":"We are facing a major challenge.","furigana":"おお|かだい|ちょくめん"},{"japanese":"現実に直面して考えが変わりました。","romaji":"Genjitsu ni chokumen shite kangae ga kawarimashita.","german":"Faced with reality, my view changed.","furigana":"げんじつ|ちょくめん|かんが|か"},{"japanese":"危機に直面したときこそ、冷静さが大切です。","romaji":"Kiki ni chokumen shita toki koso, reiseisa ga taisetsu desu.","german":"It is precisely in a crisis that staying calm matters.","furigana":"きき|ちょくめん|れいせい|たいせつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"通常 means \"usual, normal, regular\". As an adverb it stands alone or with は (通常は二日で届く), before nouns as 通常の or directly in compounds: 通常料金, 通常営業, 通常国会. 通常通り means \"as usual\". It is more formal than 普通 and typical of business notices, manuals and news; in conversation 普通 or いつも sounds more natural.","examples":[{"japanese":"通常は二日以内に届きます。","romaji":"Tsūjō wa futsuka inai ni todokimasu.","german":"Normally it arrives within two days.","furigana":"つうじょう|ふつかいない|とど"},{"japanese":"明日から通常の営業に戻ります。","romaji":"Ashita kara tsūjō no eigyō ni modorimasu.","german":"Normal business hours apply again from tomorrow.","furigana":"あした|つうじょう|えいぎょう|もど"},{"japanese":"学生は通常料金の半額で入場できます。","romaji":"Gakusei wa tsuujou ryoukin no hangaku de nyuujou dekimasu.","german":"Students get in at half the regular price.","furigana":"がくせい|つうじょうりょうきん|はんがく|にゅうじょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"提示 means to \"show\" something (ID, ticket) or to \"present, put forward\" (conditions, a plan, an example): 身分証を提示する, 条件を提示する, 例を提示する. The difference from 提出 is important: with 提出 you hand in a document and it stays with the recipient, whereas with 提示 you merely show it. 提示 is formal; in conversation people say 見せる.","examples":[{"japanese":"入口で身分証を提示してください。","romaji":"Iriguchi de mibunshō o teiji shite kudasai.","german":"Please show an ID at the entrance.","furigana":"いりぐち|みぶんしょう|ていじ"},{"japanese":"会社側が新しい条件を提示しました。","romaji":"Kaishagawa ga atarashii jōken o teiji shimashita.","german":"The company has put forward new terms.","furigana":"かいしゃがわ|あたら|じょうけん|ていじ"},{"japanese":"具体的な例を提示して説明してください。","romaji":"Gutaiteki na rei o teiji shite setsumei shite kudasai.","german":"Please explain using concrete examples.","furigana":"ぐたいてき|れい|ていじ|せつめい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"適宜 means \"as needed, depending on the situation, at one's own discretion\" and usually stands directly before the verb as an adverb: 適宜休憩を取る, 適宜変更する. Common in manuals, work instructions and recipes (塩 適宜 \"salt to taste\"). Do not confuse it with 適当: that can also mean \"suitable\", but in everyday speech also \"sloppy, any old way\", whereas 適宜 always stays neutral and businesslike.","examples":[{"japanese":"疲れたら適宜休憩を取ってください。","romaji":"Tsukaretara tekigi kyūkei o totte kudasai.","german":"Take breaks as needed if you feel tired.","furigana":"つか|てきぎきゅうけい|と"},{"japanese":"内容は状況に応じて適宜変更します。","romaji":"Naiyō wa jōkyō ni ōjite tekigi henkō shimasu.","german":"We adjust the content as appropriate to the situation.","furigana":"ないよう|じょうきょう|おう|てきぎへんこう"},{"japanese":"味を見ながら、塩を適宜加えてください。","romaji":"Aji o minagara, shio o tekigi kuwaete kudasai.","german":"Taste it and add salt to taste.","furigana":"あじ|み|しお|てきぎくわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"手元 is the area \"right at hand\": 手元にある \"to be at hand\", 手元に置く \"to keep within reach\". In presentations you often hear お手元の資料をご覧ください \"Please look at the materials in front of you\". 手元 can also mean the movement of the hands (手元が狂う \"the hand slips\") and available money (手元資金). Unlike 近く, 手元 stresses that one can reach for it immediately.","examples":[{"japanese":"手元に資料がないので確認できません。","romaji":"Temoto ni shiryō ga nai node kakunin dekimasen.","german":"I don't have the documents at hand, so I can't check.","furigana":"てもと|しりょう|かくにん"},{"japanese":"大切な書類は手元に置いてください。","romaji":"Taisetsu na shorui wa temoto ni oite kudasai.","german":"Please keep important documents with you.","furigana":"たいせつ|しょるい|てもと|お"},{"japanese":"手元が狂って、指を切ってしまいました。","romaji":"Temoto ga kurutte, yubi o kitte shimaimashita.","german":"My hand slipped and I cut my finger.","furigana":"てもと|くる|ゆび|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"投入 literally means \"to throw in\" (coins, ballots: 硬貨を投入する) and figuratively \"to commit, to pour in\" money, personnel or effort: 資金を投入する, 人員を投入する. It also means to bring a product to market (市場に投入する). Unlike 投資 (\"to invest\" with an expectation of profit), 投入 stresses the commitment of resources for a goal. The target takes に.","examples":[{"japanese":"新しい事業に資金を投入しました。","romaji":"Atarashii jigyō ni shikin o tōnyū shimashita.","german":"We invested capital in the new business.","furigana":"あたら|じぎょう|しきん|とうにゅう"},{"japanese":"機械に硬貨を投入してください。","romaji":"Kikai ni kōka o tōnyū shite kudasai.","german":"Please insert coins into the machine.","furigana":"きかい|こうか|とうにゅう"},{"japanese":"来月、新製品を市場に投入する予定です。","romaji":"Raigetsu, shinseihin o shijou ni tounyuu suru yotei desu.","german":"Next month we will launch a new product on the market.","furigana":"らいげつ|しんせいひん|しじょう|とうにゅう|よてい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"同等 means \"equivalent, of equal rank\" and is usually used with と: 〜と同等の (rights, performance, degree), 〜と同等に扱う \"to treat equally\", 同等以上 \"equal or better\". Compared with 同じ (\"the same\"), it concerns equal value or rank, not identity. 対等, by contrast, stresses the equal standing of partners (対等な関係).","examples":[{"japanese":"この資格は大学卒業と同等と認められます。","romaji":"Kono shikaku wa daigaku sotsugyō to dōtō to mitomeraremasu.","german":"This qualification is treated as equivalent to a university degree.","furigana":"しかく|だいがくそつぎょう|どうとう|みと"},{"japanese":"価格は違いますが、性能は同等です。","romaji":"Kakaku wa chigaimasu ga, seinō wa dōtō desu.","german":"The prices differ, but the performance is equivalent.","furigana":"かかく|ちが|せいのう|どうとう"},{"japanese":"給料は前の会社と同等かそれ以上を希望します。","romaji":"Kyuuryou wa mae no kaisha to doutou ka sore ijou o kibou shimasu.","german":"As for salary, I would like at least as much as at my previous company.","furigana":"きゅうりょう|まえ|かいしゃ|どうとう|いじょう|きぼう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"内部 is the \"inside\" of an object or building and, especially often, the \"internal\" side of an organization; its opposite is 外部. Typical: 建物の内部, 内部情報, 内部告発 \"whistleblowing\", 内部で検討する \"to examine internally\". In everyday speech, for rooms people prefer 中; 内側 stresses the inner side of a surface. For one's own company, 社内 is also common.","examples":[{"japanese":"建物の内部を見学しました。","romaji":"Tatemono no naibu o kengaku shimashita.","german":"We toured the building from the inside.","furigana":"たてもの|ないぶ|けんがく"},{"japanese":"内部で十分に話し合う必要があります。","romaji":"Naibu de jūbun ni hanashiau hitsuyō ga arimasu.","german":"We need to discuss the matter thoroughly internally.","furigana":"ないぶ|じゅうぶん|はな|あ|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"内部の情報が外部に漏れてしまいました。","romaji":"Naibu no jouhou ga gaibu ni morete shimaimashita.","german":"Internal information has leaked to the outside.","furigana":"ないぶ|じょうほう|がいぶ|も"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"入手 means \"to get into one's hands, to obtain\"; as a transitive する-verb: 情報を入手する, チケットを入手する. 入手困難 \"hard to obtain\" and 入手方法 are common. It is more formal than 手に入れる and neutral about the means: 購入, by contrast, explicitly means \"to buy\", and 取得 means \"to acquire (officially)\", for example a license.","examples":[{"japanese":"必要な資料を入手しました。","romaji":"Hitsuyō na shiryō o nyūshu shimashita.","german":"I obtained the necessary documents.","furigana":"ひつよう|しりょう|にゅうしゅ"},{"japanese":"その本は今では入手が難しいです。","romaji":"Sono hon wa ima de wa nyūshu ga muzukashii desu.","german":"This book is now hard to get.","furigana":"ほん|いま|にゅうしゅ|むずか"},{"japanese":"警察は事件に関する新しい情報を入手しました。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa jiken ni kansuru atarashii jouhou o nyuushu shimashita.","german":"The police have received new information about the case.","furigana":"けいさつ|じけん|かん|あたら|じょうほう|にゅうしゅ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"発言 is a \"remark\" or \"contribution\", especially in meetings, debates or in public: 会議で発言する, 大臣の発言, 発言を撤回する \"to retract a remark\". Related are 発言権 \"right to speak\" and 発言力 \"influence in discussions\"; a careless remark is a 失言. For everyday talking people simply say 言う or 話す; 発言 stresses taking the floor within a setting.","examples":[{"japanese":"会議で自由に発言してください。","romaji":"Kaigi de jiyū ni hatsugen shite kudasai.","german":"Please feel free to speak up at the meeting.","furigana":"かいぎ|じゆう|はつげん"},{"japanese":"彼の発言が議論のきっかけになりました。","romaji":"Kare no hatsugen ga giron no kikkake ni narimashita.","german":"His remark triggered the discussion.","furigana":"かれ|はつげん|ぎろん"},{"japanese":"大臣の不適切な発言が問題になっています。","romaji":"Daijin no futekisetsu na hatsugen ga mondai ni natte imasu.","german":"The minister's inappropriate remark is causing an uproar.","furigana":"だいじん|ふてきせつ|はつげん|もんだい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"引き起こす mostly means \"to cause, to trigger\" - almost always with undesirable consequences: 事故を引き起こす, 混乱を引き起こす, 議論を引き起こす. It sounds stronger and more formal than 起こす. The literal meaning \"to pick up something that has fallen over\" (倒れた自転車を引き起こす) also exists. For positive results, もたらす fits better. The intransitive counterpart is simply 起こる.","examples":[{"japanese":"小さなミスが大きな事故を引き起こしました。","romaji":"Chiisana misu ga ōkina jiko o hikiokoshimashita.","german":"A small mistake caused a serious accident.","furigana":"ちい|おお|じこ|ひ|お"},{"japanese":"その発言は激しい議論を引き起こしました。","romaji":"Sono hatsugen wa hageshii giron o hikiokoshimashita.","german":"The remark triggered a heated debate.","furigana":"はつげん|はげ|ぎろん|ひ|お"},{"japanese":"倒れたバイクを一人で引き起こすのは大変です。","romaji":"Taoreta baiku o hitori de hikiokosu no wa taihen desu.","german":"It is hard to pick up a fallen motorcycle by yourself.","furigana":"たお|ひとり|ひ|お|たいへん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"必然 is \"the necessary, the inevitable\"; its opposite is 偶然 \"chance\". As a predicate: 失敗は必然だ; before nouns mostly 必然的な結果, adverbially 必然的に \"inevitably\". The contrast 偶然ではなく必然 \"not chance but destiny\" is popular. In everyday speech people often say 当然 (\"naturally\"), but 必然 stresses logical-causal necessity, not expectedness.","examples":[{"japanese":"準備不足なら失敗は必然です。","romaji":"Junbi busoku nara shippai wa hitsuzen desu.","german":"With insufficient preparation, failure is inevitable.","furigana":"じゅんびぶそく|しっぱい|ひつぜん"},{"japanese":"この変化は社会の発展に伴う必然的な結果です。","romaji":"Kono henka wa shakai no hatten ni tomonau hitsuzenteki na kekka desu.","german":"This change is an inevitable consequence of social development.","furigana":"へんか|しゃかい|はってん|ともな|ひつぜんてき|けっか"},{"japanese":"二人が出会ったのは偶然ではなく必然だったのかもしれません。","romaji":"Futari ga deatta no wa guuzen de wa naku hitsuzen datta no kamo shiremasen.","german":"Perhaps the meeting of the two was not chance but fate.","furigana":"ふたり|であ|ぐうぜん|ひつぜん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"分散 means \"to distribute, to scatter, to disperse\"; its opposite is 集中. As a する-verb it is transitive (リスクを分散する, 作業を分散する) as well as intransitive (人口が分散する, 注意が分散する \"attention is scattered\"). Typical expressions are リスク分散, 分散投資 and 分散登校. In statistics, 分散 means \"variance\". Compared with 分ける (\"to divide\"), 分散 stresses spreading across many places.","examples":[{"japanese":"作業を複数の担当者に分散しました。","romaji":"Sagyō o fukusū no tantōsha ni bunsan shimashita.","german":"We divided the work among several people in charge.","furigana":"さぎょう|ふくすう|たんとうしゃ|ぶんさん"},{"japanese":"時間帯を分散して混雑を避けます。","romaji":"Jikantai o bunsan shite konzatsu o sakemasu.","german":"By staggering the times, we avoid crowding.","furigana":"じかんたい|ぶんさん|こんざつ|さ"},{"japanese":"リスクを分散するために、複数の会社に投資しています。","romaji":"Risuku o bunsan suru tame ni, fukusuu no kaisha ni toushi shite imasu.","german":"To spread the risk, I invest in several companies.","furigana":"ぶんさん|ふくすう|かいしゃ|とうし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"文書 is a \"document, written paper\" as an object, especially in the official sense: 公文書 \"official document\", 文書で回答する \"to answer in writing\", 文書化 \"to put in writing\". Do not confuse it with 文章 (ぶんしょう) \"text, prose\", which is about the wording. 書類 refers more to concrete papers and forms in a folder; 文書 is more general and more formal.","examples":[{"japanese":"約束の内容を文書に残しました。","romaji":"Yakusoku no naiyō o bunsho ni nokoshimashita.","german":"We put the agreement in writing.","furigana":"やくそく|ないよう|ぶんしょ|のこ"},{"japanese":"重要な文書は安全な場所に保管します。","romaji":"Jūyō na bunsho wa anzen na basho ni hokan shimasu.","german":"We keep important documents in a safe place.","furigana":"じゅうよう|ぶんしょ|あんぜん|ばしょ|ほかん"},{"japanese":"ご質問への回答は文書でお送りします。","romaji":"Goshitsumon e no kaitou wa bunsho de ookuri shimasu.","german":"We will send you the answer to your question in writing.","furigana":"しつもん|かいとう|ぶんしょ|おく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"返済 is the \"repayment\" of borrowed money, loans or debts: 借金を返済する, ローンの返済, 返済期限 \"repayment deadline\". In everyday speech people simply say お金を返す. Do not confuse it with 返却 (return of objects, e.g. books) and 返金 (refund of a purchase price by the seller).","examples":[{"japanese":"借りたお金を毎月返済しています。","romaji":"Karita okane o maitsuki hensai shite imasu.","german":"I repay the borrowed money every month.","furigana":"か|かね|まいつきへんさい"},{"japanese":"返済の計画を立てました。","romaji":"Hensai no keikaku o tatemashita.","german":"I drew up a repayment plan.","furigana":"へんさい|けいかく|た"},{"japanese":"住宅ローンの返済があと十年残っています。","romaji":"Juutaku roon no hensai ga ato juunen nokotte imasu.","german":"I still have to pay off the home loan for ten more years.","furigana":"じゅうたく|へんさい|じゅうねんのこ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"返答 is an \"answer, reply\" to a question, inquiry or request: 返答する, 返答に困る \"to not know what to answer\", 返答を待つ. In everyday speech 返事 is more usual (also for letters and emails); 回答 is the formal answer to an inquiry or survey, and 答え is the solution to a question or task. 返答 sounds somewhat more formal than 返事.","examples":[{"japanese":"質問への返答を待っています。","romaji":"Shitsumon e no hentō o matte imasu.","german":"I am waiting for an answer to the question.","furigana":"しつもん|へんとう|ま"},{"japanese":"すぐには返答できませんでした。","romaji":"Sugu ni wa hentō dekimasen deshita.","german":"I could not answer right away.","furigana":"へんとう"},{"japanese":"面接で返答に困る質問をされました。","romaji":"Mensetsu de hentou ni komaru shitsumon o saremashita.","german":"In the job interview, I was asked a question I had no answer to.","furigana":"めんせつ|へんとう|こま|しつもん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"放置 means to \"leave lying, leave to itself\" something one should take care of - usually with criticism: 問題を放置する, 自転車を放置する, 放置自転車 \"illegally parked bicycles\". In conversation people prefer 放っておく or ほったらかす. Unlike そのままにする (\"to leave as is\", neutral), 放置 stresses neglect of a duty.","examples":[{"japanese":"問題を放置すると悪化します。","romaji":"Mondai o hōchi suru to akka shimasu.","german":"If you leave the problem alone, it will get worse.","furigana":"もんだい|ほうち|あっか"},{"japanese":"道路に自転車を放置しないでください。","romaji":"Dōro ni jitensha o hōchi shinaide kudasai.","german":"Please do not leave bicycles standing in the street.","furigana":"どうろ|じてんしゃ|ほうち"},{"japanese":"車内に子どもを放置するのは絶対にやめてください。","romaji":"Shanai ni kodomo o houchi suru no wa zettai ni yamete kudasai.","german":"Never leave children alone in a car.","furigana":"しゃない|こ|ほうち|ぜったい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"本文 is the \"main text\" of a book, article or letter as opposed to the title, subject line, footnotes or appendix; for emails it is the \"body\" (メールの本文). In exams, 本文 stands for \"the text at hand\" (本文の内容と合うもの). Do not confuse it with 文章 (\"text, wording\" in general) and 本題 (\"the actual topic\").","examples":[{"japanese":"本文を読んで問いに答えてください。","romaji":"Honbun o yonde toi ni kotaete kudasai.","german":"Read the passage and answer the questions.","furigana":"ほんぶん|よ|と|こた"},{"japanese":"詳しい説明は本文に書いてあります。","romaji":"Kuwashii setsumei wa honbun ni kaite arimasu.","german":"The detailed explanation is in the main text.","furigana":"くわ|せつめい|ほんぶん|か"},{"japanese":"メールの本文に名前を書き忘れました。","romaji":"Meeru no honbun ni namae o kakiwasuremashita.","german":"I forgot to write my name in the body of the email.","furigana":"ほんぶん|なまえ|か|わす"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"前もって means \"in advance, beforehand\" and stands before a verb, often together with 〜ておく: 前もって調べておく, 前もって知らせる. It is neutral to slightly colloquial; synonymous but more formal are あらかじめ and 事前に (事前に連絡する). It is usually written in mixed kana and kanji as 前もって.","examples":[{"japanese":"人数を前もって知らせてください。","romaji":"Ninzū o maemotte shirasete kudasai.","german":"Please let us know the number of people beforehand.","furigana":"にんずう|まえ|し"},{"japanese":"前もって調べておいたので迷いませんでした。","romaji":"Maemotte shirabete oita node mayoimasen deshita.","german":"I had researched beforehand, so I didn't get lost.","furigana":"まえ|しら|まよ"},{"japanese":"前もって連絡してくれれば、駅まで迎えに行きますよ。","romaji":"Maemotte renraku shite kurereba, eki made mukae ni ikimasu yo.","german":"If you tell me in advance, I'll pick you up at the station.","furigana":"まえ|れんらく|えき|むか|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"満たす means \"to fill\" (容器を水で満たす) and figuratively \"to fulfill, to satisfy\": 条件を満たす \"to meet conditions\", 基準を満たす, お腹を満たす \"to satisfy one's hunger\", 欲求を満たす. The intransitive counterpart is 満ちる \"to be full, to be filled\" (自信に満ちる). The passive 満たされる also means \"to feel fulfilled\". The phrase 〜に満たない \"less than\" comes from the related 満つ.","examples":[{"japanese":"応募の条件をすべて満たしています。","romaji":"Ōbo no jōken o subete mitashite imasu.","german":"I meet all the application requirements.","furigana":"おうぼ|じょうけん|み"},{"japanese":"容器を水で満たしてください。","romaji":"Yōki o mizu de mitashite kudasai.","german":"Please fill the container with water.","furigana":"ようき|みず|み"},{"japanese":"温かいスープでお腹を満たしました。","romaji":"Atatakai suupu de onaka o mitashimashita.","german":"I satisfied my hunger with a hot soup.","furigana":"あたた|なか|み"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"身近 means \"close to one's own life, familiar, everyday\" - both spatially and personally: 身近な人 \"people in one's own circle\", 身近な問題, 身近に感じる \"to feel close\". As a na-adjective: 身近な, 身近に. Unlike 近く (purely spatial) and 親しい (\"closely befriended\"), 身近 stresses that something belongs to one's own daily life. The kana spelling is みぢか, not みじか.","examples":[{"japanese":"身近な問題から考えてみましょう。","romaji":"Mijika na mondai kara kangaete mimashō.","german":"Let's start with problems from everyday life.","furigana":"みぢか|もんだい|かんが"},{"japanese":"困った時は身近な人に相談してください。","romaji":"Komatta toki wa mijika na hito ni sōdan shite kudasai.","german":"If you have trouble, talk to someone close to you.","furigana":"こま|とき|みぢか|ひと|そうだん"},{"japanese":"スマートフォンは今や最も身近な道具です。","romaji":"Sumaatofon wa ima ya mottomo mijika na dougu desu.","german":"The smartphone is now our most familiar everyday tool.","furigana":"いま|もっと|みぢか|どうぐ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"未定 means \"not yet determined, open\" and usually appears as the predicate 〜は未定です or as 未定の. Typical for announcements and plans: 日程未定, 発売日は未定, 詳細は未定. The prefix 未 means \"not yet\" (cf. 未定, 未完成, 未経験). The opposite is 決定 or 確定; in conversation people also say まだ決まっていない.","examples":[{"japanese":"次の会議の日程は未定です。","romaji":"Tsugi no kaigi no nittei wa mitei desu.","german":"The date of the next meeting has not been decided yet.","furigana":"つぎ|かいぎ|にってい|みてい"},{"japanese":"参加人数が未定なので予約できません。","romaji":"Sanka ninzū ga mitei na node yoyaku dekimasen.","german":"The number of participants is still open, so I can't make a reservation.","furigana":"さんかにんずう|みてい|よやく"},{"japanese":"新作の発売日は未定ですが、決まり次第お知らせします。","romaji":"Shinsaku no hatsubaibi wa mitei desu ga, kimari shidai oshirase shimasu.","german":"The release date of the new title has not been decided; we will inform you once it is.","furigana":"しんさく|はつばいび|みてい|き|しだい|し"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"無断 means \"without permission\" or \"without prior notice\". It appears as 無断で before an action (無断で休む) or as a prefix in fixed terms: 無断欠勤 \"unexcused absence\", 無断駐車, 無断転載禁止 \"reprinting without permission prohibited\". The tone is always critical. More neutral would be 許可なく or 連絡せずに.","examples":[{"japanese":"無断で写真を撮らないでください。","romaji":"Mudan de shashin o toranaide kudasai.","german":"Please do not take photos without permission.","furigana":"むだん|しゃしん|と"},{"japanese":"彼は無断で仕事を休みました。","romaji":"Kare wa mudan de shigoto o yasumimashita.","german":"He stayed away from work without notice.","furigana":"かれ|むだん|しごと|やす"},{"japanese":"このサイトの画像の無断転載を禁止します。","romaji":"Kono saito no gazou no mudan tensai o kinshi shimasu.","german":"Redistributing the images on this website without permission is prohibited.","furigana":"がぞう|むだんてんさい|きんし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"模索 means to \"feel one's way, search for a solution\" when the answer is still unclear: 解決策を模索する, 方向性を模索する, 模索が続く. Well known is the four-character idiom 暗中模索 \"groping in the dark\". Unlike 探す (searching for something concrete), it concerns abstract ways and possibilities; 試行錯誤, by contrast, stresses trying things out by trial and error. Typical of news and reports.","examples":[{"japanese":"新しい解決策を模索しています。","romaji":"Atarashii kaiketsusaku o mosaku shite imasu.","german":"We are searching for new ways to solve it.","furigana":"あたら|かいけつさく|もさく"},{"japanese":"地域と協力する方法を模索しました。","romaji":"Chiiki to kyōryoku suru hōhō o mosaku shimashita.","german":"We explored possibilities for cooperation with the region.","furigana":"ちいき|きょうりょく|ほうほう|もさく"},{"japanese":"卒業後の進路をまだ模索しているところです。","romaji":"Sotsugyougo no shinro o mada mosaku shite iru tokoro desu.","german":"I am still looking for my path for after graduation.","furigana":"そつぎょうご|しんろ|もさく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"有力 means, on the one hand, \"influential, powerful\" (有力者, 有力企業), and on the other \"promising, convincing\": 有力な候補, 有力な説, 有力な手がかり \"a promising lead\". The passive 有力視される means \"to be considered promising\". Do not confuse it with 有能 (\"capable, competent\"), which refers to personal competence, not to power or likelihood.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は有力な候補の一人です。","romaji":"Kanojo wa yūryoku na kōho no hitori desu.","german":"She is one of the leading candidates.","furigana":"かのじょ|ゆうりょく|こうほ|ひとり"},{"japanese":"事故の原因について有力な説が示されました。","romaji":"Jiko no gen'in ni tsuite yūryoku na setsu ga shimesaremashita.","german":"A plausible explanation of the cause of the accident was presented.","furigana":"じこ|げんいん|ゆうりょく|せつ|しめ"},{"japanese":"警察は犯人につながる有力な手がかりを得ました。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa hannin ni tsunagaru yuuryoku na tegakari o emashita.","german":"The police have found a promising lead on the culprit.","furigana":"けいさつ|はんにん|ゆうりょく|て|え"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"要望 is a \"wish, request\" addressed to an organization or those in charge: 要望を出す, 要望に応える, お客様のご要望. As a する-verb: 改善を要望する. It is stronger than 希望 (a personal wish) but less demanding than 要求 (\"demand\", often with pressure). For a concrete request to do something, people say 依頼.","examples":[{"japanese":"利用者の要望を集めました。","romaji":"Riyōsha no yōbō o atsumemashita.","german":"We collected the users' requests.","furigana":"りようしゃ|ようぼう|あつ"},{"japanese":"住民は道路の改善を要望しています。","romaji":"Jūmin wa dōro no kaizen o yōbō shite imasu.","german":"The residents are calling for improvements to the road.","furigana":"じゅうみん|どうろ|かいぜん|ようぼう"},{"japanese":"お客様のご要望にお応えして、営業時間を延長しました。","romaji":"Okyakusama no goyoubou ni okotae shite, eigyou jikan o enchou shimashita.","german":"At our customers' request, we extended the opening hours.","furigana":"きゃくさま|ようぼう|こた|えいぎょうじかん|えんちょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"余地 literally means \"free space\", but is mostly used figuratively for \"leeway, room for something\". Typical frames: 〜の余地がある/ない - 改善の余地 \"room for improvement\", 疑いの余地はない \"there is no room for doubt\", 選択の余地がない \"to have no choice\", 議論の余地 \"open to debate\". Also with a verb: 入る余地がない \"no room to get in\". Compared with スペース or 空き, 余地 stresses the possibility of still fitting something in or doing something.","examples":[{"japanese":"この計画には改善の余地があります。","romaji":"Kono keikaku ni wa kaizen no yochi ga arimasu.","german":"There is room for improvement in this plan.","furigana":"けいかく|かいぜん|よち"},{"japanese":"その結果には疑いの余地がありません。","romaji":"Sono kekka ni wa utagai no yochi ga arimasen.","german":"There is no doubt about this result.","furigana":"けっか|うたが|よち"},{"japanese":"駐車場には、もう車を止める余地がありません。","romaji":"Chuushajou ni wa, mou kuruma o tomeru yochi ga arimasen.","german":"There is no room left in the parking lot for another car.","furigana":"ちゅうしゃじょう|くるま|と|よち"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"了承 means \"to take note of and agree\": 了承を得る \"to obtain approval\", 提案を了承する. Very common in notices is ご了承ください \"We ask for your understanding\" - one announces something the reader has to accept. Compared with 承認 (official approval), 了承 is less formal; 了解 is more a brief \"understood\" among equals, and 承知 is the humble form (承知しました).","examples":[{"japanese":"予定の変更について了承を得ました。","romaji":"Yotei no henkō ni tsuite ryōshō o emashita.","german":"We received approval for the schedule change.","furigana":"よてい|へんこう|りょうしょう|え"},{"japanese":"返信に時間がかかる場合がありますので、ご了承ください。","romaji":"Henshin ni jikan ga kakaru baai ga arimasu node, goryōshō kudasai.","german":"Please understand that a reply may take some time.","furigana":"へんしん|じかん|ばあい|りょうしょう"},{"japanese":"部長は私の提案を了承してくれました。","romaji":"Buchou wa watashi no teian o ryoushou shite kuremashita.","german":"The department manager agreed to my proposal.","furigana":"ぶちょう|わたし|ていあん|りょうしょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"両立 means that two things are \"compatible with each other\" or are both realized at the same time: AとBが両立する (intransitive) or AとBを両立させる; in everyday speech you also hear AとBを両立する. Classic themes are 仕事と家庭の両立, 勉強と部活の両立 and 経済と環境の両立. 両立しない means \"incompatible\". Compared with 共存 (\"coexistence\"), 両立 stresses successfully balancing the two.","examples":[{"japanese":"仕事と勉強を両立しています。","romaji":"Shigoto to benkyō o ryōritsu shite imasu.","german":"I combine work and study.","furigana":"しごと|べんきょう|りょうりつ"},{"japanese":"安全と便利さの両立を目指します。","romaji":"Anzen to benrisa no ryōritsu o mezashimasu.","german":"We aim to achieve both safety and comfort.","furigana":"あんぜん|べんり|りょうりつ|めざ"},{"japanese":"子育てと仕事を両立させるのは簡単ではありません。","romaji":"Kosodate to shigoto o ryouritsu saseru no wa kantan de wa arimasen.","german":"Balancing child-rearing and a career is not easy.","furigana":"こそだ|しごと|りょうりつ|かんたん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"労力 is the \"work and effort\" one puts into something: 労力を費やす, 労力がかかる, 労力を惜しまない \"to spare no effort\", 時間と労力. Unlike 努力 (\"effort, striving\" as a positive attitude), 労力 refers more to the labor expended as a cost factor; 手間 is the bothersome time and effort on a small scale. 労働力, by contrast, means the \"labor force\" of a society.","examples":[{"japanese":"完成まで多くの労力を費やしました。","romaji":"Kansei made ōku no rōryoku o tsuiyashimashita.","german":"I put a lot of work in until it was finished.","furigana":"かんせい|おお|ろうりょく|つい"},{"japanese":"この方法なら時間と労力を節約できます。","romaji":"Kono hōhō nara jikan to rōryoku o setsuyaku dekimasu.","german":"This method saves time and effort.","furigana":"ほうほう|じかん|ろうりょく|せつやく"},{"japanese":"彼はチームのためなら労力を惜しみません。","romaji":"Kare wa chiimu no tame nara rouryoku o oshimimasen.","german":"He spares no effort for the team.","furigana":"かれ|ろうりょく|お"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"じっくり (also じっくりと) means \"calmly and thoroughly\", without haste and with full attention: じっくり考える, じっくり話し合う, じっくり読む, and in cooking じっくり煮込む \"to simmer for a long time\". Unlike ゆっくり (\"slowly, leisurely\"), じっくり stresses care and depth; しっかり means more \"properly, firmly\". Typical of conversation, advertising and recipes.","examples":[{"japanese":"将来についてじっくり考えました。","romaji":"Shōrai ni tsuite jikkuri kangaemashita.","german":"I thought carefully about my future.","furigana":"しょうらい|かんが"},{"japanese":"今度はじっくり話し合いましょう。","romaji":"Kondo wa jikkuri hanashiaimashō.","german":"Let's discuss it calmly next time.","furigana":"こんど|はな|あ"},{"japanese":"弱火で一時間じっくり煮込みます。","romaji":"Yowabi de ichijikan jikkuri nikomimasu.","german":"You let it simmer over low heat for an hour.","furigana":"よわび|いちじかん|にこ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"てっきり expresses that one \"was firmly convinced\" - and was mistaken. It almost always appears with 〜と思っていた/〜と思った; the correction often follows with のに, けど or 〜たら: てっきり〜と思ったら, 違った. Unlike きっと (a conjecture about the future), てっきり looks back on a wrong assumption. Spoken language, written in kana without kanji.","examples":[{"japanese":"てっきり今日は休みだと思っていました。","romaji":"Tekkiri kyō wa yasumi da to omotte imashita.","german":"I was sure that today was a day off.","furigana":"きょう|やす|おも"},{"japanese":"彼が来たので、てっきり会議があるのだと思いました。","romaji":"Kare ga kita node, tekkiri kaigi ga aru no da to omoimashita.","german":"Since he came, I assumed there was a meeting.","furigana":"かれ|き|かいぎ|おも"},{"japanese":"てっきり怒られると思ったら、褒められました。","romaji":"Tekkiri okorareru to omottara, homeraremashita.","german":"I was sure I would be scolded, but I was praised instead.","furigana":"おこ|おも|ほ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"遅くとも means \"at the latest\" and stands before a time expression with までに or には: 遅くとも金曜日までに, 遅くとも八時には. It is somewhat more literary; in conversation people often say 遅くても. The counterpart is 早くとも/早くても \"at the earliest\". For deadlines in official and business language, 〜までに必ず is also common.","examples":[{"japanese":"遅くとも金曜日までに返事をします。","romaji":"Osokutomo kinyōbi made ni henji o shimasu.","german":"I will reply by Friday at the latest.","furigana":"おそ|きんようび|へんじ"},{"japanese":"遅くとも八時には出発しなければなりません。","romaji":"Osokutomo hachiji ni wa shuppatsu shinakereba narimasen.","german":"We have to leave by eight at the latest.","furigana":"おそ|はちじ|しゅっぱつ"},{"japanese":"遅くとも開演の十分前には席に着いてください。","romaji":"Osokutomo kaien no juppun mae ni wa seki ni tsuite kudasai.","german":"Please take your seat at least ten minutes before the performance starts.","furigana":"おそ|かいえん|じゅっぷんまえ|せき|つ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"組み合わせる means to \"combine, put together\" several elements: 色を組み合わせる, AとBを組み合わせる. The noun 組み合わせ means \"combination\" and, in tournaments, also \"pairing\"; accordingly the verb can mean \"to draw against each other\". Unlike 合わせる (\"to put together, to match\"), 組み合わせる stresses deliberately assembling different parts into a whole. Ichidan verb.","examples":[{"japanese":"二つの方法を組み合わせて学習します。","romaji":"Futatsu no hōhō o kumiawasete gakushū shimasu.","german":"I combine two methods when studying.","furigana":"ふた|ほうほう|く|あ|がくしゅう"},{"japanese":"色を自由に組み合わせてください。","romaji":"Iro o jiyū ni kumiawasete kudasai.","german":"Combine the colors as you like.","furigana":"いろ|じゆう|く|あ"},{"japanese":"抽選の結果、一回戦で強いチーム同士が組み合わされました。","romaji":"Chuusen no kekka, ikkaisen de tsuyoi chiimu doushi ga kumiawasaremashita.","german":"Because of the draw, two strong teams meet already in the first round.","furigana":"ちゅうせん|けっか|いっかいせん|つよ|どうし|く|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"組み込む means to insert something as a fixed component into a larger whole: AをBに組み込む (\"to build A into B\"), for example breaks into a schedule, costs into a budget or a function into a device. There is no separate intransitive counterpart; the passive 組み込まれる (\"to be built in\") is used. The noun 組み込み stands in technology for \"embedded\" (組み込みソフト). 取り入れる stresses adopting ideas from outside, 含める mere inclusion in a count.","examples":[{"japanese":"休憩時間を予定に組み込みました。","romaji":"Kyūkei jikan o yotei ni kumikomimashita.","german":"I built breaks into the schedule.","furigana":"きゅうけいじかん|よてい|く|こ"},{"japanese":"新しい機能を製品に組み込みます。","romaji":"Atarashii kinō o seihin ni kumikomimasu.","german":"We are integrating a new feature into the product.","furigana":"あたら|きのう|せいひん|く|こ"},{"japanese":"この費用はすでに予算に組み込まれています。","romaji":"Kono hiyou wa sude ni yosan ni kumikomarete imasu.","german":"These costs are already included in the budget.","furigana":"ひよう|よさん|く|こ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"深める is the transitive verb of 深い and is used almost only for abstract things: 理解を深める, 知識を深める, 交流を深める, 絆を深める, but also negatively 疑いを深める (\"to strengthen suspicion\"). The intransitive counterpart is 深まる (理解が深まる, 秋が深まる). For a concrete hole or basin, people prefer 深くする or 掘り下げる. The tone is neutral to slightly literary.","examples":[{"japanese":"議論を通して理解を深めました。","romaji":"Giron o tōshite rikai o fukamemashita.","german":"Through the discussion, I deepened my understanding.","furigana":"ぎろん|とお|りかい|ふか"},{"japanese":"地域との交流を深めたいです。","romaji":"Chiiki to no kōryū o fukametai desu.","german":"We would like to intensify exchange with the region.","furigana":"ちいき|こうりゅう|ふか"},{"japanese":"彼のあいまいな説明は、警察の疑いをさらに深めた。","romaji":"Kare no aimai na setsumei wa, keisatsu no utagai o sara ni fukameta.","german":"His vague explanation only strengthened the police's suspicion.","furigana":"かれ|せつめい|けいさつ|うたが|ふか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"明かす has two main uses: revealing something hidden, typically with 秘密, 本当の理由, 正体, 胸の内 or 手品の種 as the object; and 夜を明かす or 一夜を明かす for \"to spend the night (awake)\". 打ち明ける stresses confiding in someone, 明らかにする the factual clarification of facts, and ばらす is colloquial blabbing. Do not confuse it with the intransitive 明ける (夜が明ける \"day breaks\").","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は初めて本当の理由を明かしました。","romaji":"Kare wa hajimete hontō no riyū o akashimashita.","german":"He revealed the real reason for the first time.","furigana":"かれ|はじ|ほんとう|りゆう|あ"},{"japanese":"友人と語り合って夜を明かしました。","romaji":"Yūjin to katariatte yo o akashimashita.","german":"I spent the night talking with a friend.","furigana":"ゆうじん|かた|あ|よ|あ"},{"japanese":"マジシャンは最後に手品の種を明かした。","romaji":"Majishan wa saigo ni tejina no tane o akashita.","german":"In the end, the magician revealed how the trick works.","furigana":"さいご|てじな|たね|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"値する almost always appears in the frame \"noun + に値する\": 賞賛に値する, 注目に値する, 一読に値する, 検討に値する; negated 信用に値しない (\"not trustworthy\"). It is intransitive and elevated, written-language; in everyday speech people prefer 〜の価値がある or 〜する価値がある. It conjugates like する (値します, 値しない) and is often used attributively before a noun (検討に値する提案).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼の努力は賞賛に値します。","romaji":"Kare no doryoku wa shōsan ni ataishimasu.","german":"His effort deserves praise.","furigana":"かれ|どりょく|しょうさん|あたい"},{"japanese":"これは検討に値する提案です。","romaji":"Kore wa kentō ni atai suru teian desu.","german":"This proposal is worth considering.","furigana":"けんとう|あたい|ていあん"},{"japanese":"彼の証言は信用に値しない。","romaji":"Kare no shougen wa shin'you ni ataishinai.","german":"His testimony is not credible.","furigana":"かれ|しょうげん|しんよう|あたい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"重んじる means to attach great weight to something or some value: 伝統を重んじる, 礼儀を重んじる, 経験を重んじる, 和を重んじる. It is more formal than 大切にする; for other people's opinions or rights, 尊重する is even more common. The opposite is 軽んじる (\"to belittle\"). The literary variant 重んずる means the same.","examples":[{"japanese":"この会社は経験を重んじています。","romaji":"Kono kaisha wa keiken o omonjite imasu.","german":"This company places great value on experience.","furigana":"かいしゃ|けいけん|おも"},{"japanese":"相手の意見を重んじる姿勢が大切です。","romaji":"Aite no iken o omonjiru shisei ga taisetsu desu.","german":"It is important to respect the other person's opinion.","furigana":"あいて|いけん|おも|しせい|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"茶道では礼儀作法が重んじられる。","romaji":"Sadou de wa reigi sahou ga omonjirareru.","german":"In the tea ceremony, great importance is placed on etiquette.","furigana":"さどう|れいぎさほう|おも"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"本名 (read ほんみょう) is a person's real, official name, as opposed to 芸名 (\"stage name\"), ペンネーム, 偽名 (\"false name\") or 通称 (\"commonly used name\"). Typical are 本名を明かす, 本名を公表する and 本名で活動する. On forms, 氏名 is usually used instead; 本名 stresses that there is also another name.","examples":[{"japanese":"作家は本名を公表していません。","romaji":"Sakka wa honmyō o kōhyō shite imasen.","german":"The author has not published her real name.","furigana":"さっか|ほんみょう|こうひょう"},{"japanese":"申請には本名を使ってください。","romaji":"Shinsei ni wa honmyō o tsukatte kudasai.","german":"Please use your real name for the application.","furigana":"しんせい|ほんみょう|つか"},{"japanese":"その歌手の本名は意外と普通だ。","romaji":"Sono kashu no honmyou wa igai to futsuu da.","german":"This singer's real name is surprisingly ordinary.","furigana":"かしゅ|ほんみょう|いがい|ふつう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"履歴 refers, on the one hand, to a personal career history, above all in the compound 履歴書 (\"résumé\"); for the career itself people mostly say 経歴, 学歴 or 職歴. Today 履歴 is mainly encountered as stored history: 閲覧履歴, 検索履歴, 通話履歴, 購入履歴, 変更履歴. Fitting phrases are 履歴を確認する, 履歴を消す and 履歴が残る.","examples":[{"japanese":"履歴書に職歴を記入しました。","romaji":"Rirekisho ni shokureki o kinyū shimashita.","german":"I put my work history in my résumé.","furigana":"りれきしょ|しょくれき|きにゅう"},{"japanese":"購入履歴を確認できます。","romaji":"Kōnyū rireki o kakunin dekimasu.","german":"You can view your past purchases.","furigana":"こうにゅうりれき|かくにん"},{"japanese":"パソコンの閲覧履歴を削除しました。","romaji":"Pasokon no etsuran rireki o sakujo shimashita.","german":"I deleted the browser history on the computer.","furigana":"えつらんりれき|さくじょ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"アンケート (from French \"enquête\") is a written questionnaire or a survey of customers, users or participants. The organizer says アンケートを取る or アンケートを実施する, the respondents アンケートに答える or, politely, アンケートにご協力ください. For representative opinion polls in politics and the media, people say 世論調査 instead. アンケート用紙 and アンケート調査 are also common.","examples":[{"japanese":"利用者にアンケートを実施しました。","romaji":"Riyōsha ni ankēto o jisshi shimashita.","german":"We conducted a user survey.","furigana":"りようしゃ|じっし"},{"japanese":"アンケートの結果を改善に生かします。","romaji":"Ankēto no kekka o kaizen ni ikashimasu.","german":"We use the survey results for improvements.","furigana":"けっか|かいぜん|い"},{"japanese":"お帰りの前に、簡単なアンケートにご協力ください。","romaji":"Okaeri no mae ni, kantan na ankeeto ni go-kyouryoku kudasai.","german":"Please fill out our short questionnaire before you leave.","furigana":"かえ|まえ|かんたん|きょうりょく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"コミュニケーション in Japanese refers mainly to interpersonal exchange and getting along with one another, less to technical transmission (for that, 通信). Typical are コミュニケーションを取る (\"to communicate\"), コミュニケーション不足 and コミュニケーション能力 (colloquially shortened to コミュ力). More formal is 意思疎通 (\"mutual understanding\").","examples":[{"japanese":"職場でのコミュニケーションを大切にしています。","romaji":"Shokuba de no komyunikēshon o taisetsu ni shite imasu.","german":"We value communication in the workplace.","furigana":"しょくば|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"言葉だけがコミュニケーションの手段ではありません。","romaji":"Kotoba dake ga komyunikēshon no shudan de wa arimasen.","german":"Language is not the only means of communication.","furigana":"ことば|しゅだん"},{"japanese":"親子のコミュニケーションを取るために、毎晩一緒に夕食を食べます。","romaji":"Oyako no komyunikeeshon o toru tame ni, maiban issho ni yuushoku o tabemasu.","german":"To keep talking as a family, we eat together every evening.","furigana":"おやこ|と|まいばんいっしょ|ゆうしょく|た"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"コメント is a short remark, note or statement and can also be used as a する-verb: 〜についてコメントする. Typical are コメントを求める, コメントを書く/残す, SNSのコメント欄 and the refusal ノーコメント (\"no comment\"). 感想 is a personal impression and 意見 an opinion; コメント stresses brief feedback on something specific.","examples":[{"japanese":"記事についてコメントを求められました。","romaji":"Kiji ni tsuite komento o motomeraremashita.","german":"I was asked for a comment on the article.","furigana":"きじ|もと"},{"japanese":"具体的なコメントをいただき、修正しました。","romaji":"Gutaiteki na komento o itadaki, shūsei shimashita.","german":"I received concrete feedback and revised the text.","furigana":"ぐたいてき|しゅうせい"},{"japanese":"動画にたくさんのコメントが付きました。","romaji":"Douga ni takusan no komento ga tsukimashita.","german":"The video received many comments.","furigana":"どうが|つ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"トラブル covers technical malfunctions (機械のトラブル), disputes (近所トラブル, 金銭トラブル) and, in cosmetics, complaints (肌トラブル). Typical are トラブルが起きる, トラブルを起こす, トラブルになる and トラブルに巻き込まれる. 問題 is more general (also exam questions), and 故障 means only a device's breakdown. For \"I'm in trouble\" people don't say トラブル but 困っている.","examples":[{"japanese":"機械のトラブルで作業が止まりました。","romaji":"Kikai no toraburu de sagyō ga tomarimashita.","german":"Work came to a standstill because of a machine malfunction.","furigana":"きかい|さぎょう|と"},{"japanese":"契約内容を確認してトラブルを防ぎます。","romaji":"Keiyaku naiyō o kakunin shite toraburu o fusegimasu.","german":"We are reviewing the contract to avoid problems.","furigana":"けいやくないよう|かくにん|ふせ"},{"japanese":"騒音をめぐって隣人とトラブルになった。","romaji":"Souon o megutte rinjin to toraburu ni natta.","german":"I got into trouble with the neighbor because of the noise.","furigana":"そうおん|りんじん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"ニュアンス is the fine shade of meaning or undertone of an utterance. Typical are ニュアンスが違う, 微妙なニュアンス, ニュアンスを伝える/つかむ and \"〜というニュアンスで言う\" (\"to mean something in the sense of ...\"). 意味 is the basic meaning, 含み the hidden implication; ニュアンス stresses the difference between similar expressions.","examples":[{"japanese":"この二つの言葉はニュアンスが違います。","romaji":"Kono futatsu no kotoba wa nyuansu ga chigaimasu.","german":"These two words differ in nuance.","furigana":"ふた|ことば|ちが"},{"japanese":"翻訳では細かいニュアンスが伝わりにくいです。","romaji":"Hon'yaku de wa komakai nyuansu ga tsutawarinikui desu.","german":"Subtle nuances are hard to convey in translation.","furigana":"ほんやく|こま|つた"},{"japanese":"怒っているというニュアンスで言ったわけではありません。","romaji":"Okotte iru to iu nyuansu de itta wake de wa arimasen.","german":"It wasn't supposed to sound angry.","furigana":"おこ|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"メッセージ is both a concrete message (メッセージを送る, メッセージを残す, via a chat app or answering machine) and the message conveyed by a work or speech (作品に込められたメッセージ, メッセージを発信する). 伝言, by contrast, is a message passed on orally via a third person, and メール specifically an email.","examples":[{"japanese":"留守番電話にメッセージを残しました。","romaji":"Rusuban denwa ni messēji o nokoshimashita.","german":"I left a message on the answering machine.","furigana":"るすばんでんわ|のこ"},{"japanese":"この作品には強いメッセージが込められています。","romaji":"Kono sakuhin ni wa tsuyoi messēji ga komerarete imasu.","german":"This work conveys a strong message.","furigana":"さくひん|つよ|こ"},{"japanese":"スマホで友達にメッセージを送りました。","romaji":"Sumaho de tomodachi ni messeeji o okurimashita.","german":"I sent my friend a message on his mobile phone.","furigana":"ともだち|おく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"オンライン is used adverbially with で (オンラインで申し込む, オンラインで受講する) or as a modifier before nouns: オンライン会議, オンライン授業, オンラインショップ. Opposites are オフライン and, for meetings and classes, 対面 (\"in person\"). A verb オンラインする does not exist; people say, for example, オンラインにする or オンラインで行う.","examples":[{"japanese":"オンラインで授業を受けています。","romaji":"Onrain de jugyō o ukete imasu.","german":"I attend class online.","furigana":"じゅぎょう|う"},{"japanese":"申し込みはオンラインでも可能です。","romaji":"Mōshikomi wa onrain de mo kanō desu.","german":"Registration is also possible online.","furigana":"もう|こ|かのう"},{"japanese":"来週の打ち合わせは、対面ではなくオンラインにしましょう。","romaji":"Raishuu no uchiawase wa, taimen de wa naku onrain ni shimashou.","german":"Let's hold next week's meeting online instead of in person.","furigana":"らいしゅう|う|あ|たいめん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"ヒント is a hint that helps you find the solution yourself: ヒントを出す/あげる, ヒントをもらう. In a broader sense it is a suggestion or inspiration: 〜のヒントになる, 〜からヒントを得る. 手がかり is more a trace in an investigation, コツ a practical trick, and アドバイス direct advice.","examples":[{"japanese":"答えのヒントを一つください。","romaji":"Kotae no hinto o hitotsu kudasai.","german":"Please give me a hint for the solution.","furigana":"こた|ひと"},{"japanese":"友人の言葉が解決のヒントになりました。","romaji":"Yūjin no kotoba ga kaiketsu no hinto ni narimashita.","german":"A friend's words gave the decisive inspiration.","furigana":"ゆうじん|ことば|かいけつ"},{"japanese":"この商品は自然からヒントを得て開発されました。","romaji":"Kono shouhin wa shizen kara hinto o ete kaihatsu saremashita.","german":"This product was developed with nature as its model.","furigana":"しょうひん|しぜん|え|かいはつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"タイミング is the (right) point in time for an action: タイミングがいい/悪い, タイミングが合う, タイミングを逃す, タイミングを見計らう, いいタイミングで. Unlike 時期 (\"period, phase\"), it concerns the exact moment; チャンス stresses the favorable opportunity itself. Bad timing is expressed with タイミングが悪い.","examples":[{"japanese":"質問するタイミングを逃しました。","romaji":"Shitsumon suru taimingu o nogashimashita.","german":"I missed the right moment to ask.","furigana":"しつもん|のが"},{"japanese":"ちょうどいいタイミングで電話が来ました。","romaji":"Chōdo ii taimingu de denwa ga kimashita.","german":"The call came at exactly the right moment.","furigana":"でんわ|き"},{"japanese":"タイミングが悪くて、店は閉まったばかりでした。","romaji":"Taimingu ga warukute, mise wa shimatta bakari deshita.","german":"We had bad timing - the shop had just closed.","furigana":"わる|みせ|し"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"ファイル is both a computer file (ファイルを開く, ファイルを保存する, 添付ファイル) and a folder or binder for papers (クリアファイル \"clear file folder\"). As a する-verb, ファイルする means \"to file away\". For the folder on a computer one says フォルダ, not ファイル.","examples":[{"japanese":"資料を一つのファイルにまとめました。","romaji":"Shiryō o hitotsu no fairu ni matomemashita.","german":"I compiled the materials into a single file.","furigana":"しりょう|ひと"},{"japanese":"古いファイルを開いて内容を確認しました。","romaji":"Furui fairu o hiraite naiyō o kakunin shimashita.","german":"I opened the old file and checked the contents.","furigana":"ふる|ひら|ないよう|かくにん"},{"japanese":"書類をクリアファイルに入れて持ってきてください。","romaji":"Shorui o kuria fairu ni irete motte kite kudasai.","german":"Please bring the documents in a clear file folder.","furigana":"しょるい|い|も"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 42 (source: N4; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"In daily life フロント is above all the hotel front desk: フロントに預ける, フロントに電話する, フロントで聞く. For the reception in companies, clinics or hospitals, one says 受付 instead. In addition, フロント also means \"front\" (weather front, front side, e.g. フロントガラス \"windshield\") and, in sports, the management of a club.","examples":[{"japanese":"部屋の鍵をフロントに預けました。","romaji":"Heya no kagi o furonto ni azukemashita.","german":"I left the room key at the front desk.","furigana":"へや|かぎ|あず"},{"japanese":"困った時はフロントに連絡してください。","romaji":"Komatta toki wa furonto ni renraku shite kudasai.","german":"If you have any trouble, please contact the front desk.","furigana":"こま|とき|れんらく"},{"japanese":"フロントにモーニングコールを頼みました。","romaji":"Furonto ni mooningu kooru o tanomimashita.","german":"I asked the front desk for a wake-up call.","furigana":"たの"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 39 (source: N4; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"あやふや is a na-adjective: あやふやな記憶, 記憶があやふやだ. It describes above all uncertain memories, knowledge, answers or attitudes that cannot be relied on. あやふやにする means to deliberately leave something unresolved. 曖昧 is broader and can also mean an ambiguous wording; あやふや is somewhat more colloquial and stresses the lack of firmness.","examples":[{"japanese":"住所の記憶があやふやなので、もう一度確認します。","romaji":"Jūsho no kioku ga ayafuya na node, mō ichido kakunin shimasu.","german":"I don't remember the address for sure, so I'll check it again.","furigana":"じゅうしょ|きおく|いちどかくにん"},{"japanese":"責任の所在をあやふやにしたまま、会議が終わった。","romaji":"Sekinin no shozai o ayafuya ni shita mama, kaigi ga owatta.","german":"The meeting ended without clarifying who is responsible.","furigana":"せきにん|しょざい|かいぎ|お"},{"japanese":"あやふやな返事をしないで、はっきり言ってください。","romaji":"Ayafuya na henji o shinaide, hakkiri itte kudasai.","german":"Don't be evasive; please say it clearly.","furigana":"へんじ|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Intransitive godan verb: one does not get ahead because of clumsiness or delay (準備にもたつく). Related to the onomatopoeic もたもた.","examples":[{"japanese":"レジでお金を出すのにもたついて、後ろの人を待たせてしまった。","romaji":"Reji de okane o dasu no ni motatsuite, ushiro no hito o matasete shimatta.","german":"At the register I fumbled with the payment and kept the people behind me waiting.","furigana":"かね|だ|うし|ひと|ま"},{"japanese":"新しいシステムの導入がもたついている。","romaji":"Atarashii shisutemu no dōnyū ga motatsuite iru.","german":"The introduction of the new system is not making progress.","furigana":"あたら|どうにゅう"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"From 鯱 (しゃちほこ), the stiff fish figure on castle roofs: someone behaves in an overly formal way out of nervousness. Often used as しゃちほこばった. The kanji spelling 鯱張る is rare.","examples":[{"japanese":"面接では、しゃちほこばらずに自然に話してください。","romaji":"Mensetsu de wa, shachihokobarazu ni shizen ni hanashite kudasai.","german":"Don't be so stiff in the job interview; speak naturally.","furigana":"めんせつ|しぜん|はな"},{"japanese":"祖父はしゃちほこばった挨拶をして、みんなを笑わせた。","romaji":"Sofu wa shachihokobatta aisatsu o shite, minna o warawaseta.","german":"My grandfather greeted everyone in a stiff, formal way and made them laugh.","furigana":"そふ|あいさつ|わら"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"い-adjective for halting speech, reading or writing and unsteady steps, for example of children or learners: たどたどしい日本語.","examples":[{"japanese":"子どもがたどたどしい字で手紙を書いてくれた。","romaji":"Kodomo ga tadotadoshii ji de tegami o kaite kureta.","german":"The child wrote me a letter in clumsy handwriting.","furigana":"こ|じ|てがみ|か"},{"japanese":"彼はたどたどしい日本語で、一生懸命説明した。","romaji":"Kare wa tadotadoshii nihongo de, isshōkenmei setsumei shita.","german":"He explained it with great effort in halting Japanese.","furigana":"かれ|にほんご|いっしょうけんめいせつめい"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"な-adjective: ぶっきらぼうな返事; adverbially ぶっきらぼうに答える. It describes an unfriendly, terse way of speaking, often without ill intent.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼はぶっきらぼうだけど、本当は優しい人です。","romaji":"Kare wa bukkirabō dakedo, hontō wa yasashii hito desu.","german":"He is brusque, but actually a nice person.","furigana":"かれ|ほんとう|やさ|ひと"},{"japanese":"店員にぶっきらぼうに答えられて、少し嫌な気分になった。","romaji":"Ten'in ni bukkirabō ni kotaerarete, sukoshi iya na kibun ni natta.","german":"The salesperson answered me curtly, and I felt a bit uncomfortable.","furigana":"てんいん|こた|すこ|いや|きぶん"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"な-adjective: ぞんざいな言葉遣い; adverbially ぞんざいに扱う \"to treat carelessly\". It criticizes a lack of care or respect; not an onomatopoeic word.","examples":[{"japanese":"お客様にぞんざいな口のきき方をしてはいけません。","romaji":"Okyakusama ni zonzai na kuchi no kikikata o shite wa ikemasen.","german":"You must not talk to customers so roughly.","furigana":"きゃくさま|くち|かた"},{"japanese":"借りた本をぞんざいに扱わないでください。","romaji":"Karita hon o zonzai ni atsukawanaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't handle borrowed books carelessly.","furigana":"か|ほん|あつか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Literally \"a cry of the crane\": a single word from a powerful person ends a long discussion. Typical in companies and families.","examples":[{"japanese":"会議は長引いたが、社長の鶴の一声で決まった。","romaji":"Kaigi wa nagabiita ga, shachō no tsuru no hitokoe de kimatta.","german":"The meeting dragged on, but the boss's word settled the matter.","furigana":"かいぎ|ながび|しゃちょう|つる|ひとこえ|き"},{"japanese":"旅行先は祖父の鶴の一声で京都になった。","romaji":"Ryokōsaki wa sofu no tsuru no hitokoe de Kyōto ni natta.","german":"At a word from grandfather, Kyoto became the travel destination.","furigana":"りょこうさき|そふ|つる|ひとこえ|きょうと"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"From a Chinese anecdote: in a snake-drawing contest, the one who adds feet to the snake loses. Often used as a modest introduction: 蛇足ですが... (\"Just a small, perhaps superfluous addition ...\").","examples":[{"japanese":"蛇足ですが、一つ付け加えさせてください。","romaji":"Dasoku desu ga, hitotsu tsukekuwaesasete kudasai.","german":"It may be superfluous, but let me add one more thing.","furigana":"だそく|ひと|つ|くわ"},{"japanese":"最後の説明は蛇足だったかもしれない。","romaji":"Saigo no setsumei wa dasoku datta kamo shirenai.","german":"The last explanation was perhaps unnecessary.","furigana":"さいご|せつめい|だそく"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"From wrestling: grabbing the opponent's raised leg. It means to seize on someone's small slip of the tongue or wording error instead of engaging with the content.","examples":[{"japanese":"人の揚げ足を取るのはやめなさい。","romaji":"Hito no ageashi o toru no wa yamenasai.","german":"Stop holding every little slip against others.","furigana":"ひと|あ|あし|と"},{"japanese":"議論が揚げ足の取り合いになってしまった。","romaji":"Giron ga ageashi no toriai ni natte shimatta.","german":"The discussion degenerated into mutual nitpicking.","furigana":"ぎろん|あ|あし|と|あ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"After an initial success or advantage, one wants to try the same thing again - often with a slightly negative undertone (greed, recklessness). Often also written in kana: 味をしめる.","examples":[{"japanese":"一度宝くじが当たったのに味を占めて、毎週買っている。","romaji":"Ichido takarakuji ga atatta no ni aji o shimete, maishū katte iru.","german":"Since winning the lottery once, he has gotten a taste for it and buys tickets every week.","furigana":"いちどたから|あ|あじ|し|まいしゅうか"},{"japanese":"カラスは一度ごみをあさって味を占めると、何度も来る。","romaji":"Karasu wa ichido gomi o asatte aji o shimeru to, nando mo kuru.","german":"Once a crow has found food in the garbage, it keeps coming back.","furigana":"いちど|あじ|し|なんど|く"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"From the theater: an actor \"fits the boards\" (板 = stage). Today: a role, job or clothing looks natural and self-evident. Also written 板に付く.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女の先生ぶりもすっかり板についてきた。","romaji":"Kanojo no senseiburi mo sukkari ita ni tsuite kita.","german":"She has by now grown fully into her role as a teacher.","furigana":"かのじょ|せんせい|いた"},{"japanese":"新入社員はまだスーツ姿が板についていない。","romaji":"Shinnyū shain wa mada sūtsu sugata ga ita ni tsuite inai.","german":"The new employees don't yet look quite natural in suits.","furigana":"しんにゅうしゃいん|すがた|いた"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"In folk belief, the fox bewitches people. It describes confusion about something inexplicable, usually as 狐につままれたような顔／気分.","examples":[{"japanese":"財布が急に見つかって、狐につままれたような気分だ。","romaji":"Saifu ga kyū ni mitsukatte, kitsune ni tsumamareta yō na kibun da.","german":"The wallet suddenly turned up again - I feel bewitched.","furigana":"さいふ|きゅう|み|きつね|きぶん"},{"japanese":"説明を聞いても、みんな狐につままれたような顔をしていた。","romaji":"Setsumei o kiite mo, minna kitsune ni tsumamareta yō na kao o shite ita.","german":"Even after the explanation, everyone still looked completely baffled.","furigana":"せつめい|き|きつね|かお"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"To knit one's eyebrows - an expression of displeasure or concern about other people's behavior. Also written 眉を顰める.","examples":[{"japanese":"電車の中で大声で話す若者に、周りの人は眉をひそめた。","romaji":"Densha no naka de ōgoe de hanasu wakamono ni, mawari no hito wa mayu o hisometa.","german":"The other passengers frowned at the young people talking loudly on the train.","furigana":"でんしゃ|なか|おおごえ|はな|わかもの|まわ|ひと|まゆ"},{"japanese":"彼の失礼な言葉に、先生は眉をひそめた。","romaji":"Kare no shitsurei na kotoba ni, sensei wa mayu o hisometa.","german":"The teacher frowned at his rude words.","furigana":"かれ|しつれい|ことば|せんせい|まゆ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Literally \"a thousand differences, ten thousand variations\". It stresses the great diversity of opinions, tastes or people; similar to 十人十色.","examples":[{"japanese":"人の好みは千差万別だ。","romaji":"Hito no konomi wa sensa banbetsu da.","german":"Tastes are all different.","furigana":"ひと|この|せんさばんべつ"},{"japanese":"同じ質問でも、答えは千差万別だった。","romaji":"Onaji shitsumon demo, kotae wa sensa banbetsu datta.","german":"The same question drew completely different answers.","furigana":"おな|しつもん|こた|せんさばんべつ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Literally \"a sheep's head (displayed), dog meat (sold)\": the exterior promises more than the content delivers - for example in advertising or products. Written language.","examples":[{"japanese":"この広告は羊頭狗肉だと批判された。","romaji":"Kono kōkoku wa yōtō kuniku da to hihan sareta.","german":"This advertisement was criticized as false advertising.","furigana":"こうこく|ようとうくにく|ひはん"},{"japanese":"立派な名前の講座だったが、内容は羊頭狗肉だった。","romaji":"Rippa na namae no kōza datta ga, naiyō wa yōtō kuniku datta.","german":"The course had a grand name, but the content was all show and no substance.","furigana":"りっぱ|なまえ|こうざ|ないよう|ようとうくにく"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"A prenominal word (連体詞) used only before time expressions: 明くる日 (the next day), 明くる朝, 明くる年. Written language; in everyday speech people prefer 次の日 or 翌日.","examples":[{"japanese":"明くる日の朝、彼は早く家を出た。","romaji":"Akuru hi no asa, kare wa hayaku ie o deta.","german":"On the morning of the next day, he left the house early.","furigana":"あ|ひ|あさ|かれ|はや|いえ|で"},{"japanese":"明くる年、二人は結婚した。","romaji":"Akuru toshi, futari wa kekkon shita.","german":"The following year, the two of them married.","furigana":"あ|とし|ふたり|けっこん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The gas pedal in a car: アクセルを踏む (to step on the gas). Counterpart: ブレーキ. Figuratively also \"to push something forward\".","examples":[{"japanese":"信号が青になったので、アクセルを踏んだ。","romaji":"Shingō ga ao ni natta node, akuseru o funda.","german":"Since the light turned green, I hit the gas.","furigana":"しんごう|あお|ふ"},{"japanese":"アクセルとブレーキを間違えて事故が起きた。","romaji":"Akuseru to burēki o machigaete jiko ga okita.","german":"An accident occurred because the accelerator and brake were mixed up.","furigana":"まちが|じこ|お"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mostly as a suffix or with する: 給料アップ (pay raise), 売り上げがアップする. In film also a close-up (顔のアップ). Opposite: ダウン.","examples":[{"japanese":"来月から時給がアップする。","romaji":"Raigetsu kara jikyū ga appu suru.","german":"Starting next month, the hourly wage will go up.","furigana":"らいげつ|じきゅう"},{"japanese":"この写真は花をアップで撮ったものだ。","romaji":"Kono shashin wa hana o appu de totta mono da.","german":"This photo is a close-up of a flower.","furigana":"しゃしん|はな|と"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"An approach to tackling a problem (新しいアプローチ); as the verb アプローチする also \"to approach someone\" or \"to make advances toward someone\". In golf, the approach shot.","examples":[{"japanese":"この問題には別のアプローチが必要だ。","romaji":"Kono mondai ni wa betsu no apurōchi ga hitsuyō da.","german":"This problem calls for a different approach.","furigana":"もんだい|べつ|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"駅から美術館までのアプローチには桜並木が続いている。","romaji":"Eki kara bijutsukan made no apurōchi ni wa sakura namiki ga tsuzuite iru.","german":"The approach from the station to the museum runs along an avenue of cherry trees.","furigana":"えき|びじゅつかん|さくらなみき|つづ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Someone who does something not as a profession; short form アマ. Opposite: プロ.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼はアマチュアだが、プロ並みの腕を持っている。","romaji":"Kare wa amachua da ga, puro nami no ude o motte iru.","german":"He is an amateur, but has the skill of a professional.","furigana":"かれ|な|うで|も"},{"japanese":"アマチュアの写真コンテストに応募した。","romaji":"Amachua no shashin kontesuto ni ōbo shita.","german":"I entered a photo contest for amateurs.","furigana":"しゃしん|おうぼ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The Arabs and the Arab countries, often in compounds: アラブ諸国 (Arab states), アラブ人. Also a horse breed.","examples":[{"japanese":"アラブの国々では、アラビア語が話されている。","romaji":"Arabu no kuniguni de wa, arabiago ga hanasarete iru.","german":"Arabic is spoken in the Arab countries.","furigana":"くにぐに|ご|はな"},{"japanese":"彼はアラブの文化について研究している。","romaji":"Kare wa arabu no bunka ni tsuite kenkyū shite iru.","german":"He is researching Arab culture.","furigana":"かれ|ぶんか|けんきゅう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Chemistry: base, alkali. アルカリ性 (alkaline, basic); opposite: 酸 or 酸性.","examples":[{"japanese":"石けん水はアルカリ性だ。","romaji":"Sekkensui wa arukarisei da.","german":"Soapy water is alkaline.","furigana":"せっ|すい|せい"},{"japanese":"酸とアルカリを混ぜると中和する。","romaji":"San to arukari o mazeru to chūwa suru.","german":"When you mix an acid and an alkali, they neutralize each other.","furigana":"さん|ま|ちゅうわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"An encore at a concert; the audience calls out \"アンコール!\". Also the rerun of a popular program.","examples":[{"japanese":"観客のアンコールに応えて、もう一曲歌った。","romaji":"Kankyaku no ankōru ni kotaete, mō ikkyoku utatta.","german":"In response to the audience's calls for an encore, she sang one more song.","furigana":"かんきゃく|こた|いっきょくうた"},{"japanese":"コンサートの最後に、アンコールが二回あった。","romaji":"Konsāto no saigo ni, ankōru ga nikai atta.","german":"There were two encores at the end of the concert.","furigana":"さいご|にかい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The on- and off-ramp of an expressway, often shortened to IC: 次のインターチェンジで降りる.","examples":[{"japanese":"次のインターチェンジで高速道路を降りよう。","romaji":"Tsugi no intāchenji de kōsoku dōro o oriyō.","german":"Let's get off the expressway at the next interchange.","furigana":"つぎ|こうそくどうろ|お"},{"japanese":"インターチェンジの近くに大きな倉庫がある。","romaji":"Intāchenji no chikaku ni ōki na sōko ga aru.","german":"There is a large warehouse near the interchange.","furigana":"ちか|おお|そうこ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"な-adjective: インターナショナルな雰囲気. Often in names: インターナショナルスクール (international school). In everyday speech 国際的 is more common.","examples":[{"japanese":"子どもをインターナショナルスクールに通わせている。","romaji":"Kodomo o intānashonaru sukūru ni kayowasete iru.","german":"We send our child to an international school.","furigana":"こ|かよ"},{"japanese":"このイベントはとてもインターナショナルな雰囲気だった。","romaji":"Kono ibento wa totemo intānashonaru na fun'iki datta.","german":"The event had a very international atmosphere.","furigana":"ふんいき"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Intercom system at the front door or in a building; also written インターホン.","examples":[{"japanese":"インターフォンが鳴ったので、玄関に出た。","romaji":"Intāfon ga natta node, genkan ni deta.","german":"The intercom rang, so I went to the door.","furigana":"な|げんかん|で"},{"japanese":"インターフォン越しに宅配便の人と話した。","romaji":"Intāfon goshi ni takuhaibin no hito to hanashita.","german":"I spoke with the delivery person over the intercom.","furigana":"ご|たくはいびん|ひと|はな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"An educated person, a brainy type; often slightly mocking. Short form of インテリゲンチャ.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼はインテリだが、少しも偉そうにしない。","romaji":"Kare wa interi da ga, sukoshi mo erasō ni shinai.","german":"He is an intellectual, but doesn't act arrogant at all.","furigana":"かれ|すこ|えら"},{"japanese":"その雑誌はインテリ向けだと言われている。","romaji":"Sono zasshi wa interi muke da to iwarete iru.","german":"The magazine is said to be aimed at intellectuals.","furigana":"ざっし|む|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Often the information counter in stations or department stores: インフォメーションで聞く. In general, people prefer 情報.","examples":[{"japanese":"道がわからなければ、インフォメーションで聞いてください。","romaji":"Michi ga wakaranakereba, infomēshon de kiite kudasai.","german":"If you don't know the way, please ask at the information desk.","furigana":"みち|き"},{"japanese":"ホームページにイベントのインフォメーションが載っている。","romaji":"Hōmupēji ni ibento no infomēshon ga notte iru.","german":"Information about the event is on the website.","furigana":"の"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Luftpostbrief; Synonym: 航空便.","examples":[{"japanese":"祖母にドイツからエアメールを送った。","romaji":"Sobo ni Doitsu kara eamēru o okutta.","german":"Ich habe meiner Großmutter einen Luftpostbrief aus Deutschland geschickt.","furigana":"そぼ|おく"},{"japanese":"エアメールは一週間ぐらいで届きます。","romaji":"Eamēru wa isshūkan gurai de todokimasu.","german":"Luftpost kommt in etwa einer Woche an.","furigana":"いっしゅうかん|とど"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"な-adjective: エレガントなドレス. Somewhat more refined than 上品.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女はいつもエレガントな服を着ている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa itsumo ereganto na fuku o kite iru.","german":"She always wears elegant clothes.","furigana":"かのじょ|ふく|き"},{"japanese":"そのホテルのロビーはとてもエレガントだった。","romaji":"Sono hoteru no robī wa totemo ereganto datta.","german":"The hotel lobby was very elegant."}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Often used for IT professionals: システムエンジニア (SE). More formal: 技術者.","examples":[{"japanese":"兄はIT企業でエンジニアとして働いている。","romaji":"Ani wa IT kigyō de enjinia to shite hataraite iru.","german":"My older brother works as an engineer at an IT company.","furigana":"あに|きぎょう|はたら"},{"japanese":"このエンジニアが新しい機械を設計した。","romaji":"Kono enjinia ga atarashii kikai o sekkei shita.","german":"This engineer designed the new machine.","furigana":"あたら|きかい|せっけい"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 1 (source: N5; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"An affable, friendly manner: 愛想がいい (friendly), 愛想が悪い (unfriendly). Also read あいそう. Idiom: 愛想が尽きる (to be fed up).","examples":[{"japanese":"あの店員はいつも愛想がいい。","romaji":"Ano ten'in wa itsumo aiso ga ii.","german":"The saleswoman there is always friendly.","furigana":"てんいん|あいそ"},{"japanese":"彼は愛想がないが、仕事は丁寧だ。","romaji":"Kare wa aiso ga nai ga, shigoto wa teinei da.","german":"He isn't very sociable, but he works carefully.","furigana":"かれ|あいそ|しごと|ていねい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The relationship between two people: 親しい間柄 (close relationship), 親子の間柄.","examples":[{"japanese":"二人は昔から親しい間柄だ。","romaji":"Futari wa mukashi kara shitashii aidagara da.","german":"The two have been close for a long time.","furigana":"ふたり|むかし|した|あいだがら"},{"japanese":"上司と部下という間柄でも、言うべきことは言う。","romaji":"Jōshi to buka to iu aidagara demo, iu beki koto wa iu.","german":"Even in the relationship between boss and employee, I say what needs to be said.","furigana":"じょうし|ぶか|あいだがら|い|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Dirt that accumulates on skin or objects: 垢を落とす (to wash thoroughly). Homophone: 赤 (red).","examples":[{"japanese":"お風呂で体の垢を落とした。","romaji":"Ofuro de karada no aka o otoshita.","german":"I scrubbed myself thoroughly clean in the bath.","furigana":"ふろ|からだ|あか|お"},{"japanese":"長く使ったシャツの襟に垢がついている。","romaji":"Nagaku tsukatta shatsu no eri ni aka ga tsuite iru.","german":"Grime clings to the collar of the shirt worn for a long time.","furigana":"なが|つか|えり|あか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Elevated word for proof or sign, often emotional: 愛の証 (token of love), 努力の証. More everyday: 証拠.","examples":[{"japanese":"このメダルは彼の努力の証だ。","romaji":"Kono medaru wa kare no doryoku no akashi da.","german":"This medal is proof of his efforts.","furigana":"かれ|どりょく|あかし"},{"japanese":"指輪を友情の証として贈った。","romaji":"Yubiwa o yūjō no akashi to shite okutta.","german":"I gave him a ring as a token of friendship.","furigana":"ゆびわ|ゆうじょう|あかし|おく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Intransitive godan verb: 顔が赤らむ (to blush), 空が赤らむ (the sky turns red). Somewhat more literary than 赤くなる.","examples":[{"japanese":"褒められて、彼女の顔が少し赤らんだ。","romaji":"Homerarete, kanojo no kao ga sukoshi akaranda.","german":"When she was praised, she blushed slightly.","furigana":"ほ|かのじょ|かお|すこ|あか"},{"japanese":"夕方になって、西の空が赤らんできた。","romaji":"Yūgata ni natte, nishi no sora ga akarande kita.","german":"In the evening, the western sky turned red.","furigana":"ゆうがた|にし|そら|あか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Versatile noun: rise (物価の上がり), takings (店の上がり) and completion (仕事の上がり). As a suffix also \"fresh after\": 雨上がり (after the rain), 病み上がり.","examples":[{"japanese":"今日の店の上がりは十万円だった。","romaji":"Kyō no mise no agari wa jūman'en datta.","german":"The store's takings today came to 100,000 yen.","furigana":"きょう|みせ|あ|じゅうまんえん"},{"japanese":"この仕事は五時で上がりにしよう。","romaji":"Kono shigoto wa goji de agari ni shiyō.","german":"Let's finish this job at five o'clock.","furigana":"しごと|ごじ|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Noun of 諦める: 諦めが早い (to give up quickly), 諦めがつく (to be able to come to terms with it).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は諦めが悪く、何度も挑戦した。","romaji":"Kare wa akirame ga waruku, nando mo chōsen shita.","german":"He did not give up and kept trying again and again.","furigana":"かれ|あきら|わる|なんど|ちょうせん"},{"japanese":"全力を尽くしたので、負けても諦めがつく。","romaji":"Zenryoku o tsukushita node, makete mo akirame ga tsuku.","german":"Because I gave it my all, I can accept even a defeat.","furigana":"ぜんりょく|つ|ま|あきら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mostly \"unscrupulous, cunning\": あくどい商売 (shady business). More rarely \"loud, garish\" of colors or flavor. Also written 悪どい.","examples":[{"japanese":"お年寄りをだますなんて、あくどいやり方だ。","romaji":"Otoshiyori o damasu nante, akudoi yarikata da.","german":"Cheating elderly people is an unscrupulous method.","furigana":"としよ|かた"},{"japanese":"この服はあくどい色で、私には派手すぎる。","romaji":"Kono fuku wa akudoi iro de, watashi ni wa hadesugiru.","german":"This dress is a garish color and too eye-catching for me.","furigana":"ふく|いろ|わたし|はで"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive godan verb: to laugh at someone contemptuously: 人の失敗をあざ笑う. Kanji also 嘲笑う.","examples":[{"japanese":"人の失敗をあざ笑うのはよくない。","romaji":"Hito no shippai o azawarau no wa yoku nai.","german":"It is not good to mock other people's mistakes.","furigana":"ひと|しっぱい|わら"},{"japanese":"彼は周りにあざ笑われても、夢をあきらめなかった。","romaji":"Kare wa mawari ni azawarawarete mo, yume o akiramenakatta.","german":"Although those around him jeered at him, he did not give up his dream.","furigana":"かれ|まわ|わら|ゆめ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A polite formula used when one has to decline a request or communicate something unpleasant; often at the end: 悪しからずご了承ください. Mostly in kana: あしからず.","examples":[{"japanese":"今回は参加できませんので、悪しからずご了承ください。","romaji":"Konkai wa sanka dekimasen node, ashikarazu goryōshō kudasai.","german":"I'm afraid I cannot take part this time; I ask for your understanding.","furigana":"こんかい|さんか|あ|りょうしょう"},{"japanese":"満席の場合はお断りすることもあります。悪しからず。","romaji":"Manseki no baai wa okotowari suru koto mo arimasu. Ashikarazu.","german":"If all seats are taken, we may have to turn you away. Please understand.","furigana":"まんせき|ばあい|ことわ|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Flavor (深い味わいのコーヒー) and, figuratively, the special charm of something: 味わいのある文章.","examples":[{"japanese":"このワインは深い味わいがある。","romaji":"Kono wain wa fukai ajiwai ga aru.","german":"This wine has a full-bodied flavor.","furigana":"ふか|あじ"},{"japanese":"古い町並みには独特の味わいがある。","romaji":"Furui machinami ni wa dokutoku no ajiwai ga aru.","german":"Old streets have a charm all their own.","furigana":"ふる|まちな|どくとく|あじ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Intransitive ichidan verb: colors fade (色が褪せる), and memories fade too. Often 色褪せる. Homophone: 焦る (to hurry, to be nervous).","examples":[{"japanese":"日に当たって、カーテンの色が褪せてしまった。","romaji":"Hi ni atatte, kāten no iro ga asete shimatta.","german":"The color of the curtains has faded in the sun.","furigana":"ひ|あ|いろ|あ"},{"japanese":"あの日の思い出は今も褪せない。","romaji":"Ano hi no omoide wa ima mo asenai.","german":"The memory of that day has not faded to this day.","furigana":"ひ|おも|で|いま|あ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"当て stands for \"aim\" and \"expectation, something one counts on\", almost only in fixed phrases: 当てもなく \"aimlessly\", 当てが外れる \"for expectations not to be met\", 当てにする \"to rely on\", 当てにならない \"unreliable\". For a concrete goal or purpose, people otherwise say 目的 or 目当て. As a suffix after names or companies, あて means \"addressed to\": 田中様宛ての手紙, 会社宛てに送る; in this sense it is written 宛 or 宛て, never 当て (cf. 宛先 \"recipient, address\").","examples":[{"japanese":"休みの日は当てもなく町を歩くのが好きだ。","romaji":"Yasumi no hi wa ate mo naku machi o aruku no ga suki da.","german":"On days off, I like to wander around the city aimlessly.","furigana":"やす|ひ|あ|まち|ある|す"},{"japanese":"彼の約束は当てにならない。","romaji":"Kare no yakusoku wa ate ni naranai.","german":"You can't rely on his promises.","furigana":"かれ|やくそく|あ"},{"japanese":"この書類は人事部宛てに送ってください。","romaji":"Kono shorui wa jinjibu ate ni okutte kudasai.","german":"Please send these documents to the personnel department.","furigana":"しょるい|じんじぶあ|おく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive ichidan verb: to address a letter to someone: 母に宛てた手紙. Homophones: 当てる (to hit), 充てる (to allocate).","examples":[{"japanese":"これは先生に宛てて書いた手紙です。","romaji":"Kore wa sensei ni atete kaita tegami desu.","german":"This is a letter I wrote to my teacher.","furigana":"せんせい|あ|か|てがみ"},{"japanese":"このメッセージは誰に宛てたものですか。","romaji":"Kono messēji wa dare ni ateta mono desu ka.","german":"Who is this message addressed to?","furigana":"だれ|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"な-adjective and noun: the order or sides are swapped: 靴を左右あべこべに履く. Colloquial; the more neutral word is 逆.","examples":[{"japanese":"子どもが靴を左右あべこべに履いている。","romaji":"Kodomo ga kutsu o sayū abekobe ni haite iru.","german":"The child put his shoes on the wrong feet.","furigana":"こ|くつ|さゆう|は"},{"japanese":"注意したのに、あべこべに怒られてしまった。","romaji":"Chūi shita noni, abekobe ni okorarete shimatta.","german":"Although I had warned him, I was the one who got scolded instead.","furigana":"ちゅうい|おこ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Generic term for umbrella, raincoat and rain boots: 雨具を持参してください.","examples":[{"japanese":"山の天気は変わりやすいので、雨具を持っていこう。","romaji":"Yama no tenki wa kawariyasui node, amagu o motte ikō.","german":"The weather in the mountains changes quickly, so let's take rain gear.","furigana":"やま|てんき|か|あまぐ|も"},{"japanese":"遠足の持ち物に雨具と書いてある。","romaji":"Ensoku no mochimono ni amagu to kaite aru.","german":"Rain gear is on the packing list for the school trip.","furigana":"えんそく|も|もの|あまぐ|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A mild flavor profile, for example for curry or sake: 甘口のカレー. Opposite: 辛口 (hot, dry).","examples":[{"japanese":"子どもには甘口のカレーを作った。","romaji":"Kodomo ni wa amakuchi no karē o tsukutta.","german":"I cooked a mild curry for the kids.","furigana":"こ|あまくち|つく"},{"japanese":"このお酒は甘口で飲みやすい。","romaji":"Kono osake wa amakuchi de nomiyasui.","german":"This sake is sweet and easy to drink.","furigana":"さけ|あまくち|の"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive godan verb of written language: to have doubts about whether something will succeed: 成功を危ぶむ声. Often in the passive: 開催が危ぶまれる.","examples":[{"japanese":"台風のため、大会の開催が危ぶまれている。","romaji":"Taifū no tame, taikai no kaisai ga ayabumarete iru.","german":"Because of the typhoon, it is doubtful whether the tournament can take place.","furigana":"たいふう|たいかい|かいさい|あや"},{"japanese":"周りは計画の成功を危ぶんだ。","romaji":"Mawari wa keikaku no seikō o ayabunda.","german":"The people around him doubted the success of the plan.","furigana":"まわ|けいかく|せいこう|あや"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Godan verb: to do something wrongly: 判断を誤る (to misjudge). Often attributive: 誤った情報 (false information). Homophone: 謝る (to apologize).","examples":[{"japanese":"判断を誤って、大きな損を出してしまった。","romaji":"Handan o ayamatte, ōki na son o dashite shimatta.","german":"I made the wrong decision and suffered a big loss.","furigana":"はんだん|あやま|おお|そん|だ"},{"japanese":"誤った情報がネットで広がっている。","romaji":"Ayamatta jōhō ga netto de hirogatte iru.","german":"False information spreads on the internet.","furigana":"あやま|じょうほう|ひろ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Elevated noun: step (歩みを止める) and, figuratively, the history or development of something: 会社の百年の歩み.","examples":[{"japanese":"この本には会社の五十年の歩みが書かれている。","romaji":"Kono hon ni wa kaisha no gojūnen no ayumi ga kakarete iru.","german":"This book describes the company's fifty-year history.","furigana":"ほん|かいしゃ|ごじゅうねん|あゆ|か"},{"japanese":"彼は疲れて歩みを止めた。","romaji":"Kare wa tsukarete ayumi o tometa.","german":"He was tired and stopped walking.","furigana":"かれ|つか|あゆ|と"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Elevated godan verb; in everyday speech 歩く. Mostly figurative: 人生を歩む, 別々の道を歩む (to go separate ways).","examples":[{"japanese":"二人はそれぞれ別の道を歩むことにした。","romaji":"Futari wa sorezore betsu no michi o ayumu koto ni shita.","german":"The two decided to go their separate ways.","furigana":"ふたり|べつ|みち|あゆ"},{"japanese":"彼女は医者として長い人生を歩んできた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa isha to shite nagai jinsei o ayunde kita.","german":"She walked the path of a doctor throughout a long life.","furigana":"かのじょ|いしゃ|なが|じんせい|あゆ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Noun of 争う: dispute (争いが起きる) and competition (首位争い = fight for first place).","examples":[{"japanese":"土地をめぐって、兄弟の間で争いが起きた。","romaji":"Tochi o megutte, kyōdai no aida de arasoi ga okita.","german":"A dispute broke out between the siblings over the land.","furigana":"とち|きょうだい|あいだ|あらそ|お"},{"japanese":"リーグの優勝争いは最後まで続いた。","romaji":"Rīgu no yūshō arasoi wa saigo made tsuzuita.","german":"The fight for the league championship lasted until the end.","furigana":"ゆうしょうあらそ|さいご|つづ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"い-adjective: rough behavior (荒っぽい運転 = reckless driving) or coarse, careless work (仕事が荒っぽい).","examples":[{"japanese":"あのタクシーは運転が荒っぽくて怖かった。","romaji":"Ano takushī wa unten ga arappokute kowakatta.","german":"The taxi drove so roughly that I was scared.","furigana":"うんてん|あら|こわ"},{"japanese":"彼は言葉は荒っぽいが、根は優しい。","romaji":"Kare wa kotoba wa arappoi ga, ne wa yasashii.","german":"He speaks roughly, but is basically good-natured.","furigana":"かれ|ことば|あら|ね|やさ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Exclamation of surprise, used more by women: あら、もうこんな時間. Do not confuse it with あら (flaw, defect) or the fish アラ.","examples":[{"japanese":"あら、もうこんな時間。帰らなくちゃ。","romaji":"Ara, mō konna jikan. Kaeranakucha.","german":"Oh, it's already this late! I have to go home.","furigana":"じかん|かえ"},{"japanese":"あら、田中さん、久しぶりですね。","romaji":"Ara, Tanaka-san, hisashiburi desu ne.","german":"Oh, Mr. Tanaka, long time no see!","furigana":"たなか|ひさ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"State, often negative or reproachful: 何だこの有様は (What kind of state is this!). Also written 有り様.","examples":[{"japanese":"台風の後、町はひどい有様だった。","romaji":"Taifū no ato, machi wa hidoi arisama datta.","german":"After the typhoon, the town presented a terrible sight.","furigana":"たいふう|あと|まち|ありさま"},{"japanese":"部屋を片付けないから、こんな有様になるんだ。","romaji":"Heya o katazukenai kara, konna arisama ni naru n da.","german":"Because you never tidy up, the room looks like this.","furigana":"へや|かたづ|ありさま"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The way something actually is, without embellishment: ありのままを話す, ありのままの自分. Mostly in kana.","examples":[{"japanese":"起きたことをありのままに話してください。","romaji":"Okita koto o arinomama ni hanashite kudasai.","german":"Please tell it exactly as it happened.","furigana":"お|はな"},{"japanese":"ありのままの自分を受け入れることは大切だ。","romaji":"Arinomama no jibun o ukeireru koto wa taisetsu da.","german":"It is important to accept yourself as you are.","furigana":"じぶん|う|い|たいせつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Ichidan verb, used almost only attributively in the form ありふれた: ありふれた話 (a commonplace story). Usually written in kana.","examples":[{"japanese":"それはどこにでもあるありふれた話だ。","romaji":"Sore wa doko ni demo aru arifureta hanashi da.","german":"That is a perfectly ordinary story, the kind you hear everywhere.","furigana":"はなし"},{"japanese":"ありふれた材料でも、おいしい料理は作れる。","romaji":"Arifureta zairyō demo, oishii ryōri wa tsukureru.","german":"Even with ordinary ingredients, you can make delicious food.","furigana":"ざいりょう|りょうり|つく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Godan variant of 合わせる, a bit more colloquial: 手を合わす (to put one's hands together), 力を合わす (to join forces), 時計を合わす (to set a clock).","examples":[{"japanese":"みんなで力を合わして、困難を乗り越えた。","romaji":"Minna de chikara o awashite, konnan o norikoeta.","german":"We all joined forces and overcame the difficulties.","furigana":"ちから|あ|こんなん|の|こ"},{"japanese":"お寺の前で、静かに手を合わした。","romaji":"Otera no mae de, shizuka ni te o awashita.","german":"I quietly put my hands together in front of the temple.","furigana":"てら|まえ|しず|て|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Literary Ichidan verb: to worry (身を案じる = to be anxious about someone's welfare) or to devise a plan (一計を案じる). Also 案ずる.","examples":[{"japanese":"母はいつも遠くに住む息子の身を案じている。","romaji":"Haha wa itsumo tōku ni sumu musuko no mi o anjite iru.","german":"Mother is always worried about her son who lives far away.","furigana":"はは|とお|す|むすこ|み|あん"},{"japanese":"彼は問題を解決するために一計を案じた。","romaji":"Kare wa mondai o kaiketsu suru tame ni ikkei o anjita.","german":"He devised a plan to solve the problem.","furigana":"かれ|もんだい|かいけつ|いっけい|あん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Physical rest for recovery: 安静にする (to rest), 絶対安静 (strict bed rest).","examples":[{"japanese":"医者に一週間は安静にするように言われた。","romaji":"Isha ni isshūkan wa ansei ni suru yō ni iwareta.","german":"The doctor told me to rest for a week.","furigana":"いしゃ|いっしゅうかん|あんせい|い"},{"japanese":"手術の後、父は絶対安静が必要だ。","romaji":"Shujutsu no ato, chichi wa zettai ansei ga hitsuyō da.","german":"After the operation, my father needs strict bed rest.","furigana":"しゅじゅつ|あと|ちち|ぜったいあんせい|ひつよう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A small practice run by an independent doctor, often part of the name: 田中医院. Large hospitals are called 病院. Homophone: 委員.","examples":[{"japanese":"近所の医院で風邪を診てもらった。","romaji":"Kinjo no iin de kaze o mite moratta.","german":"I had my cold looked at by the clinic in my neighborhood.","furigana":"きんじょ|いいん|かぜ|み"},{"japanese":"この医院は土曜日の午後は休みです。","romaji":"Kono iin wa doyōbi no gogo wa yasumi desu.","german":"This clinic is closed on Saturday afternoons.","furigana":"いいん|どようび|ごご|やす"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Secretly leaving one's parents' home; as a verb 家出する.","examples":[{"japanese":"息子が家出して、三日も帰ってこない。","romaji":"Musuko ga iede shite, mikka mo kaette konai.","german":"My son ran away from home and hasn't come back for three days.","furigana":"むすこ|いえで|みっか|かえ"},{"japanese":"中学生の時、一度だけ家出したことがある。","romaji":"Chūgakusei no toki, ichido dake iede shita koto ga aru.","german":"I ran away from home once, when I was in junior high school.","furigana":"ちゅうがくせい|とき|いちど|いえで"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Intransitive Godan verb: to approach something with great eagerness: 今年こそ優勝すると意気込む. Noun: 意気込み.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は今年こそ合格すると意気込んでいる。","romaji":"Kare wa kotoshi koso gōkaku suru to ikigonde iru.","german":"He is determined to pass this year for sure.","furigana":"かれ|ことし|ごうかく|いきご"},{"japanese":"意気込んで始めたが、三日で飽きてしまった。","romaji":"Ikigonde hajimeta ga, mikka de akite shimatta.","german":"I started out full of enthusiasm, but got bored after three days.","furigana":"いきご|はじ|みっか|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Passing each other by (letters, people) and the misunderstanding that results. Fixed formula in business letters and payment reminders: 本状と行き違いの際はご容赦ください (If this letter has crossed with your payment or reply, please excuse us). Also read ゆきちがい.","examples":[{"japanese":"本メールと行き違いでお支払いいただいた場合は、ご容赦ください。","romaji":"Hon mēru to ikichigai de oshiharai itadaita baai wa, goyōsha kudasai.","german":"If your payment crossed with this email, please excuse us.","furigana":"ほん|い|ちが|しはら|ばあい|ようしゃ"},{"japanese":"ちょっとした行き違いから、二人の関係が悪くなった。","romaji":"Chotto shita ikichigai kara, futari no kankei ga waruku natta.","german":"A small misunderstanding made the relationship between the two worse.","furigana":"い|ちが|ふたり|かんけい|わる"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Literary, mostly before nouns: 幾多の困難 (countless difficulties). In everyday speech たくさんの.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は幾多の困難を乗り越えて成功した。","romaji":"Kare wa ikuta no konnan o norikoete seikō shita.","german":"He succeeded after overcoming countless difficulties.","furigana":"かれ|いくた|こんなん|の|こ|せいこう"},{"japanese":"この町は幾多の戦争を経験してきた。","romaji":"Kono machi wa ikuta no sensō o keiken shite kita.","german":"This town has gone through many wars.","furigana":"まち|いくた|せんそう|けいけん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive Ichidan verb: to arrange flowers artistically: 花を生ける. Hence 生け花 (ikebana). Also written 活ける.","examples":[{"japanese":"母は玄関に季節の花を生けた。","romaji":"Haha wa genkan ni kisetsu no hana o iketa.","german":"Mother arranged seasonal flowers in the entrance hall.","furigana":"はは|げんかん|きせつ|はな|い"},{"japanese":"生けた花が三日で枯れてしまった。","romaji":"Iketa hana ga mikka de karete shimatta.","german":"The flowers I arranged withered in three days.","furigana":"い|はな|みっか|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"An opposing opinion: 異見を述べる. Homophone and more common: 意見 (opinion in general). Synonym: 異論.","examples":[{"japanese":"会議で彼だけが異見を述べた。","romaji":"Kaigi de kare dake ga iken o nobeta.","german":"Only he voiced a dissenting opinion at the meeting.","furigana":"かいぎ|かれ|いけん|の"},{"japanese":"その案に異見のある人は手を挙げてください。","romaji":"Sono an ni iken no aru hito wa te o agete kudasai.","german":"Anyone with an objection to that proposal, please raise your hand.","furigana":"あん|いけん|ひと|て|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Formal: the will or intention of a person or organization: 本人の意向を尊重する. Homophones: 移行, 以降.","examples":[{"japanese":"本人の意向を確認してから決めましょう。","romaji":"Honnin no ikō o kakunin shite kara kimemashō.","german":"Let's confirm the person's wishes before deciding.","furigana":"ほんにん|いこう|かくにん|き"},{"japanese":"社長は来年引退する意向を示した。","romaji":"Shachō wa rainen intai suru ikō o shimeshita.","german":"The company president indicated his intention to retire next year.","furigana":"しゃちょう|らいねんいんたい|いこう|しめ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transition to a new state or system; as a verb 移行する: 新しいシステムに移行する.","examples":[{"japanese":"来月から新しいシステムに移行する予定だ。","romaji":"Raigetsu kara atarashii shisutemu ni ikō suru yotei da.","german":"We plan to switch over to the new system next month.","furigana":"らいげつ|あたら|いこう|よてい"},{"japanese":"移行期間中は古い方法も使える。","romaji":"Ikō kikanchū wa furui hōhō mo tsukaeru.","german":"The old method can also be used during the transition period.","furigana":"いこうきかんちゅう|ふる|ほうほう|つか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Introduces the decisive moment: いざとなると (when push comes to shove), いざという時 (in an emergency), いざ始めると (once we actually started).","examples":[{"japanese":"いざという時のために、水を用意しておこう。","romaji":"Iza to iu toki no tame ni, mizu o yōi shite okō.","german":"Let's have water ready for an emergency.","furigana":"とき|みず|ようい"},{"japanese":"いざ話そうとすると、言葉が出てこなかった。","romaji":"Iza hanasō to suru to, kotoba ga dete konakatta.","german":"But when I actually tried to speak, the words wouldn't come.","furigana":"はな|ことば|で"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Obstinate will or defiance: 意地を張る (to stay stubborn), 意地でも (out of sheer stubbornness). Cf. 意地悪 (mean).","examples":[{"japanese":"意地を張らないで、素直に謝ったらどう？","romaji":"Iji o haranaide, sunao ni ayamattara dō?","german":"Don't be so stubborn; why don't you just apologize honestly?","furigana":"いじ|は|すなお|あやま"},{"japanese":"彼は意地でも最後まで走り続けた。","romaji":"Kare wa iji demo saigo made hashiritsuzuketa.","german":"Out of sheer stubbornness, he kept running to the end.","furigana":"かれ|いじ|さいご|はし|つづ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Elevated word for the highest point: 山の頂 (mountain peak). More everyday: 頂上, てっぺん.","examples":[{"japanese":"山の頂にはまだ雪が残っている。","romaji":"Yama no itadaki ni wa mada yuki ga nokotte iru.","german":"There is still snow on the mountain peak.","furigana":"やま|いただき|ゆき|のこ"},{"japanese":"朝日が塔の頂を照らしていた。","romaji":"Asahi ga tō no itadaki o terashite ita.","german":"The morning sun lit up the top of the tower.","furigana":"あさひ|とう|いただき|て"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Slightly formal adverb: 至って元気だ (in perfect health), 至って普通 (perfectly ordinary).","examples":[{"japanese":"祖父は九十歳だが、至って元気だ。","romaji":"Sofu wa kyūjussai da ga, itatte genki da.","german":"My grandfather is ninety, but he is in perfect health.","furigana":"そふ|きゅうじゅっさい|いた|げんき"},{"japanese":"試験の結果は至って普通だった。","romaji":"Shiken no kekka wa itatte futsū datta.","german":"The exam result was completely average.","furigana":"しけん|けっか|いた|ふつう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive Godan verb: to treat someone considerately (お年寄りを労る) or to take care of one's own body (体を労る). Usually written in kana.","examples":[{"japanese":"お年寄りを労る気持ちを大切にしたい。","romaji":"Otoshiyori o itawaru kimochi o taisetsu ni shitai.","german":"I want to value the feeling of caring for the elderly.","furigana":"としよ|いたわ|きも|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"疲れているなら、少し体を労ったほうがいい。","romaji":"Tsukarete iru nara, sukoshi karada o itawatta hō ga ii.","german":"If you are tired, you should go easy on your body a little.","furigana":"つか|すこ|からだ|いたわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Formal, often in greetings: 社員一同 (all the employees), 家族一同 (the whole family).","examples":[{"japanese":"社員一同、心よりお礼申し上げます。","romaji":"Shain ichidō, kokoro yori orei mōshiagemasu.","german":"All of the staff sincerely thank you.","furigana":"しゃいんいちどう|こころ|れいもう|あ"},{"japanese":"一同は先生の話を静かに聞いていた。","romaji":"Ichidō wa sensei no hanashi o shizuka ni kiite ita.","german":"Everyone listened quietly to the teacher's talk.","furigana":"いちどう|せんせい|はなし|しず|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A part of a whole, slightly more emphatic than 一部: ほんの一部分にすぎない.","examples":[{"japanese":"これは問題のほんの一部分にすぎない。","romaji":"Kore wa mondai no hon no ichibubun ni suginai.","german":"This is only a small part of the problem.","furigana":"もんだい|いちぶぶん"},{"japanese":"壁の一部分だけ色が違う。","romaji":"Kabe no ichibubun dake iro ga chigau.","german":"Only one part of the wall has a different color.","furigana":"かべ|いちぶぶん|いろ|ちが"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 46 (source: N4; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"Several meanings: whole surface (一面の雪 = everything covered in snow), an aspect (意外な一面 = an unexpected side) and the front page of a newspaper.","examples":[{"japanese":"朝起きたら、外は一面の雪だった。","romaji":"Asa okitara, soto wa ichimen no yuki datta.","german":"When I woke up in the morning, it was all snow outside.","furigana":"あさお|そと|いちめん|ゆき"},{"japanese":"彼の意外な一面を見た。","romaji":"Kare no igai na ichimen o mita.","german":"I saw an unexpected side of him.","furigana":"かれ|いがい|いちめん|み"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A brief look: 一目で分かる (to understand at a glance), 一目見たい (to want to take a look), 一目惚れ (love at first sight).","examples":[{"japanese":"一目見ただけで、彼だとわかった。","romaji":"Hitome mita dake de, kare da to wakatta.","german":"I recognized him at a single glance.","furigana":"ひとめみ|かれ"},{"japanese":"有名な画家の絵を一目見ようと、たくさんの人が集まった。","romaji":"Yūmei na gaka no e o hitome miyō to, takusan no hito ga atsumatta.","german":"Many people gathered to catch a glimpse of the famous painter's picture.","furigana":"ゆうめい|がか|え|ひとめみ|ひと|あつ"}],"levelNote":"Compound: 目 (N5)"},
{"notes":"Literary: everything at once: 問題を一挙に解決する. Synonym: 一気に.","examples":[{"japanese":"新しい薬で、症状が一挙に改善した。","romaji":"Atarashii kusuri de, shōjō ga ikkyo ni kaizen shita.","german":"The new medicine improved the symptoms all at once.","furigana":"あたら|くすり|しょうじょう|いっきょ|かいぜん"},{"japanese":"溜まっていた仕事を一挙に片付けた。","romaji":"Tamatte ita shigoto o ikkyo ni katazuketa.","german":"I cleared the backlog of work in one go.","furigana":"た|しごと|いっきょ|かたづ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mostly in 〜たい一心で (driven solely by the wish to ...): 会いたい一心で. Also in 一心不乱 (utterly absorbed).","examples":[{"japanese":"母に会いたい一心で、夜通し車を走らせた。","romaji":"Haha ni aitai isshin de, yodōshi kuruma o hashiraseta.","german":"Just wanting to see my mother, I drove through the night.","furigana":"はは|あ|いっしん|よどお|くるま|はし"},{"japanese":"合格したい一心で、毎日勉強を続けた。","romaji":"Gōkaku shitai isshin de, mainichi benkyō o tsuzuketa.","german":"Solely wanting to pass, I kept studying every day.","furigana":"ごうかく|いっしん|まいにちべんきょう|つづ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Introduces a more drastic step: いっそ辞めてしまおうか (Shouldn't I just quit?). Emphasized: いっそのこと.","examples":[{"japanese":"こんなに迷うなら、いっそやめてしまおうか。","romaji":"Konna ni mayou nara, isso yamete shimaō ka.","german":"If I am this torn, shouldn't I just give it up?","furigana":"まよ"},{"japanese":"修理代が高いので、いっそ新しいのを買うことにした。","romaji":"Shūridai ga takai node, isso atarashii no o kau koto ni shita.","german":"Since the repair costs a lot, I decided to just buy a new one.","furigana":"しゅうりだい|たか|あたら|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The whole surroundings of a place: この辺り一帯, 関東一帯. Homophone: 一体 (what on earth; one body).","examples":[{"japanese":"大雨で、この辺り一帯が水につかった。","romaji":"Ōame de, kono atari ittai ga mizu ni tsukatta.","german":"Heavy rain flooded this whole area.","furigana":"おおあめ|あた|いったい|みず"},{"japanese":"駅の周辺一帯は再開発が進んでいる。","romaji":"Eki no shūhen ittai wa saikaihatsu ga susunde iru.","german":"Redevelopment is under way throughout the area around the station.","furigana":"えき|しゅうへんいったい|さいかいはつ|すす"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A sudden, complete transformation; as a verb 一変する: 状況が一変した. Homophone: 一遍 (once).","examples":[{"japanese":"事故の後、彼の生活は一変した。","romaji":"Jiko no ato, kare no seikatsu wa ippen shita.","german":"His life changed completely after the accident.","furigana":"じこ|あと|かれ|せいかつ|いっぺん"},{"japanese":"新しい店長が来て、店の雰囲気が一変した。","romaji":"Atarashii tenchō ga kite, mise no fun'iki ga ippen shita.","german":"With the new store manager, the atmosphere of the shop changed completely.","furigana":"あたら|てんちょう|き|みせ|ふんいき|いっぺん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The flash of lightning itself; thunder is 雷 or 雷鳴. Synonym: 稲妻.","examples":[{"japanese":"夜空に稲光が走った。","romaji":"Yozora ni inabikari ga hashitta.","german":"A flash of lightning ran across the night sky.","furigana":"よぞら|いなびかり|はし"},{"japanese":"稲光が見えてから、しばらくして雷が鳴った。","romaji":"Inabikari ga miete kara, shibaraku shite kaminari ga natta.","german":"A while after the lightning was visible, thunder rumbled.","furigana":"いなびかり|み|かみなり|な"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To do something only unwillingly: いやいや手伝う. Also written 嫌々. Not to be confused with the interjection いやいや (no, no!).","examples":[{"japanese":"子どもはいやいや宿題を始めた。","romaji":"Kodomo wa iyaiya shukudai o hajimeta.","german":"The child reluctantly started his homework.","furigana":"こ|しゅくだい|はじ"},{"japanese":"彼はいやいや会議に出席した。","romaji":"Kare wa iyaiya kaigi ni shusseki shita.","german":"He attended the meeting reluctantly.","furigana":"かれ|かいぎ|しゅっせき"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Stresses that something is unusually intense, often with slight unease: いやに静かだ (suspiciously quiet). Also written 嫌に.","examples":[{"japanese":"今日はいやに静かだね。何かあったの？","romaji":"Kyō wa iya ni shizuka da ne. Nanika atta no?","german":"It's strangely quiet today. Did something happen?","furigana":"きょう|しず|なに"},{"japanese":"彼はいやに親切で、少し不気味だった。","romaji":"Kare wa iya ni shinsetsu de, sukoshi bukimi datta.","german":"He was oddly kind, which was a bit creepy.","furigana":"かれ|しんせつ|すこ|ぶきみ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"い-adjective: unpleasant, mean (いやらしい言い方) and also suggestive, lecherous. Also written 嫌らしい.","examples":[{"japanese":"人の失敗を笑うなんて、いやらしい人だ。","romaji":"Hito no shippai o warau nante, iyarashii hito da.","german":"Laughing at other people's failures; what a nasty person.","furigana":"ひと|しっぱい|わら|ひと"},{"japanese":"そんないやらしい言い方はやめてほしい。","romaji":"Sonna iyarashii iikata wa yamete hoshii.","german":"I wish you would stop that unpleasant way of talking.","furigana":"い|かた"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Clothing as a product category, often in compounds: 衣料品 (clothing items), 衣料品店 (clothing store). Homophone: 医療 (medical care).","examples":[{"japanese":"このスーパーの二階では衣料を売っている。","romaji":"Kono sūpā no nikai de wa iryō o utte iru.","german":"This supermarket sells clothing on the second floor.","furigana":"にかい|いりょう|う"},{"japanese":"寄付された衣料を被災地に送った。","romaji":"Kifu sareta iryō o hisaichi ni okutta.","german":"We sent the donated clothing to the disaster area.","furigana":"きふ|いりょう|ひさいち|おく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Great effective force: 台風の威力 (the might of the typhoon), 威力を発揮する (to show one's strength).","examples":[{"japanese":"台風の威力はすさまじかった。","romaji":"Taifū no iryoku wa susamajikatta.","german":"The power of the typhoon was terrifying.","furigana":"たいふう|いりょく"},{"japanese":"新しい掃除機はその威力を十分に発揮した。","romaji":"Atarashii sōjiki wa sono iryoku o jūbun ni hakki shita.","german":"The new vacuum cleaner fully demonstrated its power.","furigana":"あたら|そうじき|いりょく|じゅうぶん|はっき"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Collective term for garments, e.g. on washing labels or when moving: 冬物の衣類 (winter clothing).","examples":[{"japanese":"引っ越しの前に、衣類を段ボールに詰めた。","romaji":"Hikkoshi no mae ni, irui o danbōru ni tsumeta.","german":"Before moving, I packed my clothes into cardboard boxes.","furigana":"ひ|こ|まえ|いるい|だん|つ"},{"japanese":"この洗剤はデリケートな衣類にも使える。","romaji":"Kono senzai wa derikēto na irui ni mo tsukaeru.","german":"This detergent can also be used on delicate clothing.","furigana":"せんざい|いるい|つか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Noun of 受け入れる: 留学生の受け入れ (accepting exchange students), 受け入れ先 (receiving party).","examples":[{"japanese":"この町は難民の受け入れに積極的だ。","romaji":"Kono machi wa nanmin no ukeire ni sekkyokuteki da.","german":"This town is proactive in accepting refugees.","furigana":"まち|なんみん|う|い|せっきょくてき"},{"japanese":"病院はけが人の受け入れを始めた。","romaji":"Byōin wa keganin no ukeire o hajimeta.","german":"The hospital began taking in the injured.","furigana":"びょういん|にん|う|い|はじ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"What one is in charge of, especially a teacher's class: 受け持ちのクラス (the class one is in charge of), 受け持ちの先生 (the teacher in charge).","examples":[{"japanese":"このクラスの受け持ちは山田先生です。","romaji":"Kono kurasu no ukemochi wa Yamada-sensei desu.","german":"Ms. Yamada is in charge of this class.","furigana":"う|も|やまだせんせい"},{"japanese":"それは私の受け持ちではないので、担当者を呼びます。","romaji":"Sore wa watashi no ukemochi de wa nai node, tantōsha o yobimasu.","german":"That isn't my responsibility, so I'll call the person in charge.","furigana":"わたし|う|も|たんとうしゃ|よ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"A short nod-off, usually unplanned and without going to bed: ソファでうたた寝する. The kanji 転寝 is rare.","examples":[{"japanese":"テレビを見ながら、ついうたた寝をしてしまった。","romaji":"Terebi o minagara, tsui utatane o shite shimatta.","german":"I dozed off while watching TV.","furigana":"み|ね"},{"japanese":"こたつでうたた寝すると風邪をひくよ。","romaji":"Kotatsu de utatane suru to kaze o hiku yo.","german":"If you doze off at the kotatsu, you'll catch a cold.","furigana":"ね|かぜ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive Ichidan verb: to discuss details in advance: 日程を打ち合わせる. The noun 打ち合わせ (meeting) is more common.","examples":[{"japanese":"会議の前に、発表の順番を打ち合わせておこう。","romaji":"Kaigi no mae ni, happyō no junban o uchiawasete okō.","german":"Let's arrange the order of the presentations before the meeting.","furigana":"かいぎ|まえ|はっぴょう|じゅんばん|う|あ"},{"japanese":"旅行の日程について、ガイドと打ち合わせた。","romaji":"Ryokō no nittei ni tsuite, gaido to uchiawaseta.","german":"I coordinated the trip schedule with the guide.","furigana":"りょこう|にってい|う|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Negation (grammar: 打ち消しの形 = negative form) and the denial of a rumor. Verb: 打ち消す.","examples":[{"japanese":"「ない」は打ち消しを表す言葉だ。","romaji":"\"Nai\" wa uchikeshi o arawasu kotoba da.","german":"\"Nai\" is a word that expresses negation.","furigana":"う|け|あらわ|ことば"},{"japanese":"会社はうわさの打ち消しに追われた。","romaji":"Kaisha wa uwasa no uchikeshi ni owareta.","german":"The company was busy denying the rumors.","furigana":"かいしゃ|う|け|お"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive Godan verb with several meanings: 釘を打ち込む (to drive in a nail), データを打ち込む (to enter data), 仕事に打ち込む (to throw oneself into work).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は研究に打ち込んで、休みもほとんど取らない。","romaji":"Kare wa kenkyū ni uchikonde, yasumi mo hotondo toranai.","german":"He is devoted to his research and hardly takes any time off.","furigana":"かれ|けんきゅう|う|こ|やす|と"},{"japanese":"名簿のデータをパソコンに打ち込んだ。","romaji":"Meibo no dēta o pasokon ni uchikonda.","german":"I entered the data from the name list into the computer.","furigana":"めいぼ|う|こ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A copy of a document, often at government offices: 住民票の写し (copy of the residence certificate). More everyday: コピー.","examples":[{"japanese":"申し込みには住民票の写しが必要です。","romaji":"Mōshikomi ni wa jūminhyō no utsushi ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"You need a copy of your residence certificate for the application.","furigana":"もう|こ|じゅうみんひょう|うつ|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"契約書の写しを一部いただけますか。","romaji":"Keiyakusho no utsushi o ichibu itadakemasu ka.","german":"Could I have one copy of the contract?","furigana":"けいやくしょ|うつ|いちぶ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Noun of 訴える: a legal lawsuit (訴えを起こす) and, more generally, a complaint: 住民の訴え.","examples":[{"japanese":"住民の訴えを受けて、市は調査を始めた。","romaji":"Jūmin no uttae o ukete, shi wa chōsa o hajimeta.","german":"Following the residents' complaints, the city started an investigation.","furigana":"じゅうみん|うった|う|し|ちょうさ|はじ"},{"japanese":"彼は会社を相手に訴えを起こした。","romaji":"Kare wa kaisha o aite ni uttae o okoshita.","german":"He filed a lawsuit against the company.","furigana":"かれ|かいしゃ|あいて|うった|お"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"な-adjective: hollow inside or, more often, expressionless and absent-minded: 虚ろな目 (vacant eyes). Also written 空ろ.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は虚ろな目で窓の外を見ていた。","romaji":"Kare wa utsuro na me de mado no soto o mite ita.","german":"He was looking out the window with vacant eyes.","furigana":"かれ|うつ|め|まど|そと|み"},{"japanese":"古い木の幹は中が虚ろになっていた。","romaji":"Furui ki no miki wa naka ga utsuro ni natte ita.","german":"The trunk of the old tree was hollow inside.","furigana":"ふる|き|みき|なか|うつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Exaggerated self-confidence: 自惚れが強い (very conceited). Verb: 自惚れる. Often in kana: うぬぼれ.","examples":[{"japanese":"少し褒められただけで天才だと思うのは自惚れだ。","romaji":"Sukoshi homerareta dake de tensai da to omou no wa unubore da.","german":"It is conceit to think you're a genius after just a little praise.","furigana":"すこ|ほ|てんさい|おも|うぬぼ"},{"japanese":"彼の自惚れには、みんなうんざりしている。","romaji":"Kare no unubore ni wa, minna unzari shite iru.","german":"Everyone is fed up with his conceit.","furigana":"かれ|うぬぼ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive Godan verb: to insert or bury something into something else: 壁に照明を埋め込む; also digitally: 動画をページに埋め込む.","examples":[{"japanese":"天井にライトが埋め込まれている。","romaji":"Tenjō ni raito ga umekomarete iru.","german":"Lights are set into the ceiling.","furigana":"てんじょう|う|こ"},{"japanese":"ブログに動画を埋め込んだ。","romaji":"Burogu ni dōga o umekonda.","german":"I embedded a video in my blog.","furigana":"どうが|う|こ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Turned the wrong way round (シャツが裏返し) and, figuratively, the flip side: 不安の裏返し (a manifestation of hidden anxiety). Verb: 裏返す.","examples":[{"japanese":"シャツを裏返しに着ていることに気づかなかった。","romaji":"Shatsu o uragaeshi ni kite iru koto ni kizukanakatta.","german":"I didn't notice I was wearing my shirt inside out.","furigana":"うらがえ|き|き"},{"japanese":"彼の強い言葉は、不安の裏返しかもしれない。","romaji":"Kare no tsuyoi kotoba wa, fuan no uragaeshi kamo shirenai.","german":"His strong words may be the flip side of his anxiety.","furigana":"かれ|つよ|ことば|ふあん|うらがえ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A sales promotion with special prices: 大売り出し (big sale). Also the launch of a new product.","examples":[{"japanese":"この店では毎月一日に売り出しがある。","romaji":"Kono mise de wa maitsuki tsuitachi ni uridashi ga aru.","german":"This store has a sale on the first of every month.","furigana":"みせ|まいつきついたち|う|だ"},{"japanese":"年末の売り出しで、服を安く買った。","romaji":"Nenmatsu no uridashi de, fuku o yasuku katta.","german":"I bought clothes cheaply at the year-end sale.","furigana":"ねんまつ|う|だ|ふく|やす|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive Godan verb: to launch a product or offer it for sale: 新商品を売り出す. Noun: 売り出し (sale); 売り出し中 also means \"up-and-coming\" (売り出し中の歌手).","examples":[{"japanese":"その会社は来月、新しいスマホを売り出す。","romaji":"Sono kaisha wa raigetsu, atarashii sumaho o uridasu.","german":"The company will put a new smartphone on the market next month.","furigana":"かいしゃ|らいげつ|あたら|う|だ"},{"japanese":"限定品は明日の朝十時から売り出される。","romaji":"Genteihin wa ashita no asa jūji kara uridasareru.","german":"The limited items go on sale from 10 a.m. tomorrow.","furigana":"げんていひん|あした|あさじゅうじ|う|だ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Intransitive Godan verb, counterpart of 植える: mostly 植わっている (... is planted there).","examples":[{"japanese":"庭には大きな桜の木が植わっている。","romaji":"Niwa ni wa ōki na sakura no ki ga uwatte iru.","german":"A big cherry tree is planted in the garden.","furigana":"にわ|おお|さくら|き|う"},{"japanese":"道の両側に花が植わっていて、とてもきれいだ。","romaji":"Michi no ryōgawa ni hana ga uwatte ite, totemo kirei da.","german":"Flowers are planted on both sides of the road, and it's very pretty.","furigana":"みち|りょうがわ|はな|う"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Conveyance of goods: 運送会社 (shipping company), 運送料 (freight charges).","examples":[{"japanese":"引っ越しの荷物は運送会社に頼んだ。","romaji":"Hikkoshi no nimotsu wa unsō gaisha ni tanonda.","german":"I asked a shipping company to handle my moving boxes.","furigana":"ひ|こ|にもつ|うんそうがいしゃ|たの"},{"japanese":"燃料費が上がって、運送のコストが増えた。","romaji":"Nenryōhi ga agatte, unsō no kosuto ga fueta.","german":"Fuel costs rose, so transport costs increased.","furigana":"ねんりょうひ|あ|うんそう|ふ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Replaces the rest of a quotation (\"... and so on\") or, as the verb 云々する, means \"to comment on something\". Literary.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は「時間がない」云々と言い訳ばかりしている。","romaji":"Kare wa \"jikan ga nai\" unnun to iiwake bakari shite iru.","german":"He keeps making excuses like \"I have no time\" and so on.","furigana":"かれ|じかん|うんぬん|い|わけ"},{"japanese":"結果が出る前に成否を云々するのは早い。","romaji":"Kekka ga deru mae ni seihi o unnun suru no wa hayai.","german":"It is too early to talk about success or failure before the results are out.","furigana":"けっか|で|まえ|せいひ|うんぬん|はや"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Letters of the Latin alphabet, often in 英字新聞 (English-language newspaper).","examples":[{"japanese":"毎朝、英字新聞を読んで英語を勉強している。","romaji":"Maiasa, eiji shinbun o yonde eigo o benkyō shite iru.","german":"Every morning I read an English-language newspaper to study English.","furigana":"まいあさ|えいじしんぶん|よ|えいご|べんきょう"},{"japanese":"パスワードには英字と数字を組み合わせてください。","romaji":"Pasuwādo ni wa eiji to sūji o kumiawasete kudasai.","german":"Please combine letters and numbers in your password.","furigana":"えいじ|すうじ|く|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Projecting films or images: 映写機 (projector), 映写室 (projection room).","examples":[{"japanese":"古い映写機で昔の映画を映写した。","romaji":"Furui eishaki de mukashi no eiga o eisha shita.","german":"We screened an old film with an old projector.","furigana":"ふる|えいしゃき|むかし|えいが|えいしゃ"},{"japanese":"映写が始まると、会場は静かになった。","romaji":"Eisha ga hajimaru to, kaijō wa shizuka ni natta.","german":"When the screening started, the hall fell quiet.","furigana":"えいしゃ|はじ|かいじょう|しず"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Liquid, often in compounds: 消毒液 (disinfectant), 血液 (blood). Homophone: 駅.","examples":[{"japanese":"容器から液が漏れている。","romaji":"Yōki kara eki ga morete iru.","german":"Liquid is leaking from the container.","furigana":"ようき|えき|も"},{"japanese":"この液を水で十倍に薄めて使ってください。","romaji":"Kono eki o mizu de jūbai ni usumete tsukatte kudasai.","german":"Dilute this liquid ten times with water before use.","furigana":"えき|みず|じゅうばい|うす|つか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Collar of a garment. Idiom: 襟を正す (to straighten up, to adopt a serious attitude).","examples":[{"japanese":"シャツの襟が汚れている。","romaji":"Shatsu no eri ga yogorete iru.","german":"The collar of the shirt is dirty.","furigana":"えり|よご"},{"japanese":"寒いので、コートの襟を立てた。","romaji":"Samui node, kōto no eri o tateta.","german":"Because it was cold, I turned up my coat collar.","furigana":"さむ|えり|た"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"な-adjective: to express something politely and indirectly: 婉曲に断る (to decline indirectly), 婉曲な言い方 (a roundabout way of putting it).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は誘いを婉曲に断った。","romaji":"Kanojo wa sasoi o enkyoku ni kotowatta.","german":"She turned down the invitation in a roundabout, polite way.","furigana":"かのじょ|さそ|えんきょく|ことわ"},{"japanese":"日本語には婉曲な言い方が多い。","romaji":"Nihongo ni wa enkyoku na iikata ga ōi.","german":"Japanese has many indirect ways of saying things.","furigana":"にほんご|えんきょく|い|かた|おお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"As a verb 演出する: to stage a play, film or event. The person is called 演出家 (director, especially in theater).","examples":[{"japanese":"この舞台の演出はとても斬新だった。","romaji":"Kono butai no enshutsu wa totemo zanshin datta.","german":"The staging of this play was very innovative.","furigana":"ぶたい|えんしゅつ|ざんしん"},{"japanese":"結婚式の演出を友人に任せた。","romaji":"Kekkonshiki no enshutsu o yūjin ni makaseta.","german":"I left the arrangements for the wedding to a friend.","furigana":"けっこんしき|えんしゅつ|ゆうじん|まか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To play a role: 主役を演じる. Figuratively \"to make a blunder\": 失態を演じる. Literary variant: 演ずる (N3).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は映画で医者の役を演じた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa eiga de isha no yaku o enjita.","german":"She played the role of a doctor in the film.","furigana":"かのじょ|えいが|いしゃ|やく|えん"},{"japanese":"彼は大事な会議で失態を演じてしまった。","romaji":"Kare wa daiji na kaigi de shittai o enjite shimatta.","german":"He made a blunder at an important meeting.","furigana":"かれ|だいじ|かいぎ|しったい|えん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Formal: 遠方から (from far away), 遠方に住む (to live far away). More everyday: 遠く.","examples":[{"japanese":"遠方からお越しいただき、ありがとうございます。","romaji":"Enpō kara okoshi itadaki, arigatō gozaimasu.","german":"Thank you for coming from so far away.","furigana":"えんぽう|こ"},{"japanese":"祖母は遠方に住んでいるので、なかなか会えない。","romaji":"Sobo wa enpō ni sunde iru node, nakanaka aenai.","german":"My grandmother lives far away, so I rarely get to see her.","furigana":"そぼ|えんぽう|す|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Literary word for tail (colloquial しっぽ); also the tail of a comet. Idiom: 尾を引く (to linger on).","examples":[{"japanese":"夜空に長い尾を引いて彗星が流れた。","romaji":"Yozora ni nagai o o hiite suisei ga nagareta.","german":"A comet streaked across the night sky, trailing a long tail.","furigana":"よぞら|なが|お|ひ|すいせい|なが"},{"japanese":"その鳥は尾が長くて美しい。","romaji":"Sono tori wa o ga nagakute utsukushii.","german":"That bird has a long, beautiful tail.","furigana":"とり|お|なが|うつく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive: 牛を柵に追い込む (to drive cattle into a pen), 相手を窮地に追い込む (to corner an opponent). Noun 追い込み (final spurt): 最後の追い込み.","examples":[{"japanese":"犯人は警察に追い込まれて、逃げ場を失った。","romaji":"Hannin wa keisatsu ni oikomarete, nigeba o ushinatta.","german":"The criminal was cornered by the police and had nowhere to run.","furigana":"はんにん|けいさつ|お|こ|に|ば|うしな"},{"japanese":"試験前の一週間、自分を追い込んで勉強した。","romaji":"Shiken mae no isshūkan, jibun o oikonde benkyō shita.","german":"In the week before the exam, I really pushed myself to study.","furigana":"しけんまえ|いっしゅうかん|じぶん|お|こ|べんきょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Literary; in everyday speech 年を取る. Often in the た-form before nouns: 老いた両親. Noun: 老い (N3).","examples":[{"japanese":"老いた両親の世話をするために、実家に戻った。","romaji":"Oita ryōshin no sewa o suru tame ni, jikka ni modotta.","german":"I returned to my parents' home to take care of my aging parents.","furigana":"お|りょうしん|せわ|じっか|もど"},{"japanese":"人は誰でも老いるものだ。","romaji":"Hito wa dare demo oiru mono da.","german":"Everyone grows old.","furigana":"ひと|だれ|お"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mainly figurative: 責任を負う (to bear responsibility), けがを負う (to suffer an injury), 〜に負うところが大きい (to owe a great deal to ...). Carrying on the back is usually 背負う.","examples":[{"japanese":"事故の責任は会社が負うべきだ。","romaji":"Jiko no sekinin wa kaisha ga ou beki da.","german":"The company should bear responsibility for the accident.","furigana":"じこ|せきにん|かいしゃ|お"},{"japanese":"彼は試合中に足にけがを負った。","romaji":"Kare wa shiaichū ni ashi ni kega o otta.","german":"He injured his leg during the match.","furigana":"かれ|しあいちゅう|あし|お"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Elevated word for gold (everyday 金（きん）). Often figurative: 黄金時代 (golden age), 黄金色 (golden color).","examples":[{"japanese":"その寺には黄金の仏像がある。","romaji":"Sono tera ni wa ōgon no butsuzō ga aru.","german":"That temple has a golden Buddha statue.","furigana":"てら|おうごん|ぶつぞう"},{"japanese":"秋の田んぼは黄金に輝いていた。","romaji":"Aki no tanbo wa ōgon ni kagayaite ita.","german":"The autumn rice fields shone golden.","furigana":"あき|た|おうごん|かがや"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"As a verb 往診する. The doctor comes to the patient's home instead of the patient going to the clinic.","examples":[{"japanese":"熱が高いので、医者に往診を頼んだ。","romaji":"Netsu ga takai node, isha ni ōshin o tanonda.","german":"Because my fever was high, I asked the doctor to make a house call.","furigana":"ねつ|たか|いしゃ|おうしん|たの"},{"japanese":"この先生は週に二回、お年寄りの家を往診している。","romaji":"Kono sensei wa shū ni nikai, otoshiyori no ie o ōshin shite iru.","german":"This doctor makes house calls to elderly people twice a week.","furigana":"せんせい|しゅう|にかい|としよ|いえ|おうしん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"As a noun \"the greater part\" (大方の人 = most people), as an adverb \"for the most part; probably\": 大方そんなことだろう (that's probably what it is).","examples":[{"japanese":"大方の予想に反して、そのチームが優勝した。","romaji":"Ōkata no yosō ni hanshite, sono chīmu ga yūshō shita.","german":"Contrary to most people's expectations, that team won the championship.","furigana":"おおかた|よそう|はん|ゆうしょう"},{"japanese":"仕事は大方終わった。","romaji":"Shigoto wa ōkata owatta.","german":"The work is mostly finished.","furigana":"しごと|おおかたお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"な-adjective and noun: 大柄な男性 (a big-framed man), 大柄の着物 (a kimono with a large pattern). Opposite: 小柄.","examples":[{"japanese":"父は大柄で、力も強い。","romaji":"Chichi wa ōgara de, chikara mo tsuyoi.","german":"My father is big and also strong.","furigana":"ちち|おおがら|ちから|つよ"},{"japanese":"大柄の花模様のワンピースを買った。","romaji":"Ōgara no hana moyō no wanpīsu o katta.","german":"I bought a dress with a large flower pattern.","furigana":"おおがら|はなもよう|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"な-adjective: 大げさに言う (to exaggerate). The kanji 大袈裟 is rare. Similar: オーバー (colloquial).","examples":[{"japanese":"少し転んだだけなのに、彼は大げさに痛がった。","romaji":"Sukoshi koronda dake na noni, kare wa ōgesa ni itagatta.","german":"Even though he had only taken a small fall, he made a big show of his pain.","furigana":"すこ|ころ|かれ|おお|いた"},{"japanese":"そんな大げさな話ではないよ。","romaji":"Sonna ōgesa na hanashi de wa nai yo.","german":"It's not such a big deal.","furigana":"おお|はなし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"大筋で合意する (to agree in principle). For the plot of a story, あらすじ is more common.","examples":[{"japanese":"両社は大筋で合意した。","romaji":"Ryōsha wa ōsuji de gōi shita.","german":"The two companies agreed in outline.","furigana":"りょうしゃ|おおすじ|ごうい"},{"japanese":"計画の大筋を説明します。","romaji":"Keikaku no ōsuji o setsumei shimasu.","german":"I will explain the outline of the plan.","furigana":"けいかく|おおすじ|せつめい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A vivid, somewhat older word; in news reports 洪水 or 浸水 is more usual.","examples":[{"japanese":"大雨で川があふれ、村は大水に襲われた。","romaji":"Ōame de kawa ga afure, mura wa ōmizu ni osowareta.","german":"Heavy rain made the river overflow, and the village was hit by flooding.","furigana":"おおあめ|かわ|むら|おおみず|おそ"},{"japanese":"大水が引いたあと、家の片付けが大変だった。","romaji":"Ōmizu ga hiita ato, ie no katazuke ga taihen datta.","german":"After the floodwater receded, cleaning up the house was hard work.","furigana":"おおみず|ひ|いえ|かたづ|たいへん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"公の場 (in public), 公にする (to make public), 公になる (to become public).","examples":[{"japanese":"公の場では言葉に気をつけなさい。","romaji":"Ōyake no ba de wa kotoba ni ki o tsukenasai.","german":"Watch your words in public.","furigana":"おおやけ|ば|ことば|き"},{"japanese":"その事件は半年後にようやく公になった。","romaji":"Sono jiken wa hantoshi go ni yōyaku ōyake ni natta.","german":"The incident finally became public half a year later.","furigana":"じけん|はんとしご|おおやけ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive; 遅らせる has the same meaning and is more common.","examples":[{"japanese":"雨のため、出発を一時間遅らすことにした。","romaji":"Ame no tame, shuppatsu o ichijikan okurasu koto ni shita.","german":"Because of the rain, we decided to delay our departure by an hour.","furigana":"あめ|しゅっぱつ|いちじかんおく"},{"japanese":"この薬には病気の進行を遅らす効果がある。","romaji":"Kono kusuri ni wa byōki no shinkō o okurasu kōka ga aru.","german":"This medicine has the effect of slowing the progression of the disease.","furigana":"くすり|びょうき|しんこう|おく|こうか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Also pronounced オッケー and written OK. オーケーを出す／もらう (to give / to get approval).","examples":[{"japanese":"部長からやっとオーケーが出た。","romaji":"Buchō kara yatto ōkē ga deta.","german":"The department head finally gave his OK.","furigana":"ぶちょう|で"},{"japanese":"「明日十時でいい？」「オーケー。」","romaji":"\"Ashita jūji de ii?\" \"Ōkē.\"","german":"\"Is ten tomorrow all right?\" \"OK.\"","furigana":"あしたじゅうじ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Behavior judged in moral terms: 日頃の行い (everyday conduct). Verb: 行う.","examples":[{"japanese":"日頃の行いがいいから、晴れたんだね。","romaji":"Higoro no okonai ga ii kara, hareta n da ne.","german":"It's because of how well you behave day to day that the weather cleared up, isn't it?","furigana":"ひごろ|おこな|は"},{"japanese":"彼の行いは皆の手本になった。","romaji":"Kare no okonai wa mina no tehon ni natta.","german":"His conduct became a model for everyone.","furigana":"かれ|おこな|みな|てほん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Intransitive: 痛みが治まる (the pain subsides), 騒ぎが治まる (the commotion dies down). Transitive: 治める. Homophones: 収まる and 納まる.","examples":[{"japanese":"薬を飲んだら、頭痛が治まった。","romaji":"Kusuri o nondara, zutsū ga osamatta.","german":"After I took the medicine, my headache subsided.","furigana":"くすり|の|ずつう|おさ"},{"japanese":"騒ぎはなかなか治まらなかった。","romaji":"Sawagi wa nakanaka osamaranakatta.","german":"The uproar would not die down for a long time.","furigana":"さわ|おさ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Everyday word for 出産. お産が軽い／重い (an easy / a difficult birth).","examples":[{"japanese":"姉のお産は予定より早かった。","romaji":"Ane no osan wa yotei yori hayakatta.","german":"My older sister gave birth earlier than planned.","furigana":"あね|さん|よてい|はや"},{"japanese":"お産のために、妻は実家に帰っている。","romaji":"Osan no tame ni, tsuma wa jikka ni kaette iru.","german":"My wife has gone back to her parents' home for the delivery.","furigana":"さん|つま|じっか|かえ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mostly 〜を押し切って (pushing through despite ...): 親の反対を押し切って結婚した (married despite her parents' objections).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は親の反対を押し切って留学した。","romaji":"Kanojo wa oya no hantai o oshikitte ryūgaku shita.","german":"She went to study abroad against her parents' opposition.","furigana":"かのじょ|おや|はんたい|お|き|りゅうがく"},{"japanese":"社長は反対意見を押し切って計画を進めた。","romaji":"Shachō wa hantai iken o oshikitte keikaku o susumeta.","german":"The company president pushed the plan through over opposing opinions.","furigana":"しゃちょう|はんたいいけん|お|き|けいかく|すす"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive: 荷物をかばんに押し込む (to stuff luggage into a bag). Less often also \"to break in\" (押し込み強盗 = home-invasion robbery).","examples":[{"japanese":"服を全部スーツケースに押し込んだ。","romaji":"Fuku o zenbu sūtsukēsu ni oshikonda.","german":"I crammed all my clothes into the suitcase.","furigana":"ふく|ぜんぶ|お|こ"},{"japanese":"満員電車に無理やり押し込まれた。","romaji":"Man'in densha ni muriyari oshikomareta.","german":"I was forced into the packed train.","furigana":"まんいんでんしゃ|むり|お|こ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Many people or masses of water surge in: 観光客が押し寄せる (tourists flood in), 波が押し寄せる (waves surge in).","examples":[{"japanese":"セールの初日、客が店に押し寄せた。","romaji":"Sēru no shonichi, kyaku ga mise ni oshiyoseta.","german":"On the first day of the sale, customers flooded into the store.","furigana":"しょにち|きゃく|みせ|お|よ"},{"japanese":"大きな波が海岸に押し寄せてきた。","romaji":"Ōki na nami ga kaigan ni oshiyosete kita.","german":"Big waves came surging onto the shore.","furigana":"おお|なみ|かいがん|お|よ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Often in katakana オス. Opposite: 雌（めす）. Not used for people.","examples":[{"japanese":"この子犬は雄ですか、雌ですか。","romaji":"Kono koinu wa osu desu ka, mesu desu ka.","german":"Is this puppy a male or a female?","furigana":"こいぬ|おす|めす"},{"japanese":"クジャクは雄のほうが羽がきれいだ。","romaji":"Kujaku wa osu no hō ga hane ga kirei da.","german":"Among peafowl, the male has the more beautiful feathers.","furigana":"おす|はね"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To praise someone to get them to do something: おだてられて引き受けた (was flattered into accepting). The kanji 煽てる is rare.","examples":[{"japanese":"おだてても、何も出ないよ。","romaji":"Odatete mo, nani mo denai yo.","german":"Flattery won't get you anything.","furigana":"なに|で"},{"japanese":"みんなにおだてられて、つい幹事を引き受けてしまった。","romaji":"Minna ni odaterarete, tsui kanji o hikiukete shimatta.","german":"Everyone egged me on, and I ended up taking on the organizing.","furigana":"かんじ|ひ|う"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Most common meaning: to be down in the dumps - 試験に落ちて落ち込んでいる. Also economically: 売り上げが落ち込む.","examples":[{"japanese":"試合に負けて、彼はひどく落ち込んでいる。","romaji":"Shiai ni makete, kare wa hidoku ochikonde iru.","german":"After losing the match, he is deeply depressed.","furigana":"しあい|ま|かれ|お|こ"},{"japanese":"今年は売り上げが大きく落ち込んだ。","romaji":"Kotoshi wa uriage ga ōkiku ochikonda.","german":"Sales have slumped sharply this year.","furigana":"ことし|う|あ|おお|お|こ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 16 (source: N4; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"落ち着きがある (to be composed), 落ち着きがない (to be restless, fidgety). Verb: 落ち着く.","examples":[{"japanese":"弟は落ち着きがなくて、じっと座っていられない。","romaji":"Otōto wa ochitsuki ga nakute, jitto suwatte irarenai.","german":"My little brother is restless and can't sit still.","furigana":"おとうと|お|つ|すわ"},{"japanese":"彼女は若いのに落ち着きがある。","romaji":"Kanojo wa wakai noni ochitsuki ga aru.","german":"Although she is young, she has an air of composure.","furigana":"かのじょ|わか|お|つ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"In contracts, 甲（こう）and 乙 stand for the two contracting parties. As a な-adjective: 乙な味 (a refined, special taste).","examples":[{"japanese":"契約書では、会社を甲、社員を乙とする。","romaji":"Keiyakusho de wa, kaisha o kō, shain o otsu to suru.","german":"In the contract, the company is party A and the employee is party B.","furigana":"けいやくしょ|かいしゃ|こう|しゃいん|おつ"},{"japanese":"冬に冷たいビールを飲むのも乙なものだ。","romaji":"Fuyu ni tsumetai bīru o nomu no mo otsu na mono da.","german":"Drinking a cold beer in winter has its own special charm.","furigana":"ふゆ|つめ|の|おつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Especially for children: お使いに行く (to go on an errand, shopping for one's parents). Also written お遣い.","examples":[{"japanese":"子どもの頃、よく母のお使いでパンを買いに行った。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, yoku haha no otsukai de pan o kai ni itta.","german":"As a child, I often went to buy bread on errands for my mother.","furigana":"こ|ころ|はは|つか|か|い"},{"japanese":"お使いを頼まれて、郵便局へ行った。","romaji":"Otsukai o tanomarete, yūbinkyoku e itta.","german":"I was asked to run an errand and went to the post office.","furigana":"つか|たの|ゆうびんきょく|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Colloquial for 怖い.","examples":[{"japanese":"あの先生は怒るとおっかない。","romaji":"Ano sensei wa okoru to okkanai.","german":"That teacher is scary when he gets angry.","furigana":"せんせい|おこ"},{"japanese":"夜の山道は暗くておっかなかった。","romaji":"Yoru no yamamichi wa kurakute okkanakatta.","german":"The mountain road at night was dark and scary.","furigana":"よる|やまみち|くら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Literally \"raising the hands\" as a sign of surrender: これはお手上げだ (I give up on this).","examples":[{"japanese":"この問題は難しすぎて、お手上げだ。","romaji":"Kono mondai wa muzukashisugite, oteage da.","german":"This problem is too hard, I give up.","furigana":"もんだい|むずか|てあ"},{"japanese":"パソコンが急に動かなくなって、私にはお手上げだった。","romaji":"Pasokon ga kyū ni ugokanaku natte, watashi ni wa oteage datta.","german":"The computer suddenly stopped working, and I was at my wits' end.","furigana":"きゅう|うご|わたし|てあ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Humble: お供します (I will accompany you). Jokingly also for side dishes: ご飯のお供 (something to go with rice).","examples":[{"japanese":"駅までお供します。","romaji":"Eki made otomo shimasu.","german":"I'll accompany you to the station.","furigana":"えき|とも"},{"japanese":"社長のお供で大阪に出張した。","romaji":"Shachō no otomo de Ōsaka ni shucchō shita.","german":"I accompanied the company president on a business trip to Osaka.","furigana":"しゃちょう|とも|おおさか|しゅっちょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Noun of 驚く. 驚きを隠せない (cannot hide one's astonishment).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼が優勝したことは、みんなにとって驚きだった。","romaji":"Kare ga yūshō shita koto wa, minna ni totte odoroki datta.","german":"His victory was a surprise to everyone.","furigana":"かれ|ゆうしょう|おどろ"},{"japanese":"彼女は驚きのあまり、声も出なかった。","romaji":"Kanojo wa odoroki no amari, koe mo denakatta.","german":"She was so astonished that she couldn't make a sound.","furigana":"かのじょ|おどろ|こえ|で"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Colloquial; more formal: 同じ年齢.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼とは同い年なので、すぐに仲良くなった。","romaji":"Kare to wa onaidoshi na node, sugu ni nakayoku natta.","german":"We are the same age, so we became friends quickly.","furigana":"かれ|おな|どし|なかよ"},{"japanese":"同い年の友達が先に結婚した。","romaji":"Onaidoshi no tomodachi ga saki ni kekkon shita.","german":"A friend my age got married before me.","furigana":"おな|どし|ともだち|さき|けっこん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Literary; same meaning as 自ずと.","examples":[{"japanese":"努力を続ければ、結果は自ずからついてくる。","romaji":"Doryoku o tsuzukereba, kekka wa onozukara tsuite kuru.","german":"If you keep making an effort, results will come naturally.","furigana":"どりょく|つづ|けっか|おの"},{"japanese":"事実を並べれば、答えは自ずから明らかになる。","romaji":"Jijitsu o narabereba, kotae wa onozukara akiraka ni naru.","german":"If you line up the facts, the answer becomes clear by itself.","furigana":"じじつ|なら|こた|おの|あき"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mostly figurative: 赤みを帯びる (to take on a red tinge), 現実味を帯びる (to become realistic), 使命を帯びる (to be entrusted with a mission).","examples":[{"japanese":"夕方になると、空が赤みを帯びてきた。","romaji":"Yūgata ni naru to, sora ga akami o obite kita.","german":"Toward evening, the sky took on a reddish tinge.","furigana":"ゆうがた|そら|あか|お"},{"japanese":"その計画は次第に現実味を帯びてきた。","romaji":"Sono keikaku wa shidai ni genjitsumi o obite kita.","german":"The plan gradually became more realistic.","furigana":"けいかく|しだい|げんじつみ|お"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Colloquial, often used by adult men about their own mother. For the father, correspondingly: 親父（おやじ）.","examples":[{"japanese":"お袋の料理が一番うまい。","romaji":"Ofukuro no ryōri ga ichiban umai.","german":"My mom's cooking is the best.","furigana":"ふくろ|りょうり|いちばん"},{"japanese":"正月にはお袋に顔を見せに帰るつもりだ。","romaji":"Shōgatsu ni wa ofukuro ni kao o mise ni kaeru tsumori da.","german":"I'm planning to go home at New Year's and show my face to my mother.","furigana":"しょうがつ|ふくろ|かお|み|かえ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"覚えがある／ない (to remember / not to remember), 覚えが早い (to learn quickly).","examples":[{"japanese":"そんなことを言った覚えはない。","romaji":"Sonna koto o itta oboe wa nai.","german":"I have no recollection of saying such a thing.","furigana":"い|おぼ"},{"japanese":"この子は覚えが早い。","romaji":"Kono ko wa oboe ga hayai.","german":"This child learns quickly.","furigana":"こ|おぼ|はや"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mostly in hiragana. Also common as the conjunction おまけに (on top of that): 雨が降ってきた。おまけに風も強い (It started to rain. On top of that, the wind is strong).","examples":[{"japanese":"お菓子におもちゃのおまけが付いていた。","romaji":"Okashi ni omocha no omake ga tsuite ita.","german":"The candy came with a free toy.","furigana":"かし|つ"},{"japanese":"たくさん買ったので、店の人が百円おまけしてくれた。","romaji":"Takusan katta node, mise no hito ga hyakuen omake shite kureta.","german":"Because I bought a lot, the shop clerk gave me a hundred-yen discount.","furigana":"か|みせ|ひと|ひゃくえん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Familiar word for 神社. A baby's first shrine visit is called お宮参り.","examples":[{"japanese":"村のお宮で夏祭りが開かれる。","romaji":"Mura no omiya de natsumatsuri ga hirakareru.","german":"A summer festival is held at the village shrine.","furigana":"むら|みや|なつまつ|ひら"},{"japanese":"祖父は毎朝お宮にお参りしている。","romaji":"Sofu wa maiasa omiya ni omairi shite iru.","german":"My grandfather visits the shrine every morning.","furigana":"そふ|まいあさ|みや|まい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Often slightly pejorative: 思いつきで決める (to decide on impulse, without thinking). Verb: 思いつく.","examples":[{"japanese":"それはただの思いつきで、よく考えたわけではない。","romaji":"Sore wa tada no omoitsuki de, yoku kangaeta wake de wa nai.","german":"That was just an idea that came to me; I didn't think it through.","furigana":"おも|かんが"},{"japanese":"いい思いつきだね。すぐにやってみよう。","romaji":"Ii omoitsuki da ne. Sugu ni yatte miyō.","german":"That's a good idea. Let's try it right away.","furigana":"おも"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Literary ずる-form of 重んじる (N1), same meaning: 伝統を重んずる (to value tradition).","examples":[{"japanese":"この家では昔から礼儀を重んずる。","romaji":"Kono ie de wa mukashi kara reigi o omonzuru.","german":"This family has valued etiquette since long ago.","furigana":"いえ|むかし|れいぎ|おも"},{"japanese":"彼は何よりも信用を重んずる商人だった。","romaji":"Kare wa nani yori mo shin'yō o omonzuru shōnin datta.","german":"He was a merchant who valued trust above all else.","furigana":"かれ|なに|しんよう|おも|しょうにん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Elevated: その折は (on that occasion), 折を見て (when the opportunity arises), 〜の折に (on the occasion of ...).","examples":[{"japanese":"京都に来た折には、ぜひ連絡してください。","romaji":"Kyōto ni kita ori ni wa, zehi renraku shite kudasai.","german":"When you come to Kyoto, please be sure to contact me.","furigana":"きょうと|き|おり|れんらく"},{"japanese":"折を見て、社長に相談してみます。","romaji":"Ori o mite, shachō ni sōdan shite mimasu.","german":"When the opportunity arises, I'll try talking to the president.","furigana":"おり|み|しゃちょう|そうだん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"For animals; figuratively also a prison cell.","examples":[{"japanese":"動物園のライオンが檻の中を歩き回っている。","romaji":"Dōbutsuen no raion ga ori no naka o arukimawatte iru.","german":"The lion at the zoo is pacing around in its cage.","furigana":"どうぶつえん|おり|なか|ある|まわ"},{"japanese":"逃げた猿は、無事に檻に戻された。","romaji":"Nigeta saru wa, buji ni ori ni modosareta.","german":"The escaped monkey was safely returned to its cage.","furigana":"に|さる|ぶじ|おり|もど"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The noun 折り返し is very common adverbially in the office: 折り返し電話する／折り返しご連絡します (to call back / get back to you right away). Also a turning point: 折り返し地点.","examples":[{"japanese":"ズボンのすそを折り返してはいた。","romaji":"Zubon no suso o orikaeshite haita.","german":"I wore the trousers with the hems turned up.","furigana":"お|かえ"},{"japanese":"マラソンの選手たちは十キロ地点で折り返した。","romaji":"Marason no senshutachi wa jukkiro chiten de orikaeshita.","german":"The marathon runners turned back at the ten-kilometer point.","furigana":"せんしゅ|じゅっ|ちてん|お|かえ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"な-adjective: 温和な気候 (a mild climate), 温和な性格 (a gentle personality).","examples":[{"japanese":"この地方は一年中温和な気候だ。","romaji":"Kono chihō wa ichinenjū onwa na kikō da.","german":"This region has a mild climate all year round.","furigana":"ちほう|いちねんじゅうおんわ|きこう"},{"japanese":"彼は温和な人で、めったに怒らない。","romaji":"Kare wa onwa na hito de, metta ni okoranai.","german":"He is a gentle person and rarely gets angry.","furigana":"かれ|おんわ|ひと|おこ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Opposite: 上位. 下位に落ちる (to drop down the ranking), 下位チーム (a bottom-ranked team).","examples":[{"japanese":"そのチームは今季ずっと下位に低迷している。","romaji":"Sono chīmu wa konki zutto kai ni teimei shite iru.","german":"That team has been languishing near the bottom all season.","furigana":"こんき|かい|ていめい"},{"japanese":"売り上げランキングで、この商品は下位だった。","romaji":"Uriage rankingu de, kono shōhin wa kai datta.","german":"In the sales ranking, this product was near the bottom.","furigana":"う|あ|しょうひん|かい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Opposite: 改善／改良. Often critical of changes to rules or laws: それは改悪だ (that makes things worse).","examples":[{"japanese":"新しいルールは改善ではなく改悪だと批判された。","romaji":"Atarashii rūru wa kaizen de wa naku kaiaku da to hihan sareta.","german":"The new rule was criticized as a change for the worse rather than an improvement.","furigana":"あたら|かいぜん|かいあく|ひはん"},{"japanese":"アプリの更新で使いにくくなり、改悪だと感じた。","romaji":"Apuri no kōshin de tsukainikuku nari, kaiaku da to kanjita.","german":"The app update made it harder to use, and it felt like a downgrade.","furigana":"こうしん|つか|かいあく|かん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Freight transport by sea; the industry is called 海運業.","examples":[{"japanese":"日本の貿易は海運に大きく頼っている。","romaji":"Nihon no bōeki wa kaiun ni ōkiku tayotte iru.","german":"Japan's trade depends heavily on sea transport.","furigana":"にほん|ぼうえき|かいうん|おお|たよ"},{"japanese":"海運は航空輸送より時間がかかるが、費用は安い。","romaji":"Kaiun wa kōkū yusō yori jikan ga kakaru ga, hiyō wa yasui.","german":"Sea transport takes longer than air freight, but it is cheaper.","furigana":"かいうん|こうくうゆそう|じかん|ひよう|やす"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"外貨を稼ぐ (to earn foreign currency), 外貨預金 (foreign-currency deposit).","examples":[{"japanese":"空港で円を外貨に両替した。","romaji":"Kūkō de en o gaika ni ryōgae shita.","german":"I exchanged yen for foreign currency at the airport.","furigana":"くうこう|えん|がいか|りょうがえ"},{"japanese":"観光業は国にとって大切な外貨の稼ぎ手だ。","romaji":"Kankōgyō wa kuni ni totte taisetsu na gaika no kasegite da.","german":"Tourism is an important earner of foreign currency for the country.","furigana":"かんこうぎょう|くに|たいせつ|がいか|かせ|て"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The shellfish as an animal or food is called 貝.","examples":[{"japanese":"子どもたちは浜辺で貝殻を拾った。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa hamabe de kaigara o hirotta.","german":"The children picked up seashells on the beach.","furigana":"こ|はまべ|かいがら|ひろ"},{"japanese":"貝殻で作ったネックレスをもらった。","romaji":"Kaigara de tsukutta nekkuresu o moratta.","german":"I received a necklace made of shells.","furigana":"かいがら|つく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"社会階級 (social class), 軍の階級 (military rank); in sports also a weight class.","examples":[{"japanese":"昔は生まれた階級によって職業が決まっていた。","romaji":"Mukashi wa umareta kaikyū ni yotte shokugyō ga kimatte ita.","german":"In the past, the class one was born into determined one's occupation.","furigana":"むかし|う|かいきゅう|しょくぎょう|き"},{"japanese":"彼は軍で高い階級にあった。","romaji":"Kare wa gun de takai kaikyū ni atta.","german":"He held a high rank in the military.","furigana":"かれ|ぐん|たか|かいきゅう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"As a verb 回収する: ゴミの回収 (garbage collection), 製品の回収 (product recall), 資金を回収する (to recover funds).","examples":[{"japanese":"アンケートの用紙は明日回収します。","romaji":"Ankēto no yōshi wa ashita kaishū shimasu.","german":"We will collect the questionnaire forms tomorrow.","furigana":"ようし|あしたかいしゅう"},{"japanese":"欠陥が見つかり、メーカーは製品を回収した。","romaji":"Kekkan ga mitsukari, mēkā wa seihin o kaishū shita.","german":"After a defect was found, the manufacturer recalled the product.","furigana":"けっかん|み|せいひん|かいしゅう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Short form used in the news for 外務大臣.","examples":[{"japanese":"両国の外相が東京で会談した。","romaji":"Ryōkoku no gaishō ga Tōkyō de kaidan shita.","german":"The foreign ministers of the two countries held talks in Tokyo.","furigana":"りょうこく|がいしょう|とうきょう|かいだん"},{"japanese":"新しい外相は就任後すぐにアメリカを訪問した。","romaji":"Atarashii gaishō wa shūnin go sugu ni Amerika o hōmon shita.","german":"The new foreign minister visited the United States right after taking office.","furigana":"あたら|がいしょう|しゅうにんご|ほうもん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Literary: 健康を害する (to harm one's health), 気分を害する (to offend someone; to be offended).","examples":[{"japanese":"働きすぎて健康を害した。","romaji":"Hatarakisugite kenkō o gaishita.","german":"I damaged my health by working too much.","furigana":"はたら|けんこう|がい"},{"japanese":"私の一言が彼の気分を害してしまったようだ。","romaji":"Watashi no hitokoto ga kare no kibun o gaishite shimatta yō da.","german":"My remark seems to have put him in a bad mood.","furigana":"わたし|ひとこと|かれ|きぶん|がい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Often in book titles: 日本史概説 (Outline of Japanese History). As a verb 概説する.","examples":[{"japanese":"最初の授業では、この分野を概説します。","romaji":"Saisho no jugyō de wa, kono bun'ya o gaisetsu shimasu.","german":"In the first class, I will give an overview of this field.","furigana":"さいしょ|じゅぎょう|ぶんや|がいせつ"},{"japanese":"報告書の冒頭に計画の概説がある。","romaji":"Hōkokusho no bōtō ni keikaku no gaisetsu ga aru.","german":"At the beginning of the report there is an outline of the plan.","furigana":"ほうこくしょ|ぼうとう|けいかく|がいせつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"On trains and buses, the sign 回送 means no passengers are carried. To forward mail: 回送する.","examples":[{"japanese":"このバスは回送なので、乗れません。","romaji":"Kono basu wa kaisō na node, noremasen.","german":"This bus is out of service, so you can't board.","furigana":"かいそう|の"},{"japanese":"引っ越し先に郵便物を回送してもらった。","romaji":"Hikkoshisaki ni yūbinbutsu o kaisō shite moratta.","german":"I had my mail forwarded to my new address.","furigana":"ひ|こ|さき|ゆうびんぶつ|かいそう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mostly between politicians or heads of companies: 首脳会談 (summit meeting). Homophones: 階段 (stairs), 怪談 (ghost story).","examples":[{"japanese":"両国の首相は二時間にわたって会談した。","romaji":"Ryōkoku no shushō wa nijikan ni watatte kaidan shita.","german":"The prime ministers of the two countries held talks for two hours.","furigana":"りょうこく|しゅしょう|にじかん|かいだん"},{"japanese":"会談の内容は公表されなかった。","romaji":"Kaidan no naiyō wa kōhyō sarenakatta.","german":"The content of the talks was not made public.","furigana":"かいだん|ないよう|こうひょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"街頭で (out on the street): 街頭演説 (street speech), 街頭インタビュー (street interview). Homophone: 街灯 (street lamp).","examples":[{"japanese":"選挙が近づき、候補者が街頭で演説している。","romaji":"Senkyo ga chikazuki, kōhosha ga gaitō de enzetsu shite iru.","german":"With the election approaching, candidates are giving speeches on the street.","furigana":"せんきょ|ちか|こうほしゃ|がいとう|えんぜつ"},{"japanese":"駅前の街頭で募金を呼びかけた。","romaji":"Ekimae no gaitō de bokin o yobikaketa.","german":"In front of the station, we appealed for donations on the street.","furigana":"えきまえ|がいとう|ぼきん|よ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Short-term care: 酔った友達を介抱する (to look after a drunk friend). Long-term care is called 介護 or 看病. Homophone: 解放 (liberation).","examples":[{"japanese":"酔いつぶれた同僚を朝まで介抱した。","romaji":"Yoitsubureta dōryō o asa made kaihō shita.","german":"I looked after my dead-drunk colleague until morning.","furigana":"よ|どうりょう|あさ|かいほう"},{"japanese":"道で倒れた人を近くの店員が介抱していた。","romaji":"Michi de taoreta hito o chikaku no ten'in ga kaihō shite ita.","german":"A nearby shop clerk was tending to the person who had collapsed on the street.","furigana":"みち|たお|ひと|ちか|てんいん|かいほう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A document is passed on from person to person in turn: 回覧する. Neighborhood notices go around via the 回覧板 (N4).","examples":[{"japanese":"会議の資料を部内で回覧した。","romaji":"Kaigi no shiryō o bunai de kairan shita.","german":"The meeting materials were circulated within the department.","furigana":"かいぎ|しりょう|ぶない|かいらん"},{"japanese":"この書類は読んだら次の人に回覧してください。","romaji":"Kono shorui wa yondara tsugi no hito ni kairan shite kudasai.","german":"Please pass this document on to the next person after reading it.","furigana":"しょるい|よ|つぎ|ひと|かいらん"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 38 (source: N4; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"To write down what is spoken: 先生の話を書き取る. Noun: 書き取り (dictation).","examples":[{"japanese":"電話番号を急いでメモに書き取った。","romaji":"Denwa bangō o isoide memo ni kakitotta.","german":"I hurriedly jotted down the phone number on a memo.","furigana":"でんわばんごう|いそ|か|と"},{"japanese":"先生の言うことを一言も漏らさず書き取った。","romaji":"Sensei no iu koto o hitokoto mo morasazu kakitotta.","german":"I wrote down every word the teacher said.","furigana":"せんせい|い|ひとこと|も|か|と"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Literally: スープをかき回す (to stir soup). Figuratively: 会議をかき回す (to stir up a meeting). Kanji 掻き回す.","examples":[{"japanese":"鍋のスープをゆっくりかき回した。","romaji":"Nabe no sūpu o yukkuri kakimawashita.","german":"I slowly stirred the soup in the pot.","furigana":"なべ|まわ"},{"japanese":"新人の一言が会議をかき回した。","romaji":"Shinjin no hitokoto ga kaigi o kakimawashita.","german":"A remark by the newcomer threw the meeting into disarray.","furigana":"しんじん|ひとこと|かいぎ|まわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Rang: 格が上 (höherrangig), 格が違う (in einer anderen Liga spielen). In der Grammatik: 格 (Kasus), 格助詞 (Kasuspartikel).","examples":[{"japanese":"あの選手は他の選手と格が違う。","romaji":"Ano senshu wa hoka no senshu to kaku ga chigau.","german":"Dieser Spieler spielt in einer ganz anderen Liga als die anderen.","furigana":"せんしゅ|ほか|せんしゅ|かく|ちが"},{"japanese":"ドイツ語には四つの格がある。","romaji":"Doitsugo ni wa yottsu no kaku ga aru.","german":"Das Deutsche hat vier Fälle.","furigana":"ご|よっ|かく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Formal, often on signs and in advertisements. More everyday: いろいろな.","examples":[{"japanese":"当店では各種クレジットカードがご利用いただけます。","romaji":"Tōten de wa kakushu kurejitto kādo ga goriyō itadakemasu.","german":"Our store accepts various credit cards.","furigana":"とうてん|かくしゅ|りよう"},{"japanese":"この工場では各種の部品を作っている。","romaji":"Kono kōjō de wa kakushu no buhin o tsukutte iru.","german":"This factory makes various kinds of parts.","furigana":"こうじょう|かくしゅ|ぶひん|つく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"掛けで買う (to buy on credit), 掛けで売る (to sell on credit). Today mainly in business dealings; colloquially also ツケ.","examples":[{"japanese":"昔は近所の店で掛けで買い物ができた。","romaji":"Mukashi wa kinjo no mise de kake de kaimono ga dekita.","german":"In the past, you could buy things on credit at neighborhood shops.","furigana":"むかし|きんじょ|みせ|か|か|もの"},{"japanese":"月末に掛けの代金をまとめて払う。","romaji":"Getsumatsu ni kake no daikin o matomete harau.","german":"At the end of the month, you pay the bills for credit purchases all at once.","furigana":"げつまつ|か|だいきん|はら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"駆け足で (at a run, in haste): 駆け足で説明する (to explain something in a rush).","examples":[{"japanese":"遅刻しそうだったので、駅まで駆け足で行った。","romaji":"Chikoku shisō datta node, eki made kakeashi de itta.","german":"I was about to be late, so I ran to the station.","furigana":"ちこく|えき|か|あし|い"},{"japanese":"時間がないので、駆け足で説明します。","romaji":"Jikan ga nai node, kakeashi de setsumei shimasu.","german":"There is little time, so I will explain it quickly.","furigana":"じかん|か|あし|せつめい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"As a verb 化合する; the result is called 化合物 (N2) (compound).","examples":[{"japanese":"水素と酸素が化合すると水ができる。","romaji":"Suiso to sanso ga kagō suru to mizu ga dekiru.","german":"When hydrogen and oxygen combine, water is formed.","furigana":"すいそ|さんそ|かごう|みず"},{"japanese":"この実験では二つの物質の化合を観察する。","romaji":"Kono jikken de wa futatsu no busshitsu no kagō o kansatsu suru.","german":"In this experiment, we observe the combination of two substances.","furigana":"じっけん|ふた|ぶっしつ|かごう|かんさつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"目が霞む (one's eyes blur). Figuratively: 彼の活躍で他の選手が霞んだ (next to his performance, the other players were overshadowed).","examples":[{"japanese":"遠くの山が霞んで見える。","romaji":"Tōku no yama ga kasunde mieru.","german":"The mountains in the distance look hazy.","furigana":"とお|やま|かす|み"},{"japanese":"疲れて目が霞んできた。","romaji":"Tsukarete me ga kasunde kita.","german":"I was tired and my eyes began to blur.","furigana":"つか|め|かす"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Literary, mostly 〜と化する／〜と化す: 町は廃墟と化した. As a suffix 〜化 (-ization): 自動化 (automation).","examples":[{"japanese":"地震で町は一瞬にして廃墟と化した。","romaji":"Jishin de machi wa isshun ni shite haikyo to kashita.","german":"The earthquake turned the town into ruins in an instant.","furigana":"じしん|まち|いっしゅん|はいきょ|か"},{"japanese":"一度の失敗で、彼の努力は水の泡と化した。","romaji":"Ichido no shippai de, kare no doryoku wa mizu no awa to kashita.","german":"With a single failure, all his efforts came to nothing.","furigana":"いちど|しっぱい|かれ|どりょく|みず|あわ|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Literary conjunction between two qualities or actions: 迅速かつ正確に (quickly and accurately). Usually written in hiragana かつ.","examples":[{"japanese":"この仕事は迅速かつ正確に行う必要がある。","romaji":"Kono shigoto wa jinsoku katsu seikaku ni okonau hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"This work needs to be done quickly and accurately.","furigana":"しごと|じんそく|せいかく|おこな|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"彼は優秀であり、かつ人柄もいい。","romaji":"Kare wa yūshū de ari, katsu hitogara mo ii.","german":"He is excellent and, moreover, a good person.","furigana":"かれ|ゆうしゅう|ひとがら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"With する or と: がっくりする, がっくりと肩を落とす (to slump one's shoulders in disappointment). Similar: がっかり.","examples":[{"japanese":"不合格の知らせに、彼はがっくりと肩を落とした。","romaji":"Fugōkaku no shirase ni, kare wa gakkuri to kata o otoshita.","german":"At the news that he had failed, he slumped his shoulders in disappointment.","furigana":"ふごうかく|し|かれ|かた|お"},{"japanese":"試合に負けて、選手たちはがっくりしていた。","romaji":"Shiai ni makete, senshutachi wa gakkuri shite ita.","german":"After losing the match, the players were dejected.","furigana":"しあい|ま|せんしゅ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mainly on forms and in documents. Read つきひ, 月日 means \"the time that passes\".","examples":[{"japanese":"領収書には月日しか書かれていなかった。","romaji":"Ryōshūsho ni wa gappi shika kakarete inakatta.","german":"The receipt had only the month and day written on it.","furigana":"りょうしゅうしょ|がっぴ|か"},{"japanese":"申込書の月日の欄を忘れずに記入してください。","romaji":"Mōshikomisho no gappi no ran o wasurezu ni kinyū shite kudasai.","german":"Please don't forget to fill in the date field on the application form.","furigana":"もうしこみしょ|がっぴ|らん|わす|きにゅう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A tool for nailing. Jokingly also for someone who can't swim: 私は金槌です (I sink like a hammer).","examples":[{"japanese":"金槌で釘を打った。","romaji":"Kanazuchi de kugi o utta.","german":"I drove a nail in with a hammer.","furigana":"かなづち|くぎ|う"},{"japanese":"実は私は金槌で、全然泳げないんです。","romaji":"Jitsu wa watashi wa kanazuchi de, zenzen oyogenai n desu.","german":"To tell the truth, I'm a non-swimmer and can't swim at all.","furigana":"じつ|わたし|かなづち|ぜんぜんおよ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"〜にはかなわない (cannot compete with ...); この暑さにはかなわない (this heat is unbearable).","examples":[{"japanese":"料理では母にはかなわない。","romaji":"Ryōri de wa haha ni wa kanawanai.","german":"When it comes to cooking, I'm no match for my mother.","furigana":"りょうり|はは"},{"japanese":"毎日こんなに暑くては、かなわない。","romaji":"Mainichi konna ni atsukute wa, kanawanai.","german":"If it's this hot every day, I can't stand it.","furigana":"まいにち|あつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Elevated, mostly かねてから／かねてより (for some time now). The kanji 予て is rare.","examples":[{"japanese":"かねてから行きたかった店に、やっと行けた。","romaji":"Kanete kara ikitakatta mise ni, yatto iketa.","german":"I was finally able to go to the shop I had wanted to visit for a long time.","furigana":"い|みせ|い"},{"japanese":"かねてよりご依頼の件について、ご報告いたします。","romaji":"Kanete yori goirai no ken ni tsuite, gohōkoku itashimasu.","german":"I would like to report on the matter you requested earlier.","furigana":"いらい|けん|ほうこく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Skin reaction: 漆にかぶれる (to get a rash from lacquer). Figuratively, mostly derogatory: 西洋かぶれ (overly Westernized). Noun: かぶれ (N4).","examples":[{"japanese":"新しい化粧品で肌がかぶれた。","romaji":"Atarashii keshōhin de hada ga kabureta.","german":"The new cosmetics gave me a skin rash.","furigana":"あたら|けしょうひん|はだ"},{"japanese":"彼は留学してから、すっかり外国にかぶれてしまった。","romaji":"Kare wa ryūgaku shite kara, sukkari gaikoku ni kaburete shimatta.","german":"Since studying abroad, he has become completely smitten with everything foreign.","furigana":"かれ|りゅうがく|がいこく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mostly figurative: 〜を加味する (to factor in ...): 経験を加味して評価する (to evaluate with experience taken into account).","examples":[{"japanese":"試験の点数に出席状況を加味して成績をつける。","romaji":"Shiken no tensū ni shusseki jōkyō o kami shite seiseki o tsukeru.","german":"Grades are given by taking attendance into account along with exam scores.","furigana":"しけん|てんすう|しゅっせきじょうきょう|かみ|せいせき"},{"japanese":"伝統的な料理に現代風のアイデアを加味した。","romaji":"Dentōteki na ryōri ni gendaifū no aidea o kami shita.","german":"Modern ideas were added to the traditional dish.","furigana":"でんとうてき|りょうり|げんだいふう|かみ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Also written かみ切る.","examples":[{"japanese":"この肉は硬くて噛み切れない。","romaji":"Kono niku wa katakute kamikirenai.","german":"This meat is so tough that I can't bite through it.","furigana":"にく|かた|か|き"},{"japanese":"犬がロープを噛み切って逃げた。","romaji":"Inu ga rōpu o kamikitte nigeta.","german":"The dog bit through the rope and ran away.","furigana":"いぬ|か|き|に"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"In daily life usually called politely お粥; a typical food when ill. On January 7th, 七草がゆ (seven-herb rice porridge) is traditionally eaten.","examples":[{"japanese":"中国では朝ご飯に粥を食べる人が多い。","romaji":"Chūgoku de wa asagohan ni kayu o taberu hito ga ōi.","german":"In China, many people eat rice porridge for breakfast.","furigana":"ちゅうごく|あさ|はん|かゆ|た|ひと|おお"},{"japanese":"胃の調子が悪い時は、柔らかい粥がいい。","romaji":"I no chōshi ga warui toki wa, yawarakai kayu ga ii.","german":"When your stomach is upset, soft rice porridge is good.","furigana":"い|ちょうし|わる|とき|やわ|かゆ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Appearance of the body: がっしりした体つき (a sturdy build). Also written 体付き.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼はスポーツ選手らしい体つきをしている。","romaji":"Kare wa supōtsu senshu rashii karadatsuki o shite iru.","german":"He has the physique of an athlete.","furigana":"かれ|せんしゅ|からだ"},{"japanese":"兄弟でも体つきはずいぶん違う。","romaji":"Kyōdai demo karadatsuki wa zuibun chigau.","german":"Even brothers can have quite different builds.","furigana":"きょうだい|からだ|ちが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"借りがある (to owe someone, also a favor), 借りを返す (to pay back a favor). Opposite: 貸し.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼には助けてもらった借りがある。","romaji":"Kare ni wa tasukete moratta kari ga aru.","german":"I owe him one for helping me.","furigana":"かれ|たす|か"},{"japanese":"この前の借りは必ず返すよ。","romaji":"Kono mae no kari wa kanarazu kaesu yo.","german":"I will definitely repay you for the other day.","furigana":"まえ|か|かなら|かえ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Of springs and wells, or figuratively of strength and tears: 井戸が涸れる, 涙が涸れる. Often also written 枯れる, which actually means \"to wither\".","examples":[{"japanese":"日照りが続いて、井戸が涸れてしまった。","romaji":"Hideri ga tsuzuite, ido ga karete shimatta.","german":"The drought continued and the well ran dry.","furigana":"ひで|つづ|いど|か"},{"japanese":"泣きすぎて、もう涙も涸れた。","romaji":"Nakisugite, mō namida mo kareta.","german":"I have cried so much that my tears have run dry.","furigana":"な|なみだ|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"あいさつを交わす (to exchange greetings), 契約を交わす (to conclude a contract). After a ます-stem: 〜交わす (... one another): 酌み交わす (to pour drinks for each other).","examples":[{"japanese":"朝、近所の人とあいさつを交わした。","romaji":"Asa, kinjo no hito to aisatsu o kawashita.","german":"In the morning I exchanged greetings with the neighbors.","furigana":"あさ|きんじょ|ひと|か"},{"japanese":"二人は正式に契約を交わした。","romaji":"Futari wa seishiki ni keiyaku o kawashita.","german":"The two formally signed a contract.","furigana":"ふたり|せいしき|けいやく|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Several people do the same thing one after another: 代わる代わる運転する (to take turns driving).","examples":[{"japanese":"長い旅だったので、三人で代わる代わる運転した。","romaji":"Nagai tabi datta node, sannin de kawarugawaru unten shita.","german":"It was a long trip, so the three of us took turns driving.","furigana":"なが|たび|さんにん|か|が|うんてん"},{"japanese":"子どもたちは代わる代わる子犬を抱っこした。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa kawarugawaru koinu o dakko shita.","german":"The children took turns holding the puppy.","furigana":"こ|か|が|こいぬ|だ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mainly in weather reports: 寒気が流れ込む (cold air flows in). Read さむけ, the same word means \"chills, shivering\".","examples":[{"japanese":"北から強い寒気が流れ込んでいる。","romaji":"Kita kara tsuyoi kanki ga nagarekonde iru.","german":"Strong cold air is flowing in from the north.","furigana":"きた|つよ|かんき|なが|こ"},{"japanese":"寒気の影響で、明日は雪になるでしょう。","romaji":"Kanki no eikyō de, ashita wa yuki ni naru deshō.","german":"Because of the cold air, it will probably snow tomorrow.","furigana":"かんき|えいきょう|あした|ゆき"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Care of the sick; as a verb 看護する. Nurse: 看護師. Home care of relatives is often called 看病.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は病院で看護の仕事をしている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa byōin de kango no shigoto o shite iru.","german":"She works in nursing at a hospital.","furigana":"かのじょ|びょういん|かんご|しごと"},{"japanese":"事故の後、母は父を献身的に看護した。","romaji":"Jiko no ato, haha wa chichi o kenshinteki ni kango shita.","german":"After the accident, my mother devotedly nursed my father.","furigana":"じこ|あと|はは|ちち|けんしんてき|かんご"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Converting into another unit or currency: 円に換算する, ドル換算で (in dollar terms). As a verb 換算する.","examples":[{"japanese":"この値段をユーロに換算するといくらですか。","romaji":"Kono nedan o yūro ni kansan suru to ikura desu ka.","german":"How much is this price converted into euros?","furigana":"ねだん|かんさん"},{"japanese":"一時間を分に換算すると六十分だ。","romaji":"Ichijikan o fun ni kansan suru to rokujuppun da.","german":"One hour converted into minutes is sixty minutes.","furigana":"いちじかん|ふん|かんさん|ろくじゅっぷん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The crowd of spectators at an event, especially in sports. For concerts and lectures 聴衆 is more usual, in general 観客.","examples":[{"japanese":"五万人の観衆が試合を見守った。","romaji":"Gomannin no kanshū ga shiai o mimamotta.","german":"Fifty thousand spectators watched the match.","furigana":"ごまんにん|かんしゅう|しあい|みまも"},{"japanese":"決勝点が入ると、観衆は総立ちになった。","romaji":"Kesshōten ga hairu to, kanshū wa sōdachi ni natta.","german":"When the winning goal was scored, the whole crowd jumped to their feet.","furigana":"けっしょうてん|はい|かんしゅう|そうだ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A written application, especially the application to a school or university: 願書を出す (to apply).","examples":[{"japanese":"大学に入学願書を提出した。","romaji":"Daigaku ni nyūgaku gansho o teishutsu shita.","german":"I submitted my application for admission to the university.","furigana":"だいがく|にゅうがくがんしょ|ていしゅつ"},{"japanese":"願書の締め切りは来週の金曜日だ。","romaji":"Gansho no shimekiri wa raishū no kin'yōbi da.","german":"The deadline for the application is next Friday.","furigana":"がんしょ|し|き|らいしゅう|きんようび"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Loud shouts of joy from a crowd: 歓声が上がる, 歓声を上げる. Homophone: 完成 (completion).","examples":[{"japanese":"花火が上がると、大きな歓声が上がった。","romaji":"Hanabi ga agaru to, ōki na kansei ga agatta.","german":"When the fireworks went up, a loud cheer rose.","furigana":"はなび|あ|おお|かんせい|あ"},{"japanese":"子どもたちは歓声を上げて海に走っていった。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa kansei o agete umi ni hashitte itta.","german":"The children ran to the sea, shouting with joy.","furigana":"こ|かんせい|あ|うみ|はし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Technical word for rock (geology); in everyday speech usually 岩 (boulder) or 石 (stone).","examples":[{"japanese":"この地域の岩石は火山活動によってできた。","romaji":"Kono chiiki no ganseki wa kazan katsudō ni yotte dekita.","german":"The rocks in this region were formed by volcanic activity.","furigana":"ちいき|がんせき|かざんかつどう"},{"japanese":"探査機が月の岩石を持ち帰った。","romaji":"Tansaki ga tsuki no ganseki o mochikaetta.","german":"The probe brought back rock from the moon.","furigana":"たんさき|つき|がんせき|も|かえ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The main line of a transport or utility network: 幹線道路 (arterial road). 新幹線 literally means \"new trunk line\".","examples":[{"japanese":"事故で幹線が止まり、多くの人が影響を受けた。","romaji":"Jiko de kansen ga tomari, ōku no hito ga eikyō o uketa.","german":"An accident stopped the main line, and many people were affected.","furigana":"じこ|かんせん|と|おお|ひと|えいきょう|う"},{"japanese":"町の中心を幹線道路が通っている。","romaji":"Machi no chūshin o kansen dōro ga tōtte iru.","german":"A major arterial road runs through the center of the town.","furigana":"まち|ちゅうしん|かんせんどうろ|とお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"How sensitively a device, film or sensor responds; for radio and mobile phones also the reception quality: 感度がいい／悪い (good / poor reception).","examples":[{"japanese":"このマイクは感度が高く、小さな声も拾う。","romaji":"Kono maiku wa kando ga takaku, chiisa na koe mo hirou.","german":"This microphone is very sensitive and picks up even quiet voices.","furigana":"かんど|たか|ちい|こえ|ひろ"},{"japanese":"山の中ではラジオの感度が悪くなる。","romaji":"Yama no naka de wa rajio no kando ga waruku naru.","german":"In the mountains, radio reception gets poor.","furigana":"やま|なか|かんど|わる"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"From English \"cunning\"; in Japanese it means only \"copying on an exam\". As a verb カンニングする.","examples":[{"japanese":"試験中にカンニングをして、先生に見つかった。","romaji":"Shikenchū ni kanningu o shite, sensei ni mitsukatta.","german":"He cheated during the exam and was caught by the teacher.","furigana":"しけんちゅう|せんせい|み"},{"japanese":"カンニングをした答案は零点になる。","romaji":"Kanningu o shita tōan wa reiten ni naru.","german":"A paper on which cheating occurred gets zero points.","furigana":"とうあん|れいてん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The first year of a Japanese era: 令和元年 (2019). Figuratively the year something begins: 電気自動車元年 (the first year of electric cars).","examples":[{"japanese":"令和元年に娘が生まれた。","romaji":"Reiwa gannen ni musume ga umareta.","german":"My daughter was born in the first year of Reiwa.","furigana":"れいわがんねん|むすめ|う"},{"japanese":"今年はこの業界にとって新しい時代の元年になるだろう。","romaji":"Kotoshi wa kono gyōkai ni totte atarashii jidai no gannen ni naru darō.","german":"This year will probably be the first year of a new era for this industry.","furigana":"ことし|ぎょうかい|あたら|じだい|がんねん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The leadership of an organization (company, party, police): 会社の幹部 (company executive), 幹部候補 (management candidate).","examples":[{"japanese":"会社の幹部が集まって今後の方針を話し合った。","romaji":"Kaisha no kanbu ga atsumatte kongo no hōshin o hanashiatta.","german":"The company's executives gathered and discussed the future direction.","furigana":"かいしゃ|かんぶ|あつ|こんご|ほうしん|はな|あ"},{"japanese":"彼は若くして党の幹部になった。","romaji":"Kare wa wakakushite tō no kanbu ni natta.","german":"He became a party executive at a young age.","furigana":"かれ|わか|とう|かんぶ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A feeling that cannot be put into words, mostly on achievements or farewells: 感無量です (I am deeply moved).","examples":[{"japanese":"長年の夢がかなって、感無量です。","romaji":"Naganen no yume ga kanatte, kanmuryō desu.","german":"My long-held dream has come true, and I am deeply moved.","furigana":"ながねん|ゆめ|かんむりょう"},{"japanese":"卒業式で息子の姿を見て、母は感無量の様子だった。","romaji":"Sotsugyōshiki de musuko no sugata o mite, haha wa kanmuryō no yōsu datta.","german":"Seeing her son at the graduation ceremony, the mother seemed overwhelmed with emotion.","furigana":"そつぎょうしき|むすこ|すがた|み|はは|かんむりょう|ようす"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"What has become generally accepted through habit, especially in language use: 慣用的な表現, 慣用読み. Idiom: 慣用句.","examples":[{"japanese":"この読み方は本来は誤りだが、慣用として認められている。","romaji":"Kono yomikata wa honrai wa ayamari da ga, kan'yō to shite mitomerarete iru.","german":"This reading is originally incorrect, but it is accepted as established usage.","furigana":"よ|かた|ほんらい|あやま|かんよう|みと"},{"japanese":"辞書には慣用による意味も載っている。","romaji":"Jisho ni wa kan'yō ni yoru imi mo notte iru.","german":"The dictionary also lists meanings that come from common usage.","furigana":"じしょ|かんよう|いみ|の"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"An impressive, dignified presence, often from experience or position: 貫禄がある, 貫禄十分.","examples":[{"japanese":"部長には貫禄がある。","romaji":"Buchō ni wa kanroku ga aru.","german":"The department head has an air of authority.","furigana":"ぶちょう|かんろく"},{"japanese":"優勝した横綱は貫禄のある取り組みを見せた。","romaji":"Yūshō shita yokozuna wa kanroku no aru torikumi o miseta.","german":"The victorious yokozuna showed a commanding bout.","furigana":"ゆうしょう|よこづな|かんろく|と|く|み"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Harm to life and limb inflicted on another person: 危害を加える (to harm someone). Formal, often used in news and warnings.","examples":[{"japanese":"犯人は人質に危害を加えるつもりはないと言った。","romaji":"Hannin wa hitojichi ni kigai o kuwaeru tsumori wa nai to itta.","german":"The culprit said he had no intention of harming the hostages.","furigana":"はんにん|ひとじち|きがい|くわ|い"},{"japanese":"この虫は人に危害を与えることはない。","romaji":"Kono mushi wa hito ni kigai o ataeru koto wa nai.","german":"This insect does no harm to people.","furigana":"むし|ひと|きがい|あた"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To dress especially nicely, for example for celebrations: 着飾った人々. Godan verb.","examples":[{"japanese":"パーティーには着飾った人々が集まっていた。","romaji":"Pātī ni wa kikazatta hitobito ga atsumatte ita.","german":"People dressed up for the occasion had gathered at the party.","furigana":"きかざ|ひとびと|あつ"},{"japanese":"娘は成人式のために朝から着飾っている。","romaji":"Musume wa seijinshiki no tame ni asa kara kikazatte iru.","german":"My daughter has been dressing up since morning for her Coming of Age ceremony.","furigana":"むすめ|せいじんしき|あさ|きかざ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Inhibition out of consideration for others: 〜に気兼ねする, 気兼ねなく (without feeling constrained).","examples":[{"japanese":"遠慮しないで、気兼ねなく使ってください。","romaji":"Enryo shinaide, kigane naku tsukatte kudasai.","german":"Don't hold back; please feel free to use it.","furigana":"えんりょ|きが|つか"},{"japanese":"義理の両親に気兼ねして、なかなか本音が言えない。","romaji":"Giri no ryōshin ni kigane shite, nakanaka honne ga ienai.","german":"Out of consideration for my in-laws, I can hardly say what I really think.","furigana":"ぎり|りょうしん|きが|ほんね|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Bodily organ in the biological sense: 消化器官, 呼吸器官. An individual internal organ is often called 臓器 in everyday speech.","examples":[{"japanese":"心臓は血液を送る大切な器官だ。","romaji":"Shinzō wa ketsueki o okuru taisetsu na kikan da.","german":"The heart is an important organ that pumps blood.","furigana":"しんぞう|けつえき|おく|たいせつ|きかん"},{"japanese":"植物の葉は光合成を行う器官である。","romaji":"Shokubutsu no ha wa kōgōsei o okonau kikan de aru.","german":"The leaves of plants are organs of photosynthesis.","furigana":"しょくぶつ|は|こうごうせい|おこな|きかん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Publication four times a year: 季刊誌 (quarterly journal). Compare 月刊 (monthly), 週刊 (weekly).","examples":[{"japanese":"この雑誌は季刊で、年に四回出る。","romaji":"Kono zasshi wa kikan de, nen ni yonkai deru.","german":"This magazine is a quarterly, published four times a year.","furigana":"ざっし|きかん|ねん|よんかいで"},{"japanese":"大学の研究会が季刊の会報を発行している。","romaji":"Daigaku no kenkyūkai ga kikan no kaihō o hakkō shite iru.","german":"The university research group publishes a quarterly bulletin.","furigana":"だいがく|けんきゅうかい|きかん|かいほう|はっこう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The effect of a remedy or measure: 効き目がある／ない, 効き目が現れる. Also written 利き目.","examples":[{"japanese":"この薬は効き目が早い。","romaji":"Kono kusuri wa kikime ga hayai.","german":"This medicine works quickly.","furigana":"くすり|き|め|はや"},{"japanese":"何度注意しても、彼には効き目がなかった。","romaji":"Nando chūi shite mo, kare ni wa kikime ga nakatta.","german":"No matter how often I warned him, it had no effect on him.","furigana":"なんどちゅうい|かれ|き|め"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Returning to Tokyo, formal and literary: 帰京する. Homophone: 桔梗 (balloon flower).","examples":[{"japanese":"社長は明日の夜に帰京する予定です。","romaji":"Shachō wa ashita no yoru ni kikyō suru yotei desu.","german":"The company president is scheduled to return to Tokyo tomorrow evening.","furigana":"しゃちょう|あした|よる|ききょう|よてい"},{"japanese":"お盆休みが終わり、帰京する車で高速道路が混んでいる。","romaji":"Obon yasumi ga owari, kikyō suru kuruma de kōsoku dōro ga konde iru.","german":"The Obon holidays are over, and the expressways are crowded with cars heading back to Tokyo.","furigana":"ぼんやす|お|ききょう|くるま|こうそくどうろ|こ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Money set aside for a specific purpose, or the foundation that manages it: 奨学基金, 国際通貨基金 (IMF). Homophone: 飢饉 (famine).","examples":[{"japanese":"被災地を支援するための基金が設立された。","romaji":"Hisaichi o shien suru tame no kikin ga setsuritsu sareta.","german":"A fund was established to support the disaster area.","furigana":"ひさいち|しえん|ききん|せつりつ"},{"japanese":"卒業生の寄付で奨学金の基金ができた。","romaji":"Sotsugyōsei no kifu de shōgakukin no kikin ga dekita.","german":"A scholarship fund was created through donations from graduates.","furigana":"そつぎょうせい|きふ|しょうがくきん|ききん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To give up a right, especially to not vote, or to withdraw from a competition. Homophone: 危険 (danger).","examples":[{"japanese":"選挙を棄権する若者が増えている。","romaji":"Senkyo o kiken suru wakamono ga fuete iru.","german":"More and more young people are abstaining from voting.","furigana":"せんきょ|きけん|わかもの|ふ"},{"japanese":"彼はけがのため、決勝を棄権した。","romaji":"Kare wa kega no tame, kesshō o kiken shita.","german":"He withdrew from the final because of an injury.","furigana":"かれ|けっしょう|きけん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The structure and functioning of a machine or organization; also in names of organizations (〜機構).","examples":[{"japanese":"時計の機構はとても複雑だ。","romaji":"Tokei no kikō wa totemo fukuzatsu da.","german":"The mechanism of a watch is very complicated.","furigana":"とけい|きこう|ふくざつ"},{"japanese":"政府は行政の機構を改革する方針だ。","romaji":"Seifu wa gyōsei no kikō o kaikaku suru hōshin da.","german":"The government plans to reform the administrative structure.","furigana":"せいふ|ぎょうせい|きこう|かいかく|ほうしん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"な-adjective: showy and artificial in manner or speech: きざなせりふ. Usually written in kana; kanji 気障.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼はきざなせりふを平気で言う。","romaji":"Kare wa kiza na serifu o heiki de iu.","german":"He says corny lines without batting an eye.","furigana":"かれ|へいき|い"},{"japanese":"そんなきざな格好はやめたほうがいい。","romaji":"Sonna kiza na kakkō wa yameta hō ga ii.","german":"You'd better drop that pretentious getup.","furigana":"かっこう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The assembly building of a parliament; in Japan especially the 国会議事堂 in Tokyo.","examples":[{"japanese":"修学旅行で国会議事堂を見学した。","romaji":"Shūgaku ryokō de kokkai gijidō o kengaku shita.","german":"On the school trip we toured the National Diet Building.","furigana":"しゅうがくりょこう|こっかいぎじどう|けんがく"},{"japanese":"議事堂の前にはデモの人々が集まっていた。","romaji":"Gijidō no mae ni wa demo no hitobito ga atsumatte ita.","german":"Demonstrators had gathered in front of the parliament building.","furigana":"ぎじどう|まえ|ひとびと|あつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To give an item (books, artworks, equipment) to an institution: 寄贈する. JMdict also lists the older reading きそう; today きぞう is the usual one. Money is usually donated with 寄付.","examples":[{"japanese":"祖父の蔵書を市の図書館に寄贈した。","romaji":"Sofu no zōsho o shi no toshokan ni kizō shita.","german":"We donated my grandfather's book collection to the city library.","furigana":"そふ|ぞうしょ|し|としょかん|きぞう"},{"japanese":"この絵は地元の画家から寄贈されたものです。","romaji":"Kono e wa jimoto no gaka kara kizō sareta mono desu.","german":"This painting was donated by a local painter.","furigana":"え|じもと|がか|きぞう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A person's nature, mostly positive: 気立てがいい／優しい (good-hearted).","examples":[{"japanese":"隣の娘さんは気立てのいい子だ。","romaji":"Tonari no musumesan wa kidate no ii ko da.","german":"The neighbors' daughter is a good-natured girl.","furigana":"となり|むすめ|きだ|こ"},{"japanese":"彼女は美人というより気立てが優しい人だ。","romaji":"Kanojo wa bijin to iu yori kidate ga yasashii hito da.","german":"She is less a beauty than a person with a gentle disposition.","furigana":"かのじょ|びじん|きだ|やさ|ひと"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Colloquial variant of きちんと: carefully, fitting exactly: きちっと片付ける.","examples":[{"japanese":"使った道具はきちっと元の場所に戻してください。","romaji":"Tsukatta dōgu wa kichitto moto no basho ni modoshite kudasai.","german":"Please put the tools you used back in their place neatly.","furigana":"つか|どうぐ|もと|ばしょ|もど"},{"japanese":"彼はいつもきちっとした服装をしている。","romaji":"Kare wa itsumo kichitto shita fukusō o shite iru.","german":"He is always neatly dressed.","furigana":"かれ|ふくそう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Exactly at a number or time: 九時きっかり, きっかり千円.","examples":[{"japanese":"会議は九時きっかりに始まった。","romaji":"Kaigi wa kuji kikkari ni hajimatta.","german":"The meeting started at nine o'clock sharp.","furigana":"かいぎ|くじ|はじ"},{"japanese":"お釣りはきっかり五百円だった。","romaji":"Otsuri wa kikkari gohyakuen datta.","german":"The change was exactly 500 yen.","furigana":"つ|ごひゃくえん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Drinking tea or coffee, also a short form for a cafe: 喫茶コーナー, 喫茶室. The cafe itself is usually called 喫茶店.","examples":[{"japanese":"喫茶の習慣は中国から日本に伝わった。","romaji":"Kissa no shūkan wa Chūgoku kara Nihon ni tsutawatta.","german":"The custom of drinking tea came to Japan from China.","furigana":"きっさ|しゅうかん|ちゅうごく|にほん|つた"},{"japanese":"この美術館には喫茶のスペースもある。","romaji":"Kono bijutsukan ni wa kissa no supēsu mo aru.","german":"This museum also has a cafe area.","furigana":"びじゅつかん|きっさ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Exactly and without deviation: きっちり払う, 時間きっちりに. Also \"snug, tight\": ふたをきっちり閉める.","examples":[{"japanese":"借りたお金はきっちり返した。","romaji":"Karita okane wa kitchiri kaeshita.","german":"I paid back the borrowed money to the last yen.","furigana":"か|かね|かえ"},{"japanese":"瓶のふたをきっちり閉めてください。","romaji":"Bin no futa o kitchiri shimete kudasai.","german":"Please close the lid of the bottle tightly.","furigana":"びん|し"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Clearly and without hesitation: きっぱり断る (to refuse flatly), きっぱりやめる (to quit for good).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は彼の誘いをきっぱり断った。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kare no sasoi o kippari kotowatta.","german":"She flatly turned down his invitation.","furigana":"かのじょ|かれ|さそ|ことわ"},{"japanese":"父は去年、たばこをきっぱりやめた。","romaji":"Chichi wa kyonen, tabako o kippari yameta.","german":"My father quit smoking for good last year.","furigana":"ちち|きょねん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The starting point of a route or development: 東海道の起点. Opposite: 終点. Homophone: 機転 (quick wit).","examples":[{"japanese":"日本橋は東海道の起点だった。","romaji":"Nihonbashi wa Tōkaidō no kiten datta.","german":"Nihonbashi was the starting point of the Tokaido.","furigana":"にほんばし|とうかいどう|きてん"},{"japanese":"この事件が改革の起点となった。","romaji":"Kono jiken ga kaikaku no kiten to natta.","german":"This incident became the starting point of the reform.","furigana":"じけん|かいかく|きてん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A fine, dignified aura of a person or work of art: 気品がある, 気品にあふれる.","examples":[{"japanese":"その老婦人には気品が感じられた。","romaji":"Sono rōfujin ni wa kihin ga kanjirareta.","german":"The old lady had an air of elegance.","furigana":"ろうふじん|きひん|かん"},{"japanese":"この茶碗には素朴な中にも気品がある。","romaji":"Kono chawan ni wa soboku na naka ni mo kihin ga aru.","german":"This tea bowl has a refinement within its simplicity.","furigana":"ちゃわん|そぼく|なか|きひん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The characteristic nature of a group, region, or era: 自由な気風, 下町の気風.","examples":[{"japanese":"この学校には自由な気風がある。","romaji":"Kono gakkō ni wa jiyū na kifū ga aru.","german":"This school has a free-spirited ethos.","furigana":"がっこう|じゆう|きふう"},{"japanese":"職人の町らしい、さっぱりした気風が残っている。","romaji":"Shokunin no machi rashii, sappari shita kifū ga nokotte iru.","german":"The unpretentious character typical of an artisans' town has been preserved.","furigana":"しょくにん|まち|きふう|のこ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Noun and な-adjective: a sudden notion or changeable nature: 気まぐれな天気, ほんの気まぐれで.","examples":[{"japanese":"山の天気は気まぐれだ。","romaji":"Yama no tenki wa kimagure da.","german":"Mountain weather is fickle.","furigana":"やま|てんき|き"},{"japanese":"ほんの気まぐれで、帰りに違う道を通ってみた。","romaji":"Hon no kimagure de, kaeri ni chigau michi o tōtte mita.","german":"On a mere whim, I took a different route home.","furigana":"き|かえ|ちが|みち|とお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"End of a school term or accounting period: 期末試験 (final exams), 期末決算.","examples":[{"japanese":"来週から期末試験が始まる。","romaji":"Raishū kara kimatsu shiken ga hajimaru.","german":"Final exams start next week.","furigana":"らいしゅう|きまつしけん|はじ"},{"japanese":"期末になると、会社は決算の準備で忙しくなる。","romaji":"Kimatsu ni naru to, kaisha wa kessan no junbi de isogashiku naru.","german":"At the end of the period, the company gets busy preparing its closing of accounts.","furigana":"きまつ|かいしゃ|けっさん|じゅんび|いそが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The feeling of being ashamed or embarrassed: 決まり悪そうに笑う. Often also きまりが悪い.","examples":[{"japanese":"名前を間違えてしまい、決まり悪い思いをした。","romaji":"Namae o machigaete shimai, kimariwarui omoi o shita.","german":"I got the name wrong and felt embarrassed.","furigana":"なまえ|まちが|き|わる|おも"},{"japanese":"彼は決まり悪そうに頭をかいた。","romaji":"Kare wa kimariwarusō ni atama o kaita.","german":"He scratched his head sheepishly.","furigana":"かれ|き|わる|あたま"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Writing one's own name on a form or object: 記名する, 記名投票. Opposite: 無記名 (anonymous).","examples":[{"japanese":"申込書に記名して、受付に出してください。","romaji":"Mōshikomisho ni kimei shite, uketsuke ni dashite kudasai.","german":"Please write your name on the application form and hand it in at the reception.","furigana":"もうしこみしょ|きめい|うけつけ|だ"},{"japanese":"アンケートは記名でも無記名でもかまいません。","romaji":"Ankēto wa kimei demo mukimei demo kamaimasen.","german":"You may fill out the survey with or without your name.","furigana":"きめい|むきめい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To adapt a novel or an event for stage, film, or television; figuratively: to embellish facts.","examples":[{"japanese":"その小説は映画用に脚色された。","romaji":"Sono shōsetsu wa eigayō ni kyakushoku sareta.","german":"That novel was adapted for film.","furigana":"しょうせつ|えいがよう|きゃくしょく"},{"japanese":"彼の話には少し脚色があると思う。","romaji":"Kare no hanashi ni wa sukoshi kyakushoku ga aru to omou.","german":"I think his story is a bit embellished.","furigana":"かれ|はなし|すこ|きゃくしょく|おも"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A situation turns around, especially in sports: 逆転勝ち (comeback win), 形勢が逆転する.","examples":[{"japanese":"最後の一分で逆転して、試合に勝った。","romaji":"Saigo no ippun de gyakuten shite, shiai ni katta.","german":"We turned the game around in the last minute and won.","furigana":"さいご|いっぷん|ぎゃくてん|しあい|か"},{"japanese":"この実験ではモーターの回転を逆転させる。","romaji":"Kono jikken de wa mōtā no kaiten o gyakuten saseru.","german":"In this experiment the rotation of the motor is reversed.","furigana":"じっけん|かいてん|ぎゃくてん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"な-adjective: slimly built and delicate (華奢な体) or looking fragile (華奢な椅子).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は華奢な体つきをしている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kyasha na karadatsuki o shite iru.","german":"She has a slender build.","furigana":"かのじょ|きゃしゃ|からだ"},{"japanese":"この椅子は華奢で、座るのが少し怖い。","romaji":"Kono isu wa kyasha de, suwaru no ga sukoshi kowai.","german":"This chair is so fragile that I'm a little afraid to sit on it.","furigana":"いす|きゃしゃ|すわ|すこ|こわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"As the verb キャッチする: to catch a ball, but also to pick up information or signals: 情報をキャッチする.","examples":[{"japanese":"外野手がボールをしっかりキャッチした。","romaji":"Gaiyashu ga bōru o shikkari kyatchi shita.","german":"The outfielder caught the ball securely.","furigana":"がいやしゅ"},{"japanese":"記者はいち早くその情報をキャッチした。","romaji":"Kisha wa ichihayaku sono jōhō o kyatchi shita.","german":"The reporter picked up the information first.","furigana":"きしゃ|はや|じょうほう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Professional career and accumulated experience: キャリアを積む. In the civil service, also the career track of those who passed the top national exam. Homophone: キャリア (carrier, mobile network operator).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は十年のキャリアを持つ通訳だ。","romaji":"Kanojo wa jūnen no kyaria o motsu tsūyaku da.","german":"She is an interpreter with ten years of experience.","furigana":"かのじょ|じゅうねん|も|つうやく"},{"japanese":"海外でキャリアを積みたいと考えている。","romaji":"Kaigai de kyaria o tsumitai to kangaete iru.","german":"I'd like to build a career abroad.","furigana":"かいがい|つ|かんが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Serving at table, or the person who serves; somewhat old-fashioned. As a verb: 給仕する.","examples":[{"japanese":"旅館では仲居さんが食事の給仕をしてくれた。","romaji":"Ryokan de wa nakaisan ga shokuji no kyūji o shite kureta.","german":"At the ryokan, the maid served us our meal.","furigana":"りょかん|なかい|しょくじ|きゅうじ"},{"japanese":"昔、祖父はホテルで給仕として働いていた。","romaji":"Mukashi, sofu wa hoteru de kyūji to shite hataraite ita.","german":"In the past, my grandfather worked as a waiter at a hotel.","furigana":"むかし|そふ|きゅうじ|はたら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A meal provided by a school or company, especially the lunch at Japanese schools: 給食当番 (lunch duty).","examples":[{"japanese":"今日の給食はカレーだった。","romaji":"Kyō no kyūshoku wa karē datta.","german":"Today's school lunch was curry.","furigana":"きょう|きゅうしょく"},{"japanese":"この工場では昼の給食が出る。","romaji":"Kono kōjō de wa hiru no kyūshoku ga deru.","german":"This factory provides a midday meal.","furigana":"こうじょう|ひる|きゅうしょく|で"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Temporary cessation of fighting: 休戦協定. The final end of a war is called 終戦.","examples":[{"japanese":"両国は三日間の休戦に合意した。","romaji":"Ryōkoku wa mikkakan no kyūsen ni gōi shita.","german":"The two countries agreed to a three-day truce.","furigana":"りょうこく|みっかかん|きゅうせん|ごうい"},{"japanese":"兄弟げんかも、母の一言で休戦になった。","romaji":"Kyōdai genka mo, haha no hitokoto de kyūsen ni natta.","german":"Even the brothers' quarrel ended in a truce with a word from their mother.","furigana":"きょうだい|はは|ひとこと|きゅうせん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Someone one has known for a long time: 旧知の仲 (old friends). Homophone: 窮地 (predicament).","examples":[{"japanese":"社長とは学生時代からの旧知の仲だ。","romaji":"Shachō to wa gakusei jidai kara no kyūchi no naka da.","german":"The president and I have known each other since our student days.","furigana":"しゃちょう|がくせいじだい|きゅうち|なか"},{"japanese":"旧知の友人に偶然駅で会った。","romaji":"Kyūchi no yūjin ni gūzen eki de atta.","german":"I ran into an old friend at the station by chance.","furigana":"きゅうち|ゆうじん|ぐうぜんえき|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A ruler's palace: ベルサイユ宮殿. The Japanese Imperial Palace is called 皇居.","examples":[{"japanese":"ウィーンでは古い宮殿を見学した。","romaji":"Wīn de wa furui kyūden o kengaku shita.","german":"In Vienna I toured an old palace.","furigana":"ふる|きゅうでん|けんがく"},{"japanese":"王は豪華な宮殿に住んでいた。","romaji":"Ō wa gōka na kyūden ni sunde ita.","german":"The king lived in a splendid palace.","furigana":"おう|ごうか|きゅうでん|す"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Something astonishing: 驚異的な (astonishing), 自然の驚異. Homophone: 脅威 (threat).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は驚異的な記録を打ち立てた。","romaji":"Kare wa kyōiteki na kiroku o uchitateta.","german":"He set an astonishing record.","furigana":"かれ|きょういてき|きろく|う|た"},{"japanese":"宇宙の広さはまさに驚異だ。","romaji":"Uchū no hirosa wa masa ni kyōi da.","german":"The vastness of the universe is truly a wonder.","furigana":"うちゅう|ひろ|きょうい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Official term for teaching staff at schools and universities: 教員免許, 教員採用試験. In everyday speech one says 先生.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は小学校の教員として二十年働いた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa shōgakkō no kyōin to shite nijūnen hataraita.","german":"She worked as an elementary school teacher for twenty years.","furigana":"かのじょ|しょうがっこう|きょういん|にじゅうねんはたら"},{"japanese":"この大学は教員の数が足りない。","romaji":"Kono daigaku wa kyōin no kazu ga tarinai.","german":"This university is short of teaching staff.","furigana":"だいがく|きょういん|かず|た"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A subject in the school curriculum: 得意な教科, 教科書 (textbook). Homophone: 強化 (strengthening).","examples":[{"japanese":"一番好きな教科は数学です。","romaji":"Ichiban suki na kyōka wa sūgaku desu.","german":"My favorite subject is mathematics.","furigana":"いちばんす|きょうか|すうがく"},{"japanese":"来年から新しい教科が加わる。","romaji":"Rainen kara atarashii kyōka ga kuwawaru.","german":"A new subject will be added from next year.","furigana":"らいねん|あたら|きょうか|くわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Boys and girls being taught together: 男女共学. Opposite: 男子校／女子校.","examples":[{"japanese":"私が通った高校は共学だった。","romaji":"Watashi ga kayotta kōkō wa kyōgaku datta.","german":"The high school I attended was coed.","furigana":"わたし|かよ|こうこう|きょうがく"},{"japanese":"その女子校は来年から共学になる。","romaji":"Sono joshikō wa rainen kara kyōgaku ni naru.","german":"That girls' school will become coed from next year.","furigana":"じょしこう|らいねん|きょうがく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Formal deliberation among several parties: 協議する, 協議会, 協議離婚. Homophone: 競技 (competition).","examples":[{"japanese":"労使は賃上げについて協議を続けている。","romaji":"Rōshi wa chin'age ni tsuite kyōgi o tsuzukete iru.","german":"Labor and management are continuing to negotiate over wage increases.","furigana":"ろうし|ちんあ|きょうぎ|つづ"},{"japanese":"詳しいことは関係者と協議して決めます。","romaji":"Kuwashii koto wa kankeisha to kyōgi shite kimemasu.","german":"We will decide the details after consulting the people concerned.","furigana":"くわ|かんけいしゃ|きょうぎ|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"な-adjective: an uncompromising attitude: 強硬な態度, 強硬派 (hardliners). Do not confuse with 強行 (to push through).","examples":[{"japanese":"相手国は強硬な姿勢を崩さなかった。","romaji":"Aitekoku wa kyōkō na shisei o kuzusanakatta.","german":"The other country did not soften its hardline stance.","furigana":"あいてこく|きょうこう|しせい|くず"},{"japanese":"社長は値上げに強硬に反対した。","romaji":"Shachō wa neage ni kyōkō ni hantai shita.","german":"The president firmly opposed the price increase.","furigana":"しゃちょう|ねあ|きょうこう|はんたい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To push something through despite resistance or bad conditions: 強行する, 強行採決 (forcing a vote). Do not confuse with 強硬.","examples":[{"japanese":"雨の中、試合は強行された。","romaji":"Ame no naka, shiai wa kyōkō sareta.","german":"The game was held despite the rain.","furigana":"あめ|なか|しあい|きょうこう"},{"japanese":"反対を押し切って、計画を強行した。","romaji":"Hantai o oshikitte, keikaku o kyōkō shita.","german":"Overriding the opposition, they pushed the plan through.","furigana":"はんたい|お|き|けいかく|きょうこう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A very bad harvest: 冷夏で米が凶作になる. Opposite: 豊作.","examples":[{"japanese":"冷夏のせいで米が凶作になった。","romaji":"Reika no sei de kome ga kyōsaku ni natta.","german":"A cool summer led to a poor rice harvest.","furigana":"れいか|こめ|きょうさく"},{"japanese":"凶作が続き、村の生活は苦しくなった。","romaji":"Kyōsaku ga tsuzuki, mura no seikatsu wa kurushiku natta.","german":"Crop failures continued, and life in the village became hard.","furigana":"きょうさく|つづ|むら|せいかつ|くる"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Practical training, especially at a driving school: 教習所 (driving school), 教習車.","examples":[{"japanese":"週に三回、車の教習を受けている。","romaji":"Shū ni sankai, kuruma no kyōshū o ukete iru.","german":"I take driving lessons three times a week.","furigana":"しゅう|さんかい|くるま|きょうしゅう|う"},{"japanese":"新しい機械の使い方について教習があった。","romaji":"Atarashii kikai no tsukaikata ni tsuite kyōshū ga atta.","german":"There was training on how to use the new machine.","furigana":"あたら|きかい|つか|かた|きょうしゅう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A formal accord between states or organizations: 協定を結ぶ, 貿易協定.","examples":[{"japanese":"両国は新しい貿易協定を結んだ。","romaji":"Ryōkoku wa atarashii bōeki kyōtei o musunda.","german":"The two countries concluded a new trade agreement.","furigana":"りょうこく|あたら|ぼうえききょうてい|むす"},{"japanese":"会社と組合の間で協定が成立した。","romaji":"Kaisha to kumiai no aida de kyōtei ga seiritsu shita.","german":"An agreement was reached between the company and the union.","furigana":"かいしゃ|くみあい|あいだ|きょうてい|せいりつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The area one comes from, or a region with its own culture: 郷土料理 (regional cuisine), 郷土史.","examples":[{"japanese":"祭りでは郷土の料理がふるまわれた。","romaji":"Matsuri de wa kyōdo no ryōri ga furumawareta.","german":"Local dishes were served at the festival.","furigana":"まつ|きょうど|りょうり"},{"japanese":"郷土の歴史を子どもたちに伝えたい。","romaji":"Kyōdo no rekishi o kodomotachi ni tsutaetai.","german":"I want to pass on the history of our hometown to the children.","furigana":"きょうど|れきし|こ|つた"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A piece of music: 好きな曲, 曲を作る (to compose). The counter is also 曲: 三曲.","examples":[{"japanese":"この曲を聞くと、学生時代を思い出す。","romaji":"Kono kyoku o kiku to, gakusei jidai o omoidasu.","german":"Whenever I hear this song, I remember my school days.","furigana":"きょく|き|がくせいじだい|おも|だ"},{"japanese":"彼は自分で曲を作って歌っている。","romaji":"Kare wa jibun de kyoku o tsukutte utatte iru.","german":"He writes his own songs and sings them.","furigana":"かれ|じぶん|きょく|つく|うた"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 35 (source: N4; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"To confine to a narrow scope: 〜に局限する. More formal than 限定する.","examples":[{"japanese":"被害は一部の地域に局限された。","romaji":"Higai wa ichibu no chiiki ni kyokugen sareta.","german":"The damage was confined to a few areas.","furigana":"ひがい|いちぶ|ちいき|きょくげん"},{"japanese":"議論をこの問題に局限しよう。","romaji":"Giron o kono mondai ni kyokugen shiyō.","german":"Let's limit the discussion to this issue.","furigana":"ぎろん|もんだい|きょくげん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Official term for \"to live, to reside\": 居住する, 居住者, 居住地.","examples":[{"japanese":"この地域に居住する外国人が増えている。","romaji":"Kono chiiki ni kyojū suru gaikokujin ga fuete iru.","german":"The number of foreigners living in this area is increasing.","furigana":"ちいき|きょじゅう|がいこくじん|ふ"},{"japanese":"申込書に現在の居住地を書いてください。","romaji":"Mōshikomisho ni genzai no kyojūchi o kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write your current place of residence on the application form.","furigana":"もうしこみしょ|げんざい|きょじゅうち|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Firm refusal: 拒絶する, 拒絶反応 (rejection reaction). Stronger than 断る.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼の申し出はきっぱりと拒絶された。","romaji":"Kare no mōshide wa kippari to kyozetsu sareta.","german":"His offer was flatly rejected.","furigana":"かれ|もう|で|きょぜつ"},{"japanese":"子どもは新しい食べ物を拒絶することがある。","romaji":"Kodomo wa atarashii tabemono o kyozetsu suru koto ga aru.","german":"Children sometimes refuse new foods outright.","furigana":"こ|あたら|た|もの|きょぜつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A village whose inhabitants live mainly from fishing. Compare 農村 (farming village).","examples":[{"japanese":"小さな漁村で夏休みを過ごした。","romaji":"Chiisa na gyoson de natsuyasumi o sugoshita.","german":"I spent the summer vacation in a small fishing village.","furigana":"ちい|ぎょそん|なつやす|す"},{"japanese":"その漁村では朝早くから船が出る。","romaji":"Sono gyoson de wa asa hayaku kara fune ga deru.","german":"In that fishing village, boats put out from early in the morning.","furigana":"ぎょそん|あさはや|ふね|で"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"な-adjective: without pressure or worries: 気楽に考える, お気楽 (happy-go-lucky, often ironic).","examples":[{"japanese":"一人暮らしは気楽でいい。","romaji":"Hitorigurashi wa kiraku de ii.","german":"Living alone is nice and carefree.","furigana":"ひとりぐ|きらく"},{"japanese":"そんなに緊張しないで、気楽にやってください。","romaji":"Sonna ni kinchō shinaide, kiraku ni yatte kudasai.","german":"Don't be so nervous; just take it easy.","furigana":"きんちょう|きらく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"な-adjective: shiny and magnificent: きらびやかな衣装. The kanji 煌びやか is rare.","examples":[{"japanese":"舞台ではきらびやかな衣装の俳優たちが踊った。","romaji":"Butai de wa kirabiyaka na ishō no haiyūtachi ga odotta.","german":"Actors in gorgeous costumes danced on the stage.","furigana":"ぶたい|いしょう|はいゆう|おど"},{"japanese":"夜の町はきらびやかな光に包まれていた。","romaji":"Yoru no machi wa kirabiyaka na hikari ni tsutsumarete ita.","german":"The night town was bathed in dazzling lights.","furigana":"よる|まち|ひかり|つつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A suitable point to stop: 切りのいいところで (at a good stopping point), 切りがない (to be endless). Often in kana: きり.","examples":[{"japanese":"切りのいいところで休憩にしましょう。","romaji":"Kiri no ii tokoro de kyūkei ni shimashō.","german":"Let's take a break at a good stopping point.","furigana":"き|きゅうけい"},{"japanese":"文句を言い出したら切りがない。","romaji":"Monku o iidashitara kiri ga nai.","german":"Once you start complaining, there is no end to it.","furigana":"もんく|い|だ|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Noun of 切り替える: switching, changing over: 気持ちの切り替え (changing one's mindset, putting something behind one), システムの切り替え.","examples":[{"japanese":"新しいシステムへの切り替えは来月行われる。","romaji":"Atarashii shisutemu e no kirikae wa raigetsu okonawareru.","german":"The switchover to the new system will take place next month.","furigana":"あたら|き|か|らいげつおこな"},{"japanese":"失敗しても、気持ちの切り替えが早い人だ。","romaji":"Shippai shite mo, kimochi no kirikae ga hayai hito da.","german":"Even after a failure, he is someone who shifts his mindset quickly.","furigana":"しっぱい|きも|き|か|はや|ひと"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Movement of air in the atmosphere: 乱気流 (turbulence), 上昇気流 (updraft).","examples":[{"japanese":"飛行機が乱気流に入り、大きく揺れた。","romaji":"Hikōki ga rankiryū ni hairi, ōkiku yureta.","german":"The airplane hit turbulence and shook violently.","furigana":"ひこうき|らんきりゅう|はい|おお|ゆ"},{"japanese":"山に沿って暖かい気流が上がっていく。","romaji":"Yama ni sotte atatakai kiryū ga agatte iku.","german":"A warm air current rises along the mountain.","furigana":"やま|そ|あたた|きりゅう|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A place where something is interrupted or cut: 雲の切れ目, 切れ目を入れる (to make a cut).","examples":[{"japanese":"雲の切れ目から太陽が見えた。","romaji":"Kumo no kireme kara taiyō ga mieta.","german":"The sun was visible through a gap in the clouds.","furigana":"くも|き|め|たいよう|み"},{"japanese":"肉に切れ目を入れてから焼いてください。","romaji":"Niku ni kireme o irete kara yaite kudasai.","german":"Please score the meat before grilling it.","furigana":"にく|き|め|い|や"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Suspicion of wrongdoing, especially in politics and the press: 汚職疑惑, 疑惑を晴らす (to clear suspicion).","examples":[{"japanese":"大臣に献金をめぐる疑惑が浮上した。","romaji":"Daijin ni kenkin o meguru giwaku ga fujō shita.","german":"Suspicions over political donations surfaced regarding the minister.","furigana":"だいじん|けんきん|ぎわく|ふじょう"},{"japanese":"彼は自分への疑惑を晴らそうと必死だった。","romaji":"Kare wa jibun e no giwaku o harasō to hisshi datta.","german":"He desperately tried to clear the suspicions against him.","furigana":"かれ|じぶん|ぎわく|は|ひっし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Microorganisms such as bacteria and fungi: 菌が繁殖する, 大腸菌. Colloquially also ばい菌 (germs).","examples":[{"japanese":"手をよく洗って、菌を落としましょう。","romaji":"Te o yoku aratte, kin o otoshimashō.","german":"Let's wash our hands well to get rid of germs.","furigana":"て|あら|きん|お"},{"japanese":"この菌は湿った場所で増えやすい。","romaji":"Kono kin wa shimetta basho de fueyasui.","german":"These germs multiply easily in damp places.","furigana":"きん|しめ|ばしょ|ふ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Colloquial for 近視 (nearsightedness). Opposite: 遠視 (farsightedness).","examples":[{"japanese":"近眼なので、眼鏡がないと黒板が見えない。","romaji":"Kingan na node, megane ga nai to kokuban ga mienai.","german":"I'm nearsighted, so I can't see the blackboard without glasses.","furigana":"きんがん|めがね|こくばん|み"},{"japanese":"スマホの使いすぎで近眼が進んだ。","romaji":"Sumaho no tsukaisugi de kingan ga susunda.","german":"My nearsightedness got worse from too much smartphone use.","furigana":"つか|きんがん|すす"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Literary ずる-variant of 禁じる (N4): 法律で禁ずる. Phrase: 〜を禁じ得ない (cannot help feeling ...).","examples":[{"japanese":"この建物内での喫煙を禁ずる。","romaji":"Kono tatemononai de no kitsuen o kinzuru.","german":"Smoking is prohibited in this building.","furigana":"たてものない|きつえん|きん"},{"japanese":"その国では夜間の外出を禁ずる命令が出された。","romaji":"Sono kuni de wa yakan no gaishutsu o kinzuru meirei ga dasareta.","german":"An order prohibiting going out at night was issued in that country.","furigana":"くに|やかん|がいしゅつ|きん|めいれい|だ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"What is harmful in a situation and should be avoided: 油断は禁物 (complacency is out of place).","examples":[{"japanese":"勝っていても油断は禁物だ。","romaji":"Katte ite mo yudan wa kinmotsu da.","german":"Even if we are winning, complacency is taboo.","furigana":"か|ゆだん|きんもつ"},{"japanese":"風邪のときに無理は禁物です。","romaji":"Kaze no toki ni muri wa kinmotsu desu.","german":"When you have a cold, overexertion is out of the question.","furigana":"かぜ|むり|きんもつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Formal term for diligent, paid work: 勤労意欲, 勤労者. The holiday on November 23: 勤労感謝の日.","examples":[{"japanese":"十一月二十三日は勤労感謝の日で、祝日です。","romaji":"Jūichigatsu nijūsannichi wa kinrō kansha no hi de, shukujitsu desu.","german":"November 23 is Labor Thanksgiving Day, a national holiday.","furigana":"じゅういちがつにじゅうさんにち|きんろうかんしゃ|ひ|しゅくじつ"},{"japanese":"日本国憲法では、勤労は国民の権利であり、義務でもある。","romaji":"Nihonkoku kenpō de wa, kinrō wa kokumin no kenri de ari, gimu de mo aru.","german":"Under the Japanese Constitution, work is both a right and a duty of the citizens.","furigana":"にほんこくけんぽう|きんろう|こくみん|けんり|ぎむ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A riddle or knowledge question: クイズを出す (to pose a quiz), クイズ番組 (quiz show).","examples":[{"japanese":"テレビのクイズ番組に出たことがある。","romaji":"Terebi no kuizu bangumi ni deta koto ga aru.","german":"I have been on a TV quiz show before.","furigana":"ばんぐみ|で"},{"japanese":"子どもたちにクイズを出して楽しんだ。","romaji":"Kodomotachi ni kuizu o dashite tanoshinda.","german":"We had fun quizzing the children.","furigana":"こ|だ|たの"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Literary for \"hungry\": 空腹を感じる, 空腹時 (on an empty stomach). In everyday speech: おなかがすいた.","examples":[{"japanese":"空腹のまま運動すると、気分が悪くなることがある。","romaji":"Kūfuku no mama undō suru to, kibun ga waruku naru koto ga aru.","german":"Exercising on an empty stomach can make you feel unwell.","furigana":"くうふく|うんどう|きぶん|わる"},{"japanese":"この薬は空腹時に飲まないでください。","romaji":"Kono kusuri wa kūfukuji ni nomanaide kudasai.","german":"Please do not take this medicine on an empty stomach.","furigana":"くすり|くうふくじ|の"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A partial stretch between two points: 運休区間 (suspended section), 区間快速 (section rapid service).","examples":[{"japanese":"事故のため、この区間は運転を見合わせています。","romaji":"Jiko no tame, kono kukan wa unten o miawasete imasu.","german":"Because of an accident, service on this section is suspended.","furigana":"じこ|くかん|うんてん|みあ"},{"japanese":"駅伝では一人が一つの区間を走る。","romaji":"Ekiden de wa hitori ga hitotsu no kukan o hashiru.","german":"In an ekiden, each runner runs one section.","furigana":"えきでん|ひとり|ひと|くかん|はし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The stem of a plant between root and leaves.","examples":[{"japanese":"このひまわりは茎がとても太い。","romaji":"Kono himawari wa kuki ga totemo futoi.","german":"This sunflower has a very thick stem.","furigana":"くき|ふと"},{"japanese":"花を生けるときは、茎を斜めに切るとよい。","romaji":"Hana o ikeru toki wa, kuki o naname ni kiru to yoi.","german":"When arranging flowers, it is good to cut the stems at an angle.","furigana":"はな|い|くき|なな|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A point at which something is divided or brought to a close: 区切りをつける (to draw a line, to wrap up), 一区切り.","examples":[{"japanese":"仕事に一区切りがついたら、休みを取るつもりだ。","romaji":"Shigoto ni hitokugiri ga tsuitara, yasumi o toru tsumori da.","german":"When I reach a stopping point in my work, I plan to take time off.","furigana":"しごと|ひとくぎ|やす|と"},{"japanese":"文の区切りに気をつけて読んでください。","romaji":"Bun no kugiri ni ki o tsukete yonde kudasai.","german":"Please read paying attention to the breaks between sentences.","furigana":"ぶん|くぎ|き|よ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A lot for drawing, or a lottery: くじを引く, くじに当たる. Usually in kana; the kanji 籤 is rare. State lottery: 宝くじ.","examples":[{"japanese":"順番はくじで決めよう。","romaji":"Junban wa kuji de kimeyō.","german":"Let's decide the order by drawing lots.","furigana":"じゅんばん|き"},{"japanese":"商店街のくじで旅行券が当たった。","romaji":"Shōtengai no kuji de ryokōken ga atatta.","german":"I won a travel voucher in the shopping street's lottery.","furigana":"しょうてんがい|りょこうけん|あ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Drawing lots to decide something or to distribute prizes: くじ引きで決める.","examples":[{"japanese":"席はくじ引きで決めた。","romaji":"Seki wa kujibiki de kimeta.","german":"The seats were decided by drawing lots.","furigana":"せき|び|き"},{"japanese":"お祭りのくじ引きで一等が当たった。","romaji":"Omatsuri no kujibiki de ittō ga atatta.","german":"I won first prize in the lottery draw at the festival.","furigana":"まつ|び|いっとう|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Ticklish; figuratively also the embarrassed feeling of being praised: 褒められてくすぐったい. Usually in kana.","examples":[{"japanese":"足の裏を触られるとくすぐったい。","romaji":"Ashi no ura o sawarareru to kusuguttai.","german":"It tickles when someone touches the soles of my feet.","furigana":"あし|うら|さわ"},{"japanese":"みんなに褒められて、なんだかくすぐったい気持ちだ。","romaji":"Minna ni homerarete, nandaka kusuguttai kimochi da.","german":"Being praised by everyone makes me feel somehow embarrassed.","furigana":"ほ|きも"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Literary and elevated for something already mentioned, used almost only as 件の + noun: 件の男 (the man in question). With the reading けん, 件 means \"matter\".","examples":[{"japanese":"件の男が、また店の前に立っていた。","romaji":"Kudan no otoko ga, mata mise no mae ni tatte ita.","german":"The aforementioned man was standing in front of the shop again.","furigana":"くだん|おとこ|みせ|まえ|た"},{"japanese":"件の書類は、すでに社長に渡してあります。","romaji":"Kudan no shorui wa, sude ni shachō ni watashite arimasu.","german":"I have already handed the documents in question to the president.","furigana":"くだん|しょるい|しゃちょう|わた"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To sing or recite a song or poem quietly to oneself. Godan verb.","examples":[{"japanese":"母は料理をしながら古い歌を口ずさんでいた。","romaji":"Haha wa ryōri o shinagara furui uta o kuchizusande ita.","german":"My mother was humming an old song while cooking.","furigana":"はは|りょうり|ふる|うた|くち"},{"japanese":"思わず好きな曲を口ずさんでしまった。","romaji":"Omowazu suki na kyoku o kuchizusande shimatta.","german":"I found myself humming my favorite song.","furigana":"おも|す|きょく|くち"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A bird's beak. Idiom: くちばしを挟む／入れる (to butt in uninvited). Kanji 嘴.","examples":[{"japanese":"その鳥は長いくちばしで魚を捕る。","romaji":"Sono tori wa nagai kuchibashi de sakana o toru.","german":"That bird catches fish with its long beak.","furigana":"とり|なが|さかな|と"},{"japanese":"関係ない人が横からくちばしを挟んできた。","romaji":"Kankei nai hito ga yoko kara kuchibashi o hasande kita.","german":"Someone uninvolved butted in from the side.","furigana":"かんけい|ひと|よこ|はさ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Clearly outlined and easy to see: くっきり見える, くっきりした輪郭.","examples":[{"japanese":"晴れた日には富士山がくっきり見える。","romaji":"Hareta hi ni wa Fujisan ga kukkiri mieru.","german":"On clear days Mt. Fuji can be seen distinctly.","furigana":"は|ひ|ふじさん|み"},{"japanese":"雪の上に動物の足跡がくっきり残っていた。","romaji":"Yuki no ue ni dōbutsu no ashiato ga kukkiri nokotte ita.","german":"Animal footprints remained clearly visible in the snow.","furigana":"ゆき|うえ|どうぶつ|あしあと|のこ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Several uses: with a jerk or firmly (ぐっと引く), considerably more (ぐっと良くなる), deeply moved (胸にぐっと来る).","examples":[{"japanese":"練習を重ねて、彼の演奏はぐっと良くなった。","romaji":"Renshū o kasanete, kare no ensō wa gutto yoku natta.","german":"With repeated practice, his playing has improved markedly.","furigana":"れんしゅう|かさ|かれ|えんそう|よ"},{"japanese":"その手紙を読んで、胸にぐっと来た。","romaji":"Sono tegami o yonde, mune ni gutto kita.","german":"Reading that letter, I was deeply moved.","furigana":"てがみ|よ|むね|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Jewelry worn around the neck; today often ネックレス.","examples":[{"japanese":"祖母から真珠の首飾りをもらった。","romaji":"Sobo kara shinju no kubikazari o moratta.","german":"I received a pearl necklace from my grandmother.","furigana":"そぼ|しんじゅ|くびかざ"},{"japanese":"博物館には古代の首飾りが展示されている。","romaji":"Hakubutsukan ni wa kodai no kubikazari ga tenji sarete iru.","german":"The museum displays necklaces from ancient times.","furigana":"はくぶつかん|こだい|くびかざ|てんじ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The collar for dogs or cats: 犬に首輪をつける.","examples":[{"japanese":"犬に新しい首輪をつけた。","romaji":"Inu ni atarashii kubiwa o tsuketa.","german":"I put a new collar on the dog.","furigana":"いぬ|あたら|くびわ"},{"japanese":"首輪に名前と電話番号が書いてある。","romaji":"Kubiwa ni namae to denwa bangō ga kaite aru.","german":"The collar has a name and phone number written on it.","furigana":"くびわ|なまえ|でんわばんごう|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Graphic representation of numbers: 棒グラフ (bar chart), 円グラフ (pie chart), 折れ線グラフ (line graph).","examples":[{"japanese":"売上の変化をグラフにしてみた。","romaji":"Uriage no henka o gurafu ni shite mita.","german":"I tried putting the change in sales into a graph.","furigana":"うりあげ|へんか"},{"japanese":"このグラフを見ると、人口が減っていることが分かる。","romaji":"Kono gurafu o miru to, jinkō ga hette iru koto ga wakaru.","german":"Looking at this graph, you can see that the population is decreasing.","furigana":"み|じんこう|へ|わ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The color gray (also 灰色). Figuratively: グレーゾーン (gray area).","examples":[{"japanese":"グレーのスーツを着て面接に行った。","romaji":"Gurē no sūtsu o kite mensetsu ni itta.","german":"I went to the interview wearing a gray suit.","furigana":"き|めんせつ|い"},{"japanese":"その方法は法律的にはグレーだと言われている。","romaji":"Sono hōhō wa hōritsuteki ni wa gurē da to iwarete iru.","german":"That method is said to be legally gray.","furigana":"ほうほう|ほうりつてき|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Lifting crane at construction sites and ports. The bird crane is called 鶴.","examples":[{"japanese":"大きなクレーンで鉄骨をつり上げている。","romaji":"Ōki na kurēn de tekkotsu o tsuriagete iru.","german":"Steel beams are being lifted with a large crane.","furigana":"おお|てっこつ|あ"},{"japanese":"港には何台ものクレーンが並んでいる。","romaji":"Minato ni wa nandai mo no kurēn ga narande iru.","german":"Several cranes stand in a row at the port.","furigana":"みなと|なんだい|なら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A set of similar things; used almost only in the phrase 群を抜く (to stand out) and as a mathematical term. Homophones: 軍, 郡.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女の成績はクラスの中で群を抜いている。","romaji":"Kanojo no seiseki wa kurasu no naka de gun o nuite iru.","german":"Her grades stand out far above the rest of the class.","furigana":"かのじょ|せいせき|なか|ぐん|ぬ"},{"japanese":"数学の授業で群の定義を学んだ。","romaji":"Sūgaku no jugyō de gun no teigi o mananda.","german":"In math class we learned the definition of a group.","furigana":"すうがく|じゅぎょう|ぐん|ていぎ|まな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Everything concerning the military: 軍事力 (military power), 軍事基地 (military base).","examples":[{"japanese":"両国の間で軍事的な緊張が高まっている。","romaji":"Ryōkoku no aida de gunjiteki na kinchō ga takamatte iru.","german":"Military tension is rising between the two countries.","furigana":"りょうこく|あいだ|ぐんじてき|きんちょう|たか"},{"japanese":"彼は軍事に詳しいジャーナリストだ。","romaji":"Kare wa gunji ni kuwashii jānarisuto da.","german":"He is a journalist who knows a lot about military matters.","furigana":"かれ|ぐんじ|くわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A hereditary ruler such as a king or emperor: 君主制 (monarchy).","examples":[{"japanese":"その国では君主が大きな権力を持っていた。","romaji":"Sono kuni de wa kunshu ga ōki na kenryoku o motte ita.","german":"In that country the monarch held great power.","furigana":"くに|くんしゅ|おお|けんりょく|も"},{"japanese":"現在も君主制を続けている国は少なくない。","romaji":"Genzai mo kunshusei o tsuzukete iru kuni wa sukunakunai.","german":"Quite a few countries still have a monarchy today.","furigana":"げんざい|くんしゅせい|つづ|くに|すく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A state's military equipment: 軍備を増強する (to build up arms), 軍備縮小 (arms reduction).","examples":[{"japanese":"各国が軍備を増強している。","romaji":"Kakkoku ga gunbi o zōkyō shite iru.","german":"Countries are building up their armaments.","furigana":"かっこく|ぐんび|ぞうきょう"},{"japanese":"会議では軍備の縮小が話し合われた。","romaji":"Kaigi de wa gunbi no shukushō ga hanashiawareta.","german":"Reducing armaments was discussed at the conference.","furigana":"かいぎ|ぐんび|しゅくしょう|はな|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The uniform of soldiers.","examples":[{"japanese":"祖父の若い頃の軍服姿の写真が残っている。","romaji":"Sofu no wakai koro no gunpuku sugata no shashin ga nokotte iru.","german":"There is still a photo of my grandfather in his youth in military uniform.","furigana":"そふ|わか|ころ|ぐんぷくすがた|しゃしん|のこ"},{"japanese":"映画の撮影のために、古い軍服が用意された。","romaji":"Eiga no satsuei no tame ni, furui gunpuku ga yōi sareta.","german":"Old military uniforms were prepared for the film shoot.","furigana":"えいが|さつえい|ふる|ぐんぷく|ようい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Court-imposed punishment: 刑が確定する (the sentence becomes final), 刑に服する (to serve a sentence), 懲役三年の刑.","examples":[{"japanese":"被告には懲役五年の刑が言い渡された。","romaji":"Hikoku ni wa chōeki gonen no kei ga iiwatasareta.","german":"The defendant was sentenced to five years of imprisonment.","furigana":"ひこく|ちょうえきごねん|けい|い|わた"},{"japanese":"彼は刑を終えて、社会に戻った。","romaji":"Kare wa kei o oete, shakai ni modotta.","german":"He finished serving his sentence and returned to society.","furigana":"かれ|けい|お|しゃかい|もど"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Skill acquired through practice, especially in entertainment and the performing arts: 芸を磨く, 犬に芸を教える. Proverb: 芸は身を助ける.","examples":[{"japanese":"あの落語家は長年芸を磨いてきた。","romaji":"Ano rakugoka wa naganen gei o migaite kita.","german":"That rakugo storyteller has honed his craft for many years.","furigana":"らくごか|ながねんげい|みが"},{"japanese":"うちの犬は簡単な芸ができる。","romaji":"Uchi no inu wa kantan na gei ga dekiru.","german":"Our dog can do simple tricks.","furigana":"いぬ|かんたん|げい"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 48 (source: N4; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"な-adjective: light-footed and spirited (軽快な足取り, 軽快な音楽); in medicine: symptoms subside (症状が軽快する). Homophone: 警戒.","examples":[{"japanese":"軽快な音楽に合わせて体を動かした。","romaji":"Keikai na ongaku ni awasete karada o ugokashita.","german":"We moved our bodies to lively music.","furigana":"けいかい|おんがく|あ|からだ|うご"},{"japanese":"薬を飲んでから、症状は軽快している。","romaji":"Kusuri o nonde kara, shōjō wa keikai shite iru.","german":"Since he started taking the medicine, the symptoms have been improving.","furigana":"くすり|の|しょうじょう|けいかい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"An instrument for measuring, e.g. in a cockpit: 計器を確認する. Homophone: 景気 (economic conditions).","examples":[{"japanese":"パイロットは離陸前に計器を確認した。","romaji":"Pairotto wa ririku mae ni keiki o kakunin shita.","german":"The pilot checked the instruments before takeoff.","furigana":"りりくまえ|けいき|かくにん"},{"japanese":"この計器で室内の湿度を測っている。","romaji":"Kono keiki de shitsunai no shitsudo o hakatte iru.","german":"The indoor humidity is measured with this instrument.","furigana":"けいき|しつない|しつど|はか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Formal closing phrase of a letter, matching the salutation 拝啓 at the beginning.","examples":[{"japanese":"手紙は「拝啓」で始め、「敬具」で結ぶ。","romaji":"Tegami wa \"haikei\" de hajime, \"keigu\" de musubu.","german":"A letter begins with \"Haikei\" and ends with \"Keigu\".","furigana":"てがみ|はいけい|はじ|けいぐ|むす"},{"japanese":"最後に敬具と書いて、日付と名前を添えた。","romaji":"Saigo ni keigu to kaite, hizuke to namae o soeta.","german":"At the end I wrote Keigu and added the date and my name.","furigana":"さいご|けいぐ|か|ひづけ|なまえ|そ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 50 (source: N4; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"Degree of inclination: 急な傾斜 (steep slope), 傾斜地 (sloping ground). As a verb: 傾斜する.","examples":[{"japanese":"この道は傾斜が急なので、自転車では大変だ。","romaji":"Kono michi wa keisha ga kyū na node, jitensha de wa taihen da.","german":"This road is steep, so it's hard on a bicycle.","furigana":"みち|けいしゃ|きゅう|じてんしゃ|たいへん"},{"japanese":"屋根に少し傾斜をつけて、雨が流れるようにした。","romaji":"Yane ni sukoshi keisha o tsukete, ame ga nagareru yō ni shita.","german":"We gave the roof a slight slope so that the rain runs off.","furigana":"やね|すこ|けいしゃ|あめ|なが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"How a contest currently stands: 形勢が不利になる (the situation turns unfavorable), 形勢を逆転する (to turn the tables). Homophone: 形成.","examples":[{"japanese":"後半に入って、形勢が逆転した。","romaji":"Kōhan ni haitte, keisei ga gyakuten shita.","german":"In the second half, the situation was reversed.","furigana":"こうはん|はい|けいせい|ぎゃくてん"},{"japanese":"選挙の形勢はまだはっきりしない。","romaji":"Senkyo no keisei wa mada hakkiri shinai.","german":"The state of the election is still unclear.","furigana":"せんきょ|けいせい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The coming into being or taking shape of a form: 人格形成 (personality formation), 形成する. Homophone: 形勢.","examples":[{"japanese":"子ども時代の経験は人格の形成に影響する。","romaji":"Kodomo jidai no keiken wa jinkaku no keisei ni eikyō suru.","german":"Childhood experiences influence the formation of personality.","furigana":"こ|じだい|けいけん|じんかく|けいせい|えいきょう"},{"japanese":"川の流れがこの谷を形成した。","romaji":"Kawa no nagare ga kono tani o keisei shita.","german":"The flow of the river formed this valley.","furigana":"かわ|なが|たに|けいせい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The outer form or organizational form: 雇用形態 (form of employment), 生活形態 (lifestyle). Homophone: 携帯.","examples":[{"japanese":"働き方の形態が多様になってきた。","romaji":"Hatarakikata no keitai ga tayō ni natte kita.","german":"The forms of working have become more diverse.","furigana":"はたら|かた|けいたい|たよう"},{"japanese":"昆虫は成長とともに形態が大きく変わる。","romaji":"Konchū wa seichō to tomo ni keitai ga ōkiku kawaru.","german":"Insects change their form greatly as they grow.","furigana":"こんちゅう|せいちょう|けいたい|おお|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A rank in the Japanese police, roughly \"inspector\"; familiar from crime dramas.","examples":[{"japanese":"事件の捜査は田中警部が担当している。","romaji":"Jiken no sōsa wa Tanaka keibu ga tantō shite iru.","german":"Inspector Tanaka is in charge of the investigation.","furigana":"じけん|そうさ|たなかけいぶ|たんとう"},{"japanese":"彼は若くして警部に昇進した。","romaji":"Kare wa wakakushite keibu ni shōshin shita.","german":"He was promoted to inspector at a young age.","furigana":"かれ|わか|けいぶ|しょうしん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Morally repulsive or impure, mostly as a strong expression of disgust: 汚らわしいお金.","examples":[{"japanese":"そんな汚らわしいお金は受け取れない。","romaji":"Sonna kegarawashii okane wa uketorenai.","german":"I can't accept such filthy money.","furigana":"けが|かね|う|と"},{"japanese":"彼の話を聞くのも汚らわしい。","romaji":"Kare no hanashi o kiku no mo kegarawashii.","german":"Even listening to him is disgusting.","furigana":"かれ|はなし|き|けが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"An invited guest on TV, radio, or at events: ゲスト出演 (guest appearance). A guest in one's own home is called 客 or お客さん.","examples":[{"japanese":"今日の番組のゲストは有名な作家です。","romaji":"Kyō no bangumi no gesuto wa yūmei na sakka desu.","german":"Today's guest on the program is a famous author.","furigana":"きょう|ばんぐみ|ゆうめい|さっか"},{"japanese":"イベントには海外からゲストが招かれた。","romaji":"Ibento ni wa kaigai kara gesuto ga manekareta.","german":"Guests from overseas were invited to the event.","furigana":"かいがい|まね"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Wild animal; today mainly pejorative for a cruel person: あいつはけだものだ. Neutral word for animals: 獣（けもの）.","examples":[{"japanese":"昔話では、山に住む恐ろしい獣が村を襲う。","romaji":"Mukashibanashi de wa, yama ni sumu osoroshii kedamono ga mura o osou.","german":"In old tales, a terrible beast living in the mountains attacks the village.","furigana":"むかしばなし|やま|す|おそ|けだもの|むら|おそ"},{"japanese":"人を傷つけて平気でいるなんて、まるで獣だ。","romaji":"Hito o kizutsukete heiki de iru nante, maru de kedamono da.","german":"To hurt people and feel nothing, he is nothing but a beast.","furigana":"ひと|きず|へいき|けだもの"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mostly with a negative: さほど難しくない (not particularly difficult). Somewhat more literary than それほど; usually written in kana.","examples":[{"japanese":"今回の試験はさほど難しくなかった。","romaji":"Konkai no shiken wa sahodo muzukashikunakatta.","german":"The exam this time was not particularly difficult.","furigana":"こんかい|しけん|むずか"},{"japanese":"駅から会場まではさほど遠くありません。","romaji":"Eki kara kaijō made wa sahodo tōku arimasen.","german":"It is not very far from the station to the venue.","furigana":"えき|かいじょう|とお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Colloquial godan verb formed from サボ(タージュ) + る: 授業をサボる (to skip class), 仕事をサボる.","examples":[{"japanese":"雨だったので、授業をサボって家で寝ていた。","romaji":"Ame datta node, jugyō o sabotte ie de nete ita.","german":"Because it was raining, I skipped class and slept at home.","furigana":"あめ|じゅぎょう|いえ|ね"},{"japanese":"練習をサボると、すぐに体力が落ちる。","romaji":"Renshū o saboru to, sugu ni tairyoku ga ochiru.","german":"If you slack off on practice, your stamina drops quickly.","furigana":"れんしゅう|たいりょく|お"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 11 (source: N5; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"寒気がする = to have the chills, to shiver (often with a fever). With the reading かんき, 寒気 means \"cold air\" in weather forecasts.","examples":[{"japanese":"朝から寒気がして、熱を測ったら三十八度あった。","romaji":"Asa kara samuke ga shite, netsu o hakattara sanjūhachido atta.","german":"I've had the chills since morning, and when I took my temperature it was 38 degrees.","furigana":"あさ|さむけ|ねつ|はか|さんじゅうはちど"},{"japanese":"怖い話を聞いて、背中に寒気が走った。","romaji":"Kowai hanashi o kiite, senaka ni samuke ga hashitta.","german":"Hearing the scary story sent a chill down my spine.","furigana":"こわ|はなし|き|せなか|さむけ|はし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mostly written in kana さらう. To take someone or something away by force (子どもをさらう, 波にさらわれる) and figuratively: 人気をさらう (to steal the spotlight).","examples":[{"japanese":"大きな波に帽子をさらわれた。","romaji":"Ōki na nami ni bōshi o sarawareta.","german":"A big wave swept my hat away.","furigana":"おお|なみ|ぼうし"},{"japanese":"新人の歌手が会場の人気をさらった。","romaji":"Shinjin no kashu ga kaijō no ninki o saratta.","german":"The new singer won the crowd's favor at the venue.","furigana":"しんじん|かしゅ|かいじょう|にんき"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"In chemistry: acid (opposite: アルカリ). The adjectival form is 酸性 (acidic in the chemical sense); a sour taste is 酸っぱい.","examples":[{"japanese":"この液体は強い酸なので、手で触らないでください。","romaji":"Kono ekitai wa tsuyoi san na node, te de sawaranaide kudasai.","german":"This liquid is a strong acid, so please do not touch it with your hands.","furigana":"えきたい|つよ|さん|て|さわ"},{"japanese":"胃の中の酸が食べ物を分解する。","romaji":"I no naka no san ga tabemono o bunkai suru.","german":"The acid in the stomach breaks down food.","furigana":"い|なか|さん|た|もの|ぶんかい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Casual \"thank you\" from English, only among friends. Polite: ありがとうございます.","examples":[{"japanese":"「これ、落としたよ。」「サンキュー！」","romaji":"\"Kore, otoshita yo.\" \"Sankyū!\"","german":"\"You dropped this.\" \"Thank you!\"","furigana":"お"},{"japanese":"手伝ってくれてサンキュー。助かったよ。","romaji":"Tetsudatte kurete sankyū. Tasukatta yo.","german":"Thanks for helping. You were a great help.","furigana":"てつだ|たす"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Money left over or still to be paid: 残金を払う (to pay the balance). An account balance is more often called 残高.","examples":[{"japanese":"手付金を払ったので、残金は来月払います。","romaji":"Tetsukekin o haratta node, zankin wa raigetsu haraimasu.","german":"I've paid the deposit, so I'll pay the remaining balance next month.","furigana":"|はら|ざんきん|らいげつはら"},{"japanese":"旅行の最後に残金を数えたら、三千円しかなかった。","romaji":"Ryokō no saigo ni zankin o kazoetara, sanzen en shika nakatta.","german":"When I counted my leftover money at the end of the trip, there was only 3,000 yen.","furigana":"りょこう|さいご|ざんきん|かぞ|さんぜんえん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The period after childbirth: 産後休暇 (postnatal leave). Opposite: 産前 (before birth). Similar sound: さんご (coral).","examples":[{"japanese":"産後はしばらく無理をしないでください。","romaji":"Sango wa shibaraku muri o shinaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't overexert yourself for a while after giving birth.","furigana":"さんご|むり"},{"japanese":"産後の体調が戻るまで、母が手伝いに来てくれた。","romaji":"Sango no taichō ga modoru made, haha ga tetsudai ni kite kureta.","german":"My mother came to help until my health recovered after the birth.","furigana":"さんご|たいちょう|もど|はは|てつだ|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mainly for mineral resources and products of a region: 石油を産出する (to produce oil). As a verb: 産出する.","examples":[{"japanese":"この国は大量の石油を産出している。","romaji":"Kono kuni wa tairyō no sekiyu o sanshutsu shite iru.","german":"This country produces large quantities of oil.","furigana":"くに|たいりょう|せきゆ|さんしゅつ"},{"japanese":"この地方は昔から良質の石を産出してきた。","romaji":"Kono chihō wa mukashi kara ryōshitsu no ishi o sanshutsu shite kita.","german":"This region has produced high-quality stone since long ago.","furigana":"ちほう|むかし|りょうしつ|いし|さんしゅつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Humble word (謙譲語) for one's own visit to a superior: 明日参上いたします. Today formal or jocular.","examples":[{"japanese":"明日の午後、改めて参上いたします。","romaji":"Ashita no gogo, aratamete sanjō itashimasu.","german":"I will call on you again tomorrow afternoon.","furigana":"あした|ごご|あらた|さんじょう"},{"japanese":"ご依頼の件で、本日参上いたしました。","romaji":"Goirai no ken de, honjitsu sanjō itashimashita.","german":"I have come today regarding your request.","furigana":"いらい|けん|ほんじつさんじょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The remaining amount in an account or on a card: 口座の残高, 残高不足 (insufficient balance).","examples":[{"japanese":"銀行のアプリで口座の残高を確認した。","romaji":"Ginkō no apuri de kōza no zandaka o kakunin shita.","german":"I checked my account balance in the bank's app.","furigana":"ぎんこう|こうざ|ざんだか|かくにん"},{"japanese":"カードの残高が足りなくて、改札を通れなかった。","romaji":"Kādo no zandaka ga tarinakute, kaisatsu o tōrenakatta.","german":"The card balance was insufficient, and I couldn't get through the ticket gate.","furigana":"ざんだか|た|かいさつ|とお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Often shortened to サンタ（さん）. In Japan, Santa Claus brings children presents on Christmas Eve.","examples":[{"japanese":"子どもたちはサンタクロースからのプレゼントを楽しみにしている。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa santakurōsu kara no purezento o tanoshimi ni shite iru.","german":"The children are looking forward to presents from Santa Claus.","furigana":"こ|たの"},{"japanese":"父がサンタクロースの服を着て、子どもを驚かせた。","romaji":"Chichi ga santakurōsu no fuku o kite, kodomo o odorokaseta.","german":"My father put on a Santa Claus costume and surprised the children.","furigana":"ちち|ふく|き|こ|おどろ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Elevated word for high praise: 自然を賛美する. In church: 賛美歌 (hymn).","examples":[{"japanese":"詩人は故郷の自然を賛美する詩を書いた。","romaji":"Shijin wa kokyō no shizen o sanbi suru shi o kaita.","german":"The poet wrote a poem praising the nature of his hometown.","furigana":"しじん|こきょう|しぜん|さんび|し|か"},{"japanese":"彼の勇気ある行動は多くの人から賛美された。","romaji":"Kare no yūki aru kōdō wa ōku no hito kara sanbi sareta.","german":"His courageous act was praised by many people.","furigana":"かれ|ゆうき|こうどう|おお|ひと|さんび"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The middle part of a mountain slope, between the foot (麓) and the summit (山頂).","examples":[{"japanese":"山腹に小さな寺が建っている。","romaji":"Sanpuku ni chiisa na tera ga tatte iru.","german":"A small temple stands on the mountainside.","furigana":"さんぷく|ちい|てら|た"},{"japanese":"大雨で山腹の一部が崩れた。","romaji":"Ōame de sanpuku no ichibu ga kuzureta.","german":"Heavy rain caused part of the mountainside to collapse.","furigana":"おおあめ|さんぷく|いちぶ|くず"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Hospital department or clinic for obstetrics (産科) and gynecology (婦人科).","examples":[{"japanese":"妊娠がわかって、産婦人科を受診した。","romaji":"Ninshin ga wakatte, sanfujinka o jushin shita.","german":"When I found out I was pregnant, I went to see an OB-GYN.","furigana":"にんしん|さんふじんか|じゅしん"},{"japanese":"この病院の産婦人科は予約が取りにくい。","romaji":"Kono byōin no sanfujinka wa yoyaku ga torinikui.","german":"It is hard to get an appointment at this hospital's OB-GYN department.","furigana":"びょういん|さんふじんか|よやく|と"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Products of a region (土地の産物) and figuratively the result of something: 努力の産物, 時代の産物.","examples":[{"japanese":"りんごはこの地方の代表的な産物だ。","romaji":"Ringo wa kono chihō no daihyōteki na sanbutsu da.","german":"Apples are a typical product of this region.","furigana":"ちほう|だいひょうてき|さんぶつ"},{"japanese":"この新しい薬は長年の研究の産物だ。","romaji":"Kono atarashii kusuri wa naganen no kenkyū no sanbutsu da.","german":"This new medicine is the product of many years of research.","furigana":"あたら|くすり|ながねん|けんきゅう|さんぶつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"How something turned out in the end: 仕上がりがきれいだ (neatly finished). Verb: 仕上がる (to be finished).","examples":[{"japanese":"このシャツは仕上がりがとても丁寧だ。","romaji":"Kono shatsu wa shiagari ga totemo teinei da.","german":"This shirt has a very careful finish.","furigana":"しあ|ていねい"},{"japanese":"写真の仕上がりは来週になります。","romaji":"Shashin no shiagari wa raishū ni narimasu.","german":"The photos will be ready next week.","furigana":"しゃしん|しあ|らいしゅう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The last step of the work: 仕上げにこしょうを振る (to sprinkle pepper at the end). Verb: 仕上げる.","examples":[{"japanese":"仕上げにパセリをのせて、完成です。","romaji":"Shiage ni paseri o nosete, kansei desu.","german":"Top it with parsley as a finishing touch, and it is done.","furigana":"しあ|かんせい"},{"japanese":"作品の仕上げに三日かかった。","romaji":"Sakuhin no shiage ni mikka kakatta.","german":"The finishing of the work took three days.","furigana":"さくひん|しあ|みっか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive ichidan verb: to bring a piece of work to completion (レポートを仕上げる). Intransitive: 仕上がる.","examples":[{"japanese":"締め切りまでにレポートを仕上げなければならない。","romaji":"Shimekiri made ni repōto o shiagenakereba naranai.","german":"I have to finish the report by the deadline.","furigana":"し|き|しあ"},{"japanese":"職人は一日で椅子を仕上げた。","romaji":"Shokunin wa ichinichi de isu o shiageta.","german":"The craftsman finished the chair in one day.","furigana":"しょくにん|いちにち|いす|しあ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"More technical than 飼う: 動物を飼育する, 飼育係 (animal keeper). For plants one says 栽培.","examples":[{"japanese":"この動物園ではパンダを飼育している。","romaji":"Kono dōbutsuen de wa panda o shiiku shite iru.","german":"This zoo keeps pandas.","furigana":"どうぶつえん|しいく"},{"japanese":"小学校で子どもたちがウサギの飼育を担当している。","romaji":"Shōgakkō de kodomotachi ga usagi no shiiku o tantō shite iru.","german":"At the elementary school, the children take care of the rabbits.","furigana":"しょうがっこう|こ|しいく|たんとう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive ichidan verb for a shop's purchasing of goods; figuratively also 情報を仕入れる. Noun: 仕入れ.","examples":[{"japanese":"この店は毎朝市場で魚を仕入れている。","romaji":"Kono mise wa maiasa ichiba de sakana o shiirete iru.","german":"This restaurant buys fish at the market every morning.","furigana":"みせ|まいあさいちば|さかな|しい"},{"japanese":"旅行の前に、現地の情報を仕入れておいた。","romaji":"Ryokō no mae ni, genchi no jōhō o shiirete oita.","german":"Before the trip, I gathered information about the local area.","furigana":"りょこう|まえ|げんち|じょうほう|しい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Psychological term for self-awareness: 自我が芽生える (the sense of self awakens, e.g. in toddlers).","examples":[{"japanese":"二歳ごろになると、子どもに自我が芽生える。","romaji":"Nisai goro ni naru to, kodomo ni jiga ga mebaeru.","german":"Around age two, a child's sense of self begins to emerge.","furigana":"にさい|こ|じが|めば"},{"japanese":"彼は自我が強く、他人の意見を聞かない。","romaji":"Kare wa jiga ga tsuyoku, tanin no iken o kikanai.","german":"He has a strong ego and does not listen to other people's opinions.","furigana":"かれ|じが|つよ|たにん|いけん|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The built-up streets of a city: 市街戦 (street fighting). Common in 市街地 (urban area).","examples":[{"japanese":"丘の上から市街が一望できる。","romaji":"Oka no ue kara shigai ga ichibō dekiru.","german":"You can see the whole city from the top of the hill.","furigana":"おか|うえ|しがい|いちぼう"},{"japanese":"台風で市街の多くの道路が水につかった。","romaji":"Taifū de shigai no ōku no dōro ga mizu ni tsukatta.","german":"Many streets in the city were flooded by the typhoon.","furigana":"たいふう|しがい|おお|どうろ|みず"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A device (からくり) or a trick: 仕掛け時計 (mechanical figure clock), 種も仕掛けもない (no trick at all). Verb: 仕掛ける.","examples":[{"japanese":"この時計は、正午になると人形が出てくる仕掛けになっている。","romaji":"Kono tokei wa, shōgo ni naru to ningyō ga dete kuru shikake ni natte iru.","german":"This clock has a mechanism where figures come out at noon.","furigana":"とけい|しょうご|にんぎょう|で|しか"},{"japanese":"手品師は「種も仕掛けもありません」と言った。","romaji":"Tejinashi wa \"tane mo shikake mo arimasen\" to itta.","german":"The magician said, \"There is no trick or gimmick.\"","furigana":"てじなし|たね|しか|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Hard-fired white ceramic; the umbrella term for pottery and porcelain: 陶磁器. Similar sound: 時期, 磁気.","examples":[{"japanese":"この皿は有田焼の磁器だ。","romaji":"Kono sara wa Aritayaki no jiki da.","german":"This plate is Arita ware porcelain.","furigana":"さら||じき"},{"japanese":"磁器は陶器より薄くて硬い。","romaji":"Jiki wa tōki yori usukute katai.","german":"Porcelain is thinner and harder than earthenware.","furigana":"じき|とうき|うす|かた"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A venue for weddings or funerals: 結婚式場 (wedding hall), 葬儀式場 (funeral hall).","examples":[{"japanese":"結婚式の一年前に式場を予約した。","romaji":"Kekkonshiki no ichinen mae ni shikijō o yoyaku shita.","german":"We booked the hall a year before the wedding.","furigana":"けっこんしき|いちねんまえ|しきじょう|よやく"},{"japanese":"式場の入り口で受付をしてください。","romaji":"Shikijō no iriguchi de uketsuke o shite kudasai.","german":"Please check in at the entrance of the hall.","furigana":"しきじょう|い|ぐち|うけつけ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A technical axis (車軸, axle) and an axis in mathematics (Ｘ軸); figuratively the core: チームの軸になる選手.","examples":[{"japanese":"地球は軸を中心に回っている。","romaji":"Chikyū wa jiku o chūshin ni mawatte iru.","german":"The Earth rotates around its axis.","furigana":"ちきゅう|じく|ちゅうしん|まわ"},{"japanese":"彼はチームの軸となる選手だ。","romaji":"Kare wa chīmu no jiku to naru senshu da.","german":"He is the central player of the team.","furigana":"かれ|じく|せんしゅ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Colloquial godan verb: 試験をしくじる (to botch an exam). More neutral: 失敗する.","examples":[{"japanese":"緊張して、面接でしくじってしまった。","romaji":"Kinchō shite, mensetsu de shikujitte shimatta.","german":"I was nervous and blew the interview.","furigana":"きんちょう|めんせつ"},{"japanese":"一度しくじっても、あきらめずにまた挑戦すればいい。","romaji":"Ichido shikujitte mo, akiramezu ni mata chōsen sureba ii.","german":"Even if you fail once, you should not give up and just try again.","furigana":"いちど|ちょうせん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Godan verb: salty snacks or matches absorb moisture and go soft: せんべいが湿気る.","examples":[{"japanese":"袋を開けたままにしたら、ポテトチップスが湿気ってしまった。","romaji":"Fukuro o aketa mama ni shitara, poteto chippusu ga shikette shimatta.","german":"I left the bag open, and the potato chips went soft.","furigana":"ふくろ|あ|しけ"},{"japanese":"梅雨の時期はマッチが湿気って火がつきにくい。","romaji":"Tsuyu no jiki wa matchi ga shikette hi ga tsukinikui.","german":"In the rainy season, matches get damp and are hard to light.","furigana":"つゆ|じき|しけ|ひ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Often as a suffix: 健康志向 (health consciousness), ブランド志向 (brand orientation). Similar sound: 思考 (thinking), 嗜好 (preference).","examples":[{"japanese":"最近は健康志向の食品がよく売れている。","romaji":"Saikin wa kenkō shikō no shokuhin ga yoku urete iru.","german":"Health-oriented foods have been selling well lately.","furigana":"さいきん|けんこうしこう|しょくひん|う"},{"japanese":"彼女は昔から海外での仕事を志向していた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa mukashi kara kaigai de no shigoto o shikō shite ita.","german":"She had long aspired to work overseas.","furigana":"かのじょ|むかし|かいがい|しごと|しこう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Thinking as a process: 思考力 (thinking ability), プラス思考 (positive thinking).","examples":[{"japanese":"疲れていると思考が止まってしまう。","romaji":"Tsukarete iru to shikō ga tomatte shimau.","german":"When I'm tired, my thinking stops.","furigana":"つか|しこう|と"},{"japanese":"この授業では論理的な思考を育てる。","romaji":"Kono jugyō de wa ronriteki na shikō o sodateru.","german":"This class fosters logical thinking.","furigana":"じゅぎょう|ろんりてき|しこう|そだ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"An official visit to see conditions on the spot: 被災地を視察する. As a verb: 視察する.","examples":[{"japanese":"首相が被災地を視察した。","romaji":"Shushō ga hisaichi o shisatsu shita.","german":"The prime minister inspected the disaster area.","furigana":"しゅしょう|ひさいち|しさつ"},{"japanese":"市の職員が海外の施設を視察に行った。","romaji":"Shi no shokuin ga kaigai no shisetsu o shisatsu ni itta.","german":"City officials went on an inspection trip to facilities overseas.","furigana":"し|しょくいん|かいがい|しせつ|しさつ|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Often in compounds: 自主的 (voluntary), 自主練習 (self-directed practice), 自主規制 (voluntary restraint). Similar sound: 自首.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は授業のあとも自主練習を続けている。","romaji":"Kare wa jugyō no ato mo jishu renshū o tsuzukete iru.","german":"He keeps practicing on his own even after class.","furigana":"かれ|じゅぎょう|じしゅれんしゅう|つづ"},{"japanese":"この学校は生徒の自主を重んじている。","romaji":"Kono gakkō wa seito no jishu o omonjite iru.","german":"This school values the independence of its students.","furigana":"がっこう|せいと|じしゅ|おも"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A perpetrator surrenders to the police before being identified: 警察に自首する. Similar sound: 自主.","examples":[{"japanese":"犯人は翌日、警察に自首した。","romaji":"Hannin wa yokujitsu, keisatsu ni jishu shita.","german":"The culprit turned himself in to the police the next day.","furigana":"はんにん|よくじつ|けいさつ|じしゅ"},{"japanese":"家族に説得されて、彼は自首することにした。","romaji":"Kazoku ni settoku sarete, kare wa jishu suru koto ni shita.","german":"Persuaded by his family, he decided to turn himself in.","furigana":"かぞく|せっとく|かれ|じしゅ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A single drop, especially of water: 雨の滴. Also written 雫. The reading てき is used as a counter (一滴).","examples":[{"japanese":"葉の先から雨の滴が落ちた。","romaji":"Ha no saki kara ame no shizuku ga ochita.","german":"A raindrop fell from the tip of the leaf.","furigana":"は|さき|あめ|しずく|お"},{"japanese":"窓ガラスに滴がついている。","romaji":"Madogarasu ni shizuku ga tsuite iru.","german":"There are drops on the windowpane.","furigana":"まど|しずく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Formal, mostly as ご子息 about someone else's son. For one's own son one says 息子.","examples":[{"japanese":"社長のご子息が来月結婚されるそうだ。","romaji":"Shachō no goshisoku ga raigetsu kekkon sareru sō da.","german":"I hear the president's son is getting married next month.","furigana":"しゃちょう|しそく|らいげつけっこん"},{"japanese":"ご子息はお元気でいらっしゃいますか。","romaji":"Goshisoku wa ogenki de irasshaimasu ka.","german":"Is your son doing well?","furigana":"しそく|げんき"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The form of a written character (新字体 vs. 旧字体, e.g. 国 and 國) and the typeface of a text. Similar sound: 事態, 辞退.","examples":[{"japanese":"この石碑の漢字は古い字体で書かれている。","romaji":"Kono sekihi no kanji wa furui jitai de kakarete iru.","german":"The kanji on this stone monument are written in the old character forms.","furigana":"せきひ|かんじ|ふる|じたい|か"},{"japanese":"この字体は画面で読みやすい。","romaji":"Kono jitai wa gamen de yomiyasui.","german":"This typeface is easy to read on a screen.","furigana":"じたい|がめん|よ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mostly negative: a hidden, self-serving intention, often romantic in nature. 下心がある (to have ulterior motives).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼の親切には下心があるように思える。","romaji":"Kare no shinsetsu ni wa shitagokoro ga aru yō ni omoeru.","german":"His kindness seems to have an ulterior motive.","furigana":"かれ|しんせつ|したごころ|おも"},{"japanese":"下心なしで手伝っただけです。","romaji":"Shitagokoro nashi de tetsudatta dake desu.","german":"I only helped, with no ulterior motive.","furigana":"したごころ|てつだ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Preparatory foundation (下地がある = to have a grounding) and, in crafts, the base coat: 化粧の下地 (makeup base).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は中国語の下地があるので、上達が早い。","romaji":"Kare wa chūgokugo no shitaji ga aru node, jōtatsu ga hayai.","german":"He has a background in Chinese, so he improves quickly.","furigana":"かれ|ちゅうごくご|したじ|じょうたつ|はや"},{"japanese":"ペンキを塗る前に、壁の下地を整える。","romaji":"Penki o nuru mae ni, kabe no shitaji o totonoeru.","german":"Before painting, prepare the base of the wall.","furigana":"ぬ|まえ|かべ|したじ|ととの"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Godan verb, mostly with に: 自然に親しむ (to enjoy nature), 本に親しむ (to get into books). Common: 親しみやすい (approachable).","examples":[{"japanese":"子どものころから音楽に親しんできた。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro kara ongaku ni shitashinde kita.","german":"I have been familiar with music since childhood.","furigana":"こ|おんがく|した"},{"japanese":"週末は山に出かけて自然に親しんでいる。","romaji":"Shūmatsu wa yama ni dekakete shizen ni shitashinde iru.","german":"On weekends I go to the mountains and enjoy nature.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|やま|で|しぜん|した"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To investigate or prepare something beforehand: 旅行の下調べ, 授業の下調べ. As a verb: 下調べする.","examples":[{"japanese":"旅行の前に、ホテルの周りを下調べしておいた。","romaji":"Ryokō no mae ni, hoteru no mawari o shitashirabe shite oita.","german":"Before the trip, I looked into the area around the hotel in advance.","furigana":"りょこう|まえ|まわ|したしら"},{"japanese":"明日の授業の下調べをしておこう。","romaji":"Ashita no jugyō no shitashirabe o shite okō.","german":"Let me prepare for tomorrow's class.","furigana":"あした|じゅぎょう|したしら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A dealer accepts the old item when you buy a new one and credits its value: 車を下取りに出す.","examples":[{"japanese":"新車を買うとき、古い車を下取りに出した。","romaji":"Shinsha o kau toki, furui kuruma o shitadori ni dashita.","german":"When I bought a new car, I traded in my old one.","furigana":"しんしゃ|か|ふる|くるま|したど|だ"},{"japanese":"この店では古いスマホを下取りしてくれる。","romaji":"Kono mise de wa furui sumaho o shitadori shite kureru.","german":"This store takes old smartphones as trade-ins.","furigana":"みせ|ふる|したど"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A fire that is weakening, and figuratively anything that is subsiding: ブームが下火になる (the boom fades).","examples":[{"japanese":"一時期のブームも、今ではすっかり下火になった。","romaji":"Ichijiki no būmu mo, ima de wa sukkari shitabi ni natta.","german":"The once-popular boom has completely died down by now.","furigana":"いちじき|いま|したび"},{"japanese":"夜になって、山火事はようやく下火になった。","romaji":"Yoru ni natte, yamakaji wa yōyaku shitabi ni natta.","german":"By night, the forest fire finally subsided.","furigana":"よる|やまかじ|したび"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The house of one's parents, where one grew up: 実家に帰る (to go home to one's parents). For married people, also the home they lived in before marriage.","examples":[{"japanese":"お正月は実家に帰ります。","romaji":"Oshōgatsu wa jikka ni kaerimasu.","german":"I'm going back to my parents' home for New Year.","furigana":"しょうがつ|じっか|かえ"},{"japanese":"実家から野菜が送られてきた。","romaji":"Jikka kara yasai ga okurarete kita.","german":"A package of vegetables came from my parents' home.","furigana":"じっか|やさい|おく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"In sports: to be disqualified (反則で失格になる). Also a verdict on someone: 親として失格だ (a failure as a parent).","examples":[{"japanese":"その選手はフライングで失格になった。","romaji":"Sono senshu wa furaingu de shikkaku ni natta.","german":"That athlete was disqualified for a false start.","furigana":"せんしゅ|しっかく"},{"japanese":"約束を守れないようでは、リーダーとして失格だ。","romaji":"Yakusoku o mamorenai yō de wa, rīdā to shite shikkaku da.","german":"If you can't keep your promises, you are unfit as a leader.","furigana":"やくそく|まも|しっかく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The loss of a position of power, mostly in politics or business: スキャンダルで失脚する.","examples":[{"japanese":"大臣は汚職事件で失脚した。","romaji":"Daijin wa oshoku jiken de shikkyaku shita.","german":"The minister fell from power in a corruption scandal.","furigana":"だいじん|おしょくじけん|しっきゃく"},{"japanese":"ライバルの失脚を狙う者もいた。","romaji":"Raibaru no shikkyaku o nerau mono mo ita.","german":"Some people were aiming for their rival's downfall.","furigana":"しっきゃく|ねら|もの"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Someone who runs or has founded a company. 実業 refers to commerce and industry as opposed to science or politics.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は若くして成功した実業家だ。","romaji":"Kare wa wakakushite seikō shita jitsugyōka da.","german":"He is an entrepreneur who succeeded at a young age.","furigana":"かれ|わか|せいこう|じつぎょうか"},{"japanese":"祖父は町で有名な実業家だった。","romaji":"Sofu wa machi de yūmei na jitsugyōka datta.","german":"My grandfather was a well-known businessman in town.","furigana":"そふ|まち|ゆうめい|じつぎょうか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"な-adjective from French \"chic\": シックな服 (elegant clothes), often for subdued, stylish colors.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女はいつもシックな服を着ている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa itsumo shikku na fuku o kite iru.","german":"She always wears chic clothes.","furigana":"かのじょ|ふく|き"},{"japanese":"部屋を黒と白でシックにまとめた。","romaji":"Heya o kuro to shiro de shikku ni matometa.","german":"I decorated the room in black and white in a stylish way.","furigana":"へや|くろ|しろ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Ichidan verb, mostly written in kana しつける: 子どもをしつける, 犬をしつける. Noun: 躾（しつけ）.","examples":[{"japanese":"子どもをきちんと躾けるのは親の役目だ。","romaji":"Kodomo o kichinto shitsukeru no wa oya no yakume da.","german":"It is the parents' job to raise children properly.","furigana":"こ|しつ|おや|やくめ"},{"japanese":"この犬はよく躾けられている。","romaji":"Kono inu wa yoku shitsukerarete iru.","german":"This dog is well trained.","furigana":"いぬ|しつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mostly in compounds: 栄養失調 (malnutrition), 自律神経失調症 (autonomic dysfunction).","examples":[{"japanese":"ストレスが続いて、自律神経の失調に悩んでいる。","romaji":"Sutoresu ga tsuzuite, jiritsu shinkei no shitchō ni nayande iru.","german":"Due to ongoing stress, I am suffering from autonomic nervous system dysfunction.","furigana":"つづ|じりつしんけい|しっちょう|なや"},{"japanese":"無理なダイエットは栄養失調を招く。","romaji":"Muri na daietto wa eiyō shitchō o maneku.","german":"An extreme diet leads to malnutrition.","furigana":"むり|えいようしっちょう|まね"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Prenominal word (連体詞): 実の親 (biological parents), 実の兄弟 (blood siblings). Also \"truly\": 実のところ (in truth).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は実の母親に二十年ぶりに会った。","romaji":"Kanojo wa jitsu no hahaoya ni nijūnenburi ni atta.","german":"She met her birth mother for the first time in twenty years.","furigana":"かのじょ|じつ|ははおや|にじゅうねん|あ"},{"japanese":"実のところ、私もよく分かっていないんです。","romaji":"Jitsu no tokoro, watashi mo yoku wakatte inai n desu.","german":"To tell the truth, I don't really understand either.","furigana":"じつ|わたし|わ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"The turning of a celestial body around its own axis; its movement around the sun is called 公転. Not to be confused with 自転車.","examples":[{"japanese":"地球は一日に一回自転している。","romaji":"Chikyū wa ichinichi ni ikkai jiten shite iru.","german":"The Earth rotates once a day.","furigana":"ちきゅう|いちにち|いっかいじてん"},{"japanese":"昼と夜ができるのは地球の自転のためだ。","romaji":"Hiru to yoru ga dekiru no wa chikyū no jiten no tame da.","german":"Day and night occur because of the Earth's rotation.","furigana":"ひる|よる|ちきゅう|じてん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Seat in vehicles and at events: シートベルト (seat belt), チャイルドシート (child seat). Homophone: シート (sheet; tarp; sheet of paper).","examples":[{"japanese":"車のシートを少し後ろに下げた。","romaji":"Kuruma no shīto o sukoshi ushiro ni sageta.","german":"I moved the car seat back a little.","furigana":"くるま|すこ|うし|さ"},{"japanese":"このバスのシートは広くて座りやすい。","romaji":"Kono basu no shīto wa hirokute suwariyasui.","german":"The seats on this bus are wide and comfortable to sit in.","furigana":"ひろ|すわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Ichidan verb, usually written in kana: vegetables or skin lose moisture and become wrinkled (野菜がしなびる).","examples":[{"japanese":"冷蔵庫に入れ忘れたキュウリが萎びてしまった。","romaji":"Reizōko ni irewasureta kyūri ga shinabite shimatta.","german":"The cucumber I forgot to put in the fridge has shriveled up.","furigana":"れいぞうこ|い|わす|しな"},{"japanese":"水をやらなかったので、花が萎びている。","romaji":"Mizu o yaranakatta node, hana ga shinabite iru.","german":"Because I didn't water them, the flowers have withered.","furigana":"みず|はな|しな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"な-adjective: elastic and not brittle (しなやかな枝) or graceful in movement (しなやかな動き).","examples":[{"japanese":"竹はしなやかで、強い風でも折れない。","romaji":"Take wa shinayaka de, tsuyoi kaze demo orenai.","german":"Bamboo is flexible and does not break even in strong wind.","furigana":"たけ|つよ|かぜ|お"},{"japanese":"ダンサーのしなやかな動きに見とれた。","romaji":"Dansā no shinayaka na ugoki ni mitoreta.","german":"I was captivated by the dancer's graceful movements.","furigana":"うご|み"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The script of a film or play, and figuratively a planned course of events: 最悪のシナリオ (worst-case scenario).","examples":[{"japanese":"この映画のシナリオは有名な作家が書いた。","romaji":"Kono eiga no shinario wa yūmei na sakka ga kaita.","german":"The screenplay of this film was written by a famous author.","furigana":"えいが|ゆうめい|さっか|か"},{"japanese":"最悪のシナリオも考えておく必要がある。","romaji":"Saiaku no shinario mo kangaete oku hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"We need to consider the worst-case scenario as well.","furigana":"さいあく|かんが|ひつよう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The owner of a plot of land, which he often leases out. The landlord of an apartment is called 大家.","examples":[{"japanese":"この辺りの土地は一人の地主が持っている。","romaji":"Kono atari no tochi wa hitori no jinushi ga motte iru.","german":"A single landowner owns the land around here.","furigana":"あた|とち|ひとり|じぬし|も"},{"japanese":"地主の許可を得て、畑を借りた。","romaji":"Jinushi no kyoka o ete, hatake o karita.","german":"I rented a field with the landowner's permission.","furigana":"じぬし|きょか|え|はたけ|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The lawn grass itself; the lawn area is usually called 芝生. 芝を刈る (to mow the lawn).","examples":[{"japanese":"庭の芝を刈るのは父の仕事だ。","romaji":"Niwa no shiba o karu no wa chichi no shigoto da.","german":"Mowing the lawn in the garden is my father's job.","furigana":"にわ|しば|か|ちち|しごと"},{"japanese":"競馬場の芝がきれいに整えられている。","romaji":"Keibajō no shiba ga kirei ni totonoerarete iru.","german":"The turf at the racecourse is neatly kept.","furigana":"けいばじょう|しば|ととの"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Japanese short form of \"jeans pants\"; today ジーンズ or デニム are also common.","examples":[{"japanese":"休みの日はたいていジーパンをはいている。","romaji":"Yasumi no hi wa taitei jīpan o haite iru.","german":"On days off I usually wear jeans.","furigana":"やす|ひ"},{"japanese":"このジーパンは十年前に買ったものだ。","romaji":"Kono jīpan wa jūnen mae ni katta mono da.","german":"I bought these jeans ten years ago.","furigana":"じゅうねんまえ|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Short for 耳鼻咽喉科. Responsible for ears, nose and throat, e.g. for hay fever.","examples":[{"japanese":"花粉症がひどいので、耳鼻科に行った。","romaji":"Kafunshō ga hidoi node, jibika ni itta.","german":"My hay fever is bad, so I went to the ENT doctor.","furigana":"かふんしょう|じびか|い"},{"japanese":"耳が痛いなら、耳鼻科で診てもらったほうがいい。","romaji":"Mimi ga itai nara, jibika de mite moratta hō ga ii.","german":"If your ear hurts, you should have it looked at by an ENT doctor.","furigana":"みみ|いた|じびか|み"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Personal items as opposed to company or school property: 私物を持ち込む (to bring personal items in).","examples":[{"japanese":"会社のパソコンに私物のデータを保存しないでください。","romaji":"Kaisha no pasokon ni shibutsu no dēta o hozon shinaide kudasai.","german":"Please do not save personal data on the company computer.","furigana":"かいしゃ|しぶつ|ほぞん"},{"japanese":"退職する前に、机の私物を片付けた。","romaji":"Taishoku suru mae ni, tsukue no shibutsu o katazuketa.","german":"Before I quit, I cleared my personal belongings from my desk.","furigana":"たいしょく|まえ|つくえ|しぶつ|かたづ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"い-adjective: someone who does not give up or is hard to beat, sometimes praising, sometimes derogatory (しぶとい相手).","examples":[{"japanese":"相手チームは最後までしぶとく戦った。","romaji":"Aite chīmu wa saigo made shibutoku tatakatta.","german":"The opposing team fought tenaciously to the end.","furigana":"あいて|さいご|たたか"},{"japanese":"この雑草はしぶとくて、抜いてもまた生えてくる。","romaji":"Kono zassō wa shibutokute, nuite mo mata haete kuru.","german":"This weed is tenacious and grows back even after I pull it out.","furigana":"ざっそう|ぬ|は"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"An important task one must fulfill: 使命を果たす (to fulfill one's mission), 使命感 (sense of mission). Homophones: 氏名, 指名.","examples":[{"japanese":"医者の使命は患者の命を救うことだ。","romaji":"Isha no shimei wa kanja no inochi o sukuu koto da.","german":"A doctor's mission is to save patients' lives.","furigana":"いしゃ|しめい|かんじゃ|いのち|すく"},{"japanese":"彼は強い使命感を持って仕事をしている。","romaji":"Kare wa tsuyoi shimeikan o motte shigoto o shite iru.","german":"He works with a strong sense of mission.","furigana":"かれ|つよ|しめいかん|も|しごと"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Dealing with other people in society: 社交的 (sociable), 社交辞令 (polite formality), 社交ダンス (ballroom dancing).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は社交が苦手で、パーティーにはあまり行かない。","romaji":"Kare wa shakō ga nigate de, pātī ni wa amari ikanai.","german":"He is bad at socializing and rarely goes to parties.","furigana":"かれ|しゃこう|にがて|い"},{"japanese":"仕事の上では社交も大切だ。","romaji":"Shigoto no ue de wa shakō mo taisetsu da.","german":"Socializing is also important in business.","furigana":"しごと|うえ|しゃこう|たいせつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A genre of music; ジャズ喫茶 are cafes in Japan where jazz records are played.","examples":[{"japanese":"週末はジャズのライブを聴きに行く。","romaji":"Shūmatsu wa jazu no raibu o kiki ni iku.","german":"On the weekend I'm going to a live jazz show.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|き|い"},{"japanese":"父は若いころからジャズが好きだ。","romaji":"Chichi wa wakai koro kara jazu ga suki da.","german":"My father has liked jazz since his youth.","furigana":"ちち|わか|す"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 9 (source: N5; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"Formal refusal, known from 面会謝絶 (no visitors allowed in a hospital).","examples":[{"japanese":"祖父は今、面会謝絶の状態だ。","romaji":"Sofu wa ima, menkai shazetsu no jōtai da.","german":"My grandfather is currently not allowed any visitors.","furigana":"そふ|いま|めんかいしゃぜつ|じょうたい"},{"japanese":"取材の申し込みを丁重に謝絶した。","romaji":"Shuzai no mōshikomi o teichō ni shazetsu shita.","german":"I politely declined the interview request.","furigana":"しゅざい|もう|こ|ていちょう|しゃぜつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Housing that a company provides to its employees at a low price.","examples":[{"japanese":"転勤してから、家族で社宅に住んでいる。","romaji":"Tenkin shite kara, kazoku de shataku ni sunde iru.","german":"Since my transfer, my family and I have been living in company housing.","furigana":"てんきん|かぞく|しゃたく|す"},{"japanese":"社宅は家賃が安いが、近所に同僚が多い。","romaji":"Shataku wa yachin ga yasui ga, kinjo ni dōryō ga ōi.","german":"Company housing has cheap rent, but many colleagues live in the neighborhood.","furigana":"しゃたく|やちん|やす|きんじょ|どうりょう|おお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Ichidan verb, mostly in the form しゃれた (stylish, tasteful): しゃれた店 (a stylish shop). Less often \"to make a pun\". Noun: 洒落.","examples":[{"japanese":"駅前にしゃれたカフェができた。","romaji":"Ekimae ni shareta kafe ga dekita.","german":"A stylish cafe has opened in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ"},{"japanese":"彼はしゃれた言い方で場を和ませた。","romaji":"Kare wa shareta iikata de ba o nagomaseta.","german":"He lightened the mood with a witty remark.","furigana":"かれ|い|かた|ば|なご"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"In Japanese, a short jacket, not the British \"jumper\" (pullover). In sports also \"ski jumper\".","examples":[{"japanese":"寒いので、ジャンパーを着て出かけた。","romaji":"Samui node, janpā o kite dekaketa.","german":"It was cold, so I put on a jacket and went out.","furigana":"さむ|き|で"},{"japanese":"作業用のジャンパーは会社から支給される。","romaji":"Sagyōyō no janpā wa kaisha kara shikyū sareru.","german":"Work jackets are issued by the company.","furigana":"さぎょうよう|かいしゃ|しきゅう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A jump; as a verb ジャンプする. In sports also the discipline of ski jumping (スキージャンプ).","examples":[{"japanese":"猫は高いところまでジャンプした。","romaji":"Neko wa takai tokoro made janpu shita.","german":"The cat jumped up to a high place.","furigana":"ねこ|たか"},{"japanese":"彼はジャンプの大会で優勝した。","romaji":"Kare wa janpu no taikai de yūshō shita.","german":"He won the jumping competition.","furigana":"かれ|たいかい|ゆうしょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mostly used as a prefix: ジャンボサイズ (jumbo size), ジャンボ宝くじ (jumbo lottery); also short for the Boeing 747 (ジャンボジェット).","examples":[{"japanese":"この店のジャンボパフェは三人で食べても多い。","romaji":"Kono mise no janbo pafe wa sannin de tabete mo ōi.","german":"This shop's jumbo parfait is too much even for three people.","furigana":"みせ|さんにん|た|おお"},{"japanese":"昔はジャンボに乗って海外へ行ったものだ。","romaji":"Mukashi wa janbo ni notte kaigai e itta mono da.","german":"In the old days, people used to fly abroad on a jumbo jet.","furigana":"むかし|の|かいがい|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A category of music, literature or film: 好きなジャンル (favorite genre), ジャンルを問わず (regardless of genre).","examples":[{"japanese":"どんなジャンルの音楽をよく聴きますか。","romaji":"Donna janru no ongaku o yoku kikimasu ka.","german":"What genre of music do you often listen to?","furigana":"おんがく|き"},{"japanese":"この書店はジャンルごとに本が並べてある。","romaji":"Kono shoten wa janru goto ni hon ga narabete aru.","german":"This bookstore arranges its books by genre.","furigana":"しょてん|ほん|なら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Legal usage: 私有地 (private land), 私有財産 (private property). Opposites: 公有 (public ownership), 国有 (state ownership).","examples":[{"japanese":"ここから先は私有地なので、入らないでください。","romaji":"Koko kara saki wa shiyūchi na node, hairanaide kudasai.","german":"Beyond this point is private land, so please do not enter.","furigana":"さき|しゆうち|はい"},{"japanese":"土地の私有を認めない国もある。","romaji":"Tochi no shiyū o mitomenai kuni mo aru.","german":"Some countries do not recognize private ownership of land.","furigana":"とち|しゆう|みと|くに"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Learning knowledge or skills, formal: 修業年限 (course length), 修業証書 (completion certificate). In the sense of a craft apprenticeship it is usually read しゅぎょう (料理の修業). Not to be confused with 修行（しゅぎょう）: religious austerities or martial arts training.","examples":[{"japanese":"この学校の修業年限は二年だ。","romaji":"Kono gakkō no shūgyō nengen wa ninen da.","german":"The course length at this school is two years.","furigana":"がっこう|しゅうぎょうねんげん|にねん"},{"japanese":"修業を終えた生徒に証書が渡された。","romaji":"Shūgyō o oeta seito ni shōsho ga watasareta.","german":"Certificates were given to the students who had completed their course.","furigana":"しゅうぎょう|お|せいと|しょうしょ|わた"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"All the people who work at a business: 従業員数 (number of employees). More general than 社員.","examples":[{"japanese":"この工場には五百人の従業員がいる。","romaji":"Kono kōjō ni wa gohyakunin no jūgyōin ga iru.","german":"This factory has five hundred employees.","furigana":"こうじょう|ごひゃくにん|じゅうぎょういん"},{"japanese":"従業員専用の入り口から入ってください。","romaji":"Jūgyōin sen'yō no iriguchi kara haitte kudasai.","german":"Please enter through the staff-only entrance.","furigana":"じゅうぎょういんせんよう|い|ぐち|はい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Formal for 一日中, typical on signs: 終日禁煙 (no smoking all day), 終日運休 (service suspended all day).","examples":[{"japanese":"この駅は終日禁煙です。","romaji":"Kono eki wa shūjitsu kin'en desu.","german":"Smoking is prohibited all day at this station.","furigana":"えき|しゅうじつきんえん"},{"japanese":"大雪のため、電車は終日運休となった。","romaji":"Ōyuki no tame, densha wa shūjitsu unkyū to natta.","german":"Because of heavy snow, trains were suspended all day.","furigana":"おおゆき|でんしゃ|しゅうじつうんきゅう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Grammatically, one word qualifies another (形容詞が名詞を修飾する); 修飾語 = modifier. Homophone: 就職.","examples":[{"japanese":"この文では、形容詞が名詞を修飾している。","romaji":"Kono bun de wa, keiyōshi ga meishi o shūshoku shite iru.","german":"In this sentence, the adjective modifies the noun.","furigana":"ぶん|けいようし|めいし|しゅうしょく"},{"japanese":"過度な修飾を避けて、簡潔な文章を書こう。","romaji":"Kado na shūshoku o sakete, kanketsu na bunshō o kakō.","german":"Let's avoid excessive embellishment and write concisely.","furigana":"かど|しゅうしょく|さ|かんけつ|ぶんしょう|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A cross-shaped road intersection; figuratively a turning point: 人生の十字路 (a crossroads in life).","examples":[{"japanese":"次の十字路を右に曲がってください。","romaji":"Tsugi no jūjiro o migi ni magatte kudasai.","german":"Turn right at the next intersection.","furigana":"つぎ|じゅうじろ|みぎ|ま"},{"japanese":"私は今、人生の十字路に立っている。","romaji":"Watashi wa ima, jinsei no jūjiro ni tatte iru.","german":"I am now at a crossroads in my life.","furigana":"わたし|いま|じんせい|じゅうじろ|た"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To take in people or things: 五万人収容のスタジアム (a stadium holding 50,000 people); also placement in camps or hospitals.","examples":[{"japanese":"このホールは千人を収容できる。","romaji":"Kono hōru wa sennin o shūyō dekiru.","german":"This hall can hold a thousand people.","furigana":"せんにん|しゅうよう"},{"japanese":"けが人は近くの病院に収容された。","romaji":"Keganin wa chikaku no byōin ni shūyō sareta.","german":"The injured were taken to a nearby hospital.","furigana":"にん|ちか|びょういん|しゅうよう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Security staff at the entrance of companies, schools or government offices: 守衛室 (guard room).","examples":[{"japanese":"夜は守衛が建物を見回っている。","romaji":"Yoru wa shuei ga tatemono o mimawatte iru.","german":"At night a guard patrols the building.","furigana":"よる|しゅえい|たてもの|みまわ"},{"japanese":"入館するときは、守衛に名前を伝えてください。","romaji":"Nyūkan suru toki wa, shuei ni namae o tsutaete kudasai.","german":"When entering the building, please tell the guard your name.","furigana":"にゅうかん|しゅえい|なまえ|つた"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To play the lead in a film or play: 主演する, 主演女優 (leading actress).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は初めて映画で主演した。","romaji":"Kanojo wa hajimete eiga de shuen shita.","german":"She played the lead in a film for the first time.","furigana":"かのじょ|はじ|えいが|しゅえん"},{"japanese":"この作品で主演を務めたのは新人の俳優だ。","romaji":"Kono sakuhin de shuen o tsutometa no wa shinjin no haiyū da.","german":"The lead role in this work was played by a newcomer actor.","furigana":"さくひん|しゅえん|つと|しんじん|はいゆう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Formal: 祝賀会 (celebration party), 祝賀パレード (victory parade).","examples":[{"japanese":"優勝を祝って、祝賀会が開かれた。","romaji":"Yūshō o iwatte, shukugakai ga hirakareta.","german":"A celebration was held in honor of the victory.","furigana":"ゆうしょう|いわ|しゅくがかい|ひら"},{"japanese":"創立百周年を祝賀して、記念式典が行われた。","romaji":"Sōritsu hyakushūnen o shukuga shite, kinen shikiten ga okonawareta.","german":"A commemorative ceremony was held to celebrate the 100th anniversary of the founding.","furigana":"そうりつひゃくしゅうねん|しゅくが|きねんしきてん|おこな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"An unchangeable, predetermined fate; stronger than 運命: 宿命のライバル (arch-rival).","examples":[{"japanese":"二人は宿命のライバルと呼ばれている。","romaji":"Futari wa shukumei no raibaru to yobarete iru.","german":"The two are called fated rivals.","furigana":"ふたり|しゅくめい|よ"},{"japanese":"それが彼に与えられた宿命だった。","romaji":"Sore ga kare ni ataerareta shukumei datta.","german":"That was the destiny given to him.","furigana":"かれ|あた|しゅくめい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Handicrafts such as knitting, sewing or embroidery: 手芸店 (craft shop).","examples":[{"japanese":"母は手芸が趣味で、よくバッグを作っている。","romaji":"Haha wa shugei ga shumi de, yoku baggu o tsukutte iru.","german":"My mother's hobby is handicrafts, and she often makes bags.","furigana":"はは|しゅげい|しゅみ|つく"},{"japanese":"手芸店で毛糸と針を買った。","romaji":"Shugeiten de keito to hari o katta.","german":"I bought yarn and needles at the craft shop.","furigana":"しゅげいてん|けいと|はり|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Literary for いろいろ: 種々の問題 (various problems), 種々雑多 (all sorts of).","examples":[{"japanese":"会議では種々の問題が話し合われた。","romaji":"Kaigi de wa shuju no mondai ga hanashiawareta.","german":"Various problems were discussed at the meeting.","furigana":"かいぎ|しゅじゅ|もんだい|はな|あ"},{"japanese":"この店では種々の文房具を扱っている。","romaji":"Kono mise de wa shuju no bunbōgu o atsukatte iru.","german":"This shop handles all kinds of stationery.","furigana":"みせ|しゅじゅ|ぶんぼうぐ|あつか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The main food of a meal or culture, traditionally rice in Japan; side dishes are called おかず.","examples":[{"japanese":"日本人の主食は米だ。","romaji":"Nihonjin no shushoku wa kome da.","german":"The staple food of the Japanese is rice.","furigana":"にほんじん|しゅしょく|こめ"},{"japanese":"朝はパンを主食にしている。","romaji":"Asa wa pan o shushoku ni shite iru.","german":"In the morning I have bread as my staple.","furigana":"あさ|しゅしょく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The main person of a story; figuratively: 自分の人生の主人公 (the master of one's own life).","examples":[{"japanese":"この小説の主人公は十六歳の少女だ。","romaji":"Kono shōsetsu no shujinkō wa jūrokusai no shōjo da.","german":"The protagonist of this novel is a sixteen-year-old girl.","furigana":"しょうせつ|しゅじんこう|じゅうろくさい|しょうじょ"},{"japanese":"主人公の気持ちになって読んでみよう。","romaji":"Shujinkō no kimochi ni natte yonde miyō.","german":"Let's try reading while putting ourselves in the protagonist's shoes.","furigana":"しゅじんこう|きも|よ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 27 (source: N4; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"To appear in film, television or on stage: テレビに出演する (to appear on TV), 出演者 (performers).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は昨日のテレビ番組に出演していた。","romaji":"Kare wa kinō no terebi bangumi ni shutsuen shite ita.","german":"He appeared on yesterday's TV program.","furigana":"かれ|きのう|ばんぐみ|しゅつえん"},{"japanese":"舞台の出演者が全員あいさつした。","romaji":"Butai no shutsuensha ga zen'in aisatsu shita.","german":"All the performers in the production greeted the audience.","furigana":"ぶたい|しゅつえんしゃ|ぜんいん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The (sudden) appearance of something new: スマホの出現で生活が変わった (life changed with the emergence of smartphones).","examples":[{"japanese":"インターネットの出現で、生活は大きく変わった。","romaji":"Intānetto no shutsugen de, seikatsu wa ōkiku kawatta.","german":"The advent of the internet greatly changed life.","furigana":"しゅつげん|せいかつ|おお|か"},{"japanese":"山道にクマが出現したので注意してください。","romaji":"Yamamichi ni kuma ga shutsugen shita node chūi shite kudasai.","german":"A bear has appeared on the mountain trail, so please be careful.","furigana":"やまみち|しゅつげん|ちゅうい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To give birth to a child: 出産する, 出産予定日 (due date). Congratulations: ご出産おめでとうございます.","examples":[{"japanese":"姉は先月、女の子を出産した。","romaji":"Ane wa sengetsu, onna no ko o shussan shita.","german":"My older sister gave birth to a girl last month.","furigana":"あね|せんげつ|おんな|こ|しゅっさん"},{"japanese":"出産予定日は来年の三月だ。","romaji":"Shussan yoteibi wa rainen no sangatsu da.","german":"The due date is in March next year.","furigana":"しゅっさんよていび|らいねん|さんがつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Official, factual: 出生率 (birth rate), 出生届 (birth registration). Also read しゅっせい.","examples":[{"japanese":"日本の出生率は年々下がっている。","romaji":"Nihon no shusshōritsu wa nennen sagatte iru.","german":"Japan's birth rate is falling year by year.","furigana":"にほん|しゅっしょうりつ|ねんねんさ"},{"japanese":"彼の出生については、よく知られていない。","romaji":"Kare no shusshō ni tsuite wa, yoku shirarete inai.","german":"Little is known about his origins.","furigana":"かれ|しゅっしょう|し"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To rise in society or in a company: 出世する, 出世が早い (to rise quickly). 出世払い = to pay back once one has made it.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は同期の中で一番早く出世した。","romaji":"Kare wa dōki no naka de ichiban hayaku shusse shita.","german":"He was promoted the fastest among his peers.","furigana":"かれ|どうき|なか|いちばんはや|しゅっせ"},{"japanese":"出世よりも家族との時間を大切にしたい。","romaji":"Shusse yori mo kazoku to no jikan o taisetsu ni shitai.","german":"I value time with my family more than career advancement.","furigana":"しゅっせ|かぞく|じかん|たいせつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To set exam questions: 試験に出題される (to appear on the exam), 出題範囲 (exam coverage).","examples":[{"japanese":"この問題は去年の試験にも出題された。","romaji":"Kono mondai wa kyonen no shiken ni mo shutsudai sareta.","german":"This question was also on last year's exam.","furigana":"もんだい|きょねん|しけん|しゅつだい"},{"japanese":"先生に出題範囲を確認した。","romaji":"Sensei ni shutsudai han'i o kakunin shita.","german":"I checked with the teacher what the exam covers.","furigana":"せんせい|しゅつだいはんい|かくにん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Fire fighters, police or the military go out on a mission: 消防車が出動する (the fire engine turns out).","examples":[{"japanese":"火事の通報を受けて、消防車が出動した。","romaji":"Kaji no tsūhō o ukete, shōbōsha ga shutsudō shita.","german":"Following the fire report, the fire engine was dispatched.","furigana":"かじ|つうほう|う|しょうぼうしゃ|しゅつどう"},{"japanese":"災害の現場に救助隊が出動している。","romaji":"Saigai no genba ni kyūjotai ga shutsudō shite iru.","german":"Rescue teams have been dispatched to the disaster site.","furigana":"さいがい|げんば|きゅうじょたい|しゅつどう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To submit a work to an exhibition or to offer goods for sale, e.g. on an auction site.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼の絵が展覧会に出品された。","romaji":"Kare no e ga tenrankai ni shuppin sareta.","german":"His painting was entered in the exhibition.","furigana":"かれ|え|てんらんかい|しゅっぴん"},{"japanese":"使わなくなった服をフリマアプリに出品した。","romaji":"Tsukawanaku natta fuku o furima apuri ni shuppin shita.","german":"I listed clothes I no longer wear on a flea market app.","furigana":"つか|ふく|しゅっぴん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The leading role in an undertaking: 主導権を握る (to seize the initiative), 政府主導の計画 (a government-led plan).","examples":[{"japanese":"この計画は政府の主導で進められている。","romaji":"Kono keikaku wa seifu no shudō de susumerarete iru.","german":"This plan is being carried out under the government's leadership.","furigana":"けいかく|せいふ|しゅどう|すす"},{"japanese":"試合の主導権を握ったのは相手チームだった。","romaji":"Shiai no shudōken o nigitta no wa aite chīmu datta.","german":"It was the opposing team that took the initiative in the match.","furigana":"しあい|しゅどうけん|にぎ|あいて"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The person responsible for an area: 学年主任 (grade-level head), 現場主任 (site supervisor). In companies often one rank below 係長.","examples":[{"japanese":"困ったことがあれば、主任に相談してください。","romaji":"Komatta koto ga areba, shunin ni sōdan shite kudasai.","german":"If you have any problems, please consult the supervisor.","furigana":"こま|しゅにん|そうだん"},{"japanese":"彼女は今年から学年主任を務めている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kotoshi kara gakunen shunin o tsutomete iru.","german":"She has been the grade-level head since this year.","furigana":"かのじょ|ことし|がくねんしゅにん|つと"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Defense in sports or the military: 守備を固める (to strengthen the defense); in baseball, fielding (守備位置 = fielding position).","examples":[{"japanese":"このチームは守備が強い。","romaji":"Kono chīmu wa shubi ga tsuyoi.","german":"This team has strong defense.","furigana":"しゅび|つよ"},{"japanese":"彼は打つのは苦手だが、守備はうまい。","romaji":"Kare wa utsu no wa nigate da ga, shubi wa umai.","german":"He is poor at batting but good in the field.","furigana":"かれ|う|にがて|しゅび"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Neutral term for trees in general, e.g. in science or urban planning: 樹木を植える (to plant trees).","examples":[{"japanese":"公園にはさまざまな樹木が植えられている。","romaji":"Kōen ni wa samazama na jumoku ga uerarete iru.","german":"All kinds of trees are planted in the park.","furigana":"こうえん|じゅもく|う"},{"japanese":"台風で多くの樹木が倒れた。","romaji":"Taifū de ōku no jumoku ga taoreta.","german":"Many trees were knocked down by the typhoon.","furigana":"たいふう|おお|じゅもく|たお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To create something lasting: 政権を樹立する (to establish a government), 新記録を樹立する (to set a new record).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は百メートルで日本新記録を樹立した。","romaji":"Kanojo wa hyaku mētoru de Nihon shinkiroku o juritsu shita.","german":"She set a new Japanese record in the 100 meters.","furigana":"かのじょ|ひゃく|にほんしんきろく|じゅりつ"},{"japanese":"戦後、新しい政府が樹立された。","romaji":"Sengo, atarashii seifu ga juritsu sareta.","german":"After the war, a new government was established.","furigana":"せんご|あたら|せいふ|じゅりつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Short for 準急行: stops more often than an 急行 but less often than a 各駅停車. Train types differ by railway company.","examples":[{"japanese":"準急は各駅停車より早く着く。","romaji":"Junkyū wa kakueki teisha yori hayaku tsuku.","german":"The semi-express arrives earlier than the local train.","furigana":"じゅんきゅう|かくえきていしゃ|はや|つ"},{"japanese":"この駅は準急が止まらない。","romaji":"Kono eki wa junkyū ga tomaranai.","german":"The semi-express does not stop at this station.","furigana":"えき|じゅんきゅう|と"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Literary Ichidan verb, mostly 〜に準じる/〜に準じて (in accordance with, according to): 規則に準じて処理する (to handle according to the rules). Also 準ずる.","examples":[{"japanese":"詳しい手続きは、本規則に準じて行う。","romaji":"Kuwashii tetsuzuki wa, hon kisoku ni junjite okonau.","german":"Detailed procedures are carried out in accordance with these rules.","furigana":"くわ|てつづ|ほんきそく|じゅん|おこな"},{"japanese":"パートの社員にも正社員に準じた手当が出る。","romaji":"Pāto no shain ni mo seishain ni junjita teate ga deru.","german":"Part-time employees also receive allowances equivalent to those of regular employees.","furigana":"しゃいん|せいしゃいん|じゅん|てあて|で"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Literary; same meaning as 準じる, but conjugated like a ずる verb. Common in regulations: これに準ずる (equivalent to this).","examples":[{"japanese":"優勝者またはそれに準ずる成績の者に賞を与える。","romaji":"Yūshōsha mata wa sore ni junzuru seiseki no mono ni shō o ataeru.","german":"A prize will be given to the winner or to those with results equivalent to that.","furigana":"ゆうしょうしゃ|じゅん|せいせき|もの|しょう|あた"},{"japanese":"この資格は大学卒業に準ずるものとして扱われる。","romaji":"Kono shikaku wa daigaku sotsugyō ni junzuru mono to shite atsukawareru.","german":"This qualification is treated as equivalent to a university degree.","furigana":"しかく|だいがくそつぎょう|じゅん|あつか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A performance or trade show: ファッションショー (fashion show), モーターショー (motor show), イルカのショー (dolphin show).","examples":[{"japanese":"水族館でイルカのショーを見た。","romaji":"Suizokukan de iruka no shō o mita.","german":"I saw a dolphin show at the aquarium.","furigana":"すいぞくかん|み"},{"japanese":"来月、東京でモーターショーが開かれる。","romaji":"Raigetsu, Tōkyō de mōtā shō ga hirakareru.","german":"A motor show will be held in Tokyo next month.","furigana":"らいげつ|とうきょう|ひら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"In a work context: 私用で休む (to take time off for personal reasons), 私用の電話 (a private call). Homophones: 使用, 仕様.","examples":[{"japanese":"私用で申し訳ありませんが、明日は休ませていただきます。","romaji":"Shiyō de mōshiwake arimasen ga, ashita wa yasumasete itadakimasu.","german":"I'm sorry to say it's a personal matter, but I will be taking tomorrow off.","furigana":"しよう|もう|わけ|あした|やす"},{"japanese":"会社の電話を私用で使ってはいけない。","romaji":"Kaisha no denwa o shiyō de tsukatte wa ikenai.","german":"You must not use the company phone for private purposes.","furigana":"かいしゃ|でんわ|しよう|つか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Warm feelings and sympathy: 情が深い (warm-hearted), 情が移る (to grow attached), 情に流される (to be swayed by emotion).","examples":[{"japanese":"長く飼っていると、どんな動物にも情が移る。","romaji":"Nagaku katte iru to, donna dōbutsu ni mo jō ga utsuru.","german":"If you keep an animal for a long time, you grow attached to it, whatever it is.","furigana":"なが|か|どうぶつ|じょう|うつ"},{"japanese":"彼は情に厚く、困っている人を放っておけない。","romaji":"Kare wa jō ni atsuku, komatte iru hito o hōtte okenai.","german":"He is warm-hearted and cannot leave people in trouble alone.","furigana":"かれ|じょう|あつ|こま|ひと|ほう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A higher place in a ranking: 上位に入る (to place among the top). Opposite: 下位.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼はマラソン大会で上位に入った。","romaji":"Kare wa marason taikai de jōi ni haitta.","german":"He placed high in the marathon.","furigana":"かれ|たいかい|じょうい|はい"},{"japanese":"この商品は人気ランキングの上位にある。","romaji":"Kono shōhin wa ninki rankingu no jōi ni aru.","german":"This product is near the top of the popularity ranking.","furigana":"しょうひん|にんき|じょうい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The area below and around a castle; mostly in 城下町 (castle town). Homophone: 浄化.","examples":[{"japanese":"城下には昔の武家屋敷が残っている。","romaji":"Jōka ni wa mukashi no buke yashiki ga nokotte iru.","german":"Old samurai residences remain in the castle town.","furigana":"じょうか|むかし|ぶけやしき|のこ"},{"japanese":"江戸時代、城下には多くの商人が住んでいた。","romaji":"Edo jidai, jōka ni wa ōku no shōnin ga sunde ita.","german":"In the Edo period, many merchants lived around the castle.","furigana":"えどじだい|じょうか|おお|しょうにん|す"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To delete data or entries: データを消去する; also 消去法 (process of elimination).","examples":[{"japanese":"古いデータをすべて消去した。","romaji":"Furui dēta o subete shōkyo shita.","german":"I deleted all the old data.","furigana":"ふる|しょうきょ"},{"japanese":"分からないときは消去法で答えを選ぶ。","romaji":"Wakaranai toki wa shōkyohō de kotae o erabu.","german":"When I don't know, I choose the answer by process of elimination.","furigana":"わ|しょうきょほう|こた|えら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The sky above a place: 東京の上空 (the sky over Tokyo), 上空の寒気 (cold air aloft).","examples":[{"japanese":"飛行機が町の上空を低く飛んでいった。","romaji":"Hikōki ga machi no jōkū o hikuku tonde itta.","german":"An airplane flew low over the town.","furigana":"ひこうき|まち|じょうくう|ひく|と"},{"japanese":"上空に寒気が入り、雪が降りやすくなっている。","romaji":"Jōkū ni kanki ga hairi, yuki ga furiyasuku natte iru.","german":"Cold air has moved in high up, so it is more likely to snow.","furigana":"じょうくう|かんき|はい|ゆき|ふ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Literary: 〜と称する (to call oneself ..., to claim to be ...): 警察官と称する男 (a man claiming to be a police officer).","examples":[{"japanese":"警察官と称する男から電話がかかってきた。","romaji":"Keisatsukan to shōsuru otoko kara denwa ga kakatte kita.","german":"I got a call from a man claiming to be a police officer.","furigana":"けいさつかん|しょう|おとこ|でんわ"},{"japanese":"この地方では、この祭りを「火祭り」と称している。","romaji":"Kono chihō de wa, kono matsuri o \"himatsuri\" to shōshite iru.","german":"In this region, this festival is called the \"Fire Festival\".","furigana":"ちほう|まつ|ひまつ|しょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A formal decision of an assembly or parliament; as a verb 決議する. Cf. 決定 (decision in general).","examples":[{"japanese":"総会で新しい規則が決議された。","romaji":"Sōkai de atarashii kisoku ga ketsugi sareta.","german":"A new rule was adopted at the general meeting.","furigana":"そうかい|あたら|きそく|けつぎ"},{"japanese":"国連は停戦を求める決議を採択した。","romaji":"Kokuren wa teisen o motomeru ketsugi o saitaku shita.","german":"The UN adopted a resolution calling for a ceasefire.","furigana":"こくれん|ていせん|もと|けつぎ|さいたく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To carry something out resolutely, often despite adverse conditions: 雨天決行 (held rain or shine). As a verb 決行する. Homophone: 結構.","examples":[{"japanese":"雨が降っても、運動会は決行します。","romaji":"Ame ga futte mo, undōkai wa kekkō shimasu.","german":"The sports day will be held even if it rains.","furigana":"あめ|ふ|うんどうかい|けっこう"},{"japanese":"計画の決行は来月に延期された。","romaji":"Keikaku no kekkō wa raigetsu ni enki sareta.","german":"The execution of the plan was postponed to next month.","furigana":"けいかく|けっこう|らいげつ|えんき"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The joining of two or more things, especially in chemistry and technology: 水素と酸素の結合 (the bonding of hydrogen and oxygen). As a verb 結合する.","examples":[{"japanese":"水は水素と酸素が結合してできている。","romaji":"Mizu wa suiso to sanso ga ketsugō shite dekite iru.","german":"Water is formed by hydrogen and oxygen bonding together.","furigana":"みず|すいそ|さんそ|けつごう"},{"japanese":"二つの会社の技術の結合で、新しい製品が生まれた。","romaji":"Futatsu no kaisha no gijutsu no ketsugō de, atarashii seihin ga umareta.","german":"A new product was born from combining the technologies of the two companies.","furigana":"ふた|かいしゃ|ぎじゅつ|けつごう|あたら|せいひん|う"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Monthly fee for private lessons, tutoring or courses (piano, dance, juku).","examples":[{"japanese":"ピアノ教室の月謝は八千円です。","romaji":"Piano kyōshitsu no gessha wa hassen en desu.","german":"Piano lessons cost 8,000 yen a month.","furigana":"きょうしつ|げっしゃ|はっせんえん"},{"japanese":"月謝を封筒に入れて先生に渡した。","romaji":"Gessha o fūtō ni irete sensei ni watashita.","german":"I put the monthly tuition in an envelope and handed it to the teacher.","furigana":"げっしゃ|ふうとう|い|せんせい|わた"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Founding of a party, band, union or club: バンドを結成する (to form a band). For companies 設立 is more usual.","examples":[{"japanese":"高校の友達とバンドを結成した。","romaji":"Kōkō no tomodachi to bando o kessei shita.","german":"I formed a band with friends from high school.","furigana":"こうこう|ともだち|けっせい"},{"japanese":"新しい政党の結成が発表された。","romaji":"Atarashii seitō no kessei ga happyō sareta.","german":"The formation of a new political party was announced.","furigana":"あたら|せいとう|けっせい|はっぴょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Close cohesion of a group: チームの結束が固い (the team is tightly knit). As a verb 結束する (to unite).","examples":[{"japanese":"優勝できたのは、チームの結束が固かったからだ。","romaji":"Yūshō dekita no wa, chīmu no kessoku ga katakatta kara da.","german":"We were able to win because the team was so tightly united.","furigana":"ゆうしょう|けっそく|かた"},{"japanese":"住民が結束して、工場の建設に反対した。","romaji":"Jūmin ga kessoku shite, kōjō no kensetsu ni hantai shita.","german":"The residents united and opposed the construction of the factory.","furigana":"じゅうみん|けっそく|こうじょう|けんせつ|はんたい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Describes someone who has lost a lot of weight (げっそり痩せる) or is completely disheartened (話を聞いてげっそりする).","examples":[{"japanese":"病気のあと、彼はげっそりやせてしまった。","romaji":"Byōki no ato, kare wa gessori yasete shimatta.","german":"After his illness, he became gaunt and thin.","furigana":"びょうき|かれ"},{"japanese":"山のような宿題を見て、げっそりした。","romaji":"Yama no yō na shukudai o mite, gessori shita.","german":"I was discouraged when I saw the mountain of homework.","furigana":"やま|しゅくだい|み"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Payment in monthly installments: 月賦で買う (to buy on installments). Today often ローン or 分割払い. Homophone: げっぷ (burp).","examples":[{"japanese":"車を三年の月賦で買った。","romaji":"Kuruma o sannen no geppu de katta.","german":"I bought the car in monthly installments over three years.","furigana":"くるま|さんねん|げっぷ|か"},{"japanese":"今月の月賦をまだ払っていない。","romaji":"Kongetsu no geppu o mada haratte inai.","german":"I haven't paid this month's installment yet.","furigana":"こんげつ|げっぷ|はら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive godan verb: to kick something away with the foot; figuratively also to flatly reject an offer.","examples":[{"japanese":"子どもが空き缶を蹴飛ばして遊んでいる。","romaji":"Kodomo ga akikan o ketobashite asonde iru.","german":"The child is playing by kicking an empty can away.","furigana":"こ|あ|かん|けと|あそ"},{"japanese":"彼は会社の提案をあっさり蹴飛ばした。","romaji":"Kare wa kaisha no teian o assari ketobashita.","german":"He flatly rejected the company's proposal.","furigana":"かれ|かいしゃ|ていあん|けと"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"い-adjective: full of smoke that stings the eyes; figuratively a person whose presence one finds uncomfortable (煙たい上司).","examples":[{"japanese":"部屋がたばこで煙たい。","romaji":"Heya ga tabako de kemutai.","german":"The room is smoky from cigarettes.","furigana":"へや|けむ"},{"japanese":"厳しい部長は、若い社員には少し煙たい存在だ。","romaji":"Kibishii buchō wa, wakai shain ni wa sukoshi kemutai sonzai da.","german":"The strict department head is somewhat of an intimidating presence for young employees.","furigana":"きび|ぶちょう|わか|しゃいん|すこ|けむ|そんざい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Intransitive godan verb: to give off smoke or to appear as if wrapped in haze: 雨に煙る町 (a town veiled in rain).","examples":[{"japanese":"湿った薪がなかなか燃えずに煙っている。","romaji":"Shimetta maki ga nakanaka moezu ni kemutte iru.","german":"The damp firewood hardly burns and just smolders.","furigana":"しめ|まき|も|けむ"},{"japanese":"雨に煙る京都の町はとても美しかった。","romaji":"Ame ni kemuru Kyōto no machi wa totemo utsukushikatta.","german":"Kyoto, veiled in rain, was very beautiful.","furigana":"あめ|けむ|きょうと|まち|うつく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A furry, four-legged animal: 獣道 (animal trail). As a derogatory term for a cruel person, 獣（けだもの）is more usual.","examples":[{"japanese":"森の奥で獣の鳴き声が聞こえた。","romaji":"Mori no oku de kemono no nakigoe ga kikoeta.","german":"I heard the cry of a beast deep in the forest.","furigana":"もり|おく|けもの|な|ごえ|き"},{"japanese":"雪の上に獣の足跡が残っていた。","romaji":"Yuki no ue ni kemono no ashiato ga nokotte ita.","german":"Animal footprints were left in the snow.","furigana":"ゆき|うえ|けもの|あしあと|のこ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Historically: followers of a lord or samurai; today jocular for someone who serves another. Known from the fairy tale 桃太郎 (the dog, monkey and pheasant become his 家来).","examples":[{"japanese":"桃太郎は犬と猿ときじを家来にした。","romaji":"Momotarō wa inu to saru to kiji o kerai ni shita.","german":"Momotaro made the dog, the monkey and the pheasant his retainers.","furigana":"ももたろう|いぬ|さる|けらい"},{"japanese":"殿様は家来を連れて城を出た。","romaji":"Tonosama wa kerai o tsurete shiro o deta.","german":"The lord left the castle with his retainers.","furigana":"とのさま|けらい|つ|しろ|で"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The matter under discussion, especially in business: その件については後で連絡します (I will contact you about that matter later). As a counter, 〜件 for cases and inquiries.","examples":[{"japanese":"例の件について、少しお話があります。","romaji":"Rei no ken ni tsuite, sukoshi ohanashi ga arimasu.","german":"I'd like to talk to you briefly about that matter.","furigana":"れい|けん|すこ|はなし"},{"japanese":"その件は部長に任せることにした。","romaji":"Sono ken wa buchō ni makaseru koto ni shita.","german":"We decided to leave that matter to the department head.","furigana":"けん|ぶちょう|まか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The original shape of something (原形をとどめない = to be unrecognizable) and, in grammar, the base form of a word. Homophone: 原型 (prototype, model).","examples":[{"japanese":"事故の車は原形をとどめていなかった。","romaji":"Jiko no kuruma wa genkei o todomete inakatta.","german":"The car in the accident was no longer recognizable.","furigana":"じこ|くるま|げんけい"},{"japanese":"この動詞の原形は何ですか。","romaji":"Kono dōshi no genkei wa nan desu ka.","german":"What is the base form of this verb?","furigana":"どうし|げんけい|なん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"What is currently in force or in use: 現行の制度 (current system), 現行法 (current law). Homophone: 原稿 (manuscript).","examples":[{"japanese":"現行の制度では、この申請はできません。","romaji":"Genkō no seido de wa, kono shinsei wa dekimasen.","german":"Under the current system, this application is not possible.","furigana":"げんこう|せいど|しんせい"},{"japanese":"現行の料金は来年四月まで変わりません。","romaji":"Genkō no ryōkin wa rainen shigatsu made kawarimasen.","german":"The current fees will remain unchanged until April next year.","furigana":"げんこう|りょうきん|らいねんしがつ|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Someone is in good health or something still works as before: 両親とも健在です (both my parents are well). Also for things: 昔の人気は健在だ (his old popularity is intact).","examples":[{"japanese":"祖父は九十歳になった今も健在です。","romaji":"Sofu wa kyūjussai ni natta ima mo kenzai desu.","german":"My grandfather is still going strong at ninety.","furigana":"そふ|きゅうじゅっさい|いま|けんざい"},{"japanese":"引退した選手だが、その人気は今も健在だ。","romaji":"Intai shita senshu da ga, sono ninki wa ima mo kenzai da.","german":"He is a retired player, but his popularity is still alive.","furigana":"いんたい|せんしゅ|にんき|いま|けんざい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Das Buch in der Originalsprache, im Gegensatz zur Übersetzung (翻訳): 原書で読む.","examples":[{"japanese":"いつかドイツ語の原書でゲーテを読みたい。","romaji":"Itsuka doitsugo no gensho de Gēte o yomitai.","german":"Irgendwann möchte ich Goethe im deutschen Original lesen.","furigana":"ご|げんしょ|よ"},{"japanese":"翻訳に疑問があったので、原書を確かめた。","romaji":"Hon'yaku ni gimon ga atta node, gensho o tashikameta.","german":"Weil ich an der Übersetzung zweifelte, habe ich im Original nachgesehen.","furigana":"ほんやく|ぎもん|げんしょ|たし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A contest or campaign where one can win a prize (懸賞に応募する, to enter a sweepstakes), also a reward: 懸賞金 (reward money).","examples":[{"japanese":"雑誌の懸賞に応募したら、旅行が当たった。","romaji":"Zasshi no kenshō ni ōbo shitara, ryokō ga atatta.","german":"I entered a magazine sweepstakes and won a trip.","furigana":"ざっし|けんしょう|おうぼ|りょこう|あ"},{"japanese":"犯人の逮捕につながる情報に懸賞がかけられた。","romaji":"Hannin no taiho ni tsunagaru jōhō ni kenshō ga kakerareta.","german":"A reward was offered for information leading to the arrest of the criminal.","furigana":"はんにん|たいほ|じょうほう|けんしょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"な-adjective: physically and mentally healthy, but also \"sound\" regarding finances or society: 健全な経営 (sound management), 健全な精神 (a healthy mind).","examples":[{"japanese":"健全な精神は健全な身体に宿る。","romaji":"Kenzen na seishin wa kenzen na shintai ni yadoru.","german":"A sound mind lives in a sound body.","furigana":"けんぜん|せいしん|けんぜん|しんたい|やど"},{"japanese":"この会社は経営が健全だ。","romaji":"Kono kaisha wa keiei ga kenzen da.","german":"This company is soundly managed.","furigana":"かいしゃ|けいえい|けんぜん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The developing of photographic film; as a verb 現像する. In digital photography also the processing of RAW files.","examples":[{"japanese":"旅行の写真を現像に出した。","romaji":"Ryokō no shashin o genzō ni dashita.","german":"I took my trip photos in for developing.","furigana":"りょこう|しゃしん|げんぞう|だ"},{"japanese":"昔は写真を現像するのに数日かかった。","romaji":"Mukashi wa shashin o genzō suru no ni sūjitsu kakatta.","german":"In the old days, it took a few days to develop photos.","furigana":"むかし|しゃしん|げんぞう|すうじつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The actual place where something happens or that one visits: 現地時間 (local time), 現地の人 (locals).","examples":[{"japanese":"現地から記者が中継で伝えています。","romaji":"Genchi kara kisha ga chūkei de tsutaete imasu.","german":"A reporter is reporting live from the scene.","furigana":"げんち|きしゃ|ちゅうけい|つた"},{"japanese":"旅行先では現地の料理を食べたい。","romaji":"Ryokōsaki de wa genchi no ryōri o tabetai.","german":"When traveling, I want to eat the local food.","furigana":"りょこうさき|げんち|りょうり|た"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The original starting point (原点に戻る = to return to one's roots) and, in mathematics, the coordinate origin.","examples":[{"japanese":"行き詰まったときは、原点に戻って考えよう。","romaji":"Ikizumatta toki wa, genten ni modotte kangaeyō.","german":"When we get stuck, let's go back to the starting point and think again.","furigana":"い|づ|げんてん|もど|かんが"},{"japanese":"グラフの原点は二本の軸が交わる点だ。","romaji":"Gurafu no genten wa nihon no jiku ga majiwaru ten da.","german":"The origin of a graph is the point where the two axes cross.","furigana":"げんてん|にほん|じく|まじ|てん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Deduction of points in exams, competitions or evaluations; as a verb 減点する. Homophone: 原点.","examples":[{"japanese":"漢字を間違えて、二点減点された。","romaji":"Kanji o machigaete, niten genten sareta.","german":"I wrote a kanji wrong and lost two points.","furigana":"かんじ|まちが|にてんげんてん"},{"japanese":"フライングで減点になった。","romaji":"Furaingu de genten ni natta.","german":"I lost points for a false start.","furigana":"げんてん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Political or social power over others: 権力を握る (to seize power), 権力者 (a person in power).","examples":[{"japanese":"権力を持つと、人は変わることがある。","romaji":"Kenryoku o motsu to, hito wa kawaru koto ga aru.","german":"Having power can sometimes change a person.","furigana":"けんりょく|も|ひと|か"},{"japanese":"新聞には権力を監視する役割がある。","romaji":"Shinbun ni wa kenryoku o kanshi suru yakuwari ga aru.","german":"Newspapers have the role of keeping watch over those in power.","furigana":"しんぶん|けんりょく|かんし|やくわり"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Public expression of opinions in speech and writing; mainly in 言論の自由 (freedom of speech).","examples":[{"japanese":"民主主義では言論の自由が守られなければならない。","romaji":"Minshu shugi de wa genron no jiyū ga mamorarenakereba naranai.","german":"In a democracy, freedom of speech must be protected.","furigana":"みんしゅしゅぎ|げんろん|じゆう|まも"},{"japanese":"暴力ではなく言論で対抗すべきだ。","romaji":"Bōryoku de wa naku genron de taikō subeki da.","german":"One should resist with words, not with violence.","furigana":"ぼうりょく|げんろん|たいこう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Suru verb: to love romantically, to be in love: 恋する乙女 (a girl in love). Somewhat more literary than 好きになる.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は初めて人に恋する気持ちを知った。","romaji":"Kanojo wa hajimete hito ni koisuru kimochi o shitta.","german":"For the first time, she knew what it is like to fall in love with someone.","furigana":"かのじょ|はじ|ひと|こい|きも|し"},{"japanese":"恋する人は、毎日が楽しく見えるものだ。","romaji":"Koisuru hito wa, mainichi ga tanoshiku mieru mono da.","german":"To someone in love, every day seems enjoyable.","furigana":"こい|ひと|まいにち|たの|み"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A friendly disposition or affection: 好意を持つ (to like someone), 好意に甘える (to accept a kind offer). Homophone: 行為 (act).","examples":[{"japanese":"私は彼に好意を持っている。","romaji":"Watashi wa kare ni kōi o motte iru.","german":"I have feelings for him.","furigana":"わたし|かれ|こうい|も"},{"japanese":"先輩の好意に甘えて、車で送ってもらった。","romaji":"Senpai no kōi ni amaete, kuruma de okutte moratta.","german":"I took my senior colleague up on his kind offer and let him drive me.","furigana":"せんぱい|こうい|あま|くるま|おく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Employee of a bank (銀行員). 行 here stands for 銀行.","examples":[{"japanese":"窓口の行員が丁寧に説明してくれた。","romaji":"Madoguchi no kōin ga teinei ni setsumei shite kureta.","german":"The bank clerk at the counter explained it to me politely.","furigana":"まどぐち|こういん|ていねい|せつめい"},{"japanese":"この銀行では約千人の行員が働いている。","romaji":"Kono ginkō de wa yaku sennin no kōin ga hataraite iru.","german":"About a thousand employees work at this bank.","furigana":"ぎんこう|やくせんにん|こういん|はたら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Trade between countries or peoples, often historical: 東西交易 (East-West trade), 交易品 (trade goods). Today 貿易 is more usual. Homophone: 公益 (public interest).","examples":[{"japanese":"昔、この港は外国との交易で栄えた。","romaji":"Mukashi, kono minato wa gaikoku to no kōeki de sakaeta.","german":"In the past, this port flourished through trade with foreign countries.","furigana":"むかし|みなと|がいこく|こうえき|さか"},{"japanese":"シルクロードは東西の交易の道だった。","romaji":"Shirukurōdo wa tōzai no kōeki no michi datta.","german":"The Silk Road was a trade route between East and West.","furigana":"とうざい|こうえき|みち"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A trip across the sea; as a verb 航海する. Homophones: 公開, 後悔.","examples":[{"japanese":"船は一か月の航海を終えて港に戻った。","romaji":"Fune wa ikkagetsu no kōkai o oete minato ni modotta.","german":"The ship returned to port after a month-long voyage.","furigana":"ふね|いっ|げつ|こうかい|お|みなと|もど"},{"japanese":"昔の航海は星を頼りにしていた。","romaji":"Mukashi no kōkai wa hoshi o tayori ni shite ita.","german":"In the old days, sea voyages relied on the stars.","furigana":"むかし|こうかい|ほし|たよ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Deliberation by several people to decide together: 合議制 (collegiate system), 合議の上で決める (to decide after joint deliberation).","examples":[{"japanese":"この件は委員の合議で決めます。","romaji":"Kono ken wa iin no gōgi de kimemasu.","german":"This matter will be decided by joint deliberation of the committee members.","furigana":"けん|いいん|ごうぎ|き"},{"japanese":"三人の裁判官が合議して判決を出した。","romaji":"Sannin no saibankan ga gōgi shite hanketsu o dashita.","german":"Three judges deliberated together and delivered the verdict.","furigana":"さんにん|さいばんかん|ごうぎ|はんけつ|だ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The residence of the Japanese Emperor in Tokyo, on the grounds of the former Edo Castle.","examples":[{"japanese":"皇居の周りを走る人が多い。","romaji":"Kōkyo no mawari o hashiru hito ga ōi.","german":"Many people run around the Imperial Palace.","furigana":"こうきょ|まわ|はし|ひと|おお"},{"japanese":"新年には皇居で一般参賀が行われる。","romaji":"Shinnen ni wa kōkyo de ippan sanga ga okonawareru.","german":"The public New Year greeting is held at the Imperial Palace.","furigana":"しんねん|こうきょ|いっぱんさんが|おこな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The founding and promotion of new industries, especially in 殖産興業 (the Meiji-era industrial promotion policy). Homophones: 工業 (industry), 鉱業 (mining), 興行 (performance, show).","examples":[{"japanese":"明治政府は殖産興業に力を入れた。","romaji":"Meiji seifu wa shokusan kōgyō ni chikara o ireta.","german":"The Meiji government put effort into promoting industry.","furigana":"めいじせいふ|しょくさんこうぎょう|ちから|い"},{"japanese":"明治時代、政府は各地で興業を奨励し、官営工場を建てた。","romaji":"Meiji jidai, seifu wa kakuchi de kōgyō o shōrei shi, kan'ei kōjō o tateta.","german":"In the Meiji period, the government encouraged the founding of new industries throughout the country and built state-run factories.","furigana":"めいじじだい|せいふ|かくち|こうぎょう|しょうれい|かんえいこうじょう|た"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"High-lying, flat land, popular in Japan as a summer retreat: 高原の避暑地 (a highland summer resort).","examples":[{"japanese":"夏休みに涼しい高原で過ごした。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi ni suzushii kōgen de sugoshita.","german":"I spent the summer vacation on a cool highland.","furigana":"なつやす|すず|こうげん|す"},{"japanese":"高原の朝は空気が澄んでいる。","romaji":"Kōgen no asa wa kūki ga sunde iru.","german":"In the morning, the air on the plateau is clear.","furigana":"こうげん|あさ|くうき|す"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Describes bright, shining light, mostly as 煌々と: 明かりが煌々とついている (the lights are blazing). Literary.","examples":[{"japanese":"夜中なのに、ビルの明かりが煌々とついている。","romaji":"Yonaka na noni, biru no akari ga kōkō to tsuite iru.","german":"Even though it is the middle of the night, the lights of the building are shining brightly.","furigana":"よなか|あ|こうこう"},{"japanese":"月が煌々と夜道を照らしていた。","romaji":"Tsuki ga kōkō to yomichi o terashite ita.","german":"The moon lit up the night road brightly.","furigana":"つき|こうこう|よみち|て"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A short course to acquire certain knowledge: 夏期講習 (summer course), 救命講習 (life-saving course), 講習を受ける (to attend a course). Homophone: 公衆.","examples":[{"japanese":"運転免許の更新で講習を受けた。","romaji":"Unten menkyo no kōshin de kōshū o uketa.","german":"I attended a course when renewing my driver's license.","furigana":"うんてんめんきょ|こうしん|こうしゅう|う"},{"japanese":"夏休みに塾の講習に通った。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi ni juku no kōshū ni kayotta.","german":"During summer vacation I attended a course at a cram school.","furigana":"なつやす|じゅく|こうしゅう|かよ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To state something orally or to dictate: 口述試験 (oral exam), 口述筆記 (dictation).","examples":[{"japanese":"作家は口述で小説を書き上げた。","romaji":"Sakka wa kōjutsu de shōsetsu o kakiageta.","german":"The writer completed the novel by dictation.","furigana":"さっか|こうじゅつ|しょうせつ|か|あ"},{"japanese":"証人の口述が記録された。","romaji":"Shōnin no kōjutsu ga kiroku sareta.","german":"The witness's oral statement was recorded.","furigana":"しょうにん|こうじゅつ|きろく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Getting better in ability, quality or standard of living; as a verb 向上する: 技術が向上する (skills improve), 向上心 (drive to improve). Homophone: 工場.","examples":[{"japanese":"毎日練習して、技術が向上した。","romaji":"Mainichi renshū shite, gijutsu ga kōjō shita.","german":"By practicing every day, my skills improved.","furigana":"まいにちれんしゅう|ぎじゅつ|こうじょう"},{"japanese":"会社はサービスの質の向上に取り組んでいる。","romaji":"Kaisha wa sābisu no shitsu no kōjō ni torikunde iru.","german":"The company is working on improving the quality of its service.","furigana":"かいしゃ|しつ|こうじょう|と|く"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To march or move in rows: 入場行進 (entrance march), デモ行進 (protest march). As a verb 行進する. Homophone: 更新.","examples":[{"japanese":"選手たちが入場行進をした。","romaji":"Senshutachi ga nyūjō kōshin o shita.","german":"The athletes marched in for the opening procession.","furigana":"せんしゅ|にゅうじょうこうしん"},{"japanese":"音楽に合わせて子どもたちが行進した。","romaji":"Ongaku ni awasete kodomotachi ga kōshin shita.","german":"The children marched in time to the music.","furigana":"おんがく|あ|こ|こうしん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Meteorological term for rain and snow: 降水確率 (probability of precipitation), 降水量 (amount of precipitation). Homophone: 香水.","examples":[{"japanese":"明日の降水確率は七十パーセントです。","romaji":"Ashita no kōsui kakuritsu wa nanajuppāsento desu.","german":"The probability of precipitation tomorrow is 70 percent.","furigana":"あした|こうすいかくりつ|ななじゅっ"},{"japanese":"今年は例年より降水が少なかった。","romaji":"Kotoshi wa reinen yori kōsui ga sukunakatta.","german":"There was less precipitation this year than usual.","furigana":"ことし|れいねん|こうすい|すく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"What is open and generally known, often as 公然と or 公然の秘密 (open secret).","examples":[{"japanese":"二人の関係は公然の秘密だった。","romaji":"Futari no kankei wa kōzen no himitsu datta.","german":"The relationship between the two was an open secret.","furigana":"ふたり|かんけい|こうぜん|ひみつ"},{"japanese":"彼は会議で公然と社長を批判した。","romaji":"Kare wa kaigi de kōzen to shachō o hihan shita.","german":"He openly criticized the company president at the meeting.","furigana":"かれ|かいぎ|こうぜん|しゃちょう|ひはん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A prolonged fight between groups: 派閥抗争 (factional struggle), 暴力団の抗争 (gang war). As a verb 抗争する.","examples":[{"japanese":"党内の派閥抗争が激しくなっている。","romaji":"Tōnai no habatsu kōsō ga hageshiku natte iru.","german":"Factional struggles within the party are intensifying.","furigana":"とうない|はばつこうそう|はげ"},{"japanese":"二つの組織の抗争で、多くの人が巻き込まれた。","romaji":"Futatsu no soshiki no kōsō de, ōku no hito ga makikomareta.","german":"Many people were caught up in the conflict between the two organizations.","furigana":"ふた|そしき|こうそう|おお|ひと|ま|こ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Shiny surface of fabric, paper, metal or lacquer: 光沢のある布 (glossy cloth), 光沢紙 (glossy paper).","examples":[{"japanese":"この絹の布には美しい光沢がある。","romaji":"Kono kinu no nuno ni wa utsukushii kōtaku ga aru.","german":"This silk cloth has a beautiful luster.","furigana":"きぬ|ぬの|うつく|こうたく"},{"japanese":"写真を光沢のある紙に印刷した。","romaji":"Shashin o kōtaku no aru kami ni insatsu shita.","german":"I printed the photos on glossy paper.","furigana":"しゃしん|こうたく|かみ|いんさつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A body established by the state for public tasks; colloquially also short for its rental housing (公団住宅).","examples":[{"japanese":"祖父母は長い間、公団の団地に住んでいた。","romaji":"Sofubo wa nagai aida, kōdan no danchi ni sunde ita.","german":"My grandparents lived for a long time in a public housing complex.","furigana":"そふぼ|なが|あいだ|こうだん|だんち|す"},{"japanese":"その道路は公団によって建設された。","romaji":"Sono dōro wa kōdan ni yotte kensetsu sareta.","german":"That road was built by a public corporation.","furigana":"どうろ|こうだん|けんせつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"な-adjective: business, sales or athletes are doing well: 売れ行きが好調だ (sales are good), 好調な滑り出し (a good start). Opposite: 不調.","examples":[{"japanese":"新商品の売れ行きは好調だ。","romaji":"Shinshōhin no ureyuki wa kōchō da.","german":"Sales of the new product are strong.","furigana":"しんしょうひん|う|ゆ|こうちょう"},{"japanese":"チームは開幕から好調を続けている。","romaji":"Chīmu wa kaimaku kara kōchō o tsuzukete iru.","german":"The team has been in good form since the start of the season.","furigana":"かいまく|こうちょう|つづ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mostly as 口頭で (orally) or 口頭の: 口頭試問 (oral examination), 口頭で伝える (to tell verbally). Opposite: 書面 (in writing).","examples":[{"japanese":"結果は口頭で伝えられた。","romaji":"Kekka wa kōtō de tsutaerareta.","german":"The result was communicated orally.","furigana":"けっか|こうとう|つた"},{"japanese":"口頭の約束だけでは不安なので、書面にしてください。","romaji":"Kōtō no yakusoku dake de wa fuan na node, shomen ni shite kudasai.","german":"A verbal promise alone makes me uneasy, so please put it in writing.","furigana":"こうとう|やくそく|ふあん|しょめん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Einen Text lesen und erklären, vor allem an der Universität: 英書講読 (Lektüre englischer Texte). Gleich klingend: 購読 (Abonnement).","examples":[{"japanese":"大学でドイツ語の講読の授業を取った。","romaji":"Daigaku de doitsugo no kōdoku no jugyō o totta.","german":"An der Uni habe ich einen Lektürekurs für deutsche Texte belegt.","furigana":"だいがく|ご|こうどく|じゅぎょう|と"},{"japanese":"ゼミでは毎週、古典を講読している。","romaji":"Zemi de wa maishū, koten o kōdoku shite iru.","german":"Im Seminar lesen und besprechen wir jede Woche klassische Texte.","furigana":"まいしゅう|こてん|こうどく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Officially recognized or approved: 公認会計士 (certified public accountant), 公認記録 (official record), 党の公認候補 (party-endorsed candidate). As a verb 公認する.","examples":[{"japanese":"この記録は大会の公認を受けた。","romaji":"Kono kiroku wa taikai no kōnin o uketa.","german":"This record received official recognition from the organizers.","furigana":"きろく|たいかい|こうにん|う"},{"japanese":"二人の交際は、今では親も公認している。","romaji":"Futari no kōsai wa, ima de wa oya mo kōnin shite iru.","german":"Even the parents now officially accept the two's relationship.","furigana":"ふたり|こうさい|いま|おや|こうにん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"When something is well received: 好評を得る (to be well received), 好評につき (due to popular demand). Opposite: 不評. Homophone: 公表 (public announcement).","examples":[{"japanese":"新しいメニューはお客さんに好評だ。","romaji":"Atarashii menyū wa okyakusan ni kōhyō da.","german":"The new menu is popular with customers.","furigana":"あたら|きゃく|こうひょう"},{"japanese":"展覧会は好評につき、一週間延長された。","romaji":"Tenrankai wa kōhyō ni tsuki, isshūkan enchō sareta.","german":"Due to popular demand, the exhibition was extended by a week.","furigana":"てんらんかい|こうひょう|いっしゅうかんえんちょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Run by a prefecture or municipality: 公立学校 (public school), 公立病院 (public hospital). Opposite: 私立 (private). Homophone: 効率 (efficiency).","examples":[{"japanese":"子どもは近くの公立の小学校に通っている。","romaji":"Kodomo wa chikaku no kōritsu no shōgakkō ni kayotte iru.","german":"My child goes to the public elementary school nearby.","furigana":"こ|ちか|こうりつ|しょうがっこう|かよ"},{"japanese":"公立の図書館は誰でも無料で使える。","romaji":"Kōritsu no toshokan wa dare demo muryō de tsukaeru.","german":"Anyone can use public libraries for free.","furigana":"こうりつ|としょかん|だれ|むりょう|つか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To accompany and protect people or transports; also the guards themselves. As a verb 護衛する.","examples":[{"japanese":"大統領は多くの護衛に囲まれていた。","romaji":"Daitōryō wa ōku no goei ni kakomarete ita.","german":"The president was surrounded by many bodyguards.","furigana":"だいとうりょう|おお|ごえい|かこ"},{"japanese":"警察が選手のバスを護衛した。","romaji":"Keisatsu ga senshu no basu o goei shita.","german":"The police escorted the team bus.","furigana":"けいさつ|せんしゅ|ごえい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive godan verb, often written in kana: ボートを漕ぐ (to row a boat), 自転車を漕ぐ (to pedal a bicycle), ブランコを漕ぐ (to swing on a swing). Idiom: 舟を漕ぐ (to nod off while sitting).","examples":[{"japanese":"湖でボートを漕いだ。","romaji":"Mizuumi de bōto o koida.","german":"I rowed a boat on the lake.","furigana":"みずうみ|こ"},{"japanese":"坂道で自転車を漕ぐのは大変だ。","romaji":"Sakamichi de jitensha o kogu no wa taihen da.","german":"Pedaling a bicycle up a slope is hard work.","furigana":"さかみち|じてんしゃ|こ|たいへん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Words and expressions in a text: 語句の意味を調べる (to look up the meaning of words and phrases). Homophone: ごく (extremely).","examples":[{"japanese":"分からない語句は辞書で調べてください。","romaji":"Wakaranai goku wa jisho de shirabete kudasai.","german":"Please look up words and phrases you don't know in a dictionary.","furigana":"わ|ごく|じしょ|しら"},{"japanese":"検索する語句を入力してください。","romaji":"Kensaku suru goku o nyūryoku shite kudasai.","german":"Please enter the words you want to search for.","furigana":"けんさく|ごく|にゅうりょく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Produced in one's own country (usually Japan): 国産の牛肉 (domestic beef), 国産車 (domestic car). Opposites: 外国産 (foreign-made), 輸入品 (imported goods).","examples":[{"japanese":"このスーパーでは国産の野菜しか売っていない。","romaji":"Kono sūpā de wa kokusan no yasai shika utte inai.","german":"This supermarket only sells domestically grown vegetables.","furigana":"こくさん|やさい|う"},{"japanese":"国産の牛肉は輸入品より高い。","romaji":"Kokusan no gyūniku wa yunyūhin yori takai.","german":"Domestic beef is more expensive than imported beef.","furigana":"こくさん|ぎゅうにく|ゆにゅうひん|たか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Determined or designated by the state: 国定公園 (quasi-national park), 国定教科書 (state-prescribed textbook).","examples":[{"japanese":"この湖の周りは国定公園に指定されている。","romaji":"Kono mizuumi no mawari wa kokutei kōen ni shitei sarete iru.","german":"The area around this lake is designated a quasi-national park.","furigana":"みずうみ|まわ|こくていこうえん|してい"},{"japanese":"戦前の学校では国定の教科書が使われていた。","romaji":"Senzen no gakkō de wa kokutei no kyōkasho ga tsukawarete ita.","german":"State-prescribed textbooks were used in prewar schools.","furigana":"せんぜん|がっこう|こくてい|きょうかしょ|つか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The territory of a country: 国土が狭い (the national territory is small), 国土交通省 (Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport).","examples":[{"japanese":"日本は国土の大部分が山地だ。","romaji":"Nihon wa kokudo no daibubun ga sanchi da.","german":"Most of Japan's land area is mountainous.","furigana":"にほん|こくど|だいぶぶん|さんち"},{"japanese":"国土が狭い国では、土地の値段が高くなりやすい。","romaji":"Kokudo ga semai kuni de wa, tochi no nedan ga takaku nariyasui.","german":"In countries with little land, land prices tend to be high.","furigana":"こくど|せま|くに|とち|ねだん|たか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Defense of the country against external attack: 国防費 (defense spending), 国防政策 (defense policy).","examples":[{"japanese":"政府は国防の予算を増やした。","romaji":"Seifu wa kokubō no yosan o fuyashita.","german":"The government increased the defense budget.","furigana":"せいふ|こくぼう|よさん|ふ"},{"japanese":"国防について国民の意見が分かれている。","romaji":"Kokubō ni tsuite kokumin no iken ga wakarete iru.","german":"The public is divided on national defense.","furigana":"こくぼう|こくみん|いけん|わ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Owned by the state: 国有地 (state land), 国有林 (state forest), 国有化 (nationalization). Opposites: 民有, 私有.","examples":[{"japanese":"この辺りの森はすべて国有だ。","romaji":"Kono atari no mori wa subete kokuyū da.","german":"The forests around here are all state-owned.","furigana":"あた|もり|こくゆう"},{"japanese":"国有の土地が安く売られたことが問題になった。","romaji":"Kokuyū no tochi ga yasuku urareta koto ga mondai ni natta.","german":"It became an issue that state-owned land was sold cheaply.","furigana":"こくゆう|とち|やす|う|もんだい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Short form of 国際連合 (United Nations): 国連本部, 国連事務総長 (UN Secretary-General).","examples":[{"japanese":"国連の本部はニューヨークにある。","romaji":"Kokuren no honbu wa Nyūyōku ni aru.","german":"The headquarters of the United Nations is in New York.","furigana":"こくれん|ほんぶ"},{"japanese":"彼女は将来、国連で働きたいと言っている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa shōrai, kokuren de hatarakitai to itte iru.","german":"She says she wants to work at the United Nations in the future.","furigana":"かのじょ|しょうらい|こくれん|はたら|い"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 42 (source: N4; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"A dark, almost black brown (literally \"scorched brown\"); also 焦げ茶色.","examples":[{"japanese":"焦げ茶のコートを買った。","romaji":"Kogecha no kōto o katta.","german":"I bought a dark brown coat.","furigana":"こ|ちゃ|か"},{"japanese":"この靴は黒ではなく焦げ茶です。","romaji":"Kono kutsu wa kuro de wa naku kogecha desu.","german":"These shoes are not black but dark brown.","furigana":"くつ|くろ|こ|ちゃ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Each one separately: 個々の問題 (individual problems), 個々に対応する (to deal with each individually). The spelling 箇箇 is rare.","examples":[{"japanese":"個々の意見を大切にしたい。","romaji":"Koko no iken o taisetsu ni shitai.","german":"I want to value each individual opinion.","furigana":"ここ|いけん|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"問題が多いので、個々に対応するのは難しい。","romaji":"Mondai ga ōi node, koko ni taiō suru no wa muzukashii.","german":"There are so many problems that it is hard to deal with each one individually.","furigana":"もんだい|おお|ここ|たいおう|むずか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A feeling or mood: 生きた心地がしない (to be scared to death), 夢を見ているような心地 (a dreamlike feeling). As a suffix 〜心地（ごこち）: 住み心地 (comfort of living), 着心地 (comfort of wearing).","examples":[{"japanese":"地震の間は生きた心地がしなかった。","romaji":"Jishin no aida wa ikita kokochi ga shinakatta.","german":"During the earthquake I felt as if my life was in danger.","furigana":"じしん|あいだ|い|ここち"},{"japanese":"合格の知らせを聞いて、夢のような心地だった。","romaji":"Gōkaku no shirase o kiite, yume no yō na kokochi datta.","german":"When I heard I had passed, it felt like a dream.","furigana":"ごうかく|し|き|ゆめ|ここち"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The inner attitude with which one approaches something: 心掛けがいい (to be thoughtful, to have a good attitude). From the verb 心掛ける (to make an effort, to keep in mind).","examples":[{"japanese":"毎日少しずつ勉強するという心掛けが大切だ。","romaji":"Mainichi sukoshi zutsu benkyō suru to iu kokorogake ga taisetsu da.","german":"The attitude of studying a little every day is important.","furigana":"まいにちすこ|べんきょう|こころが|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"傘を持ってくるとは、心掛けがいいね。","romaji":"Kasa o motte kuru to wa, kokorogake ga ii ne.","german":"You brought an umbrella; that is thoughtful of you.","furigana":"かさ|も|こころが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Godan verb: to set a life goal, mostly with を or に: 医者を志す (to aspire to be a doctor), 音楽の道を志す (to set out on a path in music).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は子どものころから医者を志していた。","romaji":"Kare wa kodomo no koro kara isha o kokorozashite ita.","german":"He aspired to become a doctor from childhood.","furigana":"かれ|こ|いしゃ|こころざ"},{"japanese":"画家を志して、パリに留学した。","romaji":"Gaka o kokorozashite, Pari ni ryūgaku shita.","german":"Aiming to become a painter, I studied abroad in Paris.","furigana":"がか|こころざ|りゅうがく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"An attempt or new approach; from the verb 試みる. Common: 新しい試み (a new attempt), 初めての試み (a first attempt).","examples":[{"japanese":"これは日本で初めての試みだ。","romaji":"Kore wa Nihon de hajimete no kokoromi da.","german":"This is the first attempt of its kind in Japan.","furigana":"にほん|はじ|こころ"},{"japanese":"新しい試みは残念ながら失敗に終わった。","romaji":"Atarashii kokoromi wa zannen nagara shippai ni owatta.","german":"Unfortunately, the new attempt ended in failure.","furigana":"あたら|こころ|ざんねん|しっぱい|お"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Polite term for a dead person, especially at funerals: 故人をしのぶ (to remember the deceased). Homophone: 個人 (individual).","examples":[{"japanese":"葬儀には故人の友人が大勢集まった。","romaji":"Sōgi ni wa kojin no yūjin ga ōzei atsumatta.","german":"Many of the deceased's friends gathered at the funeral.","furigana":"そうぎ|こじん|ゆうじん|おおぜいあつ"},{"japanese":"家族は故人の思い出を静かに語り合った。","romaji":"Kazoku wa kojin no omoide o shizuka ni katariatta.","german":"The family quietly shared their memories of the deceased.","furigana":"かぞく|こじん|おも|で|しず|かた|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive godan verb: to pass a liquid through a sieve or cloth; also written 漉す. Homophones: 越す, 超す.","examples":[{"japanese":"スープを布で濾して、なめらかにした。","romaji":"Sūpu o nuno de koshite, nameraka ni shita.","german":"I strained the soup through a cloth to make it smooth.","furigana":"ぬの|こ"},{"japanese":"コーヒーをペーパーフィルターで濾す。","romaji":"Kōhī o pēpā firutā de kosu.","german":"You filter the coffee through a paper filter.","furigana":"こ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"The outermost tips of the branches or the top of a tree; rather literary.","examples":[{"japanese":"小鳥が梢で鳴いている。","romaji":"Kotori ga kozue de naite iru.","german":"A little bird is singing in the treetop.","furigana":"ことり|こずえ|な"},{"japanese":"風が梢を揺らしていた。","romaji":"Kaze ga kozue o yurashite ita.","german":"The wind swayed the treetops.","furigana":"かぜ|こずえ|ゆ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The solid state of matter; alongside 液体 (liquid) and 気体 (gas). Homophone: 個体 (individual).","examples":[{"japanese":"水は冷やすと固体の氷になる。","romaji":"Mizu wa hiyasu to kotai no kōri ni naru.","german":"When water is cooled, it becomes solid ice.","furigana":"みず|ひ|こたい|こおり"},{"japanese":"物質には固体、液体、気体の三つの状態がある。","romaji":"Busshitsu ni wa kotai, ekitai, kitai no mittsu no jōtai ga aru.","german":"Matter has three states: solid, liquid and gas.","furigana":"ぶっしつ|こたい|えきたい|きたい|みっ|じょうたい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A low table with a heater underneath and a quilt over it; typical for Japanese winters. Usually written in kana.","examples":[{"japanese":"冬はこたつでみかんを食べるのが好きだ。","romaji":"Fuyu wa kotatsu de mikan o taberu no ga suki da.","german":"In winter I like eating mandarin oranges at the kotatsu.","furigana":"ふゆ|た|す"},{"japanese":"こたつに入ったまま寝てしまった。","romaji":"Kotatsu ni haitta mama nete shimatta.","german":"I fell asleep under the kotatsu.","furigana":"はい|ね"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Old, valuable objects for collecting: 骨董品店 (antique shop). Jokingly also for very old appliances.","examples":[{"japanese":"祖父は骨董品を集めるのが趣味だ。","romaji":"Sofu wa kottōhin o atsumeru no ga shumi da.","german":"My grandfather's hobby is collecting antiques.","furigana":"そふ|こっとうひん|あつ|しゅみ"},{"japanese":"この皿は江戸時代の骨董品らしい。","romaji":"Kono sara wa Edo jidai no kottōhin rashii.","german":"This plate seems to be an antique from the Edo period.","furigana":"さら|えどじだい|こっとうひん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A matter or point under discussion; somewhat more formal than こと.","examples":[{"japanese":"会議で決まった事柄をメールでお知らせします。","romaji":"Kaigi de kimatta kotogara o mēru de oshirase shimasu.","german":"I will let you know by email what was decided at the meeting.","furigana":"かいぎ|き|ことがら|し"},{"japanese":"個人的な事柄には答えられません。","romaji":"Kojinteki na kotogara ni wa kotaeraremasen.","german":"I cannot answer questions about personal matters.","furigana":"こじんてき|ことがら|こた"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A message that one has conveyed through a third person: 言付けを頼む (to ask someone to pass on a message). From the verb 言付ける.","examples":[{"japanese":"母から先生への言付けを預かってきました。","romaji":"Haha kara sensei e no kotozuke o azukatte kimashita.","german":"I have a message from my mother for the teacher.","furigana":"はは|せんせい|ことづ|あず"},{"japanese":"部長は不在だったので、秘書に言付けを頼んだ。","romaji":"Buchō wa fuzai datta node, hisho ni kotozuke o tanonda.","german":"The department head was out, so I asked the secretary to pass on a message.","furigana":"ぶちょう|ふざい|ひしょ|ことづ|たの"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A message passed on orally or something known by hearsay: 言伝に聞く (to hear secondhand). Somewhat more old-fashioned than 伝言.","examples":[{"japanese":"友人に言伝を頼んで、先に帰った。","romaji":"Yūjin ni kotozute o tanonde, saki ni kaetta.","german":"I asked a friend to pass on a message and went home first.","furigana":"ゆうじん|ことづて|たの|さき|かえ"},{"japanese":"彼が結婚したことを言伝に聞いた。","romaji":"Kare ga kekkon shita koto o kotozute ni kiita.","german":"I heard through the grapevine that he got married.","furigana":"かれ|けっこん|ことづて|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Expresses a cautious conjecture; often with かもしれない: ことによると雨になるかもしれない (it might rain).","examples":[{"japanese":"ことによると、明日は雪になるかもしれない。","romaji":"Koto ni yoru to, ashita wa yuki ni naru kamo shirenai.","german":"It may snow tomorrow.","furigana":"あした|ゆき"},{"japanese":"ことによると、彼はもう知っているのではないか。","romaji":"Koto ni yoru to, kare wa mō shitte iru no de wa nai ka.","german":"Maybe he already knows, doesn't he?","furigana":"かれ|し"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Bend (race track), area in a store (食品コーナー) or segment of a broadcast.","examples":[{"japanese":"本屋の外国語のコーナーで辞書を探した。","romaji":"Hon'ya no gaikokugo no kōnā de jisho o sagashita.","german":"I looked for a dictionary in the foreign-language section of the bookstore.","furigana":"ほんや|がいこくご|じしょ|さが"},{"japanese":"最後のコーナーで、選手が一人を抜いた。","romaji":"Saigo no kōnā de, senshu ga hitori o nuita.","german":"On the last bend, the runner overtook one competitor.","furigana":"さいご|せんしゅ|ひとり|ぬ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 10 (source: N5; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"な-adjective: completely broken or crushed, mostly 粉々になる / 粉々に割れる (to shatter).","examples":[{"japanese":"落としたコップが粉々に割れた。","romaji":"Otoshita koppu ga konagona ni wareta.","german":"The glass I dropped shattered into pieces.","furigana":"お|こなごな|わ"},{"japanese":"地震で窓ガラスが粉々になった。","romaji":"Jishin de madogarasu ga konagona ni natta.","german":"The window glass was shattered to bits by the earthquake.","furigana":"じしん|まど|こなごな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"An advertisement on television or radio; short form CM（シーエム）.","examples":[{"japanese":"この歌はテレビのコマーシャルで有名になった。","romaji":"Kono uta wa terebi no komāsharu de yūmei ni natta.","german":"This song became famous through a TV commercial.","furigana":"うた|ゆうめい"},{"japanese":"映画の途中にコマーシャルが多すぎる。","romaji":"Eiga no tochū ni komāsharu ga ōsugiru.","german":"There are too many commercials in the middle of the movie.","furigana":"えいが|とちゅう|おお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"な-adjective: warm and attentive (細やかな愛情 tender affection, 細やかな気配り thoughtful consideration). Not to be confused with 細やか（ささやか, modest）.","examples":[{"japanese":"旅館の細やかなサービスに感動した。","romaji":"Ryokan no komayaka na sābisu ni kandō shita.","german":"I was moved by the ryokan's attentive service.","furigana":"りょかん|こま|かんどう"},{"japanese":"母は細やかな愛情で私たちを育ててくれた。","romaji":"Haha wa komayaka na aijō de watashitachi o sodatete kureta.","german":"My mother raised us with tender affection.","furigana":"はは|こま|あいじょう|わたし|そだ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Intransitive godan verb: to withdraw (部屋にこもる), to be filled with feeling (心のこもった, heartfelt), or smoke or smell builds up (煙がこもる, smoke gets trapped).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は週末ずっと部屋にこもっていた。","romaji":"Kare wa shūmatsu zutto heya ni komotte ita.","german":"He shut himself in his room all weekend.","furigana":"かれ|しゅうまつ|へや"},{"japanese":"心のこもった手紙をもらって、うれしかった。","romaji":"Kokoro no komotta tegami o moratte, ureshikatta.","german":"I was happy to receive a heartfelt letter.","furigana":"こころ|てがみ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive godan verb, fixed in set phrases: 目を凝らす (to look closely), 工夫を凝らす (to put ingenuity into something), 息を凝らす (to hold one's breath).","examples":[{"japanese":"暗闇の中で目を凝らすと、人影が見えた。","romaji":"Kurayami no naka de me o korasu to, hitokage ga mieta.","german":"When I strained my eyes in the darkness, I could make out a figure.","furigana":"くらやみ|なか|め|こ|ひとかげ|み"},{"japanese":"店は工夫を凝らしたメニューで客を集めている。","romaji":"Mise wa kufū o korashita menyū de kyaku o atsumete iru.","german":"The restaurant attracts customers with an ingeniously devised menu.","furigana":"みせ|くふう|こ|きゃく|あつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A gentle request from an older person to a younger one: \"Look!\" After the て-form: 〜てご覧なさい (try doing ...). Shorter: ご覧.","examples":[{"japanese":"ほら、窓の外をご覧なさい。雪ですよ。","romaji":"Hora, mado no soto o goran nasai. Yuki desu yo.","german":"Look, look out the window. It's snowing.","furigana":"まど|そと|らん|ゆき"},{"japanese":"自分でもう一度考えてご覧なさい。","romaji":"Jibun de mō ichido kangaete goran nasai.","german":"Think about it once more by yourself.","furigana":"じぶん|いちどかんが|らん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To make contact (コンタクトを取る) and short for コンタクトレンズ (contact lens).","examples":[{"japanese":"朝起きたら、まずコンタクトを入れる。","romaji":"Asa okitara, mazu kontakuto o ireru.","german":"When I wake up in the morning, the first thing I do is put in my contact lenses.","furigana":"あさお|い"},{"japanese":"取引先とコンタクトを取るのが私の仕事だ。","romaji":"Torihikisaki to kontakuto o toru no ga watashi no shigoto da.","german":"My job is to get in touch with business partners.","furigana":"とりひきさき|と|わたし|しごと"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 17 (source: N4; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"A competition judged by a jury: 写真コンテスト, スピーチコンテスト.","examples":[{"japanese":"日本語のスピーチコンテストに出ることにした。","romaji":"Nihongo no supīchi kontesuto ni deru koto ni shita.","german":"I decided to enter a Japanese speech contest.","furigana":"にほんご|で"},{"japanese":"写真のコンテストで一位になった。","romaji":"Shashin no kontesuto de ichii ni natta.","german":"I won first place in the photo contest.","furigana":"しゃしん|いちい"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 40 (source: N4; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"Opposition of colors, brightness or characteristics: 色のコントラスト (color contrast), 画面のコントラストを上げる (to raise the screen contrast).","examples":[{"japanese":"青い空と白い雪のコントラストが美しい。","romaji":"Aoi sora to shiroi yuki no kontorasuto ga utsukushii.","german":"The contrast between the blue sky and white snow is beautiful.","furigana":"あお|そら|しろ|ゆき|うつく"},{"japanese":"画面が見にくいので、コントラストを上げた。","romaji":"Gamen ga minikui node, kontorasuto o ageta.","german":"The screen was hard to read, so I increased the contrast.","furigana":"がめん|み|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To steer or have something under control; as a verb コントロールする: 感情をコントロールする (to control one's emotions). In baseball: pitching accuracy.","examples":[{"japanese":"怒りをコントロールするのは難しい。","romaji":"Ikari o kontorōru suru no wa muzukashii.","german":"It is hard to control one's anger.","furigana":"いか|むずか"},{"japanese":"あのピッチャーはコントロールがいい。","romaji":"Ano picchā wa kontorōru ga ii.","german":"That pitcher has good control."}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A drawing instrument for circles (a pair of compasses) and a magnetic compass. Colloquially also leg length: コンパスが長い (long-legged).","examples":[{"japanese":"コンパスで円を描いた。","romaji":"Konpasu de en o egaita.","german":"I drew a circle with a compass.","furigana":"えん|えが"},{"japanese":"山ではコンパスと地図を持って歩く。","romaji":"Yama de wa konpasu to chizu o motte aruku.","german":"In the mountains I walk with a compass and a map.","furigana":"やま|ちず|も|ある"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Wealth, especially in 財を成す / 財を築く (to make a fortune). In compounds also \"goods\": 文化財 (cultural property).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は貿易で財を成した。","romaji":"Kare wa bōeki de zai o nashita.","german":"He made his fortune in trade.","furigana":"かれ|ぼうえき|ざい|な"},{"japanese":"祖父は一代で財を築いた。","romaji":"Sofu wa ichidai de zai o kizuita.","german":"My grandfather built a fortune in a single generation.","furigana":"そふ|いちだい|ざい|きず"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Meeting again after a long time; as a verb 再会する. Homophone: 再開 (resumption).","examples":[{"japanese":"十年ぶりに高校の友達と再会した。","romaji":"Jūnen buri ni kōkō no tomodachi to saikai shita.","german":"I met my high school friends again for the first time in ten years.","furigana":"じゅうねん|こうこう|ともだち|さいかい"},{"japanese":"二人は再会を約束して別れた。","romaji":"Futari wa saikai o yakusoku shite wakareta.","german":"The two parted, promising to meet again.","furigana":"ふたり|さいかい|やくそく|わか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Fine craftsmanship (竹細工 bamboo work, ガラス細工 glasswork) and, pejoratively, a trick or manipulation (細工をする, to tamper).","examples":[{"japanese":"この村は竹の細工で有名だ。","romaji":"Kono mura wa take no saiku de yūmei da.","german":"This village is famous for its bamboo craftwork.","furigana":"むら|たけ|さいく|ゆうめい"},{"japanese":"誰かが書類に細工をしたらしい。","romaji":"Dareka ga shorui ni saiku o shita rashii.","german":"Someone seems to have tampered with the documents.","furigana":"だれ|しょるい|さいく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To extract mineral resources from the earth: 石炭の採掘 (coal mining), 金を採掘する (to mine gold).","examples":[{"japanese":"この山では昔、銀が採掘されていた。","romaji":"Kono yama de wa mukashi, gin ga saikutsu sarete ita.","german":"Silver was once mined in this mountain.","furigana":"やま|むかし|ぎん|さいくつ"},{"japanese":"石油の採掘には大きな費用がかかる。","romaji":"Sekiyu no saikutsu ni wa ōkina hiyō ga kakaru.","german":"Extracting oil costs a great deal.","furigana":"せきゆ|さいくつ|おお|ひよう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A regularly recurring process: 生活のサイクル (daily rhythm), 景気のサイクル (economic cycle).","examples":[{"japanese":"夜勤が続いて、生活のサイクルが乱れた。","romaji":"Yakin ga tsuzuite, seikatsu no saikuru ga midareta.","german":"With night shifts continuing, my daily rhythm got disrupted.","furigana":"やきん|つづ|せいかつ|みだ"},{"japanese":"この機械は十分のサイクルで動いている。","romaji":"Kono kikai wa juppun no saikuru de ugoite iru.","german":"This machine operates in a ten-minute cycle.","furigana":"きかい|じゅっぷん|うご"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To recreate or reenact something faithfully (事故を再現する, to reconstruct an accident; 再現ドラマ, a dramatized reenactment); also the reappearance of something.","examples":[{"japanese":"警察は事故の状況を再現した。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa jiko no jōkyō o saigen shita.","german":"The police reconstructed the circumstances of the accident.","furigana":"けいさつ|じこ|じょうきょう|さいげん"},{"japanese":"この店は昔の町並みを再現している。","romaji":"Kono mise wa mukashi no machinami o saigen shite iru.","german":"This restaurant recreates the townscape of the past.","furigana":"みせ|むかし|まちな|さいげん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To collect samples or specimens: 昆虫採集 (insect collecting), 植物採集 (plant collecting). Homophone: 最終 (final).","examples":[{"japanese":"夏休みの宿題で昆虫採集をした。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi no shukudai de konchū saishū o shita.","german":"For my summer vacation homework I collected insects.","furigana":"なつやす|しゅくだい|こんちゅうさいしゅう"},{"japanese":"研究のために、海岸で貝殻を採集した。","romaji":"Kenkyū no tame ni, kaigan de kaigara o saishū shita.","german":"For research, I collected seashells on the beach.","furigana":"けんきゅう|かいがん|かいがら|さいしゅう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Size of clothing, shoes and objects: サイズが合わない, Mサイズ.","examples":[{"japanese":"このシャツのもう一つ大きいサイズはありますか。","romaji":"Kono shatsu no mō hitotsu ōkii saizu wa arimasu ka.","german":"Do you have this shirt one size larger?","furigana":"ひと|おお"},{"japanese":"靴のサイズが合わなくて、足が痛い。","romaji":"Kutsu no saizu ga awanakute, ashi ga itai.","german":"The shoes don't fit, and my feet hurt.","furigana":"くつ|あ|あし|いた"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 23 (source: N5; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"To formally adopt a motion, resolution or textbook: 決議を採択する (to adopt a resolution), 教科書の採択 (textbook adoption).","examples":[{"japanese":"会議で新しい提案が採択された。","romaji":"Kaigi de atarashii teian ga saitaku sareta.","german":"A new proposal was adopted at the meeting.","furigana":"かいぎ|あたら|ていあん|さいたく"},{"japanese":"委員会は決議案を全会一致で採択した。","romaji":"Iinkai wa ketsugian o zenkai itchi de saitaku shita.","german":"The committee adopted the draft resolution unanimously.","furigana":"いいんかい|けつぎあん|ぜんかいいっち|さいたく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Intransitive godan verb for birdsong; usually written in kana: 小鳥がさえずる (a little bird chirps).","examples":[{"japanese":"朝、窓の外で小鳥が囀っていた。","romaji":"Asa, mado no soto de kotori ga saezutte ita.","german":"In the morning, little birds were chirping outside the window.","furigana":"あさ|まど|そと|ことり|さえず"},{"japanese":"春の森では鳥たちが楽しそうに囀る。","romaji":"Haru no mori de wa toritachi ga tanoshisō ni saezuru.","german":"In the spring forest, the birds chirp happily.","furigana":"はる|もり|とり|たの|さえず"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Ichidan verb: clear and bright (冴えた月, a bright moon), mentally alert (目が冴える = to be unable to fall asleep); negated 冴えない = dull, gloomy.","examples":[{"japanese":"コーヒーを飲みすぎて、目が冴えて眠れない。","romaji":"Kōhī o nomisugite, me ga saete nemurenai.","german":"I drank too much coffee and I'm wide awake and can't sleep.","furigana":"の|め|さ|ねむ"},{"japanese":"今日は顔色が冴えないね。どうしたの。","romaji":"Kyō wa kaoiro ga saenai ne. Dō shita no.","german":"You look pale today. What's wrong?","furigana":"きょう|かおいろ|さ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A long, thin pole: 釣り竿 (fishing rod), 物干し竿 (laundry pole).","examples":[{"japanese":"洗濯物を竿に干した。","romaji":"Sentakumono o sao ni hoshita.","german":"I hung the laundry on the pole.","furigana":"せんたくもの|さお|ほ"},{"japanese":"竿がしなって、大きな魚がかかった。","romaji":"Sao ga shinatte, ōkina sakana ga kakatta.","german":"The rod bent, and a big fish was hooked.","furigana":"さお|おお|さかな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The difference between two amounts: 差額を払う, 差額ベッド (surcharge for a better hospital room).","examples":[{"japanese":"安い商品に交換したので、差額が返ってきた。","romaji":"Yasui shōhin ni kōkan shita node, sagaku ga kaette kita.","german":"I exchanged it for a cheaper product and got the difference back.","furigana":"やす|しょうひん|こうかん|さがく|かえ"},{"japanese":"大きい部屋に変えるなら、差額をお支払いください。","romaji":"Ōkii heya ni kaeru nara, sagaku o oshiharai kudasai.","german":"If you change to a larger room, please pay the difference.","furigana":"おお|へや|か|さがく|しはら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A small cup for sake; also the ritual exchange of cups as a sign of a bond (杯を交わす). As a counter, however, 杯 is read はい.","examples":[{"japanese":"祖父は杯に酒をついで、ゆっくり飲んだ。","romaji":"Sofu wa sakazuki ni sake o tsuide, yukkuri nonda.","german":"My grandfather poured sake into his cup and drank it slowly.","furigana":"そふ|さかずき|さけ|の"},{"japanese":"二人は杯を交わして、兄弟の契りを結んだ。","romaji":"Futari wa sakazuki o kawashite, kyōdai no chigiri o musunda.","german":"The two exchanged cups and swore brotherhood.","furigana":"ふたり|さかずき|か|きょうだい|ちぎ|むす"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Standing on one's hands or head; as a verb 逆立ちする. Idiom: 逆立ちしても (even if one stood on one's head, i.e. impossible).","examples":[{"japanese":"体育の時間に逆立ちの練習をした。","romaji":"Taiiku no jikan ni sakadachi no renshū o shita.","german":"We practiced handstands in P.E. class.","furigana":"たいいく|じかん|さかだ|れんしゅう"},{"japanese":"逆立ちしても、あの選手には勝てない。","romaji":"Sakadachi shite mo, ano senshu ni wa katenai.","german":"Even if I stood on my head, I could never beat that player.","furigana":"さかだ|せんしゅ|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Earlier in time (先に述べたように = as already mentioned) or first, before others: お先に失礼します.","examples":[{"japanese":"先に述べたように、この計画には問題がある。","romaji":"Saki ni nobeta yō ni, kono keikaku ni wa mondai ga aru.","german":"As stated earlier, this plan has problems.","furigana":"さき|の|けいかく|もんだい"},{"japanese":"疲れたので、先に寝ます。","romaji":"Tsukareta node, saki ni nemasu.","german":"I'm tired, so I'll go to bed first.","furigana":"つか|さき|ね"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 34 (source: N4; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"A work (ピカソの作 = a work by Picasso); also the crop yield (今年は米の作がいい). As a suffix: 〜作 (最新作, 代表作).","examples":[{"japanese":"この絵はピカソの作だと言われている。","romaji":"Kono e wa Pikaso no saku da to iwarete iru.","german":"This painting is said to be a work by Picasso.","furigana":"え|さく|い"},{"japanese":"今年は天気がよく、米の作がいい。","romaji":"Kotoshi wa tenki ga yoku, kome no saku ga ii.","german":"The weather was good this year, so the rice harvest is good.","furigana":"ことし|てんき|こめ|さく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A barrier made of posts, slats or bars: 柵を作る, 柵を越える.","examples":[{"japanese":"牧場の周りに柵がある。","romaji":"Bokujō no mawari ni saku ga aru.","german":"There is a fence around the pasture.","furigana":"ぼくじょう|まわ|さく"},{"japanese":"危ないので、柵を越えないでください。","romaji":"Abunai node, saku o koenai de kudasai.","german":"It is dangerous, so please do not climb over the railing.","furigana":"あぶ|さく|こ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Literary word for insignificant things: 些事にこだわる. Homophone: さじ (spoon).","examples":[{"japanese":"些事にこだわらず、全体を見るべきだ。","romaji":"Saji ni kodawarazu, zentai o miru beki da.","german":"You should not fuss over trifles but look at the whole picture.","furigana":"さじ|ぜんたい|み"},{"japanese":"彼は些事にまで口を出す。","romaji":"Kare wa saji ni made kuchi o dasu.","german":"He meddles even in trivial matters.","furigana":"かれ|さじ|くち|だ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Intransitive godan verb with に: to come to a place (橋に差し掛かる) or enter a phase (交渉が山場に差し掛かる).","examples":[{"japanese":"車が橋に差し掛かったとき、雨が降り出した。","romaji":"Kuruma ga hashi ni sashikakatta toki, ame ga furidashita.","german":"When the car came to the bridge, it started to rain.","furigana":"くるま|はし|さ|か|あめ|ふ|だ"},{"japanese":"交渉はいよいよ最終段階に差し掛かった。","romaji":"Kōshō wa iyoiyo saishū dankai ni sashikakatta.","german":"The negotiations have finally reached their final stage.","furigana":"こうしょう|さいしゅうだんかい|さ|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Giving instructions, often with the nuance of \"bossing people around\": 人の指図は受けない. As a verb 指図する.","examples":[{"japanese":"私は誰の指図も受けない。","romaji":"Watashi wa dare no sashizu mo ukenai.","german":"I take orders from no one.","furigana":"わたし|だれ|さしず|う"},{"japanese":"彼は何もしないで、人に指図ばかりしている。","romaji":"Kare wa nani mo shinai de, hito ni sashizu bakari shite iru.","german":"He does nothing himself and only gives orders to others.","furigana":"かれ|なに|ひと|さしず"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive godan verb: to stretch out one's hand, to hand over or submit something; 差出人 is the sender.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は笑顔で手を差し出した。","romaji":"Kare wa egao de te o sashidashita.","german":"He held out his hand with a smile.","furigana":"かれ|えがお|て|さ|だ"},{"japanese":"受付で名刺を差し出した。","romaji":"Uketsuke de meishi o sashidashita.","german":"I handed over my business card at the reception desk.","furigana":"うけつけ|めいし|さ|だ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive godan verb: to rub gently over something with the hand, usually written in kana: 背中をさする.","examples":[{"japanese":"母は泣いている子どもの背中を摩った。","romaji":"Haha wa naite iru kodomo no senaka o sasutta.","german":"The mother stroked her crying child's back.","furigana":"はは|な|こ|せなか|さす"},{"japanese":"ぶつけた膝を手で摩った。","romaji":"Butsuketa hiza o te de sasutta.","german":"I rubbed my bumped knee with my hand.","furigana":"ひざ|て|さす"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Sympathetic conjecture, usually with だろう or でしょう: さぞ大変だったでしょう. Stronger forms: さぞかし, さぞや.","examples":[{"japanese":"長い旅で、さぞお疲れでしょう。","romaji":"Nagai tabi de, sazo otsukare deshō.","german":"After such a long journey, you must be very tired.","furigana":"なが|たび|つか"},{"japanese":"合格したと聞いて、ご両親もさぞ喜んだことだろう。","romaji":"Gōkaku shita to kiite, goryōshin mo sazo yorokonda koto darō.","german":"Having heard that you passed, your parents must have been very happy.","furigana":"ごうかく|き|りょうしん|よろこ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Intransitive godan verb: 方針が定まる, 狙いが定まらない. Transitive: 定める.","examples":[{"japanese":"会社の方針がようやく定まった。","romaji":"Kaisha no hōshin ga yōyaku sadamatta.","german":"The company's policy has finally been decided.","furigana":"かいしゃ|ほうしん|さだ"},{"japanese":"手が震えて、狙いが定まらない。","romaji":"Te ga furuete, nerai ga sadamaranai.","german":"My hand is shaking and I cannot aim steadily.","furigana":"て|ふる|ねら|さだ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"An informal discussion among several people on a topic, often for magazines or events.","examples":[{"japanese":"雑誌の座談会に作家が三人参加した。","romaji":"Zasshi no zadankai ni sakka ga sannin sanka shita.","german":"Three writers took part in the magazine's round-table discussion.","furigana":"ざっし|ざだんかい|さっか|さんにんさんか"},{"japanese":"留学生を招いて座談会を開いた。","romaji":"Ryūgakusei o maneite zadankai o hiraita.","german":"We invited international students and held a round-table discussion.","furigana":"りゅうがくせい|まね|ざだんかい|ひら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"な-adjective: done without care: 仕事が雑だ, 雑な字. As a prefix 雑〜 it means \"miscellaneous, various\" (雑貨, 雑談).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼の仕事は速いけれど、雑だ。","romaji":"Kare no shigoto wa hayai keredo, zatsu da.","german":"His work is fast, but sloppy.","furigana":"かれ|しごと|はや|ざつ"},{"japanese":"字が雑で、何と書いてあるか読めない。","romaji":"Ji ga zatsu de, nan to kaite aru ka yomenai.","german":"The handwriting is so sloppy that I cannot read what it says.","furigana":"じ|ざつ|なん|か|よ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Various everyday items; 雑貨屋 are nowadays often shops for decor and design goods.","examples":[{"japanese":"駅前にかわいい雑貨の店ができた。","romaji":"Ekimae ni kawaii zakka no mise ga dekita.","german":"A cute general goods shop has opened in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|ざっか|みせ"},{"japanese":"この店では食品から雑貨まで何でも売っている。","romaji":"Kono mise de wa shokuhin kara zakka made nan demo utte iru.","german":"This store sells everything from food to sundries.","furigana":"みせ|しょくひん|ざっか|なん|う"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A casual conversation without a set topic; as a verb 雑談する.","examples":[{"japanese":"会議の前に少し雑談をした。","romaji":"Kaigi no mae ni sukoshi zatsudan o shita.","german":"We chatted a little before the meeting.","furigana":"かいぎ|まえ|すこ|ざつだん"},{"japanese":"授業中の雑談はやめてください。","romaji":"Jugyōchū no zatsudan wa yamete kudasai.","german":"Please stop chatting during class.","furigana":"じゅぎょうちゅう|ざつだん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Literary: news about how someone is and where they are. 消息を絶つ (to cut off all contact, to go missing), 消息不明 (whereabouts unknown), 消息筋 (well-informed sources).","examples":[{"japanese":"登山に出かけたまま、彼は消息を絶った。","romaji":"Tozan ni dekaketa mama, kare wa shōsoku o tatta.","german":"He went off on a mountain climb and has not been heard from since.","furigana":"とざん|で|かれ|しょうそく|た"},{"japanese":"卒業してから、田中さんの消息はまったく聞かない。","romaji":"Sotsugyō shite kara, Tanaka-san no shōsoku wa mattaku kikanai.","german":"I have heard nothing at all about Tanaka since graduation.","furigana":"そつぎょう|たなか|しょうそく|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"What lies behind a mask or appearance: 正体を現す (to show one's true colors), 正体不明 (of unknown identity/origin). Not to be confused with 招待 (invitation), which is read the same.","examples":[{"japanese":"ついに犯人が正体を現した。","romaji":"Tsui ni hannin ga shōtai o arawashita.","german":"The culprit finally revealed his true colors.","furigana":"はんにん|しょうたい|あらわ"},{"japanese":"夜中に聞こえた音の正体は、隣の家の猫だった。","romaji":"Yonaka ni kikoeta oto no shōtai wa, tonari no ie no neko datta.","german":"The source of the sound heard in the middle of the night turned out to be the neighbors' cat.","furigana":"よなか|き|おと|しょうたい|となり|いえ|ねこ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A person employed by someone, especially as household staff. Sounds old-fashioned today; for company employees one says 社員 or 従業員.","examples":[{"japanese":"その屋敷には十人の使用人がいた。","romaji":"Sono yashiki ni wa jūnin no shiyōnin ga ita.","german":"There were ten servants in that mansion.","furigana":"やしき|じゅうにん|しようにん"},{"japanese":"彼は使用人を家族のように大切にした。","romaji":"Kare wa shiyōnin o kazoku no yō ni taisetsu ni shita.","german":"He treated his servants as carefully as if they were family.","furigana":"かれ|しようにん|かぞく|たいせつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"〜する: to go ashore from a ship (passengers, troops). Also of a typhoon hitting land: 台風が上陸する.","examples":[{"japanese":"台風十号が今夜、九州に上陸する見込みです。","romaji":"Taifū jūgō ga kon'ya, Kyūshū ni jōriku suru mikomi desu.","german":"Typhoon No. 10 is expected to make landfall in Kyushu tonight.","furigana":"たいふうじゅうごう|こんや|きゅうしゅう|じょうりく|みこ"},{"japanese":"船は港に着き、乗客は島に上陸した。","romaji":"Fune wa minato ni tsuki, jōkyaku wa shima ni jōriku shita.","german":"The ship reached the harbor and the passengers went ashore on the island.","furigana":"ふね|みなと|つ|じょうきゃく|しま|じょうりく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"〜する: to (officially) promote something and urge people to do it, especially authorities, companies, schools. 奨励金 = incentive payment, grant.","examples":[{"japanese":"会社は社員に自転車通勤を奨励している。","romaji":"Kaisha wa shain ni jitensha tsūkin o shōrei shite iru.","german":"The company encourages its employees to commute by bicycle.","furigana":"かいしゃ|しゃいん|じてんしゃつうきん|しょうれい"},{"japanese":"政府は省エネを奨励するため、補助金を出している。","romaji":"Seifu wa shōene o shōrei suru tame, hojokin o dashite iru.","german":"The government is providing subsidies to promote energy saving.","furigana":"せいふ|しょう|しょうれい|ほじょきん|だ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Formal: the tasks that belong to a post. 職務を果たす (to carry out one's duties), 職務質問 (police questioning on the street).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は最後まで職務を果たした。","romaji":"Kare wa saigo made shokumu o hatashita.","german":"He fulfilled his duties to the end.","furigana":"かれ|さいご|しょくむ|は"},{"japanese":"職務中の飲酒は禁止されている。","romaji":"Shokumuchū no inshu wa kinshi sarete iru.","german":"Drinking alcohol on duty is prohibited.","furigana":"しょくむちゅう|いんしゅ|きんし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"〜する: to have something on one's person or to own it; typical of official and legal language. 所持品 (belongings carried), 所持金 (cash one has on hand).","examples":[{"japanese":"入国の際はパスポートを所持していなければならない。","romaji":"Nyūkoku no sai wa pasupōto o shoji shite inakereba naranai.","german":"You must carry your passport when entering the country.","furigana":"にゅうこく|さい|しょじ"},{"japanese":"男は刃物を所持していたとして逮捕された。","romaji":"Otoko wa hamono o shoji shite ita to shite taiho sareta.","german":"The man was arrested for carrying a bladed weapon.","furigana":"おとこ|はもの|しょじ|たいほ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Placed after the name of a distinguished woman (scholar, artist, politician): 山田女史. Rather old-fashioned and literary today. Not to be confused with 女子 (girl, women's), which is read the same.","examples":[{"japanese":"講演会では、鈴木女史が環境問題について話した。","romaji":"Kōenkai de wa, Suzuki joshi ga kankyō mondai ni tsuite hanashita.","german":"At the lecture, Ms. Suzuki spoke about environmental issues.","furigana":"こうえんかい|すずきじょし|かんきょうもんだい|はな"},{"japanese":"会議の議長はブラウン女史が務めた。","romaji":"Kaigi no gichō wa Buraun joshi ga tsutometa.","german":"Ms. Brown served as chair of the meeting.","furigana":"かいぎ|ぎちょう|じょし|つと"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Emotionally: ショックを受ける (to be shocked), ショックだ (that is a shock). Physically/technically: impact, jolt.","examples":[{"japanese":"試験に落ちて、大きなショックを受けた。","romaji":"Shiken ni ochite, ōkina shokku o uketa.","german":"I was greatly shocked by failing the exam.","furigana":"しけん|お|おお|う"},{"japanese":"着地のショックで、ひざを痛めた。","romaji":"Chakuchi no shokku de, hiza o itameta.","german":"I hurt my knee from the impact of landing.","furigana":"ちゃくち|いた"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Colloquial: again and again, incessantly. More formal: 頻繁に or 常に.","examples":[{"japanese":"弟はしょっちゅう鍵をなくす。","romaji":"Otōto wa shotchū kagi o nakusu.","german":"My little brother is constantly losing his keys.","furigana":"おとうと|かぎ"},{"japanese":"彼女とはしょっちゅう電話で話している。","romaji":"Kanojo to wa shotchū denwa de hanashite iru.","german":"I talk with her on the phone all the time.","furigana":"かのじょ|でんわ|はな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"〜する/〜される: to punish officially. More general: 罰.","examples":[{"japanese":"規則を破った者は処罰される。","romaji":"Kisoku o yabutta mono wa shobatsu sareru.","german":"Those who break the rules will be punished.","furigana":"きそく|やぶ|もの|しょばつ"},{"japanese":"飲酒運転に対する処罰が厳しくなった。","romaji":"Inshu unten ni taisuru shobatsu ga kibishiku natta.","german":"Penalties for drunk driving have become stricter.","furigana":"いんしゅうんてん|たい|しょばつ|きび"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"〜する: to own, especially in the legal sense. 所有者 (owner), 所有権 (right of ownership).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は都内に土地を所有している。","romaji":"Kare wa tonai ni tochi o shoyū shite iru.","german":"He owns land in Tokyo.","furigana":"かれ|とない|とち|しょゆう"},{"japanese":"東京では車を所有しない人が多い。","romaji":"Tōkyō de wa kuruma o shoyū shinai hito ga ōi.","german":"Many people in Tokyo do not own a car.","furigana":"とうきょう|くるま|しょゆう|ひと|おお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Noun of 調べる: 警察の調べによると (according to the police investigation), 取り調べ (interrogation). In elevated usage also \"melody, sound\": ピアノの調べ.","examples":[{"japanese":"警察の調べによると、火事の原因はたばこだった。","romaji":"Keisatsu no shirabe ni yoru to, kaji no gen'in wa tabako datta.","german":"According to the police investigation, the cause of the fire was a cigarette.","furigana":"けいさつ|しら|かじ|げんいん"},{"japanese":"静かな夜に、美しいピアノの調べが流れてきた。","romaji":"Shizuka na yoru ni, utsukushii piano no shirabe ga nagarete kita.","german":"In the quiet night, a beautiful piano melody came drifting in.","furigana":"しず|よる|うつく|しら|なが"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 47 (source: N4; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"Transitive, literary for 書く: to note down or put something in writing. 名前を記す, 日記に記す.","examples":[{"japanese":"用紙に名前と住所を記してください。","romaji":"Yōshi ni namae to jūsho o shirushite kudasai.","german":"Please write your name and address on the form.","furigana":"ようし|なまえ|じゅうしょ|しる"},{"japanese":"この本には、戦争中の生活が詳しく記されている。","romaji":"Kono hon ni wa, sensōchū no seikatsu ga kuwashiku shirusarete iru.","german":"This book describes life during the war in detail.","furigana":"ほん|せんそうちゅう|せいかつ|くわ|しる"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Instruction from above (headquarters, authorities, command): 指令を出す/受ける. Not to be confused with 司令 (command, 司令官 = commander), which is read the same.","examples":[{"japanese":"本部から撤退の指令が出た。","romaji":"Honbu kara tettai no shirei ga deta.","german":"An order to withdraw came from headquarters.","furigana":"ほんぶ|てったい|しれい|で"},{"japanese":"彼らは上からの指令を受けて動いている。","romaji":"Karera wa ue kara no shirei o ukete ugoite iru.","german":"They are acting on instructions from above.","furigana":"かれ|うえ|しれい|う|うご"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Prefix before Sino-Japanese words and loanwords: 新製品 (new product), 新学期 (new school year/semester), 新メンバー. As a free adjective one says 新しい.","examples":[{"japanese":"来月、新製品が発売される。","romaji":"Raigetsu, shinseihin ga hatsubai sareru.","german":"A new product will be released next month.","furigana":"らいげつ|しんせいひん|はつばい"},{"japanese":"チームの新メンバーを紹介します。","romaji":"Chīmu no shinmenbā o shōkai shimasu.","german":"Let me introduce the team's new member.","furigana":"しん|しょうかい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"〜する: to publicly promote and revitalize a field: 産業振興, 地域振興, 観光振興. Not to be confused with 新興 (emerging), which is read the same.","examples":[{"japanese":"町は観光の振興に力を入れている。","romaji":"Machi wa kankō no shinkō ni chikara o irete iru.","german":"The town is putting effort into promoting tourism.","furigana":"まち|かんこう|しんこう|ちから|い"},{"japanese":"県は地元の産業を振興するための計画を発表した。","romaji":"Ken wa jimoto no sangyō o shinkō suru tame no keikaku o happyō shita.","german":"The prefecture announced a plan to promote local industry.","furigana":"けん|じもと|さんぎょう|しんこう|けいかく|はっぴょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mostly before nouns: 新興国 (emerging country), 新興企業 (young company), 新興住宅地 (new housing development). Not to be confused with 振興 (promotion), which is read the same.","examples":[{"japanese":"新興のネット企業が急成長している。","romaji":"Shinkō no netto kigyō ga kyūseichō shite iru.","german":"Emerging internet companies are growing rapidly.","furigana":"しんこう|きぎょう|きゅうせいちょう"},{"japanese":"新興国の経済は、今後も成長が見込まれる。","romaji":"Shinkōkoku no keizai wa, kongo mo seichō ga mikomareru.","german":"Growth is expected to continue in the economies of emerging countries.","furigana":"しんこうこく|けいざい|こんご|せいちょう|みこ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"With だ/の or before nouns: 新婚夫婦 (newlywed couple), 新婚旅行 (honeymoon), 新婚生活 (newlywed life).","examples":[{"japanese":"二人はまだ新婚なので、毎日楽しそうだ。","romaji":"Futari wa mada shinkon na node, mainichi tanoshisō da.","german":"The two are still newlyweds, so they seem happy every day.","furigana":"ふたり|しんこん|まいにちたの"},{"japanese":"新婚旅行はハワイに行った。","romaji":"Shinkon ryokō wa Hawai ni itta.","german":"We went to Hawaii for our honeymoon.","furigana":"しんこんりょこう|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A follower of a religion: キリスト教の信者. Colloquially also for unconditional fans of a person or brand.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は熱心な仏教の信者だ。","romaji":"Kare wa nesshin na bukkyō no shinja da.","german":"He is a devout Buddhist.","furigana":"かれ|ねっしん|ぶっきょう|しんじゃ"},{"japanese":"そのアイドルの信者は、何を言われても彼女をかばう。","romaji":"Sono aidoru no shinja wa, nani o iwarete mo kanojo o kabau.","german":"That idol's devoted fans defend her no matter what is said.","furigana":"しんじゃ|なに|い|かのじょ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"〜する. A joint suicide of two lovers or of an entire family (一家心中). With the reading しんちゅう, however, 心中 means \"in one's heart, inner feelings\".","examples":[{"japanese":"江戸時代には、心中を題材にした芝居が多く作られた。","romaji":"Edo jidai ni wa, shinjū o daizai ni shita shibai ga ōku tsukurareta.","german":"In the Edo period, many plays were written about the double suicide of lovers.","furigana":"えどじだい|しんじゅう|だいざい|しばい|おお|つく"},{"japanese":"事件は一家心中とみられている。","romaji":"Jiken wa ikka shinjū to mirarete iru.","german":"The incident is believed to be a family suicide.","furigana":"じけん|いっかしんじゅう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"〜する: to advance into a new area: 海外に進出する (to expand overseas), 政界に進出する (to enter politics); in sports: 決勝に進出する (to advance to the final).","examples":[{"japanese":"その会社は来年、アジア市場に進出する予定だ。","romaji":"Sono kaisha wa rainen, Ajia shijō ni shinshutsu suru yotei da.","german":"The company plans to expand into the Asian market next year.","furigana":"かいしゃ|らいねん|しじょう|しんしゅつ|よてい"},{"japanese":"日本代表は決勝に進出した。","romaji":"Nihon daihyō wa kesshō ni shinshutsu shita.","german":"The Japanese national team advanced to the final.","furigana":"にほんだいひょう|けっしょう|しんしゅつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Formal: what someone feels inside. 心情を察する (to empathize with someone's feelings), 心情的には (emotionally speaking).","examples":[{"japanese":"被害者の家族の心情を思うと、胸が痛む。","romaji":"Higaisha no kazoku no shinjō o omou to, mune ga itamu.","german":"Thinking of the feelings of the victims' families, my heart aches.","furigana":"ひがいしゃ|かぞく|しんじょう|おも|むね|いた"},{"japanese":"心情的には彼の味方をしたいが、規則は守らなければならない。","romaji":"Shinjōteki ni wa kare no mikata o shitai ga, kisoku wa mamoranakereba naranai.","german":"Emotionally I want to side with him, but the rules must be followed.","furigana":"しんじょうてき|かれ|みかた|きそく|まも"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"New to a company, team or industry: 新人社員 (new employee), 新人研修 (training for new employees), 新人賞 (newcomer award).","examples":[{"japanese":"今年の新人はみんなまじめだ。","romaji":"Kotoshi no shinjin wa minna majime da.","german":"This year's newcomers are all serious.","furigana":"ことし|しんじん"},{"japanese":"彼女はデビュー作で新人賞を受賞した。","romaji":"Kanojo wa debyūsaku de shinjinshō o jushō shita.","german":"She won the newcomer award for her debut work.","furigana":"かのじょ|さく|しんじんしょう|じゅしょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mostly in compounds: 国際親善 (international goodwill), 親善試合 (friendly match), 親善大使 (goodwill ambassador).","examples":[{"japanese":"両国の親善を深めるため、交流会が開かれた。","romaji":"Ryōkoku no shinzen o fukameru tame, kōryūkai ga hirakareta.","german":"An exchange meeting was held to deepen friendship between the two countries.","furigana":"りょうこく|しんぜん|ふか|こうりゅうかい|ひら"},{"japanese":"日本とブラジルのサッカーの親善試合が行われた。","romaji":"Nihon to Burajiru no sakkā no shinzen jiai ga okonawareta.","german":"A friendly soccer match between Japan and Brazil was played.","furigana":"にほん|しんぜんじあい|おこな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"What really lies behind an event: 事件の真相, 真相を明らかにする/究明する. More general: 真実 (truth).","examples":[{"japanese":"事件の真相はまだ分かっていない。","romaji":"Jiken no shinsō wa mada wakatte inai.","german":"The truth about the incident is still unknown.","furigana":"じけん|しんそう|わ"},{"japanese":"記者は事故の真相を明らかにしようとした。","romaji":"Kisha wa jiko no shinsō o akiraka ni shiyō to shita.","german":"The reporter tried to uncover the truth about the accident.","furigana":"きしゃ|じこ|しんそう|あき"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Technical/formal: 人体への影響 (effects on humans), 人体実験 (human experimentation). In everyday speech: 体.","examples":[{"japanese":"この程度の放射線なら、人体への影響はない。","romaji":"Kono teido no hōshasen nara, jintai e no eikyō wa nai.","german":"At this level of radiation, there is no effect on the human body.","furigana":"ていど|ほうしゃせん|じんたい|えいきょう"},{"japanese":"人体の約六割は水分でできている。","romaji":"Jintai no yaku rokuwari wa suibun de dekite iru.","german":"About 60 percent of the human body is made of water.","furigana":"じんたい|やくろくわり|すいぶん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"〜する: a matter moves forward: 交渉が進展する, 捜査に進展がない (no progress in the investigation), 事態の進展 (development of the situation).","examples":[{"japanese":"交渉は少しずつ進展している。","romaji":"Kōshō wa sukoshi zutsu shinten shite iru.","german":"The negotiations are progressing little by little.","furigana":"こうしょう|すこ|しんてん"},{"japanese":"捜査に大きな進展があった。","romaji":"Sōsa ni ōkina shinten ga atta.","german":"There was major progress in the investigation.","furigana":"そうさ|おお|しんてん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A building in which a deity is worshipped, such as an ancient temple (パルテノン神殿) or a shrine building. Buddhist temples are called 寺.","examples":[{"japanese":"丘の上に古代の神殿の跡が残っている。","romaji":"Oka no ue ni kodai no shinden no ato ga nokotte iru.","german":"The remains of an ancient temple survive on the hill.","furigana":"おか|うえ|こだい|しんでん|あと|のこ"},{"japanese":"神殿の奥には神の像がまつられていた。","romaji":"Shinden no oku ni wa kami no zō ga matsurarete ita.","german":"A statue of a god was enshrined at the back of the temple.","furigana":"しんでん|おく|かみ|ぞう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"How far a planned course, especially a lesson plan, has progressed: 授業の進度, 進度が速い/遅れる (the pace is fast / falls behind). Not to be confused with 震度 (seismic intensity), which is read the same.","examples":[{"japanese":"このクラスは授業の進度が速い。","romaji":"Kono kurasu wa jugyō no shindo ga hayai.","german":"The lessons in this class move at a fast pace.","furigana":"じゅぎょう|しんど|はや"},{"japanese":"生徒の理解に合わせて、進度を調整した。","romaji":"Seito no rikai ni awasete, shindo o chōsei shita.","german":"I adjusted the pace to the students' understanding.","furigana":"せいと|りかい|あ|しんど|ちょうせい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A new student at a school or university (in Japan usually from April). New company employees are called 新入社員.","examples":[{"japanese":"四月になると、キャンパスは新入生でいっぱいになる。","romaji":"Shigatsu ni naru to, kyanpasu wa shinnyūsei de ippai ni naru.","german":"In April the campus is full of new students.","furigana":"しがつ|しんにゅうせい"},{"japanese":"新入生の歓迎会が体育館で開かれた。","romaji":"Shinnyūsei no kangeikai ga taiikukan de hirakareta.","german":"The welcome party for new students was held in the gymnasium.","furigana":"しんにゅうせい|かんげいかい|たいいくかん|ひら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Formal, especially in politics and organizations: 信任を得る (to win confidence), 信任投票 (vote of confidence), 不信任案 (no-confidence motion).","examples":[{"japanese":"社長は株主の信任を得て、続投が決まった。","romaji":"Shachō wa kabunushi no shinnin o ete, zokutō ga kimatta.","german":"The president won the shareholders' confidence and it was decided he would stay on.","furigana":"しゃちょう|かぶぬし|しんにん|え|ぞくとう|き"},{"japanese":"国民の信任を失った内閣は、長く続かない。","romaji":"Kokumin no shinnin o ushinatta naikaku wa, nagaku tsuzukanai.","german":"A cabinet that has lost the people's confidence will not last long.","furigana":"こくみん|しんにん|うしな|ないかく|なが|つづ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The people of a state, often with a political tone: 中華人民共和国 (People's Republic of China). More neutral: 国民, 市民.","examples":[{"japanese":"「人民の、人民による、人民のための政治」はリンカーンの有名な言葉だ。","romaji":"\"Jinmin no, jinmin ni yoru, jinmin no tame no seiji\" wa Rinkān no yūmei na kotoba da.","german":"\"Government of the people, by the people, for the people\" is a famous saying of Lincoln's.","furigana":"じんみん|じんみん|じんみん|せいじ|ゆうめい|ことば"},{"japanese":"革命は人民の力によって成功した。","romaji":"Kakumei wa jinmin no chikara ni yotte seikō shita.","german":"The revolution succeeded through the power of the people.","furigana":"かくめい|じんみん|ちから|せいこう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"〜する. 診療時間 (consultation hours), 診療所 (clinic, small medical practice), 休診 (no consultation hours).","examples":[{"japanese":"この病院は土曜日も診療している。","romaji":"Kono byōin wa doyōbi mo shinryō shite iru.","german":"This hospital is open for consultations on Saturdays too.","furigana":"びょういん|どようび|しんりょう"},{"japanese":"医師が足りず、夜間の診療ができなくなった。","romaji":"Ishi ga tarizu, yakan no shinryō ga dekinaku natta.","german":"Because of a shortage of doctors, treatment at night is no longer possible.","furigana":"いし|た|やかん|しんりょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The direction in which something moves: 台風の進路. At school: 進路を決める (to decide on further study or a career), 進路相談 (academic and career counseling).","examples":[{"japanese":"台風は進路を北に変えた。","romaji":"Taifū wa shinro o kita ni kaeta.","german":"The typhoon changed its course to the north.","furigana":"たいふう|しんろ|きた|か"},{"japanese":"卒業後の進路について、先生と相談した。","romaji":"Sotsugyōgo no shinro ni tsuite, sensei to sōdan shita.","german":"I talked with my teacher about my path after graduation.","furigana":"そつぎょうご|しんろ|せんせい|そうだん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"〜する: to rinse with water. Mostly in 水洗トイレ (flush toilet). Read the same as 推薦 (recommendation) and 水仙 (narcissus).","examples":[{"japanese":"汚れた部品を水洗してから乾かす。","romaji":"Yogoreta buhin o suisen shite kara kawakasu.","german":"Dirty parts are rinsed with water and then dried.","furigana":"よご|ぶひん|すいせん|かわ"},{"japanese":"この村でも、ようやくトイレが水洗になった。","romaji":"Kono mura de mo, yōyaku toire ga suisen ni natta.","german":"This village has finally gotten flush toilets too.","furigana":"むら|すいせん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"〜する: to play a wind instrument, formal. Common in 吹奏楽 (wind music) and 吹奏楽部 (school wind band club).","examples":[{"japanese":"式の始まりに、国歌が吹奏された。","romaji":"Shiki no hajimari ni, kokka ga suisō sareta.","german":"The national anthem was played by wind instruments at the start of the ceremony.","furigana":"しき|はじ|こっか|すいそう"},{"japanese":"高校では吹奏楽部でトランペットを吹いていた。","romaji":"Kōkō de wa suisōgakubu de toranpetto o fuite ita.","german":"In high school I played trumpet in the wind band.","furigana":"こうこう|すいそうがくぶ|ふ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Formal and technical word for 田んぼ.","examples":[{"japanese":"車窓から、どこまでも続く水田が見えた。","romaji":"Shasō kara, doko made mo tsuzuku suiden ga mieta.","german":"From the train window I could see paddy fields stretching endlessly.","furigana":"しゃそう|つづ|すいでん|み"},{"japanese":"この地域では、水田の面積が年々減っている。","romaji":"Kono chiiki de wa, suiden no menseki ga nennen hette iru.","german":"In this region, the area of rice paddies is shrinking year by year.","furigana":"ちいき|すいでん|めんせき|ねんねんへ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Noun form of 救う: 救いを求める (to ask for help). Often: 〜のが（せめてもの）救いだ (... is at least a consolation).","examples":[{"japanese":"山で遭難した人たちは、救いを求めて叫んだ。","romaji":"Yama de sōnan shita hitotachi wa, sukui o motomete sakenda.","german":"The people in distress in the mountains cried out for help.","furigana":"やま|そうなん|ひと|すく|もと|さけ"},{"japanese":"けが人が出なかったのが、せめてもの救いだ。","romaji":"Keganin ga denakatta no ga, semete mo no sukui da.","german":"The fact that nobody was hurt is at least a saving grace.","furigana":"にん|で|すく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive: to rinse with clean water (laundry, dishes). The kanji 濯ぐ is rare; ゆすぐ means the same.","examples":[{"japanese":"洗剤が残らないように、よくすすいでください。","romaji":"Senzai ga nokoranai yō ni, yoku susuide kudasai.","german":"Please rinse well so that no detergent remains.","furigana":"せんざい|のこ"},{"japanese":"洗ったシャツを水ですすいでから干した。","romaji":"Aratta shatsu o mizu de susuide kara hoshita.","german":"I rinsed the washed shirt in water and then hung it up to dry.","furigana":"あら|みず|ほ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Noun form of 進む: 進みが早い/遅い (progresses quickly/slowly).","examples":[{"japanese":"工事の進みが思ったより遅い。","romaji":"Kōji no susumi ga omotta yori osoi.","german":"The construction is progressing more slowly than I expected.","furigana":"こうじ|すす|おも|おそ"},{"japanese":"仕事の進みはどうですか。","romaji":"Shigoto no susumi wa dō desu ka.","german":"How is the work coming along?","furigana":"しごと|すす"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The lower edge of clothing: ズボンの裾 (trouser leg end), 裾上げ (hemming trousers). Figuratively: 山の裾 (foot of a mountain).","examples":[{"japanese":"ズボンの裾が長すぎるので、少し短くしてもらった。","romaji":"Zubon no suso ga nagasugiru node, sukoshi mijikaku shite moratta.","german":"The trouser hems were too long, so I had them shortened a little.","furigana":"すそ|なが|すこ|みじか"},{"japanese":"雨の中を歩いたので、スカートの裾がぬれてしまった。","romaji":"Ame no naka o aruita node, sukāto no suso ga nurete shimatta.","german":"I walked in the rain, so the hem of my skirt got wet.","furigana":"あめ|なか|ある|すそ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A recording, TV or photo studio; also a dance or yoga studio. 録音スタジオ, 写真スタジオ.","examples":[{"japanese":"歌手はスタジオで新曲を録音している。","romaji":"Kashu wa sutajio de shinkyoku o rokuon shite iru.","german":"The singer is recording a new song in the studio.","furigana":"かしゅ|しんきょく|ろくおん"},{"japanese":"番組はスタジオから生放送でお送りしています。","romaji":"Bangumi wa sutajio kara namahōsō de ookuri shite imasu.","german":"The program is being broadcast live from the studio.","furigana":"ばんぐみ|なまほうそう|おく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Water vapor, especially in technical and household terms: スチームアイロン (steam iron), スチームオーブン (steam oven). In general: 蒸気, 湯気.","examples":[{"japanese":"シャツのしわをスチームで伸ばした。","romaji":"Shatsu no shiwa o suchīmu de nobashita.","german":"I smoothed the wrinkles out of the shirt with steam.","furigana":"の"},{"japanese":"この機械は高温のスチームで床を掃除する。","romaji":"Kono kikai wa kōon no suchīmu de yuka o sōji suru.","german":"This machine cleans floors with high-temperature steam.","furigana":"きかい|こうおん|ゆか|そうじ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A work stoppage; often shortened to スト. ストライキを行う, ストライキに入る (to go on strike).","examples":[{"japanese":"運転手たちは賃上げを求めてストライキを行った。","romaji":"Untenshutachi wa chin'age o motomete sutoraiki o okonatta.","german":"The drivers went on strike demanding a pay raise.","furigana":"うんてんしゅ|ちんあ|もと|おこな"},{"japanese":"ストライキのため、電車が全線で止まっている。","romaji":"Sutoraiki no tame, densha ga zensen de tomatte iru.","german":"Because of the strike, trains are stopped on all lines.","furigana":"でんしゃ|ぜんせん|と"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A tube for drinking. Straw as a material is called 麦わら.","examples":[{"japanese":"子どもはストローでジュースを飲んだ。","romaji":"Kodomo wa sutorō de jūsu o nonda.","german":"The child drank juice through a straw.","furigana":"こ|の"},{"japanese":"最近は紙のストローを使う店が増えた。","romaji":"Saikin wa kami no sutorō o tsukau mise ga fueta.","german":"Recently, more shops have been using paper straws.","furigana":"さいきん|かみ|つか|みせ|ふ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"In everyday use mainly the camera flash (also フラッシュ): ストロボをたく (to fire the flash). Technically also a stroboscope lamp.","examples":[{"japanese":"館内ではストロボの使用は禁止です。","romaji":"Kannai de wa sutorobo no shiyō wa kinshi desu.","german":"The use of flash is prohibited inside the building.","furigana":"かんない|しよう|きんし"},{"japanese":"暗い部屋だったので、ストロボをたいて写真を撮った。","romaji":"Kurai heya datta node, sutorobo o taite shashin o totta.","german":"It was a dark room, so I used the flash to take the photo.","furigana":"くら|へや|しゃしん|と"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"い-adjective for quick, skillful movements (animals, children, athletes). Colloquially also すばしっこい.","examples":[{"japanese":"ネズミはすばしこくて、なかなか捕まらない。","romaji":"Nezumi wa subashikokute, nakanaka tsukamaranai.","german":"Mice are nimble and hard to catch.","furigana":"つか"},{"japanese":"あの選手は小柄だが、とてもすばしこい。","romaji":"Ano senshu wa kogara da ga, totemo subashikoi.","german":"That player is small but very agile.","furigana":"せんしゅ|こがら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To say something without mincing words or to hit the mark exactly: ずばり言う, ずばり当てる. Often with と: ずばりと指摘する.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は問題点をずばりと指摘した。","romaji":"Kare wa mondaiten o zubari to shiteki shita.","german":"He pointed out the problem bluntly.","furigana":"かれ|もんだいてん|してき"},{"japanese":"彼女は私の年齢をずばり当てた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa watashi no nenrei o zubari ateta.","german":"She guessed my age exactly.","furigana":"かのじょ|わたし|ねんれい|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"スピードを出す (to drive fast), スピードを落とす (to slow down), スピード違反 (speeding violation). Also figurative: 仕事のスピード.","examples":[{"japanese":"カーブの手前でスピードを落としてください。","romaji":"Kābu no temae de supīdo o otoshite kudasai.","german":"Please slow down before the curve.","furigana":"てまえ|お"},{"japanese":"彼は仕事のスピードが速い。","romaji":"Kare wa shigoto no supīdo ga hayai.","german":"He works at a fast pace.","furigana":"かれ|しごと|はや"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 48 (source: N4; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"Mostly ずぶ濡れになる (to get soaked) or ずぶ濡れの + noun. Similar: びしょ濡れ.","examples":[{"japanese":"急な雨で、ずぶ濡れになった。","romaji":"Kyū na ame de, zubunure ni natta.","german":"I got soaked in a sudden rain.","furigana":"きゅう|あめ|ぬ"},{"japanese":"ずぶ濡れの犬が玄関に座っていた。","romaji":"Zubunure no inu ga genkan ni suwatte ita.","german":"A drenched dog was sitting at the entrance.","furigana":"ぬ|いぬ|げんかん|すわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A metal spring in mattresses, sofas, machines; synonymous with ばね.","examples":[{"japanese":"古いソファーのスプリングが壊れてしまった。","romaji":"Furui sofā no supuringu ga kowarete shimatta.","german":"The springs of the old sofa have broken.","furigana":"ふる|こわ"},{"japanese":"マットレスのスプリングが弱くなって、腰が痛い。","romaji":"Mattoresu no supuringu ga yowaku natte, koshi ga itai.","german":"The mattress springs have weakened and my lower back hurts.","furigana":"よわ|こし|いた"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Free room: 駐車スペース (parking space), スペースを空ける (to leave space). In writing and typing: a blank, spacing. For outer space one usually says 宇宙.","examples":[{"japanese":"部屋が狭くて、机を置くスペースがない。","romaji":"Heya ga semakute, tsukue o oku supēsu ga nai.","german":"The room is small and there is no space to put a desk.","furigana":"へや|せま|つくえ|お"},{"japanese":"単語と単語の間にスペースを入れてください。","romaji":"Tango to tango no aida ni supēsu o irete kudasai.","german":"Please put a space between words.","furigana":"たんご|たんご|あいだ|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A fast, sporty car; from English \"sports car\".","examples":[{"japanese":"兄は赤いスポーツカーに乗っている。","romaji":"Ani wa akai supōtsukā ni notte iru.","german":"My older brother drives a red sports car.","furigana":"あに|あか|の"},{"japanese":"いつかスポーツカーで海沿いの道を走りたい。","romaji":"Itsuka supōtsukā de umizoi no michi o hashiritai.","german":"Someday I want to drive along a coastal road in a sports car.","furigana":"うみぞ|みち|はし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Long dress trousers for office or suits, for men and women. In general: ズボン, パンツ.","examples":[{"japanese":"面接には紺のスラックスをはいて行った。","romaji":"Mensetsu ni wa kon no surakkusu o haite itta.","german":"I wore navy slacks to the interview.","furigana":"めんせつ|こん|い"},{"japanese":"このスラックスは家で洗濯できる。","romaji":"Kono surakkusu wa ie de sentaku dekiru.","german":"These slacks can be washed at home.","furigana":"いえ|せんたく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Colloquial variant of ずらりと: many things or people side by side, mostly with 並ぶ.","examples":[{"japanese":"店の前に客がずらっと並んでいる。","romaji":"Mise no mae ni kyaku ga zuratto narande iru.","german":"Customers are lined up in a long row in front of the shop.","furigana":"みせ|まえ|きゃく|なら"},{"japanese":"本棚には古い辞書がずらっと並んでいた。","romaji":"Hondana ni wa furui jisho ga zuratto narande ita.","german":"Old dictionaries were lined up in a row on the bookshelf.","furigana":"ほんだな|ふる|じしょ|なら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Noun form of 刷る: 刷りがきれい/悪い (cleanly/poorly printed). In compounds read ずり: 色刷り (color printing), 試し刷り (proof print). Not to be confused with すり (pickpocket).","examples":[{"japanese":"この本は刷りがきれいで、写真も鮮やかだ。","romaji":"Kono hon wa suri ga kirei de, shashin mo azayaka da.","german":"The printing of this book is beautiful, and the photos are vivid too.","furigana":"ほん|す|しゃしん|あざ"},{"japanese":"古い版画は、刷りによって色の出方が違う。","romaji":"Furui hanga wa, suri ni yotte iro no dekata ga chigau.","german":"With old woodblock prints, the way the colors come out differs from impression to impression.","furigana":"ふる|はんが|す|いろ|でかた|ちが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Noun form of ずれる: 時間のずれ (time lag), 意見のずれ (difference of opinion), 認識のずれ (difference in perception).","examples":[{"japanese":"地震で壁と柱の間にずれが生じた。","romaji":"Jishin de kabe to hashira no aida ni zure ga shōjita.","german":"The earthquake caused a gap between the wall and the pillar.","furigana":"じしん|かべ|はしら|あいだ|しょう"},{"japanese":"二人の考え方には大きなずれがある。","romaji":"Futari no kangaekata ni wa ōkina zure ga aru.","german":"There is a big gap between their ways of thinking.","furigana":"ふたり|かんが|かた|おお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Noun form of すれ違う. Literally: passing one another (cars, pedestrians). Figuratively: constantly missing each other or drifting apart emotionally: 気持ちのすれ違い. Also written 擦れ違い.","examples":[{"japanese":"狭い道なので、車のすれ違いが難しい。","romaji":"Semai michi na node, kuruma no surechigai ga muzukashii.","german":"The road is narrow, so it is hard for cars to pass each other.","furigana":"せま|みち|くるま|ちが|むずか"},{"japanese":"仕事が忙しくて、夫婦のすれ違いが続いている。","romaji":"Shigoto ga isogashikute, fūfu no surechigai ga tsuzuite iru.","german":"The husband and wife keep missing each other because work is busy.","furigana":"しごと|いそが|ふうふ|ちが|つづ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mostly \"without difficulty\": すんなり決まる, すんなり受け入れる. Of body shapes: slender, supple (すんなりした脚).","examples":[{"japanese":"心配していたが、企画はすんなり通った。","romaji":"Shinpai shite ita ga, kikaku wa sunnari tōtta.","german":"I was worried, but the proposal passed smoothly.","furigana":"しんぱい|きかく|とお"},{"japanese":"彼女はすんなりした長い脚をしている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa sunnari shita nagai ashi o shite iru.","german":"She has slender long legs.","furigana":"かのじょ|なが|あし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Suffix after nouns: 会員制 (members only), 予約制 (by reservation only), 二交代制 (two-shift system), 六三制 (school system with six years of elementary and three of middle school).","examples":[{"japanese":"このレストランは完全予約制です。","romaji":"Kono resutoran wa kanzen yoyakusei desu.","german":"This restaurant is reservation-only.","furigana":"かんぜんよやくせい"},{"japanese":"このジムは会員制なので、会員でないと利用できない。","romaji":"Kono jimu wa kaiinsei na node, kaiin de nai to riyō dekinai.","german":"This gym is members-only, so you cannot use it unless you are a member.","furigana":"かいいんせい|かいいん|りよう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"〜する: to grow and thrive, especially of plants: 稲の生育, 生育が悪い (poor growth). For animals 成育 is usually written.","examples":[{"japanese":"今年は天候に恵まれ、野菜の生育が順調だ。","romaji":"Kotoshi wa tenkō ni megumare, yasai no seiiku ga junchō da.","german":"This year the weather has been favorable and the vegetables are growing well.","furigana":"ことし|てんこう|めぐ|やさい|せいいく|じゅんちょう"},{"japanese":"この植物は日陰でもよく生育する。","romaji":"Kono shokubutsu wa hikage de mo yoku seiiku suru.","german":"This plant grows well even in the shade.","furigana":"しょくぶつ|ひかげ|せいいく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mostly with の or in compounds: 正規の手続き (proper procedure), 正規雇用 (permanent employment; opposite 非正規), 正規品 (genuine goods).","examples":[{"japanese":"正規の手続きを経て、ビザを取得した。","romaji":"Seiki no tetsuzuki o hete, biza o shutoku shita.","german":"I obtained the visa through the proper procedures.","furigana":"せいき|てつづ|へ|しゅとく"},{"japanese":"彼は派遣社員から正規の社員になった。","romaji":"Kare wa haken shain kara seiki no shain ni natta.","german":"He went from being a temp worker to a regular employee.","furigana":"かれ|はけんしゃいん|せいき|しゃいん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"生計を立てる (to earn one's living), 一家の生計を支える (to support the family's livelihood). More formal than 暮らし. Read the same as 整形 (cosmetic surgery, orthopedics).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は絵を描いて生計を立てている。","romaji":"Kare wa e o kaite seikei o tatete iru.","german":"He makes his living by painting.","furigana":"かれ|え|か|せいけい|た"},{"japanese":"母がパートで一家の生計を支えていた。","romaji":"Haha ga pāto de ikka no seikei o sasaete ita.","german":"My mother supported the family's livelihood with a part-time job.","furigana":"はは|いっか|せいけい|ささ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"な-adjective: 精巧な細工 (fine handiwork), 精巧な機械 (precision machine), 精巧に作られた偽物 (an elaborately made fake).","examples":[{"japanese":"この時計は精巧な作りで、職人の技術の高さが分かる。","romaji":"Kono tokei wa seikō na tsukuri de, shokunin no gijutsu no takasa ga wakaru.","german":"This watch is exquisitely made, and you can see the craftsman's high skill.","furigana":"とけい|せいこう|つく|しょくにん|ぎじゅつ|たか|わ"},{"japanese":"本物そっくりの精巧な偽札が見つかった。","romaji":"Honmono sokkuri no seikō na nisesatsu ga mitsukatta.","german":"Elaborate counterfeit bills that look just like the real thing were found.","furigana":"ほんもの|せいこう|にせさつ|み"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"生死にかかわる (a matter of life and death, life-threatening), 生死不明 (unknown whether dead or alive).","examples":[{"japanese":"これは患者の生死にかかわる問題だ。","romaji":"Kore wa kanja no seishi ni kakawaru mondai da.","german":"This is a matter of life and death for the patient.","furigana":"かんじゃ|せいし|もんだい"},{"japanese":"事故から三日たっても、乗客の生死は分かっていない。","romaji":"Jiko kara mikka tatte mo, jōkyaku no seishi wa wakatte inai.","german":"Three days after the accident, it is still not known whether the passengers are alive or dead.","furigana":"じこ|みっか|じょうきゃく|せいし|わ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"な-adjective, mostly about young women and their image: 清純な少女, 清純派 (actress with an innocent image).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は清純なイメージで人気の女優だ。","romaji":"Kanojo wa seijun na imēji de ninki no joyū da.","german":"She is an actress popular for her pure image.","furigana":"かのじょ|せいじゅん|にんき|じょゆう"},{"japanese":"その歌は、清純な初恋の気持ちを歌っている。","romaji":"Sono uta wa, seijun na hatsukoi no kimochi o utatte iru.","german":"That song sings of the pure feelings of first love.","furigana":"うた|せいじゅん|はつこい|きも|うた"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive, formal. 騒ぐ人を制する (to quiet noisy people), 試合を制する (to win a match), 市場を制する (to dominate a market). Proverb: 先んずれば人を制す (he who acts first has the upper hand).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は手を上げて、騒ぐ学生たちを制した。","romaji":"Kare wa te o agete, sawagu gakuseitachi o seishita.","german":"He raised his hand and restrained the noisy students.","furigana":"かれ|て|あ|さわ|がくせい|せい"},{"japanese":"延長戦の末、ホームチームが試合を制した。","romaji":"Enchōsen no sue, hōmu chīmu ga shiai o seishita.","german":"After extra time, the home team won the match.","furigana":"えんちょうせん|すえ|しあい|せい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"〜する: to dress especially splendidly (celebration, reception). Read the same as 正装 (prescribed formal dress) and 清掃 (cleaning).","examples":[{"japanese":"パーティーには盛装した客が大勢集まった。","romaji":"Pātī ni wa seisō shita kyaku ga ōzei atsumatta.","german":"Many guests gathered at the party in formal attire.","furigana":"せいそう|きゃく|おおぜいあつ"},{"japanese":"成人式の日、娘は振り袖で盛装して出かけた。","romaji":"Seijinshiki no hi, musume wa furisode de seisō shite dekaketa.","german":"On Coming-of-Age Day, my daughter went out dressed up in a furisode.","furigana":"せいじんしき|ひ|むすめ|ふ|そで|せいそう|で"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mainly in 清濁併せのむ (to accept both the good and the bad, to be broad-minded). In linguistics: 清音 and 濁音, e.g. か and が.","examples":[{"japanese":"大きな組織のリーダーには、清濁併せのむ度量が必要だ。","romaji":"Ōkina soshiki no rīdā ni wa, seidaku awasenomu doryō ga hitsuyō da.","german":"A leader of a large organization needs the generosity to accept both good and bad.","furigana":"おお|そしき||どりょう|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"日本語の仮名には清濁の区別がある。","romaji":"Nihongo no kana ni wa seidaku no kubetsu ga aru.","german":"In Japanese kana there is a distinction between unvoiced and voiced sounds.","furigana":"にほんご|かな|せいだく|くべつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"な-adjective, opposite: 動的 (dynamic). Technical and educated usage: 静的な状態 (static state), 静的なストレッチ (static stretching).","examples":[{"japanese":"この絵は静的な構図で、落ち着いた印象を与える。","romaji":"Kono e wa seiteki na kōzu de, ochitsuita inshō o ataeru.","german":"This painting has a static composition and gives a calm impression.","furigana":"え|せいてき|こうず|お|つ|いんしょう|あた"},{"japanese":"静的なストレッチは、運動の後に行うとよい。","romaji":"Seiteki na sutoretchi wa, undō no ato ni okonau to yoi.","german":"Static stretching is best done after exercise.","furigana":"せいてき|うんどう|あと|おこな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"製鉄所 (ironworks, steel mill), 製鉄業 (iron and steel industry).","examples":[{"japanese":"この町は製鉄で栄えた。","romaji":"Kono machi wa seitetsu de sakaeta.","german":"This town flourished on ironmaking.","furigana":"まち|せいてつ|さか"},{"japanese":"製鉄には大量の石炭が使われる。","romaji":"Seitetsu ni wa tairyō no sekitan ga tsukawareru.","german":"A large amount of coal is used in ironmaking.","furigana":"せいてつ|たいりょう|せきたん|つか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Formal, e.g. in weather reports: 晴天に恵まれる (to be blessed with fine weather). The expression 青天の霹靂 (a bolt from the blue) is written with 青天.","examples":[{"japanese":"運動会は晴天に恵まれた。","romaji":"Undōkai wa seiten ni megumareta.","german":"The athletic meet was blessed with fine weather.","furigana":"うんどうかい|せいてん|めぐ"},{"japanese":"晴天が続いて、畑がすっかり乾いてしまった。","romaji":"Seiten ga tsuzuite, hatake ga sukkari kawaite shimatta.","german":"Fine weather continued, and the fields dried out completely.","furigana":"せいてん|つづ|はたけ|かわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Legal: 成年に達する (to reach the age of majority), 未成年 (minor). In Japan the age of majority has been 18 since 2022. Not to be confused with 青年 (young person), which is read the same.","examples":[{"japanese":"日本では十八歳で成年になる。","romaji":"Nihon de wa jūhassai de seinen ni naru.","german":"In Japan, you become an adult at 18.","furigana":"にほん|じゅうはっさい|せいねん"},{"japanese":"成年に達するまでは、親の同意が必要だ。","romaji":"Seinen ni tassuru made wa, oya no dōi ga hitsuyō da.","german":"Until you reach adulthood, parental consent is required.","furigana":"せいねん|たっ|おや|どうい|ひつよう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"How something is made: 伝統的な製法, 昔ながらの製法 (traditional method), 製法を守る (to keep to the method).","examples":[{"japanese":"この酒は昔ながらの製法で造られている。","romaji":"Kono sake wa mukashinagara no seihō de tsukurarete iru.","german":"This sake is brewed by a traditional method.","furigana":"さけ|むかし|せいほう|つく"},{"japanese":"店の主人は、みその製法を詳しく教えてくれた。","romaji":"Mise no shujin wa, miso no seihō o kuwashiku oshiete kureta.","german":"The shop owner taught me in detail how miso is made.","furigana":"みせ|しゅじん|せいほう|くわ|おし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The local tax authority; this is where one files, for example, the tax return (確定申告).","examples":[{"japanese":"確定申告の書類を税務署に提出した。","romaji":"Kakutei shinkoku no shorui o zeimusho ni teishutsu shita.","german":"I submitted my tax return documents to the tax office.","furigana":"かくていしんこく|しょるい|ぜいむしょ|ていしゅつ"},{"japanese":"税務署から電話があって、少し緊張した。","romaji":"Zeimusho kara denwa ga atte, sukoshi kinchō shita.","german":"I got a call from the tax office and felt a little nervous.","furigana":"ぜいむしょ|でんわ|すこ|きんちょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A public statement: 声明を出す/発表する, 共同声明 (joint statement). Read the same as 生命 (life). Buddhist chanting is also written 声明 but read しょうみょう.","examples":[{"japanese":"政府は事件について声明を発表した。","romaji":"Seifu wa jiken ni tsuite seimei o happyō shita.","german":"The government issued a statement about the incident.","furigana":"せいふ|じけん|せいめい|はっぴょう"},{"japanese":"両国の首脳は共同声明に署名した。","romaji":"Ryōkoku no shunō wa kyōdō seimei ni shomei shita.","german":"The leaders of the two countries signed a joint statement.","furigana":"りょうこく|しゅのう|きょうどうせいめい|しょめい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"1. The body's life processes: 生理学 (physiology), 生理的 (physiological). 2. Everyday word for menstruation: 生理痛 (period pain). Read the same as 整理 (sorting out).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は大学で睡眠の生理を研究している。","romaji":"Kare wa daigaku de suimin no seiri o kenkyū shite iru.","german":"He is studying the physiology of sleep at university.","furigana":"かれ|だいがく|すいみん|せいり|けんきゅう"},{"japanese":"生理のときは、おなかが痛くなることがある。","romaji":"Seiri no toki wa, onaka ga itaku naru koto ga aru.","german":"During my period, I sometimes get stomach pain.","furigana":"せいり|いた"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"〜する: to line up neatly (school, sports, platform): 二列に整列する (to line up in two rows).","examples":[{"japanese":"生徒たちは校庭に整列した。","romaji":"Seitotachi wa kōtei ni seiretsu shita.","german":"The students lined up on the schoolyard.","furigana":"せいと|こうてい|せいれつ"},{"japanese":"乗客はホームに整列して電車を待っていた。","romaji":"Jōkyaku wa hōmu ni seiretsu shite densha o matte ita.","german":"The passengers were lined up on the platform waiting for the train.","furigana":"じょうきゃく|せいれつ|でんしゃ|ま"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive: to urge someone to hurry. Often passive: 急かされる (to be rushed). To hurry oneself is 急ぐ.","examples":[{"japanese":"早く準備しなさいと母に急かされた。","romaji":"Hayaku junbi shinasai to haha ni sekasareta.","german":"My mother rushed me, saying to get ready quickly.","furigana":"はや|じゅんび|はは|せ"},{"japanese":"急かすつもりはないが、返事は今週中にほしい。","romaji":"Sekasu tsumori wa nai ga, henji wa konshūchū ni hoshii.","german":"I don't mean to rush you, but I'd like a reply this week.","furigana":"せ|へんじ|こんしゅうちゅう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A humble, somewhat old-fashioned word for one's own son: うちの倅. Also written 伜, often in kana せがれ. Neutral: 息子.","examples":[{"japanese":"うちの倅も来年から大学生です。","romaji":"Uchi no segare mo rainen kara daigakusei desu.","german":"My son will also be a university student from next year.","furigana":"せがれ|らいねん|だいがくせい"},{"japanese":"倅が店を継いでくれることになった。","romaji":"Segare ga mise o tsuide kureru koto ni natta.","german":"My son has agreed to take over the shop.","furigana":"せがれ|みせ|つ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A subdivision within a company or organization; also a section of a text.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は来月から別のセクションに異動する。","romaji":"Kare wa raigetsu kara betsu no sekushon ni idō suru.","german":"He will be transferred to a different section next month.","furigana":"かれ|らいげつ|べつ|いどう"},{"japanese":"報告書は三つのセクションに分かれている。","romaji":"Hōkokusho wa mittsu no sekushon ni wakarete iru.","german":"The report is divided into three sections.","furigana":"ほうこくしょ|みっ|わ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"〜する: to cut open with a scalpel: 切開手術 (incision surgery), 帝王切開 (cesarean section).","examples":[{"japanese":"医者は患部を切開して、うみを出した。","romaji":"Isha wa kanbu o sekkai shite, umi o dashita.","german":"The doctor made an incision in the affected area and drained the pus.","furigana":"いしゃ|かんぶ|せっかい|だ"},{"japanese":"娘は帝王切開で生まれた。","romaji":"Musume wa teiō sekkai de umareta.","german":"My daughter was born by cesarean section.","furigana":"むすめ|ていおうせっかい|う"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"〜する: 接触事故 (accident from a scrape), 接触が悪い (poor contact, loose connection). With people: 〜と接触する (to make contact with).","examples":[{"japanese":"車同士が軽く接触したが、けが人はいなかった。","romaji":"Kuruma dōshi ga karuku sesshoku shita ga, keganin wa inakatta.","german":"The cars touched lightly, but nobody was hurt.","furigana":"くるまどうし|かる|せっしょく|にん"},{"japanese":"記者は事件の関係者と接触しようとした。","romaji":"Kisha wa jiken no kankeisha to sesshoku shiyō to shita.","german":"The reporter tried to get in contact with people connected to the incident.","furigana":"きしゃ|じけん|かんけいしゃ|せっしょく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"〜する: to combine different things and take the best of each: 和洋折衷 (blend of Japanese and Western style), 折衷案 (compromise proposal).","examples":[{"japanese":"二つの案を折衷して、新しい計画を作った。","romaji":"Futatsu no an o setchū shite, atarashii keikaku o tsukutta.","german":"I blended the two proposals and made a new plan.","furigana":"ふた|あん|せっちゅう|あたら|けいかく|つく"},{"japanese":"この家は和洋折衷のつくりになっている。","romaji":"Kono ie wa wayō setchū no tsukuri ni natte iru.","german":"This house has a blend of Japanese and Western styles.","furigana":"いえ|わようせっちゅう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Noun form of 攻める; opposite 守り (defense). 攻めに転じる (to go on the offensive), 攻めの姿勢 (offensive stance). Not to be confused with 責め (blame, torment), which is read the same.","examples":[{"japanese":"後半、チームは守りから攻めに転じた。","romaji":"Kōhan, chīmu wa mamori kara seme ni tenjita.","german":"In the second half, the team switched from defense to attack.","furigana":"こうはん|まも|せ|てん"},{"japanese":"新しい社長は、攻めの経営で会社を成長させた。","romaji":"Atarashii shachō wa, seme no keiei de kaisha o seichō saseta.","german":"The new president grew the company with an aggressive management style.","furigana":"あたら|しゃちょう|せ|けいえい|かいしゃ|せいちょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A sweet dish made from gelatin or agar (フルーツゼリー); also gel-like substances (ゼリー状).","examples":[{"japanese":"デザートにオレンジのゼリーを作った。","romaji":"Dezāto ni orenji no zerī o tsukutta.","german":"I made orange jelly for dessert.","furigana":"つく"},{"japanese":"風邪で食欲がないときも、ゼリーなら食べやすい。","romaji":"Kaze de shokuyoku ga nai toki mo, zerī nara tabeyasui.","german":"Even when I have no appetite because of a cold, jelly is easy to eat.","furigana":"かぜ|しょくよく|た"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A formal celebration, often at public occasions: 開会セレモニー (opening ceremony), 表彰セレモニー (award ceremony). Japanese equivalents: 式, 式典.","examples":[{"japanese":"新しい橋の完成を祝うセレモニーが行われた。","romaji":"Atarashii hashi no kansei o iwau seremonī ga okonawareta.","german":"A ceremony was held to celebrate the completion of the new bridge.","furigana":"あたら|はし|かんせい|いわ|おこな"},{"japanese":"優勝チームは表彰のセレモニーでトロフィーを受け取った。","romaji":"Yūshō chīmu wa hyōshō no seremonī de torofī o uketotta.","german":"The winning team received the trophy at the award ceremony.","furigana":"ゆうしょう|ひょうしょう|う|と"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"〜する: to become fully healthy again. Read the same as 前回 (last time) and 全開 (fully open, full throttle).","examples":[{"japanese":"手術から二か月で、父は全快した。","romaji":"Shujutsu kara nikagetsu de, chichi wa zenkai shita.","german":"Two months after the surgery, my father recovered completely.","furigana":"しゅじゅつ|に|げつ|ちち|ぜんかい"},{"japanese":"全快までには、まだ時間がかかりそうだ。","romaji":"Zenkai made ni wa, mada jikan ga kakarisō da.","german":"It looks like it will still take time until full recovery.","furigana":"ぜんかい|じかん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"〜する: to proclaim and spread a religion. 宣教師 (missionary).","examples":[{"japanese":"ザビエルは日本でキリスト教の宣教を行った。","romaji":"Zabieru wa Nihon de Kirisutokyō no senkyō o okonatta.","german":"Xavier carried out Christian missionary work in Japan.","furigana":"にほん|きょう|せんきょう|おこな"},{"japanese":"宣教師たちは各地を回って宣教を続けた。","romaji":"Senkyōshitachi wa kakuchi o mawatte senkyō o tsuzuketa.","german":"The missionaries traveled around the regions and continued their missionary work.","furigana":"せんきょうし|かくち|まわ|せんきょう|つづ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"〜する: to go ahead, to precede in time: 先行する車 (preceding vehicle), 先行研究 (previous research), 先行販売 (advance sale). Read the same as 選考 (selection).","examples":[{"japanese":"先行する車との車間距離を十分にとってください。","romaji":"Senkō suru kuruma to no shakan kyori o jūbun ni totte kudasai.","german":"Please keep a sufficient distance from the vehicle ahead.","furigana":"せんこう|くるま|しゃかんきょり|じゅうぶん"},{"japanese":"気持ちばかりが先行して、体がついていかない。","romaji":"Kimochi bakari ga senkō shite, karada ga tsuite ikanai.","german":"My feelings are running ahead and my body can't keep up.","furigana":"きも|せんこう|からだ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"〜する: to examine and select applicants: 書類選考 (screening by documents), 選考委員 (selection committee). Read the same as 先行 (going ahead).","examples":[{"japanese":"選考の結果は、来週メールでお知らせします。","romaji":"Senkō no kekka wa, raishū mēru de oshirase shimasu.","german":"We will notify you of the selection result by email next week.","furigana":"せんこう|けっか|らいしゅう|し"},{"japanese":"書類選考を通過して、面接に進んだ。","romaji":"Shorui senkō o tsūka shite, mensetsu ni susunda.","german":"I passed the document screening and moved on to the interview.","furigana":"しょるいせんこう|つうか|めんせつ|すす"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Destruction caused by war: 戦災を受ける (to suffer war damage), 戦災を免れる (to be spared by the war), 戦災孤児 (war orphan). Read the same as 繊細 (delicate, sensitive).","examples":[{"japanese":"この寺は戦災を免れ、昔の姿のまま残っている。","romaji":"Kono tera wa sensai o manugare, mukashi no sugata no mama nokotte iru.","german":"This temple escaped war damage and remains as it was long ago.","furigana":"てら|せんさい||むかし|すがた|のこ"},{"japanese":"町の中心部は戦災でほとんど焼けてしまった。","romaji":"Machi no chūshinbu wa sensai de hotondo yakete shimatta.","german":"The center of the town was almost entirely burned down in the war.","furigana":"まち|ちゅうしんぶ|せんさい|や"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"〜する: to concentrate on one subject. 専修学校 (vocational school). More common for one's major: 専攻. Read the same as 先週 (last week).","examples":[{"japanese":"大学では日本史を専修した。","romaji":"Daigaku de wa Nihonshi o senshū shita.","german":"At university I specialized in Japanese history.","furigana":"だいがく|にほんし|せんしゅう"},{"japanese":"彼は専修学校で調理を学んだ。","romaji":"Kare wa senshū gakkō de chōri o mananda.","german":"He studied cooking at a vocational school.","furigana":"かれ|せんしゅうがっこう|ちょうり|まな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"センスがいい (tasteful, with flair), ユーモアのセンス (sense of humor), ファッションセンス. Not \"sense\" in the meaning of significance (= 意味). Read the same as 扇子 (folding fan).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は服のセンスがいい。","romaji":"Kanojo wa fuku no sensu ga ii.","german":"She has good taste in clothes.","furigana":"かのじょ|ふく"},{"japanese":"彼にはユーモアのセンスがある。","romaji":"Kare ni wa yūmoa no sensu ga aru.","german":"He has a sense of humor.","furigana":"かれ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 43 (source: N4; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"The time of greatest success: 全盛期 (heyday), 全盛を極める (to be at the peak).","examples":[{"japanese":"江戸時代、この港町は貿易で全盛を極めた。","romaji":"Edo jidai, kono minatomachi wa bōeki de zensei o kiwameta.","german":"In the Edo period, this port town reached the height of its prosperity through trade.","furigana":"えどじだい|みなとまち|ぼうえき|ぜんせい|きわ"},{"japanese":"彼は全盛期には世界一の選手だった。","romaji":"Kare wa zenseiki ni wa sekaiichi no senshu datta.","german":"In his heyday he was the best player in the world.","furigana":"かれ|ぜんせいき|せかいいち|せんしゅ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The previous owner of a business, title or head of a family: 先代の社長, 先代から続く店 (a shop that has continued from the previous generation). Read the same as the city 仙台.","examples":[{"japanese":"この店の味は、先代のころから変わらない。","romaji":"Kono mise no aji wa, sendai no koro kara kawaranai.","german":"The taste of this shop has not changed since the previous owner's time.","furigana":"みせ|あじ|せんだい|か"},{"japanese":"先代の社長は、社員をとても大切にする人だった。","romaji":"Sendai no shachō wa, shain o totemo taisetsu ni suru hito datta.","german":"The previous president was a person who valued his employees greatly.","furigana":"せんだい|しゃちょう|しゃいん|たいせつ|ひと"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Formal, especially in polite phrases and letters: 先だってはありがとうございました. Similar: 先日.","examples":[{"japanese":"先だってはお世話になりました。","romaji":"Sendatte wa osewa ni narimashita.","german":"Thank you for your help the other day.","furigana":"せん|せわ"},{"japanese":"先だってお話しした件ですが、その後いかがでしょうか。","romaji":"Sendatte ohanashi shita ken desu ga, sono go ikaga deshō ka.","german":"About the matter we discussed the other day, how are things since then?","furigana":"せん|はな|けん|ご"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mainly in 先着順 (in order of arrival) and 先着〜名 (the first ... people), e.g. for promotions and registrations.","examples":[{"japanese":"先着百名様にプレゼントを差し上げます。","romaji":"Senchaku hyakumeisama ni purezento o sashiagemasu.","german":"A gift will be given to the first 100 people.","furigana":"せんちゃくひゃくめいさま|さ|あ"},{"japanese":"席は先着順です。","romaji":"Seki wa senchakujun desu.","german":"Seats are first come, first served.","furigana":"せき|せんちゃくじゅん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"な-adjective; opposite: 後天的 (acquired). 先天的な病気 (congenital disease), 先天的な才能 (innate talent).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼の心臓の病気は先天的なものだ。","romaji":"Kare no shinzō no byōki wa sententeki na mono da.","german":"His heart disease is congenital.","furigana":"かれ|しんぞう|びょうき|せんてんてき"},{"japanese":"音楽の才能は先天的なものか、努力で身につくものか。","romaji":"Ongaku no sainō wa sententeki na mono ka, doryoku de mi ni tsuku mono ka.","german":"Is musical talent innate, or can it be acquired through effort?","furigana":"おんがく|さいのう|せんてんてき|どりょく|み"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Formal: 前途有望 (promising), 前途多難 (many difficulties ahead), 前途ある若者 (young people with a future).","examples":[{"japanese":"前途ある若者たちの活躍を期待している。","romaji":"Zento aru wakamonotachi no katsuyaku o kitai shite iru.","german":"I look forward to the achievements of the young people who have a future ahead of them.","furigana":"ぜんと|わかもの|かつやく|きたい"},{"japanese":"この計画の前途には、多くの困難が待っている。","romaji":"Kono keikaku no zento ni wa, ōku no konnan ga matte iru.","german":"Many difficulties await this plan in the future.","furigana":"けいかく|ぜんと|おお|こんなん|ま"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"〜する: to infiltrate somewhere secretly: 潜入捜査 (undercover investigation), 潜入取材 (undercover reporting).","examples":[{"japanese":"記者は工場に作業員として潜入した。","romaji":"Kisha wa kōjō ni sagyōin to shite sennyū shita.","german":"The reporter infiltrated the factory as a worker.","furigana":"きしゃ|こうじょう|さぎょういん|せんにゅう"},{"japanese":"警察官が暴力団に潜入して情報を集めた。","romaji":"Keisatsukan ga bōryokudan ni sennyū shite jōhō o atsumeta.","german":"A police officer infiltrated the yakuza and gathered information.","furigana":"けいさつかん|ぼうりょくだん|せんにゅう|じょうほう|あつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"〜する: to be completely wiped out (troops, crops). Colloquially also: everything fails (試験は全滅だった, \"I failed every exam\").","examples":[{"japanese":"大雨で畑の野菜が全滅した。","romaji":"Ōame de hatake no yasai ga zenmetsu shita.","german":"The heavy rain wiped out all the vegetables in the field.","furigana":"おおあめ|はたけ|やさい|ぜんめつ"},{"japanese":"受けた会社は全滅で、どこからも内定がもらえなかった。","romaji":"Uketa kaisha wa zenmetsu de, doko kara mo naitei ga moraenakatta.","german":"Every company I applied to rejected me; I couldn't get a job offer from anywhere.","furigana":"う|かいしゃ|ぜんめつ|ないてい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Often as a suffix: 女性専用車両 (women-only car), 歩行者専用 (pedestrians only), 専用の部屋 (a room of one's own).","examples":[{"japanese":"この駐車場は住民専用です。","romaji":"Kono chūshajō wa jūmin sen'yō desu.","german":"This parking lot is for residents only.","furigana":"ちゅうしゃじょう|じゅうみんせんよう"},{"japanese":"彼は自分専用の部屋で仕事をしている。","romaji":"Kare wa jibun sen'yō no heya de shigoto o shite iru.","german":"He works in a room reserved for his own use.","furigana":"かれ|じぶんせんよう|へや|しごと"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"〜する: to occupy militarily (占領軍 occupation forces, 占領下 under occupation). In everyday use: to claim a place for oneself alone: ソファーを占領する.","examples":[{"japanese":"戦後、日本は連合国に占領された。","romaji":"Sengo, Nihon wa rengōkoku ni senryō sareta.","german":"After the war, Japan was occupied by the Allied Powers.","furigana":"せんご|にほん|れんごうこく|せんりょう"},{"japanese":"弟がテレビの前のソファーを一人で占領している。","romaji":"Otōto ga terebi no mae no sofā o hitori de senryō shite iru.","german":"My younger brother is occupying the sofa in front of the TV all by himself.","furigana":"おとうと|まえ|ひとり|せんりょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"な-adjective, formal: 善良な市民 (law-abiding citizens), 善良な人 (a decent person).","examples":[{"japanese":"善良な市民をねらった詐欺が増えている。","romaji":"Zenryō na shimin o neratta sagi ga fuete iru.","german":"Fraud targeting honest citizens is increasing.","furigana":"ぜんりょう|しみん|さぎ|ふ"},{"japanese":"彼は善良な人間で、人をだますようなことはしない。","romaji":"Kare wa zenryō na ningen de, hito o damasu yō na koto wa shinai.","german":"He is a decent person who would never deceive anyone.","furigana":"かれ|ぜんりょう|にんげん|ひと"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Military: armed forces, fighting strength. Figuratively: someone who contributes to the success of a team or company: 即戦力 (someone who can contribute immediately).","examples":[{"japanese":"新人の彼は、すでにチームの大きな戦力になっている。","romaji":"Shinjin no kare wa, sude ni chīmu no ōkina senryoku ni natte iru.","german":"As a newcomer, he has already become a major asset to the team.","furigana":"しんじん|かれ|おお|せんりょく"},{"japanese":"両軍の戦力には大きな差があった。","romaji":"Ryōgun no senryoku ni wa ōkina sa ga atta.","german":"There was a big difference in the military strength of the two armies.","furigana":"りょうぐん|せんりょく|おお|さ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Suffix after nouns for rivers, roads, coasts etc.; read ぞい: 川沿い (along the river), 線路沿い (along the railway line), 海沿い (along the coast). Verb: 沿う (to run along).","examples":[{"japanese":"川沿いの道を散歩した。","romaji":"Kawazoi no michi o sanpo shita.","german":"I took a walk along the riverside road.","furigana":"かわぞ|みち|さんぽ"},{"japanese":"線路沿いに小さな店が並んでいる。","romaji":"Senrozoi ni chiisana mise ga narande iru.","german":"Small shops are lined up along the railway tracks.","furigana":"せんろぞ|ちい|みせ|なら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Formal; in everyday speech お坊さん. 僧侶 (monk, priest), 高僧 (high-ranking monk).","examples":[{"japanese":"寺の僧が朝早くからお経を読んでいる。","romaji":"Tera no sō ga asa hayaku kara okyō o yonde iru.","german":"The monks at the temple have been chanting sutras since early morning.","furigana":"てら|そう|あさはや|きょう|よ"},{"japanese":"彼は若いころに出家して僧になった。","romaji":"Kare wa wakai koro ni shukke shite sō ni natta.","german":"He renounced the world in his youth and became a monk.","furigana":"かれ|わか|しゅっけ|そう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Intransitive, formal; mainly 期待に添う (to meet expectations), 希望に添う (to comply with a wish); 沿う is also written for this. \"To accompany\" appears in 付き添う and 寄り添う. Do not confuse the potential 添える (to be able to meet) with the verb 添える (to attach).","examples":[{"japanese":"ご期待に添えるよう、全力を尽くします。","romaji":"Gokitai ni soeru yō, zenryoku o tsukushimasu.","german":"We will do our utmost to meet your expectations.","furigana":"きたい|そ|ぜんりょく|つ"},{"japanese":"残念ながら、ご希望に添うことはできません。","romaji":"Zannen nagara, gokibō ni sou koto wa dekimasen.","german":"Unfortunately, we cannot comply with your request.","furigana":"ざんねん|きぼう|そ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Sculpture: 銅像 (bronze statue), 仏像 (Buddha statue). Also a mental image as a suffix: 理想像 (ideal image), 人物像 (portrait of a person). Read the same as 象 (elephant).","examples":[{"japanese":"駅前に有名な犬の像が立っている。","romaji":"Ekimae ni yūmei na inu no zō ga tatte iru.","german":"A famous statue of a dog stands in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|ゆうめい|いぬ|ぞう|た"},{"japanese":"公園には、町をつくった人物の像が建てられている。","romaji":"Kōen ni wa, machi o tsukutta jinbutsu no zō ga taterarete iru.","german":"A statue of the person who founded the town has been erected in the park.","furigana":"こうえん|まち|じんぶつ|ぞう|た"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mostly after nouns or with の: 年齢相応 (appropriate to one's age), 身分相応 (suited to one's station), それ相応の (corresponding, due). Opposite: 不相応. Same kanji, different reading: 相応しい (ふさわしい).","examples":[{"japanese":"努力すれば、それ相応の結果が出るはずだ。","romaji":"Doryoku sureba, sore sōō no kekka ga deru hazu da.","german":"If you make an effort, you should get results to match.","furigana":"どりょく|そうおう|けっか|で"},{"japanese":"彼は年齢相応の落ち着きがある。","romaji":"Kare wa nenrei sōō no ochitsuki ga aru.","german":"He has a composure appropriate to his age.","furigana":"かれ|ねんれいそうおう|お|つ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"〜する: to publish a newspaper or magazine for the first time. 創刊号 (first issue); opposite: 廃刊 (discontinuation).","examples":[{"japanese":"この雑誌は五十年前に創刊された。","romaji":"Kono zasshi wa gojūnen mae ni sōkan sareta.","german":"This magazine was launched fifty years ago.","furigana":"ざっし|ごじゅうねんまえ|そうかん"},{"japanese":"創刊号には有名な作家の小説が載っている。","romaji":"Sōkangō ni wa yūmei na sakka no shōsetsu ga notte iru.","german":"The first issue contains a novel by a famous writer.","furigana":"そうかんごう|ゆうめい|さっか|しょうせつ|の"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive: 電話を取り次ぐ (to put a call through), 伝言を取り次ぐ (to pass on a message). The noun 取り次ぎ means relaying or mediation; in the book trade also wholesale distribution.","examples":[{"japanese":"社長への電話は、秘書が取り次ぐことになっている。","romaji":"Shachō e no denwa wa, hisho ga toritsugu koto ni natte iru.","german":"Calls to the president are to be put through by the secretary.","furigana":"しゃちょう|でんわ|ひしょ|と|つ"},{"japanese":"お客様がいらっしゃったら、すぐに私に取り次いでください。","romaji":"Okyakusama ga irasshattara, sugu ni watashi ni toritsuide kudasai.","german":"When a customer arrives, please announce them to me right away.","furigana":"きゃくさま|わたし|と|つ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive. Concretely: エアコンを取り付ける (to install an air conditioner). Figuratively: 同意を取り付ける (to obtain consent), 約束を取り付ける (to secure a promise).","examples":[{"japanese":"業者が来て、新しいエアコンを取り付けた。","romaji":"Gyōsha ga kite, atarashii eakon o toritsuketa.","german":"A contractor came and installed a new air conditioner.","furigana":"ぎょうしゃ|き|あたら|と|つ"},{"japanese":"部長はようやく社長の同意を取り付けた。","romaji":"Buchō wa yōyaku shachō no dōi o toritsuketa.","german":"The department head finally obtained the president's consent.","furigana":"ぶちょう|しゃちょう|どうい|と|つ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive: ファンが歌手を取り巻く (fans crowd around a singer). Common in 〜を取り巻く環境 / 〜を取り巻く状況 (the environment, the situation surrounding ...). The noun 取り巻き refers derogatorily to an entourage.","examples":[{"japanese":"空港で、大勢のファンが俳優を取り巻いた。","romaji":"Kūkō de, ōzei no fan ga haiyū o torimaita.","german":"At the airport, a crowd of fans surrounded the actor.","furigana":"くうこう|おおぜい|はいゆう|と|ま"},{"japanese":"子どもを取り巻く環境は、この二十年で大きく変わった。","romaji":"Kodomo o torimaku kankyō wa, kono nijūnen de ōkiku kawatta.","german":"The environment surrounding children has changed greatly over the past twenty years.","furigana":"こ|と|ま|かんきょう|にじゅうねん|おお|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive, mostly in the て-form: 大小取り混ぜて (large and small mixed), 新旧取り混ぜて (old and new mixed).","examples":[{"japanese":"箱には大小取り混ぜて二十個のりんごが入っている。","romaji":"Hako ni wa daishō torimazete nijukko no ringo ga haitte iru.","german":"The box contains twenty apples, large and small mixed.","furigana":"はこ|だいしょうと|ま|にじゅっこ|はい"},{"japanese":"和菓子と洋菓子を取り混ぜて、お客様に出した。","romaji":"Wagashi to yōgashi o torimazete, okyakusama ni dashita.","german":"We served the guests an assortment of Japanese and Western sweets.","furigana":"わがし|ようがし|と|ま|きゃくさま|だ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive: 本を取り寄せる (to order a book that is not in stock). The noun 取り寄せ (お取り寄せ) is already in the vocabulary.","examples":[{"japanese":"その本は在庫がないので、取り寄せることになります。","romaji":"Sono hon wa zaiko ga nai node, toriyoseru koto ni narimasu.","german":"That book is out of stock, so we will have to order it in.","furigana":"ほん|ざいこ|と|よ"},{"japanese":"北海道から新鮮なカニを取り寄せた。","romaji":"Hokkaidō kara shinsen na kani o toriyoseta.","german":"I ordered fresh crab from Hokkaido.","furigana":"ほっかいどう|しんせん|と|よ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Tool: 電動ドリル (electric drill). At school: 計算ドリル / 漢字ドリル (practice book for arithmetic / kanji).","examples":[{"japanese":"ドリルで壁に穴を開けた。","romaji":"Doriru de kabe ni ana o aketa.","german":"I drilled a hole in the wall with a drill.","furigana":"かべ|あな|あ"},{"japanese":"息子は毎朝、漢字のドリルを一ページやっている。","romaji":"Musuko wa maiasa, kanji no doriru o ichipēji yatte iru.","german":"My son does one page of a kanji drill book every morning.","furigana":"むすこ|まいあさ|かんじ|いち"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mostly ど忘れする: something you actually know well suddenly won't come to mind. Also written 度忘れ.","examples":[{"japanese":"よく知っている人なのに、名前をど忘れしてしまった。","romaji":"Yoku shitte iru hito na noni, namae o dowasure shite shimatta.","german":"Even though I know the person well, I blanked on their name.","furigana":"し|ひと|なまえ|わす"},{"japanese":"緊張のあまりど忘れして、自分の電話番号が出てこなかった。","romaji":"Kinchō no amari dowasure shite, jibun no denwa bangō ga dete konakatta.","german":"I was so nervous that I blanked, and my own phone number wouldn't come to me.","furigana":"きんちょう|わす|じぶん|でんわばんごう|で"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"声のトーン (tone of voice), トーンを落とす (to speak more quietly; also: to tone down). Also for colors: 落ち着いたトーン (a calm tone).","examples":[{"japanese":"図書館では、声のトーンを落としてください。","romaji":"Toshokan de wa, koe no tōn o otoshite kudasai.","german":"Please lower the tone of your voice in the library.","furigana":"としょかん|こえ|お"},{"japanese":"部屋の壁を落ち着いたトーンの色に塗り替えた。","romaji":"Heya no kabe o ochitsuita tōn no iro ni nurikaeta.","german":"I repainted the walls of the room in a calm tone of color.","furigana":"へや|かべ|お|つ|いろ|ぬ|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Prenominal word, used only before nouns: とんだ災難 (a terrible misfortune), とんだ目に遭う (to have a bad experience), とんだ誤解 (a huge misunderstanding).","examples":[{"japanese":"旅行先で財布を盗まれて、とんだ目に遭った。","romaji":"Ryokōsaki de saifu o nusumarete, tonda me ni atta.","german":"My wallet was stolen on the trip, and I had a terrible time.","furigana":"りょこうさき|さいふ|ぬす|め|あ"},{"japanese":"私が犯人だなんて、とんだ誤解です。","romaji":"Watashi ga hannin da nante, tonda gokai desu.","german":"To say that I'm the culprit is a complete misunderstanding.","furigana":"わたし|はんにん|ごかい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Literary conjunction, nowadays mostly written in kana ないし. Between numbers \"from ... to\" (三日ないし五日), between nouns \"or\" (本人ないし家族).","examples":[{"japanese":"完成までには三日乃至五日かかる見込みです。","romaji":"Kansei made ni wa mikka naishi itsuka kakaru mikomi desu.","german":"It is expected to take three to five days until completion.","furigana":"かんせい|みっかないしいつか|みこ"},{"japanese":"申請は本人ないし家族が行ってください。","romaji":"Shinsei wa honnin naishi kazoku ga okonatte kudasai.","german":"Please have either the person themselves or a family member submit the application.","furigana":"しんせい|ほんにん|かぞく|おこな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Often adverbial: 内心ほっとした (I was inwardly relieved). Contrasts with what one shows on the outside.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は笑っていたが、内心は怒っていたようだ。","romaji":"Kare wa waratte ita ga, naishin wa okotte ita yō da.","german":"He was smiling, but he seemed to be angry inside.","furigana":"かれ|わら|ないしん|おこ"},{"japanese":"試験に合格したと聞いて、内心ほっとした。","romaji":"Shiken ni gōkaku shita to kiite, naishin hotto shita.","german":"When I heard I had passed the exam, I was secretly relieved.","furigana":"しけん|ごうかく|き|ないしん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mostly with する or attributive: バッテリーを内蔵する (to have a built-in battery), カメラ内蔵のスマートフォン (smartphone with a built-in camera). Not to be confused with 内臓 (internal organs), which is read the same.","examples":[{"japanese":"このパソコンにはカメラが内蔵されている。","romaji":"Kono pasokon ni wa kamera ga naizō sarete iru.","german":"This computer has a built-in camera.","furigana":"ないぞう"},{"japanese":"内蔵のバッテリーは交換できません。","romaji":"Naizō no batterī wa kōkan dekimasen.","german":"The built-in battery cannot be replaced.","furigana":"ないぞう|こうかん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A word coined in Japan (from English night + -er). Mainly in baseball; counterpart: デーゲーム (day game).","examples":[{"japanese":"仕事の後、友達と野球のナイターを見に行った。","romaji":"Shigoto no ato, tomodachi to yakyū no naitā o mi ni itta.","german":"After work I went with a friend to watch a baseball night game.","furigana":"しごと|あと|ともだち|やきゅう|み|い"},{"japanese":"雨のため、今夜のナイターは中止になった。","romaji":"Ame no tame, kon'ya no naitā wa chūshi ni natta.","german":"Tonight's night game was canceled because of rain.","furigana":"あめ|こんや|ちゅうし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Collocations: 内乱が起こる (a civil war breaks out), 内乱を鎮める (to put down a rebellion).","examples":[{"japanese":"その国では内乱が続き、多くの人々が国外へ逃れた。","romaji":"Sono kuni de wa nairan ga tsuzuki, ōku no hitobito ga kokugai e nogareta.","german":"Civil war continued in that country, and many people fled abroad.","furigana":"くに|ないらん|つづ|おお|ひとびと|こくがい|のが"},{"japanese":"政府は軍を送って内乱を鎮めた。","romaji":"Seifu wa gun o okutte nairan o shizumeta.","german":"The government sent in the army and put down the rebellion.","furigana":"せいふ|ぐん|おく|ないらん|しず"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Often in compounds: 内陸部 (interior region), 内陸国 (landlocked country), 内陸性気候 (continental climate).","examples":[{"japanese":"内陸の町は、夏は暑く冬は寒い。","romaji":"Nairiku no machi wa, natsu wa atsuku fuyu wa samui.","german":"Inland towns are hot in summer and cold in winter.","furigana":"ないりく|まち|なつ|あつ|ふゆ|さむ"},{"japanese":"スイスは海に面していない内陸国だ。","romaji":"Suisu wa umi ni men shite inai nairikukoku da.","german":"Switzerland is a landlocked country with no coast.","furigana":"うみ|めん|ないりくこく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"苗を植える (to plant seedlings). In rice farming, the seedlings (稲の苗) are set out in the field in spring (田植え).","examples":[{"japanese":"庭にトマトの苗を植えた。","romaji":"Niwa ni tomato no nae o ueta.","german":"I planted tomato seedlings in the garden.","furigana":"にわ|なえ|う"},{"japanese":"五月になると、田んぼに稲の苗が植えられる。","romaji":"Gogatsu ni naru to, tanbo ni ine no nae ga uerareru.","german":"In May, rice seedlings are planted in the paddies.","furigana":"ごがつ|た|いね|なえ|う"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The kitchen sink (台所の流し); today often called シンク. Derived from 流す (to let flow).","examples":[{"japanese":"使った食器を流しに置いておいてください。","romaji":"Tsukatta shokki o nagashi ni oite oite kudasai.","german":"Please leave the used dishes in the sink.","furigana":"つか|しょっき|なが|お"},{"japanese":"台所の流しが詰まって、水が流れない。","romaji":"Daidokoro no nagashi ga tsumatte, mizu ga nagarenai.","german":"The kitchen sink is clogged and the water won't drain.","furigana":"だいどころ|なが|つ|みず|なが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mostly 長々と: 長々と話す (to talk at great length), 長々と寝そべる (to lie stretched out). Often slightly critical or apologetic: 長々と失礼しました.","examples":[{"japanese":"校長先生の話が長々と続いて、生徒たちは退屈していた。","romaji":"Kōchō sensei no hanashi ga naganaga to tsuzuite, seitotachi wa taikutsu shite ita.","german":"The principal's talk went on and on, and the students were bored.","furigana":"こうちょうせんせい|はなし|ながなが|つづ|せいと|たいくつ"},{"japanese":"長々とお邪魔して、失礼しました。","romaji":"Naganaga to ojama shite, shitsurei shimashita.","german":"I'm sorry for staying so long.","furigana":"ながなが|じゃま|しつれい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"In space or time: 列の中程 (in the middle of the line), 話の中程 (midway through the story). Typical train announcement: 中程までお進みください.","examples":[{"japanese":"混雑していますので、車内の中程までお進みください。","romaji":"Konzatsu shite imasu node, shanai no nakahodo made osusumi kudasai.","german":"It is crowded, so please move toward the middle of the car.","furigana":"こんざつ|しゃない|なかほど|すす"},{"japanese":"映画の中程で、少し眠くなってしまった。","romaji":"Eiga no nakahodo de, sukoshi nemuku natte shimatta.","german":"Around the middle of the movie, I got a little sleepy.","furigana":"えいが|なかほど|すこ|ねむ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Poetic word for the strip of beach where the waves run out; common in songs and names. More everyday: 浜辺 or 海岸.","examples":[{"japanese":"夕暮れの渚を、二人でゆっくり歩いた。","romaji":"Yūgure no nagisa o, futari de yukkuri aruita.","german":"The two of us walked slowly along the shore at dusk.","furigana":"ゆうぐ|なぎさ|ふたり|ある"},{"japanese":"子どもたちは渚で貝殻を拾っていた。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa nagisa de kaigara o hirotte ita.","german":"The children were picking up seashells at the water's edge.","furigana":"こ|なぎさ|かいがら|ひろ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive: 足を投げ出す (to stretch out one's legs), 仕事を途中で投げ出す (to abandon one's work halfway), 命を投げ出す (to sacrifice one's life).","examples":[{"japanese":"難しくても、途中で投げ出してはいけない。","romaji":"Muzukashikute mo, tochū de nagedashite wa ikenai.","german":"Even if it is difficult, you must not give up halfway.","furigana":"むずか|とちゅう|な|だ"},{"japanese":"彼は床に座り、足を投げ出して休んだ。","romaji":"Kare wa yuka ni suwari, ashi o nagedashite yasunda.","german":"He sat on the floor and stretched out his legs to rest.","furigana":"かれ|ゆか|すわ|あし|な|だ|やす"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"From 情け (compassion) + 深い. A slightly elevated description of character: 情け深い人.","examples":[{"japanese":"祖母は情け深い人で、困っている人を放っておけなかった。","romaji":"Sobo wa nasakebukai hito de, komatte iru hito o hōtte okenakatta.","german":"My grandmother was a compassionate person who couldn't ignore people in trouble.","furigana":"そぼ|なさ|ぶか|ひと|こま|ひと|ほう"},{"japanese":"領主は情け深く、貧しい村人の税を免じた。","romaji":"Ryōshu wa nasakebukaku, mazushii murabito no zei o menjita.","german":"The lord was merciful and exempted poor villagers from taxes.","furigana":"りょうしゅ|なさ|ぶか|まず|むらびと|ぜい|めん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive: 相手の不誠実を詰る (to reproach someone for their insincerity). Mostly written in kana なじる, because the spelling 詰る can also be read つまる.","examples":[{"japanese":"約束を破った友人を、彼女は激しく詰った。","romaji":"Yakusoku o yabutta yūjin o, kanojo wa hageshiku najitta.","german":"She harshly rebuked her friend who had broken a promise.","furigana":"やくそく|やぶ|ゆうじん|かのじょ|はげ|なじ"},{"japanese":"部下の失敗をなじるだけでは、問題は解決しない。","romaji":"Buka no shippai o najiru dake de wa, mondai wa kaiketsu shinai.","german":"Merely reproaching a subordinate for a mistake will not solve the problem.","furigana":"ぶか|しっぱい|もんだい|かいけつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"More elevated than 有名な: 〜で名高い (famous for ...).","examples":[{"japanese":"この町は美しい桜並木で名高い。","romaji":"Kono machi wa utsukushii sakuranamiki de nadakai.","german":"This town is famous for its beautiful rows of cherry trees.","furigana":"まち|うつく|さくらなみき|なだか"},{"japanese":"名高い画家の作品が、初めて日本で公開された。","romaji":"Nadakai gaka no sakuhin ga, hajimete Nihon de kōkai sareta.","german":"The works of a renowned painter were shown in Japan for the first time.","furigana":"なだか|がか|さくひん|はじ|にほん|こうかい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive, usually 〜を〜と名付ける: 娘を「はな」と名付ける. The noun 名付け親 means the person who gives the name (namer; godparent).","examples":[{"japanese":"両親は生まれた娘を「はな」と名付けた。","romaji":"Ryōshin wa umareta musume o \"Hana\" to nazuketa.","german":"The parents named their newborn daughter \"Hana\".","furigana":"りょうしん|う|むすめ|なづ"},{"japanese":"新しく発見された星は、発見者によって「ヤマト」と名付けられた。","romaji":"Atarashiku hakken sareta hoshi wa, hakkensha ni yotte \"Yamato\" to nazukerareta.","german":"The newly discovered star was named \"Yamato\" by its discoverer.","furigana":"あたら|はっけん|ほし|はっけんしゃ|なづ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Often used adverbially as 何気なく (casually, without intention): 何気なく言った一言. Attributively: 何気ない日常 (the perfectly ordinary everyday life).","examples":[{"japanese":"何気ない一言が、相手を深く傷つけることもある。","romaji":"Nanigenai hitokoto ga, aite o fukaku kizutsukeru koto mo aru.","german":"A casual remark can hurt the other person deeply.","furigana":"なにげ|ひとこと|あいて|ふか|きず"},{"japanese":"病気になって、何気ない日常の大切さに気づいた。","romaji":"Byōki ni natte, nanigenai nichijō no taisetsusa ni kizuita.","german":"Falling ill made me realize the value of ordinary everyday life.","furigana":"びょうき|なにげ|にちじょう|たいせつ|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"At the table: 紙ナプキン (paper napkin), ナプキンを膝に広げる. Hygiene product: 生理用ナプキン (sanitary napkin).","examples":[{"japanese":"食事の前に、ナプキンを膝の上に広げた。","romaji":"Shokuji no mae ni, napukin o hiza no ue ni hirogeta.","german":"Before the meal, I spread the napkin on my lap.","furigana":"しょくじ|まえ|ひざ|うえ|ひろ"},{"japanese":"コンビニで生理用ナプキンを買った。","romaji":"Konbini de seiriyō napukin o katta.","german":"I bought sanitary napkins at the convenience store.","furigana":"せいりよう|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"名札をつける (to wear or attach a name tag). Also used for name plates on doors or objects.","examples":[{"japanese":"会議の参加者は全員、胸に名札をつけていた。","romaji":"Kaigi no sankasha wa zen'in, mune ni nafuda o tsukete ita.","german":"All the conference participants wore name tags on their chests.","furigana":"かいぎ|さんかしゃ|ぜんいん|むね|なふだ"},{"japanese":"子どもの傘に名札をつけておいた。","romaji":"Kodomo no kasa ni nafuda o tsukete oita.","german":"I put a name tag on my child's umbrella.","furigana":"こ|かさ|なふだ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Literally: 魚が生臭い (the fish smells strongly). Figuratively, for dealings involving money and power: 生臭い話 (a sordid affair).","examples":[{"japanese":"まな板を洗わないと、生臭いにおいが残る。","romaji":"Manaita o arawanai to, namagusai nioi ga nokoru.","german":"If you don't wash the cutting board, a fishy smell remains.","furigana":"いた|あら|なまぐさ|のこ"},{"japanese":"選挙の裏では、金をめぐる生臭い話が絶えない。","romaji":"Senkyo no ura de wa, kane o meguru namagusai hanashi ga taenai.","german":"Behind the scenes of the election, there is a constant stream of sordid stories about money.","furigana":"せんきょ|うら|かね|なまぐさ|はなし|た"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Literally, usually unpleasant: 生ぬるいビール (lukewarm beer). Figuratively: 生ぬるい対策 (half-hearted measures), 生ぬるい処分 (a punishment that is too mild). Also written 生温い.","examples":[{"japanese":"冷蔵庫に入れ忘れて、ビールが生ぬるくなった。","romaji":"Reizōko ni irewasurete, bīru ga namanuruku natta.","german":"I forgot to put the beer in the fridge, and now it has gone lukewarm.","furigana":"れいぞうこ|い|わす|なま"},{"japanese":"そんな生ぬるい対策では、事故は防げない。","romaji":"Sonna namanurui taisaku de wa, jiko wa fusegenai.","german":"Such half-hearted measures won't prevent accidents.","furigana":"なま|たいさく|じこ|ふせ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Almost always 生身の人間 (a human being of flesh and blood); it emphasizes vulnerability and humanity.","examples":[{"japanese":"選手も生身の人間だから、疲れることもある。","romaji":"Senshu mo namami no ningen dakara, tsukareru koto mo aru.","german":"Athletes are also human beings of flesh and blood, so they get tired sometimes.","furigana":"せんしゅ|なまみ|にんげん|つか"},{"japanese":"画面の向こうにいるのも生身の人間だということを、忘れてはいけない。","romaji":"Gamen no mukō ni iru no mo namami no ningen da to iu koto o, wasurete wa ikenai.","german":"We must not forget that the person on the other side of the screen is also a flesh-and-blood human being.","furigana":"がめん|む|なまみ|にんげん|わす"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The metal lead (Pb). Figuratively: 鉛のように重い (heavy as lead). Not to be confused with 訛り (dialect coloring, accent), which has the same reading.","examples":[{"japanese":"古い水道管には、鉛が使われていることがある。","romaji":"Furui suidōkan ni wa, namari ga tsukawarete iru koto ga aru.","german":"Lead is sometimes used in old water pipes.","furigana":"ふる|すいどうかん|なまり|つか"},{"japanese":"疲れて、体が鉛のように重い。","romaji":"Tsukarete, karada ga namari no yō ni omoi.","german":"I'm so tired that my body feels as heavy as lead.","furigana":"つか|からだ|なまり|おも"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Intransitive, colloquial and often written in kana: 体がなまる (to get out of shape physically), 腕がなまる (to get out of practice). The same kanji is also read にぶる.","examples":[{"japanese":"しばらく運動していないので、体がなまってしまった。","romaji":"Shibaraku undō shite inai node, karada ga namatte shimatta.","german":"I haven't exercised for a while, so I've gotten out of shape.","furigana":"うんどう|からだ"},{"japanese":"毎日弾かないと、ピアノの腕がなまる。","romaji":"Mainichi hikanai to, piano no ude ga namaru.","german":"If you don't play every day, your piano skills get rusty.","furigana":"まいにちひ|うで"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Intransitive: 切れ味が鈍る (the blade gets dull), 勘が鈍る (one's instinct declines), 決心が鈍る (one's resolve wavers). The same kanji is also read colloquially as なまる.","examples":[{"japanese":"包丁の切れ味が鈍ってきたので、研いでもらった。","romaji":"Hōchō no kireaji ga nibutte kita node, toide moratta.","german":"The kitchen knife had become dull, so I had it sharpened.","furigana":"ほうちょう|き|あじ|にぶ|と"},{"japanese":"家族の顔を見たら、留学の決心が鈍った。","romaji":"Kazoku no kao o mitara, ryūgaku no kesshin ga nibutta.","german":"Seeing my family's faces weakened my resolve to study abroad.","furigana":"かぞく|かお|み|りゅうがく|けっしん|にぶ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"並みの (ordinary, average): 並みの努力では足りない. As a suffix: プロ並み (professional level), 人並み (like everyone else). For food, 並 denotes the standard grade: in the ranking 並・上・特上 it is the lowest quality, and in 並盛 (as opposed to 大盛) it is the regular portion.","examples":[{"japanese":"この試験は、並みの努力では合格できない。","romaji":"Kono shiken wa, nami no doryoku de wa gōkaku dekinai.","german":"You can't pass this exam with ordinary effort.","furigana":"しけん|な|どりょく|ごうかく"},{"japanese":"彼の料理の腕はプロ並みだ。","romaji":"Kare no ryōri no ude wa puronami da.","german":"His cooking skills are on a par with a professional's.","furigana":"かれ|りょうり|うで|な"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Today often used in the sense of \"hard to decide\": 悩ましい問題 (a thorny issue). Besides that, literary \"seductive\": 悩ましい姿.","examples":[{"japanese":"どちらの案にも長所があって、悩ましいところだ。","romaji":"Dochira no an ni mo chōsho ga atte, nayamashii tokoro da.","german":"Both proposals have advantages, so it's a tough decision.","furigana":"あん|ちょうしょ|なや"},{"japanese":"舞台の上で、踊り子が悩ましいポーズをとった。","romaji":"Butai no ue de, odoriko ga nayamashii pōzu o totta.","german":"On the stage, the dancer struck a seductive pose.","furigana":"ぶたい|うえ|おど|こ|なや"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive counterpart of 悩む. Set phrase: 頭を悩ます (to rack one's brains). Passive: 騒音に悩まされる (to suffer from noise).","examples":[{"japanese":"新しい企画のことで、ずっと頭を悩ましている。","romaji":"Atarashii kikaku no koto de, zutto atama o nayamashite iru.","german":"I've been racking my brains over the new project the whole time.","furigana":"あたら|きかく|あたま|なや"},{"japanese":"毎晩、隣の部屋の騒音に悩まされている。","romaji":"Maiban, tonari no heya no sōon ni nayamasarete iru.","german":"I am troubled by the noise from the next room every night.","furigana":"まいばん|となり|へや|そうおん|なや"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive counterpart of 慣れる: 体を寒さに慣らす (to accustom the body to the cold). For animals usually written 馴らす: 馬を馴らす. Not to be confused with 鳴らす (to ring, to sound), which has the same reading.","examples":[{"japanese":"本番の前に、少し走って体を慣らしておこう。","romaji":"Honban no mae ni, sukoshi hashitte karada o narashite okō.","german":"Before the actual event, let's run a little to get my body used to it.","furigana":"ほんばん|まえ|すこ|はし|からだ|な"},{"japanese":"この地方では、昔から馬を馴らして農作業に使ってきた。","romaji":"Kono chihō de wa, mukashi kara uma o narashite nōsagyō ni tsukatte kita.","german":"In this region, horses have long been tamed and used for farm work.","furigana":"ちほう|むかし|うま|な|のうさぎょう|つか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Formal conjunction of written and official language, similar to 及び. Often joins larger units: A及びB、並びにC.","examples":[{"japanese":"会員の氏名並びに住所を名簿に記載する。","romaji":"Kaiin no shimei narabini jūsho o meibo ni kisai suru.","german":"The members' names and addresses are entered in the register.","furigana":"かいいん|しめいなら|じゅうしょ|めいぼ|きさい"},{"japanese":"ご来賓の皆様、並びに保護者の皆様、本日はご出席いただきありがとうございます。","romaji":"Goraihin no minasama, narabini hogosha no minasama, honjitsu wa goshusseki itadaki arigatō gozaimasu.","german":"Honored guests and parents, thank you for attending today.","furigana":"らいひん|みなさま|なら|ほごしゃ|みなさま|ほんじつ|しゅっせき"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Colloquial variant of なるべく, usually in kana: なるたけ早く (as soon as possible).","examples":[{"japanese":"なるたけ早く返事をください。","romaji":"Narutake hayaku henji o kudasai.","german":"Please reply as soon as possible.","furigana":"はや|へんじ"},{"japanese":"荷物はなるたけ少なくして行こう。","romaji":"Nimotsu wa narutake sukunaku shite ikō.","german":"Let's travel with as little luggage as possible.","furigana":"にもつ|すく|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Noun of 慣れる: 慣れが必要だ (practice is needed), 慣れからくる油断 (carelessness resulting from routine). Suffix-like: 場慣れ (ease in a situation from experience).","examples":[{"japanese":"この仕事は難しくないが、慣れが必要だ。","romaji":"Kono shigoto wa muzukashikunai ga, nare ga hitsuyō da.","german":"This job isn't difficult, but it takes practice.","furigana":"しごと|むずか|な|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"事故の多くは、慣れからくる油断が原因だ。","romaji":"Jiko no ōku wa, nare kara kuru yudan ga gen'in da.","german":"Many accidents are caused by carelessness born of routine.","furigana":"じこ|おお|な|ゆだん|げんいん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Pejorative for inappropriately familiar behavior: 初対面なのに馴れ馴れしい. Often also written in kana なれなれしい.","examples":[{"japanese":"初対面なのに、彼は馴れ馴れしく話しかけてきた。","romaji":"Shotaimen na noni, kare wa narenareshiku hanashikakete kita.","german":"Even though we were meeting for the first time, he spoke to me in an overly familiar way.","furigana":"しょたいめん|かれ|な|な|はな"},{"japanese":"お客様に馴れ馴れしい態度をとってはいけない。","romaji":"Okyakusama ni narenareshii taido o totte wa ikenai.","german":"You must not act overly familiar with customers.","furigana":"きゃくさま|な|な|たいど"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"As a free-standing noun mainly in set phrases: 難を逃れる (to escape danger), 難を言えば (if one had to find fault), 難なく (without difficulty). Frequent as a word component: 困難, 就職難.","examples":[{"japanese":"火事が起きたが、家族は全員難を逃れた。","romaji":"Kaji ga okita ga, kazoku wa zen'in nan o nogareta.","german":"A fire broke out, but the whole family escaped harm.","furigana":"かじ|お|かぞく|ぜんいんなん|のが"},{"japanese":"いいホテルだが、難を言えば駅から少し遠い。","romaji":"Ii hoteru da ga, nan o ieba eki kara sukoshi tōi.","german":"It's a good hotel, but if I had to find fault, it's a little far from the station.","furigana":"なん|い|えき|すこ|とお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"As a noun or attributively with な: そんな話はナンセンスだ, ナンセンスな質問 (a nonsensical question).","examples":[{"japanese":"年齢だけで能力を判断するのはナンセンスだ。","romaji":"Nenrei dake de nōryoku o handan suru no wa nansensu da.","german":"Judging ability by age alone is nonsense.","furigana":"ねんれい|のうりょく|はんだん"},{"japanese":"彼はナンセンスな冗談ばかり言っている。","romaji":"Kare wa nansensu na jōdan bakari itte iru.","german":"He does nothing but make nonsensical jokes.","furigana":"かれ|じょうだん|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Expresses a vague feeling: なんだか寂しい (somehow sad), なんだか変だ (somehow strange). More colloquial than 何となく.","examples":[{"japanese":"今日はなんだか体がだるい。","romaji":"Kyō wa nandaka karada ga darui.","german":"I feel somehow sluggish today.","furigana":"きょう|からだ"},{"japanese":"引っ越しの日が近づくと、なんだか寂しくなってきた。","romaji":"Hikkoshi no hi ga chikazuku to, nandaka sabishiku natte kita.","german":"As moving day approached, I somehow started to feel lonely.","furigana":"ひ|こ|ひ|ちか|さび"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Colloquial: なんだかんだで忙しい (busy with one thing and another), なんだかんだ言っても (whatever one may say, in the end). Also written 何だかんだ.","examples":[{"japanese":"なんだかんだで、今週は一度も休めなかった。","romaji":"Nandakanda de, konshū wa ichido mo yasumenakatta.","german":"With one thing and another, I haven't had a single day off this week.","furigana":"こんしゅう|いちど|やす"},{"japanese":"なんだかんだ言っても、彼は頼りになる友達だ。","romaji":"Nandakanda itte mo, kare wa tayori ni naru tomodachi da.","german":"Whatever one may say, he is a friend you can rely on.","furigana":"い|かれ|たよ|ともだち"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Interrogative (なんと言えばいいか = how should I put it), exclamatory (なんと美しい景色だろう), and as a surprised opener (なんと百歳だ = a hundred years old, believe it or not).","examples":[{"japanese":"なんと美しい景色だろう。","romaji":"Nanto utsukushii keshiki darō.","german":"What a beautiful view!","furigana":"うつく|けしき"},{"japanese":"その女性は、なんと百歳だそうだ。","romaji":"Sono josei wa, nanto hyakusai da sō da.","german":"That woman is, believe it or not, said to be a hundred years old.","furigana":"じょせい|ひゃくさい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Almost only in the polite phrase 何なりと: 何なりとお申し付けください (please tell me whatever you wish).","examples":[{"japanese":"ご不明な点があれば、何なりとお尋ねください。","romaji":"Gofumei na ten ga areba, nannari to otazune kudasai.","german":"If anything is unclear, please ask whatever you like.","furigana":"ふめい|てん|なん|たず"},{"japanese":"困ったことがあったら、何なりと言ってくれ。","romaji":"Komatta koto ga attara, nannari to itte kure.","german":"If you have any trouble, just tell me, whatever it is.","furigana":"こま|なん|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Collocations: 荷を積む / 荷を降ろす (to load / to unload cargo). Figuratively: 荷が重い (too heavy a responsibility), 肩の荷が下りる (a weight is lifted from one's shoulders).","examples":[{"japanese":"トラックから荷を降ろすのを手伝った。","romaji":"Torakku kara ni o orosu no o tetsudatta.","german":"I helped unload the cargo from the truck.","furigana":"に|お|てつだ"},{"japanese":"試験が終わって、やっと肩の荷が下りた。","romaji":"Shiken ga owatte, yatto kata no ni ga orita.","german":"The exam is over, and at last a weight has been lifted from my shoulders.","furigana":"しけん|お|かた|に|お"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Intransitive, usually as 似通っている or attributively 似通った: 似通った意見 (similar opinions). Somewhat more literary than 似ている.","examples":[{"japanese":"二人の意見は似通っている。","romaji":"Futari no iken wa nikayotte iru.","german":"The two people's opinions are very similar.","furigana":"ふたり|いけん|にかよ"},{"japanese":"似通った商品が多くて、どれを選べばいいか迷う。","romaji":"Nikayotta shōhin ga ōkute, dore o erabeba ii ka mayou.","german":"There are many similar products, so I can't decide which to choose.","furigana":"にかよ|しょうひん|おお|えら|まよ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Usually in kana (also ニキビ). にきびができる (to get a pimple), にきびをつぶす (to squeeze a pimple). The kanji spelling 面皰 is rare.","examples":[{"japanese":"試験の前になると、よくにきびができる。","romaji":"Shiken no mae ni naru to, yoku nikibi ga dekiru.","german":"I often get pimples before exams.","furigana":"しけん|まえ"},{"japanese":"にきびをつぶすと、跡が残ることがある。","romaji":"Nikibi o tsubusu to, ato ga nokoru koto ga aru.","german":"If you squeeze a pimple, a scar may remain.","furigana":"あと|のこ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Emphasizes the physical body as opposed to the mind: 肉体と精神, 肉体労働 (physical labor).","examples":[{"japanese":"肉体は衰えても、心は若いままでいたい。","romaji":"Nikutai wa otoroete mo, kokoro wa wakai mama de itai.","german":"Even if the body declines, I want to stay young at heart.","furigana":"にくたい|おとろ|こころ|わか"},{"japanese":"建設現場の仕事は肉体労働なので、体力が必要だ。","romaji":"Kensetsu genba no shigoto wa nikutai rōdō na node, tairyoku ga hitsuyō da.","german":"Work at a construction site is physical labor, so you need stamina.","furigana":"けんせつげんば|しごと|にくたいろうどう|たいりょく|ひつよう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Intransitive; the place left takes を or から: 家を逃げ出す (to run away from home). Also figurative: 現実から逃げ出す (to flee from reality).","examples":[{"japanese":"檻の扉が開いていて、ライオンが逃げ出した。","romaji":"Ori no tobira ga aite ite, raion ga nigedashita.","german":"The cage door was open, and the lion escaped.","furigana":"おり|とびら|あ|に|だ"},{"japanese":"仕事がつらくても、途中で逃げ出したくはない。","romaji":"Shigoto ga tsurakute mo, tochū de nigedashitaku wa nai.","german":"Even if the work is hard, I don't want to run away halfway.","furigana":"しごと|とちゅう|に|だ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Literary: 日夜努力する (to work ceaselessly), 日夜を問わず (day or night).","examples":[{"japanese":"研究者たちは、新薬の開発に日夜取り組んでいる。","romaji":"Kenkyūshatachi wa, shin'yaku no kaihatsu ni nichiya torikunde iru.","german":"The researchers are working day and night on developing a new drug.","furigana":"けんきゅうしゃ|しんやく|かいはつ|にちやと|く"},{"japanese":"消防士は日夜、町の安全を守っている。","romaji":"Shōbōshi wa nichiya, machi no anzen o mamotte iru.","german":"Firefighters protect the safety of the town day and night.","furigana":"しょうぼうし|にちや|まち|あんぜん|まも"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Pay or expense rate per day: 日当一万円, 日当が出る. Not to be confused with 日当たり (sunniness; exposure to sunlight).","examples":[{"japanese":"このアルバイトは日当一万円だ。","romaji":"Kono arubaito wa nittō ichiman'en da.","german":"This part-time job pays 10,000 yen a day.","furigana":"にっとういちまんえん"},{"japanese":"出張には、交通費のほかに日当が支給される。","romaji":"Shutchō ni wa, kōtsūhi no hoka ni nittō ga shikyū sareru.","german":"On business trips, a daily allowance is paid in addition to travel expenses.","furigana":"しゅっちょう|こうつうひ|にっとう|しきゅう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mostly 入賞する (to win a prize, to place among the prize winners): コンクールで入賞する. It need not be first place (a victory is 優勝).","examples":[{"japanese":"娘の絵が、市のコンクールで入賞した。","romaji":"Musume no e ga, shi no konkūru de nyūshō shita.","german":"My daughter's painting won a prize at the city contest.","furigana":"むすめ|え|し|にゅうしょう"},{"japanese":"入賞者には賞状と記念品が贈られる。","romaji":"Nyūshōsha ni wa shōjō to kinenhin ga okurareru.","german":"Prize winners are given a certificate and a souvenir.","furigana":"にゅうしょうしゃ|しょうじょう|きねんひん|おく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"More formal than お風呂に入る: 入浴する, 入浴中 (while bathing), 入浴剤 (bath additive).","examples":[{"japanese":"熱があるときは、入浴を控えてください。","romaji":"Netsu ga aru toki wa, nyūyoku o hikaete kudasai.","german":"Please refrain from bathing when you have a fever.","furigana":"ねつ|にゅうよく|ひか"},{"japanese":"この旅館では、二十四時間入浴できる。","romaji":"Kono ryokan de wa, nijūyojikan nyūyoku dekiru.","german":"At this inn, you can bathe 24 hours a day.","furigana":"りょかん|にじゅうよじかんにゅうよく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Medical term; in everyday speech おしっこ. 尿検査 (urinalysis), 糖尿病 (diabetes).","examples":[{"japanese":"健康診断で尿を検査した。","romaji":"Kenkō shindan de nyō o kensa shita.","german":"My urine was tested at the health checkup.","furigana":"けんこうしんだん|にょう|けんさ"},{"japanese":"尿に糖が出ていると言われた。","romaji":"Nyō ni tō ga dete iru to iwareta.","german":"I was told that sugar was found in my urine.","furigana":"にょう|とう|で|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive: 一行抜かす (to skip a line), 朝食を抜かす (to skip breakfast), 前の走者を抜かす (to overtake the runner ahead). Set phrase: 腰を抜かす (to be so frightened that one's legs give way).","examples":[{"japanese":"急いでいて、名簿の名前を一人抜かしてしまった。","romaji":"Isoide ite, meibo no namae o hitori nukashite shimatta.","german":"In my hurry, I left one name off the list.","furigana":"いそ|めいぼ|なまえ|ひとりぬ"},{"japanese":"彼はゴールの直前で、二人の選手を抜かした。","romaji":"Kare wa gōru no chokuzen de, futari no senshu o nukashita.","german":"Just before the finish line, he overtook two runners.","furigana":"かれ|ちょくぜん|ふたり|せんしゅ|ぬ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Intransitive with を or から: 会議を抜け出す (to sneak out of a meeting), 不況から抜け出す (to emerge from a recession).","examples":[{"japanese":"退屈な会議をこっそり抜け出した。","romaji":"Taikutsu na kaigi o kossori nukedashita.","german":"I slipped out of the boring meeting unnoticed.","furigana":"たいくつ|かいぎ|ぬ|だ"},{"japanese":"この国の経済は、ようやく不況から抜け出した。","romaji":"Kono kuni no keizai wa, yōyaku fukyō kara nukedashita.","german":"This country's economy has finally pulled out of the recession.","furigana":"くに|けいざい|ふきょう|ぬ|だ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Noun of 盗む. Set phrase: 盗みを働く (to commit theft). As a first element: 盗み聞き (eavesdropping), 盗み見 (a furtive glance).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は生活に困って、盗みを働いた。","romaji":"Kare wa seikatsu ni komatte, nusumi o hataraita.","german":"He was in need and committed theft.","furigana":"かれ|せいかつ|こま|ぬす|はたら"},{"japanese":"盗みは決して許されることではない。","romaji":"Nusumi wa kesshite yurusareru koto de wa nai.","german":"Theft is never excusable.","furigana":"ぬす|けっ|ゆる"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Standing water, usually shallow and muddy. Recent colloquial usage: 〜の沼にハマる (to become completely hooked on a hobby).","examples":[{"japanese":"森の奥に、小さな沼がある。","romaji":"Mori no oku ni, chiisana numa ga aru.","german":"Deep in the forest, there is a small swamp.","furigana":"もり|おく|ちい|ぬま"},{"japanese":"沼に足を取られて、なかなか抜けなかった。","romaji":"Numa ni ashi o torarete, nakanaka nukenakatta.","german":"My foot got stuck in the swamp and I could hardly pull it out.","furigana":"ぬま|あし|と|ぬ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The characteristic sound of an instrument or a voice: バイオリンの音色. In technical contexts also read おんしょく.","examples":[{"japanese":"このバイオリンは、柔らかい音色が特徴だ。","romaji":"Kono baiorin wa, yawarakai neiro ga tokuchō da.","german":"This violin is characterized by its soft tone.","furigana":"やわ|ねいろ|とくちょう"},{"japanese":"風鈴の涼しげな音色が聞こえてきた。","romaji":"Fūrin no suzushige na neiro ga kikoete kita.","german":"I could hear the cool sound of a wind chime.","furigana":"ふうりん|すず|ねいろ|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"値打ちがある (to be worth something, to be worthwhile): 一見の値打ちがある (worth a look). More everyday than 価値.","examples":[{"japanese":"この古い時計には、かなりの値打ちがあるそうだ。","romaji":"Kono furui tokei ni wa, kanari no neuchi ga aru sō da.","german":"This old clock is said to be quite valuable.","furigana":"ふる|とけい|ねう"},{"japanese":"あの映画は一度見る値打ちがある。","romaji":"Ano eiga wa ichido miru neuchi ga aru.","german":"That movie is worth seeing once.","furigana":"えいが|いちどみ|ねう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Short for ネガフィルム; counterpart: ポジ (positive, slide).","examples":[{"japanese":"昔の写真のネガが、押し入れから出てきた。","romaji":"Mukashi no shashin no nega ga, oshiire kara dete kita.","german":"The negatives of old photos turned up from the closet.","furigana":"むかし|しゃしん|お|い|で"},{"japanese":"ネガから写真を焼き増ししてもらった。","romaji":"Nega kara shashin o yakimashi shite moratta.","german":"I had new prints made from the negatives.","furigana":"しゃしん|や|ま"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive: 子どもを寝かせる, 瓶を横に寝かせる (to store a bottle lying down), 生地を寝かせる (to let dough rest). Same meaning, slightly more casual: 寝かす.","examples":[{"japanese":"子どもを寝かせてから、ゆっくり本を読んだ。","romaji":"Kodomo o nekasete kara, yukkuri hon o yonda.","german":"After putting the children to bed, I read a book in peace.","furigana":"こ|ね|ほん|よ"},{"japanese":"パンの生地は、一時間ほど寝かせてください。","romaji":"Pan no kiji wa, ichijikan hodo nekasete kudasai.","german":"Please let the bread dough rest for about an hour.","furigana":"きじ|いちじかん|ね"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Today usually called ドライバー; ねじ回し is the Japanese term.","examples":[{"japanese":"ねじ回しで、緩んだねじを締めた。","romaji":"Nejimawashi de, yurunda neji o shimeta.","german":"I tightened the loose screw with a screwdriver.","furigana":"まわ|ゆる|し"},{"japanese":"棚を組み立てるのに、ねじ回しが一本必要だ。","romaji":"Tana o kumitateru no ni, nejimawashi ga ippon hitsuyō da.","german":"To assemble the shelf, you need one screwdriver.","furigana":"たな|く|た|まわ|いっぽんひつよう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Intransitive counterpart of ねじる (to twist), usually in kana: ネクタイがねじれる. Politics: ねじれ国会 (a parliament whose two houses have different majorities).","examples":[{"japanese":"ネクタイがねじれているよ。","romaji":"Nekutai ga nejirete iru yo.","german":"Your necktie is twisted."},{"japanese":"誤解が重なって、二人の関係はねじれてしまった。","romaji":"Gokai ga kasanatte, futari no kankei wa nejirete shimatta.","german":"After repeated misunderstandings, the two people's relationship has become warped.","furigana":"ごかい|かさ|ふたり|かんけい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive: 親におもちゃをねだる. Usually in kana; the spelling 強請る is also read ゆする (to extort).","examples":[{"japanese":"子どもがおもちゃを買ってとねだった。","romaji":"Kodomo ga omocha o katte to nedatta.","german":"The child pestered me to buy a toy.","furigana":"こ|か"},{"japanese":"彼女は父親に新しいスマホをねだった。","romaji":"Kanojo wa chichioya ni atarashii sumaho o nedatta.","german":"She coaxed her father into getting her a new smartphone.","furigana":"かのじょ|ちちおや|あたら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"熱意がある, 熱意を持つ; 熱意が伝わる (the enthusiasm comes across). More formal than やる気.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼の熱意に動かされて、協力することにした。","romaji":"Kare no netsui ni ugokasarete, kyōryoku suru koto ni shita.","german":"Moved by his enthusiasm, I decided to cooperate.","furigana":"かれ|ねつい|うご|きょうりょく"},{"japanese":"面接では、仕事への熱意を伝えることが大切だ。","romaji":"Mensetsu de wa, shigoto e no netsui o tsutaeru koto ga taisetsu da.","german":"In a job interview, it is important to convey enthusiasm for the work.","furigana":"めんせつ|しごと|ねつい|つた|たいせつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Boiling hot water, not merely warm (お湯): 熱湯を注ぐ, 熱湯で消毒する.","examples":[{"japanese":"カップ麺に熱湯を注いで、三分待つ。","romaji":"Kappumen ni nettō o sosoide, sanpun matsu.","german":"Pour boiling water over the instant noodles and wait three minutes.","furigana":"めん|ねっとう|そそ|さんぷんま"},{"japanese":"哺乳瓶は熱湯で消毒してください。","romaji":"Honyūbin wa nettō de shōdoku shite kudasai.","german":"Please sterilize the baby bottle with boiling water.","furigana":"ほにゅうびん|ねっとう|しょうどく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"In physics, measured in calories or joules: 食品の熱量 (calorific value of a food). Figuratively: 熱量のある演技 (a passionate performance).","examples":[{"japanese":"この食品の熱量は、百グラムあたり二百キロカロリーだ。","romaji":"Kono shokuhin no netsuryō wa, hyakuguramu atari nihyakukirokarorī da.","german":"The calorific value of this food is 200 kilocalories per 100 grams.","furigana":"しょくひん|ねつりょう|ひゃく|にひゃく"},{"japanese":"若い監督の作品には、すごい熱量を感じた。","romaji":"Wakai kantoku no sakuhin ni wa, sugoi netsuryō o kanjita.","german":"I felt tremendous energy in the young director's work.","furigana":"わか|かんとく|さくひん|ねつりょう|かん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Same meaning as 眠い, slightly more colloquial: 眠たそうな目 (sleepy eyes).","examples":[{"japanese":"昨日遅くまで起きていたので、今日は一日中眠たい。","romaji":"Kinō osoku made okite ita node, kyō wa ichinichijū nemutai.","german":"I stayed up late yesterday, so I'm sleepy all day today.","furigana":"きのうおそ|お|きょう|いちにちじゅうねむ"},{"japanese":"弟は眠たそうな目をこすっていた。","romaji":"Otōto wa nemutasō na me o kosutte ita.","german":"My little brother was rubbing his sleepy eyes.","furigana":"おとうと|ねむ|め"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive: 生地を練る (to knead dough), 計画を練る / 作戦を練る (to work out a plan / a strategy). Not to be confused with 寝る (to sleep), which has the same reading.","examples":[{"japanese":"小麦粉に水を加えて、よく練る。","romaji":"Komugiko ni mizu o kuwaete, yoku neru.","german":"Add water to the flour and knead it well.","furigana":"こむぎこ|みず|くわ|ね"},{"japanese":"チームで何度も話し合い、計画を練った。","romaji":"Chīmu de nando mo hanashiai, keikaku o netta.","german":"We discussed it many times as a team and refined the plan.","furigana":"なんど|はな|あ|けいかく|ね"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Free-standing mainly in set phrases: 感謝の念 (gratitude), 念を押す (to make sure once more, to remind emphatically), 念を入れる (to proceed carefully), 念のため (just to be safe).","examples":[{"japanese":"恩師に対して、感謝の念でいっぱいだ。","romaji":"Onshi ni taishite, kansha no nen de ippai da.","german":"I am filled with a sense of gratitude toward my former teacher.","furigana":"おんし|たい|かんしゃ|ねん"},{"japanese":"集合時間を間違えないよう、全員に念を押した。","romaji":"Shūgō jikan o machigaenai yō, zen'in ni nen o oshita.","german":"I reminded everyone once more not to get the meeting time wrong.","furigana":"しゅうごうじかん|まちが|ぜんいん|ねん|お"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mostly in compounds: 年賀状 (New Year's card), 年賀はがき. Free-standing: 年賀に行く (to pay a New Year's visit), 年賀の挨拶 (New Year's greetings).","examples":[{"japanese":"正月には、親戚の家へ年賀に行く。","romaji":"Shōgatsu ni wa, shinseki no ie e nenga ni iku.","german":"At New Year, we go to relatives' houses to pay New Year visits.","furigana":"しょうがつ|しんせき|いえ|ねんが|い"},{"japanese":"喪中のため、年賀の挨拶は控えさせていただきます。","romaji":"Mochū no tame, nenga no aisatsu wa hikaesasete itadakimasu.","german":"Because we are in mourning, we will refrain from sending New Year's greetings.","furigana":"もちゅう|ねんが|あいさつ|ひか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A reference work published annually: 統計年鑑 (statistical yearbook). Not to be confused with 年間 (annual, per year), which has the same reading.","examples":[{"japanese":"図書館で去年の統計年鑑を調べた。","romaji":"Toshokan de kyonen no tōkei nenkan o shirabeta.","german":"At the library, I looked through last year's statistical yearbook.","furigana":"としょかん|きょねん|とうけいねんかん|しら"},{"japanese":"その会社は、毎年業界の年鑑を発行している。","romaji":"Sono kaisha wa, maitoshi gyōkai no nenkan o hakkō shite iru.","german":"That company publishes an industry yearbook every year.","furigana":"かいしゃ|まいとしぎょうかい|ねんかん|はっこう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The Japanese system of dating by eras (also 元号): 年号が変わる. Besides the Western year (西暦), the era year is often used in Japan.","examples":[{"japanese":"2019年に、年号が平成から令和に変わった。","romaji":"2019-nen ni, nengō ga Heisei kara Reiwa ni kawatta.","german":"In 2019, the era name changed from Heisei to Reiwa.","furigana":"ねん|ねんごう|へいせい|れいわ|か"},{"japanese":"書類には、西暦ではなく年号で生年月日を書いてください。","romaji":"Shorui ni wa, seireki de wa naku nengō de seinengappi o kaite kudasai.","german":"On the form, please write your date of birth using the era name, not the Western calendar.","furigana":"しょるい|せいれき|ねんごう|せいねんがっぴ|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"燃焼する (to burn): 脂肪が燃焼する. Figuratively: 完全燃焼 (to give everything, holding nothing back).","examples":[{"japanese":"運動をすると、体の脂肪が燃焼する。","romaji":"Undō o suru to, karada no shibō ga nenshō suru.","german":"Exercise burns the fat in your body.","furigana":"うんどう|からだ|しぼう|ねんしょう"},{"japanese":"最後の試合で完全燃焼できたので、悔いはない。","romaji":"Saigo no shiai de kanzen nenshō dekita node, kui wa nai.","german":"I gave it everything in the last match, so I have no regrets.","furigana":"さいご|しあい|かんぜんねんしょう|く"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"年長の (older), 年長者 (older people). In kindergarten, the classes by age are called 年少, 年中 and 年長 (the oldest, usually five- to six-year-olds).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女はグループの中で一番年長だ。","romaji":"Kanojo wa gurūpu no naka de ichiban nenchō da.","german":"She is the oldest in the group.","furigana":"かのじょ|なか|いちばんねんちょう"},{"japanese":"娘は四月から幼稚園の年長になる。","romaji":"Musume wa shigatsu kara yōchien no nenchō ni naru.","german":"My daughter will move up to the oldest kindergarten class in April.","furigana":"むすめ|しがつ|ようちえん|ねんちょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Aus dem Deutschen (Neurose). Umgangssprachlich für starke nervliche Belastung: 騒音でノイローゼになる.","examples":[{"japanese":"毎晩の騒音で、ノイローゼになりそうだ。","romaji":"Maiban no sōon de, noirōze ni narisō da.","german":"Bei dem allnächtlichen Lärm bekomme ich noch einen Nervenzusammenbruch.","furigana":"まいばん|そうおん"},{"japanese":"仕事のストレスで、彼は一時ノイローゼ気味だった。","romaji":"Shigoto no sutoresu de, kare wa ichiji noirōze gimi datta.","german":"Wegen des Stresses bei der Arbeit war er eine Zeit lang nervlich angeschlagen.","furigana":"しごと|かれ|いちじ|ぎみ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"農地を耕す (to cultivate farmland), 農地を宅地に転用する (to convert farmland into building land).","examples":[{"japanese":"この辺りは、昔は一面の農地だった。","romaji":"Kono atari wa, mukashi wa ichimen no nōchi datta.","german":"This area used to be all farmland.","furigana":"あた|むかし|いちめん|のうち"},{"japanese":"後継者がいなくて、使われない農地が増えている。","romaji":"Kōkeisha ga inakute, tsukawarenai nōchi ga fuete iru.","german":"With no one to take over, unused farmland is increasing.","furigana":"こうけいしゃ|つか|のうち|ふ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive: 会社を乗っ取る (to take over a company), 飛行機を乗っ取る (to hijack an aircraft). Today often: アカウントを乗っ取られる (to have one's account hijacked). Not to be confused with 則る (のっとる, to follow, to conform to).","examples":[{"japanese":"誰かにSNSのアカウントを乗っ取られた。","romaji":"Dareka ni esu enu esu no akaunto o nottorareta.","german":"Someone hijacked my social media account.","furigana":"だれ|の|と"},{"japanese":"テロリストが旅客機を乗っ取った。","romaji":"Terorisuto ga ryokakuki o nottotta.","german":"Terrorists hijacked a passenger plane.","furigana":"りょかくき|の|と"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Before numbers and counters: 延べ人数 (total number, in which the same person counts several times if present on several days), 延べ一万人. Not the same as the number of distinct people.","examples":[{"japanese":"イベントには、三日間で延べ五万人が訪れた。","romaji":"Ibento ni wa, mikkakan de nobe gomannin ga otozureta.","german":"The event drew a cumulative total of 50,000 visitors over three days.","furigana":"みっかかん|の|ごまんにん|おとず"},{"japanese":"この工事には、延べ千人の作業員が関わった。","romaji":"Kono kōji ni wa, nobe sennin no sagyōin ga kakawatta.","german":"A cumulative total of 1,000 workers were involved in this construction work.","furigana":"こうじ|の|せんにん|さぎょういん|かか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive: 薬を飲み込む (to swallow a pill). Figuratively: 事情を飲み込む (to grasp the situation), 言葉を飲み込む (to hold back a remark).","examples":[{"japanese":"大きな錠剤は、うまく飲み込めない。","romaji":"Ōki na jōzai wa, umaku nomikomenai.","german":"I can't swallow large tablets well.","furigana":"おお|じょうざい|の|こ"},{"japanese":"新人は仕事の手順をすぐに飲み込んだ。","romaji":"Shinjin wa shigoto no tejun o sugu ni nomikonda.","german":"The newcomer quickly grasped the work procedures.","furigana":"しんじん|しごと|てじゅん|の|こ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Intransitive with に: 車に乗り込む. Figuratively: 敵地に乗り込む (to advance into enemy territory), 相手の会社に乗り込む (to go to the other party's company to confront them energetically).","examples":[{"japanese":"全員がバスに乗り込むと、すぐに出発した。","romaji":"Zen'in ga basu ni norikomu to, sugu ni shuppatsu shita.","german":"As soon as everyone had boarded the bus, it set off.","furigana":"ぜんいん|の|こ|しゅっぱつ"},{"japanese":"怒った客が、社長室に乗り込んできた。","romaji":"Okotta kyaku ga, shachōshitsu ni norikonde kita.","german":"An angry customer stormed into the president's office.","furigana":"おこ|きゃく|しゃちょうしつ|の|こ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"包丁の刃 (knife blade), 刃が欠ける (the blade gets chipped). Not to be confused with 歯 (tooth) and 葉 (leaf), which have the same reading.","examples":[{"japanese":"包丁の刃が欠けてしまった。","romaji":"Hōchō no ha ga kakete shimatta.","german":"The blade of the kitchen knife has chipped.","furigana":"ほうちょう|は|か"},{"japanese":"刃に触らないように気をつけてください。","romaji":"Ha ni sawaranai yō ni ki o tsukete kudasai.","german":"Please be careful not to touch the blade.","furigana":"は|さわ|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Free-standing e.g. in 二つの派に分かれる (to split into two camps). Very common as a suffix: 賛成派 / 反対派 (supporters / opponents), 印象派 (Impressionism).","examples":[{"japanese":"会議は二つの派に分かれて、議論が続いた。","romaji":"Kaigi wa futatsu no ha ni wakarete, giron ga tsuzuita.","german":"The meeting split into two factions, and the debate continued.","furigana":"かいぎ|ふた|は|わ|ぎろん|つづ"},{"japanese":"モネは印象派を代表する画家だ。","romaji":"Mone wa inshōha o daihyō suru gaka da.","german":"Monet is a leading painter of Impressionism.","furigana":"いんしょうは|だいひょう|がか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Establishment: ホテルのバー (hotel bar). In sports: バーを越える (to clear the bar).","examples":[{"japanese":"仕事の後、ホテルのバーで一杯飲んだ。","romaji":"Shigoto no ato, hoteru no bā de ippai nonda.","german":"After work, I had a drink at the hotel bar.","furigana":"しごと|あと|いっぱいの"},{"japanese":"彼女は二メートルのバーを軽々と越えた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa nimētoru no bā o karugaru to koeta.","german":"She cleared the two-meter bar with ease.","furigana":"かのじょ|に|かるがる|こ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Formal opening phrase of a letter; the corresponding closing phrase is 敬具. A seasonal greeting usually follows. Not to be confused with 背景 (background), which has the same reading.","examples":[{"japanese":"手紙は「拝啓」で始め、「敬具」で結ぶ。","romaji":"Tegami wa \"haikei\" de hajime, \"keigu\" de musubu.","german":"A letter begins with \"Haikei\" and ends with \"Keigu\".","furigana":"てがみ|はいけい|はじ|けいぐ|むす"},{"japanese":"拝啓　春暖の候、ますますご清栄のこととお喜び申し上げます。","romaji":"Haikei, shundan no kō, masumasu goseiei no koto to oyorokobi mōshiagemasu.","german":"Dear Sir or Madam, in this warm spring season, we are pleased that you continue to thrive.","furigana":"はいけい|しゅんだん|こう|せいえい|よろこ|もう|あ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 50 (source: N4; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"Humble form of 借りる: 拝借する. Polite request: お知恵を拝借したい (I would like to ask for your advice).","examples":[{"japanese":"すみません、ペンを拝借してもよろしいですか。","romaji":"Sumimasen, pen o haishaku shite mo yoroshii desu ka.","german":"Excuse me, may I borrow your pen?","furigana":"はいしゃく"},{"japanese":"この件について、皆様のお知恵を拝借したいと思います。","romaji":"Kono ken ni tsuite, minasama no ochie o haishaku shitai to omoimasu.","german":"On this matter, I would like to ask for everyone's advice.","furigana":"けん|みなさま|ちえ|はいしゃく|おも"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Historically mainly Japan's defeat in the war in 1945: 敗戦後の日本. Also in sports: 敗戦の責任 (responsibility for the defeat).","examples":[{"japanese":"敗戦後の日本は、深刻な食料不足に苦しんだ。","romaji":"Haisengo no Nihon wa, shinkoku na shokuryō busoku ni kurushinda.","german":"After the defeat in the war, Japan suffered from a severe food shortage.","furigana":"はいせんご|にほん|しんこく|しょくりょうぶそく|くる"},{"japanese":"監督は敗戦の責任をとって辞任した。","romaji":"Kantoku wa haisen no sekinin o totte jinin shita.","german":"The manager took responsibility for the defeat and resigned.","furigana":"かんとく|はいせん|せきにん|じにん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Optics: 倍率の高い望遠鏡 (a high-magnification telescope). For entrance exams: 倍率が高い (many applicants per place), 倍率十倍 (ten applicants per place).","examples":[{"japanese":"この顕微鏡は、倍率を千倍まで上げられる。","romaji":"Kono kenbikyō wa, bairitsu o senbai made agerareru.","german":"This microscope can raise the magnification up to 1,000 times.","furigana":"けんびきょう|ばいりつ|せんばい|あ"},{"japanese":"人気の大学は入試の倍率が高い。","romaji":"Ninki no daigaku wa nyūshi no bairitsu ga takai.","german":"Popular universities have high competition ratios in entrance exams.","furigana":"にんき|だいがく|にゅうし|ばいりつ|たか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"In general: キーの配列 (key layout), 五十音順に配列する (to arrange in Japanese syllabary order). In computing: 配列 = array.","examples":[{"japanese":"このキーボードは、キーの配列が普通と違う。","romaji":"Kono kībōdo wa, kī no hairetsu ga futsū to chigau.","german":"On this keyboard, the key layout is different from usual.","furigana":"はいれつ|ふつう|ちが"},{"japanese":"プログラムで、データを配列に格納する。","romaji":"Puroguramu de, dēta o hairetsu ni kakunō suru.","german":"In the program, the data is stored in an array.","furigana":"はいれつ|かくのう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive, mostly with abstract nouns: 解決を図る (to seek a solution), 便宜を図る (to show accommodation). Other はかる: 測る (to measure), 量る (to weigh), 計る (to time).","examples":[{"japanese":"政府は景気の回復を図っている。","romaji":"Seifu wa keiki no kaifuku o hakatte iru.","german":"The government is trying to bring about an economic recovery.","furigana":"せいふ|けいき|かいふく|はか"},{"japanese":"両社は話し合いによって、問題の解決を図った。","romaji":"Ryōsha wa hanashiai ni yotte, mondai no kaiketsu o hakatta.","german":"The two companies tried to resolve the problem through talks.","furigana":"りょうしゃ|はな|あ|もんだい|かいけつ|はか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive: 皮を剥ぐ (to strip off the skin), ポスターを剥ぐ. Figuratively also 地位を剥ぐ (to strip someone of rank). Intransitive counterpart: 剥げる.","examples":[{"japanese":"壁の古いポスターを剥いだ。","romaji":"Kabe no furui posutā o haida.","german":"I tore the old posters off the wall.","furigana":"かべ|ふる|は"},{"japanese":"猟師は慣れた手つきで鹿の皮を剥いだ。","romaji":"Ryōshi wa nareta tetsuki de shika no kawa o haida.","german":"With practiced hands, the hunter skinned the deer.","furigana":"りょうし|な|て|しか|かわ|は"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mostly 白状する (to confess): 罪を白状する. Also everyday: 正直に白状すると (to be honest, I have to confess). Not to be confused with 薄情 (heartless), which has the same reading.","examples":[{"japanese":"犯人はついにすべてを白状した。","romaji":"Hannin wa tsui ni subete o hakujō shita.","german":"The culprit finally confessed everything.","furigana":"はんにん|はくじょう"},{"japanese":"正直に白状すると、宿題はまだ終わっていない。","romaji":"Shōjiki ni hakujō suru to, shukudai wa mada owatte inai.","german":"To be honest, I haven't finished my homework yet.","furigana":"しょうじき|はくじょう|しゅくだい|お"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"爆破する (to blow up): ビルを爆破する. Also 爆破予告 (bomb threat).","examples":[{"japanese":"古いビルは、爆破によって一瞬で解体された。","romaji":"Furui biru wa, bakuha ni yotte isshun de kaitai sareta.","german":"The old building was demolished in an instant by blasting.","furigana":"ふる|ばくは|いっしゅん|かいたい"},{"japanese":"橋が爆破され、町への道が断たれた。","romaji":"Hashi ga bakuha sare, machi e no michi ga tatareta.","german":"The bridge was blown up, and the road to the town was cut off.","furigana":"はし|ばくは|まち|みち|た"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Intransitive counterpart of 剥ぐ: ペンキが剥げる (the paint peels off). Figuratively: 化けの皮が剥げる (the mask falls off). Not to be confused with 禿げる (to go bald), which has the same reading.","examples":[{"japanese":"古い壁のペンキが、あちこち剥げている。","romaji":"Furui kabe no penki ga, achikochi hagete iru.","german":"The paint on the old wall is peeling off in many places.","furigana":"ふる|かべ|は"},{"japanese":"何度も洗ったので、シャツのプリントが剥げてきた。","romaji":"Nando mo aratta node, shatsu no purinto ga hagete kita.","german":"I've washed the shirt so many times that the print has started to come off.","furigana":"なんど|あら|は"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"パジャマに着替える (to change into pajamas), パジャマのまま (still in one's pajamas).","examples":[{"japanese":"お風呂から出て、パジャマに着替えた。","romaji":"Ofuro kara dete, pajama ni kigaeta.","german":"After getting out of the bath, I changed into my pajamas.","furigana":"ふろ|で|きが"},{"japanese":"日曜日は、昼までパジャマのまま過ごした。","romaji":"Nichiyōbi wa, hiru made pajama no mama sugoshita.","german":"On Sunday, I stayed in my pajamas until noon.","furigana":"にちようび|ひる|す"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Intransitive, rather literary: 恥じらうように (bashfully). Noun: 恥じらい (shyness, bashfulness).","examples":[{"japanese":"少女は恥じらって、母親の後ろに隠れた。","romaji":"Shōjo wa hajiratte, hahaoya no ushiro ni kakureta.","german":"The girl hid shyly behind her mother.","furigana":"しょうじょ|は|ははおや|うし|かく"},{"japanese":"褒められた彼女は、恥じらうように下を向いた。","romaji":"Homerareta kanojo wa, hajirau yō ni shita o muita.","german":"Praised, she looked down bashfully.","furigana":"ほ|かのじょ|は|した|む"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"橋渡しをする (vermitteln), 橋渡し役 (Vermittlerrolle): 両国の橋渡し (Brücke zwischen zwei Ländern).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は日本とドイツの企業の橋渡しをしている。","romaji":"Kare wa Nihon to Doitsu no kigyō no hashiwatashi o shite iru.","german":"Er vermittelt zwischen japanischen und deutschen Unternehmen.","furigana":"かれ|にほん|きぎょう|はしわた"},{"japanese":"先輩の橋渡しで、憧れの人に会うことができた。","romaji":"Senpai no hashiwatashi de, akogare no hito ni au koto ga dekita.","german":"Durch die Vermittlung eines älteren Kollegen konnte ich die Person treffen, die ich bewundere.","furigana":"せんぱい|はしわた|あこが|ひと|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"破損する (to be damaged): 荷物が破損する. Also for data: ファイルの破損.","examples":[{"japanese":"配送中に、荷物が破損していた。","romaji":"Haisōchū ni, nimotsu ga hason shite ita.","german":"The package was damaged during delivery.","furigana":"はいそうちゅう|にもつ|はそん"},{"japanese":"ファイルが破損していて、開けない。","romaji":"Fairu ga hason shite ite, hirakenai.","german":"The file is corrupted and won't open.","furigana":"はそん|ひら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"機を織る (to weave on a loom), 機織り (weaving). The same kanji is read き (machine, opportunity); the reading はた applies only to the loom.","examples":[{"japanese":"祖母は昔、家で機を織っていた。","romaji":"Sobo wa mukashi, ie de hata o otte ita.","german":"My grandmother used to weave on a loom at home.","furigana":"そぼ|むかし|いえ|はた|お"},{"japanese":"村の博物館では、古い機を使った実演が見られる。","romaji":"Mura no hakubutsukan de wa, furui hata o tsukatta jitsuen ga mirareru.","german":"At the village museum, you can see demonstrations using an old loom.","furigana":"むら|はくぶつかん|ふる|はた|つか|じつえん|み"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Pachinko is a vertical ball game on machines in game halls (パチンコ店). Also the children's slingshot.","examples":[{"japanese":"駅前にはパチンコの店が何軒もある。","romaji":"Ekimae ni wa pachinko no mise ga nangen mo aru.","german":"There are several pachinko parlors in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|みせ|なんげん"},{"japanese":"子どもの頃、パチンコで木の実を飛ばして遊んだ。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, pachinko de ki no mi o tobashite asonda.","german":"As a child, I played by shooting nuts with a slingshot.","furigana":"こ|ころ|き|み|と|あそ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 19 (source: N5; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"Free-standing mainly as 初の (first): 日本初の (Japan's first), 史上初 (first in history). As a first element: 初雪 (first snow), 初恋 (first love).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は日本初の女性宇宙飛行士になった。","romaji":"Kanojo wa Nihon hatsu no josei uchū hikōshi ni natta.","german":"She became Japan's first female astronaut.","furigana":"かのじょ|にほんはつ|じょせいうちゅうひこうし"},{"japanese":"チームは創部以来初の優勝を果たした。","romaji":"Chīmu wa sōbu irai hatsu no yūshō o hatashita.","german":"The team won its first title since the club was founded.","furigana":"そうぶいらいはつ|ゆうしょう|は"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mainly for children, animals and plants: 発育がいい / 発育が遅れる; 発育する.","examples":[{"japanese":"この子は発育がよく、同じ年の子より背が高い。","romaji":"Kono ko wa hatsuiku ga yoku, onaji toshi no ko yori se ga takai.","german":"This child is developing well and is taller than children of the same age.","furigana":"こ|はついく|おな|とし|こ|せ|たか"},{"japanese":"栄養が足りないと、子どもの発育に影響する。","romaji":"Eiyō ga tarinai to, kodomo no hatsuiku ni eikyō suru.","german":"Insufficient nutrition affects children's growth.","furigana":"えいよう|た|こ|はついく|えいきょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"バッジをつける (to wear a badge). 議員バッジ = a legislator's badge.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼はいつも胸に会社のバッジをつけている。","romaji":"Kare wa itsumo mune ni kaisha no bajji o tsukete iru.","german":"He always wears his company badge on his chest.","furigana":"かれ|むね|かいしゃ"},{"japanese":"子どもたちは、好きなキャラクターのバッジを集めている。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa, suki na kyarakutā no bajji o atsumete iru.","german":"The children collect pin badges of their favorite characters.","furigana":"こ|す|あつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mostly 発生する (to occur, to arise): 事故が発生する, 火災が発生する, 熱が発生する. Frequent in news reports.","examples":[{"japanese":"今朝、駅の近くで火災が発生した。","romaji":"Kesa, eki no chikaku de kasai ga hassei shita.","german":"A fire broke out near the station this morning.","furigana":"けさ|えき|ちか|かさい|はっせい"},{"japanese":"モーターが動くと、熱が発生する。","romaji":"Mōtā ga ugoku to, netsu ga hassei suru.","german":"When the motor runs, heat is generated.","furigana":"うご|ねつ|はっせい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mainly the baseball bat: バットを振る (to swing the bat), 素振り (practice swings without a ball).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は思い切りバットを振った。","romaji":"Kare wa omoikiri batto o futta.","german":"He swung the bat with all his might.","furigana":"かれ|おも|き|ふ"},{"japanese":"新しいバットを買って、毎日素振りをしている。","romaji":"Atarashii batto o katte, mainichi suburi o shite iru.","german":"I bought a new bat and practice swinging every day.","furigana":"あたら|か|まいにちすぶ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"発病する (to fall ill): 感染してから発病するまで (from infection until the onset of the disease).","examples":[{"japanese":"父は五十歳で発病し、長い闘病生活を送った。","romaji":"Chichi wa gojussai de hatsubyō shi, nagai tōbyō seikatsu o okutta.","german":"My father fell ill at fifty and lived through a long struggle with the disease.","furigana":"ちち|ごじゅっさい|はつびょう|なが|とうびょうせいかつ|おく"},{"japanese":"感染しても、すぐに発病するとは限らない。","romaji":"Kansen shite mo, sugu ni hatsubyō suru to wa kagiranai.","german":"Even after infection, the disease does not necessarily break out right away.","furigana":"かんせん|はつびょう|かぎ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Noun of 果てる: 地の果て (the end of the earth), 宇宙の果て. Set phrase: 挙げ句の果てに (on top of everything, in the end).","examples":[{"japanese":"地の果てまで行ってでも、君を探し出す。","romaji":"Chi no hate made itte demo, kimi o sagashidasu.","german":"I will find you even if I have to go to the ends of the earth.","furigana":"ち|は|い|きみ|さが|だ"},{"japanese":"宇宙の果ては、どうなっているのだろう。","romaji":"Uchū no hate wa, dō natte iru no darō.","german":"I wonder what the edge of the universe is like.","furigana":"うちゅう|は"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Colloquial, often with heat or sports. Related: 夏ばて (exhaustion from summer heat).","examples":[{"japanese":"暑さでばててしまい、何もする気が起きない。","romaji":"Atsusa de batete shimai, nani mo suru ki ga okinai.","german":"The heat has worn me out, and I don't feel like doing anything.","furigana":"あつ|なに|き|お"},{"japanese":"マラソンの途中でばてて、歩いてしまった。","romaji":"Marason no tochū de batete, aruite shimatta.","german":"I ran out of steam in the middle of the marathon and walked.","furigana":"とちゅう|ある"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"桜の花びら (cherry blossom petals), 花びらが散る (petals fall). The spelling 花弁 is also read はなびら or, in technical contexts, かべん.","examples":[{"japanese":"風が吹いて、桜の花びらが舞い散った。","romaji":"Kaze ga fuite, sakura no hanabira ga maichitta.","german":"The wind blew, and cherry blossom petals fluttered down.","furigana":"かぜ|ふ|さくら|はな|ま|ち"},{"japanese":"池の水面に、花びらが浮かんでいる。","romaji":"Ike no suimen ni, hanabira ga ukande iru.","german":"Petals are floating on the surface of the pond.","furigana":"いけ|すいめん|はな|う"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A sandy shore by the sea; 浜辺 and 海岸 are more everyday. Common in place names (横浜).","examples":[{"japanese":"朝早く、浜を散歩した。","romaji":"Asa hayaku, hama o sanpo shita.","german":"Early in the morning, I took a walk on the beach.","furigana":"あさはや|はま|さんぽ"},{"japanese":"漁師たちが浜で網を干している。","romaji":"Ryōshitachi ga hama de ami o hoshite iru.","german":"Fishermen are drying their nets on the beach.","furigana":"りょうし|はま|あみ|ほ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Interjection used when one wants to look at or check something: どれどれ、見せてごらん. Almost always in kana; the spelling 何々 is usually read なになに.","examples":[{"japanese":"どれどれ、写真を見せてごらん。","romaji":"Doredore, shashin o misete goran.","german":"Well now, let me see, show me the photo.","furigana":"しゃしん|み"},{"japanese":"「熱があるみたい」「どれどれ」と、母は私の額に手を当てた。","romaji":"\"Netsu ga aru mitai\" \"Doredore\" to, haha wa watashi no hitai ni te o ateta.","german":"\"I think I have a fever.\" \"Let me see,\" said my mother, putting her hand on my forehead.","furigana":"ねつ|はは|わたし|ひたい|て|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Strengthening or expanding forces, personnel or capacity: 戦力の増強, 人員を増強する. As a verb 増強する.","examples":[{"japanese":"政府は防衛力の増強を決めた。","romaji":"Seifu wa bōeiryoku no zōkyō o kimeta.","german":"The government decided to strengthen its defense forces.","furigana":"せいふ|ぼうえいりょく|ぞうきょう|き"},{"japanese":"工場は生産設備を増強した。","romaji":"Kōjō wa seisan setsubi o zōkyō shita.","german":"The factory expanded its production facilities.","furigana":"こうじょう|せいさんせつび|ぞうきょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Short for 大蔵大臣, the finance minister before the ministry reform of 2001; today the post is called 財務大臣 (short: 財務相).","examples":[{"japanese":"蔵相は来年度の予算案を発表した。","romaji":"Zōshō wa rainendo no yosan'an o happyō shita.","german":"The finance minister announced the draft budget for the coming fiscal year.","furigana":"ぞうしょう|らいねんど|よさんあん|はっぴょう"},{"japanese":"祖父は、戦後の蔵相の名前をよく覚えている。","romaji":"Sofu wa, sengo no zōshō no namae o yoku oboete iru.","german":"My grandfather remembers well the names of the postwar finance ministers.","furigana":"そふ|せんご|ぞうしょう|なまえ|おぼ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Increase of health, appetite or capability: 食欲増進, 健康の増進. As a verb 増進する.","examples":[{"japanese":"適度な運動は食欲を増進させる。","romaji":"Tekido na undō wa shokuyoku o zōshin saseru.","german":"Moderate exercise stimulates the appetite.","furigana":"てきど|うんどう|しょくよく|ぞうしん"},{"japanese":"市は市民の健康の増進に力を入れている。","romaji":"Shi wa shimin no kenkō no zōshin ni chikara o irete iru.","german":"The city is putting great effort into promoting its citizens' health.","furigana":"し|しみん|けんこう|ぞうしん|ちから|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Ranging from riots and uprisings to a media frenzy or a quarrel (お家騒動 a family feud). Cf. 騒ぎ (noise, commotion, more general).","examples":[{"japanese":"米の値上がりで、各地で騒動が起きた。","romaji":"Kome no neagari de, kakuchi de sōdō ga okita.","german":"Due to the rise in rice prices, riots broke out in various places.","furigana":"こめ|ねあ|かくち|そうどう|お"},{"japanese":"その発言が大きな騒動を引き起こした。","romaji":"Sono hatsugen ga ōkina sōdō o hikiokoshita.","german":"That remark caused a huge uproar.","furigana":"はつげん|おお|そうどう|ひ|お"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Equipment for mountaineering, the military or vehicles; as a verb 装備する (to equip, to be equipped with).","examples":[{"japanese":"冬山に登るには、しっかりした装備が必要だ。","romaji":"Fuyuyama ni noboru ni wa, shikkari shita sōbi ga hitsuyō da.","german":"To climb a mountain in winter, you need solid equipment.","furigana":"ふゆやま|のぼ|そうび|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"この車は最新の安全装置を装備している。","romaji":"Kono kuruma wa saishin no anzen sōchi o sōbi shite iru.","german":"This car is equipped with the latest safety systems.","furigana":"くるま|さいしん|あんぜんそうち|そうび"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Colloquial: そこら(へん) \"somewhere around there\"; after quantities \"or so\": 千円かそこら. Similar: そこいら.","examples":[{"japanese":"鍵ならそこらにあるはずだよ。","romaji":"Kagi nara sokora ni aru hazu da yo.","german":"The key should be somewhere around there.","furigana":"かぎ"},{"japanese":"修理なら一時間かそこらで終わるよ。","romaji":"Shūri nara ichijikan ka sokora de owaru yo.","german":"The repair will be done in an hour or so.","furigana":"しゅうり|いちじかん|お"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Noun of 育つ. 育ちがいい (of good upbringing); after place names \"raised in\": 東京育ち.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は言葉遣いが丁寧で、育ちのよさが感じられる。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kotobazukai ga teinei de, sodachi no yosa ga kanjirareru.","german":"Her speech is polite, and you can sense her good upbringing.","furigana":"かのじょ|ことばづか|ていねい|そだ|かん"},{"japanese":"今年は雨が少なくて、野菜の育ちが悪い。","romaji":"Kotoshi wa ame ga sukunakute, yasai no sodachi ga warui.","german":"It has rained little this year, so the vegetables are growing poorly.","furigana":"ことし|あめ|すく|やさい|そだ|わる"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mostly short socks; 靴下 is more common in general. Knee-high socks are called ハイソックス.","examples":[{"japanese":"白いソックスを三足買った。","romaji":"Shiroi sokkusu o sanzoku katta.","german":"I bought three pairs of white socks.","furigana":"しろ|さんぞくか"},{"japanese":"制服には紺のソックスを合わせる決まりだ。","romaji":"Seifuku ni wa kon no sokkusu o awaseru kimari da.","german":"The rule is to wear navy blue socks with the school uniform.","furigana":"せいふく|こん|あ|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Without friendliness or warmth (reply, attitude); also unadorned (素っ気ない部屋). Often in kana: そっけない.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼の返事はいつも素っ気ない。","romaji":"Kare no henji wa itsumo sokkenai.","german":"His replies are always curt.","furigana":"かれ|へんじ|そ|け"},{"japanese":"素っ気ない態度をとってしまって、後悔している。","romaji":"Sokkenai taido o totte shimatte, kōkai shite iru.","german":"I regret having been so cold.","furigana":"そ|け|たいど|こうかい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Almost only in そっぽを向く: to turn away; figuratively \"to give the cold shoulder, to want nothing to do with\" (客にそっぽを向かれる).","examples":[{"japanese":"話しかけたのに、弟はそっぽを向いた。","romaji":"Hanashikaketa no ni, otōto wa soppo o muita.","german":"I spoke to my little brother, but he just looked away.","furigana":"はな|おとうと|む"},{"japanese":"値上げのせいで、客にそっぽを向かれてしまった。","romaji":"Neage no sei de, kyaku ni soppo o mukarete shimatta.","german":"Because of the price increase, customers have turned their backs on us.","furigana":"ねあ|きゃく|む"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To provide devices or furniture fixed in a place; often resultative: 備え付けてある. Noun: 備え付け (備え付けの家具 built-in furniture).","examples":[{"japanese":"各部屋に消火器が備え付けてある。","romaji":"Kaku heya ni shōkaki ga sonaetsukete aru.","german":"A fire extinguisher is installed in each room.","furigana":"かくへや|しょうかき|そな|つ"},{"japanese":"ホテルの部屋には冷蔵庫が備え付けられている。","romaji":"Hoteru no heya ni wa reizōko ga sonaetsukerarete iru.","german":"The hotel rooms are equipped with a refrigerator.","furigana":"へや|れいぞうこ|そな|つ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Intransitive counterpart of 備える: equipment is present, or someone possesses a quality or talent (才能が備わる).","examples":[{"japanese":"この部屋にはエアコンが備わっている。","romaji":"Kono heya ni wa eakon ga sonawatte iru.","german":"This room is equipped with an air conditioner.","furigana":"へや|そな"},{"japanese":"彼には生まれつき指導者の素質が備わっている。","romaji":"Kare ni wa umaretsuki shidōsha no soshitsu ga sonawatte iru.","german":"He has the makings of a leader by nature.","furigana":"かれ|う|しどうしゃ|そしつ|そな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Noun of 反る. Idiom: 反りが合わない (not to get along with each other; literally, the blade does not fit the scabbard).","examples":[{"japanese":"湿気で板に反りが出てしまった。","romaji":"Shikke de ita ni sori ga dete shimatta.","german":"The board warped from the humidity.","furigana":"しっけ|いた|そ|で"},{"japanese":"日本刀は刀身の反りが美しい。","romaji":"Nihontō wa tōshin no sori ga utsukushii.","german":"Japanese swords have a beautifully curved blade.","furigana":"にほんとう|とうしん|そ|うつく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Usually written in kana; kanji 橇. Dog sled: 犬ぞり (with rendaku).","examples":[{"japanese":"子どもたちは雪の坂をそりで滑り降りた。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa yuki no saka o sori de suberiorita.","german":"The children slid down the snowy slope on a sled.","furigana":"こ|ゆき|さか|すべ|お"},{"japanese":"サンタクロースはトナカイのそりに乗ってやって来る。","romaji":"Santakurōsu wa tonakai no sori ni notte yatte kuru.","german":"Santa Claus comes riding in a reindeer sleigh.","furigana":"の|く"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Intransitive: wood or paper warps, the body bends backward. Transitive: 反らす. Homophone: 剃る (to shave).","examples":[{"japanese":"日に当たって、本の表紙が反ってしまった。","romaji":"Hi ni atatte, hon no hyōshi ga sotte shimatta.","german":"Exposed to the sun, the book cover has warped.","furigana":"ひ|あ|ほん|ひょうし|そ"},{"japanese":"驚いて、思わず体が後ろに反った。","romaji":"Odoroite, omowazu karada ga ushiro ni sotta.","german":"Startled, my body arched backward involuntarily.","furigana":"おどろ|おも|からだ|うし|そ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Written-language conjunction; in conversation, だから or そのため are more usual. Cf. 故に.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は誠実な人だ。それゆえ、多くの人に信頼されている。","romaji":"Kare wa seijitsu na hito da. Soreyue, ōku no hito ni shinrai sarete iru.","german":"He is a sincere person. For that reason, he is trusted by many people.","furigana":"かれ|せいじつ|ひと|おお|ひと|しんらい"},{"japanese":"資源は限られている。それゆえ、大切に使わなければならない。","romaji":"Shigen wa kagirarete iru. Soreyue, taisetsu ni tsukawanakereba naranai.","german":"Resources are limited. Therefore, we must use them carefully.","furigana":"しげん|かぎ|たいせつ|つか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A musical solo or a performance as a solo artist: ソロで活動する. Also generally \"alone\": ソロキャンプ (solo camping).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女はバンドを辞めて、今はソロで活動している。","romaji":"Kanojo wa bando o yamete, ima wa soro de katsudō shite iru.","german":"She left the band and is now active as a solo artist.","furigana":"かのじょ|や|いま|かつどう"},{"japanese":"コンサートでは、ギターのソロが一番盛り上がった。","romaji":"Konsāto de wa, gitā no soro ga ichiban moriagatta.","german":"At the concert, the guitar solo got the crowd most excited.","furigana":"いちばんも|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"揃いの〜: matching, uniform; usually with お: お揃い (the same items, matching outfits). After nouns with rendaku, 〜揃い (ぞろい) \"all ...\": 傑作揃い (けっさくぞろい, nothing but masterpieces).","examples":[{"japanese":"応援団は揃いのユニフォームを着ていた。","romaji":"Ōendan wa soroi no yunifōmu o kite ita.","german":"The cheering squad wore matching uniforms.","furigana":"おうえんだん|そろ|き"},{"japanese":"姉とお揃いのセーターを買った。","romaji":"Ane to osoroi no sētā o katta.","german":"I bought a sweater that matches my older sister's.","furigana":"あね|そろ|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Almost only in the negative: 他意はない (no ulterior motive, no ill intent).","examples":[{"japanese":"他意はありません。ただ事実を述べただけです。","romaji":"Tai wa arimasen. Tada jijitsu o nobeta dake desu.","german":"I have no ulterior motive. I just stated the facts.","furigana":"たい|じじつ|の"},{"japanese":"彼の言葉に他意がないことは分かっている。","romaji":"Kare no kotoba ni tai ga nai koto wa wakatte iru.","german":"I know there is no hidden meaning in his words.","furigana":"かれ|ことば|たい|わ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"An organized group or military unit; usually as a suffix: 救助隊 (rescue team), 探検隊 (expedition), 自衛隊 (Self-Defense Forces).","examples":[{"japanese":"隊員の一人が隊を離れて、行方不明になった。","romaji":"Taiin no hitori ga tai o hanarete, yukue fumei ni natta.","german":"One of the squad members left the squad and went missing.","furigana":"たいいん|ひとり|たい|はな|ゆくえふめい"},{"japanese":"一行は隊を組んで山道を進んだ。","romaji":"Ikkō wa tai o kunde yamamichi o susunda.","german":"The party advanced along the mountain path in formation.","furigana":"いっこう|たい|く|やまみち|すす"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Biologically, the regression of organs; figuratively, the atrophy of abilities. Opposite: 進化 (evolution).","examples":[{"japanese":"洞窟にすむ魚は、目が退化している。","romaji":"Dōkutsu ni sumu sakana wa, me ga taika shite iru.","german":"Fish living in caves have degenerated eyes.","furigana":"どうくつ|さかな|め|たいか"},{"japanese":"便利な道具に頼りすぎると、考える力が退化する。","romaji":"Benri na dōgu ni tayorisugiru to, kangaeru chikara ga taika suru.","german":"If you rely too much on convenient tools, your ability to think deteriorates.","furigana":"べんり|どうぐ|たよ|かんが|ちから|たいか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Overall build (height, weight, strength): 体格がいい (well built). Cf. 体型 (figure).","examples":[{"japanese":"兄は体格がよくて、ラグビーに向いている。","romaji":"Ani wa taikaku ga yokute, ragubī ni muite iru.","german":"My older brother has a good build and is suited to rugby.","furigana":"あに|たいかく|む"},{"japanese":"子どもの体格は昔に比べて大きくなった。","romaji":"Kodomo no taikaku wa mukashi ni kurabete ōkiku natta.","german":"Children's physiques have become larger compared with the past.","furigana":"こ|たいかく|むかし|くら|おお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A large sum of money: 大金を払う, 大金をつぎ込む. Not to be confused with 代金 (だいきん, price paid).","examples":[{"japanese":"宝くじが当たって、大金を手にした。","romaji":"Takarakuji ga atatte, taikin o te ni shita.","german":"He won the lottery and came into a large sum of money.","furigana":"たから|あ|たいきん|て"},{"japanese":"そんな大金を持ち歩くのは危ない。","romaji":"Sonna taikin o mochiaruku no wa abunai.","german":"It is dangerous to carry around so much money.","furigana":"たいきん|も|ある|あぶ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Getting rid of something harmful: pests, or monsters in fairy tales (鬼退治). As a verb 退治する.","examples":[{"japanese":"桃太郎は鬼を退治しに鬼ヶ島へ向かった。","romaji":"Momotarō wa oni o taiji shi ni Onigashima e mukatta.","german":"Momotarou headed to Demon Island to defeat the oni.","furigana":"ももたろう|おに|たいじ||む"},{"japanese":"台所のゴキブリを退治した。","romaji":"Daidokoro no gokiburi o taiji shita.","german":"I exterminated the cockroach in the kitchen.","furigana":"だいどころ|たいじ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Almost always as 「…」と題する/と題した: \"titled ...\". Literary.","examples":[{"japanese":"「未来の都市」と題する講演が行われた。","romaji":"\"Mirai no toshi\" to daisuru kōen ga okonawareta.","german":"A lecture titled \"The City of the Future\" was given.","furigana":"みらい|とし|だい|こうえん|おこな"},{"japanese":"彼は「春」と題した詩を書いた。","romaji":"Kare wa \"Haru\" to daishita shi o kaita.","german":"He wrote a poem titled \"Spring\".","furigana":"かれ|はる|だい|し|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Organized readiness for a task: 態勢を整える, 受け入れ態勢. Homophones: 体制 (system), 大勢 (general situation), 体勢 (body posture).","examples":[{"japanese":"地震に備えて、救助の態勢を整えている。","romaji":"Jishin ni sonaete, kyūjo no taisei o totonoete iru.","german":"Preparations for rescue operations are being made in case of an earthquake.","furigana":"じしん|そな|きゅうじょ|たいせい|ととの"},{"japanese":"受け入れ態勢が整い次第、患者を移します。","romaji":"Ukeire taisei ga totonoi shidai, kanja o utsushimasu.","german":"As soon as everything is ready for admission, we will move the patient.","furigana":"う|い|たいせい|ととの|しだい|かんじゃ|うつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Title of a work (book, film, song) or a championship title in sports: タイトルを防衛する (to defend the title).","examples":[{"japanese":"この本のタイトルは覚えていない。","romaji":"Kono hon no taitoru wa oboete inai.","german":"I don't remember this book's title.","furigana":"ほん|おぼ"},{"japanese":"彼はボクシングで世界タイトルを獲得した。","romaji":"Kare wa bokushingu de sekai taitoru o kakutoku shita.","german":"He won the world title in boxing.","furigana":"かれ|せかい|かくとく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mostly 台無しになる (to be ruined) or 台無しにする (to ruin, to spoil).","examples":[{"japanese":"雨で旅行が台無しになった。","romaji":"Ame de ryokō ga dainashi ni natta.","german":"The rain ruined our trip.","furigana":"あめ|りょこう|だいな"},{"japanese":"余計な一言で、せっかくの雰囲気を台無しにしてしまった。","romaji":"Yokei na hitokoto de, sekkaku no fun'iki o dainashi ni shite shimatta.","german":"With one unnecessary remark, I spoiled the lovely atmosphere.","furigana":"よけい|ひとこと|ふんいき|だいな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Voicing other people's opinions or feelings in their place; as a verb 代弁する. 代弁者: spokesperson. Homophone: 大便.","examples":[{"japanese":"社員の気持ちを代弁して、社長に意見を伝えた。","romaji":"Shain no kimochi o daiben shite, shachō ni iken o tsutaeta.","german":"I conveyed our opinion to the president, speaking for the employees' feelings.","furigana":"しゃいん|きも|だいべん|しゃちょう|いけん|つた"},{"japanese":"この歌は若者の本音を代弁している。","romaji":"Kono uta wa wakamono no honne o daiben shite iru.","german":"This song speaks for the true feelings of young people.","furigana":"うた|わかもの|ほんね|だいべん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Neutral, medical word; childish/colloquial うんち. Counterpart: 小便 (urine). Homophone: 代弁.","examples":[{"japanese":"検査のために大便を採取してください。","romaji":"Kensa no tame ni daiben o saishu shite kudasai.","german":"Please collect a stool sample for the test.","furigana":"けんさ|だいべん|さいしゅ"},{"japanese":"犬の大便は飼い主が持ち帰るのがマナーだ。","romaji":"Inu no daiben wa kainushi ga mochikaeru no ga manā da.","german":"It is proper etiquette for dog owners to take their dogs' feces home.","furigana":"いぬ|だいべん|か|ぬし|も|かえ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mostly attributive 待望の〜 (long-awaited): 待望の新作, 待望の初優勝. As a verb 待望する (to long for).","examples":[{"japanese":"待望の新作がついに発売された。","romaji":"Taibō no shinsaku ga tsui ni hatsubai sareta.","german":"The long-awaited new work has finally been released.","furigana":"たいぼう|しんさく|はつばい"},{"japanese":"国民は平和の訪れを待望している。","romaji":"Kokumin wa heiwa no otozure o taibō shite iru.","german":"The people are longing for the arrival of peace.","furigana":"こくみん|へいわ|おとず|たいぼう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A timer on appliances (タイマーをセットする) or the camera's self-timer (セルフタイマー).","examples":[{"japanese":"朝ご飯に間に合うように、炊飯器のタイマーをセットした。","romaji":"Asagohan ni maniau yō ni, suihanki no taimā o setto shita.","german":"I set the timer on the rice cooker so the rice would be ready in time for breakfast.","furigana":"あさ|はん|ま|あ|すいはんき"},{"japanese":"カメラのタイマーを使って、みんなで写真を撮った。","romaji":"Kamera no taimā o tsukatte, minna de shashin o totta.","german":"We took a group photo using the camera's timer.","furigana":"つか|しゃしん|と"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"In sports, the measured time (タイムを計る) or a time-out (タイムを取る). Homophone: タイム (thyme).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は百メートルで自己最高のタイムを出した。","romaji":"Kare wa hyaku mētoru de jiko saikō no taimu o dashita.","german":"He ran his personal best time in the 100 meters.","furigana":"かれ|ひゃく|じこさいこう|だ"},{"japanese":"監督はタイムを取って、選手に指示を出した。","romaji":"Kantoku wa taimu o totte, senshu ni shiji o dashita.","german":"The coach called a time-out and gave the players instructions.","furigana":"かんとく|と|せんしゅ|しじ|だ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"At the right time, topical: タイムリーな話題. In baseball also タイムリーヒット (a hit that drives in a run).","examples":[{"japanese":"タイムリーな話題を取り上げた番組が人気だ。","romaji":"Taimurī na wadai o toriageta bangumi ga ninki da.","german":"Programs that take up topical issues are popular.","furigana":"わだい|と|あ|ばんぐみ|にんき"},{"japanese":"彼の助言は実にタイムリーだった。","romaji":"Kare no jogen wa jitsu ni taimurī datta.","german":"His advice was truly timely.","furigana":"かれ|じょげん|じつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A personal encounter; today often as the opposite of online: 対面授業 (in-person class). As a verb 対面する.","examples":[{"japanese":"二十年ぶりに、生き別れた兄と対面した。","romaji":"Nijūnen buri ni, ikiwakareta ani to taimen shita.","german":"I met my brother, from whom I had been separated, for the first time in twenty years.","furigana":"にじゅうねん|い|わか|あに|たいめん"},{"japanese":"オンラインではなく、対面で授業を受けたい。","romaji":"Onrain de wa naku, taimen de jugyō o uketai.","german":"I would like to take classes in person rather than online.","furigana":"たいめん|じゅぎょう|う"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Tile; タイル張り (tiled).","examples":[{"japanese":"風呂場のタイルが一枚割れている。","romaji":"Furoba no tairu ga ichimai warete iru.","german":"One tile in the bathroom is cracked.","furigana":"ふろば|いちまいわ"},{"japanese":"台所の壁に白いタイルを貼った。","romaji":"Daidokoro no kabe ni shiroi tairu o hatta.","german":"I put white tiles on the kitchen wall.","furigana":"だいどころ|かべ|しろ|は"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A versatile loanword: decline (給料ダウン), a knockdown in boxing, being laid up with illness or exhaustion (風邪でダウン). Opposite: アップ.","examples":[{"japanese":"風邪でダウンして、三日間寝込んだ。","romaji":"Kaze de daun shite, mikkakan nekonda.","german":"I went down with a cold and stayed in bed for three days.","furigana":"かぜ|みっかかんねこ"},{"japanese":"今月は売り上げが大幅にダウンした。","romaji":"Kongetsu wa uriage ga ōhaba ni daun shita.","german":"This month, sales have fallen sharply.","furigana":"こんげつ|う|あ|おおはば"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Intransitive: feelings, interest, tension or reputation increase. Transitive: 高める.","examples":[{"japanese":"試合が近づくにつれて、緊張が高まってきた。","romaji":"Shiai ga chikazuku ni tsurete, kinchō ga takamatte kita.","german":"As the match drew closer, the tension rose.","furigana":"しあい|ちか|きんちょう|たか"},{"japanese":"環境問題への関心が高まっている。","romaji":"Kankyō mondai e no kanshin ga takamatte iru.","german":"Interest in environmental issues is growing.","furigana":"かんきょうもんだい|かんしん|たか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A fire of wood or leaves outdoors; 焚き火をする (to make a bonfire). From the verb 焚く (to burn, to light a fire).","examples":[{"japanese":"キャンプの夜、みんなで焚き火を囲んだ。","romaji":"Kyanpu no yoru, minna de takibi o kakonda.","german":"On the camping night, we all sat around the bonfire.","furigana":"よる|た|び|かこ"},{"japanese":"落ち葉を集めて焚き火をした。","romaji":"Ochiba o atsumete takibi o shita.","german":"We gathered fallen leaves and made a bonfire.","furigana":"お|ば|あつ|た|び"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Literary: 〜の類い \"... and the like\"; 類いまれな \"unparalleled, extraordinary\". The spelling 類 is usually read るい.","examples":[{"japanese":"幽霊の類いは信じていない。","romaji":"Yūrei no tagui wa shinjite inai.","german":"I don't believe in ghosts and the like.","furigana":"ゆうれい|たぐ|しん"},{"japanese":"彼女は類いまれな才能の持ち主だ。","romaji":"Kanojo wa taguimare na sainō no mochinushi da.","german":"She is the possessor of a rare talent.","furigana":"かのじょ|たぐ|さいのう|も|ぬし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Today mainly the length of clothing (スカート丈, 着丈) and in 思いの丈 (all one's feelings). Not to be confused with だけ (only).","examples":[{"japanese":"このスカートは丈が少し短い。","romaji":"Kono sukāto wa take ga sukoshi mijikai.","german":"This skirt is a little short in length.","furigana":"たけ|すこ|みじか"},{"japanese":"思いの丈を手紙に書いた。","romaji":"Omoi no take o tegami ni kaita.","german":"I wrote all my feelings in a letter.","furigana":"おも|たけ|てがみ|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Literally a blow, mostly figurative: severe damage (経済的打撃). In baseball: batting (打撃力 hitting power).","examples":[{"japanese":"台風は農業に大きな打撃を与えた。","romaji":"Taifū wa nōgyō ni ōkina dageki o ataeta.","german":"The typhoon dealt a heavy blow to agriculture.","furigana":"たいふう|のうぎょう|おお|だげき|あた"},{"japanese":"彼は打撃が得意な選手だ。","romaji":"Kare wa dageki ga tokui na senshu da.","german":"He is a player who is good at batting.","furigana":"かれ|だげき|とくい|せんしゅ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Twelve pieces: 一ダース = 12. Attached directly to the number like a counter. The rare kanji spelling is 打.","examples":[{"japanese":"鉛筆を一ダース買った。","romaji":"Enpitsu o ichidāsu katta.","german":"I bought a dozen pencils.","furigana":"えんぴつ|いち|か"},{"japanese":"卵を二ダース注文した。","romaji":"Tamago o nidāsu chūmon shita.","german":"I ordered two dozen eggs.","furigana":"たまご|に|ちゅうもん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A decision by majority of votes: 多数決で決める, 多数決を取る.","examples":[{"japanese":"クラスの意見が分かれたので、多数決で決めた。","romaji":"Kurasu no iken ga wakareta node, tasūketsu de kimeta.","german":"The class was divided in opinion, so we decided by majority vote.","furigana":"いけん|わ|たすうけつ|き"},{"japanese":"多数決が必ずしも正しいとは限らない。","romaji":"Tasūketsu ga kanarazushimo tadashii to wa kagiranai.","german":"A majority decision is not necessarily right.","furigana":"たすうけつ|かなら|ただ|かぎ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Noun of 助ける: 助けを求める (to ask for help), 助けになる (to be helpful). Cry for help: 助けて!","examples":[{"japanese":"川で溺れている人が助けを求めていた。","romaji":"Kawa de oborete iru hito ga tasuke o motomete ita.","german":"A person drowning in the river was calling for help.","furigana":"かわ|おぼ|ひと|たす|もと"},{"japanese":"友達の助けがなければ、この仕事は終わらなかった。","romaji":"Tomodachi no tasuke ga nakereba, kono shigoto wa owaranakatta.","german":"Without my friends' help, I wouldn't have finished this job.","furigana":"ともだち|たす|しごと|お"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To make a short stop somewhere along the way; politely お立ち寄りください (please stop by). Colloquial: 寄る.","examples":[{"japanese":"帰りに本屋に立ち寄った。","romaji":"Kaeri ni hon'ya ni tachiyotta.","german":"On the way home, I stopped by the bookstore.","furigana":"かえ|ほんや|た|よ"},{"japanese":"近くにお越しの際は、ぜひお立ち寄りください。","romaji":"Chikaku ni okoshi no sai wa, zehi otachiyori kudasai.","german":"When you are in the neighborhood, please do stop by.","furigana":"ちか|こ|さい|た|よ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Healthy and vigorous (お達者で! \"Take care of yourself!\" when parting) or skilled: 口が達者 (eloquent; also: all talk).","examples":[{"japanese":"祖母は年を取っても、まだまだ達者だ。","romaji":"Sobo wa toshi o totte mo, madamada tassha da.","german":"Even at her age, my grandmother is still hale and hearty.","furigana":"そぼ|とし|と|たっしゃ"},{"japanese":"彼女は英語が達者だ。","romaji":"Kanojo wa eigo ga tassha da.","german":"She is proficient in English.","furigana":"かのじょ|えいご|たっしゃ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Literary: to get out of a crisis, danger or phase (危機を脱する). Colloquial: 抜け出す.","examples":[{"japanese":"患者はようやく危険な状態を脱した。","romaji":"Kanja wa yōyaku kiken na jōtai o dasshita.","german":"The patient has finally passed the danger point.","furigana":"かんじゃ|きけん|じょうたい|だっ"},{"japanese":"景気は低迷から脱しつつある。","romaji":"Keiki wa teimei kara dasshitsutsu aru.","german":"The economy is gradually emerging from the slump.","furigana":"けいき|ていめい|だっ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Leaving an organization, party, band or treaty; as a verb 脱退する. Opposite: 加入, 加盟.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は去年、労働組合を脱退した。","romaji":"Kare wa kyonen, rōdō kumiai o dattai shita.","german":"He left the labor union last year.","furigana":"かれ|きょねん|ろうどうくみあい|だったい"},{"japanese":"その国は国際条約からの脱退を表明した。","romaji":"Sono kuni wa kokusai jōyaku kara no dattai o hyōmei shita.","german":"The country announced its withdrawal from the international treaty.","furigana":"くに|こくさいじょうやく|だったい|ひょうめい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Colloquial sentence opener that picks up on what was said. More polite: でしたら; neutral: それなら.","examples":[{"japanese":"「雨が降ってきたよ。」「だったら、タクシーで行こう。」","romaji":"\"Ame ga futte kita yo.\" \"Dattara, takushī de ikō.\"","german":"\"It's started to rain.\" \"Then let's go by taxi.\"","furigana":"あめ|ふ|い"},{"japanese":"行きたくないの?だったら、無理に来なくてもいいよ。","romaji":"Ikitakunai no? Dattara, muri ni konakute mo ii yo.","german":"You don't want to go? Then you don't have to come along.","furigana":"い|むり|こ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Literary; also read とうとぶ and also written 貴ぶ.","examples":[{"japanese":"この国では昔から礼儀が尊ばれてきた。","romaji":"Kono kuni de wa mukashi kara reigi ga tattobarete kita.","german":"Politeness has been prized in this country since long ago.","furigana":"くに|むかし|れいぎ|たっと"},{"japanese":"先人の知恵を尊ぶべきだ。","romaji":"Senjin no chie o tattobu beki da.","german":"We should respect the wisdom of our predecessors.","furigana":"せんじん|ちえ|たっと"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A protective shield; figuratively 〜を盾に(取る) (to take cover behind something, to use as a pretext). Cf. 矛盾 (literally \"spear and shield\": contradiction).","examples":[{"japanese":"兵士たちは盾で矢を防いだ。","romaji":"Heishitachi wa tate de ya o fuseida.","german":"The soldiers blocked the arrows with their shields.","furigana":"へいし|たて|や|ふせ"},{"japanese":"彼は法律を盾に、支払いを拒んだ。","romaji":"Kare wa hōritsu o tate ni, shiharai o kobanda.","german":"He used the law as a pretext to refuse payment.","furigana":"かれ|ほうりつ|たて|しはら|こば"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To pay temporarily for someone else, expecting repayment. Homophone: 建て替える (to rebuild).","examples":[{"japanese":"財布を忘れたので、友達に昼ご飯代を立て替えてもらった。","romaji":"Saifu o wasureta node, tomodachi ni hirugohandai o tatekaete moratta.","german":"I had forgotten my wallet, so a friend paid for my lunch.","furigana":"さいふ|わす|ともだち|ひる|はんだい|た|か"},{"japanese":"交通費はいったん立て替えて、後で会社に請求してください。","romaji":"Kōtsūhi wa ittan tatekaete, ato de kaisha ni seikyū shite kudasai.","german":"Please pay the transportation costs yourself first, and claim them from the company later.","furigana":"こうつうひ|た|か|あと|かいしゃ|せいきゅう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Very reverent, today mainly religious or archaic. Figuratively: to put someone in a high position in name only.","examples":[{"japanese":"神前に供え物を奉った。","romaji":"Shinzen ni sonaemono o tatematsutta.","german":"Offerings were presented before the altar of the gods.","furigana":"しんぜん|そな|もの|たてまつ"},{"japanese":"彼は名ばかりの会長に奉られている。","romaji":"Kare wa na bakari no kaichō ni tatematsurarete iru.","german":"He has been installed as chairman in name only.","furigana":"かれ|な|かいちょう|たてまつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To arrive finally after effort or a long journey, also figuratively (結論にたどり着く). Cf. たどる (to follow a path).","examples":[{"japanese":"道に迷ったが、夜遅くにようやく宿にたどり着いた。","romaji":"Michi ni mayotta ga, yoru osoku ni yōyaku yado ni tadoritsuita.","german":"I lost my way, but finally reached the inn late at night.","furigana":"みち|まよ|よるおそ|やど|つ"},{"japanese":"長い議論の末、ようやく結論にたどり着いた。","romaji":"Nagai giron no sue, yōyaku ketsuron ni tadoritsuita.","german":"After a long discussion, we finally reached a conclusion.","furigana":"なが|ぎろん|すえ|けつろん|つ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Humble word for もらう, typical in speeches and business letters: ご指導を賜る. Less often also \"to bestow\" (by a superior).","examples":[{"japanese":"本日は貴重なご意見を賜り、ありがとうございました。","romaji":"Honjitsu wa kichō na goiken o tamawari, arigatō gozaimashita.","german":"Thank you for giving us your valuable opinions today.","furigana":"ほんじつ|きちょう|いけん|たまわ"},{"japanese":"今後ともご指導を賜りますよう、お願い申し上げます。","romaji":"Kongo tomo goshidō o tamawarimasu yō, onegai mōshiagemasu.","german":"We ask that you continue to favor us with your guidance in the future.","furigana":"こんご|しどう|たまわ|ねが|もう|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Literary for 簡単 or 易しい; often also in kana たやすい. 容易 (ようい), by contrast, is a na-adjective: 容易な.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼にとって、この問題を解くのは容易いことだ。","romaji":"Kare ni totte, kono mondai o toku no wa tayasui koto da.","german":"For him, solving this problem is easy.","furigana":"かれ|もんだい|と|たやす"},{"japanese":"口で言うのは容易いが、実行するのは難しい。","romaji":"Kuchi de iu no wa tayasui ga, jikkō suru no wa muzukashii.","german":"It is easy to say, but difficult to carry out.","furigana":"くち|い|たやす|じっこう|むずか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Noun of 弛む: 顔のたるみ (slack skin); figuratively laxity (気のたるみ). Often in kana; the kanji 弛み can also be read ゆるみ.","examples":[{"japanese":"年齢とともに、顔の弛みが気になってきた。","romaji":"Nenrei to tomo ni, kao no tarumi ga ki ni natte kita.","german":"With age, I've become more concerned about the sagging of my face.","furigana":"ねんれい|かお|たる|き"},{"japanese":"ロープに弛みがないように、しっかり張ってください。","romaji":"Rōpu ni tarumi ga nai yō ni, shikkari hatte kudasai.","german":"Please pull the rope tight so that there is no slack.","furigana":"たる|は"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Ropes, skin or discipline become slack: 気持ちがたるむ. Often in kana; the kanji 弛む can also be read ゆるむ.","examples":[{"japanese":"電線が弛んで、地面に近づいている。","romaji":"Densen ga tarunde, jimen ni chikazuite iru.","german":"The power line is sagging and getting closer to the ground.","furigana":"でんせん|たる|じめん|ちか"},{"japanese":"最近、練習中に気持ちが弛んでいるぞ。","romaji":"Saikin, renshūchū ni kimochi ga tarunde iru zo.","german":"Lately your attitude has been slack during practice!","furigana":"さいきん|れんしゅうちゅう|きも|たる"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Im Japanischen meist eine Person aus dem Fernsehen (Moderator, Promi), nicht „Talent“ im deutschen Sinn; dafür eher 才能.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女はテレビのタレントとして人気がある。","romaji":"Kanojo wa terebi no tarento to shite ninki ga aru.","german":"Sie ist als Fernsehpersönlichkeit beliebt.","furigana":"かのじょ|にんき"},{"japanese":"最近はお笑い芸人出身のタレントが多い。","romaji":"Saikin wa owarai geinin shusshin no tarento ga ōi.","german":"In letzter Zeit gibt es viele TV-Stars, die ursprünglich Comedians waren.","furigana":"さいきん|わら|げいにんしゅっしん|おお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A tower as a modern structure (東京タワー); also a high-rise: タワーマンション (tower apartment building). Traditional towers: 塔.","examples":[{"japanese":"駅前に高いタワーが建った。","romaji":"Ekimae ni takai tawā ga tatta.","german":"A tall tower was built in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|たか|た"},{"japanese":"東京タワーの展望台から富士山が見えた。","romaji":"Tōkyō Tawā no tenbōdai kara Fujisan ga mieta.","german":"We could see Mt. Fuji from the observation deck of Tokyo Tower.","furigana":"とうきょう|てんぼうだい|ふじさん|み"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A raised surface for speakers (壇上 on the platform, 教壇 teacher's lectern); as a suffix the professional world: 文壇 (literary world), 画壇 (art world).","examples":[{"japanese":"校長先生が壇に上がって挨拶をした。","romaji":"Kōchō sensei ga dan ni agatte aisatsu o shita.","german":"The principal stepped up onto the platform and gave a speech.","furigana":"こうちょうせんせい|だん|あ|あいさつ"},{"japanese":"彼は二十代で文壇にデビューした。","romaji":"Kare wa nijūdai de bundan ni debyū shita.","german":"He made his debut on the literary scene in his twenties.","furigana":"かれ|にじゅうだい|ぶんだん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Single, not composite: 単一の〜, 単一通貨 (single currency). For batteries: 単一 = size D, 単三 = AA.","examples":[{"japanese":"ユーロ圏では単一の通貨が使われている。","romaji":"Yūroken de wa tan'itsu no tsūka ga tsukawarete iru.","german":"A single currency is used in the eurozone.","furigana":"けん|たんいつ|つうか|つか"},{"japanese":"この製品は単一の素材で作られているので、リサイクルしやすい。","romaji":"Kono seihin wa tan'itsu no sozai de tsukurarete iru node, risaikuru shiyasui.","german":"This product is made of a single material, so it is easy to recycle.","furigana":"せいひん|たんいつ|そざい|つく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Banding together for a common goal; as a verb 団結する (to stand together).","examples":[{"japanese":"クラス全員が団結して、合唱コンクールで優勝した。","romaji":"Kurasu zen'in ga danketsu shite, gasshō konkūru de yūshō shita.","german":"The whole class pulled together and won the choral competition.","furigana":"ぜんいん|だんけつ|がっしょう|ゆうしょう"},{"japanese":"今こそ国民の団結が必要だ。","romaji":"Ima koso kokumin no danketsu ga hitsuyō da.","german":"Now more than ever, the nation needs unity.","furigana":"いま|こくみん|だんけつ|ひつよう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Exploring unknown territory; as a verb 探検する. 探検家 (explorer), 探検隊 (expedition party). Also written 探険.","examples":[{"japanese":"子どものころ、近所の森を探検するのが好きだった。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro, kinjo no mori o tanken suru no ga suki datta.","german":"As a child, I loved exploring the woods in my neighborhood.","furigana":"こ|きんじょ|もり|たんけん|す"},{"japanese":"南極の探検には、入念な準備が欠かせない。","romaji":"Nankyoku no tanken ni wa, nyūnen na junbi ga kakasenai.","german":"Thorough preparation is essential for an Antarctic expedition.","furigana":"なんきょく|たんけん|にゅうねん|じゅんび|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Asserting something with certainty; as a verb 断言する. Often negated: 断言できない.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は「絶対に勝つ」と断言した。","romaji":"Kare wa \"Zettai ni katsu\" to dangen shita.","german":"He declared, \"We will definitely win.\"","furigana":"かれ|ぜったい|か|だんげん"},{"japanese":"それが正しいとは断言できない。","romaji":"Sore ga tadashii to wa dangen dekinai.","german":"I can't say for certain that it's right.","furigana":"ただ|だんげん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Short for 短期大学: a two- to three-year college with a practical focus.","examples":[{"japanese":"姉は短大を卒業して、保育士になった。","romaji":"Ane wa tandai o sotsugyō shite, hoikushi ni natta.","german":"My older sister graduated from junior college and became a nursery teacher.","furigana":"あね|たんだい|そつぎょう|ほいくし"},{"japanese":"短大では二年間、栄養学を学んだ。","romaji":"Tandai de wa ninenkan, eiyōgaku o mananda.","german":"At junior college I studied nutrition for two years.","furigana":"たんだい|にねんかん|えいようがく|まな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A radio frequency band; cf. 中波 (medium wave), 長波 (long wave).","examples":[{"japanese":"短波のラジオで海外の放送を聞いた。","romaji":"Tanpa no rajio de kaigai no hōsō o kiita.","german":"I listened to overseas broadcasts on a shortwave radio.","furigana":"たんぱ|かいがい|ほうそう|き"},{"japanese":"船との通信には短波が使われた。","romaji":"Fune to no tsūshin ni wa tanpa ga tsukawareta.","german":"Shortwave was used to communicate with ships.","furigana":"ふね|つうしん|たんぱ|つか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Short for ダンプカー (dump truck, a Japanese coinage).","examples":[{"japanese":"工事現場にダンプが何台も出入りしている。","romaji":"Kōji genba ni danpu ga nandai mo deiri shite iru.","german":"Dump trucks are constantly going in and out of the construction site.","furigana":"こうじげんば|なんだい|でい"},{"japanese":"父はダンプの運転手として働いている。","romaji":"Chichi wa danpu no untenshu to shite hataraite iru.","german":"My father works as a dump truck driver.","furigana":"ちち|うんてんしゅ|はたら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A cut surface (断面図 cross-section drawing); figuratively a slice or aspect: 社会の一断面.","examples":[{"japanese":"木の断面には年輪が見える。","romaji":"Ki no danmen ni wa nenrin ga mieru.","german":"You can see the annual rings in the cross section of a tree.","furigana":"き|だんめん|ねんりん|み"},{"japanese":"この小説は現代社会の一断面を描いている。","romaji":"Kono shōsetsu wa gendai shakai no ichidanmen o egaite iru.","german":"This novel depicts one facet of modern society.","furigana":"しょうせつ|げんだいしゃかい|いちだんめん|えが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Colloquial for \"change\" (イメージチェンジ, short イメチェン); in baseball, the switch between batting and fielding teams. More neutral: 変更, 交代.","examples":[{"japanese":"気分転換に、髪型をチェンジした。","romaji":"Kibun tenkan ni, kamigata o chenji shita.","german":"For a change of mood, I changed my hairstyle.","furigana":"きぶんてんかん|かみがた"},{"japanese":"スリーアウトでチェンジになった。","romaji":"Surī auto de chenji ni natta.","german":"After the third out, the teams switched sides."}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive counterpart of 違う: to make something different (色を違える), to break a promise (約束を違える), to sprain or crick (筋を違える). Common in compounds: 間違える, 寝違える.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は約束を違えるような人ではない。","romaji":"Kare wa yakusoku o chigaeru yō na hito de wa nai.","german":"He is not the kind of person to break a promise.","furigana":"かれ|やくそく|ちが|ひと"},{"japanese":"重い荷物を持ち上げて、首の筋を違えてしまった。","romaji":"Omoi nimotsu o mochiagete, kubi no suji o chigaete shimatta.","german":"I lifted a heavy load and strained a muscle in my neck.","furigana":"おも|にもつ|も|あ|くび|すじ|ちが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Literary/formal: to pledge lasting fidelity, love, or a reunion. Noun: 契り (pledge, vow). Homophone: 千切る (to tear off).","examples":[{"japanese":"二人は永遠の愛を契った。","romaji":"Futari wa eien no ai o chigitta.","german":"The two pledged eternal love to each other.","furigana":"ふたり|えいえん|あい|ちぎ"},{"japanese":"固く再会を契って、二人は別れた。","romaji":"Kataku saikai o chigitte, futari wa wakareta.","german":"With a firm promise to meet again, the two parted.","furigana":"かた|さいかい|ちぎ|ふたり|わか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The ability to think and judge, often as a character trait (知性のある人). Cf. 知能 (measurable intelligence).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女の話し方には知性が感じられる。","romaji":"Kanojo no hanashikata ni wa chisei ga kanjirareru.","german":"You can sense intelligence in the way she speaks.","furigana":"かのじょ|はな|かた|ちせい|かん"},{"japanese":"知性と感性の両方を磨くことが大切だ。","romaji":"Chisei to kansei no ryōhō o migaku koto ga taisetsu da.","german":"It is important to cultivate both intellect and sensibility.","furigana":"ちせい|かんせい|りょうほう|みが|たいせつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mother's milk or animal milk, and also the breast. Homophone: 父 (father). As an everyday drink: 牛乳, ミルク.","examples":[{"japanese":"赤ちゃんが母親の乳を飲んでいる。","romaji":"Akachan ga hahaoya no chichi o nonde iru.","german":"The baby is nursing at its mother's breast.","furigana":"あか|ははおや|ちち|の"},{"japanese":"牛の乳を搾る仕事は朝早くから始まる。","romaji":"Ushi no chichi o shiboru shigoto wa asa hayaku kara hajimaru.","german":"The work of milking cows starts early in the morning.","furigana":"うし|ちち|しぼ|しごと|あさはや|はじ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Relating to the intellect or seeming intelligent: 知的な人, 知的財産 (intellectual property).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は知的な雰囲気のある人だ。","romaji":"Kare wa chiteki na fun'iki no aru hito da.","german":"He has an intellectual air about him.","furigana":"かれ|ちてき|ふんいき|ひと"},{"japanese":"読書は知的な好奇心を満たしてくれる。","romaji":"Dokusho wa chiteki na kōkishin o mitashite kureru.","german":"Reading satisfies intellectual curiosity.","furigana":"どくしょ|ちてき|こうきしん|み"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Cooperation within a group: チームワークがいい (to work well together).","examples":[{"japanese":"このチームの強さはチームワークにある。","romaji":"Kono chīmu no tsuyosa wa chīmuwāku ni aru.","german":"The strength of this team lies in its teamwork.","furigana":"つよ"},{"japanese":"職場ではチームワークが大切だ。","romaji":"Shokuba de wa chīmuwāku ga taisetsu da.","german":"Teamwork is important in the workplace.","furigana":"しょくば|たいせつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The chime at schools and stations; also the doorbell (玄関のチャイム).","examples":[{"japanese":"授業の終わりのチャイムが鳴った。","romaji":"Jugyō no owari no chaimu ga natta.","german":"The chime signaling the end of class rang.","furigana":"じゅぎょう|お|な"},{"japanese":"玄関のチャイムを鳴らしたが、誰も出てこなかった。","romaji":"Genkan no chaimu o narashita ga, dare mo dete konakatta.","german":"I rang the doorbell, but no one came out.","furigana":"げんかん|な|だれ|で"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Adding color to food, photos, or materials; 着色料 (coloring agent), 無着色 (no added coloring). As a verb 着色する.","examples":[{"japanese":"このお菓子は人工的に着色されている。","romaji":"Kono okashi wa jinkōteki ni chakushoku sarete iru.","german":"These sweets are artificially colored.","furigana":"かし|じんこうてき|ちゃくしょく"},{"japanese":"白黒写真に着色して、昔の町並みを再現した。","romaji":"Shirokuro shashin ni chakushoku shite, mukashi no machinami o saigen shita.","german":"We recreated the old townscape by coloring black-and-white photographs.","furigana":"しろくろしゃしん|ちゃくしょく|むかし|まちな|さいげん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Formal for 座る. In class: 起立、礼、着席 (stand, bow, sit).","examples":[{"japanese":"皆様、ご着席ください。","romaji":"Minasama, gochakuseki kudasai.","german":"Ladies and gentlemen, please be seated.","furigana":"みなさま|ちゃくせき"},{"japanese":"先生が入ってくると、生徒たちは静かに着席した。","romaji":"Sensei ga haitte kuru to, seitotachi wa shizuka ni chakuseki shita.","german":"When the teacher came in, the students quietly sat down.","furigana":"せんせい|はい|せいと|しず|ちゃくせき"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A TV channel, now also a video channel (動画チャンネル). The spelling チャネル is mainly used in technical contexts (販売チャネル sales channel).","examples":[{"japanese":"チャンネルを変えてもいい?","romaji":"Channeru o kaete mo ii?","german":"Is it okay if I change the channel?","furigana":"か"},{"japanese":"彼女は料理の動画チャンネルを運営している。","romaji":"Kanojo wa ryōri no dōga channeru o un'ei shite iru.","german":"She runs a cooking video channel.","furigana":"かのじょ|りょうり|どうが|うんえい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The traditional family room; figuratively, TV viewers at home: お茶の間の人気者.","examples":[{"japanese":"家族はいつも茶の間に集まってテレビを見る。","romaji":"Kazoku wa itsumo chanoma ni atsumatte terebi o miru.","german":"The family always gathers in the living room to watch TV.","furigana":"かぞく|ちゃ|ま|あつ|み"},{"japanese":"その番組は全国のお茶の間で人気を集めた。","romaji":"Sono bangumi wa zenkoku no ochanoma de ninki o atsumeta.","german":"The program became popular in living rooms across the country.","furigana":"ばんぐみ|ぜんこく|ちゃ|ま|にんき|あつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A flip in the air (gymnastics) or a loop (aircraft); 後方宙返り (backflip).","examples":[{"japanese":"体操選手が見事な宙返りを決めた。","romaji":"Taisō senshu ga migoto na chūgaeri o kimeta.","german":"The gymnast landed a beautiful somersault.","furigana":"たいそうせんしゅ|みごと|ちゅうがえ|き"},{"japanese":"飛行機が空で宙返りをして見せた。","romaji":"Hikōki ga sora de chūgaeri o shite miseta.","german":"The airplane performed a loop in the sky.","furigana":"ひこうき|そら|ちゅうがえ|み"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Broadcasting a program, especially live: 生中継 (live broadcast). Also an intermediate stop: 中継地点. As a verb 中継する.","examples":[{"japanese":"サッカーの試合をテレビで中継する。","romaji":"Sakkā no shiai o terebi de chūkei suru.","german":"The soccer match is broadcast on TV.","furigana":"しあい|ちゅうけい"},{"japanese":"現場から中継でお伝えします。","romaji":"Genba kara chūkei de otsutae shimasu.","german":"We'll bring you a live report from the scene.","furigana":"げんば|ちゅうけい|つた"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Damaging someone's reputation with false claims; 誹謗中傷 (slander and abuse, especially online). As a verb 中傷する.","examples":[{"japanese":"ネットで根拠のない中傷を受けた。","romaji":"Netto de konkyo no nai chūshō o uketa.","german":"I was subjected to baseless slander online.","furigana":"こんきょ|ちゅうしょう|う"},{"japanese":"他人を中傷するような発言は許されない。","romaji":"Tanin o chūshō suru yō na hatsugen wa yurusarenai.","german":"Remarks that slander others are not acceptable.","furigana":"たにん|ちゅうしょう|はつげん|ゆる"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The middle of a slope, usually of a mountain: 山の中腹 (also 丘の中腹).","examples":[{"japanese":"山の中腹に小さな寺がある。","romaji":"Yama no chūfuku ni chiisana tera ga aru.","german":"There is a small temple halfway up the mountain.","furigana":"やま|ちゅうふく|ちい|てら"},{"japanese":"中腹まで登ったところで、霧が出てきた。","romaji":"Chūfuku made nobotta tokoro de, kiri ga dete kita.","german":"Fog set in when we had climbed halfway up.","furigana":"ちゅうふく|のぼ|きり|で"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Neutrality of states or persons: 中立を保つ, 中立的な立場. 永世中立国: a permanently neutral state (e.g. Switzerland).","examples":[{"japanese":"審判は常に中立でなければならない。","romaji":"Shinpan wa tsune ni chūritsu de nakereba naranai.","german":"A referee must always be neutral.","furigana":"しんぱん|つね|ちゅうりつ"},{"japanese":"その国は戦争中、中立を保った。","romaji":"Sono kuni wa sensōchū, chūritsu o tamotta.","german":"That country remained neutral during the war.","furigana":"くに|せんそうちゅう|ちゅうりつ|たも"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"大腸 (large intestine), 小腸 (small intestine), 腸内環境 (gut environment). Homophone: 蝶 (butterfly).","examples":[{"japanese":"ヨーグルトは腸にいいと言われている。","romaji":"Yōguruto wa chō ni ii to iwarete iru.","german":"Yogurt is said to be good for the intestines.","furigana":"ちょう|い"},{"japanese":"腸の調子が悪くて、病院に行った。","romaji":"Chō no chōshi ga warukute, byōin ni itta.","german":"My bowels were bothering me, so I went to the hospital.","furigana":"ちょう|ちょうし|わる|びょういん|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"One of the five senses; cf. 視覚 (sight), 嗅覚 (smell), 味覚 (taste), 触覚 (touch).","examples":[{"japanese":"犬は人間よりも聴覚が鋭い。","romaji":"Inu wa ningen yori mo chōkaku ga surudoi.","german":"Dogs have sharper hearing than humans.","furigana":"いぬ|にんげん|ちょうかく|するど"},{"japanese":"事故の後、聴覚に障害が残った。","romaji":"Jiko no ato, chōkaku ni shōgai ga nokotta.","german":"Hearing impairment remained after the accident.","furigana":"じこ|あと|ちょうかく|しょうがい|のこ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Head of an agency (庁), e.g. 気象庁長官; also the title for US cabinet secretaries: 国務長官 (Secretary of State).","examples":[{"japanese":"気象庁の長官が記者会見を開いた。","romaji":"Kishōchō no chōkan ga kisha kaiken o hiraita.","german":"The head of the Meteorological Agency held a press conference.","furigana":"きしょうちょう|ちょうかん|きしゃかいけん|ひら"},{"japanese":"アメリカの国務長官が来日した。","romaji":"Amerika no kokumu chōkan ga rainichi shita.","german":"The US Secretary of State has arrived in Japan.","furigana":"こくむちょうかん|らいにち"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"From 聴診 (auscultation) + 器 (instrument).","examples":[{"japanese":"医者は聴診器を胸に当てた。","romaji":"Isha wa chōshinki o mune ni ateta.","german":"The doctor placed the stethoscope on the chest.","furigana":"いしゃ|ちょうしんき|むね|あ"},{"japanese":"聴診器で心臓の音を聞く。","romaji":"Chōshinki de shinzō no oto o kiku.","german":"You listen to the heartbeat with a stethoscope.","furigana":"ちょうしんき|しんぞう|おと|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mostly 重宝する (to find handy) or 重宝される (to be in great demand); also 重宝な (handy). With the reading じゅうほう: a precious treasure.","examples":[{"japanese":"百円ショップの便利グッズを重宝している。","romaji":"Hyakuen shoppu no benri guzzu o chōhō shite iru.","german":"I make good use of the handy goods from the 100-yen shop.","furigana":"ひゃくえん|べんり|ちょうほう"},{"japanese":"英語が話せる彼は、職場で重宝されている。","romaji":"Eigo ga hanaseru kare wa, shokuba de chōhō sarete iru.","german":"Because he speaks English, he is highly valued at the office.","furigana":"えいご|はな|かれ|しょくば|ちょうほう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Matter-of-fact word for cooking/preparing food, as in recipes and occupations: 調理師 (licensed cook), 調理器具 (cooking utensils). As a verb 調理する.","examples":[{"japanese":"この魚は調理する前に骨を取る必要がある。","romaji":"Kono sakana wa chōri suru mae ni hone o toru hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"You need to remove the bones from this fish before cooking it.","furigana":"さかな|ちょうり|まえ|ほね|と|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"調理にかかる時間は約二十分です。","romaji":"Chōri ni kakaru jikan wa yaku nijuppun desu.","german":"The cooking time is about twenty minutes.","furigana":"ちょうり|じかん|やくにじゅっぷん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Colloquial for \"often, every now and then\"; similar to ときどき, よく.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼はちょくちょく遊びに来る。","romaji":"Kare wa chokuchoku asobi ni kuru.","german":"He comes to visit every now and then.","furigana":"かれ|あそ|く"},{"japanese":"この店にはちょくちょく来ています。","romaji":"Kono mise ni wa chokuchoku kite imasu.","german":"I come to this shop quite often.","furigana":"みせ|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A book someone wrote themselves. Cf. 著者 (author).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼の著書は多くの言語に翻訳されている。","romaji":"Kare no chosho wa ōku no gengo ni hon'yaku sarete iru.","german":"His books have been translated into many languages.","furigana":"かれ|ちょしょ|おお|げんご|ほんやく"},{"japanese":"先生の新しい著書を読んだ。","romaji":"Sensei no atarashii chosho o yonda.","german":"I read my professor's new book.","furigana":"せんせい|あたら|ちょしょ|よ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Grasping something spontaneously without reasoning; 直感的 (intuitive). In philosophy also written 直観.","examples":[{"japanese":"直感で、この人は信用できると思った。","romaji":"Chokkan de, kono hito wa shin'yō dekiru to omotta.","german":"My intuition told me this person could be trusted.","furigana":"ちょっかん|ひと|しんよう|おも"},{"japanese":"迷ったときは、自分の直感を信じなさい。","romaji":"Mayotta toki wa, jibun no chokkan o shinjinasai.","german":"When in doubt, trust your own intuition.","furigana":"まよ|じぶん|ちょっかん|しん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"More formal than 有名: 著名な作家, 著名人 (prominent figures).","examples":[{"japanese":"著名な作家が講演に来た。","romaji":"Chomei na sakka ga kōen ni kita.","german":"A renowned writer came to give a lecture.","furigana":"ちょめい|さっか|こうえん|き"},{"japanese":"彼女は国際的に著名な科学者だ。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kokusaiteki ni chomei na kagakusha da.","german":"She is an internationally renowned scientist.","furigana":"かのじょ|こくさいてき|ちょめい|かがくしゃ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Only very briefly or in passing: ちらっと見る, ちらっと聞く. Similar: ちらりと; repeated: ちらちら.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女はちらっとこちらを見た。","romaji":"Kanojo wa chiratto kochira o mita.","german":"She glanced at me.","furigana":"かのじょ|み"},{"japanese":"その噂はちらっと耳にしたことがある。","romaji":"Sono uwasa wa chiratto mimi ni shita koto ga aru.","german":"I have caught a snippet of that rumor.","furigana":"うわさ|みみ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Fine dust; the proverb 塵も積もれば山となる (even small things add up). In daily life usually ほこり or ごみ.","examples":[{"japanese":"棚の上に塵が積もっていた。","romaji":"Tana no ue ni chiri ga tsumotte ita.","german":"Dust had piled up on the shelf.","furigana":"たな|うえ|ちり|つ"},{"japanese":"塵も積もれば山となる。","romaji":"Chiri mo tsumoreba yama to naru.","german":"Many a little makes a mickle.","furigana":"ちり|つ|やま"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Persistently pursuing guilt or responsibility: 責任を追及する. Homophones: 追求 (pursuit of happiness, profit) and 追究 (investigating the truth).","examples":[{"japanese":"野党は大臣の責任を厳しく追及した。","romaji":"Yatō wa daijin no sekinin o kibishiku tsuikyū shita.","german":"The opposition sharply pressed the minister on his responsibility.","furigana":"やとう|だいじん|せきにん|きび|ついきゅう"},{"japanese":"彼は警察の追及を逃れることはできなかった。","romaji":"Kare wa keisatsu no tsuikyū o nogareru koto wa dekinakatta.","german":"He could not escape the police investigation.","furigana":"かれ|けいさつ|ついきゅう|のが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Pursuit (of culprits, animals) and tracking (of shipments, studies): 追跡調査 (follow-up study). As a verb 追跡する.","examples":[{"japanese":"パトカーが逃げる車を追跡した。","romaji":"Patokā ga nigeru kuruma o tsuiseki shita.","german":"A patrol car pursued the fleeing vehicle.","furigana":"に|くるま|ついせき"},{"japanese":"荷物の配送状況はネットで追跡できる。","romaji":"Nimotsu no haisō jōkyō wa netto de tsuiseki dekiru.","german":"You can track the delivery status of a package online.","furigana":"にもつ|はいそうじょうきょう|ついせき"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Literary; from the verb 次ぐ. Not to be confused with ついで (序で, opportunity: ついでに).","examples":[{"japanese":"まず社長が挨拶し、次いで来賓が祝辞を述べた。","romaji":"Mazu shachō ga aisatsu shi, tsuide raihin ga shukuji o nobeta.","german":"First the company president gave a greeting, and next the guests of honor offered congratulatory remarks.","furigana":"しゃちょう|あいさつ|つ|らいひん|しゅくじ|の"},{"japanese":"東京は人口が最も多く、次いで横浜が多い。","romaji":"Tōkyō wa jinkō ga mottomo ōku, tsuide Yokohama ga ōi.","german":"Tokyo has the largest population, followed by Yokohama.","furigana":"とうきょう|じんこう|もっと|おお|つ|よこはま|おお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Expulsion or banishment of people (国外追放) and the eradication of evils (暴力追放). As a verb 追放する.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は国外に追放された。","romaji":"Kare wa kokugai ni tsuihō sareta.","german":"He was expelled from the country.","furigana":"かれ|こくがい|ついほう"},{"japanese":"町から暴力を追放しよう。","romaji":"Machi kara bōryoku o tsuihō shiyō.","german":"Let's drive violence out of our town.","furigana":"まち|ぼうりょく|ついほう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To experience something (usually unpleasant) firsthand and understand it deeply; as a verb 痛感する.","examples":[{"japanese":"試験に落ちて、勉強不足を痛感した。","romaji":"Shiken ni ochite, benkyō busoku o tsūkan shita.","german":"Failing the exam made me keenly aware that I hadn't studied enough.","furigana":"しけん|お|べんきょうぶそく|つうかん"},{"japanese":"災害を通して、備えの大切さを痛感した。","romaji":"Saigai o tōshite, sonae no taisetsusa o tsūkan shita.","german":"The disaster made me acutely aware of the importance of preparation.","furigana":"さいがい|とお|そな|たいせつ|つうかん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Deeply and painfully felt: 痛切に感じる. Similar: 痛感する.","examples":[{"japanese":"平和の大切さを痛切に感じた。","romaji":"Heiwa no taisetsusa o tsūsetsu ni kanjita.","german":"I felt deeply how precious peace is.","furigana":"へいわ|たいせつ|つうせつ|かん"},{"japanese":"被災者の痛切な訴えが胸に響いた。","romaji":"Hisaisha no tsūsetsu na uttae ga mune ni hibiita.","german":"The poignant appeals of the disaster victims struck a chord.","furigana":"ひさいしゃ|つうせつ|うった|むね|ひび"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A walking stick (杖をつく: to walk with a cane), also a magic wand. Proverb: 転ばぬ先の杖 (better safe than sorry).","examples":[{"japanese":"祖父は杖をついて散歩している。","romaji":"Sofu wa tsue o tsuite sanpo shite iru.","german":"My grandfather goes for walks with a cane.","furigana":"そふ|つえ|さんぽ"},{"japanese":"魔法使いが杖を振ると、花が咲いた。","romaji":"Mahōtsukai ga tsue o furu to, hana ga saita.","german":"When the wizard waved his staff, flowers bloomed.","furigana":"まほうつか|つえ|ふ|はな|さ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"What money or things are used for: 使い道がない (useless). Also written 使いみち.","examples":[{"japanese":"ボーナスの使い道をまだ決めていない。","romaji":"Bōnasu no tsukaimichi o mada kimete inai.","german":"I haven't yet decided what to spend my bonus on.","furigana":"つか|みち|き"},{"japanese":"この古い箱には、いろいろな使い道がある。","romaji":"Kono furui hako ni wa, iroiro na tsukaimichi ga aru.","german":"This old box has various uses.","furigana":"ふる|はこ|つか|みち"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A fleeting moment: 束の間の幸せ; 〜のも束の間 (... but only briefly).","examples":[{"japanese":"束の間の休息を楽しんだ。","romaji":"Tsukanoma no kyūsoku o tanoshinda.","german":"I enjoyed a brief rest.","furigana":"つか|ま|きゅうそく|たの"},{"japanese":"喜んだのも束の間、また問題が起きた。","romaji":"Yorokonda no mo tsukanoma, mata mondai ga okita.","german":"My joy was short-lived, as another problem came up.","furigana":"よろこ|つか|ま|もんだい|お"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Lacking originality: 月並みな表現 (cliche), 月並みな感想. The original meaning \"every month\" is now rare.","examples":[{"japanese":"月並みな言い方ですが、健康が一番大切です。","romaji":"Tsukinami na iikata desu ga, kenkō ga ichiban taisetsu desu.","german":"It may sound trite, but health is what matters most.","furigana":"つきな|い|かた|けんこう|いちばんたいせつ"},{"japanese":"彼のスピーチは月並みで、印象に残らなかった。","romaji":"Kare no supīchi wa tsukinami de, inshō ni nokoranakatta.","german":"His speech was banal and left no impression.","furigana":"かれ|つきな|いんしょう|のこ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A place where two parts are joined: in pipes, boards, rails, or fabric. Cf. 縫い目 (seam in sewing).","examples":[{"japanese":"パイプの継ぎ目から水が漏れている。","romaji":"Paipu no tsugime kara mizu ga morete iru.","german":"Water is leaking from the joint of the pipe.","furigana":"つ|め|みず|も"},{"japanese":"電車がレールの継ぎ目を通るたびに、ガタンと音がする。","romaji":"Densha ga rēru no tsugime o tōru tabi ni, gatan to oto ga suru.","german":"Every time the train passes over a rail joint, it goes clunk.","furigana":"でんしゃ|つ|め|とお|おと"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To join parts into a whole: 骨を接ぐ (to set a bone), 木を接ぐ (to graft a tree). Homophone: 継ぐ (to succeed, to continue).","examples":[{"japanese":"医者は折れた骨を接いで、ギプスで固定した。","romaji":"Isha wa oreta hone o tsuide, gipusu de kotei shita.","german":"The doctor set the broken bone and fixed it with a cast.","furigana":"いしゃ|お|ほね|つ|こてい"},{"japanese":"りんごの枝を別の木に接いで育てる。","romaji":"Ringo no eda o betsu no ki ni tsuide sodateru.","german":"You graft an apple branch onto another tree and grow it.","furigana":"えだ|べつ|き|つ|そだ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Describes a deep feeling, often gained from experience: つくづく思う / 感じる. Also \"to look intently\": つくづく眺める. Usually written in kana.","examples":[{"japanese":"一人暮らしを始めて、親のありがたさをつくづく感じた。","romaji":"Hitorigurashi o hajimete, oya no arigatasa o tsukuzuku kanjita.","german":"Since I started living alone, I have keenly felt how grateful I am to my parents.","furigana":"ひとりぐ|はじ|おや|かん"},{"japanese":"鏡に映った自分の顔をつくづくと眺めた。","romaji":"Kagami ni utsutta jibun no kao o tsukuzuku to nagameta.","german":"I gazed intently at my face reflected in the mirror.","furigana":"かがみ|うつ|じぶん|かお|なが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The way something is made or built: 丈夫な作り (sturdy construction). For buildings often written 造り: 石造りの家. Also appearance (若作り = made up to look young) and physique (体の作り). As a second element often voiced: 手作り (てづくり).","examples":[{"japanese":"このかばんは作りがしっかりしていて、長く使える。","romaji":"Kono kaban wa tsukuri ga shikkari shite ite, nagaku tsukaeru.","german":"This bag is solidly made and will last a long time.","furigana":"つく|なが|つか"},{"japanese":"古い寺の造りを見に、建築家が集まった。","romaji":"Furui tera no tsukuri o mi ni, kenchikuka ga atsumatta.","german":"Architects gathered to see the construction of the old temple.","furigana":"ふる|てら|つく|み|けんちくか|あつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To repair something damaged (靴下を繕う), to tidy one's appearance (身なりを繕う), or figuratively 体裁を繕う (to keep up appearances), その場を繕う (to smooth over the situation). Cf. 取り繕う.","examples":[{"japanese":"祖母は破れたシャツを丁寧に繕ってくれた。","romaji":"Sobo wa yabureta shatsu o teinei ni tsukurotte kureta.","german":"My grandmother carefully mended my torn shirt for me.","furigana":"そぼ|やぶ|ていねい|つくろ"},{"japanese":"彼は笑ってその場を繕ったが、内心は焦っていた。","romaji":"Kare wa waratte sono ba o tsukurotta ga, naishin wa asette ita.","german":"He smoothed over the situation with a laugh, but he was inwardly flustered.","furigana":"かれ|わら|ば|つくろ|ないしん|あせ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Used almost only in set phrases: 辻褄が合う (to be coherent), 辻褄が合わない (not to add up), 辻褄を合わせる (to smooth over contradictions). Often in kana: つじつま.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼の話は辻褄が合わない。","romaji":"Kare no hanashi wa tsujitsuma ga awanai.","german":"His story doesn't add up.","furigana":"かれ|はなし|つじつま|あ"},{"japanese":"犯人は嘘の辻褄を合わせようと必死だった。","romaji":"Hannin wa uso no tsujitsuma o awaseyō to hisshi datta.","german":"The culprit desperately tried to make his lies consistent.","furigana":"はんにん|うそ|つじつま|あ|ひっし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A hollow, cylindrical object: 竹の筒 (bamboo tube), 卒業証書の筒 (tube for a diploma). Cf. 管 (pipe, conduit).","examples":[{"japanese":"卒業証書を丸めて筒に入れた。","romaji":"Sotsugyō shōsho o marumete tsutsu ni ireta.","german":"I rolled up the graduation certificate and put it in the tube.","furigana":"そつぎょうしょうしょ|まる|つつ|い"},{"japanese":"竹の筒に水を入れて、花を生けた。","romaji":"Take no tsutsu ni mizu o irete, hana o iketa.","german":"I filled a bamboo tube with water and arranged flowers in it.","furigana":"たけ|つつ|みず|い|はな|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Colloquial intensified form of つつく: to poke lightly several times (肩を突っつく), to pick at food, or figuratively to pick on someone (欠点を突っつく). Often in kana.","examples":[{"japanese":"隣の人に肘で突っつかれて、目が覚めた。","romaji":"Tonari no hito ni hiji de tsuttsukarete, me ga sameta.","german":"The person next to me poked me with an elbow, and I woke up.","furigana":"となり|ひと|ひじ|つ|め|さ"},{"japanese":"上司は私の報告書の細かいミスばかり突っつく。","romaji":"Jōshi wa watashi no hōkokusho no komakai misu bakari tsuttsuku.","german":"My boss keeps nitpicking at the small mistakes in my reports.","furigana":"じょうし|わたし|ほうこくしょ|こま|つ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Basic meaning: to become stiff or taut (足が突っ張る, 肌が突っ張る). Figuratively: to stick to one's position or to act defiant and tough.","examples":[{"japanese":"急に走ったら、ふくらはぎが突っ張って痛い。","romaji":"Kyū ni hashittara, fukurahagi ga tsuppatte itai.","german":"I suddenly started running, and my calf cramped up and hurts.","furigana":"きゅう|はし|つ|ぱ|いた"},{"japanese":"彼は間違いを認めず、最後まで突っ張った。","romaji":"Kare wa machigai o mitomezu, saigo made tsuppatta.","german":"He refused to admit his mistake and held out to the end.","furigana":"かれ|まちが|みと|さいご|つ|ぱ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Intransitive counterpart of 勤める: to be able to fill a position. Mostly used negatively or in questions (勤まるだろうか), often as modesty about oneself.","examples":[{"japanese":"体の弱い彼に、夜勤の多い仕事が勤まるだろうか。","romaji":"Karada no yowai kare ni, yakin no ōi shigoto ga tsutomaru darō ka.","german":"I wonder whether someone as frail as he is can handle a job with lots of night shifts.","furigana":"からだ|よわ|かれ|やきん|おお|しごと|つと"},{"japanese":"朝が苦手な私には、この仕事は勤まらない。","romaji":"Asa ga nigate na watashi ni wa, kono shigoto wa tsutomaranai.","german":"Since I'm bad with mornings, I can't handle this job.","furigana":"あさ|にがて|わたし|しごと|つと"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The company or agency where one is employed; on forms often next to 住所. More formal: 勤務先.","examples":[{"japanese":"書類に住所と勤め先を書いてください。","romaji":"Shorui ni jūsho to tsutomesaki o kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write your address and place of work on the form.","furigana":"しょるい|じゅうしょ|つと|さき|か"},{"japanese":"父の勤め先は駅の近くにある。","romaji":"Chichi no tsutomesaki wa eki no chikaku ni aru.","german":"My father's workplace is near the station.","furigana":"ちち|つと|さき|えき|ちか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Literary: consciously and as best one can: 努めて冷静に振る舞う (to make an effort to stay calm). From 努める (to strive).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は努めて明るく振る舞っていた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa tsutomete akaruku furumatte ita.","german":"She made an effort to act cheerful.","furigana":"かのじょ|つと|あか|ふ|ま"},{"japanese":"会議では努めて冷静に話すようにしている。","romaji":"Kaigi de wa tsutomete reisei ni hanasu yō ni shite iru.","german":"In meetings I try to speak as calmly as possible.","furigana":"かいぎ|つと|れいせい|はな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To squeeze skin between the fingers: 頬をつねる. 夢じゃないかと頬をつねる = to pinch oneself to check whether one is dreaming. Usually in kana.","examples":[{"japanese":"夢じゃないかと思って、自分の頬をつねった。","romaji":"Yume ja nai ka to omotte, jibun no hō o tsunetta.","german":"Wondering if I was dreaming, I pinched my cheek.","furigana":"ゆめ|おも|じぶん|ほお"},{"japanese":"弟が妹の腕をつねって泣かせた。","romaji":"Otōto ga imōto no ude o tsunette nakaseta.","german":"My little brother pinched my little sister's arm and made her cry.","furigana":"おとうと|いもうと|うで|な"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Used almost only in つぶらな瞳 / つぶらな目 (big, round, adorable eyes), mostly of children and animals. Usually in kana.","examples":[{"japanese":"子犬がつぶらな瞳でこちらを見ている。","romaji":"Koinu ga tsubura na hitomi de kochira o mite iru.","german":"The puppy is looking at us with big round eyes.","furigana":"こいぬ|ひとみ|み"},{"japanese":"赤ちゃんのつぶらな目に、みんなが笑顔になった。","romaji":"Akachan no tsubura na me ni, minna ga egao ni natta.","german":"The baby's round, bright eyes made everyone smile.","furigana":"あか|め|えがお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Only with 目: 目をつぶる (to close one's eyes), figuratively \"to turn a blind eye\" (see the idiom 目をつぶる). Same meaning as 目を閉じる, but somewhat more colloquial.","examples":[{"japanese":"目をつぶって、ゆっくり深呼吸してください。","romaji":"Me o tsubutte, yukkuri shinkokyū shite kudasai.","german":"Please close your eyes and take a slow, deep breath.","furigana":"め|しんこきゅう"},{"japanese":"注射の時、怖くて思わず目をつぶった。","romaji":"Chūsha no toki, kowakute omowazu me o tsubutta.","german":"I was scared of the injection and shut my eyes without thinking.","furigana":"ちゅうしゃ|とき|こわ|おも|め"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A bulging vessel of clay or porcelain. In figurative senses usually written ツボ: 話のツボ (crux of a story), 肩のツボを押す (to press a pressure point on the shoulder). 思う壺 = exactly as the other person wanted.","examples":[{"japanese":"棚の上に古い壺が飾ってある。","romaji":"Tana no ue ni furui tsubo ga kazatte aru.","german":"An old vase is displayed on the shelf.","furigana":"たな|うえ|ふる|つぼ|かざ"},{"japanese":"ここで怒ったら、相手の思う壺だ。","romaji":"Koko de okottara, aite no omou tsubo da.","german":"If you get angry now, you'll be playing right into their hands.","furigana":"おこ|あいて|おも|つぼ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Intransitive; transitive counterpart: 強める. Typical with wind, rain, trends, and criticism: 風が強まる, 批判が強まる.","examples":[{"japanese":"夜になって風がさらに強まった。","romaji":"Yoru ni natte kaze ga sara ni tsuyomatta.","german":"As night fell, the wind grew even stronger.","furigana":"よる|かぜ|つよ"},{"japanese":"政府への批判が日に日に強まっている。","romaji":"Seifu e no hihan ga hi ni hi ni tsuyomatte iru.","german":"Criticism of the government is growing day by day.","furigana":"せいふ|ひはん|ひ|ひ|つよ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive counterpart of 強まる: 警戒を強める (to heighten vigilance), 語気を強める (to speak emphatically), 火を強める (to turn up the heat).","examples":[{"japanese":"警察は駅周辺の警戒を強めている。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa eki shūhen no keikai o tsuyomete iru.","german":"The police have stepped up vigilance around the station.","furigana":"けいさつ|えきしゅうへん|けいかい|つよ"},{"japanese":"最後に火を強めて、水分を飛ばします。","romaji":"Saigo ni hi o tsuyomete, suibun o tobashimasu.","german":"At the end, turn up the heat and let the liquid evaporate.","furigana":"さいご|ひ|つよ|すいぶん|と"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Many things standing in a row: 山々が連なる (a mountain range stretches), 車が連なる. Figuratively: to be connected with something (〜に連なる). Transitive: 連ねる.","examples":[{"japanese":"遠くに雪をかぶった山々が連なっている。","romaji":"Tōku ni yuki o kabutta yamayama ga tsuranatte iru.","german":"Snow-capped mountains stretch away in the distance.","furigana":"とお|ゆき|やまやま|つら"},{"japanese":"彼は古い武士の家系に連なる人物だ。","romaji":"Kare wa furui bushi no kakei ni tsuranaru jinbutsu da.","german":"He is a person who belongs to an old samurai family line.","furigana":"かれ|ふる|ぶし|かけい|つら|じんぶつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To arrange things in a row: 軒を連ねる (to stand eave to eave), 名を連ねる (to be on a list, to take part), 言葉を連ねる (to string words together). Intransitive: 連なる.","examples":[{"japanese":"駅前には飲食店が軒を連ねている。","romaji":"Ekimae ni wa inshokuten ga noki o tsuranete iru.","german":"Restaurants stand side by side in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|いんしょくてん|のき|つら"},{"japanese":"その本の著者には、有名な学者たちが名を連ねている。","romaji":"Sono hon no chosha ni wa, yūmei na gakushatachi ga na o tsuranete iru.","german":"Famous scholars are among the authors of the book.","furigana":"ほん|ちょしゃ|ゆうめい|がくしゃ|な|つら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The large hanging bronze bell of a Buddhist temple, struck with a wooden beam; on New Year's Eve it is rung 108 times (除夜の鐘).","examples":[{"japanese":"お寺の釣り鐘が夕方の六時に鳴った。","romaji":"Otera no tsurigane ga yūgata no rokuji ni natta.","german":"The temple bell rang at six in the evening.","furigana":"てら|つ|がね|ゆうがた|ろくじ|な"},{"japanese":"この釣り鐘は三百年前に作られたそうだ。","romaji":"Kono tsurigane wa sanbyakunen mae ni tsukurareta sō da.","german":"This temple bell is said to have been made three hundred years ago.","furigana":"つ|がね|さんびゃくねんまえ|つく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The hanging strap in trains and buses: つり革につかまる (to hold on to the strap).","examples":[{"japanese":"揺れますので、つり革におつかまりください。","romaji":"Yuremasu node, tsurikawa ni otsukamari kudasai.","german":"The ride may be bumpy, so please hold on to the straps.","furigana":"ゆ|かわ"},{"japanese":"背が低くて、つり革に手が届かない。","romaji":"Se ga hikukute, tsurikawa ni te ga todokanai.","german":"I'm short and can't reach the strap.","furigana":"せ|ひく|かわ|て|とど"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"In daily life usually shortened to ティッシュ: ティッシュを一枚ください. The spelling ティシュペーパー is a rarer variant.","examples":[{"japanese":"テーブルの上にティッシュペーパーの箱を置いた。","romaji":"Tēburu no ue ni tisshupēpā no hako o oita.","german":"I put a box of tissues on the table.","furigana":"うえ|はこ|お"},{"japanese":"花粉症の季節は、ティッシュペーパーがすぐなくなる。","romaji":"Kafunshō no kisetsu wa, tisshupēpā ga sugu nakunaru.","german":"During hay fever season, tissues run out quickly.","furigana":"かふんしょう|きせつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A fixed meal with rice, miso soup and side dishes, typical of cafeterias and simple restaurants: 焼き魚定食, 日替わり定食 (set meal of the day).","examples":[{"japanese":"昼は駅前の食堂で焼き魚定食を食べた。","romaji":"Hiru wa ekimae no shokudō de yakizakana teishoku o tabeta.","german":"For lunch I had a grilled fish set meal at the eatery in front of the station.","furigana":"ひる|えきまえ|しょくどう|や|ざかなていしょく|た"},{"japanese":"この店の定食はご飯のおかわりが無料だ。","romaji":"Kono mise no teishoku wa gohan no okawari ga muryō da.","german":"At this restaurant, refills of rice are free with the set meal.","furigana":"みせ|ていしょく|はん|むりょう"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 13"},
{"notes":"The time when one could still have acted has passed: もう手遅れだ; 今からでも手遅れではない (it's not too late even now). Often about illness.","examples":[{"japanese":"病院に運ばれた時には、もう手遅れだった。","romaji":"Byōin ni hakobareta toki ni wa, mō teokure datta.","german":"By the time he was taken to the hospital, it was already too late.","furigana":"びょういん|はこ|とき|ておく"},{"japanese":"勉強を始めるのに手遅れということはない。","romaji":"Benkyō o hajimeru no ni teokure to iu koto wa nai.","german":"It's never too late to start studying.","furigana":"べんきょう|はじ|ておく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Colloquial for 大きい, often emphatic: でかい家, 声がでかい (to be loud). Avoid in formal speech.","examples":[{"japanese":"駅前にでかいビルが建った。","romaji":"Ekimae ni dekai biru ga tatta.","german":"A huge building has gone up in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|た"},{"japanese":"お前、声がでかいよ。みんな見てるぞ。","romaji":"Omae, koe ga dekai yo. Minna miteru zo.","german":"Hey, your voice is too loud. Everyone is looking.","furigana":"まえ|こえ|み"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To take on a job personally or be significantly involved in it: 彼が手掛けた作品 (a work he created). Also written 手がける.","examples":[{"japanese":"この建物は有名な建築家が手掛けたものだ。","romaji":"Kono tatemono wa yūmei na kenchikuka ga tegaketa mono da.","german":"This building was designed by a famous architect.","furigana":"たてもの|ゆうめい|けんちくか|てが"},{"japanese":"彼女はこれまで多くの映画の音楽を手掛けてきた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kore made ōku no eiga no ongaku o tegakete kita.","german":"She has composed the music for many films so far.","furigana":"かのじょ|おお|えいが|おんがく|てが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Natural fitness for a task or occupation: 適性検査 (aptitude test), 適性がある. Not to be confused with 適正 (appropriate), which is pronounced the same.","examples":[{"japanese":"入社試験では、筆記試験のほかに適性検査もある。","romaji":"Nyūsha shiken de wa, hikki shiken no hoka ni tekisei kensa mo aru.","german":"The entrance exam includes an aptitude test in addition to the written exam.","furigana":"にゅうしゃしけん|ひっきしけん|てきせいけんさ"},{"japanese":"彼には教師としての適性があると思う。","romaji":"Kare ni wa kyōshi to shite no tekisei ga aru to omou.","german":"I think he has an aptitude for teaching.","furigana":"かれ|きょうし|てきせい|おも"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Everyday word for a swelling or pimple on the skin: 顔に出来物ができる. Often in kana: できもの.","examples":[{"japanese":"顔に出来物ができて、人に会いたくない。","romaji":"Kao ni dekimono ga dekite, hito ni aitakunai.","german":"I have a pimple on my face and don't want to see anyone.","furigana":"かお|できもの|ひと|あ"},{"japanese":"背中の出来物がなかなか治らないので、皮膚科に行った。","romaji":"Senaka no dekimono ga nakanaka naoranai node, hifuka ni itta.","german":"The lump on my back wouldn't heal, so I went to a dermatologist.","furigana":"せなか|できもの|なお|ひふか|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To meet someone or something unexpectedly, often something unpleasant: 事故に出くわす, 熊に出くわす. More colloquial than 出会う.","examples":[{"japanese":"駅で昔の恋人にばったり出くわした。","romaji":"Eki de mukashi no koibito ni battari dekuwashita.","german":"I bumped into an old girlfriend at the station.","furigana":"えき|むかし|こいびと|で"},{"japanese":"山道で熊に出くわしたら、走って逃げてはいけない。","romaji":"Yamamichi de kuma ni dekuwashitara, hashitte nigete wa ikenai.","german":"If you come across a bear on a mountain trail, don't run away.","furigana":"やまみち|くま|で|はし|に"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Decoration, especially of cakes and rooms: デコレーションケーキ (decorated cake). More general: 飾り, 装飾.","examples":[{"japanese":"娘の誕生日にケーキのデコレーションを手伝った。","romaji":"Musume no tanjōbi ni kēki no dekorēshon o tetsudatta.","german":"I helped decorate the cake for my daughter's birthday.","furigana":"むすめ|たんじょうび|てつだ"},{"japanese":"クリスマスのデコレーションで、店内が華やかになった。","romaji":"Kurisumasu no dekorēshon de, tennai ga hanayaka ni natta.","german":"The Christmas decorations made the shop look festive.","furigana":"てんない|はな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Design of products, clothing or graphics; as a verb デザインする. デザイナー = designer.","examples":[{"japanese":"このスマホはデザインがシンプルで気に入っている。","romaji":"Kono sumaho wa dezain ga shinpuru de ki ni itte iru.","german":"I like the simple design of this smartphone.","furigana":"き|い"},{"japanese":"彼女は自分で服をデザインして作っている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa jibun de fuku o dezain shite tsukutte iru.","german":"She designs and makes her own clothes.","furigana":"かのじょ|じぶん|ふく|つく"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo I, lesson 21"},
{"notes":"Within easy reach: 手近な所に置く, 手近にある物. Figuratively: familiar, everyday (手近な例). Cf. 身近 (close, familiar).","examples":[{"japanese":"辞書はいつも手近な所に置いている。","romaji":"Jisho wa itsumo tejika na tokoro ni oite iru.","german":"I always keep my dictionary within reach.","furigana":"じしょ|てぢか|ところ|お"},{"japanese":"手近な例で説明しましょう。","romaji":"Tejika na rei de setsumei shimashō.","german":"Let me explain with a familiar example.","furigana":"てぢか|れい|せつめい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Umbrella term for iron and steel products, mainly in economic texts: 鉄鋼業 (steel industry), 鉄鋼メーカー (steelmaker). Homophone: 鉄鉱 (iron ore).","examples":[{"japanese":"鉄鋼の輸出量が前の年より減少した。","romaji":"Tekkō no yushutsuryō ga mae no toshi yori genshō shita.","german":"The export volume of iron and steel decreased from the previous year.","furigana":"てっこう|ゆしゅつりょう|まえ|とし|げんしょう"},{"japanese":"この町は昔から鉄鋼業で栄えてきた。","romaji":"Kono machi wa mukashi kara tekkōgyō de sakaete kita.","german":"This town has prospered on the steel industry since long ago.","furigana":"まち|むかし|てっこうぎょう|さか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"From French dessin: a preliminary drawing, usually in pencil or charcoal, also as an exercise in art class.","examples":[{"japanese":"美術の授業で、りんごのデッサンをした。","romaji":"Bijutsu no jugyō de, ringo no dessan o shita.","german":"In art class, we drew a sketch of an apple.","furigana":"びじゅつ|じゅぎょう"},{"japanese":"画家は本番の前に何枚もデッサンを描く。","romaji":"Gaka wa honban no mae ni nanmai mo dessan o kaku.","german":"A painter makes many sketches before the actual painting.","furigana":"がか|ほんばん|まえ|なんまい|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The highest point: 山のてっぺん, 頭のてっぺんから足の先まで (from head to toe). More colloquial than 頂上. Usually in kana.","examples":[{"japanese":"やっと山のてっぺんに着いた。","romaji":"Yatto yama no teppen ni tsuita.","german":"We finally reached the top of the mountain.","furigana":"やま|つ"},{"japanese":"彼は頭のてっぺんから足の先までびしょぬれだった。","romaji":"Kare wa atama no teppen kara ashi no saki made bishonure datta.","german":"He was soaked from head to toe.","furigana":"かれ|あたま|あし|さき"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"In school, the horizontal bar: 鉄棒で逆上がりをする (to do a backward swing-up on the bar). Literal meaning: iron bar.","examples":[{"japanese":"子どもたちが校庭の鉄棒で遊んでいる。","romaji":"Kodomotachi ga kōtei no tetsubō de asonde iru.","german":"Children are playing on the horizontal bar in the schoolyard.","furigana":"こ|こうてい|てつぼう|あそ"},{"japanese":"練習して、やっと鉄棒で逆上がりができるようになった。","romaji":"Renshū shite, yatto tetsubō de sakaagari ga dekiru yō ni natta.","german":"After practicing, I can finally do a backward swing-up on the horizontal bar.","furigana":"れんしゅう|てつぼう|さかあ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To start over from the beginning (一からの出直し) or to come by again later. Verb: 出直す.","examples":[{"japanese":"会社を辞めて、一から出直しだ。","romaji":"Kaisha o yamete, ichi kara denaoshi da.","german":"I quit my job, so I'm starting from scratch.","furigana":"かいしゃ|や|いち|でなお"},{"japanese":"担当者が不在なら、出直しになりますね。","romaji":"Tantōsha ga fuzai nara, denaoshi ni narimasu ne.","german":"If the person in charge is absent, I'll have to come back another time.","furigana":"たんとうしゃ|ふざい|でなお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Arrangements made in advance for how something will proceed: 手筈を整える (to make all preparations), 〜する手筈になっている (it is arranged that ...). Often written 手はず.","examples":[{"japanese":"空港には迎えの車が来る手筈になっている。","romaji":"Kūkō ni wa mukae no kuruma ga kuru tehazu ni natte iru.","german":"It's arranged that a car will meet us at the airport.","furigana":"くうこう|むか|くるま|く|てはず"},{"japanese":"会議の手筈はすべて整えておきました。","romaji":"Kaigi no tehazu wa subete totonoete okimashita.","german":"I have made all the arrangements for the meeting.","furigana":"かいぎ|てはず|ととの"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Guiding or leading someone, also in crimes: 内部の者の手引き (help from an insider). As a publication: guide, handbook (利用の手引き).","examples":[{"japanese":"詳しい使い方は、この手引きを読んでください。","romaji":"Kuwashii tsukaikata wa, kono tebiki o yonde kudasai.","german":"For detailed instructions, please read this guide.","furigana":"くわ|つか|かた|てび|よ"},{"japanese":"犯人は内部の者の手引きで建物に侵入したらしい。","romaji":"Hannin wa naibu no mono no tebiki de tatemono ni shinnyū shita rashii.","german":"The culprit apparently got into the building with the help of an insider.","furigana":"はんにん|ないぶ|もの|てび|たてもの|しんにゅう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"1) Turned by hand: 手回しのオルゴール (hand-cranked music box). 2) Preparations in advance: 手回しがいい (to be well prepared).","examples":[{"japanese":"災害に備えて、手回しで充電できるラジオを買った。","romaji":"Saigai ni sonaete, temawashi de jūden dekiru rajio o katta.","german":"I bought a radio that can be charged by hand crank in case of disasters.","furigana":"さいがい|そな|てまわ|じゅうでん|か"},{"japanese":"もう予約まで済ませたの？手回しがいいね。","romaji":"Mō yoyaku made sumaseta no? Temawashi ga ii ne.","german":"You've even made the reservation already? You're well prepared.","furigana":"よやく|す|てまわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"In both senses usually shortened to デモ in daily life; the long form is more formal.","examples":[{"japanese":"新製品のデモンストレーションに多くの客が集まった。","romaji":"Shinseihin no demonsutorēshon ni ōku no kyaku ga atsumatta.","german":"Many customers gathered for the demonstration of the new product.","furigana":"しんせいひん|おお|きゃく|あつ"},{"japanese":"市民が原発に反対するデモンストレーションを行った。","romaji":"Shimin ga genpatsu ni hantai suru demonsutorēshon o okonatta.","german":"Citizens held a demonstration against nuclear power.","furigana":"しみん|げんぱつ|はんたい|おこな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Sunlight or heat is thrown back from a surface: 雪が日光を照り返す. Noun: 照り返し (reflected glare).","examples":[{"japanese":"雪が日光を照り返して、まぶしい。","romaji":"Yuki ga nikkō o terikaeshite, mabushii.","german":"The snow reflects the sunlight and it is dazzling.","furigana":"ゆき|にっこう|て|かえ"},{"japanese":"夏のアスファルトが熱を照り返して、歩くだけで汗が出る。","romaji":"Natsu no asufaruto ga netsu o terikaeshite, aruku dake de ase ga deru.","german":"In summer the asphalt radiates heat back, so you sweat just from walking.","furigana":"なつ|ねつ|て|かえ|ある|あせ|で"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mostly 手分けして〜する (to split up and do ...): 手分けして探す.","examples":[{"japanese":"みんなで手分けして、なくした鍵を探した。","romaji":"Minna de tewake shite, nakushita kagi o sagashita.","german":"Everyone split up and searched for the lost key.","furigana":"てわ|かぎ|さが"},{"japanese":"作業の手分けを決めてから始めよう。","romaji":"Sagyō no tewake o kimete kara hajimeyō.","german":"Let's decide how to divide up the work before we begin.","furigana":"さぎょう|てわ|き|はじ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Heaven as the counterpart of earth (天と地) and as a higher power: 天の恵み (heaven's blessing), 運を天に任せる (to leave it to fate). For the visible sky in daily life, usually 空.","examples":[{"japanese":"運を天に任せて、結果を待つしかない。","romaji":"Un o ten ni makasete, kekka o matsu shika nai.","german":"All we can do is leave it to fate and wait for the result.","furigana":"うん|てん|まか|けっか|ま"},{"japanese":"高い塔が天に向かってそびえている。","romaji":"Takai tō ga ten ni mukatte sobiete iru.","german":"A tall tower rises toward the sky.","furigana":"たか|とう|てん|む"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Elevated word for rural scenery: 田園風景 (pastoral scenery), 田園都市 (garden city). Everyday: 田舎.","examples":[{"japanese":"窓の外には美しい田園が広がっていた。","romaji":"Mado no soto ni wa utsukushii den'en ga hirogatte ita.","german":"A beautiful countryside stretched out outside the window.","furigana":"まど|そと|うつく|でんえん|ひろ"},{"japanese":"定年後は田園での静かな生活に憧れている。","romaji":"Teinengo wa den'en de no shizuka na seikatsu ni akogarete iru.","german":"I long for a quiet life in the countryside after retirement.","furigana":"ていねんご|でんえん|しず|せいかつ|あこが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To light a fire or ignite an engine: 聖火の点火 (lighting of the Olympic flame), エンジンに点火する. As a verb 点火する.","examples":[{"japanese":"開会式で聖火に点火された。","romaji":"Kaikaishiki de seika ni tenka sareta.","german":"The Olympic flame was lit at the opening ceremony.","furigana":"かいかいしき|せいか|てんか"},{"japanese":"ロケットのエンジンに点火し、打ち上げが始まった。","romaji":"Roketto no enjin ni tenka shi, uchiage ga hajimatta.","german":"The rocket's engines were ignited and the launch began.","furigana":"てんか|う|あ|はじ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Turning or changing direction, especially of vehicles: 転回禁止 (no U-turn; the official term for Uターン禁止). For a change of course in politics etc., usually 転換. Homophone: 展開 (development).","examples":[{"japanese":"この交差点では転回が禁止されている。","romaji":"Kono kōsaten de wa tenkai ga kinshi sarete iru.","german":"U-turns are prohibited at this intersection.","furigana":"こうさてん|てんかい|きんし"},{"japanese":"船はゆっくり転回して、港へ戻り始めた。","romaji":"Fune wa yukkuri tenkai shite, minato e modori hajimeta.","german":"The ship slowly turned around and began heading back to port.","furigana":"ふね|てんかい|みなと|もど|はじ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Formal and official word for 引っ越し: 転居届 (change-of-address notification), 転居先 (new address). As a verb 転居する.","examples":[{"japanese":"転居したら、二週間以内に届け出が必要です。","romaji":"Tenkyo shitara, nishūkan inai ni todokede ga hitsuyō desu.","german":"After moving, you must file a notification within two weeks.","furigana":"てんきょ|にしゅうかんいない|とど|で|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"郵便局に転居先の住所を知らせた。","romaji":"Yūbinkyoku ni tenkyosaki no jūsho o shiraseta.","german":"I informed the post office of my new address.","furigana":"ゆうびんきょく|てんきょさき|じゅうしょ|し"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To change schools, e.g. after a move; 転校生 = a new classmate transferred from another school. As a verb 転校する. Homophone: 天候 (weather).","examples":[{"japanese":"父の仕事の都合で、三回も転校した。","romaji":"Chichi no shigoto no tsugō de, sankai mo tenkō shita.","german":"Because of my father's job, I changed schools three times.","furigana":"ちち|しごと|つごう|さんかい|てんこう"},{"japanese":"来月、クラスに転校生が来るそうだ。","romaji":"Raigetsu, kurasu ni tenkōsei ga kuru sō da.","german":"I hear a transfer student is joining our class next month.","furigana":"らいげつ|てんこうせい|く"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A message that one asks someone to pass on: 伝言をお願いできますか (Could you pass on a message?). 伝言を残す = to leave a message.","examples":[{"japanese":"田中さんに伝言をお願いできますか。","romaji":"Tanaka-san ni dengon o onegai dekimasu ka.","german":"Could I leave a message for Mr. Tanaka?","furigana":"たなか|でんごん|ねが"},{"japanese":"母からの伝言をメモに書いて、机に置いた。","romaji":"Haha kara no dengon o memo ni kaite, tsukue ni oita.","german":"I wrote down my mother's message on a note and left it on the desk.","furigana":"はは|でんごん|か|つくえ|お"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A calamity caused by nature (earthquake, typhoon, flood); antonym 人災 (man-made disaster). Homophone: 天才 (genius).","examples":[{"japanese":"地震や台風などの天災は、いつ起こるかわからない。","romaji":"Jishin ya taifū nado no tensai wa, itsu okoru ka wakaranai.","german":"You never know when natural disasters like earthquakes or typhoons will strike.","furigana":"じしん|たいふう|てんさい|お"},{"japanese":"今回の被害は天災ではなく人災だ。","romaji":"Konkai no higai wa tensai de wa naku jinsai da.","german":"This damage is not a natural disaster but a man-made one.","furigana":"こんかい|ひがい|てんさい|じんさい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Displaying objects: 展示品 (exhibit), 展示会 (exhibition, trade show). As a verb 展示する. Homophone: 点字 (braille).","examples":[{"japanese":"博物館で恐竜の化石が展示されている。","romaji":"Hakubutsukan de kyōryū no kaseki ga tenji sarete iru.","german":"Dinosaur fossils are on display at the museum.","furigana":"はくぶつかん|きょうりゅう|かせき|てんじ"},{"japanese":"展示品には手を触れないでください。","romaji":"Tenjihin ni wa te o furenaide kudasai.","german":"Please do not touch the exhibits.","furigana":"てんじひん|て|ふ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Literary; intransitive (株価が上昇に転じる = stock prices turn upward) and transitive (話題を転じる = to change the subject). Proverb: 災い転じて福となす (to turn misfortune into good fortune). Same meaning: 転ずる.","examples":[{"japanese":"午後になって、株価は上昇に転じた。","romaji":"Gogo ni natte, kabuka wa jōshō ni tenjita.","german":"In the afternoon, stock prices turned upward.","furigana":"ごご|かぶか|じょうしょう|てん"},{"japanese":"彼は話題を仕事のことに転じた。","romaji":"Kare wa wadai o shigoto no koto ni tenjita.","german":"He steered the conversation to work.","furigana":"かれ|わだい|しごと|てん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The literary ずる-form of 転じる with the same meaning; conjugated like other ずる verbs (passive 転ぜられる, also 転じられる).","examples":[{"japanese":"景気は来年から回復に転ずると予想されている。","romaji":"Keiki wa rainen kara kaifuku ni tenzuru to yosō sarete iru.","german":"The economy is expected to turn toward recovery next year.","furigana":"けいき|らいねん|かいふく|てん|よそう"},{"japanese":"攻撃から守りに転ずる時機を見極める必要がある。","romaji":"Kōgeki kara mamori ni tenzuru jiki o mikiwameru hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"You need to judge the right moment to switch from offense to defense.","furigana":"こうげき|まも|てん|じき|みきわ|ひつよう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A line made of dots or short dashes: 点線に沿って切る (to cut along the dotted line). Cf. 実線 (solid line).","examples":[{"japanese":"点線に沿って、はさみで切ってください。","romaji":"Tensen ni sotte, hasami de kitte kudasai.","german":"Please cut along the dotted line with scissors.","furigana":"てんせん|そ|き"},{"japanese":"地図の点線は県境を表している。","romaji":"Chizu no tensen wa kenzakai o arawashite iru.","german":"The dotted line on the map shows the prefectural border.","furigana":"ちず|てんせん|けんざかい|あらわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Especially the overhead lines on poles along streets: 電線にカラスがとまっている. Homophone: 伝染 (contagion).","examples":[{"japanese":"電線にカラスが何羽もとまっている。","romaji":"Densen ni karasu ga nanba mo tomatte iru.","german":"Several crows are perched on the power line.","furigana":"でんせん|なんば"},{"japanese":"台風で電線が切れて、町全体が停電した。","romaji":"Taifū de densen ga kirete, machi zentai ga teiden shita.","german":"The typhoon snapped the power lines, and the whole town lost power.","furigana":"たいふう|でんせん|き|まちぜんたい|ていでん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Stars, planets, moons, etc.: 天体観測 (astronomical observation), 天体望遠鏡 (astronomical telescope).","examples":[{"japanese":"夜空には無数の天体が輝いている。","romaji":"Yozora ni wa musū no tentai ga kagayaite iru.","german":"Countless celestial bodies shine in the night sky.","furigana":"よぞら|むすう|てんたい|かがや"},{"japanese":"息子は天体望遠鏡で土星を見て感動した。","romaji":"Musuko wa tentai bōenkyō de dosei o mite kandō shita.","german":"My son was moved when he saw Saturn through the telescope.","furigana":"むすこ|てんたいぼうえんきょう|どせい|み|かんどう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Elevated: the universe (天地創造 = the Creation) or the world in which one lives and works: 新天地 (new world, fresh start). On parcels: 天地無用 (this side up, do not turn over).","examples":[{"japanese":"天地がひっくり返るほどの大事件だった。","romaji":"Tenchi ga hikkurikaeru hodo no daijiken datta.","german":"It was a huge incident that turned the world upside down.","furigana":"てんち|かえ|だいじけん"},{"japanese":"彼は新しい天地を求めて海外に渡った。","romaji":"Kare wa atarashii tenchi o motomete kaigai ni watatta.","german":"He went abroad in search of a new world.","furigana":"かれ|あたら|てんち|もと|かいがい|わた"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Colloquial, mostly with negation or a negative expression: てんで話にならない (completely out of the question), てんでだめ. More formal: 全然, まったく.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は私の話をてんで聞いていない。","romaji":"Kare wa watashi no hanashi o tende kiite inai.","german":"He isn't listening to me at all.","furigana":"かれ|わたし|はなし|き"},{"japanese":"数学はてんでだめだ。","romaji":"Sūgaku wa tende dame da.","german":"I'm hopeless at math.","furigana":"すうがく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A move to a different post, especially for teachers and civil servants. Cf. 転勤 (transfer to another location, mainly in companies).","examples":[{"japanese":"担任の先生が春に別の学校へ転任した。","romaji":"Tannin no sensei ga haru ni betsu no gakkō e tennin shita.","german":"Our homeroom teacher was transferred to another school in spring.","furigana":"たんにん|せんせい|はる|べつ|がっこう|てんにん"},{"japanese":"転任の挨拶をするために、校長室を訪れた。","romaji":"Tennin no aisatsu o suru tame ni, kōchōshitsu o otozureta.","german":"I visited the principal's office to give my farewell greeting on my transfer.","furigana":"てんにん|あいさつ|こうちょうしつ|おとず"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"1) Introduction from abroad: 仏教の伝来 (the introduction of Buddhism). 2) Handing down through generations: 先祖伝来の刀 (a sword handed down from ancestors).","examples":[{"japanese":"鉄砲の伝来は日本の戦い方を大きく変えた。","romaji":"Teppō no denrai wa Nihon no tatakaikata o ōkiku kaeta.","german":"The introduction of firearms greatly changed the way war was fought in Japan.","furigana":"てっぽう|でんらい|にほん|たたか|かた|おお|か"},{"japanese":"この家には先祖伝来の刀がある。","romaji":"Kono ie ni wa senzo denrai no katana ga aru.","german":"This house has a sword handed down from the ancestors.","furigana":"いえ|せんぞでんらい|かたな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Falling down (ホームから転落する) or, figuratively, a drop in rank or circumstances: 最下位に転落する (to fall to last place). As a verb 転落する.","examples":[{"japanese":"ホームからの転落を防ぐため、柵が設置された。","romaji":"Hōmu kara no tenraku o fusegu tame, saku ga secchi sareta.","german":"A barrier was installed to prevent falls from the platform.","furigana":"てんらく|ふせ|さく|せっち"},{"japanese":"チームは連敗して最下位に転落した。","romaji":"Chīmu wa renpai shite saikai ni tenraku shita.","german":"After a string of defeats, the team dropped to last place.","furigana":"れんぱい|さいかい|てんらく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mainly a counter for buildings: マンション三棟 (three apartment buildings). As a noun, e.g. 隣の棟 (the next building) or A棟 (Building A); in compounds: 病棟 (hospital ward), 研究棟 (research building). The reading むね means ridge of a roof.","examples":[{"japanese":"駅前に新しいマンションが三棟建った。","romaji":"Ekimae ni atarashii manshon ga santō tatta.","german":"Three new apartment buildings went up in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|あたら|さんとうた"},{"japanese":"友達は同じ団地の隣の棟に住んでいる。","romaji":"Tomodachi wa onaji danchi no tonari no tō ni sunde iru.","german":"My friend lives in the next building of the same housing complex.","furigana":"ともだち|おな|だんち|となり|とう|す"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The body without head and limbs: 胴が長い. 胴回り = waist or girth measurement. In kendo, also the chest protector.","examples":[{"japanese":"この犬は胴が長くて足が短い。","romaji":"Kono inu wa dō ga nagakute ashi ga mijikai.","german":"This dog has a long body and short legs.","furigana":"いぬ|どう|なが|あし|みじか"},{"japanese":"最近、胴回りが太くなってきた。","romaji":"Saikin, dōmawari ga futoku natte kita.","german":"Lately my waistline has been getting bigger.","furigana":"さいきん|どうまわ|ふと"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Gathering people or resources for a purpose: 警官を動員する; 観客動員数 (attendance figures). Historically also military mobilization.","examples":[{"japanese":"捜索のために警察官が百人以上動員された。","romaji":"Sōsaku no tame ni keisatsukan ga hyakunin ijō dōin sareta.","german":"More than a hundred police officers were mobilized for the search.","furigana":"そうさく|けいさつかん|ひゃくにんいじょうどういん"},{"japanese":"その映画は公開から一か月で多くの観客を動員した。","romaji":"Sono eiga wa kōkai kara ikkagetsu de ōku no kankyaku o dōin shita.","german":"The film drew a large audience in the month after its release.","furigana":"えいが|こうかい|いっ|げつ|おお|かんきゃく|どういん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A formal discussion aimed at reaching a result: 討議を重ねる. Cf. 議論 (discussion in general), 討論 (debate with pro and con).","examples":[{"japanese":"委員会で予算案について討議した。","romaji":"Iinkai de yosan'an ni tsuite tōgi shita.","german":"The committee deliberated on the draft budget.","furigana":"いいんかい|よさんあん|とうぎ"},{"japanese":"長い討議の末、ようやく結論が出た。","romaji":"Nagai tōgi no sue, yōyaku ketsuron ga deta.","german":"After a long deliberation, a conclusion was finally reached.","furigana":"なが|とうぎ|すえ|けつろん|で"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Classification by quality or rank: 等級をつける (to grade). In astronomy, also the magnitude of a star.","examples":[{"japanese":"果物は大きさや色によって等級が分けられる。","romaji":"Kudamono wa ōkisa ya iro ni yotte tōkyū ga wakerareru.","german":"Fruit is divided into grades according to size and color.","furigana":"くだもの|おお|いろ|とうきゅう|わ"},{"japanese":"この牛肉は最高の等級を与えられた。","romaji":"Kono gyūniku wa saikō no tōkyū o ataerareta.","german":"This beef was given the highest grade.","furigana":"ぎゅうにく|さいこう|とうきゅう|あた"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mostly in 同級生 (classmate, schoolmate of the same year). On its own, e.g.: 彼とは同級だった (we were in the same class).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼とは小学校で同級だった。","romaji":"Kare to wa shōgakkō de dōkyū datta.","german":"He and I were in the same class in elementary school.","furigana":"かれ|しょうがっこう|どうきゅう"},{"japanese":"同級生の結婚式に招待された。","romaji":"Dōkyūsei no kekkonshiki ni shōtai sareta.","german":"I was invited to a former classmate's wedding.","furigana":"どうきゅうせい|けっこんしき|しょうたい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To go to school as a pupil: 登校時間 (time to go to school), 集団登校 (walking to school in a group), 不登校 (school refusal). Opposite: 下校 (leaving school). Homophone: 投稿 (post, submission).","examples":[{"japanese":"子どもたちは毎朝八時に登校する。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa maiasa hachiji ni tōkō suru.","german":"The children go to school at eight every morning.","furigana":"こ|まいあさはちじ|とうこう"},{"japanese":"大雪のため、今日は登校しなくていいことになった。","romaji":"Ōyuki no tame, kyō wa tōkō shinakute ii koto ni natta.","german":"Because of the heavy snow, it was decided that we don't have to go to school today.","furigana":"おおゆき|きょう|とうこう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mostly a suffix: 友達同士 (among friends), 男同士 (among men), 似た者同士 (two of a kind). Homophones: 同志 (comrade), 動詞 (verb).","examples":[{"japanese":"隣同士なので、よく一緒に食事をする。","romaji":"Tonari dōshi na node, yoku issho ni shokuji o suru.","german":"Since we're neighbors, we often eat together.","furigana":"となりどうし|いっしょ|しょくじ"},{"japanese":"好きな者同士でグループを作ってください。","romaji":"Suki na mono dōshi de gurūpu o tsukutte kudasai.","german":"Please form groups with people you like.","furigana":"す|ものどうし|つく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"People with the same goals or convictions: 同志を募る (to recruit comrades). In political movements, \"comrade\". Homophone: 同士 (among each other).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼らは同じ夢を持つ同志だ。","romaji":"Karera wa onaji yume o motsu dōshi da.","german":"They are like-minded people who share the same dream.","furigana":"かれ|おな|ゆめ|も|どうし"},{"japanese":"環境を守る活動に参加する同志を募っている。","romaji":"Kankyō o mamoru katsudō ni sanka suru dōshi o tsunotte iru.","german":"We are recruiting people to join our environmental protection activities.","furigana":"かんきょう|まも|かつどう|さんか|どうし|つの"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"State or organizational control: 言論統制 (restriction of free speech), 統制が取れている (to be well organized, disciplined). As a verb 統制する.","examples":[{"japanese":"戦時中は物価が国によって統制されていた。","romaji":"Senjichū wa bukka ga kuni ni yotte tōsei sarete ita.","german":"During the war, prices were controlled by the state.","furigana":"せんじちゅう|ぶっか|くに|とうせい"},{"japanese":"このチームはよく統制が取れている。","romaji":"Kono chīmu wa yoku tōsei ga torete iru.","german":"This team is well organized.","furigana":"とうせい|と"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To be elected (選挙で当選する) or drawn in a lottery (宝くじに当選する). Opposite: 落選.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は三度目の選挙でようやく当選した。","romaji":"Kare wa sandome no senkyo de yōyaku tōsen shita.","german":"He was finally elected on his third attempt.","furigana":"かれ|さんどめ|せんきょ|とうせん"},{"japanese":"懸賞に当選して、旅行券をもらった。","romaji":"Kenshō ni tōsen shite, ryokōken o moratta.","german":"I won a sweepstakes and got a travel voucher.","furigana":"けんしょう|とうせん|りょこうけん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Escape, especially of criminals: 逃走中の犯人 (fugitive), 逃走経路 (escape route). As a verb 逃走する; more everyday: 逃げる.","examples":[{"japanese":"犯人は車で逃走した。","romaji":"Hannin wa kuruma de tōsō shita.","german":"The culprit fled by car.","furigana":"はんにん|くるま|とうそう"},{"japanese":"警察は逃走中の男の行方を追っている。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa tōsōchū no otoko no yukue o otte iru.","german":"The police are tracking the whereabouts of the man on the run.","furigana":"けいさつ|とうそうちゅう|おとこ|ゆくえ|お"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Leading and holding a group together: 統率力 (leadership ability). As a verb 統率する.","examples":[{"japanese":"キャプテンはチームをうまく統率している。","romaji":"Kyaputen wa chīmu o umaku tōsotsu shite iru.","german":"The captain leads the team well.","furigana":"とうそつ"},{"japanese":"彼には大勢の部下を統率する力がある。","romaji":"Kare ni wa ōzei no buka o tōsotsu suru chikara ga aru.","german":"He has the ability to lead a large number of subordinates.","furigana":"かれ|おおぜい|ぶか|とうそつ|ちから"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To fall in line with others' opinions or behavior: 〜に同調する; 同調圧力 (peer pressure, pressure to conform).","examples":[{"japanese":"周りの意見に同調するばかりでは、成長できない。","romaji":"Mawari no iken ni dōchō suru bakari de wa, seichō dekinai.","german":"If you only go along with the opinions around you, you can't grow.","furigana":"まわ|いけん|どうちょう|せいちょう"},{"japanese":"日本社会の同調圧力について議論した。","romaji":"Nihon shakai no dōchō atsuryoku ni tsuite giron shita.","german":"We discussed the pressure to conform in Japanese society.","furigana":"にほんしゃかい|どうちょうあつりょく|ぎろん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Technical and elevated; opposite: 静的 (static). 動的なウェブページ (dynamic web page).","examples":[{"japanese":"スポーツ写真では、選手の動的な瞬間をとらえることが大切だ。","romaji":"Supōtsu shashin de wa, senshu no dōteki na shunkan o toraeru koto ga taisetsu da.","german":"In sports photography, it is important to capture the athletes' dynamic moments.","furigana":"しゃしん|せんしゅ|どうてき|しゅんかん|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"社会を静的ではなく動的にとらえる必要がある。","romaji":"Shakai o seiteki de wa naku dōteki ni toraeru hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"We need to see society not as static but as dynamic.","furigana":"しゃかい|せいてき|どうてき|ひつよう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"As 堂々と (confidently, without hiding) or 堂々たる / 堂々とした (imposing, magnificent). Cf. 正々堂々 (fair and square).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼はみんなの前で堂々とスピーチした。","romaji":"Kare wa minna no mae de dōdō to supīchi shita.","german":"He gave a confident speech in front of everyone.","furigana":"かれ|まえ|どうどう"},{"japanese":"悪いことはしていないのだから、堂々としていればいい。","romaji":"Warui koto wa shite inai no dakara, dōdō to shite ireba ii.","german":"You haven't done anything wrong, so you can hold your head high.","furigana":"わる|どうどう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Barely, just about: どうにか間に合った (just made it). どうにかする (to do something about it), どうにかなる (it will work out somehow). Similar: なんとか.","examples":[{"japanese":"どうにか最終電車に間に合った。","romaji":"Dōnika saishū densha ni ma ni atta.","german":"I just barely made the last train.","furigana":"さいしゅうでんしゃ|ま|あ"},{"japanese":"この問題、どうにかならないかな。","romaji":"Kono mondai, dōnika naranai ka na.","german":"Isn't there something that can be done about this problem?","furigana":"もんだい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The person under discussion: 当人の気持ち (the feelings of the person concerned). Same meaning and more common: 本人.","examples":[{"japanese":"当人がいないところで悪口を言うのはよくない。","romaji":"Tōnin ga inai tokoro de waruguchi o iu no wa yoku nai.","german":"It's not good to badmouth someone behind their back.","furigana":"とうにん|わるぐち|い"},{"japanese":"周りが心配しても、当人は平気な顔をしている。","romaji":"Mawari ga shinpai shite mo, tōnin wa heiki na kao o shite iru.","german":"Even though everyone around is worried, the person himself looks unconcerned.","furigana":"まわ|しんぱい|とうにん|へいき|かお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Putting something into a letter or package: 写真を同封する; in business letters: 資料を同封いたします (I am enclosing the materials).","examples":[{"japanese":"履歴書に写真を同封してください。","romaji":"Rirekisho ni shashin o dōfū shite kudasai.","german":"Please enclose a photo with your résumé.","furigana":"りれきしょ|しゃしん|どうふう"},{"japanese":"同封の用紙に記入して、返送してください。","romaji":"Dōfū no yōshi ni kinyū shite, hensō shite kudasai.","german":"Please fill in the enclosed form and send it back.","furigana":"どうふう|ようし|きにゅう|へんそう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Power that drives machines: 動力源 (power source). Also figuratively a driving force.","examples":[{"japanese":"この船は風を動力にして進む。","romaji":"Kono fune wa kaze o dōryoku ni shite susumu.","german":"This ship moves using the wind as its power source.","furigana":"ふね|かぜ|どうりょく|すす"},{"japanese":"昔の工場では、水車が動力として使われていた。","romaji":"Mukashi no kōjō de wa, suisha ga dōryoku to shite tsukawarete ita.","german":"In factories of the past, the water wheel was used as motive power.","furigana":"むかし|こうじょう|すいしゃ|どうりょく|つか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To move farther away in space (足音が遠ざかる) or, figuratively, to lose contact with something: 政治から遠ざかる. Transitive: 遠ざける.","examples":[{"japanese":"船がゆっくりと港から遠ざかっていった。","romaji":"Fune ga yukkuri to minato kara tōzakatte itta.","german":"The ship slowly moved away from the harbor.","furigana":"ふね|みなと|とお"},{"japanese":"就職してから、すっかりスポーツから遠ざかっている。","romaji":"Shūshoku shite kara, sukkari supōtsu kara tōzakatte iru.","german":"Since I started working, I have drifted completely away from sports.","furigana":"しゅうしょく|とお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To take a longer route (遠回りする); figuratively also a roundabout approach. Proverb: 急がば回れ (more haste, less speed).","examples":[{"japanese":"道が混んでいたので、遠回りして帰った。","romaji":"Michi ga konde ita node, tōmawari shite kaetta.","german":"The road was crowded, so I took a detour home.","furigana":"みち|こ|とおまわ|かえ"},{"japanese":"遠回りに見えても、基礎から学ぶのが一番だ。","romaji":"Tōmawari ni miete mo, kiso kara manabu no ga ichiban da.","german":"Even if it looks like a detour, learning from the basics is best.","furigana":"とおまわ|み|きそ|まな|いちばん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To hold someone's mistake against them: 失敗を咎める. Intransitive in 気が咎める (to have a guilty conscience). Often in kana: とがめる.","examples":[{"japanese":"誰も彼の失敗を咎めなかった。","romaji":"Dare mo kare no shippai o togamenakatta.","german":"No one blamed him for his failure.","furigana":"だれ|かれ|しっぱい|とが"},{"japanese":"嘘をついたことで、ずっと気が咎めている。","romaji":"Uso o tsuita koto de, zutto ki ga togamete iru.","german":"Because I lied, my conscience has been bothering me the whole time.","furigana":"うそ|き|とが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"More elevated than 時々: 時折雨が降る. Also written 時おり.","examples":[{"japanese":"窓の外から時折鳥の声が聞こえる。","romaji":"Mado no soto kara tokiori tori no koe ga kikoeru.","german":"Now and then you can hear birdsong from outside the window.","furigana":"まど|そと|ときおりとり|こえ|き"},{"japanese":"彼女は時折寂しそうな表情を見せる。","romaji":"Kanojo wa tokiori sabishisō na hyōjō o miseru.","german":"She occasionally shows a lonely expression.","furigana":"かのじょ|ときおりさび|ひょうじょう|み"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Something continuous breaks off (temporarily): 話が途切れる (the conversation lulls), 電波が途切れる (the signal drops), 人通りが途切れる (foot traffic stops for a while). Cf. 途絶える (to cease entirely).","examples":[{"japanese":"電話の声が途切れて、よく聞こえない。","romaji":"Denwa no koe ga togirete, yoku kikoenai.","german":"The voice on the phone keeps cutting out, and I can hardly hear it.","furigana":"でんわ|こえ|とぎ|き"},{"japanese":"会話が途切れて、気まずい沈黙が流れた。","romaji":"Kaiwa ga togirete, kimazui chinmoku ga nagareta.","german":"The conversation stalled, and an awkward silence fell.","furigana":"かいわ|とぎ|き|ちんもく|なが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To explain a teaching or necessity earnestly: 必要性を説く, 教えを説く; also to try to persuade someone (説き伏せる = to talk someone round). Homophone: 解く (to solve).","examples":[{"japanese":"医者は運動の大切さを患者に説いた。","romaji":"Isha wa undō no taisetsusa o kanja ni toita.","german":"The doctor impressed upon the patient the importance of exercise.","furigana":"いしゃ|うんどう|たいせつ|かんじゃ|と"},{"japanese":"お釈迦様は人々に仏の教えを説いた。","romaji":"Oshakasama wa hitobito ni hotoke no oshie o toita.","german":"Buddha preached the Buddhist teachings to the people.","furigana":"|ひとびと|ほとけ|おし|と"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A product typical of a region: 特産品 (regional specialty), 〜の特産 (a specialty of ...).","examples":[{"japanese":"りんごは青森県の特産だ。","romaji":"Ringo wa Aomori-ken no tokusan da.","german":"Apples are a specialty of Aomori Prefecture.","furigana":"あおもりけん|とくさん"},{"japanese":"旅行先で地元の特産品を買った。","romaji":"Ryokōsaki de jimoto no tokusanhin o katta.","german":"At my travel destination I bought local specialties.","furigana":"りょこうさき|じもと|とくさんひん|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A featured topic in a magazine, newspaper, or program: 特集記事 (feature article), 特集番組 (special program). As a verb 特集する or 特集を組む.","examples":[{"japanese":"今月の雑誌は京都の特集だ。","romaji":"Kongetsu no zasshi wa Kyōto no tokushū da.","german":"This month's magazine has a feature on Kyoto.","furigana":"こんげつ|ざっし|きょうと|とくしゅう"},{"japanese":"テレビで地震の特集番組を見た。","romaji":"Terebi de jishin no tokushū bangumi o mita.","german":"I watched a special program about earthquakes on TV.","furigana":"じしん|とくしゅうばんぐみ|み"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mostly in 特派員 (foreign correspondent, special reporter); verb 特派する (to dispatch specially).","examples":[{"japanese":"新聞社は現地に記者を特派した。","romaji":"Shinbunsha wa genchi ni kisha o tokuha shita.","german":"The newspaper sent a reporter to the scene.","furigana":"しんぶんしゃ|げんち|きしゃ|とくは"},{"japanese":"彼はロンドン特派員として三年間働いた。","romaji":"Kare wa Rondon tokuhain to shite sannenkan hataraita.","german":"He worked as a correspondent in London for three years.","furigana":"かれ|とくはいん|さんねんかんはたら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A plant's thorn (バラのとげ), a splinter lodged in the skin (指にとげが刺さる), or figuratively a hurtful edge: とげのある言い方 (a barbed remark). Usually in kana.","examples":[{"japanese":"バラのとげが指に刺さった。","romaji":"Bara no toge ga yubi ni sasatta.","german":"A rose thorn pricked my finger.","furigana":"ゆび|さ"},{"japanese":"彼女の言葉にはとげがあった。","romaji":"Kanojo no kotoba ni wa toge ga atta.","german":"There was a sting in her words.","furigana":"かのじょ|ことば"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Securing everything before leaving or going to bed: 戸締まりをする, 戸締まりに気をつける.","examples":[{"japanese":"出かける前に戸締まりを確認した。","romaji":"Dekakeru mae ni tojimari o kakunin shita.","german":"Before going out, I checked that everything was locked.","furigana":"で|まえ|とじ|かくにん"},{"japanese":"最近空き巣が多いので、戸締まりに気をつけてください。","romaji":"Saikin akisu ga ōi node, tojimari ni ki o tsukete kudasai.","german":"There have been many burglaries lately, so please be careful to lock up.","furigana":"さいきんあ|す|おお|とじ|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Elevated: 帰宅の途上 (on the way home), 発展途上国 (developing country), 〜の途上にある (to be in the midst of ...).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は通勤の途上で事故に遭った。","romaji":"Kare wa tsūkin no tojō de jiko ni atta.","german":"He was in an accident on his way to work.","furigana":"かれ|つうきん|とじょう|じこ|あ"},{"japanese":"この技術はまだ研究の途上にある。","romaji":"Kono gijutsu wa mada kenkyū no tojō ni aru.","german":"This technology is still at the research stage.","furigana":"ぎじゅつ|けんきゅう|とじょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To staple sheets together or bind them into a booklet: ファイルに綴じる (to file). Homophone: 閉じる (to close).","examples":[{"japanese":"会議の資料をまとめて綴じた。","romaji":"Kaigi no shiryō o matomete tojita.","german":"I put the meeting materials together and bound them.","furigana":"かいぎ|しりょう|と"},{"japanese":"領収書は月ごとにファイルに綴じてある。","romaji":"Ryōshūsho wa tsukigoto ni fairu ni tojite aru.","german":"The receipts are filed in a binder by month.","furigana":"りょうしゅうしょ|つき|と"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The foundation of a building; figuratively a basis (成功の土台). As a colloquial adverb \"fundamentally, anyway\", usually with a negative statement: 土台無理な話だ (impossible from the start).","examples":[{"japanese":"家の土台がしっかりしていれば、地震にも強い。","romaji":"Ie no dodai ga shikkari shite ireba, jishin ni mo tsuyoi.","german":"If a house's foundation is solid, it will withstand earthquakes too.","furigana":"いえ|どだい|じしん|つよ"},{"japanese":"一週間で覚えるなんて、土台無理な話だ。","romaji":"Isshūkan de oboeru nante, dodai muri na hanashi da.","german":"Learning that in one week is impossible in the first place.","furigana":"いっしゅうかん|おぼ|どだいむり|はなし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Something ongoing stops: 連絡が途絶える (contact is lost), 人通りが途絶える (no one passes by anymore). Cf. 途切れる (to break off briefly, to falter).","examples":[{"japanese":"卒業後、彼との連絡が途絶えた。","romaji":"Sotsugyōgo, kare to no renraku ga todaeta.","german":"After graduation, contact with him broke off.","furigana":"そつぎょうご|かれ|れんらく|とだ"},{"japanese":"夜になると、この通りは人通りが途絶える。","romaji":"Yoru ni naru to, kono tōri wa hitodōri ga todaeru.","german":"At night, this street has no more foot traffic.","furigana":"よる|とお|ひとどお|とだ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mostly as 咄嗟の (spontaneous, on the spot: 咄嗟の判断) or 咄嗟に (instinctively, instantly). Often in kana: とっさ.","examples":[{"japanese":"咄嗟の判断で、ブレーキを踏んだ。","romaji":"Tossa no handan de, burēki o funda.","german":"On a split-second decision, I hit the brakes.","furigana":"とっさ|はんだん|ふ"},{"japanese":"名前を聞かれて、咄嗟に嘘をついてしまった。","romaji":"Namae o kikarete, tossa ni uso o tsuite shimatta.","german":"When asked my name, I lied on the spur of the moment.","furigana":"なまえ|き|とっさ|うそ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A handle on a door, drawer, cup, or pot: ドアの取っ手, カップの取っ手. Similar: 持ち手.","examples":[{"japanese":"引き出しの取っ手が取れてしまった。","romaji":"Hikidashi no totte ga torete shimatta.","german":"The drawer handle came off.","furigana":"ひ|だ|と|て|と"},{"japanese":"熱いので、鍋の取っ手を持ってください。","romaji":"Atsui node, nabe no totte o motte kudasai.","german":"It's hot, so please hold the pot by its handle.","furigana":"あつ|なべ|と|て|も"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A raised earthen bank, especially along rivers: 川の土手 (river embankment), where people often take walks.","examples":[{"japanese":"夕方、川の土手を散歩した。","romaji":"Yūgata, kawa no dote o sanpo shita.","german":"In the evening, I took a walk along the river embankment.","furigana":"ゆうがた|かわ|どて|さんぽ"},{"japanese":"大雨で土手が崩れる危険がある。","romaji":"Ōame de dote ga kuzureru kiken ga aru.","german":"There is a danger that the embankment will collapse due to the heavy rain.","furigana":"おおあめ|どて|くず|きけん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"留める／止める read as とどめる is transitive: you hold someone or something back, limit a process, or record a fact. The person or thing affected takes を. 出発をとどめる means \"to prevent the departure\", 被害を最小限にとどめる \"to keep the damage small\", and 記録にとどめる \"to put on record\". For the sense of recording, 留める is used in particular. The reading とどめる is an ichidan verb with とどめます and とどめて; とめる is a separate reading with other common expressions.","examples":[{"japanese":"被害を最小限に留めることができた。","romaji":"Higai o saishōgen ni todomeru koto ga dekita.","german":"We were able to keep the damage to a minimum.","furigana":"ひがい|さいしょうげん|とど"},{"japanese":"事故の教訓を記録に留めておく必要がある。","romaji":"Jiko no kyōkun o kiroku ni todomete oku hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"The lessons from the accident need to be put on record.","furigana":"じこ|きょうくん|きろく|とど|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"警官は危険を察し、乗客の出発を止めた。","romaji":"Keikan wa kiken o sasshi, jōkyaku no shuppatsu o todometa.","german":"The police officer sensed danger and stopped the passengers from departing.","furigana":"けいかん|きけん|さっ|じょうきゃく|しゅっぱつ|とど"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Literary: 夜の帳が下りる (night falls). Otherwise the character is usually read ちょう (手帳, 帳面).","examples":[{"japanese":"夜の帳が下りて、町は静かになった。","romaji":"Yoru no tobari ga orite, machi wa shizuka ni natta.","german":"The veil of night fell, and the town grew quiet.","furigana":"よる|とばり|お|まち|しず"},{"japanese":"部屋は厚い帳で二つに仕切られていた。","romaji":"Heya wa atsui tobari de futatsu ni shikirarete ita.","german":"The room was divided in two by a thick curtain.","furigana":"へや|あつ|とばり|ふた|しき"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"More elevated than ドア and often figurative: 未来への扉 (door to the future), 心の扉を開く (to open one's heart).","examples":[{"japanese":"重い扉をゆっくりと開けた。","romaji":"Omoi tobira o yukkuri to aketa.","german":"I slowly opened the heavy door.","furigana":"おも|とびら|あ"},{"japanese":"留学が新しい世界への扉を開いてくれた。","romaji":"Ryūgaku ga atarashii sekai e no tobira o hiraite kureta.","german":"Studying abroad opened a door to a new world for me.","furigana":"りゅうがく|あたら|せかい|とびら|ひら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Formal for 歩いて: 駅から徒歩五分 (five minutes' walk from the station), typical in apartment and hotel listings.","examples":[{"japanese":"ホテルは駅から徒歩五分です。","romaji":"Hoteru wa eki kara toho gofun desu.","german":"The hotel is a five-minute walk from the station.","furigana":"えき|とほごふん"},{"japanese":"バスがないので、徒歩で行くしかない。","romaji":"Basu ga nai node, toho de iku shika nai.","german":"There are no buses, so the only way is to go on foot.","furigana":"とほ|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Construction of roads, bridges, dams, etc.: 土木工事 (civil engineering works), 土木工学 (civil engineering). Cf. 建築 (building construction, architecture).","examples":[{"japanese":"兄は大学で土木を学んでいる。","romaji":"Ani wa daigaku de doboku o manande iru.","german":"My older brother is studying civil engineering at university.","furigana":"あに|だいがく|どぼく|まな"},{"japanese":"道路の土木工事で、しばらく通行止めになる。","romaji":"Dōro no doboku kōji de, shibaraku tsūkōdome ni naru.","german":"Because of civil engineering works, the road will be closed for a while.","furigana":"どうろ|どぼくこうじ|つうこうど"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Formal: 富を築く (to build a fortune), 富の格差 (wealth gap). More everyday: 財産, お金.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は一代で巨万の富を築いた。","romaji":"Kare wa ichidai de kyoman no tomi o kizuita.","german":"He built up an enormous fortune within a single generation.","furigana":"かれ|いちだい|きょまん|とみ|きず"},{"japanese":"富の格差が社会問題になっている。","romaji":"Tomi no kakusa ga shakai mondai ni natte iru.","german":"The wealth gap has become a social problem.","furigana":"とみ|かくさ|しゃかいもんだい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Both spouses work: 共稼ぎの夫婦. Today 共働き is more common.","examples":[{"japanese":"うちは共稼ぎなので、家事は分担している。","romaji":"Uchi wa tomokasegi na node, kaji wa buntan shite iru.","german":"We both work, so we share the housework.","furigana":"ともかせ|かじ|ぶんたん"},{"japanese":"共稼ぎの家庭が年々増えている。","romaji":"Tomokasegi no katei ga nennen fuete iru.","german":"The number of dual-income households is rising year by year.","furigana":"ともかせ|かてい|ねんねんふ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Literally dry (ドライな肌), but mostly figurative: rational and unsentimental (ドライな性格, ドライに割り切る). Can come across as cold.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は仕事と私生活をはっきり分けるドライな人だ。","romaji":"Kare wa shigoto to shiseikatsu o hakkiri wakeru dorai na hito da.","german":"He is a matter-of-fact person who strictly separates work from private life.","furigana":"かれ|しごと|しせいかつ|わ|ひと"},{"japanese":"ドライな肌には保湿クリームが欠かせない。","romaji":"Dorai na hada ni wa hoshitsu kurīmu ga kakasenai.","german":"Moisturizing cream is indispensable for dry skin.","furigana":"はだ|ほしつ|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Cleaning with solvents instead of water, e.g. for suits and coats. The shop is called クリーニング屋.","examples":[{"japanese":"このコートはドライクリーニングに出したほうがいい。","romaji":"Kono kōto wa doraikurīningu ni dashita hō ga ii.","german":"You should take this coat to the dry cleaner's.","furigana":"だ"},{"japanese":"スーツをドライクリーニングしてもらった。","romaji":"Sūtsu o doraikurīningu shite moratta.","german":"I had my suit dry-cleaned."}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"In Japan usually a roadside stop on national roads with a restaurant and parking lot; on expressways it is called サービスエリア. Also drive-in facilities such as a drive-in theater.","examples":[{"japanese":"国道沿いのドライブインで昼ご飯を食べた。","romaji":"Kokudōzoi no doraibuin de hirugohan o tabeta.","german":"We had lunch at a roadside restaurant along the national highway.","furigana":"こくどうぞ|ひる|はん|た"},{"japanese":"アメリカ旅行中、初めてドライブインシアターで映画を見た。","romaji":"Amerika ryokōchū, hajimete doraibuin shiatā de eiga o mita.","german":"On my trip to America, I watched a movie at a drive-in theater for the first time.","furigana":"りょこうちゅう|はじ|えいが|み"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Electronic component for amplifying and switching; also written トランジスター. トランジスタラジオ = transistor radio.","examples":[{"japanese":"この回路にはトランジスタが三つ使われている。","romaji":"Kono kairo ni wa toranjisuta ga mittsu tsukawarete iru.","german":"Three transistors are used in this circuit.","furigana":"かいろ|みっ|つか"},{"japanese":"祖父は古いトランジスタラジオを大切にしている。","romaji":"Sofu wa furui toranjisuta rajio o taisetsu ni shite iru.","german":"My grandfather treasures his old transistor radio.","furigana":"そふ|ふる|たいせつ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Noun of 取り替える: 部品の取り替え. Also written 取り換え.","examples":[{"japanese":"エアコンのフィルターは定期的な取り替えが必要だ。","romaji":"Eakon no firutā wa teikiteki na torikae ga hitsuyō da.","german":"The air conditioner's filter needs to be replaced regularly.","furigana":"ていきてき|と|か|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"サイズが合わなければ、取り替えに応じます。","romaji":"Saizu ga awanakereba, torikae ni ōjimasu.","german":"If the size doesn't fit, we will gladly exchange it.","furigana":"あ|と|か|おう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Official enforcement against violations: スピード違反の取り締まり (speed enforcement), 取り締まりを強化する. Verb: 取り締まる.","examples":[{"japanese":"年末は飲酒運転の取り締まりが厳しくなる。","romaji":"Nenmatsu wa inshu unten no torishimari ga kibishiku naru.","german":"Crackdowns on drunk driving get stricter at the end of the year.","furigana":"ねんまつ|いんしゅうんてん|と|し|きび"},{"japanese":"警察は違法駐車の取り締まりを強化した。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa ihō chūsha no torishimari o kyōka shita.","german":"The police stepped up enforcement against illegal parking.","furigana":"けいさつ|いほうちゅうしゃ|と|し|きょうか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mainly: to punish and stop violations (違法行為を取り締まる). Originally the general sense \"to supervise, manage\"; hence 取締役 (board member).","examples":[{"japanese":"警察はスピード違反を厳しく取り締まっている。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa supīdo ihan o kibishiku torishimatte iru.","german":"The police are strictly cracking down on speeding.","furigana":"けいさつ|いはん|きび|と|し"},{"japanese":"偽物の販売を取り締まる法律ができた。","romaji":"Nisemono no hanbai o torishimaru hōritsu ga dekita.","german":"A law was enacted to crack down on the sale of counterfeits.","furigana":"にせもの|はんばい|と|し|ほうりつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mostly in police contexts: to question suspects (容疑者を取り調べる); noun: 取り調べ (interrogation). For checking in general: 調べる.","examples":[{"japanese":"警察は容疑者を三時間取り調べた。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa yōgisha o sanjikan torishirabeta.","german":"The police questioned the suspect for three hours.","furigana":"けいさつ|ようぎしゃ|さんじかんと|しら"},{"japanese":"税関で荷物を細かく取り調べられた。","romaji":"Zeikan de nimotsu o komakaku torishiraberareta.","german":"My luggage was thoroughly inspected at customs.","furigana":"ぜいかん|にもつ|こま|と|しら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"1) To demand money: 借金を取り立てる. 2) To promote or favor someone: 部長に取り立てる. 3) To mention specially, usually in the negative: 取り立てて言うほどのことではない (not worth mentioning).","examples":[{"japanese":"金融業者が借金を取り立てに来た。","romaji":"Kin'yū gyōsha ga shakkin o toritate ni kita.","german":"The loan shark came to collect the debt.","furigana":"きんゆうぎょうしゃ|しゃっきん|と|た|き"},{"japanese":"取り立てて言うほどの問題はない。","romaji":"Toritatete iu hodo no mondai wa nai.","german":"There is no problem worth mentioning.","furigana":"と|た|い|もんだい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive counterpart of 生える (to grow): ひげを生やす (to grow a beard). Also for plants one lets grow unchecked: 雑草を生やしたままにする.","examples":[{"japanese":"父は最近ひげを生やし始めた。","romaji":"Chichi wa saikin hige o hayashi hajimeta.","german":"My father recently started growing a beard.","furigana":"ちち|さいきん|は|はじ"},{"japanese":"空き家の庭は、雑草を生やしたまま放置されている。","romaji":"Akiya no niwa wa, zassō o hayashita mama hōchi sarete iru.","german":"The garden of the vacant house is left as it is, with weeds growing freely.","furigana":"あ|や|にわ|ざっそう|は|ほうち"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive: 予定を早める (to move an appointment forward), 死期を早める (to hasten death). Intransitive: 早まる. For speed (to quicken one's pace), 速める is usually written.","examples":[{"japanese":"雨が降りそうなので、出発を一時間早めた。","romaji":"Ame ga furisō na node, shuppatsu o ichijikan hayameta.","german":"It looked like rain, so we moved the departure up by an hour.","furigana":"あめ|ふ|しゅっぱつ|いちじかんはや"},{"japanese":"会議の開始を少し早めてもらえませんか。","romaji":"Kaigi no kaishi o sukoshi hayamete moraemasen ka.","german":"Could we move the start of the meeting up a little?","furigana":"かいぎ|かいし|すこ|はや"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Noun of 腹が立つ (to get angry). Common in 腹立ちまぎれに (in anger, in a fit of rage).","examples":[{"japanese":"腹立ちまぎれに、ドアを強く閉めてしまった。","romaji":"Haradachi magire ni, doa o tsuyoku shimete shimatta.","german":"I slammed the door in anger.","furigana":"はらだ|つよ|し"},{"japanese":"彼の失礼な態度に、腹立ちを抑えられなかった。","romaji":"Kare no shitsurei na taido ni, haradachi o osaerarenakatta.","german":"I couldn't suppress my anger at his rude attitude.","furigana":"かれ|しつれい|たいど|はらだ|おさ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Colloquial for an open, usually grassy area, often a children's playground. Neutral: 野原.","examples":[{"japanese":"子どもの頃は、近くの原っぱで毎日野球をした。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro wa, chikaku no harappa de mainichi yakyū o shita.","german":"As a child I played baseball every day in the nearby field.","furigana":"こ|ころ|ちか|はら|まいにちやきゅう"},{"japanese":"昔ここは原っぱだったが、今はマンションが建っている。","romaji":"Mukashi koko wa harappa datta ga, ima wa manshon ga tatte iru.","german":"This used to be an open field, but now an apartment building stands here.","furigana":"むかし|はら|いま|た"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Usually in kana, also written ばら撒く. Figuratively often pejorative: 税金をばらまく (to hand out tax money indiscriminately); hence ばらまき (indiscriminate handouts).","examples":[{"japanese":"子どもが袋の豆を床にばらまいてしまった。","romaji":"Kodomo ga fukuro no mame o yuka ni baramaite shimatta.","german":"The child scattered the beans from the bag all over the floor.","furigana":"こ|ふくろ|まめ|ゆか"},{"japanese":"駅前で、選挙のチラシをばらまいている人がいた。","romaji":"Ekimae de, senkyo no chirashi o baramaite iru hito ga ita.","german":"Someone in front of the station was handing out election flyers in bulk.","furigana":"えきまえ|せんきょ|ひと"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A notice stuck to a wall or door with a message; also written 貼り紙. 張り紙禁止 = post no bills.","examples":[{"japanese":"店のドアに「本日休業」という張り紙がしてあった。","romaji":"Mise no doa ni \"honjitsu kyūgyō\" to iu harigami ga shite atta.","german":"A sign saying \"Closed today\" was posted on the shop door.","furigana":"みせ|ほんじつきゅうぎょう|は|がみ"},{"japanese":"迷子の猫を探す張り紙を、近所の電柱に貼った。","romaji":"Maigo no neko o sagasu harigami o, kinjo no denchū ni hatta.","german":"I put up posters looking for the lost cat on utility poles in the neighborhood.","furigana":"まいご|ねこ|さが|は|がみ|きんじょ|でんちゅう|は"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"破裂する = to burst, rupture: 水道管が破裂する (the water pipe bursts). Figuratively: 交渉が破裂する (negotiations break down), today usually 決裂.","examples":[{"japanese":"寒さで水道管が破裂した。","romaji":"Samusa de suidōkan ga haretsu shita.","german":"The water pipe burst because of the cold.","furigana":"さむ|すいどうかん|はれつ"},{"japanese":"風船に空気を入れすぎて、破裂させてしまった。","romaji":"Fūsen ni kūki o iresugite, haretsu sasete shimatta.","german":"I put too much air into the balloon and made it burst.","furigana":"ふうせん|くうき|い|はれつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"判を押す = to stamp, to confirm with a name seal; colloquially はんこ. Idiom: 判で押したような (always the same, stereotyped). With the reading ばん, 判 stands for paper sizes (A4判).","examples":[{"japanese":"書類のここに判を押してください。","romaji":"Shorui no koko ni han o oshite kudasai.","german":"Please put your seal on the document here.","furigana":"しょるい|はん|お"},{"japanese":"契約書に判を押す前に、内容をよく確認した。","romaji":"Keiyakusho ni han o osu mae ni, naiyō o yoku kakunin shita.","german":"Before stamping the contract, I checked its contents carefully.","furigana":"けいやくしょ|はん|お|まえ|ないよう|かくにん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Also as a counter: 第3版 (3rd edition). 最新版 (latest version), 日本語版 (Japanese edition). 版を重ねる = to go through many editions.","examples":[{"japanese":"この辞書は版を重ねて、今でも多くの人に使われている。","romaji":"Kono jisho wa han o kasanete, ima demo ōku no hito ni tsukawarete iru.","german":"This dictionary has gone through many editions and is still used by many people today.","furigana":"じしょ|はん|かさ|いま|おお|ひと|つか"},{"japanese":"新しい版では、古い版の誤りが直されている。","romaji":"Atarashii han de wa, furui han no ayamari ga naosarete iru.","german":"The new edition corrects the errors of the old one.","furigana":"あたら|はん|ふる|はん|あやま|なお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A small working group at school, work, or in administration: 班に分かれる (to split into groups), 班長 (group leader). As a suffix: 救護班 (rescue squad).","examples":[{"japanese":"生徒たちは五つの班に分かれて、町を調べた。","romaji":"Seitotachi wa itsutsu no han ni wakarete, machi o shirabeta.","german":"The students split into five groups and explored the town.","furigana":"せいと|いつ|はん|わ|まち|しら"},{"japanese":"同じ班の人と一緒に、発表の準備をした。","romaji":"Onaji han no hito to issho ni, happyō no junbi o shita.","german":"I prepared the presentation together with the people in my group.","furigana":"おな|はん|ひと|いっしょ|はっぴょう|じゅんび"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Clothes hanger (from English hanger). Pronounced the same but separate words: ハンガー \"aircraft hangar\" and ハンガー \"hunger\" (in ハンガーストライキ, hunger strike).","examples":[{"japanese":"コートをハンガーに掛けておいてください。","romaji":"Kōto o hangā ni kakete oite kudasai.","german":"Please hang your coat on a hanger.","furigana":"か"},{"japanese":"シャツをハンガーに掛けたまま干した。","romaji":"Shatsu o hangā ni kaketa mama hoshita.","german":"I dried the shirts hanging on the hangers.","furigana":"か|ほ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"パンクする = to get a flat tire: タイヤがパンクした. Figuratively: to be overloaded, to break down (電話回線がパンクする). Same sound: パンク (punk music).","examples":[{"japanese":"自転車のタイヤがパンクして、駅まで押して歩いた。","romaji":"Jitensha no taiya ga panku shite, eki made oshite aruita.","german":"My bicycle tire got a flat, so I pushed it to the station.","furigana":"じてんしゃ|えき|お|ある"},{"japanese":"注文が殺到して、サーバーがパンクした。","romaji":"Chūmon ga sattō shite, sābā ga panku shita.","german":"A flood of orders overloaded the server.","furigana":"ちゅうもん|さっとう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"反撃する = to strike back; also in sports and debates: 反撃に出る (to go on the counterattack).","examples":[{"japanese":"後半に入ると、相手チームの反撃が始まった。","romaji":"Kōhan ni hairu to, aite chīmu no hangeki ga hajimatta.","german":"In the second half, the opposing team began its counterattack.","furigana":"こうはん|はい|あいて|はんげき|はじ"},{"japanese":"厳しい批判に対して、彼はすぐに反撃した。","romaji":"Kibishii hihan ni taishite, kare wa sugu ni hangeki shita.","german":"He immediately hit back against the harsh criticism.","furigana":"きび|ひはん|たい|かれ|はんげき"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Written style, mostly 〜に反する / 〜に反して: 規則に反する (to violate the rules), 予想に反して (contrary to expectations; also a grammar pattern 〜に反して).","examples":[{"japanese":"他人の作品を無断で使うのは、法律に反する。","romaji":"Tanin no sakuhin o mudan de tsukau no wa, hōritsu ni hansuru.","german":"Using other people's works without permission violates the law.","furigana":"たにん|さくひん|むだん|つか|ほうりつ|はん"},{"japanese":"大方の予想に反して、新人がその賞を取った。","romaji":"Ōkata no yosō ni hanshite, shinjin ga sono shō o totta.","german":"Contrary to most expectations, a newcomer won the award.","furigana":"おおかた|よそう|はん|しんじん|しょう|と"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The late years of a life, especially of well-known people: 晩年の作品 (late work).","examples":[{"japanese":"その画家は晩年、故郷の村で静かに暮らした。","romaji":"Sono gaka wa bannen, kokyō no mura de shizuka ni kurashita.","german":"In his later years, the painter lived quietly in his home village.","furigana":"がか|ばんねん|こきょう|むら|しず|く"},{"japanese":"これは作家が晩年に書いた小説だ。","romaji":"Kore wa sakka ga bannen ni kaita shōsetsu da.","german":"This is a novel the writer wrote late in life.","furigana":"さっか|ばんねん|か|しょうせつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Often with の or as a first element: 万能ナイフ (all-purpose knife), 万能薬 (cure-all). スポーツ万能 = good at all sports.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女はスポーツ万能で、どの競技でも活躍する。","romaji":"Kanojo wa supōtsu bannō de, dono kyōgi demo katsuyaku suru.","german":"She is an all-round athlete and shines in every sport.","furigana":"かのじょ|ばんのう|きょうぎ|かつやく"},{"japanese":"お金は万能ではない。","romaji":"Okane wa bannō de wa nai.","german":"Money is not omnipotent.","furigana":"かね|ばんのう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"As a na-adjective: 半端な気持ち (half-hearted attitude); intensified as 中途半端. Colloquially 半端じゃない (also ハンパない) = unbelievable, insane.","examples":[{"japanese":"半端な気持ちでは、この仕事は続けられない。","romaji":"Hanpa na kimochi de wa, kono shigoto wa tsuzukerarenai.","german":"You can't keep up this job with a half-hearted attitude.","furigana":"はんぱ|きも|しごと|つづ"},{"japanese":"今日の暑さは半端じゃない。","romaji":"Kyō no atsusa wa hanpa ja nai.","german":"Today's heat is insane.","furigana":"きょう|あつ|はんぱ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"反乱を起こす (to start a revolt), 反乱軍 (rebel army). Similar sound: 氾濫 (flood).","examples":[{"japanese":"重い税に苦しんだ農民たちが、反乱を起こした。","romaji":"Omoi zei ni kurushinda nōmintachi ga, hanran o okoshita.","german":"Peasants suffering under heavy taxes rose in revolt.","furigana":"おも|ぜい|くる|のうみん|はんらん|お"},{"japanese":"反乱は数日で鎮圧された。","romaji":"Hanran wa sūjitsu de chin'atsu sareta.","german":"The revolt was suppressed within a few days.","furigana":"はんらん|すうじつ|ちんあつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Turns a noun of action into the party that undergoes or receives something: 被保険者 (insured person), 被選挙権 (right to stand for election), 被害 (damage suffered).","examples":[{"japanese":"会社員は、健康保険の被保険者になる。","romaji":"Kaishain wa, kenkō hoken no hihokensha ni naru.","german":"Company employees become insured persons under health insurance.","furigana":"かいしゃいん|けんこうほけん|ひほけんしゃ"},{"japanese":"日本では、二十五歳以上になると衆議院議員の被選挙権がある。","romaji":"Nihon de wa, nijūgo sai ijō ni naru to shūgiin giin no hisenkyoken ga aru.","german":"In Japan, from the age of 25 you have the right to stand for election to the House of Representatives.","furigana":"にほん|にじゅうごさいいじょう|しゅうぎいんぎいん|ひせんきょけん"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Suffix for types of costs: 交通費 (transportation costs), 生活費 (living costs), 会費 (membership fee). In general: 費用 (cost).","examples":[{"japanese":"面接に来る方には、交通費をお支払いします。","romaji":"Mensetsu ni kuru kata ni wa, kōtsūhi o oshiharai shimasu.","german":"We will pay the transportation costs for those who come for an interview.","furigana":"めんせつ|く|かた|こうつうひ|しはら"},{"japanese":"東京は生活費が高い。","romaji":"Tōkyō wa seikatsuhi ga takai.","german":"Living costs are high in Tokyo.","furigana":"とうきょう|せいかつひ|たか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A room where one waits before a performance, ceremony, or exam, e.g. for speakers, performers, or brides and grooms. Also written 控室.","examples":[{"japanese":"出演者は、本番まで控え室で待機してください。","romaji":"Shutsuensha wa, honban made hikaeshitsu de taiki shite kudasai.","german":"Performers, please wait in the waiting room until the show.","furigana":"しゅつえんしゃ|ほんばん|ひか|しつ|たいき"},{"japanese":"結婚式の前、新婦は控え室で着替えをした。","romaji":"Kekkonshiki no mae, shinpu wa hikaeshitsu de kigae o shita.","german":"Before the wedding, the bride changed in the waiting room.","furigana":"けっこんしき|まえ|しんぷ|ひか|しつ|きが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"悲観する = to be pessimistic, to despair: 将来を悲観する. Adjective: 悲観的 (pessimistic); opposite: 楽観.","examples":[{"japanese":"一度失敗したくらいで、そんなに悲観することはない。","romaji":"Ichido shippai shita kurai de, sonna ni hikan suru koto wa nai.","german":"There's no need to be so pessimistic just because you failed once.","furigana":"いちどしっぱい|ひかん"},{"japanese":"医者の説明を聞いて、彼は自分の病気を悲観した。","romaji":"Isha no setsumei o kiite, kare wa jibun no byōki o hikan shita.","german":"After hearing the doctor's explanation, he despaired about his illness.","furigana":"いしゃ|せつめい|き|かれ|じぶん|びょうき|ひかん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive: 沈没船を引き上げる (to salvage a sunken ship), 税率を引き上げる (to raise the tax rate), 部隊を引き上げる (to withdraw troops). For \"to withdraw, return home\" 引き揚げる is also used. Opposite when raising: 引き下げる.","examples":[{"japanese":"政府は来年、消費税を引き上げる方針だ。","romaji":"Seifu wa rainen, shōhizei o hikiageru hōshin da.","german":"The government plans to raise the consumption tax next year.","furigana":"せいふ|らいねん|しょうひぜい|ひ|あ|ほうしん"},{"japanese":"海に落ちた車が、クレーンで引き上げられた。","romaji":"Umi ni ochita kuruma ga, kurēn de hikiagerareta.","german":"The car that had fallen into the sea was hauled up with a crane.","furigana":"うみ|お|くるま|ひ|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive: 料金を引き下げる (to lower fees), 要求を引き下げる (to withdraw a demand). Opposite: 引き上げる.","examples":[{"japanese":"携帯電話の料金が引き下げられることになった。","romaji":"Keitai denwa no ryōkin ga hikisagerareru koto ni natta.","german":"It was decided to lower mobile phone fees.","furigana":"けいたいでんわ|りょうきん|ひ|さ"},{"japanese":"反対が多かったので、彼は提案を引き下げた。","romaji":"Hantai ga ōkatta node, kare wa teian o hikisageta.","german":"Because there was a lot of opposition, he withdrew his proposal.","furigana":"はんたい|おお|かれ|ていあん|ひ|さ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"足を引きずる (to drag a leg, to limp). Figuratively: 過去を引きずる (to be unable to let go of the past). Also written 引き摺る.","examples":[{"japanese":"重いスーツケースを引きずって、坂道を上った。","romaji":"Omoi sūtsukēsu o hikizutte, sakamichi o nobotta.","german":"I dragged the heavy suitcase up the slope.","furigana":"おも|ひ|さかみち|のぼ"},{"japanese":"彼はけがをした足を引きずりながら、ゴールまで走った。","romaji":"Kare wa kega o shita ashi o hikizuri nagara, gōru made hashitta.","german":"Dragging his injured leg, he ran all the way to the finish line.","furigana":"かれ|あし|ひ|はし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"不用品を引き取る (to take back unwanted items), 子どもを引き取る (to take a child in). Euphemism: 息を引き取る = to pass away.","examples":[{"japanese":"古い冷蔵庫は、販売店が引き取ってくれた。","romaji":"Furui reizōko wa, hanbaiten ga hikitotte kureta.","german":"The retailer took back the old refrigerator.","furigana":"ふる|れいぞうこ|はんばいてん|ひ|と"},{"japanese":"両親が亡くなった後、彼女は祖母に引き取られた。","romaji":"Ryōshin ga nakunatta ato, kanojo wa sobo ni hikitorareta.","german":"After her parents died, she was taken in by her grandmother.","furigana":"りょうしん|な|あと|かのじょ|そぼ|ひ|と"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mainly of young people: 非行に走る (to go astray), 非行少年 (juvenile delinquent). Similar sound: 飛行 (flight).","examples":[{"japanese":"家庭の問題が原因で、非行に走る子どももいる。","romaji":"Katei no mondai ga gen'in de, hikō ni hashiru kodomo mo iru.","german":"Some children turn to delinquency because of family problems.","furigana":"かてい|もんだい|げんいん|ひこう|はし|こ"},{"japanese":"地域の大人たちが、少年の非行を防ぐために見回りをしている。","romaji":"Chiiki no otonatachi ga, shōnen no hikō o fusegu tame ni mimawari o shite iru.","german":"Adults in the neighborhood make patrols to prevent juvenile delinquency.","furigana":"ちいき|おとな|しょうねん|ひこう|ふせ|みまわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Often in compounds: ビジネスマン (businessman), ビジネスホテル (simple hotel for business travelers), ビジネスクラス. More general: 仕事, 商売.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は大学を卒業してすぐ、自分でビジネスを始めた。","romaji":"Kare wa daigaku o sotsugyō shite sugu, jibun de bijinesu o hajimeta.","german":"He started his own business right after graduating from university.","furigana":"かれ|だいがく|そつぎょう|じぶん|はじ"},{"japanese":"出張のときは、駅前のビジネスホテルに泊まる。","romaji":"Shucchō no toki wa, ekimae no bijinesu hoteru ni tomaru.","german":"On business trips, I stay at the business hotel in front of the station.","furigana":"しゅっちょう|えきまえ|と"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 1 (source: N5; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"Physics: 比重が大きい (has a higher specific gravity). Figuratively: 〜に比重を置く (to place emphasis on ...).","examples":[{"japanese":"油は水より比重が小さいので、水に浮く。","romaji":"Abura wa mizu yori hijū ga chiisai node, mizu ni uku.","german":"Oil has a lower specific gravity than water, so it floats on water.","furigana":"あぶら|みず|ひじゅう|ちい|みず|う"},{"japanese":"この大学は、研究よりも教育に比重を置いている。","romaji":"Kono daigaku wa, kenkyū yori mo kyōiku ni hijū o oite iru.","german":"This university places more emphasis on teaching than on research.","furigana":"だいがく|けんきゅう|きょういく|ひじゅう|お"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Personal assistant to an executive or politician: 社長秘書, 議員秘書. General office worker: 事務員.","examples":[{"japanese":"社長の予定は、すべて秘書が管理している。","romaji":"Shachō no yotei wa, subete hisho ga kanri shite iru.","german":"The president's schedule is entirely managed by his secretary.","furigana":"しゃちょう|よてい|ひしょ|かんり"},{"japanese":"彼女は大学を出た後、国会議員の秘書として働いた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa daigaku o deta ato, kokkai giin no hisho to shite hataraita.","german":"After graduating from university, she worked as a secretary to a Diet member.","furigana":"かのじょ|だいがく|で|あと|こっかいぎいん|ひしょ|はたら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Written style: 微笑を浮かべる (to have a smile on one's face). Everyday: 微笑み (ほほえみ), verb 微笑む.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は口元に微笑を浮かべて、静かにうなずいた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kuchimoto ni bishō o ukabete, shizuka ni unazuita.","german":"With a smile on her lips, she nodded quietly.","furigana":"かのじょ|くちもと|びしょう|う|しず"},{"japanese":"絵の中の女性は、穏やかな微笑をたたえている。","romaji":"E no naka no josei wa, odayaka na bishō o tataete iru.","german":"The woman in the painting wears a gentle smile.","furigana":"え|なか|じょせい|おだ|びしょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Usually in kana. Totally focused on one thing: ひたすら待つ (to just wait), ひたすら謝る (to apologize over and over).","examples":[{"japanese":"合格するために、毎日ひたすら勉強した。","romaji":"Gōkaku suru tame ni, mainichi hitasura benkyō shita.","german":"I studied single-mindedly every day in order to pass.","furigana":"ごうかく|まいにち|べんきょう"},{"japanese":"彼は何も言わず、ひたすら頭を下げ続けた。","romaji":"Kare wa nani mo iwazu, hitasura atama o sage tsuzuketa.","german":"Without saying anything, he just kept bowing his head.","furigana":"かれ|なに|い|あたま|さ|つづ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To scratch with claws or fingernails: 猫に引っ掻かれる (to be scratched by a cat). For scratching an itch, かく is usually used.","examples":[{"japanese":"猫に手を引っ掻かれて、血が出た。","romaji":"Neko ni te o hikkakarete, chi ga deta.","german":"The cat scratched my hand and it bled.","furigana":"ねこ|て|ひ|か|ち|で"},{"japanese":"犬がドアを引っ掻いて、傷をつけてしまった。","romaji":"Inu ga doa o hikkaite, kizu o tsukete shimatta.","german":"The dog scratched at the door and left marks.","furigana":"いぬ|ひ|か|きず"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive: 上着を引っ掛ける (to throw on a jacket), 釘に服を引っ掛ける (to snag one's clothes on a nail). Colloquial: 一杯引っ掛ける (to have a quick drink). Intransitive: 引っ掛かる.","examples":[{"japanese":"出かける前に、上着を一枚引っ掛けていった。","romaji":"Dekakeru mae ni, uwagi o ichimai hikkakete itta.","german":"Before going out, I threw on a jacket.","furigana":"で|まえ|うわぎ|いちまいひ|か"},{"japanese":"釘にセーターを引っ掛けて、穴が開いてしまった。","romaji":"Kugi ni sētā o hikkakete, ana ga aite shimatta.","german":"My sweater snagged on a nail and now there's a hole.","furigana":"くぎ|ひ|か|あな|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Onomatopoeic; mostly with 濡れる, with だ, or in 汗びっしょり (drenched in sweat). Similar: ずぶ濡れ.","examples":[{"japanese":"急な雨で、服がびっしょり濡れてしまった。","romaji":"Kyū na ame de, fuku ga bisshori nurete shimatta.","german":"The sudden rain left my clothes soaking wet.","furigana":"きゅう|あめ|ふく|ぬ"},{"japanese":"走って帰ってきたら、汗でシャツがびっしょりだった。","romaji":"Hashitte kaette kitara, ase de shatsu ga bisshori datta.","german":"When I ran home, my shirt was drenched with sweat.","furigana":"はし|かえ|あせ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"一息つく (to take a breather, to pause briefly), 一息に飲む (to drink in one go), あと一息 / もう一息 (just a little more, almost there).","examples":[{"japanese":"仕事が一段落したので、コーヒーを飲んで一息ついた。","romaji":"Shigoto ga ichidanraku shita node, kōhī o nonde hitoiki tsuita.","german":"Since the work had reached a stopping point, I had a coffee and took a breather.","furigana":"しごと|いちだんらく|の|ひといき"},{"japanese":"ゴールまであと一息だ。頑張れ！","romaji":"Gōru made ato hitoiki da. Ganbare!","german":"It's just a little further to the finish line. Hang in there!","furigana":"ひといき|がんば"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Opposite: 二重 (ふたえ, e.g. 二重まぶた = eyelid with a crease) and 八重 (double-flowered). Idiom: 紙一重 (razor-thin, by a hair's breadth).","examples":[{"japanese":"この桜は花びらが一重で、八重桜より早く咲く。","romaji":"Kono sakura wa hanabira ga hitoe de, yaezakura yori hayaku saku.","german":"This cherry tree has single-petaled blossoms and blooms earlier than the double-flowered Yae cherry.","furigana":"さくら|はな|ひとえ|やえざくら|はや|さ"},{"japanese":"私は一重なので、目が小さく見えるのが悩みだ。","romaji":"Watashi wa hitoe na node, me ga chiisaku mieru no ga nayami da.","german":"My eyelids have no crease, so my eyes look small, which bothers me.","furigana":"わたし|ひとえ|め|ちい|み|なや"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mostly in statements about presence: 人影がない (deserted), 人影もまばら (hardly anyone in sight).","examples":[{"japanese":"夜の公園には、人影が一つもなかった。","romaji":"Yoru no kōen ni wa, hitokage ga hitotsu mo nakatta.","german":"There wasn't a single soul in the park at night.","furigana":"よる|こうえん|ひとかげ|ひと"},{"japanese":"窓の外に怪しい人影が見えた。","romaji":"Mado no soto ni ayashii hitokage ga mieta.","german":"I saw a suspicious figure outside the window.","furigana":"まど|そと|あや|ひとかげ|み"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Usually in kana. Refers to a past phase: ひところほどの人気はない (not as popular as at one time). Similar: 一時 (いちじ).","examples":[{"japanese":"あの歌手には、ひところほどの人気はない。","romaji":"Ano kashu ni wa, hitokoro hodo no ninki wa nai.","german":"That singer isn't as popular as she once was.","furigana":"かしゅ|にんき"},{"japanese":"ひところは毎晩のように飲みに行っていたが、今はほとんど行かない。","romaji":"Hitokoro wa maiban no yō ni nomi ni itte ita ga, ima wa hotondo ikanai.","german":"For a while I used to go out drinking almost every night, but now I hardly go at all.","furigana":"まいばん|の|い|いま|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"人質に取る (to take hostage), 人質を解放する (to release hostages). Figuratively: 〜を人質に取る (to use something as a bargaining chip).","examples":[{"japanese":"犯人は銀行員を人質に取って、立てこもった。","romaji":"Hannin wa ginkōin o hitojichi ni totte, tatekomotta.","german":"The criminal took a bank clerk hostage and barricaded himself in.","furigana":"はんにん|ぎんこういん|ひとじち|と|た"},{"japanese":"三日後、人質は全員無事に解放された。","romaji":"Mikka go, hitojichi wa zen'in buji ni kaihō sareta.","german":"Three days later, all the hostages were released unharmed.","furigana":"みっかご|ひとじち|ぜんいんぶじ|かいほう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"人目を気にする (to worry about what people think), 人目につく (to attract attention), 人目を避ける (to avoid being seen). Same sound: 一目 (a glance).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は人目を気にせず、駅で大声で歌っていた。","romaji":"Kare wa hitome o ki ni sezu, eki de ōgoe de utatte ita.","german":"He sang loudly at the station, paying no attention to the people around him.","furigana":"かれ|ひとめ|き|えき|おおごえ|うた"},{"japanese":"人目につかないように、裏口から出た。","romaji":"Hitome ni tsukanai yō ni, uraguchi kara deta.","german":"I left through the back door so as not to be seen.","furigana":"ひとめ|うらぐち|で"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The date for an event: 結婚式の日取りを決める. More general, also for the course of events: 日程.","examples":[{"japanese":"二人は結婚式の日取りをまだ決めていない。","romaji":"Futari wa kekkonshiki no hidori o mada kimete inai.","german":"The two have not yet decided the date of the wedding.","furigana":"ふたり|けっこんしき|ひど|き"},{"japanese":"引っ越しの日取りが決まったら、すぐに知らせてください。","romaji":"Hikkoshi no hidori ga kimattara, sugu ni shirasete kudasai.","german":"Please let me know right away once the date of the move is set.","furigana":"ひ|こ|ひど|き|し"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Often also in kana ひな. The dolls for the Girls' Festival on March 3rd are called 雛人形; the festival itself ひな祭り.","examples":[{"japanese":"巣の中で、ツバメの雛がえさを待っている。","romaji":"Su no naka de, tsubame no hina ga esa o matte iru.","german":"In the nest, baby swallows are waiting for food.","furigana":"す|なか|ひな|ま"},{"japanese":"三月三日には、雛人形を飾って女の子の成長を祝う。","romaji":"Sangatsu mikka ni wa, hina ningyō o kazatte onna no ko no seichō o iwau.","german":"On March 3rd, people display hina dolls and celebrate the growth of girls.","furigana":"さんがつみっか|ひなにんぎょう|かざ|おんな|こ|せいちょう|いわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"日向で (in the sun), 日向ぼっこ (sunbathing, basking in the sun). Opposite: 日陰 (shade).","examples":[{"japanese":"猫が日向で気持ちよさそうに寝ている。","romaji":"Neko ga hinata de kimochi yosasō ni nete iru.","german":"The cat is sleeping comfortably in the sun.","furigana":"ねこ|ひなた|きも|ね"},{"japanese":"洗濯物は日向に干したほうがよく乾く。","romaji":"Sentakumono wa hinata ni hoshita hō ga yoku kawaku.","german":"Laundry dries better if you hang it in the sun.","furigana":"せんたくもの|ひなた|ほ|かわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The white flag with a red disc; official name 日章旗. 日の丸弁当 = rice with a single pickled plum in the middle.","examples":[{"japanese":"表彰式で日の丸が揚がると、選手は涙を流した。","romaji":"Hyōshōshiki de hinomaru ga agaru to, senshu wa namida o nagashita.","german":"When the Japanese flag was raised at the award ceremony, the athlete shed tears.","furigana":"ひょうしょうしき|ひ|まる|あ|せんしゅ|なみだ|なが"},{"japanese":"沿道のファンが、日の丸の小旗を振って応援した。","romaji":"Endō no fan ga, hinomaru no kobata o futte ōen shita.","german":"Fans along the street cheered, waving small Japanese flags.","furigana":"えんどう|ひ|まる|こばた|ふ|おうえん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"火花が散る (sparks fly). Figuratively: 火花を散らす (to fight a fierce contest).","examples":[{"japanese":"電線から火花が出て、あたりが一瞬明るくなった。","romaji":"Densen kara hibana ga dete, atari ga isshun akaruku natta.","german":"Sparks flew from the power line and for a moment the area lit up.","furigana":"でんせん|ひばな|で|いっしゅんあか"},{"japanese":"決勝戦では、二人のエースが激しい火花を散らした。","romaji":"Kesshōsen de wa, futari no ēsu ga hageshii hibana o chirashita.","german":"In the final, the two aces fought a fierce duel.","furigana":"けっしょうせん|ふたり|はげ|ひばな|ち"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Somewhat more formal than 毎日: 日々の暮らし (daily life), 日々努力する (to make an effort day after day). Same sound: ひび (crack).","examples":[{"japanese":"日々の小さな努力が、大きな成果につながる。","romaji":"Hibi no chiisa na doryoku ga, ōki na seika ni tsunagaru.","german":"Small daily efforts lead to great results.","furigana":"ひび|ちい|どりょく|おお|せいか"},{"japanese":"留学していた日々を、今でもよく思い出す。","romaji":"Ryūgaku shite ita hibi o, ima demo yoku omoidasu.","german":"I still often think back on the days when I studied abroad.","furigana":"りゅうがく|ひび|いま|おも|だ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Usually in kana. ひびが入る (to crack); figuratively: 友情にひびが入る (the friendship develops a rift). Same sound: 日々 (the days).","examples":[{"japanese":"落としたコップにひびが入ってしまった。","romaji":"Otoshita koppu ni hibi ga haitte shimatta.","german":"The glass I dropped got a crack.","furigana":"お|はい"},{"japanese":"その事件で、二人の友情にひびが入った。","romaji":"Sono jiken de, futari no yūjō ni hibi ga haitta.","german":"The incident put a crack in the two's friendship.","furigana":"じけん|ふたり|ゆうじょう|はい"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"悲鳴を上げる (to scream; figuratively also: to cry out under overload). 嬉しい悲鳴 = \"joyful scream\", complaining about too much success or work.","examples":[{"japanese":"暗い部屋で何かに触れて、思わず悲鳴を上げた。","romaji":"Kurai heya de nanika ni furete, omowazu himei o ageta.","german":"I touched something in the dark room and involuntarily screamed.","furigana":"くら|へや|なに|ふ|おも|ひめい|あ"},{"japanese":"遠くから女性の悲鳴が聞こえた。","romaji":"Tōku kara josei no himei ga kikoeta.","german":"A woman's scream could be heard from far away.","furigana":"とお|じょせい|ひめい|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"冷やかされる = to be teased, e.g. about a romantic relationship. 冷やかし = someone who only browses; also a mocking remark. Not to be confused with 冷やす (to cool).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女と歩いているところを友達に冷やかされた。","romaji":"Kanojo to aruite iru tokoro o tomodachi ni hiyakasareta.","german":"My friends teased me when they saw me walking with my girlfriend.","furigana":"かのじょ|ある|ともだち|ひ"},{"japanese":"買うつもりはなく、店を冷やかして回った。","romaji":"Kau tsumori wa naku, mise o hiyakashite mawatta.","german":"I had no intention of buying and just browsed around the shops.","furigana":"か|みせ|ひ|まわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Also as a counter: 一票, 百票. 票を入れる (to cast one's vote), 票を集める (to collect votes). Election, voting: 投票.","examples":[{"japanese":"私は若い候補者に票を入れた。","romaji":"Watashi wa wakai kōhosha ni hyō o ireta.","german":"I voted for the young candidate.","furigana":"わたし|わか|こうほしゃ|ひょう|い"},{"japanese":"二人の候補者の差は、わずか十票だった。","romaji":"Futari no kōhosha no sa wa, wazuka juppyō datta.","german":"The difference between the two candidates was only ten votes.","furigana":"ふたり|こうほしゃ|さ|じゅっぴょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A short guiding phrase for campaigns, e.g. 交通安全の標語. From English: スローガン.","examples":[{"japanese":"学校で、いじめをなくすための標語を募集した。","romaji":"Gakkō de, ijime o nakusu tame no hyōgo o boshū shita.","german":"The school invited slogans for eliminating bullying.","furigana":"がっこう|ひょうご|ぼしゅう"},{"japanese":"駅には「かけこみ乗車はやめましょう」という標語が貼ってある。","romaji":"Eki ni wa \"kakekomi jōsha wa yamemashō\" to iu hyōgo ga hatte aru.","german":"A slogan reading \"Please don't rush onto the train\" is posted at the station.","furigana":"えき|じょうしゃ|ひょうご|は"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Almost only in fixed combinations: ひょっとしたら / ひょっとすると / ひょっとして (perhaps, possibly). Expresses a rather unexpected possibility.","examples":[{"japanese":"ひょっとしたら、彼はもう帰ったのかもしれない。","romaji":"Hyotto shitara, kare wa mō kaetta no kamo shirenai.","german":"Perhaps he has already gone home.","furigana":"かれ|かえ"},{"japanese":"ひょっとして、田中さんの妹さんですか。","romaji":"Hyotto shite, Tanaka-san no imōto-san desu ka.","german":"Might you be Mr. Tanaka's younger sister?","furigana":"たなか|いもうと"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"i-adjective: 平たい皿 (flat plate). Figuratively: 平たく言えば (put simply).","examples":[{"japanese":"平たい皿に料理を盛りつけた。","romaji":"Hiratai sara ni ryōri o moritsuketa.","german":"I arranged the food on a flat plate.","furigana":"ひら|さら|りょうり|も"},{"japanese":"平たく言えば、この計画は失敗だったということだ。","romaji":"Hirataku ieba, kono keikaku wa shippai datta to iu koto da.","german":"Put simply, this plan was a failure.","furigana":"ひら|い|けいかく|しっぱい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Colloquial: びりになる (to come in last). Neutral: 最下位.","examples":[{"japanese":"運動会のかけっこで、びりになってしまった。","romaji":"Undōkai no kakekko de, biri ni natte shimatta.","german":"I came in last in the race at the sports day.","furigana":"うんどうかい"},{"japanese":"彼は成績がクラスでびりだったが、今は医者として働いている。","romaji":"Kare wa seiseki ga kurasu de biri datta ga, ima wa isha to shite hataraite iru.","german":"He ranked last in his class, but now works as a doctor.","furigana":"かれ|せいせき|いま|いしゃ|はたら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Often 微量の〜: 微量の放射性物質 (a trace of radioactive material), 微量の毒 (a trace of poison). Opposite: 大量.","examples":[{"japanese":"水道水から微量の鉛が検出された。","romaji":"Suidōsui kara biryō no namari ga kenshutsu sareta.","german":"A trace amount of lead was detected in the tap water.","furigana":"すいどうすい|びりょう|なまり|けんしゅつ"},{"japanese":"この薬は、微量でも強い効き目がある。","romaji":"Kono kusuri wa, biryō demo tsuyoi kikime ga aru.","german":"This medicine is strongly effective even in tiny amounts.","furigana":"くすり|びりょう|つよ|き|め"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Casual (飯 = meal, rough); neutral: 昼ご飯, お昼.","examples":[{"japanese":"そろそろ昼飯にしようか。","romaji":"Sorosoro hirumeshi ni shiyō ka.","german":"Shall we have lunch soon?","furigana":"ひるめし"},{"japanese":"忙しくて、昼飯を食べる時間もなかった。","romaji":"Isogashikute, hirumeshi o taberu jikan mo nakatta.","german":"I was so busy that I didn't even have time for lunch.","furigana":"いそが|ひるめし|た|じかん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Intransitive; transitive: 広める. うわさが広まる (a rumor spreads). For spatial expansion, 広がる is more common.","examples":[{"japanese":"そのうわさは、あっという間に学校中に広まった。","romaji":"Sono uwasa wa, atto iu ma ni gakkōchū ni hiromatta.","german":"The rumor spread throughout the whole school in no time.","furigana":"ま|がっこうちゅう|ひろ"},{"japanese":"インターネットのおかげで、この言葉は若者の間に広まった。","romaji":"Intānetto no okage de, kono kotoba wa wakamono no aida ni hiromatta.","german":"Thanks to the internet, this word has spread among young people.","furigana":"ことば|わかもの|あいだ|ひろ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"na-adjective: 貧弱な体 (frail body), 貧弱な知識 (meager knowledge).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は子どもの頃、体が貧弱だったので、水泳を始めた。","romaji":"Kare wa kodomo no koro, karada ga hinjaku datta node, suiei o hajimeta.","german":"As a child he was frail, so he started swimming.","furigana":"かれ|こ|ころ|からだ|ひんじゃく|すいえい|はじ"},{"japanese":"この報告書は、データが貧弱で説得力がない。","romaji":"Kono hōkokusho wa, dēta ga hinjaku de settokuryoku ga nai.","german":"This report is based on poor data and is not convincing.","furigana":"ほうこくしょ|ひんじゃく|せっとくりょく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"For plants and animals: 米の品種 (rice variety), 品種改良 (breeding of new varieties).","examples":[{"japanese":"コシヒカリは、日本で最も多く作られている米の品種だ。","romaji":"Koshihikari wa, Nihon de mottomo ōku tsukurarete iru kome no hinshu da.","german":"Koshihikari is the most widely grown rice variety in Japan.","furigana":"にほん|もっと|おお|つく|こめ|ひんしゅ"},{"japanese":"この農家では、いろいろな品種のリンゴを育てている。","romaji":"Kono nōka de wa, iroiro na hinshu no ringo o sodatete iru.","german":"This farm grows many different varieties of apples.","furigana":"のうか|ひんしゅ|そだ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"As a cheer: ファイト！ (Let's go!, You can do it!). ファイトがある = to have fighting spirit. Also \"fight\" in boxing.","examples":[{"japanese":"最後まであきらめないファイトが、勝利につながった。","romaji":"Saigo made akiramenai faito ga, shōri ni tsunagatta.","german":"The fighting spirit of never giving up until the end led to victory.","furigana":"さいご|しょうり"},{"japanese":"試験前の友達に「ファイト！」と声をかけた。","romaji":"Shiken mae no tomodachi ni \"faito!\" to koe o kaketa.","german":"I called out \"Fight!\" to my friend before her exam.","furigana":"しけんまえ|ともだち|こえ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 33 (source: N4; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"Mostly 不意に (suddenly) or 不意を突く / 不意を打つ (to take by surprise): 不意を突かれる = to be taken by surprise.","examples":[{"japanese":"後ろから不意に名前を呼ばれて、びっくりした。","romaji":"Ushiro kara fui ni namae o yobarete, bikkuri shita.","german":"I was startled when someone suddenly called my name from behind.","furigana":"うし|ふい|なまえ|よ"},{"japanese":"相手チームは、こちらの不意を突いて攻めてきた。","romaji":"Aite chīmu wa, kochira no fui o tsuite semete kita.","german":"The opposing team attacked, catching us off guard.","furigana":"あいて|ふい|つ|せ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Coffee, air, or camera filter; also image filters in apps: フィルターをかける (to apply a filter).","examples":[{"japanese":"コーヒーのフィルターが切れていたので、買いに行った。","romaji":"Kōhī no firutā ga kirete ita node, kai ni itta.","german":"We ran out of coffee filters, so I went to buy some.","furigana":"き|か|い"},{"japanese":"写真にフィルターをかけると、雰囲気が変わる。","romaji":"Shashin ni firutā o kakeru to, fun'iki ga kawaru.","german":"Applying a filter to a photo changes its mood.","furigana":"しゃしん|ふんいき|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"封をする (to seal), 封を切る / 封を開ける (to open, break the seal). Envelope: 封筒.","examples":[{"japanese":"手紙を書き終えて、封筒に封をした。","romaji":"Tegami o kakioete, fūtō ni fū o shita.","german":"After finishing the letter, I sealed the envelope.","furigana":"てがみ|か|お|ふうとう|ふう"},{"japanese":"封を切っていない商品なら、返品できます。","romaji":"Fū o kitte inai shōhin nara, henpin dekimasu.","german":"Unopened goods can be returned.","furigana":"ふう|き|しょうひん|へんぴん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"E.g. 江戸時代の風俗 (way of life in the Edo period), 風俗画 (genre painting). Note: on its own, 風俗 in present-day everyday use is often a euphemism for the sex trade; for customs, 風習 is also used neutrally.","examples":[{"japanese":"この絵には、江戸時代の庶民の風俗が描かれている。","romaji":"Kono e ni wa, Edo jidai no shomin no fūzoku ga egakarete iru.","german":"This picture depicts the way of life of the common people in the Edo period.","furigana":"え|えどじだい|しょみん|ふうぞく|えが"},{"japanese":"服装や言葉づかいには、その時代の風俗が表れる。","romaji":"Fukusō ya kotobazukai ni wa, sono jidai no fūzoku ga arawareru.","german":"The customs of an era show in its clothing and speech.","furigana":"ふくそう|ことば|じだい|ふうぞく|あらわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Climate, soil, and landscape as living conditions: 日本の風土に合った家 (a house suited to Japan's climate). Figuratively also 企業風土 (corporate culture).","examples":[{"japanese":"この地方の風土は、米作りに適している。","romaji":"Kono chihō no fūdo wa, komezukuri ni tekishite iru.","german":"The natural conditions of this region are well suited to rice cultivation.","furigana":"ちほう|ふうど|こめづく|てき"},{"japanese":"厳しい風土の中で、独自の文化が育った。","romaji":"Kibishii fūdo no naka de, dokuji no bunka ga sodatta.","german":"A unique culture developed under the harsh natural conditions.","furigana":"きび|ふうど|なか|どくじ|ぶんか|そだ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mainly body posture and technique in sports: 投球フォーム (pitching form). In IT: 入力フォーム (input form). Same spelling: フォーム (foam, e.g. シェービングフォーム).","examples":[{"japanese":"コーチにバッティングのフォームを直してもらった。","romaji":"Kōchi ni battingu no fōmu o naoshite moratta.","german":"The coach corrected my batting form.","furigana":"なお"},{"japanese":"お問い合わせは、ホームページのフォームからお送りください。","romaji":"Otoiawase wa, hōmupēji no fōmu kara ookuri kudasai.","german":"Please send inquiries via the form on our website.","furigana":"と|あ|おく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mainly in fixed phrases: 鬼は外、福は内 (the call when throwing beans at 節分), 笑う門には福来たる (good fortune comes to the house that laughs). General: 幸せ, 幸運.","examples":[{"japanese":"節分には「鬼は外、福は内」と言いながら豆をまく。","romaji":"Setsubun ni wa \"Oni wa soto, fuku wa uchi\" to iinagara mame o maku.","german":"At Setsubun, people scatter beans while saying \"Demons out, good fortune in!\"","furigana":"せつぶん|おに|そと|ふく|うち|い|まめ"},{"japanese":"笑う門には福来たると言うから、いつも笑顔でいよう。","romaji":"Warau kado ni wa fuku kitaru to iu kara, itsumo egao de iyō.","german":"They say good fortune comes to those who laugh, so let's always smile.","furigana":"わら|かど|ふくき|い|えがお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Often as a first element: 複合語 (compound word), 複合施設 (multi-use complex). 複合する = to combine, to merge.","examples":[{"japanese":"いくつかの原因が複合して、事故が起きた。","romaji":"Ikutsuka no gen'in ga fukugō shite, jiko ga okita.","german":"Several causes combined, and the accident occurred.","furigana":"げんいん|ふくごう|じこ|お"},{"japanese":"駅前に、映画館や店が入った複合施設ができた。","romaji":"Ekimae ni, eigakan ya mise ga haitta fukugō shisetsu ga dekita.","german":"A complex housing a cinema and shops has been built in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|えいがかん|みせ|はい|ふくごうしせつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Especially in appearance: 年の割に老けて見える (to look older than one's age). Same sound: 更ける (night: to grow late), 耽る (to indulge).","examples":[{"japanese":"父はこの数年でずいぶん老けた。","romaji":"Chichi wa kono sūnen de zuibun fuketa.","german":"My father has aged a lot in the past few years.","furigana":"ちち|すうねん|ふ"},{"japanese":"彼は年の割に老けて見える。","romaji":"Kare wa toshi no wari ni fukete mieru.","german":"He looks older than his age.","furigana":"かれ|とし|わり|ふ|み"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mostly 〜に耽る, often in kana ふける: 読書に耽る (to be absorbed in reading), 物思いに耽る (to be lost in thought). Also slightly negative: 遊びに耽る (to indulge in pleasures).","examples":[{"japanese":"休みの日は、一日中読書に耽っている。","romaji":"Yasumi no hi wa, ichinichijū dokusho ni fukette iru.","german":"On days off, I spend the whole day absorbed in reading.","furigana":"やす|ひ|いちにちじゅうどくしょ|ふけ"},{"japanese":"彼女は窓の外を見ながら、物思いに耽っていた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa mado no soto o minagara, monoomoi ni fukette ita.","german":"She was lost in thought, looking out the window.","furigana":"かのじょ|まど|そと|み|ものおも|ふけ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"大富豪 (multimillionaire). More everyday: 金持ち. Similar sound: 符号 (sign, code).","examples":[{"japanese":"その富豪は、財産の多くを慈善団体に寄付した。","romaji":"Sono fugō wa, zaisan no ōku o jizen dantai ni kifu shita.","german":"The wealthy man donated much of his fortune to charities.","furigana":"ふごう|ざいさん|おお|じぜんだんたい|きふ"},{"japanese":"彼は事業に成功して、一代で富豪になった。","romaji":"Kare wa jigyō ni seikō shite, ichidai de fugō ni natta.","german":"He succeeded in business and became rich within a single generation.","furigana":"かれ|じぎょう|せいこう|いちだい|ふごう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"ブザーが鳴る (the buzzer sounds). 防犯ブザー = personal safety alarm that many Japanese schoolchildren carry.","examples":[{"japanese":"試験終了のブザーが鳴った。","romaji":"Shiken shūryō no buzā ga natta.","german":"The buzzer for the end of the exam sounded.","furigana":"しけんしゅうりょう|な"},{"japanese":"小学生はランドセルに防犯ブザーを付けている。","romaji":"Shōgakusei wa randoseru ni bōhan buzā o tsukete iru.","german":"Elementary school children have a security alarm attached to their school backpacks.","furigana":"しょうがくせい|ぼうはん|つ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Formal: 不在にしております (is currently away). 不在通知 / 不在票 = notice card left when a delivery is made in one's absence.","examples":[{"japanese":"担当者はただいま不在にしております。","romaji":"Tantōsha wa tadaima fuzai ni shite orimasu.","german":"The person in charge is currently away.","furigana":"たんとうしゃ|ふざい"},{"japanese":"不在の間に、荷物が届いていた。","romaji":"Fuzai no aida ni, nimotsu ga todoite ita.","german":"A package arrived while I was out.","furigana":"ふざい|あいだ|にもつ|とど"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mainly 天候不順 (unsettled weather) and 生理不順 (irregular periods). Similar sound: 不純 (impure).","examples":[{"japanese":"今年の夏は天候が不順で、野菜の値段が上がった。","romaji":"Kotoshi no natsu wa tenkō ga fujun de, yasai no nedan ga agatta.","german":"The weather was unsettled this summer, and vegetable prices rose.","furigana":"ことし|なつ|てんこう|ふじゅん|やさい|ねだん|あ"},{"japanese":"季節の変わり目は、天候が不順になりやすい。","romaji":"Kisetsu no kawarime wa, tenkō ga fujun ni nariyasui.","german":"The weather tends to be unsettled at the change of seasons.","furigana":"きせつ|か|め|てんこう|ふじゅん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Formal, mainly in news: 負傷する (to be injured), 負傷者 (injured person). Everyday: けが.","examples":[{"japanese":"この事故で、三人が負傷した。","romaji":"Kono jiko de, sannin ga fushō shita.","german":"Three people were injured in this accident.","furigana":"じこ|さんにん|ふしょう"},{"japanese":"彼は試合中に足を負傷して、途中で退場した。","romaji":"Kare wa shiaichū ni ashi o fushō shite, tochū de taijō shita.","german":"He injured his leg during the match and left the field midway.","furigana":"かれ|しあいちゅう|あし|ふしょう|とちゅう|たいじょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"体の不調 (physical ailments), エンジンの不調 (engine trouble), 交渉が不調に終わる (negotiations fail). Opposite: 好調.","examples":[{"japanese":"エンジンの不調で、飛行機の出発が遅れた。","romaji":"Enjin no fuchō de, hikōki no shuppatsu ga okureta.","german":"The departure of the plane was delayed due to engine trouble.","furigana":"ふちょう|ひこうき|しゅっぱつ|おく"},{"japanese":"あの選手は、今シーズンずっと不調が続いている。","romaji":"Ano senshu wa, kon shīzun zutto fuchō ga tsuzuite iru.","german":"That player has been in a slump the whole season.","furigana":"せんしゅ|こん|ふちょう|つづ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"ロングブーツ (long boots), レインブーツ (rain boots). Rubber boots are also called 長靴.","examples":[{"japanese":"雪の日は、滑りにくいブーツを履いて出かける。","romaji":"Yuki no hi wa, suberinikui būtsu o haite dekakeru.","german":"On snowy days I go out in boots that don't slip easily.","furigana":"ゆき|ひ|すべ|は|で"},{"japanese":"玄関でブーツを脱ぐのに時間がかかった。","romaji":"Genkan de būtsu o nugu no ni jikan ga kakatta.","german":"It took a while to take off my boots in the entryway.","furigana":"げんかん|ぬ|じかん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"復活する = to come back to life, to return: 古い祭りが復活する (an old festival is revived). Religious: キリストの復活 (the resurrection of Christ).","examples":[{"japanese":"五十年ぶりに、地元の伝統的な祭りが復活した。","romaji":"Gojū nen buri ni, jimoto no dentōteki na matsuri ga fukkatsu shita.","german":"After fifty years, the town's traditional festival was revived.","furigana":"ごじゅうねん|じもと|でんとうてき|まつ|ふっかつ"},{"japanese":"けがから復活した選手が、見事に優勝した。","romaji":"Kega kara fukkatsu shita senshu ga, migoto ni yūshō shita.","german":"The athlete who came back from an injury won a brilliant victory.","furigana":"ふっかつ|せんしゅ|みごと|ゆうしょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Almost only in 物議を醸す (to cause a stir, to spark controversy).","examples":[{"japanese":"大臣の発言が物議を醸している。","romaji":"Daijin no hatsugen ga butsugi o kamoshite iru.","german":"The minister's remarks are causing controversy.","furigana":"だいじん|はつげん|ぶつぎ|かも"},{"japanese":"その映画は、暴力的な場面が多いことで物議を醸した。","romaji":"Sono eiga wa, bōryokuteki na bamen ga ōi koto de butsugi o kamoshita.","german":"The film caused controversy because of its many violent scenes.","furigana":"えいが|ぼうりょくてき|ばめん|おお|ぶつぎ|かも"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Neutral and factual word for an object, especially in physics and reports: 未確認飛行物体 (UFO). Everyday: 物.","examples":[{"japanese":"空に光る物体が見えたという通報があった。","romaji":"Sora ni hikaru buttai ga mieta to iu tsūhō ga atta.","german":"There was a report that someone had seen a glowing object in the sky.","furigana":"そら|ひか|ぶったい|み|つうほう"},{"japanese":"多くの物体は、温めると膨張する。","romaji":"Ōku no buttai wa, atatameru to bōchō suru.","german":"Most objects expand when heated.","furigana":"おお|ぶったい|あたた|ぼうちょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"na-adjective: 不当な扱い (unfair treatment), 不当解雇 (unjustified dismissal). Opposite: 正当.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は会社から不当な扱いを受けたと訴えた。","romaji":"Kare wa kaisha kara futō na atsukai o uketa to uttaeta.","german":"He complained that the company had treated him unfairly.","furigana":"かれ|かいしゃ|ふとう|あつか|う|うった"},{"japanese":"この値段は、品質に比べて不当に高い。","romaji":"Kono nedan wa, hinshitsu ni kurabete futō ni takai.","german":"This price is unreasonably high compared to the quality.","furigana":"ねだん|ひんしつ|くら|ふとう|たか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The choice where you can't go wrong: 無難な色 (an unobtrusive color). 〜ほうが無難だ = it is safer to ...","examples":[{"japanese":"初めての訪問なら、手土産はお菓子が無難だ。","romaji":"Hajimete no hōmon nara, temiyage wa okashi ga bunan da.","german":"For a first visit, sweets are a safe choice as a gift.","furigana":"はじ|ほうもん|てみやげ|かし|ぶなん"},{"japanese":"彼はどんな仕事も無難にこなす。","romaji":"Kare wa donna shigoto mo bunan ni konasu.","german":"He handles any job competently without mistakes.","furigana":"かれ|しごと|ぶなん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"不評を買う (to meet with disapproval), 〜は不評だ (is poorly received). Opposite: 好評.","examples":[{"japanese":"新しいデザインは、客の間で不評だった。","romaji":"Atarashii dezain wa, kyaku no aida de fuhyō datta.","german":"The new design was unpopular among customers.","furigana":"あたら|きゃく|あいだ|ふひょう"},{"japanese":"値上げは利用者の不評を買った。","romaji":"Neage wa riyōsha no fuhyō o katta.","german":"The price increase met with disapproval from users.","furigana":"ねあ|りようしゃ|ふひょう|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"不服そうな顔 (dissatisfied look). Legal: 判決を不服として控訴する (to appeal against a verdict).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は不服そうな顔で、先生の話を聞いていた。","romaji":"Kare wa fufukusō na kao de, sensei no hanashi o kiite ita.","german":"He listened to the teacher with a dissatisfied look.","furigana":"かれ|ふふく|かお|せんせい|はなし|き"},{"japanese":"被告は判決を不服として、控訴した。","romaji":"Hikoku wa hanketsu o fufuku to shite, kōso shita.","german":"The defendant did not accept the verdict and filed an appeal.","furigana":"ひこく|はんけつ|ふふく|こうそ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"〜ブーム: 健康ブーム (health craze), ブームになる (to become popular), ブームが去る (the craze is over).","examples":[{"japanese":"今、キャンプがちょっとしたブームになっている。","romaji":"Ima, kyanpu ga chotto shita būmu ni natte iru.","german":"Camping is quite a boom right now.","furigana":"いま"},{"japanese":"ブームが去ると、その店にはほとんど客が来なくなった。","romaji":"Būmu ga saru to, sono mise ni wa hotondo kyaku ga konaku natta.","german":"Once the boom passed, hardly any customers came to the shop.","furigana":"さ|みせ|きゃく|こ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mainly 振り出しに戻る (to start again from zero; like \"back to start\" in a board game). In banking also: issuance (of a bill).","examples":[{"japanese":"交渉は決裂し、話は振り出しに戻った。","romaji":"Kōshō wa ketsuretsu shi, hanashi wa furidashi ni modotta.","german":"The negotiations broke down, and we were back to square one.","furigana":"こうしょう|けつれつ|はなし|ふ|だ|もど"},{"japanese":"すごろくで、「振り出しに戻る」のマスに止まってしまった。","romaji":"Sugoroku de, \"furidashi ni modoru\" no masu ni tomatte shimatta.","german":"In sugoroku, I landed on the \"Back to start\" square.","furigana":"ふ|だ|もど|と"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Physics: 浮力が働く (buoyancy acts). Related: 浮く (to float).","examples":[{"japanese":"海水は真水より浮力が大きいので、体が浮きやすい。","romaji":"Kaisui wa mamizu yori furyoku ga ōkii node, karada ga ukiyasui.","german":"Seawater has more buoyancy than fresh water, so the body floats more easily.","furigana":"かいすい|まみず|ふりょく|おお|からだ|う"},{"japanese":"船は浮力によって水に浮いている。","romaji":"Fune wa furyoku ni yotte mizu ni uite iru.","german":"A ship floats on water thanks to buoyancy.","furigana":"ふね|ふりょく|みず|う"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"武力衝突 (armed clash), 武力行使 (use of armed force).","examples":[{"japanese":"両国は武力ではなく、話し合いで問題を解決すべきだ。","romaji":"Ryōkoku wa buryoku de wa naku, hanashiai de mondai o kaiketsu subeki da.","german":"The two countries should solve the problem through talks, not by force of arms.","furigana":"りょうこく|ぶりょく|はな|あ|もんだい|かいけつ"},{"japanese":"国境付近で武力衝突が起きた。","romaji":"Kokkyō fukin de buryoku shōtotsu ga okita.","german":"An armed clash occurred near the border.","furigana":"こっきょうふきん|ぶりょくしょうとつ|お"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Color: ライトブルー (light blue), ネイビーブルー (navy blue). Colloquial: ブルーな気分 (a gloomy mood).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女はブルーのワンピースを着ていた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa burū no wanpīsu o kite ita.","german":"She was wearing a blue dress.","furigana":"かのじょ|き"},{"japanese":"月曜日の朝は、なんとなくブルーな気分になる。","romaji":"Getsuyōbi no asa wa, nantonaku burū na kibun ni naru.","german":"On Monday mornings I somehow feel blue.","furigana":"げつようび|あさ|きぶん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive: 声を震わせる (to speak with a trembling voice), 肩を震わせて泣く (to cry with shaking shoulders). Intransitive: 震える.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は肩を震わせて泣いていた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kata o furuwasete naite ita.","german":"She was crying with her shoulders shaking.","furigana":"かのじょ|かた|ふる|な"},{"japanese":"彼は怒りに声を震わせながら抗議した。","romaji":"Kare wa ikari ni koe o furuwase nagara kōgi shita.","german":"He protested, his voice trembling with anger.","furigana":"かれ|いか|こえ|ふる|こうぎ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"無礼な態度 (insolent behavior); stronger than 失礼. Polite: 先日はご無礼しました. 無礼講 = a party where differences in rank don't matter.","examples":[{"japanese":"客に対して、無礼な態度をとってはいけない。","romaji":"Kyaku ni taishite, burei na taido o totte wa ikenai.","german":"You must not behave rudely toward customers.","furigana":"きゃく|たい|ぶれい|たいど"},{"japanese":"先日のご無礼をお許しください。","romaji":"Senjitsu no goburei o oyurushi kudasai.","german":"Please forgive my rudeness the other day.","furigana":"せんじつ|ぶれい|ゆる"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A supplement to magazines (e.g. small gifts in children's magazines) or an appendix of a book: 巻末付録.","examples":[{"japanese":"子どもの頃は、雑誌の付録が楽しみだった。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro wa, zasshi no furoku ga tanoshimi datta.","german":"As a child I always looked forward to the freebies that came with magazines.","furigana":"こ|ころ|ざっし|ふろく|たの"},{"japanese":"詳しいデータは、本の付録に載っている。","romaji":"Kuwashii dēta wa, hon no furoku ni notte iru.","german":"The detailed data is in the book's appendix.","furigana":"くわ|ほん|ふろく|の"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Legally protected cultural properties: 重要文化財, 無形文化財 (intangible cultural property). World Heritage: 世界遺産.","examples":[{"japanese":"火事で、貴重な文化財が失われた。","romaji":"Kaji de, kichō na bunkazai ga ushinawareta.","german":"Valuable cultural properties were lost in the fire.","furigana":"かじ|きちょう|ぶんかざい|うしな"},{"japanese":"この寺には、国の重要文化財が多く残されている。","romaji":"Kono tera ni wa, kuni no jūyō bunkazai ga ōku nokosarete iru.","german":"Many nationally designated Important Cultural Properties remain in this temple.","furigana":"てら|くに|じゅうようぶんかざい|おお|のこ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"分業する (to divide the work), 分業制 (division-of-labor system). Related: 国際分業 (international division of labor).","examples":[{"japanese":"この工場では、細かく分業して製品を作っている。","romaji":"Kono kōjō de wa, komakaku bungyō shite seihin o tsukutte iru.","german":"This factory makes products with a finely divided labor system.","furigana":"こうじょう|こま|ぶんぎょう|せいひん|つく"},{"japanese":"我が家では、家事を夫婦で分業している。","romaji":"Wagaya de wa, kaji o fūfu de bungyō shite iru.","german":"In our household, my spouse and I divide up the housework.","furigana":"わ|や|かじ|ふうふ|ぶんぎょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mostly adverbial as ふんだんに: 材料をふんだんに使う (to use ingredients lavishly).","examples":[{"japanese":"この料理は、旬の野菜をふんだんに使っている。","romaji":"Kono ryōri wa, shun no yasai o fundan ni tsukatte iru.","german":"This dish uses plenty of seasonal vegetables.","furigana":"りょうり|しゅん|やさい|つか"},{"japanese":"窓が大きく、部屋には光がふんだんに入る。","romaji":"Mado ga ōkiku, heya ni wa hikari ga fundan ni hairu.","german":"The windows are large, and plenty of light enters the room.","furigana":"まど|おお|へや|ひかり|はい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"奮闘する (to exert oneself to the utmost), 孤軍奮闘 (to fight all alone).","examples":[{"japanese":"慣れない育児に、毎日奮闘している。","romaji":"Narenai ikuji ni, mainichi funtō shite iru.","german":"I struggle every day with the unfamiliar task of raising a child.","furigana":"な|いくじ|まいにちふんとう"},{"japanese":"選手たちの奮闘もむなしく、チームは負けてしまった。","romaji":"Senshutachi no funtō mo munashiku, chīmu wa makete shimatta.","german":"Despite the players' strenuous efforts, the team lost.","furigana":"せんしゅ|ふんとう|ま"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"分配する (to distribute, to divide): 利益を分配する. Related: 配分, 再分配.","examples":[{"japanese":"利益は、出資額に応じて分配される。","romaji":"Rieki wa, shusshigaku ni ōjite bunpai sareru.","german":"Profits are distributed according to the amount invested.","furigana":"りえき|しゅっしがく|おう|ぶんぱい"},{"japanese":"遺産の分配をめぐって、兄弟が争った。","romaji":"Isan no bunpai o megutte, kyōdai ga arasotta.","german":"The siblings fought over the division of the inheritance.","furigana":"いさん|ぶんぱい|きょうだい|あらそ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mathematics; opposite: 分子 (numerator). Fraction: 分数.","examples":[{"japanese":"分数の足し算では、まず分母をそろえる。","romaji":"Bunsū no tashizan de wa, mazu bunbo o soroeru.","german":"When adding fractions, first make the denominators the same.","furigana":"ぶんすう|た|ざん|ぶんぼ"},{"japanese":"四分の三の分母は四だ。","romaji":"Yon bun no san no bunbo wa yon da.","german":"The denominator of three quarters is four.","furigana":"よんぶん|さん|ぶんぼ|よん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"粉末状 (powdery), 粉末ジュース (powdered drink mix). More everyday: 粉.","examples":[{"japanese":"この薬は粉末なので、水に溶かして飲んでください。","romaji":"Kono kusuri wa funmatsu na node, mizu ni tokashite nonde kudasai.","german":"This medicine is a powder; please dissolve it in water before drinking.","furigana":"くすり|ふんまつ|みず|と|の"},{"japanese":"緑茶を粉末にして、お菓子に混ぜた。","romaji":"Ryokucha o funmatsu ni shite, okashi ni mazeta.","german":"I ground green tea into powder and mixed it into the sweets.","furigana":"りょくちゃ|ふんまつ|かし|ま"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"ペアを組む (to form a pair), ペアのグラス (a matching pair of glasses). Same sound, rare: ペア (pear).","examples":[{"japanese":"授業では、二人でペアを組んで会話の練習をした。","romaji":"Jugyō de wa, futari de pea o kunde kaiwa no renshū o shita.","german":"In class, we formed pairs and practiced conversation.","furigana":"じゅぎょう|ふたり|く|かいわ|れんしゅう"},{"japanese":"結婚祝いに、ペアのグラスを贈った。","romaji":"Kekkon iwai ni, pea no gurasu o okutta.","german":"We gave a pair of glasses as a wedding gift.","furigana":"けっこんいわ|おく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mostly 〜に閉口する: 暑さに閉口する (to groan under the heat). Literally \"to close one's mouth\", i.e. to be left speechless by annoyance. Similar sound: 平行, 並行.","examples":[{"japanese":"毎日続く暑さには閉口している。","romaji":"Mainichi tsuzuku atsusa ni wa heikō shite iru.","german":"I'm fed up with the heat that goes on every day.","furigana":"まいにちつづ|あつ|へいこう"},{"japanese":"隣の家の騒音に閉口して、引っ越しを考えている。","romaji":"Tonari no ie no sōon ni heikō shite, hikkoshi o kangaete iru.","german":"I'm so tired of the noise from the house next door that I'm thinking of moving.","furigana":"となり|いえ|そうおん|へいこう|ひ|こ|かんが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Area units: 平方メートル (square meter), 平方キロメートル (square kilometer). Mathematics: 平方根 (square root).","examples":[{"japanese":"この部屋の広さは二十平方メートルだ。","romaji":"Kono heya no hirosa wa nijū heihō mētoru da.","german":"This room is twenty square meters.","furigana":"へや|ひろ|にじゅうへいほう"},{"japanese":"五の平方は二十五である。","romaji":"Go no heihō wa nijūgo de aru.","german":"The square of five is twenty-five.","furigana":"ご|へいほう|にじゅうご"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"〜をベースにする (to build on ..., to base on ...). Same sound: ベース (bass, bass guitar). In baseball: base.","examples":[{"japanese":"この映画は、実際の事件をベースにしている。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa, jissai no jiken o bēsu ni shite iru.","german":"This movie is based on a real incident.","furigana":"えいが|じっさい|じけん"},{"japanese":"ふもとの村をベースにして、毎日山に登った。","romaji":"Fumoto no mura o bēsu ni shite, mainichi yama ni nobotta.","german":"Using the village at the foot of the mountain as our base, we climbed the mountain every day.","furigana":"むら|まいにちやま|のぼ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"ベストを尽くす (to do one's best), 自己ベスト (personal best). Same sound: ベスト (vest).","examples":[{"japanese":"結果はどうであれ、ベストを尽くしたい。","romaji":"Kekka wa dō de are, besuto o tsukushitai.","german":"Whatever the result, I want to do my best.","furigana":"けっか|つ"},{"japanese":"今の状況では、これがベストな選択だと思う。","romaji":"Ima no jōkyō de wa, kore ga besuto na sentaku da to omou.","german":"In the current situation, I think this is the best choice.","furigana":"いま|じょうきょう|せんたく|おも"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"ベストセラーになる (to become a bestseller). A long-running bestseller: ロングセラー.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女の最初の小説は、ベストセラーになった。","romaji":"Kanojo no saisho no shōsetsu wa, besutoserā ni natta.","german":"Her first novel became a bestseller.","furigana":"かのじょ|さいしょ|しょうせつ"},{"japanese":"駅の本屋には、今月のベストセラーが並んでいる。","romaji":"Eki no hon'ya ni wa, kongetsu no besutoserā ga narande iru.","german":"This month's bestsellers are lined up in the bookstore at the station.","furigana":"えき|ほんや|こんげつ|なら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Usually in kana. The basic idea of humble language (謙譲語): one lowers oneself to honor the other person.","examples":[{"japanese":"謙譲語は、自分がへりくだって相手を立てる表現だ。","romaji":"Kenjōgo wa, jibun ga herikudatte aite o tateru hyōgen da.","german":"Humble language is a form of expression in which one humbles oneself to elevate the other person.","furigana":"けんじょうご|じぶん|あいて|た|ひょうげん"},{"japanese":"彼は誰に対しても、へりくだった態度で接する。","romaji":"Kare wa dare ni taishite mo, herikudatta taido de sessuru.","german":"He treats everyone with a humble attitude.","furigana":"かれ|だれ|たい|たいど|せっ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Written style, mostly 〜を経て: 長い年月を経て (after many years), 大阪を経て (via Osaka), 審査を経て (after screening). Same sound: 減る (to decrease).","examples":[{"japanese":"十年の歳月を経て、橋がようやく完成した。","romaji":"Jūnen no saigetsu o hete, hashi ga yōyaku kansei shita.","german":"After ten years, the bridge was finally completed.","furigana":"じゅうねん|さいげつ|へ|はし|かんせい"},{"japanese":"この計画は、何度もの話し合いを経て決まった。","romaji":"Kono keikaku wa, nando mo no hanashiai o hete kimatta.","german":"This plan was decided after many discussions.","furigana":"けいかく|なんど|はな|あ|へ|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"弁解する (to justify oneself), 弁解の余地がない (inexcusable). More everyday: 言い訳.","examples":[{"japanese":"遅刻したことについて、彼は何も弁解しなかった。","romaji":"Chikoku shita koto ni tsuite, kare wa nani mo benkai shinakatta.","german":"He made no excuses for being late.","furigana":"ちこく|かれ|なに|べんかい"},{"japanese":"今回のミスには、弁解の余地がない。","romaji":"Konkai no misu ni wa, benkai no yochi ga nai.","german":"There is no excuse for this mistake.","furigana":"こんかい|べんかい|よち"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"便宜を図る (to accommodate someone, to provide favors), 便宜上 (for convenience's sake).","examples":[{"japanese":"市は、高齢者のためにさまざまな便宜を図っている。","romaji":"Shi wa, kōreisha no tame ni samazama na bengi o hakatte iru.","german":"The city makes various accommodations for the elderly.","furigana":"し|こうれいしゃ|べんぎ|はか"},{"japanese":"便宜上、参加者を三つのグループに分けた。","romaji":"Bengijō, sankasha o mittsu no gurūpu ni waketa.","german":"For convenience, we divided the participants into three groups.","furigana":"べんぎじょう|さんかしゃ|みっ|わ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"稲の穂 (ear of rice), 穂が出る (ears form). Tip of a brush or spear: 穂先.","examples":[{"japanese":"秋になると、田んぼの稲の穂が黄金色に実る。","romaji":"Aki ni naru to, tanbo no ine no ho ga koganeiro ni minoru.","german":"In autumn, the rice ears in the paddies ripen to a golden color.","furigana":"あき|た|いね|ほ|こがねいろ|みの"},{"japanese":"風に揺れるススキの穂が美しい。","romaji":"Kaze ni yureru susuki no ho ga utsukushii.","german":"The plumes of Chinese silver grass swaying in the wind are beautiful.","furigana":"かぜ|ゆ|ほ|うつく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"ボイコットする (to boycott): 授業をボイコットする, 大会をボイコットする.","examples":[{"japanese":"数か国が、その大会をボイコットした。","romaji":"Sūkakoku ga, sono taikai o boikotto shita.","german":"Several countries boycotted the tournament.","furigana":"すう|こく|たいかい"},{"japanese":"学生たちは、学費の値上げに抗議して授業をボイコットした。","romaji":"Gakuseitachi wa, gakuhi no neage ni kōgi shite jugyō o boikotto shita.","german":"The students boycotted classes in protest against the tuition increase.","furigana":"がくせい|がくひ|ねあ|こうぎ|じゅぎょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"防衛する (to defend), 防衛費 (defense spending), 正当防衛 (self-defense). In sports: タイトルを防衛する (to defend a title).","examples":[{"japanese":"国の防衛について、国会で議論が続いている。","romaji":"Kuni no bōei ni tsuite, kokkai de giron ga tsuzuite iru.","german":"Debate continues in the Diet about the nation's defense.","furigana":"くに|ぼうえい|こっかい|ぎろん|つづ"},{"japanese":"チャンピオンは、三度目のタイトル防衛に成功した。","romaji":"Chanpion wa, sandome no taitoru bōei ni seikō shita.","german":"The champion successfully defended his title for the third time.","furigana":"さんどめ|ぼうえい|せいこう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"法学部 (faculty of law), 法学を学ぶ (to study law). Similar sound: 方角 (direction), 邦楽 (Japanese music).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は大学で法学を学び、弁護士になった。","romaji":"Kare wa daigaku de hōgaku o manabi, bengoshi ni natta.","german":"He studied law at university and became a lawyer.","furigana":"かれ|だいがく|ほうがく|まな|べんごし"},{"japanese":"法学の知識は、ビジネスの場でも役に立つ。","romaji":"Hōgaku no chishiki wa, bijinesu no ba demo yaku ni tatsu.","german":"Knowledge of law is also useful in business.","furigana":"ほうがく|ちしき|ば|やく|た"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Opposite: 凶作 / 不作 (crop failure). 豊作を祈る (to pray for a good harvest).","examples":[{"japanese":"今年は天候に恵まれて、米が豊作だった。","romaji":"Kotoshi wa tenkō ni megumarete, kome ga hōsaku datta.","german":"This year, with favorable weather, there was a bumper rice harvest.","furigana":"ことし|てんこう|めぐ|こめ|ほうさく"},{"japanese":"村の人々は、神社で豊作を祈った。","romaji":"Mura no hitobito wa, jinja de hōsaku o inotta.","german":"The villagers prayed for a good harvest at the shrine.","furigana":"むら|ひとびと|じんじゃ|ほうさく|いの"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A meeting in person, especially a visit to patients, prisoners, or a high-ranking person: 面会時間 (visiting hours), 面会を申し込む (to request a meeting). Also usable as a する-verb: 面会する.","examples":[{"japanese":"入院中の祖母に面会に行った。","romaji":"Nyūinchū no sobo ni menkai ni itta.","german":"I went to visit my grandmother in the hospital.","furigana":"にゅういんちゅう|そぼ|めんかい|い"},{"japanese":"社長に面会を申し込んだが、断られた。","romaji":"Shachō ni menkai o mōshikonda ga, kotowarareta.","german":"I asked for a meeting with the company president, but was refused.","furigana":"しゃちょう|めんかい|もう|こ|ことわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"With に: 海に面した部屋 (a room facing the sea), 大通りに面する (to face a main street). Mostly in the forms 〜に面した / 〜に面している. A written-style する-verb.","examples":[{"japanese":"このホテルの部屋はすべて海に面している。","romaji":"Kono hoteru no heya wa subete umi ni menshite iru.","german":"All the rooms in this hotel face the sea.","furigana":"へや|うみ|めん"},{"japanese":"店は駅前の大通りに面していて、人通りが多い。","romaji":"Mise wa ekimae no ōdōri ni menshite ite, hitodōri ga ōi.","german":"The shop faces the main street in front of the station and gets a lot of foot traffic.","furigana":"みせ|えきまえ|おおどお|めん|ひとどお|おお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To formally address a request, proposal, or protest to an organization: 抗議を申し入れる (to lodge a protest), 交渉を申し入れる (to propose negotiations). Noun: 申し入れ.","examples":[{"japanese":"労働組合は会社に賃上げを申し入れた。","romaji":"Rōdō kumiai wa kaisha ni chin'age o mōshiireta.","german":"The labor union formally requested a wage increase from the company.","furigana":"ろうどうくみあい|かいしゃ|ちんあ|もう|い"},{"japanese":"相手の会社に正式に抗議を申し入れるつもりだ。","romaji":"Aite no kaisha ni seishiki ni kōgi o mōshiireru tsumori da.","german":"We intend to formally lodge a protest with the other company.","furigana":"あいて|かいしゃ|せいしき|こうぎ|もう|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Noun of 申し出る: something one offers, applies for, or reports on one's own initiative. 申し出を受ける / 断る (to accept / decline an offer). The spelling 申出 without okurigana is common in official documents.","examples":[{"japanese":"友人から手伝いの申し出があって、とても助かった。","romaji":"Yūjin kara tetsudai no mōshide ga atte, totemo tasukatta.","german":"A friend offered to help, which was a great relief.","furigana":"ゆうじん|てつだ|もう|で|たす"},{"japanese":"せっかくの申し出ですが、今回はお断りします。","romaji":"Sekkaku no mōshide desu ga, konkai wa okotowari shimasu.","german":"Thank you for the kind offer, but I will decline this time.","furigana":"もう|で|こんかい|ことわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Almost only in the negative: 申し分ない / 申し分のない (flawless, impeccable, nothing to complain about). More polite: 申し分ありません.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼の仕事ぶりは申し分ない。","romaji":"Kare no shigotoburi wa mōshibun nai.","german":"There is nothing to find fault with in his work.","furigana":"かれ|しごと|もう|ぶん"},{"japanese":"駅から近くて広いし、この部屋なら申し分ありません。","romaji":"Eki kara chikakute hiroishi, kono heya nara mōshibun arimasen.","german":"Close to the station and spacious - this room is perfect.","furigana":"えき|ちか|ひろ|へや|もう|ぶん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A list of items or works: 図書目録 (book catalog), 財産目録 (inventory of assets), 作品目録 (list of works).","examples":[{"japanese":"図書館の目録で、その本がどこにあるか調べた。","romaji":"Toshokan no mokuroku de, sono hon ga doko ni aru ka shirabeta.","german":"I checked the library catalog to find where the book is.","furigana":"としょかん|もくろく|ほん|しら"},{"japanese":"展覧会の作品目録は受付で無料でもらえます。","romaji":"Tenrankai no sakuhin mokuroku wa uketsuke de muryō de moraemasu.","german":"You can get the exhibition's list of works free at the reception desk.","furigana":"てんらんかい|さくひんもくろく|うけつけ|むりょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Often with a slightly negative tone (ulterior motive) or for failed plans: 目論見が外れる (the plan falls through). Verb: 目論む (to plan, to scheme).","examples":[{"japanese":"売り上げを倍にするという社長の目論見は外れた。","romaji":"Uriage o bai ni suru to iu shachō no mokuromi wa hazureta.","german":"The president's plan to double sales fell through.","furigana":"う|あ|ばい|しゃちょう|もくろみ|はず"},{"japanese":"彼には何か目論見があるに違いない。","romaji":"Kare ni wa nanika mokuromi ga aru ni chigainai.","german":"He must have some scheme in mind.","furigana":"かれ|なに|もくろみ|ちが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Spoken language, often in questions when one has a guess: もしかして田中さんですか (Are you Mr. Tanaka, by any chance?). Similar: もしかしたら, もしかすると. Usually written in kana.","examples":[{"japanese":"もしかして、田中さんじゃありませんか。","romaji":"Moshikashite, Tanaka-san ja arimasen ka.","german":"Aren't you Mr. Tanaka, by any chance?","furigana":"たなか"},{"japanese":"顔色が悪いけど、もしかして熱があるんじゃない？","romaji":"Kaoiro ga warui kedo, moshikashite netsu ga aru n ja nai?","german":"You look pale - might you have a fever?","furigana":"かおいろ|わる|ねつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Formal conjunction in writing, forms, and announcements: 電話もしくはメールで (by phone or e-mail). In everyday speech, または or か is more common.","examples":[{"japanese":"お申し込みは電話もしくはメールでお願いします。","romaji":"Omōshikomi wa denwa moshikuwa mēru de onegai shimasu.","german":"Please apply by phone or e-mail.","furigana":"もう|こ|でんわ|ねが"},{"japanese":"参加できるのは二十歳以上、もしくは保護者の同意がある方に限ります。","romaji":"Sanka dekiru no wa hatachi ijō, moshikuwa hogosha no dōi ga aru kata ni kagirimasu.","german":"Only people aged 20 or over, or with the consent of a guardian, may participate.","furigana":"さんか|はたちいじょう|ほごしゃ|どうい|かた|かぎ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Almost always in 〜の話で持ち切りだ (everyone is talking only about ...). Also written 持ちきり.","examples":[{"japanese":"学校中が新しい先生の話で持ち切りだ。","romaji":"Gakkōchū ga atarashii sensei no hanashi de mochikiri da.","german":"The whole school is talking about nothing but the new teacher.","furigana":"がっこうちゅう|あたら|せんせい|はなし|も|き"},{"japanese":"町はオリンピックの話題で持ち切りだった。","romaji":"Machi wa orinpikku no wadai de mochikiri datta.","german":"The town was buzzing with talk about the Olympics.","furigana":"まち|わだい|も|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Hotel an Fernstraßen für Autoreisende. In Japan bezeichnet モーテル oft auch ein Stundenhotel an der Landstraße; das Wort ist daher weniger neutral als im Deutschen.","examples":[{"japanese":"アメリカを車で旅行したときは、毎晩モーテルに泊まった。","romaji":"Amerika o kuruma de ryokō shita toki wa, maiban mōteru ni tomatta.","german":"Als ich mit dem Auto durch Amerika reiste, übernachtete ich jeden Abend in einem Motel.","furigana":"くるま|りょこう|まいばん|と"},{"japanese":"高速道路の出口の近くに、古いモーテルが何軒かある。","romaji":"Kōsoku dōro no deguchi no chikaku ni, furui mōteru ga nangen ka aru.","german":"In der Nähe der Autobahnausfahrt gibt es ein paar alte Motels.","furigana":"こうそくどうろ|でぐち|ちか|ふる|なんげん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Colloquially usually written モテる: 女性にモテる (to be popular with women). Not to be confused with 持てる as the potential form of 持つ (to be able to carry / hold).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は話が面白いので、クラスの女子によくモテる。","romaji":"Kare wa hanashi ga omoshiroi node, kurasu no joshi ni yoku moteru.","german":"He's funny, so he's very popular with the girls in his class.","furigana":"かれ|はなし|おもしろ|じょし"},{"japanese":"父は若いころ、ずいぶん持てたそうだ。","romaji":"Chichi wa wakai koro, zuibun moteta sō da.","german":"My father is said to have been very popular when he was young.","furigana":"ちち|わか|も"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Screen (パソコンのモニター) or monitoring (モニターする). Also a person who tests and evaluates products or programs: 商品モニター.","examples":[{"japanese":"仕事用に大きいモニターを買った。","romaji":"Shigotoyō ni ōkii monitā o katta.","german":"I bought a large monitor for work.","furigana":"しごとよう|おお|か"},{"japanese":"新しい化粧品のモニターを募集しています。","romaji":"Atarashii keshōhin no monitā o boshū shite imasu.","german":"We are looking for testers for new cosmetics.","furigana":"あたら|けしょうひん|ぼしゅう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Slightly mocking, for unusual preferences: 物好きな人 (a person with odd tastes), 物好きにも … (out of sheer fancy, of all things ...). Used as a na-adjective and as a noun.","examples":[{"japanese":"こんな寒い日に海で泳ぐなんて、物好きな人もいるものだ。","romaji":"Konna samui hi ni umi de oyogu nante, monozuki na hito mo iru mono da.","german":"Swimming in the sea on such a cold day - there are some odd people out there.","furigana":"さむ|ひ|うみ|およ|ものず|ひと"},{"japanese":"物好きにも、彼は古いタイプライターばかり集めている。","romaji":"Monozuki ni mo, kare wa furui taipuraitā bakari atsumete iru.","german":"Eccentrically, he collects nothing but old typewriters.","furigana":"ものず|かれ|ふる|あつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Written style; stresses that a state has already come about and cannot be reversed: もはや手遅れだ (it is already too late). With negation: もはや〜ない (no longer).","examples":[{"japanese":"気づいたときには、もはや手遅れだった。","romaji":"Kizuita toki ni wa, mohaya teokure datta.","german":"By the time I noticed, it was already too late.","furigana":"き|ておく"},{"japanese":"そんな古い考え方は、もはや通用しない。","romaji":"Sonna furui kangaekata wa, mohaya tsūyō shinai.","german":"Such old-fashioned ways of thinking no longer work.","furigana":"ふる|かんが|かた|つうよう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Intransitive; the subject is the people quarreling or the matter itself: 会議が揉める (the meeting gets contentious), 遺産で揉める (to quarrel over an inheritance). Often in kana: もめる. Noun: 揉め事 (dispute, trouble).","examples":[{"japanese":"兄弟が遺産のことで揉めている。","romaji":"Kyōdai ga isan no koto de momete iru.","german":"The siblings are quarreling over the inheritance.","furigana":"きょうだい|いさん|も"},{"japanese":"会議は予算の配分をめぐって揉めた。","romaji":"Kaigi wa yosan no haibun o megutte mometa.","german":"The meeting got into a dispute over the allocation of the budget.","furigana":"かいぎ|よさん|はいぶん|も"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"腿 means \"thigh\"; it is often written もも or 太もも. 股 can also be read もも in this sense. The other reading また of 股 refers to a different part of the body. 鶏もも肉 is chicken thigh meat; 桃 \"peach\" is a different word with the same sound.","examples":[{"japanese":"走りすぎて、腿の筋肉が痛い。","romaji":"Hashirisugite, momo no kinniku ga itai.","german":"I ran too much and my thigh muscles hurt.","furigana":"はし|もも|きんにく|いた"},{"japanese":"ボールが腿に当たって、あざができた。","romaji":"Bōru ga momo ni atatte, aza ga dekita.","german":"The ball hit my thigh and left a bruise.","furigana":"もも|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Intransitive, mainly for roofs and containers: 雨が漏る (rain leaks in), 屋根が漏る. Noun: 雨漏り. More general is 漏れる (to leak out, seep through). Same sound: 盛る (to heap up, to serve).","examples":[{"japanese":"古い家なので、大雨の日は天井から雨が漏る。","romaji":"Furui ie na node, ōame no hi wa tenjō kara ame ga moru.","german":"The house is old, so rain leaks through the ceiling on days of heavy rain.","furigana":"ふる|いえ|おおあめ|ひ|てんじょう|あめ|も"},{"japanese":"このバケツは底にひびがあって、水が漏る。","romaji":"Kono baketsu wa soko ni hibi ga atte, mizu ga moru.","german":"This bucket has a crack in the bottom and leaks water.","furigana":"そこ|みず|も"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Colloquial: something hits directly and unchecked: もろに影響を受ける (to be hit full force), もろにぶつかる (to collide head-on). Usually in kana.","examples":[{"japanese":"自転車が電柱にもろにぶつかった。","romaji":"Jitensha ga denchū ni moroni butsukatta.","german":"The bicycle crashed head-on into a utility pole.","furigana":"じてんしゃ|でんちゅう"},{"japanese":"円安の影響をもろに受けて、輸入品が値上がりした。","romaji":"En'yasu no eikyō o moroni ukete, yunyūhin ga neagari shita.","german":"Hit squarely by the weak yen, prices of imported goods rose.","furigana":"えんやす|えいきょう|う|ゆにゅうひん|ねあ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Arrow for a bow (弓): 矢を射る (to shoot an arrow). Idioms: 矢のように (like an arrow, swiftly), 矢の催促 (relentless pressing). Same sound: 〜屋 (shop).","examples":[{"japanese":"選手は的の真ん中に矢を命中させた。","romaji":"Senshu wa mato no mannaka ni ya o meichū saseta.","german":"The archer hit the exact center of the target with his arrow.","furigana":"せんしゅ|まと|ま|なか|や|めいちゅう"},{"japanese":"月日は矢のように過ぎていった。","romaji":"Tsukihi wa ya no yō ni sugite itta.","german":"The days and months passed like an arrow.","furigana":"つきひ|や|す"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mainly as a modifier: 野外コンサート (open-air concert), 野外活動 (outdoor activities). Similar: 屋外 (outside, outside of buildings).","examples":[{"japanese":"夏休みに、子どもたちは野外でキャンプをした。","romaji":"Natsuyasumi ni, kodomotachi wa yagai de kyanpu o shita.","german":"During summer vacation, the children went camping outdoors.","furigana":"なつやす|こ|やがい"},{"japanese":"雨のため、野外コンサートは中止になった。","romaji":"Ame no tame, yagai konsāto wa chūshi ni natta.","german":"The open-air concert was canceled because of the rain.","furigana":"あめ|やがい|ちゅうし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A somewhat old-fashioned word for futon and blankets; in everyday speech 布団 or 寝具 is used.","examples":[{"japanese":"旅館の人が部屋に夜具を敷いてくれた。","romaji":"Ryokan no hito ga heya ni yagu o shiite kureta.","german":"The inn staff laid out the bedding in our room.","furigana":"りょかん|ひと|へや|やぐ|し"},{"japanese":"冬に備えて、厚い夜具を押し入れから出した。","romaji":"Fuyu ni sonaete, atsui yagu o oshiire kara dashita.","german":"In preparation for winter, I took the thick bedding out of the closet.","furigana":"ふゆ|そな|あつ|やぐ|お|い|だ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A position in a company or agency, especially a management position: 役職に就く (to take up a post), 役職名 (title such as 部長 or 課長).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は四十歳で部長という役職に就いた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa yonjussai de buchō to iu yakushoku ni tsuita.","german":"At forty, she took the position of department manager.","furigana":"かのじょ|よんじゅっさい|ぶちょう|やくしょく|つ"},{"japanese":"名刺には名前と役職が書いてある。","romaji":"Meishi ni wa namae to yakushoku ga kaite aru.","german":"The business card shows name and title.","furigana":"めいし|なまえ|やくしょく|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A shorter, somewhat more written form of 役に立つ: 仕事に役立つ資格 (a qualification useful for work). Transitive: 役立てる (to put to use).","examples":[{"japanese":"この本は日本語の勉強に大いに役立った。","romaji":"Kono hon wa nihongo no benkyō ni ōi ni yakudatta.","german":"This book was very helpful for studying Japanese.","furigana":"ほん|にほんご|べんきょう|おお|やくだ"},{"japanese":"災害時に役立つ情報をまとめました。","romaji":"Saigaiji ni yakudatsu jōhō o matomemashita.","german":"We have compiled information that is useful in disasters.","furigana":"さいがいじ|やくだ|じょうほう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Colloquial, when something is conspicuously and unexpectedly strong: 今日はやけに暑い (it's unusually hot today). Not to be confused with やけになる (to become reckless out of despair).","examples":[{"japanese":"今日はやけに静かだね。何かあったの？","romaji":"Kyō wa yakeni shizuka da ne. Nanika atta no?","german":"You're strikingly quiet today. Did something happen?","furigana":"きょう|しず|なに"},{"japanese":"今年の夏はやけに蚊が多い。","romaji":"Kotoshi no natsu wa yakeni ka ga ōi.","german":"There are an unusual number of mosquitoes this summer.","furigana":"ことし|なつ|か|おお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Literary reading of 社 for a Shinto shrine, especially a small one; in everyday speech usually 神社. Not to be confused with 社【しゃ】 (company, e.g. 我が社).","examples":[{"japanese":"森の奥に小さな社がある。","romaji":"Mori no oku ni chiisana yashiro ga aru.","german":"There is a small shrine deep in the forest.","furigana":"もり|おく|ちい|やしろ"},{"japanese":"村人は毎年、森の社の前で祭りを行う。","romaji":"Murabito wa maitoshi, mori no yashiro no mae de matsuri o okonau.","german":"The villagers hold a festival every year in front of the shrine in the forest.","furigana":"むらびと|まいとし|もり|やしろ|まえ|まつ|おこな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"野心的 (ambitious), 野心家 (ambitious person). Often with a slightly negative tone: 野心を抱く (to harbor ambitious plans).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は政治家になるという野心を持っている。","romaji":"Kare wa seijika ni naru to iu yashin o motte iru.","german":"He has the ambition to become a politician.","furigana":"かれ|せいじか|やしん|も"},{"japanese":"若い社長は、会社を世界一にするという野心に燃えていた。","romaji":"Wakai shachō wa, kaisha o sekaiichi ni suru to iu yashin ni moete ita.","german":"The young president burned with the ambition to make the company number one in the world.","furigana":"わか|しゃちょう|かいしゃ|せかいいち|やしん|も"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Pejorative: looks cheap or inferior, regardless of the price: 安っぽい服. Also used for feelings and statements: 安っぽい同情 (cheap sympathy).","examples":[{"japanese":"このバッグは高かったのに、安っぽく見える。","romaji":"Kono baggu wa takakatta noni, yasuppoku mieru.","german":"This bag was expensive, but it looks cheap.","furigana":"たか|やす|み"},{"japanese":"安っぽい同情なんかいらない。","romaji":"Yasuppoi dōjō nanka iranai.","german":"I don't need your cheap sympathy.","furigana":"やす|どうじょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive counterpart of 休む: 体を休める (to rest), 手を休める (to take a break), 目を休める (to rest one's eyes). Not to be confused with 休める as the potential form of 休む (to be able to take time off).","examples":[{"japanese":"週末は何もしないで体を休めたい。","romaji":"Shūmatsu wa nani mo shinaide karada o yasumetai.","german":"I want to do nothing this weekend and just rest my body.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|なに|からだ|やす"},{"japanese":"彼女は手を休めずに、黙って作業を続けた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa te o yasumezu ni, damatte sagyō o tsuzuketa.","german":"She kept on working silently without pausing her hands.","furigana":"かのじょ|て|やす|だま|さぎょう|つづ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"野生の動物 (wild animals), 野生のキノコ (wild-growing mushrooms). As a する-verb: 野生する (to grow wild). Related: 野生化 (becoming feral).","examples":[{"japanese":"この島では野生の猿を見ることができる。","romaji":"Kono shima de wa yasei no saru o miru koto ga dekiru.","german":"You can see wild monkeys on this island.","furigana":"しま|やせい|さる|み"},{"japanese":"山に野生しているキノコを、むやみに食べてはいけない。","romaji":"Yama ni yasei shite iru kinoko o, muyami ni tabete wa ikenai.","german":"You shouldn't just eat mushrooms that grow wild in the mountains.","furigana":"やま|やせい|た"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"More formal than 病気: 不治の病 (incurable disease), 病に倒れる (to fall seriously ill). Proverb: 病は気から (illness begins in the mind). Figuratively also a bad habit or weakness.","examples":[{"japanese":"祖父は長い病の末に亡くなった。","romaji":"Sofu wa nagai yamai no sue ni nakunatta.","german":"My grandfather died after a long illness.","furigana":"そふ|なが|やまい|すえ|な"},{"japanese":"「病は気から」と言うから、あまり心配しすぎないで。","romaji":"\"Yamai wa ki kara\" to iu kara, amari shinpai shisuginaide.","german":"They say \"illness begins in the mind\", so don't worry too much.","furigana":"やまい|き|い|しんぱい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"暗闇 (pitch darkness). Figurative: 闇に葬る (to cover up), 闇市 (black market), 闇取引 (illegal dealing).","examples":[{"japanese":"停電で、部屋は闇に包まれた。","romaji":"Teiden de, heya wa yami ni tsutsumareta.","german":"The power outage plunged the room into darkness.","furigana":"ていでん|へや|やみ|つつ"},{"japanese":"事件の真相は闇に葬られた。","romaji":"Jiken no shinsō wa yami ni hōmurareta.","german":"The truth about the incident was buried in darkness.","furigana":"じけん|しんそう|やみ|ほうむ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Colloquial: ややこしい話 (a tricky matter), 話をややこしくする (to complicate matters). More neutral: 複雑な.","examples":[{"japanese":"この書類は書き方がややこしくて、よく分からない。","romaji":"Kono shorui wa kakikata ga yayakoshikute, yoku wakaranai.","german":"This form is so complicated to fill in that I hardly understand it.","furigana":"しょるい|か|かた|わ"},{"japanese":"君が口を出すと、話がややこしくなる。","romaji":"Kimi ga kuchi o dasu to, hanashi ga yayakoshiku naru.","german":"If you butt in, the matter will only get more complicated.","furigana":"きみ|くち|だ|はなし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To carry something out to the end despite difficulties: 最後までやり通す. Similar: やり遂げる (to complete successfully).","examples":[{"japanese":"一度始めたことは、最後までやり通すつもりだ。","romaji":"Ichido hajimeta koto wa, saigo made yaritōsu tsumori da.","german":"I intend to see through to the end whatever I have once started.","furigana":"いちどはじ|さいご|とお"},{"japanese":"彼は周りに反対されても、自分のやり方をやり通した。","romaji":"Kare wa mawari ni hantai sarete mo, jibun no yarikata o yaritōshita.","german":"Even though people around him objected, he stuck to his own way of doing things.","furigana":"かれ|まわ|はんたい|じぶん|かた|とお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To bring a difficult task to a successful conclusion: 仕事をやり遂げる. Puts more emphasis than やり通す on the result achieved.","examples":[{"japanese":"三年かかったが、ついにプロジェクトをやり遂げた。","romaji":"Sannen kakatta ga, tsui ni purojekuto o yaritogeta.","german":"It took three years, but we finally accomplished the project.","furigana":"さんねん|と"},{"japanese":"一人でこの仕事をやり遂げるのは無理だ。","romaji":"Hitori de kono shigoto o yaritogeru no wa muri da.","german":"It is impossible to accomplish this work alone.","furigana":"ひとり|しごと|と|むり"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"優位に立つ (to gain the upper hand), 優位を保つ (to keep the lead). Homophone: 有意 (significant).","examples":[{"japanese":"交渉では、相手より優位に立つことが大切だ。","romaji":"Kōshō de wa, aite yori yūi ni tatsu koto ga taisetsu da.","german":"In negotiations, it is important to gain the upper hand over the other side.","furigana":"こうしょう|あいて|ゆうい|た|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"ホームのチームは前半、終始優位に試合を進めた。","romaji":"Hōmu no chīmu wa zenhan, shūshi yūi ni shiai o susumeta.","german":"The home team was dominant throughout the first half.","furigana":"ぜんはん|しゅうしゆうい|しあい|すす"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mainly in 優越感 (feeling of superiority; opposite: 劣等感). As a する-verb: 〜に優越する (to be superior to ...).","examples":[{"japanese":"性能の面では、この製品は他社の製品に優越している。","romaji":"Seinō no men de wa, kono seihin wa tasha no seihin ni yūetsu shite iru.","german":"In terms of performance, this product is superior to those of other companies.","furigana":"せいのう|めん|せいひん|たしゃ|せいひん|ゆうえつ"},{"japanese":"彼はいつも優越感に浸っている。","romaji":"Kare wa itsumo yūetsukan ni hitatte iru.","german":"He is always basking in a sense of superiority.","furigana":"かれ|ゆうえつかん|ひた"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"More poetic than 夕方: 夕暮れ時 (at twilight). Similar: 日暮れ, 黄昏 (たそがれ).","examples":[{"japanese":"夕暮れの海辺を二人で散歩した。","romaji":"Yūgure no umibe o futari de sanpo shita.","german":"The two of us took a walk along the seashore at dusk.","furigana":"ゆうぐ|うみべ|ふたり|さんぽ"},{"japanese":"秋になると、夕暮れが早くなる。","romaji":"Aki ni naru to, yūgure ga hayaku naru.","german":"In autumn, dusk comes earlier.","furigana":"あき|ゆうぐ|はや"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Very formal (laws, contracts, news): 権利を有する (to have a right), 資格を有する者 (persons with the relevant qualification). In everyday speech: 持つ, ある.","examples":[{"japanese":"国民は、ひとしく教育を受ける権利を有する。","romaji":"Kokumin wa, hitoshiku kyōiku o ukeru kenri o yūsuru.","german":"All citizens equally have the right to receive an education.","furigana":"こくみん|きょういく|う|けんり|ゆう"},{"japanese":"この会社は国内に五つの工場を有している。","romaji":"Kono kaisha wa kokunai ni itsutsu no kōjō o yūshite iru.","german":"This company has five factories in Japan.","furigana":"かいしゃ|こくない|いつ|こうじょう|ゆう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"優勢な (dominant), 優勢になる / 優勢に立つ (to gain the upper hand). Opposite: 劣勢 (inferiority).","examples":[{"japanese":"世論調査では、野党が優勢だ。","romaji":"Yoron chōsa de wa, yatō ga yūsei da.","german":"In the opinion polls, the opposition is ahead.","furigana":"よろんちょうさ|やとう|ゆうせい"},{"japanese":"前半は相手チームが優勢だったが、後半に逆転した。","romaji":"Zenhan wa aite chīmu ga yūsei datta ga, kōhan ni gyakuten shita.","german":"The opposing team had the upper hand in the first half, but we turned the game around in the second.","furigana":"ぜんはん|あいて|ゆうせい|こうはん|ぎゃくてん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"As a する-verb: 客を出口へ誘導する (to guide guests to the exit). 誘導尋問 (leading question); in physics also induction.","examples":[{"japanese":"係員が観客を非常口へ誘導した。","romaji":"Kakariin ga kankyaku o hijōguchi e yūdō shita.","german":"The staff guided the spectators to the emergency exits.","furigana":"かかりいん|かんきゃく|ひじょうぐち|ゆうどう"},{"japanese":"事故現場では、警察官が車の誘導をしている。","romaji":"Jiko genba de wa, keisatsukan ga kuruma no yūdō o shite iru.","german":"At the scene of the accident, a police officer is directing the cars.","furigana":"じこげんば|けいさつかん|くるま|ゆうどう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"将来有望な (with a promising future), 有望な新人 (promising newcomer), 有望株 (rising star). な-adjective.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は将来有望な若手選手だ。","romaji":"Kare wa shōrai yūbō na wakate senshu da.","german":"He is a promising young player with a bright future.","furigana":"かれ|しょうらいゆうぼう|わかてせんしゅ"},{"japanese":"太陽光発電は有望な分野だと考えられている。","romaji":"Taiyōkō hatsuden wa yūbō na bun'ya da to kangaerarete iru.","german":"Solar power generation is considered a promising field.","furigana":"たいようこうはつでん|ゆうぼう|ぶんや|かんが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"遊牧民 (nomads), 遊牧生活 (nomadic life). As a する-verb: 遊牧する (to wander as nomads).","examples":[{"japanese":"モンゴルには、今も遊牧を続けている人々がいる。","romaji":"Mongoru ni wa, ima mo yūboku o tsuzukete iru hitobito ga iru.","german":"In Mongolia there are still people who continue to live as nomads.","furigana":"いま|ゆうぼく|つづ|ひとびと"},{"japanese":"遊牧民は季節ごとに家畜を連れて移動する。","romaji":"Yūbokumin wa kisetsu goto ni kachiku o tsurete idō suru.","german":"Nomads move with their livestock according to the seasons.","furigana":"ゆうぼくみん|きせつ|かちく|つ|いどう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"時間にゆとりがある (to have plenty of time), 心のゆとり (inner calm), 生活のゆとり (financial leeway).","examples":[{"japanese":"時間にゆとりを持って家を出た。","romaji":"Jikan ni yutori o motte ie o deta.","german":"I left home with time to spare.","furigana":"じかん|も|いえ|で"},{"japanese":"忙しすぎて、心のゆとりがない。","romaji":"Isogashisugite, kokoro no yutori ga nai.","german":"I'm so busy that I have no peace of mind.","furigana":"いそが|こころ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"In Japanese usually positive, in the sense of \"original, witty, offbeat\": ユニークな発想 (an original idea). Not \"one of a kind\" in the strict sense. な-adjective.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女はいつもユニークなアイデアを出す。","romaji":"Kanojo wa itsumo yunīku na aidea o dasu.","german":"She always comes up with original ideas.","furigana":"かのじょ|だ"},{"japanese":"その店は、ユニークな形の建物で有名だ。","romaji":"Sono mise wa, yunīku na katachi no tatemono de yūmei da.","german":"That shop is famous for its uniquely shaped building.","furigana":"みせ|かたち|たてもの|ゆうめい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mainly sports and work clothing: チームのユニフォーム (team kit). Also written ユニホーム. School or police uniforms are usually called 制服.","examples":[{"japanese":"選手たちは新しいユニフォームを着て試合に出た。","romaji":"Senshutachi wa atarashii yunifōmu o kite shiai ni deta.","german":"The players took the field in their new uniforms.","furigana":"せんしゅ|あたら|き|しあい|で"},{"japanese":"この店では、店員のユニフォームが毎年変わる。","romaji":"Kono mise de wa, ten'in no yunifōmu ga maitoshi kawaru.","german":"In this shop, the staff uniform changes every year.","furigana":"みせ|てんいん|まいとしか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"この世 (this world), あの世 (the afterlife), 世に出る (to become known, to come out into the public), 世の中 (the world, society). As an era: 江戸の世 (the Edo period).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼の作品は、死後に初めて世に知られた。","romaji":"Kare no sakuhin wa, shigo ni hajimete yo ni shirareta.","german":"His works only became known to the world after his death.","furigana":"かれ|さくひん|しご|はじ|よ|し"},{"japanese":"この世に完璧な人間などいない。","romaji":"Kono yo ni kanpeki na ningen nado inai.","german":"There is no perfect person in this world.","furigana":"よ|かんぺき|にんげん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Chemistry: 食塩の溶液 (salt solution), 水溶液 (aqueous solution). Verb: 溶かす (to dissolve).","examples":[{"japanese":"砂糖を水に溶かして溶液を作る。","romaji":"Satō o mizu ni tokashite yōeki o tsukuru.","german":"You dissolve sugar in water to make a solution.","furigana":"さとう|みず|と|ようえき|つく"},{"japanese":"実験で使った溶液は、流しに捨てないでください。","romaji":"Jikken de tsukatta yōeki wa, nagashi ni sutenaide kudasai.","german":"Please do not pour the solution used in the experiment down the sink.","furigana":"じっけん|つか|ようえき|なが|す"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The reason why one calls or visits someone: ご用件は何でしょうか (What can I do for you?), 用件を伝える (to state one's business). Polite with ご.","examples":[{"japanese":"失礼ですが、どのようなご用件でしょうか。","romaji":"Shitsurei desu ga, dono yō na goyōken deshō ka.","german":"Excuse me, but what is it regarding?","furigana":"しつれい|ようけん"},{"japanese":"忙しいので、用件だけ手短に言ってください。","romaji":"Isogashii node, yōken dake temijika ni itte kudasai.","german":"I'm busy, so please just tell me what you want, briefly.","furigana":"いそが|ようけん|てみじか|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mainly in fixed expressions: 児童養護施設 (children's home), 養護教諭 (teacher in charge of the school infirmary, 保健室の先生). Homophones: 擁護 (defense, advocacy), 用語 (technical term).","examples":[{"japanese":"母は老人ホームで高齢者の養護に当たっている。","romaji":"Haha wa rōjin hōmu de kōreisha no yōgo ni atatte iru.","german":"My mother takes care of elderly people at a nursing home.","furigana":"はは|ろうじん|こうれいしゃ|ようご|あ"},{"japanese":"気分が悪くなったので、保健室で養護の先生に相談した。","romaji":"Kibun ga waruku natta node, hokenshitsu de yōgo no sensei ni sōdan shita.","german":"I felt unwell, so I consulted the school nurse in the infirmary.","furigana":"きぶん|わる|ほけんしつ|ようご|せんせい|そうだん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"申込用紙 (application form), 解答用紙 (answer sheet), 原稿用紙 (manuscript paper with squares), A4の用紙 (A4 paper). Homophones: 養子 (adopted child), 要旨 (gist).","examples":[{"japanese":"この用紙に名前と住所を書いてください。","romaji":"Kono yōshi ni namae to jūsho o kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write your name and address on this form.","furigana":"ようし|なまえ|じゅうしょ|か"},{"japanese":"プリンターの用紙が切れている。","romaji":"Purintā no yōshi ga kirete iru.","german":"The printer is out of paper.","furigana":"ようし|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Systematic training of skilled workers: 教員養成 (teacher training), 人材を養成する (to train personnel), 養成所 (training institute). Homophones: 要請 (request), 妖精 (fairy).","examples":[{"japanese":"この大学は昔から教員の養成に力を入れている。","romaji":"Kono daigaku wa mukashi kara kyōin no yōsei ni chikara o irete iru.","german":"This university has long put effort into teacher training.","furigana":"だいがく|むかし|きょういん|ようせい|ちから|い"},{"japanese":"会社は若い技術者を養成するための研修を始めた。","romaji":"Kaisha wa wakai gijutsusha o yōsei suru tame no kenshū o hajimeta.","german":"The company has started a training program to train young engineers.","furigana":"かいしゃ|わか|ぎじゅつしゃ|ようせい|けんしゅう|はじ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Written language, mostly for situations: 〜の様相を呈する (to take on the appearance of ..., to develop into ...), 様相が一変する (the situation changes completely).","examples":[{"japanese":"地震の後、町の様相は一変した。","romaji":"Jishin no ato, machi no yōsō wa ippen shita.","german":"After the earthquake, the look of the town changed completely.","furigana":"じしん|あと|まち|ようそう|いっぺん"},{"japanese":"選挙は激しい争いの様相を呈している。","romaji":"Senkyo wa hageshii arasoi no yōsō o tei shite iru.","german":"The election is turning into a fierce contest.","furigana":"せんきょ|はげ|あらそ|ようそう|てい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mostly in compounds: 事務用品 (office supplies), スポーツ用品 (sporting goods), 日用品 (daily necessities).","examples":[{"japanese":"キャンプに必要な用品は、すべて現地で借りられる。","romaji":"Kyanpu ni hitsuyō na yōhin wa, subete genchi de karirareru.","german":"All the equipment needed for camping can be rented on site.","furigana":"ひつよう|ようひん|げんち|か"},{"japanese":"駅前のスポーツ用品店でランニングシューズを買った。","romaji":"Ekimae no supōtsu yōhinten de ranningu shūzu o katta.","german":"I bought running shoes at the sporting goods store in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|ようひんてん|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"用法・用量を守る (to follow the directions and dosage, for medicine), 言葉の用法 (the usage of a word).","examples":[{"japanese":"薬は用法と用量を守って飲んでください。","romaji":"Kusuri wa yōhō to yōryō o mamotte nonde kudasai.","german":"Please take the medicine according to the directions and dosage.","furigana":"くすり|ようほう|ようりょう|まも|の"},{"japanese":"この言葉には二つの用法がある。","romaji":"Kono kotoba ni wa futatsu no yōhō ga aru.","german":"This word has two usages.","furigana":"ことば|ふた|ようほう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"嫌な予感がする (to have a bad premonition), 予感が当たる (a hunch turns out right). As a する-verb: 予感する.","examples":[{"japanese":"朝から何となく嫌な予感がしていた。","romaji":"Asa kara nantonaku iya na yokan ga shite ita.","german":"I've had a bad feeling since the morning.","furigana":"あさ|なん|いや|よかん"},{"japanese":"悪い予感が当たって、電車が止まっていた。","romaji":"Warui yokan ga atatte, densha ga tomatte ita.","german":"My bad premonition came true: the trains had stopped.","furigana":"わる|よかん|あ|でんしゃ|と"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A small performance at festivities, weddings or company parties (song, skit, magic trick): 余興を披露する (to perform an act of entertainment).","examples":[{"japanese":"結婚式の余興で、友人たちが歌を歌った。","romaji":"Kekkonshiki no yokyō de, yūjintachi ga uta o utatta.","german":"At the wedding reception, friends sang a song as entertainment.","furigana":"けっこんしき|よきょう|ゆうじん|うた|うた"},{"japanese":"忘年会の余興で、新入社員が手品を披露した。","romaji":"Bōnenkai no yokyō de, shinnyū shain ga tejina o hirō shita.","german":"At the year-end party, the new employees performed magic tricks as entertainment.","furigana":"ぼうねんかい|よきょう|しんにゅうしゃいん|てじな|ひろう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"欲がない (modest, unambitious), 欲が出る (to want more, to become greedy), 欲を言えば (if I may ask for more). As a suffix: 食欲 (appetite), 物欲 (materialistic desire). Homophone: よく (often, well).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は欲がなくて、出世には興味がないようだ。","romaji":"Kare wa yoku ga nakute, shusse ni wa kyōmi ga nai yō da.","german":"He has no ambition and doesn't seem interested in getting ahead.","furigana":"かれ|よく|しゅっせ|きょうみ"},{"japanese":"欲を言えば、もう少し広い部屋がよかった。","romaji":"Yoku o ieba, mō sukoshi hiroi heya ga yokatta.","german":"If I may ask for a bit more, I'd have liked a somewhat larger room.","furigana":"よく|い|すこ|ひろ|へや"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A room for bathing (with a tub or shower); more formal than お風呂 or 風呂場. In Japan the toilet is usually separate.","examples":[{"japanese":"このマンションは浴室が広い。","romaji":"Kono manshon wa yokushitsu ga hiroi.","german":"The bathroom in this apartment is spacious.","furigana":"よくしつ|ひろ"},{"japanese":"浴室の換気扇が壊れて、カビが生えてしまった。","romaji":"Yokushitsu no kankisen ga kowarete, kabi ga haete shimatta.","german":"The bathroom ventilation fan broke and mold has grown.","furigana":"よくしつ|かんきせん|こわ|は"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Pejorative for people who always want more: 欲深い人. Also read よくぶかい. Similar: 貪欲 (voracious).","examples":[{"japanese":"欲深い商人は、貧しい人からも金を取った。","romaji":"Yokufukai shōnin wa, mazushii hito kara mo kane o totta.","german":"The greedy merchant took money even from poor people.","furigana":"よくふか|しょうにん|まず|ひと|かね|と"},{"japanese":"人間は欲深い生き物だとよく言われる。","romaji":"Ningen wa yokufukai ikimono da to yoku iwareru.","german":"People often say that humans are greedy creatures.","furigana":"にんげん|よくふか|い|もの|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Stronger and often more negative than 欲: 欲望を抑える (to restrain one's desires), 欲望に負ける (to give in to one's desires).","examples":[{"japanese":"人間の欲望には限りがない。","romaji":"Ningen no yokubō ni wa kagiri ga nai.","german":"Human desires know no limits.","furigana":"にんげん|よくぼう|かぎ"},{"japanese":"甘い物を食べたいという欲望を抑えられなかった。","romaji":"Amai mono o tabetai to iu yokubō o osaerarenakatta.","german":"I couldn't suppress the desire to eat something sweet.","furigana":"あま|もの|た|よくぼう|おさ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"As a する-verb: 未来を予言する (to predict the future). 予言者 (prophet). For sober forecasts, 予測 or 予想 are more usual.","examples":[{"japanese":"占い師の予言は見事に当たった。","romaji":"Uranaishi no yogen wa migoto ni atatta.","german":"The fortune-teller's prophecy came true perfectly.","furigana":"うらな|し|よげん|みごと|あ"},{"japanese":"彼は十年前に、今の不況を予言していた。","romaji":"Kare wa jūnen mae ni, ima no fukyō o yogen shite ita.","german":"He had predicted the current recession ten years ago.","furigana":"かれ|じゅうねんまえ|いま|ふきょう|よげん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Noun of 汚れる: 汚れを落とす (to remove dirt), 汚れが目立つ (stains stand out). Read as けがれ, 汚れ (also 穢れ) means religious impurity.","examples":[{"japanese":"この洗剤は油の汚れがよく落ちる。","romaji":"Kono senzai wa abura no yogore ga yoku ochiru.","german":"This detergent removes grease stains well.","furigana":"せんざい|あぶら|よご|お"},{"japanese":"白いシャツは汚れが目立つ。","romaji":"Shiroi shatsu wa yogore ga medatsu.","german":"Stains are conspicuous on a white shirt.","furigana":"しろ|よご|めだ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"物事の善し悪し (what is good and what is bad), 善し悪しを判断する (to judge the quality). As a verdict: それも善し悪しだ (that has its pros and cons). Also written 良し悪し.","examples":[{"japanese":"小さな子どもには、まだ物事の善し悪しが分からない。","romaji":"Chiisana kodomo ni wa, mada monogoto no yoshiashi ga wakaranai.","german":"Small children cannot yet tell right from wrong.","furigana":"ちい|こ|ものごと|よ|あ|わ"},{"japanese":"便利すぎるのも善し悪しだ。","romaji":"Benrisugiru no mo yoshiashi da.","german":"Too much convenience has its good and bad sides.","furigana":"べんり|よ|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"As a する-verb: よそ見する (to look elsewhere, to not pay attention). よそ見運転 (inattentive driving). Also written 余所見.","examples":[{"japanese":"授業中によそ見をして、先生に注意された。","romaji":"Jugyōchū ni yosomi o shite, sensei ni chūi sareta.","german":"I looked away during class and was warned by the teacher.","furigana":"じゅぎょうちゅう|み|せんせい|ちゅうい"},{"japanese":"運転中のよそ見は事故のもとだ。","romaji":"Untenchū no yosomi wa jiko no moto da.","german":"Looking away while driving is a cause of accidents.","furigana":"うんてんちゅう|み|じこ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Formal conjunction at the start of a sentence, typical in certificates, court rulings and proofs: よって、〜とする (therefore it is held that ...). On certificates: よってこれを賞します (hereby this prize is awarded). Not to be confused with 〜によって (by, depending on).","examples":[{"japanese":"あなたは本大会で優秀な成績を収めました。よってこれを賞します。","romaji":"Anata wa hon taikai de yūshū na seiseki o osamemashita. Yotte kore o shō shimasu.","german":"You achieved outstanding results in this tournament. Therefore, this prize is awarded to you.","furigana":"ほんたいかい|ゆうしゅう|せいせき|おさ|しょう"},{"japanese":"原告の主張には根拠がない。よって、本件の訴えを棄却する。","romaji":"Genkoku no shuchō ni wa konkyo ga nai. Yotte, honken no uttae o kikyaku suru.","german":"The plaintiff's claims have no basis. Therefore, the suit in this case is dismissed.","furigana":"げんこく|しゅちょう|こんきょ|ほんけん|うった|ききゃく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Colloquial, emphatic form of よほど (余程): よっぽど疲れていたんだね (you must have been very tired). With comparison: 〜よりよっぽどいい (much better than ...). Mostly written in kana.","examples":[{"japanese":"何も言わずに寝てしまうなんて、よっぽど疲れていたんだね。","romaji":"Nani mo iwazu ni nete shimau nante, yoppodo tsukarete ita n da ne.","german":"To fall asleep without saying a word, you must have been really tired.","furigana":"なに|い|ね|つか"},{"japanese":"外食するより、家で作るほうがよっぽど安い。","romaji":"Gaishoku suru yori, ie de tsukuru hō ga yoppodo yasui.","german":"Cooking at home is much cheaper than eating out.","furigana":"がいしょく|いえ|つく|やす"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To make someone stop by calling out: 警官に呼び止められる (to be stopped by a police officer). Also: タクシーを呼び止める (to hail a taxi).","examples":[{"japanese":"駅を出たところで、知らない人に呼び止められた。","romaji":"Eki o deta tokoro de, shiranai hito ni yobitomerareta.","german":"Just as I left the station, a stranger stopped me.","furigana":"えき|で|し|ひと|よ|と"},{"japanese":"帰ろうとしたら、部長に呼び止められた。","romaji":"Kaerō to shitara, buchō ni yobitomerareta.","german":"When I was about to go home, the department head called me back.","furigana":"かえ|ぶちょう|よ|と"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"To read aloud loudly and clearly, often in front of an audience: 名前を読み上げる (to call out names), 原稿を読み上げる (to read out a manuscript).","examples":[{"japanese":"先生が合格者の名前を一人ずつ読み上げた。","romaji":"Sensei ga gōkakusha no namae o hitori zutsu yomiageta.","german":"The teacher read out the names of those who passed, one by one.","furigana":"せんせい|ごうかくしゃ|なまえ|ひとり|よ|あ"},{"japanese":"司会者が式の予定を読み上げた。","romaji":"Shikaisha ga shiki no yotei o yomiageta.","german":"The master of ceremonies read out the program of the ceremony.","furigana":"しかいしゃ|しき|よてい|よ|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Intransitive: 風が弱まる (the wind dies down), 影響力が弱まる (influence wanes). Transitive: 弱める.","examples":[{"japanese":"夜になって、雨が少し弱まった。","romaji":"Yoru ni natte, ame ga sukoshi yowamatta.","german":"In the evening, the rain eased off a little.","furigana":"よる|あめ|すこ|よわ"},{"japanese":"年を取ると、筋肉の力が弱まる。","romaji":"Toshi o toru to, kinniku no chikara ga yowamaru.","german":"With age, muscle strength weakens.","furigana":"とし|と|きんにく|ちから|よわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive counterpart of 弱まる: 火を弱める (to turn down the heat), 力を弱める (to weaken the force). Opposite: 強める.","examples":[{"japanese":"沸騰したら、火を弱めて十分煮てください。","romaji":"Futtō shitara, hi o yowamete juppun nite kudasai.","german":"Once it boils, turn down the heat and simmer thoroughly.","furigana":"ふっとう|ひ|よわ|じゅっぷんに"},{"japanese":"薬の副作用を弱めるために、食後に飲む。","romaji":"Kusuri no fukusayō o yowameru tame ni, shokugo ni nomu.","german":"You take the medicine after meals to reduce its side effects.","furigana":"くすり|ふくさよう|よわ|しょくご|の"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Physical: 体が弱る (to be weakened). Colloquially also: 弱ったなあ (What am I going to do?), 〜には弱る (... is a problem for one).","examples":[{"japanese":"祖母は入院してから、足がすっかり弱った。","romaji":"Sobo wa nyūin shite kara, ashi ga sukkari yowatta.","german":"My grandmother's legs have become quite weak since she was hospitalized.","furigana":"そぼ|にゅういん|あし|よわ"},{"japanese":"財布を忘れてしまった。弱ったなあ。","romaji":"Saifu o wasurete shimatta. Yowatta nā.","german":"I forgot my wallet. What am I going to do?","furigana":"さいふ|わす|よわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Formal in announcements: ご来場の皆様 (dear visitors), ご来場ありがとうございます (thank you for coming). 来場者 (visitors).","examples":[{"japanese":"本日はご来場いただき、誠にありがとうございます。","romaji":"Honjitsu wa goraijō itadaki, makoto ni arigatō gozaimasu.","german":"Thank you very much for coming today.","furigana":"ほんじつ|らいじょう|まこと"},{"japanese":"駐車場がないため、車での来場はご遠慮ください。","romaji":"Chūshajō ga nai tame, kuruma de no raijō wa goenryo kudasai.","german":"As there is no parking, please refrain from coming by car.","furigana":"ちゅうしゃじょう|くるま|らいじょう|えんりょ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"In Western-style restaurants for cooked rice served on a plate: ライスの大盛り (large portion of rice). At home and in Japanese restaurants, ご飯 is used.","examples":[{"japanese":"ハンバーグにライスとスープが付いてくる。","romaji":"Hanbāgu ni raisu to sūpu ga tsuite kuru.","german":"The hamburger steak comes with rice and soup.","furigana":"つ"},{"japanese":"ライスは大盛りにできますか。","romaji":"Raisu wa ōmori ni dekimasu ka.","german":"Can I get a large portion of rice?","furigana":"おおも"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Formal / technical: 落下物 (falling objects), 落下傘 (parachute). As a する-verb: 屋根から落下する (to fall from the roof). In everyday speech: 落ちる. Homophone: 落果 (fallen fruit).","examples":[{"japanese":"工事現場では落下物に注意してください。","romaji":"Kōji genba de wa rakkabutsu ni chūi shite kudasai.","german":"Please watch out for falling objects at the construction site.","furigana":"こうじげんば|らっかぶつ|ちゅうい"},{"japanese":"強風で看板が歩道に落下した。","romaji":"Kyōfū de kanban ga hodō ni rakka shita.","german":"A strong wind made a sign fall onto the sidewalk.","furigana":"きょうふう|かんばん|ほどう|らっか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"楽観的 (optimistic), 楽観視する (to view optimistically), 楽観できない / 楽観を許さない (no grounds for optimism). Opposite: 悲観.","examples":[{"japanese":"景気の先行きについては楽観できない。","romaji":"Keiki no sakiyuki ni tsuite wa rakkan dekinai.","german":"We cannot be optimistic about the economic outlook.","furigana":"けいき|さきゆ|らっかん"},{"japanese":"医者は、病状はまだ楽観を許さないと言った。","romaji":"Isha wa, byōjō wa mada rakkan o yurusanai to itta.","german":"The doctor said that the patient's condition still gives no grounds for optimism.","furigana":"いしゃ|びょうじょう|らっかん|ゆる|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A label on bottles, goods or folders: ラベルを貼る (to stick on a label). For the figurative \"to label someone\", レッテルを貼る is more usual.","examples":[{"japanese":"ワインのラベルに生産地が書いてある。","romaji":"Wain no raberu ni seisanchi ga kaite aru.","german":"The place of production is written on the wine label.","furigana":"せいさんち|か"},{"japanese":"引っ越しの箱にラベルを貼っておくと便利だ。","romaji":"Hikkoshi no hako ni raberu o hatte oku to benri da.","german":"It is handy to put labels on moving boxes.","furigana":"ひ|こ|はこ|は|べんり"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mainly oil lamps or indicator lights: 石油ランプ (kerosene lamp), 警告ランプ (warning light). For room lighting, 電気 or ライト is usually used. Homophone: ランプ (highway on-ramp).","examples":[{"japanese":"山小屋では、ランプの明かりで夕食をとった。","romaji":"Yamagoya de wa, ranpu no akari de yūshoku o totta.","german":"At the mountain hut, we ate dinner by lamplight.","furigana":"やまごや|あ|ゆうしょく"},{"japanese":"車の警告ランプが点いたので、すぐに整備工場へ行った。","romaji":"Kuruma no keikoku ranpu ga tsuita node, sugu ni seibi kōjō e itta.","german":"A warning light came on in the car, so I went straight to the garage.","furigana":"くるま|けいこく|つ|せいびこうじょう|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"薬物乱用 (drug abuse), 職権乱用 (abuse of authority), 権利の濫用 (abuse of rights). 濫用 is the original spelling and is common in legal texts. As a する-verb: 乱用する.","examples":[{"japanese":"薬の乱用は健康に深刻な害を与える。","romaji":"Kusuri no ran'yō wa kenkō ni shinkoku na gai o ataeru.","german":"Drug abuse seriously harms health.","furigana":"くすり|らんよう|けんこう|しんこく|がい|あた"},{"japanese":"市長は職権乱用の疑いで調べられている。","romaji":"Shichō wa shokken ran'yō no utagai de shiraberarete iru.","german":"The mayor is being investigated on suspicion of abuse of authority.","furigana":"しちょう|しょっけんらんよう|うたが|しら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"立体的 (three-dimensional, plastic), 立体駐車場 (multi-story car park), 立体交差 (grade-separated crossing). Opposite: 平面 (plane, flat surface).","examples":[{"japanese":"円柱や球のような立体の体積を求める。","romaji":"Enchū ya kyū no yō na rittai no taiseki o motomeru.","german":"We calculate the volume of solids such as cylinders and spheres.","furigana":"えんちゅう|きゅう|りったい|たいせき|もと"},{"japanese":"この絵は、まるで立体のように見える。","romaji":"Kono e wa, marude rittai no yō ni mieru.","german":"This picture looks almost three-dimensional.","furigana":"え|りったい|み"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"立方メートル (cubic meter), 立方体 (cube), 2の立方 (2 cubed). Homophone: 立法 (legislation).","examples":[{"japanese":"このプールには、約五百立方メートルの水が入る。","romaji":"Kono pūru ni wa, yaku gohyaku rippō mētoru no mizu ga hairu.","german":"This pool holds about 500 cubic meters of water.","furigana":"やくごひゃくりっぽう|みず|はい"},{"japanese":"三の立方は二十七だ。","romaji":"San no rippō wa nijūnana da.","german":"The cube of three is twenty-seven.","furigana":"さん|りっぽう|にじゅうなな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"As a する-verb: 試合をリードする (to lead in a game), 会話をリードする (to lead the conversation). 三点のリード (a three-point lead). Also: dog leash (犬のリード).","examples":[{"japanese":"日本チームが二点リードしている。","romaji":"Nihon chīmu ga niten rīdo shite iru.","german":"The Japanese team is leading by two points.","furigana":"にほん|にてん"},{"japanese":"公園では犬にリードを付けてください。","romaji":"Kōen de wa inu ni rīdo o tsukete kudasai.","german":"Please keep dogs on a leash in the park.","furigana":"こうえん|いぬ|つ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"As a する-verb: 町を略奪する (to plunder a town). 略奪者 (plunderer). Also written 掠奪.","examples":[{"japanese":"戦争中、多くの美術品が略奪された。","romaji":"Sensōchū, ōku no bijutsuhin ga ryakudatsu sareta.","german":"During the war, many works of art were plundered.","furigana":"せんそうちゅう|おお|びじゅつひん|りゃくだつ"},{"japanese":"暴動の後、商店街では略奪が相次いだ。","romaji":"Bōdō no ato, shōtengai de wa ryakudatsu ga aitsuida.","german":"After the riot, looting occurred one after another in the shopping street.","furigana":"ぼうどう|あと|しょうてんがい|りゃくだつ|あいつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Sea area under state sovereignty (generally up to 12 nautical miles): 領海侵犯 (violation of territorial waters). Cf. 領土 (national territory), 領空 (airspace). Homophone: 了解 (understood).","examples":[{"japanese":"外国の漁船が日本の領海に入った。","romaji":"Gaikoku no gyosen ga Nihon no ryōkai ni haitta.","german":"A foreign fishing boat entered Japanese territorial waters.","furigana":"がいこく|ぎょせん|にほん|りょうかい|はい"},{"japanese":"領海の外では、この法律は適用されない。","romaji":"Ryōkai no soto de wa, kono hōritsu wa tekiyō sarenai.","german":"This law does not apply outside territorial waters.","furigana":"りょうかい|そと|ほうりつ|てきよう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"両極端 (two extremes), 両極化 (polarization). Geography: 北極と南極 (North and South Pole); electricity: プラス極とマイナス極 (positive and negative poles).","examples":[{"japanese":"二人の意見は両極に分かれた。","romaji":"Futari no iken wa ryōkyoku ni wakareta.","german":"The opinions of the two were polarized at opposite extremes.","furigana":"ふたり|いけん|りょうきょく|わ"},{"japanese":"地球の両極では、氷が解け続けている。","romaji":"Chikyū no ryōkyoku de wa, kōri ga toketsuzukete iru.","german":"At both of the Earth's poles, the ice keeps on melting.","furigana":"ちきゅう|りょうきょく|こおり|と|つづ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mainly historical: the land of a feudal lord or state. 領地を治める (to rule a territory). For present-day states, 領土 is usually used.","examples":[{"japanese":"大名はそれぞれ自分の領地を治めていた。","romaji":"Daimyō wa sorezore jibun no ryōchi o osamete ita.","german":"Each daimyo ruled over his own domain.","furigana":"だいみょう|じぶん|りょうち|おさ"},{"japanese":"戦いに勝った国は、相手の領地の一部を手に入れた。","romaji":"Tatakai ni katta kuni wa, aite no ryōchi no ichibu o te ni ireta.","german":"The victorious country gained part of the enemy's territory.","furigana":"たたか|か|くに|あいて|りょうち|いちぶ|て|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Formal, mainly in transportation: 旅客機 (passenger aircraft), 旅客船 (passenger ship). Also read りょきゃく. In everyday speech: 乗客.","examples":[{"japanese":"この空港は、毎日数万人の旅客に利用されている。","romaji":"Kono kūkō wa, mainichi sūmannin no ryokaku ni riyō sarete iru.","german":"This airport is used by tens of thousands of passengers every day.","furigana":"くうこう|まいにちすうまんにん|りょかく|りよう"},{"japanese":"大型の旅客機が滑走路に着陸した。","romaji":"Ōgata no ryokakuki ga kassōro ni chakuriku shita.","german":"A large passenger plane landed on the runway.","furigana":"おおがた|りょかくき|かっそうろ|ちゃくりく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"他に類のない (unique, without equal), 類を見ない (unprecedented). As a suffix: 哺乳類 (mammals), 書類 (documents). Proverb: 類は友を呼ぶ (birds of a feather flock together).","examples":[{"japanese":"「類は友を呼ぶ」と言うように、彼の友達はみんな釣り好きだ。","romaji":"\"Rui wa tomo o yobu\" to iu yō ni, kare no tomodachi wa minna tsurizuki da.","german":"As the saying goes, \"birds of a feather flock together\": all his friends love fishing.","furigana":"るい|とも|よ|い|かれ|ともだち|つ|ず"},{"japanese":"これは世界でも類を見ない大規模な工事だ。","romaji":"Kore wa sekai de mo rui o minai daikibo na kōji da.","german":"This is a large-scale construction project unparalleled even in the world.","furigana":"せかい|るい|み|だいきぼ|こうじ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"As a する-verb: 〜から類推する (to infer by analogy from ...). In language learning: 知っている漢字から意味を類推する (to infer the meaning from kanji one knows).","examples":[{"japanese":"知っている漢字から、知らない言葉の意味を類推した。","romaji":"Shitte iru kanji kara, shiranai kotoba no imi o ruisui shita.","german":"From the kanji I knew, I inferred the meaning of the unknown word.","furigana":"し|かんじ|し|ことば|いみ|るいすい"},{"japanese":"過去の例からの類推だけで判断するのは危険だ。","romaji":"Kako no rei kara no ruisui dake de handan suru no wa kiken da.","german":"It is dangerous to judge only by analogy with past cases.","furigana":"かこ|れい|るいすい|はんだん|きけん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mainly sloppy with time, money or promises: 時間にルーズな人 (someone who isn't strict about punctuality). For clothing: loose-fitting, baggy. な-adjective.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は時間にルーズで、いつも待ち合わせに遅れてくる。","romaji":"Kare wa jikan ni rūzu de, itsumo machiawase ni okurete kuru.","german":"He is lax about time and always arrives late to meetings.","furigana":"かれ|じかん|ま|あ|おく"},{"japanese":"お金にルーズな人には、お金を貸さないほうがいい。","romaji":"Okane ni rūzu na hito ni wa, okane o kasanai hō ga ii.","german":"You'd better not lend money to people who are careless with money.","furigana":"かね|ひと|かね|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"As a する-verb: 冷蔵する (to store cold). On packaging: 要冷蔵 (keep refrigerated). 冷蔵庫 (refrigerator); cf. 冷凍 (freezing).","examples":[{"japanese":"開封後は冷蔵して、早めにお召し上がりください。","romaji":"Kaifūgo wa reizō shite, hayame ni omeshiagari kudasai.","german":"After opening, please keep refrigerated and eat soon.","furigana":"かいふうご|れいぞう|はや|め|あ"},{"japanese":"この商品は冷蔵で配達されます。","romaji":"Kono shōhin wa reizō de haitatsu saremasu.","german":"This product is delivered refrigerated.","furigana":"しょうひん|れいぞう|はいたつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"レギュラーになる (to become a regular player), レギュラー番組 (regular program), ガソリンのレギュラー (regular gasoline; contrast: ハイオク).","examples":[{"japanese":"息子は、やっとサッカー部のレギュラーになった。","romaji":"Musuko wa, yatto sakkābu no regyurā ni natta.","german":"My son has finally become a regular on the soccer team.","furigana":"むすこ|ぶ"},{"japanese":"レギュラーを満タンでお願いします。","romaji":"Regyurā o mantan de onegai shimasu.","german":"Fill it up with regular, please.","furigana":"まん|ねが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"自動車レース (car race), レースに出る (to take part in a race). Homophone: レース (lace, as a fabric).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼はマラソンのレースで三位に入った。","romaji":"Kare wa marason no rēsu de san'i ni haitta.","german":"He came in third in the marathon race.","furigana":"かれ|さんい|はい"},{"japanese":"週末にヨットのレースを見に行った。","romaji":"Shūmatsu ni yotto no rēsu o mi ni itta.","german":"On the weekend, we went to watch a yacht race.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|み|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mainly for music, sports and languages outside of school: ピアノのレッスン, レッスンを受ける (to take lessons). For lessons at school: 授業.","examples":[{"japanese":"毎週土曜日にピアノのレッスンを受けている。","romaji":"Maishū doyōbi ni piano no ressun o ukete iru.","german":"I take piano lessons every Saturday.","furigana":"まいしゅうどようび|う"},{"japanese":"英会話のレッスンは一回四十分です。","romaji":"Eikaiwa no ressun wa ikkai yonjuppun desu.","german":"An English conversation lesson is 40 minutes each.","furigana":"えいかいわ|いっかいよんじゅっぷん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Polite or humorous: 立派なレディー (a proper young lady), レディーファースト (ladies first). Homophone: レディー (ready, e.g. レディー・ゴー).","examples":[{"japanese":"娘もすっかり立派なレディーになった。","romaji":"Musume mo sukkari rippa na redī ni natta.","german":"My daughter has really become a fine young lady.","furigana":"むすめ|りっぱ"},{"japanese":"彼はいつもレディーファーストを心がけている。","romaji":"Kare wa itsumo redīfāsuto o kokorogakete iru.","german":"He always makes a point of observing ladies first.","furigana":"かれ|こころ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Lever on machines or vehicles: レバーを引く (to pull a lever). Homophone: レバー (liver as food, e.g. レバニラ炒め).","examples":[{"japanese":"非常時にはこのレバーを引いてください。","romaji":"Hijōji ni wa kono rebā o hiite kudasai.","german":"In an emergency, please pull this lever.","furigana":"ひじょうじ|ひ"},{"japanese":"運転手はレバーを操作して、ドアを開けた。","romaji":"Untenshu wa rebā o sōsa shite, doa o aketa.","german":"The driver operated a lever and opened the door.","furigana":"うんてんしゅ|そうさ|あ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 35 (source: N4; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"三連休 (three days off in a row), 大型連休 (a longer holiday period such as Golden Week), 連休明け (after the days off).","examples":[{"japanese":"連休に家族で温泉へ行った。","romaji":"Renkyū ni kazoku de onsen e itta.","german":"We went to a hot spring with the family over the long weekend.","furigana":"れんきゅう|かぞく|おんせん|い"},{"japanese":"連休明けは、電車がとても混む。","romaji":"Renkyūake wa, densha ga totemo komu.","german":"After the holidays, the trains are very crowded.","furigana":"れんきゅうあ|でんしゃ|こ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 31 (source: N4; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"Colloquially mostly the short form of 電子レンジ: レンジで温める (to heat in the microwave). Originally also a kitchen range (ガスレンジ).","examples":[{"japanese":"お弁当をレンジで温めた。","romaji":"Obentō o renji de atatameta.","german":"I heated my lunch box in the microwave.","furigana":"べんとう|あたた"},{"japanese":"この容器はレンジでは使えません。","romaji":"Kono yōki wa renji de wa tsukaemasen.","german":"This container cannot be used in a microwave.","furigana":"ようき|つか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"レンタカーを借りる (to rent a car). Also used for the rental business itself: レンタカー会社, レンタカー屋 (car rental company).","examples":[{"japanese":"沖縄ではレンタカーを借りて島を回った。","romaji":"Okinawa de wa rentakā o karite shima o mawatta.","german":"On Okinawa, we rented a car and toured the island.","furigana":"おきなわ|か|しま|まわ"},{"japanese":"レンタカーを返す前に、ガソリンを満タンにした。","romaji":"Rentakā o kaesu mae ni, gasorin o mantan ni shita.","german":"I filled up the tank before returning the rental car.","furigana":"かえ|まえ|まん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Expressive reading aloud of literature: 詩の朗読 (poem recitation), 朗読会 (reading event). As a する-verb: 朗読する. For plain reading out: 読み上げる.","examples":[{"japanese":"授業で、生徒が一人ずつ詩を朗読した。","romaji":"Jugyō de, seito ga hitori zutsu shi o rōdoku shita.","german":"In class, the students recited a poem one by one.","furigana":"じゅぎょう|せいと|ひとり|し|ろうどく"},{"japanese":"俳優による小説の朗読がラジオで放送された。","romaji":"Haiyū ni yoru shōsetsu no rōdoku ga rajio de hōsō sareta.","german":"A reading of the novel by an actor was broadcast on the radio.","furigana":"はいゆう|しょうせつ|ろうどく|ほうそう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Only with negation: ろくなことがない (nothing good), ろくな物を食べていない (not eating anything decent). Related: ろくに〜ない (hardly, not properly). Mostly written in kana.","examples":[{"japanese":"最近は忙しくて、ろくな物を食べていない。","romaji":"Saikin wa isogashikute, rokuna mono o tabete inai.","german":"I've been so busy lately that I haven't eaten anything decent.","furigana":"さいきん|いそが|もの|た"},{"japanese":"嘘ばかりついていると、ろくなことにならないよ。","romaji":"Uso bakari tsuite iru to, rokuna koto ni naranai yo.","german":"If you keep telling lies, nothing good will come of it.","furigana":"うそ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A thick rope: ロープを張る (to stretch a rope), ロープで縛る (to tie with a rope). Thinner: ひも, 縄.","examples":[{"japanese":"ボートをロープで岸につないだ。","romaji":"Bōto o rōpu de kishi ni tsunaida.","german":"We tied the boat to the shore with a rope.","furigana":"きし"},{"japanese":"立ち入り禁止の場所にはロープが張ってある。","romaji":"Tachiiri kinshi no basho ni wa rōpu ga hatte aru.","german":"Ropes are stretched across off-limits areas.","furigana":"た|い|きんし|ばしょ|は"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Aerial cable car in mountains or tourist areas; also written ロープウエー. Similar: ケーブルカー (funicular).","examples":[{"japanese":"ロープウェイに乗って山頂まで行った。","romaji":"Rōpuwei ni notte sanchō made itta.","german":"We took the ropeway to the summit.","furigana":"の|さんちょう|い"},{"japanese":"強風のため、ロープウェイは運休している。","romaji":"Kyōfū no tame, rōpuwei wa unkyū shite iru.","german":"The ropeway is not running because of strong winds.","furigana":"きょうふう|うんきゅう"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"ロマンチックな雰囲気 (romantic atmosphere). Also written ロマンティック. A romantic person is a ロマンチスト. な-adjective.","examples":[{"japanese":"夜景の見えるロマンチックなレストランで食事をした。","romaji":"Yakei no mieru romanchikku na resutoran de shokuji o shita.","german":"We had dinner at a romantic restaurant with a view of the night scenery.","furigana":"やけい|み|しょくじ"},{"japanese":"彼には意外とロマンチックなところがある。","romaji":"Kare ni wa igai to romanchikku na tokoro ga aru.","german":"He has a surprisingly romantic side.","furigana":"かれ|いがい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Formal, often for public debates: 論議を呼ぶ (to provoke debate), 論議を尽くす (to discuss thoroughly). Similar: 議論.","examples":[{"japanese":"新しい法案は国会で大きな論議を呼んだ。","romaji":"Atarashii hōan wa kokkai de ōkina rongi o yonda.","german":"The new bill provoked a great debate in the Diet.","furigana":"あたら|ほうあん|こっかい|おお|ろんぎ|よ"},{"japanese":"十分な論議がないまま、計画が決まってしまった。","romaji":"Jūbun na rongi ga nai mama, keikaku ga kimatte shimatta.","german":"The plan was decided without sufficient discussion.","furigana":"じゅうぶん|ろんぎ|けいかく|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"窓の枠 (window frame), 枠内 / 枠外 (inside / outside the frame), 予算の枠 (budget limit), 枠にとらわれない (unconventional). Homophones: 沸く, 湧く.","examples":[{"japanese":"答えは枠の中に書いてください。","romaji":"Kotae wa waku no naka ni kaite kudasai.","german":"Please write your answer inside the box.","furigana":"こた|わく|なか|か"},{"japanese":"予算の枠を超えないように注意している。","romaji":"Yosan no waku o koenai yō ni chūi shite iru.","german":"We are careful not to exceed the budget limit.","furigana":"よさん|わく|こ|ちゅうい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Birds such as swallows (ツバメ) or wild geese (ガン) that move on with the seasons.","examples":[{"japanese":"秋になると、渡り鳥が南へ飛んでいく。","romaji":"Aki ni naru to, wataridori ga minami e tonde iku.","german":"In autumn, migratory birds fly south.","furigana":"あき|わた|どり|みなみ|と"},{"japanese":"この湖には、冬になると多くの渡り鳥がやって来る。","romaji":"Kono mizuumi ni wa, fuyu ni naru to ōku no wataridori ga yatte kuru.","german":"Many migratory birds come to this lake in winter.","furigana":"みずうみ|ふゆ|おお|わた|どり|く"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Unit of electrical power (symbol W): 百ワットの電球 (a 100-watt light bulb). Homophone: わっと (suddenly and loudly, e.g. わっと泣き出す).","examples":[{"japanese":"この電子レンジは最大千ワットまで出せる。","romaji":"Kono denshi renji wa saidai sen watto made daseru.","german":"This microwave can put out up to 1000 watts.","furigana":"でんし|さいだいせん|だ"},{"japanese":"部屋の電球を六十ワットから四十ワットに替えた。","romaji":"Heya no denkyū o rokujū watto kara yonjū watto ni kaeta.","german":"I changed the light bulb in the room from 60 watts to 40 watts.","furigana":"へや|でんきゅう|ろくじゅう|よんじゅう|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Formal, mostly with お: お詫び申し上げます (we offer our sincere apologies), お詫びの品 (a gift as an apology), 詫びを入れる (to apologize). Verb: 詫びる. Homophone: 侘び (rustic simplicity).","examples":[{"japanese":"遅れたことについて、上司に詫びを入れた。","romaji":"Okureta koto ni tsuite, jōshi ni wabi o ireta.","german":"I apologized to my boss for being late.","furigana":"おく|じょうし|わ|い"},{"japanese":"ご迷惑をおかけしたことを、心よりお詫び申し上げます。","romaji":"Gomeiwaku o okake shita koto o, kokoro yori owabi mōshiagemasu.","german":"We sincerely apologize for the inconvenience caused.","furigana":"めいわく|こころ|わ|もう|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Text in the Japanese language, especially as opposed to a foreign language: 和文英訳 (translation from Japanese into English). Counterparts: 欧文, 英文.","examples":[{"japanese":"次の和文を英語に訳しなさい。","romaji":"Tsugi no wabun o eigo ni yakushinasai.","german":"Translate the following Japanese text into English.","furigana":"つぎ|わぶん|えいご|やく"},{"japanese":"契約書は、英文と和文の二種類が用意されている。","romaji":"Keiyakusho wa, eibun to wabun no nishurui ga yōi sarete iru.","german":"The contract is prepared in two versions, English and Japanese.","furigana":"けいやくしょ|えいぶん|わぶん|にしゅるい|ようい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Often written in kana: わら. 藁葺き屋根 (thatched roof). Proverb: 溺れる者は藁をもつかむ (a drowning man will clutch at a straw).","examples":[{"japanese":"昔の農家は、藁で屋根をふいていた。","romaji":"Mukashi no nōka wa, wara de yane o fuite ita.","german":"In the old days, farmers thatched their roofs with straw.","furigana":"むかし|のうか|わら|やね"},{"japanese":"藁をもつかむ思いで、彼は占い師に相談した。","romaji":"Wara o mo tsukamu omoi de, kare wa uranaishi ni sōdan shita.","german":"Clutching at straws, he consulted a fortune-teller.","furigana":"わら|おも|かれ|うらな|し|そうだん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The bad guy in stories: 悪者を退治する (to defeat the villain). Figurative: 悪者にされる (to be made the scapegoat).","examples":[{"japanese":"昔話では、最後に悪者が退治される。","romaji":"Mukashibanashi de wa, saigo ni warumono ga taiji sareru.","german":"In folk tales, the villain is defeated in the end.","furigana":"むかしばなし|さいご|わるもの|たいじ"},{"japanese":"何も悪いことをしていないのに、私だけが悪者にされた。","romaji":"Nani mo warui koto o shite inai noni, watashi dake ga warumono ni sareta.","german":"Even though I hadn't done anything wrong, only I was made out to be the bad guy.","furigana":"なに|わる|わたし|わるもの"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A fixed way of carrying something out, often for technical or organizational systems: 支払い方式 (payment method), 選挙方式 (electoral system). For an individual approach, 方法 is more usual.","examples":[{"japanese":"会社は来年から新しい方式で給料を計算する。","romaji":"Kaisha wa rainen kara atarashii hōshiki de kyūryō o keisan suru.","german":"From next year, the company will calculate salaries using a new system.","furigana":"かいしゃ|らいねん|あたら|ほうしき|きゅうりょう|けいさん"},{"japanese":"この試験はマークシート方式で行われます。","romaji":"Kono shiken wa mākushīto hōshiki de okonawaremasu.","german":"This exam will be conducted in mark-sheet format.","furigana":"しけん|ほうしき|おこな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"As the verb 放出する: to give off energy, gas or heat (熱を放出する), or to release stockpiles or let players go (在庫を放出する).","examples":[{"japanese":"工場から有害なガスが放出されていた。","romaji":"Kōjō kara yūgai na gasu ga hōshutsu sarete ita.","german":"Harmful gases were being emitted from the factory.","furigana":"こうじょう|ゆうがい|ほうしゅつ"},{"japanese":"政府は備蓄していた米を市場に放出した。","romaji":"Seifu wa bichiku shite ita kome o shijō ni hōshutsu shita.","german":"The government released its stockpiled rice onto the market.","furigana":"せいふ|びちく|こめ|しじょう|ほうしゅつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The beginning of a text, speech or meeting: 冒頭で述べたように (as mentioned at the beginning). Homophone: 暴騰 (sharp price surge).","examples":[{"japanese":"会議の冒頭で、社長が挨拶をした。","romaji":"Kaigi no bōtō de, shachō ga aisatsu o shita.","german":"At the start of the meeting, the company president gave a greeting.","furigana":"かいぎ|ぼうとう|しゃちょう|あいさつ"},{"japanese":"小説の冒頭の一文がとても印象的だった。","romaji":"Shōsetsu no bōtō no ichibun ga totemo inshōteki datta.","german":"The opening sentence of the novel was very impressive.","furigana":"しょうせつ|ぼうとう|いちぶん|いんしょうてき"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A violent uprising of a crowd: 暴動が起きる (a riot breaks out), 暴動を鎮める (to put down a riot).","examples":[{"japanese":"物価の急な上昇に怒った市民が暴動を起こした。","romaji":"Bukka no kyū na jōshō ni okotta shimin ga bōdō o okoshita.","german":"Citizens angered by the sudden rise in prices started a riot.","furigana":"ぶっか|きゅう|じょうしょう|おこ|しみん|ぼうどう|お"},{"japanese":"警察は夜までに暴動を鎮めた。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa yoru made ni bōdō o shizumeta.","german":"The police put down the riot by evening.","furigana":"けいさつ|よる|ぼうどう|しず"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Very strong wind, mainly in weather reports: 暴風警報 (storm warning), 暴風雨 (storm with wind and rain). In everyday speech, 強い風 or 嵐 is more usual.","examples":[{"japanese":"台風の影響で、夜から暴風が吹き始めた。","romaji":"Taifū no eikyō de, yoru kara bōfū ga fukihajimeta.","german":"Because of the typhoon, a violent wind began to blow from the evening.","furigana":"たいふう|えいきょう|よる|ぼうふう|ふ|はじ"},{"japanese":"暴風のため、午後の便はすべて欠航になった。","romaji":"Bōfū no tame, gogo no bin wa subete kekkō ni natta.","german":"Because of the storm, all afternoon flights were cancelled.","furigana":"ぼうふう|ごご|びん|けっこう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive: to toss something into something carelessly or with a swing: かばんに放り込む. Counterpart: 放り出す (to throw out).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は脱いだ服を洗濯機に放り込んだ。","romaji":"Kare wa nuida fuku o sentakuki ni hōrikonda.","german":"He threw the clothes he had taken off into the washing machine.","furigana":"かれ|ぬ|ふく|せんたくき|ほう|こ"},{"japanese":"急いでいたので、書類をかばんに放り込んで家を出た。","romaji":"Isoide ita node, shorui o kaban ni hōrikonde ie o deta.","german":"I was in a hurry, so I stuffed the documents into my bag and left the house.","furigana":"いそ|しょるい|ほう|こ|いえ|で"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive: to throw or fling something out; figuratively to give up a task halfway: 仕事を途中で放り出す.","examples":[{"japanese":"急ブレーキで、トラックの荷台から荷物が道路に放り出された。","romaji":"Kyūburēki de, torakku no nidai kara nimotsu ga dōro ni hōridasareta.","german":"Because of the sudden braking, cargo was flung from the truck bed onto the road.","furigana":"きゅう|にだい|にもつ|どうろ|ほう|だ"},{"japanese":"彼は仕事を途中で放り出して、旅行に行ってしまった。","romaji":"Kare wa shigoto o tochū de hōridashite, ryokō ni itte shimatta.","german":"He dropped his work halfway and went off on a trip.","furigana":"かれ|しごと|とちゅう|ほう|だ|りょこう|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"As the verb 保温する: to keep something warm. Common with appliances: 炊飯器の保温 (keep-warm function of a rice cooker), 保温性 (heat retention).","examples":[{"japanese":"ご飯は炊飯器で保温しておいてね。","romaji":"Gohan wa suihanki de hoon shite oite ne.","german":"Please keep the rice warm in the rice cooker.","furigana":"はん|すいはんき|ほおん"},{"japanese":"このコップは保温に優れているので、コーヒーが冷めにくい。","romaji":"Kono koppu wa hoon ni sugurete iru node, kōhī ga samenikui.","german":"This cup has excellent heat retention, so coffee doesn't cool down easily.","furigana":"ほおん|すぐ|さ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"As the verb 補給する: to make up for what has been used: 水分を補給する (to take in fluids), 燃料の補給 (refueling).","examples":[{"japanese":"暑い日は、こまめに水分を補給してください。","romaji":"Atsui hi wa, komame ni suibun o hokyū shite kudasai.","german":"On hot days, please replenish your fluids frequently.","furigana":"あつ|ひ|すいぶん|ほきゅう"},{"japanese":"飛行機は途中の空港で燃料を補給した。","romaji":"Hikōki wa tochū no kūkō de nenryō o hokyū shita.","german":"The plane refueled at an airport along the way.","furigana":"ひこうき|とちゅう|くうこう|ねんりょう|ほきゅう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"As the verb 補強する: to make something sturdier: 壁を補強する (to reinforce a wall); also figuratively: チームを補強する (to strengthen a team).","examples":[{"japanese":"地震に備えて、古い校舎を補強した。","romaji":"Jishin ni sonaete, furui kōsha o hokyō shita.","german":"The old school building was reinforced in preparation for earthquakes.","furigana":"じしん|そな|ふる|こうしゃ|ほきょう"},{"japanese":"チームは新しい選手を入れて守備を補強した。","romaji":"Chīmu wa atarashii senshu o irete shubi o hokyō shita.","german":"The team strengthened its defense by bringing in new players.","furigana":"あたら|せんしゅ|い|しゅび|ほきょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The school or university one attended oneself: 母校を訪ねる (to visit one's old school). Also used for elementary and high schools, not only for universities.","examples":[{"japanese":"卒業して十年ぶりに母校を訪ねた。","romaji":"Sotsugyō shite jūnen buri ni bokō o tazuneta.","german":"I visited my old school for the first time in ten years since graduating.","furigana":"そつぎょう|じゅうねん|ぼこう|たず"},{"japanese":"母校の野球部が甲子園に出場することになった。","romaji":"Bokō no yakyūbu ga Kōshien ni shutsujō suru koto ni natta.","german":"The baseball team of my old school will take part in Koshien.","furigana":"ぼこう|やきゅうぶ|こうしえん|しゅつじょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The country where one was born and raised, mainly from the viewpoint of people abroad: 母国に帰る (to return to one's home country). Cf. 母国語 (native language).","examples":[{"japanese":"留学を終えたら、母国に帰って教師になるつもりです。","romaji":"Ryūgaku o oetara, bokoku ni kaette kyōshi ni naru tsumori desu.","german":"When I finish my studies abroad, I intend to return to my home country and become a teacher.","furigana":"りゅうがく|お|ぼこく|かえ|きょうし"},{"japanese":"彼女は母国の料理を友達にふるまった。","romaji":"Kanojo wa bokoku no ryōri o tomodachi ni furumatta.","german":"She treated her friends to dishes from her home country.","furigana":"かのじょ|ぼこく|りょうり|ともだち"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"From English: position in sports (ポジションを守る, to hold one's position) or in an organization. At work, 地位 or 役職 are often used as well.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼のサッカーでのポジションはゴールキーパーだ。","romaji":"Kare no sakkā de no pojishon wa gōrukīpā da.","german":"His position in soccer is goalkeeper.","furigana":"かれ"},{"japanese":"彼女は会社で重要なポジションに就いている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kaisha de jūyō na pojishon ni tsuite iru.","german":"She holds an important position in the company.","furigana":"かのじょ|かいしゃ|じゅうよう|つ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Things dried in the sun, mainly laundry: 干し物を取り込む (to bring in the laundry). Today 洗濯物 is usually said. Not to be confused with 干物【ひもの】 (dried fish); the spelling 乾物 is usually read かんぶつ (dried foods).","examples":[{"japanese":"雨が降りそうだから、干し物を取り込んでおいて。","romaji":"Ame ga furisō dakara, hoshimono o torikonde oite.","german":"It looks like rain, so please bring in the laundry.","furigana":"あめ|ふ|ほ|もの|と|こ"},{"japanese":"ベランダに干し物がたくさん並んでいる。","romaji":"Beranda ni hoshimono ga takusan narande iru.","german":"A lot of laundry is hanging in a row on the balcony.","furigana":"ほ|もの|なら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Water or garden hose: ホースで水をまく (to water with a hose). A different word that sounds the same: ホース (English \"horse\").","examples":[{"japanese":"庭の花にホースで水をまいた。","romaji":"Niwa no hana ni hōsu de mizu o maita.","german":"I watered the flowers in the garden with a hose.","furigana":"にわ|はな|みず"},{"japanese":"洗濯機のホースが外れて、床が水浸しになった。","romaji":"Sentakuki no hōsu ga hazurete, yuka ga mizubitashi ni natta.","german":"The washing machine hose came off, and the floor was flooded.","furigana":"せんたくき|はず|ゆか|みずびた"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"ポーズ is first of all a body posture, for example for a photo: ポーズを取る or ポーズを決める \"to pose\". Figuratively it means a feigned attitude, an \"act\": ただのポーズ, ポーズにすぎない. Written the same but derived from English \"pause\", ポーズ also means \"pause, break\" when playing back media (ポーズボタン) or when speaking (ポーズを置く \"to pause briefly\"). For a break at work or school, on the other hand, 休憩 or 休み is used, not ポーズ.","examples":[{"japanese":"写真を撮るから、何かポーズを取って。","romaji":"Shashin o toru kara, nanika pōzu o totte.","german":"I'm going to take a photo, so strike a pose.","furigana":"しゃしん|と|なに|と"},{"japanese":"彼が反省しているのは、ただのポーズにすぎない。","romaji":"Kare ga hansei shite iru no wa, tada no pōzu ni suginai.","german":"His showing remorse is nothing but an act.","furigana":"かれ|はんせい"},{"japanese":"スピーチでは、大事なことを言う前に少しポーズを置くと効果的です。","romaji":"Supiichi de wa, daiji na koto o iu mae ni sukoshi poozu o oku to koukateki desu.","german":"In a speech, it is effective to pause briefly before saying something important.","furigana":"だいじ|い|まえ|すこ|お|こうかてき"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A sudden onset of illness: 発作が起きる (an attack occurs), 心臓発作 (heart attack), 喘息の発作 (asthma attack). Note the reading: ほっさ, not はっさ.","examples":[{"japanese":"祖父は夜中に心臓の発作を起こして入院した。","romaji":"Sofu wa yonaka ni shinzō no hossa o okoshite nyūin shita.","german":"My grandfather had a heart attack in the middle of the night and was hospitalized.","furigana":"そふ|よなか|しんぞう|ほっさ|お|にゅういん"},{"japanese":"喘息の発作が起きたときのために、いつも薬を持っている。","romaji":"Zensoku no hossa ga okita toki no tame ni, itsumo kusuri o motte iru.","german":"I always carry medicine in case I have an asthma attack.","furigana":"ぜんそく|ほっさ|お|くすり|も"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"As the verb 発足する: an organization, body or government starts its work: 新政権が発足した (a new government was inaugurated). Also read はっそく.","examples":[{"japanese":"新しい委員会が来月発足する予定だ。","romaji":"Atarashii iinkai ga raigetsu hossoku suru yotei da.","german":"A new committee is scheduled to be set up next month.","furigana":"あたら|いいんかい|らいげつほっそく|よてい"},{"japanese":"この団体は発足から今年で五十年になる。","romaji":"Kono dantai wa hossoku kara kotoshi de gojūnen ni naru.","german":"This year marks fifty years since this organization was founded.","furigana":"だんたい|ほっそく|ことし|ごじゅうねん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Usually ほっとする: to feel relieved, to breathe easy. ほっと一息つく = to take a short breather. Not to be confused with ホット (hot).","examples":[{"japanese":"試験に合格したと聞いて、ほっとした。","romaji":"Shiken ni gōkaku shita to kiite, hotto shita.","german":"I felt relieved when I heard that I had passed the exam.","furigana":"しけん|ごうかく|き"},{"japanese":"子どもが無事に帰ってきて、母親はほっと胸をなで下ろした。","romaji":"Kodomo ga buji ni kaette kite, hahaoya wa hotto mune o nadeoroshita.","german":"When the child came home safely, the mother breathed a sigh of relief.","furigana":"こ|ぶじ|かえ|ははおや|むね|お"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mainly for warm drinks: ホットコーヒー; in a cafe, simply ホット (hot coffee) as opposed to アイス. Not to be confused with ほっと (relieved).","examples":[{"japanese":"「コーヒーはホットとアイス、どちらになさいますか。」「ホットでお願いします。」","romaji":"\"Kōhī wa hotto to aisu, dochira ni nasaimasu ka.\" \"Hotto de onegai shimasu.\"","german":"\"Would you like your coffee hot or iced?\" \"Hot, please.\"","furigana":"ねが"},{"japanese":"寒い日は、ホットのミルクティーが飲みたくなる。","romaji":"Samui hi wa, hotto no miruku tī ga nomitaku naru.","german":"On cold days, I feel like drinking hot milk tea.","furigana":"さむ|ひ|の"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"A teapot or coffee pot (ティーポット), and in Japanese households often the electric hot water dispenser (電気ポット) or thermos flask.","examples":[{"japanese":"ポットにお湯が入っているから、自由にお茶を飲んでね。","romaji":"Potto ni oyu ga haitte iru kara, jiyū ni ocha o nonde ne.","german":"There is hot water in the pot, so help yourself to tea.","furigana":"ゆ|はい|じゆう|ちゃ|の"},{"japanese":"紅茶の葉をポットに入れて、三分待った。","romaji":"Kōcha no ha o potto ni irete, sanpun matta.","german":"I put tea leaves in the pot and waited three minutes.","furigana":"こうちゃ|は|い|さんぷんま"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Colloquial for 頬【ほお】, mostly in kana; in child language also ほっぺ. ほっぺたが落ちる = so delicious that your cheeks fall off.","examples":[{"japanese":"赤ちゃんのほっぺたはとてもやわらかい。","romaji":"Akachan no hoppeta wa totemo yawarakai.","german":"Babies' cheeks are very soft.","furigana":"あか"},{"japanese":"このケーキ、ほっぺたが落ちるほどおいしい！","romaji":"Kono kēki, hoppeta ga ochiru hodo oishii!","german":"This cake is so delicious it melts in your mouth!","furigana":"お"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Like ぼちぼち: slowly but steadily (ぼつぼつ客が来始めた) or \"about time\" when it is becoming time for something: ぼつぼつ帰ろうか. As a noun also \"dots, pimples\".","examples":[{"japanese":"開店して一時間たつと、ぼつぼつ客が入り始めた。","romaji":"Kaiten shite ichijikan tatsu to, botsubotsu kyaku ga hairihajimeta.","german":"An hour after opening, customers gradually started to come in.","furigana":"かいてん|いちじかん|きゃく|はい|はじ"},{"japanese":"もう遅いから、ぼつぼつ帰りましょうか。","romaji":"Mō osoi kara, botsubotsu kaerimashō ka.","german":"It's already late, so shall we start heading home?","furigana":"おそ|かえ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Colloquial: to complain under one's breath in a rather resigned way, often about trifles: 仕事が多いとぼやく (to grumble about having too much work).","examples":[{"japanese":"父は「最近、物価が高すぎる」とぼやいている。","romaji":"Chichi wa \"saikin, bukka ga takasugiru\" to boyaite iru.","german":"My father is grumbling that prices are far too high these days.","furigana":"ちち|さいきん|ぶっか|たか"},{"japanese":"ぼやいてばかりいないで、少しは手伝ってよ。","romaji":"Boyaite bakari inaide, sukoshi wa tetsudatte yo.","german":"Stop grumbling all the time and help out a little.","furigana":"すこ|てつだ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Intransitive: outlines, images or memories become indistinct: 写真がぼやける, 記憶がぼやける.","examples":[{"japanese":"眼鏡を外すと、文字がぼやけて読めない。","romaji":"Megane o hazusu to, moji ga boyakete yomenai.","german":"Without my glasses, the letters blur and I can't read them.","furigana":"めがね|はず|もじ|よ"},{"japanese":"手が動いたせいで、写真がぼやけてしまった。","romaji":"Te ga ugoita sei de, shashin ga boyakete shimatta.","german":"The photo came out blurry because my hand moved.","furigana":"て|うご|しゃしん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Rest for body and mind: 温泉で保養する (to recuperate at a hot spring), 保養地 (health resort). Idiom: 目の保養 (a feast for the eyes).","examples":[{"japanese":"祖母は毎年、温泉地で一週間ほど保養している。","romaji":"Sobo wa maitoshi, onsenchi de isshūkan hodo hoyō shite iru.","german":"Every year my grandmother spends about a week recuperating at a hot spring resort.","furigana":"そぼ|まいとし|おんせんち|いっしゅうかん|ほよう"},{"japanese":"きれいな庭を見て、いい目の保養になった。","romaji":"Kirei na niwa o mite, ii me no hoyō ni natta.","german":"Looking at the beautiful garden was a treat for the eyes.","furigana":"にわ|み|め|ほよう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A large hall for events: コンサートホール. In the restaurant business, the dining room; someone who waits tables works in the ホール (as opposed to the kitchen). Homophone: ホール (a hole in golf).","examples":[{"japanese":"卒業式は市のホールで行われた。","romaji":"Sotsugyōshiki wa shi no hōru de okonawareta.","german":"The graduation ceremony was held in the city hall.","furigana":"そつぎょうしき|し|おこな"},{"japanese":"レストランのアルバイトで、キッチンではなくホールを担当している。","romaji":"Resutoran no arubaito de, kitchin de wa naku hōru o tantō shite iru.","german":"In my part-time job at the restaurant, I work on the floor rather than in the kitchen.","furigana":"たんとう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Unit of electrical voltage: 百ボルト. In Japan, household outlets supply 100 volts. A different word that sounds the same: ボルト (bolt).","examples":[{"japanese":"日本の家庭用コンセントの電圧は百ボルトだ。","romaji":"Nihon no kateiyō konsento no den'atsu wa hyaku boruto da.","german":"The voltage of household outlets in Japan is 100 volts.","furigana":"にほん|かていよう|でんあつ|ひゃく"},{"japanese":"この充電器は二百四十ボルトまで対応している。","romaji":"Kono jūdenki wa nihyaku yonjū boruto made taiō shite iru.","german":"This charger supports up to 240 volts.","furigana":"じゅうでんき|にひゃくよんじゅう|たいおう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"One's own country, mainly from the viewpoint of diplomats, companies or people abroad: 本国の政府 (the government of one's home country), 本国に戻る (to return home). Cf. 母国 (homeland, more personal).","examples":[{"japanese":"大使は本国の政府と連絡を取った。","romaji":"Taishi wa hongoku no seifu to renraku o totta.","german":"The ambassador contacted the government of his home country.","furigana":"たいし|ほんごく|せいふ|れんらく|と"},{"japanese":"その会社は日本から撤退し、社員は本国に戻った。","romaji":"Sono kaisha wa Nihon kara tettai shi, shain wa hongoku ni modotta.","german":"The company withdrew from Japan, and the employees returned to their home country.","furigana":"かいしゃ|にほん|てったい|しゃいん|ほんごく|もど"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The device itself as opposed to accessories or packaging: パソコン本体 (the computer unit). 本体価格 = price excluding tax. Further meanings: \"true form, real nature\" (本体を現す, to reveal one's true form) and the main object of worship enshrined in a shrine or temple.","examples":[{"japanese":"箱の中には本体と説明書しか入っていなかった。","romaji":"Hako no naka ni wa hontai to setsumeisho shika haitte inakatta.","german":"The box contained only the unit itself and the manual.","furigana":"はこ|なか|ほんたい|せつめいしょ|はい"},{"japanese":"カメラの本体は軽いが、レンズがかなり重い。","romaji":"Kamera no hontai wa karui ga, renzu ga kanari omoi.","german":"The camera body is light, but the lens is quite heavy.","furigana":"ほんたい|かる|おも"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The place where something comes from or is done best: 本場のイタリア料理 (authentic Italian cuisine), 本場の英語 (authentic English).","examples":[{"japanese":"本場のフランス料理を食べに、パリへ行った。","romaji":"Honba no Furansu ryōri o tabe ni, Pari e itta.","german":"I went to Paris to eat authentic French cuisine.","furigana":"ほんば|りょうり|た|い"},{"japanese":"彼は本場で料理を学ぶためにイタリアに渡った。","romaji":"Kare wa honba de ryōri o manabu tame ni Itaria ni watatta.","german":"He went to Italy to learn cooking at the source.","furigana":"かれ|ほんば|りょうり|まな|わた"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A device for moving liquids or air: ポンプで水をくみ上げる (to pump up water).","examples":[{"japanese":"井戸の水をポンプでくみ上げている。","romaji":"Ido no mizu o ponpu de kumiagete iru.","german":"Water is drawn from the well with a pump.","furigana":"いど|みず|あ"},{"japanese":"自転車のタイヤにポンプで空気を入れた。","romaji":"Jitensha no taiya ni ponpu de kūki o ireta.","german":"I pumped air into the bicycle tire with a pump.","furigana":"じてんしゃ|くうき|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"As the verb 埋蔵する, mostly in the passive: 石油が埋蔵されている (there are oil deposits). 埋蔵量 = reserves, 埋蔵金 = buried treasure.","examples":[{"japanese":"この地域の地下には大量の天然ガスが埋蔵されている。","romaji":"Kono chiiki no chika ni wa tairyō no tennen gasu ga maizō sarete iru.","german":"Large amounts of natural gas lie buried beneath this region.","furigana":"ちいき|ちか|たいりょう|てんねん|まいぞう"},{"japanese":"この城には、昔の殿様が小判を埋蔵したという伝説がある。","romaji":"Kono shiro ni wa, mukashi no tonosama ga koban o maizō shita to iu densetsu ga aru.","german":"There is a legend that a lord of old buried gold coins in this castle.","furigana":"しろ|むかし|とのさま|こばん|まいぞう|でんせつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Exactly vertically above something: 頭の真上 (directly above one's head). Opposite: 真下.","examples":[{"japanese":"棚の真上に時計が掛かっている。","romaji":"Tana no maue ni tokei ga kakatte iru.","german":"A clock hangs directly above the shelf.","furigana":"たな|まうえ|とけい|か"},{"japanese":"飛行機が家の真上を飛んでいった。","romaji":"Hikōki ga ie no maue o tonde itta.","german":"A plane flew right over our house.","furigana":"ひこうき|いえ|まうえ|と"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Sale of tickets before the day of the event: 前売り券 (advance ticket), 前売りで買う (to buy in advance). Often cheaper than tickets on the day (当日券).","examples":[{"japanese":"コンサートのチケットは前売りで買うと五百円安い。","romaji":"Konsāto no chiketto wa maeuri de kau to gohyaku en yasui.","german":"Concert tickets are 500 yen cheaper if bought in advance.","furigana":"まえう|か|ごひゃくえんやす"},{"japanese":"人気の映画なので、前売りはもう売り切れていた。","romaji":"Ninki no eiga na node, maeuri wa mō urikirete ita.","german":"The movie is popular, so advance tickets had already sold out.","furigana":"にんき|えいが|まえう|う|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Introductory words before the actual topic: 前置きが長い (to have a long-winded introduction). As a verb: 前置きする.","examples":[{"japanese":"前置きはこのくらいにして、本題に入りましょう。","romaji":"Maeoki wa kono kurai ni shite, hondai ni hairimashō.","german":"Enough of the preamble, let's get down to the main topic.","furigana":"まえお|ほんだい|はい"},{"japanese":"校長先生の話は前置きが長くて、みんな退屈していた。","romaji":"Kōchō sensei no hanashi wa maeoki ga nagakute, minna taikutsu shite ita.","german":"The principal's talk had such a long introduction that everyone was bored.","furigana":"こうちょうせんせい|はなし|まえお|なが|たいくつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive counterpart of 負ける: to defeat someone: 相手を負かす. Not to be confused with 任す (to entrust).","examples":[{"japanese":"弟は将棋で父を負かして大喜びだった。","romaji":"Otōto wa shōgi de chichi o makashite ōyorokobi datta.","german":"My little brother beat our father at shogi and was overjoyed.","furigana":"おとうと|しょうぎ|ちち|ま|おおよろこ"},{"japanese":"去年負けた相手を、今年は見事に負かした。","romaji":"Kyonen maketa aite o, kotoshi wa migoto ni makashita.","german":"This year we soundly beat the opponent we had lost to last year.","furigana":"きょねんま|あいて|ことし|みごと|ま"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"As a noun a sign or logo (星のマーク); as the verb マークする: to mark or underline something. In sports also \"to mark an opponent\".","examples":[{"japanese":"大事なところに赤でマークを付けておいた。","romaji":"Daiji na tokoro ni aka de māku o tsukete oita.","german":"I marked the important parts in red.","furigana":"だいじ|あか|つ"},{"japanese":"この店のマークは青い鳥だ。","romaji":"Kono mise no māku wa aoi tori da.","german":"This shop's logo is a blue bird.","furigana":"みせ|あお|とり"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 33 (source: N4; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"A thin layer or skin: 細胞膜 (cell membrane); a 膜 forms on warm milk. Homophones: 幕 (curtain), 巻く (to wind).","examples":[{"japanese":"温めた牛乳の表面に薄い膜ができた。","romaji":"Atatameta gyūnyū no hyōmen ni usui maku ga dekita.","german":"A thin skin formed on the surface of the heated milk.","furigana":"あたた|ぎゅうにゅう|ひょうめん|うす|まく"},{"japanese":"細胞は薄い膜に包まれている。","romaji":"Saibō wa usui maku ni tsutsumarete iru.","german":"Cells are surrounded by a thin membrane.","furigana":"さいぼう|うす|まく|つつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mostly in kana: 袖をまくる (to roll up one's sleeves). After the masu-stem, colloquial for doing something excessively: 食べまくる (to eat non-stop), 買いまくる (to buy like crazy). The kanji spelling 捲る is also read めくる (to turn over).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は袖をまくって、皿を洗い始めた。","romaji":"Kare wa sode o makutte, sara o araihajimeta.","german":"He rolled up his sleeves and began washing the dishes.","furigana":"かれ|そで|さら|あら|はじ"},{"japanese":"旅行中は毎日おいしいものを食べまくった。","romaji":"Ryokōchū wa mainichi oishii mono o tabemakutta.","german":"During the trip, I ate delicious food non-stop every day.","furigana":"りょこうちゅう|まいにち|た"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Honest, wholehearted sentiment: 真心を込めて (with all one's heart). Reading まごころ; the reading まこころ is obsolete.","examples":[{"japanese":"母は真心を込めて、手紙を書いた。","romaji":"Haha wa magokoro o komete, tegami o kaita.","german":"My mother wrote the letter with all her heart.","furigana":"はは|まごころ|こ|てがみ|か"},{"japanese":"彼女の真心のこもった看病のおかげで、父は元気になった。","romaji":"Kanojo no magokoro no komotta kanbyō no okage de, chichi wa genki ni natta.","german":"Thanks to her loving, devoted care, my father got well again.","furigana":"かのじょ|まごころ|かんびょう|ちち|げんき"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Intransitive, colloquial: to become unsure and not know what to do, for example in unfamiliar surroundings: 初めての駅でまごつく (to be at a loss at an unfamiliar station). Synonym: 戸惑う.","examples":[{"japanese":"初めて東京駅に行ったとき、出口が多くてまごついた。","romaji":"Hajimete Tōkyō eki ni itta toki, deguchi ga ōkute magotsuita.","german":"The first time I went to Tokyo Station, I was confused by the many exits.","furigana":"はじ|とうきょうえき|い|でぐち|おお"},{"japanese":"急に英語で質問されて、店員はまごついていた。","romaji":"Kyū ni eigo de shitsumon sarete, ten'in wa magotsuite ita.","german":"The clerk was flustered when suddenly asked a question in English.","furigana":"きゅう|えいご|しつもん|てんいん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Emphatic affirmation, similar to まさに: まさしくその通り (exactly so). Mostly in kana; the kanji spelling 正しく is otherwise read ただしく.","examples":[{"japanese":"これはまさしく私がなくした財布だ。","romaji":"Kore wa masashiku watashi ga nakushita saifu da.","german":"This is undoubtedly the wallet I lost.","furigana":"わたし|さいふ"},{"japanese":"彼の言うことは、まさしくその通りだと思う。","romaji":"Kare no iu koto wa, masashiku sono tōri da to omou.","german":"I think what he says is exactly right.","furigana":"かれ|い|とお|おも"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"な-adjective, mostly in kana: AよりBの方がましだ (B is still better than A). Expresses that something is not good, but less bad. As the suffix 〜増し also \"increase, surcharge\": 二割増し (20% more).","examples":[{"japanese":"何もしないより、少しでもやった方がましだ。","romaji":"Nani mo shinai yori, sukoshi demo yatta hō ga mashi da.","german":"Doing even a little is better than doing nothing.","furigana":"なに|すこ|ほう"},{"japanese":"昨日よりはましだけど、まだ頭が痛い。","romaji":"Kinō yori wa mashi dakedo, mada atama ga itai.","german":"It's better than yesterday, but my head still hurts.","furigana":"きのう|あたま|いた"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive, rather formal: 冗談を交えて話す (to talk with jokes mixed in), 専門家を交えて議論する (to discuss with experts taking part), 言葉を交える (to exchange a few words).","examples":[{"japanese":"先生は冗談を交えながら、わかりやすく説明してくれた。","romaji":"Sensei wa jōdan o majienagara, wakariyasuku setsumei shite kureta.","german":"The teacher explained it clearly, mixing in jokes.","furigana":"せんせい|じょうだん|まじ|せつめい"},{"japanese":"会議では、外部の専門家を交えて話し合った。","romaji":"Kaigi de wa, gaibu no senmonka o majiete hanashiatta.","german":"In the meeting, we discussed the matter with outside experts included.","furigana":"かいぎ|がいぶ|せんもんか|まじ|はな|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Exactly vertically below something: 橋の真下 (right under the bridge). Opposite: 真上.","examples":[{"japanese":"私の部屋の真下はコンビニになっている。","romaji":"Watashi no heya no mashita wa konbini ni natte iru.","german":"Right below my apartment there is a convenience store.","furigana":"わたし|へや|ました"},{"japanese":"橋の真下で雨宿りをした。","romaji":"Hashi no mashita de amayadori o shita.","german":"I took shelter from the rain right under the bridge.","furigana":"はし|ました|あまやど"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Intensifies a comparison: if A is already (not) so, then B all the more (not): 大人でも難しいのに、まして子どもには無理だ (it's hard even for adults, let alone children). Mostly in kana.","examples":[{"japanese":"日本人でも難しい漢字なのだから、まして外国人には読めないだろう。","romaji":"Nihonjin demo muzukashii kanji na no dakara, mashite gaikokujin ni wa yomenai darō.","german":"It's a kanji that is difficult even for Japanese people, so foreigners surely can't read it.","furigana":"にほんじん|むずか|かんじ|がいこくじん|よ"},{"japanese":"平日でも混んでいるのだから、まして週末はすごい人だろう。","romaji":"Heijitsu demo konde iru no dakara, mashite shūmatsu wa sugoi hito darō.","german":"It's crowded even on weekdays, so it must be packed on the weekend.","furigana":"へいじつ|こ|しゅうまつ|ひと"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Short for マスコミュニケーション; in everyday speech the media as a whole, often with a critical undertone: マスコミに騒がれる (to be hounded by the media). More neutral: メディア, 報道機関.","examples":[{"japanese":"事件の後、家の前にマスコミが大勢集まった。","romaji":"Jiken no ato, ie no mae ni masukomi ga ōzei atsumatta.","german":"After the incident, many reporters gathered in front of the house.","furigana":"じけん|あと|いえ|まえ|おおぜいあつ"},{"japanese":"その俳優の結婚はマスコミで大きく報道された。","romaji":"Sono haiyū no kekkon wa masukomi de ōkiku hōdō sareta.","german":"The actor's marriage was widely covered in the media.","furigana":"はいゆう|けっこん|おお|ほうどう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Intransitive, mostly in kana. With に: 馬にまたがる (to mount a horse), 自転車にまたがる (to straddle a bicycle). Figuratively: to extend over several areas: 三県にまたがる山 (a mountain spanning three prefectures).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は自転車にまたがって、坂を下りていった。","romaji":"Kare wa jitensha ni matagatte, saka o orite itta.","german":"He straddled his bicycle and rode down the slope.","furigana":"かれ|じてんしゃ|さか|お"},{"japanese":"この国立公園は三つの県にまたがっている。","romaji":"Kono kokuritsu kōen wa mittsu no ken ni matagatte iru.","german":"This national park extends across three prefectures.","furigana":"こくりつこうえん|みっ|けん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"An arranged meeting at a place: 待ち合わせ場所 (meeting place), 駅で待ち合わせをする (to meet at the station). Verb: 待ち合わせる.","examples":[{"japanese":"友達と駅の前で待ち合わせをしている。","romaji":"Tomodachi to eki no mae de machiawase o shite iru.","german":"I'm meeting a friend in front of the station.","furigana":"ともだち|えき|まえ|ま|あ"},{"japanese":"待ち合わせの時間に五分遅れてしまった。","romaji":"Machiawase no jikan ni gofun okurete shimatta.","german":"I was five minutes late for the meeting time.","furigana":"ま|あ|じかん|ごふんおく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mostly intransitive: 答えが間違っている (the answer is wrong). Common in 間違った (wrong) and 間違っても〜ない (never, under no circumstances). For \"to make a mistake, to mix up\", 間違える is more common.","examples":[{"japanese":"この計算はどこかが間違っている。","romaji":"Kono keisan wa dokoka ga machigatte iru.","german":"There is a mistake somewhere in this calculation.","furigana":"けいさん|まちが"},{"japanese":"間違った情報をネットに書かないように気をつけよう。","romaji":"Machigatta jōhō o netto ni kakanai yō ni ki o tsukeyō.","german":"Let's be careful not to post wrong information online.","furigana":"まちが|じょうほう|か|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"い-adjective: one can hardly wait for something: 夏休みが待ち遠しい (I can't wait for summer vacation). The subject is the thing awaited, not the person waiting.","examples":[{"japanese":"子どもたちはクリスマスが待ち遠しくてたまらない。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa Kurisumasu ga machidōshikute tamaranai.","german":"The children can hardly wait for Christmas.","furigana":"こ|ま|どお"},{"japanese":"遠くに住む孫に会える日が待ち遠しい。","romaji":"Tōku ni sumu mago ni aeru hi ga machidōshii.","german":"I can't wait for the day I see my grandchildren who live far away.","furigana":"とお|す|まご|あ|ひ|ま|どお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive, literary: to wait long and eagerly for something: 平和を待ち望む (to long for peace). Often 待ち望んでいた (long-awaited).","examples":[{"japanese":"村の人々は雨が降るのを待ち望んでいた。","romaji":"Mura no hitobito wa ame ga furu no o machinozonde ita.","german":"The villagers were longing for rain to fall.","furigana":"むら|ひとびと|あめ|ふ|ま|のぞ"},{"japanese":"待ち望んでいた新作のゲームが、ついに発売された。","romaji":"Machinozonde ita shinsaku no gēmu ga, tsui ni hatsubai sareta.","german":"The long-awaited new game was finally released.","furigana":"ま|のぞ|しんさく|はつばい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"な-adjective, mostly in kana (rarely 区々): opinions, figures or traits differ: 意見がまちまちだ (opinions are divided).","examples":[{"japanese":"この問題については、専門家の意見もまちまちだ。","romaji":"Kono mondai ni tsuite wa, senmonka no iken mo machimachi da.","german":"Even experts' opinions differ on this issue.","furigana":"もんだい|せんもんか|いけん"},{"japanese":"参加者の年齢はまちまちで、十代から七十代までいた。","romaji":"Sankasha no nenrei wa machimachi de, jūdai kara nanajūdai made ita.","german":"The participants' ages varied, from teens to seventies.","furigana":"さんかしゃ|ねんれい|じゅうだい|ななじゅうだい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The last phase of an era or development: 江戸時代の末期 (the last years of the Edo period); for illnesses 末期がん (terminal cancer). The reading まつご, on the other hand, means \"the hour of death\".","examples":[{"japanese":"江戸時代の末期には、多くの外国船が日本に来るようになった。","romaji":"Edo jidai no makki ni wa, ōku no gaikokusen ga Nihon ni kuru yō ni natta.","german":"In the final years of the Edo period, many foreign ships began to come to Japan.","furigana":"えどじだい|まっき|おお|がいこくせん|にほん|く"},{"japanese":"病気が末期になる前に見つかって、本当によかった。","romaji":"Byōki ga makki ni naru mae ni mitsukatte, hontō ni yokatta.","german":"I'm truly glad the illness was found before it reached the terminal stage.","furigana":"びょうき|まっき|まえ|み|ほんとう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Also as a verb マッサージする: 肩をマッサージする. To get a massage: マッサージを受ける.","examples":[{"japanese":"疲れたので、マッサージを受けに行った。","romaji":"Tsukareta node, massāji o uke ni itta.","german":"I was exhausted, so I went to get a massage.","furigana":"つか|う|い"},{"japanese":"母の肩をマッサージしてあげた。","romaji":"Haha no kata o massāji shite ageta.","german":"I massaged my mother's shoulders for her.","furigana":"はは|かた"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"Mostly 真っ二つに: 真っ二つに割れる/切る (to break/cut exactly in two). Figuratively: 意見が真っ二つに分かれる (opinions are split into two camps).","examples":[{"japanese":"皿が床に落ちて、真っ二つに割れた。","romaji":"Sara ga yuka ni ochite, mapputatsu ni wareta.","german":"The plate fell on the floor and broke exactly in two.","furigana":"さら|ゆか|お|ま|ぷた|わ"},{"japanese":"その問題で、クラスの意見は真っ二つに分かれた。","romaji":"Sono mondai de, kurasu no iken wa mapputatsu ni wakareta.","german":"On that issue, the class was split right down the middle.","furigana":"もんだい|いけん|ま|ぷた|わ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Literally the target (的に当たる, 的を外す); figuratively: 注目の的 (center of attention), 的を射た意見 (a pertinent remark). The suffix 〜的 (e.g. 経済的) is read てき.","examples":[{"japanese":"矢は的の真ん中に当たった。","romaji":"Ya wa mato no mannaka ni atatta.","german":"The arrow hit the very center of the target.","furigana":"や|まと|ま|なか|あ"},{"japanese":"新しいスマホはクラスで注目の的になった。","romaji":"Atarashii sumaho wa kurasu de chūmoku no mato ni natta.","german":"The new smartphone became the center of attention in the class.","furigana":"あたら|ちゅうもく|まと"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mostly in kana: チームのまとまり (team spirit), まとまりのない文章 (an incoherent text). Verb: まとまる.","examples":[{"japanese":"このチームはまとまりがあって、とても強い。","romaji":"Kono chīmu wa matomari ga atte, totemo tsuyoi.","german":"This team is cohesive and very strong.","furigana":"つよ"},{"japanese":"彼の話はまとまりがなくて、何が言いたいのかわからない。","romaji":"Kare no hanashi wa matomari ga nakute, nani ga iitai no ka wakaranai.","german":"What he says is so disjointed that I can't tell what he wants to say.","furigana":"かれ|はなし|なに|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mostly in kana: 授業のまとめ (summary of the lesson). Verb: まとめる. Also in まとめ買い (bulk buying).","examples":[{"japanese":"最後に、今日の授業のまとめをします。","romaji":"Saigo ni, kyō no jugyō no matome o shimasu.","german":"Finally, we will wrap up today's lesson.","furigana":"さいご|きょう|じゅぎょう"},{"japanese":"会議の内容を簡単なまとめにして、メールで送った。","romaji":"Kaigi no naiyō o kantan na matome ni shite, mēru de okutta.","german":"I put the contents of the meeting into a brief summary and sent it by email.","furigana":"かいぎ|ないよう|かんたん|おく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Formal noun of 招く: お招きいただき、ありがとうございます (thank you for the invitation). Cf. 招待 (invitation to an event).","examples":[{"japanese":"本日はお招きいただき、ありがとうございます。","romaji":"Honjitsu wa omaneki itadaki, arigatō gozaimasu.","german":"Thank you very much for inviting me today.","furigana":"ほんじつ|まね"},{"japanese":"彼は大学の招きで、日本で講演をした。","romaji":"Kare wa daigaku no maneki de, Nihon de kōen o shita.","german":"At the invitation of the university, he gave a lecture in Japan.","furigana":"かれ|だいがく|まね|にほん|こうえん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"As the verb 麻痺する. Medical: 手足が麻痺する (arms and legs are paralyzed); figuratively: 大雪で交通が麻痺した (traffic came to a standstill). Often also in kana まひ.","examples":[{"japanese":"事故の後遺症で、右手が少し麻痺している。","romaji":"Jiko no kōishō de, migite ga sukoshi mahi shite iru.","german":"As an aftereffect of the accident, my right hand is slightly paralyzed.","furigana":"じこ|こういしょう|みぎて|すこ|まひ"},{"japanese":"大雪で都内の交通が麻痺した。","romaji":"Ōyuki de tonai no kōtsū ga mahi shita.","german":"Heavy snow brought traffic in Tokyo to a standstill.","furigana":"おおゆき|とない|こうつう|まひ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Literary, mostly in ままある (happens occasionally), often also in kana. Not to be confused with まま (as is) and ママ (mama).","examples":[{"japanese":"急いでいると、こういう間違いは間々あることだ。","romaji":"Isoide iru to, kō iu machigai wa mama aru koto da.","german":"When you're in a hurry, mistakes like this happen from time to time.","furigana":"いそ|まちが|まま"},{"japanese":"天気予報が外れることも間々ある。","romaji":"Tenki yohō ga hazureru koto mo mama aru.","german":"The weather forecast also occasionally turns out wrong.","furigana":"てんきよほう|はず|まま"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Suffix on nouns: 汗まみれ (drenched in sweat), 泥まみれ (covered in mud), 借金まみれ (deep in debt). Mostly negative; the kanji spelling 塗れ is rare. Verb: 塗れる【まみれる】.","examples":[{"japanese":"子どもたちは泥まみれになって遊んでいた。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa doromamire ni natte asonde ita.","german":"The children were playing, covered all over in mud.","furigana":"こ|どろ|あそ"},{"japanese":"夏の工事現場で働いて、一日中汗まみれだった。","romaji":"Natsu no kōji genba de hataraite, ichinichijū asemamire datta.","german":"Working at a construction site in summer, I was drenched in sweat all day.","furigana":"なつ|こうじげんば|はたら|いちにちじゅうあせ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"A traditional ball, often wrapped in colorful threads, which children used to play with (手まり). Rather literary today; for sports balls, ボール is used.","examples":[{"japanese":"少女たちが庭で鞠をついて遊んでいる。","romaji":"Shōjotachi ga niwa de mari o tsuite asonde iru.","german":"The girls are playing in the garden, bouncing a ball.","furigana":"しょうじょ|にわ|まり|あそ"},{"japanese":"祖母が作った鞠は、色とりどりの糸で巻かれていた。","romaji":"Sobo ga tsukutta mari wa, irotoridori no ito de makarete ita.","german":"The ball my grandmother made was wrapped in colorful threads.","furigana":"そぼ|つく|まり|いろ|いと|ま"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Colloquial, mostly with a negative: まるっきりわからない (not to understand at all). Similar to まるで and 全然.","examples":[{"japanese":"フランス語はまるっきりわからない。","romaji":"Furansugo wa marukkiri wakaranai.","german":"I don't understand French at all.","furigana":"ご"},{"japanese":"写真で見たのとまるっきり違う部屋だった。","romaji":"Shashin de mita no to marukkiri chigau heya datta.","german":"It was a completely different room from the one I saw in the photo.","furigana":"しゃしん|み|ちが|へや"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"As an adverb: completely, a full ...: 丸々一日 (a whole day). With と/とした: plump, chubby (丸々とした赤ちゃん, a chubby baby). Also in kana まるまる.","examples":[{"japanese":"資料作りに丸々一週間かかった。","romaji":"Shiryōzukuri ni marumaru isshūkan kakatta.","german":"It took a full week to prepare the materials.","furigana":"しりょうづく|まるまるいっしゅうかん"},{"japanese":"丸々と太った猫が縁側で寝ている。","romaji":"Marumaru to futotta neko ga engawa de nete iru.","german":"A round, fat cat is sleeping on the veranda.","furigana":"まるまる|ふと|ねこ|えんがわ|ね"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive: 紙を丸める (to crumple paper), 背中を丸める (to hunch one's back). 頭を丸める = to shave one's head, traditionally as a sign of repentance.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は書き損じた紙を丸めてごみ箱に捨てた。","romaji":"Kare wa kakisonjita kami o marumete gomibako ni suteta.","german":"He crumpled the misspelled sheet of paper and threw it in the trash.","furigana":"かれ|か|そん|かみ|まる|ばこ|す"},{"japanese":"寒いので、猫が背中を丸めて寝ている。","romaji":"Samui node, neko ga senaka o marumete nete iru.","german":"Because it's cold, the cat is sleeping curled up.","furigana":"さむ|ねこ|せなか|まる|ね"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Everyone present in a hall: 満場の拍手 (applause of the whole hall). Well known from 満場一致 (unanimous).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼の演説が終わると、満場の拍手が起こった。","romaji":"Kare no enzetsu ga owaru to, manjō no hakushu ga okotta.","german":"When his speech ended, the whole hall broke into applause.","furigana":"かれ|えんぜつ|お|まんじょう|はくしゅ|お"},{"japanese":"満場の観客が、選手の最後のジャンプを見守った。","romaji":"Manjō no kankyaku ga, senshu no saigo no janpu o mimamotta.","german":"The entire audience watched the athlete's final jump.","furigana":"まんじょう|かんきゃく|せんしゅ|さいご|みまも"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A colloquial intensified 前: 駅の真ん前 (right in front of the station).","examples":[{"japanese":"私の家は駅の真ん前にある。","romaji":"Watashi no ie wa eki no manmae ni aru.","german":"My house is right in front of the station.","furigana":"わたし|いえ|えき|ま|まえ"},{"japanese":"コンサートでは、ステージの真ん前の席に座れた。","romaji":"Konsāto de wa, sutēji no manmae no seki ni suwareta.","german":"At the concert, I got a seat right in front of the stage.","furigana":"ま|まえ|せき|すわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"An い-adjective, an intensified 丸い: 真ん丸い月 (the perfectly round full moon). Also 真ん丸 (noun).","examples":[{"japanese":"今夜は真ん丸い月が出ている。","romaji":"Kon'ya wa manmarui tsuki ga dete iru.","german":"A perfectly round moon is out tonight.","furigana":"こんや|ま|まる|つき|で"},{"japanese":"驚いた子どもは、目を真ん丸くして私を見た。","romaji":"Odoroita kodomo wa, me o manmaruku shite watashi o mita.","german":"The startled child looked at me with eyes wide open.","furigana":"おどろ|こ|め|ま|まる|わたし|み"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Usually お見合い: a meeting between prospective spouses arranged by family or matchmakers. 見合い結婚 = a marriage that comes about through such a meeting (as opposed to 恋愛結婚).","examples":[{"japanese":"両親は見合いで知り合って結婚した。","romaji":"Ryōshin wa miai de shiriatte kekkon shita.","german":"My parents met through an arranged meeting and got married.","furigana":"りょうしん|みあ|し|あ|けっこん"},{"japanese":"おばに勧められて、来週見合いをすることになった。","romaji":"Oba ni susumerarete, raishū miai o suru koto ni natta.","german":"At my aunt's suggestion, I am going to have an arranged meeting next week.","furigana":"すす|らいしゅうみあ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive. 1. 顔を見合わせる: to look at each other (in surprise). 2. Formal: to suspend for the time being, often in announcements: 運転を見合わせる (to temporarily suspend train service).","examples":[{"japanese":"突然の知らせに、二人は顔を見合わせた。","romaji":"Totsuzen no shirase ni, futari wa kao o miawaseta.","german":"At the sudden news, the two of them exchanged glances.","furigana":"とつぜん|し|ふたり|かお|みあ"},{"japanese":"大雨のため、電車は運転を見合わせています。","romaji":"Ōame no tame, densha wa unten o miawasete imasu.","german":"Because of the heavy rain, train service is temporarily suspended.","furigana":"おおあめ|でんしゃ|うんてん|みあ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Not yet developed or explored: 未開の地 (undeveloped land), 未開の分野 (uncharted territory in research). The older meaning \"uncivilized\" is now considered derogatory.","examples":[{"japanese":"当時、この地域はまだ未開の地だった。","romaji":"Tōji, kono chiiki wa mada mikai no chi datta.","german":"At that time, this region was still undeveloped land.","furigana":"とうじ|ちいき|みかい|ち"},{"japanese":"この分野は研究者が少なく、まだ未開の部分が多い。","romaji":"Kono bun'ya wa kenkyūsha ga sukunaku, mada mikai no bubun ga ōi.","german":"This field has few researchers and still has many unexplored areas.","furigana":"ぶんや|けんきゅうしゃ|すく|みかい|ぶぶん|おお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"One of the five senses (五感): 味覚が鋭い (to have a fine palate). 秋の味覚 = the culinary delights of autumn.","examples":[{"japanese":"風邪を引いて、味覚がおかしくなった。","romaji":"Kaze o hiite, mikaku ga okashiku natta.","german":"I caught a cold and my sense of taste is off.","furigana":"かぜ|ひ|みかく"},{"japanese":"料理人は味覚が鋭くなければならない。","romaji":"Ryōrinin wa mikaku ga surudoku nakereba naranai.","german":"A cook must have a keen sense of taste.","furigana":"りょうりにん|みかく|するど"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mostly 微塵も〜ない: not the slightest bit (反省の色は微塵もない). In cooking: みじん切り (finely chopped).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼には反省の気持ちなど微塵もなかった。","romaji":"Kare ni wa hansei no kimochi nado mijin mo nakatta.","german":"He didn't have the slightest sense of remorse.","furigana":"かれ|はんせい|きも|みじん"},{"japanese":"玉ねぎを微塵に刻んで、ひき肉と混ぜた。","romaji":"Tamanegi o mijin ni kizande, hikiniku to mazeta.","german":"I minced the onion finely and mixed it with the ground meat.","furigana":"たま|みじん|きざ|にく|ま"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Water contained in something: 水気を切る (to drain), 水気の多い果物 (juicy fruit). Other reading: すいき.","examples":[{"japanese":"洗った野菜の水気をよく切ってください。","romaji":"Aratta yasai no mizuke o yoku kitte kudasai.","german":"Please drain the washed vegetables well.","furigana":"あら|やさい|みずけ|き"},{"japanese":"この梨は水気が多くて甘い。","romaji":"Kono nashi wa mizuke ga ōkute amai.","german":"This pear is juicy and sweet.","furigana":"なし|みずけ|おお|あま"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"An error in printed text. Synonym, more literary: 誤植.","examples":[{"japanese":"教科書にミスプリントが見つかった。","romaji":"Kyōkasho ni misupurinto ga mitsukatta.","german":"A misprint was found in the textbook.","furigana":"きょうかしょ|み"},{"japanese":"招待状の日付がミスプリントで、一日ずれていた。","romaji":"Shōtaijō no hizuke ga misupurinto de, ichinichi zurete ita.","german":"The date on the invitation was misprinted and was off by one day.","furigana":"しょうたいじょう|ひづけ|いちにち"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"An い-adjective, usually written in kana: みすぼらしい服 (shabby clothes), みすぼらしい家.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼はみすぼらしい服を着ていたが、実は大金持ちだった。","romaji":"Kare wa misuborashii fuku o kite ita ga, jitsu wa ōganemochi datta.","german":"He was wearing shabby clothes, but was actually extremely rich.","furigana":"かれ|ふく|き|じつ|おおがねも"},{"japanese":"駅前のみすぼらしい小屋が、立派なカフェに生まれ変わった。","romaji":"Ekimae no misuborashii koya ga, rippa na kafe ni umarekawatta.","german":"The shabby hut in front of the station has been reborn as a fine cafe.","furigana":"えきまえ|こや|りっぱ|う|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"From the English \"Mrs.\": used mainly in advertising and fashion for married women (ミセス向けの服). Rare as a form of address in Japanese.","examples":[{"japanese":"このブランドは三十代のミセス向けの服が多い。","romaji":"Kono burando wa sanjūdai no misesu muke no fuku ga ōi.","german":"This brand has a lot of clothing aimed at married women in their thirties.","furigana":"さんじゅうだい|む|ふく|おお"},{"japanese":"この雑誌の読者は主にミセスだそうだ。","romaji":"Kono zasshi no dokusha wa omo ni misesu da sō da.","german":"The readers of this magazine are said to be mainly married women.","furigana":"ざっし|どくしゃ|おも"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive, derogatory: to deliberately show something in order to be admired or envied: 新しい時計を見せびらかす.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は新しい時計をみんなに見せびらかしている。","romaji":"Kare wa atarashii tokei o minna ni misebirakashite iru.","german":"He is showing off his new watch to everyone.","furigana":"かれ|あたら|とけい|み"},{"japanese":"買ったばかりのスマホを見せびらかすのはやめなさい。","romaji":"Katta bakari no sumaho o misebirakasu no wa yamenasai.","german":"Stop showing off your brand-new smartphone.","furigana":"か|み"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Formerly fairground performances (見世物小屋 = sideshow booth). Today mostly derogatory: 見世物になる/にする (to become a spectacle, to put on display). Also written 見せ物.","examples":[{"japanese":"事故の現場が見世物のようになって、多くの人が写真を撮っていた。","romaji":"Jiko no genba ga misemono no yō ni natte, ōku no hito ga shashin o totte ita.","german":"The scene of the accident turned into a spectacle, and many people were taking photos.","furigana":"じこ|げんば|みせもの|おお|ひと|しゃしん|と"},{"japanese":"動物を見世物にするのはかわいそうだという声もある。","romaji":"Dōbutsu o misemono ni suru no wa kawaisō da to iu koe mo aru.","german":"Some people say it is cruel to put animals on display.","furigana":"どうぶつ|みせもの|こえ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Literally a ditch or drainage channel (溝に落ちる); figuratively an estrangement: 二人の間に溝ができる. Other reading: こう (in technical terms such as 海溝, ocean trench).","examples":[{"japanese":"自転車のタイヤが道の溝にはまってしまった。","romaji":"Jitensha no taiya ga michi no mizo ni hamatte shimatta.","german":"The bicycle tire got stuck in the gutter at the side of the road.","furigana":"じてんしゃ|みち|みぞ"},{"japanese":"あのけんか以来、二人の間には深い溝ができた。","romaji":"Ano kenka irai, futari no aida ni wa fukai mizo ga dekita.","german":"Since that fight, a deep rift has formed between the two.","furigana":"いらい|ふたり|あいだ|ふか|みぞ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Literary, mostly 未知の + noun: 未知の世界 (an unknown world), 未知数 (an unknown, an uncertain quantity). Opposite: 既知. Homophone: 道.","examples":[{"japanese":"宇宙には未知のことがまだたくさんある。","romaji":"Uchū ni wa michi no koto ga mada takusan aru.","german":"There are still many unknown things in the universe.","furigana":"うちゅう|みち"},{"japanese":"留学は、未知の世界に飛び込むようなものだった。","romaji":"Ryūgaku wa, michi no sekai ni tobikomu yō na mono datta.","german":"Studying abroad was like jumping into an unknown world.","furigana":"りゅうがく|みち|せかい|と|こ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A sweet liquid: 花の蜜 (flower nectar); honey is usually 蜂蜜. 黒蜜 = dark sugar syrup.","examples":[{"japanese":"蝶が花の蜜を吸っている。","romaji":"Chō ga hana no mitsu o sutte iru.","german":"A butterfly is sucking nectar from a flower.","furigana":"ちょう|はな|みつ|す"},{"japanese":"蜂は集めた蜜を巣にためる。","romaji":"Hachi wa atsumeta mitsu o su ni tameru.","german":"Bees store the nectar they have collected in their hive.","furigana":"はち|あつ|みつ|す"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"As a verb 密集する: to stand close together or crowd: 住宅が密集している (densely built-up). Known from 三密 (密閉・密集・密接) during the COVID pandemic.","examples":[{"japanese":"この辺りは古い住宅が密集している。","romaji":"Kono atari wa furui jūtaku ga misshū shite iru.","german":"Old houses are densely packed in this area.","furigana":"あた|ふる|じゅうたく|みっしゅう"},{"japanese":"人が密集する場所では、スリに気をつけてください。","romaji":"Hito ga misshū suru basho de wa, suri ni ki o tsukete kudasai.","german":"Beware of pickpockets in places where people are crowded together.","furigana":"ひと|みっしゅう|ばしょ|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Literally the source of a river (川の源), mostly figurative: 力の源 (source of strength), 情報の源.","examples":[{"japanese":"この川の源は、あの山の中にある。","romaji":"Kono kawa no minamoto wa, ano yama no naka ni aru.","german":"The source of this river is in those mountains.","furigana":"かわ|みなもと|やま|なか"},{"japanese":"家族の笑顔が、私の元気の源だ。","romaji":"Kazoku no egao ga, watashi no genki no minamoto da.","german":"My family's smiles are the source of my energy.","furigana":"かぞく|えがお|わたし|げんき|みなもと"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive: to take someone as a role model: 先輩を見習う. The noun 見習い = apprentice, trainee.","examples":[{"japanese":"少しはお兄さんを見習いなさい。","romaji":"Sukoshi wa oniisan o minarainasai.","german":"Take a little example from your older brother.","furigana":"すこ|にい|みなら"},{"japanese":"新人の頃は、先輩の仕事を見習って覚えた。","romaji":"Shinjin no koro wa, senpai no shigoto o minaratte oboeta.","german":"When I was new, I learned the work by watching my senior colleagues.","furigana":"しんじん|ころ|せんぱい|しごと|みなら|おぼ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"How someone is dressed and groomed: 身なりを整える (to tidy oneself up), 身なりのいい紳士.","examples":[{"japanese":"面接の前に、鏡で身なりを整えた。","romaji":"Mensetsu no mae ni, kagami de minari o totonoeta.","german":"Before the interview, I straightened my appearance in the mirror.","furigana":"めんせつ|まえ|かがみ|み|ととの"},{"japanese":"身なりのいい紳士が、店に入ってきた。","romaji":"Minari no ii shinshi ga, mise ni haitte kita.","german":"A well-dressed gentleman came into the shop.","furigana":"み|しんし|みせ|はい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The summit or ridge of a mountain, rather literary. In everyday speech one says 山頂 or 頂上.","examples":[{"japanese":"雪をかぶった峰が朝日に輝いていた。","romaji":"Yuki o kabutta mine ga asahi ni kagayaite ita.","german":"The snow-capped peaks shone in the morning sun.","furigana":"ゆき|みね|あさひ|かがや"},{"japanese":"雲の上に、遠くの山の峰が見えた。","romaji":"Kumo no ue ni, tōku no yama no mine ga mieta.","german":"Above the clouds, I could see the peaks of distant mountains.","furigana":"くも|うえ|とお|やま|みね|み"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A person's personal situation and past: 身の上話 (life story), 身の上相談 (personal advice column, e.g. in newspapers).","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は初めて自分の身の上を語り始めた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa hajimete jibun no minoue o katarihajimeta.","german":"For the first time, she began to talk about her own life.","furigana":"かのじょ|はじ|じぶん|み|うえ|かた|はじ"},{"japanese":"両親を早くに亡くした彼の身の上に、みんな同情した。","romaji":"Ryōshin o hayaku ni nakushita kare no minoue ni, minna dōjō shita.","german":"Everyone sympathized with his situation, having lost his parents early.","furigana":"りょうしん|はや|な|かれ|み|うえ|どうじょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"身の回りの物 (personal belongings), 身の回りの世話 (help with daily care, e.g. for elderly people).","examples":[{"japanese":"入院に必要な身の回りの物をかばんに詰めた。","romaji":"Nyūin ni hitsuyō na minomawari no mono o kaban ni tsumeta.","german":"I packed the personal items I need for the hospital stay into a bag.","furigana":"にゅういん|ひつよう|み|まわ|もの|つ"},{"japanese":"祖母の身の回りの世話は、主に母がしている。","romaji":"Sobo no minomawari no sewa wa, omo ni haha ga shite iru.","german":"My mother mainly takes care of my grandmother's daily needs.","furigana":"そぼ|み|まわ|せわ|おも|はは"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive. Mostly タイミングを見計らう / 頃合いを見計らう: to wait for the right moment. Also: to choose by one's own judgment.","examples":[{"japanese":"上司の機嫌がいい時を見計らって、休みを頼んだ。","romaji":"Jōshi no kigen ga ii toki o mihakaratte, yasumi o tanonda.","german":"I waited for a moment when my boss was in a good mood and asked for time off.","furigana":"じょうし|きげん|とき|みはか|やす|たの"},{"japanese":"花束は予算内で適当に見計らってください。","romaji":"Hanataba wa yosannai de tekitō ni mihakaratte kudasai.","german":"Please choose the bouquet at your discretion, within the budget.","furigana":"はなたば|よさんない|てきとう|みはか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A wide view over a landscape: 見晴らしがいい (to have a nice view). Verb: 見晴らす.","examples":[{"japanese":"山の上のホテルは見晴らしがとてもいい。","romaji":"Yama no ue no hoteru wa miharashi ga totemo ii.","german":"The hotel at the top of the mountain has a wonderful view.","furigana":"やま|うえ|みは"},{"japanese":"窓の前に高いビルが建って、見晴らしが悪くなった。","romaji":"Mado no mae ni takai biru ga tatte, miharashi ga waruku natta.","german":"Since a tall building went up in front of the window, the view has gotten worse.","furigana":"まど|まえ|たか|た|みは|わる"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mainly in compounds, names and commands: ミュージックビデオ, ポップミュージック. The general word is 音楽.","examples":[{"japanese":"「ミュージック、スタート！」の合図で、ダンスが始まった。","romaji":"\"Myūjikku, sutāto!\" no aizu de, dansu ga hajimatta.","german":"The dance began at the cue \"Music, start!\"","furigana":"あいず|はじ"},{"japanese":"私はポップミュージックが好きで、毎日聞いている。","romaji":"Watashi wa poppu myūjikku ga suki de, mainichi kiite iru.","german":"I like pop music and listen to it every day.","furigana":"わたし|す|まいにちき"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"な-adjective: 無意味な争い (a pointless quarrel). More colloquial: 意味がない.","examples":[{"japanese":"そんな議論を続けても無意味だ。","romaji":"Sonna giron o tsuzuketemo muimi da.","german":"It is pointless to continue such a discussion.","furigana":"ぎろん|つづ|むいみ"},{"japanese":"無意味な会議を減らして、仕事の時間を増やしたい。","romaji":"Muimi na kaigi o herashite, shigoto no jikan o fuyashitai.","german":"I want to reduce pointless meetings and have more time for work.","furigana":"むいみ|かいぎ|へ|しごと|じかん|ふ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Direction: 風の向き, 向きを変える. As a suffix 〜向き: suited to (子ども向き, 初心者向き) or facing toward (南向きの部屋). Cf. 〜向け (intended for ...).","examples":[{"japanese":"机の向きを変えたら、部屋が広く見えるようになった。","romaji":"Tsukue no muki o kaetara, heya ga hiroku mieru yō ni natta.","german":"After I changed the orientation of the desk, the room started to look bigger.","furigana":"つくえ|む|か|へや|ひろ|み"},{"japanese":"この仕事は、細かい作業が好きな人向きだ。","romaji":"Kono shigoto wa, komakai sagyō ga suki na hito muki da.","german":"This job is suited to people who like detailed work.","furigana":"しごと|こま|さぎょう|す|ひとむ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"な-adjective: 無口な人 (a taciturn person). Opposite: おしゃべり.","examples":[{"japanese":"父は無口だが、とても優しい人だ。","romaji":"Chichi wa mukuchi da ga, totemo yasashii hito da.","german":"My father is taciturn, but a very kind person.","furigana":"ちち|むくち|やさ|ひと"},{"japanese":"彼は緊張すると無口になる。","romaji":"Kare wa kinchō suru to mukuchi ni naru.","german":"He goes quiet when he is nervous.","furigana":"かれ|きんちょう|むくち"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive, usually in kana: 草をむしる (to weed), 鶏の羽をむしる (to pluck a chicken). Also: to pick flesh off bones (魚の身をむしる).","examples":[{"japanese":"日曜日に、庭の草をむしった。","romaji":"Nichiyōbi ni, niwa no kusa o mushitta.","german":"On Sunday, I pulled up the weeds in the garden.","furigana":"にちようび|にわ|くさ"},{"japanese":"焼いた魚の身を箸でむしって、子どもに食べさせた。","romaji":"Yaita sakana no mi o hashi de mushitte, kodomo ni tabesaseta.","german":"I picked the flesh off the grilled fish with chopsticks and fed it to the child.","furigana":"や|さかな|み|はし|こ|た"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The concluding part: 手紙の結び, 結びの言葉 (closing words). Other meanings: knot (蝶結び = bow) and おむすび (rice ball).","examples":[{"japanese":"スピーチの結びに、お世話になった人たちへの感謝を述べた。","romaji":"Supīchi no musubi ni, osewa ni natta hitotachi e no kansha o nobeta.","german":"At the end of my speech, I expressed gratitude to the people who had helped me.","furigana":"むす|せわ|ひと|かんしゃ|の"},{"japanese":"手紙の結びには「お体を大切に」と書いた。","romaji":"Tegami no musubi ni wa \"okarada o taisetsu ni\" to kaita.","german":"At the end of the letter, I wrote \"Take care of yourself.\"","furigana":"てがみ|むす|からだ|たいせつ|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A close relationship between people, countries or things: 地域の結び付き, 経済的な結び付き. Often also written 結びつき.","examples":[{"japanese":"日本とこの国は、経済的な結び付きが強い。","romaji":"Nihon to kono kuni wa, keizaiteki na musubitsuki ga tsuyoi.","german":"Japan and this country have strong economic ties.","furigana":"にほん|くに|けいざいてき|むす|つ|つよ"},{"japanese":"私は家族との結び付きを大切にしている。","romaji":"Watashi wa kazoku to no musubitsuki o taisetsu ni shite iru.","german":"I value my bond with my family.","furigana":"わたし|かぞく|むす|つ|たいせつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Intransitive: AとBが結び付く (A and B are connected); 〜に結び付く = to lead to something: 努力が結果に結び付く. Transitive: 結び付ける.","examples":[{"japanese":"毎日の練習が、ついに優勝に結び付いた。","romaji":"Mainichi no renshū ga, tsui ni yūshō ni musubitsuita.","german":"Daily practice finally led to victory.","furigana":"まいにち|れんしゅう|ゆうしょう|むす|つ"},{"japanese":"この甘い香りは、私の子どもの頃の記憶と結び付いている。","romaji":"Kono amai kaori wa, watashi no kodomo no koro no kioku to musubitsuite iru.","german":"This sweet scent is linked to my childhood memories.","furigana":"あま|かお|わたし|こ|ころ|きおく|むす|つ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Transitive counterpart of 結び付く: 木にひもを結び付ける (to tie a string to a tree); figuratively: 二つの事件を結び付ける (to link two cases).","examples":[{"japanese":"風で飛ばないように、風船をいすに結び付けた。","romaji":"Kaze de tobanai yō ni, fūsen o isu ni musubitsuketa.","german":"I tied the balloon to the chair so the wind wouldn't blow it away.","furigana":"かぜ|と|ふうせん|むす|つ"},{"japanese":"警察は二つの事件を結び付けて考えている。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa futatsu no jiken o musubitsukete kangaete iru.","german":"The police are linking the two cases.","furigana":"けいさつ|ふた|じけん|むす|つ|かんが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Radio communication (無線で連絡する) and, in general, \"wireless\": 無線LAN (Wi-Fi). Opposite: 有線.","examples":[{"japanese":"船の上から無線で港に連絡した。","romaji":"Fune no ue kara musen de minato ni renraku shita.","german":"I contacted the port by radio from the ship.","furigana":"ふね|うえ|むせん|みなと|れんらく"},{"japanese":"この部屋は無線でインターネットにつながる。","romaji":"Kono heya wa musen de intānetto ni tsunagaru.","german":"This room connects to the internet wirelessly.","furigana":"へや|むせん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The mood of a situation or group: いいムード, ムードメーカー (mood maker). Similar: 雰囲気.","examples":[{"japanese":"試合に勝って、チームのムードが明るくなった。","romaji":"Shiai ni katte, chīmu no mūdo ga akaruku natta.","german":"After winning the match, the mood of the team brightened.","furigana":"しあい|か|あか"},{"japanese":"二人はいいムードだったのに、電話が鳴って台無しになった。","romaji":"Futari wa ii mūdo datta noni, denwa ga natte dainashi ni natta.","german":"The two were in a good mood, but then the phone rang and ruined it.","furigana":"ふたり|でんわ|な|だいな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Deep disappointment or resentment over a failure: 無念の涙 (tears of regret), 無念を晴らす (to wipe out the disgrace). Stronger and more formal than 残念.","examples":[{"japanese":"決勝で負けた選手たちは、無念の涙を流した。","romaji":"Kesshō de maketa senshutachi wa, munen no namida o nagashita.","german":"The players who lost in the final shed tears of regret.","furigana":"けっしょう|ま|せんしゅ|むねん|なみだ|なが"},{"japanese":"夢を果たせずに亡くなった父の無念を思うと、胸が痛む。","romaji":"Yume o hatasezu ni nakunatta chichi no munen o omou to, mune ga itamu.","german":"When I think of my father's bitterness at dying without fulfilling his dream, my heart aches.","furigana":"ゆめ|は|な|ちち|むねん|おも|むね|いた"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"な-adjective, rather formal: 無用な争い (an unnecessary quarrel), 心配ご無用 (no need to worry). On signs, \"not allowed\": 天地無用 (do not turn upside down).","examples":[{"japanese":"無用なトラブルを避けるために、契約書をよく読んだ。","romaji":"Muyō na toraburu o sakeru tame ni, keiyakusho o yoku yonda.","german":"I read the contract carefully to avoid unnecessary trouble.","furigana":"むよう|さ|けいやくしょ|よ"},{"japanese":"私のことなら、心配は無用です。","romaji":"Watashi no koto nara, shinpai wa muyō desu.","german":"As for me, there is no need to worry.","furigana":"わたし|しんぱい|むよう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The official name of a thing, organization or product: 正式名称 (official designation). For people, one says 名前 or 氏名. Homophone: 名勝 (scenic spot).","examples":[{"japanese":"会社は来年から名称を変更する。","romaji":"Kaisha wa rainen kara meishō o henkō suru.","german":"The company will change its name starting next year.","furigana":"かいしゃ|らいねん|めいしょう|へんこう"},{"japanese":"この祭りの正式な名称を知っていますか。","romaji":"Kono matsuri no seishiki na meishō o shitte imasu ka.","german":"Do you know the official name of this festival?","furigana":"まつ|せいしき|めいしょう|し"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"As a verb 命中する: to hit the target: 的に命中する. 命中率 = hit rate.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼の投げたボールは、見事に的に命中した。","romaji":"Kare no nageta bōru wa, migoto ni mato ni meichū shita.","german":"The ball he threw hit the target perfectly.","furigana":"かれ|な|みごと|まと|めいちゅう"},{"japanese":"ミサイルは目標に命中しなかった。","romaji":"Misairu wa mokuhyō ni meichū shinakatta.","german":"The missile did not hit its target.","furigana":"もくひょう|めいちゅう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A manufacturing company, especially larger firms: 自動車メーカー (car manufacturer), 大手メーカー.","examples":[{"japanese":"このパソコンはどこのメーカーのですか。","romaji":"Kono pasokon wa doko no mēkā no desu ka.","german":"Which manufacturer is this computer from?"},{"japanese":"兄は大手の自動車メーカーに勤めている。","romaji":"Ani wa ōte no jidōsha mēkā ni tsutomete iru.","german":"My older brother works for a major car manufacturer.","furigana":"あに|おおて|じどうしゃ|つと"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A somewhat old-fashioned word for 重さ, especially when weighing goods: 目方を量る, 目方で売る (to sell by weight).","examples":[{"japanese":"魚屋さんは、魚の目方を量って値段を決めた。","romaji":"Sakanayasan wa, sakana no mekata o hakatte nedan o kimeta.","german":"The fishmonger weighed the fish and set the price.","furigana":"さかなや|さかな|めかた|はか|ねだん|き"},{"japanese":"このお菓子は目方で売られている。","romaji":"Kono okashi wa mekata de urarete iru.","german":"These sweets are sold by weight.","furigana":"かし|めかた|う"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Gifts of nature or divine grace: 自然の恵み, 恵みの雨 (longed-for rain after dryness). Verb: 恵む.","examples":[{"japanese":"海の恵みに感謝して、漁師たちは祭りを行う。","romaji":"Umi no megumi ni kansha shite, ryōshitachi wa matsuri o okonau.","german":"Grateful for the bounty of the sea, the fishermen hold a festival.","furigana":"うみ|めぐ|かんしゃ|りょうし|まつ|おこな"},{"japanese":"長い日照りの後の雨は、まさに恵みの雨だった。","romaji":"Nagai hideri no ato no ame wa, masa ni megumi no ame datta.","german":"The rain after the long drought was truly a blessing.","furigana":"なが|ひで|あと|あめ|めぐ|あめ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A female animal (also plant): 雌の猫. Opposite: 雄【おす】. Often written in kana めす or メス.","examples":[{"japanese":"うちの猫は雌で、名前はモモです。","romaji":"Uchi no neko wa mesu de, namae wa Momo desu.","german":"Our cat is a female, and her name is Momo.","furigana":"ねこ|めす|なまえ"},{"japanese":"雌のカブトムシにはツノがない。","romaji":"Mesu no kabutomushi ni wa tsuno ga nai.","german":"Female rhinoceros beetles have no horn.","furigana":"めす"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The way someone looks, often negative: 目つきが悪い (to have a nasty look), 鋭い目つき. Also written 目付き.","examples":[{"japanese":"あの男は目つきが悪いけど、本当は優しい人だ。","romaji":"Ano otoko wa metsuki ga warui kedo, hontō wa yasashii hito da.","german":"That man has a nasty look in his eyes, but he is actually a kind person.","furigana":"おとこ|め|わる|ほんとう|やさ|ひと"},{"japanese":"猫は鋭い目つきで鳥をじっと見ていた。","romaji":"Neko wa surudoi metsuki de tori o jitto mite ita.","german":"The cat was staring at the bird with a sharp gaze.","furigana":"ねこ|するど|め|とり|み"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The divisions on measuring instruments: 定規の目盛り, 温度計の目盛りを読む.","examples":[{"japanese":"温度計の目盛りを読んで、ノートに記録した。","romaji":"Ondokei no memori o yonde, nōto ni kiroku shita.","german":"I read the scale on the thermometer and recorded it in my notebook.","furigana":"おんどけい|めも|よ|きろく"},{"japanese":"計量カップの目盛りに合わせて、水を二百ミリリットル入れた。","romaji":"Keiryō kappu no memori ni awasete, mizu o nihyaku miririttoru ireta.","german":"I poured 200 milliliters of water, matching the markings on the measuring cup.","furigana":"けいりょう|めも|あ|みず|にひゃく|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The tune of a song: 懐かしいメロディー. Also written メロディ.","examples":[{"japanese":"どこかで聞いたことのあるメロディーが流れてきた。","romaji":"Dokoka de kiita koto no aru merodī ga nagarete kita.","german":"A melody I had heard somewhere before started playing.","furigana":"き|なが"},{"japanese":"この曲は歌詞よりもメロディーが好きだ。","romaji":"Kono kyoku wa kashi yori mo merodī ga suki da.","german":"I like this song's melody more than its lyrics.","furigana":"きょく|かし|す"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The neutral standard form; よっぽど is the colloquially intensified variant. Typical: よほど〜のだろう (must be very ...), 〜よりよほど (far more ... than), よほどのこと (something extraordinary). Usually in kana (余程).","examples":[{"japanese":"あんなに怒るなんて、彼はよほど嫌なことがあったのだろう。","romaji":"Anna ni okoru nante, kare wa yohodo iya na koto ga atta no darō.","german":"To get that angry, something very unpleasant must have happened to him.","furigana":"おこ|かれ|いや"},{"japanese":"よほどのことがない限り、会議は予定どおり行います。","romaji":"Yohodo no koto ga nai kagiri, kaigi wa yotei dōri okonaimasu.","german":"Unless something extraordinary happens, the meeting will be held as planned.","furigana":"かぎ|かいぎ|よてい|おこな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"な-adjective. 本格的な (genuine, authentic: 本格的な料理) and adverbial 本格的に (in earnest, fully: 本格的に始まる). Derived from 本格 (the proper, genuine way).","examples":[{"japanese":"この店では本格的なイタリア料理が食べられる。","romaji":"Kono mise de wa honkakuteki na Itaria ryōri ga taberareru.","german":"At this restaurant you can eat authentic Italian cuisine.","furigana":"みせ|ほんかくてき|りょうり|た"},{"japanese":"来週から梅雨が本格的に始まるそうだ。","romaji":"Raishū kara tsuyu ga honkakuteki ni hajimaru sō da.","german":"The rainy season is said to begin in earnest next week.","furigana":"らいしゅう|つゆ|ほんかくてき|はじ"}],"levelNote":"Compound: 本格 (N1) + 的 (N2)"},
{"notes":"節倹 means holding back on spending and living simply. As a noun it appears in phrases such as 節倹に努める \"to strive for frugality\" or 節倹を重んじる \"to value frugality\". 節倹する is also recorded. The examples show a personal way of life and the handling of public funds. The identically pronounced 石鹸・せっけん means \"soap\" and is a different word.","examples":[{"japanese":"母は長年節倹に努め、子どもたちの学費を用意した。","romaji":"Haha wa naganen sekken ni tsutome, kodomotachi no gakuhi o yōi shita.","german":"My mother worked at frugality for years and prepared the money for her children's school fees.","furigana":"はは|ながねんせっけん|つと|こ|がくひ|ようい"},{"japanese":"市長は、公金の使い方を見直し、節倹を重んじる姿勢を示した。","romaji":"Shichō wa, kōkin no tsukaikata o minaoshi, sekken o omonjiru shisei o shimeshita.","german":"The mayor reviewed how public funds are used and showed an attitude that values frugality.","furigana":"しちょう|こうきん|つか|かた|みなお|せっけん|おも|しせい|しめ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"あたし is a colloquial first-person pronoun, especially in everyday speech with a feminine flavor. It is usually written in kana; the kanji writing 私 can also have this reading. For formal conversation, わたし is the more common choice. In the first example, も means \"also\". In the second, が highlights who is cooking today; the final ね seeks agreement. The readings わたし and わたくし are covered in their own entries.","examples":[{"japanese":"あたしもその映画を見たい。","romaji":"Atashi mo sono eiga o mitai.","german":"I want to see that movie too.","furigana":"えいが|み"},{"japanese":"今日はあたしが料理するね。","romaji":"Kyō wa atashi ga ryōri suru ne.","german":"I'll cook today, okay?","furigana":"きょう|りょうり"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"いかる expresses anger or indignation and often sounds more literary than おこる. The cause can be marked with に, as in 不公平な扱いに怒る. Here it is an intransitive verb of emotion: \"to scold someone\" is not simply expressed with a direct object marked by を. It is a Godan verb: いかって, いかった and いからない.","examples":[{"japanese":"不公平な扱いに怒る人々の声が、広場に響いていた。","romaji":"Fukōhei na atsukai ni ikaru hitobito no koe ga, hiroba ni hibiite ita.","german":"The voices of people angry at the unfair treatment echoed across the square.","furigana":"ふこうへい|あつか|いか|ひとびと|こえ|ひろば|ひび"},{"japanese":"彼は怒っていたが、相手を責める言葉は口にしなかった。","romaji":"Kare wa ikatte ita ga, aite o semeru kotoba wa kuchi ni shinakatta.","german":"He was angry, but he did not say any words blaming the other person.","furigana":"かれ|いか|あいて|せ|ことば|くち"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"入口 can be read いりくち as well as いりぐち. In this reading the く stays voiceless; both writings 入口 and 入り口 are possible. It means the place where one goes in; 出口 is the exit. In 入口にできた列, に marks the place where the line formed.","examples":[{"japanese":"入口が裏通りにあるため、初めて来た人には見つけにくい。","romaji":"Irikuchi ga uradōri ni aru tame, hajimete kita hito ni wa mitsuke nikui.","german":"The entrance is on a back street, so it is hard for first-time visitors to find.","furigana":"いりくち|うらどお|はじ|き|ひと|み"},{"japanese":"係員の案内で、入口にできた列がゆっくり動き始めた。","romaji":"Kakariin no annai de, irikuchi ni dekita retsu ga yukkuri ugoki hajimeta.","german":"Following the staff's guidance, the line that had formed at the entrance slowly began to move.","furigana":"かかりいん|あんない|いりくち|れつ|うご|はじ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"角 is read かく here and means a geometric angle. In the Japanese chess variant shogi, 角 is the short form of 角行（かくぎょう）, a piece that moves diagonally. With 角を測る you measure an angle; in the game, the meaning is clear from the context of the pieces. 角 read かど means corner, and read つの it means horn or antler.","examples":[{"japanese":"この二つの直線が作る角を測ってください。","romaji":"Kono futatsu no chokusen ga tsukuru kaku o hakatte kudasai.","german":"Please measure the angle formed by these two straight lines.","furigana":"ふた|ちょくせん|つく|かく|はか"},{"japanese":"角は斜めの方向に進む駒です。","romaji":"Kaku wa naname no hōkō ni susumu koma desu.","german":"The bishop in shogi is a piece that moves diagonally.","furigana":"かく|なな|ほうこう|すす|こま"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"In this entry 門 is read かど and means the gate or entrance of a house. 門の外 names the area outside the gate; 門をたたく means to knock on the gate. The same writing can also be read もん. The reading かど also appears in 門口（かどぐち）. 角（かど）meaning corner is a different word.","examples":[{"japanese":"祖父は門の外で私を待っていました。","romaji":"Sofu wa kado no soto de watashi o matte imashita.","german":"My grandfather was waiting for me outside the gate.","furigana":"そふ|かど|そと|わたし|ま"},{"japanese":"返事がなかったので、もう一度門をたたきました。","romaji":"Henji ga nakatta node, mō ichido kado o tatakimashita.","german":"Since there was no answer, I knocked on the gate once more.","furigana":"へんじ|いちどかど"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"縁（えん）can mean a fateful encounter or the bond between people. 何かの縁ですね judges an encounter to be a kind of destiny; 縁がある says that there is a connection or contact with someone or something. ご縁 adds the polite prefix ご. When getting acquainted, 縁 means an occasion from which a relationship arises, not just any chance. 縁（ふち）meaning edge has a different reading.","examples":[{"japanese":"旅先で再び会ったのも、何かの縁ですね。","romaji":"Tabisaki de futatabi atta no mo, nanika no en desu ne.","german":"That we met again on a trip must be some kind of destiny.","furigana":"たびさき|ふたた|あ|なに|えん"},{"japanese":"父がそこで働いていたので、私もこの町には縁があります。","romaji":"Chichi ga soko de hataraite ita node, watashi mo kono machi ni wa en ga arimasu.","german":"My father worked there, so I also have a connection to this town.","furigana":"ちち|はたら|わたし|まち|えん"},{"japanese":"この集まりで、よい縁に恵まれました。","romaji":"Kono atsumari de, yoi en ni megumaremashita.","german":"At this gathering, I was fortunate to make a good acquaintance.","furigana":"あつ|えん|めぐ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"後退 is a noun for a retreat, a movement backward, or a setback. 前進 is the opposite direction. With する it forms the intransitive verb 後退する. In computer terminology, 後退 can also mean the backspace key; 後退キー makes this connection clear. The identically read 交代 means change or switching. The pitch accent of this card applies to 後退 alone, not to 後退する or 後退キー.","examples":[{"japanese":"計画の中止は、大きな後退だと思います。","romaji":"Keikaku no chūshi wa, ōkina kōtai da to omoimasu.","german":"I think canceling the plan is a major setback.","furigana":"けいかく|ちゅうし|おお|こうたい|おも"},{"japanese":"車の後退中は、後ろに立たないでください。","romaji":"Kuruma no kōtaichū wa, ushiro ni tatanaide kudasai.","german":"Please do not stand behind the car while it is reversing.","furigana":"くるま|こうたいちゅう|うし|た"},{"japanese":"入力した最後の文字を、後退キーで消しました。","romaji":"Nyūryoku shita saigo no moji o, kōtai kī de keshimashita.","german":"I deleted the last character I had typed with the backspace key.","furigana":"にゅうりょく|さいご|もじ|こうたい|け"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"音（ね）is used for the sound of a bell or the chirping of insects, for example: 鐘の音, 虫の音. The reading often appears in vivid descriptions that emphasize the perceived sound. おと is the general reading for noises; ね and おと are therefore not interchangeable in every combination. ね stays short.","examples":[{"japanese":"夕暮れの庭に、虫の音が静かに響いていた。","romaji":"Yūgure no niwa ni, mushi no ne ga shizuka ni hibiite ita.","german":"In the garden at dusk, the chirping of insects echoed quietly.","furigana":"ゆうぐ|にわ|むし|ね|しず|ひび"},{"japanese":"遠くの鐘の音を聞くたびに、故郷の朝を思い出す。","romaji":"Tōku no kane no ne o kiku tabi ni, furusato no asa o omoidasu.","german":"Every time I hear the sound of a distant bell, I remember the mornings in my hometown.","furigana":"とお|かね|ね|き|ふるさと|あさ|おも|だ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"ガル is the unit of acceleration, the gal. One gal equals one centimeter per second squared (1 cm/s²). The unit appears, for example, in measurements of gravitational acceleration or of the acceleration of earthquake motion. A number stands directly before it: 百ガル. A gal value is not a magnitude and cannot be converted into a Japanese seismic intensity level 震度 without further information. The similar-sounding がる, a verb-forming suffix, belongs to a different entry.","examples":[{"japanese":"この資料では、地震の加速度をガルで示している。","romaji":"Kono shiryō de wa, jishin no kasokudo o garu de shimeshite iru.","german":"This material gives the acceleration of the earthquake in gal.","furigana":"しりょう|じしん|かそくど|しめ"},{"japanese":"実験で測った加速度は百ガル、つまり一メートル毎秒毎秒だった。","romaji":"Jikken de hakatta kasokudo wa hyaku garu, tsumari ichi mētoru maibyō maibyō datta.","german":"The acceleration measured in the experiment was one hundred gal, that is, one meter per second squared.","furigana":"じっけん|はか|かそくど|ひゃく|いち|まいびょうまいびょう"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"宗 means a religious school or the community of its followers, especially in connection with Buddhist schools. この宗は refers to a school already mentioned. 宗を問わず means regardless of religious affiliation. The word is rather technical or written; 宗派（しゅうは）is a clearer general term. The translation \"sect\" need not carry a derogatory tone here.","examples":[{"japanese":"この宗は、鎌倉時代に広まりました。","romaji":"Kono shū wa, Kamakura jidai ni hiromarimashita.","german":"This sect spread during the Kamakura period.","furigana":"しゅう|かまくらじだい|ひろ"},{"japanese":"この墓地は、宗を問わず利用できます。","romaji":"Kono bochi wa, shū o towazu riyō dekimasu.","german":"This cemetery can be used regardless of sect.","furigana":"ぼち|しゅう|と|りよう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"After a quantity, 強 means that the actual amount is slightly above it: 三千円強 is a little more than three thousand yen. The figure is approximate; it does not state the exact extra amount. The counterpart 弱（じゃく）means a little less than. This usage goes after the whole quantity and has a different function from 強い（つよい）, strong.","examples":[{"japanese":"修理代は三千円強でした。","romaji":"Shūridai wa sanzen'en kyō deshita.","german":"The repair cost a little over three thousand yen.","furigana":"しゅうりだい|さんぜんえんきょう"},{"japanese":"参加者の九割強が、計画に賛成しました。","romaji":"Sankasha no kyūwari kyō ga, keikaku ni sansei shimashita.","german":"Slightly more than ninety percent of the participants agreed with the plan.","furigana":"さんかしゃ|きゅうわりきょう|けいかく|さんせい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The reading まつ can serve as a noun for the end of a period. The dictionary attests 三月の末: the end of March. Here の links a period with its end; a following に names the time of an action. In present-day general usage, an independent 末 is often read すえ, and 三月末（さんがつまつ）is a common compound form. This entry specifically practices the attested noun reading まつ.","examples":[{"japanese":"工事は三月の末に終わる予定です。","romaji":"Kōji wa sangatsu no matsu ni owaru yotei desu.","german":"The construction is scheduled to finish at the end of March.","furigana":"こうじ|さんがつ|まつ|お|よてい"},{"japanese":"十一月の末には、山の上に雪が見られます。","romaji":"Jūichigatsu no matsu ni wa, yama no ue ni yuki ga miraremasu.","german":"At the end of November, you can see snow on the mountaintop.","furigana":"じゅういちがつ|まつ|やま|うえ|ゆき|み"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"末 is attached to a time expression and denotes its end, for example 三月末（さんがつまつ）, end of March, or 年末（ねんまつ）, year-end. With まで you set a deadline, with に you name the time of an event. The ending follows the preceding part of the word directly. An expression like 三月末 means the last part of the month and need not refer to a single fixed day.","examples":[{"japanese":"三月末までに、申請書を提出してください。","romaji":"Sangatsumatsu made ni, shinseisho o teishutsu shite kudasai.","german":"Please submit the application form by the end of March.","furigana":"さんがつまつ|しんせいしょ|ていしゅつ"},{"japanese":"年末には、実家に帰る人が多くなります。","romaji":"Nenmatsu ni wa, jikka ni kaeru hito ga ōku narimasu.","german":"At the end of the year, more people return to their parents' home.","furigana":"ねんまつ|じっか|かえ|ひと|おお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"官 means the state or public administration and, in formal texts, is often contrasted with 民, the private sector. 官と民 names both sides, 官の役割 speaks of the task of the governmental side. The word is not a personal form of address for an individual official. For the government, 政府 is used, and for a specific agency 官庁 is often more accurate; here the public side as a whole is meant.","examples":[{"japanese":"この事業では、官と民が協力しています。","romaji":"Kono jigyō de wa, kan to min ga kyōryoku shite imasu.","german":"In this project, the public and private sectors are cooperating.","furigana":"じぎょう|かん|みん|きょうりょく"},{"japanese":"報告書は、災害対策における官の役割を明確にしました。","romaji":"Hōkokusho wa, saigai taisaku ni okeru kan no yakuwari o meikaku ni shimashita.","german":"The report clarified the role of the government in disaster preparedness.","furigana":"ほうこくしょ|さいがいたいさく|かん|やくわり|めいかく"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"In compounds, 児 means a child, for example 幼児（ようじ）, small child, and 新生児（しんせいじ）, newborn. You will meet it especially in texts about education, childcare and medicine. Such terms fix the context or age; they are not always interchangeable. In ordinary conversation, 子供 is the general word for a child. Learn each word with its whole reading, because 児 is not read じ in all compounds.","examples":[{"japanese":"この図書館では、幼児向けのお話会を開いています。","romaji":"Kono toshokan de wa, yōji muke no ohanashikai o hiraite imasu.","german":"This library holds storytelling sessions for young children.","furigana":"としょかん|ようじむ|はなしかい|ひら"},{"japanese":"看護師が新生児の体重を測りました。","romaji":"Kangoshi ga shinseiji no taijū o hakarimashita.","german":"The nurse measured the newborn's weight.","furigana":"かんごし|しんせいじ|たいじゅう|はか"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"次（じ）stands before a noun and denotes the next in a sequence, for example 次回（じかい）, next time, or 次年度（じねんど）, next business, school or fiscal year. In 次回の会議, the compound noun is specified further with の. The independent word 次（つぎ）, by contrast, is connected directly with の, for example in 次の会議. The reading depends on the whole form.","examples":[{"japanese":"次回の会議で、詳しい日程を決めましょう。","romaji":"Jikai no kaigi de, kuwashii nittei o kimemashō.","german":"Let's decide the detailed schedule at the next meeting.","furigana":"じかい|かいぎ|くわ|にってい|き"},{"japanese":"次年度の予算には、設備の修理費が含まれています。","romaji":"Jinendo no yosan ni wa, setsubi no shūrihi ga fukumarete imasu.","german":"The budget for the next fiscal year includes equipment repair costs.","furigana":"じねんど|よさん|せつび|しゅうりひ|ふく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"土 is, in calendars, schedules and opening hours, the short form of 土曜日, Saturday. After a date, a parenthesis such as （土） gives the day of the week; in 土・日 Saturday and Sunday are listed together. The short form is read ど, the full weekday name どようび. The identically written word 土（つち） for soil has a different meaning and reading here.","examples":[{"japanese":"予定表には「十日（土）に説明会」と書いてあります。","romaji":"Yoteihyō ni wa \"tōka (do) ni setsumeikai\" to kaite arimasu.","german":"The schedule says: \"Information session on Saturday the tenth.\"","furigana":"よていひょう|とおか|ど|せつめいかい|か"},{"japanese":"この店は、土・日も営業しています。","romaji":"Kono mise wa, do nichi mo eigyō shite imasu.","german":"This shop is open on Saturdays and Sundays too.","furigana":"みせ|ど|にち|えいぎょう"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 28 (source: N4; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"側 is read かわ here. It means a side and can also mean the case of a watch. With ～の側 you specify which side or which case is meant. The other common reading of the same word for side is がわ; this card specifically practices かわ. The reading そば, by contrast, means the immediate vicinity. 川（かわ）means river and belongs to a different word. The examples use 側 as a noun; the pronunciation of compound direction words is learned as a whole word each time.","examples":[{"japanese":"こちらの側から箱を開けてください。","romaji":"Kochira no kawa kara hako o akete kudasai.","german":"Please open the box from this side.","furigana":"かわ|はこ|あ"},{"japanese":"この時計の側は銀でできている。","romaji":"Kono tokei no kawa wa gin de dekite iru.","german":"The case of this watch is made of silver.","furigana":"とけい|かわ|ぎん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"眼鏡 is read がんきょう here and means eyeglasses. The usual everyday reading is めがね; がんきょう appears in technical terms such as 眼鏡店 and in older descriptions. Both readings can refer to the same vision aid. This card practices the actual object, not the figurative sense of judgment in 人を見る眼鏡. In がんきょう, きょ and the following う form the long sound kyō; you do not pronounce four separate syllables.","examples":[{"japanese":"この眼鏡では、遠くの文字が読みづらい。","romaji":"Kono gankyō de wa, tōku no moji ga yomizurai.","german":"With these glasses, it is hard to read distant letters.","furigana":"がんきょう|とお|もじ|よ"},{"japanese":"新しい眼鏡のレンズは、以前のものより軽い。","romaji":"Atarashii gankyō no renzu wa, izen no mono yori karui.","german":"The lenses of the new glasses are lighter than the previous ones.","furigana":"あたら|がんきょう|いぜん|かる"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The reading え belongs mainly to older texts about Buddhist ceremonies or festivals. 会を設ける there means holding a gathering. A present-day meeting or club is usually 会（かい）; for \"understanding\", this independent reading え is not suitable.","examples":[{"japanese":"僧たちは寺に会を設け、亡くなった人々のために祈った。","romaji":"Sōtachi wa tera ni e o mōke, nakunatta hitobito no tame ni inotta.","german":"The monks held a service at the temple and prayed for the deceased.","furigana":"そう|てら|え|もう|な|ひとびと|いの"},{"japanese":"古い記録には、祭りの会に村人が集まったとある。","romaji":"Furui kiroku ni wa, matsuri no e ni murabito ga atsumatta to aru.","german":"An old record says that villagers gathered at the festival gathering.","furigana":"ふる|きろく|まつ|え|むらびと|あつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"重（え）counts layers with the native Japanese number forms: 一重（ひとえ）、二重（ふたえ）、三重（みえ）. For fabrics, 二重に重ねる describes two layers lying on top of each other; for flowers, 八重（やえ）denotes many layers of petals. The reading え cannot simply be attached to arbitrary Sino-Japanese numbers.","examples":[{"japanese":"寒いので、毛布を二重にして寝ました。","romaji":"Samui node, mōfu o futae ni shite nemashita.","german":"Because it was cold, I slept with the blanket doubled.","furigana":"さむ|もうふ|ふたえ|ね"},{"japanese":"庭の桜には、一重の花と八重の花があります。","romaji":"Niwa no sakura ni wa, hitoe no hana to yae no hana ga arimasu.","german":"The cherry trees in the garden have single-petaled blossoms and double-petaled blossoms.","furigana":"にわ|さくら|ひとえ|はな|やえ|はな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"役（えき）appears especially in historical war names such as 文禄の役 and 慶長の役. の links the name with 役; an everyday \"task\" or a theater role, by contrast, is 役（やく）. For a present-day war, 戦争 is the usual general term.","examples":[{"japanese":"授業では、文禄の役が始まった理由を調べました。","romaji":"Jugyō de wa, Bunroku no eki ga hajimatta riyū o shirabemashita.","german":"In class, we looked into why the Bunroku Campaign began.","furigana":"じゅぎょう|ぶんろく|えき|はじ|りゆう|しら"},{"japanese":"慶長の役の後、両国の関係は少しずつ変わっていきました。","romaji":"Keichō no eki no ato, ryōkoku no kankei wa sukoshi zutsu kawatte ikimashita.","german":"After the Keicho Campaign, relations between the two countries gradually changed.","furigana":"けいちょう|えき|あと|りょうこく|かんけい|すこ|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"In chemistry, 塩（えん）denotes a class of substances that includes, for example, sodium chloride and calcium sulfate. So it is not only about edible table salt. For food, 塩 is usually read しお; in a chemical context, 塩の結晶 and 塩が生成する can have the reading えん.","examples":[{"japanese":"酸と塩基が反応すると、塩が生成する場合があります。","romaji":"San to enki ga hannō suru to, en ga seisei suru baai ga arimasu.","german":"When an acid reacts with a base, a salt may form.","furigana":"さん|えんき|はんのう|えん|せいせい|ばあい"},{"japanese":"実験では、水に溶かした塩の結晶を取り出しました。","romaji":"Jikken de wa, mizu ni tokashita en no kesshō o toridashimashita.","german":"In the experiment, we extracted crystals of a salt dissolved in water.","furigana":"じっけん|みず|と|えん|けっしょう|と|だ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"艶（えん）sounds literary and describes alluring, often sensuous beauty. As a noun it can appear in 艶を競う; as a な-adjective it connects to a noun, for example 艶な姿. The gloss of a surface is usually expressed with the other reading 艶（つや）.","examples":[{"japanese":"舞台に立つ二人の踊り手は、それぞれの艶を競っていた。","romaji":"Butai ni tatsu futari no odorite wa, sorezore no en o kisotte ita.","german":"The two dancers on the stage were competing in their respective charms.","furigana":"ぶたい|た|ふたり|おど|て|えん|きそ"},{"japanese":"彼女の艶な姿が、古い映画の観客を魅了した。","romaji":"Kanojo no en na sugata ga, furui eiga no kankyaku o miryō shita.","german":"Her alluring figure captivated the audience of the old film.","furigana":"かのじょ|えん|すがた|ふる|えいが|かんきゃく|みりょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"As an independent word, 園（えん）is a rare, rather literary term for a landscaped garden or park. In a description, 園の木陰 or 園を歩く may appear. In everyday use 庭園 or 公園 are clearer; the meaning learned here does not refer to an abbreviated kindergarten facility.","examples":[{"japanese":"旅人は園の木陰に腰を下ろした。","romaji":"Tabibito wa en no kokage ni koshi o oroshita.","german":"The traveler sat down in the shade of the trees in the garden.","furigana":"たびびと|えん|こかげ|こし|お"},{"japanese":"この園を造る際には、池の周りに遊歩道が設けられた。","romaji":"Kono en o tsukuru sai ni wa, ike no mawari ni yūhodō ga mōkerareta.","german":"When this garden was built, a promenade was laid out around the pond.","furigana":"えん|つく|さい|いけ|まわ|ゆうほどう|もう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The ending 園（えん）denotes a landscaped area in compounds: 農園 is an agricultural operation with cultivated land, 公園 a public park. The particle stands after the whole word, for example 農園で or 公園を. As an ending, 園 is attached directly to the preceding part of the word.","examples":[{"japanese":"この農園では、秋にりんごを収穫します。","romaji":"Kono nōen de wa, aki ni ringo o shūkaku shimasu.","german":"At this orchard, apples are harvested in autumn.","furigana":"のうえん|あき|しゅうかく"},{"japanese":"駅まで行くなら、公園を通ると近道になります。","romaji":"Eki made iku nara, kōen o tōru to chikamichi ni narimasu.","german":"If you're going to the station, cutting through the park is a shortcut.","furigana":"えき|い|こうえん|とお|ちかみち"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"玉（ぎょく）means a precious stone, especially jade, and in shogi the king piece. In the game it is a short form of 玉将（ぎょくしょう）; for a piece of jewelry, the context decides the gemstone meaning. The usual reading たま for a ball or sphere cannot be used here.","examples":[{"japanese":"この玉は、磨くと深い緑色の光を放ちます。","romaji":"Kono gyoku wa, migaku to fukai midoriiro no hikari o hanachimasu.","german":"This jade glows a deep green when polished.","furigana":"ぎょく|みが|ふか|みどりいろ|ひかり|はな"},{"japanese":"職人は小さな玉を彫り、帯の飾りにしました。","romaji":"Shokunin wa chīsana gyoku o hori, obi no kazari ni shimashita.","german":"The craftsman carved a small jade stone and made it into an ornament for an obi.","furigana":"しょくにん|ちい|ぎょく|ほ|おび|かざ"},{"japanese":"自分の玉が逃げられる場所を確認してください。","romaji":"Jibun no gyoku ga nigerareru basho o kakunin shite kudasai.","german":"Check the squares where your king can escape.","furigana":"じぶん|ぎょく|に|ばしょ|かくにん"},{"japanese":"相手の玉を追う前に、自分の玉の安全を考えます。","romaji":"Aite no gyoku o ou mae ni, jibun no gyoku no anzen o kangaemasu.","german":"Before chasing the opponent's king, I think about the safety of my own king.","furigana":"あいて|ぎょく|お|まえ|じぶん|ぎょく|あんぜん|かんが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"For drinks, 生（き）means \"neat, without dilution\". 生で飲む says how one drinks the beverage; 生のまま describes the unchanged state. The reading なま is used in other contexts, for example for raw food or draft beer, and is not the reading learned here.","examples":[{"japanese":"父はこのウイスキーを生で飲みます。","romaji":"Chichi wa kono uisukī o ki de nomimasu.","german":"My father drinks this whisky neat.","furigana":"ちち|き|の"},{"japanese":"この酒は、生のままだと香りがよく分かります。","romaji":"Kono sake wa, ki no mama da to kaori ga yoku wakarimasu.","german":"With this liquor, you can appreciate the aroma well when it is left undiluted.","furigana":"さけ|き|かお|わ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"As a prefix, 生（き）describes something unadulterated or not yet further processed. 生糸（きいと）is raw silk before further treatment; 生醤油（きじょうゆ）here means pure soy sauce not mixed with other ingredients. The reading belongs to each whole word and is to be distinguished from なま in other compounds.","examples":[{"japanese":"この布には、精練する前の生糸が使われています。","romaji":"Kono nuno ni wa, seiren suru mae no kiito ga tsukawarete imasu.","german":"Raw silk before degumming is used for this fabric.","furigana":"ぬの|せいれん|まえ|きいと|つか"},{"japanese":"この店では、生醤油に砂糖を加えて、たれを作ります。","romaji":"Kono mise de wa, kijōyu ni satō o kuwaete, tare o tsukurimasu.","german":"At this shop, they make the sauce by adding sugar to pure soy sauce.","furigana":"みせ|きじょうゆ|さとう|くわ|つく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The independent reading 冠（かん）appears in scholarly or historical texts for a ceremonial headdress or crown. More everyday is かんむり. In descriptions of ancient Chinese customs, 冠 can also denote the ceremony in which a young man first receives the headwear of an adult; this is not a general meaning \"to name\".","examples":[{"japanese":"この絵では、天皇が冠を着けています。","romaji":"Kono e de wa, tennō ga kan o tsukete imasu.","german":"In this picture, the emperor is wearing a ceremonial headdress.","furigana":"え|てんのう|かん|つ"},{"japanese":"金の冠には、赤い宝石がはめ込まれていた。","romaji":"Kin no kan ni wa, akai hōseki ga hamekomarete ita.","german":"Red jewels were set in the gold crown.","furigana":"きん|かん|あか|ほうせき|こ"},{"japanese":"古代中国では、成人の儀式を「冠」と呼びました。","romaji":"Kodai Chūgoku de wa, seijin no gishiki o \"kan\" to yobimashita.","german":"In ancient China, the coming-of-age ceremony was called \"kan\".","furigana":"こだいちゅうごく|せいじん|ぎしき|かん|よ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"冠（かん）appears in elevated language, especially in the pattern ～に冠たる: \"outstanding among/in ...\". たる connects the word to the following noun, as in 世界に冠たる技術. Here it is not an ordinary な-adjective and refers neither to headwear nor to the number of titles won.","examples":[{"japanese":"この地域は、世界に冠たる織物の技術を守り続けている。","romaji":"Kono chiiki wa, sekai ni kan taru orimono no gijutsu o mamoritsuzukete iru.","german":"This region continues to preserve its textile technique, outstanding in the world.","furigana":"ちいき|せかい|かん|おりもの|ぎじゅつ|まも|つづ"},{"japanese":"当時、その大学は国内に冠たる研究設備を備えていた。","romaji":"Tōji, sono daigaku wa kokunai ni kan taru kenkyū setsubi o sonaete ita.","german":"At that time, the university had research facilities unsurpassed in the country.","furigana":"とうじ|だいがく|こくない|かん|けんきゅうせつび|そな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"As an independent noun, 観（かん）denotes the outward appearance or the impression something gives. ～の観がある means roughly \"looks like ...\"; ～の観を呈する describes an appearance in elevated language. A personal worldview, by contrast, is expressed by compounds such as 人生観.","examples":[{"japanese":"改修された駅は、以前とは別の建物の観がある。","romaji":"Kaishū sareta eki wa, izen to wa betsu no tatemono no kan ga aru.","german":"The renovated station looks like a different building from before.","furigana":"かいしゅう|えき|いぜん|べつ|たてもの|かん"},{"japanese":"夕日に照らされた山々は、壮大な絵の観を呈していた。","romaji":"Yūhi ni terasareta yamayama wa, sōdai na e no kan o teishite ita.","german":"The mountains lit by the setting sun presented the sight of a magnificent painting.","furigana":"ゆうひ|て|やまやま|そうだい|え|かん|てい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The ending 観（かん）forms words for a particular way of seeing: 人生観 is an outlook on life, 価値観 are values. The compound denotes the perspective on a topic, not simply its visible surface. With が or を, the whole compound noun is worked into the sentence.","examples":[{"japanese":"留学の経験によって、私の人生観は変わりました。","romaji":"Ryūgaku no keiken ni yotte, watashi no jinseikan wa kawarimashita.","german":"My experience of studying abroad changed my outlook on life.","furigana":"りゅうがく|けいけん|わたし|じんせいかん|か"},{"japanese":"一緒に働くには、お互いの価値観を理解することが大切です。","romaji":"Issho ni hataraku ni wa, otagai no kachikan o rikai suru koto ga taisetsu desu.","german":"To work together, it is important to understand each other's values.","furigana":"いっしょ|はたら|たが|かちかん|りかい|たいせつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"This reading of 入る is found mainly in set phrases and literary texts. For ordinary entering, one now usually says はいる. In 日が入る, いる can denote sunset. In 門に入る, に marks the area entered. Despite the ending いる, this is a Godan verb: いります, いって, いった. The identically sounding 居る \"to be present\" is conjugated differently: います, いて.","examples":[{"japanese":"旅人は日暮れに城門に入った。","romaji":"Tabibito wa higure ni jōmon ni itta.","german":"The traveler entered the castle gate at dusk.","furigana":"たびびと|ひぐ|じょうもん|い"},{"japanese":"日が山の向こうに入るころ、村は静かになった。","romaji":"Hi ga yama no mukō ni iru koro, mura wa shizuka ni natta.","german":"By the time the sun set behind the mountains, the village had grown quiet.","furigana":"ひ|やま|む|い|むら|しず"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"来る／来たる with the reading きたる sounds elevated in announcements and solemn statements. Before a date, 来たる means \"coming\": 来たる五月. As a verb it denotes arrival, for example 時が来たる \"the time comes\". This reading follows Godan conjugation: きたります, きたった. It does not belong to the irregular conjugation of 来る／くる with きます and きた.","examples":[{"japanese":"来たる五月十日に、記念式典を開きます。","romaji":"Kitaru gogatsu tōka ni, kinen shikiten o hirakimasu.","german":"We will hold a commemorative ceremony on the coming May 10.","furigana":"き|ごがつとおか|きねんしきてん|ひら"},{"japanese":"長い冬が終わり、ようやく春が来たった。","romaji":"Nagai fuyu ga owari, yōyaku haru ga kitatta.","german":"The long winter ended, and spring finally came.","furigana":"なが|ふゆ|お|はる|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"堪える／こたえる here means enduring a burden. The reading is especially common in 堪えられない \"unbearable\" and in compound verbs such as 持ちこたえる; this card shows the independent verb. The burden can be marked with に. It is an Ichidan verb: こたえます, こたえて. Do not confuse it with: 応える／こたえる in 暑さが身に応える describes the effect of the heat on the body. The readings こらえる and たえる also have their own uses.","examples":[{"japanese":"窓のない部屋の暑さには、とても堪えられない。","romaji":"Mado no nai heya no atsusa ni wa, totemo kotaerarenai.","german":"I cannot possibly bear the heat in a room without windows.","furigana":"まど|へや|あつ|こた"},{"japanese":"一晩なら堪えられるが、何日も眠れないのはつらい。","romaji":"Hitoban nara kotaerareru ga, nannichi mo nemurenai no wa tsurai.","german":"I can endure one night, but being unable to sleep for several days is hard.","furigana":"ひとばん|こた|なんにち|ねむ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"出切る means that everything comes out or is completely put forward: 意見が出切る means that all opinions have been expressed. The verb describes the process with が; it is an intransitive Godan verb. Its forms are できります, できって and できった. This distinguishes it from 出来る \"can, to be made\" with できます and できた. The statement refers to complete coming-out, not in general to every form of shortage of goods.","examples":[{"japanese":"会議では、全員の意見が出切ってから結論を出した。","romaji":"Kaigi de wa, zen'in no iken ga dekitte kara ketsuron o dashita.","german":"In the meeting, a conclusion was drawn only after everyone's opinions had been fully voiced.","furigana":"かいぎ|ぜんいん|いけん|でき|けつろん|だ"},{"japanese":"箱の中の豆がすべて出切ったので、袋の口を閉じた。","romaji":"Hako no naka no mame ga subete dekitta node, fukuro no kuchi o tojita.","german":"Since all the beans in the box had come out, I closed the mouth of the bag.","furigana":"はこ|なか|まめ|でき|ふくろ|くち|と"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"する is an attested variant of 剃る／そる that also occurs in literary texts. It means cutting off hair with a blade, for example 髭をする \"to shave the beard\". The hair removed takes を. It is a Godan verb in る: すります, すらない, すって, すった. The identically sounding irregular する \"to do\", by contrast, has the forms します and した.","examples":[{"japanese":"古い小説には、男が毎朝髭をする場面が出てくる。","romaji":"Furui shōsetsu ni wa, otoko ga maiasa hige o suru bamen ga dete kuru.","german":"In the old novel, there is a scene in which a man shaves his beard every morning.","furigana":"ふる|しょうせつ|おとこ|まいあさひげ|ばめん|で"},{"japanese":"彼は頭をすってから、鏡で後ろまで確かめた。","romaji":"Kare wa atama o sutte kara, kagami de ushiro made tashikameta.","german":"After shaving his head, he checked the back in the mirror too.","furigana":"かれ|あたま|かがみ|うし|たし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"女子（おなご）denotes a woman or girl in older texts and some regional speech. Today the word can sound rough or condescending; 女性 and 女の子 are usually more suitable as a neutral term. The same kanji are mostly read じょし in present-day standard language. Here, おなご therefore appears in an old tale or as a quoted expression.","examples":[{"japanese":"昔話では、村の女子が旅人に道を教えた。","romaji":"Mukashibanashi de wa, mura no onago ga tabibito ni michi o oshieta.","german":"In the old folktale, a woman of the village showed the traveler the way.","furigana":"むかしばなし|むら|おなご|たびびと|みち|おし"},{"japanese":"祖父は幼い女の子を「小さな女子」と呼んでいた。","romaji":"Sofu wa osanai onna no ko o “chīsana onago” to yonde ita.","german":"My grandfather used to call a little girl a \"small onago\", using the regional word.","furigana":"そふ|おさな|おんな|こ|ちい|おなご|よ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"大事（おおごと）is a noun for a matter with major consequences. 大事になる means \"to become a serious matter\"; 大事にならずに済む expresses relief that it ended without serious consequences. With the other reading だいじ, 大事 can mean \"important\" or \"valuable\" as a な-adjective. For おおごと, this adjective meaning must not simply be carried over.","examples":[{"japanese":"傷は浅く、大事にならずに済みました。","romaji":"Kizu wa asaku, ōgoto ni narazu ni sumimashita.","german":"The wound was shallow, and it did not turn into anything serious.","furigana":"きず|あさ|おおごと|す"},{"japanese":"会社の秘密が外に漏れたと知り、社長は「これは大事だ」と言った。","romaji":"Kaisha no himitsu ga soto ni moreta to shiri, shachō wa “kore wa ōgoto da” to itta.","german":"Learning that a company secret had leaked outside, the president said, \"This is a serious matter.\"","furigana":"かいしゃ|ひみつ|そと|も|し|しゃちょう|おおごと|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"黄色（おうしょく）is a rather formal term for the color yellow, for example in technical descriptions. As a noun it can take を: 黄色を呈する \"to show a yellow coloring\". Before another noun, の links the color with the object. In everyday use, the reading きいろ is far more common.","examples":[{"japanese":"この溶液は、温めると黄色を呈します。","romaji":"Kono yōeki wa, atatameru to ōshoku o teishimasu.","german":"This solution takes on a yellow color when heated.","furigana":"ようえき|あたた|おうしょく|てい"},{"japanese":"機械の黄色の警告灯が点滅したので、作業を止めました。","romaji":"Kikai no ōshoku no keikokutō ga tenmetsu shita node, sagyō o tomemashita.","german":"The machine's yellow warning light was blinking, so I stopped the work.","furigana":"きかい|おうしょく|けいこくとう|てんめつ|さぎょう|と"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"空き間（あきま）denotes a room that is currently not in use or has no tenant yet. You can ask 空き間があるか or 空き間を貸す. The rarer writing 空間 has the same reading あきま here; the ordinary 空間（くうかん）, by contrast, means \"space\" in the general sense. In present-day daily life, 空き部屋 and 空室 are often more common.","examples":[{"japanese":"今夜、この宿に空き間はありますか。","romaji":"Kon'ya, kono yado ni akima wa arimasu ka.","german":"Is there a vacant room at this inn tonight?","furigana":"こんや|やど|あ|ま"},{"japanese":"大家は二階の空き間を学生に貸すことにしました。","romaji":"Ōya wa nikai no akima o gakusei ni kasu koto ni shimashita.","german":"The landlord decided to rent out the vacant room on the second floor to a student.","furigana":"おおや|にかい|あ|ま|がくせい|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"下番（かばん）denotes the end of an assigned duty, especially in the military. As a noun it appears, for example, in 下番の時刻 \"time of going off duty\" or 下番後 \"after duty\". 下番する is also possible. The opposite 上番（じょうばん）denotes the start of duty. The similar-sounding かばん \"bag\" is a different word.","examples":[{"japanese":"下番の時刻が近づくと、次の衛兵が門に来ました。","romaji":"Kaban no jikoku ga chikazuku to, tsugi no eihei ga mon ni kimashita.","german":"As the time for going off duty approached, the next guard came to the gate.","furigana":"かばん|じこく|ちか|つぎ|えいへい|もん|き"},{"japanese":"下番後、兵士たちは宿舎で休息を取りました。","romaji":"Kaban go, heishitachi wa shukusha de kyūsoku o torimashita.","german":"After going off duty, the soldiers rested in their quarters.","furigana":"かばんご|へいし|しゅくしゃ|きゅうそく|と"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"丈夫（じょうふ）in elevated or older language denotes a man portrayed as brave and imposing. It is a noun: 堂々たる丈夫 describes a man of impressive bearing. In present-day conversation the term sounds unusual. The reading じょうぶ, by contrast, mostly means \"healthy, sturdy\" or \"durable\" and belongs to a different use of the same kanji.","examples":[{"japanese":"物語の主人公は、堂々たる丈夫として描かれている。","romaji":"Monogatari no shujinkō wa, dōdō taru jōfu to shite egakarete iru.","german":"The protagonist of the story is depicted as an imposing, dignified man.","furigana":"ものがたり|しゅじんこう|どうどう|じょうふ|えが"},{"japanese":"村人を救うために危険を恐れなかった彼を、詩人は丈夫とたたえた。","romaji":"Murabito o sukuu tame ni kiken o osorenakatta kare o, shijin wa jōfu to tataeta.","german":"The poet praised him as a hero because he did not fear danger in order to save the villagers.","furigana":"むらびと|すく|きけん|おそ|かれ|しじん|じょうふ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"不憫（ふびん）describes someone whose situation arouses pity. With 不憫に思う you say that you feel sorry for someone; before a noun it takes な. The rare older writing 不便 can also be read ふびん. Today, however, 不便 is usually read ふべん and means \"inconvenient, awkward\", so 不憫 is clearer for this meaning.","examples":[{"japanese":"家族と離れて暮らす幼い子を、不憫に思いました。","romaji":"Kazoku to hanarete kurasu osanai ko o, fubin ni omoimashita.","german":"I felt pity for the small child living apart from his family.","furigana":"かぞく|はな|く|おさな|こ|ふびん|おも"},{"japanese":"飼い主に捨てられた不憫な犬を、隣の人が引き取りました。","romaji":"Kainushi ni suterareta fubin na inu o, tonari no hito ga hikitorimashita.","german":"A neighbor took in the pitiable dog that its owner had abandoned.","furigana":"か|ぬし|す|ふびん|いぬ|となり|ひと|ひ|と"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"ほとり often denotes the shore of a lake or river, but also the edge or immediate vicinity of a place. 湖のほとり is \"on the shore of the lake\"; 道のほとり sounds rather literary and means the wayside. The word is usually written in hiragana. The writing 辺り is unusual for this reading; as あたり it usually denotes a whole surrounding area.","examples":[{"japanese":"湖のほとりに小さな宿があります。","romaji":"Mizuumi no hotori ni chīsana yado ga arimasu.","german":"There is a small inn on the shore of the lake.","furigana":"みずうみ|ちい|やど"},{"japanese":"道のほとりに腰を下ろし、旅人は地図を広げた。","romaji":"Michi no hotori ni koshi o oroshi, tabibito wa chizu o hirogeta.","german":"The traveler sat down at the roadside and spread out his map.","furigana":"みち|こし|お|たびびと|ちず|ひろ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"うわて denotes an upper area or superiority in comparison. The spatial use sounds rather elevated in descriptions. In 一枚上手 \"one notch superior\", no sheets are being counted: the phrase compares skill or ability. The general \"good at something\", by contrast, is 上手／じょうず. For the stage side, 上手 is read かみて.","examples":[{"japanese":"坂の上手に、古い社が見える。","romaji":"Saka no uwate ni, furui yashiro ga mieru.","german":"An old shrine can be seen at the upper part of the slope.","furigana":"さか|うわて|ふる|やしろ|み"},{"japanese":"交渉では、経験のある彼女のほうが一枚上手だった。","romaji":"Kōshō de wa, keiken no aru kanojo no hō ga ichimai uwate datta.","german":"In the negotiations, she, with her experience, had the upper hand by one notch.","furigana":"こうしょう|けいけん|かのじょ|いちまいうわて"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"きんきん announces an event in the near future without fixing an exact day. It stands as an adverb before the planned or expected action. The writings 近々 and 近近 are attested; 近々 is the form shown here. The word is also read ちかぢか. The spatial meaning \"very close\" belongs to the reading ちかぢか; this card practices the temporal きんきん.","examples":[{"japanese":"近々、新しい支店を開く予定です。","romaji":"Kinkin, atarashii shiten o hiraku yotei desu.","german":"We plan to open a new branch soon.","furigana":"きんきん|あたら|してん|ひら|よてい"},{"japanese":"修理の見積もりは近々届くそうです。","romaji":"Shūri no mitsumori wa kinkin todoku sō desu.","german":"The repair estimate is said to arrive shortly.","furigana":"しゅうり|みつ|きんきんとど"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"床／とこ means the bed or bedding, especially in phrases like 床に就く \"to go to bed\". In illness it can mean the sickbed: 病の床に伏す means to lie ill in bed. The person concerned takes は or が. For the floor, use the other reading ゆか. Also 床の間／とこのま \"decorative alcove\" is a fixed word combination of its own.","examples":[{"japanese":"翌朝が早いので、今夜は九時に床に就く。","romaji":"Yokuasa ga hayai node, kon'ya wa kuji ni toko ni tsuku.","german":"Since I have to get up early tomorrow morning, I'm going to bed at nine tonight.","furigana":"よくあさ|はや|こんや|くじ|とこ|つ"},{"japanese":"病の床に伏す母に、家族からの手紙を読んで聞かせた。","romaji":"Yamai no toko ni fusu haha ni, kazoku kara no tegami o yonde kikaseta.","german":"I read a letter from the family to my mother, who lay ill in bed.","furigana":"やまい|とこ|ふ|はは|かぞく|てがみ|よ|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"はじく means to hit or snap something away with a quick spring-like motion, for example flicking a marble with a finger. The moved object takes を. With a material, 水をはじく means that it repels water or makes it bead off. The transitive Godan verb forms はじきます, はじいて and はじいた. For playing piano or guitar, 弾く／ひく is the usual reading; this card practices flicking and repelling.","examples":[{"japanese":"指でビー玉を弾くと、机の端まで転がった。","romaji":"Yubi de bīdama o hajiku to, tsukue no hashi made korogatta.","german":"When I flicked the marble with my finger, it rolled to the edge of the desk.","furigana":"ゆび|だま|はじ|つくえ|はし|ころ"},{"japanese":"この布は水をよく弾くので、雨具に使われている。","romaji":"Kono nuno wa mizu o yoku hajiku node, amagu ni tsukawarete iru.","german":"This fabric repels water well, so it is used for rain gear.","furigana":"ぬの|みず|はじ|あまぐ|つか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"帯／たい is a word component here, placed after a noun. It denotes a delimited area, often band-shaped in extent: 時間帯 \"time slot, time window\", 温帯 \"temperate climate zone\". The meaning is clear from the whole word; in this use たい does not stand alone as a sentence predicate. The belt of a kimono is called 帯／おび. For compound words, you must also learn the pronunciation of the whole word.","examples":[{"japanese":"この時間帯は電車が混むので、少し早く出ます。","romaji":"Kono jikantai wa densha ga komu node, sukoshi hayaku demasu.","german":"The trains are crowded at this time of day, so I leave a little earlier.","furigana":"じかんたい|でんしゃ|こ|すこ|はや|で"},{"japanese":"温帯でも、地域によって雨の量は大きく違う。","romaji":"Ontai demo, chiiki ni yotte ame no ryō wa ōkiku chigau.","german":"Even within the temperate zone, the amount of rain differs greatly by region.","furigana":"おんたい|ちいき|あめ|りょう|おお|ちが"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"きょう bezeichnet hier einen Abschnitt des Hirnstamms zwischen Mittelhirn und verlängertem Mark. Die deutsche anatomische Bezeichnung lautet „Pons“ oder „Brücke“. Diese Lesung gehört zur Fachsprache der Anatomie; eine Brücke als Bauwerk heißt はし. In einer Beschreibung kann 橋の構造 „Aufbau des Pons“ bedeuten, während eine Abbildung den Abschnitt mit 橋 beschriftet.","examples":[{"japanese":"解剖図では、中脳と延髄の間にある部分が「橋」と示されていた。","romaji":"Kaibōzu de wa, chūnō to enzui no aida ni aru bubun ga \"kyō\" to shimesarete ita.","german":"In der anatomischen Abbildung war der Abschnitt zwischen Mittelhirn und verlängertem Mark als „Pons“ bezeichnet.","furigana":"かいぼうず|ちゅうのう|えんずい|あいだ|ぶぶん|きょう|しめ"},{"japanese":"学生は、橋の構造について発表した。","romaji":"Gakusei wa, kyō no kōzō ni tsuite happyō shita.","german":"Der Student hielt einen Vortrag über den Aufbau des Pons.","furigana":"がくせい|きょう|こうぞう|はっぴょう"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"やく here is a colloquial word for narcotics; it is often written ヤク. It sounds much more slangy than 麻薬 and can appear in crime stories or in quoted speech. For ordinary medicine, one says くすり. The reading やく in compounds for medicines therefore does not automatically mean the narcotics meant here.","examples":[{"japanese":"小説の中で、男は「ヤクには手を出すな」と弟に言った。","romaji":"Shōsetsu no naka de, otoko wa \"Yaku ni wa te o dasu na\" to otōto ni itta.","german":"In the novel, the man told his younger brother, \"Stay away from drugs.\"","furigana":"しょうせつ|なか|おとこ|て|だ|おとうと|い"},{"japanese":"記者は、薬を売っていた男の逮捕について記事を書いた。","romaji":"Kisha wa, yaku o utte ita otoko no taiho ni tsuite kiji o kaita.","german":"The reporter wrote an article about the arrest of a man who had been selling drugs.","furigana":"きしゃ|やく|う|おとこ|たいほ|きじ|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"み here is an independent technical term in Buddhist descriptions of perception. It denotes taste as the object that the tongue perceives. In the classification into six objects of perception, 味 stands beside 香 こう, the object of smell. What is meant is the perceived taste, not the sense organ or the ability to taste. For the ordinary taste of a dish, one says あじ. The suffix in 甘み and the counter for kinds of food or medicine have other uses.","examples":[{"japanese":"仏教の六境の分類では、舌がとらえる対象を味と呼ぶ。","romaji":"Bukkyō no rokkyō no bunrui de wa, shita ga toraeru taishō o mi to yobu.","german":"In the Buddhist classification of the six objects of perception, the object grasped by the tongue is called taste.","furigana":"ぶっきょう|ろっきょう|ぶんるい|した|たいしょう|み|よ"},{"japanese":"仏教の授業で香と味を同じものとして説明したら、先生に区別するよう注意された。","romaji":"Bukkyō no jugyō de kō to mi o onaji mono to shite setsumei shitara, sensei ni kubetsu suru yō chūi sareta.","german":"When I explained smell and taste as the same thing in Buddhism class, the teacher cautioned me to distinguish them.","furigana":"ぶっきょう|じゅぎょう|こう|み|おな|せつめい|せんせい|くべつ|ちゅうい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"しん denotes a person's mental attitude or inner nature. The reading sounds more literary, or related to inner firmness, than the everyday こころ. In phrases like 心が強い, mental steadfastness is in the foreground. In the second example, 心から is also read specifically しんから, a literary-sounding phrase for \"from the bottom of one's heart\". In everyday speech, the reading こころから is common. The material core of an object is usually written 芯. That does not mean that 心 can be replaced by 芯 in every expression.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女はおだやかだが、心の強い人だ。","romaji":"Kanojo wa odayaka da ga, shin no tsuyoi hito da.","german":"She is gentle but has great inner strength.","furigana":"かのじょ|しん|つよ|ひと"},{"japanese":"彼は心から、その仕事を続けたいと願っていた。","romaji":"Kare wa shin kara, sono shigoto o tsuzuketai to negatte ita.","german":"Deep in his heart, he wished to continue that work.","furigana":"かれ|しん|しごと|つづ|ねが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"界 here is the biological \"kingdom\", a level in the classification of living things. It lies above 門 and below the superordinate domain. Words such as 動物界 and 植物界 name individual kingdoms; as a noun, 界 can also denote the rank itself or one of these kingdoms. The classification and the number of kingdoms depend on the system used. The meaning \"world or social sphere\" in 政界 is a different use.","examples":[{"japanese":"生物の分類では、界は門より上の階級である。","romaji":"Seibutsu no bunrui de wa, kai wa mon yori ue no kaikyū de aru.","german":"In biological classification, the kingdom is a rank above the phylum.","furigana":"せいぶつ|ぶんるい|かい|もん|うえ|かいきゅう"},{"japanese":"先生は、この二つの生物が同じ界に属するかどうかを尋ねた。","romaji":"Sensei wa, kono futatsu no seibutsu ga onaji kai ni zokusuru ka dō ka o tazuneta.","german":"The teacher asked whether these two organisms belong to the same kingdom.","furigana":"せんせい|ふた|せいぶつ|おな|かい|ぞく|たず"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"画（かく）here denotes a stroke or dot made in one continuous motion of writing that helps build up a kanji. In writing, one speaks, for example, of the last 画 of a character. It is an independent noun; the identically written counter after a number is treated separately. The reading が for a picture and the verb 書く（かく）are different words.","examples":[{"japanese":"この画は長すぎるので、少し短くしてください。","romaji":"Kono kaku wa nagasugiru node, sukoshi mijikaku shite kudasai.","german":"This stroke is too long; please make it a little shorter.","furigana":"かく|なが|すこ|みじか"},{"japanese":"先生は最後の画を赤いペンで示した。","romaji":"Sensei wa saigo no kaku o akai pen de shimeshita.","german":"The teacher marked the last stroke with a red pen.","furigana":"せんせい|さいご|かく|あか|しめ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"過多 denotes too large an amount, mostly in factual or technical language. It often follows a noun, for example 情報過多 \"information overload\" or 供給過多 \"oversupply\". Unlike 多い, it explicitly means \"too much\". As a na-adjective, 過多な is also possible; it is not an i-adjective. The identically read words 肩 and 型 have other meanings.","examples":[{"japanese":"情報過多で、必要な記事を見つけられない。","romaji":"Jōhō kata de, hitsuyō na kiji o mitsukerarenai.","german":"Because of information overload, I can't find the article I need.","furigana":"じょうほうかた|ひつよう|きじ|み"},{"japanese":"市場では供給過多が続き、価格が下がった。","romaji":"Shijō de wa kyōkyū kata ga tsuzuki, kakaku ga sagatta.","german":"Oversupply continued in the market, and prices fell.","furigana":"しじょう|きょうきゅうかた|つづ|かかく|さ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"対辺 is a geometric technical term. In a triangle, the 対辺 lies opposite a particular interior angle; in a quadrilateral, two sides facing each other can be called that. The reference point must be clear from the problem or the drawing. The identically read 大変 means, among other things, \"difficult\" and does not belong to this card.","examples":[{"japanese":"この三角形で、赤い角の対辺を指してください。","romaji":"Kono sankakkei de, akai kaku no taihen o sashite kudasai.","german":"In this triangle, please point to the side opposite the red angle.","furigana":"さんかっけい|あか|かく|たいへん|さ"},{"japanese":"長方形の対辺は長さが等しい。","romaji":"Chōhōkei no taihen wa nagasa ga hitoshii.","german":"The opposite sides of a rectangle are equal in length.","furigana":"ちょうほうけい|たいへん|なが|ひと"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"おおい is a loud call to someone at some distance. It can, for example, get the attention of a friend on the other side of the street or call for a person. The writing おーい is also common. The call is casual; for politely addressing someone nearby, something like すみません is suitable. 多い（おおい）\"many\" and 覆い（おおい）\"cover\" are different words.","examples":[{"japanese":"おおい、川の向こうにいるのは君か。","romaji":"Ōi, kawa no mukō ni iru no wa kimi ka.","german":"Hey, is that you across the river?","furigana":"かわ|む|きみ"},{"japanese":"船が離れる前に、岸から「おおい、忘れ物だぞ」と叫んだ。","romaji":"Fune ga hanareru mae ni, kishi kara \"ōi, wasuremono da zo\" to sakenda.","german":"Before the boat pulled away, I shouted from the shore, \"Hey, you forgot something!\"","furigana":"ふね|はな|まえ|きし|わす|もの|さけ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"佳句（かく）denotes an especially beautiful or successful phrasing in a poem; an outstanding haiku can also be called that. The word belongs to literary criticism and sounds elevated. It does not denote arbitrary everyday sentences. In conversations about poetry, the context is usually needed. 書く \"to write\", 画 \"stroke\" and 核 \"core\" are different words despite the same reading.","examples":[{"japanese":"詩集を読みながら、心に残る佳句に線を引いた。","romaji":"Shishū o yominagara, kokoro ni nokoru kaku ni sen o hiita.","german":"While reading the poetry collection, I underlined lines that stayed in my mind.","furigana":"ししゅう|よ|こころ|のこ|かく|せん|ひ"},{"japanese":"俳句の会で、先生は参加者の佳句を紹介した。","romaji":"Haiku no kai de, sensei wa sankasha no kaku o shōkai shita.","german":"At the haiku gathering, the teacher presented the participants' fine haiku.","furigana":"はいく|かい|せんせい|さんかしゃ|かく|しょうかい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"戸（こ）follows a number and counts houses, households or individual dwelling units. For an apartment building with several flats, it therefore often counts the flats. Examples are 五百戸（ごひゃっこ）\"five hundred units\" and 十戸（じゅっこ）\"ten units\". The number can change the pronunciation: here こ becomes a doubled k. This card deals with the counter, not an independent word for a door; a door is called 戸（と）. The accents of complete number combinations are determined separately.","examples":[{"japanese":"この地区には住宅が五百戸ある。","romaji":"Kono chiku ni wa jūtaku ga gohyakko aru.","german":"There are five hundred dwelling units in this district.","furigana":"ちく|じゅうたく|ごひゃっこ"},{"japanese":"新しいマンションでは、来月十戸を売り出す。","romaji":"Atarashii manshon de wa, raigetsu jukko o uridasu.","german":"In the new apartment building, ten units will be put on sale next month.","furigana":"あたら|らいげつじゅっこ|う|だ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"衆（しゅう）here is an independent noun for a large number of people or the numerical superiority of a group. It sounds elevated and appears in phrases such as 衆に先んじる \"to be ahead of others\" or 衆を頼む \"to rely on superior numbers\". The attached ～衆 in 旦那衆 or 若い衆 has a different grammatical function. The reading しゅ and historical court offices are not practiced on this card. 週 \"week\" is a different word.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は衆に先んじて新しい方法を提案した。","romaji":"Kanojo wa shū ni sakinjite atarashii hōhō o teian shita.","german":"She proposed a new method ahead of everyone else.","furigana":"かのじょ|しゅう|さき|あたら|ほうほう|ていあん"},{"japanese":"衆を頼んで相手を脅すのは卑怯だ。","romaji":"Shū o tanonde aite o odosu no wa hikyō da.","german":"It is cowardly to rely on one's superior numbers to intimidate the other side.","furigana":"しゅう|たの|あいて|おど|ひきょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"体（てい）here denotes the outwardly recognizable form or state of a person or thing, sometimes also a deliberately created appearance. It sounds literary and often follows の or a descriptive form, as in 何も知らない体 \"as if one knew nothing\". 態 is also attested for this reading. 体（からだ）\"body\" and 体（たい）in other technical or counter uses are separate readings. The ending ～体 as a disparaging designation is not practiced here.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は何も知らない体で質問に答えた。","romaji":"Kare wa nani mo shiranai tei de shitsumon ni kotaeta.","german":"He answered the question as if he knew nothing.","furigana":"かれ|なに|し|てい|しつもん|こた"},{"japanese":"雨にぬれた一行は、見るも哀れな体だった。","romaji":"Ame ni nureta ikkō wa, miru mo aware na tei datta.","german":"The party, soaked by the rain, was a pitiful sight.","furigana":"あめ|いっこう|み|あわ|てい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"干し（ほし）as a noun denotes drying or the degree of drying, for example in 干しが足りない \"not dried enough\". Before another noun, it forms words for dried products, such as 干し柿（ほしがき）\"dried persimmons\" or 干し肉（ほしにく）\"dried meat\". Here \"dried\" is a prefix meaning, not an adjective used independently. 乾し is also attested; 干 and 乾 without し are irregular writing variants for exactly this reading and meaning. Other readings of 乾 are not derived from this. The accent of the nominal entry is not carried over unchecked to the prefix and its compounds.","examples":[{"japanese":"この魚は干しが足りないので、もう少し日に当てよう。","romaji":"Kono sakana wa hoshi ga tarinai node, mō sukoshi hi ni ateyō.","german":"This fish isn't dried enough; let's leave it in the sun a bit longer.","furigana":"さかな|ほ|た|すこ|ひ|あ"},{"japanese":"海苔の干しには、十分な日差しが必要だ。","romaji":"Nori no hoshi ni wa, jūbun na hizashi ga hitsuyō da.","german":"Drying nori requires plenty of sunshine.","furigana":"のり|ほ|じゅうぶん|ひざ|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"干し柿は、涼しい場所に保存してください。","romaji":"Hoshigaki wa, suzushii basho ni hozon shite kudasai.","german":"Please store the dried persimmons in a cool place.","furigana":"ほ|がき|すず|ばしょ|ほぞん"},{"japanese":"山では、干し肉を少しずつ食べて体力を保った。","romaji":"Yama de wa, hoshiniku o sukoshi zutsu tabete tairyoku o tamotta.","german":"In the mountains, I ate jerky little by little to keep up my strength.","furigana":"やま|ほ|にく|すこ|た|たいりょく|たも"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"と is, in shogi, the short form of と金（ときん）, the promoted pawn. The piece arises from a 歩（ふ）through promotion and then moves like a 金（きん）. The short form appears mainly in commentary, notation and talk about a game. The expression is an independent noun, not a conditional particle.","examples":[{"japanese":"相手のとを取って、攻めを止めた。","romaji":"Aite no to o totte, seme o tometa.","german":"I captured the opponent's promoted pawn and stopped the attack.","furigana":"あいて|と|せ|と"},{"japanese":"歩を進めてとを作り、王に迫った。","romaji":"Fu o susumete to o tsukuri, ō ni sematta.","german":"I advanced a pawn, promoted it, and pressed in on the king.","furigana":"ふ|すす|つく|おう|せま"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"画期（かっき）is a noun for a break that separates a past era from a new one. It is used in factual or historical language, for example in 画期をなす \"to form an epochal turning point\". The na-adjective \"groundbreaking, epoch-making\" is 画期的（かっきてき）; the bare 画期 is not used as an adjective here. The rarer writing 劃期 is also attested. 活気（かっき）\"liveliness\" means something different.","examples":[{"japanese":"この発見は、研究の歴史に大きな画期をなした。","romaji":"Kono hakken wa, kenkyū no rekishi ni ōkina kakki o nashita.","german":"This discovery marked a major turning point in the history of research.","furigana":"はっけん|けんきゅう|れきし|おお|かっき"},{"japanese":"戦争の終結は、この国の歴史を分ける画期となった。","romaji":"Sensō no shūketsu wa, kono kuni no rekishi o wakeru kakki to natta.","german":"The end of the war became a turning point dividing this country's history.","furigana":"せんそう|しゅうけつ|くに|れきし|わ|かっき"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"持ち（もち）here denotes a draw: in a contest, no winner can be determined. The reading is attested especially in older texts about 歌合わせ \"poetry contests\" or board games. Today 引き分け is usually more common. 持ちとなる and 持ちとされる mean in this context \"to end in a draw\" and \"to be ruled a draw\" respectively. The reading じ of 持 as well as \"possession\" and \"durability\" are not equated on this card.","examples":[{"japanese":"碁の勝負は持ちとなり、二人は笑って盤を片づけた。","romaji":"Go no shōbu wa mochi to nari, futari wa waratte ban o katazuketa.","german":"The Go game ended in a draw, and the two laughed as they cleared the board.","furigana":"ご|しょうぶ|も|ふたり|わら|ばん|かた"},{"japanese":"歌合わせで優劣がつかず、その番は持ちとされた。","romaji":"Utaawase de yūretsu ga tsukazu, sono ban wa mochi to sareta.","german":"In the poetry contest, no better entry could be determined, and that round was ruled a draw.","furigana":"うたあ|ゆうれつ|ばん|も"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Colloquial verb for \"to have an orgasm\", often written in katakana as イク. It is an expression for intimate conversations between adults; in neutral language オルガスムに達する is clearer. Only いく is read here. The form ゆく does not belong to this meaning. This intransitive godan verb forms the te-form いって and the past tense いった; the negative is いかない. The motion meaning \"to go\" is covered on its own card.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は、いったふりをしたことをパートナーに打ち明けた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa, itta furi o shita koto o pātonā ni uchiaketa.","german":"She confessed to her partner that she had faked an orgasm.","furigana":"かのじょ|う|あ"},{"japanese":"彼は「まだいっていない」とパートナーに伝えた。","romaji":"Kare wa “mada itte inai” to pātonā ni tsutaeta.","german":"He told his partner, \"I haven't climaxed yet.\"","furigana":"かれ|つた"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5 (source: N5; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"A historical noun for a younger brother or younger sister. Today 弟（おとうと）usually denotes the younger brother; the short reading おと appears in older texts. The writing 乙 is also attested for this old word. The examples are narrative contexts with the deliberately historical reading おと. The noun is kept separate here from the old prefix for \"younger\" and from the name of a kyogen mask.","examples":[{"japanese":"兄が旅立つ朝、弟は門の前で見送った。","romaji":"Ani ga tabidatsu asa, oto wa mon no mae de miokutta.","german":"On the morning his older brother set off, the younger brother saw him off in front of the gate.","furigana":"あに|たびだ|あさ|おと|もん|まえ|みおく"},{"japanese":"姉は幼い弟に、昔話を聞かせた。","romaji":"Ane wa osanai oto ni, mukashibanashi o kikaseta.","german":"The older sister told her little sister a tale from long ago.","furigana":"あね|おさな|おと|むかしばなし|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"An elevated or literary noun for the visible appearance, form or state of a thing. Here 象 is read specifically しょう; the everyday reading ぞう means \"elephant\". For a concrete phenomenon, one now usually says 現象（げんしょう）. The examples use the older independent sense \"appearance\", for example in a landscape description or when viewing a painting.","examples":[{"japanese":"山々は、夕日を受けて荘厳な象を示した。","romaji":"Yamayama wa, yūhi o ukete sōgon na shō o shimeshita.","german":"The mountains showed a majestic aspect in the light of the setting sun.","furigana":"やまやま|ゆうひ|う|そうごん|しょう|しめ"},{"japanese":"この絵は、荒れた海の象をよく伝えている。","romaji":"Kono e wa, areta umi no shō o yoku tsutaete iru.","german":"This painting conveys the look of the stormy sea well.","furigana":"え|あ|うみ|しょう|つた"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Sehr seltene japanische Zahleneinheit für 10²⁴, also eine Quadrillion im deutschen Zahlensystem. Sie entspricht zehntausend 垓（がい）. Die kana-Schreibung し bezeichnet auf dieser Karte ausschließlich diese Zahleneinheit. Ältere Zählsysteme wiesen 秭 auch andere Werte zu; hier gilt der in der Quelle gewählte Wert 10²⁴. Die Einheit gehört zur Reihe großer Zahlennamen und wird nicht mit der gleich klingenden Partikel し oder 四 für „vier“ verwechselt.","examples":[{"japanese":"秭は、十の二十四乗を表す数の単位だ。","romaji":"Shi wa, jū no nijūyonjō o arawasu kazu no tan'i da.","german":"Shi ist eine Zahleneinheit, die zehn hoch vierundzwanzig bezeichnet.","furigana":"し|じゅう|にじゅうよんじょう|あらわ|かず|たんい"},{"japanese":"この計算では、粒子の数を秭で表した。","romaji":"Kono keisan de wa, ryūshi no kazu o shi de arawashita.","german":"In dieser Berechnung wurde die Zahl der Teilchen in der Einheit Shi angegeben.","furigana":"けいさん|りゅうし|かず|し|あらわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A historical noun, found mainly in texts about ancient China, for the four horses of a team or the chariot they pull. The reading is し; on this card, the kana writing し is to be understood exclusively as a phonetic writing of this noun. Today one usually says 四頭立ての馬車 or names the team explicitly. The two examples distinguish riding in the chariot from leading the four horses.","examples":[{"japanese":"王は駟に乗って、城を出た。","romaji":"Ō wa shi ni notte, shiro o deta.","german":"The king left the castle in a four-horse chariot.","furigana":"おう|し|の|しろ|で"},{"japanese":"御者は、駟を小川へ連れていった。","romaji":"Gyosha wa, shi o ogawa e tsurete itta.","german":"The coachman led the team of four to a stream.","furigana":"ぎょしゃ|し|おがわ|つ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"An older or deliberately elevated sentence-final question particle marking a question or rhetorical question. The rare writing 哉 is read や here; the usual writing is the kana や. In present-day everyday speech, one would mostly use か or a corresponding question form. 哉 here denotes a spoken word marking a question, not a punctuation mark like \"?\". The examples deliberately use an elevated question tone. The reading かな for an exclamation and the enumerating particle や are other uses.","examples":[{"japanese":"この知らせは、真実であろう哉。","romaji":"Kono shirase wa, shinjitsu de arō ya.","german":"Could this news be true?","furigana":"し|しんじつ|や"},{"japanese":"どうしてその約束を破れよう哉。","romaji":"Dōshite sono yakusoku o yabureyō ya.","german":"How could one break that promise?","furigana":"やくそく|やぶ|や"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"As a mathematical noun, 周（しゅう）denotes the boundary or perimeter of a closed figure. As a counter, it counts complete laps or revolutions: 一周（いっしゅう）is one lap, 三周（さんしゅう）is three laps. When counting, the number comes directly before. The accent information applies to the single word stem; number and counter together form their own word whose accent can differ. The Zhou dynasty 周 and the rare noun for surroundings have their own cards; 週 means \"week\".","examples":[{"japanese":"この三角形の周は、十二センチメートルだ。","romaji":"Kono sankakkei no shū wa, jūni senchimētoru da.","german":"The perimeter of this triangle is twelve centimeters.","furigana":"さんかっけい|しゅう|じゅうに"},{"japanese":"選手たちは、競技場のトラックを三周走った。","romaji":"Senshu-tachi wa, kyōgijō no torakku o sanshū hashitta.","german":"The athletes ran three laps around the stadium track.","furigana":"せんしゅ|きょうぎじょう|さんしゅうはし"},{"japanese":"宇宙船は、地球の周りを二周した。","romaji":"Uchūsen wa, chikyū no mawari o nishū shita.","german":"The spacecraft orbited the Earth twice.","furigana":"うちゅうせん|ちきゅう|まわ|にしゅう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"An elevated or older independent noun for the area around a place. Here 周 is read しゅう; the everyday words for \"surroundings\" are 周囲（しゅうい）or まわり. In a deliberately elevated description, 城の周 can, for example, denote the surroundings of a castle. This spatial sense means neither the mathematically measured perimeter nor a counted lap, nor the Zhou dynasty. The examples reproduce the older nominal usage in a historical narrative and a landscape description.","examples":[{"japanese":"兵は、城の周に陣を張った。","romaji":"Hei wa, shiro no shū ni jin o hatta.","german":"The soldiers made camp in the area around the castle.","furigana":"へい|しろ|しゅう|じん|は"},{"japanese":"寺の周には、深い森が広がっていた。","romaji":"Tera no shū ni wa, fukai mori ga hirogatte ita.","german":"A dense forest spread around the temple.","furigana":"てら|しゅう|ふか|もり|ひろ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A noun derived from English \"crab\", for a crab or its meat. In Japanese it appears especially as part of food or species names, such as クラブケーキ \"crab cake\" and ストーンクラブ \"stone crab\". The ordinary Japanese word for the animal is かに（蟹）. In such compound names, クラブ designates the animal or its meat. The identically written word derived from \"club\", for an association, nightclub or golf club, belongs to the other card. Its accent information does not automatically apply to the crab sense.","examples":[{"japanese":"前菜に、クラブケーキを一つ注文した。","romaji":"Zensai ni, kurabu kēki o hitotsu chūmon shita.","german":"For an appetizer, I ordered one crab cake.","furigana":"ぜんさい|ひと|ちゅうもん"},{"japanese":"料理人は、ストーンクラブを蒸して殻を割った。","romaji":"Ryōrinin wa, sutōn kurabu o mushite kara o watta.","german":"The cook steamed a stone crab and cracked open its shell.","furigana":"りょうりにん|む|から|わ"}],"levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 14 (source: N4; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"A prefix before verbs and in set words that expresses reciprocity or simultaneity: 相次ぐ \"to happen one after another\", 相変わらず \"as always\", 相対する \"to face each other\". In 相成る or 相済まない, it merely gives the sentence a solemn, formal tone. Not productive; the Sino-Japanese reading そう (相談, 相当) is a different use of the character, and 相手 (あいて) is learned as a word of its own.","examples":[{"japanese":"最近、大きな地震が相次いでいる。","romaji":"Saikin, ookina jishin ga aitsuide iru.","german":"Recently, big earthquakes have been occurring one after another.","furigana":"さいきん|おお|じしん|あいつ"},{"japanese":"久しぶりに会ったが、彼は相変わらず元気だった。","romaji":"Hisashiburi ni atta ga, kare wa aikawarazu genki datta.","german":"I hadn't seen him in a long time, but he was as lively as ever.","furigana":"ひさ|あ|かれ|あいか|げんき"},{"japanese":"当店は本日をもって閉店と相成りました。","romaji":"Touten wa honjitsu o motte heiten to ainarimashita.","german":"Our store has closed as of today.","furigana":"とうてん|ほんじつ|へいてん|あいな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"悪日 denotes, according to the traditional calendar or folk belief, an unlucky day on which one avoids important undertakings such as moving or weddings; the opposite is 吉日 \"lucky day\". Besides あくび, the reading あくにち is also used. The word sounds exactly like あくび \"yawn\" (separate card), which only context distinguishes; today it is encountered mainly in calendar and fortune-telling texts.","examples":[{"japanese":"祖母は悪日に引っ越しをするのを嫌がった。","romaji":"Sobo wa akubi ni hikkoshi o suru no o iyagatta.","german":"My grandmother did not want to move house on an unlucky day.","furigana":"そぼ|あくび|ひ|こ|いや"},{"japanese":"暦を見て、悪日を避けて結婚式の日を決めた。","romaji":"Koyomi o mite, akubi o sakete kekkonshiki no hi o kimeta.","german":"We looked at the calendar and set the wedding date so as to avoid an unlucky day.","furigana":"こよみ|み|あくび|さ|けっこんしき|ひ|き"},{"japanese":"今日は悪日だから、新しいことは始めないほうがいいと言われた。","romaji":"Kyou wa akubi da kara, atarashii koto wa hajimenai hou ga ii to iwareta.","german":"I was told that today is an unlucky day, so it is better not to start anything new.","furigana":"きょう|あくび|あたら|はじ|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"A na-adjective for brief, clear presentation, mostly adverbial as 簡潔に (簡潔に説明する, 簡潔にまとめる). Unlike 簡単 \"simple, easy\", 簡潔 describes only style and expression, not difficulty. Do not confuse it with the homophone 完結 \"conclusion, completion\".","examples":[{"japanese":"要点を簡潔に説明してください。","romaji":"Youten o kanketsu ni setsumei shite kudasai.","german":"Please explain the main points concisely.","furigana":"ようてん|かんけつ|せつめい"},{"japanese":"彼のメールはいつも簡潔で分かりやすい。","romaji":"Kare no meeru wa itsumo kanketsu de wakariyasui.","german":"His emails are always concise and easy to understand.","furigana":"かれ|かんけつ|わ"},{"japanese":"報告書は一枚に簡潔にまとめること。","romaji":"Houkokusho wa ichimai ni kanketsu ni matomeru koto.","german":"The report should be summarized concisely on one page.","furigana":"ほうこくしょ|いちまい|かんけつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A written-language noun for publishing books, magazines or series: 刊行物 \"publication\", 定期刊行物 \"periodical\", 刊行が始まる. The verb form is on the card 刊行する; in everyday speech, one rather says 出版 or 発行. Do not confuse it with the homophones 観光 \"tourism\" and 慣行 \"custom\".","examples":[{"japanese":"この雑誌の刊行は来月から始まります。","romaji":"Kono zasshi no kankou wa raigetsu kara hajimarimasu.","german":"Publication of this magazine will begin next month.","furigana":"ざっし|かんこう|らいげつ|はじ"},{"japanese":"図書館には大学の刊行物が並んでいます。","romaji":"Toshokan ni wa daigaku no kankoubutsu ga narande imasu.","german":"The university's publications are lined up in the library.","furigana":"としょかん|だいがく|かんこうぶつ|なら"},{"japanese":"全集の刊行が予定より一年遅れた。","romaji":"Zenshuu no kankou ga yotei yori ichinen okureta.","german":"Publication of the complete works was delayed by a year from the plan.","furigana":"ぜんしゅう|かんこう|よてい|いちねんおく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"慣行 is a formal word for an established practice in business, administration or society: 商慣行 \"trade custom\", 慣行に従う \"to follow the usual practice\", 慣行を改める. Attributively it takes の (慣行の手続き); it is not used as a する-verb. For personal habits, one says 習慣; 慣例 is a close synonym. Do not confuse it with the homophones 観光 \"tourism\" and 刊行 \"publication\".","examples":[{"japanese":"日本の商慣行は外国の会社にとって分かりにくいことがある。","romaji":"Nihon no shoukankou wa gaikoku no kaisha ni totte wakarinikui koto ga aru.","german":"Japanese business practices can be hard for foreign companies to understand.","furigana":"にほん|しょうかんこう|がいこく|かいしゃ|わ"},{"japanese":"長年の慣行に従って、新年最初の会議では社長があいさつする。","romaji":"Naganen no kankou ni shitagatte, shinnen saisho no kaigi de wa shachou ga aisatsu suru.","german":"In accordance with a long-standing custom, the president gives a speech at the first meeting of the new year.","furigana":"ながねん|かんこう|したが|しんねんさいしょ|かいぎ|しゃちょう"},{"japanese":"政府は古い慣行を見直すことにした。","romaji":"Seifu wa furui kankou o minaosu koto ni shita.","german":"The government decided to review old practices.","furigana":"せいふ|ふる|かんこう|みなお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The reading けがす means moral or ritual defilement: 名を汚す \"to dishonor the name\", 家名を汚す, 神聖な場所を汚す. For physical soiling (服を汚す), the reading is よごす (own card). Since both readings are written the same, the context decides; けがす is elevated and written-language and is also written 穢す.","examples":[{"japanese":"父の名を汚すようなことはしたくない。","romaji":"Chichi no na o kegasu you na koto wa shitakunai.","german":"I don't want to do anything that would dishonor my father's name.","furigana":"ちち|な|けが"},{"japanese":"彼は不正をして学校の名誉を汚した。","romaji":"Kare wa fusei o shite gakkou no meiyo o kegashita.","german":"He tarnished the school's honor by cheating.","furigana":"かれ|ふせい|がっこう|めいよ|けが"},{"japanese":"神聖な場所を汚してはいけません。","romaji":"Shinsei na basho o kegashite wa ikemasen.","german":"You must not desecrate a sacred place.","furigana":"しんせい|ばしょ|けが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"けがれる means becoming morally or ritually impure, for example through lies, crimes, or, according to traditional belief, contact with death; the related noun is 汚れ/穢れ（けがれ）. For physical soiling (服が汚れる), the reading is よごれる (own card). The word is elevated and written-language and is often written 穢れる to make the reading unambiguous. The transitive counterpart is 汚す（けがす）.","examples":[{"japanese":"嘘をつき続けると、心が汚れてしまう気がする。","romaji":"Uso o tsukitsuzukeru to, kokoro ga kegarete shimau ki ga suru.","german":"I feel that if I keep lying, my heart will become impure.","furigana":"うそ|つづ|こころ|けが|き"},{"japanese":"昔の人は、死に触れると身が汚れると考えていた。","romaji":"Mukashi no hito wa, shi ni fureru to mi ga kegareru to kangaete ita.","german":"People in the past believed that contact with death made one ritually impure.","furigana":"むかし|ひと|し|ふ|み|けが|かんが"},{"japanese":"神聖な土地が戦争で穢れてしまった。","romaji":"Shinsei na tochi ga sensou de kegarete shimatta.","german":"The sacred land was desecrated by the war.","furigana":"しんせい|とち|せんそう|けが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"さかる is intransitive and describes something gaining strength, above all fire: 火が盛る and especially the compound 燃え盛る \"to burn fiercely\". The meaning \"to thrive, prosper\" is obsolete; for animals, さかる means \"to be in heat\" (often written サカる). Do not confuse it with the transitive 盛る/もる \"to serve, to pile up\" (own card), as in ご飯を盛る.","examples":[{"japanese":"火が盛ってきたので、薪を足すのをやめた。","romaji":"Hi ga sakatte kita node, maki o tasu no o yameta.","german":"Since the fire was blazing well, I stopped adding firewood.","furigana":"ひ|さか|まき|た"},{"japanese":"燃え盛る炎の中から、消防士が子どもを助け出した。","romaji":"Moesakaru honoo no naka kara, shouboushi ga kodomo o tasukedashita.","german":"A firefighter rescued the child from the raging flames.","furigana":"も|さか|ほのお|なか|しょうぼうし|こ|たす|だ"},{"japanese":"春になると、猫が盛って夜中に鳴く。","romaji":"Haru ni naru to, neko ga sakatte yonaka ni naku.","german":"In spring, cats are in heat and cry at night.","furigana":"はる|ねこ|さか|よなか|な"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The noun for 設置する (own card): the setting up of equipment (エアコンの設置, 設置場所, 設置工事) and the establishment of bodies or offices (委員会の設置). Typical of official, corporate and manual texts; for devices, one colloquially often says 取り付け. Do not confuse it with the homophone 接地 \"grounding\".","examples":[{"japanese":"エアコンの設置は来週の月曜日です。","romaji":"Eakon no secchi wa raishuu no getsuyoubi desu.","german":"The air conditioner will be installed next Monday.","furigana":"せっち|らいしゅう|げつようび"},{"japanese":"駅に防犯カメラの設置が決まりました。","romaji":"Eki ni bouhan kamera no secchi ga kimarimashita.","german":"It has been decided to install security cameras at the station.","furigana":"えき|ぼうはん|せっち|き"},{"japanese":"政府は新しい委員会の設置を発表した。","romaji":"Seifu wa atarashii iinkai no secchi o happyou shita.","german":"The government announced the establishment of a new committee.","furigana":"せいふ|あたら|いいんかい|せっち|はっぴょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Almost only in the phrase verb in the past form + ためしがない \"it has never happened that ...\", usually slightly reproachful and almost always written in kana. The reading れい (例, \"example\") is the normal word for examples (例えば, 例を挙げる). 試し \"attempt, trial\" (試しに, お試し) is a separate word with the same pronunciation.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼が約束の時間に来たためしがない。","romaji":"Kare ga yakusoku no jikan ni kita tameshi ga nai.","german":"He has never once come at the appointed time.","furigana":"かれ|やくそく|じかん|き"},{"japanese":"宝くじなんて、当たったためしがない。","romaji":"Takarakuji nante, atatta tameshi ga nai.","german":"I have never won anything in a lottery.","furigana":"たから|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"ちゅうし is the Sino-Japanese reading of 中指 and occurs almost only in medicine, anatomy and technical texts, for example in doctors' reports or X-ray findings. In everyday life, 中指 is always read なかゆび (own card); in conversation you should use that reading. 中止 \"cancellation\" sounds the same, and only the context tells them apart.","examples":[{"japanese":"診察の結果、右手の中指に骨折が見つかった。","romaji":"Shinsatsu no kekka, migite no chuushi ni kossetsu ga mitsukatta.","german":"The examination found a fracture in the middle finger of the right hand.","furigana":"しんさつ|けっか|みぎて|ちゅうし|こっせつ|み"},{"japanese":"患者は中指の関節の痛みを訴えている。","romaji":"Kanja wa chuushi no kansetsu no itami o uttaete iru.","german":"The patient is complaining of pain in the joint of the middle finger.","furigana":"かんじゃ|ちゅうし|かんせつ|いた|うった"},{"japanese":"レントゲンで左足の中指を確認します。","romaji":"Rentogen de hidariashi no chuushi o kakunin shimasu.","german":"We will check the middle toe of the left foot on the X-ray.","furigana":"ひだりあし|ちゅうし|かくにん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Denotes something that seems strange or suspicious; also as a na-adjective (不審な荷物) and in 不審者 \"suspicious person\", 挙動不審 \"suspicious behavior\", 不審に思う \"to find strange\". Two homophones: 不信 \"distrust\" is directed against a person or institution (不信感), 不振 \"slump, weakness\" concerns performance or business (食欲不振).","examples":[{"japanese":"不審な荷物を見つけたら、駅員に知らせてください。","romaji":"Fushin na nimotsu o mitsuketara, ekiin ni shirasete kudasai.","german":"If you find a suspicious package, please tell the station staff.","furigana":"ふしん|にもつ|み|えきいん|し"},{"japanese":"夜中に家の前を不審者がうろうろしていた。","romaji":"Yonaka ni ie no mae o fushinsha ga urouro shite ita.","german":"In the middle of the night, a suspicious person was loitering in front of the house.","furigana":"よなか|いえ|まえ|ふしんしゃ"},{"japanese":"母は電話の相手の話を不審に思った。","romaji":"Haha wa denwa no aite no hanashi o fushin ni omotta.","german":"My mother found what the caller said suspicious.","furigana":"はは|でんわ|あいて|はなし|ふしん|おも"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"On its own, 了 appears almost only in written texts: as \"end\" at the close of a manuscript, a novel or a serial, and in the elevated phrase 了とする \"to approve, to accept\". In normal usage, the character is met mainly in compounds such as 終了 \"end, completion\" and 了解 \"understood\" (both own cards). For \"end\", people say 終わり in everyday life.","examples":[{"japanese":"長い連載も「了」の一字で終わった。","romaji":"Nagai rensai mo \"ryō\" no ichiji de owatta.","german":"Even the long serial ended with the single character \"end\".","furigana":"なが|れんさい|りょう|いちじ|お"},{"japanese":"原稿の最後のページに「了」と書いておいた。","romaji":"Genkō no saigo no pēji ni \"ryō\" to kaite oita.","german":"I wrote \"end\" on the last page of the manuscript.","furigana":"げんこう|さいご|りょう|か"},{"japanese":"部長は私の説明を了とした。","romaji":"Buchō wa watashi no setsumei o ryō to shita.","german":"The department head accepted my explanation.","furigana":"ぶちょう|わたし|せつめい|りょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A rare secondary reading of 連中（れんちゅう）\"people, guys, gang\". In present-day standard language, one almost always says れんちゅう (あの連中); れんじゅう sounds old-fashioned and survives mainly in the traditional stage arts, where 〜連中 denotes the group of musicians and singers of an ensemble (長唄連中, 清元連中). As active vocabulary, れんちゅう is usually enough.","examples":[{"japanese":"長唄の連中が舞台の後ろにずらりと並んだ。","romaji":"Nagauta no renjuu ga butai no ushiro ni zurari to naranda.","german":"The nagauta ensemble lined up in a row at the back of the stage.","furigana":"ながうた|れんじゅう|ぶたい|うし|なら"},{"japanese":"祖父は昔の仲間の連中と、毎年温泉に出かけていた。","romaji":"Sofu wa mukashi no nakama no renjuu to, maitoshi onsen ni dekakete ita.","german":"My grandfather went to a hot spring every year with his old friends.","furigana":"そふ|むかし|なかま|れんじゅう|まいとしおんせん|で"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"劫 in Buddhism is an unimaginably long span of time, a \"world age\". In the game of Go, it denotes the ko position, in which stones could be captured back and forth again and again; there it is usually written コウ. In compounds it is read ごう (永劫 \"eternity\", 未来永劫 \"for all eternity\"), and 億劫 おっくう \"troublesome\" is a word of its own.","examples":[{"japanese":"仏教では、劫はとても長い時間を表す言葉だ。","romaji":"Bukkyō de wa, kō wa totemo nagai jikan o arawasu kotoba da.","german":"In Buddhism, kalpa is a word for an immeasurably long time.","furigana":"ぶっきょう|こう|なが|じかん|あらわ|ことば"},{"japanese":"囲碁で劫になると、相手はすぐには石を取り返せない。","romaji":"Igo de kō ni naru to, aite wa sugu ni wa ishi o torikaesenai.","german":"When a ko arises in Go, the opponent cannot immediately recapture the stone.","furigana":"いご|こう|あいて|いし|と|かえ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"A colloquial intensifying prefix that doubles the following consonant; only in a few set words such as 素っ裸 \"stark naked\", 素っ頓狂 \"shrill, utterly crazy\" and 素っ飛ぶ \"to fly off, to dash off\". Not productive. 素っ気ない, by contrast, is read そっけない, and 素 (す) in 素足 or 素顔 means \"bare, uncovered\".","examples":[{"japanese":"子どもたちは素っ裸で川で遊んでいた。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa suppadaka de kawa de asonde ita.","german":"The children were playing in the river stark naked.","furigana":"こ|す|ぱだか|かわ|あそ"},{"japanese":"急に名前を呼ばれて、素っ頓狂な声を出してしまった。","romaji":"Kyuu ni namae o yobarete, suttonkyou na koe o dashite shimatta.","german":"When my name was suddenly called, I let out an utterly shrill voice.","furigana":"きゅう|なまえ|よ|す|とんきょう|こえ|だ"},{"japanese":"知らせを聞いて、母は病院へ素っ飛んで行った。","romaji":"Shirase o kiite, haha wa byouin e suttonde itta.","german":"When my mother heard the news, she dashed off to the hospital.","furigana":"し|き|はは|びょういん|す|と|い"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"As an independent word it is written-language and appears almost only in set phrases: 感謝の意を表す \"to express thanks\", 意を決する \"to make up one's mind\", 意に介さない \"to pay no heed\", 意のままに \"as one pleases\". Otherwise the character appears in everyday life in compounds like 意味, 意見, 注意. Do not confuse it with the homophones 胃 \"stomach\" and 〜位 \"rank\".","examples":[{"japanese":"皆様のご協力に、心から感謝の意を表します。","romaji":"Minasama no go-kyouryoku ni, kokoro kara kansha no i o arawashimasu.","german":"I sincerely express my gratitude for everyone's cooperation.","furigana":"みなさま|きょうりょく|こころ|かんしゃ|い|あらわ"},{"japanese":"彼女は意を決して、社長に本当のことを話した。","romaji":"Kanojo wa i o kesshite, shachou ni hontou no koto o hanashita.","german":"She made up her mind and told the president the truth.","furigana":"かのじょ|い|けっ|しゃちょう|ほんとう|はな"},{"japanese":"彼は周りの反対をまったく意に介さなかった。","romaji":"Kare wa mawari no hantai o mattaku i ni kaisanakatta.","german":"He paid no attention at all to the opposition around him.","furigana":"かれ|まわ|はんたい|い|かい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"As an independent word, 異 lives almost only in set phrases: 異を唱える \"to object, to raise objections\" and in the proverb 縁は異なもの \"fate is strange\" (異な = \"strange\"). As a prefix it means \"different, foreign\" (異文化, 異国). For \"to be different\", one uses 違う or 異なる in everyday life (own cards); 胃 \"stomach\" is only a homophone.","examples":[{"japanese":"会議で彼の意見に異を唱える人はいなかった。","romaji":"Kaigi de kare no iken ni i o tonaeru hito wa inakatta.","german":"No one in the meeting objected to his opinion.","furigana":"かいぎ|かれ|いけん|い|とな|ひと"},{"japanese":"縁は異なものと言うが、二人は旅先で出会った。","romaji":"En wa i na mono to iu ga, futari wa tabisaki de deatta.","german":"They say fate works in strange ways, and the two met on a trip.","furigana":"えん|い|い|ふたり|たびさき|であ"},{"japanese":"留学して異文化に触れるのは大切な経験だ。","romaji":"Ryūgaku shite ibunka ni fureru no wa taisetsu na keiken da.","german":"Encountering a foreign culture while studying abroad is an important experience.","furigana":"りゅうがく|いぶんか|ふ|たいせつ|けいけん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"目下 read もっか is a formal word for \"currently\", typical of news and business language: 目下調査中, 目下のところ \"for the time being\". In conversation, one says 今 or 現在. The same kanji read めした mean \"junior, subordinate\" (own card); as a time expression before a statement it is もっか, as a person めした.","examples":[{"japanese":"事故の原因は目下調査中です。","romaji":"Jiko no gen'in wa mokka chōsachū desu.","german":"The cause of the accident is currently under investigation.","furigana":"じこ|げんいん|もっかちょうさちゅう"},{"japanese":"目下のところ、新しい情報はありません。","romaji":"Mokka no tokoro, atarashii jōhō wa arimasen.","german":"For the time being, there is no new information.","furigana":"もっか|あたら|じょうほう"},{"japanese":"彼は目下、新しい小説を書いている。","romaji":"Kare wa mokka, atarashii shōsetsu o kaite iru.","german":"He is currently writing a new novel.","furigana":"かれ|もっか|あたら|しょうせつ|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Means \"sign of human presence\" and is almost always used in the negative: 人気がない / 人気のない場所 \"deserted\". Written exactly like 人気（にんき）\"popularity\", so the context decides: 人気のない店 can mean \"unpopular shop\" (にんき) or \"deserted shop\" (ひとけ). To avoid misunderstandings, it is often written 人け or ひと気.","examples":[{"japanese":"夜の公園は人気がなくて、少し怖かった。","romaji":"Yoru no kouen wa hitoke ga nakute, sukoshi kowakatta.","german":"The park at night had no one around, and it was a little scary.","furigana":"よる|こうえん|ひとけ|すこ|こわ"},{"japanese":"彼女は人気のない道を一人で歩いて帰った。","romaji":"Kanojo wa hitoke no nai michi o hitori de aruite kaetta.","german":"She walked home alone along a deserted road.","furigana":"かのじょ|ひとけ|みち|ひとり|ある|かえ"},{"japanese":"朝早い駅のホームには、まだ人けがほとんどなかった。","romaji":"Asa hayai eki no hoomu ni wa, mada hitoke ga hotondo nakatta.","german":"On the station platform early in the morning, there were hardly any people yet.","furigana":"あさはや|えき|ひと"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The same writing as 大家 (おおや) \"landlord\", which is far more common in everyday life; たいか denotes a recognized master of his field, mostly in the pattern X の大家 (書道の大家, 経済学の大家). Which reading is meant becomes clear from the context: apartment and rent → おおや, art and science → たいか. The homophones 耐火 \"fireproof\" and 退化 \"degeneration\" are different words.","examples":[{"japanese":"その先生は日本画の大家として知られている。","romaji":"Sono sensei wa nihonga no taika to shite shirarete iru.","german":"That teacher is known as a great authority on Japanese painting.","furigana":"せんせい|にほんが|たいか|し"},{"japanese":"彼は若い頃、料理の大家に弟子入りした。","romaji":"Kare wa wakai koro, ryouri no taika ni deshiiri shita.","german":"In his youth, he became an apprentice to a great master chef.","furigana":"かれ|わか|ころ|りょうり|たいか|でしい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A rare, independent reading of 相対: \"only between the parties involved, without third parties\". Today it survives almost only in compounds such as 相対取引 \"direct (off-exchange) trading between two parties\" or 相対売買 and in 相対で決める \"to settle between themselves\". The usual reading そうたい (own card) means \"relative\" (相対的, opposite 絶対); which reading is meant becomes clear from the context, and in trade and negotiation it is mostly あいたい.","examples":[{"japanese":"その株は取引所を通さず、相対取引で売られた。","romaji":"Sono kabu wa torihikijo o tōsazu, aitai torihiki de urareta.","german":"The shares were not sold through the exchange but in a direct transaction between the parties.","furigana":"かぶ|とりひきじょ|とお|あいたいとりひき|う"},{"japanese":"価格は市場ではなく、相対で決められた。","romaji":"Kakaku wa shijō de wa naku, aitai de kimerareta.","german":"The price was set not by the market but directly between the parties involved.","furigana":"かかく|しじょう|あいたい|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"域外 is a formal word from politics, economics and news and means \"outside a (economic) area\": EU域外 \"outside the EU\", 域外からの輸入 \"imports from outside\". As an attribute, it takes の (域外の国). The opposite is 域内 \"inside the area\". Do not confuse it with the similar-sounding 生き甲斐/いきがい \"purpose in life\" (own card).","examples":[{"japanese":"EU域外からの輸入品には関税がかかります。","romaji":"EU ikigai kara no yunyūhin ni wa kanzei ga kakarimasu.","german":"Tariffs are charged on imports from outside the EU.","furigana":"いきがい|ゆにゅうひん|かんぜい"},{"japanese":"この保険は域外での事故には使えません。","romaji":"Kono hoken wa ikigai de no jiko ni wa tsukaemasen.","german":"This insurance cannot be used for accidents outside the area.","furigana":"ほけん|いきがい|じこ|つか"},{"japanese":"域外の国とも協力していく必要がある。","romaji":"Ikigai no kuni to mo kyōryoku shite iku hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"We need to cooperate with countries outside the region as well.","furigana":"いきがい|くに|きょうりょく|ひつよう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"一見/いちげん denotes the first visit to an inn or establishment without an introduction by a regular customer, and also such an unknown guest. It is best known from 一見さんお断り \"no customers without an introduction\", typical of traditional teahouses and restaurants in Kyoto. The card 一見/いっけん with the same writing, by contrast, means \"at first glance\" and is much more common; いちげん is used almost only in connection with guests and establishments.","examples":[{"japanese":"この店は一見さんお断りです。","romaji":"Kono mise wa ichigen-san okotowari desu.","german":"This place does not accept first-time customers without an introduction.","furigana":"みせ|いちげん|ことわ"},{"japanese":"京都には一見の客を入れないお茶屋がまだある。","romaji":"Kyōto ni wa ichigen no kyaku o irenai ochaya ga mada aru.","german":"In Kyoto, there are still teahouses that don't let in unknown first-time customers.","furigana":"きょうと|いちげん|きゃく|い|ちゃや"},{"japanese":"一見の客でも気軽に入れるバーを探しています。","romaji":"Ichigen no kyaku demo kigaru ni haireru bā o sagashite imasu.","german":"I'm looking for a bar that even a first-time customer can easily walk into.","furigana":"いちげん|きゃく|きがる|はい|さが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"うずまる is a secondary reading of 埋まる (own card 埋まる/うまる) with the same meanings: \"to be buried, covered over, completely covered\" and \"to be packed\". It sounds somewhat more literary and is used especially for sinking into or being embedded in something: 雪にうずまる, がれきにうずまる. In everyday speech, one mostly says うまる (席がうまる); because 埋まる is read that way, うずまる is often written in kana. The transitive counterpart is うずめる \"to bury, to fill\".","examples":[{"japanese":"車が雪にうずまって動けなくなった。","romaji":"Kuruma ga yuki ni uzumatte ugokenaku natta.","german":"The car was buried in snow and couldn't move.","furigana":"くるま|ゆき|うご"},{"japanese":"地震で多くの家ががれきにうずまった。","romaji":"Jishin de ōku no ie ga gareki ni uzumatta.","german":"Many houses were buried under rubble by the earthquake.","furigana":"じしん|おお|いえ"},{"japanese":"広場は祭りの見物客でうずまっていた。","romaji":"Hiroba wa matsuri no kenbutsukyaku de uzumatte ita.","german":"The square was filled with festival spectators.","furigana":"ひろば|まつ|けんぶつきゃく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"甕 is a large, bulging vessel made of clay for storing water, sake, miso or pickles: 水がめ \"water jar\", 甕に漬ける \"to pickle in a clay pot\". It is also written 瓶, but 瓶 is usually read びん \"bottle\" (own card); the context decides. The similar-sounding 亀/かめ \"turtle\" is an entirely different word. Because the kanji 甕 is difficult, かめ is often seen in kana.","examples":[{"japanese":"昔は台所の甕に水をためていた。","romaji":"Mukashi wa daidokoro no kame ni mizu o tamete ita.","german":"In the old days, water was stored in a jar in the kitchen.","furigana":"むかし|だいどころ|かめ|みず"},{"japanese":"祖母は甕で梅干しを漬けている。","romaji":"Sobo wa kame de umeboshi o tsukete iru.","german":"My grandmother pickles umeboshi in a clay pot.","furigana":"そぼ|かめ|うめぼ|つ"},{"japanese":"この酒蔵では大きな甕で焼酎を寝かせます。","romaji":"Kono sakagura de wa ōki na kame de shōchū o nekasemasu.","german":"This brewery ages shochu in large clay jars.","furigana":"さかぐら|おお|かめ|しょうちゅう|ね"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"桐 is the paulownia (empress tree), a fast-growing tree with large leaves and violet flowers. Its light, moisture-resistant wood is used for 桐のたんす \"clothing chest of paulownia wood\", 桐の箱 and the instrument koto; the paulownia crest is the emblem of the Japanese government. Do not confuse it with the similar-sounding 霧 \"fog\". In biology, it is written キリ.","examples":[{"japanese":"祖母は着物を桐のたんすにしまっています。","romaji":"Sobo wa kimono o kiri no tansu ni shimatte imasu.","german":"My grandmother keeps her kimonos in a paulownia chest.","furigana":"そぼ|きもの|きり"},{"japanese":"五月になると、桐の花が紫色に咲く。","romaji":"Gogatsu ni naru to, kiri no hana ga murasakiiro ni saku.","german":"When May comes, the paulownia flowers bloom purple.","furigana":"ごがつ|きり|はな|むらさきいろ|さ"},{"japanese":"高い茶碗は桐の箱に入って売られている。","romaji":"Takai chawan wa kiri no hako ni haitte urarete iru.","german":"Expensive tea bowls are sold in paulownia-wood boxes.","furigana":"たか|ちゃわん|きり|はこ|はい|う"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"したためる is an elevated, written-language verb for \"to compose carefully (a letter, a document)\": 手紙をしたためる, 一筆したためる \"to write a few lines\". It is mostly written in kana because the kanji writing 認める is normally read みとめる \"to recognize, to admit\" (own card). In everyday speech, one simply says 書く; したためる sounds solemn or old-fashioned. The meaning \"to take (a meal)\" is obsolete.","examples":[{"japanese":"祖父は毎年、恩師に手紙をしたためていた。","romaji":"Sofu wa maitoshi, onshi ni tegami o shitatamete ita.","german":"My grandfather wrote a letter to his former teacher every year.","furigana":"そふ|まいとし|おんし|てがみ"},{"japanese":"お礼の気持ちを一筆したためました。","romaji":"Orei no kimochi o ippitsu shitatamemashita.","german":"I wrote a few lines to express my gratitude.","furigana":"れい|きも|いっぴつ"},{"japanese":"彼は旅の思い出を日記にしたためた。","romaji":"Kare wa tabi no omoide o nikki ni shitatameta.","german":"He recorded his memories of the trip in his diary.","furigana":"かれ|たび|おも|で|にっき"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"しょう is a colloquial reading of 背負う (own card 背負う/せおう) and likewise means \"to carry on the back\" and figuratively \"to take something upon oneself\". You hear it mainly in the て-form: リュックをしょって. しょってる (from しょっている) also means \"to be conceited, to be very sure of oneself\"; this meaning exists only with しょう, not with せおう. Since 背負う is normally read せおう, しょう is mostly written in kana.","examples":[{"japanese":"子どもたちはランドセルをしょって学校へ行く。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa randoseru o shotte gakkō e iku.","german":"The children go to school with their school backpacks on their backs.","furigana":"こ|がっこう|い"},{"japanese":"重い荷物をしょって山を登った。","romaji":"Omoi nimotsu o shotte yama o nobotta.","german":"I climbed the mountain carrying a heavy load on my back.","furigana":"おも|にもつ|やま|のぼ"},{"japanese":"自分のことを天才だなんて、ずいぶんしょってるね。","romaji":"Jibun no koto o tensai da nante, zuibun shotteru ne.","german":"Calling yourself a genius - you're pretty full of yourself.","furigana":"じぶん|てんさい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The reading しろ now occurs almost only in set compounds: 身代金 \"ransom\", 糊代 \"glue margin\", 苗代 \"rice seedbed\", 代物 \"thing, goods\" (often derogatory). On its own, 代 is mostly read だい: the card 〜代 \"-cost, -fee\" (電気代, 食事代) is a different word. 城 \"castle\" and 白 \"white\" sound the same but have nothing to do with it.","examples":[{"japanese":"犯人は一億円の身代金を要求した。","romaji":"Hannin wa ichioku-en no minoshirokin o yōkyū shita.","german":"The culprit demanded a ransom of one hundred million yen.","furigana":"はんにん|いちおくえん||ようきゅう"},{"japanese":"紙を切るときは、端に糊代を残してください。","romaji":"Kami o kiru toki wa, hashi ni norishiro o nokoshite kudasai.","german":"When cutting the paper, please leave a glue margin at the edge.","furigana":"かみ|き|はし|のりしろ|のこ"},{"japanese":"春になると、農家は苗代で稲の苗を育てる。","romaji":"Haru ni naru to, nōka wa nawashiro de ine no nae o sodateru.","german":"In spring, farmers raise rice seedlings in seedbeds.","furigana":"はる|のうか||いね|なえ|そだ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"長大 is a literary na-adjective that mainly stresses great length: 長大な橋, 長大な小説, 長大な報告書. 巨大 (own card), on the other hand, means enormous size or mass in general. The identically read 頂戴 \"to receive; give me\" is a completely different word; in conversation one usually says とても長い or 大きい instead of 長大.","examples":[{"japanese":"明石海峡大橋は、世界でも有数の長大な吊り橋だ。","romaji":"Akashi Kaikyō Ōhashi wa, sekai de mo yūsū no chōdai na tsuribashi da.","german":"The Akashi Kaikyo Bridge is one of the longest and grandest suspension bridges in the world.","furigana":"|せかい|ゆうすう|ちょうだい|つ|ばし"},{"japanese":"その小説は全十巻にもなる長大な作品だ。","romaji":"Sono shōsetsu wa zen-jikkan ni mo naru chōdai na sakuhin da.","german":"That novel is a monumental work of ten volumes in all.","furigana":"しょうせつ|ぜんじっかん|ちょうだい|さくひん"},{"japanese":"こんなに長大な報告書を、一晩で読むのは無理だ。","romaji":"Konna ni chōdai na hōkokusho o, hitoban de yomu no wa muri da.","german":"It is impossible to read such a long, voluminous report in one night.","furigana":"ちょうだい|ほうこくしょ|ひとばん|よ|むり"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"どさん is the Sino-Japanese reading of 土産 and means \"product of a country or region\"; it appears almost only in historical or very formal texts. In ordinary text, 土産 is always read みやげ \"souvenir, gift brought back\" (separate card), so you can recognize どさん only from context or reading aids. Today, for regional products people usually say 特産品 or 名産.","examples":[{"japanese":"江戸時代、絹はこの地方の重要な土産であった。","romaji":"Edo jidai, kinu wa kono chihō no jūyō na dosan de atta.","german":"In the Edo period, silk was an important product of this region.","furigana":"えどじだい|きぬ|ちほう|じゅうよう|どさん"},{"japanese":"古い記録には、各国の土産が詳しく書かれている。","romaji":"Furui kiroku ni wa, kakkoku no dosan ga kuwashiku kakarete iru.","german":"Old records describe in detail the products of each province.","furigana":"ふる|きろく|かっこく|どさん|くわ|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"やちゅう is a rare, written-language reading of 夜中 and means \"during the night\" in general, often in contrast to 日中 \"during the day\". The usual reading is よなか \"the middle of the night\" (separate card), and without a reading aid the kanji is almost always read that way. In formal style, 夜間 (separate card) is usually used instead of やちゅう.","examples":[{"japanese":"砂漠では、日中は暑いが夜中は氷点下まで冷え込む。","romaji":"Sabaku de wa, nitchū wa atsui ga yachū wa hyōtenka made hiekomu.","german":"In the desert it is hot during the day, but at night it gets cold enough to fall below freezing.","furigana":"さばく|にっちゅう|あつ|やちゅう|ひょうてんか|ひ|こ"},{"japanese":"警察は住民に、夜中の外出を控えるよう呼びかけた。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa jūmin ni, yachū no gaishutsu o hikaeru yō yobikaketa.","german":"The police called on residents to refrain from going out at night.","furigana":"けいさつ|じゅうみん|やちゅう|がいしゅつ|ひか|よ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"An elevated, literary reading of 面 for \"face, countenance\" (面を上げる \"to raise one's gaze\", 面を伏せる \"to lower one's face\"), for a water surface (湖の面) and for the Noh mask. The card 面/めん teaches the usual on-reading (\"side, aspect; surface\", e.g. この面では); 表/おもて \"front side, outside\" is a different word. In everyday language one says 顔 for \"face\".","examples":[{"japanese":"「面を上げよ」と殿様が家来に命じた。","romaji":"\"Omote o ageyo\" to tonosama ga kerai ni meijita.","german":"\"Raise your face,\" the lord commanded his retainer.","furigana":"おもて|あ|とのさま|けらい|めい"},{"japanese":"静かな湖の面に月が映っていた。","romaji":"Shizuka na mizuumi no omote ni tsuki ga utsutte ita.","german":"The moon was reflected on the surface of the quiet lake.","furigana":"しず|みずうみ|おもて|つき|うつ"},{"japanese":"彼女は恥ずかしさに面を伏せた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa hazukashisa ni omote o fuseta.","german":"She lowered her face in embarrassment.","furigana":"かのじょ|は|おもて|ふ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4 (source: N4; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"A hardened patch of skin caused by constant friction, almost always written in kana (たこ or タコ); the kanji 胼胝 are very rare. Typical: 指にたこができる, ペンだこ \"writing callus\". The idiom 耳にたこができる means \"to have heard something to the point of boredom\". Same sound, but different words: 蛸/たこ \"octopus\" and 凧/たこ \"kite\".","examples":[{"japanese":"毎日ギターを弾いていたら、指先にたこができた。","romaji":"Mainichi gitaa o hiite itara, yubisaki ni tako ga dekita.","german":"Because I played guitar every day, I got calluses on my fingertips.","furigana":"まいにち|ひ|ゆびさき"},{"japanese":"その話は耳にたこができるほど聞いたよ。","romaji":"Sono hanashi wa mimi ni tako ga dekiru hodo kiita yo.","german":"I've heard that story so many times that I'm sick of it.","furigana":"はなし|みみ|き"},{"japanese":"靴が合わなくて、足の裏にたこができてしまった。","romaji":"Kutsu ga awanakute, ashi no ura ni tako ga dekite shimatta.","german":"My shoes didn't fit, and I got a callus on the sole of my foot.","furigana":"くつ|あ|あし|うら"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Written-language term for (dry) field land as opposed to rice paddies (水田), common in news, statistics and administrative texts (畑地を宅地に転用する). In everyday speech people simply say 畑/はたけ; 田畑/たはた covers both rice paddies and dry fields. It sounds the same as 二十歳/はたち \"twenty years old\", which is distinguished only by context.","examples":[{"japanese":"この地域には広い畑地が広がっている。","romaji":"Kono chiiki ni wa hiroi hatachi ga hirogatte iru.","german":"Wide farmland stretches across this area.","furigana":"ちいき|ひろ|はたち|ひろ"},{"japanese":"市は畑地の一部を住宅地に転用する計画だ。","romaji":"Shi wa hatachi no ichibu o juutakuchi ni ten'you suru keikaku da.","german":"The city plans to convert part of the farmland into residential land.","furigana":"し|はたち|いちぶ|じゅうたくち|てんよう|けいかく"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Counter for small boats such as rowboats, fishing boats or rubber boats: 一艘 (いっそう), 二艘, 三艘, 数艘. For large ships 隻/せき is preferred (タンカー三隻), and in casual speech simply 〜つ or 台 is also used. The kanji is rare; the expression appears mainly in narratives, news and phrases such as 二艘の舟に乗る.","examples":[{"japanese":"港には小さな漁船が三艘つないであった。","romaji":"Minato ni wa chiisa na gyosen ga sansou tsunaide atta.","german":"Three small fishing boats were moored in the harbor.","furigana":"みなと|ちい|ぎょせん|さんそう"},{"japanese":"一艘のボートが湖の真ん中に浮かんでいる。","romaji":"Issou no booto ga mizuumi no mannaka ni ukande iru.","german":"A boat is floating in the middle of the lake.","furigana":"いっそう|みずうみ|ま|なか|う"},{"japanese":"救助のため、ゴムボート数艘が出された。","romaji":"Kyuujo no tame, gomu booto suusou ga dasareta.","german":"Several rubber boats were launched for the rescue.","furigana":"きゅうじょ|すうそう|だ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Technical term of biological classification for the genus, which stands between 科 \"family\" and 種 \"species\"; usually a suffix after the genus name (ヒョウ属, バラ属). The verb 属する \"to belong to\" uses the same kanji and is often used together with it (ヒョウ属に属する). Rare outside biology and technical texts.","examples":[{"japanese":"ライオンはネコ科ヒョウ属に属する動物だ。","romaji":"Raion wa nekoka hyouzoku ni zokusuru doubutsu da.","german":"The lion is an animal of the genus Panthera in the cat family.","furigana":"か|ぞく|ぞく|どうぶつ"},{"japanese":"分類では、科の下に属、属の下に種がある。","romaji":"Bunrui de wa, ka no shita ni zoku, zoku no shita ni shu ga aru.","german":"In classification, below the family comes the genus, and below the genus the species.","furigana":"ぶんるい|か|した|ぞく|ぞく|した|しゅ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Collective term for a motley mix of trees of little economic value that used to serve as firewood; rarely also read ざつぼく. Far more common is the compound 雑木林/ぞうきばやし \"mixed woodland, coppice\". Do not confuse it with the homophone 臓器 \"organ\".","examples":[{"japanese":"家の裏には雑木が生い茂っている。","romaji":"Ie no ura ni wa zōki ga oishigette iru.","german":"Dense brushwood of mixed trees grows thick behind the house.","furigana":"いえ|うら|ぞうき||しげ"},{"japanese":"秋になると、山の雑木が赤や黄色に色づく。","romaji":"Aki ni naru to, yama no zōki ga aka ya kiiro ni irozuku.","german":"In autumn, the mixed trees on the mountain turn red and yellow.","furigana":"あき|やま|ぞうき|あか|きいろ|いろ"},{"japanese":"昔はこの辺りの雑木を切って薪にしていた。","romaji":"Mukashi wa kono atari no zōki o kitte maki ni shite ita.","german":"In the past, people cut the trees around here and made firewood from them.","furigana":"むかし|あた|ぞうき|き|まき"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"As an independent noun it is rare: in fortune-telling \"signs in the face or hand\" (良い相をしている, 死相), in physics \"phase\" (固相, 液相). It is more familiar in compounds such as 人相, 手相, 様相. The card 相〜/あい is the prefix in 相手, 相変わらず; in 首相 it is read しょう.","examples":[{"japanese":"占い師は私の顔を見て「珍しい相をしている」と言った。","romaji":"Uranaishi wa watashi no kao o mite \"mezurashii sō o shite iru\" to itta.","german":"The fortune-teller looked at my face and said, \"You have unusual features.\"","furigana":"うらな|し|わたし|かお|み|めずら|そう|い"},{"japanese":"水は温度によって固体、液体、気体の三つの相に変化する。","romaji":"Mizu wa ondo ni yotte kotai, ekitai, kitai no mittsu no sō ni henka suru.","german":"Water changes into three phases, solid, liquid and gas, depending on temperature.","furigana":"みず|おんど|こたい|えきたい|きたい|みっ|そう|へんか"},{"japanese":"祭りで手相を見てもらったら、長生きする相だと言われた。","romaji":"Matsuri de tesō o mite morattara, nagaiki suru sō da to iwareta.","german":"When I had my palm read at the festival, I was told that I had the signs of a long life.","furigana":"まつ|てそう|み|ながい|そう|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"斜 \"slanting\" is rare as an independent word and appears almost only in fixed phrases: 斜に切る \"to cut diagonally\" (cooking), 斜に構える \"to adopt a cynical, detached attitude\" (also read しゃに構える) and in the compound 斜向かい \"diagonally opposite\". In everyday speech one says 斜め/ななめ (separate card), e.g. 斜めに切る. Not to be confused with the homophone 蓮 \"lotus\".","examples":[{"japanese":"彼はいつも斜に構えて、何事にも本気にならない。","romaji":"Kare wa itsumo hasu ni kamaete, nanigoto ni mo honki ni naranai.","german":"He always takes a cynical stance and never takes anything seriously.","furigana":"かれ|はす|かま|なにごと|ほんき"},{"japanese":"長ねぎを斜に切って、鍋に入れてください。","romaji":"Naganegi o hasu ni kitte, nabe ni irete kudasai.","german":"Please cut the long green onion diagonally and put it in the pot.","furigana":"なが|はす|き|なべ|い"},{"japanese":"私の家は郵便局の斜向かいにあります。","romaji":"Watashi no ie wa yūbinkyoku no hasumukai ni arimasu.","german":"My house is diagonally across from the post office.","furigana":"わたし|いえ|ゆうびんきょく|はすむ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"碗 is a bowl made of ceramic or porcelain, whereas 椀 (own card) refers to a wooden or lacquer bowl, typically for soup; in everyday writing one often simply writes お椀 or お碗. On its own 碗 is rare; one meets it mainly in 茶碗 \"rice bowl, tea bowl\"; in the tea ceremony it also counts bowls (一碗のお茶). Not to be confused with the homophone 湾 \"bay\".","examples":[{"japanese":"茶碗にご飯をよそってください。","romaji":"Chawan ni gohan o yosotte kudasai.","german":"Please serve the rice into the rice bowls.","furigana":"ちゃわん|はん"},{"japanese":"この碗は有田焼で、とても薄く作られている。","romaji":"Kono wan wa Aritayaki de, totemo usuku tsukurarete iru.","german":"This bowl is Arita ware and is made very thin.","furigana":"わん||うす|つく"},{"japanese":"茶室で一碗の抹茶をいただいた。","romaji":"Chashitsu de ichiwan no matcha o itadaita.","german":"In the tea room, I was served a bowl of matcha.","furigana":"ちゃしつ|いちわん|まっちゃ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"あかがね \"red metal\" is the old native Japanese reading of 銅 and belongs to the series こがね (gold), しろがね (silver) and くろがね (iron). Today it appears almost only in literature, poems and color words such as あかがね色 \"copper-colored\"; in everyday use, technology and medals, 銅 is always read どう (separate card), e.g. 銅メダル. Because the reading cannot be seen from the kanji, furigana or hiragana is often added.","examples":[{"japanese":"夕日を受けて、寺の屋根が銅色（あかがねいろ）に輝いていた。","romaji":"Yūhi o ukete, tera no yane ga akaganeiro ni kagayaite ita.","german":"In the evening sun, the temple roof shone copper-colored (あかがねいろ)."},{"japanese":"山寺の銅（あかがね）の鐘が、静かな谷に響いた。","romaji":"Yamadera no akagane no kane ga, shizuka na tani ni hibiita.","german":"The copper (あかがね) bell of the mountain temple echoed through the quiet valley."},{"japanese":"昔は金・銀・銅を「こがね」「しろがね」「あかがね」と呼んだ。","romaji":"Mukashi wa kin, gin, dō o \"kogane\", \"shirogane\", \"akagane\" to yonda.","german":"In the past, gold, silver and copper were called \"kogane\", \"shirogane\" and \"akagane\".","furigana":"むかし|きん|ぎん|どう|よ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"乙女 \"young girl, maiden\" sounds poetic or romantically old-fashioned and emphasizes innocence and girlishness; today it appears mainly in 乙女心 \"a girl's heart\", 乙女座 \"Virgo (zodiac sign)\" or jokingly in 乙女チック. The spelling 少女 read おとめ is rare (poems, song lyrics); normally 少女 is read しょうじょ (separate card), the neutral word for \"girl\".","examples":[{"japanese":"恋する乙女の気持ちは複雑だ。","romaji":"Koi suru otome no kimochi wa fukuzatsu da.","german":"The feelings of a girl in love are complicated.","furigana":"こい|おとめ|きも|ふくざつ"},{"japanese":"彼女は乙女座で、誕生日は九月だ。","romaji":"Kanojo wa otomeza de, tanjōbi wa kugatsu da.","german":"She is a Virgo, and her birthday is in September.","furigana":"かのじょ|おとめざ|たんじょうび|くがつ"},{"japanese":"古い和歌には、花を摘む乙女の姿がよく詠まれている。","romaji":"Furui waka ni wa, hana o tsumu otome no sugata ga yoku yomarete iru.","german":"Old waka poems often depict a maiden picking flowers.","furigana":"ふる|わか|はな|つ|おとめ|すがた|よ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Godan verb, almost always written in kana: 弾が腕をかする \"the bullet grazes the arm\"; in the negative often かすりもしない \"not even come close\". Do not confuse it with 擦る/する \"to rub\" (vocab-n2:2006), which has the same kanji, nor with 化する/かする \"to turn into\" (a different word, a suru verb). The meaning \"to skim off, to pocket a share\" is colloquial and derogatory.","examples":[{"japanese":"弾が腕をかすっただけで、大きなけがはなかった。","romaji":"Tama ga ude o kasutta dake de, ookina kega wa nakatta.","german":"The bullet only grazed his arm, so there was no serious injury.","furigana":"たま|うで|おお"},{"japanese":"シュートはゴールポストをかすって外れた。","romaji":"Shuuto wa gooru posuto o kasutte hazureta.","german":"The shot grazed the goalpost and went wide.","furigana":"はず"},{"japanese":"仲介業者が売り上げの一部をかすっていたことが分かった。","romaji":"Chuukai gyousha ga uriage no ichibu o kasutte ita koto ga wakatta.","german":"It turned out that the middleman had been skimming off part of the sales.","furigana":"ちゅうかいぎょうしゃ|う|あ|いちぶ|わ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Native Japanese reading of 敵 for a personal enemy or rival, mainly in fixed phrases: 目の敵にする \"to have it in for someone\", 敵を討つ \"to take revenge\", 商売敵 \"business rival\", 恋敵 \"love rival\" (in compounds voiced: がたき). For revenge, 仇 is often written (仇討ち). The general enemy in war or opponent in sports is 敵/てき (vocab-n2:116).","examples":[{"japanese":"部長はなぜか私を目の敵にしている。","romaji":"Buchou wa naze ka watashi o me no kataki ni shite iru.","german":"For some reason the department head has it in for me.","furigana":"ぶちょう|わたし|め|かたき"},{"japanese":"父の敵を討つために、彼は剣の修行を続けた。","romaji":"Chichi no kataki o utsu tame ni, kare wa ken no shugyou o tsuzuketa.","german":"To avenge his father, he continued his sword training.","furigana":"ちち|かたき|う|かれ|けん|しゅぎょう|つづ"},{"japanese":"隣の店とは長年の商売敵だ。","romaji":"Tonari no mise to wa naganen no shoubaigataki da.","german":"We have been business rivals with the shop next door for years.","furigana":"となり|みせ|ながねん|しょうばいがたき"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Native Japanese reading of 国境, in which 国 usually means the old province: the border between 信濃 and 越後 or between two feudal domains. It sounds literary and historical; famous is the opening of 雪国 \"国境の長いトンネルを抜けると…\", which is traditionally read this way. For modern national borders, 国境/こっきょう (vocab-n2:25) is always used, e.g. 国境を越える \"to cross the national border\".","examples":[{"japanese":"国境の長いトンネルを抜けると雪国であった。","romaji":"Kunizakai no nagai tonneru o nukeru to yukiguni de atta.","german":"When the train came out of the long tunnel at the border, there lay the snow country.","furigana":"くにざかい|なが|ぬ|ゆきぐに"},{"japanese":"昔はこの峠が信濃と越後の国境だった。","romaji":"Mukashi wa kono touge ga Shinano to Echigo no kunizakai datta.","german":"In the past, this pass was the border between Shinano and Echigo.","furigana":"むかし|とうげ|||くにざかい"},{"japanese":"侍は国境の山を越えて隣の藩へ逃げた。","romaji":"Samurai wa kunizakai no yama o koete tonari no han e nigeta.","german":"The samurai fled over the border mountains to the neighboring domain.","furigana":"さむらい|くにざかい|やま|こ|となり|はん|に"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A rarer, elevated alternative reading of 万人 with the same meaning \"all people\". The standard reading is ばんにん (separate card), which you hear practically always in fixed expressions such as 万人向け and 万人受け; ばんじん appears mainly in solemn or older written language (万人の願い). Do not confuse it with 一万人 (いちまんにん) \"ten thousand people\".","examples":[{"japanese":"彼の作品は時代を超えて万人に愛されている。","romaji":"Kare no sakuhin wa jidai o koete banjin ni aisarete iru.","german":"His works are loved by everyone across the ages.","furigana":"かれ|さくひん|じだい|こ|ばんじん|あい"},{"japanese":"平和は万人の願いである。","romaji":"Heiwa wa banjin no negai de aru.","german":"Peace is the wish of all people.","furigana":"へいわ|ばんじん|ねが"},{"japanese":"法の前では万人が平等でなければならない。","romaji":"Hō no mae de wa banjin ga byōdō de nakereba naranai.","german":"Before the law, all people must be equal.","furigana":"ほう|まえ|ばんじん|びょうどう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Formal, written-language verb, used almost only as 〜に即して \"in accordance with, in line with\" or 〜に即した + noun: 現実に即した計画 \"a plan grounded in reality\", 実情に即して. Close in meaning are 〜に沿って and 〜に基づいて; 則する is preferred when it is about rules and laws. Do not confuse it with 即/そく \"immediately\" (vocab-n2:1848).","examples":[{"japanese":"現実に即した計画を立てるべきだ。","romaji":"Genjitsu ni sokushita keikaku o tateru beki da.","german":"We should make a plan that is grounded in reality.","furigana":"げんじつ|そく|けいかく|た"},{"japanese":"本件は法律に即して適切に処理いたします。","romaji":"Honken wa houritsu ni sokushite tekisetsu ni shori itashimasu.","german":"We will handle this matter appropriately in accordance with the law.","furigana":"ほんけん|ほうりつ|そく|てきせつ|しょり"},{"japanese":"時代に即さない校則は見直すべきだという声が多い。","romaji":"Jidai ni sokusanai kousoku wa minaosu beki da to iu koe ga ooi.","german":"Many voices say that school rules that no longer suit the times should be reviewed.","furigana":"じだい|そく|こうそく|みなお|こえ|おお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"宝器 is a rare, elevated written word for a precious object, often a ritual or ceremonial vessel, and figuratively for an exceptionally capable person. In everyday speech 宝物 or 宝 is used more. The reading ほうき is shared by several cards - 放棄 \"abandonment\", 箒 \"broom\" and 法規 \"laws and regulations\" - so 宝器 can practically only be recognized by its written form.","examples":[{"japanese":"この寺には国の宝器とされる古い鏡が伝わっている。","romaji":"Kono tera ni wa kuni no hōki to sareru furui kagami ga tsutawatte iru.","german":"This temple preserves an old mirror regarded as a national treasure.","furigana":"てら|くに|ほうき|ふる|かがみ|つた"},{"japanese":"博物館で王家の宝器が特別に公開された。","romaji":"Hakubutsukan de ōke no hōki ga tokubetsu ni kōkai sareta.","german":"The royal family's treasures were put on special display at the museum.","furigana":"はくぶつかん|おうけ|ほうき|とくべつ|こうかい"},{"japanese":"彼は我が社の宝器とも言える人材だ。","romaji":"Kare wa wagasha no hōki to mo ieru jinzai da.","german":"He is a talent you could call a treasure of our company.","furigana":"かれ|わ|しゃ|ほうき|い|じんざい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"仏 with the reading ふつ is the short form of 仏蘭西 and stands for \"France\" or \"French\", almost only in headlines, tables and compounds such as 日仏, 英仏, 仏語 or 仏文学. In spoken everyday language, フランス is always used. The same kanji with the reading ほとけ (separate card) is a completely different word: \"Buddha\" or \"the deceased\".","examples":[{"japanese":"日仏首脳会談が東京で開かれた。","romaji":"Nichifutsu shunō kaidan ga Tōkyō de hirakareta.","german":"The Japan-France summit meeting was held in Tokyo.","furigana":"にちふつしゅのうかいだん|とうきょう|ひら"},{"japanese":"仏大統領が三日間の日程で来日した。","romaji":"Futsu daitōryō ga mikkakan no nittei de rainichi shita.","german":"The French president came to Japan for a three-day visit.","furigana":"ふつだいとうりょう|みっかかん|にってい|らいにち"},{"japanese":"彼女は大学で仏文学を専攻している。","romaji":"Kanojo wa daigaku de futsubungaku o senkō shite iru.","german":"She majors in French literature at university.","furigana":"かのじょ|だいがく|ふつぶんがく|せんこう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"保母 is the older term for a female childcare worker in a nursery or kindergarten; since 1999 the state-certified profession has had the gender-neutral name 保育士, which should be used today. 保母 is still found in older texts, memoirs and in the form of address 保母さん. Not to be confused with the common adverb ほぼ \"almost, nearly\" (own card), which sounds the same.","examples":[{"japanese":"母は若いころ、保育園で保母として働いていた。","romaji":"Haha wa wakai koro, hoikuen de hobo to shite hataraite ita.","german":"In her youth, my mother worked as a nursery teacher at a daycare center.","furigana":"はは|わか|ほいくえん|ほぼ|はたら"},{"japanese":"子どもたちは保母さんの周りに集まって歌を歌った。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa hobo-san no mawari ni atsumatte uta o utatta.","german":"The children gathered around the teacher and sang songs.","furigana":"こ|ほぼ|まわ|あつ|うた|うた"},{"japanese":"今は保母ではなく、保育士という呼び方が一般的だ。","romaji":"Ima wa hobo de wa naku, hoikushi to iu yobikata ga ippanteki da.","german":"Today, rather than 保母, the term 保育士 is the common designation.","furigana":"いま|ほぼ|ほいくし|よ|かた|いっぱんてき"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"瞬き with the reading またたき refers mainly to the twinkling of stars and the flickering of light (星の瞬き, 灯の瞬き); it is the literary noun of the verb 瞬く/またたく (separate card), which is also in またたく間に \"in an instant\". The same spelling with the reading まばたき (separate card) is the usual word for blinking of the eyes. Rule of thumb: eyes → まばたき, stars and light → またたき.","examples":[{"japanese":"夜空に星の瞬きが美しく見えた。","romaji":"Yozora ni hoshi no matataki ga utsukushiku mieta.","german":"The twinkling of the stars in the night sky looked beautiful.","furigana":"よぞら|ほし|またた|うつく|み"},{"japanese":"遠くの街の灯の瞬きが、霧の向こうにかすかに見える。","romaji":"Tōku no machi no hi no matataki ga, kiri no mukō ni kasuka ni mieru.","german":"The flickering of the lights of a distant town can faintly be seen beyond the fog.","furigana":"とお|まち|ひ|またた|きり|む|み"},{"japanese":"ろうそくの炎の瞬きを、彼女はじっと見つめていた。","romaji":"Rōsoku no honoo no matataki o, kanojo wa jitto mitsumete ita.","german":"She gazed intently at the flickering of the candle flame.","furigana":"ほのお|またた|かのじょ|み"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"利根 is an archaic literary word for innate cleverness and quickness of understanding; as a な-adjective, 利根な means \"clever, bright\". In present-day Japanese one says 賢い, 利口 or 聡明 instead. The common 離婚 \"divorce\" (own card) sounds the same; furthermore, the spelling 利根 is mainly known from the river 利根川, which is however read とね.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は幼いころから利根な子として知られていた。","romaji":"Kare wa osanai koro kara rikon na ko to shite shirarete ita.","german":"He was known from early childhood as a bright child.","furigana":"かれ|おさな|りこん|こ|し"},{"japanese":"生まれつきの利根を生かして、彼は若くして名を上げた。","romaji":"Umaretsuki no rikon o ikashite, kare wa wakakushite na o ageta.","german":"Making use of his innate cleverness, he made a name for himself at a young age.","furigana":"う|りこん|い|かれ|わか|な|あ"},{"japanese":"利根な者ほど慢心しやすいと、師は弟子を戒めた。","romaji":"Rikon na mono hodo manshin shiyasui to, shi wa deshi o imashimeta.","german":"The master warned his disciples that the gifted are the ones who easily grow arrogant.","furigana":"りこん|もの|まんしん|し|でし|いまし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"明〜 (みょう) is a formal prefix meaning \"next, coming\" in time words: 明朝 (みょうちょう), 明年 (みょうねん), 明後日 (みょうごにち), 明日 (みょうにち). These readings belong in announcements, business letters and news; in daily life one says あした, あさって, 来年. In Buddhism, 明 also means \"wisdom\" (vidya) or a mantra. Not to be confused with 妙 (みょう) \"strange\".","examples":[{"japanese":"明朝九時に本社で会議を行います。","romaji":"Myōchō kuji ni honsha de kaigi o okonaimasu.","german":"A meeting will be held at the head office at nine o'clock tomorrow morning.","furigana":"みょうちょうくじ|ほんしゃ|かいぎ|おこな"},{"japanese":"明年度の予算案は来月発表される予定だ。","romaji":"Myōnendo no yosan'an wa raigetsu happyō sareru yotei da.","german":"The draft budget for the coming fiscal year is scheduled to be announced next month.","furigana":"みょうねんど|よさんあん|らいげつはっぴょう|よてい"},{"japanese":"明後日の式典には社長も出席いたします。","romaji":"Myōgonichi no shikiten ni wa shachō mo shusseki itashimasu.","german":"The company president will also attend the ceremony the day after tomorrow.","furigana":"みょうごにち|しきてん|しゃちょう|しゅっせき"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N3"},
{"notes":"粗衣 (そい) is a written-language word for \"plain, simple clothing\" and appears almost only in the four-character phrase 粗衣粗食 \"plain clothes and meager food\", which describes a modest, frugal life. In everyday speech, 質素な服 or 粗末な服 is said instead.","examples":[{"japanese":"祖父は戦後、粗衣粗食に耐えて家族を養った。","romaji":"Sofu wa sengo, soi soshoku ni taete kazoku o yashinatta.","german":"After the war, my grandfather endured a life of plain clothes and meager food to support his family.","furigana":"そふ|せんご|そいそしょく|た|かぞく|やしな"},{"japanese":"彼は粗衣をまとい、山寺で修行を続けた。","romaji":"Kare wa soi o matoi, yamadera de shugyō o tsuzuketa.","german":"Dressed in plain clothing, he continued his ascetic training at a mountain temple.","furigana":"かれ|そい|やまでら|しゅぎょう|つづ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Formal Sino-Japanese reading of 一昨昨日, typical of news, minutes and official speeches; it can also be used adverbially without a particle. In everyday speech さきおととい (separate card) is said, and even when written 一昨昨日 is usually read that way. Similarly, 一昨日 \"the day before yesterday\" has the formal reading いっさくじつ alongside おととい.","examples":[{"japanese":"一昨昨日の理事会において、本件は正式に承認されました。","romaji":"Issakusakujitsu no rijikai ni oite, honken wa seishiki ni shōnin saremashita.","german":"At the board meeting three days ago, this matter was formally approved.","furigana":"いっさくさくじつ|りじかい|ほんけん|せいしき|しょうにん"},{"japanese":"警察によると、男性は一昨昨日の夕方から行方が分からなくなっている。","romaji":"Keisatsu ni yoru to, dansei wa issakusakujitsu no yūgata kara yukue ga wakaranaku natte iru.","german":"According to the police, the man has been missing since the evening three days ago.","furigana":"けいさつ|だんせい|いっさくさくじつ|ゆうがた|ゆくえ|わ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"A literary or biblical word for a single child, for example in novels or in the Bible translation (ひとり子 \"the only begotten Son\"). In everyday speech 一人っ子 (separate card) is used, which is much more common and neutral. Note: 一人子 can also appear as an irregular spelling of ひとりっこ.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は老夫婦の一人子として大切に育てられた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa rōfūfu no hitorigo to shite taisetsu ni sodaterareta.","german":"She was raised with great care as the only child of an elderly couple.","furigana":"かのじょ|ろうふうふ|ひとりご|たいせつ|そだ"},{"japanese":"神はそのひとり子をお与えになったほどに、世を愛された。","romaji":"Kami wa sono hitorigo o oatae ni natta hodo ni, yo o aisareta.","german":"God so loved the world that he gave his only Son.","furigana":"かみ|ご|あた|よ|あい"}],"levelNote":"Compound: 一人 (N5) + 子 (N4)"},
{"notes":"Technical term from forensic medicine and archaeology for the waxy substance that forms on corpses in a damp, oxygen-poor environment; also found in crime novels. Often used as 屍蝋化する \"to turn into adipocere\". The spelling 死蝋 is a rarer variant; the character 蝋 means \"wax\" as in 蝋燭.","examples":[{"japanese":"湿った粘土層に埋まっていた遺体は、屍蝋化していた。","romaji":"Shimetta nendosō ni umatte ita itai wa, shirōka shite ita.","german":"The body buried in a damp clay layer had turned into adipocere.","furigana":"しめ|ねんどそう|う|いたい|しろうか"},{"japanese":"法医学の授業で、屍蝋ができる条件について学んだ。","romaji":"Hōigaku no jugyō de, shirō ga dekiru jōken ni tsuite mananda.","german":"In forensic medicine class, we learned about the conditions under which adipocere forms.","furigana":"ほういがく|じゅぎょう|しろう|じょうけん|まな"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Noun of 攻める \"to attack\" (separate card), mainly in sports reports, go/shogi and combat sports. Typical phrases: 攻め手を欠く \"to lack means of attack\", 攻め手に回る \"to go on the offensive\"; the opposite is 守り手. Not to be confused with the identically sounding adverb せめて \"at least\", which is always written in kana.","examples":[{"japanese":"後半は攻め手を欠き、チームは無得点に終わった。","romaji":"Kōhan wa semete o kaki, chīmu wa mutokuten ni owatta.","german":"In the second half the team lacked attacking options and ended up scoreless.","furigana":"こうはん|せ|て|か|むとくてん|お"},{"japanese":"相手の守りが固くて、なかなか攻め手が見つからない。","romaji":"Aite no mamori ga katakute, nakanaka semete ga mitsukaranai.","german":"The opponent's defense is solid, and I just can't find a way to attack.","furigana":"あいて|まも|かた|せ|て|み"},{"japanese":"守りに徹していた選手が、終盤で一転して攻め手に回った。","romaji":"Mamori ni tesshite ita senshu ga, shūban de itten shite semete ni mawatta.","german":"The player who had focused entirely on defense suddenly switched to attack in the closing stages.","furigana":"まも|てっ|せんしゅ|しゅうばん|いってん|せ|て|まわ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Literary taru-adjective, mostly 蕩々たる before a noun or 蕩々と adverbially, especially for wide, calm stretches of water. Do not confuse it with the identically sounding cards とうとう \"at last\" and 〜等々 \"and so on\", or with 滔々 \"flowing torrentially; eloquent\" (滔々と語る), which is more common in everyday use. Usually written with 々.","examples":[{"japanese":"眼下には蕩々たる大河がゆったりと流れていた。","romaji":"Ganka ni wa tōtō taru taiga ga yuttari to nagarete ita.","german":"Below us, a broad, mighty river flowed leisurely.","furigana":"がんか|とうとう|たいが|なが"},{"japanese":"穏やかな日差しの下、蕩々たる春の海が広がっている。","romaji":"Odayaka na hizashi no shita, tōtō taru haru no umi ga hirogatte iru.","german":"Under the gentle sunlight, the wide, calm spring sea stretches out.","furigana":"おだ|ひざ|した|とうとう|はる|うみ|ひろ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"The noun of 縒る \"to twist, to spin\"; on its own mainly in crafts and textile terminology (縒りが甘い \"loosely twisted\"). It is well known from the phrases よりを戻す \"to get back together (with an ex)\" and 腕によりをかける \"to put all one's skill into it\", which are mostly written in kana. Not to be confused with the comparison particle より \"than\".","examples":[{"japanese":"別れた二人が、最近またよりを戻したらしい。","romaji":"Wakareta futari ga, saikin mata yori o modoshita rashii.","german":"The two who had broken up have apparently gotten back together recently.","furigana":"わか|ふたり|さいきん|もど"},{"japanese":"母は腕によりをかけて誕生日のごちそうを作った。","romaji":"Haha wa ude ni yori o kakete tanjōbi no gochisō o tsukutta.","german":"My mother pulled out all the stops and made a birthday feast.","furigana":"はは|うで|たんじょうび|つく"},{"japanese":"この糸は縒りが甘いので、引っ張るとすぐ切れる。","romaji":"Kono ito wa yori ga amai node, hipparu to sugu kireru.","german":"This thread is loosely twisted, so it breaks as soon as you pull on it.","furigana":"いと|よ|あま|ひ|ぱ|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N5 (source: N5; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"Sino-Japanese reading of 物, mainly as a suffix in technical words: 危険物, 印刷物, 郵便物, 農産物. On its own, ぶつ is slang from crime stories and police jargon for stolen goods, drugs or \"the stuff\", and is then usually written ブツ. In everyday speech \"thing\" is もの (separate card); the verb ぶつ \"to hit\" is a different word.","examples":[{"japanese":"危険物の機内への持ち込みは禁止されています。","romaji":"Kikenbutsu no kinai e no mochikomi wa kinshi sarete imasu.","german":"Carrying dangerous items onto the aircraft is prohibited.","furigana":"きけんぶつ|きない|も|こ|きんし"},{"japanese":"会議で使う印刷物は、事前に配っておいてください。","romaji":"Kaigi de tsukau insatsubutsu wa, jizen ni kubatte oite kudasai.","german":"Please distribute the printed materials for the meeting in advance.","furigana":"かいぎ|つか|いんさつぶつ|じぜん|くば"},{"japanese":"刑事たちはついにブツの隠し場所を突き止めた。","romaji":"Keijitachi wa tsui ni butsu no kakushibasho o tsukitometa.","german":"The detectives finally found out where the goods were hidden.","furigana":"けいじ|かく|ばしょ|つ|と"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Sino-Japanese reading of 主; as a free word it is rare and elevated: in Christianity \"the Lord\" (主イエス, 主の祈り \"the Lord's Prayer\"), as well as in 主として \"mainly\", 主たる \"main, principal\" and 主と従 \"principal and subordinate\". In everyday speech 主に (おもに, separate card) is said. しゅ is very common in compounds such as 主人, 主婦, 主要; do not confuse it with the homophone 種/しゅ \"species\".","examples":[{"japanese":"この保険は主として高齢者を対象としている。","romaji":"Kono hoken wa shu to shite kōreisha o taishō to shite iru.","german":"This insurance is aimed mainly at elderly people.","furigana":"ほけん|しゅ|こうれいしゃ|たいしょう"},{"japanese":"日曜日の礼拝では、みんなで主の祈りを唱える。","romaji":"Nichiyōbi no reihai de wa, minna de shu no inori o tonaeru.","german":"At the Sunday service, everyone recites the Lord's Prayer together.","furigana":"にちようび|れいはい|しゅ|いの|とな"},{"japanese":"当社の主たる業務は食品の輸入です。","romaji":"Tōsha no shutaru gyōmu wa shokuhin no yunyū desu.","german":"Our company's main business is food import.","furigana":"とうしゃ|しゅ|ぎょうむ|しょくひん|ゆにゅう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Taxonomic term for the rank between 科 \"family\" and 属 \"genus\", usually attached directly to an animal or plant name in katakana: ネコ亜科, ヒト亜科. Formed analogously are 亜目 \"suborder\" and 亜種 \"subspecies\". Outside biology the word hardly occurs; do not confuse it with the homophones 赤 \"red\" and 垢 \"dirt\".","examples":[{"japanese":"ライオンやトラはヒョウ亜科に分類される。","romaji":"Raion ya tora wa hyou aka ni bunrui sareru.","german":"Lions and tigers are classified in the subfamily Pantherinae.","furigana":"あか|ぶんるい"},{"japanese":"分類学では、科と属の間に亜科を設けることがある。","romaji":"Bunruigaku de wa, ka to zoku no aida ni aka o moukeru koto ga aru.","german":"In taxonomy, a subfamily is sometimes established between the family and the genus.","furigana":"ぶんるいがく|か|ぞく|あいだ|あか|もう"},{"japanese":"この化石の昆虫のために、新しい亜科が設けられた。","romaji":"Kono kaseki no konchuu no tame ni, atarashii aka ga moukerareta.","german":"A new subfamily was established for this fossil insect.","furigana":"かせき|こんちゅう|あたら|あか|もう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Designation for a district or section within a municipality, today mainly in rural addresses: 大字 \"larger district\" and 小字 \"small section name\", often also simply 字 before the name. It is written the same as 字/じ \"character\" (separate card), but is a completely different word; in addresses it is always read あざ. In everyday speech 地区 or 集落 is used instead.","examples":[{"japanese":"祖父の家の住所は「中野村大字山田字上原」だ。","romaji":"Sofu no ie no juusho wa \"Nakano-mura Ooaza Yamada Aza Uehara\" da.","german":"My grandfather's house address is \"Nakano Village, Ōaza Yamada, Aza Uehara\".","furigana":"そふ|いえ|じゅうしょ|なかのむらおおあざやまだあざうえはら"},{"japanese":"この村では、字ごとに祭りの山車を出す。","romaji":"Kono mura de wa, aza goto ni matsuri no dashi o dasu.","german":"In this village, each district puts out its own festival float.","furigana":"むら|あざ|まつ|だし|だ"},{"japanese":"区画整理で、古い字の名前が地図から消えた。","romaji":"Kukaku seiri de, furui aza no namae ga chizu kara kieta.","german":"Because of land readjustment, the old aza names disappeared from the map.","furigana":"くかくせいり|ふる|あざ|なまえ|ちず|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Old Japanese reading of 天地 with the same basic meaning \"heaven and earth, the whole world\" as 天地/てんち (separate card). あめつち is found almost only in classical poetry, in myths such as the 古事記, and in deliberately archaizing language, often referring to the gods of heaven and earth. In present-day use and in phrases such as 天地の差 or 天地無用, it is always read てんち.","examples":[{"japanese":"古事記は、天地の始まりの物語から書き起こされている。","romaji":"Kojiki wa, ametsuchi no hajimari no monogatari kara kakiokosarete iru.","german":"The Kojiki begins with the story of the beginning of heaven and earth.","furigana":"こじき|あめつち|はじ|ものがたり|か|お"},{"japanese":"古の歌人は、天地の神々に国の平和を祈った。","romaji":"Inishie no kajin wa, ametsuchi no kamigami ni kuni no heiwa o inotta.","german":"The poets of old prayed to the gods of heaven and earth for peace in the land.","furigana":"|かじん|あめつち|かみがみ|くに|へいわ|いの"},{"japanese":"天地の間に生きるものは、みな同じ命を持つと詩人は詠んだ。","romaji":"Ametsuchi no aida ni ikiru mono wa, mina onaji inochi o motsu to shijin wa yonda.","german":"The poet sang that all living things between heaven and earth share the same life.","furigana":"あめつち|あいだ|い|おな|いのち|も|しじん|よ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Noun of the verb 合わせる (separate card): the joining or facing of two things. On its own it is rare; it is mostly the first or last element of compounds: 合わせ鏡 \"two mirrors facing each other\", 向かい合わせ \"facing each other\", 背中合わせ \"back to back\", 詰め合わせ \"assortment\" or 打ち合わせ \"meeting\" (separate card). Do not confuse it with the homophone 袷 \"lined kimono\".","examples":[{"japanese":"合わせ鏡にすると、自分の後ろ髪が見える。","romaji":"Awasekagami ni suru to, jibun no ushirogami ga mieru.","german":"If you put two mirrors facing each other, you can see the back of your own hair.","furigana":"あ|かがみ|じぶん|うし|がみ|み"},{"japanese":"電車で知らない人と向かい合わせに座った。","romaji":"Densha de shiranai hito to mukaiawase ni suwatta.","german":"On the train, I sat directly facing a stranger.","furigana":"でんしゃ|し|ひと|む|あ|すわ"},{"japanese":"お歳暮に、お菓子の詰め合わせを贈った。","romaji":"Oseibo ni, okashi no tsumeawase o okutta.","german":"I sent an assortment of sweets as a year-end gift.","furigana":"せいぼ|かし|つ|あ|おく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Noun of the verb 暗示する (separate card): an indirect hint or a hidden clue, as opposed to 明示 \"explicit statement\". In psychology it means \"suggestion\": 暗示にかかる \"to be influenced\", 暗示をかける \"to suggest\", 自己暗示 \"self-suggestion\". More elevated than ヒント, typical in texts about literature, psychology and advertising.","examples":[{"japanese":"小説の冒頭には、結末への暗示が隠されている。","romaji":"Shousetsu no boutou ni wa, ketsumatsu e no anji ga kakusarete iru.","german":"A hint of the ending is hidden at the beginning of the novel.","furigana":"しょうせつ|ぼうとう|けつまつ|あんじ|かく"},{"japanese":"彼は暗示にかかりやすいタイプだ。","romaji":"Kare wa anji ni kakariyasui taipu da.","german":"He is the type who is easily influenced (susceptible to suggestion).","furigana":"かれ|あんじ"},{"japanese":"試合の前に「絶対に勝てる」と自己暗示をかけた。","romaji":"Shiai no mae ni \"zettai ni kateru\" to jiko anji o kaketa.","german":"Before the match, I told myself through self-suggestion, \"I can definitely win.\"","furigana":"しあい|まえ|ぜったい|か|じこあんじ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A rarer reading of 家主 alongside 家主/やぬし (separate card), with the same meaning. In a rental context (家主に家賃を払う), やぬし is usual; いえぬし sounds more like \"owner or master of the house\" and appears in narratives, older texts and reports about residential houses. Colloquially, 大家さん is mostly said for the landlord, and 貸主 in contracts.","examples":[{"japanese":"空き家の家主が見つからず、市は対応に困っている。","romaji":"Akiya no ienushi ga mitsukarazu, shi wa taiou ni komatte iru.","german":"The owner of the vacant house cannot be found, and the city is at a loss how to respond.","furigana":"あ|や|いえぬし|み|し|たいおう|こま"},{"japanese":"家主の留守中に、近所の人が庭の手入れをしてくれた。","romaji":"Ienushi no rusuchuu ni, kinjo no hito ga niwa no teire o shite kureta.","german":"While the owner of the house was away, a neighbor took care of the garden.","furigana":"いえぬし|るすちゅう|きんじょ|ひと|にわ|てい"},{"japanese":"昔話では、旅人が家主に一晩の宿を頼む場面がよくある。","romaji":"Mukashibanashi de wa, tabibito ga ienushi ni hitoban no yado o tanomu bamen ga yoku aru.","german":"In old tales, there are often scenes where a traveler asks the master of the house for a night's lodging.","furigana":"むかしばなし|たびびと|いえぬし|ひとばん|やど|たの|ばめん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Old Japanese word for \"war, battle\", today mainly in historical contexts (samurai era), literature and proverbs: 戦に出る, 負け戦, 腹が減っては戦ができぬ. Less often written 軍. For modern wars 戦争 is used, for fights in general 戦い (both separate cards); the reading せん occurs only as a suffix (〜戦, separate card, e.g. 決勝戦).","examples":[{"japanese":"腹が減っては戦ができぬ。","romaji":"Hara ga hette wa ikusa ga dekinu.","german":"An army marches on its stomach. (proverb; lit. \"you can't fight on an empty stomach\")","furigana":"はら|へ|いくさ"},{"japanese":"若い武士は、初めての戦に出た。","romaji":"Wakai bushi wa, hajimete no ikusa ni deta.","german":"The young samurai went into battle for the first time.","furigana":"わか|ぶし|はじ|いくさ|で"},{"japanese":"負け戦だと分かっていても、彼らは城を守り続けた。","romaji":"Makeikusa da to wakatte ite mo, karera wa shiro o mamoritsuzuketa.","german":"Even though they knew it was a losing battle, they kept defending the castle.","furigana":"ま|いくさ|わ|かれ|しろ|まも|つづ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Native Japanese reading of 碑 with the same meaning as 碑/ひ (separate card): a stone with an inscription carved into it, meant to commemorate an event or a person. いしぶみ sounds literary and archaic and appears in poems, song lyrics and titles; also written 石文. In compounds such as 記念碑 or 慰霊碑 it is always read ひ.","examples":[{"japanese":"峠の道端に、古い碑がひっそりと立っている。","romaji":"Touge no michibata ni, furui ishibumi ga hissori to tatte iru.","german":"An old monument stands quietly by the roadside on the pass.","furigana":"とうげ|みちばた|ふる|いしぶみ|た"},{"japanese":"碑に刻まれた文字は、雨風に削られてほとんど読めない。","romaji":"Ishibumi ni kizamareta moji wa, amekaze ni kezurarete hotondo yomenai.","german":"The characters carved on the stone have been worn away by wind and rain and are almost unreadable.","furigana":"いしぶみ|きざ|もじ|あめかぜ|けず|よ"},{"japanese":"村人は津波の教訓を碑に刻み、後世に残した。","romaji":"Murabito wa tsunami no kyoukun o ishibumi ni kizami, kousei ni nokoshita.","german":"The villagers carved the lesson of the tsunami into a monument and left it for posterity.","furigana":"むらびと|つなみ|きょうくん|いしぶみ|きざ|こうせい|のこ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Elevated Sino-Japanese reading of 一言 alongside the everyday 一言/ひとこと (separate card). いちげん survives almost only in fixed expressions: 一言居士 \"someone who has to put in his two cents on everything\", 一言のもとに \"with a single word, out of hand\" and 一言一行 \"every word and deed\". If you simply want to \"say a word\", you always say ひとこと; do not confuse it with the homophone 一見/いちげん \"first-time visitor without an introduction\".","examples":[{"japanese":"部長は、会議のたびに口をはさむ一言居士だ。","romaji":"Buchou wa, kaigi no tabi ni kuchi o hasamu ichigen koji da.","german":"The department head is the sort of person who has to put in his two cents at every meeting.","furigana":"ぶちょう|かいぎ|くち|"},{"japanese":"私の提案は、一言のもとに退けられた。","romaji":"Watashi no teian wa, ichigen no moto ni shirizokerareta.","german":"My proposal was rejected out of hand with a single word.","furigana":"わたし|ていあん|いちげん|しりぞ"},{"japanese":"指導者の一言一行が、若者に大きな影響を与える。","romaji":"Shidousha no ichigen ikkou ga, wakamono ni ookina eikyou o ataeru.","german":"Every word and deed of a leader has a great influence on young people.","furigana":"しどうしゃ|いちげんいっこう|わかもの|おお|えいきょう|あた"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Elevated reading of 一日 alongside 一日/いちにち \"one day, all day\" and 一日/ついたち \"the first of the month\" (both separate cards). いちじつ appears almost only in fixed phrases: 一日の長 \"a slight edge in experience\" and 一日千秋 \"to wait longingly (one day feels like a thousand autumns)\". In normal talk about days and dates, it is never read いちじつ.","examples":[{"japanese":"料理に関しては、姉のほうに一日の長がある。","romaji":"Ryouri ni kanshite wa, ane no hou ni ichijitsu no chou ga aru.","german":"When it comes to cooking, my older sister has a slight edge in experience.","furigana":"りょうり|かん|あね|いちじつ|ちょう"},{"japanese":"留学中の息子の帰国を、母は一日千秋の思いで待っている。","romaji":"Ryuugakuchuu no musuko no kikoku o, haha wa ichijitsu senshuu no omoi de matte iru.","german":"The mother is waiting with great longing for her son, who is studying abroad, to come home.","furigana":"りゅうがくちゅう|むすこ|きこく|はは|いちじつせんしゅう|おも|ま"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Sino-Japanese reading of 一人 alongside the everyday 一人/ひとり (separate card). On its own, いちにん is hardly heard anymore; the reading is, however, found in important compounds such as 一人前 \"full-fledged, adult; one portion\" and 一人称 \"first person (grammar)\" (separate card). For counting people and for \"alone\", ひとり is always used.","examples":[{"japanese":"息子もようやく一人前の大工になった。","romaji":"Musuko mo youyaku ichininmae no daiku ni natta.","german":"My son has finally become a full-fledged carpenter, too.","furigana":"むすこ|いちにんまえ|だいく"},{"japanese":"この小説は、主人公の一人称で語られる。","romaji":"Kono shousetsu wa, shujinkou no ichininshou de katarareru.","german":"This novel is told in the first person from the protagonist's viewpoint.","furigana":"しょうせつ|しゅじんこう|いちにんしょう|かた"},{"japanese":"出前で寿司を一人前だけ注文した。","romaji":"Demae de sushi o ichininmae dake chuumon shita.","german":"I ordered just one serving of sushi for delivery.","furigana":"でまえ|すし|いちにんまえ|ちゅうもん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Elevated word for the separation since the last meeting, used almost only in letters and greetings: ご一別以来 \"since we last saw each other\" is a fixed opening formula, often followed by ご無沙汰しております. As a suru verb (一別する) it is rare. Do not confuse it with the homophone 一瞥 \"a quick glance\".","examples":[{"japanese":"ご一別以来、早いもので三年がたちました。","romaji":"Goichibetsu irai, hayai mono de sannen ga tachimashita.","german":"Three years have flown by since we last saw each other.","furigana":"いちべついらい|はや|さんねん"},{"japanese":"一別以来の再会に、二人は思わず手を取り合った。","romaji":"Ichibetsu irai no saikai ni, futari wa omowazu te o toriatta.","german":"At their first reunion since parting, the two instinctively took each other's hands.","furigana":"いちべついらい|さいかい|ふたり|おも|て|と|あ"},{"japanese":"「ご一別以来ご無沙汰しております」と、手紙を書き始めた。","romaji":"\"Goichibetsu irai gobusata shite orimasu\" to, tegami o kakihajimeta.","german":"\"I have not been in touch for a long time since we last met,\" he began the letter.","furigana":"いちべついらい|ぶさた|てがみ|か|はじ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A separate word alongside 一目/ひとめ (separate card): いちもく also means \"a glance\", but occurs almost only in fixed expressions: 一目瞭然 \"clear at a glance\", 一目散に \"headlong (run away)\" and 一目置く \"to respect someone, to acknowledge as superior\". The last comes from Go, where 一目 means \"one stone\": the weaker player places a stone in advance. A simple short glance (一目で分かる) is read ひとめ.","examples":[{"japanese":"グラフにすれば、売上の変化は一目瞭然だ。","romaji":"Gurafu ni sureba, uriage no henka wa ichimoku ryouzen da.","german":"Shown as a graph, the change in sales is clear at a glance.","furigana":"うりあげ|へんか|いちもくりょうぜん"},{"japanese":"彼の実力には、ベテラン選手たちも一目置いている。","romaji":"Kare no jitsuryoku ni wa, beteran senshutachi mo ichimoku oite iru.","german":"Even veteran players hold his ability in respect.","furigana":"かれ|じつりょく|せんしゅ|いちもくお"},{"japanese":"犬に吠えられて、子どもたちは一目散に逃げ出した。","romaji":"Inu ni hoerarete, kodomotachi wa ichimokusan ni nigedashita.","german":"Barked at by a dog, the children ran away as fast as they could.","furigana":"いぬ|ほ|こ|いちもくさん|に|だ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"na-adjective (also with の) for \"the same everywhere, uniform\": 一様な色, 一様に広がる. Very common adverbially as 一様に \"all in the same way\", e.g. 参加者は一様に驚いた. In the negative (一様ではない) it means \"not uniform, varying from case to case\". More written-language than 同じ; related to 一律 \"across the board, without exception\" (separate card), which is used more for rules and amounts.","examples":[{"japanese":"質問を聞いて、学生たちは一様に首をかしげた。","romaji":"Shitsumon o kiite, gakuseitachi wa ichiyou ni kubi o kashigeta.","german":"Hearing the question, the students all tilted their heads in the same way.","furigana":"しつもん|き|がくせい|いちよう|くび"},{"japanese":"空は一様な灰色の雲に覆われていた。","romaji":"Sora wa ichiyou na haiiro no kumo ni oowarete ita.","german":"The sky was covered with a uniformly gray layer of clouds.","furigana":"そら|いちよう|はいいろ|くも|おお"},{"japanese":"地域によって事情は一様ではない。","romaji":"Chiiki ni yotte jijou wa ichiyou de wa nai.","german":"The circumstances are not the same everywhere, depending on the region.","furigana":"ちいき|じじょう|いちよう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"一気 is the base word behind the adverb 一気に \"in one go, all at once\" (separate card). On its own it is today mainly a colloquial drinking cheer \"Bottoms up!\" (often doubled: 一気！一気！) and appears in compounds such as 一気飲み \"chugging\" or 一気呵成 \"in one go\". Pressuring someone to chug is now regarded as harassment; in a normal sentence, 一気に is used for \"all at once\".","examples":[{"japanese":"宴会で先輩たちが「一気！一気！」と手をたたいた。","romaji":"Enkai de senpai-tachi ga \"Ikki! Ikki!\" to te o tataita.","german":"At the party, the senior colleagues clapped and shouted \"Bottoms up! Bottoms up!\"","furigana":"えんかい|せんぱい|いっき|いっき|て"},{"japanese":"一気飲みは体に危険なので、絶対にやめましょう。","romaji":"Ikki-nomi wa karada ni kiken na node, zettai ni yamemashō.","german":"Chugging a drink is dangerous for the body, so by all means don't do it.","furigana":"いっきの|からだ|きけん|ぜったい"},{"japanese":"締め切り前の夜、彼は論文を一気呵成に書き上げた。","romaji":"Shimekiri mae no yoru, kare wa ronbun o ikki kasei ni kakiageta.","german":"The night before the deadline, he wrote the paper in one go.","furigana":"し|き|まえ|よる|かれ|ろんぶん||か|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"いとま is a word in its own right, more elevated than 暇/ひま \"free time\" (separate card), and is mostly written in kana. It is alive mainly in おいとまする \"to politely take one's leave\" and in 枚挙にいとまがない \"to be countless\"; 暇を乞う \"to ask for leave/dismissal\" sounds historical. For everyday free time one says ひま, never いとま.","examples":[{"japanese":"夜も遅いので、そろそろおいとまします。","romaji":"Yoru mo osoi node, sorosoro oitoma shimasu.","german":"It's getting late, so I'll be taking my leave soon.","furigana":"よる|おそ"},{"japanese":"この種のトラブルは枚挙にいとまがない。","romaji":"Kono shu no toraburu wa maikyo ni itoma ga nai.","german":"There is no end of troubles of this kind.","furigana":"しゅ|まいきょ"},{"japanese":"侍は主君に暇を乞い、故郷へ帰った。","romaji":"Samurai wa shukun ni itoma o koi, kokyō e kaetta.","german":"The samurai asked his lord for leave and returned to his hometown.","furigana":"さむらい|しゅくん|いとま|こ|こきょう|かえ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"一敗 \"one defeat\" appears mainly in sports records (三勝一敗, 一勝一敗) and can be used as a verb with する (一敗する). Not to be confused with the similar-sounding いっぱい \"full, a lot\" (own card); in writing, the kanji 敗 \"to lose\" makes it clear. Elevated is the phrase 一敗地にまみれる \"to suffer a crushing defeat\"; the general \"victory or defeat\" is 勝敗.","examples":[{"japanese":"バスケ部は三勝一敗で予選を通過した。","romaji":"Basuke-bu wa sanshō ippai de yosen o tsūka shita.","german":"The basketball team got through the qualifying round with three wins and one loss.","furigana":"ぶ|さんしょういっぱい|よせん|つうか"},{"japanese":"初戦で一敗したが、まだ優勝の望みはある。","romaji":"Shosen de ippai shita ga, mada yūshō no nozomi wa aru.","german":"We lost the first match, but there is still hope for the title.","furigana":"しょせん|いっぱい|ゆうしょう|のぞ"},{"japanese":"王者は無名の新人に一敗地にまみれた。","romaji":"Ōja wa mumei no shinjin ni ippai chi ni mamireta.","german":"The champion suffered a crushing defeat against an unknown newcomer.","furigana":"おうじゃ|むめい|しんじん|いっぱいち"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N4 (source: N4; change limited to two levels)"},
{"notes":"印/いん is the (name) seal or its impression, especially in formal phrases such as 印を押す \"to stamp\" and in compounds such as 実印 or 認め印. In everyday speech, はんこ or 印鑑 (separate cards) is said instead. Written the same but read differently is 印/しるし \"sign, mark\"; 印を結ぶ refers to the Buddhist hand gesture (mudra) or ninja finger signs.","examples":[{"japanese":"書類のここに印を押してください。","romaji":"Shorui no koko ni in o oshite kudasai.","german":"Please stamp your seal here on the document.","furigana":"しょるい|いん|お"},{"japanese":"この印は銀行に届けてあるものと違います。","romaji":"Kono in wa ginkō ni todokete aru mono to chigaimasu.","german":"This seal is different from the one registered with the bank.","furigana":"いん|ぎんこう|とど|ちが"},{"japanese":"仏像は胸の前で両手を合わせて印を結んでいる。","romaji":"Butsuzō wa mune no mae de ryōte o awasete in o musunde iru.","german":"The Buddha statue forms a mudra with both hands in front of its chest.","furigana":"ぶつぞう|むね|まえ|りょうて|あ|いん|むす"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"うえした is the native Japanese reading of 上下 and refers concretely to the top and bottom of an object, or to \"upside down\" (上下が逆). The card 上下/じょうげ is the more general Sino-Japanese reading: movement up and down (上下する), hierarchy (上下関係), two-volume works (上下巻). With 上下逆さま both readings are common; うえした sounds more everyday.","examples":[{"japanese":"箱の上下を確かめてから開けてください。","romaji":"Hako no ueshita o tashikamete kara akete kudasai.","german":"Please check which side is up and which is down before opening the box.","furigana":"はこ|うえした|たし|あ"},{"japanese":"この写真、上下が逆さまに貼ってあるよ。","romaji":"Kono shashin, ueshita ga sakasama ni hatte aru yo.","german":"This photo is pasted in upside down.","furigana":"しゃしん|うえした|さか|は"},{"japanese":"この絵は上下どちらから見ても顔に見える。","romaji":"Kono e wa ueshita dochira kara mite mo kao ni mieru.","german":"This picture looks like a face whether you view it from the top or from the bottom.","furigana":"え|うえした|み|かお|み"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"うじ is the old native Japanese reading of 氏 for lineage, family name and the clan of early times (e.g. in the 氏姓制度). Today it appears mainly in the proverb 氏より育ち \"upbringing counts more than birth\" and in 氏神 \"tutelary deity of a place or clan\". To be distinguished are the cards 〜氏/し (polite name suffix \"Mr./Ms.\") and 氏/し \"clan\"; names like 藤原氏 are today mostly read with し.","examples":[{"japanese":"氏より育ちと言うように、人柄は家柄より環境で決まる。","romaji":"Uji yori sodachi to iu yō ni, hitogara wa iegara yori kankyō de kimaru.","german":"As the saying goes, upbringing counts for more than birth: character is determined more by environment than by family background.","furigana":"うじ|そだ|い|ひとがら|いえがら|かんきょう|き"},{"japanese":"村では毎年秋に氏神の祭りが開かれる。","romaji":"Mura de wa maitoshi aki ni ujigami no matsuri ga hirakareru.","german":"Every autumn, the festival of the local tutelary deity is held in the village.","furigana":"むら|まいとしあき|うじがみ|まつ|ひら"},{"japanese":"古代の日本では、氏ごとに朝廷での役割が決まっていた。","romaji":"Kodai no Nihon de wa, uji goto ni chōtei de no yakuwari ga kimatte ita.","german":"In ancient Japan, each clan had a fixed role at the imperial court.","furigana":"こだい|にほん|うじ|ちょうてい|やくわり|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Animal names such as エイ \"ray\" are mostly written in katakana, just like サメ \"shark\" (separate card); the kanji 鱝, 鱏 or 海鷂魚 are rare and hardly used anymore. Well known is エイヒレ, dried ray fin eaten as a snack with beer. It sounds the same as the pronunciation of the letter A (エイ/エー), but context always makes it clear.","examples":[{"japanese":"水族館の大きな水槽で、エイがゆったりと泳いでいた。","romaji":"Suizokukan no ōkina suisō de, ei ga yuttari to oyoide ita.","german":"A ray was swimming leisurely in the large tank at the aquarium.","furigana":"すいぞくかん|おお|すいそう|およ"},{"japanese":"浅瀬ではエイの尾のとげに刺されないよう注意してください。","romaji":"Asase de wa ei no o no toge ni sasarenai yō chūi shite kudasai.","german":"In the shallows, be careful not to be stung by the barb on a ray's tail.","furigana":"あさせ|お|さ|ちゅうい"},{"japanese":"居酒屋でエイヒレをつまみにビールを飲んだ。","romaji":"Izakaya de eihire o tsumami ni bīru o nonda.","german":"At the izakaya, I drank beer with dried ray fin as a snack.","furigana":"いざかや|の"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"応急 \"emergency, makeshift\" refers to a quick, provisional measure until a proper solution is found, and almost always stands before another noun or with の: 応急手当 \"first aid\", 応急修理, 応急の措置. The fixed combination 応急処置 has its own card. 救急 \"emergency rescue\", by contrast, stresses the emergency itself, 応急 the interim solution.","examples":[{"japanese":"救急車が来るまで、けが人に応急手当をした。","romaji":"Kyūkyūsha ga kuru made, keganin ni ōkyū teate o shita.","german":"I gave the injured person first aid until the ambulance arrived.","furigana":"きゅうきゅうしゃ|く|にん|おうきゅうてあて"},{"japanese":"応急の修理として、ガムテープで穴をふさいだ。","romaji":"Ōkyū no shūri to shite, gamutēpu de ana o fusaida.","german":"As an emergency repair, I plugged the hole with duct tape.","furigana":"おうきゅう|しゅうり|あな"},{"japanese":"震災の後、被災者のために応急仮設住宅が建てられた。","romaji":"Shinsai no ato, hisaisha no tame ni ōkyū kasetsu jūtaku ga taterareta.","german":"After the earthquake, temporary emergency housing was built for the victims.","furigana":"しんさい|あと|ひさいしゃ|おうきゅうかせつじゅうたく|た"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"驕る (also 傲る, often in kana) means \"to be arrogant, haughty\" and is a different word from the identically sounding 奢る \"to treat someone; to live in luxury\" (separate card). Typical is 〜に驕る \"to be conceited about ...\" (才能に驕る, 勝利に驕る) and the proverb 驕る平家は久しからず. The word is written-language and elevated; in everyday speech 威張る or 傲慢だ (separate cards) is said.","examples":[{"japanese":"驕る平家は久しからず。","romaji":"Ogoru Heike wa hisashikarazu.","german":"Pride goes before a fall (lit. the arrogant Taira do not last long).","furigana":"おご|へいけ|ひさ"},{"japanese":"一度勝ったくらいで驕ってはいけない。","romaji":"Ichido katta kurai de ogotte wa ikenai.","german":"You must not get arrogant just because you won once.","furigana":"いちどか|おご"},{"japanese":"彼は才能に驕って、練習を怠るようになった。","romaji":"Kare wa sainō ni ogotte, renshū o okotaru yō ni natta.","german":"Conceited about his talent, he began to neglect his practice.","furigana":"かれ|さいのう|おご|れんしゅう|おこた"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"おさ is the old native Japanese reading of 長 for the head of a community (村の長, 一族の長, 群れの長) and sounds elevated, literary or fairy-tale-like. The card 長/ちょう is the Sino-Japanese reading for leader/boss and the suffix in 社長 or 校長. In modern factual language, 村長 or リーダー is said rather than 村の長.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は長年、村の長として人々に慕われてきた。","romaji":"Kare wa naganen, mura no osa to shite hitobito ni shitawarete kita.","german":"For many years he was loved by the people as the village head.","furigana":"かれ|ながねん|むら|おさ|ひとびと|した"},{"japanese":"一族の長の言葉には誰も逆らえなかった。","romaji":"Ichizoku no osa no kotoba ni wa dare mo sakaraenakatta.","german":"No one could go against the word of the head of the clan.","furigana":"いちぞく|おさ|ことば|だれ|さか"},{"japanese":"群れの長であるオオカミが先頭を走っている。","romaji":"Mure no osa de aru ōkami ga sentō o hashitte iru.","german":"The wolf that leads the pack is running at the front.","furigana":"む|おさ|せんとう|はし"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"おどおど is an onomatopoeic word describing fearful, unsure behavior out of fear or shyness, mostly as おどおどする, おどおどした様子 or おどおどと + verb. The opposite is 堂々と \"confidently, with composure\". Related are びくびく (flinching out of fear of something specific) and おずおず (approaching hesitantly); it is occasionally also written オドオド.","examples":[{"japanese":"子どもは知らない大人の前でおどおどしていた。","romaji":"Kodomo wa shiranai otona no mae de odoodo shite ita.","german":"The child was timid in front of the unfamiliar adults.","furigana":"こ|し|おとな|まえ"},{"japanese":"彼はおどおどした様子で遅刻を謝った。","romaji":"Kare wa odoodo shita yōsu de chikoku o ayamatta.","german":"He apologized for being late in a nervous manner.","furigana":"かれ|ようす|ちこく|あやま"},{"japanese":"面接ではおどおどせず、堂々と答えなさい。","romaji":"Mensetsu de wa odoodo sezu, dōdō to kotaenasai.","german":"In the interview, don't be timid; answer confidently.","furigana":"めんせつ|どうどう|こた"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"織り is the noun of 織る \"to weave\" (separate card) and refers to the weave or its quality (織りが細かい) or the woven product. In place-of-origin names of fabrics it usually stands without okurigana and is pronounced -おり: 西陣織, 博多織; likewise in 手織り \"hand-woven\". 織物 (separate card) is the general word for textiles as a product.","examples":[{"japanese":"この帯は織りが細かくて、とても上品だ。","romaji":"Kono obi wa ori ga komakakute, totemo jōhin da.","german":"This obi has a fine weave and is very elegant.","furigana":"おび|お|こま|じょうひん"},{"japanese":"西陣織は京都を代表する伝統工芸だ。","romaji":"Nishijin-ori wa Kyōto o daihyō suru dentō kōgei da.","german":"Nishijin-ori is a traditional craft representing Kyoto.","furigana":"にしじんおり|きょうと|だいひょう|でんとうこうげい"},{"japanese":"祖母が手織りの布でかばんを作ってくれた。","romaji":"Sobo ga teori no nuno de kaban o tsukutte kureta.","german":"My grandmother made me a bag from hand-woven cloth.","furigana":"そぼ|てお|ぬの|つく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The reading かたぎ appears almost only as a suffix after a noun and refers to the typical mentality of a group or an era: 職人気質, 昔気質, 江戸っ子気質. The card 気質/きしつ is the neutral word, also used in psychology, for the temperament of an individual (気質が穏やか); 職人気質 is sometimes also read with きしつ. Do not confuse it with 堅気 \"respectable, solid (not in the underworld)\", which is also read かたぎ.","examples":[{"japanese":"祖父は昔気質の大工で、手抜き仕事を何より嫌った。","romaji":"Sofu wa mukashi katagi no daiku de, tenuki shigoto o nani yori kiratta.","german":"My grandfather was a carpenter of the old school and hated sloppy work above all.","furigana":"そふ|むかしかたぎ|だいく|てぬ|しごと|なに|きら"},{"japanese":"職人気質の彼は、納得がいくまで何度でも作り直す。","romaji":"Shokunin katagi no kare wa, nattoku ga iku made nando demo tsukurinaosu.","german":"With his craftsman's temperament, he remakes things as many times as it takes until he is satisfied.","furigana":"しょくにんかたぎ|かれ|なっとく|なんど|つく|なお"},{"japanese":"江戸っ子気質の店主は、気前がよくて口が悪い。","romaji":"Edokko katagi no tenshu wa, kimae ga yokute kuchi ga warui.","german":"The shop owner has the typical Edo temperament: generous, but sharp-tongued.","furigana":"えど|こかたぎ|てんしゅ|きまえ|くち|わる"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"金棒 \"iron rod, iron club\" is known mainly as the weapon of the oni and from the saying 鬼に金棒 \"like an oni with an iron club\", i.e. \"even stronger, practically unbeatable\". The rare spelling 鉄棒 is listed here as an alias; read normally, however, 鉄棒/てつぼう means \"horizontal bar\" (separate card). For an ordinary crowbar, バール is more commonly said today.","examples":[{"japanese":"経験豊富な彼がチームに加われば、まさに鬼に金棒だ。","romaji":"Keiken hōfu na kare ga chīmu ni kuwawareba, masa ni oni ni kanabō da.","german":"If he, with all his experience, joins the team, it will be like giving an ogre an iron club.","furigana":"けいけんほうふ|かれ|くわ|おに|かなぼう"},{"japanese":"節分の絵本には、金棒を持った赤鬼が描かれている。","romaji":"Setsubun no ehon ni wa, kanabō o motta akaoni ga egakarete iru.","german":"In the Setsubun picture book, a red oni holding an iron club is drawn.","furigana":"せつぶん|えほん|かなぼう|も|あかおに|えが"},{"japanese":"昔は夜回りが金棒を突きながら町を歩いた。","romaji":"Mukashi wa yomawari ga kanabō o tsukinagara machi o aruita.","german":"In the past, night watchmen walked through the town striking an iron rod on the ground.","furigana":"むかし|よまわ|かなぼう|つ|まち|ある"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Onomatopoeic word for a strong, broad physique or a heavy, solid construction; mostly as がっしりした + noun (がっしりした体格) or がっしりしている. With と as an adverb it describes a firm grip (がっしりと握る). The similar がっちり also means \"robust, firm\", but can additionally mean \"shrewd in business, calculating\"; がっしり emphasizes more the massive, stable build.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼はがっしりした体つきをしている。","romaji":"Kare wa gasshiri shita karadatsuki o shite iru.","german":"He has a sturdy build.","furigana":"かれ|からだ"},{"japanese":"この机は古いが、がっしりしていて丈夫だ。","romaji":"Kono tsukue wa furui ga, gasshiri shite ite jōbu da.","german":"This desk is old, but it is solidly built and durable.","furigana":"つくえ|ふる|じょうぶ"},{"japanese":"父は私の手をがっしりと握った。","romaji":"Chichi wa watashi no te o gasshiri to nigitta.","german":"My father gripped my hand firmly.","furigana":"ちち|わたし|て|にぎ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A written-language word, rare today, for \"past happenings, old stories\". The reading くじ is the older one and is known mainly from the title of the historical work 先代旧事本紀 (せんだいくじほんぎ); in modern texts 旧事 is mostly read きゅうじ. Do not confuse it with くじ \"lot, lottery\" (籤); in everyday speech 昔のこと or 過去の出来事 is said instead.","examples":[{"japanese":"『先代旧事本紀』は平安時代初期に成立したとされる史書だ。","romaji":"\"Sendai Kuji Hongi\" wa Heian jidai shoki ni seiritsu shita to sareru shisho da.","german":"The \"Sendai Kuji Hongi\" is a historical work said to have been compiled in the early Heian period.","furigana":"せんだいくじほんぎ|へいあんじだいしょき|せいりつ|ししょ"},{"japanese":"祖父は村の旧事を懐かしそうに語った。","romaji":"Sofu wa mura no kuji o natsukashisō ni katatta.","german":"My grandfather talked nostalgically about the old events of the village.","furigana":"そふ|むら|くじ|なつ|かた"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Onomatopoeic word for something robust and firmly put together: がっちりした体格 \"strong build\", がっちり握手する \"to shake hands firmly\", がっちり守る \"to defend tightly\". Unlike がっしり, it additionally has the colloquial meaning \"shrewd in business, calculating\" (がっちり稼ぐ \"to make good money\", がっちりした人 \"a calculating person\"). Often also written in katakana ガッチリ.","examples":[{"japanese":"ラグビー選手らしいがっちりした体格だ。","romaji":"Ragubī senshu rashii gacchiri shita taikaku da.","german":"He has a sturdy build, just as you'd expect of a rugby player.","furigana":"せんしゅ|たいかく"},{"japanese":"二人はがっちりと握手を交わした。","romaji":"Futari wa gacchiri to akushu o kawashita.","german":"The two exchanged a firm handshake.","furigana":"ふたり|あくしゅ|か"},{"japanese":"その会社は年末商戦でがっちり稼いだ。","romaji":"Sono kaisha wa nenmatsu shōsen de gacchiri kaseida.","german":"The company made a good profit in the year-end sales season.","furigana":"かいしゃ|ねんまつしょうせん|かせ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Calendar term: the first of the ten celestial stems (十干), derived from 木の兄 \"elder brother of wood\". It appears mainly together with the twelve earthly branches as the name of a year in the sixty-year cycle, e.g. 甲子 (きのえね) or 甲辰 (きのえたつ). The same character is read こう when it means \"first rank, party A (in a contract)\" or \"shell, carapace\" (甲乙, 甲羅); the reading きのえ is purely calendrical.","examples":[{"japanese":"甲は十干の一番目で、「木の兄」を意味する。","romaji":"Kinoe wa jikkan no ichiban-me de, \"ki no e\" o imi suru.","german":"Kinoe is the first of the ten stems and means \"elder brother of wood\".","furigana":"きのえ|じっかん|いちばんめ|き||いみ"},{"japanese":"甲子園球場は、完成した年が甲子だったことから名付けられた。","romaji":"Kōshien kyūjō wa, kansei shita toshi ga kinoene datta koto kara nazukerareta.","german":"Koshien Stadium was named after the fact that the year it was completed was a Kinoe-Ne year.","furigana":"こうしえんきゅうじょう|かんせい|とし||なづ"},{"japanese":"2024年の干支は甲辰だった。","romaji":"Nisen nijūyo-nen no eto wa kinoe-tatsu datta.","german":"The sexagenary-cycle year 2024 was Kinoe-Tatsu (Wood Dragon).","furigana":"ねん||"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Rarely used on its own; mainly the basic component of 共和国 \"republic\", 共和制 \"republican form of government\" and 共和党 \"Republican Party\". The second meaning \"to work together in harmony\" (共和する) is written-language and appears mainly in historical slogans such as 五族共和. The opposite of 共和制 is 君主制 \"monarchy\".","examples":[{"japanese":"日本は君主制だが、フランスは共和制をとっている。","romaji":"Nihon wa kunshusei da ga, Furansu wa kyōwasei o totte iru.","german":"Japan is a monarchy, but France has a republican system.","furigana":"にほん|くんしゅせい|きょうわせい"},{"japanese":"アメリカの二大政党は民主党と共和党だ。","romaji":"Amerika no nidai seitō wa minshutō to kyōwatō da.","german":"The two major parties in the United States are the Democratic Party and the Republican Party.","furigana":"にだいせいとう|みんしゅとう|きょうわとう"},{"japanese":"異なる民族が共和して新しい国を築いた。","romaji":"Kotonaru minzoku ga kyōwa shite atarashii kuni o kizuita.","german":"Different peoples worked together in harmony and built a new nation.","furigana":"こと|みんぞく|きょうわ|あたら|くに|きず"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Native reading of 際: \"edge, outermost limit\" (崖の際 \"edge of a cliff\") or \"the moment immediately before or at something\". As a suffix it becomes voiced ぎわ: 窓際 \"by the window\", 瀬戸際 \"at the critical juncture\", 帰り際 \"as one is leaving\"; likewise in 際どい \"risky, close\" and 際立つ \"to stand out\". The card 際/さい \"occasion; on the occasion of\" is a different word and stands after clauses (その際, 出発の際); きわ, by contrast, denotes a spatial or temporal boundary and is rather rare on its own.","examples":[{"japanese":"崖の際に立つと足がすくんだ。","romaji":"Gake no kiwa ni tatsu to ashi ga sukunda.","german":"Standing at the edge of the cliff, my legs froze.","furigana":"がけ|きわ|た|あし"},{"japanese":"窓際の席に座って外を眺めた。","romaji":"Madogiwa no seki ni suwatte soto o nagameta.","german":"I sat in a seat by the window and looked outside.","furigana":"まどぎわ|せき|すわ|そと|なが"},{"japanese":"帰り際に、彼女は小さな声で「ありがとう」と言った。","romaji":"Kaerigiwa ni, kanojo wa chiisana koe de \"arigatō\" to itta.","german":"As she was leaving, she said \"thank you\" in a small voice.","furigana":"かえ|ぎわ|かのじょ|ちい|こえ|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Counting suffix that, after a number, denotes the best or most outstanding members of a group: 三傑 \"the three greats\", 十傑 (じっけつ) \"the top ten\". It occurs almost only in fixed combinations and in rankings from sports and history; in everyday speech ベストテン or トップテン is more usual. The same character is in 傑作 \"masterpiece\" and 豪傑 \"hero\".","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は今季の打撃十傑に入った。","romaji":"Kare wa konki no dageki jikketsu ni haitta.","german":"He made it into this season's top ten batters.","furigana":"かれ|こんき|だげきじっけつ|はい"},{"japanese":"維新の三傑とは西郷隆盛、大久保利通、木戸孝允のことだ。","romaji":"Ishin no sanketsu to wa Saigō Takamori, Ōkubo Toshimichi, Kido Takayoshi no koto da.","german":"The \"Three Great Men of the Restoration\" refers to Saigo Takamori, Okubo Toshimichi and Kido Takayoshi.","furigana":"いしん|さんけつ|||"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Prefix meaning \"original, primordial, raw\": 原材料 \"raw materials\", 原作 \"original work\", 原文 \"original text\", 原日本人 \"proto-Japanese\". Do not confuse it with the identically sounding prefix 現〜 \"current\" (現会長 \"the current chairman\"): 原〜 points to the origin, 現〜 to the present state. Read on its own, 原 means はら \"field, plain\" (separate card).","examples":[{"japanese":"原材料の値上がりで、商品の価格も上がった。","romaji":"Genzairyō no neagari de, shōhin no kakaku mo agatta.","german":"Because of rising raw material prices, the prices of goods have also gone up.","furigana":"げんざいりょう|ねあ|しょうひん|かかく|あ"},{"japanese":"翻訳ではなく、原作を読んでみたい。","romaji":"Hon'yaku de wa naku, gensaku o yonde mitai.","german":"I want to read the original work, not a translation.","furigana":"ほんやく|げんさく|よ"},{"japanese":"原日本人の起源については諸説がある。","romaji":"Gen-Nihonjin no kigen ni tsuite wa shosetsu ga aru.","german":"There are various theories about the origin of the proto-Japanese.","furigana":"げんにほんじん|きげん|しょせつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Formal prefix before the name or title of a deceased person, \"the late\": 故田中氏, 故山田一郎先生. Typical of obituaries, news, dedications and funeral speeches; in conversation 亡くなった田中さん is more usual. Related are 故人 \"the deceased\" (separate card) and 故郷 \"hometown\", where 故 means \"old, former\".","examples":[{"japanese":"故田中氏の葬儀は来週行われる。","romaji":"Ko-Tanaka-shi no sōgi wa raishū okonawareru.","german":"The funeral of the late Mr. Tanaka will be held next week.","furigana":"こたなかし|そうぎ|らいしゅうおこな"},{"japanese":"会場には故ジョン・レノンの曲が流れていた。","romaji":"Kaijō ni wa ko-Jon Renon no kyoku ga nagarete ita.","german":"A song by the late John Lennon was playing in the venue.","furigana":"かいじょう|こ|きょく|なが"},{"japanese":"本書を故恩師に捧げる。","romaji":"Honsho o ko-onshi ni sasageru.","german":"I dedicate this book to my late mentor.","furigana":"ほんしょ|こおんし|ささ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Intransitive verb \"to congeal, to gel, to solidify\", especially of liquids such as broth, fat or blood that become solid when cooled. Mostly written in kana; known from 煮凝り (にこごり) \"aspic of fish or meat stock\". Despite the same spelling, it is a different verb from 凝る/こる \"to be stiff; to be absorbed in something\" (肩が凝る). In everyday speech 固まる is mostly said instead of こごる.","examples":[{"japanese":"冷蔵庫に入れておいたら、煮魚の汁がこごった。","romaji":"Reizōko ni irete oitara, nizakana no shiru ga kogotta.","german":"When I put it in the refrigerator, the sauce of the simmered fish set into jelly.","furigana":"れいぞうこ|い|にざかな|しる"},{"japanese":"寒さで池の水がこごり始めた。","romaji":"Samusa de ike no mizu ga kogori hajimeta.","german":"The pond water began to freeze over from the cold.","furigana":"さむ|いけ|みず|はじ"},{"japanese":"煮こごりは魚の煮汁を冷やして固めた料理だ。","romaji":"Nikogori wa sakana no nijiru o hiyashite katameta ryōri da.","german":"Nikogori is a dish made by cooling and setting fish simmering liquid.","furigana":"に|さかな|にじる|ひ|かた|りょうり"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Sino-Japanese reading of 濠/壕 \"ditch\": 濠 refers more to a water-filled castle moat, 壕 to a dug-out trench or shelter, e.g. 塹壕 \"trench\" and 防空壕 \"air-raid shelter\". Standing alone, 濠 is mostly read ほり (card 堀/ほり); ごう appears mainly in compounds such as 環濠 and in texts about war and fortification. Do not confuse it with 業/ごう \"karma\" or 号/ごう \"number\".","examples":[{"japanese":"空襲警報が鳴ると、人々は防空壕に逃げ込んだ。","romaji":"Kūshū keihō ga naru to, hitobito wa bōkūgō ni nigekonda.","german":"When the air-raid siren sounded, people fled into the air-raid shelter.","furigana":"くうしゅうけいほう|な|ひとびと|ぼうくうごう|に|こ"},{"japanese":"兵士たちは壕に身を隠した。","romaji":"Heishitachi wa gō ni mi o kakushita.","german":"The soldiers hid in the trench.","furigana":"へいし|ごう|み|かく"},{"japanese":"弥生時代には、周りを濠で囲んだ環濠集落が作られた。","romaji":"Yayoi jidai ni wa, mawari o gō de kakonda kangō shūraku ga tsukurareta.","german":"In the Yayoi period, moated settlements surrounded by a ditch were built.","furigana":"やよいじだい|まわ|ごう|かこ|かんごうしゅうらく|つく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Colloquial, somewhat old-fashioned na-adjective for \"magnificent, tremendous, splendid\", often admiring or slightly ironic about generous, lavish deeds (豪儀な話, 豪儀なものだ). Written 豪儀, 豪気 or 強気; the spelling 強気 is easily confused with 強気/つよき \"confident, bold\" (separate card), which is a different word. It shares only the sound with 合議/ごうぎ \"consultation\".","examples":[{"japanese":"一晩で百万円使うとは、ずいぶん豪儀な話だ。","romaji":"Hitoban de hyakuman-en tsukau to wa, zuibun gōgi na hanashi da.","german":"Spending a million yen in one evening is quite a tremendous thing.","furigana":"ひとばん|ひゃくまんえんつか|ごうぎ|はなし"},{"japanese":"社員全員にボーナスを倍出すとは、社長も豪儀だね。","romaji":"Shain zen'in ni bōnasu o bai dasu to wa, shachō mo gōgi da ne.","german":"Paying all employees a double bonus - the president really is generous, isn't he?","furigana":"しゃいんぜんいん|ばいだ|しゃちょう|ごうぎ"},{"japanese":"豪気な山車が町を練り歩いた。","romaji":"Gōgi na dashi ga machi o neriaruita.","german":"Magnificent festival floats paraded through the town.","furigana":"ごうぎ|だし|まち|ね|ある"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Elevated, written-language word for a literary or artistic \"work\"; in everyday speech 作品 is said. Same spelling as 作物/さくもつ \"crops\", but a separate word - which reading is meant follows from the context (literature and art versus agriculture). Typical of literary criticism and older texts, often with a possessor such as 彼の作物.","examples":[{"japanese":"この短編は、作者が二十代で書いた初期の作物である。","romaji":"Kono tanpen wa, sakusha ga nijūdai de kaita shoki no sakubutsu de aru.","german":"This short story is an early work the author wrote in his twenties.","furigana":"たんぺん|さくしゃ|にじゅうだい|か|しょき|さくぶつ"},{"japanese":"批評家たちは彼の作物を厳しく評価した。","romaji":"Hihyōka-tachi wa kare no sakubutsu o kibishiku hyōka shita.","german":"Critics judged his works harshly.","furigana":"ひひょうか|かれ|さくぶつ|きび|ひょうか"},{"japanese":"芸術家の作物には、その時代の空気が映し出されている。","romaji":"Geijutsuka no sakubutsu ni wa, sono jidai no kūki ga utsushidasarete iru.","german":"The atmosphere of an era is reflected in an artist's works.","furigana":"げいじゅつか|さくぶつ|じだい|くうき|うつ|だ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Na-adjective, mostly adverbial as 早急に \"immediately\" or attributive in 早急な対策/早急な対応. The traditional reading is さっきゅう; そうきゅう is by now just as widespread and generally accepted - it is the same word. Typical of business language, government offices and news; related are 至急 \"urgent, immediate\" and 緊急 \"emergency, urgent\", which stress haste and an emergency respectively.","examples":[{"japanese":"ご迷惑をおかけしました。早急に対応いたします。","romaji":"Gomeiwaku o okake shimashita. Sakkyū ni taiō itashimasu.","german":"We apologize for the inconvenience. We will respond immediately.","furigana":"めいわく|さっきゅう|たいおう"},{"japanese":"事故の多いこの交差点には、早急な対策が必要だ。","romaji":"Jiko no ōi kono kōsaten ni wa, sakkyū na taisaku ga hitsuyō da.","german":"This accident-prone intersection needs urgent countermeasures.","furigana":"じこ|おお|こうさてん|さっきゅう|たいさく|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"書類に不備があったので、早急に再提出してください。","romaji":"Shorui ni fubi ga atta node, sakkyū ni saiteishutsu shite kudasai.","german":"Your documents were incomplete, so please resubmit them as soon as possible.","furigana":"しょるい|ふび|さっきゅう|さいていしゅつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A rarer alternative reading of 雑木 with the same meaning as the card 雑木/ぞうき: mixed small trees and bushes of little economic value. ぞうき is usual, especially in fixed combinations such as 雑木林/ぞうきばやし; ざつぼく is heard only occasionally. When in doubt, use ぞうき, but be able to recognize ざつぼく when you hear it.","examples":[{"japanese":"空き地には雑木が生い茂っていた。","romaji":"Akichi ni wa zatsuboku ga oishigette ita.","german":"Brushwood was growing thick on the vacant lot.","furigana":"あ|ち|ざつぼく||しげ"},{"japanese":"冬の間に、裏山の雑木を切って薪にした。","romaji":"Fuyu no aida ni, urayama no zatsuboku o kitte maki ni shita.","german":"During the winter, we cut the trees on the hill behind the house and made firewood from them.","furigana":"ふゆ|あいだ|うらやま|ざつぼく|き|まき"},{"japanese":"杉の植林地に雑木が混じって生えている。","romaji":"Sugi no shokurinchi ni zatsuboku ga majitte haete iru.","german":"Assorted other trees are growing mixed in with the cedar plantation.","furigana":"すぎ|しょくりんち|ざつぼく|ま|は"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A separate word alongside 躾ける (card: \"to train, to teach manners\"), which today is mostly written in kana or as 躾ける. 仕付ける carries older meanings: \"to be accustomed to something\" (almost only negated: 仕付けない仕事 \"unaccustomed work\"), \"to baste with large stitches\" in sewing (cf. しつけ糸 \"basting thread\") and \"to plant rice seedlings\". The sewing term is the one that is still alive; the rest sounds archaic.","examples":[{"japanese":"本縫いの前に、しつけ糸で袖を仕付けておく。","romaji":"Honnui no mae ni, shitsukeito de sode o shitsukete oku.","german":"Before the final sewing, you baste the sleeve with basting thread.","furigana":"ほんぬ|まえ|いと|そで|しつ"},{"japanese":"仕付けない仕事で、すっかり疲れてしまった。","romaji":"Shitsukenai shigoto de, sukkari tsukarete shimatta.","german":"I'm completely exhausted from work I'm not used to.","furigana":"しつ|しごと|つか"},{"japanese":"村では五月になると、田に苗を仕付ける。","romaji":"Mura de wa gogatsu ni naru to, ta ni nae o shitsukeru.","german":"In May, the village plants rice seedlings in the paddies.","furigana":"むら|ごがつ|た|なえ|しつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The original reading of 消耗, since 耗 actually has the on reading こう; today the customary reading しょうもう (card 消耗/しょうもう) is the generally used one, in news, in everyday speech and in words such as 消耗品. しょうこう appears only in older texts or with deliberately traditional pronunciation. Meaning and usage are identical, as a noun and as a suru verb (体力を消耗する).","examples":[{"japanese":"長引く戦争で、兵力の消耗が激しかった。","romaji":"Nagabiku sensō de, heiryoku no shōkō ga hageshikatta.","german":"Because of the protracted war, the attrition of troop strength was severe.","furigana":"ながび|せんそう|へいりょく|しょうこう|はげ"},{"japanese":"猛暑の中の作業で、体力を著しく消耗した。","romaji":"Mōsho no naka no sagyō de, tairyoku o ichijirushiku shōkō shita.","german":"Working in the intense heat, I used up a great deal of my stamina.","furigana":"もうしょ|なか|さぎょう|たいりょく|いちじる|しょうこう"},{"japanese":"機械の部品は、使ううちに少しずつ消耗する。","romaji":"Kikai no buhin wa, tsukau uchi ni sukoshi zutsu shōkō suru.","german":"Machine parts wear out little by little as they are used.","furigana":"きかい|ぶひん|つか|すこ|しょうこう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Technical word from administration and environmental engineering for human excretions, feces and urine together. Since 屎 is not a jōyō kanji, officially it is almost always written し尿, e.g. in し尿処理 \"human waste disposal\" or し尿処理場. In everyday speech the word is avoided; medically, 便 and 尿 are spoken of separately.","examples":[{"japanese":"この施設では、地域のし尿を処理している。","romaji":"Kono shisetsu de wa, chiiki no shinyō o shori shite iru.","german":"This facility treats the human waste of the region.","furigana":"しせつ|ちいき|にょう|しょり"},{"japanese":"昔は屎尿を肥料として畑にまいていた。","romaji":"Mukashi wa shinyō o hiryō to shite hatake ni maite ita.","german":"In the past, human waste was spread on the fields as fertilizer.","furigana":"むかし|しにょう|ひりょう|はたけ"},{"japanese":"災害時には、避難所のし尿処理が深刻な問題になる。","romaji":"Saigaiji ni wa, hinanjo no shinyō shori ga shinkoku na mondai ni naru.","german":"In a disaster, the disposal of human waste at evacuation shelters becomes a serious problem.","furigana":"さいがいじ|ひなんじょ|にょうしょり|しんこく|もんだい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Literary word for \"servant, menial\", today almost always written in kana (しもべ); the spelling 僕 is rare and is usually read ぼく \"I\" (card 僕/ぼく). Typical in Bible translations (神のしもべ), fairy tales and fantasy, and figuratively as in 金のしもべ \"slave to money\". In modern usage, 召使い or 使用人 correspond to it.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は生涯、王の忠実なしもべとして仕えた。","romaji":"Kare wa shōgai, ō no chūjitsu na shimobe to shite tsukaeta.","german":"All his life he served the king as a faithful servant.","furigana":"かれ|しょうがい|おう|ちゅうじつ|つか"},{"japanese":"聖書では、信者が自らを神のしもべと呼ぶ。","romaji":"Seisho de wa, shinja ga mizukara o kami no shimobe to yobu.","german":"In the Bible, believers call themselves servants of God.","furigana":"せいしょ|しんじゃ|みずか|かみ|よ"},{"japanese":"お金のしもべになってはいけない。","romaji":"Okane no shimobe ni natte wa ikenai.","german":"You must not become a slave to money.","furigana":"かね"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A rare na-adjective of elevated written language for a character that is free of pettiness and open, e.g. 洒落な人柄. Same spelling as 洒落/しゃれ \"pun, joke\" (card), but a separate word; without a reading aid, 洒落 is almost always read しゃれ. Close in meaning are 洒脱 \"smart, unconstrained\" and 磊落 \"magnanimous, easygoing\".","examples":[{"japanese":"祖父は物事にこだわらない洒落な人だった。","romaji":"Sofu wa monogoto ni kodawaranai sharaku na hito datta.","german":"My grandfather was an easygoing person who didn't fuss over things.","furigana":"そふ|ものごと|しゃらく|ひと"},{"japanese":"彼の洒落な人柄は、多くの友人を引きつけた。","romaji":"Kare no sharaku na hitogara wa, ōku no yūjin o hikitsuketa.","german":"His free and easy character attracted many friends.","furigana":"かれ|しゃらく|ひとがら|おお|ゆうじん|ひ"},{"japanese":"晩年の作品には、洒落な境地がうかがえる。","romaji":"Bannen no sakuhin ni wa, sharaku na kyōchi ga ukagaeru.","german":"In his late works, a carefree state of mind can be detected.","furigana":"ばんねん|さくひん|しゃらく|きょうち"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Sino-Japanese, written-language reading of 所々; in normal usage 所々 is almost always read ところどころ (card 所々/ところどころ). In the reading しょしょ the word is usually written 諸所 or 諸処 and sounds formal or old-fashioned, e.g. in reports and older literature. Usage as with ところどころ: adverbially or with に (所々に).","examples":[{"japanese":"古い町並みには、所々に江戸時代の建物が残っている。","romaji":"Furui machinami ni wa, shosho ni Edo jidai no tatemono ga nokotte iru.","german":"In the old streetscape, buildings from the Edo period remain here and there.","furigana":"ふる|まちな|しょしょ|えどじだい|たてもの|のこ"},{"japanese":"報告書には所々誤りが見られる。","romaji":"Hōkokusho ni wa shosho ayamari ga mirareru.","german":"Errors can be seen here and there in the report.","furigana":"ほうこくしょ|しょしょあやま|み"},{"japanese":"山腹の所々に、まだ雪が残っていた。","romaji":"Sanpuku no shosho ni, mada yuki ga nokotte ita.","german":"Snow still remained in places on the mountainside.","furigana":"さんぷく|しょしょ|ゆき|のこ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Originally military: the \"rear guard\" that covers a retreat (殿を務める \"to form the rear guard\"). Figuratively it means \"the last in a line\", or in relays the anchor runner (≈ アンカー). Mostly written in kana, because 殿 would otherwise be read どの (card 〜殿, form of address in letters) or との \"lord\"; the opposite is 先頭 \"the head, the front\".","examples":[{"japanese":"退却の際、若い武将が殿を務めた。","romaji":"Taikyaku no sai, wakai bushō ga shingari o tsutometa.","german":"During the retreat, a young commander served as the rear guard.","furigana":"たいきゃく|さい|わか|ぶしょう|しんがり|つと"},{"japanese":"山道では、経験の多いガイドがしんがりを歩く。","romaji":"Yamamichi de wa, keiken no ōi gaido ga shingari o aruku.","german":"On mountain paths, an experienced guide walks at the rear.","furigana":"やまみち|けいけん|おお|ある"},{"japanese":"リレーのしんがりを任されて、プレッシャーを感じた。","romaji":"Rirē no shingari o makasarete, puresshā o kanjita.","german":"I was entrusted with the anchor leg of the relay and felt the pressure.","furigana":"まか|かん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mainly a suffix after quantities, meaning \"a little less than, just under\": 1時間弱 \"just under an hour\", 100人弱. The counterpart is 強/きょう \"a little more than\" (card). In the Japanese earthquake scale, 弱 denotes the lower level (震度5弱, 6弱), and on devices the weak setting (弱・中・強). In this usage it is always read じゃく, not よわ-.","examples":[{"japanese":"駅から家まで歩いて二十分弱かかる。","romaji":"Eki kara ie made aruite nijuppun jaku kakaru.","german":"It takes just under twenty minutes on foot from the station to my house.","furigana":"えき|いえ|ある|にじゅっぷんじゃく"},{"japanese":"昨夜、この地域で震度5弱の地震があった。","romaji":"Sakuya, kono chiiki de shindo go-jaku no jishin ga atta.","german":"Last night, an earthquake of intensity 5-lower occurred in this area.","furigana":"さくや|ちいき|しんど|じゃく|じしん"},{"japanese":"寝るときは、エアコンを弱にしておく。","romaji":"Neru toki wa, eakon o jaku ni shite oku.","german":"When I sleep, I set the air conditioner to low.","furigana":"ね|じゃく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Rarely appears alone; as an independent noun mostly in enumerations such as 食と住 or in reference to 衣食住 \"clothing, food, shelter\" (card). More often it is part of words such as 住環境 \"living environment\", 住宅 or 住居. For a concrete dwelling, 住まい or 家 is said in everyday speech.","examples":[{"japanese":"衣食住のうち、住にかかる費用が最も大きい。","romaji":"Ishokujū no uchi, jū ni kakaru hiyō ga mottomo ōkii.","german":"Of clothing, food and housing, the cost of housing is the largest.","furigana":"いしょくじゅう|じゅう|ひよう|もっと|おお"},{"japanese":"この団体は、高齢者の住を支える活動をしている。","romaji":"Kono dantai wa, kōreisha no jū o sasaeru katsudō o shite iru.","german":"This organization works to support housing for the elderly.","furigana":"だんたい|こうれいしゃ|じゅう|ささ|かつどう"},{"japanese":"子育てには、安全な住環境が欠かせない。","romaji":"Kosodate ni wa, anzen na jūkankyō ga kakasenai.","german":"A safe living environment is indispensable for raising children.","furigana":"こそだ|あんぜん|じゅうかんきょう|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"In the meaning \"precious treasure, valuable heirloom\", 重宝 is read じゅうほう; in the far more common meaning \"handy, useful; to use gladly\", on the other hand, ちょうほう (card 重宝/ちょうほう, e.g. 重宝する). じゅうほう is written-language and occurs mainly with family heirlooms, temple treasures and works of art. Close in meaning are 宝物 and 家宝.","examples":[{"japanese":"この刀は家の重宝として代々受け継がれてきた。","romaji":"Kono katana wa ie no jūhō to shite daidai uketsugarete kita.","german":"This sword has been handed down through the generations as a family treasure.","furigana":"かたな|いえ|じゅうほう|だいだいう|つ"},{"japanese":"寺に伝わる重宝が、初めて一般に公開された。","romaji":"Tera ni tsutawaru jūhō ga, hajimete ippan ni kōkai sareta.","german":"Treasures preserved at the temple were shown to the general public for the first time.","furigana":"てら|つた|じゅうほう|はじ|いっぱん|こうかい"},{"japanese":"これは国の重宝ともいうべき名画だ。","romaji":"Kore wa kuni no jūhō to mo iu beki meiga da.","german":"This is a famous painting that could be called a national treasure.","furigana":"くに|じゅうほう|めいが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Prefix before job and role designations that marks a helping, subordinate position: 助監督 \"assistant director\", 助演 \"supporting role\", 助教 \"assistant professor\". To be distinguished from 副〜 \"vice-, deputy\" (副社長), which implies authority to act as a substitute. 助 does not occur as an independent word; cf. also 助手 \"assistant\" (card).","examples":[{"japanese":"兄は映画の助監督として撮影現場で働いている。","romaji":"Ani wa eiga no jokantoku to shite satsuei genba de hataraite iru.","german":"My older brother works on a film set as an assistant director.","furigana":"あに|えいが|じょかんとく|さつえいげんば|はたら"},{"japanese":"彼女はこの映画で助演女優賞を受賞した。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kono eiga de joen joyūshō o jushō shita.","german":"She won the best supporting actress award for this film.","furigana":"かのじょ|えいが|じょえんじょゆうしょう|じゅしょう"},{"japanese":"大学院を出て、地方の大学で助教になった。","romaji":"Daigakuin o dete, chihō no daigaku de jokyō ni natta.","german":"After graduate school, he became an assistant professor at a regional university.","furigana":"だいがくいん|で|ちほう|だいがく|じょきょう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"As an independent word, it appears today only in technical contexts: in Noh, 尉 denotes the role or mask of the old man (counterpart 姥 \"old woman\"), and also the white ash on glowing charcoal. The officer ranks 大尉, 中尉, 少尉 and the collective term 尉官 contain the same kanji, but are read with い and are not this word. Do not confuse it with the identically sounding cards 上, 状, 畳 and 情.","examples":[{"japanese":"能「高砂」では、尉と姥が松の精として現れる。","romaji":"Nō \"Takasago\" de wa, jō to uba ga matsu no sei to shite arawareru.","german":"In the Noh play \"Takasago\", the old man and the old woman appear as spirits of the pines.","furigana":"のう||じょう||まつ|せい|あらわ"},{"japanese":"火鉢の炭がすっかり白い尉に覆われていた。","romaji":"Hibachi no sumi ga sukkari shiroi jō ni ōwarete ita.","german":"The charcoal in the brazier was completely covered with white ash.","furigana":"ひばち|すみ|しろ|じょう|おお"},{"japanese":"律令制の衛府では、尉は四等官の三番目の位だった。","romaji":"Ritsuryōsei no efu de wa, jō wa shitōkan no sanbanme no kurai datta.","german":"In the guard offices of the ritsuryo system, the jo was the third of the four official ranks.","furigana":"|えふ|じょう|しとうかん|さんばんめ|くらい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Rare on its own; mostly a suffix: after names (山田嬢 \"Miss Yamada\", formal and old-fashioned) or in job titles such as 受付嬢 \"receptionist\", 案内嬢 \"hostess\", which are often regarded as old-fashioned today (more neutral: 受付係). The polite form of address for someone else's daughter or a young lady is お嬢さん (separate card), and familiarly also 嬢ちゃん. Do not confuse it with the homophones 上, 状, 畳, 情.","examples":[{"japanese":"受付嬢に会議室の場所を尋ねた。","romaji":"Uketsukejō ni kaigishitsu no basho o tazuneta.","german":"I asked the receptionist where the meeting room was.","furigana":"うけつけじょう|かいぎしつ|ばしょ|たず"},{"japanese":"新聞に山田嬢の婚約が発表された。","romaji":"Shinbun ni Yamada-jō no kon'yaku ga happyō sareta.","german":"The engagement of Miss Yamada was announced in the newspaper.","furigana":"しんぶん|やまだじょう|こんやく|はっぴょう"},{"japanese":"お隣の嬢ちゃんは今年で五歳になる。","romaji":"Otonari no jōchan wa kotoshi de gosai ni naru.","german":"The little girl next door turns five this year.","furigana":"となり|じょう|ことし|ごさい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Sino-Japanese, written-language reading of 人目; in everyday speech and in fixed phrases such as 人目を引く or 人目を気にする, it is almost always read ひとめ (separate card). じんもく appears mainly in elevated phrases such as 人目を驚かす \"to cause a stir\" or 人目を聳動する \"to shake the public\". Without a reading aid, ひとめ is the standard reading.","examples":[{"japanese":"その奇抜な建物は人目を驚かせた。","romaji":"Sono kibatsu na tatemono wa jinmoku o odorokaseta.","german":"That eccentric building astonished the public.","furigana":"きばつ|たてもの|じんもく|おどろ"},{"japanese":"人目を聳動する大事件が起きた。","romaji":"Jinmoku o shōdō suru daijiken ga okita.","german":"A major incident occurred that shook the public.","furigana":"じんもく||だいじけん|お"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Elevated or technical reading of 水気; in everyday speech (vegetables, laundry, cooking), みずけ (separate card) is said, e.g. 水気を切る \"to drain\". すいき is used rather in written language for \"water vapor, mist\" and in traditional (Kampo) medicine for \"edema, dropsy\". When in doubt, みずけ is the usual reading.","examples":[{"japanese":"早朝の湖面から白い水気が立ちのぼっていた。","romaji":"Sōchō no komen kara shiroi suiki ga tachinobotte ita.","german":"White vapor was rising from the lake surface in the early morning.","furigana":"そうちょう|こめん|しろ|すいき|た"},{"japanese":"漢方医学では、体のむくみを水気という。","romaji":"Kanpō igaku de wa, karada no mukumi o suiki to iu.","german":"In Kampo medicine, swelling of the body is called \"suiki\" (edema).","furigana":"かんぽういがく|からだ|すいき"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Onomatopoeic adverb, mostly with と or directly before the verb: ずるずる引きずる \"to drag along\", ずるずる滑り落ちる \"to slide down slowly\", ずるずる延びる \"to drag on endlessly\". Figuratively it describes a state that drags on without a clear decision (ずるずるとした関係), and also the slurping of noodles. In manga and advertising often in katakana ズルズル.","examples":[{"japanese":"重いスーツケースをずるずる引きずって駅まで歩いた。","romaji":"Omoi sūtsukēsu o zuruzuru hikizutte eki made aruita.","german":"I walked to the station dragging the heavy suitcase behind me.","furigana":"おも|ひ|えき|ある"},{"japanese":"返事をずるずると先延ばしにしてしまった。","romaji":"Henji o zuruzuru to sakinobashi ni shite shimatta.","german":"I kept putting off my reply.","furigana":"へんじ|さきの"},{"japanese":"父はそばをずるずるとすすった。","romaji":"Chichi wa soba o zuruzuru to susutta.","german":"My father slurped his soba noodles.","furigana":"ちち"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Base form of お世辞 (separate card), which is almost always used in present-day conversation. Without お, 世辞 appears mainly in written language and fixed phrases: 世辞がうまい \"to be good at flattery\", 世辞を言う \"to pay compliments\", 世辞抜きで \"flattery aside, honestly\". When speaking freely, it is better to say お世辞.","examples":[{"japanese":"あの営業マンは世辞がうまい。","romaji":"Ano eigyōman wa seji ga umai.","german":"That salesman is good at flattery.","furigana":"えいぎょう|せじ"},{"japanese":"心にもない世辞を言うのは苦手だ。","romaji":"Kokoro ni mo nai seji o iu no wa nigate da.","german":"I'm bad at paying compliments I don't mean.","furigana":"こころ|せじ|い|にがて"},{"japanese":"世辞抜きで、この料理は本当においしい。","romaji":"Sejinuki de, kono ryōri wa hontō ni oishii.","german":"Flattery aside, this dish is really delicious.","furigana":"せじぬ|りょうり|ほんとう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Sino-Japanese reading of 節, as opposed to ふし (separate card) \"knot, joint; melody\" as a concrete word. せつ appears in elevated phrases such as その節はお世話になりました \"thank you for your help back then\", for text sections (第二節 \"section 2\") and for principles in 節を曲げる \"to betray one's principles\". Do not confuse it with the homophone 説 \"theory\".","examples":[{"japanese":"その節は大変お世話になりました。","romaji":"Sono setsu wa taihen osewa ni narimashita.","german":"Thank you very much for your great help on that occasion.","furigana":"せつ|たいへん|せわ"},{"japanese":"来週までに第三章の第二節を読んでおいてください。","romaji":"Raishū made ni daisanshō no dainisetsu o yonde oite kudasai.","german":"Please read section 2 of chapter 3 by next week.","furigana":"らいしゅう|だいさんしょう|だいにせつ|よ"},{"japanese":"彼はどんな圧力を受けても節を曲げなかった。","romaji":"Kare wa donna atsuryoku o ukete mo setsu o magenakatta.","german":"No matter what pressure he was under, he did not compromise his principles.","furigana":"かれ|あつりょく|う|せつ|ま"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Written-language short form of 仙人 (separate card), the usual word for the Taoist mountain hermit. Rare on its own, but common in compounds: 歌仙 \"poet saint, master of poetry\", 詩仙 \"master of Chinese poetry\", 仙境 \"fairyland\". Do not confuse it with the homophones 千, 線, 栓 and 〜戦.","examples":[{"japanese":"古代中国では、仙になることを目指して山で修行する者がいた。","romaji":"Kodai Chūgoku de wa, sen ni naru koto o mezashite yama de shugyō suru mono ga ita.","german":"In ancient China, there were people who trained in the mountains aiming to become immortals.","furigana":"こだいちゅうごく|せん|めざ|やま|しゅぎょう|もの"},{"japanese":"柿本人麻呂は歌仙の一人に数えられる。","romaji":"Kakinomoto no Hitomaro wa kasen no hitori ni kazoerareru.","german":"Kakinomoto no Hitomaro is counted among the great masters of poetry.","furigana":"|かせん|ひとり|かぞ"},{"japanese":"俗世を離れた老人は、まるで仙のような暮らしをしていた。","romaji":"Zokuse o hanareta rōjin wa, marude sen no yō na kurashi o shite ita.","german":"The old man who had left the secular world lived just like a hermit-immortal.","furigana":"ぞくせ|はな|ろうじん|せん|く"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The te-form of 対する as a fixed phrase, almost always after に: 先生に対して \"toward the teacher\", 学生三人に対して \"per three students\", それに対して \"in contrast to that\". Before nouns, に対する or に対しての is used instead (客に対する態度). Not to be confused with the identically read adverb 大して \"not particularly\", which is used with negation.","examples":[{"japanese":"お客様に対して失礼なことを言ってはいけない。","romaji":"Okyakusama ni taishite shitsurei na koto o itte wa ikenai.","german":"You must not say rude things to customers.","furigana":"きゃくさま|たい|しつれい|い"},{"japanese":"このクラスでは学生三人に対して先生が一人いる。","romaji":"Kono kurasu de wa gakusei sannin ni taishite sensei ga hitori iru.","german":"In this class, there is one teacher for every three students.","furigana":"がくせいさんにん|たい|せんせい|ひとり"},{"japanese":"兄は背が高い。それに対して弟は小柄だ。","romaji":"Ani wa se ga takai. Sore ni taishite otōto wa kogara da.","german":"The older brother is tall. The younger one, by contrast, is small.","furigana":"あに|せ|たか|たい|おとうと|こがら"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N2"},
{"notes":"Mainly with の before nouns: 大部の書物 \"a voluminous work\", 大部の報告書 \"a thick report\". In the sense of \"the greater part\" it is written-language; in everyday speech 大部分 \"most part\" (separate card) is said. Do not confuse it with 大分 (だいぶ) \"considerably, quite a lot\", which is a different word.","examples":[{"japanese":"大部の辞書を三年かけて読み通した。","romaji":"Taibu no jisho o sannen kakete yomitōshita.","german":"I read through the voluminous dictionary over three years.","furigana":"たいぶ|じしょ|さんねん|よ|とお"},{"japanese":"会議の前に大部の報告書が配られた。","romaji":"Kaigi no mae ni taibu no hōkokusho ga kubarareta.","german":"A thick report was distributed before the meeting.","furigana":"かいぎ|まえ|たいぶ|ほうこくしょ|くば"},{"japanese":"被害の大部は沿岸地域に集中した。","romaji":"Higai no taibu wa engan chiiki ni shūchū shita.","german":"The bulk of the damage was concentrated in the coastal areas.","furigana":"ひがい|たいぶ|えんがんちいき|しゅうちゅう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Technical word of the traditional stage arts: in geisha and Kabuki dance, the 立方 dance while the 地方 (じかた) sing and play the shamisen. In the meaning \"way of standing, posture\" 立ち方 is usually written. Do not confuse it with 立方 (りっぽう) \"cube, third power\" (separate card), which appears in mathematics and in units such as 立方メートル.","examples":[{"japanese":"お座敷では立方が優雅に舞い、地方が三味線を弾いた。","romaji":"Ozashiki de wa tachikata ga yūga ni mai, jikata ga shamisen o hiita.","german":"At the banquet, the dancer danced gracefully while the musician played the shamisen.","furigana":"ざしき|たちかた|ゆうが|ま|じかた|しゃみせん|ひ"},{"japanese":"彼女は十五歳で立方として初めて舞台に立った。","romaji":"Kanojo wa jūgosai de tachikata to shite hajimete butai ni tatta.","german":"She stood on stage as a dancer for the first time at the age of fifteen.","furigana":"かのじょ|じゅうごさい|たちかた|はじ|ぶたい|た"},{"japanese":"正しい立ち方を身につけると、腰への負担が減る。","romaji":"Tadashii tachikata o mi ni tsukeru to, koshi e no futan ga heru.","german":"If you acquire the correct way of standing, the strain on your lower back is reduced.","furigana":"ただ|た|かた|み|こし|ふたん|へ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Literary reading of 掌; the normal word is 手のひら (てのひら, separate card), which can also be written 掌. たなごころ lives on mainly in idioms: 掌を指す \"to be perfectly clear\" (like pointing at one's palm) and 掌を返す \"in the blink of an eye; to suddenly change one's attitude\". In novels and poems 掌 often stands for てのひら, so context helps with the reading.","examples":[{"japanese":"犯人が誰かは、掌を指すように明らかだった。","romaji":"Hannin ga dare ka wa, tanagokoro o sasu yō ni akiraka datta.","german":"Who the culprit was was as plain as the palm of one's hand.","furigana":"はんにん|だれ|たなごころ|さ|あき"},{"japanese":"彼は勝負に負けると、掌を返すように態度を変えた。","romaji":"Kare wa shōbu ni makeru to, tanagokoro o kaesu yō ni taido o kaeta.","german":"When he lost the game, he changed his attitude as quickly as turning over his hand.","furigana":"かれ|しょうぶ|ま|たなごころ|かえ|たいど|か"},{"japanese":"老僧は小さな仏像を掌にのせて見せてくれた。","romaji":"Rōsō wa chiisana butsuzō o tanagokoro ni nosete misete kureta.","german":"The old monk placed a small Buddha statue on his palm and showed it to me.","furigana":"ろうそう|ちい|ぶつぞう|たなごころ|み"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Archaizing honorific verb, today almost only in prayers, Bible and song lyrics, or deliberately old-fashioned style. More common as an auxiliary after the verb stem: 守り給え \"protect (us)\", 与え給うた \"graciously gave\", where 給うた is pronounced たもうた. The more modern verb, still in use, is 賜る (たまわる, separate card) \"to receive; to be granted\".","examples":[{"japanese":"神は我らに日々の糧を給う。","romaji":"Kami wa warera ni hibi no kate o tamau.","german":"God gives us our daily bread.","furigana":"かみ|われ|ひび|かて|たま"},{"japanese":"主よ、我らを守り給え。","romaji":"Shu yo, warera o mamori tamae.","german":"Lord, protect us.","furigana":"しゅ|われ|まも|たま"},{"japanese":"神の与え給うた命を大切にしなさい。","romaji":"Kami no atae tamōta inochi o taisetsu ni shinasai.","german":"Cherish the life that God has given you.","furigana":"かみ|あた||いのち|たいせつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Noun of the verb 溜まる (たまる, \"to accumulate\"): it refers to what has accumulated, or the place where something or someone gathers. On its own it is rather rare; mostly it is in compounds such as 水溜まり \"puddle\" or 溜まり場 \"gathering place, regular haunt\". As soy sauce it is almost always written in kana: たまり醤油. The spelling 溜り without the まり okurigana is obsolete.","examples":[{"japanese":"大雨の後、道のくぼみに雨水の溜まりができた。","romaji":"Ōame no ato, michi no kubomi ni amamizu no tamari ga dekita.","german":"After the heavy rain, a pool of rainwater formed in a dip in the road.","furigana":"おおあめ|あと|みち|あまみず|た"},{"japanese":"この公園は近所の若者たちの溜まり場になっている。","romaji":"Kono kōen wa kinjo no wakamonotachi no tamariba ni natte iru.","german":"This park has become a hangout for the young people of the neighborhood.","furigana":"こうえん|きんじょ|わかもの|た|ば"},{"japanese":"刺身にはたまり醤油をつけて食べる。","romaji":"Sashimi ni wa tamari jōyu o tsukete taberu.","german":"You eat sashimi with tamari soy sauce.","furigana":"さしみ|じょうゆ|た"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Written-language noun that today appears almost only in fixed phrases of the form \"〜の嘆\": 髀肉の嘆をかこつ \"to lament enforced idleness\", 望洋の嘆 \"a sigh at one's own inadequacy\", 亡羊の嘆. In everyday speech the verb 嘆く (なげく) or the noun 嘆き is used instead; in compounds it is found in 感嘆 \"admiration\" and 悲嘆 \"grief\". The variant 歎 is an older spelling.","examples":[{"japanese":"出番のないまま月日が過ぎ、彼は髀肉の嘆をかこっていた。","romaji":"Deban no nai mama tsukihi ga sugi, kare wa hiniku no tan o kakotte ita.","german":"Months and days passed with no chance for him to act, and he was lamenting his enforced idleness.","furigana":"でばん|つきひ|す|かれ||たん"},{"japanese":"研究の範囲があまりに広く、望洋の嘆を禁じ得ない。","romaji":"Kenkyū no han'i ga amari ni hiroku, bōyō no tan o kinjienai.","german":"The scope of the research is so vast that one cannot help sighing in despair.","furigana":"けんきゅう|はんい|ひろ|ぼうよう|たん|きん|え"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Traditional unit of measure that follows numbers: 一反 (いったん), 二反, 三反 (さんたん). For cloth, one 反 is the bolt for one kimono, hence 反物 \"kimono fabric\"; as a unit of area it lives on in agriculture (一反あたりの収量). Do not confuse it with the prefix 反〜 (はん) \"anti-\" or the identically sounding adverb 一旦 \"for the time being, once\".","examples":[{"japanese":"着物を一着仕立てるには、反物が一反必要だ。","romaji":"Kimono o itchaku shitateru ni wa, tanmono ga ittan hitsuyō da.","german":"To tailor one kimono, you need one bolt of cloth (one tan).","furigana":"きもの|いっちゃくした|たんもの|いったんひつよう"},{"japanese":"祖父は田んぼを三反持っている。","romaji":"Sofu wa tanbo o santan motte iru.","german":"My grandfather owns three tan of rice fields.","furigana":"そふ|た|さんたんも"},{"japanese":"新しい品種のおかげで、一反あたりの米の収穫量が増えた。","romaji":"Atarashii hinshu no okage de, ittan atari no kome no shūkakuryō ga fueta.","german":"Thanks to the new variety, the rice yield per tan has increased.","furigana":"あたら|ひんしゅ|いったん|こめ|しゅうかくりょう|ふ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Formal noun for breaking open a stalled situation, typical in politics and economics: 局面の打開, 現状打開, 打開策 \"approach to a solution\". The verbal use is taught by the separate card 打開する. Unlike 突破 (とっぱ), which means forcibly breaking through or exceeding a mark, 打開 is about a new way out of a dead end.","examples":[{"japanese":"両国は交渉の行き詰まりの打開を目指している。","romaji":"Ryōkoku wa kōshō no ikizumari no dakai o mezashite iru.","german":"Both countries are aiming to break the deadlock in the negotiations.","furigana":"りょうこく|こうしょう|い|づ|だかい|めざ"},{"japanese":"政府は不況の打開策を打ち出した。","romaji":"Seifu wa fukyō no dakaisaku o uchidashita.","german":"The government put forward measures to overcome the recession.","furigana":"せいふ|ふきょう|だかいさく|う|だ"},{"japanese":"現状打開のためには、思い切った改革が必要だ。","romaji":"Genjō dakai no tame ni wa, omoikitta kaikaku ga hitsuyō da.","german":"Bold reforms are needed to break through the current situation.","furigana":"げんじょうだかい|おも|き|かいかく|ひつよう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Formal noun for the successful conclusion of negotiations, especially in wage and trade talks: 交渉が妥結に至る, 早期妥結, 妥結額 \"agreed pay raise\". The verbal use is taught by the card 妥結する. 妥協 (だきょう) is the mutual giving way itself, 妥結 the resulting outcome; the opposite is 決裂 \"breakdown\".","examples":[{"japanese":"長引いた賃金交渉がようやく妥結に至った。","romaji":"Nagabiita chingin kōshō ga yōyaku daketsu ni itatta.","german":"The protracted wage negotiations finally reached a settlement.","furigana":"ながび|ちんぎんこうしょう|だけつ|いた"},{"japanese":"今年の春闘の妥結額は昨年を上回った。","romaji":"Kotoshi no shuntō no daketsugaku wa sakunen o uwamawatta.","german":"The settlement amount in this year's spring wage talks exceeded last year's.","furigana":"ことし|しゅんとう|だけつがく|さくねん|うわまわ"},{"japanese":"両社は早期妥結を目指して協議を続けている。","romaji":"Ryōsha wa sōki daketsu o mezashite kyōgi o tsuzukete iru.","german":"The two companies continue talks aiming for an early agreement.","furigana":"りょうしゃ|そうきだけつ|めざ|きょうぎ|つづ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The native Japanese reading ちちはは is literary and emotional, e.g. in poems or letters about one's hometown (故郷の父母). The same spelling is read ふぼ in factual texts and administrative language (separate card, e.g. 父母会, 父母の同意). In everyday speech 両親 (りょうしん) or 父と母 is said. Without furigana, 父母 is mostly to be read ふぼ.","examples":[{"japanese":"故郷の父母を思うと、胸が熱くなる。","romaji":"Kokyō no chichihaha o omou to, mune ga atsuku naru.","german":"When I think of my parents in my hometown, my heart grows warm.","furigana":"こきょう|ちちはは|おも|むね|あつ"},{"japanese":"遠く離れて暮らす父母に手紙を書いた。","romaji":"Tōku hanarete kurasu chichihaha ni tegami o kaita.","german":"I wrote a letter to my parents, who live far away.","furigana":"とお|はな|く|ちちはは|てがみ|か"},{"japanese":"防人の歌には、父母との別れを詠んだものが多い。","romaji":"Sakimori no uta ni wa, chichihaha to no wakare o yonda mono ga ōi.","german":"Many of the songs of the frontier guards sing of parting from father and mother.","furigana":"|うた|ちちはは|わか|よ|おお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Onomatopoeic word, mostly for clothing that is too loose: だぶだぶのズボン, このシャツはだぶだぶだ. With する it describes flabby flesh or sloshing liquid; as an adverb also \"generously\" when pouring (ソースをだぶだぶかける). ぶかぶか stresses more \"too big\" (also for shoes), だぼだぼ is a close variant. Mostly in hiragana, ダブダブ for emphasis.","examples":[{"japanese":"兄のお下がりのズボンはだぶだぶだ。","romaji":"Ani no osagari no zubon wa dabudabu da.","german":"The hand-me-down trousers from my older brother are baggy.","furigana":"あに|さ"},{"japanese":"運動不足でお腹の肉がだぶだぶしてきた。","romaji":"Undō busoku de onaka no niku ga dabudabu shite kita.","german":"From lack of exercise, the flesh on my belly has become flabby.","furigana":"うんどうぶそく|なか|にく"},{"japanese":"彼はとんかつにソースをだぶだぶかける。","romaji":"Kare wa tonkatsu ni sōsu o dabudabu kakeru.","german":"He pours sauce all over his tonkatsu.","furigana":"かれ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Onomatopoeic word, almost always as ちやほやする or in the passive ちやほやされる \"to be doted on, courted\". It sounds slightly derogatory: someone gets excessive attention and flattery, often because of looks or success. 甘やかす (あまやかす), by contrast, means indulgent spoiling, typically of children. Mostly written in hiragana.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は子どもの頃から周りにちやほやされて育った。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kodomo no koro kara mawari ni chiyahoya sarete sodatta.","german":"She grew up fussed over by everyone around her from childhood.","furigana":"かのじょ|こ|ころ|まわ|そだ"},{"japanese":"新人をちやほやしすぎると、本人のためにならない。","romaji":"Shinjin o chiyahoya shisugiru to, honnin no tame ni naranai.","german":"If you fawn over newcomers too much, it doesn't do them any good.","furigana":"しんじん|ほんにん"},{"japanese":"人気が落ちると、ちやほやしていた人たちは離れていった。","romaji":"Ninki ga ochiru to, chiyahoya shite ita hitotachi wa hanarete itta.","german":"When his popularity declined, the people who had been fawning over him drifted away.","furigana":"にんき|お|ひと|はな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The Sino-Japanese reading ちゅうかん appears almost only in official and technical compounds, mostly as the counterpart to 夜間 (やかん): 昼間人口 \"daytime population\", 昼間部 \"daytime division\", 昼間定時制. Alone and in everyday speech, 昼間 is always read ひるま (separate card). Do not confuse it with the homophone 中間 \"middle\".","examples":[{"japanese":"東京の都心は、夜間人口に比べて昼間人口が非常に多い。","romaji":"Tōkyō no toshin wa, yakan jinkō ni kurabete chūkan jinkō ga hijō ni ōi.","german":"In central Tokyo, the daytime population is very large compared with the nighttime population.","furigana":"とうきょう|としん|やかんじんこう|くら|ちゅうかんじんこう|ひじょう|おお"},{"japanese":"彼は働きながら、大学の昼間部ではなく夜間部に通った。","romaji":"Kare wa hatarakinagara, daigaku no chūkanbu de wa naku yakanbu ni kayotta.","german":"While working, he attended not the daytime division of the university but the evening division.","furigana":"かれ|はたら|だいがく|ちゅうかんぶ|やかんぶ|かよ"},{"japanese":"この高校には昼間定時制の課程もある。","romaji":"Kono kōkō ni wa chūkan teijisei no katei mo aru.","german":"This high school also has a daytime part-time course.","furigana":"こうこう|ちゅうかんていじせい|かてい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The reading てかず is preferred when counted moves or blows are meant: 手数が多い/少ない, 手数で勝る; in Go also the liberties of a stone in a capturing race (攻め合い). For \"trouble, effort\", on the other hand, てすう is usual, especially in the polite formula お手数 (おてすう, separate card) and in 手数料 \"fee\".","examples":[{"japanese":"この詰将棋は手数が長くて、なかなか解けない。","romaji":"Kono tsumeshōgi wa tekazu ga nagakute, nakanaka tokenai.","german":"This shogi mating problem takes so many moves that it is hard to solve.","furigana":"|てかず|なが|と"},{"japanese":"挑戦者は手数で上回り、判定勝ちを収めた。","romaji":"Chōsensha wa tekazu de uwamawari, hanteigachi o osameta.","german":"The challenger landed more blows and won by decision.","furigana":"ちょうせんしゃ|てかず|うわまわ|はんていが|おさ"},{"japanese":"攻め合いでは、手数の多いほうが勝つ。","romaji":"Semeai de wa, tekazu no ōi hō ga katsu.","german":"In a capturing race, the side with more moves wins.","furigana":"せ|あ|てかず|おお|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Literary onomatopoeia for the rhythmic chopping of an axe or the driving in of a stake, mostly as 丁々と or attributively 丁々たる. Do not confuse it with the identically sounding cards とうとう \"at last\", 等々 \"etc.\" and 蕩蕩 \"vast, boundless\". The same spelling is read ちょうちょう for clashing swords, e.g. in 丁々発止 \"a heated exchange of blows\".","examples":[{"japanese":"山奥から丁々と斧の音が響いてくる。","romaji":"Yamaoku kara tōtō to ono no oto ga hibiite kuru.","german":"The sound of an axe echoes from deep in the mountains.","furigana":"やまおく|とうとう|おの|おと|ひび"},{"japanese":"木こりが丁々と大木を伐っていた。","romaji":"Kikori ga tōtō to taiboku o kitte ita.","german":"A woodcutter was chopping down a big tree with thudding blows.","furigana":"き|とうとう|たいぼく|"},{"japanese":"丁々と杭を打つ音が朝の静けさを破った。","romaji":"Tōtō to kui o utsu oto ga asa no shizukesa o yabutta.","german":"The thud of driving in stakes broke the morning stillness.","furigana":"とうとう|くい|う|おと|あさ|しず|やぶ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Elevated word for a (small) light in the darkness, often figurative: 希望の灯火 \"light of hope\", 風前の灯火 \"a light in the wind\" (= on the verge of ruin). Mostly written 灯火; the same spelling is read とうか in technical words (灯火管制). The reading ひ (separate card, 灯を消す) and 明かり (あかり) are the plain everyday words for light.","examples":[{"japanese":"遠くに村の灯が見えて、ほっとした。","romaji":"Tōku ni mura no tomoshibi ga miete, hotto shita.","german":"I saw the lights of a village in the distance and felt relieved.","furigana":"とお|むら|ともしび|み"},{"japanese":"その活動は被災した人々にとって希望の灯火となった。","romaji":"Sono katsudō wa hisai shita hitobito ni totte kibō no tomoshibi to natta.","german":"That activity became a light of hope for the people affected by the disaster.","furigana":"かつどう|ひさい|ひとびと|きぼう|ともしび"},{"japanese":"業績が悪化し、会社は今や風前の灯火だ。","romaji":"Gyōseki ga akka shi, kaisha wa ima ya fūzen no tomoshibi da.","german":"Business results worsened, and the company is now hanging by a thread.","furigana":"ぎょうせき|あっか|かいしゃ|いま|ふうぜん|ともしび"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Sino-Japanese reading of 南 in compounds: 南米 \"South America\", 南極 \"South Pole\", 南西 \"southwest\", 南部 \"the south (of an area)\", 南下 \"to move south\". Alone and before Japanese words or katakana, it is read みなみ (separate card): 南口, 南アメリカ. Do not confuse it with the cards 何 (なん) \"what\" and 難 (なん) \"difficulty\".","examples":[{"japanese":"来年、南米を一か月かけて旅行するつもりだ。","romaji":"Rainen, Nanbei o ikkagetsu kakete ryokō suru tsumori da.","german":"Next year I plan to travel through South America for a month.","furigana":"らいねん|なんべい|いっ|げつ|りょこう"},{"japanese":"南極の氷が年々減っている。","romaji":"Nankyoku no kōri ga nennen hette iru.","german":"The ice of Antarctica is shrinking year by year.","furigana":"なんきょく|こおり|ねんねんへ"},{"japanese":"台風は九州の南西の海上を北上している。","romaji":"Taifū wa Kyūshū no nansei no kaijō o hokujō shite iru.","german":"The typhoon is moving north over the sea to the southwest of Kyushu.","furigana":"たいふう|きゅうしゅう|なんせい|かいじょう|ほくじょう"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Exclamatory word before adjectives or nouns: なんてきれいなんだろう \"how beautiful!\", なんて素敵な人 \"what a wonderful person\"; the sentence often ends in 〜んだ, 〜んだろう or 〜でしょう. Mostly written in kana. Do not confuse it with the particle なんて \"something like\" (separate card), which stands after a noun (勉強なんて嫌い). Colloquially also as the question なんて？ \"what did you say?\".","examples":[{"japanese":"なんてきれいな夕焼けなんだろう。","romaji":"Nante kirei na yūyake nan darō.","german":"What a beautiful sunset!","furigana":"ゆうや"},{"japanese":"何て親切な人なんでしょう。","romaji":"Nante shinsetsu na hito nan deshō.","german":"What a kind person!","furigana":"なん|しんせつ|ひと"},{"japanese":"ごめん、なんて？よく聞こえなかった。","romaji":"Gomen, nante? Yoku kikoenakatta.","german":"Sorry, what? I couldn't hear well.","furigana":"き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Stands as a suffix after paper and book formats: A4判 (エーよんばん), B5判, A3判; also 大判 (おおばん) \"large format\". Common in offices and publishing; in conversation people often say just A4. Do not confuse it with 判/はん \"stamp, seal\" (separate card): same spelling, but different reading and meaning.","examples":[{"japanese":"この書類はA4判の用紙に印刷してください。","romaji":"Kono shorui wa ē-yon-ban no yōshi ni insatsu shite kudasai.","german":"Please print this document on A4-size paper."},{"japanese":"新しい雑誌はB5判で、持ち歩きやすい。","romaji":"Atarashii zasshi wa bī-go-ban de, mochiarukiyasui.","german":"The new magazine is B5 size and easy to carry around."},{"japanese":"旅行の写真をA3判に引き伸ばしてもらった。","romaji":"Ryokō no shashin o ē-san-ban ni hikinobashite moratta.","german":"I had the vacation photo enlarged to A3."}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The eighth of the twelve zodiac signs (十二支), mainly in 未年 (ひつじどし) \"year of the Sheep\"; on New Year's cards 未 often stands instead of 羊. In the old system of timekeeping it is also the hour of the Sheep (about 1-3 p.m.) and the direction south-southwest. The animal itself is written 羊 (separate card); 未〜/み as a prefix means \"not yet\" (separate card).","examples":[{"japanese":"私は未年生まれです。","romaji":"Watashi wa hitsujidoshi umare desu.","german":"I was born in the year of the Sheep.","furigana":"わたし|ひつじどしう"},{"japanese":"十二支で八番目にあたるのが未です。","romaji":"Jūnishi de hachiban-me ni ataru no ga hitsuji desu.","german":"The eighth sign of the twelve zodiac signs is the Sheep.","furigana":"じゅうにし|はちばんめ|ひつじ"},{"japanese":"昔は午後二時ごろを未の刻と呼んだ。","romaji":"Mukashi wa gogo niji goro o hitsuji no koku to yonda.","german":"In the past, the time around two in the afternoon was called the hour of the Sheep.","furigana":"むかし|ごごにじ|ひつじ|こく|よ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Short form of 歩兵 (ふひょう), the weakest shogi piece, which moves only one square forward and is promoted to と金 in the enemy camp. Phrases: 歩を突く \"to advance a pawn\", 歩を打つ \"to drop a captured pawn\". In Western chess, ポーン is said more. Same spelling as 歩/ほ \"step\" (separate card), but a different reading.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は最初に歩を一つ前に進めた。","romaji":"Kare wa saisho ni fu o hitotsu mae ni susumeta.","german":"First, he advanced one pawn one square.","furigana":"かれ|さいしょ|ふ|ひと|まえ|すす"},{"japanese":"取った歩を自分の駒として盤に打った。","romaji":"Totta fu o jibun no koma to shite ban ni utta.","german":"He dropped the captured pawn onto the board as his own piece.","furigana":"と|ふ|じぶん|こま|ばん|う"},{"japanese":"歩が敵陣に入ると、と金に成れる。","romaji":"Fu ga tekijin ni hairu to, tokin ni nareru.","german":"When a pawn enters the enemy camp, it can promote to a tokin.","furigana":"ふ|てきじん|はい|きん|な"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Elevated, literary word for \"letter, message\" and \"writings, books\"; in everyday speech 手紙 is said. It lives on in compounds such as 恋文 (こいぶみ) \"love letter\". Same spelling as 文/ぶん \"sentence, text\" (separate card): ぶん is the neutral word for sentences and texts, ふみ belongs to poetic or archaic style.","examples":[{"japanese":"遠く離れた恋人に文を送った。","romaji":"Tōku hanareta koibito ni fumi o okutta.","german":"She sent a letter to her distant lover.","furigana":"とお|はな|こいびと|ふみ|おく"},{"japanese":"古い箱の中から祖母の恋文が見つかった。","romaji":"Furui hako no naka kara sobo no koibumi ga mitsukatta.","german":"A love letter from my grandmother was found in an old box.","furigana":"ふる|はこ|なか|そぼ|こいぶみ|み"},{"japanese":"平安時代の貴族は和歌を添えて文を交わした。","romaji":"Heian jidai no kizoku wa waka o soete fumi o kawashita.","german":"The nobles of the Heian period exchanged letters with poems attached.","furigana":"へいあんじだい|きぞく|わか|そ|ふみ|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Onomatopoeic word for clothes or shoes that are too big and flap around: ぶかぶかのズボン, 靴がぶかぶかだ, ぶかぶかになる. The opposites are きつい \"tight\" and ぴったり \"a perfect fit\". Colloquial and mostly in hiragana, more rarely ブカブカ; the meaning \"bulging, warped\" is rare.","examples":[{"japanese":"兄のお下がりのズボンはぶかぶかだ。","romaji":"Ani no osagari no zubon wa bukabuka da.","german":"The hand-me-down trousers from my older brother are baggy.","furigana":"あに|さ"},{"japanese":"やせたので、前のスーツがぶかぶかになった。","romaji":"Yaseta node, mae no sūtsu ga bukabuka ni natta.","german":"Because I lost weight, my old suit has become baggy.","furigana":"まえ"},{"japanese":"娘はわざとぶかぶかのセーターを着ている。","romaji":"Musume wa wazato bukabuka no sētā o kite iru.","german":"My daughter wears an oversized sweater on purpose.","furigana":"むすめ|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Refers to spots in the fur of animals, especially in ぶち猫 \"spotted cat\" or 白黒のぶちの犬; ブチ is a popular name for spotted pets. Mostly in kana. The kanji spelling 斑 is mostly read まだら (separate card): まだら \"mottled, uneven\" is more general and also applies to patterns, colors or snow.","examples":[{"japanese":"うちの猫は白と黒のぶちだ。","romaji":"Uchi no neko wa shiro to kuro no buchi da.","german":"Our cat is a black-and-white spotted one.","furigana":"ねこ|しろ|くろ"},{"japanese":"公園で茶色いぶちのある犬を見かけた。","romaji":"Kōen de chairoi buchi no aru inu o mikaketa.","german":"I saw a dog with brown patches in the park.","furigana":"こうえん|ちゃいろ|いぬ|み"},{"japanese":"その牛は体に大きなぶちがある。","romaji":"Sono ushi wa karada ni ōkina buchi ga aru.","german":"That cow has big spots on its body.","furigana":"うし|からだ|おお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Formal word for the normal, usual state, often in notices and announcements: 平常通り \"as usual\", 平常運転 \"regular service\", 平常心 \"composure, inner calm\". With の before nouns: 平常の業務. In conversation 普段 or いつも is said more; 日頃, by contrast, stresses habit over a longer period. Opposite: 異常 \"abnormal\".","examples":[{"japanese":"台風が過ぎ、電車は平常通り運転している。","romaji":"Taifū ga sugi, densha wa heijō-dōri unten shite iru.","german":"The typhoon has passed, and the trains are running as usual.","furigana":"たいふう|す|でんしゃ|へいじょうどお|うんてん"},{"japanese":"試合では平常心を保つことが大切だ。","romaji":"Shiai de wa heijōshin o tamotsu koto ga taisetsu da.","german":"In a match, it is important to keep a calm mind.","furigana":"しあい|へいじょうしん|たも|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"検査の結果、血圧は平常の範囲内だった。","romaji":"Kensa no kekka, ketsuatsu wa heijō no han'inai datta.","german":"According to the examination, the blood pressure was within the normal range.","furigana":"けんさ|けっか|けつあつ|へいじょう|はんいない"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Technical term of kanji study for the left component of a character, often as a suffix -へん/-べん: 人偏 (にんべん), 木偏 (きへん), 糸偏 (いとへん). The counterpart is 旁 (つくり), the right component; on top is 冠 (かんむり). In teaching it is often written in kana. Do not confuse it with the identically read 辺, 変, 〜編 and 〜遍 (separate cards).","examples":[{"japanese":"「休」という字の偏は人偏だ。","romaji":"\"Kyū\" to iu ji no hen wa ninben da.","german":"The left component of the character 休 is the person radical.","furigana":"きゅう|じ|へん|にんべん"},{"japanese":"「海」や「池」の偏はさんずいだ。","romaji":"\"Umi\" ya \"ike\" no hen wa sanzui da.","german":"The left component of 海 and 池 is the water radical.","furigana":"うみ|いけ|へん"},{"japanese":"漢字には偏と旁を組み合わせたものが多い。","romaji":"Kanji ni wa hen to tsukuri o kumiawaseta mono ga ōi.","german":"Many kanji are made by combining a left component and a right component.","furigana":"かんじ|へん||く|あ|おお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Occurs almost only in compounds: 封建制度 \"feudal system\", 封建時代 \"feudal era\", 封建社会 \"feudal society\" and 封建的 \"feudalistic\", figuratively also \"backward, authoritarian\" (封建的な考え方). A technical word from history and social criticism. For Japan, 封建時代 usually means the era of samurai rule until the Meiji Restoration.","examples":[{"japanese":"江戸時代の日本は封建社会だった。","romaji":"Edo jidai no Nihon wa hōken shakai datta.","german":"Japan in the Edo period was a feudal society.","furigana":"えどじだい|にほん|ほうけんしゃかい"},{"japanese":"授業でヨーロッパの封建制度について学んだ。","romaji":"Jugyō de Yōroppa no hōken seido ni tsuite mananda.","german":"In class, we learned about the European feudal system.","furigana":"じゅぎょう|ほうけんせいど|まな"},{"japanese":"父の考え方はいまだに封建的だ。","romaji":"Chichi no kangaekata wa imada ni hōkenteki da.","german":"My father's way of thinking is still feudalistic.","furigana":"ちち|かんが|かた|ほうけんてき"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Written-language ずる variant of 報じる (separate card) with the same meaning: \"to report, to announce\" (新聞が事件を報ずる) and, elevated, \"to repay, to return\" (恩に報ずる). Typical of newspaper language and older literature; in modern usage 報じる is more common. The forms follow partly 報じる (報じた, 報じない), partly the classical pattern (報ぜず, 報ぜられる).","examples":[{"japanese":"地元の新聞は、その事件を詳しく報ずる記事を載せた。","romaji":"Jimoto no shinbun wa, sono jiken o kuwashiku hōzuru kiji o noseta.","german":"The local newspaper ran an article that reported the incident in detail.","furigana":"じもと|しんぶん|じけん|くわ|ほう|きじ|の"},{"japanese":"首相の辞任は各紙で大きく報ぜられた。","romaji":"Shushō no jinin wa kakushi de ōkiku hōzerareta.","german":"The prime minister's resignation was reported prominently in all the papers.","furigana":"しゅしょう|じにん|かくし|おお|ほう"},{"japanese":"受けた恩には必ず報ずるつもりだ。","romaji":"Uketa on ni wa kanarazu hōzuru tsumori da.","german":"I intend to repay the kindness I received without fail.","furigana":"う|おん|かなら|ほう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Base word of 本格的 (separate card); rare on its own, but productive in compounds: 本格化 \"to get going in earnest\", 本格派 \"representative of the classical style\". It also stands directly before nouns in the sense of \"genuine, authentic\": 本格カレー, 本格ミステリー \"classic puzzle mystery\". For adjectival use, 本格的な is usually taken.","examples":[{"japanese":"梅雨が明けて、夏が本格化してきた。","romaji":"Tsuyu ga akete, natsu ga honkakuka shite kita.","german":"The rainy season has ended, and summer has begun in earnest.","furigana":"つゆ|あ|なつ|ほんかくか"},{"japanese":"駅前に本格インド料理の店ができた。","romaji":"Ekimae ni honkaku Indo ryōri no mise ga dekita.","german":"An authentic Indian restaurant has opened in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ|ほんかく|りょうり|みせ"},{"japanese":"彼は本格派の推理作家として知られている。","romaji":"Kare wa honkakuha no suiri sakka to shite shirarete iru.","german":"He is known as a detective-story writer of the orthodox school.","furigana":"かれ|ほんかくは|すいりさっか|し"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"巻（まき） is the native Japanese reading of 巻 and refers to what is wound or to winding; as an independent noun it is mostly written 巻き (巻きがしっかりしている), in titles on the other hand 〜の巻. Numbered volumes are called 第一巻 with the reading かん (separate card 巻/かん); 〜の巻 names rather chapters of classical works or, humorously, an \"episode\". The word is very often found in compounds such as 巻き寿司, 春巻き or ねじ巻き; in television and stage jargon, 巻きで means \"to speed up\".","examples":[{"japanese":"授業で源氏物語の「若紫の巻」を読んだ。","romaji":"Jugyō de Genji monogatari no \"Wakamurasaki no maki\" o yonda.","german":"In class, we read the \"Wakamurasaki\" chapter of The Tale of Genji.","furigana":"じゅぎょう|げんじものがたり|わかむらさき|まき|よ"},{"japanese":"このキャベツは巻きがしっかりしていて重い。","romaji":"Kono kyabetsu wa maki ga shikkari shite ite omoi.","german":"This cabbage is tightly wrapped and heavy.","furigana":"ま|おも"},{"japanese":"撮影が押しているので、少し巻きでお願いします。","romaji":"Satsuei ga oshite iru node, sukoshi maki de onegai shimasu.","german":"The shoot is running behind, so please pick up the pace a bit.","furigana":"さつえい|お|すこ|ま|ねが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Elevated noun for \"sincerity\" and \"truth\", typical in fixed phrases such as 誠を尽くす \"to make a sincere effort\" or the proverb 嘘から出た誠 \"a joke turns into earnest\". The adverb 誠に \"truly, sincerely\" (separate card) is a separate word and much more common in polite formulas such as 誠に申し訳ございません. For the meaning \"truth\", the spellings 真 and 実 are also seen; as the ideal of the Shinsengumi and as a given name, 誠 is culturally present.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は誠を尽くして被害者に謝罪した。","romaji":"Kare wa makoto o tsukushite higaisha ni shazai shita.","german":"He apologized to the victims with all sincerity.","furigana":"かれ|まこと|つ|ひがいしゃ|しゃざい"},{"japanese":"冗談のつもりが本当になるなんて、嘘から出た誠だね。","romaji":"Jōdan no tsumori ga hontō ni naru nante, uso kara deta makoto da ne.","german":"I meant it as a joke, but it came true - a lie turned into truth.","furigana":"じょうだん|ほんとう|うそ|で|まこと"},{"japanese":"新選組の旗には「誠」の一字が染め抜かれていた。","romaji":"Shinsengumi no hata ni wa \"makoto\" no ichiji ga somenukarete ita.","german":"The single character \"sincerity\" was dyed onto the Shinsengumi banner.","furigana":"しんせんぐみ|はた|まこと|いちじ|そ|ぬ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Formal function word, almost always in kana and mostly as 〜をもって: \"by means of\" (書面をもって), \"with/as of\" for a cutoff date (本日をもって閉店) or \"even with\" in 〜をもってしても. Typical of announcements, business letters and speeches; in everyday speech で is used instead. Do not confuse it with 持って, the te-form of 持つ \"to hold, to carry\"; the spelling 以て is rare today. As an intensifier it appears in まったくもって \"really, absolutely\".","examples":[{"japanese":"当店は本日をもって閉店いたします。","romaji":"Tōten wa honjitsu o motte heiten itashimasu.","german":"Our store will close as of today.","furigana":"とうてん|ほんじつ|へいてん"},{"japanese":"結果は後日、書面をもってお知らせいたします。","romaji":"Kekka wa gojitsu, shomen o motte oshirase itashimasu.","german":"We will notify you of the result in writing at a later date.","furigana":"けっか|ごじつ|しょめん|し"},{"japanese":"彼の実力をもってしても、優勝は難しかった。","romaji":"Kare no jitsuryoku o motte shite mo, yūshō wa muzukashikatta.","german":"Even with his ability, winning was difficult.","furigana":"かれ|じつりょく|ゆうしょう|むずか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Elevated, literary reading of 基 for \"basis, foundation\", mostly in phrases such as 〜の基を築く \"to lay the foundation of ...\" or 〜の基となる. In normal usage, もと (card 元/もと, which lists 基 as a spelling) or 基礎 or 土台 is said. Do not confuse it with the interjection もとい \"uh, I mean\", with which one corrects oneself.","examples":[{"japanese":"教育こそが国の基である。","romaji":"Kyōiku koso ga kuni no motoi de aru.","german":"Education is precisely the foundation of a nation.","furigana":"きょういく|くに|もとい"},{"japanese":"祖父は一代でこの会社の基を築いた。","romaji":"Sofu wa ichidai de kono kaisha no motoi o kizuita.","german":"My grandfather laid the foundation of this company within a single generation.","furigana":"そふ|いちだい|かいしゃ|もとい|きず"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Historical word for the servants and spear-bearers of the samurai in the Edo period. Today it appears almost only in compounds: 冷奴（ひややっこ） \"chilled tofu\", 奴に切る \"to cut tofu into cubes\" (after the crest pattern on the servants' clothing) or 奴凧 \"kite in the form of a servant\". The reading やつ (separate card 奴/やつ) is a completely different, colloquial word for \"guy, fellow\".","examples":[{"japanese":"大名行列の先頭では、奴が槍を振りながら歩いた。","romaji":"Daimyō gyōretsu no sentō de wa, yakko ga yari o furinagara aruita.","german":"At the head of the daimyo procession, the retainers walked brandishing their spears.","furigana":"だいみょうぎょうれつ|せんとう|やっこ||ふ|ある"},{"japanese":"暑い日は冷奴にしょうがとねぎをのせて食べる。","romaji":"Atsui hi wa hiyayakko ni shōga to negi o nosete taberu.","german":"On hot days, I eat chilled tofu topped with ginger and scallions.","furigana":"あつ|ひ||た"},{"japanese":"豆腐を奴に切って味噌汁に入れた。","romaji":"Tōfu o yakko ni kitte misoshiru ni ireta.","german":"I cut the tofu into cubes and put it in the miso soup.","furigana":"とうふ|やっこ|き|みそしる|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"The same plant as あし (also written 葦): because あし recalls 悪し \"bad\", よし \"good\" prevailed as a euphemistic reading. In botany and nature conservation, ヨシ in katakana is the standard name, and よし is in words such as 葦簀（よしず） \"reed screen\". Literary phrases like 考える葦 (Pascal), on the other hand, are read あし; the word has nothing to do with 足/脚 \"leg\".","examples":[{"japanese":"川岸に葦が生い茂っている。","romaji":"Kawagishi ni yoshi ga oishigette iru.","german":"Dense reeds grow along the riverbank.","furigana":"かわぎし|よし||しげ"},{"japanese":"夏は葦簀を立てかけて日差しを遮る。","romaji":"Natsu wa yoshizu o tatekakete hizashi o saegiru.","german":"In summer, people lean up reed screens to block the sunlight.","furigana":"なつ||た|ひざ|さえぎ"},{"japanese":"琵琶湖のヨシ原には多くの野鳥が集まる。","romaji":"Biwako no yoshihara ni wa ōku no yachō ga atsumaru.","german":"Many wild birds gather in the reed beds of Lake Biwa.","furigana":"びわこ|はら|おお|やちょう|あつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Counter for single blossoms (一輪, 二輪, 三輪 - without sound change) and for wheels, especially in vehicle words such as 一輪車 \"unicycle\" or 二輪車 \"two-wheeler\". 一輪挿し is a vase for a single flower. The noun 輪（わ） \"ring, circle\" is a separate card; 車輪 \"wheel\" shows the same reading りん in a fixed word.","examples":[{"japanese":"花瓶にバラを一輪だけ挿した。","romaji":"Kabin ni bara o ichirin dake sashita.","german":"I put just a single rose in the vase.","furigana":"かびん|いちりん|さ"},{"japanese":"庭の梅が二、三輪咲き始めた。","romaji":"Niwa no ume ga ni, sanrin sakihajimeta.","german":"Two or three plum blossoms in the garden have begun to bloom.","furigana":"にわ|うめ|に|さんりんさ|はじ"},{"japanese":"娘は一輪車に乗れるようになった。","romaji":"Musume wa ichirinsha ni noreru yō ni natta.","german":"My daughter can now ride a unicycle.","furigana":"むすめ|いちりんしゃ|の"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Loanword from English \"hour\" that occurs almost only in compounds: ラッシュアワー \"rush hour\" (separate card), ハッピーアワー or the unit キロワットアワー \"kilowatt-hour\". It hardly stands alone; the normal hour as a unit of time is 時間, the time of day 〜時.","examples":[{"japanese":"朝のラッシュアワーを避けて、早めに家を出る。","romaji":"Asa no rasshu awā o sakete, hayame ni ie o deru.","german":"I leave home early to avoid the morning rush hour.","furigana":"あさ|さ|はや|いえ|で"},{"japanese":"このバーは五時から七時までハッピーアワーで、ビールが半額になる。","romaji":"Kono bā wa goji kara shichiji made happī awā de, bīru ga hangaku ni naru.","german":"This bar has a happy hour from five to seven, and beer is half price.","furigana":"ごじ|しちじ|はんがく"},{"japanese":"この冷蔵庫の年間消費電力は約三百キロワットアワーだ。","romaji":"Kono reizōko no nenkan shōhi denryoku wa yaku sanbyaku kirowatto awā da.","german":"This refrigerator's annual power consumption is about 300 kilowatt-hours.","furigana":"れいぞうこ|ねんかんしょうひでんりょく|やくさんびゃく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Loanword \"yes\", which does not serve as an ordinary answer (for that, はい/ええ), but stands in fixed patterns: イエスかノーか \"yes or no\", イエスと言う \"to agree\" or イエスマン \"yes-man\". The spelling イェス is a rarer variant. The identically written イエス \"Jesus\" is a proper name and is not learned here.","examples":[{"japanese":"イエスかノーかで答えてください。","romaji":"Iesu ka nō ka de kotaete kudasai.","german":"Please answer yes or no.","furigana":"こた"},{"japanese":"上司の言うことに何でもイエスと言うのはよくない。","romaji":"Jōshi no iu koto ni nan demo iesu to iu no wa yoku nai.","german":"It is not good to say yes to everything your boss says.","furigana":"じょうし|い|なん|い"},{"japanese":"彼は社長のイエスマンだと陰で言われている。","romaji":"Kare wa shachō no iesuman da to kage de iwarete iru.","german":"Behind his back, he is said to be the president's yes-man.","furigana":"かれ|しゃちょう|かげ|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Loanword for \"sexual intercourse\", also as the verb セックスする (with a partner marked by と); it is direct, but not vulgar. 性行為 sounds more factual or official, エッチ is a colloquial euphemism. In biology and medicine, セックス also denotes biological sex as opposed to ジェンダー; in everyday speech 性別 is said for this.","examples":[{"japanese":"保健の授業では、セックスと避妊について正しい知識を学ぶ。","romaji":"Hoken no jugyō de wa, sekkusu to hinin ni tsuite tadashii chishiki o manabu.","german":"In health class, students learn correct knowledge about sex and contraception.","furigana":"ほけん|じゅぎょう|ひにん|ただ|ちしき|まな"},{"japanese":"医学では、生物学的な性をセックス、社会的な性をジェンダーと呼んで区別する。","romaji":"Igaku de wa, seibutsugakuteki na sei o sekkusu, shakaiteki na sei o jendā to yonde kubetsu suru.","german":"In medicine, biological sex is called \"sex\" and social sex \"gender\" to distinguish them.","furigana":"いがく|せいぶつがくてき|せい|しゃかいてき|せい|よ|くべつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Loan element from English \"new\" that stands only before other loanwords: ニューモデル \"new model\", ニューフェース \"new face\", ニューオープン \"new opening\"; also in place names such as ニューヨーク. Alone or before Japanese words, 新しい or the prefix 新 is used. It shares only a similar sound with ニュース \"news\".","examples":[{"japanese":"来月、ニューモデルのスマホが発売される。","romaji":"Raigetsu, nyū moderu no sumaho ga hatsubai sareru.","german":"A new-model smartphone will be released next month.","furigana":"らいげつ|はつばい"},{"japanese":"駅前にニューオープンのカフェができた。","romaji":"Ekimae ni nyū ōpun no kafe ga dekita.","german":"A newly opened cafe has appeared in front of the station.","furigana":"えきまえ"},{"japanese":"今年のドラマにはニューフェースが多い。","romaji":"Kotoshi no dorama ni wa nyū fēsu ga ōi.","german":"This year's TV dramas have many new faces.","furigana":"ことし|おお"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Onomatopoeic adverb for a sudden, forceful movement, often with verbs such as 飛び上がる, 跳ね上げる or 広がる. It is the voiced, more forceful counterpart to ぱっと, which seems lighter and quicker. It has nothing to do with the loanword バット \"bat\" (separate card).","examples":[{"japanese":"物音に驚いて、猫がばっと飛び上がった。","romaji":"Monooto ni odoroite, neko ga batto tobiagatta.","german":"Startled by a noise, the cat leapt up in a flash.","furigana":"ものおと|おどろ|ねこ|と|あ"},{"japanese":"目覚ましが鳴ると、彼は布団をばっと跳ね上げて起きた。","romaji":"Mezamashi ga naru to, kare wa futon o batto haneagete okita.","german":"When the alarm clock rang, he threw back the futon and got up in one motion.","furigana":"めざ|な|かれ|ふとん|は|あ|お"},{"japanese":"その噂はばっと町中に広まった。","romaji":"Sono uwasa wa batto machijū ni hiromatta.","german":"The rumor spread through the whole town in an instant.","furigana":"うわさ|まちじゅう|ひろ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Ältere, aus dem Deutschen „Virus“ entlehnte Form, die bis etwa in die 1970er Jahre üblich war und heute in alten Texten oder bei älteren Sprechern begegnet. Standard ist heute ウイルス (eigene Karte), das auch in Fachsprache und Medien ausschließlich verwendet wird. Wer selbst schreibt, sollte daher immer ウイルス wählen.","examples":[{"japanese":"昔の新聞では「ウイルス」ではなく「ビールス」と書かれていた。","romaji":"Mukashi no shinbun de wa \"uirusu\" de wa naku \"bīrusu\" to kakarete ita.","german":"In alten Zeitungen stand nicht „ウイルス“, sondern „ビールス“.","furigana":"むかし|しんぶん|か"},{"japanese":"祖父は風邪をひくと「ビールスにやられた」と言う。","romaji":"Sofu wa kaze o hiku to \"bīrusu ni yarareta\" to iu.","german":"Wenn mein Großvater erkältet ist, sagt er: „Mich hat ein Virus erwischt.“","furigana":"そふ|かぜ|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Onomatopoeic adverb for something that appears softly and suddenly: redness in the face (顔をぽっと赤らめる), a light coming on, or a person who turns up unexpectedly, hence ぽっと出 \"newcomer, upstart\". Do not confuse it with ほっと \"relieved\" (separate card) and the loanword ポット \"pot\" (separate card).","examples":[{"japanese":"褒められて、彼女は顔をぽっと赤らめた。","romaji":"Homerarete, kanojo wa kao o potto akarameta.","german":"When she was praised, she blushed slightly.","furigana":"ほ|かのじょ|かお|あか"},{"japanese":"暗い部屋に明かりがぽっとともった。","romaji":"Kurai heya ni akari ga potto tomotta.","german":"A light suddenly came on in the dark room.","furigana":"くら|へや|あ"},{"japanese":"ぽっと出の新人に負けるわけにはいかない。","romaji":"Potto de no shinjin ni makeru wake ni wa ikanai.","german":"I can't afford to lose to a newcomer who has just popped up out of nowhere.","furigana":"で|しんじん|ま"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"A somewhat old-fashioned-sounding loanword from English that stands mainly attributively: ヤング向け (\"for young people\"), ヤングの客. It sounds like the advertising and media language of the 70s and 80s and is often used jokingly today. In normal usage 若者 (わかもの, separate card) or 若い is said.","examples":[{"japanese":"この雑誌はヤング向けに作られている。","romaji":"Kono zasshi wa yangu-muke ni tsukurarete iru.","german":"This magazine is made for young people.","furigana":"ざっし|む|つく"},{"japanese":"父は自分のことをまだヤングだと思っている。","romaji":"Chichi wa jibun no koto o mada yangu da to omotte iru.","german":"My father still thinks of himself as young.","furigana":"ちち|じぶん|おも"},{"japanese":"この店はヤングの客でいつもにぎわっている。","romaji":"Kono mise wa yangu no kyaku de itsumo nigiwatte iru.","german":"This shop is always bustling with young customers.","furigana":"みせ|きゃく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Onomatopoeic adverb for something that bursts out suddenly and loudly: わっと泣き出す (\"to burst into tears\"), わっと笑う (\"to burst into loud laughter\"), わっと歓声が上がる. It also describes many people suddenly rushing somewhere (客がわっと押し寄せる). Often written in katakana ワッと; not to be confused with the loanword ワット (\"watt\", separate card).","examples":[{"japanese":"試合に負けた瞬間、彼女はわっと泣き出した。","romaji":"Shiai ni maketa shunkan, kanojo wa watto nakidashita.","german":"The moment she lost the game, she burst into tears.","furigana":"しあい|ま|しゅんかん|かのじょ|な|だ"},{"japanese":"先生の冗談に、教室中がわっと笑った。","romaji":"Sensei no jōdan ni, kyōshitsu-jū ga watto waratta.","german":"At the teacher's joke, the whole classroom burst out laughing.","furigana":"せんせい|じょうだん|きょうしつじゅう|わら"},{"japanese":"セールが始まると、客がワッと売り場に押し寄せた。","romaji":"Sēru ga hajimaru to, kyaku ga watto uriba ni oshiyoseta.","german":"When the sale began, customers rushed all at once to the sales floor.","furigana":"はじ|きゃく|う|ば|お|よ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Formal, written-language attributive form of 主な (\"main\"): it stands only directly before a noun (主なる目的, 主なる原因) and cannot be used predicatively. In everyday speech and in the news, 主な is almost always used; the adverb is 主に (おもに, separate card). Caution: in 主なる神 (\"God the Lord\"), 主 is read しゅ, and the rare spelling 重なる has nothing to do with かさなる (\"to overlap\").","examples":[{"japanese":"本研究の主なる目的は、地域経済の現状を明らかにすることである。","romaji":"Hon kenkyū no omonaru mokuteki wa, chiiki keizai no genjō o akiraka ni suru koto de aru.","german":"The main purpose of this study is to clarify the current state of the regional economy.","furigana":"ほんけんきゅう|おも|もくてき|ちいきけいざい|げんじょう|あき"},{"japanese":"事故の主なる原因は運転手の不注意であった。","romaji":"Jiko no omonaru gen'in wa untenshu no fuchūi de atta.","german":"The main cause of the accident was the driver's carelessness.","furigana":"じこ|おも|げんいん|うんてんしゅ|ふちゅうい"},{"japanese":"式典には各界の主なる人々が招かれた。","romaji":"Shikiten ni wa kakkai no omonaru hitobito ga manekareta.","german":"Leading figures from all walks of life were invited to the ceremony.","furigana":"しきてん|かっかい|おも|ひとびと|まね"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"やかた is the native, elevated reading of 館 and refers to a manor, a noble residence or a small castle; it is typical of novels, fairy tales and titles (〜の館, 幽霊の館). The Sino-Japanese reading かん (separate cards), by contrast, means a public building or hall and appears almost only in compounds such as 図書館 or 美術館. The spelling 屋形 appears today mainly in 屋形船 (\"covered pleasure boat\").","examples":[{"japanese":"森の奥に古い館が建っている。","romaji":"Mori no oku ni furui yakata ga tatte iru.","german":"An old mansion stands deep in the forest.","furigana":"もり|おく|ふる|やかた|た"},{"japanese":"その館には昔、この地方の領主が住んでいた。","romaji":"Sono yakata ni wa mukashi, kono chihō no ryōshu ga sunde ita.","german":"The lord of this region used to live in that residence.","furigana":"やかた|むかし|ちほう|りょうしゅ|す"},{"japanese":"夏の夜に屋形船で隅田川を下った。","romaji":"Natsu no yoru ni yakatabune de Sumida-gawa o kudatta.","german":"On a summer night, we went down the Sumida River on a covered pleasure boat.","furigana":"なつ|よる|やかたぶね|すみだがわ|くだ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"供 (とも) refers to someone who accompanies a person of higher rank, or the whole retinue; typical are 供をする (\"to accompany\"), 供を連れる and 供の者 (\"attendants\"). The plain 供 sounds historical or literary today; in everyday speech the polite form お供 (おとも, separate card) is used, e.g. お供します. Not to be confused with 友 (とも, \"friend\", separate card).","examples":[{"japanese":"殿様は大勢の供を連れて江戸へ向かった。","romaji":"Tonosama wa ōzei no tomo o tsurete Edo e mukatta.","german":"The lord set out for Edo with a large retinue.","furigana":"とのさま|おおぜい|とも|つ|えど|む"},{"japanese":"若い侍が姫の供をすることになった。","romaji":"Wakai samurai ga hime no tomo o suru koto ni natta.","german":"A young samurai was to accompany the princess.","furigana":"わか|さむらい|ひめ|とも"},{"japanese":"主人の供として、彼は毎日城へ通った。","romaji":"Shujin no tomo to shite, kare wa mainichi shiro e kayotta.","german":"As his master's attendant, he went to the castle every day.","furigana":"しゅじん|とも|かれ|まいにちしろ|かよ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"陣 originally means a battle formation or a field camp, as in 陣を張る (\"to pitch camp, to take up position\") or 背水の陣 (\"with one's back to the wall\"). Today it is mainly productive as a suffix for a group or team: 報道陣 (\"press corps\"), 首脳陣 (\"leadership\"), 投手陣 (\"pitching staff\"). Compounds such as 陣営 (\"camp, faction\") have their own cards.","examples":[{"japanese":"武田軍は川の向こうに陣を張った。","romaji":"Takeda-gun wa kawa no mukō ni jin o hatta.","german":"The Takeda army set up camp on the far side of the river.","furigana":"たけだぐん|かわ|む|じん|は"},{"japanese":"会見場の前には報道陣が大勢詰めかけていた。","romaji":"Kaikenjō no mae ni wa hōdōjin ga ōzei tsumekakete ita.","german":"Numerous reporters were crowded in front of the press conference hall.","furigana":"かいけんじょう|まえ|ほうどうじん|おおぜいつ"},{"japanese":"今年のチームは投手陣が弱いと言われている。","romaji":"Kotoshi no chīmu wa tōshujin ga yowai to iwarete iru.","german":"This year's team is said to have a weak pitching staff.","furigana":"ことし|とうしゅじん|よわ|い"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"有 (ゆう) is an abstract, written-language noun for \"being, existence\" and almost always stands in contrast to 無 (む, separate card): 無から有を生む (\"to create something from nothing\"). As a prefix it means \"with, equipped with\" (有資格者 \"qualified person\"), and (有) is the abbreviation for 有限会社 (\"limited company\"). In everyday speech existence is expressed with ある; 有 alone appears mainly in philosophy and fixed phrases.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は無から有を生み出す天才だ。","romaji":"Kare wa mu kara yū o umidasu tensai da.","german":"He is a genius who creates something out of nothing.","furigana":"かれ|む|ゆう|う|だ|てんさい"},{"japanese":"哲学では、有と無の関係が古くから論じられてきた。","romaji":"Tetsugaku de wa, yū to mu no kankei ga furuku kara ronjirarete kita.","german":"In philosophy, the relation between being and nothingness has been debated since ancient times.","furigana":"てつがく|ゆう|む|かんけい|ふる|ろん"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"スト is the common short form of ストライキ (\"strike\", separate card) and appears mainly in news and headlines. Typical combinations are ストに入る (\"to go on strike\"), ストを行う, ストを打つ and スト決行; in compounds such as 時限スト (\"time-limited strike\") or ゼネスト (\"general strike\") the short form is almost always used. The full ストライキ sounds more explanatory, スト terser and more journalistic.","examples":[{"japanese":"鉄道会社の労働組合が明日からストに入る。","romaji":"Tetsudō-gaisha no rōdō kumiai ga ashita kara suto ni hairu.","german":"The railway company's labor union goes on strike from tomorrow.","furigana":"てつどうがいしゃ|ろうどうくみあい|あした|はい"},{"japanese":"ストの影響で、朝の電車がほとんど動かなかった。","romaji":"Suto no eikyō de, asa no densha ga hotondo ugokanakatta.","german":"Because of the strike, almost no trains ran in the morning.","furigana":"えいきょう|あさ|でんしゃ|うご"},{"japanese":"賃上げを求めて、従業員が24時間のストを決行した。","romaji":"Chin'age o motomete, jūgyōin ga nijūyo-jikan no suto o kekkō shita.","german":"Demanding a wage increase, the employees carried out a 24-hour strike.","furigana":"ちんあ|もと|じゅうぎょういん|じかん|けっこう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Elevated noun with two main uses: \"means, possibility\" in the fixed phrase 知る由もない (\"to have no way of knowing\"), and \"I hear that ...\" in letters: 〜の由, お元気の由 (\"I hear that you are well\"). Outside these patterns it is hardly used; in everyday speech 方法 or 〜そうだ is said. The Sino-Japanese reading is in 理由 and 自由.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼がその時何を考えていたのか、今となっては知る由もない。","romaji":"Kare ga sono toki nani o kangaete ita no ka, ima to natte wa shiru yoshi mo nai.","german":"There is no way to know now what he was thinking at the time.","furigana":"かれ|ときなに|かんが|いま|し|よし"},{"japanese":"ご家族の皆様もお元気の由、何よりと存じます。","romaji":"Go-kazoku no minasama mo o-genki no yoshi, nani yori to zonjimasu.","german":"I hear that your whole family is well as well, which is the best news.","furigana":"かぞく|みなさま|げんき|よし|なに|ぞん"},{"japanese":"来月こちらへお越しになる由、楽しみにしております。","romaji":"Raigetsu kochira e o-koshi ni naru yoshi, tanoshimi ni shite orimasu.","german":"I hear that you will be coming here next month, and I am looking forward to it.","furigana":"らいげつ|こ|よし|たの"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"ゆかり refers to a historical or personal connection to a place or person, typical in 〜ゆかりの地 (\"place associated with ...\") and in the phrase 縁もゆかりもない (\"to have no connection at all\"), in which 縁 is read えん. The word is mostly written in kana; the spelling 縁 is otherwise read えん (\"bond of fate, relationship\") or ふち (\"edge\"), both with their own cards.","examples":[{"japanese":"京都には源氏物語ゆかりの地が多い。","romaji":"Kyōto ni wa Genji monogatari yukari no chi ga ōi.","german":"There are many places in Kyoto connected with The Tale of Genji.","furigana":"きょうと|げんじものがたり|ち|おお"},{"japanese":"縁もゆかりもない町に、仕事で引っ越してきた。","romaji":"En mo yukari mo nai machi ni, shigoto de hikkoshite kita.","german":"I moved for work to a town with which I have no connection whatsoever.","furigana":"えん|まち|しごと|ひ|こ"},{"japanese":"記念館には作家ゆかりの品々が展示されている。","romaji":"Kinenkan ni wa sakka yukari no shinajina ga tenji sarete iru.","german":"Items associated with the writer are on display in the memorial hall.","furigana":"きねんかん|さっか|しなじな|てんじ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"著 appears mainly as a suffix after an author's name and means \"written by\" (山田太郎著); as a noun \"work\" it appears in 〜の著 (\"a work by ...\"). In everyday speech, for an author's book 著書 (ちょしょ, separate card) or 作品 is said; 著 is typical of bibliographic information, book covers and reviews. For translations, 訳 (\"translated by\") often stands next to 著.","examples":[{"japanese":"この本は夏目漱石著の『こころ』です。","romaji":"Kono hon wa Natsume Sōseki-cho no \"Kokoro\" desu.","german":"This book is \"Kokoro\" by Natsume Soseki.","furigana":"ほん|なつめそうせきちょ"},{"japanese":"本書は経済学者の田中氏の著で、十年かけて書かれたものだ。","romaji":"Honsho wa keizai gakusha no Tanaka-shi no cho de, jūnen kakete kakareta mono da.","german":"This book is by the economist Tanaka and was written over ten years.","furigana":"ほんしょ|けいざいがくしゃ|たなかし|ちょ|じゅうねん|か"},{"japanese":"参考文献には「山田一郎著、2020年」と書いてある。","romaji":"Sankō bunken ni wa \"Yamada Ichirō-cho, nisen nijū-nen\" to kaite aru.","german":"The reference list says \"Ichiro Yamada, 2020\".","furigana":"さんこうぶんけん|やまだいちろうちょ|ねん|か"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"As a suffix, 〜寄り denotes a position close to something or a leaning in one direction: 駅寄り (\"closer to the station\"), 北寄りの風 (\"wind from a northerly direction\"), 保守寄り or 左寄り (\"conservative / left-leaning\"). It comes from the verb 寄る (\"to approach\", separate card); it has nothing to do with the comparative particle より (\"than\"). In sumo, 寄り denotes the forward pushing against the opponent in a belt grip (cf. 寄り切り).","examples":[{"japanese":"席は窓寄りの方がいい。","romaji":"Seki wa mado-yori no hō ga ii.","german":"I'd prefer a seat nearer the window.","furigana":"せき|まどよ|ほう"},{"japanese":"明日は北寄りの風が強く吹くでしょう。","romaji":"Ashita wa kita-yori no kaze ga tsuyoku fuku deshō.","german":"Tomorrow, a strong wind from the north is expected.","furigana":"あした|きたよ|かぜ|つよ|ふ"},{"japanese":"あの新聞は政府寄りの記事が多いと批判されている。","romaji":"Ano shinbun wa seifu-yori no kiji ga ōi to hihan sarete iru.","german":"That newspaper is criticized for having many pro-government articles.","furigana":"しんぶん|せいふよ|きじ|おお|ひはん"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Sino-Japanese prefix of technical and written language meaning \"sub-, secondary, subordinate\": 亜熱帯 (\"subtropics\", separate card), 亜種 (\"subspecies\"), 亜寒帯 (\"subarctic zone\"). In chemistry it marks a lower oxidation state (亜硫酸 \"sulfurous acid\"), and 亜流 means pejoratively \"epigone, mere imitation\". Alone, 亜 also stands as an abbreviation for Asia, e.g. in 東亜.","examples":[{"japanese":"沖縄は亜熱帯の気候で、冬でも暖かい。","romaji":"Okinawa wa anettai no kikō de, fuyu demo atatakai.","german":"Okinawa has a subtropical climate and is warm even in winter.","furigana":"おきなわ|あねったい|きこう|ふゆ|あたた"},{"japanese":"この島には珍しい亜種の鳥が生息している。","romaji":"Kono shima ni wa mezurashii ashu no tori ga seisoku shite iru.","german":"A rare subspecies of bird lives on this island.","furigana":"しま|めずら|あしゅ|とり|せいそく"},{"japanese":"彼の作品は有名な画家の亜流にすぎないと評された。","romaji":"Kare no sakuhin wa yūmei na gaka no aryū ni suginai to hyō sareta.","german":"His works were judged to be merely imitations of a famous painter.","furigana":"かれ|さくひん|ゆうめい|がか|ありゅう|ひょう"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Written-language noun for \"reason, cause\", mostly written in kana. It follows nouns with の or verb and adjective forms: 若さゆえの過ち (\"a mistake born of youthful rashness\"), 〜がゆえに (\"precisely because\"), 故あって (\"for certain reasons\"). The sentence-opening conjunction 故に (ゆえに, \"therefore, consequently\") has its own card; in everyday speech ため or から is usually said instead of 故.","examples":[{"japanese":"若さゆえの過ちは、誰にでもあるものだ。","romaji":"Wakasa yue no ayamachi wa, dare ni demo aru mono da.","german":"Mistakes born of youth are something everyone makes.","furigana":"わか|あやま|だれ"},{"japanese":"彼は正直であるがゆえに、損をすることも多い。","romaji":"Kare wa shōjiki de aru ga yue ni, son o suru koto mo ōi.","german":"Precisely because he is honest, he often loses out.","furigana":"かれ|しょうじき|そん|おお"},{"japanese":"故あって、詳しい事情はお話しできません。","romaji":"Yue atte, kuwashii jijō wa o-hanashi dekimasen.","german":"For certain reasons, I cannot tell you the details.","furigana":"ゆえ|くわ|じじょう|はな"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"As an independent noun it is elevated and appears almost only in fixed phrases: 苦にする (\"to take to heart, to feel as a burden\"), 苦もなく (\"effortlessly\"), 苦あれば楽あり (\"after pain comes pleasure\"). For everyday trouble, 苦労 (vocab-n3:269) or 苦しみ is said. Not to be confused with the identically sounding cards 九, 区 and 句.","examples":[{"japanese":"彼は朝早く起きるのを苦にしない。","romaji":"Kare wa asa hayaku okiru no o ku ni shinai.","german":"He doesn't mind getting up early in the morning.","furigana":"かれ|あさはや|お|く"},{"japanese":"彼女は重い荷物を苦もなく持ち上げた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa omoi nimotsu o ku mo naku mochiageta.","german":"She lifted the heavy luggage without any effort.","furigana":"かのじょ|おも|にもつ|く|も|あ"},{"japanese":"苦あれば楽ありと言うから、今は頑張ろう。","romaji":"Ku areba raku ari to iu kara, ima wa ganbarō.","german":"They say that after pain comes pleasure, so let's keep at it now.","furigana":"く|らく|い|いま|がんば"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Classic in 天下を取る (\"to win rule over the country\") and 天下一品 (\"matchless, the best in the land\"). Figuratively, 〜の天下 means \"where ... has the say\", e.g. かかあ天下 (\"the wife wears the pants\"). Do not confuse it with 天下り (あまくだり, vocab-n3:1777, officials moving into private business) and the identically sounding 点火 \"ignition\".","examples":[{"japanese":"豊臣秀吉はついに天下を取った。","romaji":"Toyotomi Hideyoshi wa tsui ni tenka o totta.","german":"Toyotomi Hideyoshi finally took control of the whole country.","furigana":"|てんか|と"},{"japanese":"この店のラーメンは天下一品だ。","romaji":"Kono mise no rāmen wa tenka ippin da.","german":"The ramen at this shop is without equal.","furigana":"みせ|てんかいっぴん"},{"japanese":"家に帰れば妻の天下だ。","romaji":"Ie ni kaereba tsuma no tenka da.","german":"When I get home, my wife rules the roost.","furigana":"いえ|かえ|つま|てんか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"On the stock exchange, ブル stands for rising prices (ブル相場 \"bull market\", opposite ベア相場); in general Japanese 強気相場 is said instead. It is also the short form of ブルドーザー \"bulldozer\" and ブルドッグ \"bulldog\" (e.g. フレンチブル). Do not confuse it with ブルー \"blue\".","examples":[{"japanese":"株式市場ではブル相場が続いている。","romaji":"Kabushiki shijō de wa buru sōba ga tsuzuite iru.","german":"The bull market continues in the stock market.","furigana":"かぶしきしじょう|そうば|つづ"},{"japanese":"工事現場でブルが土を押していた。","romaji":"Kōji genba de buru ga tsuchi o oshite ita.","german":"A bulldozer was pushing earth at the construction site.","furigana":"こうじげんば|つち|お"},{"japanese":"友達がフレンチブルを飼い始めた。","romaji":"Tomodachi ga furenchi buru o kaihajimeta.","german":"A friend has started keeping a French bulldog.","furigana":"ともだち|か|はじ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"As a word in its own right it appears almost only in the meeting formula 決を採る (also 決を取る) \"to bring to a vote, to have a vote taken\". Equivalent in meaning, but more common, is 採決する (vocab-n3:1785 採決); otherwise 決 lives on in compounds such as 多数決, 可決, 否決. Formal, typical of parliament, clubs and conferences.","examples":[{"japanese":"議長は法案について決を採った。","romaji":"Gichō wa hōan ni tsuite ketsu o totta.","german":"The chairman put the bill to a vote.","furigana":"ぎちょう|ほうあん|けつ|と"},{"japanese":"意見が出尽くしたので、そろそろ決を採りましょう。","romaji":"Iken ga detsukushita node, sorosoro ketsu o torimashō.","german":"Since all opinions have been voiced, let's take a vote soon.","furigana":"いけん|でつ|けつ|と"},{"japanese":"学級会で決を採ると、賛成は十五人だった。","romaji":"Gakkyūkai de ketsu o toru to, sansei wa jūgo-nin datta.","german":"When a vote was taken at the class meeting, fifteen were in favor.","furigana":"がっきゅうかい|けつ|と|さんせい|じゅうごにん"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mostly written in kana (むら/ムラ), typically with がある/のない: 色むら \"color patches\", 成績にむらがある \"uneven performance\", 気分にむらがある \"to be moody\". The spelling 斑 with the reading まだら (vocab-n1:3050) is a different word and means \"spotted, mottled\"; 村/むら \"village\" is merely a homophone.","examples":[{"japanese":"壁のペンキの塗り方にむらがある。","romaji":"Kabe no penki no nurikata ni mura ga aru.","german":"The paint on the wall is applied unevenly.","furigana":"かべ|ぬ|かた"},{"japanese":"彼は成績にむらがあって、安定しない。","romaji":"Kare wa seiseki ni mura ga atte, antei shinai.","german":"His grades fluctuate and are not stable.","furigana":"かれ|せいせき|あんてい"},{"japanese":"彼女は気分にムラがあるので付き合いにくい。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kibun ni mura ga aru node tsukiainikui.","german":"She is moody, so she is hard to get along with.","furigana":"かのじょ|きぶん|つ|あ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Technical term for growing up to maturity, also as 成育する. Identical in sound with 生育 (vocab-n1:correction:seiiku), which by newspaper and government usage is rather used for plants, while 成育 is preferred for animals and humans. The general everyday word for growth is 成長 (vocab-n2:149).","examples":[{"japanese":"稚魚は暖かい海で順調に成育している。","romaji":"Chigyo wa atatakai umi de junchō ni seiiku shite iru.","german":"The fry are growing well in the warm sea.","furigana":"ちぎょ|あたた|うみ|じゅんちょう|せいいく"},{"japanese":"子どもの成育には十分な睡眠が欠かせない。","romaji":"Kodomo no seiiku ni wa jūbun na suimin ga kakasenai.","german":"Sufficient sleep is indispensable for the growth of children.","furigana":"こ|せいいく|じゅうぶん|すいみん|か"},{"japanese":"この動物園ではパンダの成育記録をつけている。","romaji":"Kono dōbutsuen de wa panda no seiiku kiroku o tsukete iru.","german":"This zoo keeps records of the pandas' growth.","furigana":"どうぶつえん|せいいくきろく"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Neutral base word of お手数 (vocab-n3:1432), which is used toward others as the polite formula お手数をおかけします; 手数 itself appears in 手数がかかる \"to be troublesome\" and 手数をかける \"to cause trouble\". In board games it denotes the number of moves. There is also the reading てかず; the derivative 手数料 \"fee\" is well known too.","examples":[{"japanese":"この書類の手続きは意外と手数がかかる。","romaji":"Kono shorui no tetsuzuki wa igai to tesū ga kakaru.","german":"The procedure for these documents takes surprisingly much effort.","furigana":"しょるい|てつづ|いがい|てすう"},{"japanese":"人に手数をかけるのは好きじゃない。","romaji":"Hito ni tesū o kakeru no wa suki ja nai.","german":"I don't like causing other people trouble.","furigana":"ひと|てすう|す"},{"japanese":"彼は少ない手数で相手の王を詰ませた。","romaji":"Kare wa sukunai tesū de aite no ō o tsumaseta.","german":"He checkmated the opponent's king with few moves.","furigana":"かれ|すく|てすう|あいて|おう|つ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Interjection said with the index finger in front of the mouth to make someone be quiet; doubled, しっしっ serves to shoo away animals or annoying people. Normally written in kana; the spelling 叱 is ateji and rare. It has nothing to do with 叱る \"to scold\", even though it is the same kanji.","examples":[{"japanese":"しっ、静かに！赤ちゃんが寝てるよ。","romaji":"Shi', shizuka ni! Akachan ga neteru yo.","german":"Shh, be quiet! The baby is sleeping."},{"japanese":"図書館で話していたら、先生に「しっ」と注意された。","romaji":"Toshokan de hanashite itara, sensei ni \"shi'\" to chūi sareta.","german":"When we were talking in the library, the teacher warned us with a \"shh\"."},{"japanese":"庭に入ってきた猫を「しっ、しっ」と追い払った。","romaji":"Niwa ni haitte kita neko o \"shi', shi'\" to oiharatta.","german":"I shooed away the cat that had come into the garden with \"shoo, shoo\"."}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Classical adjective (ku-inflection, attributive 良き), the predecessor of 良い/よい (vocab-n5:71). Today it survives in set phrases: 良しとする \"to approve, to be satisfied with\", 〜も良し \"... is fine too\", and the proverb 終わり良ければすべて良し. Do not confuse it with the interjection よし! \"good, let's go!\", which is written in kana.","examples":[{"japanese":"終わり良ければすべて良し。","romaji":"Owari yokereba subete yoshi.","german":"All's well that ends well.","furigana":"お|よ|よ"},{"japanese":"一人で行くも良し、友達と行くも良し。","romaji":"Hitori de iku mo yoshi, tomodachi to iku mo yoshi.","german":"You can go alone or with friends; either is fine.","furigana":"ひとり|い|よ|ともだち|い|よ"},{"japanese":"上司は彼の勝手な行動を良しとしなかった。","romaji":"Jōshi wa kare no katte na kōdō o yoshi to shinakatta.","german":"His boss did not approve of his arbitrary behavior.","furigana":"じょうし|かれ|かって|こうどう|よ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mimetic word for unsteady swaying, mostly used with する or だ: 熱でふらふらする \"to reel from a fever\", 疲れてふらふらだ \"to be completely exhausted\". With と it also means \"to wander around aimlessly\" (ふらふらと歩き回る) or, figuratively, \"to be indecisive\". Unlike ぐらぐら (objects wobbling), ふらふら describes mainly people and their gait or attitude.","examples":[{"japanese":"熱があって、頭がふらふらする。","romaji":"Netsu ga atte, atama ga furafura suru.","german":"I have a fever and my head is spinning.","furigana":"ねつ|あたま"},{"japanese":"暇だったので、駅前をふらふらと歩き回った。","romaji":"Hima datta node, ekimae o furafura to arukimawatta.","german":"I had nothing to do, so I wandered aimlessly around the station.","furigana":"ひま|えきまえ|ある|まわ"},{"japanese":"彼は考えがいつもフラフラしていて、決められない。","romaji":"Kare wa kangae ga itsumo furafura shite ite, kimerarenai.","german":"He is always wavering in his thinking and cannot make up his mind.","furigana":"かれ|かんが|き"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"ハラハラする describes the anxious tension felt when watching someone or waiting for an outcome (ハラハラさせる \"to keep in suspense\"). Unlike ドキドキ (vocab-n4:1251), which means a pounding heart from excitement or love, ハラハラ is about worry that something will go wrong. With と and usually in hiragana, はらはら depicts blossoms, leaves or tears falling softly.","examples":[{"japanese":"試合は最後までハラハラさせられた。","romaji":"Shiai wa saigo made harahara saserareta.","german":"The game kept us on the edge of our seats until the very end.","furigana":"しあい|さいご"},{"japanese":"子どもが木に登るのを見て、母親はハラハラしていた。","romaji":"Kodomo ga ki ni noboru no o mite, hahaoya wa harahara shite ita.","german":"The mother watched her child climbing the tree with her heart in her mouth.","furigana":"こ|き|のぼ|み|ははおや"},{"japanese":"桜の花びらがはらはらと散っている。","romaji":"Sakura no hanabira ga harahara to chitte iru.","german":"Cherry blossom petals are fluttering down softly.","furigana":"さくら|はな|ち"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Full form of マイク (vocab-n3:3350); in everyday life, at karaoke or on stage people almost always say マイク. マイクロフォン, or the older technical spelling マイクロホン, appears mainly in technical texts, product specifications and standards. There is no difference in meaning.","examples":[{"japanese":"会議室の天井にマイクロフォンが設置されている。","romaji":"Kaigishitsu no tenjō ni maikurofon ga setchi sarete iru.","german":"A microphone is installed in the ceiling of the conference room.","furigana":"かいぎしつ|てんじょう|せっち"},{"japanese":"このノートパソコンにはマイクロホンが内蔵されている。","romaji":"Kono nōto pasokon ni wa maikurohon ga naizō sarete iru.","german":"This laptop has a built-in microphone.","furigana":"ないぞう"},{"japanese":"録音スタジオでは高性能のマイクロフォンを使う。","romaji":"Rokuon sutajio de wa kōseinō no maikurofon o tsukau.","german":"High-performance microphones are used in recording studios.","furigana":"ろくおん|こうせいのう|つか"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Historical word for the fireproof storehouse (蔵) in which lords and merchants kept their money; familiar from samurai dramas, for example 金蔵破り \"breaking into the treasury\". Figuratively also \"financial backer, patron\". Today's safe is called 金庫 (vocab-n3:2721); the reading きんぞう is obsolete.","examples":[{"japanese":"昔の大きな商家には立派な金蔵があった。","romaji":"Mukashi no ōkina shōka ni wa rippa na kanegura ga atta.","german":"The large merchant houses of the past had splendid money storehouses.","furigana":"むかし|おお|しょうか|りっぱ|かねぐら"},{"japanese":"盗賊が殿様の金蔵を破った。","romaji":"Tōzoku ga tonosama no kanegura o yabutta.","german":"Thieves broke into the lord's treasury.","furigana":"とうぞく|とのさま|かねぐら|やぶ"},{"japanese":"彼は長年この劇団の金蔵として支えてきた。","romaji":"Kare wa naganen kono gekidan no kanegura to shite sasaete kita.","german":"He has supported this theater troupe as its financial backer for many years.","furigana":"かれ|ながねん|げきだん|かねぐら|ささ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Read independently as ぬし when it means the \"master\" or \"owner\" of something: 家の主, 声の主 (\"the one the voice belongs to\"), うわさの主. Also typical is the old fish or spirit that has always lived in a lake (沼の主), and, figuratively, the old-timer who has been at a company forever. It appears in many compounds such as 持ち主, 飼い主, 株主, 地主. Do not confuse it with 主/しゅ (lord, God; main thing, e.g. 主として).","examples":[{"japanese":"この沼の主は、百年生きている大きな鯉だと言われている。","romaji":"Kono numa no nushi wa, hyakunen ikite iru ōkina koi da to iwarete iru.","german":"They say the master of this pond is a big carp that has lived for a hundred years.","furigana":"ぬま|ぬし|ひゃくねんい|おお|こい|い"},{"japanese":"電話の声の主が誰なのか、すぐには分からなかった。","romaji":"Denwa no koe no nushi ga dare na no ka, sugu ni wa wakaranakatta.","german":"I could not immediately tell whose voice it was on the phone.","furigana":"でんわ|こえ|ぬし|だれ|わ"},{"japanese":"田中さんは三十年もこの課にいて、まるで主のような存在だ。","romaji":"Tanaka-san wa sanjūnen mo kono ka ni ite, marude nushi no yō na sonzai da.","german":"Mr. Tanaka has been in this section for thirty years and is practically its fixture.","furigana":"たなか|さんじゅうねん|か|ぬし|そんざい"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Loan prefix from English \"my\" that expresses personal ownership in Japanese coinages: マイカー (\"one's own car\"), マイホーム (\"one's own home\"), マイバッグ (\"one's own shopping bag\"), マイペース (\"one's own pace\"). It is used only before loanwords and is not used as an independent word. Do not confuse it with 毎〜/まい (\"every\"), マイク or マイナス.","examples":[{"japanese":"父は毎日マイカーで会社に通っている。","romaji":"Chichi wa mainichi maikā de kaisha ni kayotte iru.","german":"My father commutes to work every day in his own car.","furigana":"ちち|まいにち|かいしゃ|かよ"},{"japanese":"レジ袋が有料になってから、マイバッグを持ち歩くようになった。","romaji":"Rejibukuro ga yūryō ni natte kara, maibaggu o mochiaruku yō ni natta.","german":"Since plastic bags started costing money, I carry my own bag with me.","furigana":"ぶくろ|ゆうりょう|も|ある"},{"japanese":"いつかマイホームを建てるのが夢です。","romaji":"Itsuka maihōmu o tateru no ga yume desu.","german":"My dream is to build a home of my own someday.","furigana":"た|ゆめ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Usually written in kana and used mainly in the forms ことによっては (\"depending on the circumstances, if necessary\") and ことによったら (\"possibly\"). After a verb, 〜ことによる means \"is due to the fact that ...\" (原因は…ことによる). Related to the fixed expression ことによると (\"possibly, perhaps\"), which has its own card.","examples":[{"japanese":"ことによっては、計画を一から見直す必要がある。","romaji":"Koto ni yotte wa, keikaku o ichi kara minaosu hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"Depending on the circumstances, we may have to rethink the plan from scratch.","furigana":"けいかく|いち|みなお|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"ことによったら、来週は休みが取れるかもしれない。","romaji":"Koto ni yottara, raishū wa yasumi ga toreru ka mo shirenai.","german":"Perhaps I can take next week off.","furigana":"らいしゅう|やす|と"},{"japanese":"事故の原因は、運転手が居眠りをしたことによるものだった。","romaji":"Jiko no gen'in wa, untenshu ga inemuri o shita koto ni yoru mono datta.","german":"The cause of the accident was that the driver had dozed off.","furigana":"じこ|げんいん|うんてんしゅ|いねむ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Read せんの in colloquial speech (先の) for \"former, previous\" (先の家, 先の社長); in written, formal language 先の is usually read さきの instead. It is also a technical term for the first move or the initiative in go, shogi and kendo (先を取る). The everyday reading さき (\"ahead, tip, before\") has its own card (先/さき).","examples":[{"japanese":"これは先の家に住んでいたころの写真だよ。","romaji":"Kore wa sen no ie ni sunde ita koro no shashin da yo.","german":"This is a photo from when we lived in our previous house.","furigana":"せん|いえ|す|しゃしん"},{"japanese":"碁では黒が先で打つことになっている。","romaji":"Go de wa kuro ga sen de utsu koto ni natte iru.","german":"In go, Black is supposed to play first.","furigana":"ご|くろ|せん|う"},{"japanese":"剣道では、相手より先を取ることが大切だ。","romaji":"Kendō de wa, aite yori sen o toru koto ga taisetsu da.","german":"In kendo, it is important to take the initiative before your opponent.","furigana":"けんどう|あいて|せん|と|たいせつ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Refers to anger that one does not show openly but carries around inside; mostly used as 中っ腹になる or 中っ腹で (annoyed, huffy). Somewhat old-fashioned and found mostly in novels; also read ちゅっぱら. In everyday speech people more often say むっとする or 腹が立つ.","examples":[{"japanese":"一時間も待たされて、彼はすっかり中っ腹になっていた。","romaji":"Ichijikan mo matasarete, kare wa sukkari chūppara ni natte ita.","german":"After being kept waiting for a whole hour, he was thoroughly irritated inside.","furigana":"いちじかん|ま|かれ|ちゅう|ぱら"},{"japanese":"父は中っ腹で、何も言わずに部屋を出ていった。","romaji":"Chichi wa chūppara de, nani mo iwazu ni heya o dete itta.","german":"My father left the room in a huff without saying a word.","furigana":"ちち|ちゅう|ぱら|なに|い|へや|で"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mainly the top grade in the 優・良・可・不可 rating system used at universities and on older report cards: 優を取る/もらう (\"to get an A\"). The meaning \"gentle, graceful\" (優な) is literary. Do not confuse it with the adverb 優に (\"easily, at least\") or with 優しい (やさしい) and 優れる (すぐれる).","examples":[{"japanese":"この授業で優を取るのは、なかなか難しい。","romaji":"Kono jugyō de yū o toru no wa, nakanaka muzukashii.","german":"It is quite difficult to get an A in this class.","furigana":"じゅぎょう|ゆう|と|むずか"},{"japanese":"この大学では、成績は優・良・可・不可の四段階で評価される。","romaji":"Kono daigaku de wa, seiseki wa yū, ryō, ka, fuka no yondankai de hyōka sareru.","german":"At this university, grades are rated on four levels: excellent, good, pass, fail.","furigana":"だいがく|せいせき|ゆう|りょう|か|ふか|よんだんかい|ひょうか"},{"japanese":"奨学金を続けてもらうには、半分以上の科目で優が必要だ。","romaji":"Shōgakukin o tsuzukete morau ni wa, hanbun ijō no kamoku de yū ga hitsuyō da.","german":"To keep the scholarship, you need an A in more than half of your subjects.","furigana":"しょうがくきん|つづ|はんぶんいじょう|かもく|ゆう|ひつよう"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A day that is inauspicious according to the calendar or fortune-telling, on which people avoid weddings, moves or starting a business; the opposite of 吉日 (\"lucky day\"). あくにち is the usual reading; the secondary reading あくび has its own card but is rare, also because あくび otherwise means \"yawn\" (欠伸).","examples":[{"japanese":"今日は悪日だから、引っ越しは来週にしよう。","romaji":"Kyō wa akunichi da kara, hikkoshi wa raishū ni shiyō.","german":"Today is an unlucky day, so let's move next week.","furigana":"きょう|あくにち|ひ|こ|らいしゅう"},{"japanese":"祖母は暦で悪日を避けて、結婚式の日取りを決めた。","romaji":"Sobo wa koyomi de akunichi o sakete, kekkonshiki no hidori o kimeta.","german":"My grandmother chose the wedding date by the calendar so as to avoid unlucky days.","furigana":"そぼ|こよみ|あくにち|さ|けっこんしき|ひど|き"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Has several separate meanings: the hard shell of turtles and crabs (亀の甲), the back of the hand or instep (手の甲, 足の甲), and the first rank in the series 甲・乙・丙. In contracts, 甲 and 乙 stand for the first and second contracting party. Cf. 甲乙 (\"difference in rank, A and B\"), e.g. 甲乙つけがたい.","examples":[{"japanese":"契約書では、売主を甲、買主を乙とする。","romaji":"Keiyakusho de wa, urinushi o kō, kainushi o otsu to suru.","german":"In the contract, the seller is designated Party A and the buyer Party B.","furigana":"けいやくしょ|うりぬし|こう|かいぬし|おつ"},{"japanese":"転んで手の甲をすりむいてしまった。","romaji":"Koronde te no kō o surimuite shimatta.","german":"I fell and scraped the back of my hand.","furigana":"ころ|て|こう"},{"japanese":"「亀の甲より年の功」と言うように、経験は大事だ。","romaji":"\"Kame no kō yori toshi no kō\" to iu yō ni, keiken wa daiji da.","german":"As the saying goes, \"Age beats a turtle's shell\": experience is important.","furigana":"かめ|こう|とし|こう|い|けいけん|だいじ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"A doubled \"no\" used to wave something off or to modestly decline (e.g. in response to praise or thanks). The spelling 否々 is rare and literary; in everyday life it is almost always written いやいや or, more politely, いえいえ (JMdict lists both readings). Do not confuse it with いやいや/嫌々 (\"reluctantly, unwillingly\"), which has its own card.","examples":[{"japanese":"「本当にありがとうございました」「否々、大したことはしていません」","romaji":"\"Hontō ni arigatō gozaimashita\" \"Iya iya, taishita koto wa shite imasen\"","german":"\"Thank you so much.\" \"Oh no, I didn't do anything special.\"","furigana":"ほんとう|いやいや|たい"},{"japanese":"否々、それは君の思い違いだよ。","romaji":"Iya iya, sore wa kimi no omoichigai da yo.","german":"No, no, you are mistaken there.","furigana":"いやいや|きみ|おも|ちが"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Onomatopoeic adverb for repeated hard striking: swords meeting, axe blows when felling trees, the clack of go stones; usually with と (丁々と). Literary, and today known mainly from the four-character compound 丁々発止 (\"heated exchange of blows\", also in debates). Do not confuse it with the homophone 蝶々 (\"butterfly\").","examples":[{"japanese":"山の奥から、丁々と木を切る音が響いてきた。","romaji":"Yama no oku kara, chōchō to ki o kiru oto ga hibiite kita.","german":"From deep in the mountains came the sound of wood being chopped.","furigana":"やま|おく|ちょうちょう|き|き|おと|ひび"},{"japanese":"二人の議員は、国会で丁々発止の議論を繰り広げた。","romaji":"Futari no giin wa, kokkai de chōchō hasshi no giron o kurihirogeta.","german":"The two lawmakers engaged in a heated debate in the Diet.","furigana":"ふたり|ぎいん|こっかい|ちょうちょうはっし|ぎろん|く|ひろ"},{"japanese":"静かな和室に、碁石を打つ丁々という音だけが聞こえる。","romaji":"Shizuka na washitsu ni, goishi o utsu chōchō to iu oto dake ga kikoeru.","german":"In the quiet tatami room, only the clack of go stones can be heard.","furigana":"しず|わしつ|ごいし|う|ちょうちょう|おと|き"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"notes":"Refers to a narrow, strip-like edge: the cloth border of a tatami mat (畳の縁), the brim of a hat, a hem, or the edge of a path or river. Often written in kana as へり. 縁/ふち is the more general word for the edge or rim of round things (眼鏡の縁, 崖の縁); 縁/えん, on the other hand, means \"fateful bond, relationship\".","examples":[{"japanese":"畳の縁を踏むのは行儀が悪いとされている。","romaji":"Tatami no heri o fumu no wa gyōgi ga warui to sarete iru.","german":"Stepping on the border of a tatami mat is considered bad manners.","furigana":"たたみ|へり|ふ|ぎょうぎ|わる"},{"japanese":"母は帽子の縁に小さなリボンを縫い付けた。","romaji":"Haha wa bōshi no heri ni chiisana ribon o nuitsuketa.","german":"My mother sewed a small ribbon onto the brim of the hat.","furigana":"はは|ぼうし|へり|ちい|ぬ|つ"},{"japanese":"子どもたちは川の縁に座って、足を水に浸していた。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa kawa no heri ni suwatte, ashi o mizu ni hitashite ita.","german":"The children sat on the bank of the river, dipping their feet in the water.","furigana":"こ|かわ|へり|すわ|あし|みず|ひた"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Mainly in references to laws and contracts: 第〇条 (\"Article ...\"), e.g. 憲法第9条 (\"Article 9 of the Constitution\"); the subdivision below it is called 項. As a literary counter for strips and lines (一条の光 \"a ray of light\"). It appears in 条件, 条約, 条例 and 信条.","examples":[{"japanese":"憲法第九条について、授業で議論した。","romaji":"Kenpō daikyūjō ni tsuite, jugyō de giron shita.","german":"We discussed Article 9 of the Constitution in class.","furigana":"けんぽうだいきゅうじょう|じゅぎょう|ぎろん"},{"japanese":"契約書の第五条に、解約の条件が書いてある。","romaji":"Keiyakusho no daigojō ni, kaiyaku no jōken ga kaite aru.","german":"Article 5 of the contract states the conditions for cancellation.","furigana":"けいやくしょ|だいごじょう|かいやく|じょうけん|か"},{"japanese":"雲の間から、一条の光が差し込んだ。","romaji":"Kumo no aida kara, ichijō no hikari ga sashikonda.","german":"A single ray of light shone in between the clouds.","furigana":"くも|あいだ|いちじょう|ひかり|さ|こ"}],"levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"notes":"Traditional unit of length: 一丈 = 十尺 (approx. 3.03 m), usually with a numeral such as 一丈, 三丈; familiar from 丈六 \"Buddha statue of 1 jō 6 shaku\". As a suffix after the names of kabuki actors, 丈 expresses respect. Do not confuse it with the kanji in the reading たけ (\"height, length\", e.g. 身の丈) or with 丈夫 (じょうぶ \"sturdy\"). Obsolete in everyday use; today mostly in historical and literary texts.","examples":[{"japanese":"昔の単位では、一丈は十尺で、約三メートルにあたる。","romaji":"Mukashi no tan'i de wa, ichijō wa jusshaku de, yaku san-mētoru ni ataru.","german":"In the old units of measurement, one jō is ten shaku, about three meters.","furigana":"むかし|たんい|いちじょう|じゅっしゃく|やくさん"},{"japanese":"奈良の古い寺には、丈六の仏像が安置されている。","romaji":"Nara no furui tera ni wa, jōroku no butsuzō ga anchi sarete iru.","german":"In an old temple in Nara, a Buddha statue one jō and six shaku tall is enshrined.","furigana":"なら|ふる|てら|じょうろく|ぶつぞう|あんち"},{"japanese":"劇評では、歌舞伎役者の名前の後に丈を付けて敬意を表す。","romaji":"Gekihyō de wa, kabuki yakusha no namae no ato ni jō o tsukete keii o arawasu.","german":"In theater reviews, 丈 is attached after the names of kabuki actors to show respect.","furigana":"げきひょう|かぶきやくしゃ|なまえ|あと|じょう|つ|けいい|あらわ"}],"levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一期一会の精神で人と接する。","romaji":"Ichigo ichie no seishin de hito to sessuru.","german":"I treat people in the spirit of ichigo ichie, treasuring each encounter.","furigana":"いちごいちえ|せいしん|ひと|せっ"},{"japanese":"旅先での一期一会を大切にしたい。","romaji":"Tabisaki de no ichigo ichie o taisetsu ni shitai.","german":"I want to cherish the once-in-a-lifetime encounters I have while traveling.","furigana":"たびさき|いちごいちえ|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"店主は一期一会の気持ちで、毎日お客様を迎えている。","romaji":"Tenshu wa ichigo ichie no kimochi de, mainichi okyakusama o mukaete iru.","german":"The shop owner welcomes customers every day in the spirit that each encounter happens only once.","furigana":"てんしゅ|いちごいちえ|きも|まいにち|きゃくさま|むか"}],"notes":"一期一会 is composed of 一期 (いちご) \"a whole lifetime\" and 一会 (いちえ) \"one encounter\": every encounter occurs in one's life only once in just this way. The expression comes from the tea ceremony and urges one to honor every meeting with full attention and hospitality. Typical are 一期一会の出会い, 一期一会を大切にする and 一期一会の精神. It sounds elevated, but as a life motto it is also popular in everyday life (travel, farewells, the restaurant trade).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"温故知新の姿勢で研究を進める。","romaji":"Onko chishin no shisei de kenkyū o susumeru.","german":"He carries out his research in the spirit of learning the new from the old.","furigana":"おんこちしん|しせい|けんきゅう|すす"},{"japanese":"古典を読むことは温故知新だ。","romaji":"Koten o yomu koto wa onko chishin da.","german":"Reading the classics is learning the new from the old.","furigana":"こてん|よ|おんこちしん"},{"japanese":"昔の町並みを生かした街づくりは、まさに温故知新の発想だ。","romaji":"Mukashi no machinami o ikashita machizukuri wa, masa ni onko chishin no hassō da.","german":"Urban development that makes use of the old townscape is exactly this idea of learning the new from the old.","furigana":"むかし|まちな|い|まち|おんこちしん|はっそう"}],"notes":"温故知新 goes back to the Analects of Confucius: 故きを温ねて新しきを知る (\"to study the old and learn the new from it\"). It means gaining insights for the present from history and tradition. Typical are 温故知新の精神/姿勢 and まさに温故知新だ. It is elevated in tone and often appears in speeches, school mottos or advertising copy.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"勉強しなかったから不合格は自業自得だ。","romaji":"Benkyō shinakatta kara fugōkaku wa jigō jitoku da.","german":"Failing the exam is his own fault, since he didn't study.","furigana":"べんきょう|ふごうかく|じごうじとく"},{"japanese":"夜更かししすぎて風邪を引いたのは、自業自得だよ。","romaji":"Yofukashi shisugite kaze o hiita no wa, jigō jitoku da yo.","german":"You caught a cold from staying up too late all the time; you brought it on yourself.","furigana":"よふ|かぜ|ひ|じごうじとく"},{"japanese":"自業自得だけど、会社を辞めることになった彼が少しかわいそうだ。","romaji":"Jigō jitoku da kedo, kaisha o yameru koto ni natta kare ga sukoshi kawaisō da.","german":"It's his own doing, but I feel a little sorry for him having to leave the company.","furigana":"じごうじとく|かいしゃ|や|かれ|すこ"}],"notes":"自業自得 comes from Buddhism: 自業 \"one's own deeds (karma)\" and 自得 \"to receive oneself\" - one experiences the consequences of one's own actions. It is used almost only for bad consequences, often coolly or reproachfully: それは自業自得だ \"You brought that on yourself\". Typical frames are 〜のは自業自得だ and 自業自得だけど (with some sympathy). Said directly to the person concerned, it comes across as harsh.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ビジネスの世界は弱肉強食だ。","romaji":"Bijinesu no sekai wa jakuniku kyōshoku da.","german":"The business world is dog-eat-dog.","furigana":"せかい|じゃくにくきょうしょく"},{"japanese":"自然界は弱肉強食で、弱い動物はすぐに食べられてしまう。","romaji":"Shizenkai wa jakuniku kyōshoku de, yowai dōbutsu wa sugu ni taberarete shimau.","german":"In nature the law of the jungle applies; weak animals are quickly eaten.","furigana":"しぜんかい|じゃくにくきょうしょく|よわ|どうぶつ|た"},{"japanese":"弱肉強食の競争社会に疑問を持つ若者も多い。","romaji":"Jakuniku kyōshoku no kyōsō shakai ni gimon o motsu wakamono mo ōi.","german":"Many young people have doubts about a competitive society ruled by survival of the fittest.","furigana":"じゃくにくきょうしょく|きょうそうしゃかい|ぎもん|も|わかもの|おお"}],"notes":"弱肉強食 literally means \"the flesh of the weak is the food of the strong\" (弱/肉/強/食). It describes a world in which the stronger displaces the weaker, originally in nature, and by extension in business, sports or society. Typical are 弱肉強食の世界/社会 and 〜は弱肉強食だ; it often carries a note of criticism or resignation.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"油断大敵だから、気を抜かないで。","romaji":"Yudan taiteki da kara, ki o nukanaide.","german":"Carelessness is your worst enemy, so don't let your guard down.","furigana":"ゆだんたいてき|き|ぬ"},{"japanese":"三点リードしていても油断大敵だ。最後まで集中しよう。","romaji":"San-ten rīdo shite ite mo yudan taiteki da. Saigo made shūchū shiyō.","german":"Even with a three-point lead, we can't get complacent. Let's stay focused until the end.","furigana":"さんてん|ゆだんたいてき|さいご|しゅうちゅう"},{"japanese":"治りかけの風邪こそ油断大敵です。","romaji":"Naorikake no kaze koso yudan taiteki desu.","german":"A cold that is almost over is exactly when you must not be careless.","furigana":"なお|かぜ|ゆだんたいてき"}],"notes":"油断大敵 combines 油断 (\"carelessness, negligence\") with 大敵 (\"great enemy\"): anyone who feels too safe makes mistakes. It is usually used as a stand-alone warning, often with a reason before it or an exhortation after it: 最後まで油断大敵だ, 油断大敵だから気を抜くな. It is a common saying in sports, at school and in health contexts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"健康のためにストレスを感じるのは本末転倒だ。","romaji":"Kenkō no tame ni sutoresu o kanjiru no wa honmatsu tentō da.","german":"Stressing yourself out for the sake of your health is putting the cart before the horse.","furigana":"けんこう|かん|ほんまつてんとう"},{"japanese":"お金のために働きすぎて体を壊したら、本末転倒だ。","romaji":"Okane no tame ni hatarakisugite karada o kowashitara, honmatsu tentō da.","german":"If you work too hard for money and ruin your health, you've got it backward.","furigana":"かね|はたら|からだ|こわ|ほんまつてんとう"},{"japanese":"点数ばかり気にして内容を理解しないのは本末転倒です。","romaji":"Tensū bakari ki ni shite naiyō o rikai shinai no wa honmatsu tentō desu.","german":"Caring only about scores without understanding the content is putting the cart before the horse.","furigana":"てんすう|き|ないよう|りかい|ほんまつてんとう"}],"notes":"Literally: 本 \"root, the main thing\" and 末 \"tip, the side issue\" are 転倒 \"reversed, turned upside down\". It means that one confuses means and ends, or the important and the unimportant. Typical is the frame 〜のは本末転倒だ or 〜たら本末転倒だ, often as a critical comment on absurd behavior. Neutral to slightly elevated, equally usual in conversation and in writing.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"政府の朝令暮改に国民は混乱している。","romaji":"Seifu no chōrei bokai ni kokumin wa konran shite iru.","german":"The public is confused by the government's constant flip-flopping.","furigana":"せいふ|ちょうれいぼかい|こくみん|こんらん"},{"japanese":"社長の指示が朝令暮改で、現場は振り回されている。","romaji":"Shachō no shiji ga chōrei bokai de, genba wa furimawasarete iru.","german":"The president's instructions keep changing, and the staff on site are being jerked around.","furigana":"しゃちょう|しじ|ちょうれいぼかい|げんば|ふ|まわ"},{"japanese":"ルールを朝令暮改するようでは、誰も従わなくなる。","romaji":"Rūru o chōrei bokai suru yō de wa, dare mo shitagawanaku naru.","german":"If the rules keep being changed, nobody will follow them anymore.","furigana":"ちょうれいぼかい|だれ|したが"}],"notes":"朝令暮改 literally means \"to give an order in the morning (朝令), to change it in the evening (暮改)\". It criticizes governments, authorities or superiors whose instructions and rules change constantly and without a clear line: 朝令暮改の政策, 方針が朝令暮改だ, also as a する-verb 朝令暮改する. In business language it is occasionally used positively for flexible course corrections, but the basic tone remains reproachful and literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は品行方正な学生だ。","romaji":"Kare wa hinkō hōsei na gakusei da.","german":"He is a student of impeccable conduct.","furigana":"かれ|ひんこうほうせい|がくせい"},{"japanese":"品行方正だと思われていた政治家が、汚職で逮捕された。","romaji":"Hinkō hōsei da to omowarete ita seijika ga, oshoku de taiho sareta.","german":"A politician who had been considered upright was arrested for corruption.","furigana":"ひんこうほうせい|おも|せいじか|おしょく|たいほ"},{"japanese":"品行方正な兄と違って、弟はいたずらばかりしていた。","romaji":"Hinkō hōsei na ani to chigatte, otōto wa itazura bakari shite ita.","german":"Unlike his exemplary older brother, the younger one was always playing pranks.","furigana":"ひんこうほうせい|あに|ちが|おとうと"}],"notes":"品行方正 combines 品行 (\"conduct, way of life\") with 方正 (\"upright, correct\") and describes a blameless, morally impeccable person. It is used as a na-adjective or with の: 品行方正な生徒, 品行方正で知られる. The expression is elevated and sounds somewhat stiff; it is also used ironically for model students or for people whose spotless reputation turns out to be false.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"食べ物の好みは十人十色だ。","romaji":"Tabemono no konomi wa jūnin toiro da.","german":"When it comes to food, everyone has their own taste.","furigana":"た|もの|この|じゅうにんといろ"},{"japanese":"勉強のやり方は十人十色だから、自分に合う方法を見つけてください。","romaji":"Benkyō no yarikata wa jūnin toiro da kara, jibun ni au hōhō o mitsukete kudasai.","german":"Everyone studies in their own way, so please find the method that suits you.","furigana":"べんきょう|かた|じゅうにんといろ|じぶん|あ|ほうほう|み"},{"japanese":"同じ映画を見ても、感想は十人十色だった。","romaji":"Onaji eiga o mite mo, kansō wa jūnin toiro datta.","german":"Even though everyone watched the same film, the impressions were all different.","furigana":"おな|えいが|み|かんそう|じゅうにんといろ"}],"notes":"十人十色 literally means \"ten people, ten colors\": every person has their own preferences, views and peculiarities. It is used to calmly state differences, mostly in the pattern 〜は十人十色だ (好み・考え方・生き方は十人十色). Note the reading: 十色 is read といろ here, not じゅういろ. In casual conversation the same thing is often simply said with 人それぞれ \"everyone is different\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は独立独歩の精神を持っている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa dokuritsu doppo no seishin o motte iru.","german":"She has a spirit of independence and self-reliance.","furigana":"かのじょ|どくりつどっぽ|せいしん|も"},{"japanese":"彼は親の援助を受けず、独立独歩で会社を立ち上げた。","romaji":"Kare wa oya no enjo o ukezu, dokuritsu doppo de kaisha o tachiageta.","german":"Without help from his parents, he started a company entirely on his own.","furigana":"かれ|おや|えんじょ|う|どくりつどっぽ|かいしゃ|た|あ"},{"japanese":"その会社は大手の系列に入らず、独立独歩の経営を続けている。","romaji":"Sono kaisha wa ōte no keiretsu ni hairazu, dokuritsu doppo no keiei o tsuzukete iru.","german":"The company does not join any large corporate group and continues to run its business independently.","furigana":"かいしゃ|おおて|けいれつ|はい|どくりつどっぽ|けいえい|つづ"}],"notes":"独立独歩 combines 独立 (\"independence\") with 独歩 (\"walking alone\"): one does not rely on others but goes one's own way by one's own strength. Typical are 独立独歩の精神/人生 and 独立独歩で〜する; also used of companies that belong to no conglomerate. The expression is praising and rather literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"付和雷同せず、自分の意見を持つべきだ。","romaji":"Fuwa raidō sezu, jibun no iken o motsu beki da.","german":"You should have your own opinion and not just follow the crowd.","furigana":"ふわらいどう|じぶん|いけん|も"},{"japanese":"周りの意見に付和雷同するばかりでは、議論の意味がない。","romaji":"Mawari no iken ni fuwa raidō suru bakari de wa, giron no imi ga nai.","german":"If everyone just goes along with other people's opinions, there is no point in a discussion.","furigana":"まわ|いけん|ふわらいどう|ぎろん|いみ"},{"japanese":"ネット上の付和雷同的な批判には注意が必要だ。","romaji":"Netto-jō no fuwa raidō-teki na hihan ni wa chūi ga hitsuyō da.","german":"One must be careful about the herd-like criticism online.","furigana":"じょう|ふわらいどうてき|ひはん|ちゅうい|ひつよう"}],"notes":"付和雷同 consists of 付和 (\"to join in\") and 雷同 (\"to echo like thunder\"): one simply agrees with the majority or a loud voice without any conviction of one's own. It is an intransitive する-verb (〜に付和雷同する) and clearly pejorative; it often appears negated as an admonition: 付和雷同せず. The tone is literary; in colloquial speech people more often say 周りに流される.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の主張は我田引水だ。","romaji":"Kare no shuchō wa gaden insui da.","german":"His argument is self-serving.","furigana":"かれ|しゅちょう|がでんいんすい"},{"japanese":"データを自分に都合よく解釈するのは我田引水だ。","romaji":"Dēta o jibun ni tsugō yoku kaishaku suru no wa gaden insui da.","german":"Interpreting data in a way that suits oneself is self-serving.","furigana":"じぶん|つごう|かいしゃく|がでんいんすい"},{"japanese":"各党が我田引水の議論を繰り返し、改革は進まなかった。","romaji":"Kakutō ga gaden insui no giron o kurikaeshi, kaikaku wa susumanakatta.","german":"Each party kept repeating arguments in its own interest, and the reform made no progress.","furigana":"かくとう|がでんいんすい|ぎろん|く|かえ|かいかく|すす"}],"notes":"我田引水 literally means \"to channel water into one's own (我) rice field (田) (引水)\". It means interpreting arguments, data or rules so that they serve only one's own advantage. Typical are 我田引水の議論/解釈 and 〜は我田引水だ. The expression is critical and rather literary, for example in commentary on politics and business.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ダイエットを始めたが、三日坊主で終わった。","romaji":"Daietto o hajimeta ga, mikka bōzu de owatta.","german":"I started a diet, but it ended after three days.","furigana":"はじ|みっかぼうず|お"},{"japanese":"三日坊主にならないように頑張る。","romaji":"Mikka bōzu ni naranai yō ni ganbaru.","german":"I'll try hard not to give up after three days.","furigana":"みっかぼうず|がんば"},{"japanese":"日記を書き始めても、私はいつも三日坊主です。","romaji":"Nikki o kakihajimete mo, watashi wa itsumo mikka bōzu desu.","german":"Even if I start keeping a diary, I always quit after a few days.","furigana":"にっき|か|はじ|わたし|みっかぼうず"}],"notes":"Literally \"three-day monk\": someone who becomes a monk and gives up after three days. It refers to a person or behavior lacking perseverance, a \"flash in the pan\". Typical frames are 三日坊主で終わる \"to stop after a short time\" and 三日坊主になる; often used self-ironically about diets, diaries or exercise. Colloquial and slightly humorous.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自画自賛になるが、このケーキは美味しくできた。","romaji":"Jiga jisan ni naru ga, kono kēki wa oishiku dekita.","german":"This may sound like bragging, but this cake turned out delicious.","furigana":"じがじさん|おい"},{"japanese":"社長は新商品の出来を自画自賛していた。","romaji":"Shachō wa shin-shōhin no deki o jiga jisan shite ita.","german":"The president praised the quality of the new product to the skies.","furigana":"しゃちょう|しんしょうひん|でき|じがじさん"},{"japanese":"自画自賛ばかりしていると、周りから信用されなくなる。","romaji":"Jiga jisan bakari shite iru to, mawari kara shin'yō sarenaku naru.","german":"If you keep praising yourself, others will stop trusting you.","furigana":"じがじさん|まわ|しんよう"}],"notes":"自画自賛 literally means to write a eulogy (賛) on one's own painting (自画), that is, self-praise. It is a する-verb (〜を自画自賛する) and is often placed in front self-deprecatingly: 自画自賛になりますが / 自画自賛ですが, similar to \"not to toot my own horn\". Said of others, it sounds critical. Similar is 手前味噌.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女の説明は理路整然としていた。","romaji":"Kanojo no setsumei wa riro seizen to shite ita.","german":"Her explanation was logical and well-organized.","furigana":"かのじょ|せつめい|りろせいぜん"},{"japanese":"彼は緊張していたが、理路整然と自分の考えを述べた。","romaji":"Kare wa kinchō shite ita ga, riro seizen to jibun no kangae o nobeta.","german":"Although he was nervous, he laid out his thoughts clearly and logically.","furigana":"かれ|きんちょう|りろせいぜん|じぶん|かんが|の"},{"japanese":"理路整然とした文章は読みやすい。","romaji":"Riro seizen to shita bunshō wa yomiyasui.","german":"Logically structured writing is easy to read.","furigana":"りろせいぜん|ぶんしょう|よ"}],"notes":"理路整然 combines 理路 (\"line of reasoning, logic\") with 整然 (\"orderly\"). It almost always appears with と: 理路整然と話す/説明する or attributively 理路整然とした説明/文章. The opposite is 支離滅裂 (\"incoherent, disjointed\"). The expression is praising and rather elevated.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"散歩しながら買い物もできて一石二鳥だ。","romaji":"Sanpo shinagara kaimono mo dekite isseki nichō da.","german":"Beim Spazierengehen gleich noch einkaufen – so schlägt man zwei Fliegen mit einer Klappe.","furigana":"さんぽ|か|もの|いっせきにちょう"},{"japanese":"日本語の映画を見ると、勉強にもなるし、楽しめるし、一石二鳥だ。","romaji":"Nihongo no eiga o miru to, benkyō ni mo naru shi, tanoshimeru shi, isseki nichō da.","german":"Japanische Filme zu schauen bringt Lernen und Spaß zugleich – zwei Fliegen mit einer Klappe.","furigana":"にほんご|えいが|み|べんきょう|たの|いっせきにちょう"},{"japanese":"自転車通勤に変えたら、運動不足も解消できて、交通費も浮いた。まさに一石二鳥だ。","romaji":"Jitensha tsūkin ni kaetara, undō busoku mo kaishō dekite, kōtsūhi mo uita. Masa ni isseki nichō da.","german":"Seit ich mit dem Rad zur Arbeit fahre, bewege ich mich genug und spare noch das Fahrgeld. Echt zwei Fliegen mit einer Klappe.","furigana":"じてんしゃつうきん|か|うんどうぶそく|かいしょう|こうつうひ|う|いっせきにちょう"}],"notes":"一石二鳥 heißt wörtlich „ein Stein, zwei Vögel“: Mit einem einzigen Wurf trifft man zwei Vögel. Der Ausdruck gilt als Übersetzung des englischen „to kill two birds with one stone“ und entspricht dem deutschen „zwei Fliegen mit einer Klappe schlagen“. Typische Rahmen sind 〜て一石二鳥だ, 一石二鳥の方法 und まさに一石二鳥. Ein bedeutungsgleicher älterer Ausdruck ist 一挙両得; das Gegenstück, bei dem man beides verfehlt, ist 二兎を追う者は一兎をも得ず.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は不言実行の人だ。","romaji":"Kare wa fugen jikkō no hito da.","german":"He is a man of action rather than words.","furigana":"かれ|ふげんじっこう|ひと"},{"japanese":"彼女は目標を口にせず、不言実行で資格を取った。","romaji":"Kanojo wa mokuhyō o kuchi ni sezu, fugen jikkō de shikaku o totta.","german":"She did not talk about her goal and quietly earned the qualification.","furigana":"かのじょ|もくひょう|くち|ふげんじっこう|しかく|と"},{"japanese":"不言実行もいいが、チームでは報告も大切だ。","romaji":"Fugen jikkō mo ii ga, chīmu de wa hōkoku mo taisetsu da.","german":"Deeds over words is all well and good, but in a team reporting matters too.","furigana":"ふげんじっこう|ほうこく|たいせつ"}],"notes":"不言実行 literally means \"not speaking (不言) but carrying out (実行)\": one does not announce things loudly but simply gets them done. Typical are 不言実行の人 and 不言実行で〜する; it is praise. The counterpart 有言実行 (\"announce and then also carry out\") was coined later and is now just as common.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"単刀直入に聞きますが、賛成ですか？","romaji":"Tantō chokunyū ni kikimasu ga, sansei desu ka?","german":"I'll ask you directly: do you agree?","furigana":"たんとうちょくにゅう|き|さんせい"},{"japanese":"単刀直入に言うと、この計画には反対です。","romaji":"Tantō chokunyū ni iu to, kono keikaku ni wa hantai desu.","german":"To put it bluntly, I am against this plan.","furigana":"たんとうちょくにゅう|い|けいかく|はんたい"},{"japanese":"彼女の単刀直入な物言いは、誤解されることもある。","romaji":"Kanojo no tantō chokunyū na monoii wa, gokai sareru koto mo aru.","german":"Her straightforward way of speaking is sometimes misunderstood.","furigana":"かのじょ|たんとうちょくにゅう|ものい|ごかい"}],"notes":"単刀直入 literally means \"to charge straight in with a single sword (単刀) (直入)\", that is, to get straight to the point without preamble. Most common is 単刀直入に言う/聞く, often as an introduction: 単刀直入に言うと / 単刀直入に伺いますが. Attributively: 単刀直入な質問/物言い. It can be used neutrally to politely; it announces that something direct is about to follow.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"適材適所の人事配置が重要だ。","romaji":"Tekizai tekisho no jinji haichi ga jūyō da.","german":"Placing the right people in the right positions is important.","furigana":"てきざいてきしょ|じんじはいち|じゅうよう"},{"japanese":"新しい部長は社員を適材適所に配置した。","romaji":"Atarashii buchō wa shain o tekizai tekisho ni haichi shita.","german":"The new department head assigned each employee to the position that suited them best.","furigana":"あたら|ぶちょう|しゃいん|てきざいてきしょ|はいち"},{"japanese":"文化祭では、絵が得意な人にポスターを任せるなど、適材適所で役割を決めた。","romaji":"Bunkasai de wa, e ga tokui na hito ni posutā o makaseru nado, tekizai tekisho de yakuwari o kimeta.","german":"For the school festival, we decided roles according to aptitude, for example leaving the posters to someone good at drawing.","furigana":"ぶんかさい|え|とくい|ひと|まか|てきざいてきしょ|やくわり|き"}],"notes":"適材適所 literally means \"the suitable timber (適材) in the suitable place (適所)\", originally from carpentry. It means deploying people according to their abilities. Typical are 適材適所の人事/配置 and 適材適所に配置する; used mainly in companies, administration and teamwork.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"天変地異に備えて防災グッズを用意した。","romaji":"Tenpen chii ni sonaete bōsai guzzu o yōi shita.","german":"I prepared disaster-preparedness supplies in case of natural disasters.","furigana":"てんぺんちい|そな|ぼうさい|ようい"},{"japanese":"昔の人々は、天変地異を神の怒りだと考えた。","romaji":"Mukashi no hitobito wa, tenpen chii o kami no ikari da to kangaeta.","german":"People in the past considered natural disasters to be the wrath of the gods.","furigana":"むかし|ひとびと|てんぺんちい|かみ|いか|かんが"},{"japanese":"天変地異でも起きない限り、試合は予定どおり行われる。","romaji":"Tenpen chii demo okinai kagiri, shiai wa yotei dōri okonawareru.","german":"Unless some cataclysm happens, the game will be held as scheduled.","furigana":"てんぺんちい|お|かぎ|しあい|よてい|おこな"}],"notes":"天変地異 combines 天変 (\"unusual celestial phenomena\" such as storms or eclipses) with 地異 (\"upheavals of the earth\" such as earthquakes or floods). It denotes great, extraordinary natural events and sounds weightier than 自然災害. The exaggeration 天変地異でも起きない限り (\"unless the world ends\") is also common. The tone is literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"長年の友達とは以心伝心で通じ合える。","romaji":"Naganen no tomodachi to wa ishin denshin de tsūjiaeru.","german":"With old friends, you can understand each other without words.","furigana":"ながねん|ともだち|いしんでんしん|つう|あ"},{"japanese":"何も言わなくても分かり合える。以心伝心だね。","romaji":"Nani mo iwanakute mo wakariaeru. Ishin denshin da ne.","german":"We understand each other without saying anything - real telepathy.","furigana":"なに|い|わ|あ|いしんでんしん"},{"japanese":"仕事では以心伝心に頼らず、きちんと言葉で伝えることが大切だ。","romaji":"Shigoto de wa ishin denshin ni tayorazu, kichinto kotoba de tsutaeru koto ga taisetsu da.","german":"At work, you should not rely on unspoken understanding but state things clearly in words.","furigana":"しごと|いしんでんしん|たよ|ことば|つた|たいせつ"}],"notes":"以心伝心 literally means \"to transmit (伝心) from heart to heart (以心)\" and comes from Zen Buddhism, where the teaching was passed from master to disciple without words. Today it means \"tacit understanding, comprehension without words\" between close friends, couples or well-coordinated colleagues. Typical patterns: 以心伝心で通じる, 以心伝心の仲. Critically, it is often said that in companies one should not rely on 以心伝心.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"半信半疑で話を聞いていた。","romaji":"Hanshin hangi de hanashi o kiite ita.","german":"I listened to the story half believing, half doubting.","furigana":"はんしんはんぎ|はなし|き"},{"japanese":"合格の知らせを受けても、まだ半信半疑だった。","romaji":"Gōkaku no shirase o ukete mo, mada hanshin hangi datta.","german":"Even after receiving the news that I had passed, I still could not quite believe it.","furigana":"ごうかく|し|う|はんしんはんぎ"},{"japanese":"半信半疑で試した薬が、意外とよく効いた。","romaji":"Hanshin hangi de tameshita kusuri ga, igai to yoku kiita.","german":"The medicine I tried skeptically worked surprisingly well.","furigana":"はんしんはんぎ|ため|くすり|いがい|き"}],"notes":"半信半疑 means \"half believing, half doubting\". Typical are 半信半疑で聞く/試す, 半信半疑の顔 and 半信半疑だった. It describes skepticism toward surprising news or a promise, often with the undertone that it turns out to be true after all. The expression is neutral and common in everyday speech.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"試験の結果に一喜一憂する。","romaji":"Shiken no kekka ni ikki ichiyū suru.","german":"I swing between joy and worry over the exam results.","furigana":"しけん|けっか|いっきいちゆう"},{"japanese":"株価の上下に一喜一憂しないほうがいい。","romaji":"Kabuka no jōge ni ikki ichiyū shinai hō ga ii.","german":"You shouldn't get worked up over the ups and downs of stock prices.","furigana":"かぶか|じょうげ|いっきいちゆう"},{"japanese":"選挙速報を見ながら、陣営は一喜一憂していた。","romaji":"Senkyo sokuhō o minagara, jin'ei wa ikki ichiyū shite ita.","german":"Watching the election projections, the campaign team swung between elation and anxiety.","furigana":"せんきょそくほう|み|じんえい|いっきいちゆう"}],"notes":"一喜一憂 literally means \"now joy, now worry\": one reacts to every small change alternately with joy and worry. It is an intransitive する-verb used with に: 結果に一喜一憂する. It often appears negated as advice to stay calm: 〜に一喜一憂しない(ほうがいい). Neutral and common in everyday speech and in the media.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"腹痛で七転八倒した。","romaji":"Fukutsū de shichiten battō shita.","german":"I writhed in agony from a stomachache.","furigana":"ふくつう|しちてんばっとう"},{"japanese":"歯の痛みで一晩中七転八倒の苦しみを味わった。","romaji":"Ha no itami de hitobanjū shichiten battō no kurushimi o ajiwatta.","german":"I suffered agonizing toothache pain all night long.","furigana":"は|いた|ひとばんじゅうしちてんばっとう|くる|あじ"},{"japanese":"締め切り前は、原稿が書けずに七転八倒していた。","romaji":"Shimekiri mae wa, genkō ga kakezu ni shichiten battō shite ita.","german":"Before the deadline, I was agonizing because I couldn't get the manuscript written.","furigana":"し|き|まえ|げんこう|か|しちてんばっとう"}],"notes":"七転八倒 literally means \"to roll over seven times, to fall eight times\" and describes writhing in violent pain: 七転八倒する, 七転八倒の苦しみ. Figuratively also for desperately struggling with a task. Do not confuse it with 七転び八起き (\"to fall down and get up again and again\"), which praises perseverance. The reading しってんばっとう is also widespread.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いくら注意しても馬耳東風だ。","romaji":"Ikura chūi shite mo baji tōfū da.","german":"No matter how often you warn him, it goes in one ear and out the other.","furigana":"ちゅうい|ばじとうふう"},{"japanese":"彼は批判を馬耳東風と聞き流して、自分のやり方を貫いた。","romaji":"Kare wa hihan o baji tōfū to kikinagashite, jibun no yarikata o tsuranuita.","german":"He let the criticism go in one ear and out the other and stuck to his own way.","furigana":"かれ|ひはん|ばじとうふう|き|なが|じぶん|かた|つらぬ"},{"japanese":"医者の忠告も馬耳東風で、父はまだたばこをやめない。","romaji":"Isha no chūkoku mo baji tōfū de, chichi wa mada tabako o yamenai.","german":"Even the doctor's advice falls on deaf ears; my father still hasn't quit smoking.","furigana":"いしゃ|ちゅうこく|ばじとうふう|ちち"}],"notes":"馬耳東風 literally means \"east wind in a horse's ear\" (after a poem by Li Bai): just as a horse does not notice the mild spring wind, someone completely ignores advice or criticism. Typical are 〜も馬耳東風だ and 馬耳東風と聞き流す. Synonymous but more colloquial is 馬の耳に念仏. The tone is mildly reproachful.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"全員が異口同音に賛成した。","romaji":"Zen'in ga iku dōon ni sansei shita.","german":"Everyone agreed unanimously.","furigana":"ぜんいん|いくどうおん|さんせい"},{"japanese":"参加者は異口同音に「楽しかった」と答えた。","romaji":"Sankasha wa iku dōon ni \"tanoshikatta\" to kotaeta.","german":"The participants answered with one voice, \"It was fun.\"","furigana":"さんかしゃ|いくどうおん|たの|こた"},{"japanese":"専門家たちは異口同音に、早めの対策が必要だと指摘している。","romaji":"Senmonka-tachi wa iku dōon ni, hayame no taisaku ga hitsuyō da to shiteki shite iru.","german":"Experts are unanimously pointing out that early countermeasures are needed.","furigana":"せんもんか|いくどうおん|はや|たいさく|ひつよう|してき"}],"notes":"異口同音 means \"different mouths (異口), the same sound (同音)\": many people say the same thing independently of one another. Almost always adverbial with に: 異口同音に言う/答える/賛成する. Watch the reading: いくどうおん, not いこうどうおん. Neutral to literary, common in reports.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"どの案も大同小異だ。","romaji":"Dono an mo daidō shōi da.","german":"All the proposals are much the same.","furigana":"あん|だいどうしょうい"},{"japanese":"各社のスマホは、機能の面では大同小異になってきた。","romaji":"Kakusha no sumaho wa, kinō no men de wa daidō shōi ni natte kita.","german":"Smartphones from different companies have become more or less identical in terms of functions.","furigana":"かくしゃ|きのう|めん|だいどうしょうい"},{"japanese":"二人の意見は大同小異で、すぐにまとまった。","romaji":"Futari no iken wa daidō shōi de, sugu ni matomatta.","german":"The two of them held nearly the same opinion, so they quickly reached an agreement.","furigana":"ふたり|いけん|だいどうしょうい"}],"notes":"大同小異 means \"the same in the large (大同), different in the small (小異)\". It states that several things hardly differ, often with a slightly disappointed undertone: どれも大同小異だ. It is used predicatively with だ, as 大同小異の〜 or 大同小異になる. Similar but more colloquial is 似たり寄ったり.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その委員会は有名無実だ。","romaji":"Sono iinkai wa yūmei mujitsu da.","german":"That committee exists in name only.","furigana":"いいんかい|ゆうめいむじつ"},{"japanese":"罰則がないので、その規則は有名無実化している。","romaji":"Bassoku ga nai node, sono kisoku wa yūmei mujitsu-ka shite iru.","german":"Since there are no penalties, the rule has become a dead letter.","furigana":"ばっそく|きそく|ゆうめいむじつか"},{"japanese":"肩書きは部長だが、実際は有名無実のポストだ。","romaji":"Katagaki wa buchō da ga, jissai wa yūmei mujitsu no posuto da.","german":"The title is department head, but in reality it is a post without any authority.","furigana":"かたが|ぶちょう|じっさい|ゆうめいむじつ"}],"notes":"有名無実 means \"the name (名) exists, the substance (実) is missing\"; 有名 has nothing to do with \"famous\" here. It describes rules, bodies or titles that exist only on paper: 有名無実の規則/ポスト, 〜は有名無実だ. Very common is 有名無実化する (\"to become an empty shell\"). Critical and literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"起死回生のホームランで逆転した。","romaji":"Kishi kaisei no hōmuran de gyakuten shita.","german":"He turned the game around with a game-saving home run.","furigana":"きしかいせい|ぎゃくてん"},{"japanese":"会社は起死回生をねらって、新しい商品を発売した。","romaji":"Kaisha wa kishi kaisei o neratte, atarashii shōhin o hatsubai shita.","german":"Hoping to rescue itself from the crisis, the company launched a new product.","furigana":"かいしゃ|きしかいせい|あたら|しょうひん|はつばい"},{"japanese":"最後の起死回生の作戦も、結局失敗に終わった。","romaji":"Saigo no kishi kaisei no sakusen mo, kekkyoku shippai ni owatta.","german":"The final last-ditch strategy also ended in failure.","furigana":"さいご|きしかいせい|さくせん|けっきょくしっぱい|お"}],"notes":"Literally: 起死 \"to wake the dying\" and 回生 \"to bring back to life\". It means a rescue or turnaround from a seemingly hopeless situation. Typically attributive: 起死回生の一打, 起死回生のホームラン, 起死回生の策; also 起死回生を狙う／図る. Common in sports and business reports; rather elevated to journalistic.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の天気予報は百発百中だ。","romaji":"Kare no tenki yohō wa hyappatsu hyakuchū da.","german":"His weather forecasts are always right.","furigana":"かれ|てんきよほう|ひゃっぱつひゃくちゅう"},{"japanese":"練習を重ねて、彼女のシュートは百発百中になった。","romaji":"Renshū o kasanete, kanojo no shūto wa hyappatsu hyakuchū ni natta.","german":"After lots of practice, every one of her shots goes in.","furigana":"れんしゅう|かさ|かのじょ|ひゃっぱつひゃくちゅう"},{"japanese":"占いが百発百中なんてことはありえない。","romaji":"Uranai ga hyappatsu hyakuchū nante koto wa arienai.","german":"It's impossible for fortune-telling to be right every time.","furigana":"うらな|ひゃっぱつひゃくちゅう"}],"notes":"Literally: \"a hundred shots, a hundred hits\". Originally from archery, today used for someone or something that always hits or never errs - predictions, throws, tips. Typical as the predicate 百発百中だ or 百発百中になる, also adverbially 百発百中で当たる. Neutral, often appreciative or, in negated form, skeptical.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼のキャリアは順風満帆だ。","romaji":"Kare no kyaria wa junpū manpan da.","german":"His career is going smoothly.","furigana":"かれ|じゅんぷうまんぱん"},{"japanese":"創業から順風満帆だったわけではない。","romaji":"Sōgyō kara junpū manpan datta wake de wa nai.","german":"It is not the case that things have gone smoothly since the founding.","furigana":"そうぎょう|じゅんぷうまんぱん"},{"japanese":"二人の新婚生活は順風満帆に見えた。","romaji":"Futari no shinkon seikatsu wa junpū manpan ni mieta.","german":"The couple's newlywed life seemed to be going smoothly.","furigana":"ふたり|しんこんせいかつ|じゅんぷうまんぱん|み"}],"notes":"順風満帆 means \"tailwind (順風) with full sails (満帆)\": everything goes smoothly and successfully. Typical for life, career, marriage or companies: 順風満帆な人生, 順風満帆だ. Very often negated to hint at difficulties: 順風満帆ではなかった. Reading まんぱん, not まんぽ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"これは千載一遇のチャンスだ。","romaji":"Kore wa senzai ichigū no chansu da.","german":"This is a once-in-a-lifetime chance.","furigana":"せんざいいちぐう"},{"japanese":"憧れの監督と仕事ができるなんて、千載一遇の好機だ。","romaji":"Akogare no kantoku to shigoto ga dekiru nante, senzai ichigū no kōki da.","german":"Getting to work with the director I admire is a golden opportunity.","furigana":"あこが|かんとく|しごと|せんざいいちぐう|こうき"},{"japanese":"千載一遇のチャンスを逃して、彼はひどく後悔した。","romaji":"Senzai ichigū no chansu o nogashite, kare wa hidoku kōkai shita.","german":"He deeply regretted missing the chance of a lifetime.","furigana":"せんざいいちぐう|のが|かれ|こうかい"}],"notes":"千載一遇 literally means \"to meet once (一遇) in a thousand years (千載)\". It appears almost exclusively before nouns such as チャンス, 好機 or 機会: 千載一遇のチャンス. Typical verbs are 掴む (\"to seize\") and 逃す (\"to miss\"). A common writing mistake: 一隅 instead of 一遇.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"外国語は一朝一夕には身につかない。","romaji":"Gaikokugo wa itchō isseki ni wa mi ni tsukanai.","german":"A foreign language cannot be mastered overnight.","furigana":"がいこくご|いっちょういっせき|み"},{"japanese":"信頼関係は一朝一夕に築けるものではない。","romaji":"Shinrai kankei wa itchō isseki ni kizukeru mono de wa nai.","german":"A relationship of trust cannot be built overnight.","furigana":"しんらいかんけい|いっちょういっせき|きず"},{"japanese":"町の伝統は一朝一夕にできたものではなく、何百年もかけて育まれた。","romaji":"Machi no dentō wa itchō isseki ni dekita mono de wa naku, nanbyaku-nen mo kakete hagukumareta.","german":"The town's traditions were not created overnight but nurtured over hundreds of years.","furigana":"まち|でんとう|いっちょういっせき|なんびゃくねん|はぐく"}],"notes":"一朝一夕 means \"one morning, one evening\", that is, a very short time. It appears almost always in negated sentences: 一朝一夕には身につかない, 一朝一夕にできるものではない (\"not overnight\"). Typical topics are languages, skills, trust and traditions. The expression is slightly elevated.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一心不乱に勉強した。","romaji":"Isshin furan ni benkyō shita.","german":"I studied with total concentration.","furigana":"いっしんふらん|べんきょう"},{"japanese":"子どもたちは一心不乱に砂の城を作っていた。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa isshin furan ni suna no shiro o tsukutte ita.","german":"The children were building a sand castle, completely absorbed.","furigana":"こ|いっしんふらん|すな|しろ|つく"},{"japanese":"彼は一心不乱にピアノの練習に打ち込んだ。","romaji":"Kare wa isshin furan ni piano no renshū ni uchikonda.","german":"He devoted himself wholeheartedly to practicing the piano.","furigana":"かれ|いっしんふらん|れんしゅう|う|こ"}],"notes":"一心不乱 means \"one heart (一心), not distracted (不乱)\": completely absorbed in an activity without letting oneself be disturbed. It almost always appears adverbially: 一心不乱に勉強する/祈る/働く. Similar are 無我夢中 (more like being lost in one's zeal) and 脇目も振らず. Neutral, praising.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"絶体絶命のピンチに追い込まれた。","romaji":"Zettai zetsumei no pinchi ni oikomareta.","german":"I was driven into a desperate predicament.","furigana":"ぜったいぜつめい|お|こ"},{"japanese":"道に迷い、携帯の電池も切れて、絶体絶命だと思った。","romaji":"Michi ni mayoi, keitai no denchi mo kirete, zettai zetsumei da to omotta.","german":"I had lost my way and my phone battery was dead - I thought it was all over.","furigana":"みち|まよ|けいたい|でんち|き|ぜったいぜつめい|おも"},{"japanese":"チームは絶体絶命の状況から逆転勝ちした。","romaji":"Chīmu wa zettai zetsumei no jōkyō kara gyakutengachi shita.","german":"The team came back to win from a seemingly hopeless situation.","furigana":"ぜったいぜつめい|じょうきょう|ぎゃくてんが"}],"notes":"Literally: 絶体 and 絶命 - \"the body at its end, life at its end\" (from astrology). It means a seemingly hopeless situation. Very common is 絶体絶命のピンチ, also 絶体絶命の状況／危機 and 絶体絶命だ. Note: it is written 絶体, not 絶対 - a classic writing mistake. Neutral, often dramatizing in sports, thrillers and everyday storytelling.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"前代未聞の事件が起きた。","romaji":"Zendai mimon no jiken ga okita.","german":"An unprecedented incident occurred.","furigana":"ぜんだいみもん|じけん|お"},{"japanese":"大臣が会議に三時間も遅れるなんて、前代未聞だ。","romaji":"Daijin ga kaigi ni sanjikan mo okureru nante, zendai mimon da.","german":"For a minister to be three hours late to a meeting is unheard of.","furigana":"だいじん|かいぎ|さんじかん|おく|ぜんだいみもん"},{"japanese":"前代未聞の大雪で、電車がすべて止まった。","romaji":"Zendai mimon no ōyuki de, densha ga subete tomatta.","german":"Because of a snowfall unlike any before, all the trains stopped.","furigana":"ぜんだいみもん|おおゆき|でんしゃ|と"}],"notes":"Literally: 前代 \"earlier ages\" and 未聞 \"never heard of\". It means something completely unprecedented, mostly in a negative or indignant sense (scandals, mistakes, accidents), more rarely appreciative. Typical are 前代未聞の事件／出来事 and 〜なんて前代未聞だ. Somewhat elevated; similar is 空前絶後, which stresses even more strongly \"unique for all time\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"古い本を二束三文で売った。","romaji":"Furui hon o nisoku sanmon de utta.","german":"I sold my old books for next to nothing.","furigana":"ふる|ほん|にそくさんもん|う"},{"japanese":"祖父の土地は、売ろうとしても二束三文にしかならない。","romaji":"Sofu no tochi wa, urō to shite mo nisoku sanmon ni shika naranai.","german":"Even if I tried to sell my grandfather's land, it would fetch only a pittance.","furigana":"そふ|とち|う|にそくさんもん"},{"japanese":"高かったゲーム機も、今では二束三文で買いたたかれる。","romaji":"Takakatta gēmuki mo, ima de wa nisoku sanmon de kaitatakareru.","german":"Even the game console that was once expensive would now be haggled down to a pittance.","furigana":"たか|き|いま|にそくさんもん|か"}],"notes":"二束三文 literally means \"two bundles for three mon\" (an old copper coin), that is, a bargain-basement price. It is mostly used with selling: 二束三文で売る, 二束三文で買いたたかれる, 二束三文にしかならない, or attributively 二束三文の値段/土地. The tone is colloquial and idiomatic and often regretful.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の奇想天外なアイデアに驚いた。","romaji":"Kare no kisō tengai na aidea ni odoroita.","german":"I was surprised by his wildly original idea.","furigana":"かれ|きそうてんがい|おどろ"},{"japanese":"その映画は奇想天外なストーリーで話題になった。","romaji":"Sono eiga wa kisō tengai na sutōrī de wadai ni natta.","german":"The film became a talking point for its outlandish story.","furigana":"えいが|きそうてんがい|わだい"},{"japanese":"子どもの発想は、時に奇想天外で大人を驚かせる。","romaji":"Kodomo no hassō wa, toki ni kisō tengai de otona o odorokaseru.","german":"Children's ideas are sometimes so unconventional that they amaze adults.","furigana":"こ|はっそう|とき|きそうてんがい|おとな|おどろ"}],"notes":"奇想天外 combines 奇想 (\"strange idea\") with 天外 (\"beyond the heavens\"): an idea nobody else would come up with. It is a na-adjective: 奇想天外なアイデア/発想/ストーリー. Mostly positive and astonished about surprising creativity, but it can also suggest \"far-fetched\". Neutral, common in reviews and advertising.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"引っ越しの準備に四苦八苦している。","romaji":"Hikkoshi no junbi ni shiku hakku shite iru.","german":"I'm struggling with the preparations for moving.","furigana":"ひ|こ|じゅんび|しくはっく"},{"japanese":"毎月の家賃を払うのに四苦八苦している。","romaji":"Maitsuki no yachin o harau no ni shiku hakku shite iru.","german":"I struggle to pay the rent every month.","furigana":"まいつき|やちん|はら|しくはっく"},{"japanese":"慣れない敬語に四苦八苦しました。","romaji":"Narenai keigo ni shiku hakku shimashita.","german":"I struggled quite a bit with the unfamiliar honorific language.","furigana":"な|けいご|しくはっく"}],"notes":"Literally: \"four sufferings, eight sufferings\" - a Buddhist term for the basic sufferings of life (birth, old age, illness, death and four more). Today it means to struggle hard with something, often with money or a difficult task. Typical is 〜に四苦八苦する or 〜のに四苦八苦する. Colloquial to neutral, with a slightly humorous tone.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼はいつも針小棒大に話す。","romaji":"Kare wa itsumo shinshō bōdai ni hanasu.","german":"He always exaggerates when he talks.","furigana":"かれ|しんしょうぼうだい|はな"},{"japanese":"週刊誌は小さな失言を針小棒大に書き立てた。","romaji":"Shūkanshi wa chiisana shitsugen o shinshō bōdai ni kakitateta.","german":"The weekly magazine blew a minor slip of the tongue out of all proportion.","furigana":"しゅうかんし|ちい|しつげん|しんしょうぼうだい|か|た"},{"japanese":"ちょっと転んだだけなのに、母は針小棒大に近所に言いふらした。","romaji":"Chotto koronda dake na no ni, haha wa shinshō bōdai ni kinjo ni iifurashita.","german":"I only fell down a little, but my mother made a huge fuss of it all over the neighborhood.","furigana":"ころ|はは|しんしょうぼうだい|きんじょ|い"}],"notes":"Literally \"needle small, stick big\": something as small as a needle is presented as big as a stick. 針小棒大 criticizes the excessive exaggeration of trifles, almost always in the form 針小棒大に言う／話す／報じる／書き立てる. A neutral everyday expression for it is 大げさに言う or 話を盛る; 針小棒大 is more literary and sounds reproachful.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"危機一髪で電車に間に合った。","romaji":"Kiki ippatsu de densha ni ma ni atta.","german":"I caught the train by a hair's breadth.","furigana":"ききいっぱつ|でんしゃ|ま|あ"},{"japanese":"危機一髪のところで助けられた。","romaji":"Kiki ippatsu no tokoro de tasukerareta.","german":"I was rescued at the last moment.","furigana":"ききいっぱつ|たす"},{"japanese":"ブレーキが間に合って、危機一髪で事故を避けられた。","romaji":"Burēki ga ma ni atte, kiki ippatsu de jiko o sakerareta.","german":"The brakes worked in time, and an accident was narrowly avoided.","furigana":"ま|あ|ききいっぱつ|じこ|さ"}],"notes":"Literally: 危機 \"crisis, danger\" and 一髪 \"one (single) hair\" - the danger hangs by a hair. It means an extremely narrow rescue or a success that comes just in time. Typical are 危機一髪で助かる／間に合う and 危機一髪のところで. Note: 危機一発 is a common writing mistake. Neutral, often in storytelling.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このプロジェクトは竜頭蛇尾に終わった。","romaji":"Kono purojekuto wa ryūtō dabi ni owatta.","german":"This project ended in an anticlimax.","furigana":"りゅうとうだび|お"},{"japanese":"前半は面白かったのに、最終回は竜頭蛇尾だった。","romaji":"Zenhan wa omoshirokatta no ni, saishūkai wa ryūtō dabi datta.","german":"The first half was interesting, but the final episode was a letdown.","furigana":"ぜんはん|おもしろ|さいしゅうかい|りゅうとうだび"},{"japanese":"改革が竜頭蛇尾にならないよう、最後まで気を抜かないでください。","romaji":"Kaikaku ga ryūtō dabi ni naranai yō, saigo made ki o nukanaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't let your guard down until the end so that the reform does not fizzle out.","furigana":"かいかく|りゅうとうだび|さいご|き|ぬ"}],"notes":"Literally \"dragon head, snake tail\": something begins big and magnificent and ends miserably. Typical are 竜頭蛇尾に終わる and 竜頭蛇尾だ／になる, referring to projects, reforms, novels or series. Also written 龍頭蛇尾 and occasionally read りょうとうだび. The opposite, a consistently strong effort to the very end, is described with expressions such as 初志貫徹 or 有終の美を飾る.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"地震の後、住民は右往左往していた。","romaji":"Jishin no ato, jūmin wa uō saō shite ita.","german":"After the earthquake, the residents were running around in confusion.","furigana":"じしん|あと|じゅうみん|うおうさおう"},{"japanese":"突然の変更に右往左往する社員たち。","romaji":"Totsuzen no henkō ni uō saō suru shain-tachi.","german":"Employees flustered by the sudden change.","furigana":"とつぜん|へんこう|うおうさおう|しゃいん"},{"japanese":"会場の場所が分からず、駅の周りで右往左往した。","romaji":"Kaijō no basho ga wakarazu, eki no mawari de uō saō shita.","german":"I didn't know where the venue was and wandered around the station in confusion.","furigana":"かいじょう|ばしょ|わ|えき|まわ|うおうさおう"}],"notes":"Literally: 右へ往き、左へ往く - \"to go to the right, to go to the left\". It means headless, confused running back and forth, mostly out of panic or helplessness. As a する-verb: 右往左往する; often with the trigger marked by に (突然の変更に右往左往する). Neutral, slightly critical or vividly descriptive.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二人は初対面で意気投合した。","romaji":"Futari wa shotaimen de iki tōgō shita.","german":"The two hit it off at their first meeting.","furigana":"ふたり|しょたいめん|いきとうごう"},{"japanese":"共通の趣味があり、意気投合した。","romaji":"Kyōtsū no shumi ga ari, iki tōgō shita.","german":"We had a shared hobby and hit it off.","furigana":"きょうつう|しゅみ|いきとうごう"},{"japanese":"居酒屋で隣の席の客と意気投合して、連絡先を交換した。","romaji":"Izakaya de tonari no seki no kyaku to iki tōgō shite, renrakusaki o kōkan shita.","german":"At the izakaya, I hit it off with the customer at the next seat, and we exchanged contact information.","furigana":"いざかや|となり|せき|きゃく|いきとうごう|れんらくさき|こうかん"}],"notes":"Literally: 意気 \"spirit, disposition\" and 投合 \"to agree, to match\". It means that people understand each other at once and are on the same wavelength. Typical: 〜と意気投合する or 二人は意気投合した, often with an occasion such as 初対面で or 共通の趣味で. Neutral and positive, common when telling how one got to know someone.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"試験に合格して、意気揚々と帰ってきた。","romaji":"Shiken ni gōkaku shite, iki yōyō to kaette kita.","german":"He came back triumphantly after passing the exam.","furigana":"しけん|ごうかく|いきようよう|かえ"},{"japanese":"優勝チームは意気揚々とパレードした。","romaji":"Yūshō chīmu wa iki yōyō to parēdo shita.","german":"The winning team paraded proudly.","furigana":"ゆうしょう|いきようよう"},{"japanese":"リーダーに選ばれた彼は、意気揚々と会議室に入ってきた。","romaji":"Rīdā ni erabareta kare wa, iki yōyō to kaigishitsu ni haitte kita.","german":"Having been chosen as leader, he strode into the meeting room in high spirits.","furigana":"えら|かれ|いきようよう|かいぎしつ|はい"}],"notes":"Literally: 意気 \"spirit, courage\" and 揚々 \"raised high, proud\". It means a triumphant, confident bearing after a success or full of confidence. Almost always adverbial as 意気揚々と + a verb of motion (帰る, 引き上げる, 入ってくる). Slightly elevated; can also be used ironically for exaggerated confidence of victory.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"警察は犯罪グループを一網打尽にした。","romaji":"Keisatsu wa hanzai gurūpu o ichimō dajin ni shita.","german":"The police rounded up the criminal group in one sweep.","furigana":"けいさつ|はんざい|いちもうだじん"},{"japanese":"害虫を一網打尽に駆除する方法。","romaji":"Gaichū o ichimō dajin ni kujo suru hōhō.","german":"A method for exterminating pests all at once.","furigana":"がいちゅう|いちもうだじん|くじょ|ほうほう"},{"japanese":"ゲームで必殺技を使って、敵を一網打尽にした。","romaji":"Gēmu de hissatsuwaza o tsukatte, teki o ichimō dajin ni shita.","german":"In the game, I used a special attack and wiped out all the enemies at once.","furigana":"ひっさつわざ|つか|てき|いちもうだじん"}],"notes":"Literally \"to catch everything with a single cast of the net\" (一網 \"one net\", 打尽 \"to capture completely\"). Figuratively it means catching a whole group in one go, typically criminals in a raid. Fixed frames are 〜を一網打尽にする and 〜が一網打尽になる. Rather literary and known from the news; jokingly also used for games or pests.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"グラフを見れば、結果は一目瞭然だ。","romaji":"Gurafu o mireba, kekka wa ichimoku ryōzen da.","german":"If you look at the graph, the result is obvious at a glance.","furigana":"み|けっか|いちもくりょうぜん"},{"japanese":"彼の才能は一目瞭然である。","romaji":"Kare no sainō wa ichimoku ryōzen de aru.","german":"His talent is obvious.","furigana":"かれ|さいのう|いちもくりょうぜん"},{"japanese":"どちらが本物か、並べてみれば一目瞭然です。","romaji":"Dochira ga honmono ka, narabete mireba ichimoku ryōzen desu.","german":"Which one is genuine is obvious if you put them side by side.","furigana":"ほんもの|なら|いちもくりょうぜん"}],"notes":"Literally: 一目 \"one glance\" and 瞭然 \"clear, distinct\". It means that something is obvious at first sight, often in comparisons or from graphs. Typical are 〜は一目瞭然だ and 〜ば一目瞭然だ (見れば, 比べれば). Neutral to slightly elevated, common in presentations, advertising and explanations.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"運動しながら音楽を聴くのは一挙両得だ。","romaji":"Undō shinagara ongaku o kiku no wa ikkyo ryōtoku da.","german":"Listening to music while exercising kills two birds with one stone.","furigana":"うんどう|おんがく|き|いっきょりょうとく"},{"japanese":"この方法なら時間も費用も節約できて一挙両得だ。","romaji":"Kono hōhō nara jikan mo hiyō mo setsuyaku dekite ikkyo ryōtoku da.","german":"With this method you can save both time and money - two benefits at once.","furigana":"ほうほう|じかん|ひよう|せつやく|いっきょりょうとく"}],"notes":"Literally: 一挙 \"a single action\" and 両得 \"double gain\". It means achieving two advantages at the same time with one measure - \"killing two birds with one stone\". Mostly as the predicate 〜は一挙両得だ. Synonymous and much more common in everyday speech is 一石二鳥; 一挙両得 sounds somewhat more elevated.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"社長は問題を一刀両断に解決した。","romaji":"Shachō wa mondai o ittō ryōdan ni kaiketsu shita.","german":"The company president resolved the problem decisively.","furigana":"しゃちょう|もんだい|いっとうりょうだん|かいけつ"},{"japanese":"彼女はその提案を一刀両断に断った。","romaji":"Kanojo wa sono teian o ittō ryōdan ni kotowatta.","german":"She turned down the proposal flatly.","furigana":"かのじょ|ていあん|いっとうりょうだん|ことわ"},{"japanese":"侍は竹を一刀両断にした。","romaji":"Samurai wa take o ittō ryōdan ni shita.","german":"The samurai cut the bamboo in two with a single stroke.","furigana":"さむらい|たけ|いっとうりょうだん"}],"notes":"Literally \"in two pieces with one sword stroke\" (一刀 \"one sword (stroke)\", 両断 \"to cut in two\"). Figuratively it means dealing with a problem or opinion decisively and without hesitation, e.g. 一刀両断に解決する or 一刀両断に切り捨てる. Often also in 〜を一刀両断する about critics who tear an argument apart in a single sentence. Rather literary and vivid.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの政治家は海千山千のしたたか者だ。","romaji":"Ano seijika wa umisen yamasen no shitataka-mono da.","german":"That politician is a shrewd old hand.","furigana":"せいじか|うみせんやません|もの"},{"japanese":"海千山千の交渉人に気をつけろ。","romaji":"Umisen yamasen no kōshōnin ni ki o tsukero.","german":"Watch out for that cunning negotiator.","furigana":"うみせんやません|こうしょうにん|き"},{"japanese":"相手は海千山千の古物商だから、簡単には値引きしてくれないよ。","romaji":"Aite wa umisen yamasen no kobutsushō da kara, kantan ni wa nebiki shite kurenai yo.","german":"The dealer in second-hand goods is an old fox, so he won't give you a discount easily.","furigana":"あいて|うみせんやません|こぶつしょう|かんたん|ねび"}],"notes":"According to legend, a snake that has lived a thousand years in the sea and a thousand years in the mountains becomes a dragon. 海千山千 therefore denotes a person who has become seasoned and cunning through a great deal of life experience. Mostly attributive: 海千山千の商人／政治家／したたか者. Unlike 経験豊富 (\"experienced\", positive), there is always an undertone of distrust; it should not be used as a compliment for someone.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"公明正大な裁判を求める。","romaji":"Kōmei seidai na saiban o motomeru.","german":"I demand a fair and just trial.","furigana":"こうめいせいだい|さいばん|もと"},{"japanese":"彼の公明正大な態度は信頼を集めた。","romaji":"Kare no kōmei seidai na taido wa shinrai o atsumeta.","german":"His fair and upright attitude earned trust.","furigana":"かれ|こうめいせいだい|たいど|しんらい|あつ"},{"japanese":"選挙は公明正大に行われなければならない。","romaji":"Senkyo wa kōmei seidai ni okonawarenakereba naranai.","german":"Elections must be conducted fairly and openly.","furigana":"せんきょ|こうめいせいだい|おこな"}],"notes":"公明 means \"open and clear\", 正大 \"upright and great\": 公明正大 describes conduct without ulterior motives that anyone can verify. Typically a na-adjective: 公明正大な態度／裁判／選挙, adverbially 公明正大に行う. Unlike 公平 (\"impartial\"), it additionally stresses transparency and honesty; 正々堂々, on the other hand, is said more of fighting fairly and openly in competition.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"古今東西、愛は人類の共通テーマだ。","romaji":"Kokon tōzai, ai wa jinrui no kyōtsū tēma da.","german":"In all ages and all places, love is a universal theme of humankind.","furigana":"ここんとうざい|あい|じんるい|きょうつう"},{"japanese":"古今東西の名作を集めた図書館。","romaji":"Kokon tōzai no meisaku o atsumeta toshokan.","german":"A library that has collected masterpieces from all times and countries.","furigana":"ここんとうざい|めいさく|あつ|としょかん"}],"notes":"Literally: 古今 \"past and present\" and 東西 \"east and west\". It means \"at all times and in all places\", that is, universally. Typical adverbially at the start of a sentence (古今東西、〜は変わらない) or attributively as 古今東西の〜 (名作, 料理, 物語). Slightly elevated, often in essays, advertising and TV titles.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は才色兼備の女性として知られている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa saishoku kenbi no josei to shite shirarete iru.","german":"She is known as a woman who has both talent and beauty.","furigana":"かのじょ|さいしょくけんび|じょせい|し"},{"japanese":"才色兼備な人はなかなかいない。","romaji":"Saishoku kenbi na hito wa nakanaka inai.","german":"People with both talent and beauty are hard to find.","furigana":"さいしょくけんび|ひと"},{"japanese":"彼女は医師の資格も持つ才色兼備の女優だ。","romaji":"Kanojo wa ishi no shikaku mo motsu saishoku kenbi no joyū da.","german":"She is an actress of both brains and beauty who even holds a medical license.","furigana":"かのじょ|いし|しかく|も|さいしょくけんび|じょゆう"}],"notes":"才 is \"talent, intelligence\", 色 \"appearance, beauty\", 兼備 \"to possess both at once\". 才色兼備 is traditionally said almost only of women (才色兼備の女性／女優); for men it sounds unusual, and people rather say 文武両道 or 眉目秀麗. Usual forms are 才色兼備の and 才色兼備な. It is praise, but today, depending on the context, it may be felt to be old-fashioned or fixated on appearance.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"情報の取捨選択が大切だ。","romaji":"Jōhō no shusha sentaku ga taisetsu da.","german":"It is important to sift and select information carefully.","furigana":"じょうほう|しゅしゃせんたく|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"引っ越しの際に荷物を取捨選択する。","romaji":"Hikkoshi no sai ni nimotsu o shusha sentaku suru.","german":"When moving, you choose which belongings to keep and which to discard.","furigana":"ひ|こ|さい|にもつ|しゅしゃせんたく"}],"notes":"Literally: 取捨 \"to take and to throw away\" and 選択 \"selection\". It means deliberate sorting out: keeping what is useful and discarding what is unnecessary. Typical are 情報の取捨選択 and 〜を取捨選択する, also 取捨選択が必要だ. Slightly elevated, common in texts about media literacy, work and organizing.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の主張は首尾一貫している。","romaji":"Kare no shuchō wa shubi ikkan shite iru.","german":"His argument is consistent from beginning to end.","furigana":"かれ|しゅちょう|しゅびいっかん"},{"japanese":"首尾一貫した方針を貫くべきだ。","romaji":"Shubi ikkan shita hōshin o tsuranuku beki da.","german":"One should stick to a consistent policy.","furigana":"しゅびいっかん|ほうしん|つらぬ"},{"japanese":"彼の話は首尾一貫していないので、信用できない。","romaji":"Kare no hanashi wa shubi ikkan shite inai node, shin'yō dekinai.","german":"His story is inconsistent, so he can't be trusted.","furigana":"かれ|はなし|しゅびいっかん|しんよう"}],"notes":"Literally: 首尾 \"head and tail, beginning and end\" and 一貫 \"running through on one line\". It means that statements, attitude or actions remain free of contradiction from beginning to end. Typical are 首尾一貫している, 首尾一貫した態度／方針 and, negated, 首尾一貫していない \"contradictory\". Rather elevated, used in discussions, criticism and evaluations.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"前人未到の記録を打ち立てた。","romaji":"Zenjin mitō no kiroku o uchitateta.","german":"He set a record that no one had ever achieved before.","furigana":"ぜんじんみとう|きろく|う|た"},{"japanese":"前人未到の領域に挑戦する。","romaji":"Zenjin mitō no ryōiki ni chōsen suru.","german":"They take on a challenge in uncharted territory.","furigana":"ぜんじんみとう|りょういき|ちょうせん"},{"japanese":"探検隊はついに前人未到の山頂に立った。","romaji":"Tankentai wa tsui ni zenjin mitō no sanchō ni tatta.","german":"The expedition finally stood on a summit no one had ever reached.","furigana":"たんけんたい|ぜんじんみとう|さんちょう|た"}],"notes":"前人未到 literally means \"earlier people have not yet arrived\": a place, area or record that no one has reached before. It almost always stands attributively with の before a noun: 前人未到の記録, 前人未到の領域, 前人未到の地. It is especially common in sports reports about records and in texts about research or expeditions. The expression is elevated and literary; similar is 史上初 \"first time in history\", whereas 空前絶後 additionally stresses that it will never happen again.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は大器晩成のタイプだ。","romaji":"Kare wa taiki bansei no taipu da.","german":"Er ist der Typ Spätentwickler.","furigana":"かれ|たいきばんせい"},{"japanese":"自分は大器晩成型だと信じて、努力を続けている。","romaji":"Jibun wa taiki bansei-gata da to shinjite, doryoku o tsuzukete iru.","german":"Ich glaube daran, dass ich mich spät entfalte, und arbeite weiter hart.","furigana":"じぶん|たいきばんせいがた|しん|どりょく|つづ"},{"japanese":"四十歳を過ぎて作家として成功するなんて、まさに大器晩成だね。","romaji":"Yonjussai o sugite sakka to shite seikō suru nante, masa ni taiki bansei da ne.","german":"Mit über vierzig als Schriftsteller Erfolg zu haben – das ist wirklich ein später Durchbruch eines großen Talents.","furigana":"よんじゅっさい|す|さっか|せいこう|たいきばんせい"}],"notes":"大器晩成 bedeutet wörtlich „ein großes Gefäß wird spät fertig“ (aus dem Daodejing): Wirklich begabte Menschen entfalten sich oft erst spät. Man benutzt es meist für Personen, oft als 大器晩成型 oder 大器晩成のタイプ, und gern tröstend oder anerkennend: Jemand, der jung nicht auffiel, kann später groß herauskommen. Es ist kein Verb; man sagt also nicht 大器晩成する, sondern まさに大器晩成だ. Das deutsche „Spätzünder“ ist ähnlich, klingt aber abwertender als das grundsätzlich positive 大器晩成.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は猪突猛進で計画性がない。","romaji":"Kare wa chototsu mōshin de keikakusei ga nai.","german":"He charges ahead recklessly and has no sense of planning.","furigana":"かれ|ちょとつもうしん|けいかくせい"},{"japanese":"猪突猛進型のリーダーは危険だ。","romaji":"Chototsu mōshin-gata no rīdā wa kiken da.","german":"A leader who charges ahead blindly is dangerous.","furigana":"ちょとつもうしんがた|きけん"},{"japanese":"猪突猛進するのではなく、まず計画を立てよう。","romaji":"Chototsu mōshin suru no de wa naku, mazu keikaku o tateyō.","german":"Instead of rushing in headlong, let's make a plan first.","furigana":"ちょとつもうしん|けいかく|た"}],"notes":"Literally \"to rush (突) like a wild boar (猪) and press wildly forward (猛進)\". It means heading blindly toward a goal without thinking. Mostly critical (計画性がない), but sometimes also appreciative of drive. Frames: 猪突猛進する, 猪突猛進型の人, 猪突猛進で〜. Often used in New Year's greetings in the Year of the Boar (亥年).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"それは朝三暮四の策略にすぎない。","romaji":"Sore wa chōsan boshi no sakuryaku ni suginai.","german":"That is nothing more than a trick of superficial changes.","furigana":"ちょうさんぼし|さくりゃく"},{"japanese":"月々の支払いが減っても総額が同じなら、朝三暮四だよ。","romaji":"Tsukizuki no shiharai ga hette mo sōgaku ga onaji nara, chōsan boshi da yo.","german":"If the monthly payment decreases but the total stays the same, it's a shell game.","furigana":"つきづき|しはら|へ|そうがく|おな|ちょうさんぼし"},{"japanese":"値段を据え置いて量を減らすのは、朝三暮四のやり方だ。","romaji":"Nedan o sueoite ryō o herasu no wa, chōsan boshi no yarikata da.","german":"Keeping the price the same and reducing the quantity is a sleight-of-hand tactic.","furigana":"ねだん|す|お|りょう|へ|ちょうさんぼし|かた"}],"notes":"From the Zhuangzi: a monkey keeper wants to give his monkeys three chestnuts in the morning and four in the evening; the monkeys protest, but with \"four in the morning, three in the evening\" they are satisfied, although the total is the same. 朝三暮四 therefore means being dazzled by superficial differences, or deceiving others with such tricks. Typical: 朝三暮四だ, 朝三暮四の策／やり方. Do not confuse it with 朝令暮改 (\"constantly changing orders\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"電光石火の早業で問題を解決した。","romaji":"Denkō sekka no hayawaza de mondai o kaiketsu shita.","german":"He solved the problem with lightning-fast skill.","furigana":"でんこうせっか|はやわざ|もんだい|かいけつ"},{"japanese":"彼女は電光石火のごとく反応した。","romaji":"Kanojo wa denkō sekka no gotoku hannō shita.","german":"She reacted like lightning.","furigana":"かのじょ|でんこうせっか|はんのう"},{"japanese":"試合開始直後、電光石火のゴールで先制した。","romaji":"Shiai kaishi chokugo, denkō sekka no gōru de sensei shita.","german":"Right after the start of the match, they took the lead with a lightning goal.","furigana":"しあいかいしちょくご|でんこうせっか|せんせい"}],"notes":"電光石火 combines 電光 \"lightning\" and 石火 \"the spark that arises when stone is struck\": something happens in a tiny instant, lightning-fast. Typical combinations are 電光石火の早業 \"lightning-fast feat\", 電光石火の速さ and 電光石火のごとく (elevated). It describes quick actions and reactions, especially in sports, fight scenes or surprisingly quick decisions. In everyday speech one usually simply says あっという間に or 素早く; 電光石火 sounds vivid and somewhat dramatic.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"資金集めのために東奔西走した。","romaji":"Shikin atsume no tame ni tōhon seisō shita.","german":"I ran around in all directions to raise funds.","furigana":"しきんあつ|とうほんせいそう"},{"japanese":"イベントの準備で東奔西走する毎日だ。","romaji":"Ibento no junbi de tōhon seisō suru mainichi da.","german":"Every day is spent rushing about preparing for the event.","furigana":"じゅんび|とうほんせいそう|まいにち"},{"japanese":"社長は新しい取引先を求めて東奔西走している。","romaji":"Shachō wa atarashii torihikisaki o motomete tōhon seisō shite iru.","german":"The president is dashing about in search of new business partners.","furigana":"しゃちょう|あたら|とりひきさき|もと|とうほんせいそう"}],"notes":"Literally \"to rush east, to run west\": 東奔西走 describes someone hurrying about everywhere for a cause, for example raising money, making preparations or looking for supporters. It is used as an intransitive する-verb: 〜のために東奔西走する. Unlike a simple 忙しい, it stresses constantly being on the move and working toward a goal; colloquially people say 走り回る or 駆けずり回る.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"技術は日進月歩で発展している。","romaji":"Gijutsu wa nisshin geppo de hatten shite iru.","german":"Technology is advancing steadily.","furigana":"ぎじゅつ|にっしんげっぽ|はってん"},{"japanese":"医学の分野は日進月歩だ。","romaji":"Igaku no bun'ya wa nisshin geppo da.","german":"The field of medicine advances constantly.","furigana":"いがく|ぶんや|にっしんげっぽ"},{"japanese":"スマホは日進月歩なので、二、三年で古くなってしまう。","romaji":"Sumaho wa nisshin geppo na node, ni, san-nen de furuku natte shimau.","german":"Smartphones advance so fast that they become outdated in two or three years.","furigana":"にっしんげっぽ|に|さんねん|ふる"}],"notes":"Literally \"forward by the day, a step by the month\" (日進 + 月歩). It means constant, rapid progress, almost always for technology, medicine or science, hardly for personal skills. Typical frames are 〜は日進月歩だ, 日進月歩の技術 and 日進月歩で進歩する. Neutral to literary, common in news and advertising copy.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"レオナルド・ダ・ヴィンチは博学多才な人物だった。","romaji":"Reonarudo da Vinchi wa hakugaku tasai na jinbutsu datta.","german":"Leonardo da Vinci was a learned and versatile man.","furigana":"はくがくたさい|じんぶつ"},{"japanese":"博学多才な先生に教わりたい。","romaji":"Hakugaku tasai na sensei ni osowaritai.","german":"I want to be taught by a teacher who is both learned and versatile.","furigana":"はくがくたさい|せんせい|おそ"},{"japanese":"祖父は博学多才で、何を聞いても答えてくれた。","romaji":"Sofu wa hakugaku tasai de, nani o kiite mo kotaete kureta.","german":"My grandfather was both learned and talented, and he could answer whatever I asked.","furigana":"そふ|はくがくたさい|なに|き|こた"}],"notes":"博学 is \"widely learned\", 多才 \"versatilely talented\": 博学多才 praises someone who has both broad knowledge and many skills. Common are 博学多才な人物 and 博学多才で…. 博学 or 博識 alone stresses knowledge only, 多才 or 多芸 only the talents. The expression is elevated and is usually said about others, not about oneself.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"家族の無病息災を願って初詣に行く。","romaji":"Kazoku no mubyō sokusai o negatte hatsumōde ni iku.","german":"I go to the first shrine visit of the year, wishing for my family's good health.","furigana":"かぞく|むびょうそくさい|ねが|はつもうで|い"},{"japanese":"無病息災が一番の幸せだ。","romaji":"Mubyō sokusai ga ichiban no shiawase da.","german":"Good health is the greatest happiness.","furigana":"むびょうそくさい|いちばん|しあわ"},{"japanese":"祖母に無病息災のお守りを買ってあげました。","romaji":"Sobo ni mubyō sokusai no omamori o katte agemashita.","german":"I bought my grandmother an amulet for good health.","furigana":"そぼ|むびょうそくさい|まも|か"}],"notes":"無病 \"without illness\" and 息災 \"safe and sound, free from misfortune\" (a Buddhist expression) together make \"healthy and well\". It is used above all in wishes and prayers: 家族の無病息災を願う, 無病息災を祈る, often at the first shrine visit of the year or on good-luck charms. It often stands next to 家内安全. Elevated and somewhat traditional; in daily life one simply says 元気 or 健康.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"明鏡止水の心境で試合に臨む。","romaji":"Meikyō shisui no shinkyō de shiai ni nozomu.","german":"I approach the match with a calm and serene state of mind.","furigana":"めいきょうしすい|しんきょう|しあい|のぞ"},{"japanese":"瞑想で明鏡止水の境地に達した。","romaji":"Meisō de meikyō shisui no kyōchi ni tasshita.","german":"Through meditation he reached a state of perfect calm.","furigana":"めいそう|めいきょうしすい|きょうち|たっ"},{"japanese":"怒りを抑えて、明鏡止水の心で判断したい。","romaji":"Ikari o osaete, meikyō shisui no kokoro de handan shitai.","german":"I want to set my anger aside and make my judgment with a clear, calm mind.","furigana":"いか|おさ|めいきょうしすい|こころ|はんだん"}],"notes":"明鏡 is a brightly polished mirror, 止水 still, unmoving water: both reflect things without distortion. 明鏡止水 describes a clear, calm mind without agitation, greed or ulterior motives. Fixed frames: 明鏡止水の心境／境地, 明鏡止水の心で. It is more elevated than 落ち着いている or 冷静 and is gladly used before competitions, exams or important decisions.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このイベントは老若男女が楽しめる。","romaji":"Kono ibento wa rōnyaku nannyo ga tanoshimeru.","german":"This event can be enjoyed by people of all ages and genders.","furigana":"ろうにゃくなんにょ|たの"},{"japanese":"老若男女を問わず人気がある。","romaji":"Rōnyaku nannyo o towazu ninki ga aru.","german":"It is popular regardless of age or gender.","furigana":"ろうにゃくなんにょ|と|にんき"},{"japanese":"お祭りの日、神社には老若男女がたくさん集まっていた。","romaji":"Omatsuri no hi, jinja ni wa rōnyaku nannyo ga takusan atsumatte ita.","german":"On the festival day, young and old gathered at the shrine in great numbers.","furigana":"まつ|ひ|じんじゃ|ろうにゃくなんにょ|あつ"}],"notes":"Literally \"old and young, men and women\"; it means \"all people, regardless of age or gender\". Note the special readings 老若 (ろうにゃく) and 男女 (なんにょ) instead of the usual ろうじゃく and だんじょ. Typical frames: 老若男女を問わず \"regardless of age and gender\" and 老若男女が楽しめる. Neutral, common in advertising and event announcements.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"我武者羅に働いた若い頃が懐かしい。","romaji":"Gamushara ni hataraita wakai koro ga natsukashii.","german":"I fondly remember my younger days when I worked with all-out determination.","furigana":"がむしゃら|はたら|わか|ころ|なつ"},{"japanese":"我武者羅に走り続けてゴールした。","romaji":"Gamushara ni hashiri tsuzukete gōru shita.","german":"I kept running doggedly and reached the finish line.","furigana":"がむしゃら|はし|つづ"},{"japanese":"試験までの一か月、我武者羅に勉強した。","romaji":"Shiken made no ikkagetsu, gamushara ni benkyō shita.","german":"In the month before the exam, I studied like mad.","furigana":"しけん|いっ|げつ|がむしゃら|べんきょう"}],"notes":"The kanji 我武者羅 are a purely phonetic spelling (ateji); the word is mostly written in kana: がむしゃら. It describes someone who goes at something regardless of the cost, doggedly and with all their strength. Almost always adverbial as がむしゃらに働く／勉強する／走る. Colloquial and rather positive about dedication, but can also hint at recklessness.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一所懸命に勉強したおかげで合格した。","romaji":"Issho kenmei ni benkyō shita okage de gōkaku shita.","german":"Thanks to studying with all my might, I passed.","furigana":"いっしょけんめい|べんきょう|ごうかく"},{"japanese":"一所懸命に働く姿が立派だ。","romaji":"Issho kenmei ni hataraku sugata ga rippa da.","german":"The way he works with total dedication is admirable.","furigana":"いっしょけんめい|はたら|すがた|りっぱ"},{"japanese":"一所懸命やったのだから、結果は気にしなくていいよ。","romaji":"Issho kenmei yatta no da kara, kekka wa ki ni shinakute ii yo.","german":"You did your best, so you don't have to worry about the result.","furigana":"いっしょけんめい|けっか|き"}],"notes":"一所懸命 is the original form: samurai staked their lives (懸命) on their single fief (一所). Today the variant 一生懸命 \"staking one's whole life\" is much more common; both mean \"with all one's might, to the best of one's ability\". Typically adverbial (一所懸命（に）勉強する) or as a na-adjective (一所懸命な姿). Neutral; in newspapers 一所懸命 is sometimes preferred.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"空前絶後の大ヒットとなった映画。","romaji":"Kūzen zetsugo no dai hitto to natta eiga.","german":"A film that became a hit like none before or since.","furigana":"くうぜんぜつご|だい|えいが"},{"japanese":"空前絶後の記録を達成した。","romaji":"Kūzen zetsugo no kiroku o tassei shita.","german":"He achieved a record unmatched before or since.","furigana":"くうぜんぜつご|きろく|たっせい"},{"japanese":"その地震は空前絶後の被害をもたらした。","romaji":"Sono jishin wa kūzen zetsugo no higai o motarashita.","german":"That earthquake caused damage unlike anything before, and probably unlike anything to come.","furigana":"じしん|くうぜんぜつご|ひがい"}],"notes":"空前絶後 is composed of 空前 \"never before\" and 絶後 \"never after\": so extraordinary that it has never happened and probably never will again. It mostly stands with の before a noun (空前絶後の大ヒット, 空前絶後の記録) and can describe both positive things and catastrophes. It is stronger than 前人未到 or 前代未聞, which stress only the \"never before\". The expression is elevated, but is readily used with exaggeration in advertising and the media.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女の天真爛漫な笑顔に癒される。","romaji":"Kanojo no tenshin ranman na egao ni iyasareru.","german":"Her innocent, natural smile is soothing.","furigana":"かのじょ|てんしんらんまん|えがお|いや"},{"japanese":"子供の天真爛漫さは素晴らしい。","romaji":"Kodomo no tenshin ranman-sa wa subarashii.","german":"The natural openness of children is wonderful.","furigana":"こども|てんしんらんまん|すば"},{"japanese":"新しい同僚は天真爛漫な性格で、すぐにみんなと仲良くなった。","romaji":"Atarashii dōryō wa tenshin ranman na seikaku de, sugu ni minna to nakayoku natta.","german":"The new colleague has such an open, unaffected nature that she quickly became friends with everyone.","furigana":"あたら|どうりょう|てんしんらんまん|せいかく|なかよ"}],"notes":"天真爛漫 combines 天真 \"naturally unadulterated\" and 爛漫 \"in full bloom, radiant\": someone shows their nature openly, without ulterior motives or pretense. It is a な-adjective: 天真爛漫な子ども, 天真爛漫な笑顔, and as a noun 天真爛漫さ. It is almost always positive (\"unselfconscious, warm, childlike-open\") and is used especially for children and young women. A derogatory \"naive\" is expressed more with 世間知らず or 単純.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"質実剛健な校風で知られる学校だ。","romaji":"Shitsujitsu gōken na kōfū de shirareru gakkō da.","german":"It is a school known for its simple and sturdy school spirit.","furigana":"しつじつごうけん|こうふう|し|がっこう"},{"japanese":"彼は質実剛健な人柄で信頼されている。","romaji":"Kare wa shitsujitsu gōken na hitogara de shinrai sarete iru.","german":"He is trusted for his plain and sturdy character.","furigana":"かれ|しつじつごうけん|ひとがら|しんらい"},{"japanese":"このメーカーの家具は質実剛健で、何十年も使える。","romaji":"Kono mēkā no kagu wa shitsujitsu gōken de, nanjū-nen mo tsukaeru.","german":"This manufacturer's furniture is unadorned and solid and lasts for decades.","furigana":"かぐ|しつじつごうけん|なんじゅうねん|つか"}],"notes":"質実 means \"plain and honest, without ostentation\", 剛健 \"strong and steadfast\". 質実剛健 praises a down-to-earth, unpretentious and at the same time robust nature, in people, schools (often as a school motto, 校訓), companies or products. As a na-adjective: 質実剛健な人柄／校風. In contrast to 地味 (\"unobtrusive\", rather negative), it is clearly positive; the opposite would be 華美 or 軟弱.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"夫婦二人三脚で店を経営している。","romaji":"Fūfu ninin sankyaku de mise o keiei shite iru.","german":"The couple runs the shop hand in hand.","furigana":"ふうふににんさんきゃく|みせ|けいえい"},{"japanese":"二人三脚で困難を乗り越えた。","romaji":"Ninin sankyaku de konnan o norikoeta.","german":"They overcame the difficulties through close cooperation.","furigana":"ににんさんきゃく|こんなん|の|こ"},{"japanese":"運動会で父と二人三脚に出ました。","romaji":"Undōkai de chichi to ninin sankyaku ni demashita.","german":"At the sports day, I entered the three-legged race with my father.","furigana":"うんどうかい|ちち|ににんさんきゃく|で"}],"notes":"Literally \"two people, three legs\": the three-legged race at the sports day, in which two runners tie one leg together. Figuratively it means that two partners work closely and in step, such as married couples, coaches and athletes or two companies. Typical: 二人三脚で〜する and 〜と二人三脚で歩む. Neutral and positively connoted.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は英語を自由自在に操る。","romaji":"Kare wa eigo o jiyū jizai ni ayatsuru.","german":"He commands English freely.","furigana":"かれ|えいご|じゆうじざい|あやつ"},{"japanese":"自由自在にドローンを操縦する。","romaji":"Jiyū jizai ni dorōn o sōjū suru.","german":"He pilots the drone freely.","furigana":"じゆうじざい|そうじゅう"},{"japanese":"この机は高さを自由自在に変えられます。","romaji":"Kono tsukue wa takasa o jiyū jizai ni kaeraremasu.","german":"The height of this desk can be changed freely.","furigana":"つくえ|たか|じゆうじざい|か"}],"notes":"Two expressions for freedom (自由 \"free\", 自在 \"at will\") reinforce each other: to do or master something completely freely and effortlessly at one's own will. Almost always adverbial with verbs such as 操る, 使いこなす, 動かす or 変える: 自由自在に操る \"to wield masterfully\". Also possible as a na-adjective (自由自在な動き). Neutral.","levelNote":"Compound: 自由 (N4) + 自在 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"山紫水明の地に別荘を建てた。","romaji":"Sanshi suimei no chi ni bessō o tateta.","german":"He built a villa in a place of scenic beauty.","furigana":"さんしすいめい|ち|べっそう|た"},{"japanese":"京都は山紫水明の美しい街だ。","romaji":"Kyōto wa sanshi suimei no utsukushii machi da.","german":"Kyoto is a beautiful city of scenic mountains and clear waters.","furigana":"きょうと|さんしすいめい|うつく|まち"},{"japanese":"旅館の窓の外には山紫水明の景色が広がっていた。","romaji":"Ryokan no mado no soto ni wa sanshi suimei no keshiki ga hirogatte ita.","german":"Outside the inn's window spread a landscape of picturesque mountains and rivers.","furigana":"りょかん|まど|そと|さんしすいめい|けしき|ひろ"}],"notes":"Literally \"mountains purple, waters clear\": in the evening light the mountains shimmer violet and rivers run clear. 山紫水明 praises a picturesque, seemingly unspoiled landscape; the expression became known through the scholar 頼山陽 and his view over Kyōto. Almost always attributive: 山紫水明の地／景色. For a simple view people say 景色がいい or 風光明媚; 山紫水明 is more literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"岡目八目というから、第三者の意見も聞こう。","romaji":"Okame hachimoku to iu kara, daisansha no iken mo kikō.","german":"As they say, onlookers see more, so let's hear a third party's opinion as well.","furigana":"おかめはちもく|だいさんしゃ|いけん|き"},{"japanese":"岡目八目とはよく言ったもので、友人のアドバイスは的確だった。","romaji":"Okame hachimoku to wa yoku itta mono de, yūjin no adobaisu wa tekikaku datta.","german":"The saying about onlookers is well put; my friend's advice was spot on.","furigana":"おかめはちもく|い|ゆうじん|てきかく"},{"japanese":"将棋は横で見ていると、岡目八目でいい手がすぐ見える。","romaji":"Shōgi wa yoko de mite iru to, okame hachimoku de ii te ga sugu mieru.","german":"Watching shogi from the side, you can see the good moves right away as an onlooker.","furigana":"しょうぎ|よこ|み|おかめはちもく|て|み"}],"notes":"From the game of go: someone who merely watches (岡目, \"view from the side\") sees eight moves (八目) further than the players themselves. 岡目八目 means that uninvolved people can often judge a situation more clearly than those affected. Mostly quoted as a proverb: 岡目八目というように／とはよく言ったもので. Also written 傍目八目. It is often used to justify why one should seek the opinion of third parties.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"疑心暗鬼になって友人まで疑った。","romaji":"Gishin anki ni natte yūjin made utagatta.","german":"Consumed by suspicion, he even doubted his friends.","furigana":"ぎしんあんき|ゆうじん|うたが"},{"japanese":"疑心暗鬼に陥らないように気をつけよう。","romaji":"Gishin anki ni ochiiranai yō ni ki o tsukeyō.","german":"Let's be careful not to fall into excessive suspicion.","furigana":"ぎしんあんき|おちい|き"},{"japanese":"上司が何も説明しないので、社員の間に疑心暗鬼が広がっている。","romaji":"Jōshi ga nani mo setsumei shinai node, shain no aida ni gishin anki ga hirogatte iru.","german":"Because the boss explains nothing, suspicion is spreading among the employees.","furigana":"じょうし|なに|せつめい|しゃいん|あいだ|ぎしんあんき|ひろ"}],"notes":"疑心暗鬼 is the short form of 疑心暗鬼を生ず: a \"suspicious heart\" (疑心) produces \"demons in the dark\" (暗鬼) - once someone is suspicious, they see threats everywhere. Typical are 疑心暗鬼になる, 疑心暗鬼に陥る and 疑心暗鬼が広がる; it is often about groups in which, after an incident, everyone suspects everyone. The expression is somewhat elevated, but well known from the news and everyday conversation.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"粉骨砕身の努力で会社を立て直した。","romaji":"Funkotsu saishin no doryoku de kaisha o tatenaoshita.","german":"He rebuilt the company through tireless effort.","furigana":"ふんこつさいしん|どりょく|かいしゃ|た|なお"},{"japanese":"粉骨砕身して働いてくれた社員に感謝する。","romaji":"Funkotsu saishin shite hataraite kureta shain ni kansha suru.","german":"I am grateful to the employees who worked with total devotion.","furigana":"ふんこつさいしん|はたら|しゃいん|かんしゃ"},{"japanese":"新しい役職でも、粉骨砕身、会社のために尽くす所存です。","romaji":"Atarashii yakushoku de mo, funkotsu saishin, kaisha no tame ni tsukusu shozon desu.","german":"Even in my new post, I intend to devote myself with all my strength to the company.","furigana":"あたら|やくしょく|ふんこつさいしん|かいしゃ|つ|しょぞん"}],"notes":"Literally \"to grind the bones to powder, to shatter the body\": 粉骨砕身 means to give one's all, to the point of self-sacrifice. Typical in inaugural speeches and pledges: 粉骨砕身努力いたします, 粉骨砕身（して）働く／尽くす; also 粉骨砕身の努力. It is very formal and solemn; in everyday speech people say 一生懸命 or 全力で.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"完全無欠な人間なんていない。","romaji":"Kanzen muketsu na ningen nante inai.","german":"There is no such thing as a perfectly flawless person.","furigana":"かんぜんむけつ|にんげん"},{"japanese":"完全無欠の計画を立てたつもりだった。","romaji":"Kanzen muketsu no keikaku o tateta tsumori datta.","german":"I thought I had made a flawless plan.","furigana":"かんぜんむけつ|けいかく|た"},{"japanese":"完全無欠に見えるヒーローにも、意外な弱点がある。","romaji":"Kanzen muketsu ni mieru hīrō ni mo, igai na jakuten ga aru.","german":"Even a hero who seems flawless has an unexpected weakness.","furigana":"かんぜんむけつ|み|いがい|じゃくてん"}],"notes":"完全無欠 means \"complete and without any deficiency\" (無欠 = \"without a flaw\"), that is, absolutely perfect. It is used as a な- or の-adjective: 完全無欠な人, 完全無欠の計画. It often appears in negated or ironic sentences such as 完全無欠な人間なんていない, because absolute perfection is rare. It is stronger and more formal than 完璧; in everyday speech one usually simply says 完璧.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"病状は一進一退を繰り返している。","romaji":"Byōjō wa isshin ittai o kurikaeshite iru.","german":"The patient's condition keeps fluctuating between better and worse.","furigana":"びょうじょう|いっしんいったい|く|かえ"},{"japanese":"交渉は一進一退の状況だ。","romaji":"Kōshō wa isshin ittai no jōkyō da.","german":"The negotiations are in a back-and-forth stalemate.","furigana":"こうしょう|いっしんいったい|じょうきょう"},{"japanese":"試合は一進一退で、最後まで結果が分からなかった。","romaji":"Shiai wa isshin ittai de, saigo made kekka ga wakaranakatta.","german":"The match swung back and forth, and the outcome was unclear until the end.","furigana":"しあい|いっしんいったい|さいご|けっか|わ"}],"notes":"Literally \"once forward, once back\" (一進 + 一退). It means a state that fluctuates back and forth without a clear direction emerging - with illnesses, negotiations, games or share prices. Typical frames: 一進一退を繰り返す, 一進一退の状態／攻防 and 〜は一進一退だ. Neutral to literary, common in news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の説明は支離滅裂だった。","romaji":"Kare no setsumei wa shiri metsuretsu datta.","german":"His explanation was totally incoherent.","furigana":"かれ|せつめい|しりめつれつ"},{"japanese":"支離滅裂な議論では何も決まらない。","romaji":"Shiri metsuretsu na giron de wa nani mo kimaranai.","german":"Nothing gets decided in a muddled discussion.","furigana":"しりめつれつ|ぎろん|なに|き"},{"japanese":"酔っ払った部長が支離滅裂なことを言い出した。","romaji":"Yopparatta buchō ga shiri metsuretsu na koto o iidashita.","german":"The drunken department head began saying incoherent things.","furigana":"よ|ぱら|ぶちょう|しりめつれつ|い|だ"}],"notes":"支離滅裂 combines 支離 \"fragmented, fallen apart\" and 滅裂 \"torn, destroyed\": speech, thoughts or actions lack a common thread and make no sense. It is a な-adjective and is often used predicatively: 話が支離滅裂だ, 支離滅裂なことを言う. Typical subjects are 説明, 話, 言い訳, 主張 or 文章. It is clearly critical; more neutral are 筋が通らない \"not logical\" or まとまりがない \"unstructured\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"威風堂々とした態度で演説した。","romaji":"Ifū dōdō to shita taido de enzetsu shita.","german":"He gave a speech with a majestic, dignified bearing.","furigana":"いふうどうどう|たいど|えんぜつ"},{"japanese":"威風堂々と入場する選手団。","romaji":"Ifū dōdō to nyūjō suru senshudan.","german":"The team marches in with an imposing air.","furigana":"いふうどうどう|にゅうじょう|せんしゅだん"},{"japanese":"丘の上には古い城が威風堂々とそびえている。","romaji":"Oka no ue ni wa furui shiro ga ifū dōdō to sobiete iru.","german":"An old castle towers majestically on the hill.","furigana":"おか|うえ|ふる|しろ|いふうどうどう"}],"notes":"威風堂々 combines 威風 \"dignity, awe-inspiring presence\" with 堂々 \"stately, confident\": imposing, majestic. It mostly stands adverbially with と or as とした before nouns: 威風堂々と行進する, 威風堂々とした態度. It is used to describe people, appearances, buildings or landscapes. 堂々 alone is more everyday (\"confident, unabashed\"); 威風堂々 sounds solemn; in Japan it is also the title of Elgar's marches \"Pomp and Circumstance\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一攫千金を夢見て宝くじを買った。","romaji":"Ikkaku senkin o yumemite takarakuji o katta.","german":"He bought a lottery ticket, dreaming of getting rich quick.","furigana":"いっかくせんきん|ゆめみ|たから|か"},{"japanese":"一攫千金を狙って、全財産を株に使うのは危険だ。","romaji":"Ikkaku senkin o neratte, zenzaisan o kabu ni tsukau no wa kiken da.","german":"Putting all your assets into stocks in hopes of striking it rich is dangerous.","furigana":"いっかくせんきん|ねら|ぜんざいさん|かぶ|つか|きけん"},{"japanese":"ゴールドラッシュの時代、一攫千金の夢を追って多くの人が西部へ向かった。","romaji":"Gōrudo rasshu no jidai, ikkaku senkin no yume o otte ōku no hito ga seibu e mukatta.","german":"In the Gold Rush era, many people headed west chasing the dream of instant riches.","furigana":"じだい|いっかくせんきん|ゆめ|お|おお|ひと|せいぶ|む"}],"notes":"一攫千金 means \"a thousand pieces of gold (千金) in one grab (一攫)\": to get rich in one stroke and without much effort. Typical are 一攫千金を狙う, 一攫千金を夢見る and 一攫千金の夢. It is often about lotteries, gambling, speculation or gold rushes and often has a slightly skeptical undertone. The kanji 攫 is rare; in the media 一獲千金 is sometimes written.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"正々堂々と戦おう。","romaji":"Seisei dōdō to tatakaō.","german":"Let's fight fair and square.","furigana":"せいせいどうどう|たたか"},{"japanese":"彼女は正々堂々と意見を述べた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa seisei dōdō to iken o nobeta.","german":"She stated her opinion openly and honestly.","furigana":"かのじょ|せいせいどうどう|いけん|の"},{"japanese":"選手代表は「正々堂々と戦うことを誓います」と宣誓した。","romaji":"Senshu daihyō wa \"Seisei dōdō to tatakau koto o chikaimasu\" to sensei shita.","german":"The athletes' representative took the oath: \"We swear to compete fairly.\"","furigana":"せんしゅだいひょう|せいせいどうどう|たたか|ちか|せんせい"}],"notes":"正々堂々 doubles 正 \"right, correct\" and 堂 \"dignified, stately\" and means \"fair, open and upright\", without tricks or secrecy. Typically adverbial with と: 正々堂々と戦う, 正々堂々と勝負する; adnominal 正々堂々たる態度 or 正々堂々の勝負. Classic is the athletes' oath: 正々堂々と戦うことを誓います. The tone is elevated to solemn; the spelling 正正堂堂 is rare.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"インターネットの情報は玉石混交だ。","romaji":"Intānetto no jōhō wa gyokuseki konkō da.","german":"Information on the Internet is a mixed bag.","furigana":"じょうほう|ぎょくせきこんこう"},{"japanese":"応募作品は玉石混交で、審査に時間がかかった。","romaji":"Ōbo sakuhin wa gyokuseki konkō de, shinsa ni jikan ga kakatta.","german":"The submitted works were of very mixed quality, so the judging took a long time.","furigana":"おうぼさくひん|ぎょくせきこんこう|しんさ|じかん"},{"japanese":"古本市の本は玉石混交だが、たまに掘り出し物がある。","romaji":"Furuhon'ichi no hon wa gyokuseki konkō da ga, tama ni horidashimono ga aru.","german":"The books at the used book fair are a mixed lot, but occasionally there is a hidden gem.","furigana":"ふるほんいち|ほん|ぎょくせきこんこう|ほ|だ|もの"}],"notes":"玉 is a gemstone, 石 an ordinary stone, 混交 \"mixed\": valuable and worthless things lie jumbled together. 玉石混交 describes collections whose quality varies greatly, such as information, applications, products or books. Mostly predicative: 〜は玉石混交だ. The original spelling is 玉石混淆; 混交 is the simplified form common today. The expression implies that one has to do the selecting oneself.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女の才能は唯一無二のものだ。","romaji":"Kanojo no sainō wa yuiitsu muni no mono da.","german":"Her talent is one of a kind.","furigana":"かのじょ|さいのう|ゆいいつむに"},{"japanese":"彼は私にとって唯一無二の親友だ。","romaji":"Kare wa watashi ni totte yuiitsu muni no shin'yū da.","german":"He is my one and only irreplaceable best friend.","furigana":"かれ|わたし|ゆいいつむに|しんゆう"},{"japanese":"この店のラーメンの味は唯一無二で、どこも真似できない。","romaji":"Kono mise no rāmen no aji wa yuiitsu muni de, doko mo mane dekinai.","german":"The taste of the ramen at this shop is unique - nobody else can imitate it.","furigana":"みせ|あじ|ゆいいつむに|まね"}],"notes":"唯一無二 combines 唯一 \"sole\" and 無二 \"without a second\": there is only one of it, and it is irreplaceable. It mostly stands with の before a noun (唯一無二の存在, 唯一無二の親友) or predicatively with のものだ. It is more emphatic than 唯一 alone and stresses special value. It sounds rather elevated and is readily used in advertising, speeches and song lyrics.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大義名分がなければ、人はついてこない。","romaji":"Taigi meibun ga nakereba, hito wa tsuite konai.","german":"Without a just cause, people will not follow.","furigana":"たいぎめいぶん|ひと"},{"japanese":"その国は自衛という大義名分を掲げて戦争を始めた。","romaji":"Sono kuni wa jiei to iu taigi meibun o kakagete sensō o hajimeta.","german":"The country started the war under the banner of self-defense.","furigana":"くに|じえい|たいぎめいぶん|かか|せんそう|はじ"},{"japanese":"「健康のため」という大義名分で、高いマッサージチェアを買った。","romaji":"\"Kenkō no tame\" to iu taigi meibun de, takai massāji chea o katta.","german":"I bought an expensive massage chair under the pretext of \"it's for my health.\"","furigana":"けんこう|たいぎめいぶん|たか|か"}],"notes":"Originally the subject's duty toward the ruler (大義) and the correct order of ranks (名分). Today 大義名分 mainly means a plausible, morally defensible justification for an action, often with the undertone of \"pretext\". Typical: 大義名分がある／ない, 大義名分を掲げる, 〜という大義名分のもとに. Unlike 理由, it stresses the public justification before others.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この店には多種多様な商品が並んでいる。","romaji":"Kono mise ni wa tashu tayō na shōhin ga narande iru.","german":"A wide variety of goods are lined up in this shop.","furigana":"みせ|たしゅたよう|しょうひん|なら"},{"japanese":"会議では多種多様な意見が出された。","romaji":"Kaigi de wa tashu tayō na iken ga dasareta.","german":"A wide range of opinions was expressed at the meeting.","furigana":"かいぎ|たしゅたよう|いけん|だ"},{"japanese":"この森には多種多様な生き物が暮らしている。","romaji":"Kono mori ni wa tashu tayō na ikimono ga kurashite iru.","german":"A great variety of creatures live in this forest.","furigana":"もり|たしゅたよう|い|もの|く"}],"notes":"多種多様 means \"many kinds and many forms\": very diverse, of great range. It stands as a な- or の-adjective before nouns (多種多様な商品, 多種多様な意見, 多種多様な生き物) and stresses the large number of different kinds. It is somewhat more formal and stronger than さまざまな or いろいろな and appears often in reports, advertising and non-fiction. The related noun is 多様性 \"diversity\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女の演技は天衣無縫で、観客を魅了した。","romaji":"Kanojo no engi wa ten'i muhō de, kankyaku o miryō shita.","german":"Her performance was natural and flawless, and it captivated the audience.","furigana":"かのじょ|えんぎ|てんいむほう|かんきゃく|みりょう"},{"japanese":"この詩は天衣無縫で、技巧の跡をまったく感じさせない。","romaji":"Kono shi wa ten'i muhō de, gikō no ato o mattaku kanjisasenai.","german":"This poem is perfectly natural; one senses not the slightest trace of artifice.","furigana":"し|てんいむほう|ぎこう|あと|かん"},{"japanese":"彼の天衣無縫な人柄は、誰からも愛された。","romaji":"Kare no ten'i muhō na hitogara wa, dare kara mo aisareta.","german":"His unaffected, artless character made him beloved by everyone.","furigana":"かれ|てんいむほう|ひとがら|だれ|あい"}],"notes":"Literally \"heavenly robe without a seam\": the garments of celestial beings have no seams. 天衣無縫 therefore praises works such as poems, texts or performances that seem perfectly natural, without a trace of artificiality. Applied to people, it means an unaffected, unselfconscious nature, similar to 天真爛漫. Common: 天衣無縫な／の, 天衣無縫で.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は馬鹿正直だから、嘘がつけない。","romaji":"Kare wa baka shōjiki da kara, uso ga tsukenai.","german":"He is so honest to a fault that he can't tell a lie.","furigana":"かれ|ばかしょうじき|うそ"},{"japanese":"面接で前の会社の悪口を馬鹿正直に話す必要はない。","romaji":"Mensetsu de mae no kaisha no waruguchi o baka shōjiki ni hanasu hitsuyō wa nai.","german":"In an interview, you don't need to be naively honest and badmouth your previous company.","furigana":"めんせつ|まえ|かいしゃ|わるぐち|ばかしょうじき|はな|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"落とし物の財布を馬鹿正直に全額そのまま届けた。","romaji":"Otoshimono no saifu o baka shōjiki ni zengaku sono mama todoketa.","german":"He honestly turned in the lost wallet with every last yen in it.","furigana":"お|もの|さいふ|ばかしょうじき|ぜんがく|とど"}],"notes":"馬鹿正直 combines 馬鹿 \"stupid\" (here as an intensifier) with 正直 \"honest\": so honest that it is unwise, for example because one also says things one had better keep quiet. It is a な-adjective and often adverbial: 馬鹿正直に答える, 馬鹿正直に話す. The tone is half critical, half affectionate - one blames the clumsiness but acknowledges the sincerity. It is rather colloquial and, strictly speaking, not a classic four-character idiom but a compound.","levelNote":"Compound: 馬鹿 (N3) + 正直 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"武田信玄の旗印は風林火山だった。","romaji":"Takeda Shingen no hata-jirushi wa fūrinkazan datta.","german":"Takeda Shingen's battle standard was Fūrinkazan.","furigana":"たけだしんげん|はたじるし|ふうりんかざん"},{"japanese":"監督は「風林火山」をチームのスローガンに掲げた。","romaji":"Kantoku wa \"fūrinkazan\" o chīmu no surōgan ni kakageta.","german":"The coach made \"Fūrinkazan\" his team's slogan.","furigana":"かんとく|ふうりんかざん|かか"},{"japanese":"風林火山という言葉は、もともと『孫子』に由来する。","romaji":"Fūrinkazan to iu kotoba wa, motomoto \"Sonshi\" ni yurai suru.","german":"The expression Fūrinkazan originally derives from Sunzi's \"The Art of War\".","furigana":"ふうりんかざん|ことば|そんし|ゆらい"}],"notes":"Abbreviation of a passage from Sunzi's \"The Art of War\": 疾きこと風の如く、徐かなること林の如く、侵掠すること火の如く、動かざること山の如し. 風林火山 became famous as the banner of the warlord 武田信玄. Today it serves as a motto for a flexible, resolute strategy, in sports, business or as a title; in everyday life it usually appears as a quotation, not as a freely usable adjective.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"平穏無事な毎日を過ごしたい。","romaji":"Heion buji na mainichi o sugoshitai.","german":"I want to spend peaceful, quiet days.","furigana":"へいおんぶじ|まいにち|す"},{"japanese":"今年も平穏無事に一年が終わって、ほっとした。","romaji":"Kotoshi mo heion buji ni ichinen ga owatte, hotto shita.","german":"I'm relieved that this year, too, ended without incident.","furigana":"ことし|へいおんぶじ|いちねん|お"},{"japanese":"心配されていた祭りは、事故もなく平穏無事に終了した。","romaji":"Shinpai sarete ita matsuri wa, jiko mo naku heion buji ni shūryō shita.","german":"The festival, about which there had been worries, ended peacefully without any accidents.","furigana":"しんぱい|まつ|じこ|へいおんぶじ|しゅうりょう"}],"notes":"平穏無事 combines 平穏 \"calm, peaceful\" and 無事 \"without incident, safe and sound\": a state in which nothing bad happens. Typical are 平穏無事な毎日, 平穏無事に暮らす and 平穏無事に終わる. The expression sounds warm and somewhat formal and is readily used in New Year's greetings or retrospectives. It stresses the absence of problems, not luck or excitement; similar but broader is 無病息災 \"free from illness and misfortune\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その提案は満場一致で可決された。","romaji":"Sono teian wa manjō icchi de kaketsu sareta.","german":"The proposal was passed unanimously.","furigana":"ていあん|まんじょういっち|かけつ"},{"japanese":"新しい会長には、満場一致で田中さんが選ばれた。","romaji":"Atarashii kaichō ni wa, manjō icchi de Tanaka-san ga erabareta.","german":"Mr. Tanaka was unanimously elected as the new chairman.","furigana":"あたら|かいちょう|まんじょういっち|たなか|えら"},{"japanese":"審査員の満場一致で、彼女の作品が大賞に決まった。","romaji":"Shinsain no manjō icchi de, kanojo no sakuhin ga taishō ni kimatta.","german":"By the unanimous decision of the judges, her work was awarded the grand prize.","furigana":"しんさいん|まんじょういっち|かのじょ|さくひん|たいしょう|き"}],"notes":"満場一致 means \"the whole hall (満場) is of one opinion (一致)\": everyone present agrees. It almost always appears with で: 満場一致で可決される, 満場一致で選ばれる, 満場一致で決まる. It belongs to the language of assemblies, parliaments, juries and clubs and is rather formal. 全会一致 is a close synonym, especially in committees; in everyday speech people also simply say 全員賛成.","levelNote":"Compound: 満場 (N1) + 一致 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"部屋が無茶苦茶に散らかっている。","romaji":"Heya ga muchakucha ni chirakatte iru.","german":"The room is a complete mess.","furigana":"へや|むちゃくちゃ|ち"},{"japanese":"無茶苦茶なことを言わないでくれ。","romaji":"Muchakucha na koto o iwanaide kure.","german":"Please don't talk nonsense.","furigana":"むちゃくちゃ|い"},{"japanese":"昨日のテストは無茶苦茶難しかった。","romaji":"Kinō no tesuto wa muchakucha muzukashikatta.","german":"The test yesterday was incredibly hard.","furigana":"きのう|むちゃくちゃむずか"}],"notes":"The kanji are ateji; it is usually written むちゃくちゃ in kana. 無茶 alone means \"unreasonable, absurd\" (無茶をする). As a na-adjective: 無茶苦茶な話 \"absurd story\", 無茶苦茶になる \"to get completely messed up\". Colloquially also an intensifier, \"extremely\": むちゃくちゃおいしい. The variant めちゃくちゃ (short: めっちゃ) means the same and is even more casual.","levelNote":"JLPT word list (J. Waller): N1"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"問答無用で追い出された。","romaji":"Mondō muyō de oidasareta.","german":"He was thrown out without further discussion.","furigana":"もんどうむよう|お|だ"},{"japanese":"この大会では、ルール違反は問答無用で失格です。","romaji":"Kono taikai de wa, rūru ihan wa mondō muyō de shikkaku desu.","german":"In this tournament, a rule violation means disqualification, no ifs or buts.","furigana":"たいかい|いはん|もんどうむよう|しっかく"},{"japanese":"「問答無用！早く部屋を片付けなさい。」","romaji":"\"Mondō muyō! Hayaku heya o katazukenasai.\"","german":"\"No arguments! Clean up your room right now.\"","furigana":"もんどうむよう|はや|へや|かたづ"}],"notes":"問答 is \"question and answer, discussion\", 無用 \"unnecessary\": 問答無用 declares any further discussion superfluous. Adverbial 問答無用で means \"without ifs and buts, summarily\" (問答無用で退場／追い出す); as an exclamation, 問答無用！ cuts off any objection. The tone is authoritarian and curt; a politer alternative would be 議論の余地はない.","levelNote":"Compound: 問答 (N2) + 無用 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"定年後は悠々自適の生活を送っている。","romaji":"Teinengo wa yūyū jiteki no seikatsu o okutte iru.","german":"After retirement, he lives a leisurely and contented life.","furigana":"ていねんご|ゆうゆうじてき|せいかつ|おく"},{"japanese":"いつか田舎で悠々自適に暮らすのが夢だ。","romaji":"Itsuka inaka de yūyū jiteki ni kurasu no ga yume da.","german":"My dream is to live carefree in the countryside someday.","furigana":"いなか|ゆうゆうじてき|く|ゆめ"},{"japanese":"彼は会社を売って、三十代で悠々自適の身になった。","romaji":"Kare wa kaisha o utte, sanjūdai de yūyū jiteki no mi ni natta.","german":"He sold his company and, in his thirties, was able to live entirely as he pleased.","furigana":"かれ|かいしゃ|う|さんじゅうだい|ゆうゆうじてき|み"}],"notes":"悠々自適 combines 悠々 \"calm, unhurried\" and 自適 \"to arrange things as one pleases\": to live as one wishes, free from obligations and worries. Most often it describes retirement: 悠々自適の生活, 悠々自適に暮らす, 定年後は悠々自適. It usually presupposes that one is financially secure. The tone is positive and somewhat elevated; in everyday speech one also says のんびり暮らす.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"利害関係が一致したので、協力することにした。","romaji":"Rigai kankei ga icchi shita node, kyōryoku suru koto ni shita.","german":"Because our interests coincided, we decided to cooperate.","furigana":"りがいかんけい|いっち|きょうりょく"},{"japanese":"その会社と利害関係のある人は、審査に参加できない。","romaji":"Sono kaisha to rigai kankei no aru hito wa, shinsa ni sanka dekinai.","german":"People with a stake in that company cannot take part in the review.","furigana":"かいしゃ|りがいかんけい|ひと|しんさ|さんか"},{"japanese":"利害関係が絡むと、友人同士でも話し合いは難しくなる。","romaji":"Rigai kankei ga karamu to, yūjin dōshi demo hanashiai wa muzukashiku naru.","german":"Once interests are involved, discussion becomes difficult even among friends.","furigana":"りがいかんけい|から|ゆうじんどうし|はな|あ|むずか"}],"notes":"利害関係 means \"the relationship of benefit (利) and harm (害)\": a relationship in which one's own economic or other interests are at stake. Typical are 利害関係がある／ない, 利害関係が一致する／対立する and 利害関係者 \"stakeholder, parties concerned\". It is a factual expression from business, law and politics, often in connection with conflicts of interest. Strictly speaking, it is not a four-character idiom but an ordinary compound of 利害 and 関係.","levelNote":"Compound: 利害 (N2) + 関係 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この建物は和洋折衷のデザインだ。","romaji":"Kono tatemono wa wayō secchū no dezain da.","german":"This building has a design combining Japanese and Western styles.","furigana":"たてもの|わようせっちゅう"},{"japanese":"うちの朝ごはんは、ご飯とスクランブルエッグの和洋折衷です。","romaji":"Uchi no asagohan wa, gohan to sukuranburu eggu no wayō secchū desu.","german":"Our breakfast is a Japanese-Western mix of rice and scrambled eggs.","furigana":"あさ|はん|わようせっちゅう"},{"japanese":"神前式とホテルの披露宴を組み合わせた、和洋折衷の結婚式を挙げた。","romaji":"Shinzenshiki to hoteru no hirōen o kumiawaseta, wayō secchū no kekkonshiki o ageta.","german":"We had a wedding in a mixed style: a Shinto ceremony followed by a reception at a hotel.","furigana":"しんぜんしき|ひろうえん|く|あ|わようせっちゅう|けっこんしき|あ"}],"notes":"和 stands for \"Japanese\", 洋 for \"Western\", 折衷 for \"combining the best of both, finding a middle way\". 和洋折衷 describes architecture, interiors, cuisine, fashion or ceremonies that mix Japanese and Western elements. Mostly attributive: 和洋折衷の建物／料理／結婚式. It is a neutral, very common expression; analogously there is 和洋中 for \"Japanese, Western and Chinese\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"計画が二転三転して、混乱している。","romaji":"Keikaku ga niten santen shite, konran shite iru.","german":"The plan keeps changing, and there is confusion.","furigana":"けいかく|にてんさんてん|こんらん"},{"japanese":"容疑者の証言が二転三転している。","romaji":"Yōgisha no shōgen ga niten santen shite iru.","german":"The suspect's testimony keeps changing.","furigana":"ようぎしゃ|しょうげん|にてんさんてん"},{"japanese":"試合は二転三転し、最後まで目が離せなかった。","romaji":"Shiai wa niten santen shi, saigo made me ga hanasenakatta.","german":"The game swung back and forth, and I couldn't take my eyes off it until the end.","furigana":"しあい|にてんさんてん|さいご|め|はな"}],"notes":"二転三転 literally means \"to turn over two times, three times\": something changes again and again, so that one does not know where one stands. It is used as a する-verb (計画が二転三転する) and typically describes plans, statements, decisions, scores or negotiations. The tone is often critical, for example with witness statements or political decisions (証言が二転三転する). Similar is コロコロ変わる, which is more colloquial.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自問自答を繰り返しながら、答えを探した。","romaji":"Jimon jitō o kurikaeshi nagara, kotae o sagashita.","german":"I searched for the answer, repeatedly asking myself questions.","furigana":"じもんじとう|く|かえ|こた|さが"},{"japanese":"本当にこの仕事でいいのかと、毎日自問自答している。","romaji":"Hontō ni kono shigoto de ii no ka to, mainichi jimon jitō shite iru.","german":"Every day I ask myself whether this job is really right for me.","furigana":"ほんとう|しごと|まいにちじもんじとう"},{"japanese":"試合に負けた後、「何が足りなかったのか」と自問自答した。","romaji":"Shiai ni maketa ato, \"Nani ga tarinakatta no ka\" to jimon jitō shita.","german":"After losing the game, he asked himself, \"What was I missing?\"","furigana":"しあい|ま|あと|なに|た|じもんじとう"}],"notes":"自問自答 means \"to ask oneself and answer oneself\": to brood inwardly over something and wrestle with oneself. It is used as a する-verb or noun, often with a quoted thought: 「これでいいのか」と自問自答する, 自問自答を繰り返す. It describes serious reflection on decisions, mistakes or one's own path. More neutral is 考え込む; 反省, by contrast, stresses remorse.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"右顧左眄せずに、自分の信念を貫け。","romaji":"Uko saben sezu ni, jibun no shinnen o tsuranuke.","german":"Don't hesitate; stick to your convictions.","furigana":"うこさべん|じぶん|しんねん|つらぬ"},{"japanese":"世論調査の結果に右顧左眄する政府には期待できない。","romaji":"Yoron chōsa no kekka ni uko saben suru seifu ni wa kitai dekinai.","german":"You can't expect anything from a government that dithers over every opinion poll.","furigana":"よろんちょうさ|けっか|うこさべん|せいふ|きたい"},{"japanese":"部長は役員の顔色ばかりうかがって、右顧左眄している。","romaji":"Buchō wa yakuin no kaoiro bakari ukagatte, uko saben shite iru.","german":"The department head only watches the executives' expressions and dithers.","furigana":"ぶちょう|やくいん|かおいろ|うこさべん"}],"notes":"右顧 means \"to look to the right\", 左眄 \"to glance to the left\": 右顧左眄 describes someone who, out of consideration for others' opinions, constantly hesitates and cannot decide. As an intransitive する-verb: 右顧左眄する, negated 右顧左眄せず（に）. The expression is literary and critical, often about politicians or superiors. Similar is 優柔不断; however, that stresses weakness of character, whereas 右顧左眄 stresses glancing at others.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は孤軍奮闘してプロジェクトを完成させた。","romaji":"Kare wa kogun funtō shite purojekuto o kansei saseta.","german":"He fought on alone and completed the project.","furigana":"かれ|こぐんふんとう|かんせい"},{"japanese":"人手不足の店で、店長が一人で孤軍奮闘している。","romaji":"Hitode busoku no mise de, tenchō ga hitori de kogun funtō shite iru.","german":"In the understaffed shop, the manager is struggling single-handedly.","furigana":"ひとでぶそく|みせ|てんちょう|ひとり|こぐんふんとう"},{"japanese":"試合には負けたが、エースの孤軍奮闘が光った。","romaji":"Shiai ni wa maketa ga, ēsu no kogun funtō ga hikatta.","german":"The game was lost, but the ace's one-man effort stood out.","furigana":"しあい|ま|こぐんふんとう|ひか"}],"notes":"孤軍 is an isolated army without reinforcements, 奮闘 \"to fight doggedly\". 孤軍奮闘 describes someone who battles great difficulties alone and without help, at work, in sports or in a task that actually needs a team. Used as a する-verb (孤軍奮闘する) or as a noun (〜の孤軍奮闘). It expresses recognition of the effort, often with the undertone that others did not help.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"事故現場は阿鼻叫喚の様相を呈していた。","romaji":"Jiko genba wa abi kyōkan no yōsō o teishite ita.","german":"The accident site presented a scene of utter pandemonium.","furigana":"じこげんば|あびきょうかん|ようそう|てい"},{"japanese":"地震の直後、町は阿鼻叫喚の地獄絵図と化した。","romaji":"Jishin no chokugo, machi wa abi kyōkan no jigokue-zu to kashita.","german":"Right after the earthquake, the town turned into a hellish scene.","furigana":"じしん|ちょくご|まち|あびきょうかん|じごくえず|か"},{"japanese":"セール初日の売り場は、まさに阿鼻叫喚だった。","romaji":"Sēru shonichi no uriba wa, masa ni abi kyōkan datta.","german":"On the first day of the sale, the sales floor was sheer pandemonium.","furigana":"しょにち|う|ば|あびきょうかん"}],"notes":"阿鼻 and 叫喚 are two of the Buddhist hells, where the damned scream in torment. 阿鼻叫喚 therefore describes a scene in which people scream in panic and pain, for example after disasters or accidents. Fixed frames: 阿鼻叫喚の地獄絵図, 阿鼻叫喚の様相を呈する, 阿鼻叫喚の巷と化す. In everyday life it is also used jokingly as an exaggeration for chaotic situations.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二人は異体同心で困難を乗り越えた。","romaji":"Futari wa itai dōshin de konnan o norikoeta.","german":"The two overcame the difficulties as one in heart.","furigana":"ふたり|いたいどうしん|こんなん|の|こ"},{"japanese":"夫婦は異体同心であるべきだと祖父はよく言っていた。","romaji":"Fūfu wa itai dōshin de aru beki da to sofu wa yoku itte ita.","german":"My grandfather often said that husband and wife should be two bodies with one heart.","furigana":"ふうふ|いたいどうしん|そふ|い"},{"japanese":"全社員が異体同心となって、新しい目標に挑もう。","romaji":"Zen-shain ga itai dōshin to natte, atarashii mokuhyō ni idomō.","german":"Let us all, the entire staff, unite as one and take on the new goal.","furigana":"ぜんしゃいん|いたいどうしん|あたら|もくひょう|いど"}],"notes":"Literally \"different bodies, same heart\": 異体同心 describes people who are separate individuals but hold firmly together and pursue the same goal, such as married couples, teams or communities. Typical frames: 異体同心で〜する, 異体同心となって. It is elevated and known mainly from Buddhist (Nichiren) texts and speeches; in everyday speech people say 心を一つにする. The opposite is 同体異心.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"我々は一蓮托生だ。最後まで一緒に頑張ろう。","romaji":"Wareware wa ichiren takushō da. Saigo made issho ni ganbarō.","german":"We are in this together. Let's do our best until the end.","furigana":"われわれ|いちれんたくしょう|さいご|いっしょ|がんば"},{"japanese":"不正を知りながら黙っていたなら、君も一蓮托生だよ。","romaji":"Fusei o shirinagara damatte ita nara, kimi mo ichiren takushō da yo.","german":"If you knew about the fraud and kept silent, you are in it just as deep.","furigana":"ふせい|し|だま|きみ|いちれんたくしょう"},{"japanese":"親会社が倒れれば、下請けも一蓮托生で潰れてしまう。","romaji":"Oyagaisha ga taorereba, shitauke mo ichiren takushō de tsuburete shimau.","german":"If the parent company collapses, the subcontractors will go under with it.","furigana":"おやがいしゃ|たお|したう|いちれんたくしょう|つぶ"}],"notes":"Originally Buddhist: after death, to be reborn together (托生) on the same lotus blossom (一蓮) in paradise. Today 一蓮托生 means that one shares the same fate for better or worse, often with the undertone that it concerns failure, guilt or shared responsibility. Typical: 〜とは一蓮托生だ, 一蓮托生の関係. Comparison: 呉越同舟 stresses rivals who happen to come together; 一蓮托生 stresses the shared fate.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"言行一致の人は信頼される。","romaji":"Genkō icchi no hito wa shinrai sareru.","german":"People whose words and deeds match are trusted.","furigana":"げんこういっち|ひと|しんらい"},{"japanese":"政治家にはなによりも言行一致が求められる。","romaji":"Seijika ni wa nani yori mo genkō icchi ga motomerareru.","german":"Above all, politicians are expected to practice what they preach.","furigana":"せいじか|げんこういっち|もと"},{"japanese":"「遅刻するな」と言う先生自身が遅刻しては、言行一致とは言えない。","romaji":"\"Chikoku suru na\" to iu sensei jishin ga chikoku shite wa, genkō icchi to wa ienai.","german":"If the teacher who says \"Don't be late!\" is late himself, you can't say he practices what he preaches.","furigana":"ちこく|い|せんせいじしん|ちこく|げんこういっち|い"}],"notes":"言行一致 means \"words (言) and deeds (行) agree\": one does what one says. It is used as a noun (言行一致が求められる, 言行一致の人) or as a する-verb (言行が一致している) and is a typical requirement for politicians, teachers and executives. The opposite is 言行不一致. The tone is rather formal and moral; colloquially one says 言うこととやることが同じ／違う.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"無味乾燥な説明で、聞いている方が眠くなった。","romaji":"Mumi kansō na setsumei de, kiite iru hō ga nemuku natta.","german":"The dry and dull explanation made the listeners sleepy.","furigana":"むみかんそう|せつめい|き|ほう|ねむ"},{"japanese":"数字ばかりの無味乾燥な報告書は読む気がしない。","romaji":"Sūji bakari no mumi kansō na hōkokusho wa yomu ki ga shinai.","german":"I don't feel like reading a dry report full of nothing but numbers.","furigana":"すうじ|むみかんそう|ほうこくしょ|よ|き"},{"japanese":"仕事だけの無味乾燥な生活から抜け出したい。","romaji":"Shigoto dake no mumi kansō na seikatsu kara nukedashitai.","german":"I want to escape from a dull life consisting only of work.","furigana":"しごと|むみかんそう|せいかつ|ぬ|だ"}],"notes":"無味乾燥 means \"without taste (無味) and dried out (乾燥)\": boring, dry, without any appeal. It is a な-adjective and describes texts, explanations, speeches, work or a whole life (無味乾燥な説明, 無味乾燥な毎日). It never means literally dry food, but a lack of liveliness or emotion. More everyday are つまらない or 退屈.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の剣術は天下無双と称えられた。","romaji":"Kare no kenjutsu wa tenka musō to tataerareta.","german":"His swordsmanship was praised as unrivaled in the land.","furigana":"かれ|けんじゅつ|てんかむそう|たた"},{"japanese":"伝説の力士は天下無双の怪力の持ち主だったという。","romaji":"Densetsu no rikishi wa tenka musō no kairiki no mochinushi datta to iu.","german":"The legendary sumo wrestler is said to have possessed unrivaled brute strength.","furigana":"でんせつ|りきし|てんかむそう|かいりき|も|ぬし"},{"japanese":"兄は「餃子作りなら天下無双だ」と胸を張った。","romaji":"Ani wa \"gyōza-zukuri nara tenka musō da\" to mune o hatta.","german":"My older brother proudly declared, \"When it comes to making gyoza, I'm unbeatable.\"","furigana":"あに|ぎょうざづく|てんかむそう|むね|は"}],"notes":"Literally: 天下 \"under heaven, the whole world\" + 無双 \"without a second, peerless\", that is, \"unsurpassed in the whole world\". It is used for outstanding strength or martial skill, mostly with a historical or heroic ring: 天下無双の剣豪, 天下無双の怪力, 天下無双と称される. Close in meaning are 天下一 and 古今無双. In today's everyday speech it sounds exaggerated and is therefore gladly used jokingly for one's own talents; attributively it takes の.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"被害者の家族に誠心誠意謝罪した。","romaji":"Higaisha no kazoku ni seishin seii shazai shita.","german":"He apologized sincerely to the victim's family.","furigana":"ひがいしゃ|かぞく|せいしんせいいしゃざい"},{"japanese":"お客様のご要望には、誠心誠意対応いたします。","romaji":"Okyakusama no goyōbō ni wa, seishin seii taiō itashimasu.","german":"We will respond to our customers' requests with all sincerity.","furigana":"きゃくさま|ようぼう|せいしんせいいたいおう"},{"japanese":"誠心誠意尽くしたが、彼女の気持ちは変わらなかった。","romaji":"Seishin seii tsukushita ga, kanojo no kimochi wa kawaranakatta.","german":"I devoted myself to her wholeheartedly, but her feelings did not change.","furigana":"せいしんせいいつ|かのじょ|きも|か"}],"notes":"誠心誠意 doubles 誠 \"sincerity\" in 誠心 \"sincere heart\" and 誠意 \"sincere intention\": with complete honesty and devotion. It is mostly used directly as an adverb before a verb: 誠心誠意謝る, 誠心誠意対応する, 誠心誠意尽くす. It is polite-formal and typical of apologies, customer service and company statements. 誠心誠意を込めて is redundant (it says the same thing twice); correct is 誠意を込めて or simply 誠心誠意.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"田舎で自給自足の生活をしている。","romaji":"Inaka de jikyū jisoku no seikatsu o shite iru.","german":"He lives a self-sufficient life in the countryside.","furigana":"いなか|じきゅうじそく|せいかつ"},{"japanese":"日本は食料を自給自足することができない。","romaji":"Nihon wa shokuryō o jikyū jisoku suru koto ga dekinai.","german":"Japan cannot be self-sufficient in food.","furigana":"にほん|しょくりょう|じきゅうじそく"},{"japanese":"うちでは野菜をほとんど自給自足しています。","romaji":"Uchi de wa yasai o hotondo jikyū jisoku shite imasu.","german":"At our house, we produce almost all our vegetables ourselves.","furigana":"やさい|じきゅうじそく"}],"notes":"自給 \"supplying oneself\" and 自足 \"being content with oneself\" together make \"self-sufficiency\". One speaks of a 自給自足の生活 in the countryside, but also of states that are 自給自足する in food or energy. As a する-verb, the supplied good takes を: 野菜を自給自足する. Related is 自給率 \"self-sufficiency rate\". Neutral.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"両国の関係は一触即発の状態にある。","romaji":"Ryōkoku no kankei wa isshoku sokuhatsu no jōtai ni aru.","german":"Relations between the two countries are on a knife's edge.","furigana":"りょうこく|かんけい|いっしょくそくはつ|じょうたい"},{"japanese":"会議室は一触即発の雰囲気で、誰も口を開かなかった。","romaji":"Kaigishitsu wa isshoku sokuhatsu no fun'iki de, dare mo kuchi o hirakanakatta.","german":"The meeting room had a volatile atmosphere, and no one opened their mouth.","furigana":"かいぎしつ|いっしょくそくはつ|ふんいき|だれ|くち|ひら"},{"japanese":"二人は一触即発で、いつけんかが始まってもおかしくなかった。","romaji":"Futari wa isshoku sokuhatsu de, itsu kenka ga hajimatte mo okashiku nakatta.","german":"The two were so tense that a fight could have broken out at any moment.","furigana":"ふたり|いっしょくそくはつ|はじ"}],"notes":"一触即発 means \"one touch (一触) and immediately (即) explosion (発)\": a situation is so tense that even a small trigger sets off a conflict. Typical are 一触即発の状態／事態／雰囲気 and 一触即発の危機. It is used for international crises, but also for quarrels between people or tense meetings. The expression is rather formal and appears a lot in the news; more everyday is ピリピリしている.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自然と人間の共存共栄を目指すべきだ。","romaji":"Shizen to ningen no kyōzon kyōei o mezasu beki da.","german":"We should aim for coexistence and mutual prosperity between nature and humans.","furigana":"しぜん|にんげん|きょうぞんきょうえい|めざ"},{"japanese":"地元の商店と大型スーパーが共存共栄できる道を探っている。","romaji":"Jimoto no shōten to ōgata sūpā ga kyōzon kyōei dekiru michi o sagutte iru.","german":"They are exploring a way for local shops and large supermarkets to coexist and thrive together.","furigana":"じもと|しょうてん|おおがた|きょうぞんきょうえい|みち|さぐ"},{"japanese":"取引先とは共存共栄の関係を築きたいと考えています。","romaji":"Torihikisaki to wa kyōzon kyōei no kankei o kizukitai to kangaete imasu.","german":"We would like to build a mutually beneficial relationship with our business partners.","furigana":"とりひきさき|きょうぞんきょうえい|かんけい|きず|かんが"}],"notes":"共存共栄 means \"to exist together (共存) and to prosper together (共栄)\": two or more sides live side by side and both profit from it. Typical are AとBの共存共栄, 共存共栄を目指す／図る and 共存共栄の関係. The expression comes from business, politics and environmental discussions and is formal. 共存 alone means only \"coexistence\"; 共存共栄 additionally stresses mutual benefit. The reading きょうそん for 共存 is also common.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は用意周到に旅行の準備をした。","romaji":"Kanojo wa yōi shūtō ni ryokō no junbi o shita.","german":"She prepared for the trip meticulously.","furigana":"かのじょ|よういしゅうとう|りょこう|じゅんび"},{"japanese":"用意周到な彼は、雨に備えて傘を二本持ってきていた。","romaji":"Yōi shūtō na kare wa, ame ni sonaete kasa o nihon motte kite ita.","german":"Always well prepared, he had brought two umbrellas in case of rain.","furigana":"よういしゅうとう|かれ|あめ|そな|かさ|にほんも"},{"japanese":"警察によると、犯行は用意周到に計画されていたという。","romaji":"Keisatsu ni yoru to, hankō wa yōi shūtō ni keikaku sarete ita to iu.","german":"According to the police, the crime had been carefully planned.","furigana":"けいさつ|はんこう|よういしゅうとう|けいかく"}],"notes":"用意周到 combines 用意 \"preparation\" and 周到 \"thorough, careful\": everything is prepared down to the detail, and every eventuality has been thought of. It is a な-adjective: 用意周到な人, 用意周到に準備する／計画する. It is mostly praise, but in crime and news texts it is also neutral for carefully planned crimes (用意周到な犯行). Similar but more everyday is 準備がいい; 念入り stresses more the thoroughness of an action.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"心機一転、新しい仕事を始めた。","romaji":"Shinki itten, atarashii shigoto o hajimeta.","german":"He made a fresh start and began a new job.","furigana":"しんきいってん|あたら|しごと|はじ"},{"japanese":"引っ越しを機に心機一転した。","romaji":"Hikkoshi o ki ni shinki itten shita.","german":"Taking the move as an opportunity, he made a completely fresh start.","furigana":"ひ|こ|き|しんきいってん"},{"japanese":"新しい年は心機一転、毎朝ジョギングをするつもりです。","romaji":"Atarashii toshi wa shinki itten, maiasa jogingu o suru tsumori desu.","german":"In the new year I'm making a fresh start and plan to jog every morning.","furigana":"あたら|とし|しんきいってん|まいあさ"}],"notes":"心機 means \"state of mind\" and 一転 means \"to turn completely\": you change your inner attitude and start anew. Typically used as an introductory phrase (心機一転、〜を始める), as a する-verb (心機一転する), and with a triggering occasion: 〜を機に心機一転. Note: writing it as 新規一転 is a common mistake. Neutral; often found in New Year's and farewell greetings.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"危機管理が甘かったため、大きな被害が出た。","romaji":"Kiki kanri ga amakatta tame, ōkina higai ga deta.","german":"Because crisis management was lax, great damage resulted.","furigana":"ききかんり|あま|おお|ひがい|で"},{"japanese":"会社は地震に備えて危機管理のマニュアルを作った。","romaji":"Kaisha wa jishin ni sonaete kiki kanri no manyuaru o tsukutta.","german":"The company created a crisis management manual in preparation for earthquakes.","furigana":"かいしゃ|じしん|そな|ききかんり|つく"},{"japanese":"リーダーには危機管理の能力が必要だ。","romaji":"Rīdā ni wa kiki kanri no nōryoku ga hitsuyō da.","german":"A leader needs the ability to manage crises.","furigana":"ききかんり|のうりょく|ひつよう"}],"notes":"危機 \"crisis, danger\" and 管理 \"administration, management\": crisis management, that is, prevention and response in disasters, accidents or scandals. Not a figurative idiom but a technical term from business, politics and administration. Typical: 危機管理が甘い \"crisis management is lax\", 危機管理能力, 危機管理マニュアル. Formal and common in the news.","levelNote":"Compound: 危機 (N1) + 管理 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"競争の激しい業界では自然淘汰が起こる。","romaji":"Kyōsō no hageshii gyōkai de wa shizen tōta ga okoru.","german":"In a fiercely competitive industry, natural selection takes place.","furigana":"きょうそう|はげ|ぎょうかい|しぜんとうた|お"},{"japanese":"自然淘汰によって、環境に適した種が生き残る。","romaji":"Shizen tōta ni yotte, kankyō ni tekishita shu ga ikinokoru.","german":"Through natural selection, species suited to their environment survive.","furigana":"しぜんとうた|かんきょう|てき|しゅ|い|のこ"},{"japanese":"時代に合わない古いサービスは、いずれ自然淘汰されるだろう。","romaji":"Jidai ni awanai furui sābisu wa, izure shizen tōta sareru darō.","german":"Old services that do not fit the times will eventually be weeded out naturally.","furigana":"じだい|あ|ふる|しぜんとうた"}],"notes":"自然淘汰 is the technical term for \"natural selection\" in biology: 淘汰 originally means \"to sift out, to sort out\". Figuratively it describes how, in business or society, what does not fit drops out by itself; then it often appears in the passive: 淘汰される, 自然淘汰されていく. Typical are 自然淘汰が起こる／働く and 自然淘汰によって. The expression is factual-formal; for competition among companies one also hears 生き残り競争.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は優柔不断で、なかなか決められない。","romaji":"Kare wa yūjū fudan de, nakanaka kimerarenai.","german":"He is so indecisive that he can hardly make a decision.","furigana":"かれ|ゆうじゅうふだん|き"},{"japanese":"優柔不断な性格を直したい。","romaji":"Yūjū fudan na seikaku o naoshitai.","german":"I want to change my indecisive personality.","furigana":"ゆうじゅうふだん|せいかく|なお"},{"japanese":"店員に「どちらにしますか」と聞かれても、優柔不断な私は選べなかった。","romaji":"Ten'in ni \"dochira ni shimasu ka\" to kikarete mo, yūjū fudan na watashi wa erabenakatta.","german":"Even when the shop assistant asked which one I'd take, I was so indecisive that I couldn't choose.","furigana":"てんいん|き|ゆうじゅうふだん|わたし|えら"}],"notes":"優柔 means \"soft, yielding\" and 不断 means \"lacking resolve\", together giving \"indecisive, wavering\". It describes a character trait of being unable to decide: 優柔不断な性格／人／態度, 優柔不断で決められない. Slightly critical; its opposite is 決断力がある \"decisive\". Neutral, common in everyday speech, also used self-critically.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"チームが一致団結して勝利を収めた。","romaji":"Chīmu ga icchi danketsu shite shōri o osameta.","german":"The team stood united and won the victory.","furigana":"いっちだんけつ|しょうり|おさ"},{"japanese":"クラス全員で一致団結して、文化祭の準備をした。","romaji":"Kurasu zen'in de icchi danketsu shite, bunkasai no junbi o shita.","german":"The whole class worked together as one to prepare for the school festival.","furigana":"ぜんいん|いっちだんけつ|ぶんかさい|じゅんび"},{"japanese":"今こそ町の人々が一致団結するときだ。","romaji":"Ima koso machi no hitobito ga icchi danketsu suru toki da.","german":"Now is the time for the people of the town to stand together.","furigana":"いま|まち|ひとびと|いっちだんけつ"}],"notes":"一致 \"agreement\" and 団結 \"solidarity\": a group stands together and works toward a common goal. Mostly as 一致団結して〜する or 一致団結する. Typical for teams, classes, companies or whole communities; often in appeals and speeches. Neutral to slightly solemn, with a positive connotation.","levelNote":"Compound: 一致 (N3) + 団結 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"初志貫徹で、十年かけて夢を実現した。","romaji":"Shoshi kantetsu de, jūnen kakete yume o jitsugen shita.","german":"Sticking to his original resolve, he realized his dream over ten years.","furigana":"しょしかんてつ|じゅうねん|ゆめ|じつげん"},{"japanese":"何度もやめたくなったが、初志貫徹して医者になった。","romaji":"Nando mo yametaku natta ga, shoshi kantetsu shite isha ni natta.","german":"She often wanted to quit, but she stayed true to her goal and became a doctor.","furigana":"なんど|しょしかんてつ|いしゃ"},{"japanese":"私のモットーは初志貫徹です。","romaji":"Watashi no mottō wa shoshi kantetsu desu.","german":"My motto is to see through the resolve I made at the start.","furigana":"わたし|しょしかんてつ"}],"notes":"初志 means \"original resolve\" and 貫徹 means \"to carry through, to realize to the end\": you stay true to your first decision even when it gets hard. Patterns: 初志貫徹する, 初志貫徹で〜, often as a life motto (「初志貫徹」が座右の銘). Rather literary and solemn, popular in interviews with athletes or in graduation speeches.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子供たちは元気一杯に遊んでいる。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa genki ippai ni asonde iru.","german":"The children are playing full of energy.","furigana":"こども|げんきいっぱい|あそ"},{"japanese":"病気が治って、今は元気一杯です。","romaji":"Byōki ga naotte, ima wa genki ippai desu.","german":"I've recovered from the illness, and now I'm full of energy.","furigana":"びょうき|なお|いま|げんきいっぱい"}],"notes":"元気一杯 means full of energy or vitality. With に it describes an activity: 元気一杯に遊ぶ. With な it stands before a noun: 元気一杯な子ども. The also attested spelling 元気いっぱい is kept as an alias. This card is about the whole compound, not about 一杯 as a single word for a glass or a full amount.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このコンビニは年中無休で営業している。","romaji":"Kono konbini wa nenjū mukyū de eigyō shite iru.","german":"This convenience store is open all year round without a day off.","furigana":"ねんじゅうむきゅう|えいぎょう"},{"japanese":"母は家族のために年中無休で働いています。","romaji":"Haha wa kazoku no tame ni nenjū mukyū de hataraite imasu.","german":"My mother works for her family without a single day off.","furigana":"はは|かぞく|ねんじゅうむきゅう|はたら"},{"japanese":"店の入り口に「年中無休」と書いてあります。","romaji":"Mise no iriguchi ni \"nenjū mukyū\" to kaite arimasu.","german":"At the entrance of the shop it says \"Open every day\".","furigana":"みせ|い|ぐち|ねんじゅうむきゅう|か"}],"notes":"Literally: 年中 \"throughout the year\" and 無休 \"without a day off\". It means \"open every day of the year\"; this often appears on signs and in advertising. Typical frames are 年中無休で営業する, 年中無休の店 and, jokingly for people, 年中無休で働く. Related are 24時間営業 \"open around the clock\" and the opposite 定休日 \"regular day off\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本は四季折々の美しさがある。","romaji":"Nihon wa shiki oriori no utsukushisa ga aru.","german":"Japan has its own beauty in every season.","furigana":"にほん|しきおりおり|うつく"},{"japanese":"この庭では四季折々の花が楽しめます。","romaji":"Kono niwa de wa shiki oriori no hana ga tanoshimemasu.","german":"In this garden you can enjoy different flowers in every season.","furigana":"にわ|しきおりおり|はな|たの"},{"japanese":"その旅館では四季折々の料理が出ます。","romaji":"Sono ryokan de wa shiki oriori no ryōri ga demasu.","german":"This ryokan serves dishes of the respective season.","furigana":"りょかん|しきおりおり|りょうり|で"}],"notes":"Literally: 四季 \"the four seasons\" and 折々 \"each at its own time\". It refers to what each season brings that is its own. It almost always stands with の before a noun: 四季折々の花, 四季折々の景色, 四季折々の料理; adverbial 四季折々に is rarer. The expression sounds elevated and is typical of travel brochures, garden descriptions and menus.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"毎日毎日同じ仕事で疲れた。","romaji":"Mainichi mainichi onaji shigoto de tsukareta.","german":"Day after day, the same work - I'm exhausted.","furigana":"まいにちまいにちおな|しごと|つか"},{"japanese":"毎日毎日練習して、やっとこの曲が弾けるようになりました。","romaji":"Mainichi mainichi renshū shite, yatto kono kyoku ga hikeru yō ni narimashita.","german":"I practiced day after day, and now I can finally play this piece.","furigana":"まいにちまいにちれんしゅう|きょく|ひ"}],"notes":"毎日毎日 intensifies 毎日 and means day after day, every single day. The repetition can emphasize weariness, as with the same work every day, or the persistence of an activity. As an adverb, the compound stands before the content of the sentence without an additional particle; with の it can also modify a noun.","levelNote":"Compound: 毎日 (N5) + 毎日 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いつか世界一周の旅をしたい。","romaji":"Itsuka sekai isshū no tabi o shitai.","german":"Someday I want to take a trip around the world.","furigana":"せかいいっしゅう|たび"},{"japanese":"祖父は仕事をやめてから、船で世界一周しました。","romaji":"Sofu wa shigoto o yamete kara, fune de sekai isshū shimashita.","german":"After he stopped working, my grandfather went around the world by ship.","furigana":"そふ|しごと|ふね|せかいいっしゅう"},{"japanese":"世界一周のチケットはいくらぐらいですか。","romaji":"Sekai isshū no chiketto wa ikura gurai desu ka.","german":"About how much does a round-the-world ticket cost?","furigana":"せかいいっしゅう"}],"notes":"Literally: 世界 \"world\" and 一周 \"one round, once around\". It means a journey once around the world. Typical frames are 世界一周の旅, 世界一周旅行 and, as a する-verb, 世界一周する; with a means of transport: 船で世界一周する. The register is neutral; 一周 alone is also used for laps on a track or around a lake.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日明日中にお返事します。","romaji":"Kyō asu-jū ni ohenji shimasu.","german":"I will reply to you today or tomorrow.","furigana":"きょうあすじゅう|へんじ"},{"japanese":"この問題は今日明日には解決しません。","romaji":"Kono mondai wa kyō asu ni wa kaiketsu shimasen.","german":"This problem won't be solved as soon as today or tomorrow.","furigana":"もんだい|きょうあす|かいけつ"}],"notes":"今日明日 here means today or tomorrow, or in the very near future. 今日明日中に sets a very near deadline. On this card the compound is read きょうあす, with あす as the reading of 明日. With 今日明日には…ない one denies that something can be expected within this short time. The meaning \"today and tomorrow together\" is a different usage.","levelNote":"Compound: 今日 (N5) + 明日 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本は春夏秋冬の四季がはっきりしている。","romaji":"Nihon wa shunka shūtō no shiki ga hakkiri shite iru.","german":"In Japan the four seasons - spring, summer, autumn and winter - are distinct.","furigana":"にほん|しゅんかしゅうとう|しき"},{"japanese":"春夏秋冬の景色を写真に撮りました。","romaji":"Shunka shūtō no keshiki o shashin ni torimashita.","german":"I took photos of the scenery in spring, summer, autumn and winter.","furigana":"しゅんかしゅうとう|けしき|しゃしん|と"}],"notes":"春夏秋冬 names the four seasons in order: spring, summer, autumn and winter. The whole compound is read しゅんかしゅうとう. With の it can express a seasonal reference before a noun. The card shows the Sino-Japanese reading of the compound; the individual seasons are also read 春／はる, 夏／なつ, 秋／あき and 冬／ふゆ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自分自身を信じることが大切だ。","romaji":"Jibun jishin o shinjiru koto ga taisetsu da.","german":"It is important to believe in yourself.","furigana":"じぶんじしん|しん|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"これは自分自身の問題だから、自分で決めます。","romaji":"Kore wa jibun jishin no mondai da kara, jibun de kimemasu.","german":"This is my own problem, so I'll decide myself.","furigana":"じぶんじしん|もんだい|じぶん|き"},{"japanese":"旅行をして、自分自身のことがよく分かりました。","romaji":"Ryokō o shite, jibun jishin no koto ga yoku wakarimashita.","german":"Traveling taught me a lot about myself.","furigana":"りょこう|じぶんじしん|わ"}],"notes":"Literally: 自分 \"oneself\" and 自身 \"oneself, one's own person\" - the doubling is emphatic: \"oneself and nobody else\". It works like a pronoun with particles: 自分自身を信じる, 自分自身で決める, 自分自身のために. Compared with plain 自分 it stresses that it really is about one's own person, often in advice and reflections. The register is neutral.","levelNote":"Compound: 自分 (N5) + 自身 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"工事現場では安全第一だ。","romaji":"Kōji genba de wa anzen daiichi da.","german":"At a construction site, safety comes first.","furigana":"こうじげんば|あんぜんだいいち"},{"japanese":"雪の日は安全第一で、ゆっくり運転してください。","romaji":"Yuki no hi wa anzen daiichi de, yukkuri unten shite kudasai.","german":"On snowy days, safety comes first, so please drive slowly.","furigana":"ゆき|ひ|あんぜんだいいち|うんてん"},{"japanese":"山登りは安全第一だから、天気が悪い日はやめよう。","romaji":"Yamanobori wa anzen daiichi da kara, tenki ga warui hi wa yameyō.","german":"Safety comes first in mountain climbing, so let's call it off on days with bad weather.","furigana":"やまのぼ|あんぜんだいいち|てんき|わる|ひ"}],"notes":"Literally: 安全 \"safety\" and 第一 \"number one, first\" - \"safety first\". The expression is a well-known slogan on construction sites and in factories (often with a green cross), but is also used in everyday life. Typical frames are 〜は安全第一だ, 安全第一で〜する and 何よりも安全第一. The register is neutral; on a sign it is terse and formulaic.","levelNote":"Compound: 安全 (N4) + 第一 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は有言実行の人だから信頼できる。","romaji":"Kare wa yūgen jikkō no hito da kara shinrai dekiru.","german":"He always keeps his word, so you can trust him.","furigana":"かれ|ゆうげんじっこう|ひと|しんらい"},{"japanese":"今年こそ十キロやせると言ったので、有言実行します。","romaji":"Kotoshi koso jukkiro yaseru to itta node, yūgen jikkō shimasu.","german":"I announced that I would lose ten kilos this year - and now I'm going to follow through.","furigana":"ことし|じゅっ|い|ゆうげんじっこう"},{"japanese":"社長は有言実行で、約束どおり新しい工場を作った。","romaji":"Shachō wa yūgen jikkō de, yakusoku dōri atarashii kōjō o tsukutta.","german":"The company president kept his word and built a new factory as promised.","furigana":"しゃちょう|ゆうげんじっこう|やくそく|あたら|こうじょう|つく"}],"notes":"Literally: 有言 \"to have words, to announce something\" and 実行 \"to carry out\" - you say beforehand what you will do, and then you actually do it. The expression is a play on the older 不言実行 \"act without making many words\". Typical frames are 有言実行の人, 有言実行する and 〜は有言実行だ; it sounds appreciative and motivating. The register is neutral to slightly formal.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの夫婦は一心同体だ。","romaji":"Ano fūfu wa isshin dōtai da.","german":"That married couple is one heart and soul.","furigana":"ふうふ|いっしんどうたい"},{"japanese":"選手とファンは一心同体です。","romaji":"Senshu to fan wa isshin dōtai desu.","german":"Players and fans are one inseparable unit.","furigana":"せんしゅ|いっしんどうたい"},{"japanese":"社員が一心同体となって、会社を立て直した。","romaji":"Shain ga isshin dōtai to natte, kaisha o tatenaoshita.","german":"The employees pulled together as one and put the company back on its feet.","furigana":"しゃいん|いっしんどうたい|かいしゃ|た|なお"}],"notes":"Literally \"one heart, same body\": two or more people are so closely united that they think and act like one person. Typical for married couples, partners, or teams and fans: 〜は一心同体だ, 一心同体となって〜する. Not to be confused with 以心伝心 \"understanding each other without words\". Neutral, warm and positive.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は言行不一致だから信用できない。","romaji":"Kare wa genkō fuitchi da kara shin'yō dekinai.","german":"His words and deeds don't match, so he can't be trusted.","furigana":"かれ|げんこうふいっち|しんよう"},{"japanese":"「健康が一番」と言いながら毎晩飲んでいるなんて、言行不一致だよ。","romaji":"\"Kenkō ga ichiban\" to iinagara maiban nonde iru nante, genkō fuitchi da yo.","german":"Saying \"health comes first\" and then drinking every night - your words and deeds don't match.","furigana":"けんこう|いちばん|い|まいばんの|げんこうふいっち"},{"japanese":"首相の言行不一致が、新聞で厳しく批判された。","romaji":"Shushō no genkō fuitchi ga, shinbun de kibishiku hihan sareta.","german":"The prime minister's gap between words and deeds was sharply criticized in the newspapers.","furigana":"しゅしょう|げんこうふいっち|しんぶん|きび|ひはん"}],"notes":"言行不一致 is composed of 言行 \"words and deeds\" and 不一致 \"lack of agreement\": someone says one thing and does another. The opposite is 言行一致, which is regarded as a virtue. Typical patterns are 言行不一致な人, 〜は言行不一致だ and 言行不一致を指摘される; the reproach hits politicians and superiors especially often. The expression is rather matter-of-fact and formal, and sounds clearly like criticism.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は自由奔放に生きている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa jiyū honpō ni ikite iru.","german":"She lives freely and uninhibitedly.","furigana":"かのじょ|じゆうほんぽう|い"},{"japanese":"弟は自由奔放な性格で、家族をよく驚かせる。","romaji":"Otōto wa jiyū honpō na seikaku de, kazoku o yoku odorokaseru.","german":"My younger brother is a free spirit and often surprises the family.","furigana":"おとうと|じゆうほんぽう|せいかく|かぞく|おどろ"},{"japanese":"子供たちは大きな紙に自由奔放に絵を描いていた。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa ōkina kami ni jiyū honpō ni e o kaite ita.","german":"The children were drawing freely and uninhibitedly on a big sheet of paper.","furigana":"こども|おお|かみ|じゆうほんぽう|え|か"}],"notes":"自由 means \"free\" and 奔放 means \"unrestrained, exuberant\": someone lives or acts freely, without caring about rules and conventions. Patterns: 自由奔放な性格／生き方, 自由奔放に生きる／描く. Depending on context admiring (\"unconventional, creative\") or slightly critical (\"unruly\"). Neutral to literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この箱には天地無用と書いてある。","romaji":"Kono hako ni wa tenchi muyō to kaite aru.","german":"\"Tenchi muyō\" is written on this box.","furigana":"はこ|てんちむよう|か"},{"japanese":"ケーキを送るので、箱に天地無用のシールを貼った。","romaji":"Kēki o okuru node, hako ni tenchi muyō no shīru o hatta.","german":"Since I am sending a cake, I put a \"this way up\" sticker on the box.","furigana":"おく|はこ|てんちむよう|は"},{"japanese":"天地無用は「上下を気にしなくていい」という意味ではありません。","romaji":"Tenchi muyō wa \"jōge o ki ni shinakute ii\" to iu imi de wa arimasen.","german":"\"Tenchi muyō\" does not mean \"it doesn't matter which way is up or down.\"","furigana":"てんちむよう|じょうげ|き|いみ"}],"notes":"天地 \"top and bottom\" and 無用 \"prohibited, not allowed\" (as in 他言無用 \"do not tell anyone\"): the notice on packages means \"do not drop, do not turn upside down\". Many Japanese people misunderstand it as \"top and bottom don't matter\", which is why an arrow or 上 is often added as well. A fixed print or sticker (天地無用のシール); formal, almost only in writing.","levelNote":"Compound: 天地 (N1) + 無用 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このプロジェクトには全力投球で取り組む。","romaji":"Kono purojekuto ni wa zenryoku tōkyū de torikumu.","german":"I'm tackling this project with everything I've got.","furigana":"ぜんりょくとうきゅう|と|く"},{"japanese":"今は仕事より子育てに全力投球しています。","romaji":"Ima wa shigoto yori kosodate ni zenryoku tōkyū shite imasu.","german":"Right now I'm devoting myself to raising my children more than to my job.","furigana":"いま|しごと|こそだ|ぜんりょくとうきゅう"},{"japanese":"ピッチャーは最後の一球まで全力投球した。","romaji":"Pitchā wa saigo no ikkyū made zenryoku tōkyū shita.","german":"The pitcher threw with all his strength until the very last pitch.","furigana":"さいご|いっきゅう|ぜんりょくとうきゅう"}],"notes":"Literally \"to pitch with full strength\" (全力 + 投球), originally from baseball. Figuratively it means to devote oneself to something with all one's strength: 〜に全力投球する, 全力投球で取り組む. Common in interviews and resolutions about work, training or family. Neutral, sporty and motivating.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"面接では自然体でいることが大切だ。","romaji":"Mensetsu de wa shizentai de iru koto ga taisetsu da.","german":"In a job interview, it is important to be natural and relaxed.","furigana":"めんせつ|しぜんたい|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"試合の前は緊張するけど、自然体で臨みたいと思います。","romaji":"Shiai no mae wa kinchō suru kedo, shizentai de nozomitai to omoimasu.","german":"I get nervous before the match, but I want to take it on in a relaxed way.","furigana":"しあい|まえ|きんちょう|しぜんたい|のぞ|おも"},{"japanese":"彼女の自然体の笑顔が、多くのファンに愛されている。","romaji":"Kanojo no shizentai no egao ga, ōku no fan ni aisarete iru.","german":"Her natural smile is loved by many fans.","furigana":"かのじょ|しぜんたい|えがお|おお|あい"}],"notes":"自然体 comes from martial arts such as judo, where it refers to the relaxed, upright basic stance. In everyday life it means a natural, unforced manner - without pretending or putting yourself under pressure. Typical are 自然体でいる, 自然体で臨む and 自然体の〜 (自然体の笑顔); the word has positive connotations and often appears in interviews with athletes or actors. Its opposites would be, for example, 無理をする or かっこつける.","levelNote":"Compound: 自然 (N4) + 体 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の説明は意味不明だった。","romaji":"Kare no setsumei wa imi fumei datta.","german":"His explanation made no sense.","furigana":"かれ|せつめい|いみふめい"},{"japanese":"このメール、意味不明なんだけど。","romaji":"Kono mēru, imi fumei nan da kedo.","german":"This email makes no sense to me.","furigana":"いみふめい"},{"japanese":"弟は夜中に意味不明な寝言を言っていた。","romaji":"Otōto wa yonaka ni imi fumei na negoto o itte ita.","german":"My little brother was saying incomprehensible things in his sleep in the middle of the night.","furigana":"おとうと|よなか|いみふめい|ねごと|い"}],"notes":"意味 \"meaning\" and 不明 \"unclear\": something is unintelligible or makes no sense. As a na-adjective or predicate: 意味不明な言葉／行動, 〜は意味不明だ. In youth slang it is very common as an exclamation (\"What's that supposed to mean?\"), even shortened to イミフ. Neutral in writing, in conversation often annoyed and colloquial.","levelNote":"Compound: 意味 (N5) + 不明 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この漫画は若者に大人気だ。","romaji":"Kono manga wa wakamono ni daininki da.","german":"This manga is extremely popular with young people.","furigana":"まんが|わかもの|だいにんき"},{"japanese":"駅前に新しくできたパン屋は大人気で、いつも行列ができている。","romaji":"Ekimae ni atarashiku dekita pan'ya wa daininki de, itsumo gyōretsu ga dekite iru.","german":"The new bakery in front of the station is so popular that there is always a line.","furigana":"えきまえ|あたら|や|だいにんき|ぎょうれつ"},{"japanese":"この大人気のゲームは、発売日にすぐ売り切れた。","romaji":"Kono daininki no gēmu wa, hatsubaibi ni sugu urikireta.","german":"This hugely popular game sold out immediately on the release date.","furigana":"だいにんき|はつばいび|う|き"}],"notes":"大人気（だいにんき） intensifies 人気 \"popularity\" with 大 to \"hugely popular\". Typical are 〜に大人気だ (子どもに大人気), 大人気の店 and 大人気商品 - a favorite word in advertising and the news; 超人気 sounds even more casual. Note: the same kanji read おとなげ appear in 大人気ない \"childish, immature (for an adult)\"; this has nothing to do with popularity and is therefore often written 大人げない.","levelNote":"Compound: 大人 (N5) + 気 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この法律は賛否両論を呼んでいる。","romaji":"Kono hōritsu wa sanpi ryōron o yonde iru.","german":"This law is provoking divided opinions.","furigana":"ほうりつ|さんぴりょうろん|よ"},{"japanese":"その映画の結末は賛否両論だった。","romaji":"Sono eiga no ketsumatsu wa sanpi ryōron datta.","german":"The ending of that movie received very mixed reactions.","furigana":"えいが|けつまつ|さんぴりょうろん"},{"japanese":"新しい制服については、学校の中でも賛否両論がある。","romaji":"Atarashii seifuku ni tsuite wa, gakkō no naka de mo sanpi ryōron ga aru.","german":"Opinions are divided on the new school uniform, even within the school.","furigana":"あたら|せいふく|がっこう|なか|さんぴりょうろん"}],"notes":"賛否 means \"approval and disapproval\" and 両論 means \"both opinions\": on a topic there are both supporters and opponents. Typical patterns: 賛否両論がある, 賛否両論を呼ぶ \"to provoke controversial reactions\" and 〜は賛否両論だ. Related: 賛否が分かれる \"opinions are divided\". Neutral, common in the news and in reviews.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先生の話を一言半句も聞き漏らさなかった。","romaji":"Sensei no hanashi o ichigon hanku mo kikimorasanakatta.","german":"I didn't miss a single word of the teacher's talk.","furigana":"せんせい|はなし|いちごんはんく|き|も"},{"japanese":"その件について、彼は一言半句も話さなかった。","romaji":"Sono ken ni tsuite, kare wa ichigon hanku mo hanasanakatta.","german":"He didn't say a single word about that matter.","furigana":"けん|かれ|いちごんはんく|はな"},{"japanese":"作家の原稿は一言半句も変えずに本にした。","romaji":"Sakka no genkō wa ichigon hanku mo kaezu ni hon ni shita.","german":"The author's manuscript was made into a book without changing a single word.","furigana":"さっか|げんこう|いちごんはんく|か|ほん"}],"notes":"一言 (read いちごん here, not ひとこと) means \"one word\" and 半句 means \"half a phrase\": the smallest conceivable amount of words. Almost always with も and a negative: 一言半句も聞き漏らさない \"not to miss a single word\", 一言半句も話さない. Literary and elevated; in everyday speech one rather says 一言も.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"弱音を吐かないで、最後まで頑張ろう。","romaji":"Yowane o hakanaide, saigo made ganbarō.","german":"Don't complain; let's hang in there until the end.","furigana":"よわね|は|さいご|がんば"},{"japanese":"母は病気の間も、一度も弱音を吐かなかった。","romaji":"Haha wa byōki no aida mo, ichido mo yowane o hakanakatta.","german":"My mother never once complained, even while she was ill.","furigana":"はは|びょうき|あいだ|いちど|よわね|は"},{"japanese":"仕事がつらくて、友達につい弱音を吐いてしまった。","romaji":"Shigoto ga tsurakute, tomodachi ni tsui yowane o haite shimatta.","german":"Work was so hard that I ended up venting my weakness to a friend.","furigana":"しごと|ともだち|よわね|は"}],"notes":"弱音を吐く is composed of 弱音 \"faint-hearted talk, complaints\" and 吐く \"to spit out, to utter\": you whine, complain about difficulties or say you can't go on. It is mostly used in the negative or as an admonition: 弱音を吐くな, 弱音を吐かずに頑張る, 一度も弱音を吐かなかった. If you do pour your heart out to a confidant for once, you say つい弱音を吐いてしまった. The expression is neutral and common in everyday speech.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"このプロジェクトは共同作業で進めている。","romaji":"Kono purojekuto wa kyōdō sagyō de susumete iru.","german":"This project is being carried out as joint work.","furigana":"きょうどうさぎょう|すす"},{"japanese":"ケーキカットは二人の初めての共同作業です。","romaji":"Kēki katto wa futari no hajimete no kyōdō sagyō desu.","german":"Cutting the cake is the couple's first joint task.","furigana":"ふたり|はじ|きょうどうさぎょう"},{"japanese":"グループで共同作業をするときは、役割を決めることが大切だ。","romaji":"Gurūpu de kyōdō sagyō o suru toki wa, yakuwari o kimeru koto ga taisetsu da.","german":"When working in a group, it is important to assign roles.","furigana":"きょうどうさぎょう|やくわり|き|たいせつ"}],"notes":"共同 means \"joint\" and 作業 means \"work, task\": work that several people do together. Not a figurative idiom but an ordinary compound: 共同作業をする, 共同作業で進める. Well known is the wedding phrase 二人の初めての共同作業 at the cake cutting. Neutral; 協力 stresses helping more, 共同作業 the doing together.","levelNote":"Compound: 共同 (N3) + 作業 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"投資は自己責任で行ってください。","romaji":"Tōshi wa jiko sekinin de okonatte kudasai.","german":"Please invest at your own risk.","furigana":"とうし|じこせきにん|おこな"},{"japanese":"立入禁止の場所に入るのは自己責任だ。","romaji":"Tachiiri kinshi no basho ni hairu no wa jiko sekinin da.","german":"Entering a prohibited area is at your own risk.","furigana":"たちいりきんし|ばしょ|はい|じこせきにん"},{"japanese":"何でも自己責任と言って片付けるのはよくない。","romaji":"Nan demo jiko sekinin to itte katazukeru no wa yoku nai.","german":"It is not good to dismiss everything by saying it's your own responsibility.","furigana":"なん|じこせきにん|い|かたづ"}],"notes":"自己 means \"oneself\" and 責任 means \"responsibility\": you bear the consequences of your actions yourself. Typical in warning notices and terms and conditions: 自己責任で〜してください \"at your own responsibility\". In debates 自己責任論 refers to the attitude of blaming victims themselves, and then sounds critical. Neutral to formal.","levelNote":"Compound: 自己 (N3) + 責任 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"皆さんの応援に感謝感激です。","romaji":"Minasan no ōen ni kansha kangeki desu.","german":"I am deeply grateful and moved by everyone's support.","furigana":"みな|おうえん|かんしゃかんげき"},{"japanese":"こんなに素敵なプレゼントをもらえるなんて、感謝感激です！","romaji":"Konna ni suteki na purezento o moraeru nante, kansha kangeki desu!","german":"To receive such a lovely present - I'm so grateful and touched!","furigana":"すてき|かんしゃかんげき"},{"japanese":"引っ越しを手伝ってくれて、感謝感激雨あられだよ。","romaji":"Hikkoshi o tetsudatte kurete, kansha kangeki ame arare da yo.","german":"Thank you for helping me move - I can't thank you enough!","furigana":"ひ|こ|てつだ|かんしゃかんげきあめ"}],"notes":"感謝 \"thanks\" and 感激 \"deep emotion\": one is overjoyed, thankful and moved. Typical as the exclamation 感謝感激です！ or with the occasion marked by に: 応援に感謝感激です. Jokingly lengthened, one says 感謝感激雨あられ (\"thanks and emotion like rain and hail\"). Casual and emphatic; in formal letters one prefers 心より感謝申し上げます.","levelNote":"Compound: 感謝 (N3) + 感激 (N2)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"明日の試合は真剣勝負だ。","romaji":"Ashita no shiai wa shinken shōbu da.","german":"Tomorrow's match is a serious contest, with everything at stake.","furigana":"あした|しあい|しんけんしょうぶ"},{"japanese":"昔の侍は真剣勝負で命をかけて戦った。","romaji":"Mukashi no samurai wa shinken shōbu de inochi o kakete tatakatta.","german":"The samurai of old fought with real swords, staking their lives.","furigana":"むかし|さむらい|しんけんしょうぶ|いのち|たたか"},{"japanese":"面接は真剣勝負だと思って、しっかり準備した。","romaji":"Mensetsu wa shinken shōbu da to omotte, shikkari junbi shita.","german":"I treated the job interview as the real thing and prepared thoroughly.","furigana":"めんせつ|しんけんしょうぶ|おも|じゅんび"}],"notes":"真剣 here means \"real sword\" (as opposed to a wooden sword) and 勝負 means \"fight, contest\": originally a fight with sharp blades to the death. Figuratively it means a confrontation in which everything is seriously at stake, with no playing around: 真剣勝負だ, 真剣勝負をする, 真剣勝負の場. Neutral; common in sports, exams or negotiations.","levelNote":"Compound: 真剣 (N3) + 勝負 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"全身全霊を込めて演奏した。","romaji":"Zenshin zenrei o komete ensō shita.","german":"I played with body and soul.","furigana":"ぜんしんぜんれい|こ|えんそう"},{"japanese":"新しい社長は「全身全霊で会社のために働きます」と言った。","romaji":"Atarashii shachō wa \"zenshin zenrei de kaisha no tame ni hatarakimasu\" to itta.","german":"The new company president said, \"I will work for the company with all my strength.\"","furigana":"あたら|しゃちょう|ぜんしんぜんれい|かいしゃ|はたら|い"},{"japanese":"母は全身全霊をかけて私たちを育ててくれた。","romaji":"Haha wa zenshin zenrei o kakete watashitachi o sodatete kureta.","german":"My mother raised us with all her devotion.","furigana":"はは|ぜんしんぜんれい|わたし|そだ"}],"notes":"全身 means \"the whole body\" and 全霊 means \"the whole soul\": with body and soul, with everything one has. Fixed patterns are 全身全霊を込める／かける／傾ける and 全身全霊で〜する. Solemn and emphatic, typical for pledges, politicians' inaugural speeches and interviews with athletes or artists.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"合格の知らせに有頂天になった。","romaji":"Gōkaku no shirase ni uchōten ni natta.","german":"I was ecstatic at the news that I had passed.","furigana":"ごうかく|し|うちょうてん"},{"japanese":"初めて彼女ができて、弟は有頂天になっている。","romaji":"Hajimete kanojo ga dekite, otōto wa uchōten ni natte iru.","german":"My younger brother got his first girlfriend and is on cloud nine.","furigana":"はじ|かのじょ|おとうと|うちょうてん"},{"japanese":"ほめられて有頂天になっていたら、次のテストで大失敗した。","romaji":"Homerarete uchōten ni natte itara, tsugi no tesuto de daishippai shita.","german":"I was on cloud nine after being praised, then failed miserably on the next test.","furigana":"うちょうてん|つぎ|だいしっぱい"}],"notes":"有頂天 denotes the highest heaven in Buddhism; in everyday use it means to be beside oneself with joy. Typical are 有頂天になる and 〜に有頂天になる; often it carries the implication that someone becomes careless or arrogant out of sheer joy: 有頂天になって失敗する. More neutral expressions for great joy are 大喜び or, colloquially, 舞い上がる.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"四方八方に探したが、見つからなかった。","romaji":"Shihō happō ni sagashita ga, mitsukaranakatta.","german":"I searched in all directions, but couldn't find it.","furigana":"しほうはっぽう|さが|み"},{"japanese":"ハトがびっくりして、四方八方に飛んでいった。","romaji":"Hato ga bikkuri shite, shihō happō ni tonde itta.","german":"The pigeons were startled and flew off in all directions.","furigana":"しほうはっぽう|と"},{"japanese":"記者会見で、社長は四方八方から質問を受けた。","romaji":"Kisha kaiken de, shachō wa shihō happō kara shitsumon o uketa.","german":"At the press conference, the company president was bombarded with questions from all sides.","furigana":"きしゃかいけん|しゃちょう|しほうはっぽう|しつもん|う"}],"notes":"四方 means \"the four directions\" and 八方 means \"the eight directions\"; they reinforce each other to mean \"in all directions, from all sides\". Typical with に or から: 四方八方に散らばる／探す, 四方八方から集まる. Neutral and vivid; similar to but stronger than あちこち. Related is 八方ふさがり \"blocked in every direction, with no way out\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二人は相思相愛のカップルだ。","romaji":"Futari wa sōshi sōai no kappuru da.","german":"The two are a couple who love each other.","furigana":"ふたり|そうしそうあい"},{"japanese":"相思相愛で結婚した二人は、今もとても仲がいい。","romaji":"Sōshi sōai de kekkon shita futari wa, ima mo totemo naka ga ii.","german":"The two married out of mutual love and still get along very well.","furigana":"そうしそうあい|けっこん|ふたり|いま|なか"},{"japanese":"その選手とチームは相思相愛で、すぐに契約が決まった。","romaji":"Sono senshu to chīmu wa sōshi sōai de, sugu ni keiyaku ga kimatta.","german":"The player and the team really wanted each other, so the contract was quickly settled.","furigana":"せんしゅ|そうしそうあい|けいやく|き"}],"notes":"相思 means \"to be fond of each other\" and 相愛 means \"to love each other\": two people love one another. Patterns: 相思相愛の二人／カップル, 〜と相思相愛だ. Figuratively it is often used for mutual interest, for example between a company and an applicant or a club and a player. Neutral, slightly humorous in the figurative sense.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"来年は海外旅行に行きたい。","romaji":"Rainen wa kaigai ryokō ni ikitai.","german":"Next year I want to take a trip abroad.","furigana":"らいねん|かいがいりょこう|い"},{"japanese":"初めての海外旅行のためにパスポートを作った。","romaji":"Hajimete no kaigai ryokō no tame ni pasupōto o tsukutta.","german":"I got a passport for my first trip abroad.","furigana":"はじ|かいがいりょこう|つく"},{"japanese":"海外旅行中に財布をなくして大変だった。","romaji":"Kaigai ryokō-chū ni saifu o nakushite taihen datta.","german":"I lost my wallet during my trip abroad, and it was a real mess.","furigana":"かいがいりょこうちゅう|さいふ|たいへん"}],"notes":"海外旅行 combines 海外 \"overseas, abroad\" (literally \"beyond the sea\") with 旅行 \"trip\". Since Japan is an island nation, every foreign country is \"beyond the sea\"; 海外旅行 is therefore the usual expression, while 外国旅行 sounds noticeably rarer. Typical combinations are 海外旅行に行く, 海外旅行をする and 海外旅行中に \"during the trip abroad\"; the counterpart is 国内旅行 \"domestic trip\". For a study stay abroad, on the other hand, one says 留学.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 23"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"経験豊富なガイドが案内してくれる。","romaji":"Keiken hōfu na gaido ga annai shite kureru.","german":"An experienced guide will show us the way.","furigana":"けいけんほうふ|あんない"},{"japanese":"困ったときは、経験豊富な先輩に相談するといい。","romaji":"Komatta toki wa, keiken hōfu na senpai ni sōdan suru to ii.","german":"When you're in trouble, it's good to ask an experienced senior colleague for advice.","furigana":"こま|けいけんほうふ|せんぱい|そうだん"},{"japanese":"この会社では経験豊富なエンジニアを募集しています。","romaji":"Kono kaisha de wa keiken hōfu na enjinia o boshū shite imasu.","german":"This company is looking for experienced engineers.","furigana":"かいしゃ|けいけんほうふ|ぼしゅう"}],"notes":"経験豊富 consists of 経験 \"experience\" and 豊富 \"abundant, rich in\" and means \"experienced, with a lot of experience\". It is used like a な-adjective, mostly attributively before people: 経験豊富な先生, 経験豊富なスタッフ; predicatively it is 彼は経験豊富だ. In an ordinary sentence the split form 経験が豊富だ is also very common. The opposite is 経験不足 or 経験が浅い. The expression is neutral to slightly formal and typical for job ads, introductions and advertising.","levelNote":"Compound: 経験 (N4) + 豊富 (N3)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一念発起してダイエットを始めた。","romaji":"Ichinen hokki shite daietto o hajimeta.","german":"I made a firm resolution and started a diet.","furigana":"いちねんほっき|はじ"},{"japanese":"父は四十歳で一念発起して、大学で勉強を始めた。","romaji":"Chichi wa yonjussai de ichinen hokki shite, daigaku de benkyō o hajimeta.","german":"At forty, my father made up his mind and started studying at university.","furigana":"ちち|よんじゅっさい|いちねんほっき|だいがく|べんきょう|はじ"},{"japanese":"部屋が汚すぎるので、一念発起して大掃除をした。","romaji":"Heya ga kitanasugiru node, ichinen hokki shite ōsōji o shita.","german":"My room was so messy that I finally pulled myself together and did a thorough cleaning.","furigana":"へや|きたな|いちねんほっき|おおそうじ"}],"notes":"一念発起 comes from Buddhism: 一念 means \"a single (firm) thought\" and 発起 means \"to bring forth, to make a resolution\" - originally the resolution to take the path to enlightenment. Today it means making a firm resolution, after long hesitation, to start something new or change one's life. It almost always appears as a する-verb in the form 一念発起して〜を始める/〜する, e.g. for a diet, studies, or quitting smoking. It sounds somewhat elevated, but is widespread in everyday conversation and personal accounts; a simpler word would be 決心する.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の説明はいつも単純明快だ。","romaji":"Kare no setsumei wa itsumo tanjun meikai da.","german":"His explanations are always simple and clear.","furigana":"かれ|せつめい|たんじゅんめいかい"},{"japanese":"このゲームのルールは単純明快で、子供でもすぐ分かる。","romaji":"Kono gēmu no rūru wa tanjun meikai de, kodomo demo sugu wakaru.","german":"The rules of this game are so simple and clear that even children understand them right away.","furigana":"たんじゅんめいかい|こども|わ"},{"japanese":"答えは単純明快だ。毎日練習するしかない。","romaji":"Kotae wa tanjun meikai da. Mainichi renshū suru shika nai.","german":"The answer is simple and clear: you just have to practice every day.","furigana":"こた|たんじゅんめいかい|まいにちれんしゅう"}],"notes":"単純明快 combines 単純 \"simple, plain\" with 明快 \"clear, unambiguous\" and means \"simple and clear, easy to understand\". Unlike 単純 alone, which can also mean \"simplistic, naive\", 単純明快 is almost always positive: 単純明快な説明, 単純明快なルール, 答えは単純明快だ. It is used like a な-adjective; when followed by another clause it takes the form 単純明快で. Neutral to slightly literary; in casual conversation one also simply says 分かりやすい.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"祖母の料理は天下一品だ。","romaji":"Sobo no ryōri wa tenka ippin da.","german":"My grandmother's cooking is unrivaled.","furigana":"そぼ|りょうり|てんかいっぴん"},{"japanese":"彼の手品の腕は天下一品だ。","romaji":"Kare no tejina no ude wa tenka ippin da.","german":"His skill at magic tricks is second to none.","furigana":"かれ|てじな|うで|てんかいっぴん"},{"japanese":"この旅館の温泉は天下一品だと評判です。","romaji":"Kono ryokan no onsen wa tenka ippin da to hyōban desu.","german":"The hot spring of this inn is said to be one of a kind.","furigana":"りょかん|おんせん|てんかいっぴん|ひょうばん"}],"notes":"天下一品 consists of 天下 \"all under heaven, the whole world\" and 一品 \"a unique piece, a masterpiece\" and means \"unsurpassed, uniquely good\". It is strong praise, often somewhat jokingly exaggerated, especially for food and skill: 〜の味は天下一品だ, 天下一品の腕前. It is mostly used predicatively with だ or attributively with の. In normal conversation it sounds somewhat old-fashioned and flowery; more neutral would be 最高 or 抜群. The expression is also known as the name of a ramen chain.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"投手が完全試合を達成した。","romaji":"Tōshu ga kanzen shiai o tassei shita.","german":"The pitcher achieved a perfect game.","furigana":"とうしゅ|かんぜんしあい|たっせい"},{"japanese":"九回まで完全試合だったが、最後の打者にヒットを打たれた。","romaji":"Kyūkai made kanzen shiai datta ga, saigo no dasha ni hitto o utareta.","german":"It was a perfect game through the ninth inning, but the last batter got a hit.","furigana":"きゅうかい|かんぜんしあい|さいご|だしゃ|う"},{"japanese":"完全試合は何十年に一度しか見られない記録だ。","romaji":"Kanzen shiai wa nan-jūnen ni ichido shika mirarenai kiroku da.","german":"A perfect game is a record you only get to see once every few decades.","furigana":"かんぜんしあい|なんじゅうねん|いちど|み|きろく"}],"notes":"完全試合, from 完全 \"complete, perfect\" and 試合 \"game, match\", is a technical term from baseball: the perfect game, in which the pitcher lets no opposing player reach base over the whole game. Typical combinations are 完全試合を達成する and 完全試合を逃す \"to narrowly miss a perfect game\". It should be distinguished from ノーヒットノーラン, in which no hit, but for example a walk, is allowed. Outside sports 完全試合 is hardly used figuratively; for a \"flawless performance\" one rather says 完璧.","levelNote":"Compound: 完全 (N3) + 試合 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"留学は異文化交流の良い機会だ。","romaji":"Ryūgaku wa ibunka kōryū no yoi kikai da.","german":"Studying abroad is a good opportunity for cross-cultural exchange.","furigana":"りゅうがく|いぶんかこうりゅう|よ|きかい"},{"japanese":"市役所で外国人住民との異文化交流イベントが開かれた。","romaji":"Shiyakusho de gaikokujin jūmin to no ibunka kōryū ibento ga hirakareta.","german":"A cross-cultural exchange event with foreign residents was held at city hall.","furigana":"しやくしょ|がいこくじんじゅうみん|いぶんかこうりゅう|ひら"},{"japanese":"料理は異文化交流のきっかけになりやすい。","romaji":"Ryōri wa ibunka kōryū no kikkake ni nariyasui.","german":"Food easily becomes an occasion for cross-cultural exchange.","furigana":"りょうり|いぶんかこうりゅう"}],"notes":"異文化交流 is composed of 異文化 \"foreign culture\" (異 \"different\" + 文化) and 交流 \"exchange\": \"intercultural exchange\". It is not a classic four-character idiom but a modern compound that is common in schools, communities and universities: 異文化交流イベント, 異文化交流会, 異文化交流を深める. Similar is 国際交流, which stresses exchange between countries more.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"「春宵一刻値千金」という詩の一節が、この言葉の由来だ。","romaji":"\"Shunshō ikkoku atai senkin\" to iu shi no issetsu ga, kono kotoba no yurai da.","german":"The line of poetry \"A moment of a spring evening is worth a thousand gold pieces\" is the origin of this phrase.","furigana":"しゅんしょういっこくあたいせんきん|し|いっせつ|ことば|ゆらい"},{"japanese":"試験直前の受験生にとって、毎日が一刻千金だ。","romaji":"Shiken chokuzen no jukensei ni totte, mainichi ga ikkoku senkin da.","german":"For examinees just before the exam, every day is precious.","furigana":"しけんちょくぜん|じゅけんせい|まいにち|いっこくせんきん"},{"japanese":"一刻千金の休日を、寝て過ごすなんてもったいない。","romaji":"Ikkoku senkin no kyūjitsu o, nete sugosu nante mottainai.","german":"It would be a waste to spend a precious day off sleeping.","furigana":"いっこくせんきん|きゅうじつ|ね|す"}],"notes":"一刻千金 means \"one moment (一刻) is worth a thousand pieces of gold (千金)\": precious time that should not be wasted. It comes from the poem of Su Shi 「春宵一刻値千金」, which praises a beautiful spring evening. Today it mostly stands predicatively (今は一刻千金だ) or with の before time words (一刻千金の時間). It is elevated and literary; in everyday speech one more often says 時は金なり or 時間がもったいない.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の言外之意を汲み取る必要がある。","romaji":"Kare no gengai no i o kumitoru hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"You need to pick up on what he is implying.","furigana":"かれ|げんがいのい|く|と|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"「考えておきます」という返事には、断るという言外之意がある。","romaji":"\"Kangaete okimasu\" to iu henji ni wa, kotowaru to iu gengai no i ga aru.","german":"The reply \"I'll think about it\" carries the unspoken meaning that it's a refusal.","furigana":"かんが|へんじ|ことわ|げんがいのい"},{"japanese":"部長のメールの言外之意に気づかず、同じミスを繰り返してしまった。","romaji":"Buchō no mēru no gengai no i ni kizukazu, onaji misu o kurikaeshite shimatta.","german":"I didn't notice the implied meaning in my boss's email and repeated the same mistake.","furigana":"ぶちょう|げんがいのい|き|おな|く|かえ"}],"notes":"言外之意 means \"the meaning (意) outside the words (言外)\": what someone means but does not say explicitly. 之 here is the classical Chinese equivalent of の; in modern Japanese one almost always writes 言外の意味 or 言外の意, and the four-character form is found rather in idiom collections. Typical are verbs such as 汲み取る \"to read into\", 読み取る or 察する. In content it is close to 行間を読む \"to read between the lines\", which plays a large role in Japanese communication.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"孤立無援の状態で戦い続けた。","romaji":"Koritsu muen no jōtai de tatakai tsuzuketa.","german":"He kept fighting while isolated and without help.","furigana":"こりつむえん|じょうたい|たたか|つづ"},{"japanese":"不正を告発した彼は、社内で孤立無援になってしまった。","romaji":"Fusei o kokuhatsu shita kare wa, shanai de koritsu muen ni natte shimatta.","german":"After exposing the wrongdoing, he found himself utterly isolated and unsupported in the company.","furigana":"ふせい|こくはつ|かれ|しゃない|こりつむえん"},{"japanese":"知り合いのいない外国で病気になり、孤立無援の不安を感じた。","romaji":"Shiriai no inai gaikoku de byōki ni nari, koritsu muen no fuan o kanjita.","german":"When I fell ill in a foreign country where I knew no one, I felt the anxiety of being alone and without help.","furigana":"し|あ|がいこく|びょうき|こりつむえん|ふあん|かん"}],"notes":"孤立無援 combines 孤立 \"isolated, cut off\" and 無援 \"without support\": to stand completely alone with no one helping. Typical are 孤立無援の状態／戦い and 孤立無援になる. Originally it describes besieged troops, today also people who have no backing at work, in politics or in everyday life. The expression is formal and more dramatic than 一人ぼっち \"all alone, lonely\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女はついに才能が開花して、世界的に認められた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa tsui ni sainō ga kaika shite, sekaiteki ni mitomerareta.","german":"She finally came into her talent and gained worldwide recognition.","furigana":"かのじょ|さいのう|かいか|せかいてき|みと"},{"japanese":"新聞に「十八歳、才能開花」という見出しが出た。","romaji":"Shinbun ni \"Jūhassai, sainō kaika\" to iu midashi ga deta.","german":"A newspaper headline read: \"Eighteen years old, talent in bloom.\"","furigana":"しんぶん|じゅうはっさい|さいのうかいか|みだ|で"},{"japanese":"良い指導者に出会って、彼は音楽の才能を開花させた。","romaji":"Yoi shidōsha ni deatte, kare wa ongaku no sainō o kaika saseta.","german":"After meeting a good mentor, he brought his musical talent to full bloom.","furigana":"よ|しどうしゃ|であ|かれ|おんがく|さいのう|かいか"}],"notes":"才能開花 is a shortened, headline-style form of 才能が開花する \"one's talent blossoms, develops\"; 開花 literally means \"flowers coming into bloom\". In ordinary sentences you therefore say 才能が開花する or 才能を開花させる (transitive: \"to bring one's talent to full bloom\"). The four-character form is seen mainly in headlines, advertising and sports reports. It is not a classical four-character idiom but a modern compound.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"武道では心身一如が大切だ。","romaji":"Budō de wa shinshin ichinyo ga taisetsu da.","german":"In the martial arts, the unity of body and mind is important.","furigana":"ぶどう|しんしんいちにょ|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"心身一如の考え方では、心の疲れは体にも表れるとされる。","romaji":"Shinshin ichinyo no kangaekata de wa, kokoro no tsukare wa karada ni mo arawareru to sareru.","german":"According to the idea of the unity of body and mind, mental fatigue also shows up in the body.","furigana":"しんしんいちにょ|かんが|かた|こころ|つか|からだ|あらわ"},{"japanese":"座禅を続けるうちに、心身一如の境地に近づいた気がする。","romaji":"Zazen o tsuzukeru uchi ni, shinshin ichinyo no kyōchi ni chikazuita ki ga suru.","german":"As I keep up my zazen, I feel I am getting closer to a state of unity of body and mind.","furigana":"ざぜん|つづ|しんしんいちにょ|きょうち|ちか|き"}],"notes":"心身一如 means \"body (身) and mind (心) are one (一如)\"; 一如 is a Buddhist term for \"unity, non-duality\". The expression comes from Zen Buddhism and is used today in martial arts, yoga, medicine and health writing: mental and physical condition influence each other. Typical are 心身一如の境地 and 心身一如の考え方. It is elevated and technical; in everyday speech one says 心と体はつながっている.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は徹頭徹尾、自分の信念を貫いた。","romaji":"Kare wa tettō tetsubi, jibun no shinnen o tsuranuita.","german":"From beginning to end, he held fast to his convictions.","furigana":"かれ|てっとうてつび|じぶん|しんねん|つらぬ"},{"japanese":"私はその計画には徹頭徹尾反対だ。","romaji":"Watashi wa sono keikaku ni wa tettō tetsubi hantai da.","german":"I am thoroughly opposed to that plan.","furigana":"わたし|けいかく|てっとうてつびはんたい"},{"japanese":"この小説は徹頭徹尾、犯人の視点で書かれている。","romaji":"Kono shōsetsu wa tettō tetsubi, hannin no shiten de kakarete iru.","german":"This novel is written entirely from the culprit's point of view.","furigana":"しょうせつ|てっとうてつび|はんにん|してん|か"}],"notes":"徹頭徹尾 means \"to go through the head (頭) and go through the tail (尾)\": from beginning to end, completely, without deviation. It is mainly an adverb and stands before verbs or judgments: 徹頭徹尾反対する, 徹頭徹尾貫く. It often stresses an uncompromising attitude or that something is consistent throughout. It is elevated; more everyday are 最初から最後まで and 一貫して.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は南船北馬の生活を送っている。","romaji":"Kare wa nansen hokuba no seikatsu o okutte iru.","german":"He leads a life of constant travel.","furigana":"かれ|なんせんほくば|せいかつ|おく"},{"japanese":"若い頃は営業で、南船北馬の日々を過ごした。","romaji":"Wakai koro wa eigyō de, nansen hokuba no hibi o sugoshita.","german":"As a young salesman, I spent my days traveling all over the country.","furigana":"わか|ころ|えいぎょう|なんせんほくば|ひび|す"},{"japanese":"講演の依頼が多く、先生は南船北馬で全国を回っている。","romaji":"Kōen no irai ga ōku, sensei wa nansen hokuba de zenkoku o mawatte iru.","german":"The professor receives so many lecture requests that he travels restlessly around the whole country.","furigana":"こうえん|いらい|おお|せんせい|なんせんほくば|ぜんこく|まわ"}],"notes":"南船北馬 literally means \"by ship in the south, on horseback in the north\": in ancient China people traveled by boat in the water-rich south and on horseback in the north. From this came the meaning \"to be constantly on the road, to travel restlessly about\". It is usually used with の (南船北馬の日々／生活) and describes travel required by work, for example by sales representatives, politicians or lecturers. The expression is literary and rare; in everyday speech one says 全国を飛び回る or 出張続き.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"無知蒙昧な偏見にとらわれてはいけない。","romaji":"Muchi mōmai na henken ni torawarete wa ikenai.","german":"We must not be taken in by ignorant prejudice.","furigana":"むちもうまい|へんけん"},{"japanese":"昔の人々を無知蒙昧だと決めつけるのは間違いだ。","romaji":"Mukashi no hitobito o muchi mōmai da to kimetsukeru no wa machigai da.","german":"It is wrong to write off people of the past as ignorant and unenlightened.","furigana":"むかし|ひとびと|むちもうまい|き|まちが"},{"japanese":"専門家の話を聞いて、自分の無知蒙昧を恥じた。","romaji":"Senmonka no hanashi o kiite, jibun no muchi mōmai o hajita.","german":"Listening to the expert, I felt ashamed of my own ignorance.","furigana":"せんもんか|はなし|き|じぶん|むちもうまい|は"}],"notes":"無知蒙昧 combines 無知 \"ignorance\" and 蒙昧 \"intellectual darkness, lack of enlightenment\": utterly ignorant and unenlightened. It is a な-adjective or noun (無知蒙昧な人々, 自分の無知蒙昧を恥じる). The expression is very derogatory and condescending, so it is rude when applied to others; applied modestly to oneself it is more acceptable. It is a written-language word; normally one simply says 無知 or 物を知らない.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は面目躍如の活躍を見せた。","romaji":"Kare wa menmoku yakujo no katsuyaku o miseta.","german":"He gave a performance that lived up to his reputation.","furigana":"かれ|めんもくやくじょ|かつやく|み"},{"japanese":"決勝点を決めたのはベテラン選手で、まさに面目躍如だった。","romaji":"Kesshōten o kimeta no wa beteran senshu de, masa ni menmoku yakujo datta.","german":"It was the veteran who scored the winning goal, truly living up to his name.","furigana":"けっしょうてん|き|せんしゅ|めんもくやくじょ"},{"japanese":"老舗旅館の面目躍如たるもてなしに、客は皆感動した。","romaji":"Shinise ryokan no menmoku yakujo taru motenashi ni, kyaku wa mina kandō shita.","german":"All the guests were moved by the hospitality of the long-established ryokan, which did full honor to its reputation.","furigana":"しにせりょかん|めんもくやくじょ|きゃく|みなかんどう"}],"notes":"面目躍如 literally means \"the face (面目, honor) leaps to life (躍如)\": someone impressively shows what he is made of and fully lives up to his reputation. Typical forms are 面目躍如たるものがある, 面目躍如の活躍 and 〜の面目躍如だ, often used of veterans, experts or long-established companies. It is positive and elevated, frequent in sports and culture reports. Do not confuse it with 面目丸つぶれ \"to lose face completely\"; the reading めんぼくやくじょ is also correct.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二人はまさに落花流水の仲だ。","romaji":"Futari wa masa ni rakka ryūsui no naka da.","german":"The two of them are truly drawn to each other, like blossom and water.","furigana":"ふたり|らっかりゅうすい|なか"},{"japanese":"小説には、落花流水の情で結ばれた二人の恋が描かれている。","romaji":"Shōsetsu ni wa, rakka ryūsui no jō de musubareta futari no koi ga egakarete iru.","german":"The novel depicts the love of two people bound by mutual affection.","furigana":"しょうせつ|らっかりゅうすい|じょう|むす|ふたり|こい|えが"},{"japanese":"川面を流れる桜の花びらを見て、落花流水という言葉を思い出した。","romaji":"Kawamo o nagareru sakura no hanabira o mite, rakka ryūsui to iu kotoba o omoidashita.","german":"Seeing cherry petals drifting on the river, I remembered the expression 落花流水 (\"falling blossoms, flowing water\").","furigana":"|なが|さくら|はな|み|らっかりゅうすい|ことば|おも|だ"}],"notes":"落花流水 means \"falling blossoms and flowing water\": the blossom wants to drift with the water, and the water wants to carry the blossom, an image of mutual affection between a man and a woman. Originally it also describes the scenery of late spring and, in the sense of 落花流水の如し, can stand for decline or dispersal. It is found with の: 落花流水の情, 落花流水の仲. The expression is literary and rare in everyday speech.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"臥薪嘗胆の思いでリベンジに挑む。","romaji":"Gashin shōtan no omoi de ribenji ni idomu.","german":"With a spirit of enduring hardship, he takes on the rematch.","furigana":"がしんしょうたん|おも|いど"},{"japanese":"去年の決勝で負けてから、臥薪嘗胆の一年を過ごしてきた。","romaji":"Kyonen no kesshō de makete kara, gashin shōtan no ichinen o sugoshite kita.","german":"Since losing last year's final, I have spent a year of hardship to make it this time.","furigana":"きょねん|けっしょう|ま|がしんしょうたん|いちねん|す"},{"japanese":"三度目の受験で合格できたのは、臥薪嘗胆して努力した結果だ。","romaji":"Sandome no juken de gōkaku dekita no wa, gashin shōtan shite doryoku shita kekka da.","german":"Passing the entrance exam on my third attempt was the result of working hard through hardship.","furigana":"さんどめ|じゅけん|ごうかく|がしんしょうたん|どりょく|けっか"}],"notes":"臥薪嘗胆 literally means \"to sleep on brushwood (臥薪) and taste gall (嘗胆)\": according to a Chinese legend, two kings toughened themselves this way so as not to forget the shame of a defeat and to take revenge. Today it means to take on great hardship to reach a goal or to make up for a defeat. Typical are 臥薪嘗胆の日々／思い and 臥薪嘗胆して. It is elevated and sounds heroic, often used in sports and success stories.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は一騎当千のプログラマーだ。","romaji":"Kanojo wa ikki tōsen no puroguramā da.","german":"She is an outstanding programmer, worth a thousand others.","furigana":"かのじょ|いっきとうせん"},{"japanese":"このチームは一騎当千の選手ばかりだ。","romaji":"Kono chīmu wa ikki tōsen no senshu bakari da.","german":"This team consists only of players who are each worth a thousand.","furigana":"いっきとうせん|せんしゅ"},{"japanese":"彼は戦国時代、一騎当千の武将として知られていた。","romaji":"Kare wa Sengoku jidai, ikki tōsen no bushō to shite shirarete ita.","german":"In the Warring States period, he was known as a commander who could take on thousands single-handedly.","furigana":"かれ|せんごくじだい|いっきとうせん|ぶしょう|し"}],"notes":"一騎当千 means \"one rider (一騎) is a match for a thousand (千)\": a fighter who alone is as strong as a thousand opponents. Originally applied to warriors, it is now used figuratively for exceptionally capable people in sports, work or games. It almost always stands before a noun with の: 一騎当千の武将, 一騎当千の強者（つわもの）. The expression is elevated and appreciative, and is also used jokingly as an exaggeration.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の大胆不敵な行動に周囲は驚いた。","romaji":"Kare no daitan futeki na kōdō ni shūi wa odoroita.","german":"Everyone around him was surprised at his bold and fearless behavior.","furigana":"かれ|だいたんふてき|こうどう|しゅうい|おどろ"},{"japanese":"昼間に堂々と銀行を襲うとは、大胆不敵な犯人だ。","romaji":"Hiruma ni dōdō to ginkō o osou to wa, daitan futeki na hannin da.","german":"Attacking a bank openly in broad daylight, what an audacious criminal.","furigana":"ひるま|どうどう|ぎんこう|おそ|だいたんふてき|はんにん"},{"japanese":"新人の彼は大胆不敵にも、会議で社長の意見に反論した。","romaji":"Shinjin no kare wa daitan futeki ni mo, kaigi de shachō no iken ni hanron shita.","german":"Newcomer that he is, he audaciously argued against the president's opinion in the meeting.","furigana":"しんじん|かれ|だいたんふてき|かいぎ|しゃちょう|いけん|はんろん"}],"notes":"大胆不敵 combines 大胆 \"bold, daring\" and 不敵 \"fearless, without respect for the opponent\": so bold that one shrinks from nothing. It is a な-adjective (大胆不敵な行動, 大胆不敵な笑み) and also appears as 大胆不敵にも \"brazenly\". The tone ranges between admiration and criticism; in the news it often describes brazen crimes (大胆不敵な犯行). It is stronger than 大胆 alone; 無謀, by contrast, stresses recklessness.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"不撓不屈の精神で困難を乗り越えた。","romaji":"Futō fukutsu no seishin de konnan o norikoeta.","german":"With an indomitable spirit, he overcame the difficulties.","furigana":"ふとうふくつ|せいしん|こんなん|の|こ"},{"japanese":"彼は何度失敗してもあきらめない、不撓不屈の人だ。","romaji":"Kare wa nando shippai shite mo akiramenai, futō fukutsu no hito da.","german":"He is an indomitable person who never gives up, no matter how many times he fails.","furigana":"かれ|なんどしっぱい|ふとうふくつ|ひと"},{"japanese":"昇進の挨拶で、「不撓不屈の精神で努力します」と述べた。","romaji":"Shōshin no aisatsu de, \"Futō fukutsu no seishin de doryoku shimasu\" to nobeta.","german":"In his promotion greeting, he said, \"I will strive with an indomitable spirit.\"","furigana":"しょうしん|あいさつ|ふとうふくつ|せいしん|どりょく|の"}],"notes":"不撓不屈 means \"not to bend (不撓) and not to yield (不屈)\": unbending, not to be broken by any difficulty. It almost always appears in the fixed combination 不撓不屈の精神 or as 不撓不屈の人／努力. In Japan it also became well known through sumo wrestlers who used it in their speech on promotion to yokozuna. It is very elevated and solemn; in everyday speech one says 何度でもあきらめない or 粘り強い.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"立身出世を夢見て上京した。","romaji":"Risshin shusse o yumemite jōkyō shita.","german":"He moved to Tokyo dreaming of getting ahead in the world.","furigana":"りっしんしゅっせ|ゆめみ|じょうきょう"},{"japanese":"貧しい農家の息子が立身出世して、大臣にまでなった。","romaji":"Mazushii nōka no musuko ga risshin shusse shite, daijin ni made natta.","german":"The son of a poor farming family rose in the world and even became a minister.","furigana":"まず|のうか|むすこ|りっしんしゅっせ|だいじん"},{"japanese":"立身出世よりも、家族との時間を大切にしたい。","romaji":"Risshin shusse yori mo, kazoku to no jikan o taisetsu ni shitai.","german":"I value time with my family more than career success.","furigana":"りっしんしゅっせ|かぞく|じかん|たいせつ"}],"notes":"立身出世 combines 立身 \"to establish oneself in life\" and 出世 \"to rise, make a career\": to move up socially and professionally and gain standing. Typical are 立身出世を夢見る／目指す and 立身出世する. The expression sounds somewhat old-fashioned and recalls the Meiji era, when advancement through education was an ideal. In everyday speech today one usually just says 出世する or キャリアアップ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自由と責任は表裏一体だ。","romaji":"Jiyū to sekinin wa hyōri ittai da.","german":"Freedom and responsibility are two sides of the same coin.","furigana":"じゆう|せきにん|ひょうりいったい"},{"japanese":"薬の効果と副作用は、表裏一体の関係にある。","romaji":"Kusuri no kōka to fukusayō wa, hyōri ittai no kankei ni aru.","german":"The effect and the side effects of a medicine are inseparably linked.","furigana":"くすり|こうか|ふくさよう|ひょうりいったい|かんけい"},{"japanese":"彼の長所である頑固さは、短所と表裏一体だ。","romaji":"Kare no chōsho de aru ganko-sa wa, tansho to hyōri ittai da.","german":"His stubbornness is a strength, but it is also the flip side of his weakness.","furigana":"かれ|ちょうしょ|がんこ|たんしょ|ひょうりいったい"}],"notes":"表裏一体 means \"front and back (表裏) form one body (一体)\": two seemingly opposite things are inseparably linked, like two sides of a coin. Typical are AとBは表裏一体だ and 表裏一体の関係にある. It often concerns abstract pairs such as freedom and responsibility, success and failure, effect and side effect. The expression is matter-of-fact and elevated, and frequent in commentaries and essays.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は温厚篤実な人柄で周囲に信頼されている。","romaji":"Kare wa onkō tokujitsu na hitogara de shūi ni shinrai sarete iru.","german":"He is trusted by those around him for his warm-hearted and sincere character.","furigana":"かれ|おんこうとくじつ|ひとがら|しゅうい|しんらい"},{"japanese":"推薦状には「温厚篤実な人物である」と書かれていた。","romaji":"Suisenjō ni wa \"Onkō tokujitsu na jinbutsu de aru\" to kakarete ita.","german":"The letter of recommendation said, \"He is a kind and conscientious person.\"","furigana":"すいせんじょう|おんこうとくじつ|じんぶつ|か"},{"japanese":"温厚篤実な祖父が怒ったところを、一度も見たことがない。","romaji":"Onkō tokujitsu na sofu ga okotta tokoro o, ichido mo mita koto ga nai.","german":"I have never once seen my gentle, upright grandfather angry.","furigana":"おんこうとくじつ|そふ|おこ|いちど|み"}],"notes":"温厚篤実 combines 温厚 \"gentle, friendly\" and 篤実 \"sincere and conscientious\": a calm, warm and dependable character. Typical is 温厚篤実な人柄／人物. The expression is very formal and appears mainly in letters of recommendation, obituaries, wedding speeches and personal descriptions. In conversation one is more likely to say 優しくて真面目な人.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女の当意即妙な答えに皆が笑った。","romaji":"Kanojo no tōi sokumyō na kotae ni mina ga waratta.","german":"Everyone laughed at her quick-witted answer.","furigana":"かのじょ|とういそくみょう|こた|みな|わら"},{"japanese":"司会者の当意即妙なトークで、会場が一気に盛り上がった。","romaji":"Shikaisha no tōi sokumyō na tōku de, kaijō ga ikki ni moriagatta.","german":"The host's quick-witted patter instantly livened up the hall.","furigana":"しかいしゃ|とういそくみょう|かいじょう|いっき|も|あ"},{"japanese":"面接で予想外の質問をされても、当意即妙に答えられる人は少ない。","romaji":"Mensetsu de yosōgai no shitsumon o sarete mo, tōi sokumyō ni kotaerareru hito wa sukunai.","german":"Few people can answer on the spot even when asked unexpected questions in an interview.","furigana":"めんせつ|よそうがい|しつもん|とういそくみょう|こた|ひと|すく"}],"notes":"当意即妙 means \"suited (当) to the occasion (意), and apt (妙) on the spot (即)\": quick-witted, responding with spontaneous wit in just the right way. It is a な-adjective: 当意即妙な答え／受け答え, 当意即妙に切り返す. It is used to praise hosts, speakers or conversation partners who answer surprising questions wittily. In everyday speech one says 頭の回転が速い or 機転が利く.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一罰百戒の意味を込めて厳しく処罰した。","romaji":"Ichibatsu hyakkai no imi o komete kibishiku shobatsu shita.","german":"He was severely punished as an example to others.","furigana":"いちばつひゃっかい|いみ|こ|きび|しょばつ"},{"japanese":"監督は一罰百戒として、遅刻した選手を試合に出さなかった。","romaji":"Kantoku wa ichibatsu hyakkai to shite, chikoku shita senshu o shiai ni dasanakatta.","german":"To make an example, the manager did not let the player who arrived late play in the match.","furigana":"かんとく|いちばつひゃっかい|ちこく|せんしゅ|しあい|だ"},{"japanese":"飲酒運転には、一罰百戒の厳しい処分が必要だという声もある。","romaji":"Inshu unten ni wa, ichibatsu hyakkai no kibishii shobun ga hitsuyō da to iu koe mo aru.","german":"Some say that drunk driving calls for harsh penalties as a deterrent.","furigana":"いんしゅうんてん|いちばつひゃっかい|きび|しょぶん|ひつよう|こえ"}],"notes":"一罰百戒 means \"one punishment (一罰), a hundred warnings (百戒)\": to punish one person severely so that everyone else is deterred, making an example of him. Typical are 一罰百戒の意味で／として and 一罰百戒の処分. It appears in news about disciplinary punishment, sports and company rules, and is formal. It is often used to justify a harsh penalty, and sometimes it is also critically questioned.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は外柔内剛な人だ。","romaji":"Kanojo wa gaijū naigō na hito da.","german":"She is gentle on the outside but strong-willed within.","furigana":"かのじょ|がいじゅうないごう|ひと"},{"japanese":"新しい部長は外柔内剛で、穏やかだが決して意見を曲げない。","romaji":"Atarashii buchō wa gaijū naigō de, odayaka da ga kesshite iken o magenai.","german":"The new department head is soft outside and firm inside: calm, but he never gives up his opinion.","furigana":"あたら|ぶちょう|がいじゅうないごう|おだ|けっ|いけん|ま"},{"japanese":"リーダーには外柔内剛の姿勢が求められる。","romaji":"Rīdā ni wa gaijū naigō no shisei ga motomerareru.","german":"A leader is expected to have an attitude that is gentle yet firm.","furigana":"がいじゅうないごう|しせい|もと"}],"notes":"外柔内剛 means \"soft outside (外柔), hard inside (内剛)\": someone appears gentle and friendly but has a firm will. It is mostly used of people, with の or な: 外柔内剛の人, 外柔内剛なタイプ. It is a compliment that combines kindness with inner strength. The opposite is 内柔外剛 \"hard outside, weak inside\"; the expression is elevated and rather literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この報告書は内容はいいが、結論が弱くて画竜点睛を欠いている。","romaji":"Kono hōkokusho wa naiyō wa ii ga, ketsuron ga yowakute garyō tensei o kaite iru.","german":"The report is good in content, but the conclusion is weak, so it lacks the finishing touch.","furigana":"ほうこくしょ|ないよう|けつろん|よわ|がりょうてんせい|か"},{"japanese":"最後にのせたゆずの皮が、この料理の画竜点睛になっている。","romaji":"Saigo ni noseta yuzu no kawa ga, kono ryōri no garyō tensei ni natte iru.","german":"The yuzu peel placed on top at the end is the finishing touch of this dish.","furigana":"さいご|かわ|りょうり|がりょうてんせい"},{"japanese":"準備が完璧でも、最後の挨拶で失敗したら画竜点睛を欠くことになる。","romaji":"Junbi ga kanpeki demo, saigo no aisatsu de shippai shitara garyō tensei o kaku koto ni naru.","german":"Even if the preparation is perfect, it will lack the finishing touch if the closing greeting goes wrong.","furigana":"じゅんび|かんぺき|さいご|あいさつ|しっぱい|がりょうてんせい|か"}],"notes":"画竜点睛 literally means \"to put the pupils into a painted dragon\": according to legend, a painted dragon came to life as soon as the painter painted in its eyes. It means the decisive final touch that makes a work come alive. By far the most common use is in the fixed phrase 画竜点睛を欠く \"the decisive final touch is missing\". It is an elevated, written-language expression; note that 睛 (pupil) is often wrongly written as 晴.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"プレゼンの後に質疑応答の時間がある。","romaji":"Purezen no ato ni shitsugi ōtō no jikan ga aru.","german":"There is a Q&A session after the presentation.","furigana":"あと|しつぎおうとう|じかん"},{"japanese":"それでは、質疑応答に移りたいと思います。","romaji":"Sore de wa, shitsugi ōtō ni utsuritai to omoimasu.","german":"Now, I would like to move on to the Q&A session.","furigana":"しつぎおうとう|うつ|おも"},{"japanese":"記者会見の質疑応答で、大臣は厳しい質問を受けた。","romaji":"Kisha kaiken no shitsugi ōtō de, daijin wa kibishii shitsumon o uketa.","german":"In the Q&A of the press conference, the minister was asked tough questions.","furigana":"きしゃかいけん|しつぎおうとう|だいじん|きび|しつもん|う"}],"notes":"質疑応答 is made up of 質疑 \"asking questions, inquiry\" and 応答 \"answer, reply\" and refers to the question round after a lecture, a presentation or at a press conference. Typical combinations are 質疑応答の時間, 質疑応答に移る \"to move on to the Q&A\" and 質疑応答を行う. The word is neutral to formal; in everyday speech one also casually says Ｑ＆Ａ or 質問コーナー.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この作品は粒粒辛苦の結晶だ。","romaji":"Kono sakuhin wa ryūryū shinku no kesshō da.","german":"This work is the fruit of painstaking labor.","furigana":"さくひん|りゅうりゅうしんく|けっしょう"},{"japanese":"彼は粒粒辛苦して貯めたお金で、自分の店を開いた。","romaji":"Kare wa ryūryū shinku shite tameta okane de, jibun no mise o hiraita.","german":"He opened his own shop with money he had painstakingly saved.","furigana":"かれ|りゅうりゅうしんく|た|かね|じぶん|みせ|ひら"},{"japanese":"農家の人の粒粒辛苦を思うと、ご飯を残すことはできない。","romaji":"Nōka no hito no ryūryū shinku o omou to, gohan o nokosu koto wa dekinai.","german":"When I think of the farmers' hard work, I cannot leave any rice uneaten.","furigana":"のうか|ひと|りゅうりゅうしんく|おも|はん|のこ"}],"notes":"粒粒辛苦 (usually written 粒々辛苦) literally means \"every grain is hardship\" and goes back to a Chinese poem according to which every grain of rice contains the hard work of the farmers. Figuratively it means patient, laborious effort over a long time. Typical are 粒粒辛苦の末 \"after years of effort\", 粒粒辛苦の結晶 \"the fruit of painstaking work\" and 粒粒辛苦して \"with great effort\". It is a written-language expression and rather rare; in conversation one says more simply 苦労して.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"天下泰平の世の中が続くことを願う。","romaji":"Tenka taihei no yononaka ga tsuzuku koto o negau.","german":"I hope the peaceful world continues.","furigana":"てんかたいへい|よ|なか|つづ|ねが"},{"japanese":"江戸時代には、約二百六十年も天下泰平の時代が続いた。","romaji":"Edo jidai ni wa, yaku nihyaku rokujū-nen mo tenka taihei no jidai ga tsuzuita.","german":"In the Edo period, an age of peace lasted about 260 years.","furigana":"えどじだい|やくにひゃくろくじゅうねん|てんかたいへい|じだい|つづ"},{"japanese":"試験の前日なのに寝てばかりいて、弟は天下泰平だね。","romaji":"Shiken no zenjitsu na no ni nete bakari ite, otōto wa tenka taihei da ne.","german":"It's the day before the exam and my little brother does nothing but sleep. He's carefree, isn't he?","furigana":"しけん|ぜんじつ|ね|おとうと|てんかたいへい"}],"notes":"天下泰平 (also 天下太平) literally means \"great peace under heaven\" and describes a time when calm and order prevail throughout the country, for example 天下泰平の世 or 天下泰平の時代. As a な-adjective it is also used slightly ironically for people who are carefree and unconcerned although there would be reason to worry: \"He's got a sunny disposition.\" In the historical sense it is rather elevated, in the ironic sense colloquial.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"連絡を取らないうちに友情が自然消滅した。","romaji":"Renraku o toranai uchi ni yūjō ga shizen shōmetsu shita.","german":"As we did not keep in contact, the friendship fizzled out.","furigana":"れんらく|と|ゆうじょう|しぜんしょうめつ"},{"japanese":"その計画は予算がつかず、いつの間にか自然消滅した。","romaji":"Sono keikaku wa yosan ga tsukazu, itsu no ma ni ka shizen shōmetsu shita.","german":"The project got no budget and fizzled out before long.","furigana":"けいかく|よさん|ま|しぜんしょうめつ"},{"japanese":"遠距離になってから会えなくなり、彼とは自然消滅してしまった。","romaji":"Enkyori ni natte kara aenaku nari, kare to wa shizen shōmetsu shite shimatta.","german":"Once we were in a long-distance relationship, we could no longer meet, and things between him and me just faded away.","furigana":"えんきょり|あ|かれ|しぜんしょうめつ"}],"notes":"自然消滅 means \"to disappear by itself\" and is used as an intransitive する-verb: plans, clubs, rules or relationships 自然消滅する when nobody takes care of them any more and they fade away without a formal end. It is particularly common for romantic relationships: 彼女とは自然消滅した \"We just stopped getting in touch, and it fizzled out\". Unlike 解散 or 中止, there is thus no deliberate decision. A neutral expression, common in daily life and in the news.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"やっと車の修理が終わったと思ったら、今度は財布をなくした。一難去ってまた一難だ。","romaji":"Yatto kuruma no shūri ga owatta to omottara, kondo wa saifu o nakushita. Ichinan satte mata ichinan da.","german":"Just when I thought the car repair was finally done, I lost my wallet. One misfortune after another.","furigana":"くるま|しゅうり|お|おも|こんど|さいふ|いちなんさ|いちなん"},{"japanese":"地震の後片付けがやっと終わったら、今度は台風が来た。まさに一難去ってまた一難だね。","romaji":"Jishin no atokatazuke ga yatto owattara, kondo wa taifū ga kita. Masa ni ichinan satte mata ichinan da ne.","german":"Just when the earthquake cleanup was finally over, a typhoon came. It really is one thing after another.","furigana":"じしん|あとかたづ|お|こんど|たいふう|き|いちなんさ|いちなん"},{"japanese":"バグを一つ直したら、別のバグが出てきた。一難去ってまた一難だ。","romaji":"Bagu o hitotsu naoshitara, betsu no bagu ga dete kita. Ichinan satte mata ichinan da.","german":"I fixed one bug and another one showed up. One problem after another.","furigana":"ひと|なお|べつ|で|いちなんさ|いちなん"}],"notes":"一難去ってまた一難 literally means \"When one misfortune (一難) has gone (去って), another (また) misfortune comes\". It means: as soon as one problem is solved, the next one appears, roughly \"out of the frying pan into the fire\". Unlike 泣きっ面に蜂 (\"it never rains but it pours\", misfortunes pile up), it stresses the sequence: first relief, then new worry. It usually stands as a comment at the end of a sentence: …。一難去ってまた一難だ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の傲岸不遜な態度は周囲の反感を買った。","romaji":"Kare no gōgan fuson na taido wa shūi no hankan o katta.","german":"His arrogant and insolent attitude drew the resentment of those around him.","furigana":"かれ|ごうがんふそん|たいど|しゅうい|はんかん|か"},{"japanese":"新人のくせに先輩に傲岸不遜な口をきく。","romaji":"Shinjin no kuse ni senpai ni gōgan fuson na kuchi o kiku.","german":"Though he's a newcomer, he speaks to his seniors in an insolent tone.","furigana":"しんじん|せんぱい|ごうがんふそん|くち"},{"japanese":"権力を握ると、彼は傲岸不遜に振る舞うようになった。","romaji":"Kenryoku o nigiru to, kare wa gōgan fuson ni furumau yō ni natta.","german":"Once he took power, he started behaving arrogantly.","furigana":"けんりょく|にぎ|かれ|ごうがんふそん|ふ|ま"}],"notes":"傲岸 means \"haughty, unyielding\", 不遜 \"disrespectful, presumptuous\": 傲岸不遜 condemns an arrogant attitude in which someone treats others condescendingly. Typical: 傲岸不遜な態度／物言い, 傲岸不遜に振る舞う. It is strongly judgmental and literary; in everyday speech people say 偉そう or 生意気. 傲慢 is the ordinary word for \"arrogant\"; 傲岸不遜 sounds even sharper.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その計画は曖昧模糊としていて具体性に欠ける。","romaji":"Sono keikaku wa aimai moko to shite ite gutaisei ni kakeru.","german":"That plan is vague and lacks concreteness.","furigana":"けいかく|あいまいもこ|ぐたいせい|か"},{"japanese":"大臣の答弁は曖昧模糊として、要点がつかめなかった。","romaji":"Daijin no tōben wa aimai moko to shite, yōten ga tsukamenakatta.","german":"The minister's reply was so nebulous that the main point could not be grasped.","furigana":"だいじん|とうべん|あいまいもこ|ようてん"},{"japanese":"幼い頃の記憶は曖昧模糊としている。","romaji":"Osanai koro no kioku wa aimai moko to shite iru.","german":"My memories of early childhood are hazy.","furigana":"おさな|ころ|きおく|あいまいもこ"}],"notes":"曖昧 means \"unclear, ambiguous\", 模糊 \"blurred, misty\": 曖昧模糊 intensifies the meaning of 曖昧 and describes plans, statements, memories or boundaries that cannot be clearly grasped. Most often with と: 曖昧模糊とした説明, 曖昧模糊としている. It is literary; in everyday speech 曖昧 or はっきりしない is enough. The opposite would be 明確 or 明瞭.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"試験前なのに遊びたい気持ちが抑えられず、意馬心猿の状態だ。","romaji":"Shiken mae na no ni asobitai kimochi ga osaerarezu, iba shin'en no jōtai da.","german":"Even though the exam is coming up, I can't suppress my urge to play, and my mind is restless.","furigana":"しけんまえ|あそ|きも|おさ|いばしんえん|じょうたい"},{"japanese":"意馬心猿を抑えるために、毎朝座禅を組んでいる。","romaji":"Iba shin'en o osaeru tame ni, maiasa zazen o kunde iru.","german":"To curb my restless desires, I sit in Zen meditation every morning.","furigana":"いばしんえん|おさ|まいあさざぜん|く"},{"japanese":"修行僧でさえ、意馬心猿に悩まされることがあるという。","romaji":"Shugyōsō de sae, iba shin'en ni nayamasareru koto ga aru to iu.","german":"It is said that even monks in training are sometimes plagued by unruly desires.","furigana":"しゅぎょうそう|いばしんえん|なや"}],"notes":"From Buddhism: the will (意) gallops like a horse (馬), the heart (心) leaps about like a monkey (猿). 意馬心猿 describes worldly desires and passions that cannot be reined in, leaving one inwardly restless. Typical: 意馬心猿を抑える, 意馬心猿の状態. It is very literary and concerns desire and temptation, not mere nervousness; for general restlessness people say 落ち着かない or そわそわする.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"古典作品を換骨奪胎して現代風にアレンジした。","romaji":"Koten sakuhin o kankotsu dattai shite gendaifū ni arenji shita.","german":"The classic work was reworked and arranged in a modern style.","furigana":"こてんさくひん|かんこつだったい|げんだいふう"},{"japanese":"この映画はシェイクスピアの悲劇を換骨奪胎したものだ。","romaji":"Kono eiga wa Sheikusupia no higeki o kankotsu dattai shita mono da.","german":"This film is a free reworking of one of Shakespeare's tragedies.","furigana":"えいが|ひげき|かんこつだったい"},{"japanese":"単なる模倣ではなく、換骨奪胎して独自の作品に仕上げた。","romaji":"Tannaru mohō de wa naku, kankotsu dattai shite dokuji no sakuhin ni shiageta.","german":"Rather than simply imitating, he transformed the source and finished it as an original work.","furigana":"たん|もほう|かんこつだったい|どくじ|さくひん|しあ"}],"notes":"Literally \"to change the bones and take over the womb\": originally a term from Chinese poetry for taking the idea of an older work and recasting it into something of one's own. 換骨奪胎 is a transitive する-verb: 〜を換骨奪胎する. Used correctly it is positive (creative reinvention); however, it is often used disparagingly for mere rewriting or copying. Not to be confused with 盗作 (\"plagiarism\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"疾風怒濤の時代を生き抜いた。","romaji":"Shippū dotō no jidai o ikinuita.","german":"Er hat eine stürmische, turbulente Zeit überstanden.","furigana":"しっぷうどとう|じだい|い|ぬ"},{"japanese":"ゲーテとシラーは疾風怒濤運動の代表的な作家だ。","romaji":"Gēte to Shirā wa shippū dotō undō no daihyōteki na sakka da.","german":"Goethe und Schiller sind die wichtigsten Dichter des Sturm und Drang.","furigana":"しっぷうどとううんどう|だいひょうてき|さっか"},{"japanese":"思春期は疾風怒濤の時期と言われる。","romaji":"Shishunki wa shippū dotō no jiki to iwareru.","german":"Die Pubertät gilt als eine Zeit des Sturm und Drang.","furigana":"ししゅんき|しっぷうどとう|じき|い"}],"notes":"疾風 ist ein heftiger Wind, 怒濤 tosende Wogen. 疾風怒濤 ist die japanische Übersetzung von „Sturm und Drang“ und bezeichnet sowohl die deutsche Literaturepoche um Goethe und Schiller als auch allgemein eine stürmische, aufgewühlte Zeit. In der Psychologie nennt man die Pubertät 疾風怒濤の時代. Fast immer attributiv: 疾風怒濤の時代／青春. Für Naturereignisse selbst sagt man eher 嵐 oder 暴風.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大言壮語ばかりで行動が伴わない。","romaji":"Taigen sōgo bakari de kōdō ga tomonawanai.","german":"He's all talk and no action.","furigana":"たいげんそうご|こうどう|ともな"},{"japanese":"「必ず優勝する」と大言壮語した手前、負けるわけにはいかない。","romaji":"\"Kanarazu yūshō suru\" to taigen sōgo shita temae, makeru wake ni wa ikanai.","german":"Having so boldly declared, \"We will definitely win the championship,\" I can't afford to lose.","furigana":"かなら|ゆうしょう|たいげんそうご|てまえ|ま"},{"japanese":"選挙前の大言壮語に、有権者はもう騙されない。","romaji":"Senkyo mae no taigen sōgo ni, yūkensha wa mō damasarenai.","german":"Voters are no longer fooled by the grand talk before an election.","furigana":"せんきょまえ|たいげんそうご|ゆうけんしゃ|だま"}],"notes":"大言 is \"big words\", 壮語 \"grandiose talk\": 大言壮語 means to boast, to announce things one cannot deliver. It can stand as a noun (大言壮語ばかり) or as an intransitive する-verb (〜と大言壮語する). The tone is pejorative and literary; colloquially people say 大口を叩く or ビッグマウス. Unlike 自慢 (\"showing off about something one has\"), it is mostly about boastful promises.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この地方は風光明媚で観光客が多い。","romaji":"Kono chihō wa fūkō meibi de kankōkyaku ga ōi.","german":"This region has picturesque scenery and attracts many tourists.","furigana":"ちほう|ふうこうめいび|かんこうきゃく|おお"},{"japanese":"老後は風光明媚な海辺の町で暮らしたい。","romaji":"Rōgo wa fūkō meibi na umibe no machi de kurashitai.","german":"In retirement, I want to live in a scenic seaside town.","furigana":"ろうご|ふうこうめいび|うみべ|まち|く"},{"japanese":"風光明媚な湖畔に、小さなホテルが建っている。","romaji":"Fūkō meibi na kohan ni, chiisana hoteru ga tatte iru.","german":"A small hotel stands on the shore of a scenic lake.","furigana":"ふうこうめいび|こはん|ちい|た"}],"notes":"風光 is the scenery, 明媚 \"clear and lovely\": 風光明媚 is the most common expression for a picturesque area and frequent in travel brochures and descriptions of places. As a na-adjective: 風光明媚な土地／湖畔, predicatively 風光明媚だ／で. Compared with 山紫水明, which is more literary and refers specifically to mountains and clear water, 風光明媚 is more general and also suits coasts or islands.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"乾坤一擲の勝負に出た。","romaji":"Kenkon itteki no shōbu ni deta.","german":"He made an all-or-nothing bet.","furigana":"けんこんいってき|しょうぶ|で"},{"japanese":"会社の命運をかけて、乾坤一擲の新製品を発表した。","romaji":"Kaisha no meiun o kakete, kenkon itteki no shinseihin o happyō shita.","german":"Staking the company's fate on it, they unveiled a make-or-break new product.","furigana":"かいしゃ|めいうん|けんこんいってき|しんせいひん|はっぴょう"},{"japanese":"試合終了間際、乾坤一擲のロングシュートが決まった。","romaji":"Shiai shūryō magiwa, kenkon itteki no rongu shūto ga kimatta.","german":"Just before the end of the game, a desperate long shot went in.","furigana":"しあいしゅうりょうまぎわ|けんこんいってき|き"}],"notes":"乾坤 means \"heaven and earth\", 一擲 \"a single throw\" (of the dice): one stakes everything, even heaven and earth, on one throw. 乾坤一擲 describes a decisive, risky commitment. Fixed frames: 乾坤一擲の勝負／大勝負に出る, 乾坤一擲の一手. It is pathetic in tone and literary; in everyday speech people say 一か八か or 大きな賭けに出る.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼はこの分野の泰山北斗だ。","romaji":"Kare wa kono bun'ya no taizan hokuto da.","german":"He is the undisputed authority in this field.","furigana":"かれ|ぶんや|たいざんほくと"},{"japanese":"故・山田教授は、日本の物理学界の泰山北斗と仰がれた。","romaji":"Ko Yamada kyōju wa, Nihon no butsurigakkai no taizan hokuto to aogareta.","german":"The late Professor Yamada was revered as the foremost authority in Japanese physics.","furigana":"こ|やまだきょうじゅ|にほん|ぶつりがっかい|たいざんほくと|あお"},{"japanese":"泰山北斗と呼ばれる書道家に、弟子入りを願い出た。","romaji":"Taizan hokuto to yobareru shodōka ni, deshiiri o negaideta.","german":"I asked a calligrapher said to be the undisputed master to take me on as a disciple.","furigana":"たいざんほくと|よ|しょどうか|でしい|ねが|で"}],"notes":"泰山 is Mount Tai, the sacred mountain in China, 北斗 the Big Dipper: both are points of orientation to which everyone looks up. 泰山北斗 denotes an outstanding, universally revered authority in a field. Typical: 〜界の泰山北斗, 泰山北斗と仰がれる. More common is the short form 泰斗 (医学の泰斗). It is very formal and said about others; more neutral would be 第一人者 or 権威.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"虚心坦懐に意見を聞くべきだ。","romaji":"Kyoshin tankai ni iken o kiku beki da.","german":"One should listen to opinions with an open mind.","furigana":"きょしんたんかい|いけん|き"},{"japanese":"両国の首脳は虚心坦懐に話し合った。","romaji":"Ryōkoku no shunō wa kyoshin tankai ni hanashiatta.","german":"The leaders of the two countries talked frankly and without reservations.","furigana":"りょうこく|しゅのう|きょしんたんかい|はな|あ"},{"japanese":"批判されても、虚心坦懐に受け止めて改善したい。","romaji":"Hihan sarete mo, kyoshin tankai ni uketomete kaizen shitai.","german":"Even if I am criticized, I want to accept it without bias and improve.","furigana":"ひはん|きょしんたんかい|う|と|かいぜん"}],"notes":"虚心 means \"without prejudice, with an empty heart\", 坦懐 \"open and calm in temperament\": 虚心坦懐 describes an attitude with which one listens or speaks without bias and ulterior motives. Mostly adverbial: 虚心坦懐に話し合う／耳を傾ける. Popular in politics and negotiations. Related is 素直 (\"sincere, obedient\"), which describes more of a character trait; 虚心坦懐 means a deliberately adopted attitude.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その企業は業界で金城湯池の地位を築いた。","romaji":"Sono kigyō wa gyōkai de kinjō tōchi no chii o kizuita.","german":"That company has built an unassailable position in its industry.","furigana":"きぎょう|ぎょうかい|きんじょうとうち|ちい|きず"},{"japanese":"この選挙区は長年、保守党の金城湯池だった。","romaji":"Kono senkyoku wa naganen, hoshutō no kinjō tōchi datta.","german":"This electoral district was for many years a stronghold of the conservatives.","furigana":"せんきょく|ながねん|ほしゅとう|きんじょうとうち"},{"japanese":"新興企業が、大手の金城湯池だった市場を崩しつつある。","romaji":"Shinkō kigyō ga, ōte no kinjō tōchi datta shijō o kuzushitsutsu aru.","german":"A start-up is breaking into a market that had been the stronghold of the big corporations.","furigana":"しんこうきぎょう|おおて|きんじょうとうち|しじょう|くず"}],"notes":"金城 is a castle with metal walls, 湯池 a moat of boiling water: a fortress that cannot be taken. Today 金城湯池 mainly denotes a secure stronghold or firmly held territory, for example of a politician or party in an electoral district or of a company in a market. Typical: 〜の金城湯池だ, 金城湯池を守る／崩す. Similar are 牙城 and 地盤.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"百花繚乱の芸術シーンが広がっている。","romaji":"Hyakka ryōran no geijutsu shīn ga hirogatte iru.","german":"A dazzling profusion of art styles is spreading.","furigana":"ひゃっかりょうらん|げいじゅつ|ひろ"},{"japanese":"春の庭園は、まさに百花繚乱だった。","romaji":"Haru no teien wa, masa ni hyakka ryōran datta.","german":"The garden in spring was truly a sea of blossoms.","furigana":"はる|ていえん|ひゃっかりょうらん"},{"japanese":"今年の新人賞の候補は、百花繚乱の顔ぶれだ。","romaji":"Kotoshi no shinjinshō no kōho wa, hyakka ryōran no kaobure da.","german":"This year's candidates for the newcomer award are a lineup of diverse talents.","furigana":"ことし|しんじんしょう|こうほ|ひゃっかりょうらん|かお"}],"notes":"Literally \"a hundred flowers bloom in wild profusion\". 百花繚乱 first describes a lush display of flowers, and figuratively the simultaneous appearance of many talents, beautiful people or different styles and works. Typical: 百花繚乱の時代／顔ぶれ, predicatively 百花繚乱だ. Also written 百花撩乱. Positive and colorful in tone, often used in culture and sports reports.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子供の教育のために引っ越したのは、まさに孟母三遷だ。","romaji":"Kodomo no kyōiku no tame ni hikkoshita no wa, masa ni mōbo sansen da.","german":"Moving for the sake of the child's education is exactly the example of Mencius's mother.","furigana":"こども|きょういく|ひ|こ|もうぼさんせん"},{"japanese":"孟母三遷の教えにならい、図書館の近くに家を買った。","romaji":"Mōbo sansen no oshie ni narai, toshokan no chikaku ni ie o katta.","german":"Following the lesson of Mencius's mother, we bought a house near the library.","furigana":"もうぼさんせん|おし|としょかん|ちか|いえ|か"},{"japanese":"孟母三遷というように、子どもは環境から大きな影響を受ける。","romaji":"Mōbo sansen to iu yō ni, kodomo wa kankyō kara ōkina eikyō o ukeru.","german":"As the story of Mencius's mother shows, children are greatly influenced by their environment.","furigana":"もうぼさんせん|こ|かんきょう|おお|えいきょう|う"}],"notes":"According to tradition, the mother of Mencius (孟母) moved three times (三遷): away from the cemetery, away from the market, until she lived next to a school, where her son imitated the learning. 孟母三遷 stresses how important a good environment is for upbringing. Mostly quoted as a lesson: 孟母三遷の教え, 孟母三遷というように. A related expression about the same mother is 孟母断機.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の冗談に全員が呵呵大笑した。","romaji":"Kare no jōdan ni zen'in ga kaka taishō shita.","german":"Everyone burst into roaring laughter at his joke.","furigana":"かれ|じょうだん|ぜんいん|かかたいしょう"},{"japanese":"社長は部下の失敗を聞くと、呵呵大笑して許してくれた。","romaji":"Shachō wa buka no shippai o kiku to, kaka taishō shite yurushite kureta.","german":"When the president heard about his subordinate's mistake, he laughed heartily and forgave him.","furigana":"しゃちょう|ぶか|しっぱい|き|かかたいしょう|ゆる"},{"japanese":"老僧は弟子の問いに答えず、ただ呵呵大笑した。","romaji":"Rōsō wa deshi no toi ni kotaezu, tada kaka taishō shita.","german":"The old monk did not answer his disciple's question but simply laughed heartily.","furigana":"ろうそう|でし|と|こた|かかたいしょう"}],"notes":"呵呵 is onomatopoeic for loud laughter (like \"haha\"), 大笑 \"to laugh big, heartily\". 呵呵大笑 describes a booming, carefree laugh, often from someone with a generous, composed manner. Mostly as a する-verb: 呵呵大笑する. It is very literary; in everyday speech people say 大笑いする or 爆笑する. The expression sounds more like a serene old master than like giggling.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"心頭滅却すれば火もまた涼し。","romaji":"Shintō mekkyaku sureba hi mo mata suzushi.","german":"If you clear your mind completely, even fire feels cool.","furigana":"しんとうめっきゃく|ひ|すず"},{"japanese":"猛暑の中、心頭滅却して練習を続けた。","romaji":"Mōsho no naka, shintō mekkyaku shite renshū o tsuzuketa.","german":"In the intense heat, I cleared my mind and kept practicing.","furigana":"もうしょ|なか|しんとうめっきゃく|れんしゅう|つづ"},{"japanese":"雑念を払い、心頭滅却して座禅に集中する。","romaji":"Zatsunen o harai, shintō mekkyaku shite zazen ni shūchū suru.","german":"I dispel stray thoughts and concentrate on Zen meditation with an empty mind.","furigana":"ざつねん|はら|しんとうめっきゃく|ざぜん|しゅうちゅう"}],"notes":"心頭 is \"the inside, the mind\", 滅却 \"to extinguish\": 心頭滅却 means to switch off all disturbing thoughts and sensations. Famous is the saying 心頭滅却すれば火もまた涼し, which the Zen monk 快川紹喜 is said to have uttered as his temple was burned: with a purified mind, even fire is cool. Today it is mostly quoted for enduring heat, pain or stress through willpower; also as a する-verb: 心頭滅却して.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"勧善懲悪をテーマにした時代劇が人気だ。","romaji":"Kanzen chōaku o tēma ni shita jidaigeki ga ninki da.","german":"Historiendramen mit dem Thema Gut gegen Böse sind beliebt.","furigana":"かんぜんちょうあく|じだいげき|にんき"},{"japanese":"「水戸黄門」は勧善懲悪の典型的な時代劇だ。","romaji":"\"Mito Kōmon\" wa kanzen chōaku no tenkeiteki na jidaigeki da.","german":"„Mito Kōmon“ ist das klassische Samurai-Drama, in dem am Ende stets das Gute siegt.","furigana":"|かんぜんちょうあく|てんけいてき|じだいげき"},{"japanese":"単純な勧善懲悪の話では、大人の読者は満足しない。","romaji":"Tanjun na kanzen chōaku no hanashi de wa, otona no dokusha wa manzoku shinai.","german":"Mit einer simplen Gut-gegen-Böse-Geschichte geben sich erwachsene Leser nicht zufrieden.","furigana":"たんじゅん|かんぜんちょうあく|はなし|おとな|どくしゃ|まんぞく"}],"notes":"勧善 heißt „das Gute fördern“, 懲悪 „das Böse bestrafen“. 勧善懲悪 beschreibt vor allem Geschichten mit klarer Moral, in denen die Guten belohnt und die Bösen bestraft werden – typisch für 時代劇, Märchen und Heldenserien. Häufige Rahmen: 勧善懲悪の物語／ドラマ, 勧善懲悪をテーマにする. Oft schwingt leise Kritik an Schwarz-Weiß-Malerei mit; im Deutschen entspricht es etwa „ausgleichende Gerechtigkeit“ in der Fiktion.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の深謀遠慮のおかげで会社は危機を免れた。","romaji":"Kare no shinbō enryo no okage de kaisha wa kiki o manukareta.","german":"Thanks to his far-sighted planning, the company avoided a crisis.","furigana":"かれ|しんぼうえんりょ|かいしゃ|きき|まぬか"},{"japanese":"一見無謀な決断も、実は深謀遠慮の末だった。","romaji":"Ikken mubō na ketsudan mo, jitsu wa shinbō enryo no sue datta.","german":"The seemingly reckless decision was actually the result of deep foresight.","furigana":"いっけんむぼう|けつだん|じつ|しんぼうえんりょ|すえ"},{"japanese":"天下統一のために、家康は深謀遠慮をめぐらした。","romaji":"Tenka tōitsu no tame ni, Ieyasu wa shinbō enryo o megurashita.","german":"Ieyasu laid far-sighted plans to unify the country.","furigana":"てんかとういつ||しんぼうえんりょ"}],"notes":"深謀 is \"profound planning\", 遠慮 here \"thinking far ahead\", not the everyday meaning \"restraint\". 深謀遠慮 denotes careful, far-sighted strategies that take future developments into account. Typical: 深謀遠慮をめぐらす, 〜の深謀遠慮, 深謀遠慮に富む. It is appreciative; for cunning schemes in a pejorative sense there is 権謀術数.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"傍目八目というように、当事者より第三者のほうが冷静に判断できるものだ。","romaji":"Okame hachimoku to iu yō ni, tōjisha yori daisansha no hō ga reisei ni handan dekiru mono da.","german":"As they say, a third party can judge more calmly than those involved.","furigana":"おかめはちもく|とうじしゃ|だいさんしゃ|れいせい|はんだん"},{"japanese":"傍目八目で言わせてもらうと、君たちの計画は少し無理があると思う。","romaji":"Okame hachimoku de iwasete morau to, kimitachi no keikaku wa sukoshi muri ga aru to omou.","german":"If I may say so as an outsider, I think your plan is a bit unrealistic.","furigana":"おかめはちもく|い|きみ|けいかく|すこ|むり|おも"},{"japanese":"囲碁を横で見ていた友人が、傍目八目で勝ち筋に気づいた。","romaji":"Igo o yoko de mite ita yūjin ga, okame hachimoku de kachisuji ni kizuita.","german":"A friend who was watching the go game from the side noticed the winning line, as onlookers do.","furigana":"いご|よこ|み|ゆうじん|おかめはちもく|か|すじ|き"}],"notes":"Literally: 傍目 \"watching from the side\" + 八目 \"eight moves\": someone who merely watches go sees eight moves further than the players. It means that outsiders often judge a situation more clearly than those involved. Common frames: 傍目八目というように, 傍目八目で言わせてもらうと (\"speaking as an outsider\"). It is also written 岡目八目; the reading remains おかめはちもく. Slightly elevated, but also common in conversation.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"有為転変の世を生きるには、柔軟さが必要だ。","romaji":"Ui tenpen no yo o ikiru ni wa, jūnansa ga hitsuyō da.","german":"To live in a world of constant change, one needs flexibility.","furigana":"ういてんぺん|よ|い|じゅうなん|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"有為転変は世の習いとはいえ、あの大企業が倒産するとは思わなかった。","romaji":"Ui tenpen wa yo no narai to wa ie, ano daikigyō ga tōsan suru to wa omowanakatta.","german":"Change may be the way of the world, but I never thought that large company would go bankrupt.","furigana":"ういてんぺん|よ|なら|だいきぎょう|とうさん|おも"},{"japanese":"祖父は戦争と復興という有為転変の時代を生き抜いた。","romaji":"Sofu wa sensō to fukkō to iu ui tenpen no jidai o ikinuita.","german":"My grandfather lived through the turbulent era of war and reconstruction.","furigana":"そふ|せんそう|ふっこう|ういてんぺん|じだい|い|ぬ"}],"notes":"A Buddhist expression: 有為 (\"the conditioned\", everything that arises through causes) + 転変 (\"unceasing change\"): nothing in the world remains as it is. Well known is the phrase 有為転変は世の習い (\"change is the way of the world\"); attributively: 有為転変の世, 有為転変の人生. Note: here 有為 is read うい, not ゆうい (\"capable, promising\"). Related are 諸行無常 and 栄枯盛衰; literary, typical of speeches, essays and obituaries.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"閑話休題、本題に戻りましょう。","romaji":"Kanwa kyūdai, hondai ni modorimashō.","german":"Anyway, let's get back to the main topic.","furigana":"かんわきゅうだい|ほんだい|もど"},{"japanese":"閑話休題。今日の本題は新しいカメラのレビューです。","romaji":"Kanwa kyūdai. Kyō no hondai wa atarashii kamera no rebyū desu.","german":"But enough digressing; today's main topic is a review of the new camera.","furigana":"かんわきゅうだい|きょう|ほんだい|あたら"},{"japanese":"昔話が長くなりましたが、閑話休題、来年度の計画についてお話しします。","romaji":"Mukashibanashi ga nagaku narimashita ga, kanwa kyūdai, rainendo no keikaku ni tsuite ohanashi shimasu.","german":"My reminiscing has gone on too long, but to get back to the point, I will now talk about plans for the next fiscal year.","furigana":"むかしばなし|なが|かんわきゅうだい|らいねんど|けいかく|はな"}],"notes":"閑話休題 is composed of 閑話 \"idle talk, digression\" and 休題 \"to let the topic rest\" and means \"enough of the digression, back to the topic\". It stands as a conjunction at the beginning of a sentence, mostly in essays, columns, blogs or speeches, often reinforced by 本題に戻ろう. Many native speakers wrongly understand it as introducing a digression (\"by the way\"); the correct meaning is the opposite. In everyday speech, people say more simply それはさておき or 話を戻すと.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"優勝に加えてMVPも獲得し、錦上添花だ。","romaji":"Yūshō ni kuwaete MVP mo kakutoku shi, kinjō tenka da.","german":"On top of winning the championship, he also took the MVP, the icing on the cake.","furigana":"ゆうしょう|くわ|かくとく|きんじょうてんか"},{"japanese":"美しい庭園に新しい噴水ができ、まさに錦上添花となった。","romaji":"Utsukushii teien ni atarashii funsui ga deki, masa ni kinjō tenka to natta.","german":"A new fountain was added to the beautiful garden, which really put the finishing touch on it.","furigana":"うつく|ていえん|あたら|ふんすい|きんじょうてんか"},{"japanese":"二人の結婚式に恩師も駆けつけてくれ、錦上添花の一日となった。","romaji":"Futari no kekkonshiki ni onshi mo kaketsukete kure, kinjō tenka no ichinichi to natta.","german":"Their former teacher also rushed to the couple's wedding, making it a day crowned with joy.","furigana":"ふたり|けっこんしき|おんし|か|きんじょうてんか|いちにち"}],"notes":"錦上添花 literally means \"to add (添) flowers (花) onto brocade (錦)\" and comes from Chinese: something already beautiful or pleasing is crowned by yet another good thing. In Japanese the expression is rather rare and elevated; it appears in speeches and texts, for example 錦上添花だ or 錦上添花となる. More common is the resolved form 錦上花を添える or simply 花を添える. Opposite: 泣きっ面に蜂 (misfortune upon misfortune); do not confuse it with 蛇足 (superfluous addition).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"九死に一生を得て生還した。","romaji":"Kyūshi ni isshō o ete seikan shita.","german":"He narrowly escaped death and returned alive.","furigana":"きゅうし|いっしょう|え|せいかん"},{"japanese":"遭難から救出された登山者が、九死一生の体験を語った。","romaji":"Sōnan kara kyūshutsu sareta tozansha ga, kyūshi isshō no taiken o katatta.","german":"The climber rescued from the mountain disaster told of his brush with death.","furigana":"そうなん|きゅうしゅつ|とざんしゃ|きゅうしいっしょう|たいけん|かた"},{"japanese":"大地震で建物の下敷きになったが、九死に一生を得た。","romaji":"Ōjishin de tatemono no shitajiki ni natta ga, kyūshi ni isshō o eta.","german":"He was buried under a building in the big earthquake but survived by a hair's breadth.","furigana":"おおじしん|たてもの||きゅうし|いっしょう|え"}],"notes":"九死一生 literally means \"nine parts death, one part life\": the situation was almost hopeless, and yet one survived. By far the most common form is the phrase 九死に一生を得る \"to narrowly escape death\"; as a compound it appears attributively, for example 九死一生の体験 or 九死一生の思い. Typical in reports about accidents, disasters or serious illness; the tone is serious and rather literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"多事多難な一年だったが、乗り越えた。","romaji":"Taji tanan na ichinen datta ga, norikoeta.","german":"It was a year full of troubles, but I got through it.","furigana":"たじたなん|いちねん|の|こ"},{"japanese":"社長は年頭のあいさつで、昨年は多事多難の年だったと振り返った。","romaji":"Shachō wa nentō no aisatsu de, sakunen wa taji tanan no toshi datta to furikaetta.","german":"In his New Year's address, the president looked back on last year as an eventful, difficult year.","furigana":"しゃちょう|ねんとう|さくねん|たじたなん|とし|ふ|かえ"},{"japanese":"多事多難な時代を生き抜いた祖父の話を聞いた。","romaji":"Taji tanan na jidai o ikinuita sofu no hanashi o kiita.","german":"I listened to the stories of my grandfather, who lived through a time of turmoil and hardship.","furigana":"たじたなん|じだい|い|ぬ|そふ|はなし|き"}],"notes":"多事多難 combines 多事 \"many incidents\" and 多難 \"many difficulties\" and describes a time full of problems and unsettling events. It is very typical in year-end reviews, New Year's addresses and editorials: 多事多難な一年, 多事多難の年, 多事多難な時代. It is used as a na-adjective or with の. Do not confuse it with 多事多端 (\"a lot to do, very busy\") and 前途多難 (difficulties that still lie ahead).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"粗製乱造の製品では消費者の信頼を得られない。","romaji":"Sosei ranzō no seihin de wa shōhisha no shinrai o erarenai.","german":"Shoddily made products cannot win consumers' trust.","furigana":"そせいらんぞう|せいひん|しょうひしゃ|しんらい|え"},{"japanese":"ブームに乗って、似たような番組が粗製乱造されている。","romaji":"Būmu ni notte, nita yō na bangumi ga sosei ranzō sarete iru.","german":"Riding the boom, similar-looking programs are being churned out.","furigana":"の|に|ばんぐみ|そせいらんぞう"},{"japanese":"当時は粗製乱造の建売住宅も多かった。","romaji":"Tōji wa sosei ranzō no tateuri jūtaku mo ōkatta.","german":"At that time there were also many shoddily built ready-made houses.","furigana":"とうじ|そせいらんぞう||おお"}],"notes":"粗製乱造 consists of 粗製 \"crude, careless manufacture\" and 乱造 \"indiscriminate mass production\": cheap goods are turned out in large numbers with no regard for quality. The original spelling is 粗製濫造; 乱 is the simplified substitute. It is also a transitive する-verb (〜を粗製乱造する, passive 粗製乱造される) and is often applied figuratively to TV programs, books, laws or qualifications. The expression is always critical and rather literary.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"パーティーにちょっと顔を出した。","romaji":"Pātī ni chotto kao o dashita.","german":"I dropped by the party for a bit.","furigana":"かお|だ"},{"japanese":"時間があったら、たまには実家に顔を出しなさい。","romaji":"Jikan ga attara, tama ni wa jikka ni kao o dashinasai.","german":"If you have time, go visit your parents' home once in a while.","furigana":"じかん|じっか|かお|だ"},{"japanese":"雲の間から太陽が顔を出した。","romaji":"Kumo no aida kara taiyō ga kao o dashita.","german":"The sun came out from between the clouds.","furigana":"くも|あいだ|たいよう|かお|だ"}],"notes":"顔を出す literally means \"to stick out one's face\" and usually means \"to drop by briefly, to make an appearance\". The place takes に: パーティーに顔を出す, 会社に顔を出す, 実家に顔を出す; often with ちょっと or たまには. It sounds more casual than 出席する \"to attend\" and stresses that you are only there briefly. It can also mean that something hidden \"comes into view\": 太陽が顔を出す. Similar is 顔を見せる \"to show oneself\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女の変身ぶりに目を丸くした。","romaji":"Kanojo no henshin-buri ni me o maruku shita.","german":"I opened my eyes wide at her transformation.","furigana":"かのじょ|へんしん|め|まる"},{"japanese":"値段を聞いて、母は目を丸くした。","romaji":"Nedan o kiite, haha wa me o maruku shita.","german":"When she heard the price, my mother opened her eyes wide.","furigana":"ねだん|き|はは|め|まる"},{"japanese":"子どもたちは初めて見る象の大きさに目を丸くしていた。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa hajimete miru zō no ōkisa ni me o maruku shite ita.","german":"The children stared wide-eyed at the size of the elephant they were seeing for the first time.","furigana":"こ|はじ|み|ぞう|おお|め|まる"}],"notes":"目を丸くする literally means \"to make one's eyes round\" and describes visible astonishment or amazement, mostly reported in the past tense: 〜に目を丸くした or 目を丸くして驚く. The cause of the astonishment is marked with に. Similar is the more elevated 目を見張る \"to open one's eyes wide, to marvel (often in admiration)\"; the colloquial 目が点になる, by contrast, expresses speechless bewilderment. Neutral, common in narratives and reports.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今回だけは目をつぶってください。","romaji":"Konkai dake wa me o tsubutte kudasai.","german":"Please turn a blind eye just this once.","furigana":"こんかい|め"},{"japanese":"新人の小さなミスには目をつぶることにした。","romaji":"Shinjin no chiisana misu ni wa me o tsuburu koto ni shita.","german":"I decided to overlook the newcomer's small mistakes.","furigana":"しんじん|ちい|め"},{"japanese":"目をつぶって、ゆっくり深呼吸してください。","romaji":"Me o tsubutte, yukkuri shinkokyū shite kudasai.","german":"Close your eyes and take a slow, deep breath.","furigana":"め|しんこきゅう"}],"notes":"目をつぶる literally means \"to close one's eyes\" (the more neutral word is 目を閉じる). Figuratively, 〜に目をつぶる means \"to turn a blind eye to something, to deliberately overlook something\", typically for small mistakes or rule violations: ミスに目をつぶる. Similar is 大目に見る \"to be lenient\". つぶる is almost always written in hiragana (rarely 瞑る); it is a godan verb ending in -ru.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その噂を耳にしたことがある。","romaji":"Sono uwasa o mimi ni shita koto ga aru.","german":"I have heard that rumor before.","furigana":"うわさ|みみ"},{"japanese":"最近よく耳にする言葉ですね。","romaji":"Saikin yoku mimi ni suru kotoba desu ne.","german":"That's a word you hear a lot lately, isn't it.","furigana":"さいきん|みみ|ことば"},{"japanese":"駅で偶然、二人が結婚するという話を耳にした。","romaji":"Eki de gūzen, futari ga kekkon suru to iu hanashi o mimi ni shita.","german":"At the station I happened to overhear that the two are getting married.","furigana":"えき|ぐうぜん|ふたり|けっこん|はなし|みみ"}],"notes":"耳にする literally means \"to make something one's ear\" and means \"to (happen to) hear, to get wind of\". Unlike 聞く, which can also mean listening actively or asking, 耳にする describes casually catching rumors, news or words: 噂を耳にする, よく耳にする言葉, 〜という話を耳にした. It is transitive with を; the intransitive variant is 耳に入る \"to reach one's ears\". The expression sounds somewhat more refined than 聞く; parallel to it there is 目にする \"to catch sight of\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先生の言葉は耳が痛い。","romaji":"Sensei no kotoba wa mimi ga itai.","german":"The teacher's words hurt because they are true.","furigana":"せんせい|ことば|みみ|いた"},{"japanese":"「もっと早く寝なさい」と言われると、耳が痛い。","romaji":"\"Motto hayaku nenasai\" to iwareru to, mimi ga itai.","german":"When I'm told to go to bed earlier, it stings because it's true.","furigana":"はや|ね|い|みみ|いた"},{"japanese":"耳が痛い話ですが、確かにおっしゃる通りです。","romaji":"Mimi ga itai hanashi desu ga, tashika ni ossharu tōri desu.","german":"It's hard to hear, but you are absolutely right.","furigana":"みみ|いた|はなし|たし|とお"}],"notes":"耳が痛い literally means \"the ears hurt\" and figuratively means that criticism or a reprimand is uncomfortable because it hits the mark exactly. Typical are 耳が痛い話 and 〜と言われると耳が痛い; with it, one often admits that the reproach is justified. Literally it can also simply mean \"to have an earache\". Neutral, common in conversation and columns.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は口が軽いから、秘密を教えないで。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kuchi ga karui kara, himitsu o oshienaide.","german":"She has a loose tongue, so don't tell her any secrets.","furigana":"かのじょ|くち|かる|ひみつ|おし"},{"japanese":"お酒を飲むと、部長は急に口が軽くなる。","romaji":"Osake o nomu to, buchō wa kyū ni kuchi ga karuku naru.","german":"When he drinks, the department head suddenly becomes talkative.","furigana":"さけ|の|ぶちょう|きゅう|くち|かる"},{"japanese":"私の口が軽いせいで、友達へのサプライズがばれてしまった。","romaji":"Watashi no kuchi ga karui sei de, tomodachi e no sapuraizu ga barete shimatta.","german":"Because I couldn't keep my mouth shut, the surprise for my friend was spoiled.","furigana":"わたし|くち|かる|ともだち"}],"notes":"口が軽い literally means \"the mouth is light\" and describes someone who blabs secrets and cannot keep anything to himself. The opposite is 口が堅い \"discreet\". With なる it describes a state: お酒を飲むと口が軽くなる \"when drinking, one becomes talkative\". Not to be confused with 口が悪い \"to have a sharp, hurtful tongue\". Neutral, clearly negative in evaluation.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人の問題に口を出さないでほしい。","romaji":"Hito no mondai ni kuchi o dasanaide hoshii.","german":"I don't want people meddling in my problems.","furigana":"ひと|もんだい|くち|だ"},{"japanese":"母は私の結婚相手のことにまで口を出してくる。","romaji":"Haha wa watashi no kekkon aite no koto ni made kuchi o dashite kuru.","german":"My mother even interferes in the choice of my marriage partner.","furigana":"はは|わたし|けっこんあいて|くち|だ"},{"japanese":"部長は細かいことにまで口を出すので、みんな困っている。","romaji":"Buchō wa komakai koto ni made kuchi o dasu node, minna komatte iru.","german":"The department head interferes even in minor details, which bothers everyone.","furigana":"ぶちょう|こま|くち|だ|こま"}],"notes":"口を出す literally means \"to stick out one's mouth\" and means \"to (uninvited) butt in, to interfere\". The matter takes に: 人のことに口を出す, 〜にまで口を出す \"to interfere even in ...\". The expression is clearly negative; as a noun there is 口出し (口出しする, 口出しするな). Related is 口を挟む, which rather means \"to cut in on someone\" in an ongoing conversation, whereas 口を出す refers to other people's decisions and affairs.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"やっと限定品を手に入れた。","romaji":"Yatto genteihin o te ni ireta.","german":"I finally got my hands on the limited-edition item.","furigana":"げんていひん|て|い"},{"japanese":"苦労して、夢のマイホームを手に入れた。","romaji":"Kurō shite, yume no mai hōmu o te ni ireta.","german":"After a lot of hard work, I got my dream home.","furigana":"くろう|ゆめ|て|い"},{"japanese":"その情報はどこで手に入れたんですか。","romaji":"Sono jōhō wa doko de te ni ireta n desu ka.","german":"Where did you get that information?","furigana":"じょうほう|て|い"}],"notes":"手に入れる literally means \"to put into the hand\" and means \"to get, obtain, procure\". It often stresses that something was hard to get: 限定品, チケット, 情報, 夢のマイホームを手に入れる. Unlike 買う, the way does not matter (purchase, gift, prize), and it sounds more everyday than 得る. Transitive with を; the intransitive counterpart is 手に入る \"to fall into one's hands, to be available\" (このチケットはなかなか手に入らない).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"引っ越しに手を貸してくれませんか。","romaji":"Hikkoshi ni te o kashite kuremasen ka.","german":"Could you help me with my move?","furigana":"ひ|こ|て|か"},{"japanese":"重い荷物を持ったおばあさんに手を貸した。","romaji":"Omoi nimotsu o motta obāsan ni te o kashita.","german":"I helped an old woman who was carrying heavy luggage.","furigana":"おも|にもつ|も|て|か"},{"japanese":"悪いことに手を貸すつもりはない。","romaji":"Warui koto ni te o kasu tsumori wa nai.","german":"I have no intention of helping with anything wrong.","furigana":"わる|て|か"}],"notes":"手を貸す literally means \"to lend a hand\" and corresponds to \"to give someone a hand, to help\". The thing one helps with, or the person, takes に: 引っ越しに手を貸す, お年寄りに手を貸す; as a request 手を貸してくれませんか. The counterpart from the point of view of the person seeking help is 手を借りる (猫の手も借りたい). Compared with 手伝う it sounds somewhat more figurative and can also be used negatively: 悪いことに手を貸す \"to be party to something wrong\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女の帰りを首を長くして待っている。","romaji":"Kanojo no kaeri o kubi o nagaku shite matte iru.","german":"I am waiting eagerly for her return.","furigana":"かのじょ|かえ|くび|なが|ま"},{"japanese":"子どもたちはクリスマスを首を長くして待っている。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa Kurisumasu o kubi o nagaku shite matte iru.","german":"The children are eagerly waiting for Christmas.","furigana":"こ|くび|なが|ま"},{"japanese":"お返事を首を長くしてお待ちしております。","romaji":"Ohenji o kubi o nagaku shite omachi shite orimasu.","german":"We are looking forward very much to your reply.","furigana":"へんじ|くび|なが|ま"}],"notes":"首を長くする literally means \"to make one's neck long\", like someone craning to see whether the person they are expecting is coming yet. It practically always appears in the form 首を長くして待つ \"to wait eagerly\"; what one is waiting for is marked by を. In letters and emails, お返事を首を長くしてお待ちしております is a friendly formula. Neutral, warm in tone.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の態度に腹が立った。","romaji":"Kare no taido ni hara ga tatta.","german":"His attitude made me angry.","furigana":"かれ|たいど|はら|た"},{"japanese":"何度言っても分かってくれないので、本当に腹が立つ。","romaji":"Nando itte mo wakatte kurenai node, hontō ni hara ga tatsu.","german":"No matter how many times I tell him, he doesn't understand, which really irritates me.","furigana":"なんどい|わ|ほんとう|はら|た"},{"japanese":"自分の失敗に腹が立って、眠れなかった。","romaji":"Jibun no shippai ni hara ga tatte, nemurenakatta.","german":"I was so angry at my own mistake that I couldn't sleep.","furigana":"じぶん|しっぱい|はら|た|ねむ"}],"notes":"腹が立つ literally means \"the belly stands up\" - in Japanese the belly is regarded as the seat of feelings. It means \"to get angry, to be irritated\" and mostly describes one's own anger; the trigger takes に: 彼の態度に腹が立つ, 腹が立って仕方がない. For other people one rather uses the transitive form 腹を立てる (彼は腹を立てた). Compared with 怒る, which can also mean visibly scolding, 腹が立つ stresses the inner feeling; more colloquial are 頭にくる and むかつく.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いつも弟の肩を持つのはやめてほしい。","romaji":"Itsumo otōto no kata o motsu no wa yamete hoshii.","german":"I wish you would stop always taking my little brother's side.","furigana":"おとうと|かた|も"},{"japanese":"あの先生は成績のいい学生の肩ばかり持つ。","romaji":"Ano sensei wa seiseki no ii gakusei no kata bakari motsu.","german":"That teacher only ever takes the side of students with good grades.","furigana":"せんせい|せいせき|がくせい|かた|も"},{"japanese":"どちらの肩を持つつもりもないが、彼の言うことも分かる。","romaji":"Dochira no kata o motsu tsumori mo nai ga, kare no iu koto mo wakaru.","german":"I don't intend to take either side, but I understand what he is saying, too.","furigana":"かた|も|かれ|い|わ"}],"notes":"肩を持つ literally means \"to hold someone's shoulder\" and means to take someone's side in a dispute: 〜の肩を持つ. It often carries an accusation of partiality (いつもあの人の肩を持つ). More neutral alternatives are 味方する \"to be on someone's side\" or 応援する \"to support\". Colloquial and neutral.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"その景色の美しさに息を呑んだ。","romaji":"Sono keshiki no utsukushisa ni iki o nonda.","german":"I held my breath at the beauty of the scenery.","furigana":"けしき|うつく|いき|の"},{"japanese":"事故の瞬間、観客は一斉に息を呑んだ。","romaji":"Jiko no shunkan, kankyaku wa issei ni iki o nonda.","german":"At the moment of the accident, the spectators all gasped at once.","furigana":"じこ|しゅんかん|かんきゃく|いっせい|いき|の"},{"japanese":"試合は最後まで息を呑む展開が続いた。","romaji":"Shiai wa saigo made iki o nomu tenkai ga tsuzuita.","german":"The game stayed breathtaking until the very end.","furigana":"しあい|さいご|いき|の|てんかい|つづ"}],"notes":"息を呑む literally means \"to swallow one's breath\": from astonishment, fright or suspense, one's breath catches. Typical frames are 〜に息を呑む (美しさに息を呑む), 息を呑むほど美しい and 息を呑む展開／光景. The kanji 呑 is not on the Jōyō list, so newspapers usually write 息をのむ; 息を飲む is also widespread. To be distinguished are 息を止める (deliberately holding one's breath, e.g. underwater) and 息を殺す (breathing quietly so as not to be noticed).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"父は気が短いので、すぐ怒る。","romaji":"Chichi wa ki ga mijikai node, sugu okoru.","german":"My father is short-tempered, so he gets angry quickly.","furigana":"ちち|き|みじか|おこ"},{"japanese":"私は気が短いので、長い行列に並ぶのが苦手だ。","romaji":"Watashi wa ki ga mijikai node, nagai gyōretsu ni narabu no ga nigate da.","german":"I am impatient, so I hate standing in long lines.","furigana":"わたし|き|みじか|なが|ぎょうれつ|なら|にがて"},{"japanese":"気が短い客は、五分待っただけで帰ってしまった。","romaji":"Ki ga mijikai kyaku wa, go-fun matta dake de kaette shimatta.","german":"The impatient customer left after waiting only five minutes.","furigana":"き|みじか|きゃく|ごふんま|かえ"}],"notes":"気が短い literally means \"the temper is short\" and describes someone who is impatient and quickly irritated. The opposite is 気が長い \"patient\". Similar is せっかち \"hasty, impatient\", which, however, stresses haste rather than irritability; 短気 is the corresponding noun or な-adjective. Neutral, often used to describe oneself.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"過去のことは水に流そう。","romaji":"Kako no koto wa mizu ni nagasō.","german":"Let's let bygones be bygones.","furigana":"かこ|みず|なが"},{"japanese":"ちゃんと謝ってくれたので、今回のことは水に流すことにした。","romaji":"Chanto ayamatte kureta node, konkai no koto wa mizu ni nagasu koto ni shita.","german":"Since he apologized properly, I decided to let this matter go.","furigana":"あやま|こんかい|みず|なが"},{"japanese":"あんなにひどいことを言われたら、簡単には水に流せない。","romaji":"Anna ni hidoi koto o iwaretara, kantan ni wa mizu ni nagasenai.","german":"After being told such terrible things, I can't simply forgive and forget.","furigana":"い|かんたん|みず|なが"}],"notes":"水に流す literally means \"to let flow away in the water\" and means to forgive past quarrels or offenses and not speak about them any more. Typical: 過去のことは水に流す, 今回のことは水に流す. In the negative potential form it expresses that one cannot forgive something: 水に流せない. Neutral, common in reconciliation situations.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"自分のことを棚に上げて、人を批判するな。","romaji":"Jibun no koto o tana ni agete, hito o hihan suru na.","german":"Don't criticize others while ignoring your own faults.","furigana":"じぶん|たな|あ|ひと|ひはん"},{"japanese":"彼は自分の遅刻は棚に上げて、部下の遅刻ばかり責める。","romaji":"Kare wa jibun no chikoku wa tana ni agete, buka no chikoku bakari semeru.","german":"He overlooks his own lateness but keeps blaming his subordinates for theirs.","furigana":"かれ|じぶん|ちこく|たな|あ|ぶか|ちこく|せ"},{"japanese":"自分のことは棚に上げて言うけど、もっと早く寝たほうがいいよ。","romaji":"Jibun no koto wa tana ni agete iu kedo, motto hayaku neta hō ga ii yo.","german":"I'm no better myself, but you should go to bed earlier.","furigana":"じぶん|たな|あ|い|はや|ね"}],"notes":"棚に上げる literally means \"to put on the shelf\", that is, to remove from view. It almost always appears in the frame 自分のこと(は)棚に上げて…: one passes over one's own weaknesses while criticizing others. Self-ironically one says 自分のことは棚に上げて言うけど \"I'm no better myself, but...\". The noun 棚上げ(する), on the other hand, usually means \"to shelve a problem or plan\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"道草を食わないで、まっすぐ帰りなさい。","romaji":"Michikusa o kuwanaide, massugu kaerinasai.","german":"Don't dawdle; go straight home.","furigana":"みちくさ|く|かえ"},{"japanese":"学校の帰りに道草を食って、母に叱られた。","romaji":"Gakkō no kaeri ni michikusa o kutte, haha ni shikarareta.","german":"I dawdled on the way home from school and got scolded by my mother.","furigana":"がっこう|かえ|みちくさ|く|はは|しか"},{"japanese":"大学を出てからずいぶん道草を食ったが、ようやく教師になれた。","romaji":"Daigaku o dete kara zuibun michikusa o kutta ga, yōyaku kyōshi ni nareta.","german":"I took many detours after finishing university, but I finally became a teacher.","furigana":"だいがく|で|みちくさ|く|きょうし"}],"notes":"道草を食う literally means \"to eat grass at the roadside\", like a horse that stops on the way and grazes. It means to dawdle or linger along the way, classically on the way home from school; more briefly people also say 道草する. Figuratively it denotes detours in life or career. 食う is used here quite neutrally despite its rough sound; 道草を食べる is not used.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"それは虫がいい話だ。","romaji":"Sore wa mushi ga ii hanashi da.","german":"That is a selfish proposition.","furigana":"むし|はなし"},{"japanese":"何もしないで給料だけ上げてほしいなんて、虫がよすぎるよ。","romaji":"Nani mo shinaide kyūryō dake agete hoshii nante, mushi ga yosugiru yo.","german":"Wanting a raise without doing anything is way too much to ask.","furigana":"なに|きゅうりょう|あ|むし"},{"japanese":"困った時だけ連絡してくるなんて、虫のいい人だ。","romaji":"Komatta toki dake renraku shite kuru nante, mushi no ii hito da.","german":"He only contacts me when he is in trouble; he really is self-serving.","furigana":"こま|とき|れんらく|むし|ひと"}],"notes":"虫がいい describes expectations or demands that brazenly consider only one's own advantage. 虫 stands here for one's inner mood, as in 虫が好かない or 虫の居所が悪い. Typical are 虫のいい話, 虫のいい考え and above all 虫がよすぎる \"that is too brazen\". Unlike わがまま or 自分勝手, which mean generally selfish behavior, 虫がいい specifically criticizes a one-sidedly convenient calculation.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"根も葉もない噂を信じないでください。","romaji":"Ne mo ha mo nai uwasa o shinjinaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't believe completely groundless rumors.","furigana":"ね|は|うわさ|しん"},{"japanese":"週刊誌の記事は根も葉もない作り話だった。","romaji":"Shūkanshi no kiji wa ne mo ha mo nai tsukuribanashi datta.","german":"The weekly magazine's article was a fabricated story.","furigana":"しゅうかんし|きじ|ね|は|つく|ばなし"},{"japanese":"根も葉もないことを言われて、本当に腹が立った。","romaji":"Ne mo ha mo nai koto o iwarete, hontō ni hara ga tatta.","german":"Being accused of totally baseless things really made me angry.","furigana":"ね|は|い|ほんとう|はら|た"}],"notes":"根も葉もない literally means \"to have neither roots nor leaves\": a claim has no basis whatsoever. It almost always appears attributively before 噂, 嘘, 話, 中傷 or こと. It is inflected like an い-adjective (根も葉もなかった). The written equivalent in official denials is 事実無根.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"十年続けてきて、やっと芽が出た。","romaji":"Jū-nen tsuzukete kite, yatto me ga deta.","german":"After keeping at it for ten years, it has finally paid off.","furigana":"じゅうねんつづ|め|で"},{"japanese":"彼はお笑い芸人として、何年も芽が出なかった。","romaji":"Kare wa owarai geinin to shite, nannen mo me ga denakatta.","german":"As a comedian, he did not make his breakthrough for many years.","furigana":"かれ|わら|げいにん|なんねん|め|で"},{"japanese":"春になって、庭にまいた種から芽が出た。","romaji":"Haru ni natte, niwa ni maita tane kara me ga deta.","german":"In spring, sprouts came up from the seeds I had sown in the garden.","furigana":"はる|にわ|たね|め|で"}],"notes":"芽が出る literally means \"a sprout comes out\", that is, \"to sprout\"; it is also used quite normally this way for plants. Figuratively it means that success finally comes after a long time, or that someone makes a breakthrough as an artist, athlete and so on. The negative is particularly common: なかなか芽が出ない \"just not making a breakthrough\". The transitive counterpart of 出る is 出す: 芽を出す.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"石の上にも三年というから、もう少し頑張ろう。","romaji":"Ishi no ue ni mo san nen to iu kara, mō sukoshi ganbarō.","german":"They say perseverance pays off, so let's hang in there a little longer.","furigana":"いし|うえ|さんねん|すこ|がんば"},{"japanese":"すぐ辞めたいと言う新入社員に、部長は「石の上にも三年だよ」と言った。","romaji":"Sugu yametai to iu shinnyū shain ni, buchō wa \"Ishi no ue ni mo san nen da yo\" to itta.","german":"To the new employee who wanted to quit right away, the department head said, \"Patience pays off, you know.\"","furigana":"や|い|しんにゅうしゃいん|ぶちょう|いし|うえ|さんねん|い"},{"japanese":"石の上にも三年で、ピアノの練習がやっと楽しくなってきた。","romaji":"Ishi no ue ni mo san nen de, piano no renshū ga yatto tanoshiku natte kita.","german":"Perseverance pays off: practicing the piano has finally started to be fun.","furigana":"いし|うえ|さんねん|れんしゅう|たの"}],"notes":"石の上にも三年 literally means \"even on a (cold) stone, three years\": if you just sit on it long enough, even a stone becomes warm. It means that patience and perseverance pay off in the end. It is often said to new employees who want to quit quickly; typical frames are 〜と言うから / 〜で. Neutral, somewhat preachy.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"花見に行ったのに、花より団子で食べてばかりだった。","romaji":"Hanami ni itta no ni, hana yori dango de tabete bakari datta.","german":"I went to see the cherry blossoms, but ended up just eating, preferring dumplings to flowers.","furigana":"はなみ|い|はな|だんご|た"},{"japanese":"うちの子は花より団子で、旅行先でも景色より食べ物に夢中だ。","romaji":"Uchi no ko wa hana yori dango de, ryokōsaki demo keshiki yori tabemono ni muchū da.","german":"My child prefers dumplings to flowers: even when traveling, he's more into the food than the scenery.","furigana":"こ|はな|だんご|りょこうさき|けしき|た|もの|むちゅう"},{"japanese":"「花束より、おいしいお菓子のほうがうれしい」「花より団子だね」","romaji":"\"Hanataba yori, oishii okashi no hō ga ureshii.\" \"Hana yori dango da ne.\"","german":"\"I'm happier with tasty sweets than with a bouquet.\" \"Dumplings over flowers, huh?\"","furigana":"はなたば|かし|はな|だんご"}],"notes":"花より団子 literally means \"dumplings rather than blossoms\": at cherry blossom viewing (花見) many people are more interested in eating and drinking than in the blossoms. The proverb describes someone who prefers practical or material benefit (above all food) to beauty. It is mostly said jokingly about oneself or friends: 花より団子で〜, まさに花より団子だ, 私は花より団子タイプ. It stands as a noun with だ or で and is not meant as an insult.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"百聞は一見に如かずだから、実際に見に行こう。","romaji":"Hyakubun wa ikken ni shikazu da kara, jissai ni mi ni ikō.","german":"Seeing is believing, so let's go and see it for real.","furigana":"ひゃくぶん|いっけん|し|じっさい|み|い"},{"japanese":"写真で見るのとは全然違う。百聞は一見に如かずだね。","romaji":"Shashin de miru no to wa zenzen chigau. Hyakubun wa ikken ni shikazu da ne.","german":"It's completely different from seeing it in photos. Seeing is believing, isn't it.","furigana":"しゃしん|み|ぜんぜんちが|ひゃくぶん|いっけん|し"},{"japanese":"百聞は一見に如かずというように、工場を見学して仕組みがよく分かった。","romaji":"Hyakubun wa ikken ni shikazu to iu yō ni, kōjō o kengaku shite shikumi ga yoku wakatta.","german":"As the saying goes, seeing is believing: touring the factory, I understood the process well.","furigana":"ひゃくぶん|いっけん|し|こうじょう|けんがく|しく|わ"}],"notes":"百聞は一見に如かず literally means \"hearing a hundred times does not equal seeing once\"; 如かず is the classical negation of 如く \"to equal\". One says it to encourage someone to see something for themselves, or after having understood something on the spot. Typical frames: 百聞は一見に如かずだ / 〜というように. Slightly elevated but universally known; 如かず is often written in hiragana as well.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"急がば回れで、基礎からしっかり勉強しよう。","romaji":"Isogaba maware de, kiso kara shikkari benkyō shiyō.","german":"More haste, less speed: let's study carefully from the basics.","furigana":"いそ|まわ|きそ|べんきょう"},{"japanese":"急がば回れと言うから、知らない近道より、いつもの道で行こう。","romaji":"Isogaba maware to iu kara, shiranai chikamichi yori, itsumo no michi de ikō.","german":"They say more haste, less speed, so let's take the usual road rather than an unfamiliar shortcut.","furigana":"いそ|まわ|い|し|ちかみち|みち|い"},{"japanese":"急がば回れだよ。焦って書くとミスが増えるから。","romaji":"Isogaba maware da yo. Asette kaku to misu ga fueru kara.","german":"More haste, less speed. If you write in a rush, you'll only make more mistakes.","furigana":"いそ|まわ|あせ|か|ふ"}],"notes":"急がば回れ literally means \"if you are in a hurry, take the detour\": 急がば is a classical conditional form of 急ぐ, and 回れ is the imperative of 回る. It means that the safe, thorough way gets you to the goal faster in the end than a risky shortcut. Typical frames: 急がば回れで… or 急がば回れと言うから…. Neutral and very common.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本にいるなら、郷に入っては郷に従えだよ。","romaji":"Nihon ni iru nara, gō ni itte wa gō ni shitagae da yo.","german":"If you are in Japan, then when in Rome, do as the Romans do.","furigana":"にほん|ごう|い|ごう|したが"},{"japanese":"郷に入っては郷に従えで、新しい会社のやり方に慣れようと思う。","romaji":"Gō ni itte wa gō ni shitagae de, atarashii kaisha no yarikata ni nareyō to omou.","german":"When in Rome: I want to get used to the way things are done at the new company.","furigana":"ごう|い|ごう|したが|あたら|かいしゃ|かた|な|おも"},{"japanese":"郷に入っては郷に従えと言うし、ここでは靴を脱ごう。","romaji":"Gō ni itte wa gō ni shitagae to iu shi, koko de wa kutsu o nugō.","german":"They say you should do as the locals do, so let's take our shoes off here.","furigana":"ごう|い|ごう|したが|い|くつ|ぬ"}],"notes":"郷に入っては郷に従え literally means \"when you enter a village, follow the village\": you should adapt to the customs of the place or group you come into. Watch the readings: 郷 is read ごう here (not きょう), and 入って is いって (classical, not はいって). It applies not only to countries but also to new companies, schools or families. Neutral, very well known.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二兎を追う者は一兎をも得ずだから、一つに集中しよう。","romaji":"Ni to o ou mono wa itto o mo ezu da kara, hitotsu ni shūchū shiyō.","german":"He who chases two hares catches none, so let's focus on one.","furigana":"にと|お|もの|いっと|え|ひと|しゅうちゅう"},{"japanese":"仕事と資格の勉強を同時に頑張ろうとしたが、二兎を追う者は一兎をも得ずで、どちらも中途半端になった。","romaji":"Shigoto to shikaku no benkyō o dōji ni ganbarō to shita ga, ni to o ou mono wa itto o mo ezu de, dochira mo chūto hanpa ni natta.","german":"I tried to give my all to both work and studying for a qualification, but he who chases two hares catches none, and I ended up doing both halfway.","furigana":"しごと|しかく|べんきょう|どうじ|がんば|にと|お|もの|いっと|え|ちゅうとはんぱ"},{"japanese":"二兎を追う者は一兎をも得ずと言うし、部活はどちらか一つに絞ったら？","romaji":"Ni to o ou mono wa itto o mo ezu to iu shi, bukatsu wa dochira ka hitotsu ni shibottara?","german":"They say he who chases two hares catches none; why don't you narrow your club activities down to one?","furigana":"にと|お|もの|いっと|え|い|ぶかつ|ひと|しぼ"}],"notes":"The proverb literally reads \"he who chases two hares (兎) does not obtain even one\"; 一兎をも得ず is a classical negation. It warns against pursuing several goals at once and ending up reaching none. It is quoted with と言う／と言うから or followed by だ／で; abbreviated, people also say 二兎を追う. The counterpart is 一石二鳥 (killing two birds with one stone).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"案ずるより産むが易しだから、まずやってみよう。","romaji":"Anzuru yori umu ga yasushi da kara, mazu yatte miyō.","german":"It's easier done than worried about, so let's try it first.","furigana":"あん|う|やす"},{"japanese":"初めてのスピーチで緊張したが、案ずるより産むが易しで、うまくいった。","romaji":"Hajimete no supīchi de kinchō shita ga, anzuru yori umu ga yasushi de, umaku itta.","german":"I was nervous about my first speech, but it was easier than I feared, and it went well.","furigana":"はじ|きんちょう|あん|う|やす"},{"japanese":"一人暮らしは不安だったけど、案ずるより産むが易しだったよ。","romaji":"Hitorigurashi wa fuan datta kedo, anzuru yori umu ga yasushi datta yo.","german":"I was anxious about living alone, but it turned out to be much easier than expected.","furigana":"ひとりぐ|ふあん|あん|う|やす"}],"notes":"Literally: \"giving birth (産む) is easier (易し, classical form of 易しい) than worrying (案ずる) about it\". The proverb says that something one is afraid of is usually easier in the doing than feared. It is used as encouragement beforehand (案ずるより産むが易しだから、やってみよう) or in retrospect (やってみたら案ずるより産むが易しだった). The related 習うより慣れよ, by contrast, stresses learning by practice.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"三人寄れば文殊の知恵だから、みんなで考えよう。","romaji":"Sannin yoreba Monju no chie da kara, minna de kangaeyō.","german":"Two heads are better than one, so let's think together.","furigana":"さんにんよ|もんじゅ|ちえ|かんが"},{"japanese":"一人で悩まずに相談してよ。三人寄れば文殊の知恵って言うでしょう。","romaji":"Hitori de nayamazu ni sōdan shite yo. Sannin yoreba Monju no chie tte iu deshō.","german":"Don't agonize alone; talk to us. They say two heads are better than one, right?","furigana":"ひとり|なや|そうだん|さんにんよ|もんじゅ|ちえ|い"},{"japanese":"三人寄れば文殊の知恵で、ついに難しいパズルが解けた。","romaji":"Sannin yoreba Monju no chie de, tsui ni muzukashii pazuru ga toketa.","german":"Working together as three, we finally solved the difficult puzzle.","furigana":"さんにんよ|もんじゅ|ちえ|むずか|と"}],"notes":"Literally: \"when three people gather (寄れば), the wisdom of Monju (文殊) arises\"; 文殊 is the bodhisattva of wisdom (Manjushri). It means that even ordinary people can come up with good ideas together. It is used to invite joint thinking or to comment on a team result, often with って言うでしょう. The counterpart is 船頭多くして船山に上る (too many cooks spoil the broth).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一日百円でも、塵も積もれば山となるで、一年で三万円以上になる。","romaji":"Ichinichi hyaku-en demo, chiri mo tsumoreba yama to naru de, ichinen de sanman-en ijō ni naru.","german":"Even 100 yen a day comes to more than 30,000 yen in a year: many a little makes a mickle.","furigana":"いちにちひゃくえん|ちり|つ|やま|いちねん|さんまんえんいじょう"},{"japanese":"一回五分の勉強でも、塵も積もれば山となるというから続けよう。","romaji":"Ikkai go-fun no benkyō demo, chiri mo tsumoreba yama to naru to iu kara tsuzukeyō.","german":"Even if it is only five minutes of study at a time, little by little adds up, so let's keep going.","furigana":"いっかいごふん|べんきょう|ちり|つ|やま|つづ"},{"japanese":"電気のつけっぱなしも、塵も積もれば山となるで、電気代が高くなる。","romaji":"Denki no tsukeppanashi mo, chiri mo tsumoreba yama to naru de, denkidai ga takaku naru.","german":"Even leaving the lights on adds up, and the electricity bill goes up.","furigana":"でんき|ちり|つ|やま|でんきだい|たか"}],"notes":"塵も積もれば山となる literally means \"even dust becomes a mountain when it piles up\": small amounts or efforts add up to something big. It is used positively for saving money or studying, but also as a warning about small wastes. Typical frames: 塵も積もれば山となるで… or 〜と言うから. In daily life often only the first half is quoted, or ちりも is written in hiragana. Neutral.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"猿も木から落ちるというから、油断しないで。","romaji":"Saru mo ki kara ochiru to iu kara, yudan shinaide.","german":"They say even monkeys fall from trees, so don't let your guard down.","furigana":"さる|き|お|ゆだん"},{"japanese":"プロの料理人でも失敗することがある。猿も木から落ちるだね。","romaji":"Puro no ryōrinin demo shippai suru koto ga aru. Saru mo ki kara ochiru da ne.","german":"Even professional chefs sometimes fail. Even monkeys fall from trees.","furigana":"りょうりにん|しっぱい|さる|き|お"},{"japanese":"数学が得意な兄が計算を間違えるなんて、猿も木から落ちるとはこのことだ。","romaji":"Sūgaku ga tokui na ani ga keisan o machigaeru nante, saru mo ki kara ochiru to wa kono koto da.","german":"My brother, who is good at math, made a calculation error - that's exactly what \"even monkeys fall from trees\" means.","furigana":"すうがく|とくい|あに|けいさん|まちが|さる|き|お"}],"notes":"猿も木から落ちる literally means \"even monkeys fall from trees\": even someone who is excellent at something makes a mistake once. It is said when an expert has a mishap, as consolation or as a warning to be careful; typical are 猿も木から落ちるというから〜 and 猿も木から落ちるとはこのことだ. Similar proverbs are 弘法にも筆の誤り and 河童の川流れ. Use it carefully with superiors, since you are indirectly comparing them to a monkey.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お父さんも医者だったんだ。蛙の子は蛙だね。","romaji":"Otōsan mo isha datta n da. Kaeru no ko wa kaeru da ne.","german":"Your father was a doctor too. Like father, like son.","furigana":"とう|いしゃ|かえる|こ|かえる"},{"japanese":"母も歌が上手だったから、蛙の子は蛙だね。","romaji":"Haha mo uta ga jōzu datta kara, kaeru no ko wa kaeru da ne.","german":"Her mother was also good at singing, so like mother, like daughter.","furigana":"はは|うた|じょうず|かえる|こ|かえる"},{"japanese":"蛙の子は蛙で、私も結局父と同じ普通の会社員になった。","romaji":"Kaeru no ko wa kaeru de, watashi mo kekkyoku chichi to onaji futsū no kaishain ni natta.","german":"Like father, like son: in the end I too became an ordinary office worker like my father.","furigana":"かえる|こ|かえる|わたし|けっきょくちち|おな|ふつう|かいしゃいん"}],"notes":"蛙の子は蛙 literally means \"a frog's child is a frog\". Today it mostly means that children inherit their parents' talent or profession: 蛙の子は蛙だね. Originally it was meant more modestly: the children of ordinary parents remain ordinary; it is also used this way in self-deprecation. The counterpart is 鳶が鷹を生む \"a hawk is born of a kite\" (an exceptional child of ordinary parents). Neutral.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"もっと世界を見なさい。井の中の蛙大海を知らずだよ。","romaji":"Motto sekai o minasai. I no naka no kawazu taikai o shirazu da yo.","german":"See more of the world. You're a frog in a well.","furigana":"せかい|み|い|なか|かわずたいかい|し"},{"japanese":"地元では一番だったが、全国大会で井の中の蛙大海を知らずだったと思い知らされた。","romaji":"Jimoto de wa ichiban datta ga, zenkoku taikai de i no naka no kawazu taikai o shirazu datta to omoishirasareta.","german":"I was the best in my hometown, but at the national tournament I was made to realize that I had been a frog in a well.","furigana":"じもと|いちばん|ぜんこくたいかい|い|なか|かわずたいかい|し|おも|し"},{"japanese":"海外で働いてみて、井の中の蛙大海を知らずという言葉の意味がよく分かった。","romaji":"Kaigai de hataraite mite, i no naka no kawazu taikai o shirazu to iu kotoba no imi ga yoku wakatta.","german":"Since I started working abroad, I truly understand what the saying about the frog in the well means.","furigana":"かいがい|はたら|い|なか|かわずたいかい|し|ことば|いみ|わ"}],"notes":"Literally: \"the frog in the well does not know the great ocean\"; 蛙 is read classically as かわず here, and 知らず is a classical negation. The proverb of Chinese origin criticizes someone who knows only his own small world and therefore overestimates himself. It is often shortened to 井の中の蛙, also self-critically (自分は井の中の蛙だった). In Japan there is a later positive continuation: されど空の深さを知る.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"隣の芝生は青いというけど、自分の生活も悪くない。","romaji":"Tonari no shibafu wa aoi to iu kedo, jibun no seikatsu mo warukunai.","german":"They say the grass is always greener on the other side, but my life isn't bad either.","furigana":"となり|しばふ|あお|じぶん|せいかつ|わる"},{"japanese":"友達の会社がよく見えるのは、隣の芝生は青いってことだよ。","romaji":"Tomodachi no kaisha ga yoku mieru no wa, tonari no shibafu wa aoi tte koto da yo.","german":"Your friend's company looks better to you simply because the grass is always greener elsewhere.","furigana":"ともだち|かいしゃ|み|となり|しばふ|あお"},{"japanese":"他人の家庭は幸せそうに見えるが、隣の芝生は青く見えるものだ。","romaji":"Tanin no katei wa shiawase-sō ni mieru ga, tonari no shibafu wa aoku mieru mono da.","german":"Other families look happy, but things always look nicer at other people's places.","furigana":"たにん|かてい|しあわ|み|となり|しばふ|あお|み"}],"notes":"隣の芝生は青い literally means \"the neighbor's lawn is green\" (青い here stands for lush green). It means that what others have always seems better than what one has oneself. The variant 隣の芝生は青く見える is also common; typical frames are 〜というけど and 〜ってことだよ. The proverb is a borrowing from English and sounds modern and neutral.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"財布を落とした上に雨に降られた。泣きっ面に蜂だ。","romaji":"Saifu o otoshita ue ni ame ni furareta. Nakittsura ni hachi da.","german":"Ich habe mein Portemonnaie verloren und wurde dann auch noch vom Regen erwischt. Ein Unglück kommt selten allein.","furigana":"さいふ|お|うえ|あめ|ふ|な|つら|はち"},{"japanese":"残業で終電を逃した上に、タクシーも全然来ない。まさに泣きっ面に蜂だ。","romaji":"Zangyō de shūden o nogashita ue ni, takushī mo zenzen konai. Masa ni nakittsura ni hachi da.","german":"Wegen Überstunden habe ich den letzten Zug verpasst, und jetzt kommt auch noch kein Taxi. Es kommt wirklich alles zusammen.","furigana":"ざんぎょう|しゅうでん|のが|うえ|ぜんぜんこ|な|つら|はち"},{"japanese":"怪我で試合に出られない上に風邪までひくなんて、泣きっ面に蜂だね。","romaji":"Kega de shiai ni derarenai ue ni kaze made hiku nante, nakittsura ni hachi da ne.","german":"Verletzt und kein Spiel – und dann auch noch erkältet? Das ist ja wie verhext.","furigana":"けが|しあい|で|うえ|かぜ|な|つら|はち"}],"notes":"泣きっ面に蜂 heißt wörtlich „eine Biene (sticht) ins weinende Gesicht“: Zu einem Unglück kommt noch ein weiteres hinzu. 泣きっ面 ist die umgangssprachlich verstärkte Form von 泣き面. Typisch ist der Rahmen 〜た上に…、泣きっ面に蜂だ. Bedeutungsgleich, aber gehobener ist 弱り目に祟り目; das deutsche Pendant ist „ein Unglück kommt selten allein“.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子供に高級な時計をあげても猫に小判だ。","romaji":"Kodomo ni kōkyū na tokei o agete mo neko ni koban da.","german":"Giving an expensive watch to a child is like giving a gold coin to a cat.","furigana":"こども|こうきゅう|とけい|ねこ|こばん"},{"japanese":"高いパソコンを買ったのに、メールしか使わないなら猫に小判だ。","romaji":"Takai pasokon o katta noni, mēru shika tsukawanai nara neko ni koban da.","german":"If you bought an expensive computer but only use it for email, it's wasted on you.","furigana":"たか|か|つか|ねこ|こばん"},{"japanese":"その専門書は私には難しすぎて、まさに猫に小判だった。","romaji":"Sono senmonsho wa watashi ni wa muzukashisugite, masa ni neko ni koban datta.","german":"That specialist book was far too difficult for me; it was truly wasted on me.","furigana":"せんもんしょ|わたし|むずか|ねこ|こばん"}],"notes":"猫に小判 literally means \"(giving) a gold coin to a cat\"; 小判 was an oval gold coin of the Edo period. It means that something valuable is completely wasted on someone who cannot appreciate it - like \"pearls before swine\". Typical is the pattern 〜に〜をあげても猫に小判だ. Said about others it can sound derogatory; said self-deprecatingly about oneself it is harmless. A proverb with the same meaning is 豚に真珠.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"何度注意しても馬の耳に念仏だ。","romaji":"Nando chūi shite mo uma no mimi ni nenbutsu da.","german":"No matter how many times I warn him, it's like talking to a brick wall.","furigana":"なんどちゅうい|うま|みみ|ねんぶつ"},{"japanese":"息子に勉強しなさいと言っても、馬の耳に念仏で、ゲームばかりしている。","romaji":"Musuko ni benkyō shinasai to itte mo, uma no mimi ni nenbutsu de, gēmu bakari shite iru.","german":"However often I tell my son to study, it goes in one ear and out the other, and he just plays video games.","furigana":"むすこ|べんきょう|い|うま|みみ|ねんぶつ"},{"japanese":"部長に健康のためにたばこをやめるよう勧めたが、馬の耳に念仏だった。","romaji":"Buchō ni kenkō no tame ni tabako o yameru yō susumeta ga, uma no mimi ni nenbutsu datta.","german":"I advised the department head to quit smoking for his health, but it fell on deaf ears.","furigana":"ぶちょう|けんこう|すす|うま|みみ|ねんぶつ"}],"notes":"Literally \"to recite a prayer (念仏, the Buddhist invocation of Amida) into a horse's ear\": the horse does not understand the pious text, no matter how often it is repeated. It means that advice or admonitions have no effect at all on someone, that is, \"preaching to deaf ears\". Typical frames are 何度言っても馬の耳に念仏だ or 彼に注意しても馬の耳に念仏. The expression is colloquial and critical and is said about third parties; said directly to a superior it would be rude. Synonymous are the rarer variant 馬の耳に風 and the four-character expression 馬耳東風.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本では出る杭は打たれると言われる。","romaji":"Nihon de wa deru kui wa utareru to iwareru.","german":"In Japan, it is said that the nail that sticks out gets hammered down.","furigana":"にほん|で|くい|う|い"},{"japanese":"出る杭は打たれるのが怖くて、会議で意見が言えなかった。","romaji":"Deru kui wa utareru no ga kowakute, kaigi de iken ga ienakatta.","german":"Afraid of standing out and being attacked, I couldn't voice my opinion in the meeting.","furigana":"で|くい|う|こわ|かいぎ|いけん|い"},{"japanese":"新人なのに目立ちすぎると、出る杭は打たれるよ。","romaji":"Shinjin na noni medachisugiru to, deru kui wa utareru yo.","german":"If you stand out too much as a newcomer, you'll be cut down to size.","furigana":"しんじん|めだ|で|くい|う"}],"notes":"出る杭は打たれる literally means \"the stake that sticks out gets hammered in\". It means that anyone who stands out, is too talented or pushes themselves forward too much will be attacked or cut down to size. It is often cited to explain the pressure to conform in groups; there is also the counter-saying 出過ぎた杭は打たれない \"a stake that sticks out far enough is no longer hammered down\". Neutral, often critical and reflective.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"早起きは三文の徳だから、明日から早く起きよう。","romaji":"Hayaoki wa sanmon no toku da kara, ashita kara hayaku okiyō.","german":"The early bird catches the worm, so let's get up early from tomorrow.","furigana":"はやお|さんもん|とく|あした|はや|お"},{"japanese":"早起きしたら、きれいな朝日が見られた。早起きは三文の徳だね。","romaji":"Hayaoki shitara, kirei na asahi ga mirareta. Hayaoki wa sanmon no toku da ne.","german":"Because I got up early, I was able to see a beautiful sunrise. Getting up early really pays off.","furigana":"はやお|あさひ|み|はやお|さんもん|とく"},{"japanese":"祖父の口癖は「早起きは三文の徳」だった。","romaji":"Sofu no kuchiguse wa \"hayaoki wa sanmon no toku\" datta.","german":"My grandfather always used to say, \"The early bird catches the worm.\"","furigana":"そふ|くちぐせ|はやお|さんもん|とく"}],"notes":"早起きは三文の徳 literally means \"rising early brings three mon of profit\"; 三文 was a very small sum of money. Originally, then: getting up early brings a small advantage - today generally \"the early bird catches the worm\". It is said as an admonition or when getting up early has paid off: 早起きは三文の徳だね. It is also written 三文の得. It is mostly quoted unchanged, often with だから or って言うし.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"宝くじが当たるなんて、棚からぼたもちだ。","romaji":"Takarakuji ga ataru nante, tana kara botamochi da.","german":"Winning the lottery is a real windfall.","furigana":"たから|あ|たな"},{"japanese":"先輩が急に辞めたので、棚からぼたもちで僕がリーダーになった。","romaji":"Senpai ga kyū ni yameta node, tana kara botamochi de boku ga rīdā ni natta.","german":"My senior colleague suddenly quit, so I became the leader quite unexpectedly.","furigana":"せんぱい|きゅう|や|たな|ぼく"},{"japanese":"相手チームが棄権して、棚ぼたの決勝進出となった。","romaji":"Aite chīmu ga kiken shite, tanabota no kesshō shinshutsu to natta.","german":"The opposing team withdrew, so we got into the final as a pure windfall.","furigana":"あいて|きけん|たな|けっしょうしんしゅつ"}],"notes":"Literally \"a ぼたもち falls from the shelf (棚)\", that is, a rice cake covered in sweet bean paste falls straight into one's open mouth. It means unexpected good luck for which one did nothing. In colloquial speech the short form 棚ぼた is very common, often used adjectivally: 棚ぼたの勝利, 棚ぼた式に. The expression is casual and slightly jocular; it does not fit hard-earned success, as it stresses precisely the element of chance. A rarer spelling is 棚から牡丹餅.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"車に気をつけてください。","romaji":"Kuruma ni ki o tsukete kudasai.","german":"Please watch out for cars.","furigana":"くるま|き"},{"japanese":"体に気をつけてください。","romaji":"Karada ni ki o tsukete kudasai.","german":"Please take good care of your health.","furigana":"からだ|き"}],"notes":"気をつける means to watch out or to be mindful. With に you name what the attention is directed at. 車に気をつけて is a warning about vehicles; 体に気をつけて can serve as a friendly wish for good health. The expression contains を and the ichidan verb つける, whose request form is 気をつけてください. 気を付ける is the same expression in a different spelling.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 15"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"元気を出して！明日はきっといい日だよ。","romaji":"Genki o dashite! Ashita wa kitto ii hi da yo.","german":"Cheer up! Tomorrow will surely be a good day.","furigana":"げんき|だ|あした|ひ"},{"japanese":"試験に落ちた友達に、元気を出すように言いました。","romaji":"Shiken ni ochita tomodachi ni, genki o dasu yō ni iimashita.","german":"I told a friend who had failed the exam to cheer up.","furigana":"しけん|お|ともだち|げんき|だ|い"}],"notes":"元気を出す encourages someone to regain confidence after a disappointment. The request 元気を出して often corresponds to \"Cheer up\". 元気 stands before を as what one should muster again; 出す becomes 出して or 出しました. The expression thus describes a change, not just the state of being healthy.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"食べすぎて気持ちが悪くなった。","romaji":"Tabesugite kimochi ga waruku natta.","german":"I ate too much and felt sick.","furigana":"た|きも|わる"},{"japanese":"この暗い部屋は、変な音がして気持ちが悪いです。","romaji":"Kono kurai heya wa, hen na oto ga shite kimochi ga warui desu.","german":"This dark room is creepy because of the strange noises.","furigana":"くら|へや|へん|おと|きも|わる"}],"notes":"気持ちが悪い can express physical discomfort or an unpleasant, creepy impression. After eating too much, it usually means feeling nauseous. For a sound or a sight, on the other hand, the expression can mean that one finds it repulsive or creepy. The i-adjective 悪い becomes 悪くなる with なる.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 38"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"勉強している時に邪魔をしないでください。","romaji":"Benkyō shite iru toki ni jama o shinaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't disturb me while I'm studying.","furigana":"べんきょう|とき|じゃま"},{"japanese":"お仕事の邪魔をしてしまって、すみません。","romaji":"Oshigoto no jama o shite shimatte, sumimasen.","german":"I'm sorry for disturbing you at work.","furigana":"しごと|じゃま"},{"japanese":"うちの猫は、私が本を読んでいると、いつも邪魔をしに来ます。","romaji":"Uchi no neko wa, watashi ga hon o yonde iru to, itsumo jama o shi ni kimasu.","german":"Whenever I'm reading a book, our cat always comes to disturb me.","furigana":"ねこ|わたし|ほん|よ|じゃま|き"}],"notes":"邪魔 originally consisted of 邪 \"evil\" and 魔 \"demon\" - in Buddhism, a demon that disturbs practice. 邪魔をする means \"to disturb, to hinder someone\"; the person or activity is marked with の: 勉強の邪魔をする, 人の邪魔をする. Often を is dropped (邪魔する), and お邪魔します is the set polite formula when entering someone else's home. Merely \"being in the way\" without intent is expressed with 邪魔になる or 邪魔だ.","levelNote":"Derived from 邪魔 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日本でたくさんの人にお世話になりました。","romaji":"Nihon de takusan no hito ni o-sewa ni narimashita.","german":"Many people helped me in Japan.","furigana":"にほん|ひと|せわ"},{"japanese":"いつもお世話になっております。","romaji":"Itsumo osewa ni natte orimasu.","german":"Thank you for your continued support.","furigana":"せわ"},{"japanese":"三年間、本当にお世話になりました。","romaji":"Sannenkan, hontō ni osewa ni narimashita.","german":"Thank you so much for everything over these three years.","furigana":"さんねんかん|ほんとう|せわ"}],"notes":"Literally: 世話 \"care, looking after\" and になる \"to come to one\" - you receive the care or help of others and are therefore in their debt. The benefactor is marked with に: 先生にお世話になる; almost always with the polite お. Set formulas are いつもお世話になっております as an opening for business emails and phone calls, and お世話になりました when parting. Actively, \"to take care of someone\" is 世話をする.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"無理をしないで、ゆっくり休んでね。","romaji":"Muri o shinaide, yukkuri yasunde ne.","german":"Don't overdo it, and get plenty of rest.","furigana":"むり|やす"},{"japanese":"熱があるのに無理をして会社に行きました。","romaji":"Netsu ga aru noni muri o shite kaisha ni ikimashita.","german":"Even though I had a fever, I forced myself to go to the office.","furigana":"ねつ|むり|かいしゃ|い"},{"japanese":"お金があまりなかったけど、無理をして高いかばんを買いました。","romaji":"Okane ga amari nakatta kedo, muri o shite takai kaban o kaimashita.","german":"I didn't have much money, but I stretched myself and bought an expensive bag.","furigana":"かね|むり|たか|か"}],"notes":"Literally: 無 \"without\" and 理 \"reason\" - 無理 is the unreasonable, the impossible; 無理をする means \"to overreach, to take on too much\". 無理をしないでね or 無理しないで is a caring phrase toward people who are ill or stressed. With the て-form, 無理をして〜する describes forcing something despite poor conditions. Do not confuse it with 無理だ \"that's impossible\".","levelNote":"Derived from 無理 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子どもたちはゲームに夢中になっている。","romaji":"Kodomotachi wa gēmu ni muchū ni natte iru.","german":"The children are absorbed in their game.","furigana":"こ|むちゅう"},{"japanese":"本に夢中になって、駅で降りるのを忘れました。","romaji":"Hon ni muchū ni natte, eki de oriru no o wasuremashita.","german":"I was so absorbed in my book that I forgot to get off at the station.","furigana":"ほん|むちゅう|えき|お|わす"},{"japanese":"彼は新しい彼女に夢中になっている。","romaji":"Kare wa atarashii kanojo ni muchū ni natte iru.","german":"He is crazy about his new girlfriend.","furigana":"かれ|あたら|かのじょ|むちゅう"}],"notes":"Literally: 夢 \"dream\" and 中 \"in the middle of\" - as in a dream, forgetting everything else. 夢中になる means \"to become absorbed in something, to be captivated or crazy about it\"; the object is marked with に: ゲームに夢中になる, 彼女に夢中だ. With the て-form, 夢中になって〜する describes forgetting other things because of it. The state is expressed with 夢中になっている or 夢中だ; the register is neutral.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女はいつも上司の顔色を窺っている。","romaji":"Kanojo wa itsumo jōshi no kaoiro o ukagatte iru.","german":"She is always watching her boss's expression.","furigana":"かのじょ|じょうし|かおいろ|うかが"},{"japanese":"子供は親の顔色を窺いながら、「ゲームを買ってほしい」と言った。","romaji":"Kodomo wa oya no kaoiro o ukagai nagara, \"gēmu o katte hoshii\" to itta.","german":"The child cautiously watched his parents' faces and said, \"I want a video game.\"","furigana":"こども|おや|かおいろ|うかが|か|い"},{"japanese":"人の顔色ばかり窺っていないで、自分の意見を言いなさい。","romaji":"Hito no kaoiro bakari ukagatte inaide, jibun no iken o iinasai.","german":"Stop always reading other people's faces and say your own opinion.","furigana":"ひと|かおいろ|うかが|じぶん|いけん|い"}],"notes":"顔色を窺う literally means \"to observe the color of the face\": you anxiously watch another person's expression to gauge their mood and not to upset them. Typical are 上司/親の顔色を窺う and 人の顔色ばかり窺う, which criticizes a dependent, conformist attitude. The kanji 窺う is rare; it is mostly written 顔色をうかがう. Not to be confused with 伺う \"to visit, to ask (humble)\". Related is 空気を読む \"to read the room\", which is more neutral.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"年末は目が回るほど忙しい。","romaji":"Nenmatsu wa me ga mawaru hodo isogashii.","german":"At the end of the year I'm so busy my head spins.","furigana":"ねんまつ|め|まわ|いそが"},{"japanese":"コーヒーカップに何回も乗って、目が回った。","romaji":"Kōhī kappu ni nankai mo notte, me ga mawatta.","german":"I rode the teacup ride so many times that I got dizzy.","furigana":"なんかい|の|め|まわ"},{"japanese":"引っ越しと新しい仕事が重なって、目が回る毎日です。","romaji":"Hikkoshi to atarashii shigoto ga kasanatte, me ga mawaru mainichi desu.","german":"Moving and a new job coincided, so my days are a whirl.","furigana":"ひ|こ|あたら|しごと|かさ|め|まわ|まいにち"}],"notes":"目が回る literally means \"the eyes spin\". First, physically, it means \"to feel dizzy\", for example after a merry-go-round; medically more neutral is めまいがする. Second, and very commonly, it describes extreme busyness in an exaggerated way: 目が回るほど忙しい, 目が回る忙しさ, 目が回る毎日. In this meaning it is almost always used attributively or with ほど before 忙しい. It is intransitive and colloquial.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"息子が大学に合格して、鼻が高いです。","romaji":"Musuko ga daigaku ni gōkaku shite, hana ga takai desu.","german":"I'm proud that my son passed the university entrance exam.","furigana":"むすこ|だいがく|ごうかく|はな|たか"},{"japanese":"生徒が全国大会で優勝して、先生も鼻が高い。","romaji":"Seito ga zenkoku taikai de yūshō shite, sensei mo hana ga takai.","german":"The students won the national championship, and their teacher is very proud too.","furigana":"せいと|ぜんこくたいかい|ゆうしょう|せんせい|はな|たか"},{"japanese":"彼は鼻が高くて、目が大きい。","romaji":"Kare wa hana ga takakute, me ga ōkii.","german":"He has a prominent nose and big eyes.","furigana":"かれ|はな|たか|め|おお"}],"notes":"鼻が高い literally means \"the nose is high\" and means \"to be proud\", especially of the achievements of family, students or one's own group: 息子が合格して鼻が高い. It is meant positively and is not the same as showing off: \"to boast\" is 鼻にかける, \"to get conceited\" is 天狗になる. Literally, 鼻が高い can also mean \"to have a prominent, high nose\". It behaves like an い-adjective (鼻が高かった, 鼻が高くて).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"借金の問題で頭を抱えている。","romaji":"Shakkin no mondai de atama o kakaete iru.","german":"I'm at my wits' end over my debt problem.","furigana":"しゃっきん|もんだい|あたま|かか"},{"japanese":"売り上げが下がって、社長は頭を抱えている。","romaji":"Uriage ga sagatte, shachō wa atama o kakaete iru.","german":"Sales have dropped, and the company president is at a loss.","furigana":"う|あ|さ|しゃちょう|あたま|かか"},{"japanese":"試験の問題があまりに難しくて、思わず頭を抱えた。","romaji":"Shiken no mondai ga amari ni muzukashikute, omowazu atama o kakaeta.","german":"The exam questions were so difficult that I involuntarily held my head in my hands.","furigana":"しけん|もんだい|むずか|おも|あたま|かか"}],"notes":"頭を抱える literally means \"to hold one's head (in one's hands)\" - the gesture of despair. It means \"to be at a loss, to rack one's brains over a problem\". The cause takes に or で: 借金の問題で頭を抱える, 難問に頭を抱える; often in the progressive form 頭を抱えている. Similar are 頭が痛い \"something is a headache\" and 悩む \"to worry, to brood\"; 頭を抱える stresses the hopelessness more.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"被災地のニュースを見て胸が痛む。","romaji":"Hisaichi no nyūsu o mite mune ga itamu.","german":"It pains me to see the news from the disaster area.","furigana":"ひさいち|み|むね|いた"},{"japanese":"約束を守れなかったことを思うと、今でも胸が痛む。","romaji":"Yakusoku o mamorenakatta koto o omou to, ima demo mune ga itamu.","german":"When I think about how I couldn't keep my promise, it still hurts to this day.","furigana":"やくそく|まも|おも|いま|むね|いた"},{"japanese":"走った後で急に胸が痛んだので、病院に行った。","romaji":"Hashitta ato de kyū ni mune ga itanda node, byōin ni itta.","german":"I suddenly had chest pain after running, so I went to the hospital.","furigana":"はし|あと|きゅう|むね|いた|びょういん|い"}],"notes":"胸が痛む literally means \"the chest aches\". Physically it can mean real chest pain, but mostly it means \"it hurts in the heart\": pity, guilt or sorrow. Typical patterns are 〜を見て/聞いて胸が痛む and 〜と思うと胸が痛む. The transitive counterpart is 胸を痛める \"to worry (about someone), to be distressed\". The expression is neutral to slightly elevated and also suits news and letters.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"明日の会議のことを考えると気が重い。","romaji":"Ashita no kaigi no koto o kangaeru to ki ga omoi.","german":"I feel heavy-hearted when I think about tomorrow's meeting.","furigana":"あした|かいぎ|かんが|き|おも"},{"japanese":"彼に悪いニュースを伝えなければならないので、気が重い。","romaji":"Kare ni warui nyūsu o tsutaenakereba naranai node, ki ga omoi.","german":"I have to give him bad news, so I'm dreading it.","furigana":"かれ|わる|つた|き|おも"},{"japanese":"連休明けの月曜日は、なんだか気が重い。","romaji":"Renkyū ake no getsuyōbi wa, nan da ka ki ga omoi.","german":"The Monday after a long holiday weekend somehow feels heavy.","furigana":"れんきゅうあ|げつようび|き|おも"}],"notes":"気が重い literally means \"the mind is heavy\" and describes a depressed feeling about something unpleasant that lies ahead. Typical are 〜と思うと/考えると気が重い or 〜ので気が重い; the occasion is often a meeting, bad news or the start of the week. Similar is 気が進まない \"not to feel like it\", which expresses more reluctance, while 憂鬱 means a more general gloom. It behaves like an い-adjective.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"わざわざ足を運んでいただき、ありがとうございます。","romaji":"Wazawaza ashi o hakonde itadaki, arigatō gozaimasu.","german":"Thank you very much for making the trip all the way here.","furigana":"あし|はこ"},{"japanese":"記者は何度も現場に足を運んで話を聞いた。","romaji":"Kisha wa nando mo genba ni ashi o hakonde hanashi o kiita.","german":"The reporter went to the scene many times to listen to people.","furigana":"きしゃ|なんど|げんば|あし|はこ|はなし|き"},{"japanese":"一度、地元の美術館にも足を運んでみてください。","romaji":"Ichido, jimoto no bijutsukan ni mo ashi o hakonde mite kudasai.","german":"Please also stop by the local art museum once.","furigana":"いちど|じもと|びじゅつかん|あし|はこ"}],"notes":"足を運ぶ literally means \"to carry one's feet\" and means \"to (specially) go somewhere, to visit a place\". Unlike 行く or 来る, it stresses the effort and trouble of the visit, which is why it is typical in thank-you phrases: わざわざ足を運んでいただき, ありがとうございます. The destination takes に or へ: 現場に何度も足を運ぶ, 美術館に足を運ぶ. The expression is polite to elevated and common in business language, advertising and reportage.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の嘘に愛想が尽きた。","romaji":"Kare no uso ni aiso ga tsukita.","german":"I'm fed up with his lies.","furigana":"かれ|うそ|あいそ|つ"},{"japanese":"何度注意しても遅刻する部下に、もう愛想が尽きた。","romaji":"Nando chūi shite mo chikoku suru buka ni, mō aiso ga tsukita.","german":"I've completely lost patience with my subordinate who is late no matter how many times I warn him.","furigana":"なんどちゅうい|ちこく|ぶか|あいそ|つ"},{"japanese":"口だけの政治家には、国民も愛想が尽きている。","romaji":"Kuchi dake no seijika ni wa, kokumin mo aiso ga tsukite iru.","german":"The public, too, is fed up with politicians who are all talk.","furigana":"くち|せいじか|こくみん|あいそ|つ"}],"notes":"愛想が尽きる literally means \"the amiability is used up\": you have finally had enough of someone and want nothing more to do with them. The person or their behavior takes に: 彼の嘘に愛想が尽きた, もう愛想が尽きた. The transitive counterpart is 愛想を尽かす (〜に愛想を尽かす, passive 愛想を尽かされる \"someone has had enough of you\"). 愛想 is usually read あいそ here, less often あいそう. The expression is clearly stronger than 嫌になる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は要領がいいから、仕事が早い。","romaji":"Kanojo wa yōryō ga ii kara, shigoto ga hayai.","german":"She knows how to get things done, so she works fast.","furigana":"かのじょ|ようりょう|しごと|はや"},{"japanese":"彼は要領がいいから、面倒な仕事はいつもうまく人に任せている。","romaji":"Kare wa yōryō ga ii kara, mendō na shigoto wa itsumo umaku hito ni makasete iru.","german":"He is shrewd: he always skillfully hands off the troublesome work to others.","furigana":"かれ|ようりょう|めんどう|しごと|ひと|まか"},{"japanese":"私は要領が悪くて、いつも残業になってしまう。","romaji":"Watashi wa yōryō ga warukute, itsumo zangyō ni natte shimau.","german":"I'm not efficient, so I always end up working overtime.","furigana":"わたし|ようりょう|わる|ざんぎょう"}],"notes":"要領がいい literally means \"to grasp the main point (要領) well\" and describes someone who gets things done skillfully and efficiently. Depending on context, criticism can also resonate: \"clever, cunning\" - someone who skillfully avoids unpleasant work or sells themselves well. The opposite is 要領が悪い \"clumsy, inefficient\"; not to be confused with 要領を得ない \"unclear, not to the point\". It behaves like いい: 要領がよくて, 要領がよかった.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"根を詰めて勉強したので、体調を崩した。","romaji":"Kon o tsumete benkyō shita node, taichō o kuzushita.","german":"I studied so intensely that my health suffered.","furigana":"こん|つ|べんきょう|たいちょう|くず"},{"japanese":"あまり根を詰めすぎないで、少し休んでね。","romaji":"Amari kon o tsumesuginaide, sukoshi yasunde ne.","german":"Don't overdo it; take a little rest.","furigana":"こん|つ|すこ|やす"},{"japanese":"根を詰めて作業したので、一日で模型が完成した。","romaji":"Kon o tsumete sagyō shita node, ichinichi de mokei ga kansei shita.","german":"I worked without a break and finished the model in one day.","furigana":"こん|つ|さぎょう|いちにち|もけい|かんせい"}],"notes":"根を詰める: 根 is read こん here and means \"perseverance, energy\" (as in 根気), not ね \"root\". The expression means \"to bury oneself in a task without a break, to wear oneself out\". Typical are 根を詰めて働く/勉強する/作業する and the caring admonition あまり根を詰めないでね \"don't overdo it\". It describes intense, often too intense concentration on a task; neutral to slightly elevated.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"上司と部下の板挟みになって困っている。","romaji":"Jōshi to buka no itabasami ni natte komatte iru.","german":"I'm caught in the middle between my boss and my subordinates.","furigana":"じょうし|ぶか|いたばさ|こま"},{"japanese":"夫は自分の母親と私の板挟みになって、何も言えなかった。","romaji":"Otto wa jibun no hahaoya to watashi no itabasami ni natte, nani mo ienakatta.","german":"My husband was caught between his mother and me and couldn't say anything.","furigana":"おっと|じぶん|ははおや|わたし|いたばさ|なに|い"},{"japanese":"店長は、お客さんと本社の板挟みになることが多い。","romaji":"Tenchō wa, okyakusan to honsha no itabasami ni naru koto ga ōi.","german":"A store manager is often caught between customers and headquarters.","furigana":"てんちょう|きゃく|ほんしゃ|いたばさ|おお"}],"notes":"板挟み originally refers to being squeezed between two boards (板 \"board\", 挟む \"to pinch\"). 板挟みになる therefore means \"to be caught between two fronts\", when you have to satisfy two sides with conflicting expectations. Typical is A と B の板挟みになる, for example between boss and team or spouse and parents; also 板挟みに苦しむ. Similar is ジレンマ, but that rather means a choice between two options, not between people.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"料理コンテストに出場できて、腕が鳴る。","romaji":"Ryōri kontesuto ni shutsujō dekite, ude ga naru.","german":"I'm itching to show what I can do, having entered the cooking contest.","furigana":"りょうり|しゅつじょう|うで|な"},{"japanese":"久しぶりに大きな仕事を任されて、腕が鳴るよ。","romaji":"Hisashiburi ni ōkina shigoto o makasarete, ude ga naru yo.","german":"I've been given a big job for the first time in a long while, and I can't wait to get started.","furigana":"ひさ|おお|しごと|まか|うで|な"},{"japanese":"強い相手と戦えると聞いて、選手たちは腕が鳴っているようだ。","romaji":"Tsuyoi aite to tatakaeru to kiite, senshutachi wa ude ga natte iru yō da.","german":"Hearing that they would be fighting a strong opponent, the players seem to be itching to go.","furigana":"つよ|あいて|たたか|き|せんしゅ|うで|な"}],"notes":"腕が鳴る literally means \"the arm rings/cracks\" and describes the eager anticipation of finally being allowed to show one's skill: one is burning to get started. Usually one speaks this way about oneself before a challenge: 腕が鳴るよ. Unlike 腕を磨く (to improve one's skill), this is about eagerness to act. Neutral to colloquial.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼のピアノの腕前には舌を巻いた。","romaji":"Kare no piano no udemae ni wa shita o maita.","german":"I was speechless with admiration at his piano skills.","furigana":"かれ|うでまえ|した|ま"},{"japanese":"子どもの鋭い質問に、専門家も舌を巻いた。","romaji":"Kodomo no surudoi shitsumon ni, senmonka mo shita o maita.","german":"Even the expert marveled at the child's sharp question.","furigana":"こ|するど|しつもん|せんもんか|した|ま"},{"japanese":"彼女の日本語の上手さには、誰もが舌を巻く。","romaji":"Kanojo no Nihongo no jōzu-sa ni wa, dare mo ga shita o maku.","german":"Everyone marvels at how good her Japanese is.","furigana":"かのじょ|にほんご|じょうず|だれ|した|ま"}],"notes":"舌を巻く literally means \"to roll up one's tongue\" and means to be so amazed at someone's skill or achievement that words fail. The cause is marked with に: 〜の腕前に舌を巻く. Unlike 目を丸くする (general astonishment), it always expresses admiration for an ability. Slightly elevated, common in reports.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"長年美術に触れてきたので目が肥えている。","romaji":"Naganen bijutsu ni furete kita node me ga koete iru.","german":"Having been exposed to art for many years, I have a trained eye.","furigana":"ながねんびじゅつ|ふ|め|こ"},{"japanese":"この店には目の肥えたお客さんが多いので、品質には気を付けている。","romaji":"Kono mise ni wa me no koeta okyakusan ga ōi node, hinshitsu ni wa ki o tsukete iru.","german":"Many customers at this shop have a discerning eye, so we pay attention to quality.","furigana":"みせ|め|こ|きゃく|おお|ひんしつ|き|つ"},{"japanese":"高級品ばかり見ていると目が肥えて、安い物では満足できなくなる。","romaji":"Kōkyūhin bakari mite iru to me ga koete, yasui mono de wa manzoku dekinaku naru.","german":"If you only look at luxury goods, your eye becomes refined and you can no longer be satisfied with cheap things.","furigana":"こうきゅうひん|み|め|こ|やす|もの|まんぞく"}],"notes":"目が肥える literally means \"the eyes grow fat\" and means to gain a trained, demanding eye through much experience with good things, for example for art, fashion or antiques. Mostly used as a state, 目が肥えている, or attributively, 目の肥えた客. For food one says 舌が肥える instead, for music 耳が肥える. Neutral.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"高いけど、背に腹は代えられないから買うしかない。","romaji":"Takai kedo, se ni hara wa kaerarenai kara kau shika nai.","german":"It's expensive, but needs must, so I have no choice but to buy it.","furigana":"たか|せ|はら|か|か"},{"japanese":"背に腹は代えられず、大事にしていたギターを売った。","romaji":"Se ni hara wa kaerarezu, daiji ni shite ita gitā o utta.","german":"Out of necessity, I sold my beloved guitar.","furigana":"せ|はら|か|だいじ|う"},{"japanese":"人が足りないので、背に腹は代えられないと思って休日も出勤した。","romaji":"Hito ga tarinai node, se ni hara wa kaerarenai to omotte kyūjitsu mo shukkin shita.","german":"We were short of staff, so I thought I had no choice and went to work on my day off, too.","furigana":"ひと|た|せ|はら|か|おも|きゅうじつ|しゅっきん"}],"notes":"背に腹は代えられない literally means \"you cannot trade your belly for your back\": to protect the more important thing (the belly with the organs), you must sacrifice the less important. One says it when doing something unpleasant out of necessity because there is no other choice. In written language 背に腹は代えられず also occurs; the spelling 換えられない is also found. Neutral.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"去年の失敗をもう忘れたの？喉元過ぎれば熱さを忘れるだね。","romaji":"Kyonen no shippai o mō wasureta no? Nodomoto sugireba atsusa o wasureru da ne.","german":"Have you already forgotten last year's mistake? Once it's over, you forget how bad it was.","furigana":"きょねん|しっぱい|わす|のどもとす|あつ|わす"},{"japanese":"地震の後はみんな防災用品を買ったのに、喉元過ぎれば熱さを忘れるで、今は誰も気にしていない。","romaji":"Jishin no ato wa minna bōsai yōhin o katta noni, nodomoto sugireba atsusa o wasureru de, ima wa dare mo ki ni shite inai.","german":"After the earthquake everyone bought disaster-preparedness supplies, but once the danger had passed, nobody cares about it now.","furigana":"じしん|あと|ぼうさいようひん|か|のどもとす|あつ|わす|いま|だれ|き"},{"japanese":"病気が治ったとたん、また夜更かしを始めた。喉元過ぎれば熱さを忘れるとはこのことだ。","romaji":"Byōki ga naotta totan, mata yofukashi o hajimeta. Nodomoto sugireba atsusa o wasureru to wa kono koto da.","german":"As soon as he recovered from his illness, he started staying up late again. This is exactly what it means to forget the pain once it's over.","furigana":"びょうき|なお|よふ|はじ|のどもとす|あつ|わす"}],"notes":"喉元過ぎれば熱さを忘れる literally means \"once it has passed the throat, you forget the heat\". It means that one quickly forgets hardship, danger or suffering as soon as it is over, and likewise the help one received back then. It is used critically about people who learn nothing from experience; typical frames are 〜で, 〜とはこのことだ. Neutral.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は気が置けない友人だ。","romaji":"Kanojo wa ki ga okenai yūjin da.","german":"She is a friend I can be completely at ease with.","furigana":"かのじょ|き|お|ゆうじん"},{"japanese":"気が置けない仲間と飲むお酒は最高だ。","romaji":"Ki ga okenai nakama to nomu osake wa saikō da.","german":"A drink with close friends you can be yourself with is the best.","furigana":"き|お|なかま|の|さけ|さいこう"},{"japanese":"兄とは気が置けない仲なので、何でも相談できる。","romaji":"Ani to wa ki ga okenai naka na node, nan demo sōdan dekiru.","german":"I'm so close to my older brother that I can talk to him about anything.","furigana":"あに|き|お|なか|なん|そうだん"}],"notes":"気が置けない means \"so familiar that one does not have to hold back or put on a front\" (from 気を置く \"to be on one's guard, to keep one's distance\"). It is praise for close friends: 気が置けない友人, 気が置けない仲. Note: many Japanese today wrongly understand it as \"he cannot be trusted\", but the correct meaning is positive. Neutral, rather elevated.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"たまには腹を割って話そうよ。","romaji":"Tama ni wa hara o watte hanasō yo.","german":"Let's have a frank talk for once.","furigana":"はら|わ|はな"},{"japanese":"一度、社長と腹を割って話す必要がある。","romaji":"Ichido, shachō to hara o watte hanasu hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"I need to have a frank talk with the president once.","furigana":"いちど|しゃちょう|はら|わ|はな|ひつよう"},{"japanese":"腹を割って話し合った結果、二人は仲直りした。","romaji":"Hara o watte hanashiatta kekka, futari wa nakanaori shita.","german":"After a frank discussion, the two of them made up.","furigana":"はら|わ|はな|あ|けっか|ふたり|なかなお"}],"notes":"腹を割って話す literally means \"to cut open the belly and talk\"; in Japanese the belly is regarded as the seat of one's true intentions. It means an open, honest conversation without ulterior motives, often to clear up misunderstandings. Also common as 腹を割って話し合う \"to talk things out openly\". Neutral, used rather in personal or professional conflict situations.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの問題は難しすぎて、手も足も出なかった。","romaji":"Ano mondai wa muzukashisugite, te mo ashi mo denakatta.","german":"That problem was so difficult that I was completely helpless.","furigana":"もんだい|むずか|て|あし|で"},{"japanese":"相手チームが強すぎて、手も足も出なかった。","romaji":"Aite chīmu ga tsuyosugite, te mo ashi mo denakatta.","german":"The opposing team was so strong that we couldn't do anything.","furigana":"あいて|つよ|て|あし|で"},{"japanese":"資金がなくては、手も足も出ない。","romaji":"Shikin ga nakute wa, te mo ashi mo denai.","german":"Without funds, our hands are tied.","furigana":"しきん|て|あし|で"}],"notes":"手も足も出ない literally means \"neither hands nor feet come out\" and means to be completely powerless against an opponent or a problem. Typical with tasks that are too hard, superior opponents or lack of money. Do not confuse it with 手が出ない \"cannot afford something, cannot get at it\". Almost always in the past: 手も足も出なかった. Neutral.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は家族のために身を粉にして働いている。","romaji":"Kare wa kazoku no tame ni mi o ko ni shite hataraite iru.","german":"He works himself to the bone for his family.","furigana":"かれ|かぞく|み|こ|はたら"},{"japanese":"母は身を粉にして、私たち三人を育ててくれた。","romaji":"Haha wa mi o ko ni shite, watashitachi sannin o sodatete kureta.","german":"My mother devoted herself to raising the three of us.","furigana":"はは|み|こ|わたし|さんにん|そだ"},{"japanese":"社員が身を粉にして働いても、給料はなかなか上がらない。","romaji":"Shain ga mi o ko ni shite hataraite mo, kyūryō wa nakanaka agaranai.","german":"Even if employees work themselves to the bone, their salaries hardly rise.","furigana":"しゃいん|み|こ|はたら|きゅうりょう|あ"}],"notes":"身を粉にする literally means \"to turn one's own body into powder\"; 粉 is read こ here, not こな. It means to sacrifice oneself to the point of exhaustion for someone or something. Almost always in the form 身を粉にして働く, often for parents who slave away for their family. Neutral to slightly elevated.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"手が空いたら手伝ってくれませんか。","romaji":"Te ga aitara tetsudatte kuremasen ka.","german":"Could you help me when you have a moment?","furigana":"て|あ|てつだ"},{"japanese":"今、手が空いている人はいますか。","romaji":"Ima, te ga aite iru hito wa imasu ka.","german":"Is anyone free right now?","furigana":"いま|て|あ|ひと"},{"japanese":"午後になれば手が空くので、その時に資料を見ます。","romaji":"Gogo ni nareba te ga aku node, sono toki ni shiryō o mimasu.","german":"I'll be free in the afternoon, so I will look at the materials then.","furigana":"ごご|て|あ|とき|しりょう|み"}],"notes":"手が空く literally means \"the hands become free\" and means to have no work at the moment and so have time for something else. Typical are 手が空いたら… \"when you have a moment\" and 手が空いている人. The opposite is 手が離せない \"cannot get away right now\"; similar is 暇がある, which, however, means more generally \"to have free time\". 空く is read あく here. Neutral, very common in the workplace.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先生の説明を聞いて目から鱗が落ちた。","romaji":"Sensei no setsumei o kiite me kara uroko ga ochita.","german":"Nach der Erklärung des Lehrers fiel es mir wie Schuppen von den Augen.","furigana":"せんせい|せつめい|き|め|うろこ|お"},{"japanese":"この本を読んで、目から鱗が落ちる思いがした。","romaji":"Kono hon o yonde, me kara uroko ga ochiru omoi ga shita.","german":"Als ich dieses Buch las, gingen mir die Augen auf.","furigana":"ほん|よ|め|うろこ|お|おも"},{"japanese":"ご飯に少し水をかけてから温めるといいと知って、目から鱗が落ちた。","romaji":"Gohan ni sukoshi mizu o kakete kara atatameru to ii to shitte, me kara uroko ga ochita.","german":"Als ich erfuhr, dass man Reis vor dem Aufwärmen mit etwas Wasser beträufeln sollte, war das für mich eine echte Offenbarung.","furigana":"はん|すこ|みず|あたた|し|め|うろこ|お"}],"notes":"目から鱗が落ちる stammt aus der Bibel (Apostelgeschichte 9,18) und entspricht genau dem deutschen „es fällt einem wie Schuppen von den Augen“. Man verwendet es, wenn eine Erklärung oder Erfahrung einem plötzlich etwas klar macht. Oft verkürzt man zu 目から鱗 (目から鱗だった, 目から鱗の話) oder sagt 目から鱗が落ちる思いがした. Das Kanji 鱗 wird häufig in Kana geschrieben: 目からうろこ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の努力家ぶりは、爪の垢を煎じて飲みたいほどだ。","romaji":"Kare no doryokuka-buri wa, tsume no aka o senjite nomitai hodo da.","german":"He is so hardworking that one should take him as an example.","furigana":"かれ|どりょくか|つめ|あか|せん|の"},{"japanese":"少しはお兄ちゃんの爪の垢を煎じて飲みなさい。","romaji":"Sukoshi wa onīchan no tsume no aka o senjite nominasai.","german":"Take an example from your big brother for once!","furigana":"すこ|にい|つめ|あか|せん|の"},{"japanese":"うちの部下たちに、彼の爪の垢を煎じて飲ませたいくらいだ。","romaji":"Uchi no buka-tachi ni, kare no tsume no aka o senjite nomasetai kurai da.","german":"I'd love to hold him up to my staff as a shining example.","furigana":"ぶか|かれ|つめ|あか|せん|の"}],"notes":"Literally: \"to boil the dirt under (someone's) fingernails and drink it\", in the hope of picking up some of that person's virtues. The expression is half joking, half reproachful and almost always appears in the frame 〜の爪の垢を煎じて飲ませたい／飲みなさい: one holds up a role model to someone. For the neutral statement \"to take an example from\" people say 見習う or 手本にする.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの笑顔の裏には腹に一物ありそうだ。","romaji":"Ano egao no ura ni wa hara ni ichimotsu arisō da.","german":"There seem to be ulterior motives behind that smile.","furigana":"えがお|うら|はら|いちもつ"},{"japanese":"彼は腹に一物あるような顔で黙っていた。","romaji":"Kare wa hara ni ichimotsu aru yō na kao de damatte ita.","german":"He was silent, looking like he had something up his sleeve.","furigana":"かれ|はら|いちもつ|かお|だま"},{"japanese":"急に親切になるなんて、何か腹に一物あるに違いない。","romaji":"Kyū ni shinsetsu ni naru nante, nani ka hara ni ichimotsu aru ni chigainai.","german":"He's suddenly being nice; he must have something in mind.","furigana":"きゅう|しんせつ|なに|はら|いちもつ|ちが"}],"notes":"腹に一物 literally means \"one thing in the belly\"; in Japanese the belly is considered the seat of hidden intentions (cf. 腹黒い, 腹を探る). It means secretly having ulterior motives or a plan. It is mostly followed by ある: 腹に一物ある, 腹に一物ありそうだ, 腹に一物ある人; 胸に一物 means the same. 一物 is read いちもつ here.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先輩の助言を肝に銘じて頑張ります。","romaji":"Senpai no jogen o kimo ni meijite ganbarimasu.","german":"I will take my senior's advice to heart and do my best.","furigana":"せんぱい|じょげん|きも|めい|がんば"},{"japanese":"今回の失敗を肝に銘じ、再発防止に努めます。","romaji":"Konkai no shippai o kimo ni meiji, saihatsu bōshi ni tsutomemasu.","german":"We will take this failure to heart and work to prevent a recurrence.","furigana":"こんかい|しっぱい|きも|めい|さいはつぼうし|つと"},{"japanese":"父の「人に迷惑をかけるな」という言葉を今も肝に銘じている。","romaji":"Chichi no \"hito ni meiwaku o kakeru na\" to iu kotoba o ima mo kimo ni meijite iru.","german":"I still keep my father's words, \"Don't cause trouble for others,\" engraved on my heart.","furigana":"ちち|ひと|めいわく|ことば|いま|きも|めい"}],"notes":"肝に銘じる literally means \"to engrave into the liver\"; the liver (肝) was regarded as the seat of the spirit. One firmly imprints a lesson, a piece of advice or a mistake in order to act accordingly in the future: 〜を肝に銘じる. It is very typical in formal apologies and resolutions (肝に銘じます); there is also the older form 肝に銘ずる. Less solemn are 心に刻む or simply 覚えておく.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新プロジェクトのリーダーとして彼に白羽の矢が立った。","romaji":"Shin purojekuto no rīdā to shite kare ni shiraha no ya ga tatta.","german":"He was singled out as the leader of the new project.","furigana":"しん|かれ|しらは|や|た"},{"japanese":"会社は再建の責任者として、若手の田中さんに白羽の矢を立てた。","romaji":"Kaisha wa saiken no sekininsha to shite, wakate no Tanaka-san ni shiraha no ya o tateta.","german":"The company chose the young Mr. Tanaka to head the restructuring.","furigana":"かいしゃ|さいけん|せきにんしゃ|わかて|たなか|しらは|や|た"},{"japanese":"なぜ自分に白羽の矢が立ったのか、今でも分からない。","romaji":"Naze jibun ni shiraha no ya ga tatta no ka, ima demo wakaranai.","german":"I still don't know why I was the one chosen.","furigana":"じぶん|しらは|や|た|いま|わ"}],"notes":"白羽の矢が立つ goes back to a legend: a god demanding a maiden sacrifice shot a white-feathered arrow onto the roof of the chosen house. Originally negative, it now mostly means, positively, \"to be singled out from many\": 〜に白羽の矢が立つ, transitive 〜に白羽の矢を立てる. The form 白羽の矢が当たる is a common mistake. Typical in personnel and sports reports.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの双子は瓜二つで見分けがつかない。","romaji":"Ano futago wa uri futatsu de miwake ga tsukanai.","german":"Those twins are identical and cannot be told apart.","furigana":"ふたご|うりふた|みわ"},{"japanese":"彼女は若い頃の母親と瓜二つだ。","romaji":"Kanojo wa wakai koro no hahaoya to uri futatsu da.","german":"She is the spitting image of her mother when she was young.","furigana":"かのじょ|わか|ころ|ははおや|うりふた"},{"japanese":"街で有名人と瓜二つの人を見かけた。","romaji":"Machi de yūmeijin to uri futatsu no hito o mikaketa.","german":"In town I saw someone who looked just like a celebrity.","furigana":"まち|ゆうめいじん|うりふた|ひと|み"}],"notes":"瓜二つ literally means \"a melon in two halves\": the two cut surfaces look exactly the same. It almost always refers to the outward resemblance of people, especially faces: AとBは瓜二つだ, AはBに瓜二つだ, 瓜二つの顔／人. It is stronger than the more general そっくり, which also fits things, voices or behavior.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"試験に落ちて、彼は青菜に塩のようにしょげている。","romaji":"Shiken ni ochite, kare wa aona ni shio no yō ni shogete iru.","german":"He failed the exam and is down in the dumps.","furigana":"しけん|お|かれ|あおな|しお"},{"japanese":"監督に叱られた選手たちは、青菜に塩でうなだれていた。","romaji":"Kantoku ni shikarareta senshu-tachi wa, aona ni shio de unadarete ita.","german":"The players who were scolded by the coach hung their heads dejectedly.","furigana":"かんとく|しか|せんしゅ|あおな|しお"},{"japanese":"さっきまで元気だった子どもが、おもちゃを取り上げられて青菜に塩だ。","romaji":"Sakki made genki datta kodomo ga, omocha o toriagerarete aona ni shio da.","german":"The child who was full of energy a moment ago is crestfallen now that his toy was taken away.","furigana":"げんき|こ|と|あ|あおな|しお"}],"notes":"青菜に塩 literally means \"salt on green vegetables\": salted leafy greens wilt immediately. It describes someone who is suddenly completely dejected and hangs his head. Typical frames are 青菜に塩のようにしょげる／しょんぼりする or 青菜に塩でうなだれる; also predicatively 青菜に塩だ. The expression is vivid and colloquial.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"引っ越しを考えていたら、ちょうど友人がいい物件を紹介してくれて渡りに船だった。","romaji":"Hikkoshi o kangaete itara, chōdo yūjin ga ii bukken o shōkai shite kurete watari ni fune datta.","german":"I was thinking about moving, and a friend just happened to introduce me to a good place; it was perfect timing.","furigana":"ひ|こ|かんが|ゆうじん|ぶっけん|しょうかい|わた|ふね"},{"japanese":"手伝いを申し出てくれたので、渡りに船とばかりにお願いした。","romaji":"Tetsudai o mōshidete kureta node, watari ni fune to bakari ni onegai shita.","german":"He offered to help, so I took him up on it as a godsend.","furigana":"てつだ|もう|で|わた|ふね|ねが"},{"japanese":"資金に困っていた会社にとって、その提携話は渡りに船だった。","romaji":"Shikin ni komatte ita kaisha ni totte, sono teikei-banashi wa watari ni fune datta.","german":"For the cash-strapped company, the partnership offer was a godsend.","furigana":"しきん|こま|かいしゃ|ていけいばなし|わた|ふね"}],"notes":"渡りに船 literally means \"a boat just when you want to cross\": exactly at the right moment, the help or opportunity one needed presents itself. Typical are 渡りに船だ, 渡りに船の話／申し出 and 渡りに船とばかりに \"seizing the opportunity right away\". Unlike 棚からぼた餅 (unearned luck without any effort of one's own), 渡りに船 stresses that something comes along perfectly timed for an existing need.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼とは初めて会った時から気が合った。","romaji":"Kare to wa hajimete atta toki kara ki ga atta.","german":"I clicked with him from the first time we met.","furigana":"かれ|はじ|あ|とき|き|あ"},{"japanese":"新しいルームメイトとはあまり気が合わない。","romaji":"Atarashii rūmumeito to wa amari ki ga awanai.","german":"I don't really get along with my new roommate.","furigana":"あたら|き|あ"},{"japanese":"気の合う友達と旅行するのは本当に楽しい。","romaji":"Ki no au tomodachi to ryokō suru no wa hontō ni tanoshii.","german":"Traveling with friends you click with is really fun.","furigana":"き|あ|ともだち|りょこう|ほんとう|たの"}],"notes":"Literally \"the spirit (気) fits together (合う)\": two people have a similar nature and understand each other right away. The person is marked with と: 彼とは気が合う; before a noun, が usually becomes の: 気の合う友達. Unlike 仲がいい, which describes an existing good relationship, 気が合う emphasizes the natural match of characters. Negated: 気が合わない \"cannot get along\". Neutral, common in everyday life and at work.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この絵を選ぶなんて、お目が高いですね。","romaji":"Kono e o erabu nante, o-me ga takai desu ne.","german":"Choosing this painting shows you have a good eye.","furigana":"え|えら|め|たか"},{"japanese":"これは今年いちばん人気のワインです。お客様、お目が高いですね。","romaji":"Kore wa kotoshi ichiban ninki no wain desu. O-kyakusama, o-me ga takai desu ne.","german":"This is the most popular wine this year. You have a good eye, sir.","furigana":"ことし|にんき|きゃくさま|め|たか"},{"japanese":"田中さんは目が高いから、プレゼント選びを手伝ってもらった。","romaji":"Tanaka-san wa me ga takai kara, purezento-erabi o tetsudatte moratta.","german":"Tanaka has a good eye, so I asked him to help me pick a present.","furigana":"たなか|め|たか|えら|てつだ"}],"notes":"Literally \"the eye (目) is high (高い)\": someone has fine judgment and recognizes quality. It is almost always praise for others, especially as a polite phrase from sales staff: お客様、お目が高いですね. One does not say it about oneself. Do not confuse it with 鼻が高い \"to be proud\"; similar is 目が肥えている \"to have a refined taste through a lot of experience\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの二人はとても仲がいい。","romaji":"Ano futari wa totemo naka ga ii.","german":"Those two get along very well.","furigana":"ふたり|なか"},{"japanese":"新しいクラスでは、みんなと仲がよくなりました。","romaji":"Atarashii kurasu de wa, minna to naka ga yoku narimashita.","german":"In my new class, I became friends with everyone.","furigana":"あたら|なか"}],"notes":"仲がいい describes a good personal relationship. The people involved can stand together as the topic: あの二人は仲がいい. With と you name the person you get along well with. Before なる, いい becomes よく: 仲がよくなる means that the relationship improves or that people grow closer.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 19"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"困っている人に声をかけた。","romaji":"Komatte iru hito ni koe o kaketa.","german":"I spoke to a person who was in trouble.","furigana":"こま|ひと|こえ"},{"japanese":"今度みんなで飲みに行くときは、私にも声をかけてね。","romaji":"Kondo minna de nomi ni iku toki wa, watashi ni mo koe o kakete ne.","german":"The next time everyone goes out for a drink, let me know too!","furigana":"こんど|の|い|わたし|こえ"},{"japanese":"試合中、みんなで選手に声をかけて応援した。","romaji":"Shiai-chū, minna de senshu ni koe o kakete ōen shita.","german":"During the match, we all called out to the players to cheer them on.","furigana":"しあいちゅう|せんしゅ|こえ|おうえん"}],"notes":"Literally \"to hang a voice (声) on someone\"; the person is marked with に: 〜に声をかける. It means to speak to or greet someone, but also \"to let someone know, to invite\" (今度声をかけてね) and \"to cheer on\". Passive 声をかけられる \"to be spoken to\", for example by strangers. Unlike 話しかける \"to start a conversation\", 声をかける is broader. かける is usually written in kana, less often 掛ける.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"準備もしないで来るなんて、話にならない。","romaji":"Junbi mo shinaide kuru nante, hanashi ni naranai.","german":"Ganz ohne Vorbereitung herzukommen – das ist doch indiskutabel.","furigana":"じゅんび|く|はなし"},{"japanese":"この値段では高すぎて話になりません。","romaji":"Kono nedan de wa takasugite hanashi ni narimasen.","german":"Zu diesem Preis ist das viel zu teuer – darüber brauchen wir gar nicht zu reden.","furigana":"ねだん|たか|はなし"},{"japanese":"プロと比べたら、私のテニスなんて話にならないよ。","romaji":"Puro to kurabetara, watashi no tenisu nante hanashi ni naranai yo.","german":"Im Vergleich zu Profis kann man mein Tennis völlig vergessen.","furigana":"くら|わたし|はなし"}],"notes":"Wörtlich „wird nicht zu einem Gespräch“: Etwas ist so schlecht oder unzureichend, dass man gar nicht ernsthaft darüber reden kann – „indiskutabel, das kann man vergessen“. Achtung: Es entspricht nicht dem deutschen „nicht der Rede wert“ (das wäre eher 大したことない). Typisch als scharfe Kritik oder in Verhandlungen: この値段では話になりません. Auch bei riesigen Unterschieden: プロと比べたら話にならない. Umgangssprachlich und deutlich; höflich 話になりません.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"私の生活に口を出さないで。大きなお世話だよ。","romaji":"Watashi no seikatsu ni kuchi o dasanaide. Ōkina osewa da yo.","german":"Don't interfere in my life. Mind your own business.","furigana":"わたし|せいかつ|くち|だ|おお|せわ"},{"japanese":"親戚に「早く結婚しなさい」と言われたが、大きなお世話だと思った。","romaji":"Shinseki ni \"hayaku kekkon shinasai\" to iwareta ga, ōkina osewa da to omotta.","german":"My relatives told me to get married soon, but I just thought: mind your own business.","furigana":"しんせき|はや|けっこん|い|おお|せわ|おも"},{"japanese":"「髪、切ったほうがいいよ。」「大きなお世話!」","romaji":"\"Kami, kitta hō ga ii yo.\" \"Ōkina osewa!\"","german":"\"You should get a haircut.\" \"Mind your own business!\"","furigana":"かみ|き|おお|せわ"}],"notes":"お世話 means \"care, help\"; 大きな \"big\" turns it ironically into \"too much unrequested care\". It is used to bluntly reject interference or unasked advice: 大きなお世話だ \"It's none of your business!\". 余計なお世話 means the same. Because it is impolite, it is only said to close acquaintances or in anger, never to superiors. The opposite in tone is the thank-you formula お世話になります.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"言葉が違っても、気持ちは通じるものだ。","romaji":"Kotoba ga chigatte mo, kimochi wa tsūjiru mono da.","german":"Even if the languages differ, feelings do get across.","furigana":"ことば|ちが|きも|つう"},{"japanese":"何度も話して、やっと私の気持ちが母に通じました。","romaji":"Nando mo hanashite, yatto watashi no kimochi ga haha ni tsūjimashita.","german":"After talking many times, my feelings finally got through to my mother.","furigana":"なんど|はな|わたし|きも|はは|つう"}],"notes":"気持ちが通じる means that feelings or one's intentions reach the other person and are understood. 気持ち stands as the subject before が; に can name the person who understands. 通じる is used here without を, for \"to be understood\". One can also say that feelings become understandable despite different languages.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"いつも助けてもらって、彼女には頭が上がらない。","romaji":"Itsumo tasukete moratte, kanojo ni wa atama ga agaranai.","german":"She always helps me, so I can never stand up to her.","furigana":"たす|かのじょ|あたま|あ"},{"japanese":"うちの父は母に頭が上がらない。","romaji":"Uchi no chichi wa haha ni atama ga agaranai.","german":"My father can't stand up to my mother.","furigana":"ちち|はは|あたま|あ"},{"japanese":"社長も、会社を作った会長には頭が上がらないらしい。","romaji":"Shachō mo, kaisha o tsukutta kaichō ni wa atama ga agaranai rashii.","german":"Apparently even the company president can't hold his head up before the chairman who founded the company.","furigana":"しゃちょう|かいしゃ|つく|かいちょう|あたま|あ"}],"notes":"頭が上がらない literally means \"unable to raise one's head\": you cannot face someone as an equal because you are indebted to them or inferior to them. The person takes に, often with は: 〜には頭が上がらない. Reasons are gratitude (having received a lot of help), debts or authority; humorously also about spouses: 父は母に頭が上がらない. The form is fixed in the negative and behaves like an い-adjective (頭が上がらなかった).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"散歩中に旧友に会うとは、犬も歩けば棒に当たるだね。","romaji":"Sanpo-chū ni kyūyū ni au to wa, inu mo arukeba bō ni ataru da ne.","german":"Running into an old friend on a walk - a dog that walks will bump into a stick.","furigana":"さんぽちゅう|きゅうゆう|あ|いぬ|ある|ぼう|あ"},{"japanese":"家にいるより外に出てみよう。犬も歩けば棒に当たるって言うし。","romaji":"Ie ni iru yori soto ni dete miyō. Inu mo arukeba bō ni ataru tte iu shi.","german":"Let's go out rather than stay home. They say a dog that walks bumps into a stick.","furigana":"いえ|そと|で|いぬ|ある|ぼう|あ|い"},{"japanese":"余計なことをすると、犬も歩けば棒に当たるで、痛い目にあうよ。","romaji":"Yokei na koto o suru to, inu mo arukeba bō ni ataru de, itai me ni au yo.","german":"If you meddle in things that don't concern you, you'll get a bitter lesson.","furigana":"よけい|いぬ|ある|ぼう|あ|いた|め"}],"notes":"犬も歩けば棒に当たる literally means \"even a dog that walks around bumps into a stick\". Originally (Iroha karuta of the Edo period) it was a warning: if you meddle unnecessarily, you get beaten. Today it is mostly understood positively: if you become active, you unexpectedly run into luck. It is quoted after a surprising event or as encouragement, e.g. with って言うし or だね. Which reading is meant depends on the context.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"つらい時こそ笑おう。笑う門には福来たるだよ。","romaji":"Tsurai toki koso waraō. Warau kado ni wa fuku kitaru da yo.","german":"Especially in hard times, let's laugh. Fortune comes to the house where there is laughter.","furigana":"とき|わら|わら|かど|ふくき"},{"japanese":"店の壁に「笑う門には福来たる」と書かれた紙が貼ってある。","romaji":"Mise no kabe ni \"warau kado ni wa fuku kitaru\" to kakareta kami ga hatte aru.","german":"A paper with the words \"Fortune comes to those who laugh\" is stuck on the wall of the shop.","furigana":"みせ|かべ|わら|かど|ふくき|か|かみ|は"},{"japanese":"いつも笑顔の家族を見ると、笑う門には福来たるだなと思う。","romaji":"Itsumo egao no kazoku o miru to, warau kado ni wa fuku kitaru da na to omou.","german":"Seeing the always smiling family, I think: fortune comes to the house where there is laughter.","furigana":"えがお|かぞく|み|わら|かど|ふくき|おも"}],"notes":"笑う門には福来たる literally means \"fortune comes to the gate where there is laughter\"; 門 is read かど here and stands for house and family, and 来たる is a classical form of 来る. It means: whoever stays cheerful attracts good fortune. It is said encouragingly in hard times or as a life motto; it also appears on New Year cards and good-luck charms. The spelling 笑う門には福来る is also common. It is quoted unchanged, often with だよ or って言うでしょ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"またすぐ壊れた。安物買いの銭失いだ。","romaji":"Mata sugu kowareta. Yasumono-gai no zeni-ushinai da.","german":"It broke again right away. Buy cheap, lose money.","furigana":"こわ|やすものが|ぜにうしな"},{"japanese":"安物買いの銭失いにならないように、少し高くてもいい物を選ぼう。","romaji":"Yasumono-gai no zeni-ushinai ni naranai yō ni, sukoshi takakute mo ii mono o erabō.","german":"So that we don't end up penny wise and pound foolish, let's choose something good even if it costs a little more.","furigana":"やすものが|ぜにうしな|すこ|たか|もの|えら"},{"japanese":"母は「安物買いの銭失い」が口癖で、いつも品質を確かめてから買う。","romaji":"Haha wa \"yasumono-gai no zeni-ushinai\" ga kuchiguse de, itsumo hinshitsu o tashikamete kara kau.","german":"\"Buying cheap means losing money\" is my mother's catchphrase, and she always checks the quality before buying.","furigana":"はは|やすものが|ぜにうしな|くちぐせ|ひんしつ|たし|か"}],"notes":"安物買いの銭失い literally means \"buying cheap goods, losing money\"; 銭 is an old word for money. It means: if you skimp on quality, you soon have to buy again and end up paying more - \"buy cheap, buy twice\". It is said about oneself after a bad purchase or as advice before shopping: 安物買いの銭失いにならないように. It is quoted unchanged and used like a noun with だ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お金がなくても大丈夫。金は天下の回り物だよ。","romaji":"Okane ga nakute mo daijōbu. Kane wa tenka no mawarimono da yo.","german":"It's fine even if you have no money. Money comes and goes.","furigana":"かね|だいじょうぶ|かね|てんか|まわ|もの"},{"japanese":"「金は天下の回り物」と言って、父はすぐにお金を使ってしまう。","romaji":"\"Kane wa tenka no mawarimono\" to itte, chichi wa sugu ni okane o tsukatte shimau.","german":"Using the saying \"money circulates in the world\", my father always spends his money right away.","furigana":"かね|てんか|まわ|もの|い|ちち|かね|つか"},{"japanese":"観光客がお金を使えば、町の人にもお金が回る。まさに金は天下の回り物だ。","romaji":"Kankōkyaku ga okane o tsukaeba, machi no hito ni mo okane ga mawaru. Masa ni kane wa tenka no mawarimono da.","german":"If tourists spend money, it reaches the local people too. Money really does circulate.","furigana":"かんこうきゃく|かね|つか|まち|ひと|かね|まわ|かね|てんか|まわ|もの"}],"notes":"金は天下の回り物 literally means \"money is something that goes around the world\". It means: money does not stay with one person but constantly changes hands - if you have none now, you will get some again sometime. It is said consolingly to someone short of money or half jokingly as a justification for spending. The reading is かね, not きん; 回り物 means \"something that circulates\". Quoted unchanged, mostly with だ/だよ/だから.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"二人とも本好きなんだね。類は友を呼ぶだ。","romaji":"Futari tomo hon-zuki nan da ne. Rui wa tomo o yobu da.","german":"You both like books, don't you. Birds of a feather flock together.","furigana":"ふたり|ほんず|るい|とも|よ"},{"japanese":"彼の友達はみんな遅刻ばかりする。類は友を呼ぶというやつだ。","romaji":"Kare no tomodachi wa minna chikoku bakari suru. Rui wa tomo o yobu to iu yatsu da.","german":"All of his friends are always late. That's birds of a feather for you.","furigana":"かれ|ともだち|ちこく|るい|とも|よ"},{"japanese":"趣味のサークルに入ったら、似た性格の人ばかりで、類は友を呼ぶと実感した。","romaji":"Shumi no sākuru ni haittara, nita seikaku no hito bakari de, rui wa tomo o yobu to jikkan shita.","german":"When I joined the hobby club, everyone had a similar personality, and I realized that birds of a feather flock together.","furigana":"しゅみ|はい|に|せいかく|ひと|るい|とも|よ|じっかん"}],"notes":"類は友を呼ぶ literally means \"like calls friends\" and corresponds to \"birds of a feather flock together\". It is said when people with similar interests or traits come together - neutral or slightly ironic, especially with bad habits. Common patterns: 類は友を呼ぶだね, 類は友を呼ぶというやつだ, 類は友を呼ぶと実感する. Colloquially there is the short form 類友.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの教授はとても謙虚だ。「実るほど頭を垂れる稲穂かな」だね。","romaji":"Ano kyōju wa totemo kenkyo da. \"Minoru hodo atama o tareru inaho kana\" da ne.","german":"That professor is very humble. \"The riper the ear of rice, the lower it bows,\" as they say.","furigana":"きょうじゅ|けんきょ|みの|あたま|た|いなほ"},{"japanese":"社長になっても誰にでも丁寧な彼を見ると、「実るほど頭を垂れる稲穂かな」という言葉を思い出す。","romaji":"Shachō ni natte mo dare ni demo teinei na kare o miru to, \"Minoru hodo atama o tareru inaho kana\" to iu kotoba o omoidasu.","german":"Seeing him treat everyone politely even after becoming company president, I remember the saying \"the riper the ear of rice, the lower it bows.\"","furigana":"しゃちょう|だれ|ていねい|かれ|み|みの|あたま|た|いなほ|ことば|おも|だ"},{"japanese":"祖父の口癖は「実るほど頭を垂れる稲穂かな」だった。","romaji":"Sofu no kuchiguse wa \"Minoru hodo atama o tareru inaho kana\" datta.","german":"My grandfather's favorite saying was \"The riper the ear of rice, the lower it bows.\"","furigana":"そふ|くちぐせ|みの|あたま|た|いなほ"}],"notes":"Literally: \"The more it bears, the lower the ear of rice bows its head.\" 実る means \"to ripen, to bear fruit\", 頭を垂れる \"to lower one's head\", and かな is a classical exclamatory particle as in haiku. It means: those who are truly educated or important remain humble. The saying is quoted in praise of a humble person or as a reminder to the successful; the variant 実るほど頭の下がる稲穂かな is also common.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"余計なことを言って怒らせてしまった。口は災いの元だ。","romaji":"Yokei na koto o itte okorasete shimatta. Kuchi wa wazawai no moto da.","german":"I said something unnecessary and made him angry. The mouth is the source of misfortune.","furigana":"よけい|い|おこ|くち|わざわ|もと"},{"japanese":"SNSに書き込む前によく考えて。口は災いの元だよ。","romaji":"SNS ni kakikomu mae ni yoku kangaete. Kuchi wa wazawai no moto da yo.","german":"Think carefully before posting on social media. The mouth is the source of misfortune.","furigana":"か|こ|まえ|かんが|くち|わざわ|もと"},{"japanese":"口は災いの元というから、上司の悪口は言わないようにしている。","romaji":"Kuchi wa wazawai no moto to iu kara, jōshi no waruguchi wa iwanai yō ni shite iru.","german":"Since they say the mouth is the source of misfortune, I make a point of not badmouthing my boss.","furigana":"くち|わざわ|もと|じょうし|わるぐち|い"}],"notes":"口は災いの元 literally means \"the mouth is the origin of misfortune\": careless words get you into trouble. It is said after a verbal slip (about oneself or others) or as a warning to be careful; typical: 〜。口は災いの元だ, 口は災いの元というから〜. An older variant is 口は禍の門. In content it corresponds to \"silence is golden\", but stresses the harm more.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"大喧嘩の後、二人の関係はもっと強くなった。雨降って地固まるだ。","romaji":"Ōgenka no ato, futari no kankei wa motto tsuyoku natta. Ame futte ji katamaru da.","german":"After the big fight, their relationship became even stronger. After the rain, the ground hardens.","furigana":"|あと|ふたり|かんけい|つよ|あめふ|じかた"},{"japanese":"試合に負けて言い合いになったが、それからチームはまとまった。雨降って地固まるだね。","romaji":"Shiai ni makete iiai ni natta ga, sore kara chīmu wa matomatta. Ame futte ji katamaru da ne.","german":"We lost the game and argued, but since then the team has come together. After the rain, the ground hardens.","furigana":"しあい|ま|い|あ|あめふ|じかた"},{"japanese":"親子で本音をぶつけ合ってから、前より話せるようになった。雨降って地固まるとはこのことだ。","romaji":"Oyako de honne o butsukeatte kara, mae yori hanaseru yō ni natta. Ame futte ji katamaru to wa kono koto da.","german":"Since my parent and I have said what we really think to each other, we can talk better than before. That is exactly what this saying means.","furigana":"おやこ|ほんね|あ|まえ|はな|あめふ|じかた"}],"notes":"雨降って地固まる literally means \"it rains, and the ground hardens\" (地 is read じ here). It means: after a quarrel or crisis, a relationship or group becomes stronger than before. It is said in retrospect after a reconciliation, often with だ/だね or 〜とはこのことだ. It is a popular saying in wedding speeches and in team conflicts. The verbs stand in a classically terse form and are not changed.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"無駄な時間を過ごさないで。時は金なりだよ。","romaji":"Muda na jikan o sugosanaide. Toki wa kane nari da yo.","german":"Don't waste time. Time is money.","furigana":"むだ|じかん|す|とき|かね"},{"japanese":"時は金なりだから、通勤時間に英語を勉強している。","romaji":"Toki wa kane nari da kara, tsūkin jikan ni eigo o benkyō shite iru.","german":"Time is money, so I study English during my commute.","furigana":"とき|かね|つうきんじかん|えいご|べんきょう"},{"japanese":"社長は「時は金なり」と言って、会議を三十分以内に終わらせる。","romaji":"Shachō wa \"toki wa kane nari\" to itte, kaigi o sanjuppun inai ni owaraseru.","german":"The company president says \"time is money\" and always ends meetings within thirty minutes.","furigana":"しゃちょう|とき|かね|い|かいぎ|さんじゅっぷんいない|お"}],"notes":"時は金なり is the Japanese translation of \"Time is money\" (Benjamin Franklin); なり is the classical copula instead of だ. It means: time is precious and should not be wasted. It is quoted as an admonition or to justify efficient behavior, often with だから or と言って. In everyday life it is used unchanged, e.g. 時は金なりだよ; the reading of 金 is かね.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は普段おとなしいのに、実はすごい才能がある。能ある鷹は爪を隠すだ。","romaji":"Kanojo wa fudan otonashii noni, jitsu wa sugoi sainō ga aru. Nō aru taka wa tsume o kakusu da.","german":"Sie ist normalerweise ruhig, hat aber tatsächlich großes Talent. Stille Wasser sind tief.","furigana":"かのじょ|ふだん|じつ|さいのう|のう|たか|つめ|かく"},{"japanese":"能ある鷹は爪を隠すというから、自分の成績をあまり自慢しないほうがいい。","romaji":"Nō aru taka wa tsume o kakusu to iu kara, jibun no seiseki o amari jiman shinai hō ga ii.","german":"Wer wirklich etwas kann, muss es nicht zeigen – prahl also besser nicht mit deinen Noten.","furigana":"のう|たか|つめ|かく|じぶん|せいせき|じまん"},{"japanese":"新人の田中さんは、トラブルの時に誰よりも早く問題を解決した。能ある鷹は爪を隠すとはこのことだ。","romaji":"Shinjin no Tanaka-san wa, toraburu no toki ni dare yori mo hayaku mondai o kaiketsu shita. Nō aru taka wa tsume o kakusu to wa kono koto da.","german":"Der Neue, Herr Tanaka, hat das Problem schneller gelöst als alle anderen. Wer etwas kann, braucht es nicht vorzuführen.","furigana":"しんじん|たなか|とき|だれ|はや|もんだい|かいけつ|のう|たか|つめ|かく"}],"notes":"能ある鷹は爪を隠す heißt wörtlich „der fähige Falke verbirgt seine Krallen“ (能 „Fähigkeit“, 鷹 „Habicht, Falke“, 爪 „Kralle“). Gemeint ist: Wirklich Fähige stellen ihr Können nicht zur Schau. Man sagt es anerkennend über jemanden, der überraschend Talent zeigt, oder als Rat, nicht anzugeben. Typisch: 能ある鷹は爪を隠すだね, 〜とはこのことだ, 〜というから. Das deutsche „stille Wasser sind tief“ trifft es nur teilweise, da es eher um verborgene Fähigkeiten als um verborgenen Charakter geht.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"やる気がある今こそ始めよう。鉄は熱いうちに打てだ。","romaji":"Yaruki ga aru ima koso hajimeyō. Tetsu wa atsui uchi ni ute da.","german":"Jetzt, wo die Motivation da ist, sollten wir anfangen. Man muss das Eisen schmieden, solange es heiß ist.","furigana":"き|いま|はじ|てつ|あつ|う"},{"japanese":"子供の教育は鉄は熱いうちに打てで、小さい頃から始めたほうがいい。","romaji":"Kodomo no kyōiku wa tetsu wa atsui uchi ni ute de, chiisai koro kara hajimeta hō ga ii.","german":"Bei der Erziehung gilt: Was Hänschen nicht lernt … – man fängt besser früh damit an.","furigana":"こども|きょういく|てつ|あつ|う|ちい|ころ|はじ"},{"japanese":"お客さんが興味を持っているうちに話を進めよう。鉄は熱いうちに打てだ。","romaji":"Okyakusan ga kyōmi o motte iru uchi ni hanashi o susumeyō. Tetsu wa atsui uchi ni ute da.","german":"Lass uns die Sache vorantreiben, solange der Kunde interessiert ist. Man muss das Eisen schmieden, solange es heiß ist.","furigana":"きゃく|きょうみ|も|はなし|すす|てつ|あつ|う"}],"notes":"鉄は熱いうちに打て heißt wörtlich „schmiede das Eisen, solange es heiß ist“; 打て ist der Imperativ von 打つ. Es hat zwei Bedeutungen: „die günstige Gelegenheit sofort nutzen“ und – im Bildungskontext – „Menschen formen, solange sie jung und formbar sind“. Man zitiert es unverändert, oft mit だ/だよ nach einem Vorschlag oder in der Form 〜は鉄は熱いうちに打てで. Bedeutungsgleich mit dem deutschen Sprichwort.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あんな失敗をして、会社の顔に泥を塗った。","romaji":"Anna shippai o shite, kaisha no kao ni doro o nutta.","german":"With a failure like that, he brought shame on the company.","furigana":"しっぱい|かいしゃ|かお|どろ|ぬ"},{"japanese":"親の顔に泥を塗るようなことはするな。","romaji":"Oya no kao ni doro o nuru yō na koto wa suru na.","german":"Don't do anything that would bring shame on your parents.","furigana":"おや|かお|どろ|ぬ"},{"japanese":"推薦してくださった先生の顔に泥を塗ってしまい、申し訳ありません。","romaji":"Suisen shite kudasatta sensei no kao ni doro o nutte shimai, mōshiwake arimasen.","german":"I am sorry for having brought shame on the teacher who recommended me.","furigana":"すいせん|せんせい|かお|どろ|ぬ|もう|わけ"}],"notes":"顔に泥を塗る literally means \"to smear mud on someone's face\" and means to embarrass someone through one's own misconduct or to damage their reputation, typically parents, teachers, superiors or one's own company: Xの顔に泥を塗る. The expression is serious and is often used in admonitions or apologies. Related: 顔をつぶす \"to make someone lose face\". Neutral to elevated.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"余計なことを聞いたら薮蛇になるよ。","romaji":"Yokei na koto o kiitara yabuhebi ni naru yo.","german":"If you ask unnecessary questions, you'll stir up trouble.","furigana":"よけい|き|やぶへび"},{"japanese":"値下げを頼んだら、逆に手数料を取られて薮蛇だった。","romaji":"Nesage o tanondara, gyaku ni tesūryō o torarete yabuhebi datta.","german":"I asked for a discount and was charged a fee instead: a case of asking for trouble.","furigana":"ねさ|たの|ぎゃく|てすうりょう|と|やぶへび"},{"japanese":"下手に言い訳すると薮蛇になるから、素直に謝ったほうがいい。","romaji":"Heta ni iiwake suru to yabuhebi ni naru kara, sunao ni ayamatta hō ga ii.","german":"Clumsy excuses will only backfire, so you'd better apologize honestly.","furigana":"へた|い|わけ|やぶへび|すなお|あやま"}],"notes":"薮蛇 is the short form of 薮をつついて蛇を出す \"to poke around in the bushes and flush out a snake\". It refers to trouble that one brings on oneself through unnecessary action, just like \"to let sleeping dogs lie\" gone wrong. Typical are 薮蛇になる and 薮蛇だ. It is often written in hiragana (やぶへび); the standard spelling is 藪蛇, and 薮 is a simplified variant. Colloquial.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"最初は赤字でも、将来のために投資しよう。損して得取れだ。","romaji":"Saisho wa akaji demo, shōrai no tame ni tōshi shiyō. Son shite toku tore da.","german":"Even if we run a deficit at first, let's invest in the future. You have to lose to gain.","furigana":"さいしょ|あかじ|しょうらい|とうし|そん|とくと"},{"japanese":"損して得取れで、開店初日はコーヒーを無料にした。","romaji":"Son shite toku tore de, kaiten shonichi wa kōhī o muryō ni shita.","german":"In the spirit of \"lose a little, gain a lot\", we gave out free coffee on the opening day.","furigana":"そん|とくと|かいてんしょにち|むりょう"},{"japanese":"友達との関係でも、損して得取れで、少し譲ることが大切だ。","romaji":"Tomodachi to no kankei demo, son shite toku tore de, sukoshi yuzuru koto ga taisetsu da.","german":"Even among friends, it pays to give way a little, as you gain in the end.","furigana":"ともだち|かんけい|そん|とくと|すこ|ゆず|たいせつ"}],"notes":"損して得取れ literally means \"take a loss and take a gain\": whoever accepts a small loss at first obtains a bigger advantage later. It is a classic merchants' saying, for example for introductory discounts or free samples, but it is also applied to personal relationships. Typical frames: 損して得取れだ / 損して得取れで. Neutral.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あのシェフでも料理を焦がすことがあるんだ。弘法にも筆の誤りだね。","romaji":"Ano shefu demo ryōri o kogasu koto ga arun da. Kōbō ni mo fude no ayamari da ne.","german":"Even that chef sometimes burns the food. Even the best make mistakes.","furigana":"りょうり|こ|こうぼう|ふで|あやま"},{"japanese":"ベテランの先生が計算を間違えるなんて、弘法にも筆の誤りですね。","romaji":"Beteran no sensei ga keisan o machigaeru nante, kōbō ni mo fude no ayamari desu ne.","german":"For such an experienced teacher to get a calculation wrong, even masters slip up, don't they?","furigana":"せんせい|けいさん|まちが|こうぼう|ふで|あやま"},{"japanese":"有名な作家の新刊に誤字があったが、弘法にも筆の誤りというものだろう。","romaji":"Yūmei na sakka no shinkan ni goji ga atta ga, kōbō ni mo fude no ayamari to iu mono darō.","german":"There was a typo in the famous author's new book, but even the best slip up now and then, I suppose.","furigana":"ゆうめい|さっか|しんかん|ごじ|こうぼう|ふで|あやま"}],"notes":"Literally \"even 弘法 (弘法大師, the monk 空海) makes a slip of the brush\": even Japan's most famous calligrapher made mistakes. It means that even a master of a field can get something wrong. It is used leniently about experts whose blunder is surprising, never about oneself, since that would declare oneself a master. Often also without に as 弘法も筆の誤り. Similar is 猿も木から落ちる (\"even monkeys fall from trees\"), which sounds a little more casual.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"困っていたら、知らない人が助けてくれた。渡る世間に鬼はなしだ。","romaji":"Komatte itara, shiranai hito ga tasukete kureta. Wataru seken ni oni wa nashi da.","german":"When I was in trouble, a stranger helped me. There are kind people everywhere.","furigana":"こま|し|ひと|たす|わた|せけん|おに"},{"japanese":"財布を落としたが、交番に届けられていた。渡る世間に鬼はなしだね。","romaji":"Saifu o otoshita ga, kōban ni todokerarete ita. Wataru seken ni oni wa nashi da ne.","german":"I lost my wallet, but someone had turned it in to the police box. There really are good people everywhere.","furigana":"さいふ|お|こうばん|とど|わた|せけん|おに"},{"japanese":"渡る世間に鬼はなしというように、旅先で多くの人に親切にしてもらった。","romaji":"Wataru seken ni oni wa nashi to iu yō ni, tabisaki de ōku no hito ni shinsetsu ni shite moratta.","german":"As the proverb says, there are kind people everywhere: many people were very kind to me on my trip.","furigana":"わた|せけん|おに|たびさき|おお|ひと|しんせつ"}],"notes":"渡る世間に鬼はなし literally means \"in the world one passes through, there are no demons\": even among strangers one finds helpful, kind people everywhere. One usually says it after unexpectedly receiving help. なし is the classical form of ない; JMdict lists the saying under 渡る世間に鬼はない. Also well known is the TV series 渡る世間は鬼ばかり, which ironically reverses the proverb. Neutral.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"結果を焦らないで。果報は寝て待てだよ。","romaji":"Kekka o aseranaide. Kahō wa nete mate da yo.","german":"Don't be impatient about the result. Good things come to those who wait.","furigana":"けっか|あせ|かほう|ね|ま"},{"japanese":"面接はもう終わったんだから、果報は寝て待てと言うし、今日はゆっくり休もう。","romaji":"Mensetsu wa mō owatta n da kara, kahō wa nete mate to iu shi, kyō wa yukkuri yasumō.","german":"The interview is over now. Let's just wait and see: let's get some rest today.","furigana":"めんせつ|お|かほう|ね|ま|い|きょう|やす"},{"japanese":"株価が上がるのを毎日チェックしても仕方がない。果報は寝て待てだ。","romaji":"Kabuka ga agaru no o mainichi chekku shite mo shikata ga nai. Kahō wa nete mate da.","german":"There's no point checking every day whether the stock price is rising. Good things come to those who wait.","furigana":"かぶか|あ|まいにち|しかた|かほう|ね|ま"}],"notes":"Literally \"wait for good fortune while sleeping\": 果報 is \"good fortune, a good fate\", and 寝て待て is the imperative \"sleep and wait\". It means that someone who has done what they could should calmly await the result instead of driving themselves crazy; it is not a call to laziness. Typical as encouragement after exams, job applications or negotiations: 果報は寝て待てだよ／果報は寝て待てと言うし. Colloquial-neutral; ironically also said about someone who does nothing at all and still hopes for luck.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人に親切にしていたら、いつか自分にも返ってくる。情けは人のためならずだ。","romaji":"Hito ni shinsetsu ni shite itara, itsuka jibun ni mo kaette kuru. Nasake wa hito no tame narazu da.","german":"If you are kind to others, it comes back to you someday. Kindness pays off.","furigana":"ひと|しんせつ|じぶん|かえ|なさ|ひと"},{"japanese":"困っている隣人を手伝ったら、今度は私が助けてもらった。情けは人のためならずとはよく言ったものだ。","romaji":"Komatte iru rinjin o tetsudattara, kondo wa watashi ga tasukete moratta. Nasake wa hito no tame narazu to wa yoku itta mono da.","german":"I helped my neighbor when he was in need, and now he has helped me. How true: one good turn deserves another.","furigana":"こま|りんじん|てつだ|こんど|わたし|たす|なさ|ひと|い"},{"japanese":"情けは人のためならずと言うように、ボランティアの経験は就職活動でも役に立った。","romaji":"Nasake wa hito no tame narazu to iu yō ni, borantia no keiken wa shūshoku katsudō de mo yaku ni tatta.","german":"As the saying goes, kindness comes back: my volunteer experience even helped me in my job hunt.","furigana":"なさ|ひと|い|けいけん|しゅうしょくかつどう|やく|た"}],"notes":"Literally \"compassion is not (only) for the sake of others\": ならず is the classical negation of なり (= だ). It means that kindness returns to oneself by a roundabout route. The proverb is very often misunderstood as \"helping does the other person no good, it spoils them\"; this reading is considered a mistake, even though JMdict lists it as a second meaning. Typical embedding: 情けは人のためならずと言うように／〜とはよく言ったものだ. Slightly elevated, but universally known.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"同僚の遅刻癖を見て反省した。人の振り見て我が振り直せだ。","romaji":"Dōryō no chikoku-guse o mite hansei shita. Hito no furi mite waga furi naose da.","german":"Seeing my colleague's habit of being late made me reflect. One should learn from other people's behavior.","furigana":"どうりょう|ちこくぐせ|み|はんせい|ひと|ふ|み|わ|ふ|なお"},{"japanese":"電車で大声で電話する人を見て、人の振り見て我が振り直せだと思った。","romaji":"Densha de ōgoe de denwa suru hito o mite, hito no furi mite waga furi naose da to omotta.","german":"Seeing someone talk loudly on the phone on the train, I thought I should correct my own behavior by watching others.","furigana":"でんしゃ|おおごえ|でんわ|ひと|み|ひと|ふ|み|わ|ふ|なお|おも"},{"japanese":"部下の報告書のミスを指摘したら、自分の書類にも同じミスがあった。人の振り見て我が振り直せだ。","romaji":"Buka no hōkokusho no misu o shiteki shitara, jibun no shorui ni mo onaji misu ga atta. Hito no furi mite waga furi naose da.","german":"I pointed out a mistake in my subordinate's report, and then found the same mistake in my own documents. Watch others and fix yourself.","furigana":"ぶか|ほうこくしょ|してき|じぶん|しょるい|おな|ひと|ふ|み|わ|ふ|なお"}],"notes":"Literally \"look at other people's behavior and correct your own\": 振り is \"manners, conduct\", and 我が is archaic for \"my, own\". One uses it when, from other people's bad behavior, one realizes that one makes similar mistakes oneself, that is, self-criticism rather than mocking others. Typical: 〜を見て、人の振り見て我が振り直せだと思った. A well-known maxim from school and upbringing; the imperative 直せ is fixed.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"古くなっても、この時計はブランド品だ。腐っても鯛だよ。","romaji":"Furuku natte mo, kono tokei wa burando-hin da. Kusatte mo tai da yo.","german":"Even though it is old, this watch is a brand-name item. Quality stays quality.","furigana":"ふる|とけい|ひん|くさ|たい"},{"japanese":"もうすぐ引退するベテラン選手が決勝点を決めた。腐っても鯛だね。","romaji":"Mō sugu intai suru beteran senshu ga kesshōten o kimeta. Kusatte mo tai da ne.","german":"The veteran, close to retirement, scored the winning goal. Class is class.","furigana":"いんたい|せんしゅ|けっしょうてん|き|くさ|たい"},{"japanese":"業績が落ちたとはいえ、あの会社の技術力は腐っても鯛だ。","romaji":"Gyōseki ga ochita to wa ie, ano kaisha no gijutsuryoku wa kusatte mo tai da.","german":"Even though business results have dropped, that company's technical strength is still first-rate.","furigana":"ぎょうせき|お|かいしゃ|ぎじゅつりょく|くさ|たい"}],"notes":"Literally \"even rotten, still a sea bream\": the 鯛 is regarded in Japan as the noblest fish for celebrations. It means that something or someone of high rank keeps its value even after decline, age or a slump. Typical for former stars, long-established companies or brands: さすが、腐っても鯛だ. Slightly jocular; said directly about a person who is present it can come across as rude, because 腐っても \"even if run down\" resonates in it.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"秘密の話はここではやめよう。壁に耳あり障子に目ありだ。","romaji":"Himitsu no hanashi wa koko de wa yameyō. Kabe ni mimi ari shōji ni me ari da.","german":"Let's not talk about secrets here. Walls have ears.","furigana":"ひみつ|はなし|かべ|みみ|しょうじ|め"},{"japanese":"社内で上司の悪口を言うのはやめたほうがいい。壁に耳あり障子に目ありだよ。","romaji":"Shanai de jōshi no warukuchi o iu no wa yameta hō ga ii. Kabe ni mimi ari shōji ni me ari da yo.","german":"You'd better not badmouth your boss at the company. Walls have ears.","furigana":"しゃない|じょうし|わるくち|い|かべ|みみ|しょうじ|め"},{"japanese":"壁に耳ありと言うから、パスワードは電車の中で口に出さないように。","romaji":"Kabe ni mimi ari to iu kara, pasuwādo wa densha no naka de kuchi ni dasanai yō ni.","german":"You never know who is listening, so don't say your password out loud on the train.","furigana":"かべ|みみ|い|でんしゃ|なか|くち|だ"}],"notes":"Literally \"the wall has ears, the paper sliding door has eyes\": あり is the classical form of ある, and 障子 is the paper-covered sliding door. A warning that conversations are easily overheard or watched, equivalent to \"walls have ears\". Often only the first half is used: 壁に耳ありだから小声で. Typical as a reminder against gossip in the office or confidential topics in public; neutral to jocular.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この部屋は事務所には狭いし、倉庫には広い。帯に短し襷に長しだ。","romaji":"Kono heya wa jimusho ni wa semai shi, sōko ni wa hiroi. Obi ni mijikashi tasuki ni nagashi da.","german":"This room is too small for an office and too big for a storeroom. Neither one thing nor the other.","furigana":"へや|じむしょ|せま|そうこ|ひろ|おび|みじか|たすき|なが"},{"japanese":"このかばんは旅行には小さすぎるし、普段使いには大きすぎる。帯に短し襷に長しだ。","romaji":"Kono kaban wa ryokō ni wa chiisasugiru shi, fudanzukai ni wa ōkisugiru. Obi ni mijikashi tasuki ni nagashi da.","german":"This bag is too small for travel and too big for everyday use. It doesn't fit anywhere properly.","furigana":"りょこう|ちい|ふだんづか|おお|おび|みじか|たすき|なが"},{"japanese":"応募者は何人かいるが、帯に短し襷に長しで、なかなか決められない。","romaji":"Ōbosha wa nannin ka iru ga, obi ni mijikashi tasuki ni nagashi de, nakanaka kimerarenai.","german":"There are a few applicants, but none is quite right, so we just can't decide.","furigana":"おうぼしゃ|なんにん|おび|みじか|たすき|なが|き"}],"notes":"Literally \"too short for an obi (kimono sash), too long for a tasuki\": a 襷 is the cord used to tie up kimono sleeves, and 短し／長し are classical adjective forms. It means something halfway that is really suitable for neither of the two possible purposes. Typical with sizes, quantities, time spans or candidates, often in the pattern 〜には〜し、〜には〜。帯に短し襷に長しだ. Neutral, usually said with a sigh.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"リスクを取らなければ成功はない。虎穴に入らずんば虎子を得ずだ。","romaji":"Risuku o toranakereba seikō wa nai. Koketsu ni irazunba koji o ezu da.","german":"Without taking risks there is no success. Nothing ventured, nothing gained.","furigana":"と|せいこう|こけつ|い|こじ|え"},{"japanese":"虎穴に入らずんば虎子を得ずと言うように、彼は思い切って海外で起業した。","romaji":"Koketsu ni irazunba koji o ezu to iu yō ni, kare wa omoikitte kaigai de kigyō shita.","german":"True to the saying \"nothing ventured, nothing gained,\" he boldly founded a company abroad.","furigana":"こけつ|い|こじ|え|い|かれ|おも|き|かいがい|きぎょう"},{"japanese":"記者は虎穴に入らずんば虎子を得ずの覚悟で、危険な地域の取材に向かった。","romaji":"Kisha wa koketsu ni irazunba koji o ezu no kakugo de, kiken na chiiki no shuzai ni mukatta.","german":"Resolved to take the risk, the reporter set out to cover the dangerous region.","furigana":"きしゃ|こけつ|い|こじ|え|かくご|きけん|ちいき|しゅざい|む"}],"notes":"Literally: \"he who does not enter the tiger's den (虎穴) does not get the tiger cub (虎子)\"; 入らずんば is a classical conditional form \"if one does not enter\", 得ず a classical negation. The proverb comes from a Chinese chronicle and means that great gain is only available through risk. It sounds elevated and resolute and often appears with と言うように or の覚悟で. The counterpart is 君子危うきに近寄らず (the wise man avoids danger).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"言ってしまったことは取り消せない。覆水盆に返らずだ。","romaji":"Itte shimatta koto wa torikesenai. Fukusui bon ni kaerazu da.","german":"What has been said cannot be taken back. What's done is done.","furigana":"い|と|け|ふくすいぼん|かえ"},{"japanese":"別れた恋人によりを戻したいと言われたが、覆水盆に返らずだ。","romaji":"Wakareta koibito ni yori o modoshitai to iwareta ga, fukusui bon ni kaerazu da.","german":"My ex said she wanted to get back together, but what's done is done.","furigana":"わか|こいびと|もど|い|ふくすいぼん|かえ"},{"japanese":"会社を辞めたことを後悔しても、覆水盆に返らずで、もう戻れない。","romaji":"Kaisha o yameta koto o kōkai shite mo, fukusui bon ni kaerazu de, mō modorenai.","german":"Even if I regret quitting the company, what's done is done and I can't go back.","furigana":"かいしゃ|や|こうかい|ふくすいぼん|かえ|もど"}],"notes":"Literally: \"spilled water (覆水) does not return to the tray (盆)\"; 盆 is a tray or shallow dish here, not the Obon festival. The saying goes back to a Chinese anecdote about 太公望: when his wife, who had left him, wanted to return, he poured water on the ground and demanded that she gather it up again. It therefore suits broken relationships and irrevocable words or deeds in particular. Typical at the end of a sentence: …覆水盆に返らずだ; close in content is 後悔先に立たず (\"regret always comes too late\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"細かいことばかり気にして全体が見えていない。木を見て森を見ずだ。","romaji":"Komakai koto bakari ki ni shite zentai ga miete inai. Ki o mite mori o mizu da.","german":"Du achtest nur auf Kleinigkeiten und siehst das Gesamtbild nicht. Du siehst den Wald vor lauter Bäumen nicht.","furigana":"こま|き|ぜんたい|み|き|み|もり|み"},{"japanese":"数字の細部にこだわりすぎると、木を見て森を見ずになってしまう。","romaji":"Sūji no saibu ni kodawarisugiru to, ki o mite mori o mizu ni natte shimau.","german":"Wer sich zu sehr an Zahlendetails festbeißt, sieht schnell den Wald vor lauter Bäumen nicht mehr.","furigana":"すうじ|さいぶ|き|み|もり|み"},{"japanese":"目先の対策ばかりで、木を見て森を見ずの政策だと批判された。","romaji":"Mesaki no taisaku bakari de, ki o mite mori o mizu no seisaku da to hihan sareta.","german":"Die Politik wurde als kurzsichtiges Stückwerk kritisiert, das das große Ganze aus den Augen verliert.","furigana":"めさき|たいさく|き|み|もり|み|せいさく|ひはん"}],"notes":"木を見て森を見ず heißt wörtlich „die Bäume sehen, den Wald aber nicht“ und entspricht genau dem deutschen „den Wald vor lauter Bäumen nicht sehen“; 見ず ist die klassische Verneinung. Man kritisiert damit, dass sich jemand in Details verliert und das große Ganze übersieht. Typische Rahmen sind 〜になる／ならないように, 〜だ und attributiv 木を見て森を見ずの議論／政策. Besonders häufig in Geschäfts- und Politikkontexten.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"借金が多すぎて、少し返しても焼け石に水だ。","romaji":"Shakkin ga ōsugite, sukoshi kaeshite mo yakeishi ni mizu da.","german":"The debt is so large that paying back a little is a drop in the bucket.","furigana":"しゃっきん|おお|すこ|かえ|や|いし|みず"},{"japanese":"猛暑の中、打ち水をしても焼け石に水だった。","romaji":"Mōsho no naka, uchimizu o shite mo yakeishi ni mizu datta.","german":"In the scorching heat, sprinkling water in front of the house was futile.","furigana":"もうしょ|なか|う|みず|や|いし|みず"},{"japanese":"政府の支援金は、被害の大きさを考えれば焼け石に水だ。","romaji":"Seifu no shienkin wa, higai no ōkisa o kangaereba yakeishi ni mizu da.","german":"Considering the scale of the damage, the government's relief funds are a drop in the bucket.","furigana":"せいふ|しえんきん|ひがい|おお|かんが|や|いし|みず"}],"notes":"焼け石に水 literally means \"water on a red-hot stone\": the water evaporates immediately without cooling the stone. It means help or a measure that remains ineffective given the size of the problem, just like \"a drop in the bucket\". Typical are 〜ても焼け石に水だ and 焼け石に水の対策. 雀の涙, by contrast, only stresses that an amount is tiny, not the ineffectiveness.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"スーツを着たら見違えるほど立派に見える。馬子にも衣装だね。","romaji":"Sūtsu o kitara michigaeru hodo rippa ni mieru. Mago ni mo ishō da ne.","german":"In a suit, he looks amazingly impressive. Fine feathers make fine birds.","furigana":"き|みちが|りっぱ|み|まご|いしょう"},{"japanese":"成人式の振袖姿の娘を見て、父は「馬子にも衣装だな」と笑った。","romaji":"Seijinshiki no furisode sugata no musume o mite, chichi wa \"mago ni mo ishō da na\" to waratta.","german":"Seeing his daughter in a furisode at her coming-of-age ceremony, her father laughed, \"Fine clothes make the person.\"","furigana":"せいじんしき|ふりそですがた|むすめ|み|ちち|まご|いしょう|わら"},{"japanese":"「馬子にも衣装」と言われたけど、褒められているのかな。","romaji":"\"Mago ni mo ishō\" to iwareta kedo, homerarete iru no ka na.","german":"I was told \"clothes make the man,\" but I wonder if that was a compliment.","furigana":"まご|いしょう|い|ほ"}],"notes":"馬子 was formerly a simple packhorse driver; 馬子にも衣装 therefore means \"even a horse driver looks good in fine clothes\". It corresponds to \"clothes make the man\", but is a teasing, backhanded compliment: one says it jokingly about family members, close friends or oneself; toward strangers or superiors it would be rude. 馬子 has nothing to do with 孫 \"grandchild\", even though both are read まご.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"厳しいことを言われたけど、良薬は口に苦しだ。","romaji":"Kibishii koto o iwareta kedo, ryōyaku wa kuchi ni nigashi da.","german":"I was told harsh things, but good medicine tastes bitter.","furigana":"きび|い|りょうやく|くち|にが"},{"japanese":"良薬は口に苦しと言うから、先生の厳しい指摘も素直に受け止めよう。","romaji":"Ryōyaku wa kuchi ni nigashi to iu kara, sensei no kibishii shiteki mo sunao ni uketomeyō.","german":"They say good medicine tastes bitter, so I'll accept the teacher's strict remarks with an open mind.","furigana":"りょうやく|くち|にが|い|せんせい|きび|してき|すなお|う|と"},{"japanese":"部下の耳の痛い意見こそ、良薬は口に苦しで、会社のためになる。","romaji":"Buka no mimi no itai iken koso, ryōyaku wa kuchi ni nigashi de, kaisha no tame ni naru.","german":"It is precisely the subordinates' uncomfortable opinions that are bitter medicine and good for the company.","furigana":"ぶか|みみ|いた|いけん|りょうやく|くち|にが|かいしゃ"}],"notes":"Literally: \"good medicine is bitter in the mouth\"; 苦し is the classical form of 苦い. The proverb of Chinese origin says that honest, helpful advice is often unpleasant to hear, and it is frequently paired with 忠言耳に逆らう (\"loyal advice grates on the ear\"). It is used to accept harsh criticism as useful: 良薬は口に苦しと言うから…, …良薬は口に苦しだ. Related is the expression 耳が痛い for unpleasant truths.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は自分の悪口を言われていることを知らない。知らぬが仏だ。","romaji":"Kare wa jibun no waruguchi o iwarete iru koto o shiranai. Shiranu ga hotoke da.","german":"He doesn't know that people are badmouthing him. Ignorance is bliss.","furigana":"かれ|じぶん|わるぐち|い|し|し|ほとけ"},{"japanese":"持っている株が暴落しているのも知らず、父はのんきに旅行中だ。知らぬが仏とはこのことだ。","romaji":"Motte iru kabu ga bōraku shite iru no mo shirazu, chichi wa nonki ni ryokōchū da. Shiranu ga hotoke to wa kono koto da.","german":"My father is blithely traveling, unaware that the stocks he owns are crashing. This is what they mean by ignorance is bliss.","furigana":"も|かぶ|ぼうらく|し|ちち|りょこうちゅう|し|ほとけ"},{"japanese":"このスープに何が入っているかは、知らぬが仏ということもあるよ。","romaji":"Kono sūpu ni nani ga haitte iru ka wa, shiranu ga hotoke to iu koto mo aru yo.","german":"When it comes to what's in this soup, sometimes ignorance is bliss.","furigana":"なに|はい|し|ほとけ"}],"notes":"Literally: \"not knowing is Buddha\": whoever knows nothing about something unpleasant remains as serene as a Buddha. 知らぬ is the classical negation of 知る. Often said ironically of someone who is the only one who suspects nothing: 知らぬが仏とはこのことだ, or as advice that one had better not find something out. Do not confuse it with 言わぬが花 (\"some things are better left unsaid\"), a proverb of its own.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"嘘をついていたのがばれて、信用を失った。身から出た錆だ。","romaji":"Uso o tsuite ita no ga barete, shin'yō o ushinatta. Mi kara deta sabi da.","german":"His lies were exposed and he lost people's trust. He brought it on himself.","furigana":"うそ|しんよう|うしな|み|で|さび"},{"japanese":"試験勉強をサボったんだから、不合格は身から出た錆だよ。","romaji":"Shiken benkyō o sabotta n da kara, fugōkaku wa mi kara deta sabi da yo.","german":"You skipped studying for the exam, so failing is your own fault.","furigana":"しけんべんきょう|ふごうかく|み|で|さび"},{"japanese":"身から出た錆とはいえ、彼が全てを失ったのは気の毒だ。","romaji":"Mi kara deta sabi to wa ie, kare ga subete o ushinatta no wa kinodoku da.","german":"Even if he brought it on himself, I feel sorry that he lost everything.","furigana":"み|で|さび|かれ|すべ|うしな|き|どく"}],"notes":"身から出た錆 literally means \"rust that comes from the blade itself\"; 身 is the blade of a sword here, which rusts through its owner's negligence. It means misfortune one has brought upon oneself through one's own misconduct. Typical are 身から出た錆だ and 身から出た錆とはいえ; often also in kana, 身から出たさび. Equivalent in meaning, but more sober and more literary, is 自業自得.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ずっと探していた鍵がポケットにあった。灯台下暗しだ。","romaji":"Zutto sagashite ita kagi ga poketto ni atta. Tōdai moto kurashi da.","german":"The key I had been looking for all along was in my pocket. What's right under your nose is easy to miss.","furigana":"さが|かぎ|とうだいもとくら"},{"japanese":"犯人は被害者のすぐ近くにいた。まさに灯台下暗しだ。","romaji":"Hannin wa higaisha no sugu chikaku ni ita. Masa ni tōdai moto kurashi da.","german":"The culprit was right next to the victim the whole time. It really is darkest under the lamp.","furigana":"はんにん|ひがいしゃ|ちか|とうだいもとくら"},{"japanese":"いい店を探して遠くまで行ったが、灯台下暗しで、近所に評判の店があった。","romaji":"Ii mise o sagashite tōku made itta ga, tōdai moto kurashi de, kinjo ni hyōban no mise ga atta.","german":"I went far to find a good restaurant, but there was a well-known one right in my neighborhood.","furigana":"みせ|さが|とお|い|とうだいもとくら|きんじょ|ひょうばん|みせ"}],"notes":"灯台 here does not mean a lighthouse but the old lamp stand or oil-lamp holder: directly under the lamp there is shadow. The proverb says that one most easily overlooks what is closest at hand: the object one is looking for in one's own pocket, the culprit in one's immediate circle, the good restaurant in the neighborhood. It is usually placed at the end as a comment (灯台下暗しだ) or connected with で. 下 is read もと here.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は破産してから友達がいなくなった。金の切れ目が縁の切れ目だ。","romaji":"Kare wa hasan shite kara tomodachi ga inaku natta. Kane no kireme ga en no kireme da.","german":"Since he went bankrupt, he has no friends left. When the money runs out, so does the friendship.","furigana":"かれ|はさん|ともだち|かね|き|め|えん|き|め"},{"japanese":"スポンサーがいなくなると、チームの関係者も次々と離れていった。金の切れ目が縁の切れ目だった。","romaji":"Suponsā ga inaku naru to, chīmu no kankeisha mo tsugitsugi to hanarete itta. Kane no kireme ga en no kireme datta.","german":"When the sponsor disappeared, the team's other associates also left one after another. When the money ran out, the ties ended.","furigana":"かんけいしゃ|つぎつぎ|はな|かね|き|め|えん|き|め"},{"japanese":"お金で付き合う友達なんて、金の切れ目が縁の切れ目だよ。","romaji":"Okane de tsukiau tomodachi nante, kane no kireme ga en no kireme da yo.","german":"Friends you only keep through money are gone as soon as the money is gone.","furigana":"かね|つ|あ|ともだち|かね|き|め|えん|き|め"}],"notes":"Literally \"the break in the money is the break in the bond\": 切れ目 is \"end, break\", and 縁 is \"relationship, connection\". The proverb says cynically that relationships based on money end as soon as the money is gone, whether friendships, love affairs or business partners. Often bitter in hindsight or as a warning: 結局、金の切れ目が縁の切れ目だった. Neutral, widely used.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"悪い仲間と付き合うと自分も悪くなる。朱に交われば赤くなるだ。","romaji":"Warui nakama to tsukiau to jibun mo waruku naru. Shu ni majiwareba akaku naru da.","german":"If you hang out with bad company, you turn bad yourself. You become like those you associate with.","furigana":"わる|なかま|つ|あ|じぶん|わる|しゅ|まじ|あか"},{"japanese":"朱に交われば赤くなると言うから、付き合う友達は選びなさい。","romaji":"Shu ni majiwareba akaku naru to iu kara, tsukiau tomodachi wa erabinasai.","german":"They say you become like the company you keep, so choose your friends carefully.","furigana":"しゅ|まじ|あか|い|つ|あ|ともだち|えら"},{"japanese":"勉強熱心な仲間に囲まれて、朱に交われば赤くなるで、私も毎日勉強するようになった。","romaji":"Benkyō nesshin na nakama ni kakomarete, shu ni majiwareba akaku naru de, watashi mo mainichi benkyō suru yō ni natta.","german":"Surrounded by studious peers, I caught the bug, and now I study every day too.","furigana":"べんきょうねっしん|なかま|かこ|しゅ|まじ|あか|わたし|まいにちべんきょう"}],"notes":"Literally: \"whoever mixes with vermilion (朱) turns red\"; 交われば is the conditional form of 交わる \"to associate, to mingle\". The proverb says that one is shaped by one's company, usually as a warning against bad company, but it can also stand positively for an inspiring environment. It is quoted with と言うから or followed by だ／で. Similar, but somewhat differently oriented, is 類は友を呼ぶ (birds of a feather flock together).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"朝ご飯を食べなかったから、腹が減った。","romaji":"Asagohan o tabenakatta kara, hara ga hetta.","german":"I didn't eat breakfast, so I'm hungry.","furigana":"あさ|はん|た|はら|へ"},{"japanese":"部活の後は、いつもすごく腹が減る。","romaji":"Bukatsu no ato wa, itsumo sugoku hara ga heru.","german":"After club activities, I'm always really hungry.","furigana":"ぶかつ|あと|はら|へ"},{"japanese":"あー、腹減った。ラーメンでも食べに行こうぜ。","romaji":"Ā, hara hetta. Rāmen demo tabe ni ikō ze.","german":"Man, I'm hungry. Let's go eat some ramen.","furigana":"はらへ|た|い"}],"notes":"Literally \"the belly (腹) shrinks (減る)\" - \"to get hungry\". Mostly in the past tense: 腹が減った \"I'm hungry\"; in casual conversation が is often dropped: 腹減った. The expression is rough colloquial speech and rather masculine; neutrally and politely one says お腹が空いた. It lives on in the proverb 腹が減っては戦ができぬ \"you can't fight on an empty stomach\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"手作りの料理は手間がかかるが、美味しい。","romaji":"Tezukuri no ryōri wa tema ga kakaru ga, oishii.","german":"Homemade cooking takes a lot of effort, but it is delicious.","furigana":"てづく|りょうり|てま|おい"},{"japanese":"この書類は手続きに手間がかかるので、早めに出してください。","romaji":"Kono shorui wa tetsuzuki ni tema ga kakaru node, hayame ni dashite kudasai.","german":"Processing this document takes a lot of trouble, so please submit it early.","furigana":"しょるい|てつづ|てま|はや|だ"},{"japanese":"下の子はまだ小さくて、何かと手間がかかる。","romaji":"Shita no ko wa mada chiisakute, nani ka to tema ga kakaru.","german":"My youngest is still small and takes a lot of care in all sorts of ways.","furigana":"した|こ|ちい|なに|てま"}],"notes":"手間 means \"labor, time and effort\"; 手間がかかる means \"to require a lot of effort, to be troublesome\". The thing takes が or は as topic: この料理は手間がかかる. It is intensified with とても or すごく; about people one says 手間がかかる子 \"a child who needs a lot of care\". The transitive counterpart is 手間をかける \"to put effort in\", politely お手間を取らせてすみません \"sorry for the trouble\". Similar is 面倒だ, but it stresses the annoyance more.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"気分転換に散歩に出かけた。","romaji":"Kibun tenkan ni sanpo ni dekaketa.","german":"I went for a walk for a change of pace.","furigana":"きぶんてんかん|さんぽ|で"},{"japanese":"家具の位置を変えたら、いい気分転換になった。","romaji":"Kagu no ichi o kaetara, ii kibun tenkan ni natta.","german":"Rearranging the furniture was a nice change of scenery.","furigana":"かぐ|いち|か|きぶんてんかん"},{"japanese":"勉強に疲れたら、少し気分転換をしたほうがいいよ。","romaji":"Benkyō ni tsukaretara, sukoshi kibun tenkan o shita hō ga ii yo.","german":"When you're tired of studying, you should take a little break.","furigana":"べんきょう|つか|すこ|きぶんてんかん"}],"notes":"気分転換 is composed of 気分 \"mood, humor\" and 転換 \"switch, change\" and means \"change of pace, change of scenery, diversion\": a small interruption to clear one's head. Typical frames are 気分転換に〜する \"to ... for a change of pace\", 気分転換をする and 〜はいい気分転換になる. It is a noun (also possible as a する-verb), not a verb phrase. Similar is 息抜き \"breather\", which stresses relaxation from work more strongly.","levelNote":"Compound: 気分 (N4) + 転換 (N1)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日が暮れる前に家に帰ろう。","romaji":"Hi ga kureru mae ni ie ni kaerō.","german":"Let's go home before it gets dark.","furigana":"ひ|く|まえ|いえ|かえ"},{"japanese":"子どものころは、日が暮れるまで公園で遊んでいた。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro wa, hi ga kureru made kōen de asonde ita.","german":"As a child, I used to play in the park until dusk.","furigana":"こ|ひ|く|こうえん|あそ"},{"japanese":"冬は日が暮れるのが早いですね。","romaji":"Fuyu wa hi ga kureru no ga hayai desu ne.","german":"It gets dark early in winter, doesn't it?","furigana":"ふゆ|ひ|く|はや"}],"notes":"Literally \"the day / sun (日) comes to an end (暮れる)\" - \"dusk falls, evening comes\". Typical frames: 日が暮れる前に \"before it gets dark\", 日が暮れるまで \"until dusk\", 日が暮れるのが早い. The counterpart is 夜が明ける \"dawn breaks\"; 日が沈む, on the other hand, only describes the sinking of the sun. The related noun is 日暮れ \"dusk\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"遠慮しないで。水臭いなあ。","romaji":"Enryo shinaide. Mizukusai nā.","german":"Don't hold back. Don't be so formal with me.","furigana":"えんりょ|みずくさ"},{"japanese":"引っ越すなら言ってくれればよかったのに、水臭いじゃないか。","romaji":"Hikkosu nara itte kurereba yokatta noni, mizukusai ja nai ka.","german":"You could have told me if you were moving. Why are you being so distant?","furigana":"ひ|こ|い|みずくさ"},{"japanese":"親友なのに、お礼にお金を払うなんて水臭いよ。","romaji":"Shin'yū na noni, orei ni okane o harau nante mizukusai yo.","german":"We're best friends, so paying me as thanks is way too formal.","furigana":"しんゆう|れい|かね|はら|みずくさ"}],"notes":"水臭い literally means \"watery, diluted\" (for example of thin sake). Figuratively it means \"distant, too formal\" toward people who are close to you. It is almost always used as a friendly reproach when a friend does not accept help, keeps something secret or is excessively polite: 水臭いなあ, 水臭いこと言うなよ, 水臭いじゃないか. It does not suit strangers or superiors. It is an ordinary い-adjective.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"日が経つのが本当に早い。","romaji":"Hi ga tatsu no ga hontō ni hayai.","german":"Time really flies.","furigana":"ひ|た|ほんとう|はや"},{"japanese":"日が経つにつれて、新しい仕事にも慣れてきた。","romaji":"Hi ga tatsu ni tsurete, atarashii shigoto ni mo narete kita.","german":"As time went on, I got used to my new job as well.","furigana":"ひ|た|あたら|しごと|な"},{"japanese":"このパンは日が経つと固くなるので、早めに食べてください。","romaji":"Kono pan wa hi ga tatsu to kataku naru node, hayame ni tabete kudasai.","german":"This bread gets hard as time passes, so please eat it soon.","furigana":"ひ|た|かた|はや|た"}],"notes":"経つ \"to pass (of time)\" with 日 \"day\": \"the days pass\". Common frames: 日が経つのが早い \"time passes quickly\", 日が経つにつれて \"as time goes by\". For concrete periods one says 三日経つ or 一年が経つ. Do not confuse it with the identically read 立つ \"to stand\"; it is also written 日がたつ in kana. Neutral.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"明日の午後は都合がいいですか。","romaji":"Ashita no gogo wa tsugō ga ii desu ka.","german":"Is tomorrow afternoon convenient for you?","furigana":"あした|ごご|つごう"},{"japanese":"みんなの都合がいい日に会議をしましょう。","romaji":"Minna no tsugō ga ii hi ni kaigi o shimashō.","german":"Let's hold the meeting on a day that suits everyone.","furigana":"つごう|ひ|かいぎ"},{"japanese":"彼は自分に都合がいいことしか言わない。","romaji":"Kare wa jibun ni tsugō ga ii koto shika iwanai.","german":"He only ever says things that are convenient for himself.","furigana":"かれ|じぶん|つごう|い"}],"notes":"都合 means \"circumstances, schedule\"; 都合がいい means \"it suits, it is convenient\". Typical when arranging appointments: 明日は都合がいいですか, more politely ご都合はいかがですか; the opposite is 都合が悪い. Before nouns usually 都合のいい日. With 自分に it can have a negative meaning of \"self-serving, whatever suits oneself\": 自分に都合のいい話. In formal writing also 都合がよい.","levelNote":"Textbook: Minna no Nihongo II, lesson 26"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"新人にはこの仕事は荷が重い。","romaji":"Shinjin ni wa kono shigoto wa ni ga omoi.","german":"This job is too much of a burden for a newcomer.","furigana":"しんじん|しごと|に|おも"},{"japanese":"私にはリーダーの役は荷が重すぎます。","romaji":"Watashi ni wa rīdā no yaku wa ni ga omosugimasu.","german":"The role of leader is too heavy a burden for me.","furigana":"わたし|やく|に|おも"},{"japanese":"社長の代わりにスピーチをするのは、ちょっと荷が重いなあ。","romaji":"Shachō no kawari ni supīchi o suru no wa, chotto ni ga omoi nā.","german":"Giving a speech in place of the company president is a bit much for me.","furigana":"しゃちょう|か|に|おも"}],"notes":"荷が重い literally means \"the load is heavy\" and means that a task or responsibility is too big for someone. The person concerned takes には: 新人には荷が重い, 私には荷が重すぎます; the latter is a modest way of declining a task. Related are 重荷 \"burden\" and 肩の荷が下りる \"a load is lifted from one's shoulders\". It behaves like an い-adjective (荷が重すぎる, 荷が重かった).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"電車を待つ間、スマホで暇を潰した。","romaji":"Densha o matsu aida, sumaho de hima o tsubushita.","german":"I killed time on my smartphone while waiting for the train.","furigana":"でんしゃ|ま|あいだ|ひま|つぶ"},{"japanese":"待ち合わせまで時間があったので、本屋で暇を潰した。","romaji":"Machiawase made jikan ga atta node, hon'ya de hima o tsubushita.","german":"I had time before the meeting, so I killed time in a bookstore.","furigana":"ま|あ|じかん|ほんや|ひま|つぶ"},{"japanese":"入院中は、テレビを見て暇を潰すしかなかった。","romaji":"Nyūin-chū wa, terebi o mite hima o tsubusu shika nakatta.","german":"In the hospital, all I could do to pass the time was watch TV.","furigana":"にゅういんちゅう|み|ひま|つぶ"}],"notes":"暇を潰す literally means \"to crush free time\" and means \"to kill time\", mostly while waiting or out of boredom. The means takes で: スマホで暇を潰す, 本屋で暇を潰す. Equivalent is 時間を潰す; the related noun is 暇つぶし (暇つぶしに〜する \"to do as a pastime\"). The expression is neutral to colloquial and does not have the negative undertone of \"wasting time\" (時間を無駄にする).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"出発前に腹ごしらえをしよう。","romaji":"Shuppatsu mae ni haragoshirae o shiyō.","german":"Let's get something to eat before we set out.","furigana":"しゅっぱつまえ|はら"},{"japanese":"試合の前に、おにぎりで軽く腹ごしらえした。","romaji":"Shiai no mae ni, onigiri de karuku haragoshirae shita.","german":"Before the match, I ate a bit of rice balls to fuel up.","furigana":"しあい|まえ|かる|はら"},{"japanese":"まずは腹ごしらえだ。話はそれからにしよう。","romaji":"Mazu wa haragoshirae da. Hanashi wa sore kara ni shiyō.","german":"Food first. We can talk afterwards.","furigana":"はら|はなし"}],"notes":"腹ごしらえ is composed of 腹 \"belly\" and こしらえ (from 拵える \"to prepare\"): \"preparing the stomach\", that is, fortifying oneself with a meal before an exertion. Typical are 腹ごしらえをする, 〜の前に腹ごしらえ, まずは腹ごしらえだ. It is a noun or する-verb and, because of 腹, sounds casual, somewhat masculine and colloquial; more neutral would be 先に何か食べておく.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"久しぶりに会って、話が弾んだ。","romaji":"Hisashiburi ni atte, hanashi ga hazunda.","german":"We met after a long time and had a lively conversation.","furigana":"ひさ|あ|はなし|はず"},{"japanese":"共通の趣味があると分かって、初対面なのに話が弾んだ。","romaji":"Kyōtsū no shumi ga aru to wakatte, shotaimen na noni hanashi ga hazunda.","german":"When we found out we had a hobby in common, we got talking even though it was our first meeting.","furigana":"きょうつう|しゅみ|わ|しょたいめん|はなし|はず"},{"japanese":"デートで話が弾まなくて、気まずかった。","romaji":"Dēto de hanashi ga hazumanakute, kimazukatta.","german":"The conversation didn't flow on the date, which was awkward.","furigana":"はなし|はず|き"}],"notes":"話が弾む literally means \"the conversation bounces (like a ball)\" and means \"to chat animatedly, for the conversation to pick up\". The topic takes で: 昔の話で話が弾む, 共通の趣味で話が弾む. The negative 話が弾まない \"the conversation doesn't get going\" is also common. It is intransitive; the participants are not the subject, 話 is the subject. The expression is neutral and very everyday.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼はお金を湯水のように使っている。","romaji":"Kare wa okane o yumizu no yō ni tsukatte iru.","german":"He spends money like water.","furigana":"かれ|かね|ゆみず|つか"},{"japanese":"税金を湯水のように使う政治家に国民は怒っている。","romaji":"Zeikin o yumizu no yō ni tsukau seijika ni kokumin wa okotte iru.","german":"The public is angry at politicians who throw tax money around.","furigana":"ぜいきん|ゆみず|つか|せいじか|こくみん|おこ"},{"japanese":"宝くじに当たった男は、賞金を湯水のように使って、一年で全部なくしてしまった。","romaji":"Takarakuji ni atatta otoko wa, shōkin o yumizu no yō ni tsukatte, ichinen de zenbu nakushite shimatta.","german":"The man who won the lottery spent his prize money like water and lost all of it within a year.","furigana":"たから|あ|おとこ|しょうきん|ゆみず|つか|いちねん|ぜんぶ"}],"notes":"Literally \"to use like hot and cold water\": 湯水 stands for something that seems available without limit. It means extravagant spending, almost always with お金, 税金 or 予算 as the object: 〜を湯水のように使う. Reproachful and critical, common in the media about tax money; the verb conjugates normally (使って, 使った). More neutral and general is 無駄遣いする \"to waste\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"突然の解雇は寝耳に水だった。","romaji":"Totsuzen no kaiko wa nemimi ni mizu datta.","german":"The sudden dismissal came as a bolt from the blue.","furigana":"とつぜん|かいこ|ねみみ|みず"},{"japanese":"親会社の売却は、社員にとって寝耳に水の話だった。","romaji":"Oyagaisha no baikyaku wa, shain ni totte nemimi ni mizu no hanashi datta.","german":"The sale of the parent company was a bolt from the blue for the employees.","furigana":"おやがいしゃ|ばいきゃく|しゃいん|ねみみ|みず|はなし"},{"japanese":"兄が来月結婚するなんて、寝耳に水だよ。","romaji":"Ani ga raigetsu kekkon suru nante, nemimi ni mizu da yo.","german":"My brother is getting married next month? That's news to me!","furigana":"あに|らいげつけっこん|ねみみ|みず"}],"notes":"寝耳に水 literally means \"water in the ear while one is asleep\": news hits one completely unprepared and shocks. It is mostly about unpleasant news such as layoffs, closures or scandals. Typical are 〜は寝耳に水だった and 寝耳に水の話／出来事. Synonymous but more elevated is 青天の霹靂.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"毎日仕事に追われて、休む暇もない。","romaji":"Mainichi shigoto ni owarete, yasumu hima mo nai.","german":"I'm swamped with work every day and have no time to rest.","furigana":"まいにちしごと|お|やす|ひま"},{"japanese":"年度末は仕事に追われて、家族と話す時間がない。","romaji":"Nendomatsu wa shigoto ni owarete, kazoku to hanasu jikan ga nai.","german":"At the end of the fiscal year I'm swamped with work and have no time to talk with my family.","furigana":"ねんどまつ|しごと|お|かぞく|はな|じかん"},{"japanese":"仕事に追われる生活をやめて、田舎に引っ越した。","romaji":"Shigoto ni owareru seikatsu o yamete, inaka ni hikkoshita.","german":"I gave up my life of being chased by work and moved to the countryside.","furigana":"しごと|お|せいかつ|いなか|ひ|こ"}],"notes":"仕事に追われる is the passive of 追う \"to chase\": literally \"to be chased by work\", that is, \"to be swamped with work, to work constantly under time pressure\". The cause takes に; the pattern also works for other things: 家事に追われる, 締め切りに追われる, 時間に追われる. Common in the progressive form 追われている or as a て-form with a consequence: 仕事に追われて休む暇もない. Neutral, very common in everyday life and the media.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"シェフが腕を振るった料理を楽しんだ。","romaji":"Shefu ga ude o furutta ryōri o tanoshinda.","german":"We enjoyed the dish that the chef put all his skill into.","furigana":"うで|ふ|りょうり|たの"},{"japanese":"週末は父が台所で腕を振るって、家族にカレーを作ってくれた。","romaji":"Shūmatsu wa chichi ga daidokoro de ude o furutte, kazoku ni karē o tsukutte kureta.","german":"On the weekend, my father showed what he can do in the kitchen and made curry for the family.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|ちち|だいどころ|うで|ふ|かぞく|つく"},{"japanese":"新しい部長は、会社の立て直しに腕を振るうことになった。","romaji":"Atarashii buchō wa, kaisha no tatenaoshi ni ude o furuu koto ni natta.","german":"The new department head is to put his full ability into rebuilding the company.","furigana":"あたら|ぶちょう|かいしゃ|た|なお|うで|ふ"}],"notes":"Literally \"to swing the arm\": 腕 stands here for \"skill, dexterity\". It means to bring one's skill fully to bear, especially in cooking or in manual and professional work: 料理の腕を振るう, 〜に腕を振るう. Positive, neutral to slightly elevated (restaurant advertising, reports). Do not confuse with 腕を磨く \"to hone one's skills\" and 腕が上がる \"to get better\". Rarely also written 腕を揮う.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"早く一人前の職人になりたい。","romaji":"Hayaku ichininmae no shokunin ni naritai.","german":"I want to become a full-fledged craftsman soon.","furigana":"はや|いちにんまえ|しょくにん"},{"japanese":"息子が一人前になるまで、もう少し頑張って働こう。","romaji":"Musuko ga ichininmae ni naru made, mō sukoshi ganbatte hatarakō.","german":"Let's keep working a bit longer until my son is independent.","furigana":"むすこ|いちにんまえ|すこ|がんば|はたら"},{"japanese":"料理人として一人前になるには十年かかると言われている。","romaji":"Ryōrinin to shite ichininmae ni naru ni wa jūnen kakaru to iwarete iru.","german":"It is said that it takes ten years to become a full-fledged chef.","furigana":"りょうりにん|いちにんまえ|じゅうねん|い"}],"notes":"一人前 literally means \"a portion for one person\" (as with food: 寿司一人前) and refers to someone who counts as a full person. 一人前になる therefore means \"to become an adult and independent\" or, in a profession, \"to have completed one's training, to master one's craft\". Typical are 一人前の職人／社会人になる and the transitive 一人前に育てる (\"to raise into a full-fledged ...\"). Neutral; there is also the disparaging 一人前の口をきく (\"to talk as if one were already somebody\").","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ミスが多くて、職場で肩身が狭い。","romaji":"Misu ga ōkute, shokuba de katami ga semai.","german":"I make a lot of mistakes, so I feel small at work.","furigana":"おお|しょくば|かたみ|せま"},{"japanese":"最近はどこも禁煙で、たばこを吸う人は肩身が狭い。","romaji":"Saikin wa doko mo kin'en de, tabako o suu hito wa katami ga semai.","german":"These days smoking is banned almost everywhere, so smokers are made to feel small.","furigana":"さいきん|きんえん|す|ひと|かたみ|せま"},{"japanese":"兄弟の中で私だけ大学に落ちて、親戚の集まりで肩身の狭い思いをした。","romaji":"Kyōdai no naka de watashi dake daigaku ni ochite, shinseki no atsumari de katami no semai omoi o shita.","german":"I was the only one among my siblings to fail the university exam, and I felt awkward at the family gathering.","furigana":"きょうだい|なか|わたし|だいがく|お|しんせき|あつ|かたみ|せま|おも"}],"notes":"Literally \"the width of the shoulders is narrow\": 肩身 stands for one's standing in front of others. It means the oppressive feeling of having to be ashamed or make oneself small before others, because one has failed, belongs to a minority or is a burden on others. Typical: 〜で肩身が狭い, 肩身の狭い思いをする. Unlike 恥ずかしい, it stresses one's social position, that is, the feeling of having hardly any room or standing.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"締め切り前で机に釘付けになっている。","romaji":"Shimekiri mae de tsukue ni kugizuke ni natte iru.","german":"With the deadline coming up, I'm nailed to my desk.","furigana":"し|き|まえ|つくえ|くぎづ"},{"japanese":"ワールドカップの決勝で、国中の人がテレビに釘付けになった。","romaji":"Wārudo kappu no kesshō de, kunijū no hito ga terebi ni kugizuke ni natta.","german":"During the World Cup final, the whole country was glued to the TV.","furigana":"けっしょう|くにじゅう|ひと|くぎづ"},{"japanese":"ショーウィンドウのケーキに、子供の目が釘付けになっていた。","romaji":"Shōwindō no kēki ni, kodomo no me ga kugizuke ni natte ita.","german":"The child's eyes were riveted on the cakes in the shop window.","furigana":"こども|め|くぎづ"}],"notes":"Literally \"to be nailed down\": 釘付け is actually the nailing down. It means that one cannot tear oneself away from a place or sight, out of fascination, fright or duty. Typical frames: テレビに釘付けになる, 目が〜に釘付けになる, その場に釘付けになる. The transitive side is 釘付けにする \"to rivet, to hold someone fast\" (観客を釘付けにする). Neutral, common in the media and daily life.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"質問に答えず、お茶を濁した。","romaji":"Shitsumon ni kotaezu, ocha o nigoshita.","german":"He did not answer the question and dodged the issue.","furigana":"しつもん|こた|ちゃ|にご"},{"japanese":"本格的な対策をせず、ポスターを貼っただけでお茶を濁した。","romaji":"Honkakuteki na taisaku o sezu, posutā o hatta dake de ocha o nigoshita.","german":"Instead of real measures, they just put up posters and left it at that.","furigana":"ほんかくてき|たいさく|は|ちゃ|にご"},{"japanese":"スピーチを頼まれたが、準備できなかったので、簡単なあいさつでお茶を濁した。","romaji":"Supīchi o tanomareta ga, junbi dekinakatta node, kantan na aisatsu de ocha o nigoshita.","german":"I was asked to give a speech but wasn't prepared, so I got by with a short greeting.","furigana":"たの|じゅんび|かんたん|ちゃ|にご"}],"notes":"Literally \"to muddy the tea\": someone who cannot do the tea ceremony just stirs the tea somehow until it is cloudy and pretends. It means to evade a matter with a half-hearted, superficial answer or makeshift solution: 適当な返事でお茶を濁す, 〜だけでお茶を濁す. Similar are 言葉を濁す (to be vague, only in speech) and ごまかす (to cover up); お茶を濁す stresses the half-hearted, provisional. Neutral, mostly critical.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この案なら上司の顔が立つだろう。","romaji":"Kono an nara jōshi no kao ga tatsu darō.","german":"With this proposal, the boss will probably be able to save face.","furigana":"あん|じょうし|かお|た"},{"japanese":"皆さんが協力してくださったので、幹事としての顔が立ちました。","romaji":"Minasan ga kyōryoku shite kudasatta node, kanji to shite no kao ga tachimashita.","german":"Thanks to everyone's cooperation, I saved face as the organizer.","furigana":"みな|きょうりょく|かんじ|かお|た"},{"japanese":"約束の日までに商品を届けられなければ、紹介してくれた先輩の顔が立たない。","romaji":"Yakusoku no hi made ni shōhin o todokerarenakereba, shōkai shite kureta senpai no kao ga tatanai.","german":"If we can't deliver the goods by the agreed date, the senior colleague who introduced us will lose face.","furigana":"やくそく|ひ|しょうひん|とど|しょうかい|せんぱい|かお|た"}],"notes":"Literally \"the face stands\": 顔 stands for standing and honor. It means that someone's reputation is preserved, usually thanks to the behavior of others: 〜の顔が立つ, negative 顔が立たない. The transitive counterpart 顔を立てる means \"to save someone's face, to show someone honor\" (部長の顔を立てる); the opposite is 顔が潰れる／顔を潰す \"to lose face / to embarrass someone\". Typical at work and in the family, where consideration for hierarchy counts.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この仕事は大変なのに給料が安くて割に合わない。","romaji":"Kono shigoto wa taihen na noni kyūryō ga yasukute wari ni awanai.","german":"This job is hard but the pay is low, so it isn't worth it.","furigana":"しごと|たいへん|きゅうりょう|やす|わり|あ"},{"japanese":"往復三時間かけて千円の買い物をするのは割に合わない。","romaji":"Ōfuku sanjikan kakete sen'en no kaimono o suru no wa wari ni awanai.","german":"Spending three hours round trip for a 1,000-yen purchase isn't worth it.","furigana":"おうふくさんじかん|せんえん|か|もの|わり|あ"},{"japanese":"そんなに危ない仕事なら、この金額じゃ割に合わないよ。","romaji":"Sonna ni abunai shigoto nara, kono kingaku ja wari ni awanai yo.","german":"If the job is that dangerous, this amount isn't worth it.","furigana":"あぶ|しごと|きんがく|わり|あ"}],"notes":"割 here is the ratio and 合う means \"to fit\": literally \"doesn't fit the ratio\" - the effort, risk or cost is out of proportion to the return. Usually a contrast with のに or わりに precedes it: 大変なのに安くて割に合わない. The positive form 割に合う (\"it pays off\") is rarer, often negated as 割に合う仕事じゃない; variant 割が合わない. Not to be confused with the adverb 割に／割と (\"fairly, relatively\"). Neutral to colloquial.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女がプロジェクトの采配を振っている。","romaji":"Kanojo ga purojekuto no saihai o futte iru.","german":"She is in charge of the project.","furigana":"かのじょ|さいはい|ふ"},{"japanese":"文化祭では、生徒会長が全体の采配を振った。","romaji":"Bunkasai de wa, seitokaichō ga zentai no saihai o futta.","german":"At the school festival, the student council president was in charge of the whole thing.","furigana":"ぶんかさい|せいとかいちょう|ぜんたい|さいはい|ふ"},{"japanese":"社長が入院中のため、副社長が会社の采配を振ることになった。","romaji":"Shachō ga nyūinchū no tame, fukushachō ga kaisha no saihai o furu koto ni natta.","german":"Because the company president is in the hospital, the vice president will take charge of the company.","furigana":"しゃちょう|にゅういんちゅう|ふくしゃちょう|かいしゃ|さいはい|ふ"}],"notes":"Literally \"to wave the commander's baton\": 采配 is the tasseled baton with which generals used to direct their troops. Today it means to take over leadership and command, for example for projects, events or in a company: 〜の采配を振る. Also used as 采配を振るう. Neutral to slightly elevated; 采配 alone often refers to a coach's tactical decisions: 監督の采配が当たった.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"何事も筋を通すことが大切だ。","romaji":"Nanigoto mo suji o tōsu koto ga taisetsu da.","german":"In everything, it is important to follow the proper procedure.","furigana":"なにごと|すじ|とお|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"転職するなら、まず今の上司に筋を通してから話を進めるべきだ。","romaji":"Tenshoku suru nara, mazu ima no jōshi ni suji o tōshite kara hanashi o susumeru beki da.","german":"If you want to change jobs, you should first inform your current boss properly before going further.","furigana":"てんしょく|いま|じょうし|すじ|とお|はなし|すす"},{"japanese":"彼は損をしても筋を通す人だから、信用できる。","romaji":"Kare wa son o shite mo suji o tōsu hito da kara, shin'yō dekiru.","german":"He is a man who sticks to his principles even when it costs him, so he can be trusted.","furigana":"かれ|そん|すじ|とお|ひと|しんよう"}],"notes":"Literally \"to pass a thread through\": 筋 means \"logic, correct procedure, principle\" here. It means to act consistently by principle and decency and to follow the proper channels, for example informing one's superior first before doing something: 〜に筋を通す. The opposite is 筋が通らない \"illogical, not proper\". Positive in tone, typical in business and politics.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ライバルの成功を見て、やる気に火がついた。","romaji":"Raibaru no seikō o mite, yaruki ni hi ga tsuita.","german":"Seeing my rival's success fired up my motivation.","furigana":"せいこう|み|き|ひ"},{"japanese":"SNSで紹介されてから、この店の人気に火がついた。","romaji":"SNS de shōkai sarete kara, kono mise no ninki ni hi ga tsuita.","german":"Since it was featured on social media, this shop's popularity has taken off.","furigana":"しょうかい|みせ|にんき|ひ"},{"japanese":"風が強くて、なかなかろうそくに火がつかない。","romaji":"Kaze ga tsuyokute, nakanaka rōsoku ni hi ga tsukanai.","german":"The wind is so strong that the candle won't light.","furigana":"かぜ|つよ|ひ"}],"notes":"Literally \"fire attaches\": something catches fire or can be lit (ろうそくに火がつく). Figuratively, feelings, arguments or a trend suddenly flare up: やる気に火がつく (\"motivation is fired up\"), 人気に火がつく (\"suddenly becomes popular\"), 議論に火がつく. What starts to burn takes に. It is the intransitive partner of 火をつける (\"to light, to kindle\"): 彼の言葉が私のやる気に火をつけた. The kanji can also be written 火が付く／点く; neutral.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は会社員と作家の二足のわらじを履いている。","romaji":"Kare wa kaishain to sakka no nisoku no waraji o haite iru.","german":"He works as both a company employee and a writer.","furigana":"かれ|かいしゃいん|さっか|にそく|は"},{"japanese":"プロ野球で投手と打者の二足のわらじを履く選手が話題だ。","romaji":"Puro yakyū de tōshu to dasha no nisoku no waraji o haku senshu ga wadai da.","german":"A professional baseball player who plays both pitcher and batter is the talk of the town.","furigana":"やきゅう|とうしゅ|だしゃ|にそく|は|せんしゅ|わだい"},{"japanese":"二足のわらじを履くのに疲れて、会社を辞めて作曲に専念することにした。","romaji":"Nisoku no waraji o haku no ni tsukarete, kaisha o yamete sakkyoku ni sennen suru koto ni shita.","german":"I got tired of juggling two jobs, so I quit my company to concentrate on composing.","furigana":"にそく|は|つか|かいしゃ|や|さっきょく|せんねん"}],"notes":"二足のわらじを履く literally means \"to wear two pairs of straw sandals (わらじ)\". Originally, in the Edo period, it referred to people with incompatible roles, such as gamblers who also worked as police informants; today it means, neutrally to appreciatively, to hold two jobs or roles at once. Typical frame: AとBの二足のわらじを履く. わらじ is usually written in kana, less often 草鞋.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"長年の研究がようやく芽を出し始めた。","romaji":"Naganen no kenkyū ga yōyaku me o dashi hajimeta.","german":"Years of research have finally begun to bear fruit.","furigana":"ながねん|けんきゅう|め|だ|はじ"},{"japanese":"春になって、まいた種が芽を出した。","romaji":"Haru ni natte, maita tane ga me o dashita.","german":"When spring came, the seeds I sowed sprouted.","furigana":"はる|たね|め|だ"},{"japanese":"何年も売れなかった漫画家が、やっと芽を出した。","romaji":"Nannen mo urenakatta mangaka ga, yatto me o dashita.","german":"The manga artist who hadn't sold for years has finally made it.","furigana":"なんねん|う|まんがか|め|だ"}],"notes":"芽 is \"sprout, bud\"; 芽を出す literally means \"to put out a sprout\" (seeds, plants). Figuratively, efforts, talents or projects show first successes, or a previously unknown person makes a breakthrough. The intransitive variant 芽が出る is at least as common, especially in the negative: なかなか芽が出ない (\"just can't get a breakthrough\"). The opposite image is 芽を摘む (\"to nip in the bud\"). Neutral, figurative.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"母は財布の紐が固くて、無駄遣いを許さない。","romaji":"Haha wa saifu no himo ga katakute, mudazukai o yurusanai.","german":"My mother is thrifty and doesn't allow waste.","furigana":"はは|さいふ|ひも|かた|むだづか|ゆる"},{"japanese":"景気が悪いので、消費者の財布の紐が固くなっている。","romaji":"Keiki ga warui node, shōhisha no saifu no himo ga kataku natte iru.","german":"Because of the bad economy, consumers are keeping a tight hold on their money.","furigana":"けいき|わる|しょうひしゃ|さいふ|ひも|かた"},{"japanese":"うちの夫は財布の紐が固いので、旅行の話をしてもなかなか賛成してくれない。","romaji":"Uchi no otto wa saifu no himo ga katai node, ryokō no hanashi o shite mo nakanaka sansei shite kurenai.","german":"My husband is so tight-fisted that he hardly ever agrees when I bring up travel plans.","furigana":"おっと|さいふ|ひも|かた|りょこう|はなし|さんせい"}],"notes":"Literally \"the string of the purse is tight\"; in the past, purses were tied shut with a cord. It means that someone is reluctant to spend money: thrifty to stingy. Related: 財布の紐を締める \"to tighten one's belt\", 財布の紐を緩める \"to spend money more freely\", 財布の紐を握る \"to control the household money\". Neutral; in business news also about consumers: 消費者の財布の紐が固い. Also written 財布のひもが堅い.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ボーナス前で懐が寒い。","romaji":"Bōnasu mae de futokoro ga samui.","german":"It's before the bonus, so I'm short of cash.","furigana":"まえ|ふところ|さむ"},{"japanese":"今月は引っ越しで懐が寒いから、飲み会は遠慮しておくよ。","romaji":"Kongetsu wa hikkoshi de futokoro ga samui kara, nomikai wa enryo shite oku yo.","german":"I'm broke this month because of the move, so I'll pass on the drinking party.","furigana":"こんげつ|ひ|こ|ふところ|さむ|の|かい|えんりょ"},{"japanese":"懐が寒い学生にとって、この定食屋の安さはありがたい。","romaji":"Futokoro ga samui gakusei ni totte, kono teishokuya no yasusa wa arigatai.","german":"For students short on money, the cheap prices at this set-meal restaurant are a blessing.","furigana":"ふところ|さむ|がくせい|ていしょくや|やす"}],"notes":"Literally \"the breast pocket is cold\": 懐 is the fold of the kimono in front of the chest in which money used to be carried. It means that one has little money at the moment; similar is 懐が寂しい, the opposite is 懐が温かい. Typical: 給料日前で懐が寒い. Colloquial and slightly jocular; it describes a temporary situation, not real poverty (for that, 貧乏).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子供の教育には金に糸目をつけない。","romaji":"Kodomo no kyōiku ni wa kane ni itome o tsukenai.","german":"I spare no expense on my children's education.","furigana":"こども|きょういく|かね|いとめ"},{"japanese":"コレクターは金に糸目をつけずに古い切手を買い集めた。","romaji":"Korekutā wa kane ni itome o tsukezu ni furui kitte o kaiatsumeta.","german":"The collector bought up old stamps regardless of cost.","furigana":"かね|いとめ|ふる|きって|か|あつ"},{"japanese":"一生に一度の旅行だから、今回は金に糸目をつけないことにした。","romaji":"Isshō ni ichido no ryokō da kara, konkai wa kane ni itome o tsukenai koto ni shita.","german":"It's a once-in-a-lifetime trip, so this time I decided not to worry about money.","furigana":"いっしょう|いちど|りょこう|こんかい|かね|いとめ"}],"notes":"糸目 are the guide strings of a kite: without them it flies off uncontrolled. 金に糸目をつけない therefore means to spend money without any limit, to spare no expense. Typical are 〜には金に糸目をつけない and adverbial 金に糸目をつけずに買う／集める. The expression is used almost only in the negative; an affirmative form is not usual.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"借金が多すぎて首が回らない。","romaji":"Shakkin ga ōsugite kubi ga mawaranai.","german":"I have so many debts that I'm in financial straits.","furigana":"しゃっきん|おお|くび|まわ"},{"japanese":"店の売り上げが落ちて、店長は借金で首が回らなくなった。","romaji":"Mise no uriage ga ochite, tenchō wa shakkin de kubi ga mawaranaku natta.","german":"Sales dropped, and the shop manager ended up unable to move because of debt.","furigana":"みせ|う|あ|お|てんちょう|しゃっきん|くび|まわ"},{"japanese":"カードで買い物をしすぎると、そのうち首が回らなくなるよ。","romaji":"Kādo de kaimono o shisugiru to, sono uchi kubi ga mawaranaku naru yo.","german":"If you shop too much on your card, you'll soon be up to your neck in debt.","furigana":"か|もの|くび|まわ"}],"notes":"Literally \"the neck cannot turn\": one is so deep in debt that one can no longer move. Almost always linked with 借金: 借金で首が回らない, 首が回らなくなる. Only the negative form is idiomatic; there is also the literal meaning \"cannot turn one's head\", for example with a stiff neck. Colloquial-neutral; similar is 火の車 \"in utmost financial distress\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"旅行で予算から足が出てしまった。","romaji":"Ryokō de yosan kara ashi ga dete shimatta.","german":"I went over budget on the trip.","furigana":"りょこう|よさん|あし|で"},{"japanese":"パーティーの費用が一万円足が出たので、幹事が払った。","romaji":"Pātī no hiyō ga ichiman en ashi ga deta node, kanji ga haratta.","german":"The party cost 10,000 yen more than planned, so the organizer paid the difference.","furigana":"ひよう|いちまんえんあし|で|かんじ|はら"},{"japanese":"安いと思って買ったが、送料で足が出てしまった。","romaji":"Yasui to omotte katta ga, sōryō de ashi ga dete shimatta.","german":"I thought it was cheap when I bought it, but the shipping costs put me over budget.","furigana":"やす|おも|か|そうりょう|あし|で"}],"notes":"Literally \"the feet stick out\", as from under a blanket that is too short. It means that expenses exceed the budget: 予算から足が出る, 一万円足が出た. More rarely it means that a secret comes out; for that, however, 足がつく \"to leave a trail\" is much more common. Neutral and everyday; similar is 赤字になる \"to go into the red\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"食べ放題でたくさん食べて元を取った。","romaji":"Tabehōdai de takusan tabete moto o totta.","german":"I ate a lot at the all-you-can-eat and got my money's worth.","furigana":"た|ほうだい|た|もと|と"},{"japanese":"年会費が高いから、たくさん使わないと元が取れない。","romaji":"Nenkaihi ga takai kara, takusan tsukawanai to moto ga torenai.","german":"The annual fee is high, so unless I use it a lot I won't get my money's worth.","furigana":"ねんかいひ|たか|つか|もと|と"},{"japanese":"店を開くのに大金をかけたが、三年で元を取った。","romaji":"Mise o hiraku no ni taikin o kaketa ga, sannen de moto o totta.","german":"Opening the shop cost a lot of money, but I recouped it in three years.","furigana":"みせ|ひら|たいきん|さんねん|もと|と"}],"notes":"元 here is the invested money, the cost; 取る means \"to take back\". Literally \"to take back what was put in\": to use something so much or earn so much from it that the expense was worth it. Typical with flat prices and investments: 食べ放題で元を取る, 定期券の元を取る. Very common in the potential form 元が取れる／元が取れない. Colloquial to neutral; not to be confused with 元 \"former, original\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ボーナスが入って懐が温かい。","romaji":"Bōnasu ga haitte futokoro ga atatakai.","german":"I got my bonus, so I'm flush with cash.","furigana":"はい|ふところ|あたた"},{"japanese":"今日は懐が温かいから、晩ご飯は私がおごるよ。","romaji":"Kyō wa futokoro ga atatakai kara, bangohan wa watashi ga ogoru yo.","german":"I'm flush today, so dinner is on me.","furigana":"きょう|ふところ|あたた|ばん|はん|わたし"},{"japanese":"競馬で勝って懐が温かいうちに、ほしかったカメラを買った。","romaji":"Keiba de katte futokoro ga atatakai uchi ni, hoshikatta kamera o katta.","german":"While I was still flush after winning at the horse races, I bought the camera I had wanted.","furigana":"けいば|か|ふところ|あたた|か"}],"notes":"Literally \"the breast pocket is warm\": 懐 is the kimono fold in which money used to be carried. It means that one is well off at the moment, often temporarily after payday, a bonus or winnings. Opposite: 懐が寒い／懐が寂しい. Also written 懐が暖かい. Colloquial, often used jokingly as a reason to treat others.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は彼を金づるにしているだけだ。","romaji":"Kanojo wa kare o kanezuru ni shite iru dake da.","german":"She is only using him as a source of money.","furigana":"かのじょ|かれ|かね"},{"japanese":"詐欺グループはお年寄りを金づるにしていた。","romaji":"Sagi gurūpu wa otoshiyori o kanezuru ni shite ita.","german":"The fraud ring had been using elderly people as a source of money.","furigana":"さぎ|としよ|かね"},{"japanese":"親を金づるにするような生活は、もうやめなさい。","romaji":"Oya o kanezuru ni suru yō na seikatsu wa, mō yamenasai.","german":"Stop living like that, treating your parents like a cash cow.","furigana":"おや|かね|せいかつ"}],"notes":"金づる (also 金蔓) is literally a \"money vine\", a vine from which one can pull money, that is, a source of money or a wealthy patron. 〜を金づるにする means to exploit someone as a source of money; passive 金づるにされる. Clearly derogatory and colloquial; neutral words would be スポンサー or 資金源. Mostly written in hiragana.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"給料日前で、財布には一銭も残っていない。","romaji":"Kyūryōbi mae de, saifu ni wa issen mo nokotte inai.","german":"It's right before payday, and there isn't a single sen left in my wallet.","furigana":"きゅうりょうびまえ|さいふ|いっせん|のこ"},{"japanese":"家賃と電気代を払ったら、一銭も残らない。","romaji":"Yachin to denkidai o harattara, issen mo nokoranai.","german":"After paying rent and electricity, nothing is left.","furigana":"やちん|でんきだい|はら|いっせん|のこ"},{"japanese":"旅行で使いすぎて、ボーナスは一銭も残らなかった。","romaji":"Ryokō de tsukaisugite, bōnasu wa issen mo nokoranakatta.","german":"I spent too much on the trip, and not a single sen of my bonus was left.","furigana":"りょこう|つか|いっせん|のこ"}],"notes":"銭 is an old coin unit (one hundredth of a yen) that now survives only in set phrases. 一銭も + negative means \"not a single penny\": 一銭も残らない \"not a penny is left\", typical after expenses: 〜を払ったら一銭も残らない. For the current state one says 一銭も残っていない. Same pattern: 一銭も払わない, 一銭にもならない (\"brings in not a penny\"); in everyday speech 一円も is just as common. Colloquial and emphatic.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は金離れがいいから、よくおごってくれる。","romaji":"Kare wa kanebanare ga ii kara, yoku ogotte kureru.","german":"He is generous with money, so he often treats us.","furigana":"かれ|かねばな"},{"japanese":"あの社長は金離れがよくて、社員旅行の費用も全部出してくれた。","romaji":"Ano shachō wa kanebanare ga yokute, shain ryokō no hiyō mo zenbu dashite kureta.","german":"That president is free-handed and even paid for the entire company trip.","furigana":"しゃちょう|かねばな|しゃいんりょこう|ひよう|ぜんぶだ"},{"japanese":"金離れがいいのはいいが、貯金もしておいたほうがいい。","romaji":"Kanebanare ga ii no wa ii ga, chokin mo shite oita hō ga ii.","german":"Being generous is fine, but you should save a bit as well.","furigana":"かねばな|ちょきん"}],"notes":"金離れ is literally \"parting from money\", that is, the way someone spends money. 金離れがいい means to pay generously and without hesitation, for example for invitations, gifts or tips. Opposite: 金離れが悪い or 財布の紐が固い. Neutral to appreciative and not necessarily wasteful; for that one says 湯水のように使う. Instead of いい, よい is also used; it conjugates via よ-: よくて, よかった.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"後輩に花を持たせてあげた。","romaji":"Kōhai ni hana o motasete ageta.","german":"I let the junior take the credit.","furigana":"こうはい|はな|も"},{"japanese":"部長はわざと会議で発言せず、新人に花を持たせた。","romaji":"Buchō wa wazato kaigi de hatsugen sezu, shinjin ni hana o motaseta.","german":"The department head deliberately stayed quiet in the meeting so the newcomer could shine.","furigana":"ぶちょう|かいぎ|はつげん|しんじん|はな|も"},{"japanese":"孫と将棋をして、最後は花を持たせて負けてあげた。","romaji":"Mago to shōgi o shite, saigo wa hana o motasete makete ageta.","german":"I played shogi with my grandson and let him win in the end.","furigana":"まご|しょうぎ|さいご|はな|も|ま"}],"notes":"Literally \"to let someone hold the flowers\": one leaves the glory or the victory to someone else although one could have shone oneself, usually out of consideration, for example toward juniors, guests or superiors. Structure: 〜に花を持たせる (causative of 持つ). Neutral and positive-modest; typical at work, in sports and in games. Do not confuse with 花を添える \"to add some further beauty to something\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"世論の風向きが変わった。","romaji":"Yoron no kazamuki ga kawatta.","german":"The direction of public opinion has changed.","furigana":"よろん|かざむ|か"},{"japanese":"午後から風向きが変わって、急に寒くなった。","romaji":"Gogo kara kazamuki ga kawatte, kyū ni samuku natta.","german":"The wind changed in the afternoon, and it suddenly got cold.","furigana":"ごご|かざむ|か|きゅう|さむ"},{"japanese":"後半にゴールが決まって、試合の風向きが変わった。","romaji":"Kōhan ni gōru ga kimatte, shiai no kazamuki ga kawatta.","german":"A goal in the second half changed the course of the game.","furigana":"こうはん|き|しあい|かざむ|か"}],"notes":"風向き \"wind direction\" + 変わる \"to change\": literally the wind shifts; figuratively the situation, mood or public opinion turns, often unexpectedly. Usually preceded by 〜の: 世論の風向きが変わる, 試合の風向きが変わる. The transitive form is 風向きを変える (\"to turn the tide\"). Neutral, common in news and sports reports. Reading かざむき (less often かぜむき).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"みんな静かだな。嵐の前の静けさだろうか。","romaji":"Minna shizuka da na. Arashi no mae no shizukesa darō ka.","german":"Alle sind so ruhig. Ob das die Ruhe vor dem Sturm ist?","furigana":"しず|あらし|まえ|しず"},{"japanese":"年末の忙しい時期の前で、今は嵐の前の静けさだ。","romaji":"Nenmatsu no isogashii jiki no mae de, ima wa arashi no mae no shizukesa da.","german":"Kurz vor der hektischen Jahresendzeit herrscht gerade die Ruhe vor dem Sturm.","furigana":"ねんまつ|いそが|じき|まえ|いま|あらし|まえ|しず"},{"japanese":"ファンが黙って試合開始を待っている。まさに嵐の前の静けさだ。","romaji":"Fan ga damatte shiai kaishi o matte iru. Masa ni arashi no mae no shizukesa da.","german":"Die Fans warten schweigend auf den Anpfiff – die reinste Ruhe vor dem Sturm.","furigana":"だま|しあいかいし|ま|あらし|まえ|しず"}],"notes":"Wörtlich „die Stille vor dem Sturm“, genau wie im Deutschen: eine trügerische Ruhe vor einem erwarteten Aufruhr, Streit oder großem Arbeitsandrang. Steht meist prädikativ mit だ, のようだ oder かもしれない, gern verstärkt durch まさに. Neutral, im Alltag auch scherzhaft (Büro vor der Deadline, plötzlich stille Kinder).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"風の便りに聞いたが、彼は結婚したらしい。","romaji":"Kaze no tayori ni kiita ga, kare wa kekkon shita rashii.","german":"I heard through the grapevine that he got married.","furigana":"かぜ|たよ|き|かれ|けっこん"},{"japanese":"昔の同僚が田舎でカフェを開いたと、風の便りに聞いた。","romaji":"Mukashi no dōryō ga inaka de kafe o hiraita to, kaze no tayori ni kiita.","german":"I heard through the grapevine that a former colleague opened a cafe in the countryside.","furigana":"むかし|どうりょう|いなか|ひら|かぜ|たよ|き"},{"japanese":"先生がお元気だと風の便りに聞いて、安心しました。","romaji":"Sensei ga ogenki da to kaze no tayori ni kiite, anshin shimashita.","german":"I was relieved to hear through the grapevine that my teacher is doing well.","furigana":"せんせい|げんき|かぜ|たよ|き|あんしん"}],"notes":"Literally \"to hear by the letter of the wind\": 風の便り is news whose source is unclear and which has somehow been carried to you. Typical for news about acquaintances with whom one no longer has direct contact: 〜と風の便りに聞いた, often with らしい or そうだ. Slightly poetic, but also used in conversation; unlike 噂, it does not sound like gossip but rather nostalgic.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今回の事件は氷山の一角にすぎない。","romaji":"Konkai no jiken wa hyōzan no ikkaku ni suginai.","german":"Dieser Vorfall ist nur die Spitze des Eisbergs.","furigana":"こんかい|じけん|ひょうざん|いっかく"},{"japanese":"報告されたいじめは氷山の一角で、実際はもっと多いと言われている。","romaji":"Hōkoku sareta ijime wa hyōzan no ikkaku de, jissai wa motto ōi to iwarete iru.","german":"Die gemeldeten Mobbingfälle sind nur die Spitze des Eisbergs; tatsächlich soll es viel mehr geben.","furigana":"ほうこく|ひょうざん|いっかく|じっさい|おお|い"},{"japanese":"見つかったバグは氷山の一角かもしれないから、全部のコードを確認しよう。","romaji":"Mitsukatta bagu wa hyōzan no ikkaku ka mo shirenai kara, zenbu no kōdo o kakunin shiyō.","german":"Der gefundene Bug ist vielleicht nur die Spitze des Eisbergs – lass uns den ganzen Code prüfen.","furigana":"み|ひょうざん|いっかく|ぜんぶ|かくにん"}],"notes":"Wörtlich „eine Ecke des Eisbergs“: Wie im Deutschen ist gemeint, dass das Sichtbare nur ein kleiner Teil eines viel größeren, verborgenen Problems ist. Fast immer negativ über Skandale, Betrug, Unfälle oder Missstände: 〜は氷山の一角にすぎない／〜は氷山の一角だ. Neutral bis formell, sehr häufig in Nachrichten. Die Wendung ist ein Nomen und steht mit だ, にすぎない oder かもしれない.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"会議の雲行きが怪しくなってきた。","romaji":"Kaigi no kumoyuki ga ayashiku natte kita.","german":"The meeting is starting to look troublesome.","furigana":"かいぎ|くもゆ|あや"},{"japanese":"雲行きが怪しいから、傘を持っていこう。","romaji":"Kumoyuki ga ayashii kara, kasa o motte ikō.","german":"The sky looks threatening, so let's take an umbrella.","furigana":"くもゆ|あや|かさ|も"},{"japanese":"二人の関係の雲行きが怪しくなっているらしい。","romaji":"Futari no kankei no kumoyuki ga ayashiku natte iru rashii.","german":"The relationship between the two seems to be getting shaky.","furigana":"ふたり|かんけい|くもゆ|あや"}],"notes":"雲行き is \"the movement of the clouds\" and 怪しい is \"suspicious, worrying\". Literally the sky looks like rain; figuratively a situation threatens to go wrong - negotiations, meetings, relationships, the economy. Very common as a development: 雲行きが怪しくなる／怪しくなってきた (\"something is brewing\"). Whose situation is meant takes の: 会議の雲行き. Neutral.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この問題は根が深いので、簡単には解決できない。","romaji":"Kono mondai wa ne ga fukai node, kantan ni wa kaiketsu dekinai.","german":"This problem is deeply rooted and cannot be solved easily.","furigana":"もんだい|ね|ふか|かんたん|かいけつ"},{"japanese":"二つの国の対立は歴史的に根が深い。","romaji":"Futatsu no kuni no tairitsu wa rekishiteki ni ne ga fukai.","german":"The conflict between the two countries has deep historical roots.","furigana":"ふた|くに|たいりつ|れきしてき|ね|ふか"},{"japanese":"あの二人の仲の悪さは根が深くて、少し話し合ったくらいでは直らない。","romaji":"Ano futari no naka no warusa wa ne ga fukakute, sukoshi hanashiatta kurai de wa naoranai.","german":"The bad blood between those two runs deep and won't be fixed by a short talk.","furigana":"ふたり|なか|わる|ね|ふか|すこ|はな|あ|なお"}],"notes":"Literally \"the root is deep\": it refers to problems, conflicts or feelings whose causes reach far back and which are therefore hard to resolve: 根が深い問題, 〜の対立は根が深い. Neutral, also used in the news; equivalent but more written is the single word 根深い. Do not confuse with 根はいい／根は優しい, where 根 means \"basic character\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"退職する時は立つ鳥跡を濁さずの気持ちで引き継ぎをした。","romaji":"Taishoku suru toki wa tatsu tori ato o nigosazu no kimochi de hikitsugi o shita.","german":"When I left the company, I did the handover cleanly.","furigana":"たいしょく|とき|た|とりあと|にご|きも|ひ|つ"},{"japanese":"立つ鳥跡を濁さずと言うから、引っ越しの前に部屋をきれいに掃除した。","romaji":"Tatsu tori ato o nigosazu to iu kara, hikkoshi no mae ni heya o kirei ni sōji shita.","german":"Before moving, I cleaned the room thoroughly, as one should leave properly.","furigana":"た|とりあと|にご|い|ひ|こ|まえ|へや|そうじ"},{"japanese":"キャンプ場を出る時は、立つ鳥跡を濁さずで、ごみを全部持ち帰りましょう。","romaji":"Kyanpujō o deru toki wa, tatsu tori ato o nigosazu de, gomi o zenbu mochikaerimashō.","german":"When we leave the campsite, let's take all the trash back with us and leave no trace.","furigana":"じょう|で|とき|た|とりあと|にご|ぜんぶも|かえ"}],"notes":"Literally \"the bird that takes off does not muddy (the water) behind it\": water birds leave clear water when they fly off. It means to leave a place, a post or a task properly: tidying up, handing over cleanly, leaving no trouble behind. Typical for resignation, moving or departure: 立つ鳥跡を濁さずと言うから／立つ鳥跡を濁さずの気持ちで. Neutral, positive and admonishing; the opposite is 後は野となれ山となれ \"after me, the deluge\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女は批判を柳に風と受け流す。","romaji":"Kanojo wa hihan o yanagi ni kaze to ukenagasu.","german":"She brushes off criticism like a willow in the wind.","furigana":"かのじょ|ひはん|やなぎ|かぜ|う|なが"},{"japanese":"上司に何を言われても、彼は柳に風で気にしない。","romaji":"Jōshi ni nani o iwarete mo, kare wa yanagi ni kaze de ki ni shinai.","german":"No matter what the boss says, he shrugs it off and doesn't mind.","furigana":"じょうし|なに|い|かれ|やなぎ|かぜ|き"},{"japanese":"ネットの悪口なんて、柳に風と受け流すのが一番だよ。","romaji":"Netto no warukuchi nante, yanagi ni kaze to ukenagasu no ga ichiban da yo.","german":"The best way to deal with online insults is to let them roll off you.","furigana":"わるくち|やなぎ|かぜ|う|なが|いちばん"}],"notes":"柳に風 literally means \"wind against the willow\": the pliant willow yields to the wind and does not break. It means calmly letting criticism, mockery or attacks bounce off without fighting back. Typical is 柳に風と受け流す, also 柳に風で気にしない. Depending on the context it sounds appreciative (composed) or slightly critical (won't be told anything).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"部下の意見に耳を傾けるべきだ。","romaji":"Buka no iken ni mimi o katamukeru beki da.","german":"You should listen attentively to your subordinates' opinions.","furigana":"ぶか|いけん|みみ|かたむ"},{"japanese":"首相は記者会見で「国民の声に耳を傾けます」と述べた。","romaji":"Shushō wa kisha kaiken de \"Kokumin no koe ni mimi o katamukemasu\" to nobeta.","german":"At the press conference, the prime minister said, \"We will listen to the voice of the people.\"","furigana":"しゅしょう|きしゃかいけん|こくみん|こえ|みみ|かたむ|の"},{"japanese":"目を閉じて、波の音に耳を傾けた。","romaji":"Me o tojite, nami no oto ni mimi o katamuketa.","german":"I closed my eyes and listened to the sound of the waves.","furigana":"め|と|なみ|おと|みみ|かたむ"}],"notes":"Literally \"to tilt the ear\": to listen attentively and seriously, especially to opinions, requests or criticism: 〜に耳を傾ける. Somewhat more elevated than 聞く and stresses the willingness to engage with the other person; typical in speeches, politics and leadership contexts (国民の声に耳を傾ける). The opposite is 耳を貸さない \"to turn a deaf ear\". Also for music or sounds: 波の音に耳を傾ける.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人の話に口を挟まないでください。","romaji":"Hito no hanashi ni kuchi o hasamanaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't butt into other people's conversations.","furigana":"ひと|はなし|くち|はさ"},{"japanese":"これは家族の問題だから、他人が口を挟むことではない。","romaji":"Kore wa kazoku no mondai da kara, tanin ga kuchi o hasamu koto de wa nai.","german":"This is a family matter, so it's not for outsiders to interfere.","furigana":"かぞく|もんだい|たにん|くち|はさ"},{"japanese":"すみません、ちょっと口を挟んでもいいですか。","romaji":"Sumimasen, chotto kuchi o hasande mo ii desu ka.","german":"Excuse me, may I cut in for a moment?","furigana":"くち|はさ"}],"notes":"Literally \"to wedge in one's mouth\": to interfere uninvited in a conversation or in other people's affairs: 人の話に口を挟む. Mostly reproachful and often negated or used as a request: 口を挟まないで. Similar is 口出しする (to interfere, more general). As a polite opening to briefly interject: ちょっと口を挟んでもいいですか.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女はよく舌が回る人だ。","romaji":"Kanojo wa yoku shita ga mawaru hito da.","german":"She is a very fluent talker.","furigana":"かのじょ|した|まわ|ひと"},{"japanese":"お酒を飲みすぎて、舌が回らなくなってきた。","romaji":"Osake o nomisugite, shita ga mawaranaku natte kita.","german":"I've had too much to drink, and my tongue is getting heavy.","furigana":"さけ|の|した|まわ"},{"japanese":"早口言葉は舌が回らなくて、なかなかうまく言えない。","romaji":"Hayakuchi kotoba wa shita ga mawaranakute, nakanaka umaku ienai.","german":"With tongue twisters my tongue just won't cooperate, and I can hardly say them well.","furigana":"はやくちことば|した|まわ|い"}],"notes":"Literally \"the tongue turns\": to speak fluently and skillfully. Positive or ironic (\"he talks like a waterfall\"), often with よく: よく舌が回る. The negative 舌が回らない is very common: one cannot speak clearly, for example from tiredness, when drunk or with tongue twisters (similar to ろれつが回らない). Colloquial-neutral.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は本音を言わず、いつも言葉を濁す。","romaji":"Kare wa honne o iwazu, itsumo kotoba o nigosu.","german":"He never says what he really thinks and always beats around the bush.","furigana":"かれ|ほんね|い|ことば|にご"},{"japanese":"辞任について聞かれると、大臣は言葉を濁した。","romaji":"Jinin ni tsuite kikareru to, daijin wa kotoba o nigoshita.","german":"Asked about his possible resignation, the minister gave a vague reply.","furigana":"じにん|き|だいじん|ことば|にご"},{"japanese":"テストの結果を聞いたら、息子は「まあまあ」と言葉を濁した。","romaji":"Tesuto no kekka o kiitara, musuko wa \"māmā\" to kotoba o nigoshita.","german":"When I asked my son about his test result, he said evasively, \"Oh, so-so.\"","furigana":"けっか|き|むすこ|ことば|にご"}],"notes":"Literally \"to muddy the words\": to answer deliberately vaguely to avoid a clear statement, out of politeness, uncertainty or because one wants to hide something. Typical: 〜について言葉を濁す, 〜と聞かれて言葉を濁した. Unlike お茶を濁す (to deal with a matter half-heartedly), it refers only to speaking. Neutral; frequent in news about politicians.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"胸に手を当てて考えてみなさい。","romaji":"Mune ni te o atete kangaete minasai.","german":"Put your hand on your heart and think about it.","furigana":"むね|て|あ|かんが"},{"japanese":"なぜ友達が離れていったのか、胸に手を当てて考えてみた。","romaji":"Naze tomodachi ga hanarete itta no ka, mune ni te o atete kangaete mita.","german":"I searched my conscience as to why my friends had drifted away.","furigana":"ともだち|はな|むね|て|あ|かんが"},{"japanese":"胸に手を当てて考えれば、誰が悪いかわかるはずだ。","romaji":"Mune ni te o atete kangaereba, dare ga warui ka wakaru hazu da.","german":"If you search your conscience, you should know who is to blame.","furigana":"むね|て|あ|かんが|だれ|わる"}],"notes":"Literally \"to put one's hand on one's chest\": to reflect and honestly examine one's own conscience. Almost always in the form 胸に手を当てて考える／考えてみる, often as a reproachful demand to someone who does not see their own guilt: 胸に手を当ててよく考えなさい. It can also be meant literally, for example when swearing an oath. Neutral to sternly admonishing.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"旧友と再会して、話に花が咲いた。","romaji":"Kyūyū to saikai shite, hanashi ni hana ga saita.","german":"Meeting my old friend again, we got into a lively conversation.","furigana":"きゅうゆう|さいかい|はなし|はな|さ"},{"japanese":"同窓会では、学生時代の思い出話に花が咲いた。","romaji":"Dōsōkai de wa, gakusei jidai no omoidebanashi ni hana ga saita.","german":"At the class reunion, we chatted enthusiastically about memories of our school days.","furigana":"どうそうかい|がくせいじだい|おも|でばなし|はな|さ"},{"japanese":"アニメ好きが集まって、好きな作品の話に花が咲いた。","romaji":"Anime-zuki ga atsumatte, suki na sakuhin no hanashi ni hana ga saita.","german":"Some anime fans got together, and the talk about their favorite series blossomed.","furigana":"ず|あつ|す|さくひん|はなし|はな|さ"}],"notes":"Literally \"flowers bloom in the conversation\": a conversation becomes lively and animated, usually among friends about shared memories or interests: 思い出話に花が咲く, 〜の話に花が咲く. Almost always told in the past tense (咲いた). Positive and neutral; similar is 話が弾む \"the conversation gets going\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先輩にため口を利くなんて失礼だ。","romaji":"Senpai ni tamekuchi o kiku nante shitsurei da.","german":"It's rude to speak casually to a senior.","furigana":"せんぱい|くち|き|しつれい"},{"japanese":"新人が社長にため口を利いて、みんな驚いた。","romaji":"Shinjin ga shachō ni tamekuchi o kiite, minna odoroita.","german":"The newcomer spoke casually to the company president, and everyone was shocked.","furigana":"しんじん|しゃちょう|くち|き|おどろ"},{"japanese":"同い年なんだから、ため口で話そうよ。","romaji":"Onaidoshi nan da kara, tamekuchi de hanasō yo.","german":"We're the same age, so let's talk casually.","furigana":"おな|どし|くち|はな"}],"notes":"ため口 (often written タメ口) is the casual way of speaking among equals, without です／ます and without keigo; 口を利く means \"to speak, to address someone\". Among friends this is normal, but toward elders, superiors or 先輩 it counts as disrespectful, so 〜にため口を利く usually sounds reproachful. More neutral are ため口で話す and the offer ため口でいいよ (\"you can speak casually with me\"). Colloquial; 利く is often written in kana.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"セールスマンの口車に乗って、高い物を買ってしまった。","romaji":"Sērusuman no kuchiguruma ni notte, takai mono o katte shimatta.","german":"I fell for the salesman's patter and bought something expensive.","furigana":"くちぐるま|の|たか|もの|か"},{"japanese":"知人の口車に乗せられて怪しい投資に手を出し、大金を失った。","romaji":"Chijin no kuchiguruma ni noserarete ayashii tōshi ni te o dashi, taikin o ushinatta.","german":"An acquaintance talked me into a dubious investment, and I lost a lot of money.","furigana":"ちじん|くちぐるま|の|あや|とうし|て|だ|たいきん|うしな"},{"japanese":"彼の口車に乗っちゃだめだよ。いつも調子のいいことばかり言うんだから。","romaji":"Kare no kuchiguruma ni notcha dame da yo. Itsumo chōshi no ii koto bakari iu n da kara.","german":"Don't fall for his smooth talk. He always just says what sounds good.","furigana":"かれ|くちぐるま|の|ちょうし|い"}],"notes":"口車 is skillful, glib talk with which one wins someone over; 口車に乗る means to fall for it and be talked into something. Typical are 〜の口車に乗る and the passive 口車に乗せられる; the transitive counterpart is 〜を口車に乗せる. It is about persuasion or deception, not just praise; for flattery people rather say おだてに乗る.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女に図星を指されて、何も言えなかった。","romaji":"Kanojo ni zuboshi o sasarete, nani mo ienakatta.","german":"She hit the nail on the head, and I couldn't say anything.","furigana":"かのじょ|ずぼし|さ|なに|い"},{"japanese":"「本当は行きたくないんでしょう」と図星を指されて、苦笑いした。","romaji":"\"Hontō wa ikitakunai n deshō\" to zuboshi o sasarete, nigawarai shita.","german":"\"You don't really want to go, do you?\" Caught out, I smiled wryly.","furigana":"ほんとう|い|ずぼし|さ|にがわら"},{"japanese":"母は私の嘘をすぐに見抜いて、いつも図星を指す。","romaji":"Haha wa watashi no uso o sugu ni minuite, itsumo zuboshi o sasu.","german":"My mother sees through my lies immediately and always hits the mark.","furigana":"はは|わたし|うそ|みぬ|ずぼし|さ"}],"notes":"図星 is literally the center of a target, and 指す is \"to point at\". 図星を指す means to hit exactly on someone's hidden thoughts or sore spots. Especially common in the passive from the viewpoint of the person found out: 図星を指される; in conversation, short 図星だ！ \"Bull's-eye!\". Colloquial-neutral; similar to 言い当てる, but 図星 stresses that it is uncomfortable for the person concerned.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"人生何があるかわからない。一寸先は闇だ。","romaji":"Jinsei nani ga aru ka wakaranai. Issun saki wa yami da.","german":"You never know what will happen in life. The future is uncertain.","furigana":"じんせいなに|いっすんさき|やみ"},{"japanese":"業界トップの会社が倒産するなんて、まさに一寸先は闇だ。","romaji":"Gyōkai toppu no kaisha ga tōsan suru nante, masa ni issun saki wa yami da.","german":"For the industry leader to go bankrupt, you really never know what's coming.","furigana":"ぎょうかい|かいしゃ|とうさん|いっすんさき|やみ"},{"japanese":"政治の世界は一寸先は闇と言われるが、今回の選挙結果には誰もが驚いた。","romaji":"Seiji no sekai wa issun saki wa yami to iwareru ga, konkai no senkyo kekka ni wa dare mo ga odoroita.","german":"In politics, they say you never know what's around the corner, but nobody expected this election result.","furigana":"せいじ|せかい|いっすんさき|やみ|い|こんかい|せんきょけっか|だれ|おどろ"}],"notes":"Literally \"one sun ahead is darkness\": 一寸 is an old unit of length of about three centimeters. It means that nobody knows what will happen even in the near future, often with a view to sudden misfortune, but also soberly about politics and business (政界は一寸先は闇). Neutral; mostly stands with だ or と言われる. Related but more optimistic: 明日は明日の風が吹く.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"最後に残ったケーキが一番美味しかった。残り物には福がある。","romaji":"Saigo ni nokotta kēki ga ichiban oishikatta. Nokorimono ni wa fuku ga aru.","german":"The last remaining cake was the most delicious. There's luck in leftovers.","furigana":"さいご|のこ|いちばんおい|のこ|もの|ふく"},{"japanese":"くじを最後に引いたら一等が当たった。残り物には福があるね。","romaji":"Kuji o saigo ni hiitara ittō ga atatta. Nokorimono ni wa fuku ga aru ne.","german":"I drew a lot last and won first prize. There's luck in what's left.","furigana":"さいご|ひ|いっとう|あ|のこ|もの|ふく"},{"japanese":"一番最後でごめんね。でも、残り物には福があるって言うよ。","romaji":"Ichiban saigo de gomen ne. Demo, nokorimono ni wa fuku ga aru tte iu yo.","german":"Sorry you're last. But they say there's luck in what's left over.","furigana":"いちばんさいご|のこ|もの|ふく|い"}],"notes":"残り物 means \"leftovers, remains\" and 福 means \"luck, blessing\": literally \"there is luck in the leftovers\". It is said when someone chooses last, draws a lot or has their turn last - as consolation or encouragement in the sense of \"the best comes last\". Typical when drawing lots or distributing gifts or food, often quoted with って言う. Neutral, friendly and jokingly; variant 余り物には福がある.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"保険に入るのは転ばぬ先の杖だ。","romaji":"Hoken ni hairu no wa korobanu saki no tsue da.","german":"Taking out insurance is a precaution: better safe than sorry.","furigana":"ほけん|はい|ころ|さき|つえ"},{"japanese":"転ばぬ先の杖として、データのバックアップを毎週取っている。","romaji":"Korobanu saki no tsue to shite, dēta no bakkuappu o maishū totte iru.","german":"As a precaution, I back up my data every week.","furigana":"ころ|さき|つえ|まいしゅうと"},{"japanese":"山に行く時は、転ばぬ先の杖だと思って、雨具と地図を必ず持っていく。","romaji":"Yama ni iku toki wa, korobanu saki no tsue da to omotte, amagu to chizu o kanarazu motte iku.","german":"When I go to the mountains, I always take rain gear and a map just in case.","furigana":"やま|い|とき|ころ|さき|つえ|おも|あまぐ|ちず|かなら|も"}],"notes":"Literally \"the cane before one falls\": 転ばぬ is the classical negation of 転ぶ. It means taking precautionary measures before something happens, such as insurance, data backups or an umbrella. Mostly stands with だ or as 転ばぬ先の杖として. Neutral, slightly didactic; similar is 備えあれば憂いなし \"if you are prepared, you have nothing to worry about\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"思い立ったら善は急げで行動しよう。","romaji":"Omoitattara zen wa isoge de kōdō shiyō.","german":"When you get an idea, act on it right away.","furigana":"おも|た|ぜん|いそ|こうどう"},{"japanese":"善は急げだ。今日のうちに申し込もう。","romaji":"Zen wa isoge da. Kyō no uchi ni mōshikomō.","german":"Do good things without delay - let's apply today.","furigana":"ぜん|いそ|きょう|もう|こ"},{"japanese":"いい部屋が見つかったなら、善は急げだよ。すぐ連絡しよう。","romaji":"Ii heya ga mitsukatta nara, zen wa isoge da yo. Sugu renraku shiyō.","german":"If you've found a good room, don't hesitate. Let's contact them right away.","furigana":"へや|み|ぜん|いそ|れんらく"}],"notes":"善 means \"the good\" and 急げ is the imperative of 急ぐ: literally \"With the good - hurry!\". It means: if you plan something good or worthwhile, do it immediately before the opportunity passes. Often used as a prompt to act: 善は急げだ、すぐ〜しよう or 善は急げで早速〜. Similar is 思い立ったが吉日. Neutral, in everyday life often half jokingly.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子供の頃の性格は変わらない。三つ子の魂百までだ。","romaji":"Kodomo no koro no seikaku wa kawaranai. Mitsugo no tamashii hyaku made da.","german":"A childhood personality doesn't change. The child is father of the man.","furigana":"こども|ころ|せいかく|か|み|ご|たましいひゃく"},{"japanese":"祖父は小さい頃から負けず嫌いだったそうで、今もゲームで本気になる。三つ子の魂百までだね。","romaji":"Sofu wa chiisai koro kara makezugirai datta sō de, ima mo gēmu de honki ni naru. Mitsugo no tamashii hyaku made da ne.","german":"My grandfather is said to have been a poor loser since he was little, and even now he takes games seriously. The child is father of the man, isn't it.","furigana":"そふ|ちい|ころ|ま|ぎら|いま|ほんき|み|ご|たましいひゃく"},{"japanese":"三つ子の魂百までと言うから、小さいうちにあいさつの習慣をつけさせたい。","romaji":"Mitsugo no tamashii hyaku made to iu kara, chiisai uchi ni aisatsu no shūkan o tsukesasetai.","german":"They say that what is learned young lasts, so I want her to get into the habit of greeting people while she is small.","furigana":"み|ご|たましいひゃく|い|ちい|しゅうかん"}],"notes":"Literally \"the soul of a three-year-old until a hundred\": 三つ子 means \"three-year-old child\" here, not \"triplets\". It means that character and habits from early childhood last a lifetime, for better or worse. It is not about learning but about the unchangeable core of a person. Neutral, often said with a smile about adults, or as an argument for early upbringing: 三つ子の魂百までと言うから.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ゴルフは下手の横好きだが、毎週通っている。","romaji":"Gorufu wa heta no yokozuki da ga, maishū kayotte iru.","german":"I'm not good at golf, but I love it and go every week.","furigana":"へた|よこず|まいしゅうかよ"},{"japanese":"「ピアノがお上手ですね」「いえいえ、下手の横好きです。」","romaji":"\"Piano ga ojōzu desu ne.\" \"Ie ie, heta no yokozuki desu.\"","german":"\"You play the piano well!\" \"Not at all, I just enjoy it more than I'm good at it.\"","furigana":"じょうず|へた|よこず"},{"japanese":"父は下手の横好きで、三十年も俳句を作り続けている。","romaji":"Chichi wa heta no yokozuki de, sanjūnen mo haiku o tsukuritsuzukete iru.","german":"My father loves writing haiku and has kept at it for thirty years, though he has no great talent for it.","furigana":"ちち|へた|よこず|さんじゅうねん|はいく|つく|つづ"}],"notes":"Literally \"the side-fondness of the unskilled\": 下手 means \"unskilled\", and 横好き is a fondness for something that actually does not suit one. It refers to someone who pursues a hobby with great enthusiasm although they are bad at it. Mostly said modestly about oneself: 下手の横好きで〜を続けています; said about others it sounds mocking. Neutral to modest-polite; the phrase is a noun and stands with だ／です or で.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの時勉強しておけばよかった。後悔先に立たずだ。","romaji":"Ano toki benkyō shite okeba yokatta. Kōkai saki ni tatazu da.","german":"Hätte ich damals gelernt. Aber Reue kommt immer zu spät.","furigana":"ときべんきょう|こうかいさき|た"},{"japanese":"後悔先に立たずと言うから、契約書はサインする前によく読もう。","romaji":"Kōkai saki ni tatazu to iu kara, keiyakusho wa sain suru mae ni yoku yomō.","german":"Hinterher ist es zu spät – lesen wir den Vertrag also genau, bevor wir unterschreiben.","furigana":"こうかいさき|た|い|けいやくしょ|まえ|よ"},{"japanese":"祖母にもっと会いに行けばよかったが、後悔先に立たずだ。","romaji":"Sobo ni motto ai ni ikeba yokatta ga, kōkai saki ni tatazu da.","german":"Ich hätte meine Großmutter öfter besuchen sollen, aber jetzt ist es zu spät.","furigana":"そぼ|あ|い|こうかいさき|た"}],"notes":"Wörtlich „die Reue steht nicht vorne“: Reue kommt immer erst hinterher, wenn es zu spät ist; 立たず ist die klassische Verneinung von 立つ. Gesagt im Rückblick auf eigene Versäumnisse oder als Warnung, vorher gut nachzudenken: 後悔先に立たずと言うから、今のうちに〜. Neutral; im Deutschen etwa „Hinterher ist man immer klüger“ oder „Reue kommt zu spät“.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の話をしていたら本人が来た。噂をすれば影が差すだ。","romaji":"Kare no hanashi o shite itara honnin ga kita. Uwasa o sureba kage ga sasu da.","german":"We were talking about him, and he showed up. Speak of the devil.","furigana":"かれ|はなし|ほんにん|き|うわさ|かげ|さ"},{"japanese":"「田中さん、最近見ないね」「噂をすれば影が差す、ほら、来たよ。」","romaji":"\"Tanaka-san, saikin minai ne.\" \"Uwasa o sureba kage ga sasu, hora, kita yo.\"","german":"\"I haven't seen Mr. Tanaka lately.\" \"Speak of the devil, look, here he comes.\"","furigana":"たなか|さいきんみ|うわさ|かげ|さ|き"},{"japanese":"噂をすれば影が差すと言うけど、本当に部長が部屋に入ってきたので驚いた。","romaji":"Uwasa o sureba kage ga sasu to iu kedo, hontō ni buchō ga heya ni haitte kita node odoroita.","german":"They say \"speak of the devil\", but I was amazed when the department head actually walked into the room.","furigana":"うわさ|かげ|さ|い|ほんとう|ぶちょう|へや|はい|おどろ"}],"notes":"Literally \"if you talk about someone, his shadow falls in\": just when you are talking about a person, he turns up. Usually shortened to 噂をすれば影 or just 噂をすれば… as soon as the person walks in. Colloquial and jocular, equivalent to \"speak of the devil\". 差す is also written 射す or さす.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女の目を見れば気持ちがわかる。目は口ほどに物を言う。","romaji":"Kanojo no me o mireba kimochi ga wakaru. Me wa kuchi hodo ni mono o iu.","german":"You can tell her feelings from her eyes. The eyes say as much as words.","furigana":"かのじょ|め|み|きも|め|くち|もの|い"},{"japanese":"子供は何も言わなかったが、プレゼントをじっと見つめていた。目は口ほどに物を言うとはこのことだ。","romaji":"Kodomo wa nani mo iwanakatta ga, purezento o jitto mitsumete ita. Me wa kuchi hodo ni mono o iu to wa kono koto da.","german":"The child said nothing but stared at the present. This is just what they mean by the eyes saying as much as the mouth.","furigana":"こども|なに|い|み|め|くち|もの|い"},{"japanese":"目は口ほどに物を言うと言うから、面接では相手の目を見て話しなさい。","romaji":"Me wa kuchi hodo ni mono o iu to iu kara, mensetsu de wa aite no me o mite hanashinasai.","german":"They say the eyes speak, so look the other person in the eye when you talk in an interview.","furigana":"め|くち|もの|い|い|めんせつ|あいて|め|み|はな"}],"notes":"Literally \"the eyes say as much as the mouth\": ほど expresses equivalence here (\"to the same degree as\"). It means that looks reveal feelings and intentions just as clearly as words, for example when someone is silent but you can see from the eyes what they think. Neutral, rather cultured and literary; mostly stands as a sentence of its own, with と言う or in 〜とはこのことだ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"仕事を失ったが、新しい出会いがあった。捨てる神あれば拾う神ありだ。","romaji":"Shigoto o ushinatta ga, atarashii deai ga atta. Suteru kami areba hirou kami ari da.","german":"Ich verlor die Arbeit, aber es gab neue Begegnungen. Wenn eine Tür sich schließt, öffnet sich eine andere.","furigana":"しごと|うしな|あたら|であ|す|かみ|ひろ|かみ"},{"japanese":"十社に落ちたけど、捨てる神あれば拾う神ありで、最後に希望の会社から内定をもらった。","romaji":"Jissha ni ochita kedo, suteru kami areba hirou kami ari de, saigo ni kibō no kaisha kara naitei o moratta.","german":"Ich bin bei zehn Firmen abgeblitzt, aber am Ende bekam ich doch eine Zusage von meiner Wunschfirma – irgendwo öffnet sich immer eine Tür.","furigana":"じっしゃ|お|す|かみ|ひろ|かみ|さいご|きぼう|かいしゃ|ないてい"},{"japanese":"捨てる神あれば拾う神ありと言うから、そんなに落ち込まないで。","romaji":"Suteru kami areba hirou kami ari to iu kara, sonna ni ochikomanaide.","german":"Wenn sich eine Tür schließt, öffnet sich eine andere, heißt es – also sei nicht so niedergeschlagen.","furigana":"す|かみ|ひろ|かみ|い|お|こ"}],"notes":"Wörtlich: „Gibt es einen Gott, der einen fallen lässt, so gibt es auch einen, der einen aufhebt“. Das Sprichwort tröstet nach Ablehnung oder Verlust: Wer von den einen abgewiesen wird, findet Hilfe oder Anerkennung bei anderen. Man verwendet es ermutigend mit と言うから／と言うし oder rückblickend mit で. Das deutsche „wenn sich eine Tür schließt, öffnet sich eine andere“ trifft den Sinn gut.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"上司に逆らうな。長い物には巻かれろだよ。","romaji":"Jōshi ni sakarau na. Nagai mono ni wa makarero da yo.","german":"Don't go against your boss. If you can't beat them, join them.","furigana":"じょうし|さか|なが|もの|ま"},{"japanese":"納得できなかったが、長い物には巻かれろで、会社の方針に従った。","romaji":"Nattoku dekinakatta ga, nagai mono ni wa makarero de, kaisha no hōshin ni shitagatta.","german":"I wasn't convinced, but I followed the company line on the principle of going along with those in power.","furigana":"なっとく|なが|もの|ま|かいしゃ|ほうしん|したが"},{"japanese":"長い物には巻かれろという生き方は、私には合わない。","romaji":"Nagai mono ni wa makarero to iu ikikata wa, watashi ni wa awanai.","german":"The way of living by always yielding to the powerful doesn't suit me.","furigana":"なが|もの|ま|い|かた|わたし|あ"}],"notes":"Literally: \"let yourself be wrapped by long things\"; 巻かれろ is the imperative of the passive of 巻く. It means: one cannot prevail against the powerful or one's superiors, so it is better to submit. The expression sounds resigned or cynical and is often rejected (長い物には巻かれろという生き方は嫌だ). Related is 寄らば大樹の陰 (seeking shelter with the powerful).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"思いがけずプレゼントをもらって、まさに棚ぼただ。","romaji":"Omoigakezu purezento o moratte, masa ni tanabota da.","german":"Getting a present unexpectedly is a real windfall.","furigana":"おも|たな"},{"japanese":"相手チームが試合に来なかったので、棚ぼたで決勝に進めた。","romaji":"Aite chīmu ga shiai ni konakatta node, tanabota de kesshō ni susumeta.","german":"The other team didn't show up to the match, so we advanced to the final by a stroke of luck.","furigana":"あいて|しあい|こ|たな|けっしょう|すす"},{"japanese":"先輩が辞めたおかげで、棚ぼた式に課長になった。","romaji":"Senpai ga yameta okage de, tanabotashiki ni kachō ni natta.","german":"Thanks to my senior colleague quitting, I became section chief by sheer luck.","furigana":"せんぱい|や|たな|しき|かちょう"}],"notes":"棚ぼた is the colloquial short form of 棚からぼたもち: literally, a sweet rice cake (ぼたもち) falls from the shelf (棚) into one's mouth, a piece of luck for which one has done nothing. Typical are まさに棚ぼただ, 棚ぼたで〜 and 棚ぼた式に; one says it half jokingly, sometimes slightly disparagingly, about an undeserved gain. The full form is also common, but in conversation one mostly hears the short form.","levelNote":"Rare word according to frequency data (wordfreq), so not a beginner word"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"毎日少しずつ練習すれば上手になる。継続は力なりだ。","romaji":"Mainichi sukoshi zutsu renshū sureba jōzu ni naru. Keizoku wa chikara nari da.","german":"If you practice a little every day, you'll get good. Persistence is strength.","furigana":"まいにちすこ|れんしゅう|じょうず|けいぞく|ちから"},{"japanese":"毎朝ジョギングを続けていたら、マラソンを最後まで走れた。継続は力なりだ。","romaji":"Maiasa jogingu o tsuzukete itara, marason o saigo made hashireta. Keizoku wa chikara nari da.","german":"Because I kept jogging every morning, I was able to run the whole marathon. Persistence is strength.","furigana":"まいあさ|つづ|さいご|はし|けいぞく|ちから"},{"japanese":"教室の壁に「継続は力なり」と書いてある。","romaji":"Kyōshitsu no kabe ni keizoku wa chikara nari to kaite aru.","german":"\"Persistence is strength\" is written on the classroom wall.","furigana":"きょうしつ|かべ|けいぞく|ちから|か"}],"notes":"継続 means \"continuation, sticking with it\", 力 means \"strength\", and なり is the classical copula (equivalent to である). Literally \"sticking with it is strength\": whoever keeps going steadily becomes strong, even if each step is small. A popular motto in school, sports and work, often on posters or as a watchword; in a sentence it usually ends with だ or ですね. Slightly elevated because of なり, but ubiquitous.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"まずは自分で頑張ろう。天は自ら助くる者を助くだ。","romaji":"Mazu wa jibun de ganbarō. Ten wa mizukara tasukuru mono o tasuku da.","german":"First, do your best on your own. God helps those who help themselves.","furigana":"じぶん|がんば|てん|みずか|たす|もの|たす"},{"japanese":"天は自ら助くる者を助くと言うように、努力を続けた彼にやがてチャンスが訪れた。","romaji":"Ten wa mizukara tasukuru mono o tasuku to iu yō ni, doryoku o tsuzuketa kare ni yagate chansu ga otozureta.","german":"As they say, God helps those who help themselves; he kept at it, and eventually his chance came.","furigana":"てん|みずか|たす|もの|たす|い|どりょく|つづ|かれ|おとず"},{"japanese":"祖父の口癖は「天は自ら助くる者を助く」だった。","romaji":"Sofu no kuchiguse wa \"Ten wa mizukara tasukuru mono o tasuku\" datta.","german":"My grandfather's favorite saying was \"God helps those who help themselves.\"","furigana":"そふ|くちぐせ|てん|みずか|たす|もの|たす"}],"notes":"The proverb is the Meiji-era translation of \"Heaven helps those who help themselves\" from Samuel Smiles's \"Self-Help\". 助くる and 助く are classical forms of 助ける: \"Heaven helps those who help themselves\". It encourages making an effort rather than waiting for outside help. The tone is elevated; it is quoted with と言うように or as a life motto.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"何度失敗しても七転八起の精神が大事だ。","romaji":"Nando shippai shite mo shichiten hakki no seishin ga daiji da.","german":"No matter how many times you fail, the spirit of getting back up is important.","furigana":"なんどしっぱい|しちてんはっき|せいしん|だいじ"},{"japanese":"祖父の人生はまさに七転八起だった。","romaji":"Sofu no jinsei wa masa ni shichiten hakki datta.","german":"My grandfather's life was truly one of falling down and getting back up.","furigana":"そふ|じんせい|しちてんはっき"},{"japanese":"「七転八起」を座右の銘にして、会社を立て直した。","romaji":"\"Shichiten hakki\" o zayū no mei ni shite, kaisha o tatenaoshita.","german":"Taking \"fall seven times, get up eight\" as his motto, he rebuilt the company.","furigana":"しちてんはっき|ざゆう|めい|かいしゃ|た|なお"}],"notes":"Literally \"seven (七) falls (転), eight (八) risings (起)\": one falls seven times and gets up eight times. It is the Sino-Japanese reading of the proverb 七転び八起き, which is much more common in daily life; 七転八起 sounds written-language and often appears as a motto. Typical are 七転八起の精神 or 七転八起の人生. Do not confuse with 七転八倒（しちてんばっとう）, which means \"to writhe in pain\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼がチームに入れば鬼に金棒だ。","romaji":"Kare ga chīmu ni haireba oni ni kanabō da.","german":"If he joins the team, it will be unbeatable.","furigana":"かれ|はい|おに|かなぼう"},{"japanese":"料理が上手な母に新しいオーブンがあれば、鬼に金棒だ。","romaji":"Ryōri ga jōzu na haha ni atarashii ōbun ga areba, oni ni kanabō da.","german":"My mother is a good cook, and with a new oven she'd be unstoppable.","furigana":"りょうり|じょうず|はは|あたら|おに|かなぼう"},{"japanese":"英語も中国語も話せるなら、就職には鬼に金棒だよ。","romaji":"Eigo mo Chūgokugo mo hanaseru nara, shūshoku ni wa oni ni kanabō da yo.","german":"If you can speak both English and Chinese, you'll have a great advantage in job hunting.","furigana":"えいご|ちゅうごくご|はな|しゅうしょく|おに|かなぼう"}],"notes":"鬼 is the Japanese ogre and 金棒 an iron-studded club. An already strong ogre gets an iron club on top: someone who is already strong or capable becomes unbeatable through an extra advantage. Mostly predicative in conditional sentences: AがあればBは鬼に金棒だ, AとBが組めば鬼に金棒. Always meant positively; neutral, popular in sports, work and everyday life.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの件には関わらない方がいい。触らぬ神に祟りなしだ。","romaji":"Ano ken ni wa kakawaranai hō ga ii. Sawaranu kami ni tatari nashi da.","german":"You'd better stay out of that matter. Let sleeping dogs lie.","furigana":"けん|かか|ほう|さわ|かみ|たた"},{"japanese":"部長の機嫌が悪い日は、触らぬ神に祟りなしと思って、話しかけないようにしている。","romaji":"Buchō no kigen ga warui hi wa, sawaranu kami ni tatari nashi to omotte, hanashikakenai yō ni shite iru.","german":"On days when the department head is in a bad mood, I try not to talk to him at all.","furigana":"ぶちょう|きげん|わる|ひ|さわ|かみ|たた|おも|はな"},{"japanese":"姉夫婦のけんかには口を出さないことにしている。触らぬ神に祟りなしと言うしね。","romaji":"Ane fūfu no kenka ni wa kuchi o dasanai koto ni shite iru. Sawaranu kami ni tatari nashi to iu shi ne.","german":"I've decided not to meddle in my sister and her husband's quarrels. Let sleeping dogs lie, as they say.","furigana":"あねふうふ|くち|だ|さわ|かみ|たた|い"}],"notes":"Literally \"a god one does not touch brings no curse\": 触らぬ is the classical negation of 触る, and 祟り is the curse of a deity. It means keeping away from delicate matters or moody people so as not to bring trouble on oneself. Mostly added afterwards as a justification: 〜。触らぬ神に祟りなしだ or 触らぬ神に祟りなしと思って. Colloquial-neutral; equivalent to \"let sleeping dogs lie\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"心配だけど、可愛い子には旅をさせよだから一人で行かせた。","romaji":"Shinpai da kedo, kawaii ko ni wa tabi o saseyo da kara hitori de ikaseta.","german":"I'm worried, but you have to let a beloved child go out into the world, so I let him go alone.","furigana":"しんぱい|かわい|こ|たび|ひとり|い"},{"japanese":"可愛い子には旅をさせよと言うし、娘を留学させてみようと思う。","romaji":"Kawaii ko ni wa tabi o saseyo to iu shi, musume o ryūgaku sasete miyō to omou.","german":"They say you should let a beloved child travel, so I'm thinking of letting my daughter study abroad.","furigana":"かわい|こ|たび|い|むすめ|りゅうがく|おも"},{"japanese":"可愛い子には旅をさせよで、息子を厳しいことで有名な寮に入れた。","romaji":"Kawaii ko ni wa tabi o saseyo de, musuko o kibishii koto de yūmei na ryō ni ireta.","german":"Since children grow through challenges, we sent our son to a dormitory famous for its strictness.","furigana":"かわい|こ|たび|むすこ|きび|ゆうめい|りょう|い"}],"notes":"Literally: \"let your beloved child travel\"; させよ is the classical imperative of させる. In the past, travel was arduous and dangerous, so whoever loves their child should let them experience hardship rather than pamper them. It is used when parents let their children go, for example for time abroad, boarding school or training away from home. The kanji 可愛い is often also written in kana: かわいい子には旅をさせよ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"ここまで来たら毒を食らわば皿までだ。最後までやろう。","romaji":"Koko made kitara doku o kurawaba sara made da. Saigo made yarō.","german":"Now that we've come this far, in for a penny, in for a pound. Let's see it through.","furigana":"き|どく|く|さら|さいご"},{"japanese":"ダイエットはもう諦めた。毒を食らわば皿までで、デザートも頼もう。","romaji":"Daietto wa mō akirameta. Doku o kurawaba sara made de, dezāto mo tanomō.","german":"My diet is shot anyway. Might as well go all the way and order dessert too.","furigana":"あきら|どく|く|さら|たの"},{"japanese":"一度不正に手を染めた彼は、毒を食らわば皿までと、さらに罪を重ねた。","romaji":"Ichido fusei ni te o someta kare wa, doku o kurawaba sara made to, sara ni tsumi o kasaneta.","german":"Having once gotten involved in fraud, he went on to commit even more crimes, figuring he might as well.","furigana":"いちどふせい|て|そ|かれ|どく|く|さら|つみ|かさ"}],"notes":"Literally: \"if you are going to eat poison, then also the plate\"; 食らわば is a classical conditional form of 食らう. Originally it means: whoever has once committed a wrong may as well carry it through completely. Today it is often used jokingly, for example when abandoning good resolutions, or in the sense of \"in for a penny, in for a pound\". The tone is colloquial and slightly defiant.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"部長の名前を出して威張るなんて、虎の威を借る狐だ。","romaji":"Buchō no namae o dashite ibaru nante, tora no i o karu kitsune da.","german":"Throwing around the department head's name to act important: that's a fox in a tiger's clothing.","furigana":"ぶちょう|なまえ|だ|いば|とら|い|か|きつね"},{"japanese":"社長の息子だからといって威張る彼は、虎の威を借る狐だと陰口を言われている。","romaji":"Shachō no musuko da kara to itte ibaru kare wa, tora no i o karu kitsune da to kageguchi o iwarete iru.","german":"He swaggers just because he is the president's son, and people badmouth him as a fox borrowing the tiger's might.","furigana":"しゃちょう|むすこ|いば|かれ|とら|い|か|きつね|かげぐち|い"},{"japanese":"大国の後ろ盾を得て強気に出るその国は、虎の威を借る狐のようだ。","romaji":"Taikoku no ushirodate o ete tsuyoki ni deru sono kuni wa, tora no i o karu kitsune no yō da.","german":"That country, which acts boldly with the backing of a great power, is like a fox borrowing the tiger's might.","furigana":"たいこく|うし|だて|え|つよき|で|くに|とら|い|か|きつね"}],"notes":"Literally: \"the fox who borrows the authority (威) of the tiger\", after an old Chinese fable in which the fox walks ahead of the tiger and the other animals flee out of fear of the tiger. It means a small person who relies on the power of someone more powerful in order to put on airs and intimidate others, for example with the name of the boss or one's parents. Typical: 〜は虎の威を借る狐だ, 虎の威を借る狐のように. Not the same as \"to strut in borrowed feathers\" (passing off others' achievements as one's own).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"口で説明するより、結果を見せてくれ。論より証拠だ。","romaji":"Kuchi de setsumei suru yori, kekka o misete kure. Ron yori shōko da.","german":"Statt nur zu reden, zeig mir Ergebnisse. Beweise zählen mehr als Worte.","furigana":"くち|せつめい|けっか|み|ろん|しょうこ"},{"japanese":"論より証拠、このアプリを実際に使ってみてください。","romaji":"Ron yori shōko, kono apuri o jissai ni tsukatte mite kudasai.","german":"Probieren geht über Studieren – testen Sie die App doch einfach selbst.","furigana":"ろん|しょうこ|じっさい|つか"},{"japanese":"「この薬は本当に効くの？」「論より証拠、私はこれで三日で治ったよ。」","romaji":"\"Kono kusuri wa hontō ni kiku no?\" \"Ron yori shōko, watashi wa kore de mikka de naotta yo.\"","german":"„Wirkt das Medikament wirklich?“ – „Der beste Beweis: Bei mir war es damit nach drei Tagen weg.“","furigana":"くすり|ほんとう|き|ろん|しょうこ|わたし|みっか|なお"}],"notes":"Wörtlich „Beweis statt Argument“: Statt lange zu diskutieren, überzeugt ein konkreter Beleg oder eine Vorführung schneller. Typisch, wenn man etwas zeigt statt erklärt, oft wie ein Ausruf am Satzanfang: 論より証拠、実際に見てください. Neutral; bekannt aus den いろはかるた. Im Deutschen am ehesten „Probieren geht über Studieren“.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日のことは忘れよう。明日は明日の風が吹くよ。","romaji":"Kyō no koto wa wasureyō. Ashita wa ashita no kaze ga fuku yo.","german":"Let's forget about today. Tomorrow a different wind will blow.","furigana":"きょう|わす|あした|あした|かぜ|ふ"},{"japanese":"試験に落ちたけど、明日は明日の風が吹く。また頑張ろう。","romaji":"Shiken ni ochita kedo, ashita wa ashita no kaze ga fuku. Mata ganbarō.","german":"I failed the exam, but tomorrow is another day. Let's try again.","furigana":"しけん|お|あした|あした|かぜ|ふ|がんば"},{"japanese":"彼はいつも「明日は明日の風が吹く」と言って、貯金をしない。","romaji":"Kare wa itsumo ashita wa ashita no kaze ga fuku to itte, chokin o shinai.","german":"He always says \"tomorrow is another day\" and doesn't save money.","furigana":"かれ|あした|あした|かぜ|ふ|い|ちょきん"}],"notes":"Literally \"tomorrow the wind of tomorrow will blow\": tomorrow the circumstances will be different, so there is no need to worry unnecessarily today. It is said to console or to switch off after a bad day; depending on tone calm or carefree, similar to \"tomorrow is another day\". Often with よ or さ at the end of the sentence or quoted with と言って. 明日 is read あした here. Colloquial to neutral.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あれだけ助けたのに裏切るなんて、恩を仇で返すとはこのことだ。","romaji":"Are dake tasuketa no ni uragiru nante, on o ada de kaesu to wa kono koto da.","german":"After helping him so much, he betrays me. This is what repaying kindness with spite means.","furigana":"たす|うらぎ|おん|あだ|かえ"},{"japanese":"育ててくれた会社の情報をライバル会社に渡すなんて、恩を仇で返す行為だ。","romaji":"Sodatete kureta kaisha no jōhō o raibaru gaisha ni watasu nante, on o ada de kaesu kōi da.","german":"Handing over information about the company that trained you to a rival firm is an act of repaying kindness with spite.","furigana":"そだ|かいしゃ|じょうほう|がいしゃ|わた|おん|あだ|かえ|こうい"},{"japanese":"お世話になった先生に恩を仇で返すようなことはしたくない。","romaji":"Osewa ni natta sensei ni on o ada de kaesu yō na koto wa shitakunai.","german":"I don't want to do anything that repays my kind teacher with ingratitude.","furigana":"せわ|せんせい|おん|あだ|かえ"}],"notes":"Literally \"to repay a favor with enmity\": 恩 is a favor received, and 仇（あだ） is enmity or harm. It means serious ingratitude, harming or betraying someone who has helped you. Typical: 恩を仇で返すようなことをする, 恩を仇で返すとはこのことだ. Strongly censuring; the opposite is 恩返しをする or 恩に報いる \"to show gratitude\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの噂は本当かもしれない。火のない所に煙は立たぬというから。","romaji":"Ano uwasa wa hontō ka mo shirenai. Hi no nai tokoro ni kemuri wa tatanu to iu kara.","german":"That rumor may be true. They say there is no smoke without fire.","furigana":"うわさ|ほんとう|ひ|ところ|けむり|た"},{"japanese":"政治家は否定しているが、火のない所に煙は立たぬとも言う。","romaji":"Seijika wa hitei shite iru ga, hi no nai tokoro ni kemuri wa tatanu to mo iu.","german":"The politician denies it, but they also say there's no smoke without fire.","furigana":"せいじか|ひてい|ひ|ところ|けむり|た|い"},{"japanese":"あの店は悪い口コミが多い。火のない所に煙は立たぬと言うし、行くのはやめておこう。","romaji":"Ano mise wa warui kuchikomi ga ōi. Hi no nai tokoro ni kemuri wa tatanu to iu shi, iku no wa yamete okō.","german":"That shop has many bad reviews. There's no smoke without fire, so let's not go.","furigana":"みせ|わる|くち|おお|ひ|ところ|けむり|た|い|い"}],"notes":"Literally \"where there is no fire, no smoke rises\": 立たぬ is the classical negation of 立つ; the modern 火のない所に煙は立たない is also used. It means that there is usually something true in a rumor. Typical with と言う／と言うから／とも言う and in connection with 噂 or scandals. Neutral; corresponds exactly to \"no smoke without fire\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"無理して働くな。命あっての物種だよ。","romaji":"Muri shite hataraku na. Inochi atte no monodane da yo.","german":"Don't overwork yourself. Life comes first.","furigana":"むり|はたら|いのち|ものだね"},{"japanese":"家は流されたが、家族は全員無事だった。命あっての物種だ。","romaji":"Ie wa nagasareta ga, kazoku wa zen'in buji datta. Inochi atte no monodane da.","german":"Our house was washed away, but the whole family is safe. As long as we're alive, that's what matters.","furigana":"いえ|なが|かぞく|ぜんいんぶじ|いのち|ものだね"},{"japanese":"天気が悪ければ引き返す勇気も大事だ。命あっての物種だからね。","romaji":"Tenki ga warukereba hikikaesu yūki mo daiji da. Inochi atte no monodane da kara ne.","german":"If the weather is bad, the courage to turn back is also important. After all, without your life, nothing else matters.","furigana":"てんき|わる|ひ|かえ|ゆうき|だいじ|いのち|ものだね"}],"notes":"物種 means \"seed, origin of all things\"; 命あっての物種 therefore means: only someone who is alive can achieve anything at all. It is used to warn against dangerous recklessness or overwork, or to console after a loss in which life was spared (家は失ったが命あっての物種だ). Colloquial, often with だよ／だからね. 物種 is read ものだね.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"最初は嫌だったけど、住めば都で今はこの町が好きだ。","romaji":"Saisho wa iya datta kedo, sumeba miyako de ima wa kono machi ga suki da.","german":"I didn't like it at first, but you get used to anywhere, and now I love this town.","furigana":"さいしょ|いや|す|みやこ|いま|まち|す"},{"japanese":"狭いアパートだけど、住めば都だよ。","romaji":"Semai apāto da kedo, sumeba miyako da yo.","german":"It's a small apartment, but home is where you make it.","furigana":"せま|す|みやこ"},{"japanese":"転勤で田舎に行くのは不安だろうけど、住めば都って言うよ。","romaji":"Tenkin de inaka ni iku no wa fuan darō kedo, sumeba miyako tte iu yo.","german":"You're probably anxious about being transferred to the countryside, but they say you get used to anywhere.","furigana":"てんきん|いなか|い|ふあん|す|みやこ|い"}],"notes":"住めば is the ば-form of 住む (\"if you live there\") and 都 means \"capital\", here the best place of all. Literally \"if you live there, it is the capital\": you get used to any place of residence, and over time you come to like it. Typical with moves, small apartments or transfers, often as 住めば都で… or 住めば都って言うよ. Neutral, comforting.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"千里の道も一歩からだから、まず始めよう。","romaji":"Senri no michi mo ippo kara da kara, mazu hajimeyō.","german":"A journey of a thousand ri begins with a single step, so let's get started.","furigana":"せんり|みち|いっぽ|はじ"},{"japanese":"漢字を二千字覚えるのは大変だけど、千里の道も一歩からだ。今日は五つ覚えよう。","romaji":"Kanji o nisenji oboeru no wa taihen da kedo, senri no michi mo ippo kara da. Kyō wa itsutsu oboeyō.","german":"Memorizing two thousand kanji is tough, but a journey of a thousand ri begins with a single step. Today I'll learn five.","furigana":"かんじ|にせんじおぼ|たいへん|せんり|みち|いっぽ|きょう|いつ|おぼ"},{"japanese":"社長は「千里の道も一歩から」と言って、小さな店から始めた。","romaji":"Shachō wa senri no michi mo ippo kara to itte, chiisana mise kara hajimeta.","german":"The company president said \"a journey of a thousand ri begins with a single step\" and started with a small shop.","furigana":"しゃちょう|せんり|みち|いっぽ|い|ちい|みせ|はじ"}],"notes":"千里 means \"a thousand ri\" (an old unit of length, one ri is about 3.9 km), 一歩 means \"one step\", and も means \"even\": literally \"even a road of a thousand ri begins with one step\", originally from the Daodejing. It means that big goals are only reached by starting small and proceeding step by step. Typical as encouragement before a big undertaking: 千里の道も一歩からだから、まず〜. Neutral, slightly elevated.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"教科書ばかり読まないで、習うより慣れろだよ。","romaji":"Kyōkasho bakari yomanaide, narau yori narero da yo.","german":"Don't just read textbooks; practice beats study.","furigana":"きょうかしょ|よ|なら|な"},{"japanese":"運転は習うより慣れろだから、毎日少しずつ乗るといいよ。","romaji":"Unten wa narau yori narero da kara, mainichi sukoshi zutsu noru to ii yo.","german":"Driving is something you learn by doing, so it's good to drive a little every day.","furigana":"うんてん|なら|な|まいにちすこ|の"},{"japanese":"日本語の会話は習うより慣れろです。どんどん話してみてください。","romaji":"Nihongo no kaiwa wa narau yori narero desu. Dondon hanashite mite kudasai.","german":"Japanese conversation is learn-by-doing. Please try speaking as much as you can.","furigana":"にほんご|かいわ|なら|な|はな"}],"notes":"習う means \"to be taught, to learn\", より means \"rather than\", and 慣れろ is the imperative of 慣れる \"to get used to\". Literally \"get used to it rather than being taught\": skills are acquired through practice, not theory. Often said by teachers, coaches or experienced people, for example about languages, sports or driving. The imperative is part of the fixed saying here and is not impolite; it usually ends with だ／だよ／です.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"六十歳でスカイダイビングとは年寄りの冷や水だ。","romaji":"Rokujussai de sukaidaibingu to wa toshiyori no hiyamizu da.","german":"Skydiving at sixty is a bit much for your age.","furigana":"ろくじゅっさい|としよ|ひ|みず"},{"japanese":"年寄りの冷や水と笑われるかもしれないが、七十歳でフルマラソンに挑戦する。","romaji":"Toshiyori no hiyamizu to warawareru ka mo shirenai ga, nanajussai de furu marason ni chōsen suru.","german":"People might laugh and say I'm too old for this, but at seventy I'm taking on a full marathon.","furigana":"としよ|ひ|みず|わら|ななじゅっさい|ちょうせん"},{"japanese":"おじいちゃん、屋根に上るなんて年寄りの冷や水だよ。","romaji":"Ojiichan, yane ni noboru nante toshiyori no hiyamizu da yo.","german":"Grandpa, climbing onto the roof is far too reckless at your age.","furigana":"やね|のぼ|としよ|ひ|みず"}],"notes":"Literally: \"the cold water of the old\"; an old person who bathes in cold water or drinks cold water harms himself. It means reckless behavior that is no longer appropriate for one's age. Said directly to older people it sounds mocking; the self-ironic use is very common: 年寄りの冷や水と言われるかもしれないが…. Typical are 〜とは年寄りの冷や水だ and 〜なんて年寄りの冷や水だよ.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"全部正直に言う必要はない。言わぬが花だ。","romaji":"Zenbu shōjiki ni iu hitsuyō wa nai. Iwanu ga hana da.","german":"Man muss nicht alles ehrlich sagen. Schweigen ist manchmal Gold.","furigana":"ぜんぶしょうじき|い|ひつよう|い|はな"},{"japanese":"結婚式のサプライズの内容は、言わぬが花だね。","romaji":"Kekkonshiki no sapuraizu no naiyō wa, iwanu ga hana da ne.","german":"Was die Überraschung bei der Hochzeit ist, verraten wir lieber nicht.","furigana":"けっこんしき|ないよう|い|はな"},{"japanese":"彼が昔どんな失敗をしたかは、言わぬが花だろう。","romaji":"Kare ga mukashi donna shippai o shita ka wa, iwanu ga hana darō.","german":"Welche Fehler er früher gemacht hat, behalten wir lieber für uns.","furigana":"かれ|むかし|しっぱい|い|はな"}],"notes":"Wörtlich „nicht zu sagen ist die Blüte“: 言わぬ ist die klassische Verneinung von 言う, 花 steht für das Schönste, Beste. Gemeint ist, dass manche Dinge besser ungesagt bleiben – weil Schweigen taktvoller ist, eine Überraschung nicht verdorben werden soll oder Andeutungen reizvoller sind. Typisch: それは言わぬが花だ. Neutral, leicht gebildet; im Deutschen „Schweigen ist Gold“.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"税金のことは税理士に任せよう。餅は餅屋だから。","romaji":"Zeikin no koto wa zeirishi ni makaseyō. Mochi wa mochiya da kara.","german":"Überlassen wir Steuerfragen dem Steuerberater. Fachleute wissen es am besten.","furigana":"ぜいきん|ぜいりし|まか|もち|もちや"},{"japanese":"自分で直そうとしたが、かえって壊してしまった。やっぱり餅は餅屋だ。","romaji":"Jibun de naosō to shita ga, kaette kowashite shimatta. Yappari mochi wa mochiya da.","german":"Ich wollte es selbst reparieren und habe alles nur noch schlimmer gemacht. Das überlässt man eben besser den Profis.","furigana":"じぶん|なお|こわ|もち|もちや"},{"japanese":"餅は餅屋と言うし、ウェブサイトのデザインはプロに頼もう。","romaji":"Mochi wa mochiya to iu shi, webusaito no dezain wa puro ni tanomō.","german":"Jeder nach seinem Fach – lass uns das Webdesign einem Profi überlassen.","furigana":"もち|もちや|い|たの"}],"notes":"Wörtlich „Reiskuchen (macht) der Reiskuchenladen“: Für jede Sache ist der Fachmann am besten, also überlässt man sie lieber einem Profi, statt selbst herumzuprobieren. Typisch als Begründung: 餅は餅屋だから、専門家に任せよう. Neutral bis umgangssprachlich; im Deutschen am ehesten „Schuster, bleib bei deinen Leisten“ – wobei 餅は餅屋 eher dazu rät, einen Profi zu beauftragen, als jemanden zurechtzuweisen.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"落ち込むな。失敗は成功のもとだよ。","romaji":"Ochikomu na. Shippai wa seikō no moto da yo.","german":"Don't be discouraged. Failure is the mother of success.","furigana":"お|こ|しっぱい|せいこう"},{"japanese":"実験はまた失敗したけど、失敗は成功のもとだと思って、もう一度やってみよう。","romaji":"Jikken wa mata shippai shita kedo, shippai wa seikō no moto da to omotte, mō ichido yatte miyō.","german":"The experiment failed again, but let's treat failure as the foundation of success and try once more.","furigana":"じっけん|しっぱい|しっぱい|せいこう|おも|いちど"},{"japanese":"社長はいつも「失敗は成功のもと」と言って、新しいことに挑戦させてくれる。","romaji":"Shachō wa itsumo \"Shippai wa seikō no moto\" to itte, atarashii koto ni chōsen sasete kureru.","german":"The company president always says \"failure is the foundation of success\" and lets us take on new challenges.","furigana":"しゃちょう|しっぱい|せいこう|い|あたら|ちょうせん"}],"notes":"Literally: \"failure is the origin (もと) of success\" - you learn from mistakes, and they ultimately lead to success. The saying is used mainly to console and encourage, for example after a botched exam or a failed attempt, often with だよ or 「…」って言うでしょ. もと is also written 元 or 基; the variant 失敗は成功の母 sounds somewhat more elevated. Someone who merely uses it to excuse themselves without learning anything, however, seems unconvincing.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"怪我がきっかけで新しい仕事を見つけた。禍を転じて福と為すだ。","romaji":"Kega ga kikkake de atarashii shigoto o mitsuketa. Wazawai o tenjite fuku to nasu da.","german":"Thanks to the injury I found a new job. Misfortune turned into good fortune.","furigana":"けが|あたら|しごと|み|わざわい|てん|ふく|な"},{"japanese":"事故で入院したが、禍を転じて福と為すで、病院で今の妻と出会った。","romaji":"Jiko de nyūin shita ga, wazawai o tenjite fuku to nasu de, byōin de ima no tsuma to deatta.","german":"I was hospitalized after an accident, but it turned out for the best: I met my current wife at the hospital.","furigana":"じこ|にゅういん|わざわい|てん|ふく|な|びょういん|いま|つま|であ"},{"japanese":"工場の火災を機に設備を一新し、禍を転じて福と為すことができた。","romaji":"Kōjō no kasai o ki ni setsubi o isshin shi, wazawai o tenjite fuku to nasu koto ga dekita.","german":"After the fire at the factory, we renewed all the equipment and managed to turn disaster into gain.","furigana":"こうじょう|かさい|き|せつび|いっしん|わざわい|てん|ふく|な"}],"notes":"Literally: \"to turn (転じて) misfortune (禍) and make (為す) it good fortune (福)\": to draw an advantage from a misfortune through one's own action. The more common form in everyday life is 災い転じて福となす (without を, with 災い and なす in kana). It is used in retrospect (…で、禍を転じて福と為すことができた) or as a resolution. The tone is rather elevated; similar is 怪我の功名, which, however, means a more accidental stroke of luck.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"そう言ってしまったら身も蓋もないよ。","romaji":"Sō itte shimattara mi mo futa mo nai yo.","german":"If you put it that way, it's rather too blunt.","furigana":"い|み|ふた"},{"japanese":"「結局、顔が良ければもてるんだよ」なんて、身も蓋もない言い方だね。","romaji":"\"Kekkyoku, kao ga yokereba moteru n da yo\" nante, mi mo futa mo nai iikata da ne.","german":"\"In the end, good-looking people are popular\" is a pretty blunt way of putting it.","furigana":"けっきょく|かお|よ|み|ふた|い|かた"},{"japanese":"「努力しても才能には勝てない」という身も蓋もない結論に、学生たちはがっかりした。","romaji":"\"Doryoku shite mo sainō ni wa katenai\" to iu mi mo futa mo nai ketsuron ni, gakuseitachi wa gakkari shita.","german":"The students were disappointed by the blunt conclusion that effort cannot beat talent.","furigana":"どりょく|さいのう|か|み|ふた|けつろん|がくせい"}],"notes":"Literally \"neither body (of the vessel) nor lid\": everything lies open, nothing remains covered. It refers to a statement so direct and sober that it destroys any subtlety, hope or charm. Typical as a reaction: それを言ったら身も蓋もない, 身も蓋もない言い方. Colloquial and slightly reproachful: possibly accurate in substance, but tactless. Also written 身もふたもない.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一日一善を心がけている。","romaji":"Ichinichi ichizen o kokorogakete iru.","german":"I try to do one good deed every day.","furigana":"いちにちいちぜん|こころ"},{"japanese":"一日一善として、道のごみを拾いました。","romaji":"Ichinichi ichizen to shite, michi no gomi o hiroimashita.","german":"As my good deed for the day, I picked up trash on the road.","furigana":"いちにちいちぜん|みち|ひろ"}],"notes":"一日一善 is the motto of doing one good deed every day. As a noun it stands, for example, with を in 一日一善を心がける, to set oneself this daily goal. The reading is いちにちいちぜん; 一善 here means a good deed. The compound formulates a resolution, not a statement that everyone fulfills it.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"味の分からない私に高級ワインなんて、豚に真珠だよ。","romaji":"Aji no wakaranai watashi ni kōkyū wain nante, buta ni shinju da yo.","german":"Teuren Wein für mich, der keinen Unterschied schmeckt – das sind Perlen vor die Säue.","furigana":"あじ|わ|わたし|こうきゅう|ぶた|しんじゅ"},{"japanese":"本を読まない弟に高い辞書をあげても、豚に真珠だ。","romaji":"Hon o yomanai otōto ni takai jisho o agete mo, buta ni shinju da.","german":"Meinem kleinen Bruder, der nie liest, ein teures Wörterbuch zu schenken, wäre Perlen vor die Säue.","furigana":"ほん|よ|おとうと|たか|じしょ|ぶた|しんじゅ"},{"japanese":"料理に興味がない彼に、プロ用の包丁は豚に真珠だった。","romaji":"Ryōri ni kyōmi ga nai kare ni, puro-yō no hōchō wa buta ni shinju datta.","german":"Für ihn, der sich nicht fürs Kochen interessiert, war das Profimesser völlig verschwendet.","furigana":"りょうり|きょうみ|かれ|よう|ほうちょう|ぶた|しんじゅ"}],"notes":"豚 „Schwein“, 真珠 „Perle“: wörtlich „einem Schwein Perlen“, aus der Bibel übernommen wie das deutsche „Perlen vor die Säue werfen“. Gemeint ist, dass etwas Wertvolles nichts nützt, wenn der Empfänger es nicht zu schätzen weiß – nicht bloß Verschwendung. Gleichbedeutend und typisch japanisch ist 猫に小判. Meist prädikativ: 〜に…をあげても豚に真珠だ. Abwertend gegenüber der betroffenen Person, daher eher über Dritte oder selbstironisch verwenden.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"息子から連絡がないけど、便りのないのは良い便りだろう。","romaji":"Musuko kara renraku ga nai kedo, tayori no nai no wa yoi tayori darō.","german":"I haven't heard from my son, but no news is good news, I suppose.","furigana":"むすこ|れんらく|たよ|よ|たよ"},{"japanese":"検査の結果について病院から連絡がない。便りのないのは良い便りだといいけど。","romaji":"Kensa no kekka ni tsuite byōin kara renraku ga nai. Tayori no nai no wa yoi tayori da to ii kedo.","german":"The hospital hasn't contacted me about the test results. I hope no news is good news.","furigana":"けんさ|けっか|びょういん|れんらく|たよ|よ|たよ"},{"japanese":"心配しすぎだよ。便りのないのは良い便りって言うじゃない。","romaji":"Shinpai shisugi da yo. Tayori no nai no wa yoi tayori tte iu ja nai.","german":"You're worrying too much. They say no news is good news, don't they.","furigana":"しんぱい|たよ|よ|たよ|い"}],"notes":"便り means \"news, letter\": literally \"that no news comes is good news\" - it corresponds to \"no news is good news\". It is said for reassurance when children, friends or an office haven't been in touch for a long time: if something had happened, you would have heard. The の in 便りのない replaces が in the relative clause; also 便りがないのは良い便り, with よい often in kana. Often quoted with って言う or as だろう／と思う. Neutral.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"知らないことに手を出さないほうがいい。","romaji":"Shiranai koto ni te o dasanai hō ga ii.","german":"You shouldn't meddle in things you know nothing about.","furigana":"し|て|だ"},{"japanese":"窓から手を出さないでください。","romaji":"Mado kara te o dasanaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't stick your hand out of the window.","furigana":"まど|て|だ"},{"japanese":"けんかになっても、相手に手を出してはいけません。","romaji":"Kenka ni natte mo, aite ni te o dashite wa ikemasen.","german":"Even if a fight breaks out, you must not lay a hand on the other person.","furigana":"あいて|て|だ"}],"notes":"手を出す has different meanings depending on the context. With an activity, it means to get involved in it or try it. Toward a person, it can mean to become physically violent. With a location such as 窓から, the hand is meant literally. The phrase before the expression, such as 相手に or 窓から, helps distinguish the meanings.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"朝六時に目を覚ました。","romaji":"Asa roku-ji ni me o samashita.","german":"I woke up at six in the morning.","furigana":"あさろくじ|め|さ"},{"japanese":"彼はそのうそに気づき、やっと目を覚ましました。","romaji":"Kare wa sono uso ni kizuki, yatto me o samashimashita.","german":"He realized the lie and finally came to his senses.","furigana":"かれ|き|め|さ"}],"notes":"目を覚ます first means to wake up. The eyes stand as the object before を, even though the person himself or herself wakes from sleep. Figuratively, the expression can also mean to come to one's senses or to see through a false idea. The context decides whether sleep or a realization is meant; 覚ます becomes 覚ました.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"あの人の態度には頭に来る。","romaji":"Ano hito no taido ni wa atama ni kuru.","german":"His attitude makes me angry.","furigana":"ひと|たいど|あたま|く"},{"japanese":"頭に来ても、大声で話さないようにしています。","romaji":"Atama ni kite mo, ōgoe de hanasanai yō ni shite imasu.","german":"Even when I get angry, I try not to speak loudly.","furigana":"あたま|き|おおごえ|はな"}],"notes":"頭に来る is a colloquial expression for getting angry. The head is not an actual place of movement here. The cause can be given with に, as in あの人の態度には. In the past tense it is 頭に来た（あたまにきた）. The expression clearly conveys personal annoyance and is less suited to a factual report.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"赤いドレスが目を引いた。","romaji":"Akai doresu ga me o hiita.","german":"The red dress caught the eye.","furigana":"あか|め|ひ"},{"japanese":"店は、客の目を引くために大きな看板を出しました。","romaji":"Mise wa, kyaku no me o hiku tame ni ōkina kanban o dashimashita.","german":"The store put up a large sign to attract customers' attention.","furigana":"みせ|きゃく|め|ひ|おお|かんばん|だ"}],"notes":"目を引く means to attract attention. The eye-catching object stands as the subject before が, as in 赤いドレスが目を引いた. Whose attention is meant can be given with 客の目. Unlike looking at something, the expression describes an object that stands out or is designed so that others notice it.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"試験の結果が気になる。","romaji":"Shiken no kekka ga ki ni naru.","german":"I'm curious about the exam results.","furigana":"しけん|けっか|き"},{"japanese":"子供の帰りが遅いのが気になります。","romaji":"Kodomo no kaeri ga osoi no ga ki ni narimasu.","german":"It worries me that the child comes home late.","furigana":"こども|かえ|おそ|き"}],"notes":"気になる means that something occupies one's attention. With an exam result it can mean curiosity or anxious anticipation; with a child coming home late, rather worry. The object stands before が. This is not the same as 気に入る, which expresses liking: even something unpleasant can be 気になる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この荷物が邪魔になっている。","romaji":"Kono nimotsu ga jama ni natte iru.","german":"This luggage is in the way.","furigana":"にもつ|じゃま"},{"japanese":"話し声が勉強の邪魔になるので、静かにしてください。","romaji":"Hanashigoe ga benkyō no jama ni naru node, shizuka ni shite kudasai.","german":"Voices are disturbing my studying, so please be quiet.","furigana":"はな|ごえ|べんきょう|じゃま|しず"}],"notes":"邪魔になる means that something stands in the way or hinders an activity. A piece of luggage can block the path physically; 話し声 can disturb studying. 邪魔になっている means the obstruction is happening right now. The person or activity that is disturbed can be named with の or に. Unlike 邪魔する, the expression describes the obstacle itself.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"友達の手を借りて引っ越しをした。","romaji":"Tomodachi no te o karite hikkoshi o shita.","german":"I got a friend's help with moving.","furigana":"ともだち|て|か|ひ|こ"},{"japanese":"一人ではできないので、少し手を借りてもいいですか。","romaji":"Hitori de wa dekinai node, sukoshi te o karite mo ii desu ka.","german":"I can't do it alone, so may I ask for a little help?","furigana":"ひとり|すこ|て|か"}],"notes":"手を借りる uses \"hand\" figuratively for a person's help. With 友達の手, you name whose support you are using. The te-form 手を借りて can connect to the action in which help is given. The polite question 手を借りてもいいですか asks for assistance and does not mean that one borrows a hand as an object.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"変な噂が耳に入った。","romaji":"Hen na uwasa ga mimi ni haitta.","german":"A strange rumor reached my ears.","furigana":"へん|うわさ|みみ|はい"},{"japanese":"プールで泳いでいる時、水が耳に入りました。","romaji":"Pūru de oyoide iru toki, mizu ga mimi ni hairimashita.","german":"While swimming in the pool, water got into my ear.","furigana":"およ|とき|みず|みみ|はい"}],"notes":"耳に入る can mean figuratively that news or a rumor reaches you. It does not say that you deliberately asked for it. With water, the literal use is possible: it gets into the ear. What reaches you or gets into the ear stands before が; 入る becomes 入った in the past tense.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"きれいな花が目に入った。","romaji":"Kirei na hana ga me ni haitta.","german":"A beautiful flower caught my eye.","furigana":"はな|め|はい"},{"japanese":"財布を探していたら、机の下の鍵が目に入りました。","romaji":"Saifu o sagashite itara, tsukue no shita no kagi ga me ni hairimashita.","german":"While looking for my wallet, I noticed the key under the desk.","furigana":"さいふ|さが|つくえ|した|かぎ|め|はい"}],"notes":"目に入る means that you notice something or it comes into view, often without explicitly looking for it. The perceived object stands before が. It can be a lovely sight while walking by or something else that stands out during a search. The somewhat similar expression 目を引く, by contrast, emphasizes that something attracts attention.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"質問がある人は手を上げてください。","romaji":"Shitsumon ga aru hito wa te o agete kudasai.","german":"Those who have a question, please raise your hand.","furigana":"しつもん|ひと|て|あ"},{"japanese":"相手の方が強く、私はついに手を上げました。","romaji":"Aite no hō ga tsuyoku, watashi wa tsui ni te o agemashita.","german":"The other side was stronger, and I finally gave up.","furigana":"あいて|ほう|つよ|わたし|て|あ"},{"japanese":"子供に手を上げてはいけません。","romaji":"Kodomo ni te o agete wa ikemasen.","german":"You must not hit a child.","furigana":"こども|て|あ"}],"notes":"手を上げる can literally mean to raise a hand, for example to ask a question. In a conflict, it can also mean to give up or surrender. With a person before に, it often means that someone is about to strike or becomes physically violent toward them. The context is therefore decisive; 上げる is an ichidan verb and becomes 上げて.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この魚は足が速いから早く食べて。","romaji":"Kono sakana wa ashi ga hayai kara hayaku tabete.","german":"This fish spoils quickly, so eat it soon.","furigana":"さかな|あし|はや|はや|た"},{"japanese":"足が速い友達と一緒に走りました。","romaji":"Ashi ga hayai tomodachi to issho ni hashirimashita.","german":"I ran with a friend who is a fast runner.","furigana":"あし|はや|ともだち|いっしょ|はし"}],"notes":"For people, 足が速い means to be able to run or walk fast. For food, the identically read expression can mean that it spoils quickly; for this, the spelling 足が早い is especially common. The sentence about the fish therefore describes its short shelf life, not its swimming speed. In the first sentence, the additional 早く食べて means \"eat it soon\"; this 早く refers to the timing of eating.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お客さんに気を使って疲れた。","romaji":"Okyaku-san ni ki o tsukatte tsukareta.","german":"I got tired from being so attentive to the guests.","furigana":"きゃく|き|つか|つか"},{"japanese":"そんなに気を使わないでください。","romaji":"Sonna ni ki o tsukawanaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't go to so much trouble.","furigana":"き|つか"}],"notes":"気を使う describes attentive consideration for others, often with the effort of not causing anyone trouble. に names the person you are looking after. 気を使わないでください can mean in a friendly way: please don't go to any trouble. The godan verb 使う becomes 使って and 使わない; the expression keeps を.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"毎朝顔を洗ってから朝ご飯を食べる。","romaji":"Maiasa kao o aratte kara asa-gohan o taberu.","german":"Every morning I wash my face before eating breakfast.","furigana":"まいあさかお|あら|あさ|はん|た"},{"japanese":"顔に泥が付いたので、顔を洗いました。","romaji":"Kao ni doro ga tsuita node, kao o araimashita.","german":"I had mud on my face, so I washed it.","furigana":"かお|どろ|つ|かお|あら"}],"notes":"顔を洗う is an everyday action: to wash one's face. 顔 stands before を as the body part being washed; that it is one's own is normally clear from the context. 洗う is a godan verb: 洗います, 洗って and 洗いました. With 洗ってから you can present the washing as a step before another action.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"会議で彼の名前を出さないでください。","romaji":"Kaigi de kare no namae o dasanaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't mention his name in the meeting.","furigana":"かいぎ|かれ|なまえ|だ"},{"japanese":"先生はみんなの前で私の名前を出して、ほめてくれました。","romaji":"Sensei wa minna no mae de watashi no namae o dashite, homete kuremashita.","german":"The teacher mentioned my name in front of everyone and praised me.","furigana":"せんせい|まえ|わたし|なまえ|だ"}],"notes":"名前を出す means to mention a name in conversation or bring it up. The person can stand before 名前 with の, as in 彼の名前. 会議で names the occasion at which the name is to come up. 出さないでください asks that it be left unmentioned; 出す is a godan verb here, becoming 出さない or 出して.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"困った時に力になってくれた。","romaji":"Komatta toki ni chikara ni natte kureta.","german":"He helped me when I was in trouble.","furigana":"こま|とき|ちから"},{"japanese":"何か困ったことがあれば、力になります。","romaji":"Nanika komatta koto ga areba, chikara ni narimasu.","german":"If you have any problems, I'll help you.","furigana":"なに|こま|ちから"}],"notes":"力になる means to stand by someone and be helpful. 誰かの力になる names the person supported; 力 here means the help they can rely on. なってくれた expresses that someone gave this help for the speaker's benefit. 力になります is suitable as an offer of support.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"この仕事は時間がかかる。","romaji":"Kono shigoto wa jikan ga kakaru.","german":"This work takes time.","furigana":"しごと|じかん"},{"japanese":"新しい機械を使えば、あまり時間がかかりません。","romaji":"Atarashii kikai o tsukaeba, amari jikan ga kakarimasen.","german":"If you use the new machine, it doesn't take very long.","furigana":"あたら|きかい|つか|じかん"}],"notes":"時間がかかる states the time a task requires. Without further qualification, the phrase can mean that something takes a long time; 少し or あまり changes this assessment. あまり時間がかかりません means it doesn't take very long. かかる is a godan verb with かかります and かかった; in the basic construction 時間 stands before が.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"子供の教育にはお金がかかる。","romaji":"Kodomo no kyōiku ni wa okane ga kakaru.","german":"Educating children costs a lot of money.","furigana":"こども|きょういく|かね"},{"japanese":"この公園に入るのに、お金はかかりません。","romaji":"Kono kōen ni hairu no ni, okane wa kakarimasen.","german":"It doesn't cost anything to enter this park.","furigana":"こうえん|はい|かね"}],"notes":"お金がかかる describes that money is needed for something. The activity or thing can be named with に or には, as in 子供の教育には. In a negative statement, お金はかかりません can explicitly say that no costs arise. かかる becomes かかります and かかりません; お金 is the money required.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"先生に返事をしなさい。","romaji":"Sensei ni henji o shinasai.","german":"Answer the teacher.","furigana":"せんせい|へんじ"},{"japanese":"明日、メールで返事をします。","romaji":"Ashita, mēru de henji o shimasu.","german":"I'll reply by email tomorrow.","furigana":"あした|へんじ"}],"notes":"返事をする means to give a reply, orally or in writing. に names the recipient, while メールで gives the medium. 先生に返事をしなさい is a direct command from a person in authority; for a polite request, 返事をしてください is suitable. The verb する becomes します, して and しなさい.","levelNote":"Derived from 返事 (N4)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"田中さんは席を外しています。","romaji":"Tanaka-san wa seki o hazushite imasu.","german":"Mr. Tanaka is away from his desk.","furigana":"たなか|せき|はず"},{"japanese":"電話をかけるので、少し席を外してもいいですか。","romaji":"Denwa o kakeru node, sukoshi seki o hazushite mo ii desu ka.","german":"I need to make a phone call, so may I step away for a moment?","furigana":"でんわ|すこ|せき|はず"}],"notes":"席を外す means to temporarily leave one's own seat, for example during a meeting or at one's workplace. 席を外しています often describes that someone is currently away from their desk and not reachable. With 外してもいいですか you ask permission to step away briefly. 外す is a godan verb, becoming 外して and 外しました.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"駅への道を聞いた。","romaji":"Eki e no michi o kiita.","german":"I asked for directions to the station.","furigana":"えき|みち|き"},{"japanese":"道を聞く時は、地図を見せると便利です。","romaji":"Michi o kiku toki wa, chizu o miseru to benri desu.","german":"When asking for directions, it's handy to show a map.","furigana":"みち|き|とき|ちず|み|べんり"}],"notes":"道を聞く means to ask for directions. The destination can stand before への道, as in 駅への道; with 人に you name the person being asked. 聞く means \"to ask\" in this combination. It is a godan verb: 聞きます, 聞いて and 聞いた. A map can help show which way you are asking about.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"母に電話をかけた。","romaji":"Haha ni denwa o kaketa.","german":"I called my mother.","furigana":"はは|でんわ"},{"japanese":"ここでは電話をかけないでください。","romaji":"Koko de wa denwa o kakenaide kudasai.","german":"Please don't make phone calls here.","furigana":"でんわ"}],"notes":"電話をかける means making a call. に names the person or place called, for example 母に. 電話 stands before を; かける is an ichidan verb and becomes かけます, かけて or かけない. Here では can emphasize the place where calls should not be made.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki I, lesson 6"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"お客さんにお茶を入れた。","romaji":"Okyaku-san ni ocha o ireta.","german":"I made tea for the guests.","furigana":"きゃく|ちゃ|い"},{"japanese":"お茶を入れる間、ここで待っていてください。","romaji":"Ocha o ireru aida, koko de matte ite kudasai.","german":"Please wait here while I make the tea.","furigana":"ちゃ|い|あいだ|ま"}],"notes":"お茶を入れる means to prepare tea. お客さんに names the people the tea is made for; お茶 remains the object before を. 入れる is an ichidan verb here, with 入れます and 入れて. お茶を入れる間 names the span of time during which one is preparing the tea.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"約束を守ることは大切だ。","romaji":"Yakusoku o mamoru koto wa taisetsu da.","german":"It is important to keep promises.","furigana":"やくそく|まも|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"雨でしたが、約束を守って友達に会いに行きました。","romaji":"Ame deshita ga, yakusoku o mamotte tomodachi ni ai ni ikimashita.","german":"It was raining, but I kept my promise and went to meet my friend.","furigana":"あめ|やくそく|まも|ともだち|あ|い"}],"notes":"約束を守る means to stick to a promise. 約束 is the object before を; with 約束を守ること the action becomes the topic as the keeping of promises. 守る is a godan verb: 守ります and 守って. The expression can describe a general rule or a personal promise that was actually kept.","levelNote":"Textbook: Genki II, lesson 13"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"一緒に写真を撮りましょう。","romaji":"Issho ni shashin o torimashō.","german":"Let's take a photo together.","furigana":"いっしょ|しゃしん|と"},{"japanese":"この美術館では、写真を撮ってもいいですか。","romaji":"Kono bijutsukan de wa, shashin o totte mo ii desu ka.","german":"May I take photos in this art museum?","furigana":"びじゅつかん|しゃしん|と"}],"notes":"写真を撮る is the usual combination for taking a photo. 写真 stands before を; 撮る is a godan verb, becoming 撮ります and 撮って. 撮りましょう invites someone to take a photo, while 撮ってもいいですか asks for permission. With 一緒に it can mean that you want to be in the picture together.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"毎朝公園で散歩をする。","romaji":"Maiasa kōen de sanpo o suru.","german":"Every morning I take a walk in the park.","furigana":"まいあさこうえん|さんぽ"},{"japanese":"雨がやんだら、少し散歩をしましょう。","romaji":"Ame ga yandara, sukoshi sanpo o shimashō.","german":"Let's take a short walk once the rain stops.","furigana":"あめ|すこ|さんぽ"}],"notes":"散歩をする describes taking a walk. The place usually stands before で, for example 公園で; a time expression can come first. 散歩 is a noun that expresses an activity here with を and する. 散歩をしましょう is an invitation to go for a walk. The shorter 散歩する is also common.","levelNote":"Derived from 散歩 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"週末にスーパーで買い物をする。","romaji":"Shūmatsu ni sūpā de kaimono o suru.","german":"On weekends I go shopping at the supermarket.","furigana":"しゅうまつ|か|もの"},{"japanese":"夜は店が閉まっているので、インターネットで買い物をしました。","romaji":"Yoru wa mise ga shimatte iru node, intānetto de kaimono o shimashita.","german":"The shops are closed at night, so I shopped on the internet.","furigana":"よる|みせ|し|か|もの"}],"notes":"買い物をする means to shop or to run errands. The place or medium stands before で: スーパーで names the supermarket, インターネットで shopping on the internet. 買い物 is the noun and する the verb; here they are linked by を. Besides regular shopping, the expression can also refer to a single purchase made.","levelNote":"Derived from 買い物 (N5)"},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"寒いから風邪を引かないようにね。","romaji":"Samui kara kaze o hikanai yō ni ne.","german":"It's cold, so be careful not to catch a cold.","furigana":"さむ|かぜ|ひ"},{"japanese":"風邪を引いたので、昨日は会社を休みました。","romaji":"Kaze o hiita node, kinō wa kaisha o yasumimashita.","german":"I caught a cold, so I took yesterday off from work.","furigana":"かぜ|ひ|きのう|かいしゃ|やす"}],"notes":"風邪を引く means to catch a cold. 風邪 stands before を as the illness; 引く is a godan verb and becomes 引いた, 引きます or 引かない. 風邪を引かないようにね is a caring request between close people. With 風邪を引いたので you can explain why you skipped an activity.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"心を込めて手紙を書いた。","romaji":"Kokoro o komete tegami o kaita.","german":"I wrote the letter with all my heart.","furigana":"こころ|こ|てがみ|か"},{"japanese":"母のために心を込めて料理を作りました。","romaji":"Haha no tame ni kokoro o komete ryōri o tsukurimashita.","german":"I cooked for my mother with a lot of love.","furigana":"はは|こころ|こ|りょうり|つく"},{"japanese":"心を込めて歌えば、気持ちはきっと伝わるよ。","romaji":"Kokoro o komete utaeba, kimochi wa kitto tsutawaru yo.","german":"If you sing with all your heart, your feelings will surely come across.","furigana":"こころ|こ|うた|きも|つた"}],"notes":"Literally \"to put the heart (心) into it (込める)\": to do something with devotion and genuine feelings. Mostly adverbial in the て-form: 心を込めて書く／作る／歌う, or before a noun: 心を込めた料理. The intransitive counterpart shows in 心のこもったプレゼント \"a gift that comes from the heart\". Similar is 気持ちを込める. Also in kana: 心をこめて.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"会社は新商品の開発に力を入れている。","romaji":"Kaisha wa shin-shōhin no kaihatsu ni chikara o irete iru.","german":"The company is focusing on developing new products.","furigana":"かいしゃ|しんしょうひん|かいはつ|ちから|い"},{"japanese":"このビンのふた、力を入れても開かないよ。","romaji":"Kono bin no futa, chikara o irete mo akanai yo.","german":"This jar's lid won't open, even if I put force into it.","furigana":"ちから|い|あ"},{"japanese":"今年は日本語の勉強に力を入れたい。","romaji":"Kotoshi wa nihongo no benkyō ni chikara o iretai.","german":"This year I want to put more effort into studying Japanese.","furigana":"ことし|にほんご|べんきょう|ちから|い"}],"notes":"Literally \"to put strength (力) into it (入れる)\". Physically it means \"to apply force, to tense\": 手に力を入れる. Figuratively, the target is marked with に: 〜に力を入れる \"to place special emphasis on, to put particular effort into\" - typical for companies, schools or personal resolutions. The opposite is 力を抜く \"to ease off\". Intransitively it is 力が入る: 肩に力が入る \"to be tense\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"努力が新しい道を開いた。","romaji":"Doryoku ga atarashii michi o hiraita.","german":"The effort opened up a new path.","furigana":"どりょく|あたら|みち|ひら"},{"japanese":"この発見が新しい薬の開発への道を開いた。","romaji":"Kono hakken ga atarashii kusuri no kaihatsu e no michi o hiraita.","german":"This discovery paved the way for the development of new drugs.","furigana":"はっけん|あたら|くすり|かいはつ|みち|ひら"},{"japanese":"村の人たちが雪をどけて、道を開いてくれた。","romaji":"Mura no hitotachi ga yuki o dokete, michi o hiraite kureta.","german":"The villagers cleared the snow and opened the road for us.","furigana":"むら|ひと|ゆき|みち|ひら"}],"notes":"Literally \"to open a path\": concretely to clear a path, figuratively \"to pave the way, to open up new possibilities\". Often 〜への道を開く or 〜に道を開く; the subject is often an achievement or discovery. 開く is read ひらく here, not あく; sometimes also written 拓く. Intransitively it is 道が開ける \"a path opens up\". Rather elevated, written style (news, speeches).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は話が早いから助かる。","romaji":"Kare wa hanashi ga hayai kara tasukaru.","german":"He is quick on the uptake, which is a big help.","furigana":"かれ|はなし|はや|たす"},{"japanese":"え、田中さんを知っているんですか。それなら話が早い。","romaji":"E, Tanaka-san o shitte iru n desu ka. Sore nara hanashi ga hayai.","german":"Oh, you know Mr. Tanaka? Then this is easy.","furigana":"たなか|し|はなし|はや"},{"japanese":"直接部長に聞いたほうが話が早いよ。","romaji":"Chokusetsu buchō ni kiita hō ga hanashi ga hayai yo.","german":"It's quicker if you ask the department head directly.","furigana":"ちょくせつぶちょう|き|はなし|はや"}],"notes":"Literally \"the talk is quick\": a matter can be settled without long explanations, because the other person understands right away or the situation is favorable. Very common: それなら話が早い \"Then it's easy\" and 話が早くて助かる as praise for someone who catches on quickly. Do not confuse it with \"speaking fast\" (早口) or 話が長い \"to be long-winded\". Colloquial to business-neutral.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"失敗して上司に頭を下げた。","romaji":"Shippai shite jōshi ni atama o sageta.","german":"After making a mistake, I apologized to my boss.","furigana":"しっぱい|じょうし|あたま|さ"},{"japanese":"お金を借りるために、兄に頭を下げて頼んだ。","romaji":"Okane o kariru tame ni, ani ni atama o sagete tanonda.","german":"To borrow money, I humbly asked my older brother.","furigana":"かね|か|あに|あたま|さ|たの"},{"japanese":"生徒たちは先生に頭を下げて「ありがとうございました」と言った。","romaji":"Seitotachi wa sensei ni atama o sagete \"arigatō gozaimashita\" to itta.","german":"The students bowed to the teacher and said, \"Thank you very much.\"","furigana":"せいと|せんせい|あたま|さ|い"}],"notes":"Literally \"to lower the head\": to bow, and figuratively to apologize or to humbly ask for something (頭を下げて頼む). The person is marked with に: 上司に頭を下げる. While お辞儀をする is the neutral polite bow, 頭を下げる emphasizes apology, a request or subordination. Note: the intransitive 頭が下がる means \"to feel respect\" (彼の努力には頭が下がる).","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"書類に目を通してください。","romaji":"Shorui ni me o tōshite kudasai.","german":"Please look over the documents.","furigana":"しょるい|め|とお"},{"japanese":"毎朝、新聞にさっと目を通してから出かけます。","romaji":"Maiasa, shinbun ni satto me o tōshite kara dekakemasu.","german":"Every morning I skim the newspaper before I leave the house.","furigana":"まいあさ|しんぶん|め|とお|で"},{"japanese":"テストの前に、もう一度ノートに目を通した。","romaji":"Tesuto no mae ni, mō ichido nōto ni me o tōshita.","german":"Before the exam, I looked over my notes once more.","furigana":"まえ|いちど|め|とお"}],"notes":"Literally \"to pass the eyes through\": to look through or skim something without studying it thoroughly. The particle に is important: 書類に目を通す, not を. Very common at work: 目を通しておいてください; very polite お目通しください. In contrast to 読む, 目を通す emphasizes quickly checking the content, not reading thoroughly.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"問題が大きくなる前に手を打とう。","romaji":"Mondai ga ōkiku naru mae ni te o utō.","german":"Let's take action before the problem gets bigger.","furigana":"もんだい|おお|まえ|て|う"},{"japanese":"じゃあ、五千円で手を打ちましょう。","romaji":"Jā, gosen'en de te o uchimashō.","german":"All right, let's settle on 5,000 yen.","furigana":"ごせんえん|て|う"},{"japanese":"いい考えが浮かんで、思わず手を打った。","romaji":"Ii kangae ga ukande, omowazu te o utta.","german":"A good idea occurred to me, and I clapped my hands without thinking.","furigana":"かんが|う|おも|て|う"}],"notes":"手 here means \"means, move\": 手を打つ mainly means \"to take measures\", often preventively: 早めに手を打つ, 何か手を打たないと. In negotiation it means \"to settle, to come to an agreement\", with で for the price: 五千円で手を打とう. Literally it is also \"to clap one's hands\", for example when an idea occurs to you. Neutral; more formal would be 対策を取る.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼はなかなか口を開かなかった。","romaji":"Kare wa nakanaka kuchi o hirakanakatta.","german":"He wouldn't open his mouth for a long time.","furigana":"かれ|くち|ひら"},{"japanese":"彼は口を開けば文句ばかり言う。","romaji":"Kare wa kuchi o hirakeba monku bakari iu.","german":"Every time he opens his mouth, he complains.","furigana":"かれ|くち|ひら|もんく|い"},{"japanese":"しばらく誰も話さなかったが、やがて社長が口を開いた。","romaji":"Shibaraku dare mo hanasanakatta ga, yagate shachō ga kuchi o hiraita.","german":"For a while nobody spoke, but then the company president began to speak.","furigana":"だれ|はな|しゃちょう|くち|ひら"}],"notes":"口を開く (ひらく) means \"to open one's mouth\" in the sense of \"to begin to speak, to express oneself\" - often after a silence: やっと口を開いた. With ば: 口を開けば〜ばかり \"every time he opens his mouth...\". The purely physical opening, for instance at the dentist, is 口を開ける (あける). The opposite is 口を閉ざす \"to fall silent\". Slightly elevated, frequent in narratives.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"腰を据えて勉強しよう。","romaji":"Koshi o suete benkyō shiyō.","german":"Let's settle down and study seriously.","furigana":"こし|す|べんきょう"},{"japanese":"転勤続きだったが、そろそろこの町に腰を据えて暮らしたい。","romaji":"Tenkin tsuzuki datta ga, sorosoro kono machi ni koshi o suete kurashitai.","german":"I was transferred constantly, but I'd like to finally settle down in this town.","furigana":"てんきんつづ|まち|こし|す|く"},{"japanese":"この問題は時間をかけて、腰を据えて話し合う必要がある。","romaji":"Kono mondai wa jikan o kakete, koshi o suete hanashiau hitsuyō ga aru.","german":"We need to take our time and discuss this problem thoroughly.","furigana":"もんだい|じかん|こし|す|はな|あ|ひつよう"}],"notes":"Literally \"to set the hips down firmly\": one sits down and stays. Figuratively it means (1) \"to apply oneself calmly and seriously to something\", mostly adverbial 腰を据えて取り組む/考える/勉強する, and (2) \"to settle down in a place\": 〜に腰を据える. The opposite is a half-hearted, unsteady attitude; the intransitive phrase 腰が据わらない (\"not to stay on task\") fits here. Neutral to slightly elevated.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"京都まで足を伸ばした。","romaji":"Kyōto made ashi o nobashita.","german":"I made a side trip as far as Kyoto.","furigana":"きょうと|あし|の"},{"japanese":"大きいお風呂で足を伸ばすと、疲れが取れる。","romaji":"Ōkii o-furo de ashi o nobasu to, tsukare ga toreru.","german":"When I stretch my legs in a big bath, my tiredness goes away.","furigana":"おお|ふろ|あし|の|つか|と"},{"japanese":"せっかくだから、もう少し足を伸ばして海を見に行こうか。","romaji":"Sekkaku da kara, mō sukoshi ashi o nobashite umi o mi ni ikō ka.","german":"Since we're here anyway, shall we go a little farther and see the sea?","furigana":"すこ|あし|の|うみ|み|い"}],"notes":"Literally \"to stretch out the legs\", for example in a bathtub. Figuratively it means \"to make a side trip, to go a bit farther\", with まで for the destination: 京都まで足を伸ばす. In this travel meaning 足を延ばす (延ばす \"to extend\") is also written. Unlike 寄る \"to drop by on the way\", it is about extending the route beyond the actual destination. Neutral.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"試験に落ちて肩を落とした。","romaji":"Shiken ni ochite kata o otoshita.","german":"I failed the exam and was crestfallen.","furigana":"しけん|お|かた|お"},{"japanese":"試合に負けた選手たちは、肩を落として帰っていった。","romaji":"Shiai ni maketa senshutachi wa, kata o otoshite kaette itta.","german":"The players who lost the match went home with drooping shoulders.","furigana":"しあい|ま|せんしゅ|かた|お|かえ"},{"japanese":"そんなに肩を落とさないで。また頑張ればいいよ。","romaji":"Sonna ni kata o otosanaide. Mata ganbareba ii yo.","german":"Don't be so downcast. You can just try hard again.","furigana":"かた|お|がんば"}],"notes":"Literally \"to drop the shoulders\": to be visibly disappointed or dejected. The expression mainly describes outward posture, often in narratives about others: 肩を落として帰る. The mere feeling is がっかりする; 気を落とす rather emphasizes the inner loss of courage and often appears in consolation. With ないで also as encouragement: そんなに肩を落とさないで.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼の説明に首を傾げた。","romaji":"Kare no setsumei ni kubi o kashigeta.","german":"I was skeptical about his explanation.","furigana":"かれ|せつめい|くび|かし"},{"japanese":"うちの犬は名前を呼ぶと首を傾げる。","romaji":"Uchi no inu wa namae o yobu to kubi o kashigeru.","german":"When you call our dog by name, it tilts its head.","furigana":"いぬ|なまえ|よ|くび|かし"},{"japanese":"どうして急に売れなくなったのか、店長も首を傾げている。","romaji":"Dōshite kyū ni urenaku natta no ka, tenchō mo kubi o kashigete iru.","german":"Even the store manager is puzzled about why it suddenly stopped selling.","furigana":"きゅう|う|てんちょう|くび|かし"}],"notes":"傾げる means \"to tilt\": to tilt the head as a gesture of doubt or wonder. The cause is marked with に: 説明に首を傾げる \"to be puzzled by the explanation\". Common in reports: 専門家も首を傾げている \"even experts are baffled\". Literal also for animals. Do not confuse it with 首を振る \"to shake one's head\". Often in kana: 首をかしげる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"旅行の計画を立てると心が弾む。","romaji":"Ryokō no keikaku o tateru to kokoro ga hazumu.","german":"My heart leaps with joy when I make travel plans.","furigana":"りょこう|けいかく|た|こころ|はず"},{"japanese":"久しぶりに友達に会えるので、朝から心が弾んでいる。","romaji":"Hisashiburi ni tomodachi ni aeru node, asa kara kokoro ga hazunde iru.","german":"Because I'm finally meeting my friends again, my heart has been leaping since morning.","furigana":"ひさ|ともだち|あ|あさ|こころ|はず"},{"japanese":"春になって花が咲くと、自然に心が弾みます。","romaji":"Haru ni natte hana ga saku to, shizen ni kokoro ga hazumimasu.","german":"When the flowers bloom in spring, one's heart naturally lifts.","furigana":"はる|はな|さ|しぜん|こころ|はず"}],"notes":"弾む means \"to bounce, to hop (like a ball)\": the heart bounces with anticipation or happiness - \"to be in high spirits, to feel cheerful\". Often with the cause + と or ので: 旅行のことを考えると心が弾む. Before nouns also 心弾む春. Related are 声が弾む \"to sound cheerful\" and 話が弾む \"the conversation gets lively\". Somewhat more elevated than the everyday わくわくする.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼女のスピーチはみんなの心を打った。","romaji":"Kanojo no supīchi wa minna no kokoro o utta.","german":"Her speech touched everyone's heart.","furigana":"かのじょ|こころ|う"},{"japanese":"その子の優しい言葉に、心を打たれました。","romaji":"Sono ko no yasashii kotoba ni, kokoro o utaremashita.","german":"I was deeply moved by the child's kind words.","furigana":"こ|やさ|ことば|こころ|う"},{"japanese":"心を打つ映画を見たいなら、これがおすすめだよ。","romaji":"Kokoro o utsu eiga o mitai nara, kore ga osusume da yo.","german":"If you want to see a film that really touches you, I recommend this one.","furigana":"こころ|う|えいが|み"}],"notes":"Literally \"to strike the heart\": words, a speech or an act deeply move someone. The subject is the thing that moves; the person is marked with の: 人々の心を打つ. The person who is moved usually uses the passive: 〜に心を打たれる. Before nouns: 心を打つ話. 胸を打つ means the same; 感動する is more everyday. Rather elevated.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"気を落とさないで、次があるよ。","romaji":"Ki o otosanaide, tsugi ga aru yo.","german":"Don't lose heart, there's always next time.","furigana":"き|お|つぎ"},{"japanese":"仕事が見つからなくて、兄はすっかり気を落としている。","romaji":"Shigoto ga mitsukaranakute, ani wa sukkari ki o otoshite iru.","german":"Because he can't find a job, my older brother is completely discouraged.","furigana":"しごと|み|あに|き|お"},{"japanese":"お父様のこと、大変でしたね。どうか気を落とさないでください。","romaji":"O-tōsama no koto, taihen deshita ne. Dōka ki o otosanaide kudasai.","german":"What happened with your father must have been hard. Please don't lose heart.","furigana":"とうさま|たいへん|き|お"}],"notes":"Literally \"to drop the spirit (気)\": to lose heart after a failure or loss. Very common as consolation: 気を落とさないで(ください); in condolences also the very polite お気を落とさずに. For description, the ている-form is usually used: すっかり気を落としている. がっかりする is rather brief disappointment, 落ち込む colloquially \"to be depressed\", 肩を落とす the visible posture.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"今日は外出するのは気が進まない。","romaji":"Kyō wa gaishutsu suru no wa ki ga susumanai.","german":"I don't feel like going out today.","furigana":"きょう|がいしゅつ|き|すす"},{"japanese":"誘ってくれてありがとう。でも、今回はあまり気が進まないんだ。","romaji":"Sasotte kurete arigatō. Demo, konkai wa amari ki ga susumanai n da.","german":"Thank you for inviting me, but I'm not really up for it this time.","furigana":"さそ|こんかい|き|すす"},{"japanese":"気が進まない仕事でも、最後までやらなければならない。","romaji":"Ki ga susumanai shigoto demo, saigo made yaranakereba naranai.","german":"Even work you don't feel like doing has to be finished.","furigana":"き|すす|しごと|さいご"}],"notes":"Literally \"the spirit (気) doesn't move forward\": an inner reluctance toward something you should do or are invited to. Typical: 〜のは気が進まない, あまり／どうも気が進まない, before a noun 気が進まない仕事. Popular as a soft, polite refusal: ちょっと気が進まないんです. Compared with the direct 〜たくない, it sounds more reserved; やる気がない means a general lack of motivation.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"失敗したけど気を取り直してもう一度やろう。","romaji":"Shippai shita kedo ki o torinaoshite mō ichido yarō.","german":"It didn't work out, but let's pick ourselves up and try once more.","furigana":"しっぱい|き|と|なお|いちど"},{"japanese":"雨で試合が中止になったが、気を取り直して練習に行った。","romaji":"Ame de shiai ga chūshi ni natta ga, ki o torinaoshite renshū ni itta.","german":"The match was canceled because of rain, but I pulled myself together and went to practice anyway.","furigana":"あめ|しあい|ちゅうし|き|と|なお|れんしゅう|い"},{"japanese":"彼女は少し泣いたあと、気を取り直して話を続けた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa sukoshi naita ato, ki o torinaoshite hanashi o tsuzuketa.","german":"After crying a little, she composed herself and continued speaking.","furigana":"かのじょ|すこ|な|き|と|なお|はなし|つづ"}],"notes":"Literally \"to take the spirit anew\" (取り直す \"to take again\"): to recover after a setback or disappointment. Almost always in the て-form, followed by the next action: 気を取り直して、もう一度. Unlike 立ち直る, which describes a longer recovery, this is a brief inner restart. Similar is 元気を出す \"to cheer up\". Neutral.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼はこの町で顔が売れている。","romaji":"Kare wa kono machi de kao ga urete iru.","german":"He is a familiar face in this town.","furigana":"かれ|まち|かお|う"},{"japanese":"テレビに出るようになって、彼女はだんだん顔が売れてきた。","romaji":"Terebi ni deru yō ni natte, kanojo wa dandan kao ga urete kita.","german":"Since she started appearing on TV, she has gradually become better known.","furigana":"で|かのじょ|かお|う"},{"japanese":"あの俳優は顔が売れているので、帽子とマスクで出かけるそうだ。","romaji":"Ano haiyū wa kao ga urete iru node, bōshi to masuku de dekakeru sō da.","german":"That actor is so well known that he reportedly goes out in a hat and mask.","furigana":"はいゆう|かお|う|ぼうし|で"}],"notes":"Literally \"the face (顔) sells (売れる)\": someone is known to the public or within a certain circle. Mostly in the ている-form: この町で顔が売れている; the development is shown by 顔が売れてきた \"is becoming better and better known\". Similar is 名が売れる. Do not confuse it with 顔が広い \"to know many people, to be well connected\". Neutral to colloquial.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"彼は先に手を回して準備していた。","romaji":"Kare wa saki ni te o mawashite junbi shite ita.","german":"He had arranged everything beforehand behind the scenes.","furigana":"かれ|さき|て|まわ|じゅんび"},{"japanese":"父が裏で手を回して、私をその会社に入れてくれたらしい。","romaji":"Chichi ga ura de te o mawashite, watashi o sono kaisha ni irete kureta rashii.","german":"Apparently my father pulled strings in the background and got me into that company.","furigana":"ちち|うら|て|まわ|わたし|かいしゃ|い"},{"japanese":"彼は彼女の肩にそっと手を回した。","romaji":"Kare wa kanojo no kata ni sotto te o mawashita.","german":"He gently put his arm around her shoulders.","furigana":"かれ|かのじょ|かた|て|まわ"}],"notes":"Literally \"to move the hand around\": to make arrangements in the background, to use connections, to pull the strings. Common are 裏で手を回す or 〜に手を回す (whom one turns to) and 手を回しておく \"to arrange in advance\". Often with a slightly negative flavor of exerting influence. Literally it also means \"to put an arm around someone\": 肩に手を回す. Distinguish from 手を打つ \"to take measures\".","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"小さい子供は手がかかる。","romaji":"Chiisai kodomo wa te ga kakaru.","german":"Small children take a lot of work.","furigana":"ちい|こども|て"},{"japanese":"この料理はおいしいけど、作るのに手がかかる。","romaji":"Kono ryōri wa oishii kedo, tsukuru no ni te ga kakaru.","german":"This dish is delicious, but it takes a lot of effort to make.","furigana":"りょうり|つく|て"},{"japanese":"上の子はあまり手がかからなかったので、楽でした。","romaji":"Ue no ko wa amari te ga kakaranakatta node, raku deshita.","german":"Our oldest child didn't need much care, so it was easy.","furigana":"うえ|こ|て|らく"}],"notes":"手 here stands for \"work, care\": 手がかかる means \"to take a lot of effort, to need a lot of looking after, to be laborious\" - with children, pets, the sick, or with tasks such as cooking. Before nouns usually 手のかかる子／料理. Negated it is praise: 手のかからない子 \"an easy, independent child\". Similar is 手間がかかる, which rather emphasizes the time and labor involved. かかる is mostly in kana.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"毎日電車で同じ人と顔を合わせる。","romaji":"Mainichi densha de onaji hito to kao o awaseru.","german":"I run into the same person on the train every day.","furigana":"まいにちでんしゃ|おな|ひと|かお|あ"},{"japanese":"けんかしたばかりなので、彼と顔を合わせるのが気まずい。","romaji":"Kenka shita bakari na node, kare to kao o awaseru no ga kimazui.","german":"We just had an argument, so it's awkward to face him.","furigana":"かれ|かお|あ|き"},{"japanese":"この二つのチームが決勝で顔を合わせるのは初めてだ。","romaji":"Kono futatsu no chīmu ga kesshō de kao o awaseru no wa hajimete da.","german":"This is the first time these two teams have met in the final.","furigana":"ふた|けっしょう|かお|あ|はじ"}],"notes":"Literally \"to bring the faces together\": to meet each other in person, to face one another. The other person is marked with と: 〜と顔を合わせる. Often used for regular or unavoidable meetings and for discomfort: 顔を合わせるのが気まずい. In sports and performances it means \"to face each other, to appear together\". Unlike the neutral 会う, it emphasizes direct confrontation; the noun 顔合わせ is a first get-to-know-you meeting.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"たまには息を抜くことも大切だ。","romaji":"Tama ni wa iki o nuku koto mo taisetsu da.","german":"It's important to take a break once in a while, too.","furigana":"いき|ぬ|たいせつ"},{"japanese":"試験勉強ばかりだと疲れるから、ときどき息を抜いてね。","romaji":"Shiken benkyō bakari da to tsukareru kara, tokidoki iki o nuite ne.","german":"Studying only for exams is tiring, so take a break every now and then.","furigana":"しけんべんきょう|つか|いき|ぬ"},{"japanese":"忙しくて、息を抜く時間もない。","romaji":"Isogashikute, iki o nuku jikan mo nai.","german":"I'm so busy that I don't even have time to catch my breath.","furigana":"いそが|いき|ぬ|じかん"}],"notes":"Literally \"to let the breath (息) out (抜く)\": to release tension, to take a short breather. Much more common is the noun 息抜き \"breather\": 息抜きに散歩する. Typical frames: たまには息を抜く, 息を抜く時間もない. Do not confuse it with 手を抜く \"to slack off, to not put in effort\", which is negative. Neutral, used in everyday life and at work or study.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"腹を決めて転職した。","romaji":"Hara o kimete tenshoku shita.","german":"I made up my mind and changed jobs.","furigana":"はら|き|てんしょく"},{"japanese":"いつまでも悩んでいないで、そろそろ腹を決めたら?","romaji":"Itsumade mo nayande inaide, sorosoro hara o kimetara?","german":"Stop agonizing forever - why don't you make up your mind already?","furigana":"なや|はら|き"},{"japanese":"社長は会社を売ると腹を決めたようだ。","romaji":"Shachō wa kaisha o uru to hara o kimeta yō da.","german":"The company president seems to have resolved to sell the company.","furigana":"しゃちょう|かいしゃ|う|はら|き"}],"notes":"In Japanese, 腹 \"belly\" is regarded as the seat of inner resolve; 腹を決める therefore means \"to make a firm decision\", usually for a hard or risky decision. The content is often marked with と: 会社を辞めると腹を決める. 腹をくくる and 覚悟を決める mean the same; 決心する is more neutral. Colloquial, sounds determined and somewhat gutsy.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"怖い話を聞いて鳥肌が立った。","romaji":"Kowai hanashi o kiite torihada ga tatta.","german":"I got goose bumps when I heard the scary story.","furigana":"こわ|はなし|き|とりはだ|た"},{"japanese":"薄着で外に出たら、寒くて鳥肌が立った。","romaji":"Usugi de soto ni detara, samukute torihada ga tatta.","german":"When I went out lightly dressed, I got goose bumps from the cold.","furigana":"うすぎ|そと|で|さむ|とりはだ|た"},{"japanese":"彼女の歌声があまりに美しくて、鳥肌が立ちました。","romaji":"Kanojo no utagoe ga amari ni utsukushikute, torihada ga tachimashita.","german":"Her singing voice was so beautiful that I got goose bumps.","furigana":"かのじょ|うたごえ|うつく|とりはだ|た"}],"notes":"Literally \"the bird skin (鳥肌) stands up\": the skin looks like that of a plucked chicken. Originally it describes goose bumps from cold, fear or disgust. Today it is also very often used positively for deep emotion, for example at music or sports, although some feel this is not standard usage. The verb 立つ is intransitive and conjugates normally (鳥肌が立った). Colloquially one also says 鳥肌もの (\"something that gives you goose bumps\") or simply 鳥肌！.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"そのニュースを聞いて耳を疑った。","romaji":"Sono nyūsu o kiite mimi o utagatta.","german":"When I heard the news, I couldn't believe my ears.","furigana":"き|みみ|うたが"},{"japanese":"宝くじが当たったと聞いて、思わず耳を疑った。","romaji":"Takarakuji ga atatta to kiite, omowazu mimi o utagatta.","german":"When I heard I'd won the lottery, I could hardly believe my ears.","furigana":"たから|あ|き|おも|みみ|うたが"},{"japanese":"先生の口から耳を疑うような言葉が出た。","romaji":"Sensei no kuchi kara mimi o utagau yō na kotoba ga deta.","german":"Words came out of the teacher's mouth that were hard to believe.","furigana":"せんせい|くち|みみ|うたが|ことば|で"}],"notes":"Literally \"to doubt the ears\": to hear something so surprising that one can hardly believe it. Almost always in the past tense: 〜を聞いて耳を疑った. Before nouns: 耳を疑うような話／ニュース \"an unbelievable story/piece of news\". The counterpart for things seen is 目を疑う. Neutral, common in narratives and reports; often intensified with 思わず.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"目を閉じてゆっくり深呼吸してください。","romaji":"Me o tojite yukkuri shinkokyū shite kudasai.","german":"Please close your eyes and breathe deeply and slowly.","furigana":"め|と|しんこきゅう"},{"japanese":"目を閉じると、ふるさとの景色が浮かんでくる。","romaji":"Me o tojiru to, furusato no keshiki ga ukande kuru.","german":"When I close my eyes, the scenery of my hometown comes to mind.","furigana":"め|と|けしき|う"},{"japanese":"祖母は家族に囲まれて、静かに目を閉じました。","romaji":"Sobo wa kazoku ni kakomarete, shizuka ni me o tojimashita.","german":"My grandmother, surrounded by her family, quietly closed her eyes for good.","furigana":"そぼ|かぞく|かこ|しず|め|と"}],"notes":"閉じる \"to close\": 目を閉じる means \"to close one's eyes\", for example to relax, think or remember. More colloquial is 目をつぶる, which also means \"to turn a blind eye, to overlook something\" - 目を閉じる does not normally have this meaning. In elevated style, 静かに目を閉じる serves as a euphemism for \"to die\". Opposite: 目を開ける. Intransitive: 目が閉じる.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"映画を見て涙を流した。","romaji":"Eiga o mite namida o nagashita.","german":"I shed tears watching the movie.","furigana":"えいが|み|なみだ|なが"},{"japanese":"優勝した選手たちは、みんなうれし涙を流していた。","romaji":"Yūshō shita senshutachi wa, minna ureshi-namida o nagashite ita.","german":"The players who won were all shedding tears of joy.","furigana":"ゆうしょう|せんしゅ|なみだ|なが"},{"japanese":"彼女は何も言わずに、静かに涙を流していた。","romaji":"Kanojo wa nani mo iwazu ni, shizuka ni namida o nagashite ita.","german":"She said nothing and quietly shed tears.","furigana":"かのじょ|なに|い|しず|なみだ|なが"}],"notes":"涙を流す means \"to shed tears\" and is more descriptive than 泣く: it emphasizes the visible tears, often silent and also from joy or emotion, e.g. うれし涙を流す, 感動して涙を流す. Common in narratives and reports. Intransitively one says 涙が流れる or 涙が出る \"tears come to my eyes\". Neutral to slightly elevated.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."},
{"examples":[{"japanese":"卒業式で胸が一杯になった。","romaji":"Sotsugyō-shiki de mune ga ippai ni natta.","german":"I was overcome with emotion at the graduation ceremony.","furigana":"そつぎょうしき|むね|いっぱい"},{"japanese":"みんなの温かい言葉に、胸がいっぱいになりました。","romaji":"Minna no atatakai kotoba ni, mune ga ippai ni narimashita.","german":"Everyone's warm words filled my heart.","furigana":"あたた|ことば|むね"},{"japanese":"娘の結婚式では胸がいっぱいで、何も食べられなかった。","romaji":"Musume no kekkonshiki de wa mune ga ippai de, nani mo taberarenakatta.","german":"At my daughter's wedding I was so choked up that I couldn't eat anything.","furigana":"むすめ|けっこんしき|むね|なに|た"}],"notes":"Literally \"the chest (胸) becomes full\": to be overwhelmed by feelings such as joy, gratitude, emotion or sadness, so that one can hardly speak or eat. The trigger is marked with に or で: 感謝の気持ちで胸がいっぱいになる. いっぱい is mostly written in kana. Do not confuse it with お腹がいっぱい \"to be full (of food)\". Neutral, often at celebrations and farewells.","levelNote":"Estimated level – no source confirms it."}
];
